Object.assign((window.KB_ENRICHMENT = window.KB_ENRICHMENT || { byId: {} }).byId, {"intermediate_uveitis":{"name":"Intermediate uveitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["intermediate uveitis","vitritis","peripheral uveitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Inflammation is centred on the vitreous, the peripheral retina and the pars plana rather than the anterior segment","The usual symptoms are floaters and blurred vision, typically in a white, comfortable eye with little pain or redness","Vitreous inflammatory aggregates (snowballs) and exudative material over the pars plana (snowbanking) are the hallmark signs, best seen on indented indirect ophthalmoscopy","Cystoid macular oedema is the leading cause of visual loss and should be sought on optical coherence tomography","Recognised associations include multiple sclerosis, sarcoidosis, Lyme disease and tuberculosis, although many cases are idiopathic","Peripheral retinal vasculitis and neovascularisation of the snowbank may occur and can bleed","Treatment follows a stepladder of corticosteroids, then steroid-sparing immunosuppression, with control of macular oedema as the main goal"],"pathophysiology":"Intermediate uveitis is a chronic, predominantly T-cell mediated inflammation of the vitreous base, peripheral retina and pars plana. Inflammatory cells aggregate within the vitreous as snowballs and become organised into a snowbank overlying the inferior pars plana. Breakdown of the blood-retinal barrier and release of inflammatory cytokines lead to cystoid macular oedema, the principal threat to vision. The disorder may be idiopathic or form part of a systemic inflammatory or infective disease such as sarcoidosis, multiple sclerosis or tuberculosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Posterior uveitis","Sarcoid uveitis","Multiple sclerosis-associated uveitis","Lyme disease","Tuberculous uveitis","Primary intraocular lymphoma (masquerade syndrome)","Ocular toxocariasis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Indirect ophthalmoscopy with scleral indentation to detect snowballs and snowbanking","Optical coherence tomography of the macula for cystoid macular oedema","Fluorescein angiography to demonstrate peripheral vasculitis and leakage","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain when demyelination is suspected","Serology for Lyme disease and syphilis","Chest imaging and serum angiotensin-converting enzyme for sarcoidosis, with tuberculosis testing"],"redFlags":["Dense vitritis in an older patient, raising the possibility of masquerade lymphoma","Neurological symptoms suggesting multiple sclerosis","Profound or refractory cystoid macular oedema","Vitreous haemorrhage or tractional detachment from snowbank neovascularisation"],"prognosis":"The course is frequently chronic and relapsing; the visual outcome is largely determined by how well cystoid macular oedema is controlled, and many patients maintain good vision with appropriate treatment.","pitfalls":["Failing to investigate for an underlying systemic association such as multiple sclerosis or sarcoidosis","Overlooking masquerade lymphoma in older patients with persistent vitritis","Under-treating chronic cystoid macular oedema","Not examining the peripheral retina and pars plana adequately"],"management":["Control inflammation with corticosteroids by periocular or intravitreal injection or systemic route, using a stepladder approach","Add steroid-sparing immunosuppression (methotrexate or mycophenolate) and escalate to biologics such as anti-TNF agents for chronic or refractory disease","Treat cystoid macular oedema, the main threat to vision, and monitor it with optical coherence tomography","Identify and treat any underlying systemic cause (sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, Lyme disease or multiple sclerosis) with appropriate directed therapy","Manage peripheral retinal neovascularisation of the snowbank with laser photocoagulation or cryotherapy, and consider vitrectomy for non-clearing vitreous haemorrhage or persistent inflammation","Coordinate care between ophthalmology and relevant physicians (neurology, respiratory or infectious diseases) for associated systemic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course: Uveitis and Ocular Inflammation"],"crossLinks":[]},"intermittent_exotropia":{"name":"intermittent exotropia","system":"Visual system / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["X(T)","intermittent divergent squint"],"chapter":null,"source":"Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common type of childhood exotropia: an intermittent outward deviation controlled by fusion much of the time that breaks down with fatigue, illness, inattention or distance fixation","Classic history is of one eye drifting outward when the child is tired or daydreaming, with characteristic closure or squinting of one eye in bright sunlight","It is often more manifest for distance than near and is classified by the distance-near relationship into basic, divergence-excess/pseudo-divergence-excess, and convergence-insufficiency types","Control is assessed clinically and graded (for example with the Newcastle Control Score), using spontaneous control, recovery after cover and the frequency of manifest deviation to guide management and the timing of surgery","Because binocular single vision is present for much of the time, amblyopia is less common than in constant strabismus, but binocular function and stereopsis should be monitored","Management ranges from observation, refractive correction, orthoptic convergence exercises and part-time occlusion to surgery (usually bilateral lateral rectus recession or a unilateral recess-resect) when control deteriorates","Diplopia is uncommon in children because they suppress, but adults with a decompensating exodeviation may report intermittent diplopia or asthenopia","Deterioration to a constant exotropia, reducing stereoacuity, or an increasing frequency or size of deviation are indications to intervene"],"pathophysiology":"Intermittent exotropia reflects an imbalance between an excessive tonic divergence position of the eyes and the fusional convergence mechanisms that normally maintain alignment. When fusional convergence is adequate the eyes stay straight; when fusional control is overcome, by fatigue, illness, inattention, bright light or dissociation under monocular or distance conditions, the latent exophoria becomes a manifest exotropia. The precise cause is not fully understood but involves anatomical and innervational factors and the balance between accommodative convergence and the resting divergent position. Over time control may deteriorate, with a longer duration of manifest deviation, suppression of the deviating eye and possible progression to a constant exotropia.","additionalDifferentials":["Exophoria, which is fully compensated and never manifest","Constant or congenital (infantile) exotropia","Sensory exotropia from monocular visual loss","Convergence insufficiency with a near exodeviation and a distinct near-distance pattern","Consecutive exotropia following previous esotropia surgery","Duane retraction syndrome type II or third nerve palsy","Neurological or internuclear causes of an exodeviation in acquired cases"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Prism cover test for distance and near, including with plus lenses or patching to unmask true divergence excess","Assessment and grading of control (for example the Newcastle Control Score)","Cycloplegic refraction and correction of significant refractive error","Stereoacuity and sensory or binocular function testing","Amblyopia assessment with monocular acuities","Extended monocular occlusion to reveal the maximum deviation","Dilated fundus examination to exclude a sensory cause"],"redFlags":["Acquired exotropia with neurological symptoms or signs","Reduced vision in one eye suggesting a sensory cause, requiring exclusion of organic pathology","Progression from an intermittent to a constant deviation with loss of stereopsis","New-onset diplopia in a child, which is uncommon and warrants investigation"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally good: many children maintain useful binocular vision and stereopsis, and surgery achieves satisfactory alignment in most, although under- and over-correction and recurrence occur and repeat surgery is sometimes needed. Binocular outcomes are best when good control and stereoacuity are preserved before intervention.","pitfalls":["Measuring only the near angle and missing a distance or divergence-excess deviation (use prolonged occlusion and plus lenses)","Overlooking a small refractive error, including myopia, that affects control","Mistaking a constant sensory exotropia for the benign intermittent form","Operating too early or on poorly documented control, risking consecutive esotropia","Failing to monitor stereopsis and control over time"],"management":["Correct significant refractive error after cycloplegic refraction with spectacles, including overminus lenses to stimulate accommodative convergence","Use non-surgical measures for well-controlled deviation: active observation, orthoptic convergence exercises and part-time occlusion","Treat any amblyopia with occlusion or penalisation of the dominant eye","Offer strabismus surgery, usually bilateral lateral rectus recession or a unilateral recess-resect, when control deteriorates or the deviation becomes frequent or constant","Monitor control (for example with the Newcastle Control Score), binocular single vision and stereoacuity to time intervention","Manage under paediatric ophthalmology and orthoptics, counselling on the possibility of under-correction, over-correction and recurrence requiring further surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","von Noorden's Binocular Vision and Ocular Motility"],"crossLinks":["accommodative_esotropia","dissociated_vertical_deviation"]},"internal_impingement_shoulder":{"name":"internal impingement shoulder","system":"musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["posterosuperior glenoid impingement","throwers shoulder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Matsen's The Shoulder","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Internal impingement describes contact between the articular (undersurface) fibres of the posterosuperior rotator cuff and the posterosuperior glenoid labrum when the arm is abducted and maximally externally rotated.","It characteristically affects overhead athletes such as throwers, tennis players and swimmers, who present with posterior shoulder pain in the late cocking phase.","Although a degree of posterosuperior contact is physiological in abduction and external rotation, it becomes pathological when repetitive, causing articular-sided cuff fraying and posterosuperior labral damage.","Glenohumeral internal rotation deficit from posteroinferior capsular tightness is a central contributing factor and a key rehabilitation target.","Scapular dyskinesis and subtle anterior microinstability shift the contact zone and aggravate the impingement, so assess the whole kinetic chain.","It is distinct from classic external subacromial impingement; the pain is posterior and provoked in the abducted externally rotated position rather than on forward elevation.","First-line management is non-operative, emphasising posterior capsular stretching, scapular control and correction of throwing mechanics.","Surgery is reserved for refractory cases and addresses the specific pathology found, such as debridement of articular-sided cuff fraying and labral treatment, rather than routine subacromial decompression."],"pathophysiology":"During the late cocking phase of overhead activity the abducted, maximally externally rotated humerus brings the articular surface of the supraspinatus and infraspinatus into contact with the posterosuperior glenoid rim and labrum. When repetitive, this normally protective contact produces shear and compressive loading that frays the undersurface of the cuff and damages the posterosuperior labrum. A tight posteroinferior capsule causes glenohumeral internal rotation deficit and a posterosuperior shift of the humeral rotation point, increasing the peel-back force on the biceps-labral complex and the degree of contact. Concurrent anterior capsular laxity or microinstability and scapular malposition further increase external rotation and posterior contact. The result is a self-perpetuating cycle of articular-sided partial cuff tearing, labral injury and pain that limits throwing performance.","additionalDifferentials":["SLAP (superior labral) tear","Articular-sided partial rotator cuff tear from other causes","Anterior glenohumeral microinstability","Subacromial (external) impingement syndrome","Biceps tendinopathy","Suprascapular neuropathy","Posterior labral tear"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MR arthrography to demonstrate articular-sided cuff fraying and posterosuperior labral pathology","Standard MRI to assess cuff, labrum and any cysts","Assessment and quantification of glenohumeral internal rotation deficit","Evaluation of scapular kinematics and posture","Examination for anterior microinstability including apprehension and relocation testing","Diagnostic arthroscopy with dynamic assessment in abduction and external rotation for refractory cases"],"redFlags":["Progressive weakness suggesting a significant or full-thickness rotator cuff tear","Features of frank instability with recurrent subluxation","Infraspinatus wasting suggesting suprascapular nerve involvement or a paralabral cyst","Night and rest pain unrelated to activity raising concern for other pathology"],"prognosis":"Most overhead athletes improve with a structured programme addressing posterior capsular tightness, scapular control and throwing mechanics, though return to the pre-injury level of high-demand throwing is not guaranteed. Refractory structural lesions may require arthroscopic treatment, with variable rates of return to elite performance.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as external subacromial impingement and performing an inappropriate acromioplasty.","Neglecting glenohumeral internal rotation deficit and scapular dyskinesis, the principal reversible drivers.","Interpreting the physiological posterosuperior contact seen on imaging as pathological in an asymptomatic athlete.","Overlooking associated anterior microinstability that perpetuates the impingement."],"management":["Prioritise a structured rehabilitation programme targeting posterior capsular tightness (glenohumeral internal rotation deficit) with posterior capsule stretching such as the sleeper stretch","Restore scapular control and rotator cuff strength, and correct throwing mechanics and the wider kinetic chain","Modify activity with relative rest from overhead throwing and a graded return-to-throwing programme","Use analgesia and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for symptomatic relief during rehabilitation","Reserve arthroscopic surgery (debridement of articular-sided cuff fraying, labral repair and capsular management) for structural lesions refractory to conservative treatment","Manage with sports medicine, physiotherapy and orthopaedic input, counselling that return to pre-injury high-demand throwing is not guaranteed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Matsen's The Shoulder","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics","Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Trauma"],"crossLinks":[]},"internuclear_ophthalmoplegia":{"name":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["INO","MLF syndrome","medial longitudinal fasciculus syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The hallmark is impaired adduction of the eye ipsilateral to the lesion with dissociated abducting nystagmus of the contralateral eye on attempted horizontal gaze","Convergence is characteristically preserved when the lesion is caudal (pontine), since the near reflex uses a separate midbrain pathway, but may be lost in rostral (midbrain) lesions","The lesion lies in the medial longitudinal fasciculus, which relays the abducens internuclear signal to the contralateral medial rectus subnucleus of the oculomotor complex","Bilateral INO in a young adult strongly suggests multiple sclerosis, whereas unilateral INO in an older patient suggests a brainstem infarct","Skew deviation with a vertical misalignment and gaze-evoked or upbeat nystagmus may accompany the horizontal deficit","Adduction lag may be subtle and best detected by observing the speed of saccades rather than the range of movement","The WEBINO variant (wall-eyed bilateral INO) shows bilateral adduction failure with exotropia in primary position"],"pathophysiology":"The medial longitudinal fasciculus carries signals from abducens internuclear neurones in the pons upward to the contralateral medial rectus subnucleus of the oculomotor nucleus, coordinating conjugate horizontal gaze. A lesion of the fasciculus interrupts this yoking so that the adducting eye fails to move while the abducting eye develops a dissociated nystagmus, thought to reflect an adaptive increase in innervation. In younger patients the commonest cause is a demyelinating plaque of multiple sclerosis, while in older patients small-vessel brainstem infarction predominates; tumour, Wernicke encephalopathy, and drug toxicity are less common causes.","additionalDifferentials":["Partial oculomotor nerve palsy with medial rectus weakness","Myasthenia gravis mimicking an INO (pseudo-INO)","Thyroid eye disease restricting ocular motility","Orbital myositis or restrictive strabismus","One-and-a-half syndrome when a horizontal gaze palsy coexists","Skew deviation from other posterior fossa pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brainstem with attention to the dorsal pons and midbrain","MRI of the whole brain and spinal cord to detect demyelinating lesions","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis for oligoclonal bands when demyelination is suspected","Acetylcholine receptor antibodies and repetitive nerve stimulation to exclude myasthenia","Vascular risk assessment and stroke workup in older patients","Thiamine status where nutritional deficiency is possible"],"redFlags":["Acute onset with other brainstem signs suggesting infarction requiring urgent stroke pathway assessment","Bilateral INO in a young patient prompting evaluation for multiple sclerosis","Associated reduced consciousness, ataxia, or ophthalmoplegia suggesting Wernicke encephalopathy","Rapidly progressive signs suggesting a mass lesion in the brainstem"],"prognosis":"Demyelinating INO frequently improves or resolves over weeks, whereas ischaemic INO has a variable outcome depending on infarct size; residual adduction lag and diplopia may persist and reflect the underlying cause more than the sign itself.","pitfalls":["Attributing the adduction failure to a medial rectus palsy and missing the internuclear localisation","Overlooking myasthenia, which can perfectly mimic an INO and fluctuate","Failing to image the spinal cord and brain fully when demyelination is suspected","Missing an accompanying skew deviation or one-and-a-half syndrome that changes localisation"],"management":["Treat the underlying cause rather than the sign: corticosteroids for an acute demyelinating relapse and disease-modifying therapy for multiple sclerosis","Manage ischaemic internuclear ophthalmoplegia with stroke secondary prevention using antiplatelet therapy and vascular risk factor control","Correct thiamine deficiency promptly where Wernicke encephalopathy is possible, and treat other identified causes such as tumour or drug toxicity","Relieve symptomatic diplopia with prisms or temporary occlusion while the underlying process is treated","Investigate and monitor with neuroimaging and, where relevant, cerebrospinal fluid analysis, and exclude myasthenia gravis as a mimic","Refer to neurology and neuro-ophthalmology, coordinating long-term management of the causative disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"interrupted_aortic_arch":{"name":"Interrupted aortic arch","system":"Cardiovascular / Congenital","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IAA","aortic arch interruption","interrupted arch"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["There is complete anatomical discontinuity between the ascending and descending aorta, so the lower body depends on flow through the arterial duct","Types are classified by the site of interruption relative to the arch vessels, with interruption distal to the left subclavian, between the left carotid and subclavian, or between the carotids","The systemic circulation to the lower body is duct-dependent, and closure of the duct precipitates shock, acidosis and collapse","It almost always coexists with a ventricular septal defect, and a subaortic narrowing is common","Prostaglandin infusion to maintain ductal patency is life-saving while the neonate is stabilised and prepared for surgery","Type B interruption is strongly associated with 22q11.2 deletion and the DiGeorge phenotype","Definitive treatment is surgical reconstruction re-establishing arch continuity and repairing associated defects"],"pathophysiology":"Interrupted aortic arch is an extreme conotruncal and arch anomaly in which a segment of the aortic arch is absent, leaving no luminal continuity between the proximal and distal aorta. Perfusion of the lower body and any arch vessels beyond the interruption depends entirely on right-to-left flow through the arterial duct. While the duct remains open the circulation is balanced, but as the duct constricts after birth lower-body perfusion falls abruptly, causing profound metabolic acidosis, renal and gut hypoperfusion and cardiovascular collapse. An almost invariable ventricular septal defect and frequent posterior malalignment causing subaortic obstruction shape the intracardiac haemodynamics and pulmonary overcirculation. The lesion, particularly type B, is closely linked to 22q11.2 microdeletion.","additionalDifferentials":["Critical coarctation of the aorta","Hypoplastic left heart syndrome","Critical aortic stenosis","Other duct-dependent systemic circulation lesions","Sepsis or metabolic disease causing neonatal collapse","Aortic arch hypoplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to confirm arch discontinuity, the ventricular septal defect and subaortic anatomy","Cardiac CT or MR angiography to define the interruption site and arch vessels","Preductal and postductal saturation and blood pressure comparison","Genetic testing for 22q11.2 deletion with calcium and immune assessment","Arterial blood gas to detect metabolic acidosis from lower-body hypoperfusion","Cardiac catheterisation in selected complex cases"],"redFlags":["Circulatory collapse and severe acidosis as the duct closes","Absent or weak lower-limb pulses with differential upper and lower body perfusion","Oliguria or signs of gut ischaemia from lower-body hypoperfusion","Hypocalcaemia and immune deficiency in the DiGeorge spectrum"],"prognosis":"Untreated the condition is rapidly fatal once the duct closes, but with prostaglandin stabilisation and surgical repair survival is good, although recurrent arch obstruction and residual subaortic stenosis may require reintervention.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing neonatal collapse as sepsis and missing the duct-dependent lesion","Allowing the duct to close before prostaglandin is started","Overlooking subaortic obstruction that complicates surgical planning","Failing to test for 22q11.2 deletion, with its calcium, immune and irradiated blood product implications"],"management":["Commence immediate prostaglandin E infusion to maintain ductal patency and preserve lower-body perfusion pending surgery","Stabilise the neonate with correction of metabolic acidosis, inotropic support and mechanical ventilation, avoiding excessive supplemental oxygen to limit pulmonary overcirculation","Undertake definitive surgical repair re-establishing aortic arch continuity with concurrent ventricular septal defect closure and relief of subaortic obstruction","Test for 22q11.2 deletion and manage associated hypocalcaemia and immune deficiency, using irradiated blood products and caution with live vaccines","Maintain lifelong cardiology surveillance for recurrent arch obstruction and progressive residual subaortic stenosis requiring reintervention","Provide genetic counselling and family assessment given the 22q11.2 microdeletion association","Coordinate care across neonatal intensive care, congenital cardiology and cardiac surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","Anderson's Paediatric Cardiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"intersection_syndrome":{"name":"Intersection syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Hand & Peripheral nerve","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["proximal intersection syndrome","crossover syndrome","oarsmen's wrist","squeaker's wrist"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with pain and swelling on the dorsoradial forearm a short distance proximal to the wrist, above the extensor retinaculum","The site corresponds to where the first dorsal compartment tendons cross over the second compartment","The first compartment (abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis) crosses the second (extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis) at an oblique angle","Friction at the crossover produces a tenosynovitis or peritendinous inflammation of the second compartment","There may be palpable crepitus or a squeak, sometimes likened to wet leather, over the affected area","It is an overuse condition seen in rowers, weightlifters, racquet sports and skiers","It is distinguished from de Quervain tenosynovitis, which occurs more distally at the radial styloid within the first compartment","Generic management includes activity modification, wrist splinting and corticosteroid injection into the second compartment, with surgical release rarely required"],"pathophysiology":"Intersection syndrome arises from repetitive wrist flexion and extension causing friction where the first dorsal compartment tendons, the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis, cross obliquely over the second compartment tendons, the extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis, a few centimetres proximal to Lister tubercle. The friction sets up a tenosynovitis and peritendinous inflammation with oedema, and sometimes an adventitial bursitis, producing the pain, swelling and crepitus that characterise the condition.","additionalDifferentials":["De Quervain tenosynovitis at the radial styloid","Wartenberg syndrome from superficial radial nerve irritation","Distal radius stress injury or fracture","Wrist osteoarthritis, for example scaphotrapeziotrapezoid or radioscaphoid","Extensor tendinopathy","Dorsal wrist ganglion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical localisation of tenderness proximal to the wrist with crepitus over the crossover","Ultrasound demonstrating peritendinous fluid and oedema at the intersection","MRI when the diagnosis is uncertain or to exclude other pathology","Plain radiographs to exclude a bony cause such as fracture or arthritis","The Finkelstein test to help differentiate the condition from de Quervain tenosynovitis"],"redFlags":["Signs of infection suggesting a septic tenosynovitis","An underlying fracture or bony injury","Features of compartment compromise after acute trauma","Neurological signs indicating superficial radial nerve involvement"],"prognosis":"The condition is generally self-limiting and resolves with rest and conservative treatment, though it may recur if the provoking activity is not modified.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with de Quervain tenosynovitis, which lies at a different site and is treated differently","Injecting the wrong compartment","Overlooking superficial radial nerve involvement","Failing to modify the provoking activity, leading to recurrence"],"management":["Modify activity with relative rest and avoidance of the provoking repetitive wrist flexion and extension","Apply thumb-spica or wrist splinting to rest the first and second dorsal compartment tendons","Use ice and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain and peritendinous inflammation","Consider corticosteroid injection into the second-compartment tendon sheath for refractory symptoms","Refer for physiotherapy with graded stretching, strengthening and correction of technique and ergonomics before return to sport","Reserve surgical decompression and tenosynovectomy of the second dorsal compartment for cases failing conservative measures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"intersection_syndrome_distal":{"name":"intersection syndrome distal","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["distal intersection syndrome","EPL-ECRL/ECRB intersection tenosynovitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Distal intersection syndrome is a tenosynovitis occurring where the extensor pollicis longus crosses over the extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis, distal to Lister's tubercle.","It is much less common and more distal than the classic proximal intersection syndrome, which occurs where the first-compartment muscles cross the radial wrist extensors.","Pain, swelling and sometimes crepitus localise to the dorsal wrist around Lister's tubercle rather than the more proximal forearm.","The extensor pollicis longus is at risk because it angulates sharply around Lister's tubercle, where friction and tenosynovitis can precede attritional rupture.","Ultrasound or MRI shows peritendinous oedema and fluid at the crossover point of the extensor pollicis longus with the radial wrist extensors.","First-line management is activity modification, splinting and anti-inflammatory measures, with corticosteroid injection used cautiously given the rupture risk.","It must be distinguished from de Quervain tenosynovitis, isolated extensor pollicis longus tenosynovitis and dorsal wrist ganglion."],"pathophysiology":"At the dorsal wrist the extensor pollicis longus turns sharply around Lister's tubercle and crosses superficial to the second-compartment radial wrist extensors, the extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis. Repetitive wrist and thumb motion generates friction at this distal crossover, producing a peritendinous tenosynovitis with oedema and, at times, crepitus. Persistent inflammation combined with the sharp angulation of the tendon around the tubercle can compromise the extensor pollicis longus and predispose it to attritional rupture, analogous to the rupture seen after distal radius fracture.","additionalDifferentials":["Proximal intersection syndrome","De Quervain tenosynovitis","Extensor pollicis longus tenosynovitis or impending rupture","Dorsal wrist ganglion","Scapholunate pathology or dorsal wrist impingement","Wartenberg syndrome (superficial radial nerve irritation)","Second carpometacarpal or carpal boss pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound demonstrating peritendinous fluid at the extensor pollicis longus crossover","MRI showing oedema and tenosynovitis at the crossover distal to Lister's tubercle","Plain radiographs to exclude bony causes and prior distal radius fracture","Clinical Finkelstein test to help differentiate de Quervain tenosynovitis","Dynamic assessment for crepitus on wrist and thumb motion","Diagnostic local anaesthetic infiltration"],"redFlags":["Signs of impending or actual extensor pollicis longus rupture, such as loss of thumb retropulsion","History of distal radius fracture with new dorsal wrist pain","Failure to respond to appropriate conservative care","Rapidly progressive swelling suggesting infection or inflammatory arthropathy"],"prognosis":"The condition is usually self-limiting and responds well to conservative measures, though delayed recognition risks extensor pollicis longus attrition and rupture.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with the more common proximal intersection syndrome or de Quervain tenosynovitis.","Injecting corticosteroid carelessly and risking extensor pollicis longus rupture.","Missing an impending tendon rupture, especially after distal radius fracture.","Mislocalising the pathology proximally in the forearm."],"management":["Modify activity with relative rest from repetitive wrist and thumb motion","Apply wrist and thumb splinting to offload the extensor pollicis longus at Lister's tubercle","Use ice and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory measures for peritendinous inflammation","Give any corticosteroid injection cautiously because of the risk of attritional extensor pollicis longus rupture","Monitor vigilantly for impending or actual extensor pollicis longus rupture with prompt hand-surgery referral","Undertake surgical decompression and tenosynovectomy, with tendon transfer or reconstruction if rupture occurs","Provide physiotherapy and ergonomic or technique correction before return to activity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","Wheeless' Textbook of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"interstitial_cystitis":{"name":"Interstitial Cystitis / Bladder Pain Syndrome","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["interstitial cystitis","bladder pain syndrome","IC/BPS","painful bladder syndrome","Hunner lesion cystitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Interstitial cystitis / bladder pain syndrome is a chronic condition of bladder or pelvic pain perceived to relate to the bladder, accompanied by urinary urgency and frequency, in the absence of infection or other identifiable cause","Pain characteristically worsens as the bladder fills and is relieved by voiding, a pattern that helps distinguish it from overactive bladder","It is far more common in women and frequently coexists with other chronic pain and functional conditions such as irritable bowel syndrome, fibromyalgia, and chronic pelvic pain","Diagnosis is essentially clinical and one of exclusion, requiring that infection, malignancy, stones, and other bladder pathology be ruled out","Cystoscopy may be normal or may reveal Hunner lesions, whose presence defines a distinct phenotype that responds to lesion-directed treatment","Management is stepwise, beginning with education, dietary trigger avoidance, stress and pain management, and pelvic floor physical therapy","Oral and intravesical therapies form later steps, and Hunner lesions are specifically treated by fulguration or resection","Refractory disease may be considered for neuromodulation or, rarely, major reconstructive surgery, reserved for carefully selected severe cases","New haematuria, especially in older patients or smokers, must trigger evaluation for bladder cancer rather than being ascribed to the syndrome"],"pathophysiology":"The mechanism is incompletely understood and probably multifactorial. A leading hypothesis is a defective bladder urothelial glycosaminoglycan (GAG) layer, which normally protects the underlying tissue; increased permeability allows urinary solutes such as potassium to penetrate the urothelium and irritate sensory nerves and detrusor muscle. Mast cell activation, neurogenic inflammation, and up-regulation of sensory pathways contribute to chronic pain and central sensitisation. In the Hunner lesion subtype there is a demonstrable inflammatory bladder wall change, whereas non-lesion disease is dominated by neuropathic and functional pain mechanisms, explaining the frequent overlap with other chronic pain disorders.","additionalDifferentials":["Urinary tract infection","Overactive bladder","Bladder carcinoma, including carcinoma in situ","Bladder or ureteric calculi","Endometriosis and other gynaecological pelvic pain","Chronic prostatitis / chronic pelvic pain syndrome in men","Radiation or chemical cystitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and urine culture to exclude infection","Urine cytology and cystoscopy where malignancy is a concern, particularly with haematuria","Bladder diary documenting frequency, voided volumes, and symptom pattern","Validated symptom and pain questionnaires to assess severity and monitor response","Cystoscopy, with or without hydrodistension, to identify Hunner lesions","Imaging to exclude stones or other structural pathology when indicated"],"redFlags":["Visible or microscopic haematuria requiring exclusion of bladder cancer","New or changing symptoms in an older patient or smoker","Persistent sterile pyuria warranting exclusion of tuberculosis or malignancy","Abnormal urine cytology","Rapidly worsening or atypical pain suggesting an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The condition is chronic and typically follows a relapsing and remitting course; it is rarely progressive but can significantly impair quality of life. Many patients gain meaningful symptom control with a multimodal, stepwise approach, and the Hunner lesion subtype often responds well to lesion-directed therapy. A minority with severe refractory disease may ultimately require advanced interventions.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating for infection despite negative cultures","Missing bladder cancer by attributing haematuria to the syndrome","Failing to identify and treat Hunner lesions, which need specific therapy","Escalating to invasive surgery before conservative and behavioural measures are exhausted","Overlooking coexisting pelvic floor dysfunction and other chronic pain conditions"],"management":["Begin with conservative care: patient education, dietary trigger avoidance, fluid and stress management and bladder training","Refer for pelvic floor physical therapy directed at associated myofascial dysfunction, avoiding pelvic floor strengthening","Offer oral therapy such as amitriptyline, an antihistamine (hydroxyzine) or pentosan polysulfate alongside multimodal pain management","Escalate to intravesical instillations such as dimethyl sulfoxide, heparin or local anaesthetic for persistent symptoms","Perform cystoscopy with hydrodistension and treat Hunner lesions with fulguration, resection or intralesional corticosteroid","Reserve sacral neuromodulation and, in refractory disease, reconstructive surgery such as augmentation cystoplasty or urinary diversion","Manage flares and coexisting irritable bowel syndrome, fibromyalgia and chronic pelvic pain with multidisciplinary input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"interstitial_cystitis_bladder_pain":{"name":"Interstitial cystitis / bladder pain syndrome","system":"Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IC/BPS","Bladder pain syndrome","Painful bladder syndrome","Hunner lesion disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Urology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Chronic pelvic pain, pressure or discomfort perceived to relate to the bladder, accompanied by urinary urgency or frequency, in the absence of infection or another identifiable cause.","Predominantly affects women and frequently coexists with other central sensitivity and functional syndromes such as fibromyalgia, irritable bowel syndrome and vulvodynia.","Pain characteristically worsens as the bladder fills and is transiently relieved by voiding.","Two clinically important phenotypes exist: Hunner lesion (classic, ulcerative) disease and non-Hunner disease, which require different treatment strategies.","Diagnosis is largely clinical and one of exclusion; cystoscopy is used mainly to identify Hunner lesions and to exclude other pathology.","Management is stepwise and multimodal, spanning behavioural and dietary measures, oral agents, intravesical therapy, hydrodistension, lesion-directed treatment, neuromodulation and, rarely, reconstructive surgery.","Hunner lesions can be treated directly with fulguration or intralesional steroid, often giving marked symptom relief.","A defective glycosaminoglycan layer and mast cell activation are central to the leading pathophysiological model."],"pathophysiology":"The pathogenesis is multifactorial and incompletely understood. A leading model centres on a defective urothelial glycosaminoglycan layer and impaired urothelial barrier, allowing urinary solutes such as potassium to penetrate the urothelium, depolarise sensory afferents and trigger mast cell activation. Mast cell degranulation releases histamine and other mediators, producing neurogenic inflammation, upregulation of sensory nerves and, over time, central sensitisation that amplifies pain and accounts for the overlap with other chronic pain syndromes. An antiproliferative factor produced by the urothelium is thought to impair epithelial repair and perpetuate barrier dysfunction. Hunner lesion disease appears to represent a distinct inflammatory phenotype, with a dense infiltrate of lymphocytes and plasma cells and features suggesting an autoimmune contribution, whereas non-Hunner disease shows less pronounced inflammatory change.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial cystitis, including infection with fastidious organisms","Carcinoma in situ of the bladder","Overactive bladder","Endometriosis","Urethral diverticulum","Radiation or chemical (for example cyclophosphamide) cystitis","Pelvic floor dysfunction and vulvodynia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and urine culture to exclude infection","Urine cytology to exclude malignancy, particularly carcinoma in situ","Cystoscopy, often with hydrodistension under anaesthesia, to identify Hunner lesions and glomerulations","Bladder biopsy where malignancy or the degree of inflammation needs assessment","Frequency-volume (voiding) diary","Validated symptom and quality-of-life questionnaires","Post-void residual measurement and pelvic ultrasound"],"redFlags":["Visible haematuria, which mandates exclusion of bladder cancer and carcinoma in situ","New irritative symptoms in an older patient or a smoker, raising malignancy risk","Sterile pyuria, prompting consideration of tuberculosis or malignancy","A positive urine culture indicating infection rather than the syndrome","Rapidly progressive symptoms or a palpable pelvic mass"],"prognosis":"IC/BPS is a chronic, relapsing and remitting condition in which symptom control rather than cure is the usual goal, and it can substantially impair quality of life. Hunner lesion disease often responds well to lesion-directed therapy, though symptoms may recur.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating culture-negative urinary tract infections with antibiotics","Missing Hunner lesions and therefore withholding phenotype-specific therapy","Overlooking bladder carcinoma in situ, which can mimic the syndrome","Reliance on the discredited potassium sensitivity test","Neglecting pelvic floor dysfunction and central pain contributors"],"management":["Start with behavioural and dietary measures: trigger-food avoidance, fluid management, bladder training and stress reduction","Provide pelvic floor physical therapy for associated pelvic floor muscle tenderness","Add oral agents such as amitriptyline, hydroxyzine or pentosan polysulfate within a multimodal analgesic approach","Use intravesical instillation therapy with dimethyl sulfoxide, heparin or local anaesthetic when oral treatment is insufficient","Undertake cystoscopic hydrodistension and lesion-directed fulguration, resection or intralesional corticosteroid for the Hunner-lesion phenotype","Reserve neuromodulation and reconstructive or diversion surgery for refractory disease","Coordinate care addressing coexisting fibromyalgia, irritable bowel syndrome and vulvodynia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Urology society guidance on interstitial cystitis and bladder pain syndrome","European urology guidance on chronic pelvic pain","Urology and urogynaecology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"interstitial_cystitis_bladder_pain_syndrome":{"name":"Interstitial cystitis / bladder pain syndrome","system":"Renal / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bladder pain syndrome","painful bladder syndrome","IC/BPS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic condition of persistent pelvic or bladder pain, pressure, or discomfort perceived to relate to the bladder, accompanied by urinary urgency and frequency, in the absence of infection or other identifiable cause","It is essentially a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring symptoms to be present for a sustained period (typically several weeks) with negative cultures and exclusion of confusable diseases","Predominantly affects women and is frequently associated with other chronic pain and functional disorders such as fibromyalgia, irritable bowel syndrome, and chronic fatigue","Cystoscopy with hydrodistension may reveal Hunner lesions (inflamed mucosal patches) in a minority, defining a distinct ulcerative phenotype, or glomerulations, which are non-specific","Pain characteristically worsens with bladder filling and is relieved, at least transiently, by voiding","Symptoms often fluctuate with flares, and certain foods, stress, or menstruation may act as triggers","Management is multimodal and stepwise, including patient education, dietary and behavioural measures, physical therapy for pelvic floor dysfunction, oral and intravesical treatments, and, for refractory Hunner lesions, targeted endoscopic therapy, with major surgery a last resort"],"pathophysiology":"The cause of interstitial cystitis / bladder pain syndrome is not fully understood and is probably multifactorial. A leading hypothesis is that a defective urothelial glycosaminoglycan (GAG) mucous layer allows urinary solutes such as potassium to penetrate the bladder wall, provoking sensory nerve activation, mast cell recruitment, and neurogenic inflammation. Chronic activation leads to central and peripheral sensitisation, upregulated bladder afferents, and, in some patients, mucosal inflammation with Hunner lesions. Overlap with other chronic pain syndromes suggests that abnormal pain processing and visceral hypersensitivity contribute substantially, so the bladder may be one manifestation of a wider sensory disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent bacterial cystitis or other urinary tract infection","Overactive bladder without pain","Carcinoma in situ of the bladder","Endometriosis and other gynaecological pelvic pain","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome / prostatitis in men","Bladder outlet obstruction or neurogenic bladder","Urethral diverticulum or pelvic floor dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and culture to exclude infection","Urine cytology and cystoscopy to exclude malignancy, particularly carcinoma in situ","Cystoscopy with hydrodistension to identify Hunner lesions or glomerulations","Symptom and pain questionnaires and a voiding diary to characterise severity","Pelvic examination and assessment of pelvic floor muscle tenderness","Gynaecological evaluation and imaging where pelvic pathology is suspected"],"redFlags":["Macroscopic haematuria or abnormal cytology suggesting bladder malignancy","Persistent sterile pyuria warranting exclusion of tuberculosis or carcinoma in situ","New neurological symptoms or a suspected neurogenic bladder","Escalating opioid use and worsening disability"],"prognosis":"The condition is chronic and often relapsing rather than curable; many patients achieve meaningful symptom control with a stepwise multimodal approach, though the Hunner-lesion phenotype behaves differently and a minority with refractory disease may ultimately require major reconstructive or ablative surgery.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing the syndrome without excluding infection, carcinoma in situ, and gynaecological causes","Treating repeatedly for presumed infection despite negative cultures","Overlooking pelvic floor dysfunction, which is a key and treatable contributor","Escalating to irreversible surgery before exhausting conservative and intravesical options"],"management":["Begin with education, dietary trigger avoidance, fluid moderation, bladder training and stress management","Offer pelvic floor physiotherapy for coexisting pelvic floor dysfunction","Escalate to oral agents such as amitriptyline, an antihistamine or pentosan polysulfate with structured pain management","Add intravesical therapy with dimethyl sulfoxide, heparin or local anaesthetic for inadequate response","Perform cystoscopy with hydrodistension, reserving fulguration, resection or intralesional corticosteroid for identified Hunner lesions","Reserve sacral neuromodulation and, rarely, augmentation cystoplasty or urinary diversion for refractory cases","Manage associated chronic pain and functional disorders across specialties, with a flare-management plan"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","European Association of Urology Guidelines on Chronic Pelvic Pain"],"crossLinks":[]},"interstitial_granulomatous_dermatitis":{"name":"Interstitial granulomatous dermatitis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IGD","interstitial granulomatous dermatitis with arthritis","Ackerman syndrome","palisaded neutrophilic and granulomatous dermatitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A reactive granulomatous dermatosis presenting as erythematous to violaceous plaques, papules or annular lesions, often on the trunk, flanks, proximal limbs and axillae","The classic but uncommon rope sign describes linear, cord-like indurated bands on the lateral trunk","It is strongly associated with autoimmune and connective tissue disease, particularly rheumatoid arthritis and systemic lupus erythematosus","Arthritis or arthralgia frequently accompanies or precedes the skin eruption, giving rise to the term interstitial granulomatous dermatitis with arthritis","A range of drugs, notably certain antihypertensives, lipid-lowering agents and biologic tumour necrosis factor inhibitors, can provoke a very similar interstitial granulomatous drug reaction","Histology shows an interstitial and palisaded histiocytic infiltrate between collagen bundles with piecemeal collagen degeneration and variable neutrophils, overlapping with palisaded neutrophilic and granulomatous dermatitis","The eruption may wax and wane with activity of the underlying systemic disease","Management targets any culprit drug and the associated systemic disorder, using topical or systemic corticosteroids and immunomodulatory agents for symptomatic skin disease"],"pathophysiology":"Interstitial granulomatous dermatitis is considered a reactive inflammatory pattern rather than a specific disease. It is thought to reflect immune-complex deposition within small dermal vessels and the interstitium, triggering a histiocytic response to altered dermal collagen. Histiocytes accumulate between collagen bundles and palisade around foci of degenerate collagen, sometimes with admixed neutrophils and nuclear dust. This process is frequently linked to circulating immune complexes in autoimmune connective tissue disease, and an identical pattern can be induced by certain drugs, supporting the concept of a shared reactive mechanism to a range of systemic immunological triggers.","additionalDifferentials":["Granuloma annulare, especially the interstitial variant","Palisaded neutrophilic and granulomatous dermatitis","Interstitial granulomatous drug reaction","Necrobiosis lipoidica and rheumatoid nodules","Cutaneous sarcoidosis","Urticarial or figurate erythemas"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing an interstitial and palisaded histiocytic infiltrate with collagen degeneration","Autoimmune serology including antinuclear antibodies, rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies","Assessment for arthritis and referral for rheumatological evaluation","Detailed medication history to identify a possible drug trigger","Screening investigations for associated systemic disease guided by symptoms","Inflammatory markers to gauge disease activity"],"redFlags":["Newly identified underlying connective tissue disease requiring systemic management","A temporally related culprit drug that should be reviewed and withdrawn","Systemic symptoms suggesting active or progressive autoimmune disease","Atypical features prompting exclusion of infection or malignancy on biopsy"],"prognosis":"The skin lesions often fluctuate with the activity of the associated disease and may resolve when a culprit drug is withdrawn or the underlying disorder is controlled, though the eruption can be persistent or recurrent; the overall outlook is largely determined by the associated systemic condition.","pitfalls":["Treating the skin in isolation and failing to investigate for an underlying autoimmune disorder","Overlooking a drug trigger, so that the eruption persists despite topical therapy","Confusing it histologically with granuloma annulare and missing the systemic associations","Not recognising the rope sign as a clue to the diagnosis"],"management":["Identify and withdraw any culprit drug, notably antihypertensives, lipid-lowering agents or tumour necrosis factor inhibitors implicated as a trigger","Treat the associated connective tissue disease, particularly rheumatoid arthritis or systemic lupus erythematosus, as the principal determinant of skin activity","Apply topical or intralesional corticosteroids for localised cutaneous lesions","Use systemic therapy such as corticosteroids, hydroxychloroquine or other immunomodulators for widespread or persistent eruption","Refer to rheumatology for evaluation and management of accompanying arthritis","Monitor cutaneous activity as a marker of underlying disease control and for recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Weedon's Skin Pathology"],"crossLinks":[]},"interstitial_keratitis":{"name":"Interstitial keratitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IK","non-ulcerative stromal keratitis","syphilitic interstitial keratitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology / Cornea by Krachmer","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A non-ulcerative inflammation of the corneal stroma with intact epithelium, usually representing an immune response to a systemic infection rather than direct corneal infection","Congenital syphilis is the classic cause, typically bilateral and part of the Hutchinson triad (interstitial keratitis, notched incisors, and sensorineural deafness)","Active disease shows stromal oedema, cellular infiltration, and deep stromal vascularisation; when vessels lose their blood column they leave residual ghost vessels","Cogan syndrome links interstitial keratitis with vestibuloauditory dysfunction and may herald a systemic vasculitis","Other infective associations include tuberculosis, Lyme disease, leprosy, and herpes simplex or zoster (which more often cause unilateral disease)","Chronic sequelae include stromal scarring, thinning, and lipid keratopathy, with vision reduced by scarring and irregular astigmatism","Management combines treatment of the underlying systemic cause with topical corticosteroids to control stromal inflammation, and later keratoplasty for dense scarring","Identifying and treating the systemic cause is essential, so serological and systemic workup should accompany ocular treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Interstitial keratitis is an inflammation of the corneal stroma in which the epithelium and endothelium are typically spared and there is no primary ulceration. It usually reflects an immune (often antigen-driven or hypersensitivity) reaction to a systemic infection such as syphilis, tuberculosis, Lyme disease, or a viral agent, rather than active replication of organisms within the cornea. Inflammatory cells infiltrate the stroma, causing oedema and the ingrowth of deep stromal vessels. When inflammation subsides the vessels may empty, leaving ghost vessels, and the stroma is left scarred and sometimes thinned or laden with lipid. The resulting opacity and irregular astigmatism impair vision.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpetic stromal (disciform) keratitis","Bacterial or fungal keratitis with stromal infiltrate","Acanthamoeba keratitis","Corneal graft rejection with stromal vascularisation","Peripheral ulcerative keratitis from systemic vasculitis","Stromal deposits from corneal dystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination documenting stromal oedema, infiltrate, and deep or ghost vessels","Syphilis serology (treponemal and non-treponemal tests)","Tuberculosis testing (interferon-gamma release assay or tuberculin skin test and chest imaging)","Lyme serology and other targeted infective workup based on exposure history","Audiovestibular assessment when Cogan syndrome is suspected","Systemic inflammatory and vasculitis screen when an autoimmune cause is considered"],"redFlags":["Associated vestibuloauditory symptoms suggesting Cogan syndrome and possible systemic vasculitis","Evidence of active systemic infection such as syphilis or tuberculosis requiring urgent treatment","Progressive stromal thinning with perforation risk","Dense central scarring causing significant visual loss"],"prognosis":"Active inflammation usually settles with treatment of the underlying cause and topical corticosteroids, but residual stromal scarring, ghost vessels, and irregular astigmatism can leave lasting visual impairment. Dense central scarring may ultimately require keratoplasty.","pitfalls":["Treating the cornea without identifying and treating the underlying systemic infection","Missing Cogan syndrome and the risk of systemic vasculitis when hearing symptoms coexist","Confusing immune stromal inflammation with active infective keratitis","Overlooking congenital syphilis in a child with bilateral disease and systemic stigmata"],"management":["Treat the underlying systemic cause, for example penicillin for syphilis or standard antitubercular therapy, rather than the cornea alone","Use topical corticosteroids to suppress stromal inflammation and limit scarring and deep vascularisation","Add a cycloplegic agent for pain relief and to prevent posterior synechiae during active inflammation","Institute systemic immunosuppression urgently for Cogan syndrome to preserve hearing","Arrange audiovestibular and systemic evaluation for associated disease such as Cogan syndrome or vasculitis","Follow up long term for residual stromal scarring, lipid keratopathy and irregular astigmatism, with keratoplasty for dense central scarring","Undertake partner notification and public health measures where an infective cause such as syphilis is confirmed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Cornea by Krachmer, Mannis and Holland"],"crossLinks":[]},"intestinal_atresia":{"name":"Intestinal atresia (jejunoileal/colonic)","system":"Paediatric surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["jejunal atresia","ileal atresia","jejunoileal atresia","colonic atresia","apple-peel atresia","Christmas-tree deformity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Ashcraft Pediatric Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Congenital interruption of the intestinal lumen producing neonatal obstruction, most commonly in the jejunum and ileum and less often in the colon","Jejunoileal atresia is largely attributed to a late intrauterine mesenteric vascular accident rather than a primary failure of recanalisation","The more distal the atresia, the greater the number of dilated loops and air-fluid levels; proximal jejunal atresia gives few dilated loops","Classified into types I (membrane), II (fibrous cord), IIIa (V-shaped mesenteric gap), IIIb (apple-peel/Christmas-tree), and IV (multiple atresias)","The apple-peel (type IIIb) variant features a proximal atresia with the distal bowel coiled around a single perfusing vessel and carries higher morbidity","Bilious vomiting, abdominal distension, and failure to pass meconium in a neonate should prompt urgent evaluation for atresia","Treatment is surgical resection of the atretic segment with primary anastomosis, often tapering the dilated proximal bowel to restore peristalsis"],"pathophysiology":"Jejunoileal atresia is thought to result from a localised intrauterine mesenteric vascular insult (for example volvulus, intussusception, or a strangulated segment) that devitalises a portion of already-formed bowel, which is then resorbed, leaving an atretic gap and a variable mesenteric defect. This contrasts with duodenal atresia, which reflects failed recanalisation of the solid embryonic cord. The proximal bowel becomes dilated, hypertrophied, and dysmotile, while the distal bowel is small and unused. Colonic atresia is rarer and follows a similar vascular mechanism. Extensive or apple-peel patterns may leave a foreshortened bowel and predispose to short-bowel syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Meconium ileus","Malrotation with midgut volvulus","Hirschsprung disease","Duodenal atresia or stenosis","Meconium plug syndrome or small left colon","Incarcerated inguinal hernia causing obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal radiograph to count dilated loops and gauge the level of obstruction","Water-soluble contrast enema to assess a microcolon and exclude colonic atresia or meconium ileus","Upper gastrointestinal contrast study when malrotation cannot be excluded","Prenatal ultrasound review for dilated bowel loops and polyhydramnios","Screening for cystic fibrosis given overlap with meconium ileus","Assessment of bowel length at operation to anticipate short-bowel risk"],"redFlags":["Bilious vomiting in a neonate, which must be treated as a surgical emergency until proven otherwise","Abdominal tenderness, discoloration, or distension suggesting ischaemia or perforation","Signs of associated volvulus compromising bowel viability","Extensive atresia threatening inadequate residual bowel length","Rapid deterioration with sepsis or peritonitis"],"prognosis":"Isolated single atresias repaired promptly have excellent outcomes, whereas apple-peel and multiple atresias, or those complicated by extensive bowel loss, may lead to short-bowel syndrome and prolonged parenteral nutrition dependence. Overall survival with modern neonatal surgical and nutritional care is high.","pitfalls":["Delaying laparotomy in a neonate with bilious vomiting while awaiting non-urgent tests","Overlooking a second, more distal atresia at operation (multiple atresias)","Anastomosing grossly dilated proximal bowel without tapering, risking functional obstruction","Failing to measure remaining bowel length and anticipate nutritional needs","Attributing distal obstruction solely to atresia and missing coexisting cystic fibrosis"],"management":["Stabilise preoperatively with nasogastric decompression, intravenous fluid resuscitation and correction of electrolyte disturbance","Resect the atretic segment with primary anastomosis, tapering or plicating dilated proximal bowel where needed","Assess and preserve bowel length at operation to anticipate short-bowel risk","Provide postoperative parenteral nutrition with graded introduction of enteral feeds as motility returns","Screen for cystic fibrosis and exclude coexisting malrotation given the overlapping presentations","Monitor nutrition long term and manage short-bowel syndrome in extensive, apple-peel or multiple atresias","Coordinate paediatric surgical, neonatal and dietetic multidisciplinary care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Ashcraft's Pediatric Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"intestinal_duplication_cyst":{"name":"intestinal duplication cyst","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["enteric duplication cyst","alimentary tract duplication"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ashcraft's Pediatric Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Duplications are spherical (cystic, commoner) or tubular structures that arise on the mesenteric border of the native bowel and share a common muscular wall and blood supply with it.","The ileum is the single most frequent site, but duplications can occur anywhere from the oral cavity to the anus; most are non-communicating with the true lumen.","The epithelial lining is typically alimentary mucosa, and ectopic gastric or pancreatic tissue is common, predisposing to peptic ulceration and gastrointestinal bleeding.","Ultrasound shows the classic 'gut signature' or double-wall sign — an inner echogenic mucosa with an outer hypoechoic muscular rim — which strongly suggests the diagnosis.","Presentation is usually in infancy or early childhood with a palpable mass, obstruction, or bleeding, but incidental adult diagnosis is well recognised.","Complications include bowel obstruction, volvulus, intussusception (the cyst acting as a lead point), and pain from distension or intracystic haemorrhage.","Thoracic and thoraco-abdominal (oesophageal) duplications are frequently associated with vertebral anomalies and a persistent connection to the spinal canal, in keeping with the split-notochord theory.","A technetium-99m pertechnetate scan can localise ectopic gastric mucosa when bleeding is the presenting feature."],"pathophysiology":"Intestinal duplications are congenital malformations whose precise embryogenesis is uncertain; leading theories include aberrant recanalisation of the gut lumen, persistence of embryonic diverticula, partial twinning, and the split-notochord hypothesis for the thoracic and thoraco-abdominal forms associated with vertebral defects. The lesion invariably lies on the mesenteric aspect of the adjacent alimentary tract, shares a common muscular coat and mesenteric blood supply, and is lined by gastrointestinal-type epithelium. Ectopic gastric mucosa, present in a substantial minority, secretes acid that ulcerates the lining or adjacent bowel and produces haemorrhage or perforation; ectopic pancreatic tissue may also occur. Progressive accumulation of secretions within a non-communicating cyst causes mass effect, obstruction, or a lead point for intussusception or volvulus.","additionalDifferentials":["mesenteric or omental cyst","choledochal cyst","cystic lymphangioma","ovarian cyst","Meckel diverticulum","cystic teratoma","pancreatic pseudocyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["abdominal ultrasound to demonstrate the gut-signature double-wall sign","contrast-enhanced CT to define anatomy and relationship to bowel","MRI, particularly for thoracic and spinal involvement","technetium-99m pertechnetate scintigraphy for ectopic gastric mucosa","contrast luminal studies to assess communication","spinal imaging when a thoracic duplication is identified","histopathology of the resected specimen"],"redFlags":["gastrointestinal haemorrhage suggesting ectopic gastric mucosa","features of bowel obstruction, volvulus, or intussusception","acute abdomen from perforation or intracystic haemorrhage","respiratory compromise from a thoracic duplication","new solid or nodular components raising concern for rare malignant change in adults"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent following complete surgical excision, which is curative. Malignant transformation is rare and largely confined to lesions presenting in adulthood.","pitfalls":["mistaking the cyst for another abdominal cystic lesion and overlooking its shared wall with native bowel","failing to resect adjacent bowel when the shared blood supply precludes simple enucleation, leading to incomplete removal","overlooking ectopic gastric mucosa as the source of occult bleeding","missing an associated spinal or vertebral anomaly in thoracic duplications","attempting enucleation of a long tubular duplication and leaving retained mucosa"],"management":["Undertake complete surgical excision, which is curative, usually resecting the shared-wall bowel segment with anastomosis","Resuscitate and correct anaemia preoperatively where bleeding or obstruction has occurred","Address ectopic gastric mucosa as the source of peptic ulceration and haemorrhage, using acid suppression as a temporising measure","Treat acute complications such as obstruction, volvulus or intussusception with the cyst as lead point by urgent surgery","Obtain spinal imaging and involve neurosurgery where a thoracic duplication is associated with vertebral anomalies","Provide paediatric surgical follow-up after resection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ashcraft's Pediatric Surgery","Coran, Pediatric Surgery","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"intestinal_lymphangiectasia":{"name":"Intestinal lymphangiectasia","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Waldmann disease","primary intestinal lymphangiectasia","protein-losing enteropathy from lymphangiectasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with peripheral oedema, sometimes effusions or chylous ascites, together with diarrhoea and features of malabsorption","Characteristic laboratory findings are hypoalbuminaemia, low immunoglobulins and lymphopenia caused by enteric loss of protein and lymph","Fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies and hypocalcaemia may occur because of impaired fat and lymphatic absorption","Primary disease is a congenital malformation of intestinal lymphatics, while secondary disease results from any process obstructing lymphatic drainage","Endoscopy may show scattered white villous tips or a snow-flake mucosal appearance, and biopsy shows dilated mucosal and submucosal lymphatics","Diagnosis is supported by demonstrating enteric protein loss, for example with faecal alpha-1-antitrypsin clearance","Management is a low-fat diet enriched with medium-chain triglycerides that bypass the lymphatics, with treatment of any underlying secondary cause"],"pathophysiology":"Intestinal lymphangiectasia is characterised by dilatation of the lymphatic channels of the small-bowel mucosa and submucosa, which may be a primary congenital anomaly or secondary to obstruction of lymphatic flow by cardiac disease, malignancy, infection or fibrosis. The dilated, incompetent lymphatics leak or rupture into the bowel lumen, causing loss of protein-rich lymph containing albumin, immunoglobulins and lymphocytes. This produces a protein-losing enteropathy with hypoalbuminaemia and consequent oedema, hypogammaglobulinaemia with susceptibility to infection, and lymphopenia, while impaired lymphatic transport of long-chain fats causes fat malabsorption.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of protein-losing enteropathy such as Menetrier disease","Nephrotic syndrome","Congestive cardiac failure and constrictive pericarditis","Cirrhosis with hypoalbuminaemia","Coeliac disease and other malabsorptive enteropathies","Inflammatory bowel disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum albumin, immunoglobulins and lymphocyte count","Faecal alpha-1-antitrypsin clearance to confirm enteric protein loss","Endoscopy with small-bowel biopsy showing dilated lymphatics","Cross-sectional imaging and capsule endoscopy to define extent and secondary causes","Assessment of fat-soluble vitamins and calcium"],"redFlags":["Severe hypoalbuminaemia with anasarca and serous effusions","Recurrent or serious infection from immunoglobulin and lymphocyte depletion","Chylous ascites or chylothorax","Features suggesting an underlying malignancy or cardiac cause of secondary disease"],"prognosis":"Primary disease is usually controlled but not cured by lifelong dietary modification; secondary disease may resolve if the underlying cause is treated, and prognosis then depends largely on that condition.","pitfalls":["Attributing hypoalbuminaemia to renal or hepatic causes without considering enteric protein loss","Missing patchy lymphangiectasia when only a single biopsy site is sampled","Overlooking a secondary cause such as cardiac disease or malignancy","Failing to substitute medium-chain triglycerides and monitor fat-soluble vitamins"],"management":["Institute a lifelong low-fat, high-protein diet with medium-chain triglyceride supplementation to bypass the obstructed lymphatics","Supplement fat-soluble vitamins and calcium to correct malabsorption","Treat the underlying cause in secondary disease, such as cardiac, malignant or infiltrative lymphatic obstruction","Support the protein-losing enteropathy with albumin for symptomatic hypoalbuminaemia and management of oedema and effusions","Consider a somatostatin analogue or antiplasmin agent in refractory disease and surgical resection of localised segmental involvement","Monitor albumin, immunoglobulins and lymphocyte count, with vigilance for infection given hypogammaglobulinaemia and lymphopenia","Provide dietetic and gastroenterology multidisciplinary follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Yamada's Textbook of Gastroenterology"],"crossLinks":[]},"intestinal_malrotation":{"name":"Intestinal malrotation","system":"Paediatric surgery / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["malrotation","midgut malrotation","malrotation with volvulus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Bailey & Love (paediatric surgery)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Arises from incomplete rotation and fixation of the midgut, leaving a narrow mesenteric base that predisposes to twisting (volvulus)","The classic presentation is a previously well neonate who develops sudden bilious vomiting, which must be treated as a surgical emergency until proven otherwise","Bilious vomiting in an infant should trigger urgent imaging because midgut volvulus can infarct the entire small bowel within hours","The upper gastrointestinal contrast study is the key investigation, showing an abnormally positioned duodenojejunal junction and a corkscrew or spiral appearance if volvulus is present","Ultrasound may reveal an inverted relationship of the superior mesenteric artery and vein or a whirlpool sign of twisted mesentery","Abnormal peritoneal (Ladd) bands can cross and obstruct the duodenum even without volvulus, producing intermittent obstructive symptoms","The definitive operation is the Ladd procedure: untwisting the volvulus, dividing Ladd bands, broadening the mesenteric base, and placing the bowel in a non-rotated position, usually with appendicectomy","Some children present later in life with intermittent, vague abdominal pain or feeding difficulty rather than acute obstruction"],"pathophysiology":"During normal development the midgut herniates, rotates counterclockwise around the superior mesenteric artery, and returns to the abdomen with broad mesenteric fixation. In malrotation this process is incomplete, so the caecum sits abnormally and the mesenteric root is short and narrow. This narrow pedicle allows the midgut to twist around the superior mesenteric vessels, compromising blood supply and causing ischaemia, while abnormal fibrous bands may simultaneously compress the duodenum and produce obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Duodenal atresia or stenosis","Jejunoileal atresia","Necrotising enterocolitis","Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis (non-bilious vomiting)","Incarcerated hernia causing obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper gastrointestinal contrast series to locate the duodenojejunal junction","Abdominal ultrasound to assess superior mesenteric vessel orientation and look for a whirlpool sign","Plain abdominal radiograph, which may show a paucity of distal gas or a double-bubble pattern","Blood gas and lactate to detect metabolic acidosis suggesting bowel ischaemia","Baseline bloods and cross-match in preparation for emergency laparotomy"],"redFlags":["Bilious vomiting in a neonate or infant, which mandates emergency evaluation for midgut volvulus","Abdominal distension, tenderness, or rectal bleeding suggesting established bowel ischaemia","Rapidly worsening metabolic acidosis or haemodynamic instability indicating strangulated midgut requiring immediate laparotomy","Any delay in diagnosis, as the whole midgut can become necrotic within a few hours of volvulus"],"prognosis":"When volvulus is corrected before ischaemia sets in, outcomes are excellent with low recurrence after a Ladd procedure. Delayed presentation with extensive bowel infarction can necessitate massive resection, leading to short bowel syndrome and long-term nutritional dependence, and carries significant mortality.","pitfalls":["Dismissing bilious vomiting as feed intolerance or reflux and delaying imaging","Assuming a normal plain radiograph excludes malrotation or volvulus","Failing to arrange emergency surgery once volvulus is suspected while awaiting further tests","Overlooking older children with intermittent symptoms in whom malrotation is still possible"],"management":["Perform emergency laparotomy with the Ladd procedure, comprising derotation of volvulus, division of Ladd bands, appendicectomy and bowel repositioning, for confirmed or suspected midgut volvulus","Resuscitate immediately with intravenous fluids, nasogastric decompression and correction of metabolic acidosis","Treat bilious vomiting in a neonate as a surgical emergency with urgent upper gastrointestinal contrast imaging","Assess bowel viability at operation, resecting infarcted segments and planning a second-look laparotomy where needed","Provide long-term nutritional support and manage short-bowel syndrome after extensive resection","Arrange paediatric surgical follow-up, noting the low recurrence risk after a Ladd procedure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"intestinal_nematode_infections":{"name":"Intestinal Nematode (Roundworm) Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["soil-transmitted helminths","Ascaris","hookworm","Strongyloides"],"chapter":239,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1814","clinicalPearls":["More than one billion people worldwide are infected with one or more intestinal nematode species; they are most common where sanitation and fecal-waste disposal are inadequate, especially resource-limited tropical and subtropical regions, but are seen with increasing frequency among immigrants and refugees to developed countries (p.1814).","The major intestinal roundworms are Ascaris lumbricoides, the hookworms (Necator americanus, Ancylostoma duodenale, A. ceylanicum), Strongyloides stercoralis, Trichuris trichiura (whipworm), and Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm) (p.1815).","Ascaris, Trichuris, and the hookworms require a soil phase for development, whereas S. stercoralis and E. vermicularis can be transmitted directly from person to person (p.1814).","Because most helminths cannot fully complete their life cycle within the same human host, heavy adult-worm burdens require repeated exposure; clinical disease (versus asymptomatic/subclinical infection) generally develops only with repeated exposure and is typically related to infection intensity (p.1814).","Eosinophilia and elevated serum IgE are features of many helminth infections, the latter particularly when the life cycle involves tissue migration (Ascaris, Strongyloides, hookworms) (p.1814).","Ascaris larval migration through the lungs (~9-12 days after egg ingestion) can cause an irritating nonproductive cough, substernal discomfort, eosinophilia, and transient rounded pulmonary infiltrates - eosinophilic pneumonitis (Loffler's syndrome) (p.1815).","Heavy Ascaris infection can cause small-bowel obstruction by a bolus of entangled worms (more common in children with narrow lumens), sometimes complicated by perforation, intussusception, or volvulus; an adult worm migrating into the biliary tree can cause biliary colic, cholecystitis, cholangitis, pancreatitis, or rarely intrahepatic abscess (p.1815).","In highly endemic areas, intestinal and biliary ascariasis can rival acute appendicitis as a cause of surgical acute abdomen (p.1815).","The major consequence of chronic hookworm infection is iron deficiency; symptoms are minimal if iron stores are adequate, but undernourished individuals develop progressive iron-deficiency anemia and hypoproteinemia (fatigue, weakness, dyspnea). Hookworm disease requires the combination of heavy worm burden, prolonged duration, and inadequate iron intake (p.1816).","Hookworm larvae may cause a pruritic maculopapular rash (ground itch) at the skin penetration site; hypochromic microcytic anemia, sometimes with eosinophilia or hypoalbuminemia, is characteristic of hookworm disease (p.1816).","S. stercoralis is distinguished by its ability - unique among helminths except Capillaria - to complete its life cycle and replicate within a human host (autoinfection), so infection can persist for decades without further exogenous exposure (p.1816).","Larva currens, a rapidly advancing (up to ~10 cm/h) pruritic serpiginous skin eruption, is pathognomonic of strongyloidiasis; recurrent urticaria, often on the buttocks, is the most common cutaneous manifestation (p.1816).","Impaired cell-mediated immunity - especially high-dose glucocorticoid therapy, and less commonly other immunosuppressants or HTLV-1 infection - can trigger Strongyloides hyperinfection with massive larval numbers, leading to colitis/enteritis/malabsorption and disseminated disease (CNS, skin, peritoneum, liver, kidneys) (p.1817).","Disseminated strongyloidiasis is often complicated by gram-negative bacteremia, pneumonia, or meningitis from enteric flora translocating through disrupted mucosal barriers; eosinophilia is often ABSENT in severely infected patients (p.1817).","Even asymptomatic strongyloidiasis must be treated because of the potential for later hyperinfection and fatal dissemination; Strongyloides serology should be performed before starting glucocorticoids or solid-organ transplantation in patients with prior potential exposure, especially with eosinophilia (p.1817).","Most Trichuris (whipworm) infections are asymptomatic, but heavy infections may cause anemia and bloody/mucoid diarrhea resembling inflammatory bowel disease; massive infection in children can cause rectal prolapse from protracted tenesmus (p.1818).","Enterobius (pinworm) is more common in temperate climates than the tropics; perianal pruritus, worse at night when gravid females emerge, is the cardinal symptom, and eosinophilia is uncommon (p.1818)."],"pathophysiology":"Intestinal nematodes are nonsegmented roundworms ranging from ~1 mm to many centimeters. Ascaris and the hookworms have life cycles involving migration through the heart and lungs before maturing in the intestine (larvae penetrate alveoli, ascend the bronchial tree, are swallowed, and reach the small bowel). Ascaris adults live in the small-intestinal lumen and females are extraordinarily fecund (>200,000 eggs/day); eggs are highly environmentally resistant and become infective after maturation in warm, moist soil. Hookworm adults attach to the small-bowel mucosa using buccal teeth (Ancylostoma) or cutting plates (Necator), invade submucosa, and ingest blood and villous tissue, producing iron-deficiency anemia; filariform larvae penetrate skin. S. stercoralis filariform larvae penetrate skin or mucous membranes, migrate via blood to the lungs and then the small intestine, where parthenogenetic adult females embed in the mucosa; rhabditiform larvae can transform directly into filariform larvae that re-penetrate the colonic wall or perianal skin to drive ongoing autoinfection - which can amplify into hyperinfection and dissemination under immunosuppression. Trichuris adults embed their thin anterior portions in colonic/cecal mucosa with no pulmonary larval passage. Enterobius adults dwell in the cecum; gravid females migrate nocturnally to the perianal skin to deposit eggs, which become infective within hours and spread by hand-to-mouth (autoinfection from scratching) and retroinfection. (p.1814-1818)","additionalDifferentials":["Strongyloides midepigastric pain resembles peptic ulcer disease, except it is aggravated by food ingestion (p.1816)","Heavy trichuriasis with bloody/mucoid diarrhea may resemble inflammatory bowel disease (p.1818)","Intestinal/biliary ascariasis may present as a surgical acute abdomen mimicking acute appendicitis in endemic areas (p.1815)"],"infectionMimics":["Strongyloides hyperinfection-associated gram-negative sepsis, pneumonia, or meningitis can dominate or be mistaken for primary bacterial infection (p.1817)","Hookworm rhabditiform larvae in non-fresh stool must be differentiated from Strongyloides larvae (p.1816)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease (heavy trichuriasis) (p.1818)","Peptic ulcer disease (strongyloidiasis epigastric pain) (p.1816)","Acute appendicitis / surgical acute abdomen (intestinal or biliary ascariasis) (p.1815)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy for characteristic ova and parasites: Ascaris eggs (~65 x 45 um), hookworm eggs (~40 x 60 um, species indistinguishable by light microscopy), Trichuris lemon-shaped eggs (~50 x 20 um) (p.1815-1818)","Strongyloides: rhabditiform larvae (~250 um, short buccal cavity) on stool microscopy; single stool detects only ~one-third of uncomplicated cases, so serial examinations and agar plate method improve sensitivity; duodenojejunal aspirate/biopsy; ELISA serum antibody serology (sensitive for uncomplicated infection); in dissemination, filariform larvae in stool, sputum, bronchoalveolar lavage, or surgical drainage fluid (p.1817)","Enterobius: eggs are NOT found in stool - diagnose with morning cellulose acetate tape applied to the perianal region (eggs oval, ~55 x 25 um, flattened on one side) (p.1818)","Ascaris lung phase: larvae detectable in sputum or gastric aspirates before eggs appear in stool; chest imaging showing transient rounded infiltrates (Loffler's syndrome) (p.1815)","PCR of stool worm DNA (increasing use, mostly research/some clinical) for species-level diagnosis (p.1815-1818)","Abdominal imaging: plain film for masses of worms in obstruction; ultrasound and ERCP for pancreaticobiliary Ascaris (ERCP can also extract worms) (p.1815)","Hookworm disease bloodwork: hypochromic microcytic anemia, sometimes eosinophilia or hypoalbuminemia (p.1816)"],"redFlags":["Disseminated strongyloidiasis - particularly in patients with unsuspected infection who are taking glucocorticoids - has a HIGH mortality rate (p.1817)","Strongyloides hyperinfection with gram-negative sepsis, pneumonia, or meningitis from enteric flora translocation (p.1817)","Eosinophilia is often ABSENT in severe/disseminated strongyloidiasis, removing a reassuring marker (p.1817)","Complete intestinal obstruction with perforation, intussusception, or volvulus from a heavy Ascaris worm bolus (p.1815)","Biliary Ascaris causing cholangitis, pancreatitis, or intrahepatic abscess (p.1815)"],"prognosis":"Intestinal nematode infections are not usually fatal but contribute to malnutrition, impaired physical and cognitive development, and diminished work capacity, particularly in children with marginal nutritional status. Significant protective immunity does not appear to develop in humans. The major exception is strongyloidiasis: autoinfection allows decades-long persistence, and in immunocompromised hosts hyperinfection and dissemination can be fatal (high mortality, especially with unrecognized infection on glucocorticoids). (p.1814, p.1816-1817)","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for and treat asymptomatic strongyloidiasis before glucocorticoids or solid-organ transplantation - asymptomatic infection must still be treated because of the risk of later hyperinfection and fatal dissemination (p.1817)","Relying on eosinophilia to exclude strongyloidiasis - it is often absent in severe/disseminated disease (p.1817)","Using routine stool ova-and-parasite microscopy to diagnose pinworm - Enterobius eggs are not passed in feces; perianal cellulose acetate tape is required (p.1818)","Single stool examination for Strongyloides detects only about one-third of uncomplicated cases (p.1817)","Misidentifying hookworm rhabditiform larvae (in non-fresh stool) as Strongyloides (p.1816)"],"management":["Give a benzimidazole anthelmintic (albendazole or mebendazole) for Ascaris, hookworm, whipworm and pinworm infections","Treat Strongyloides with ivermectin, using prolonged or repeated courses for hyperinfection","Correct iron-deficiency anaemia with iron supplementation in heavy hookworm infection","Treat close household contacts and reinforce hygiene, handwashing and laundering for pinworm to prevent reinfection","Screen for and treat latent Strongyloides before immunosuppression to avert hyperinfection syndrome","Manage complications such as biliary or intestinal obstruction from Ascaris by endoscopic or surgical extraction","Reinforce sanitation, safe faecal disposal and mass deworming programmes to reduce reinfection","Provide nutritional rehabilitation, particularly in children with impaired growth and cognition"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity classification for intestinal nematode infections; severity correlates with infection intensity (worm burden) and host factors. Disease ranges from asymptomatic/subclinical light infection to clinical disease with heavy burden (e.g., Ascaris bowel/biliary obstruction; hookworm iron-deficiency anemia/hypoproteinemia; Trichuris dysentery and rectal prolapse). For Strongyloides, the spectrum is uncomplicated strongyloidiasis vs. hyperinfection (amplified autoinfection, larvae confined largely to the normal migration route) vs. disseminated strongyloidiasis (larvae in CNS, skin, peritoneum, liver, kidneys). (p.1814-1817)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1814"],"crossLinks":["iron_deficiency_anemia","bowel_obstruction"]},"intestinal_neuronal_dysplasia":{"name":"Intestinal neuronal dysplasia","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IND","intestinal neuronal dysplasia type B","colonic dysganglionosis","neuronal intestinal dysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An enteric neuromuscular disorder in which the myenteric and submucosal plexuses are dysplastic rather than absent, mimicking Hirschsprung disease","Presents in infancy or childhood with chronic constipation, abdominal distension, and features of functional obstruction","The commoner form (type B) is characterised by hyperplasia of the submucosal plexus with giant ganglia and ectopic ganglion cells","It can occur in isolation or alongside true aganglionosis, sometimes proximal to a Hirschsprung segment","Diagnosis requires full-thickness or deep rectal biopsy with specialised histochemistry and immunohistochemistry, and is subject to interobserver variability","Management is largely conservative with bowel-management measures, reserving surgery for refractory or clearly localised disease","The diagnosis remains debated, with evolving histological criteria and uncertain reproducibility"],"pathophysiology":"Intestinal neuronal dysplasia is a form of enteric dysganglionosis in which ganglion cells are present but abnormally organised. In the classic type B pattern there is hyperplasia of the submucosal (and myenteric) plexus with giant ganglia, increased ganglion cell numbers, and ectopic neurons within the lamina propria and around submucosal vessels, together with abnormal acetylcholinesterase activity. This disordered innervation impairs coordinated peristalsis, producing a functional obstruction that clinically resembles Hirschsprung disease despite the presence of ganglion cells.","additionalDifferentials":["Hirschsprung disease (aganglionosis)","Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction of other cause","Hypoganglionosis","Functional constipation","Anorectal malformation or mechanical obstruction","Hollow visceral myopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full-thickness or deep suction rectal biopsy with acetylcholinesterase histochemistry and immunohistochemistry","Contrast enema to assess calibre change and transition zone","Anorectal manometry to assess the rectoanal inhibitory reflex","Abdominal radiography to evaluate distension and obstruction","Assessment for associated aganglionosis or systemic neuromuscular disease"],"redFlags":["Features of complete or worsening intestinal obstruction","Enterocolitis with sepsis, particularly where Hirschsprung disease coexists","Failure to thrive or severe malnutrition","Bowel ischaemia or perforation from chronic distension"],"prognosis":"Many children improve over time with conservative bowel management, and outcome depends on the extent of disease and whether aganglionosis coexists; the uncertain diagnostic criteria complicate prognostication.","pitfalls":["Confidently diagnosing or excluding the condition despite substantial interobserver variability in histology","Missing coexisting Hirschsprung aganglionosis in the distal segment","Proceeding to resection when conservative management would suffice","Interpreting inadequate or superficial biopsies as definitive"],"management":["Establish a conservative bowel-management programme with laxatives, enemas and dietary fibre as the mainstay","Use regular rectal washouts for refractory constipation and functional obstruction","Provide nutritional support and management of abdominal distension","Reserve surgery such as myectomy or resection for clearly localised or refractory disease","Exclude and manage coexisting Hirschsprung aganglionosis, which alters the surgical approach","Follow up through paediatric gastroenterology and surgery with periodic reassessment, as many children improve over time"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Walker's Pediatric Gastrointestinal Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"intraabdominal_infections_and_abscesses":{"name":"Intraabdominal Infections and Abscesses","system":"Gastrointestinal/Abdominal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Peritonitis","Primary (spontaneous) bacterial peritonitis","SBP","Secondary peritonitis","Intraperitoneal abscess","Pyogenic liver abscess","Splenic abscess","Perinephric abscess","Renal abscess","Psoas abscess"],"chapter":137,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1069-1076","clinicalPearls":["Intraperitoneal infection follows disruption of a normal anatomic barrier (ruptured appendix, diverticulum, or ulcer; ischemic, tumor-related, or inflammatory bowel wall weakening; or leakage from adjacent pancreatitis or pelvic inflammatory disease), and evolves in two sequential stages: peritonitis first, then abscess formation if the patient survives untreated.","Primary (spontaneous) bacterial peritonitis (PBP) in adults occurs chiefly with cirrhotic ascites, develops in up to about 10% of cirrhotic patients, and is thought to arise from hematogenous spread when impaired hepatic filtration and altered portal flow allow organisms to seed and multiply in ascitic fluid.","A peritoneal-fluid count of more than 250 polymorphonuclear leukocytes/uL is diagnostic of PBP; this threshold does not apply to secondary peritonitis. Normal peritoneal fluid contains under 300 WBC/uL (mainly mononuclear) and protein under 30 g/L.","PBP is typically caused by a single organism (often enteric gram-negative bacilli such as E. coli, but increasingly gram-positive cocci and ESBL-producing Enterobacteriaceae); recovery of multiple organisms including anaerobes should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis and a search for a secondary source.","Fever is the most common manifestation of PBP (up to 80%), but abdominal pain and peritoneal signs may be absent; nonlocalizing features such as malaise, encephalopathy, jaundice, or unexplained acute kidney injury in a cirrhotic with ascites should also trigger diagnostic paracentesis.","Yield of peritoneal-fluid culture is improved by inoculating about 10 mL directly into a blood-culture bottle, and simultaneous blood cultures should be drawn because bacteremia frequently accompanies PBP; samples are best obtained before antibiotics.","Secondary peritonitis results from spillage from an intraabdominal viscus and yields a mixed flora dominated by facultative gram-negative bacilli and anaerobes, especially with a colonic source; early death is driven by gram-negative bacillary sepsis and circulating endotoxin.","The bacterial burden of a perforation depends on site: a ruptured gastric ulcer (if not achlorhydric) causes mainly low-pH chemical irritation with a low bacterial load, whereas leaked colonic contents (pH 7-8, ~10^11 anaerobes/g feces) cause intense infection from heavy bacterial load.","Bacteroides fragilis, though only about 0.5% of normal colonic flora, is the anaerobe most frequently isolated from intraabdominal infections and abscesses and the most common anaerobic bloodstream isolate; its zwitterionic capsular polysaccharide (polysaccharide A) drives abscess localization via a CD4+ T-cell-dependent host response.","Of all intraabdominal abscesses, about 74% are intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal (non-visceral); most intraperitoneal abscesses arise from fecal spillage (e.g., an inflamed appendix), and diverticular abscesses are the least likely to rupture.","The liver is the organ most subject to abscess; biliary tract disease is now the most common source (formerly ruptured appendicitis), and pylephlebitis (suppurative portal vein thrombosis) is another route of seeding. Half of liver-abscess patients lack hepatomegaly, right-upper-quadrant tenderness, or jaundice, so liver abscess can present as fever of unknown origin, especially in the elderly.","Hypermucoviscous (hypervirulent) Klebsiella pneumoniae causes a distinctive community-acquired syndrome of liver abscess with bacteremia and metastatic infection of the eye(s) and lung(s), originally described in Southeast Asia and now emerging in North America and elsewhere.","Splenic abscess is rare but frequently fatal if untreated and often diagnosed late (at autopsy in about 37% of one series); hematogenous spread predominates, with bacterial endocarditis the most common associated infection and streptococci then S. aureus the usual isolates.","More than 75% of perinephric and renal abscesses now arise from ascending urinary tract infection rather than hematogenous seeding; concomitant obstructing nephrolithiasis is the most important risk factor (renal stones in 20-60% of perinephric abscess cases)."],"pathophysiology":"Intraperitoneal infection begins when a normal anatomic barrier is breached and organisms normally contained within the bowel or another organ enter the sterile peritoneal cavity. Inflammation triggers an early influx of polymorphonuclear leukocytes followed by a prolonged phase of mononuclear cell migration, with the leukocyte phenotype shaped by resident-cell chemokine synthesis. In primary (spontaneous) bacterial peritonitis, a diseased liver with altered portal circulation impairs the normal filtration function, allowing hematogenous organisms to seed ascites; cirrhosis-related defects (low peritoneal complement, diminished PMN opsonic and phagocytic capacity, gut dysbiosis with overgrowth of Enterobacteriaceae, reduced Paneth cell defensins, decreased motility, and portal-hypertensive enteropathy) promote bacterial translocation. Secondary peritonitis follows direct viscus spillage; an early containment phase produces a fibrin- and PMN-rich exudate, and severity reflects whether spillage is chemically irritant (acidic gastric contents) or heavily bacterial (colonic contents). If overt gram-negative sepsis does not prove fatal, the host walls off persisting organisms and PMNs in a fibrous capsule to form an abscess; B. fragilis is uniquely able to induce abscesses, with its zwitterionic capsular polysaccharide A stimulating peritoneal macrophages (TNF-alpha, ICAM-1) and CD4+ T lymphocytes that secrete leukoattractant cytokines, while the alternative complement pathway and fibrinogen also participate.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary versus secondary peritonitis (single organism without source versus mixed flora with an identifiable viscus source)","Pancreatitis versus its infectious complications (infected pseudocyst, pancreatic abscess, infected pancreatic necrosis) - fever, leukocytosis, and abdominal pain alone do not distinguish them","Diverticular abscess versus simple diverticulitis","Tuboovarian abscess and other female genital tract infection extending into the pelvis","Perinephric/renal abscess versus uncomplicated pyelonephritis when fever persists beyond 4-5 days of therapy","Hemoperitoneum (especially in trauma) mimicking infective peritonitis"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculous peritonitis (cross-reference Chap. 183)","Amebic liver abscess (cross-reference Chap. 230) - positive amebic serology in over 95% of cases","Candidal liver or renal abscess following fungemia, often unmasked when PMNs recover after neutropenia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute pancreatitis without infected necrosis (sterile inflammation producing fever and leukocytosis)","Chemical peritonitis from a ruptured gastric ulcer in the absence of significant bacterial load","Malignant ascites or metastatic intraabdominal malignancy","Congestive heart failure, systemic lupus erythematosus, and lymphedema as noncirrhotic settings reported with PBP"],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic paracentesis of any cirrhotic with ascites and fever, with PMN count (>250/uL diagnostic of PBP) and culture of fluid in blood-culture bottles plus simultaneous blood cultures","Contrast-enhanced abdominal CT to exclude or identify a primary intraabdominal source (highest yield for abscess localization)","Chest and abdominal radiography to exclude free air signaling perforation (pneumoperitoneum) and to suggest liver abscess via raised right hemidiaphragm, basilar infiltrate, or pleural effusion","Ultrasonography (particularly useful for right upper quadrant, kidneys, and pelvis) and MRI for abscess characterization","Indium-labeled WBC scan or gallium scan to localize occult collections (indium preferred near bowel); PET scanning for resolution and availability","Serum alkaline phosphatase (elevated in about 70% of liver abscess - the single most reliable laboratory finding), bilirubin (elevated in ~50%), AST (~48%); leukocytosis, normochromic normocytic anemia (~50%), hypoalbuminemia (~33%)","Amebic serology and PCR to exclude amebic liver abscess","In CAPD-associated peritonitis, dialysate cell count with differential (cloudy fluid with >100 WBC/uL, >50% neutrophils, or >50% PMN with shortened dwell time) and concentrated dialysate culture in blood-culture bottles","Needle aspiration under CT guidance for culture in suspected infected pancreatic necrosis"],"redFlags":["Free air under the diaphragm on upright chest film indicating bowel perforation with peritonitis requiring urgent intervention","Hemodynamic instability in suspected secondary peritonitis warranting surgery without delay for imaging","Persistent fever after 4-5 days of treatment for pyelonephritis suggesting perinephric or renal abscess","Polymicrobial growth in PBP-suspected peritoneal fluid signaling missed secondary peritonitis source","Liver abscess presenting only as fever of unknown origin, especially in the elderly","Hypervirulent K. pneumoniae liver abscess with metastatic eye and lung involvement","Splenic abscess with underlying endocarditis or hemoglobinopathy given high untreated fatality"],"prognosis":"PBP carries substantial morbidity and mortality, reflecting its concentration among patients with advanced liver disease; reported in-hospital mortality was about 17.6% in a 2018 US cohort, and another series found 23% mortality at 30 days and 37% at 90 days. PBP recurs frequently, with up to 70% of patients experiencing recurrence within 1 year. Patients with PBP usually respond to appropriate antibiotics within 72 h, and delayed therapy increases mortality. In secondary peritonitis, early death is attributable to gram-negative bacillary sepsis and endotoxemia, and the urgency and success of source control - rather than the choice of empiric antibiotics - drive survival odds. Pyogenic liver abscess mortality averaged about 15% despite treatment. Splenic and perinephric abscesses are frequently fatal when diagnosis is delayed, so early diagnosis is the most important determinant of outcome.","pitfalls":["Failing to perform diagnostic paracentesis in a febrile cirrhotic with ascites, as PBP is often subtle and abdominal pain/peritoneal signs may be absent","Misclassifying secondary peritonitis as PBP - the >250 PMN/uL threshold and single-organism rule apply only to PBP; mixed flora or anaerobes indicate a secondary source","Relying on cultures from chronic drain sites, which are not reliable for defining infection etiology","Attributing fever, leukocytosis, and pain in pancreatitis to the inflammation itself and overlooking an infected pseudocyst, pancreatic abscess, or infected necrosis","Most clinical treatment failures in abscesses result from failure to achieve source control (drainage), not antibiotic selection","Injecting barium when perforation is suspected (contraindicated), even though barium enema may occasionally reveal a diverticular abscess","Missing splenic or perinephric abscess because of nonspecific presentation and frequently delayed diagnosis","Overlooking polymicrobial urine culture, known renal stones, or sterile pyuria as clues to perinephric/renal abscess"],"management":["Start prompt empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics covering enteric gram-negative bacilli and anaerobes, narrowing by culture results","Achieve source control by draining abscesses, percutaneously under image guidance or surgically, and repairing any perforation","For spontaneous bacterial peritonitis in cirrhotic ascites, give a third-generation cephalosporin with intravenous albumin to reduce renal impairment","Perform diagnostic paracentesis before antibiotics, inoculating fluid into blood-culture bottles alongside simultaneous blood cultures","Resuscitate with fluids and provide haemodynamic support for sepsis, escalating to critical care as needed","Give secondary antibiotic prophylaxis after spontaneous bacterial peritonitis because of high recurrence, and treat the underlying liver disease","Involve surgery and interventional radiology in multidisciplinary source identification and control"],"severityClassification":"No single formal severity score is given; the text frames disease along the peritonitis-to-abscess continuum and stratifies risk by setting. PBP is graded operationally by ascitic-fluid PMN count (>250/uL diagnostic) and by markers of advanced liver disease (serum creatinine, BUN, total bilirubin, Child-Turcotte-Pugh score) that identify highest-risk and worst-prognosis patients. Secondary and abscess-related infection severity is stratified clinically as community-acquired mild-to-moderate disease versus critically ill or health care-associated infection, which guides anticipated flora and management intensity.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1069-1076"],"crossLinks":[]},"intraductal_papillary_mucinous_neoplasm":{"name":"Intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm","system":"Gastrointestinal / Pancreas","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IPMN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Gastroenterology and pancreatic cyst guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mucin-producing cystic neoplasm arising from the pancreatic ductal epithelium, with malignant potential","It is classified as main-duct, branch-duct or mixed type, which differ substantially in cancer risk","Many are found incidentally on cross-sectional imaging as pancreatic cysts communicating with the duct","Main-duct and mixed types carry a high risk of malignancy and generally warrant resection","Worrisome and high-risk features (such as a dilated main duct, an enhancing mural nodule or obstructive jaundice) guide management","Branch-duct lesions without high-risk features are often kept under surveillance"],"pathophysiology":"Intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms are proliferations of mucin-secreting epithelium within the pancreatic ducts, producing papillary growth and cystic ductal dilatation from mucin accumulation. They represent a spectrum from low-grade dysplasia to invasive carcinoma, following an adenoma-to-carcinoma sequence. Main-duct involvement reflects more extensive, higher-grade disease with greater malignant potential, whereas branch-duct lesions are generally lower risk. Mucin extrusion from a patulous papilla may be seen endoscopically.","additionalDifferentials":["Mucinous cystic neoplasm of the pancreas","Serous cystadenoma","Pancreatic pseudocyst","Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","Chronic pancreatitis with ductal changes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cross-sectional imaging (CT/MRI with MRCP) characterising duct communication and features","Endoscopic ultrasound with cyst fluid analysis where indicated","Assessment of worrisome and high-risk stigmata","Surveillance imaging for low-risk branch-duct lesions","Multidisciplinary review for resection decisions"],"redFlags":["Obstructive jaundice from a lesion in the pancreatic head","An enhancing mural nodule or markedly dilated main duct","Rapid cyst growth or new solid component","New-onset or worsening diabetes or weight loss"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent for resected non-invasive lesions, but invasive IPMN carries a prognosis related to the associated carcinoma; appropriate surveillance and timely resection are key.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying a mucinous cystic neoplasm or pseudocyst as IPMN","Under-recognising the high malignant risk of main-duct disease","Discontinuing surveillance of branch-duct lesions prematurely","Overlooking worrisome features that should prompt resection"],"management":["Resect main-duct and mixed-type lesions, for example by pancreatoduodenectomy or distal pancreatectomy, given their high malignant potential","Resect branch-duct lesions harbouring worrisome or high-risk features such as an enhancing mural nodule, dilated main duct or obstructive jaundice","Keep low-risk branch-duct lesions under structured surveillance imaging with MRI and MRCP or endoscopic ultrasound","Use endoscopic ultrasound with cyst-fluid analysis to clarify indeterminate lesions","Individualise resection versus surveillance decisions through multidisciplinary review","Continue surveillance of the pancreatic remnant for metachronous disease and manage pancreatic exocrine insufficiency with enzyme replacement after resection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Pancreatic cyst management guidelines (standard reference)","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"intraductal_papilloma":{"name":"Intraductal papilloma","system":"Breast","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["intraductal papilloma","breast papilloma","solitary intraductal papilloma","papillomatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard breast surgery textbook and benign breast disease guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An intraductal papilloma is a benign wart-like growth within a breast duct and is the commonest cause of pathological bloodstained or serous single-duct nipple discharge","A solitary (central) papilloma typically arises in a large subareolar duct in middle-aged women and presents with spontaneous discharge from a single duct","Multiple (peripheral) papillomas occur in younger women, are located more peripherally, and carry a somewhat higher associated risk of malignancy","A small subareolar lump may be palpable, and gentle pressure at a specific point around the areola may reproduce discharge from the responsible duct","Triple assessment is used to evaluate the discharge and any mass and to exclude an underlying carcinoma","Ultrasound may show a dilated duct containing a mass, and ductography or ductoscopy can localise the lesion in selected cases","Definitive management is usually excision of the affected duct (microdochectomy) or a total duct excision, which provides both treatment and full histology","Because a papilloma can harbour or be associated with atypia or malignancy, thorough histological assessment of the excised lesion is important"],"pathophysiology":"An intraductal papilloma is a benign proliferation of ductal epithelium supported by fibrovascular cores that projects into the lumen of a breast duct. Its delicate, highly vascular fronds are fragile and bleed easily, which accounts for the characteristic bloodstained or serous nipple discharge, and the growth may partially obstruct the duct producing localised dilatation. Solitary central lesions are generally isolated benign growths, whereas multiple peripheral papillomas represent a more diffuse proliferative process associated with a modestly increased risk of concurrent or subsequent malignancy, so histological confirmation is important to detect any atypia.","additionalDifferentials":["Ductal carcinoma in situ or invasive carcinoma","Duct ectasia","Fibroadenoma or other benign mass","Physiological nipple discharge","Paget disease of the nipple","Periductal mastitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination characterising the discharge and identifying a trigger point or lump","Assessment of the discharge, including testing for blood","Ultrasound of the subareolar ducts as part of triple assessment","Mammography in women of appropriate age","Ductography or ductoscopy to localise the lesion in selected cases","Duct excision (microdochectomy) with histology for definitive diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Bloodstained single-duct discharge, which mandates exclusion of malignancy","An associated hard or irregular mass or suspicious imaging","Atypia or malignancy identified on biopsy or excision histology","Multiple peripheral papillomas, given the higher associated cancer risk","Discordant triple assessment findings"],"prognosis":"A solitary intraductal papilloma is benign and generally cured by duct excision with an excellent prognosis, whereas multiple peripheral papillomas or lesions with atypia warrant closer surveillance because of their higher associated risk of malignancy.","pitfalls":["Attributing bloodstained single-duct discharge to a benign papilloma without excluding carcinoma","Relying on imaging alone and not obtaining histology of the affected duct","Underestimating the malignancy risk associated with multiple peripheral papillomas","Failing to identify the responsible duct before surgery, leading to incomplete excision","Confusing pathological single-duct discharge with benign physiological discharge"],"management":["Undertake microdochectomy (single-duct excision) as definitive treatment and to obtain histology excluding atypia or malignancy","Complete triple assessment with clinical examination, imaging and pathology to characterise the discharge and exclude underlying carcinoma","Perform total duct excision where multiple ducts are involved or the discharge cannot be localised to one duct","Arrange closer surveillance for multiple peripheral papillomas or lesions showing atypia given the higher associated malignancy risk","Reassure and follow up conservatively for an incidental benign solitary papilloma without troublesome discharge","Provide breast surgical multidisciplinary follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard breast surgery reference text on nipple discharge and benign breast disease","Guidance on evaluation of nipple discharge"],"crossLinks":[]},"intrahepatic_cholestasis_of_pregnancy":{"name":"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ICP","obstetric cholestasis","cholestasis of pregnancy","pruritus gravidarum","ABCB4-related cholestasis of pregnancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / RCOG Green-top Guideline","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents in the second or third trimester with intense pruritus, classically of the palms and soles, in the absence of a primary skin rash","The diagnostic hallmark is raised fasting serum bile acids; transaminases may also be mildly elevated","Jaundice is uncommon; itch typically precedes biochemical change and resolves within days to weeks after delivery","The principal concern is fetal risk: spontaneous preterm birth, meconium-stained liquor, and, with high bile acid levels, an increased risk of stillbirth","Susceptibility is influenced by heterozygous variants in biliary transporter genes, notably ABCB4 (MDR3), and by oestrogen and progesterone metabolites","It tends to recur in subsequent pregnancies and with combined oestrogen-containing contraception","Management centres on ursodeoxycholic acid for maternal symptoms, fetal monitoring, and planned delivery timed according to bile acid levels and gestation, with vitamin K if there is prolonged cholestasis or coagulopathy"],"pathophysiology":"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is a reversible, hormonally influenced cholestasis of late pregnancy. Rising oestrogen and progesterone metabolites impair hepatocellular bile acid transport in genetically susceptible women, many of whom carry heterozygous variants in canalicular transporter genes such as ABCB4 (MDR3), ABCB11 (BSEP), or ATP8B1. Reduced canalicular excretion raises circulating bile acids, causing maternal pruritus; transplacental passage of elevated bile acids is implicated in fetal complications including arrhythmia, preterm birth, and sudden intrauterine death, with risk rising steeply at very high maternal bile acid concentrations. The condition characteristically resolves after delivery.","additionalDifferentials":["Pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP)","Pemphigoid gestationis and atopic eruption of pregnancy","Pre-eclampsia and HELLP syndrome","Acute fatty liver of pregnancy","Viral hepatitis or drug-induced liver injury","Gallstone disease with biliary obstruction","Primary biliary cholangitis presenting in pregnancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting serum bile acid concentration (key test, guiding severity and delivery timing)","Liver function tests including transaminases and bilirubin","Coagulation profile and vitamin K status if cholestasis is prolonged","Viral hepatitis serology and liver autoantibodies to exclude other causes","Liver and biliary ultrasound to exclude obstruction","Fetal surveillance including cardiotocography and growth assessment"],"redFlags":["Very high maternal serum bile acids indicating heightened stillbirth risk","Reduced fetal movements or abnormal cardiotocography","Jaundice, right upper quadrant pain, or features suggesting acute fatty liver or pre-eclampsia","Coagulopathy from vitamin K deficiency in prolonged cholestasis","Persistent abnormal liver tests beyond the postpartum period suggesting underlying liver disease"],"prognosis":"Maternal prognosis is excellent with resolution of pruritus and biochemistry after delivery, though recurrence in future pregnancies is common; the main determinant of outcome is fetal, with planned delivery and bile-acid-guided monitoring aimed at reducing the risk of stillbirth.","pitfalls":["Reassuring on the basis of mild itch without measuring serum bile acids","Overlooking the rising stillbirth risk at very high bile acid concentrations when planning delivery","Failing to exclude pre-eclampsia, HELLP, or acute fatty liver of pregnancy","Not arranging postpartum follow-up to confirm resolution and detect underlying liver disease","Neglecting vitamin K when cholestasis is prolonged, risking maternal or neonatal bleeding"],"management":["Give ursodeoxycholic acid to relieve maternal pruritus and lower serum bile acids","Provide symptomatic relief of itch with emollients and a sedating antihistamine at night","Supplement vitamin K where cholestasis is prolonged or coagulation is deranged","Monitor fasting serum bile acids and liver function serially to gauge severity and guide delivery timing","Undertake foetal surveillance and plan early delivery timed to bile-acid concentration to reduce stillbirth risk","Avoid oestrogen-containing contraception postpartum given recurrence risk, and confirm biochemical resolution after delivery","Coordinate obstetric and hepatology care, with genetic counselling where a biliary transporter variant such as ABCB4 (MDR3) is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","RCOG Green-top Guideline on intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy"],"crossLinks":[]},"intramuscular_myxoma":{"name":"Intramuscular myxoma","system":"Musculoskeletal / Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Muscle myxoma","Intramuscular myxoma of soft tissue"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours / Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign, hypocellular, myxoid tumour that arises within skeletal muscle, most often in the thigh, shoulder, buttock, and upper arm","It typically presents in middle-aged and older adults, with a female predominance, as a slowly enlarging painless mass","Activating GNAS mutations underlie the tumour, the same gene implicated in fibrous dysplasia","The coexistence of one or more intramuscular myxomas with fibrous dysplasia constitutes Mazabraud syndrome","Histology shows abundant hypovascular myxoid matrix with only scattered bland spindle and stellate cells and no significant atypia","MRI demonstrates a well-defined lesion of low T1 and high T2 signal that behaves almost like fluid, often with a thin rim of surrounding fat and muscle oedema","The cellular myxoma variant is more cellular and vascular and can be mistaken for a myxoid sarcoma","Marginal excision is curative and local recurrence is rare"],"pathophysiology":"Intramuscular myxoma is a benign mesenchymal neoplasm characterised by the production of copious mucoid, hyaluronic acid-rich matrix by scattered fibroblast-like cells. The tumour is driven by post-zygotic activating missense mutations in GNAS, which encodes the alpha subunit of a stimulatory G protein; constitutive signalling through this pathway alters fibroblast behaviour and matrix production. Because the same somatic GNAS mutation can affect skeletal progenitor cells, the lesion shares its molecular basis with fibrous dysplasia, and when the two coexist the combination is termed Mazabraud syndrome. The tumour grows by expansion within the muscle, remaining well demarcated and hypovascular, which accounts for its fluid-like imaging characteristics and its indolent, non-metastasising behaviour. The chief diagnostic challenge is separating it, and especially its more cellular variant, from myxoid soft tissue sarcomas.","additionalDifferentials":["Myxoid liposarcoma","Low-grade fibromyxoid sarcoma","Myxofibrosarcoma","Nodular fasciitis with myxoid change","Ganglion or myxoid cyst","Neurogenic tumour with myxoid stroma","Cellular myxoma variant"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI to characterise the fluid-like myxoid mass within muscle","Image-guided core biopsy when imaging is not definitive","Histology demonstrating hypocellular myxoid tissue with bland cells","GNAS mutation analysis in equivocal cases","Skeletal survey or targeted imaging for fibrous dysplasia when Mazabraud syndrome is suspected","Correlation of imaging, cellularity, and vascularity to exclude a myxoid sarcoma"],"redFlags":["Increased cellularity, vascularity, or atypia raising concern for a myxoid sarcoma","Rapid growth or large size","Bone lesions suggesting associated fibrous dysplasia and Mazabraud syndrome","Recurrence after excision prompting re-evaluation of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Intramuscular myxoma is benign with an excellent prognosis, and marginal excision is generally curative with only rare local recurrence. Recognition of associated fibrous dysplasia in Mazabraud syndrome is important, as those bony lesions carry their own morbidity.","pitfalls":["Overdiagnosing a myxoid sarcoma from the cellular myxoma variant","Underestimating a hypercellular lesion and missing a genuine myxoid malignancy","Failing to look for fibrous dysplasia and thereby missing Mazabraud syndrome","Attempting enucleation of an ill-defined myxoid mass before imaging characterisation"],"management":["Perform marginal surgical excision, which is generally curative with only rare local recurrence","Characterise the lesion with MRI and obtain image-guided core biopsy in equivocal cases to exclude a myxoid sarcoma","Use GNAS mutation analysis to support the diagnosis where imaging and histology are indeterminate","Screen for fibrous dysplasia with a skeletal survey when Mazabraud syndrome is suspected and manage symptomatic bony lesions","Provide routine follow-up after excision given the benign, non-metastasising nature","Review through orthopaedic oncology and pathology multidisciplinary input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"intraosseous_lipoma":{"name":"Intraosseous lipoma","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Intramedullary lipoma","Bone lipoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Resnick, Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare benign tumour of mature adipose tissue arising within the medullary cavity, with a striking predilection for the calcaneus, and also seen in the intertrochanteric femur and long-bone metaphyses.","In the calcaneus it typically occupies the neutral triangle, the region of sparse trabeculae, and is usually an incidental finding.","Radiographs show a well-defined lucent lesion with a thin sclerotic rim, frequently containing a central focus of dystrophic calcification producing a target or cockade appearance.","CT demonstrating fat attenuation (negative Hounsfield values) and MRI showing signal that follows subcutaneous fat on all sequences and suppresses with fat saturation are effectively diagnostic.","The Milgram staging reflects natural involution: stage 1 viable fat, stage 2 partial fat necrosis with calcification, and stage 3 extensive necrosis with cyst formation, calcification and ossification.","Advanced involuted lesions may become cystic and calcified and mimic a simple bone cyst or bone infarct.","Most lesions require no treatment; curettage and grafting are reserved for persistent pain or fracture risk.","Malignant transformation to liposarcoma is exceedingly rare and remains debated."],"pathophysiology":"Intraosseous lipoma is a benign neoplasm composed of lobules of mature adipocytes that replace the medullary bone and its haematopoietic marrow. Its behaviour is characterised by a tendency to spontaneous involution, described in the Milgram stages: initially viable fat, then progressive fat necrosis with dystrophic calcification, and finally cystic degeneration with reactive ossification, which explains the variable and sometimes confusing imaging appearance. The calcaneal predilection is attributed to the biomechanically determined region of sparse trabeculae, and the fatty composition gives the lesion its characteristic low CT attenuation and fat-matched MRI signal that permit confident non-invasive diagnosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Simple (unicameral) bone cyst","Calcaneal pseudocyst (normal trabecular lucency)","Bone infarct (medullary osteonecrosis)","Fibrous dysplasia","Intraosseous ganglion","Aneurysmal bone cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs demonstrating a lucent lesion with a sclerotic rim and possible central calcification","MRI to confirm fat signal on all sequences","CT to document fat attenuation with negative Hounsfield values","Fat-suppressed MRI sequences to prove suppression of the fatty component","Comparison with prior imaging to confirm stability","Biopsy only in the rare lesion with atypical or aggressive features"],"redFlags":["Aggressive imaging features such as cortical destruction or ill-defined margins","Soft-tissue extension beyond the bone","Rapid interval growth","New or escalating pain localised to the lesion","Loss of the characteristic fatty signal or attenuation"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; the lesion is benign, frequently involutes spontaneously and rarely requires intervention.","pitfalls":["Confusing the lesion with a simple bone cyst or the normal calcaneal pseudocyst and intervening unnecessarily","Misreading an involuted, calcified stage 3 lesion as an aggressive process","Failing to obtain fat-specific imaging that would establish the diagnosis non-invasively","Overtreating an asymptomatic incidental lesion"],"management":["Observe conservatively asymptomatic, incidentally found lesions given the benign, often self-involuting nature","Confirm the diagnosis non-invasively with CT fat attenuation and fat-suppressed MRI to avoid unnecessary intervention","Obtain serial imaging to document stability where the diagnosis is confident","Reserve curettage and bone grafting for symptomatic, enlarging or structurally weakened lesions at fracture risk","Biopsy only lesions with atypical or aggressive features to exclude other pathology","Provide orthopaedic and musculoskeletal radiology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Resnick, Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders","Greenspan, Orthopedic Imaging: A Practical Approach","WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone (current edition)"],"crossLinks":[]},"intrauterine_growth_restriction":{"name":"Intrauterine Growth Restriction","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IUGR","fetal growth restriction","FGR","small for gestational age with pathology"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Intrauterine growth restriction describes a fetus that fails to achieve its genetically determined growth potential, and it should be distinguished from constitutionally small but healthy fetuses","It is broadly divided into placental (uteroplacental insufficiency), maternal, and fetal causes, with placental insufficiency the commonest mechanism in developed settings","Symmetrical restriction, in which head and body are proportionally small, often reflects early insults such as chromosomal abnormality or congenital infection, whereas asymmetrical restriction with relative head sparing suggests later placental insufficiency","Accurate dating early in pregnancy is essential, as growth restriction cannot be assessed reliably without a correct gestational age","Surveillance combines serial ultrasound biometry, amniotic fluid assessment, and umbilical artery Doppler to detect deterioration","A progression of Doppler abnormalities, from raised umbilical artery resistance to absent or reversed end-diastolic flow and abnormal ductus venosus, reflects worsening fetal compromise","Management balances the risks of prematurity against those of continued exposure to a hostile intrauterine environment, and antenatal corticosteroids are given when early delivery is anticipated","Growth-restricted fetuses have higher risks of stillbirth, intrapartum compromise, and neonatal complications, and later cardiovascular and metabolic disease"],"pathophysiology":"The commonest pathway is uteroplacental insufficiency, in which inadequate trophoblastic remodelling of the spiral arteries produces a poorly perfused placenta with reduced capacity to deliver oxygen and nutrients. The fetus adapts by redistributing cardiac output to preserve the brain, heart, and adrenals at the expense of the abdominal viscera and peripheral tissues, producing asymmetrical growth and oligohydramnios from reduced renal perfusion. Progressive hypoxaemia and acidaemia manifest as deteriorating Doppler indices and, ultimately, cardiovascular decompensation. Other cases stem from fetal chromosomal or structural abnormalities, congenital infection, or maternal disease reducing substrate supply.","additionalDifferentials":["Constitutionally small for gestational age but healthy fetus","Incorrect gestational dating","Chromosomal abnormality or genetic syndrome","Congenital infection such as cytomegalovirus or toxoplasmosis","Multiple pregnancy with unequal placental sharing","Maternal disease such as chronic hypertension, renal disease, or thrombophilia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial ultrasound biometry with estimated fetal weight and growth trajectory","Umbilical artery Doppler, with middle cerebral artery and ductus venosus Doppler as disease advances","Amniotic fluid volume assessment","Detailed anatomy scan to identify structural anomalies","Screening for congenital infection and consideration of karyotyping when appropriate","Maternal assessment for hypertension, pre-eclampsia, and other predisposing conditions"],"redFlags":["Absent or reversed umbilical artery end-diastolic flow indicating severe placental compromise","Abnormal ductus venosus flow or a non-reassuring cardiotocograph signalling impending fetal decompensation","Reduced or absent fetal movements","Static or falling growth on serial scans with oligohydramnios","Coexisting severe pre-eclampsia threatening both mother and fetus"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the underlying cause, the severity and gestation at onset, and the timeliness of surveillance and delivery. Early-onset severe placental disease carries the greatest risk of stillbirth and neonatal morbidity, whereas late-onset restriction is often milder but still increases perinatal risk. Survivors have higher rates of neurodevelopmental impairment and of adult cardiovascular and metabolic disease, underscoring the importance of careful monitoring and appropriately timed birth.","pitfalls":["Confusing a constitutionally small healthy fetus with pathological growth restriction, or vice versa","Relying on a single scan rather than serial measurements to judge growth","Neglecting accurate early dating, which undermines all subsequent assessment","Delivering too early for prematurity risk or too late with ongoing fetal hypoxia","Overlooking congenital infection or aneuploidy in early symmetrical restriction"],"management":["Confirm the diagnosis against accurate early dating and distinguish true growth restriction from a constitutionally small but healthy foetus before intervening","Institute serial surveillance with ultrasound biometry, amniotic fluid volume and umbilical artery Doppler, escalating to middle cerebral artery and ductus venosus Doppler as compromise advances","Treat modifiable maternal contributors, optimising control of hypertension and pre-eclampsia and advising smoking cessation","Give antenatal corticosteroids for foetal lung maturation and magnesium sulphate for neuroprotection when preterm delivery is anticipated","Time delivery according to gestation and Doppler deterioration, expediting birth when absent or reversed umbilical end-diastolic flow or an abnormal ductus venosus signals decompensation","Plan mode and place of delivery in a unit with neonatal intensive care, favouring caesarean section when the foetus is unlikely to tolerate labour","Investigate for an underlying cause with a detailed anatomy scan, congenital infection screen and karyotyping where a foetal aetiology is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"intravascular_large_b_cell_lymphoma":{"name":"Intravascular large B-cell lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IVLBCL","angiotropic large cell lymphoma","intravascular lymphomatosis","malignant angioendotheliomatosis","CD20+ intraluminal large B-cell lymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A great mimic that presents with fever of unknown origin, neurological deficits, and skin lesions in the absence of a discrete mass or overt lymphadenopathy","Malignant large B-cells proliferate selectively within the lumina of small and medium blood vessels, causing occlusion and organ ischaemia","A Western pattern often features neurological and cutaneous disease, while an Asian variant is characterised by haemophagocytic syndrome, marrow involvement, hepatosplenomegaly, and cytopenias","Common laboratory clues include raised lactate dehydrogenase, elevated inflammatory markers, anaemia, and thrombocytopenia","Diagnosis frequently requires biopsy of clinically involved skin, or random skin and other tissue biopsies, because there is often nothing to image","Tumour cells are CD20-positive B-cells confined to vessel lumina, thought to lack the adhesion molecules needed for transmural migration","A high index of suspicion is essential, as many cases are only diagnosed at autopsy"],"pathophysiology":"Intravascular large B-cell lymphoma is a rare aggressive B-cell neoplasm in which malignant cells remain confined within the lumina of small vessels rather than forming tumour masses. The cells are thought to lack surface adhesion and homing molecules required to exit the vasculature, so they accumulate intraluminally and occlude capillaries, arterioles, and venules. This produces widespread microvascular obstruction and ischaemic dysfunction of affected organs, most often the central nervous system and skin, and in the Asian-type variant the marrow, liver, and spleen with an associated haemophagocytic syndrome. The absence of masses and lymphadenopathy accounts for the elusive, multisystem presentation.","additionalDifferentials":["Central nervous system vasculitis or multi-infarct disease","Infective endocarditis with embolic phenomena","Thrombotic microangiopathy","Systemic vasculitis such as polyarteritis nodosa","Disseminated infection or fever of unknown origin from other causes","Paraneoplastic or autoimmune encephalopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy of suspicious lesions, or random skin biopsies, and biopsy of other clinically involved organs","Immunohistochemistry confirming intraluminal CD20-positive large B-cells","Bone marrow examination, particularly in the haemophagocytic variant","MRI of the brain and lactate dehydrogenase, which are often abnormal","Ferritin, triglycerides, and fibrinogen to assess for haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","PET-CT, which may show non-specific organ uptake, and staging investigations"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive neurological deterioration or stroke-like episodes","Features of haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis with multi-organ failure","Unexplained progressive cytopenias with rising lactate dehydrogenase","Deteriorating organ function without an identifiable infective or vascular cause"],"prognosis":"Untreated disease is rapidly fatal, but prompt recognition and anthracycline-based combination immunochemotherapy, with central nervous system-directed treatment where appropriate, can achieve remission in a substantial proportion of patients.","pitfalls":["Failing to consider the diagnosis in fever of unknown origin with neurological or cutaneous signs","Relying on imaging, which typically shows no mass","Not pursuing random or targeted skin biopsy when suspicion is high","Overlooking an accompanying haemophagocytic syndrome, especially in the Asian variant"],"management":["Secure a tissue diagnosis urgently by biopsy of involved or random skin and other affected organs, since untreated disease is rapidly fatal","Treat with anthracycline-based immunochemotherapy incorporating the anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody rituximab as the mainstay of therapy","Add central-nervous-system-directed treatment such as intrathecal chemotherapy or high-dose methotrexate given the high risk of neurological relapse","Identify and treat associated haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis in the Asian-type variant alongside the lymphoma","Provide supportive care with transfusion for cytopenias, tumour lysis prophylaxis and infection prophylaxis during immunochemotherapy","Monitor response and toxicity with lactate dehydrogenase, blood counts and interval imaging","Refer to haemato-oncology and consider consolidation with autologous stem cell transplantation in fit patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","Swerdlow SH et al. WHO monograph on lymphoid neoplasms"],"crossLinks":[]},"intraventricular_hemorrhage_neonatal":{"name":"Neonatal intraventricular haemorrhage","system":"Neonatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IVH","germinal matrix haemorrhage","germinal matrix-intraventricular haemorrhage","GMH-IVH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cloherty and Stark Manual of Neonatal Care / Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Intraventricular haemorrhage most often originates from the fragile germinal matrix and is predominantly a disease of preterm and very low birth weight infants","Risk is greatest in the first days of life, so routine cranial ultrasound screening is performed early and repeated in at-risk preterm infants","Severity is described by a grading system from mild germinal matrix or small intraventricular bleeds through to bleeds with ventricular dilatation and associated haemorrhagic parenchymal infarction","Presentation ranges from clinically silent to a sudden catastrophic deterioration with collapse, falling haematocrit, bulging fontanelle, and seizures","Fluctuations in cerebral blood flow, systemic blood pressure, hypercarbia, acidosis, and coagulation disturbance all contribute to bleeding from immature vessels","Post-haemorrhagic ventricular dilatation is an important complication requiring serial head circumference and ultrasound monitoring and sometimes cerebrospinal fluid drainage","Prevention focuses on antenatal corticosteroids, careful delivery and resuscitation, and minimising haemodynamic and respiratory instability in the early neonatal period"],"pathophysiology":"Neonatal intraventricular haemorrhage in the preterm infant arises from the germinal matrix, a highly vascular and metabolically active region near the caudate that is present until around term. Its capillaries are structurally immature and fragile, and the region has limited autoregulatory support, so it is vulnerable to rupture when cerebral blood flow or venous pressure fluctuates. Contributing factors include pressure-passive cerebral circulation, swings in arterial pressure, hypercarbia and hypoxia, coagulopathy, and rapid volume shifts. Bleeding may remain confined to the germinal matrix or rupture into the ventricular system, and when it obstructs cerebrospinal fluid flow it causes post-haemorrhagic ventricular dilatation. Associated venous congestion can lead to periventricular haemorrhagic infarction of the surrounding white matter, the most severe pattern.","additionalDifferentials":["Periventricular leukomalacia and other white matter injury","Neonatal seizures from hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy or metabolic causes","Neonatal sepsis or meningitis causing acute deterioration","Other intracranial haemorrhage such as subdural or subarachnoid bleeding","Congenital hydrocephalus from a non-haemorrhagic cause","Coagulopathy or thrombocytopenia causing bleeding elsewhere"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cranial ultrasound for screening, grading, and serial monitoring of ventricular size","Serial head circumference measurement to detect post-haemorrhagic ventricular dilatation","Full blood count and coagulation studies to identify anaemia or bleeding tendency","Blood gas and metabolic monitoring for hypercarbia, acidosis, and instability","MRI for detailed assessment of parenchymal injury and prognosis when needed","Cerebrospinal fluid assessment when infection or progressive hydrocephalus is suspected"],"redFlags":["Acute collapse with a sudden fall in haematocrit, bulging fontanelle, and seizures","High-grade haemorrhage with ventricular dilatation or parenchymal infarction","Rapidly increasing head circumference and progressive ventricular enlargement","Signs of raised intracranial pressure such as apnoea, bradycardia, and altered consciousness"],"prognosis":"Outcome is closely linked to grade: low-grade haemorrhage confined to the germinal matrix or with minimal intraventricular blood often has a good neurodevelopmental outcome, whereas high-grade haemorrhage with ventricular dilatation or parenchymal infarction carries a substantial risk of cerebral palsy, cognitive impairment, and post-haemorrhagic hydrocephalus. Prematurity and coexisting complications also influence long-term prognosis.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal neurological examination excludes significant haemorrhage, as many bleeds are clinically silent","Missing the early high-risk window by not screening at-risk preterm infants promptly","Overlooking gradual post-haemorrhagic ventricular dilatation without serial measurements","Causing haemodynamic swings through rapid boluses, aggressive ventilation, or excessive handling","Attributing deterioration to sepsis alone without considering acute haemorrhage"],"management":["Prioritise prevention with antenatal corticosteroids, gentle handling and avoidance of rapid swings in blood pressure, carbon dioxide and circulating volume that provoke germinal matrix bleeding","Maintain physiological stability with lung-protective ventilation to avoid hypercarbia and hypoxia, cautious fluid and inotrope use and correction of any coagulopathy","Screen and grade with routine early cranial ultrasound, repeated serially in at-risk preterm infants","Monitor for post-haemorrhagic ventricular dilatation with serial head circumference measurement and cranial ultrasound","Manage progressive ventricular dilatation with cerebrospinal fluid drainage via lumbar punctures or a ventricular access device, and neurosurgical referral for a ventriculoperitoneal shunt if hydrocephalus persists","Treat seizures with anticonvulsants and provide supportive neuroprotective intensive care","Arrange structured neurodevelopmental follow-up and early intervention services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cloherty and Stark Manual of Neonatal Care","Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn"],"crossLinks":[]},"intussusception":{"name":"Intussusception","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["intestinal invagination","bowel telescoping","ileocolic intussusception"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common cause of intestinal obstruction in infants and toddlers, typically between 6 months and 3 years, in which one segment of bowel telescopes into the adjacent distal segment.","The classic triad is intermittent colicky abdominal pain (with drawing up of the legs), vomiting, and red currant jelly stool, but all three are present in only a minority.","Between painful episodes the infant may appear well or lethargic; lethargy can be a prominent and misleading feature.","A sausage-shaped mass may be palpable, often in the right upper quadrant, with an empty right lower quadrant.","Most cases are ileocolic and idiopathic, sometimes following a viral illness with hypertrophied Peyer patches acting as a lead point.","Ultrasound is the diagnostic investigation of choice, showing a target or doughnut sign on cross-section.","In a stable child without peritonitis or perforation, non-operative reduction with an air or contrast enema is both diagnostic and therapeutic.","Surgery is required when enema reduction fails, or when there is perforation, peritonitis, shock, or a pathological lead point."],"pathophysiology":"A proximal segment of bowel (the intussusceptum) invaginates into the lumen of the adjacent distal segment (the intussuscipiens), dragging its mesentery with it. This compromises venous and lymphatic drainage, producing bowel wall oedema and engorgement; the mixture of sloughed mucosa, blood, and mucus produces the characteristic red currant jelly stool. Progressive swelling obstructs the lumen and, if unrelieved, arterial compromise leads to ischaemia, necrosis, and perforation. In infants a lead point is usually hypertrophied lymphoid tissue, whereas older children more often have a pathological lead point such as a Meckel diverticulum or polyp.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastroenteritis","Incarcerated hernia","Malrotation with volvulus","Appendicitis","Henoch-Schonlein purpura with bowel involvement","Constipation or other causes of colicky pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal ultrasound (target/doughnut sign) as first-line imaging","Abdominal radiograph to look for obstruction or free air","Air or contrast enema (diagnostic and therapeutic in stable children)","Full blood count and electrolytes to assess dehydration","Assessment for an underlying lead point in older or atypical cases"],"redFlags":["Signs of peritonitis or a rigid, tender abdomen","Free air on radiograph indicating perforation","Shock, marked lethargy, or haemodynamic instability","Bilious vomiting and abdominal distension indicating established obstruction","Fever with toxic appearance suggesting bowel ischaemia or sepsis"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and reduction the outlook is excellent; delay increases the risk of ischaemia, resection, and complications, and a proportion recur after non-operative reduction.","pitfalls":["Waiting for the full classic triad, which is often incomplete, and delaying diagnosis","Attributing marked lethargy to another cause and missing intussusception","Attempting enema reduction in a child with peritonitis, perforation, or shock","Failing to consider a pathological lead point in older children or recurrent cases"],"management":["Resuscitate with intravenous fluids and correct electrolyte disturbance, and decompress with a nasogastric tube where there is obstruction","Perform image-guided non-operative reduction with an air (pneumatic) or hydrostatic contrast enema as first-line treatment in the stable child without peritonitis or perforation","Escalate to operative reduction or bowel resection when enema reduction fails or there is peritonitis, perforation or non-viable bowel","Cover with broad-spectrum antibiotics and provide analgesia peri-procedurally where ischaemia or perforation is suspected","Identify and manage a pathological lead point such as a Meckel diverticulum or polyp, particularly in older or recurrent cases","Observe after successful reduction for early recurrence and re-image if symptoms return"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"intussusception_pediatric":{"name":"Paediatric intussusception","system":"Paediatric surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["intussusception","ileocolic intussusception","childhood intussusception","bowel telescoping"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Ashcraft Pediatric Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common cause of intestinal obstruction in infants and toddlers, typically ileocolic, when one bowel segment telescopes into the adjacent distal segment","Classically presents with sudden intermittent colicky pain and drawing up of the legs, alternating with quiet or lethargic intervals","The late triad of colicky pain, a palpable sausage-shaped abdominal mass, and redcurrant-jelly stool is characteristic but incomplete in many children","Most cases in infants are idiopathic, often following a viral illness with lymphoid (Peyer patch) hypertrophy acting as the lead point","Ultrasound is the investigation of choice, showing the target/doughnut sign in transverse section and a pseudokidney sign longitudinally","Non-operative reduction by air or hydrostatic (contrast) enema is first-line in the stable child without peritonitis or perforation","A pathological lead point such as a Meckel diverticulum, polyp, or lymphoma is more likely in older children and in recurrent cases"],"pathophysiology":"Intussusception occurs when a proximal segment of bowel (the intussusceptum) invaginates into the lumen of the contiguous distal segment (the intussuscipiens), dragging its mesentery with it. The commonest ileocolic form is usually idiopathic, frequently preceded by a viral infection that causes hypertrophy of ileal lymphoid tissue serving as a lead point. Progressive telescoping compresses the mesenteric vessels, producing venous congestion, mucosal oedema, and bleeding that gives rise to the redcurrant-jelly stool; if unrelieved, arterial compromise leads to ischaemia, necrosis, and perforation. In older children an anatomical lead point such as a Meckel diverticulum, duplication cyst, polyp, or tumour is more often responsible.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastroenteritis with colic","Malrotation with volvulus","Incarcerated inguinal hernia","Henoch-Schonlein purpura (which can also trigger intussusception)","Meckel diverticulum with bleeding","Constipation or colic in an unsettled infant"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal ultrasound for target and pseudokidney signs and to seek a lead point","Abdominal radiograph to assess for obstruction or free air before reduction","Air or contrast enema, which is both diagnostic and therapeutic for reduction","Basic bloods and hydration assessment to guide resuscitation","Evaluation for a pathological lead point in older or recurrent cases","Post-reduction observation and repeat imaging if symptoms recur"],"redFlags":["Peritonitis, abdominal rigidity, or signs of perforation contraindicating enema reduction","Marked lethargy or shock, which may be the presenting feature and mimic neurological illness","Prolonged symptoms with signs of bowel ischaemia","Free intraperitoneal air on imaging","Recurrent intussusception suggesting a pathological lead point requiring surgery"],"prognosis":"Early diagnosis with prompt enema reduction resolves most idiopathic cases with excellent outcomes, though a minority recur. Delayed presentation with ischaemic or necrotic bowel requires operative reduction or resection and carries higher morbidity.","pitfalls":["Attributing lethargy or altered consciousness to a neurological cause and missing intussusception","Waiting for the full classic triad, which delays diagnosis and reduction","Attempting enema reduction in the presence of peritonitis or perforation","Failing to consider a pathological lead point in older children and recurrent cases","Under-resuscitating a dehydrated child before attempting reduction"],"management":["Resuscitate with intravenous fluids and electrolyte correction, and decompress with a nasogastric tube if the bowel is obstructed","Undertake image-guided non-operative reduction by air (pneumatic) or hydrostatic contrast enema as first-line therapy in the stable child without peritonitis or perforation","Proceed to operative reduction, and resection of non-viable bowel, when enema reduction fails or where there is peritonitis, perforation or established ischaemia","Give peri-procedural broad-spectrum antibiotics and adequate analgesia when perforation or ischaemic bowel is suspected","Seek and treat an anatomical lead point such as a Meckel diverticulum, duplication cyst or polyp in older children and recurrent cases","Observe after reduction for early recurrence and repeat imaging if symptoms recur","Refer to paediatric surgery and manage in a centre equipped for both radiological reduction and operative intervention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Ashcraft's Pediatric Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"involutional_ptosis":{"name":"Involutional ptosis","system":"Eye / Orbit and adnexa","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["aponeurotic ptosis","age-related ptosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oculoplastic surgery references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest form of acquired ptosis, caused by age-related weakening or dehiscence of the levator aponeurosis","Features are a low upper lid margin with good levator function and a high or indistinct upper lid skin crease","In severe cases the lid can obstruct the superior visual field","It is typically bilateral but often asymmetric and gradually progressive","A key task is to distinguish it from neurogenic (third nerve, Horner) and myogenic (myasthenia, myopathy) ptosis","Levator advancement or reinsertion surgery is highly effective given the preserved muscle function"],"pathophysiology":"Involutional ptosis results from stretching, thinning or disinsertion of the levator palpebrae superioris aponeurosis from the tarsal plate, an age-related degenerative change also promoted by chronic eyelid rubbing, prior surgery and contact lens use. Because the levator muscle itself remains strong, levator function is preserved, but the weakened aponeurotic attachment fails to hold the lid elevated, producing a low lid margin and elevation of the skin crease as the crease is pulled upward.","additionalDifferentials":["Neurogenic ptosis (third nerve palsy, Horner syndrome)","Myogenic ptosis (myasthenia gravis, chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia, myotonic dystrophy)","Mechanical ptosis (lid mass, dermatochalasis, oedema)","Congenital ptosis","Pseudoptosis (enophthalmos, contralateral lid retraction)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Measurement of margin-reflex distance, levator function and lid crease position","Assessment for fatigability and other features of myasthenia","Pupil and ocular motility examination to exclude neurogenic causes","Superior visual field testing for functional impairment","Further investigation (for example acetylcholine-receptor antibodies, imaging) if a neurogenic or myogenic cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Ptosis with anisocoria or motility deficit suggesting third nerve palsy or Horner syndrome","Variable or fatigable ptosis suggesting myasthenia gravis","Sudden onset with pain or other neurological signs","Associated systemic or neurological symptoms"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and progressive, and surgical repair of the aponeurosis is very effective and durable because levator function is retained.","pitfalls":["Missing myasthenia gravis or a third nerve palsy masquerading as simple age-related ptosis","Overlooking coexisting dermatochalasis or brow ptosis","Attributing pseudoptosis from enophthalmos to true ptosis","Failing to document levator function before surgery"],"management":["Confirm the diagnosis and exclude neurogenic (third nerve palsy, Horner syndrome) and myogenic (myasthenia, myopathy) causes before intervention, as their management differs","Offer levator aponeurosis advancement or reinsertion surgery as the definitive and highly effective treatment given preserved levator function","Consider a posterior Muller-muscle-conjunctival resection approach in selected patients","Manage conservatively with observation or a ptosis prop mounted on spectacles where surgery is declined or the patient is unfit","Document superior visual field impairment to establish the functional indication for repair","Address contributing factors such as chronic eyelid rubbing and contact lens wear","Monitor postoperatively for over- or under-correction, lagophthalmos and exposure keratopathy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Oculoplastic surgery references (standard reference)","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"iodine_deficiency":{"name":"Iodine deficiency","system":"Nutrition / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["iodine deficiency disorders","endemic goitre","endemic cretinism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO nutrition guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Iodine deficiency is the leading preventable cause of goitre and of acquired intellectual disability worldwide","It occurs in regions with iodine-poor soil and water, historically including large parts of the Himalayan and sub-Himalayan belt of India, and salt iodisation is the mainstay of control","Deficiency in pregnancy is the most serious consequence, causing impaired fetal brain development and, in severe cases, endemic cretinism","The clinical spectrum ranges from diffuse or nodular goitre to hypothyroidism, growth and developmental delay, and neurological deficits","The population iodine status is assessed by median urinary iodine concentration rather than by individual blood tests","Rapid correction of longstanding deficiency, or excess iodine, can occasionally provoke thyroid dysfunction, so replacement is at physiological population levels","Neurological damage sustained in fetal life and infancy is largely irreversible, making prevention through adequate maternal intake essential"],"pathophysiology":"Iodine is an essential substrate for the synthesis of the thyroid hormones thyroxine and triiodothyronine. When dietary intake is inadequate, reduced hormone production lowers feedback inhibition and raises thyroid-stimulating hormone, driving thyroid hyperplasia and goitre as a compensatory response; over time nodularity and, if compensation fails, hypothyroidism develop. Thyroid hormone is critical for normal neuronal migration and myelination in the developing brain, so maternal and early-life deficiency impairs neurodevelopment irreversibly, producing the cognitive and neurological features of iodine deficiency disorders, including cretinism in severe endemic areas.","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune thyroid disease (Hashimoto or Graves)","Sporadic non-toxic goitre and thyroid nodules","Congenital hypothyroidism from thyroid dysgenesis or dyshormonogenesis","Goitre from goitrogen exposure","Other causes of developmental delay"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thyroid function tests including TSH and free thyroid hormones","Urinary iodine concentration for assessing population and individual status","Thyroid ultrasound to characterise goitre or nodules","Neonatal thyroid screening where available","Developmental and neurological assessment in affected children"],"redFlags":["Deficiency in pregnancy threatening fetal neurodevelopment","Large goitre causing airway or oesophageal compression","Overt hypothyroidism, especially in infants where it threatens development","Signs of endemic cretinism requiring urgent public health and clinical action"],"prognosis":"Goitre and hypothyroidism from deficiency can improve with adequate iodine intake, but neurodevelopmental damage from fetal and early-life deficiency is largely permanent, underscoring prevention.","pitfalls":["Relying on individual blood iodine levels rather than urinary iodine for status assessment","Overlooking maternal iodine adequacy in pregnancy","Assuming a goitre is always autoimmune and missing endemic iodine deficiency","Causing iodine-induced thyroid dysfunction through excessive supplementation"],"management":["Correct the deficiency through universal salt iodisation and iodine supplementation delivered at physiological population levels","Ensure adequate iodine intake in pregnancy and lactation with iodine-containing supplements to protect foetal neurodevelopment","Treat established hypothyroidism with levothyroxine replacement","Introduce repletion gradually in longstanding or nodular goitre to avoid provoking iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis","Monitor with thyroid function tests and track population status with median urinary iodine concentration","Refer children with developmental delay for neurodevelopmental assessment and early intervention","Consider surgery for large, compressive or obstructive goitre"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO nutrition guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"ipex_syndrome":{"name":"IPEX syndrome","system":"Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IPEX","immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X-linked syndrome","FOXP3 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["IPEX syndrome is an X-linked disorder caused by mutations in FOXP3, the master transcription factor for regulatory T cells","Loss of functional regulatory T cells leads to severe, early-onset systemic autoimmunity affecting multiple organs","The classic triad is severe enteropathy with intractable watery diarrhoea, early-onset endocrinopathy (especially neonatal type 1 diabetes and thyroiditis), and eczematous dermatitis","It typically presents in male infants and can be rapidly life-threatening from malabsorption, failure to thrive, and metabolic derangement","Additional autoimmune features include haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, nephropathy, and hepatitis","Infections may occur, partly reflecting the underlying immune dysregulation and partly the intensive immunosuppression used to control the disease","The autoimmune enteropathy often shows characteristic small-bowel histology with villous atrophy and does not respond to dietary exclusion alone","Treatment relies on immunosuppression (for example calcineurin inhibitors and sirolimus) to control disease, with haematopoietic stem cell transplantation offering the only definitive cure"],"pathophysiology":"IPEX syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in FOXP3 on the X chromosome, the transcription factor essential for the development and function of CD4-positive CD25-positive regulatory T cells. Without functional regulatory T cells, peripheral tolerance collapses and autoreactive effector T cells drive widespread tissue-specific autoimmunity. The resulting immune attack on the gut epithelium, endocrine organs (pancreatic islets, thyroid), skin, blood cells, and kidneys produces the characteristic multi-organ autoimmune phenotype. Because the defect is one of immune regulation rather than a simple failure of host defence, the dominant problem is aggressive autoimmunity from early infancy, though secondary infection is common.","additionalDifferentials":["Other monogenic autoimmune enteropathies and IPEX-like syndromes (for example CD25 or STAT deficiencies)","Severe combined immunodeficiency with enteropathy such as Omenn syndrome","Autoimmune polyendocrinopathy syndromes (including APECED)","Cow's milk protein enteropathy and other infantile enteropathies","Congenital infections causing failure to thrive"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["FOXP3 genetic testing and analysis of FOXP3 protein expression","Enumeration and functional assessment of regulatory T cells","Autoantibody panels (including anti-enterocyte and endocrine autoantibodies)","Blood glucose, thyroid function, and other endocrine screening","Small-bowel biopsy showing autoimmune enteropathy","Full blood count and haemolysis screen for autoimmune cytopenias"],"redFlags":["Intractable diarrhoea with dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, and failure to thrive as a neonatal emergency","Neonatal or early-onset diabetes with metabolic decompensation","Severe autoimmune cytopenias or nephropathy","Live vaccines should be avoided given the immune dysregulation and immunosuppressive therapy","Overwhelming infection during intensive immunosuppression"],"prognosis":"Untreated IPEX is frequently fatal in infancy or early childhood from enteropathy, metabolic complications, and infection; immunosuppression can achieve temporary control, but haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, ideally undertaken early, provides the best prospect of long-term survival.","pitfalls":["Attributing intractable infantile diarrhoea to food allergy and missing autoimmune enteropathy","Overlooking a monogenic cause of neonatal diabetes in a boy with multi-organ autoimmunity","Delaying genetic testing and transplant referral in a critically ill infant","Giving live vaccines during immunosuppression","Confusing IPEX with APECED, which is autosomal recessive, AIRE-related, and presents later"],"management":["Control the autoimmunity with immunosuppression using corticosteroids together with a calcineurin inhibitor or sirolimus as a bridge to definitive treatment","Pursue allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation early, as it offers the only prospect of cure","Manage the enteropathy with parenteral nutrition and fluid and electrolyte support for intractable diarrhoea and failure to thrive","Treat the endocrinopathy with insulin for neonatal diabetes and thyroid hormone replacement for autoimmune thyroiditis","Manage autoimmune cytopenias and provide intensive skin care for the eczematous dermatitis","Give infection prophylaxis and treat intercurrent infection promptly, reflecting both immune dysregulation and iatrogenic immunosuppression","Offer genetic counselling and FOXP3 testing, with maternal carrier and family testing for this X-linked disorder","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across immunology, gastroenterology, endocrinology and transplantation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"ipmn":{"name":"Intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IPMN","intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm of the pancreas","main-duct IPMN","branch-duct IPMN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mucin-producing epithelial neoplasm arising within the pancreatic ducts, classified as main-duct, branch-duct, or mixed type","Main-duct and mixed types carry a substantially higher risk of high-grade dysplasia and invasive carcinoma than branch-duct disease","Branch-duct lesions communicate with the main duct and are frequently multifocal, often found incidentally as cystic lesions","A classic endoscopic finding of main-duct disease is a patulous, mucin-extruding ampulla (the fish-mouth papilla)","Worrisome features and high-risk stigmata (as in the international consensus and European guidelines) drive decisions between surveillance and resection","High-risk stigmata include obstructive jaundice with a pancreatic head cyst, an enhancing mural nodule of significant size, and marked main pancreatic duct dilatation","Worrisome features include cyst growth, thickened enhancing cyst walls, smaller mural nodules, an abrupt duct calibre change with distal atrophy, and raised CA 19-9","Management ranges from periodic imaging surveillance for low-risk branch-duct lesions to surgical resection for main-duct disease and lesions with high-risk features"],"pathophysiology":"Intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms are proliferations of mucin-secreting columnar epithelium that grow within and dilate the pancreatic ductal system. Excess mucin production and papillary epithelial growth obstruct the ducts, producing cystic dilatation of branch ducts, diffuse or segmental dilatation of the main duct, or both. These lesions progress through a spectrum from low-grade dysplasia to high-grade dysplasia and, ultimately, invasive adenocarcinoma, following an adenoma-to-carcinoma sequence. The malignant potential correlates with the ductal compartment involved and with morphological features such as mural nodules, main duct dilatation, and epithelial subtype, with pancreatobiliary and intestinal subtypes behaving differently.","additionalDifferentials":["Mucinous cystic neoplasm of the pancreas","Serous cystadenoma","Pancreatic pseudocyst","Chronic obstructive pancreatitis with duct dilatation","Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","Solid pseudopapillary neoplasm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI with MRCP to assess ductal communication, cyst morphology, and main duct calibre","Endoscopic ultrasound with fine-needle aspiration for cyst fluid and mural nodule assessment","Cyst fluid CEA (elevated in mucinous lesions) and cytology","Cyst fluid molecular analysis for KRAS and GNAS mutations to support the diagnosis","Serum CA 19-9 as a marker of concern for malignancy","Contrast-enhanced CT to evaluate for a solid mass or vascular involvement","ERCP or pancreatoscopy in selected cases to inspect the duct directly"],"redFlags":["Obstructive jaundice attributable to a cystic head lesion","An enhancing mural nodule of significant size or a solid component","Marked dilatation of the main pancreatic duct","New-onset diabetes, weight loss, or rapid cyst growth","Positive or high-grade atypical cytology on aspiration"],"prognosis":"Branch-duct lesions without worrisome features generally have a low short-term risk of malignancy and can be safely surveyed, whereas main-duct and mixed lesions carry a high risk of harbouring or progressing to invasive cancer; outcomes after resection depend on whether invasive carcinoma is present and on its stage and subtype.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying a mucinous cyst as a benign serous cystadenoma or pseudocyst","Under-recognising main-duct involvement and its high malignant potential","Discontinuing surveillance prematurely, as risk persists and can increase over time","Attributing main duct dilatation to chronic pancreatitis and missing an IPMN","Over-reliance on cyst size alone rather than the full set of worrisome and high-risk features"],"management":["Stratify risk using worrisome features and high-risk stigmata to decide between surveillance and resection","Surveil branch-duct lesions without worrisome features with serial MRI/MRCP and endoscopic ultrasound","Resect main-duct, mixed-type and high-risk lesions surgically by pancreaticoduodenectomy or distal pancreatectomy given their malignant potential","Characterise indeterminate lesions with endoscopic ultrasound and fine-needle aspiration for cyst fluid analysis and mural nodule assessment","Manage post-resection exocrine and endocrine insufficiency with pancreatic enzyme replacement and insulin","Continue long-term surveillance of the pancreatic remnant after resection given the field defect and risk of new lesions","Refer to a hepatobiliary and pancreatic multidisciplinary team for management decisions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","International (Fukuoka/Kyoto) consensus guidelines on IPMN of the pancreas","European evidence-based guidelines on pancreatic cystic neoplasms"],"crossLinks":[]},"iqsec2_encephalopathy":{"name":"IQSEC2-related developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IQSEC2","IQSEC2-related developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","IQSEC2-related X-linked intellectual disability"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by pathogenic variants in IQSEC2 on Xp11.22, encoding a brain-enriched guanine nucleotide exchange factor for the small GTPase ARF6 at excitatory postsynaptic densities.","Unusual for an X-linked gene: de novo variants cause severe encephalopathy in both sexes, and heterozygous girls are frequently affected as severely as hemizygous boys because the gene partially escapes X-inactivation and is dosage sensitive.","Phenotype spans a spectrum from milder non-syndromic intellectual disability (often inherited hypomorphic missense variants in males) to severe developmental and epileptic encephalopathy.","Rett-like features — hand stereotypies, developmental regression and acquired microcephaly — occur, especially in girls; consider IQSEC2 when MECP2, CDKL5 and FOXG1 testing is negative.","Epilepsy is common and frequently drug-resistant, with infantile spasms and multiple seizure types; the EEG may evolve towards a Lennox-Gastaut-like pattern.","Truncating variants tend to produce the most severe phenotypes, while pathogenic missense variants cluster in the IQ-like, catalytic Sec7 and PH domains.","Autism, stereotypies, self-injurious or aggressive behaviour, hypotonia, gait disturbance and disrupted sleep are frequent accompaniments.","Diagnosis rests on molecular testing (exome or an epilepsy/intellectual disability gene panel); management is supportive and symptomatic with no disease-specific therapy."],"pathophysiology":"IQSEC2 (Xp11.22) encodes IQ motif and Sec7 domain-containing protein 2, a guanine nucleotide exchange factor (GEF) for the small GTPase ARF6 (ADP-ribosylation factor 6). The protein is concentrated in the postsynaptic density of excitatory glutamatergic synapses, where it binds the scaffolding protein PSD-95 and, through cyclical activation of ARF6, governs actin cytoskeletal remodelling, dendritic spine morphology and the trafficking and internalisation of AMPA-type glutamate receptors — processes central to synaptic strength and plasticity. Loss-of-function (truncating) variants and missense variants within the IQ-like, catalytic Sec7 or pleckstrin homology domains disrupt this GEF activity and downstream synaptic signalling. Because IQSEC2 partially escapes X-inactivation and is dosage sensitive, heterozygous females carrying de novo variants can be as severely affected as hemizygous males, whereas some inherited hypomorphic missense alleles in males produce a milder, non-syndromic intellectual disability.","additionalDifferentials":["Rett syndrome (MECP2-related)","CDKL5 deficiency disorder","FOXG1-related congenital Rett variant","MECP2 duplication syndrome","Other X-linked intellectual disability disorders (e.g. ARX-related, Christianson syndrome/SLC9A6)","Angelman syndrome","Other genetic developmental and epileptic encephalopathies (STXBP1, SCN2A, SYNGAP1)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing — exome sequencing or a targeted epilepsy/intellectual disability gene panel including IQSEC2","Interictal and prolonged/video EEG for seizure and epilepsy-syndrome characterisation","Brain MRI (frequently normal or non-specific; helps exclude structural and alternative causes)","Structured developmental, neuropsychological and autism assessment","Parental segregation testing to establish de novo versus inherited status","X-inactivation studies in affected females as an interpretive adjunct","Targeted metabolic screen where clinically indicated to exclude mimics"],"redFlags":["New, worsening or prolonged drug-resistant seizures, including status epilepticus, requiring urgent review","Developmental regression or loss of previously acquired skills","Deceleration of head growth (acquired microcephaly)","Self-injurious behaviour or feeding and swallowing difficulty with aspiration risk"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable but often guarded: severe developmental and epileptic encephalopathy is associated with profound intellectual disability, absent speech and drug-resistant epilepsy, whereas inherited hypomorphic missense variants in males may cause milder, non-progressive intellectual disability. Long-term outlook is largely determined by seizure severity and associated complications.","pitfalls":["Assuming an X-linked gene spares females — girls with de novo IQSEC2 variants are frequently severely affected and may be mislabelled as Rett syndrome.","Overlooking IQSEC2 once first-line Rett/encephalopathy genes (MECP2, CDKL5, FOXG1) return negative.","Reporting missense variants of uncertain significance without domain and segregation context.","Attributing regression solely to seizures and missing the underlying genetic diagnosis.","Failing to identify and counsel carrier females in families with apparently non-syndromic X-linked intellectual disability."],"management":["Treat epilepsy with antiseizure medication, using hormonal therapy (corticotropin or corticosteroids) or vigabatrin for infantile spasms","Manage frequently drug-resistant seizures with rational combination therapy and consider the ketogenic diet","Provide developmental support through early intervention, physiotherapy, occupational therapy and augmentative communication support","Address Rett-like features, hand stereotypies and behavioural or autistic symptoms with tailored behavioural and supportive measures","Confirm the diagnosis by molecular genetic testing and characterise seizures with prolonged or video EEG","Offer genetic counselling with parental segregation testing to establish de novo versus inherited status and inform family planning","Coordinate multidisciplinary care under paediatric neurology with allied health and family support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics and neurogenetics reference texts","Paediatric epilepsy and developmental and epileptic encephalopathy consensus reviews","Peer-reviewed neurodevelopmental disorder review literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"irak4_deficiency":{"name":"IRAK-4 Deficiency","system":"Immunology / Inborn errors of immunity","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IRAK4","IRAK-4","Interleukin-1 receptor-associated kinase 4 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive deficiency of IRAK-4 impairs signalling through most Toll-like receptors (all except TLR3) and through the whole interleukin-1 receptor family.","Susceptibility is narrow but dangerous: recurrent invasive pyogenic bacterial infection, especially Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas aeruginosa.","The hallmark is a blunted inflammatory response, so patients may lack fever and have deceptively low acute-phase markers despite invasive infection.","Resistance to most viruses, fungi, parasites and other bacteria is preserved, unlike broad immunodeficiencies.","The risk of life-threatening infection is highest in infancy and early childhood and characteristically improves with age.","Routine immunological screening such as immunoglobulins and blood counts is often normal, so a high index of suspicion is essential.","Prophylactic antibiotics, immunoglobulin replacement and vaccination against encapsulated organisms reduce the infection burden."],"pathophysiology":"IRAK-4 is a serine/threonine kinase that acts immediately downstream of the adaptor MyD88 in Toll-like receptor signalling (all receptors except TLR3) and in interleukin-1 receptor family signalling. Biallelic loss-of-function variants abolish IRAK-4 kinase activity, preventing recruitment and activation of IRAK-1, downstream NF-kappaB and MAPK activation, and production of proinflammatory cytokines in response to Toll-like and interleukin-1 receptor stimulation. The resulting failure of early innate inflammatory responses explains susceptibility to invasive pyogenic and encapsulated bacteria and the strikingly attenuated fever and acute-phase response, while intact TLR3 and MyD88-independent pathways preserve antiviral and broader host defence. Susceptibility tends to narrow with age as adaptive immunity matures.","additionalDifferentials":["MyD88 deficiency (clinically indistinguishable)","Congenital or functional asplenia","Complement deficiency, particularly early and terminal components","Primary antibody deficiency","NEMO/IKBKG-related immunodeficiency","Severe congenital neutropenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic sequencing of IRAK4","Functional Toll-like and interleukin-1 receptor assays showing impaired cytokine responses to agonists","Whole-blood cytokine production tests after receptor stimulation","Baseline immunoglobulins, blood counts and complement to exclude alternative diagnoses","Blood film for splenic-function markers such as Howell-Jolly bodies","Prompt cultures and microbiological sampling during febrile or invasive episodes"],"redFlags":["Invasive pyogenic infection (bacteraemia, meningitis, deep abscess) with a paradoxically muted fever or acute-phase response","Recurrent invasive pneumococcal or staphylococcal disease in a young child","Rapid clinical deterioration despite minimal inflammatory markers"],"prognosis":"Mortality is highest in the first years of life during invasive episodes, but with prophylaxis, vaccination and prompt aggressive treatment the outlook improves markedly, and susceptibility wanes with age.","pitfalls":["Relying on fever or acute-phase markers to gauge severity, since both may be misleadingly low.","Assuming that normal immunoglobulins and blood counts exclude an inborn error of immunity.","Delaying empirical antibiotics while awaiting confirmatory signs of inflammation.","Overlooking the diagnosis in an older, less symptomatic patient who has a suggestive childhood history."],"management":["Give continuous antibacterial prophylaxis, typically with penicillin, through the high-risk years of infancy and early childhood","Immunise fully against encapsulated organisms with pneumococcal, Haemophilus influenzae type b and meningococcal conjugate vaccines","Treat any febrile or suspected invasive episode promptly and aggressively with empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics, since the inflammatory response is blunted and acute-phase markers may mislead","Educate the family to seek urgent medical care for any fever given the attenuated acute-phase response","Consider immunoglobulin replacement in selected patients with recurrent infection","Maintain a low threshold for cultures and microbiological sampling during episodes","Offer genetic counselling and family testing for this autosomal recessive condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity","Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies","ESID clinical guidance on innate immune defects"],"crossLinks":[]},"iridocorneal_endothelial_syndrome":{"name":"Iridocorneal endothelial (ICE) syndrome","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ICE syndrome","Chandler syndrome","essential (progressive) iris atrophy","Cogan-Reese (iris naevus) syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology / Cornea by Krachmer","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A spectrum of disorders caused by an abnormal, proliferative corneal endothelium that migrates across the angle and onto the iris","Typically unilateral and affecting middle-aged women, presenting with corneal oedema, iris changes, or secondary glaucoma","The three overlapping clinical variants are essential (progressive) iris atrophy, Chandler syndrome, and Cogan-Reese (iris naevus) syndrome","Essential iris atrophy shows marked iris thinning, hole formation, corectopia (pupil displacement), and pseudopolycoria","Chandler syndrome is dominated by endothelial change and corneal oedema with relatively mild iris atrophy","Cogan-Reese syndrome features pigmented iris nodules on the abnormal endothelial-lined membrane","Specular microscopy shows characteristic ICE cells with a dark-light reversal (epithelial-like) appearance of the endothelium","Management targets the two main problems: corneal oedema (medical measures then endothelial keratoplasty) and secondary angle-closure glaucoma (medical, laser, or surgical, often with drainage devices)"],"pathophysiology":"Iridocorneal endothelial syndrome is characterised by an acquired abnormality of the corneal endothelium, whose cells take on epithelial-like proliferative and migratory behaviour. These abnormal endothelial cells and an accompanying basement-membrane-like layer proliferate across the trabecular meshwork and over the anterior iris surface. Contraction of this membrane produces peripheral anterior synechiae, angle closure, iris stretching with corectopia, iris hole formation, and, in the Cogan-Reese variant, pigmented iris nodules. Blockage of the trabecular meshwork by the membrane and synechiae raises intraocular pressure, causing secondary glaucoma, while endothelial dysfunction leads to corneal oedema. The condition is typically unilateral and slowly progressive.","additionalDifferentials":["Posterior polymorphous corneal dystrophy","Fuchs endothelial dystrophy","Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome and other anterior segment dysgenesis","Iris melanoma or naevus","Peripheral anterior synechiae from chronic angle-closure or uveitis","Traumatic or surgical iris damage"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination documenting corneal oedema, iris atrophy, corectopia, and nodules","Specular or confocal microscopy showing abnormal ICE endothelial cells","Gonioscopy to assess peripheral anterior synechiae and angle closure","Intraocular pressure measurement and glaucoma assessment with optic nerve and visual field evaluation","Anterior segment optical coherence tomography or ultrasound biomicroscopy of the angle and iris membrane","Pachymetry to assess corneal oedema and guide surgical planning"],"redFlags":["Progressive secondary glaucoma with optic nerve damage and field loss","Corneal decompensation with worsening oedema and visual loss","Rapidly advancing iris destruction and corectopia","Features suggesting iris melanoma rather than the benign nodules of Cogan-Reese syndrome"],"prognosis":"The syndrome is slowly progressive and often difficult to control, with secondary glaucoma frequently requiring surgery and a tendency for corneal decompensation over time. Endothelial keratoplasty can restore corneal clarity, but glaucoma commonly limits the long-term visual outcome.","pitfalls":["Mistaking ICE syndrome for Fuchs or posterior polymorphous dystrophy, which are usually bilateral","Underestimating the aggressive, often refractory secondary glaucoma","Confusing Cogan-Reese iris nodules with iris melanoma","Attributing unilateral corneal oedema to primary endothelial dystrophy without gonioscopy and specular microscopy"],"management":["Lower intraocular pressure medically with aqueous suppressants such as topical beta-blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and alpha-agonists as first-line for the secondary glaucoma","Proceed to glaucoma surgery, either trabeculectomy with an antimetabolite or a glaucoma drainage device, when medical therapy fails, as it commonly does","Treat corneal decompensation with endothelial or penetrating keratoplasty to restore clarity","Relieve symptomatic corneal oedema initially with hypertonic saline drops alongside control of intraocular pressure","Monitor with intraocular pressure, optic nerve and visual field assessment, specular or confocal microscopy and pachymetry","Refer for combined glaucoma and corneal subspecialist management given the progressive, often refractory course"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Cornea by Krachmer, Mannis and Holland"],"crossLinks":[]},"iron_deficiency_anemia":{"name":"Iron Deficiency Anemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IDA"],"chapter":102,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.764","clinicalPearls":["Classic blood picture is a microcytic, hypochromic anemia (MCV <80 fL in established disease), but microcytosis can be ABSENT in early iron deficiency or masked by liver disease or concomitant folate/B12 deficiency (Harrison 22e p.765, p.450).","Low serum ferritin is the mainstay of absolute iron deficiency, reflecting depleted stores; ferritin <30 µg/L has high sensitivity and specificity (92% and 98%) and can be used as a single diagnostic test (Harrison 22e p.765).","Transferrin saturation (serum iron/TIBC) measures iron available for erythropoiesis; erythropoiesis becomes iron-restricted at values <15-20% (Harrison 22e p.765).","Anemia develops only in the LATE stage because the erythron is relatively spared compared with heart and skeletal muscle, which become iron-depleted earlier; slow progression plus compensation means anemia is often undetected, especially in the young, until hemoglobin falls below ~8 g/dL (Harrison 22e p.765).","Clinical clues include pica (compulsive ingestion of nonnutritive substances such as ice, soil, or clay), nail fragility and koilonychia, hair loss, cheilosis, atrophic glossitis, and dysphagia from esophageal webs (Plummer-Vinson syndrome) (Harrison 22e p.765; Koilonychia association noted p.~).","Even symptomatic isolated iron deficiency WITHOUT anemia matters: suboptimal myocyte, cardiomyocyte, and neuron function can cause fatigue, irritability, poor concentration, restless legs syndrome, and reduced exercise capacity (Harrison 22e p.765).","Severe iron-deficiency anemia may be accompanied by thrombocytosis, due to prevalent megakaryocyte commitment of the common erythroid-platelet progenitor when iron is insufficient (Harrison 22e p.766).","Serum hepcidin is low/undetectable in absolute iron deficiency (maximizing dietary iron absorption) but elevated in inflammation and in IRIDA (TMPRSS6 mutations) (Harrison 22e p.766).","Inadequate therapeutic response (no reticulocytosis after 1 week, or <1 g/dL hemoglobin rise after 2 weeks of iron therapy) should prompt a search for the cause: noncompliance, ongoing blood loss, or malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.766)."],"pathophysiology":"Iron supply becomes inadequate for needs through excessive demand, insufficient intake, defective absorption, or blood loss; multiple causes frequently co-occur, especially in the elderly and in multimorbidity (Harrison 22e p.764). In ISOLATED iron deficiency, stores are depleted while hemoglobin is still normal; when iron remains sufficient for erythropoiesis, red cells are normochromic/normocytic with decreased ferritin (<15-30 µg/L). As transferrin saturation falls <15-20%, iron supply to erythropoiesis is insufficient and cells become hypochromic and microcytic, the classic feature of iron-deficiency anemia (Harrison 22e p.764). Iron deficiency is ABSOLUTE when stores are exhausted and FUNCTIONAL when erythropoiesis is iron-restricted but stores are adequate or even increased. Low hepcidin in iron deficiency maximizes dietary absorption; TMPRSS6 mutations cause hereditary iron-refractory IDA (IRIDA) with reduced absorption and oral-iron refractoriness. In inflammation, high hepcidin sequesters iron into stores, producing functional iron deficiency with low serum iron and transferrin saturation despite elevated ferritin; absolute and functional deficiency often coexist (IBD, CKD, aging). Beyond anemia, heart, muscle, and brain are affected; immune response may be blunted as lymphocytes require iron during the metabolic burst (Harrison 22e p.764).","additionalDifferentials":["Anemia of inflammation (anemia of chronic disease) - the second most common anemia worldwide, due to functional iron deficiency; a diagnosis of exclusion usually occurring with an inflammatory disorder such as cancer or infection (Harrison 22e p.765, p.450).","Thalassemia trait - microcytosis with normal-to-high ferritin and serum iron; red cells are more homogeneous in volume with a low/normal RDW (Harrison 22e p.450, p.767).","Mixed forms (combined absolute and functional iron deficiency) (Harrison 22e p.767).","Other nutritional anemias - vitamin B12 and folate deficiency (megaloblastic, macrocytic); may coexist with and mask iron deficiency in malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.765).","Sideroblastic anemia (rare) as a cause of microcytic hypochromic red cells (Harrison 22e p.~)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anemia of inflammation / chronic disease (functional iron deficiency from high hepcidin: low serum iron and transferrin saturation with NORMAL-to-HIGH ferritin) (Harrison 22e p.764, p.767).","Thalassemia trait (microcytic but with normal-to-high ferritin and iron, and a low/normal RDW) (Harrison 22e p.450, p.767)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ferritin (mainstay; <30 µg/L diagnostic, sensitivity 92% / specificity 98%); note ferritin is an acute-phase reactant and may rise severalfold in inflammation (Harrison 22e p.765, p.450).","Transferrin saturation (serum iron/TIBC); iron-restricted erythropoiesis at <15-20% (Harrison 22e p.765).","Serum iron (reduced, but diurnal fluctuation prevents use as a single test) (Harrison 22e p.765).","MCV and RDW from the CBC (MCV <80 fL when established; RDW often elevated reflecting anisocytosis) (Harrison 22e p.765).","Soluble transferrin receptor (sTfR) - increased in iron deficiency; sTfR/log ferritin ratio >2 favors iron deficiency (Harrison 22e p.766, p.767).","Reticulocyte hemoglobin content (CHr, reduced) and percentage of hypochromic red cells (%HYPO, increased >6%), reflecting iron availability days to months before testing (Harrison 22e p.766, p.767).","Serum hepcidin (low/undetectable in absolute deficiency; not routinely measured, reference values not standardized) (Harrison 22e p.766, p.767).","Absence of stainable iron in bone marrow remains the gold standard, although it is rarely needed and unacceptably invasive (Harrison 22e p.765).","Endoscopy and/or radiographic imaging to identify the cause - essential in males and postmenopausal females to rule out malignancy or other severe bleeding sources (Harrison 22e p.765, p.~)."],"redFlags":["Unexplained iron-deficiency anemia in any adult (with the possible exception of a premenopausal, multiparous woman) MANDATES thorough endoscopic and/or radiographic visualization of the entire large bowel - right (ascending) colon cancer causes insidious blood loss and often presents as IDA (Harrison 22e p.~, colorectal cancer chapter).","Imaging and endoscopy to trace the cause are essential in males and postmenopausal females to rule out malignancy or other severe causes of bleeding (Harrison 22e p.765).","Hemoglobin falling below ~8 g/dL or otherwise severe anemia (Harrison 22e p.765).","Myelophthisic blood smear (nucleated red cells, teardrop cells, immature white cells) suggesting marrow infiltration mandates marrow examination - a competing cause of anemia to recognize (Harrison 22e p.450)."],"prognosis":"Iron deficiency and IDA are highly treatable with iron replacement. Oral iron usually requires at least ~3 months of therapy to restore hemoglobin and replenish stores and prevent early recurrence; inadequate response (no reticulocytosis at 1 week or <1 g/dL hemoglobin rise at 2 weeks) should prompt a search for noncompliance, ongoing blood loss, or malabsorption. Prognosis ultimately depends on the underlying cause (e.g., occult GI malignancy) rather than the anemia itself (Harrison 22e p.766).","pitfalls":["Classic microcytosis/hypochromia can be ABSENT in early iron deficiency, in elderly patients, or when masked by concomitant folate/B12 deficiency or liver disease - do not exclude IDA on a normal MCV alone (Harrison 22e p.765, p.450).","Ferritin is an acute-phase reactant; normal or high ferritin during inflammation can mask coexisting iron deficiency (Harrison 22e p.450).","Serum iron alone is unreliable as a single test due to diurnal variation (Harrison 22e p.765).","Treating IDA without investigating the cause can miss occult GI malignancy; isolated symptomatic iron deficiency without anemia should not be underestimated (Harrison 22e p.764, p.765).","Biomarkers other than ferritin and transferrin saturation are limited by poor standardization and accessibility (Harrison 22e p.766)."],"management":["Replace iron with oral iron salts such as ferrous sulphate, fumarate or gluconate as first-line, continuing for months after the haemoglobin normalises to replenish stores and prevent early recurrence","Use intravenous iron such as ferric carboxymaltose or iron sucrose when oral iron is not tolerated, is ineffective, absorption is impaired, or in functional iron deficiency","Identify and treat the underlying cause, investigating for gastrointestinal blood loss, menstrual loss, malabsorption including coeliac disease, and dietary inadequacy","Reserve red cell transfusion for severe symptomatic anaemia or haemodynamic compromise","Monitor the response with reticulocytosis and haemoglobin rise, and reassess adherence, absorption and ongoing loss if the response is inadequate","Optimise dietary iron intake and counsel on taking oral iron with vitamin C and away from tea and calcium to improve absorption","Refer at-risk adults for endoscopic evaluation to exclude a gastrointestinal malignancy"],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes a continuum rather than a formal grading scale: (1) isolated/absolute iron deficiency with depleted stores (ferritin <15-30 µg/L) but NORMAL hemoglobin and normochromic-normocytic cells; (2) iron-restricted erythropoiesis once transferrin saturation falls <15-20%, with hypochromic microcytic cells; (3) overt iron-deficiency anemia. Severe anemia is generally taken as hemoglobin <=8 g/dL (the threshold at which anemia commonly becomes detectable and at which rapid IV correction is considered) (Harrison 22e p.764-766).","references":["Harrison 22e p.764","Harrison 22e p.765","Harrison 22e p.766","Harrison 22e p.767","Harrison 22e p.450","Camaschella C: Iron deficiency. Blood 133:30, 2019"],"crossLinks":["colorectal_cancer"]},"iron_poisoning":{"name":"Iron poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["iron overdose","iron toxicity","ferrous sulfate poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Toxicity relates to the amount of elemental iron ingested, not the total salt weight, so the specific preparation must be identified","The classic course has recognised stages: early GI toxicity, a deceptive latent phase of apparent improvement, then shock and metabolic acidosis, hepatic failure, and finally late gastric scarring and outlet obstruction","Early severe vomiting, diarrhoea, and haematemesis reflect direct corrosive injury to the gut mucosa","Do not be misled by the quiescent second phase; serious systemic toxicity can still evolve after the patient seems to settle","Radiopaque tablets may be visible on an abdominal radiograph and can confirm ingestion and guide decontamination","Whole-bowel irrigation is the preferred decontamination method for significant ingestions, as activated charcoal does not bind iron","Deferoxamine is the specific chelating antidote, indicated for systemic toxicity, shock, significant acidosis, or high serum iron levels","A high anion-gap metabolic acidosis, hypotension, and progressive hepatic injury mark severe poisoning requiring urgent chelation and intensive support"],"pathophysiology":"In overdose, free iron overwhelms the body's binding capacity (transferrin) and circulates unbound. It is directly corrosive to the gastrointestinal mucosa, causing haemorrhagic gastroenteritis and fluid loss. Absorbed free iron is a potent catalyst of free-radical formation and disrupts mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation, causing cellular injury most prominently in the liver, along with a high anion-gap metabolic (lactic) acidosis. Iron-induced vasodilatation, increased capillary permeability, and myocardial depression produce shock. Healing of the corrosive gastric injury can later cause strictures and gastric outlet obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Other corrosive or caustic ingestions","Acute gastroenteritis of infective cause","Other causes of high anion-gap metabolic acidosis","Paracetamol or other hepatotoxic ingestion","Sepsis with shock"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum iron level, ideally timed to peak absorption","Abdominal radiograph for radiopaque tablets","Arterial blood gas with anion gap and lactate","Liver function tests and coagulation profile to track hepatic injury","Full blood count, electrolytes, renal function, and glucose"],"redFlags":["Haematemesis or melaena indicating corrosive GI injury","Hypotension, shock, or lethargy signalling systemic toxicity","High anion-gap metabolic acidosis with elevated lactate","Evidence of hepatic injury or coagulopathy","Persistent or worsening symptoms after the apparent latent phase"],"prognosis":"Small ingestions causing only GI upset usually resolve, but severe poisoning with shock, acidosis, and hepatic failure carries significant mortality; early recognition, decontamination, and deferoxamine chelation greatly improve survival, though late gastric strictures may still occur.","pitfalls":["Calculating dose from tablet weight rather than elemental iron content","Discharging a patient during the deceptive latent phase before systemic toxicity manifests","Giving activated charcoal, which does not adsorb iron","Delaying deferoxamine in a patient with shock, acidosis, or high iron levels"],"management":["Resuscitate aggressively with intravenous fluids to counter corrosive gastrointestinal losses and iron-induced vasodilatory shock","Decontaminate the gut with whole-bowel irrigation for significant ingestions, since activated charcoal does not bind iron, confirming clearance with abdominal radiography","Chelate with intravenous deferoxamine in severe poisoning marked by shock, metabolic acidosis, high serum iron or significant symptoms","Correct the metabolic acidosis and support evolving hepatic failure and coagulopathy","Monitor closely through the deceptive latent phase, as systemic toxicity can progress after apparent improvement","Anticipate and manage late gastric outlet obstruction from strictures with endoscopy or surgery","Involve the poisons information service and, in children, assess safeguarding and future prevention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"irritable_bowel_syndrome":{"name":"Irritable bowel syndrome","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IBS","spastic colon"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rome IV criteria / gastroenterology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disorder of gut-brain interaction defined by recurrent abdominal pain related to defecation and associated with a change in stool frequency or form","It is subclassified by predominant bowel habit into constipation-predominant, diarrhoea-predominant, mixed and unclassified types","It is a positive clinical diagnosis based on symptom criteria with limited targeted testing, not merely a diagnosis of exclusion","Alarm features such as weight loss, rectal bleeding, anaemia, older age of onset or a family history of bowel cancer warrant investigation","Coeliac serology and, where indicated, faecal calprotectin help exclude coeliac disease and inflammatory bowel disease","Management combines dietary measures, symptom-directed pharmacotherapy and psychological therapies"],"pathophysiology":"Irritable bowel syndrome is a disorder of gut-brain interaction in which altered gastrointestinal motility, visceral hypersensitivity, changes in the gut microbiota, low-grade mucosal immune activation, and disturbed central processing of visceral signals combine to produce pain and altered bowel habit. Bidirectional signalling along the gut-brain axis, often modulated by stress and psychological factors, amplifies visceral perception. There is no structural or biochemical abnormality that defines the condition, which is characterised instead by its symptom pattern.","additionalDifferentials":["Coeliac disease","Inflammatory bowel disease","Microscopic colitis","Bile acid diarrhoea","Colorectal cancer","Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Symptom-based diagnosis using recognised criteria","Coeliac serology","Faecal calprotectin where inflammatory bowel disease is a concern","Full blood count and inflammatory markers","Colonoscopy or further imaging when alarm features are present"],"redFlags":["Rectal bleeding or iron-deficiency anaemia","Unintentional weight loss","New symptoms in an older patient","Family history of colorectal cancer or inflammatory bowel disease","Nocturnal symptoms waking the patient"],"prognosis":"The condition is chronic and relapsing but benign, and most patients achieve worthwhile symptom control with an individualised combination of diet, medication and psychological approaches.","pitfalls":["Treating it purely as a diagnosis of exclusion and over-investigating","Missing coeliac disease or inflammatory bowel disease presenting similarly","Ignoring alarm features","Overlooking the role of psychological factors and the gut-brain axis in management"],"management":["Make a positive diagnosis on symptom criteria and exclude alarm features, coeliac disease and inflammatory bowel disease with targeted testing","Begin with dietary and lifestyle measures including regular meals, fibre adjustment and a supervised trial of a low-FODMAP diet with a dietitian","Direct pharmacotherapy at the predominant symptom, using antispasmodics for pain, laxatives for constipation and loperamide for diarrhoea","Add a gut-brain neuromodulator such as a tricyclic antidepressant or an SSRI for refractory pain","Consider disease-specific agents such as linaclotide for constipation-predominant disease and eluxadoline or a bile-acid sequestrant for diarrhoea-predominant disease","Offer psychological therapies such as cognitive behavioural therapy or gut-directed hypnotherapy for persistent symptoms","Provide reassurance within a positive therapeutic relationship and review periodically"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rome IV diagnostic criteria","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"irukandji_syndrome":{"name":"Irukandji syndrome","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Carukia barnesi sting","Irukandji jellyfish envenomation","irukandji","cubozoan catecholamine syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / WHO envenoming guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by small cubozoans (classically Carukia barnesi); the initial sting is often trivial or barely noticed","After a delay of roughly 20-40 minutes, patients develop severe generalised pain, especially in the back, chest and abdomen, with muscle cramps","A striking catecholamine surge produces sweating, agitation, hypertension, tachycardia, nausea, vomiting and a sense of impending doom","Serious complications include hypertensive emergency, myocardial dysfunction (stress cardiomyopathy) and potentially fatal pulmonary oedema","Vinegar is applied to the sting site to inactivate undischarged nematocysts, but the syndrome is driven by systemic venom effects rather than local injury","Management principles are aggressive analgesia, antiemetics, and control of hypertension and cardiac dysfunction with monitoring in a high-dependency setting","There is no specific antivenom; treatment is supportive and directed at the hyperadrenergic state and its cardiac consequences"],"pathophysiology":"The venom of Irukandji-type jellyfish triggers a massive endogenous release of catecholamines, producing a delayed systemic syndrome disproportionate to the minor local sting. Sympathetic overactivity causes severe pain, hypertension, tachycardia and diaphoresis, while catecholamine excess can injure the myocardium, producing a stress (takotsubo-type) cardiomyopathy with left ventricular dysfunction. Cardiac failure and capillary changes can lead to acute pulmonary oedema, and the profound autonomic activation accounts for the anxiety and feeling of doom that characterise the syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute coronary syndrome","Phaeochromocytoma crisis or sympathomimetic toxidrome","Box jellyfish (Chironex) envenomation","Acute abdomen or renal colic","Decompression illness in a diver","Subarachnoid haemorrhage given severe pain and hypertension"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electrocardiography for ischaemia and arrhythmia","Serial cardiac troponin and echocardiography for stress cardiomyopathy","Blood pressure monitoring for hypertensive emergency","Chest imaging for pulmonary oedema","Electrolytes, renal function and glucose"],"redFlags":["Severe or refractory hypertension with end-organ features","Evidence of myocardial injury or pulmonary oedema","Progressive dyspnoea or hypoxia","Altered consciousness or neurological deficit","Haemodynamic instability"],"prognosis":"Most cases resolve over hours to a couple of days with supportive care, but a minority develop life-threatening cardiac failure or pulmonary oedema, and deaths have occurred, so cardiac monitoring is essential.","pitfalls":["Discharging early after a trivial-looking sting before the delayed systemic syndrome has been excluded","Failing to anticipate and monitor for venom-induced cardiomyopathy and pulmonary oedema","Providing inadequate analgesia for what is often extreme pain","Overlooking hypertensive emergency requiring controlled blood pressure reduction"],"management":["Apply vinegar to the sting site to inactivate any undischarged nematocysts","Provide aggressive parenteral analgesia, typically with opioids, together with antiemetics","Control the catecholamine-driven hypertension with vasodilators or short-acting antihypertensive agents, and consider magnesium sulphate","Monitor in a high-dependency setting with continuous cardiac rhythm and blood pressure monitoring","Detect and manage stress (takotsubo-type) cardiomyopathy using serial troponin, electrocardiography and echocardiography with supportive cardiac care","Support respiration and treat acute pulmonary oedema promptly if it develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Australian marine envenomation management guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"isaacs_syndrome":{"name":"Isaacs Syndrome","system":"Neurology / Neuro-immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Acquired neuromyotonia","Peripheral nerve hyperexcitability syndrome","Continuous muscle fibre activity syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disorder of peripheral nerve hyperexcitability characterised by continuous muscle fibre activity that persists during sleep and general anaesthesia.","The clinical hallmarks are muscle twitching (myokymia), cramps, stiffness and delayed relaxation, with visible rippling movements described as myokymia and often excessive sweating.","Electromyography is central to diagnosis, showing spontaneous myokymic and neuromyotonic discharges with a characteristic high intraburst firing frequency.","Frequently autoimmune, associated with antibodies to components of the voltage-gated potassium channel complex, particularly CASPR2 and sometimes LGI1.","May be paraneoplastic, with thymoma the classic association, so a thymic and broader malignancy search is important, especially with CASPR2 antibodies.","When peripheral nerve hyperexcitability combines with dysautonomia and encephalopathy or insomnia, consider Morvan syndrome, a related CASPR2-associated disorder.","Symptomatic relief comes from membrane-stabilising anticonvulsants such as carbamazepine or phenytoin that reduce ectopic nerve firing.","Immunotherapy including plasma exchange, intravenous immunoglobulin or immunosuppression benefits antibody-positive and refractory cases."],"pathophysiology":"Isaacs syndrome results from hyperexcitability of the motor nerve, most prominently at its distal, terminal arborisation, where sustained ectopic and repetitive firing generates continuous involuntary muscle fibre activity. In the autoimmune form, antibodies are directed against proteins of the voltage-gated potassium channel complex, chiefly the axonal protein CASPR2 and its partner LGI1, rather than against the channel pore itself; loss of the normal potassium conductance that repolarises and stabilises the axonal membrane lowers the threshold for firing and permits spontaneous, self-sustaining discharges. The persistence of activity during sleep, proximal nerve block and general anaesthesia localises the generator to the peripheral nerve rather than central circuits, and its abolition by neuromuscular junction blockade confirms that the final common pathway is transmission across the junction to muscle. Continuous firing produces the clinical myokymia, cramps, impaired relaxation and pseudomyotonia, while associated autonomic overactivity, particularly hyperhidrosis, reflects involvement of autonomic axons. The frequent link with thymoma and other tumours indicates a paraneoplastic trigger of the antibody response in a subset, and when the same CASPR2 immunity extends to the central and autonomic nervous systems the broader Morvan phenotype of insomnia, dysautonomia and encephalopathy emerges.","additionalDifferentials":["Cramp-fasciculation syndrome","Morvan syndrome","Stiff-person syndrome","Motor neurone disease with prominent fasciculation and cramps","Myotonic disorders such as myotonic dystrophy or myotonia congenita","Tetany from hypocalcaemia or hypomagnesaemia","Hereditary or toxic peripheral nerve hyperexcitability"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electromyography demonstrating myokymic and neuromyotonic discharges with high intraburst frequency","Serum CASPR2 and LGI1 (voltage-gated potassium channel complex) antibodies","Cross-sectional imaging of the chest for thymoma and broader tumour screening","Nerve conduction studies to characterise any coexisting peripheral neuropathy","Creatine kinase, which may be mildly raised from continuous muscle activity","Serum electrolytes including calcium and magnesium to exclude metabolic causes","Autoimmune and paraneoplastic antibody panel where Morvan features are present"],"redFlags":["Features of Morvan syndrome with severe insomnia, confusion and autonomic instability","Identification of an underlying thymoma or other malignancy requiring urgent management","Bulbar or respiratory muscle involvement with laryngospasm compromising the airway","Rapidly progressive weakness or wasting suggesting an alternative motor neurone disorder","Severe autonomic disturbance with cardiac dysrhythmia or marked blood pressure swings"],"prognosis":"Symptoms are often controllable with membrane-stabilising drugs, and antibody-positive or paraneoplastic cases frequently improve with immunotherapy or tumour treatment. The overall outlook is strongly influenced by any underlying malignancy and by whether the illness extends to the Morvan phenotype.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the continuous activity for a primary myotonic or muscle disorder rather than nerve hyperexcitability","Failing to search for thymoma, particularly in CASPR2-antibody-positive patients","Overlooking evolution into Morvan syndrome when insomnia and autonomic features appear","Attributing myokymia and cramps to benign fasciculation and missing the wider syndrome","Neglecting immunotherapy in antibody-positive disease that is refractory to symptomatic drugs"],"management":["Control the muscle hyperexcitability symptomatically with membrane-stabilising anticonvulsants such as carbamazepine or phenytoin","Treat the autoimmune process with immunotherapy using corticosteroids, intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange in antibody-positive disease","Escalate to steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as azathioprine for refractory or relapsing disease","Search for and treat an underlying tumour, especially thymoma, with resection","Confirm the diagnosis with electromyography showing myokymic and neuromyotonic discharges and with CASPR2 and LGI1 antibody testing","Recognise and manage the Morvan phenotype with its dysautonomia and encephalopathy if the illness extends beyond the peripheral nerve"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Peripheral Neuropathy (Dyck and Thomas)"],"crossLinks":[]},"ischaemic_colitis":{"name":"Ischaemic colitis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["colonic ischaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Gastroenterology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest form of intestinal ischaemia, usually a transient, non-occlusive reduction in colonic perfusion","Presents with sudden crampy abdominal pain followed by bloody diarrhoea, typically in older patients","Watershed areas such as the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction are most vulnerable","It is usually self-limiting, unlike acute mesenteric ischaemia of the small bowel, which is a surgical emergency","CT may show colonic wall thickening, and colonoscopy shows segmental mucosal changes","Most cases resolve with supportive care, but gangrenous or fulminant colitis needs surgery"],"pathophysiology":"Ischaemic colitis results from a temporary compromise of colonic blood flow that is insufficient to meet metabolic demand, most often non-occlusive and related to a low-flow state, with the poorly collateralised watershed regions most affected. Mucosal injury ranges from reversible superficial changes to transmural infarction. Precipitants include hypotension, hypovolaemia, cardiac failure, vasoconstrictors and, in younger patients, causes such as vasculitis, thrombophilia, cocaine use and strenuous exertion.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute mesenteric ischaemia (small bowel)","Infective colitis","Inflammatory bowel disease","Diverticulitis","Colorectal malignancy","Pseudomembranous colitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT of the abdomen with contrast","Colonoscopy showing segmental changes (avoided if peritonitis)","Blood tests including lactate and inflammatory markers","Stool studies to exclude infection","Assessment for a precipitating low-flow or thrombotic cause"],"redFlags":["Peritonitis or signs of transmural infarction","Rising lactate, acidosis or systemic toxicity","Right-sided (isolated) ischaemic colitis, which behaves more aggressively","Failure to improve, suggesting gangrene or stricture"],"prognosis":"Most transient cases resolve within days with supportive care; gangrenous or fulminant colitis carries significant mortality, and some develop a chronic segment or stricture.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with acute mesenteric ischaemia and delaying surgery when the small bowel is involved","Mistaking it for infective colitis or inflammatory bowel disease","Underestimating isolated right colonic ischaemia","Performing colonoscopy in a patient with peritonitis"],"management":["Manage most non-gangrenous cases conservatively with bowel rest, intravenous fluids and correction of the precipitating low-flow state","Give broad-spectrum antibiotics for moderate-to-severe disease","Optimise haemodynamics and review and withdraw offending vasoconstrictor or precipitating drugs","Proceed to surgical resection for gangrenous, fulminant or perforated colitis or peritonitis","Monitor with serial abdominal examination and lactate and inflammatory markers to detect deterioration","Investigate and treat any underlying cardiac, vascular or thrombophilic cause","Follow up for the late complications of chronic segmental colitis or stricture, which may need endoscopic or surgical treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Gastroenterology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"ischaemic_hepatitis":{"name":"ischaemic hepatitis","system":"hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["shock liver","hypoxic hepatitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Ischaemic hepatitis, or shock liver, is diffuse hepatocellular injury caused by inadequate oxygen delivery to the liver rather than by a primary hepatic disease.","Cardiac failure with hepatic venous congestion is the most frequent setting, while circulatory shock, severe hypoxaemia and sepsis are other important precipitants.","The biochemical hallmark is a massive, abrupt rise in serum transaminases that peaks quickly and then falls rapidly once perfusion is restored.","A markedly elevated lactate dehydrogenase with a low ALT-to-LDH ratio helps distinguish it from acute viral hepatitis.","Mild jaundice, a transient coagulopathy and acute kidney injury commonly accompany the transaminase spike.","The diagnosis is clinical and biochemical, and liver biopsy, rarely needed, shows centrilobular zone 3 necrosis.","Management is directed entirely at restoring haemodynamics and treating the underlying cardiac or circulatory disorder rather than the liver itself.","A precipitating haemodynamic event is not always overt, and cardiac failure can cause the picture without a documented episode of hypotension."],"pathophysiology":"Hepatocytes in the centrilobular zone, acinar zone 3, lie furthest from the incoming oxygenated blood and are the most metabolically active, making them exquisitely sensitive to hypoxia. Ischaemic hepatitis develops when oxygen delivery falls below hepatic demand through reduced cardiac output, systemic hypotension, passive venous congestion, arterial hypoxaemia or a combination of these. Congestive cardiac failure is especially important because raised central venous pressure produces hepatic congestion that both lowers effective perfusion pressure and sensitises the parenchyma, so that even a modest fall in output precipitates injury. The resulting zone 3 necrosis releases large quantities of intracellular enzymes, generating the characteristic transaminase and lactate dehydrogenase surge, and a reperfusion component may follow restoration of flow. Because the insult is typically brief and the hepatic architecture is preserved, enzymes normalise rapidly and function usually recovers once the circulation is corrected.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute viral hepatitis","Paracetamol or other drug-induced hepatotoxicity","Acute Budd-Chiari syndrome","Hepatic infarction","Autoimmune hepatitis flare","Acute biliary obstruction","Heat stroke-related liver injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial liver function tests demonstrating the rapid rise and fall in transaminases","Serum lactate dehydrogenase with calculation of the ALT-to-LDH ratio","Echocardiography and assessment of cardiac output","Arterial blood gas and lactate","Coagulation profile and renal function","Viral hepatitis and paracetamol screen to exclude alternatives","Doppler assessment of the hepatic vessels where outflow obstruction is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive rise in bilirubin and INR after transaminases have peaked, suggesting evolving liver failure","Persistent shock or refractory hypotension","Encephalopathy or other features of acute liver failure","Concurrent multi-organ dysfunction"],"prognosis":"The liver injury itself usually resolves quickly once perfusion is restored, but overall prognosis is poor and is driven by the severity of the underlying cardiac, circulatory or respiratory illness.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the transaminase spike as acute viral or drug-induced hepatitis and missing the haemodynamic cause.","Waiting for a documented episode of hypotension, which may be absent in congestive cardiac failure.","Focusing on hepatic management rather than urgently correcting the underlying circulatory problem.","Attributing the accompanying acute kidney injury to a separate process."],"management":["Restore hepatic perfusion by treating the underlying cause, optimising cardiac output, managing heart failure and reversing shock and hypoxaemia","Support the circulation with fluids and inotropes or vasopressors as appropriate, and relieve hepatic venous congestion in cardiac failure with diuretics","Correct hypoxaemia with supplemental oxygen and ventilatory support where needed","Provide supportive care for the accompanying transient coagulopathy and acute kidney injury","Avoid or dose-adjust hepatotoxic and hepatically cleared drugs during the injury","Monitor with serial liver function tests, expecting rapid resolution once perfusion is restored, and exclude viral hepatitis and paracetamol toxicity as alternatives","Escalate to critical care, since the prognosis is driven by the severity of the underlying cardiac, circulatory or respiratory illness"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"ischemic_colitis":{"name":"Ischaemic colitis","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ischemic colitis","colonic ischaemia","colonic ischemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AASLD & ACG","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest form of intestinal ischaemia, usually affecting older adults and typically arising from a transient, non-occlusive fall in colonic perfusion rather than a fixed arterial blockage","Classic presentation is sudden mild crampy left-sided abdominal pain followed within a day by the passage of bloody or maroon stool, with only modest blood loss","The watershed zones (splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction) are most vulnerable, while the rectum is usually spared because of its dual blood supply","CT often shows segmental bowel-wall thickening, and colonoscopy is the key diagnostic test showing segmental oedema, haemorrhage, and mucosal ulceration with an abrupt transition to normal mucosa","Most cases are non-gangrenous and settle with supportive care: bowel rest, intravenous fluids, and correction of any precipitating hypoperfusion","Right-sided (isolated right colon) involvement carries a worse prognosis and should raise concern for coexisting acute mesenteric ischaemia","Distinguish from infective colitis and inflammatory bowel disease; the segmental distribution and rapid resolution favour an ischaemic cause","Recognised triggers include hypotension, cardiac or vascular surgery, dehydration, hypercoagulable states, and certain drugs such as vasoconstrictors"],"pathophysiology":"Ischaemic colitis results from a temporary reduction in blood flow to a colonic segment, most often a low-flow (non-occlusive) event superimposed on the marginal circulation of watershed areas. Reduced perfusion injures the mucosa first, producing oedema, submucosal haemorrhage, and superficial ulceration. In most patients reperfusion allows healing, but persistent or severe ischaemia can progress to transmural (gangrenous) necrosis with perforation or, later, to fibrotic stricture. It is usually a disease of small vessels and systemic hypoperfusion rather than a large-artery occlusion, which distinguishes it from acute mesenteric ischaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute mesenteric ischaemia (small bowel)","Infective (bacterial) colitis","Inflammatory bowel disease, especially Crohn colitis","Diverticular haemorrhage or diverticulitis","Colorectal malignancy","Pseudomembranous (Clostridioides difficile) colitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT of the abdomen and pelvis to assess segmental wall thickening and exclude perforation","Colonoscopy or flexible sigmoidoscopy showing segmental mucosal injury with an abrupt transition zone","Serum lactate and inflammatory markers to gauge severity and detect possible gangrene","Stool studies to exclude infective causes when the picture is atypical","Cardiac and vascular assessment to identify a precipitating low-flow state"],"redFlags":["Peritonism, fever, marked leucocytosis, or rising lactate suggesting gangrenous colitis and impending perforation","Isolated right-sided colonic involvement, which may signal concurrent acute mesenteric ischaemia","Persistent bleeding, haemodynamic instability, or failure to improve on supportive care","Development of a colonic stricture with obstruction during recovery"],"prognosis":"Non-gangrenous ischaemic colitis usually resolves within a few days with conservative management and has a good outcome. Gangrenous disease requiring surgery carries substantial mortality, and right-sided involvement predicts a worse course. A minority develop chronic segmental colitis or a stricture.","pitfalls":["Assuming rectal sparing excludes colonic ischaemia, or mistaking segmental ischaemic changes for inflammatory bowel disease","Delaying surgical review when signs of transmural necrosis are present","Overlooking a precipitating hypoperfusion event that will otherwise recur","Failing to recognise that isolated right colon disease may reflect small-bowel mesenteric ischaemia needing urgent vascular evaluation"],"management":["Treat most non-gangrenous cases conservatively with bowel rest, intravenous fluid resuscitation and correction of the precipitating hypoperfusion","Give broad-spectrum antibiotics in moderate-to-severe disease","Optimise the circulation and review offending vasoconstrictor or precipitating medications","Proceed urgently to surgical resection for gangrenous, fulminant or perforated colitis, and hold heightened concern for coexisting acute mesenteric ischaemia in isolated right-colon disease","Monitor with serial examination and lactate and inflammatory markers to detect transmural progression","Identify and address an underlying cardiac, vascular or thrombophilic low-flow cause","Follow up for the development of chronic segmental colitis or a fibrotic stricture requiring later intervention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AASLD/ACG guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"ischemic_heart_disease":{"name":"Ischemic Heart Disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IHD","Chronic Coronary Disease","Chronic Coronary Syndrome","Coronary Artery Disease","Stable Angina Pectoris"],"chapter":284,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2090","clinicalPearls":["IHD reflects an imbalance between myocardial oxygen supply and demand; the dominant cause is atherosclerotic narrowing of one or more epicardial coronary arteries reducing regional myocardial perfusion (Harrison 22e p.2090).","Anginal discomfort is classically described as heaviness, pressure, squeezing, smothering or choking rather than frank pain, is crescendo-decrescendo, typically lasts 2-5 minutes, and may be signaled by a clenched fist over the sternum (Levine's sign) (Harrison 22e p.2092).","Anginal pain may radiate to either shoulder, both arms (especially the ulnar forearm/hand), back, interscapular region, neck, jaw, teeth, or epigastrium; it is rarely localized below the umbilicus or above the mandible (Harrison 22e p.2092).","Radiation to the trapezius muscles is NOT typical of myocardial ischemia and points more toward pericarditis; this is a useful discriminating feature (Harrison 22e p.2092).","Anginal equivalents (dyspnea, nausea, fatigue, faintness) and atypical presentations are more common in women, the elderly, and patients with diabetes mellitus, in whom angina may not relate strictly to provoking factors (Harrison 22e p.2093).","A resting 12-lead ECG may be entirely normal in typical angina; ST-T and LVH changes are suggestive but nonspecific, whereas dynamic ST-T changes appearing with an anginal episode and resolving afterward are more specific (Harrison 22e p.2094).","A stenosis must reduce epicardial luminal diameter by about 50% to limit flow augmentation on demand, and by roughly 80% before resting flow falls; beyond that, small further reductions cut flow dramatically (Harrison 22e p.2091).","Reversible ischemic injury occurs within roughly 20 minutes of total occlusion (without collaterals); occlusion beyond 20 minutes leads to myocardial necrosis (Harrison 22e p.2091).","The Canadian Cardiovascular Society class grades angina severity and the New York Heart Association class grades functional limitation (Harrison 22e p.2093).","An ischemic stress-ECG response is flat or downsloping ST depression >0.1 mV lasting >0.08 s; upsloping or junctional ST changes are not diagnostic, and a test failing to reach 85% of age/sex-predicted maximal heart rate is nondiagnostic (Harrison 22e p.2094-2095).","Chest wall tenderness, pain reproduced by palpation, or pain localizable with a single fingertip makes a myocardial ischemic origin unlikely (Harrison 22e p.2093-2094).","Most patients who die suddenly from IHD do so from ischemia-induced ventricular tachyarrhythmias (Harrison 22e p.2092)."],"pathophysiology":"Myocardial oxygen demand (MVO2) is governed by heart rate, contractility, and wall tension, while supply depends on oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood and coronary blood flow (predominantly diastolic). Coronary resistance resides mainly in prearteriolar (R2) and arteriolar/capillary (R3) microvascular beds, which normally dilate to match demand (metabolic regulation and autoregulation). Atherosclerosis of epicardial arteries, driven by endothelial dysfunction from high LDL, smoking, hypertension, and diabetes, produces subintimal plaques; plaque rupture or erosion exposes contents to blood, triggering platelet activation/aggregation and the coagulation cascade with fibrin deposition and thrombus formation. As proximal stenoses worsen, distal resistance vessels dilate maximally and flow becomes pressure-dependent, so increased demand (exertion, emotion, tachycardia) precipitates ischemia. Ischemia causes regional contractile dysfunction (hypokinesis to dyskinesis), impaired relaxation, a metabolic shift to anaerobic lactate production with falling intracellular pH and high-energy phosphate depletion, membrane dysfunction with potassium leak and cytosolic calcium rise, characteristic ECG repolarization changes, and electrical instability. The subendocardium is most vulnerable to underperfusion (Harrison 22e p.2090-2092).","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic stenosis with severe LVH causing subendocardial ischemia and angina without coronary atherosclerosis (Harrison 22e p.2091)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (Harrison 22e p.2093)","Aortic regurgitation and pulmonary hypertension as causes of angina-like chest discomfort (Harrison 22e p.2093)","Microvascular angina / abnormal cardiac nociception in patients with ischemic-type chest pain but no flow-limiting epicardial obstruction, more common in women (Harrison 22e p.2093)","Vasospastic (variant) angina due to coronary spasm (Harrison 22e p.2091, 2093)","Pericarditis (suggested by discomfort radiating to the trapezius) (Harrison 22e p.2092)","Esophageal/gastrointestinal disease and musculoskeletal chest wall pain (Harrison 22e p.2093-2094)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Aortic stenosis (subendocardial ischemia)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Pericarditis","Esophageal disease","Chest wall / musculoskeletal pain reproducible on palpation"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting lipid panel (total, LDL, HDL cholesterol, triglycerides, lipoprotein(a)) (Harrison 22e p.2094)","Glucose / hemoglobin A1c, creatinine, hematocrit; urinalysis including microalbuminuria; thyroid function if indicated (Harrison 22e p.2094)","High-sensitivity C-reactive protein (1-3 mg/L is an independent risk marker, most useful for reclassifying intermediate-risk patients) (Harrison 22e p.2094)","Resting 12-lead ECG (Harrison 22e p.2094)","Chest x-ray for cardiac enlargement, ventricular aneurysm, or heart failure signs (Harrison 22e p.2094)","Exercise treadmill ECG stress testing (sensitivity about 75%) (Harrison 22e p.2094-2095)","Stress myocardial perfusion imaging (thallium-201 or 99m-technetium sestamibi SPECT) or PET (N-13 ammonia, rubidium-82) with exercise or pharmacologic (adenosine, dobutamine) stress (Harrison 22e p.2095)","Stress echocardiography and cardiac MR stress testing (Harrison 22e p.2095-2096)","Coronary artery calcium scoring (Agatston score) by EBCT/MDCT as adjunctive, not primary screening (Harrison 22e p.2095-2097)","Coronary CT angiography for intermediate pretest probability and plaque assessment (Harrison 22e p.2097)","Invasive coronary arteriography (with FFR/iwFR) per ISCHEMIA-informed indications (Harrison 22e p.2097-2098)"],"redFlags":["Angina occurring with less exertion than before, at rest, or awakening the patient from sleep (features of an unstable syndrome) (Harrison 22e p.2093)","Angina accompanied by symptoms or signs of heart failure, pulmonary edema, transient third heart sound, or mitral regurgitation (Harrison 22e p.2092, 2097)","Inability to exercise beyond 6 minutes / stage II of the Bruce protocol (Harrison 22e p.2097)","Ischemia at low workload, >=0.2 mV ST depression, or ST depression persisting >5 minutes after exercise (Harrison 22e p.2097)","A fall in systolic blood pressure >10 mmHg during exercise or exercise-induced ventricular tachyarrhythmia (Harrison 22e p.2096-2097)","Large or multiple perfusion defects, increased lung tracer uptake, or a fall in LV ejection fraction during stress imaging (Harrison 22e p.2097)","Reduced resting LV ejection fraction (<0.40) (Harrison 22e p.2097)","Sudden cardiac death may be the unheralded presenting manifestation of IHD (Harrison 22e p.2092)"],"prognosis":"Principal prognostic determinants are age, left ventricular function, the location and severity of coronary stenoses, and the severity/activity of ischemia. Recent-onset or unstable angina, early post-MI angina, angina poorly responsive to therapy, and angina with heart failure all carry increased risk. Elevated LV end-diastolic pressure/volume and reduced ejection fraction signal poor prognosis. Left main (>50% diameter) or proximal LAD obstruction carries greater risk than right or circumflex lesions because more myocardium is jeopardized. With any obstructive disease, mortality rises sharply when LV function is impaired; the larger the established necrosis, the poorer the prognosis. Conversely, patients with chest discomfort but normal LV function and normal coronary arteries have an excellent prognosis, as do those completing stage III of the Bruce protocol with a normal perfusion scan or negative stress echo. Greater number/severity of risk factors (advanced age >75, hypertension, dyslipidemia, diabetes, morbid obesity, peripheral/cerebrovascular disease, prior MI), elevated CRP, extensive coronary calcification, and increased carotid intimal thickening all worsen prognosis (Harrison 22e p.2097).","pitfalls":["Relying on coronary artery calcium as the primary screening test or sole basis for therapeutic decisions; it is adjunctive only (Harrison 22e p.2092).","Interpreting a stress ECG without accounting for pretest probability; false positives rise in low-probability patients (e.g., asymptomatic men <40, premenopausal women without risk factors) and in those on digitalis/antiarrhythmics, with conduction disturbances, LVH, resting ST-T abnormalities, or abnormal potassium (Harrison 22e p.2095).","Assuming a negative stress ECG excludes CAD; sensitivity is only about 75%, though it makes three-vessel or left main disease unlikely (Harrison 22e p.2095).","Circumflex-territory disease may yield a false-negative stress test because the posterolateral wall is poorly represented on the surface 12-lead ECG (Harrison 22e p.2095).","Coronary arteriography images only the lumen and can miss severe non-luminal (remodeled, outwardly bulging) atherosclerosis (Harrison 22e p.2097).","Missing atypical/anginal-equivalent presentations in women, the elderly, and patients with diabetes (Harrison 22e p.2093).","Overlooking that about 25% of acute MI survivors never come to medical attention yet share the same adverse prognosis (Harrison 22e p.2092-2093)."],"management":["Antiplatelet therapy with aspirin, adding a second agent such as clopidogrel after acute events or revascularisation, together with a high-intensity statin to stabilise plaque and reduce cardiovascular events","Anti-anginal therapy with beta-blockers, calcium-channel blockers and long-acting nitrates to lower myocardial oxygen demand and relieve angina","Aggressive risk-factor modification: smoking cessation, blood-pressure and glycaemic control, and diet, exercise and weight optimisation","Coronary revascularisation by percutaneous coronary intervention or coronary artery bypass grafting for refractory symptoms or high-risk anatomy","Surveillance of lipids, glycaemic control, blood pressure and left ventricular function, with functional or anatomical ischaemia testing when symptoms change","Urgent treatment of acute coronary syndrome with antiplatelets, anticoagulation and timely reperfusion","Provide sublingual nitrate for acute angina and educate on warning symptoms that warrant emergency escalation"],"severityClassification":"Angina severity is graded by the Canadian Cardiovascular Society (CCS) functional classification (Class I: angina only with strenuous/rapid/prolonged exertion; Class II: slight limitation of ordinary activity; Class III: marked limitation with ordinary activity such as walking 1-2 blocks; Class IV: inability to perform any physical activity without discomfort, angina may be present at rest). Functional impact is described by the New York Heart Association (NYHA) classification (Classes I-IV) (Harrison 22e p.2093).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2090","Harrison 22e p.2091","Harrison 22e p.2092","Harrison 22e p.2093","Harrison 22e p.2094","Harrison 22e p.2095","Harrison 22e p.2096","Harrison 22e p.2097"],"crossLinks":[]},"ischiofemoral_impingement":{"name":"ischiofemoral impingement","system":"musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ischiofemoral impingement syndrome","quadratus femoris impingement"],"chapter":null,"source":"DeLee, Drez & Miller's Orthopaedic Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with deep posterior hip, buttock or groin pain that may radiate distally, characteristically worsened by hip extension, adduction and external rotation.","Pain is typically reproduced during the terminal stance phase of a long stride; the long-stride walking test provokes symptoms whereas short strides relieve them.","Oedema, and in chronic cases fatty infiltration, of the quadratus femoris muscle on MRI is the hallmark imaging finding.","Narrowing of both the ischiofemoral space (between lateral ischium and lesser trochanter) and the quadratus femoris space supports the diagnosis.","It is more common in women, related to a wider pelvis, coxa valga and increased femoral anteversion.","Secondary causes include hip instability, proximal femoral or ischial malunion, prior arthroplasty and space-occupying lesions.","First-line management is activity modification, physiotherapy to correct pelvic mechanics, and an image-guided local anaesthetic and corticosteroid injection into the quadratus femoris, which is also diagnostic.","Refractory cases may be treated by lesser trochanter resection or endoscopic decompression, addressing any underlying morphology or instability."],"pathophysiology":"The ischiofemoral space is bounded by the lateral cortex of the ischial tuberosity and the medial and posterior aspect of the lesser trochanter, and transmits the quadratus femoris muscle. Congenital or acquired narrowing of this corridor causes dynamic entrapment of quadratus femoris, particularly during hip extension combined with adduction and external rotation, when the space narrows further. Contributing factors include coxa valga, an increased femoral neck-shaft angle or anteversion, prominence of the ischium or lesser trochanter, hip instability, and post-traumatic or post-surgical malalignment. Repeated impingement produces muscle oedema, tears and eventually fatty atrophy, and may irritate the adjacent hamstring origin and sciatic nerve.","additionalDifferentials":["Proximal hamstring tendinopathy","Deep gluteal syndrome with sciatic nerve entrapment","Ischiogluteal bursitis","Hip osteoarthritis","Lumbar radiculopathy","Piriformis syndrome","Femoral neck or ischial stress fracture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the pelvis with measurement of the ischiofemoral and quadratus femoris spaces","MRI assessment of quadratus femoris oedema, tears or fatty atrophy","Dynamic ultrasound of the ischiofemoral interval","Image-guided diagnostic local anaesthetic injection into the quadratus femoris","Radiographs to assess coxa valga and proximal femoral morphology","Assessment of femoral version by CT or MRI"],"redFlags":["Progressive sciatic neurological deficit, indicating significant nerve involvement","Rest pain or night pain suggesting a neoplasm around the ischium or proximal femur","A palpable soft-tissue mass narrowing the space","Bilateral symptoms with systemic features prompting a search for an underlying disease"],"prognosis":"Many patients improve with conservative measures and image-guided injection, particularly when a reversible mechanical driver is present. Surgical decompression or lesser trochanter resection gives good reported outcomes in carefully selected refractory cases.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a narrowed ischiofemoral space in isolation as diagnostic, since space narrowing occurs in asymptomatic individuals and must be correlated with quadratus femoris signal change and clinical findings.","Relying on neutral-position static MRI, which can miss impingement that only occurs dynamically in extension and adduction.","Mislabelling the condition as piriformis syndrome or a simple hamstring strain.","Ignoring associated femoral version or instability, leading to persistent symptoms after isolated soft-tissue treatment."],"management":["Activity modification avoiding provocative hip extension, adduction and external rotation, adopting a shorter stride to unload the ischiofemoral space","Physiotherapy targeting hip and core strengthening, gait retraining and stretching of the quadratus femoris and hip external rotators","Simple analgesia and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain arising from quadratus femoris oedema","Image-guided local anaesthetic and corticosteroid injection into the quadratus femoris for diagnostic confirmation and symptom relief","Address underlying mechanical drivers such as coxa valga, increased femoral anteversion or hip instability","Refer to orthopaedic surgery for arthroscopic decompression or lesser trochanter resection in refractory cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["DeLee, Drez & Miller's Orthopaedic Sports Medicine","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"iselin_disease":{"name":"Iselin disease","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Iselin's disease","fifth metatarsal base apophysitis","traction apophysitis of the fifth metatarsal","osteochondrosis of the fifth metatarsal base"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A traction apophysitis at the base of the fifth metatarsal rather than a true osteonecrosis","Affected site is the apophysis at the base of the fifth metatarsal, at the insertion of the peroneus brevis tendon","Typically affects active children, roughly eight to thirteen years of age around the time the apophysis appears","Presents with pain, tenderness and sometimes swelling over the lateral border of the midfoot, aggravated by running and cutting or inversion activities","The radiographic hallmark is an enlarged or fragmented apophysis at the fifth metatarsal base oriented obliquely, roughly parallel to the shaft","A key distinction is that the normal apophysis and its fragmentation lie parallel to the metatarsal, whereas an avulsion fracture line runs transversely across the bone","Pain is reproduced by resisted eversion or passive inversion that tensions the peroneus brevis","Management is conservative with activity modification, offloading and short-term immobilisation for symptom control as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Iselin disease is a traction apophysitis of the secondary ossification centre at the base of the fifth metatarsal, the site of insertion of the peroneus brevis tendon. During the period when this apophysis is developing, repetitive traction from the peroneus brevis, particularly during running, jumping and side-to-side cutting movements, stresses the cartilaginous bone-tendon junction. The repeated microtrauma causes inflammation, and the apophysis may appear enlarged or fragmented on imaging. Because the apophysis lies obliquely, roughly parallel to the long axis of the metatarsal, it can be mistaken for an avulsion fracture, which instead produces a transverse lucent line. The condition resolves once the apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity.","additionalDifferentials":["Avulsion fracture of the fifth metatarsal base","Jones fracture of the proximal fifth metatarsal","Peroneal tendinopathy or peroneus brevis insertional pathology","Lateral ankle sprain","Os vesalianum (accessory ossicle at the fifth metatarsal base)","Osteomyelitis of the fifth metatarsal"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Foot radiographs including an oblique view to assess the apophysis and exclude a fracture","Recognition of the obliquely oriented apophyseal line versus a transverse fracture line","Comparison views of the contralateral foot to gauge normal apophyseal appearance","Magnetic resonance imaging in atypical, unilateral or persistent cases","Clinical provocation with resisted eversion tensioning the peroneus brevis","Inflammatory markers if infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["A transverse lucent line indicating an avulsion or Jones fracture rather than apophysitis","Inability to bear weight or acute severe pain after an inversion injury","Rest pain, night pain or systemic features suggesting infection or tumour","Persistent unilateral symptoms not settling with conservative treatment"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; symptoms are self-limiting and resolve as the apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity, with activity modification and offloading controlling pain and no long-term sequelae expected.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the normal obliquely oriented apophysis for an avulsion fracture, or vice versa","Overlooking a Jones fracture, which requires different management and has a risk of nonunion","Failing to consider infection when there is swelling, warmth or systemic upset","Prolonged immobilisation when graded activity modification would suffice"],"management":["Relative rest and activity modification, reducing running, jumping and cutting movements until symptoms settle","Ice and simple analgesia or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain over the fifth metatarsal base","A short period of immobilisation in a stiff-soled shoe or walking boot for more severe or persistent pain","Peroneus brevis and calf stretching with a graded strengthening programme before return to sport","Reassurance that the condition is self-limiting and resolves once the apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity","Gradual, symptom-guided return to activity once local tenderness has resolved"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"isolated_acth_deficiency":{"name":"Isolated ACTH deficiency","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["isolated ACTH deficiency","IAD","isolated corticotropin deficiency","isolated secondary adrenal insufficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A selective deficiency of pituitary ACTH secretion with preservation of the other anterior pituitary hormones, causing secondary adrenal insufficiency","Presents with fatigue, weight loss, anorexia, hypoglycaemia and hyponatraemia, but without hyperpigmentation because ACTH is low, unlike in primary adrenal insufficiency","Aldosterone secretion is preserved because it is renin-angiotensin driven, so severe salt wasting and hyperkalaemia are not typical, though hyponatraemia can still occur from cortisol-deficiency-related water retention","It can be an overlooked cause of unexplained hyponatraemia or recurrent hypoglycaemia","Causes include lymphocytic hypophysitis (including autoimmune and, increasingly, immune checkpoint inhibitor-induced disease), congenital TBX19 (TPIT) or POMC defects, and idiopathic disease","Immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy is now an important acquired cause and should be sought in the drug history","Diagnosis rests on low or inappropriately normal ACTH with low cortisol and an impaired cortisol response to stimulation, with the other pituitary axes intact","Treatment is glucocorticoid replacement, and mineralocorticoid is not required"],"pathophysiology":"Isolated ACTH deficiency is characterised by impaired secretion of corticotropin from the pituitary corticotrophs while the other anterior pituitary hormones remain intact, resulting in secondary adrenal insufficiency with glucocorticoid deficiency but preserved mineralocorticoid function. Acquired cases most often reflect autoimmune corticotroph destruction as in lymphocytic hypophysitis, including that triggered by immune checkpoint inhibitor drugs; congenital cases result from mutations in TBX19 (TPIT), a transcription factor essential for pro-opiomelanocortin expression in corticotrophs, or from defects in pro-opiomelanocortin processing. Because aldosterone is regulated by the renin-angiotensin system rather than ACTH, salt and potassium handling is largely maintained, and the absence of ACTH excess explains the lack of hyperpigmentation.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease), with high ACTH, hyperpigmentation and mineralocorticoid deficiency","Panhypopituitarism or multiple pituitary hormone deficiency","Suppression of the axis by exogenous glucocorticoids, the commonest cause of secondary adrenal insufficiency","Other causes of hyponatraemia such as SIADH or hypothyroidism","Chronic fatigue from non-endocrine causes","Sepsis or another cause of hypoglycaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Paired morning serum cortisol and plasma ACTH, showing low or inappropriately normal ACTH with low cortisol","Dynamic testing of cortisol reserve, such as a short synacthen test or, in specialist settings, insulin tolerance or CRH testing","Assessment of the other anterior pituitary axes to confirm the deficiency is isolated, covering thyroid, gonadal, growth hormone and prolactin function","Serum sodium and glucose for hyponatraemia and hypoglycaemia","Review of glucocorticoid and immune checkpoint inhibitor drug exposure","Pituitary MRI to look for hypophysitis or another structural lesion"],"redFlags":["Adrenal crisis with hypotension, hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia and collapse, especially during intercurrent illness","New ACTH deficiency after starting an immune checkpoint inhibitor","Severe symptomatic hyponatraemia","Failure to increase glucocorticoid at times of physiological stress"],"prognosis":"Excellent with appropriate glucocorticoid replacement and sick-day dosing education; the main danger is unrecognised or under-treated adrenal crisis, and checkpoint-inhibitor-induced deficiency is typically permanent and requires lifelong replacement.","pitfalls":["Expecting hyperpigmentation and hyperkalaemia and so missing the diagnosis, since these belong to primary insufficiency","Attributing hyponatraemia to SIADH without checking cortisol","Overlooking immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy as a cause","Failing to give stress-dose glucocorticoid and crisis-prevention advice"],"management":["Lifelong glucocorticoid replacement with hydrocortisone to correct cortisol deficiency; mineralocorticoid replacement is unnecessary because aldosterone secretion is preserved","Sick-day rules with glucocorticoid dose escalation during intercurrent illness and parenteral steroid for vomiting or major physiological stress","Emergency treatment of adrenal crisis with parenteral hydrocortisone and intravenous fluid resuscitation","Patient education, a steroid emergency card and provision of injectable hydrocortisone for home use","Review immune checkpoint inhibitor and exogenous glucocorticoid exposure as precipitants, continuing replacement as checkpoint-induced deficiency is usually permanent","Monitor clinical response and periodically assess the other anterior pituitary axes to confirm the deficiency remains isolated","Endocrinology referral for diagnostic confirmation and long-term supervision"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline on Adrenal Insufficiency","DeGroot's Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"isolated_aortitis":{"name":"Clinically isolated aortitis","system":"Rheumatology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["idiopathic aortitis","isolated aortitis","non-infectious aortitis","clinically isolated aortitis (CIA)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology / large-vessel vasculitis guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Often discovered incidentally when aortic tissue removed at aneurysm surgery shows inflammation without a preceding systemic diagnosis","Histology commonly demonstrates giant-cell or lymphoplasmacytic inflammation of the aortic wall","By definition there are no features of a defined systemic vasculitis or infection at presentation","The thoracic aorta, particularly the ascending segment, is the most frequent site","Aneurysm formation and dissection are the principal structural risks","A proportion of patients later declare a systemic large-vessel vasculitis such as giant-cell arteritis, warranting follow-up","Postoperative imaging surveillance is needed because disease may progress at other aortic segments"],"pathophysiology":"Clinically isolated aortitis is inflammation confined to the aortic wall without an identified systemic vasculitis or infection. Immune-mediated infiltration of the media and adventitia, frequently with giant cells and lymphoplasmacytic cells, damages elastic and smooth-muscle components of the wall. Loss of structural integrity weakens the aorta, predisposing to aneurysmal dilatation and dissection. Because it may represent a limited or early form of large-vessel vasculitis, some cases evolve into a recognised systemic disease over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Giant-cell arteritis with aortic involvement","Takayasu arteritis","IgG4-related aortitis","Infective (mycotic) aortitis","Behcet disease with vascular involvement","Atherosclerotic aortic aneurysm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cross-sectional aortic imaging with CT or MR angiography","Histology of resected aortic tissue with attention to giant cells and IgG4-positive plasma cells","Inflammatory markers (erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein)","Serum IgG4 to exclude an IgG4-related process","Screening for systemic large-vessel vasculitis including temporal artery assessment where indicated","PET imaging to assess extent of vascular inflammation"],"redFlags":["Acute severe chest or back pain suggesting dissection or impending rupture","Rapid aneurysm enlargement on surveillance imaging","Systemic features heralding evolution to giant-cell or Takayasu arteritis","Aortic regurgitation from ascending aortic involvement"],"prognosis":"With surgical repair of the affected segment and appropriate immunosuppression when active inflammation persists, outcomes are generally favourable, but ongoing surveillance is essential because new aneurysms and dissection can develop elsewhere in the aorta.","pitfalls":["Assuming resection is curative and abandoning imaging surveillance","Failing to check IgG4 and exclude an IgG4-related aortitis","Missing an underlying systemic large-vessel vasculitis","Overlooking infective aortitis before attributing inflammation to an idiopathic cause"],"management":["Surgical repair of the affected aortic segment when aneurysmal or dissecting, as is often identified at aneurysm surgery","Glucocorticoid therapy when active inflammation persists, with steroid-sparing immunosuppression for relapsing or steroid-dependent disease","Serial cross-sectional aortic imaging with CT or MR angiography to detect new aneurysm formation or dissection elsewhere in the aorta","Monitor inflammatory markers, erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein, to track disease activity","Screen for and monitor evolution into a systemic large-vessel vasculitis such as giant-cell arteritis, and exclude an IgG4-related process","Cardiovascular risk-factor control and blood-pressure management to reduce aortic wall stress","Multidisciplinary follow-up with rheumatology and vascular or cardiothoracic surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology","Chapel Hill Consensus Conference vasculitis nomenclature"],"crossLinks":[]},"isolated_dominant_hypomagnesemia":{"name":"Isolated dominant hypomagnesaemia (FXYD2)","system":"Renal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["isolated dominant hypomagnesaemia","IDH","FXYD2","gamma-subunit of Na-K-ATPase deficiency","hypomagnesaemia with hypocalciuria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / OMIM","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant renal magnesium-wasting disorder caused by variants in FXYD2, encoding the gamma-subunit of the sodium-potassium ATPase","The biochemical signature is hypomagnesaemia accompanied by hypocalciuria, mirroring the pattern seen in Gitelman syndrome","Presentation is often mild, with fatigue, muscle cramps, or an incidental low magnesium, though childhood convulsions have been described","Unlike FHHNC, there is no nephrocalcinosis, hypercalciuria, or progressive renal impairment","A recurrent dominant-negative variant affecting the gamma-subunit accounts for many reported families","Management is long-term oral magnesium supplementation, titrated to relieve symptoms rather than to fully normalise serum levels","Consider the diagnosis in autosomal dominant hypomagnesaemia with hypocalciuria and confirm with FXYD2 testing"],"pathophysiology":"The gamma-subunit encoded by FXYD2 modulates the activity of the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase in the distal convoluted tubule, the main site of active transcellular magnesium reabsorption. A dominant-negative variant impairs correct routing or function of the ATPase, reducing the electrochemical gradient that drives apical magnesium entry through TRPM6. The result is defective distal magnesium reabsorption and renal magnesium wasting. As in Gitelman syndrome, reduced distal tubular sodium chloride handling promotes compensatory proximal sodium and calcium reabsorption, producing the associated hypocalciuria. Because the defect is confined to distal magnesium handling, calcium deposition and progressive nephron loss do not occur.","additionalDifferentials":["Gitelman syndrome","FHHNC (CLDN16/CLDN19)","Hypomagnesaemia with secondary hypocalcaemia (TRPM6)","Drug-induced hypomagnesaemia (e.g. proton-pump inhibitors, diuretics)","Gastrointestinal magnesium loss","EAST/SeSAME syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum magnesium with fractional excretion of magnesium confirming renal loss","Spot urinary calcium-to-creatinine ratio demonstrating hypocalciuria","Serum potassium and venous blood gas to distinguish from Gitelman syndrome","Renal ultrasound to exclude nephrocalcinosis","Family biochemical screening consistent with dominant inheritance","FXYD2 gene sequencing"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic hypomagnesaemia causing seizures or tetany","Profound hypomagnesaemia with secondary hypocalcaemia and cardiac irritability","Refractory symptoms despite adequate supplementation"],"prognosis":"The condition is generally benign with a normal life expectancy and preserved renal function; symptoms are controlled with lifelong magnesium supplementation, though complete biochemical correction is often not achievable.","pitfalls":["Assuming a Gitelman-like biochemical picture excludes an FXYD2 cause","Attempting to fully normalise serum magnesium and provoking diarrhoea with high oral doses","Overlooking a dominant family history that points to the diagnosis","Attributing symptoms solely to concurrent medication such as proton-pump inhibitors"],"management":["Lifelong oral magnesium supplementation, titrated to relieve symptoms rather than to fully normalise serum magnesium","Use of better-tolerated organic magnesium salts in divided intake to limit diarrhoea and improve absorption","Correction of symptomatic hypomagnesaemia during acute decompensation, with parenteral magnesium if severe or accompanied by tetany or seizures","Periodic monitoring of serum magnesium and symptom control, with renal ultrasound reassurance that nephrocalcinosis does not develop","Genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and biochemical screening of at-risk family members","Nephrology or clinical genetics referral to confirm the FXYD2 diagnosis and guide long-term care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","OMIM entry for isolated dominant hypomagnesaemia"],"crossLinks":[]},"isolated_gnrh_deficiency":{"name":"Isolated GnRH deficiency (normosmic idiopathic hypogonadotropic hypogonadism)","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["normosmic idiopathic hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","isolated hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","nIHH","congenital GnRH deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by absent or incomplete puberty due to deficient hypothalamic GnRH secretion or action, with low sex steroids and inappropriately low or normal gonadotropins (hypogonadotropic hypogonadism)","The normosmic form has a preserved sense of smell, distinguishing it from Kallmann syndrome, in which GnRH deficiency is accompanied by anosmia or hyposmia from defective olfactory-GnRH neuronal migration","The 'fertile eunuch' variant is a partial form in which limited gonadotropin secretion supports some spermatogenesis and testicular growth despite inadequate virilisation","In boys it may present in infancy with micropenis and cryptorchidism, reflecting the absent activity of the reproductive axis during the neonatal minipuberty","It is caused by mutations in genes governing GnRH neuron function and signalling (for example GNRHR, GNRH1, KISS1R, and TAC3/TACR3), with variable inheritance and incomplete penetrance","The chief diagnostic difficulty is distinguishing it from constitutional delay of growth and puberty, since both present with low gonadotropins before puberty","Management uses sex-steroid replacement to induce and maintain secondary sexual characteristics, with gonadotropin therapy or pulsatile GnRH used to achieve fertility","Reversal of the hypogonadotropic state occurs in a minority, so periodic reassessment off treatment is warranted"],"pathophysiology":"Isolated GnRH deficiency arises from failure of the hypothalamic GnRH pulse generator, either from deficient GnRH synthesis or secretion or from defective signalling at the pituitary GnRH receptor. Without adequate pulsatile GnRH stimulation, the pituitary secretes insufficient LH and FSH, so the gonads are not driven to produce sex steroids or gametes, resulting in hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with absent or arrested puberty. In the normosmic form the olfactory system develops normally, unlike Kallmann syndrome where a shared developmental defect impairs migration of both olfactory and GnRH neurons. Numerous causative genes affect GnRH neuronal function, the kisspeptin and neurokinin B signalling pathways, and receptor activity, and the incomplete penetrance and occasional later reversibility indicate that the defect is one of axis regulation rather than fixed structural loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Constitutional delay of growth and puberty","Kallmann syndrome (GnRH deficiency with anosmia)","Acquired hypothalamic-pituitary disease (tumour, infiltration, irradiation)","Hyperprolactinaemia suppressing gonadotropin secretion","Functional hypogonadotropism from chronic illness, undernutrition, or excessive exercise","Combined pituitary hormone deficiency","Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism from primary gonadal failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Basal LH, FSH, and sex-steroid levels (all low or inappropriately normal)","Formal assessment of olfaction to separate normosmic disease from Kallmann syndrome","Magnetic resonance imaging of the hypothalamus, pituitary, and olfactory structures","Anterior pituitary hormone profile to confirm the deficiency is isolated","Serum prolactin to exclude hyperprolactinaemia","Genetic testing of GnRH-axis genes and assessment for associated non-reproductive features"],"redFlags":["Neonatal micropenis with bilateral cryptorchidism, which should prompt early endocrine evaluation","Visual field defects, headache, or other pituitary hormone deficiencies suggesting an intracranial mass","Associated midline or skeletal anomalies indicating a syndromic cause","Complete absence of pubertal onset well beyond the normal age range"],"prognosis":"Secondary sexual development and, in many cases, fertility can be achieved with appropriate hormonal and gonadotropin therapy; a subset of patients experience reversal of the deficiency, but untreated disease leaves patients hypogonadal with associated bone loss and infertility.","pitfalls":["Confusing the condition with constitutional delay, since the two are biochemically similar before puberty and only time or careful testing distinguishes them","Failing to test smell, thereby missing the distinction between normosmic disease and Kallmann syndrome","Assuming the deficiency is permanent and not reassessing for the possibility of reversal","Overlooking neonatal signs (micropenis, cryptorchidism) that offer an early diagnostic clue","Not screening for other pituitary deficits before concluding the defect is isolated"],"management":["Sex-steroid replacement, testosterone in males and staged oestrogen then combined oestrogen-progestogen in females, to induce and maintain secondary sexual characteristics","Gonadotropin therapy or pulsatile GnRH to stimulate spermatogenesis or ovulation when fertility is desired","Specialist-directed short-course androgen or gonadotropin therapy in infant boys with micropenis or cryptorchidism","Bone-health optimisation through adequate sex-steroid replacement, calcium and vitamin D, with monitoring of bone mineral density to counter hypogonadal bone loss","Periodic reassessment for spontaneous reversal of the deficiency, and careful distinction from constitutional delay of growth and puberty","Genetic counselling given the identified GnRH-axis gene defects and their variable inheritance and penetrance","Reproductive and paediatric endocrinology referral for puberty induction and fertility planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"isolated_growth_hormone_deficiency":{"name":"Isolated growth hormone deficiency","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IGHD","isolated GH deficiency","GH1 mutation","GHRHR mutation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Endocrine Society clinical guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Deficient secretion of growth hormone with otherwise intact pituitary hormones, presenting as proportionate postnatal growth failure with a slow growth velocity","Genetic forms involve GH1 (growth hormone gene) or GHRHR (growth-hormone-releasing-hormone receptor) mutations, and inheritance may be recessive, dominant, or X-linked depending on subtype","Both growth hormone and IGF-1 are low, distinguishing it from Laron syndrome where growth hormone is high and IGF-1 is low","Affected children may show truncal adiposity, a youthful or immature face, and delayed bone age, and infants can present with hypoglycaemia","Diagnosis relies on growth-hormone stimulation testing alongside low IGF-1 and IGF binding protein-3","Growth hormone replacement is effective and, when started early, can substantially improve growth and often final height","Some type IA cases develop antibodies to growth hormone during replacement, blunting the response","Pituitary imaging and screening for additional deficiencies help distinguish isolated from combined pituitary hormone deficiency"],"pathophysiology":"Isolated growth hormone deficiency arises when the somatotroph axis fails to produce adequate growth hormone while other anterior pituitary functions remain intact. Genetic causes include mutations in the growth hormone gene (GH1) or the growth-hormone-releasing-hormone receptor (GHRHR), and acquired cases follow structural, inflammatory, or idiopathic hypothalamic-pituitary disease. Because growth hormone drives hepatic IGF-1 production and skeletal growth, its deficiency lowers IGF-1 and slows linear growth, and in infancy the loss of growth hormone's counter-regulatory action can cause hypoglycaemia. Unlike growth hormone insensitivity, the receptor and downstream signalling are intact, so exogenous growth hormone restores IGF-1 and promotes growth.","additionalDifferentials":["Laron syndrome and growth hormone insensitivity","Combined pituitary hormone deficiency","Constitutional delay of growth and puberty","Hypothyroidism","Small-for-gestational-age short stature without catch-up","Turner syndrome and other chromosomal causes","Chronic systemic illness or undernutrition"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Growth-hormone stimulation (provocation) testing","IGF-1 and IGF binding protein-3 (both low)","Assessment of other pituitary axes to exclude combined deficiency","MRI of the hypothalamic-pituitary region for structural lesions","Bone age and growth-velocity assessment","Genetic testing of GH1 and GHRHR in familial or early cases"],"redFlags":["Neonatal hypoglycaemia, prolonged jaundice, or micropenis suggesting hypopituitarism","Rapidly declining growth velocity crossing centiles","Features of an intracranial mass such as headache or visual disturbance","Evidence of additional pituitary hormone deficiencies"],"prognosis":"With timely growth hormone replacement the growth outlook is good and many children reach a height within the normal range; prognosis worsens when treatment is delayed or when the deficiency is part of broader pituitary disease.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with growth hormone insensitivity by not measuring IGF-1 and growth hormone together","Overlooking additional pituitary deficiencies that need replacement","Missing an underlying structural lesion by omitting pituitary imaging","Attributing short stature to constitutional delay without adequate testing","Not recognising anti-growth-hormone antibodies as a cause of poor response in certain genetic subtypes"],"management":["Recombinant human growth hormone replacement, started early to maximise linear growth and final adult height","Management of neonatal and infantile hypoglycaemia arising from loss of growth hormone counter-regulation","Monitoring of growth velocity, IGF-1 and IGF binding protein-3 to titrate dosing and confirm treatment response","Surveillance for treatment side effects alongside periodic bone-age assessment","Reassessment of the somatotroph axis at completion of linear growth to determine the need for adult growth hormone replacement","Genetic counselling in familial or early-onset cases associated with GH1 or GHRHR variants","Endocrinology referral for stimulation testing, diagnostic confirmation and long-term supervision"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society clinical practice guidance on growth hormone deficiency"],"crossLinks":[]},"isoniazid_toxicity":{"name":"Isoniazid toxicity","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["isoniazid poisoning","isoniazid overdose","INH toxicity","isonicotinic acid hydrazide poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic acute overdose triad is refractory seizures, severe high anion-gap metabolic (lactic) acidosis and coma","Seizures typically begin within an hour or two of a significant ingestion and are often resistant to standard anticonvulsants","Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is the specific antidote and usually terminates the seizures when benzodiazepines alone fail","Suspect isoniazid in any patient being treated for tuberculosis who presents with unexplained seizures and profound acidosis","The lactic acidosis is driven largely by seizure activity but also by impaired lactate metabolism, and it may persist until seizures are controlled","Benzodiazepines act synergistically with pyridoxine and are the first-line anticonvulsant while pyridoxine is prepared","Chronic therapeutic use can cause a separate spectrum of toxicity including peripheral neuropathy (prevented by pyridoxine) and hepatotoxicity","If the ingested amount is unknown and seizures are present, empirical pyridoxine is given, with dosing guided by the poisons centre"],"pathophysiology":"Isoniazid inhibits the enzyme that regenerates the active form of pyridoxine (pyridoxal-5-phosphate) and also complexes directly with pyridoxine, depleting this essential cofactor. Because pyridoxal-5-phosphate is required for synthesis of the inhibitory neurotransmitter gamma-aminobutyric acid, its depletion reduces central inhibition and precipitates seizures. Sustained seizure activity, together with impaired conversion of lactate to pyruvate, produces a severe high anion-gap lactic acidosis. Restoring pyridoxine replenishes the cofactor and allows gamma-aminobutyric acid synthesis to resume, which is why it controls the otherwise refractory seizures.","additionalDifferentials":["Other toxin-induced seizures with acidosis (e.g. theophylline, sympathomimetics)","Alcohol or sedative withdrawal seizures","Primary central nervous system infection or structural lesion","Metabolic causes of seizure such as hypoglycaemia or hyponatraemia","Toxic alcohol poisoning causing high anion-gap acidosis","Status epilepticus from primary epilepsy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Capillary and laboratory glucose to exclude hypoglycaemia as a seizure cause","Arterial blood gas with lactate and anion-gap assessment","Serum electrolytes, renal and liver function","Creatine kinase for rhabdomyolysis after prolonged seizures","Paracetamol and salicylate levels in intentional overdose","Continuous monitoring and neuroimaging if the cause is unclear"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or refractory seizures unresponsive to benzodiazepines","Severe or persistent high anion-gap lactic acidosis","Coma or need for airway protection","Rhabdomyolysis or acute kidney injury after prolonged seizure activity","Ingestion by a patient on antituberculous therapy with unknown quantity"],"prognosis":"With early pyridoxine and benzodiazepines to control seizures and correct the acidosis, most patients recover fully; delayed treatment with prolonged seizures and profound acidosis is associated with significant morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Persisting with standard anticonvulsants without giving pyridoxine","Failing to consider isoniazid in tuberculosis patients presenting with seizures","Attributing the acidosis solely to seizures and undertreating the underlying cause","Not securing sufficient pyridoxine, which may be needed in substantial quantity","Overlooking chronic hepatotoxicity and neuropathy in long-term therapeutic users"],"management":["Intravenous pyridoxine (vitamin B6) as the specific antidote to terminate isoniazid-induced seizures, given empirically when the ingested amount is unknown","Benzodiazepines as the first-line anticonvulsant, acting synergistically with pyridoxine to control seizures","Airway protection, oxygenation and supportive care for coma and respiratory depression","Correction of the high anion-gap lactic acidosis, which typically resolves once seizures are controlled","Activated charcoal for early presentation after a significant ingestion once the airway is secured","Haemodialysis for refractory acidosis or massive ingestion not responding to antidotal therapy","Monitor for rhabdomyolysis after prolonged seizures, screen for co-ingestants and involve clinical toxicology and critical care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","UpToDate: Isoniazid poisoning"],"crossLinks":[]},"isopropanol_poisoning":{"name":"Isopropanol poisoning","system":"Multisystem / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["isopropyl alcohol poisoning","rubbing alcohol ingestion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Isopropanol causes central nervous system depression that is typically deeper and longer than an equivalent amount of ethanol","It produces ketosis without a significant metabolic acidosis, because it is metabolised to acetone rather than to an organic acid","There is a raised osmolar gap but characteristically a normal anion gap, distinguishing it from methanol and ethylene glycol","Acetone on the breath and ketonaemia with a near-normal glucose are useful clues","Gastritis with haematemesis and hypotension can occur after large ingestions","Management is largely supportive, with haemodialysis reserved for severe or refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Isopropanol is absorbed rapidly and metabolised by alcohol dehydrogenase to acetone. Both the parent alcohol and acetone are central nervous system depressants, accounting for the prominent and prolonged sedation. Because acetone is a ketone rather than an organic acid, the result is ketosis with a raised osmolar gap but without the severe high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis seen with methanol and ethylene glycol. Direct mucosal irritation causes the associated haemorrhagic gastritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Methanol poisoning","Ethylene glycol poisoning","Ethanol intoxication","Diabetic or alcoholic ketoacidosis","Other causes of coma with a raised osmolar gap"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum osmolality and calculation of the osmolar gap","Anion gap and blood gas (acidosis characteristically absent)","Serum and urine ketones and glucose","Isopropanol and acetone levels where available","Assessment for gastritis and haemodynamic compromise"],"redFlags":["Deep coma or airway compromise","Hypotension or gastrointestinal bleeding after large ingestion","A significant metabolic acidosis, which should prompt reconsideration of methanol or ethylene glycol","Co-ingestion of other toxic alcohols"],"prognosis":"With supportive care most patients recover fully; serious outcomes relate to profound central nervous system depression, aspiration, haemorrhagic gastritis or very large ingestions.","pitfalls":["Assuming a high-anion-gap acidosis and misclassifying it as methanol or ethylene glycol poisoning","Overlooking the deep, prolonged sedation and airway risk","Attributing ketosis solely to diabetes","Failing to reconsider the diagnosis if acidosis develops"],"management":["Supportive care as the mainstay, with airway protection and monitoring for deep and prolonged central nervous system depression","Intravenous fluid resuscitation and vasopressor support for hypotension after large ingestions","Management of haemorrhagic gastritis and haematemesis with a proton-pump inhibitor and endoscopy where indicated","Fomepizole or ethanol are not required because isopropanol is metabolised to acetone rather than to a toxic organic acid","Haemodialysis reserved for severe intoxication, refractory hypotension or very high isopropanol levels","Monitor the osmolar gap, ketones and acid-base status, and reassess for co-ingestion of methanol or ethylene glycol"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"isovaleric_acidaemia":{"name":"Isovaleric acidaemia","system":"Metabolic / Inborn errors","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IVA","Isovaleric aciduria","Isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive organic acidaemia caused by deficiency of isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase (IVD), a step in leucine catabolism.","Accumulating isovaleric acid gives a characteristic odour of sweaty feet during acute decompensation, a valuable bedside clue.","Presents in two broad patterns: an acute neonatal encephalopathic crisis and a chronic intermittent form with recurrent episodes triggered by illness or protein load.","Acute crises feature vomiting, poor feeding, lethargy progressing to coma, high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, ketosis and often hyperammonaemia.","Bone-marrow suppression with neutropenia, thrombocytopenia or pancytopenia is common during acute episodes.","Diagnosis is confirmed by the acylcarnitine profile (raised isovalerylcarnitine, C5) and urine organic acids showing isovalerylglycine and 3-hydroxyisovaleric acid; it is detectable on newborn screening.","Long-term management uses a leucine-restricted diet with glycine and carnitine supplementation to enhance conjugation and excretion of isovaleryl-CoA.","Acute management reverses catabolism with generous energy provision, stops protein transiently and treats intercurrent illness aggressively."],"pathophysiology":"Isovaleric acidaemia is caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in IVD, encoding the mitochondrial flavoenzyme isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase, which catalyses the third step in the catabolism of the branched-chain amino acid leucine, converting isovaleryl-CoA to 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA. When the enzyme is deficient, isovaleryl-CoA and its derivatives accumulate upstream of the block. The excess isovaleryl-CoA is hydrolysed to free isovaleric acid, which is directly toxic and responsible for the characteristic odour, and is conjugated with glycine and carnitine to form isovalerylglycine and isovalerylcarnitine that are excreted in urine; these conjugation pathways are the basis of both diagnosis and treatment. Accumulating organic acids and coenzyme-A esters disturb mitochondrial energy metabolism and inhibit other pathways, contributing to high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, secondary hyperammonaemia through interference with ureagenesis, and ketosis. Bone-marrow precursors are sensitive to these metabolites, producing the neutropenia, thrombocytopenia and sometimes pancytopenia seen in acute episodes. Because leucine is derived both from dietary protein and from endogenous protein breakdown, any catabolic stress such as fasting, infection or a protein load accelerates flux through the blocked pathway and can precipitate life-threatening decompensation, particularly in the neonatal period and during intercurrent illness.","additionalDifferentials":["Other organic acidaemias (methylmalonic and propionic acidaemia)","Maple syrup urine disease","Urea cycle disorders presenting with hyperammonaemia","Multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (glutaric aciduria type 2)","Neonatal sepsis and other causes of neonatal encephalopathy","Fatty-acid oxidation disorders","Ketotic hypoglycaemia and other causes of high-anion-gap acidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma acylcarnitine profile showing raised isovalerylcarnitine (C5)","Urine organic acids showing isovalerylglycine and 3-hydroxyisovaleric acid","Blood gas, anion gap, glucose, ketones, lactate and ammonia during illness","Full blood count to detect neutropenia, thrombocytopenia or pancytopenia","Plasma amino acids and carnitine status","IVD gene sequencing for confirmation and family counselling","Newborn screening result review and confirmatory testing"],"redFlags":["Progressive lethargy, seizures or coma indicating metabolic encephalopathy","Severe high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis unresponsive to initial resuscitation","Significant hyperammonaemia","Neutropenic sepsis or severe cytopenias during an acute crisis","Signs of cerebral oedema or raised intracranial pressure"],"prognosis":"With newborn screening, prompt treatment of crises and a leucine-restricted diet with glycine and carnitine, many patients achieve normal or near-normal development, though severe neonatal crises can be fatal or leave neurological sequelae.","pitfalls":["Attributing a neonatal presentation to sepsis alone and missing the underlying metabolic disorder","Continuing normal protein intake or allowing fasting during acute illness, worsening decompensation","Overlooking the characteristic odour as a diagnostic clue","Misreading the acute cytopenias as primary haematological or infective disease","Under-treating intercurrent illnesses that can rapidly precipitate a crisis"],"management":["Lifelong leucine (protein) restriction with a specialised metabolic formula to limit isovaleryl-CoA accumulation","Glycine and L-carnitine supplementation to conjugate isovaleric acid into non-toxic, readily excreted products","Emergency sick-day regimen during intercurrent illness: temporary withdrawal of natural protein with high-energy intake and intravenous glucose to reverse catabolism","Acute crisis management with fluids and glucose, correction of high anion-gap metabolic acidosis and ammonia-lowering measures, escalating to haemodialysis for severe hyperammonaemia","Monitoring of growth and development, acylcarnitine profile, ammonia and full blood count for neutropenia and thrombocytopenia","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance with sibling and family screening, aided by newborn screening detection","Involvement of a metabolic dietitian and specialist metabolic team for coordinated long-term care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited Metabolic Diseases (standard reference text)","Newborn screening and metabolic medicine guidelines","Clinical biochemistry references"],"crossLinks":[]},"isovaleric_acidemia":{"name":"Isovaleric acidemia","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IVA","isovaleric aciduria","isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","sweaty feet syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An organic acidaemia of leucine catabolism caused by deficiency of isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase","A distinctive odour of sweaty feet during acute episodes, from accumulated isovaleric acid, is a useful clinical clue","Acute neonatal form presents with vomiting, lethargy, poor feeding progressing to encephalopathy with high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis and ketosis","A milder chronic intermittent form presents later with recurrent episodes of vomiting and acidosis triggered by illness or protein load","Common laboratory features include acidosis, ketosis, hyperammonaemia, hypoglycaemia and cytopenias such as neutropenia and thrombocytopenia","Elevated isovalerylcarnitine (C5) on acylcarnitine profile and isovalerylglycine in urine confirm the diagnosis","Management principles are leucine (protein) restriction, and conjugation of the toxic metabolite using glycine and carnitine supplementation to enhance excretion, plus aggressive treatment of catabolism","Readily identified on newborn screening through elevated C5 acylcarnitine, allowing pre-symptomatic treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Isovaleric acidemia is an autosomal recessive defect of isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase, an enzyme of the leucine degradation pathway. Deficiency causes isovaleryl-CoA and its derivatives (isovaleric acid, isovalerylglycine, isovalerylcarnitine and 3-hydroxyisovaleric acid) to accumulate. Isovaleric acid is responsible for the characteristic odour and, together with the other metabolites, produces metabolic acidosis and is toxic to the central nervous system and bone marrow. Accumulated organic acids also secondarily impair the urea cycle, contributing to hyperammonaemia. Conjugation of isovaleryl-CoA with glycine and carnitine forms non-toxic, readily excreted products, which is exploited therapeutically.","additionalDifferentials":["Methylmalonic and propionic acidemia","Glutaric aciduria","Maple syrup urine disease","Multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (glutaric aciduria type 2)","Neonatal sepsis and diabetic ketoacidosis","Urea cycle disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood gas with anion gap, glucose, lactate and ammonia","Urine organic acids showing isovalerylglycine and 3-hydroxyisovaleric acid","Plasma acylcarnitine profile with elevated C5 (isovalerylcarnitine)","Full blood count for neutropenia and thrombocytopenia","Newborn screening acylcarnitine analysis","Molecular testing of the IVD gene"],"redFlags":["Acute metabolic decompensation with severe acidosis and encephalopathy is an emergency","Hyperammonaemia contributing to altered consciousness during crises","Sepsis-like collapse in a neonate that is actually metabolic decompensation","Severe neutropenia with risk of overwhelming infection during acute illness"],"prognosis":"With newborn screening and early treatment many patients have a favourable outcome and normal development; the chronic intermittent form is often milder, but untreated acute neonatal disease can be fatal or cause neurological damage, and outcome depends on preventing and promptly treating decompensations.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnostic sweaty-feet odour or attributing the presentation to sepsis","Neglecting glycine and carnitine conjugation therapy that enhances detoxification","Under-treating catabolic stress during intercurrent illness","Overlooking neutropenia and infection risk during acute crises"],"management":["Lifelong leucine-restricted diet with specialised low-protein metabolic formula to reduce isovaleryl-CoA and its toxic derivatives","L-carnitine and glycine supplementation to detoxify isovaleryl-CoA through conjugation and urinary excretion","Structured emergency regimen for illness or protein load: stop natural protein temporarily and provide generous carbohydrate energy with intravenous glucose to suppress catabolism","Acute decompensation managed with rehydration, glucose, correction of metabolic acidosis and hyperammonaemia, with dialysis if ammonia is markedly raised","Regular monitoring of acylcarnitine profile, ammonia, glucose and full blood count for neutropenia and thrombocytopenia","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance and family testing, supported by newborn screening","Metabolic specialist and dietitian oversight for long-term nutritional and developmental care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"jackhammer_esophagus":{"name":"Jackhammer oesophagus (hypercontractile)","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hypercontractile oesophagus","jackhammer esophagus","hypercontractile esophagus","nutcracker oesophagus (historical overlap)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with dysphagia and non-cardiac chest pain, often with a sensation of food sticking retrosternally","Defined manometrically by at least one swallow with markedly elevated contractile vigour (very high distal contractile integral), often with repetitive or vigorous multi-peaked contractions","Unlike spasm, contractions are excessively strong but retain normal or near-normal timing and propagation","Symptoms can fluctuate and may overlap with reflux and with spastic motility disorders","Outlet obstruction at the oesophagogastric junction should be excluded, as hypercontractility can be a compensatory response","Management uses smooth-muscle relaxants and neuromodulators, with peroral endoscopic myotomy considered in selected refractory cases","The finding may be an incidental manometric pattern in some patients with few symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"Jackhammer oesophagus is a hypercontractile motility disorder in which the smooth-muscle segment of the oesophagus generates abnormally forceful, high-amplitude, and often repetitive contractions. The mechanism is incompletely understood but is thought to involve excessive cholinergic drive and altered inhibitory neural control, sometimes as a compensatory response to distal outflow resistance at the oesophagogastric junction. The exaggerated contractile force is captured on high-resolution manometry as a very high distal contractile integral and correlates with episodes of chest pain and dysphagia.","additionalDifferentials":["Distal oesophageal spasm","Achalasia with preserved or exaggerated contractility","Oesophagogastric junction outflow obstruction","Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease","Eosinophilic oesophagitis","Cardiac chest pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution oesophageal manometry to quantify contractile vigour and pattern","Upper endoscopy to exclude mucosal disease and mechanical obstruction","Timed barium oesophagram to assess emptying and morphology","Cardiac assessment to exclude ischaemic chest pain","Functional lumen imaging or reflux monitoring in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Chest pain indistinguishable from cardiac ischaemia","Progressive dysphagia or weight loss suggesting obstruction or malignancy","Food impaction requiring urgent endoscopy","Coexisting outflow obstruction that changes management"],"prognosis":"The natural history is variable; many patients respond to pharmacological and neuromodulatory therapy, while a subset have persistent symptoms, and a minority evolve towards a spastic achalasia phenotype.","pitfalls":["Treating the manometric label rather than correlating with symptoms","Overlooking oesophagogastric junction outflow obstruction driving the hypercontractility","Confusing it with distal spasm, which has abnormal contraction timing rather than excessive vigour","Attributing chest pain to the oesophagus before excluding cardiac disease"],"management":["Smooth-muscle relaxants such as calcium-channel blockers or nitrates to reduce contractile vigour and relieve chest pain and dysphagia","Neuromodulators, including tricyclic antidepressants and other visceral analgesics, for pain modulation and oesophageal hypersensitivity","Identify and treat coexisting gastro-oesophageal reflux with proton-pump inhibitors","Exclude and relieve outflow obstruction at the oesophagogastric junction, as hypercontractility may be a compensatory response","Peroral endoscopic myotomy considered in selected refractory cases","Botulinum toxin injection as an alternative endoscopic therapy in refractory disease","Reassurance with dietary and lifestyle measures, given the often benign and fluctuating course"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Chicago Classification of oesophageal motility disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"jackhammer_oesophagus":{"name":"jackhammer oesophagus","system":"gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hypercontractile oesophagus","hypercontractile peristalsis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined on high-resolution manometry (Chicago Classification) by at least two swallows with markedly elevated distal contractile integral above the hypercontractile threshold, with preserved peristaltic sequence.","The exaggerated contractions may involve repetitive or multi-peaked waves and can include vigorous contraction of the lower oesophageal sphincter, distinguishing it from diffuse spasm where contractions are premature rather than merely vigorous.","Chest pain and dysphagia are the dominant complaints; a proportion of patients are minimally symptomatic and the manometric finding is incidental.","Always exclude obstruction at the oesophagogastric junction, as outflow resistance (including early achalasia or a tight fundoplication) can provoke a compensatory hypercontractile pattern.","Coexisting gastro-oesophageal reflux and oesophageal hypersensitivity are common and should be actively assessed and treated.","Smooth-muscle relaxants and neuromodulators (tricyclics, other visceral analgesics) form first-line therapy; peppermint oil may reduce contractile vigour.","Endoscopic botulinum toxin injection can give temporary relief, and peroral endoscopic myotomy is reserved for refractory, clearly symptom-correlated disease.","The diagnosis carries prognostic uncertainty because some patients progress to spastic achalasia, so follow-up manometry may be warranted if symptoms evolve."],"pathophysiology":"Jackhammer oesophagus is a hypercontractile disorder of the oesophageal smooth-muscle segment defined by excessively vigorous peristaltic contractions, quantified by an abnormally high distal contractile integral. The peristaltic sequence remains intact, distinguishing it from the premature contractions of distal spasm. The underlying mechanism is thought to involve an imbalance favouring excitatory cholinergic drive over inhibitory nitrergic input to the oesophageal musculature, sometimes compounded by asynchronous or prolonged contraction of the longitudinal and circular muscle layers and hypertrophy of the muscularis. In a subset, hypercontractility is a secondary or compensatory response to distal outflow obstruction at the oesophagogastric junction. Oesophageal hypersensitivity contributes substantially to symptom generation, which explains the frequent dissociation between manometric severity and clinical severity.","additionalDifferentials":["distal (diffuse) oesophageal spasm","type III (spastic) achalasia","oesophagogastric junction outflow obstruction","gastro-oesophageal reflux disease","cardiac ischaemic chest pain","functional chest pain and oesophageal hypersensitivity","eosinophilic oesophagitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["high-resolution oesophageal manometry with distal contractile integral quantification","upper gastrointestinal endoscopy to exclude mucosal, structural and obstructing lesions","timed barium oesophagram to assess emptying and outflow","functional lumen imaging probe (FLIP) to evaluate oesophagogastric junction distensibility","ambulatory pH-impedance monitoring for reflux","cardiac evaluation to exclude ischaemia","provocative testing (multiple rapid swallows, solid test meal) during manometry"],"redFlags":["progressive dysphagia or weight loss suggesting evolving achalasia or mechanical obstruction","chest pain requiring urgent exclusion of acute coronary syndrome","food bolus impaction or inability to manage secretions","manometric evidence of impaired oesophagogastric junction relaxation indicating an obstructive process","rapid symptom progression in an older patient raising concern for malignant pseudo-obstruction"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are variable and often benign, with many patients stable or improving on medical therapy and neuromodulation; symptom severity correlates poorly with manometric findings. A minority progress to spastic achalasia or have refractory symptoms, and response to myotomy is less predictable than in classic achalasia.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing jackhammer oesophagus without excluding oesophagogastric junction outflow obstruction that can secondarily drive hypercontractility.","Assuming that severe manometric findings mandate invasive therapy in a patient who is minimally symptomatic.","Overlooking coexistent reflux and hypersensitivity as treatable contributors to chest pain.","Failing to repeat manometry when symptoms evolve, thereby missing progression to spastic achalasia.","Attributing chest pain to the manometric abnormality before cardiac disease has been excluded."],"management":["Smooth-muscle relaxants such as calcium-channel blockers and nitrates, with peppermint oil, to reduce contractile vigour","Neuromodulators such as tricyclic antidepressants for pain and oesophageal hypersensitivity","Assess for and treat coexisting gastro-oesophageal reflux with proton-pump inhibitors","Exclude oesophagogastric junction outflow obstruction, including early achalasia or a tight fundoplication, before attributing symptoms to primary hypercontractility","Peroral endoscopic myotomy or botulinum toxin injection for selected refractory symptomatic patients","Reassurance and conservative management given the frequently benign course and poor correlation between symptoms and manometric findings"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease, current edition","Chicago Classification of Oesophageal Motility Disorders (current version)","ACG Clinical Guideline on oesophageal motility disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"jacobsen_syndrome":{"name":"Jacobsen syndrome (11q terminal deletion)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["11q terminal deletion disorder","distal 11q monosomy","del(11)(q23qter)","partial monosomy 11q"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A terminal deletion of the long arm of chromosome 11 (11q23 to the telomere), usually de novo and of variable size","Paris-Trousseau thrombocytopenia is characteristic: a constitutional platelet disorder with reduced numbers and impaired function, causing a lifelong bleeding tendency","Congenital heart disease is common, often involving left-sided lesions such as hypoplastic left heart, as well as septal defects","Trigonocephaly with a prominent metopic ridge is a recognisable craniofacial feature","Developmental delay and intellectual disability of variable degree, frequently with attention and behavioural difficulties","Additional features include feeding difficulty, recurrent infection, and eye, kidney and gastrointestinal anomalies","Haploinsufficiency of FLI1 is implicated in the platelet phenotype","Chromosomal microarray defines the deletion and its extent; a bleeding history warrants haematology involvement"],"pathophysiology":"Jacobsen syndrome is caused by loss of the terminal long arm of chromosome 11, most often as a de novo terminal deletion of variable size, with some cases arising from an unbalanced parental translocation. It is a contiguous-gene disorder in which haploinsufficiency of multiple dosage-sensitive genes produces the multisystem phenotype. Loss of FLI1, a transcription factor required for normal megakaryocyte and platelet development, underlies the Paris-Trousseau thrombocytopenia, while loss of other genes contributes to the cardiac, craniofacial and neurodevelopmental features. Larger deletions tend to be associated with more extensive involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Other terminal deletion or contiguous-gene syndromes with congenital heart disease and developmental delay","Inherited thrombocytopenias and platelet-function disorders from other causes","Other syndromic causes of trigonocephaly and metopic synostosis","Isolated congenital heart disease","Non-syndromic intellectual disability","Unbalanced products of a parental chromosomal rearrangement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal microarray as the first-line test to detect and size the terminal deletion","FISH or MLPA to confirm the deletion and assess for a parental rearrangement","Parental karyotype and studies to exclude a balanced translocation with recurrence implications","Full blood count and platelet studies to characterise the thrombocytopenia and platelet dysfunction","Echocardiography to define congenital heart disease","Renal, ophthalmological and developmental assessment; brain and other imaging as indicated"],"redFlags":["Significant bleeding or the need for surgery in a child with the platelet disorder","Haemodynamically significant congenital heart disease","Faltering growth or severe feeding difficulty in infancy","Recurrent or severe infection suggesting immune involvement"],"prognosis":"Outlook is largely determined by the severity of the congenital heart disease and the bleeding tendency, both of which are important early causes of morbidity; the thrombocytopenia persists lifelong and requires bleeding precautions, particularly around surgery. With cardiac correction and appropriate developmental support, many children survive into adulthood with variable intellectual disability.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the constitutional platelet disorder and failing to take bleeding precautions before procedures or surgery","Omitting echocardiography given the high frequency of serious congenital heart disease","Failing to arrange parental studies to identify an inherited translocation with recurrence implications","Attributing the thrombocytopenia to an acquired cause and missing its constitutional Paris-Trousseau nature"],"management":["Bleeding precautions for Paris-Trousseau thrombocytopenia, with platelet transfusion cover around surgery and avoidance of antiplatelet drugs","Cardiology assessment and surgical correction of congenital heart disease, frequently left-sided lesions and septal defects","Early developmental support with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech therapy and tailored educational intervention for intellectual disability","Management of recurrent infection with immune evaluation, and feeding support for feeding difficulties","Surveillance and management of associated renal, ophthalmological and gastrointestinal anomalies","Genetic counselling with parental karyotype and studies to assess recurrence risk from a balanced translocation","Multidisciplinary follow-up coordinating cardiac, haematological and developmental care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (University of Washington, Seattle)","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"jansen_metaphyseal_chondrodysplasia":{"name":"Jansen metaphyseal chondrodysplasia","system":"Skeletal / Endocrine genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTH1R","Metaphyseal chondrodysplasia Jansen type","Jansen-type metaphyseal dysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and paediatric endocrinology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare autosomal dominant dysplasia caused by activating (constitutively active) mutations of PTH1R, the receptor shared by parathyroid hormone and PTH-related peptide.","The mutant receptor signals independently of its ligands, producing a biochemical picture of parathyroid hormone excess despite low or undetectable circulating PTH and PTHrP.","Severe short-limbed short stature with characteristic facies (frontal bossing, hypertelorism, micrognathia) and a flexed, crouching stance.","Radiographs show gross metaphyseal cupping, fraying and expansion that can closely resemble severe rickets.","Biochemistry shows hypercalcaemia with hypophosphataemia and suppressed PTH — the mirror image of most rickets.","Chronic hypercalcaemia and hypercalciuria predispose to nephrocalcinosis and progressive renal impairment.","With age the metaphyseal lesions may appear cyst-like and later calcify, while limb deformity and disability remain marked.","Most cases are sporadic and arise from de novo mutations, so a negative family history does not exclude the diagnosis."],"pathophysiology":"The disorder results from heterozygous activating mutations in PTH1R (PTHR1), which encodes the class B G-protein-coupled receptor for both parathyroid hormone and PTH-related peptide. Mutations affecting the transmembrane helices (such as the classic His223Arg, Thr410Pro and Ile458Arg substitutions) lock the receptor in a ligand-independent, constitutively active state that drives tonic Gs–cAMP signalling. In the growth plate, PTHrP acting through PTH1R normally keeps chondrocytes proliferating and delays their hypertrophic differentiation; constitutive activation exaggerates and disorganises this signal, delaying hypertrophic differentiation and mineralisation and producing the severe metaphyseal chondrodysplasia. In bone and the renal proximal tubule the same unregulated signalling mimics chronic parathyroid hormone excess, giving hypercalcaemia, hypophosphataemia and increased bone turnover even though endogenous PTH and PTHrP are appropriately suppressed. Inheritance is autosomal dominant, and most cases are de novo.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe rickets, whether nutritional or hereditary hypophosphataemic","Schmid metaphyseal chondrodysplasia","Other causes of infantile hypercalcaemia (Williams syndrome, subcutaneous fat necrosis, hypervitaminosis D)","Primary or neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism related to the calcium-sensing receptor","Other metaphyseal dysplasias such as the McKusick and Spahr types","Hypophosphatasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey demonstrating severe metaphyseal cupping and fraying","Corrected or ionised calcium, phosphate and magnesium","Parathyroid hormone and, where available, PTHrP (both typically low or undetectable)","Urine calcium-to-creatinine ratio and renal ultrasound to detect nephrocalcinosis","Renal function and imaging","PTH1R gene sequencing","Alkaline phosphatase and vitamin D metabolites to help exclude rickets"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic hypercalcaemia with dehydration, vomiting, lethargy or polyuria","Nephrocalcinosis or declining renal function","Progressive skeletal deformity limiting mobility","Failure to thrive or feeding difficulty in infancy","Airway or feeding compromise from craniofacial involvement"],"prognosis":"The outlook is guarded, with lifelong severe short stature and skeletal deformity and a risk of nephrocalcinosis and renal impairment from chronic hypercalcaemia. Hypercalcaemia often becomes less marked in adulthood, but the deformity and disability persist.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing rickets from the radiographs and giving vitamin D, which can aggravate the hypercalcaemia.","Assuming that hypercalcaemia must mean a raised parathyroid hormone, when in Jansen disease PTH is suppressed.","Missing nephrocalcinosis because renal imaging and urinary calcium were not checked.","Discounting the diagnosis in a sporadic case simply because there is no family history."],"management":["Management of chronic hypercalcaemia and hypercalciuria with generous hydration and calcium-lowering measures, as no established disease-specific drug therapy exists","Bisphosphonates used to attenuate bone resorption and control hypercalcaemia in selected cases","Orthopaedic management of severe metaphyseal deformity and short-limbed stature, including bracing and corrective surgery","Renal surveillance with urine calcium, renal function and ultrasound for nephrocalcinosis and progressive impairment","Provide adequate but not excessive vitamin D and avoid additional calcium loading","Multidisciplinary care spanning clinical genetics, paediatric endocrinology, nephrology and orthopaedics","Genetic counselling for autosomal dominant PTH1R inheritance, with confirmatory PTH1R gene testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of clinical and medical genetics","Paediatric endocrinology reference","Skeletal dysplasia atlas and review"],"crossLinks":[]},"jansen_metaphyseal_dysplasia":{"name":"Jansen metaphyseal chondrodysplasia","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTH1R","metaphyseal chondrodysplasia Jansen type","Jansen-type metaphyseal dysostosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, severe autosomal dominant metaphyseal dysplasia caused by activating variants in PTH1R, the parathyroid hormone/PTH-related peptide receptor","The receptor is constitutively active, producing biochemical hypercalcaemia and hypophosphataemia despite low or undetectable circulating parathyroid hormone","This PTH-independent hypercalcaemia is a key diagnostic clue distinguishing it from other metaphyseal dysplasias and from hyperparathyroidism","Presents with severe disproportionate short stature, marked limb deformity, joint contractures and a waddling gait","Radiographs show grossly expanded, ragged, cystic-appearing metaphyses that can mimic rickets or a metabolic bone disease","Craniofacial features include frontonasal prominence, hypertelorism, micrognathia and choanal stenosis","Nephrocalcinosis and hypercalciuria may complicate the chronic disturbance of calcium handling"],"pathophysiology":"Jansen metaphyseal chondrodysplasia results from dominant activating variants in PTH1R, which encodes the receptor shared by parathyroid hormone and PTH-related peptide. Constitutive, ligand-independent receptor signalling mimics persistent PTH and PTHrP action. In the growth plate, PTHrP signalling normally delays chondrocyte hypertrophy; its unregulated activation disrupts the orderly transition of proliferating to hypertrophic chondrocytes, producing the disorganised, expanded metaphyses. Systemically, the same overactive signalling drives bone resorption and renal calcium retention, causing hypercalcaemia and hypophosphataemia even though endogenous parathyroid hormone is appropriately suppressed.","additionalDifferentials":["Nutritional or hypophosphataemic rickets","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Schmid metaphyseal chondrodysplasia","Metaphyseal dysplasia with other systemic features","Hypophosphatasia","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia (for the biochemistry)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium, phosphate and parathyroid hormone demonstrating hypercalcaemia with suppressed PTH","Skeletal survey showing expanded, ragged, cystic metaphyses","PTH1R molecular genetic testing","Renal ultrasound for nephrocalcinosis and urine calcium excretion","Assessment for choanal stenosis and airway patency in infancy"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic hypercalcaemia (dehydration, vomiting, altered consciousness) requiring urgent management","Nephrocalcinosis or renal impairment from chronic hypercalciuria","Airway compromise from choanal stenosis or craniofacial involvement in infancy","Severe progressive limb deformity limiting mobility"],"prognosis":"A severe dysplasia with marked short stature and deformity; the biochemical calcium disturbance may cause nephrocalcinosis and renal complications over time, but the metaphyseal changes tend to remodel toward maturity. Multidisciplinary metabolic and orthopaedic care is required.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the hypercalcaemia as hyperparathyroidism and pursuing parathyroid surgery","Diagnosing rickets on the radiographic appearance without checking calcium biochemistry","Missing nephrocalcinosis by omitting renal imaging","Overlooking choanal stenosis as a cause of neonatal respiratory difficulty"],"management":["Control of PTH-independent hypercalcaemia and hypophosphataemia with hydration and calcium-lowering measures, as no specific approved therapy exists","Bisphosphonates considered to reduce bone resorption and hypercalcaemia in selected cases","Airway assessment and management of choanal stenosis in infancy","Orthopaedic management of disproportionate short stature, limb deformity and joint contractures with bracing and corrective surgery","Renal surveillance with urine calcium excretion and ultrasound for nephrocalcinosis","Multidisciplinary metabolic and orthopaedic care with clinical genetics input","Genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance, confirmed by PTH1R molecular testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews / Orphanet: Jansen Metaphyseal Chondrodysplasia"],"crossLinks":[]},"japanese_encephalitis":{"name":"Japanese encephalitis","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JE","Japanese B encephalitis","JEV infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by a mosquito-borne flavivirus transmitted mainly by Culex species, with pigs acting as amplifying hosts and wading birds as the natural reservoir","The great majority of infections are silent; only a small fraction progress to clinical encephalitis, but that subset carries high mortality and disability","In India it is a leading cause of acute encephalitis syndrome, occurring in outbreaks in rice-growing and pig-rearing regions and disproportionately affecting children","Classic presentation is fever, headache and altered sensorium progressing over days, often with seizures and a Parkinsonian movement disorder (masked facies, tremor, rigidity)","There is no specific antiviral therapy; care is supportive, centred on seizure control, airway protection and management of raised intracranial pressure","A safe and effective vaccine exists and is incorporated into immunisation programmes in endemic Indian districts, making prevention the cornerstone of control","MRI often shows characteristic bilateral thalamic lesions, sometimes with haemorrhage, which supports the diagnosis","Confirmation relies on detecting JEV-specific IgM in cerebrospinal fluid or serum by capture ELISA"],"pathophysiology":"Japanese encephalitis virus is a single-stranded RNA flavivirus maintained in an enzootic cycle between Culex mosquitoes and amplifying vertebrate hosts, chiefly pigs and water birds. Humans are incidental dead-end hosts. Following a mosquito bite the virus replicates locally and in regional lymph nodes, producing a viraemia that may seed the central nervous system across the blood-brain barrier. Neuronal infection and the accompanying inflammatory response cause neuronal death particularly in the thalamus, basal ganglia, brainstem and cerebral cortex, accounting for the movement disorders and depressed consciousness seen clinically.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex encephalitis","Other arboviral encephalitides (e.g. West Nile, dengue encephalopathy)","Bacterial and tuberculous meningitis","Cerebral malaria","Enteroviral encephalitis","Scrub typhus with CNS involvement"],"infectionMimics":["Autoimmune (anti-NMDA receptor) encephalitis","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Metabolic or toxic encephalopathy","Non-convulsive status epilepticus"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["JEV-specific IgM capture ELISA in CSF or serum for confirmation","CSF analysis showing lymphocytic pleocytosis with mildly raised protein and normal glucose","MRI brain looking for bilateral thalamic and basal ganglia signal change","EEG to assess for seizure activity and encephalopathic patterns","Investigations to exclude treatable mimics such as HSV PCR and malaria films"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining consciousness or coma indicating severe encephalitis and need for critical care","Status epilepticus or repeated seizures","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or brainstem involvement (abnormal breathing, pupillary changes)","Progressive respiratory compromise requiring airway protection and ventilation"],"prognosis":"Symptomatic encephalitis carries substantial mortality, and a large proportion of survivors are left with lasting neurological or psychiatric sequelae including motor deficits, movement disorders, cognitive impairment and epilepsy. Outcome is worse at the extremes of age and with delayed presentation.","pitfalls":["Attributing acute encephalitis syndrome to JE without confirmatory serology and missing treatable causes such as herpes encephalitis","Overlooking the diagnosis outside the classic transmission season or in adults in newly endemic areas","Failing to protect the airway and control seizures early in a deteriorating patient","Neglecting vaccination and vector-control opportunities in endemic districts"],"management":["Supportive intensive care, as no specific antiviral exists, with airway protection and ventilation for depressed consciousness","Seizure control with benzodiazepines and maintenance anticonvulsants","Management of raised intracranial pressure with head positioning, osmotic therapy and neuroprotective measures","Fluid, electrolyte and nutritional support, with treatment of secondary infections","Early rehabilitation through physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy for motor, movement-disorder and cognitive sequelae","Prevention through Japanese encephalitis vaccination in endemic areas alongside mosquito-bite avoidance and vector control","Exclude and empirically treat mimics such as herpes simplex encephalitis with aciclovir and malaria while awaiting confirmation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"jefferson_fracture":{"name":"Jefferson fracture (C1 burst)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["C1 burst fracture","atlas burst fracture","atlas ring fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A burst fracture of the ring of the atlas (C1), classically a four-part fracture of the anterior and posterior arches produced by axial loading","Axial compression (a dive or a blow to the vertex) drives the occipital condyles onto the sloped lateral masses, splaying them apart and breaking the ring","Stability depends chiefly on the integrity of the transverse atlantal ligament","The rule of Spence uses combined lateral mass overhang on the open-mouth view to infer transverse ligament rupture and instability","Because the canal is wide at this level, neurological injury is frequently absent","Stable fractures are treated with a rigid collar or orthosis, while transverse ligament incompetence may require halo immobilisation or surgical fixation and fusion","The open-mouth (odontoid peg) view and CT are key, alongside assessment of the atlanto-dens interval"],"pathophysiology":"An axial load transmitted through the occipital condyles onto the obliquely oriented lateral masses of the atlas forces them apart, fracturing the ring at its weakest points where the arches join the lateral masses. Lateral displacement of the masses tensions the transverse atlantal ligament, which may rupture or avulse its bony attachment. Because the fracture tends to enlarge rather than narrow the canal, the spinal cord is usually spared, but loss of transverse ligament competence renders the atlantoaxial articulation unstable and threatens later displacement.","additionalDifferentials":["Hangman (C2) fracture","Odontoid (dens) fracture","Occipital condyle fracture","Atlanto-occipital dissociation","Isolated posterior arch fracture of the atlas","Atlantoaxial instability from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Open-mouth (odontoid) and lateral cervical radiographs","CT of the cervical spine to define the ring fracture and lateral mass displacement","Application of the rule of Spence to lateral mass overhang","MRI to assess the integrity of the transverse atlantal ligament","CT or MR angiography for suspected vertebral artery injury"],"redFlags":["Rupture of the transverse atlantal ligament indicating instability","Lateral mass overhang exceeding the rule-of-Spence threshold","Associated axis or occipito-cervical injury","Vertebral artery injury"],"prognosis":"Isolated stable atlas fractures usually unite with immobilisation and a good outcome; transverse ligament incompetence signifies instability that may require fusion, but neurological injury is uncommon because the canal is capacious.","pitfalls":["Failing to assess the competence of the transverse atlantal ligament","Missing the fracture on plain films by not obtaining CT","Overlooking associated cervical injuries","Misjudging stability and under-treating an unstable injury"],"management":["Determine stability from the integrity of the transverse atlantal ligament, which governs treatment choice","Rigid cervical collar or orthosis immobilisation for stable fractures with an intact transverse ligament","Halo vest immobilisation or surgical atlantoaxial fixation and fusion when the transverse ligament is incompetent","Assess for and manage associated vertebral artery injury with CT or MR angiography and antithrombotic therapy as indicated","Analgesia and strict spinal precautions during initial stabilisation and imaging","Spinal surgery referral with follow-up imaging to confirm bony union"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","The Spine (Rothman-Simeone)"],"crossLinks":[]},"jersey_finger":{"name":"Jersey finger","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["flexor digitorum profundus avulsion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hand surgery and sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Avulsion of the flexor digitorum profundus tendon from its insertion on the distal phalanx, causing loss of active flexion of the fingertip","It classically occurs when a gripping finger is forcibly extended, as in grabbing an opponent's jersey","The ring finger is the most commonly affected digit","The patient cannot actively flex the distal interphalangeal joint of the involved finger when it is isolated","The retracted tendon end may be palpable more proximally, and the level of retraction guides urgency of repair","It is a surgical injury, and early diagnosis improves the outcome, as greater retraction compromises repair"],"pathophysiology":"Jersey finger results from sudden forced extension of a finger during active flexion, generating tension that avulses the flexor digitorum profundus tendon from the distal phalanx, sometimes with a bony fragment. Once detached, the tendon retracts proximally to a degree determined by whether its vincular blood supply and the fibro-osseous pulleys remain attached; greater retraction jeopardises the tendon's nutrition and complicates repair. Loss of the profundus insertion abolishes active flexion at the distal interphalangeal joint.","additionalDifferentials":["Mallet finger (extensor injury)","Distal phalanx fracture","Flexor tendon laceration","Trigger finger with locking","Distal interphalangeal joint dislocation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical testing of isolated distal interphalangeal joint flexion","Radiographs to detect a bony avulsion fragment and its position","Ultrasound or MRI to locate the retracted tendon end","Assessment of the level of tendon retraction","Prompt hand surgery referral"],"redFlags":["Marked proximal retraction of the tendon threatening viability","Delayed presentation limiting repair options","Associated fracture or joint injury","Signs of neurovascular compromise"],"prognosis":"Early surgical repair or reattachment generally restores good function, but delayed diagnosis, significant retraction or chronic injury can necessitate more complex reconstruction with poorer outcomes.","pitfalls":["Missing the injury by not testing isolated distal interphalangeal flexion","Confusing it with mallet finger","Delaying referral so that the tendon retracts further","Overlooking an associated avulsion fracture"],"management":["Prompt surgical reattachment or repair of the avulsed flexor digitorum profundus tendon, the definitive treatment","Early referral to hand surgery, as greater tendon retraction compromises repair and dictates urgency","Protective splinting and immobilisation of the finger while awaiting surgery to limit further retraction","Tendon reconstruction or grafting, or distal interphalangeal joint arthrodesis, for chronic or irreparable injuries","Radiographic assessment for a bony avulsion fragment to guide the repair technique","Structured post-operative hand therapy with a graded tendon-protection and mobilisation protocol"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Hand surgery reference texts (standard reference)","Sports medicine references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"jervell_lange_nielsen":{"name":"Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome","system":"Hearing / Vision genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JLNS","Cardioauditory syndrome","Surdocardiac syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is the autosomal recessive form of congenital long QT syndrome combining profound bilateral sensorineural deafness with marked QT prolongation and life-threatening ventricular arrhythmia.","It is caused by biallelic variants in KCNQ1 (type 1, most cases) or KCNE1 (type 2), the genes encoding the slow delayed-rectifier potassium channel IKs.","The same potassium current maintains the high-potassium endolymph of the cochlea, so complete loss of channel function abolishes hearing while heterozygous carriers may show only autosomal dominant Romano-Ward long QT without deafness.","Presentation is often in early childhood with syncope, seizures or cardiac arrest triggered by exertion, emotion or fright.","The corrected QT interval is usually very markedly prolonged, considerably longer on average than in Romano-Ward syndrome, conferring a high arrhythmic risk.","Torsades de pointes and ventricular fibrillation are the mechanism of syncope and sudden death; iron-deficiency anaemia and hypokalaemia can worsen risk.","Deafness is congenital, profound and prelingual, and cochlear implantation is frequently required, with careful cardiac monitoring during anaesthesia.","Beta-blockade is the cornerstone of therapy but residual risk is high, so left cardiac sympathetic denervation and implantable defibrillators are often needed."],"pathophysiology":"Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome is a recessive channelopathy resulting from complete or near-complete loss of the slow delayed-rectifier potassium current IKs, produced when a person inherits two pathogenic alleles of KCNQ1 (the pore-forming alpha subunit, causing type 1) or, less commonly, KCNE1 (the ancillary beta subunit, causing type 2). In cardiac myocytes IKs contributes to phase-3 repolarisation of the action potential; its loss prolongs the action-potential duration and the QT interval, creating a substrate for early after-depolarisations and reentrant torsades de pointes ventricular tachycardia, particularly under adrenergic stress. The identical KCNQ1/KCNE1 channel is expressed in the marginal cells of the stria vascularis, where it secretes potassium into the scala media to generate the potassium-rich endolymph and endocochlear potential essential for hair-cell transduction; biallelic loss collapses this potential, causing endolymphatic degeneration and profound congenital sensorineural deafness. Because heterozygotes retain roughly half the channel function, they typically have normal hearing but may manifest a milder dominant long QT phenotype (Romano-Ward), which explains the recessive inheritance of the combined cardioauditory syndrome and the importance of screening apparently unaffected family members.","additionalDifferentials":["Romano-Ward syndrome (autosomal dominant long QT without deafness)","Acquired QT prolongation from drugs or electrolyte disturbance such as hypokalaemia or hypomagnesaemia","Other congenital long QT genotypes including sodium- and potassium-channel subtypes","Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia presenting with exertional syncope","Pendred syndrome and other causes of congenital sensorineural deafness","Epilepsy misdiagnosed from arrhythmic syncope with convulsive movements","Timothy syndrome and other multisystem arrhythmia syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Twelve-lead electrocardiogram with corrected QT measurement and morphology assessment","Exercise and adrenaline provocation testing to unmask QT dynamics","Ambulatory and event ECG monitoring for arrhythmia detection","Genetic testing of KCNQ1 and KCNE1 with cascade screening of relatives","Comprehensive audiological assessment including brainstem-evoked responses","Serum potassium, magnesium and iron studies to correct modifiable arrhythmic risk factors","Echocardiography to exclude structural heart disease"],"redFlags":["Syncope, seizure or aborted cardiac arrest triggered by exercise, emotion or startle","Documented torsades de pointes or ventricular fibrillation","Very markedly prolonged corrected QT interval on electrocardiography","Family history of sudden unexplained death or congenital deafness with cardiac events","Planned anaesthesia or QT-prolonging medication in an unmonitored patient"],"prognosis":"Untreated, the syndrome carries a high risk of sudden cardiac death in childhood; with beta-blockade, avoidance of QT-prolonging drugs and, where indicated, sympathetic denervation and defibrillator implantation, survival improves substantially, although the very long QT of type 1 disease still confers appreciable residual arrhythmic risk.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing arrhythmic syncope as epilepsy and delaying cardiac evaluation","Assessing hearing without an electrocardiogram, so the cardiac component is missed in a deaf child","Administering QT-prolonging drugs or performing anaesthesia without cardiac monitoring","Assuming beta-blockade alone confers full protection despite ongoing high-risk features","Overlooking a milder dominant long QT phenotype in heterozygous relatives who need screening"],"management":["Institute long-term non-selective beta-blockade (nadolol or propranolol) as the mainstay to blunt adrenergic-triggered torsades de pointes","Strictly avoid QT-prolonging medications and provide the patient with an up-to-date list of agents to shun","Correct modifiable arrhythmic risk factors by treating hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and iron-deficiency anaemia","Implant a cardioverter-defibrillator in survivors of cardiac arrest or those with breakthrough syncope despite beta-blockade","Consider left cardiac sympathetic denervation for recurrent events on optimal medical therapy","Offer cochlear implantation and audiological rehabilitation for the profound congenital sensorineural deafness","Arrange genetic counselling with cascade ECG and KCNQ1/KCNE1 testing of first-degree relatives","Give a written emergency action plan, restrict competitive and startle-provoking activity, and escalate promptly for syncope or arrhythmia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical genetics reference texts","Cardiology guidelines on inherited arrhythmia syndromes","Audiology and paediatric cardiology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"jervell_lange_nielsen_syndrome":{"name":"Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome","system":"Ear / Audiology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JLNS","KCNQ1","KCNE1","cardio-auditory syndrome","congenital deafness with long QT"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology / Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Combines profound congenital sensorineural hearing loss with a markedly prolonged QT interval on the ECG","Caused by biallelic mutations in KCNQ1 (or less often KCNE1), which encode subunits of a potassium channel; inheritance is autosomal recessive","The same channel is needed for endolymph production in the inner ear and for cardiac repolarisation, explaining the combined phenotype","Presents with syncope, seizures or sudden collapse triggered by exertion, emotion or startle, reflecting torsades de pointes","Any deaf child with unexplained syncope, seizures or a family history of sudden death must have an ECG to look for QT prolongation","Heterozygous carriers may have isolated long QT (Romano-Ward pattern) without deafness","Cardiac risk is high and untreated patients face a substantial risk of sudden cardiac death, so cardiology referral is urgent"],"pathophysiology":"Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in KCNQ1 or KCNE1, subunits forming the slow delayed-rectifier potassium current channel. In the stria vascularis of the inner ear this current is essential for secreting potassium-rich endolymph; its loss disrupts the endocochlear potential and causes profound congenital sensorineural deafness. In cardiac myocytes the same current contributes to repolarisation, so its loss prolongs the action potential and the QT interval, predisposing to torsades de pointes ventricular arrhythmia, syncope and sudden death. The recessive combined phenotype requires two defective alleles, whereas single-allele defects tend to cause isolated long QT syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Romano-Ward long QT syndrome without deafness","Acquired QT prolongation from drugs or electrolyte disturbance","Seizure disorder or epilepsy misattributed to a primary neurological cause","Vasovagal or reflex syncope","Other syndromic congenital sensorineural hearing loss","Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia and other inherited arrhythmias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Twelve-lead ECG with corrected QT measurement, the key diagnostic test","Audiometry including auditory brainstem response to confirm profound hearing loss","Ambulatory ECG and exercise testing to capture arrhythmia and QT behaviour","Serum electrolytes to exclude reversible causes of QT prolongation","Molecular genetic testing of KCNQ1 and KCNE1 and cascade screening of relatives"],"redFlags":["Syncope, seizures or cardiac arrest, particularly during exertion, emotion or sudden noise","Family history of sudden unexplained death or long QT syndrome","Documented torsades de pointes or very prolonged corrected QT interval","Exposure to QT-prolonging medications that could precipitate arrhythmia"],"prognosis":"Untreated the syndrome carries a high risk of sudden cardiac death from malignant arrhythmia, but prognosis improves substantially with beta-blockade, avoidance of QT-prolonging drugs, and consideration of an implantable defibrillator, alongside hearing rehabilitation for the profound deafness.","pitfalls":["Not performing an ECG in a deaf child with syncope or seizures and missing the cardiac diagnosis","Misdiagnosing arrhythmic collapse as epilepsy and delaying cardiac treatment","Prescribing QT-prolonging medications, which can precipitate fatal arrhythmia","Overlooking the recessive inheritance and failing to screen at-risk relatives"],"management":["Start lifelong non-selective beta-blockade (nadolol or propranolol) as first-line protection against catecholamine-driven torsades de pointes","Avoid all QT-prolonging drugs and counsel on agents to be shunned lifelong","Correct hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and iron-deficiency anaemia to remove reversible arrhythmic triggers","Implant a cardioverter-defibrillator after aborted cardiac arrest or for arrhythmia recurring despite beta-blockade","Consider left cardiac sympathetic denervation when events persist on maximal drug therapy","Provide cochlear implantation and hearing rehabilitation for the profound bilateral sensorineural deafness","Arrange autosomal recessive genetic counselling with cascade ECG and genetic screening of relatives","Restrict startle and strenuous exertion triggers and seek urgent cardiology review for syncope or documented arrhythmia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"jet_lag_disorder":{"name":"Jet lag disorder","system":"Environmental / Emergency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["jet lag","desynchronosis","circadian rhythm sleep-wake disorder, jet lag type"],"chapter":null,"source":"Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine / Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A circadian rhythm sleep-wake disorder that follows rapid travel across several time zones, when the internal body clock is misaligned with the destination's local time","Typical features are difficulty initiating or maintaining sleep, daytime sleepiness, impaired daytime function, fatigue, and gastrointestinal upset","Eastward travel, which requires advancing the body clock, is generally harder to adjust to than westward travel","Severity broadly increases with the number of time zones crossed and tends to improve as the internal clock realigns over subsequent days","Management uses appropriately timed light exposure and avoidance, timed melatonin, and good sleep hygiene to shift the circadian clock in the desired direction","Strategic napping, adjusting meal and activity timing, and judicious caffeine use can support daytime alertness","It is self-limiting, resolving as the circadian system entrains to the new time zone"],"pathophysiology":"Jet lag disorder results from a temporary mismatch between the endogenous circadian clock, governed by the hypothalamic suprachiasmatic nucleus, and the external light-dark cycle at the destination after rapid transmeridian travel. The internal clock realigns only gradually, at a limited number of hours per day, so sleep-wake timing, alertness, hormone secretion such as melatonin, and gastrointestinal rhythms remain desynchronised until entrainment is complete. Because the clock naturally runs slightly longer than a day, delaying it (westward travel) is usually easier than advancing it (eastward travel).","additionalDifferentials":["Other circadian rhythm sleep-wake disorders (e.g. shift work disorder)","Insomnia disorder unrelated to travel","Travel fatigue without circadian misalignment","Obstructive sleep apnoea","Depression or anxiety-related sleep disturbance","Effects of alcohol, caffeine, or medication on sleep"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from history of recent transmeridian travel and characteristic symptoms","Sleep diary documenting sleep-wake timing before and after travel","Assessment for coexisting sleep disorders where symptoms are prolonged","Screening for mood disorder if sleep disturbance persists","Actigraphy in selected or research settings to characterise the sleep-wake pattern"],"redFlags":["Symptoms persisting well beyond the expected adjustment period, suggesting another sleep disorder","Excessive daytime sleepiness causing safety risk (e.g. while driving)","Features of obstructive sleep apnoea such as loud snoring and witnessed apnoeas","Significant mood disturbance accompanying the sleep problem"],"prognosis":"Jet lag is self-limiting and resolves as the circadian clock entrains to the new time zone, typically over several days depending on the number of time zones crossed and direction of travel; well-timed light and melatonin can shorten the adjustment.","pitfalls":["Timing light exposure incorrectly and inadvertently shifting the clock the wrong way","Taking melatonin at the wrong time relative to the desired shift","Attributing persistent symptoms to jet lag rather than an underlying sleep disorder","Overusing alcohol or hypnotics, which can fragment sleep and delay adaptation"],"management":["Use appropriately timed bright-light exposure and light avoidance to shift the circadian clock in the required direction, advancing it for eastward and delaying it for westward travel","Take timed melatonin around the destination bedtime to accelerate re-entrainment","Reinforce sleep hygiene and begin pre-flight gradual adjustment of sleep and meal timing towards the destination clock","Use strategic short naps and judicious daytime caffeine to support alertness while avoiding late-day stimulants","Offer a short course of a hypnotic for disabling insomnia during the adjustment period","Reassure that the disorder is self-limiting and resolves as the clock entrains over successive days","Reassess for a coexisting primary sleep or mood disorder if symptoms persist well beyond expected adjustment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine","Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"jeune_syndrome":{"name":"Jeune syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy","ATD","Jeune asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy","short-rib thoracic dysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A recessive ciliopathy defined by a small, narrow, bell-shaped thorax with short horizontal ribs, causing variable respiratory compromise from birth","Genetically heterogeneous, most often caused by biallelic variants in genes encoding intraflagellar transport proteins of the primary cilium","Thoracic insufficiency with restrictive lung disease is the principal early threat and can be fatal in infancy, though survivors may see relative thoracic growth","Additional skeletal features include short limbs, short broad hands, occasional postaxial polydactyly, and a characteristic trident acetabular roof","Progressive renal disease, and sometimes hepatic and retinal involvement, may emerge later and requires ongoing surveillance","Radiographs show a narrow thorax with short ribs, high clavicles, short iliac bones with medial and lateral acetabular spurs, and cone-shaped epiphyses","Management centres on respiratory support, and in selected cases thoracic expansion surgery, with monitoring for renal and other organ involvement"],"pathophysiology":"Jeune syndrome is a ciliopathy caused by biallelic variants in genes required for intraflagellar transport and normal primary cilium function. Because the primary cilium is essential for hedgehog and other signalling that governs skeletal patterning and growth-plate function, its disruption impairs endochondral ossification, producing a constricted thoracic cage and short limbs. The small thorax restricts lung expansion and causes the characteristic respiratory insufficiency. The same ciliary dysfunction in the kidney, liver, and retina accounts for the progressive extraskeletal disease seen in longer-term survivors.","additionalDifferentials":["Ellis-van Creveld syndrome","Short-rib polydactyly syndromes","Thanatophoric dysplasia","Barnes syndrome and other thoracic dysplasias","Cranioectodermal dysplasia (Sensenbrenner syndrome)","Other skeletal ciliopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey demonstrating a narrow thorax, short ribs, and acetabular spurs","Respiratory assessment including lung function and imaging of thoracic capacity","Molecular genetic testing of a skeletal ciliopathy gene panel","Renal function tests and renal imaging with periodic surveillance","Liver function tests and hepatic imaging for fibrosis","Ophthalmological review for retinal dystrophy"],"redFlags":["Severe restrictive respiratory failure in infancy from thoracic insufficiency","Recurrent respiratory infection with deteriorating gas exchange","Progressive renal impairment","Signs of hepatic fibrosis or retinal dystrophy in survivors"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends heavily on the severity of thoracic constriction: severe cases are frequently fatal in infancy from respiratory failure, whereas those surviving early childhood may have relative thoracic growth but face progressive renal and other organ complications.","pitfalls":["Focusing on the thorax and overlooking progressive renal and hepatic disease in survivors","Confusing it with Ellis-van Creveld syndrome without cardiac and ectodermal assessment","Underestimating the risk of respiratory decompensation with intercurrent infection","Failing to establish long-term multisystem surveillance"],"management":["Provide respiratory support proportionate to thoracic insufficiency, from supplemental oxygen and non-invasive ventilation to mechanical ventilation in severe infantile disease","Consider thoracic expansion surgery such as vertical expandable prosthetic titanium rib or lateral thoracoplasty to enlarge the constricted chest and relieve restrictive lung disease","Treat intercurrent chest infections promptly and provide airway clearance and immunisation to protect fragile lung function","Institute renal surveillance and manage progressive nephronopathy with medical measures, dialysis or transplantation as it evolves","Undertake periodic hepatic and ophthalmological review for fibrosis and retinal dystrophy in longer-term survivors","Give nutritional support to optimise growth and coordinate care through a multidisciplinary skeletal-dysplasia team","Arrange autosomal recessive genetic counselling and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"job_syndrome":{"name":"Job syndrome","system":"Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["autosomal dominant Hyper-IgE syndrome","AD-HIES","STAT3 Hyper-IgE syndrome","HIES"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Job syndrome is the classic autosomal dominant form of Hyper-IgE syndrome caused by dominant-negative mutations in STAT3","The name derives from the biblical figure afflicted with boils, reflecting the recurrent staphylococcal skin abscesses that are a hallmark","The characteristic triad is recurrent skin abscesses, recurrent pneumonia, and markedly elevated serum IgE","Skin abscesses are often 'cold', lacking the usual warmth and redness because of the blunted inflammatory response","Recurrent pneumonias, typically staphylococcal, tend to heal with pneumatocele formation that can later be colonised by fungi such as Aspergillus","Chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis reflects the impaired Th17 immunity that follows STAT3 dysfunction","Non-immune features distinguish it from other immunodeficiencies: a coarse (leonine) facial appearance, retained primary teeth, joint hyperextensibility, scoliosis, minimal-trauma fractures, and later vascular aneurysms","Care combines long-term antistaphylococcal and antifungal prophylaxis, prompt treatment of infections, and diligent skin care, with surveillance of lungs and vasculature"],"pathophysiology":"Job syndrome results from heterozygous dominant-negative mutations in STAT3, a transcription factor central to signalling downstream of numerous cytokines. Defective STAT3 signalling impairs the differentiation of Th17 cells and reduces the production of antimicrobial peptides at epithelial surfaces, which underlies the susceptibility to Staphylococcus aureus and Candida and the failure to mount a brisk inflammatory response (the 'cold' abscess phenomenon). Because STAT3 also regulates connective tissue, bone, dental, and vascular biology, patients develop the characteristic facial, skeletal, and dental abnormalities and are prone to aneurysm formation. IgE production is markedly increased and eosinophilia is common, reflecting the skewed cytokine environment.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal recessive Hyper-IgE syndromes (for example DOCK8 deficiency)","Severe atopic dermatitis with secondary infection","Chronic granulomatous disease","Cystic fibrosis","Other combined immunodeficiencies with elevated IgE"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum IgE level and eosinophil count","STAT3 genetic testing","Clinical scoring to estimate the likelihood of the STAT3 form","Chest imaging for pneumatoceles, bronchiectasis, and fungal colonisation","Dental review for retained primary teeth and skeletal assessment for scoliosis and fractures","Vascular imaging where aneurysm is suspected"],"redFlags":["Recurrent deep or 'cold' abscesses and cavitating pneumonia requiring urgent immunological evaluation","Fungal colonisation or invasion of pneumatoceles risking haemorrhage","Coronary or cerebral aneurysm causing acute vascular events","Minimal-trauma fractures indicating significant bone fragility"],"prognosis":"Many patients survive into adulthood with consistent prophylaxis and prompt treatment of infections, but chronic lung damage, fungal superinfection of pneumatoceles, and vascular aneurysms are important sources of long-term morbidity and can be life-threatening.","pitfalls":["Mistaking recurrent boils for ordinary staphylococcal skin disease and missing the syndrome","Relying on a raised IgE alone rather than the combination of clinical and genetic features","Underestimating cardiovascular risk from aneurysms in adults","Failing to monitor pneumatoceles for secondary Aspergillus infection","Confusing it with the recessive DOCK8 form, which has a different infection profile and management"],"management":["Maintain long-term antistaphylococcal antibiotic prophylaxis (such as cotrimoxazole) to reduce recurrent skin and lung infection","Add antifungal prophylaxis to prevent mucocutaneous candidiasis and Aspergillus colonisation of pneumatoceles","Manage the eczematoid dermatitis with emollients, topical anti-inflammatories and antiseptic bleach baths to lower skin staphylococcal burden","Treat active infections aggressively with culture-directed antimicrobials and surgical drainage of abscesses","Undertake respiratory surveillance for pneumatoceles and bronchiectasis with imaging and prompt treatment of fungal superinfection","Arrange dental review for retained primary teeth and skeletal monitoring for scoliosis and minimal-trauma fractures","Screen for arterial aneurysms with vascular imaging given the STAT3-related vasculopathy","Provide autosomal dominant genetic counselling and consider haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in severely affected patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"job_syndrome_stat3":{"name":"Job Syndrome (Autosomal Dominant Hyper-IgE Syndrome, STAT3 Deficiency)","system":"Immunology / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AD-HIES","STAT3 hyper-IgE syndrome","Job's syndrome","Buckley syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad is recurrent staphylococcal skin and lung abscesses, chronic eczematoid dermatitis, and markedly elevated serum IgE.","Caused by dominant-negative mutations in STAT3, impairing Th17 differentiation and thereby mucocutaneous defence against Staphylococcus aureus and Candida.","Pulmonary abscesses and pneumonias heal with pneumatoceles and bronchiectasis, which later become niches for Aspergillus and Pseudomonas superinfection.","Skin abscesses are characteristically 'cold', lacking the usual warmth, erythema and systemic signs of inflammation.","A distinctive multisystem, non-immune phenotype includes a coarse facial appearance, retained primary teeth, hyperextensible joints, scoliosis, osteopenia with minimal-trauma fractures, and arterial aneurysms.","Blood eosinophilia is common; the very high IgE may fall towards normal in adulthood and does not correlate with disease severity.","A clinical scoring system combining immunological and connective-tissue features aids recognition, with STAT3 sequencing confirming the diagnosis.","Management relies on long-term anti-staphylococcal and antifungal prophylaxis, aggressive treatment of infections and skin care rather than routine immunoglobulin replacement."],"pathophysiology":"Job syndrome is caused by heterozygous dominant-negative mutations in STAT3, a transcription factor that transduces signals from numerous cytokine receptors including IL-6, IL-10, IL-21 and IL-23. Because the mutant protein interferes with the normal allele, STAT3-dependent signalling is broadly impaired. A pivotal consequence is failure of Th17 cell differentiation, since STAT3 is required downstream of IL-6 and IL-23 for RORgammaT-driven Th17 commitment; the resulting deficiency of IL-17 and IL-22 cripples the recruitment of neutrophils and the induction of epithelial antimicrobial peptides at skin and mucosal surfaces, explaining the striking susceptibility to Staphylococcus aureus and mucocutaneous Candida. Impaired STAT3 signalling also disturbs class-switching and the germinal-centre response, producing dysregulated, grossly elevated IgE and diminished memory B-cell generation, and blunts the acute-phase and inflammatory responses so that infections are paradoxically 'cold'. Beyond immunity, STAT3 participates in tissue remodelling, bone metabolism and vascular integrity, so its loss produces the characteristic skeletal, dental, facial and connective-tissue abnormalities and predisposes to aneurysm formation, making the disorder a true multisystem syndrome rather than a pure immunodeficiency.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal recessive hyper-IgE syndromes (for example DOCK8 deficiency)","Severe atopic dermatitis with recurrent infection","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","Chronic granulomatous disease","Netherton syndrome and other ichthyosiform disorders with high IgE","Omenn syndrome and other combined immunodeficiencies","Cystic fibrosis when bronchiectasis dominates"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum IgE with serial monitoring and full blood count for eosinophilia","STAT3 gene sequencing to confirm the diagnosis","Th17 (IL-17-producing CD4) cell enumeration by flow cytometry","High-resolution chest imaging for pneumatoceles and bronchiectasis","Skeletal survey or bone density assessment for fractures and osteopenia","Vascular imaging to screen for coronary and other arterial aneurysms","Microbiological and fungal cultures of sputum and abscess material"],"redFlags":["Haemoptysis or cavitary lung lesions suggesting Aspergillus or Pseudomonas superinfection of pneumatoceles","Rapidly enlarging or ruptured arterial aneurysm","Recurrent minimal-trauma fractures indicating significant osteopenia","Systemic candidiasis or invasive fungal disease","A 'cold' abscess with few systemic signs masking severe underlying infection"],"prognosis":"With sustained antimicrobial prophylaxis and vigilant management of pulmonary and skin infection, many patients reach adulthood, though chronic lung damage, invasive fungal infection and vascular complications remain important causes of morbidity and premature death.","pitfalls":["Relying on the absolute IgE level, which fluctuates and may normalise in adults, to include or exclude the diagnosis","Missing serious infection because abscesses are 'cold' and lack expected inflammatory signs","Overlooking the connective-tissue, skeletal and vascular features and treating it as isolated severe eczema","Failing to distinguish it from DOCK8 and other autosomal recessive hyper-IgE syndromes, which differ in viral susceptibility and prognosis","Neglecting to protect and monitor pneumatoceles that later seed fungal superinfection"],"management":["Give continuous antistaphylococcal antibiotic prophylaxis (such as cotrimoxazole) to curb recurrent staphylococcal skin and pulmonary abscesses","Provide antifungal prophylaxis against mucocutaneous Candida and Aspergillus superinfection of pneumatoceles","Control the eczematoid dermatitis with emollients, topical anti-inflammatories and antiseptic bleach baths","Treat established infections with prompt culture-guided antimicrobials and drainage of abscesses","Perform serial high-resolution chest imaging surveillance for pneumatoceles and bronchiectasis and act early on fungal colonisation","Monitor the skeleton and dentition for osteopenia, fractures, scoliosis and retained primary teeth, and screen arteries for aneurysm","Consider immunoglobulin replacement or haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in selected severe cases","Offer autosomal dominant genetic counselling and family assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical immunology textbook","International primary immunodeficiency classification guidance","Peer-reviewed hyper-IgE syndrome review"],"crossLinks":[]},"johanson_blizzard_syndrome":{"name":"Johanson-Blizzard syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JBS","UBR1","UBR1-related syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews / Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distinctive multisystem disorder combining exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, hypoplasia of the nasal alae (nasal wings) and scalp aplasia cutis congenita","Autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in UBR1, a component of the ubiquitin-proteasome protein degradation pathway","Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency presents early with malabsorption, steatorrhoea and failure to thrive and requires pancreatic enzyme replacement and fat-soluble vitamin support","The characteristic facial appearance includes the hypoplastic, beak-like nasal alae with midline scalp defects","Sensorineural hearing loss is common and warrants early audiological assessment","Additional features include hypothyroidism, dental anomalies, growth failure, developmental delay of variable degree and genitourinary or anorectal malformations","Diabetes mellitus can develop over time as the endocrine pancreas becomes involved","Diagnosis rests on the recognisable pattern of malformations confirmed by UBR1 genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Johanson-Blizzard syndrome is caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in UBR1, which encodes an E3 ubiquitin ligase of the N-end rule pathway that targets specific proteins for degradation by the proteasome. Loss of UBR1 function disturbs regulated protein turnover, and the acinar cells of the exocrine pancreas are especially vulnerable, undergoing destruction and fatty or fibrotic replacement that produces early and severe exocrine pancreatic insufficiency. The same developmental disturbance affects craniofacial, ectodermal, endocrine and other structures, giving the hypoplastic nasal alae, aplasia cutis of the scalp, hearing loss, hypothyroidism, dental and genitourinary anomalies and variable developmental delay. Management is supportive, centred on enzyme replacement, nutrition and treatment of the associated endocrine and sensory deficits.","additionalDifferentials":["Shwachman-Diamond syndrome","Cystic fibrosis","Pearson marrow-pancreas syndrome","Other syndromic causes of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","Ectodermal dysplasia syndromes","Other dysmorphic syndromes with aplasia cutis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["UBR1 molecular genetic testing","Faecal elastase and other tests of exocrine pancreatic function","Nutritional assessment with fat-soluble vitamin levels","Audiological evaluation for sensorineural hearing loss","Thyroid function tests and, over time, glucose monitoring for diabetes","Imaging and examination for genitourinary, anorectal and dental anomalies"],"redFlags":["Severe malabsorption with faltering growth in infancy","Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency and its complications","Untreated hypothyroidism affecting development","Development of diabetes mellitus"],"prognosis":"With early pancreatic enzyme replacement, nutritional support and treatment of hypothyroidism and hearing loss, growth and development can improve; long-term outcome depends on the severity of malabsorption, endocrine involvement and any associated malformations and developmental impairment.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with Shwachman-Diamond syndrome or cystic fibrosis and missing the characteristic dysmorphology","Overlooking sensorineural hearing loss that affects development","Failing to monitor for evolving hypothyroidism and diabetes","Under-treating exocrine insufficiency and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency"],"management":["Give pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy to correct exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, steatorrhoea and malabsorption","Supplement fat-soluble vitamins and provide dietetic-led nutritional support to restore growth","Treat associated hypothyroidism with levothyroxine replacement","Provide early audiological rehabilitation with hearing aids for sensorineural hearing loss","Undertake endocrine surveillance with glucose monitoring for later diabetes from progressive pancreatic disease","Arrange surgical correction of genitourinary and anorectal malformations and dedicated dental care","Offer developmental support and multidisciplinary follow-up","Provide autosomal recessive genetic counselling and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["OMIM entry for Johanson-Blizzard Syndrome (UBR1)","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"jones_fracture":{"name":"Jones fracture (fifth metatarsal base)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fifth metatarsal metaphyseal-diaphyseal fracture","zone 2 fifth metatarsal fracture","base of fifth metatarsal fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A true Jones fracture lies at the metaphyseal-diaphyseal junction of the fifth metatarsal (zone 2), extending into the fourth-fifth intermetatarsal articulation, whereas the more distal zone-3 diaphyseal stress fracture lies beyond that joint","This zone sits at a vascular watershed, predisposing to delayed union and non-union","It must be distinguished from a proximal tuberosity avulsion (pseudo-Jones, zone 1), which usually heals well with symptomatic treatment","The typical mechanism is an adduction or inversion load on a plantarflexed foot, or repetitive stress in athletes","Acute fractures may be managed with non-weight-bearing cast immobilisation, but intramedullary screw fixation is often preferred in athletes and high-demand patients","Chronic zone 3 proximal diaphyseal stress fractures show sclerosis and medullary narrowing and behave differently","Careful attention to which zone is involved directs both prognosis and management"],"pathophysiology":"The proximal fifth metatarsal has a zonal blood supply, and the metaphyseal-diaphyseal junction (zone 2) lies at a watershed between the metaphyseal vessels and the nutrient artery supplying the diaphysis. Fractures in this relatively poorly perfused region heal slowly and are prone to delayed and non-union, in contrast to the well-vascularised tuberosity where avulsion fractures unite readily. Tensile and bending forces transmitted through the lateral border of the foot during adduction or inversion, or accumulated microdamage from repetitive loading, exceed the bone's capacity to remodel, producing an acute fracture or a chronic stress fracture.","additionalDifferentials":["Proximal fifth metatarsal tuberosity avulsion (pseudo-Jones)","Zone 3 proximal diaphyseal stress fracture","Lateral ankle ligament sprain","Lisfranc (tarsometatarsal) injury","Cuboid fracture","Peroneal tendon injury","Unfused apophysis or os vesalianum in skeletally immature patients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anteroposterior, oblique and lateral foot radiographs to localise the fracture zone","Weight-bearing views to assess alignment and stability","Comparison of fracture position against the tuberosity and intermetatarsal joint","CT to evaluate suspected non-union or medullary sclerosis","MRI for early diagnosis of stress fracture before radiographic change"],"redFlags":["Established non-union with sclerosis and obliteration of the medullary canal","Recurrent or refractory fracture in an athlete","Misclassification of the fracture zone altering prognosis and treatment","Persistent pain despite immobilisation suggesting failure of union"],"prognosis":"True zone 2 Jones fractures have notable rates of delayed and non-union owing to their watershed blood supply, whereas proximal tuberosity avulsions generally heal uneventfully; surgical fixation improves and speeds union in high-demand patients.","pitfalls":["Confusing a proximal tuberosity avulsion (pseudo-Jones) with a true Jones fracture","Permitting early weight-bearing that promotes non-union","Failing to recognise chronic stress-fracture features on radiographs","Not counselling athletes about the option and benefits of early fixation"],"management":["Treat acute fractures with non-weight-bearing cast or boot immobilisation, recognising the watershed blood supply predisposes to slow union","Offer intramedullary screw fixation for athletes, high-demand patients and displaced fractures to speed and secure union","Optimise bone healing by correcting vitamin D and calcium status, and advising smoking cessation","Monitor union radiographically given the substantial risk of delayed and non-union at the metaphyseal-diaphyseal junction","Manage established non-union with revision fixation and bone grafting, with adjunctive bone stimulation where appropriate","Guide graded return to loading with lateral-column offloading orthotics and rehabilitation","Escalate to surgical fixation if conservative treatment fails to progress to union"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle"],"crossLinks":[]},"joubert_syndrome":{"name":"Joubert syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Joubert syndrome and related disorders","JSRD","molar tooth sign syndrome","AHI1","CEP290","NPHP1","TMEM67","CC2D2A"],"chapter":null,"source":"Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A ciliopathy defined neuroradiologically by the molar tooth sign, produced by cerebellar vermis hypoplasia or aplasia together with thickened, horizontally oriented and elongated superior cerebellar peduncles and a deepened interpeduncular fossa","Classic neonatal picture combines episodic tachypnoea alternating with apnoea, abnormal eye movements including oculomotor apraxia and nystagmus, hypotonia evolving into ataxia, and developmental delay","Inheritance is usually autosomal recessive with marked genetic heterogeneity; more than thirty causative genes encode proteins of the primary cilium and basal body","Because it is a ciliopathy, extra-neurological involvement is common and defines subtypes, including retinal dystrophy, nephronophthisis or cystic renal disease, hepatic fibrosis, and polydactyly","The CEP290 and NPHP1 genotypes carry higher risk of renal disease, while TMEM67 is associated with hepatic fibrosis, guiding organ-directed surveillance","Cognitive outcome is variable and many children have moderate impairment, so early developmental support and multidisciplinary follow-up are important","Prenatal suspicion may arise from vermian abnormality on foetal imaging, and diagnosis is confirmed by the molar tooth sign on postnatal MRI plus molecular testing"],"pathophysiology":"Joubert syndrome is a disorder of the primary cilium and basal body (a ciliopathy). Pathogenic variants in genes encoding ciliary transition-zone and axonemal proteins disrupt ciliary signalling that guides mid-hindbrain patterning and axonal decussation. The resulting failure of cerebellar vermis development and abnormal crossing of superior cerebellar peduncle and corticospinal fibres produce the characteristic mid-hindbrain malformation seen as the molar tooth sign. The same ciliary dysfunction in the retina, renal tubule, and biliary tract accounts for the frequently associated multi-organ features.","additionalDifferentials":["Dandy-Walker malformation and other posterior fossa cystic malformations","Cerebellar vermis hypoplasia from other causes such as congenital disorders of glycosylation","Rhombencephalosynapsis","Pontocerebellar hypoplasia","Other ciliopathies such as Meckel-Gruber and Bardet-Biedl syndrome","Isolated nephronophthisis or Senior-Loken syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to demonstrate the molar tooth sign and characterise vermian and brainstem anatomy","Molecular genetic testing, typically a ciliopathy gene panel or exome sequencing","Renal ultrasound and renal function testing to screen for nephronophthisis or cystic disease","Ophthalmological assessment with electroretinography for retinal dystrophy and oculomotor evaluation","Liver ultrasound and liver function tests to detect hepatic fibrosis","Developmental and neuropsychological assessment to guide support"],"redFlags":["Neonatal apnoea and irregular breathing requiring respiratory monitoring","Progressive renal impairment from nephronophthisis leading to end-stage kidney disease","Deteriorating vision from retinal dystrophy","Signs of portal hypertension or hepatic decompensation from fibrosis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the degree of neurological impairment and the extent of renal, hepatic, and retinal involvement. Many individuals survive into adulthood with variable cognitive and motor disability, but progressive kidney or liver disease can substantially affect life expectancy.","pitfalls":["Attributing neonatal apnoea and hypotonia to birth asphyxia and missing the underlying malformation","Failing to recognise the molar tooth sign on suboptimal or non-dedicated posterior fossa imaging","Not screening for renal, hepatic, and retinal disease that may present years after the neurological diagnosis","Assuming a uniform prognosis despite wide genotype and phenotype variability"],"management":["Provide supportive neonatal care for the abnormal breathing pattern, monitoring and treating episodic apnoea and tachypnoea","Deliver early developmental support with physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy for hypotonia, ataxia and delay","Treat associated seizures with antiseizure medication as required","Institute renal surveillance for nephronophthisis and cystic disease and manage progressive renal failure with dialysis or transplantation","Arrange ophthalmological care for retinal dystrophy and oculomotor apraxia, and monitor for hepatic fibrosis","Tailor organ-directed surveillance to genotype, intensifying renal monitoring in CEP290 and NPHP1 and hepatic monitoring in TMEM67","Coordinate multidisciplinary follow-up and provide autosomal recessive genetic counselling and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","GeneReviews: Joubert Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"junctional_ectopic_tachycardia":{"name":"junctional ectopic tachycardia","system":"cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JET","His bundle tachycardia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["JET is a narrow-complex tachycardia arising from enhanced automaticity of the atrioventricular junction or proximal His bundle, characteristically with atrioventricular dissociation and a ventricular rate exceeding the atrial rate.","The postoperative form is a feared complication of paediatric cardiac surgery, especially repairs near the atrioventricular node such as ventricular septal defect and tetralogy of Fallot correction.","A rarer congenital form presents in infancy, is often incessant and can lead to tachycardia-mediated cardiomyopathy and carries a familial tendency.","The QRS is usually narrow and identical to sinus conduction unless rate-related aberrancy or pre-existing bundle branch block is present.","Retrograde ventriculoatrial conduction may occasionally produce 1 to 1 association, mimicking other supraventricular tachycardias and obscuring the diagnosis.","Cornerstones of postoperative management include avoiding hyperthermia, minimising catecholamines, correcting electrolytes and inducing mild hypothermia.","Because the mechanism is abnormal automaticity, direct-current cardioversion is ineffective, though atrial pacing faster than the junctional rate can restore atrioventricular synchrony.","Loss of the atrial contribution to ventricular filling makes JET poorly tolerated in the vulnerable early postoperative period."],"pathophysiology":"JET is caused by abnormal enhanced automaticity of tissue in the atrioventricular junction or proximal His bundle, which discharges more rapidly than the sinus node and drives the ventricles independently, producing atrioventricular dissociation with a faster ventricular than atrial rate. In the postoperative setting, mechanical trauma, oedema, suture lines and stretch near the conduction system combine with high circulating catecholamines, hyperthermia and electrolyte shifts to provoke this automatic focus. The congenital form reflects an intrinsic abnormality of junctional automaticity presenting in early life. Because activation originates at or below the node, ventricular depolarisation follows the normal His-Purkinje route and the QRS remains narrow unless conduction is otherwise disturbed.","additionalDifferentials":["atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia","atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia","focal atrial tachycardia","ectopic atrial tachycardia in infancy","accelerated junctional rhythm","ventricular tachycardia with narrow QRS"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["twelve-lead ECG and rhythm strip seeking atrioventricular dissociation with ventricular rate exceeding atrial rate","atrial electrogram recording via temporary epicardial wires in the postoperative patient","continuous telemetry to monitor rate, dissociation and haemodynamic effect","serum electrolytes including potassium, magnesium and calcium","core temperature monitoring in the postoperative period","echocardiography to assess ventricular function and structural substrate"],"redFlags":["haemodynamic deterioration in the early postoperative period from loss of atrioventricular synchrony","incessant tachycardia leading to tachycardia-mediated cardiomyopathy in infants","very high junctional rates unresponsive to first-line measures","escalating inotrope and catecholamine requirement aggravating the arrhythmia","signs of low cardiac output syndrome after congenital heart surgery"],"prognosis":"Postoperative JET is usually transient and resolves within a few days with supportive measures, though it increases perioperative morbidity. The congenital form is more difficult to control and, if incessant, may cause cardiomyopathy, but responsiveness to modern rhythm control and ablation has improved outcomes.","pitfalls":["Attempting direct-current cardioversion for an automatic rhythm that will not respond.","Failing to recognise atrioventricular dissociation and misclassifying JET as another supraventricular tachycardia.","Aggravating the arrhythmia with escalating catecholamines and permissive hyperthermia in the postoperative child.","Overlooking retrograde 1 to 1 conduction that masks the diagnosis.","Neglecting electrolyte correction and temperature control as primary therapeutic levers."],"management":["Reduce adrenergic drive in the postoperative form by weaning catecholamine inotropes and deepening sedation and analgesia","Avoid and actively treat hyperthermia, and induce mild systemic hypothermia to slow the automatic junctional focus","Aggressively correct electrolytes, replacing magnesium and potassium","Use amiodarone as the principal antiarrhythmic to suppress the ectopic focus","Restore atrioventricular synchrony with atrial or sequential pacing once the junctional rate is controlled","Treat the incessant congenital form with amiodarone and consider catheter ablation of the focus","Manage tachycardia-mediated cardiomyopathy with heart-failure therapy and rhythm control","Escalate to mechanical circulatory support such as extracorporeal membrane oxygenation for refractory haemodynamic collapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","PACES/HRS Expert Consensus on paediatric and congenital arrhythmias","Zipes Cardiac Electrophysiology: From Cell to Bedside"],"crossLinks":[]},"junctional_epidermolysis_bullosa":{"name":"Junctional epidermolysis bullosa","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JEB","laminin-332 deficiency","LAMA3","LAMB3","LAMC2","collagen XVII deficiency","COL17A1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive form of epidermolysis bullosa caused by defects in components of the hemidesmosome anchoring complex, chiefly laminin-332 (LAMA3, LAMB3, LAMC2) and collagen XVII (COL17A1)","Blistering occurs within the lamina lucida of the basement membrane zone, giving a junctional level of cleavage","The severe generalised subtype presents in the neonate with widespread blistering and characteristic exuberant granulation tissue, often around the mouth, nose, and airway","Prominent perioral and perinasal granulation tissue is a helpful clinical clue to the junctional form","Dental enamel hypoplasia is a characteristic associated feature owing to the role of these proteins in enamel formation","Extracutaneous involvement of the respiratory, gastrointestinal, and genitourinary tracts contributes to significant morbidity","Diagnosis relies on immunofluorescence antigen mapping and electron microscopy to localise the split, with genetic confirmation; management is supportive with wound care and nutritional support"],"pathophysiology":"The dermo-epidermal junction is held together by hemidesmosomes and their associated anchoring filaments, which span the lamina lucida of the basement membrane and connect basal keratinocytes to the underlying lamina densa. Laminin-332 and collagen XVII (the bullous pemphigoid antigen 180) are key structural components of this adhesion complex. In junctional epidermolysis bullosa, biallelic mutations reduce or abolish these proteins, weakening adhesion so that the skin separates within the lamina lucida under minimal mechanical stress. The same proteins are important in mucosal epithelia and in tooth enamel formation, which explains the mucosal involvement and enamel defects. The most severe subtypes cause extensive skin and mucosal loss with a high risk of infection, fluid loss, and airway compromise in infancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Epidermolysis bullosa simplex","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa","Neonatal infective blistering such as staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome and herpes","Incontinentia pigmenti and other neonatal blistering disorders","Aplasia cutis congenita","Autoimmune blistering disease in older patients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with immunofluorescence antigen mapping localising the split to the lamina lucida","Transmission electron microscopy showing hemidesmosomal abnormalities","Molecular genetic testing of LAMA3, LAMB3, LAMC2, and COL17A1","Nutritional and growth assessment with monitoring for anaemia and micronutrient deficiency","Airway, dental, and mucosal evaluation for extracutaneous involvement","Microbiological swabs when infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Airway involvement with stridor or respiratory compromise from laryngeal granulation tissue","Extensive skin loss with sepsis, fluid, and electrolyte disturbance in a neonate","Failure to thrive and severe anaemia from chronic wounds and poor intake","Rapid deterioration in the severe generalised subtype, which carries high infant mortality"],"prognosis":"Outlook varies markedly by subtype: the severe generalised form carries high mortality in infancy from sepsis, respiratory involvement, and failure to thrive, whereas milder junctional variants are compatible with survival into adulthood with chronic blistering and extracutaneous complications.","pitfalls":["Failing to identify the severe generalised subtype early, delaying appropriate supportive and palliative planning","Overlooking airway involvement heralded by perioral and perinasal granulation tissue","Neglecting nutritional support, dental care, and anaemia management","Mistaking neonatal junctional disease for infection","Assuming a uniform prognosis rather than recognising the wide spectrum of severity"],"management":["Provide meticulous atraumatic wound care with non-adherent dressings, blister lancing and protection of fragile skin from shearing","Prevent and treat wound and systemic infection with surveillance swabs, topical antiseptics and culture-directed antibiotics","Give aggressive nutritional support and correct anaemia and micronutrient deficiencies to sustain growth and healing","Monitor and protect the airway for laryngeal and tracheal granulation tissue and involvement","Provide specialist dental care for enamel hypoplasia and manage gastrointestinal and genitourinary complications","Deliver proactive analgesia and coordinate care through a multidisciplinary epidermolysis bullosa team","Offer autosomal recessive genetic counselling with carrier and prenatal testing, and involve palliative care in the severe generalised subtype"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_ankylosing_spondylitis":{"name":"Juvenile ankylosing spondylitis","system":"Rheumatology / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["juvenile-onset ankylosing spondylitis","JAS","juvenile axial spondyloarthritis","juvenile spondyloarthropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology / ILAR & PRINTO criteria","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A juvenile-onset axial spondyloarthritis sharing strong HLA-B27 association and clinical features with enthesitis-related arthritis","In children, peripheral arthritis and enthesitis of the lower limbs and hips often precede axial symptoms by years","Inflammatory back pain (worse at rest and in the early morning, improving with activity) and buttock pain from sacroiliitis mark axial disease","Hip involvement is common in juvenile-onset disease and is an important cause of long-term disability","Acute symptomatic anterior uveitis (painful red eye) can occur, in contrast to the chronic silent uveitis of oligoarticular JIA","MRI detects active sacroiliitis before plain radiographs show structural change, aiding early diagnosis","Management uses NSAIDs and physiotherapy first line, with TNF inhibition for persistent axial disease or inadequate response"],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile ankylosing spondylitis is an axial spondyloarthritis in which HLA-B27-associated immune dysregulation, with prominent involvement of the interleukin-23/interleukin-17 pathway, drives inflammation at entheses, sacroiliac joints and the spine. Enthesitis and osteitis are followed by reparative new bone formation, which over time can produce syndesmophytes, joint fusion and progressive loss of spinal and sacroiliac mobility. Because the skeleton is still growing, hip and axial involvement in childhood can have particularly significant functional and structural consequences.","additionalDifferentials":["Enthesitis-related arthritis and other juvenile spondyloarthritis","Mechanical back pain and postural causes","Chronic non-bacterial osteomyelitis affecting the spine","Infective sacroiliitis or discitis","Inflammatory bowel disease-associated arthritis","Other JIA subtypes","Malignancy involving the spine or pelvis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["HLA-B27 typing to support the diagnosis","MRI of the sacroiliac joints and spine to detect early inflammatory change","Inflammatory markers (erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein)","Radiographs of the sacroiliac joints and spine in established disease","Assessment of spinal mobility and posture for monitoring","Ophthalmology review for symptomatic anterior uveitis","Screening for inflammatory bowel disease when gastrointestinal symptoms occur"],"redFlags":["Progressive loss of spinal mobility or fixed deformity","Significant hip involvement threatening mobility","Acute painful red eye requiring urgent ophthalmology assessment","Neurological symptoms or severe night pain suggesting an alternative serious cause","Systemic features suggesting infection or inflammatory bowel disease"],"prognosis":"Juvenile-onset disease tends to have more peripheral and hip involvement than adult-onset ankylosing spondylitis and can progress to significant axial disease and disability over years. Early diagnosis, physiotherapy and TNF inhibition improve symptom control and may limit structural progression.","pitfalls":["Attributing inflammatory back and buttock pain to mechanical causes and delaying imaging","Relying on plain radiographs, which are insensitive to early sacroiliitis","Underestimating hip disease as a driver of disability","Assuming HLA-B27 negativity excludes the diagnosis","Missing associated uveitis or inflammatory bowel disease"],"management":["Use non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs as first-line therapy for inflammatory back pain and enthesitis","Escalate to TNF inhibitors for active axial disease inadequately controlled by NSAIDs, with IL-17 inhibitors as an alternative biologic","Add sulfasalazine for predominant peripheral arthritis","Provide structured physiotherapy and regular exercise to preserve spinal mobility, posture and hip function","Use intra-articular corticosteroid injection for troublesome peripheral joint or entheseal inflammation","Screen for and treat symptomatic acute anterior uveitis with topical corticosteroids and mydriatics alongside ophthalmology","Monitor hip involvement and refer for arthroplasty where destruction causes disability","Follow axial disease with clinical mobility assessment and MRI to detect active sacroiliitis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology","Consensus guidance on juvenile spondyloarthritis","ASAS criteria for axial spondyloarthritis (adapted to juvenile disease)"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_dermatomyositis":{"name":"Juvenile Dermatomyositis","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JDM","juvenile dermatomyositis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common inflammatory myopathy of childhood, characterised by proximal muscle weakness together with distinctive skin changes.","Look for the heliotrope rash (violaceous discolouration of the upper eyelids) and Gottron papules over the knuckles, elbows, and knees.","Weakness is typically symmetrical and proximal, so children struggle to climb stairs, rise from the floor, or lift their arms overhead.","Nailfold capillary changes (dilated, dropout, or tortuous loops) are a useful bedside clue and correlate with disease activity.","Calcinosis of skin and soft tissues is a late complication, more likely when treatment is delayed or inadequate.","Unlike adult dermatomyositis, an underlying malignancy is rarely associated in children.","A small vessel vasculopathy can affect the gastrointestinal tract, potentially causing ulceration, bleeding, or perforation.","Early diagnosis and adequate immunosuppression improve outcomes and reduce the risk of chronic disability and calcinosis."],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile dermatomyositis is an immune-mediated systemic vasculopathy affecting small vessels of skeletal muscle, skin, and other organs. Complement-mediated injury to the capillary endothelium leads to capillary dropout, ischaemia, and perifascicular muscle fibre atrophy. Interferon-driven inflammation and immune complex deposition contribute to ongoing muscle and skin damage, producing the characteristic weakness and rash.","additionalDifferentials":["Muscular dystrophy","Systemic lupus erythematosus / overlap connective tissue disease","Viral or post-viral myositis","Polymyositis","Hypothyroid or metabolic myopathy","Spinal muscular atrophy or other neuromuscular disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum muscle enzymes (creatine kinase, aldolase, LDH, transaminases)","MRI of muscle to identify inflammation and guide biopsy site","Nailfold capillaroscopy","Myositis-specific and myositis-associated autoantibodies","Muscle biopsy when the diagnosis is uncertain","Electromyography in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Severe dysphagia or nasal regurgitation indicating pharyngeal muscle involvement with aspiration risk","Respiratory muscle weakness or interstitial lung disease causing hypoventilation","Gastrointestinal vasculopathy with abdominal pain, bleeding, or perforation","Rapidly progressive weakness or inability to protect the airway","Extensive skin ulceration reflecting severe vasculopathy"],"prognosis":"With prompt, adequate immunosuppression many children achieve remission and regain function; delayed or insufficient treatment increases the risk of chronic disease, calcinosis, and long-term disability. A monocyclic course carries the best prognosis.","pitfalls":["Attributing subtle proximal weakness to clumsiness or normal fatigue and delaying diagnosis","Assuming normal muscle enzymes exclude the disease, as they can be near-normal in some children","Under-treating early disease and thereby increasing the risk of calcinosis","Overlooking dysphagia and aspiration risk when pharyngeal muscles are involved"],"management":["Induce remission with systemic corticosteroids as the initial mainstay of immunosuppression","Add methotrexate early as a steroid-sparing agent to control disease and limit steroid toxicity","Use intravenous immunoglobulin for refractory skin and muscle disease and escalate to ciclosporin, mycophenolate or rituximab when needed","Provide strict photoprotection with sunscreen and topical therapy for the heliotrope rash and Gottron papules","Deliver physiotherapy to maintain muscle strength and range and to prevent contractures","Treat disease adequately and early to prevent calcinosis, and manage established calcinosis actively","Monitor disease activity with muscle enzymes and nailfold capillaroscopy and watch for dysphagia and respiratory involvement","Coordinate multidisciplinary paediatric rheumatology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_fibroadenoma":{"name":"Juvenile fibroadenoma","system":"Breast","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cellular fibroadenoma","Giant juvenile fibroadenoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Breast Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A variant of fibroadenoma occurring predominantly in adolescents and young women, characterised by increased stromal cellularity and epithelial hyperplasia.","Tends to grow more rapidly and reach a larger size than the common adult fibroadenoma; large lesions are termed giant juvenile fibroadenoma.","Presents as a firm, mobile, painless mass that may cause obvious breast asymmetry, skin stretching and dilated overlying veins.","Rapid growth can compress and displace surrounding normal breast tissue rather than invading it.","Ultrasound is the imaging modality of choice in this young age group, showing a well-defined solid mass.","The principal differential on core biopsy is a benign phyllodes tumour, which juvenile fibroadenoma can closely resemble.","Treatment is enucleation or excision, preserving the developing breast and nipple where possible; the outlook is excellent.","Despite alarming size and rapid growth, it is entirely benign with negligible malignant potential."],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile fibroadenoma is a benign biphasic fibroepithelial tumour arising from the hormone-responsive stroma of the terminal duct lobular unit, most likely reflecting an exaggerated proliferative response to the hormonal milieu of adolescence. It differs from the ordinary adult fibroadenoma in its greater stromal cellularity and its pericanalicular growth pattern with florid, sometimes gynaecomastoid, epithelial hyperplasia. This heightened cellular activity drives the characteristic rapid enlargement and large size, yet the stroma lacks the atypia, mitotic activity, leaf-like architecture and overgrowth that define a phyllodes tumour, so the lesion remains benign.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign phyllodes tumour","Ordinary adult fibroadenoma","Pseudoangiomatous stromal hyperplasia forming a mass","Virginal (juvenile) breast hypertrophy","Breast hamartoma","Tubular or lactating adenoma","Secretory carcinoma (rare in adolescents)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical breast examination with documentation of size and asymmetry","Breast ultrasound as first-line imaging in adolescents","Core needle biopsy to characterise the fibroepithelial lesion","Histology assessing stromal cellularity, atypia and architecture","MRI in very large lesions to plan excision","Follow-up imaging to confirm stability or excision completeness","Multidisciplinary review if phyllodes tumour cannot be excluded"],"redFlags":["Very rapid enlargement with skin compromise or ulceration","Histological features raising the possibility of a phyllodes tumour","Stromal overgrowth or mitotic activity on biopsy","Recurrence after excision","Skin changes or fixity atypical for a benign lesion"],"prognosis":"Juvenile fibroadenoma is benign with an excellent prognosis after complete excision, and malignant transformation is not a feature. Recurrence is uncommon, though further fibroadenomas may develop elsewhere in the breast over time.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying a benign phyllodes tumour as juvenile fibroadenoma on limited biopsy","Delaying excision of a large lesion and allowing further distortion of the developing breast","Performing overly aggressive surgery that damages the breast bud or nipple in an adolescent","Confusing the lesion with virginal breast hypertrophy","Assuming rapid growth implies malignancy and causing unnecessary alarm"],"management":["Confirm the benign fibroepithelial nature with ultrasound-guided core biopsy to distinguish it from a phyllodes tumour before deciding treatment","Excise (enucleate) large or giant lesions and those causing asymmetry or overlying skin compromise","Preserve the developing breast bud and normal breast tissue during surgery in adolescents and use oncoplastic techniques for large defects","Observe small, asymptomatic, radiologically typical lesions with interval ultrasound follow-up","Provide reassurance about the benign course and negligible malignant potential","Arrange follow-up imaging to confirm stability or completeness of excision and to detect further fibroadenomas"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Breast Tumours (current edition)","Standard textbook of paediatric and adolescent breast disorders","National guidance on management of fibroepithelial breast lesions"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_idiopathic_arthritis":{"name":"Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JIA","juvenile rheumatoid arthritis","juvenile chronic arthritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common chronic rheumatic disease of childhood, defined by arthritis beginning before 16 years of age and persisting for at least 6 weeks, with other causes excluded.","It is a heterogeneous group of subtypes, including oligoarticular, polyarticular, systemic, enthesitis-related, and psoriatic forms, each with distinct features and prognosis.","Arthritis presents with joint swelling, morning stiffness or gelling, and limited movement, often with less pain than expected; young children may present with limp or refusal to use a limb.","Oligoarticular JIA is the most common subtype, typically affecting large joints in young girls and carrying a significant risk of chronic anterior uveitis.","Uveitis is frequently asymptomatic, so regular ophthalmological slit-lamp screening is essential to prevent visual loss, particularly in young ANA-positive children.","Systemic JIA features prominent extra-articular disease with quotidian high fever, an evanescent salmon-coloured rash, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, and serositis.","The diagnosis is clinical and one of exclusion; laboratory tests support rather than confirm it, and inflammatory markers may be normal in oligoarticular disease.","A serious complication of systemic JIA is macrophage activation syndrome, a form of haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis that can be rapidly fatal.","Management principles combine anti-inflammatory therapy, disease-modifying agents, and increasingly biologic therapy, alongside physiotherapy to preserve joint function and growth."],"pathophysiology":"JIA results from immune dysregulation leading to chronic inflammation of the synovium. In most subtypes, an aberrant adaptive immune response with T-cell activation and autoantibody production drives synovial proliferation (pannus), which can erode cartilage and bone and disturb the growth of nearby physes. Systemic JIA is instead an autoinflammatory condition driven predominantly by the innate immune system and proinflammatory cytokines such as interleukin-1 and interleukin-6, accounting for its prominent systemic features and its predisposition to macrophage activation syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Septic arthritis and osteomyelitis","Reactive and post-infectious arthritis (including acute rheumatic fever)","Leukaemia and other malignancy presenting with bone or joint pain","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other connective tissue diseases","Inflammatory bowel disease-associated arthritis","Non-inflammatory causes such as trauma, hypermobility, and benign limb pains"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count, ESR, and C-reactive protein","Antinuclear antibody (informs uveitis risk) and rheumatoid factor","HLA-B27 in suspected enthesitis-related arthritis","Slit-lamp ophthalmological examination for uveitis screening","Joint imaging (ultrasound or MRI) to assess synovitis and effusion","Ferritin, triglycerides, and fibrinogen if macrophage activation syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["Features of macrophage activation syndrome (persistent fever, falling cell counts, very high ferritin, hepatic dysfunction)","Findings suggesting malignancy such as severe night pain, cytopenias, or systemic decline","A hot, acutely swollen joint with fever suggesting septic arthritis","Visual symptoms or complications of uveitis","Systemic features such as high spiking fever with serositis"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies by subtype; many children with oligoarticular disease do well, while polyarticular and systemic forms may follow a more persistent course, and modern therapy has substantially improved remission rates and reduced long-term joint damage. Uveitis and macrophage activation syndrome remain important causes of morbidity.","pitfalls":["Missing asymptomatic uveitis by omitting scheduled slit-lamp screening","Overlooking leukaemia in a child with bone pain and abnormal counts before starting steroids","Failing to recognise early macrophage activation syndrome in systemic JIA","Attributing a single hot joint to JIA and missing septic arthritis"],"management":["Use non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for initial symptom control of active arthritis","Give intra-articular corticosteroid injections, particularly effective in oligoarticular disease","Start methotrexate as the first-line disease-modifying drug for polyarticular and persistent disease","Escalate to biologics, using TNF inhibitors for polyarticular disease and IL-1 or IL-6 inhibitors for systemic JIA","Undertake regular slit-lamp ophthalmology screening and treat chronic anterior uveitis to prevent visual loss","Provide physiotherapy and occupational therapy to preserve joint function and growth","Recognise and treat macrophage activation syndrome urgently in systemic JIA with anakinra and corticosteroids","Coordinate multidisciplinary paediatric rheumatology care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_localized_scleroderma":{"name":"Juvenile localised scleroderma (morphoea)","system":"Rheumatology / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["juvenile morphoea","localised scleroderma","linear scleroderma","en coup de sabre"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology / ILAR & PRINTO criteria","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Localised scleroderma is confined to the skin and underlying tissues and, unlike systemic sclerosis, does not cause Raynaud phenomenon or internal organ fibrosis","The linear subtype is the commonest form in children and can extend through subcutaneous fat, muscle and bone, impairing growth of the affected limb","The en coup de sabre variant is a linear lesion on the scalp or face that can be associated with underlying neurological and ophthalmological problems and hemifacial atrophy","Active lesions show an erythematous or violaceous advancing border, while established plaques become sclerotic, waxy and hypo- or hyperpigmented","Linear lesions crossing joints may cause contractures and limb-length discrepancy, so functional and growth monitoring is important","Extracutaneous features such as arthritis, seizures and uveitis can accompany more extensive disease","Methotrexate, often with a course of systemic corticosteroids, is used for active, extending or functionally threatening disease"],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile localised scleroderma is an immune-mediated fibrosing disorder of the skin and deeper tissues. Early inflammation with lymphocytic infiltration and endothelial activation is followed by fibroblast activation and excessive deposition of collagen and extracellular matrix, producing progressive sclerosis. In linear and en coup de sabre forms the fibrotic process can extend into fascia, muscle, bone and, on the face, adjacent structures, so that involvement over a growing limb or the developing face leads to atrophy, contracture and growth disturbance rather than the internal organ fibrosis seen in systemic sclerosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic sclerosis","Eosinophilic fasciitis","Lichen sclerosus","Chronic graft-versus-host disease","Scleredema and scleromyxoedema","Post-inflammatory or post-radiation fibrosis","Panniculitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical scoring of lesion activity and skin thickness for monitoring","Antinuclear antibody, which may be positive but is non-specific","MRI of affected limb or face to assess depth of involvement and central nervous system changes in en coup de sabre","Ophthalmology assessment for uveitis in craniofacial disease","Skin biopsy where the diagnosis is uncertain","Limb-length and joint range assessment for functional monitoring","Thermography or ultrasound to help gauge disease activity where available"],"redFlags":["Rapidly extending linear lesions crossing joints threatening contracture","Craniofacial (en coup de sabre) disease with seizures, headache or focal neurological signs","Progressive limb-length discrepancy or hemifacial atrophy in a growing child","New ocular symptoms suggesting uveitis"],"prognosis":"Many lesions eventually become inactive, but linear and deep disease can leave permanent cosmetic and functional deficits, including limb-length discrepancy, contractures and facial atrophy. Early immunosuppression during the active phase improves outcomes and reduces long-term morbidity.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early inflammatory lesions for eczema or infection and delaying treatment","Assuming localised disease will always remain purely cutaneous and missing deep or neurological extension","Failing to monitor growth and joint function in linear disease","Overlooking ophthalmological and neurological screening in en coup de sabre","Treating only established fibrosis, when therapy is most effective during active inflammation"],"management":["Treat active linear and deep disease with systemic methotrexate, often combined with systemic corticosteroids, to halt the fibrosing process","Manage superficial plaque morphoea with topical corticosteroids, calcineurin inhibitors or calcipotriol and consider phototherapy","Provide physiotherapy and stretching to prevent joint contractures across involved limbs","Monitor lesion activity, skin thickness, limb length and joint range to detect progression and growth disturbance","Arrange ophthalmology and neurology assessment for en coup de sabre and craniofacial disease","Involve reconstructive and orthopaedic surgery for established atrophy, contracture and limb-length discrepancy through a multidisciplinary team","Escalate or restart immunosuppression promptly if disease reactivates or extends"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology","PRINTO/PReS recommendations for juvenile localised scleroderma","Consensus guidance on paediatric morphoea management"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_myelomonocytic_leukaemia":{"name":"Juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JMML"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO haematolymphoid classification / paediatric haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An aggressive clonal myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative neoplasm of early childhood","Presents with hepatosplenomegaly, monocytosis, leucocytosis, cytopenias and often a rash","It is driven by dysregulation of the RAS pathway, with mutations in PTPN11, NRAS, KRAS, NF1 or CBL","There are associations with neurofibromatosis type 1 and Noonan syndrome (the latter can cause a self-limiting JMML-like disorder)","Hypersensitivity of myeloid progenitors to granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor is characteristic","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is the main curative treatment for most cases"],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia arises from constitutive activation of the RAS signal transduction pathway in a haematopoietic stem cell, through mutations in genes such as PTPN11, NRAS, KRAS, NF1 or CBL. This hyperactive signalling makes myeloid progenitors hypersensitive to granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor, driving excessive proliferation of monocytes and granulocytes that infiltrate the spleen, liver, skin and lungs. The clonal proliferation with dysplasia produces the mixed myelodysplastic and myeloproliferative picture and, untreated, is rapidly progressive.","additionalDifferentials":["Infection causing reactive monocytosis/hepatosplenomegaly (for example viral)","Other myeloproliferative or myelodysplastic disorders","Noonan-syndrome-associated myeloproliferative disorder (often transient)","Infantile leukaemoid reactions","Langerhans cell histiocytosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and film showing monocytosis and immature cells","Bone marrow examination with cytogenetics (including monosomy 7 assessment)","RAS-pathway mutation analysis (PTPN11, NRAS, KRAS, NF1, CBL)","Assessment for neurofibromatosis type 1 and Noonan syndrome","Exclusion of infective mimics"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising white cell count and organomegaly","Progressive cytopenias with bleeding or infection","Respiratory compromise from leukaemic infiltration","Transformation to acute myeloid leukaemia"],"prognosis":"Untreated JMML is rapidly fatal in most children; allogeneic stem cell transplantation cures many, though relapse occurs, and certain CBL- and Noonan-associated forms may follow a milder or self-resolving course.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a viral illness with hepatosplenomegaly for JMML and vice versa","Overlooking an underlying RASopathy (NF1, Noonan)","Delaying referral for transplantation","Assuming all RAS-mutant infantile disease is aggressive"],"management":["Proceed to allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the principal curative treatment for most children","Use cytoreduction such as a hypomethylating agent or low-intensity chemotherapy to control disease burden before transplant","Provide supportive care with blood product transfusion and prompt treatment of infection during the disease course","Consider splenectomy in selected cases for symptomatic splenomegaly or to aid engraftment","Monitor closely for relapse after transplant and manage with donor lymphocyte infusion or second transplantation","Identify NF1 and Noonan syndrome associations and adopt watchful waiting for CBL-mutated and Noonan-associated forms that may self-resolve","Provide genetic counselling and family evaluation for the germline predisposition syndromes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","Paediatric haematology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_myoclonic_epilepsy":{"name":"Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy","system":"Neurological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JME","Janz syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common idiopathic (genetic) generalised epilepsy syndrome, usually beginning in adolescence.","The defining seizure type is myoclonic jerks, typically of the arms and shoulders, occurring soon after waking.","Most patients also have generalised tonic-clonic seizures, and a subset have absence seizures.","Early morning clumsiness, such as dropping objects like a toothbrush or cup, is a classic historical clue that is often overlooked.","Seizures are strongly provoked by sleep deprivation, alcohol, and sometimes photic stimulation.","The interictal EEG characteristically shows generalised polyspike-and-wave discharges, and a photoparoxysmal response may be seen.","Development and intelligence are typically normal, and neuroimaging is usually unremarkable.","Seizures are generally well controlled with appropriate broad-spectrum treatment, but the condition usually requires long-term therapy as relapse on withdrawal is common; certain narrow-spectrum antiseizure medicines can worsen it."],"pathophysiology":"JME is a genetically determined generalised epilepsy involving abnormal thalamocortical network excitability and synchronisation rather than a focal structural lesion. Inherited susceptibility, likely polygenic in most cases, produces a lowered threshold for generalised epileptiform discharges. The characteristic myoclonus and generalised seizures arise from these hyperexcitable cortico-thalamic circuits, and the strong link with the sleep-wake transition reflects state-dependent changes in cortical excitability.","additionalDifferentials":["Other idiopathic generalised epilepsies (childhood or juvenile absence epilepsy)","Progressive myoclonic epilepsies","Focal epilepsy with secondary generalisation","Benign physiological (hypnagogic) myoclonus","Non-epileptic movements or tics","Psychogenic non-epileptic events"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["EEG, ideally including a recording after sleep deprivation and on awakening","Photic stimulation during EEG to elicit a photoparoxysmal response","MRI brain, mainly to exclude structural or progressive causes in atypical cases","Detailed history focusing on early-morning jerks and provoking factors","Metabolic or genetic testing when a progressive myoclonic epilepsy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive cognitive decline or neurological deterioration suggesting a progressive myoclonic epilepsy","Focal neurological signs or focal features on EEG or imaging","Seizures worsening after starting treatment (possible inappropriate narrow-spectrum drug)","Frequent generalised tonic-clonic seizures or status epilepticus","Recurrent seizures driven by unrecognised sleep deprivation or non-adherence"],"prognosis":"Prognosis for seizure control is favourable with suitable therapy and lifestyle measures, and cognition is preserved, but JME is generally a lifelong tendency; most patients relapse if medication is stopped, so long-term or indefinite treatment is often needed.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis by not asking specifically about early-morning myoclonic jerks","Misclassifying generalised seizures as focal and selecting a drug that aggravates myoclonus and absences","Underestimating the impact of sleep deprivation, alcohol, and poor adherence on seizure control","Attempting early withdrawal of treatment and precipitating relapse","Overlooking coexisting absence seizures"],"management":["Control seizures with a broad-spectrum antiseizure medication, using sodium valproate for efficacy or levetiracetam and lamotrigine where valproate is unsuitable","Avoid narrow-spectrum agents such as carbamazepine and phenytoin that can aggravate myoclonus and absences","Advise lifestyle measures, avoiding sleep deprivation and excess alcohol and managing photic triggers","Counsel that treatment is usually long-term or indefinite because relapse is common on withdrawal, and reinforce adherence","Provide preconception counselling to women of childbearing potential given the teratogenicity of valproate and adjust therapy accordingly","Reassess the diagnosis and consider a progressive myoclonic epilepsy if seizures prove drug-resistant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_nasopharyngeal_angiofibroma":{"name":"Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JNA","nasopharyngeal angiofibroma","juvenile angiofibroma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign but locally aggressive and highly vascular tumour occurring almost exclusively in adolescent males","The classic presentation is progressive unilateral nasal obstruction with recurrent, sometimes profuse, spontaneous epistaxis","It is thought to arise near the sphenopalatine foramen and spreads submucosally into the nasopharynx, nasal cavity, pterygopalatine fossa, and potentially the orbit and intracranial compartment","Examination reveals a firm reddish mass in the nasopharynx or nasal cavity, and a mass in an adolescent male should raise strong suspicion of angiofibroma","Contrast-enhanced imaging shows an intensely enhancing mass, often with anterior bowing of the posterior maxillary wall (Holman-Miller sign), and defines intracranial or orbital extension","Biopsy is generally avoided because of the serious bleeding risk, and diagnosis rests largely on the characteristic clinical and imaging picture","Preoperative angiography with embolisation of feeding vessels reduces intraoperative haemorrhage","Surgical excision, endoscopic or open depending on extent, is the mainstay, with radiotherapy reserved for extensive or unresectable intracranial disease"],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma is a histologically benign tumour composed of a fibrous stroma interspersed with numerous thin-walled vascular channels that frequently lack a complete muscular coat, which explains the tendency to torrential bleeding. It typically originates around the sphenopalatine foramen and pterygopalatine fossa and grows submucosally along natural pathways, expanding into the nasopharynx and nasal cavity and eroding bone to reach the orbit, infratemporal fossa, and skull base. Its almost exclusive occurrence in adolescent males has prompted the hypothesis of hormonal (androgen) sensitivity influencing growth.","additionalDifferentials":["Antrochoanal polyp","Rhinosporidiosis","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","Nasal or nasopharyngeal lymphoma","Rhabdomyosarcoma in a young patient","Encephalocoele presenting as a nasopharyngeal mass"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced computed tomography and magnetic resonance imaging to define the tumour extent, bony erosion, and intracranial or orbital spread","Angiography to identify feeding vessels (commonly branches of the internal maxillary artery) and enable preoperative embolisation","Nasal endoscopy to assess the mass while avoiding biopsy","Full blood count and cross-matching in anticipation of significant blood loss"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or profuse epistaxis that can be life-threatening from this highly vascular tumour","Biopsy in the clinic setting risking uncontrolled haemorrhage and generally to be avoided","Intracranial or orbital extension threatening vision or neurological structures","Rapid enlargement or cranial nerve involvement requiring urgent specialist management"],"prognosis":"Although histologically benign, the tumour is locally aggressive and prone to recurrence when incompletely excised; complete removal, aided by preoperative embolisation, gives good long-term control, while extensive skull-base or intracranial disease is more difficult to eradicate and may require radiotherapy.","pitfalls":["Performing an outpatient biopsy of a vascular nasopharyngeal mass and precipitating severe bleeding","Dismissing recurrent epistaxis in an adolescent male as trivial","Underestimating tumour extent by omitting cross-sectional imaging and angiography","Inadequate resection leaving residual tumour at the skull base with subsequent recurrence"],"management":["Undertake surgical excision, endoscopic or open depending on extent, as the definitive treatment","Perform preoperative angiographic embolisation of feeding vessels, typically internal maxillary artery branches, to reduce intraoperative haemorrhage","Avoid diagnostic biopsy because of the risk of torrential bleeding, relying on characteristic imaging instead","Ensure blood is cross-matched and available in anticipation of significant perioperative blood loss","Reserve radiotherapy for extensive skull-base or intracranial disease not amenable to complete resection","Undertake postoperative surveillance imaging to detect recurrence after incomplete excision","Manage through a multidisciplinary team of ENT surgery, interventional radiology and neurosurgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_pagets_disease":{"name":"Juvenile Paget disease (idiopathic hyperphosphatasia, TNFRSF11B)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["idiopathic hyperphosphatasia","familial idiopathic hyperphosphatasia","hereditary hyperphosphatasia","osteoprotegerin deficiency","JPD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism (ASBMR)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in TNFRSF11B, the gene encoding osteoprotegerin","Loss of osteoprotegerin removes inhibition of the RANK-RANKL pathway, driving grossly accelerated bone turnover throughout the skeleton","Presents in infancy or early childhood with bone pain, fractures, progressive long-bone bowing, and enlargement and deformity of the skull","Markedly elevated serum alkaline phosphatase and other bone turnover markers reflect the very high remodelling rate","Progressive deafness is common, and skull expansion can raise concern for cranial nerve compromise","Unlike classic adult Paget disease it is generalised rather than focal and manifests in childhood","Antiresorptive therapy with bisphosphonates can suppress the excessive turnover and improve symptoms and deformity"],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile Paget disease is caused by biallelic inactivating mutations in TNFRSF11B, which encodes osteoprotegerin, a decoy receptor that normally binds RANKL and prevents it from activating RANK on osteoclast precursors. Without functional osteoprotegerin, RANKL signalling is unopposed, leading to greatly increased osteoclast formation and activity. Coupled to this is a compensatory surge in osteoblastic bone formation, so the skeleton undergoes extremely rapid but disorganised remodelling. The resulting woven, mechanically weak bone produces widespread deformity, bowing, fractures, and skull enlargement, and the very high turnover is mirrored by strikingly raised alkaline phosphatase.","additionalDifferentials":["Classic (adult) Paget disease of bone","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Fibrous dysplasia","Rickets and osteomalacia","Hyperphosphatasia with mental retardation syndrome","Other high-turnover metabolic bone disorders of childhood"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum alkaline phosphatase and additional bone turnover markers","Serum calcium, phosphate, and vitamin D to characterise mineral status","Plain radiographs showing generalised osteopenia or sclerosis, bowing, and skull thickening","Genetic testing for TNFRSF11B mutations","Audiological assessment for hearing loss","Cranial imaging when skull-base involvement or neurological features are present"],"redFlags":["Progressive hearing loss or cranial neuropathy from skull involvement","Recurrent fractures and rapidly worsening bony deformity","Signs of raised intracranial pressure from skull expansion","Retinal or vascular calcification and cardiovascular complications reported in some forms"],"prognosis":"Untreated disease causes progressive deforming bone disease, fractures, and deafness with substantial disability, but antiresorptive therapy can markedly reduce turnover and improve the clinical course, making early diagnosis important.","pitfalls":["Confusing the generalised childhood disease with focal adult Paget disease","Overlooking early hearing loss that warrants audiological follow-up","Attributing the very high alkaline phosphatase to liver disease rather than bone turnover","Delaying antiresorptive treatment that can alter the disease trajectory"],"management":["Suppress the grossly accelerated bone turnover with antiresorptive bisphosphonate therapy as the mainstay of treatment","Ensure adequate calcium and vitamin D repletion to support mineralisation during antiresorptive therapy","Monitor treatment response with serial alkaline phosphatase and other bone turnover markers","Provide orthopaedic management of fractures, long-bone bowing and deformity","Undertake audiological assessment and provide hearing aids for progressive deafness","Perform cranial and skull-base surveillance for nerve compromise from skull expansion","Offer physiotherapy and mobility support to preserve function","Arrange autosomal recessive genetic counselling and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism (ASBMR)","GeneReviews / peer-reviewed reviews of juvenile Paget disease (idiopathic hyperphosphatasia)"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_plantar_dermatosis":{"name":"juvenile plantar dermatosis","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["forefoot dermatitis","sweaty sock dermatitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents in prepubertal children as symmetrical, shiny, glazed erythema with fissuring over the weight-bearing forefoot and plantar surfaces of the great toes.","The instep and the interdigital web spaces are characteristically spared, a pattern that helps distinguish it from tinea pedis and allergic contact dermatitis.","Occlusive synthetic footwear and socks with alternating sweating and rapid drying are central to the process, hence the descriptive term sweaty sock dermatitis.","It is often painful rather than itchy, with a smooth glazed cracked-porcelain appearance and painful fissures that impair walking.","There is an association with atopy, and many affected children have a personal or family history of eczema, asthma or hay fever.","Management centres on reducing occlusion and friction with breathable leather or open footwear, cotton socks, emollients and greasy barrier ointments.","Potassium hydroxide microscopy and fungal culture should be negative and are useful to exclude tinea, which typically involves the web spaces and instep.","The condition tends to settle around puberty, so reassurance about eventual spontaneous resolution is an important part of counselling."],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile plantar dermatosis is a friction and moisture-related dermatitis of the weight-bearing plantar skin, thought to arise from repeated cycles of hyperhydration and rapid dehydration within occlusive modern footwear and synthetic socks. Sweat is trapped against the skin during activity, then evaporates quickly, and this alternating wetting and drying, combined with mechanical friction on pressure-bearing surfaces, disrupts the stratum corneum barrier of the forefoot. The resulting impaired barrier produces the glazed, fissured appearance. An atopic tendency predisposes to the disorder through an inherently compromised epidermal barrier, and the condition is not primarily allergic or infective, although secondary irritant and, occasionally, contact factors may aggravate it.","additionalDifferentials":["tinea pedis with web-space and instep involvement","allergic contact dermatitis to footwear components such as rubber or chromate","atopic dermatitis of the feet","palmoplantar psoriasis","pitted keratolysis","hereditary or acquired palmoplantar keratoderma","dyshidrotic (pompholyx) eczema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["potassium hydroxide microscopy and fungal culture of scale to exclude tinea pedis","patch testing when footwear allergic contact dermatitis is suspected","bacterial swab if secondary infection or pitted keratolysis is considered","clinical assessment for atopic stigmata elsewhere on the skin","skin biopsy only in atypical or treatment-resistant cases","examination of footwear and sock materials as part of history","review for koebnerising psoriasis at other sites such as nails and scalp"],"redFlags":["web-space maceration or unilateral disease suggesting tinea rather than juvenile plantar dermatosis","spreading vesiculation or oozing indicating secondary bacterial infection","nail pitting, onycholysis or plaques elsewhere pointing to psoriasis","onset outside the typical prepubertal age range prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","severe painful fissuring limiting mobility and quality of life"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limiting, characteristically improving and resolving spontaneously around puberty. Symptoms fluctuate with footwear and activity, and consistent barrier and footwear measures usually control discomfort in the interim.","pitfalls":["misdiagnosing it as tinea pedis and prescribing prolonged antifungals that do not help","overlooking the diagnostic sparing of the instep and web spaces","relying on potent topical corticosteroids alone rather than addressing footwear occlusion and friction","failing to reassure families that resolution around puberty is expected","missing coexisting atopic dermatitis or early psoriasis"],"management":["Reduce occlusion and friction by switching to breathable leather or open footwear and cotton socks, alternating pairs to allow drying","Apply liberal greasy emollients and barrier ointments to restore the disrupted stratum corneum barrier","Use a short course of a topical corticosteroid ointment for inflamed fissured areas, with topical calcineurin inhibitors as a steroid-sparing option","Protect painful fissures with occlusive hydrocolloid dressings or tape to aid healing and walking","Treat any confirmed secondary bacterial infection or coexisting tinea pedis identified on swab or mycology","Address coexisting atopic eczema and refer to paediatric dermatology for atypical or treatment-resistant cases","Reassure that the condition is benign and self-limiting, usually resolving around puberty, while reinforcing footwear measures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Neonatal and Infant Dermatology (Eichenfield, Frieden)"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_polyposis":{"name":"Juvenile polyposis syndrome","system":"Oncology / Genetic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JPS","juvenile polyposis","familial juvenile polyposis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / NCCN & genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by germline variants in SMAD4 or BMPR1A, disrupting the TGF-beta/BMP signalling pathway, inherited autosomal dominantly with a significant de novo rate","The term juvenile refers to the histological type of hamartomatous polyp rather than the age of onset, though presentation is often in childhood or young adulthood","Multiple juvenile polyps develop throughout the gastrointestinal tract, most commonly in the colon and rectum but also in the stomach and small bowel","Common presenting features include rectal bleeding, iron-deficiency anaemia, and, with large polyps, intussusception, prolapse, or protein-losing enteropathy","Confers an increased lifetime risk of colorectal cancer and, especially with gastric involvement, gastric cancer, warranting endoscopic surveillance and polypectomy","SMAD4 variants are associated with a combined phenotype overlapping hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia, so carriers should be screened for arteriovenous malformations","Surveillance involves periodic upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and colonoscopy with polyp clearance, and colectomy or gastrectomy may be needed for uncontrollable polyp burden or cancer","A negative family history does not exclude the diagnosis given frequent de novo variants, so isolated cases with multiple juvenile polyps warrant genetic evaluation"],"pathophysiology":"SMAD4 and BMPR1A are components of the TGF-beta/BMP signalling pathway, which restrains proliferation and promotes differentiation of gastrointestinal epithelial and stromal cells. Loss of function disrupts this growth-suppressing signalling, producing the characteristic hamartomatous juvenile polyps and creating a field of mucosa predisposed to dysplastic and malignant change. SMAD4 additionally regulates vascular integrity, which explains the overlap of SMAD4-related juvenile polyposis with hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia and its arteriovenous malformations.","additionalDifferentials":["Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","Cowden syndrome and other PTEN hamartoma tumour syndromes","Familial adenomatous polyposis","Solitary sporadic juvenile polyp (not a syndrome)","Other mixed or hamartomatous polyposis syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Germline SMAD4 and BMPR1A sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis with genetic counselling","Colonoscopy and upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with polypectomy for surveillance","Full blood count and iron studies to assess chronic gastrointestinal blood loss","Small-bowel evaluation where clinically indicated","Screening for hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia features (including pulmonary and cerebral arteriovenous malformations) in SMAD4 carriers"],"redFlags":["Significant or recurrent gastrointestinal bleeding with anaemia","Intussusception, rectal prolapse, or protein-losing enteropathy from heavy polyp burden","Endoscopic or histological features of dysplasia or malignancy","Complications of arteriovenous malformations in SMAD4-related disease"],"prognosis":"With regular endoscopic surveillance and polyp clearance, the risk of gastrointestinal cancer can be reduced and outcomes are favourable, though heavy polyp burden may ultimately require surgical resection. In SMAD4 carriers, prognosis also depends on identifying and managing arteriovenous malformations.","pitfalls":["Regarding a juvenile polyp as invariably benign and failing to recognise the polyposis syndrome and its cancer risk","Omitting upper gastrointestinal surveillance and missing gastric polyps or cancer","Not screening SMAD4 carriers for hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia and dangerous arteriovenous malformations","Assuming a negative family history excludes the diagnosis despite common de novo variants"],"management":["Endoscopic polypectomy at colonoscopy and upper gastrointestinal endoscopy to clear hamartomatous polyps and reduce malignant potential","Regular endoscopic surveillance of the colon, rectum, stomach and duodenum with intervals guided by polyp burden","Correct iron-deficiency anaemia from chronic gastrointestinal blood loss with iron replacement and manage protein-losing enteropathy","Consider risk-reducing colectomy or gastrectomy when polyp burden becomes endoscopically unmanageable or dysplasia develops","Screen SMAD4 carriers for hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia, including pulmonary and cerebral arteriovenous malformations","Offer genetic counselling with germline SMAD4/BMPR1A testing and predictive testing and surveillance of first-degree relatives","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across cancer genetics, gastroenterology and colorectal surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","NCCN guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_polyposis_syndrome":{"name":"Juvenile polyposis syndrome","system":"Oncology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JPS","SMAD4","BMPR1A","juvenile polyposis coli"],"chapter":null,"source":"DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer / NCCN genetic/familial guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by germline pathogenic variants in SMAD4 or BMPR1A, both components of the BMP/TGF-beta signalling pathway, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern","Characterised by multiple hamartomatous juvenile polyps throughout the gastrointestinal tract, most often colorectal but also gastric and small-bowel","The term juvenile refers to the histological polyp type, not the age of the patient, and polyps carry a raised risk of colorectal and gastric cancer","SMAD4 carriers may have a combined phenotype with hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia, so screening for arteriovenous malformations is important","Presentation is often with gastrointestinal bleeding, anaemia, protein-losing enteropathy or intussusception in childhood or early adulthood","Surveillance relies on regular colonoscopy and upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with polypectomy, and risk-reducing colectomy or gastrectomy is considered when polyp burden becomes unmanageable","Massive gastric polyposis is more typical of SMAD4-related disease and can require surgical management"],"pathophysiology":"The syndrome arises from germline loss-of-function variants in SMAD4 or BMPR1A, genes that transmit bone morphogenetic protein and transforming growth factor-beta signals regulating epithelial and stromal growth in the gut. Disruption of this pathway impairs the normal restraint on cell proliferation and alters the epithelial-mesenchymal interactions of the intestinal wall, favouring the development of hamartomatous polyps. Accumulation of further somatic changes within these polyps underlies the progression to dysplasia and gastrointestinal malignancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Peutz-Jeghers syndrome (STK11)","PTEN hamartoma tumour syndrome (Cowden and related conditions)","Familial adenomatous polyposis","Serrated polyposis syndrome","Hereditary mixed polyposis syndrome","Cronkhite-Canada syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Germline SMAD4 and BMPR1A sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis","Colonoscopy with polypectomy and histological confirmation of juvenile-type polyps","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy to assess gastric and duodenal polyps","Full blood count and iron studies to detect chronic blood loss and anaemia","Screening for hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia features in SMAD4 carriers, including pulmonary and cerebral vascular assessment","Cross-sectional or capsule imaging of the small bowel where indicated"],"redFlags":["Significant or recurrent gastrointestinal bleeding with anaemia","Rapidly increasing polyp burden or a focal mass suggesting malignant change","Features of pulmonary or cerebral arteriovenous malformation in a SMAD4 carrier","Intussusception or obstruction from large polyps"],"prognosis":"With regular endoscopic surveillance and polypectomy most patients are managed effectively, but cumulative gastrointestinal cancer risk is substantial and increases with polyp burden. SMAD4 carriers require additional vigilance for vascular malformations, and severe gastric polyposis can necessitate surgery, so lifelong multidisciplinary follow-up is needed.","pitfalls":["Confusing sporadic solitary juvenile polyps in children with the inherited syndrome","Overlooking the hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia overlap and its vascular complications in SMAD4 carriers","Focusing on the colon and neglecting gastric surveillance where cancer risk is also raised","Failing to offer cascade genetic testing to at-risk relatives"],"management":["Clear polyps by endoscopic polypectomy at colonoscopy and upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with histological confirmation of juvenile-type polyps","Maintain lifelong endoscopic surveillance of colon, stomach and small bowel, with frequency dictated by polyp number and dysplasia","Replace iron and manage anaemia, gastrointestinal bleeding and protein-losing enteropathy","Reserve risk-reducing colectomy or gastrectomy for unmanageable polyposis or high-grade dysplasia","Screen SMAD4 carriers for arteriovenous malformations of the lungs and brain given overlap with hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia","Provide genetic counselling and germline SMAD4/BMPR1A testing with cascade screening of at-risk relatives","Manage within a multidisciplinary medical genetics, gastroenterology and surgical team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer: Principles and Practice of Oncology","NCCN Guidelines: Genetic/Familial High-Risk Assessment - Colorectal"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_psoriatic_arthritis":{"name":"Juvenile psoriatic arthritis","system":"Rheumatology / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["juvenile PsA","psoriatic JIA","childhood psoriatic arthritis","JPsA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology / ILAR & PRINTO criteria","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Under the ILAR system, diagnosis requires arthritis with psoriasis, or arthritis with at least two of dactylitis, nail pitting or onycholysis, and a first-degree relative with psoriasis","Arthritis often precedes the skin disease in children, so psoriasis may not be evident at presentation","Dactylitis (sausage digit) reflects combined joint and tenosynovial inflammation and is a useful clue","Nail changes such as pitting and onycholysis support the diagnosis even when skin psoriasis is subtle or absent","A bimodal pattern is described, with a younger group resembling oligoarticular disease (antinuclear-antibody positive, uveitis risk) and an older group resembling spondyloarthritis","Chronic anterior uveitis surveillance is required, particularly in younger antinuclear-antibody-positive children","Treatment follows JIA principles with NSAIDs, intra-articular corticosteroids, methotrexate and TNF inhibition for resistant disease"],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile psoriatic arthritis is an immune-mediated inflammatory arthropathy sharing mechanisms with both juvenile idiopathic arthritis and the spondyloarthritis spectrum. Activation of T cells and cytokine pathways, including tumour necrosis factor and the interleukin-23/interleukin-17 axis, drives synovitis, enthesitis and the tenosynovial inflammation responsible for dactylitis, alongside the epidermal hyperproliferation of psoriasis and nail disease. The condition is heterogeneous, with a younger subgroup behaving like early-onset oligoarticular disease and an older subgroup showing entheseal and axial features.","additionalDifferentials":["Other JIA subtypes, particularly oligoarticular and enthesitis-related arthritis","Reactive arthritis","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis","Inflammatory bowel disease-associated arthritis","Post-viral arthritis","Non-psoriatic causes of dactylitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful skin and nail examination for psoriasis, pitting and onycholysis","Antinuclear antibody to stratify uveitis risk","Inflammatory markers (erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein)","Joint ultrasound or MRI to confirm synovitis, tenosynovitis and enthesitis","HLA-B27 in older children with axial or entheseal features","Slit-lamp ophthalmology assessment for uveitis surveillance","Radiographs of affected joints in established disease"],"redFlags":["Silent sight-threatening uveitis in younger antinuclear-antibody-positive children","Axial or sacroiliac involvement suggesting a spondyloarthritis phenotype","Rapidly erosive or destructive joint disease","Systemic upset or night pain suggesting infection or malignancy"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary with phenotype; some children have limited disease while others develop persistent or erosive arthritis. As with other JIA subtypes, early treatment and diligent uveitis surveillance improve long-term joint and visual outcomes.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because psoriasis is not yet apparent, when arthritis often comes first","Overlooking subtle nail changes and dactylitis","Neglecting uveitis surveillance in younger antinuclear-antibody-positive children","Failing to recognise an evolving spondyloarthritis phenotype in older children","Delaying disease-modifying therapy in progressive disease"],"management":["Intra-articular corticosteroid injections for active oligoarticular disease, with NSAIDs for symptomatic joint and entheseal relief","Methotrexate as first-line disease-modifying therapy for persistent or polyarticular disease and for dactylitis","Escalate to TNF inhibitors or IL-17/IL-23-axis biologics for methotrexate-refractory joint, axial or skin disease","Treat cutaneous psoriasis and nail disease with topical therapy in coordination with dermatology","Undertake regular slit-lamp uveitis surveillance, particularly in younger antinuclear-antibody-positive children","Provide physiotherapy and occupational therapy to preserve joint range and function","Coordinate multidisciplinary paediatric rheumatology, dermatology and ophthalmology care with structured transition to adult services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology","ILAR classification of juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Consensus guidance on juvenile psoriatic arthritis"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_sjogren_syndrome":{"name":"Juvenile Sjogren syndrome","system":"Rheumatology / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["childhood Sjogren syndrome","paediatric Sjogren syndrome","juvenile Sjögren syndrome","juvenile sicca syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology / ILAR & PRINTO criteria","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic autoimmune exocrinopathy targeting salivary and lacrimal glands, but children often present differently from adults","Recurrent bilateral parotid gland swelling (parotitis) is a common and sometimes presenting feature in children, more so than the classic sicca symptoms","Dry eyes and dry mouth (sicca) may be underreported by children, so a low threshold for objective testing is needed","Extraglandular manifestations include arthralgia, arthritis, renal tubular acidosis, cytopenias and neurological involvement","Anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB antibodies, antinuclear antibody, rheumatoid factor and polyclonal hypergammaglobulinaemia support the diagnosis","There is a long-term, though low in childhood, risk of B-cell lymphoma, warranting attention to persistent glandular enlargement","Management is largely supportive for sicca symptoms, with immunosuppression reserved for significant systemic involvement"],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile Sjogren syndrome is characterised by lymphocytic infiltration of exocrine glands, chiefly the salivary and lacrimal glands, driven by autoreactive T and B cells. Glandular epithelial activation, type I interferon signalling and B-cell hyperactivity lead to progressive glandular dysfunction and the production of autoantibodies such as anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB. Chronic B-cell stimulation underlies the hypergammaglobulinaemia and the long-term predisposition to lymphoma, while immune complex and interferon-driven inflammation account for extraglandular features affecting joints, kidneys, blood and the nervous system.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent juvenile parotitis (idiopathic)","Viral parotitis (e.g. mumps, Epstein-Barr virus, HIV) and other infective sialadenitis","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other overlapping connective tissue disease","Sarcoidosis","IgG4-related disease","Lymphoma or other glandular neoplasm","Cystic fibrosis-related sialadenopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB antibodies, antinuclear antibody and rheumatoid factor","Immunoglobulin levels looking for polyclonal hypergammaglobulinaemia","Objective ocular testing (e.g. Schirmer test and ocular surface staining)","Salivary gland ultrasound and, where indicated, minor salivary gland biopsy","Renal function and urinalysis to detect tubular acidosis","Full blood count for cytopenias","Inflammatory markers and complement levels"],"redFlags":["Persistent unilateral or hard glandular enlargement raising concern for lymphoma","Renal tubular acidosis with electrolyte disturbance","Neurological involvement such as myelopathy or neuropathy","Significant cytopenias or vasculitic features"],"prognosis":"Childhood disease is often dominated by recurrent parotitis and may follow a relatively indolent glandular course, but systemic involvement and the lifelong, if low, lymphoma risk require ongoing surveillance. Sicca symptoms tend to persist and can affect dental and ocular health over time.","pitfalls":["Attributing recurrent parotid swelling to simple infection and missing the underlying autoimmune process","Relying on adult sicca-based criteria when children present with glandular swelling instead","Overlooking renal tubular acidosis and its metabolic consequences","Neglecting dental and ocular preventive care","Failing to investigate persistent asymmetrical glandular enlargement for lymphoma"],"management":["Manage ocular sicca with lubricating artificial tears and ocular surface protection, and stimulate saliva with sialogogues alongside intensive dental caries prevention","Use hydroxychloroquine for arthralgia, fatigue and mucocutaneous features","Add corticosteroids with further immunosuppression or rituximab for significant extraglandular disease","Detect and treat renal tubular acidosis with alkali replacement and monitor for cytopenias","Treat recurrent parotitis supportively and evaluate persistent or asymmetrical glandular enlargement for B-cell lymphoma","Maintain long-term surveillance for systemic involvement and the low but lifelong lymphoma risk","Coordinate multidisciplinary paediatric rheumatology, ophthalmology and dental care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology","Proposed criteria for childhood Sjogren syndrome","ACR-EULAR classification criteria for Sjogren syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_spring_eruption":{"name":"Juvenile spring eruption","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JSE","juvenile spring eruption of the ears","spring eruption of the ears"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign photodermatosis, widely regarded as a localised variant of polymorphic light eruption, that chiefly affects the ears of boys and young men","It characteristically occurs in spring, often after cold, bright and sunny weather, and outbreaks may cluster among schoolchildren","Itchy papules and papulovesicles develop on the light-exposed helices of the ears and evolve into crusted lesions over a few days to weeks","The relatively unprotected ears of those with short hair are most susceptible, and a combination of ultraviolet exposure and cold is thought to contribute","The eruption is self-limiting and typically heals without scarring","Diagnosis is clinical, and the distribution on the ears in a young male during spring is highly characteristic","Management is with photoprotection of the ears, and the tendency often lessens with age"],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile spring eruption is considered a topographical variant of polymorphic light eruption in which a delayed hypersensitivity response to an ultraviolet-induced cutaneous antigen is provoked on the exposed, thin skin of the helices. The predilection for the ears of boys reflects their relative lack of protection by hair, and the springtime clustering suggests that low ambient temperature acting together with ultraviolet exposure lowers the threshold for the reaction. As with polymorphic light eruption, the process is benign and self-limited.","additionalDifferentials":["Polymorphic light eruption at other sites","Erythropoietic protoporphyria","Contact dermatitis of the ears","Impetigo or other bacterial infection of the ear","Herpes simplex or other vesicular viral infection","Chilblains (perniosis) of the ears"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on the characteristic springtime eruption on the ears of a young male","History correlating onset with cold, sunny weather and light exposure","Phototesting only in atypical or persistent cases","Skin biopsy reserved for diagnostic uncertainty","Porphyrin screen if erythropoietic protoporphyria is suspected","Bacterial or viral swabs if secondary infection or a vesicular infection is considered"],"redFlags":["Marked burning or pain on sun exposure suggesting erythropoietic protoporphyria","Persistent, scarring or recurrent lesions outside the expected pattern","Features suggesting secondary bacterial infection requiring treatment","Extension to widespread or covered sites prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Juvenile spring eruption is benign and self-limiting, resolving over a few weeks without scarring; recurrences in spring are common during childhood and adolescence but the tendency usually diminishes with age, and photoprotection of the ears reduces further episodes.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the crusted lesions for impetigo and treating with antibiotics alone","Overlooking the diagnosis because the eruption is confined to the ears rather than typical polymorphic light eruption sites","Failing to consider erythropoietic protoporphyria when there is prominent burning","Not advising ear photoprotection, so that predictable spring recurrences continue"],"management":["Photoprotect the ears with hats, hair cover and broad-spectrum sunscreen to prevent recurrences","Apply topical corticosteroids to settle the itchy inflamed papulovesicular eruption on the helices","Relieve itch with emollients and oral antihistamines","Provide wound care with cool compresses for crusted lesions and treat any secondary bacterial infection","Reassure that the eruption is benign and self-limiting and heals without scarring","Advise that the springtime tendency diminishes with age, reserving phototesting for atypical or persistent cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","British Photodermatology Group guidance on the idiopathic photodermatoses"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_systemic_sclerosis":{"name":"Juvenile systemic sclerosis","system":"Rheumatology / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["juvenile scleroderma","paediatric systemic sclerosis","juvenile SSc","childhood systemic sclerosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology / ILAR & PRINTO criteria","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare multisystem connective tissue disease characterised by skin fibrosis together with vascular and internal organ involvement, distinguishing it from localised scleroderma","Raynaud phenomenon is usually the earliest feature and may precede skin changes by months to years, sometimes with digital ulcers or pitting scars","Skin changes progress from oedematous puffy fingers to sclerodactyly and proximal skin tightening, and nailfold capillaroscopy typically shows abnormal capillary loops","Interstitial lung disease and pulmonary arterial hypertension are the leading causes of mortality and require active surveillance","Gastrointestinal involvement causes reflux, dysmotility and malabsorption, while renal crisis, though rarer in children, is a hypertensive emergency","Autoantibodies such as anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase I) and anticentromere help characterise the disease and its organ-risk profile","Management is organ-directed and immunosuppressive, using agents such as methotrexate or mycophenolate, with vasodilators for vascular complications"],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile systemic sclerosis arises from the interplay of three processes: microvascular injury and dysfunction, immune activation with autoantibody production, and progressive fibrosis. Endothelial damage and obliterative vasculopathy underlie Raynaud phenomenon, digital ischaemia and pulmonary hypertension, while activated fibroblasts deposit excess collagen in skin and internal organs. The resulting fibrosis of lung, gut, heart and other tissues produces the characteristic multisystem morbidity. Compared with adults, children more often have overlap features and, overall, a somewhat different organ-involvement pattern.","additionalDifferentials":["Localised scleroderma (morphoea)","Mixed connective tissue disease and overlap syndromes","Juvenile dermatomyositis","Eosinophilic fasciitis","Primary Raynaud phenomenon","Scleroedema and other sclerosing skin disorders","Chronic graft-versus-host disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antinuclear antibody with anti-Scl-70 and anticentromere antibody profiling","Nailfold capillaroscopy to detect a scleroderma pattern","Pulmonary function tests and high-resolution chest CT for interstitial lung disease","Echocardiography to screen for pulmonary arterial hypertension","Blood pressure monitoring and renal function to detect renal involvement","Gastrointestinal investigations for reflux and dysmotility when symptomatic","Skin scoring and inflammatory markers for disease monitoring"],"redFlags":["New or worsening breathlessness suggesting interstitial lung disease or pulmonary hypertension","Accelerated hypertension with renal impairment indicating scleroderma renal crisis","Critical digital ischaemia or ulceration","Cardiac symptoms suggesting myocardial or pericardial involvement","Rapidly progressive proximal skin thickening"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is determined chiefly by cardiopulmonary and renal involvement; interstitial lung disease and pulmonary arterial hypertension are the principal causes of death. Early detection of organ involvement and timely immunosuppressive and vasoactive therapy improve outcomes, though the disease can be chronic and disabling.","pitfalls":["Dismissing isolated Raynaud phenomenon without checking for early systemic disease and abnormal capillaroscopy","Failing to screen proactively for lung and pulmonary vascular disease before symptoms appear","Confusing juvenile systemic sclerosis with localised scleroderma and underestimating organ risk","Overlooking hypertension as an early sign of renal crisis","Attributing gastrointestinal symptoms to unrelated causes"],"management":["Immunosuppress with methotrexate or mycophenolate, escalating to cyclophosphamide or rituximab for progressive interstitial lung disease","Manage Raynaud phenomenon and digital ischaemia with calcium-channel blockers and phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors, adding endothelin-receptor antagonists or prostacyclin analogues for refractory digital ulcers","Treat pulmonary arterial hypertension with targeted vasodilator therapy under specialist guidance","Control gastro-oesophageal reflux and dysmotility with proton-pump inhibitors and prokinetics and address malabsorption","Treat scleroderma renal crisis as a hypertensive emergency with ACE inhibitors","Undertake active cardiopulmonary surveillance with pulmonary function tests, high-resolution CT and echocardiography","Coordinate multidisciplinary paediatric rheumatology care with physiotherapy, skin protection and specialist referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology","PRINTO/PReS/ACR-EULAR criteria for juvenile systemic sclerosis","Consensus guidance on paediatric scleroderma management"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_xanthogranuloma":{"name":"Juvenile xanthogranuloma","system":"Skin / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JXG","naevoxanthoendothelioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest non-Langerhans cell histiocytosis of childhood, typically presenting in infancy as one or a few firm yellow-orange to reddish-brown papules or nodules on the head, neck or upper trunk.","Solitary lesions predominate; most appear within the first year of life and a proportion are congenital.","The majority are cutaneous and self-limiting, involuting spontaneously over months to a few years, often leaving atrophy or hyperpigmentation.","Ocular involvement, most often unilateral iris disease, is the commonest extracutaneous site and may cause spontaneous hyphaema, glaucoma or visual loss; young children with skin lesions warrant ophthalmological vigilance.","An association with neurofibromatosis type 1 and, in a subset, with juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia (the classic triple association) should prompt review of café-au-lait macules and full blood count.","Histology shows a dermal infiltrate of foamy histiocytes with characteristic Touton giant cells; lesions are CD68 and factor XIIIa positive but CD1a and langerin (CD207) negative, distinguishing them from Langerhans cell histiocytosis.","Systemic or visceral disease (liver, lung, central nervous system) is rare and occurs mainly in infants with multiple cutaneous lesions.","Reassurance and observation suffice for uncomplicated skin disease; intervention is reserved for symptomatic ocular or systemic involvement."],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile xanthogranuloma is a benign proliferative disorder of the mononuclear phagocyte system, classified among the non-Langerhans cell (so-called C group / cutaneous) histiocytoses. The lesional cell is a dermal dendrocyte-derived histiocyte that accumulates lipid to produce the characteristic foamy, xanthomatous appearance despite normal serum lipids. The infiltrate is composed of histiocytes expressing macrophage markers such as CD68, CD163 and factor XIIIa but lacking the Langerhans cell markers CD1a and langerin, and Touton giant cells with a wreath of nuclei surrounding a central eosinophilic cytoplasm and a peripheral foamy rim are typical. The proliferation is thought to represent a reactive granulomatous response of dermal dendrocytes rather than a clonal neoplasm in most cases, which accords with its usual self-limiting behaviour and spontaneous involution as lipidised histiocytes are cleared.","additionalDifferentials":["Langerhans cell histiocytosis","Spitz naevus","Mastocytoma / solitary mastocytosis","Benign cephalic histiocytosis","Dermatofibroma","Molluscum contagiosum","Xanthoma associated with hyperlipidaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with histology and immunohistochemistry (CD68, factor XIIIa, CD1a, langerin)","Slit-lamp ophthalmological examination when ocular symptoms or multiple lesions in young children","Full blood count and blood film to screen for juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia in at-risk infants","Examination for café-au-lait macules and features of neurofibromatosis type 1","Serum lipid profile to exclude hyperlipidaemic xanthoma when clinically indicated","Imaging (ultrasound, MRI) if visceral or central nervous system involvement is suspected","Dermoscopy showing the characteristic setting-sun or clouds pattern as an adjunct"],"redFlags":["New ocular symptoms such as a red eye, spontaneous hyphaema or raised intraocular pressure suggesting iris involvement","Multiple cutaneous lesions in an infant, which increase the likelihood of ocular or systemic disease","Hepatosplenomegaly, cytopenias or a leukaemoid blood picture suggesting juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia","Neurological signs or a mass suggesting central nervous system or visceral xanthogranuloma","Café-au-lait macules and axillary freckling indicating coexistent neurofibromatosis type 1"],"prognosis":"The cutaneous prognosis is excellent, with most solitary lesions involuting spontaneously over months to a few years leaving little or no residual change. Ocular and systemic disease carries greater morbidity and requires specialist management, but life-threatening visceral involvement is uncommon.","pitfalls":["Mistaking juvenile xanthogranuloma for Langerhans cell histiocytosis; immunohistochemistry for CD1a and langerin resolves the distinction.","Overlooking asymptomatic ocular involvement in a young child with multiple skin lesions and thereby missing preventable visual loss.","Assuming all yellowish paediatric papules reflect hyperlipidaemia and ordering unnecessary lipid work-up for lesions that are histologically classic.","Failing to screen for neurofibromatosis type 1 and juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia in children with the recognised triple association.","Recommending disfiguring excision of a lesion destined to involute spontaneously."],"management":["Observe and reassure for solitary cutaneous lesions, as most involute spontaneously over months to a few years","Reserve surgical excision for symptomatic, diagnostically uncertain or functionally troublesome lesions","Undertake slit-lamp ophthalmological surveillance for iris disease, treating ocular involvement with topical or intraocular corticosteroids and managing hyphaema or glaucoma","Screen at-risk infants for juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia with full blood count and film and examine for neurofibromatosis type 1 café-au-lait macules","Manage systemic or visceral disease with chemotherapy under paediatric haematology-oncology guidance","Coordinate multidisciplinary dermatology, ophthalmology and haematology input for extracutaneous disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology (current edition)","WHO Classification of Histiocytoses and revised histiocytosis classification (current)","Standard paediatric dermatology reference texts (current editions)"],"crossLinks":[]},"kabuki_syndrome":{"name":"Kabuki syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Kabuki make-up syndrome","Niikawa-Kuroki syndrome","KMT2D","MLL2","KDM6A"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The facial gestalt of long palpebral fissures with eversion of the lateral lower eyelid, arched and sparse eyebrows, short nasal septum and prominent ears evokes the make-up of Kabuki theatre actors","Persistent fetal fingertip pads are a helpful and relatively specific clinical clue","Most cases follow autosomal dominant inheritance from de novo KMT2D variants, with a minority due to X-linked KDM6A","Postnatal growth impairment, hypotonia and joint laxity with mild to moderate intellectual disability are typical","Recurrent otitis media with conductive hearing loss and cleft or high-arched palate are common and affect speech development","Immune dysfunction, including humoral deficiency and susceptibility to infection with autoimmune features, is well recognised","Congenital heart disease, particularly left-sided obstructive lesions such as coarctation, should be actively sought"],"pathophysiology":"Kabuki syndrome is a disorder of the epigenetic machinery caused by loss of function in histone-modifying enzymes: KMT2D, a histone methyltransferase that adds activating methyl marks, and KDM6A, a histone demethylase that removes repressive marks. These enzymes cooperate to open chromatin and permit transcription of genes governing development and cellular differentiation. Their deficiency perturbs the balance of histone modification, disturbing craniofacial, cardiac, skeletal, immune and neuronal development and yielding the recognisable multisystem phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["CHARGE syndrome","Hardikar syndrome","22q11.2 deletion syndrome","Branchio-oto-renal syndrome","Turner syndrome","IRF6-related orofacial clefting syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis of KMT2D and KDM6A, or gene-panel/exome testing","Echocardiography for congenital heart disease including coarctation","Immunoglobulin levels and vaccine-response assessment for humoral immunodeficiency","Audiology with monitoring for recurrent otitis media and conductive loss","Renal tract ultrasound for structural anomalies","Blood glucose monitoring given the risk of neonatal hypoglycaemia and later hyperinsulinism"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or severe infections suggesting significant immunodeficiency","Signs of critical left-heart obstruction such as coarctation in the neonate","Hypoglycaemia from hyperinsulinism requiring urgent correction","Progressive hearing loss threatening language development"],"prognosis":"Most individuals reach adulthood with mild to moderate intellectual disability and can achieve substantial independence; long-term outlook depends on cardiac disease, immune function and infection burden. Autoimmune complications may emerge over time.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating otitis media without recognising an underlying immunodeficiency","Missing left-sided cardiac obstruction that presents insidiously","Overlooking neonatal or later hypoglycaemia from hyperinsulinism","Failing to recognise the diagnosis when facial features are subtle in infancy"],"management":["Evaluate and repair congenital heart disease, including coarctation, after echocardiographic assessment","Provide immunoglobulin replacement and infection prophylaxis for humoral immunodeficiency, monitoring vaccine responses and treating infections promptly","Undertake audiology surveillance and manage recurrent otitis media with grommets and hearing support","Repair cleft or high-arched palate and provide feeding support and speech and language therapy","Support development with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and educational intervention for hypotonia and intellectual disability","Monitor blood glucose for neonatal hypoglycaemia and later hyperinsulinism and surveil for emerging autoimmune complications","Offer genetic counselling with KMT2D/KDM6A testing and coordinate multidisciplinary follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","GeneReviews: Kabuki syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"kagami_ogata_syndrome":{"name":"Kagami-Ogata syndrome (paternal UPD14)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["upd(14)pat syndrome","paternal uniparental disomy 14","KOS14","14q32.2 imprinting disorder (paternal)"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An imprinting disorder of the chromosome 14q32.2 DLK1/MEG3 region in which the maternal contribution is lost, so paternally expressed genes are over-represented","The pregnancy is often complicated by marked polyhydramnios and a large placenta, prompting antenatal referral","The chest radiograph is the diagnostic clue: a small bell-shaped thorax with ribs that flare into a characteristic coat-hanger configuration","Neonates typically present with respiratory distress from thoracic constriction, and many require prolonged ventilatory support","Abdominal wall problems (omphalocele or diastasis recti) and feeding difficulty are common in the newborn period","It is the clinical mirror image of Temple syndrome, which arises from loss of the paternal contribution at the same locus (maternal uniparental disomy 14)","Later childhood may bring developmental delay, laryngomalacia, and a tendency to become overweight","Confirmation rests on methylation analysis of the 14q32.2 imprinted region, supported by microarray and uniparental disomy studies"],"pathophysiology":"The condition results from a disturbance of genomic imprinting at the DLK1/MEG3 domain on chromosome 14q32.2, a region controlled by differentially methylated regions that are normally set differently on the maternal and paternal chromosomes. When the maternally derived contribution is absent or silenced, whether through paternal uniparental disomy, a maternal deletion, or an epimutation, the paternally expressed genes such as DLK1 and RTL1 are over-dosed while the maternally expressed transcripts including MEG3 are lost. This imbalance in imprinted gene expression disrupts skeletal, placental, and neurological development, producing the thoracic dysplasia, abdominal wall defects, and neurodevelopmental features that define the phenotype. Because the same locus underlies Temple syndrome when the opposite parental contribution is affected, the two disorders behave as reciprocal imprinting conditions.","additionalDifferentials":["Temple syndrome (maternal uniparental disomy 14), the reciprocal imprinting disorder","Prader-Willi syndrome and other neonatal hypotonia/imprinting disorders","Skeletal dysplasias with small thorax such as asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy","Beckwith-Wiedemann spectrum when overgrowth and abdominal wall defects predominate","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia as a cause of neonatal respiratory failure","Trisomy 18 and other aneuploidies presenting with polyhydramnios and dysmorphism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Methylation-specific analysis of the 14q32.2 imprinted region to confirm the imprinting defect","Chromosomal microarray to detect deletions or copy-number change at the locus","Uniparental disomy testing using informative microsatellite or SNP markers with parental samples","Chest radiography to demonstrate the bell-shaped thorax and coat-hanger rib pattern","Antenatal ultrasound assessment for polyhydramnios, thoracic size, and abdominal wall integrity","Neonatal respiratory and feeding assessment including consideration of laryngomalacia"],"redFlags":["Severe neonatal respiratory distress from a constricted thorax requiring urgent airway and ventilatory support","Marked antenatal polyhydramnios raising the risk of preterm labour","Large abdominal wall defect needing prompt surgical planning","Failure to establish feeding with resultant faltering growth"],"prognosis":"Outlook is dominated by the neonatal respiratory compromise, which can be life-threatening in the most severely affected infants; those who survive the newborn period often show gradual improvement in respiratory mechanics as the chest grows, though developmental delay, feeding problems, and a later tendency to excess weight gain frequently persist and warrant long-term multidisciplinary follow-up.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the coat-hanger rib sign on the neonatal chest film and attributing distress to other causes","Testing with karyotype or microarray alone, which can miss an imprinting defect that requires methylation analysis","Failing to counsel that recurrence risk differs markedly depending on the underlying molecular mechanism","Not recognising the reciprocal relationship with Temple syndrome when interpreting locus-specific results"],"management":["Provide neonatal respiratory support with mechanical ventilation for thoracic constriction, anticipating prolonged weaning","Surgically repair abdominal wall defects such as omphalocele","Support nutrition with nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding for feeding difficulty","Assess and manage upper-airway problems including laryngomalacia","Undertake developmental surveillance with early intervention for delay","Confirm the diagnosis by methylation-specific analysis of the 14q32.2 region and provide genetic counselling on recurrence risk","Coordinate multidisciplinary neonatology, respiratory, surgical and clinical genetics care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Kagami-Ogata syndrome / upd(14)pat and related imprinting disorders","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"kala_azar":{"name":"Kala-azar (visceral leishmaniasis)","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["visceral leishmaniasis","VL","black fever","Leishmania donovani infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A systemic protozoal infection caused by Leishmania donovani, transmitted by the bite of female phlebotomine sandflies","In India it is concentrated in Bihar and adjoining eastern states and is the target of a national elimination programme, with humans the main reservoir for the Indian form","Classic presentation is prolonged fever with progressive splenomegaly (often massive), hepatomegaly, weight loss, and darkening of the skin that gives the disease its name","Laboratory hallmarks are pancytopenia and a marked polyclonal hypergammaglobulinaemia","Untreated visceral leishmaniasis is almost uniformly fatal, and secondary bacterial infections and bleeding are common causes of death","Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis can appear after apparent cure and acts as a human reservoir, so its recognition is important for elimination efforts","Diagnosis combines serology (e.g. rK39 rapid test) with demonstration of amastigotes in splenic, bone marrow or other aspirates","Effective antileishmanial therapy exists, with liposomal amphotericin B favoured in India; HIV co-infection worsens response and relapse risk"],"pathophysiology":"Sandfly bites inoculate Leishmania promastigotes, which are taken up by macrophages and transform into amastigotes that multiply within the mononuclear phagocyte system. The parasite disseminates to the spleen, liver and bone marrow, driving reticuloendothelial hyperplasia that produces organomegaly and, through hypersplenism and marrow infiltration, the characteristic pancytopenia. A vigorous but non-protective humoral response causes hypergammaglobulinaemia, while impaired cell-mediated immunity permits unchecked parasite proliferation and progressive, potentially fatal systemic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Malaria","Enteric (typhoid) fever","Miliary or disseminated tuberculosis","Haematological malignancy (leukaemia, lymphoma)","Chronic myeloproliferative disorders causing massive splenomegaly","Infective endocarditis or other causes of prolonged fever with organomegaly"],"infectionMimics":["Lymphoma or leukaemia with organomegaly and cytopenias","Tropical splenomegaly syndrome","Disseminated histoplasmosis","Chronic malaria (hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["rK39 rapid serological test for L. donovani","Demonstration of amastigotes in bone marrow, splenic or lymph node aspirates","Full blood count showing pancytopenia and protein electrophoresis showing hypergammaglobulinaemia","HIV testing given the impact of co-infection on treatment and relapse","PCR-based parasite detection where available"],"redFlags":["Prolonged fever with massive splenomegaly and pancytopenia requiring urgent diagnosis and treatment","Severe anaemia, bleeding or intercurrent bacterial infection from marrow suppression, which can be fatal","Failure to respond to therapy or relapse, especially with HIV co-infection","Emergence of post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis, signalling a transmission reservoir"],"prognosis":"With prompt, effective antileishmanial therapy most patients recover fully, and liposomal amphotericin B has markedly improved outcomes in India. Untreated disease is nearly always fatal, and prognosis is poorer in the very young, malnourished, and those with HIV co-infection or advanced disease.","pitfalls":["Attributing prolonged fever with splenomegaly to malaria or typhoid and missing visceral leishmaniasis in endemic areas","Overlooking HIV co-infection, which alters treatment response and relapse risk","Failing to recognise post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis as a source of ongoing transmission","Delaying treatment while awaiting invasive confirmation when serology and clinical picture are diagnostic"],"management":["Treat with antileishmanial therapy, liposomal amphotericin B being first-line, with miltefosine or paromomycin as alternatives or in combination regimens","Correct anaemia, manage bleeding and provide blood-product support for the pancytopenia","Provide nutritional rehabilitation and treat intercurrent secondary bacterial infections","Test for HIV and, in co-infection, combine antiretroviral and antileishmanial therapy with relapse-directed maintenance","Monitor for treatment response and relapse and recognise and treat post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis, which acts as a human reservoir","Support public-health control through sandfly vector measures and case reporting within the elimination programme"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"kallmann_syndrome":{"name":"Kallmann syndrome (hypogonadotropic hypogonadism + anosmia)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Kallmann syndrome","congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with anosmia","ANOS1","KAL1","FGFR1-related Kallmann syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism combined with impaired or absent sense of smell (anosmia or hyposmia)","Results from defective embryonic migration of gonadotropin-releasing hormone neurons alongside the olfactory nerves","Genetically heterogeneous, with X-linked ANOS1 and autosomal genes such as FGFR1 among the recognised causes","Typically presents as absent or incomplete puberty with low sex steroids and inappropriately low or normal gonadotropins","Associated features vary by gene and can include midline defects such as cleft lip or palate, dental agenesis, and unilateral renal agenesis","Synkinesia (mirror movements) is a recognised feature of some X-linked cases","Fertility can often be achieved with gonadotropin therapy or pulsatile GnRH, distinguishing it from primary gonadal failure"],"pathophysiology":"During development, gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) neurons originate in the olfactory placode and migrate along the olfactory nerve fibres to reach the hypothalamus. In Kallmann syndrome, mutations affecting the genes that guide this migration disrupt both the olfactory pathway and the arrival of GnRH neurons in the hypothalamus. The resulting deficiency of hypothalamic GnRH leads to reduced pituitary secretion of luteinising and follicle-stimulating hormones and consequent hypogonadism, while the shared developmental defect explains the coexisting impairment of smell.","additionalDifferentials":["Normosmic congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Constitutional delay of growth and puberty","Functional hypogonadotropic hypogonadism from illness, stress or excessive exercise","Hyperprolactinaemia or other pituitary lesions suppressing gonadotropins","Primary (hypergonadotropic) gonadal failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Basal luteinising hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone and sex steroid measurement","Formal or structured assessment of the sense of smell","Pituitary and hypothalamic MRI, which may show olfactory bulb hypoplasia or aplasia","Molecular genetic testing of ANOS1, FGFR1 and other associated genes","Renal imaging and screening for associated midline or skeletal anomalies"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting an expanding pituitary or hypothalamic mass rather than a congenital defect","Associated anomalies such as renal agenesis or midline defects requiring dedicated evaluation","Absence of any pubertal progression by the expected age warranting prompt endocrine assessment","Signs of broader pituitary hormone deficiency indicating combined hypopituitarism"],"prognosis":"With appropriate hormone replacement, secondary sexual characteristics and bone health can be maintained, and fertility is frequently achievable with gonadotropin or pulsatile GnRH therapy; a minority experience reversal of the hypogonadism.","pitfalls":["Attributing delayed puberty to constitutional delay and missing a genuine congenital deficiency","Failing to test the sense of smell, which is a key discriminating clinical feature","Overlooking associated anomalies such as unilateral renal agenesis","Assuming infertility is irreversible rather than treatable with gonadotropin therapy"],"management":["Replace sex steroids, with testosterone in males and oestrogen-progestogen in females, to induce and maintain secondary sexual characteristics and bone health","Induce fertility when desired with gonadotropin therapy or pulsatile GnRH","Monitor and optimise bone mineral density given the hypogonadism","Screen for associated anomalies such as unilateral renal agenesis, cleft lip or palate and dental agenesis","Offer genetic counselling with testing of ANOS1, FGFR1 and other associated genes","Coordinate multidisciplinary endocrinology, fertility and clinical genetics care with psychological support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"kaposi_sarcoma":{"name":"Kaposi Sarcoma","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KS","Kaposi's sarcoma","HHV-8-associated sarcoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Kaposi sarcoma is a vascular tumour driven by human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8) infection, classified as a neoplasm although its cause is infective; it presents as violaceous to red-brown macules, plaques, or nodules","Four clinical-epidemiological forms are recognised: classic (older men of Mediterranean or Eastern European origin), endemic (African), iatrogenic (immunosuppressed transplant recipients), and epidemic (AIDS-associated)","AIDS-associated Kaposi sarcoma is an AIDS-defining illness, so a new diagnosis should prompt HIV testing and assessment of immune status","Lesions often follow skin lines, may be multifocal, and can involve the oral mucosa, gastrointestinal tract, and lungs, sometimes with few or no skin findings","Lymphoedema, particularly of the lower limbs and face, can be prominent and disproportionate to the visible cutaneous disease","Restoring immune function is central to management: antiretroviral therapy in HIV-associated disease and reduction of immunosuppression in transplant-related disease often lead to regression","Localised skin disease may be treated with local measures, while widespread, visceral, or aggressive disease requires systemic chemotherapy under specialist care","Diagnosis is confirmed on biopsy showing proliferating spindle cells, slit-like vascular channels, and extravasated red cells, with HHV-8 (LANA) staining"],"pathophysiology":"Kaposi sarcoma arises from HHV-8 (Kaposi sarcoma-associated herpesvirus) infection of endothelial and progenitor cells, which reprogrammes them towards a spindle-cell, angiogenic phenotype. Viral genes drive cell proliferation, inhibit apoptosis, and stimulate production of angiogenic and inflammatory cytokines, producing the characteristic proliferation of abnormal slit-like vascular channels, spindle cells, and extravasated erythrocytes with haemosiderin deposition. Immune dysfunction, whether from HIV infection or iatrogenic immunosuppression, permits viral reactivation and tumour development, which is why the disease is strongly linked to immunocompromise and often regresses when immunity is restored.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacillary angiomatosis (important infective mimic in immunocompromised patients)","Pyogenic granuloma and other vascular proliferations","Angiosarcoma","Haemangioma and vascular malformations","Melanoma and pigmented lesions (for dark violaceous nodules)","Dermatofibroma and stasis dermatitis changes on the lower legs"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin or mucosal biopsy with HHV-8 (LANA) immunostaining to confirm the diagnosis","HIV testing and assessment of CD4 count and viral load in newly diagnosed disease","Review of immunosuppressive medication in transplant recipients","Investigation for visceral involvement (for example endoscopy or imaging) when gastrointestinal or pulmonary disease is suspected","Staging assessment to guide whether local or systemic therapy is appropriate"],"redFlags":["New diagnosis prompting HIV testing, as epidemic Kaposi sarcoma is an AIDS-defining condition","Pulmonary involvement causing breathlessness, cough, or haemoptysis, which can be life-threatening","Gastrointestinal disease with bleeding or obstruction","Rapidly progressive or widely disseminated disease requiring urgent oncological input","Extensive lymphoedema causing functional impairment or skin breakdown with secondary infection"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the clinical form and immune status: classic Kaposi sarcoma is usually indolent, and iatrogenic and epidemic disease often regress when immune function is restored, whereas disseminated or visceral disease, particularly pulmonary involvement, carries significant morbidity and can be fatal without effective treatment.","pitfalls":["Failing to test for HIV when Kaposi sarcoma is diagnosed","Confusing lesions with bacillary angiomatosis in an immunocompromised patient and missing a treatable infection","Overlooking visceral (gastrointestinal or pulmonary) involvement in patients with limited skin disease","Not reviewing and adjusting immunosuppression in transplant-associated disease","Treating aggressively without first optimising immune reconstitution, which alone may induce regression"],"management":["Restore immune function as the cornerstone: antiretroviral therapy in HIV-associated disease and reduction of immunosuppression in transplant recipients, which often induces regression","Treat limited cutaneous disease with local measures such as intralesional chemotherapy, radiotherapy, cryotherapy or topical agents","Use systemic chemotherapy such as liposomal anthracyclines or taxanes for extensive, visceral or rapidly progressive disease","Test for HIV with CD4 count and viral load and stage for visceral involvement to guide therapy","Manage disproportionate lymphoedema and provide symptomatic supportive care","Coordinate multidisciplinary oncology, HIV and transplant medicine input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"kaposiform_haemangioendothelioma":{"name":"Kaposiform haemangioendothelioma","system":"Skin / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KHE","Kaposiform haemangioendothelioma of infancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies: Hemangiomas and Malformations","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Locally aggressive, borderline vascular tumour of infancy that infiltrates tissue planes but does not metastasise.","The principal tumour underlying the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon: severe thrombocytopenia with consumptive coagulopathy.","Presents as a firm, indurated, violaceous and warm plaque, commonly on the trunk, extremities or retroperitoneum.","GLUT1-negative with lymphatic endothelial (D2-40) positivity, sharing a spectrum with tufted angioma.","Somatic GNA14 mutations are reported in a subset of lesions.","Platelet transfusion should generally be avoided unless there is active bleeding, as it aggravates trapping and swelling.","Sirolimus (mTOR inhibitor), often combined with corticosteroids, is the mainstay of systemic therapy; vincristine is an alternative.","Retroperitoneal and intrathoracic lesions carry higher morbidity and mortality."],"pathophysiology":"Kaposiform haemangioendothelioma is an infiltrative vascular neoplasm composed of coalescing lobules and sheets of spindled endothelial cells forming slit-like, crescentic vessels reminiscent of Kaposi sarcoma, intermingled with abnormal lymphatic channels (D2-40 positive) and microthrombi. The disorganised vasculature and abnormal endothelium trap and activate platelets, triggering intralesional platelet aggregation, profound thrombocytopenia and secondary consumptive coagulopathy, together termed the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon. Activating GNA14 mutations and dysregulated angiogenic and lymphangiogenic signalling drive growth, and mTOR pathway activity provides the rationale for sirolimus.","additionalDifferentials":["Tufted angioma","Infantile haemangioma","Kaposi sarcoma","Infantile fibrosarcoma","Angiosarcoma","Venous or lymphatic malformation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy showing characteristic infiltrative spindled vascular lobules with D2-40 positivity","Full blood count demonstrating marked thrombocytopenia","Coagulation profile with low fibrinogen, raised D-dimer and prolonged clotting times","Contrast-enhanced MRI delineating an ill-defined infiltrative enhancing mass crossing tissue planes","Doppler ultrasound","Baseline and serial monitoring during sirolimus therapy"],"redFlags":["Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon with profound thrombocytopenia and bleeding","Rapidly expanding infiltrative mass","Retroperitoneal, mediastinal or airway involvement","Signs of disseminated intravascular coagulation","Haemodynamic compromise"],"prognosis":"Mortality can be significant when the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon complicates large or deep lesions, but sirolimus-based therapy has markedly improved outcomes. Residual fibrosis, chronic pain and lymphoedema may persist.","pitfalls":["Routine platelet transfusion, which aggravates platelet trapping and lesion swelling.","Mistaking the coagulopathy for primary immune thrombocytopenia or sepsis.","Underestimating deep or visceral extent by relying on the surface appearance.","Delaying systemic therapy while awaiting spontaneous resolution."],"management":["Give sirolimus as first-line systemic therapy, often with corticosteroids, especially when the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon is active","Support the consumptive coagulopathy with fibrinogen or cryoprecipitate and fresh frozen plasma","Avoid platelet transfusion unless there is active bleeding or planned surgery, as transfused platelets are trapped and may worsen swelling","Consider surgical resection for accessible, localised lesions","Undertake serial monitoring of platelet count, fibrinogen and D-dimer, with imaging and laboratory surveillance during sirolimus therapy","Manage residual pain, fibrosis and lymphoedema within a multidisciplinary vascular anomalies team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of paediatric dermatology","ISSVA classification of vascular anomalies","Consensus guidance on management of kaposiform haemangioendothelioma and the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon"],"crossLinks":[]},"kaposiform_hemangioendothelioma":{"name":"Kaposiform haemangioendothelioma","system":"Vascular anomalies / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KHE","kaposiform hemangioendothelioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"ISSVA classification / Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A locally aggressive vascular tumour of infancy and early childhood, not a malformation, that classically presents as a firm, indurated, purpuric plaque or mass","It is the principal tumour that triggers the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon, a profound consumptive coagulopathy with severe thrombocytopenia","Lesions are typically solitary, may be cutaneous, deep soft tissue, or retroperitoneal, and often feel woody and ill-defined","Overlying skin change with rapid enlargement, tension and bruising should raise concern for platelet trapping","Histology shows infiltrating lobules and sheets of spindled endothelial cells with slit-like vessels; it is GLUT1-negative, distinguishing it from infantile haemangioma","Tufted angioma is considered part of the same spectrum and can also cause the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon","First-line medical therapy is commonly sirolimus, often with corticosteroids, particularly when there is coagulopathy","Imaging (MRI) helps define the infiltrative extent and biopsy confirms the diagnosis when it is safe to perform"],"pathophysiology":"Kaposiform haemangioendothelioma is a rare, locally invasive vascular tumour composed of infiltrating nodules of spindled endothelial cells forming slit-like, capillary-like channels reminiscent of Kaposi sarcoma but without its viral association. Its disordered, tortuous vasculature traps and activates platelets, which can precipitate the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon: intralesional platelet consumption produces severe thrombocytopenia, secondary consumption of fibrinogen and clotting factors, and a life-threatening coagulopathy. The tumour is GLUT1-negative, does not spontaneously involute like infantile haemangioma, and although it does not typically metastasise, its infiltrative growth causes considerable local morbidity.","additionalDifferentials":["Tufted angioma (same biological spectrum)","Infantile haemangioma (GLUT1-positive, involuting)","Congenital haemangioma","Infantile fibrosarcoma or other soft-tissue sarcoma","Venous or lymphatic malformation with bleeding or infection","Kaposiform lymphangiomatosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with platelet count to detect severe thrombocytopenia","Coagulation studies including fibrinogen and D-dimer for consumptive coagulopathy","MRI to characterise the infiltrative extent and tissue planes involved","Image-guided or open biopsy for histological confirmation when bleeding risk allows","GLUT1 immunostaining to distinguish from infantile haemangioma","Baseline renal and metabolic panel before starting systemic therapy"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging, tense, bruised lesion with falling platelets indicating Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon","Bleeding, petechiae or haemorrhage from profound thrombocytopenia and coagulopathy","Retroperitoneal or intrathoracic disease causing mass effect","Haemodynamic instability or high-output physiology","Signs of visceral involvement or compromise of adjacent structures"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on tumour extent and whether the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon develops; the coagulopathy can be life-threatening acutely, but with sirolimus-based therapy many lesions stabilise and regress partially, though residual fibrotic tissue, pain and lymphoedema-like sequelae often persist.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for a bruise, infection or ordinary haemangioma and delaying treatment","Transfusing platelets routinely, which can be consumed within the tumour and worsen swelling","Assuming spontaneous involution as would occur with infantile haemangioma","Attempting biopsy or surgery without correcting or anticipating coagulopathy"],"management":["Treat with sirolimus as mainstay systemic therapy, adding corticosteroids during active consumptive coagulopathy","Replace fibrinogen and clotting factors with cryoprecipitate and fresh frozen plasma to support the coagulopathy","Withhold platelet transfusion except for active bleeding or surgery, since transfused platelets are consumed and can aggravate the lesion","Reserve image-guided or surgical intervention for accessible or refractory lesions where bleeding risk allows","Monitor platelet count, fibrinogen and D-dimer serially and follow lesion extent on MRI","Manage chronic pain and lymphoedema-like sequelae through a multidisciplinary vascular anomalies service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ISSVA Classification of Vascular Anomalies","Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies: Haemangiomas and Malformations"],"crossLinks":[]},"kaposiform_lymphangiomatosis":{"name":"Kaposiform lymphangiomatosis","system":"Vascular anomalies / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KLA","NRAS-associated kaposiform lymphangiomatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"ISSVA classification / Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An aggressive complex lymphatic anomaly distinguished from generalised lymphatic anomaly by clusters of spindled lymphatic endothelial cells and a marked bleeding tendency","It characteristically causes haemorrhagic pleural and pericardial effusions and a consumptive coagulopathy with thrombocytopenia and hypofibrinogenaemia","Thoracic and mediastinal involvement is common and drives respiratory morbidity and mortality","Somatic activating NRAS variants have been identified, placing it within the RAS-pathway lymphatic anomalies","Histology shows dilated lymphatic channels accompanied by characteristic foci of spindled lymphatic endothelial cells, unlike generalised lymphatic anomaly","The coagulopathy resembles but is mechanistically distinct from the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon of vascular tumours","Sirolimus, sometimes combined with MEK-pathway inhibition in refractory disease, is used for medical control alongside supportive management","It carries the worst prognosis among the complex lymphatic anomalies and needs specialist multidisciplinary care"],"pathophysiology":"Kaposiform lymphangiomatosis is an aggressive, multifocal lymphatic malformation in which abnormal, dilated lymphatic channels are interspersed with clusters and sheets of spindled lymphatic endothelial cells. Somatic activating variants in NRAS drive dysregulated RAS-MAPK signalling within the lymphatic endothelium. The disordered lymphatics and abnormal endothelium promote platelet activation and consumption of clotting factors, producing thrombocytopenia, hypofibrinogenaemia and a bleeding diathesis that manifests as haemorrhagic effusions. Extensive thoracic involvement leads to progressive respiratory compromise.","additionalDifferentials":["Generalised lymphatic anomaly (no spindle cells, milder coagulopathy)","Gorham-Stout disease","Central conducting lymphatic anomaly","Kaposiform haemangioendothelioma with Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon","Malignancy with pleural effusion","Diffuse pulmonary lymphangiectasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and coagulation profile including fibrinogen and D-dimer to document consumptive coagulopathy","MRI and CT of the chest to map mediastinal and pulmonary involvement","Analysis of pleural or pericardial fluid, often haemorrhagic","Biopsy showing spindled lymphatic endothelial cells with lymphatic markers","Molecular testing for NRAS and related RAS-pathway variants","Echocardiography to assess pericardial involvement"],"redFlags":["Haemorrhagic pleural or pericardial effusions with respiratory or cardiac compromise","Severe thrombocytopenia and hypofibrinogenaemia with bleeding","Rapidly progressive mediastinal disease","Haemodynamic instability","Refractory chylous or bloody effusions despite drainage"],"prognosis":"Kaposiform lymphangiomatosis has the most guarded prognosis of the complex lymphatic anomalies, with significant mortality from haemorrhage and respiratory failure; sirolimus and RAS/MEK-directed therapy have improved outcomes but the disease is chronic and often difficult to control.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling it as generalised lymphatic anomaly and underestimating the bleeding and effusion risk","Overlooking the consumptive coagulopathy before drainage or surgery","Assuming effusions are infective or malignant rather than haemorrhagic-lymphatic","Delaying systemic therapy in favour of repeated procedural drainage alone"],"management":["Use sirolimus as the mainstay systemic therapy, with RAS/MEK-directed agents such as trametinib given the activating NRAS pathway","Support the consumptive coagulopathy with fibrinogen and clotting-factor replacement and cautious blood-product use","Drain and manage haemorrhagic pleural and pericardial effusions with respiratory support","Monitor platelets, fibrinogen and D-dimer serially and map thoracic disease with cross-sectional imaging","Undertake molecular testing for NRAS and related RAS-pathway variants to guide targeted therapy","Manage within a multidisciplinary vascular anomalies team given the guarded, chronic course"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ISSVA Classification of Vascular Anomalies","Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies: Haemangiomas and Malformations"],"crossLinks":[]},"kartagener_syndrome":{"name":"Kartagener syndrome","system":"Respiratory / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Kartagener's syndrome","PCD with situs inversus","sinusitis-bronchiectasis-situs inversus triad","primary ciliary dyskinesia with situs inversus totalis"],"chapter":null,"source":"ERS/ATS primary ciliary dyskinesia guidance and respiratory genetics texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Recognised by the classic triad of chronic sinusitis, bronchiectasis, and situs inversus totalis","It is the subset of primary ciliary dyskinesia in which randomisation of embryonic laterality has produced complete mirror-image organ arrangement","Dextrocardia on examination or chest radiograph in a patient with chronic respiratory symptoms should prompt evaluation for the syndrome","Features overlap with primary ciliary dyskinesia generally, including lifelong wet cough, recurrent otitis media, and neonatal respiratory distress in a term infant","Nasal nitric oxide is characteristically low, and diagnosis is confirmed with ciliary video microscopy, electron microscopy, and genetic testing","Fertility is often impaired in both sexes because sperm flagella and fallopian tube cilia share the affected axonemal structures","Management mirrors that of primary ciliary dyskinesia, centring on airway clearance and prompt treatment of infective exacerbations"],"pathophysiology":"Kartagener syndrome is a form of primary ciliary dyskinesia, an autosomal recessive disorder in which mutations affecting motile ciliary components (such as the dynein arms) impair coordinated ciliary beating. Failure of mucociliary clearance leads to chronic retention of secretions with recurrent sinopulmonary infection and progressive bronchiectasis. Because embryonic nodal cilia also fail to establish normal left-right asymmetry, laterality is randomised and roughly half of affected individuals develop complete situs inversus, defining the Kartagener phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystic fibrosis","Primary ciliary dyskinesia without situs inversus","Primary immunodeficiency with recurrent sinopulmonary infection","Isolated situs inversus without ciliary disease","Heterotaxy syndromes with complex congenital heart disease","Chronic rhinosinusitis with secondary bronchiectasis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph and imaging demonstrating dextrocardia and situs inversus","Nasal nitric oxide measurement","High-speed video microscopy of ciliary beat","Transmission electron microscopy of ciliary ultrastructure","Genetic testing for primary ciliary dyskinesia genes","High-resolution chest imaging and sputum microbiology to assess bronchiectasis"],"redFlags":["Progressive bronchiectasis with declining lung function","Recurrent isolation of Pseudomonas or non-tuberculous mycobacteria","Associated complex congenital heart disease in heterotaxy","Haemoptysis from established bronchiectasis"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and consistent airway clearance many patients maintain reasonable lung function, but chronic infection can cause progressive bronchiectasis; the situs inversus itself is usually of little functional consequence unless accompanied by cardiac anomalies.","pitfalls":["Assuming isolated situs inversus is benign and missing the underlying ciliary disorder","Delaying diagnosis by attributing chronic wet cough to recurrent infection or asthma","Relying on ultrastructure alone, which can be normal in some genetic subtypes","Neglecting fertility counselling"],"management":["Perform daily airway clearance with chest physiotherapy and mucoactive measures to compensate for defective mucociliary clearance","Treat sinopulmonary exacerbations with prompt, often prolonged antibiotics guided by sputum microbiology","Manage chronic sinusitis and recurrent otitis media, with hearing support and ENT input","Surveil lung function and bronchiectasis with high-resolution imaging and immunise against respiratory pathogens","Provide fertility counselling and assisted-reproduction referral given impaired sperm and fallopian tube ciliary function","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive condition and coordinate multidisciplinary respiratory, ENT and genetics care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["European Respiratory Society statement on primary ciliary dyskinesia","Standard respiratory medicine texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"karyomegalic_interstitial_nephritis":{"name":"Karyomegalic interstitial nephritis","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KIN","FAN1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Renal pathology and nephrology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive chronic tubulointerstitial nephritis caused by biallelic FAN1 variants; FAN1 encodes a DNA interstrand-crosslink repair nuclease.","Defined histologically by karyomegaly, that is strikingly enlarged, hyperchromatic and bizarre nuclei, of renal tubular epithelial cells.","Presents insidiously with progressive chronic kidney disease, usually detected in early-to-mid adulthood.","Urinalysis typically shows a bland sediment with modest tubular proteinuria and little or no haematuria.","Recurrent respiratory tract infections are a frequently reported association.","Karyomegaly is a systemic finding, also affecting liver, lung and neurons, although the clinical picture is renal-predominant.","The underlying DNA-repair defect confers heightened cellular sensitivity to genotoxic agents and a possible predisposition to malignancy.","Diagnosis requires renal biopsy and is confirmed by FAN1 molecular genetic testing."],"pathophysiology":"FAN1 on chromosome 15q13.3 encodes the FANCD2/FANCI-associated nuclease 1, a structure-specific DNA nuclease that acts in the repair of DNA interstrand crosslinks and in the protection and restart of stalled replication forks, working in concert with the Fanconi anaemia pathway. Biallelic loss-of-function variants leave cells unable to repair crosslink damage, so they accumulate DNA lesions and undergo repeated rounds of DNA replication without completing mitosis (endoreduplication), generating the polyploid, grossly enlarged karyomegalic nuclei that are the histological signature of the disease. This damage is most conspicuous in renal tubular epithelium, where progressive tubular injury drives chronic interstitial inflammation, tubular atrophy and interstitial fibrosis, and hence slowly progressive chronic kidney disease; the same repair defect underlies the reported sensitivity to genotoxic agents and the concern about cancer risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease (UMOD, MUC1, REN, HNF1B)","Nephronophthisis and related ciliopathies","Analgesic nephropathy and aristolochic acid or Balkan endemic nephropathy","Chronic lithium and other drug-induced tubulointerstitial nephritis","Viral cytopathic change (cytomegalovirus, BK polyomavirus, adenovirus) mimicking karyomegaly","Reflux nephropathy and obstructive uropathy","Chronic hypokalaemic nephropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal biopsy with light microscopy for karyomegaly, interstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy","Immunohistochemistry or in-situ studies to exclude viral inclusions","Serum creatinine and estimated glomerular filtration rate","Urinalysis and quantification of proteinuria","Renal ultrasound","Careful drug and environmental or toxin exposure history","Molecular genetic testing of FAN1 with screening of relatives"],"redFlags":["Progressive decline in renal function towards end-stage","Features of advanced chronic kidney disease","Recurrent or severe infections","Signs suggesting malignancy, given the DNA-repair defect"],"prognosis":"The disease is progressive and most affected individuals reach end-stage renal disease requiring dialysis or transplantation by mid-adulthood; kidney transplantation is feasible as the defect does not recur in the graft.","pitfalls":["Frequently dismissed as chronic interstitial nephritis of unknown cause when no biopsy is performed.","Karyomegaly can be mistaken for a viral cytopathic effect or for malignancy.","Genotoxic chemotherapy and other DNA-damaging agents should be used with particular caution.","Affected relatives may be undiagnosed and warrant screening."],"management":["Slow chronic kidney disease progression with blood-pressure control and renin-angiotensin-system blockade, as no disease-specific therapy exists","Avoid nephrotoxic drugs and adjust medication doses to renal function","Manage complications of chronic kidney disease including anaemia, metabolic acidosis and mineral-bone disorder","Treat recurrent respiratory tract infections, a recognised association","Plan and initiate renal replacement therapy, with transplantation feasible as the defect does not recur in the graft","Provide genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive FAN1 disorder with ongoing nephrology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard renal pathology texts","Nephrology reference texts","Reviews of hereditary tubulointerstitial kidney disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"kasabach_merritt_phenomenon":{"name":"Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon","system":"Vascular anomalies / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Kasabach-Merritt syndrome","KMP","haemangioma-thrombocytopenia syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"ISSVA classification / Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A life-threatening consumptive coagulopathy characterised by profound thrombocytopenia occurring within a vascular tumour, not within an ordinary infantile haemangioma","It is caused almost exclusively by kaposiform haemangioendothelioma and tufted angioma, which trap and activate platelets","The associated tumour is typically tense, enlarging, purpuric and warm, with disproportionate swelling and bruising","Laboratory hallmarks are severe thrombocytopenia, low fibrinogen and a markedly raised D-dimer","It is distinct from the localised intravascular coagulopathy of pure venous malformations, which produces milder platelet changes with high D-dimer","Platelet transfusion is generally avoided unless there is active bleeding or surgery, because transfused platelets are consumed and may worsen tumour swelling","First-line treatment targets the underlying tumour, commonly with sirolimus and corticosteroids","Prompt haematology and vascular anomalies specialist involvement is essential given the mortality risk"],"pathophysiology":"Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon is the severe consumptive coagulopathy that complicates certain vascular tumours, principally kaposiform haemangioendothelioma and tufted angioma. The abnormal, tortuous intratumoral vasculature traps circulating platelets, which become activated and aggregate within the lesion; this intralesional platelet destruction produces profound thrombocytopenia and secondarily consumes fibrinogen and clotting factors, generating a picture that overlaps with disseminated intravascular coagulation. The resulting bleeding tendency, combined with tumour enlargement, can be rapidly life-threatening.","additionalDifferentials":["Localised intravascular coagulopathy of a venous malformation (milder, without severe thrombocytopenia)","Disseminated intravascular coagulation from sepsis or other cause","Immune thrombocytopenia","Neonatal alloimmune or infection-related thrombocytopenia","Bone marrow failure or infiltration","Hypersplenism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial full blood counts documenting profound thrombocytopenia","Fibrinogen, D-dimer and coagulation profile to confirm consumptive coagulopathy","MRI of the causative lesion to define extent and tissue involvement","Biopsy of the tumour, with GLUT1 staining, when safe to confirm kaposiform haemangioendothelioma or tufted angioma","Peripheral blood film to assess for microangiopathic changes","Baseline organ function tests before systemic therapy"],"redFlags":["Rapidly falling platelet count with an enlarging, tense vascular mass","Active bleeding, mucosal haemorrhage or intracranial bleeding","Hypofibrinogenaemia with markedly elevated D-dimer","Haemodynamic instability","Airway, visceral or retroperitoneal involvement causing mass effect"],"prognosis":"The phenomenon carries significant mortality if untreated, but with modern therapy directed at the underlying tumour (particularly sirolimus-based regimens) the coagulopathy usually resolves; residual tumour, chronic pain and fibrotic or lymphatic sequelae may remain after the acute phase.","pitfalls":["Assuming a common infantile haemangioma is the cause when the culprit is a kaposiform tumour","Routine prophylactic platelet transfusion, which feeds intralesional consumption and swelling","Confusing it with the milder localised intravascular coagulopathy of a venous malformation","Delaying tumour-directed therapy while treating the coagulopathy in isolation"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying vascular tumour (kaposiform haemangioendothelioma or tufted angioma), with sirolimus and corticosteroids as mainstays","Support the consumptive coagulopathy with fibrinogen or cryoprecipitate and fresh frozen plasma","Avoid platelet transfusion unless there is active bleeding or planned surgery, as transfused platelets are consumed and may worsen tumour swelling","Consider embolisation or surgical resection for accessible or refractory lesions","Monitor platelet count, fibrinogen and D-dimer serially to track response","Manage acutely in a high-dependency setting with multidisciplinary vascular anomalies and haematology input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ISSVA Classification of Vascular Anomalies","Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies: Haemangiomas and Malformations"],"crossLinks":[]},"kawasaki_disease_incomplete":{"name":"kawasaki disease incomplete","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Incomplete Kawasaki disease","Atypical Kawasaki disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"American Heart Association Scientific Statement on Kawasaki Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Incomplete Kawasaki disease should be considered in any child with prolonged unexplained fever and fewer than the required number of principal clinical criteria, particularly in infants.","Infants under about six months of age are at highest risk of both incomplete presentation and coronary artery aneurysms, so the threshold for evaluation and treatment should be low.","The principal features may appear sequentially rather than together: bilateral non-exudative conjunctival injection, oral mucosal changes with cracked red lips or strawberry tongue, polymorphous rash, extremity changes with erythema and oedema followed by periungual peeling, and cervical lymphadenopathy.","Supportive laboratory findings — markedly raised inflammatory markers, anaemia, a rising platelet count in the second week, sterile pyuria, transaminitis, hypoalbuminaemia and hyponatraemia — strengthen the diagnosis when clinical criteria are incomplete.","Echocardiography is central: coronary artery dilatation or aneurysm supports the diagnosis and mandates treatment even when full clinical criteria are absent.","Timely immunoglobulin therapy, ideally within the first ten days of illness, substantially reduces the risk of coronary artery aneurysms.","Perineal desquamation and erythema or reactivation at a previous BCG scar site are useful supportive clues.","Incomplete Kawasaki disease belongs in the differential of fever of unknown origin and of a prolonged, viral-seeming illness that fails to settle."],"pathophysiology":"Kawasaki disease is a self-limiting, medium-vessel systemic vasculitis of childhood with a striking predilection for the coronary arteries; the incomplete form shares identical pathology but presents with insufficient clinical criteria to meet the classic case definition. An infectious or environmental trigger is thought to provoke an aberrant, dysregulated immune response in a genetically susceptible child, driving neutrophilic and then mononuclear infiltration of the arterial wall. Inflammation destroys the media and internal elastic lamina, weakening the vessel and predisposing to dilatation and aneurysm formation, most importantly in the coronary tree, where subsequent thrombosis, stenosis or rupture accounts for the principal morbidity and mortality.","additionalDifferentials":["Viral exanthems such as measles, adenovirus, enterovirus and Epstein-Barr virus","Streptococcal and staphylococcal toxin-mediated disease, including scarlet fever, toxic shock and scalded skin syndrome","Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (paediatric inflammatory multisystem syndrome)","Stevens-Johnson syndrome and drug hypersensitivity reactions including DRESS","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Other systemic infections such as leptospirosis","Mercury exposure (acrodynia)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial echocardiography to assess coronary artery dimensions using body-surface-area-adjusted z-scores","Full blood count showing anaemia and later thrombocytosis, with ESR and CRP","Serum albumin, sodium and liver enzymes","Urinalysis for sterile pyuria","Electrocardiogram for arrhythmia, ischaemia and a baseline recording","Cultures, serology and viral studies to exclude infective mimics","N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide as an adjunctive supportive marker"],"redFlags":["Coronary artery dilatation or aneurysm on echocardiography","Myocarditis, heart failure or circulatory shock (Kawasaki disease shock syndrome)","Persistent or recrudescent fever despite immunoglobulin, indicating treatment resistance","Young infants with prolonged fever and marked irritability","Features suggesting macrophage activation syndrome"],"prognosis":"With prompt immunoglobulin therapy the great majority of children recover fully, whereas untreated or delayed disease carries a substantial risk of coronary artery aneurysms. Such aneurysms are the leading cause of long-term morbidity and may lead to thrombosis, myocardial ischaemia or sudden death.","pitfalls":["Waiting for all classic criteria before treating and thereby delaying immunoglobulin beyond the optimal window","Attributing prolonged fever to a self-limiting viral illness and missing incomplete disease, especially in infants","Being falsely reassured by a normal initial echocardiogram, as coronary changes may evolve later","Overlooking supportive laboratory criteria that would justify treatment","Failing to recognise immunoglobulin resistance requiring escalation of therapy"],"management":["Give intravenous immunoglobulin as early as possible in the acute illness to reduce the risk of coronary artery aneurysms","Add aspirin as adjunctive anti-inflammatory and subsequently antiplatelet therapy","For high-risk or immunoglobulin-resistant disease, add corticosteroids, a further dose of immunoglobulin, or a TNF inhibitor such as infliximab","Perform serial echocardiography using body-surface-area-adjusted z-scores to detect and monitor coronary artery aneurysms","Tailor antithrombotic therapy to coronary findings, adding anticoagulation for large aneurysms","Maintain long-term cardiology follow-up with multidisciplinary paediatric rheumatology and cardiology care","Keep a low threshold to treat infants with prolonged fever and incomplete criteria given their high aneurysm risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["American Heart Association Scientific Statement on Diagnosis, Treatment and Long-term Management of Kawasaki Disease","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Cassidy's Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"kawasaki_shock":{"name":"Kawasaki Disease Shock Syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KDSS","Kawasaki shock syndrome","Kawasaki disease with shock"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe presentation of Kawasaki disease in which children develop haemodynamic instability and hypotension requiring fluid resuscitation or inotropic support.","It should be considered in a child with features of Kawasaki disease (prolonged fever, mucocutaneous changes, conjunctival injection, extremity changes, rash, cervical lymphadenopathy) who also appears shocked.","Compared with classic Kawasaki disease, shock syndrome is more often associated with incomplete presentations, greater systemic inflammation, and a higher risk of coronary artery involvement.","Laboratory markers tend to show more intense inflammation, lower platelet counts, and evidence of consumptive or capillary-leak processes.","These children are more likely to be initially resistant to intravenous immunoglobulin and may need additional immunomodulatory therapy.","The overlap with toxic shock syndrome and septic shock frequently leads to diagnostic delay, so a high index of suspicion is essential.","Prompt echocardiography is important because of the elevated risk of coronary artery aneurysms and myocardial dysfunction.","Timely treatment of the underlying vasculitis, in addition to circulatory support, is key to reducing coronary complications."],"pathophysiology":"Kawasaki disease is a self-limited medium-vessel vasculitis of childhood driven by intense systemic immune activation. In the shock variant, an exaggerated inflammatory response with high circulating cytokine levels produces vasodilation, myocardial dysfunction, and increased vascular permeability with capillary leak. The combination of impaired cardiac contractility and fluid shifts out of the intravascular space results in hypotension and organ hypoperfusion, superimposed on the vasculitic damage that threatens the coronary arteries.","additionalDifferentials":["Toxic shock syndrome (staphylococcal or streptococcal)","Septic shock","Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C)","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis with macrophage activation","Severe adenoviral or other viral infection","Drug reaction with systemic involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to assess coronary arteries and ventricular function","Inflammatory markers (CRP, ESR)","Full blood count with attention to platelets and anaemia","Serum albumin, sodium, and liver function tests","Cardiac biomarkers (troponin, natriuretic peptides)","Blood cultures and infection screen to exclude sepsis and toxic shock","Coagulation studies"],"redFlags":["Hypotension or shock requiring fluids or inotropes (EMERGENCY)","Signs of myocardial dysfunction or heart failure","Evidence of coronary artery aneurysm on echocardiography","Resistance to first-line immunoglobulin therapy","Rapidly rising inflammatory markers with falling platelets","Multi-organ hypoperfusion or deteriorating conscious level"],"prognosis":"With early recognition and treatment most children recover, but the shock variant carries a higher rate of coronary artery abnormalities, myocardial dysfunction, and immunoglobulin resistance than classic disease, so outcomes depend on prompt immunomodulation and cardiac follow-up.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the presentation as septic or toxic shock and delaying Kawasaki-directed treatment","Missing incomplete or atypical Kawasaki features in a shocked child","Aggressive fluid loading without recognising myocardial dysfunction","Assuming a single dose of immunoglobulin has controlled the disease without monitoring for resistance","Neglecting early and repeat echocardiography for coronary surveillance"],"management":["Prompt intravenous immunoglobulin as the mainstay immunomodulatory therapy to quell inflammation and protect the coronary arteries","Adjunctive corticosteroids given the high rate of immunoglobulin resistance in the shock variant, escalating to infliximab, ciclosporin or anakinra for refractory inflammation","Anti-inflammatory aspirin during the acute febrile phase transitioning to antiplatelet aspirin once fever settles","Fluid resuscitation with vasoactive and inotropic support and intensive care admission for the haemodynamic instability and capillary leak","Empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics until septic shock and toxic shock syndrome are excluded, given the diagnostic overlap","Serial echocardiography to detect and monitor coronary artery aneurysms and myocardial dysfunction","Thromboprophylaxis with antiplatelet therapy and anticoagulation if giant coronary aneurysms develop","Paediatric cardiology and intensive care multidisciplinary follow-up with long-term coronary surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"kbg_syndrome":{"name":"KBG syndrome (ANKRD11)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ANKRD11","KBG","16q24.3 microdeletion (ANKRD11)"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A recognisable syndrome combining macrodontia of the upper central incisors with a characteristic face and developmental delay","Large upper central incisors (macrodontia) are a near-specific clue and give the syndrome its dental signature","Facial features include a triangular face, a broad eyebrow with a tendency to meet in the midline (synophrys), a prominent nasal bridge and a long philtrum","Short stature, delayed bone age, skeletal and hand anomalies (such as brachydactyly and clinodactyly) are common","Developmental delay is usually mild to moderate, with behavioural difficulties, autistic features, hearing loss and epilepsy in some","Caused by loss-of-function variants in ANKRD11 (or 16q24.3 deletions including it), usually autosomal dominant, encoding a chromatin regulator that interacts with histone deacetylase complexes","The name derives from the initials of the surnames in the original family descriptions"],"pathophysiology":"ANKRD11 encodes ankyrin repeat domain 11, a chromatin regulator that interacts with histone deacetylase complexes to modulate histone acetylation and thereby the transcription of genes controlling neural development and bone formation. Loss-of-function variants or deletions cause haploinsufficiency, dysregulating chromatin-dependent gene expression during development of the brain, skeleton and teeth. This produces the intellectual disability, characteristic facies, macrodontia and skeletal features. As a chromatin-modifying regulator, it belongs to the broad group of disorders in which altered histone regulation disturbs neurodevelopment.","additionalDifferentials":["Cornelia de Lange syndrome","Coffin-Siris syndrome","Silver-Russell syndrome","22q11.2 deletion syndrome","Other chromatin-related intellectual disability syndromes","Isolated short stature with delayed bone age"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dental assessment noting macrodontia of the upper central incisors","Bone-age radiograph, skeletal survey and growth assessment","Audiological evaluation for hearing loss","EEG and brain MRI where seizures or other neurological features occur","Chromosomal microarray for 16q24.3 deletions and gene panel or exome sequencing including ANKRD11"],"redFlags":["Significant hearing loss impairing communication","Seizures where present","Feeding difficulty or faltering growth in infancy","Cardiac or other structural anomalies needing evaluation"],"prognosis":"KBG syndrome generally has a favourable outlook, with mild to moderate intellectual disability and manageable skeletal, dental and sensory features; most individuals achieve meaningful independence with support. Care is supportive with dental, audiological and developmental surveillance.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis when macrodontia is subtle or the child is pre-dentate","Confusing it with Cornelia de Lange syndrome based on synophrys alone","Overlooking hearing loss that affects speech and learning","Relying only on sequencing and missing 16q24.3 deletions detectable by microarray"],"management":["Early developmental intervention with speech, occupational and physiotherapy for the developmental delay","Dental review and orthodontic management of macrodontia and malocclusion of the upper central incisors","Audiological surveillance with hearing aids for conductive or sensorineural hearing loss","Growth and skeletal monitoring with bone-age assessment, and endocrine referral to consider growth hormone evaluation where short stature warrants","Antiseizure medication and neurology input for those who develop epilepsy","Behavioural and educational support addressing autistic features, attention difficulties and learning needs","Clinical genetics referral with counselling for this autosomal dominant ANKRD11 disorder and testing of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: KBG Syndrome","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"kcnb1_encephalopathy":{"name":"KCNB1 encephalopathy","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KCNB1","KCNB1-related epilepsy","Kv2.1 encephalopathy","EIEE26"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / ILAE","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by usually de novo variants in KCNB1, which encodes the delayed-rectifier voltage-gated potassium channel Kv2.1 that shapes repolarisation and regulates repetitive neuronal firing","Presents as a developmental and epileptic encephalopathy, though some individuals have neurodevelopmental impairment with few or no seizures","Seizure onset is usually in infancy or early childhood, with multiple types including focal, tonic, absence, myoclonic, and epileptic spasms","The electroencephalogram often shows multifocal or generalised epileptiform discharges and may include hypsarrhythmia","Intellectual disability, autism spectrum features, behavioural problems, and attention difficulties are prominent parts of the phenotype","Many pathogenic variants impair the potassium current or confer altered channel properties, disturbing the balance of excitability rather than acting like classic gain-of-function sodium channelopathies","Broad-spectrum antiseizure agents are generally used, and no single medication is uniformly effective"],"pathophysiology":"KCNB1 encodes Kv2.1, a delayed-rectifier voltage-gated potassium channel that carries a major outward potassium current controlling membrane repolarisation, spike-frequency adaptation, and the ability of neurons to fire repetitively. Kv2.1 also has a structural role in organising membrane microdomains. Pathogenic variants disrupt channel gating, selectivity, or expression, and can additionally alter non-conducting functions; the net effect is impaired regulation of neuronal excitability. This dysfunction lowers the seizure threshold and disturbs developmental network activity, producing the combined epileptic and neurodevelopmental phenotype seen clinically.","additionalDifferentials":["KCNQ2 and KCNA2 potassium channel encephalopathies","SCN2A and SCN8A developmental and epileptic encephalopathies","STXBP1 encephalopathy","SYNGAP1 encephalopathy","CDKL5 deficiency disorder","Other genetic causes of infantile-onset developmental and epileptic encephalopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Epilepsy gene panel or whole-exome sequencing to identify the KCNB1 variant","Electroencephalography to characterise multifocal or generalised discharges","Brain magnetic resonance imaging to exclude structural causes","Developmental, cognitive, and autism-spectrum assessment","Behavioural and attention evaluation","Where available, functional characterisation of the variant"],"redFlags":["Evolution to epileptic spasms with hypsarrhythmia","Drug-resistant mixed seizures with developmental regression","Status epilepticus","Severe behavioural deterioration affecting safety"],"prognosis":"Most affected individuals have intellectual disability with behavioural and autistic features; seizures range from readily controlled to drug-resistant, and cognitive outcome is generally guarded even when seizures improve.","pitfalls":["Assuming absent seizures excludes the diagnosis in a child with neurodevelopmental impairment","Applying sodium-channelopathy treatment logic to a potassium channel disorder","Underestimating the behavioural and attention comorbidity","Missing evolving epileptic spasms on the electroencephalogram"],"management":["Broad-spectrum antiseizure medications such as valproate, levetiracetam or benzodiazepines titrated to the seizure types, as no targeted therapy exists","Consideration of the ketogenic diet for drug-resistant seizures","Multidisciplinary developmental support with speech, occupational and physiotherapy for the intellectual disability and autistic features","Behavioural, psychological and educational interventions for attention difficulties and behavioural problems","EEG-guided reassessment of the antiseizure regimen as the multifocal or generalised discharges and seizure types evolve","Rescue benzodiazepine protocol and a status epilepticus plan for prolonged or clustering seizures","Clinical genetics counselling; most variants are de novo so recurrence risk is usually low, but parental testing refines advice"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews and OMIM resources on KCNB1-related disorders","ILAE resources on potassium channel developmental and epileptic encephalopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"kcnma1_channelopathy":{"name":"KCNMA1 channelopathy","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KCNMA1","BK channel channelopathy","KCNMA1-related disorder","PNKD3","Slo1 channelopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / ILAE","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in KCNMA1, which encodes the pore-forming alpha subunit of the large-conductance calcium- and voltage-activated potassium (BK, Slo1) channel that couples intracellular calcium to potassium efflux and shapes the afterhyperpolarisation","The phenotype combines epilepsy, a paroxysmal movement disorder, and variable developmental impairment, with considerable overlap between features","A characteristic presentation is paroxysmal non-kinesigenic dyskinesia, sometimes co-occurring with generalised epilepsy in the same individual or family","Gain-of-function variants tend to associate with paroxysmal dyskinesia and absence-type epilepsy, whereas loss-of-function variants more often cause developmental delay, ataxia, and structural or movement abnormalities","Seizure types include absence, generalised tonic-clonic, and other generalised or focal seizures depending on the variant","Distinguishing gain from loss of function is therapeutically relevant, as the two ends of the spectrum behave differently","Cerebellar signs, ataxia, and cognitive impairment can feature at the loss-of-function end"],"pathophysiology":"KCNMA1 encodes the alpha subunit of the BK channel (Slo1), a large-conductance potassium channel activated jointly by membrane depolarisation and rising intracellular calcium. By linking calcium entry to rapid potassium efflux, BK channels sharpen action potentials, drive the fast afterhyperpolarisation, and regulate neurotransmitter release and neuronal and smooth-muscle excitability. Gain-of-function variants increase potassium current and can produce paroxysmal dyskinesia and absence epilepsy through altered firing dynamics, whereas loss-of-function variants reduce the current and are associated with developmental delay, ataxia, and cerebellar involvement. The dual calcium and voltage sensitivity of the channel explains why disturbances manifest as paroxysmal movement and seizure phenomena.","additionalDifferentials":["PRRT2-related paroxysmal kinesigenic dyskinesia","PNKD (MR-1) paroxysmal non-kinesigenic dyskinesia","ADCY5-related dyskinesia","GLUT1 deficiency with paroxysmal movement and epilepsy","Genetic generalised epilepsies with absence seizures","Episodic ataxias and other channelopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gene panel or whole-exome sequencing to identify the KCNMA1 variant","Electroencephalography to characterise absence or other seizure types","Brain magnetic resonance imaging, particularly to assess for cerebellar or structural changes","Video documentation of paroxysmal dyskinetic episodes and their triggers","Functional characterisation to distinguish gain from loss of function where available","Developmental and cerebellar/ataxia assessment"],"redFlags":["Frequent or prolonged paroxysmal dyskinetic attacks impairing function","Status epilepticus or escalating seizure frequency","Progressive ataxia or developmental regression","Falls or injury from paroxysmal episodes"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the variant and phenotype: gain-of-function paroxysmal dyskinesia with absence epilepsy may have relatively preserved cognition, while loss-of-function disease with developmental delay and ataxia typically carries greater long-term impairment.","pitfalls":["Treating the paroxysmal dyskinesia as psychogenic rather than a channelopathy","Assuming a single functional mechanism when gain and loss of function diverge clinically","Overlooking the co-occurrence of movement disorder and epilepsy in the same patient","Missing cerebellar signs at the loss-of-function end"],"management":["Determine whether the variant is gain- or loss-of-function, as this steers therapy across the paroxysmal dyskinesia and epilepsy spectrum","Anti-absence antiseizure medication such as ethosuximide or valproate for absence and generalised seizures","Benzodiazepines such as clonazepam, together with avoidance of triggers like stress, fatigue, caffeine and alcohol, for paroxysmal non-kinesigenic dyskinesia","Developmental, physiotherapy and cerebellar rehabilitation, particularly for loss-of-function disease with developmental delay and ataxia","Video-EEG and clinical review to reassess treatment response and reclassify paroxysmal episodes","Clinical genetics referral with counselling and assessment of at-risk family members"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews and OMIM resources on KCNMA1-related disorders","ILAE and movement disorder resources on BK channelopathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"kcnq2_encephalopathy":{"name":"KCNQ2 developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KCNQ2","KCNQ2 encephalopathy","Kv7.2 encephalopathy","neonatal epileptic encephalopathy KCNQ2"],"chapter":null,"source":"ILAE classification / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in KCNQ2 encoding the Kv7.2 voltage-gated potassium channel subunit that carries the neuronal M-current","The encephalopathy form usually presents in the first days of life with frequent tonic seizures, often with focal or asymmetric features","The interictal EEG is typically markedly abnormal, and a burst-suppression or multifocal pattern may be seen","It sits at the severe end of a spectrum whose benign end is self-limited familial neonatal epilepsy, also caused by KCNQ2 variants","Sodium-channel-blocking antiseizure medications are often particularly effective in this potassium channelopathy, unlike in sodium-channel loss-of-function disorders","Brain MRI may show reversible signal changes in the basal ganglia and thalamus in the neonatal period","Development is impaired, though outcome varies with the specific variant and its functional effect"],"pathophysiology":"KCNQ2 encephalopathy is caused by pathogenic variants, frequently dominant-negative or gain- and loss-of-function missense changes, in KCNQ2 encoding the Kv7.2 potassium channel subunit. Kv7.2 combines with Kv7.3 to generate the M-current, a slow potassium current that stabilises the resting membrane potential and limits repetitive firing. Disruption of this current increases neuronal excitability in the neonatal brain, producing early tonic seizures and a severe epileptic encephalopathy, in contrast to the milder self-limited phenotype seen with less disruptive variants.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign familial neonatal epilepsy (KCNQ2/KCNQ3)","Ohtahara syndrome of other cause","SCN2A developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy with neonatal seizures","Inborn errors of metabolism presenting in the neonate","Structural neonatal seizures from malformation or stroke"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["KCNQ2 testing or a neonatal epilepsy gene panel or exome sequencing","EEG documenting the interictal pattern and seizure semiology","Brain MRI, which may show transient basal ganglia and thalamic signal change","Metabolic screening to exclude treatable causes of neonatal seizures","Developmental follow-up to track the encephalopathic course"],"redFlags":["Frequent neonatal tonic seizures with a severely abnormal interictal EEG","Refractoriness to standard first-line neonatal seizure treatment","Evidence of developmental arrest in early infancy","Diagnostic uncertainty between the benign and encephalopathic ends of the spectrum"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies with the underlying variant: many children with the encephalopathy form have intellectual disability and persistent epilepsy, though seizures may improve over the first years. Early recognition and use of sodium-channel-blocking therapy can improve seizure control in responsive cases.","pitfalls":["Assuming all KCNQ2 neonatal seizures are benign and self-limited","Avoiding sodium-channel blockers, which are often the most effective agents here","Misreading transient neonatal MRI signal change as an alternative diagnosis","Delaying genetic testing in an unexplained neonatal epileptic encephalopathy"],"management":["Sodium-channel-blocking antiseizure medications such as carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine or phenytoin, which are often particularly effective in this potassium channelopathy","Prompt initiation in the neonatal period once KCNQ2 is confirmed or strongly suspected, given the early tonic seizures","Exclusion and treatment of other reversible neonatal seizure causes while awaiting genetic confirmation","Multidisciplinary neurodevelopmental support with early intervention therapies for the encephalopathic course","EEG-guided medication adjustment as the burst-suppression or multifocal interictal pattern and seizures evolve","Neonatal intensive care support with a rescue and status epilepticus plan for seizure clusters","Clinical genetics counselling; distinguishing de novo encephalopathy variants from familial self-limited neonatal epilepsy informs recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ILAE classification of the epilepsies","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"kcnt1_epilepsy":{"name":"KCNT1-related epilepsy","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KCNT1","Slack channel epilepsy","KNa1.1 epilepsy","ADNFLE (KCNT1)","sleep-related hypermotor epilepsy KCNT1"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / ILAE","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in KCNT1, which encodes the sodium-activated potassium channel Slack (KNa1.1) that regulates neuronal firing and the slow afterhyperpolarisation","Almost all pathogenic variants are gain-of-function, increasing potassium current in a way that paradoxically enhances network excitability","The two most recognised phenotypes are epilepsy of infancy with migrating focal seizures and autosomal dominant sleep-related hypermotor (nocturnal frontal lobe) epilepsy","The migrating focal seizure phenotype presents in early infancy with near-continuous multifocal seizures that shift between hemispheres on electroencephalography and severe developmental impairment","The sleep-related hypermotor phenotype can be familial, with clusters of brief nocturnal motor seizures, and may be associated with psychiatric and cognitive comorbidity","Quinidine has been trialled as a targeted blocker of the gain-of-function channel, but clinical responses have been inconsistent and it requires cardiac monitoring","Seizures are frequently drug-resistant, and multidisciplinary supportive care is central to management"],"pathophysiology":"KCNT1 encodes Slack (KNa1.1), a sodium-activated potassium channel that contributes to the slow afterhyperpolarisation and shapes the timing and adaptation of neuronal firing. Pathogenic variants are predominantly gain-of-function, producing constitutively increased potassium current. Although greater potassium conductance might be expected to dampen activity, in affected networks it disrupts normal firing patterns and inhibitory balance, promoting the intense multifocal or frontal-predominant seizures seen clinically. The gain-of-function mechanism underlies the rationale for quinidine, which partially blocks the channel, though its clinical utility has proven variable.","additionalDifferentials":["SCN1A-related Dravet syndrome","SCN2A and SCN8A developmental and epileptic encephalopathies","CHRNA4/CHRNB2 autosomal dominant sleep-related hypermotor epilepsy","DEPDC5 and other GATOR1 familial focal epilepsies","Structural focal epilepsy from focal cortical dysplasia","Other genetic causes of epilepsy of infancy with migrating focal seizures"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Epilepsy gene panel or whole-exome sequencing to confirm the KCNT1 variant","Prolonged video electroencephalography to demonstrate migrating multifocal ictal patterns or nocturnal frontal seizures","Brain magnetic resonance imaging to exclude a structural lesion","Electrocardiography and cardiac assessment before and during any quinidine trial","Developmental and cognitive evaluation","Family history and segregation analysis in dominant sleep-related hypermotor cases"],"redFlags":["Continuous or near-continuous migrating seizures with developmental arrest in infancy","Cardiac conduction abnormalities, especially with quinidine exposure","Sudden clustering of nocturnal seizures with injury risk","Progressive cognitive or behavioural deterioration"],"prognosis":"The migrating focal seizure phenotype generally has a poor outcome with refractory seizures and severe developmental impairment, whereas the sleep-related hypermotor phenotype varies from relatively mild to disabling, sometimes with significant psychiatric and cognitive comorbidity.","pitfalls":["Expecting a uniform quinidine response, when benefit is inconsistent and requires careful cardiac monitoring","Assuming a potassium-channel gain-of-function must reduce excitability rather than disrupt network firing","Missing the familial nature of the sleep-related hypermotor phenotype and failing to counsel relatives","Mislabelling nocturnal hypermotor seizures as parasomnias"],"management":["Trial of quinidine as a targeted blocker of the gain-of-function channel in selected cases, recognising inconsistent responses and the need for cardiac monitoring","Broad-spectrum antiseizure medications for the migrating focal seizures, which are typically drug-resistant","Adjunctive options such as cannabidiol and consideration of the ketogenic diet for refractory seizures","Electrocardiography and cardiac surveillance for QT prolongation before and during any quinidine trial","Seizure control for the sleep-related hypermotor phenotype, with attention to associated psychiatric and cognitive comorbidity","Multidisciplinary developmental support and intensive care with rescue planning for the severe migrating focal seizure phenotype","Clinical genetics counselling, including segregation analysis in autosomal dominant sleep-related hypermotor families"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: KCNT1-Related Epilepsy","ILAE resources on epilepsy of infancy with migrating focal seizures and sleep-related hypermotor epilepsy"],"crossLinks":["malignant_migrating_partial_seizures_infancy"]},"kearns_sayre_syndrome":{"name":"Kearns-Sayre syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KSS","mitochondrial DNA deletion syndrome","oculocraniosomatic disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews (NCBI) and standard mitochondrial disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Mitochondrial disorder usually caused by a single large-scale deletion of mitochondrial DNA, typically sporadic","Defined by a classic triad: onset before the age of twenty, progressive external ophthalmoplegia and pigmentary retinopathy","At least one of cardiac conduction block, cerebrospinal fluid protein above one gram per litre, or cerebellar ataxia is required to complete the diagnosis","The pigmentary retinopathy has a salt-and-pepper appearance and may cause relatively mild visual symptoms","Progressive cardiac conduction disease can culminate in complete heart block and sudden death, warranting close cardiac monitoring and timely pacing","Additional features include proximal myopathy, sensorineural deafness, short stature and endocrinopathies such as diabetes and hypoparathyroidism","Muscle biopsy classically shows ragged-red fibres and cytochrome c oxidase-deficient fibres","Elevated blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate and molecular analysis of mitochondrial DNA support the diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Kearns-Sayre syndrome usually results from a single large-scale deletion of mitochondrial DNA arising during oogenesis or early embryogenesis, so it is generally sporadic. The deletion removes genes encoding subunits of the oxidative phosphorylation chain and mitochondrial transfer RNAs, impairing energy production. Heteroplasmic distribution of deleted genomes across tissues, with a bias toward post-mitotic high-energy-demand organs such as extraocular muscle, retina, cardiac conduction tissue, brain and endocrine glands, explains the characteristic multisystem phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia without the full syndrome","Myasthenia gravis","Oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy","Other mitochondrial encephalomyopathies such as MELAS and MERRF","Pearson marrow-pancreas syndrome (which can evolve into Kearns-Sayre syndrome)","Congenital or acquired causes of pigmentary retinopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["ECG and Holter monitoring for conduction disease, with cardiology surveillance","Molecular analysis for a single large-scale mitochondrial DNA deletion","Blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate and cerebrospinal fluid protein","Muscle biopsy for ragged-red and cytochrome c oxidase-deficient fibres","Ophthalmological assessment including retinal examination","Endocrine screen and audiometry for associated organ involvement"],"redFlags":["New or progressive cardiac conduction block, syncope or bradyarrhythmia","Symptomatic hypoparathyroidism or diabetic decompensation","Rapid neurological or bulbar deterioration","Progressive respiratory muscle weakness"],"prognosis":"The condition is progressive and multisystem; prognosis is largely determined by cardiac conduction disease, which can cause sudden death and is mitigated by surveillance and pacing. Life expectancy is reduced, with care focused on supportive and organ-specific management.","pitfalls":["Failing to institute cardiac surveillance and offer timely pacing for conduction block","Mistaking the ophthalmoplegia for myasthenia gravis","Overlooking treatable associated endocrinopathies","Not recognising that Pearson syndrome in a survivor may evolve into Kearns-Sayre syndrome"],"management":["Cardiac conduction surveillance with regular ECG and Holter monitoring and timely pacemaker insertion for progressive block, since conduction disease drives mortality","Consideration of a defibrillator-capable device given the risk of sudden death from complete heart block","Supportive mitochondrial cofactor supplementation such as coenzyme Q10, with avoidance of mitochondrial-toxic drugs including sodium valproate and aminoglycosides","Ophthalmological management of ptosis and ophthalmoplegia with ptosis props or surgery, and low-vision support for the pigmentary retinopathy","Endocrine screening and replacement for associated diabetes, hypoparathyroidism and other endocrinopathies","Audiological rehabilitation with hearing aids or cochlear implantation for sensorineural deafness, plus physiotherapy and swallowing support for proximal myopathy","Clinical genetics and metabolic specialist input with counselling; recurrence risk is usually low as single large-scale deletions are typically sporadic"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (NCBI): Mitochondrial DNA deletion syndromes","OMIM: Kearns-Sayre syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"keloid":{"name":"Keloid","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["keloid scar","keloidal scar"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A keloid is firm, rubbery, and shiny, and crucially extends beyond the boundaries of the original wound, distinguishing it from a hypertrophic scar that stays within the wound margins","Lesions often itch or feel tender and may continue to enlarge over months to years rather than regressing","Predilection sites include the earlobes, presternal chest, shoulders, upper back, and jawline","Risk is higher in people with darker skin phototypes, in younger patients, and where there is a family history","Even minor triggers such as ear piercing, acne, vaccination, or surgery can precipitate lesions in predisposed individuals","Prevention matters: minimise tension on wounds, avoid unnecessary elective procedures in prone sites, and counsel high-risk patients before piercings or cosmetic surgery","Treatment is often multimodal and includes intralesional anti-inflammatory injection, pressure or silicone therapy, and carefully planned surgery combined with adjuvant measures to reduce recurrence","Surgical excision alone carries a high recurrence rate and can produce a larger keloid, so it should be paired with adjuvant therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Keloids arise from dysregulated wound healing in which the normal resolution of the proliferative phase fails and fibroblasts remain persistently activated. Overexpression of profibrotic mediators, notably transforming growth factor-beta, drives excessive and disorganised deposition of thick, hyalinised collagen bundles that extend into surrounding normal dermis. Reduced apoptosis of fibroblasts, altered mechanotransduction from wound tension, and a genetic predisposition all contribute. Unlike hypertrophic scars, keloids do not remodel and regress over time, reflecting a sustained imbalance between collagen synthesis and breakdown.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertrophic scar (raised but confined to the wound edges and tends to regress)","Dermatofibroma","Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans","Foreign-body granuloma","Cutaneous manifestation of sarcoidosis in a scar","Nodular presentation of acne keloidalis nuchae on the occipital scalp"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical based on history and morphology","Skin biopsy only when an alternative or malignant process is suspected, recognising that biopsy itself may stimulate keloid growth","Photographic documentation to monitor size and response to treatment"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, ulceration, bleeding, or fixation suggesting a fibrosarcomatous tumour rather than a keloid","Recurrent or aggressive lesions in a scar raising the possibility of dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans","Functional impairment or contracture over a joint or the ear requiring specialist input"],"prognosis":"Keloids are chronic and prone to recurrence; some stabilise with combined therapy, but complete and permanent resolution is difficult, and lesions frequently recur after excision unless adjuvant treatment is used. They do not spontaneously involute in the way hypertrophic scars often do.","pitfalls":["Excising a keloid without adjuvant therapy, which commonly produces a larger recurrence","Failing to distinguish a keloid from a self-limiting hypertrophic scar and over-treating","Performing elective cosmetic procedures or piercings in high-risk patients without counselling","Overlooking the symptom burden of itch and pain and treating only for appearance"],"management":["Intralesional corticosteroid injection as first-line therapy to flatten and soften lesions and relieve pruritus","Silicone gel sheeting and pressure therapy to reduce bulk and prevent recurrence","Intralesional 5-fluorouracil, alone or combined with corticosteroid, and cryotherapy for resistant lesions","Surgical excision reserved for refractory keloids and always combined with adjuvant corticosteroid, radiotherapy or pressure to limit the high recurrence rate","Pulsed-dye laser for associated erythema and to improve texture","Preventive measures such as minimising wound tension, avoiding elective procedures at prone sites, and counselling high-risk patients before piercings or cosmetic surgery","Symptomatic relief of itch and tenderness with photographic monitoring of size and treatment response"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"kennedy_disease":{"name":"Kennedy disease","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SBMA","spinal and bulbar muscular atrophy","X-linked bulbospinal neuronopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked recessive lower motor neuron disorder caused by an expanded CAG repeat in the androgen receptor gene, so it affects men while female carriers are usually spared or minimally affected","Presents in adult men with slowly progressive proximal limb weakness, muscle cramps, and prominent bulbar involvement causing dysarthria and dysphagia","Fasciculations, especially of the face, chin, and perioral muscles, and postural or action tremor are characteristic clues","Because the androgen receptor is affected, signs of partial androgen insensitivity such as gynaecomastia, testicular atrophy, and reduced fertility are common","There are no upper motor neuron signs, which helps distinguish it from amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Sensory involvement can be demonstrated electrophysiologically even when clinically subtle, reflecting an associated sensory neuronopathy","Serum creatine kinase is often elevated, and metabolic features such as impaired glucose tolerance may accompany the disorder","Diagnosis is confirmed by detecting the expanded CAG repeat in the androgen receptor gene"],"pathophysiology":"Kennedy disease is a polyglutamine disorder caused by an expanded CAG repeat in the androgen receptor gene on the X chromosome. The elongated polyglutamine tract renders the mutant, ligand-bound androgen receptor prone to misfolding and nuclear accumulation, which is toxic to lower motor neurons in the brainstem and spinal cord and to dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons. The androgen-dependent toxicity explains why the phenotype is expressed in males and why partial androgen insensitivity features accompany the neuronopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (particularly lower motor neuron predominant forms)","Spinal muscular atrophy of adult onset","Chronic inflammatory or immune-mediated motor neuropathies","Inclusion body myositis and other myopathies with proximal weakness and dysphagia","Myasthenic and other neuromuscular junction disorders with bulbar features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Androgen receptor CAG repeat analysis as the confirmatory test","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies showing chronic denervation and sensory involvement","Serum creatine kinase measurement","Endocrine and metabolic evaluation including glucose tolerance and androgen status","Swallowing assessment to guide aspiration risk management"],"redFlags":["Progressive dysphagia with aspiration and weight loss","Choking episodes or compromised airway protection","Rapid deterioration suggesting an alternative or superimposed diagnosis","Emergence of upper motor neuron signs prompting reconsideration of ALS"],"prognosis":"The disorder progresses slowly over many years, and life expectancy is often near normal, though bulbar weakness can lead to aspiration and disability; mobility and swallowing decline gradually and management is supportive.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and giving an unduly poor prognosis","Overlooking the endocrine and metabolic features that support the diagnosis","Underestimating aspiration risk from bulbar involvement","Not offering genetic counselling to at-risk male relatives and carrier daughters"],"management":["Supportive, symptomatic care as there is no disease-modifying therapy; androgen-lowering approaches have not shown clinical benefit","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain mobility, manage proximal weakness and prevent falls","Speech and language therapy with swallowing assessment to manage dysarthria and dysphagia and reduce aspiration risk","Aspiration precautions with dietary texture modification and enteral feeding for advanced bulbar involvement","Monitoring and management of associated metabolic and endocrine features such as glucose intolerance, dyslipidaemia and gynaecomastia","Symptomatic treatment of troublesome muscle cramps and postural tremor","Genetic counselling for this X-linked disorder, including carrier testing of female relatives and predictive testing where appropriate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"kenny_caffey_syndrome":{"name":"Kenny-Caffey syndrome","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KCS","Kenny-Caffey syndrome type 1 (TBCE)","Kenny-Caffey syndrome type 2 (FAM111A)","hypoparathyroidism with medullary stenosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard endocrinology and clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare inherited disorder combining hypoparathyroidism with a distinctive skeletal dysplasia and proportionate short stature","Skeletal features include thickened long-bone cortices with narrowing (stenosis) of the medullary cavity, delayed closure of the anterior fontanelle, and slender tubular bones","Two genetically distinct forms exist: an autosomal recessive type from TBCE variants (often with intellectual disability, seen in Middle Eastern populations) and an autosomal dominant type from FAM111A variants","Hypoparathyroidism presents as hypocalcaemia that may manifest with tetany, carpopedal spasm, or seizures, especially in infancy","Ophthalmic abnormalities such as small eyes and hyperopia and recurrent infections may accompany the phenotype","The TBCE-related form overlaps with hypoparathyroidism-retardation-dysmorphism (Sanjad-Sakati) syndrome, reflecting shared molecular pathology","Diagnosis rests on the combination of biochemical hypoparathyroidism, characteristic radiographic bone changes, and confirmatory genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Kenny-Caffey syndrome is a monogenic disorder of bone development and parathyroid function. The autosomal recessive form arises from loss-of-function variants in TBCE, which encodes a tubulin-folding cofactor essential for microtubule assembly, disturbing intracellular trafficking and organ development including the parathyroid glands. The autosomal dominant form results from variants in FAM111A, a protein implicated in genome maintenance and possibly parathyroid and skeletal morphogenesis. The net effect is defective or absent parathyroid tissue causing hypoparathyroidism with consequent hypocalcaemia and hyperphosphataemia, together with abnormal endochondral and cortical bone growth producing medullary stenosis, cortical thickening, and short stature.","additionalDifferentials":["Sanjad-Sakati (hypoparathyroidism-retardation-dysmorphism) syndrome","Autoimmune or surgical hypoparathyroidism with unrelated skeletal disease","DiGeorge (22q11.2 deletion) syndrome with hypoparathyroidism","Osteopetrosis and other sclerosing bone dysplasias","Pseudohypoparathyroidism / Albright hereditary osteodystrophy","Vitamin D-related rickets or osteomalacia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium, phosphate, magnesium and albumin with corrected calcium","Intact parathyroid hormone level (inappropriately low or undetectable relative to hypocalcaemia)","Skeletal survey to demonstrate cortical thickening, medullary stenosis and delayed fontanelle closure","25-hydroxyvitamin D and renal function to contextualise mineral metabolism","Molecular genetic testing of TBCE and FAM111A with pedigree-based family screening","Ophthalmological assessment for microphthalmia and refractive error"],"redFlags":["Acute symptomatic hypocalcaemia with tetany, laryngospasm or seizures requiring urgent correction","Neonatal or infantile presentation with failure to thrive and recurrent infections","Prolonged QT interval on the ECG from hypocalcaemia","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or CNS complications"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the severity of hypocalcaemia control and any associated intellectual impairment or infections, which are more prominent in the recessive TBCE-related form. With careful lifelong management of calcium and vitamin D metabolism, biochemical stability is achievable, though short stature and skeletal changes persist.","pitfalls":["Attributing infantile seizures to primary epilepsy rather than checking calcium and parathyroid status","Overlooking the diagnosis when skeletal radiographic clues are subtle or misread as osteopetrosis","Failing to distinguish the recessive and dominant forms, which affects inheritance counselling","Aggressive calcium and vitamin D therapy precipitating hypercalciuria and nephrocalcinosis if unmonitored"],"management":["Correction of hypocalcaemia with oral calcium and activated vitamin D analogues such as alfacalcidol or calcitriol as the mainstay of hypoparathyroidism management","Intravenous calcium for acute symptomatic hypocalcaemia presenting with tetany, carpopedal spasm or seizures","Careful monitoring of calcium, phosphate, magnesium and urinary calcium to avoid nephrocalcinosis from over-treatment","Consideration of recombinant parathyroid hormone replacement in difficult-to-control hypoparathyroidism","Management of recurrent infections with immune evaluation, particularly in the recessive TBCE-related form","Ophthalmological correction of hyperopia and monitoring for microphthalmia-related complications","Developmental and growth support with paediatric and endocrine multidisciplinary follow-up","Clinical genetics referral and counselling, distinguishing autosomal recessive TBCE from autosomal dominant FAM111A disease to guide family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","GeneReviews / OMIM entries on Kenny-Caffey syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"keratitis_ichthyosis_deafness_syndrome":{"name":"Keratitis-ichthyosis-deafness syndrome","system":"Hearing / Vision genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KID syndrome","Keratitis-ichthyosis-deafness"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This rare ectodermal disorder combines vascularising keratitis, an ichthyosiform erythrokeratoderma and congenital sensorineural deafness.","It is usually caused by dominant gain-of-function variants in GJB2, the gene for connexin 26, most classically a specific recurrent substitution affecting the channel.","Unlike common recessive GJB2 deafness, the KID mutations act on the gap-junction hemichannel to produce a toxic gain of function affecting skin, cochlea and cornea.","The skin shows well-demarcated erythematous hyperkeratotic plaques with a characteristic stippled or leathery grainy surface, plus thickened palms and soles.","The keratitis is progressive and vascularising, causing photophobia, corneal neovascularisation and potential blindness rather than a simple dry-eye complaint.","Deafness is congenital, bilateral and profound, and cochlear implantation may be considered.","Patients are prone to recurrent mucocutaneous candidal and bacterial infection and to cicatricial alopecia and nail dystrophy.","There is a lifelong increased risk of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, warranting long-term skin surveillance."],"pathophysiology":"Keratitis-ichthyosis-deafness syndrome is a connexin disorder caused predominantly by heterozygous missense variants in GJB2, which encodes connexin 26, a gap-junction protein that oligomerises into hexameric hemichannels (connexons) in the epidermis, cochlea and corneal epithelium. Whereas loss-of-function GJB2 variants cause common recessive non-syndromic deafness, the variants responsible for KID syndrome act through a dominant gain of function: they impair the normal gating of the hemichannel so that connexons remain aberrantly open at the plasma membrane, allowing pathological leak of ions and small molecules, cellular calcium dysregulation and disturbed intercellular communication. In the cochlea this disrupts potassium recycling through the supporting-cell gap-junction network needed to maintain endolymph and the endocochlear potential, producing profound congenital sensorineural deafness; in the epidermis it deranges keratinocyte differentiation and barrier formation, giving erythrokeratoderma, palmoplantar keratoderma and increased susceptibility to infection and malignant transformation; and in the avascular cornea it drives epithelial breakdown, neovascularisation and progressive keratitis. A minority of cases, particularly a more severe lethal neonatal form, arise from variants in GJB6 (connexin 30), reflecting the shared role of these connexins in the same tissues.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-syndromic recessive GJB2 deafness without skin or eye disease","Other congenital ichthyoses such as ichthyosiform erythroderma and Netherton syndrome","Erythrokeratodermia variabilis from connexin 31 variants","Vohwinkel syndrome with palmoplantar keratoderma and deafness","Clouston hidrotic ectodermal dysplasia (GJB6) with nail and hair changes","Hystrix-like ichthyosis with deafness, an allelic severe phenotype","Acquired vascularising keratitis and chronic ocular surface disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of GJB2 and, where indicated, GJB6","Comprehensive audiometry and brainstem-evoked response testing","Slit-lamp ophthalmological assessment for keratitis and corneal neovascularisation","Skin biopsy and histopathology of hyperkeratotic plaques","Microbiological sampling for recurrent candidal and bacterial infection","Regular dermatological surveillance for squamous cell carcinoma","Family and pedigree evaluation given usually de novo dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Progressive corneal neovascularisation and visual deterioration despite ocular lubrication","New, enlarging or ulcerated skin lesion suggesting squamous cell carcinoma","Severe or systemic mucocutaneous infection in the neonatal or lethal variant","Profound congenital deafness delaying language acquisition","Extensive erythroderma with impaired thermoregulation and fluid loss in infancy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies with severity: many patients survive into adulthood with chronic skin, hearing and corneal morbidity, but the neonatal severe form carries high mortality from infection, and the lifelong risk of squamous cell carcinoma and progressive keratitis threatens both survival and sight.","pitfalls":["Assuming any GJB2 variant is recessive and non-syndromic, thereby missing the dominant gain-of-function KID mutations","Managing the keratitis as ordinary dry eye and overlooking progressive neovascularisation","Failing to institute skin-cancer surveillance in a patient with chronic erythrokeratoderma","Attributing recurrent candidal infection to hygiene rather than the underlying barrier and immune defect","Overlooking hearing loss in a neonate whose skin disease dominates the presentation"],"management":["Skin-directed therapy with emollients and keratolytics, and systemic retinoids such as acitretin for extensive erythrokeratoderma with treatment monitoring","Prompt antifungal and antibacterial treatment of recurrent candidal and bacterial skin infections","Ophthalmological care of the vascularising keratitis with ocular surface lubrication, topical therapy and corneal surgery where needed, plus photophobia management","Audiological rehabilitation including cochlear implantation for the profound congenital sensorineural deafness","Lifelong dermatological surveillance with biopsy of suspicious or non-healing lesions given the squamous cell carcinoma risk","Intensive infection control in the neonatal severe form, which carries high mortality","Clinical genetics referral and counselling for this dominant GJB2 (connexin 26) disorder, most cases arising de novo"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical genetics reference texts","Dermatology reference texts","Audiology and ophthalmology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"keratoacanthoma":{"name":"keratoacanthoma","system":"Skin and integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KA","molluscum sebaceum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a rapidly growing, dome-shaped, flesh-coloured nodule with a central keratin-filled crater, typically on sun-exposed skin of older, fair-skinned patients.","Classically follows a triphasic natural history of rapid proliferation, a stationary phase and then spontaneous involution over months, often leaving a depressed scar.","Widely regarded as a well-differentiated variant of squamous cell carcinoma that cannot be reliably distinguished from invasive disease on clinical or histological grounds.","Complete excision with a deep margin is generally preferred, because superficial or partial biopsy may miss an invasive squamous cell carcinoma at the base.","Multiple lesions suggest a syndrome: Ferguson-Smith type (multiple self-healing), Grzybowski type (eruptive) or Muir-Torre syndrome with sebaceous neoplasms and visceral malignancy.","Associations include ultraviolet exposure, immunosuppression, trauma and BRAF inhibitor therapy.","Dermoscopy typically shows a central keratin mass with surrounding white structures and looped vessels.","Histology shows a central keratin plug with buttressing (lipping) epidermal edges and a glassy, well-differentiated keratinocyte proliferation."],"pathophysiology":"Keratoacanthoma arises from a neoplastic proliferation of keratinocytes, thought to originate from the follicular infundibulum, driven largely by ultraviolet-induced DNA damage and, in some cases, immunosuppression, trauma or drug exposure such as BRAF inhibitors. The proliferative phase produces the characteristic crateriform nodule, while a cell-mediated immune response is believed to underlie the spontaneous involution seen in many lesions. Because the proliferation shares molecular and morphological features with well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma, the two exist on a continuum.","additionalDifferentials":["Invasive squamous cell carcinoma","Nodular basal cell carcinoma","Hypertrophic actinic keratosis","Verruca vulgaris (viral wart)","Giant molluscum contagiosum","Amelanotic melanoma","Merkel cell carcinoma or deep fungal infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional biopsy that includes the deep base to allow assessment for invasion","Dermoscopy showing central keratin and peripheral white circles or looped vessels","Review of immunosuppression status and drug history including BRAF inhibitors","Assessment for multiplicity and family history to identify a syndromic cause","Mismatch repair immunohistochemistry and colonoscopy when Muir-Torre syndrome is suspected","Regional lymph node examination if invasive squamous cell carcinoma is confirmed"],"redFlags":["Ulceration, induration or continued rapid growth without the expected involution","Lesions on the lip, ear or in a periungual or mucosal site, where malignant behaviour is more likely","Occurrence in an immunosuppressed patient","Recurrence after treatment or perineural symptoms","Multiple or eruptive lesions suggesting an underlying syndrome or malignancy"],"prognosis":"Although many lesions involute spontaneously, they leave scars and cannot be reliably distinguished from invasive squamous cell carcinoma, so complete excision is recommended and generally curative, with an excellent outcome.","pitfalls":["Assuming a lesion is benign and observing it, thereby missing an invasive squamous cell carcinoma.","Relying on a superficial shave or partial biopsy that fails to sample the tumour base.","Overlooking Muir-Torre syndrome in patients with multiple or atypical lesions.","Failing to consider drug-induced lesions in patients on BRAF inhibitor therapy."],"management":["Complete surgical excision with a deep margin as preferred management, since the lesion cannot be reliably distinguished from invasive squamous cell carcinoma","Histopathological assessment of the excised deep base to detect underlying invasion","Intralesional methotrexate or 5-fluorouracil for large lesions or where surgery is difficult","Systemic retinoids such as acitretin for multiple or eruptive lesions, as in the Grzybowski or Ferguson-Smith types","Review and modification of contributory factors including immunosuppression and BRAF inhibitor therapy","Investigation for Muir-Torre syndrome with mismatch-repair immunohistochemistry and colonoscopy when sebaceous neoplasms or multiplicity are present","Sun protection and dermatological surveillance for further lesions and other keratinocyte skin cancers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine","British Association of Dermatologists guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"keratoconus":{"name":"Keratoconus","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["conical cornea","KC"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents in adolescence or early adulthood with progressive blurring and distortion of vision from irregular astigmatism","Frequent changes of spectacle prescription that fail to give satisfactory vision should raise suspicion of a developing ectasia","Often bilateral but asymmetric, and associated with eye rubbing, atopy, and connective tissue disorders such as Down syndrome","Clinical signs include a cone-shaped protrusion of the cornea, thinning at the apex, Vogt striae, Fleischer ring, and a scissoring reflex on retinoscopy","Munson sign is an angular bulging of the lower lid margin seen on downgaze in advanced disease","Corneal topography and tomography are key to early diagnosis and to monitoring progression","Management is staged: spectacles or soft lenses early, rigid gas-permeable or specialty contact lenses for irregular astigmatism, corneal collagen cross-linking to arrest progression, and keratoplasty for advanced scarring or intolerance to lenses","Acute corneal hydrops causes sudden pain, redness, and reduced vision from a break in Descemet membrane allowing aqueous into the stroma"],"pathophysiology":"Keratoconus is a progressive, non-inflammatory ectasia in which the cornea thins and steepens into a conical shape, producing irregular astigmatism and myopia. The stromal thinning reflects disorganisation of collagen lamellae and altered enzyme activity with increased degradation of the corneal matrix. Mechanical stress from chronic eye rubbing and an association with atopy and certain systemic syndromes are recognised contributors. As the cone advances, the apex scars and Descemet membrane may rupture, precipitating acute hydrops with corneal oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Pellucid marginal degeneration","Keratoglobus","Post-refractive surgery ectasia","Irregular astigmatism from corneal scarring","Contact lens-induced corneal warpage"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Corneal topography and tomography to map curvature and detect early ectasia","Pachymetry to quantify corneal thinning","Retinoscopy to detect the scissoring reflex","Serial imaging to assess progression and guide cross-linking decisions","Assessment for associated atopy or connective tissue disorders when indicated"],"redFlags":["Sudden pain, marked visual loss, and corneal oedema suggesting acute hydrops","Rapidly progressive steepening in young patients requiring urgent cross-linking assessment","Corneal scarring or lens intolerance indicating need for keratoplasty referral"],"prognosis":"Progression is most rapid in younger patients and often stabilises with age; timely cross-linking can halt deterioration, and most patients achieve good functional vision with lenses, though a minority need corneal transplantation.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly changing spectacle prescriptions instead of recognising an underlying ectasia","Overlooking eye rubbing as a modifiable driver of progression","Delaying cross-linking in demonstrable progression, allowing avoidable visual loss","Mistaking acute hydrops for infective keratitis"],"management":["Corneal collagen cross-linking to halt progression in documented or early progressive ectasia","Rigid gas-permeable or scleral contact lenses, with spectacles in mild cases, to correct irregular astigmatism and optimise vision","Counselling to stop eye rubbing and treatment of associated atopy and ocular allergy to slow progression","Intrastromal corneal ring segments to flatten the cone and improve lens tolerance in selected eyes","Management of acute hydrops with topical hypertonic agents and supportive measures while the corneal oedema resolves","Corneal transplantation by deep anterior lamellar or penetrating keratoplasty for advanced scarring or contact-lens intolerance","Serial corneal topography and tomography to monitor progression and guide the timing of cross-linking"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"keratolytic_winter_erythema":{"name":"Keratolytic winter erythema","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Oudtshoorn skin","Oudtshoorn disease","erythrokeratolysis hiemalis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant disorder of keratinisation first characterised in Afrikaner families around Oudtshoorn in South Africa","Characterised by cyclical palmoplantar erythema with centrifugal peeling of the stratum corneum, producing annular collarettes of desquamation","Symptoms classically worsen in cold winter weather and improve in summer, hence the name","It is associated with duplications in a regulatory region on chromosome 8 affecting expression of CTSB (cathepsin B)","Affected skin shows recurrent superficial peeling that can extend beyond the palms and soles, sometimes with hyperhidrosis","Diagnosis is clinical, based on the recurrent annular peeling and seasonal pattern with a supportive family history; biopsy shows a distinctive band of parakeratosis with underlying separation","Management is supportive with emollients and keratolytics; some patients benefit from oral retinoids in severe disease"],"pathophysiology":"Keratolytic winter erythema is linked to non-coding genomic duplications on chromosome 8 that upregulate cathepsin B, a lysosomal cysteine protease active in keratinocyte differentiation and cornified-envelope turnover. Increased proteolytic activity is thought to promote premature and disordered breakdown of the stratum corneum, so the cornified layer separates and peels in a centrifugal, annular fashion. Environmental cold appears to trigger or accentuate the cycles of erythema and desquamation, explaining the striking seasonal variation.","additionalDifferentials":["Peeling skin syndrome","Erythrokeratodermia variabilis","Palmoplantar exfoliative dermatitis of other cause","Dyshidrotic (pompholyx) eczema","Tinea manuum or pedis","Acral peeling from contact or irritant dermatitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing a band of parakeratosis with subcorneal or intracorneal separation","Family history and pedigree analysis for autosomal dominant inheritance","Molecular studies of the chromosome 8 regulatory duplication where available","Mycological examination to exclude dermatophyte infection","Patch testing if contact dermatitis is considered","Clinical photography to document the seasonal cyclical pattern"],"redFlags":["Widespread desquamation with barrier failure and secondary infection","Painful fissuring impairing hand or foot function","Atypical persistent lesions not following the seasonal pattern, prompting reconsideration of diagnosis","Signs of secondary bacterial infection in peeling areas"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign, tending to improve after childhood and adolescence, with a chronic relapsing but non-scarring course punctuated by winter exacerbations.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing recurrent peeling as tinea and prescribing repeated antifungals","Overlooking the seasonal history that is central to recognition","Failing to note the autosomal dominant family history","Escalating to systemic therapy before optimising emollient and keratolytic care"],"management":["Emollients and keratolytic agents such as urea or salicylic acid preparations to reduce scaling and peeling","Protection from cold exposure, since winter and cold weather trigger exacerbations","Gentle debridement and topical keratolytics during acute peeling episodes","Management of associated palmoplantar hyperhidrosis where troublesome","Reassurance about the benign, non-scarring course that tends to improve after adolescence","Clinical genetics counselling for this autosomal dominant disorder with family assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"keratosis_follicularis_spinulosa_decalvans":{"name":"Keratosis follicularis spinulosa decalvans","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KFSD","Siemens-1 syndrome","MBTPS2"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare disorder of follicular keratinisation causing widespread spiny follicular papules with progressive scarring alopecia of the scalp, eyebrows and eyelashes","The classic X-linked form is caused by variants in MBTPS2, the same gene implicated in IFAP syndrome, giving considerable clinical overlap","Facial follicular keratoses often appear in infancy, followed by cicatricial loss of eyebrows, lashes and scalp hair","Photophobia and corneal changes can occur, echoing the ocular involvement of IFAP syndrome","Palmoplantar keratoderma and nail changes are reported in some patients","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by biopsy showing follicular hyperkeratosis and perifollicular inflammation with later scarring, and by MBTPS2 molecular testing","Management is supportive with keratolytics and emollients, treatment of secondary inflammation and infection, and ophthalmology input for ocular involvement"],"pathophysiology":"Keratosis follicularis spinulosa decalvans is usually caused by variants in MBTPS2, encoding a membrane-bound zinc metalloprotease required for regulated intramembrane proteolysis governing sterol and lipid homeostasis and the endoplasmic reticulum stress response. Disturbed function impairs follicular keratinisation, producing spiny follicular plugs, while chronic perifollicular inflammation gradually destroys hair follicles to give a scarring (cicatricial) alopecia. Because the same gene underlies IFAP syndrome, the two conditions represent an allelic spectrum with overlapping cutaneous and ocular features.","additionalDifferentials":["IFAP syndrome","Keratosis pilaris atrophicans faciei (Ulerythema ophryogenes)","Lichen spinulosus","Folliculitis decalvans","Ichthyosis follicularis of other cause","Graham-Little-Piccardi-Lassueur syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of MBTPS2","Skin/scalp biopsy showing follicular hyperkeratosis, perifollicular inflammation and later fibrosis","Ophthalmological assessment including slit-lamp examination for corneal changes","Trichoscopy of the scarring alopecia","Bacterial swabs where secondary infection is suspected","Family history and pedigree analysis"],"redFlags":["Progressive corneal disease threatening vision","Extensive rapidly progressive scarring alopecia","Recurrent secondary infection of inflamed follicular lesions","Features suggesting the broader IFAP phenotype"],"prognosis":"The follicular keratoses may improve after puberty, but the associated scarring alopecia is permanent; visual prognosis depends on the extent of corneal involvement.","pitfalls":["Not recognising the shared MBTPS2 basis and overlap with IFAP syndrome","Mistaking early disease for ordinary keratosis pilaris and missing the scarring alopecia","Neglecting ophthalmological surveillance for corneal disease","Delaying anti-inflammatory measures that might limit follicular scarring"],"management":["Emollients and keratolytics such as urea- or salicylic-acid-based preparations to soften the spiny follicular keratoses","Topical or systemic retinoids for widespread follicular hyperkeratosis, with variable benefit","Anti-inflammatory and antibacterial treatment of perifollicular inflammation and secondary infection to limit follicular destruction","Ophthalmological management of photophobia and corneal changes","Camouflage, wigs or eyebrow reconstruction for the permanent scarring alopecia, which does not regrow","Clinical genetics referral and counselling for this usually X-linked MBTPS2-related disorder with family assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"keratosis_pilaris":{"name":"Keratosis pilaris","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KP","follicular keratosis","keratosis pilaris"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An extremely common, benign disorder of follicular keratinisation producing rough, gritty, keratotic follicular papules with a goose-flesh texture","Classically affects the extensor upper arms, thighs, buttocks, and cheeks in a symmetrical distribution","Often accompanied by perifollicular erythema; a variant with prominent redness and atrophy affects the face (keratosis pilaris rubra and atrophicans)","Strongly associated with atopic dermatitis and ichthyosis vulgaris and tends to run in families with an autosomal dominant tendency","Frequently improves with age and is usually worse in winter and in dry skin","Caused by a keratin plug occluding the follicular opening, sometimes trapping a coiled hair","Usually asymptomatic and cosmetic, with a clinical diagnosis that seldom needs a biopsy","Management is symptomatic with regular emollients and keratolytics (urea, lactic acid, salicylic acid) and gentle exfoliation, and topical retinoids for resistant cases, with realistic expectations"],"pathophysiology":"Keratosis pilaris results from excessive or abnormal keratinisation of the hair-follicle infundibulum, so that a keratin plug forms and occludes the follicular opening, sometimes enclosing a coiled or trapped hair. The plug produces the palpable, rough papule, while surrounding low-grade inflammation gives the perifollicular erythema. The disorder is constitutional and often familial, and its association with atopic dermatitis and ichthyosis vulgaris reflects a shared tendency to disordered keratinisation and impaired epidermal barrier function; the atrophic facial variants involve additional follicular inflammation and scarring.","additionalDifferentials":["Lichen nitidus","Follicular eczema","Folliculitis","Phrynoderma (vitamin A deficiency)","Lichen spinulosus","Milia or comedones","Keratosis pilaris atrophicans variants"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis, generally requiring no investigation","Dermoscopy showing coiled hairs and keratin plugs within follicular openings","Assessment for associated atopic dermatitis and ichthyosis vulgaris","Skin biopsy only in atypical presentations, showing follicular hyperkeratosis with a keratin plug","Consideration of an underlying syndrome when atrophy or scarring is prominent (keratosis pilaris atrophicans)"],"redFlags":["Prominent facial erythema with scarring loss of the eyebrows suggesting a syndromic form (keratosis follicularis spinulosa decalvans)","Atrophy or scarring that may cause permanent hair loss","Atypical, inflammatory, or rapidly evolving lesions prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Benign and chronic but tends to improve over time, often lessening markedly by adulthood; treatment controls rather than cures, and lesions recur if therapy is stopped.","pitfalls":["Over-treating a benign, cosmetic condition and causing irritation","Mistaking the atrophic facial variants for a scarring alopecia or another condition","Failing to recognise associated atopy or ichthyosis vulgaris","Promising cure rather than control"],"management":["Regular emollients and gentle skin care to soften the rough follicular papules and support barrier function","Keratolytic preparations containing urea, lactic acid or salicylic acid to reduce follicular plugging","Topical retinoids for persistent or cosmetically troublesome papules","Short-term topical corticosteroid or vascular laser for prominent perifollicular erythema","Treatment of coexisting atopic dermatitis and ichthyosis vulgaris and avoidance of skin drying","Reassurance that the condition is benign and tends to improve with age, with treatment controlling rather than curing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"keratosis_pilaris_atrophicans":{"name":"Keratosis pilaris atrophicans","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["keratosis pilaris atrophicans faciei","ulerythema ophryogenes","atrophoderma vermiculatum","keratosis follicularis spinulosa decalvans spectrum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of related disorders in which follicular keratosis is accompanied by inflammation that heals with atrophic scarring, distinguishing it from ordinary keratosis pilaris","Ulerythema ophryogenes affects the eyebrows and lateral cheeks with follicular papules, erythema and later loss of the outer eyebrows","Atrophoderma vermiculatum causes a distinctive worm-eaten (honeycomb) pitted scarring of the cheeks, usually beginning in childhood","Keratosis follicularis spinulosa decalvans is the most severe variant, with widespread spiny follicular keratosis and scarring alopecia","Some cases occur in isolation while others are associated with syndromes such as Noonan and cardiofaciocutaneous syndromes or with MBTPS2-related disease","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by biopsy showing follicular hyperkeratosis, perifollicular inflammation and dermal fibrosis; genetic testing is guided by the phenotype","Management is supportive with emollients and keratolytics, anti-inflammatory measures for the erythema, and cosmetic treatment such as laser for residual redness once inflammation settles"],"pathophysiology":"Keratosis pilaris atrophicans encompasses several disorders sharing abnormal follicular keratinisation with a significant inflammatory component. Keratotic plugging of follicles is accompanied by perifollicular inflammation that, unlike simple keratosis pilaris, damages the follicle and surrounding dermis and heals with atrophy and scarring, hence the eyebrow loss, honeycomb facial atrophy or scarring alopecia depending on the variant. Genetic contributions vary, ranging from isolated dominant traits to association with RASopathies such as Noonan and cardiofaciocutaneous syndromes and with MBTPS2-related keratinisation disorders at the severe end of the spectrum.","additionalDifferentials":["Ordinary keratosis pilaris","Keratosis follicularis spinulosa decalvans and IFAP syndrome","Lichen spinulosus","Acne vulgaris (for the facial variants)","Discoid lupus erythematosus (for scarring facial lesions)","Folliculitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing follicular hyperkeratosis, perifollicular inflammation and dermal fibrosis","Assessment for associated syndromes (Noonan, cardiofaciocutaneous) including cardiac and developmental review","Molecular genetic testing guided by phenotype (e.g. RASopathy genes, MBTPS2)","Trichoscopy and dermoscopy of affected areas","Family history and pedigree analysis","Clinical photography to monitor scarring and erythema"],"redFlags":["Progressive scarring alopecia suggesting the severe decalvans variant","Dysmorphic, cardiac or developmental features indicating an associated RASopathy","Rapidly progressive facial scarring","Secondary infection of inflamed follicular lesions"],"prognosis":"The inflammatory phase and follicular keratosis often improve after puberty, but any atrophic scarring, eyebrow loss or scarring alopecia is permanent; overall prognosis depends on whether an underlying syndrome is present.","pitfalls":["Dismissing early disease as ordinary keratosis pilaris and missing the scarring potential","Overlooking an associated RASopathy such as Noonan or cardiofaciocutaneous syndrome","Using ablative cosmetic treatments while active inflammation persists","Failing to recognise the continuum with keratosis follicularis spinulosa decalvans and IFAP syndrome"],"management":["Emollients and keratolytics to reduce the follicular hyperkeratosis","Anti-inflammatory therapy including topical corticosteroids, with topical or systemic retinoids, to dampen the inflammatory phase before scarring occurs","Pulsed-dye or vascular laser for facial erythema and telangiectasia in ulerythema ophryogenes","Early treatment aimed at minimising permanent atrophic scarring, eyebrow loss and scarring alopecia","Assessment for and management of associated syndromes such as Noonan and cardiofaciocutaneous syndromes, including cardiac review","Camouflage or cosmetic reconstruction for established atrophic scarring and eyebrow loss","Clinical genetics referral and counselling guided by phenotype, covering RASopathy and MBTPS2-related disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"kernicterus":{"name":"Kernicterus","system":"Neonatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bilirubin encephalopathy","chronic bilirubin encephalopathy","acute bilirubin encephalopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Kernicterus refers to the neurological injury caused by deposition of unconjugated bilirubin in the brain, classically in the basal ganglia and brainstem nuclei.","It is a preventable complication of severe neonatal unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia and represents the chronic, permanent form of bilirubin neurotoxicity.","Acute bilirubin encephalopathy evolves through phases: early lethargy, hypotonia, and poor feeding, followed by hypertonia with backward arching (retrocollis and opisthotonus), high-pitched cry, and fever, and later apnoea, seizures, and coma.","The classic chronic tetrad includes choreoathetoid cerebral palsy, high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss (or auditory neuropathy), impaired upward gaze, and dental enamel dysplasia.","Only unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin that is not bound to albumin can cross the blood-brain barrier, so free bilirubin and factors lowering albumin binding increase risk.","Prematurity, haemolysis, sepsis, acidosis, hypoalbuminaemia, and certain drugs that displace bilirubin heighten vulnerability at any given bilirubin level.","Prevention rests on identifying and monitoring at-risk newborns, timely phototherapy, and exchange transfusion when bilirubin approaches neurotoxic thresholds.","Once established, chronic bilirubin encephalopathy is largely irreversible, underscoring the importance of early recognition and treatment of hyperbilirubinaemia.","Structured predischarge risk assessment and follow-up of jaundiced newborns reduce the incidence of this now-rare but devastating condition."],"pathophysiology":"Unconjugated bilirubin is normally tightly bound to albumin and does not enter the brain. When the bilirubin load overwhelms albumin binding capacity, or binding is reduced by acidosis, hypoalbuminaemia, prematurity, or competing substances, free unconjugated bilirubin crosses the blood-brain barrier. It is neurotoxic, being preferentially deposited in and damaging the basal ganglia (notably the globus pallidus and subthalamic nuclei), the brainstem auditory and oculomotor nuclei, hippocampus, and cerebellum. Bilirubin impairs mitochondrial function and neuronal signalling, causing acute dysfunction and, if sustained, permanent neuronal loss and gliosis with the characteristic clinical sequelae.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis or meningitis","Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Inborn errors of metabolism causing encephalopathy","Neonatal seizures of other cause","Hypoglycaemic or electrolyte-related encephalopathy","Other causes of neonatal hypertonia or abnormal movements"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial total and unconjugated bilirubin, interpreted against age-in-hours nomograms","Albumin level and bilirubin-to-albumin ratio to gauge binding reserve","Haemolysis workup (blood group, direct antiglobulin test, blood film, reticulocytes)","Auditory brainstem response testing for auditory neuropathy","MRI showing characteristic basal ganglia signal changes in established disease","Evaluation for contributing factors such as sepsis, acidosis, and prematurity"],"redFlags":["Signs of acute bilirubin encephalopathy: lethargy or hypertonia, retrocollis/opisthotonus, high-pitched cry, poor feeding","Bilirubin approaching or exceeding exchange transfusion thresholds for the infant's age and risk","Apnoea, seizures, or coma in a severely jaundiced newborn","Rapidly rising bilirubin, especially with haemolysis, sepsis, or acidosis","Jaundice in a preterm or unwell infant where binding reserve is reduced"],"prognosis":"Acute bilirubin encephalopathy may be partly reversible if bilirubin is lowered promptly, but progression to chronic kernicterus produces permanent, often severe disability including movement disorder, hearing loss, and gaze abnormalities. Because the condition is largely preventable, prognosis hinges on timely detection and treatment of severe hyperbilirubinaemia.","pitfalls":["Interpreting bilirubin values without reference to the infant's age in hours and risk factors","Underestimating neurotoxic risk in preterm, acidotic, hypoalbuminaemic, or septic infants at lower bilirubin levels","Delaying phototherapy or exchange transfusion while bilirubin rises","Missing early, subtle neurological signs of acute encephalopathy","Discharging a jaundiced newborn without adequate follow-up and risk assessment"],"management":["Intensive phototherapy to lower unconjugated bilirubin as the primary intervention in acute bilirubin encephalopathy","Exchange transfusion for severe or rapidly rising hyperbilirubinaemia or signs of acute encephalopathy","Intravenous immunoglobulin for isoimmune haemolytic disease to reduce ongoing haemolysis","Correction of aggravating factors such as sepsis, acidosis, hypoalbuminaemia and dehydration that increase free bilirubin and neurotoxicity","Treatment of the underlying cause of haemolysis with adequate hydration and feeding support","Long-term management of choreoathetoid cerebral palsy with medications and rehabilitation therapies, and cochlear implantation or hearing aids for auditory neuropathy","Multidisciplinary developmental, audiological and visual follow-up, with prevention through timely bilirubin screening against age-in-hours nomograms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"kid_syndrome":{"name":"Keratitis-ichthyosis-deafness (KID) syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KID syndrome","GJB2","connexin 26","keratitis-ichthyosis-deafness syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A triad of vascularising keratitis, ichthyosiform (often erythrokeratodermic) skin, and congenital sensorineural deafness","Skin shows thickened, leathery, verrucous or spiky hyperkeratotic plaques, characteristically with well-demarcated 'stippled' or reticulated palmoplantar keratoderma","Progressive corneal neovascularisation and keratitis threaten vision and often worsen over time","Caused by dominant mutations in GJB2 encoding the gap-junction protein connexin 26, which also underlies non-syndromic deafness","Increased susceptibility to chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis and bacterial skin infection, and a lifelong risk of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma","Follicular occlusion, alopecia, dystrophic nails and dental abnormalities are common associated features","Management is largely supportive with emollients/keratolytics, meticulous skin and eye care, infection surveillance and audiological/ophthalmological input"],"pathophysiology":"KID syndrome results from usually dominant, often de novo mutations in GJB2, the gene encoding connexin 26. Connexins assemble into gap-junction channels that allow direct intercellular exchange of small molecules and ions; the disease-causing mutations act in a dominant fashion that disturbs channel gating and epidermal and cochlear cell communication. The resulting defect impairs epidermal differentiation and keratinocyte homeostasis, producing hyperkeratosis and a compromised barrier, disrupts inner-ear function causing sensorineural deafness, and affects the corneal epithelium leading to keratitis with neovascularisation. Impaired barrier and immune function predispose to infection and, over time, to keratinocyte malignancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Erythrokeratodermia variabilis","Congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma / other congenital ichthyoses","Vohwinkel syndrome (mutilating keratoderma)","HID (hystrix-like ichthyosis-deafness) syndrome, an allelic variant","Clouston hidrotic ectodermal dysplasia","Olmsted syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing hyperkeratosis, acanthosis and papillomatosis","Molecular genetic testing for GJB2 (connexin 26) mutations","Audiological assessment for sensorineural hearing loss","Ophthalmological (slit-lamp) examination for keratitis and corneal neovascularisation","Microbiological swabs/cultures for candidal and bacterial superinfection","Periodic dermatological surveillance and biopsy of suspicious hyperkeratotic or ulcerated lesions"],"redFlags":["New, enlarging, ulcerated or non-healing hyperkeratotic lesion suggesting squamous cell carcinoma","Progressive corneal opacification or neovascularisation with visual deterioration","Recurrent or invasive candidal or bacterial infection","Rapidly extending erythroderma with barrier failure in infancy"],"prognosis":"KID syndrome is a lifelong, progressive condition; vision may deteriorate from keratitis and deafness is usually permanent. The most serious long-term concern is a substantially increased risk of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, so continued surveillance is essential.","pitfalls":["Attributing recurrent skin or mucosal infection to poor hygiene rather than the intrinsic immune/barrier defect","Overlooking malignant transformation within chronic hyperkeratotic plaques","Assuming systemic retinoids will reliably help, as response is variable and they may aggravate keratitis","Missing the diagnosis when the deafness or ocular features precede overt skin change"],"management":["Emollients and keratolytics with systemic retinoids such as acitretin for the ichthyosiform erythrokeratoderma, monitoring retinoid therapy","Prompt antifungal and antibacterial treatment of chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis and bacterial skin infection","Ophthalmological management of the vascularising keratitis with ocular surface lubrication, topical therapy and corneal surgery as needed","Audiological rehabilitation including cochlear implantation for the congenital sensorineural deafness","Lifelong dermatological surveillance with biopsy of suspicious or non-healing hyperkeratotic lesions given the high squamous cell carcinoma risk","Care of associated follicular occlusion, alopecia and nail and dental abnormalities","Clinical genetics referral and counselling for this dominant GJB2 (connexin 26) disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"kienbock_disease":{"name":"Kienbock disease","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lunatomalacia","lunate osteonecrosis","avascular necrosis of the lunate","osteochondrosis of the lunate"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A true osteonecrosis affecting the lunate, the central carpal bone of the proximal row, rather than a traction apophysitis","Typically presents in young adults, often manual labourers aged roughly twenty to forty, and is uncommon in children","Complaints include activity-related dorsal wrist pain, stiffness, swelling, reduced grip strength and limited wrist extension","The radiographic hallmark is early sclerosis (increased density) of the lunate, progressing to fragmentation, collapse and eventual carpal disruption","Frequently associated with a negative ulnar variance (relatively short ulna), which is thought to concentrate load across the lunate","The Lichtman staging system, based on lunate density, collapse and carpal alignment, guides treatment selection","Early disease may be radiographically silent, so magnetic resonance imaging is valuable for confirming devascularisation and marrow change","Management ranges from immobilisation and activity modification in early stages to joint-levelling, revascularisation or salvage surgery as needed for advanced collapse"],"pathophysiology":"Kienbock disease is an osteonecrosis of the lunate in which the bone loses its blood supply and undergoes progressive death, collapse and fragmentation. The cause is regarded as multifactorial, combining interruption of the tenuous intraosseous and extraosseous vascular supply with mechanical overload. A relatively short ulna (negative ulnar variance) is commonly present and is believed to increase compressive stress on the lunate, while repetitive loading and single or repeated microtrauma may precipitate ischaemia. As the necrotic bone weakens it fractures and collapses, altering carpal mechanics, producing proximal migration of the capitate and rotation of the scaphoid, and ultimately leading to secondary degenerative arthritis of the wrist.","additionalDifferentials":["Scaphoid fracture or scaphoid nonunion","Scapholunate ligament injury and carpal instability","Wrist osteoarthritis or post-traumatic arthritis","Ulnar impaction syndrome","De Quervain tenosynovitis or other wrist tendinopathy","Ganglion cyst causing dorsal wrist pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain wrist radiographs including a neutral posteroanterior view to assess ulnar variance and lunate density","Magnetic resonance imaging to detect early avascular change before collapse is visible on plain films","Computed tomography to define fragmentation, collapse and articular surface integrity","Comparison of ulnar variance with the contralateral wrist","Bone scintigraphy where diagnostic uncertainty remains and cross-sectional imaging is unavailable","Staging assessment using the Lichtman classification to plan treatment"],"redFlags":["Progressive lunate collapse with carpal shortening indicating advanced, potentially irreversible disease","Established secondary wrist arthritis with persistent pain and functional loss","Fixed loss of grip and wrist motion suggesting carpal disintegration","Bilateral or atypical presentation prompting consideration of an underlying systemic cause of osteonecrosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the stage at presentation; early disease treated with offloading or joint-levelling procedures may preserve function, whereas advanced lunate collapse and carpal disruption tend to progress to degenerative wrist arthritis with lasting stiffness and reduced grip.","pitfalls":["Attributing persistent wrist pain to a soft-tissue strain and missing early sclerosis on plain films","Relying on normal early radiographs and failing to obtain magnetic resonance imaging when suspicion is high","Overlooking negative ulnar variance as a contributing mechanical factor","Underestimating carpal collapse and applying joint-preserving surgery when salvage is more appropriate"],"management":["In early (pre-collapse) disease, trial non-operative care with wrist immobilisation or splinting, activity modification and simple analgesia or NSAIDs to offload the lunate.","Address negative ulnar variance with a joint-levelling operation such as radial shortening osteotomy to reduce compressive load across the lunate.","Consider procedures to restore lunate vascularity or unload the carpus, such as pedicled vascularised bone grafting, core decompression or capitate-shortening, guided by Lichtman stage.","For established lunate collapse, use salvage carpal procedures such as proximal row carpectomy or limited intercarpal (scaphotrapeziotrapezoid or scaphocapitate) fusion to relieve pain and preserve function.","Reserve total wrist arthrodesis or arthroplasty for end-stage degenerative carpal collapse with refractory pain.","Monitor progression with serial plain radiographs and MRI, restaging by the Lichtman classification to time surgical intervention.","Involve hand therapy and occupational therapy for splinting, grip strengthening, range-of-motion work and workplace adaptation, and refer to a hand surgeon for progressive collapse or persistent pain."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","Green's Operative Hand Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"kikuchi_fujimoto_disease":{"name":"Kikuchi-Fujimoto disease","system":"Rheumatology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["histiocytic necrotising lymphadenitis","Kikuchi disease","Kikuchi's disease","Kikuchi-Fujimoto lymphadenitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign, self-limiting cause of cervical lymphadenopathy, classically in young women of Asian descent","Presents with tender posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, fever, and sometimes night sweats, weight loss, and a maculopapular rash","Leucopenia with atypical lymphocytes is a common laboratory clue","The main clinical importance is that it mimics lymphoma and tuberculous lymphadenitis, and it can be confused with lupus lymphadenopathy on histology","Diagnosis rests on excisional lymph node biopsy showing paracortical necrosis with abundant histiocytes and karyorrhectic debris and a characteristic absence of neutrophils","There is a recognised association with systemic lupus erythematosus, which may precede, coincide with, or follow the lymphadenitis","Management is supportive with analgesia and antipyretics; corticosteroids are reserved for severe or recurrent disease"],"pathophysiology":"Kikuchi-Fujimoto disease is a self-limiting histiocytic necrotising lymphadenitis of uncertain cause, thought to represent an exuberant T-cell and histiocyte-mediated immune response, possibly triggered by a viral or other infectious agent in a genetically predisposed individual. Affected lymph nodes show paracortical zones of coagulative necrosis populated by proliferating histiocytes, plasmacytoid dendritic cells, and CD8-positive T lymphocytes, with prominent karyorrhectic (apoptotic) debris and a striking paucity of neutrophils and plasma cells. The same mechanisms of immune dysregulation link it to systemic lupus erythematosus.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphoma (Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin)","Tuberculous and other granulomatous lymphadenitis","Systemic lupus erythematosus lymphadenopathy","Reactive or viral lymphadenitis (including infectious mononucleosis)","Metastatic carcinoma to cervical nodes","Adult-onset Still disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional lymph node biopsy showing paracortical necrosis, karyorrhexis, and histiocytes without neutrophils","Full blood count and film often showing leucopenia with atypical lymphocytes","Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP) and lactate dehydrogenase","Autoimmune screen including antinuclear antibody to assess for associated lupus","Investigations to exclude tuberculosis and other infective causes","Cross-sectional imaging if lymphoma is a concern"],"redFlags":["Persistent or progressive lymphadenopathy raising concern for lymphoma","Development of features of systemic lupus erythematosus","Prolonged high fever or systemic deterioration","Rare aseptic meningitis or other extranodal involvement"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and usually resolves spontaneously over several weeks to a few months; recurrence occurs in a minority, and patients warrant follow-up for the possible later emergence of systemic lupus erythematosus.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as lymphoma on histology and initiating unnecessary treatment","Failing to consider or later screen for systemic lupus erythematosus","Relying on fine-needle aspiration, which is often non-diagnostic, rather than excisional biopsy","Mistaking the necrosis for tuberculous lymphadenitis","Prescribing antibiotics for a non-infective, self-limiting condition"],"management":["Reassurance and observation, since the necrotising lymphadenitis is benign and self-limiting, resolving over weeks to months","Symptomatic relief with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and antipyretics for fever and tender lymphadenopathy","A course of corticosteroids for severe, persistent or systemic disease, with hydroxychloroquine considered for refractory or recurrent cases","Confirm the diagnosis on excisional biopsy and exclude the key mimics, lymphoma and tuberculous lymphadenitis, before settling on conservative care","Screen for and monitor associated systemic lupus erythematosus with antinuclear antibody and clinical review, as it may precede, coincide with or follow the lymphadenitis","Rheumatology or haematology referral where diagnosis is uncertain or the course is atypical","Structured follow-up for recurrence and for later emergence of lupus"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"killian_jamieson_diverticulum":{"name":"killian jamieson diverticulum","system":"gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Killian-Jamieson diverticulum","proximal lateral cervical oesophageal diverticulum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A Killian-Jamieson diverticulum is a pulsion outpouching that herniates through the Killian-Jamieson space, an anterolateral muscular gap in the proximal cervical oesophagus just below the cricopharyngeus and lateral to the longitudinal muscle insertion.","It is much less common than a Zenker diverticulum and arises below rather than above the cricopharyngeus, which is the key anatomical distinction.","On barium swallow it projects anterolaterally (more often on the left) below the upper oesophageal sphincter, whereas a Zenker diverticulum arises posteriorly above the sphincter.","Most are small and asymptomatic; larger ones may cause dysphagia, regurgitation of undigested food, cough or a palpable neck fullness.","The recurrent laryngeal nerve runs in close relationship to the diverticular neck, so surgical or endoscopic treatment carries a real risk of vocal cord palsy.","Because of the nerve relationship, endoscopic septotomy (as used for Zenker) is generally avoided or approached with great caution, and open transcervical diverticulectomy is often preferred for symptomatic lesions.","It may be discovered incidentally during thyroid ultrasound as a cystic or mixed lesion abutting the thyroid, and can be mistaken for a thyroid nodule.","Cricopharyngeal dysfunction and raised intraluminal pressure are thought to contribute, so myotomy considerations may apply as in other pulsion diverticula."],"pathophysiology":"The Killian-Jamieson diverticulum is a pulsion (false) diverticulum containing mucosa and submucosa but lacking a muscular wall, herniating through the Killian-Jamieson triangle. This is a muscular weak point in the anterolateral wall of the proximal cervical oesophagus, bounded by the oblique fibres of the cricopharyngeus superiorly and the longitudinal and circular muscle of the oesophagus, located just distal to the cricopharyngeus and inferior to the recurrent laryngeal nerve entry. Elevated intraluminal pressure, often related to incoordination or spasm of the upper oesophageal sphincter and cricopharyngeal dysfunction, drives mucosal herniation through this defect. Because the sac lies below the sphincter and adjacent to the recurrent laryngeal nerve, it differs anatomically and surgically from the posteriorly located, supra-sphincteric Zenker diverticulum.","additionalDifferentials":["Zenker (pharyngo-oesophageal) diverticulum","thyroid nodule or cyst on imaging","lateral cervical (branchial) cyst","cricopharyngeal bar or achalasia","parathyroid cyst or adenoma","laryngocoele","cervical oesophageal duplication cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["barium swallow with lateral and oblique cervical views to define sac position relative to the cricopharyngeus","upper gastrointestinal endoscopy to inspect the ostium and exclude mucosal disease","neck ultrasound, which may first detect the lesion adjacent to the thyroid","cross-sectional imaging (computed tomography of the neck) to clarify anatomy before intervention","high-resolution manometry to assess upper sphincter function","laryngoscopic assessment of vocal cord function before any planned surgery"],"redFlags":["progressive dysphagia with weight loss","recurrent aspiration or pneumonia","new hoarseness or vocal cord palsy indicating recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement","signs of perforation such as neck pain, crepitus or fever after instrumentation","rapidly enlarging neck mass"],"prognosis":"Small asymptomatic diverticula have an excellent prognosis and can be observed, while symptomatic lesions generally respond well to surgical diverticulectomy. The principal prognostic concern is iatrogenic recurrent laryngeal nerve injury during treatment.","pitfalls":["Misidentifying the sac as a thyroid nodule on ultrasound and subjecting it to inappropriate biopsy.","Applying a standard endoscopic Zenker septotomy and injuring the adjacent recurrent laryngeal nerve.","Confusing it with a Zenker diverticulum because the anatomical distinction below versus above the cricopharyngeus is not appreciated.","Attempting fine-needle aspiration of a presumed thyroid lesion and causing perforation or infection.","Overlooking coexisting cricopharyngeal dysfunction that may need to be addressed."],"management":["Conservative observation for small, incidental, asymptomatic diverticula","Dietary and postural measures for mild symptoms, including thorough chewing, an upright posture at meals and avoiding food before lying down","Open transcervical diverticulectomy as the preferred definitive treatment for symptomatic lesions, given the intimate relation to the recurrent laryngeal nerve","Avoid or approach with great caution endoscopic septotomy, which carries a high risk of recurrent laryngeal nerve injury in this location, unlike for a Zenker diverticulum","High-resolution manometry to assess upper oesophageal sphincter dysfunction, with cricopharyngeal myotomy considered where indicated","Pre-operative laryngoscopic assessment of vocal cord function and post-operative monitoring for vocal cord palsy","Multidisciplinary ENT and upper gastrointestinal surgical input for planning intervention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease, current edition","Shackelford's Surgery of the Alimentary Tract, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"kimura_disease":{"name":"Kimura disease","system":"Rheumatology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["eosinophilic lymphogranuloma","Kimura's disease","eosinophilic hyperplastic lymphogranuloma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic inflammatory disorder typically affecting young to middle-aged men of Asian descent","Presents with painless subcutaneous nodules or masses in the head and neck, often with regional lymphadenopathy and salivary gland involvement","Marked peripheral blood eosinophilia and strikingly elevated serum IgE are characteristic","Renal involvement, particularly nephrotic syndrome, occurs in a notable minority and should be actively sought","Histology shows lymphoid follicles with germinal centres, dense eosinophilic infiltration with eosinophilic microabscesses, and proliferation of postcapillary venules","Distinct from angiolymphoid hyperplasia with eosinophilia, which is more superficial and occurs in a different demographic","Benign but frequently relapses after excision; corticosteroids and other systemic immunosuppression are used for extensive or recurrent disease"],"pathophysiology":"Kimura disease is a chronic immune-mediated inflammatory condition of unknown cause, possibly reflecting an aberrant T-helper-2 driven allergic or autoimmune response. There is exuberant reactive lymphoid proliferation with well-formed germinal centres accompanied by heavy eosinophil recruitment, elevated IgE, and proliferation of small blood vessels within affected soft tissue and lymph nodes. Cytokines such as interleukin-4 and interleukin-5 are thought to promote the eosinophilia and IgE production. The immune dysregulation may also injure the glomerulus, accounting for the associated nephrotic syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Angiolymphoid hyperplasia with eosinophilia (epithelioid haemangioma)","Lymphoma, particularly Hodgkin and T-cell types","Langerhans cell histiocytosis","Salivary gland tumour or reactive lymphadenopathy","Parasitic infection with eosinophilia","IgG4-related disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional or core biopsy of the mass for histology showing lymphoid follicles and eosinophilic infiltration","Full blood count with differential documenting peripheral eosinophilia","Serum total IgE, which is characteristically very high","Urinalysis and renal function to detect associated nephrotic syndrome or proteinuria","Cross-sectional imaging (ultrasound or MRI) to define extent and lymph node involvement","Exclusion of parasitic infection and lymphoma where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Nephrotic-range proteinuria indicating renal involvement","Rapidly enlarging or systemic lymphadenopathy suggesting lymphoma","Constitutional symptoms such as fever and weight loss requiring exclusion of malignancy","Airway compromise from large head and neck masses"],"prognosis":"Kimura disease is benign and does not undergo malignant transformation, but it is characterised by a chronic, relapsing course with frequent recurrence after excision; associated nephrotic syndrome influences long-term outcome.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with angiolymphoid hyperplasia with eosinophilia, a distinct entity","Failing to check for renal involvement and missing associated nephrotic syndrome","Attributing the mass to malignancy without histological confirmation, or vice versa","Assuming surgical excision is curative when relapse is common","Overlooking the diagnosis in a patient with unexplained eosinophilia and raised IgE"],"management":["Surgical excision of symptomatic head-and-neck nodules or masses as first-line treatment, accepting that recurrence is common","Systemic or intralesional corticosteroids for extensive, recurrent or renal-associated disease","Steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as ciclosporin for relapsing or steroid-dependent disease","Radiotherapy reserved for refractory or repeatedly recurrent lesions","Actively seek and manage renal involvement, particularly nephrotic syndrome, with urinalysis and proteinuria assessment","Monitor peripheral eosinophilia and serum IgE as markers of disease activity","Long-term follow-up given the chronic, relapsing course without malignant transformation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"kindler_syndrome":{"name":"Kindler Syndrome","system":"Integumentary system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Kindler epidermolysis bullosa","Kindler-Weary syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal recessive genodermatosis, now classified within the epidermolysis bullosa spectrum, caused by loss of the focal-adhesion protein kindlin-1.","Presents from birth or early infancy with acral trauma-induced skin blistering that tends to lessen with age.","Progressive poikiloderma develops on sun-exposed skin, with mottled pigmentation, telangiectasia and atrophy.","Diffuse cutaneous atrophy gives characteristic fine wrinkling with a 'cigarette-paper' texture, especially over the dorsal hands and feet.","Marked photosensitivity is typical in childhood, and mucosal involvement causes gingivitis, periodontal disease, oesophageal and urethral strictures and ocular problems.","Caused by biallelic mutations in the FERMT1 (KIND1) gene encoding kindlin-1, which anchors basal keratinocytes to the basement membrane.","There is a heightened lifetime risk of aggressive mucocutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, mandating lifelong surveillance.","Diagnosis rests on the clinical triad with immunofluorescence or electron microscopy showing a variable plane of cleavage and reduced kindlin-1, confirmed by genetic testing."],"pathophysiology":"Kindler syndrome results from biallelic loss-of-function mutations in FERMT1 (also called KIND1), which encodes kindlin-1, a member of the kindlin family of focal-adhesion proteins expressed predominantly in basal keratinocytes. Kindlin-1 localises to focal contacts at the dermo-epidermal junction where it cooperates with talin to activate integrins, linking the actin cytoskeleton to the extracellular matrix and stabilising keratinocyte adhesion, spreading, proliferation and polarity. Loss of functional kindlin-1 destabilises this actin-integrin coupling, so basal keratinocytes detach under mechanical stress; because the defect lies within the basal cell adhesion machinery rather than a single fixed structural component, the plane of blister cleavage is variable and may sit intraepidermally, within the lamina lucida or beneath the lamina densa, a feature that distinguishes Kindler syndrome from the other epidermolysis bullosa subtypes. Disrupted adhesion and impaired signalling also cause reduplication and disorganisation of the basement membrane, progressive epidermal atrophy and the poikilodermatous changes of pigmentary mottling and telangiectasia. Defective keratinocyte function contributes to abnormal photosensitivity and, over time, to chronic inflammation, mucosal scarring and stricture formation. The same disturbance of adhesion, proliferation control and wound repair underlies the strong predisposition to squamous cell carcinoma that characterises the disorder in adulthood.","additionalDifferentials":["Other subtypes of epidermolysis bullosa, particularly dystrophic forms","Rothmund-Thomson syndrome","Xeroderma pigmentosum","Dyskeratosis congenita","Bloom syndrome and other poikiloderma-associated genodermatoses","Hereditary acrokeratotic poikiloderma (Weary type)","Cockayne syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with immunofluorescence antigen mapping showing variable cleavage plane and reduced kindlin-1","Transmission electron microscopy of perilesional skin","FERMT1 (KIND1) molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Regular dermatological surveillance with biopsy of suspicious or non-healing lesions","Dental and periodontal assessment and oral mucosal review","Endoscopic evaluation for oesophageal strictures and urological review for urethral stricture","Ophthalmological assessment for ectropion, conjunctival and corneal involvement"],"redFlags":["A new indurated, ulcerated or non-healing lesion suggesting squamous cell carcinoma","Progressive dysphagia indicating oesophageal stricture","Obstructive urinary symptoms from urethral or anal stenosis","Severe periodontal breakdown with early tooth loss","Rapidly enlarging or bleeding cutaneous lesion in an adult with the condition"],"prognosis":"Blistering usually improves after childhood, but poikiloderma, atrophy, photosensitivity and mucosal strictures progress, and adults face a substantial risk of aggressive squamous cell carcinoma that is the principal determinant of long-term outcome. With diligent sun protection and cancer surveillance many patients have a reasonable life expectancy.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying the disorder as a conventional epidermolysis bullosa subtype and missing the poikiloderma and cancer risk","Attributing skin fragility solely to trauma and overlooking the underlying genodermatosis","Neglecting long-term squamous cell carcinoma surveillance in adults","Failing to screen for and manage mucosal strictures and periodontal disease","Underestimating the importance of rigorous photoprotection from early childhood"],"management":["Meticulous atraumatic skin and wound care, minimising mechanical and frictional trauma and managing blisters gently, as there is no disease-specific therapy","Rigorous lifelong photoprotection with sun avoidance, high-factor sunscreen and protective clothing to limit poikiloderma and skin cancer risk","Regular dermatological surveillance with prompt biopsy of non-healing or suspicious lesions to detect aggressive squamous cell carcinoma","Dental and periodontal care for gingivitis and periodontal disease, with regular oral mucosal review","Endoscopic surveillance and dilatation of oesophageal strictures and urological management of urethral strictures","Ophthalmological review for ocular involvement","Genetic counselling and family screening, reflecting autosomal recessive FERMT1 inheritance","Coordinated multidisciplinary care across dermatology, dentistry, gastroenterology and urology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology (standard reference)","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology (standard reference)","Consensus classification of epidermolysis bullosa (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"king_denborough_syndrome":{"name":"King-Denborough syndrome (RYR1, malignant hyperthermia-associated)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["King syndrome","KDS","RYR1-related King-Denborough syndrome","malignant hyperthermia with dysmorphism and myopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare congenital myopathy caused predominantly by RYR1 variants, combining slowly progressive muscle weakness, characteristic dysmorphic features, and malignant hyperthermia susceptibility","Dysmorphic features classically include short stature, a myopathic facies, low-set ears, downslanting palpebral fissures, ptosis, high-arched palate, webbed neck, pectus deformity, and scoliosis","Presents in childhood with hypotonia, proximal weakness, and delayed motor milestones, and progression is usually slow","Malignant hyperthermia susceptibility is the defining safety concern, so exposure to triggering anaesthetic agents must be avoided","Volatile inhalational anaesthetics and depolarising neuromuscular blockers (suxamethonium) are the principal malignant hyperthermia triggers to avoid, and dantrolene must be immediately available where anaesthesia is undertaken","Creatine kinase is often mildly to moderately elevated and electromyography shows a myopathic pattern","Muscle biopsy findings are variable and may show non-specific myopathic changes, cores, or fibre-type disproportion, so genetics are important for confirmation","Diagnosis integrates the myopathy, the recognisable dysmorphic phenotype, and RYR1 genetic testing, with formal malignant hyperthermia (in-vitro contracture) testing where available"],"pathophysiology":"King-Denborough syndrome is most often caused by variants in RYR1, the gene encoding the skeletal muscle ryanodine receptor, the calcium-release channel of the sarcoplasmic reticulum that is central to excitation-contraction coupling. Abnormal channel function disturbs regulated calcium release, impairing muscle contraction and producing a congenital myopathy, while the same channel abnormality renders muscle liable to uncontrolled calcium release and hypermetabolic crisis on exposure to triggering anaesthetic agents, the basis of malignant hyperthermia susceptibility. The associated dysmorphic and skeletal features form part of the recognisable syndromic phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Other RYR1-related congenital myopathies (central core disease, multiminicore disease)","Noonan syndrome and other dysmorphic syndromes with short stature and webbed neck","Congenital fibre-type disproportion","Other congenital myopathies (nemaline, centronuclear)","Congenital muscular dystrophy","Isolated malignant hyperthermia susceptibility without overt myopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography","Muscle biopsy with histochemistry, which may show cores or fibre-type disproportion","Genetic testing of RYR1 and a congenital myopathy panel","Referral for malignant hyperthermia evaluation, including in-vitro/caffeine-halothane contracture testing where available","Cardiac and respiratory assessment and monitoring of scoliosis"],"redFlags":["Any planned anaesthesia, given the high risk of triggering a malignant hyperthermia crisis","Personal or family history of an unexplained anaesthetic reaction, rigidity, or hyperthermia","Respiratory muscle weakness or progressive scoliosis compromising ventilation","Episodes of rhabdomyolysis or exertional hyperthermia","Cardiac symptoms warranting evaluation"],"prognosis":"The myopathy itself is usually slowly progressive and compatible with prolonged ambulation and a reasonable lifespan, but the malignant hyperthermia susceptibility poses a serious perioperative risk; with recognition, trigger avoidance, and availability of dantrolene, life-threatening anaesthetic crises are largely preventable.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise malignant hyperthermia susceptibility before surgery and using triggering agents","Not ensuring dantrolene and a trigger-free anaesthetic technique are available for affected patients","Attributing the dysmorphic features to another syndrome and missing the underlying RYR1 myopathy","Assuming a normal or non-specific muscle biopsy excludes the diagnosis without genetic testing","Omitting family screening and anaesthetic counselling for at-risk relatives"],"management":["Strict avoidance of malignant hyperthermia triggers, namely volatile inhalational anaesthetics and the depolarising neuromuscular blocker suxamethonium, using trigger-free total intravenous anaesthesia instead","Ensure dantrolene is immediately available wherever anaesthesia is undertaken","Malignant hyperthermia crisis protocol with intravenous dantrolene, active cooling and correction of hyperkalaemia and acidosis","Physiotherapy and rehabilitation to maintain strength and ambulation in the congenital myopathy","Respiratory monitoring, cardiac assessment and orthopaedic management of scoliosis","Documented malignant hyperthermia alert and medical-alert identification, with evaluation and counselling of at-risk family members","Referral for malignant hyperthermia contracture testing and RYR1 genetic testing","Multidisciplinary neuromuscular and anaesthetic care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: RYR1-related myopathies and malignant hyperthermia susceptibility"],"crossLinks":[]},"kleefstra_syndrome":{"name":"Kleefstra syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["9q34.3 deletion syndrome","EHMT1-related disorder","9q subtelomeric deletion syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A neurodevelopmental syndrome with intellectual disability, childhood hypotonia, and severely delayed or absent speech","A recognisable facial gestalt includes brachycephaly, a flat face with hypertelorism, synophrys, anteverted nares, a protruding tongue, and a prognathic jaw that becomes more evident with age","Autistic and other behavioural features are common, and some individuals show a regression or apathetic, catatonia-like state in adolescence or adulthood","Congenital heart defects, genitourinary anomalies, recurrent infections, and later obesity are associated systemic features","Caused by haploinsufficiency of EHMT1 at chromosome 9q34.3, from either a subtelomeric microdeletion or an intragenic EHMT1 variant","EHMT1 encodes a histone methyltransferase, placing Kleefstra syndrome among the chromatin-modification (epigenetic machinery) disorders","Diagnosis uses chromosomal microarray to detect the 9q34.3 deletion, with EHMT1 sequencing when the array is normal"],"pathophysiology":"Kleefstra syndrome is caused by haploinsufficiency of EHMT1, which encodes euchromatic histone methyltransferase 1, an enzyme that dimethylates histone H3 lysine 9 to establish repressive chromatin marks and silence target genes. Reduced EHMT1 activity, whether from a 9q34.3 deletion or an intragenic variant, dysregulates transcriptional programmes controlling neuronal development and function, producing intellectual disability, hypotonia, speech impairment, and behavioural features. Contiguous deletions removing neighbouring genes can add to the systemic and dysmorphic phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Down syndrome (given overlapping facial and hypotonia features)","Smith-Magenis syndrome","Pitt-Hopkins syndrome","Phelan-McDermid syndrome","Rubinstein-Taybi and other chromatin-modification disorders","Angelman syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal microarray to detect and size the 9q34.3 deletion","EHMT1 sequencing when microarray is normal but the phenotype is suggestive","Echocardiography for congenital heart disease","Renal tract ultrasound for genitourinary anomalies","Developmental, speech, and behavioural or autism assessment","Neurological and, where regression occurs, psychiatric review"],"redFlags":["Behavioural or developmental regression or catatonia-like state in adolescence or adulthood","New seizures requiring evaluation","Significant congenital heart disease needing cardiology management","Recurrent or severe infection prompting immune assessment"],"prognosis":"Intellectual disability and speech impairment are lifelong and most individuals require ongoing support, with the additional long-term concern of possible regression or psychiatric deterioration in later life. Systemic complications are managed as they arise, and multidisciplinary care optimises function and wellbeing.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the facial features and hypotonia for Down syndrome and not pursuing microarray","Assuming a normal microarray excludes the diagnosis when an intragenic EHMT1 variant may be responsible","Failing to anticipate and monitor for adolescent or adult regression and psychiatric change","Overlooking associated cardiac or renal anomalies that need screening"],"management":["Early developmental intervention with speech and language therapy, occupational therapy and physiotherapy for the hypotonia and delay, as there is no disease-specific therapy","Individualised educational support and structured behavioural or autism-focused interventions","Echocardiographic evaluation and cardiology management of congenital heart defects","Renal tract imaging and urological management of genitourinary anomalies","Surveillance for and treatment of recurrent infections, with weight management for later obesity","Psychiatric review and monitoring for adolescent or adult regression or a catatonia-like state","Genetic counselling and family testing, reflecting EHMT1 haploinsufficiency at 9q34.3","Coordinated multidisciplinary neurodevelopmental follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Kleefstra Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"kleine_levin_syndrome":{"name":"Kleine-Levin syndrome","system":"Sleep medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KLS","recurrent hypersomnia","periodic hypersomnia","Sleeping Beauty syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / International Classification of Sleep Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by recurrent episodes of profound hypersomnia lasting days to several weeks, during which the patient may sleep the majority of each day","Episodes feature cognitive disturbance with a dreamlike sense of derealisation, apathy, confusion and slowed thinking","Behavioural changes such as hyperphagia and, in some patients, disinhibited hypersexuality occur during episodes","Between episodes sleep, cognition, mood and behaviour return to normal","It most often begins in adolescence with a male predominance, and episodes are frequently triggered by infection or alcohol","The disorder is typically self-limiting, with episodes becoming less frequent and eventually resolving over years","Diagnosis rests on the recurrent stereotyped pattern after excluding other medical, neurological and psychiatric causes"],"pathophysiology":"The cause of Kleine-Levin syndrome is not established, but the episodic nature and the frequent infective trigger suggest a transient, possibly immune-mediated or inflammatory disturbance affecting hypothalamic and thalamic circuits that regulate sleep, appetite and behaviour. Functional imaging during episodes has shown reduced activity in thalamic and other regions, supporting a reversible functional disturbance rather than a fixed structural lesion.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic hypersomnia","Depression with hypersomnic features or bipolar depression","Substance intoxication or withdrawal","Focal temporal or frontal epilepsy","Encephalitis or another structural hypothalamic lesion","Narcolepsy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed episode history and a sleep diary documenting the recurrent pattern","Polysomnography and multiple sleep latency testing, ideally comparing periods within and between episodes","MRI brain to exclude a hypothalamic or thalamic structural lesion","Screening for infection, metabolic disturbance and substance use around episode onset","Psychiatric assessment to exclude a primary mood disorder"],"redFlags":["Focal neurological signs or persistent deficits between episodes","A first episode in later adulthood or an atypical, non-remitting course","Fever, meningism or other features of central nervous system infection","Evidence of a structural hypothalamic lesion on imaging"],"prognosis":"The natural history is generally favourable, with episodes tending to shorten and become less frequent over several years before remitting, although the unpredictability of relapses causes considerable disruption to education and work.","pitfalls":["Dismissing episodes as psychiatric illness or malingering in an adolescent","Missing an encephalitic or structural cause at first presentation","Failing to counsel about the episodic, self-limiting nature and about safety during episodes","Over-investigating between episodes when examination is normal"],"management":["Supportive care during episodes in a safe, low-stimulation environment with supervision and reassurance","Education of patient and family about the episodic, self-limiting nature and avoidance of recognised triggers such as alcohol, sleep deprivation and infection","Prompt treatment of precipitating infections","A trial of stimulants such as modafinil or amfetamines for disabling hypersomnolence during episodes, accepting limited benefit","Lithium considered for prophylaxis in patients with frequent or severely disruptive episodes","Practical support to protect education and employment given the disruption of recurrent episodes","Sleep medicine referral and longitudinal follow-up, tracking episode frequency as the disorder tends to remit over years"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd edition (ICSD-3)","Principles and Practice of Sleep Medicine (Kryger, Roth, Dement)"],"crossLinks":["idiopathic_hypersomnia"]},"klinefelter_syndrome":{"name":"Klinefelter syndrome","system":"Genetic / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["47,XXY","XXY syndrome","seminiferous tubule dysgenesis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard medical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most common sex chromosome aneuploidy in males, caused by one or more extra X chromosomes, typically a 47,XXY karyotype","Often unrecognised until adolescence or adulthood, when hypogonadism and infertility prompt evaluation","Characteristic features include tall stature with long legs, small firm testes, reduced facial and body hair, and gynaecomastia","Testicular failure produces low testosterone with elevated gonadotrophins and, usually, azoospermia","Learning difficulties, particularly with language and executive function, are common though intelligence is usually within the normal range","Testosterone replacement supports pubertal development, bone health, and wellbeing but does not restore fertility","Sperm retrieval techniques can occasionally allow biological fatherhood in selected men","Associated with an increased risk of osteoporosis, metabolic syndrome, venous thromboembolism, and certain tumours"],"pathophysiology":"Klinefelter syndrome results from the presence of one or more supernumerary X chromosomes in a phenotypic male, most commonly a single extra X producing a 47,XXY karyotype, arising from meiotic nondisjunction in either parent. The additional X chromosome material leads to progressive seminiferous tubule fibrosis and hyalinisation, causing primary testicular failure with impaired spermatogenesis and reduced testosterone production, while Leydig cell dysfunction raises luteinising and follicle-stimulating hormone levels. Extra X-linked gene dosage, including genes escaping X-inactivation, is thought to underlie the tall stature, cognitive profile, and other systemic features. Higher-grade aneuploidies produce more severe phenotypes.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of primary hypogonadism","Kallmann syndrome and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Marfan syndrome (for tall stature)","Fragile X syndrome (for learning difficulties)","Constitutional tall stature"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype to confirm the diagnosis and define any mosaicism or higher-grade aneuploidy","Testosterone with luteinising and follicle-stimulating hormone measurement","Semen analysis when fertility is a concern","Bone density assessment given osteoporosis risk","Metabolic screening for glucose intolerance and dyslipidaemia"],"redFlags":["Untreated hypogonadism leading to osteoporosis and fragility fractures","Increased risk of venous thromboembolism","Mediastinal germ cell tumours and a raised risk of male breast cancer","Significant psychosocial and educational difficulties requiring support"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is close to normal, and with testosterone replacement and multidisciplinary support most men lead full lives, though infertility is usual and comorbidity risk is increased.","pitfalls":["Delaying diagnosis by attributing tall stature and learning issues to other causes","Overlooking hypogonadism and its long-term skeletal and metabolic consequences","Assuming complete infertility without offering fertility counselling and sperm retrieval options","Not screening for associated metabolic and thromboembolic risks"],"management":["Testosterone replacement therapy to support virilisation, bone health, muscle mass, mood and wellbeing, recognising it does not restore fertility","Fertility counselling with testicular sperm extraction and assisted reproduction where paternity is desired","Bone density monitoring and management of osteoporosis","Metabolic surveillance and management of glucose intolerance, dyslipidaemia and cardiovascular risk, with treatment of troublesome gynaecomastia","Educational and speech and language support for language and executive difficulties, alongside psychological support","Surveillance for associated conditions including breast malignancy, venous thromboembolism and autoimmune disease","Genetic counselling and karyotype confirmation, with multidisciplinary endocrine, andrology and fertility input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"klippel_feil_syndrome":{"name":"Klippel-Feil syndrome","system":"Genetics / Craniofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Klippel-Feil sequence","congenital cervical vertebral fusion","cervical vertebral fusion syndrome","KFS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from failure of normal segmentation of the cervical vertebrae, producing congenital fusion of two or more cervical segments","The classic clinical triad is a short neck, a low posterior hairline, and restricted neck movement, though not all features are present in every case","Fusion may be limited to a single level or extensive, and the clinical severity varies accordingly","Associated anomalies are common and include scoliosis, Sprengel deformity, hearing loss, and cardiac and renal malformations","Neurological risk arises because fused segments concentrate stress on adjacent mobile levels, predisposing to instability and cord injury","It may occur in isolation or as part of broader spectrums such as the oculo-auriculo-vertebral spectrum","Wildervanck syndrome is a related presentation adding sensorineural deafness and Duane retraction anomaly, seen chiefly in females"],"pathophysiology":"Klippel-Feil syndrome originates from a disturbance of somite segmentation and resegmentation in the cervical region during early embryogenesis, so that adjacent vertebrae fail to separate and instead fuse. This defective segmentation of the paraxial mesoderm produces the shortened, immobile cervical spine and low hairline, and the same developmental window overlaps with the formation of the ear, heart, kidneys, and scapula, accounting for the associated anomalies. Some familial forms are linked to variants in genes governing segmentation and mesodermal patterning, while many cases are sporadic. The fused rigid segments alter cervical biomechanics, transferring load to neighbouring mobile levels and creating a long-term risk of degenerative change and instability.","additionalDifferentials":["Wildervanck (cervico-oculo-acoustic) syndrome","Spondylocostal and spondylothoracic dysostoses","Oculo-auriculo-vertebral (Goldenhar) spectrum","Isolated congenital scoliosis","Acquired cervical fusion from juvenile arthritis","MURCS association"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cervical spine radiographs including flexion-extension views to detect fusion and instability","Magnetic resonance imaging of the spine to assess the cord and craniocervical junction","Audiological assessment for hearing loss","Echocardiography to screen for congenital heart disease","Renal ultrasound to detect urinary tract anomalies","Assessment for scoliosis and Sprengel deformity"],"redFlags":["Neurological signs suggesting cervical cord compression or myelopathy","Craniocervical or segmental instability increasing the risk of catastrophic cord injury","New or progressive weakness, numbness, or sphincter disturbance","High anaesthetic risk from restricted neck movement and potential instability","Significant undiagnosed cardiac or renal malformation"],"prognosis":"Many individuals with limited fusion lead normal lives, but extensive fusion or associated instability carries a lifelong risk of neurological injury and degenerative disease, and activities with high cervical impact are generally discouraged.","pitfalls":["Overlooking cervical instability and clearing patients for contact sport or manipulation","Failing to anticipate a difficult airway from restricted neck extension","Missing associated hearing, cardiac, and renal anomalies","Not recognising Wildervanck syndrome when deafness and Duane anomaly coexist","Attributing new neurological symptoms to unrelated causes rather than adjacent-level stress"],"management":["Activity modification with avoidance of contact and high-impact sports to protect the cervical cord from injury given stress transfer to mobile segments, as there is no disease-specific therapy","Physiotherapy and postural work, with analgesia for degenerative neck pain","Surveillance for cervical instability and adjacent-level degeneration with flexion-extension imaging, and surgical stabilisation or decompression if instability or neurological compromise develops","Audiological assessment and hearing support","Echocardiography and cardiology management of congenital heart disease","Renal ultrasound and management of urinary tract anomalies","Assessment and orthopaedic management of scoliosis and Sprengel deformity","Multidisciplinary follow-up across spinal, ENT and cardiology services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews and Gorlin's Syndromes of the Head and Neck"],"crossLinks":[]},"klippel_trenaunay_syndrome":{"name":"Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome","system":"Genetics / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KTS","Klippel-Trenaunay-Weber syndrome","angio-osteohypertrophy syndrome","PIK3CA-related overgrowth"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / relevant specialty textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A combined vascular malformation and overgrowth disorder, most often caused by a postzygotic mosaic activating variant in PIK3CA and placed within the PIK3CA-related overgrowth spectrum","Classic triad comprises a capillary malformation (port-wine stain), venous and/or lymphatic malformation, and limb overgrowth, usually affecting one lower limb","Persistent embryonic lateral (marginal) veins and deep venous anomalies predispose to venous insufficiency and thromboembolism","Limb-length and girth discrepancy can cause gait disturbance, scoliosis and orthopaedic complications requiring monitoring through growth","Lymphatic malformations may cause recurrent cellulitis, lymphatic vesicles and leakage","The presence of significant arteriovenous fistulae suggests Parkes-Weber syndrome, a related but distinct high-flow entity with different management","Care is multidisciplinary, with compression, orthopaedic and interventional radiology input, and targeted mTOR/PI3K pathway inhibitors used in selected severe cases"],"pathophysiology":"Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome is largely driven by somatic mosaic activating variants in PIK3CA, which encodes the catalytic subunit of phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase. Because the variant arises after fertilisation, only a proportion of cells are affected, producing the segmental and asymmetric pattern of disease. Constitutive activation of the PI3K/AKT/mTOR pathway promotes tissue growth and abnormal vascular development, giving rise to the combined capillary, venous and lymphatic malformations and the overgrowth of soft tissue and bone. Anomalous and persistent embryonic veins contribute to venous hypertension and thrombotic risk. The disorder is sporadic and not inherited.","additionalDifferentials":["Parkes-Weber syndrome (high-flow arteriovenous malformations with overgrowth)","CLOVES syndrome and other PIK3CA-related overgrowth phenotypes","Proteus syndrome (AKT1 mosaic overgrowth)","Sturge-Weber syndrome with facial capillary malformation","Isolated venous or lymphatic malformation","Maffucci syndrome with vascular malformations and enchondromas"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted molecular testing for a mosaic PIK3CA variant on affected tissue where available","Duplex ultrasound to map venous anatomy and detect deep venous anomalies or thrombosis","MRI/MR venography to delineate the extent of vascular malformation and soft-tissue overgrowth","Serial limb-length and orthopaedic assessment through childhood","Coagulation studies (including D-dimer and fibrinogen) to assess localised intravascular coagulopathy","Lymphoscintigraphy where lymphatic malformation is prominent"],"redFlags":["Signs of deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism arising from venous anomalies","Evidence of arteriovenous fistulae or high-output cardiac features suggesting Parkes-Weber syndrome","Severe or recurrent bleeding from vascular malformations or gastrointestinal/pelvic involvement","Recurrent cellulitis or rapidly worsening limb function and deformity"],"prognosis":"Most affected individuals have a normal life expectancy, but chronic pain, venous insufficiency, orthopaedic problems and recurrent infections cause significant long-term morbidity. Thromboembolic complications and, in some, extensive malformations are the principal serious risks, and targeted pathway inhibitors offer benefit in severe disease.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish Klippel-Trenaunay (low-flow) from Parkes-Weber (high-flow) disease, which changes management","Overlooking localised intravascular coagulopathy and thromboembolic risk before procedures","Neglecting orthopaedic surveillance for progressive limb-length discrepancy and scoliosis","Intervening on anomalous superficial veins without confirming adequate deep venous drainage"],"management":["Conservative venous and lymphatic care with graduated compression garments for venous insufficiency and lymphoedema","Targeted pathway-directed therapy with the mTOR inhibitor sirolimus, or a PIK3CA inhibitor such as alpelisib, for extensive or complicated malformations","Sclerotherapy, endovenous laser ablation or surgical treatment of symptomatic venous or lymphatic malformations and persistent marginal veins","Thromboprophylaxis and anticoagulation for venous thromboembolism and localised intravascular coagulopathy, particularly around surgery","Prompt antibiotic treatment of recurrent cellulitis complicating lymphatic malformation","Orthopaedic monitoring of limb-length discrepancy with shoe raises or epiphysiodesis, and management of scoliosis and gait disturbance","Genetic counselling explaining the sporadic, postzygotic mosaic PIK3CA origin","Multidisciplinary vascular anomalies team with pain management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","GeneReviews: PIK3CA-Related Overgrowth Spectrum"],"crossLinks":[]},"kluver_bucy_syndrome":{"name":"Kluver-Bucy syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Kluver-Bucy","bilateral temporal lobe syndrome","amygdala syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A behavioural syndrome resulting from bilateral damage to the medial temporal lobes, particularly the amygdalae","Core features include hyperorality (examining and placing objects in the mouth), hypersexuality, and abnormal placidity or blunted fear and anger","Visual and other agnosias may occur, with an inability to recognise objects or their significance (psychic blindness)","Hypermetamorphosis, an excessive tendency to attend and react to every visual stimulus, is characteristic","In humans the full syndrome is uncommon and often partial, arising from herpes simplex encephalitis, head injury, stroke, frontotemporal dementia, or after temporal lobectomy","Memory impairment frequently accompanies the syndrome because of medial temporal involvement","Management is supportive and directed at the underlying cause, with behavioural measures and cautious pharmacotherapy for disruptive symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"Kluver-Bucy syndrome follows bilateral destruction or dysfunction of the anterior temporal lobes and amygdalae, structures central to assigning emotional and biological significance to sensory stimuli. Loss of amygdala function disconnects perception from appropriate emotional appraisal, producing tameness and loss of fear (placidity), indiscriminate oral and sexual exploration, and an inability to attach meaning to what is seen despite intact primary vision. Involvement of adjacent hippocampal and temporal cortex accounts for the accompanying amnesia and agnosia. In humans the commonest cause is herpes simplex encephalitis, with other aetiologies including trauma, infarction, neurodegeneration, and bilateral temporal surgery.","additionalDifferentials":["Behavioural-variant frontotemporal dementia","Herpes simplex and other limbic encephalitis","Anti-NMDA-receptor autoimmune encephalitis","Post-ictal or interictal behavioural change in temporal lobe epilepsy","Frontal lobe disinhibition syndromes","Hypersexuality from medication or endocrine disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI brain to demonstrate bilateral temporal or amygdala lesions","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis and viral PCR for herpes simplex and autoimmune encephalitis","Electroencephalography to assess for temporal epileptiform activity","Autoimmune and paraneoplastic antibody panel where encephalitis is suspected","Neuropsychological assessment of memory, recognition, and behaviour","Bloods and metabolic screen"],"redFlags":["Fever, headache, and altered consciousness suggesting active encephalitis requiring urgent treatment","Seizures indicating temporal lobe irritation","Rapidly progressive behavioural change pointing to autoimmune or neurodegenerative disease","Hypersexual or disinhibited behaviour posing risk to the patient or others"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the underlying cause; features following herpes simplex encephalitis or extensive bilateral injury may be permanent, whereas some post-ictal or reversible cases improve. Residual memory and behavioural impairment commonly persists.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise and treat herpes simplex encephalitis promptly as an underlying cause","Mistaking the disinhibition for primary psychiatric illness","Overlooking accompanying amnesia and agnosia when assessing capacity and safety","Attributing hyperorality or hypersexuality to behavioural choice rather than organic disease"],"management":["Treat the underlying cause, with aciclovir for herpes simplex encephalitis and immunotherapy for autoimmune encephalitis","Structured behavioural management and supervision for hyperorality, hypersexuality and disinhibition","Pharmacological control of behavioural symptoms with antipsychotics, mood stabilisers such as carbamazepine, or selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors for hypersexuality and aggression","Environmental safety measures, including removing hazardous objects given indiscriminate oral exploration","Neurorehabilitation with cognitive and memory support","Antiseizure medication where temporal lobe seizures occur","Multidisciplinary neurology and neuropsychiatry input with carer education and support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"kniest_dysplasia":{"name":"Kniest dysplasia","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["COL2A1","Kniest syndrome","metatropic dysplasia type II (historical)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A type II collagenopathy caused by dominant COL2A1 variants, part of a spectrum that also includes achondrogenesis type II, spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita and Stickler syndrome","Features disproportionate short stature with a short trunk and short limbs, prominent enlarged joints with restricted movement, and progressive contractures","Craniofacial features include a round flat face, midface hypoplasia, prominent eyes and cleft palate in many patients","Ocular involvement (high myopia, retinal detachment, cataract) reflects the shared type II collagen defect and requires ophthalmological surveillance","Sensorineural or conductive hearing loss and recurrent otitis media are common","Radiographs show dumbbell-shaped femora, platyspondyly with coronal clefts, and characteristic large 'swiss-cheese' cartilage on histology","Overlap with other COL2A1 disorders means phenotype and severity vary; the tell-tale combination is stiff enlarged joints plus ocular and hearing problems"],"pathophysiology":"Kniest dysplasia arises from dominant-negative variants in COL2A1, encoding the alpha-1 chain of type II collagen, the principal collagen of cartilage, the vitreous and the nucleus pulposus. Most are in-frame exon-skipping or splice variants that yield a shortened but incorporated collagen chain, disrupting the fibril network throughout cartilaginous tissues. The abnormal matrix produces soft, vacuolated cartilage (the histological 'swiss-cheese' appearance), leading to defective endochondral ossification, joint stiffness and the ocular and auditory manifestations shared across the type II collagenopathies.","additionalDifferentials":["Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita","Metatropic dysplasia","Stickler syndrome","Dyggve-Melchior-Clausen syndrome","Morquio syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis IV)","Diastrophic dysplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey documenting dumbbell femora, platyspondyly and coronal vertebral clefts","COL2A1 molecular genetic testing","Ophthalmological assessment including dilated retinal examination for myopia and detachment risk","Audiometry and ENT review for hearing loss and middle-ear disease","Cervical spine imaging to assess odontoid hypoplasia and instability"],"redFlags":["Symptoms of retinal detachment (flashes, floaters, field loss) demanding urgent ophthalmology review","Cervical instability with odontoid hypoplasia before anaesthesia or manipulation","Progressive respiratory compromise from restrictive chest and airway involvement","Cleft palate with feeding or airway difficulties in infancy"],"prognosis":"Survival is generally good, but morbidity from progressive joint stiffness, contractures, myopia with retinal detachment risk, and hearing loss is substantial. Lifelong multidisciplinary surveillance mitigates avoidable complications.","pitfalls":["Labelling the disorder purely as a skeletal problem and neglecting mandatory eye and hearing surveillance","Missing retinal detachment because myopia was assumed to be simple refractive error","Overlooking cervical instability prior to airway management","Confusing Kniest with metatropic dysplasia given historical naming and radiographic overlap"],"management":["Ophthalmological surveillance with dilated retinal examination and prompt treatment of high myopia, cataract and retinal detachment, as there is no disease-specific therapy","Audiological monitoring and management of hearing loss and recurrent otitis media with grommets and hearing aids","Physiotherapy and orthopaedic care for joint stiffness, contractures and progressive deformity, with joint replacement in adulthood","Cervical spine imaging with anaesthetic and activity precautions for odontoid hypoplasia and instability","Cleft palate repair with feeding and speech support","Pain management for progressive joint disease","Genetic counselling reflecting dominant COL2A1 inheritance, often arising de novo","Multidisciplinary skeletal dysplasia team follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Type II Collagen Disorders Overview"],"crossLinks":[]},"knobloch_syndrome":{"name":"Knobloch syndrome","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KNO","COL18A1","vitreoretinal degeneration with occipital encephalocele"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ryan's Retina / Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive vitreoretinal degeneration caused by COL18A1 mutations affecting collagen XVIII and its endostatin fragment","Classic triad is high (often extreme) myopia, vitreoretinal degeneration with a high risk of retinal detachment, and an occipital skull defect such as an encephalocele or meningocele","Macular abnormalities and chorioretinal atrophy are common and can markedly reduce central vision","The occipital defect may be subtle (a scalp lesion or bony defect) and warrants neuroimaging when the ocular phenotype is recognised","Retinal detachment often occurs early and can be bilateral, so prophylactic surveillance is important","Cataract, lens subluxation and glaucoma may also occur","Management includes correction of high myopia, vigilant monitoring and surgical treatment of retinal detachment, neurosurgical assessment of the encephalocele, and genetic counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Knobloch syndrome is caused by biallelic mutations in COL18A1, which encodes collagen XVIII, a basement-membrane collagen expressed in the eye and brain and the precursor of the anti-angiogenic fragment endostatin. Loss of functional collagen XVIII disrupts the internal limiting membrane and vitreoretinal interface, producing severe vitreoretinal degeneration, high myopia and a strong predisposition to retinal detachment. The same developmental defect in neural tissue underlies the occipital skull and meningeal defects, linking the ocular and central nervous system features.","additionalDifferentials":["Stickler syndrome","Marfan syndrome and other high-myopia connective tissue disorders","Wagner syndrome and other vitreoretinopathies","Walker-Warburg syndrome and other syndromic high myopia with brain malformation","Isolated pathological (degenerative) myopia","Familial exudative vitreoretinopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination and wide-field imaging for vitreoretinal degeneration and detachment risk","Neuroimaging (MRI) to characterise the occipital encephalocele or skull defect","Optical coherence tomography for macular atrophy and vitreoretinal interface changes","Axial length and refraction to quantify high myopia","Electroretinography to assess retinal function","Molecular genetic testing for COL18A1 mutations"],"redFlags":["Signs of retinal detachment (floaters, flashes, field loss) demanding urgent vitreoretinal care","An occipital scalp or skull lesion requiring neurosurgical evaluation","Rapidly progressive myopia or macular change in early childhood","Affected siblings needing screening given recessive inheritance"],"prognosis":"Visual prognosis is often guarded because of high myopia, macular degeneration and recurrent retinal detachment; systemic prognosis depends on the severity of the occipital defect and any associated neurological features.","pitfalls":["Missing the occipital encephalocele when it presents only as a subtle scalp or bony abnormality","Attributing the ocular picture to isolated pathological myopia and underestimating detachment risk","Failing to arrange neuroimaging once the syndrome is suspected","Not counselling parents about recessive recurrence risk"],"management":["Lifelong ophthalmological surveillance with dilated and wide-field examination for vitreoretinal degeneration and retinal detachment, as there is no disease-specific therapy","Prophylactic retinopexy or laser and prompt surgical repair by vitrectomy or scleral buckling for retinal detachment","Correction of high myopia with refraction and low-vision aids","Neuroimaging and neurosurgical assessment and repair of the occipital encephalocele or skull defect","Monitoring and management of associated cataract, lens subluxation and glaucoma","Visual rehabilitation and low-vision support","Genetic counselling and family screening, reflecting autosomal recessive COL18A1 inheritance","Multidisciplinary ophthalmology and neurosurgery follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ryan's Retina","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"kohler_disease":{"name":"Kohler disease","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Kohler's bone disease","tarsal navicular osteochondrosis","osteonecrosis of the tarsal navicular","navicular osteochondrosis of childhood"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A true osteonecrosis of the tarsal navicular occurring in the immature foot, not a traction apophysitis","Affected bone is the tarsal navicular, the last tarsal bone to ossify, which is vulnerable during its ossification","Typically affects children aged around four to seven years and is more common in boys","Presents with a painful limp, tenderness and sometimes swelling over the medial midfoot, with weight-bearing on the outer border of the foot","The radiographic hallmark is a flattened, sclerotic and fragmented navicular with narrowing of the ossific nucleus","Frequently unilateral, though it can be bilateral, and radiographic changes may lag or persist behind clinical improvement","It is a self-limiting condition, with the navicular typically reconstituting to a normal or near-normal shape over time","Management is supportive with offloading, activity modification and short-term immobilisation for symptom relief as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Kohler disease is an osteochondrosis of the tarsal navicular caused by temporary interruption of its blood supply during a vulnerable period of ossification. Because the navicular is the last tarsal bone to ossify and lies at the apex of the medial longitudinal arch, it is subjected to compressive forces at a time when its single ossific nucleus and vascular supply are still immature. Transient ischaemia leads to sclerosis, flattening and fragmentation of the developing bone, which is often exacerbated by weight-bearing stress. Because the vascular insult is temporary and the child continues to grow, revascularisation and remodelling usually restore normal or near-normal navicular architecture, distinguishing it from the adult navicular osteonecrosis of Mueller-Weiss syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Mueller-Weiss syndrome (adult tarsal navicular osteonecrosis)","Tarsal navicular stress fracture","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis of the midfoot","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis of the midfoot","Accessory navicular syndrome","Normal irregular or multicentric ossification of the navicular"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Weight-bearing foot radiographs demonstrating navicular sclerosis, flattening and fragmentation","Comparison views of the contralateral foot to distinguish disease from normal ossification variation","Magnetic resonance imaging where the diagnosis is uncertain or infection is suspected","Inflammatory markers and full blood count if septic arthritis or osteomyelitis is a concern","Clinical assessment of gait and site of maximal tenderness over the medial midfoot","Follow-up radiographs to document reconstitution of the navicular"],"redFlags":["Fever, marked swelling or systemic upset suggesting infection rather than osteochondrosis","Rest pain or night pain raising concern for an alternative pathology","Failure of symptoms to settle over the expected course prompting reassessment of the diagnosis","Bilateral or atypical disease warranting consideration of a systemic condition"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; Kohler disease is self-limiting and the navicular almost always reconstitutes to normal shape with resolution of symptoms, typically within months to a couple of years and without long-term sequelae.","pitfalls":["Mistaking normal irregular navicular ossification for disease and over-investigating an asymptomatic child","Failing to consider infection when swelling, warmth or systemic features are present","Confusing the benign childhood condition with the more serious adult Mueller-Weiss navicular osteonecrosis","Prolonged unnecessary immobilisation given the naturally self-limiting course"],"management":["Reassurance, as the navicular osteochondrosis is self-limiting with an excellent prognosis and near-universal reconstitution of the bone","Simple analgesia and activity or weight-bearing modification for pain","A short-leg walking cast or immobilisation for a period in more painful cases to hasten symptom relief","Medial arch support or orthoses for comfort once immobilisation is discontinued","Avoidance of surgery, which is not indicated","Clinical and radiographic follow-up to confirm reconstitution, recognising radiographic change lags behind clinical improvement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"koilonychia":{"name":"Koilonychia (spoon nails)","system":"Dermatology / Nail","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["spoon nails","spoon-shaped nails","concave nails"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Koilonychia describes a thin, flattened or concave nail with everted edges, so that the plate is spoon-shaped and can theoretically hold a drop of water","It is classically linked to iron-deficiency anaemia and may accompany the dysphagia of Plummer-Vinson (Paterson-Kelly) syndrome","A gentle, self-limiting form is normal in many infants and young children and resolves as the nail thickens with age","Occupational and environmental softening, from prolonged contact with solvents, oils, or repeated wetting, is a recognised acquired cause","It can also occur with haemochromatosis, thyroid disease, and some hereditary nail disorders","Because iron deficiency is the most treatable systemic association, checking iron status is a sensible early step in unexplained adult cases","Management centres on correcting the underlying deficiency or disease and protecting the nails from softening chemicals and trauma"],"pathophysiology":"Koilonychia develops when the normal transverse and longitudinal curvature of the nail is lost or reversed, producing a flattened or upturned, concave plate. A thin, soft plate is more easily deformed by the ordinary pressures of daily use, and reduced availability of iron, which is required for the keratinisation and structural integrity of the nail, is thought to contribute in deficiency states. Softening from repeated exposure to water, solvents, or oils has a similar mechanical effect, while in infancy the nail is naturally thin and pliable, explaining the transient physiological form that corrects itself as the plate matures.","additionalDifferentials":["Physiological koilonychia of infancy","Iron-deficiency anaemia and Plummer-Vinson syndrome","Occupational or chemical softening of the nail","Haemochromatosis and other metabolic disorders","Thyroid dysfunction","Hereditary or congenital koilonychia","Lichen planus or psoriasis producing nail thinning"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and iron studies (ferritin, serum iron, transferrin saturation)","History of occupational and domestic exposure to solvents, oils, and wet work","Thyroid function tests where clinically indicated","Assessment for dysphagia and consideration of Plummer-Vinson syndrome","Iron overload screen (transferrin saturation, ferritin) if haemochromatosis suspected","Onychoscopy and examination of all nails to define the pattern and severity"],"redFlags":["Koilonychia with dysphagia, suggesting Plummer-Vinson syndrome and possible upper oesophageal web","Persistent unexplained iron deficiency requiring investigation for occult blood loss","Associated systemic features of haemochromatosis such as skin pigmentation and joint disease","New adult-onset koilonychia without an obvious mechanical or occupational cause"],"prognosis":"The physiological form of childhood resolves spontaneously, and acquired koilonychia usually improves once the cause, particularly iron deficiency, is corrected, although the flattened nail only normalises gradually as new plate grows out.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing pathological koilonychia in an infant when the finding is a normal transient variant","Treating the nail deformity without checking iron status in an adult","Missing an occult source of blood loss underlying the iron deficiency","Overlooking occupational chemical exposure as a reversible cause"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying cause, with oral or intravenous iron replacement for iron-deficiency anaemia as the most treatable association","Investigate and manage other systemic associations, including venesection for haemochromatosis and correction of thyroid dysfunction","Assess for dysphagia and an oesophageal web and manage associated Plummer-Vinson syndrome","Reduce occupational and environmental nail softening by minimising contact with solvents, oils and repeated wetting, using protective gloves and emollients","Reassurance and observation for the physiological infantile form, which resolves as the nail plate thickens with age","Monitor gradual normalisation of the nail as new plate grows out once the cause is corrected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"kommerell_diverticulum":{"name":"Kommerell diverticulum","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Kommerell's diverticulum","diverticulum of Kommerell","aberrant subclavian artery diverticulum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy / Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A bulbous outpouching at the origin of an aberrant subclavian artery, representing a remnant of the embryonic aortic arch","Most often accompanies an aberrant right subclavian artery arising from a left arch, or an aberrant left subclavian arising from a right arch","Together with the aberrant vessel it may complete a vascular ring that encircles and compresses the trachea and oesophagus","Frequently asymptomatic and found incidentally, but can cause dysphagia (classically dysphagia lusoria), stridor, cough, or chest and back discomfort","Cross-sectional CT or MR angiography defines the arch anatomy, the diverticulum size, and any airway or oesophageal compression","The diverticulum is prone to aneurysmal enlargement and carries a risk of dissection or rupture, which guides the threshold for repair","Symptomatic compression or significant aneurysmal change is treated by surgical or endovascular repair with revascularisation of the involved subclavian artery"],"pathophysiology":"Kommerell diverticulum is a congenital anomaly of aortic arch development in which failure of normal regression of a segment of the embryonic dorsal aorta leaves a saccular dilatation at the base of an aberrant subclavian artery. The aberrant vessel typically courses behind the oesophagus, and the diverticulum with its associated ligamentum arteriosum can complete a vascular ring encircling the trachea and oesophagus. Compression of these structures produces respiratory and swallowing symptoms, while the diverticulum wall, being structurally abnormal, is predisposed to progressive aneurysmal dilatation with attendant risks of dissection and rupture. Symptoms therefore stem from two mechanisms: mechanical compression of adjacent airway and oesophagus, and the consequences of aneurysmal degeneration of the diverticulum itself.","additionalDifferentials":["Aberrant subclavian artery without diverticulum","Double aortic arch or other complete vascular ring","Thoracic aortic aneurysm of other cause","Oesophageal or mediastinal mass causing dysphagia","Achalasia or other primary oesophageal dysmotility","Mediastinal lymphadenopathy compressing the airway"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT angiography of the thoracic aorta to define arch anatomy and diverticulum dimensions","MR angiography as a radiation-free alternative for anatomy and surveillance","Barium swallow to demonstrate posterior oesophageal indentation in dysphagia lusoria","Echocardiography to assess associated congenital cardiac anomalies","Bronchoscopy or oesophagoscopy where airway or luminal compression is suspected","Pulmonary function or airway assessment when respiratory compression is present"],"redFlags":["Rapid diverticulum enlargement or signs of dissection or rupture","Progressive stridor or respiratory compromise from airway compression","Severe or worsening dysphagia with weight loss","Acute severe chest or back pain suggesting an aortic emergency"],"prognosis":"Small asymptomatic diverticula may remain stable and can be monitored, whereas symptomatic or enlarging lesions carry a meaningful risk of complications, including dissection and rupture. Elective repair of appropriately selected lesions has good outcomes and relieves compressive symptoms.","pitfalls":["Dismissing an incidental diverticulum without characterising its size or growth potential","Attributing dysphagia to a primary oesophageal disorder and missing a vascular ring","Underestimating the aneurysmal and rupture risk of the abnormal diverticulum wall","Failing to plan subclavian revascularisation when repairing the aberrant vessel","Overlooking associated arch or congenital cardiac anomalies"],"management":["Observation with serial cross-sectional CT or MR angiography for small, asymptomatic diverticula","Blood pressure control and cardiovascular risk factor modification to limit aneurysmal degeneration and dissection risk","Surgical repair with diverticulectomy and re-implantation or division of the aberrant subclavian artery, plus division of the vascular ring, for symptomatic compression or an enlarging or aneurysmal lesion","Relief of tracheo-oesophageal compression causing dysphagia lusoria or stridor by dividing the ring","Endovascular or hybrid repair in selected anatomy","Assessment and management of associated congenital cardiac anomalies","Surveillance for dissection and rupture to guide the threshold for intervention","Referral to cardiothoracic and vascular surgery for operative planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"koolen_de_vries_syndrome":{"name":"Koolen-de Vries syndrome (17q21.31 deletion/KANSL1)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["17q21.31 microdeletion syndrome","KANSL1-related intellectual disability syndrome","KdVS"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused either by a recurrent 17q21.31 microdeletion or by an intragenic variant in KANSL1, the single critical gene at the locus","Affected individuals are often noted to have a friendly, sociable, and amiable disposition","Neonatal and childhood hypotonia with developmental delay and intellectual disability is characteristic","Epilepsy occurs in a substantial proportion and should be actively sought and treated","Ptosis and distinctive facial features, including a long face and tubular or pear-shaped nose, are common","The recurrent deletion is associated with a specific parental chromosome 17 inversion polymorphism that predisposes to the rearrangement","Because KANSL1 is the causative gene, an intragenic sequence variant produces the same phenotype as the whole-gene deletion","Diagnosis is by chromosomal microarray for the deletion or by sequencing of KANSL1"],"pathophysiology":"Koolen-de Vries syndrome results from reduced dosage of KANSL1 on chromosome 17q21.31, whether through a recurrent microdeletion of the surrounding region or a loss-of-function variant within the gene itself. KANSL1 encodes a component of a histone acetyltransferase complex that modifies chromatin and thereby regulates the expression of many downstream genes during development. Haploinsufficiency disturbs this epigenetic regulation, affecting neuronal development and function and producing the intellectual disability, hypotonia, epilepsy, and characteristic facial features. The recurrent deletion is facilitated by a common inversion polymorphism of the region carried by a parent, which places the chromosome at increased risk of the non-allelic homologous recombination that generates the deletion.","additionalDifferentials":["Other microdeletion syndromes presenting with hypotonia and intellectual disability","Other chromatin-modifier disorders such as Kabuki syndrome","Congenital myopathies and other causes of early hypotonia","Genetic epilepsy syndromes with developmental delay","Ptosis-associated syndromes such as blepharophimosis syndrome","Non-specific syndromic intellectual disability identified on broad genetic testing"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal microarray to detect the recurrent 17q21.31 deletion","Sequencing of KANSL1 when microarray is normal but the phenotype fits","Electroencephalography and neurological assessment for suspected seizures","Ophthalmological review for ptosis and associated eye findings","Developmental and cognitive assessment to guide support","Parental studies including assessment for the predisposing inversion polymorphism"],"redFlags":["New or poorly controlled seizures requiring prompt neurological management","Ptosis obstructing the visual axis and threatening amblyopia","Significant feeding difficulty or aspiration related to hypotonia","Congenital anomalies such as cardiac or renal defects needing evaluation"],"prognosis":"Lifespan is generally not shortened, and the outlook is defined by lifelong intellectual disability of variable degree together with the sociable temperament that is typical of the condition; epilepsy is often manageable, and most individuals make developmental progress with appropriate educational and therapeutic support, though independent living is usually limited.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal microarray excludes the diagnosis when a KANSL1 sequence variant can produce the same condition","Overlooking epilepsy because the child's amiable behaviour masks concern","Missing ptosis severe enough to threaten vision in a young child","Failing to test a parent for the predisposing inversion when counselling on recurrence"],"management":["Early developmental intervention with physiotherapy for hypotonia and speech and language and occupational therapy, as there is no disease-specific therapy","Antiseizure medication with EEG-guided management of epilepsy, which affects a substantial proportion","Individualised educational and behavioural support that draws on the characteristically sociable temperament","Ophthalmological assessment and correction of ptosis and associated eye findings","Surveillance and management of associated congenital heart, renal and feeding problems","Genetic counselling with parental testing, including for the predisposing 17q21.31 inversion polymorphism","Coordinated multidisciplinary neurodevelopmental follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Koolen-de Vries syndrome","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"koro":{"name":"Koro","system":"Nervous system / Psychiatry","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Genital retraction syndrome","Shook yang"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan and Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Culture-bound syndrome of acute anxiety or panic that the genitals (penis, or vulva and nipples in women) are retracting into the body, with a feared outcome of death.","Reported predominantly in South and East Asia, with well-documented epidemic or mass outbreaks.","The patient may use physical means to prevent retraction, such as clamps, string, or having others hold the organ.","Acute onset with intense autonomic anxiety; in the classical epidemic form it is usually short-lived.","Distinguish culturally sanctioned epidemic koro from koro-like symptoms secondary to psychiatric or organic disease.","In epidemic cases the belief is often overvalued rather than frankly delusional, and reassurance and education are central.","Secondary koro can occur within psychotic disorders, depression, substance use, and neurological disease."],"pathophysiology":"Koro is best understood as a culturally shaped anxiety syndrome in which culturally transmitted beliefs about genital loss and mortality interact with acute autonomic arousal. In the classical epidemic form, shared cultural models and social contagion generate an overvalued fear of genital retraction with a florid panic response, often precipitated by sexual guilt or perceived transgression. Sympathetic activation can produce genuine cremasteric contraction and smooth-muscle changes that the individual misinterprets as retraction, reinforcing the fear. Secondary or sporadic koro reflects the same cognitive theme emerging from an underlying psychotic, affective, substance-related, or organic disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Panic disorder or acute anxiety","Psychotic disorder with somatic delusion","Body dysmorphic disorder","Substance intoxication or withdrawal","Depressive disorder with somatic preoccupation","Genuine urological or endocrine pathology","Temporal lobe epilepsy or organic brain disease in secondary koro"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful history including cultural context and precipitants","Mental state examination for psychosis and mood disorder","Physical and genital examination to exclude organic pathology and to reassure","Substance and toxicology screen","Endocrine and metabolic bloods if an organic cause is suspected","Neuroimaging or EEG only where secondary organic koro is suspected","Collateral and cultural-informant history"],"redFlags":["Self-inflicted genital injury from attempts to prevent retraction","Underlying psychosis, severe depression, or suicidal ideation","Features suggesting organic brain disease in sporadic cases","A genuine urological emergency mislabelled as koro"],"prognosis":"Epidemic, culturally sanctioned koro typically resolves rapidly with reassurance and abatement of the outbreak, whereas secondary koro follows the course of the underlying psychiatric or organic disorder.","pitfalls":["Dismissing distress as merely cultural and failing to treat comorbid anxiety or psychosis","Missing self-inflicted genital trauma","Overlooking a secondary organic or psychotic cause in sporadic cases","Imposing a purely Western delusional framework on a culturally normative fear"],"management":["Reassurance and culturally sensitive education about the benign, self-limiting nature of the overvalued fear as the mainstay in the epidemic form","Physical and genital examination to demonstrate normality and reassure, while gently discouraging harmful restraining measures such as clamps or string","Short-term anxiolytics such as benzodiazepines for the acute autonomic panic","Identify and treat secondary koro, with antipsychotics for psychosis, antidepressants for mood disorder, and treatment of any organic or substance-related cause","Psychological therapy, including cognitive and supportive approaches, for persistent cases","Community-level reassurance and public health measures to contain epidemic outbreaks","Psychiatric referral where symptoms are secondary to an underlying disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan and Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","New Oxford Textbook of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR (Cultural concepts of distress)"],"crossLinks":[]},"krabbe_disease":{"name":"Krabbe disease","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["globoid cell leukodystrophy","galactosylceramidase deficiency","galactocerebrosidase deficiency","GALC deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard metabolic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive leukodystrophy caused by deficiency of the enzyme galactocerebrosidase (galactosylceramidase), leading to demyelination and formation of characteristic multinucleated globoid cells","The classic early-infantile form presents in the first months with irritability, feeding difficulty, stiffness, and hyperirritability, followed by rapid neurodegeneration","Marked irritability, hypertonicity, and exaggerated startle to stimuli are prominent early features","Accumulation of the toxic metabolite psychosine (galactosylsphingosine) is central to the destruction of myelin-forming cells","Peripheral neuropathy with slowed nerve conduction and raised cerebrospinal fluid protein commonly accompany the central disease","Later-onset (late-infantile, juvenile, and adult) forms present with more insidious motor, visual, and cognitive decline","MRI shows white matter change, and globoid cells are a defining pathological hallmark","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in presymptomatic infants can alter the course, while symptomatic infantile disease responds poorly"],"pathophysiology":"Krabbe disease is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in GALC, encoding the lysosomal enzyme galactocerebrosidase. Deficient activity impairs degradation of galactolipids found in myelin, notably galactosylceramide, and leads to accumulation of the cytotoxic metabolite psychosine (galactosylsphingosine). Psychosine is toxic to oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells, causing widespread loss of myelin-forming cells and demyelination of the central and peripheral nervous system. The pathological response includes infiltration by characteristic multinucleated macrophages (globoid cells), giving the alternative name globoid cell leukodystrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Metachromatic leukodystrophy","Other leukodystrophies including X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy","GM1 and GM2 gangliosidoses","Non-accidental injury or other causes of infantile irritability and hypertonia","Acquired encephalopathies of infancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assay of galactocerebrosidase enzyme activity, including newborn screening in some programmes","GALC molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Psychosine measurement to support diagnosis and assess severity","Brain MRI to characterise white matter involvement","Nerve conduction studies and cerebrospinal fluid protein assessment"],"redFlags":["Extreme irritability with hypertonia and feeding failure in early infancy","Rapid neurodevelopmental regression","Emerging seizures or loss of vision","Bulbar dysfunction with aspiration risk"],"prognosis":"The early-infantile form is rapidly progressive and usually fatal within the first few years of life. Later-onset forms progress more slowly and variably. Stem cell transplantation offers benefit mainly when performed in presymptomatic infants, and once symptoms are established the outlook remains poor.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early extreme irritability and stiffness for colic, reflux, or a behavioural problem","Confusing it with metachromatic leukodystrophy without appropriate enzyme testing","Missing the narrow presymptomatic window for transplantation","Overlooking peripheral neuropathy as a supportive diagnostic clue"],"management":["Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the only disease-modifying option, of benefit mainly in presymptomatic infants identified on newborn screening or in early later-onset disease","Antispasticity agents such as baclofen and measures to control the marked irritability","Antiseizure medication for seizures","Nutritional support and feeding management, including gastrostomy, for feeding difficulty","Respiratory care with secretion management and physiotherapy for tone and contractures","Occupational therapy and communication aids for slower later-onset forms","Genetic counselling and family testing, reflecting autosomal recessive GALC inheritance","Multidisciplinary palliative and supportive care given the progressive, often fatal course"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"krukenberg_tumor":{"name":"Krukenberg tumour","system":"Gynaecology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["metastatic signet-ring cell carcinoma of ovary","ovarian metastasis from gastrointestinal primary"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A metastatic ovarian tumour defined by mucin-filled signet-ring adenocarcinoma cells within a reactive ovarian stroma, not a primary ovarian cancer","The primary site is most often the stomach, followed by colorectum, appendix, breast, and other gastrointestinal sources","Characteristically bilateral and solid, which helps distinguish it from most primary epithelial ovarian tumours","May present before the primary is recognised, with abdominal distension, ascites, or a pelvic mass in a relatively young woman","Occasionally the stroma becomes luteinised and secretes oestrogen or androgen, producing abnormal bleeding or virilisation","Signet-ring morphology reflects intracytoplasmic mucin that displaces the nucleus to the periphery of the cell","Management centres on identifying and treating the primary malignancy; ovarian surgery is largely palliative or diagnostic"],"pathophysiology":"A Krukenberg tumour represents ovarian metastasis of a mucin-producing signet-ring cell adenocarcinoma, classically from the stomach. Tumour cells are thought to reach the ovaries chiefly by lymphatic and haematogenous spread, and possibly transcoelomic seeding, where they proliferate within and stimulate the ovarian stroma. The reactive proliferation of ovarian stromal cells, sometimes with luteinisation, is a defining histological feature and can drive hormone production. Because the ovaries provide a receptive stromal environment, metastatic deposits often grow into sizeable bilateral masses that may dominate the clinical picture even when the primary lesion is small or clinically silent.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary ovarian mucinous carcinoma","Primary ovarian signet-ring stromal tumour","Bilateral primary epithelial ovarian carcinoma","Other ovarian metastases (colorectal, breast, appendiceal)","Ovarian lymphoma","Sclerosing stromal tumour with a reactive appearance"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal and abdominal ultrasound demonstrating bilateral solid ovarian masses","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) to assess the abdomen and identify a primary source","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and colonoscopy to locate a gastric or colorectal primary","Tumour markers including CA125, CEA, and CA19-9 for baseline and monitoring","Immunohistochemistry (e.g. CK7, CK20, CDX2) to establish the site of origin","Mammography or breast imaging when a breast primary is suspected"],"redFlags":["Bilateral solid ovarian masses in a woman with weight loss or gastrointestinal symptoms","Ascites and rapid clinical deterioration suggesting disseminated malignancy","New virilisation or abnormal uterine bleeding from stromal hormone production","Evidence of an occult gastric or colorectal primary on investigation"],"prognosis":"Generally poor, as the tumour signifies disseminated metastatic carcinoma; survival is usually measured in months and depends on the primary tumour and the extent of spread.","pitfalls":["Mistaking bilateral ovarian masses for a primary ovarian cancer and omitting a search for the primary","Overlooking a small or asymptomatic gastric primary because the ovarian disease dominates","Failing to use immunohistochemistry to confirm the metastatic nature and origin","Attributing hormonal symptoms to a benign functioning tumour rather than luteinised metastatic stroma"],"management":["Identify and treat the primary malignancy, directing systemic chemotherapy at the source, most often a gastric or colorectal signet-ring adenocarcinoma","Surgical cytoreduction with bilateral oophorectomy in selected patients for symptom control and resection of ovarian deposits","Palliative management of ascites, bowel obstruction and pain in advanced disease","Monitoring of tumour markers such as CA125, CEA and CA19-9 to assess treatment response","Nutritional support and management of cancer-related complications","Multidisciplinary oncology decision-making across gynaecological, upper gastrointestinal and colorectal teams","Palliative and hospice care with clear prognostic discussion, given the generally poor outcome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","Blaustein's Pathology of the Female Genital Tract"],"crossLinks":[]},"krukenberg_tumour":{"name":"krukenberg tumour","system":"reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Krukenberg tumor","metastatic signet-ring cell carcinoma of ovary"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours; Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A Krukenberg tumour is a metastatic ovarian carcinoma characterised by mucin-filled signet-ring cells set in a cellular, often sarcomatoid-appearing ovarian stroma, and by definition is a secondary rather than a primary ovarian tumour.","The stomach is the commonest primary site, followed by the colon, appendix, breast and other gastrointestinal sources; the gallbladder, biliary tree and cervix are less frequent origins.","Ovarian involvement is bilateral in the large majority of cases, an important clue that an apparently ovarian mass is in fact metastatic.","It often affects women younger than those with primary ovarian carcinoma, and may occasionally present before the primary tumour is recognised.","The stroma can be hormonally active, producing oestrogen or androgen, so patients may present with abnormal uterine bleeding or virilisation in addition to a mass.","Immunohistochemistry helps confirm a gastrointestinal origin, with signet-ring cells typically expressing cytokeratin 20 and CDX2 and often being negative or only focally positive for cytokeratin 7 and characteristically negative for markers of primary ovarian epithelium.","Whenever bilateral solid ovarian masses with signet-ring morphology are found, a diligent search for an occult gastrointestinal or breast primary, including upper and lower endoscopy, is mandatory.","Management is directed at the underlying disseminated malignancy; oophorectomy is usually palliative and the overall outlook is poor."],"pathophysiology":"The Krukenberg tumour represents metastatic spread to the ovary of a mucin-producing adenocarcinoma, most often gastric, in which malignant epithelial cells accumulate intracytoplasmic mucin that displaces the nucleus to give the signet-ring appearance. Metastasis is thought to occur predominantly by lymphatic and haematogenous routes, and possibly transcoelomic spread, which helps explain the frequent bilaterality and the involvement of ovaries even without gross peritoneal disease. A striking feature is the reactive proliferation of ovarian stroma around the infiltrating tumour cells; this stroma may undergo luteinisation and secrete steroid hormones, producing oestrogenic or androgenic effects. Because the ovary provides a hormonally responsive, vascular environment, it is a favoured site for such metastases, particularly in premenopausal women.","additionalDifferentials":["primary ovarian mucinous carcinoma","primary signet-ring stromal tumour of the ovary","other metastatic carcinomas to the ovary (colorectal, breast, pancreatobiliary)","primary ovarian carcinoid or goblet cell adenocarcinoma of appendiceal origin","sclerosing stromal tumour","sex-cord stromal tumour with luteinised stroma","lymphoma involving the ovary"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with biopsy","colonoscopy and assessment of the appendix","bilateral breast examination and mammography","CT of chest, abdomen and pelvis to identify the primary and disease extent","immunohistochemistry panel including CK7, CK20, CDX2 and ovarian markers","serum tumour markers such as CA19-9, CEA and CA125","pelvic ultrasound demonstrating bilateral solid adnexal masses"],"redFlags":["bilateral solid ovarian masses, which strongly suggest metastatic rather than primary disease","signet-ring cell histology on ovarian sampling","new virilisation or abnormal uterine bleeding from hormonally active stroma","weight loss, dyspepsia or gastrointestinal bleeding pointing to a gastric or colonic primary","widespread peritoneal disease and ascites indicating advanced malignancy"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally poor because the tumour reflects disseminated metastatic carcinoma, and survival is measured in months for most patients, being influenced chiefly by the site, stage and treatability of the primary tumour.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a Krukenberg tumour for a primary ovarian mucinous carcinoma and failing to search for the true primary.","Stopping the work-up after ovarian surgery without endoscopic evaluation of the stomach and bowel.","Overlooking a small or early gastric primary that may be clinically silent when the ovarian metastasis presents first.","Misattributing virilisation or bleeding to a primary sex-cord tumour rather than hormonally active metastatic stroma.","Assuming unilateral disease excludes a Krukenberg tumour, since a minority are unilateral."],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying primary adenocarcinoma (most often gastric) with systemic chemotherapy appropriate to that primary site, such as fluoropyrimidine- and platinum-based combinations for a gastric origin","Consider palliative cytoreductive surgery with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy where the ovarian masses cause pain, obstruction or hormonal symptoms and the primary is controlled","Treat hormonally-active stromal effects such as abnormal uterine bleeding or virilisation and provide symptom control including analgesia and management of ascites","Coordinate multidisciplinary oncology input across upper gastrointestinal, gynaecological oncology and medical oncology teams to define resectability and systemic options","Undertake serial cross-sectional imaging and tumour markers (CA19-9, CEA, CA125) to track response and progression","Integrate palliative and supportive care early given the generally poor prognosis, with attention to nutrition and relief of gastric outlet or bowel obstruction","Escalate for management of complications such as bowel obstruction, symptomatic ascites or venous thromboembolism"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours, current edition","Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, current edition","Blaustein's Pathology of the Female Genital Tract, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"kufor_rakeb_syndrome":{"name":"Kufor-Rakeb syndrome","system":"Neurology / Neurodegeneration","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Kufor-Rakeb disease","PARK9","ATP13A2-related parkinsonism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurology and neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive juvenile or young-adult-onset parkinsonism caused by ATP13A2 (PARK9) variants, first described in a consanguineous family from the Kufor-Rakeb region of Jordan.","The signature is levodopa-responsive parkinsonism combined with pyramidal (spastic) signs, marking it as an atypical parkinsonism rather than classic young-onset Parkinson disease.","Supranuclear vertical gaze palsy with slow saccades is characteristic.","Facial-faucial-finger mini-myoclonus (fine myoclonic twitching) is a distinctive sign; oculogyric dystonic spasms and visual hallucinations also occur.","Despite a good initial response, levodopa-induced dyskinesias and motor fluctuations tend to appear early.","Progressive cognitive decline leading to dementia, together with psychiatric symptoms, is typical.","ATP13A2 encodes a lysosomal P5-type transport ATPase, so this is fundamentally a lysosomal storage/handling disorder; allelic variants also cause a form of neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis.","MRI often shows diffuse cerebral and cerebellar atrophy, and basal ganglia iron accumulation may be seen, placing the syndrome within the NBIA spectrum."],"pathophysiology":"ATP13A2, on chromosome 1p36, encodes a lysosomal type 5 P-type transport ATPase that handles cations such as zinc and manganese and transports polyamines, supporting lysosomal acidification and the autophagy-lysosome degradation pathway. Loss-of-function variants impair lysosomal proteolysis and autophagic clearance, so that alpha-synuclein and other substrates accumulate, mitochondrial function is compromised, and cellular handling of divalent metals (manganese, zinc and iron) becomes dysregulated. The resulting neuronal loss is prominent in the substantia nigra, producing levodopa-responsive parkinsonism, but extends widely to cause pyramidal signs, supranuclear gaze impairment, myoclonus and dementia. The overlap of lysosomal dysfunction with iron dyshomeostasis explains why the disorder sits at the intersection of the parkinsonism, NBIA and neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis spectra, and inheritance is autosomal recessive, often in consanguineous families.","additionalDifferentials":["Young-onset/juvenile Parkinson disease (PRKN, PINK1, DJ-1)","Other NBIA subtypes","Wilson disease","Niemann-Pick disease type C (young-onset extrapyramidal features with vertical gaze palsy)","Dopa-responsive dystonia","Progressive supranuclear palsy (for the gaze palsy)","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI with iron-sensitive sequences and volumetric assessment to demonstrate atrophy and basal ganglia iron","ATP13A2 testing within a parkinsonism/NBIA gene panel","Serum caeruloplasmin, copper studies and slit-lamp examination for Kayser-Fleischer rings to exclude Wilson disease","Dopamine transporter (DAT) imaging showing a presynaptic dopaminergic deficit","Oculomotor assessment for supranuclear gaze palsy and saccadic slowing","Specialist metabolic testing to exclude Niemann-Pick disease type C, and neuropsychological assessment"],"redFlags":["Rapid cognitive and motor deterioration in a young person with parkinsonism","Severe or disabling levodopa-induced dyskinesias","Recurrent falls from combined parkinsonism, spasticity and gaze palsy","Emergent psychosis or visual hallucinations"],"prognosis":"The syndrome is progressive, with severe combined disability from parkinsonism, spasticity and dementia typically developing within roughly a decade of onset. Levodopa helps early on, but treatment is limited by early dyskinesias.","pitfalls":["Labelling it as ordinary young-onset Parkinson disease and missing the pyramidal signs, supranuclear gaze palsy and mini-myoclonus that mark an atypical parkinsonism.","Failing to exclude Wilson disease and Niemann-Pick type C, which share young-onset extrapyramidal features and vertical gaze abnormalities.","Overlooking the lysosomal/NBIA and neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis overlap and its implications for targeted testing.","Attributing the early dyskinesias to overtreatment rather than to the underlying disorder."],"management":["Initiate levodopa combined with a dopa-decarboxylase inhibitor as first-line therapy for the levodopa-responsive parkinsonism","Anticipate and manage early levodopa-induced dyskinesias and motor fluctuations by adjusting the regimen and considering amantadine or adjunctive dopaminergic agents","Provide symptomatic treatment for spasticity with agents such as baclofen and for myoclonus and dystonic or oculogyric spasms","Manage neuropsychiatric and cognitive features with cognitive support and cautious pharmacotherapy for hallucinations and dementia, avoiding drugs that worsen parkinsonism","Deliver multidisciplinary rehabilitation with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech and language therapy and falls prevention","Offer genetic counselling and consider testing at-risk relatives given autosomal recessive inheritance","Monitor disease progression with attention to swallowing, mobility and safety, escalating supportive care as combined disability advances"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Movement disorders neurology references","Neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation (NBIA) clinical references","Neurogenetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"kwashiorkor":{"name":"Kwashiorkor","system":"Nutrition / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oedematous malnutrition","protein-energy malnutrition (oedematous)","protein malnutrition"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO nutrition guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The defining feature is bilateral pitting oedema, which can mask underlying wasting and lead to underestimation of the severity of malnutrition","Other signs include a distended abdomen with fatty liver, sparse depigmented hair, flaky-paint dermatosis, apathy, and irritability","It typically affects young children after weaning onto a starchy, protein-poor diet, often precipitated by an intercurrent infection","It remains relevant in parts of India and other low-income settings, particularly in food-insecure and displaced populations","Affected children are immunodeficient, so infection is common, frequently without fever, and is a leading cause of death","Feeding must follow a cautious, staged protocol because rapid refeeding risks fatal metabolic and cardiac complications","Hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, dehydration, and electrolyte disturbance must be actively sought and corrected"],"pathophysiology":"Kwashiorkor is a form of severe acute malnutrition in which protein deficiency is relatively greater than energy deficiency. Reduced hepatic synthesis of albumin lowers plasma oncotic pressure, and this, together with increased capillary permeability, sodium retention, and oxidative stress, produces the characteristic oedema. Fatty infiltration of the liver reflects impaired lipoprotein export. Loss of immune competence, gut mucosal atrophy, and micronutrient deficiencies compound the picture, and metabolic adaptation to starvation makes the child vulnerable to hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, and dangerous fluid and electrolyte shifts during treatment.","additionalDifferentials":["Marasmus and marasmic-kwashiorkor","Nephrotic syndrome causing oedema and hypoalbuminaemia","Protein-losing enteropathy","Chronic liver disease with ascites and oedema","Congestive cardiac failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anthropometry with weight-for-height and mid-upper-arm circumference, and assessment for oedema","Blood glucose to detect hypoglycaemia and temperature to detect hypothermia","Electrolytes including potassium, magnesium, and phosphate","Screening for infection, which is often occult","Assessment for micronutrient deficiencies and anaemia"],"redFlags":["Hypoglycaemia and hypothermia, which are life-threatening in severe malnutrition","Signs of sepsis, which may present without fever","Circulatory or cardiac failure and danger of fluid overload with rehydration","Refeeding-related electrolyte shifts during early nutritional rehabilitation"],"prognosis":"With careful staged nutritional rehabilitation and treatment of infection, recovery is possible, but mortality is high when complications such as sepsis, hypoglycaemia, or refeeding are not anticipated and managed.","pitfalls":["Underestimating malnutrition because oedema masks wasting","Refeeding too aggressively and precipitating cardiac failure or refeeding syndrome","Overzealous intravenous rehydration causing fluid overload","Missing occult infection because the usual signs of fever and inflammation are blunted"],"management":["Follow a cautious, staged nutritional rehabilitation protocol, beginning with a low-protein, low-energy stabilisation formula before transitioning to catch-up feeding, to avoid fatal refeeding syndrome","Detect and correct hypoglycaemia and hypothermia promptly with frequent feeds and active warming","Restore electrolytes with potassium and magnesium and give cautious rehydration to avoid fluid overload and cardiac failure","Treat presumed infection empirically with broad-spectrum antibiotics because sepsis is often occult and afebrile","Provide micronutrient replacement including vitamin A, zinc and folate, deferring iron until the recovery phase","Attend to the flaky-paint dermatosis with gentle skin care and manage the oedema","Arrange multidisciplinary follow-up with caregiver education and measures to address the underlying food insecurity","Escalate to inpatient or high-dependency care for signs of shock, worsening oedema or feeding intolerance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO nutrition guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"kyasanur_forest_disease":{"name":"Kyasanur Forest disease","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KFD","monkey fever","Kyasanur forest disease virus infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A tick-borne flaviviral haemorrhagic fever endemic to India, first recognised in the Kyasanur forest of Karnataka and now reported across several Western Ghats states","Transmitted mainly by Haemaphysalis ticks, with monkeys serving as amplifying hosts, so the disease clusters among forest workers, farmers, and people with rural forest exposure","Illness typically begins abruptly with high fever, severe headache, myalgia, and prostration, often with conjunctival injection and gastrointestinal symptoms","A haemorrhagic phase with bleeding from gums, nose, or gut and thrombocytopenia may develop, and a subset of patients experience a biphasic course with a later neurological phase","The second phase, after an afebrile interval, can bring meningoencephalitic features such as headache, tremor, and altered mentation","Laboratory clues include leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and raised transaminases; diagnosis is confirmed by RT-PCR early and serology later, handled with appropriate biosafety","Management is supportive; a vaccine has been used in endemic districts of Karnataka, and tick-bite avoidance is central to prevention"],"pathophysiology":"Kyasanur Forest disease virus is an RNA flavivirus transmitted to humans through the bite of infected hard ticks, with monkeys acting as amplifying hosts during epizootics. After inoculation the virus disseminates and infects multiple organs, producing viraemia with a systemic inflammatory response, increased vascular permeability, and haemorrhagic manifestations together with cytopenias and hepatic involvement. In some patients the virus involves the central nervous system during a second phase, producing meningoencephalitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever","Severe dengue","Leptospirosis","Severe malaria","Scrub typhus and other rickettsial infections","Other viral encephalitides (for the neurological phase)"],"infectionMimics":["Dengue haemorrhagic fever","Leptospirosis","Scrub typhus","Severe malaria","Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR for KFD viral RNA during the early viraemic phase","KFD-specific IgM and IgG serology in the later course","Full blood count, liver function, and coagulation studies to assess severity","CSF examination and neuroimaging if the neurological second phase is suspected","Exclusion of dengue, malaria, leptospirosis, and scrub typhus given the endemic overlap in India"],"redFlags":["Mucosal or gastrointestinal bleeding with progressive thrombocytopenia","Return of fever with neurological features such as altered mentation, tremor, or meningism marking the second phase","Hypotension, dehydration, or signs of multi-organ involvement","Severe hepatic derangement or coagulopathy indicating a complicated course"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover with supportive care, though the illness can be severe with haemorrhagic and neurological complications and a meaningful case-fatality rate; the biphasic form with encephalitis tends to have a more protracted recovery.","pitfalls":["Attributing a forest-exposed febrile patient's illness to dengue or malaria and missing KFD in an endemic Western Ghats setting","Interpreting the afebrile interval as recovery and discharging before the neurological second phase declares itself","Neglecting biosafety precautions when handling blood and specimens from a suspected haemorrhagic fever","Overlooking occupational and vaccination history in patients from endemic districts"],"management":["Provide supportive care as there is no specific antiviral, with careful fluid and electrolyte balance and haemodynamic support","Manage haemorrhagic complications with transfusion of blood components such as platelets and fresh frozen plasma as bleeding or thrombocytopenia dictate","Monitor for the biphasic neurological phase and treat meningoencephalitis supportively, including seizure control","Apply infection-control and biosafety precautions when handling blood and clinical samples","Emphasise prevention through the KFD vaccine in endemic areas, tick-bite avoidance and use of repellents and protective clothing","Exclude and treat co-endemic mimics such as dengue, malaria, leptospirosis and scrub typhus","Escalate to intensive care for severe haemorrhage, shock or encephalitic deterioration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"kyrle_disease":{"name":"kyrle disease","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hyperkeratosis follicularis et parafollicularis in cutem penetrans","Kyrle's disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A perforating dermatosis characterised by large keratotic papules and nodules with a central horny plug, typically on the lower limbs, and often only mildly pruritic compared with other perforating disorders.","Classically described in adults with chronic kidney disease, diabetes mellitus or hepatic dysfunction, overlapping clinically with acquired perforating dermatosis.","Lesions may coalesce into verrucous plaques and characteristically follow a Koebner (isomorphic) distribution along scratch lines.","Histology shows a keratotic plug filling an epidermal invagination with a parakeratotic column and focal perforation through the epidermis, with basophilic connective-tissue debris in the plug.","In classic Kyrle disease the perforating channel is said not to specifically eliminate collagen or elastic fibres, distinguishing it from reactive perforating collagenosis and elastosis perforans serpiginosa, though overlap is common.","Diagnosis rests on correlating the clinical picture (keratotic plugged papules on legs of a patient with renal or metabolic disease) with the perforating histology.","Treatment mirrors other acquired perforating dermatoses: emollients, keratolytics, topical or oral retinoids, phototherapy, and control of the underlying systemic disease.","A rare familial form with childhood onset and no systemic association is described, but most cases are acquired in adulthood."],"pathophysiology":"Kyrle disease is regarded by many authorities as a variant within the acquired perforating dermatosis spectrum. The primary abnormality is thought to be a focal disturbance of epidermal keratinisation producing a keratotic plug and a clone of abnormally differentiating keratinocytes that accelerates upward, breaching the basement membrane and perforating the epidermis. Basophilic degenerate connective-tissue debris accumulates within the plug and is eliminated transepidermally. In acquired cases, metabolic derangements of uraemia, diabetes and hepatic disease, together with microangiopathy and repetitive scratch-induced trauma (Koebnerisation), are believed to trigger the aberrant keratinisation and perforation.","additionalDifferentials":["reactive perforating collagenosis","perforating folliculitis","prurigo nodularis","hypertrophic lichen planus","keratosis pilaris and other follicular keratoses","multiple keratoacanthomas","perforating granuloma annulare"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["skin biopsy of an intact keratotic papule to demonstrate the epidermal invagination, parakeratotic plug and perforation","renal function tests and estimated glomerular filtration rate","fasting glucose and glycated haemoglobin","liver function tests","serum calcium, phosphate and albumin","thyroid function tests","urinalysis for proteinuria"],"redFlags":["presentation without recognised renal, hepatic or metabolic disease, warranting systemic evaluation","features suggesting secondary infection of excoriated lesions","rapid enlargement of a solitary nodule raising concern for keratoacanthoma or squamous cell carcinoma","intractable pruritus indicating uncontrolled uraemia or hyperglycaemia"],"prognosis":"The course is chronic with a tendency to relapse; individual lesions may resolve leaving scars or hyperpigmentation, and overall activity generally parallels control of the underlying systemic disease.","pitfalls":["labelling any leg-predominant perforating eruption as Kyrle disease without histological correlation, given substantial overlap with other perforating dermatoses","overlooking the need to screen for chronic kidney disease, diabetes and hepatic dysfunction","mistaking a coalescent verrucous plaque for a viral wart or squamous neoplasm","sampling a superinfected or excoriated lesion, which distorts the characteristic histology"],"management":["Optimise treatment of the underlying systemic disorder - adequate dialysis and management of chronic kidney disease, tight glycaemic control in diabetes and treatment of hepatic disease - as skin activity parallels this","Apply topical keratolytics such as urea, salicylic acid or topical retinoids together with topical corticosteroids to individual lesions","Control pruritus with emollients and oral antihistamines to interrupt the itch-scratch cycle and reduce Koebnerisation","Consider narrowband ultraviolet B phototherapy for widespread disease","Use systemic retinoids as a second-line option for resistant, extensive lesions","Provide wound care and monitor lesions for secondary infection","Refer to dermatology and coordinate with nephrology or the relevant specialty managing the systemic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology","Andrews' Diseases of the Skin"],"crossLinks":[]},"l_2_hydroxyglutaric_aciduria":{"name":"L-2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria","system":"Metabolic / nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["L-2-HGA","L2HGDH deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive neurometabolic disorder caused by biallelic L2HGDH mutations, encoding the mitochondrial FAD-dependent L-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase.","Brain MRI is often diagnostic: a subcortical (peripheral-to-central) leukoencephalopathy with symmetrical involvement of the dentate nuclei, globus pallidus, putamen and caudate, plus cerebellar atrophy.","Slowly progressive childhood-onset disease with developmental delay, cerebellar ataxia, pyramidal and extrapyramidal signs, epilepsy, macrocephaly and cognitive decline.","There is a markedly increased lifetime risk of brain tumours (malignant gliomas, medulloblastoma, primitive neuroectodermal tumours), so surveillance imaging matters.","Urine organic acids show raised 2-hydroxyglutarate; chiral separation confirms the L-enantiomer and excludes the D and combined forms.","L-2-hydroxyglutarate is a metabolite-repair substrate that, when it accumulates, acts as an oncometabolite inhibiting alpha-ketoglutarate-dependent dioxygenases.","Riboflavin and FAD supplementation have been tried given the FAD-dependent enzyme, but benefit is unproven; genetic confirmation is by L2HGDH sequencing."],"pathophysiology":"L-2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic mutations in L2HGDH, which encodes L-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase, a mitochondrial FAD-linked enzyme that oxidises L-2-hydroxyglutarate back to 2-oxoglutarate (alpha-ketoglutarate). L-2-hydroxyglutarate arises as an error metabolite when malate dehydrogenase and related dehydrogenases act on alpha-ketoglutarate, and L2HGDH normally repairs this by reconverting it. Loss of the enzyme leads to accumulation of L-2-hydroxyglutarate in plasma, cerebrospinal fluid, urine and brain. Like its D-enantiomer, L-2-hydroxyglutarate competitively inhibits alpha-ketoglutarate-dependent dioxygenases, including histone and DNA demethylases and prolyl hydroxylases, disturbing epigenetic and metabolic regulation; this underlies the characteristic leukoencephalopathy and the predisposition to malignant brain tumours.","additionalDifferentials":["D-2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria","Combined D,L-2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria","Other leukoencephalopathies (e.g. Canavan disease, vanishing white matter disease)","Mitochondrial disorders including Kearns-Sayre spectrum","Glutaric aciduria type I (macrocephaly with basal ganglia change)","Other hereditary cerebellar ataxias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine organic acid analysis with chiral separation","Plasma and cerebrospinal fluid 2-hydroxyglutarate quantification","Brain MRI focused on subcortical white matter, dentate nuclei and basal ganglia","L2HGDH molecular testing","Electroencephalography","Surveillance neuroimaging for brain tumour","Neuropsychometric assessment"],"redFlags":["New focal neurological deficit or rapidly progressive symptoms suggesting a brain tumour","Signs of raised intracranial pressure","Status epilepticus","Rapid cognitive or motor regression"],"prognosis":"The disorder is slowly progressive over years to decades with variable disability, and many patients survive into adulthood. The principal serious complication is the substantially increased risk of malignant brain tumours; no curative treatment is available.","pitfalls":["Not performing chiral separation, leading to misclassification of the enantiomer.","Missing the elevated brain-tumour risk and omitting surveillance imaging.","Mistaking the leukoencephalopathy for an acquired demyelinating disorder.","Attributing macrocephaly and basal ganglia signal change solely to glutaric aciduria type I."],"management":["Offer supportive symptomatic care as no curative treatment exists, with a trial of riboflavin given the FAD-dependent nature of the deficient enzyme","Control seizures with appropriate antiseizure medication","Manage cerebellar ataxia, spasticity and extrapyramidal features with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and antispasticity measures","Undertake regular surveillance neuroimaging because of the markedly increased lifetime risk of malignant brain tumours","Provide developmental, educational and multidisciplinary support for cognitive decline and disability","Offer genetic counselling and testing of at-risk relatives given autosomal recessive inheritance","Escalate to neuro-oncology promptly if surveillance imaging or new focal signs suggest tumour development"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited metabolic disease reference texts","Neurometabolic leukoencephalopathy references","Paediatric neurology references"],"crossLinks":[]},"labyrinthine_concussion":{"name":"Labyrinthine concussion","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Otology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["inner ear concussion","cochleovestibular concussion","post-traumatic labyrinthine dysfunction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Follows head trauma, often a temporal-bone or occipital impact, without a visible fracture of the otic capsule","Presents with sensorineural hearing loss, frequently in the higher frequencies, together with tinnitus and vertigo or imbalance beginning soon after the injury","Vertigo may be constant at first and then settle, or may present as positional vertigo if otoconia are dislodged (traumatic BPPV)","The mechanism is an energy or pressure wave transmitted through the skull to the membranous labyrinth and hair cells, causing microscopic damage without frank bony disruption","It is a diagnosis of exclusion - temporal-bone fracture, perilymph fistula, ossicular disruption, and central brain injury must be ruled out","Management is largely supportive, with vestibular rehabilitation for persistent imbalance and repositioning manoeuvres for traumatic BPPV, while hearing loss is often permanent","High-resolution CT of the temporal bone and audiometry are central to distinguishing it from fracture and conductive causes"],"pathophysiology":"A blow to the head transmits kinetic energy through the skull to the fluid-filled inner ear even when the bony otic capsule stays intact. The resulting pressure wave and shearing forces damage the delicate cochlear and vestibular hair cells and their supporting structures, and may dislodge utricular otoconia into the semicircular canals. Cochlear injury produces sensorineural hearing loss, classically affecting higher frequencies, together with tinnitus, while vestibular injury causes vertigo and disequilibrium. Because the damage is at the cellular level rather than a gross fracture, imaging is frequently normal, and the diagnosis rests on the temporal relationship to trauma and on exclusion of other injuries.","additionalDifferentials":["Temporal bone fracture, otic-capsule sparing or violating","Perilymph fistula","Traumatic benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Ossicular chain disruption causing conductive loss","Post-concussion syndrome or central vestibular injury","Tympanic membrane perforation","Acoustic trauma from an associated blast"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure-tone audiometry to document the pattern and degree of hearing loss","High-resolution CT of the temporal bone to exclude fracture","Tympanometry to assess middle-ear and ossicular status","Videonystagmography with positional testing","Vestibular-evoked myogenic potentials","MRI of the brain if central injury is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive or fluctuating hearing loss suggesting a perilymph fistula","Otorrhoea or clear fluid leak raising concern for a CSF fistula","Facial nerve weakness","Focal neurological deficit or deteriorating conscious level","Vertigo accompanied by severe or worsening headache"],"prognosis":"Vertigo and imbalance often improve over weeks to months through central compensation and vestibular rehabilitation, but sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus are frequently permanent.","pitfalls":["Attributing all post-traumatic dizziness to concussion and missing a perilymph fistula or temporal-bone fracture","Overlooking traumatic BPPV, which is readily treated with repositioning manoeuvres","Failing to obtain baseline audiometry, which is important both clinically and medicolegally after head injury","Missing a co-existing CSF leak or facial nerve injury"],"management":["Manage supportively as there is no specific pharmacological cure, using vestibular suppressants such as prochlorperazine or an antihistamine only briefly for severe acute vertigo to avoid delaying central compensation","Refer for vestibular rehabilitation exercises to promote central compensation for persistent imbalance","Perform canalith repositioning manoeuvres such as the Epley if traumatic benign paroxysmal positional vertigo develops from dislodged otoconia","Provide audiological rehabilitation with hearing aids for persistent sensorineural hearing loss and offer tinnitus management strategies","Exclude and treat associated injuries such as temporal-bone fracture, perilymph fistula, ossicular disruption or central brain injury","Give falls-prevention and safety advice during the recovery period","Coordinate ENT, audiology and vestibular therapy follow-up to monitor recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"lactate_dehydrogenase_deficiency":{"name":"Lactate dehydrogenase deficiency","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LDH deficiency","LDH-A subunit deficiency","LDH-B subunit deficiency","LDHA gene","LDHB gene"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A recessive enzyme defect that, unusually among glycolytic disorders, characteristically does not cause haemolytic anaemia","Deficiency of the muscle (M/A) subunit produces exercise intolerance and exertional myoglobinuria, resembling a glycogenoses-type myopathy","A dermatological phenotype with erythematosquamous skin lesions has been described with M-subunit deficiency","The subunit composition differs between tissues, so red cells tolerate the defect while muscle does not, explaining the paradoxical absence of haemolysis","A characteristic laboratory clue is a very low or absent serum LDH activity, which may be an incidental finding on routine biochemistry","Diagnosis is confirmed by isoenzyme analysis and enzyme assay, with LDHA or LDHB mutation testing","Management centres on avoiding the strenuous exertion that provokes rhabdomyolysis; specific treatment for the red cell is not required as haemolysis is absent"],"pathophysiology":"Lactate dehydrogenase interconverts pyruvate and lactate, regenerating the NAD+ needed to sustain anaerobic glycolysis under conditions of high energy demand. The enzyme is a tetramer of M (A) and H (B) subunits combined in tissue-specific ratios. In the red cell, alternative routes for NAD+ regeneration mean that deficiency does not appreciably shorten survival, so haemolysis is characteristically absent; in skeletal muscle, however, loss of the M subunit impairs anaerobic energy supply during exertion and predisposes to cramps, exercise intolerance and rhabdomyolysis.","additionalDifferentials":["McArdle disease and other glycogen storage myopathies","Phosphofructokinase deficiency","Phosphoglycerate mutase deficiency","Fatty-acid oxidation disorders causing exertional rhabdomyolysis","Statin- or drug-induced myopathy","Analytical interference causing a spuriously low LDH"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and red-cell lactate dehydrogenase activity with isoenzyme analysis","Forearm ischaemic exercise test assessing the lactate response","Creatine kinase and myoglobin during exertional symptoms","Full blood count and blood film to confirm the absence of haemolysis","Skin assessment for the associated erythematosquamous lesions","Molecular testing of the LDHA or LDHB genes"],"redFlags":["Exertional rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria and risk of acute kidney injury","Recurrent disabling muscle cramps limiting activity","Extensive or ulcerating skin lesions requiring dermatological input","Pregnancy complications reported in M-subunit deficiency"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally good; because haemolysis is absent, morbidity relates to muscle symptoms and skin involvement, which are usually manageable with avoidance of heavy exertion.","pitfalls":["Expecting a haemolytic anaemia and being misled when it is absent","Dismissing an incidentally low serum LDH as artefact without considering the diagnosis","Not testing the lactate response to exercise in a patient with exertional symptoms","Overlooking the associated skin manifestations"],"management":["There is no specific drug therapy; the mainstay is avoidance of strenuous, high-intensity anaerobic exertion to prevent exertional cramps and rhabdomyolysis","Advise pre-exercise carbohydrate intake and a graded, moderate activity pattern","Treat episodes of exertional rhabdomyolysis with prompt fluid resuscitation and monitoring for myoglobinuric renal injury","Manage the associated erythematosquamous skin lesions with dermatological care","Note the association of muscle-subunit deficiency with pregnancy complications and provide obstetric awareness where relevant","Offer genetic counselling and family testing given autosomal recessive inheritance, and flag the characteristically low serum lactate dehydrogenase to prevent misinterpretation","Escalate to hospital care during severe rhabdomyolysis to protect renal function"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Wintrobe's Clinical Hematology"],"crossLinks":[]},"lactational_mastitis":{"name":"Lactational Mastitis","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lactational mastitis","puerperal mastitis","breastfeeding mastitis","postpartum mastitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lactational mastitis is an inflammatory, usually infective, condition of the breast in a breastfeeding woman, presenting with a tender, hot, erythematous, wedge-shaped area often accompanied by fever and flu-like malaise","It typically begins with milk stasis from ineffective or infrequent drainage, which then becomes secondarily infected, most often by Staphylococcus aureus entering through a cracked or damaged nipple","Continued effective milk removal is the cornerstone of treatment; breastfeeding from the affected side should continue and is safe for the infant","Supportive measures include frequent feeding or expression, correcting attachment and positioning, warmth before feeds and cold after for comfort, adequate hydration, rest, and simple analgesia","Antibiotics active against Staphylococcus aureus are indicated when symptoms are severe, systemic, or fail to improve within a short period of conservative management, or when a nipple fissure is infected","A localised fluctuant, non-resolving mass suggests a breast abscess, which requires drainage by needle aspiration or incision alongside antibiotics","Failure to improve with appropriate treatment should prompt consideration of an abscess or, in a non-lactating or persistent case, inflammatory breast cancer","Prevention centres on good breastfeeding technique, ensuring full drainage, avoiding abrupt weaning, and prompt attention to nipple trauma","Recurrent mastitis warrants review of feeding technique and evaluation for a persistent focus or underlying breast pathology"],"pathophysiology":"Inadequate or infrequent milk drainage leads to milk stasis and rising intraductal pressure, causing milk to leak into the surrounding stroma and provoke a local inflammatory response. Nipple trauma, commonly from poor latch, provides a portal of entry for skin flora, predominantly Staphylococcus aureus, so the stagnant milk becomes a culture medium and infection is established. If untreated, the localised infection can organise into a walled-off collection of pus, forming a breast abscess. The wedge-shaped distribution reflects involvement of a segment of the ductal-lobular system.","additionalDifferentials":["Breast abscess","Blocked (plugged) duct without infection","Engorgement","Inflammatory breast carcinoma (especially if non-lactating or non-resolving)","Candidal nipple or ductal infection causing pain","Galactocele"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis is usually sufficient; investigations are reserved for atypical or non-resolving cases","Breast milk culture and sensitivity where infection is severe, recurrent, hospital-associated, or unresponsive to first-line antibiotics","Breast ultrasound to identify or exclude an abscess when a fluctuant or non-resolving mass is present","Needle aspiration of a suspected abscess for both diagnosis and drainage with culture of the aspirate","Consideration of biopsy and specialist referral if inflammatory changes fail to resolve, to exclude malignancy"],"redFlags":["A fluctuant or non-resolving mass indicating breast abscess requiring drainage","Systemic sepsis with high fever, rigors, tachycardia, or hypotension","Failure to respond to appropriate antibiotics and milk drainage within a few days","Persistent inflammatory changes, skin thickening, or peau d'orange raising concern for inflammatory breast cancer","Overlying skin breakdown or rapidly spreading erythema"],"prognosis":"Most cases resolve quickly with effective milk removal and, when needed, appropriate antibiotics, and breastfeeding can continue throughout. Delayed or inadequate treatment increases the risk of abscess formation, which then requires drainage. Recurrence is uncommon when feeding technique is optimised, and the majority of women recover fully without lasting effect on lactation.","pitfalls":["Advising cessation of breastfeeding, which worsens stasis and prolongs the illness","Failing to recognise and drain a breast abscess, treating with antibiotics alone","Overlooking inflammatory breast cancer in non-lactating or persistent presentations","Not addressing the underlying poor attachment or drainage, leading to recurrence","Delaying antibiotics when systemic or severe features are present"],"management":["Prioritise effective milk removal - continue breastfeeding from the affected breast, feed or express frequently, and correct attachment and positioning","Add supportive measures: warmth before feeds, cold compresses afterwards, simple analgesia such as paracetamol or ibuprofen, rest and adequate hydration","Prescribe antibiotics active against Staphylococcus aureus, such as flucloxacillin or a macrolide or clindamycin if penicillin-allergic, when symptoms are severe, systemic, associated with an infected fissure or failing to improve with conservative care","Investigate a fluctuant or non-resolving mass with ultrasound and drain a confirmed abscess by needle aspiration or incision, sending pus for culture","Send breast-milk culture in severe, recurrent, hospital-associated or unresponsive infection to guide antibiotic choice","Provide lactation-consultant support and refer for biopsy to exclude inflammatory carcinoma if inflammation does not resolve","Safety-net with clear advice to return if symptoms worsen or a collection develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"lafora_disease":{"name":"Lafora disease (progressive myoclonic epilepsy, EPM2A/NHLRC1)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lafora body disease","EPM2A","NHLRC1","progressive myoclonic epilepsy type 2","malin/laforin deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lafora disease is a severe autosomal recessive progressive myoclonic epilepsy caused by biallelic variants in EPM2A (laforin) or NHLRC1 (malin)","It typically begins in previously healthy adolescents with myoclonus, occipital and generalised seizures including visual seizures, and progressive cognitive decline","The hallmark is relentless deterioration into dementia, worsening intractable myoclonus, and dependency, unlike the milder course of Unverricht-Lundborg disease","Diagnosis is confirmed by molecular genetic testing; historically periodic acid-Schiff-positive Lafora bodies (polyglucosan inclusions) are demonstrated in skin (eccrine duct) or other tissues","EEG shows progressive background slowing with generalised and occipital epileptiform discharges and photosensitivity","Treatment is symptomatic with anti-myoclonic and antiseizure medication; sodium-channel-blocking agents can worsen myoclonus and should generally be avoided","Genetic counselling and early palliative planning are important given the poor prognosis"],"pathophysiology":"Lafora disease results from defective regulation of glycogen synthesis. Laforin (a glycogen phosphatase) and malin (an E3 ubiquitin ligase) normally act together to control glycogen branching and chain length. Loss of either protein leads to synthesis of abnormally long, poorly branched, and hyperphosphorylated glycogen that is insoluble and precipitates as polyglucosan Lafora bodies. These inclusions accumulate in neurones and other tissues, driving neuronal dysfunction, neuroinflammation, and progressive neurodegeneration that manifests as the characteristic progressive myoclonic epilepsy and dementia.","additionalDifferentials":["Unverricht-Lundborg disease (EPM1)","Mitochondrial disease including MERRF","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses","Sialidosis (cherry-red spot myoclonus syndrome)","Juvenile Huntington disease","Dentatorubral-pallidoluysian atrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of EPM2A and NHLRC1","EEG to document background slowing, generalised and occipital discharges, and photosensitivity","Skin biopsy for periodic acid-Schiff-positive Lafora bodies when genetics are inconclusive","Brain MRI to exclude structural causes and assess for atrophy","Metabolic and mitochondrial screening to exclude other progressive myoclonic epilepsies"],"redFlags":["Rapidly worsening myoclonus and seizures becoming refractory to treatment","Progressive dementia and loss of independence in a previously healthy teenager","Aspiration, immobility, or status epilepticus signalling advanced disease","Deterioration after introduction of sodium-channel-blocking antiseizure drugs"],"prognosis":"Lafora disease is relentlessly progressive, with escalating myoclonus, refractory seizures, and dementia leading to profound disability and death typically within about a decade of onset.","pitfalls":["Confusing Lafora disease with the more treatable Unverricht-Lundborg disease, giving falsely reassuring prognosis","Prescribing sodium-channel-blocking agents that aggravate myoclonus","Attributing early cognitive decline to psychiatric illness or medication","Delaying genetic confirmation and counselling in a suspected progressive myoclonic epilepsy"],"management":["Treat symptomatically with antiseizure medications effective against myoclonus and generalised seizures, such as valproate, levetiracetam, clonazepam and add-on perampanel, brivaracetam or zonisamide","Avoid sodium-channel-blocking agents such as phenytoin, carbamazepine and lamotrigine, which typically aggravate the myoclonus","Manage acute seizure clusters and status epilepticus with benzodiazepine rescue therapy and an emergency seizure plan","Provide supportive care for progressive disability including physiotherapy, nutritional support and gastrostomy feeding for dysphagia","Coordinate multidisciplinary and palliative input across neurology, dietetics, psychology and community services","Offer genetic counselling and testing of at-risk relatives given autosomal recessive inheritance","Plan proactively for advancing dementia and dependency, escalating to end-of-life care as the disease progresses"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Progressive Myoclonus Epilepsy, Lafora Type","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"lagophthalmos":{"name":"Lagophthalmos","system":"Eye / Orbit and adnexa","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["incomplete eyelid closure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ophthalmology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Incomplete closure of the eyelids, leaving the ocular surface exposed and at risk of drying","The commonest cause is facial (seventh nerve) palsy weakening the orbicularis oculi","Nocturnal lagophthalmos, exposure from proptosis or lid retraction, and cicatricial lid shortening are other causes","A protective upward rotation of the globe on attempted closure (Bell phenomenon) helps guard the cornea","Exposure keratopathy, ranging from punctate staining to ulceration, is the main threat to sight","Management centres on intensive ocular surface lubrication and protection, with surgical measures for persistent cases"],"pathophysiology":"Lagophthalmos occurs when the eyelids cannot fully appose, whether from weakness of the orbicularis oculi (as in facial nerve palsy), mechanical factors such as proptosis or lid retraction, or cicatricial shortening of the lids. Failure of the normal blink and closure leaves the interpalpebral cornea and conjunctiva exposed, so the tear film evaporates and the surface dries. Without an effective Bell phenomenon or protective measures, exposure leads to epithelial breakdown, keratopathy and potential ulceration or perforation.","additionalDifferentials":["Facial nerve palsy","Thyroid eye disease with proptosis or retraction","Cicatricial eyelid shortening","Proptosis from orbital disease","Nocturnal lagophthalmos","Floppy eyelid syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Measurement of the closure gap and assessment of blink","Corneal examination with fluorescein for exposure keratopathy","Assessment of the Bell phenomenon and corneal sensation","Evaluation for facial nerve palsy or orbital cause","Thyroid assessment where relevant"],"redFlags":["Corneal ulceration or thinning","Reduced corneal sensation compounding exposure risk (neurotrophic component)","Absent Bell phenomenon","Progressive or severe exposure with visual loss"],"prognosis":"With diligent lubrication and protection the cornea can be preserved, and many neurogenic cases improve as the palsy recovers; persistent or severe exposure may need surgical lid procedures to protect sight.","pitfalls":["Underestimating exposure risk when corneal sensation is also reduced","Relying on infrequent lubrication in significant lagophthalmos","Overlooking nocturnal lagophthalmos as a cause of morning irritation","Failing to protect the eye during the recovery phase of facial palsy"],"management":["Intensive ocular surface lubrication is the mainstay - frequent artificial tears through the day and a lubricating ointment at night","Provide mechanical protection with lid taping, a moisture chamber or an overnight eyelid dressing to shield the exposed cornea","Treat the underlying cause, such as managing facial nerve palsy or thyroid eye disease and addressing proptosis or lid retraction","Offer surgical measures for persistent exposure - upper-lid weight implantation, tarsorrhaphy, lower-lid tightening or correction of cicatricial lid shortening","Monitor the cornea with fluorescein for exposure keratopathy and treat secondary microbial keratitis promptly","Refer to ophthalmology or oculoplastics for assessment and surgical planning","Escalate urgently if corneal ulceration or perforation threatens sight"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Oculoplastic surgery references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"laing_distal_myopathy":{"name":"Laing distal myopathy (MYH7)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPD1","Laing early-onset distal myopathy","MYH7-related distal myopathy","myosin heavy chain 7 distal myopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by pathogenic variants in MYH7, the gene encoding slow/beta-cardiac myosin heavy chain, with autosomal dominant inheritance","Typically an early-onset distal myopathy, often beginning in childhood or adolescence, though onset can extend into adulthood","Weakness starts in the anterior compartment of the lower legs, producing foot drop and weakness of great-toe and ankle dorsiflexion, classically with a 'hanging big toe' sign","Finger extensor and neck flexor weakness commonly follow, while progression is slow and many patients remain ambulant for decades","Because MYH7 also causes cardiomyopathy, cardiac surveillance is prudent even though overt cardiac involvement in Laing myopathy is variable","Creatine kinase is normal or only mildly elevated and electromyography shows a myopathic pattern","Muscle biopsy may show fibre-type disproportion, type 1 fibre predominance, and sometimes cores or hyaline bodies, but findings can be non-specific so genetic confirmation is important","Diagnosis rests on the characteristic distal weakness distribution combined with MYH7 genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Laing distal myopathy is caused by dominant variants in MYH7, which encodes the slow/beta-cardiac myosin heavy chain expressed in type 1 skeletal muscle fibres and cardiac muscle. Mutant myosin impairs the assembly and function of the thick filament of the sarcomere, disturbing contraction of predominantly slow-twitch fibres. This selectively affects muscles rich in type 1 fibres, accounting for the early anterior lower-leg and neck flexor involvement, and the shared gene explains the potential for associated cardiac disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease and other causes of distal neurogenic foot drop","Myotonic dystrophy","Other distal myopathies (Udd/tibial, Welander, Markesbery-Griggs)","Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy with foot extensor weakness","Chronic acquired distal neuropathy","Congenital myopathies with distal predominance"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase (usually normal or mildly raised)","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to confirm a myopathic, non-neurogenic process","Muscle biopsy with histochemistry and immunohistochemistry","Muscle MRI to define the pattern of selective muscle involvement","Targeted MYH7 sequencing or a distal myopathy gene panel","Baseline and periodic cardiac evaluation with ECG and echocardiography"],"redFlags":["Symptoms or signs of cardiomyopathy or arrhythmia given the MYH7 association","Rapidly progressive or asymmetric weakness suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Respiratory muscle involvement with reduced vital capacity","Prominent dysphagia or bulbar features atypical for Laing myopathy"],"prognosis":"Progression is typically slow, and most patients retain independent walking well into adulthood, though distal weakness gradually extends more proximally over decades; life expectancy is usually normal unless significant cardiac or respiratory involvement develops.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the foot drop for a peripheral neuropathy and missing the myopathic cause","Assuming normal creatine kinase excludes a myopathy","Over-interpreting non-specific biopsy changes without genetic confirmation","Neglecting cardiac screening given the MYH7 gene's cardiac role"],"management":["Manage supportively as no disease-modifying therapy exists, focusing on preserving mobility and function","Provide physiotherapy and ankle-foot orthoses for foot drop, with occupational therapy and walking aids as needed","Undertake baseline and periodic cardiac surveillance with ECG and echocardiography because MYH7 also causes cardiomyopathy, treating any conduction disease or cardiomyopathy that emerges","Monitor respiratory function and treat ventilatory involvement as weakness progresses","Involve orthopaedics and podiatry for foot deformity and contractures","Coordinate care through a specialist neuromuscular clinic","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for autosomal dominant inheritance, including cardiac evaluation of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: MYH7-related distal myopathy (Laing distal myopathy)"],"crossLinks":[]},"lambert_eaton":{"name":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LEMS","Lambert-Eaton syndrome","myasthenic syndrome of Lambert-Eaton"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A presynaptic disorder of neuromuscular transmission caused by antibodies against voltage-gated calcium channels that reduce acetylcholine release","Presents with proximal limb weakness, especially the legs, together with autonomic features such as dry mouth, constipation, and erectile dysfunction","Strength may transiently improve with sustained or repeated effort (post-exercise facilitation), and depressed reflexes can augment after exercise","Ocular and bulbar symptoms are usually milder than in myasthenia gravis, though some overlap occurs","About half of cases are paraneoplastic, most often linked to small-cell lung cancer, and may precede the tumour diagnosis","Repetitive nerve stimulation shows a low baseline compound muscle action potential with decrement at low frequency and marked increment at high frequency or after exercise","Treatment addresses any underlying tumour and enhances acetylcholine release; immunotherapy is used for symptom control","A validated screening score helps stratify the likelihood of underlying small-cell lung cancer and guides surveillance"],"pathophysiology":"In LEMS, IgG autoantibodies target P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels on the presynaptic motor nerve terminal. Reduced calcium influx during depolarisation diminishes the quantal release of acetylcholine, so the endplate potential often fails to reach threshold at rest, causing weakness. Repeated activity allows calcium to accumulate in the terminal, temporarily augmenting release and improving strength, which explains facilitation. The same antibodies act on autonomic ganglia and terminals, producing cholinergic autonomic failure. In paraneoplastic cases the tumour expresses calcium channels that trigger the cross-reactive immune response.","additionalDifferentials":["Myasthenia gravis","Inflammatory or metabolic myopathy","Motor neuron disease","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy","Botulism (also a presynaptic disorder)","Guillain-Barre syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum antibodies against P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels","Repetitive nerve stimulation at low and high frequency and post-exercise testing","CT of the chest and, if negative, FDG-PET to search for small-cell lung cancer","Interval tumour surveillance imaging when initial screening is negative but risk is high","SOX1 antibodies as a marker supporting an underlying small-cell lung cancer","Single-fibre electromyography in equivocal neuromuscular transmission cases"],"redFlags":["Any new diagnosis should trigger a directed search for occult malignancy, especially small-cell lung cancer","Weight loss, haemoptysis, or a smoking history increasing suspicion of underlying tumour","Progressive bulbar or respiratory weakness requiring monitoring of airway and ventilation","Severe autonomic failure causing symptomatic hypotension or gastrointestinal stasis"],"prognosis":"Symptoms often improve with treatment of any underlying cancer and with therapies that enhance acetylcholine release; the overall outlook is dominated by the associated malignancy when present, whereas non-paraneoplastic disease tends to follow a more indolent, manageable course.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing LEMS as myasthenia gravis and missing the search for lung cancer","Stopping tumour surveillance too early after an initially negative chest scan","Overlooking autonomic symptoms that are an important diagnostic clue","Failing to appreciate post-exercise facilitation on examination and electrophysiology","Attributing proximal weakness to a primary myopathy without neuromuscular junction testing"],"management":["Start symptomatic treatment with amifampridine (3,4-diaminopyridine) to enhance presynaptic acetylcholine release, adding pyridostigmine where helpful","Search for and treat underlying small-cell lung cancer, since successful tumour treatment often improves the neurological syndrome","Use immunotherapy - corticosteroids, azathioprine, intravenous immunoglobulin, plasma exchange or rituximab - for disabling or refractory weakness","Continue interval tumour surveillance with CT and FDG-PET when initial screening is negative but malignancy risk remains high","Provide supportive care including physiotherapy and symptomatic treatment of autonomic features such as dry mouth and constipation","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across neurology, oncology and respiratory medicine","Escalate and monitor ventilation if severe, bulbar or respiratory weakness develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"lambert_eaton_myasthenic_syndrome":{"name":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LEMS","Lambert-Eaton syndrome","myasthenic-myopathic syndrome","Eaton-Lambert syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A presynaptic disorder of the neuromuscular junction causing proximal limb weakness, typically most marked in the lower limbs, with a waddling gait","Strength characteristically shows transient post-exercise facilitation, briefly improving after a few seconds of sustained contraction","Autonomic features such as dry mouth, constipation, and erectile dysfunction are common and help distinguish it from myasthenia gravis","Reflexes are typically reduced or absent but may become detectable immediately after exercise (post-tetanic potentiation)","Approximately half of cases are paraneoplastic, most often associated with small-cell lung cancer, so a tumour search is essential","Repetitive nerve stimulation shows a low baseline compound muscle action potential with a decrement at low rates and marked increment at high rates or after exercise","Management includes tumour treatment where relevant, symptomatic therapy with 3,4-diaminopyridine, and immunotherapy in refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome results from autoantibodies directed against presynaptic voltage-gated P/Q-type calcium channels at the motor nerve terminal and in autonomic nerve endings. Reduced calcium influx during the nerve action potential impairs the quantal release of acetylcholine, weakening neuromuscular transmission and producing muscle weakness and autonomic dysfunction. Because more calcium accumulates in the terminal with repeated rapid firing, sustained or high-frequency activity transiently boosts transmitter release, explaining the characteristic post-exercise facilitation of strength and reflexes. In paraneoplastic cases, small-cell lung cancer expresses the same calcium channels, triggering the cross-reactive antibody response.","additionalDifferentials":["Myasthenia gravis","Inflammatory or metabolic myopathy","Motor neurone disease","Guillain-Barre syndrome and chronic inflammatory neuropathies","Botulism","Paraneoplastic or other autonomic neuropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repetitive nerve stimulation showing low baseline amplitude with high-frequency or post-exercise increment","Serum voltage-gated P/Q-type calcium-channel antibodies","CT chest and FDG-PET to detect small-cell lung cancer, with repeat surveillance if initially negative","Single-fibre electromyography where diagnosis is uncertain","Autonomic function testing where indicated","Screening for associated autoimmune disease"],"redFlags":["Respiratory muscle weakness compromising ventilation","Bulbar involvement affecting swallow and airway protection","Positive calcium-channel antibodies indicating high likelihood of underlying malignancy","Severe autonomic instability","Rapid progression of generalised weakness"],"prognosis":"Symptomatic and immunomodulatory treatment often improves strength, and non-paraneoplastic disease may be well controlled long term; in paraneoplastic cases the neurological syndrome frequently improves with successful tumour treatment, though overall prognosis is dominated by the underlying cancer.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as myasthenia gravis and overlooking the autonomic features and post-exercise facilitation","Failing to search for and re-screen for small-cell lung cancer","Misinterpreting reduced reflexes as a peripheral neuropathy","Overlooking drugs that further impair neuromuscular transmission","Not recognising respiratory involvement early"],"management":["Provide symptomatic therapy with amifampridine to prolong nerve-terminal depolarisation and boost acetylcholine release, with pyridostigmine as an adjunct","Identify and treat an underlying malignancy, most often small-cell lung cancer, as tumour-directed treatment frequently improves strength","Add immunomodulation with corticosteroids and steroid-sparing agents such as azathioprine, or intravenous immunoglobulin and plasma exchange for rapid effect, and rituximab in resistant disease","Repeat surveillance imaging for occult small-cell lung cancer if the initial search is negative in high-risk patients","Offer physiotherapy and manage autonomic dysfunction such as dry mouth, constipation and erectile dysfunction symptomatically","Screen for and manage associated autoimmune disease","Monitor for and escalate care if respiratory or bulbar involvement develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"lamellar_ichthyosis":{"name":"Lamellar ichthyosis","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TGM1","transglutaminase-1 deficiency","autosomal recessive congenital ichthyosis","LI"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of autosomal recessive congenital ichthyosis, most often caused by variants in TGM1 encoding transglutaminase-1, with several other causative genes","Typically presents at birth as a collodion baby encased in a taut, shiny membrane that sheds over the first weeks","Evolves into large, plate-like, dark brown adherent scales with relatively little underlying erythema, contrasting with the erythrodermic phenotype","Ectropion and eclabium are common and cause chronic ocular exposure problems","Scalp scarring alopecia, palmoplantar keratoderma, and reduced sweating with heat intolerance are frequent","Sits on a clinical spectrum with congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma; some patients show overlapping features","Management centres on topical emollients and keratolytics, with oral retinoids for severe disease"],"pathophysiology":"Lamellar ichthyosis most commonly arises from deficiency of transglutaminase-1, an enzyme that cross-links structural proteins to form the cornified cell envelope and covalently attaches lipids to the envelope surface. Loss of enzyme activity prevents formation of a normal cornified envelope and disrupts the lipid scaffold of the stratum corneum, impairing the permeability barrier and desquamation. Compensatory hyperproliferation and retention of corneocytes produce the characteristic thick, plate-like scaling. Other causative genes (including those for lipid transport and processing) can produce overlapping phenotypes within the autosomal recessive congenital ichthyosis spectrum.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma","Harlequin ichthyosis (severe end of spectrum)","X-linked ichthyosis","Netherton syndrome","Ichthyosis vulgaris","Acquired ichthyosis from systemic disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing including TGM1 and other congenital ichthyosis genes","Skin biopsy showing marked orthohyperkeratosis with a normal or thickened granular layer","In vitro transglutaminase-1 activity assay where available","Ophthalmology assessment for ectropion and corneal exposure","Assessment of thermoregulation and hydration, particularly in neonates","Prenatal or carrier genetic testing for at-risk families"],"redFlags":["Neonatal dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, and temperature instability in the collodion phase","Corneal exposure and ulceration from severe ectropion","Heat intolerance and hyperthermia from impaired sweating","Secondary skin infection of fissured skin"],"prognosis":"It is a lifelong disorder; after the vulnerable collodion period most patients stabilise into chronic generalised scaling that requires continuous skin care, with visual and thermoregulatory complications influencing overall morbidity.","pitfalls":["Assuming all collodion babies have lamellar ichthyosis when the membrane can precede several distinct disorders","Overlooking heat intolerance from anhidrosis, risking hyperthermia in warm environments or exercise","Neglecting ophthalmic monitoring for ectropion-related corneal damage","Failing to counsel on the recessive inheritance and recurrence risk"],"management":["Base daily care on liberal emollients and keratolytics such as urea and alpha-hydroxy acids to soften and reduce the plate-like scale","In the collodion neonate, nurse in a humidified incubator with careful fluid and electrolyte balance, emollients and monitoring for sepsis and thermoregulatory instability","Consider systemic retinoids such as acitretin for severe, disabling scaling","Provide ophthalmology care for ectropion and corneal exposure with lubrication and surgical correction where needed","Manage heat intolerance from impaired sweating and remain vigilant for skin and systemic infection","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across dermatology, ophthalmology and ENT with psychological support","Offer genetic counselling with carrier and prenatal testing for at-risk families given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"laminopathy_dilated_cardiomyopathy":{"name":"LMNA laminopathy with dilated cardiomyopathy and muscular dystrophy","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LMNA-related dilated cardiomyopathy","cardiolaminopathy","lamin A/C cardiomyopathy","Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy (LMNA)","limb-girdle muscular dystrophy 1B"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in LMNA, which encodes the nuclear envelope proteins lamin A and C; the same gene underlies a spectrum including dilated cardiomyopathy, Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy and limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 1B","The neuromuscular phenotype often features humeroperoneal or limb-girdle weakness with early, prominent joint contractures (elbows, Achilles tendons and spine) and rigid spine, sometimes preceding overt cardiac symptoms","Cardiac disease characteristically begins with conduction-system involvement, such as atrioventricular block and atrial arrhythmias, and progresses to dilated cardiomyopathy with heart failure","LMNA cardiolaminopathy carries a high risk of malignant ventricular arrhythmia and sudden cardiac death, which can occur even before or out of proportion to left ventricular dysfunction","Because of this arrhythmic risk, guidelines set a lower threshold for implantable cardioverter-defibrillator placement rather than a pacemaker alone, taking account of risk markers such as non-sustained ventricular tachycardia, atrioventricular conduction delay, non-missense variants and male sex","Lifelong cardiac surveillance with ECG, ambulatory monitoring and cardiac imaging (echocardiography and cardiac MRI) is essential, and competitive or intense exertion is generally discouraged","There is no cure; management is multidisciplinary and supportive, combining heart failure and device therapy, contracture and respiratory care, and cascade genetic screening of relatives"],"pathophysiology":"LMNA encodes lamins A and C, intermediate filament proteins that form the nuclear lamina underlying the inner nuclear membrane. This meshwork provides mechanical support to the nucleus, organises chromatin and regulates gene expression and mechanotransduction. Pathogenic variants weaken the lamina and disturb these functions, rendering mechanically stressed tissues such as cardiac and skeletal muscle particularly vulnerable. In the heart this produces fibrosis and disease of the conduction system and myocardium, leading to arrhythmia, conduction block and dilated cardiomyopathy, while skeletal muscle involvement causes weakness and the early contractures characteristic of the Emery-Dreifuss and limb-girdle phenotypes.","additionalDifferentials":["Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy from EMD (emerin) and other genes","Other genetic dilated cardiomyopathies with conduction disease (including desmin and SCN5A)","Myotonic dystrophy with cardiac conduction involvement","Sarcoglycanopathies and other limb-girdle muscular dystrophies","Cardiac sarcoidosis or other infiltrative conduction-system disease","Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["LMNA sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis, often within a cardiomyopathy or muscular dystrophy gene panel","12-lead ECG and ambulatory (Holter) monitoring to detect conduction disease and ventricular arrhythmia","Echocardiography and cardiac MRI to assess ventricular function and myocardial fibrosis","Serum creatine kinase, electromyography and, where needed, muscle biopsy for the neuromuscular phenotype","Baseline and serial respiratory function testing given diaphragmatic and spinal involvement","Arrhythmic risk stratification to guide the timing of defibrillator implantation","Cascade genetic testing and cardiac screening of at-risk relatives"],"redFlags":["Syncope, presyncope or documented non-sustained ventricular tachycardia signalling high arrhythmic risk","High-grade atrioventricular block or symptomatic bradyarrhythmia","Rapidly declining left ventricular function or decompensated heart failure","Family history of sudden cardiac death in a gene-positive relative","Respiratory insufficiency from diaphragmatic weakness"],"prognosis":"Prognosis in LMNA disease is dominated by the cardiac phenotype: conduction disease and dilated cardiomyopathy tend to progress, and the substantial risk of ventricular arrhythmia and sudden death makes early defibrillator protection central to reducing mortality. With timely device therapy, heart failure management and cardiac surveillance, outcomes can be improved, though the neuromuscular component contributes to cumulative disability.","pitfalls":["Implanting a pacemaker alone for conduction disease when a defibrillator is warranted because of the sudden-death risk","Underestimating arrhythmic risk in patients with preserved or only mildly reduced ejection fraction","Focusing on the neuromuscular weakness and under-recognising the life-threatening cardiac involvement","Failing to arrange cascade screening, leaving at-risk relatives unprotected","Permitting intense or competitive exercise despite the arrhythmic risk"],"management":["Treat the dilated cardiomyopathy with guideline-directed heart failure therapy including ACE inhibitors or an angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor, beta-blockers and mineralocorticoid-receptor antagonists","Implant a cardioverter-defibrillator at a low threshold, rather than a pacemaker alone, because of the high risk of malignant ventricular arrhythmia and sudden death","Manage conduction disease and atrial arrhythmias, with anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation as appropriate","Maintain lifelong cardiac surveillance with ECG, ambulatory monitoring, echocardiography and cardiac MRI, and perform arrhythmic risk stratification","Address the neuromuscular phenotype with physiotherapy for contractures, orthoses and monitoring and support of respiratory function including non-invasive ventilation","Advise against competitive or intense exertion given the arrhythmic risk","Refer for advanced heart failure therapies such as mechanical circulatory support or transplantation when indicated","Provide genetic counselling and cascade cardiac and genetic screening of relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"landau_kleffner_syndrome":{"name":"Landau-Kleffner syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LKS","acquired epileptic aphasia","acquired aphasia with epilepsy"],"chapter":null,"source":"ILAE classification / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acquired epileptic aphasia in which a previously normally developing child loses language, typically between three and eight years of age","The hallmark is acquired verbal auditory agnosia, so children may appear not to understand speech and can be mistaken for deaf","The EEG shows marked activation of epileptiform discharges during sleep, often reaching electrical status epilepticus in slow-wave sleep","Clinical seizures may be infrequent or absent, so the language regression and EEG findings are central to diagnosis","Behavioural disturbance and features overlapping with autism spectrum disorder commonly accompany the language loss","It lies on a continuum with the broader condition of epileptic encephalopathy with spike-wave activation in sleep","Treatment aims to suppress the sleep-activated discharges, using antiseizure medication and corticosteroid or immunomodulatory therapy in resistant cases"],"pathophysiology":"Landau-Kleffner syndrome is an epileptic encephalopathy in which intense epileptiform activity, markedly activated during non-REM sleep, disrupts the function of language-related cortical networks, particularly perisylvian regions. Continuous or near-continuous spike-wave discharges through slow-wave sleep are thought to interfere with the sleep-dependent consolidation and maturation of language and other higher functions, producing acquired aphasia and cognitive-behavioural regression even when overt seizures are few. The underlying cause is often unknown, with genetic and immune contributions implicated in some cases.","additionalDifferentials":["Autism spectrum disorder with language regression","Acquired hearing loss or auditory processing disorder","Developmental and epileptic encephalopathy with spike-wave activation in sleep (broader spectrum)","Other causes of acquired childhood aphasia (structural or inflammatory)","Selective mutism and psychiatric causes of language loss","Neurodegenerative or metabolic regression"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sleep EEG or prolonged/overnight EEG to demonstrate electrical status epilepticus in slow-wave sleep","Formal audiological assessment to exclude deafness","Language, developmental and neuropsychological evaluation","Brain MRI to exclude a structural or inflammatory cause","Genetic and, where indicated, metabolic or autoimmune investigations"],"redFlags":["Progressive loss of language and comprehension in a previously typical child","Electrical status epilepticus in slow-wave sleep on EEG","Marked behavioural or autistic regression accompanying language loss","Failure of language to recover despite apparent seizure control"],"prognosis":"Seizures, when present, usually remit by adolescence, but language and cognitive outcomes are variable and a significant proportion of children have persistent language or learning difficulties. Earlier recognition and effective suppression of the sleep-activated discharges are associated with better language recovery.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the child as deaf or as having primary autism and missing the EEG diagnosis","Relying on a routine awake EEG, which may miss the sleep-activated discharges","Judging treatment success by seizure control alone rather than language recovery","Delaying immunomodulatory or steroid therapy in resistant cases with ongoing regression"],"management":["Suppress the sleep-activated epileptiform discharges with antiseizure medications such as valproate, levetiracetam, ethosuximide or benzodiazepines like clobazam","Use a course of corticosteroids or ACTH, or intravenous immunoglobulin, early and for a sustained period to try to reverse the language regression","Avoid antiseizure drugs that can aggravate the condition, such as carbamazepine and phenytoin","Provide intensive speech and language therapy with educational support and alternative or augmentative communication","Address the accompanying behavioural and autism-spectrum-like difficulties","Monitor with follow-up sleep EEG and serial language and neuropsychological assessment, coordinated through neurology, audiology and speech therapy","Consider epilepsy surgery such as multiple subpial transection in carefully selected refractory cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ILAE classification of the epilepsies","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"langerhans_cell_histiocytosis":{"name":"Langerhans Cell Histiocytosis","system":"Haematology/Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LCH","histiocytosis X","eosinophilic granuloma","Hand-Schuller-Christian disease","Letterer-Siwe disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A clonal proliferative disorder of Langerhans-type dendritic cells that can range from a single isolated lesion to widespread multisystem disease.","Bone is the most commonly involved site, often presenting as a painful lytic lesion, classically in the skull but also in other flat and long bones.","Skin involvement can mimic seborrhoeic dermatitis or a persistent scaly, petechial rash, particularly in infants.","Diabetes insipidus from pituitary or hypothalamic involvement is a characteristic endocrine manifestation and may be the presenting feature.","Risk organ involvement (liver, spleen, and bone marrow) identifies higher-risk multisystem disease with greater mortality.","Diagnosis rests on biopsy showing characteristic cells expressing CD1a and CD207 (langerin); Birbeck granules may be seen on electron microscopy.","Many cases harbour activating mutations in the MAPK pathway, most commonly BRAF V600E, which has therapeutic implications.","Treatment ranges from observation or local therapy for single-system disease to systemic chemotherapy for multisystem or risk-organ disease."],"pathophysiology":"Langerhans cell histiocytosis is now understood as a myeloid neoplasm driven by activating mutations of the MAPK/ERK signalling pathway, most often BRAF V600E, in dendritic cell precursors. Constitutive pathway activation causes accumulation of pathological CD1a-positive, langerin-positive histiocytes that, together with eosinophils and other inflammatory cells, form granulomatous infiltrates. These lesions destroy local tissue (such as lytic bone lesions) and, when disseminated, impair function of organs including the liver, spleen, marrow, and pituitary.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteomyelitis or Ewing sarcoma (for bone lesions)","Leukaemia or lymphoma","Neuroblastoma with bony metastases","Seborrhoeic or atopic dermatitis (for skin lesions)","Other histiocytic disorders (e.g. haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, juvenile xanthogranuloma)","Chronic infection (e.g. tuberculosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy with immunohistochemistry for CD1a and CD207 (langerin)","BRAF V600E and broader MAPK pathway mutation testing","Skeletal imaging survey (radiographs and/or PET) to map bone lesions","Assessment of risk organs: liver and spleen imaging, full blood count, liver function","Endocrine evaluation and MRI of the pituitary/hypothalamus if diabetes insipidus is suspected","Baseline evaluation for pulmonary and CNS involvement when indicated"],"redFlags":["Risk-organ (liver, spleen, marrow) involvement indicating high-risk multisystem disease","Cytopenias suggesting marrow infiltration","Polyuria and polydipsia indicating diabetes insipidus from CNS/pituitary disease","Rapidly progressive multisystem disease in an infant","Neurodegenerative CNS involvement causing progressive neurological decline"],"prognosis":"Single-system disease, especially isolated bone lesions, often has an excellent prognosis and may even regress; multisystem disease with risk-organ involvement carries higher mortality. Survivors are at risk of long-term sequelae such as diabetes insipidus, other endocrinopathies, orthopaedic problems, and neurodegeneration.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the infantile skin rash for simple seborrhoeic dermatitis and delaying diagnosis","Overlooking diabetes insipidus as an early or isolated manifestation","Assuming a solitary lytic bone lesion is infection or a benign cyst without biopsy","Failing to assess for risk-organ involvement, which changes treatment intensity and prognosis"],"management":["Treat isolated single-system bone lesions with local measures such as observation, curettage or intralesional corticosteroid","Treat multisystem or multifocal disease with systemic chemotherapy, standardly vinblastine combined with a corticosteroid such as prednisolone","Use targeted MAPK-pathway inhibitors - BRAF inhibitors such as vemurafenib or dabrafenib, or MEK inhibitors - for BRAF V600E-mutated or refractory disease","Manage endocrine sequelae, notably desmopressin for diabetes insipidus and replacement of other deficient pituitary hormones","Assess and monitor risk organs (liver, spleen and bone marrow) to identify high-risk disease and guide salvage therapy","Provide long-term surveillance for late sequelae including endocrinopathy, neurodegenerative CNS involvement and pulmonary disease, with smoking cessation for pulmonary disease","Coordinate multidisciplinary care through paediatric haematology-oncology and endocrinology, escalating to salvage regimens for risk-organ or refractory disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"large_granular_lymphocytic_leukemia":{"name":"T-cell large granular lymphocytic leukaemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["T-LGL leukaemia","LGL leukaemia","large granular lymphocyte leukaemia","STAT3-mutated LGL"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An indolent clonal proliferation of cytotoxic T lymphocytes that classically presents with neutropenia, often with recurrent bacterial infection or an incidental finding","The blood film shows an increase in large granular lymphocytes with abundant cytoplasm containing azurophilic granules","A characteristic association exists with rheumatoid arthritis and with Felty-like syndrome (neutropenia and splenomegaly)","The typical immunophenotype is CD3, CD8 and CD57 positive with expression of cytotoxic markers, and clonality is confirmed by T-cell receptor gene rearrangement","Activating mutations in the STAT3 gene are found in a substantial proportion of cases and support the diagnosis","Many patients follow a chronic course and are observed; immunosuppression is reserved for symptomatic cytopenias or transfusion dependence","Anaemia, including a pure red cell aplasia pattern, and thrombocytopenia may accompany the neutropenia"],"pathophysiology":"T-cell large granular lymphocytic leukaemia arises from a clonal expansion of mature cytotoxic (usually CD8 positive) T lymphocytes. Chronic antigen-driven stimulation is thought to trigger the expansion, which then escapes normal apoptotic control through constitutive activation of the JAK/STAT signalling pathway, frequently driven by acquired activating mutations in STAT3. The persistent cytotoxic clone suppresses granulopoiesis and erythropoiesis in the marrow and, together with autoimmune mechanisms, produces the neutropenia, anaemia and thrombocytopenia that dominate the clinical picture. The strong link with rheumatoid arthritis reflects the shared background of dysregulated immune activation.","additionalDifferentials":["Reactive large granular lymphocytosis from viral infection","Chronic lymphoproliferative disorder of NK cells","T-cell prolymphocytic leukaemia","Felty syndrome without a demonstrable clone","Aplastic anaemia or hypoplastic myelodysplastic syndrome","Autoimmune neutropenia of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood film review to quantify large granular lymphocytes","Flow cytometry immunophenotyping (CD3, CD8, CD57, CD16, cytotoxic markers)","T-cell receptor gene rearrangement studies to demonstrate clonality","Molecular testing for STAT3 (and STAT5b) mutations","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine to assess infiltration and cytopenia cause","Autoimmune serology including rheumatoid factor given the arthritis association"],"redFlags":["Severe neutropenia with recurrent or life-threatening infection","Transfusion-dependent anaemia or symptomatic pancytopenia","Rapidly rising lymphocyte count or transformation to an aggressive phenotype","Constitutional symptoms suggesting an alternative aggressive lymphoproliferative disorder"],"prognosis":"Most patients have an indolent disease with prolonged survival, and morbidity relates mainly to infection from neutropenia; a minority have refractory cytopenias or, rarely, aggressive transformation.","pitfalls":["Assuming a modest large granular lymphocytosis is reactive without checking clonality","Overlooking STAT3 mutation testing that can clarify a borderline diagnosis","Attributing neutropenia solely to rheumatoid arthritis or its drugs","Treating asymptomatic patients who could simply be monitored"],"management":["Observe asymptomatic patients, as many follow an indolent course requiring no immediate treatment","Treat symptomatic cytopenias or transfusion dependence with immunosuppressive therapy such as methotrexate, ciclosporin or cyclophosphamide as first-line agents","Support the neutropenia with prompt treatment of infection, infection prophylaxis and granulocyte colony-stimulating factor when needed, and transfuse for symptomatic anaemia","Treat any associated rheumatoid arthritis or other autoimmune disease","Monitor blood counts for progression, refractory cytopenias or rare aggressive transformation","Refer to haematology and consider second-line or JAK/STAT-directed agents or clinical trials in refractory disease","Escalate immunosuppression for disease unresponsive to first-line treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"laron_syndrome":{"name":"Laron syndrome (GH receptor / GH insensitivity)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Laron dwarfism","growth hormone insensitivity syndrome","GHR mutation","primary IGF-1 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Endocrine Society clinical guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Usually autosomal recessive growth hormone insensitivity, most often from growth hormone receptor (GHR) mutations, causing severe postnatal growth failure","The defining biochemistry is high or normal circulating growth hormone with low IGF-1 that fails to rise after growth hormone administration","Clinical features include proportionate short stature, frontal bossing, a depressed nasal bridge, small genitalia, and a high-pitched voice","Fasting hypoglycaemia is common in infancy because IGF-1 deficiency and altered fuel handling impair glucose maintenance","Because the defect lies downstream of growth hormone, growth hormone replacement is ineffective; recombinant IGF-1 is the specific treatment for growth and metabolic benefit","Populations with Laron syndrome show notably low rates of diabetes and cancer, illustrating the growth hormone/IGF-1 axis in growth and proliferation","It contrasts with isolated growth hormone deficiency, where growth hormone itself is low and replacement works","Diagnosis rests on the growth pattern, the high-GH/low-IGF-1 profile with a failed IGF-1 generation test, and GHR genetics"],"pathophysiology":"Laron syndrome is a state of growth hormone insensitivity in which growth hormone is secreted normally or in excess but cannot act, most commonly because of loss-of-function mutations in the growth hormone receptor (GHR). Growth hormone normally binds its hepatic receptor to stimulate IGF-1 production, which mediates most of its growth-promoting effects; when the receptor is defective, IGF-1 generation fails despite high growth hormone levels. The resulting profound IGF-1 deficiency produces severe postnatal growth failure and characteristic craniofacial features, while loss of IGF-1's metabolic actions contributes to fasting hypoglycaemia in early life. Because the block is at or beyond the receptor, only IGF-1 replacement, not growth hormone, restores signalling.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated growth hormone deficiency","IGF-1 gene or IGF-1 receptor defects","Constitutional delay of growth and puberty","Small-for-gestational-age short stature without catch-up","Hypothyroidism causing growth failure","Other skeletal dysplasias or syndromic short stature","Chronic systemic illness or malnutrition"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Basal growth hormone (high or normal) and IGF-1 (low)","IGF-1 generation test, which fails to raise IGF-1 after growth hormone","IGF binding protein-3 and acid-labile subunit levels","GHR gene sequencing and testing of downstream signalling genes where indicated","Fasting glucose monitoring for hypoglycaemia in infants","Bone age and growth velocity assessment"],"redFlags":["Recurrent fasting or nocturnal hypoglycaemia in infancy","Severe growth failure crossing centiles downward","Signs of hypoglycaemic seizure or encephalopathy","Marked developmental or feeding difficulties in an affected infant"],"prognosis":"Untreated individuals have marked short stature but often a favourable metabolic profile with low rates of diabetes and malignancy; recombinant IGF-1 improves growth, though final height usually remains below average.","pitfalls":["Giving growth hormone replacement, which fails because the defect is downstream","Misdiagnosing the condition as isolated growth hormone deficiency without checking IGF-1 response","Overlooking infantile hypoglycaemia as a presenting feature","Ignoring the diagnostic value of the IGF-1 generation test","Failing to distinguish GHR defects from IGF-1 or IGF-1-receptor defects"],"management":["Give the specific treatment - recombinant human IGF-1 (mecasermin) - since growth hormone replacement is ineffective when the block lies at or beyond its receptor","Prevent and treat fasting hypoglycaemia, especially in infancy, with regular feeding and by giving IGF-1 together with food, as the therapy itself can cause hypoglycaemia","Monitor growth velocity and bone age and watch for IGF-1 treatment effects such as lymphoid and tonsillar hypertrophy, raised intracranial pressure and slipped capital femoral epiphysis","Coordinate multidisciplinary care through paediatric endocrinology, dietetics and auxology","Provide psychosocial support for the effects of short stature","Offer genetic counselling and family testing given the usually autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society clinical practice guidance on growth disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"larsen_syndrome":{"name":"Larsen syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FLNB","filamin B disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by congenital dislocations of large joints, notably bilateral knee dislocations, together with hip and elbow dislocations and severe talipes","The dominant form is caused by variants in FLNB encoding filamin B, a cytoskeletal protein regulating chondrocyte function; rarer recessive Larsen-like phenotypes have distinct causes","A characteristic flat facial appearance with a depressed nasal bridge, prominent forehead, and widely spaced eyes aids recognition","Hand findings include cylindrical, spatulate fingers and, radiographically, an accessory carpal ossification centre","Cervical spine abnormalities with kyphosis and instability are a critical hazard that can cause myelopathy and complicate airway management","Additional features may include cleft palate, hearing loss, and, in some, cardiac and respiratory (tracheomalacia) involvement","Diagnosis integrates the pattern of congenital joint dislocations and facial features with FLNB molecular testing"],"pathophysiology":"Larsen syndrome most often results from dominant variants in FLNB, which encodes filamin B, a cytoskeletal protein that cross-links actin and regulates signalling and mechanical responses in chondrocytes. Disturbed filamin B function alters chondrocyte proliferation and differentiation and the development of joints, ligaments, and the vertebral column. This produces the hallmark congenital large-joint dislocations, ligamentous laxity, cervical spine anomalies, and characteristic craniofacial and hand features. FLNB variants underlie a spectrum of skeletal disorders ranging from Larsen syndrome to more severe atelosteogenesis phenotypes.","additionalDifferentials":["Atelosteogenesis (also FLNB-related)","Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita","Ehlers-Danlos syndromes with joint instability","Desbuquois dysplasia","Otopalatodigital spectrum disorders","Recessive Larsen-like syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey documenting joint dislocations, accessory carpal ossification, and spinal anomalies","Cervical spine imaging including flexion-extension views to assess kyphosis and instability","FLNB molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Airway and respiratory assessment for tracheomalacia before anaesthesia","Echocardiography where cardiac involvement is suspected","Audiological assessment for hearing loss"],"redFlags":["Cervical kyphosis or instability with myelopathy, particularly around anaesthesia","Airway compromise from tracheomalacia or cervical involvement","Neurological deterioration from spinal cord compression","Respiratory distress in infancy"],"prognosis":"With careful orthopaedic and spinal management, intelligence and lifespan are generally normal, but untreated cervical instability can cause devastating neurological injury, and joint dislocations and foot deformity require staged surgical and physiotherapy management.","pitfalls":["Overlooking cervical kyphosis and instability, risking cord injury during airway management","Reducing knee or hip dislocations without recognising the underlying syndrome and its spinal risks","Failing to assess for tracheomalacia before anaesthesia","Confusing it with arthrogryposis without appropriate radiographic and genetic evaluation"],"management":["No disease-modifying drug exists; the mainstay is staged orthopaedic reduction and surgical stabilisation of the congenital large-joint dislocations (bilateral knees, hips, elbows) with serial casting and soft-tissue release for talipes","Protect and stabilise the cervical spine, bracing or surgically fusing cervical kyphosis and instability to prevent myelopathy from cord compression","Plan every anaesthetic as a difficult airway, anticipating cervical instability and tracheomalacia, using fibreoptic and cervical-protection techniques","Provide supportive physiotherapy and orthotics to maintain joint function, with cleft palate repair and hearing amplification for sensorineural loss","Undertake surveillance with serial flexion-extension cervical imaging, echocardiography where cardiac involvement is suspected, and periodic audiology","Coordinate care across clinical genetics, orthopaedic and spinal surgery, ENT, anaesthesia, and cardiology","Offer genetic counselling for the autosomal dominant FLNB inheritance and screen at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"laryngeal_cancer":{"name":"Laryngeal cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["larynx cancer","laryngeal carcinoma","cancer of the voice box"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The vast majority are squamous cell carcinomas, and tobacco and alcohol use are the dominant, synergistic risk factors, with HPV playing a lesser role than in oropharyngeal cancer","Persistent hoarseness lasting more than a few weeks is the classic early symptom of glottic tumours and warrants prompt laryngoscopic assessment","Supraglottic and subglottic tumours often present later with sore throat, referred otalgia, dysphagia, a neck mass, or airway symptoms because they cause voice change only when advanced","Anatomical subsite matters: glottic cancers tend to present early with hoarseness and have relatively sparse lymphatics, while supraglottic cancers spread earlier to cervical nodes","Diagnosis requires visualisation of the larynx with flexible or rigid endoscopy and biopsy, together with imaging to define tumour extent and nodal disease","Early-stage disease can be treated with either radiotherapy or conservation surgery, including transoral laser microsurgery, with excellent voice-preservation outcomes","Advanced disease is managed with organ-preservation chemoradiotherapy or total laryngectomy, balancing cure against loss of natural voice and swallowing function","Stridor or rapidly worsening airway obstruction is an emergency that may require urgent airway intervention before definitive treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Laryngeal carcinoma arises from the squamous epithelium lining the supraglottis, glottis, or subglottis. Chronic carcinogen exposure from tobacco smoke and alcohol produces field change with progressive dysplasia, carcinoma in situ, and invasive squamous cell carcinoma. Behaviour depends on subsite anatomy: the true vocal cords have limited lymphatic drainage so glottic tumours cause early hoarseness but spread to nodes late, whereas the richly lymphatic supraglottis allows earlier cervical nodal metastasis. Local extension can involve cartilage, paraglottic and pre-epiglottic spaces, and adjacent hypopharynx.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign vocal cord lesions such as nodules, polyps, or cysts","Chronic laryngitis or reflux-related laryngeal irritation","Vocal cord palsy from other causes","Laryngeal papillomatosis","Hypopharyngeal or thyroid carcinoma involving the larynx","Granulomatous or infective laryngitis (e.g. tuberculosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible or rigid laryngoscopy to inspect the larynx and vocal cord mobility","Direct laryngoscopy with biopsy under anaesthesia for tissue diagnosis and mapping","CT or MRI of the neck to assess local invasion, cartilage involvement, and nodal spread","CT chest and PET-CT for distant staging and synchronous second primaries","Assessment of voice, swallowing, and airway to guide organ-preservation decisions"],"redFlags":["Hoarseness persisting beyond a few weeks, especially in a smoker","Stridor or progressive airway obstruction requiring urgent airway management","A firm cervical lymph node with throat symptoms","Dysphagia, odynophagia, or referred ear pain with weight loss"],"prognosis":"Early glottic cancers have high cure and voice-preservation rates, whereas advanced or node-positive supraglottic and subglottic tumours carry poorer prognosis; overall outcome depends on stage, subsite, and treatment response.","pitfalls":["Attributing persistent hoarseness to benign causes without laryngoscopy","Missing advanced supraglottic tumours that present without early voice change","Underestimating impending airway compromise in a patient with stridor","Neglecting the functional impact of treatment on voice and swallowing when counselling patients"],"management":["Treat by stage and subsite: early glottic disease with radiotherapy or transoral laser microsurgery/conservation surgery to preserve voice, and advanced disease with concurrent platinum-based chemoradiotherapy or total laryngectomy with neck dissection","Use platinum-based chemotherapy concurrently with radiotherapy for organ preservation, reserving cetuximab or immune checkpoint inhibitors for recurrent or metastatic disease","Mandate tobacco and alcohol cessation and optimise nutrition to improve treatment tolerance and reduce second primaries","Provide speech-and-language therapy for voice and swallowing, including tracheo-oesophageal puncture or electrolarynx voice restoration after laryngectomy","Secure the airway urgently in obstructing tumours, with tracheostomy where needed","Undertake regular endoscopic and imaging surveillance for locoregional recurrence and metachronous second primaries","Manage within a head-and-neck multidisciplinary team, integrating early palliative care for advanced incurable disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology"],"crossLinks":[]},"laryngeal_cleft":{"name":"Laryngeal cleft","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Laryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["posterior laryngeal cleft","laryngotracheo-oesophageal cleft","cleft larynx"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital defect in the posterior partition between the larynx or trachea and the oesophagus, allowing communication and consequent aspiration","Presents in infancy with feeding-related coughing and choking, recurrent chest infections, stridor, and cyanotic episodes","Severity is graded by the inferior extent of the cleft, from an interarytenoid defect to an extensive laryngotracheo-oesophageal cleft","Frequently associated with other anomalies, including tracheo-oesophageal fistula and syndromes such as Opitz-Frias and VACTERL/Pallister-Hall","Diagnosis rests on rigid endoscopy under anaesthesia with active probing of the interarytenoid region, as shallow clefts are easily missed","Minor clefts may be managed conservatively with feeding modification and reflux measures, while deeper clefts require endoscopic or open surgical repair","Aspiration risk makes airway protection and nutrition the immediate priorities in a symptomatic infant"],"pathophysiology":"A laryngeal cleft results from failure of the posterior cricoid lamina and the tracheo-oesophageal septum to fuse during embryogenesis, leaving an abnormal midline communication between the airway posteriorly and the digestive tract. The deficient partition removes the normal separation that protects the airway during swallowing, so liquids and secretions spill directly into the larynx and trachea. The degree of aspiration and respiratory compromise parallels the depth of the cleft, ranging from occasional aspiration with a shallow interarytenoid defect to life-threatening soiling of the airway and recurrent pneumonia with an extensive cleft reaching the carina.","additionalDifferentials":["Tracheo-oesophageal fistula","Laryngomalacia","Neurological (central or bulbar) dysphagia with aspiration","Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease","Vocal fold paralysis causing aspiration","Swallowing incoordination of prematurity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Rigid microlaryngoscopy and bronchoscopy with probing of the interarytenoid area, the diagnostic gold standard","Flexible fibreoptic laryngoscopy for initial airway assessment","Functional endoscopic or videofluoroscopic swallow study to document aspiration","Contrast swallow to define the tracheo-oesophageal anatomy","Screening for associated anomalies given the syndromic associations"],"redFlags":["Cyanotic or apnoeic episodes during feeding","Recurrent aspiration pneumonia and failure to thrive","Severe stridor or respiratory distress in a neonate","A deep cleft extending into the trachea, which is life-threatening and demands urgent airway protection"],"prognosis":"Shallow clefts often do well with conservative feeding and reflux management, and repaired clefts generally have a good outcome, but deep laryngotracheo-oesophageal clefts carry substantial morbidity and mortality, especially where associated anomalies coexist.","pitfalls":["Missing a shallow interarytenoid cleft because it was not actively probed during endoscopy","Attributing feeding-related symptoms solely to reflux and delaying definitive assessment","Overlooking associated congenital anomalies that alter management","Underestimating aspiration risk and permitting unmodified oral feeding in a symptomatic infant"],"management":["Match treatment to cleft depth: manage shallow interarytenoid clefts conservatively with thickened feeds and feeding modification, and repair deeper clefts endoscopically or by open cervical/thoracic approach","Control gastro-oesophageal reflux with anti-reflux measures and acid-suppressing therapy to reduce airway soiling and protect any repair","Optimise feeding with speech-and-language-led swallow therapy and adjusted feed consistency to minimise aspiration","Treat recurrent aspiration pneumonia promptly with appropriate antimicrobials and respiratory support","Monitor with serial swallow assessment and respiratory review, and re-endoscope to confirm healing after repair","Screen for associated anomalies such as tracheo-oesophageal fistula, VACTERL, Opitz-Frias, and Pallister-Hall given the syndromic associations","Coordinate paediatric ENT, gastroenterology, respiratory, and speech-and-language teams"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"laryngeal_papilloma":{"name":"Laryngeal papilloma","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Laryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["recurrent respiratory papillomatosis","RRP","laryngeal papillomatosis","juvenile-onset recurrent respiratory papillomatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Benign exophytic squamous papillomas of the larynx that are the laryngeal manifestation of recurrent respiratory papillomatosis, driven by human papillomavirus, chiefly types 6 and 11","Juvenile-onset disease presents in childhood with progressive hoarseness, an abnormal cry, and sometimes stridor, and tends to be more aggressive and multifocal","Adult-onset disease more often causes persistent dysphonia and follows a comparatively less florid course","Lesions favour anatomical transition zones, especially the true vocal folds and the limen vestibuli","Progressive stridor and airway obstruction from bulky disease can be life-threatening, particularly in young children","Treatment is repeated surgical debulking to preserve airway and voice while sparing normal mucosa, as recurrence is expected and cure elusive","Distal spread to the trachea and lungs and malignant transformation, more likely with type 11 or after prior irradiation, are recognised complications"],"pathophysiology":"Laryngeal papillomas are the airway expression of recurrent respiratory papillomatosis, in which human papillomavirus, predominantly the low-risk genotypes 6 and 11, infects the respiratory epithelium and drives proliferation of the basal squamous cells. Juvenile-onset disease is thought to result from vertical transmission during passage through an infected birth canal, whereas adult acquisition is less clearly defined. The virus persists within apparently normal-looking mucosa, which explains the characteristic tendency to recur after removal. Lesions cluster at squamociliary junctions and produce friable, frond-like growths that add mass to the vocal folds and, when bulky, obstruct the airway; rarely the process spreads distally or undergoes malignant change.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal fold polyp","Vocal fold leukoplakia or dysplasia","Verrucous or invasive squamous cell carcinoma","Vocal fold granuloma","Laryngeal candidiasis with keratosis","Congenital laryngeal cyst (in the child with stridor)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible fibreoptic laryngoscopy to identify and map the papillomatous lesions","Rigid endoscopy under anaesthesia for assessment, mapping, and biopsy","Histopathology to confirm papilloma and exclude dysplasia or malignancy","Human papillomavirus typing of tissue, given prognostic relevance of type 11","Tracheobronchoscopy or imaging where distal airway or pulmonary spread is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive stridor or airway obstruction, especially in a young child, which is potentially life-threatening","Rapid growth, keratinisation, or an ulcerated mass suggesting malignant transformation","Distal tracheal or pulmonary involvement","Frequent recurrences requiring escalating numbers of procedures"],"prognosis":"The course is unpredictable and often chronic with repeated recurrences, and some childhood cases eventually remit spontaneously, but aggressive multifocal or distally spreading disease carries significant morbidity and a small but important risk of malignant transformation.","pitfalls":["Aggressive deep resection that damages the vocal ligament and anterior commissure, risking scarring and web formation","Assuming complete surgical clearance eradicates the virus, when latent virus in normal mucosa drives recurrence","Overlooking malignant transformation in longstanding or type 11 disease","Neglecting airway planning in a child with bulky papillomas before anaesthesia"],"management":["The mainstay is repeated conservative surgical debulking with microdebrider or CO2 laser to maintain airway and voice while sparing normal mucosa, as recurrence is expected","Add adjuvant therapy in aggressive or frequently recurring disease, such as intralesional cidofovir or systemic bevacizumab, with HPV vaccination as an adjunct","Manage acute airway obstruction promptly, avoiding tracheostomy where possible because it risks distal seeding of papillomas","Provide voice therapy and support voice outcomes given the cumulative scarring from repeated procedures","Undertake surveillance endoscopy for recurrence, distal tracheobronchial or pulmonary spread, and malignant transformation, particularly with HPV type 11","Type HPV on excised tissue for prognostic stratification and confirm histology to exclude dysplasia","Coordinate ENT and respiratory specialist care with regular structured review"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"laryngeal_papillomatosis":{"name":"Laryngeal papillomatosis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["recurrent respiratory papillomatosis","RRP","juvenile laryngeal papillomatosis","laryngeal papilloma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common benign neoplasm of the larynx, caused by human papillomavirus, chiefly types 6 and 11","It occurs in a juvenile form, typically multiple and more aggressive, and an adult form that is often more localised","The usual presentation is progressive hoarseness or a weak or abnormal cry in a child, and with growth it can cause stridor and airway obstruction","Juvenile disease is thought to be acquired through vertical transmission from maternal genital HPV infection during birth","Laryngoscopy shows soft, warty, exophytic lesions favouring the vocal cords and other sites of squamous-ciliary junction","Because there is no definitive cure, treatment centres on repeated conservative surgical removal (commonly microlaryngeal or laser) to maintain the airway and voice while preserving normal tissue","The disease is characterised by frequent recurrence and can spread down the tracheobronchial tree, and a tracheostomy is best avoided as it may promote distal spread","Malignant transformation is uncommon but recognised, particularly with certain HPV types or after irradiation, so persistent or atypical lesions warrant biopsy"],"pathophysiology":"Laryngeal papillomatosis results from infection of the respiratory epithelium by human papillomavirus, most often types 6 and 11, which drives proliferation of squamous epithelium into finger-like papillary fronds supported by fibrovascular cores. Lesions favour the transition zones between squamous and ciliary epithelium, so the vocal cords are commonly affected. In children the virus is usually acquired at birth from an infected maternal genital tract, and the immature or immunologically naive host tends to develop multiple, recurrent lesions. Latent virus in adjacent normal-looking mucosa underlies the persistent recurrence after removal, and lesions may seed distally in the airway.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal cord nodules or polyps","Laryngeal keratosis or leukoplakia","Laryngeal carcinoma","Croup or other infective laryngitis in a child","Vocal cord granuloma","Subglottic haemangioma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible and rigid laryngoscopy to identify and map the papillomas","Microlaryngoscopy under anaesthesia for detailed assessment and tissue removal","Histopathology of excised tissue to confirm the diagnosis and exclude dysplasia or malignancy","HPV typing where available, as certain types carry higher risk","Airway assessment for distal (tracheobronchial) spread in aggressive disease"],"redFlags":["Progressive stridor and airway obstruction from bulky laryngeal disease requiring urgent airway management","Distal spread into the trachea and bronchi indicating aggressive disease","Features suggesting malignant transformation such as a firm, ulcerated, or rapidly enlarging lesion","Rapid recurrence needing frequent surgical intervention"],"prognosis":"The course is unpredictable and marked by recurrence; juvenile disease may require many procedures over years before eventual regression around puberty in some children, while adult disease is often more limited, and although malignant change is rare it is an important long-term consideration.","pitfalls":["Aggressive surgery that damages the vocal cords and causes scarring or web formation","Performing a tracheostomy that may encourage distal tracheobronchial spread","Assuming benignity and not biopsying atypical or persistent lesions","Overlooking airway compromise in a child presenting only with a hoarse cry"],"management":["There is no curative drug, so treatment centres on repeated microlaryngoscopic or laser removal of papillomas to preserve airway patency and voice while conserving normal tissue","Consider adjuvant intralesional cidofovir or systemic bevacizumab for aggressive, multifocal, or rapidly recurring disease, and offer HPV vaccination","Relieve airway obstruction urgently in children with bulky disease and stridor, avoiding tracheostomy where feasible to limit distal spread","Support voice with speech-and-language therapy and minimise iatrogenic scarring during repeated procedures","Monitor for recurrence and distal tracheobronchial seeding, with HPV typing to identify higher-risk genotypes","Confirm histology of excised tissue to exclude dysplasia or malignant change over the long term","Manage through paediatric or adult ENT services with structured surveillance until eventual regression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"laryngitis":{"name":"Laryngitis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute laryngitis","chronic laryngitis","laryngeal inflammation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Inflammation of the larynx presenting chiefly with hoarseness or voice change, often with throat discomfort and a dry cough","Acute laryngitis is usually viral and self-limiting, frequently accompanying an upper respiratory tract infection","Chronic laryngitis develops over weeks and is linked to vocal abuse, smoking, reflux, chronic sinus infection and occupational irritants","Voice rest and hydration are central to recovery in acute cases, as continued voice use aggravates the inflamed cords","Reflux laryngitis (laryngopharyngeal reflux) can cause posterior laryngeal inflammation, throat clearing and a globus sensation","Management principles emphasise treating the underlying cause, vocal hygiene, and addressing irritants such as smoking and reflux","In young children, inflammatory laryngeal narrowing can cause croup or, in acute epiglottitis, life-threatening airway obstruction","Hoarseness persisting beyond a few weeks, particularly in a smoker, requires laryngeal visualisation to exclude malignancy"],"pathophysiology":"Laryngitis is inflammation of the laryngeal mucosa, especially the vocal folds. In acute laryngitis, viral (or sometimes bacterial) infection produces mucosal hyperaemia and oedema that increase the mass and stiffness of the vocal folds, disturbing their vibratory pattern and causing hoarseness. In chronic laryngitis, sustained irritation from voice overuse, tobacco smoke, gastric reflux or persistent infection leads to ongoing mucosal changes such as oedema, thickening and keratosis. In children, the narrow subglottis means that inflammatory swelling can significantly compromise the airway.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal cord nodules or polyps","Laryngeal carcinoma","Vocal cord palsy","Laryngopharyngeal reflux","Croup or acute epiglottitis in children","Functional or muscle tension dysphonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Laryngoscopy (indirect, flexible or videostroboscopy) to inspect the vocal folds","Assessment for reflux and its contribution to symptoms","Voice assessment where vocal misuse is suspected","Biopsy of suspicious lesions to exclude malignancy","Imaging where deep infection, mass or airway compromise is a concern"],"redFlags":["Stridor or respiratory difficulty, especially in a child, suggesting airway compromise or epiglottitis","Hoarseness persisting beyond a few weeks in a smoker raising concern for laryngeal cancer","Associated dysphagia, weight loss, haemoptysis or a neck mass","Rapidly worsening symptoms with drooling and toxaemia"],"prognosis":"Acute laryngitis usually resolves within days to a couple of weeks with conservative measures, whereas chronic laryngitis requires removal of the underlying irritant for improvement; persistent hoarseness always warrants exclusion of a serious cause.","pitfalls":["Attributing persistent hoarseness to simple laryngitis and delaying laryngoscopy in a smoker","Overlooking laryngopharyngeal reflux as a driver of chronic symptoms","Underestimating airway risk in a child with laryngeal inflammation","Failing to address vocal misuse and continued irritant exposure"],"management":["Manage acute viral laryngitis conservatively with voice rest, hydration, and humidification, reserving antibiotics for proven bacterial infection","For chronic laryngitis, treat the underlying cause with smoking cessation, removal of occupational irritants, and control of laryngopharyngeal reflux through lifestyle measures and acid-suppressing therapy","Institute vocal hygiene and refer for speech-and-language voice therapy where vocal misuse or overuse is contributory","Give simple symptomatic relief for throat discomfort and cough","Investigate and exclude malignancy by laryngoscopy in any hoarseness persisting beyond a few weeks","In children, monitor closely for subglottic airway compromise and escalate for stridor or respiratory distress"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"laryngocele":{"name":"Laryngocele","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Laryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["laryngeal air sac","saccular cyst (air-filled)","internal laryngocele","external laryngocele"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An abnormal air-filled dilatation of the laryngeal saccule that communicates with the airway, classified as internal, external, or combined depending on its relation to the thyrohyoid membrane","Internal laryngoceles bulge into the false fold and aryepiglottic fold causing hoarseness or a globus sensation, while external ones present as a reducible lateral neck swelling","Classically expands with raised intralaryngeal pressure, as in glassblowers, wind-instrument players, and chronic coughers","The lesion may enlarge on a Valsalva manoeuvre and produce a resonant swelling that empties with gentle compression","An air-filled cavity becoming fluid-filled or infected forms a laryngopyocele, which can present acutely with pain and airway threat","Imaging with CT demonstrates the air- or fluid-filled sac and defines its internal and external components","Definitive treatment is surgical excision, by an endoscopic or external cervical approach according to type and size"],"pathophysiology":"A laryngocele is an air-containing dilatation of the laryngeal saccule, the small blind-ending pouch that arises from the anterior ventricle between the true and false vocal folds. Persistently raised pressure within the larynx, or narrowing of the saccular opening, allows air to distend the saccule while maintaining its communication with the airway. If it stays medial to the thyrohyoid membrane it remains internal; if it herniates through the membrane alongside the superior laryngeal neurovascular bundle it becomes external. Because a saccular obstruction can also produce a fluid-filled saccular cyst, an important consideration is a coexisting mucosal neoplasm at the saccular opening, particularly in adults.","additionalDifferentials":["Saccular cyst (fluid-filled)","Branchial cleft cyst","Thyroglossal or other cervical cystic swelling","Zenker (pharyngeal) pouch","Laryngopyocele","Supraglottic tumour obstructing the saccule"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible fibreoptic laryngoscopy, ideally with a Valsalva manoeuvre to demonstrate expansion","CT of the neck to characterise the air- or fluid-filled sac and its components","MRI where soft-tissue detail or an underlying tumour is in question","Careful endoscopic inspection of the ventricle and saccular opening for an obstructing lesion","Histology of the excised sac and any associated mucosal lesion"],"redFlags":["An underlying obstructing tumour at the saccular opening, especially in an older adult who does not overuse the voice","Features of a laryngopyocele with pain, fever, and rapidly enlarging swelling threatening the airway","Progressive stridor or dyspnoea from a large internal component","Rapid or unilateral enlargement without a pressure-related occupation"],"prognosis":"Surgical excision is usually curative with a low recurrence rate, and the outlook is excellent provided any underlying obstructing neoplasm is identified and treated and infective complications are avoided.","pitfalls":["Assuming a laryngocele is purely idiopathic and missing an obstructing saccular tumour in an adult","Draining a laryngopyocele without addressing the underlying sac and airway risk","Underestimating the external component and planning an inadequate approach","Failing to warn wind players or glassblowers about the aggravating role of raised intralaryngeal pressure"],"management":["Definitive treatment is surgical excision, using endoscopic marsupialisation or CO2 laser for internal laryngoceles and an external cervical approach for external or combined lesions, which is usually curative","Actively exclude and treat an underlying obstructing mucosal neoplasm at the saccular opening, particularly in adults","Treat a secondarily infected laryngopyocele with antibiotics and drainage while securing the airway","Advise modifying activities that raise intralaryngeal pressure, such as wind-instrument playing and glassblowing, and manage chronic cough","Manage acute airway threat from a large or infected sac with urgent airway protection","Send the excised sac and any mucosal lesion for histology and follow up for recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"laryngomalacia":{"name":"Laryngomalacia","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["floppy larynx","congenital laryngeal stridor","soft larynx"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Laryngomalacia is the most common cause of chronic stridor in infants and results from collapse of soft, immature supraglottic structures during inspiration.","The typical presentation is inspiratory stridor that begins in the first weeks of life, worsens with feeding, crying, agitation, or lying supine, and improves when the infant is calm or prone.","In most infants the condition is mild and self-limiting, with symptoms peaking in the early months and resolving as the airway matures, usually within the first one to two years.","Flexible laryngoscopy in the awake infant is the diagnostic gold standard, demonstrating the characteristic dynamic supraglottic collapse.","Gastro-oesophageal reflux commonly coexists and can aggravate symptoms, so it is often addressed as part of management.","Feeding difficulty, choking or coughing with feeds, and poor weight gain indicate more significant disease and warrant closer evaluation.","Severe disease with significant obstruction, apnoea, or failure to thrive may require surgical intervention such as supraglottoplasty.","Persistent or atypical stridor should prompt evaluation for additional or synchronous airway lesions."],"pathophysiology":"In laryngomalacia the supraglottic tissues, including the epiglottis and aryepiglottic folds, are excessively soft and poorly supported, and there is often associated neuromuscular immaturity affecting airway tone and coordination. During inspiration the negative airway pressure draws these floppy structures inward, causing dynamic collapse over the laryngeal inlet and producing turbulent airflow and inspiratory stridor. The obstruction is worse when airflow demand or negative pressure increases, such as with crying, feeding, or agitation, and lessens with maturation as the tissues become firmer and neuromuscular control improves. Coexisting reflux can worsen supraglottic oedema and symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal cord paralysis","Subglottic stenosis","Vascular ring or other extrinsic airway compression","Laryngeal web, cyst, or haemangioma","Tracheomalacia","Acquired causes of stridor such as croup or foreign body"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible awake laryngoscopy","Assessment of feeding and growth","Pulse oximetry or sleep study if obstruction or apnoea is suspected","Bronchoscopy or airway endoscopy to exclude synchronous lesions in severe or atypical cases","Evaluation for gastro-oesophageal reflux when contributory"],"redFlags":["Cyanosis, apnoea, or significant desaturation","Marked work of breathing or worsening obstruction","Feeding difficulty with choking, aspiration, or failure to thrive","Stridor that is severe, progressive, or atypical for laryngomalacia","Life-threatening obstructive episodes"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent for the majority, in whom mild laryngomalacia resolves spontaneously as the airway matures; a minority with severe disease require surgery, which is generally effective.","pitfalls":["Assuming all infant stridor is laryngomalacia and missing another airway lesion","Underestimating severity by not assessing feeding, growth, and oxygenation","Overlooking a synchronous second airway abnormality","Failing to recognise and manage contributing gastro-oesophageal reflux"],"management":["Manage the mild majority expectantly with parental reassurance, as symptoms peak in early infancy and resolve spontaneously as the supraglottic airway matures","Treat coexisting gastro-oesophageal reflux with anti-reflux measures and acid suppression, as reflux aggravates supraglottic oedema","Support feeding with modified feed consistency and positioning, monitoring weight gain to prevent faltering growth","Reserve supraglottoplasty, dividing the aryepiglottic folds and trimming redundant supraglottic tissue, for severe disease with airway obstruction or failure to thrive","Monitor growth, oxygen saturation, and sleep study where obstructive apnoea is suspected","Escalate promptly for feeding failure, cyanotic episodes, or significant obstruction, and exclude synchronous airway lesions by endoscopy in severe or atypical cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"lassa_fever":{"name":"Lassa fever","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lassa virus infection","Lassa haemorrhagic fever","VHF"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A viral haemorrhagic fever endemic to West Africa, caused by an arenavirus, with the multimammate rat as the natural reservoir; humans are infected through contact with rodent excreta or contaminated food","Onset is typically gradual with fever, malaise, headache, sore throat, and retrosternal or muscular pain, which makes early distinction from other febrile illnesses difficult","Many infections are mild or subclinical, but severe cases progress to bleeding, facial and neck oedema, pleural and pericardial effusions, encephalopathy, and shock","Sensorineural hearing loss is a characteristic and often permanent complication, sometimes occurring during recovery","Person-to-person and nosocomial transmission through body fluids is well recognised, so isolation, barrier precautions, and safe specimen handling are essential","It is especially severe in pregnancy, with high maternal and fetal mortality, particularly in the third trimester","This is a medical emergency and a notifiable disease; ribavirin is used and is most effective when started early, alongside intensive supportive care"],"pathophysiology":"Lassa virus is an enveloped RNA arenavirus that, after exposure to rodent excreta or infected body fluids, replicates in multiple organs and suppresses innate immune responses. Widespread infection of endothelial cells and immune dysregulation lead to impaired vascular integrity and platelet dysfunction rather than large-scale tissue necrosis, producing capillary leak, oedema, and haemorrhage. Hepatic, adrenal, and endothelial involvement, together with a poorly controlled inflammatory response, drive progression to shock and multi-organ failure in severe cases.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe malaria","Typhoid (enteric) fever","Other viral haemorrhagic fevers (Ebola, CCHF)","Severe bacterial sepsis","Leptospirosis","Severe dengue"],"infectionMimics":["Severe malaria","Typhoid fever","Ebola virus disease","Bacterial sepsis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR for Lassa virus RNA in early illness, performed under strict biosafety","Lassa-specific IgM and IgG serology and antigen detection where available","Full blood count, liver and renal function, and coagulation studies to gauge severity","Exclusion of malaria and typhoid, which are common and treatable co-considerations in endemic regions","All sampling coordinated with the laboratory and public health authorities under enhanced biosafety"],"redFlags":["Mucosal bleeding, facial or neck oedema, or effusions indicating severe disease","Encephalopathy, seizures, or rapidly rising transaminases","Hypotension, shock, or signs of multi-organ failure","Any suspected case requires immediate isolation, barrier nursing, and notification because of high nosocomial transmission risk, with pregnancy signalling especially high risk"],"prognosis":"Most infections are mild, but hospitalised severe cases carry a high case-fatality rate, worse with delayed treatment, bleeding, and organ failure; survivors may be left with permanent sensorineural hearing loss, and pregnancy is associated with markedly poorer maternal and fetal outcomes.","pitfalls":["Delaying isolation and barrier precautions, exposing staff and other patients to nosocomial spread","Attributing the gradual febrile onset solely to malaria or typhoid and missing Lassa in an endemic or travel context","Starting antiviral therapy too late, when its benefit is greatest early in the illness","Overlooking evolving hearing loss and failing to counsel about this recognised sequela"],"management":["Give the antiviral ribavirin as early as possible, which improves survival in severe disease","Provide intensive supportive care with careful fluid and electrolyte balance, correction of coagulopathy and bleeding, and organ support for shock and multi-organ failure","Nurse under strict isolation with barrier precautions, personal protective equipment, and safe specimen handling to prevent nosocomial and person-to-person transmission","Empirically exclude and treat co-endemic malaria and typhoid, which mimic early illness","In pregnancy, involve obstetrics early given high maternal and foetal mortality, as uterine evacuation may improve maternal outcome","Monitor for and rehabilitate sensorineural hearing loss, a characteristic complication that may appear during recovery","Notify public health authorities, trace contacts, and offer ribavirin post-exposure prophylaxis to high-risk contacts"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"latent_autoimmune_diabetes":{"name":"Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LADA","latent autoimmune diabetes of adults","type 1.5 diabetes","slowly progressive autoimmune diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A slowly progressive form of autoimmune diabetes presenting in adulthood, often mistaken for type 2 diabetes at diagnosis","Distinguishing features include adult onset, the presence of islet autoantibodies (typically anti-GAD), and a lack of the marked obesity and insulin resistance often seen in type 2 diabetes","Unlike classic type 1 diabetes, beta-cell destruction is gradual, so patients may not need insulin for months to years after diagnosis","It sits on a spectrum between type 1 and type 2 diabetes and is sometimes called type 1.5 diabetes","Suspect it when a leaner adult with apparent type 2 diabetes shows early failure of oral agents or unexpectedly rapid progression to insulin dependence","Anti-GAD antibodies are the most commonly positive marker and support the diagnosis","There may be an association with other organ-specific autoimmune conditions such as thyroid disease","Recognition matters because these patients progress to insulin requirement and benefit from monitoring beta-cell reserve rather than assuming a stable type 2 course"],"pathophysiology":"Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults is an autoimmune diabetes in which immune-mediated destruction of pancreatic beta cells proceeds more slowly than in classic childhood type 1 diabetes. Circulating islet autoantibodies, most often against glutamic acid decarboxylase, reflect this ongoing autoimmune process. Because residual beta-cell function persists at diagnosis, patients initially maintain some endogenous insulin secretion and may be controlled without insulin, but progressive loss of beta-cell mass leads to eventual insulin dependence. The disorder shares genetic and immunological features with type 1 diabetes while its adult onset and initial non-insulin-requiring course cause frequent misclassification as type 2 diabetes.","additionalDifferentials":["Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Classic type 1 diabetes mellitus","Maturity-onset diabetes of the young","Ketosis-prone diabetes","Secondary diabetes from pancreatic disease","Drug- or steroid-induced hyperglycaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Islet autoantibody testing, particularly anti-GAD antibodies","C-peptide to gauge residual beta-cell function and its decline over time","Standard glycaemic assessment including HbA1c","Screening for associated autoimmune disease such as thyroid dysfunction","Lipid profile and assessment of features of insulin resistance to help differentiate from type 2 diabetes","Periodic reassessment of beta-cell reserve to anticipate insulin requirement"],"redFlags":["Development of ketosis or ketoacidosis indicating significant insulin deficiency","Rapid deterioration of glycaemic control on oral agents","Unexplained weight loss suggesting advancing insulin deficiency","Coexisting autoimmune disorders such as adrenal or thyroid disease"],"prognosis":"Patients generally progress to insulin dependence over months to years as beta-cell function declines; with timely recognition and appropriate transition to insulin, glycaemic control and complication risk can be managed similarly to other forms of diabetes, and monitoring for associated autoimmune conditions is advisable.","pitfalls":["Assuming all adult-onset diabetes is type 2 and missing the autoimmune nature","Delaying insulin when oral agents are clearly failing","Not testing autoantibodies in a leaner adult with atypical type 2 diabetes","Overlooking associated autoimmune endocrine disease"],"management":["Transition to insulin replacement earlier than in type 2 diabetes as beta-cell function declines, since insulin dependence is ultimately inevitable","Avoid sulfonylureas, which may accelerate beta-cell exhaustion, and individualise use of metformin or incretin-based agents while endogenous secretion persists","Monitor glycaemic control with HbA1c and track residual beta-cell reserve with C-peptide to anticipate the need for insulin","Screen for and manage associated autoimmune disorders, particularly thyroid dysfunction","Provide structured diabetes education with dietary and lifestyle support and self-monitoring skills","Undertake systematic surveillance for retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, and cardiovascular risk as in other forms of diabetes","Educate on sick-day rules and prevention of diabetic ketoacidosis, and refer to specialist endocrine and diabetes services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"latent_nystagmus":{"name":"Latent nystagmus","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fusion maldevelopment nystagmus syndrome","manifest latent nystagmus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A conjugate horizontal jerk nystagmus that appears or intensifies when one eye is occluded, now termed fusion maldevelopment nystagmus syndrome","The fast phase beats toward the viewing (uncovered) eye, reversing direction depending on which eye fixates","It is strongly associated with early disruption of binocular vision, such as infantile esotropia and dissociated vertical deviation","When some nystagmus is present even with both eyes open it is called manifest latent nystagmus","Covering one eye to measure visual acuity can worsen the nystagmus and falsely reduce the measured acuity","Recognition matters mainly to avoid misinterpreting it as a sign of intracranial disease"],"pathophysiology":"Latent nystagmus arises from maldevelopment of binocular fusion during early infancy, typically in the context of infantile strabismus, which disturbs the normal cortical monocular motion processing that underlies gaze holding. Monocular occlusion unbalances this system and elicits a jerk nystagmus whose fast phase beats toward the fixating eye. The condition reflects a developmental abnormality of the visual motion pathways rather than an acquired brainstem or cerebellar lesion.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital (infantile) nystagmus syndrome","Acquired gaze-evoked nystagmus","Spasmus nutans","Peripheral or central vestibular nystagmus","Periodic alternating nystagmus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Orthoptic assessment of ocular alignment and fusion","Careful acuity testing that avoids full occlusion (using a fogging lens)","Documentation of the nystagmus with each eye fixating","Assessment for associated infantile esotropia and dissociated vertical deviation","Eye-movement recording where diagnostic doubt persists"],"redFlags":["Vertical or purely torsional nystagmus suggesting central disease","Associated neurological signs","New onset in an older child or adult","Optic atrophy or other afferent pathway signs"],"prognosis":"The condition itself is benign and stable; visual outcome depends on the associated strabismus and amblyopia, which should be managed on their own merits.","pitfalls":["Underestimating acuity by fully occluding one eye during testing","Mistaking it for acquired nystagmus and ordering unnecessary imaging","Missing the associated infantile strabismus and amblyopia","Confusing manifest latent nystagmus with congenital nystagmus syndrome"],"management":["There is no drug therapy; management targets the underlying infantile strabismus and any amblyopia on their own merits","Treat amblyopia with occlusion or atropine penalisation, but avoid abrupt full occlusion which intensifies the nystagmus, favouring fogging or partial penalisation","Correct significant refractive error with spectacles to optimise vision","Offer strabismus surgery for the associated infantile esotropia or dissociated vertical deviation as indicated","Test acuity monocularly using a fogging lens rather than a full occluder to avoid falsely reducing measured vision","Recognise the benign developmental nature to avoid unnecessary neuroimaging, and follow up with orthoptic assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"latent_tuberculosis":{"name":"Latent tuberculosis infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LTBI","latent TB","TB infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India NTEP","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Latent tuberculosis infection is a state of persistent immune response to Mycobacterium tuberculosis without clinical, radiological or bacteriological evidence of active disease","People with LTBI are asymptomatic and non-infectious, but carry a lifetime risk of reactivation into active tuberculosis, greatest in the first two years after infection","Diagnosis relies on the tuberculin skin test or interferon-gamma release assays, both of which detect immune sensitisation but cannot distinguish latent from active disease","Active tuberculosis must always be excluded, by symptom screening and chest imaging, before treating someone for latent infection","Reactivation risk is markedly increased by HIV, anti-TNF therapy, organ transplantation, silicosis, diabetes, malnutrition and other immunosuppressive states","TB preventive treatment substantially reduces the risk of progression, using rifamycin- or isoniazid-based short-course regimens without specifying doses","India's NTEP prioritises TB preventive treatment for household contacts of pulmonary TB, people living with HIV and other high-risk groups as part of the End TB strategy"],"pathophysiology":"After inhalation of Mycobacterium tuberculosis, most immunocompetent individuals mount a cell-mediated immune response that contains the organism within granulomas, where bacilli persist in a dormant, metabolically quiescent state. This equilibrium between host immunity and bacillary persistence defines latency, and there is likely a spectrum from complete eradication to subclinical infection. When immunity wanes or is suppressed, dormant bacilli can resume replication, breaching the granuloma and producing active, potentially transmissible disease. The tuberculin and interferon-gamma tests measure the memory T-cell response to mycobacterial antigens rather than the presence of live bacilli.","additionalDifferentials":["Active pulmonary tuberculosis (must be excluded)","Prior BCG vaccination causing tuberculin reactivity","Non-tuberculous mycobacterial sensitisation","Old healed tuberculosis on imaging without active disease"],"infectionMimics":["Subclinical active tuberculosis","Non-tuberculous mycobacterial exposure causing test cross-reactivity","BCG-related tuberculin positivity"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Tuberculin skin test or interferon-gamma release assay to detect infection","Symptom screening and chest radiography to exclude active tuberculosis","HIV testing given its major impact on reactivation risk","Assessment for other risk factors such as diabetes, immunosuppressive therapy and silicosis","Sputum evaluation if any features suggest active disease"],"redFlags":["Any symptoms or imaging findings suggesting active tuberculosis, which changes management entirely","Recent close contact with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis, especially in children under five","Planned immunosuppression such as anti-TNF therapy or transplantation","Coexisting HIV infection markedly raising reactivation risk"],"prognosis":"Most people with latent infection never develop active disease, but progression risk is highest soon after infection and in the immunocompromised; TB preventive treatment substantially lowers this risk and is central to India's elimination goals.","pitfalls":["Starting preventive treatment without first excluding active tuberculosis, risking monotherapy of active disease and resistance","Interpreting a positive test as active disease when it only indicates infection","Overlooking preventive treatment in eligible high-risk contacts and people living with HIV","Ignoring the reduced sensitivity of immune-based tests in severe immunosuppression"],"management":["Give TB preventive treatment using a rifamycin-based short-course regimen (rifampicin or rifapentine with isoniazid) or isoniazid monotherapy to reduce progression to active disease","Exclude active tuberculosis by symptom screening and chest radiography before starting preventive treatment","Test for and treat HIV, and assess other reactivation risks such as anti-TNF therapy, transplantation, silicosis, and diabetes that heighten the need for preventive treatment","Monitor adherence and watch for drug-induced hepatotoxicity during the treatment course, with liver-function checks in those at risk","Prioritise preventive treatment in high-risk groups including HIV co-infection and recent contacts of infectious cases","Educate on symptoms of reactivation and escalate to full multidrug treatment if active tuberculosis develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/NTEP guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"lateral_epicondylitis":{"name":"Lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tennis elbow","lateral epicondylalgia","common extensor origin tendinopathy","lateral elbow tendinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lateral elbow pain arising from the common extensor origin, chiefly extensor carpi radialis brevis","Despite the '-itis' name it is a degenerative tendinopathy (angiofibroblastic tendinosis) from overuse, not primary inflammation","Provoked by gripping and wrist and finger extension; pain is reproduced by resisted wrist extension and by Cozen's test","Tenderness is typically just distal and anterior to the lateral epicondyle","Diagnosis is largely clinical, with ultrasound or MRI reserved for uncertain or refractory cases","Often self-limiting; management is load modification, avoiding aggravating grip activity, and progressive or eccentric loading with physiotherapy","Corticosteroid injection may give short-term relief but is associated with poorer longer-term outcomes and possible tendon harm, so it is used cautiously","Counterforce bracing, and shockwave therapy in resistant cases, may help"],"pathophysiology":"Lateral epicondylitis is an overuse tendinopathy of the common extensor origin at the lateral humeral epicondyle, predominantly involving extensor carpi radialis brevis. Repetitive gripping and wrist extension generate cumulative tensile load that exceeds the tendon's repair capacity. Histology shows angiofibroblastic degeneration with disorganised collagen, increased ground substance and neovascularisation, and a notable absence of inflammatory cells, so it is best regarded as a tendinosis rather than a true '-itis'. Chronic change reflects a failed healing response at the enthesis.","additionalDifferentials":["Radial tunnel syndrome (posterior interosseous nerve entrapment)","Radiocapitellar osteoarthritis","Cervical radiculopathy (C6 or C7)","Posterolateral rotatory instability of the elbow","Osteochondral or plica pathology of the radiocapitellar joint","Referred pain from the shoulder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical provocative tests (resisted wrist extension, Cozen's test, Mill's test)","Ultrasound to confirm common extensor tendinopathy or a tear","MRI in refractory or diagnostically unclear cases","Plain radiographs to assess the joint and exclude calcification or arthritis","Nerve conduction studies if radial tunnel syndrome or radiculopathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neurological symptoms suggesting nerve entrapment or cervical radiculopathy","Night pain, systemic upset or a mass suggesting other pathology","Locking or instability implying intra-articular derangement","Failure to respond, prompting reassessment of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The majority recover within a year with load management and rehabilitation, although symptoms can be prolonged; a minority with refractory disease may need shockwave therapy or, rarely, surgery.","pitfalls":["Treating as inflammatory and relying on rest or repeated steroid injections rather than progressive loading","Corticosteroid injection giving short-term relief but poorer long-term outcomes","Missing radial tunnel syndrome, which can mimic or coexist","Overlooking the cervical spine as a source of referred pain"],"management":["First-line management is load and activity modification, avoiding aggravating gripping and repetitive wrist extension while maintaining function","Refer for physiotherapy with a progressive, eccentric extensor-loading programme, the cornerstone of recovery","Provide symptomatic relief with topical or short-course oral NSAIDs and a counterforce forearm brace","Reserve corticosteroid injection for short-term relief only, recognising poorer long-term outcomes, and consider platelet-rich plasma in selected refractory cases","Offer extracorporeal shockwave therapy, and rarely surgical debridement of the extensor origin, for disease refractory to sustained conservative care","Reassure on the generally self-limiting natural history, and investigate atypical cases with nerve conduction studies to exclude radial tunnel syndrome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","British Journal of Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"lateral_medullary_syndrome":{"name":"Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Wallenberg syndrome","posterior inferior cerebellar artery syndrome","PICA syndrome","lateral bulbar syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from infarction of the dorsolateral medulla, most often due to intracranial vertebral artery disease rather than a discrete posterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion","The hallmark is a crossed sensory pattern: ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature over the face with contralateral loss over the trunk and limbs","Vertigo, nausea, vomiting, and nystagmus reflect vestibular nucleus involvement and are frequently the presenting complaint","Ipsilateral Horner syndrome (partial ptosis, miosis, facial anhidrosis) arises from disruption of the descending sympathetic fibres","Dysphagia, hoarseness, and reduced gag reflex follow nucleus ambiguus involvement and carry a genuine aspiration risk","Ipsilateral limb and gait ataxia occur from inferior cerebellar peduncle and cerebellar involvement, and intractable hiccups may be prominent","Motor power is characteristically preserved because the pyramidal tract lies ventromedially and is spared"],"pathophysiology":"The syndrome follows ischaemia of the dorsolateral medulla oblongata, a territory supplied by penetrating branches of the intracranial vertebral artery and the posterior inferior cerebellar artery. Because vertebral disease is the usual culprit, the infarct straddles several closely packed structures: the spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract (ipsilateral facial sensory loss), the crossed spinothalamic tract (contralateral body sensory loss), the vestibular nuclei (vertigo and nystagmus), the nucleus ambiguus (dysphagia and hoarseness), the descending sympathetic pathway (Horner syndrome), and the inferior cerebellar peduncle (ipsilateral ataxia). The medial position of the corticospinal tract explains the preservation of limb strength, producing the classic dissociated, crossed clinical picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Medial medullary (Dejerine) syndrome","Vestibular neuronitis or acute peripheral vertigo","Cerebellar infarction or haemorrhage","Demyelinating plaque of the brainstem in multiple sclerosis","Vertebral artery dissection presenting with posterior circulation ischaemia","Brainstem neoplasm or metastasis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diffusion-weighted MRI of the brainstem to confirm acute dorsolateral medullary infarction","MR or CT angiography of the vertebrobasilar system to identify vertebral stenosis or dissection","HINTS bedside oculomotor examination to distinguish central from peripheral vertigo","Formal swallow assessment to quantify aspiration risk before oral intake","Cardiac and vascular risk workup including ECG and lipid and glucose profiling","Vessel wall imaging where dissection or intracranial atherosclerosis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive drowsiness or new long-tract signs suggesting brainstem or cerebellar swelling with mass effect","Silent aspiration with subsequent pneumonia in an apparently mild presentation","Vertebral artery dissection with risk of recurrent embolism or basilar extension","Central vertigo mistaken for benign peripheral disease and discharged"],"prognosis":"Many patients recover functional swallowing and gait over weeks to months, though disabling ataxia, persistent dysphagia, or central neuropathic facial pain can remain; overall outcome is generally more favourable than for medial or ventral brainstem strokes.","pitfalls":["Attributing isolated vertigo and vomiting to a peripheral labyrinthine cause and missing the stroke","Allowing oral feeding before a formal swallow assessment and precipitating aspiration","Overlooking a subtle ipsilateral Horner syndrome that would localise the lesion","Assuming preserved limb power excludes a significant brainstem infarct"],"management":["Manage as an acute posterior-circulation stroke, considering intravenous thrombolysis within the window then antiplatelet therapy, while imaging for vertebral artery dissection which may instead warrant anticoagulation","Institute secondary prevention with antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy, high-intensity statin, and control of blood pressure, glucose, and other vascular risk factors","Keep nil by mouth until formal swallow assessment, using nasogastric feeding and aspiration precautions given the high dysphagia and aspiration risk","Treat vertigo and nausea with vestibular sedatives and antiemetics, and control intractable hiccups with agents such as baclofen or chlorpromazine","Manage persistent central neuropathic facial pain with neuropathic agents","Provide multidisciplinary rehabilitation with physiotherapy for ataxia and speech-and-language therapy for swallowing and voice","Monitor for cerebellar swelling and brainstem compression, escalating to neurosurgical care if deterioration occurs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":["medial_medullary_syndrome","locked_in_syndrome"]},"lateral_pontine_syndrome":{"name":"Lateral pontine (Marie-Foix) syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Marie-Foix syndrome","anterior inferior cerebellar artery syndrome","AICA syndrome","lateral inferior pontine syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from infarction of the lateral pons, most often in the territory of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery arising from the basilar artery","Ipsilateral facial weakness, hearing loss, and vertigo follow involvement of the facial and vestibulocochlear nerve nuclei and fibres","Ipsilateral limb ataxia reflects damage to the middle cerebellar peduncle and cerebellar input","A crossed sensory pattern occurs with ipsilateral facial and contralateral body loss of pain and temperature from trigeminal and spinothalamic involvement","Ipsilateral Horner syndrome may arise from disruption of descending sympathetic fibres","Sudden unilateral deafness is a distinguishing feature because the labyrinthine artery commonly arises from the anterior inferior cerebellar artery","Motor power is usually preserved as the corticospinal tract lies more medially in the basis pontis"],"pathophysiology":"Lateral pontine syndrome follows ischaemia of the lateral pontine tegmentum and adjacent cerebellum, supplied chiefly by the anterior inferior cerebellar artery, a branch of the basilar artery. The infarct affects the facial nucleus and nerve (ipsilateral facial weakness), the vestibulocochlear nucleus and often the labyrinthine artery territory (vertigo and ipsilateral hearing loss), the trigeminal nucleus and crossing spinothalamic tract (ipsilateral facial and contralateral body sensory loss), the middle cerebellar peduncle (ipsilateral ataxia), and the descending sympathetic fibres (Horner syndrome). The medial location of the corticospinal tract usually spares limb strength, giving a crossed brainstem picture analogous to Wallenberg syndrome but at pontine level and with prominent auditory involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome","Vestibular neuronitis or labyrinthitis","Cerebellopontine angle lesion such as vestibular schwannoma","Cerebellar infarction or haemorrhage","Pontine demyelination or tumour","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss of peripheral origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diffusion-weighted MRI of the lateral pons and cerebellum to confirm the infarct","MR or CT angiography of the basilar artery and its cerebellar branches","Audiometry and vestibular testing to characterise the auditory and balance deficit","HINTS bedside oculomotor examination to distinguish central from peripheral vertigo","Cardiovascular and stroke risk workup including ECG and metabolic profiling","Vessel wall imaging where basilar atherosclerosis or dissection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive drowsiness or brainstem compression from associated cerebellar swelling","Evolving basilar thrombosis with risk of extensive pontine infarction","Central vertigo misattributed to a peripheral cause and discharged","New long-tract or bulbar signs suggesting medial extension"],"prognosis":"Vertigo and facial weakness often improve over weeks, but ipsilateral hearing loss may be permanent and cerebellar ataxia can persist; outcome is generally favourable unless the infarct is large or heralds basilar disease.","pitfalls":["Attributing acute vertigo and hearing loss to a peripheral labyrinthine cause and missing the stroke","Overlooking a subtle Horner syndrome that helps localise the lesion","Confusing lateral pontine with lateral medullary syndrome when auditory signs are not sought","Assuming preserved limb power excludes a significant brainstem infarct"],"management":["Manage as an acute basilar-territory stroke with thrombolysis within the window then antiplatelet therapy, imaging for basilar atherosclerosis or dissection","Start secondary prevention with antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy, statin, and aggressive vascular risk-factor control","Protect against aspiration with swallow assessment and modified feeding while facial weakness and bulbar dysfunction persist","Treat vertigo, nausea, and vomiting with vestibular sedatives and antiemetics","Manage sudden sensorineural hearing loss with audiological rehabilitation, and protect the eye in facial-nerve palsy with lubrication and taping","Deliver multidisciplinary rehabilitation with physiotherapy for cerebellar ataxia and speech and swallow therapy","Monitor for evolving basilar disease and posterior-fossa swelling, escalating to neurosurgery if brainstem compression develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":["lateral_medullary_syndrome","millard_gubler_syndrome","foville_syndrome"]},"latex_allergy":{"name":"Latex allergy","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["natural rubber latex allergy","latex hypersensitivity","NRL allergy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["IgE-mediated (type I) reactions to natural rubber latex proteins range from contact urticaria to rhinoconjunctivitis, asthma, and anaphylaxis, and are distinct from irritant or allergic contact dermatitis from rubber-processing chemicals","Healthcare workers, patients with spina bifida or urogenital anomalies, and those with repeated surgical or catheter exposure are at highest risk owing to frequent mucosal and cutaneous contact","Powdered gloves historically amplified sensitisation because latex allergen adsorbed to cornstarch became airborne, so a shift to powder-free low-protein or non-latex gloves has markedly reduced new cases","The latex-fruit syndrome reflects cross-reactivity between latex proteins and plant allergens, so patients may also react to banana, avocado, kiwi, and chestnut","Diagnosis combines a compatible history with skin testing and serum latex-specific IgE; a validated skin test extract may not be uniformly available, making the history and in-vitro testing important","Reactions can occur through skin, mucosal, inhalational, or parenteral routes, and intraoperative anaphylaxis may be the presenting event when latex is contacted internally","Cornerstones of care are strict avoidance, a latex-safe environment for procedures, medical-alert identification, and a self-injectable adrenaline device for those with systemic reactions","Type IV allergic contact dermatitis from rubber accelerators (e.g. thiurams, carbamates) presents as delayed eczema and is confirmed by patch testing, not by IgE tests"],"pathophysiology":"Type I latex allergy results from IgE sensitisation to water-soluble proteins in natural rubber harvested from Hevea brasiliensis. Repeated cutaneous, mucosal, or airborne exposure drives production of latex-specific IgE bound to mast cells and basophils; re-exposure cross-links this IgE and triggers mediator release causing urticaria, bronchospasm, and anaphylaxis. Airborne allergen bound to glove powder facilitates respiratory sensitisation. Structural homology between latex proteins (e.g. hevein-related and profilin allergens) and plant proteins accounts for cross-reactivity in the latex-fruit syndrome. A separate delayed, T-cell-mediated contact dermatitis is caused not by latex protein but by rubber-manufacturing chemicals.","additionalDifferentials":["Irritant contact dermatitis from gloves or hand hygiene","Type IV allergic contact dermatitis to rubber accelerator chemicals","Anaphylaxis from perioperative drugs, antiseptics (chlorhexidine), or antibiotics","Food-related latex-fruit cross-reactive allergy","Idiopathic urticaria or angioedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum latex-specific IgE testing","Skin prick testing with a standardised latex extract where available","Patch testing for suspected delayed contact dermatitis to rubber chemicals","Serum tryptase during a suspected acute systemic reaction","Supervised glove-use or challenge testing in specialist settings when the diagnosis is uncertain"],"redFlags":["Intraoperative or procedural anaphylaxis with hypotension or bronchospasm requiring immediate intramuscular adrenaline and removal of all latex sources","Progressive airway or respiratory symptoms after mucosal or inhalational latex exposure","Systemic reaction in a high-risk patient (spina bifida, multiple surgeries) not previously identified as latex-allergic","Recurrent reactions despite avoidance suggesting unrecognised hidden latex exposure"],"prognosis":"With reliable avoidance and a latex-safe healthcare environment, most patients avoid further reactions and occupational sensitisation often improves after exposure ceases. Unrecognised sensitisation, however, can lead to severe or fatal perioperative anaphylaxis.","pitfalls":["Confusing delayed contact dermatitis from rubber chemicals with true IgE-mediated latex allergy","Failing to arrange a latex-safe environment for surgery or dental and radiological procedures","Overlooking cross-reactive foods in the latex-fruit syndrome","Not counselling the patient to carry medical-alert identification and an adrenaline device"],"management":["The cornerstone is strict avoidance of natural rubber latex within a latex-safe environment, substituting powder-free non-latex (nitrile or vinyl) gloves and equipment","Equip patients for anaphylaxis with an adrenaline auto-injector plus antihistamine and provide a written emergency action plan","Ensure a latex-free perioperative pathway, scheduling affected patients first on the list and flagging the allergy prominently to prevent intraoperative anaphylaxis","Counsel on latex-fruit cross-reactivity, advising caution with banana, avocado, kiwi, and chestnut","Reduce occupational exposure through powder-free low-protein or non-latex gloves for at-risk healthcare workers","Provide a medical-alert identifier and ensure the allergy is documented across all care settings","Refer to allergy and immunology for confirmatory latex-specific IgE and skin testing and ongoing management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"latrodectism":{"name":"Latrodectism (widow spider envenomation)","system":"Multisystem / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["black widow spider bite","widow spider envenomation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Envenomation by widow spiders causes painful muscle cramps and spasm that spread from the bite site to the abdomen, back and limbs","Severe abdominal rigidity can mimic an acute surgical abdomen","Autonomic features such as hypertension, tachycardia, sweating and restlessness are common","The local bite may be surprisingly minor despite intense systemic symptoms","Most cases are managed with analgesia and supportive care, with antivenom reserved for severe envenoming","The characteristic pattern of diffuse painful spasms with autonomic upset helps distinguish it from other bites"],"pathophysiology":"Widow spider venom contains alpha-latrotoxin, which binds presynaptic nerve terminals and triggers massive release of neurotransmitters including acetylcholine and noradrenaline, followed by depletion. This excessive neurotransmission produces sustained muscle contraction, pain and autonomic overactivity. The systemic syndrome of cramping pain and autonomic disturbance reflects this widespread presynaptic effect rather than local tissue destruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute abdomen from a surgical cause","Other spider or arthropod envenomation","Tetanus","Hypocalcaemic or metabolic tetany","Sympathomimetic toxidrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the bite history and characteristic spasms","Assessment of vital signs for autonomic disturbance","Exclusion of a surgical abdomen where abdominal rigidity dominates","Basic bloods and electrolytes to exclude metabolic mimics","Cardiac monitoring in severe autonomic involvement"],"redFlags":["Severe uncontrolled hypertension or chest pain","Respiratory distress","Priapism or marked autonomic instability","Symptoms in very young, elderly or pregnant patients"],"prognosis":"The condition is rarely fatal and symptoms usually settle over hours to a few days; antivenom can shorten severe episodes but carries a risk of hypersensitivity.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the abdominal rigidity for a surgical emergency and operating","Underestimating pain severity despite a minor-looking bite","Overlooking autonomic complications","Using antivenom without weighing hypersensitivity risk"],"management":["Treat most cases with analgesia, including opioids, and muscle relaxants such as benzodiazepines to relieve the painful cramps and spasms","Reserve widow-spider antivenom for severe or refractory envenoming, weighing its benefit against the risk of hypersensitivity","Manage autonomic disturbance with monitoring and control of hypertension and tachycardia","Provide local wound care and ensure tetanus prophylaxis is up to date","Exclude an acute surgical abdomen where abdominal rigidity dominates before attributing it to envenomation","Observe with cardiac monitoring in significant autonomic involvement and reassure that symptoms usually settle over hours to days"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"lattice_corneal_dystrophy":{"name":"Lattice corneal dystrophy","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LCD","TGFBI-related lattice dystrophy","Biber-Haab-Dimmer dystrophy","gelsolin amyloidosis (lattice type II / Meretoja syndrome)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology / Cornea by Krachmer","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A stromal dystrophy characterised by branching refractile lattice lines representing amyloid deposition, best seen on slit-lamp retroillumination","The common form (type I) is autosomal dominant and caused by mutations in the TGFBI gene on chromosome 5q31, part of the TGFBI dystrophy cluster","Amyloid deposits stain with Congo red and show apple-green birefringence and dichroism under polarised light","Recurrent corneal erosions are frequent early in the disease and can dominate the symptomatic picture before vision declines","Progressive stromal haze between the lattice lines eventually reduces vision as deposits coalesce over decades","Type II lattice dystrophy (Meretoja syndrome) is a systemic gelsolin amyloidosis with cranial and peripheral neuropathy, facial mask-like features, and cutis laxa in addition to sparse corneal lattice lines","Management is largely supportive with lubrication and treatment of erosions, with phototherapeutic keratectomy or lamellar/penetrating keratoplasty for advanced visual loss","Recurrence of deposits within a corneal graft over time is characteristic and should be anticipated when counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Lattice corneal dystrophy results from the abnormal deposition of amyloid within the corneal stroma. In the classic autosomal dominant form, mutations in the transforming growth factor beta-induced gene (TGFBI) produce an abnormal protein that misfolds and aggregates as amyloid fibrils, forming the branching lattice lines and intervening haze. In the systemic type II variant (Meretoja syndrome), mutations in the gelsolin gene lead to widespread amyloid deposition affecting the cornea, cranial and peripheral nerves, and skin. Amyloid accumulation progressively disrupts stromal transparency and undermines epithelial adhesion, producing recurrent erosions and gradual loss of vision.","additionalDifferentials":["Granular corneal dystrophy and combined granular-lattice (Avellino) dystrophy","Macular corneal dystrophy","Amyloid deposition secondary to chronic ocular surface disease (polymorphic amyloid degeneration)","Recurrent corneal erosion syndrome from other basement membrane dystrophy","Stromal scarring from prior keratitis","Reis-Bucklers and Thiel-Behnke anterior stromal dystrophies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp biomicroscopy with retroillumination to identify branching lattice lines","Histology of corneal tissue with Congo red staining showing apple-green birefringence","Genetic testing for TGFBI mutations to confirm type and distinguish from gelsolin type II","Anterior segment optical coherence tomography to map depth of deposits","Systemic evaluation for neuropathy and skin changes when Meretoja syndrome is suspected","Corneal topography to assess irregular astigmatism before surgical planning"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive visual loss suggesting dense confluent stromal amyloid","Systemic features of cranial or peripheral neuropathy indicating gelsolin type II amyloidosis","Severe or non-healing epithelial defects raising concern for secondary infection","Recurrence of deposits in a corneal graft threatening the visual result"],"prognosis":"Vision typically declines slowly over decades, with recurrent erosions often preceding visual loss. Keratoplasty can restore clarity but amyloid deposits frequently recur in the graft, and the systemic type II form carries additional neurological morbidity.","pitfalls":["Attributing recurrent erosions to trauma or dry eye and missing the underlying dystrophy","Failing to recognise systemic features that point to Meretoja gelsolin amyloidosis","Not counselling patients about the near-inevitable recurrence of deposits after grafting","Overlooking the shared TGFBI origin with granular dystrophy when interpreting mixed phenotypes"],"management":["No therapy halts amyloid deposition, so treat recurrent corneal erosions with ocular lubricants, hypertonic saline, bandage contact lenses, and epithelial debridement","Use phototherapeutic keratectomy to remove superficial deposits and manage recurrent erosions and mild visual loss","Offer lamellar or penetrating keratoplasty for advanced visual decline, counselling that amyloid frequently recurs within the graft","Assess and address irregular astigmatism with topography-guided planning before surgery","In Meretoja (type II) disease, evaluate and manage the systemic gelsolin amyloidosis with its cranial and peripheral neuropathy and skin involvement","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal dominant TGFBI inheritance and screen affected relatives","Follow up with slit-lamp surveillance to monitor deposit progression and graft health"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Cornea by Krachmer, Mannis and Holland"],"crossLinks":[]},"lattice_degeneration":{"name":"Lattice degeneration","system":"Special senses / Eye (peripheral retina)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lattice retinal degeneration","peripheral lattice degeneration"],"chapter":null,"source":"American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice Pattern / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest clinically significant peripheral retinal degeneration predisposing to retinal breaks, more frequent in myopic eyes and often bilateral","Appears as sharply demarcated, circumferentially oriented, spindle-shaped areas of retinal thinning, usually anterior to the equator, often crossed by a network of white sclerosed vessels (the lattice) with pigment clumping and yellow-white flecks","Overlying the lesion the vitreous is liquefied, while at its margins there is exaggerated, firm vitreoretinal adhesion, and it is this combination that predisposes to tears","Two break types occur: atrophic round holes within the lesion, which are more common, lower risk and often seen in younger myopes, and tractional tears at the posterior or lateral margins during posterior vitreous detachment, which carry a higher detachment risk","Although common in the general population, only a minority of eyes with lattice ever develop retinal detachment, so routine prophylactic treatment of asymptomatic lattice is generally not recommended","Prophylactic laser retinopexy or cryotherapy is considered in higher-risk situations, for example symptomatic tears, the fellow eye of a patient who has had a retinal detachment, or before intraocular surgery in selected cases","Patients should be counselled about retinal detachment symptoms such as flashes, new floaters and a field defect and advised to seek urgent review if these occur"],"pathophysiology":"Lattice degeneration is characterised by focal thinning of the inner retinal layers with an overlying pocket of liquefied vitreous and abnormally strong vitreoretinal adhesion at the margins of the lesion. Sclerosis and hyalinisation of retinal vessels crossing the lesion produce the characteristic white branching lattice pattern, while disturbance of the retinal pigment epithelium gives the pigmentary changes. Atrophic round holes may develop within the thinned retina. The principal risk arises during posterior vitreous detachment, when dynamic traction transmitted to the firm marginal adhesions can tear the retina at the edge of a lattice lesion, allowing liquefied vitreous to enter the subretinal space and produce a rhegmatogenous retinal detachment.","additionalDifferentials":["Other peripheral retinal degenerations such as snailtrack degeneration, paving-stone (cobblestone) degeneration and white-without-pressure","Peripheral chorioretinal atrophy or old chorioretinitis scarring","Retinal holes or breaks from other causes","Peripheral retinoschisis","Pigmented lesions such as congenital hypertrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium","Peripheral chorioretinal changes of high myopia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated indirect ophthalmoscopy with scleral indentation to examine the peripheral retina","Slit-lamp biomicroscopy with a wide-field or three-mirror lens to characterise lesions and their margins","Wide-field fundus imaging to document extent and to monitor over time","Examination of the fellow eye given the frequent bilaterality","OCT where available to assess associated retinoschisis or subclinical detachment","Assessment for a symptomatic break, including Shafer's sign and subretinal fluid, if new symptoms develop"],"redFlags":["New flashes, a shower of floaters or a field defect suggesting a retinal tear or detachment","Subretinal fluid extending from a break","Tobacco dust (Shafer's sign) in the vitreous","A tractional tear at the margin of a lattice lesion, particularly during acute posterior vitreous detachment"],"prognosis":"Most eyes with lattice degeneration remain stable and never develop retinal detachment, so the overall prognosis is good. Risk is higher in high myopes and in the fellow eye of a patient who has already suffered a detachment, and outcomes are favourable when breaks are detected and treated promptly.","pitfalls":["Over-treating asymptomatic lattice with prophylactic laser when the evidence does not support routine intervention","Failing to examine the full peripheral retina with indentation and so missing lattice or an associated break","Neglecting to examine the fellow eye","Providing inadequate detachment-symptom safety-netting to patients with known lattice","Assuming laser retinopexy abolishes all future detachment risk"],"management":["Observe asymptomatic lattice without treatment in most eyes, since only a minority ever progress to retinal detachment","Apply prophylactic laser retinopexy or cryotherapy in higher-risk situations such as symptomatic tears, the fellow eye after a detachment, or before intraocular surgery in selected cases","Educate patients on retinal-detachment warning symptoms of new floaters, flashes, or a visual-field shadow, prompting urgent review","Repair any resulting rhegmatogenous retinal detachment promptly with appropriate vitreoretinal surgery","Monitor with dilated indirect ophthalmoscopy and scleral indentation, examining the frequently affected fellow eye","Give particular attention to high myopes, who carry greater detachment risk, and escalate on new symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice Pattern on posterior vitreous detachment, retinal breaks and lattice degeneration","Ryan's Retina","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach"],"crossLinks":[]},"lchad_deficiency":{"name":"Long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LCHAD deficiency","LCHADD","HADHA deficiency","mitochondrial trifunctional protein alpha-subunit deficiency","long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-coenzyme A dehydrogenase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by HADHA variants affecting the alpha subunit of the mitochondrial trifunctional protein, which carries the long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase activity; the common c.1528G>C variant is characteristic","Distinctive complications not seen in most other oxidation defects are progressive pigmentary retinopathy and peripheral neuropathy","Carrying an affected fetus is strongly associated with maternal acute fatty liver of pregnancy and HELLP syndrome","Newborn screening shows elevated long-chain hydroxyacylcarnitines, notably 3-hydroxytetradecanoylcarnitine (C16-OH) and related hydroxy species","Acute presentations include hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, cardiomyopathy, hepatic dysfunction and rhabdomyolysis, overlapping with generalised trifunctional protein deficiency","Management combines long-chain fat restriction with medium-chain triglyceride substitution, avoidance of fasting and an emergency regimen during illness","Regular ophthalmological surveillance is needed because retinopathy can progress despite metabolic control"],"pathophysiology":"The mitochondrial trifunctional protein is a membrane-bound complex that catalyses three sequential steps of long-chain fatty-acid beta-oxidation; isolated LCHAD deficiency reflects selective loss of the 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase activity of its alpha subunit. Blocked long-chain oxidation impairs energy supply to heart, muscle and liver during fasting and stress and prevents hepatic ketogenesis, while accumulation of long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl metabolites is thought to be toxic and to underlie the retinopathy and neuropathy. Transplacental passage of these hydroxy intermediates from an affected fetus is implicated in the maternal liver disease of pregnancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Generalised mitochondrial trifunctional protein deficiency (alpha and beta subunit involvement)","Very-long-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II and translocase deficiencies","Other causes of infantile cardiomyopathy or hepatopathy","Inherited retinal dystrophies and peripheral neuropathies of other cause","Sepsis or metabolic acidosis in a decompensating infant"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma acylcarnitine profile showing raised long-chain hydroxyacylcarnitines (e.g. C16-OH, C18:1-OH)","Urine organic acids seeking 3-hydroxydicarboxylic aciduria","Blood glucose, ketones, ammonia, lactate and liver function during illness","Ophthalmological assessment and electroretinography for retinopathy, and nerve conduction studies for neuropathy","HADHA molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Echocardiography and creatine kinase during acute decompensation"],"redFlags":["Maternal acute fatty liver of pregnancy or HELLP syndrome prompting testing of the infant","Hypoketotic hypoglycaemia with encephalopathy or cardiomyopathy","Rhabdomyolysis with pigmenturia and acute kidney injury risk","Progressive visual loss or neuropathic symptoms indicating chronic complications"],"prognosis":"Early screen-detected diagnosis and dietary management improve survival and reduce acute crises, but retinopathy and neuropathy may progress over time, so long-term morbidity remains a concern even with good metabolic control.","pitfalls":["Not testing the neonate after maternal acute fatty liver of pregnancy or HELLP syndrome","Overlooking retinopathy or neuropathy because acute metabolic control appears adequate","Using unrestricted long-chain fat instead of medium-chain triglyceride substitution","Permitting fasting for procedures without a glucose-containing infusion"],"management":["Base dietary treatment on long-chain fat restriction with medium-chain triglyceride substitution, frequent feeds, and complex carbohydrate to bypass the block in long-chain fat oxidation, with triheptanoin as an anaplerotic fat source in some","Strictly avoid fasting and implement an emergency regimen of high carbohydrate intake, with intravenous glucose during illness, to prevent catabolic decompensation","Supplement docosahexaenoic acid to slow the characteristic pigmentary retinopathy","Manage acute crises with intravenous dextrose and treatment of hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, rhabdomyolysis, and cardiomyopathy","Undertake surveillance with acylcarnitine profiles, growth and dietary review, echocardiography and creatine kinase, and ophthalmological (electroretinography) and nerve-conduction monitoring for retinopathy and neuropathy","Coordinate care through a specialist metabolic team with dietetic support","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal recessive HADHA inheritance, offer family and newborn screening, and counsel mothers on the risk of acute fatty liver of pregnancy and HELLP when carrying an affected foetus"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"lead_nephropathy":{"name":"Lead nephropathy","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["saturnine nephropathy","lead-induced kidney disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nephrology and toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Chronic kidney disease caused by prolonged lead exposure, producing a chronic tubulointerstitial nephropathy","It is classically associated with hyperuricaemia and gout (saturnine gout) and with hypertension","Acute high-dose exposure in children can cause a reversible proximal tubulopathy (Fanconi-type) with lead inclusion bodies","Sources include occupational exposure, old lead paint and pipes, contaminated traditional remedies and illicit alcohol","Blood lead reflects recent exposure; body burden may be assessed with a chelation-mobilisation test where available","Management is removal from exposure and treatment of hypertension, with chelation for significant lead burden"],"pathophysiology":"Lead nephropathy results from accumulation of lead in renal tubular cells, where it impairs mitochondrial and enzymatic function and, with chronic exposure, causes tubulointerstitial fibrosis and progressive chronic kidney disease. Lead also reduces renal urate excretion, producing hyperuricaemia and gout, and contributes to hypertension, which further damages the kidney. In acute childhood poisoning, lead causes a proximal tubular dysfunction with characteristic intranuclear inclusion bodies that is often reversible.","additionalDifferentials":["Other chronic tubulointerstitial nephropathies (analgesic, aristolochic acid)","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","Gouty (urate) nephropathy of other cause","Chronic kidney disease of other aetiology","Cadmium or other heavy-metal nephropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood lead level and exposure history","Assessment of renal function, urate and blood pressure","Lead-mobilisation (chelation) test for body burden where indicated","Urinalysis and markers of tubular dysfunction","Occupational and environmental exposure assessment"],"redFlags":["Progressive chronic kidney disease with ongoing exposure","Features of systemic lead toxicity (neurological, anaemia)","Severe hypertension","Childhood exposure with encephalopathy"],"prognosis":"Removing exposure and controlling hypertension can stabilise renal function, and acute childhood tubulopathy may reverse, but established chronic interstitial fibrosis is irreversible.","pitfalls":["Overlooking lead as a cause of chronic kidney disease with gout and hypertension","Failing to take an occupational and environmental exposure history","Relying only on blood lead, which reflects recent rather than cumulative exposure","Not addressing ongoing exposure sources"],"management":["Definitive management is removal from ongoing lead exposure, with source identification and occupational hygiene measures","Chelation therapy (for example succimer or sodium calcium edetate) for a significant lead body burden confirmed on blood lead or a mobilisation test","Control hypertension with renoprotective agents such as ACE inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers to slow progression","Treat saturnine gout and hyperuricaemia with urate-lowering therapy such as allopurinol, plus anti-inflammatory treatment for acute flares","Correct concurrent iron deficiency, which increases gastrointestinal lead absorption","Monitor renal function, blood pressure, serum urate and blood lead over time, with general chronic kidney disease and cardiovascular risk measures","Refer to nephrology and occupational medicine, escalating to renal replacement planning if progressive chronic kidney disease supervenes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nephrology reference texts (standard reference)","Occupational medicine references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"lead_poisoning":{"name":"Lead poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["plumbism","saturnism","lead toxicity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A cumulative heavy-metal poisoning from ingestion or inhalation of lead, historically from paint, contaminated water, traditional remedies, batteries and occupational dust","Children are especially vulnerable, with neurodevelopmental harm at blood levels once thought safe; no threshold is considered entirely safe","Chronic exposure causes abdominal colic, constipation, anaemia, peripheral (motor) neuropathy and cognitive impairment","A blue line at the gingival margin (Burton line) and basophilic stippling of red cells are classic but insensitive signs","Encephalopathy with raised intracranial pressure is a life-threatening presentation, more common in young children","Diagnosis rests on a raised blood lead concentration; anaemia may be microcytic or normocytic","Management combines exposure removal with chelation therapy for significant toxicity, alongside public-health source control"],"pathophysiology":"Lead has no physiological role and disrupts multiple systems by binding sulfhydryl groups and displacing essential divalent cations such as calcium and zinc. It inhibits several enzymes of haem synthesis (including delta-aminolaevulinic acid dehydratase and ferrochelatase), causing anaemia with basophilic stippling and accumulation of haem precursors. Interference with calcium-dependent processes and mitochondrial function underlies its neurotoxicity, affecting the developing brain and peripheral motor nerves. Lead is stored long-term in bone, from which it can be remobilised during pregnancy or illness.","additionalDifferentials":["Iron deficiency anaemia (may coexist and enhance lead absorption)","Other causes of abdominal pain (acute abdomen, porphyria)","Acute intermittent porphyria (similar neurovisceral picture)","Peripheral neuropathy of other cause","Other heavy-metal poisoning","Sideroblastic anaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Venous blood lead concentration","Full blood count and film (anaemia, basophilic stippling)","Iron studies (deficiency worsens absorption)","Abdominal radiograph if recent ingestion of lead-containing material","Radiographs of long bones in children (lead lines) where relevant","Assessment of renal function and neurodevelopment"],"redFlags":["Encephalopathy: altered consciousness, seizures or signs of raised intracranial pressure","Markedly elevated blood lead concentration","Wrist or foot drop indicating motor neuropathy","Ongoing high-level exposure, especially in a child or pregnancy"],"prognosis":"Prompt removal from exposure and chelation improve acute toxicity, but neurodevelopmental deficits in children may be irreversible; prevention and source control are paramount.","pitfalls":["Assuming a safe threshold exists in children","Missing the diagnosis in vague abdominal pain, constipation and anaemia","Chelating without first removing the source (risking continued or increased absorption)","Overlooking traditional remedies, cosmetics or occupational hobbies as sources"],"management":["Immediate removal from the source of exposure, with environmental and occupational source control and public-health notification","Chelation therapy for symptomatic poisoning or a high blood lead burden, using oral succimer for milder cases and parenteral dimercaprol with sodium calcium edetate for encephalopathy or very high burden","Gastrointestinal decontamination such as whole-bowel irrigation when radio-opaque lead is seen within the gut","Supportive care including correction of iron deficiency, adequate calcium and zinc, and treatment of constipation and abdominal colic","Manage lead encephalopathy as a neurological emergency with seizure control, measures to reduce raised intracranial pressure and intensive care support","Monitor blood lead, full blood count and neurodevelopment, continuing surveillance as skeletal stores remobilise","Screen exposed children and household or workplace contacts, and provide developmental and early-intervention support for affected children","Refer to a toxicology service alongside paediatric or occupational specialists for coordinated management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies (standard reference)","Public health lead exposure guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"leber_congenital_amaurosis":{"name":"Leber congenital amaurosis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LCA","RPE65","CEP290","GUCY2D","early-onset severe retinal dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of severe inherited retinal dystrophies presenting in infancy with profound visual impairment, usually autosomal recessive","Caused by variants in many genes, including RPE65, CEP290, GUCY2D, CRB1, and others, each with characteristic features","Infants show poor visual behaviour, roving nystagmus, sluggish pupillary responses, and the oculodigital sign (eye-poking/pressing)","The electroretinogram is severely reduced or non-recordable early, which is key to the diagnosis","Fundus may look near-normal in infancy, later developing pigmentary retinopathy, vessel attenuation, and disc pallor","RPE65-associated disease is treatable with an approved subretinal gene therapy, making genetic diagnosis clinically actionable","Certain genotypes have systemic associations (e.g. CEP290 with ciliopathy features), so systemic evaluation may be warranted"],"pathophysiology":"Leber congenital amaurosis encompasses genetically heterogeneous defects that disrupt photoreceptor development or function and the visual (retinoid) cycle. In RPE65-related disease, for example, the retinal pigment epithelium cannot regenerate the chromophore needed to restore light sensitivity to photoreceptors, so phototransduction fails and photoreceptors degenerate. Other causative genes impair photoreceptor ciliary transport, guanylate cyclase signalling, or outer-segment structure. The common consequence is early and severe loss of rod and cone function, reflected in a profoundly abnormal electroretinogram and progressive retinal degeneration.","additionalDifferentials":["Achromatopsia (congenital cone dysfunction)","Congenital stationary night blindness","Other early-onset/severe retinal dystrophies","Cortical (cerebral) visual impairment","Ocular albinism with nystagmus","Joubert and Senior-Loken syndromes and other ciliopathies with retinal involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full-field electroretinography (severely reduced or non-recordable)","Molecular genetic testing via retinal dystrophy gene panels","Optical coherence tomography to assess retinal layer and photoreceptor integrity","Detailed dilated fundus examination and retinal imaging","Systemic evaluation (including renal and neurological) where a ciliopathy is suspected","Assessment of vision and refraction, noting frequent high hyperopia"],"redFlags":["Systemic features suggesting an associated ciliopathy (renal impairment, neurological or developmental signs)","Rapidly progressive visual loss or atypical course prompting reassessment","Failure to consider a treatable genotype (e.g. RPE65) that has an approved therapy"],"prognosis":"Vision is severely impaired from infancy and most genotypes are progressive, though some are relatively stable; the prognosis is being transformed for RPE65-related disease by gene therapy, and genotype-specific trials are altering outlook for other subtypes.","pitfalls":["Attributing infantile nystagmus and poor vision to cortical visual impairment without electrophysiology","Failing to obtain genetic testing and missing a treatable RPE65 genotype","Overlooking systemic ciliopathy associations that require renal or neurological monitoring","Not correcting the frequently high hyperopic refractive error"],"management":["Establish the molecular genetic diagnosis, since genotype determines treatment eligibility and prognosis and identifies treatable subtypes","Offer subretinal gene therapy with voretigene neparvovec for confirmed biallelic RPE65-associated disease","Optimise residual vision with refractive correction of the frequent high hyperopia and provision of low-vision aids","Provide early visual rehabilitation, educational support and mobility training for children with profound impairment","Screen for systemic renal and neurological involvement where a ciliopathy such as CEP290-related disease is suspected","Offer genetic counselling for this usually autosomal recessive condition, with testing of at-risk relatives","Refer to a specialist inherited retinal disease centre and consider enrolment in genotype-specific therapeutic trials"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina"],"crossLinks":[]},"leber_hereditary_optic_neuropathy":{"name":"Leber hereditary optic neuropathy","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LHON","mitochondrial optic neuropathy","MT-ND4","MT-ND1","MT-ND6"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Maternally inherited mitochondrial disorder, most often from one of three primary mtDNA point mutations affecting complex I (m.11778, m.3460, m.14484)","Typically affects young adult males with subacute, painless, severe central visual loss in one eye followed by the fellow eye within weeks to months","Acute phase shows peripapillary telangiectatic microangiopathy, disc hyperaemia, and swelling of the retinal nerve fibre layer without leakage on angiography","Progresses to optic atrophy with a dense central or centrocaecal scotoma and impaired colour vision","Incomplete penetrance and marked male predominance indicate modifying genetic and environmental factors","Smoking and heavy alcohol use are recognised triggers/aggravators and should be avoided","The m.14484 mutation carries the best chance of spontaneous visual recovery"],"pathophysiology":"Leber hereditary optic neuropathy is caused by pathogenic point mutations in mitochondrial DNA that impair complex I of the respiratory chain, reducing ATP production and increasing oxidative stress. Retinal ganglion cells, particularly the small fibres of the papillomacular bundle, are exquisitely dependent on mitochondrial energy and are preferentially vulnerable. Energy failure and oxidative damage trigger dysfunction and then apoptotic loss of these ganglion cells, producing the characteristic central visual loss and subsequent optic atrophy. Incomplete penetrance and the male bias reflect the influence of mitochondrial heteroplasmy, nuclear modifiers, hormonal factors, and environmental triggers.","additionalDifferentials":["Optic neuritis (demyelinating)","Toxic or nutritional optic neuropathy","Dominant optic atrophy (OPA1)","Compressive or infiltrative optic neuropathy","Non-arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy","Bilateral sequential optic neuropathy from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Mitochondrial DNA testing for the primary LHON point mutations","Optical coherence tomography of the retinal nerve fibre layer and ganglion cell complex","Automated perimetry documenting central or centrocaecal scotomata","Fundus fluorescein angiography (characteristically no leakage despite disc appearance)","MRI of brain and orbits to exclude compressive or demyelinating causes","Colour vision assessment"],"redFlags":["Rapid sequential involvement of the second eye","Atypical features (pain, response to steroids, or optic disc leakage) suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Systemic or neurological signs indicating a 'LHON-plus' phenotype","Continued smoking or alcohol exposure accelerating visual loss"],"prognosis":"Most patients suffer severe and permanent bilateral central visual loss, although a minority recover useful vision, most commonly those with the m.14484 mutation and younger patients; avoidance of smoking and excess alcohol and emerging gene and pharmacological therapies may modify outcomes.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as atypical optic neuritis and giving prolonged unhelpful immunosuppression","Failing to counsel on maternal inheritance and the risk to relatives","Overlooking modifiable triggers such as smoking and alcohol in carriers","Not recognising the disc appearance can mimic true papilloedema early on"],"management":["Consider idebenone, a synthetic short-chain quinone, particularly during the acute or subacute phase to support residual vision","Strongly advise smoking cessation and avoidance of heavy alcohol, which are recognised triggers and aggravators","Provide low-vision aids and visual rehabilitation for the central scotoma and impaired colour vision","Monitor both eyes with optical coherence tomography and perimetry, anticipating sequential involvement of the fellow eye","Offer genetic counselling explaining maternal mitochondrial inheritance, incomplete penetrance and male predominance, with assessment of at-risk maternal relatives","Refer to neuro-ophthalmology and clinical genetics, and consider emerging mitochondrial-targeted gene therapies within specialist or trial settings"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"leber_plus_dystonia":{"name":"Leber hereditary optic neuropathy plus / dystonia (LHON-plus)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LHON-plus","Leber hereditary optic neuropathy plus dystonia","LHON with extraocular features","MT-ND"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews Leber Hereditary Optic Neuropathy / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["LHON-plus denotes the optic neuropathy of Leber hereditary optic neuropathy accompanied by extraocular features, most characteristically dystonia and other movement or neurological disorders","The visual component is a subacute, painless, usually sequential bilateral central visual loss from optic nerve degeneration, typically in young adult men","It is maternally inherited, caused by mitochondrial DNA point mutations affecting complex I subunits, classically m.11778G>A, m.3460G>A and m.14484T>C","Incomplete penetrance and a strong male predominance mean many mutation carriers never lose vision, and additional environmental factors such as tobacco and alcohol modify risk","In LHON-plus, dystonia often reflects bilateral striatal (putaminal) lesions, and MRI may show basal ganglia signal change","Some pedigrees combine optic neuropathy with dystonia and additional features resembling other mitochondrial or extrapyramidal syndromes","Management is supportive with low-vision rehabilitation, avoidance of mitochondrial toxins including tobacco and excess alcohol, and generic use of quinone-based cofactor therapy under specialist guidance"],"pathophysiology":"Leber hereditary optic neuropathy results from maternally inherited point mutations in mitochondrial DNA that encode subunits of respiratory-chain complex I, impairing electron transport, reducing ATP synthesis and increasing reactive oxygen species. Retinal ganglion cells, whose small unmyelinated axons in the papillomacular bundle have high energy demand, are selectively vulnerable, leading to their degeneration and central visual loss. In LHON-plus the same energy failure and oxidative stress extend to other high-demand neurons, particularly in the basal ganglia, producing dystonia and additional neurological features; the reasons some carriers develop the extended phenotype relate to mutant load, nuclear and mitochondrial genetic background, hormonal factors and environmental exposures.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated classic Leber hereditary optic neuropathy without extraocular features","Dominant optic atrophy (OPA1) and other hereditary optic neuropathies","Optic neuritis from demyelinating disease","Toxic or nutritional optic neuropathy","Primary dystonia syndromes and Wilson disease","Leigh syndrome and other complex I mitochondrial disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing for the common LHON mitochondrial DNA mutations with heteroplasmy assessment","Ophthalmological evaluation including visual fields, colour vision and optical coherence tomography of the retinal nerve fibre layer","Brain MRI to assess basal ganglia and optic pathway involvement","Visual evoked potentials to document optic nerve dysfunction","Blood lactate and, where indicated, muscle respiratory-chain enzymology","Assessment for modifiable risk factors including tobacco and alcohol use"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive bilateral visual loss requiring urgent specialist assessment","New or worsening generalised dystonia impairing function","Additional bulbar or extrapyramidal deterioration suggesting a broader mitochondrial syndrome","Continued exposure to mitochondrial toxins during acute visual decline"],"prognosis":"Visual prognosis is generally poor with most affected individuals reaching legal blindness, though the m.14484T>C mutation carries a better chance of partial recovery; when dystonia and other extraocular features are present the neurological disability tends to persist and may progress.","pitfalls":["Assuming carriers will inevitably lose vision, ignoring the marked incomplete penetrance and sex bias","Overlooking dystonia and basal ganglia lesions that distinguish LHON-plus from isolated LHON","Failing to counsel about maternal inheritance and avoidance of tobacco and alcohol","Misattributing subacute visual loss to optic neuritis and delaying mitochondrial testing"],"management":["Consider idebenone to support residual vision, and advise avoidance of tobacco and alcohol as recognised aggravators","Manage dystonia symptomatically with agents such as anticholinergics, baclofen, a trial of levodopa, or focal botulinum toxin injections","Consider deep brain stimulation for disabling, medically refractory dystonia in appropriate patients","Provide visual rehabilitation and low-vision aids alongside physiotherapy and occupational therapy for motor disability","Monitor visual and neurological status with ophthalmic imaging, perimetry and neuroimaging of the basal ganglia","Offer genetic counselling for maternal mitochondrial inheritance with heteroplasmy assessment and evaluation of maternal relatives","Coordinate care through mitochondrial medicine, movement-disorder neurology and neuro-ophthalmology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Leber Hereditary Optic Neuropathy","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"left_atrial_myxoma":{"name":"Left Atrial Myxoma","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Atrial myxoma","Cardiac myxoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cardiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest primary cardiac tumour in adults; the large majority arise in the left atrium, typically pedunculated from the margin of the fossa ovalis of the interatrial septum.","The classic clinical triad comprises valvular obstruction, systemic embolisation, and constitutional symptoms.","A low-pitched early-diastolic 'tumour plop' may be audible as the pedunculated mass prolapses into the mitral orifice.","Symptoms and auscultatory findings are often positional, varying with posture as the mobile tumour shifts within the atrium.","Constitutional features (fever, weight loss, arthralgia, anaemia and raised inflammatory markers) are largely driven by tumour interleukin-6 and can mimic infective endocarditis or vasculitis.","The obstructive presentation can closely imitate rheumatic mitral stenosis with exertional breathlessness and pulmonary congestion.","Histological examination of fragments retrieved at embolectomy may first reveal the diagnosis after an embolic event.","Multiple, recurrent, or atypically sited myxomas in a young patient suggest Carney complex, associated with lentigines and endocrine overactivity."],"pathophysiology":"Cardiac myxoma is a benign neoplasm believed to originate from primitive multipotential mesenchymal cells within the subendocardium, most characteristically at the border of the fossa ovalis, from which it projects into the left atrial cavity as a gelatinous, often pedunculated and mobile mass. Clinical consequences arise through three principal mechanisms. Mechanical obstruction occurs when the tumour intermittently occludes the mitral valve orifice, producing findings that mimic mitral stenosis, with pulmonary venous congestion, positional dyspnoea and syncope. Embolisation of friable tumour fragments or of thrombus formed on the tumour surface disperses material to the systemic circulation, causing cerebral, retinal, visceral or limb ischaemia. Systemic constitutional effects are mediated largely by tumour secretion of interleukin-6, producing fever, weight loss, arthralgia and an acute-phase response. Most cases are sporadic; a minority are familial as part of the autosomal dominant Carney complex, associated with mutations in the PRKAR1A gene and a tendency to multiple and recurrent tumours.","additionalDifferentials":["Rheumatic mitral stenosis","Left atrial thrombus","Infective endocarditis with a valvular vegetation","Papillary fibroelastoma and other benign cardiac tumours","Cardiac sarcoma or metastatic tumour","Systemic vasculitis or connective tissue disease","Primary cardiac lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic and transoesophageal echocardiography to define size, attachment site and mobility","Cardiac MRI for tissue characterisation and to help distinguish thrombus or malignant tumour","Cardiac CT where MRI is unavailable or contraindicated","Histopathological examination of the resected mass or of an embolectomy specimen","Full blood count and inflammatory markers (anaemia and a raised acute-phase response)","Coronary angiography before surgery in appropriate patients","Genetic assessment and screening for Carney complex where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Acute embolic stroke or other systemic embolisation","Syncope or presyncope suggesting critical intermittent valve obstruction","Acute pulmonary oedema","Imaging features suggesting a malignant tumour (broad-based, infiltrative, right-sided or multiple lesions)","Young age or a positive family history raising the possibility of Carney complex"],"prognosis":"The outlook after complete surgical excision is excellent, with low operative mortality and durable symptom relief. Recurrence of sporadic tumours is rare, but familial and Carney-complex cases recur more frequently and warrant long-term echocardiographic surveillance.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosis as rheumatic mitral stenosis or as infective endocarditis.","Attributing constitutional symptoms to occult malignancy or connective tissue disease while overlooking the tumour.","Omitting echocardiography in the investigation of embolic stroke of undetermined source.","Failing to screen for Carney complex in young patients or in multiple or recurrent cases.","Assuming every cardiac mass is benign and missing a rare malignant tumour."],"management":["Definitive treatment is prompt surgical excision of the tumour together with its base of attachment on cardiopulmonary bypass","Expedite surgery given the ongoing risk of valvular obstruction and systemic embolisation while awaiting operation","Manage obstructive heart failure and positional symptoms supportively pending resection","Address embolic complications such as cerebral or peripheral ischaemia as they arise, including embolectomy where indicated","Undertake histopathological confirmation of the resected mass or embolectomy specimen","Perform long-term echocardiographic surveillance for recurrence, especially in familial and Carney-complex disease","Screen for Carney complex and offer genetic counselling and family screening when features suggest a heritable syndrome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cardiology references","Cardiac surgery references","Cardiovascular pathology references"],"crossLinks":[]},"left_ventricular_noncompaction":{"name":"Left ventricular non-compaction cardiomyopathy","system":"Cardiovascular / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LVNC","left ventricular non-compaction","spongy myocardium","non-compaction cardiomyopathy","hypertrabeculation"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC/AHA cardiomyopathy guidelines; Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by prominent left ventricular trabeculations with deep intertrabecular recesses and a two-layered myocardium of thin compacted and thick non-compacted layers","The clinical triad of concern is heart failure, arrhythmia (including sudden death) and systemic thromboembolism","May present in neonates and children or be discovered incidentally in adults; presentation ranges from asymptomatic to severe cardiomyopathy","Frequently familial with heterogeneous genetics, including sarcomeric gene variants that overlap with hypertrophic and dilated cardiomyopathy","Cardiac MRI is more sensitive than echocardiography for quantifying the non-compacted to compacted myocardial ratio and detecting fibrosis","Anticoagulation is considered when ventricular function is impaired, atrial fibrillation is present, or thrombus is identified, given the risk of embolism from deep recesses","Management otherwise follows standard heart failure and arrhythmia principles, with defibrillator therapy for those at high arrhythmic risk","It can occur in isolation or in association with neuromuscular disorders and congenital heart disease"],"pathophysiology":"Left ventricular non-compaction is thought to reflect arrest of the normal compaction of the trabeculated embryonic myocardium during development, though acquired remodelling may also contribute. The result is a distinctive two-layered ventricular wall with a thin compacted epicardial layer and a thick, heavily trabeculated non-compacted endocardial layer containing deep recesses that communicate with the cavity. This architecture impairs contractile function, promotes microvascular dysfunction and fibrosis, provides a substrate for ventricular arrhythmia, and creates areas of stasis within the recesses that favour thrombus formation and systemic embolism. The genetics overlap with other inherited cardiomyopathies, particularly sarcomeric protein variants.","additionalDifferentials":["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Prominent normal trabeculation or physiological hypertrabeculation in athletes and pregnancy","Endomyocardial fibrosis","Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Cardiac masses or apical thrombus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography with assessment of the non-compacted to compacted layer ratio","Cardiac MRI for tissue characterisation, late gadolinium enhancement and trabecular quantification","12-lead ECG and ambulatory monitoring for conduction abnormalities and arrhythmia","Assessment of left ventricular systolic function and volumes","Genetic testing and cascade screening of relatives","Evaluation for associated neuromuscular disease when clinically suggested"],"redFlags":["New or worsening heart failure symptoms","Syncope or documented sustained ventricular arrhythmia","Systemic embolic event such as stroke","Family history of cardiomyopathy or premature sudden death"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends chiefly on ventricular function and arrhythmic burden; incidentally detected cases with preserved function may remain stable, whereas those with impaired systolic function face risks of progressive heart failure, arrhythmia and thromboembolism.","pitfalls":["Overdiagnosing the condition from prominent trabeculation in athletes or pregnancy on echocardiography alone","Failing to consider anticoagulation in patients with impaired function or atrial fibrillation","Assuming an incidental finding is benign without assessing arrhythmic and thromboembolic risk","Neglecting family screening given the frequently inherited nature","Overlooking associated neuromuscular or congenital conditions"],"management":["Treat systolic dysfunction with guideline-directed heart failure therapy including beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers (or an angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor), mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists and SGLT2 inhibitors","Provide oral anticoagulation when systolic function is impaired, atrial fibrillation is present, or intracavitary thrombus is identified, to reduce embolism from the deep recesses","Manage ventricular arrhythmia and assess for an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator for sudden-death prevention in high-risk patients","Monitor with serial echocardiography or cardiac MRI and ambulatory ECG for progression of dysfunction and arrhythmic burden","Offer genetic testing and cascade screening of first-degree relatives given the frequently familial, sarcomeric basis","Evaluate for an associated neuromuscular disease where clinically suggested","Refer advanced or refractory heart failure for consideration of transplantation or mechanical circulatory support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiomyopathies","Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"legg_calve_perthes_disease":{"name":"Legg-Calve-Perthes disease","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Perthes disease","Legg-Calve-Perthes","coxa plana","idiopathic osteonecrosis of the capital femoral epiphysis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A true idiopathic osteonecrosis of the capital femoral epiphysis in the growing child, not a traction apophysitis","Affected bone is the femoral head (capital femoral epiphysis) of the hip","Typically affects children roughly four to eight years of age, and is several times more common in boys, usually unilateral","Presents with a painless or mildly painful limp and referred pain to the groin, thigh or knee, with restricted hip abduction and internal rotation","The radiographic hallmark evolves from a smaller, denser epiphysis and a subchondral crescent fracture line through fragmentation to reossification and residual deformity","The lateral pillar (Herring) and Catterall classifications describe the extent of femoral head involvement and guide prognosis","Knee or thigh pain in a young child should always prompt examination of the hip to avoid missing the diagnosis","Management centres on maintaining hip motion and femoral head containment using activity modification, physiotherapy, bracing or containment surgery as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Legg-Calve-Perthes disease is an idiopathic osteonecrosis of the capital femoral epiphysis caused by interruption of its blood supply in the immature hip. The precarious vascular supply of the developing femoral head, dependent largely on the lateral epiphyseal vessels, undergoes one or more episodes of ischaemia, leading to death of the subchondral bone and marrow. The necrotic bone becomes mechanically weak, and a subchondral fracture may occur followed by fragmentation and resorption. Revascularisation and creeping substitution then rebuild the epiphysis, but during this vulnerable phase the softened femoral head can deform under load, producing flattening and enlargement (coxa plana and coxa magna). The ultimate shape of the femoral head and its congruency with the acetabulum determine long-term hip function.","additionalDifferentials":["Transient synovitis of the hip","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis of the hip","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis","Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia or spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia","Secondary osteonecrosis (for example from corticosteroids, sickle cell disease or Gaucher disease)","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis of the hip"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anteroposterior and frog-lateral pelvic radiographs to assess the epiphysis and stage the disease","Magnetic resonance imaging for early diagnosis before radiographic change and to assess femoral head coverage","Lateral pillar (Herring) classification to help predict outcome","Assessment of hip range of motion, particularly abduction and internal rotation","Bilateral imaging and consideration of an epiphyseal dysplasia if changes are symmetrical","Investigations for a secondary cause of osteonecrosis when the presentation is atypical"],"redFlags":["Fever, systemic upset or a markedly irritable hip suggesting septic arthritis","Bilateral symmetrical epiphyseal changes indicating a skeletal dysplasia rather than Perthes disease","An acute inability to bear weight, which may signal slipped capital femoral epiphysis","Progressive femoral head extrusion and loss of containment during the fragmentation phase"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on age at onset, extent of femoral head involvement and residual head shape; younger children with limited involvement often remodel well, whereas older children with extensive involvement and an aspherical, incongruent head are at greater risk of premature osteoarthritis.","pitfalls":["Attributing a child's knee or thigh pain to a local cause and failing to examine and image the hip","Mistaking early Perthes disease for transient synovitis and not arranging follow-up","Overlooking bilateral symmetrical changes that indicate an epiphyseal dysplasia","Losing femoral head containment by neglecting range of motion during the vulnerable fragmentation phase"],"management":["Base management on the principle of containment, keeping the femoral head within the acetabulum during the biologically plastic phase","In younger children with limited involvement, use non-operative care with activity modification, protected weight-bearing and simple analgesia","Maintain and restore hip movement, especially abduction and internal rotation, with physiotherapy and where needed an abduction orthosis","Consider containment surgery such as femoral varus or pelvic (acetabular) osteotomy for older children or those with extensive femoral head involvement","Provide symptomatic pain relief with anti-inflammatory agents during painful phases","Monitor with serial radiographs, staging progression and femoral head coverage (for example the lateral pillar classification) to guide treatment","Refer to paediatric orthopaedics with long-term follow-up for residual deformity and later risk of premature osteoarthritis requiring joint-preserving or replacement surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"legionellosis":{"name":"Legionellosis (Legionnaires' Disease)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Legionnaires disease","Pontiac fever"],"chapter":164,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1269-1276","clinicalPearls":["Legionella species cause two distinct illnesses collectively termed legionellosis: Legionella pneumonia (Legionnaires' disease) and Pontiac fever; most clinical disease is driven by L. pneumophila, predominantly serotype 1 (Harrison 22e p.1269).","Almost all cases trace to waterborne exposure; aerosolizing or aspiration sources include cooling towers, fountains, water misters, HVAC systems, hot tubs/spas, ice machines, and showerheads/sinks in large premise-plumbing systems (Harrison 22e p.1270-1271).","Legionella pneumonia presents as an 'atypical pneumonia' with nonspecific onset of fever, myalgias, headache, breathlessness, and dry or productive cough; accompanying neurologic or gastrointestinal symptoms (anorexia, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, confusion) raise the likelihood of legionellosis (Harrison 22e p.1272).","A characteristic laboratory cluster supports the diagnosis: hyponatremia and/or renal dysfunction are frequent, with elevated liver enzymes, and leukocytosis or leukopenia plus thrombocytopenia (Harrison 22e p.1272).","Per CDC, a single confirmed Legionella case in a patient who did not leave a health care facility/campus for the 10 days before onset should trigger an outbreak investigation, because nosocomial cases may signal water-system contamination (Harrison 22e p.1270-1273).","Pontiac fever is a milder, self-limited influenza-like illness defined by the ABSENCE of pneumonia, with fever, headache, myalgias, chills, vertigo, nausea/vomiting and diarrhea; antibiotics are not required (Harrison 22e p.1273)."],"pathophysiology":"Legionellae are aerobic gram-negative bacteria ubiquitous in aquatic environments, damp soil, and compost. Their natural hosts are amoebae and other free-living protozoa, within which they replicate intracellularly; humans are accidental hosts. They have a biphasic life cycle (a nutrient-rich replicative phase and a transmissive phase under resource scarcity) and persist in biofilms within natural and engineered water systems, including building premise plumbing where temperature and nutrients support protozoal hosts. After inhalation or aspiration of small aerosol particles, the organisms are phagocytized by alveolar macrophages and monocytes; pathogenic species evade intracellular defenses (forming Legionella-containing vacuoles, downregulating cytokine receptors, inhibiting host protein synthesis, and avoiding lysosomal degradation) and replicate. Innate pattern-recognition receptors (Toll-like and NOD-like receptors) together with adaptive CD4 and CD8 T-cell responses drive interferon-gamma, TNF, and neutrophil recruitment, which can clear the pathogen but may also produce immunopathology. L. pneumophila is more cytopathogenic than most non-pneumophila species, which may underlie its association with severe disease (Harrison 22e p.1269).","additionalDifferentials":["Other atypical-pneumonia pathogens, notably Mycoplasma pneumoniae and Chlamydia pneumoniae (Harrison 22e p.1272)"],"infectionMimics":["In immunocompromised patients, Legionella can present as pulmonary nodules with or without cavitation that mimic fungal infection, or as lung abscesses (Harrison 22e p.1273)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lower-respiratory culture (sputum, BAL fluid, lung tissue, or extrapulmonary sites) on cysteine-supplemented buffered charcoal yeast extract (BCYE) agar is the gold standard and is essential for epidemiologic typing; growth is slow (typically 3-5 days, longer for non-pneumophila species) (Harrison 22e p.1273-1274)","Polymerase chain reaction / nucleic acid amplification (including LAMP and multiplex respiratory panels) on lower-tract specimens is more sensitive than culture and remains useful during antibiotic therapy, but does not provide serotype information and most assays target only L. pneumophila (Harrison 22e p.1274-1275)","Bronchoscopy when urinary antigen is negative and the patient cannot produce adequate sputum, or in severe disease requiring ICU care (Harrison 22e p.1273)","Serology (paired acute/convalescent titers; fourfold rise to L. pneumophila serogroup 1 confirms) has limited acute sensitivity but aids outbreak investigation (Harrison 22e p.1274)","Direct fluorescent antibody testing of sputum has low and variable sensitivity (~20-70%) and is not recommended for low-risk screening due to false positives (Harrison 22e p.1274)"],"redFlags":["Severe pneumonia with acute respiratory failure or a sepsis-like presentation warrants testing for Legionella (Harrison 22e p.1272)","Pneumonia developing during hospitalization, after recent travel, or during a known or possible outbreak should prompt Legionella testing (Harrison 22e p.1272)","Immunosuppressed patients may lack typical features such as fever and may show radiographic progression during early therapy (Harrison 22e p.1272-1273)"],"prognosis":"Legionella pneumonia carries significant morbidity and mortality, with most patients hospitalized and many requiring ICU admission. The reported case-fatality rate is about 10%, rising to roughly 25% in health care-associated L. pneumophila pneumonia and to approximately 30-50% in immunocompromised hosts. Delayed antibiotic treatment is associated with about a threefold higher mortality. More than a quarter of survivors of severe disease have lasting complications, including recurrent hospitalizations, acute renal failure, respiratory sequelae, and recurrent pneumonias. By contrast, Pontiac fever is self-limited, usually resolving within 3-5 days, with hospitalization, complications, and death extremely rare (Harrison 22e p.1275).","pitfalls":["Urinary antigen testing detects ONLY L. pneumophila serotype 1; it gives false-negative results in most cases caused by non-serotype 1 L. pneumophila and non-pneumophila species, so a negative result does not exclude legionellosis (sensitivity for serotype 1 is ~70%, though specificity is high) (Harrison 22e p.1274).","Urinary antigen can be falsely negative very early in disease yet remain positive for months after infection (especially in immunosuppressed patients), and it cannot be used in anuric patients (Harrison 22e p.1274).","Because community-acquired pneumonia guidelines recommend empirical coverage active against Legionella, specific testing is often skipped and cultures are frequently collected only after antibiotics, reducing yield (Harrison 22e p.1273-1274).","Diagnosis on clinical grounds alone is unreliable; clinical-prediction tools (e.g., Winthrop-University Hospital Criteria, Legionella Score) are more useful for their negative than their positive predictive value (Harrison 22e p.1272).","Routine testing is less common in children, so pediatric cases may be underreported; immunosuppressed patients may present without fever or pulmonary exam findings (Harrison 22e p.1271-1272)."],"management":["Treat Legionnaires' disease with an antibiotic active against intracellular organisms, namely a respiratory fluoroquinolone such as levofloxacin or a macrolide such as azithromycin","Manage Pontiac fever supportively, as it is self-limiting and does not require antibiotics","Provide supportive care with supplemental oxygen, fluid management and respiratory support as needed","Monitor for and correct associated hyponatraemia, and track renal and hepatic dysfunction","Escalate severe pneumonia with respiratory failure to intensive care for ventilatory support","Notify public-health authorities and trigger source investigation of implicated water systems, particularly for health-care-associated cases"],"severityClassification":"Treatment is stratified by severity: Pontiac fever requires no antibiotics (supportive care only), whereas Legionella pneumonia is managed as mild versus moderate/severe disease. All immunosuppressed patients are considered to have moderate or severe disease and should receive IV therapy where possible; all patients needing inpatient care should receive IV therapy until improving before switching to an oral agent. CDC distinguishes health care-associated cases as presumptive (Legionella pneumonia after >=10 days continuous stay in a health care facility during the 14 days before symptom onset) versus possible (some health care exposure in that window but insufficient to meet presumptive criteria) (Harrison 22e p.1272-1275).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1269-1276"],"crossLinks":[]},"legius_syndrome":{"name":"Legius syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPRED1","neurofibromatosis type 1-like syndrome","NFLS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as multiple cafe-au-lait macules with or without axillary/inguinal freckling, closely mimicking neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1)","Crucially lacks the tumour features of NF1: no neurofibromas, no optic pathway gliomas, no Lisch nodules and no plexiform tumours","Caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in SPRED1, a negative regulator of RAS-MAPK signalling, so it belongs to the RASopathy family","Inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern; a positive family history of pigmentary features without tumours is a useful clue","Some children have mild learning difficulties, ADHD or macrocephaly, but overall the phenotype is far milder than NF1","Molecular testing distinguishes it from NF1 and spares patients unnecessary tumour surveillance and anxiety","Should be considered in any child meeting pigmentary criteria for NF1 who has no other NF1 features, especially when a parent is similarly affected"],"pathophysiology":"Legius syndrome results from inactivating variants in SPRED1, which encodes a protein that normally dampens the RAS-mitogen-activated protein kinase pathway by facilitating neurofibromin function. Reduced SPRED1 activity leads to mild overactivity of RAS-MAPK signalling in melanocytes, producing cafe-au-lait macules and freckling, yet without the broader tumour-suppressor loss seen when neurofibromin itself is deficient. This explains the shared pigmentary phenotype with NF1 but the absence of the neurofibromas and other tumours that characterise true NF1.","additionalDifferentials":["Neurofibromatosis type 1","Constitutional mismatch repair deficiency syndrome","Noonan syndrome with multiple lentigines","McCune-Albright syndrome (cafe-au-lait macules)","Familial multiple cafe-au-lait macules without a syndrome","Piebaldism and other pigmentary conditions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted SPRED1 sequencing, usually as part of an NF1/RASopathy gene panel","NF1 gene analysis to exclude neurofibromatosis type 1","Detailed dermatological examination and documentation of pigmentary lesions","Slit-lamp examination to look for Lisch nodules (absent in Legius)","Developmental and neuropsychological assessment where learning concerns exist","Family cascade screening once a variant is identified"],"redFlags":["Appearance of a true neurofibroma or plexiform tumour, which points away from Legius towards NF1","Optic pathway symptoms or an orbital mass suggesting glioma (an NF1 feature)","Rapidly progressive or numerous cafe-au-lait lesions with systemic features suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Findings meeting full NF1 diagnostic criteria despite an initial Legius label"],"prognosis":"Legius syndrome carries a favourable outlook with a normal life expectancy and no established increase in tumour risk, though a minority have mild cognitive or attentional difficulties that benefit from educational support.","pitfalls":["Labelling a child with NF1 on pigmentary criteria alone and imposing lifelong tumour surveillance when the true diagnosis is Legius","Assuming a normal SPRED1 result excludes NF1 without dedicated NF1 testing","Overlooking the diagnosis in a mildly affected parent, missing the autosomal dominant pattern","Failing to reassure families about the benign nature once molecular confirmation is obtained"],"management":["Confirm the diagnosis by SPRED1 molecular testing and exclude NF1, which spares patients unnecessary tumour surveillance and anxiety","Reassure that, unlike NF1, there is no established increase in tumour risk requiring neuroimaging or Lisch-nodule surveillance","Provide educational support and assessment for the mild learning difficulties or attention-deficit problems that affect a minority","Offer dermatological review of cafe-au-lait macules and freckling where there is cosmetic concern","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant condition with cascade testing of relatives once a variant is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Legius Syndrome","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"leigh_syndrome":{"name":"Leigh syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Subacute necrotising encephalomyelopathy","Leigh disease","SURF1","MT-ATP6"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / GeneReviews mitochondrial disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents in infancy or early childhood with psychomotor regression, hypotonia, feeding and swallowing difficulty, and often follows an intercurrent illness","The radiological signature is bilateral symmetrical T2 hyperintense lesions in the basal ganglia and brainstem, frequently with elevated lactate on MR spectroscopy","It is genetically heterogeneous: causative defects lie in either mitochondrial DNA (for example MT-ATP6, giving maternal inheritance) or numerous nuclear genes such as SURF1 (autosomal recessive)","The common final pathway is impaired oxidative phosphorylation, most often affecting complex I, complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase) or pyruvate metabolism","Brainstem involvement can produce respiratory irregularity, abnormal eye movements, nystagmus and central apnoea","Blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate are commonly raised, and the CSF-to-blood lactate ratio can be informative","Management is supportive with attention to nutrition, intercurrent illness and generic cofactor supplementation, avoiding respiratory-chain toxins and, where a pyruvate dehydrogenase defect is confirmed, considering a generically high-fat ketogenic dietary approach under specialist supervision"],"pathophysiology":"Leigh syndrome is a genetically diverse but phenotypically convergent disorder of mitochondrial energy metabolism. Whatever the underlying gene, the result is deficient ATP production, most often from defects of respiratory-chain complex I or IV, ATP synthase, or the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex. When the defect resides in mitochondrial DNA the disorder is maternally inherited and heteroplasmic with a threshold effect, whereas nuclear-encoded forms follow Mendelian, usually autosomal recessive, patterns. Chronically energy-starved neurons in the basal ganglia, thalamus, brainstem and cerebellum undergo the characteristic focal, bilaterally symmetrical necrosis with capillary proliferation, gliosis and demyelination that gives the disorder its descriptive name.","additionalDifferentials":["Other mitochondrial encephalopathies including MELAS and MERRF","Biotinidase or biotin-thiamine-responsive basal ganglia disease","Wilson disease and other basal ganglia metabolic disorders","Kernicterus or hypoxic-ischaemic basal ganglia injury","Organic acidaemias such as glutaric aciduria","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate and pyruvate with calculation of the lactate/pyruvate ratio","Brain MRI with spectroscopy demonstrating symmetrical basal ganglia and brainstem lesions and a lactate peak","Plasma amino acids and urine organic acids to exclude mimicking metabolic disorders","Molecular genetic testing including mitochondrial DNA analysis and nuclear gene panels (e.g. SURF1)","Skeletal muscle biopsy for respiratory-chain enzyme assays where genetics are inconclusive","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex enzyme activity when a PDH defect is suspected"],"redFlags":["Brainstem dysfunction with central hypoventilation or apnoea","Rapid neurological regression triggered by intercurrent infection or fasting","Progressive bulbar weakness with aspiration risk","Decompensating lactic acidosis"],"prognosis":"Leigh syndrome is generally progressive with a guarded outlook; classic infantile forms often follow a stepwise decline with intercurrent illnesses and reduced survival, though later-onset and some nuclear-gene forms can run a more indolent course.","pitfalls":["Assuming a single gene: Leigh syndrome requires both mitochondrial DNA and nuclear testing because it is highly heterogeneous","Overlooking treatable phenocopies such as biotin-thiamine-responsive basal ganglia disease or a pyruvate dehydrogenase defect","Missing brainstem respiratory involvement that can cause sudden deterioration","Exposing patients to mitochondrial toxins or prolonged fasting that precipitate crises"],"management":["There is no curative therapy; offer mitochondrial supportive supplements such as thiamine, riboflavin, coenzyme Q10 and biotin, with thiamine or a ketogenic diet where a pyruvate dehydrogenase defect is identified","Avoid mitochondrial toxins and aggravating drugs such as sodium valproate, and prevent catabolism by giving intravenous glucose and treating intercurrent illness promptly","Provide nutritional support and safe feeding, including nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding for swallowing difficulty, and treat seizures and spasticity","Manage acute metabolic decompensation with correction of lactic acidosis and aggressive treatment of the precipitating stress","Monitor for brainstem-related respiratory irregularity and central apnoea, with respiratory support as needed","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across metabolic medicine, neurology, respiratory, physiotherapy and palliative care","Offer genetic counselling addressing both maternal mitochondrial DNA and autosomal recessive nuclear-gene inheritance, with family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Nuclear Gene-Encoded Leigh Syndrome","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"leigh_syndrome_french_canadian":{"name":"Leigh syndrome, French-Canadian type","system":"Metabolic / Mitochondrial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LSFC","Saguenay-Lac-Saint-Jean cytochrome c oxidase deficiency","LRPPRC-related Leigh syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mitochondrial medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive Leigh syndrome variant caused by LRPPRC mutations; a founder missense variant predominates in the Saguenay-Lac-Saint-Jean region of Quebec.","LRPPRC stabilises and polyadenylates mitochondrial messenger RNAs, so its loss causes a tissue-specific cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV) deficiency that is most marked in brain and liver.","The chronic phenotype includes developmental delay, hypotonia, characteristic facial features and persistently elevated blood lactate.","The defining and life-threatening feature is recurrent acute metabolic crises with severe lactic acidosis and hepatic dysfunction, typically triggered by infection, fasting or other catabolic stress.","Crises carry high mortality and can progress rapidly to coma and death; most deaths occur during such episodes in early childhood.","Neuroimaging shows the classic Leigh pattern of bilateral symmetrical basal ganglia and brainstem signal change.","Children may be relatively stable between crises, which can lead carers to underestimate the risk of decompensation.","Management is preventive and supportive, centred on avoidance of catabolism, prompt aggressive treatment of intercurrent illness, and crisis-anticipating sick-day planning."],"pathophysiology":"LRPPRC encodes a leucine-rich pentatricopeptide-repeat protein that, together with SLIRP, binds mitochondrial messenger RNAs to maintain their stability, polyadenylation and coordinated translation. The founder variant destabilises the protein, lowering steady-state levels of several mtDNA-encoded transcripts and disproportionately impairing assembly of cytochrome c oxidase in brain and liver. The resulting failure of oxidative phosphorylation produces chronic lactic acidaemia and the subacute necrotising encephalopathy lesions characteristic of Leigh syndrome, while a low threshold for energy failure under catabolic stress precipitates the fulminant acidotic and hepatic crises. The abruptness and tissue selectivity of these crises reflect the tissue-specific dependence on LRPPRC-supported mitochondrial gene expression.","additionalDifferentials":["Other genetic causes of Leigh syndrome (mtDNA MT-ATP6, SURF1, nuclear complex I or IV assembly defects)","MELAS and other mitochondrial encephalopathies","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex deficiency","Biotinidase deficiency and biotin-thiamine-responsive basal ganglia disease","Organic acidaemias and urea-cycle disorders presenting with encephalopathic crises","Fatty-acid oxidation defects with hepatic decompensation","Other mitochondrial hepatopathies (DGUOK, MPV17, POLG)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and CSF lactate and pyruvate with the lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","LRPPRC sequencing and targeted founder-variant testing","Respiratory-chain enzyme assays showing isolated complex IV deficiency in muscle, liver or fibroblasts","Brain MRI and magnetic resonance spectroscopy (basal ganglia, brainstem, lactate peak)","Liver function tests and coagulation studies during crises","Plasma amino acids, urine organic acids and acylcarnitines to exclude mimics","Ammonia and glucose monitoring during acute illness"],"redFlags":["Intercurrent infection or fasting heralding an acute crisis","Rapidly rising lactate with encephalopathy","Acute hepatic dysfunction with coagulopathy","Cardiorespiratory instability or reduced consciousness"],"prognosis":"Outcome is poor: recurrent acidotic crises are frequently fatal in infancy or early childhood, and survivors typically have significant neurodevelopmental disability.","pitfalls":["Underestimating crisis risk during apparently minor infections.","Delaying anti-catabolic (glucose-containing) support and correction of acidosis while awaiting confirmatory results.","Attributing chronic lactate elevation to a sampling artefact and missing the diagnosis.","Assuming that stability between crises indicates low risk.","Failing to provide families with a written emergency and sick-day plan."],"management":["Prioritise prevention of metabolic crises by avoiding fasting, ensuring prompt sick-day intake and providing an emergency care plan for intercurrent illness","Treat acute decompensation aggressively with intravenous glucose, correction of severe lactic acidosis and support of hepatic dysfunction and coagulopathy","Offer mitochondrial supportive supplements and avoid drugs that impair mitochondrial function","Provide nutritional support, developmental therapies and management of hypotonia","Monitor blood lactate, liver function and coagulation, with heightened vigilance during illness","Coordinate multidisciplinary and palliative care given the high crisis-related mortality in early childhood","Offer genetic counselling and carrier testing for the autosomal recessive LRPPRC founder variant, particularly in the Saguenay-Lac-Saint-Jean population"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mitochondrial medicine reference texts","Leigh syndrome and mitochondrial disease clinical reference texts","Inborn errors of metabolism reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"leiomyosarcoma":{"name":"Leiomyosarcoma","system":"Oncology / Sarcoma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LMS","smooth muscle sarcoma","uterine leiomyosarcoma","retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma","vascular leiomyosarcoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A malignant tumour of smooth muscle differentiation arising most often in the uterus, retroperitoneum, large blood vessels (notably the inferior vena cava), and deep soft tissue of the limbs","Predominantly a tumour of middle-aged and older adults, with uterine disease occurring in women typically around or after the menopause","Retroperitoneal tumours often present late with a large abdominal mass and vague symptoms, while vascular tumours may cause obstruction or oedema","Diagnosis rests on morphology plus smooth muscle immunohistochemistry, including smooth muscle actin, desmin, and h-caldesmon","Genetically complex with highly aberrant karyotypes and frequent TP53 and RB1 pathway alterations rather than a single defining fusion","Wide surgical excision with clear margins is the mainstay of treatment; radiotherapy and multi-agent chemotherapy are used selectively by site and risk","Cutaneous (dermal) leiomyosarcoma is relatively indolent, whereas deep, retroperitoneal, and uterine tumours behave far more aggressively","Staging should include cross-sectional imaging of the primary and chest CT, as haematogenous spread to lung is characteristic"],"pathophysiology":"Leiomyosarcoma arises from smooth muscle or its precursor cells, including the smooth muscle of vessel walls, uterine myometrium, and mesenchymal cells of the retroperitoneum. Unlike translocation-associated sarcomas, it is genomically complex, characterised by widespread chromosomal instability and recurrent inactivation of tumour-suppressor pathways such as TP53 and RB1, which permits unchecked proliferation and impaired apoptosis. The retained smooth muscle phenotype accounts for its immunohistochemical profile, while accumulating genetic damage underlies its capacity for aggressive local growth and haematogenous metastasis, particularly to the lungs.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastrointestinal stromal tumour","Leiomyoma and cellular/atypical smooth muscle tumours (including uterine variants)","Dedifferentiated liposarcoma (in the retroperitoneum)","Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma","Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour","Metastatic sarcomatoid carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT or MRI of the primary site to define extent and vascular involvement","Chest CT to detect pulmonary metastases","Whole-body functional imaging in higher-grade or large tumours","Core biopsy with careful tract planning where feasible (recognising some uterine tumours are diagnosed after hysterectomy)","Immunohistochemistry (smooth muscle actin, desmin, h-caldesmon) to confirm smooth muscle differentiation","Echocardiography or dedicated vascular imaging for tumours involving the inferior vena cava or great vessels"],"redFlags":["A large retroperitoneal mass with weight loss or compressive symptoms","Rapidly enlarging uterine mass, particularly around or after the menopause, with postmenopausal bleeding","Lower-limb oedema or Budd-Chiari-like features from inferior vena cava involvement","New respiratory symptoms indicating pulmonary metastases","Recurrent disease after prior resection"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on site, tumour size, grade, and completeness of resection; superficial cutaneous tumours have an excellent prognosis, whereas deep soft-tissue, vascular, retroperitoneal, and uterine leiomyosarcomas are aggressive with significant metastatic risk and poorer survival.","pitfalls":["Assuming a uterine smooth muscle tumour is benign and using morcellation, which can disseminate an unsuspected sarcoma","Confusing retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma with dedifferentiated liposarcoma without appropriate immunohistochemistry and MDM2 testing","Underestimating vascular involvement and its surgical implications","Focusing on local control while neglecting pulmonary metastatic surveillance","Overtreating indolent superficial cutaneous tumours as if they were deep high-grade disease"],"management":["Wide surgical excision with clear margins is the mainstay of treatment for localised disease","Use adjuvant or neoadjuvant radiotherapy selectively to improve local control in deep soft-tissue tumours or where margins are close","Reserve systemic chemotherapy, typically anthracycline-based regimens such as doxorubicin with or without ifosfamide, for advanced, high-risk or metastatic disease, with later-line options including gemcitabine-docetaxel, trabectedin or pazopanib","Consider pulmonary metastasectomy for resectable lung metastases in selected patients","Undertake surveillance with cross-sectional imaging of the primary site and chest CT for pulmonary metastases","Manage all cases through a specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team","Provide supportive and palliative care for symptom control in advanced disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"leishmaniasis":{"name":"Leishmaniasis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["kala-azar","visceral leishmaniasis"],"chapter":233,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1781-1788","clinicalPearls":["Caused by obligate intracellular protozoa of the genus Leishmania (~20 species), transmitted by phlebotomine sandflies (Phlebotomus in the Old World, Lutzomyia in the New World); syndromes fall into three categories: visceral (VL), cutaneous (CL), and mucosal (ML) (p.1781).","Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar, Hindi for 'black fever') is caused by the L. donovani complex (L. donovani, L. infantum); classic presentation is abrupt moderate-to-high fever with rigors, progressive massive splenomegaly, moderate hepatomegaly, weight loss/wasting, and skin hyperpigmentation (p.1782-1783).","Lymphadenopathy is common in most VL-endemic regions but is rare on the Indian subcontinent (p.1783).","VL causes pancytopenia from bone marrow dysfunction and reticuloendothelial infiltration: leukopenia and anemia appear early, followed by thrombocytopenia; there is a marked polyclonal increase in serum immunoglobulins (hypergammaglobulinemia) (p.1782-1783).","Fever with neutropenia or pancytopenia in a patient from an endemic region strongly suggests VL; hypergammaglobulinemia in long-standing illness strengthens the diagnosis (p.1784).","Demonstration of amastigotes in tissue aspirate smears is the gold standard for VL: splenic smear sensitivity is >95%, bone marrow 60-85%, lymph node aspirate ~50% (p.1783).","The rK39 rapid immunochromatographic antibody test is used worldwide for VL field diagnosis (sensitivity ~98%, specificity ~90% in immunocompetent individuals; both lower in East Africa) (p.1783).","Cutaneous leishmaniasis: a sandfly bite is followed days-to-weeks later by a papule that becomes a nodule and ulcerates over weeks-to-months, with raised, indurated margins and a usually painless base; most lesions heal spontaneously over 2-15 months, leaving a scar (p.1786).","Mucosal leishmaniasis (espundia) is typically caused by L. (Viannia) braziliensis and is almost entirely confined to the Americas; it presents with nasal stuffiness and bleeding followed by destruction of nasal cartilage, septal perforation, and collapse of the nasal bridge (p.1787-1788).","Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (PKDL) develops in 2-50% of patients during or after cure of VL on the Indian subcontinent and in East Africa, presenting as hypopigmented macules, papules, nodules, or diffuse skin infiltration (p.1785).","Visceral leishmaniasis is a recognized cause of secondary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH); Leishmania is the most common parasite associated with HLH (p.1369-1370)."],"pathophysiology":"Sandflies inoculate flagellate promastigotes into the skin; neutrophils first take up the organisms, then macrophages and dendritic cells engulf them, and parasites multiply as nonflagellate amastigotes within macrophages, rupturing cells and invading new macrophages. Acquired resistance depends on a T helper 1 (TH1) response driven by IL-12, IFN-gamma, and TNF-alpha; most infected individuals control infection and never develop symptomatic disease. In active VL there is markedly elevated IL-10, which conditions macrophages for parasite survival, downregulates TNF-alpha and nitric oxide, and suppresses antigen presentation, so patients lack a positive leishmanin (Montenegro) skin test. In VL the reticuloendothelial organs (spleen, liver, lymph nodes, tonsils, intestinal submucosa) are heavily parasitized, and bone marrow dysfunction produces pancytopenia. Diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis reflects an absent cell-mediated/DTH response with high IL-10, TGF-beta, and IL-4, allowing unchecked parasite multiplication (p.1781-1786).","additionalDifferentials":["Malaria (VL is easily mistaken for it)","Typhoid (enteric) fever","Tuberculosis","Brucellosis","Schistosomiasis","Histoplasmosis","Splenomegaly from portal hypertension","Chronic myeloid leukemia","Tropical splenomegaly syndrome (hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly)","For CL: cutaneous tuberculosis, fungal infections, leprosy, sarcoidosis, and malignant ulcers"],"infectionMimics":["Malaria","Typhoid fever","Tuberculosis","Brucellosis","Schistosomiasis","Histoplasmosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Splenomegaly from portal hypertension","Chronic myeloid leukemia","Tropical splenomegaly syndrome","Malignant ulcers (for cutaneous disease)","Sarcoidosis (for cutaneous disease)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Splenic aspirate smear (highest sensitivity >95%, but invasive and potentially dangerous)","Bone marrow aspirate smear (sensitivity 60-85%)","Lymph node aspirate smear (sensitivity ~50%)","Culture of tissue aspirates (increases sensitivity)","rK39 rapid immunochromatographic antibody test (fingerprick blood/serum, result in ~15 min)","ELISA and indirect immunofluorescent antibody test (IFAT)","Qualitative and quantitative (real-time) PCR for leishmanial nucleic acid (allows species identification)","Loop-mediated isothermal amplification (LAMP) of nucleic acids (point-of-care for VL and PKDL)","Slit skin smears, lesion aspirates/biopsies for CL; tissue biopsy plus PCR for ML"],"redFlags":["Massive (hugely enlarged) splenomegaly with fever and pancytopenia in a patient from or returning from an endemic region","Severe anemia, which may cause congestive heart failure","Bleeding (epistaxis, retinal hemorrhages, gastrointestinal bleeding) from thrombocytopenia","Hypoalbuminemia with pedal edema and ascites in advanced illness","HIV co-infection (untreated VL is fatal in 100% of HIV-co-infected patients)","Secondary infections (pneumonia, tuberculosis, dysentery, gastroenteritis)","In ML: destruction of nasal cartilage/septal perforation; aspiration pneumonia may be fatal"],"prognosis":"Untreated visceral leishmaniasis is fatal in most patients, including 100% of those with HIV co-infection. With effective treatment, recovery is quick: defervescence, regression of splenomegaly, weight gain, and hematologic recovery occur within a week, and continued improvement over 12 months suggests cure; a small percentage relapse (usually within 6-12 months) but respond to retreatment. Cutaneous lesions heal spontaneously in most cases (2-15 months), leaving a scar, but may cause permanent scarring and social stigma. Diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis does not heal spontaneously and is difficult to treat. Mucosal leishmaniasis does not heal spontaneously despite strong TH1/DTH immunity; the more extensive the disease, the worse the prognosis (p.1782-1788).","pitfalls":["VL is easily mistaken for malaria and other tropical febrile illnesses; a careful travel history is essential in nonendemic countries.","Antibody detection tests (rK39, ELISA, IFAT) remain positive for years after cure, so they cannot be used to assess cure or detect relapse, and positive serology does not help diagnose PKDL.","In HIV/VL co-infection serodiagnostic tests may be negative in up to 50% of patients, and parasites may be found in unusual sites (e.g., bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, buffy coat).","Splenic aspiration, though most sensitive, is invasive and may be dangerous in untrained hands.","In PKDL, parasites are scanty and skin smear sensitivity is low, especially in hypopigmented macules (parasites are more readily found in nodular lesions).","Patients with active VL and IL-10-mediated suppression do not have a positive leishmanin (Montenegro) skin test.","In ML the strongly positive DTH response fails to distinguish past from present infection."],"management":["Treat visceral leishmaniasis with liposomal amphotericin B as first-line therapy, with alternatives including pentavalent antimonials, miltefosine and paromomycin depending on region and resistance","Manage cutaneous disease with local or topical/intralesional therapy for simple lesions and systemic antileishmanial therapy for complex, mucosal or diffuse disease","Provide supportive care in visceral disease including nutritional support, transfusion for severe anaemia and treatment of intercurrent secondary infections","In HIV co-infection, combine antileishmanial treatment with antiretroviral therapy and secondary prophylaxis against relapse","Monitor for treatment response, relapse and post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis after visceral disease","Implement vector control and personal protection such as insecticide-treated bed nets, alongside public-health case reporting"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal grading system; leishmaniasis is classified clinically by syndrome: visceral leishmaniasis (VL/kala-azar), cutaneous leishmaniasis (CL, with Old World and New World forms, plus leishmaniasis recidivans and diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis [DCL]), and mucosal leishmaniasis (ML). Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (PKDL) is a sequel of VL.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1781-1788"],"crossLinks":[]},"lemierre_syndrome":{"name":"Lemierre syndrome","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Postanginal sepsis","Necrobacillosis","Fusobacterium necrophorum sepsis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Infectious diseases references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic sequence is a recent oropharyngeal infection followed by septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein and metastatic septic emboli.","Fusobacterium necrophorum, an obligate anaerobic gram-negative bacillus of the oropharyngeal flora, is the usual pathogen.","It characteristically strikes previously healthy adolescents and young adults.","Suspect it when a sore throat is followed by unilateral neck pain or swelling, rigors and pleuritic chest pain rather than by recovery.","Septic pulmonary emboli typically produce multiple peripheral, often cavitating, lung nodules; other seeding sites include joints, bone and liver.","Contrast-enhanced neck imaging or Doppler ultrasound demonstrates the internal jugular vein thrombus that defines the syndrome.","Blood cultures grow the organism, but anaerobes are slow, so prolonged incubation and a high index of suspicion are essential.","Prolonged anaerobic-active antibiotic therapy is the mainstay; the role of adjunctive anticoagulation remains debated."],"pathophysiology":"Lemierre syndrome usually begins with an acute oropharyngeal infection, most often pharyngotonsillitis and occasionally otitis, mastoiditis or dental sepsis. Fusobacterium necrophorum, a fastidious obligate anaerobic gram-negative bacillus that lives as a commensal of the mouth and throat, invades the deep pharyngeal tissues and the peritonsillar and parapharyngeal spaces, gaining access to the tonsillar veins and thence the internal jugular vein. Virulence factors, notably a leucocyte-destroying toxin and lipopolysaccharide together with the organism's capacity to promote platelet aggregation, provoke local septic thrombophlebitis. The infected thrombus then becomes a source of septic emboli that disseminate through the bloodstream, most frequently to the lungs, producing septic pulmonary emboli and cavitating nodules, but also to joints, bone, soft tissue and liver. The combination of an anaerobic bacteraemia and a persistent endovascular focus drives a severe systemic inflammatory response.","additionalDifferentials":["Peritonsillar or parapharyngeal abscess without venous involvement","Deep neck space infection or Ludwig angina","Infective endocarditis with septic emboli","Community-acquired pneumonia or septic pulmonary emboli from another source","Bacterial meningitis","Epstein-Barr virus infectious mononucleosis, which may precede or predispose","Cervical lymphadenitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Aerobic and anaerobic blood cultures held for prolonged incubation","Contrast-enhanced CT of the neck and chest to show internal jugular vein thrombosis and emboli","Doppler ultrasound of the neck veins","Chest imaging for septic pulmonary emboli and cavitation","Full blood count and inflammatory markers","Echocardiography if endocarditis is suspected","Throat aspirate with species-specific or broad-range molecular testing"],"redFlags":["Unilateral neck pain, swelling or trismus following a sore throat","Rigors and spiking fever with pleuritic chest pain, suggesting septic pulmonary emboli","New respiratory distress or hypoxaemia","Features of metastatic infection such as septic arthritis or hepatic abscess","Rapid clinical deterioration or septic shock"],"prognosis":"Once frequently fatal, Lemierre syndrome now has a favourable outcome in most patients when it is recognised promptly and treated with prolonged appropriate antibiotics. Delayed diagnosis, extensive metastatic complications and septic shock still carry appreciable morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a sore throat that has worsened and thereby missing the diagnosis.","Attributing the pulmonary nodules to ordinary pneumonia and overlooking the neck as the primary focus.","Stopping antibiotics too early given the endovascular nature of the infection.","Failing to incubate anaerobic cultures for long enough to recover the organism.","Using anticoagulation uncritically without weighing bleeding risk, since the evidence for it is uncertain."],"management":["Give prolonged intravenous antibiotics with reliable anaerobic cover, such as a beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor like piperacillin-tazobactam, a carbapenem, or metronidazole-containing combinations, targeting Fusobacterium necrophorum","Achieve source control with drainage of peritonsillar, parapharyngeal or metastatic abscesses and empyema","Provide sepsis management with fluid resuscitation and organ support","Consider anticoagulation on a case-by-case basis, particularly with thrombus propagation or lack of clinical improvement","Monitor for and manage septic pulmonary emboli and other metastatic foci in joints, bone and liver","Escalate to intensive care for septic shock or respiratory failure, and involve ENT and infectious-diseases specialists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Infectious diseases reference texts","Anaerobic infection clinical reviews","Head and neck infection guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"lemmel_syndrome":{"name":"Lemmel syndrome","system":"Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["juxtapapillary duodenal diverticulum syndrome","periampullary diverticulum-induced obstructive jaundice"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lemmel syndrome describes obstructive jaundice or biliary obstruction caused by a juxtapapillary (periampullary) duodenal diverticulum compressing or distorting the distal common bile duct, in the absence of choledocholithiasis or tumour","A periampullary diverticulum arises within a short distance of the major papilla, typically along the medial duodenal wall where mesenteric vessels and the biliopancreatic ducts penetrate the muscular layer, an area of relative wall weakness","Presentation overlaps with classic biliary obstruction: right upper quadrant or epigastric pain, jaundice, and deranged liver biochemistry, sometimes with recurrent cholangitis or pancreatitis","The diverticulum is frequently missed on ultrasound and may masquerade as a periampullary mass, a pancreatic cystic lesion, or a gas-containing collection on cross-sectional imaging if not specifically considered","Diagnosis is one of exclusion, requiring demonstration of a periampullary diverticulum together with biliary dilatation and no obstructing stone or neoplasm","Cross-sectional imaging and MRCP characterise the diverticulum and the biliary tree, while endoscopy directly visualises the diverticulum and its relationship to the papilla","Management is individualised: many patients are managed conservatively or endoscopically, while surgery (diverticulectomy or biliary bypass) is reserved for refractory or complicated disease","The syndrome is more common in older adults, in whom periampullary diverticula are increasingly prevalent, and it may complicate or coexist with gallstone disease"],"pathophysiology":"Periampullary duodenal diverticula are acquired outpouchings that herniate through a point of muscular weakness where vessels and the biliopancreatic ducts traverse the duodenal wall, becoming more frequent with advancing age as the wall loses elasticity. In Lemmel syndrome, such a diverticulum produces biliary obstruction through several overlapping mechanisms: direct mechanical compression of the intramural or distal common bile duct and papilla by a distended or food-filled diverticulum; distortion or angulation of the terminal bile duct and sphincter complex altering its anatomy and drainage; and chronic inflammation or fibrosis around the papilla (papillitis) impairing sphincter function and bile flow. Stasis within the diverticulum can also promote bactibilia and predispose to ascending cholangitis and to stone formation, while impaired pancreatic outflow may precipitate pancreatitis. The net effect is intermittent or progressive obstruction of bile drainage with proximal ductal dilatation and cholestasis, without an intrinsic ductal stone or tumour.","additionalDifferentials":["Choledocholithiasis with distal common bile duct stone","Periampullary or pancreatic head carcinoma","Ampullary adenoma or carcinoma","Benign biliary stricture or sphincter of Oddi dysfunction","Chronic pancreatitis with distal duct stenosis","Mirizzi syndrome from an impacted cystic duct stone","Duodenal or periampullary neuroendocrine tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Liver biochemistry showing a cholestatic pattern with raised alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin","Abdominal ultrasound to assess biliary dilatation and exclude gallstones","Contrast-enhanced CT to characterise a periampullary diverticulum and exclude a mass","MRCP to delineate the biliary tree, the diverticulum, and the level of obstruction","Side-viewing endoscopy (duodenoscopy) to visualise the diverticulum and papilla directly","Endoscopic ultrasound to exclude a small periampullary or pancreatic tumour and assess the duct","ERCP where therapeutic intervention on the biliary tree is anticipated"],"redFlags":["Features of acute cholangitis such as fever, rigors, jaundice, and haemodynamic instability","Progressive painless jaundice or weight loss raising concern for underlying malignancy","Signs of acute pancreatitis complicating the obstruction","Evidence of duodenal diverticular perforation such as peritonitis or retroperitoneal gas","Gastrointestinal haemorrhage from a bleeding diverticulum"],"prognosis":"With accurate diagnosis and appropriate endoscopic, medical, or surgical management the outlook is generally good, and many patients are controlled conservatively; prognosis worsens where complications such as recurrent cholangitis, pancreatitis, or diverticular perforation supervene, or where an underlying malignancy is initially overlooked.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a gas- or debris-filled periampullary diverticulum for a pancreatic cystic lesion, abscess, or tumour on imaging","Attributing obstruction to the diverticulum without adequately excluding choledocholithiasis and periampullary malignancy","Overlooking a periampullary diverticulum on ultrasound and failing to pursue MRCP or endoscopy","Increased technical difficulty and higher complication risk of ERCP cannulation in the presence of a periampullary diverticulum","Failing to recognise the diverticulum as a source of recurrent cholangitis or pancreatitis in an older patient"],"management":["Relieve biliary obstruction endoscopically, using ERCP with sphincterotomy, biliary drainage or stenting as required","Treat associated acute cholangitis with appropriate antibiotics and urgent biliary decompression","Manage milder or intermittent obstruction conservatively with analgesia and monitoring","Reserve surgery such as diverticulectomy or biliary diversion for refractory obstruction or complications like diverticular perforation","Monitor liver biochemistry and watch for recurrent cholangitis or pancreatitis","Refer to hepatobiliary and endoscopy specialists for coordinated management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Blumgart's Surgery of the Liver, Biliary Tract and Pancreas"],"crossLinks":[]},"lennox_gastaut_syndrome":{"name":"Lennox-Gastaut syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGS","childhood epileptic encephalopathy with diffuse slow spike-wave"],"chapter":null,"source":"ILAE classification / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by a triad of multiple drug-resistant seizure types, a characteristic EEG, and intellectual impairment, usually beginning between one and eight years of age","Tonic seizures, especially in sleep, are the hallmark, alongside atonic (drop) attacks, atypical absences and frequent episodes of non-convulsive status epilepticus","The EEG shows diffuse slow spike-wave complexes when awake and paroxysmal fast activity during sleep","It is aetiologically heterogeneous, arising from structural, genetic, metabolic or perinatal causes, and may evolve from earlier encephalopathies such as West syndrome","Drop attacks cause frequent injury, so protective measures and helmets are often needed","Management usually requires broad-spectrum antiseizure medications and combination therapy, with dietary therapy, callosotomy or vagus nerve stimulation considered for refractory drop seizures","Cognitive and behavioural problems tend to worsen with ongoing seizures and are core to the encephalopathy"],"pathophysiology":"Lennox-Gastaut syndrome is a developmental and epileptic encephalopathy representing a shared final pathway of diffuse cortical network dysfunction from many underlying causes. Widespread abnormal thalamocortical synchronisation and impaired inhibition generate the characteristic generalised tonic seizures and slow spike-wave activity. Because the epileptic activity itself contributes to cognitive stagnation and regression, ongoing seizures and interictal discharges drive the intellectual and behavioural deterioration that characterises the syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["West syndrome (from which LGS may evolve)","Epilepsy with myoclonic-atonic seizures (Doose syndrome)","Dravet syndrome","Atypical benign focal epilepsy of childhood (pseudo-Lennox)","Developmental and epileptic encephalopathy with spike-wave activation in sleep","Structural focal epilepsy with secondary generalisation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Prolonged or video EEG demonstrating slow spike-wave and sleep-activated paroxysmal fast activity","Brain MRI to identify structural or malformative causes","Genetic and metabolic testing when no structural cause is found","Developmental and neuropsychological assessment","Evaluation for surgical or neuromodulation options in refractory disease"],"redFlags":["Frequent drop attacks causing recurrent head and facial injury","Prolonged non-convulsive status epilepticus with obtundation","Progressive cognitive regression accompanying uncontrolled seizures","Evolution from an earlier infantile encephalopathy signalling a severe course"],"prognosis":"The outlook is generally poor, with lifelong drug-resistant seizures and significant intellectual disability in most patients. Seizure freedom is uncommon, and there is increased mortality including from injury and SUDEP, though seizure burden and quality of life can be improved with tailored therapy.","pitfalls":["Labelling any child with mixed seizures and learning difficulty as LGS without the characteristic EEG features","Failing to investigate for a treatable structural or metabolic cause","Aggressive polytherapy causing sedation that worsens function without reducing seizures","Underestimating injury risk from drop attacks and omitting protective measures"],"management":["Use broad-spectrum antiseizure medication, typically starting with sodium valproate and adding agents such as lamotrigine, rufinamide, topiramate, clobazam or cannabidiol in combination","Target drop attacks specifically with agents such as rufinamide, cannabidiol or clobazam given their high risk of injury","Consider non-pharmacological options including the ketogenic diet, vagus nerve stimulation and corpus callosotomy for drug-resistant drop seizures, and resective surgery where a focal structural lesion is identified","Provide protective measures such as helmets to prevent injury from atonic seizures","Manage episodes of non-convulsive status epilepticus and provide a rescue plan for prolonged seizures","Provide developmental, educational and behavioural support with multidisciplinary input","Investigate and address the underlying structural, genetic or metabolic cause, with genetic counselling where a heritable cause is found"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ILAE classification of the epilepsies","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"lenz_majewski_syndrome":{"name":"Lenz-Majewski hyperostotic dwarfism","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTDSS1","Lenz-Majewski syndrome","hyperostotic dwarfism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare condition combining progressive skeletal hyperostosis (dense, thickened bone) with cutis laxa and growth failure","Skin is loose and wrinkled with prominent veins, contrasting strikingly with the increasingly sclerotic skeleton","Characteristic features include cutaneous syndactyly, proximal symphalangism, enamel hypoplasia and a large head with wide fontanelles","Caused by gain-of-function variants in PTDSS1, which encodes phosphatidylserine synthase 1, a lipid biosynthetic enzyme","Intellectual disability is common, and the progressive cranial and skull-base hyperostosis can compromise cranial nerves","The paradox of lax skin with dense bone is a useful diagnostic clue","Management is supportive, with monitoring for neurological complications of progressive hyperostosis"],"pathophysiology":"Lenz-Majewski syndrome is caused by gain-of-function variants in PTDSS1, the gene for phosphatidylserine synthase 1, which loses normal feedback inhibition and overproduces phosphatidylserine. The resulting disturbance of membrane phospholipid metabolism dysregulates bone and connective tissue cells, driving progressive hyperostosis of the skull and skeleton while simultaneously weakening dermal elastic tissue to produce cutis laxa. Digit anomalies, enamel defects and neurological effects reflect the widespread impact of this metabolic imbalance on developing tissues.","additionalDifferentials":["Craniodiaphyseal dysplasia and other sclerosing bone dysplasias","Cutis laxa syndromes","Pyle disease and metaphyseal dysplasias","Osteopetrosis","Progeroid disorders","Other syndromic causes of syndactyly and hyperostosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PTDSS1 sequencing where available, within a skeletal dysplasia panel","Skeletal survey demonstrating progressive hyperostosis and diaphyseal changes","Cranial imaging to assess skull-base thickening and neural foramina","Skin examination and, where needed, dermal histology for elastic tissue","Developmental assessment for intellectual disability","Dental review for enamel hypoplasia"],"redFlags":["Cranial nerve compromise (visual, auditory or facial) from progressive skull-base hyperostosis","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or neural foraminal narrowing","Progressive hearing or visual loss","Feeding or airway difficulty from craniofacial changes"],"prognosis":"The condition is progressive; survival into adulthood occurs but morbidity accrues from advancing hyperostosis, intellectual disability and cranial nerve complications, so long-term neurological surveillance is important.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis because the combination of lax skin and dense bone seems contradictory","Mistaking it for another sclerosing bone dysplasia without noting the cutis laxa and digital anomalies","Failing to monitor for cranial nerve compromise as hyperostosis progresses","Underestimating the developmental support needs"],"management":["There is no disease-specific drug therapy; management is supportive with structured surveillance for the progressive hyperostosis","Undertake regular neurological and cranial-nerve surveillance with cranial imaging, since skull-base hyperostosis can compress cranial nerves and narrow neural foramina","Refer for neurosurgical decompression when progressive hyperostosis threatens cranial nerves or the skull base","Provide developmental and educational support for intellectual disability","Arrange dental care for enamel hypoplasia and orthopaedic or hand-surgery assessment for syndactyly and digital anomalies","Offer genetic counselling for this dominant PTDSS1-related condition, which usually arises as a de novo variant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews / OMIM: Lenz-Majewski Hyperostotic Dwarfism","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"lenz_microphthalmia_syndrome":{"name":"Lenz microphthalmia syndrome (BCOR)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LMS","BCOR","microphthalmia with associated anomalies","oculo-digital syndrome of Lenz"],"chapter":null,"source":"Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked disorder characterised by microphthalmia or anophthalmia, often asymmetrical, associated with a range of extraocular malformations","Caused by variants in BCOR, a transcriptional corepressor important in early eye, skeletal, cardiac and dental development","Extraocular features include skeletal anomalies (digital and limb malformations), dental abnormalities, cardiac and urogenital defects and variable developmental delay","As an X-linked condition, affected males show the full phenotype while carrier females are typically mildly or not affected, though minor dental or ocular signs may occur","The ocular malformation is the most consistent feature and frequently causes significant visual impairment","BCOR variants also underlie oculofaciocardiodental syndrome in females, indicating a related allelic spectrum","Management is supportive and multidisciplinary, with ophthalmological care, dental and orthopaedic input and developmental support"],"pathophysiology":"Lenz microphthalmia syndrome is caused by variants in the X-linked gene BCOR, which encodes a corepressor that partners with transcription factors to regulate gene expression during development. BCOR is required for the coordinated formation of the eye, skeleton, heart, teeth and urogenital tract, so its disruption produces microphthalmia or anophthalmia together with a spectrum of extraocular malformations including limb and digital anomalies and dental defects. The X-linked inheritance explains the sex difference, with hemizygous males fully affected and heterozygous carrier females usually mildly affected or unaffected. Allelic variants in BCOR give rise to the related oculofaciocardiodental syndrome, reflecting the gene's broad developmental role.","additionalDifferentials":["Anophthalmia-microphthalmia from other genetic causes (e.g. SOX2, OTX2)","Oculofaciocardiodental syndrome (allelic BCOR disorder)","MIDAS/microphthalmia with linear skin defects","Fraser syndrome","CHARGE syndrome","Other X-linked syndromic microphthalmias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed ophthalmological examination and orbital imaging to characterise microphthalmia or anophthalmia","Skeletal survey to define digital and limb anomalies","Echocardiography to detect cardiac defects","Renal tract imaging for urogenital anomalies","BCOR molecular genetic testing with X-linked segregation analysis","Developmental assessment and dental evaluation"],"redFlags":["Bilateral severe microphthalmia or anophthalmia with profound visual impairment requiring early support","Significant congenital cardiac defect","Urogenital anomalies threatening renal function","Features suggesting a broader syndromic diagnosis needing urgent evaluation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the severity of the ocular and systemic malformations; visual outcome is often poor because of the microphthalmia, while overall prognosis and development vary with the associated cardiac, renal and neurodevelopmental involvement.","pitfalls":["Attributing isolated microphthalmia to a single cause without considering the broader BCOR spectrum and X-linked inheritance","Overlooking extraocular skeletal, cardiac and renal anomalies that require dedicated assessment","Failing to identify mildly affected carrier females and the allelic oculofaciocardiodental phenotype when counselling","Neglecting early developmental and visual support for affected children"],"management":["There is no specific drug therapy; management focuses on the ocular malformation and the associated systemic anomalies","Manage microphthalmia or anophthalmia with orbital conformers or expanders and prosthetics to promote orbital growth, alongside low-vision aids and visual rehabilitation","Assess and treat associated cardiac defects and urogenital anomalies, with surgical correction where indicated","Provide developmental support, dental care and management of skeletal and digital anomalies","Monitor cardiac and renal function and developmental progress through multidisciplinary follow-up","Offer genetic counselling for this X-linked BCOR condition, including carrier assessment of female relatives and family segregation analysis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders","GeneReviews: BCOR-related disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"leopard_syndrome":{"name":"LEOPARD syndrome (Noonan syndrome with multiple lentigines)","system":"Multisystem / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Noonan syndrome with multiple lentigines","NSML","multiple lentigines syndrome","cardiocutaneous syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["LEOPARD syndrome, now more often termed Noonan syndrome with multiple lentigines, is an autosomal dominant RASopathy that overlaps clinically with Noonan syndrome","The acronym summarises multiple Lentigines, ECG conduction abnormalities, Ocular hypertelorism, Pulmonary stenosis, Abnormalities of the genitalia, Retardation of growth and sensorineural Deafness","Most cases arise from specific missense variants in PTPN11 (encoding the phosphatase SHP-2); unlike the activating variants of Noonan syndrome, these are catalytically impairing and act in a dominant-negative manner","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is more characteristic than in Noonan syndrome and is a major determinant of outcome; pulmonary valve stenosis and conduction abnormalities also occur","The lentigines are numerous small dark macules that appear in childhood, increase with age and spare the mucosae; scattered cafe-au-lait macules are common","Overlap with neurofibromatosis (cafe-au-lait macules) and with other RASopathies can cause diagnostic confusion that is resolved by phenotype and genetic testing","Sensorineural deafness and variable learning difficulty may be present, and growth is often reduced","Management is multidisciplinary, with particular attention to cardiac surveillance (echocardiography and ECG) and hearing assessment"],"pathophysiology":"LEOPARD syndrome belongs to the RASopathies, a group of developmental disorders caused by germline variants that dysregulate the RAS-mitogen-activated protein kinase (RAS-MAPK) signalling pathway. The majority of cases result from heterozygous missense variants in PTPN11, which encodes the protein tyrosine phosphatase SHP-2. The variants responsible for LEOPARD syndrome characteristically reduce catalytic activity and act in a dominant-negative fashion, in contrast to the gain-of-function variants that cause most Noonan syndrome; a minority of cases are due to variants in other pathway genes such as RAF1 and BRAF. Perturbed RAS-MAPK signalling during development gives rise to the characteristic pigmentary lesions, craniofacial features, cardiac malformation and cardiomyopathy, growth restriction, genital anomalies and sensorineural hearing loss. The particular predilection for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy reflects the specific downstream signalling consequences of these variants in the developing and postnatal heart.","additionalDifferentials":["Noonan syndrome (allelic, but with activating PTPN11 variants and fewer lentigines)","Neurofibromatosis type 1 (cafe-au-lait macules, neurofibromas and Lisch nodules)","Other RASopathies including cardiofaciocutaneous and Costello syndromes","Carney complex and other lentiginosis syndromes, some with mucosal lentigines","Peutz-Jeghers syndrome (mucocutaneous pigmentation, but perioral and gastrointestinal)","Familial or isolated hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and pulmonary valve stenosis","Electrocardiography for conduction abnormalities","Audiological assessment for sensorineural hearing loss","Molecular genetic testing of PTPN11 and, if negative, other RASopathy genes such as RAF1 and BRAF","Growth monitoring with assessment of pubertal and genital development","Dermatological review to characterise the lentigines and cafe-au-lait macules","Developmental and cognitive assessment where learning difficulty is suspected"],"redFlags":["Symptoms or signs of obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy such as exertional syncope, chest pain or heart failure","Significant conduction abnormality or arrhythmia on ECG","Family history of sudden cardiac death","Severe pulmonary valve stenosis with evidence of right heart strain"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends largely on the cardiac phenotype: many individuals do well with surveillance, but significant or obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy increases the risk of heart failure and sudden cardiac death. Most other features are managed supportively, and life expectancy is often near normal in the absence of severe cardiomyopathy.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the condition as neurofibromatosis because of the cafe-au-lait macules","Assuming the cardiac risk mirrors Noonan syndrome and underestimating the propensity to hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Attributing multiple lentigines to benign familial freckling and missing the syndrome","Overlooking sensorineural deafness in a child with pigmentary and cardiac findings","Expecting a standard Noonan interpretation of PTPN11 testing when the causative variants and their functional effect differ"],"management":["There is no curative therapy; management centres on the cardiac phenotype, especially hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Manage hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with beta-blockers and assessment for septal reduction in obstructive disease, and treat significant pulmonary valve stenosis with balloon valvuloplasty","Assess sudden-cardiac-death risk and consider an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator, and pace clinically important conduction abnormalities","Provide hearing aids or other audiological support for sensorineural deafness, and monitor growth and pubertal and genital development","Offer dermatological review of the lentigines, with laser therapy where there is cosmetic concern","Undertake regular surveillance with echocardiography, electrocardiography and audiometry","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant PTPN11-related condition with cascade screening of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics reference texts","RASopathy management guidance","GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle"],"crossLinks":[]},"leprosy":{"name":"Leprosy (Hansen's Disease)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Hansen's disease"],"chapter":184,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1404","clinicalPearls":["Leprosy is a chronic infectious disease caused by Mycobacterium leprae, with manifestations largely confined to skin, peripheral nerves, eyes, and the upper respiratory tract; M. leprae has a predilection for peripheral nerves (p.1404).","Diagnosis rests on three cardinal signs, of which at least one establishes the diagnosis: (1) a hypopigmented or erythematous skin lesion with definite loss/impairment of sensation, (2) involvement of peripheral nerves shown by definite thickening with sensory impairment, and (3) a positive result for acid-fast bacilli on slit-skin smear or biopsy, or a positive biopsy PCR (p.1409-1410).","The differing immune responses to M. leprae produce a clinical spectrum; immunologic resistance is strong at the tuberculoid end and weakest at the lepromatous end (p.1404, p.1406).","Skin surfaces in tuberculoid lesions are dry, hairless, and anaesthetic owing to destruction of underlying cutaneous nerves, plus autonomic damage causing loss of sweating (p.1407).","Slit-skin smear bacteriologic index runs from negative (tuberculoid) up to 4+ to 6+ with globi (lepromatous), and falls an average of ~1 log unit per year on multidrug therapy (p.1404, p.1407-1408).","Leprosy is the principal treatable cause of peripheral neuropathy worldwide in some settings; nerve enlargement is often palpable (e.g., greater auricular, ulnar, median, radial, lateral popliteal, posterior tibial, sural, superficial peroneal) (p.1409-1410).","Treatment principle: WHO-recommended multidrug therapy (a combination of rifampin, dapsone, and clofazimine) is the standard of care and cures the disease; paucibacillary disease uses two drugs and multibacillary disease uses three, with the cure rate ~99% (no doses given here) (p.1410, p.1412).","M. leprae has never been grown in artificial media; it is propagated only in animal models (mouse footpad, armadillo), which underlies the difficulty of culture-based diagnosis (p.1404)."],"pathophysiology":"Leprosy is caused by Mycobacterium leprae, an obligate intracellular, acid-fast, rod-shaped bacterium with a preferred growth temperature of 27-30 degrees C, explaining its predilection for cooler surface tissues (skin, peripheral nerves, testes, upper airways) and relative sparing of inner viscera. After entry (assumed mainly via the respiratory tract, sometimes through damaged skin), the pathogenic process usually begins in peripheral nerves once bacilli are engulfed by Schwann cells. The histopathologic outcome - and thus the type of leprosy - depends on the host cell-mediated immune (CMI) response. The Ridley-Jopling spectrum (TT, BT, BB, BL, LL, with indeterminate IL as an early form) reflects this: at the tuberculoid pole, strong CMI produces epithelioid/giant-cell granulomas that destroy nerves (causing anaesthesia and muscle weakness) but contain few bacilli; toward the lepromatous pole, weak CMI permits unrestricted multiplication of bacilli within macrophages (lepra cells) as clumps/globi, with widespread dissemination and high bacterial load. Tissue damage is largely due to delayed hypersensitivity. M. leprae carries the species-specific phenolic glycolipid PGL-1, used in serodiagnosis. M. lepromatosis is a related species associated with diffuse leprosy of Lucio and Latapi.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypopigmented macules: pityriasis alba, vitiligo, progressive macular hypomelanosis, pityriasis versicolor, pityriasis rosea, postinflammatory hypopigmentation, sarcoidosis, post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis, morphea (p.1410)","Plaque/nodular lesions: granuloma annulare, cutaneous sarcoidosis, cutaneous leishmaniasis, lupus miliaris disseminatus faciei, nodular histiocytosis, lupus erythematosus, cutaneous T-cell lymphomas (especially mycosis fungoides), secondary syphilis (p.1410)","ENL (type 2 reaction) lesions: erythema nodosum of other etiologies, nodular vasculitis, cutaneous polyarteritis nodosa (p.1410)","Mononeuropathy: diabetes, amyloidosis, myxedema (p.1410)","Acute-onset polyneuropathy: Guillain-Barre syndrome, toxic polyneuropathy (p.1410)"],"infectionMimics":["Cutaneous leishmaniasis (p.1410)","Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (p.1410)","Secondary syphilis (p.1410)","Pityriasis versicolor (p.1410)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Vitiligo (p.1410)","Sarcoidosis / cutaneous sarcoidosis (p.1410)","Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, especially mycosis fungoides (p.1410)","Lupus erythematosus (p.1410)","Granuloma annulare (p.1410)","Diabetic, amyloid, or myxedema-related mononeuropathy (p.1410)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-skin smear from standard sites (right earlobe, forehead, chin, and left buttock/upper thigh), Ziehl-Neelsen stained, graded by bacteriologic index (0-6) and microbiologic index (p.1410)","Skin biopsy to confirm diagnosis, classify disease, support diagnosis of reactions, and assess cure; immunochemical staining for M. leprae antigens aids detection of early lesions (p.1410)","PCR / gene amplification (conventional, RT-PCR, multiplex targeting RLEP, 16S rRNA, SodA, antigen genes) - useful in bacteriologic-index-negative cases and for subclinical infection in contacts (p.1410)","PGL-1 antibody testing (ELISA, ML flow lateral-flow assay, UPC-LFA) - more often positive in multibacillary than paucibacillary disease (p.1410)","Lepromin (Mitsuda) skin test - measures cellular immunity; negative in LL/BL leprosy (p.1410)","Nerve function assessment: Semmes-Weinstein monofilament (or WHO-recommended ballpoint pen) touch testing, voluntary muscle testing, nerve conduction studies, and ultrasound of peripheral nerves (p.1410-1411)"],"redFlags":["Type 1 (reversal) reaction: delayed-hypersensitivity reaction with sudden upgrading of CMI; acute swelling and redness of existing lesions, painful/tender nerves with neuritis; can cause silent neuritis and irreversible sensory/motor nerve damage - a medical urgency to prevent disability (p.1407, p.1408-1409)","Type 2 reaction / erythema nodosum leprosum (ENL): immune-complex-mediated syndrome with crops of painful red nodules, fever, malaise, and possible neuritis, iridocyclitis, orchitis, nephritis/renal failure, and (late) amyloidosis (p.1408-1409)","Neuritis with nerve function impairment, including 'silent neuritis' (progressive sensory/motor loss without pain or tenderness) - requires prompt treatment to avoid permanent disability and blindness (p.1409, p.1410-1411)","Lucio's phenomenon in diffuse leprosy of Lucio and Latapi: vasculitis and thrombosis causing hemorrhagic skin infarction and necrotic ulcers (p.1409)","Eye involvement (corneal anaesthesia, iridocyclitis, lagophthalmos, corneal ulceration) threatening sight (p.1408)"],"prognosis":"Leprosy can be cured effectively: the cure rate with multidrug therapy is ~99% and treatment-failure rates are very low when therapy is completed. Relapse rates are well below 1% except in patients with a very high baseline bacillary load (bacteriologic index >=4), and relapses usually occur within 5 years of finishing therapy; antimicrobial resistance is rare, so relapsed multibacillary patients can be re-treated with the same regimen. The major determinant of long-term outcome is nerve damage and resulting disability, which drives stigma and social exclusion. Highest risk of (additional) nerve damage is in multibacillary patients with nerve impairment already present at diagnosis (up to ~65%); intermediate risk (~16%) in multibacillary patients without impairment or paucibacillary patients with impairment at diagnosis; and lowest (~3%) in paucibacillary patients without impairment. Leprosy reactions and new nerve damage can occur even after completing therapy, up to 3 years afterward. Cure (cessation of bacterial activity) must be distinguished from sequelae (nerve impairment, reactions, disabilities such as claw hand or neuropathic foot) that may persist and require chronic care.","pitfalls":["Leprosy is frequently diagnosed late - in non-endemic countries it is often misdiagnosed simply because it is not considered - increasing the risk of irreversible nerve damage and disability (p.1404, p.1410)","On the face, sensory impairment may be difficult to demonstrate because of generous bilateral sensory innervation, which can compensate for damage to certain nerve branches and obscure the indeterminate stage (p.1407, p.1409)","'Silent neuritis' / 'quiet nerve paralysis': nerve function can be lost progressively without pain, paresthesia, tenderness, or other signs of reaction - regular nerve function assessment is needed during and after therapy (p.1409, p.1411)","Distinguishing relapse from a type 1 reaction in paucibacillary disease can be difficult; a therapeutic glucocorticoid trial helps - improvement within 4 weeks favors T1R, lack of response favors relapse (p.1412)","Patients often equate cure with absence of symptoms, but in leprosy bacteriologic cure does not eliminate established nerve-impairment sequelae - this must be explained at completion of therapy (p.1412-1413)","Multidrug therapy for clinical leprosy has apparently not interrupted transmission, and subclinical/undiagnosed multibacillary cases are likely a main source of ongoing spread (p.1405)"],"management":["WHO multidrug therapy combining rifampicin, dapsone and clofazimine, tailored to paucibacillary versus multibacillary classification","Prompt treatment of lepra reactions: corticosteroids for type 1 (reversal) reactions and neuritis, and corticosteroids, clofazimine or thalidomide for type 2 erythema nodosum leprosum","Regular nerve-function assessment with sensory and motor testing, and corticosteroids for acute neuritis to prevent nerve damage and disability","Protection of anaesthetic hands and feet with footwear, wound and ulcer care, and ophthalmic review for ocular involvement","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and reconstructive surgery for established deformity and disability","Contact tracing, examination of household contacts and single-dose rifampicin post-exposure prophylaxis","Screen for glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency before dapsone and monitor for drug-induced haemolysis and other toxicity"],"severityClassification":"Ridley-Jopling classification (1962) defines five overlapping categories along the immunologic spectrum: tuberculoid (TT), borderline tuberculoid (BT), mid-borderline (BB), borderline lepromatous (BL), and lepromatous (LL), with indeterminate leprosy (IL) as an early form; immunologic resistance is strongest at TT and weakest at LL, with TT and LL relatively stable and BT/BB/BL unstable. For operational/treatment purposes the WHO simplified classification is used: paucibacillary disease = negative slit-skin smears at all sites (or, on clinical criteria, one to five skin lesions and no/only one involved nerve), and multibacillary disease = positive smear at any site (or six or more lesions and/or more than one involved nerve). The WHO disability grading system scores eyes, hands, and feet (grades 0-2), summed into the Eye-Hand-Foot (EHF) score as an overall impairment indicator (p.1405-1406).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1404"],"crossLinks":[]},"leptomeningeal_metastasis":{"name":"leptomeningeal metastasis","system":"Neurological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["leptomeningeal carcinomatosis","neoplastic meningitis","carcinomatous meningitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"European Association of Neuro-Oncology / EANO-ESMO guidance on leptomeningeal metastasis","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Suspect leptomeningeal disease when neurological signs are multifocal, involving the cerebral hemispheres, cranial nerves and spinal roots simultaneously in a patient with known cancer.","The commonest solid tumours are breast, lung and melanoma; among haematological malignancies, acute lymphoblastic leukaemia and aggressive non-Hodgkin lymphoma are typical.","Gadolinium-enhanced MRI of the whole neuroaxis should precede lumbar puncture and may show leptomeningeal or nerve-root enhancement, subarachnoid nodules or communicating hydrocephalus.","CSF cytology is the diagnostic gold standard but has limited sensitivity on a single sample; large-volume sampling and repeated taps substantially raise the yield.","Typical CSF findings are a raised opening pressure, elevated protein, low glucose and a lymphocytic pleocytosis; flow cytometry is essential when a haematological malignancy is suspected.","Treatment combines intrathecal chemotherapy (methotrexate, cytarabine, including the liposomal formulation), radiotherapy to symptomatic or bulky sites, and CNS-penetrant systemic or targeted therapy.","An intraventricular reservoir allows more uniform drug distribution than repeated lumbar administration but requires confirmation of unobstructed CSF flow first.","Management is often palliative, prioritising neurological function and symptom control; prognosis is generally measured in weeks to a few months without effective systemic options."],"pathophysiology":"Tumour cells reach the leptomeninges by haematogenous spread, direct extension from parenchymal or dural deposits, perineural or perivascular migration, or iatrogenic seeding, and then disseminate widely within the cerebrospinal fluid. Coating of the pia-arachnoid and cranial and spinal nerve roots produces the characteristic multifocal deficits, while obstruction of CSF pathways at the arachnoid villi or ventricular outflow causes communicating or obstructive hydrocephalus and raised intracranial pressure. Competition for glucose and an inflammatory response account for the low CSF glucose and raised protein, and disruption of nerve conduction gives the cranial neuropathies and radiculopathies.","additionalDifferentials":["Infective meningitis, including tuberculous and fungal","Neurosarcoidosis","Paraneoplastic or autoimmune encephalomyelitis","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculopathy","Radiation-induced neurological injury","Central nervous system vasculitis","Hypertrophic pachymeningitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gadolinium-enhanced MRI of brain and whole spine","Large-volume, repeated CSF cytology","CSF flow cytometry for haematological malignancy","CSF biochemistry with protein and glucose and paired serum glucose","CSF opening pressure at lumbar puncture","CSF tumour markers or circulating tumour DNA where available","Radioisotope CSF flow study before intraventricular therapy"],"redFlags":["Signs of raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus","Rapidly progressive multiple cranial neuropathies","Cauda equina syndrome","New seizures","Declining consciousness"],"prognosis":"Untreated leptomeningeal metastasis progresses rapidly and median survival is short, typically a few weeks to a few months. Outcome is somewhat better where effective, CNS-penetrant systemic therapy exists (for example targetable lung cancer or chemosensitive lymphoma or leukaemia).","pitfalls":["Performing lumbar puncture before imaging in a patient with raised intracranial pressure","Accepting a single negative cytology as excluding the diagnosis","Overlooking communicating hydrocephalus as a treatable cause of deterioration","Giving intraventricular therapy without first confirming unobstructed CSF flow","Attributing multifocal neurological signs to a single parenchymal lesion"],"management":["Intrathecal chemotherapy via lumbar puncture or an Ommaya reservoir, using methotrexate, cytarabine or liposomal cytarabine","CNS-penetrant systemic or targeted therapy against the primary tumour, such as tyrosine kinase inhibitors for targetable lung cancer or chemosensitive regimens for lymphoma and leukaemia","Radiotherapy to symptomatic or bulky sites and to relieve sites of cerebrospinal fluid flow obstruction","Corticosteroids for raised intracranial pressure and vasogenic oedema, with ventriculoperitoneal shunting or cerebrospinal fluid diversion for symptomatic hydrocephalus","Symptom control with analgesia, antiemetics and anticonvulsants for seizures","Early neuro-oncology multidisciplinary input and integration of palliative care given the poor prognosis","Monitoring for progressive cranial neuropathies, radiculopathies and treatment-related neurotoxicity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["EANO-ESMO clinical practice guidelines on leptomeningeal metastasis from solid tumours","DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer: Principles and Practice of Oncology","Oxford Handbook of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"leri_weill_dyschondrosteosis":{"name":"Léri-Weill dyschondrosteosis","system":"Skeletal dysplasia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LWD","SHOX-related dyschondrosteosis","Dyschondrosteosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Skeletal dysplasia references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Mesomelic (middle-segment) limb shortening with disproportionate short stature is the core feature.","Madelung deformity of the wrist is the clinical hallmark: bowing of the radius, dorsal prominence of the distal ulna and volar-ulnar tilt of the carpus.","Caused by SHOX (short-stature homeobox) haploinsufficiency — deletions or mutations of SHOX or its regulatory enhancers in the pseudoautosomal region (PAR1).","SHOX escapes X-inactivation and is present on both the X and Y chromosomes, which is why its loss underlies the short stature of Turner syndrome.","The phenotype is typically more marked in females, attributed to oestrogen effects on the growth plate.","Biallelic (homozygous or compound-heterozygous) SHOX loss causes the severe Langer mesomelic dysplasia, whereas Léri-Weill is the milder heterozygous form.","An increased carrying angle, an apparent muscular build, and forearm and wrist symptoms are commonly seen.","Early recognition guides growth-promoting therapy considerations, orthopaedic planning and genetic counselling."],"pathophysiology":"Léri-Weill dyschondrosteosis results from haploinsufficiency of SHOX, the short-stature homeobox-containing gene located in pseudoautosomal region 1 (PAR1) at the tips of the short arms of the X and Y chromosomes. SHOX encodes a transcription factor important for the proliferation and orderly differentiation of growth-plate chondrocytes. Most cases arise from whole-gene deletions, from deletions of upstream or downstream enhancer elements, or from point mutations that reduce functional gene dosage. Because SHOX escapes X-inactivation and is normally expressed from both sex chromosomes, loss of a single functional copy lowers gene dosage sufficiently to disturb endochondral growth, producing mesomelic limb shortening and the Madelung deformity of the wrist. The greater severity in females is attributed to the influence of oestrogen on the growth plate. Homozygous or compound-heterozygous loss of SHOX produces the far more severe Langer mesomelic dysplasia, while heterozygous loss also underlies a proportion of idiopathic short stature and contributes to the short stature of Turner syndrome, in which monosomy X leaves only a single SHOX copy. Inheritance is described as pseudoautosomal dominant.","additionalDifferentials":["Langer mesomelic dysplasia (biallelic SHOX loss) — the severe end of the same spectrum","Turner syndrome (45,X) — SHOX haploinsufficiency plus additional systemic features","Other mesomelic dysplasias, e.g., Robinow syndrome (ROR2/WNT5A), Nievergelt and Kozlowski types","Hereditary multiple exostoses causing forearm deformity and a pseudo-Madelung appearance","Post-traumatic or infective growth-plate injury producing an acquired Madelung-like deformity","Enchondromatosis (Ollier disease) affecting the forearm","Idiopathic short stature, a proportion of which is SHOX-related"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wrist and forearm radiographs for Madelung deformity (radial bowing, pyramidalisation of the carpus, dorsal ulnar subluxation)","Anthropometry with segment ratios (arm span and sitting height) to document mesomelia","SHOX molecular analysis: deletion/duplication testing (MLPA or array) including enhancers, plus sequencing for point mutations","Karyotype or genomic array to exclude Turner syndrome in girls with short stature","Skeletal survey to characterise the disproportion and exclude mimics","Serial growth-chart plotting and bone-age assessment","Endocrine and growth assessment to inform consideration of growth-promoting therapy"],"redFlags":["Progressive wrist pain, deformity or restricted movement from an advancing Madelung deformity","Marked disproportion or severe short stature suggesting biallelic (Langer) disease","Features suggesting Turner syndrome, which mandate a karyotype and carry cardiac, renal and gonadal implications"],"prognosis":"Stature is reduced but intellect and life expectancy are normal. The main morbidity is wrist dysfunction and cosmetic deformity from the Madelung deformity, which may require orthopaedic intervention. Early diagnosis allows growth, orthopaedic and reproductive counselling to be planned.","pitfalls":["Overlooking Turner syndrome in a short girl with a SHOX-type phenotype — always consider a karyotype","Relying on sequencing alone and missing SHOX or enhancer deletions, which require deletion/duplication analysis","Attributing the Madelung deformity solely to trauma or to exostoses","Underestimating the female predominance and severity, delaying diagnosis in girls","Forgetting the pseudoautosomal (not purely X-linked) inheritance when counselling families"],"management":["Recombinant growth hormone therapy to improve stature, as used for SHOX deficiency","Orthopaedic management of the Madelung deformity, including corrective radial osteotomy and Vickers ligament release where symptomatic","Wrist splinting, physiotherapy and analgesia for wrist pain and dysfunction","Serial monitoring of growth, skeletal proportions and bone age to guide timing of intervention","Paediatric endocrinology and orthopaedic multidisciplinary care","Genetic counselling and family screening given SHOX haploinsufficiency in the pseudoautosomal region","Reproductive counselling regarding transmission risk and the more severe biallelic Langer mesomelic dysplasia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nosology and classification of genetic skeletal disorders","Clinical genetics reference texts","Paediatric endocrinology and growth reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"leriche_syndrome":{"name":"Leriche syndrome (aortoiliac occlusive disease)","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["aortoiliac occlusive disease","Leriche syndrome","aortoiliac occlusion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard vascular surgery reference texts and peripheral arterial disease consensus guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of severe aortoiliac occlusive disease described by a classic triad of bilateral buttock, hip and thigh claudication, erectile dysfunction (impotence) in men, and absent or diminished femoral pulses","Results from atherosclerotic occlusion at the aortic bifurcation and iliac arteries, reducing inflow to both lower limbs","Typically affects patients with major atherosclerotic risk factors, particularly smoking, and often at a relatively younger age than distal disease","Claudication is proximal (buttock and thigh) rather than calf-predominant because the flow limitation is high in the arterial tree","Chronic collateralisation may mean the limbs are viable at rest despite extensive occlusion, so critical ischaemia is not always present","The ankle-brachial index is reduced and cross-sectional angiography defines the extent of aortoiliac disease","Management combines cardiovascular risk reduction and supervised exercise with revascularisation (endovascular stenting or aortobifemoral bypass) for lifestyle-limiting or critical disease"],"pathophysiology":"Leriche syndrome is caused by progressive atherosclerotic narrowing and occlusion of the distal abdominal aorta and both common iliac arteries at the aortic bifurcation. The resulting reduction in inflow to the pelvis and lower limbs produces exercise-induced ischaemia of the gluteal and thigh muscles, causing proximal claudication, and impaired penile arterial supply, causing erectile dysfunction. Because the disease develops gradually, collateral pathways enlarge to maintain resting perfusion, which explains why the limbs are often viable despite absent femoral pulses; when demand exceeds this collateral supply, symptoms and, in advanced disease, tissue loss occur.","additionalDifferentials":["Femoropopliteal or infrainguinal peripheral arterial disease","Spinal (neurogenic) claudication from lumbar canal stenosis","Hip or lumbar degenerative joint disease","Chronic venous disease with venous claudication","Erectile dysfunction from non-vascular causes","Aortic dissection or coarctation affecting distal flow"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ankle-brachial pressure index and segmental limb pressures to quantify inflow disease","Duplex ultrasound of the aortoiliac and femoral segments","CT or MR angiography to map the extent of aortoiliac occlusion and plan intervention","Catheter angiography when endovascular treatment is planned","Assessment of cardiovascular risk factors and coexisting coronary and carotid disease","Exercise testing where the diagnosis of claudication is uncertain"],"redFlags":["Rest pain, ulceration or gangrene indicating critical limb-threatening ischaemia","Acute limb ischaemia from superimposed thrombosis or embolism","Rapidly progressive symptoms or tissue loss","Widespread atherosclerosis heralding high coronary and cerebrovascular event risk"],"prognosis":"Revascularisation, particularly aortobifemoral bypass, has durable long-term patency and reliably relieves claudication and often erectile dysfunction; however, the underlying atherosclerosis means overall prognosis is dominated by cardiovascular risk, so aggressive risk-factor management is essential.","pitfalls":["Attributing buttock and thigh symptoms to spinal or hip disease and missing aortoiliac occlusion","Failing to palpate or document femoral pulses in a patient with claudication","Overlooking erectile dysfunction as a vascular symptom of the syndrome","Neglecting systemic cardiovascular risk reduction while focusing only on the limbs"],"management":["Revascularisation by aortobifemoral bypass grafting or endovascular aortoiliac angioplasty and stenting to restore inflow","Aggressive cardiovascular risk-factor modification: smoking cessation, statin therapy, blood-pressure control and glycaemic optimisation","Antiplatelet therapy, with consideration of a low-dose anticoagulant and antiplatelet combination for peripheral arterial disease","Supervised exercise programme for claudication, with cilostazol as symptomatic adjunct where appropriate","Management of erectile dysfunction, including phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors","Surveillance of graft patency and screening for coexisting coronary and carotid atherosclerosis","Foot care and monitoring for critical limb ischaemia and tissue loss, with vascular surgery multidisciplinary input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Global vascular guidelines on peripheral arterial disease management"],"crossLinks":[]},"lesch_nyhan_syndrome":{"name":"Lesch-Nyhan syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HPRT deficiency","HGPRT deficiency","hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase deficiency","juvenile gout with choreoathetosis and self-mutilation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked recessive disorder caused by variants in HPRT1, encoding hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase, so it almost exclusively affects males","Loss of purine salvage drives overproduction of uric acid, causing hyperuricaemia, hyperuricosuria, gout and urate nephrolithiasis","The complete deficiency (classic Lesch-Nyhan syndrome) produces a triad of hyperuricaemia, a dystonic-choreoathetoid movement disorder and compulsive self-injurious behaviour","Self-mutilation, typically biting of the lips and fingers, is a distinctive and highly characteristic feature","Early clues include orange 'sand' (urate crystals) in the nappy and delayed motor development with evolving dystonia","Partial HPRT deficiency (Kelley-Seegmiller syndrome) causes hyperuricaemia and gout with little or no neurological involvement","Urate-lowering therapy controls the systemic complications but does not improve the neurological or behavioural features","Protective measures and behavioural strategies are needed to prevent self-inflicted injury"],"pathophysiology":"Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase catalyses the salvage of the purine bases hypoxanthine and guanine back into nucleotides. When the enzyme is deficient, salvage fails and the purine bases are instead degraded to uric acid, while de novo purine synthesis is disinhibited; the combined effect is marked uric acid overproduction with hyperuricaemia and its complications. The neurological and behavioural phenotype is less well understood but is linked to dysfunction of dopaminergic pathways in the basal ganglia, which correlates with the dystonia, choreoathetosis and compulsive self-injury. The severity of the phenotype tracks closely with the amount of residual enzyme activity.","additionalDifferentials":["Cerebral palsy (dyskinetic type)","Other causes of childhood dystonia and choreoathetosis","Primary or secondary hyperuricaemia and juvenile gout","Neuroacanthocytosis and other self-mutilation syndromes","Autism spectrum disorder with self-injurious behaviour","Other X-linked intellectual disability syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and urine uric acid with the urinary urate-to-creatinine ratio","HPRT enzyme activity assay in erythrocytes or fibroblasts","Molecular genetic testing of HPRT1","Renal tract imaging for urate stones and nephrocalcinosis","Assessment of renal function","Developmental and behavioural evaluation"],"redFlags":["Evolving dystonia with developmental delay in a young boy","Compulsive self-injurious behaviour, particularly self-biting","Urate crystals or stones with renal impairment","Acute urate nephropathy or obstructive uropathy"],"prognosis":"Systemic urate complications can be controlled with urate-lowering therapy, preventing gout and nephropathy, but the neurological and behavioural features of the complete syndrome are not reversible and cause lifelong disability. Life expectancy is reduced, with renal failure and complications of the movement and behavioural disorder contributing to mortality.","pitfalls":["Assuming that urate-lowering therapy will improve neurological or behavioural symptoms","Misdiagnosing the movement disorder as dyskinetic cerebral palsy","Overlooking hyperuricaemia as a diagnostic clue in a boy with dystonia and self-injury","Failing to implement protective measures against self-mutilation"],"management":["Urate-lowering therapy with the xanthine oxidase inhibitor allopurinol to prevent gout, urate nephrolithiasis and urate nephropathy","Generous fluid intake and urinary alkalinisation to reduce urate and xanthine stone formation","Treatment of the dystonic-choreoathetoid movement disorder with agents such as baclofen and benzodiazepines","Prevention of self-injurious behaviour with protective devices, lip and dental guards, sometimes dental extraction, alongside behavioural therapy","Multidisciplinary care involving neurology, metabolic medicine, physiotherapy, occupational therapy and psychiatry","Monitoring of renal function and imaging surveillance for urate stones and nephrocalcinosis","Genetic counselling and carrier testing for this X-linked recessive HPRT1 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"leser_trelat_sign":{"name":"leser trelat sign","system":"Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sign of Leser-Trelat","eruptive seborrhoeic keratoses"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the sudden appearance and/or rapid increase in the number and size of multiple seborrhoeic keratoses, frequently on the trunk in a Christmas-tree distribution.","The eruption is often accompanied by pruritus of the lesions.","Classic associations are gastrointestinal adenocarcinoma (gastric, colorectal) and lymphoproliferative disease, with breast and lung carcinoma also reported.","It frequently coexists with malignant acanthosis nigricans and tripe palms, consistent with a shared growth-factor mechanism.","It remains a debated entity because seborrhoeic keratoses are very common with age; the abrupt eruption is the key discriminating feature.","New eruptive keratoses may be provoked by inflammatory dermatoses such as erythroderma or eczema, which must be excluded before attributing them to malignancy.","Lesions may regress following successful treatment of the underlying tumour."],"pathophysiology":"The sign of Leser-Trelat is postulated to result from tumour-secreted growth factors, particularly transforming growth factor alpha acting on epidermal growth factor receptors, with possible contributions from epidermal growth factor and other mediators. These stimulate rapid keratinocyte proliferation, producing the eruptive formation of seborrhoeic keratoses. The frequent association with acanthosis nigricans and tripe palms supports a common humoral, growth-factor-mediated pathway. A true paraneoplastic relationship is difficult to prove given the high background prevalence of seborrhoeic keratoses in older adults.","additionalDifferentials":["Age-related non-paraneoplastic seborrhoeic keratoses","Eruptive keratoses secondary to inflammatory dermatoses or erythroderma","Dermatosis papulosa nigra","Viral warts (verrucae)","Stucco keratoses","Epidermal naevi","Melanoma or other pigmented lesions mimicking a seborrhoeic keratosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Age-appropriate cancer screening including upper and lower gastrointestinal evaluation","CT of chest, abdomen and pelvis","Full blood count, blood film and LDH to screen for lymphoproliferative disease","Skin biopsy of a representative lesion to confirm seborrhoeic keratosis and exclude mimics","Faecal occult blood testing and colonoscopy","Mammography and other age- and sex-appropriate screening"],"redFlags":["Genuinely abrupt eruption of numerous seborrhoeic keratoses","Concurrent acanthosis nigricans or tripe palms","Associated weight loss, anaemia or altered bowel habit","Pruritic truncal eruption with systemic symptoms"],"prognosis":"When a true paraneoplastic marker, prognosis follows the underlying malignancy, often gastrointestinal adenocarcinoma or lymphoma; the keratoses themselves are benign and may regress with tumour treatment.","pitfalls":["Over-diagnosing the sign given how common seborrhoeic keratoses are with ageing","Not excluding a preceding inflammatory dermatosis as the cause of eruptive lesions","Missing coexisting paraneoplastic skin signs","Failing to biopsy and thereby missing a melanoma masquerading as a keratosis"],"management":["Investigate for and treat the underlying malignancy, most often gastrointestinal adenocarcinoma or lymphoproliferative disease, as the definitive intervention","Age- and sex-appropriate cancer screening including upper and lower gastrointestinal endoscopy and cross-sectional imaging","Exclude inflammatory dermatoses such as erythroderma and eczema that can provoke eruptive seborrhoeic keratoses","Symptomatic relief of pruritus with emollients and antipruritic agents","Destruction of troublesome keratoses by cryotherapy or curettage for symptoms or cosmesis","Monitor the eruption, which may regress with successful treatment of the tumour","Oncology referral and coordinated multidisciplinary care directed at the causative neoplasm"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"less_common_lymphoid_and_myeloid":{"name":"Less Common Lymphoid and Myeloid Malignancies","system":"Hematologic / Oncologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rare lymphoid malignancies","Rare myeloid malignancies","Unusual hematologic neoplasms"],"chapter":115,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.871","clinicalPearls":["This chapter covers the uncommon hematologic neoplasms, each individually accounting for under 1% of hematologic malignancies; the rare lymphoid tumors are all mature B-cell, T-cell, or NK-cell neoplasms (Harrison 22e p.871).","Splenic marginal zone lymphoma cells express surface immunoglobulin and CD20 but are negative for CD5, CD10, CD43, and CD103; absence of CD5 separates it from CLL and absence of CD103 separates it from hairy cell leukemia, and circulating cells are villous lymphocytes (Harrison 22e p.872).","Hairy cell leukemia is a frequent cause of a 'dry tap' on marrow aspiration because of increased reticulin fibers; it shows profound monocytopenia (predisposing to atypical mycobacterial infection), strong CD22/CD25/CD103 expression, tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase positivity, and the activating BRAF V600E mutation in most cases (Harrison 22e p.872-873).","Mediastinal (thymic) large B-cell lymphoma typically remains confined to the mediastinum, affects women more than men (ratio about 1:2-3) at a median age of 35-40 years, is CD20 positive and CD45 positive (unlike classical Hodgkin's), and may lack surface immunoglobulin and HLA class I/II expression (Harrison 22e p.873).","Primary effusion lymphoma presents with effusions usually without a tumor mass, is caused by HHV-8/KSHV (often EBV co-infected), occurs mainly in immunodeficiency such as AIDS, and follows a rapidly fatal course (Harrison 22e p.873).","T-cell prolymphocytic leukemia is aggressive with very high WBC counts often exceeding 100,000/uL, hepatosplenomegaly, and adenopathy; chromosome 14 inversion between q11 and q32 occurs in about 80% of patients (Harrison 22e p.874).","T-cell large granular lymphocytic leukemia is dominated by severe neutropenia and is frequently associated with systemic autoimmune disease such as rheumatoid arthritis; pure red cell aplasia occurs in 15-20% and the course is generally indolent (Harrison 22e p.874).","Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type, is an EBV-related angiocentric/angiodestructive CD56-positive lesion prevalent in Asia, Mexico, and Central/South America; historic names include lethal midline granuloma and polymorphic reticulosis (Harrison 22e p.874).","Enteropathy-type T-cell lymphoma is a rare complication of longstanding celiac disease, usually arising in jejunum or ileum, with most patients carrying the celiac-associated HLA DQA1*0501 or DQB1*0201 genotype (Harrison 22e p.874).","Chronic neutrophilic leukemia is a clonal proliferation of mature neutrophils molecularly defined by activating CSF3R mutations (most commonly the membrane-proximal T618I, found in about 90% of cases); diagnosis requires excluding reactive neutrophilia and other myeloid neoplasms (Harrison 22e p.875-876).","Systemic mastocytosis diagnosis rests on a major criterion (multifocal aggregates of >=15 mast cells) or three minor criteria including >25% atypical mast cells, aberrant mast cell expression of CD25/CD2/CD30, KIT D816V or other activating KIT mutation, and serum tryptase >20 ng/mL (Harrison 22e p.881).","Transient myeloproliferative disorder occurs in about 10% of infants with Down syndrome, carries somatic GATA1 mutations, and either regresses spontaneously (about 75%) or progresses to acute megakaryoblastic leukemia (about 25%) (Harrison 22e p.879)."],"pathophysiology":"These entities are individually rare clonal neoplasms of the lymphoid or myeloid lineages. Rare lymphoid tumors are mature B-cell, T-cell, or NK-cell neoplasms; several B-cell variants of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma have distinctive biology, such as intravascular large B-cell lymphoma (confined to vessel lumens, postulated to reflect defective adhesion molecule and homing function with absent beta-1 integrin and ICAM-1), primary effusion lymphoma (HHV-8/KSHV-driven, frequently EBV co-infected), and lymphomatoid granulomatosis (an angiodestructive EBV-infected monoclonal B-cell proliferation outnumbered by reactive T cells). Many T/NK entities are EBV-associated and angiocentric. On the myeloid side, distinct molecular drivers define the entities: activating CSF3R mutations in chronic neutrophilic leukemia, GM-CSF hypersensitivity from dysregulated RAS/MAPK signaling (mutually exclusive RAS, PTPN11, NF1 mutations) in juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia, somatic GATA1 mutations in transient myeloproliferative disorder of Down syndrome, and constitutively active tyrosine kinase gene fusions (involving PDGFRA such as FIP1L1-PDGFRA, PDGFRB, FGFR1, ABL1, JAK2, FLT3) in eosinophilic myeloid/lymphoid neoplasms. Mastocytosis reflects neoplastic mast cell proliferation typically driven by KIT D816V. Dendritic/histiocytic neoplasms arise from antigen-presenting or antigen-processing cell lineages; Langerhans cell histiocytosis cells are S100+/CD1a+ with Birbeck granules and about 55% carry BRAF V600E.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (distinguished from splenic marginal zone lymphoma by CD5 positivity)","Mantle cell lymphoma (overlaps with B-cell prolymphocytic leukemia via CD5 and t(11;14))","Nodular sclerosing Hodgkin's disease (versus mediastinal large B-cell lymphoma; the latter is CD45 positive, lacks lacunar Reed-Sternberg variants)","Anaplastic large T-cell lymphoma with t(2;5) (versus primary cutaneous CD30+ T-cell lymphoma, which lacks t(2;5))","Chronic myeloid leukemia, BCR::ABL1 positive (versus atypical CML and chronic neutrophilic leukemia)"],"infectionMimics":["Reactive neutrophilia from infection or inflammation (mimics chronic neutrophilic leukemia)","Reactive monocytosis from tuberculosis, fungal infection, subacute bacterial endocarditis, viral or protozoal infection (mimics chronic myelomonocytic leukemia)","Hypereosinophilia from tissue-invasive helminths and other parasitic infection (mimics primary/clonal eosinophilic disorders)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Plasma cell neoplasms and metastatic solid tumors producing secondary neutrophilia (mimic chronic neutrophilic leukemia)","Connective tissue disease, sarcoidosis, lipid storage disorders, postsplenectomy state, and marrow regeneration after chemotherapy (mimic CMML monocytosis)","Allergic/vasculitic disease and drug reactions (mimic primary hypereosinophilia)","Hereditary alpha-tryptasemia (raises serum tryptase, confounding mastocytosis criteria)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood smear and bone marrow morphology with immunophenotyping/flow cytometry for lineage markers","Cytogenetics and molecular testing: CSF3R (CNL), KIT D816V (mastocytosis), GATA1 (transient myeloproliferative disorder), SETBP1, RAS-pathway mutations (JMML)","FISH or RT-PCR screening for PDGFRA and PDGFRB rearrangements in hypereosinophilia","Serum tryptase (mast cell disease; elevation also prompts FIP1L1-PDGFRA testing) and soluble CD25 as a hairy cell leukemia activity marker","T-cell receptor gene rearrangement studies and T-cell immunophenotyping in hypereosinophilia to detect lymphocytic-variant disease","Echocardiogram and serum troponin to screen for cardiac involvement in hypereosinophilic syndrome (apical thrombus, endocardial thickening, valve abnormality)","Electron microscopy/immunostaining for Birbeck granules, CD1a, S100, and langerin in Langerhans cell histiocytosis"],"redFlags":["Aggressive NK cell leukemia presenting with fever, pancytopenia, hepatosplenomegaly, hemophagocytosis, coagulopathy, or multiorgan failure","Intravascular large B-cell lymphoma with small-vessel occlusion, neurologic symptoms, and thrombus formation, often diagnosed only late or at autopsy","Hemophagocytic syndrome complicating extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma or subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma (can drive a fulminant downhill course)","Cardiac involvement in hypereosinophilic syndrome (cardiomyopathy, ventricular apical thrombus, elevated troponin)","Mast cell organopathy C findings: cytopenias, hepatomegaly with portal hypertension/ascites, splenomegaly with hypersplenism, GI infiltration with malabsorption and weight loss, large osteolytic lesions with pathologic fractures","True mast cell leukemia, regarded as an oncologic emergency requiring expedited diagnosis","Bowel perforation from tumor response in treated enteropathy-type T-cell lymphoma"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely by entity. Indolent disorders include splenic marginal zone lymphoma (median survival over 10 years), T-cell large granular lymphocytic leukemia, and primary cutaneous CD30+ T-cell lymphoma (5-year survival over 90%). Aggressive entities carry poor outcomes: T-cell prolymphocytic leukemia (median survival about 12 months), enteropathy-type T-cell lymphoma (median 7-11 months), hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma (median about 2 years), primary effusion lymphoma (death typically within 6 months), and intravascular large B-cell lymphoma. Mediastinal large B-cell lymphoma is potentially curable with good multi-year survival. In extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type, survival tracks with the number of risk factors (B symptoms, advanced stage, elevated LDH, regional node involvement), with 5-year survival ranging from 81% (zero risk factors) down to 7% (three to four factors). Chronic neutrophilic leukemia has a median survival of about 2 years, atypical CML around 10-25 months, and CMML survival ranges by Mayo Molecular Model risk from about 16 to 97 months. Histiocytic sarcoma has a poor prognosis with median survival measured in months to about 2 years.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin's lymphoma as classical Hodgkin's; the L&H 'popcorn' cell is a clonal B cell expressing CD45/CD79a/CD20/CD19/BCL2 and lacking CD30/CD15, behaving more like indolent B-cell NHL","Confusing B-cell prolymphocytic leukemia with leukemic mantle cell lymphoma, since both can express CD5 and t(11;14) and no reliable distinguishing criteria have emerged","Overlooking that a dry tap on marrow aspiration may signal hairy cell leukemia, myelofibrosis, metastatic carcinoma, CML, or acute leukemia rather than a technical failure","Mistaking lymphomatoid granulomatosis for extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type, since both are angiodestructive and EBV-related","Attributing the presence of t(2;5) in a cutaneous CD30+ lesion to primary cutaneous disease; its presence should prompt evaluation for systemic anaplastic T-cell lymphoma","Failing to exclude reactive causes of neutrophilia, monocytosis, or eosinophilia before diagnosing CNL, CMML, or a primary eosinophilic disorder","Diagnosing systemic mastocytosis without accounting for hereditary alpha-tryptasemia when interpreting elevated serum tryptase"],"management":["Entity-specific therapy directed by precise histological and molecular classification, such as purine analogues (cladribine) or BRAF-directed therapy for hairy cell leukaemia","Rituximab-based immunochemotherapy for CD20-positive B-cell entities such as splenic marginal zone and mediastinal large B-cell lymphoma, with splenectomy for symptomatic splenic marginal zone disease","Tyrosine kinase inhibitor therapy (imatinib) for eosinophilic neoplasms with PDGFRA or PDGFRB rearrangement, and KIT-directed therapy for advanced systemic mastocytosis","Treatment of underlying viral drivers, including antiretroviral therapy for HIV-associated HHV-8-driven primary effusion lymphoma","Alemtuzumab-based therapy for T-cell prolymphocytic leukaemia, with consideration of allogeneic stem cell transplantation","Supportive care with infection prophylaxis (especially given the monocytopenia of hairy cell leukaemia), transfusion support and management of cytopenias","Surveillance for organ involvement, including cardiac assessment for endomyocardial disease in hypereosinophilic syndrome","Referral to specialist haemato-oncology and consideration of clinical trials given the rarity of these entities"],"severityClassification":"Several entities have formal staging/risk schemes. Myeloid neoplasms are classified by the ICC and WHO systems using blood counts, smear, marrow morphology, and cytogenetic/molecular tests. CMML is graded as CMML-1 (<10% marrow and <5% blood blasts) and CMML-2 (10-19% marrow or 5-19% blood blasts), with the Mayo Molecular Model assigning low to high risk by truncating ASXL1 mutation, absolute monocytes >10 x10^9/L, hemoglobin <10 g/dL, platelets <100 x10^9/L, and circulating immature myeloid cells. Systemic mastocytosis is subclassified into indolent, smoldering (requires two or more B findings), aggressive, SM with associated myeloid/hematologic neoplasm, and mast cell leukemia (>=20% mast cells in marrow aspirate); advanced SM is defined by an associated neoplasm or organopathy (C findings). Hypereosinophilia is defined as absolute eosinophil count >=1500 x10^9/L, with hypereosinophilic syndrome requiring eosinophil-mediated organ damage. Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma nasal type uses a four-factor risk model (B symptoms, advanced stage, elevated LDH, regional node involvement).","references":["Harrison 22e p.871","Harrison 22e p.872","Harrison 22e p.873","Harrison 22e p.874","Harrison 22e p.875","Harrison 22e p.876","Harrison 22e p.877","Harrison 22e p.878","Harrison 22e p.879","Harrison 22e p.880","Harrison 22e p.881","Harrison 22e p.882"],"crossLinks":[]},"lethal_multiple_pterygium_syndrome":{"name":"Lethal multiple pterygium syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LMPS","lethal multiple pterygium syndrome, CHRNG-related","fetal akinesia with pterygia","CHRNG","CHRNA1","CHRND"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / Gorlin's Syndromes of the Head and Neck","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A prenatally lethal form of multiple pterygium syndrome with severe foetal akinesia, multiple contractures and extensive webbing across joints","Foetal hydrops, cystic hygroma and generalised oedema are characteristic and often lead to intrauterine or early neonatal death","Pulmonary hypoplasia from absent foetal breathing movements is a usual cause of death","Craniofacial features include hypertelorism, micrognathia, a small mouth and low-set ears","Most commonly caused by autosomal recessive variants in acetylcholine receptor subunit genes, particularly CHRNG, with CHRNA1 and CHRND also implicated","It lies at the severe end of the spectrum whose milder counterpart is Escobar (non-lethal) syndrome","Management is supportive and palliative, with the emphasis on accurate diagnosis, postmortem confirmation and genetic counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Lethal multiple pterygium syndrome is caused by severe loss of function in genes encoding subunits of the foetal acetylcholine receptor, most often CHRNG (gamma subunit) but also CHRNA1 and CHRND. Complete failure of foetal neuromuscular transmission abolishes foetal movement during a critical developmental period. The profound akinesia produces multiple joint contractures, webbing across the flexed joints, pulmonary hypoplasia from absent breathing movements, and impaired lymphatic drainage manifesting as cystic hygroma, hydrops and generalised oedema. The completeness of the receptor defect explains why this form is lethal in utero or shortly after birth, in contrast to the survivable Escobar variant.","additionalDifferentials":["Escobar (non-lethal) multiple pterygium syndrome","Pena-Shokeir syndrome / foetal akinesia deformation sequence","Congenital myotonic dystrophy","Spinal muscular atrophy with arthrogryposis","Trisomy 18 and other aneuploidies","Congenital myasthenic syndromes with severe prenatal onset"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed prenatal ultrasound showing akinesia, contractures, webbing, hydrops and cystic hygroma","Postmortem/foetal examination with clinical photographs to confirm the phenotype","Molecular genetic testing for CHRNG, CHRNA1 and CHRND and a foetal akinesia panel","Chromosomal microarray or karyotype to exclude aneuploidy","Muscle and nerve histology if the neuromuscular basis is uncertain","Placental and lymphatic assessment where hydrops is present"],"redFlags":["Foetal hydrops and cystic hygroma indicating a lethal course","Severe pulmonary hypoplasia incompatible with postnatal survival","Progressive antenatal akinesia and polyhydramnios","Recurrence in subsequent pregnancies given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is uniformly poor, with death occurring in utero or in the early neonatal period; care is supportive and palliative, and the priority is establishing a genetic diagnosis to inform recurrence risk and prenatal options.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish the lethal form from the survivable Escobar variant when counselling","Not securing genetic confirmation, leaving recurrence risk uncertain","Overlooking the autosomal recessive one-in-four recurrence risk","Missing the opportunity for postmortem examination to guide future pregnancies"],"management":["Comfort-focused palliative care given the uniformly lethal prognosis in utero or the early neonatal period","Confirmation of the genetic diagnosis by testing of CHRNG, CHRNA1 and CHRND and a foetal akinesia panel to guide counselling","Perinatal palliative care planning and bereavement support for the family","Non-directive genetic counselling regarding the autosomal recessive recurrence risk","Offer of prenatal diagnosis and preimplantation genetic testing in future pregnancies","Multidisciplinary involvement of foetal medicine, neonatology, clinical genetics and palliative care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Gorlin's Syndromes of the Head and Neck"],"crossLinks":[]},"leukocyte_adhesion_deficiency":{"name":"Leukocyte adhesion deficiency","system":"Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LAD","LAD-1","leucocyte adhesion deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Leukocyte adhesion deficiency is a group of autosomal recessive disorders in which neutrophils cannot adhere to and migrate across the vascular endothelium to reach sites of infection","The most common form, LAD-1, results from defects in the beta-2 integrin common chain (CD18), impairing firm adhesion of leucocytes","A cardinal clinical clue is delayed separation of the umbilical cord accompanied by omphalitis in the newborn period","Recurrent bacterial and fungal soft-tissue infections occur characteristically without pus formation, because neutrophils fail to enter the tissues","A persistently high blood neutrophil count (marked leucocytosis) is typical, since neutrophils remain in the circulation rather than migrating out","Severe gingivitis, periodontitis, and poor wound healing are frequent features","LAD-2 involves defective fucosylation of selectin ligands and is associated with the Bombay blood group and developmental abnormalities, while LAD-3 combines adhesion failure with a bleeding tendency due to impaired integrin activation in platelets","Management includes aggressive treatment of infections and antimicrobial prophylaxis, with haematopoietic stem cell transplantation offering cure in severe LAD-1"],"pathophysiology":"Leukocyte adhesion deficiency arises from defects in the adhesion cascade that normally allows leucocytes to roll along, firmly adhere to, and transmigrate across activated endothelium. In LAD-1, mutations in ITGB2 reduce or abolish the shared CD18 subunit of the beta-2 integrins, so neutrophils cannot form the firm adhesion needed to exit the bloodstream, leaving tissues undefended despite abundant circulating neutrophils. LAD-2 results from a defect in GDP-fucose transport that impairs synthesis of sialyl-Lewis X, the selectin ligand required for rolling, and also causes the rare Bombay phenotype and neurodevelopmental problems. LAD-3 stems from defective inside-out signalling (kindlin-3/FERMT3) that prevents activation of integrins on both leucocytes and platelets, combining infection susceptibility with a Glanzmann-like bleeding disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic granulomatous disease","Congenital or cyclic neutropenia","Chediak-Higashi syndrome","Other neutrophil functional disorders","Glanzmann thrombasthenia (for the bleeding seen in LAD-3)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flow cytometry for CD18 and CD11 expression on neutrophils (reduced or absent in LAD-1)","Full blood count demonstrating marked neutrophil leucocytosis","Genetic testing (ITGB2 for LAD-1, SLC35C1 for LAD-2, FERMT3 for LAD-3)","Neutrophil adhesion and chemotaxis functional assays","Assessment of sialyl-Lewis X expression and blood group testing where LAD-2 is suspected","Platelet aggregation studies when LAD-3 is considered"],"redFlags":["Delayed umbilical cord separation with omphalitis in a neonate requiring urgent evaluation","Severe soft-tissue or invasive infection without pus formation requiring prompt antimicrobial therapy","Life-threatening sepsis given the severe impairment of neutrophil migration","Live vaccines and non-irradiated blood products should be approached cautiously, especially peri-transplant","Bleeding episodes in suspected LAD-3"],"prognosis":"Severe LAD-1 with very low CD18 expression carries a high risk of early fatal infection unless corrected by haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, whereas moderate phenotypes with partial expression can survive longer with vigilant infection control; LAD-2 tends to have a milder infectious course dominated by developmental issues, and LAD-3 combines infection and bleeding risks.","pitfalls":["Interpreting the high neutrophil count as ongoing bacterial infection rather than a diagnostic clue to impaired migration","Expecting pus and abscess formation and being falsely reassured by its absence","Missing the neonatal sign of delayed cord separation","Failing to distinguish the LAD subtypes, which differ in associated features and management","Delaying transplant referral in severe LAD-1"],"management":["Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the definitive curative treatment for severe LAD-1","Prompt aggressive antimicrobial therapy for bacterial and fungal soft-tissue infections with broad-spectrum antibiotics and antifungals","Long-term antibacterial and antifungal prophylaxis to reduce recurrent infection","Oral fucose supplementation as specific therapy in LAD-2","Meticulous oral and periodontal care and careful wound management given impaired healing","Vigilant surveillance for infection, and management of the bleeding tendency in LAD-3","Genetic counselling and family screening (ITGB2, SLC35C1, FERMT3), with consideration of gene therapy where available, under clinical immunology multidisciplinary care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"leukocyte_chemotactic_factor_2_amyloidosis":{"name":"Leukocyte Chemotactic Factor 2 Amyloidosis (ALECT2)","system":"Multisystem / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ALECT2 amyloidosis","LECT2 amyloidosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["ALECT2 is caused by deposition of leukocyte chemotactic factor 2, a protein of largely wild-type sequence, rather than by a classic amyloidogenic mutation.","It is a common cause of renal amyloidosis in specific populations, notably people of Mexican or Hispanic, Punjabi, First Nations, Native American and Egyptian ancestry.","The kidney is the dominant target, typically producing slowly progressive chronic kidney disease with only sub-nephrotic proteinuria, which is lighter than in AL or AA amyloidosis.","The liver is the second most commonly affected organ, and incidental hepatic ALECT2 is sometimes found at biopsy or resection.","Amyloid characteristically deposits in the renal cortical interstitium and glomeruli.","A homozygous common polymorphism in the LECT2 gene is associated, but the disease is not inherited in a classic Mendelian pattern.","There is no specific antifibril therapy, so management is supportive with attention to chronic kidney disease and its complications.","Accurate typing by mass spectrometry is essential, as ALECT2 is easily misclassified."],"pathophysiology":"Leukocyte chemotactic factor 2 is a hepatically synthesised protein involved in inflammation and tissue repair. In ALECT2 the essentially wild-type protein deposits as amyloid, with a strong ancestral predisposition suggesting genetic susceptibility linked to a common LECT2 polymorphism rather than a destabilising point mutation. Fibrils accumulate preferentially in the renal interstitium and glomeruli, and in the liver, producing indolent organ dysfunction. Because proteinuria is characteristically modest despite significant renal impairment, the disease can be clinically silent until chronic kidney disease is advanced. The mechanisms triggering deposition of the normal-sequence protein remain incompletely defined.","additionalDifferentials":["AL (immunoglobulin light-chain) amyloidosis","AA amyloidosis","Other hereditary amyloidoses such as fibrinogen and apolipoprotein A-I types","Diabetic and hypertensive nephropathy","Other tubulointerstitial diseases","Other causes of chronic kidney disease with modest proteinuria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal biopsy with Congo red staining showing interstitial and glomerular amyloid","Amyloid typing by mass spectrometry, which is essential to identify LECT2","Serum free light chains and immunofixation to exclude AL amyloidosis","Serum amyloid A and C-reactive protein to exclude AA amyloidosis","Renal function and quantified proteinuria","Liver function tests and hepatic imaging","SAP scintigraphy to assess organ burden"],"redFlags":["Progressive chronic kidney disease with disproportionately mild proteinuria in a patient of at-risk ancestry","Unexplained hepatic amyloid found on biopsy","Declining renal function without an obvious alternative cause"],"prognosis":"ALECT2 is generally indolent, but renal involvement tends to progress slowly to end-stage kidney disease over years. There is no proven disease-specific therapy, so outcomes depend on chronic kidney disease management and on renal replacement or transplantation.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying as AL or AA amyloidosis without mass spectrometry typing","Discounting amyloid because proteinuria is only sub-nephrotic","Overlooking the diagnosis outside the classically affected ethnic groups","Giving inappropriate chemotherapy on the assumption of AL amyloidosis"],"management":["Supportive chronic kidney disease management, as there is no proven disease-specific therapy, with blood-pressure control and renin-angiotensin system blockade to reduce proteinuria","Confirm the amyloid type by mass spectrometry and exclude AL and AA amyloidosis before any misdirected treatment","General renoprotective measures and cardiovascular risk-factor modification","Monitoring of renal function, proteinuria and hepatic involvement over time","Renal replacement therapy by dialysis and consideration of kidney transplantation for end-stage disease","Nephrology and specialist amyloidosis multidisciplinary care","Family assessment given the strong ancestral and population predisposition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology, chapter on renal amyloidosis","Oxford Textbook of Medicine, chapter on amyloidosis","International Society of Amyloidosis nomenclature guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"leukoencephalopathy_brainstem_spinal_cord_lactate":{"name":"Leukoencephalopathy with brainstem and spinal cord involvement and lactate (LBSL, DARS2)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LBSL","DARS2-related leukoencephalopathy","mitochondrial aspartyl-tRNA synthetase leukoencephalopathy","leukoencephalopathy with brainstem and spinal cord involvement and elevated lactate"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive leukoencephalopathy caused by variants in DARS2, which encodes mitochondrial aspartyl-tRNA synthetase","The MRI pattern is highly specific: selective involvement of particular tracts rather than the whole white matter, including the cerebral white matter with sparing of the U-fibres, the pyramidal tracts and dorsal columns of the spinal cord, and defined brainstem tracts such as the medial lemniscus, superior and inferior cerebellar peduncles and the medullary pyramids","Proton MR spectroscopy typically shows raised lactate within the abnormal white matter, an important confirmatory clue","Onset ranges from infancy to adulthood; the common presentation is slowly progressive cerebellar ataxia with spasticity and dorsal-column sensory loss causing impaired proprioception and a positive Romberg sign","Deterioration or acute worsening can be provoked by minor head trauma, febrile illness or other stressors in some patients","Diagnosis rests on the combination of the selective tract pattern, elevated white-matter lactate on spectroscopy and biallelic DARS2 variants; a common intronic splice variant is frequently paired with a second allele","There is no specific treatment, so care is supportive and multidisciplinary, with physiotherapy, mobility aids and management of spasticity and sensory ataxia"],"pathophysiology":"LBSL is caused by biallelic variants in DARS2, which encodes the mitochondrial enzyme that charges transfer RNA with aspartate for mitochondrial protein synthesis. Reduced enzyme activity, often driven by a leaky intronic splice-site variant that lowers the amount of correctly spliced transcript, impairs translation of mitochondrially encoded respiratory-chain subunits in neural tissue. The resulting energy deficit and mitochondrial dysfunction selectively affect long myelinated tracts with high metabolic demand, producing the distinctive pattern of tract-specific white-matter signal change and the elevated lactate seen on spectroscopy.","additionalDifferentials":["Other mitochondrial leukoencephalopathies and MELAS-spectrum disorders","Multiple sclerosis and other inflammatory demyelination","Subacute combined degeneration from vitamin B12 deficiency (dorsal column involvement)","Hereditary spastic paraplegia with white matter change","Leukoencephalopathy with brainstem involvement from other tRNA synthetase defects","Adult-onset vanishing white matter disease and other genetic leukoencephalopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain and spinal cord MRI to demonstrate the selective tract involvement and cord signal change","Proton MR spectroscopy to detect elevated lactate within affected white matter","DARS2 sequencing, including analysis for the common intronic splice variant and a second pathogenic allele","Nerve conduction and somatosensory evoked potentials to characterise dorsal-column and peripheral involvement","Blood and CSF lactate as supportive metabolic markers","Leukodystrophy or mitochondrial gene panel if targeted DARS2 testing is inconclusive"],"redFlags":["Acute neurological deterioration following minor head trauma or febrile illness","Rapidly progressive weakness or bulbar dysfunction requiring reassessment","Severe sensory ataxia with recurrent falls and injury","Cognitive decline or seizures suggesting more extensive disease"],"prognosis":"The course of LBSL is variable but usually slowly progressive, with gradually worsening ataxia, spasticity and dorsal-column dysfunction; some patients remain mildly affected for many years, while early-onset cases can be more severe. Episodic decline after stressors and cumulative disability influence outcome, and life expectancy is often relatively preserved in milder phenotypes.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis by not appreciating the selective tract pattern and treating it as non-specific white matter change","Failing to perform MR spectroscopy and thereby overlooking the elevated white-matter lactate","Mislabelling the presentation as multiple sclerosis and exposing the patient to unnecessary immunotherapy","Not testing for the common intronic DARS2 variant, which can be missed by some assays","Overlooking the risk of stress- or trauma-related deterioration when counselling patients"],"management":["Supportive management as no disease-modifying therapy exists, with physiotherapy and mobility aids for progressive cerebellar ataxia and spasticity","Antispasticity treatment such as baclofen for spasticity","Balance training and falls-prevention measures for dorsal-column proprioceptive loss and a positive Romberg sign","Avoidance and prompt treatment of stressors such as head trauma and febrile illness that can provoke acute deterioration","Occupational therapy and speech and swallowing assessment as disability progresses","Neurology, clinical genetics and rehabilitation multidisciplinary care","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive DARS2 disorder, including the common intronic splice variant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"leukoencephalopathy_calcifications_cysts":{"name":"Leukoencephalopathy with calcifications and cysts (Labrune, SNORD118)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LCC","Labrune syndrome","SNORD118-related leukoencephalopathy","leukoencephalopathy with brain calcifications and cysts"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive cerebral microangiopathy caused by variants in SNORD118, which encodes a small nucleolar RNA","The defining imaging triad is leukoencephalopathy, intracranial calcifications and parenchymal cysts, and this combination is the key to pattern recognition","Computed tomography is particularly useful for demonstrating the calcification, while MRI shows the white matter change and the cysts, which may enlarge over time and exert mass effect","Presentation varies from infancy to adulthood and includes seizures, focal neurological deficits, movement disorders, cognitive decline and features of raised intracranial pressure from expanding cysts","Unlike Coats plus (a CTC1-related disorder), Labrune syndrome is confined to the nervous system without retinal, gastrointestinal, bone marrow or systemic manifestations","Diagnosis combines the characteristic imaging triad with SNORD118 sequencing, remembering that variants affect a non-coding RNA gene and may be missed by analyses focused on protein-coding regions","Management is largely neurosurgical and supportive, with consideration of surgical drainage or resection of symptomatic enlarging cysts and control of seizures and raised pressure"],"pathophysiology":"Labrune syndrome is caused by biallelic variants in SNORD118, a gene encoding the box C/D small nucleolar RNA U8, which is involved in the processing and maturation of ribosomal RNA. Disturbed ribosomal RNA biogenesis is thought to render cerebral small blood vessels vulnerable, producing a progressive microangiopathy with vascular wall thickening, calcification and breakdown of the blood-brain barrier. The resulting chronic tissue injury generates the leukoencephalopathy, dystrophic calcification and progressively enlarging fluid-filled cysts that together define the disorder, while the disease remains restricted to the central nervous system.","additionalDifferentials":["Coats plus syndrome and other CTC1-related telomere disorders","Congenital infections causing calcification (the TORCH group, including cytomegalovirus and toxoplasmosis)","Aicardi-Goutieres syndrome and other interferonopathies with calcification","Primary familial brain calcification (SLC20A2 and related genes)","Cerebral neoplasm or abscess when a solitary enlarging cyst dominates","Other genetic small-vessel diseases with white matter change and calcification"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Computed tomography of the brain to define the distribution and extent of calcification","MRI to characterise leukoencephalopathy, cysts and any mass effect, with follow-up to monitor cyst growth","SNORD118 sequencing, ensuring the non-coding gene is specifically analysed","Congenital infection screen to exclude TORCH causes of calcification","Interferon signature or related testing if an interferonopathy is being considered","Neurosurgical assessment when cysts are symptomatic or enlarging"],"redFlags":["Signs of raised intracranial pressure from an enlarging cyst requiring urgent neurosurgical review","New focal deficit or rapid neurological deterioration","Status epilepticus or poorly controlled seizures","Progressive cognitive decline with loss of independence"],"prognosis":"The course of Labrune syndrome is variable and often slowly progressive, punctuated by episodes related to cyst enlargement or seizures; outcome depends on lesion location, the rate of cyst growth and the success of neurosurgical intervention. Some patients stabilise for long periods, whereas others accumulate neurological disability over time.","pitfalls":["Attributing the calcification to a congenital infection and not pursuing the genetic diagnosis","Confusing the disorder with Coats plus by failing to look for the systemic features that distinguish CTC1 disease","Missing SNORD118 variants because sequencing was targeted only at protein-coding genes","Underestimating the tendency of cysts to enlarge and cause delayed mass effect","Overlooking treatable contributors such as seizures while focusing on the imaging findings"],"management":["Neurosurgical intervention for symptomatic or enlarging cysts by fenestration, drainage or shunting to relieve mass effect","Antiseizure medication for seizures","Serial CT and MRI surveillance to monitor cyst growth, calcification and white-matter change","Management of raised intracranial pressure from expanding cysts","Symptomatic treatment of movement disorders and neurorehabilitation support for cognitive and focal deficits","Neurology, neurosurgery and clinical genetics multidisciplinary care","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive SNORD118 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"leukostasis":{"name":"leukostasis","system":"Haematological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["symptomatic hyperleucocytosis","leucostasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"British Committee for Standards in Haematology acute myeloid leukaemia guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A medical emergency of symptomatic hyperleucocytosis, most often in acute myeloid leukaemia (especially monocytic and myelomonocytic subtypes) with very high blast counts, or in the blast phase of chronic myeloid leukaemia.","Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia causes leukostasis less readily at any given count because lymphoblasts are smaller and less adhesive than myeloblasts.","The dominant clinical picture is pulmonary (dyspnoea, hypoxia, diffuse infiltrates) and neurological (confusion, headache, visual change, stupor), reflecting microcirculatory obstruction in lung and brain.","Early mortality is high, so urgent cytoreduction with hydroxycarbamide and/or prompt induction chemotherapy, together with leucapheresis in selected unstable patients, should not be delayed.","Tumour lysis syndrome and disseminated intravascular coagulation frequently coexist and must be anticipated and managed concurrently.","Red-cell transfusion should be avoided or minimised until the white-cell count is controlled, because it raises whole-blood viscosity and can precipitate clinical deterioration.","Platelet transfusion, in contrast, does not appreciably raise viscosity and may be given to reduce the risk of intracranial haemorrhage.","Beware spurious laboratory results from metabolically active leucocytes: pseudohyperkalaemia, artefactually low arterial oxygen ('leucocyte larceny') and spurious hypoglycaemia."],"pathophysiology":"A greatly elevated count of large, poorly deformable and adhesive myeloid blasts increases blood viscosity and plugs the microcirculation. Blasts compete with tissues for oxygen, adhere to and damage the endothelium, and release cytokines that promote further leucocyte-endothelial interaction and local hypoxia. The lungs and central nervous system are most vulnerable, producing hypoxaemic respiratory failure and neurological dysfunction; endothelial injury, coagulation activation and concurrent tumour lysis add the risks of haemorrhage, disseminated intravascular coagulation and metabolic derangement.","additionalDifferentials":["Pneumonia or neutropenic sepsis","Pulmonary embolism","Tumour lysis syndrome","Intracranial haemorrhage or ischaemic stroke","Hyperviscosity syndrome from a paraprotein","Acute respiratory distress syndrome from other causes","Disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with white-cell differential and blast percentage","Blood film for blasts and features of leukaemia","Coagulation screen with fibrinogen and D-dimer for DIC","Urea, electrolytes, urate, phosphate and calcium for tumour lysis","Arterial blood gas (interpreted with awareness of leucocyte artefact)","Chest radiograph and CT head where neurological signs are present","Lactate dehydrogenase and rapid confirmatory bone marrow or immunophenotyping"],"redFlags":["Hypoxaemic respiratory failure","Altered mental status or focal neurological deficit","Intracranial haemorrhage","Priapism","Rapidly rising blast count with clinical deterioration"],"prognosis":"Leukostasis carries a high risk of early death from respiratory or cerebral complications and intracranial haemorrhage; prompt cytoreduction improves survival. Longer-term outcome is determined by the biology and treatment response of the underlying leukaemia.","pitfalls":["Transfusing red cells before cytoreduction and worsening leukostasis","Trusting an artefactually low arterial oxygen tension caused by leucocyte oxygen consumption","Delaying cytoreductive therapy while awaiting definitive diagnosis","Failing to anticipate and manage concurrent tumour lysis syndrome and DIC","Misinterpreting pseudohyperkalaemia and administering unnecessary or harmful treatment"],"management":["Urgent cytoreduction with hydroxycarbamide and prompt induction chemotherapy for the underlying leukaemia","Leucapheresis in selected unstable patients to rapidly lower the circulating blast burden","Anticipate and treat tumour lysis syndrome with vigorous hydration and rasburicase or allopurinol","Manage concurrent disseminated intravascular coagulation with blood-product support","Avoid or minimise red-cell transfusion until the white-cell count is controlled, as it raises whole-blood viscosity","Respiratory support with oxygen and ventilatory assistance, and close neurological monitoring for cerebral involvement","Emergency haematology referral and definitive treatment of the underlying acute myeloid or blast-phase chronic myeloid leukaemia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["British Committee for Standards in Haematology guideline on the management of acute myeloid leukaemia","Wintrobe's Clinical Hematology","Oxford Handbook of Clinical Haematology"],"crossLinks":[]},"levator_ani_syndrome":{"name":"Levator ani syndrome","system":"Colorectal / Pelvic floor","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["levator syndrome","puborectalis syndrome","chronic proctalgia","levator ani spasm"],"chapter":null,"source":"ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A functional anorectal pain disorder marked by a chronic or recurrent dull ache or pressure high in the rectum, often worse with sitting","Episodes typically last twenty minutes or longer, distinguishing it from the brief attacks of proctalgia fugax","Digital rectal examination classically reproduces the pain on posterior traction of the puborectalis, which is often tender and tight","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, so structural, inflammatory, and neoplastic causes of anorectal pain must first be ruled out","Associated with pelvic floor tension, psychological stress, and dyssynergic defecation","Management is conservative and directed at the pelvic floor: reassurance, warm baths, pelvic floor physiotherapy and biofeedback, digital massage, and muscle-relaxation or neuromodulation strategies","Overlaps with other functional pelvic floor and defecatory disorders"],"pathophysiology":"Levator ani syndrome is thought to arise from chronic spasm and tension of the pelvic floor muscles, particularly the puborectalis and levator ani, producing a persistent aching or pressure sensation high in the rectum. There is no structural lesion; central and peripheral pain sensitisation, pelvic floor dyssynergia, and psychological stress are all implicated. It sits within the spectrum of functional anorectal pain, sharing mechanisms with proctalgia fugax and dyssynergic defecation.","additionalDifferentials":["Proctalgia fugax","Coccygodynia","Chronic anal fissure","Anorectal abscess or fistula","Pudendal neuralgia","Endometriosis or other gynaecological pelvic pain","Presacral or pelvic tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Digital rectal examination assessing puborectalis tenderness and pain on traction","Proctoscopy or sigmoidoscopy to exclude mucosal disease","Anorectal manometry and balloon expulsion to assess for dyssynergia","Defecography where obstructed defecation is suspected","Pelvic MRI or cross-sectional imaging to exclude structural or neoplastic causes","Colonoscopy where alarm features or age warrant exclusion of malignancy"],"redFlags":["Rectal bleeding, weight loss, or a palpable mass suggesting malignancy","Nocturnal or progressive pain unresponsive to conservative measures","Neurological deficit suggesting a sacral or cauda equina lesion","New anorectal pain in an older patient without a prior functional history"],"prognosis":"A benign but often chronic and relapsing condition; many patients improve with pelvic floor physiotherapy, biofeedback, and reassurance, though symptoms can persist or recur.","pitfalls":["Labelling anorectal pain as functional without excluding structural or neoplastic disease","Overlooking coexisting dyssynergic defecation amenable to biofeedback","Repeated ineffective surgery for presumed fissure or haemorrhoids","Underestimating the contribution of psychological stress and pelvic floor tension"],"management":["Reassurance and explanation of the benign functional nature of the disorder","Pelvic-floor physiotherapy and biofeedback to retrain and relax the puborectalis","Digital transrectal massage of the levator ani and warm sitz baths for symptomatic relief","Electrogalvanic stimulation and neuromodulation strategies for refractory pain","Address dyssynergic defecation and treat coexisting constipation","Diagnosis of exclusion: rule out structural, inflammatory and neoplastic anorectal disease first","Psychological support for associated stress and chronic pain"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","Rome IV Functional Gastrointestinal Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"levator_syndrome":{"name":"Levator syndrome","system":"Gastrointestinal / Anorectal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["levator ani syndrome","puborectalis syndrome","chronic proctalgia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rome Foundation criteria for functional anorectal disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Levator syndrome is a functional anorectal pain disorder characterised by chronic or recurrent dull, aching, or pressure-like pain high in the rectum, often described as sitting on a ball","It falls within the Rome classification of functional anorectal pain, distinguished from proctalgia fugax (brief fleeting episodes) by its longer duration, typically lasting twenty minutes or more","The defining physical sign is tenderness on posterior traction of the puborectalis during digital rectal examination, often more pronounced on the left","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring the elimination of structural and inflammatory causes of anorectal pain such as abscess, fissure, thrombosis, malignancy, and coccygeal pathology","Pain is frequently worse with prolonged sitting and may be relieved by walking or lying down, and can be associated with pelvic-floor dysfunction and dyssynergic defecation","The Rome framework separates highly likely levator ani syndrome (with puborectalis tenderness) from unspecified functional anorectal pain (without it)","Management centres on reassurance, pelvic-floor physiotherapy and biofeedback, and adjuncts such as digital massage, warm baths, electrogalvanic stimulation, and muscle relaxation techniques","It commonly coexists with other functional gastrointestinal and pelvic-floor disorders and with psychological stressors, warranting a holistic approach"],"pathophysiology":"Levator syndrome is understood as a disorder of chronic spasm and tension in the pelvic-floor musculature, particularly the levator ani and puborectalis. Sustained or dysregulated contraction of these muscles, often in the setting of pelvic-floor dyssynergia, produces ischaemic, aching pain referred to the rectum and perineum. Central and peripheral sensitisation, abnormal pain processing, and the interplay of stress and anxiety contribute to symptom persistence, in keeping with a disorder of gut-brain and pelvic-floor interaction. There is no structural lesion; rather, heightened muscle tone, tenderness of the puborectalis, and impaired relaxation during defecation underlie the symptoms, which may be exacerbated by prolonged sitting and by dyssynergic straining.","additionalDifferentials":["Proctalgia fugax (brief episodic anorectal pain)","Coccygodynia or other musculoskeletal pelvic pain","Anal fissure, abscess, or thrombosed haemorrhoid","Pudendal neuralgia","Endometriosis or gynaecological pelvic pain","Rectal or presacral neoplasm","Chronic prostatitis or pelvic pain syndrome in men"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Digital rectal examination assessing puborectalis tenderness on posterior traction","Proctosigmoidoscopy to exclude mucosal or structural disease","Anorectal manometry and balloon expulsion to assess dyssynergia","Defecating proctography where obstructed defecation coexists","Pelvic MRI where structural, neurological, or gynaecological causes are suspected","Colonoscopy or cross-sectional imaging to exclude neoplasia in at-risk or alarm-symptom patients"],"redFlags":["Rectal bleeding, weight loss, or a palpable mass suggesting malignancy","Fever or localised swelling suggesting abscess","Progressive neurological symptoms or saddle anaesthesia","New severe pain of abrupt onset with structural findings","Pain unresponsive to treatment with evolving alarm features"],"prognosis":"Levator syndrome is benign but often chronic and relapsing; a substantial proportion of patients improve with biofeedback, physiotherapy, and reassurance, though symptoms can persist and fluctuate, and response is generally better when puborectalis tenderness is present and pelvic-floor dyssynergia is addressed.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing levator syndrome without adequately excluding structural or malignant causes","Overlooking coexisting dyssynergic defecation that is amenable to biofeedback","Attributing pain solely to haemorrhoids and performing unnecessary anal surgery","Neglecting the psychological and gut-brain dimension of the disorder","Repeated invasive investigations for a functional disorder once alarm features are excluded"],"management":["Reassurance and patient education about this functional anorectal pain disorder","Pelvic-floor physiotherapy and biofeedback targeting relaxation of the puborectalis","Digital massage of the levator muscles and warm baths for symptomatic relief","Electrogalvanic stimulation and neuromodulation for refractory symptoms","Muscle-relaxant strategies and treatment of coexisting dyssynergic defecation and constipation","Exclude structural and inflammatory anorectal causes, as it is a diagnosis of exclusion","Psychological support and stress management for the gut-brain component"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rome Foundation criteria for functional anorectal disorders","ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","Standard reference texts on functional gastrointestinal and pelvic-floor disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"leydig_cell_tumor":{"name":"Leydig cell tumour","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Leydig cell tumor","interstitial cell tumour","testicular Leydig cell tumour","hilus cell tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A sex cord-stromal tumour of the testicular interstitial (Leydig) cells, and rarely of the ovarian hilus cells, that is often hormonally active","In boys it typically secretes testosterone autonomously, producing gonadotropin-independent precocious pseudopuberty with virilisation and, characteristically, a unilateral testicular enlargement or nodule","Because the androgen production is independent of the pituitary, gonadotropins are suppressed and the GnRH stimulation test remains prepubertal, distinguishing it from central precocious puberty","Some tumours also aromatise androgens to oestrogens and can cause gynaecomastia","The classic clue in a boy is asymmetric testicular enlargement disproportionate to the degree of virilisation, prompting scrotal examination and ultrasound","It must be distinguished from Leydig cell hyperplasia (for example due to chronic hCG or LH-receptor stimulation, or the testicular adrenal rest tissue of poorly controlled congenital adrenal hyperplasia) and from familial testotoxicosis","Most childhood Leydig cell tumours are benign, and testis-sparing surgery is often possible, with orchidectomy reserved for larger or suspicious lesions"],"pathophysiology":"A Leydig cell tumour arises from the testosterone-producing interstitial cells of the testis (or the analogous hilus cells of the ovary) and commonly retains steroidogenic activity, secreting androgens autonomously and sometimes aromatising them to oestrogens. The resulting sex-steroid excess is independent of pituitary gonadotropin drive, so the axis is suppressed and the clinical picture is one of precocious pseudopuberty rather than true, centrally mediated puberty. In boys this manifests as virilisation with an enlarged or nodular testis, whereas oestrogen production can cause gynaecomastia. The principal differential considerations are conditions that expand or stimulate Leydig cells without a true neoplasm, such as hyperplasia from chronic gonadotropin or LH-receptor stimulation, testicular adrenal rest tumours in congenital adrenal hyperplasia, and the activating LH-receptor disorder of familial testotoxicosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial male-limited precocious puberty (testotoxicosis) from an activating LHCGR mutation","Leydig cell hyperplasia from chronic hCG or LH-receptor stimulation","Testicular adrenal rest tumour in congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Other sex cord-stromal or germ cell tumours of the testis","Central precocious puberty","Adrenal androgen-secreting tumour","hCG-secreting tumour causing peripheral precocity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum testosterone (and oestradiol where gynaecomastia is present) with basal LH and FSH (suppressed)","GnRH (LHRH) stimulation test demonstrating a prepubertal, suppressed response","Scrotal ultrasound to identify and characterise a testicular mass","Adrenal androgen profile including 17-hydroxyprogesterone to exclude congenital adrenal hyperplasia and rest tumours","Beta-hCG measurement to exclude an hCG-driven process","Histological examination of the resected lesion to confirm the diagnosis and assess for atypical features"],"redFlags":["A distinct testicular mass or asymmetric enlargement in a boy with signs of androgen excess","Histological features suggesting malignancy (more common in adults than children), such as large size, necrosis, or vascular invasion","Rapidly progressive virilisation with markedly elevated testosterone","Persistence or recurrence of hormonal signs after surgery, suggesting residual or recurrent tumour"],"prognosis":"The great majority of Leydig cell tumours in childhood are benign, and removal (often testis-sparing) reverses the hormonal effects with an excellent outcome; malignant behaviour is uncommon in children but requires more extensive surgery and surveillance when present.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the tumour for familial testotoxicosis or Leydig cell hyperplasia and thereby missing a resectable mass","Failing to examine and image the testes in a boy with gonadotropin-independent precocity","Overlooking testicular adrenal rest tissue of congenital adrenal hyperplasia, which mimics a tumour but responds to glucocorticoid control","Performing orchidectomy when testis-sparing surgery would suffice for a benign lesion","Not confirming gonadotropin independence with a stimulation test before attributing signs to a central cause"],"management":["Testis-sparing surgical excision (enucleation) as the definitive treatment, reversing the sex-steroid excess","Radical inguinal orchidectomy where organ preservation is not feasible or malignancy is suspected","Histological confirmation with assessment for atypical or malignant features","Distinguish preoperatively from Leydig cell hyperplasia, testicular adrenal rest tumours and familial testotoxicosis","Monitor resolution of virilisation or gynaecomastia and recovery of the suppressed gonadotropin axis","Assessment and management of accelerated bone age and predicted final height","Paediatric endocrinology and urology multidisciplinary care with postoperative surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"leydig_cell_tumor_testis":{"name":"Leydig cell tumour of the testis","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Leydig cell tumor","interstitial cell tumour of the testis","testicular Leydig cell tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Leydig cell tumour is the commonest sex cord-stromal tumour of the testis and most are benign, though a minority behave malignantly, particularly in adults","It can be hormonally active, secreting testosterone or oestrogen, so presentation varies by age","In prepubertal boys androgen excess may cause isosexual precocious puberty, while oestrogen excess can cause gynaecomastia","In adults it may present as a painless testicular mass, with gynaecomastia, reduced libido, or infertility from hormonal effects","Germ cell tumour markers (AFP, beta-hCG) are normal, and hormone profiling (testosterone, oestradiol, and relevant pituitary hormones) helps characterise a functioning tumour","Small, clearly benign-appearing lesions may be amenable to testis-sparing surgery, whereas radical inguinal orchidectomy is used when malignancy is suspected or organ preservation is not feasible","Features suggesting malignant potential include larger size, vascular invasion, necrosis, increased mitoses, and infiltrative margins, prompting staging and closer follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"Leydig cell tumours arise from the interstitial Leydig cells that normally produce testosterone under luteinising hormone stimulation. Neoplastic Leydig cells may retain steroidogenic activity and secrete androgens or, through peripheral or intratumoral aromatisation, oestrogens, producing the endocrine syndromes seen clinically. Excess oestrogen can suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis and impair spermatogenesis, contributing to gynaecomastia and infertility. Most tumours are benign, but a subset shows histological features associated with metastatic behaviour, chiefly in adults.","additionalDifferentials":["Germ cell tumour of the testis","Sertoli cell tumour","Leydig cell hyperplasia (non-neoplastic)","Testicular adrenal rest tumour in congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Testicular lymphoma","Other sex cord-stromal tumours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound to localise and characterise the intratesticular lesion","Serum AFP and beta-hCG (normal) to exclude a germ cell tumour","Hormonal profile including testosterone, oestradiol, LH, and FSH","Assessment to exclude testicular adrenal rest tumour when congenital adrenal hyperplasia is possible","CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis when malignancy is suspected","Histological review of resection margins and malignancy-associated features"],"redFlags":["Large or rapidly growing tumour with features suggesting malignancy","Evidence of retroperitoneal or distant metastases on staging","Rapidly progressive precocious puberty in a boy","Marked hormonal disturbance with new gynaecomastia in an adult"],"prognosis":"The majority of Leydig cell tumours are benign and cured by surgical excision, with resolution or improvement of hormonal effects; malignant Leydig cell tumours, more frequent in adults, respond poorly to chemotherapy and radiotherapy and carry a guarded prognosis once metastatic.","pitfalls":["Assuming a hormonally active mass is benign without assessing malignancy-associated histological features","Overlooking bilateral testicular adrenal rest tumours in congenital adrenal hyperplasia and mistaking them for neoplasm","Proceeding to orchidectomy for a small benign lesion where testis-sparing surgery was feasible","Failing to investigate gynaecomastia or precocious puberty as a clue to a functioning tumour"],"management":["Testis-sparing surgery for small, benign-appearing lesions with intra-operative frozen-section assessment","Radical inguinal orchidectomy when malignancy is suspected or organ preservation is not feasible","Histological assessment of margins and malignancy-associated features to guide further treatment","Retroperitoneal lymph node dissection and staging when malignant behaviour is confirmed","Management of hormonal sequelae such as gynaecomastia and reduced libido, with fertility counselling and semen cryopreservation","Surveillance imaging of chest, abdomen and pelvis and clinical follow-up given metastatic potential in adults","Urology and oncology multidisciplinary care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","WHO Classification of Tumours: Urinary and Male Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"lgi1_encephalitis":{"name":"LGI1 limbic encephalitis","system":"Nervous system / Neuroimmunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anti-LGI1 encephalitis","leucine-rich glioma-inactivated 1 antibody encephalitis","VGKC-complex (LGI1) limbic encephalitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Faciobrachial dystonic seizures — brief, frequent unilateral contractions of the face and arm — are highly characteristic and often precede the cognitive syndrome","The limbic phase brings subacute short-term memory loss, confusion, temporal-lobe seizures, and disturbed sleep or behaviour","Hyponatraemia is common and helps raise suspicion of the diagnosis","Antibodies target LGI1, a secreted neuronal protein once grouped within the voltage-gated potassium channel complex","Most cases are non-paraneoplastic, but tumour screening remains appropriate as a minority harbour an underlying neoplasm","Recognising and treating faciobrachial dystonic seizures early with immunotherapy may prevent progression to fixed cognitive impairment","First-line immunotherapy is corticosteroids with intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange, escalating to rituximab in refractory disease"],"pathophysiology":"Antibodies are directed against leucine-rich glioma-inactivated 1 (LGI1), a protein secreted by neurons that bridges presynaptic ADAM23 and postsynaptic ADAM22 to regulate synaptic excitability and AMPA-receptor clustering. Antibody-mediated disruption of this signalling increases neuronal excitability within the temporal lobes and basal ganglia, producing seizures, and impairs limbic circuits underlying memory. Inflammation concentrates in the medial temporal structures, and associated hypothalamic involvement is thought to contribute to the frequent hyponatraemia. The response is usually not tumour-driven but is strongly linked to specific HLA types.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex and other infective limbic encephalitis","Paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis with intracellular antibodies","Other surface-antigen encephalitides (CASPR2, GABA-B, AMPA)","Temporal-lobe epilepsy of another cause","Rapidly progressive dementia including Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease","Wernicke encephalopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and cerebrospinal fluid testing for LGI1 antibodies, with serum often more sensitive for this antigen","MRI of the brain showing medial temporal and sometimes basal ganglia signal change","Electroencephalography to characterise temporal seizures and subclinical activity","Serum sodium measurement to detect hyponatraemia","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis for inflammation and to exclude infection","Targeted tumour screening given the small paraneoplastic association"],"redFlags":["Frequent faciobrachial dystonic seizures signalling active, treatable disease before cognitive decline","Rapidly progressive memory loss or confusion","Severe hyponatraemia risking seizures and reduced consciousness","Status epilepticus or evolving encephalopathy"],"prognosis":"Prompt immunotherapy often improves seizures and cognition, but delayed treatment is associated with persistent memory impairment and medial temporal atrophy. Relapses can occur, so ongoing monitoring is warranted.","pitfalls":["Mistaking faciobrachial dystonic seizures for tics, myoclonus, or psychogenic movements and delaying treatment","Relying on antiseizure drugs alone rather than starting immunotherapy","Overlooking hyponatraemia as a diagnostic clue","Assuming the disorder is always non-paraneoplastic and omitting tumour screening"],"management":["First-line immunotherapy with corticosteroids, intravenous immunoglobulin and/or plasma exchange","Second-line immunotherapy with rituximab or cyclophosphamide for refractory or relapsing disease","Early immunotherapy for faciobrachial dystonic seizures to prevent progression to fixed cognitive impairment, with antiseizure medication as adjunct","Correction and monitoring of hyponatraemia","Tumour screening despite the mostly non-paraneoplastic nature, treating any identified neoplasm","Cognitive rehabilitation and management of sleep and behavioural disturbance","Clinical and antibody follow-up to detect relapse, under neurology and neuroimmunology multidisciplinary care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Continuum: Autoimmune Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"li_fraumeni_syndrome":{"name":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome","system":"Oncology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TP53","LFS","sarcoma, breast, leukaemia and adrenal gland (SBLA) syndrome","germline TP53 mutation"],"chapter":null,"source":"DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer / NCCN genetic/familial guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by germline pathogenic variants in the TP53 tumour-suppressor gene, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with very high lifetime cancer risk","The core tumour spectrum comprises soft-tissue and osteosarcomas, premenopausal breast cancer, brain tumours, adrenocortical carcinoma and leukaemia, often with childhood or early-adult onset","Suspect the syndrome when there are multiple primary tumours in one individual, cancers at unusually young ages, or clustering of the characteristic tumours across generations","Paediatric adrenocortical carcinoma and choroid plexus carcinoma are near-pathognomonic and warrant TP53 testing regardless of family history","Intensive whole-body MRI surveillance (Toronto protocol) with breast MRI, brain imaging and biochemical monitoring is the cornerstone of early detection","Therapeutic radiotherapy is used cautiously because of the risk of radiation-induced second malignancies in TP53 carriers","Classic and Chompret criteria guide who should be offered germline testing, but many carriers fall outside strict clinical criteria"],"pathophysiology":"The syndrome results from a heterozygous germline loss-of-function variant in TP53, the gene encoding the p53 protein that acts as a master regulator of the cellular response to DNA damage. Normally p53 arrests the cell cycle to permit repair or triggers apoptosis when damage is irreparable, so its impairment allows cells carrying genomic damage to survive and proliferate. Loss or mutation of the remaining wild-type allele in somatic tissue removes this checkpoint entirely, producing genomic instability and driving the diverse, early-onset tumours characteristic of the condition.","additionalDifferentials":["Hereditary breast and ovarian cancer (BRCA1/BRCA2)","Constitutional mismatch-repair deficiency syndrome","Neurofibromatosis type 1 with malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumours","Sporadic early-onset breast cancer without an identifiable germline cause","Familial adenomatous polyposis presenting with childhood brain tumours","Beckwith-Wiedemann spectrum with embryonal tumours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Germline TP53 sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis, ideally within a multigene cancer panel","Whole-body MRI as part of a structured surveillance protocol","Annual breast MRI in adult female carriers","Dedicated brain MRI and clinical neurological review","Abdominal ultrasound and biochemical adrenal screening in children","Detailed multigenerational pedigree assessment against classic and Chompret criteria"],"redFlags":["Multiple primary malignancies in a single individual, especially with early onset","Childhood adrenocortical carcinoma or choroid plexus tumour","A new mass or persistent symptom in a known carrier suggesting a further primary cancer","Radiation-field second malignancy after prior therapeutic irradiation"],"prognosis":"Carriers face a very high cumulative lifetime cancer risk, frequently developing more than one primary tumour, and childhood-onset disease is common. Structured surveillance improves early detection and survival, but the diversity and aggressiveness of tumours mean prognosis remains guarded and lifelong monitoring is required.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because family history is limited, since de novo variants and incomplete pedigrees are frequent","Using therapeutic radiotherapy without weighing the heightened risk of radiation-induced tumours","Overlooking a second primary by attributing new symptoms solely to a prior cancer","Failing to offer cascade testing and paediatric surveillance to at-risk relatives"],"management":["Intensive surveillance using the Toronto protocol: whole-body MRI, dedicated brain MRI, annual breast MRI in women, and biochemical and abdominal ultrasound screening in children","Discussion of risk-reducing bilateral mastectomy in female carriers, with prompt surgical treatment of detected tumours","Minimise diagnostic and therapeutic ionising radiation because of radiation-induced second malignancy risk, favouring surgery and non-radiation modalities","Prompt evaluation of new symptoms given the diverse, early-onset tumour spectrum","Germline TP53 testing, genetic counselling and cascade testing of at-risk relatives","Reproductive counselling including prenatal and preimplantation genetic testing options","Lifestyle risk reduction with smoking avoidance and sun protection, plus psychological support, through a specialist cancer genetics service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer: Principles and Practice of Oncology","NCCN Guidelines: Genetic/Familial High-Risk Assessment"],"crossLinks":[]},"lichen_amyloidosis":{"name":"Lichen amyloidosis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lichenoid amyloidosis","primary cutaneous lichen amyloidosis","lichen amyloidosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of primary localised cutaneous amyloidosis presenting as intensely itchy, discrete, firm, hyperkeratotic and often hyperpigmented papules","Characteristically involves the shins (pretibial) bilaterally and may also affect the extensor forearms, thighs, and upper back","Papules frequently coalesce into rippled or rough plaques and commonly show the Koebner phenomenon from chronic scratching","The amyloid is keratin-derived, from degenerate keratinocyte (filamentous) material, and is not associated with systemic (AL) amyloidosis","Chronic rubbing and scratching are central, and the condition overlaps with, and may be a variant of, lichen simplex chronicus and prurigo","More common in South-East Asian, Middle-Eastern, and South American populations, with a familial subset associated with OSMR and IL31RA mutations","Diagnosis is confirmed histologically by amyloid in the papillary dermis that stains with Congo red and shows apple-green birefringence under polarised light","Treatment is difficult and centres on breaking the itch-scratch cycle with potent topical corticosteroids (often under occlusion), keratolytics, and phototherapy"],"pathophysiology":"In lichen amyloidosis the amyloid deposits are of keratinocyte origin. Chronic friction and scratching are thought to injure basal keratinocytes, which undergo apoptosis (filamentous degeneration) and shed cytokeratin that is then converted, with the help of dermal cells, into amyloid deposited in the papillary dermis. The keratin-derived nature of the amyloid explains why the disease remains confined to the skin without systemic organ involvement, and the persistence of the itch-scratch cycle continuously supplies degenerate keratin, perpetuating and enlarging the deposits.","additionalDifferentials":["Lichen simplex chronicus","Hypertrophic lichen planus","Prurigo nodularis","Macular or nodular cutaneous amyloidosis","Pretibial myxoedema","Colloid milium","Papular mucinosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with Congo red staining showing apple-green birefringence and confirming papillary dermal amyloid","Immunohistochemistry for cytokeratin to demonstrate the keratinous origin of the amyloid","Assessment for a family history of primary cutaneous amyloidosis in familial forms","Evaluation to exclude systemic amyloidosis where the presentation is atypical, although this form is not systemic","Documentation of the itch-scratch pattern and Koebnerisation"],"redFlags":["Atypical or widespread deposition prompting exclusion of systemic amyloidosis","Severe intractable pruritus with marked functional or sleep disturbance","Rapidly changing or ulcerating lesions requiring re-biopsy"],"prognosis":"Benign but chronic and often refractory; it poses no systemic threat, but the itch and cosmetic appearance persist and lesions recur while scratching continues.","pitfalls":["Assuming an association with systemic amyloidosis, which does not apply to this keratin-derived form","Failing to address the itch-scratch cycle that perpetuates the deposits","Confusing it with lichen simplex chronicus or hypertrophic lichen planus without biopsy","Overlooking familial cases and their inheritance"],"management":["Break the itch-scratch cycle as the mainstay, using potent topical corticosteroids, often under occlusion, together with antipruritic measures","Control pruritus with emollients, menthol preparations, oral antihistamines and, in resistant cases, gabapentinoids","Reduce hyperkeratotic papules and plaques with topical keratolytics, dimethyl sulfoxide or intralesional corticosteroids","Phototherapy with narrowband ultraviolet B or systemic retinoids such as acitretin for extensive or refractory disease","Physical measures and behavioural advice to prevent scratching and Koebnerisation","Reassurance that the condition is skin-limited and not associated with systemic amyloidosis","Consider familial evaluation for OSMR and IL31RA variants where there is a family history"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":["macular_amyloidosis"]},"lichen_nitidus":{"name":"Lichen nitidus","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lichen nitidus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by numerous tiny, pinpoint (around 1 mm), flat-topped, shiny papules that are skin-coloured or hypopigmented","Papules are typically grouped in clusters on the flexor forearms, genitalia (often the penile shaft), abdomen, and chest","The Koebner phenomenon is common, with lesions appearing in a line along sites of scratching or trauma","Predominantly affects children and young adults and is usually asymptomatic","Considered by some to be a variant or close relative of lichen planus, though most regard it as a distinct entity","Nail changes (ridging, pitting) and, uncommonly, palmoplantar or generalised forms may occur","Histology is characteristic, showing a well-circumscribed lymphohistiocytic infiltrate embraced by elongated rete ridges in a ball-and-claw configuration","Usually self-limiting; treatment (topical corticosteroids, occasionally phototherapy) is reserved for symptomatic or cosmetically troublesome disease"],"pathophysiology":"Lichen nitidus is an inflammatory dermatosis in which a focal, cell-mediated response involving T-lymphocytes and histiocytes targets the epidermis, producing the characteristic sharply circumscribed papular infiltrate. The precise trigger is unknown, and although it shares a lichenoid tissue reaction with lichen planus, the two are usually considered separate conditions. The dense but tiny granulomatous focus, held between elongated rete ridges, corresponds to the minute shiny papule seen clinically, and the tendency to Koebnerise reflects an antigen-independent response to local trauma.","additionalDifferentials":["Lichen planus","Keratosis pilaris","Lichen scrofulosorum","Molluscum contagiosum","Plane warts (verruca plana)","Frictional lichenoid dermatitis","Papular eczema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy demonstrating the characteristic ball-and-claw granulomatous focus in the papillary dermis","Dermoscopy to appreciate the uniform, shiny, discrete papules","Assessment for the Koebner phenomenon along scratch lines as a diagnostic clue","Nail examination where nail dystrophy is suspected","Review for features of lichen planus overlap (oral, nail, and scalp involvement)","Evaluation for tuberculosis where lichen scrofulosorum is a serious consideration"],"redFlags":["Widespread or generalised eruption that fails to remit and warrants reassessment","Features suggesting lichen scrofulosorum, prompting evaluation for underlying tuberculosis","Nail dystrophy or mucosal change suggesting overlap with lichen planus"],"prognosis":"Benign, typically resolving spontaneously over months to a few years without scarring, although the course can occasionally be more protracted.","pitfalls":["Confusing the pinpoint papules with keratosis pilaris or plane warts","Overlooking the Koebner phenomenon as a helpful diagnostic feature","Failing to distinguish it from lichen scrofulosorum, which mandates a tuberculosis workup"],"management":["Reassure that the condition is benign and usually self-resolving, so active treatment is often unnecessary","Prescribe topical corticosteroids for pruritic or cosmetically troublesome papules","Use topical calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus or pimecrolimus) as a steroid-sparing option on the face and genitalia","Offer oral antihistamines to relieve itch","Consider narrowband UVB phototherapy for widespread, generalised or persistent disease","Advise minimising scratching and local trauma to reduce Koebnerisation","Refer to dermatology for biopsy in atypical cases and evaluate for tuberculosis where lichen scrofulosorum is a serious consideration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"lichen_planopilaris":{"name":"Lichen planopilaris","system":"Dermatology / Trichology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LPP","follicular lichen planus","lichen planus follicularis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A primary lymphocytic cicatricial alopecia representing follicular involvement by lichen planus, typically affecting the vertex and crown with patchy scarring","Perifollicular erythema and follicular hyperkeratosis, with tubular scale hugging the emerging hair, are characteristic at the margins of expanding bald patches","Patients may report itching, burning, tenderness or trichodynia over the affected scalp","Loss of follicular ostia within the patches confirms scarring, and several small patches may coalesce into larger areas","The Graham-Little-Piccardi-Lassueur syndrome combines scalp scarring alopecia, non-scarring loss of axillary and pubic hair, and follicular keratotic papules on the body","It may be associated with lichen planus of the skin, nails or mucosae, and frontal fibrosing alopecia is regarded as a clinical variant","Treatment targets the inflammation with potent topical, intralesional or systemic anti-inflammatory therapy to arrest progression, since scarred areas will not regrow"],"pathophysiology":"Lichen planopilaris is a T-cell-mediated inflammatory disorder in which a lichenoid lymphocytic infiltrate targets the follicular epithelium, particularly the bulge region housing the follicular stem cells. Destruction of these stem cells and of the sebaceous glands leads to permanent follicular loss with perifollicular fibrosis. It is considered a follicular expression of lichen planus, and loss of follicular immune privilege as well as altered peroxisome-proliferator-activated receptor signalling have been implicated, but the end result is irreversible cicatricial alopecia.","additionalDifferentials":["Discoid lupus erythematosus, with scaling, dyspigmentation and follicular plugging","Frontal fibrosing alopecia, a clinical variant affecting the hairline and eyebrows","Central centrifugal cicatricial alopecia, centred on the crown in afro-textured hair","Folliculitis decalvans, which is neutrophilic with pustules and tufting","Pseudopelade of Brocq or other end-stage scarring alopecia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Trichoscopy showing perifollicular erythema, tubular scaling and loss of ostia","Scalp biopsy from an active margin demonstrating lichenoid perifollicular inflammation and fibrosis","Direct immunofluorescence to help distinguish it from discoid lupus","Examination of skin, nails and mucosae for coexisting lichen planus","Disease-activity assessment to guide and monitor treatment intensity"],"redFlags":["Rapidly expanding or highly symptomatic patches indicating active disease and impending permanent loss","Features overlapping with discoid lupus that require different systemic management","Extensive multifocal scarring threatening widespread irreversible alopecia"],"prognosis":"Lichen planopilaris follows a chronic, unpredictable course; treatment can reduce symptoms and slow progression but cannot regrow hair in scarred areas, and disease may reactivate after periods of quiescence.","pitfalls":["Failing to biopsy an active margin and so missing a treatable inflammatory scarring alopecia","Confusing it with discoid lupus, which differs in management and immunofluorescence","Sampling a burnt-out scarred centre rather than the active edge on biopsy","Underestimating the symptom burden of itch and pain that should guide treatment intensity"],"management":["Apply potent or superpotent topical corticosteroids and give intralesional corticosteroid injections to active margins to suppress perifollicular inflammation","Use topical calcineurin inhibitors as a steroid-sparing anti-inflammatory adjunct","Start systemic hydroxychloroquine as first-line oral therapy for progressive disease","Escalate to oral retinoids, ciclosporin, methotrexate or mycophenolate mofetil, or a short course of systemic corticosteroids for rapidly advancing scarring, with pioglitazone (PPAR-gamma agonist) as a further option","Control symptoms of itch, burning and trichodynia","Monitor progression with serial trichoscopy and disease-activity scoring, recognising that hair cannot regrow in scarred areas","Offer camouflage, wigs and psychological support for the alopecia","Refer to dermatology or trichology and examine skin, nails and mucosae for associated lichen planus and frontal fibrosing alopecia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"lichen_planus":{"name":"Lichen planus","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LP","lichen planus (cutaneous)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Remember the classic six P's: pruritic, purple (violaceous), polygonal, planar (flat-topped) papules and plaques","Lesions favour the flexor wrists, forearms, and ankles, and fine white lacy lines on the surface (Wickham striae) are a helpful diagnostic clue","New lesions can appear along lines of trauma or scratching (Koebner phenomenon)","Oral lichen planus commonly presents as a white reticular lacy pattern on the buccal mucosa; erosive oral or genital disease is painful and warrants monitoring","Nail involvement can cause ridging and, if severe, scarring with pterygium formation, while scalp disease (lichen planopilaris) causes scarring alopecia","Consider a lichenoid drug eruption when a similar rash follows introduction of a new medication, and screen for hepatitis C where locally relevant","Treatment centres on topical corticosteroids for cutaneous and localised mucosal disease, with phototherapy or systemic immunomodulators for widespread or resistant cases","Long-standing erosive oral or genital lichen planus carries a small risk of malignant transformation and should be followed up"],"pathophysiology":"Lichen planus is a T-cell-mediated inflammatory disorder in which cytotoxic CD8 T lymphocytes target basal keratinocytes, apparently in response to an altered self-antigen. The resulting band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction produces the characteristic histological features of basal cell degeneration, a saw-tooth epidermal profile, hypergranulosis, and apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte bodies). Pigment released from damaged basal cells accounts for the post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation often seen, particularly in darker skin. Triggers can include certain drugs (lichenoid reactions) and, in some populations, hepatitis C infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Lichenoid drug eruption","Psoriasis","Discoid lupus erythematosus (for scalp and mucosal disease)","Secondary syphilis","Leukoplakia or candidiasis for oral white lesions","Chronic graft-versus-host disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing a lichenoid interface dermatitis in uncertain cases","Direct immunofluorescence to help distinguish from lupus or immunobullous disease when needed","Hepatitis C testing where epidemiologically appropriate","Review of medication history to identify a lichenoid drug trigger","Biopsy of persistent erosive or indurated oral or genital lesions to exclude dysplasia or malignancy"],"redFlags":["Erosive oral or genital disease with non-healing or indurated areas suggesting malignant change","Scarring alopecia or nail destruction indicating a need for prompt treatment to limit permanent damage","Rapidly extensive eruption suggesting a drug cause requiring medication review","Severe painful mucosal disease impairing eating or causing significant morbidity"],"prognosis":"Cutaneous lichen planus is often self-limiting, frequently resolving within one to two years but leaving post-inflammatory pigmentation; mucosal, nail, and scalp forms tend to be chronic, and erosive mucosal disease requires long-term surveillance for malignant transformation.","pitfalls":["Overlooking a lichenoid drug reaction and continuing the causative medication","Failing to examine the mouth, genitalia, nails, and scalp for additional sites of involvement","Not monitoring erosive oral or genital disease for malignant change","Attributing scarring alopecia to non-scarring causes and delaying treatment"],"management":["Prescribe potent topical corticosteroids as first-line treatment for cutaneous lesions, with antihistamines for pruritus","Use topical calcineurin inhibitors or corticosteroid gels and mouthwashes for oral and genital erosive disease","Offer phototherapy (narrowband UVB or PUVA) for widespread cutaneous involvement","Reserve systemic therapy — corticosteroids, acitretin, ciclosporin, methotrexate or mycophenolate mofetil — for severe or refractory disease","Review medications and withdraw a suspected lichenoid drug trigger, and screen for and treat hepatitis C where epidemiologically relevant","Maintain long-term surveillance of erosive oral and genital disease, with biopsy of persistent or indurated lesions to exclude malignant transformation","Refer nail and scalp (lichen planopilaris) involvement early to limit permanent scarring"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"lichen_planus_pemphigoides":{"name":"Lichen planus pemphigoides","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LPP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autoimmune overlap disorder combining features of lichen planus and bullous pemphigoid, in which tense subepidermal blisters arise both on lichenoid papules and on clinically normal-appearing skin.","Blistering on uninvolved skin is a key clue distinguishing it from bullous lichen planus, where blisters form only within pre-existing lichenoid lesions.","Typically the lichenoid eruption appears first and blistering follows, often favouring the limbs; mucosal and nail involvement can occur.","Direct immunofluorescence shows linear IgG and C3 along the basement membrane zone, as in bullous pemphigoid, distinct from the fibrinogen band and cytoid bodies of lichen planus alone.","Circulating antibodies target the BP180 (collagen XVII) antigen, characteristically the C-terminal portion of the NC16A domain, differing from classic bullous pemphigoid epitope patterns.","Can be triggered by drugs (including certain antihypertensives, antibiotics and biologic/checkpoint agents), phototherapy and, rarely, underlying malignancy.","Salt-split skin immunofluorescence localises immunoreactants to the epidermal (roof) side, supporting a BP180-directed process.","Tends to be more responsive to therapy and to affect younger patients than classic bullous pemphigoid; topical and systemic corticosteroids with steroid-sparing agents are mainstays."],"pathophysiology":"Lichen planus pemphigoides is understood as a two-step autoimmune phenomenon. A T-cell-mediated lichenoid interface reaction, as in lichen planus, damages basal keratinocytes and the dermo-epidermal junction; this inflammatory injury is thought to expose or release hemidesmosomal basement-membrane antigens that are normally sequestered, a form of epitope spreading. The immune system then generates autoantibodies against BP180 (type XVII collagen), particularly epitopes within and adjacent to the NC16A domain, producing a bullous-pemphigoid-like humoral response. Binding of these IgG autoantibodies, with complement activation and recruitment of eosinophils and neutrophils, disrupts the hemidesmosomal attachment and creates tense subepidermal blisters. Because the antibody response is directed at the basement membrane generally rather than only at inflamed skin, blisters can develop on normal-appearing skin as well as on lichenoid lesions, which is the defining clinical and immunopathological distinction from bullous lichen planus.","additionalDifferentials":["Bullous lichen planus (blisters confined to lichenoid lesions only)","Classic bullous pemphigoid","Lichenoid drug eruption with blistering","Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita","Erythema multiforme and other subepidermal blistering disorders","Paraneoplastic pemphigus","Bullous fixed drug eruption"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy of a lichenoid lesion and of a blister edge for routine histology","Direct immunofluorescence showing linear IgG and C3 at the basement membrane zone","Indirect immunofluorescence and salt-split skin to localise antibodies to the blister roof","Serum BP180 (NC16A) enzyme immunoassay for circulating autoantibodies","Detailed drug history including antihypertensives, antibiotics and checkpoint inhibitors","Age-appropriate malignancy screening where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Rapidly extending blistering with widespread skin denudation and risk of fluid loss or secondary infection","Mucosal involvement compromising oral intake or vision","New onset following a checkpoint inhibitor, suggesting an immune-related adverse event needing oncology input","Features suggesting underlying malignancy or paraneoplastic pemphigus"],"prognosis":"Lichen planus pemphigoides generally carries a better prognosis than classic bullous pemphigoid, often responding well to corticosteroids and steroid-sparing therapy with sustained remission. Drug-induced cases typically settle after the trigger is withdrawn.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling it as bullous lichen planus and missing the basement-membrane autoantibody process","Failing to biopsy normal-appearing perilesional skin for direct immunofluorescence","Overlooking a drug or checkpoint-inhibitor trigger","Assuming the same therapeutic intensity and prognosis as classic bullous pemphigoid","Not distinguishing the epidermal-side (roof) binding on salt-split skin from dermal-side patterns"],"management":["Use systemic corticosteroids as the mainstay to control both the lichenoid and the blistering components","Add steroid-sparing agents such as tetracycline antibiotics with nicotinamide, dapsone, methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate mofetil","Apply potent topical corticosteroids to localised lichenoid or bullous lesions","Withdraw any implicated trigger drug (antihypertensives, antibiotics or checkpoint inhibitors) and stop phototherapy","Provide blister and erosion wound care to prevent secondary infection","Reserve rituximab or omalizumab for refractory disease","Monitor clinical response and circulating BP180/NC16A autoantibody titres, and undertake age-appropriate malignancy screening where indicated","Refer to dermatology for diagnostic confirmation and immunosuppression planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard dermatology textbooks","Autoimmune bullous disease clinical guidelines","Peer-reviewed immunodermatology literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"lichen_sclerosus":{"name":"Lichen Sclerosus","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lichen sclerosus et atrophicus","LS","balanitis xerotica obliterans (penile form)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lichen sclerosus most often affects the anogenital skin, producing porcelain-white, atrophic, wrinkled or thickened plaques that are frequently intensely itchy or sore","It is commonest in postmenopausal women and prepubertal girls, but also occurs in men, where it can affect the glans and foreskin","In women the vulva may show loss of architecture with resorption of the labia minora, clitoral hood adhesions, and introital narrowing, often in a figure-of-eight distribution around the vulva and anus","In boys and men it can cause a tight, non-retractile foreskin (phimosis) and meatal narrowing","Extragenital lesions appear as white atrophic patches, classically on the trunk, and are usually asymptomatic","Long-term topical anti-inflammatory therapy and regular emollient use are the mainstays, aiming to control symptoms and reduce scarring and malignant risk","There is a recognised, though modest, increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma at affected genital sites, so persistent, thickened, ulcerated, or non-healing areas need biopsy","Arrange long-term follow-up to monitor for scarring, functional problems, and malignant change, and address sexual and urinary dysfunction sympathetically"],"pathophysiology":"Lichen sclerosus is a chronic inflammatory dermatosis thought to have an autoimmune basis, with associations to other autoimmune conditions and to specific autoantibodies in some patients. The characteristic histology shows epidermal atrophy, hyperkeratosis, a homogenised (hyalinised) upper dermis with loss of normal elastic fibres, and a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate beneath this zone. Genetic predisposition, hormonal influences, local trauma or the Koebner phenomenon, and possibly infective triggers have all been implicated. Chronic inflammation and scarring drive the architectural distortion, and sustained epithelial disturbance underlies the small risk of malignant transformation.","additionalDifferentials":["Vulval or penile lichen planus","Morphea (extragenital plaques can look similar)","Vitiligo or post-inflammatory hypopigmentation","Chronic eczema or lichen simplex chronicus of the genitals","Vulval or penile intraepithelial neoplasia and squamous cell carcinoma","Mucous membrane pemphigoid causing genital scarring"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy to confirm diagnosis in atypical cases and to exclude malignancy in suspicious areas","Screening or a low threshold for testing for associated autoimmune disease, such as thyroid dysfunction","Assessment of urinary and sexual function where scarring is significant","Photographic documentation to track architectural change over time","Referral for surgical assessment where phimosis or introital stenosis impairs function"],"redFlags":["Persistent thickened, warty, ulcerated, or non-healing areas suggesting squamous cell carcinoma and requiring biopsy","Progressive scarring causing urinary obstruction or inability to retract the foreskin","Significant introital stenosis interfering with micturition or intercourse","New fixed nodule or enlarging lesion at a chronically affected site","Symptoms unresponsive to adequate treatment, prompting reassessment of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Lichen sclerosus is typically a chronic relapsing condition; symptoms and inflammation can be well controlled with maintenance therapy, but architectural scarring is often permanent and lifelong surveillance is advised because of the small risk of genital squamous cell carcinoma.","pitfalls":["Dismissing genital symptoms as thrush or non-specific irritation and delaying diagnosis","Under-treating with only intermittent or weak topical therapy, allowing progressive scarring","Failing to biopsy persistent or changing lesions and missing early malignancy","Overlooking the condition in men and children, in whom presentation differs","Neglecting the psychological and sexual impact of chronic genital disease"],"management":["Use superpotent topical corticosteroids (such as clobetasol propionate) for induction and maintenance to control inflammation and reduce scarring","Apply regular emollients and soap substitutes and avoid irritants to protect the skin barrier","Consider topical calcineurin inhibitors as a second-line, steroid-sparing option","Maintain lifelong surveillance with biopsy of thickened, ulcerated or non-resolving areas given the small risk of genital squamous cell carcinoma","Refer for circumcision in corticosteroid-resistant phimosis and for surgical release of introital stenosis or adhesions where function is impaired","Screen for associated autoimmune disease such as thyroid dysfunction","Refer to dermatology, gynaecology or urology, with attention to sexual and urinary function"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"lichen_simplex_chronicus":{"name":"Lichen simplex chronicus (vulval)","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lichen simplex chronicus","vulval lichen simplex","neurodermatitis","squamous cell hyperplasia of the vulva"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology and vulval dermatology textbook guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Vulval lichen simplex chronicus is a reactive skin change produced by a persistent itch-scratch cycle rather than a primary disease in its own right","The dominant symptom is relentless itch, often worse at night and during stress, which drives rubbing and scratching that in turn thickens the skin and perpetuates the itch","Chronic scratching produces lichenification, seen as thickened, leathery skin with accentuated skin markings, often with excoriations and variable pigment change","It commonly arises on a background of an underlying trigger such as atopic or contact dermatitis, a pre-existing dermatosis, or environmental irritants, which should be sought and treated","Diagnosis is usually clinical, but biopsy is indicated when the appearance is atypical, unilateral, or fails to respond, to exclude a specific dermatosis or neoplasia","Management aims to break the itch-scratch cycle with generic measures: avoiding irritants, restoring the skin barrier with emollients, potent topical anti-inflammatory treatment for flares, and addressing nocturnal scratching","Identifying and treating any underlying dermatosis or allergen is essential to prevent relapse","Persistent, non-resolving, or ulcerated areas require re-biopsy because chronic vulval skin disease can rarely harbour intraepithelial neoplasia"],"pathophysiology":"Lichen simplex chronicus is the end result of repeated mechanical trauma to the skin from chronic rubbing and scratching. An initial pruritic stimulus, whether an underlying dermatosis, irritant contact, dryness, or psychological stress, provokes scratching that damages the epidermis and stimulates further itch through nerve and inflammatory mediators, establishing a self-sustaining itch-scratch cycle. Persistent trauma leads to epidermal thickening (acanthosis and hyperkeratosis) and dermal changes that clinically appear as lichenification. The condition is therefore secondary and reactive, and lasting improvement depends on interrupting the cycle and removing the provoking cause.","additionalDifferentials":["Lichen sclerosus","Lichen planus","Psoriasis of the vulva","Contact or irritant dermatitis","Candidal or dermatophyte infection","Vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia","Extramammary Paget disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination for lichenification, excoriation, and features of an underlying dermatosis","Vulval biopsy for atypical, unilateral, or treatment-resistant lesions","Skin swabs or scrapings to exclude candidal or dermatophyte infection","Patch testing where allergic contact dermatitis is suspected","Assessment for atopy and systemic causes of pruritus"],"redFlags":["A persistent unilateral plaque, ulcer, or nodule requiring biopsy to exclude neoplasia","Failure to respond to appropriate anti-inflammatory treatment","Progressive architectural change suggesting lichen sclerosus rather than simple lichenification","New pigmented or bleeding lesion"],"prognosis":"The condition usually improves well once the itch-scratch cycle is broken and any underlying trigger is treated, but it is prone to relapse if irritants recur or scratching resumes, so ongoing skin care is often needed.","pitfalls":["Treating the lichenification without identifying and correcting the underlying itch trigger, leading to relapse","Missing an underlying lichen sclerosus or intraepithelial neoplasia by not biopsying atypical or resistant areas","Under-treating with weak topical preparations so the itch-scratch cycle continues","Ignoring nocturnal scratching, which sustains the process","Overlooking contact allergens or irritants from hygiene products"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying trigger, such as atopic or contact dermatitis, another dermatosis, infection or an irritant","Restore the skin barrier with emollients and soap substitutes and eliminate identified irritants","Apply potent topical corticosteroids to settle flares of lichenification and inflammation","Break the itch-scratch cycle with sedating antihistamines and measures to reduce nocturnal scratching","Consider topical calcineurin inhibitors as a steroid-sparing maintenance option","Address psychological stress that aggravates scratching","Biopsy atypical, unilateral or treatment-resistant lesions to exclude a specific dermatosis or neoplasia, and patch test where contact allergy is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard vulval dermatology reference text","Gynaecology guidance on vulval skin disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"lichen_striatus":{"name":"Lichen striatus","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lichen striatus","linear lichenoid dermatosis","blaschkitis (related entity)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A self-limiting linear inflammatory dermatosis that follows the lines of Blaschko, typically as a continuous or interrupted band running down a limb","Most common in children and often appears abruptly over days to weeks","Comprises small pink, tan, or hypopigmented flat-topped papules that may become confluent and scaly","Usually unilateral and solitary, most often on a limb but occasionally on the trunk, neck, or face","Nail involvement can occur when the band crosses a digit, causing ridging, splitting, or onycholysis","Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation is common and may be the most conspicuous residual feature in darker skin, persisting after the papules settle","Thought to reflect a T-cell response against a clone of cutaneous cells carrying a post-zygotic (mosaic) mutation expressed along Blaschko's lines","Generally needs only reassurance and emollients, with topical corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors used for itch"],"pathophysiology":"Lichen striatus is believed to arise when an acquired trigger (possibly viral or environmental) provokes a T-lymphocyte-mediated reaction directed against a mosaic population of epidermal cells carrying a somatic mutation acquired during embryogenesis. Because these mutant clones are distributed along the developmental lines of Blaschko, the resulting lichenoid inflammation produces the characteristic linear band. The interface and sometimes spongiotic inflammation, often extending around adnexal structures deep in the dermis, damages the epidermis and disturbs pigmentation, accounting for the papules and the frequent residual hypopigmentation.","additionalDifferentials":["Linear lichen planus","Inflammatory linear verrucous epidermal naevus (ILVEN)","Linear psoriasis","Linear vitiligo (for the hypopigmented phase)","Linear porokeratosis","Blaschkitis (adult linear dermatosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical recognition of the Blaschko-linear distribution, which is usually sufficient for diagnosis","Skin biopsy in atypical cases showing lichenoid and sometimes spongiotic change with a deep peri-adnexal infiltrate","Wood's lamp examination to delineate the extent of post-inflammatory hypopigmentation","Nail examination and, if needed, nail-unit biopsy where there is dystrophy","Dermoscopy to support the diagnosis","Photographic follow-up to confirm spontaneous resolution"],"redFlags":["Persistent or spreading linear lesions that do not resolve, prompting reconsideration of ILVEN or linear lichen planus","Progressive nail destruction","Atypical widespread or bilateral distribution requiring biopsy"],"prognosis":"Excellent; most cases resolve spontaneously within months to about a year, although post-inflammatory hypopigmentation and nail changes may take longer to normalise.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for linear lichen planus or ILVEN, which run more chronic courses","Attributing the residual hypopigmentation to vitiligo","Over-treating what is a self-limiting condition"],"management":["Reassure that the condition is benign and self-resolving, so active treatment is often unnecessary","Use emollients for dryness and scaling","Apply topical corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors for itch or inflammation","Explain that post-inflammatory hypopigmentation fades slowly and needs no specific therapy","Monitor and manage nail dystrophy where the band crosses a digit","Arrange photographic follow-up to confirm spontaneous resolution and refer to dermatology for atypical or persistent cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"lichenoid_drug_eruption":{"name":"Lichenoid drug eruption","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["drug-induced lichen planus","lichenoid drug reaction","lichenoid drug eruption","drug-induced lichenoid dermatitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A drug-induced eruption that clinically and histologically mimics idiopathic lichen planus, with violaceous flat-topped papules and plaques","Compared with classic lichen planus it tends to be more widespread and symmetrical, may favour sun-exposed sites, and Wickham striae and mucosal involvement are often less prominent","There is characteristically a latent period of weeks to months between starting the drug and the onset of the eruption, which can hamper recognition","Frequently implicated drugs include certain antihypertensives, antimalarials, diuretics, gold, and increasingly immune checkpoint inhibitors and other biologic agents","Healing often leaves marked post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, particularly in more pigmented skin","Histology resembles lichen planus but may show more eosinophils, parakeratosis and a deeper, more perivascular infiltrate as clues to a drug cause","The eruption typically improves after the culprit drug is withdrawn, though resolution can be slow over weeks to months","Management is identification and withdrawal of the responsible drug together with topical or, if extensive, systemic corticosteroids and symptomatic measures"],"pathophysiology":"Lichenoid drug eruption is a T-cell-mediated interface reaction in which a drug or its metabolite triggers a cytotoxic lymphocytic attack on basal keratinocytes. The resulting band-like (lichenoid) infiltrate at the dermoepidermal junction, with basal cell degeneration and apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte bodies), reproduces the histological pattern of lichen planus. The drug is thought to act as a hapten or to alter self-antigens so that basal keratinocytes are recognised as foreign, and the relatively long latent period reflects the time required for sensitisation. Damage to basal cells releases melanin that is taken up by dermal macrophages, accounting for the prominent residual pigmentation.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic lichen planus","Lichenoid variant of graft-versus-host disease","Lichenoid keratosis and hypertrophic lichenoid dermatoses","Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus","Pityriasis rosea and other papulosquamous eruptions","Photodistributed drug eruption when photoexposed sites are involved"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed drug history noting agents started weeks to months before onset","Skin biopsy demonstrating a lichenoid interface dermatitis, with features suggesting a drug cause","Review of temporal relationship between drug introduction, withdrawal and improvement","Autoimmune serology to help exclude lupus in photodistributed cases","Assessment of mucosae and nails to compare with idiopathic lichen planus","Patch testing in selected cases to support identification of the culprit"],"redFlags":["Extensive or erythrodermic involvement requiring urgent assessment","Painful or erosive mucosal disease impairing function","Eruption associated with immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy needing oncology liaison","Failure to improve after drug withdrawal, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The eruption usually resolves after the responsible drug is stopped, although clearance may take several weeks to months and often leaves lasting hyperpigmentation; recurrence follows re-exposure to the culprit drug.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise the long latent period and therefore not linking the eruption to a drug","Mistaking it for idiopathic lichen planus and missing the treatable drug cause","Expecting rapid clearance and stopping investigation prematurely","Overlooking newer agents such as checkpoint inhibitors as triggers"],"management":["Identify and withdraw the culprit drug (antihypertensives, antimalarials, diuretics, gold, immune checkpoint inhibitors or other biologics) as the definitive step","Prescribe potent topical corticosteroids for localised disease, with a short course of systemic corticosteroids for widespread eruption","Offer oral antihistamines for pruritus","Consider phototherapy in selected persistent cases","Advise photoprotection and reassurance for post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, which fades slowly","Document the culprit agent to avoid re-exposure, which provokes recurrence, and liaise with the prescriber to find an alternative for the original indication"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","British Association of Dermatologists guidance on cutaneous drug reactions"],"crossLinks":[]},"liddle_syndrome":{"name":"Liddle syndrome","system":"Renal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pseudoaldosteronism","ENaC gain-of-function","SCNN1B","SCNN1G","epithelial sodium channel disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oxford Handbook of Nephrology and Hypertension / Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Liddle syndrome is an autosomal dominant cause of early-onset, low-renin hypertension caused by gain-of-function variants in the beta or gamma subunits of the epithelial sodium channel (SCNN1B, SCNN1G)","The variants disrupt channel removal from the collecting-duct membrane, so ENaC remains constitutively active regardless of aldosterone","The typical picture is hypertension with hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis, and characteristically both renin and aldosterone are suppressed","It behaves like a mineralocorticoid excess state but there is no excess hormone, hence the older term pseudoaldosteronism","Because the defect is at the sodium channel itself, an ENaC-blocking potassium-sparing diuretic (amiloride or triamterene) is effective, whereas an aldosterone-receptor antagonist is not","Dietary salt restriction complements pharmacological ENaC blockade","Consider it in young patients with resistant hypertension, hypokalaemia and a suppressed renin and aldosterone, especially with a supportive family history"],"pathophysiology":"Liddle syndrome is driven by activating variants in the SCNN1B or SCNN1G genes that encode the beta and gamma subunits of the epithelial sodium channel in the distal nephron. These variants typically delete or alter a cytoplasmic motif that is normally recognised by the ubiquitin ligase machinery, so the channel is not internalised and degraded and instead accumulates at the apical membrane. The result is excessive, aldosterone-independent sodium reabsorption with parallel renal loss of potassium and hydrogen ion, generating volume expansion, hypertension, hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis. The expanded volume and raised pressure suppress renin release and therefore aldosterone secretion, giving the paradoxical combination of a mineralocorticoid-excess phenotype with low aldosterone.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome)","Apparent mineralocorticoid excess (HSD11B2 deficiency)","Glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism (familial hyperaldosteronism type 1)","Cushing syndrome with mineralocorticoid effect","Renovascular or other secondary hypertension","Licorice ingestion or exogenous mineralocorticoid effect"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma renin activity and aldosterone (both suppressed)","Serum potassium and bicarbonate to document hypokalaemic alkalosis","Aldosterone-to-renin ratio to exclude primary hyperaldosteronism","Assessment of therapeutic response to ENaC blockade versus aldosterone antagonism","Screening for licorice and exogenous mineralocorticoids","Molecular genetic testing of SCNN1B and SCNN1G"],"redFlags":["Severe or resistant hypertension in a young person","Profound hypokalaemia with muscle weakness or arrhythmia","End-organ damage from long-standing untreated hypertension","Strong family history of early hypertension or premature stroke"],"prognosis":"With ENaC-blocking therapy and salt restriction, blood pressure and potassium usually normalise and long-term outlook is good; untreated disease carries the cardiovascular and renal risks of chronic hypertension.","pitfalls":["Treating with an aldosterone-receptor antagonist, which is ineffective because the defect is downstream of the receptor at the channel","Misdiagnosing it as primary hyperaldosteronism despite the suppressed aldosterone","Overlooking the diagnosis in young hypertensive patients without checking renin and aldosterone","Failing to screen and counsel first-degree relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"management":["Treat with ENaC-blocking potassium-sparing diuretics (amiloride or triamterene) as the specific and effective therapy","Combine dietary sodium restriction with pharmacological ENaC blockade","Avoid aldosterone-receptor antagonists, which are ineffective because aldosterone is already suppressed","Correct hypokalaemia and monitor blood pressure, serum potassium and acid-base status","Advise avoidance of liquorice and exogenous mineralocorticoids","Manage the cardiovascular and renal risks associated with chronic hypertension","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and screen first-degree relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (Feehally, Floege, Tonelli, Johnson)","Oxford Handbook of Nephrology and Hypertension"],"crossLinks":[]},"ligamentum_flavum_hypertrophy":{"name":"Ligamentum flavum hypertrophy","system":"Musculoskeletal system / Spine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ligamentum flavum thickening","hypertrophic ligamentum flavum","yellow ligament hypertrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"The Adult and Pediatric Spine (Rothman-Simeone) / Apley and Solomon's System of Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The ligamentum flavum connects adjacent laminae and is normally rich in elastin, giving it its characteristic recoil; hypertrophy reflects loss of elastic fibres with fibrosis and increased collagen rather than simple thickening","It is a major posterior contributor to degenerative spinal canal stenosis, combining with disc bulging and facet hypertrophy to produce the trefoil-shaped canal of lumbar stenosis","The thickened ligament buckles and infolds into the canal during extension and axial loading, producing a dynamic component to stenosis that worsens on standing and walking and eases on flexion","This dynamic behaviour underlies neurogenic claudication, with the shopping-trolley sign and relief on sitting or leaning forward","It should be distinguished from ossification of the ligamentum flavum, a distinct entity that predominantly affects the thoracic spine and can cause myelopathy, particularly in East Asian populations and in association with diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis","MRI shows the thickened low-signal ligament indenting the dorsolateral thecal sac, and the degree of thickening correlates with the severity of canal narrowing","Definitive treatment of symptomatic stenosis is surgical decompression to remove the hypertrophied ligament, with conservative measures reserved for milder disease"],"pathophysiology":"Ligamentum flavum hypertrophy is a degenerative process in which chronic mechanical stress and low-grade inflammation drive fibrosis of the normally elastic ligament. Repetitive loading, disc-height loss and segmental instability increase strain on the ligament and stimulate fibroblasts through transforming growth factor beta and other profibrotic mediators, leading to loss of elastic fibres, disorganised collagen deposition, scarring and sometimes calcification or ossification. Reduced disc height also allows the ligament to buckle and infold into the canal rather than stretch. The combined static bulk and dynamic buckling narrow the central canal and lateral recesses, compressing neural elements and, with axial loading and extension, producing intermittent ischaemia of the cauda equina or nerve roots that manifests as neurogenic claudication.","additionalDifferentials":["Ossification of the ligamentum flavum, particularly in the thoracic spine","Ossification of the posterior longitudinal ligament","Degenerative disc herniation or bulge causing anterior canal compromise","Facet joint hypertrophy or synovial cyst compressing the lateral recess","Epidural lipomatosis narrowing the canal","Spondylolisthesis with dynamic instability","Vascular claudication as a cause of exertional leg symptoms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the affected spinal region to measure ligamentum flavum thickness and grade canal and recess stenosis","CT to demonstrate any calcification or ossification and bony anatomy for surgical planning","Standing and flexion-extension radiographs to assess dynamic instability and spondylolisthesis","Upright or axially loaded MRI where dynamic stenosis is suspected but supine imaging is equivocal","Ankle-brachial pressure index or vascular studies to exclude peripheral arterial disease","Electrophysiological studies where a peripheral neuropathy or radiculopathy needs clarification"],"redFlags":["Progressive myelopathy with hyperreflexia and gait disturbance, especially with thoracic ossification","Cauda equina syndrome with saddle anaesthesia and bladder or bowel dysfunction","Rapidly progressive or profound lower-limb weakness","New or nocturnal back pain with systemic features suggesting infection or malignancy rather than degenerative stenosis"],"prognosis":"Symptomatic degenerative stenosis due to ligamentum flavum hypertrophy tends to progress slowly, and surgical decompression reliably relieves neurogenic claudication in appropriately selected patients, although established myelopathy from thoracic ossification recovers less predictably.","pitfalls":["Relying on supine MRI alone and underestimating dynamic stenosis that only manifests on standing or extension","Confusing neurogenic with vascular claudication and missing peripheral arterial disease","Overlooking thoracic ossification of the ligamentum flavum as a cause of myelopathy","Attributing all symptoms to ligamentum flavum thickening when a synovial cyst, disc herniation or instability is the dominant lesion"],"management":["Begin conservative care with flexion-biased physiotherapy, activity modification and postural advice for neurogenic claudication","Provide analgesia with NSAIDs and neuropathic agents such as gabapentinoids for symptom control","Offer epidural or transforaminal corticosteroid injections for persistent radicular or claudicant symptoms","Optimise weight and general conditioning","Refer for surgical decompression (laminectomy or laminotomy) in refractory or progressive neurogenic claudication, and for decompression of thoracic ligamentum flavum ossification causing myelopathy","Monitor for progressive neurological deficit and escalate urgently for cauda equina syndrome or evolving myelopathy","Involve spinal surgery or neurosurgery for appropriate operative selection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","Apley and Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Trauma"],"crossLinks":[]},"light_chain_deposition":{"name":"Light chain deposition disease","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LCDD","monoclonal immunoglobulin deposition disease","MIDD","Randall-type deposition disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disorder in which monoclonal light chains (most often kappa) are deposited in a granular, non-fibrillar, non-organised form throughout the kidney and other organs","It is part of the monoclonal immunoglobulin deposition disease spectrum and reflects an underlying plasma cell or B-cell clone, sometimes with overt multiple myeloma","Unlike amyloidosis, the deposits are Congo red negative and do not form fibrils on electron microscopy","Renal involvement typically causes proteinuria, often nephrotic range, with hypertension and progressive kidney impairment","The classic glomerular lesion is nodular glomerulosclerosis, which can resemble diabetic nodular disease, so light chain deposition should be considered in nodular patterns without diabetes","Immunofluorescence shows monotypic light chain staining along tubular and glomerular basement membranes, a key diagnostic feature","Evaluation requires serum and urine studies for a monoclonal protein, free light chain assay, and often bone marrow examination to characterise the clone","Treatment targets the underlying clone with clone-directed therapy, since reducing light chain production is central to preserving organ function"],"pathophysiology":"Light chain deposition disease occurs when a clonal population of plasma cells or B cells overproduces a monoclonal light chain that, because of its physicochemical properties, precipitates in a granular non-fibrillar form. These deposits accumulate along the glomerular and tubular basement membranes and within the mesangium, driving mesangial matrix expansion and nodular glomerulosclerosis as well as tubular injury. The deposited light chains are Congo red negative and lack the beta-pleated sheet organisation of amyloid. Continued deposition disrupts the filtration barrier and tubular structures, producing proteinuria and progressive kidney failure, and similar deposits can affect the heart, liver, and other organs.","additionalDifferentials":["AL amyloidosis (Congo red positive)","Diabetic nodular glomerulosclerosis","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Fibrillary and immunotactoid glomerulopathy","Light chain cast nephropathy (myeloma kidney)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation","Serum free light chain assay and ratio","Kidney biopsy with immunofluorescence for monotypic light chains and electron microscopy","Congo red staining to exclude amyloidosis","Bone marrow examination to identify and quantify the underlying clone"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining kidney function or nephrotic-range proteinuria","Features of multiple myeloma (hypercalcaemia, anaemia, bone lesions)","Cardiac or hepatic involvement from extrarenal deposition","New nodular glomerulosclerosis in a patient without diabetes"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend on the extent of organ deposition and the response of the underlying clone to therapy; effective suppression of light chain production can stabilise kidney function, whereas untreated disease progresses to kidney failure and carries risk from extrarenal deposits.","pitfalls":["Mistaking nodular glomerulosclerosis for diabetic nephropathy in a non-diabetic patient","Confusing it with amyloidosis without Congo red and ultrastructural confirmation","Failing to identify and treat the underlying plasma cell or B-cell clone","Overlooking extrarenal organ involvement"],"management":["Deliver clone-directed therapy to suppress the pathogenic light chain, with bortezomib-based (proteasome inhibitor) regimens plus corticosteroids and autologous stem-cell transplantation in eligible patients","Treat any underlying multiple myeloma per haematology protocols","Institute renoprotection with ACE inhibitors or ARBs and blood-pressure control, managing nephrotic-range proteinuria and oedema","Monitor serum free light chains and the ratio alongside renal function to gauge haematological and organ response","Undertake surveillance for extrarenal (cardiac and hepatic) light-chain deposition","Use renal replacement therapy for advanced kidney failure, considering kidney transplantation only after sustained clonal remission","Coordinate combined nephrology and haematology multidisciplinary management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"light_chain_deposition_disease":{"name":"Light chain deposition disease","system":"Renal / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LCDD","monoclonal light chain deposition disease","Randall-type monoclonal immunoglobulin deposition disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Brenner and Rector's The Kidney","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A monoclonal immunoglobulin deposition disease in which non-organised, non-amyloid monoclonal light chains (most often kappa) deposit along basement membranes","Presents commonly with proteinuria, nephrotic syndrome, hypertension and progressive renal impairment, frequently with nodular glomerulosclerosis on biopsy","Deposits are Congo-red negative and lack the fibrillar or microtubular organisation of amyloid and immunotactoid disease","Immunofluorescence shows monoclonal light-chain restriction along glomerular and tubular basement membranes; electron microscopy shows granular, punctate electron-dense deposits","Reflects an underlying plasma-cell or B-cell clone, so a monoclonal gammopathy workup is mandatory, and it may accompany myeloma","It falls within the spectrum of monoclonal gammopathy of renal significance when the clone is small but nephrotoxic","Treatment is clone-directed therapy for the paraprotein-related disease alongside renin-angiotensin blockade and supportive care"],"pathophysiology":"Light chain deposition disease arises from a clonal plasma-cell or B-cell population that secretes a monoclonal light chain with physicochemical properties favouring tissue deposition rather than fibril formation. The light chains deposit as granular, non-Congophilic material along the glomerular and tubular basement membranes and in the mesangium, where they stimulate mesangial matrix production and nodular expansion resembling diabetic glomerulosclerosis. Tubular basement membrane deposition contributes to tubulointerstitial injury. The result is progressive proteinuria, nodular glomerulosclerosis and declining renal function; because the causative clone may be small, the systemic tumour burden can be modest even when renal damage is substantial.","additionalDifferentials":["Diabetic nodular glomerulosclerosis","Renal amyloidosis (Congo-red positive)","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Myeloma cast nephropathy","Immunotactoid glomerulopathy","Fibrillary glomerulonephritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal biopsy with immunofluorescence showing monoclonal light-chain restriction and electron microscopy showing granular deposits","Congo-red staining to exclude amyloid","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation","Serum free light chains and the free light-chain ratio","Bone marrow examination for a plasma-cell clone and to assess for myeloma","Skeletal survey or cross-sectional imaging and calcium where myeloma is suspected"],"redFlags":["Features of overt multiple myeloma such as hypercalcaemia, anaemia or bone lesions","Rapidly progressive renal impairment","Coexisting cardiac or hepatic light-chain deposition","Nephrotic syndrome with thromboembolic risk"],"prognosis":"Renal and overall outcome depend on the underlying clone and its response to clone-directed therapy; suppression of the pathogenic light chain can stabilise or improve renal function, whereas advanced renal impairment at diagnosis and coexisting myeloma predict worse outcomes. Extra-renal deposition, particularly cardiac, further worsens prognosis, and the disease may recur after transplantation if the clone is not controlled.","pitfalls":["Mistaking nodular glomerulosclerosis for diabetic nephropathy without immunofluorescence","Confusing it with amyloidosis when Congo-red staining is not performed","Failing to identify and treat the underlying clone","Overlooking small but nephrotoxic clones within the monoclonal gammopathy of renal significance spectrum","Not screening for concurrent cast nephropathy and myeloma"],"management":["Give clone-directed treatment tailored to the underlying plasma-cell or B-cell clone, commonly bortezomib-based (proteasome inhibitor) regimens with corticosteroids and autologous stem-cell transplantation where appropriate, even when the clone is small in monoclonal gammopathy of renal significance","Treat coexisting multiple myeloma when present","Apply renoprotective measures with renin-angiotensin blockade and blood-pressure control, plus management of nephrotic syndrome","Monitor serum free light-chain levels and ratio as the key marker of treatment response, alongside renal function","Assess for extrarenal deposition and treat accordingly","Use renal replacement therapy for end-stage disease, deferring transplantation until the clone is in sustained remission","Manage jointly between nephrology and haematology given the disorder's monoclonal gammopathy of renal significance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Brenner and Rector's The Kidney"],"crossLinks":[]},"lightning_injury":{"name":"Lightning injury","system":"Environmental / Emergency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lightning strike injury","keraunopathy","lightning-related injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine / Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The chief cause of immediate death is cardiac arrest, as the massive brief current can depolarise the entire myocardium and produce asystole; a secondary respiratory arrest from medullary paralysis may outlast the cardiac standstill","Because primary arrest may be reversible, lightning casualties warrant a 'reverse triage' approach in which apparently lifeless victims are resuscitated first","Keraunoparalysis is a transient limb paralysis, often of the legs, with cold, mottled, pulseless extremities that usually resolves over hours","Lichtenberg figures are transient, fern-like or feathering skin markings that are effectively pathognomonic of a lightning strike and fade within a day or two","Most of the current flashes over the body surface rather than passing deeply, so extensive deep tissue destruction and severe myoglobinuria are less typical than in high-voltage electrical injury","Tympanic membrane rupture, cataracts, autonomic disturbance, and a range of neurological and neuropsychological sequelae are recognised complications","Emergency management prioritises resuscitation and cardiac monitoring, with assessment for associated blunt trauma from the blast or a fall and evaluation of the eyes and ears"],"pathophysiology":"Lightning delivers an extremely high-voltage current over a very short duration, and much of it passes over the body surface as a flashover rather than through deep tissues, which distinguishes its injury pattern from that of sustained high-voltage electrical contact. The instantaneous current can act as a massive countershock that depolarises the myocardium and causes asystole; cardiac automaticity may then resume while the respiratory centre remains paralysed, so ongoing ventilation can be life-saving. Neurological effects include transient autonomic and vascular dysregulation producing keraunoparalysis. The characteristic skin ferning (Lichtenberg figures) reflects a transient electrical effect on cutaneous vessels rather than a true burn. Blast effect and secondary trauma from being thrown add to the injury burden.","additionalDifferentials":["High-voltage electrical injury","Cardiac arrest from primary arrhythmia or ischaemia","Blunt polytrauma from a fall or blast","Seizure with postictal deficit","Acute stroke","Syncope or collapse of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous cardiac monitoring and electrocardiography to detect arrhythmia and conduction abnormality","Cardiac biomarkers where myocardial injury is suspected","Serum creatine kinase and renal function to assess for muscle injury, recognising it is usually less severe than in high-voltage injury","Trauma assessment with imaging directed at possible blunt injuries","Otoscopic examination for tympanic membrane rupture and audiological review","Ophthalmological assessment for cataract and other ocular injury, and neurological evaluation of persistent deficits"],"redFlags":["Cardiac arrest or significant arrhythmia requiring immediate resuscitation and monitoring","Respiratory arrest persisting after return of cardiac activity","Depressed consciousness or focal neurological deficit","Evidence of major associated blunt trauma from blast or fall"],"prognosis":"Prognosis hinges on whether cardiac and respiratory arrest are promptly and effectively treated; those who survive the initial event and receive early resuscitation can recover well, though a proportion are left with chronic neurological, cognitive, or autonomic sequelae. Keraunoparalysis and the cutaneous markings themselves are transient.","pitfalls":["Applying conventional triage and deprioritising an apparently dead lightning victim who could be resuscitated","Stopping ventilation once a pulse returns, when persistent respiratory arrest still requires support","Expecting the deep tissue destruction and severe rhabdomyolysis pattern of high-voltage injury and mismanaging fluids accordingly","Overlooking tympanic, ocular, or occult traumatic injuries while focused on the cardiac presentation"],"management":["Apply reverse triage, prioritising cardiopulmonary resuscitation of apparently lifeless casualties, with prolonged ventilatory support because respiratory arrest often outlasts the reversible cardiac arrest","Provide continuous cardiac monitoring and ECG with treatment of arrhythmias and conduction disturbance","Give assisted ventilation for medullary respiratory paralysis until spontaneous breathing returns","Perform a trauma survey with spinal precautions for blast and secondary blunt injuries","Offer supportive care for keraunoparalysis, which is transient, and wound care for the usually superficial cutaneous injury","Examine for tympanic membrane rupture and arrange ophthalmological review for cataract and other ocular injury","Provide neurological and neuropsychological rehabilitation for persistent cognitive, autonomic or motor sequelae"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine: A Comprehensive Study Guide","Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD","sarcoglycanopathy","calpainopathy","dysferlinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A genetically diverse group of muscular dystrophies unified by predominantly proximal weakness of the shoulder and pelvic girdles, with both autosomal dominant and autosomal recessive forms","Autosomal recessive subtypes are generally more common and include calpainopathy, dysferlinopathy, and the sarcoglycanopathies","Onset ranges from childhood to adulthood, typically with difficulty rising from a chair, climbing stairs, and lifting the arms","Serum creatine kinase is usually elevated, sometimes markedly, especially in the recessive subtypes","Some subtypes carry a significant risk of cardiomyopathy or respiratory muscle involvement, which guides surveillance","Dysferlinopathy characteristically presents with distal calf involvement and very high creatine kinase in young adults","Diagnosis increasingly relies on next-generation sequencing panels, complemented by muscle biopsy with protein immunostaining and imaging","Management is supportive and multidisciplinary, with physiotherapy, aids to mobility, and cardiac and respiratory monitoring targeted to the subtype"],"pathophysiology":"The limb-girdle muscular dystrophies result from variants in a wide range of genes encoding proteins essential to muscle-fibre structure and function, including components of the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex (such as the sarcoglycans), membrane-repair proteins (dysferlin), enzymes (calpain-3), and nuclear and glycosylation-related proteins. Loss of these proteins impairs sarcolemmal stability, membrane repair, or muscle-fibre maintenance, leading to repeated fibre injury, necrosis, and progressive replacement of muscle by fat and connective tissue. The predominant involvement of proximal girdle muscles produces the characteristic pattern of weakness, while the specific protein defect determines any associated cardiac or respiratory involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Becker and Duchenne muscular dystrophies","Inflammatory myopathies","Metabolic and mitochondrial myopathies","Spinal muscular atrophy","Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy","Congenital and myofibrillar myopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase measurement","Next-generation sequencing gene panel for limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","Muscle biopsy with immunohistochemistry for specific structural proteins","Muscle MRI to characterise the pattern of muscle involvement","Electrocardiography and echocardiography for cardiomyopathy","Pulmonary function testing for respiratory muscle weakness"],"redFlags":["Symptoms of cardiomyopathy or conduction disturbance in cardiac-associated subtypes","Declining respiratory function or nocturnal hypoventilation","Rapidly progressive weakness suggesting an inflammatory or alternative cause","Dysphagia or aspiration risk","Acute rhabdomyolysis with very high creatine kinase and dark urine"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies widely by subtype, from slowly progressive forms compatible with a near-normal lifespan to more severe childhood-onset disease with early loss of ambulation. Cardiac and respiratory involvement in certain subtypes are the principal determinants of survival, underscoring the value of precise genetic diagnosis.","pitfalls":["Grouping all cases together and missing subtype-specific cardiac or respiratory risks","Overlooking a treatable inflammatory myopathy that can mimic the phenotype","Failing to pursue genetic confirmation, which is essential for prognosis and counselling","Attributing a very high creatine kinase solely to exercise in dysferlinopathy","Neglecting cardiac surveillance in subtypes with known cardiomyopathy risk"],"management":["Recognise there is no disease-modifying drug and use physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain mobility, prevent contractures and provide orthoses and mobility aids","Establish precise genetic subtyping to direct organ surveillance and prognosis","Undertake cardiac surveillance with ECG and echocardiography, treating cardiomyopathy with ACE inhibitors and beta-blockers and device therapy in at-risk subtypes","Monitor respiratory function with pulmonary function testing and introduce non-invasive ventilation for respiratory-muscle weakness","Manage contractures and scoliosis with orthopaedic input","Deliver multidisciplinary neuromuscular care and rehabilitation","Provide genetic counselling and family screening, clarifying dominant or recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy overview","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_d1":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy D1 (DNAJB6-related)","system":"Neuromuscular / Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD1D","LGMD1E","DNAJB6-related LGMD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant; heterozygous DNAJB6 variants clustering in the glycine/phenylalanine-rich (G/F) domain act by a dominant-negative mechanism that impairs chaperone anti-aggregation function.","Presents as a myofibrillar myopathy with rimmed vacuoles and aggregates of myotilin, desmin, TDP-43 and DNAJB6 itself.","Usually adult-onset (childhood-onset in some), with proximal and sometimes distal limb weakness.","Cardiac involvement is characteristically mild or absent, unlike many other myofibrillar myopathies.","Creatine kinase ranges from normal to moderately elevated.","Muscle MRI often shows a selective pattern of involvement that aids diagnosis.","Formerly designated LGMD1D (and LGMD1E in older schemes).","Late respiratory muscle involvement is possible and warrants monitoring."],"pathophysiology":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy D1 is an autosomal dominant myofibrillar-type dystrophy caused by heterozygous variants in DNAJB6 on chromosome 7q36.3, which encodes a ubiquitously expressed HSP40/DnaJ co-chaperone that partners with HSP70 to prevent the aggregation of misfolded and aggregation-prone proteins. Disease-causing missense changes cluster in the glycine/phenylalanine-rich (G/F) domain and act by a dominant-negative mechanism that cripples the anti-aggregation activity of the chaperone complex, so poly-ubiquitinated and Z-disc proteins accumulate within the myofibre. The result is a myofibrillar myopathy with rimmed vacuoles and aggregates of myotilin, desmin, TDP-43 and DNAJB6 itself, typically presenting in adulthood with proximal and sometimes distal limb weakness. Unlike several other myofibrillar myopathies, cardiac involvement is characteristically absent or mild, although late respiratory muscle weakness can develop.","additionalDifferentials":["Other myofibrillar myopathies (myotilin, desmin, filamin C, BAG3, ZASP/LDB3)","LGMD D2 (TNPO3-related)","LGMD D3 (HNRNPDL-related)","LGMD D4 (autosomal dominant calpainopathy, CAPN3)","Sporadic inclusion body myositis (rimmed vacuoles)","Distal myopathies (when distal onset predominates)","Recessive calpainopathy (LGMD R1)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Next-generation sequencing gene panel or whole-exome sequencing including DNAJB6","Muscle biopsy (myofibrillar pathology, rimmed vacuoles and protein aggregates)","Muscle MRI to define the selective pattern of involvement","Electromyography","Baseline ECG and echocardiography given the differential diagnosis","Respiratory function testing during follow-up"],"redFlags":["Symptoms of nocturnal hypoventilation such as morning headache and unrefreshing sleep","New cardiac symptoms prompting reassessment of the diagnosis","Rapid functional decline","Dysphagia"],"prognosis":"Progression is usually slow over decades, and independent ambulation is often preserved into later life. Respiratory monitoring is advised because late respiratory involvement can occur.","pitfalls":["Rimmed vacuoles may be misread as sporadic inclusion body myositis, delaying genetic diagnosis.","Dominant inheritance can be missed with de novo variants or marked variable expressivity.","Distal-onset cases may be classified as a distal myopathy.","Assuming prominent cardiac involvement, as in other myofibrillar myopathies, can be misleading."],"management":["Recognise there is no disease-specific drug therapy and use physiotherapy and occupational therapy to preserve function, prevent contractures and provide orthoses and mobility aids","Undertake respiratory surveillance with pulmonary function testing, introducing non-invasive ventilation for the late respiratory involvement that can occur","Perform baseline and periodic cardiac assessment, although cardiac involvement is characteristically mild or absent","Deliver multidisciplinary neuromuscular rehabilitation to maintain the ambulation often preserved into later life","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and assess at-risk relatives","Refer to specialist neuromuscular services for confirmation and long-term follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular medicine textbooks","Reviews of the myofibrillar myopathies","Clinical neurogenetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_d2":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy D2 (transportin-3-related)","system":"Neuromuscular / Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD D2","LGMD1F","TNPO3","transportinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by dominant variants in TNPO3 encoding transportin-3, a nuclear import receptor; the D-prefix denotes autosomal dominant inheritance, unlike the recessive R-subtypes.","The disease-causing change characteristically affects the stop codon or adjacent sequence, producing an abnormally extended transportin-3 protein with a toxic gain-of-function effect.","Inheritance is dominant, so a positive family history across generations is typical and helps separate it from the recessive limb-girdle subtypes.","Age at onset is strikingly wide even within one family, spanning early childhood to late adulthood.","Weakness is proximal and limb-girdle in distribution, and distal involvement as well as respiratory muscle weakness can develop as the disease advances.","Serum creatine kinase is usually normal or only modestly raised, in contrast with the very high values seen in sarcoglycanopathy or dysferlinopathy.","The originally described large kindred was of Italian and Spanish origin, and reports remain relatively rare worldwide."],"pathophysiology":"LGMD D2 is caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in TNPO3 on chromosome 7q32, encoding transportin-3, a member of the importin-beta family that mediates nuclear import of serine/arginine-rich splicing factors and other cargo. The characteristic disease alleles disrupt the natural termination of the protein, generating a C-terminally extended transportin-3 that acts through a dominant, gain-of-function or dominant-negative mechanism rather than simple haploinsufficiency. The abnormal protein is thought to perturb nucleocytoplasmic trafficking and the handling of splicing factors within myonuclei, with mislocalisation of transportin-3 and disturbed RNA-processing pathways in muscle. This produces a chronic myopathy with proximal-predominant weakness; the dominant mechanism and the highly variable age at onset distinguish it mechanistically from the recessive structural sarcolemmal defects of the R-subtypes.","additionalDifferentials":["Other autosomal dominant limb-girdle subtypes such as LGMD D1 (DNAJB6) and LGMD D3 (HNRNPDL)","Recessive limb-girdle subtypes (LGMD R1 calpainopathy, R2 dysferlinopathy) when family history is unclear","Myotonic dystrophy given multisystem dominant inheritance","Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy","Late-onset Pompe disease with proximal and respiratory weakness","Inclusion body and other rimmed-vacuole myopathies","Congenital or dominant myopathies with wide intrafamilial variability"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase (often normal or only mildly elevated)","Detailed multigenerational family history and examination of relatives","Muscle MRI to characterise the pattern of selective muscle involvement","Muscle biopsy, which may show myopathic changes and rimmed vacuoles","Targeted TNPO3 sequencing focusing on the terminal region, or a comprehensive LGMD gene panel","Pulmonary function testing where respiratory involvement is suspected"],"redFlags":["Emerging respiratory muscle weakness with symptoms of nocturnal hypoventilation","Progression to loss of independent ambulation","New swallowing difficulty suggesting bulbar or more widespread involvement","Unexplained functional decline warranting reassessment of the pattern of weakness"],"prognosis":"Progression is generally slow but the course is highly variable, mirroring the wide range in age at onset, so some individuals remain mildly affected while others develop substantial proximal weakness. Long-term outlook is influenced chiefly by the degree of respiratory muscle involvement.","pitfalls":["Excluding a muscular dystrophy because creatine kinase is normal or only mildly raised","Overlooking the dominant inheritance and failing to take a detailed family history","Expecting a uniform age at onset when intrafamilial variability is a hallmark","Missing the specific stop-codon-region variant if TNPO3 analysis does not cover the terminal sequence"],"management":["Recognise there is no disease-modifying pharmacotherapy and use physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain proximal strength, prevent contractures and supply orthoses and aids","Provide pulmonary function surveillance with non-invasive ventilation for the respiratory-muscle weakness that can develop as disease advances","Undertake periodic cardiac assessment with ECG and echocardiography","Deliver multidisciplinary neuromuscular rehabilitation adapted to the highly variable rate of progression","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance, assessing affected relatives across generations","Arrange specialist neuromuscular follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular and neurogenetics textbooks","Peer-reviewed limb-girdle muscular dystrophy classification and consensus statements","Muscular dystrophy clinical care guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_d3":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy D3 (HNRNPDL-related)","system":"Neuromuscular / Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD1G","HNRNPDL-related LGMD","HNRNPDL"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant; heterozygous HNRNPDL variants (classically affecting a conserved residue in the second RNA-recognition motif) impair the function of an RNA-binding protein with a prion-like low-complexity domain.","Presents as adult-onset proximal limb-girdle weakness.","A distinctive tendency to limitation of finger and toe flexion was described in the original families.","Cataracts have been reported in a proportion of affected individuals.","Creatine kinase is mildly to moderately elevated.","Muscle biopsy shows dystrophic change with rimmed vacuoles.","First delineated in Brazilian and Uruguayan kindreds.","Formerly designated LGMD1G."],"pathophysiology":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy D3 is an autosomal dominant subtype caused by heterozygous variants in HNRNPDL on chromosome 4q21, which encodes heterogeneous nuclear ribonucleoprotein D-like, an RNA-binding protein that shuttles between nucleus and cytoplasm and regulates transcription, alternative splicing and messenger-RNA metabolism. Nearly all reported families carry variants affecting a conserved residue within the second RNA-recognition motif, and because the protein contains a prion-like low-complexity domain, the mechanism is thought to involve disturbed RNA processing together with aberrant protein aggregation, akin to other RNA-binding-protein myopathies. First delineated in Brazilian and Uruguayan kindreds, the phenotype is an adult-onset proximal limb-girdle weakness with a distinctive tendency to limitation of finger and toe flexion, and cataracts have been reported in some affected individuals. Muscle biopsy shows dystrophic change with rimmed vacuoles.","additionalDifferentials":["LGMD D1 (DNAJB6-related)","LGMD D2 (TNPO3-related)","LGMD D4 (autosomal dominant calpainopathy, CAPN3)","Myotonic dystrophy (cataracts and distal features)","Myofibrillar myopathies","Sporadic inclusion body myositis (rimmed vacuoles)","Other RNA-binding-protein myopathies (for example hnRNPA1 and hnRNPA2B1)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Next-generation sequencing gene panel or whole-exome sequencing including HNRNPDL","Muscle biopsy (dystrophic change with rimmed vacuoles)","Slit-lamp examination for cataracts","Muscle MRI to map involvement","Electromyography","Baseline ECG and echocardiography"],"redFlags":["Progressive dysphagia","New respiratory symptoms","Rapid deterioration prompting diagnostic reassessment","Visual symptoms attributable to cataract"],"prognosis":"Progression is slow and many patients retain ambulation for years. Cataracts, when present, may require ophthalmic management.","pitfalls":["The combination of cataracts and weakness may be misattributed to myotonic dystrophy.","Rimmed vacuoles may falsely suggest sporadic inclusion body myositis.","Rarity and ancestry-specific reporting can delay recognition.","Variable expressivity within families may obscure the dominant inheritance pattern."],"management":["Recognise there is no disease-specific drug therapy and use physiotherapy and occupational therapy to preserve proximal strength and address limitation of finger and toe flexion","Arrange ophthalmological review and cataract surgery where cataracts develop","Provide respiratory monitoring with ventilatory support as required","Deliver multidisciplinary neuromuscular rehabilitation, with ambulation often retained for years","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and family screening","Arrange specialist neuromuscular follow-up for diagnosis and surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular medicine textbooks","Consensus classification of the limb-girdle muscular dystrophies","Clinical neurogenetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_r1":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R1 (calpain3-related)","system":"Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD2A","Calpainopathy","CAPN3-related limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","LGMDR1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neuromuscular medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic CAPN3 variants; calpainopathy is the most common form of limb-girdle muscular dystrophy worldwide.","Classically autosomal recessive, although a dominant form due to specific CAPN3 variants is now also recognised.","Predominantly an atrophic phenotype — the absence of calf hypertrophy helps distinguish it from dystroglycanopathies and sarcoglycanopathies.","Early and selective involvement of the posterior thigh (hamstrings and adductors) and hip-girdle and gluteal muscles, with scapular winging and a scapulo-humeral pattern.","Achilles and other tendon contractures may precede overt weakness, and abdominal wall laxity can occur.","Cardiac muscle is characteristically spared, and respiratory involvement is usually late and comparatively mild.","Serum creatine kinase is elevated, often markedly so in the early symptomatic phase.","Immunoblot may show reduced calpain-3, but a normal result does not exclude the diagnosis because function can be lost with preserved protein quantity — genetic confirmation is essential."],"pathophysiology":"CAPN3 encodes calpain-3, a skeletal-muscle-specific, calcium-dependent cysteine protease anchored to titin within the sarcomere. It participates in sarcomere remodelling, in the regulated turnover of cytoskeletal and signalling substrates, and in muscle regeneration. Loss of its proteolytic and regulatory activity disturbs sarcomere maintenance and the calcium-dependent signalling that supports myofibre adaptation, producing disorganised regeneration, myofibre atrophy and necrosis with progressive fibrofatty replacement. Because binding to titin also protects calpain-3 from autolysis, disorders of titin can cause a secondary reduction of calpain-3 on immunoblot, a recognised diagnostic trap.","additionalDifferentials":["Dysferlinopathy (LGMD R2)","Sarcoglycanopathies (LGMD R3 to R6)","FKRP-related limb-girdle dystrophy (R9) and other dystroglycanopathies","Becker muscular dystrophy and manifesting dystrophinopathy carriers","Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy (given scapular winging)","Titinopathy (a cause of secondary calpain-3 deficiency)","Late-onset Pompe disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","CAPN3 sequencing with deletion and duplication analysis, or a next-generation neuromuscular gene panel","Muscle biopsy showing dystrophic features, with calpain-3 immunoblot","Muscle MRI — selective posterior thigh and paraspinal involvement supports the diagnosis","Electromyography demonstrating myopathic motor units","Baseline cardiac and respiratory assessment to document typically preserved cardiac function and to monitor for late respiratory decline"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive weakness or accelerated loss of ambulation","Late symptoms of nocturnal hypoventilation such as morning headache, daytime somnolence and orthopnoea","Progressive scoliosis affecting respiratory function","Falls and fracture risk arising from proximal weakness","Unexpected cardiac symptoms, which should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis because cardiac involvement is atypical"],"prognosis":"The disease is slowly progressive, with loss of independent ambulation typically occurring years to decades after onset and with wide individual variability. Cardiac sparing and generally later respiratory involvement give a comparatively favourable systemic prognosis relative to several other muscular dystrophies.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal calpain-3 immunoblot excludes the disease — enzymatic function may be lost with preserved protein, so confirm genetically.","Stopping at a reduced calpain-3 immunoblot, which can be secondary to a titinopathy rather than primary calpainopathy.","Misreading early eosinophilic infiltrates on biopsy as inflammatory myositis and initiating inappropriate immunosuppression.","Interpreting scapular winging as evidence of facioscapulohumeral dystrophy.","Discounting the diagnosis because calf hypertrophy is absent — its absence is expected here."],"management":["Recognise there is no disease-modifying drug and use physiotherapy, stretching and occupational therapy to maintain function and prevent Achilles and other tendon contractures","Undertake respiratory surveillance with pulmonary function testing and non-invasive ventilation for the usually late respiratory decline","Perform baseline cardiac assessment, recognising that cardiac muscle is characteristically spared","Provide orthoses and mobility aids as weakness progresses, with loss of ambulation typically only after years to decades","Deliver multidisciplinary neuromuscular rehabilitation","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance, noting a dominant form is also recognised, and screen relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular medicine references","Muscular dystrophy management guidelines","Clinical neurology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_r11":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R11 (POMT1-related)","system":"Neuromuscular / Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD2K","POMT1-related dystroglycanopathy","POMT1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An alpha-dystroglycanopathy caused by biallelic POMT1 variants; POMT1 acts with POMT2 to initiate O-mannosylation of alpha-dystroglycan.","Represents the mild end of the POMT1 spectrum, whose severe end is Walker-Warburg syndrome with brain and eye malformation.","Childhood-onset proximal weakness, frequently accompanied by calf (and sometimes thigh or tongue) hypertrophy.","Cognitive impairment or microcephaly may occur even when brain imaging is relatively normal.","Creatine kinase is typically markedly elevated.","Muscle biopsy shows reduced glycosylated alpha-dystroglycan (for example with the IIH6 antibody) despite a preserved core protein.","First described in Turkish families.","Genetic testing distinguishes it from other dystroglycanopathies caused by FKRP, FKTN, POMT2 and POMGNT1."],"pathophysiology":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R11 is an alpha-dystroglycanopathy caused by biallelic hypomorphic variants in POMT1 on chromosome 9q34.13, which encodes protein O-mannosyltransferase 1. Working in a complex with POMT2, this enzyme catalyses the first committed step of O-mannosyl glycan synthesis on alpha-dystroglycan; the resulting matriglycan is essential for high-affinity binding of alpha-dystroglycan to laminin and other extracellular-matrix ligands that anchor the sarcolemma. Severe null variants abolish glycosylation and produce lethal Walker-Warburg syndrome with cobblestone lissencephaly and eye malformation, whereas the milder residual-function variants underlying LGMD R11 permit partial glycosylation, giving a limb-girdle phenotype often accompanied by calf hypertrophy and cognitive impairment or microcephaly with relatively preserved brain structure. Muscle shows reduced immunolabelling of glycosylated alpha-dystroglycan despite a normally expressed core protein.","additionalDifferentials":["LGMD R9 (FKRP-related dystroglycanopathy)","LGMD R13 (fukutin/FKTN-related dystroglycanopathy)","LGMD R14 (POMT2-related dystroglycanopathy)","LGMD R15 (POMGNT1-related dystroglycanopathy)","Walker-Warburg syndrome and muscle-eye-brain disease (severe end of the spectrum)","Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy (calf hypertrophy with high creatine kinase)","Congenital muscular dystrophies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Next-generation sequencing gene panel or whole-exome sequencing covering POMT1 and other dystroglycanopathy genes","Muscle biopsy with glycosylated alpha-dystroglycan immunostaining","Brain MRI to exclude structural malformation","Formal cognitive assessment","Muscle MRI to map involvement","Baseline ECG and echocardiography"],"redFlags":["Developmental regression or a new focal neurological deficit","Seizures","Visual failure suggesting ocular involvement","Cardiac or respiratory decline"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable; the limb-girdle end of the spectrum progresses more slowly than congenital forms, but the rate of motor progression and the degree of cognitive involvement strongly influence prognosis.","pitfalls":["Markedly raised creatine kinase with calf hypertrophy can be mistaken for a dystrophinopathy.","A normal brain MRI does not exclude clinically significant cognitive involvement.","Reduced alpha-dystroglycan glycosylation is shared across dystroglycanopathies and is not gene-specific.","Milder cases may present later than expected and be misclassified."],"management":["Recognise there is no disease-specific pharmacotherapy and use physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain motor function and prevent contractures","Provide cognitive assessment with educational and developmental support for associated intellectual impairment or microcephaly","Undertake respiratory surveillance with non-invasive ventilation and cardiac monitoring, as dystroglycanopathies can involve heart and respiratory muscle","Arrange neuroimaging to exclude a structural brain malformation","Deliver multidisciplinary paediatric neuromuscular care and rehabilitation","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular medicine textbooks","Diagnostic guidance for the alpha-dystroglycanopathies","Clinical neurogenetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_r12":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R12 (anoctamin-5-related)","system":"Neuromuscular / Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD2L","ANO5-related LGMD","Anoctaminopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic ANO5 variants; anoctamin-5 participates in sarcolemmal membrane repair.","One of the commoner adult-onset recessive limb-girdle dystrophies in Northern Europe, linked to the c.191dupA founder allele.","Male-predominant and often asymmetric, with prominent calf and quadriceps involvement.","May present with exercise-induced myalgia, cramps, rhabdomyolysis or asymptomatic hyperCKaemia before overt weakness.","Creatine kinase is usually markedly elevated, frequently preceding clinical weakness.","Allelic with distal Miyoshi-type myopathy (MMD3), so the phenotype spans a proximal-to-distal continuum.","The course is relatively benign with ambulation often preserved for a long period.","Cardiac involvement is uncommon but has been reported."],"pathophysiology":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R12 is caused by biallelic variants in ANO5 on chromosome 11p14.3, which encodes anoctamin-5, a member of the TMEM16 family that functions at the sarcolemma and its internal membranes as a phospholipid scramblase and putative calcium-activated ion channel involved in membrane repair. The recurrent c.191dupA founder variant is common in Northern European populations, making ANO5-related disease one of the more frequent adult-onset recessive limb-girdle dystrophies in that region. Defective resealing of the membrane after contraction-induced injury is thought to drive myofibre necrosis, producing a characteristically asymmetric and often male-predominant phenotype with prominent calf and quadriceps involvement, exercise-related myalgia and marked hyperCKaemia. The same gene underlies distal Miyoshi-type myopathy (MMD3) and isolated hyperCKaemia, so ANO5 disease spans a proximal-to-distal continuum.","additionalDifferentials":["LGMD R2 (dysferlinopathy, with overlapping distal Miyoshi phenotype)","LGMD R1 (calpainopathy, CAPN3)","Miyoshi myopathy due to dysferlin (phenotypic overlap)","Metabolic and glycogen-storage myopathies presenting with rhabdomyolysis","Idiopathic or paucisymptomatic hyperCKaemia","Idiopathic inflammatory myopathy (when asymmetry raises suspicion)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase (characteristically high)","Next-generation sequencing gene panel or whole-exome sequencing including ANO5 and DYSF","Muscle MRI (asymmetric posterior thigh and calf involvement)","Muscle biopsy with dysferlin and anoctamin studies","Electromyography","Baseline ECG and echocardiography","Renal function and urinary myoglobin assessment during rhabdomyolysis episodes"],"redFlags":["Rhabdomyolysis with dark urine and risk of acute kidney injury","Chest symptoms or breathlessness suggesting cardiomyopathy","Rapidly worsening weakness","Severe exertional myalgia limiting activity"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally favourable, with slow progression and ambulation retained for many years. Episodes of rhabdomyolysis require vigilance because of the associated risk to the kidneys.","pitfalls":["Isolated hyperCKaemia may be wrongly dismissed as benign.","Phenotypic overlap with dysferlinopathy can lead to misdiagnosis without genetic testing.","Marked asymmetry may prompt inappropriate suspicion of an acquired or inflammatory cause.","Affected or carrier women may be under-recognised because of the male predominance."],"management":["Recognise there is no disease-modifying drug and use physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain strength and function while avoiding overexertion that can trigger rhabdomyolysis","Recognise and treat episodes of rhabdomyolysis promptly with fluid resuscitation and renal protection","Advise on graded exercise and avoidance of muscle-injury triggers given exercise-induced myalgia and cramps","Perform baseline cardiac assessment with ECG and echocardiography and respiratory monitoring","Deliver multidisciplinary neuromuscular rehabilitation, with ambulation usually retained for many years","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular medicine textbooks","Consensus classification of the limb-girdle muscular dystrophies","Clinical neurogenetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_r2":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R2 (dysferlin-related)","system":"Neuromuscular / Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD R2","LGMD2B","DYSF","dysferlinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by recessive loss-of-function variants in DYSF encoding dysferlin, a sarcolemmal membrane-repair protein; the same gene underlies Miyoshi myopathy, and the two phenotypes may coexist within one family.","Onset is typically in late adolescence or early adulthood in a previously athletic individual, and many patients recall excellent prior sporting ability before insidious weakness begins.","Two overlapping patterns are recognised: proximal pelvic-girdle weakness (the LGMD R2 pattern) and distal posterior-calf wasting with difficulty standing on tiptoe (the Miyoshi pattern).","Serum creatine kinase is characteristically very markedly elevated, often to a degree that can be mistaken for polymyositis and prompt inappropriate immunosuppression.","Muscle imaging often shows early selective involvement of the gastrocnemius and soleus with relative sparing until later of more proximal groups, aiding differentiation from sarcoglycanopathies.","Inflammatory infiltrates on biopsy are common and can lead to a mistaken diagnosis of idiopathic inflammatory myopathy; absent dysferlin on immunolabelling redirects the diagnosis.","Cardiac involvement is generally mild or absent, distinguishing dysferlinopathy from the sarcoglycanopathies and from LGMD R9 (FKRP-related)."],"pathophysiology":"LGMD R2 results from biallelic pathogenic variants in DYSF on chromosome 2p13, which encodes dysferlin, a large type II transmembrane protein enriched at the sarcolemma and in T-tubules. Dysferlin is central to calcium-dependent resealing of membrane wounds: when the sarcolemma is torn during contraction, dysferlin coordinates the recruitment and fusion of intracellular repair vesicles to patch the defect. Loss of functional dysferlin leaves myofibres unable to reseal microtears, producing progressive membrane leakage, secondary inflammatory activation, fibre necrosis and regeneration, and eventual fibro-fatty replacement. Because the same gene defect can present as either proximal (limb-girdle) or distal (Miyoshi) weakness, the disorder is best conceptualised as a single dysferlinopathy spectrum. The prominent secondary inflammation explains both the very high creatine kinase and the frequent misclassification as an acquired inflammatory myopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic inflammatory myopathy (polymyositis) given inflammatory biopsy and very high creatine kinase","Miyoshi distal myopathy (allelic DYSF phenotype)","Anoctaminopathy / LGMD R12 (ANO5), which also causes calf-predominant weakness with high creatine kinase","Sarcoglycanopathies (LGMD R3-R6) with more prominent cardiac and childhood involvement","LGMD R9 (FKRP-related) with calf hypertrophy and cardiorespiratory disease","Calpainopathy / LGMD R1 with scapular winging and contractures"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase (characteristically very high, especially early)","Muscle MRI of thighs and calves to define the posterior-compartment predominant pattern","Muscle biopsy with immunohistochemistry and immunoblot for dysferlin (reduced or absent)","Monocyte dysferlin assay by flow cytometry as an accessible screening test","Targeted DYSF sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis or a multigene LGMD panel","Electromyography to confirm a myopathic pattern and exclude neurogenic mimics"],"redFlags":["Progressive loss of ability to walk on tiptoe or climb stairs signalling advancing posterior-compartment involvement","Erroneous immunosuppressive treatment for presumed inflammatory myopathy without genetic confirmation","Rapid functional decline after unaccustomed intense exercise","New exertional dyspnoea or reduced exercise tolerance prompting respiratory assessment"],"prognosis":"Progression is variable but generally slow, with many patients remaining ambulant for a long period after onset before pelvic-girdle weakness eventually threatens independent walking. Life expectancy is usually near normal because significant cardiac and respiratory failure are uncommon compared with other subtypes.","pitfalls":["Labelling the disorder polymyositis on the basis of inflammatory infiltrate and very high creatine kinase and starting steroids","Assuming a limb-girdle presentation excludes the allelic distal Miyoshi phenotype within the same family","Expecting the marked cardiomyopathy typical of sarcoglycanopathies, which is not a feature here","Relying on a single normal proximal-muscle biopsy when the process is patchy and posterior-calf predominant"],"management":["No disease-modifying drug therapy exists; a key management step is to avoid the corticosteroids/immunosuppression often given when the markedly raised creatine kinase and inflammatory biopsy are mistaken for polymyositis, as these do not help and may worsen dysferlinopathy","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to preserve range of movement, prevent contractures and provide orthoses and mobility aids as weakness progresses","Guidance to avoid strenuous eccentric and high-impact exercise that provokes sarcolemmal microtears, with low-impact activity and weight management encouraged","Periodic respiratory surveillance with erect and supine spirometry, introducing non-invasive ventilation if respiratory muscle weakness emerges","Baseline and periodic cardiac assessment with ECG and echocardiography, though clinically significant cardiac involvement is uncommon in this subtype","Multidisciplinary neuromuscular clinic care with orthopaedic and spinal input for contracture and posture management","Genetic counselling and carrier testing for relatives given autosomal recessive inheritance, noting the shared DYSF basis with Miyoshi myopathy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular and neurogenetics textbooks","Peer-reviewed limb-girdle muscular dystrophy classification and consensus statements","Muscular dystrophy clinical care guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_r3":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R3 (alpha-sarcoglycan-related)","system":"Neuromuscular / Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD R3","LGMD2D","SGCA","alpha-sarcoglycanopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by recessive variants in SGCA encoding alpha-sarcoglycan; it is the commonest of the four sarcoglycanopathies (LGMD R3-R6) in most populations.","Presentation is often in childhood with a Duchenne-like picture: proximal pelvic then shoulder-girdle weakness, calf hypertrophy, toe-walking and a positive Gowers manoeuvre.","Serum creatine kinase is typically greatly elevated, in the range seen with dystrophinopathies, reflecting active muscle breakdown.","Because loss of one sarcoglycan destabilises the whole complex, immunostaining frequently shows reduced labelling of all four sarcoglycans, so protein testing localises the complex but genetics identifies the specific subunit.","Cognition is preserved, which helps separate it clinically from Duchenne muscular dystrophy despite the overlapping motor picture.","Respiratory muscle weakness tends to develop as disease advances and warrants scheduled surveillance of ventilatory function.","Cardiac involvement occurs but is generally less prominent and less consistent than in beta- and delta-sarcoglycanopathy (LGMD R4 and R6)."],"pathophysiology":"LGMD R3 arises from biallelic pathogenic variants in SGCA on chromosome 17q21, encoding alpha-sarcoglycan, a transmembrane glycoprotein of the sarcoglycan subcomplex within the larger dystrophin-associated glycoprotein complex at the sarcolemma. The sarcoglycans (alpha, beta, gamma, delta) assemble interdependently, so a pathogenic change in the alpha subunit destabilises and reduces membrane expression of the entire tetramer. This weakens the mechanical link between the intracellular cytoskeleton and the extracellular matrix, so contractile stress is transmitted poorly and the membrane becomes vulnerable to contraction-induced injury. The result is repeated cycles of fibre necrosis and regeneration with progressive fibrosis, closely mirroring the mechanism of dystrophinopathy and accounting for the Duchenne-like clinical and biochemical picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy (dystrophinopathy) given the overlapping childhood proximal phenotype","Beta-sarcoglycanopathy / LGMD R4 (SGCB)","Gamma-sarcoglycanopathy / LGMD R5 (SGCG)","Delta-sarcoglycanopathy / LGMD R6 (SGCD)","LGMD R9 (FKRP-related) with calf hypertrophy","Calpainopathy / LGMD R1"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase (markedly elevated)","Muscle biopsy with immunohistochemistry for all four sarcoglycans and for dystrophin to exclude dystrophinopathy","Immunoblot to quantify sarcoglycan complex reduction","Targeted SGCA sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis, or an LGMD gene panel covering all sarcoglycans","Baseline and surveillance echocardiography and electrocardiography","Pulmonary function testing including seated and supine spirometry"],"redFlags":["Loss of independent ambulation in later childhood or adolescence","Symptoms or signs of nocturnal hypoventilation such as morning headache and daytime somnolence","New cardiac symptoms or conduction changes on electrocardiography","Perioperative anaesthetic risk related to reduced respiratory reserve"],"prognosis":"Untreated, many patients lose independent walking during the second decade, with the pace influenced by the severity of the underlying variants. Long-term outlook depends chiefly on respiratory muscle involvement and, less consistently than in beta- or delta-subtypes, on cardiac status.","pitfalls":["Assuming the diagnosis is Duchenne muscular dystrophy in a boy and stopping the workup once dystrophin looks borderline","Reading reduced staining of all sarcoglycans as pointing to a single subtype without confirmatory genetics","Underestimating respiratory decline because limb weakness appears to have plateaued","Overlooking female patients because the pattern superficially resembles X-linked dystrophinopathy"],"management":["No disease-specific pharmacotherapy is available; care is supportive and delivered through a specialist neuromuscular multidisciplinary team","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain function, prevent contractures and provide orthoses, walking aids and wheelchair provision as ambulation declines","Scheduled respiratory surveillance with seated and supine spirometry, introducing non-invasive ventilation for nocturnal hypoventilation","Cardiac surveillance with echocardiography and ECG, treating any cardiomyopathy with standard heart-failure therapy such as ACE inhibitors and beta-blockers","Orthopaedic and spinal monitoring for scoliosis and contractures, with corrective surgery where indicated","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance","Escalate to assisted ventilation and structured palliative and respiratory support as ventilatory failure develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular and neurogenetics textbooks","Peer-reviewed limb-girdle muscular dystrophy classification and consensus statements","Muscular dystrophy cardiac and respiratory surveillance guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_r4":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R4 (beta-sarcoglycan-related)","system":"Neuromuscular / Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD R4","LGMD2E","SGCB","beta-sarcoglycanopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by recessive variants in SGCB encoding beta-sarcoglycan, one of the four sarcoglycanopathies (LGMD R3-R6).","The phenotype is frequently more severe than alpha-sarcoglycanopathy, with earlier onset and a higher likelihood of significant cardiac involvement.","Dilated cardiomyopathy is a well-recognised and sometimes early complication, so cardiac surveillance should not wait for advanced limb weakness.","Presentation resembles Duchenne muscular dystrophy with proximal weakness, calf hypertrophy, Gowers sign and greatly elevated creatine kinase.","Beta-sarcoglycan is considered a core scaffold of the sarcoglycan complex, so its loss tends to destabilise the whole tetramer strongly on immunostaining.","Respiratory muscle weakness commonly accompanies cardiac disease and both contribute to long-term morbidity.","Intellect is normal, helping distinguish it from dystrophinopathy despite the shared motor pattern."],"pathophysiology":"LGMD R4 is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in SGCB on chromosome 4q12, encoding beta-sarcoglycan. Beta-sarcoglycan is a central structural component of the sarcoglycan subcomplex within the dystrophin-associated glycoprotein complex and is thought to act as a scaffold around which alpha, gamma and delta assemble at the sarcolemma. Pathogenic changes in beta-sarcoglycan therefore tend to cause profound loss of the entire complex, severing the mechanical bridge between the cytoskeleton and the basal lamina. Myofibre membranes are consequently prone to contraction-induced injury, driving repeated necrosis-regeneration cycles, inflammation and fibrosis in skeletal muscle. Because the same complex is expressed in cardiac muscle and vascular smooth muscle, its disruption also predisposes to dilated cardiomyopathy, which is a more consistent and sometimes early feature of the beta- and delta-subtypes than of the alpha-subtype.","additionalDifferentials":["Delta-sarcoglycanopathy / LGMD R6 (SGCD), which shares prominent cardiomyopathy","Alpha-sarcoglycanopathy / LGMD R3 (SGCA)","Gamma-sarcoglycanopathy / LGMD R5 (SGCG)","Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy","LGMD R9 (FKRP-related) with cardiorespiratory disease","Other genetic causes of childhood dilated cardiomyopathy with myopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase (markedly elevated)","Echocardiography and electrocardiography at diagnosis with regular follow-up, plus cardiac MRI where available","Muscle biopsy with immunohistochemistry for the sarcoglycan complex and dystrophin","Immunoblot to quantify sarcoglycan reduction","Targeted SGCB sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis or an LGMD gene panel","Pulmonary function testing including supine spirometry"],"redFlags":["Symptoms of heart failure such as breathlessness, orthopnoea or oedema, or arrhythmia","Early loss of ambulation suggesting a severe phenotype","Nocturnal hypoventilation with morning headache and disturbed sleep","Falling ventricular function on surveillance imaging"],"prognosis":"Beta-sarcoglycanopathy is often among the more severe sarcoglycanopathies, with earlier loss of ambulation in severe cases. Prognosis is heavily influenced by the frequently prominent dilated cardiomyopathy and by respiratory muscle involvement, both of which require proactive surveillance.","pitfalls":["Deferring cardiac assessment until limb weakness is advanced, missing early dilated cardiomyopathy","Attributing the picture to dystrophinopathy without genetic confirmation","Assuming all sarcoglycanopathies behave like the milder alpha-subtype","Missing arrhythmia risk that can be independent of the degree of skeletal weakness"],"management":["No disease-modifying drug therapy exists; management is supportive within a specialist neuromuscular multidisciplinary team","Proactive lifelong cardiac surveillance for the frequently prominent dilated cardiomyopathy using echocardiography, ECG and cardiac MRI, not deferring until limb weakness is advanced","Treat established cardiomyopathy with standard heart-failure therapy such as ACE inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers, beta-blockers and aldosterone antagonists, with device therapy where indicated","Respiratory surveillance including supine spirometry, with non-invasive ventilation for hypoventilation","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy for contracture prevention, orthoses and mobility aids, plus orthopaedic and spinal monitoring for scoliosis","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance","Escalate to advanced heart-failure care with transplant assessment and to ventilatory support as cardiac or respiratory failure progresses"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular and neurogenetics textbooks","Peer-reviewed limb-girdle muscular dystrophy classification and consensus statements","Muscular dystrophy cardiac surveillance guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_r5":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R5 (gamma-sarcoglycan-related)","system":"Neuromuscular / Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD R5","LGMD2C","SGCG","gamma-sarcoglycanopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by recessive variants in SGCG encoding gamma-sarcoglycan; historically termed the severe childhood autosomal recessive muscular dystrophy of North African populations.","A recurrent founder deletion in SGCG is particularly common in North African communities, so ancestry and founder-variant testing can shorten the diagnostic pathway.","The phenotype is typically severe and Duchenne-like, with early childhood onset, calf hypertrophy, Gowers sign and often early loss of ambulation.","Serum creatine kinase is very high, in the range associated with dystrophinopathy.","As with the other sarcoglycanopathies, loss of gamma-sarcoglycan usually reduces immunostaining of the whole sarcoglycan complex.","Cardiac involvement occurs but has generally been reported as less consistent than in the beta- and delta-subtypes (LGMD R4 and R6).","Cognition is preserved, which helps separate it clinically from Duchenne muscular dystrophy."],"pathophysiology":"LGMD R5 results from biallelic pathogenic variants in SGCG on chromosome 13q12, encoding gamma-sarcoglycan, a transmembrane component of the sarcoglycan subcomplex of the dystrophin-associated glycoprotein complex. Like the other sarcoglycans, gamma assembles interdependently with alpha, beta and delta, so a pathogenic change reduces membrane expression of the whole tetramer and disrupts the structural link between the subsarcolemmal cytoskeleton and the extracellular matrix. The destabilised sarcolemma is vulnerable to mechanical stress during contraction, producing repetitive fibre necrosis and regeneration, inflammation and progressive fibro-fatty replacement. A specific recurrent founder deletion in SGCG accounts for a large share of cases in North African ancestry populations and typically produces a severe, early-onset phenotype, underpinning the historical designation of this subtype as a severe childhood recessive dystrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy given the severe childhood proximal picture","Alpha-sarcoglycanopathy / LGMD R3 (SGCA)","Beta-sarcoglycanopathy / LGMD R4 (SGCB)","Delta-sarcoglycanopathy / LGMD R6 (SGCD)","LGMD R9 (FKRP-related) with calf hypertrophy","Calpainopathy / LGMD R1"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase (very high)","Targeted testing for the recurrent SGCG founder deletion in patients of relevant ancestry","Muscle biopsy with immunohistochemistry for the sarcoglycan complex and dystrophin","Immunoblot to quantify sarcoglycan reduction","SGCG sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis or an LGMD gene panel","Baseline and surveillance echocardiography and pulmonary function testing"],"redFlags":["Early loss of independent walking in childhood","Emerging respiratory muscle weakness with nocturnal hypoventilation","New cardiac symptoms or reduced ventricular function on imaging","Rapid scoliosis progression after loss of ambulation"],"prognosis":"The phenotype is frequently severe, and many affected children lose independent ambulation relatively early, broadly resembling the trajectory of dystrophinopathy. Long-term outlook is shaped by progressive respiratory muscle weakness and, less consistently than in beta- or delta-subtypes, by cardiac involvement.","pitfalls":["Not testing for the recurrent SGCG founder deletion in patients of North African ancestry, delaying diagnosis","Assuming milder disease by analogy with alpha-sarcoglycanopathy","Mislabelling as Duchenne muscular dystrophy without genetic confirmation","Neglecting scoliosis and respiratory surveillance once a child becomes non-ambulant"],"management":["No disease-specific pharmacotherapy exists; management is supportive within a specialist neuromuscular multidisciplinary team, reflecting the frequently severe Duchenne-like course","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to prevent contractures, prolong ambulation and provide orthoses, walking aids and timely wheelchair provision","Respiratory surveillance with spirometry and introduction of non-invasive ventilation as respiratory muscle weakness advances","Cardiac surveillance with echocardiography and ECG, treating any cardiomyopathy with standard heart-failure therapy, while noting cardiac involvement is less consistent than in the beta- and delta-subtypes","Orthopaedic and spinal monitoring for scoliosis with corrective surgery where indicated","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance, including targeted testing for the recurrent SGCG founder deletion in relatives of North African ancestry","Escalate to assisted ventilation and coordinated supportive care as ventilatory failure develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular and neurogenetics textbooks","Peer-reviewed limb-girdle muscular dystrophy classification and consensus statements","Muscular dystrophy clinical care guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_r7":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R7 (telethonin-related)","system":"Neuromuscular / Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD2G","TCAP-related LGMD","Telethoninopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in TCAP, encoding telethonin (T-cap), a small Z-disc protein that caps the N-terminus of titin and stabilises the sarcomere.","Distinctive phenotype combines proximal limb-girdle weakness with unusually early distal lower-limb involvement, notably anterior tibial weakness and foot drop.","Onset is typically in childhood to early adulthood with slowly progressive pelvic- and shoulder-girdle weakness.","First and most extensively characterised in Brazilian kindreds; it remains one of the rarer recessive limb-girdle subtypes worldwide.","Creatine kinase is usually moderately raised.","Muscle biopsy characteristically shows rimmed vacuoles, and immunolabelling demonstrates absent telethonin.","Cardiac surveillance is warranted because dilated cardiomyopathy has been reported in a subset of patients.","Either calf hypertrophy or distal lower-limb atrophy may be observed on examination."],"pathophysiology":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R7 is a recessive skeletal-muscle disorder caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in TCAP on chromosome 17q12, which encodes telethonin (also called T-cap), a small titin-cap protein of the sarcomeric Z-disc. Telethonin is phosphorylated by the titin kinase domain and binds the N-terminal Z-disc region of titin, anchoring the thin filaments and contributing to sarcomere assembly, mechanical stability and stretch-sensing signalling. Pathogenic frameshift and nonsense variants (classically first identified in Brazilian families) abolish telethonin expression, so the Z-disc loses its cap, sarcomeres become mechanically unstable during repeated contraction, and progressive myofibre degeneration with rimmed-vacuole formation ensues. Because telethonin is also expressed in cardiac muscle, a proportion of patients develop dilated cardiomyopathy, which helps distinguish this subtype from several other recessive limb-girdle dystrophies.","additionalDifferentials":["LGMD R1 (calpainopathy, CAPN3)","LGMD R2 (dysferlinopathy, DYSF)","LGMD R10 (titinopathy, TTN)","Distal myopathies such as tibial muscular dystrophy","GNE myopathy (distal weakness with rimmed vacuoles)","Myofibrillar myopathies","Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease (when foot drop dominates)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Next-generation sequencing gene panel or whole-exome sequencing including TCAP","Muscle biopsy with telethonin immunohistochemistry","Muscle MRI to map the pattern of selective muscle involvement","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies (myopathic pattern; exclude neuropathy)","ECG and echocardiography for cardiac surveillance","Respiratory function testing when weakness is advanced"],"redFlags":["New breathlessness, orthopnoea or ankle oedema suggesting cardiomyopathy","Frequent falls related to foot drop with risk of injury","Rapid loss of independent ambulation","Symptoms of nocturnal hypoventilation such as morning headache and unrefreshing sleep"],"prognosis":"Progression is slow and ambulation is often retained well into adulthood, although loss of walking can occur over time. Prognosis is chiefly modified by the presence and severity of cardiac involvement.","pitfalls":["Prominent distal weakness may cause misclassification as a distal myopathy or a peripheral neuropathy.","Rimmed vacuoles can be misattributed to sporadic inclusion body myositis.","Reduced telethonin staining may be secondary in other conditions, so genetic confirmation is essential.","Its rarity outside Brazilian populations means the diagnosis is frequently overlooked."],"management":["No disease-specific pharmacotherapy exists; management is supportive within a specialist neuromuscular multidisciplinary team","Ankle-foot orthoses for the characteristic anterior tibial weakness and foot drop, with physiotherapy to maintain gait and prevent contractures","Occupational therapy and mobility aids to preserve independence as pelvic- and shoulder-girdle weakness progresses","Cardiac surveillance with ECG and echocardiography, since cardiac involvement is the principal modifier of prognosis in this subtype","Respiratory function monitoring with spirometry and non-invasive ventilation if hypoventilation develops","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular medicine textbooks","Consensus classification of the limb-girdle muscular dystrophies","Clinical neurogenetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_r8":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R8 (TRIM32-related)","system":"Neuromuscular / Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD2H","TRIM32-related LGMD","Sarcotubular myopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic variants in TRIM32, which encodes an E3 ubiquitin ligase; the recurrent p.Asp487Asn founder variant is characteristic of the North American Hutterite population.","Typically presents as a mild, slowly progressive proximal limb-girdle weakness with onset in adulthood.","Allelic with sarcotubular myopathy (same gene, overlapping histology) and, through distinct variants, with Bardet-Biedl syndrome type 11.","Creatine kinase is usually mildly to moderately elevated.","Muscle biopsy may show dystrophic change with rimmed vacuoles or tubular aggregates.","Facial and respiratory involvement are generally mild or absent.","Cardiac involvement is uncommon, in contrast to several other limb-girdle subtypes.","Consider the diagnosis in mild adult-onset limb-girdle weakness, particularly with relevant ancestry."],"pathophysiology":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R8 results from biallelic variants in TRIM32 on chromosome 9q33.1, which encodes tripartite motif-containing protein 32, an E3 ubiquitin ligase that tags sarcomeric and cytoskeletal substrates (including actin- and desmin-associated proteins) for regulated proteasomal turnover. The recurrent p.Asp487Asn founder variant was first identified in the Hutterite population, and most disease-causing changes lie within the C-terminal NHL beta-propeller domain that mediates substrate recognition. Loss of ligase activity is thought to disturb the balance of muscle protein synthesis and degradation, impairing myofibrillar maintenance and producing slowly progressive dystrophic change, sometimes with a sarcotubular myopathy pattern that is an allelic condition. Notably, different variants in the same gene cause Bardet-Biedl syndrome type 11, illustrating that the muscular dystrophy and the ciliopathy phenotypes arise from distinct functional consequences at the TRIM32 locus.","additionalDifferentials":["LGMD R1 (calpainopathy, CAPN3)","LGMD R2 (dysferlinopathy, DYSF)","Sarcoglycanopathies (LGMD R3-R6)","Sarcotubular myopathy (allelic TRIM32 phenotype)","Bardet-Biedl syndrome type 11 (allelic, distinct multisystem phenotype)","Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy","Metabolic myopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Next-generation sequencing gene panel or whole-exome sequencing including TRIM32","Muscle biopsy with histochemistry (dystrophic change, rimmed vacuoles or tubular aggregates)","Muscle MRI to characterise the pattern of involvement","Electromyography (myopathic pattern)","Baseline ECG and echocardiography","Ophthalmological, renal and systemic review if ciliopathy overlap features are present"],"redFlags":["Prominent cardiac symptoms suggesting an alternative or additional diagnosis","Retinal dystrophy, renal anomaly or polydactyly raising concern for Bardet-Biedl overlap","Rapidly progressive weakness inconsistent with the usually mild course","New respiratory symptoms"],"prognosis":"The course is generally mild and slowly progressive, with independent ambulation usually preserved for many years. Life expectancy is typically normal in the absence of unusual complications.","pitfalls":["The mild, non-specific weakness is easily dismissed and the diagnosis missed.","Histological overlap with sarcotubular myopathy can cause classification confusion.","Assuming TRIM32 always causes myopathy overlooks the allelic Bardet-Biedl phenotype.","A normal or only mildly raised creatine kinase may falsely reassure the clinician."],"management":["No disease-specific pharmacotherapy exists; management is conservative and supportive given the generally mild, slowly progressive course","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain proximal strength, function and mobility, with aids provided as required","Baseline and periodic respiratory function monitoring, though facial and respiratory involvement are usually mild or absent","Baseline and periodic cardiac assessment with ECG and echocardiography","Multidisciplinary neuromuscular follow-up to track slow progression","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance, including testing for the recurrent p.Asp487Asn founder variant in Hutterite kindreds"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular medicine textbooks","Consensus classification of the limb-girdle muscular dystrophies","Clinical neurogenetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"limb_girdle_muscular_dystrophy_r9":{"name":"Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R9 (FKRP-related)","system":"Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGMD2I","FKRP-related limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","LGMDR9"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neuromuscular medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic variants in FKRP (fukutin-related protein); a common founder variant (L276I) predominates in Northern European populations and typically produces a milder, later-onset limb-girdle phenotype.","Belongs to the secondary dystroglycanopathies — hypoglycosylation of alpha-dystroglycan impairs its binding to laminin in the muscle basement membrane.","Proximal limb-girdle weakness with prominent calf (and sometimes tongue and thigh) hypertrophy; the phenotype can closely mimic Becker or Duchenne muscular dystrophy.","Dilated cardiomyopathy and respiratory muscle weakness are common and may be disproportionate to limb weakness, so both warrant systematic lifelong surveillance.","Serum creatine kinase is characteristically markedly elevated.","Exertional myalgia, cramps and myoglobinuria can occur and are sometimes the presenting feature.","Allelic severity spectrum: the same gene causes severe congenital muscular dystrophy (MDC1C) and structural brain and eye malformation syndromes (muscle-eye-brain disease, Walker-Warburg syndrome) at the severe end.","Muscle immunostaining shows reduced glycosylated alpha-dystroglycan with a preserved protein core; secondary reduction of laminin-alpha2 may also be seen."],"pathophysiology":"FKRP encodes fukutin-related protein, a ribitol-phosphate transferase acting in the Golgi apparatus during the post-translational glycosylation of alpha-dystroglycan. Alpha-dystroglycan is a central element of the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex that anchors the subsarcolemmal cytoskeleton to laminin-211 in the extracellular matrix. Loss of transferase activity shortens the functional matriglycan chain so that alpha-dystroglycan can no longer bind laminin, weakening the mechanical link across the sarcolemma. The resulting membrane fragility permits contraction-induced injury, myofibre necrosis and repeated cycles of regeneration that are eventually outpaced, with progressive replacement of muscle by fibrofatty tissue. The degree of residual glycosylation broadly correlates with clinical severity, explaining the wide spectrum from adult limb-girdle weakness to congenital brain and eye malformation.","additionalDifferentials":["Becker and Duchenne muscular dystrophy (dystrophinopathy)","Sarcoglycanopathies (LGMD R3 to R6)","Other dystroglycanopathies (POMT1, POMT2, fukutin or GMPPB-related limb-girdle dystrophy)","Calpainopathy (LGMD R1) and dysferlinopathy (LGMD R2)","Manifesting carriers of dystrophinopathy","Late-onset Pompe disease (acid maltase deficiency)","Idiopathic inflammatory myopathy (given raised creatine kinase and myalgia)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Genetic testing — FKRP sequencing or a next-generation neuromuscular gene panel, with targeted analysis for the common founder variant","Muscle biopsy with immunostaining and immunoblot for glycosylated alpha-dystroglycan and laminin-alpha2","Muscle MRI to define the pattern of selective involvement (posterior thigh, adductors, gastrocnemius)","Cardiac assessment with echocardiography and electrocardiography, and cardiac MRI where available","Respiratory assessment including erect and supine forced vital capacity and overnight oximetry with capnography","Electromyography demonstrating myopathic motor units"],"redFlags":["New or progressive breathlessness, orthopnoea or morning headache suggesting respiratory muscle failure and nocturnal hypoventilation","Symptoms of heart failure or arrhythmia","Pigmented urine (myoglobinuria) after exertion or intercurrent illness, with risk of acute kidney injury","Peri-operative and anaesthetic risk, including avoidance of triggering agents and risk of rhabdomyolysis","Rapid loss of ambulation"],"prognosis":"The course is variable: the common founder genotype often permits ambulation into adulthood with slowly progressive weakness, whereas severe genotypes lead to earlier loss of walking. Cardiac and respiratory complications are the principal determinants of morbidity and mortality and are potentially treatable when detected early.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the condition as Becker or Duchenne muscular dystrophy because of calf hypertrophy and a raised creatine kinase.","Overlooking cardiomyopathy or nocturnal hypoventilation because limb weakness appears comparatively mild.","Assuming a normal dystrophin stain excludes the diagnosis — specific glycosylated alpha-dystroglycan staining is required.","Attributing exertional myoglobinuria to a metabolic myopathy and missing the underlying dystroglycanopathy.","Expecting intellectual disability — cognition is typically normal in the limb-girdle phenotype, in contrast with the severe congenital forms."],"management":["No disease-modifying drug therapy exists; management is supportive within a specialist neuromuscular multidisciplinary team","Systematic lifelong cardiac surveillance for dilated cardiomyopathy with echocardiography, ECG and cardiac MRI, treating established disease with standard heart-failure therapy such as ACE inhibitors and beta-blockers","Respiratory surveillance with erect and supine forced vital capacity and overnight oximetry with capnography, introducing non-invasive ventilation for nocturnal hypoventilation","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy with orthoses and mobility aids, and counselling to maintain hydration and avoid triggers during episodes of exertional myalgia, cramps and myoglobinuria","Orthopaedic and spinal monitoring for contractures and scoliosis","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance, noting the milder later-onset course typical of the common L276I founder variant","Escalate to advanced heart-failure care and assisted ventilation as cardiac or respiratory failure progresses"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular medicine references","Muscular dystrophy management guidelines","Clinical neurology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"limbic_encephalitis":{"name":"Limbic encephalitis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["autoimmune limbic encephalitis","paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis","LE"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An inflammatory syndrome of the medial temporal lobes presenting subacutely with short-term memory loss, confusion, psychiatric change, and seizures","It may be paraneoplastic (driven by an underlying cancer) or a primary autoimmune process, and identifying the antibody helps predict the association with tumour","Antibodies to intracellular antigens (e.g. Hu, Ma2) carry a strong cancer association and respond less well to immunotherapy, whereas surface-antigen antibodies (e.g. LGI1, NMDA receptor, GABA-B) often respond better","LGI1 antibody encephalitis characteristically causes faciobrachial dystonic seizures and hyponatraemia","NMDA-receptor encephalitis often affects younger patients with psychiatric onset, movement disorders, autonomic instability, and reduced consciousness, and is associated with ovarian teratoma","Brain MRI typically shows increased T2/FLAIR signal in the medial temporal lobes, and CSF may show a lymphocytic pleocytosis or oligoclonal bands","A thorough search for an occult malignancy is essential and may need repeating over time if initial screening is negative","Early immunotherapy and treatment of any underlying tumour improve outcome, so prompt recognition matters"],"pathophysiology":"Limbic encephalitis results from an immune attack, often antibody-mediated, directed at neuronal antigens concentrated in the limbic system, particularly the hippocampi and amygdalae. In paraneoplastic disease, a systemic tumour expresses neuronal antigens that trigger a cross-reactive immune response; antibodies against intracellular antigens mark a T-cell-mediated, less reversible process, whereas antibodies against neuronal surface proteins (such as NMDA receptor, LGI1, CASPR2, and GABA-B receptor) are considered directly pathogenic and more treatment-responsive. Inflammation of limbic structures produces the memory, behavioural, and seizure manifestations.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex and other viral encephalitis","Status epilepticus and other seizure disorders","Rapidly progressive dementia including prion disease","Wernicke encephalopathy and metabolic encephalopathies","CNS lymphoma or other neoplastic infiltration","Primary psychiatric illness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and cerebrospinal fluid neuronal antibody panels (surface and intracellular antigens)","Brain MRI focused on the medial temporal lobes","Lumbar puncture for cell count, protein, oligoclonal bands, and to exclude infection","EEG for seizures and encephalopathic patterns","Whole-body CT and FDG-PET to search for an underlying malignancy, with repeat surveillance if negative","Age- and antibody-directed tumour screening (e.g. pelvic imaging for ovarian teratoma in NMDA-receptor disease)"],"redFlags":["Subacute encephalopathy warrants urgent workup for autoimmune and paraneoplastic causes alongside exclusion of infective encephalitis","Positive onconeural or surface antibodies mandate a directed and, if negative, repeated search for occult cancer","Refractory seizures or status epilepticus requiring urgent management","Autonomic instability or reduced consciousness (notably in NMDA-receptor encephalitis) needing intensive monitoring"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the antibody and cause: surface-antigen syndromes and prompt immunotherapy often allow substantial recovery, whereas classic paraneoplastic encephalitis with intracellular-antigen antibodies tends to respond poorly and is dominated by the underlying malignancy.","pitfalls":["Failing to start empirical aciclovir while herpes simplex encephalitis remains possible","Not searching thoroughly, or repeatedly, for an occult tumour after a negative initial screen","Missing faciobrachial dystonic seizures as a treatable clue to LGI1 antibody disease","Attributing early psychiatric presentations solely to a primary psychiatric disorder","Delaying immunotherapy while awaiting antibody results in a clinically compatible case"],"management":["Institute first-line immunotherapy promptly with high-dose corticosteroids, intravenous immunoglobulin and/or plasma exchange, which is most effective in the surface-antigen antibody syndromes","Escalate to second-line immunotherapy such as rituximab or cyclophosphamide for refractory, severe or relapsing disease","Search for and treat any underlying malignancy, for example resection of an ovarian teratoma in NMDA-receptor disease, which is central to controlling paraneoplastic forms","Control seizures, including faciobrachial dystonic seizures in LGI1 disease, with antiseizure medication","Provide supportive and symptomatic care, managing psychiatric disturbance, movement disorder and autonomic instability, with intensive care and airway support for reduced consciousness in NMDA-receptor disease","Correct associated hyponatraemia, characteristic of LGI1 antibody disease","Undertake surveillance tumour imaging when the initial screen is negative, as the malignancy may only declare itself later","Refer for neurorehabilitation and neuropsychological support to aid cognitive and functional recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"limbic_predominant_age_related_tdp43":{"name":"Limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy (LATE)","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LATE","limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy","LATE-NC","hippocampal sclerosis dementia of ageing"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Describes an amnestic dementia of the very old driven by limbic-predominant TDP-43 proteinopathy","Clinically mimics amnestic Alzheimer's disease, with insidious, progressive episodic memory impairment","Increasingly recognised in individuals in their eighth decade and beyond","Progression tends to be more gradual than in typical Alzheimer's disease","Frequently coexists with Alzheimer pathology and hippocampal sclerosis, contributing to mixed dementia","Amyloid biomarkers are typically negative when it occurs in isolation, which helps distinguish it from Alzheimer's disease","A neuropathological staging scheme tracks TDP-43 spread from the amygdala to the hippocampus and then the middle frontal gyrus"],"pathophysiology":"Limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy is characterised by accumulation of abnormally phosphorylated TDP-43 protein within limbic structures, beginning in the amygdala and spreading to the hippocampus and neocortex, often accompanied by hippocampal sclerosis. This proteinopathy disproportionately affects the oldest old and produces a predominantly amnestic syndrome. It is a distinct entity from Alzheimer's disease but frequently coexists with it, and the combination of pathologies tends to accelerate cognitive decline.","additionalDifferentials":["Amnestic Alzheimer's disease","Hippocampal sclerosis of ageing","Primary age-related tauopathy","Argyrophilic grain disease","Autoimmune or limbic encephalitis","Mild cognitive impairment of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Neuropsychological testing documenting an amnestic profile","MRI brain assessing hippocampal and medial temporal atrophy","Amyloid PET or CSF amyloid and tau biomarkers to gauge the contribution of Alzheimer pathology","FDG-PET to assess regional metabolism","Investigations to exclude autoimmune or limbic encephalitis where onset is atypical","Vascular risk assessment and structural imaging for coexisting cerebrovascular disease"],"redFlags":["Subacute or rapidly progressive amnesia suggesting autoimmune limbic encephalitis","Seizures or fluctuating consciousness","Focal neurological signs indicating an alternative structural cause","Onset in a much younger patient than expected for this age-related entity"],"prognosis":"This is a slowly progressive dementia; when it occurs in isolation the course tends to be more indolent than Alzheimer's disease, but coexisting Alzheimer or vascular pathology worsens the outlook. It is a neuropathologically defined entity, so definitive confirmation remains post-mortem, and management is supportive with attention to safety, function and carer support.","pitfalls":["Assuming all late-life amnestic dementia is Alzheimer's disease and not considering this entity, especially in amyloid-negative patients","Interpreting negative amyloid biomarkers as excluding a neurodegenerative cause of memory loss","Overlooking mixed pathology in the very old","Expecting a definitive antemortem diagnosis, which is not yet possible"],"management":["No disease-specific or disease-modifying therapy exists; management is symptomatic and supportive as for an amnestic dementia","Provide cognitive support with structured routines, orientation aids and memory strategies; a trial of a cholinesterase inhibitor may be considered where coexisting Alzheimer pathology is likely, though benefit is uncertain","Manage neuropsychiatric symptoms with non-pharmacological measures first, reserving pharmacotherapy for refractory cases and avoiding antipsychotic-related harm","Optimise vascular risk factors given the frequent coexisting cerebrovascular disease that worsens the trajectory","Address safety and function, including falls prevention, driving assessment and support with activities of daily living","Provide carer education and support, advance care planning and coordinated memory-service and social-care involvement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice, current edition","Nelson et al., consensus report defining limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy (Brain)"],"crossLinks":[]},"linear_iga_bullous_dermatosis":{"name":"Linear IgA bullous dermatosis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LABD","linear IgA disease","chronic bullous disease of childhood","linear IgA dermatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A subepidermal blistering disease defined by linear IgA deposition along the basement membrane zone on direct immunofluorescence","The childhood form (chronic bullous disease of childhood) classically shows annular or rosette-like clusters of tense blisters ('string of pearls' or 'cluster of jewels') around the perineum and lower trunk","The adult form is more heterogeneous and can mimic bullous pemphigoid or dermatitis herpetiformis","Frequently drug-induced, with vancomycin being the most commonly implicated agent; withdrawal often leads to resolution","Target antigens are typically fragments of BP180 (the LAD-1/LABD97 ectodomain) within the basement membrane zone","Unlike dermatitis herpetiformis it is not associated with gluten-sensitive enteropathy and does not respond to a gluten-free diet","Dapsone is a mainstay of treatment given the neutrophil-rich subepidermal infiltrate"],"pathophysiology":"Linear IgA bullous dermatosis is an immune-mediated subepidermal blistering disease in which IgA autoantibodies bind antigens in the basement membrane zone, most often cleaved ectodomain fragments of BP180 (LAD-1/LABD97). Antibody deposition along the dermo-epidermal junction recruits neutrophils, whose enzymatic and inflammatory activity separates the epidermis from the dermis to form tense subepidermal blisters. The characteristic linear (rather than granular) IgA pattern distinguishes it from dermatitis herpetiformis. Many adult cases are triggered by drugs, particularly vancomycin, in which the aberrant immune response abates once the drug is stopped.","additionalDifferentials":["Bullous pemphigoid","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita","Bullous erythema multiforme / Stevens-Johnson syndrome (especially in children)","Bullous impetigo","Pemphigoid gestationis (in pregnancy)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Perilesional biopsy for direct immunofluorescence showing linear IgA at the basement membrane zone","Lesional biopsy for histology demonstrating a subepidermal blister with neutrophils","Indirect immunofluorescence for circulating IgA basement membrane antibodies","Salt-split skin study to localise antibody binding, typically to the epidermal (roof) side","Detailed drug history, especially recent vancomycin exposure","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase level before starting dapsone"],"redFlags":["Extensive blistering with mucosal or ocular involvement risking scarring","Recent initiation of a culprit drug requiring prompt withdrawal","Secondary infection of denuded skin","Haemolysis or methaemoglobinaemia as complications of dapsone therapy"],"prognosis":"The childhood form frequently remits spontaneously within a few years, and drug-induced adult cases usually resolve after the trigger is withdrawn; idiopathic adult disease may be more persistent but generally responds well to dapsone.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with dermatitis herpetiformis and instituting an unnecessary gluten-free diet","Overlooking a drug trigger, particularly vancomycin, that would resolve on withdrawal","Starting dapsone without checking glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status","Missing mucosal involvement that requires closer monitoring for scarring"],"management":["Identify and withdraw any implicated drug, especially vancomycin, as drug-induced disease frequently resolves once the trigger is stopped","First-line pharmacotherapy for idiopathic disease is dapsone, checking glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status beforehand to avoid haemolysis","Use sulfapyridine or add systemic corticosteroids where dapsone is insufficient or not tolerated, sometimes in combination","Provide skin and wound care for blisters and erosions, with attention to preventing and treating secondary infection","Monitor for dapsone-related haemolytic anaemia and methaemoglobinaemia during treatment","Reassure in the childhood form (chronic bullous disease of childhood), which frequently remits spontaneously over a few years","Refer to dermatology for direct-immunofluorescence-guided diagnosis and management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"linear_iga_disease":{"name":"Linear IgA disease","system":"Skin","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Linear IgA bullous dermatosis","LABD","Chronic bullous disease of childhood"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autoimmune subepidermal blistering disease defined by linear deposition of IgA along the basement membrane zone on direct immunofluorescence — the diagnostic hallmark.","Has two peaks: a childhood form (chronic bullous disease of childhood) and an adult form; the childhood pattern classically shows annular or polycyclic lesions with peripheral blisters — the 'cluster of jewels' or 'string of pearls' sign.","Childhood lesions favour the lower abdomen, perineum, genitals, buttocks and inner thighs, and are often perioral; adult disease is more variable and may mimic bullous pemphigoid or dermatitis herpetiformis.","Drug-induced disease is well recognised, with vancomycin the classic culprit; other reported triggers include some antibiotics, NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors.","Target antigens are cleavage fragments of the ectodomain of collagen XVII/BP180 (termed LAD-1 and LABD97) located within the lamina lucida.","Unlike dermatitis herpetiformis, it is NOT associated with coeliac disease or gluten sensitivity, so a gluten-free diet is not indicated.","Mucosal involvement can occur, including scarring conjunctivitis that resembles mucous membrane pemphigoid.","Dapsone is the usual first-line therapy (glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status should be checked first), and drug-induced cases typically resolve after withdrawal of the offending agent."],"pathophysiology":"Linear IgA disease is caused by IgA autoantibodies directed against antigens in the cutaneous basement membrane zone, predominantly proteolytically processed fragments of the ectodomain of collagen XVII (BP180), known as LAD-1 and LABD97, which reside within the lamina lucida. Deposition of IgA along the dermo-epidermal junction activates complement and recruits neutrophils, whose released proteases and reactive species degrade adhesion structures and split the epidermis from the dermis, producing tense subepidermal blisters. Histologically this generates a subepidermal blister with a neutrophil-rich infiltrate and neutrophilic microabscesses in the dermal papillae, features that can closely mimic dermatitis herpetiformis. The disease may be idiopathic or drug-induced, with vancomycin the archetypal precipitant, and it can affect skin and mucous membranes. Crucially, unlike dermatitis herpetiformis it is not linked to gluten-sensitive enteropathy, reflecting a different antigenic target and immunopathological mechanism.","additionalDifferentials":["Bullous pemphigoid","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Mucous membrane (cicatricial) pemphigoid","Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita","Bullous systemic lupus erythematosus","Erythema multiforme or Stevens-Johnson syndrome in acute severe presentations","In children: bullous impetigo and scabies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lesional skin biopsy (subepidermal blister with a neutrophil-rich infiltrate and papillary microabscesses)","Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin showing homogeneous linear IgA at the basement membrane zone","Indirect immunofluorescence including salt-split skin (binding typically to the epidermal/roof side)","Immunoblot or ELISA for BP180-related antigens","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase assay before starting dapsone","Detailed drug history, particularly for recent vancomycin","Ophthalmology and mucosal assessment if mucosal symptoms are present"],"redFlags":["Ocular involvement with conjunctival scarring or symblepharon, carrying a risk of blindness and requiring ophthalmology input","Laryngeal or pharyngeal mucosal disease threatening the airway","Extensive blistering with skin-barrier failure","Recent vancomycin or other suspect drug as a potentially reversible trigger"],"prognosis":"The childhood form is frequently self-limiting, often remitting spontaneously within a few years or around puberty, whereas adult idiopathic disease tends to be more chronic though usually controllable. Drug-induced disease generally resolves after withdrawal of the causative agent.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with dermatitis herpetiformis and wrongly imposing a lifelong gluten-free diet.","Diagnosing on histology alone without direct immunofluorescence, which is the definitive test.","Missing a drug trigger such as vancomycin.","Overlooking mucosal or ocular disease and its scarring potential.","Starting dapsone without first checking glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status."],"management":["Take a careful drug history and withdraw any implicated agent, particularly vancomycin but also some antibiotics, NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors, as drug-induced disease usually resolves on withdrawal","First-line pharmacotherapy is dapsone, with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase assay beforehand to reduce the risk of haemolysis","Add or substitute sulfapyridine or systemic corticosteroids for inadequate response, with immunosuppressants reserved for resistant disease","Provide blister and wound care with prevention of secondary infection, and topical corticosteroids for localised disease","Monitor for dapsone-related haemolytic anaemia and methaemoglobinaemia during therapy","Reassure in the childhood form, which is frequently self-limiting and often remits around puberty, and avoid an unnecessary gluten-free diet as it is not coeliac-associated","Refer to dermatology for direct-immunofluorescence-confirmed diagnosis and ongoing management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology","British Association of Dermatologists guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"linear_porokeratosis":{"name":"Linear porokeratosis","system":"Dermatology / Neurocutaneous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["porokeratosis linearis","linear porokeratosis of Mibelli","segmental porokeratosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Neurocutaneous Disorders (current reference texts)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A segmental form of porokeratosis presenting as linear, Blaschko-distributed plaques with the characteristic raised keratotic border (cornoid lamella)","It represents a mosaic manifestation, often reflecting a post-zygotic second hit in the mevalonate-pathway genes that also cause the generalised inherited porokeratoses","Dermoscopy and histology show the cornoid lamella, a column of parakeratosis marking the advancing edge","Lesions typically begin in infancy or childhood and follow the lines of Blaschko along a limb","It carries the highest malignant potential of the porokeratosis variants, with squamous cell carcinoma and Bowen disease arising within long-standing plaques","Extensive or facial linear porokeratosis may rarely coexist with skeletal or developmental anomalies, warranting broader assessment","Management is generic: sun protection, topical keratolytics or field therapies, and long-term surveillance with biopsy of any suspicious change"],"pathophysiology":"Linear porokeratosis is regarded as a mosaic disorder in which a post-zygotic second hit occurs in a cell already carrying a germline variant in the mevalonate biosynthetic pathway, most often affecting genes such as MVK, PMVK, MVD or FDPS. Loss of function in this pathway within the affected clone disturbs epidermal keratinocyte proliferation and differentiation, producing the cornoid lamella, a column of abnormal parakeratotic cells that defines the advancing border of each lesion. Because the second hit occurs during development, the lesions are distributed along the lines of Blaschko. The clonal, genetically unstable epidermis explains the heightened risk of malignant transformation within these plaques.","additionalDifferentials":["Other porokeratosis variants (porokeratosis of Mibelli, disseminated superficial actinic porokeratosis)","Linear epidermal naevus","Lichen striatus","Incontinentia pigmenti (verrucous stage)","Linear lichen planus","Inflammatory linear verrucous epidermal naevus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy to demonstrate the cornoid lamella and exclude malignancy","Dermoscopy to identify the characteristic peripheral keratotic ridge","Serial photography to monitor for change","Biopsy of any nodular, ulcerated or rapidly changing area","Skeletal or developmental assessment where lesions are extensive or facial","Molecular testing for mevalonate-pathway variants where available"],"redFlags":["Nodule, ulceration or bleeding within a plaque suggesting squamous cell carcinoma","Rapid enlargement of a previously stable lesion","Extensive disease with associated skeletal or developmental anomalies","Non-healing erosion within a long-standing lesion"],"prognosis":"The condition is chronic and persistent; while many lesions remain stable, linear porokeratosis has the greatest malignant potential among the porokeratoses, so lifelong surveillance is warranted and prognosis worsens if squamous cell carcinoma develops.","pitfalls":["Dismissing linear keratotic plaques as an epidermal naevus and missing the cornoid lamella","Failing to biopsy a changing lesion because porokeratosis was assumed benign","Underestimating the malignant potential of the linear variant","Neglecting sun protection, which accelerates malignant change"],"management":["No definitive cure exists; management centres on lesion control and vigilant malignancy surveillance because this variant carries the highest malignant potential of the porokeratoses","Topical therapies for symptomatic lesions include topical retinoids, topical 5-fluorouracil and vitamin D analogues, with topical cholesterol-statin combinations directed at the underlying mevalonate-pathway defect","Advise strict photoprotection to limit keratinocyte damage and reduce the risk of malignant transformation","Undertake regular skin surveillance with serial photography and biopsy any nodular, ulcerated or rapidly changing area to detect squamous cell carcinoma or Bowen disease early","Use cryotherapy, laser or surgical excision for localised, symptomatic or suspicious lesions","Arrange skeletal and developmental assessment where lesions are extensive or facial","Escalate to surgical excision and oncological management if squamous cell carcinoma develops within a plaque"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Happle R. Mosaicism in Human Skin"],"crossLinks":[]},"linear_scleroderma_en_coup_de_sabre":{"name":"Linear scleroderma en coup de sabre","system":"Integumentary system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["En coup de sabre","Linear morphoea of the face","Frontoparietal linear scleroderma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A subtype of linear morphoea presenting as a linear, atrophic, sclerotic band on the frontoparietal scalp and forehead, likened to the stroke of a sabre.","Predominantly affects children and young adults and follows the lines of Blaschko, suggesting cutaneous mosaicism.","Overlaps with Parry-Romberg progressive hemifacial atrophy, which may represent a severe end of the same spectrum.","May extend beyond skin into subcutaneous tissue, muscle and underlying bone, producing facial asymmetry.","Neurological complications include seizures and headache, with ipsilateral white-matter change, calcification and atrophy seen on MRI.","Ocular and dental or orofacial abnormalities may occur on the affected side.","It is a localised, not systemic, sclerosing disorder, so Raynaud phenomenon and internal organ sclerosis are absent.","Active disease is treated with immunosuppression such as methotrexate with or without systemic corticosteroids, while established deformity may need later reconstructive surgery."],"pathophysiology":"En coup de sabre is a form of localised scleroderma in which immune activation drives fibroblast stimulation, excessive collagen deposition and progressive sclerosis and atrophy of skin and deeper tissues in a linear, Blaschko-distributed segment of the face and scalp. Postulated mechanisms include autoimmune-mediated microvascular injury and inflammation, with possible roles for cutaneous mosaicism and, in some, antecedent trauma or infection as triggers; contiguous involvement of underlying structures accounts for the bony atrophy and neurological findings.","additionalDifferentials":["Parry-Romberg syndrome (progressive hemifacial atrophy)","Other morphoea subtypes (plaque, generalised)","Systemic sclerosis","Focal facial lipodystrophy or atrophy","Lupus panniculitis and discoid lupus erythematosus","Post-traumatic or post-surgical scarring","Scleroderma-like conditions such as porphyria or graft-versus-host disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy demonstrating dermal sclerosis","Autoantibody profile including antinuclear antibodies to characterise autoimmunity","MRI brain for intracranial involvement when neurological symptoms are present","MRI of the affected soft tissue and face to assess depth of involvement","Inflammatory markers to gauge disease activity","Ophthalmological and dental or maxillofacial assessment","Serial clinical photography with a validated skin activity score"],"redFlags":["New seizures or focal neurological signs","Progressive extension across the midline or rapid deepening of the lesion","Ocular inflammation or visual symptoms","Involvement threatening growth of the facial skeleton in a child"],"prognosis":"The disease often becomes inactive over years but leaves permanent atrophy and asymmetry; early immunosuppression can limit progression, while neurological and ocular involvement determine additional morbidity.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for systemic sclerosis and pursuing unnecessary internal-organ investigation.","Underestimating deep and intracranial extension.","Delaying immunosuppression during the active inflammatory phase and allowing avoidable deformity.","Overlooking the overlap with Parry-Romberg syndrome.","Attempting reconstructive surgery before the disease is quiescent."],"management":["Institute systemic immunosuppression during active disease, with methotrexate, frequently combined with systemic corticosteroids, to halt progression of sclerosis and atrophy","Use mycophenolate mofetil or other immunosuppressants for refractory or progressive disease","Provide physiotherapy and topical measures to maintain soft-tissue mobility and skin condition","Monitor disease activity clinically and with inflammatory markers and imaging to guide duration of immunosuppression","Undertake neurological surveillance with brain MRI where seizures or headache occur, treating seizures with antiseizure medication","Arrange ophthalmology and dental or maxillofacial assessment for the ocular and orofacial complications on the affected side","Offer reconstructive intervention such as autologous fat grafting or corrective surgery for established atrophy and asymmetry once disease is inactive","Coordinate care across dermatology, rheumatology and neurology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Consensus guidance on juvenile localised scleroderma (morphoea)","Standard textbook of paediatric rheumatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"lipodermatosclerosis":{"name":"Lipodermatosclerosis (sclerosing panniculitis)","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sclerosing panniculitis","hypodermitis sclerodermiformis","chronic panniculitis with lipomembranous change"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents on the lower legs of middle-aged and older adults, especially women, as induration, erythema, hyperpigmentation and tenderness above the medial malleolus","Chronic disease produces the characteristic 'inverted champagne bottle' or 'bowling pin' leg, with a hard, constricted gaiter area and swelling above","It is a manifestation of chronic venous insufficiency and venous hypertension, often with associated varicosities, oedema and venous ulceration","The acute phase can mimic cellulitis with a hot, red, painful leg, but it is usually bilateral or recurrent and afebrile","Biopsy, when needed, shows a mostly lobular panniculitis with fat necrosis, septal fibrosis and lipomembranous (membranocystic) change","Duplex ultrasound helps confirm underlying venous reflux","Compression therapy is the cornerstone of treatment, alongside leg elevation, exercise and management of venous disease"],"pathophysiology":"Lipodermatosclerosis is a sclerosing panniculitis driven by chronic venous insufficiency. Sustained venous hypertension leads to capillary proliferation, leakage of fibrinogen and red cells into the dermis, and localised tissue ischaemia and hypoxia. Fibrin cuffs around capillaries, activation of leucocytes and release of proteolytic and profibrotic mediators cause progressive fat necrosis followed by fibrosis of the subcutis and dermis. Over time the affected lower leg becomes woody and constricted, and the compromised skin is prone to ulceration.","additionalDifferentials":["Cellulitis / erysipelas","Erythema nodosum","Morphoea (localised scleroderma)","Necrobiosis lipoidica","Deep vein thrombosis","Stasis dermatitis without sclerosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Venous duplex ultrasound to assess reflux and exclude thrombosis","Ankle-brachial pressure index before applying compression","Skin biopsy only if the diagnosis is unclear, taken with care given poor healing","Inflammatory markers to help distinguish from cellulitis","Assessment of oedema and cardiac/renal contributors","Wound swab if an ulcer shows signs of infection"],"redFlags":["Genuine superimposed cellulitis with fever and systemic upset","Coexisting deep vein thrombosis presenting as acute unilateral swelling","Significant peripheral arterial disease that contraindicates firm compression","Non-healing or atypical ulceration raising concern for malignancy"],"prognosis":"The condition is chronic and progressive if venous hypertension is untreated, but consistent compression and management of venous disease can relieve pain, halt progression and reduce ulceration; established sclerosis and skin changes are only partially reversible.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating recurrent episodes as cellulitis with antibiotics rather than recognising venous disease","Applying compression without first excluding significant arterial insufficiency","Performing an unnecessary biopsy on a poorly healing leg","Overlooking a coexisting deep vein thrombosis in an acutely swollen leg"],"management":["Graduated compression therapy is the mainstay, applied after the ankle-brachial pressure index has excluded significant arterial disease","Treat the underlying chronic venous insufficiency with leg elevation and duplex-guided venous intervention such as endovenous ablation for correctable reflux","Provide skin care and emollients, and manage any associated venous ulceration with appropriate dressings","Offer analgesia, and consider an anabolic agent such as stanozolol or pentoxifylline for the painful acute inflammatory phase, with appropriate monitoring","Encourage calf-muscle-pump activation through exercise and weight management to reduce venous hypertension","Distinguish the acute presentation from cellulitis, since it is usually bilateral, recurrent and afebrile, to avoid unnecessary antibiotics","Refer for specialist venous assessment where reflux or ulceration is not controlled by conservative measures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"lipoedema":{"name":"lipoedema","system":"subcutaneous adipose and lymphatic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lipohyperplasia dolorosa","painful fat syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disorder of symmetrical, disproportionate subcutaneous adipose accumulation affecting the buttocks, hips and legs and sometimes the arms, occurring almost exclusively in women.","Onset or worsening clusters around periods of hormonal change, notably puberty, pregnancy and the menopause, pointing to an oestrogen-related influence.","The feet are characteristically spared, producing an abrupt cuff or bracelet at the ankle, in contrast to lymphoedema which typically involves the foot.","The affected tissue is tender and painful, and patients bruise easily owing to increased capillary fragility.","The Stemmer sign is negative in early disease and the swelling is non-pitting, helping to separate it from lymphoedema.","The disproportion is resistant to dietary energy restriction and generalised weight loss, which reduce visceral and upper-body fat while sparing the affected depots.","Longstanding lipoedema may cause secondary lymphatic overload, producing lipo-lymphoedema with foot involvement and a positive Stemmer sign.","It is frequently misdiagnosed as simple obesity, causing considerable psychological distress and inappropriate blame for perceived dietary failure."],"pathophysiology":"Lipoedema is characterised by hyperplasia and hypertrophy of subcutaneous adipose tissue in a symmetrical, gynoid distribution that spares the trunk, hands and feet. The precise mechanism is incompletely understood, but proposed contributors include a hormonally modulated adipose disorder, microangiopathy with increased capillary permeability and fragility (accounting for pain and bruising), altered adipocyte and extracellular matrix behaviour, and a hereditary predisposition given frequent familial clustering. In early disease lymphatic transport is preserved, but progressive adipose expansion mechanically overloads the lymphatics and may culminate in secondary lymphatic insufficiency (lipo-lymphoedema). Chronic inflammation within the expanded fat and low-grade tissue oedema are thought to sustain symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["primary or secondary lymphoedema","generalised obesity with bilateral lipohypertrophy","chronic venous insufficiency","Dercum disease (adiposis dolorosa)","hypothyroid myxoedema","systemic oedema from cardiac, renal or hepatic disease","familial multiple symmetrical lipomatosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["clinical assessment of distribution, tenderness, bruising and Stemmer sign","lymphoscintigraphy where lymphoedema or lipo-lymphoedema is suspected","venous duplex ultrasonography to exclude venous disease","dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry or bioimpedance for body composition","thyroid, renal, hepatic and cardiac evaluation to exclude systemic causes","high-resolution ultrasound of subcutaneous tissue to gauge fat thickness"],"redFlags":["new pitting oedema and a positive Stemmer sign indicating secondary lymphatic failure","skin breakdown, recurrent cellulitis or lymphorrhoea","disabling pain or mobility loss","features of a coexisting eating disorder or severe psychological distress","rapidly progressive or asymmetrical swelling prompting reassessment of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Lipoedema is a chronic, often slowly progressive condition that is not corrected by diet alone; conservative measures such as compression, exercise and complex decongestive therapy control symptoms, and liposuction can achieve durable reduction of affected fat in selected patients. Progression to lipo-lymphoedema worsens the outlook if untreated.","pitfalls":["labelling the condition as simple obesity and attributing failure to lose the affected fat to poor adherence","confusing it with lymphoedema and applying the wrong management emphasis","overlooking evolution into lipo-lymphoedema when the foot becomes involved","neglecting the pain, bruising and psychological burden that define the patient experience","expecting bariatric or dietary weight loss alone to resolve the disproportion"],"management":["No pharmacotherapy corrects the abnormal adipose tissue; conservative complex decongestive therapy is the mainstay, combining graduated compression garments with manual lymphatic drainage","Encourage regular low-impact exercise, particularly aquatic exercise, to support mobility and lymphatic flow","Offer weight management for coexisting obesity while explaining that the affected gynoid depots resist dietary energy restriction","Consider liposuction using tumescent or water-jet-assisted techniques for durable reduction of the affected fat in selected patients","Provide pain management and psychological support, addressing the tenderness, easy bruising and body-image impact","Monitor for progression to lipo-lymphoedema and treat secondary lymphatic insufficiency with intensified decongestive therapy","Advise attentive skin care to prevent complications in oedematous limbs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Wold, Hines and Allen description and subsequent lipoedema consensus guidance","International Society of Lymphology Consensus Document on peripheral oedema"],"crossLinks":[]},"lipoid_congenital_adrenal_hyperplasia":{"name":"Lipoid congenital adrenal hyperplasia","system":"Endocrine / Adrenal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lipoid CAH","StAR deficiency","Congenital lipoid adrenal hyperplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic and endocrine medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most severe form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia, usually caused by variants in STAR encoding the steroidogenic acute regulatory protein.","StAR mediates transport of cholesterol into the mitochondrion, the rate-limiting first step of all steroid hormone synthesis.","The block impairs production of every class of adrenal and gonadal steroid: mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids and sex steroids.","Cholesterol accumulates within steroidogenic cells, producing lipid-laden, enlarged adrenal glands that give the condition its name.","Affected infants typically present with severe salt-wasting adrenal crisis in the neonatal period.","46,XY infants have female-appearing external genitalia because testicular androgen synthesis fails in utero.","46,XX individuals have normal external genitalia at birth but develop ovarian failure, and cyclical ovarian steroidogenesis can cause later cyst formation.","A phenotypically milder, non-classic form with predominant glucocorticoid deficiency and later presentation is increasingly recognised."],"pathophysiology":"Lipoid congenital adrenal hyperplasia is an autosomal recessive disorder that most often results from loss-of-function variants in STAR, the gene encoding the steroidogenic acute regulatory protein, and less commonly from severe variants in the cholesterol side-chain cleavage enzyme (CYP11A1). StAR shuttles cholesterol from the outer to the inner mitochondrial membrane, where side-chain cleavage converts it to pregnenolone; this is the obligatory first and rate-limiting step of all steroidogenesis. When StAR function is lost, the adrenal cortex and gonads cannot initiate hormone synthesis, so mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids and sex steroids are all deficient. The disease is classically described as a two-hit process: an initial impairment of StAR-dependent steroidogenesis is compounded by progressive intracellular accumulation of cholesterol esters, which are toxic and physically disrupt the remaining StAR-independent steroidogenic capacity, leading to massively engorged, lipid-laden glands. Loss of cortisol feedback drives high corticotrophin, and loss of aldosterone causes salt wasting with hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia and hypovolaemia. In 46,XY fetuses the failure of testicular testosterone production prevents masculinisation, resulting in female-appearing genitalia, whereas 46,XX fetuses appear normal at birth because ovarian steroid synthesis is quiescent in fetal life; at expected puberty gonadotrophin-driven stimulation can transiently produce oestrogen and provoke ovarian cyst formation before the gonad also fails.","additionalDifferentials":["P450 side-chain cleavage (CYP11A1) deficiency","3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 deficiency","21-hydroxylase deficiency (salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia)","Congenital adrenal hypoplasia (for example NR0B1/DAX1-related)","Adrenoleukodystrophy and other causes of primary adrenal insufficiency in infancy","Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome in a 46,XY infant with female genitalia","Familial glucocorticoid deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum electrolytes for hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia","Serum cortisol with a raised ACTH indicating primary adrenal failure","Plasma renin activity and aldosterone confirming mineralocorticoid deficiency","Broadly low adrenal and gonadal steroid profile across all pathways","Adrenal imaging that may show enlarged lipid-laden glands","Karyotype with pelvic and gonadal imaging","STAR and CYP11A1 molecular genetic testing"],"redFlags":["Neonatal salt-wasting crisis with hypovolaemic shock, hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia","Hyperpigmentation and failure to thrive from unrecognised adrenal insufficiency","46,XY infant with female-appearing genitalia and intra-abdominal testes carrying malignancy risk","Circulatory collapse or arrhythmia during intercurrent illness","Delayed puberty or ovarian cysts in a 46,XX adolescent with the condition"],"prognosis":"Untreated classic disease is fatal in infancy from adrenal crisis, but with early diagnosis, lifelong glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement, salt supplementation in infancy and sex-steroid replacement, survival into adulthood is expected. Fertility is severely limited, and prognosis depends on avoidance of adrenal crises, monitoring of intra-abdominal gonads in 46,XY individuals, and multidisciplinary care of the disorder of sex development.","pitfalls":["Assuming a 46,XY infant with female genitalia is unaffected until a salt-wasting crisis develops.","Attributing neonatal collapse to sepsis alone and missing underlying primary adrenal failure.","Expecting a single elevated precursor as in other forms; here essentially all steroids are low.","Overlooking the milder non-classic form that presents later with glucocorticoid deficiency and preserved mineralocorticoid function.","Neglecting surveillance of intra-abdominal gonads and later ovarian cyst formation."],"management":["Lifelong glucocorticoid replacement with hydrocortisone to correct the profound cortisol deficiency","Mineralocorticoid replacement with fludrocortisone, together with sodium chloride supplementation in infancy, to manage salt-wasting","Emergency treatment of adrenal crisis with parenteral hydrocortisone, fluid resuscitation and correction of hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia and hypoglycaemia, supported by sick-day dosing rules and an emergency steroid card","Sex-steroid replacement from the expected time of puberty, guided by karyotype, gonadal status and sex of rearing, noting 46,XY infants have female-appearing genitalia","Regular monitoring of growth, electrolytes, renin and pubertal development to titrate replacement","Multidisciplinary paediatric endocrine care with input on sex assignment and later fertility counselling","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance of the STAR (or CYP11A1) defect"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard endocrinology and paediatric endocrinology textbooks","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia and disorders of sex development guidelines","Inborn errors of steroidogenesis reference literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"lipoid_proteinosis":{"name":"lipoid proteinosis","system":"Skin / Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Urbach-Wiethe disease","hyalinosis cutis et mucosae","lipoglycoproteinosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function variants in ECM1 (extracellular matrix protein 1), leading to deposition of hyaline material in skin, mucosae and viscera","A hoarse cry or weak, husky voice from early infancy, due to infiltration of the vocal cords, is often the earliest and most characteristic feature","Moniliform blepharosis, a row of beaded waxy papules along the eyelid margins, is a classic diagnostic clue","Skin lesions evolve from early blistering and pox-like scars to waxy yellow papules, plaques and verrucous, hyperkeratotic lesions over extensor and friction sites","Infiltration of the tongue produces a firm, woody organ with restricted protrusion, and the frenulum may become thickened and bound down","Bilateral bean- or sickle-shaped calcification in the medial temporal lobes (amygdala) is frequently seen on imaging and is associated with epilepsy and impaired recognition of fear","Diagnosis rests on the clinical picture with PAS-positive hyaline deposits on biopsy, confirmed by ECM1 sequencing; management is supportive as no specific therapy exists"],"pathophysiology":"Lipoid proteinosis is caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in ECM1, which encodes extracellular matrix protein 1, a glycoprotein important in basement membrane structure, dermal homeostasis and regulation of growth factors and collagen organisation. ECM1 deficiency disrupts the dermo-epidermal junction and perivascular connective tissue, leading to progressive deposition of amorphous, periodic acid-Schiff-positive hyaline material composed of excess basement membrane components and disordered collagen around blood vessels, adnexa and at the epidermal-dermal interface. This infiltration thickens and stiffens the skin and mucosae, while similar deposition and dystrophic calcification within the brain, particularly the amygdala, account for the neurological and neuropsychiatric features.","additionalDifferentials":["Erythropoietic protoporphyria and other cutaneous porphyrias with photosensitivity and scarring","Amyloidosis with mucocutaneous deposition","Colloid milium","Xanthomatosis and other deposition disorders","Systemic sclerosis or other causes of a bound-down tongue and skin","Papular mucinosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin or mucosal biopsy showing PAS-positive, diastase-resistant hyaline deposits around vessels and at the dermo-epidermal junction","ECM1 gene sequencing to confirm the diagnosis","Brain CT or MRI to detect characteristic medial temporal lobe calcification","Laryngoscopy to assess vocal cord and upper airway infiltration","Electroencephalography if seizures are suspected","Neuropsychological and psychiatric assessment where cognitive or behavioural features are present"],"redFlags":["Progressive airway compromise from laryngeal and pharyngeal infiltration","Seizures related to temporal lobe involvement","Feeding or swallowing difficulty from tongue and oropharyngeal deposition","Corneal or ocular involvement threatening vision"],"prognosis":"Lipoid proteinosis generally follows a benign, slowly progressive course with a normal life expectancy, though hoarseness, cutaneous scarring and neurological features such as epilepsy may persist lifelong. Airway compromise is uncommon but can require intervention, and the overall outlook is determined largely by the extent of laryngeal and neurological involvement.","pitfalls":["Attributing lifelong hoarseness to recurrent laryngitis rather than recognising it as an early sign of the disease","Confusing the hyaline deposits with amyloid, which requires Congo red staining and clinicopathological correlation to distinguish","Overlooking the diagnosis in a child with unexplained scarring, beaded eyelid papules and a husky voice","Failing to image the brain and missing epilepsy-associated temporal lobe calcification","Not offering genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive condition"],"management":["No curative therapy exists; management is symptomatic, with oral dimethyl sulfoxide and oral retinoids such as acitretin tried for skin and mucosal deposits with variable benefit","Provide voice and airway care with laryngoscopic surveillance, and surgical removal of vocal-cord deposits by CO2 laser or microlaryngoscopy where hoarseness is severe or the airway is compromised","Treat cosmetically or functionally significant skin lesions with dermabrasion or laser","Control epilepsy related to medial temporal-lobe (amygdala) calcification with antiseizure medication","Offer neuropsychological and psychiatric support for cognitive and behavioural features","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across dermatology, ENT, neurology and clinical genetics","Provide genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance of the ECM1 defect"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle"],"crossLinks":["colloid_milium","primary_cutaneous_amyloidosis"]},"lipoma":{"name":"Lipoma","system":"Musculoskeletal / Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Benign lipomatous tumour","Simple lipoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours / Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest soft tissue tumour, composed of mature white adipocytes, typically presenting as a soft, mobile, painless subcutaneous mass","Most are superficial and small; deep-seated, intramuscular, or large lesions (over roughly ten centimetres) warrant greater suspicion for an atypical lipomatous tumour","On MRI a simple lipoma follows subcutaneous fat on all sequences and suppresses completely on fat-saturated images, with no thick septa or non-fatty nodules","Common cytogenetic events involve rearrangements of the 12q13-15 region affecting HMGA2, distinct from the MDM2 and CDK4 amplification seen in atypical lipomatous tumour","Multiple lipomas suggest familial multiple lipomatosis or syndromic associations such as Gardner syndrome, Bannayan-Riley-Ruvalcaba syndrome, or benign symmetric lipomatosis (Madelung disease)","Variants include angiolipoma, spindle cell lipoma, and chondroid lipoma, each with characteristic histology","Asymptomatic superficial lipomas need no intervention; marginal excision is reserved for symptomatic, enlarging, or cosmetically troublesome lesions","Any deep or rapidly growing fatty mass should be imaged and, if atypical, biopsied before excision rather than simply shelled out"],"pathophysiology":"A lipoma is a benign clonal neoplasm of mature adipocytes rather than a simple overgrowth of normal fat. The majority are solitary, well-circumscribed, and enclosed by a thin fibrous capsule, arising most often in the subcutaneous tissue of the trunk, neck, and proximal limbs. A large proportion harbour clonal chromosomal rearrangements, most frequently involving the high-mobility group gene HMGA2 at 12q13-15, which drives adipocyte proliferation while preserving terminal differentiation, so the cells remain cytologically bland. The key biological distinction is from atypical lipomatous tumour and well-differentiated liposarcoma, which show amplification of MDM2 and CDK4 on 12q and carry a risk of dedifferentiation and local recurrence; this molecular separation, rather than morphology alone, underpins modern classification and management.","additionalDifferentials":["Atypical lipomatous tumour and well-differentiated liposarcoma","Angiolipoma and spindle cell lipoma","Hibernoma","Epidermoid or sebaceous cyst","Nodular fasciitis or other benign soft tissue proliferation","Myxoid soft tissue tumour when myxoid change is present","Lipoblastoma in a young child"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound as a first-line assessment of a superficial soft tissue lump","MRI with fat-saturated sequences to confirm uncomplicated fat and exclude worrying features","Image-guided core biopsy for deep, large, or radiologically atypical lesions","MDM2 amplification testing by FISH when atypical lipomatous tumour is a consideration","Cross-sectional imaging of the affected compartment to define depth and relationship to muscle","Histological examination of the excised specimen to confirm the diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, size beyond roughly ten centimetres, or a deep or intramuscular location","Pain, fixation to deeper structures, or overlying skin change","Thick or nodular septa, non-fatty enhancing components, or incomplete fat suppression on MRI","Recurrence after previous excision","New neurological or vascular symptoms from local mass effect"],"prognosis":"Simple lipomas are benign with an excellent prognosis and rarely recur after complete marginal excision. The principal clinical task is to identify the minority of fatty masses that are in fact atypical lipomatous tumours, since these carry a risk of local recurrence and, in certain sites, dedifferentiation.","pitfalls":["Assuming any fatty lump is a simple lipoma and shelling out a deep mass that proves to be an atypical lipomatous tumour","Failing to image deep or large lesions before intervention","Overlooking multiple lipomas as a clue to an underlying syndrome","Mistaking myxoid or spindle cell change for malignancy, or vice versa, without molecular confirmation"],"management":["Reassure and observe typical small, superficial, asymptomatic subcutaneous lipomas, which are benign and need no intervention","Offer marginal surgical excision for lesions that are symptomatic, enlarging, cosmetically troublesome or diagnostically uncertain","Characterise deep, large or radiologically atypical lesions with MRI using fat-saturated sequences, and obtain image-guided core biopsy with MDM2 amplification testing by FISH to exclude an atypical lipomatous tumour or well-differentiated liposarcoma","Refer to a specialist sarcoma service for wider excision when an atypical lipomatous tumour or liposarcoma is confirmed","Investigate for syndromic or familial multiple lipomatosis, such as Gardner syndrome, Bannayan-Riley-Ruvalcaba syndrome or Madelung disease, when lipomas are multiple","Confirm the diagnosis histologically on the excised specimen"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","Bone and Soft Tissue Sarcoma clinical practice guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"lipomatous_atrial_septal_hypertrophy":{"name":"Lipomatous hypertrophy of the atrial septum","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LHIS","lipomatous hypertrophy of the interatrial septum","lipomatous atrial septal hypertrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign, non-encapsulated deposition of mature and brown fat within the interatrial septum, distinct from a true encapsulated lipoma","Characteristically produces a dumbbell or bilobed thickening that spares the fossa ovalis, giving a waisted appearance","Commoner with increasing age and obesity and usually discovered incidentally on echocardiography or cross-sectional imaging","Most cases are asymptomatic, but very bulky deposits can encroach on the atrium or the region of the sinus node and provoke atrial arrhythmias","The brown-fat component is metabolically active and can be FDG-avid on positron emission tomography, a benign appearance that must not be misread as tumour","Cardiac MRI and CT confirm the fatty nature and the typical fossa-sparing shape, distinguishing it from a mass or thrombus","No treatment is needed for asymptomatic disease; intervention is reserved for the rare case causing significant obstruction or refractory arrhythmia"],"pathophysiology":"Lipomatous hypertrophy of the atrial septum is an overgrowth of adipose tissue, including metabolically active brown fat, within the interatrial septum. Because the fossa ovalis contains little fat, it is characteristically spared, so the deposit expands the muscular septum above and below it to create the classic dumbbell shape. The tissue is hyperplastic rather than neoplastic, which explains its benign course, but the brown-fat element takes up glucose and can appear avid on FDG imaging, mimicking malignancy. Extensive deposits may bulge into the right atrium, interfere with the adjacent conduction tissue, and predispose to supraventricular arrhythmias.","additionalDifferentials":["Cardiac lipoma (encapsulated)","Atrial myxoma or other cardiac tumour","Atrial septal aneurysm","Interatrial thrombus","Metastatic deposit to the atrial septum","Amyloid or other infiltrative septal thickening"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography showing echobright septal thickening that spares the fossa ovalis","Transoesophageal echocardiography for clearer characterisation of the septal mass","Cardiac MRI with fat-suppression sequences to confirm the adipose nature","Cardiac CT demonstrating fat density and the typical dumbbell morphology","Recognition of benign FDG uptake on positron emission tomography to avoid misdiagnosis as tumour"],"redFlags":["Very bulky deposits causing right atrial inflow obstruction or superior vena cava compromise","Refractory atrial arrhythmias attributable to the septal infiltration","Imaging features atypical for fat that raise concern for a true neoplasm","Rapid growth prompting reassessment of the benign diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and generally carries an excellent prognosis, with most patients remaining asymptomatic and requiring only reassurance and recognition of its characteristic imaging appearance.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting benign FDG avidity as malignancy and pursuing unnecessary biopsy or surgery","Confusing the deposit with an atrial tumour or thrombus","Failing to note the characteristic sparing of the fossa ovalis that confirms the diagnosis","Attributing unrelated symptoms to an incidental, asymptomatic septal deposit"],"management":["Reassurance with no active treatment for the typical asymptomatic incidental finding","Confirm the benign fatty nature with cardiac MRI using fat-suppression sequences or cardiac CT to exclude a true tumour or thrombus","Recognise benign FDG-avid brown fat on positron emission tomography to avoid misdiagnosis as malignancy and unnecessary biopsy or intervention","Treat supraventricular and atrial arrhythmias, when bulky deposits provoke them, with standard rate or rhythm control and antiarrhythmic therapy","Reserve surgical resection for the rare cases causing significant right atrial obstruction or refractory arrhythmia","Provide periodic imaging follow-up for very bulky deposits that encroach on the atrium or the region of the sinus node"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","Cardiovascular imaging references on cardiac masses"],"crossLinks":[]},"lipoprotein_glomerulopathy":{"name":"Lipoprotein Glomerulopathy","system":"Renal / Glomerular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LPG"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nephrology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare glomerular disease defined by intracapillary lipoprotein thrombi that dilate and occlude glomerular capillary loops.","Strongly associated with mutations in the apolipoprotein E gene, several of which are named after the region where they were first described.","Reported predominantly in individuals of East Asian ancestry but described worldwide, and it may present sporadically or in families.","Presents with proteinuria, frequently nephrotic, and progressive renal impairment, often without prominent systemic lipid deposition elsewhere.","Serum lipids show a type III hyperlipoproteinaemia-like pattern with elevated apolipoprotein E, though xanthomas and premature atheroma are usually absent.","Diagnosis rests on renal biopsy demonstrating pale, laminated lipoprotein thrombi that stain for lipid within markedly dilated capillary lumina.","Because it is defined by an apolipoprotein E abnormality, standard immunosuppression is generally ineffective and lipid-directed strategies are preferred.","The disease characteristically recurs in the transplanted kidney, reflecting its systemic apolipoprotein basis."],"pathophysiology":"Lipoprotein glomerulopathy is driven by abnormalities of apolipoprotein E, most often specific pathogenic variants that alter the protein's structure and its handling of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins. The mutant apolipoprotein E promotes the intraglomerular aggregation of lipoproteins into laminated thrombi that fill and grossly dilate the capillary loops. These thrombi are composed of layered lipid and apolipoprotein material rather than fibrin, and their accumulation obstructs glomerular blood flow, distorts the capillary architecture and progressively damages the filtration barrier, producing heavy proteinuria. The circulating lipid profile typically resembles type III hyperlipoproteinaemia with raised apolipoprotein E, yet, unlike familial dysbetalipoproteinaemia, systemic manifestations such as xanthomas and accelerated atherosclerosis are conspicuously absent, indicating that the injury is peculiarly targeted to the glomerulus. The condition can be inherited, often in an autosomal dominant pattern with incomplete penetrance, or arise sporadically, and because the defect resides in a circulating apolipoprotein the disease reliably recurs after transplantation of a normal kidney into an affected recipient.","additionalDifferentials":["Type III hyperlipoproteinaemia (familial dysbetalipoproteinaemia) with lipid-related renal changes","Thrombotic microangiopathy with capillary thrombi","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Fibrillary or immunotactoid glomerulopathy","Lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase deficiency affecting the glomerulus","Cryoglobulinaemic glomerulonephritis with intracapillary thrombi","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis presenting with nephrotic proteinuria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal biopsy with light microscopy and lipid stains (such as oil red O on frozen tissue) demonstrating laminated intracapillary lipoprotein thrombi","Electron microscopy showing the layered lipid vacuoles within dilated capillary lumina","Serum lipid profile and lipoprotein electrophoresis","Apolipoprotein E genotyping and serum apolipoprotein E level","Quantification of proteinuria and monitoring of renal function","Family screening given the hereditary nature","Exclusion of secondary causes of nephrotic syndrome"],"redFlags":["Nephrotic-range proteinuria with progressive oedema and hypoalbuminaemia","Steady decline in renal function towards kidney failure","Recurrence of proteinuria and thrombi in a kidney allograft","Refractoriness to conventional immunosuppression","Uncontrolled hyperlipidaemia complicating the clinical picture"],"prognosis":"Without effective lipid-directed treatment the disease tends to progress to kidney failure over years, and it frequently recurs in transplanted kidneys. Some patients improve with intensive lipid-lowering approaches, so prognosis is variable.","pitfalls":["Misreading the intracapillary thrombi as fibrin and diagnosing a thrombotic microangiopathy","Expecting xanthomas or premature atherosclerosis, whose absence can wrongly lower suspicion","Relying on immunosuppression, which does not address the apolipoprotein defect","Failing to pursue apolipoprotein E genotyping and family screening","Not anticipating post-transplant recurrence when counselling patients"],"management":["Intensive lipid-lowering therapy is the mainstay, with fibrates and statins to lower the triglyceride-rich lipoproteins that form the intracapillary thrombi, an approach that can reduce proteinuria and stabilise renal function","Reduce proteinuria and control blood pressure with renin-angiotensin system blockade using ACE inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers","Consider lipoprotein apheresis as an additional lipid-directed measure in refractory disease","Monitor proteinuria and renal function regularly and manage progressive chronic kidney disease with standard measures","Base the diagnosis on renal biopsy and maintain ongoing nephrology follow-up","Provide genetic counselling and family screening given the underlying apolipoprotein E variants","Counsel on the high risk of recurrence in a kidney transplant, continue lipid control after transplantation, and escalate to renal replacement therapy as kidney failure approaches"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (standard reference)","Heptinstall's Pathology of the Kidney (standard reference)","Brenner and Rector's The Kidney (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"liposarcoma":{"name":"Liposarcoma (WDLPS/DDLPS/myxoid/pleomorphic)","system":"Oncology / Sarcoma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["well-differentiated liposarcoma","WDLPS","atypical lipomatous tumour","dedifferentiated liposarcoma","DDLPS","myxoid liposarcoma","pleomorphic liposarcoma","MDM2-amplified sarcoma","FUS-DDIT3 fusion sarcoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["One of the commonest adult soft-tissue sarcomas, comprising biologically distinct subtypes with very different behaviour","Well-differentiated and dedifferentiated liposarcomas characteristically arise in the retroperitoneum and deep extremities and share amplification of the 12q region with MDM2 and CDK4 overexpression","Well-differentiated liposarcoma (atypical lipomatous tumour in the limbs) does not metastasise but recurs locally, whereas dedifferentiation confers metastatic potential","Myxoid liposarcoma affects younger adults, favours the deep soft tissue of the extremities, and is defined by a FUS-DDIT3 (or rarely EWSR1-DDIT3) fusion","Myxoid liposarcoma has a distinctive tendency to metastasise to unusual soft-tissue and bony sites rather than lung, so staging should look beyond the chest","Pleomorphic liposarcoma is a rare, high-grade, genetically complex tumour with aggressive behaviour","MDM2 amplification testing by FISH reliably separates well-differentiated/dedifferentiated liposarcoma from benign lipomatous lesions","Wide surgical excision is the cornerstone of treatment, with radiotherapy and chemotherapy used selectively according to subtype and risk"],"pathophysiology":"Liposarcoma encompasses several distinct entities. Well-differentiated and dedifferentiated liposarcomas are driven by supernumerary ring or giant marker chromosomes containing amplified 12q13-15 material, leading to overexpression of MDM2 and CDK4 that inactivates p53-mediated control and drives the cell cycle; dedifferentiation represents progression to a non-lipogenic high-grade component. Myxoid liposarcoma is a translocation-associated tumour defined by a FUS-DDIT3 fusion gene that produces an aberrant transcription factor impairing adipocytic differentiation. Pleomorphic liposarcoma, by contrast, has a complex, unstable karyotype without a defining recurrent alteration, explaining its high-grade, aggressive phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign lipoma and lipoma variants","Other retroperitoneal sarcomas (e.g. leiomyosarcoma)","Myxofibrosarcoma and other myxoid soft-tissue tumours","Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma","Retroperitoneal fibrosis or benign fatty proliferation","Metastatic carcinoma within retroperitoneal fat"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT or MRI of the primary site to characterise fatty and non-fatty components and define resectability","Chest CT for pulmonary metastases in higher-grade subtypes","Whole-body and spinal MRI for myxoid liposarcoma given its atypical metastatic pattern","Core biopsy targeting non-fatty or enhancing areas to capture any dedifferentiated component","MDM2 (and CDK4) amplification testing by FISH or immunohistochemistry","Molecular testing for DDIT3 rearrangement when myxoid liposarcoma is suspected"],"redFlags":["A large, deep, or retroperitoneal fatty mass with non-fatty enhancing areas suggesting dedifferentiation","Rapid enlargement or new pain in a previously indolent lipomatous lesion","Neurological, bony, or soft-tissue symptoms suggesting atypical metastases from myxoid liposarcoma","Recurrent retroperitoneal disease compromising adjacent organs"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies markedly by subtype, grade, size, and site: well-differentiated tumours have an excellent survival but recur locally, retroperitoneal dedifferentiated disease carries a high recurrence risk, myxoid tumours have an intermediate course dependent on round-cell content, and pleomorphic liposarcoma has the poorest outlook.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a well-differentiated liposarcoma for a benign lipoma without MDM2 testing","Sampling only the fatty component and missing a coexisting dedifferentiated area on biopsy","Staging myxoid liposarcoma with chest imaging alone and overlooking extrapulmonary metastases","Underestimating the local recurrence risk of retroperitoneal disease and the need for meticulous margins","Applying a single management approach across the biologically diverse subtypes"],"management":["Wide en-bloc surgical resection with clear margins is the mainstay for localised disease, tailored to subtype and site (retroperitoneal versus extremity)","Neoadjuvant or adjuvant radiotherapy to improve local control, particularly for extremity and higher-grade tumours","Anthracycline-based chemotherapy for dedifferentiated, pleomorphic or advanced disease, with trabectedin especially active in myxoid liposarcoma","Explore MDM2/CDK4-directed therapies within clinical trials given the characteristic 12q amplification in well-differentiated and dedifferentiated disease","Long-term cross-sectional imaging surveillance for local recurrence, adding whole-body and spinal MRI in the myxoid subtype for its atypical soft-tissue and osseous metastases","Manage within a specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team with expert histopathology and MDM2/DDIT3 molecular confirmation","Palliative care and symptom control for unresectable or progressive disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"lisfranc_injury":{"name":"Lisfranc injury (tarsometatarsal)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tarsometatarsal fracture-dislocation","Lisfranc fracture-dislocation","midfoot Lisfranc injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An injury to the tarsometatarsal (Lisfranc) joint complex spanning a spectrum from a subtle ligamentous sprain to a frank fracture-dislocation","The Lisfranc ligament runs from the medial cuneiform to the base of the second metatarsal, whose recessed base acts as the keystone of the midfoot","A small avulsion fragment in the space between the first and second metatarsal bases (the fleck sign) is highly suggestive of the injury","Weight-bearing or stress radiographs reveal instability that non-weight-bearing films miss, with widening between the first and second metatarsal bases or the medial and middle cuneiforms","Mechanisms include a direct crush and an indirect axial load on a plantarflexed, rotated foot, including low-energy sporting injuries","Plantar ecchymosis of the midfoot is a useful clinical clue","Unstable injuries require anatomical reduction with fixation or primary arthrodesis; missed injuries lead to chronic pain and midfoot arthritis"],"pathophysiology":"The tarsometatarsal joints are stabilised by their bony architecture, particularly the recessed base of the second metatarsal locked between the cuneiforms, and by strong plantar and interosseous ligaments including the Lisfranc ligament. An axial or twisting force on a plantarflexed foot, or a direct crush, disrupts these restraints and allows the metatarsal bases to displace, usually dorsally and laterally. Even subtle diastasis reflects loss of the midfoot's stabilising arch, and if left unreduced the resulting incongruity and instability progress to painful post-traumatic arthritis and arch collapse.","additionalDifferentials":["Midfoot sprain without instability","Metatarsal base fractures including a Jones fracture","Cuboid or cuneiform fracture","Navicular fracture","Ankle sprain","Neuropathic (Charcot) midfoot","Foot compartment syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Weight-bearing anteroposterior, lateral and oblique foot radiographs with bilateral comparison","Inspection for the fleck sign and for diastasis at the first-second metatarsal interval","Stress radiographs under anaesthesia when standing views are not tolerated","CT to define fracture detail and subtle malalignment","MRI to assess integrity of the Lisfranc ligament in occult ligamentous injury"],"redFlags":["Foot compartment syndrome with tense swelling and disproportionate pain","Plantar ecchymosis accompanying a midfoot injury","Vascular compromise of the foot","Instability missed on non-weight-bearing films"],"prognosis":"Anatomical reduction with stable fixation offers the best outcome; non-anatomical reduction or missed instability commonly results in post-traumatic midfoot arthritis, deformity and chronic pain.","pitfalls":["Relying on non-weight-bearing films and missing subtle diastasis","Dismissing significant midfoot pain as a simple sprain","Overlooking evolving foot compartment syndrome","Failing to recognise the significance of the fleck sign"],"management":["Manage non-displaced, stable ligamentous injuries with non-weight-bearing immobilisation and a protected, graded return to weight-bearing","Perform anatomical open reduction with rigid internal fixation (transarticular screws or dorsal bridge plating) for any displacement or instability","Consider primary tarsometatarsal arthrodesis for severe comminution or purely ligamentous injuries","Provide analgesia, elevation and swelling control, remaining vigilant for compartment syndrome after high-energy crush injuries","Deliver structured physiotherapy with orthotic arch support and progressive loading after fixation","Monitor for post-traumatic midfoot arthritis and arch collapse, offering delayed arthrodesis if painful degeneration develops","Refer to orthopaedic trauma for accurate reduction, as missed instability leads to poor outcomes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle"],"crossLinks":[]},"lissencephaly":{"name":"Lissencephaly","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["agyria-pachygyria spectrum","smooth brain","classic (type I) lissencephaly","LIS1 (PAFAH1B1)","DCX (doublecortin)","Miller-Dieker syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A malformation of cortical development due to arrested neuronal migration, producing a smooth brain surface with absent (agyria) or reduced and broadened (pachygyria) gyri","MRI shows a thickened, poorly organised cortex, a smooth grey-white junction, and often a characteristic figure-of-eight or hourglass appearance in severe classic forms","Classic type I lissencephaly is commonly caused by LIS1 (PAFAH1B1) deletions or variants, giving a posterior-predominant (posterior worse than anterior) gradient of malformation","DCX variants are X-linked and cause anterior-predominant lissencephaly in hemizygous males and subcortical band heterotopia (double cortex) in heterozygous females","Miller-Dieker syndrome is a contiguous gene deletion at 17p13.3 involving LIS1, combining severe lissencephaly with a distinctive facial appearance","Children typically present with early hypotonia, feeding difficulty, profound developmental delay, and drug-resistant epilepsy including infantile spasms","Diagnosis relies on the MRI malformation pattern and gradient combined with targeted or panel-based molecular genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Lissencephaly results from failure of the tangential and radial migration of neurons from the germinal zones to the developing cortical plate during the first half of gestation. Variants in genes that regulate the microtubule cytoskeleton and motor-driven nucleokinesis, most notably LIS1 and DCX, impair neuronal movement so that neurons arrest before reaching their proper cortical layers. The cortex becomes abnormally thick and disorganised with few or no gyri, and the anteroposterior gradient of severity reflects the particular gene involved. Disrupted cortical architecture underlies the profound epilepsy and neurodevelopmental impairment.","additionalDifferentials":["Cobblestone (type II) lissencephaly of the dystroglycanopathies such as Walker-Warburg syndrome","Polymicrogyria","Subcortical band heterotopia and other heterotopias","Microcephaly with simplified gyral pattern","Tubulinopathies from TUBA1A and related genes","Congenital infection causing a migration-like disturbance"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to define the gyral pattern, cortical thickness, and anteroposterior severity gradient","Molecular genetic testing including LIS1 and DCX analysis and a cortical malformation panel","Chromosomal microarray to detect 17p13.3 deletion in suspected Miller-Dieker syndrome","Electroencephalography to characterise the epilepsy, including hypsarrhythmia","Developmental and feeding assessment","Genetic counselling and, where relevant, testing of parents for mosaicism or carrier status"],"redFlags":["Drug-resistant epilepsy including infantile spasms causing developmental regression","Recurrent aspiration and failure to thrive from bulbar dysfunction","Progressive respiratory compromise in severe forms","Facial dysmorphism suggesting Miller-Dieker syndrome that warrants microarray testing"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally poor in severe classic lissencephaly, with profound intellectual disability, refractory epilepsy, and often shortened survival due to respiratory and feeding complications. Milder pachygyria and band heterotopia can be associated with better cognitive and seizure outcomes.","pitfalls":["Confusing classic lissencephaly with cobblestone lissencephaly, which has different genetics and eye and muscle involvement","Overlooking subcortical band heterotopia in a mother of an affected child, missing X-linked DCX inheritance","Failing to arrange microarray testing when facial features suggest Miller-Dieker syndrome","Underestimating the anteroposterior gradient as a clue to the causative gene"],"management":["Control the drug-resistant epilepsy with anti-seizure medication, using vigabatrin or hormonal/corticosteroid therapy for infantile spasms","Provide multidisciplinary neurodisability care with physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy for hypotonia and developmental impairment","Support safe feeding and nutrition, with gastrostomy placement for dysphagia and aspiration risk","Manage respiratory complications, including secretion clearance and treatment of recurrent aspiration and chest infection","Consider a ketogenic diet or epilepsy-surgery evaluation for refractory seizures where appropriate","Offer genetic counselling and testing (LIS1, DCX, chromosomal microarray for 17p13.3), including parental mosaicism and carrier assessment for recurrence risk","Undertake advance care planning and paediatric palliative care given the poor prognosis in severe forms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","GeneReviews: LIS1-Associated and DCX-Associated Lissencephaly"],"crossLinks":[]},"listeriosis":{"name":"Listeriosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Listeria monocytogenes infection"],"chapter":156,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1227-1230","clinicalPearls":["Listeriosis is acquired almost always via foodborne transmission; implicated foods are refrigerated ready-to-eat items (deli/delicatessen meats, hot dogs), soft cheeses made with raw/unpasteurised milk, unpasteurised dairy, raw produce (packaged salad, sprouts, enoki mushrooms, cantaloupe, caramel apples, frozen vegetables) and pate/meat spreads (p.1227-1228).","Major risk groups for invasive disease are pregnant women and their neonates, older adults (risk rises with each decade over age 59), and immunocompromised persons (impaired cellular immunity from haematologic malignancy, transplant, HIV, glucocorticoids/immunosuppressants, or TNF-alpha inhibitors) (p.1227-1228).","Bacteraemia without a focus is the most common manifestation of invasive listeriosis; presents with fever, chills, myalgias and arthralgias sometimes preceded by nausea/diarrhoea (p.1228).","Neurolisteriosis is the second most common invasive manifestation; in a French cohort 84% of neurolisteriosis cases presented as meningoencephalitis (meningitis plus altered mental status, seizures, or focal findings) (p.1228).","Compared with other bacterial meningitides, CSF pleocytosis and neutrophil predominance are typically LESS pronounced; ~60-75% have CSF WBC <1000/uL and only ~30-40% have low CSF glucose, so normal-ish CSF does not exclude Listeria (p.1228).","Empiric bacterial-meningitis cover (ceftriaxone + vancomycin) does NOT cover Listeria; add ampicillin for Listeria coverage in patients >50 years old or with comorbid/immunocompromising disease (Chap.143, p.140372 / p.1122).","Rhombencephalitis (brainstem/cerebellar encephalitis) disproportionately affects otherwise healthy older adults, classically biphasic (fever and headache, then asymmetric cranial nerve palsies, ataxia, tremor, hemiparesis or hemisensory deficits); nearly half develop respiratory failure (p.1228).","MRI is superior to CT for diagnosis of neurolisteriosis, including rhombencephalitis (p.1228).","Diagnosis is by isolation from a normally sterile site (blood, CSF, amniotic fluid, placental or fetal tissue); blood cultures are positive only about half the time in pregnant women, and CSF multiplex PCR panels and MALDI-TOF aid rapid identification (p.1229).","Pregnant women usually have only mild flulike illness and rarely develop neurolisteriosis or death, yet transmit infection transplacentally to the fetus with high fetal morbidity; prepartum antibiotics started >=1 day before delivery improve the chance of a healthy infant (p.1228-1229).","In invasive disease virtually all patients are hospitalised and 18% die; consider listeriosis acquired in hospital and investigate hospital-provided food when a hospitalised (especially immunocompromised) patient develops new symptoms (p.1227-1228).","TMP-SMX given as Pneumocystis jirovecii prophylaxis (e.g. in HIV or transplant recipients) also helps prevent listeriosis (p.1229)."],"pathophysiology":"L. monocytogenes is a ubiquitous, nonsporulating, facultatively anaerobic gram-positive rod (showing small zones of beta-haemolysis and characteristic tumbling motility) that lives in soil and decaying vegetation and can grow at refrigerator temperatures as low as 4 C and tolerate low-pH/high-salt environments. Infection follows ingestion of contaminated food; the infectious dose is likely very low in the severely immunocompromised, and raised gastric pH (e.g. from proton-pump inhibitors) probably promotes gut survival. After transcytosis across the intestinal epithelium the organism spreads via mesenteric lymph nodes and bloodstream to its target organs (liver and spleen) and can cross the blood-brain barrier and the placenta. Virulence factors include internalins (InlA, InlB) for invasion of nonphagocytic cells, listeriolysin O and phospholipases for escape from the phagocytic vacuole, and ActA for actin-based cell-to-cell spread that evades antibodies and complement. Iron promotes growth, explaining the association of listeriosis with iron-overload states such as haemochromatosis. Host defence is predominantly cell-mediated (interferon-gamma, TNF-alpha, and CD8+ T cells), accounting for the predilection for those with impaired cellular immunity. (p.1227)","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Other causes of community-acquired bacterial meningitis (S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, H. influenzae, group B streptococcus) (p.1228, Chap.143)","Fungal or tuberculous meningitis (low CSF glucose with mononuclear pleocytosis can mimic Listeria) (p.1228, Chap.143)","Brain abscess or other space-occupying CNS infection (Listeria abscess) (p.1228)","Other bacteraemic/septic illnesses presenting with fever without a focus (p.1228)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary or metastatic CNS malignancy (Listeria brain abscess may be misdiagnosed as tumour) (p.1228)","Neoplasia / haematologic malignancy presenting with fever (also a risk factor and prognostic comorbidity) (p.1228)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures (note positive only ~half the time in pregnancy) (p.1229)","CSF culture and Gram stain (may show gram-positive rods, but often no organisms or misleading gram-positive cocci/diplococci/diphtheroids) (p.1228-1229)","Culture of normally sterile sites: amniotic fluid, placental tissue, fetal tissue (p.1229)","Multiplex PCR panel on CSF (includes L. monocytogenes as a target) (p.1229)","MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry for rapid isolate identification (p.1229)","MRI brain (superior to CT for neurolisteriosis and rhombencephalitis) (p.1228)","Selective enrichment medium (PALCAM Listeria Selective Agar or Oxford Agar) for non-sterile specimens / outbreak work-up (p.1229)","Whole-genome sequencing for outbreak investigation (p.1229)"],"redFlags":["Signs of meningitis with altered mental status, seizures or focal neurologic deficits (meningoencephalitis) (p.1228)","Brainstem/cerebellar signs - asymmetric cranial nerve palsies, ataxia, tremor, hemiparesis, hemisensory deficits (rhombencephalitis), with respiratory failure in nearly half (p.1228)","Fever or signs of fetal distress in a pregnant woman (meconium-stained amniotic fluid, abnormal fetal heart rate) (p.1229)","Multiorgan failure and monocytopenia (<200 cells/uL) - markers associated with death (p.1228)"],"prognosis":"Invasive listeriosis carries substantial mortality: 18% of reported cases die and virtually all are hospitalised. In a large French (MONALISA) cohort the 3-month mortality was 46% for bacteraemia and 30% for neurolisteriosis; death was associated with multiorgan failure, neoplasia, worsening of preexisting comorbidities, monocytopenia (<200 cells/uL), older age and female sex, and (for neurolisteriosis) a positive blood culture and adjunctive dexamethasone use. Nearly half of neurolisteriosis survivors have long-term neurologic impairment. In pregnancy-associated disease, fetal/neonatal outcomes are poor: in a cohort of 107 pregnancies 24% ended in fetal loss, 45% in premature birth and only ~5% had a normal delivery and postpartum course; overall 3% of 189 live-born infants died. (p.1228-1229)","pitfalls":["Cephalosporins are NOT effective against L. monocytogenes; standard empiric meningitis cover (ceftriaxone +/- vancomycin) misses Listeria, so ampicillin must be added empirically in patients >50 years or with comorbid/immunocompromising disease (p.1229, Chap.143 p.140372).","Febrile gastroenteritis form is rarely diagnosed because Listeria is not detected by routine stool cultures or rapid stool tests, and stool culture is NOT indicated in evaluating invasive listeriosis (p.1227, p.1229).","Gram-variable appearance as cocci, diplococci or diphtheroids can obscure the diagnosis; Listeria must be distinguished from other gram-positive rods, especially diphtheroids (p.1227, p.1229).","Relatively bland CSF (WBC often <1000/uL, frequently no organisms on Gram stain, only ~30-40% with low glucose) can falsely reassure - do not exclude Listeria meningitis on CSF profile alone (p.1228).","Brain abscess due to Listeria may be misdiagnosed as primary or metastatic malignancy; CSF can be normal when abscess occurs without concurrent meningitis (p.1228).","Adjunctive dexamethasone is associated with reduced survival in neurolisteriosis and should be avoided; it is also not recommended for established Listeria meningitis (p.1229, Chap.143).","Treatment failures have been reported with meropenem despite in vitro susceptibility, and TMP-SMX resistance occurs - perform susceptibility testing if TMP-SMX is considered (p.1229).","Testing/treatment is NOT indicated for an asymptomatic person who ate a recalled product, even if they have risk factors (p.1229)."],"management":["Give intravenous ampicillin or amoxicillin as first-line therapy, adding an aminoglycoside such as gentamicin for synergy in severe invasive disease","Use co-trimoxazole as the principal alternative in penicillin-allergic patients","Treat neurolisteriosis, endocarditis and immunocompromised patients with prolonged antibiotic courses","Add empirical ampicillin to standard meningitis cover in older or immunocompromised patients, since ceftriaxone plus vancomycin does not treat Listeria","Provide supportive care with fluid resuscitation and neurocritical care for meningoencephalitis and rhombencephalitis","Treat pregnant women urgently to prevent foetal loss and neonatal infection, with paediatric assessment of the neonate","Notify public health, support source-tracing of contaminated food, and give dietary avoidance advice to high-risk groups"],"severityClassification":"No formal severity score. Harrison frames the clinical spectrum from non-invasive self-limited febrile gastroenteritis in healthy persons to invasive listeriosis (bacteraemia without a focus, neurolisteriosis [meningoencephalitis, cerebritis, focal abscess, rhombencephalitis], focal infections, and pregnancy-associated/neonatal disease [granulomatosis infantiseptica, early-onset sepsis, late-onset meningitis]). Severity is judged clinically by syndrome, with prognostic markers including multiorgan failure and monocytopenia. (p.1227-1229)","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Chapter 156: Listeria monocytogenes Infections, pp.1227-1230","Charlier C et al. Clinical features and prognostic factors of listeriosis: The MONALISA National Prospective Cohort Study. Lancet Infect Dis 17:510, 2017","Charlier C et al. Neonatal listeriosis presentation and outcome: A prospective study of 189 cases. Clin Infect Dis 74:8, 2022","Koopmans MM et al. Human listeriosis. Clin Microbiol Rev 36:e0006019, 2023","Thomas J et al. Outbreak of listeriosis in South Africa associated with processed meat. N Engl J Med 382:632, 2020"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","ENCEPHALITIS"]},"lithium_nephropathy":{"name":"Lithium-associated nephropathy","system":"Renal / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lithium nephropathy","lithium-induced nephrotoxicity","lithium-associated chronic tubulointerstitial nephropathy","nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (lithium)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Brenner and Rector's The Kidney","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Long-term lithium therapy causes a chronic tubulointerstitial nephropathy with slowly progressive renal impairment","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is the earliest and commonest renal effect, with polyuria, polydipsia and impaired urinary concentration","Biopsy typically shows tubular atrophy, interstitial fibrosis and characteristic distal tubular and collecting-duct microcysts","Lithium enters collecting-duct principal cells via the epithelial sodium channel and interferes with aquaporin-2 water channels, causing resistance to antidiuretic hormone","Nephrotoxicity relates to cumulative dose and duration, so the decision to continue lithium must balance renal risk against psychiatric benefit","Distinguish chronic nephropathy from acute lithium toxicity, which can cause acute kidney injury and neurotoxicity, sometimes requiring dialysis","Management centres on drug withdrawal and supportive care where feasible, careful monitoring, and adequate hydration, with a specialist mental-health discussion before stopping"],"pathophysiology":"Lithium is freely filtered and reabsorbed in the proximal tubule and enters collecting-duct principal cells through the epithelial sodium channel. Within these cells it disrupts signalling and downregulates aquaporin-2 water channels, impairing the antidiuretic response and producing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. Chronic exposure injures the distal nephron and collecting ducts, driving tubular atrophy, interstitial fibrosis and the formation of distal tubular and collecting-duct microcysts, which correlate with progressive loss of glomerular filtration. Some patients also develop a distal renal tubular acidification defect and, less commonly, proteinuria. The cumulative burden of exposure determines the degree of irreversible chronic damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Other chronic tubulointerstitial nephropathies (analgesic, aristolochic acid)","Central diabetes insipidus","Primary polydipsia","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","Obstructive uropathy with polyuria","Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (given microcysts)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum lithium level and review of cumulative exposure and duration","Renal function trend and estimated glomerular filtration rate","Urine osmolality with a water-deprivation and desmopressin assessment for nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Urinalysis and quantification of proteinuria","Renal ultrasound or MRI, which may show microcysts","Renal biopsy in selected cases to confirm chronic tubulointerstitial disease"],"redFlags":["Acute lithium toxicity with neurological features and acute kidney injury","Severe hypernatraemia from uncontrolled nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Rapidly declining renal function suggesting an additional insult","Volume depletion precipitating lithium accumulation and toxicity"],"prognosis":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and early functional changes are often partly reversible if lithium is stopped, but established interstitial fibrosis and reduced glomerular filtration may be irreversible and can progress even after discontinuation, occasionally to end-stage kidney disease. Careful monitoring and shared decision-making about continuing therapy improve long-term renal outcomes.","pitfalls":["Stopping lithium abruptly without psychiatric input, risking relapse of mood disorder","Attributing polyuria to primary polydipsia rather than nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Allowing volume depletion or NSAID use that precipitates lithium toxicity","Assuming renal impairment will reverse fully once the drug is withdrawn","Confusing lithium microcysts with polycystic kidney disease on imaging"],"management":["Review the ongoing need for lithium with psychiatry, weighing renal risk against relapse risk, and consider switching to an alternative mood stabiliser","Use the lowest effective lithium exposure and avoid toxic peaks if the drug is continued","Treat lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus with amiloride, which blocks principal-cell uptake via the epithelial sodium channel, ensuring free access to water","Avoid nephrotoxic and interacting drugs (NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, thiazides) and maintain hydration, especially during intercurrent illness","Monitor renal function, lithium levels and urinary concentrating ability, and manage any distal renal tubular acidification defect","Apply general chronic kidney disease measures including blood-pressure control and cardiovascular risk reduction","Refer to nephrology, planning renal replacement therapy if disease progresses towards end-stage kidney failure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Brenner and Rector's The Kidney"],"crossLinks":[]},"lithium_toxicity":{"name":"Lithium toxicity","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lithium poisoning","lithium overdose","lithium intoxication"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lithium has a narrow therapeutic window, and toxicity is usually neurological - tremor, hyperreflexia, ataxia, dysarthria, agitation, confusion, and in severe cases seizures and coma","Distinguish acute, acute-on-chronic, and chronic toxicity, as chronic toxicity produces serious neurological features at lower serum levels and is more likely to cause lasting harm","Serum levels correlate poorly with severity in chronic toxicity because lithium distributes slowly into tissues including the central nervous system","Common precipitants of chronic toxicity include dehydration, renal impairment, and interacting drugs such as thiazide diuretics, ACE inhibitors or ARBs, and NSAIDs","There is no antidote; management centres on generous isotonic fluid resuscitation to restore volume and promote renal elimination","Activated charcoal does not bind lithium, but whole-bowel irrigation may help after ingestion of sustained-release preparations","Haemodialysis is the definitive means of removal and is indicated for severe neurological features, renal failure, or very high levels, with rebound rises possible afterwards requiring repeat treatment","Chronic toxicity can leave a permanent cerebellar and neurological deficit (the syndrome of irreversible lithium-effectuated neurotoxicity)"],"pathophysiology":"Lithium is a monovalent cation handled by the kidney similarly to sodium, so states that reduce renal perfusion or increase sodium reabsorption (volume depletion, diuretics, renal impairment) reduce its clearance and raise levels. It moves slowly across cell membranes, accumulating intracellularly and particularly in the central nervous system, which explains the delay between serum levels and clinical effect and the persistence of neurotoxicity. Intracellularly it interferes with neurotransmission and second-messenger systems. Lithium also impairs renal concentrating ability, causing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus that promotes dehydration and a vicious cycle of rising concentration.","additionalDifferentials":["Serotonin syndrome or neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Other causes of encephalopathy (metabolic, infective)","Cerebellar stroke or other structural lesion","Sepsis with delirium","Other drug intoxications causing tremor and confusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial serum lithium levels to track trend rather than a single value","Serum electrolytes, urea, and creatinine to assess renal function","ECG for T-wave changes, QT effects, and arrhythmia","Thyroid function, as chronic use affects the thyroid","Assessment for precipitants such as dehydration and interacting medications"],"redFlags":["Seizures, marked confusion, or reduced consciousness","Severe neurological signs at only modestly raised levels, suggesting chronic toxicity","Acute kidney injury impairing lithium clearance","Cardiac arrhythmia or significant ECG changes","Persistent or rebound rises in level after initial treatment"],"prognosis":"Acute overdose in a lithium-naive patient often does well with fluids and, if needed, dialysis, whereas chronic toxicity carries a higher risk of prolonged or permanent neurological (especially cerebellar) impairment; outcome depends on severity, renal function, and timeliness of dialysis.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single serum level and underestimating chronic toxicity with a modest number","Giving activated charcoal, which does not adsorb lithium","Overlooking interacting drugs and dehydration that precipitated the toxicity","Delaying haemodialysis in severe or chronic toxicity, risking permanent neurological damage"],"management":["Stop lithium immediately and remove ongoing sources, including sustained-release preparations","Give generous intravenous isotonic saline resuscitation to restore volume and enhance renal lithium clearance","Institute haemodialysis for severe neurotoxicity, markedly elevated or rising levels, or renal failure, anticipating rebound from tissue redistribution","Use whole-bowel irrigation after sustained-release ingestion, recognising that activated charcoal does not bind lithium","Correct precipitants and withdraw interacting drugs such as thiazides, ACE inhibitors, ARBs and NSAIDs","Provide supportive care with airway protection, seizure control and cardiac monitoring for conduction and QT abnormalities","Track serial lithium levels and neurological status, involving critical care and toxicology or nephrology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"little_league_elbow":{"name":"Little League elbow","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["medial epicondyle apophysitis","medial elbow overuse injury in throwers"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A spectrum of overuse injury to the medial elbow in skeletally immature throwing athletes, centred on the medial epicondyle apophysis","Repetitive valgus stress during the throwing motion causes traction injury at the medial epicondyle","Presents with medial elbow pain during and after throwing, sometimes with reduced throwing velocity and control","Severe cases can produce avulsion of the medial epicondyle apophysis","It is strongly linked to excessive pitching volume, and pitch-count and rest guidelines are central to prevention","Management is relative rest from throwing, mechanics correction and graded return, with surgery for significant displaced avulsion"],"pathophysiology":"Little League elbow results from repetitive valgus forces generated during overhead throwing, which place traction on the medial structures of the immature elbow, particularly the medial epicondyle apophysis where the flexor-pronator muscles and ulnar collateral ligament attach. The growth cartilage is weaker than the adjacent tendon and ligament, so repetitive stress causes apophysitis and, when overload is severe, apophyseal separation or avulsion. Lateral compressive forces can additionally injure the capitellum in the same throwing mechanism.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteochondritis dissecans of the capitellum","Ulnar collateral ligament injury","Ulnar neuritis","Medial epicondylitis (in adults)","Elbow fracture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of medial elbow tenderness and throwing history","Radiographs, including comparison views, for apophyseal widening or avulsion","MRI where soft-tissue or osteochondral injury is suspected","Assessment of throwing mechanics and workload","Evaluation of ulnar nerve function"],"redFlags":["Displaced medial epicondyle avulsion","Locking or mechanical symptoms suggesting osteochondritis dissecans with loose bodies","Ulnar nerve symptoms","Persistent pain despite rest"],"prognosis":"With early recognition and rest most young throwers recover fully, but continued overload risks apophyseal avulsion and longer-term elbow problems; adherence to workload limits is protective.","pitfalls":["Allowing continued throwing through pain","Missing capitellar osteochondritis dissecans as a coexisting lateral injury","Overlooking a displaced avulsion needing surgery","Not addressing pitch counts and throwing mechanics"],"management":["Institute relative rest with cessation of throwing until symptom-free, the mainstay of management","Provide analgesia and ice for the medial epicondyle apophysitis","Correct throwing mechanics and enforce pitch-count and rest guidelines to prevent recurrence","Deliver physiotherapy for flexor-pronator and shoulder-girdle strengthening before a graded, staged return to throwing","Undertake surgical fixation for significantly displaced medial epicondyle avulsion","Follow the apophysis radiographically and assess ulnar nerve function","Educate the athlete, family and coaches on workload limits and early reporting of pain"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric sports medicine references (standard reference)","Orthopaedic reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"livedo_reticularis":{"name":"livedo reticularis","system":"Skin and integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["livedo racemosa (pathological variant)","cutis marmorata"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A net-like, reticular violaceous mottling of the skin caused by altered flow within the dermal vascular network.","The physiological form (cutis marmorata) produces complete, symmetrical rings that reverse with warming and is common and benign in infants and young women.","Livedo racemosa, by contrast, shows broken, irregular, asymmetrical rings that persist on warming and signals an underlying vasculopathy.","Livedo racemosa is an important cutaneous marker of Sneddon syndrome (with cerebrovascular events) and of antiphospholipid syndrome.","Secondary causes include vasculitis such as polyarteritis nodosa, cholesterol embolisation, calciphylaxis, hyperviscosity, cryoglobulinaemia and certain drugs.","Reversibility with warming and the regularity of the pattern help separate the benign from the pathological forms.","When biopsy is needed it should be deep and taken from the pale centre of the network rather than the blue rim, where the culprit vessel is more likely to be sampled."],"pathophysiology":"The reticular pattern reflects the cone-shaped territories supplied by ascending dermal arterioles: deoxygenated blood pooling in the venous plexus at the periphery of each cone produces the bluish reticulate network. Anything that reduces inflow or increases stasis at the cone periphery accentuates the pattern, including vasospasm, luminal obstruction from thrombosis, emboli or hyperviscosity, and inflammatory vessel-wall disease. In the physiological form the change is purely vasomotor and reverses with warming, whereas in pathological livedo racemosa fixed vascular occlusion or wall damage produces a persistent, irregular pattern.","additionalDifferentials":["Cutis marmorata (physiological livedo)","Livedo racemosa secondary to systemic disease","Livedoid vasculopathy","Erythema ab igne","Cutis marmorata telangiectatica congenita","Reticulate erythema of connective tissue disease","Cholesterol embolisation syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antiphospholipid antibody testing (lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin and anti-beta-2-glycoprotein-I antibodies)","Autoimmune screen including antinuclear antibodies and ANCA","Cryoglobulins and complement levels","Deep skin biopsy taken from the centre of the pale area","Screening for an embolic source and a thrombophilia workup","Neuroimaging when Sneddon syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neurological symptoms such as stroke or transient ischaemic attack suggesting Sneddon syndrome","Skin ulceration or necrosis","Systemic features of vasculitis or embolic disease","A fixed, irregular racemosa pattern that does not resolve with warming"],"prognosis":"The physiological form is entirely benign, whereas the prognosis of pathological livedo depends on the underlying disorder, with thrombotic risk in antiphospholipid and Sneddon syndromes being of particular concern.","pitfalls":["Dismissing pathological livedo racemosa as benign cutis marmorata.","Taking a superficial or peripherally placed biopsy that misses the affected vessel.","Failing to test for antiphospholipid syndrome in a young patient presenting with livedo and stroke.","Attributing drug-induced or embolic livedo to a primary skin disorder."],"management":["Reassure and advise warmth with avoidance of cold for benign physiological cutis marmorata, which needs no specific treatment","Identify and treat the underlying cause, distinguishing benign livedo reticularis from pathological livedo racemosa","Give antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy where associated with antiphospholipid syndrome or Sneddon syndrome and thrombotic risk","Treat specific secondary causes, such as immunosuppression for polyarteritis nodosa or management of cholesterol embolisation, calciphylaxis and cryoglobulinaemia","Address modifiable vascular risk factors and withdraw any causative drugs","Investigate and monitor for cerebrovascular events when Sneddon syndrome is suspected","Refer to dermatology, rheumatology or neurology according to the underlying disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"livedoid_vasculopathy":{"name":"Livedoid vasculopathy","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["livedoid vasculitis","atrophie blanche","segmental hyalinizing vasculopathy","PURPLE (painful purpuric ulcers with reticular pattern of the lower extremities)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic thrombo-occlusive disorder of dermal vessels presenting with painful purpuric macules and recurrent ulcers of the lower legs, ankles, and feet","Healing leaves characteristic atrophie blanche: smooth, porcelain-white stellate scars ringed by telangiectasia and pigmentation","Pain is often disproportionate to the visible lesions and is a prominent, disabling feature","It is primarily a vasculopathy driven by intravascular thrombosis, not a true inflammatory vasculitis, so histology shows hyaline thrombi and fibrin with little primary vessel-wall inflammation","Frequently associated with thrombophilic and prothrombotic states, and screening for these is important","Disease is typically chronic with a seasonal (summer) flare pattern in many patients","Management is directed at reducing thrombosis and improving blood flow, using antithrombotic and other vasoactive approaches alongside wound care"],"pathophysiology":"Livedoid vasculopathy results from occlusion of dermal microvessels by fibrin thrombi rather than from primary inflammatory destruction of vessel walls. Hypercoagulable and prothrombotic tendencies promote intravascular thrombosis, causing localised ischaemia and infarction of the skin. The resulting tissue hypoxia produces the painful purpura and punched-out ulcers, and healing of these infarcts yields the sclerotic, avascular white scars of atrophie blanche surrounded by reactive telangiectasia. Because the process is occlusive, any inflammatory infiltrate is usually secondary, and identifying and treating underlying thrombophilia is central to control.","additionalDifferentials":["Cutaneous small-vessel (leukocytoclastic) vasculitis","Venous stasis ulceration and lipodermatosclerosis","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Cryoglobulinaemia","Polyarteritis nodosa (cutaneous)","Pyoderma gangrenosum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing hyaline fibrin thrombi with minimal vessel-wall inflammation","Thrombophilia screen (including antiphospholipid antibodies and relevant clotting factor abnormalities)","Autoimmune and connective-tissue disease serology","Cryoglobulins and, where indicated, hepatitis serology","Assessment of venous insufficiency with duplex studies","Wound swab if secondary infection of ulcers is suspected"],"redFlags":["Detection of an underlying thrombophilia or antiphospholipid syndrome requiring specialist management","Rapidly progressive or extensive ulceration with severe pain","Secondary infection of chronic ulcers","Features suggesting an alternative systemic vasculitis or connective-tissue disease"],"prognosis":"Livedoid vasculopathy is a chronic, relapsing condition with recurrent painful ulceration and progressive atrophie blanche scarring; outcomes improve when an underlying prothrombotic state is identified and treated, though many patients experience persistent disease.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling it as an inflammatory vasculitis and relying on immunosuppression rather than antithrombotic therapy","Failing to screen for thrombophilia and antiphospholipid syndrome","Confusing atrophie blanche scarring with simple venous stasis change","Underestimating the severity of pain and its impact on function"],"management":["Give antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy (aspirin, dipyridamole, pentoxifylline or anticoagulation) to counter the fibrin-thrombus occlusion, the therapeutic mainstay","Screen for and treat any underlying thrombophilic or prothrombotic state","Provide effective analgesia for the disproportionate, disabling pain, including neuropathic agents","Deliver wound care and compression for lower-leg ulcers, treating secondary infection","Use intravenous immunoglobulin for refractory or severe disease","Advise smoking cessation and reduction of thrombotic risk factors","Arrange dermatology and haematology follow-up for the chronic, relapsing course"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"liver_hemangioma":{"name":"Liver haemangioma","system":"Hepatobiliary surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hepatic haemangioma","cavernous haemangioma of the liver","hepatic cavernous haemangioma","liver hemangioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hepatic haemangioma is the commonest benign liver tumour and is usually an incidental finding on imaging in an asymptomatic patient","Most are small and require no treatment; large (giant) lesions may cause dull right upper quadrant discomfort, early satiety, or a mass effect","Contrast imaging shows a characteristic pattern of peripheral nodular enhancement with progressive centripetal fill-in on CT or MRI, which is typically diagnostic","MRI demonstrates a markedly hyperintense lesion on heavily T2-weighted sequences, helping to confirm the diagnosis non-invasively","Percutaneous biopsy is generally avoided because of the theoretical risk of bleeding and because imaging is usually diagnostic","Management is overwhelmingly conservative with reassurance and, for uncertain or enlarging lesions, surveillance; intervention is reserved for significant symptoms, diagnostic doubt, or complications","Kasabach-Merritt syndrome, a consumptive coagulopathy, is a rare association with very large lesions, chiefly in infants"],"pathophysiology":"A hepatic haemangioma is a benign vascular malformation composed of multiple dilated, blood-filled vascular channels lined by a single layer of endothelium and separated by thin fibrous septa. It is thought to be congenital in origin, enlarging by ectasia rather than by cellular proliferation, and its growth can be influenced by hormonal factors such as pregnancy and oestrogen exposure. Blood flow through the lesion is slow, which underlies the characteristic delayed centripetal enhancement on imaging. Very large lesions can trap and consume platelets and clotting factors, producing the Kasabach-Merritt consumptive coagulopathy, and rarely a superficial lesion may bleed after trauma.","additionalDifferentials":["Focal nodular hyperplasia","Hepatic adenoma","Hepatocellular carcinoma","Hypervascular liver metastasis","Simple hepatic cyst","Angiosarcoma (rare)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT showing peripheral nodular discontinuous enhancement with centripetal fill-in","MRI with heavily T2-weighted sequences for a characteristically bright lesion","Ultrasound (often a well-defined hyperechoic lesion) as an initial or incidental finding","Labelled red-cell scintigraphy in selected diagnostically uncertain cases","Coagulation screen and platelet count if a very large lesion raises concern for consumptive coagulopathy","Interval imaging surveillance for indeterminate or enlarging lesions"],"redFlags":["Spontaneous or traumatic rupture with intraperitoneal haemorrhage and haemodynamic instability (rare but life-threatening)","Consumptive coagulopathy (Kasabach-Merritt syndrome) with a very large lesion","Rapid enlargement or atypical imaging raising doubt about the benign diagnosis","Severe, progressive symptoms from a giant lesion causing mass effect"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; most haemangiomas remain stable, cause no symptoms, and never require treatment, with an extremely low risk of complications. Even large lesions usually behave benignly, and surgical resection of genuinely symptomatic or complicated lesions gives good long-term results.","pitfalls":["Performing percutaneous biopsy of a suspected haemangioma and risking haemorrhage","Misinterpreting the lesion as a metastasis or hepatocellular carcinoma and subjecting the patient to unnecessary intervention","Over-treating small asymptomatic lesions that only need reassurance","Attributing unrelated abdominal symptoms to a small incidental haemangioma","Overlooking coagulopathy in a patient with a very large lesion"],"management":["Reassure and give no treatment for typical small asymptomatic lesions, the usual approach","Arrange interval imaging surveillance for indeterminate or enlarging lesions","Reserve surgical resection or enucleation for genuinely symptomatic giant lesions, diagnostic uncertainty or complications","Counsel on oestrogen avoidance and around pregnancy, given the hormonal influence on growth","Avoid percutaneous biopsy because of bleeding risk and because imaging is usually diagnostic","Monitor coagulation and platelets in very large lesions for Kasabach-Merritt consumptive coagulopathy","Refer to hepatobiliary surgery for large, symptomatic or complicated tumours, with transarterial embolisation as an alternative in selected cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"liver_transplantation":{"name":"Liver Transplantation","system":"Gastrointestinal / Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Orthotopic liver transplantation","OLT","Hepatic allograft transplantation","Living-donor liver transplantation"],"chapter":356,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2718","clinicalPearls":["Orthotopic transplantation (native liver removed, donor organ placed in the same anatomic site) is the preferred and technically most advanced approach; 1-year survival has risen from roughly 30% in the 1970s to over 90% today, attributed more to better patient selection and timing than to technical or immunologic advances alone.","Optimal timing matters: the disease should be advanced enough that medical options are exhausted, yet the procedure performed early enough to allow success. Better pretransplant clinical status predicts higher posttransplant survival.","Allocation of deceased-donor organs is driven by the MELD score (bilirubin, albumin, sodium, creatinine, INR, plus a female adjustment), a continuous scale from 6 to 40 in which higher values indicate greater illness severity; it replaced the older Child-Turcotte-Pugh-based scheme and reduced waiting-list mortality.","Status 1 (highest priority) is reserved for acute liver failure and for early posttransplant catastrophes such as primary graft nonfunction and hepatic artery thrombosis, superseding the MELD score.","Liver recipients with MELD scores below 15 have higher posttransplant mortality than comparable patients who remain on the waiting list, so transplant at very low MELD offers limited benefit.","HLA tissue typing is NOT required for liver transplantation, and preformed cytotoxic HLA antibodies do not preclude the procedure; ABO compatibility and organ size are the key matching considerations.","Tacrolimus has supplanted cyclosporine as primary immunosuppression; it is 10-100 times more potent, but nephrotoxicity and neurotoxicity (tremor, seizures, hallucinations, psychosis, coma) are its principal adverse effects. Cyclosporine and tacrolimus should not be combined because of overlapping nephrotoxicity.","Both calcineurin inhibitors are metabolized by cytochrome P450 IIIA: enzyme inducers (phenytoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine, rifampin) lower drug levels, while inhibitors (azoles, macrolides, verapamil, diltiazem, ritonavir) raise them.","Sirolimus is avoided early posttransplant because it impairs wound healing and is associated with increased hepatic artery thrombosis in the first 30 days; its antiproliferative effect makes it attractive in recipients with prior or current malignancy such as HCC.","Infection timing follows a predictable pattern: conventional postoperative infections (pneumonia, wound, urinary, line, intraabdominal) dominate the first month, after which opportunistic pathogens (CMV, herpesviruses, Aspergillus, Candida, Cryptococcus, mycobacteria, Pneumocystis, Toxoplasma, Nocardia, Legionella, Listeria) predominate as immunosuppression takes its toll.","Long-term HBIg prophylaxis transformed transplantation for chronic hepatitis B; without prophylaxis hepatitis B almost always recurs, but indefinite/long-term HBIg (often combined with high-barrier oral antivirals such as entecavir or tenofovir) reduces reinfection and mortality, yielding outcomes indistinguishable from non-HBV indications.","Direct-acting antiviral (DAA) agents against HCV have markedly improved both pre- and posttransplant outcomes, allowing pretransplant clearance of viremia (sometimes delaying or obviating transplant) and high sustained virologic response rates in allograft HCV infection."],"pathophysiology":"End-stage liver disease produces portal hypertension, coagulopathy (prolonged prothrombin time, thrombocytopenia), and a hyperdynamic high-cardiac-output circulatory state; transplantation replaces the failing organ and rapidly reverses the hyperdynamic state. The graft is vulnerable to immunologic and ischemic injury. Acute cellular rejection arises 1-2 weeks postoperatively from a T-cell-mediated response, with histology showing a mixed portal infiltrate, bile duct injury, and endothelialitis. Chronic rejection produces progressive cholestasis, vascular lesions (intimal fibrosis, subintimal foam cells, fibrinoid necrosis), and ductopenia (vanishing bile duct syndrome). Calcineurin inhibitors act on the calcium-dependent T-cell signal transduction pathway to block lymphokine (IL-2/3/4, TNF-alpha) gene activation. Ischemic injury during harvesting underlies primary graft nonfunction; preservation in University of Wisconsin solution (rich in lactobionate and raffinose) extends tolerable cold-ischemic time. ABO-mismatched grafts (e.g., group O liver into a group A or B recipient) can cause transient passenger-lymphocyte autoimmune hemolytic anemia that resolves once recipient marrow repopulates the graft.","additionalDifferentials":["Posttransplant graft dysfunction: rejection vs biliary obstruction vs primary graft nonfunction vs vascular compromise (portal vein or hepatic artery thrombosis) vs drug hepatotoxicity vs recurrent primary disease vs viral hepatitis/CMV — these are clinically and biochemically difficult to distinguish, often requiring biliary imaging and percutaneous liver biopsy.","Recurrent primary biliary cholangitis vs chronic rejection (histologically nearly indistinguishable; a florid inflammatory bile duct lesion favors recurrent PBC but is not specific).","Recurrent autoimmune hepatitis or sclerosing cholangitis vs rejection or posttransplant bile duct injury."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MELD/MELD-Na/MELD-3.0 score calculation (bilirubin, albumin, sodium, creatinine, INR) for allocation and prognosis; PELD for children under 12.","Serum bilirubin and aminotransferases — most reliable, though nonspecific, indicators of allograft rejection.","Percutaneous liver biopsy and radiographic visualization of the biliary tree to differentiate causes of graft dysfunction.","Donor screening: hemodynamic stability, oxygenation, absence of infection/abdominal trauma/hepatic dysfunction; serologic testing for HBV, HCV, HIV (infected organs now used selectively with matching and informed consent).","Recipient cardiopulmonary evaluation (especially age >70) to exclude ischemic cardiac disease; assessment of pulmonary artery pressure and intrapulmonary shunting.","anti-HBs titer monitoring (threshold ~100 mIU/mL) to guide HBIg dosing in hepatitis B recipients."],"redFlags":["Acute liver failure with impending cerebral edema — a transplant emergency requiring rapid donor identification before life-threatening complications set in.","Fever, right upper quadrant pain, and falling bile pigment/volume with rising bilirubin and aminotransferases — suggest acute allograft rejection.","Fibrosing cholestatic hepatitis (marked hyperbilirubinemia and prolonged prothrombin time out of proportion to modest aminotransferase rises) in recurrent hepatitis B — rapidly progressive liver failure.","Postoperative intraperitoneal bleeding raising intraabdominal pressure and reducing renal blood flow — reversible with laparotomy to evacuate clot and ligate the bleeding site.","New opportunistic infection beyond the first postoperative month in an immunosuppressed host demands prompt recognition and treatment.","Severe portopulmonary hypertension (mean pulmonary artery pressure >35 mmHg) or severe hypoxemia from right-to-left intrapulmonary shunts (PO2 <50 mmHg) — relative contraindications signaling high operative risk."],"prognosis":"One-year survival is now about 85-90%, and 5-year survival exceeds 60%. Outcome correlates strongly with pretransplant status: patients still working or only partially disabled achieve roughly >85% 1-year survival; those needing continuous in-hospital care about 70%; those requiring ICU life support about 50%. High pretransplant disease severity (MELD >25) and high-risk features (malignancy, fulminant hepatitis, age >65, concurrent renal failure, respirator dependence, portal vein thrombosis, prior portacaval shunt or multiple right-upper-quadrant operations) reduce survival to about 60% at 1 year and 35% at 5 years. Survival after retransplantation for primary graft nonfunction is about 50%. Failures within the first 3 months are mainly technical, infectious, or hemorrhagic; later failures stem from infection, rejection, or recurrent disease (malignancy, viral hepatitis, return to alcohol use). Renal failure (18% at 5 years, 25% at 10 years) and cardiovascular disease are leading causes of late mortality.","pitfalls":["Treating acute rejection with pulse glucocorticoids in HCV-infected recipients risks triggering severe recurrent allograft hepatitis C (though DAAs now mitigate this concern).","Cyclosporine and tacrolimus must not be co-administered because of overlapping nephrotoxicity and because tacrolimus reduces cyclosporine clearance.","Prior extensive biliary/hepatobiliary surgery complicates transplantation and is a relative contraindication, so biliary diversion has been largely abandoned in sclerosing cholangitis candidates.","Cholangiocarcinoma recurs almost invariably; only highly selected stage I/II patients after neoadjuvant chemoradiation should be considered.","Eradicating HCV before transplant can lower the MELD score and paradoxically reduce allocation priority in patients who still need a graft for ongoing decompensation.","Advanced age alone is no longer an absolute contraindication, but in patients over 70 a thorough cardiac and comorbidity workup is mandatory.","Relying on short-term (2 months or less) HBIg, preoperative vaccination, or interferon alone is ineffective for preventing hepatitis B recurrence; long-term prophylaxis is required."],"management":["Maintain lifelong immunosuppression with a calcineurin inhibitor (tacrolimus or ciclosporin) backbone, usually with an antiproliferative such as mycophenolate and tapering corticosteroids","Treat acute cellular rejection with pulsed corticosteroids, escalating to antithymocyte globulin for steroid-resistant rejection","Provide infection prophylaxis against opportunistic organisms, including cover for Pneumocystis, cytomegalovirus and fungal disease","Prevent recurrent disease, using hepatitis B immunoglobulin with antiviral therapy for HBV and direct-acting antivirals for HCV","Surveil for vascular and biliary complications (hepatic artery thrombosis, biliary strictures) and investigate graft dysfunction with biochemistry, imaging and liver biopsy","Monitor long term for immunosuppression-related harms such as renal impairment, metabolic syndrome and malignancy, and manage cardiovascular risk","Prioritise listing by MELD-based allocation and manage throughout within a specialist transplant multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":"Allocation and prognosis use the Model for End-Stage Liver Disease (MELD) score, a continuous scale from 6 to 40 (values above 40 counted as 40) derived from serum bilirubin, albumin, sodium, creatinine, and INR with a female adjustment; higher scores indicate greater illness severity. Variants include MELD-Na (2016) and MELD-3.0 (2023). PELD is used for candidates under 12. Status 1 (acute liver failure, primary graft nonfunction, hepatic artery thrombosis) supersedes MELD. The older Child-Turcotte-Pugh score (encephalopathy, ascites, bilirubin, albumin, prothrombin time) has been replaced for allocation. MELD exception points are granted (e.g., for HCC) via the National Liver Review Board.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2718","Harrison 22e p.2719","Harrison 22e p.2720","Harrison 22e p.2721","Harrison 22e p.2722","Harrison 22e p.2723","Harrison 22e p.2724","Harrison 22e p.2725"],"crossLinks":[]},"lmna_cardiomyopathy":{"name":"LMNA cardiomyopathy","system":"Cardiovascular / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lamin A/C cardiomyopathy","LMNA-related dilated cardiomyopathy","laminopathy cardiomyopathy","LMNA dilated cardiomyopathy with conduction disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard cardiology and inherited cardiac conditions references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in LMNA, encoding the nuclear envelope proteins lamin A and C, and characterised by dilated cardiomyopathy with prominent conduction system disease","The typical sequence is early atrioventricular conduction disturbance and atrial arrhythmia, followed by ventricular arrhythmia and progressive left ventricular dysfunction","It carries a notably high risk of malignant ventricular arrhythmia and sudden death, which can precede or exceed the degree of pump failure","Because sudden death may occur even with only mildly reduced ejection fraction, defibrillator implantation is considered at a lower threshold than in many other cardiomyopathies","Skeletal muscle involvement (Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy or limb-girdle patterns) may coexist, reflecting the wider laminopathy spectrum","Recognised high-risk markers include non-sustained ventricular tachycardia, male sex, non-missense variants, and progressive reduction in ejection fraction","Family screening is essential given autosomal dominant inheritance and the arrhythmic risk in relatives"],"pathophysiology":"Lamins A and C form a meshwork underlying the inner nuclear membrane that provides structural support to the nucleus and regulates gene expression and mechanotransduction. Pathogenic LMNA variants weaken this nuclear lamina, rendering cardiomyocytes and their conduction-system counterparts vulnerable to mechanical stress and abnormal signalling. This leads to progressive fibrosis and cell loss, first affecting the atria and the specialised conduction tissue, causing conduction block and atrial arrhythmia, and later the ventricular myocardium, producing chamber dilatation, systolic dysfunction and a substrate for re-entrant ventricular arrhythmia. The disproportionate arrhythmic and conduction burden relative to the degree of ventricular impairment is the defining clinical hallmark.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy of other genetic cause (for example titin)","Cardiac sarcoidosis","Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy","Myocarditis with conduction disease","Progressive cardiac conduction defect (SCN5A)","Other muscular dystrophies with cardiac involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial 12-lead ECGs to detect evolving conduction disease and atrial arrhythmia","Ambulatory ECG monitoring for non-sustained ventricular tachycardia","Echocardiography to assess chamber size and systolic function","Cardiac MRI to quantify function and detect myocardial fibrosis","Genetic testing of LMNA with cascade family screening","Creatine kinase and neurological assessment where skeletal myopathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Non-sustained or sustained ventricular tachycardia","High-grade atrioventricular block or need for pacing at a young age","Progressive fall in left ventricular ejection fraction","Family history of sudden death or LMNA-confirmed disease"],"prognosis":"LMNA cardiomyopathy is among the more aggressive inherited cardiomyopathies, with high rates of malignant arrhythmia, progressive heart failure and sudden death; outcome is improved by early rhythm surveillance, defibrillator therapy in high-risk patients, and heart failure management, with transplantation for end-stage disease.","pitfalls":["Applying standard ejection-fraction-based defibrillator thresholds and underestimating arrhythmic risk","Attributing early conduction disease to benign causes and missing the underlying laminopathy","Overlooking coexisting skeletal muscle involvement","Failing to screen relatives who may harbour the variant and face sudden-death risk"],"management":["Deliver guideline-directed heart failure therapy for left ventricular dysfunction (ACE inhibitor/ARB or ARNI, beta-blocker, mineralocorticoid antagonist and SGLT2 inhibitor)","Implant a cardioverter-defibrillator at a lower threshold given the high sudden-death risk, even with only mildly reduced ejection fraction","Manage atrioventricular conduction disease, providing pacing where indicated, preferably within a defibrillator device","Anticoagulate for atrial arrhythmia and apply rate or rhythm control for atrial fibrillation","Advise against strenuous competitive exercise and consider antiarrhythmic strategies for ventricular arrhythmia","Undertake regular rhythm surveillance with ECG and ambulatory monitoring alongside serial imaging of ventricular function","Arrange genetic testing with cascade family screening and assess for coexisting skeletal myopathy","Refer for advanced heart failure evaluation and transplantation in end-stage disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC guidelines on cardiomyopathies and on prevention of sudden cardiac death","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"loa_loa_filariasis":{"name":"Loiasis (Loa loa filariasis)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["loiasis","Loa loa infection","African eye worm","Calabar swellings","loa loa filariasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / WHO guidance on filariasis","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by the filarial nematode Loa loa, transmitted by the day-biting Chrysops (mango or deer) fly in the rainforests of West and Central Africa","The classic features are transient, localised, non-pitting angio-oedematous 'Calabar swellings', often around joints, and the sensation or sight of an adult worm migrating across the subconjunctiva of the eye","Peripheral eosinophilia is typically prominent, and symptoms are often more marked in expatriates and visitors than in endemic residents","Diagnosis rests on detecting sheathed microfilariae with daytime (midday) periodicity on a stained blood film, reflecting the vector's daytime biting","Adult worms may be identified during their migration across the eye or under the skin","Treatment options include diethylcarbamazine (which targets microfilariae and adult worms) and albendazole, with careful attention to microfilarial load","High Loa loa microfilarial loads carry a risk of severe encephalopathy when treated with certain antifilarial drugs, so the burden must be assessed before therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Loiasis is caused by infection with the filarial worm Loa loa. Infective larvae are deposited when an infected Chrysops fly takes a blood meal; the larvae mature into adult worms that migrate through subcutaneous and connective tissues over years. Adult females release sheathed microfilariae that circulate in the peripheral blood with a diurnal periodicity matching the daytime feeding of the vector, allowing onward transmission. Host immune and hypersensitivity responses to migrating adults and their products produce the transient inflammatory Calabar swellings and eosinophilia, while migration across the bulbar conjunctiva gives the classic 'eye worm'. Heavy microfilaraemia is largely asymptomatic itself but becomes clinically important because rapid microfilarial killing during treatment can trigger severe inflammatory reactions, including encephalopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Onchocerciasis","Lymphatic filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti, Brugia species)","Other causes of angio-oedema or urticaria","Gnathostomiasis and other migratory helminths","Cutaneous larva migrans","Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Daytime (midday) peripheral blood film for sheathed microfilariae","Full blood count documenting eosinophilia","Quantification of Loa loa microfilarial density before treatment","Filarial serology or antigen/antibody testing where available","Slit-lamp or direct examination if a worm is visible in the eye","Molecular (PCR) assays for species confirmation where accessible"],"redFlags":["Very high microfilarial load, given the risk of treatment-associated encephalopathy","Neurological deterioration or encephalopathy during antifilarial therapy","Renal involvement or severe systemic hypersensitivity reactions"],"prognosis":"Loiasis is generally a chronic, non-fatal infection with a good prognosis when managed carefully; the principal danger is treatment-related severe reactions, especially encephalopathy in heavily microfilaraemic individuals.","pitfalls":["Starting antifilarial treatment without first measuring the microfilarial load and considering encephalopathy risk","Timing the diagnostic blood film incorrectly and missing the daytime-periodic microfilariae","Co-treating without recognising overlap with onchocerciasis or lymphatic filariasis in co-endemic regions","Attributing Calabar swellings to simple allergy in a returning traveller"],"management":["Give diethylcarbamazine as definitive therapy targeting microfilariae and adult worms, but only after quantifying microfilarial density","Reduce heavy microfilaraemia first with albendazole or apheresis to lower the risk of treatment-related encephalopathy","Avoid or use extreme caution with ivermectin and diethylcarbamazine in high microfilarial loads because of severe post-treatment reactions","Remove an adult worm surgically or mechanically when it migrates across the conjunctiva or subcutis and is accessible","Monitor closely during treatment for encephalopathy and other reactions, providing supportive care","Advise vector-avoidance measures against the day-biting Chrysops fly","Involve tropical medicine or infectious diseases specialists and consider repeat courses given relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","WHO guidance on filarial infections"],"crossLinks":[]},"lobomycosis":{"name":"Lobomycosis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lacazia loboi","Loboa loboi","lacaziosis","Jorge Lobo disease","keloidal blastomycosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lobomycosis is a chronic cutaneous and subcutaneous fungal infection caused by the uncultivated organism Lacazia loboi, endemic to the humid tropics of the Amazon basin and Central/South America","It occurs in rural forest and riverine workers and has also been reported in dolphins, with rare dolphin-to-human transmission","Infection follows traumatic inoculation of the organism into the skin; there is no evidence of person-to-person spread","The characteristic lesions are slowly growing, firm, shiny keloid-like nodules and plaques, typically on exposed cooler sites such as the ears, limbs and face","Lesions are usually painless but may itch; they spread by local extension and autoinoculation over years while sparing internal organs","Diagnosis is made histologically by finding chains of uniform, thick-walled yeast-like cells linked by tubular bridges; the organism cannot be cultured in vitro","Treatment is difficult: surgical excision of localised lesions is preferred, and antifungal drugs alone are generally disappointing, so wide excision with clear margins is often required"],"pathophysiology":"Lacazia loboi is thought to reside in soil, vegetation or aquatic environments and is introduced into the dermis through minor trauma. The organism proliferates as yeast-like cells within the dermis, provoking a granulomatous response, and spreads slowly by contiguous extension and by autoinoculation from scratching. Because it does not disseminate haematogenously or invade viscera, disease remains confined to the skin and subcutaneous tissue, producing progressively enlarging fibrotic, keloid-like nodules. The inability to culture the fungus reflects its obligate in vivo growth and has limited understanding of its biology.","additionalDifferentials":["Keloid or hypertrophic scar","Chromoblastomycosis","Cutaneous leishmaniasis","Lepromatous leprosy","Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans or other cutaneous tumours","Other subcutaneous mycoses (e.g. paracoccidioidomycosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with fungal stains showing chains of yeast cells joined by tubular bridges","Direct microscopy of tissue scrapings for the characteristic yeast cells","Clinical and exposure history (forest/riverine work, dolphin contact)","Assessment of lesion extent and margins to plan excision","Exclusion of other subcutaneous mycoses and leprosy","Molecular identification where available given inability to culture"],"redFlags":["Rapid change, ulceration or induration in a long-standing lesion raising concern for malignant transformation","Extensive or multifocal disease limiting surgical options","Involvement of functionally or cosmetically critical sites","Recurrence after excision","Diagnostic uncertainty with a cutaneous malignancy"],"prognosis":"Lobomycosis is indolent and non-life-threatening but chronic and disfiguring; localised lesions may be cured by complete excision, whereas extensive disease tends to recur and responds poorly to medical therapy, and rare malignant transformation is reported in long-standing lesions.","pitfalls":["Mistaking lesions for keloid and delaying biopsy and diagnosis","Expecting antifungal drugs alone to be curative","Incomplete excision leading to recurrence","Attempting culture and being misled by its failure to grow","Overlooking malignant change in chronic, altering lesions"],"management":["Undertake complete surgical excision with clear margins, the mainstay offering the best chance of cure for localised lesions","Use cryotherapy or Mohs micrographic surgery for selected accessible lesions","Add prolonged antifungal therapy such as itraconazole or posaconazole, recognising that medical treatment alone is often disappointing","Combine surgery with antifungal therapy in extensive disease to reduce recurrence","Follow up long term for local recurrence and the rare malignant transformation of longstanding lesions","Provide wound care and advise against scratching to prevent autoinoculation","Refer to dermatology or infectious diseases with histological confirmation, since the organism cannot be cultured"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"local_anaesthetic_systemic_toxicity":{"name":"Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LAST","local anaesthetic toxicity","systemic local anaesthetic poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs when plasma levels of local anaesthetic rise excessively, usually from inadvertent intravascular injection or absorption from a large or repeated dose","Classic progression moves from early neurological signs (perioral numbness, tinnitus, metallic taste, agitation, dysarthria) to seizures, then to cardiovascular collapse, but atypical or delayed presentations are common","Cardiac toxicity can appear with little warning, especially with more lipophilic and potent agents such as bupivacaine, and may manifest as conduction block, arrhythmia, or refractory arrest","Immediate priorities are stopping the injection, calling for help, securing the airway with oxygenation, and controlling seizures while avoiding large doses of propofol in the unstable patient","Lipid emulsion therapy (intravenous lipid rescue) is the specific antidote and should be started early alongside high-quality resuscitation","During arrest, resuscitation is often prolonged; cardiopulmonary bypass or extracorporeal support may be needed and efforts should not be abandoned prematurely","Prevention relies on aspiration before injection, incremental dosing, use of a test dose, ultrasound guidance, and staying within weight-based maximum limits","Standard-dose adrenaline may be less effective and vasopressin is generally avoided; arrhythmias are managed while lipid rescue takes effect"],"pathophysiology":"Local anaesthetics block voltage-gated sodium channels. When systemic concentrations become excessive, they also disrupt cardiac and central nervous system membrane excitability and interfere with cardiac ion channels, mitochondrial energy production, and myocardial fatty acid metabolism. The central nervous system is affected first because inhibitory pathways are suppressed before excitatory ones, producing an excitatory phase followed by global depression. Cardiac effects include impaired conduction, reduced contractility, and malignant arrhythmias. More potent, lipid-soluble agents bind cardiac tissue avidly, making toxicity harder to reverse. Lipid emulsion is thought to act as a circulating sink that sequesters drug away from tissue and to support myocardial energetics.","additionalDifferentials":["Vasovagal syncope or simple faint during a block","High or total spinal anaesthesia","Primary cardiac arrhythmia or ischaemic event","Anaphylaxis to an injected agent or additive","Hyperventilation or panic reaction","Adrenaline effect from an epinephrine-containing solution"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous ECG and cardiovascular monitoring for arrhythmia and conduction abnormality","Pulse oximetry and capnography to confirm oxygenation and ventilation","Arterial blood gas to assess acidosis and guide resuscitation","Serial electrolytes and glucose during prolonged resuscitation","Plasma local anaesthetic level where available (rarely used acutely)"],"redFlags":["Seizure activity or rapidly declining consciousness after a regional block","Cardiovascular collapse, malignant arrhythmia, or cardiac arrest following local anaesthetic administration","Refractory hypotension or wide-complex rhythms unresponsive to standard measures","Progressive symptoms after a large-volume or repeated injection suggesting rising systemic levels"],"prognosis":"With early recognition, prompt lipid emulsion therapy, and sustained high-quality resuscitation, many patients recover fully; delayed treatment or severe bupivacaine-related arrest carries a higher risk of death or hypoxic injury.","pitfalls":["Attributing early neurological symptoms to anxiety and continuing injection","Delaying lipid emulsion until after prolonged, unsuccessful resuscitation","Abandoning resuscitation too early despite the potential for good recovery","Exceeding weight-based maximum doses or omitting aspiration and incremental dosing","Using large sedative doses that worsen cardiovascular instability"],"management":["Stop injecting the local anaesthetic immediately and call for help","Manage the airway with high-flow oxygen and ventilation to prevent hypoxia and acidosis","Control seizures with a benzodiazepine, avoiding large doses of propofol in the haemodynamically unstable patient","Give intravenous lipid emulsion (lipid rescue) as the specific antidote, started early as a bolus and infusion","Use modified high-quality resuscitation for arrest, giving small adrenaline doses and avoiding vasopressin, calcium-channel blockers and certain antiarrhythmics","Continue resuscitation for a prolonged period, with early recourse to cardiopulmonary bypass or extracorporeal life support","Prevent recurrence through dose limits, aspiration before injection, incremental dosing and ultrasound guidance, with post-event monitoring for delayed toxicity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"localised_amyloidosis":{"name":"Localised Amyloidosis","system":"Multisystem / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Localised AL amyloidosis","Organ-limited amyloidosis","Focal amyloidosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Localised amyloidosis is confined to a single organ or site without systemic deposition, and is most often of immunoglobulin light-chain (AL) type produced by a local clone of plasma cells or B-cells.","Frequently affected sites include the urinary tract (especially the bladder), larynx, tracheobronchial tree, skin, conjunctiva or orbit, and gastrointestinal tract.","Presentation reflects the site, with painless haematuria in bladder disease, hoarseness in laryngeal disease, and wheeze or haemoptysis in airway disease.","Unlike systemic AL amyloidosis, there is usually no significant circulating monoclonal protein and no risk to vital organs such as the heart or kidney.","Progression to systemic amyloidosis is uncommon, so the prognosis is generally excellent.","Management is local, using endoscopic or surgical excision, local radiotherapy, or watchful waiting, rather than systemic chemotherapy.","A thorough evaluation to exclude underlying systemic disease is mandatory before labelling amyloid as localised.","Cerebral amyloid angiopathy is a distinct organ-limited beta-amyloid form affecting the cerebral vasculature."],"pathophysiology":"In localised AL amyloidosis a small clone of plasma cells or B-lymphocytes resident within the affected tissue secretes amyloidogenic immunoglobulin light chains that misfold and deposit as fibrils in situ. Because the clone and the deposition are confined to that site, there is no meaningful systemic light-chain excess and other organs are spared. The deposits form tumour-like masses or diffuse infiltration that disrupt local function, for example by obstructing or ulcerating the urinary tract or by stiffening the vocal cords. The reasons for the localised behaviour of the clone are incompletely understood, but the process rarely disseminates.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic AL amyloidosis","Malignancy at the affected site such as bladder or laryngeal carcinoma","Chronic inflammatory or granulomatous lesions","Other benign masses","Lymphoproliferative disease for orbital and conjunctival lesions","Sarcoidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy of the lesion with Congo red staining and amyloid typing by mass spectrometry or immunohistochemistry","Serum free light chains and serum and urine immunofixation to exclude a systemic clone","SAP scintigraphy to confirm the absence of systemic deposits","Bone marrow examination if a systemic clone is suspected","Site-specific imaging and endoscopy","Echocardiography with renal and liver function to exclude systemic organ involvement"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting systemic disease such as proteinuria, cardiac symptoms or a significant serum monoclonal protein","Rapid local growth or bleeding","Airway compromise from laryngeal or tracheal disease","Recurrent obstruction"],"prognosis":"Localised amyloidosis carries an excellent prognosis with rare progression to systemic disease. Local recurrence can occur and may require repeat local treatment, but organ-threatening systemic complications are unusual.","pitfalls":["Assuming localised disease without excluding systemic amyloidosis","Giving systemic chemotherapy inappropriately for organ-limited disease","Missing a co-existing malignancy at the biopsy site","Failing to type the amyloid and presuming it is AL"],"management":["Treat locally as the mainstay, with endoscopic or surgical excision of amyloid deposits at the affected site","Use local radiotherapy for laryngeal, tracheobronchial or other sites less amenable to excision","Adopt watchful waiting for stable, minimally symptomatic disease","Exclude systemic amyloidosis with serum free light chains, immunofixation, SAP scintigraphy and organ assessment before deciding management","Avoid systemic chemotherapy, which is not indicated in truly localised disease","Apply site-specific supportive measures for manifestations such as haematuria, airway compromise or voice change","Monitor long term for local recurrence and the uncommon progression to systemic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology, chapter on amyloidosis","Oxford Textbook of Medicine, chapter on amyloidosis","International Society of Amyloidosis nomenclature guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"locked_in_syndrome":{"name":"Locked-in syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ventral pontine syndrome","de-efferented state","pseudocoma","cerebromedullospinal disconnection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most often caused by extensive infarction of the ventral pons from basilar artery thrombosis, damaging the corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts bilaterally","The patient is quadriplegic and anarthric but fully conscious and aware, with preserved cognition","Communication is typically possible only through vertical eye movements and blinking, which are mediated by spared midbrain structures","The reticular activating system is preserved, so the sleep-wake cycle and awareness remain intact, distinguishing it from coma","It must be actively distinguished from coma, persistent vegetative state, and severe Guillain-Barre syndrome","Other causes include central pontine myelinolysis, pontine haemorrhage, and rarely advanced neuromuscular disease","Early recognition allows a communication code to be established and appropriate supportive and rehabilitative care to begin"],"pathophysiology":"Locked-in syndrome results from bilateral interruption of the ventral pons, classically through basilar artery occlusion causing infarction of the basis pontis. The corticospinal and corticobulbar fibres are destroyed, abolishing voluntary movement of the limbs and lower cranial musculature and producing quadriplegia and anarthria. Crucially, the dorsal pontine tegmentum containing the reticular activating system is spared, so consciousness and cognition are retained, and supranuclear pathways for vertical gaze and blinking in the midbrain remain intact, providing the only avenue for voluntary responses. The disconnection of efferent motor output from an intact conscious brain gives the characteristic de-efferented state.","additionalDifferentials":["Coma from bilateral hemispheric or brainstem dysfunction","Persistent vegetative or minimally conscious state","Severe Guillain-Barre syndrome with total paralysis","Myasthenic or other neuromuscular crisis","Akinetic mutism from bifrontal lesions","Deep sedation or neuromuscular blockade in critical care"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI with diffusion imaging demonstrating ventral pontine infarction","MR or CT angiography confirming basilar artery occlusion or stenosis","Electroencephalography showing preserved reactive background to confirm awareness","Assessment of vertical eye movements and blinking to establish communication and awareness","Electrolyte and osmolality review where central pontine myelinolysis is possible","Nerve conduction studies where a neuromuscular mimic is suspected"],"redFlags":["Failure to recognise preserved awareness and misdiagnosis as coma or vegetative state","Evolving basilar thrombosis requiring urgent recanalisation consideration","Respiratory failure demanding airway protection and ventilatory support","Rapid osmotic shifts precipitating or worsening central pontine myelinolysis"],"prognosis":"Mortality is high in the acute basilar occlusion phase, and most survivors remain severely disabled, though some regain limited movement and many retain a meaningful quality of life with communication aids; recovery is generally slow and incomplete.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a fully aware patient for being comatose and communicating inappropriately at the bedside","Overlooking preserved vertical gaze as the key sign of retained consciousness","Delaying recognition of basilar occlusion amenable to intervention","Correcting hyponatraemia too rapidly and inducing osmotic demyelination"],"management":["Treat the underlying basilar artery occlusion acutely with thrombolysis and/or mechanical thrombectomy when the patient presents within window","Provide neurocritical care with airway protection, ventilatory support and management of the acute stroke","Establish a reliable communication channel using preserved vertical eye movements and blinking, with eye-tracking and other assistive technology","Deliver comprehensive supportive care: enteral feeding via gastrostomy, tracheostomy, pressure-area and eye care, and venous thromboembolism prophylaxis","Prevent and treat complications such as aspiration pneumonia, contractures, spasticity and autonomic instability","Institute secondary stroke prevention with antithrombotic therapy and vascular risk-factor control, or correct the specific cause such as cautious sodium correction in central pontine myelinolysis","Provide intensive multidisciplinary neurorehabilitation with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and communication therapy","Offer psychological support, honest prognostic discussion, goals-of-care planning and family support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":["top_of_the_basilar_syndrome","millard_gubler_syndrome","foville_syndrome"]},"loeffler_endocarditis":{"name":"Loeffler endocarditis","system":"Cardiovascular / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Loffler endocarditis","eosinophilic endomyocardial disease","eosinophilic endomyocarditis","hypereosinophilic restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC/AHA cardiomyopathy and pericardial/myocardial disease guidelines; Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of eosinophilic endomyocardial disease occurring in the setting of persistent hypereosinophilia, leading to a restrictive cardiomyopathy","Despite the name, it is a non-infective inflammatory and fibrotic process rather than infective endocarditis","Cardiac involvement evolves through an acute necrotic phase, a thrombotic phase and a late fibrotic phase with endomyocardial scarring","Mural thrombus within the ventricular apices and along the inflow tract predisposes to systemic embolism","Fibrosis and thrombus can tether the atrioventricular valves and obliterate the ventricular cavities, producing restrictive physiology and valvular regurgitation","Underlying causes of the eosinophilia include primary (clonal) and secondary hypereosinophilic syndromes, and it overlaps with tropical endomyocardial fibrosis","Treatment targets the eosinophilia (for example corticosteroids and cause-specific therapy) alongside heart failure management, anticoagulation for thrombus and surgery for advanced valvular or fibrotic disease","Cardiac MRI is valuable for detecting thrombus, inflammation and the characteristic endomyocardial fibrosis"],"pathophysiology":"Loeffler endocarditis results from sustained hypereosinophilia in which activated eosinophils infiltrate the endomyocardium and release toxic granule proteins. These damage the endocardium and myocardium, initiating an acute necrotic and inflammatory phase. Endocardial injury then promotes mural thrombus formation, and over time organising thrombus and reactive fibrosis produce dense endomyocardial scarring. The resulting stiff, non-compliant ventricles impair filling to cause restrictive cardiomyopathy, while fibrosis and thrombus involving the subvalvular apparatus lead to atrioventricular valve regurgitation and apical cavity obliteration. Systemic embolism from mural thrombus is an important complication.","additionalDifferentials":["Endomyocardial fibrosis (tropical)","Other restrictive cardiomyopathies such as cardiac amyloidosis","Constrictive pericarditis","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis with cardiac involvement","Infective endocarditis with intracardiac thrombus or vegetation","Cardiac tumour or apical thrombus of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film confirming and quantifying eosinophilia","Echocardiography for apical thrombus, endomyocardial thickening, valve involvement and restrictive filling","Cardiac MRI for thrombus, inflammation and endomyocardial fibrosis","Investigation of the cause of eosinophilia (clonal markers, secondary causes, parasitic and allergic screens)","Endomyocardial biopsy in selected cases","Cardiac biomarkers and assessment of embolic complications"],"redFlags":["Signs of heart failure with restrictive physiology in a patient with marked eosinophilia","Systemic embolic event from intracardiac thrombus","Rapidly progressive valvular regurgitation or cavity obliteration","Very high or rising eosinophil count with cardiac symptoms"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on early control of the eosinophilia and the extent of endomyocardial fibrosis; treatment during the earlier phases can limit damage, whereas established fibrotic restrictive disease carries a poor prognosis with heart failure and embolic risk.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for infective endocarditis because of the name","Overlooking mild eosinophilia and failing to link it to the cardiac picture","Missing apical mural thrombus and the consequent need for anticoagulation","Delaying treatment of the eosinophilia until irreversible fibrosis has developed","Not investigating the underlying cause of the hypereosinophilia"],"management":["Start corticosteroids as first-line therapy to suppress the hypereosinophilia and limit endomyocardial damage","Treat the underlying cause of eosinophilia, using imatinib for FIP1L1-PDGFRA-positive clonal disease, other cytoreduction such as hydroxycarbamide, and treatment of secondary or parasitic causes","Anticoagulate for mural thrombus to prevent systemic embolism","Give heart failure therapy for the restrictive physiology, including diuretics and standard measures","Undertake surgery in the fibrotic phase, including endocardial decortication and atrioventricular valve repair or replacement","Monitor disease phase and response with serial echocardiography, cardiac MRI and eosinophil counts","Manage jointly across cardiology and haematology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiomyopathies","Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"loeys_dietz_syndrome":{"name":"Loeys-Dietz syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LDS","TGFBR1-related aortopathy","TGFBR2-related aortopathy","TGF-beta signalling vasculopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant connective-tissue disorder caused by pathogenic variants in genes of the TGF-beta signalling pathway, most often TGFBR1 and TGFBR2, and also SMAD3, TGFB2, and TGFB3","Characterised by arterial tortuosity and aneurysms that can affect the aorta and its branches well beyond the aortic root","The triad of arterial tortuosity, hypertelorism, and a bifid or broad uvula/cleft palate is a classic clinical clue","Aneurysms tend to dissect at smaller diameters and younger ages than in Marfan syndrome, so surveillance and surgical thresholds are more aggressive","Whole-arterial-tree imaging from head to pelvis is recommended because aneurysms are not confined to the aortic root","Ectopia lentis is characteristically absent, which helps distinguish it from Marfan syndrome","Craniofacial, skeletal, and cutaneous features overlap with Marfan and Ehlers-Danlos syndromes, so genetic testing guides definitive classification","Management combines medical aortic protection (beta-blockers and/or angiotensin-receptor blockers), close imaging surveillance, and earlier prophylactic vascular surgery"],"pathophysiology":"Loeys-Dietz syndrome arises from variants that disturb transforming growth factor beta signalling within the vascular wall. Paradoxically, despite loss-of-function receptor variants, downstream TGF-beta signalling is upregulated, driving abnormal extracellular matrix remodelling and smooth-muscle cell dysfunction. The result is a fragile, tortuous arterial tree with a propensity to aneurysm formation and dissection throughout the aorta and its branches. The same signalling disturbance underlies the craniofacial, skeletal, and connective-tissue features, and the widespread vascular fragility explains the elevated risk of rupture at relatively small vessel diameters.","additionalDifferentials":["Marfan syndrome","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Arterial tortuosity syndrome","Familial thoracic aortic aneurysm and dissection","Shprintzen-Goldberg syndrome","Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, hypermobile type"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography with aortic root measurement","Head-to-pelvis CT or MR angiography to map arterial tortuosity and aneurysms","Molecular genetic testing of the TGF-beta pathway aortopathy gene panel","Slit-lamp examination to confirm absence of ectopia lentis and assess other ocular features","Cervical spine imaging for instability before intervention or anaesthesia","Skeletal review for scoliosis, pectus deformity, and club foot"],"redFlags":["Acute severe chest, back, or abdominal pain suggesting arterial dissection or rupture","Aneurysm growth or an aortic diameter reaching the individualised surgical threshold","Aneurysms in cerebral or visceral arteries detected on surveillance imaging","Signs of cervical spine instability, especially peri-operatively","Pregnancy complications given the heightened maternal dissection risk"],"prognosis":"Prognosis has improved substantially with recognition, aggressive imaging surveillance, medical aortic protection, and earlier surgery, but the widespread and aggressive arterial disease still carries meaningful risk of dissection and rupture. Outcomes vary with the causative gene and the extent of vascular involvement.","pitfalls":["Applying Marfan aortic surgical thresholds, which are too conservative for Loeys-Dietz syndrome","Imaging only the aortic root and missing distal or branch-vessel aneurysms","Overlooking cervical spine instability before intubation or surgery","Confusing it with Marfan syndrome despite the absence of ectopia lentis","Underestimating the maternal vascular risk during pregnancy"],"management":["Use beta-blockade and/or angiotensin-receptor blockade to reduce aortic wall stress and slow aneurysm growth","Undertake prophylactic aortic root and vascular surgery at smaller diameters and younger ages than in Marfan syndrome, given dissection at small calibre","Perform whole-arterial-tree imaging surveillance from head to pelvis at regular intervals, not just the aortic root","Advise activity modification, avoiding heavy isometric exertion and contact or competitive sport","Control blood pressure and counsel on the high vascular risk of pregnancy","Manage within a multidisciplinary team spanning clinical genetics, cardiology and cardiothoracic/vascular surgery, addressing skeletal and craniofacial features","Provide genetic counselling and cascade family testing of the TGF-beta pathway gene given autosomal dominant inheritance","Assess the cervical spine for instability before anaesthesia or intervention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Loeys-Dietz syndrome","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"loin_pain_haematuria_syndrome":{"name":"Loin pain haematuria syndrome","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LPHS","Loin pain-haematuria syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A diagnosis of exclusion: severe recurrent loin pain with macroscopic or microscopic haematuria and no identifiable urological or glomerular cause.","It predominantly affects young women.","Pain may be unilateral or bilateral, is often severe and disproportionate to findings, and may be triggered by minor events.","Extensive imaging and cystoscopy are needed to exclude stones, obstruction, tumour and vascular anomalies.","Renal biopsy may show C3 deposition in arteriolar walls or glomerular basement membrane abnormalities, but is often near-normal and mainly serves to exclude glomerulonephritis.","There is a strong association with psychological distress and analgesic dependence, so multidisciplinary pain management is central.","Actively exclude nutcracker syndrome and thin basement membrane disease as mimics.","Invasive options such as nerve blocks, renal denervation, autotransplantation and nephrectomy are last resort with inconsistent and often disappointing results."],"pathophysiology":"The mechanism is poorly understood and probably heterogeneous. Proposed contributors include a renal microvascular or glomerular abnormality allowing red cells to escape into tubules with tubular obstruction and capsular distension, abnormally thick or thin glomerular basement membranes, complement (C3) deposition in vessel walls, intrarenal vascular spasm, and crystalluria causing microtrauma. Altered central pain processing and visceral hypersensitivity are increasingly recognised components, and by definition no obstructing, neoplastic, infective or defined glomerular cause is found.","additionalDifferentials":["Nephrolithiasis or crystalluria","Nutcracker syndrome (left renal vein compression)","Thin basement membrane disease or IgA nephropathy","Ureteropelvic junction obstruction","Renal or urothelial malignancy","Renal papillary necrosis","Medullary sponge kidney or musculoskeletal loin pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and microscopy for dysmorphic red cells and casts","Urine culture and cytology","CT urography for stones, obstruction and masses","Doppler or cross-sectional venography for nutcracker phenomenon","Cystoscopy to localise bleeding and exclude bladder pathology","Glomerular screen (complement, autoimmune serology, protein quantification)","Renal biopsy to exclude glomerulonephritis"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting malignancy such as a mass or unexplained weight loss","Signs of urinary obstruction or infection with sepsis","New proteinuria, red-cell casts or rising creatinine suggesting glomerulonephritis","Escalating opioid requirement and dependence"],"prognosis":"It is a chronic and often debilitating condition with a relapsing course; pain diminishes over time in some patients, but many endure years of symptoms and invasive interventions frequently fail to provide durable relief.","pitfalls":["Making the diagnosis without adequately excluding treatable causes such as stones, nutcracker syndrome and glomerulonephritis","Escalating opioids without a multidisciplinary pain strategy","Proceeding to nephrectomy or autotransplantation with unrealistic expectations and risk of contralateral recurrence","Dismissing the patient as functional and missing evolving organic disease"],"management":["Exclude treatable urological and glomerular causes thoroughly before making this diagnosis of exclusion","Centre care on multidisciplinary chronic pain management, involving a specialist pain team","Provide multimodal analgesia with cautious, structured opioid use given dependence risk, and adjuncts such as neuropathic agents","Offer psychological support and treat associated distress, recognising the risk of analgesic dependence","Consider nerve blocks or catheter-directed local anaesthetic techniques for refractory pain in selected patients","Reserve invasive options such as renal denervation, autotransplantation or nephrectomy for carefully selected refractory cases, recognising frequent failure and recurrence","Avoid repeated unnecessary invasive investigations and procedures, maintaining nephrology and pain-service follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Oxford Textbook of Medicine","Oxford Handbook of Nephrology and Hypertension"],"crossLinks":[]},"loin_pain_hematuria":{"name":"Loin pain haematuria syndrome","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LPHS","loin pain-hematuria syndrome","loin pain haematuria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare and poorly understood syndrome of severe, often disabling loin (flank) pain accompanied by haematuria, in the absence of an identifiable urological or glomerular cause","It is fundamentally a diagnosis of exclusion after stones, tumours, infection, obstruction, and glomerular disease have been ruled out","The pain may be unilateral or bilateral, can be constant or episodic, and is frequently out of proportion to any objective findings","The haematuria is usually microscopic but may be macroscopic, and dysmorphic red cells can occur, sometimes suggesting a subtle glomerular contribution","It predominantly affects younger women and is often associated with substantial psychological distress and impaired quality of life","Proposed mechanisms include abnormalities of the glomerular basement membrane allowing red cell leakage, microcrystalluria, and intrarenal vascular or nociceptive factors, but none is definitive","Management is challenging and centres on multidisciplinary pain control and support, with cautious use of invasive interventions given inconsistent results","Kidney function and long-term renal survival are generally preserved, which is important for reassurance"],"pathophysiology":"The cause of loin pain haematuria syndrome remains uncertain and is likely multifactorial. Leading hypotheses propose that structural abnormalities of the glomerular basement membrane permit red blood cells to pass into the tubules, where they may obstruct flow and distend the renal capsule, generating pain. Other proposed contributors include microcrystal deposition, intrarenal vasospasm or microvascular disease, and heightened visceral nociceptive signalling. Because objective pathology is minimal, central sensitisation and chronic pain mechanisms are thought to play an important role in perpetuating symptoms. The absence of progressive structural damage explains why kidney function is usually maintained.","additionalDifferentials":["Nephrolithiasis and microlithiasis","Urinary tract infection or pyelonephritis","IgA nephropathy and thin basement membrane nephropathy","Ureteric obstruction or ureteropelvic junction abnormality","Nutcracker syndrome (renal vein compression)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and microscopy for red cell morphology and casts","Cross-sectional imaging (CT) to exclude stones, obstruction, and masses","Cystoscopy and urine cytology where malignancy or bladder source is a concern","Assessment of proteinuria and kidney function, with biopsy considered in selected cases","Vascular imaging to exclude nutcracker phenomenon when suspected"],"redFlags":["New or changing haematuria that could indicate malignancy","Fever or systemic signs suggesting infection","Findings of obstruction or a renal mass on imaging","Escalating opioid use and dependence"],"prognosis":"Kidney function is typically preserved and the syndrome is not life-threatening, but the pain is often chronic, relapsing, and difficult to control, with a considerable impact on quality of life and mental health.","pitfalls":["Making the diagnosis before adequately excluding treatable structural and glomerular causes","Escalating opioids and invasive procedures with limited evidence of benefit","Dismissing patients' pain as purely psychological","Missing an alternative such as nutcracker syndrome or a subtle glomerulonephritis"],"management":["Explain that no disease-specific drug cures the syndrome and that care is symptomatic once stones, tumour, infection, obstruction and glomerular disease have been excluded","Anchor treatment in a multidisciplinary chronic-pain programme using simple analgesia and neuropathic agents such as tricyclic antidepressants or gabapentinoids, minimising escalating opioid use","Provide psychological therapy such as cognitive behavioural therapy to address the central sensitisation and associated distress","Offer interventional analgesia such as splanchnic or coeliac plexus nerve blocks or catheter-based techniques in refractory pain, with realistic expectations of benefit","Reserve ablative and surgical options such as renal denervation, autotransplantation or nephrectomy for carefully selected refractory cases, counselling that results are unpredictable and pain may recur","Monitor renal function and blood pressure periodically, reassuring that kidney function is typically preserved, and avoid repeated invasive investigation","Refer jointly to nephrology and a specialist pain service, escalating when pain becomes disabling or opioid dependence develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"loin_pain_hematuria_syndrome":{"name":"Loin pain-haematuria syndrome","system":"Renal / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["loin pain haematuria syndrome","LPHS","loin pain-hematuria syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A diagnosis of exclusion characterised by severe, often disabling, unilateral or bilateral loin pain accompanied by haematuria in the absence of an identifiable structural, infective, or glomerular cause","Predominantly affects young to middle-aged women","Haematuria may be macroscopic or microscopic and is often intermittent; the pain is frequently out of proportion to objective findings","The condition is defined only after thorough exclusion of stones, tumour, infection, glomerular disease, clotting disorders, and vascular causes such as nutcracker syndrome","Renal biopsy may show non-specific findings such as C3 deposition or thickening of the glomerular basement membrane, but is mainly used to exclude other pathology","Management is challenging and centres on multidisciplinary chronic pain control; invasive and ablative procedures have unpredictable and often disappointing results","Careful psychological assessment and support are integral, without dismissing the pain as non-organic"],"pathophysiology":"The cause of loin pain-haematuria syndrome is poorly understood and probably heterogeneous. Proposed mechanisms include abnormalities of the glomerular basement membrane allowing red cells to escape into tubules, where they may form microcrystals or casts that provoke tubular obstruction and pain; intrarenal vascular or microvascular abnormalities; and abnormal pain processing or sensitisation. Because no single consistent lesion is found, the syndrome is best regarded as a clinical entity defined by its symptom complex once other renal and urological diseases have been rigorously excluded.","additionalDifferentials":["Urolithiasis or microlithiasis","Glomerulonephritis (e.g. IgA nephropathy)","Nutcracker syndrome (renal vein compression)","Urothelial or renal malignancy","Chronic or recurrent pyelonephritis","Ureteric obstruction or clot colic","Coagulopathy or anticoagulant-related bleeding"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis, microscopy for dysmorphic red cells and casts, and culture","CT urography and ultrasound to exclude stones, tumour, and obstruction","Cystoscopy and, where indicated, ureteroscopy to localise bleeding and exclude urothelial lesions","Doppler or cross-sectional venous imaging to exclude nutcracker phenomenon","Coagulation screen and renal function tests","Renal biopsy to exclude glomerular disease when clinically warranted"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting malignancy, such as a mass, weight loss, or persistent unexplained macroscopic haematuria","Signs of obstruction, infection, or sepsis requiring urgent intervention","Escalating opioid requirements and dependence","New proteinuria or declining renal function pointing to an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The natural history is variable; some patients improve or remit over time, but many experience a chronic relapsing course with substantial impact on quality of life, and durable relief from invasive interventions is inconsistent.","pitfalls":["Labelling a patient with the syndrome before adequately excluding treatable structural, glomerular, or vascular causes","Escalating to irreversible surgery such as nephrectomy or renal denervation with unpredictable benefit","Dismissing the pain as purely psychogenic and failing to provide multidisciplinary support","Overlooking nutcracker syndrome, which can mimic the presentation but is potentially treatable"],"management":["Establish the diagnosis of exclusion after ruling out stones, tumour, infection, glomerular disease, coagulopathy and nutcracker phenomenon before committing to symptomatic treatment","Base management on multidisciplinary chronic-pain control with simple analgesia and neuropathic agents such as amitriptyline or gabapentinoids, limiting opioid escalation","Incorporate psychological support and pain-coping strategies given the central pain component and impaired quality of life","Consider interventional options such as nerve blocks or ureteric catheter analgesia in refractory pain, counselling that response is variable","Reserve ablative or surgical procedures such as renal denervation, ureterolysis, autotransplantation or nephrectomy for refractory disease, warning that durable relief is inconsistent and symptoms may recur or transfer to the other side","Monitor renal function and blood pressure and avoid repeated invasive investigation once the diagnosis is secure","Manage jointly through urology, nephrology and a specialist pain service, escalating for disabling pain or opioid dependence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (Feehally et al.)"],"crossLinks":[]},"long_chain_3_hydroxyacyl_coa_dehydrogenase_deficiency":{"name":"Long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Fatty acid oxidation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LCHADD","LCHAD deficiency","HADHA deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by loss of the LCHAD activity of the mitochondrial trifunctional protein, most often from the common c.1528G>C (p.Glu474Gln) variant in HADHA; the trifunctional protein catalyses the final three steps of each long-chain beta-oxidation cycle.","Distinctive long-term complications set it apart from most other fatty-acid-oxidation disorders: a progressive pigmentary chorioretinopathy causing visual loss and a length-dependent peripheral neuropathy.","Acute presentation overlaps with other long-chain defects, with hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, cardiomyopathy, hepatopathy, sudden collapse and exercise-induced rhabdomyolysis.","Newborn screening shows accumulation of long-chain hydroxyacylcarnitines, notably 3-hydroxypalmitoylcarnitine (C16-OH) and 3-hydroxyoleylcarnitine (C18:1-OH).","Carrying an affected foetus predisposes the mother to acute fatty liver of pregnancy and to HELLP syndrome, an obstetric clue that should prompt testing of the infant.","Isolated LCHAD deficiency, in which only the LCHAD activity of the alpha subunit is impaired, is generally milder than complete trifunctional protein deficiency affecting all three activities.","Dietary management mirrors other long-chain disorders (fasting avoidance, long-chain-fat restriction, medium-chain triglyceride) with additional docosahexaenoic acid supplementation aimed at slowing the retinopathy."],"pathophysiology":"The mitochondrial trifunctional protein is an inner-membrane complex assembled from alpha subunits (HADHA) carrying long-chain enoyl-CoA hydratase and long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase (LCHAD) activities and beta subunits (HADHB) carrying long-chain 3-ketoacyl-CoA thiolase activity. When the LCHAD activity is deficient, oxidation of long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA esters halts, so long-chain 3-hydroxy fatty acids and their acylcarnitines accumulate while hepatic ketogenesis fails during fasting, giving hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and energy deficit in heart and muscle. The accumulating hydroxylated intermediates are thought to be directly cytotoxic and are implicated in the retinal pigmentary degeneration and peripheral nerve injury that are unique to this disorder, and in the maternal hepatotoxicity seen when a heterozygous mother gestates an affected foetus whose hydroxy-fatty-acid metabolites enter the maternal circulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Generalised mitochondrial trifunctional protein deficiency","Very long-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase 2 deficiency","Carnitine-acylcarnitine translocase deficiency","Inherited retinal dystrophies such as retinitis pigmentosa syndromes","Other causes of infantile cardiomyopathy","Peroxisomal disorders with retinopathy and neuropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma acylcarnitine profile showing raised long-chain hydroxyacylcarnitines","Urine organic acids with 3-hydroxydicarboxylic aciduria during metabolic crisis","Molecular testing of HADHA and HADHB","Trifunctional protein enzyme or fatty-acid flux assay in cultured fibroblasts","Ophthalmological assessment with fundoscopy, electroretinography and optical coherence tomography","Nerve conduction studies for the peripheral neuropathy","Echocardiography and electrocardiography"],"redFlags":["Hypoketotic hypoglycaemia with cardiorespiratory collapse in an infant","New or worsening cardiomyopathy or arrhythmia","Rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria and threatened acute kidney injury","Maternal acute fatty liver of pregnancy or HELLP syndrome in a current or previous pregnancy","Rapidly declining vision suggesting progression of the chorioretinopathy"],"prognosis":"Even with treatment the retinopathy and neuropathy tend to be progressive, and early cardiac decompensation can be fatal. Newborn screening and strict dietary management improve survival and reduce the frequency of metabolic crises.","pitfalls":["Neglecting retinal and neurological surveillance because acute metabolic control looks satisfactory","Failing to test the infant after maternal acute fatty liver of pregnancy or HELLP syndrome","Conflating isolated LCHAD deficiency with complete trifunctional protein deficiency, which carries different severity and counselling","Administering long-chain lipids during an acute crisis"],"management":["Prevent catabolism by avoiding fasting with frequent carbohydrate-rich feeds and, in infants, overnight feeds or uncooked cornstarch to maintain glucose supply","Restrict dietary long-chain fat and substitute medium-chain triglyceride oil, which bypasses the blocked long-chain beta-oxidation, while ensuring essential fatty acid provision","Supplement docosahexaenoic acid to slow the progressive pigmentary chorioretinopathy","Institute an emergency regimen during intercurrent illness with intravenous glucose to abort hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and rhabdomyolysis","Monitor for disorder-specific complications with serial echocardiography for cardiomyopathy, ophthalmological review with electroretinography for retinopathy, nerve conduction studies for neuropathy and creatine kinase for rhabdomyolysis","Coordinate care through a metabolic physician, specialist dietitian, cardiologist and ophthalmologist multidisciplinary team","Provide genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder with cascade family testing, and highlight the maternal risk of acute fatty liver of pregnancy and HELLP syndrome","Escalate promptly for acute metabolic decompensation, cardiac failure or exertional rhabdomyolysis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited metabolic disease reference texts","Fatty acid oxidation disorder management consensus guidelines","Paediatric metabolic medicine references"],"crossLinks":[]},"long_qt_syndrome":{"name":"Long QT syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LQTS","congenital long QT syndrome","acquired long QT","prolonged QT interval"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["LQTS is characterised by prolongation of the rate-corrected QT interval on ECG and a predisposition to the polymorphic ventricular tachycardia torsades de pointes","It may be congenital, from ion-channel gene mutations, or acquired, most often from QT-prolonging drugs and electrolyte disturbances such as hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, and hypocalcaemia","Presentations range from an incidental ECG finding to palpitations, syncope, seizures, or sudden cardiac death, frequently triggered by exertion, emotion, or auditory stimuli depending on the genotype","The QT interval must be corrected for heart rate, and serial or exercise ECGs may be needed because the interval can be borderline at rest","A careful family history of syncope, seizures, or premature sudden death supports a congenital cause","Management of acquired LQTS centres on withdrawing offending drugs and correcting electrolytes; congenital LQTS is often managed with beta-blockade and activity modification","An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator is considered for survivors of cardiac arrest or those with high-risk features despite therapy","Torsades is treated acutely with magnesium, correction of precipitants, and measures to raise the heart rate, with defibrillation if it degenerates to a sustained unstable rhythm"],"pathophysiology":"The QT interval reflects the duration of ventricular repolarisation. In congenital LQTS, mutations affecting cardiac potassium, sodium, or calcium channels delay repolarisation by reducing outward current or increasing inward current, prolonging the action potential. This heterogeneous prolongation favours early afterdepolarisations that can trigger torsades de pointes. Acquired LQTS most often results from drugs that block the rapid delayed rectifier potassium current or from electrolyte disturbances that impair repolarisation. Bradycardia and pauses further lengthen the QT and can precipitate pause-dependent torsades.","additionalDifferentials":["Acquired QT prolongation from drugs or electrolyte disturbance","Other inherited arrhythmia syndromes such as Brugada syndrome and catecholaminergic polymorphic VT","Vasovagal or other non-cardiac syncope","Epilepsy","Short QT syndrome (opposite repolarisation abnormality)","Structural causes of ventricular arrhythmia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG with accurate rate-corrected QT measurement, repeated as needed","Electrolyte panel including potassium, magnesium, and calcium","Review of all medications for QT-prolonging agents","Exercise or ambulatory ECG to unmask latent QT prolongation and assess dynamics","Genetic testing and family screening when a congenital syndrome is suspected","Echocardiography to exclude structural heart disease"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: torsades de pointes, which may cause syncope and can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation and cardiac arrest","Syncope or seizures in a patient with a prolonged QT, indicating a high risk of malignant arrhythmia","Family history of sudden cardiac death at a young age","Marked QT prolongation compounded by multiple QT-prolonging drugs or profound electrolyte disturbance"],"prognosis":"With recognition, avoidance of QT-prolonging triggers, and appropriate therapy including beta-blockade or a defibrillator, prognosis is generally favourable; risk of sudden death rises with greater QT prolongation, prior syncope or arrhythmia, and specific high-risk genotypes.","pitfalls":["Failing to correct the QT for heart rate or missing intermittent prolongation on a single resting ECG","Overlooking cumulative QT effects when several QT-prolonging drugs or electrolyte disturbances coexist","Attributing syncope or seizures to a neurological cause without measuring the QT","Neglecting family screening once a congenital syndrome is diagnosed"],"management":["Withdraw all QT-prolonging drugs and correct hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and hypocalcaemia, the mainstay for acquired long QT syndrome","For congenital disease, start beta-blockade with a non-selective agent such as propranolol or nadolol and advise activity and trigger modification","Treat torsades de pointes acutely with intravenous magnesium, electrolyte correction, and rate support using temporary pacing or isoprenaline for pause-dependent, bradycardia-related episodes","Provide immediate defibrillation for sustained polymorphic ventricular tachycardia or cardiac arrest","Implant a cardioverter-defibrillator for arrest survivors or those with recurrent syncope or arrhythmia despite therapy, and consider left cardiac sympathetic denervation in refractory cases","Educate patients to avoid QT-prolonging medications lifelong and to maintain electrolyte balance during illness","Offer genetic testing and cascade family screening when a congenital syndrome is suspected, with ECG evaluation of relatives","Refer to a cardiologist or inherited cardiac conditions service and provide resuscitation planning for high-risk individuals"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"long_qt_syndrome_type_1":{"name":"Long QT syndrome type 1","system":"Cardiac channelopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LQT1","Romano-Ward syndrome type 1","KCNQ1-related long QT syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Inherited arrhythmia and cardiac electrophysiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by loss-of-function variants in KCNQ1, which encodes the Kv7.1 alpha subunit carrying the slow delayed rectifier potassium current (IKs); it is the commonest genotype of congenital long QT syndrome.","IKs is the principal current recruited to shorten repolarisation during sympathetic activation, so patients characteristically fail to shorten (or paradoxically prolong) the QT interval with exercise and are prone to events during physical exertion.","Swimming and diving into cold water are near-signature triggers; a young person with syncope or a near-drowning episode while swimming should prompt consideration of LQT1.","The resting ECG typically shows a broad-based, smooth, prolonged T wave.","Beta-blockers are more protective in LQT1 than in any other subtype, and good adherence markedly reduces cardiac events.","Biallelic (homozygous or compound heterozygous) KCNQ1 variants produce Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome, combining profound congenital sensorineural deafness with a severe, markedly prolonged QT phenotype.","A substantial proportion of gene carriers have a normal or borderline resting QT interval (concealed LQT1), so genetic testing and cascade family screening remain valuable despite a non-diagnostic ECG.","Participation in competitive sport, especially swimming, warrants individualised risk assessment, supervision and precautions."],"pathophysiology":"LQT1 arises from loss-of-function variants in KCNQ1, encoding the Kv7.1 pore-forming subunit that co-assembles with the KCNE1 (minK) accessory subunit to conduct the slow delayed rectifier potassium current, IKs. IKs activates slowly and constitutes the main repolarisation reserve recruited when heart rate rises and beta-adrenergic tone increases, being augmented by protein kinase A phosphorylation. When IKs is reduced through haploinsufficiency or dominant-negative subunit interactions, the ventricular action potential fails to shorten appropriately during sympathetic surges; the QT interval lengthens rather than adapts, transmural dispersion of repolarisation widens, and early afterdepolarisations can trigger torsades de pointes. This mechanism explains the strong adrenergic and exertional trigger profile. Inheritance is autosomal dominant in Romano-Ward syndrome; because Kv7.1/IKs also drives endolymph production in the stria vascularis of the inner ear, biallelic loss of function causes the Jervell and Lange-Nielsen phenotype of congenital deafness with severe QT prolongation.","additionalDifferentials":["Other congenital long QT subtypes, particularly LQT2 and LQT3","Acquired or drug-induced QT prolongation","Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (exertional events with a normal resting QT)","Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome when congenital deafness coexists","Neurocardiogenic or vasovagal syncope","Electrolyte-related QT prolongation (hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hypocalcaemia)","Structural causes of exertional syncope such as hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Twelve-lead ECG with QT interval measurement and T-wave morphology assessment","Exercise stress testing examining QT adaptation, looking for failure to shorten or paradoxical prolongation during recovery","Ambulatory Holter monitoring","Targeted or panel genetic testing including KCNQ1, with cascade screening of relatives","Serum electrolytes including potassium, magnesium and calcium","Audiological assessment when biallelic disease or deafness is suspected","Adrenaline (catecholamine) provocation testing in selected diagnostic uncertainty"],"redFlags":["Syncope or seizure-like collapse during exertion, particularly while swimming","Documented torsades de pointes or aborted cardiac arrest","Family history of sudden unexplained death, drowning, or sudden infant death","Very marked QT prolongation or T-wave alternans on the ECG","Recurrent syncopal events despite beta-blockade"],"prognosis":"With beta-blocker therapy and avoidance of exertional and adrenergic triggers the prognosis is generally favourable, and per-event lethality is lower than in LQT3. Residual risk rises with greater QT prolongation, prior syncope, young age at first event and poor treatment adherence.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal resting QT excludes disease, when concealed LQT1 is common","Failing to recognise swimming-related syncope as a hallmark warning sign","Discontinuing beta-blockers or introducing QT-prolonging medicines","Overlooking audiological review and biallelic testing when deafness coexists","Relying on a resting ECG alone and missing failure of QT shortening on exercise"],"management":["Establish beta-blockade with a non-selective agent such as nadolol or propranolol as first-line therapy, more protective in this subtype than any other, and stress strict adherence","Advise avoidance of unsupervised swimming and diving and modification of strenuous or competitive exertion, the characteristic triggers","Correct and maintain potassium, magnesium and calcium, and avoid QT-prolonging drugs lifelong","Treat acute torsades de pointes with intravenous magnesium and electrolyte correction, and defibrillate sustained polymorphic ventricular tachycardia or arrest","Add left cardiac sympathetic denervation or an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator for breakthrough events, prior arrest or high-risk features despite beta-blockade","Offer targeted genetic testing of KCNQ1 with cascade screening of relatives, and assess for biallelic Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome with audiological evaluation","Manage through an inherited cardiac conditions service and escalate for syncope, documented arrhythmia or poor treatment response"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cardiac electrophysiology reference texts","International inherited arrhythmia / long QT syndrome consensus guidelines","Clinical cardiovascular genetics references"],"crossLinks":[]},"long_qt_syndrome_type_2":{"name":"Long QT syndrome type 2","system":"Cardiac channelopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LQT2","Romano-Ward syndrome type 2","KCNH2-related long QT syndrome","hERG-related long QT syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Inherited arrhythmia and cardiac electrophysiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by loss-of-function variants in KCNH2 (hERG, Kv11.1), reducing the rapid delayed rectifier potassium current (IKr); it is the second commonest long QT syndrome genotype.","Characteristic triggers are sudden auditory stimuli (alarm clocks, telephones, doorbells) and abrupt emotional arousal; events also occur at rest and during sleep more often than in LQT1.","The postpartum period carries a particularly high arrhythmic risk in women with LQT2, warranting planned surveillance.","The resting ECG typically shows low-amplitude, notched or bifid T waves.","The hERG channel is the molecular target of most QT-prolonging drugs, so LQT2 patients are especially vulnerable to drug-induced torsades and to hypokalaemia.","IKr is paradoxically reduced by low extracellular potassium, so maintaining potassium and correcting hypokalaemia are protective, and potassium repletion can be beneficial.","Beta-blockers reduce events but are somewhat less protective than in LQT1; minimising sudden loud alarms and startle exposure remains important.","Concealed forms with borderline resting QT exist, so genetic testing and cascade family screening add value."],"pathophysiology":"LQT2 results from loss-of-function variants in KCNH2, which encodes the hERG (Kv11.1) subunit forming the rapid delayed rectifier potassium current, IKr, the dominant current driving phase-3 repolarisation of the ventricular action potential. Many pathogenic variants act by impairing channel trafficking, so misfolded protein is retained in the endoplasmic reticulum and fails to reach the membrane, rather than by altering pore conduction alone. Reduced IKr prolongs the action potential and QT interval, increases dispersion of repolarisation and permits early afterdepolarisations that initiate torsades de pointes. IKr is unusually sensitive to extracellular potassium, so hypokalaemia further suppresses the current and heightens risk. The channel is also promiscuously blocked by numerous drugs, making hERG the principal mediator of acquired QT prolongation. Inheritance is autosomal dominant (Romano-Ward). The distinctive auditory, startle and emotional trigger profile, together with heightened postpartum risk, reflects autonomic and hormonal influences on repolarisation reserve.","additionalDifferentials":["Other congenital long QT subtypes, particularly LQT1 and LQT3","Acquired drug-induced QT prolongation from hERG blockade","Electrolyte disturbance (hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hypocalcaemia)","Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","Neurocardiogenic or startle-induced (vasovagal) syncope","Epilepsy misdiagnosed in place of convulsive arrhythmic syncope","Peripartum causes of collapse including postpartum cardiomyopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Twelve-lead ECG with attention to notched or bifid, low-amplitude T-wave morphology","Serum potassium, magnesium and calcium","Ambulatory Holter and symptom-triggered event monitoring","Genetic testing including KCNH2, with cascade family screening","Review of the medication list for QT-prolonging (hERG-blocking) agents","Exercise and recovery ECG, with adrenaline provocation in selected cases","Postpartum monitoring and risk-management planning in affected women"],"redFlags":["Syncope triggered by sudden noise (alarm clock, telephone) or intense emotion","Arrhythmic events occurring in the postpartum period","Documented torsades de pointes or aborted cardiac arrest","Family history of sudden unexplained death","Symptomatic exposure to QT-prolonging drugs or hypokalaemia"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally favourable with beta-blockade, strict avoidance of QT-prolonging drugs, maintenance of potassium and reduction of auditory and emotional triggers. The postpartum months and periods of drug or electrolyte challenge are windows of heightened arrhythmic risk.","pitfalls":["Prescribing hERG-blocking, QT-prolonging medicines","Failing to anticipate and monitor the postpartum high-risk window","Tolerating hypokalaemia rather than actively correcting it","Assuming a normal resting QT excludes disease","Leaving loud alarm clocks or telephones at the bedside despite the auditory-trigger profile"],"management":["Start beta-blockade with a non-selective agent such as nadolol or propranolol as first-line therapy and reinforce adherence","Maintain and replete potassium, since the IKr current is suppressed by hypokalaemia, and correct magnesium","Reduce startle triggers by removing alarm clocks, telephones and doorbells from the bedroom and counselling against sudden auditory stimuli","Rigorously avoid QT-prolonging hERG-blocking drugs, to which these patients are especially vulnerable","Institute planned surveillance and continued beta-blockade through the high-risk postpartum period","Treat torsades de pointes acutely with intravenous magnesium and potassium repletion, and defibrillate sustained arrhythmia or arrest","Add left cardiac sympathetic denervation or an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator for recurrent events or high-risk features","Offer KCNH2 genetic testing with cascade family screening and manage through an inherited cardiac conditions service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cardiac electrophysiology reference texts","International inherited arrhythmia / long QT syndrome consensus guidelines","Clinical cardiovascular genetics references"],"crossLinks":[]},"long_qt_syndrome_type_3":{"name":"Long QT syndrome type 3","system":"Cardiac channelopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LQT3","Romano-Ward syndrome type 3","SCN5A-related long QT syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Inherited arrhythmia and cardiac electrophysiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by gain-of-function variants in SCN5A (Nav1.5) that impair inactivation of the cardiac sodium channel, producing a persistent late inward sodium current (late INa).","Less common than LQT1 and LQT2 but tends to carry the highest lethality per cardiac event.","Events characteristically occur at rest, during sleep or with bradycardia rather than on exertion, the opposite trigger profile to LQT1.","The resting ECG classically shows a long, flat isoelectric ST segment with a late-appearing, often narrow or peaked T wave.","Because QT lengthens at slow heart rates (reverse rate dependence), bradycardia and pauses are proarrhythmic and can precipitate torsades de pointes.","Late-sodium-current blockers such as mexiletine (and flecainide for selected variants) can shorten the QT interval and are used as adjuncts, sometimes guided by an acute drug-response assessment.","Beta-blockers are used but are less consistently protective than in LQT1 and LQT2.","SCN5A is allelic with Brugada syndrome, progressive cardiac conduction disease and sinus node dysfunction, so overlapping or mixed sodium-channel phenotypes occur."],"pathophysiology":"LQT3 is caused by gain-of-function variants in SCN5A, encoding Nav1.5, the cardiac voltage-gated sodium channel responsible for the rapid phase-0 depolarisation of the action potential. The variants disrupt fast inactivation of the channel, so a small fraction of channels repeatedly reopen or fail to close during the plateau, generating a sustained late (persistent) sodium current. This inward current prolongs action potential duration and the QT interval, an effect that is exaggerated at slow heart rates (reverse rate dependence), thereby favouring early afterdepolarisations and torsades de pointes when the heart is slow, at rest or during sleep. This stands in contrast to loss-of-function SCN5A variants, which reduce sodium current and cause Brugada syndrome and conduction disease; because the same gene can produce opposing functional effects, overlap syndromes with mixed features are recognised. Inheritance is autosomal dominant, and the nocturnal, bradycardia-dependent event pattern is a direct consequence of the reverse-rate-dependent repolarisation delay.","additionalDifferentials":["Other congenital long QT subtypes, particularly LQT1 and LQT2","Brugada syndrome and other SCN5A overlap or conduction disorders","Acquired or drug-induced QT prolongation","Electrolyte disturbances affecting repolarisation","Sinus node dysfunction or bradyarrhythmia causing pause-dependent torsades","Sudden unexplained nocturnal death and sudden infant death","Structural heart disease with secondary repolarisation abnormality"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Twelve-lead ECG assessing ST-segment length and the late-appearing T-wave morphology","Ambulatory Holter monitoring to capture rest and night-time events and rate dependence","Genetic testing including SCN5A, with cascade family screening","Serum electrolytes","Evaluation for overlapping Brugada or conduction-system phenotype","Acute late-sodium-current-blocker (for example mexiletine) response testing for QT shortening","Exercise testing to demonstrate rate-dependent QT behaviour"],"redFlags":["Syncope or cardiac arrest occurring at rest or during sleep","Extremely long QT with pause-dependent torsades de pointes","Family history of sudden nocturnal death or sudden infant death","Symptomatic marked bradycardia","Aborted cardiac arrest or documented torsades de pointes"],"prognosis":"Per-event lethality is the highest of the classic subtypes. With late-sodium-current blockade, prevention of bradycardia and pauses (including pacing where indicated) and defibrillator therapy for high-risk individuals, outcomes improve, but the malignant nocturnal and rest-related event profile warrants sustained vigilance.","pitfalls":["Offering false reassurance from a negative exercise test, since events are rest and sleep related","Permitting bradycardia or long pauses, which are proarrhythmic in this subtype","Relying on beta-blockers alone for protection","Overlooking a Brugada or conduction overlap phenotype, especially before using sodium channel blockers","Assuming a borderline resting QT excludes disease"],"management":["Use late-sodium-current blockade with mexiletine, or flecainide for selected variants, to shorten the QT interval, ideally guided by an acute drug-response assessment","Add beta-blockade as adjunctive therapy, recognising it is less protective than in the potassium-channel subtypes","Prevent bradycardia and pauses, which are proarrhythmic through reverse rate dependence, using cardiac pacing where indicated","Avoid QT-prolonging drugs and maintain normal electrolytes","Implant a cardioverter-defibrillator for high-risk individuals given the high per-event lethality and the malignant rest and nocturnal event profile","Treat acute torsades de pointes with intravenous magnesium and rate support, and defibrillate sustained polymorphic ventricular tachycardia or arrest","Offer SCN5A genetic testing with cascade family screening and evaluate for an overlapping Brugada or conduction-system phenotype","Manage through an inherited cardiac conditions service and escalate for syncope or documented arrhythmia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cardiac electrophysiology reference texts","International inherited arrhythmia / long QT syndrome consensus guidelines","Clinical cardiovascular genetics references"],"crossLinks":[]},"longitudinal_melanonychia":{"name":"Longitudinal melanonychia","system":"Dermatology / Nail","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["melanonychia striata","longitudinal pigmented band","melanonychia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Longitudinal melanonychia is a brown-to-black pigmented band running lengthwise along the nail, caused by increased melanin deposition within the plate","It has many benign causes, including racial or ethnic pigmentation (often multiple nails in darker skin), melanocytic activation, drugs, trauma, and benign naevi","The crucial task is to separate benign bands from those arising from a subungual melanoma, especially when a single nail is involved in a fair-skinned adult","Worrying features can be remembered with the ABCDEF guide (age and ethnicity, band characteristics, change, digit involved, extension of pigment, family or personal history)","Hutchinson sign, spread of pigment onto the proximal or lateral nail fold, is an important warning sign for melanoma, though a pseudo-Hutchinson sign can mislead","Onychoscopy helps by showing regular parallel lines in benign activation versus irregular, variably coloured lines with disrupted parallelism in melanoma","A suspicious band requires a nail matrix biopsy for histology, as pigment alone cannot reliably distinguish benign from malignant"],"pathophysiology":"Longitudinal melanonychia occurs when melanin is deposited into the developing nail plate by melanocytes in the nail matrix, producing a pigmented streak that grows out along the length of the nail. The pigment may reflect simple activation of matrix melanocytes without an increase in their number, as seen with racial pigmentation, drugs, trauma, or inflammation, or a true increase in melanocytes such as a benign naevus or, importantly, a melanoma. Because a malignant melanocytic proliferation in the matrix can present in exactly the same way as a benign band, the pattern, regularity, and behaviour of the pigment, rather than its mere presence, guide the assessment.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign melanocytic activation (racial, drug-induced, traumatic, inflammatory)","Nail matrix naevus","Subungual melanoma","Subungual haematoma (usually non-longitudinal and moves out with growth)","Fungal or bacterial pigmentation (for example Pseudomonas or dematiaceous fungi)","Pigmentation from systemic disease or endocrinopathy","Exogenous staining of the nail"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Onychoscopy (dermoscopy of the nail) to assess the pattern and regularity of pigmented lines","Assessment for Hutchinson sign and periungual pigment spread","Nail matrix biopsy for histology of a suspicious or changing band","Photographic documentation to monitor change over time","Review of drug history and examination of other nails and skin","Mycology or microbiology if an infective pigment source is suspected"],"redFlags":["A single broad, irregular, or widening band in an adult, particularly of the thumb, index finger, or great toe","Hutchinson sign with pigment extending onto the nail folds","Variegated colour, blurred borders, or loss of parallelism on onychoscopy","Associated nail dystrophy, bleeding, or a nodule suggesting invasive melanoma","Rapid change in the width or colour of an established band"],"prognosis":"Most bands are benign and require only monitoring, but subungual melanoma carries a serious prognosis that worsens with delayed diagnosis, so appropriately timed biopsy of suspicious lesions is critical to outcome.","pitfalls":["Reassuring a patient without biopsy when a single band shows suspicious features","Relying on pigment colour alone rather than pattern, change, and histology","Misinterpreting a pseudo-Hutchinson sign or, conversely, dismissing a true one","Assuming multiple bands in darker skin are always benign without examining each nail","Delaying matrix biopsy for fear of nail deformity in a potentially malignant lesion"],"management":["Stratify risk clinically and with onychoscopy using the ABCDEF features and the Hutchinson sign to separate benign bands from possible subungual melanoma","Reassure and observe benign patterns such as racial pigmentation or multiple-nail involvement, using photographic documentation to monitor for change","Identify and withdraw provoking causes such as offending drugs, and address trauma or inflammation driving melanocytic activation","Perform a nail matrix biopsy for histology of any single, changing, irregular or worrying band to exclude melanoma","Refer confirmed subungual melanoma for definitive surgical excision and oncological staging","Arrange dermatology referral and timely re-review of suspicious or evolving lesions, escalating to biopsy rather than delaying"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"loose_anagen_syndrome":{"name":"Loose anagen syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Trichology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LAS","loose anagen hair syndrome","LAHS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disorder mainly of young children in which anagen hairs are poorly anchored and can be pulled out painlessly with little force","It typically presents in fair-haired girls aged around two to six with sparse, dull, unruly hair that seems never to need cutting and does not grow long","The hair-pull test easily extracts numerous anagen hairs, and light microscopy shows anagen roots lacking sheaths with a ruffled proximal cuticle and a distorted floppy-sock appearance","Trichoscopy may show rectangular black granular structures and solitary yellow dots","It is non-scarring with preserved follicular ostia, and hair anchorage tends to improve as the child grows","It is usually sporadic, though familial cases with autosomal dominant inheritance and associations with certain genetic syndromes are described","No specific treatment is required beyond gentle hair care and reassurance, as the condition generally improves spontaneously through childhood"],"pathophysiology":"Loose anagen syndrome results from defective anchorage of the hair shaft within the follicle owing to abnormal or premature keratinisation of the inner root sheath, so that cohesion between the shaft and the surrounding sheaths is impaired and anagen hairs are easily and painlessly extracted. Mutations affecting the companion-layer and inner-root-sheath keratin, including K6HF (KRT75), have been implicated. Because the follicles are structurally intact and not destroyed, the process is non-scarring and anchorage typically strengthens with maturity.","additionalDifferentials":["Telogen effluvium, with club hairs, older patients and a trigger history","Alopecia areata, which is patchy with exclamation-mark hairs","Short anagen syndrome","Hair-shaft disorders causing fragility such as trichorrhexis nodosa","Trichotillomania"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gentle hair-pull test extracting numerous anagen hairs","Light microscopy (trichogram) showing sheathless anagen roots with ruffled cuticles","Trichoscopy for solitary yellow dots and black granular structures","Assessment for associated ectodermal or syndromic features","Reassurance-based follow-up to document improvement with age"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting an associated genetic syndrome such as dysmorphism or ectodermal defects","Progressive loss or scarring, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Onset or persistence into adulthood requiring exclusion of other causes"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and non-scarring, with hair anchorage and density generally improving spontaneously as the child grows, although hair may remain somewhat sparse or slow-growing.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting easily pulled hairs as alopecia areata or an effluvium","Overlooking associated syndromic features in familial cases","Subjecting children to unnecessary treatment for a self-limiting condition","Failing to perform trichogram microscopy, which is the key diagnostic test"],"management":["Reassure families that this is a benign, non-scarring condition in which hair anchorage and density usually improve spontaneously as the child grows","Advise gentle hair care, avoiding traction, harsh brushing and chemical or heat treatments that dislodge the poorly anchored anagen hairs","Confirm the diagnosis with the hair-pull test and trichogram rather than pursuing unnecessary investigation, avoiding an erroneous label of alopecia areata","Consider topical minoxidil in persistent or cosmetically troublesome cases, recognising the evidence is limited","Assess for associated ectodermal or syndromic features and refer to clinical genetics if a broader syndrome is suspected","Provide dermatology follow-up to document improvement with age"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"loricrin_keratoderma":{"name":"Loricrin keratoderma","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Vohwinkel syndrome variant with ichthyosis","mutilating keratoderma with ichthyosis","LOR"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant disorder of keratinisation caused by variants in LOR, which encodes loricrin, a major protein of the cornified cell envelope","Presents as a mutilating (Vohwinkel-like) palmoplantar keratoderma combined with a generalised ichthyosis","The palmoplantar keratoderma typically has a honeycomb surface and may show starfish-shaped keratoses over the knuckles","Constricting fibrous bands (pseudo-ainhum) can form around digits and threaten autoamputation","Unlike classic connexin-related Vohwinkel syndrome, the loricrin variant is associated with ichthyosis rather than sensorineural deafness","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by biopsy showing hyperkeratosis with retained nuclei and by LOR molecular testing","Management is supportive with keratolytics, emollients and foot care, oral retinoids for severe keratoderma, and surgical release of constricting bands where necessary"],"pathophysiology":"Loricrin keratoderma arises from frameshift variants in LOR that alter loricrin, the principal structural protein of the cornified cell envelope. The mutant protein carries an abnormal carboxy-terminal extension with a nuclear localisation signal, causing it to be mistargeted to the nucleus rather than incorporated correctly into the cornified envelope. Defective envelope assembly impairs the epidermal barrier and terminal differentiation, producing generalised ichthyosis and a thick mutilating palmoplantar keratoderma, with chronic fibrosis around digits generating pseudo-ainhum.","additionalDifferentials":["Vohwinkel syndrome due to GJB2 (connexin 26) with deafness","Mal de Meleda","Olmsted syndrome","Other mutilating keratodermas","Bart-Pumphrey syndrome","Diffuse epidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of LOR","Skin biopsy showing hyperkeratosis with retained nuclei (parakeratosis) in the stratum corneum","Examination for pseudo-ainhum and starfish keratoses","Audiological assessment to distinguish from connexin-related Vohwinkel syndrome","Family history and pedigree analysis","Assessment of foot and hand function"],"redFlags":["Constricting bands (pseudo-ainhum) risking digital autoamputation","Deep secondary infection of fissured keratoderma","Rapid or asymmetrical change raising concern for malignant transformation","Severe functional impairment of hands or feet"],"prognosis":"The disorder is lifelong; with keratolytic and emollient care, retinoids and timely surgical release of constricting bands most patients preserve function, though pseudo-ainhum and disability can complicate severe disease.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with connexin-related Vohwinkel syndrome and overlooking the associated ichthyosis","Missing early pseudo-ainhum before irreversible digital constriction","Failing to arrange audiology to distinguish the two forms of mutilating keratoderma","Delaying surgical release of constricting bands"],"management":["Provide intensive topical care with emollients and keratolytics such as urea or salicylic acid to soften the palmoplantar keratoderma and ichthyosis","Use systemic retinoids such as acitretin to reduce hyperkeratosis in severe disease, monitoring for their side effects","Arrange timely surgical or medical release of constricting fibrous bands (pseudo-ainhum) to prevent digital strangulation and autoamputation","Treat secondary bacterial or fungal infection of fissured hyperkeratotic skin","Support hand and foot function with podiatry, physiotherapy and appropriate footwear","Distinguish from connexin-related Vohwinkel syndrome with audiological assessment given the differing associations","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant LOR disorder with family screening","Coordinate lifelong care through dermatology, clinical genetics and surgical teams, escalating urgently for threatened digits"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"louse_borne_relapsing_fever":{"name":"Louse-borne relapsing fever","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LBRF","epidemic relapsing fever","Borrelia recurrentis infection","spirochaetal fever"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by Borrelia recurrentis and transmitted by the human body louse; infection follows crushing of the louse so that its coelomic fluid contaminates broken skin or mucosae rather than the bite itself","Epidemic in conditions of overcrowding, poverty, cold and poor hygiene, and re-emerging among refugees and migrants travelling from endemic regions of the Horn of Africa","After an incubation of about a week there is sudden high fever, rigors, severe headache, myalgia and prostration lasting several days, followed by a crisis and an afebrile interval before relapse","Compared with the tick-borne form it typically causes fewer relapses, often only one or two, but tends to be more severe with a higher untreated mortality","Jaundice, a bleeding tendency with petechiae, hepatosplenomegaly and neurological involvement are prominent features of severe disease","Diagnosis is made by demonstrating abundant spirochaetes on a Giemsa- or Wright-stained peripheral blood film taken during fever","A single dose of an appropriate antibiotic can be curative, but the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction after treatment is frequent and can be life-threatening, so patients must be closely monitored"],"pathophysiology":"Borrelia recurrentis is a spirochaete confined to humans and spread by the body louse. Once inoculated it proliferates to produce intense spirochaetaemia driving the febrile episode. Host antibody responses clear the dominant antigenic variant, but antigenic variation allows escape variants to expand and cause relapse, though fewer cycles occur than in the tick-borne disease. Heavy spirochaetal loads and the associated inflammatory response cause hepatic dysfunction with jaundice, thrombocytopenia and bleeding, splenic enlargement and neurological involvement. Antibiotic-induced mass lysis of spirochaetes releases inflammatory mediators, producing the often severe Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction with abrupt rigors, hyperpyrexia and haemodynamic instability.","additionalDifferentials":["Tick-borne relapsing fever","Malaria","Typhus and other rickettsioses","Leptospirosis","Typhoid fever","Viral haemorrhagic fever in relevant epidemiological settings","Yellow fever"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thick and thin peripheral blood films during fever showing numerous spirochaetes","PCR of blood for Borrelia recurrentis where available","Full blood count for thrombocytopenia and anaemia","Liver enzymes and bilirubin","Coagulation studies given the bleeding tendency","Examination of clothing for body lice to support the diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Severe Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction with hyperpyrexia, rigors and hypotension after the first dose","Jaundice with a marked bleeding tendency","Neurological deterioration or meningism","Myocarditis or circulatory collapse","Occurrence in a crowded refugee or migrant population indicating epidemic potential"],"prognosis":"Louse-borne relapsing fever is more severe than the tick-borne form and untreated epidemics carry high mortality, but prompt antibiotic treatment is very effective; the main early hazard of treatment is the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, which itself can be fatal without supportive care.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the severity and life-threatening potential of the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction","Not examining blood films during fever, missing the abundant spirochaetes","Failing to recognise the diagnosis in migrants from endemic regions","Neglecting delousing and public-health control measures","Confusing it with malaria and delaying appropriate antibiotics and monitoring"],"management":["Treat with an appropriate antibiotic such as a tetracycline (doxycycline) or a macrolide such as erythromycin, with penicillin as an alternative, to clear the spirochaetaemia","Anticipate and manage the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction after the first dose with close monitoring, fluid resuscitation and supportive care, as it can be life-threatening","Provide supportive care for hepatic dysfunction, thrombocytopenia and bleeding, with fluids and blood-product support as required","Decontaminate the patient by delousing, treating clothing and improving hygiene to eliminate the body-louse vector and prevent reinfection","Screen and treat close contacts and notify public health authorities given the epidemic potential in overcrowded settings","Monitor for relapse during the afebrile interval and escalate for haemodynamic instability, severe bleeding or neurological deterioration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"low_grade_fibromyxoid_sarcoma":{"name":"Low-grade fibromyxoid sarcoma","system":"Oncology / Pathology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGFMS","Evans tumour","hyalinizing spindle cell tumour with giant rosettes","FUS-CREB3L2 sarcoma","low-grade fibromyxoid sarcoma (FUS-CREB3L2)"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A deceptively bland-looking sarcoma that, despite its innocuous histology, has genuine metastatic potential, often after a long interval","Typically presents as a painless deep soft-tissue mass in the trunk, limbs or limb girdles of young to middle-aged adults","Histology shows alternating fibrous and myxoid zones with bland spindle cells arranged in whorls and characteristic arcades of small curvilinear vessels","The great majority carry a FUS-CREB3L2 gene fusion, with a smaller proportion showing FUS-CREB3L1 or EWSR1-CREB3L1","Strong and diffuse MUC4 immunohistochemical expression is a highly sensitive and specific diagnostic marker","The hyalinizing spindle cell tumour with giant rosettes is a morphological variant within the same entity, featuring collagen rosettes","Because the cells look benign, the diagnosis is easily missed unless MUC4 staining or molecular testing is applied"],"pathophysiology":"Low-grade fibromyxoid sarcoma is a fibroblastic malignancy defined by a recurrent gene fusion, most often FUS-CREB3L2, which joins an RNA-binding protein gene to a transcription-factor gene and deregulates downstream transcriptional programmes. This single driver produces a tumour of monotonous, cytologically bland spindle cells set in alternating collagenous and myxoid matrix. The fusion upregulates expression of MUC4, providing a reliable diagnostic marker, and although proliferation is low the tumour retains the capacity for late local recurrence and distant metastasis, underscoring its fully malignant nature.","additionalDifferentials":["Desmoid-type fibromatosis","Perineurioma and other benign nerve sheath tumours","Sclerosing epithelioid fibrosarcoma (which shares MUC4 expression and fusions)","Low-grade myxofibrosarcoma","Neurofibroma and schwannoma","Solitary fibrous tumour","Cellular myxoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MUC4 immunohistochemistry as the key diagnostic screen","FISH or molecular testing for FUS and CREB3L2/CREB3L1 rearrangements","Panel to exclude nerve sheath tumours and fibromatosis (S100, SOX10, beta-catenin)","MRI to define the deep soft-tissue mass and plan resection","Staging chest imaging given the risk of late pulmonary metastasis","Long-term imaging surveillance because recurrence and metastasis can occur years later"],"redFlags":["Late local recurrence or distant metastasis, often to the lungs, sometimes after many years","Increasing size or deep fixation of a previously stable mass","Areas of increased cellularity or higher-grade transformation within the tumour","Incomplete excision margins predisposing to recurrence"],"prognosis":"Although low-grade fibromyxoid sarcoma grows slowly and looks histologically bland, it can recur locally and metastasise, characteristically to the lungs and sometimes decades after initial treatment; complete wide excision offers the best chance of cure, and prolonged follow-up is essential because the risk of late relapse persists.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the bland cytology as a benign fibrous or nerve sheath lesion","Omitting MUC4 staining, which readily distinguishes it from benign mimics","Discontinuing surveillance too early and missing late metastasis","Confusing it with sclerosing epithelioid fibrosarcoma, which overlaps molecularly and by MUC4","Underestimating malignant potential because of the low mitotic rate"],"management":["Achieve complete wide local excision with clear margins, the mainstay of treatment and the best chance of cure","Consider adjuvant radiotherapy for close or positive margins or when adequate re-excision is not feasible","Confirm the diagnosis with MUC4 immunohistochemistry and FUS-CREB3L2 fusion testing to guide management","Manage all cases through a specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team","Recognise the limited role of conventional chemotherapy, reserving systemic therapy for unresectable or metastatic disease","Maintain prolonged imaging surveillance of the primary site and the chest, since local recurrence and pulmonary metastasis can occur many years later","Refer isolated resectable pulmonary metastases for metastasectomy where appropriate and escalate to systemic oncology for widespread disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","WHO Classification of Tumours: Skin Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"lowe_syndrome":{"name":"Lowe syndrome","system":"Renal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oculocerebrorenal syndrome","oculocerebrorenal syndrome of Lowe","OCRL","Lowe oculocerebrorenal syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / OMIM","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked multisystem disorder affecting males, caused by loss-of-function variants in OCRL, which encodes a phosphatidylinositol phosphatase","Defined by a triad of eye, brain and kidney involvement: congenital cataracts, neurological impairment and a renal proximal tubulopathy","Congenital bilateral cataracts are present from birth and glaucoma may develop, so early ophthalmological assessment is essential","Neurological features include neonatal hypotonia, developmental delay, intellectual disability and, in some, behavioural problems and seizures","The kidney phenotype is a proximal (Fanconi-type) tubulopathy with low-molecular-weight proteinuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, bicarbonate wasting and hypercalciuria","OCRL variants also underlie Dent disease 2, and the two conditions represent a phenotypic spectrum of the same gene","Female carriers are usually asymptomatic but may show characteristic lenticular opacities on slit-lamp examination"],"pathophysiology":"Lowe syndrome is caused by loss of function of OCRL, which encodes an inositol polyphosphate 5-phosphatase that regulates phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate at the Golgi, endosomes and primary cilium. Disrupted phosphoinositide metabolism impairs membrane trafficking, actin cytoskeletal organisation and endocytosis in multiple tissues. In the proximal tubule this compromises receptor-mediated endocytosis of filtered proteins, producing low-molecular-weight proteinuria and a generalised Fanconi-type tubulopathy; in the developing lens and brain the same trafficking defects lead to congenital cataract and neurodevelopmental impairment. The overlap with Dent disease 2 reflects a shared molecular basis with differing severity of extrarenal involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Dent disease 2 (OCRL)","Cystinosis and other causes of renal Fanconi syndrome","Galactosaemia (cataract with metabolic disease)","Congenital infection causing cataract and neurological impairment","Other inherited proximal tubulopathies","Mitochondrial disease with multisystem involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ophthalmological examination for congenital cataract and glaucoma","Assessment of proximal tubular function including low-molecular-weight proteinuria, aminoaciduria, phosphate, bicarbonate and urinary calcium","Genetic testing of OCRL","Developmental and neurological evaluation, with brain imaging where indicated","Serial kidney function and growth monitoring","Slit-lamp examination of the mother to detect carrier lens opacities"],"redFlags":["Untreated congenital cataract threatening visual development, or rising intraocular pressure","Failure to thrive and growth failure from tubulopathy and acidosis","Progressive decline in kidney function","Seizures or significant electrolyte and acid-base disturbance"],"prognosis":"Lowe syndrome is a chronic multisystem condition; the proximal tubulopathy and, later, progressive chronic kidney disease, together with visual and neurodevelopmental impairment, determine outcome, and life expectancy is reduced though many survive into adulthood with supportive care.","pitfalls":["Delaying cataract surgery and vision support, harming neurodevelopmental outcome","Failing to recognise and treat the Fanconi-type tubulopathy and its metabolic consequences","Not appreciating the OCRL spectrum linking Lowe syndrome and Dent disease 2","Overlooking carrier detection in the mother by slit-lamp examination"],"management":["Correct the proximal (Fanconi) tubulopathy with alkali such as bicarbonate or citrate for the acidosis, oral phosphate and active vitamin D (calcitriol) for the phosphaturic bone disease, and potassium replacement","Arrange early cataract surgery for the congenital bilateral cataracts and lifelong ophthalmological monitoring and treatment of glaucoma","Provide neurodevelopmental support with physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy, and treat seizures and behavioural problems as they arise","Monitor kidney function and growth serially and manage progressive chronic kidney disease, providing renal replacement therapy as it advances","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across nephrology, ophthalmology, neurology, clinical genetics and allied therapists","Offer genetic counselling for this X-linked disorder, with carrier testing of the mother including slit-lamp examination for lens opacities and cascade family screening","Escalate for acute metabolic derangement, uncontrolled glaucoma or declining renal function"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","OMIM: Lowe oculocerebrorenal syndrome (OCRL)"],"crossLinks":[]},"loxoscelism":{"name":"Loxoscelism (recluse spider envenomation)","system":"Multisystem / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["brown recluse spider bite","necrotic arachnidism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Recluse spider bites can cause a locally necrotic skin lesion (cutaneous loxoscelism) and, less often, a systemic (viscerocutaneous) illness","The lesion often evolves over days from an erythematous area to a central blister and then a dark eschar with surrounding pallor and redness","Systemic loxoscelism can include haemolysis, disseminated intravascular coagulation and acute kidney injury, more often in children","Definitive diagnosis is difficult because the spider is rarely captured and many necrotic lesions are wrongly blamed on spiders","Most cutaneous lesions heal with supportive wound care alone","Because presentation overlaps with infection, careful reassessment is needed before attributing necrosis to a spider"],"pathophysiology":"Recluse spider venom contains sphingomyelinase D, which damages cell membranes and activates complement and neutrophils, producing local dermonecrosis and, when venom effects are systemic, intravascular haemolysis and a coagulopathy. The characteristic necrotic ulcer results from vascular injury, thrombosis and inflammatory tissue destruction at the bite site. Systemic loxoscelism reflects widespread red-cell membrane injury and immune activation.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial skin and soft-tissue infection or abscess","Necrotising fasciitis","Cutaneous anthrax or other necrotic infections","Pyoderma gangrenosum","Vasculitis or other causes of skin necrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial wound examination and documentation of lesion evolution","Full blood count and haemolysis markers where systemic illness is suspected","Coagulation studies and renal function in suspected viscerocutaneous loxoscelism","Urinalysis for haemoglobinuria","Microbiological assessment to exclude infective mimics"],"redFlags":["Haemolysis, dark urine or falling haemoglobin","Features of disseminated intravascular coagulation or acute kidney injury","Rapidly spreading necrosis or systemic sepsis suggesting necrotising infection","Systemic illness in a child"],"prognosis":"Most cutaneous cases heal over weeks with supportive care, though large lesions may scar; systemic loxoscelism is uncommon but can be serious, particularly in children.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing a spider bite for any necrotic ulcer without a confirmed exposure","Missing a necrotising soft-tissue infection masquerading as loxoscelism","Overlooking systemic haemolysis, especially in children","Performing early aggressive surgery on an evolving lesion"],"management":["Manage most cutaneous lesions with supportive wound care, cleansing, analgesia, elevation and tetanus prophylaxis, allowing the eschar to demarcate before any debridement","Delay surgical debridement or reconstruction until the necrotic lesion has fully declared, as early excision worsens outcomes","Monitor for systemic (viscerocutaneous) loxoscelism with serial haemolysis markers, coagulation studies and renal function, particularly in children","Treat systemic disease supportively with intravenous fluids, transfusion for significant haemolysis, and management of disseminated intravascular coagulation and acute kidney injury","Reassess to exclude infective mimics before attributing necrosis to a spider, and treat secondary infection if it develops","Escalate to critical care for severe haemolysis, coagulopathy or renal failure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"lrba_deficiency":{"name":"LRBA deficiency","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LRBA","LPS-responsive beige-like anchor protein deficiency","CVID with autoimmunity and enteropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification of inborn errors of immunity","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Combines early-onset immune dysregulation with immunodeficiency, typically presenting in childhood with severe autoimmunity, enteropathy, lymphoproliferation and recurrent infection","Autoimmune cytopenias, chronic diarrhoea with an inflammatory bowel disease-like enteropathy and endocrinopathies such as type 1 diabetes and thyroid disease are common","Hypogammaglobulinaemia with poor vaccine responses frequently develops, so cases are often initially labelled common variable immunodeficiency","The mechanism converges on CTLA-4, because LRBA loss accelerates CTLA-4 degradation, so the phenotype closely resembles CTLA4 haploinsufficiency","Granulomatous-lymphocytic interstitial lung disease and organ-infiltrating lymphocytic disease can occur","Inheritance is autosomal recessive, from biallelic loss-of-function variants in LRBA","CTLA4-Ig fusion therapy (abatacept) is effective because it substitutes for the reduced CTLA-4 signalling, and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is curative in severe disease","Diagnosis rests on reduced or absent LRBA protein by flow cytometry together with biallelic variants"],"pathophysiology":"LRBA (lipopolysaccharide-responsive beige-like anchor protein) is an intracellular trafficking protein that protects CTLA-4 from lysosomal degradation and recycles it back to the cell surface. In its absence, CTLA-4 is degraded excessively, so regulatory T cells cannot deliver the inhibitory signal that normally restrains effector T-cell activation. The resulting loss of peripheral tolerance produces widespread autoimmunity and lymphocytic organ infiltration, mirroring CTLA4 haploinsufficiency, while impaired B-cell survival and differentiation lead to progressive hypogammaglobulinaemia and infection susceptibility. Because the defect ultimately reduces functional CTLA-4, replacing that signal is therapeutically effective.","additionalDifferentials":["CTLA4 haploinsufficiency","Common variable immunodeficiency","IPEX and IPEX-like syndromes","Autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome (ALPS)","Very-early-onset inflammatory bowel disease of other genetic cause","Activated PI3K-delta syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flow cytometry for LRBA protein expression in lymphocytes","Lymphocyte subsets and regulatory T-cell enumeration with CTLA-4 expression studies","Serum immunoglobulins and specific antibody responses","Autoantibody screen and direct antiglobulin test for autoimmune cytopenias","LRBA gene sequencing for biallelic loss-of-function variants","Endoscopy with biopsy and chest imaging for enteropathy and interstitial lung disease"],"redFlags":["Severe autoimmune cytopenia with life-threatening anaemia or bleeding","Refractory enteropathy with malnutrition and growth failure","Rapidly progressive interstitial lung disease","Lymphoproliferation or organomegaly raising concern for malignant transformation","New endocrine crisis such as diabetic ketoacidosis or adrenal insufficiency"],"prognosis":"Untreated disease is severe and often life-limiting from autoimmune and infective complications, but recognition allows targeted CTLA4-Ig therapy and, where indicated, haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, both of which substantially improve outcome.","pitfalls":["Settling on a diagnosis of common variable immunodeficiency and not pursuing genetic and protein testing","Treating the enteropathy as ordinary inflammatory bowel disease","Overlooking the shared CTLA-4 mechanism and the resulting response to targeted therapy","Missing associated endocrinopathies until they present in crisis","Delaying definitive treatment until multi-organ autoimmune damage is established"],"management":["Use CTLA4-Ig therapy (abatacept) as targeted treatment, replacing the deficient CTLA-4 inhibitory signal to control the immune dysregulation","Give immunoglobulin replacement for hypogammaglobulinaemia and antimicrobial prophylaxis to reduce recurrent infection","Control autoimmunity and lymphoproliferation with immunosuppression, using agents such as sirolimus, corticosteroids or other steroid-sparing drugs as needed","Consider allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as a potentially curative option in severe disease","Monitor for and treat organ-specific complications including autoimmune cytopenias, enteropathy, endocrinopathies such as type 1 diabetes and thyroid disease, and granulomatous-lymphocytic interstitial lung disease","Coordinate care through clinical immunology alongside relevant organ specialists","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder with cascade family testing","Escalate for severe cytopenias, uncontrolled autoimmunity or progressive lung disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"ludwig_angina":{"name":"Ludwig angina","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ludwig's angina","submandibular space infection","bilateral submandibular cellulitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Ludwig angina is a rapidly spreading cellulitis of the floor of the mouth involving the submandibular, sublingual and submental spaces bilaterally","The great majority arise from dental infection, especially of the lower molar teeth whose roots relate to the mylohyoid line","It produces a tense, brawny, tender swelling of the upper neck and floor of mouth that does not usually fluctuate, since it is a cellulitis rather than a discrete abscess","The tongue is pushed upward and backward, causing drooling, difficulty swallowing, muffled speech and a serious threat to the airway","Infection is typically polymicrobial with mixed aerobic and anaerobic mouth organisms","The overriding priority is airway management, as swelling can rapidly cause life-threatening obstruction","Treatment combines securing the airway, high-dose intravenous antibiotics and surgical decompression or drainage together with removal of the dental source","Contrast CT delineates the extent of spread and any collection but must never delay airway assessment in a deteriorating patient"],"pathophysiology":"Ludwig angina begins most often as an odontogenic infection of the mandibular molars. Because the roots of the second and third molars lie below the attachment of the mylohyoid muscle, infection spreads into the submandibular space and then communicates with the sublingual and submental spaces, involving all three bilaterally. The result is a rapidly advancing cellulitis of the floor of the mouth with dense inflammatory oedema rather than a localised abscess. Swelling of the sublingual tissues elevates and displaces the tongue posteriorly, while induration of the submandibular region narrows the oropharyngeal airway. Untreated, infection can extend along fascial planes to the parapharyngeal and retropharyngeal spaces and into the mediastinum, and airway obstruction or sepsis can be fatal.","additionalDifferentials":["Peritonsillar or parapharyngeal abscess","Submandibular sialadenitis or salivary gland abscess","Deep neck space abscess from other sources","Angioedema of the floor of mouth and neck","Cervical lymphadenitis with cellulitis","Sublingual haematoma (for example after anticoagulation)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Early airway assessment and continuous monitoring, taking priority over imaging","Contrast-enhanced CT of the neck to define spread and detect any collection or mediastinal extension","Full blood count, inflammatory markers and blood cultures","Culture and sensitivity of any pus obtained at drainage","Orthopantomogram or dental assessment to identify the causative tooth","Blood glucose and screening for diabetes or immunosuppression"],"redFlags":["Any sign of airway compromise (stridor, drooling, inability to swallow secretions, respiratory distress) constituting an airway emergency","Rapidly progressive neck swelling and inability to protrude the tongue","Features of spreading sepsis, high fever and haemodynamic instability","Chest pain, dyspnoea or widened mediastinum suggesting descending mediastinitis","Trismus limiting mouth opening and airway access"],"prognosis":"With early airway control, prompt intravenous antibiotics and timely surgical drainage the outlook is good, but delay in securing the airway or in treatment can lead to fatal airway obstruction, descending mediastinitis or sepsis.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the speed of airway deterioration and delaying definitive airway management","Waiting for fluctuation before intervening, when the process is a cellulitis without a drainable abscess early on","Sending an unstable patient for prolonged imaging instead of prioritising the airway","Overlooking and failing to treat the underlying dental source","Missing spread to the mediastinum in a patient who is not improving"],"management":["Prioritise securing the airway, with early anaesthetic and ENT involvement and a low threshold for awake fibreoptic intubation or surgical tracheostomy before obstruction develops","Start empirical intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics covering mouth aerobes and anaerobes, such as a beta-lactam with beta-lactamase-inhibitor combination plus metronidazole, or clindamycin","Undertake urgent surgical incision, drainage and decompression of the involved submandibular, sublingual and submental spaces when there is a collection or airway compromise","Eliminate the source by extracting or treating the causative molar tooth","Provide supportive care with intravenous fluids, analgesia and close monitoring in a high-dependency setting, and control coexisting diabetes or immunosuppression","Watch for and treat spread to the parapharyngeal or retropharyngeal spaces or mediastinum (descending mediastinitis) and evolving sepsis","Escalate to critical care with combined ENT, maxillofacial and anaesthetic management for airway failure or systemic deterioration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"lumbar_disc_herniation":{"name":"Lumbar disc herniation","system":"Musculoskeletal / Spine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prolapsed intervertebral disc","slipped disc","herniated nucleus pulposus","lumbar disc prolapse","sciatica"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most herniations occur posterolaterally at L4/5 and L5/S1, compressing the traversing nerve root and producing dermatomal leg pain (sciatica)","Leg pain typically exceeds back pain and follows a dermatomal distribution: L5 to the dorsum of the foot and great toe, S1 to the sole and lateral foot","The straight-leg-raise (Lasegue) test reproduces radicular pain within roughly 30 to 70 degrees of elevation, and a positive crossed straight-leg-raise is highly specific","Motor, reflex, and sensory findings localise the level: an absent ankle jerk suggests S1, while weak great-toe dorsiflexion suggests L5","Symptoms are typically aggravated by sitting, coughing, sneezing, or the Valsalva manoeuvre","The natural history is largely favourable, with most radiculopathy settling within weeks to a few months as the herniation resorbs, so initial management is conservative","MRI is reserved for persistent, severe, or progressive symptoms or when injection or surgery is contemplated, because asymptomatic disc bulges are common","Management includes analgesia, activity modification, physiotherapy, and nerve-root or epidural injection; discectomy is considered for refractory pain or significant neurological deficit"],"pathophysiology":"A lumbar disc herniation occurs when the gelatinous nucleus pulposus displaces through a tear or weakness in the surrounding annulus fibrosus. The displaced material, together with accompanying inflammatory mediators, irritates and compresses the adjacent nerve root, causing radicular pain and dermatomal sensory, motor, or reflex changes. Posterolateral herniations most often affect the traversing root at that level, whereas far-lateral (foraminal) herniations tend to compress the exiting root. Chemical radiculitis from inflammatory cytokines can generate pain even without marked mechanical compression, and gradual resorption of extruded disc material underlies the frequently favourable natural course.","additionalDifferentials":["Lumbar spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication","Facet joint or sacroiliac joint referred pain","Piriformis syndrome or extraspinal sciatic nerve entrapment","Hip osteoarthritis or trochanteric bursitis","Peripheral neuropathy or diabetic amyotrophy","Spinal tumour or infection when red flags are present"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the lumbar spine when symptoms are severe, persistent, or progressive","Plain radiographs to assess alignment and exclude other bony pathology","Nerve conduction studies and EMG in equivocal cases or to exclude peripheral neuropathy","CT or CT myelography where MRI is contraindicated","Targeted diagnostic selective nerve-root block to confirm the symptomatic level"],"redFlags":["Saddle anaesthesia, bladder or bowel dysfunction, or bilateral sciatica indicating possible cauda equina syndrome and mandating urgent MRI","Progressive or severe motor weakness such as a foot drop","Constitutional symptoms suggesting malignancy or infection"],"prognosis":"The outlook is generally excellent: the majority of patients improve substantially within six to twelve weeks with conservative care, and surgery for persistent radiculopathy provides faster relief of leg pain, although longer-term outcomes often converge with non-operative treatment.","pitfalls":["Rushing to image or operate on radiculopathy that would resolve spontaneously","Missing an evolving cauda equina syndrome by not asking about bladder, bowel, and saddle symptoms","Over-attributing symptoms to an incidental disc bulge seen on MRI","Overlooking a far-lateral herniation that compresses the exiting rather than the traversing root"],"management":["Manage most cases conservatively, given the favourable natural history, with reassurance, continued activity, simple analgesia and NSAIDs, and physiotherapy","Add neuropathic pain agents such as amitriptyline or gabapentinoids for radicular pain where simple analgesia is insufficient","Offer a fluoroscopically guided epidural or transforaminal corticosteroid injection for persistent radicular pain not settling with conservative care","Reserve surgical microdiscectomy for radiculopathy that is severe, fails to improve over weeks to months, or is accompanied by a progressive neurological deficit","Recognise cauda equina syndrome as a surgical emergency requiring urgent MRI and immediate decompression","Investigate with MRI when symptoms are severe, persistent or progressive, correlating imaging with the clinical level","Refer to spinal surgery for progressive weakness, intractable pain or diagnostic uncertainty"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","NICE guideline: Low back pain and sciatica in over 16s"],"crossLinks":["lumbar_spinal_stenosis","degenerative_disc_disease"]},"lumbar_disc_prolapse":{"name":"Lumbar disc prolapse","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prolapsed intervertebral disc","herniated lumbar disc","slipped disc","lumbar disc herniation","sciatica from disc prolapse"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with low back pain followed by radicular leg pain (sciatica) that typically radiates below the knee in a dermatomal pattern and is worsened by coughing, sneezing, or sitting","Most prolapses occur at L4/5 and L5/S1, affecting the L5 and S1 nerve roots respectively; the level is inferred from the pattern of pain, weakness, sensory loss, and reflex change","A positive straight-leg-raise (or its variants) reproduces radicular pain and supports nerve-root tension from a disc lesion","The great majority settle with conservative management: reassurance, maintained activity, analgesia, and physiotherapy, as most herniations resorb over weeks to months","Cauda equina syndrome from a large central prolapse is a surgical emergency and must be actively excluded in every patient","Imaging (MRI) is reserved for persistent or severe symptoms, progressive neurological deficit, or when surgery is being considered, since asymptomatic disc bulges are common","Surgery (discectomy) is considered for intractable radicular pain unresponsive to conservative care, significant or progressive motor weakness, or cauda equina compression","Isolated back pain without leg symptoms is usually not due to a surgically treatable disc prolapse"],"pathophysiology":"The intervertebral disc comprises a gelatinous nucleus pulposus contained by the fibrous annulus fibrosus. With age and repetitive loading the annulus weakens and fissures, allowing nuclear material to bulge or herniate, usually posterolaterally where the annulus is thinnest and the posterior longitudinal ligament offers least support. The herniated fragment compresses and inflames the traversing or exiting nerve root, producing radicular pain, and inflammatory mediators from the nucleus contribute to root irritation. A large central herniation can compress the cauda equina. Over time herniated material often shrinks through dehydration and resorption, correlating with symptom resolution.","additionalDifferentials":["Lumbar spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication","Cauda equina syndrome from other causes","Spinal infection (discitis, epidural abscess) or vertebral osteomyelitis","Spinal or metastatic tumour","Peripheral neuropathy or diabetic amyotrophy","Hip osteoarthritis or greater trochanteric pain referring to the leg","Piriformis syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the lumbar spine as the imaging modality of choice when indicated","Detailed neurological examination for the affected root (motor, sensory, reflexes)","Straight-leg-raise and femoral stretch tests to localise root tension","Urgent MRI if cauda equina syndrome is suspected","Inflammatory markers and further imaging if infection or malignancy is a concern"],"redFlags":["SURGICAL EMERGENCY: cauda equina syndrome (saddle anaesthesia, bladder or bowel dysfunction, bilateral leg symptoms) requires urgent MRI and decompression","Progressive or severe motor weakness (e.g. foot drop) indicating significant root compression","Features suggesting infection or malignancy (fever, weight loss, night pain, history of cancer)","New neurological deficit with structural back pain in the presence of trauma or osteoporosis"],"prognosis":"The natural history is favourable, with most patients improving substantially within weeks to a few months as the herniation resorbs; surgery gives faster relief of radicular pain in selected cases but does not clearly improve very long-term outcome, and recurrence or residual back pain can occur.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for and act on cauda equina syndrome","Ordering MRI too early and over-treating incidental disc bulges that do not explain the symptoms","Attributing pure axial back pain to a disc prolapse amenable to discectomy","Prolonged bed rest, which worsens outcomes compared with maintained activity"],"management":["Reassure and treat the majority conservatively with maintained activity, simple analgesia and a short course of NSAIDs, and structured physiotherapy, as most prolapses resorb over weeks to months","Use neuropathic analgesics such as amitriptyline or gabapentinoids for persistent radicular leg pain","Consider an epidural or nerve-root corticosteroid injection for refractory sciatica before contemplating surgery","Actively exclude cauda equina syndrome in every patient and arrange urgent MRI with emergency surgical decompression if suspected","Reserve discectomy for persistent or severe radiculopathy, or a progressive motor deficit, after failure of conservative treatment","Restrict MRI to persistent, severe or progressive symptoms or surgical planning, since asymptomatic disc bulges are common","Refer to orthopaedic or spinal surgery for progressive neurological deficit, disabling pain or surgical consideration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"lumbar_spinal_stenosis":{"name":"Lumbar spinal stenosis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Spine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LSS","central canal stenosis","lateral recess stenosis","neurogenic claudication"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically affects older adults and reflects degenerative narrowing of the central canal, lateral recesses, or neural foramina","The hallmark symptom is neurogenic claudication: bilateral buttock, thigh, and calf discomfort, heaviness, or paraesthesiae brought on by standing and walking","Symptoms are characteristically relieved by lumbar flexion, such as sitting, leaning forward on a shopping trolley, or cycling, because flexion enlarges the canal","Walking downhill (which extends the spine) tends to worsen symptoms whereas bending forward or walking uphill eases them, a useful contrast with vascular claudication","Vascular claudication is relieved simply by stopping and standing still and is associated with diminished peripheral pulses, unlike neurogenic claudication which requires postural flexion for relief","Neurological examination is often normal at rest, and deficits may only emerge after a provocative period of walking","MRI is the investigation of choice, and standing or extension imaging can reveal dynamic compression not apparent when supine","Initial management is conservative (activity modification, physiotherapy, analgesia, epidural steroid injection); decompressive surgery is considered for disabling or progressive symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"Lumbar spinal stenosis arises from progressive degenerative narrowing of the spinal canal and its lateral recesses and neural foramina. A combination of intervertebral disc bulging, facet joint hypertrophy, thickening and buckling of the ligamentum flavum, and sometimes degenerative spondylolisthesis reduces the space available for the cauda equina and exiting nerve roots. The compression is dynamic: spinal extension during standing and walking further narrows the canal and compromises neural blood flow, producing the characteristic exertional leg symptoms, whereas flexion enlarges the canal and relieves them. Venous congestion and transient ischaemia of the compressed nerve roots are thought to underlie the claudicant pattern.","additionalDifferentials":["Vascular (arterial) claudication from peripheral arterial disease","Lumbar disc herniation with radiculopathy","Diabetic or other peripheral neuropathy","Hip or knee osteoarthritis","Trochanteric bursitis or other referred hip pathology","Peripheral nerve entrapment"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the lumbar spine as the investigation of choice","Standing and flexion-extension radiographs to assess instability or degenerative spondylolisthesis","CT or CT myelography where MRI is contraindicated","Ankle-brachial pressure index and vascular studies to exclude arterial claudication","Nerve conduction studies and EMG if peripheral neuropathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["New saddle anaesthesia, urinary retention or incontinence, or faecal incontinence suggesting cauda equina syndrome and mandating urgent MRI","Rapidly progressive bilateral leg weakness","Constitutional features such as weight loss, fever, or night pain suggesting malignancy or infection"],"prognosis":"Many patients have a stable or slowly progressive course and can be managed conservatively for years; decompressive surgery gives good relief of leg symptoms in appropriately selected patients, although back pain and other degenerative changes may persist.","pitfalls":["Mistaking neurogenic for vascular claudication, or vice versa, and instituting the wrong management","Attributing symptoms to imaging findings when incidental degenerative narrowing is common in the asymptomatic elderly","Overlooking coexisting hip osteoarthritis as a cause of buttock and leg pain","Missing an evolving cauda equina syndrome"],"management":["Manage conservatively first with flexion-based physiotherapy and exercise, activity modification and simple analgesia, since the course is often stable or slowly progressive","Trial neuropathic analgesics for leg symptoms, recognising the limited evidence in neurogenic claudication","Offer an epidural corticosteroid injection for symptom relief in patients unsuitable for or awaiting surgery","Refer for decompressive surgery such as laminectomy when neurogenic claudication is disabling or refractory to conservative care","Add instrumented fusion where there is associated degenerative spondylolisthesis or instability","Exclude vascular claudication with ankle-brachial pressure index and peripheral pulse assessment before attributing symptoms to stenosis","Escalate urgently for a progressive neurological deficit or features of cauda equina syndrome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","North American Spine Society Clinical Guidelines: Degenerative Lumbar Spinal Stenosis"],"crossLinks":["degenerative_disc_disease","lumbar_disc_herniation","facet_joint_syndrome"]},"lunotriquetral_instability":{"name":"lunotriquetral instability","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lunotriquetral ligament injury","LT dissociation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lunotriquetral instability arises from injury to the lunotriquetral interosseous ligament, causing ulnar-sided wrist pain and a sense of clunking.","It may occur in isolation, often after a fall on the extended, radially deviated and pronated wrist, or as part of a reverse perilunate (progressive perilunar) injury.","The Reagan ballottement test and the Kleinman shear test reproduce pain and abnormal motion at the lunotriquetral interval.","When secondary stabilisers also fail, the lunate follows the scaphoid into flexion, producing a volar intercalated segment instability (VISI) posture.","Static radiographs are often normal; a break in the smooth proximal Gilula arcs or a lunotriquetral step-off is suggestive.","Ulnar impaction syndrome frequently coexists and should be assessed, as positive ulnar variance may need addressing.","Arthroscopy is the reference standard for confirming and grading the tear when imaging is inconclusive."],"pathophysiology":"The lunotriquetral interosseous ligament, strongest volarly, tightly couples the lunate and triquetrum within the proximal carpal row. Disruption, whether traumatic or degenerative and often associated with ulnar impaction, uncouples this linkage. With loss of the palmar lunotriquetral ligament and secondary volar stabilisers, the triquetrum extends while the lunate, still tethered to the scaphoid, is drawn into flexion, generating a VISI collapse pattern. The resulting abnormal midcarpal kinematics produce pain, clicking and reduced load tolerance on the ulnar side of the wrist.","additionalDifferentials":["Triangular fibrocartilage complex (TFCC) tear","Ulnar impaction syndrome","Extensor carpi ulnaris tendinopathy or subluxation","Pisotriquetral arthritis","Midcarpal instability","Distal radioulnar joint pathology","Hamate or triquetral fracture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PA, lateral and clenched-fist radiographs assessing Gilula arcs and carpal alignment","Lateral view to detect a VISI pattern with abnormal scaphoid flexion and capitolunate angulation","MRI or MR arthrography of the lunotriquetral ligament and TFCC","Wrist arthroscopy for definitive diagnosis and grading","Assessment of ulnar variance","Fluoroscopic dynamic motion study","Contralateral comparison views"],"redFlags":["Features of a progressive perilunate injury or dislocation","Fixed VISI deformity indicating chronic complete disruption","Associated ulnar impaction with lunate or triquetral chondral change","Neurovascular compromise after a high-energy injury"],"prognosis":"Isolated partial injuries often respond to non-operative care, whereas complete or chronic instability may require repair, reconstruction or lunotriquetral arthrodesis, with outcomes influenced by coexisting ulnar impaction.","pitfalls":["Overlooking associated ulnar impaction and treating the ligament in isolation.","Interpreting normal static radiographs as excluding instability.","Missing a reverse perilunate injury pattern.","Confusing lunotriquetral instability with a primary TFCC tear."],"management":["Treat isolated partial or acute injuries non-operatively with wrist splinting, activity modification, hand therapy and a corticosteroid injection into the lunotriquetral interval","Address coexisting ulnar impaction, including an ulnar shortening osteotomy or wafer procedure for positive ulnar variance","Confirm and grade the instability with MR arthrography and wrist arthroscopy when the diagnosis is unclear or symptoms persist","Offer ligament repair or reconstruction for complete or symptomatic chronic instability without fixed deformity","Perform lunotriquetral arthrodesis for refractory or chronic instability, particularly when a VISI deformity is established","Rehabilitate with hand therapy after intervention and refer to a hand and wrist surgeon for persistent ulnar-sided pain or clunking"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","Wheeless' Textbook of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"lupus_miliaris_disseminatus_faciei":{"name":"Lupus miliaris disseminatus faciei","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LMDF","acne agminata","FIGURE (facial idiopathic granulomas with regressive evolution)","lupus miliaris disseminatus faciei"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with crops of monomorphic reddish-brown to yellowish dome-shaped papules symmetrically distributed over the central face","The eyelids and periocular region are characteristically involved, and lesions may cluster around the lower eyelids and upper cheeks","Diascopy typically shows an apple-jelly colour, reflecting the underlying dermal granulomata","Histology reveals dermal epithelioid granulomata that frequently show central caseation-like necrosis, once causing confusion with a tuberculid","It is now regarded as an idiopathic granulomatous disorder unrelated to tuberculosis, despite the historical name","The condition is self-limiting but often persists for months to years and characteristically resolves with pitted or depressed scars","Its relationship to granulomatous rosacea is debated, and the two share overlapping clinical and histological features","Management is difficult and largely empirical, using oral tetracyclines and other systemic agents while avoiding potent topical corticosteroids"],"pathophysiology":"The cause of lupus miliaris disseminatus faciei is unknown. It is characterised by dermal granulomatous inflammation, classically epithelioid granulomata with central caseation-like necrosis, arising in a symmetrical facial distribution. Although the historical name and the necrotising histology suggested a tuberculid, no consistent link to mycobacterial infection has been demonstrated, and tuberculin reactions and mycobacterial studies are typically negative. It is now considered an idiopathic granulomatous reaction pattern, possibly related to a follicular or perifollicular process, and is discussed alongside granulomatous rosacea as part of a shared spectrum. The tendency to scar reflects destruction of dermal structures by the granulomatous infiltrate.","additionalDifferentials":["Granulomatous rosacea","Cutaneous sarcoidosis of the face","Cutaneous tuberculosis (lupus vulgaris) and tuberculids","Papular acne vulgaris","Perioral (periorificial) dermatitis, including its granulomatous form","Demodex folliculitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy demonstrating caseating epithelioid granulomata","Mycobacterial stains, culture and molecular testing to exclude tuberculosis","Investigations for sarcoidosis, including chest imaging, when granulomata are found","Tuberculin or interferon-gamma release testing to help exclude active tuberculosis","Assessment of Demodex density where clinically relevant","Review of previous topical corticosteroid exposure as an aggravating factor"],"redFlags":["Necrotising facial granulomata requiring exclusion of tuberculosis before immunosuppression","Rapidly progressive or ulcerating lesions atypical for the condition","Periocular involvement threatening the eyelids or ocular surface","Development of disfiguring scarring during the active phase"],"prognosis":"The disorder is self-limiting and usually resolves spontaneously over one to two years, but it frequently leaves permanent pitted or depressed scars, and treatment often produces only modest benefit.","pitfalls":["Being misled by the caseating histology into diagnosing tuberculosis without supporting microbiology","Using potent topical corticosteroids, which tend to aggravate the eruption","Delaying treatment and allowing avoidable scarring to develop","Overlooking sarcoidosis as an alternative cause of facial granulomata"],"management":["Prolonged oral tetracycline-class antibiotics (such as doxycycline or minocycline) as the mainstay, used for their anti-inflammatory rather than antimicrobial effect","Consider oral isotretinoin or dapsone in refractory or heavily scarring disease","Short courses of oral corticosteroids to dampen intense granulomatous inflammation","Discontinue any provoking topical corticosteroids, which act as an aggravating factor","Exclude tuberculosis and sarcoidosis before and during immunomodulatory treatment given the caseation-like granulomata","Address residual pitted or depressed scarring with laser resurfacing or other scar-revision once active disease has settled","Refer to dermatology to confirm the diagnosis and set realistic expectations of a self-limiting course with only modest treatment benefit"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","British Association of Dermatologists clinical guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"lupus_nephritis":{"name":"Lupus nephritis","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LN","SLE nephritis","systemic lupus erythematosus nephritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lupus nephritis is renal involvement in systemic lupus erythematosus and is a major determinant of morbidity and long-term outcome in the disease","It can present across a spectrum from asymptomatic urinary abnormalities to nephrotic syndrome or rapidly progressive nephritic disease","Kidney biopsy is the cornerstone of assessment, classifying disease into histological classes that directly guide the intensity of treatment","The proliferative classes (particularly diffuse involvement) carry the greatest risk of progression and generally require intensive immunosuppression","Low complement levels and rising anti-double-stranded DNA antibodies often accompany active renal disease and can herald flares","Screening urinalysis and quantification of proteinuria at intervals is essential in all lupus patients because early nephritis is frequently silent","Treatment is divided into an induction phase to control active inflammation and a maintenance phase to prevent relapse, alongside general measures such as blood pressure control and reduction of proteinuria","Antiphospholipid antibodies can cause a thrombotic microangiopathy that contributes to renal injury independent of immune-complex nephritis"],"pathophysiology":"Lupus nephritis results from deposition of immune complexes and autoantibodies within the glomerulus, driving complement activation and inflammatory injury. Circulating and in-situ immune complexes, including those containing anti-DNA antibodies, localise in the mesangium, subendothelial, and subepithelial regions, and the site of deposition largely determines the clinical and histological pattern. Subendothelial and mesangial deposits provoke inflammatory, proliferative lesions with a nephritic picture, whereas predominantly subepithelial (membranous) deposits produce heavy proteinuria with less inflammation. Complement consumption, cytokine release, and recruitment of inflammatory cells damage the glomerular capillaries, and in severe forms crescents develop. Chronic activity leads to glomerulosclerosis, tubulointerstitial scarring, and progressive loss of function. Concurrent antiphospholipid antibodies may add a thrombotic microangiopathic component.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary membranous nephropathy","Other immune-complex glomerulonephritides","ANCA-associated or anti-GBM crescentic disease","Thrombotic microangiopathy from antiphospholipid syndrome","Drug-induced lupus-like nephritis","Mixed connective tissue disease with renal involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis with microscopy and quantification of proteinuria","Serum complement (C3 and C4) and anti-double-stranded DNA antibody titres","Full autoimmune panel including antinuclear and antiphospholipid antibodies","Kidney biopsy for histological class, activity, and chronicity indices","Serial renal function and blood pressure monitoring","Assessment for infection and other organ involvement before immunosuppression"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising creatinine with an active nephritic sediment suggesting proliferative or crescentic disease","Nephrotic-range proteinuria with hypoalbuminaemia and oedema","Features of thrombotic microangiopathy such as haemolysis and thrombocytopenia","Signs of severe extrarenal lupus or infection complicating immunosuppression"],"prognosis":"Outcome has improved substantially with modern immunosuppression, but proliferative lupus nephritis still carries a meaningful risk of progression to end-stage kidney disease, especially with delayed treatment, high chronicity on biopsy, persistent proteinuria, or frequent relapses. Adherence, blood pressure control, and reduction of proteinuria strongly influence long-term renal survival.","pitfalls":["Relying on serology alone without biopsy to determine the class and intensity of treatment","Missing silent early nephritis by not performing regular urinalysis in lupus patients","Overlooking antiphospholipid-related thrombotic microangiopathy as a cause of worsening function","Stopping maintenance therapy prematurely and precipitating a relapse"],"management":["Biopsy-class-guided immunosuppression, with induction using glucocorticoids plus mycophenolate mofetil or cyclophosphamide for proliferative disease","Add-on belimumab or the calcineurin inhibitor voclosporin to intensify induction and spare glucocorticoids","Maintenance immunosuppression with mycophenolate or azathioprine to prevent relapse","Background hydroxychloroquine in all patients, with renin-angiotensin system blockade to reduce proteinuria and control blood pressure","Monitor complement levels, anti-double-stranded DNA titres, urinalysis, quantified proteinuria and renal function to detect flares early","Treat rapidly progressive crescentic disease with pulsed intravenous methylprednisolone","Refer to nephrology and plan renal replacement therapy or transplantation for progression to end-stage kidney disease","General protection with vaccination, bone and cardiovascular risk reduction, and pregnancy planning given teratogenic agents"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"lupus_panniculitis":{"name":"Lupus panniculitis (lupus profundus)","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lupus profundus","lupus erythematosus panniculitis","LEP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as firm, tender, deep subcutaneous nodules or plaques favouring the face, upper arms, shoulders, buttocks and thighs, often overlying areas of relative fat abundance","Lesions frequently heal with disfiguring, saucer-like lipoatrophy and depressed scars, a clue to the diagnosis","Overlying skin may be normal or show features of discoid lupus (erythema, follicular plugging, atrophy) when the process is termed lupus profundus","Occurs most often in middle-aged women and may exist in isolation or alongside systemic or discoid lupus erythematosus","Biopsy shows a predominantly lobular lymphocytic panniculitis with lymphoid follicles, hyaline fat necrosis and mucin, often with an overlying interface dermatitis","The main differential of concern is subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma, which can mimic it clinically and histologically","Management centres on antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine) with systemic immunosuppression for resistant disease, plus photoprotection and avoidance of trauma"],"pathophysiology":"Lupus panniculitis is an autoimmune, predominantly lobular inflammation of subcutaneous fat that forms part of the spectrum of cutaneous lupus erythematosus. A T-lymphocyte-driven immune response targets the deep dermis and fat lobules, producing hyaline necrosis of adipocytes, lymphoid aggregates, mucin deposition and lymphocytic vascular injury. Repeated bouts of fat inflammation and necrosis are followed by fibrosis and loss of fat volume, accounting for the characteristic depressed atrophic scars. It may occur alone or in association with discoid or systemic lupus, reflecting a shared loss of immune tolerance.","additionalDifferentials":["Subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma","Erythema nodosum","Morphoea profunda / deep morphoea","Traumatic or factitial panniculitis","Cytophagic histiocytic panniculitis","Sarcoidosis of subcutaneous fat (Darier-Roussy)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Deep incisional or excisional biopsy including ample subcutaneous fat for histology","Immunohistochemistry and clonality studies to distinguish from subcutaneous T-cell lymphoma","Antinuclear antibody and extractable nuclear antigen panel","Complement levels, full blood count and inflammatory markers","Assessment for systemic lupus (urinalysis, renal function) where indicated","Direct immunofluorescence of lesional skin"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging nodules with systemic B-symptoms, cytopenias or haemophagocytosis suggesting lymphoma","Ulceration, extensive necrosis or secondary infection of lesions","Features of active systemic lupus with organ involvement","Progressive facial disfigurement or lipoatrophy"],"prognosis":"The condition tends to run a chronic, relapsing course; inflammation usually responds to antimalarials but residual lipoatrophy and scarring are frequently permanent and cosmetically distressing. Prognosis worsens if there is coexisting active systemic lupus or if an underlying lymphoma has been mistaken for it.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma as benign lupus panniculitis","Taking a biopsy that is too shallow to sample the fat lobules adequately","Attributing new depressed scars to injections or trauma rather than recognising the panniculitis","Overlooking coexisting systemic lupus and its organ complications"],"management":["Antimalarials such as hydroxychloroquine as first-line disease-modifying therapy for the panniculitis","Systemic or intralesional corticosteroids to control active inflammatory nodules","Escalate refractory disease to methotrexate, mycophenolate, thalidomide, dapsone or rituximab","Strict photoprotection and avoidance of trauma to lesional sites","Exclude subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma histologically before committing to immunosuppression","Monitor for coexisting systemic or discoid lupus with periodic clinical and laboratory review","Defer soft-tissue augmentation for residual lipoatrophy until the disease is quiescent"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"luscan_lumish_syndrome":{"name":"Luscan-Lumish syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LLS","SETD2-related overgrowth syndrome","SETD2 overgrowth and intellectual disability"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is a chromatin-related overgrowth syndrome characterised by macrocephaly, tall stature, and neurodevelopmental difficulties","It is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in SETD2, a histone methyltransferase gene responsible for trimethylation of histone H3 at lysine 36","Intellectual disability, autism spectrum features, and speech and language delay are prominent","Obesity and advanced bone age may accompany the tall stature and macrocephaly","It sits within the differential of Sotos and related overgrowth syndromes and is usually distinguished by molecular testing rather than clinical features alone","Diagnosis is established by exome or genome sequencing, or a targeted overgrowth/intellectual disability panel","Management is supportive and anticipatory, focusing on developmental, educational, and behavioural interventions with surveillance of growth and associated features"],"pathophysiology":"Luscan-Lumish syndrome is an overgrowth disorder within the group of chromatin-modification syndromes. It results from germline heterozygous loss-of-function variants in SETD2, which encodes a histone methyltransferase that catalyses trimethylation of histone H3 at lysine 36, an epigenetic mark that regulates transcription, splicing, and DNA repair. Reduced SETD2 activity disturbs these chromatin-dependent gene-expression programmes during development, giving rise to generalised overgrowth, macrocephaly, and neurodevelopmental impairment. Inheritance is autosomal dominant, and reported cases are frequently de novo. As with other genes in this pathway, distinct classes of SETD2 variant have been linked to different clinical presentations.","additionalDifferentials":["Sotos syndrome (NSD1-related)","Weaver syndrome (EZH2-related)","Tatton-Brown-Rahman syndrome (DNMT3A-related)","Malan syndrome (NFIX-related)","Fragile X syndrome","Other histone-modification-related intellectual disability syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or genome sequencing, or a targeted overgrowth/intellectual disability gene panel","Chromosomal microarray to exclude copy number causes","Developmental and autism-focused behavioural assessment","Growth monitoring including consideration of bone age","MRI of the brain if neurological signs or marked macrocephaly are present","Ophthalmological and general paediatric review as indicated"],"redFlags":["Rapidly increasing head circumference or focal neurological signs","Developmental regression prompting reassessment","Severe behavioural disturbance affecting safety or function","Emergence of features suggesting an alternative or additional diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The condition is not known to shorten lifespan for most individuals, and the outlook is governed principally by the severity of intellectual disability and autism spectrum features. Early and sustained developmental and educational support optimises functional outcomes.","pitfalls":["Anchoring on a clinical diagnosis of Sotos syndrome and not extending genetic testing to SETD2","Assuming overgrowth syndromes are clinically distinguishable when molecular testing is usually required","Underestimating the autism spectrum and language needs that benefit from targeted intervention","Omitting genetic counselling regarding autosomal dominant, commonly de novo, inheritance"],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy; care centres on early developmental and educational intervention for intellectual disability and speech and language delay","Autism-focused behavioural therapy alongside speech, occupational and physiotherapy support","Serial growth monitoring including bone age, with dietetic input to address obesity and overgrowth","Neurological assessment and brain MRI where macrocephaly is marked or neurological signs emerge","Routine ophthalmological and general paediatric surveillance","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary paediatric and clinical genetics team","Genetic counselling for the autosomal dominant inheritance, with parental testing given the frequency of de novo variants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (University of Washington)","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"lutembacher_syndrome":{"name":"Lutembacher syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lutembacher's syndrome","atrial septal defect with mitral stenosis","Lutembacher complex"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The combination of an atrial septal defect with mitral stenosis, the latter usually rheumatic and the septal defect most often a secundum type","The mitral stenosis may be congenital or acquired and the atrial defect congenital or, occasionally, iatrogenic after transseptal procedures","The atrial septal defect decompresses the left atrium into the right, so the classic symptoms of mitral stenosis may be muted while right heart and pulmonary features become prominent","The left-to-right shunt is augmented by the raised left atrial pressure of mitral stenosis, increasing right heart volume load and pulmonary blood flow","Auscultation blends the findings of both lesions, and the true diagnosis often rests on echocardiography rather than the physical signs alone","Echocardiography defines the size and severity of both the defect and the valve lesion and guides the treatment strategy","Modern management often combines percutaneous balloon mitral valvuloplasty for suitable valves with device or surgical closure of the atrial septal defect"],"pathophysiology":"In Lutembacher syndrome coexisting mitral stenosis and an atrial septal defect interact haemodynamically. Mitral stenosis obstructs left atrial emptying and raises left atrial pressure; the atrial septal defect then allows this pressurised blood to shunt into the right atrium, so left atrial pressure is partly relieved and the pulmonary venous congestion typical of isolated mitral stenosis may be attenuated. The trade-off is a larger left-to-right shunt than the defect alone would produce, imposing a volume load on the right atrium and ventricle and increasing pulmonary blood flow, which over time can lead to right heart dilatation, atrial arrhythmia, and pulmonary hypertension.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated atrial septal defect","Isolated mitral stenosis","Cor triatriatum","Partial anomalous pulmonary venous connection","Mitral stenosis with pulmonary hypertension from other causes","Other left-to-right shunts with a mitral inflow lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography to assess mitral valve morphology and gradient and the atrial septal defect","Transoesophageal echocardiography to size the defect and evaluate suitability for closure and valvuloplasty","Assessment of mitral valve suitability for balloon valvuloplasty, including a morphology score","Right heart catheterisation to quantify the shunt and measure pulmonary pressures when needed","Cardiac MRI to quantify shunt volume and right ventricular size in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Progressive right heart failure from combined volume and pressure loading","Development of pulmonary hypertension and shunt reversal","New atrial fibrillation with haemodynamic deterioration or embolic risk","Severe mitral stenosis with a valve unsuitable for balloon valvuloplasty"],"prognosis":"With timely treatment of both the valve lesion and the septal defect the outlook is generally good, but untreated disease can progress to right heart failure, atrial arrhythmia, and pulmonary hypertension.","pitfalls":["Missing coexisting mitral stenosis because the atrial defect masks its classic symptoms and signs","Attributing all findings to an isolated atrial septal defect and overlooking the valve lesion","Closing the atrial septal defect without addressing significant mitral stenosis, abruptly raising left atrial pressure","Failing to assess mitral valve suitability before planning balloon valvuloplasty"],"management":["Definitive correction of both lesions, combining percutaneous balloon mitral valvuloplasty (or surgical mitral repair or replacement) with device or surgical closure of the atrial septal defect","Diuretics to relieve pulmonary and systemic congestion pending intervention","Rate control and anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation","Secondary antibiotic prophylaxis against rheumatic fever where the mitral stenosis is rheumatic","Serial echocardiography to monitor shunt size, mitral valve gradient, right ventricular function and pulmonary pressures","Manage right heart failure and pulmonary hypertension if the diagnosis is made late","Refer to a cardiology and structural intervention team to plan the combined percutaneous or surgical strategy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC Guidelines for the management of adult congenital heart disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"lyme_disease":{"name":"Lyme Disease (Lyme Borreliosis)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lyme borreliosis"],"chapter":191,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1448","clinicalPearls":["Caused by spirochetes of the Borrelia burgdorferi sensu lato group (also called Borreliella spp.), transmitted by Ixodes ricinus complex ticks; B. burgdorferi sensu stricto is the predominant cause in the United States, while B. afzelii, B. garinii, and B. bavariensis also occur in Europe and Asia. (p.1448-1449)","Staged illness: stage 1 (early localized) presents with erythema migrans (EM); stage 2 (early disseminated) may follow with secondary skin lesions, neurologic involvement (meningitis, cranial neuritis, radiculoneuritis), carditis with atrioventricular block, and migratory musculoskeletal pain; stage 3 (late/persistent) typically manifests as intermittent arthritis and, rarely, chronic encephalopathy, polyneuropathy, or acrodermatitis. (p.1448-1449)","EM begins after an incubation period of roughly 3-32 days as a red macule or papule at the bite site that expands into a large annular lesion, often with a bright red outer border and partial central clearing; common sites include the thigh, groin, and axilla, and the lesion is warm but usually not painful. (p.1451)","Approximately 20% of patients do NOT develop the characteristic EM skin lesion, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis. (p.1451)","Cardiac involvement occurs in about 8% of patients within weeks of onset; the most common abnormality is fluctuating atrioventricular block (first-degree, Wenckebach, or complete heart block), and most cardiac involvement lasts only a few weeks. (p.1451)","About 15% of untreated patients develop frank neurologic abnormalities after weeks to months; the typical U.S. pattern is fluctuating meningitis with facial palsy (cranial neuritis, often the facial nerve, sometimes bilateral) and peripheral radiculoneuropathy. CSF in U.S. neuroborreliosis shows lymphocytic pleocytosis (~100 cells/uL) with elevated protein and normal or slightly low glucose. (p.1451)","In Europe and Asia the characteristic first neurologic sign is radicular pain followed by CSF pleocytosis (meningopolyneuritis or Bannwarth's syndrome), often without meningeal or encephalitic signs. (p.1451)","Lyme arthritis (stage 3) develops in about 60% of untreated U.S. patients months after onset; the typical pattern is intermittent oligoarticular attacks in large joints, especially the knees. Joint fluid white cell counts range widely (500-110,000/uL, average ~25,000/uL), mostly neutrophils, and rheumatoid factor and ANA tests are usually negative. (p.1451)","Most patients experience early symptoms during summer, reflecting transmission by nymphal Ixodes ticks; the tick must usually remain attached for at least about 24 hours for transmission of B. burgdorferi. (p.1449-1450)","Lyme disease is the most common vector-borne infection in the United States and Europe; in the U.S. more than 30,000 cases are reported annually but true cases are estimated at about 476,000 per year. (p.1450)","Posttreatment Lyme disease syndrome (PTLDS) affects roughly 10% of treated patients (reported figures vary widely) with persistent subjective pain, neurocognitive symptoms, or fatigue; there is NO evidence these symptoms reflect active infection, and randomized trials show repeated antibiotic courses do not help and are not recommended. (p.1451-1453)","Reinfection can occur after EM treated with antibiotics because the immune response after early infection is not protective; however, patients who develop an expanded immune response over months (e.g., those with Lyme arthritis) have protective immunity for years and rarely become reinfected. (p.1454)","Maternal-fetal transmission of B. burgdorferi appears to occur rarely if at all; standard therapy is recommended for pregnant women. (p.1453)","No Lyme disease vaccine is currently commercially available; the previously marketed vaccine was discontinued by its manufacturer. (p.1454)"],"pathophysiology":"B. burgdorferi is a fastidious microaerophilic spirochete with an unusually small genome and more than 100 predicted lipoproteins, mostly plasmid-encoded and displayed on the outer membrane to interact with host and tick. It expresses outer-surface protein A (OspA) in the tick midgut and upregulates OspC during migration to the salivary gland, where OspC binds the tick salivary protein Salp15 (required for mammalian infection). After injection into skin the organism downregulates OspC and upregulates the antigenically variable VlsE lipoprotein, then may migrate outward to produce EM and spread hematogenously or via lymphatics to skin, nervous system, heart, and joints. Its only known virulence factors are surface proteins that attach to host proteins, integrins, glycosaminoglycans, or glycoproteins (e.g., plasminogen binding aids tissue spread; complement regulators such as Factor H protect against lysis; decorin-binding proteins bind collagen-associated decorin, possibly explaining alignment with collagen fibrils). The host mounts innate and adaptive responses producing proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha, IL-1beta, IFN-gamma) and opsonizing antibodies; immune mechanisms eventually control disseminated infection even without antibiotics, though spirochetes may persist in localized niches for years. Postinfectious (antibiotic-refractory) Lyme arthritis is not due to persistent infection (culture and PCR of synovium are uniformly negative after antibiotics) but to a dysregulated, excessive proinflammatory response with very high IFN-gamma, host genetic factors (HLA-DRB1*0401, TLR1 1805GG polymorphism), and autoimmune responses to vascular and extracellular matrix autoantigens.","additionalDifferentials":["Uninfected tick bite (red papule that expands little) versus EM","Cellulitis (e.g., streptococcal) or allergic reaction to tick saliva when a lesion expands rapidly","Erythema multiforme in patients with secondary annular lesions (but mucosal blistering and palm/sole involvement are NOT features of B. burgdorferi infection)","Southern tick-associated rash illness (STARI) from Amblyomma americanum bites, an EM-like lesion whose cause is not yet identified","Peripheral spondyloarthropathy or pauciarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis versus oligoarticular Lyme arthritis","Bell's palsy (herpes simplex virus type 1) and Ramsay Hunt syndrome (varicella-zoster virus) versus Lyme facial palsy","Chronic fatigue syndrome and fibromyalgia versus late-stage Lyme disease or PTLDS"],"infectionMimics":["Babesiosis (Babesia microti), co-transmitted by I. scapularis, causing an influenza-like illness with thrombocytopenia and hemolytic anemia","Human granulocytotropic anaplasmosis (Anaplasma phagocytophilum), which may cause leukopenia/thrombocytopenia and can elicit a false-positive IgM response to B. burgdorferi","Borrelia miyamotoi relapsing fever, co-transmitted by I. scapularis","Borrelia mayonii and Ehrlichia muris-like agent infections in the upper midwestern United States","Powassan virus (deer tick virus) infection, which may be fatal","Ehrlichia chaffeensis transmitted by Amblyomma americanum in the same regions as STARI"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Fibromyalgia","Chronic fatigue syndrome","Allergic reaction to tick saliva","Peripheral spondyloarthropathy / juvenile idiopathic arthritis (for the arthritis presentation)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Two-tier serology: conventional approach with ELISA followed by Western blot for equivocal/positive results, or a modified two-test approach using two enzyme immunoassays (e.g., whole-sonicate ELISA followed by VlsE C6 peptide IgG ELISA) that dispenses with the Western blot","IgM and IgG responses during early infection, ideally in paired acute- and convalescent-phase sera (~20-30% positive in acute phase, ~70-80% during convalescence)","After 4-8 weeks, the IgG response by ELISA and Western blot has very high sensitivity and specificity (~99%); a single IgG test is then usually sufficient","Western blot band criteria: IgM positive if 2 of 3 bands (23, 39, 41 kDa); IgG positive if 5 of 10 bands (18, 23, 28, 30, 39, 41, 45, 58, 66, 93 kDa)","PCR of joint fluid for B. burgdorferi DNA (its main PCR application; superior to culture later in disease, though DNA may persist after killing so it does NOT confirm active infection or therapy adequacy)","Culture of B. burgdorferi in Barbour-Stoenner-Kelly (BSK) medium, used mainly in research and positive chiefly early (EM biopsy, occasionally plasma or CSF)","CSF analysis showing lymphocytic pleocytosis with elevated protein in neuroborreliosis","Peripheral blood smear and blood PCR to evaluate for tick-borne co-infections; standard blood counts may give clues (cytopenias)"],"redFlags":["High-degree (third-degree) atrioventricular block or PR interval >0.3 s in carditis, warranting IV therapy and cardiac monitoring","Severe objective neurologic abnormalities (e.g., meningitis, encephalitis, severe neuroborreliosis)","Optic nerve involvement in children from inflammation or raised intracranial pressure, which may lead to blindness","Borrelial encephalomyelitis/leukoencephalitis (rare, mainly B. garinii in Europe) with spastic paraparesis and bladder dysfunction","Rare fatal pancarditis or cardiomegaly","Suspected tick-borne co-infection (e.g., babesiosis, anaplasmosis, Powassan virus), which can cause severe or fatal illness, especially in the very young or elderly"],"prognosis":"Response to treatment is best when given early in the disease; later treatment remains effective but the convalescent period may be longer. Eventually most patients recover with minimal or no residual deficits. Even without antibiotics, innate and adaptive immunity usually control disseminated infection within weeks to months, though untreated spirochetes may persist in localized niches for years. A small percentage develop persistent postinfectious arthritis or, rarely, chronic encephalopathy or polyneuropathy, and about 10% have PTLDS, which usually improves over months but may last years.","pitfalls":["Assuming a negative serologic test excludes acute Lyme disease - serology may be negative during the first several weeks (early seronegativity), so diagnosis of early Lyme disease is clinical","Missing the diagnosis because of atypical EM morphology or because ~20% of patients never develop EM","Relying on a positive IgM result alone in illness lasting more than 2 months - such an isolated IgM is likely false-positive and should not support the diagnosis","Misusing or misinterpreting IgM Western blots, a recognized cause of incorrect Lyme diagnoses in patients with other illnesses","Using serology as a screening test in patients with nonspecific pain or fatigue (low pretest probability), where a false-positive result is more likely than a true-positive","Interpreting persisting seropositivity (including VlsE C6 and IgM responses, which can persist for years after therapy) as evidence of active, recent, or recurrent infection","Treating persistent subjective symptoms as active infection - 'chronic Lyme disease' equated with chronic B. burgdorferi infection is a misnomer, and repeated/prolonged antibiotic courses lack benefit and carry documented harm","Using PCR positivity in joint fluid to judge active infection or adequacy of therapy, since DNA may persist for weeks after spirochetal killing"],"management":["First-line oral doxycycline for erythema migrans and early disease, with amoxicillin or cefuroxime as alternatives in pregnancy or young children","Intravenous ceftriaxone or penicillin for neuroborreliosis, high-grade atrioventricular block or other severe disseminated disease","Oral doxycycline for Lyme arthritis, reserving parenteral therapy or a repeat course for persistent synovitis","Cardiac monitoring with temporary pacing support for symptomatic high-grade heart block","Symptomatic measures such as non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for arthralgia and eye protection for facial-nerve palsy","Tick-bite prevention, prompt tick removal and post-exposure doxycycline prophylaxis after high-risk bites","Reassure and monitor recovery, since most patients resolve fully and prolonged antibiotic courses are not indicated for lingering non-specific symptoms"],"severityClassification":"Staged clinical classification per Harrison: Stage 1 - early localized infection (erythema migrans at the bite site). Stage 2 - early disseminated infection (secondary annular skin lesions, neurologic involvement such as meningitis/cranial neuritis/radiculoneuritis/peripheral neuritis, carditis with atrioventricular nodal block, and migratory musculoskeletal pain). Stage 3 - late/persistent infection (intermittent or persistent arthritis, and rarely chronic encephalopathy, polyneuropathy, or acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans). For diagnostic/testing purposes, the American College of Physicians algorithm stratifies by pretest probability: high (EM - treat empirically without serology), intermediate (e.g., oligoarticular arthritis - test and treat if positive), and low (nonspecific symptoms - neither test nor treat).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1448"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphangioleiomyomatosis":{"name":"Lymphangioleiomyomatosis","system":"Respiratory / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LAM","sporadic LAM","TSC-associated LAM","pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATS/ERS lymphangioleiomyomatosis guidance and respiratory texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider in a premenopausal woman with progressive breathlessness, recurrent pneumothorax, or unexplained diffuse cystic lung disease","High-resolution imaging shows numerous thin-walled, round cysts distributed diffusely throughout otherwise normal lung","LAM occurs sporadically and in association with tuberous sclerosis complex, so examine for skin, renal, and neurological features of that condition","Renal angiomyolipomas and chylous pleural effusions or ascites support the diagnosis","A raised serum VEGF-D level can support the diagnosis non-invasively in the right clinical context","mTOR inhibition is the recognised disease-modifying therapy for progressive disease and for symptomatic angiomyolipomas and chylous complications","Disease may worsen with oestrogen exposure, and pneumothorax can be recurrent and difficult to manage"],"pathophysiology":"Lymphangioleiomyomatosis is driven by inactivating mutations in the TSC1 or TSC2 tumour suppressor genes, which normally restrain the mechanistic target of rapamycin (mTOR) pathway. Loss of this control leads to abnormal proliferation of smooth-muscle-like LAM cells that infiltrate the lung, airways, lymphatics, and blood vessels. These cells drive cystic destruction of lung parenchyma and obstruction of lymphatics, producing progressive airflow obstruction, cyst formation prone to rupture, and chylous fluid collections; the same pathway dysregulation underlies associated renal angiomyolipomas.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis","Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome with pulmonary cysts","Emphysema, especially in younger or non-smoking patients","Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia","Follicular bronchiolitis","Metastatic cystic lung disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution chest computed tomography to characterise cysts","Serum VEGF-D measurement","Abdominal imaging for renal angiomyolipomas and lymphatic masses","Assessment for tuberous sclerosis complex including skin, brain, and genetic evaluation","Pulmonary function tests, typically showing obstruction with reduced gas transfer","Pleural fluid analysis for chyle where an effusion is present"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or tension pneumothorax","Large or bleeding renal angiomyolipoma","Rapidly declining lung function or worsening hypoxaemia","Large chylous effusion causing respiratory compromise"],"prognosis":"The course is variable but generally slowly progressive; mTOR inhibition can stabilise lung function and shrink angiomyolipomas, and with modern management many patients maintain function for years, though some progress to respiratory failure requiring transplantation.","pitfalls":["Dismissing diffuse cystic change in a young woman as emphysema","Failing to screen for tuberous sclerosis and renal angiomyolipomas","Overlooking VEGF-D testing that could avoid a lung biopsy","Not counselling about oestrogen exposure and pneumothorax recurrence risk"],"management":["mTOR inhibition with sirolimus (or everolimus) as disease-modifying therapy for declining lung function, chylous complications and renal angiomyolipomas","Bronchodilators for airflow obstruction, supplemental oxygen and pulmonary rehabilitation for symptomatic support","Avoid oestrogen-containing therapies and counsel on the risks of pregnancy","Manage recurrent pneumothorax with drainage and early pleurodesis given the high recurrence rate","Treat renal angiomyolipomas with mTOR inhibition or selective arterial embolisation to prevent haemorrhage","Monitor with serial pulmonary function tests and screen for tuberous sclerosis complex, with genetic counselling where it is confirmed","Ensure vaccination and refer for lung transplantation assessment in progressive respiratory failure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATS/ERS clinical practice guideline on lymphangioleiomyomatosis","Standard respiratory medicine texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphangioma_circumscriptum":{"name":"Lymphangioma circumscriptum","system":"Skin / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Microcystic lymphatic malformation","Superficial lymphatic malformation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common cutaneous (microcystic) lymphatic malformation, now classified as a superficial lymphatic malformation.","Presents as clusters of clear, translucent, thick-walled vesicles likened to frogspawn, often with haemorrhagic red-black vesicles when blood enters.","Commonly affects the proximal limbs, limb girdles, axillae, trunk and oral cavity or tongue.","Frequently connected to deeper, larger lymphatic cisterns beneath the skin, which explains the high recurrence after superficial treatment.","Prone to recurrent leakage of clear lymph, oozing, bleeding and secondary cellulitis.","Usually congenital or appearing in early childhood, though it can present later in life.","MRI helps define the depth of the deeper cisternal component before intervention.","Treatment options include sclerotherapy, ablative laser and excision of the deep component, but recurrences are common."],"pathophysiology":"Lymphangioma circumscriptum is a superficial (microcystic) lymphatic malformation in which dilated lymphatic channels form thin-walled vesicles within the papillary dermis. These superficial cisterns typically communicate with larger, muscular-walled lymphatic cisterns in the deeper dermis and subcutis that are disconnected from the normal lymphatic system; rhythmic contraction of the deep cisterns transmits pressure to the surface vesicles. Failure to address the deep component underlies the characteristic recurrence. As with other lymphatic malformations, many lesions harbour somatic PIK3CA-pathway activation, and communication with dermal capillaries accounts for the intermittent haemorrhagic appearance.","additionalDifferentials":["Macrocystic lymphatic malformation","Venous malformation","Angiokeratoma","Herpes simplex or zoster with grouped vesicles","Molluscum contagiosum","Acquired cutaneous lymphangiectasia from lymphatic obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical and dermoscopic assessment showing lacunae and frogspawn-like vesicles","Skin biopsy showing dilated papillary dermal lymphatics","MRI to define the deep cisternal component and overall extent","Ultrasound","PIK3CA mutation testing in selected cases","Assessment for secondary infection when the lesion is inflamed"],"redFlags":["Recurrent cellulitis or sepsis arising within the malformation","Persistent lymph leakage with skin breakdown","New firm nodularity in longstanding lymphoedema, raising concern for lymphangiosarcoma","Extensive lesions causing functional impairment"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign but chronic and notoriously recurrent after treatment unless the deep cisternal component is addressed. Complications are mainly recurrent infection, leakage and cosmetic concern.","pitfalls":["Treating only the visible surface vesicles and ignoring the deep cisterns, which guarantees recurrence.","Misdiagnosing grouped haemorrhagic vesicles as herpes infection.","Underestimating the extent without MRI before surgery.","Overlooking recurrent cellulitis as a driver of long-term morbidity."],"management":["Complete surgical excision incorporating the deep dermal and subcutaneous cisterns as the most definitive treatment, since leaving them drives recurrence","Ablative options such as carbon dioxide laser, sclerotherapy or radiofrequency for superficial vesicles or where surgery is unsuitable","Prompt antibiotics for secondary cellulitis, with skin and wound care for chronic lymph leakage and oozing","Consider mTOR inhibition with sirolimus for extensive or recurrent PIK3CA-driven disease","Define the deep cisternal extent with MRI before intervention to plan complete treatment","Refer to a vascular anomalies service for multidisciplinary planning","Counsel on the benign but notoriously recurrent nature and the cosmetic implications"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of dermatology","ISSVA classification of vascular anomalies","Reviews of superficial lymphatic malformations and their management"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphatic_malformation":{"name":"Lymphatic malformation","system":"Skin / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lymphangioma","Cystic hygroma","Macrocystic/microcystic lymphatic malformation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies: Hemangiomas and Malformations","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Slow-flow malformation of dysplastic lymphatic channels, classified as macrocystic, microcystic or combined.","Macrocystic lesions are soft, transilluminant and often cervicofacial or axillary (historically termed cystic hygroma); microcystic lesions are ill-defined and infiltrative.","Somatic activating PIK3CA mutations underlie most lesions, placing them within the PIK3CA-related overgrowth spectrum.","Sudden enlargement typically follows intralesional bleeding or infection.","Superficial microcystic mucosal or cutaneous lesions produce characteristic clear or blood-filled vesicles resembling frogspawn.","Sclerotherapy is first-line for macrocystic disease; microcystic disease is harder to treat and sirolimus is increasingly used.","Large cervicofacial lesions can compromise the airway and may require antenatal airway planning such as an EXIT procedure.","Antenatally detected cystic hygroma is associated with chromosomal abnormalities such as Turner syndrome and with other structural anomalies."],"pathophysiology":"Lymphatic malformation results from abnormal embryonic development of lymphatic channels that fail to establish normal connections with the venous system, producing dilated, endothelium-lined cystic spaces filled with lymph. Most sporadic lesions carry somatic activating mutations in PIK3CA, driving PI3K/AKT/mTOR signalling and abnormal lymphatic endothelial growth. Because the channels drain poorly they are prone to stasis, intralesional haemorrhage and infection, which cause abrupt and sometimes dramatic enlargement.","additionalDifferentials":["Venous malformation","Infantile haemangioma","Thyroglossal or branchial cyst for cervical lesions","Teratoma","Kaposiform lymphangiomatosis","Combined lymphaticovenous malformation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound distinguishing macrocystic from microcystic disease","Contrast-enhanced MRI (T2-bright cysts with fluid-fluid levels after bleeding)","Antenatal ultrasound and, where indicated, fetal karyotyping","Aspiration and analysis of cyst fluid when the diagnosis is unclear","PIK3CA mutation testing","Airway assessment with nasendoscopy or imaging for cervicofacial lesions"],"redFlags":["Airway compromise from a cervicofacial lesion","Rapid enlargement from intralesional haemorrhage or infection","Orbital lesions threatening vision","Sepsis arising within an infected malformation","Chylous effusions or generalised lymphatic anomaly"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on site and type. Well-defined macrocystic lesions respond well to sclerotherapy, whereas diffuse microcystic and cervicofacial disease tends to recur and cause chronic morbidity, although mTOR inhibition has improved control.","pitfalls":["Attributing sudden swelling to malignancy rather than to bleeding or infection.","Expecting sclerotherapy to be as effective for microcystic as for macrocystic disease.","Incomplete excision leading to recurrence.","Overlooking airway planning in a large antenatally detected cervical lesion."],"management":["Sclerotherapy as first-line treatment for macrocystic lesions","mTOR inhibition with sirolimus for extensive, microcystic or complicated disease not amenable to sclerotherapy","Surgical resection or debulking for localised or functionally troublesome lesions","Prompt antibiotics for infective flares and management of intralesional haemorrhage causing sudden enlargement","Emergency airway assessment and protection for large cervicofacial lesions","Emerging targeted PIK3CA-pathway inhibition, such as alpelisib, for refractory PIK3CA-driven disease","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary vascular anomalies team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of paediatric dermatology","ISSVA classification of vascular anomalies","Consensus guidance on management of lymphatic malformations"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphedema":{"name":"Lymphoedema","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lymphedema","lymphatic oedema","primary lymphoedema","secondary lymphoedema"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from impaired lymphatic drainage causing accumulation of protein-rich interstitial fluid and progressive limb swelling","Primary forms arise from congenital or inherited lymphatic maldevelopment, while secondary forms follow damage from surgery, radiotherapy, malignancy, infection, or trauma","Worldwide, filarial infection is the leading cause of secondary lymphoedema, whereas in high-income settings cancer treatment predominates","Early swelling is soft and pitting but becomes non-pitting and fibrotic over time, with skin thickening and a positive Stemmer sign at the base of the toes","The distribution characteristically includes the dorsum of the foot and toes, helping distinguish it from other causes of limb swelling","Diagnosis is largely clinical; lymphoscintigraphy can confirm lymphatic dysfunction when the cause is uncertain","Cornerstone management is complete decongestive therapy with skin care, manual lymphatic drainage, compression, and exercise","Meticulous skin hygiene reduces recurrent cellulitis, which itself further damages lymphatics and worsens the oedema"],"pathophysiology":"Lymphoedema develops when lymphatic transport capacity is exceeded by the lymphatic load, leading to accumulation of protein-rich fluid in the interstitium. In primary disease the lymphatic vessels or nodes are congenitally hypoplastic, aplastic, or dysfunctional. In secondary disease, previously normal lymphatics are obstructed or destroyed by surgery, radiotherapy, tumour infiltration, recurrent infection (notably filariasis), or trauma. Stagnant protein draws further fluid osmotically and incites chronic inflammation, fibrosis, adipose deposition, and skin changes, creating a self-reinforcing cycle. Impaired local immune surveillance predisposes to recurrent cellulitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic venous insufficiency and venous oedema","Deep vein thrombosis","Systemic causes of oedema (cardiac, renal, hepatic, hypoalbuminaemia)","Lipoedema","Dependent or immobility-related oedema","Myxoedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment including Stemmer sign and limb circumference or volume measurement","Lymphoscintigraphy to confirm lymphatic dysfunction when the diagnosis is unclear","Duplex ultrasound to exclude venous disease or thrombosis","Cross-sectional imaging to identify malignancy or nodal obstruction in secondary cases","Evaluation for filariasis in endemic regions"],"redFlags":["Features of cellulitis (spreading erythema, warmth, fever) requiring prompt treatment","Rapidly progressive or unilateral swelling raising concern for underlying malignancy","New or changing cutaneous lesions suggesting lymphangiosarcoma in longstanding lymphoedema","Sudden painful swelling that could indicate concurrent deep vein thrombosis"],"prognosis":"Lymphoedema is generally a chronic, progressive condition without cure, but sustained decongestive therapy and compression can control swelling, preserve function, and reduce complications. Poor adherence, recurrent infection, and untreated underlying causes worsen the outlook, and rarely longstanding disease can undergo malignant change.","pitfalls":["Confusing lymphoedema with venous oedema or lipoedema and applying inappropriate treatment","Applying compression without first excluding significant arterial disease","Overlooking an underlying malignancy in new unilateral limb swelling","Neglecting skin care, allowing recurrent cellulitis that accelerates progression"],"management":["Complex decongestive therapy with manual lymphatic drainage, multilayer compression bandaging transitioning to fitted compression garments, and remedial exercise","Meticulous skin and nail care with emollients to protect the barrier and reduce infection","Prompt antibiotics for cellulitis, with antibiotic prophylaxis for recurrent attacks","Treat the underlying cause, such as antifilarial therapy with diethylcarbamazine in filariasis or relieving nodal obstruction in malignancy","Weight management and limb elevation as adjuncts to compression","Surgical options such as lymphaticovenous anastomosis, vascularised lymph-node transfer, or liposuction and debulking for refractory or advanced disease","Monitor limb volume and remain alert to the rare cutaneous angiosarcoma complicating longstanding lymphoedema"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphedema_distichiasis":{"name":"Lymphoedema-distichiasis syndrome","system":"Vascular anomalies / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lymphedema-distichiasis syndrome","LDS","FOXC2-related lymphoedema-distichiasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"ISSVA classification / Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant primary lymphoedema caused by loss-of-function variants in FOXC2","The two defining features are lower-limb lymphoedema and distichiasis, an accessory row of eyelashes arising from the meibomian gland openings","Lymphoedema characteristically appears later than in Milroy disease, typically around puberty or in early adulthood, and predominantly affects the legs","Distichiasis may cause ocular irritation, corneal changes and photophobia, and can be present from birth even before oedema develops","Associated features include varicose veins (often of early onset), ptosis, cleft palate and, importantly, congenital heart disease","Unlike Milroy disease, the lymphatic defect is thought to involve lymphatic valve dysfunction and reflux rather than simple hypoplasia","Diagnosis combines the clinical dyad with family history and FOXC2 genetic testing, supported by lymphoscintigraphy","Management is supportive lymphoedema care with compression and skin care, ophthalmic treatment of distichiasis, and cardiac evaluation"],"pathophysiology":"Lymphoedema-distichiasis syndrome is an autosomal dominant primary lymphatic disorder caused by loss-of-function variants in the transcription factor FOXC2, which is essential for normal development of lymphatic valves and vessels. The resulting valvular dysfunction leads to lymphatic reflux and impaired drainage, producing lower-limb lymphoedema, and the same developmental disturbance affects venous valves (accounting for varicose veins) and eyelash follicles (distichiasis). FOXC2 also contributes to eyelid, palatal and cardiac development, explaining the associated anomalies.","additionalDifferentials":["Milroy disease (congenital VEGFR3-related lymphoedema without distichiasis)","Other primary lymphoedemas (e.g. Meige/late-onset lymphoedema)","Secondary lymphoedema (post-surgical, infective, malignant)","Chronic venous insufficiency with oedema","Lipoedema","Systemic causes of leg oedema (cardiac, renal, hepatic)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["FOXC2 molecular genetic testing","Slit-lamp examination to confirm distichiasis and assess corneal effects","Lymphoscintigraphy to characterise lymphatic drainage and reflux","Echocardiography to screen for congenital heart disease","Venous duplex assessment for varicose veins and reflux","Family history review and cascade screening"],"redFlags":["Recurrent cellulitis of the lymphoedematous limb","Corneal damage or visual compromise from distichiasis","Undiagnosed congenital heart disease","Rapidly progressive or unilateral limb swelling warranting exclusion of secondary cause","Signs of significant venous complications"],"prognosis":"The condition is chronic but generally compatible with a normal lifespan; lymphoedema is manageable with sustained conservative care, and distichiasis is treatable, though recurrent infection, progressive swelling and any associated cardiac disease influence long-term outcome.","pitfalls":["Missing distichiasis, which may be subtle and requires careful eyelid examination","Attributing leg swelling to venous disease alone and overlooking the lymphatic diagnosis","Failing to screen for associated congenital heart disease","Not recognising the autosomal dominant inheritance and offering family screening"],"management":["Manage the lymphoedema with complex decongestive therapy, compression garments and skin care as for other primary lymphoedemas","Treat distichiasis with lubrication and definitive follicle ablation by electrolysis, cryotherapy or laser to relieve corneal irritation","Prompt antibiotics for cellulitis and prophylaxis for recurrent episodes","Screen for and manage associated congenital heart disease, varicose veins, ptosis and cleft palate","Ophthalmology referral for corneal protection and management of the aberrant eyelashes","Genetic counselling and cascade family screening for the autosomal dominant FOXC2 variant","Coordinate care across vascular anomalies, ophthalmology and cardiology services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ISSVA Classification of Vascular Anomalies","Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies: Haemangiomas and Malformations"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphocytic_colitis":{"name":"Lymphocytic colitis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["microscopic colitis, lymphocytic type","lymphocytic type microscopic colitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with chronic watery, non-bloody diarrhoea similar to collagenous colitis, often with urgency and nocturnal symptoms","Affects both sexes more equally than collagenous colitis, though still with a female predominance, and typically in middle-aged and older adults","Colonoscopy shows normal-appearing mucosa, so diagnosis is made only on biopsy histology","The defining feature is a marked increase in intraepithelial lymphocytes without the thickened subepithelial collagen band that characterises collagenous colitis","Strongly associated with coeliac disease, autoimmune thyroid disease and drugs such as non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, proton-pump inhibitors and certain other agents","Multiple biopsies from several colonic segments improve diagnostic yield because changes may be patchy","Budesonide for microscopic colitis is first-line, with withdrawal of implicated drugs an essential first step"],"pathophysiology":"Lymphocytic colitis is a subtype of microscopic colitis defined histologically by a raised intraepithelial lymphocyte count in the surface epithelium with associated chronic inflammation of the lamina propria, but crucially without the thickened subepithelial collagen band seen in collagenous colitis, and with an endoscopically normal mucosa. It is thought to result from a dysregulated mucosal immune response to luminal antigens, including drugs, in predisposed individuals, disturbing epithelial water and electrolyte transport and producing a secretory watery diarrhoea. It shares many clinical and immunological features with collagenous colitis and the two are considered ends of a spectrum.","additionalDifferentials":["Collagenous colitis","Coeliac disease","Irritable bowel syndrome with diarrhoea","Bile acid diarrhoea","Infective diarrhoea","Inflammatory bowel disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Colonoscopy with segmental biopsies despite normal mucosal appearance","Histological quantification of intraepithelial lymphocytes and assessment for a collagen band","Coeliac serology and duodenal biopsy if indicated","Thyroid function tests","Stool studies to exclude infective causes"],"redFlags":["Rectal bleeding, which is not typical and should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis","Marked weight loss requiring exclusion of coeliac disease or malignancy","Dehydration and electrolyte disturbance from severe diarrhoea","Anaemia suggesting an alternative or coexisting diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The disease is benign with no increased colorectal cancer risk; many patients respond to treatment and some experience spontaneous remission, though relapses may occur and require further courses of treatment.","pitfalls":["Assuming irritable bowel syndrome and not biopsying normal-looking colonic mucosa","Failing to distinguish it from collagenous colitis by looking for the collagen band","Overlooking coexisting coeliac disease as a cause of persistent symptoms","Not reviewing medications that can precipitate or maintain the colitis"],"management":["Oral budesonide as first-line therapy to induce remission","Withdraw implicated drugs such as non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, proton-pump inhibitors and other culprit agents","Antidiarrhoeal agents such as loperamide for mild symptoms, with bile-acid sequestrants where bile-acid malabsorption contributes","Escalate refractory or steroid-dependent disease to immunomodulators or biologic therapy","Screen for and treat associated coeliac disease with a gluten-free diet, and associated autoimmune thyroid disease","Reassure regarding the benign course with no increased colorectal cancer risk, and re-treat relapses with repeat budesonide courses"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","British Society of Gastroenterology guidance on microscopic colitis"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphocytic_gastritis":{"name":"Lymphocytic gastritis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["varioliform gastritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined histologically by a marked increase in intraepithelial lymphocytes (predominantly CD8-positive T cells) within the surface and foveolar epithelium","Strongly associated with coeliac disease and with Helicobacter pylori infection, which together account for most cases","May appear endoscopically as varioliform gastritis, with nodularity, thickened folds, and aphthoid erosions typically in the gastric body","Many patients are asymptomatic or have non-specific dyspepsia, and the diagnosis is often made on biopsy performed for other reasons","Treatment is directed at the underlying cause, such as eradicating Helicobacter pylori or instituting a gluten-free diet for coeliac disease","The lymphocytosis and symptoms frequently resolve once the associated condition is treated","Rarely it is linked to protein loss producing a Menetrier-like picture or to lymphoma, warranting careful assessment"],"pathophysiology":"Lymphocytic gastritis is a histological pattern characterised by a striking accumulation of intraepithelial T lymphocytes within the gastric surface and pit epithelium, accompanied by a chronic mononuclear infiltrate in the lamina propria. It is regarded as an immune-mediated mucosal response rather than a distinct disease, most often triggered by gluten sensitivity in coeliac disease or by Helicobacter pylori infection, both of which drive an intraepithelial lymphocytic reaction analogous to that seen in the coeliac duodenum. The inflammatory response can distort the surface epithelium and produce the nodular, erosive (varioliform) appearance seen at endoscopy.","additionalDifferentials":["Coeliac disease with gastric involvement","Helicobacter pylori-associated chronic active gastritis","Collagenous gastritis","Menetrier disease and other hypertrophic gastropathies","Crohn disease involving the stomach","Gastric lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with gastric biopsies for histology and immunohistochemical quantification of intraepithelial lymphocytes","Helicobacter pylori testing by histology, urea breath test, stool antigen, or serology","Coeliac serology and duodenal biopsies to detect associated coeliac disease","Full blood count and iron studies to assess for anaemia","Serum albumin where a protein-losing, Menetrier-like picture is suspected","Repeat biopsy after treatment of the underlying cause to confirm resolution"],"redFlags":["Marked protein loss with hypoalbuminaemia suggesting a hypertrophic, Menetrier-like variant","Persistent nodularity or a mass raising concern for lymphoma","Refractory symptoms or histology despite treating coeliac disease and Helicobacter pylori"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally good, with the lymphocytic infiltrate and symptoms typically improving after eradication of Helicobacter pylori or introduction of a gluten-free diet, although the histological pattern may persist in a minority.","pitfalls":["Reporting the pattern without searching for the underlying coeliac disease or Helicobacter pylori","Missing coeliac disease by not taking duodenal biopsies or checking serology","Overlooking a rare protein-losing or lymphomatous complication","Assuming persistence of the histological pattern equates to treatment failure"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying cause, eradicating Helicobacter pylori with combination antibiotic and proton-pump-inhibitor therapy where present","Institute a gluten-free diet when associated coeliac disease is identified","Acid suppression with proton-pump inhibitors for dyspeptic symptoms","Correct iron-deficiency anaemia and replace iron where indicated","Repeat endoscopic biopsy after treatment to confirm resolution of the lymphocytic infiltrate","Refer to gastroenterology and investigate a protein-losing, Menetrier-like picture where albumin is low"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Yamada's Textbook of Gastroenterology"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphocytic_hypophysitis":{"name":"Lymphocytic hypophysitis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["autoimmune hypophysitis","primary hypophysitis","lymphocytic adenohypophysitis","immune checkpoint inhibitor hypophysitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autoimmune inflammatory disorder of the pituitary that classically affects women in late pregnancy or the postpartum period","Increasingly recognised as an immune-related adverse effect of checkpoint-inhibitor cancer therapy","Headache and visual disturbance from an enlarging, inflamed gland can mimic a pituitary adenoma","Hormone loss often occurs in a characteristic sequence, with corticotroph and thyrotroph function frequently affected early","Involvement of the posterior pituitary or stalk can cause diabetes insipidus, which is unusual in adenomas and a useful discriminating clue","Imaging typically shows symmetrical pituitary enlargement with stalk thickening and avid, homogeneous enhancement","Corticotroph deficiency must be identified and treated with hormone replacement before any thyroid hormone is started","The condition may be self-limiting, and glucocorticoid therapy or drug withdrawal is used depending on the underlying trigger"],"pathophysiology":"Lymphocytic hypophysitis is characterised by immune-mediated infiltration of the pituitary gland by lymphocytes and plasma cells, producing inflammation, gland enlargement and progressive destruction of hormone-secreting cells. The classic form has a strong association with the peripartum state and other autoimmune conditions, suggesting a breakdown in immune tolerance to pituitary antigens. Checkpoint-inhibitor therapy can trigger a similar inflammatory hypophysitis by unleashing autoreactive immunity. Inflammation may involve the anterior lobe, the stalk and the posterior lobe, accounting for the variable pattern of anterior hormone deficiencies and, in some cases, diabetes insipidus, with eventual fibrosis and atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-functioning pituitary adenoma","Craniopharyngioma","Sarcoidosis or other granulomatous hypophysitis","Langerhans cell histiocytosis involving the pituitary","Metastatic disease to the pituitary","IgG4-related hypophysitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Magnetic resonance imaging of the pituitary and stalk","Full anterior pituitary hormone profile including cortisol assessment","Assessment for diabetes insipidus with paired plasma and urine osmolality","Prolactin measurement, which may be mildly elevated or reduced","Screening for coexisting autoimmune disease","Review of any recent checkpoint-inhibitor or other immunotherapy exposure"],"redFlags":["Acute secondary adrenal insufficiency","Rapid visual deterioration from chiasmal compression","New diabetes insipidus indicating posterior or stalk involvement","Severe headache with a rapidly enlarging sellar mass","Hypopituitarism emerging during immunotherapy"],"prognosis":"Many cases stabilise or partially recover, particularly with treatment of the trigger, but some hormone deficits, especially corticotroph loss, are permanent and require lifelong replacement; checkpoint-inhibitor-related corticotroph deficiency is often irreversible.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the inflamed gland as a pituitary adenoma and proceeding to unnecessary surgery","Starting thyroid hormone before replacing cortisol and precipitating an adrenal crisis","Overlooking diabetes insipidus as a distinguishing feature","Failing to consider checkpoint-inhibitor therapy as a cause in cancer patients","Assuming full recovery of pituitary function when deficits may be permanent"],"management":["Replace deficient hormones, giving glucocorticoids first to avoid adrenal crisis, then thyroid hormone, sex steroids and growth hormone as needed","Desmopressin for cranial diabetes insipidus arising from posterior lobe or stalk involvement","High-dose glucocorticoids to reduce inflammatory gland enlargement causing headache or visual compromise","Withhold the triggering checkpoint inhibitor and manage jointly with oncology when therapy-induced","Reserve trans-sphenoidal surgery for significant mass effect or diagnostic uncertainty rather than as routine treatment","Serial pituitary hormone testing and interval MRI to monitor recovery, relapse or gland atrophy","Counsel on likely lifelong corticotroph replacement and sick-day steroid rules given frequent irreversibility"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","DeGroot's Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphocytic_interstitial_pneumonia":{"name":"Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LIP","Lymphoid interstitial pneumonia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, largely benign lymphoproliferative interstitial process classified among the idiopathic interstitial pneumonias but usually secondary to an underlying disorder.","Strongly associated with Sjögren syndrome, other connective tissue diseases, HIV infection (especially in children), common variable immunodeficiency and autoimmune thyroid disease.","Presents insidiously with cough and breathlessness, sometimes with systemic or autoimmune features.","The HRCT hallmark is diffuse ground-glass opacity with scattered thin-walled perivascular cysts; centrilobular nodules and septal thickening are also seen.","Histology shows a dense polyclonal lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate expanding the alveolar septa.","It must be distinguished from pulmonary mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma, and clonality studies may be required.","Disease often responds to corticosteroids and to treatment of the underlying disorder.","Always undertake an immunodeficiency and autoimmune work-up, including immunoglobulins and HIV testing."],"pathophysiology":"LIP represents a reactive, polyclonal proliferation of lymphocytes and plasma cells that diffusely infiltrate the pulmonary interstitium and alveolar septa, frequently driven by autoimmune states (notably Sjögren syndrome), immunodeficiency and chronic antigenic or viral stimulation. Peribronchiolar lymphoid infiltration with air trapping is thought to produce the characteristic thin-walled cysts.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary MALT lymphoma and other lymphoproliferative disease","Follicular bronchiolitis","Cellular nonspecific interstitial pneumonia","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Sarcoidosis","Lymphangioleiomyomatosis or Birt-Hogg-Dubé syndrome as cystic mimics","Pneumocystis or other opportunistic infection in the immunocompromised"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["HRCT thorax to characterise ground-glass change and cysts","Autoimmune serology and a Sjögren work-up including anti-Ro/La antibodies and Schirmer test","HIV testing and serum immunoglobulins with electrophoresis","Surgical lung biopsy with immunohistochemistry and clonality studies to exclude lymphoma","Bronchoalveolar lavage typically showing lymphocytosis","Pulmonary function tests with transfer factor"],"redFlags":["Rapid enlargement of nodules or masses suggesting transformation to lymphoma","Constitutional symptoms raising concern for malignancy","Progressive respiratory failure","New opportunistic infection in an immunocompromised patient"],"prognosis":"The course is variable; many patients stabilise or improve with treatment of the underlying disorder, but some progress to fibrosis or, uncommonly, transform to lymphoma.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing low-grade pulmonary lymphoma as LIP or vice versa","Failing to investigate for Sjögren syndrome, HIV or immunodeficiency","Attributing pulmonary cysts solely to lymphangioleiomyomatosis or emphysema","Assuming a purely idiopathic process without searching for a secondary cause"],"management":["Treat the underlying disorder, with antiretroviral therapy in HIV, immunoglobulin replacement in common variable immunodeficiency, and control of Sjogren syndrome or other connective tissue disease","Systemic corticosteroids as the mainstay for symptomatic or progressive interstitial disease","Steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as azathioprine, mycophenolate or rituximab in refractory or relapsing disease","Supportive oxygen and pulmonary rehabilitation for breathlessness","Monitor with serial pulmonary function tests including transfer factor and interval imaging","Maintain surveillance for progression to fibrosis or transformation to pulmonary lymphoma","Refer to respiratory medicine and relevant specialties for multidisciplinary care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","ATS/ERS international multidisciplinary classification of the idiopathic interstitial pneumonias"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphogranuloma_venereum":{"name":"Lymphogranuloma venereum","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LGV","Durand-Nicolas-Favre disease","climatic bubo"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India NTEP","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A sexually transmitted infection caused by the invasive L1-L3 serovars of Chlamydia trachomatis, distinct from the serovars causing ordinary genital chlamydia","Classically progresses through a primary transient painless genital ulcer or papule, a secondary stage of painful inguinal lymphadenopathy (buboes), and a late destructive fibrotic stage","The 'groove sign', where enlarged nodes straddle the inguinal ligament, is a characteristic secondary-stage finding","In men who have sex with men, LGV commonly presents as a severe haemorrhagic proctocolitis that can mimic inflammatory bowel disease","Untreated late disease causes lymphatic obstruction with genital elephantiasis, strictures and fistulae","Diagnosis rests on detecting Chlamydia trachomatis by nucleic acid amplification with genotyping to identify LGV serovars, alongside serology in some settings","Treatment is a prolonged course of doxycycline as the preferred agent, with buboes aspirated rather than incised, and partners evaluated and treated"],"pathophysiology":"The L-serovars of Chlamydia trachomatis are more invasive than the ocular and genital serovars and preferentially infect macrophages and lymphatic tissue. After inoculation at a mucosal site, the organism spreads to draining lymph nodes, provoking a granulomatous and suppurative lymphadenitis with stellate abscesses. Persistent inflammation leads to lymphatic obstruction, fibrosis and tissue destruction in the chronic stage. Rectal inoculation produces an intense proctocolitis with ulceration and, if untreated, strictures and fistulae.","additionalDifferentials":["Chancroid with suppurative buboes","Primary or secondary syphilis","Genital herpes","Inflammatory bowel disease (for LGV proctocolitis)","Tuberculous or reactive inguinal lymphadenopathy"],"infectionMimics":["Chancroid","Tuberculous lymphadenitis of inguinal nodes","Cat-scratch disease lymphadenitis","Anorectal gonorrhoea or herpes proctitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nucleic acid amplification testing for Chlamydia trachomatis from ulcer, rectal or bubo aspirate specimens","Genotyping to confirm LGV (L1-L3) serovars","Chlamydia serology as supportive evidence in some settings","Screening for HIV, syphilis and other sexually transmitted infections","Sigmoidoscopy with biopsy when proctocolitis mimics inflammatory bowel disease"],"redFlags":["Severe proctocolitis with bleeding and pain, especially in HIV-positive men who have sex with men","Fluctuant buboes at risk of rupture requiring aspiration","Late fibrotic complications such as genital elephantiasis, strictures or fistulae","Coexisting HIV infection altering severity and management"],"prognosis":"Early treatment gives complete recovery, but delayed diagnosis allows irreversible lymphatic damage with strictures, fistulae and genital lymphoedema; extended antibiotic courses may be needed in advanced or HIV-associated disease.","pitfalls":["Mistaking LGV proctocolitis for inflammatory bowel disease and delaying antibiotics","Using a standard short chlamydia course rather than the extended regimen LGV requires","Incising buboes instead of aspirating, risking chronic sinus formation","Failing to genotype and thus missing invasive LGV serovars"],"management":["Extended-course oral doxycycline as first-line antimicrobial therapy, with erythromycin or azithromycin as alternatives (for example in pregnancy)","Aspiration of fluctuant buboes to relieve symptoms and prevent rupture, avoiding incision where possible","Prolonged antibiotic courses for late-stage or HIV-associated disease","Screen for and treat concurrent HIV, syphilis and other sexually transmitted infections","Notify, test and empirically treat recent sexual partners","Surgical management of late complications such as strictures, fistulae and genital lymphoedema","Refer to genitourinary or sexual health services for treatment and contact tracing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/NTEP guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphomatoid_granulomatosis":{"name":"Lymphomatoid granulomatosis (EBV)","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LYG","angiocentric immunoproliferative lesion","EBV-positive angiocentric B-cell lymphoproliferative disorder","pulmonary lymphomatoid granulomatosis","EBV-driven T-cell-rich B-cell proliferation"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An Epstein-Barr virus-driven angiocentric and angiodestructive lymphoproliferative disorder that most often involves the lungs, typically with bilateral nodules that may cavitate","Extrapulmonary sites include the skin, central nervous system, kidney, and liver, while the disease characteristically spares lymph nodes and bone marrow","Occurs more frequently in patients with underlying immunodeficiency, whether congenital, iatrogenic, or acquired","The lesion is composed of scattered EBV-infected atypical large B-cells set within an abundant reactive T-cell background, an angiocentric pattern that damages vessel walls","Disease is graded (one to three) according to the number of EBV-positive large B-cells and the extent of necrosis, guiding whether it behaves as a low-grade process or an overt large B-cell lymphoma","Presentation includes cough, dyspnoea, fever, weight loss, and neurological or skin manifestations","Management ranges from immune modulation such as interferon in low-grade disease to combination immunochemotherapy for high-grade lesions"],"pathophysiology":"Lymphomatoid granulomatosis reflects an Epstein-Barr virus-driven proliferation of B-cells that occurs when T-cell immune surveillance is deficient. EBV-infected atypical B-cells accumulate and invade the walls of small and medium vessels (angiocentric and angiodestructive growth), producing ischaemic necrosis within affected organs, most prominently the lung. A prominent reactive T-cell infiltrate accompanies the relatively sparse malignant B-cells. The number of EBV-positive B-cells and the degree of necrosis define the histological grade, and higher-grade lesions represent a form of EBV-positive large B-cell lymphoma, explaining the spectrum from indolent to aggressive behaviour.","additionalDifferentials":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Pulmonary infection with mycobacteria or fungi","Metastatic malignancy with multiple lung nodules","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma of the lung","Sarcoidosis with nodular disease","Other EBV-associated lymphoproliferative disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Tissue biopsy (often lung) with immunohistochemistry and in situ hybridisation for EBV-encoded RNA","Grading based on numbers of EBV-positive large B-cells and extent of necrosis","CT of the chest to characterise nodular, often cavitating disease","MRI of the brain when neurological involvement is suspected","Assessment of immune status including HIV testing and immunoglobulins","PET-CT and clonality studies to distinguish low- from high-grade disease"],"redFlags":["Progressive respiratory failure from extensive pulmonary involvement","Central nervous system involvement with neurological deficits","Histological progression to high-grade (grade 3) EBV-positive large B-cell lymphoma","Underlying severe immunodeficiency requiring correction"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable and grade-dependent: low-grade disease may respond to immune-modulating therapy and occasionally regresses with immune restoration, whereas high-grade disease behaves as an aggressive lymphoma requiring immunochemotherapy.","pitfalls":["Mistaking pulmonary nodules for infection or granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Undergrading disease and undertreating a lesion that is effectively a large B-cell lymphoma","Failing to identify and address an underlying immunodeficiency","Missing central nervous system involvement that alters management"],"management":["Grade-directed therapy, restoring immune surveillance in low-grade disease by reducing iatrogenic immunosuppression and considering interferon-alpha","Rituximab-based immunochemotherapy, such as with a CHOP-like regimen, for high-grade disease behaving as EBV-positive large B-cell lymphoma","Single-agent rituximab as an option in CD20-positive lower-grade disease","Correct or address underlying immunodeficiency, including HIV where present","Directed treatment and imaging surveillance for central nervous system and other extrapulmonary involvement","Serial imaging and repeat biopsy to detect progression or histological transformation","Manage under haemato-oncology with multidisciplinary input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","Swerdlow SH et al. WHO monograph on lymphoid neoplasms"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphomatoid_papulosis":{"name":"Lymphomatoid papulosis","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LyP","CD30-positive lymphoproliferative disorder of the skin","primary cutaneous CD30+ lymphoproliferative disorder","self-healing CD30+ eruption","CD30+ atypical T-cell papulosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as recurrent crops of self-regressing papules and nodules that may ulcerate and heal spontaneously over weeks, often leaving scars","Belongs to the spectrum of primary cutaneous CD30-positive lymphoproliferative disorders and follows a chronic but indolent course despite atypical, alarming histology","Multiple histological subtypes (including types A, B, C, D, and E) exist and can coexist, sometimes closely mimicking mycosis fungoides or anaplastic large-cell lymphoma microscopically","The atypical cells are usually CD30-positive T-cells, and clonal T-cell receptor rearrangements are common yet do not equate to systemic malignancy","There is an increased lifetime risk of an associated lymphoma, particularly mycosis fungoides, primary cutaneous anaplastic large-cell lymphoma, or Hodgkin lymphoma","The clinicopathological correlation is essential: the benign, waxing-and-waning clinical behaviour distinguishes it from aggressive lymphoma","Management is often expectant, with skin-directed therapy or low-dose methotrexate for extensive or symptomatic disease"],"pathophysiology":"Lymphomatoid papulosis is an indolent, chronic clonal proliferation of CD30-positive T-lymphocytes confined to the skin. Despite harbouring monoclonal T-cell receptor rearrangements and cytologically atypical, often anaplastic-looking cells, the lesions characteristically regress spontaneously, reflecting effective local immune control and apoptosis of the neoplastic clone. The same aberrant clone can, in a minority of patients, underlie or precede an associated lymphoma such as mycosis fungoides, cutaneous anaplastic large-cell lymphoma, or Hodgkin lymphoma, placing lymphomatoid papulosis at the indolent end of the CD30-positive lymphoproliferative spectrum.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary cutaneous anaplastic large-cell lymphoma","Mycosis fungoides","Pityriasis lichenoides et varioliformis acuta (PLEVA)","Arthropod bite reactions or other benign papular eruptions","Systemic anaplastic large-cell lymphoma with skin involvement","Cutaneous involvement by other T-cell lymphomas"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with immunohistochemistry for CD30 and T-cell markers","T-cell receptor clonality analysis of lesional skin","Clinicopathological correlation documenting the self-regressing behaviour","Surveillance for an associated systemic or cutaneous lymphoma","Staging investigations only if a concurrent lymphoma is suspected clinically","Photographic mapping to monitor the recurrent, migratory eruption"],"redFlags":["A lesion that fails to regress and progressively enlarges, suggesting cutaneous anaplastic large-cell lymphoma","New lymphadenopathy or systemic symptoms indicating an associated lymphoma","Development of persistent plaques or tumours typical of mycosis fungoides","Rapidly evolving widespread disease that departs from the usual indolent pattern"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent with a normal life expectancy in most patients, but lifelong surveillance is warranted because a subset develop an associated lymphoma over time.","pitfalls":["Overtreating a benign, self-regressing condition as aggressive lymphoma because of alarming histology","Interpreting a clonal T-cell population as proof of systemic malignancy","Missing an evolving associated lymphoma through inadequate follow-up","Confusing type C histology with primary cutaneous anaplastic large-cell lymphoma without clinical correlation"],"management":["Active observation with reassurance for limited disease, given the self-regressing indolent course","Phototherapy with PUVA or narrowband UVB, or low-dose methotrexate, to suppress extensive or symptomatic eruptions","Topical corticosteroids or topical retinoids for individual lesions","Reserve brentuximab vedotin or oral retinoids such as bexarotene for refractory or highly symptomatic disease, avoiding aggressive chemotherapy","Lifelong surveillance for an associated lymphoma, particularly mycosis fungoides, primary cutaneous anaplastic large-cell lymphoma or Hodgkin lymphoma","Photographic mapping to track the recurrent, migratory lesions","Refer to dermatology and haemato-oncology for diagnostic confirmation and long-term follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","Swerdlow SH et al. WHO monograph on lymphoid neoplasms"],"crossLinks":[]},"lynch_syndrome":{"name":"Lynch syndrome","system":"Oncology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer","HNPCC","MLH1","MSH2","MSH6","PMS2","EPCAM","mismatch-repair deficiency syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer / NCCN genetic/familial guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by germline pathogenic variants in a DNA mismatch-repair gene (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6 or PMS2) or by EPCAM deletions silencing MSH2, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern","The dominant risk is colorectal cancer, characteristically right-sided and early-onset, together with endometrial cancer as the leading extracolonic tumour in women","The wider spectrum includes ovarian, gastric, small-bowel, urothelial, hepatobiliary, pancreatic and brain tumours, with risk varying by the gene involved","Tumours typically show microsatellite instability and loss of mismatch-repair protein expression on immunohistochemistry, which guides germline testing","MLH1 promoter methylation and BRAF testing help distinguish sporadic microsatellite-unstable tumours from true Lynch syndrome","Regular colonoscopic surveillance with polyp removal substantially reduces colorectal cancer incidence and mortality","Amsterdam II and revised Bethesda criteria, alongside universal tumour screening, identify individuals who should proceed to germline testing"],"pathophysiology":"The syndrome stems from a germline defect in the DNA mismatch-repair system, which normally corrects base-base mismatches and small insertion-deletion loops that arise during replication. When the remaining functional allele is lost in a somatic cell, repair fails and errors accumulate, most conspicuously in repetitive microsatellite sequences, producing the microsatellite instability that characterises these tumours. The resulting hypermutable state generates numerous mutations in growth-regulatory genes, accelerating carcinogenesis and also creating neoantigens that make the tumours responsive to immune checkpoint blockade.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial adenomatous polyposis and attenuated variants","MUTYH-associated polyposis","Constitutional mismatch-repair deficiency syndrome","Familial colorectal cancer type X without mismatch-repair deficiency","Sporadic microsatellite-unstable cancer due to MLH1 promoter methylation","Polymerase proofreading-associated polyposis (POLE/POLD1)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Tumour testing with mismatch-repair immunohistochemistry and microsatellite instability analysis","MLH1 promoter methylation and BRAF V600E testing to exclude sporadic causes when MLH1 is lost","Germline sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis of mismatch-repair genes and EPCAM","Colonoscopic surveillance at accelerated intervals","Gynaecological assessment including endometrial evaluation in women","Consideration of upper gastrointestinal and urinary tract surveillance based on the affected gene and family history"],"redFlags":["Early-onset or right-sided colorectal cancer, particularly with mismatch-repair-deficient histology","Synchronous or metachronous Lynch-spectrum tumours in one individual","Postmenopausal or unexplained abnormal uterine bleeding in a carrier suggesting endometrial cancer","Painless haematuria in a carrier raising concern for urothelial malignancy"],"prognosis":"Cancer risk is high but gene-dependent, being greatest for MLH1 and MSH2 and lower for MSH6 and PMS2. Colonoscopic surveillance markedly reduces colorectal cancer mortality, and mismatch-repair-deficient tumours often respond well to immune checkpoint inhibitors, so outcomes are frequently favourable when the diagnosis is recognised.","pitfalls":["Labelling a microsatellite-unstable tumour as Lynch syndrome without excluding MLH1 promoter methylation","Relying solely on family history criteria and missing carriers who do not meet Amsterdam or Bethesda thresholds","Overlooking endometrial and other extracolonic risks by focusing only on the colon","Failing to arrange cascade testing of relatives once a germline variant is confirmed"],"management":["Regular colonoscopic surveillance at accelerated intervals with removal of polyps to reduce colorectal cancer incidence and mortality","Aspirin chemoprevention to lower colorectal cancer risk","Gynaecological surveillance with risk-reducing hysterectomy and bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy after completion of childbearing to address endometrial and ovarian risk","Gene- and family-history-directed upper gastrointestinal and urinary tract surveillance, with Helicobacter pylori testing and eradication","Immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy for mismatch-repair-deficient cancers, which are highly responsive","Consider extended rather than segmental colectomy when a colorectal cancer arises","Genetic counselling with germline testing and cascade screening of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer: Principles and Practice of Oncology","NCCN Guidelines: Genetic/Familial High-Risk Assessment - Colorectal"],"crossLinks":[]},"lysinuric_protein_intolerance":{"name":"Lysinuric protein intolerance","system":"Gastroenterology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SLC7A7","LPI","cationic aminoaciduria","y+LAT1 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive defect of dibasic amino acid transport caused by SLC7A7 variants affecting the y+LAT1 transporter","Symptoms often begin after weaning onto protein-rich feeds, with vomiting, diarrhoea, aversion to protein and failure to thrive","Impaired handling of lysine, arginine and ornithine limits urea-cycle intermediates, causing hyperammonaemia after protein loads","Characteristic biochemistry includes low plasma dibasic amino acids with increased urinary excretion of lysine, arginine and ornithine","Multisystem complications include hepatosplenomegaly, osteoporosis, and a potentially fatal pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","Management combines protein restriction with citrulline supplementation and ammonia-lowering strategies to protect against hyperammonaemia","Because the transporter defect involves gut and kidney, both malabsorption and renal amino acid wasting contribute to the phenotype"],"pathophysiology":"Lysinuric protein intolerance results from loss of function of the SLC7A7-encoded y+LAT1 transporter, which mediates efflux of the cationic amino acids lysine, arginine and ornithine across the basolateral membrane of enterocytes and renal tubular cells. Defective transport impairs intestinal absorption and promotes renal loss of these amino acids, lowering their plasma concentrations. Because arginine and ornithine are urea-cycle intermediates, their deficiency limits ureagenesis and leads to hyperammonaemia, especially after protein intake. The disorder also disturbs macrophage and immune function, contributing to the systemic features such as pulmonary alveolar proteinosis and haematological abnormalities.","additionalDifferentials":["Urea cycle disorders","Cystinuria (dibasic aminoaciduria without hyperammonaemia)","Organic acidaemias presenting with hyperammonaemia","Other congenital enteropathies causing protein malnutrition","Haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (overlapping features)","Primary causes of pulmonary alveolar proteinosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma and urine amino acid profiles showing low plasma with high urinary lysine, arginine and ornithine","Blood ammonia, particularly after protein intake, and orotic acid measurement","Molecular genetic testing for SLC7A7 variants","Assessment for hepatosplenomegaly and haematological abnormalities","Chest imaging and respiratory evaluation for pulmonary alveolar proteinosis"],"redFlags":["Acute hyperammonaemic encephalopathy after a protein load","Respiratory deterioration from pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","Progressive hepatosplenomegaly or features of a haemophagocytic process","Severe growth failure and osteoporosis from chronic malnutrition"],"prognosis":"With protein restriction, citrulline supplementation and ammonia control, metabolic stability and growth can be maintained, but multisystem complications such as pulmonary alveolar proteinosis and renal or immune involvement affect long-term outcome. Prognosis varies with the severity of these systemic manifestations.","pitfalls":["Missing intermittent hyperammonaemia because ammonia may be normal when protein intake is low","Confusing the aminoaciduria with cystinuria and overlooking the urea-cycle consequences","Excessive protein restriction causing malnutrition rather than balanced protein control with citrulline","Failing to screen for pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, a major cause of morbidity"],"management":["Moderate dietary protein restriction combined with oral citrulline supplementation to replenish urea-cycle intermediates and support ureagenesis","Nitrogen-scavenging agents such as sodium benzoate or sodium phenylbutyrate to control hyperammonaemia","Emergency management of hyperammonaemic crises with cessation of protein intake, intravenous glucose and, if severe, haemodialysis or haemofiltration","Lysine and other targeted supplementation to correct deficiencies, with calcium and vitamin D, and bisphosphonates where needed, for osteoporosis","Surveillance and treatment of pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, including whole-lung lavage, with monitoring of haematological and renal complications","Regular monitoring of plasma ammonia, amino acid profiles and growth","A written emergency regimen for intercurrent illness to pre-empt metabolic decompensation","Genetic counselling for the autosomal recessive inheritance with testing of at-risk family members"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"lysosomal_acid_lipase_deficiency":{"name":"Lysosomal acid lipase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LAL deficiency","LAL-D","LIPA deficiency","Wolman disease (infantile form)","acid lipase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive LIPA deficiency spanning a continuous spectrum from severe infantile Wolman disease to milder late-onset cholesteryl ester storage disease","Infantile Wolman disease presents in the first weeks of life with vomiting, diarrhoea, malabsorption, failure to thrive, marked hepatosplenomegaly and rapid deterioration","Adrenal enlargement with punctate calcification on imaging is a highly suggestive feature of the infantile form","Later-onset disease presents with hepatomegaly, transaminitis, hepatic steatosis and an atherogenic dyslipidaemia (high LDL, low HDL)","Diagnosis rests on low lysosomal acid lipase enzyme activity (readily measured on dried blood spot) confirmed by LIPA genetic testing","Enzyme replacement therapy with recombinant lysosomal acid lipase (sebelipase alfa) is the disease-specific treatment and is transformative in the infantile form","Supportive care includes nutritional support, lipid-lowering therapy and monitoring for hepatic fibrosis in the later-onset spectrum"],"pathophysiology":"Lysosomal acid lipase hydrolyses cholesteryl esters and triglycerides taken up by cells through lipoprotein endocytosis, releasing free cholesterol and fatty acids for cellular use. Complete or near-complete deficiency causes massive lysosomal accumulation of cholesteryl esters and triglycerides in liver, spleen, intestine, adrenal glands and macrophages, producing the fulminant multi-organ infantile Wolman phenotype; partial deficiency causes the slower cholesteryl ester storage disease phenotype. Impaired liberation of free cholesterol disrupts cholesterol homeostasis, driving compensatory synthesis and the characteristic dyslipidaemia, while progressive tissue storage leads to hepatic failure or, in milder disease, fibrosis and premature atherosclerosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of infantile hepatosplenomegaly with failure to thrive (e.g. Niemann-Pick, Gaucher)","Neonatal haemophagocytic or infiltrative disorders","Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease in the later-onset form","Familial hypercholesterolaemia and mixed dyslipidaemias","Cystic fibrosis or other causes of malabsorption in infancy","Glycogen storage disorders with hepatomegaly"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lysosomal acid lipase enzyme activity assay (dried blood spot or leucocytes)","LIPA molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Abdominal imaging for hepatosplenomegaly and adrenal calcification","Fasting lipid profile and liver function tests","Liver biopsy showing microvesicular steatosis and lipid-laden macrophages where needed","Growth, nutritional and cardiovascular risk assessment across the spectrum"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive failure to thrive with hepatosplenomegaly in early infancy","Adrenal calcification with signs of adrenal insufficiency","Progression to hepatic fibrosis, cirrhosis or portal hypertension","Premature cardiovascular disease from severe dyslipidaemia"],"prognosis":"Untreated infantile Wolman disease is almost uniformly fatal within the first months of life, whereas the later-onset form is more indolent; enzyme replacement therapy substantially improves survival and hepatic and metabolic outcomes across the spectrum.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis in infants by not measuring enzyme activity in unexplained hepatosplenomegaly and failure to thrive","Mislabelling later-onset disease as non-alcoholic fatty liver disease or familial hypercholesterolaemia","Overlooking adrenal insufficiency in the infantile form","Delaying enzyme replacement therapy in rapidly progressive infantile disease"],"management":["Enzyme replacement therapy with recombinant lysosomal acid lipase (sebelipase alfa) as the disease-specific mainstay, transformative in the infantile Wolman form","In infantile disease, specialised low-fat/modified formula with aggressive nutritional support including parenteral nutrition for malabsorption and failure to thrive","Corticosteroid and mineralocorticoid replacement where adrenal involvement produces adrenal insufficiency","Lipid-lowering therapy with a statin and ezetimibe for the atherogenic dyslipidaemia of later-onset cholesteryl ester storage disease","Surveillance of liver function, hepatic fibrosis progression on elastography or imaging, lipid profile, growth and cardiovascular risk","Consider liver transplantation for progressive hepatic failure, alongside continued enzyme replacement","Multidisciplinary metabolic, hepatology and dietetic care with genetic counselling and sibling screening for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"lysosomal_storage_diseases":{"name":"Lysosomal Storage Diseases","system":"Metabolic / Inherited (Endocrinology and Metabolism)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lysosomal system diseases","LSDs","Tay-Sachs disease","Fabry disease","Gaucher disease","Niemann-Pick disease","Mucopolysaccharidoses","Pompe disease","Lysosomal acid lipase deficiency","LALD","Krabbe disease","CLN2 Batten disease","Alpha-mannosidosis"],"chapter":429,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3351-3358","clinicalPearls":["Lysosomes are subcellular organelles holding specific hydrolases that process or degrade proteins, nucleic acids, carbohydrates and lipids; there are more than 50 distinct lysosomal storage diseases (now also called lysosomal system diseases), grouped by the predominant accumulated material.","Consider an LSD in the differential when a patient has unexplained neurologic, renal or muscular degeneration, or unexplained hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, cardiomyopathy, or skeletal dysplasias/deformities; physical findings are disease-specific and diagnosis is confirmed by enzyme assay or genetic testing.","Nearly all LSDs are autosomal recessive, with notable exceptions: Hunter disease (MPS II), Danon disease and Fabry disease are X-linked, and two autosomal dominant conditions exist (Parry-type CLN from DNAJC5 mutations, and frontotemporal dementia/CLN11 from GRN/progranulin mutations).","Tay-Sachs disease (total hexosaminidase A deficiency) is carried by roughly 1 in 30 Ashkenazi Jews; the infantile form features macrocephaly, loss of motor skills, an exaggerated startle reaction and a macular cherry-red spot, with carrier screening recommended in this population.","Fabry disease (X-linked, alpha-galactosidase A deficiency) presents with angiokeratomas, hypohidrosis, corneal and lenticular opacities, debilitating episodic burning pain of the hands and feet (acroparesthesia), and progressive renal, cardiac and cerebrovascular disease; angiokeratomas are punctate, dark red to blue-black, do not blanch and cluster in the 'bathing suit area' between umbilicus and knees.","Gaucher disease (acid beta-glucosidase deficiency, GBA1) is classified by CNS involvement: type 1 is nonneuronopathic with hepatosplenomegaly and bone disease, type 2 is a severe progressive CNS disease causing death by about 2 years, and type 3 has variable CNS and visceral disease; early CNS signs in type 3 may be limited to supranuclear lateral gaze palsy.","GBA1 mutations, whether heterozygous or homozygous, substantially raise the lifetime risk of Parkinson disease.","Niemann-Pick types A and B reflect acid sphingomyelinase deficiency: type A has early onset with rapid CNS deterioration, while type B is later/variable with hepatosplenomegaly, cirrhosis, and lethal progressive pulmonary disease (foam cells in alveoli, lymphatics and pulmonary arteries); Niemann-Pick C is a separate disorder from NPC1/NPC2 defects in cholesterol/sphingolipid transport.","Pompe disease (acid alpha-glucosidase/acid maltase deficiency, GAA) is the only LSD causing primary glycogen storage; the classic infantile form has hypotonia, cardiomyopathy and hepatosplenomegaly with near-total enzyme deficiency, whereas late-onset forms resemble a limb-girdle myopathy and may not have cardiomyopathy.","Lysosomal acid lipase deficiency (LIPA mutations) spans infantile Wolman syndrome (failure to thrive, vomiting, hepatosplenomegaly; median death about 3.7 months) and childhood/adult-onset cholesterol ester storage disease (elevated liver enzymes, microvesicular fatty liver, cryptogenic cirrhosis); neither variant causes primary CNS disease and statins do not halt the underlying tissue pathology.","CLN2 (neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 2, TPP1/tripeptidyl peptidase 1 deficiency) usually begins at age 2-4 years with seizures, ataxia, myoclonus and vision loss, progressing to severe disability and death around age 12; it is the only NCL with a specific approved treatment.","Alpha-mannosidosis (MAN2B1 biallelic loss of function) accumulates mannose-rich oligosaccharides and causes intellectual disability, incapacitating skeletal changes, immunodeficiency, ataxia, hearing loss, neurodegeneration and psychosis."],"pathophysiology":"Lysosomal biogenesis requires ongoing synthesis of lysosomal hydrolases, membrane proteins and new membranes; lysosomes form by fusion of trans-Golgi network vesicles with late endosomes, and progressive acidification creates a pH gradient that drives pH-dependent dissociation of receptors from ligands and activates the hydrolases. Lysosomes are part of the lysosome/autophagy/mitophagy system regulated by mTORC1-mediated phosphorylation of transcription factors TFEB/TFE3, which balances anabolic and catabolic pathways; this regulation is disrupted to varying degrees in tissues affected by specific LSDs. Defects at any biosynthetic step can impair enzyme function: oligosaccharide remodeling in the Golgi (including the mannose-6-phosphate lysosomal targeting ligand), membrane sorting signals, phosphorylation, sulfation, additional proteolysis and heteromer assembly are all critical, and abnormalities in these shared processes can produce multiple simultaneous enzyme/protein deficiencies. The final common pathway is accumulation of specific macromolecules within tissues and cells that normally carry a high flux of those substrates; most deficiencies arise from point mutations or rearrangements at a single hydrolase/subunit locus, though some mutations affecting targeting, active sites, macromolecular assembly or trafficking impair several hydrolases at once. Substrate buildup distorts lysosomes and may exert pharmacologic effects, notably activation of innate immune responses. Some substrates are made within the tissue of pathology, while others are delivered exogenously (LDL receptor-mediated uptake in Fabry and LALD; phagocytosis in Gaucher type 1). The threshold hypothesis holds that disease emerges below a certain level of residual enzyme activity, and that activity can be challenged by changes in substrate flux from genetic background, cell turnover, recycling or metabolic demand, so a given residual level may suffice in some tissues but not others.","additionalDifferentials":["Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (mimicked by late-onset Pompe disease)","Idiopathic hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (about 1-3% have Fabry disease)","Idiopathic stroke at 35-50 years (about 2-5% carry GLA mutations)","Idiopathic renal failure in males (about 5% have GLA mutations)","Cryptogenic cirrhosis / nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (childhood/adult-onset LALD)","Familial hyperlipidemia (LALD)","Parkinson disease (associated with GBA1 carriage)","Acute appendicitis or renal colic (Fabry abdominal pain can mimic these)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (Fabry cardiac variant)","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (late-onset Pompe)","Cryptogenic/idiopathic cirrhosis (childhood/adult-onset LALD)","Other neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses (significant clinical overlap with CLN2)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Enzyme assay in nucleated cells, serum/plasma, or leukocytes specific to the suspected disorder","Molecular/genetic testing: whole GBA1 sequencing is the standard for Gaucher (enzyme testing has poor sensitivity for heterozygote detection)","Slit-lamp examination for corneal and lenticular lesions to support a Fabry diagnosis","Decreased acid beta-glucosidase activity (0-20% of normal) in nucleated cells establishes Gaucher disease; the enzyme is normally absent from bodily fluids","Markedly reduced acid sphingomyelinase activity (1-10% of normal) in nucleated cells confirms Niemann-Pick A/B","Confirmation of specific enzyme deficiency in Morquio (GALNS vs acid beta-galactosidase) before therapy","Gene sequencing to confirm CLN2 given overlap among the neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses","Newborn screening (now includes MPS I and, in many states, Pompe disease)","Bone imaging / nuclear scan in Gaucher bone crises (localized absent uptake of pyrophosphate agents indicates acute bone infarction)"],"redFlags":["Macular cherry-red spot with macrocephaly and exaggerated startle in an infant (infantile Tay-Sachs)","Splenic infarction in Gaucher disease that can present as an acute abdomen","Gaucher 'bone crisis' with excruciating localized pain, sometimes erythema, fever and leukocytosis, representing acute bone infarction","Pulmonary hypertension or alveolar Gaucher cell accumulation (uncommon but life-threatening, more common in splenectomized females)","Vertebral collapse and avascular necrosis (femoral head) with potential spinal cord involvement in Gaucher disease","Infantile-onset LALD presenting as a medical emergency with severe failure to thrive, vomiting and hepatosplenomegaly","Fetal hydrops in severe MPS VII (Sly), which can cause stillbirth or perinatal demise","Progressive pulmonary disease with dyspnea and hypoxemia in Niemann-Pick type B","Respiratory insufficiency requiring ventilation in late-onset Pompe disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies enormously across and within disorders, each spanning a spectrum from severe to attenuated forms. Infantile Tay-Sachs is fatal in infancy; juvenile-onset Tay-Sachs causes death by 10-15 years. In Fabry disease, death results from cardiovascular, renal or cerebrovascular disease; renal insufficiency, cardiac fibrosis and stroke are irreversible, so early therapy offers the best chance to slow life-threatening complications, and heart disease is the most common life-threatening manifestation in females while renal then cardiovascular disease predominates in males. Gaucher type 2 leads to death by about 2 years, while type 1 and type 3 are highly variable. Niemann-Pick type B can progress to death in adolescence or early adulthood from hepatic or pulmonary disease. Infantile Pompe is rapidly progressive with death typically in the first year without treatment. Infantile-onset LALD is fatal by about 1 year (median 3.7 months) untreated, and untreated childhood/adult-onset LALD progresses to early cirrhosis (mean about age 13) and early-adulthood atherosclerosis. Early infantile Krabbe presents around 4 months and progresses rapidly to death at a mean of about 18 months. CLN2 progresses to severe disability with most children wheelchair-bound in late childhood, death at a mean of about 12 years, and survival beyond adolescence rare.","pitfalls":["Treating the disease nosology as fixed discrete entities is misleading: each LSD is heuristic and clinically expresses a continuous spectrum from severe to attenuated forms.","Angiokeratomas of Fabry disease can be easily overlooked and also occur in several other rare LSDs, so they are not specific.","Fabry abdominal pain may be mistaken for appendicitis or renal colic, delaying diagnosis.","Enzyme testing has poor sensitivity for Gaucher heterozygote detection; molecular GBA1 sequencing is required.","In Niemann-Pick, hepatic or bone marrow transplantation has produced more complications than expected (recurrent hepatic disease from ASM-deficient myeloid repopulation after liver transplant, or failure to clear hepatocyte sphingomyelin after BMT/HSCT).","Enzyme replacement therapy does not cross the blood-brain barrier and does not directly treat CNS disease (e.g., MPS I, MPS II); HSCT has not succeeded against CNS disease in MPS II.","Statins lower hypercholesterolemia in LALD but do not alter the underlying progressive tissue (e.g., liver) pathology.","Late cardiac and respiratory complications of MPS I (obstructive breathing, cardiomyopathy, valve disease) can emerge even after successful HSCT, so lifelong follow-up is required.","Morquio variants A and B require confirmation of the specific enzyme deficiency because approved enzyme therapy (elosulfase alfa) applies only to MPS IVA."],"management":["Disease-specific enzyme replacement therapy where a recombinant enzyme exists (imiglucerase for Gaucher, agalsidase for Fabry, alglucosidase alfa for Pompe, laronidase or idursulfase for the mucopolysaccharidoses)","Substrate reduction therapy (miglustat, eliglustat) or pharmacological chaperone therapy (migalastat for amenable Fabry variants) as alternatives or adjuncts","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for selected neuronopathic disorders such as certain mucopolysaccharidoses when performed early","Organ-directed supportive care: management of hepatosplenomegaly and cytopenias, cardiac and renal support, seizure control, analgesia for neuropathic pain, and skeletal or orthopaedic care","Structured surveillance for the disorder-specific complications across cardiac, renal, neurological, skeletal and ophthalmological systems","Multidisciplinary care coordinated through a specialist metabolic centre","Genetic counselling with carrier and family screening, noting X-linked inheritance in Fabry, Hunter and Danon disease"],"severityClassification":"Within individual diseases, severity is typically stratified by age of onset and CNS involvement. Examples: Tay-Sachs is divided into infantile, juvenile-onset and adult-onset forms. MPS I is graded as Hurler (MPS I H, severe with neurodegeneration), Hurler-Scheie (MPS I H/S, intermediate, severe somatic disease without major neurologic decline), and Scheie (MPS I S, later-onset without neurologic involvement). Gaucher disease is classed as type 1 (nonneuronopathic), type 2 (acute severe CNS), and type 3 (variable subacute/chronic CNS). Niemann-Pick distinguishes type A (early-onset, progressive CNS), type B (later, variable, with an A/B variant having slowly progressive CNS involvement), and the separate type C. LALD ranges from infantile-onset (Wolman) to childhood/adult-onset (cholesterol ester storage disease). Pompe and Krabbe are divided into early/infantile and later-onset variants.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3351","Harrison 22e p.3355","Harrison 22e p.3356","Harrison 22e p.3357","Harrison 22e p.3358"],"crossLinks":[]},"lysozyme_amyloidosis":{"name":"Lysozyme Amyloidosis (ALys)","system":"Multisystem / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ALys amyloidosis","Hereditary lysozyme amyloidosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oxford Textbook of Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["ALys is a rare autosomal dominant amyloidosis caused by mutations in the lysozyme gene (LYZ).","It is characteristically multi-visceral, with prominent involvement of the kidney, liver, spleen and gastrointestinal tract.","Spontaneous and potentially life-threatening haemorrhage is a hallmark, with reports of hepatic and splenic rupture and gastrointestinal bleeding.","Sicca symptoms of dry eyes and mouth and cutaneous petechiae are recognised features.","Renal involvement is common but often slowly progressive, with nephrotic syndrome developing over years.","Cardiac and neurological involvement are typically mild or absent, which helps distinguish ALys from ATTR.","Diagnosis rests on tissue typing and LYZ sequencing; there is no specific antifibril therapy, so management is supportive and directed at complications."],"pathophysiology":"Lysozyme is a bacteriolytic enzyme secreted by macrophages, Paneth cells and various epithelia. Amyloidogenic variants destabilise the folded protein, promoting partial unfolding, aggregation and deposition of lysozyme-derived amyloid fibrils in the extracellular matrix of viscera. Deposition is widespread but shows particular avidity for the gastrointestinal tract, liver, spleen and kidney, where infiltration weakens tissue integrity and predisposes to haemorrhage and organ dysfunction. Because lysozyme is produced by many cell types, the amyloid source cannot be removed by transplantation of a single organ, and deposition continues over decades in a slowly progressive fashion.","additionalDifferentials":["AL (immunoglobulin light-chain) amyloidosis","Other hereditary amyloidoses (fibrinogen, apolipoprotein A-I, transthyretin)","AA amyloidosis","Other causes of gastrointestinal bleeding or hepatosplenomegaly","Sjögren syndrome for the sicca features","Other causes of nephrotic syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy of affected tissue (gut, liver or kidney) with Congo red staining","Amyloid typing by mass spectrometry or immunohistochemistry","LYZ gene sequencing","Cross-sectional imaging of the liver and spleen to assess size and rupture risk","Renal function and quantified proteinuria","Full blood count and clotting studies given the bleeding risk","Serum free light chains and immunofixation to exclude AL amyloidosis"],"redFlags":["Acute abdominal pain with haemodynamic compromise suggesting hepatic or splenic rupture","Gastrointestinal haemorrhage","Rapidly enlarging liver or spleen","Progressive proteinuria and renal decline"],"prognosis":"The disorder is generally slowly progressive, with survival often measured in decades, but life-threatening visceral haemorrhage from organ rupture is the principal cause of acute mortality.","pitfalls":["Mistyping as AL amyloidosis and exposing patients to chemotherapy","Underestimating the risk of spontaneous organ rupture","Attributing sicca symptoms to Sjögren syndrome without considering amyloid","Relying on single-organ transplantation to remove the amyloid source"],"management":["No single-organ transplant removes the amyloid source, as lysozyme is produced by many cell types, so management is largely supportive and surveillance-based","Vigilant management of spontaneous visceral haemorrhage from hepatic or splenic rupture and gastrointestinal bleeding, with resuscitation, correction of coagulopathy, and interventional radiological embolisation or surgery","Manage renal involvement with renin-angiotensin blockade for proteinuria and nephrotic syndrome, progressing to renal replacement therapy as disease advances","Surveillance cross-sectional imaging of liver and spleen for organomegaly and rupture risk, with advice to avoid contact sports and abdominal trauma","Symptomatic treatment of sicca symptoms with ocular lubricants and saliva substitutes","Multidisciplinary care through a specialist amyloidosis centre spanning haematology, nephrology and gastroenterology","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal dominant LYZ inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Oxford Textbook of Medicine, chapter on hereditary amyloidosis","Williams Hematology, chapter on amyloidosis","International Society of Amyloidosis nomenclature guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"machado_joseph_disease":{"name":"Machado-Joseph disease (SCA3)","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA3","spinocerebellar ataxia type 3","Machado-Joseph disease","ATXN3-related ataxia","ataxin-3 CAG repeat expansion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxia worldwide, caused by a CAG repeat expansion in the ATXN3 gene encoding a polyglutamine tract in ataxin-3","Presents with progressive cerebellar ataxia combined with variable pyramidal, extrapyramidal, and peripheral features, giving a broad clinical spectrum","Characteristic signs include prominent, bulging eyes with lid retraction, gaze-evoked nystagmus, and progressive external ophthalmoplegia","Faciolingual fasciculations and myokymia, dystonia, spasticity, and a peripheral neuropathy with amyotrophy are recognised features","Recognised clinical subtypes range from early-onset spastic-dystonic forms to later-onset predominantly ataxic or peripheral-neuropathy phenotypes depending on repeat length and age","Demonstrates anticipation, with earlier onset and greater severity as the repeat expands across generations","Confirmed by molecular testing for the ATXN3 CAG expansion; treatment is symptomatic and multidisciplinary"],"pathophysiology":"Machado-Joseph disease (SCA3) is a polyglutamine disorder caused by expansion of the CAG repeat in the ATXN3 gene. Ataxin-3 normally functions as a deubiquitinating enzyme involved in protein quality control; the expanded polyglutamine tract confers a toxic gain of function, promoting misfolding, aggregation, and formation of neuronal intranuclear inclusions that impair the ubiquitin-proteasome system and cellular homeostasis. Neurodegeneration affects the cerebellum and its connections, brainstem and cranial nerve nuclei, basal ganglia, spinal motor and sensory pathways, and peripheral nerves, accounting for the wide phenotypic range. Repeat length correlates inversely with age at onset, underlying anticipation.","additionalDifferentials":["Other dominant spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA1, SCA2, SCA6, SCA7)","Multiple system atrophy","Parkinson disease or dystonia syndromes when extrapyramidal features dominate","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis when amyotrophy is prominent","Friedreich ataxia","Acquired cerebellar degenerations"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted molecular genetic testing for ATXN3 CAG repeat expansion","Brain MRI showing cerebellar, brainstem, and sometimes basal ganglia atrophy","Nerve conduction studies for associated peripheral neuropathy","Detailed oculomotor examination for ophthalmoplegia and nystagmus","Assessment for dystonia, spasticity, and parkinsonism","Swallowing and speech assessment as bulbar involvement progresses"],"redFlags":["Progressive dysphagia with aspiration risk","Severe spasticity or dystonia impairing mobility and safety","Respiratory or bulbar decline in advanced disease"],"prognosis":"The disease is progressive over one to several decades with steadily accumulating disability; life expectancy is reduced, usually as a consequence of bulbar dysfunction and respiratory complications, although progression rate varies with subtype.","pitfalls":["Attributing the diverse features to separate diagnoses rather than recognising the SCA3 spectrum","Assuming a subtype without gene-specific confirmation","Missing the characteristic bulging eyes and faciolingual fasciculations as diagnostic clues","Neglecting anticipation and genetic counselling for at-risk relatives"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so care centres on symptomatic treatment and maintaining function","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and balance and gait training with walking aids for progressive cerebellar ataxia","Targeted pharmacotherapy for specific features: dopaminergic agents such as levodopa for parkinsonism, baclofen for spasticity, and botulinum toxin for dystonia","Speech and language therapy with swallowing assessment for dysarthria and dysphagia, with attention to aspiration risk","Monitoring and management of complications including falls, contractures, sleep disturbance, depression, and respiratory compromise from bulbar dysfunction","Multidisciplinary neurology-led care with rehabilitation, dietetics and palliative planning as disease advances","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant CAG-repeat disorder, with predictive and reproductive testing options and awareness of anticipation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"macrocephaly_capillary_malformation":{"name":"Macrocephaly-capillary malformation","system":"Skin / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Macrocephaly-capillary malformation (M-CM)","Megalencephaly-capillary malformation (MCAP)","Macrocephaly-cutis marmorata telangiectatica congenita"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews and standard paediatric neurology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital overgrowth syndrome within the PIK3CA-related overgrowth spectrum (PROS).","Combines macrocephaly or megalencephaly with a midline facial capillary malformation and often reticulate or patchy capillary staining of the trunk and limbs.","Caused by somatic mosaic activating PIK3CA mutations.","Additional features include body asymmetry (hemihypertrophy), syndactyly or polydactyly, connective-tissue laxity, developmental delay and hypotonia.","Neuroimaging may show megalencephaly, ventriculomegaly, polymicrogyria and cerebellar tonsillar herniation.","There is a risk of progressive hydrocephalus and a Chiari-like malformation from ongoing brain overgrowth.","Cardiac arrhythmia has been reported and warrants baseline and periodic assessment.","Requires multidisciplinary neurodevelopmental, orthopaedic and neurosurgical follow-up."],"pathophysiology":"Macrocephaly-capillary malformation is one of the PIK3CA-related overgrowth spectrum disorders, arising from postzygotic somatic mosaic activating mutations in PIK3CA that constitutively activate the PI3K/AKT/mTOR pathway and drive excessive cellular growth and proliferation in affected tissues. The mosaic distribution explains the segmental and asymmetric overgrowth, capillary malformations and brain overgrowth (megalencephaly) with cortical malformations such as polymicrogyria. Progressive brain and skull growth can secondarily cause ventriculomegaly, hydrocephalus and cerebellar tonsillar ectopia.","additionalDifferentials":["Other PROS conditions such as CLOVES and Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome","Sturge-Weber syndrome","Proteus syndrome","Sotos syndrome and other overgrowth syndromes","Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","Isolated cutis marmorata telangiectatica congenita"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI for megalencephaly, polymicrogyria, ventriculomegaly and tonsillar herniation","Serial head circumference and growth measurements","Echocardiography and arrhythmia screening","Skeletal assessment for asymmetry and limb-length discrepancy","PIK3CA mutation testing on affected tissue, since blood may be mutation-negative in mosaic disease","Developmental and neurological assessment"],"redFlags":["Signs of raised intracranial pressure or progressive hydrocephalus","Symptomatic Chiari-like malformation","Cardiac arrhythmia","Significant or progressive limb-length discrepancy and asymmetry","Seizures"],"prognosis":"The condition is associated with variable developmental delay and neurological complications; prognosis depends on the severity of the brain malformation and overgrowth, and lifelong multidisciplinary surveillance is required.","pitfalls":["Testing blood rather than affected tissue for the mosaic PIK3CA mutation and being falsely reassured by a negative result.","Attributing macrocephaly to a benign familial cause and missing megalencephaly.","Overlooking an evolving Chiari-like malformation or hydrocephalus.","Failing to arrange multidisciplinary developmental support."],"management":["Consider targeted pharmacotherapy of the PI3K/AKT/mTOR pathway with the PI3K-alpha inhibitor alpelisib, or mTOR inhibition with sirolimus, for significant overgrowth or complications","Neurosurgical management of progressive hydrocephalus with ventricular shunting and of symptomatic Chiari-like malformation with posterior fossa decompression","Serial head circumference measurement and neuroimaging surveillance for megalencephaly, ventriculomegaly and tonsillar herniation","Cardiac surveillance with echocardiography and arrhythmia screening","Developmental support and physiotherapy for hypotonia and delay, with treatment of associated epilepsy","Orthopaedic management of body asymmetry and limb-length discrepancy, and pulsed-dye laser for cosmetically significant capillary malformations","Multidisciplinary vascular-anomalies and genetics team care, with PIK3CA testing on affected tissue and counselling that the mutation is postzygotic somatic mosaic and not inherited"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard reference on PIK3CA-related overgrowth spectrum disorders","ISSVA classification of vascular anomalies","Consensus diagnostic criteria for macrocephaly-capillary malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"macrophage_activation_syndrome":{"name":"Macrophage Activation Syndrome","system":"Haematological / Immunological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAS","secondary haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","secondary HLH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A life-threatening hyperinflammatory state, considered a form of secondary haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, characterised by uncontrolled activation of macrophages and T cells.","It most commonly complicates rheumatic conditions, especially systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis, but can be triggered by infection or malignancy.","Suspect it when a child with an underlying inflammatory disease develops persistent high fever, worsening general condition, and hepatosplenomegaly.","A characteristic and counterintuitive clue is falling blood counts (cytopenias) and a paradoxically falling or normalising ESR despite active inflammation.","Markedly elevated ferritin is a hallmark laboratory feature and useful for both suspicion and monitoring.","Other typical findings include high triglycerides, low fibrinogen, deranged liver function, and coagulopathy, sometimes with overt bleeding.","Bone marrow examination may show haemophagocytosis, but a normal marrow does not exclude the diagnosis and treatment should not be delayed for it.","This is a medical emergency requiring rapid immunosuppression to interrupt the cytokine storm before multi-organ failure develops."],"pathophysiology":"MAS results from defective downregulation of the immune response, with impaired cytotoxic function of natural killer cells and cytotoxic T lymphocytes. This allows persistent activation and proliferation of T cells and well-differentiated macrophages (histiocytes), which phagocytose blood cells and release a flood of pro-inflammatory cytokines. This cytokine storm produces sustained fever, cytopenias from marrow phagocytosis and suppression, hepatic dysfunction, coagulopathy resembling disseminated intravascular coagulation, and progressive multi-organ damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary (familial) haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","Sepsis with disseminated intravascular coagulation","Flare of the underlying rheumatic disease","Systemic infection (EBV and other viruses, intracellular pathogens)","Malignancy, particularly lymphoma or leukaemia","Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ferritin (very high levels are characteristic and trended for response)","Full blood count to detect evolving cytopenias","Fibrinogen, D-dimer, and full coagulation profile","Fasting triglycerides","Liver function tests and lactate dehydrogenase","Soluble interleukin-2 receptor (soluble CD25) and natural killer cell function where available","Bone marrow aspirate to look for haemophagocytosis when the diagnosis is uncertain"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive multi-organ dysfunction from cytokine storm (EMERGENCY)","Persistent high fever with deteriorating clinical state","Falling cell counts across multiple lineages","Falling fibrinogen with rising ferritin and clinical bleeding","Neurological involvement: seizures, altered consciousness","Progression toward disseminated intravascular coagulation or shock"],"prognosis":"MAS carries significant mortality if unrecognised, but early aggressive immunosuppression can be life-saving and many children recover; outcome depends on prompt diagnosis, control of the trigger, and reversal of organ dysfunction before it becomes irreversible.","pitfalls":["Being falsely reassured by a falling or normal ESR, which can accompany the low fibrinogen of MAS","Interpreting the picture solely as a disease flare or sepsis and delaying specific treatment","Waiting for bone marrow confirmation of haemophagocytosis before starting therapy","Underappreciating how quickly the condition can progress to coagulopathy and organ failure","Not monitoring ferritin and blood counts closely to track response"],"management":["Prompt immunosuppression is life-saving, with high-dose pulsed corticosteroids (methylprednisolone) as first-line therapy, escalated early","Interleukin-1 blockade with anakinra and/or ciclosporin for inadequate response, reserving the anti-interferon-gamma agent emapalumab or an etoposide-based haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis protocol for refractory disease","Identify and treat the trigger by controlling the underlying rheumatic disease (especially systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis), treating precipitating infection with appropriate antimicrobials, or addressing malignancy","Intensive supportive care including management of the disseminated intravascular coagulation-like coagulopathy with blood products, organ support and correction of metabolic derangement","Serial monitoring of ferritin, blood counts, fibrinogen, triglycerides, liver function and coagulation to gauge treatment response","Multidisciplinary care with paediatric rheumatology, haematology and intensive care, escalating to critical care early for multi-organ dysfunction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"macroprolactinoma":{"name":"Macroprolactinoma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prolactin-secreting macroadenoma","large prolactinoma","lactotroph macroadenoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard endocrinology reference texts and pituitary tumour consensus guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A prolactin-secreting pituitary adenoma of at least 10 mm, presenting both with hyperprolactinaemia and with mass effects such as headache and visual field loss","Hyperprolactinaemia causes hypogonadism, with oligomenorrhoea or amenorrhoea and galactorrhoea in women and reduced libido, erectile dysfunction and infertility in men","Serum prolactin is usually very high and tends to correlate with tumour size, so a large tumour with only modest prolactin elevation suggests a non-functioning adenoma with stalk effect instead","Mass effect on the optic chiasm classically produces a bitemporal hemianopia, and lateral extension can involve the cavernous sinus and cranial nerves","Unlike most pituitary tumours, first-line treatment is medical: dopamine agonists usually shrink the tumour and normalise prolactin, reserving surgery for resistant or complicated cases","A crucial laboratory trap is the hook effect, where extremely high prolactin saturates the immunoassay and produces a falsely low reading unless the sample is diluted","Macroprolactinaemia (aggregated biologically inactive prolactin) is a separate benign cause of raised prolactin and should be excluded to avoid misdiagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"A macroprolactinoma is a benign monoclonal tumour of pituitary lactotroph cells that autonomously secretes prolactin. High circulating prolactin suppresses hypothalamic gonadotropin-releasing hormone pulsatility, lowering LH and FSH and causing hypogonadism and galactorrhoea. As the tumour enlarges beyond the sella it compresses adjacent structures, causing headache, optic chiasm compression with visual field defects, and, by disrupting the pituitary stalk or gland, hypopituitarism. Because prolactin secretion is under dominant inhibitory dopaminergic control, dopamine agonists both suppress hormone output and reduce tumour bulk.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-functioning pituitary macroadenoma with stalk-effect hyperprolactinaemia","Physiological or drug-induced hyperprolactinaemia (e.g. antipsychotics, antiemetics)","Primary hypothyroidism causing raised prolactin","Macroprolactinaemia (biologically inactive aggregated prolactin)","Other sellar or parasellar masses (craniopharyngioma, meningioma)","Chronic kidney disease-related hyperprolactinaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum prolactin measurement, with serial dilution to exclude the hook effect when a large tumour has apparently modest prolactin","Screening for macroprolactin (e.g. polyethylene glycol precipitation) when clinical picture and level are discordant","Pituitary MRI to define tumour size, suprasellar extension and cavernous sinus invasion","Formal visual field assessment (perimetry) when the tumour abuts the optic chiasm","Anterior pituitary hormone profile to detect coexisting hypopituitarism","Thyroid function and pregnancy testing to exclude secondary causes of hyperprolactinaemia"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual field loss or acute visual deterioration from chiasmal compression","Signs of pituitary apoplexy (sudden severe headache, ophthalmoplegia, collapse)","New or worsening hypopituitarism, including secondary adrenal insufficiency","Cerebrospinal fluid rhinorrhoea developing during medical shrinkage of an invasive tumour"],"prognosis":"Most macroprolactinomas respond well to dopamine agonists with normalisation of prolactin, restoration of gonadal function and significant tumour shrinkage; long-term outcome is generally favourable, though some tumours are resistant or require surgery, and lifelong monitoring is usual.","pitfalls":["Missing the hook effect and underestimating prolactin in a large tumour, leading to misdiagnosis as a non-functioning adenoma","Operating first when a dopamine agonist would have shrunk the tumour and controlled prolactin","Failing to exclude drug-induced hyperprolactinaemia and macroprolactinaemia before imaging","Overlooking coexisting hypopituitarism, particularly cortisol deficiency, before treatment"],"management":["First-line medical therapy with a dopamine agonist (cabergoline preferred, or bromocriptine) to normalise prolactin, restore gonadal function and shrink the tumour","Transsphenoidal surgery reserved for dopamine-agonist resistance or intolerance, cerebrospinal fluid leak, or threatening mass effect not responding to medical therapy","Replace deficient anterior pituitary hormones where hypopituitarism coexists, including thyroid hormone, glucocorticoid and sex steroids","Monitoring of serum prolactin, serial pituitary MRI and formal visual field perimetry to track tumour size and chiasmal compression","Management of acute pituitary apoplexy with glucocorticoid cover and urgent surgical decompression if vision deteriorates","Radiotherapy for aggressive or resistant tumours not controlled by medical or surgical means","Pre-conception and pregnancy planning with endocrine input, adjusting the dopamine agonist and monitoring for symptomatic tumour expansion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline on hyperprolactinaemia"],"crossLinks":[]},"macular_amyloidosis":{"name":"Macular amyloidosis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["macular cutaneous amyloidosis","macular amyloidosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of primary localised cutaneous amyloidosis presenting as itchy, poorly defined, greyish-brown hyperpigmented macules, often with a characteristic rippled or reticulate pattern","The classic site is the upper back, especially the interscapular region, and the extensor arms, and it may be extensive","The rippled interscapular pigmentation, sometimes related to friction from towels or brushes, is highly suggestive","As with lichen amyloidosis, the amyloid is keratin-derived and the disease is confined to the skin without systemic involvement","Chronic friction and scratching (for example from nylon towels or back-scratchers) are important contributory factors, giving overlap with friction amyloidosis and notalgia paraesthetica","More prevalent in people with darker skin from South-East Asia, the Middle East, and Latin America, and the lichen and macular forms can coexist as biphasic amyloidosis","Diagnosis rests on biopsy showing small deposits of amyloid in the papillary dermis with pigment incontinence, confirmed by Congo red staining and apple-green birefringence","Management focuses on reducing friction and itch by stopping vigorous rubbing and using topical corticosteroids, keratolytics, and phototherapy, generally with modest results"],"pathophysiology":"Macular amyloidosis, like the lichen form, involves deposition of keratin-derived amyloid in the papillary dermis. Repeated friction and scratching are believed to injure basal keratinocytes, which degenerate and release cytokeratin that is transformed into amyloid. Associated release of melanin into the dermis (pigment incontinence) accounts for the grey-brown discolouration and the rippled appearance. Because the amyloid originates from epidermal keratin rather than immunoglobulin, the process stays localised to the skin and carries no systemic risk, while ongoing friction sustains and extends the deposits.","additionalDifferentials":["Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation","Notalgia paraesthetica","Ashy dermatosis / erythema dyschromicum perstans","Lichen amyloidosis (overlapping or biphasic disease)","Confluent and reticulated papillomatosis","Poikiloderma","Melasma (at other sites)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with Congo red staining confirming papillary dermal amyloid and pigment incontinence","Immunohistochemistry for cytokeratin to establish the keratinous origin","History of friction from towels or brushes and any associated notalgia paraesthetica","Wood's lamp examination or dermoscopy to characterise the pigmentary pattern","Exclusion of other causes of reticulate or rippled truncal pigmentation"],"redFlags":["Atypical distribution prompting reconsideration and exclusion of systemic amyloidosis","Persistent neuropathic itch suggesting associated notalgia paraesthetica needing separate management","Diagnostic uncertainty with other pigmentary disorders requiring biopsy"],"prognosis":"Benign and chronic; there is no systemic risk, but the pigmentation is persistent and often only partly responsive to treatment, tending to recur while friction continues.","pitfalls":["Treating it as simple post-inflammatory pigmentation and missing the amyloid","Failing to identify and stop the causative friction","Not recognising the overlap with notalgia paraesthetica","Assuming a link to systemic amyloidosis, which does not apply"],"management":["Reduce chronic friction and scratching by avoiding nylon towels, back-scratchers and abrasive garments, addressing the sustaining cause","Break the itch-scratch cycle with emollients, topical corticosteroids and antipruritic agents such as menthol or a topical calcineurin inhibitor, with oral antihistamines for itch","Pigmentation-directed treatment with topical retinoids or keratolytics, and consideration of dermabrasion or Q-switched laser for persistent hyperpigmentation","Adjuncts for refractory pruritus including phototherapy and agents used in notalgia paraesthetica such as topical capsaicin, given the overlap","Reassurance and counselling that the condition is confined to the skin with no systemic amyloid risk","Set expectations of a chronic course with partial response and tendency to recur while friction continues, with dermatology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":["lichen_amyloidosis"]},"macular_corneal_dystrophy":{"name":"Macular corneal dystrophy","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCD","Groenouw type II dystrophy","CHST6-related corneal dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology / Cornea by Krachmer","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The least common but most severe of the classic stromal dystrophies, and the only one inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern","Caused by mutations in the CHST6 gene, which encodes a carbohydrate sulfotransferase needed for normal keratan sulfate sulfation","Deposits are glycosaminoglycan (abnormal keratan sulfate) accumulations staining with Alcian blue and colloidal iron on histology","Opacities are ill-defined greyish-white spots with cloudy intervening stroma extending limbus to limbus and full thickness, unlike the clear stroma seen between granular deposits","The diffuse stromal haze and full-thickness, edge-to-edge involvement cause earlier and more marked visual loss than granular or lattice dystrophy","Corneal thinning and endothelial involvement can occur, distinguishing it from other stromal dystrophies","The systemic mucopolysaccharidoses are an important differential for stromal clouding and should be excluded when there are systemic features","Management centres on lamellar or penetrating keratoplasty for advanced disease, with lubrication and erosion care as adjuncts"],"pathophysiology":"Macular corneal dystrophy is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in the CHST6 gene encoding a corneal N-acetylglucosamine-6-O-sulfotransferase. Defective sulfation of keratan sulfate leads to accumulation of abnormal, poorly soluble glycosaminoglycan within keratocytes, the extracellular stroma, and the endothelium. This deposition, together with diffuse clouding of the intervening stroma, spreads from limbus to limbus and through the full corneal thickness, progressively degrading transparency. Because the defect involves a systemic sulfation pathway that is clinically confined to the cornea, deposits accumulate throughout the stroma rather than in discrete islands.","additionalDifferentials":["Granular and lattice corneal dystrophies","Systemic mucopolysaccharidoses causing corneal clouding","Congenital hereditary stromal or endothelial dystrophy","Schnyder crystalline dystrophy","Diffuse stromal scarring from prior keratitis","Interstitial keratitis with residual stromal haze"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination documenting diffuse full-thickness stromal clouding limbus to limbus","Histology with Alcian blue and colloidal iron staining of glycosaminoglycan deposits","CHST6 genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Pachymetry, as the cornea is often abnormally thin","Systemic and urinary glycosaminoglycan evaluation when a mucopolysaccharidosis is suspected","Specular microscopy to assess endothelial involvement before surgery"],"redFlags":["Early and progressive visual loss from diffuse stromal clouding","Systemic features suggesting an underlying mucopolysaccharidosis rather than isolated corneal disease","Corneal thinning increasing the risk of perforation or surgical complication","Endothelial dysfunction contributing to oedema and graft failure risk"],"prognosis":"Macular dystrophy tends to progress earlier and more severely than other stromal dystrophies, often requiring keratoplasty by early adulthood. Grafts can restore useful vision, though recurrence in the graft is possible over time.","pitfalls":["Assuming autosomal dominant inheritance and missing the recessive pattern and family risk","Failing to exclude systemic mucopolysaccharidosis when stromal clouding is present","Underestimating corneal thinning and endothelial involvement before surgery","Mistaking diffuse haze for scarring from previous inflammation"],"management":["Keratoplasty is the definitive treatment, with penetrating or deep anterior lamellar keratoplasty typically required by early adulthood for visual rehabilitation","Optical management in earlier disease with refractive correction and rigid gas-permeable contact lenses to optimise vision before surgery","Symptomatic relief of photophobia and surface discomfort with ocular lubricants and tinted lenses","Pre-operative specular microscopy and pachymetry to assess endothelial involvement and corneal thinning, guiding choice of lamellar versus penetrating graft","Long-term post-graft surveillance for graft recurrence, rejection and thin-cornea complications","Multidisciplinary corneal specialist care with CHST6 genetic confirmation","Genetic counselling and family screening given the autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Cornea by Krachmer, Mannis and Holland"],"crossLinks":[]},"macular_hole":{"name":"Macular hole","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["full-thickness macular hole","FTMH","idiopathic macular hole"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with gradual central visual loss and metamorphopsia, often first noticed as a distortion on an Amsler grid","Most cases are idiopathic and age-related, affecting women more often, and are usually unilateral at presentation","The Watzke-Allen slit-beam test and a positive scotoma to a fixation target support the diagnosis at the bedside","Optical coherence tomography is the diagnostic standard and stages the hole while distinguishing it from a pseudohole or lamellar hole","Vitreomacular traction from posterior vitreous separation is the principal mechanism in idiopathic disease","Surgical repair by vitrectomy with internal limiting membrane peel and gas tamponade closes most holes, sometimes with face-down posturing","Trauma and high myopia are recognised secondary causes and myopic holes carry a higher detachment risk","Earlier and smaller holes achieve better closure rates and visual recovery than large chronic ones"],"pathophysiology":"An idiopathic full-thickness macular hole develops when tangential and anteroposterior traction from the perifoveal vitreous acts on the fovea during posterior vitreous detachment. This traction elevates and then dehisces the foveal tissue, creating a full-thickness defect with surrounding cuff of subretinal fluid and cystic change. Progressive stages run from foveal detachment and pseudocyst through to an established round hole. Secondary holes may follow blunt trauma or the mechanical stresses of high myopia, where posterior staphyloma adds traction.","additionalDifferentials":["Macular pseudohole from an epiretinal membrane","Lamellar macular hole","Vitreomacular traction syndrome without a hole","Cystoid macular oedema","Central serous chorioretinopathy","Solar or laser maculopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Optical coherence tomography to confirm and stage a full-thickness hole and measure its diameter","Amsler grid and Watzke-Allen testing to characterise the central defect","Dilated fundus examination of both eyes as the fellow eye carries some risk","Axial length or refraction assessment when myopic traction is suspected","History of ocular trauma in atypical or younger patients"],"redFlags":["Myopic macular hole with signs of retinal detachment requiring urgent surgical assessment","Sudden worsening or new peripheral field loss suggesting an associated detachment","Bilateral or fellow-eye symptoms indicating risk to the only good eye","Traumatic hole with other posterior segment injury"],"prognosis":"Modern vitrectomy with membrane peeling closes the large majority of idiopathic holes, and visual improvement is best when the hole is small and of short duration; large, chronic, or myopic holes have lower closure rates and more limited recovery.","pitfalls":["Confusing a pseudohole or lamellar hole with a full-thickness hole without OCT confirmation","Delaying referral so that a small, highly repairable hole enlarges and chronifies","Overlooking a coexisting retinal detachment in a myopic eye","Setting unrealistic expectations for visual recovery in long-standing holes"],"management":["Vitrectomy with internal limiting membrane peel and gas tamponade is the definitive treatment, closing the large majority of full-thickness holes","Post-operative prone or face-down posturing and avoidance of air travel and altitude while intraocular gas persists","Consider observation for very early vitreomacular-traction-stage holes that may resolve spontaneously, with pharmacological vitreolysis using ocriplasmin in selected traction cases","Optical coherence tomography to confirm and stage the hole and to monitor anatomical closure after surgery","Manage the cataract that commonly follows vitrectomy","Examine and monitor the fellow eye given its risk, with Amsler grid self-monitoring","Prompt referral to a vitreoretinal surgeon, as earlier repair of small, recent holes gives the best visual outcome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"macular_telangiectasia":{"name":"macular telangiectasia","system":"ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MacTel","idiopathic juxtafoveal telangiectasia","parafoveal telangiectasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Type 2 (idiopathic perifoveal) is by far the most common form, presenting in middle age with gradual bilateral blurring, metamorphopsia and difficulty reading despite a relatively preserved distance acuity early on.","The perifoveal telangiectatic vessels are frequently subtle on ophthalmoscopy; the diagnosis often rests on characteristic optical coherence tomography and fluorescein angiographic findings rather than the visible fundus appearance.","A loss of retinal transparency with a greyish sheen temporal to the fovea, right-angled venules and refractile crystalline deposits at the inner retinal surface are classic type 2 signs.","Optical coherence tomography shows hyporeflective inner and outer retinal cavities and, importantly, disruption of the ellipsoid zone without correspondingly large fluid accumulation.","Depletion of the parafoveal Muller cells and macular pigment is a central feature, and macular pigment optical density imaging can reveal a characteristic parafoveal loss.","Fluorescein angiography demonstrates deep telangiectatic capillaries that leak in the late phase, typically temporal to the fovea, but this leakage does not respond to the anti-angiogenic or laser strategies used for other exudative maculopathies.","Type 2 is subdivided into a non-proliferative stage and a sight-threatening proliferative stage in which subretinal neovascularisation grows from the deep retinal circulation.","Type 1 (aneurysmal telangiectasia) is usually unilateral, males predominate, and it behaves more like a congenital retinal vascular anomaly with exudation akin to Coats disease."],"pathophysiology":"Macular telangiectasia type 2 is now understood primarily as a neurodegenerative disorder of the macula in which the Muller glial cells, which maintain retinal architecture and the blood-retinal barrier and sequester macular pigment, are progressively depleted in the parafoveal region. Loss of these glial cells produces retinal cavitation, photoreceptor (ellipsoid zone) disruption and secondary abnormalities of the deep retinal capillary network that manifest as the visible telangiectasia; the vascular changes are therefore considered a consequence rather than the primary event. Impaired retinal handling of serine and glycine metabolism has been implicated, and reduced circulating serine with accumulation of neurotoxic deoxysphingolipids is associated with the condition. In the proliferative stage, ischaemia and glial loss permit neovascular complexes to invade from the deep capillary plexus into the subretinal space, causing haemorrhage, exudation and disciform scarring. Type 1 disease, by contrast, reflects a developmental telangiectatic and aneurysmal malformation of the retinal capillaries with breakdown of the inner blood-retinal barrier and lipid exudation.","additionalDifferentials":["Diabetic maculopathy with parafoveal microaneurysms","Branch retinal vein occlusion with macular telangiectasia","Coats disease and other exudative retinal telangiectasias","Age-related macular degeneration with neovascularisation","Radiation retinopathy","Adult-onset foveomacular vitelliform dystrophy","Macular hole or pseudohole causing metamorphopsia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Spectral-domain optical coherence tomography for retinal cavitation and ellipsoid zone integrity","Fluorescein angiography demonstrating deep temporal parafoveal telangiectasia and late leakage","Optical coherence tomography angiography to map deep capillary plexus abnormalities and detect neovascularisation","Fundus autofluorescence showing loss of the normal foveal masking by macular pigment","Macular pigment optical density measurement","Blue-light or confocal imaging of refractile crystalline deposits","Microperimetry to quantify parafoveal scotomata"],"redFlags":["New or worsening metamorphopsia with subretinal fluid suggesting progression to the proliferative neovascular stage","Sudden central visual loss from subretinal haemorrhage","Development of a full-thickness macular hole","Rapidly enlarging pigment plaque or disciform scar involving fixation","Marked exudation or a retinal detachment in suspected type 1 (aneurysmal) disease"],"prognosis":"Non-proliferative type 2 typically causes slow, progressive parafoveal visual decline over years with relatively preserved distance acuity until late, whereas the proliferative stage can cause rapid central loss from neovascular haemorrhage and scarring. Anti-angiogenic therapy is reserved for the proliferative neovascular stage and does not benefit the non-proliferative leakage; overall central vision often remains functional for reading tasks for a prolonged period.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the subtle telangiectasia and leakage for diabetic maculopathy and applying focal laser or anti-angiogenic injections that are ineffective in non-proliferative disease.","Interpreting late fluorescein leakage as an indication for treatment when it does not correlate with treatable exudation in type 2.","Overlooking the diagnosis when distance acuity is preserved and the fundus appears near-normal, delaying recognition of the ellipsoid zone loss on optical coherence tomography.","Failing to distinguish the neurodegenerative type 2 from the vascular malformation type 1, which have different natural histories and management.","Attributing reading difficulty and metamorphopsia to early cataract or refractive error rather than examining the macula in detail."],"management":["Non-proliferative disease has no proven effective medical therapy, so management centres on monitoring, low-vision support and counselling","Avoid laser and anti-angiogenic therapy for the non-proliferative parafoveal leakage, which does not respond as it does in other exudative maculopathies","Reserve intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy for the proliferative neovascular stage to limit haemorrhage and scarring","Regular optical coherence tomography and OCT-angiography surveillance for ellipsoid-zone loss and development of neovascularisation","Low-vision aids, reading support and counselling regarding metamorphopsia and the slow parafoveal decline","Address comorbidities such as optimising glycaemic control given the diabetes association, and consider emerging neuroprotective approaches such as ciliary neurotrophic factor within clinical trials","Amsler grid self-monitoring with prompt review for any sudden change suggesting neovascular progression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ryan's Retina (current edition)","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology (current edition)","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Retina and Vitreous"],"crossLinks":[]},"madarosis":{"name":"Madarosis","system":"Eye / Orbit and adnexa","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["eyelash loss","eyebrow and eyelash loss"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ophthalmology and dermatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Loss of eyelashes, and sometimes eyebrows, which is a sign rather than a disease in itself","Causes are broad: local lid disease, dermatological conditions, systemic disease, drugs, trauma and psychiatric causes","Chronic blepharitis is a common local cause, while alopecia areata and cicatricial alopecias affect the follicles","Loss of the lateral third of the eyebrows is a classic sign of hypothyroidism and of leprosy","Trichotillomania and factitious causes should be considered, especially with irregular patchy loss","A lid margin lesion with associated madarosis raises concern for eyelid malignancy"],"pathophysiology":"Madarosis results from any process that damages or destroys the eyelash (and eyebrow) follicles or disrupts the hair cycle. Non-scarring loss occurs with conditions such as alopecia areata, telogen effluvium, hypothyroidism and drug effects, where follicles are preserved and regrowth is possible. Scarring (cicatricial) loss follows chronic inflammation, cicatrising skin disease or infiltrating eyelid tumours that destroy the follicles, causing permanent loss. Identifying whether the loss is scarring or non-scarring guides the search for cause and the likelihood of regrowth.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic blepharitis","Alopecia areata or other alopecias","Hypothyroidism","Eyelid malignancy (for example basal or sebaceous cell carcinoma)","Trichotillomania","Leprosy (in endemic settings)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination of the lid margin, skin and pattern of hair loss","Assessment for scarring versus non-scarring loss","Thyroid function tests where indicated","Biopsy of any suspicious lid margin lesion","Investigation for systemic or dermatological causes as directed by the history"],"redFlags":["A localised lid margin lesion with lash loss suggesting malignancy","Scarring loss indicating follicle destruction","Systemic features suggesting endocrine or infiltrative disease","Rapidly progressive or asymmetric loss"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the cause: non-scarring loss often recovers when the underlying condition is treated, whereas scarring loss and tumour-related loss are permanent.","pitfalls":["Overlooking an eyelid malignancy causing localised lash loss","Missing hypothyroidism with lateral eyebrow loss","Not distinguishing scarring from non-scarring loss","Failing to consider trichotillomania"],"management":["Treat the identified underlying cause, since madarosis is a sign rather than a disease in itself","Manage local lid disease, especially chronic blepharitis, with lid hygiene, warm compresses and topical or systemic antibiotics as indicated","Treat dermatological causes, using topical or intralesional corticosteroids or immunotherapy for alopecia areata and managing cicatricial alopecias","Correct systemic contributors with thyroid hormone replacement for hypothyroidism, multidrug antimicrobial therapy for leprosy, and review of causative drugs","Address psychiatric causes such as trichotillomania with behavioural therapy and psychiatric support","Biopsy and refer any suspicious lid-margin lesion for management of possible eyelid malignancy","Cosmetic and restorative options including eyelash and eyebrow restoration, a prostaglandin-analogue eyelash agent, tattooing or grafting for permanent scarring loss"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Dermatology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"madelung_deformity":{"name":"Madelung deformity","system":"Paediatric orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Madelung's deformity","radial physeal dysplasia of the wrist","dyschondrosteosis wrist deformity","volar-ulnar distal radial physeal dysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery / Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A growth disturbance of the volar-ulnar part of the distal radial physis produces progressive volar and ulnar tilt of the distal radial articular surface","The distal ulna becomes dorsally prominent and subluxed, the radius bows, and the carpus (with the lunate at its apex) sinks into a V-shaped notch between radius and ulna","It is typically bilateral, more common in adolescent girls, and usually presents in the second decade with wrist pain, deformity, and reduced extension and rotation","A tethering anomalous volar radiolunate ligament (Vickers ligament) crosses the deficient physis and contributes to the progressive deformity","There is a strong association with Leri-Weill dyschondrosteosis and with mutations or deletions of the SHOX gene, which also cause mesomelic short stature","Radiographs show the characteristic volar and ulnar tilt of the distal radius, triangulation of the carpus, and dorsal ulnar prominence","Management ranges from observation and activity modification to surgery: release of the Vickers ligament with physiolysis in the growing child, or corrective radial osteotomy (often with ulnar shortening) once skeletally mature"],"pathophysiology":"Madelung deformity results from a localised dysplasia and premature growth arrest of the volar-ulnar quadrant of the distal radial physis. Continued growth of the dorsal-radial portion of the physis, combined with tethering by a thick anomalous volar ligament (Vickers ligament) running from the radius to the lunate, produces progressive volar and ulnar angulation of the distal radial joint surface. Relative overgrowth of the ulna leads to dorsal ulnar prominence and carpal triangulation. Many cases are genetically determined through SHOX haploinsufficiency, as seen in Leri-Weill dyschondrosteosis, which links the wrist deformity to a wider skeletal dysplasia.","additionalDifferentials":["Post-traumatic distal radial physeal arrest","Multiple hereditary exostoses affecting the distal forearm","Distal radius malunion","Enchondromatosis (Ollier disease) of the forearm","Rickets or other metabolic bone disease with wrist deformity","Turner syndrome-related skeletal changes (also SHOX-related)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Posteroanterior and lateral wrist radiographs to assess radial tilt, ulnar variance, and carpal triangulation","MRI to demonstrate the anomalous Vickers ligament and any physeal bar","CT for detailed bony assessment when planning an osteotomy","Assessment of stature and limb proportions with SHOX gene testing where dyschondrosteosis is suspected","Bilateral wrist imaging given the frequent bilateral involvement"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive deformity or pain during the adolescent growth spurt","Signs of ulnar impaction or distal radioulnar joint instability","Features suggesting an underlying skeletal dysplasia that warrants genetic evaluation"],"prognosis":"Progression tends to occur during skeletal growth and then stabilises at maturity; early ligament release with physiolysis can halt progression in the growing child, while corrective osteotomy in the mature wrist reliably improves pain and appearance, although some loss of rotation may persist.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early Madelung deformity for a simple wrist sprain and missing its progression","Failing to release the Vickers ligament, allowing continued tethering after bony surgery","Not recognising the association with SHOX-related short stature (dyschondrosteosis)","Operating on skeletally immature bone without addressing the physeal tether"],"management":["Observation with analgesia and activity modification for mild, minimally symptomatic wrists","In the growing child, Vickers ligament release with physiolysis (resection of the abnormal physeal tether) to halt progression","Corrective radial osteotomy, with or without ulnar shortening, in the mature or markedly deformed wrist to relieve pain and improve alignment and appearance","Distal ulnar procedures such as ulnar shortening or the Sauve-Kapandji procedure to address dorsal ulnar prominence and improve forearm rotation","Hand therapy and splinting for pain relief and to optimise function before and after surgery","Serial radiographic monitoring of radial tilt, ulnar variance and carpal triangulation through skeletal growth","Assess stature and limb proportions with SHOX gene testing and genetics referral where Leri-Weill dyschondrosteosis is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"madelung_disease":{"name":"Madelung disease (multiple symmetric lipomatosis)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["multiple symmetric lipomatosis","MSL","Launois-Bensaude syndrome","benign symmetric lipomatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Endocrine Society clinical guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by symmetric, non-encapsulated masses of adipose tissue around the neck, shoulders, and upper trunk, producing a 'horse-collar' or pseudoathletic appearance","Predominantly affects middle-aged men and is strongly associated with chronic heavy alcohol use","Distinct from lipodystrophy in that fat accumulates abnormally rather than being lost, though the lower limbs may appear relatively fat-depleted","Some cases are linked to mitochondrial dysfunction (including MERRF-associated mutations), suggesting impaired brown-fat metabolism","The accumulating tissue resembles brown adipose tissue with defective catecholamine-driven lipolysis","Neck and mediastinal fat deposition can cause compressive symptoms such as dysphagia, dyspnoea, or airway compromise","An associated peripheral or autonomic neuropathy is common, and metabolic disturbances including insulin resistance may coexist","Diagnosis rests on the characteristic symmetric fat distribution and imaging; alcohol cessation is advised and debulking surgery is used for compression or cosmesis"],"pathophysiology":"Madelung disease is a disorder of abnormal proliferation and accumulation of adipose tissue in a symmetric distribution around the neck, nape, shoulders, and upper trunk. The affected fat has features of brown adipose tissue with impaired catecholamine-stimulated lipolysis, so it expands unchecked rather than being mobilised. Mitochondrial dysfunction, including mutations associated with MERRF and heavy alcohol exposure, is implicated in disrupting normal adipocyte metabolism. Unlike the lipodystrophies, the primary problem is pathological fat gain in specific depots; the growing masses are cosmetically disfiguring and can compress the airway, oesophagus, and vascular structures, while an associated neuropathy and metabolic disturbance may accompany the condition.","additionalDifferentials":["Cushing syndrome (dorsocervical and supraclavicular fat pads)","Familial multiple lipomatosis (discrete encapsulated lipomas)","Obesity with prominent cervical fat","Liposarcoma or other soft-tissue tumour","Lymphoma or other neck/mediastinal mass","Dercum disease (adiposis dolorosa)","Angiolipomatosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT or MRI of the neck and thorax to map fat deposits and assess compression","Liver function tests and markers reflecting chronic alcohol use","Fasting glucose/HbA1c and lipid profile for associated metabolic disturbance","Nerve conduction studies if neuropathy is suspected","Evaluation for mitochondrial disease where clinically indicated","Cortisol testing to exclude Cushing syndrome when the picture is ambiguous"],"redFlags":["Airway compromise, stridor, or dyspnoea from cervical/mediastinal fat","Progressive dysphagia from oesophageal compression","Rapid growth or atypical features raising concern for malignancy","Superior vena cava or vascular compression"],"prognosis":"Madelung disease is benign but often progressive and disfiguring; alcohol cessation may slow growth, and surgical debulking relieves compression and improves appearance, though recurrence is common and any associated neuropathy or metabolic disease persists.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the fat distribution for Cushing syndrome or simple obesity","Overlooking airway or oesophageal compression from neck deposits","Failing to recognise the strong association with chronic alcohol use","Assuming the masses are discrete encapsulated lipomas amenable to simple excision","Missing an associated neuropathy or mitochondrial disorder"],"management":["Alcohol abstinence is central, as cessation may slow progression and reduce recurrence, with structured support for alcohol-use disorder","Surgical debulking by lipectomy or liposuction to relieve compression of the airway, oesophagus and vessels and to improve appearance","Urgent surgical decompression for compressive complications such as airway compromise, dysphagia or dyspnoea","Manage associated metabolic disturbance and comorbidity, including glucose and lipid control and correction of alcohol-related liver and nutritional deficiency","Symptomatic treatment of the associated peripheral and autonomic neuropathy","Investigation for mitochondrial disease where clinically indicated, with appropriate counselling","Multidisciplinary care across surgery, endocrinology and addiction services, with counselling that recurrence is common after debulking"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society clinical practice guidance on disorders of adipose tissue"],"crossLinks":[]},"maffucci_syndrome":{"name":"Maffucci syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["enchondromatosis with haemangiomas","IDH1","IDH2","Maffucci-Kast syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the combination of multiple enchondromas with soft-tissue vascular lesions, classically spindle-cell haemangiomas","Like Ollier disease it is a sporadic, non-hereditary condition caused by somatic mosaic IDH1 or IDH2 variants","Enchondromas show an asymmetric distribution affecting the hands, feet and long bones, causing deformity, swelling and limb-length discrepancy","The vascular lesions present as compressible, sometimes bluish subcutaneous nodules and may contain phleboliths visible on radiographs","Carries a higher overall risk of malignancy than Ollier disease, including chondrosarcoma and an increased risk of other neoplasms","Associated tumours reported include gliomas, and ovarian and pancreatic neoplasms, so surveillance extends beyond the skeleton","The presence of haemangiomas is the feature that separates Maffucci syndrome from isolated enchondromatosis"],"pathophysiology":"Maffucci syndrome shares its origin with Ollier disease in post-zygotic somatic mosaic gain-of-function variants of IDH1 or IDH2. The resulting oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate perturbs epigenetic regulation, impairing chondrocyte differentiation so residual cartilage persists as intramedullary enchondromas. In Maffucci syndrome the mosaic mutation additionally affects vascular tissue, giving rise to spindle-cell haemangiomas and other vascular malformations. The broader mutational burden is thought to underlie the higher risk of malignant transformation and of non-skeletal tumours compared with Ollier disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Ollier disease (enchondromatosis without vascular lesions)","Multiple hereditary exostoses","Blue rubber bleb naevus syndrome","Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome","Metachondromatosis","Chondrosarcoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey to map enchondromas and identify phleboliths within vascular lesions","MRI to characterise both cartilaginous and vascular soft-tissue lesions","Consideration of somatic IDH1/IDH2 testing on lesional tissue where diagnosis is uncertain","Surveillance imaging for malignant transformation of enchondromas","Broader clinical vigilance and targeted imaging for associated non-skeletal tumours"],"redFlags":["Growth or new pain in an enchondroma suggesting transformation to chondrosarcoma","Neurological symptoms that may indicate an associated intracranial tumour","A rapidly enlarging or bleeding vascular lesion","Systemic features suggesting an associated visceral neoplasm"],"prognosis":"Morbidity arises from skeletal deformity, fractures and vascular lesions, but the defining concern is a substantially increased lifetime risk of malignancy, both chondrosarcoma and other tumours, mandating lifelong multi-system surveillance.","pitfalls":["Managing it as simple enchondromatosis and neglecting surveillance for the broader malignancy risk","Giving inherited recurrence-risk counselling for what is a sporadic mosaic disorder","Overlooking the vascular lesions that define the syndrome and its higher risk","Failing to investigate new neurological or systemic symptoms for associated neoplasms"],"management":["No medical cure exists, so management centres on surveillance for malignant transformation and treatment of complications in this somatic mosaic, non-hereditary IDH1/IDH2 disorder","Surgical treatment of symptomatic enchondromas with curettage, grafting, and correction of deformity and limb-length discrepancy","Prompt biopsy and wide surgical resection of any lesion suspicious for chondrosarcoma, such as one showing growth, new pain or cortical destruction","Management of the spindle-cell haemangiomas and other vascular lesions surgically or with sclerotherapy when symptomatic","Lifelong surveillance imaging of the skeleton for malignant transformation, with broader vigilance for associated non-skeletal tumours including gliomas and ovarian and pancreatic neoplasms","Orthopaedic and rehabilitation support for deformity, fractures and functional limitation","Multidisciplinary care through skeletal-dysplasia and oncology services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Orphanet / WHO Classification of Tumours: Maffucci Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"mainzer_saldino_syndrome":{"name":"Mainzer-Saldino syndrome","system":"Genetics / Ciliopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["conorenal syndrome","cono-renal syndrome","Saldino-Mainzer syndrome","IFT140","IFT172"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by the triad of cone-shaped phalangeal epiphyses, nephronophthisis (chronic tubulointerstitial kidney disease) and retinal dystrophy","Cone-shaped epiphyses are best seen on hand radiographs and give the condition its alternative name of conorenal syndrome","The retinal dystrophy resembles a rod-cone dystrophy or Leber congenital amaurosis and may precede renal signs","Nephronophthisis presents with polyuria, polydipsia, anaemia and growth failure before progressing to end-stage renal disease","Cerebellar ataxia and mild vermis changes may accompany the renal and retinal features","Considered part of the nephronophthisis-related ciliopathy spectrum, overlapping with Senior-Loken and Jeune syndromes","Caused by biallelic variants in intraflagellar transport genes, notably IFT140 and IFT172"],"pathophysiology":"Mainzer-Saldino syndrome is caused by biallelic variants in intraflagellar transport genes, particularly IFT140 and IFT172, whose products carry cargo along the primary cilium. Impaired ciliary transport disturbs the specialised sensory cilia of retinal photoreceptors, the primary cilia of renal tubular epithelium and the signalling that governs epiphyseal cartilage, producing the retinal dystrophy, nephronophthisis and cone-shaped phalangeal epiphyses respectively. The same ciliary dependence in the cerebellum accounts for the ataxia seen in some individuals. The condition therefore sits within the wider group of nephronophthisis-related ciliopathies, sharing mechanisms with Senior-Loken and Jeune syndromes while showing milder skeletal change than the short-rib thoracic dysplasias.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated nephronophthisis","Senior-Loken syndrome (nephronophthisis with retinal dystrophy)","Jeune asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy","Bardet-Biedl syndrome","Isolated Leber congenital amaurosis","Tricho-rhino-phalangeal syndrome (other causes of cone-shaped epiphyses)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Hand and skeletal radiographs to identify cone-shaped epiphyses","Renal function testing, urine concentrating ability and renal ultrasound","Electroretinography and ophthalmological assessment for retinal dystrophy","Brain imaging for cerebellar vermis changes when ataxia is present","Molecular testing with a nephronophthisis / ciliopathy gene panel"],"redFlags":["Progressive renal impairment towards end-stage disease","Rapidly declining vision","Growth failure and anaemia from renal disease","Cerebellar signs indicating central nervous system involvement"],"prognosis":"Renal failure typically develops during childhood or adolescence and requires dialysis or transplantation, while visual loss from retinal dystrophy is variable but may be severe; overall outlook is largely determined by the renal course.","pitfalls":["Attributing early visual loss to isolated retinal disease and missing the progressive nephropathy","Overlooking subtle cone-shaped epiphyses on hand radiographs","Failing to monitor renal concentrating ability so that nephronophthisis is recognised late","Not distinguishing it from other nephronophthisis-related ciliopathies without genetic testing"],"management":["No disease-specific therapy exists, so management targets the individual organ manifestations of this ciliopathy","Manage progressive nephronophthisis and chronic kidney disease with blood pressure control, correction of anaemia, growth support and fluid-electrolyte balance, progressing to dialysis and renal transplantation","Ophthalmological management of the retinal dystrophy with low-vision aids and visual rehabilitation","Nutritional and growth support, treating the polyuria and polydipsia and addressing growth failure","Orthopaedic assessment of the cone-shaped epiphyseal skeletal changes and monitoring for cerebellar ataxia","Multidisciplinary long-term care coordinated across paediatric nephrology, ophthalmology and genetics","Genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive intraflagellar-transport disorder (IFT140/IFT172)"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Nephronophthisis-Related Ciliopathies","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"maisonneuve_fracture":{"name":"Maisonneuve fracture","system":"Musculoskeletal / Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["proximal fibular fracture with syndesmotic injury","Maisonneuve ankle injury","high fibular fracture with mortise instability"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A proximal fibular fracture combined with disruption of the interosseous membrane and syndesmosis and a medial ankle injury (either a medial malleolus fracture or a deltoid ligament rupture)","An external-rotation force drives energy up the interosseous membrane, exiting proximally in the fibula","The proximal fibula must be palpated in every ankle injury; proximal tenderness or a medial injury with a widened mortise mandates imaging of the whole leg","The ankle mortise may be widened at the medial clear space despite an apparently isolated medial malleolar injury","Treatment focuses on restoring the mortise and stabilising the syndesmosis with a screw or suture-button construct; the proximal fibular fracture itself is usually not fixed","The injury is easily missed when the proximal fibula is neither examined nor imaged","Assess and document common peroneal nerve function at the fibular neck"],"pathophysiology":"A pronation-external rotation force at the ankle is transmitted proximally through the interosseous membrane. As the talus externally rotates, the syndesmosis and interosseous membrane tear and the energy exits as a fracture of the proximal fibula, while the medial restraint fails either as a deltoid ligament rupture or a medial malleolus fracture. The result is an unstable ankle mortise despite an intact distal fibula, and the key to a good outcome is anatomical reduction of the syndesmosis rather than the proximal fracture.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated ankle fracture (Weber A or B pattern)","Isolated medial malleolus fracture","Isolated syndesmotic (high ankle) sprain","Proximal fibular fracture from direct trauma","Weber C ankle fracture","Common peroneal nerve injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full-length tibia and fibula radiographs in addition to a dedicated ankle series","Ankle mortise view assessing the medial clear space for widening","Stress or weight-bearing views to demonstrate syndesmotic instability","CT to evaluate the adequacy of syndesmotic reduction","Examination and documentation of common peroneal nerve function"],"redFlags":["Common peroneal nerve injury at the fibular neck","Unrecognised syndesmotic instability","A widened mortise indicating an unstable ankle","Open injury requiring urgent management"],"prognosis":"With anatomical restoration of the mortise and adequate syndesmotic fixation the outcome is generally good; malreduction of the syndesmosis is the strongest predictor of pain and post-traumatic arthritis.","pitfalls":["Failing to examine and image the proximal fibula in an ankle injury","Treating an apparently isolated medial malleolus fracture without recognising syndesmotic instability","Malreduction of the syndesmosis during fixation","Overlooking a common peroneal nerve injury"],"management":["Anatomical reduction and stabilisation of the syndesmosis with a screw or suture-button construct is the priority, rather than fixation of the proximal fibular fracture","Fix or repair the medial injury, with medial malleolus fixation or attention to the deltoid ligament rupture, to restore the ankle mortise","Confirm adequacy of syndesmotic reduction intra-operatively and with CT, as malreduction is the strongest predictor of poor outcome","Non-weight-bearing immobilisation followed by protected mobilisation and structured physiotherapy rehabilitation","Assess and document common peroneal nerve function given the proximal fibular injury","Analgesia and venous thromboembolism prophylaxis, with early orthopaedic trauma referral for this unstable ankle injury","Longer-term surveillance for post-traumatic ankle arthritis, noting the proximal fibular fracture itself is generally treated non-operatively"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","AO Principles of Fracture Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"majeed_syndrome":{"name":"Majeed syndrome","system":"Immunology / Autoinflammatory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis with congenital dyserythropoietic anaemia","LPIN2 deficiency","LPIN2","lipin-2 associated autoinflammation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Autoinflammation / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A recessive autoinflammatory disease caused by loss-of-function mutations in LPIN2, encoding the lipid-regulating enzyme lipin-2","Classic triad is chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis, congenital dyserythropoietic anaemia, and a neutrophilic dermatosis","Bone disease begins in early childhood with recurrent painful, sterile inflammatory bone lesions and periosteal reaction","The anaemia is microcytic and dyserythropoietic, sometimes requiring transfusion, and reflects abnormal red cell production","Skin involvement typically resembles Sweet syndrome (neutrophilic dermatosis) or pustulosis","Growth impairment and recurrent fevers accompany the bone and marrow disease","Interleukin-1 blockade is generally more effective than tumour necrosis factor inhibition and can control the inflammatory features"],"pathophysiology":"Majeed syndrome results from biallelic loss-of-function mutations in LPIN2, which encodes lipin-2, an enzyme involved in lipid metabolism and control of inflammation. Deficiency of lipin-2 dysregulates lipid handling in myeloid cells and enhances inflammasome activation, leading to excess interleukin-1 production. This drives sterile neutrophilic inflammation in bone (chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis) and skin, while associated effects on erythropoiesis produce a congenital dyserythropoietic anaemia. The central role of interleukin-1 explains the therapeutic benefit of IL-1 blockade.","additionalDifferentials":["Sporadic chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis (CRMO)","DIRA","Infective osteomyelitis","Congenital dyserythropoietic anaemia of other cause","Sweet syndrome from other triggers","SAPHO syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for LPIN2 mutations","Radiographs and MRI for multifocal osteomyelitis and periosteal reaction","Full blood count and blood film showing microcytic dyserythropoietic anaemia","Bone marrow examination demonstrating dyserythropoiesis","Acute-phase reactants during flares","Skin biopsy of neutrophilic dermatosis lesions"],"redFlags":["Severe anaemia requiring transfusion support","Extensive bone involvement causing deformity or growth arrest","Failure to thrive with persistent systemic inflammation","Development of complications from chronic transfusion such as iron overload"],"prognosis":"Majeed syndrome is a chronic disease of childhood; with interleukin-1 blockade the inflammatory bone and skin disease can be well controlled, though anaemia and growth effects may persist and require additional support.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent bone lesions as infective osteomyelitis with prolonged antibiotics","Overlooking the associated anaemia as unrelated iron deficiency","Choosing tumour necrosis factor inhibition when interleukin-1 blockade is generally more effective","Failing to test for LPIN2 in a child with CRMO and unexplained anaemia"],"management":["Interleukin-1 blockade with anakinra or canakinumab is the disease-specific mainstay and controls the inflammatory bone and skin disease","Corticosteroids for acute flares, with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs providing only limited benefit for the bone pain","Management of the congenital dyserythropoietic anaemia with iron-appropriate care and red-cell transfusion when required","Symptomatic treatment of the neutrophilic dermatosis and analgesia for the painful osteomyelitic bone lesions","Growth monitoring and nutritional support given the associated growth impairment","Surveillance of acute-phase reactants, blood counts and imaging to track disease activity and treatment response","Multidisciplinary paediatric rheumatology, haematology and genetics care, with counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive LPIN2 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Autoinflammation","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"major_depressive_disorder":{"name":"Major depressive disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MDD","major depression","clinical depression","unipolar depression"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Diagnosis requires a sustained period (at least around two weeks) of either persistently low mood or loss of interest/pleasure, together with several additional symptoms causing significant distress or functional impairment","Associated features commonly include disturbed sleep, appetite or weight change, fatigue, poor concentration, psychomotor slowing or agitation, feelings of worthlessness or excessive guilt, and recurrent thoughts of death","Always screen actively for suicidal ideation, intent, and plan, and ask about prior attempts and access to means","Screen for a past manic or hypomanic episode before starting an antidepressant, because an unrecognised bipolar diathesis can be destabilised","Exclude medical and substance-related contributors such as hypothyroidism, anaemia, and medication effects before attributing symptoms solely to a primary mood disorder","Management principles combine evidence-based psychotherapy (such as cognitive behavioural therapy or interpersonal therapy) with antidepressant pharmacotherapy, typically first-line SSRIs, chosen by side-effect profile and comorbidity","Antidepressant benefit is often delayed by several weeks, adherence should be reinforced, and treatment is generally continued well beyond symptom remission to reduce relapse","Severe, psychotic, treatment-resistant, or high-suicide-risk presentations may warrant combination pharmacotherapy, augmentation, or electroconvulsive therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Major depressive disorder is understood as a multifactorial condition arising from interactions among genetic vulnerability, dysregulation of monoamine neurotransmission (serotonin, noradrenaline, and dopamine), and disturbance of hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis stress responses. Contemporary models also emphasise impaired neuroplasticity and neurotrophic signalling, altered function in limbic and prefrontal circuits, and the role of chronic stress and adverse life events acting on a predisposed individual.","additionalDifferentials":["Bipolar disorder in a depressive phase","Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia)","Adjustment disorder with depressed mood","Grief and bereavement reactions","Hypothyroidism and other endocrine or metabolic disorders","Substance-induced or medication-induced mood disorder","Depression in the context of a neurocognitive disorder (pseudodementia)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured suicide risk assessment","Standardised symptom rating scales to gauge severity and track response","Thyroid function testing","Full blood count and metabolic panel to exclude anaemia and other medical contributors","Vitamin B12 and folate where clinically indicated","Substance use screening"],"redFlags":["Active suicidal ideation with plan, intent, or access to means","Psychotic features such as mood-congruent delusions or hallucinations","Profound self-neglect, refusal of food or fluids, or catatonia","Rapid deterioration or emerging manic symptoms after starting an antidepressant"],"prognosis":"Major depressive disorder is frequently recurrent; many patients respond to treatment but relapse risk rises with each subsequent episode and with residual symptoms. Prognosis is generally better with early treatment, good adherence, and psychosocial support, and worse with comorbid substance use, chronic medical illness, and untreated recurrent episodes.","pitfalls":["Starting an antidepressant without screening for a prior manic or hypomanic episode","Underestimating suicide risk or failing to reassess it during treatment","Stopping antidepressants too early once mood improves, increasing relapse risk","Attributing symptoms to depression while missing an underlying medical cause such as hypothyroidism","Judging non-response before an adequate dose and duration of treatment"],"management":["First-line antidepressant pharmacotherapy, typically an SSRI chosen by side-effect profile and comorbidity, with alternatives such as SNRIs, mirtazapine or bupropion as needed","Evidence-based psychotherapy such as cognitive behavioural therapy or interpersonal therapy, alone or combined with medication according to severity","Structured and repeated suicide risk assessment with safety planning and restriction of access to means","Screen for a prior manic or hypomanic episode before starting an antidepressant, and exclude and treat medical contributors such as hypothyroidism and anaemia","Manage treatment-resistant disease with switching, augmentation using lithium or an atypical antipsychotic, and electroconvulsive therapy for severe, psychotic or life-threatening depression","Monitor response with standardised rating scales, continue treatment to prevent relapse, and address psychosocial support and comorbid substance use","Escalate to specialist psychiatric services and consider admission for high suicide risk, psychosis or severe functional impairment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"mal_de_debarquement_syndrome":{"name":"Mal de debarquement syndrome","system":"Environmental / Emergency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MdDS","disembarkment syndrome","sickness of disembarkment"],"chapter":null,"source":"Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine / Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by a persistent sensation of rocking, swaying, or bobbing that continues after returning to stable ground following passive motion, most often a sea voyage or long flight","The perceived self-motion is typically relieved, at least temporarily, by re-exposure to passive motion such as driving a car","Unlike motion sickness, there is usually no nausea or vertigo, and the dominant complaint is the internal sense of movement","Diagnosis is clinical and requires exclusion of vestibular, neurological, and other causes of imbalance","Symptoms lasting only days after travel are common and self-limiting; the syndrome proper is diagnosed when the rocking persists for weeks or longer","Vestibular rehabilitation and habituation strategies are central to management, and stress or fatigue tends to aggravate symptoms","It is thought to reflect a failure of the brain to readapt from the motion-adapted state back to a stable environment"],"pathophysiology":"Mal de debarquement syndrome is understood as a disorder of central vestibular adaptation. During sustained passive motion the brain adapts its internal model of self-motion and postural control; on return to a stable environment this adaptation normally reverses quickly, but in MdDS the maladaptive state persists, so the person continues to perceive rocking or swaying. Re-exposure to motion transiently restores the adapted state and eases symptoms, which supports a central sensory reweighting mechanism rather than peripheral vestibular dysfunction.","additionalDifferentials":["Vestibular migraine","Persistent postural-perceptual dizziness","Peripheral vestibular disorders (e.g. vestibular neuritis, benign paroxysmal positional vertigo)","Central neurological causes of imbalance","Ordinary motion sickness or transient post-voyage unsteadiness","Anxiety-related dizziness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed clinical history linking onset to a period of passive motion","Neurological and vestibular examination to exclude central and peripheral disease","Audiometry and vestibular function testing where a peripheral cause is suspected","Brain imaging (MRI) if focal neurological features or atypical presentation","Assessment for migraine and psychological contributors"],"redFlags":["Focal neurological signs suggesting a central lesion","True rotational vertigo, hearing loss, or tinnitus pointing to a vestibular disorder","Progressive or asymmetric symptoms atypical for MdDS","Severe functional impairment or associated mood disturbance"],"prognosis":"Many cases that follow travel resolve spontaneously within days to weeks, but a proportion become persistent and can wax and wane over months to years; vestibular rehabilitation and habituation help, though a subset have a chronic, relapsing course.","pitfalls":["Confusing MdDS with ordinary motion sickness or peripheral vertigo","Failing to note the characteristic relief with re-exposure to motion","Missing vestibular migraine or persistent postural-perceptual dizziness as alternatives","Under-recognising the impact of stress, fatigue, and anxiety on symptoms"],"management":["Vestibular rehabilitation and habituation exercises are the mainstay, retraining central vestibular adaptation","Reassurance and explanation, with early expectation that many travel-triggered cases resolve spontaneously","Avoid habitual re-exposure to passive motion as a coping strategy despite its transient relief, and manage stress and fatigue that aggravate symptoms","Optic-flow readaptation protocols, using full-field visual stimulation synchronised to head roll, in specialised centres for persistent cases","Pharmacological adjuncts targeting central mechanisms, such as a benzodiazepine (clonazepam) or an SSRI/SNRI, for symptom relief, with treatment of coexisting migraine","Address psychological comorbidity such as anxiety and depression with cognitive behavioural therapy and support","Neuro-otology referral with confirmation of the diagnosis by excluding vestibular and neurological causes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine","Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"mal_de_meleda":{"name":"Mal de Meleda","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["keratosis palmoplantaris transgrediens of Siemens","Meleda disease","SLURP1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal recessive transgredient palmoplantar keratoderma originally described on the Adriatic island of Mljet (Meleda)","Caused by biallelic variants in SLURP1, which encodes a secreted lymphocyte antigen-6 protein modulating cholinergic signalling and keratinocyte differentiation","The keratoderma extends beyond the palms and soles onto the dorsa of the hands and feet (the transgredient pattern), often with a well-demarcated erythematous border","Progressive constricting bands (pseudo-ainhum) can develop around digits and may lead to autoamputation","Associated features include hyperhidrosis with malodour, secondary fungal and bacterial infection, nail changes and perioral erythema","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by the family history and SLURP1 molecular testing","Management is supportive with keratolytics, emollients, oral retinoids in severe disease, meticulous foot care and treatment of secondary infection"],"pathophysiology":"Mal de Meleda is caused by loss-of-function variants in SLURP1, encoding secreted Ly-6/uPAR-related protein 1, which acts on nicotinic acetylcholine receptors to regulate keratinocyte proliferation, differentiation and apoptosis and to modulate skin inflammation. Deficiency disturbs the balance of epidermal proliferation and terminal differentiation in acral skin, producing a thick, diffuse transgredient keratoderma. Accompanying hyperhidrosis and barrier disruption predispose to maceration, malodour and secondary infection, while chronic inflammation and fibrous constriction can create pseudo-ainhum.","additionalDifferentials":["Other diffuse hereditary palmoplantar keratodermas (e.g. Vörner/Unna-Thost)","Loricrin keratoderma (Vohwinkel variant)","Olmsted syndrome","Papillon-Lefèvre syndrome","Huriez syndrome","Acquired keratoderma from inflammatory or infective causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of SLURP1","Skin biopsy showing orthohyperkeratosis with acanthosis","Mycological and bacterial swabs for secondary infection","Clinical examination for pseudo-ainhum and digital constriction","Nail assessment for associated dystrophy","Family history and pedigree analysis"],"redFlags":["Constricting fibrous bands (pseudo-ainhum) risking digital autoamputation","Deep secondary infection or cellulitis of macerated acral skin","Rapidly progressive or asymmetrical thickening raising concern for malignant change","Severe functional impairment of hands or feet"],"prognosis":"The keratoderma is lifelong and tends to progress through childhood; with diligent skin and foot care most patients maintain function, but pseudo-ainhum, recurrent infection and disability can complicate severe cases.","pitfalls":["Missing the transgredient spread onto dorsal skin that distinguishes it from common diffuse keratodermas","Overlooking early pseudo-ainhum that is amenable to intervention before autoamputation","Failing to treat the secondary fungal and bacterial colonisation that worsens odour and maceration","Not offering genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"management":["Regular application of keratolytic emollients such as urea, salicylic acid or lactic acid preparations to soften and reduce the transgredient keratoderma","Topical or, for severe disease, systemic retinoids such as acitretin to reduce hyperkeratosis, with monitoring for mucocutaneous and skeletal adverse effects","Mechanical paring and debridement of thickened palmoplantar skin with podiatry input to preserve function and mobility","Control of hyperhidrosis and malodour with antiperspirants, absorbent measures and topical botulinum toxin where troublesome","Prompt treatment of secondary bacterial and fungal infection with appropriate antimicrobials guided by mycological and bacterial swabs","Regular surveillance for constricting digital bands (pseudo-ainhum) with early surgical release to prevent autoamputation","Autosomal recessive inheritance warrants genetic counselling and SLURP1 testing for at-risk family members"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"malakoplakia":{"name":"Malakoplakia","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["malacoplakia","Michaelis-Gutmann body disease","von Hansemann histiocyte disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Malakoplakia is a rare chronic granulomatous inflammatory condition arising from defective macrophage killing of bacteria, most often coliforms such as Escherichia coli","It most commonly affects the urinary tract, particularly the bladder, but can involve the kidneys, gastrointestinal tract, skin and other organs","It is strongly associated with immunosuppression, chronic infection, malignancy and debilitated states","Cystoscopically it appears as soft yellow-brown mucosal plaques or nodules","The diagnostic histological hallmark is the Michaelis-Gutmann body, a laminated calcified inclusion within large macrophages (von Hansemann cells)","Management combines prolonged antibiotics that concentrate intracellularly, such as fluoroquinolones or trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, with reduction of immunosuppression where possible","Surgical excision may be required for large, obstructive or destructive lesions"],"pathophysiology":"Malakoplakia is thought to arise from a defect in the intracellular killing and digestion of phagocytosed bacteria, typically coliform organisms, by macrophages. Incompletely degraded bacterial fragments accumulate within these cells, and mineralisation of the residual material with calcium and iron produces the characteristic laminated Michaelis-Gutmann bodies. The resulting collections of engorged macrophages form the soft plaques and nodules seen clinically. Because the underlying problem is impaired bacterial clearance, the condition is closely linked to immunosuppression and chronic infection, and can produce mass-like or obstructive lesions in affected organs.","additionalDifferentials":["Genitourinary or gastrointestinal malignancy","Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis","Tuberculosis or other chronic granulomatous infection","Inflammatory pseudotumour","Other chronic inflammatory or infective plaques","Whipple disease when the gut is involved"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy for histology demonstrating von Hansemann macrophages and Michaelis-Gutmann bodies","Special stains (von Kossa for calcium, Perls for iron, periodic acid-Schiff) to highlight the inclusions","Urine and tissue cultures, commonly yielding coliform organisms","Cystoscopy or endoscopy to characterise mucosal plaques","Cross-sectional imaging to define the extent of organ involvement","Assessment of immune status and screening for underlying malignancy"],"redFlags":["Obstructive uropathy or renal impairment from urinary tract lesions","Mass lesions mimicking or coexisting with malignancy","Systemic infection in an immunosuppressed patient","Progressive or destructive organ involvement"],"prognosis":"Localised disease often responds to prolonged intracellularly active antibiotics and control of predisposing factors, but extensive or renal involvement, particularly in immunosuppressed patients, carries a worse outlook and may require surgery.","pitfalls":["Mistaking mass-forming lesions for malignancy without biopsy","Choosing antibiotics that do not penetrate macrophages well","Overlooking and failing to reduce contributory immunosuppression","Underestimating the risk of urinary obstruction and renal damage"],"management":["Prolonged courses of antibiotics that concentrate intracellularly, such as fluoroquinolones or trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, directed by urine and tissue culture of the causative coliform organisms","Adjunctive agents that enhance macrophage bactericidal function, such as bethanechol and ascorbic acid","Reduce or modify immunosuppression wherever clinically feasible to restore intracellular bacterial clearance","Surgical excision or debulking for mass-like, obstructive or medically refractory lesions","Identify and treat predisposing factors, including screening for occult malignancy and correcting debilitated or immunosuppressed states","Monitor with repeat imaging, cystoscopy or endoscopy for resolution and to detect recurrence, as therapy is often prolonged","Refer for urological, surgical or infectious diseases input according to the organ involved"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"malan_syndrome":{"name":"Malan syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sotos syndrome 2","NFIX-related overgrowth syndrome","Malan overgrowth syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is an overgrowth-with-intellectual-disability syndrome characterised by tall stature, macrocephaly, and a recognisable facial appearance including a long, narrow face and prominent forehead","It is caused by variants in NFIX, a transcription factor gene; the overgrowth phenotype arises from a distinct molecular mechanism to that producing the separate, unrelated Marshall-Smith syndrome caused by other NFIX variants","Developmental delay and intellectual disability are typical, and behavioural features such as anxiety and autistic traits are common","A slender, marfanoid habitus with long limbs may prompt consideration of connective tissue disorders","Ophthalmological anomalies including strabismus and optic atrophy are recognised associations","Diagnosis is confirmed by molecular genetic testing, often through genome or exome sequencing or a targeted overgrowth panel, having considered Sotos syndrome in the differential","There is no specific treatment; care is anticipatory and multidisciplinary, addressing development, behaviour, vision, and skeletal and cardiovascular surveillance as clinically indicated"],"pathophysiology":"Malan syndrome is a chromatin- and transcription-related overgrowth disorder caused by heterozygous variants in NFIX, which encodes a nuclear factor I transcription factor involved in regulating cell proliferation and differentiation during development, including in brain and skeletal tissue. Loss-of-function variants that reduce functional NFIX dosage disturb the tightly controlled transcriptional programmes governing growth, producing the characteristic tall stature, macrocephaly, and neurodevelopmental features. Notably, a separate class of NFIX variants causes the clinically distinct Marshall-Smith syndrome, illustrating how different molecular consequences at one locus generate divergent phenotypes. Most cases arise de novo and inheritance, when relevant, is autosomal dominant.","additionalDifferentials":["Sotos syndrome (NSD1-related)","Weaver syndrome (EZH2-related)","Tatton-Brown-Rahman syndrome (DNMT3A-related)","Marfan syndrome and related connective tissue disorders","Fragile X syndrome","Other transcription- or chromatin-related overgrowth syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or genome sequencing, or a targeted overgrowth/intellectual disability gene panel","Chromosomal microarray to exclude copy number causes of overgrowth and developmental delay","MRI of the brain where neurological signs or macrocephaly warrant imaging","Ophthalmological assessment for strabismus and optic atrophy","Developmental and behavioural evaluation","Skeletal and cardiovascular assessment given the marfanoid habitus"],"redFlags":["Rapidly increasing head circumference or neurological signs suggesting intracranial pathology","Features suggesting aortic root dilatation or other cardiovascular involvement in a marfanoid child","Significant regression of skills prompting re-evaluation","Progressive visual loss from optic atrophy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis relates chiefly to the degree of intellectual disability and any associated medical complications; lifespan is not clearly reduced in most individuals. Ongoing developmental, educational, and behavioural support improves function, and surveillance addresses ophthalmological and skeletal issues as they arise.","pitfalls":["Confusing the disorder with Sotos syndrome and stopping testing at NSD1","Attributing the marfanoid habitus to a primary connective tissue disorder and missing the overgrowth syndrome","Overlooking anxiety and autistic features that benefit from targeted support","Assuming NFIX variants always cause the same phenotype, when they may instead cause Marshall-Smith syndrome"],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy exists; management is supportive with early developmental intervention including speech and language, occupational and physiotherapy and tailored educational support","Behavioural and psychological support for anxiety and autistic traits, with input from developmental paediatrics or psychiatry","Ophthalmological review and treatment of strabismus with surveillance for optic atrophy","Skeletal and cardiovascular assessment given the marfanoid habitus, with monitoring for related complications","Growth and head circumference monitoring coordinated by a multidisciplinary team including clinical genetics","Genetic counselling recognising autosomal dominant inheritance with most cases arising de novo, and confirmatory NFIX molecular testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (University of Washington)","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"male_infertility":{"name":"Male infertility","system":"Reproductive medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["male factor infertility","male subfertility","impaired male fertility"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard sleep & reproductive medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A male factor contributes to roughly half of couples presenting with infertility, so both partners should be evaluated in parallel from the outset","Semen analysis is the cornerstone investigation and should be repeated because of considerable biological variability before conclusions are drawn","Abnormalities are described in terms of sperm concentration, motility, and morphology, with azoospermia, oligozoospermia, and asthenozoospermia denoting the main patterns","Causes are grouped into pre-testicular (hypothalamic-pituitary and hormonal), testicular (primary spermatogenic failure), and post-testicular (obstruction or ejaculatory disorders)","Endocrine evaluation with gonadotrophins and testosterone helps localise the problem; low gonadotrophins suggest a central cause whereas high FSH indicates primary testicular failure","Varicocele is a common and potentially correctable cause, and reversible contributors such as heat, smoking, alcohol, obesity, and certain drugs should be sought","Azoospermia should be characterised as obstructive or non-obstructive, since surgical sperm retrieval with assisted reproduction can achieve fatherhood in many cases","Genetic evaluation, including karyotype and Y-chromosome microdeletion testing, is indicated in severe oligozoospermia or non-obstructive azoospermia"],"pathophysiology":"Male fertility depends on the integrity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis, on spermatogenesis within the seminiferous tubules driven by FSH and intratesticular testosterone, and on an unobstructed ductal system allowing sperm transport and ejaculation. Pre-testicular disease reflects deficient gonadotrophin stimulation (hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism from pituitary or hypothalamic disorders, hyperprolactinaemia, or exogenous androgen use). Testicular causes include genetic conditions such as Klinefelter syndrome, cryptorchidism, prior orchitis, chemotherapy or radiation, and varicocele-related impairment of the testicular thermal and vascular environment. Post-testicular causes include congenital absence or acquired obstruction of the vas deferens, ejaculatory duct obstruction, retrograde ejaculation, and antisperm antibodies. The final common pathway is a reduction in the number or function of sperm reaching the oocyte.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism (pituitary or hypothalamic)","Primary testicular failure including Klinefelter syndrome","Varicocele","Obstructive azoospermia including congenital absence of the vas deferens","Retrograde or failed ejaculation","Exogenous androgen or anabolic steroid use suppressing spermatogenesis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repeated semen analysis performed to standard laboratory criteria","Hormonal profile including FSH, LH, testosterone, and prolactin","Scrotal examination and ultrasound to detect varicocele or obstruction","Karyotype and Y-chromosome microdeletion analysis in severe cases","Post-ejaculatory urinalysis when retrograde ejaculation is suspected"],"redFlags":["Azoospermia requiring prompt distinction between obstructive and non-obstructive causes","Signs of a pituitary mass such as visual field loss or headache with low gonadotrophins","Bilateral testicular atrophy or gynaecomastia suggesting significant endocrine or genetic disease","A testicular mass discovered during fertility evaluation, raising concern for malignancy"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the cause; hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism and obstruction are often correctable, varicocele repair may improve parameters in selected men, and even severe spermatogenic failure can frequently be overcome by surgical sperm retrieval combined with assisted reproductive techniques.","pitfalls":["Investigating and treating only the female partner while overlooking male factor contribution","Drawing conclusions from a single abnormal semen analysis without repeat testing","Missing exogenous androgen or anabolic steroid use as a reversible cause of azoospermia","Failing to offer genetic counselling before assisted reproduction in men with genetic causes"],"management":["Address reversible contributors by stopping exogenous androgens, reducing alcohol and smoking, managing obesity and avoiding scrotal heat and gonadotoxic drugs","Gonadotrophin or pulsatile gonadotrophin-releasing hormone therapy for hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism, and a dopamine agonist where hyperprolactinaemia is the cause","Surgical varicocele repair in selected men with a clinical varicocele and impaired semen parameters","Surgical reconstruction or relief of ductal obstruction, or surgical sperm retrieval where reconstruction is not feasible","Assisted reproductive techniques, particularly intracytoplasmic sperm injection, using ejaculated or surgically retrieved sperm in severe spermatogenic failure","Sympathomimetic therapy or retrieval of sperm from post-ejaculatory urine for retrograde ejaculation","Karyotype, Y-chromosome microdeletion and CFTR analysis with genetic counselling before assisted conception in appropriate cases","Evaluate and manage both partners in parallel through a reproductive medicine service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"malignant_acanthosis_nigricans":{"name":"malignant acanthosis nigricans","system":"Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["paraneoplastic acanthosis nigricans","acanthosis nigricans maligna"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Abrupt onset and rapid extension of symmetrical velvety, hyperpigmented plaques in flexures (neck, axillae, groin) in a non-obese, often older adult should prompt an urgent malignancy search.","Overwhelmingly associated with intra-abdominal adenocarcinoma; gastric carcinoma accounts for the majority, with pancreatic, colorectal, hepatobiliary and ovarian primaries also reported.","Mucosal and mucocutaneous involvement (oral cavity, lips, conjunctiva, anogenital) and periorbital or palmar disease are far more frequent than in the benign obesity-related form.","Commonly coexists with other paraneoplastic signs, notably tripe palms (palmar acanthosis) and the sign of Leser-Trelat, reflecting a shared growth-factor mechanism.","Pruritus is frequently marked, in contrast to the largely asymptomatic insulin-resistance-associated variant.","The eruption may precede, accompany or follow diagnosis of the tumour and characteristically parallels the malignancy, regressing with successful treatment and recurring with relapse.","Weight loss rather than obesity, together with rapid progression over weeks to months, helps distinguish it clinically from the common benign insulin-resistance type."],"pathophysiology":"Malignant acanthosis nigricans is thought to arise from tumour-secreted growth factors that stimulate epidermal proliferation. Transforming growth factor alpha (TGF-alpha), which is structurally similar to epidermal growth factor and binds the epidermal growth factor receptor on keratinocytes, is considered central, with contributions from fibroblast growth factor and insulin-like growth factor 1. This drives keratinocyte and fibroblast hyperproliferation producing papillomatosis and hyperkeratosis, while relatively modest increases in melanin account for the hyperpigmented appearance. The parallel course with tumour burden supports a humoral, growth-factor-mediated aetiology rather than local insulin-receptor activation as seen in benign disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign obesity/insulin-resistance-related acanthosis nigricans","Drug-induced acanthosis nigricans (nicotinic acid, systemic corticosteroids, oestrogens, protease inhibitors)","Confluent and reticulated papillomatosis of Gougerot-Carteaud","Endocrinopathy-associated acanthosis nigricans (HAIR-AN syndrome, acromegaly, Cushing syndrome)","Dowling-Degos disease","Pellagra and other nutritional dermatoses affecting flexures","Terra firma-forme dermatosis / retention hyperkeratosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with biopsy, given the leading association with gastric adenocarcinoma","CT of chest, abdomen and pelvis to identify an occult primary and to stage disease","Tumour markers as directed by suspected primary (CEA, CA 19-9, CA-125)","Skin biopsy demonstrating papillomatosis and hyperkeratosis with minimal hyperpigmentation","Fasting glucose, HbA1c and insulin to exclude insulin-resistance-related disease","Colonoscopy and faecal occult blood testing when a lower gastrointestinal primary is suspected","PET-CT when conventional imaging is unrevealing"],"redFlags":["New extensive acanthosis nigricans in a non-obese adult over 40","Rapid progression with mucosal involvement and unexplained weight loss","Concurrent tripe palms or eruptive seborrhoeic keratoses","Marked pruritus with associated systemic symptoms"],"prognosis":"The prognosis reflects the underlying malignancy, which is frequently an advanced, aggressive adenocarcinoma with short median survival. Cutaneous lesions may improve or regress following effective tumour treatment.","pitfalls":["Attributing new acanthosis nigricans to obesity and missing an underlying tumour","Failing to examine the mucosae and palms or to look for the sign of Leser-Trelat","Assuming a normal initial endoscopy excludes malignancy when clinical suspicion is high","Overlooking recurrence of the skin signs as a marker of tumour relapse"],"management":["Urgent identification and treatment of the underlying malignancy, most often an intra-abdominal adenocarcinoma, since the eruption characteristically regresses with successful tumour control","Symptomatic skin care with emollients and keratolytics such as topical retinoids, salicylic acid or vitamin D analogues to reduce the velvety hyperkeratotic plaques","Antipruritic measures for the often marked itch, which is more prominent than in the benign insulin-resistance-related form","Use the cutaneous course as a clinical marker, since recurrence of lesions may herald tumour relapse","Coordinate care between oncology and dermatology, with palliative and supportive input given the frequently advanced malignancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"malignant_glaucoma":{"name":"Malignant glaucoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["aqueous misdirection","aqueous misdirection syndrome","ciliary block glaucoma","malignant glaucoma (aqueous misdirection)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare but serious secondary angle closure caused by misdirection of aqueous into or behind the vitreous, pushing the whole lens-iris diaphragm forward","It classically arises after intraocular surgery, especially incisional glaucoma or cataract surgery in eyes with angle closure, but can follow laser procedures or occur spontaneously","The hallmark is a uniformly shallow or flat anterior chamber, both centrally and peripherally, in combination with raised or normal pressure and a patent iridotomy","The patent iridotomy and shallow central chamber distinguish it from pupillary-block angle closure, which the iridotomy would relieve","It must be differentiated from suprachoroidal haemorrhage and choroidal effusion, which can also flatten the chamber after surgery","Initial treatment is intensive cycloplegia to tighten the zonule and pull the lens back, together with pressure-lowering and anti-inflammatory therapy, and aqueous suppressants","Laser disruption of the anterior hyaloid, sometimes with the posterior capsule, can re-establish forward aqueous flow, and refractory cases require vitrectomy to relieve the block","Miotics tend to worsen the condition and should be avoided, whereas cycloplegics help"],"pathophysiology":"Malignant glaucoma, or aqueous misdirection, is thought to result from an abnormal anteriorly displaced relationship between the ciliary processes, lens equator, and anterior vitreous face, which diverts aqueous posteriorly into or behind the vitreous body instead of forward into the anterior chamber. Trapped aqueous raises pressure in the vitreous compartment and pushes the lens-iris diaphragm forward, producing uniform shallowing or flattening of the entire anterior chamber and secondary angle closure. A ciliary-block or hyaloid element impedes normal forward aqueous movement. Because the mechanism is behind the iris, a patent peripheral iridotomy does not relieve it, and interventions that draw the diaphragm backward or open the hyaloid pathway are needed to break the cycle.","additionalDifferentials":["Pupillary-block angle closure (relieved by patent iridotomy)","Suprachoroidal haemorrhage","Serous choroidal effusion","Overfiltration with a flat bleb after trabeculectomy","Pupillary block from a swollen or dislocated lens","Plateau iris syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp assessment showing uniform central and peripheral chamber shallowing","Confirmation of a patent peripheral iridotomy","Applanation tonometry to gauge pressure","B-scan ultrasonography to exclude suprachoroidal haemorrhage and choroidal effusion","Ultrasound biomicroscopy or anterior segment OCT to assess the ciliary body and anterior chamber configuration","Gonioscopy to document the closed angle"],"redFlags":["A uniformly shallow or flat chamber after intraocular surgery despite a patent iridotomy","Rising pressure with progressive chamber flattening requiring urgent intervention","Failure to distinguish it from a suprachoroidal haemorrhage, which needs very different management","Worsening after miotic treatment given in the mistaken belief it is pupillary block"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition many eyes respond to cycloplegic and medical therapy or to laser hyaloidotomy, but the condition can be difficult to control and may need vitrectomy; delayed or misdirected treatment risks persistent flat chamber, corneal decompensation, and glaucomatous visual loss.","pitfalls":["Treating it as pupillary block and giving miotics, which aggravate the misdirection","Missing a suprachoroidal haemorrhage or choroidal effusion as the cause of the flat chamber","Repeating an iridotomy when one is already patent instead of addressing the hyaloid block","Delaying vitrectomy in medically unresponsive cases","Failing to treat the fellow eye's angle-closure risk that predisposed to the original condition"],"management":["Intensive topical cycloplegia with an agent such as atropine to tighten the zonule and draw the lens-iris diaphragm posteriorly","Aqueous suppressants including beta-blockers, alpha-agonists and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, with hyperosmotic agents to reduce vitreous volume and lower intraocular pressure","Topical corticosteroid to reduce inflammation, and cessation of any miotics, which aggravate the condition","Nd:YAG laser disruption of the anterior hyaloid face, with capsulotomy in pseudophakic eyes, to re-establish forward aqueous flow when medical therapy fails","Pars plana vitrectomy, often combined with irido-zonulo-hyaloidectomy or lens extraction, for refractory cases","Exclude and manage mimics such as suprachoroidal haemorrhage and choroidal effusion before escalating surgery","Prophylactic cycloplegia and avoidance of miotics in the fellow eye, with prompt vitreoretinal referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"malignant_hyperthermia":{"name":"Malignant hyperthermia","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MH","malignant hyperpyrexia","anaesthetic-induced hypermetabolic crisis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A pharmacogenetic hypermetabolic crisis triggered by volatile anaesthetics and depolarising neuromuscular blockers in susceptible individuals","The earliest and most sensitive sign is an unexplained rise in end-tidal carbon dioxide despite increased ventilation, often with tachycardia and mixed acidosis","Masseter or generalised muscle rigidity, rapidly rising temperature, and dark urine from myoglobin are later features; hyperthermia is frequently a late rather than early sign","Immediate management is to stop triggering agents, call for help and the emergency supply of dantrolene, hyperventilate with high-flow oxygen, and switch to a non-triggering technique","Dantrolene is the specific antidote and should be given without delay and repeated until the hypermetabolic state resolves","Supportive care includes active cooling, treating hyperkalaemia and arrhythmias, correcting acidosis, and maintaining urine output to protect against myoglobinuric renal injury","Susceptibility is largely inherited (commonly RYR1 gene variants) and is linked to certain myopathies; a family or personal history should prompt a trigger-free anaesthetic","Confirmatory testing uses the in-vitro contracture (caffeine-halothane) test with genetic testing, and survivors should be counselled and referred to an MH unit"],"pathophysiology":"Malignant hyperthermia results from abnormal regulation of skeletal muscle calcium, most often due to mutations affecting the ryanodine receptor (RYR1) that controls calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. On exposure to volatile agents or succinylcholine, uncontrolled calcium release produces sustained muscle contraction and a massive increase in aerobic and anaerobic metabolism. This drives rapid carbon dioxide and heat generation, oxygen consumption, lactic acidosis, ATP depletion, and eventually muscle breakdown with hyperkalaemia, myoglobinaemia, and rhabdomyolysis. Dantrolene reduces calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum and interrupts the cycle.","additionalDifferentials":["Inadequate anaesthesia or analgesia with sympathetic response","Sepsis or systemic inflammatory response","Thyroid storm or phaeochromocytoma crisis","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome or serotonin syndrome","Equipment-related rebreathing or exhausted carbon dioxide absorbent","Environmental or iatrogenic hyperthermia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous capnography and core temperature monitoring to detect the crisis early","Arterial blood gas showing combined respiratory and metabolic acidosis","Serial potassium, creatine kinase, and myoglobin to track rhabdomyolysis","Coagulation studies to detect disseminated intravascular coagulation","Later in-vitro contracture testing and RYR1 genetic analysis for confirmation"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising end-tidal carbon dioxide with tachycardia and rigidity indicating an evolving MH crisis","Severe hyperthermia with mixed acidosis and hyperkalaemia threatening arrhythmia and arrest","Cardiac arrhythmia or arrest during or after a triggering anaesthetic","Dark urine and rising creatine kinase signalling rhabdomyolysis and renal risk"],"prognosis":"Mortality has fallen dramatically with early recognition and prompt dantrolene, but delayed treatment can lead to disseminated intravascular coagulation, renal failure, and death; survivors and relatives require testing and lifelong trigger avoidance.","pitfalls":["Attributing rising carbon dioxide or tachycardia to light anaesthesia and delaying diagnosis","Failing to have dantrolene immediately available or delaying its administration","Confusing an isolated masseter spasm with a full crisis or, conversely, dismissing it entirely","Neglecting active cooling and management of hyperkalaemia and myoglobinuria","Not counselling the patient and family or arranging specialist referral after recovery"],"management":["Immediately discontinue all volatile anaesthetics and succinylcholine, call for help and the emergency dantrolene supply, and convert to a trigger-free anaesthetic technique","Administer intravenous dantrolene without delay, repeating until the hypermetabolic state resolves","Hyperventilate with high-flow oxygen and remove or flush the volatile agent from the breathing circuit","Institute active cooling while avoiding overcooling","Treat hyperkalaemia with insulin and dextrose, calcium and other measures, and manage arrhythmias, avoiding calcium channel blockers alongside dantrolene","Correct metabolic acidosis and maintain a brisk urine output with fluids to protect against myoglobinuric renal injury","Admit to critical care for continued monitoring, watching for disseminated intravascular coagulation and recrudescence","Refer the patient and relatives for RYR1 genetic and in-vitro contracture testing, document the reaction and counsel on lifelong trigger avoidance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"malignant_hyperthermia_susceptibility":{"name":"Malignant hyperthermia susceptibility","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MH","MHS","malignant hyperpyrexia","RYR1","CACNA1S","anaesthetic-triggered hypermetabolic crisis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A pharmacogenetic disorder of skeletal muscle in which exposure to volatile anaesthetic agents or the depolarising relaxant succinylcholine can trigger a life-threatening hypermetabolic crisis","A developing crisis shows a rising end-tidal carbon dioxide, tachycardia, muscle rigidity (sometimes masseter spasm), hyperthermia that is often a late sign, and metabolic acidosis with hyperkalaemia and rhabdomyolysis","Most susceptibility is autosomal dominant and linked to mutations in RYR1, encoding the ryanodine receptor calcium-release channel, with a minority linked to CACNA1S","Emergency management is immediate withdrawal of triggering agents, dantrolene, and aggressive supportive cooling and correction of acidosis and hyperkalaemia, alongside a switch to a trigger-free anaesthetic technique","The in-vitro (caffeine-halothane) contracture test on freshly biopsied muscle remains the diagnostic reference standard, complemented by genetic testing","Susceptibility overlaps with certain RYR1-related myopathies such as central core disease, and a personal or family history of anaesthetic death or unexplained rigidity is an important clue","Patients and relatives should carry documentation of their status and be referred to a specialist malignant hyperthermia unit for family investigation"],"pathophysiology":"Malignant hyperthermia susceptibility most often results from mutations in RYR1, the gene encoding the ryanodine receptor that releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum during excitation-contraction coupling, and less often from mutations in the CACNA1S calcium channel that triggers it. The abnormal channel is hypersensitive, so on exposure to volatile anaesthetics or succinylcholine it releases uncontrolled calcium into the myoplasm. Sustained high calcium drives continuous muscle contraction and a runaway increase in aerobic and anaerobic metabolism, generating heat, carbon dioxide and lactate, depleting adenosine triphosphate, and causing membrane breakdown with rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia and acidosis. Without prompt treatment this hypermetabolic cascade progresses to multi-organ failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome","Sepsis or thyroid storm causing intraoperative hyperthermia","Exertional or drug-induced rhabdomyolysis","Inadequate anaesthesia or ventilation causing hypercapnia and tachycardia","Other RYR1-related myopathies with anaesthetic sensitivity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["In-vitro contracture testing (caffeine-halothane) on freshly biopsied skeletal muscle at a specialist unit","Targeted RYR1 and CACNA1S genetic testing","Creatine kinase and myoglobin, with arterial blood gases and potassium during a suspected crisis","Electrocardiography and continuous monitoring for hyperkalaemic and acidotic changes","Muscle biopsy histology where an associated core myopathy is suspected","Detailed personal and family anaesthetic history"],"redFlags":["Unexplained rising end-tidal carbon dioxide with tachycardia and rigidity under anaesthesia","Masseter spasm after succinylcholine","Rapidly rising core temperature with metabolic acidosis, hyperkalaemia or dark myoglobinuric urine","Family history of anaesthetic death or unexplained perioperative crisis"],"prognosis":"With early recognition, prompt dantrolene and modern supportive care the mortality of an acute crisis is now low, and susceptible individuals can undergo safe anaesthesia using trigger-free agents, but an unrecognised or untreated crisis remains rapidly fatal.","pitfalls":["Waiting for hyperthermia, which is frequently a late sign, before acting on rising carbon dioxide and rigidity","Confusing a crisis with neuroleptic malignant or serotonin syndrome and delaying dantrolene","Assuming a previously uneventful anaesthetic excludes susceptibility, since crises can occur on later exposures","Failing to refer the patient and family for contracture and genetic testing and to document trigger-agent avoidance"],"management":["Plan trigger-free anaesthesia using total intravenous techniques and non-depolarising relaxants, avoiding all volatile agents and succinylcholine","Prepare a vapour-free or thoroughly flushed anaesthetic machine and ensure an adequate dantrolene supply is immediately available","Confirm susceptibility with in-vitro caffeine-halothane contracture testing and targeted RYR1 and CACNA1S genetic testing","Should a crisis occur, withdraw triggers, give dantrolene and provide aggressive supportive cooling with correction of acidosis and hyperkalaemia","Document susceptibility clearly, issue medical-alert identification and inform future anaesthetic teams","Genetic counselling and cascade family screening given autosomal dominant inheritance","Recognise overlap with RYR1-related myopathies such as central core disease and screen accordingly"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: Malignant Hyperthermia Susceptibility"],"crossLinks":[]},"malignant_migrating_partial_seizures_infancy":{"name":"Epilepsy of infancy with migrating focal seizures (KCNT1)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["EIMFS","malignant migrating partial seizures of infancy","MMPSI","migrating partial epilepsy of infancy","KCNT1 migrating focal seizures"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / ILAE","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe developmental and epileptic encephalopathy of early infancy, most often caused by gain-of-function variants in KCNT1, which encodes the sodium-activated potassium channel Slack","The defining feature is near-continuous focal seizures that migrate independently between multiple cortical regions and between hemispheres, seen dramatically on video electroencephalography","Seizure onset is typically in the first six months of life, often within the first weeks, with rapidly escalating frequency","Development arrests or regresses with seizure onset, leading to profound impairment, acquired microcephaly, and loss of visual and social responsiveness","Other genes, including SCN2A, SCN1A, SCN8A, SLC12A5, and TBC1D24, can cause the same clinical syndrome, so it is genetically heterogeneous","Seizures are characteristically drug-resistant; quinidine has been trialled to target the KCNT1 gain-of-function channel but with inconsistent benefit and a need for cardiac monitoring","There is significant morbidity and mortality, including a raised risk of sudden unexpected death, so supportive multidisciplinary care and family counselling are central"],"pathophysiology":"Epilepsy of infancy with migrating focal seizures is most commonly driven by gain-of-function variants in KCNT1, encoding the sodium-activated potassium channel Slack that shapes the slow afterhyperpolarisation and neuronal firing patterns. Enhanced channel activity disrupts the normal control of excitability and inhibitory balance, generating multiple independent seizure foci that migrate across the cortex. The relentless multifocal ictal activity impairs the developing brain, producing the characteristic developmental arrest and regression. Because the syndrome can also arise from sodium-channel and other genes, the migrating seizure phenotype reflects a final common pathway of severely disordered early network excitability.","additionalDifferentials":["SCN2A, SCN1A, and SCN8A developmental and epileptic encephalopathies","Other KCNT1-related epilepsy phenotypes","SLC12A5 (KCC2) related epilepsy","TBC1D24-related epileptic encephalopathy","Structural or metabolic epilepsies of infancy","Ohtahara syndrome and early myoclonic encephalopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Prolonged video electroencephalography to demonstrate migrating independent multifocal ictal patterns","Epilepsy gene panel or whole-exome sequencing, with KCNT1 a priority target","Brain magnetic resonance imaging to exclude structural causes and document acquired changes","Metabolic screen to exclude treatable mimics","Electrocardiography and cardiac review before and during any quinidine trial","Developmental and growth monitoring including head circumference"],"redFlags":["Near-continuous migrating seizures with developmental arrest in early infancy","Acquired microcephaly and loss of previously attained skills","Cardiac conduction changes during quinidine therapy","Features suggesting elevated sudden unexpected death in epilepsy risk"],"prognosis":"The syndrome generally carries a poor prognosis, with drug-resistant seizures, profound developmental impairment, and significant mortality including sudden unexpected death, though occasional milder courses are reported.","pitfalls":["Expecting a reliable quinidine response when benefit is variable and requires cardiac monitoring","Assuming KCNT1 is the only cause and stopping the genetic workup","Overlooking treatable metabolic mimics early in the presentation","Neglecting family counselling about prognosis and sudden unexpected death risk"],"management":["Broad-spectrum antiseizure medications are tried but seizures are characteristically drug-resistant, so rational combinations and early consideration of the ketogenic diet are used","A trial of quinidine to target the KCNT1 gain-of-function channel may be considered, with electrocardiographic and cardiac monitoring for QT prolongation, though benefit is inconsistent","Supportive care addressing feeding, respiratory and developmental needs with physiotherapy and occupational therapy input","Monitor development, growth and head circumference given the risk of regression and acquired microcephaly","Manage acute prolonged seizures and status epilepticus with an individualised rescue protocol","Coordinate care through paediatric epileptology and clinical genetics with confirmatory KCNT1 and broader gene-panel testing","Genetic counselling for the family alongside discussion of prognosis, sudden unexpected death risk and palliative support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: KCNT1-Related Epilepsy","ILAE resources on epilepsy of infancy with migrating focal seizures"],"crossLinks":["kcnt1_epilepsy"]},"malignant_peripheral_nerve_sheath_tumor":{"name":"Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour (NF1-associated)","system":"Oncology / Sarcoma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPNST","malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor","neurofibrosarcoma","malignant schwannoma","NF1-associated MPNST"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A malignant sarcoma of peripheral nerve sheath differentiation, arising sporadically, after radiotherapy, or in association with neurofibromatosis type 1","About half of cases occur in patients with neurofibromatosis type 1, in whom they frequently develop from a pre-existing plexiform neurofibroma","In neurofibromatosis type 1, new or worsening pain, rapid enlargement, or a neurological deficit in a known neurofibroma should raise strong suspicion of malignant transformation","Commonly involves major nerves and nerve plexuses of the trunk and proximal limbs, and can produce motor or sensory deficits along the affected nerve","Molecularly characterised by loss of NF1 and CDKN2A together with inactivation of the PRC2 complex (loss of SUZ12 or EED), reflected in loss of H3K27me3 staining","S100 and SOX10 are often only focally positive, and loss of H3K27 trimethylation on immunohistochemistry aids the diagnosis","Wide surgical excision offers the best chance of control, with radiotherapy for local control and chemotherapy in selected high-grade or advanced cases","Functional imaging can help identify malignant transformation within a plexiform neurofibroma and guide biopsy of the most suspicious area"],"pathophysiology":"Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour is an aggressive sarcoma showing nerve sheath (usually Schwann cell) differentiation. In neurofibromatosis type 1 it typically arises through malignant progression of a plexiform neurofibroma, driven by biallelic loss of the NF1 tumour-suppressor gene, subsequent loss of CDKN2A, and inactivation of the polycomb repressive complex 2 (through SUZ12 or EED mutation). Loss of PRC2 function results in global loss of histone H3 lysine-27 trimethylation, deregulating gene expression and promoting a high-grade phenotype. Sporadic and radiation-associated tumours accumulate analogous alterations, and the resulting cells display variable, often incomplete, Schwannian marker expression.","additionalDifferentials":["Cellular or atypical neurofibroma and plexiform neurofibroma","Synovial sarcoma","Fibrosarcoma and other spindle-cell sarcomas","Leiomyosarcoma","Melanoma (including spindle-cell/desmoplastic types)","Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the primary lesion and involved nerve to define extent and identify features suggesting malignancy","Whole-body functional imaging to detect malignant transformation within plexiform neurofibromas and stage disease","Chest CT for pulmonary metastases","Image-guided biopsy targeting the most metabolically active or suspicious region, with careful tract planning","Immunohistochemistry including S100, SOX10, and H3K27me3 (loss supports the diagnosis)","Assessment for neurofibromatosis type 1 features and, where relevant, genetic evaluation"],"redFlags":["New, persistent, or worsening pain in a known neurofibroma in a patient with neurofibromatosis type 1","Rapid enlargement of a previously stable nerve-associated mass","New or progressive motor or sensory deficit referable to an involved nerve","A deep-seated enlarging mass along a major nerve or plexus","New respiratory symptoms suggesting pulmonary metastases"],"prognosis":"Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour is an aggressive sarcoma with substantial recurrence and metastatic risk; prognosis depends on tumour size, grade, resectability, and setting, and neurofibromatosis type 1-associated and large or incompletely resected tumours generally carry a worse outcome.","pitfalls":["Attributing new pain or growth in a neurofibromatosis type 1 patient to a benign neurofibroma and delaying investigation","Relying on S100 positivity, which is often only focal, without additional markers such as loss of H3K27me3","Sampling a benign area of a large plexiform lesion and missing the malignant component","Underestimating the extent of nerve involvement and achieving inadequate margins","Failing to consider prior radiotherapy as a predisposing factor in sporadic-appearing tumours"],"management":["Wide surgical resection to achieve histologically negative margins is the mainstay of curative treatment, undertaken at a specialist sarcoma centre","Adjuvant radiotherapy for local control, particularly with close margins or high-grade tumours, recognising relative radioresistance","Anthracycline- and ifosfamide-based chemotherapy for high-grade, unresectable or metastatic disease, with palliative intent in advanced cases","Complete staging and management of metastatic disease, especially pulmonary metastases, with metastasectomy considered for limited lung deposits","Long-term surveillance for local recurrence and metastasis with clinical review and cross-sectional imaging","Manage within a multidisciplinary sarcoma team spanning surgical, medical and clinical oncology","In neurofibromatosis type 1, monitor plexiform neurofibromas for transformation and provide genetic counselling and family assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"malignant_peripheral_nerve_sheath_tumour":{"name":"malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour","system":"musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neurofibrosarcoma","malignant schwannoma","neurogenic sarcoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Roughly half of cases arise in neurofibromatosis type 1, typically from malignant transformation of a pre-existing plexiform neurofibroma; the remainder are sporadic or radiation-associated.","A rapidly enlarging, newly painful mass in a known NF1 plexiform neurofibroma is the classic warning of transformation and warrants urgent imaging and biopsy.","FDG PET-CT is valuable for distinguishing malignant transformation from a benign neurofibroma and for directing biopsy to the most metabolically active focus.","Loss of nuclear H3K27me3 on immunohistochemistry, reflecting PRC2 (SUZ12/EED) inactivation, is a useful diagnostic adjunct, though not entirely specific.","S100 and SOX10 staining is typically patchy or lost, unlike the diffuse positivity seen in benign nerve sheath tumours.","Wide surgical resection with negative margins is the cornerstone of treatment; the tumour is relatively resistant to radiotherapy and chemotherapy.","Radiation-associated tumours may arise years to decades after therapeutic irradiation and carry a particularly poor outcome.","The lung is the dominant site of distant metastasis, so staging chest imaging is mandatory."],"pathophysiology":"The tumour originates from Schwann cell or perineurial lineage of the peripheral nerve. In neurofibromatosis type 1, biallelic loss of the NF1 tumour suppressor drives dysregulated RAS-MAPK signalling within a plexiform neurofibroma, and stepwise acquisition of further hits, notably inactivation of CDKN2A and of the polycomb repressive complex 2 components SUZ12 or EED, precipitates high-grade transformation. Loss of PRC2 function causes global loss of the repressive histone mark H3K27me3, promoting aberrant transcription and a spindle-cell sarcoma with fascicular architecture, nuclear pleomorphism and brisk mitotic activity. Sporadic and post-irradiation tumours converge on similar molecular pathways without a germline NF1 defect.","additionalDifferentials":["benign neurofibroma or plexiform neurofibroma","cellular or ancient schwannoma","synovial sarcoma","fibrosarcoma and myxofibrosarcoma","leiomyosarcoma","spindle cell melanoma","dedifferentiated liposarcoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["contrast-enhanced MRI of the primary site to assess nerve of origin and local extent","FDG PET-CT to identify malignant transformation and guide biopsy","image-guided core or open incisional biopsy of the most active focus","immunohistochemistry including S100, SOX10 and H3K27me3","staging CT of the thorax for pulmonary metastases","molecular assessment for NF1, CDKN2A and PRC2 alterations","clinical and genetic assessment for underlying neurofibromatosis type 1"],"redFlags":["rapid enlargement of a previously stable neurofibroma","new persistent or nocturnal pain within a known nerve sheath lesion","progressive neurological deficit referable to the involved nerve","hard, fixed mass with rapid growth over weeks"],"prognosis":"Outcome is guarded, with high rates of local recurrence and distant metastasis and a substantial risk of death; large size, high grade, truncal location, incomplete resection and an NF1 background all worsen survival.","pitfalls":["mistaking malignant transformation for a benign neurofibroma and delaying biopsy","sampling error from a non-representative biopsy of a heterogeneous mass","assuming radiotherapy or chemotherapy will substitute for inadequate surgical margins","overlooking a second occult tumour or NF1 diagnosis in a young patient","interpreting H3K27me3 loss in isolation as definitive without correlating morphology"],"management":["Wide en-bloc surgical excision with histologically negative margins is the cornerstone of treatment, ideally at a specialist sarcoma unit","Adjuvant radiotherapy to improve local control for large, high-grade or marginally resected tumours, despite limited radiosensitivity","Anthracycline- and ifosfamide-based chemotherapy reserved for high-grade, metastatic or unresectable disease given only modest chemosensitivity","Staging computed tomography of the thorax and management of pulmonary metastases, including metastasectomy for oligometastatic disease","Structured post-treatment surveillance with imaging for local recurrence and distant spread","Coordinated care through a multidisciplinary sarcoma team spanning surgery, radiology, pathology and oncology","Assess for neurofibromatosis type 1, with surveillance of plexiform neurofibromas and genetic counselling where a germline NF1 defect is present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","ESMO Clinical Practice Guidelines for soft tissue sarcoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"malignant_pleural_effusion":{"name":"malignant pleural effusion","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPE","metastatic pleural effusion"],"chapter":null,"source":"BTS Pleural Disease Guideline","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A malignant pleural effusion indicates disseminated or advanced malignancy and generally portends a poor prognosis.","Lung and breast carcinoma account for the majority; lymphoma, ovarian and gastric cancers and mesothelioma are other important causes, and a proportion arise from an unknown primary.","Effusions are typically exudative by Light's criteria and are frequently blood-stained; a massive effusion with mediastinal shift towards the effusion suggests a central obstructing tumour or mesothelioma.","Low pleural fluid pH and glucose correlate with higher tumour burden, poorer pleurodesis success and shorter survival.","Pleural fluid cytology confirms malignancy in only around 60 percent of cases, with sensitivity higher in adenocarcinoma and low in mesothelioma.","When cytology is negative, image-guided or thoracoscopic pleural biopsy provides tissue for diagnosis and molecular characterisation.","Definitive management aims at symptom control through therapeutic aspiration, talc pleurodesis or an indwelling pleural catheter, guided by patient preference and the presence of trapped lung.","Prognostic scores such as LENT and PROMISE help select the least burdensome yet most durable management strategy."],"pathophysiology":"Malignant effusions form when tumour involves the pleura, most often through haematogenous or lymphatic metastasis to the parietal and visceral pleura or by direct invasion. Pleural tumour increases capillary and mesothelial permeability, elaborates vascular endothelial growth factor and other mediators, and obstructs the lymphatic stomata and mediastinal lymphatics that normally drain the pleural space. The combination of increased fluid formation and impaired lymphatic reabsorption produces a progressive exudate. Extensive visceral pleural tumour or fibrosis may prevent the lung re-expanding after drainage (trapped lung), which strongly influences the choice between pleurodesis and an indwelling catheter.","additionalDifferentials":["Parapneumonic effusion or empyema","Tuberculous pleuritis","Effusion secondary to pulmonary embolism","Transudative effusion from heart failure or hepatic hydrothorax","Benign asbestos-related pleural effusion","Chylothorax (for example from lymphoma)","Paramalignant effusion from lymphatic obstruction or post-obstructive pneumonia rather than direct pleural involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic pleural aspiration with cytology, protein, LDH, pH and glucose","Contrast-enhanced CT of the thorax with abdomen and pelvis for staging","Thoracic ultrasound to assess pleural nodularity, septations and guide sampling","Image-guided or local anaesthetic thoracoscopic pleural biopsy","Immunohistochemistry and molecular studies on pleural tissue or fluid","Assessment of lung re-expansion after drainage to detect trapped lung","Prognostic scoring (LENT or PROMISE) alongside performance status assessment"],"redFlags":["Rapid re-accumulation with breathlessness at rest","Massive effusion with contralateral mediastinal shift","Ipsilateral or absent mediastinal shift suggesting trapped lung or central airway obstruction","Features of superior vena cava obstruction","Signs of pleural infection complicating an indwelling catheter"],"prognosis":"Survival is generally measured in months and varies markedly with tumour type and fluid characteristics, being worse with low pleural fluid pH or glucose and poor performance status; mesothelioma and lung primaries carry the poorest outlook, whereas breast cancer and haematological malignancies may respond to systemic therapy.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single negative cytology to exclude malignancy.","Attempting pleurodesis in a trapped lung, which is likely to fail.","Draining a large effusion too rapidly and precipitating re-expansion pulmonary oedema.","Mislabelling a paramalignant effusion, which may respond to systemic therapy, as directly malignant.","Not offering an indwelling pleural catheter to patients with limited prognosis or a non-expandable lung."],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying malignancy with systemic chemotherapy, targeted or hormonal therapy according to tumour type, which may control fluid reaccumulation","Therapeutic pleural aspiration to relieve breathlessness, with the volume drained guided by symptoms","Chemical pleurodesis, typically with talc, for recurrent effusion when the lung re-expands fully after drainage","Indwelling pleural catheter for trapped lung, failed pleurodesis or patient preference, allowing ambulatory drainage","Assess lung re-expansion after drainage to choose between pleurodesis and an indwelling catheter","Symptomatic relief of breathlessness with opioids and other measures alongside early palliative care involvement","Manage through a multidisciplinary team of respiratory medicine, oncology and palliative care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["BTS Pleural Disease Guideline","Light's Pleural Diseases","ERS/EACTS Statement on the Management of Malignant Pleural Effusions"],"crossLinks":[]},"malignant_pleural_mesothelioma":{"name":"Malignant pleural mesothelioma","system":"Respiratory / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPM","diffuse malignant mesothelioma","pleural mesothelioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Aggressive malignancy arising from the mesothelial lining of the pleura, strongly linked to prior asbestos exposure with a latency often of several decades","Typically presents with progressive breathlessness, a unilateral pleural effusion, and non-pleuritic chest wall pain","Imaging shows a nodular or rind-like pleural thickening that encases the lung, frequently with a contracted hemithorax","Diagnosis requires tissue, ideally by image-guided or thoracoscopic pleural biopsy, since cytology alone is often insufficient","Epithelioid, sarcomatoid, and biphasic subtypes differ in behaviour, with sarcomatoid carrying the worst outlook","Unlike benign pleural plaques it is diffuse, invasive, and carries a poor prognosis","Management is usually multimodality, combining systemic therapy with selected surgery and radiotherapy, alongside effusion control and palliative care","Recognised as an industrial disease in many jurisdictions, so exposure documentation and compensation advice are important"],"pathophysiology":"Asbestos fibres retained in the pleura, particularly amphibole types, cause chronic inflammation, oxidative injury, and genetic damage to mesothelial cells over many years. Loss of tumour-suppressor function and accumulated somatic mutations drive malignant transformation of the mesothelium. The tumour then spreads diffusely along the pleural surfaces, forming a thickened rind that encases the lung, invades the chest wall, diaphragm, and mediastinal structures, and commonly produces a recurrent exudative and often bloody effusion. Progressive encasement restricts lung expansion and causes the characteristic contracted hemithorax.","additionalDifferentials":["Metastatic adenocarcinoma involving the pleura","Benign asbestos-related pleural plaques and diffuse pleural thickening","Solitary fibrous tumour of the pleura","Fibrothorax following empyema or haemothorax","Tuberculous or other chronic infective pleuritis","Thymic or other mediastinal tumour extending to the pleura"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the thorax to characterise pleural thickening and assess extent","Thoracoscopic or image-guided pleural biopsy with immunohistochemistry for definitive diagnosis","Pleural fluid analysis, recognising that cytology alone is often inconclusive","PET-CT to assess disease extent and guide staging","MRI where chest wall or diaphragmatic invasion needs clarification"],"redFlags":["Progressive breathlessness with a large or rapidly reaccumulating unilateral effusion","Persistent chest wall pain, especially if boring or radiating along an intercostal distribution","A shrinking hemithorax with nodular circumferential pleural thickening","Weight loss, sweats, and constitutional decline","New palpable chest wall mass or biopsy-tract seeding"],"prognosis":"Overall prognosis is poor, with survival usually measured in months to a small number of years; the epithelioid subtype and earlier-stage, resectable disease fare relatively better, whereas sarcomatoid tumours carry the worst outlook.","pitfalls":["Assuming benign pleural plaques exclude mesothelioma in a patient with asbestos exposure","Relying on pleural fluid cytology alone and missing the diagnosis","Delaying tissue diagnosis and specialist referral in a patient with an unexplained persistent effusion","Overlooking the potential for tumour seeding along biopsy or drain tracts","Failing to document occupational exposure for medico-legal and compensation purposes"],"management":["First-line systemic therapy with platinum plus pemetrexed chemotherapy, or combination immunotherapy with nivolumab and ipilimumab, particularly for non-epithelioid disease","Radical surgery such as pleurectomy-decortication within a multimodality approach for selected fit patients with early epithelioid tumours","Radiotherapy for palliation of chest wall pain or as part of multimodality treatment","Control of recurrent pleural effusion by talc pleurodesis or an indwelling pleural catheter","Attention to symptom control, particularly pain and breathlessness, with early palliative care input","Document asbestos exposure and advise on compensation, industrial injury benefit and statutory reporting","Manage through a specialist mesothelioma multidisciplinary team and consider enrolment in clinical trials"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Shields' General Thoracic Surgery","Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"malignant_rhabdoid_tumor":{"name":"Malignant rhabdoid tumour (SMARCB1)","system":"Oncology / Sarcoma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MRT","malignant rhabdoid tumor","extrarenal rhabdoid tumour","SMARCB1-deficient tumour","INI1-deficient tumour","atypical teratoid/rhabdoid tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A highly aggressive malignancy of infancy and early childhood, occurring in the kidney, central nervous system (as atypical teratoid/rhabdoid tumour), and soft tissue","Named for the characteristic rhabdoid cells with eccentric nuclei, prominent nucleoli, and eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions","Defined by loss of function of the SMARCB1 (INI1) gene, part of the SWI/SNF chromatin-remodelling complex, with rare cases showing SMARCA4 loss","Loss of nuclear INI1 (SMARCB1) expression on immunohistochemistry is the diagnostic hallmark","May occur as part of the rhabdoid tumour predisposition syndrome, so germline SMARCB1 testing and genetic counselling should be considered, particularly in the very young or with multifocal disease","Frequently presents with disseminated disease, and central nervous system involvement should be actively sought","Treatment involves intensive multimodality therapy combining surgery, multi-agent chemotherapy, and radiotherapy, though outcomes remain poor","Staging requires cross-sectional imaging of the primary, chest CT, whole-body assessment, and neuraxis imaging"],"pathophysiology":"Malignant rhabdoid tumour is defined by biallelic inactivation of the SMARCB1 (INI1) tumour-suppressor gene, a core subunit of the SWI/SNF (BAF) chromatin-remodelling complex; a small subset instead show SMARCA4 loss. Loss of this subunit disrupts the complex's control of gene expression, impairing differentiation and permitting unchecked proliferation despite an otherwise remarkably simple, stable genome. This single epigenetic driver, often present in the germline in the predisposition syndrome, explains why the tumour arises in very young children and its shared biology across renal, central nervous system, and soft-tissue sites. Loss of nuclear INI1 staining directly reflects the underlying genetic defect.","additionalDifferentials":["Other small round cell and undifferentiated tumours of childhood","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Wilms tumour and other paediatric renal tumours","Neuroblastoma","Epithelioid sarcoma (also INI1-deficient)","Medulloblastoma and other embryonal central nervous system tumours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI or CT of the primary site to define extent","Neuraxis MRI to detect synchronous central nervous system disease","Chest CT for pulmonary metastases","Whole-body functional imaging and, where relevant, cerebrospinal fluid assessment for staging","Biopsy with immunohistochemistry demonstrating loss of nuclear INI1 (SMARCB1)","Germline SMARCB1 (and, if indicated, SMARCA4) testing with genetic counselling"],"redFlags":["A rapidly growing mass in an infant or young child","Neurological signs suggesting central nervous system involvement","Multifocal or disseminated disease at presentation","A family history suggesting a rhabdoid tumour predisposition syndrome","Rapid clinical deterioration reflecting aggressive tumour biology"],"prognosis":"Malignant rhabdoid tumour carries a poor prognosis, particularly in very young children and those with metastatic, central nervous system, or germline-associated disease; despite intensive multimodality treatment, survival remains limited, though outcomes are somewhat better in older children with localised, completely resected tumours.","pitfalls":["Failing to test for INI1 (SMARCB1) loss when a rhabdoid or undifferentiated paediatric tumour is encountered","Overlooking synchronous central nervous system disease by omitting neuraxis imaging","Not arranging germline testing and genetic counselling for a possible predisposition syndrome","Confusing the tumour with other small round cell tumours without appropriate immunohistochemistry","Underestimating the aggressive course and delaying intensive multimodality therapy"],"management":["Intensive multimodality treatment combining maximal safe surgical resection, multi-agent chemotherapy and radiotherapy","Multi-agent chemotherapy regimens, sometimes incorporating high-dose chemotherapy with autologous stem-cell rescue, and intrathecal therapy where there is central nervous system involvement","Site-directed radiotherapy, balancing local control against toxicity in very young children","Full staging including neuraxis imaging and cerebrospinal fluid assessment, with treatment of disseminated and central nervous system disease","Comprehensive paediatric supportive care coordinated by a paediatric oncology team","Germline SMARCB1 testing, and SMARCA4 where indicated, with genetic counselling and family screening for the rhabdoid tumour predisposition syndrome","Consider enrolment in clinical trials given the poor prognosis with conventional therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"malignant_spinal_cord_compression":{"name":"malignant spinal cord compression","system":"Neurological / Musculoskeletal (spine)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MSCC","metastatic spinal cord compression","malignant epidural spinal cord compression"],"chapter":null,"source":"NICE guideline on metastatic spinal cord compression","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Progressive or unremitting back pain, often thoracic, band-like or worse on lying, coughing or straining, frequently precedes neurological signs by days to weeks and should trigger urgent whole-spine imaging.","The thoracic spine is the commonest site; multiple non-contiguous levels are present in up to a third of patients, so imaging must cover the entire spine, not just the symptomatic region.","Breast, lung, prostate, myeloma and renal malignancies account for most cases; MSCC is the presenting feature of cancer in a substantial minority.","Whole-spine MRI is the investigation of choice and should be obtained within 24 hours of suspicion (sooner if neurology is progressing).","Start high-dose corticosteroid (dexamethasone) with gastroprotection immediately on suspicion unless contraindicated—but withhold if untreated lymphoma is likely and tissue is needed, as steroids can lyse tumour and obscure diagnosis.","Ambulatory and continent status at presentation is the strongest predictor of functional outcome, underscoring the value of treating before deficits are established.","Definitive treatment is decompressive surgery (favoured for good performance status, single-level disease, spinal instability or radioresistant tumour) or radiotherapy; the choice depends on histology, prognosis and stability.","Nurse the patient flat with neutral spine alignment and log-roll until spinal stability is confirmed, and provide venous thromboembolism prophylaxis and bladder care."],"pathophysiology":"Metastatic tumour, most often within a vertebral body, expands into the epidural space and compresses the thecal sac and cord (or cauda equina below the conus). Direct mechanical pressure is compounded by venous plexus obstruction causing vasogenic cord oedema, and by arterial compromise producing ischaemia and, ultimately, infarction. Vertebral collapse or retropulsed bone or tumour fragments may add an element of instability and further canal compromise. The progression from reversible oedema to irreversible infarction explains why outcome is time-critical and why neurological deficit, once fixed, seldom recovers.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign disc prolapse or degenerative canal stenosis","Epidural abscess or discitis","Spinal epidural haematoma","Leptomeningeal metastasis or intramedullary metastasis","Radiation myelopathy","Transverse myelitis or other inflammatory myelopathy","Pathological vertebral fracture without cord compression"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent whole-spine MRI with and without contrast","CT of the spine to assess bony stability and for surgical planning","CT staging of chest, abdomen and pelvis to identify primary and disease extent","Prostate-specific antigen and myeloma screen where primary is unknown","Image-guided or surgical biopsy when the diagnosis of malignancy is not established","Bloods including calcium, renal and liver function","Bone scintigraphy or PET for disease mapping"],"redFlags":["New or progressive limb weakness or gait disturbance","Sensory level or saddle anaesthesia","Bladder or bowel dysfunction (a late and ominous sign)","Severe, progressive or nocturnal thoracic spinal pain","Rapidly evolving neurological deficit over hours"],"prognosis":"Neurological outcome hinges on the deficit at the time treatment starts: patients ambulant at presentation usually remain so, whereas established paraplegia rarely reverses. Overall survival is governed by the underlying malignancy and its treatability rather than the compression itself.","pitfalls":["Waiting for neurological signs before imaging in a cancer patient with new back pain","Imaging only the symptomatic level and missing higher or lower compressions","Giving corticosteroids before biopsy when lymphoma is the likely cause","Overlooking spinal instability and mobilising the patient prematurely","Attributing bladder dysfunction to opioids or immobility rather than to cord or cauda equina compromise"],"management":["Start high-dose corticosteroid such as dexamethasone with gastroprotection immediately on suspicion, withholding it if untreated lymphoma is likely and diagnostic tissue is required","Obtain urgent whole-spine magnetic resonance imaging to confirm the diagnosis and define all levels of compression","Surgical decompression and stabilisation for suitable patients, particularly with spinal instability, bony compression or a single level, followed by radiotherapy","Radiotherapy for radiosensitive tumours or patients unfit for surgery, aiming to preserve neurological function","Systemic anticancer therapy for chemosensitive or hormone-sensitive malignancies such as myeloma, lymphoma, prostate and breast cancer","Supportive care with analgesia, venous thromboembolism prophylaxis, bladder and bowel management and pressure-area care","Treat within a coordinated multidisciplinary team with early rehabilitation, and discuss prognosis, which depends on neurological status at presentation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["NICE clinical guideline on the diagnosis and management of metastatic spinal cord compression","DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer: Principles and Practice of Oncology","Oxford Handbook of Oncology"],"crossLinks":[]},"mallet_finger":{"name":"Mallet finger","system":"Musculoskeletal / Hand & Peripheral nerve","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["baseball finger","drop finger","terminal extensor tendon injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as loss of active extension at the distal interphalangeal joint with a characteristic dropped, flexed fingertip","Typically caused by sudden forced flexion of an actively extended fingertip, such as a ball striking the end of the digit","The injury may be purely tendinous (soft-tissue avulsion of the terminal extensor tendon) or bony (a dorsal avulsion fracture of the distal phalanx base)","A large bony fragment associated with volar subluxation of the distal phalanx renders the joint unstable and warrants surgical assessment","A chronic untreated mallet can lead to a secondary swan-neck deformity as extensor force is transmitted proximally to hyperextend the proximal interphalangeal joint","Lateral radiographs are essential to define the size of any fragment and confirm joint congruity","Generic management is continuous extension splinting of the distal interphalangeal joint for several weeks in most closed injuries, with the proximal interphalangeal joint left free","Surgery is reserved for a large displaced fragment, joint subluxation or an open injury"],"pathophysiology":"A mallet finger arises from disruption of the terminal extensor tendon at its insertion on the dorsal base of the distal phalanx, either through a tendinous avulsion or through a dorsal avulsion fracture. Loss of active extensor input leaves the flexor digitorum profundus unopposed, so the distal phalanx rests in flexion. Over time the disrupted balance of the extensor mechanism can shift force proximally through the central slip and lateral bands, hyperextending the proximal interphalangeal joint and producing a secondary swan-neck posture.","additionalDifferentials":["Distal phalanx or distal interphalangeal joint fracture-dislocation","Flexor digitorum profundus avulsion (jersey finger), which causes the opposite deficit","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis of the distal interphalangeal joint","Distal interphalangeal osteoarthritis with a mallet-like posture","A more proximal extensor mechanism or sagittal band injury","Open laceration of the terminal tendon"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment documenting the extension lag at the distal interphalangeal joint","Lateral plain radiograph to identify a bony mallet, quantify fragment size and detect volar subluxation","Assessment of overall joint congruity and stability","Examination of the overlying skin to exclude an open injury or skin loss","Ultrasound occasionally to evaluate the terminal tendon when imaging is equivocal"],"redFlags":["Volar subluxation of the distal phalanx indicating an unstable joint","An open injury or threatened skin necrosis beneath the splint","An established fixed deformity or secondary swan-neck","Signs of joint infection"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are generally good with early continuous splinting; delayed presentation or interrupted splinting commonly leaves a small residual extension lag that is usually well tolerated functionally.","pitfalls":["Inadequate or interrupted splinting, since allowing the joint to flex even briefly resets the healing process","Skin maceration or pressure necrosis from an ill-fitting or overtightened splint","Missing volar subluxation that requires surgical rather than splint treatment","Overlooking the development of a secondary swan-neck deformity"],"management":["Continuous splinting of the distal interphalangeal joint in extension for an uninterrupted period, leaving the proximal interphalangeal joint free, for closed tendinous and most bony injuries","Emphasise uninterrupted splint wear, since any episode of flexion during healing restarts the process, supported by hand therapy education","Surgical fixation, such as with a Kirschner wire, for a large avulsion fragment with volar subluxation, an unstable joint or an open injury","Wound care and soft-tissue cover for open injuries with skin loss","Graduated mobilisation and splint weaning under hand therapy guidance after the immobilisation period","For chronic deformity or secondary swan-neck, consider surgical reconstruction or arthrodesis","Refer to hand surgery when the joint is unstable, the fragment large, or presentation is delayed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"mallet_toe":{"name":"Mallet toe","system":"Musculoskeletal / Foot & Ankle","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mallet toe deformity","DIP flexion deformity of the lesser toe"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mallet toe is an isolated flexion deformity of the distal interphalangeal joint, with the metatarsophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints held in relatively neutral positions","This isolated distal deformity is the distinguishing feature against hammer toe (proximal interphalangeal flexion) and claw toe (metatarsophalangeal hyperextension plus flexion of both interphalangeal joints)","It most often affects the longest lesser toe, typically the second, where the toe tip is repeatedly buckled against the shoe","A painful callosity or corn develops at the tip of the toe or under the nail, and nail deformity may accompany chronic pressure","Repetitive footwear pressure and minor trauma are common causes, and contracture of the long flexor tendon perpetuates the deformity","Flexibility should be tested, since a passively correctable deformity may respond to flexor tenotomy while a fixed one needs joint resection or fusion","Conservative care includes deep-toe-box footwear, toe caps or crest pads to relieve tip pressure, and callus management","Surgical treatment uses flexor tenotomy for flexible deformity and distal interphalangeal arthrodesis or resection for fixed deformity"],"pathophysiology":"Mallet toe is a flexion deformity confined to the distal interphalangeal joint, produced by an imbalance favouring the long flexor tendon across that joint while the more proximal joints remain in neutral alignment. It is commonly precipitated by chronic pressure from footwear that is too short or too shallow, so that the longest toe is repeatedly forced into flexion, and by minor trauma. Over time the flexor tendon and joint capsule contract, converting an initially flexible deformity into a fixed one. The buckled distal phalanx concentrates load at the toe tip, producing a painful end-of-toe corn or callosity, subungual pressure changes, and occasionally ulceration, particularly where sensation or perfusion is impaired. Because the deformity is distal and isolated, associated metatarsophalangeal subluxation and generalised clawing are typically absent.","additionalDifferentials":["Hammer toe with proximal interphalangeal flexion","Claw toe with combined metatarsophalangeal and interphalangeal deformity","Curly toe deformity","Subungual exostosis or nail pathology causing tip pain","Distal phalanx fracture or post-traumatic deformity","Inflammatory arthritis affecting the distal interphalangeal joint"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination localising the deformity to the distal interphalangeal joint and testing its flexibility","Weight-bearing foot radiographs to confirm the level of deformity and exclude bony lesions","Inspection of the toe tip and nail for callosity, corn, or ulceration","Assessment of the other toes and forefoot to exclude a wider deformity pattern","Vascular and neurological assessment of the toe before surgery","Inflammatory markers or serology only if inflammatory arthritis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Ulceration or infection at the toe tip, particularly in diabetes or peripheral vascular disease","Signs of digital ischaemia that would jeopardise healing","Multiple or symmetrical toe deformities suggesting an underlying neuromuscular or inflammatory disorder","A firm, growing subungual lesion suggesting exostosis or, rarely, tumour"],"prognosis":"Mallet toe generally responds well to simple measures. Flexible deformity is often corrected by flexor tenotomy with reliable relief of tip pain, and fixed deformity does well with distal interphalangeal arthrodesis or resection. Recurrence is uncommon when appropriate footwear is maintained, though tip numbness, mild residual deformity, or persistent nail changes can occur.","pitfalls":["Failing to test flexibility and performing joint resection when a simple flexor tenotomy would suffice","Missing an underlying bony lesion such as subungual exostosis as the cause of tip pain","Overlooking ulceration or ischaemia at the toe tip in the neuropathic or vasculopathic foot","Attributing an isolated distal deformity to a broader claw or hammer toe pattern and over-treating","Neglecting footwear correction so that pressure recurs after treatment"],"management":["Footwear advice with a roomy, deep toe box and protective silicone sleeves or padding to relieve pressure on the buckled toe tip","Regular paring of the tip callosity or corn and attention to associated nail deformity, with podiatry input","Flexor tenotomy for a passively correctable flexible deformity to relieve tip pain","Distal interphalangeal arthrodesis or resection arthroplasty for a fixed deformity","Vascular and neurological assessment before surgery, with protective offloading where sensation or perfusion is impaired to prevent ulceration","Treat any ulceration or secondary infection and reinforce appropriate footwear to prevent recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"mallory_weiss_tear":{"name":"Mallory-Weiss tear","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mallory-Weiss syndrome","gastro-oesophageal mucosal tear","Mallory-Weiss laceration"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A longitudinal mucosal tear near the gastro-oesophageal junction, classically provoked by forceful retching or vomiting","Typically presents as haematemesis following an episode of non-bloody vomiting, retching, coughing, or straining","Alcohol excess, hiatus hernia, and any cause of repeated vomiting (including pregnancy and bulimia) are common predisposing factors","Most tears are single and lie on the gastric side of the junction along the lesser curve","Bleeding stops spontaneously in the majority of patients and the tear heals within days","Endoscopy is diagnostic and allows haemostasis (injection, thermal therapy, clips, or band ligation) when active or high-risk bleeding is present","Must be distinguished from a full-thickness Boerhaave perforation, which is a surgical emergency"],"pathophysiology":"A Mallory-Weiss tear is a mucosal and submucosal laceration at or just below the gastro-oesophageal junction produced by a sudden rise in intra-abdominal and intragastric pressure, most often during forceful retching or vomiting. The abrupt pressure gradient across the junction stretches and splits the mucosa longitudinally, disrupting submucosal vessels and causing bleeding. A hiatus hernia is thought to predispose by accentuating the pressure differential across the herniated segment, and the tear characteristically remains confined to the mucosa, distinguishing it from the transmural rupture of Boerhaave syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Bleeding peptic ulcer","Oesophageal or gastric varices","Erosive oesophagitis or gastritis","Boerhaave syndrome (transmural oesophageal rupture)","Dieulafoy lesion","Upper gastrointestinal malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy to visualise the linear tear and assess for active or recent bleeding","Full blood count to gauge blood loss, with coagulation studies and group-and-save or crossmatch","Assessment for underlying causes such as alcohol use, hiatus hernia, or an eating disorder","Erect chest and abdominal imaging if perforation is suspected clinically","Risk stratification using an accepted upper gastrointestinal bleeding score","Repeat endoscopy if rebleeding occurs"],"redFlags":["Severe or ongoing haematemesis with haemodynamic instability","Features suggesting Boerhaave perforation such as severe chest pain, subcutaneous emphysema, or sepsis","Rebleeding after initial haemostasis","Bleeding in a patient with coagulopathy or portal hypertension"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent for most patients, as bleeding is self-limiting and the mucosal tear heals rapidly; rebleeding is uncommon and is more likely with coagulopathy or portal hypertension, where the prognosis is correspondingly poorer.","pitfalls":["Confusing a Mallory-Weiss tear with the far more dangerous Boerhaave perforation","Failing to identify and address an underlying cause of recurrent vomiting","Overlooking coexisting varices or portal hypertension that raise the bleeding risk","Assuming all tears are benign and not applying endoscopic haemostasis to actively bleeding lesions"],"management":["Resuscitate with intravenous access and fluids, transfuse as required and correct any coagulopathy","Manage conservatively in most patients with antiemetics and acid suppression using a proton pump inhibitor, since bleeding usually stops spontaneously","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy to confirm the tear and achieve haemostasis by adrenaline injection, thermal therapy, clips or band ligation when bleeding is active or high-risk","Risk-stratify with a validated upper gastrointestinal bleeding score to guide admission and timing of endoscopy","Treat the underlying precipitant, addressing alcohol excess, an eating disorder or persistent vomiting","For refractory or recurrent bleeding, repeat endoscopic therapy and escalate to angiographic embolisation or, rarely, surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Yamada's Textbook of Gastroenterology"],"crossLinks":[]},"malnutrition_and_nutritional_assessment":{"name":"Malnutrition and Nutritional Assessment","system":"Gastrointestinal / Nutritional","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Protein-calorie malnutrition","Protein-energy malnutrition","Undernutrition"],"chapter":345,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2615","clinicalPearls":["Malnutrition affects roughly 30-50% of hospitalized patients, with the exact prevalence varying by clinical setting, underlying diagnosis, and the diagnostic criteria applied (Harrison 22e p.2615).","The 2019 GLIM (Global Leadership Initiative on Malnutrition) consensus requires at least one phenotypic criterion (nonvolitional weight loss, low BMI, or reduced muscle mass) PLUS at least one etiologic criterion (reduced food intake/assimilation, or disease burden/inflammation) to diagnose malnutrition (Harrison 22e p.2616).","Severity grading in GLIM is determined by the phenotypic criterion: moderate (stage 1) malnutrition is 5-10% weight loss within 6 months (or 10-20% beyond 6 months), and severe (stage 2) is >10% within 6 months (or >20% beyond) (Harrison 22e p.2616).","Patients at any BMI can be malnourished; overweight or obese individuals may have sarcopenic obesity from muscle loss, and conversely being underweight does not by itself equal malnutrition (Harrison 22e p.2617).","Handgrip strength is among the more clinically useful functional measures because it is easy to perform and helps identify which patients are most likely to benefit from individualized nutritional support (Harrison 22e p.2617).","Patients with apparently adequate oral intake may still be at risk of malnutrition when chronic gastrointestinal disease compromises nutrient assimilation or absorption (Harrison 22e p.2617).","A 5-10% loss of body weight over 6 months is clinically relevant, while >10% loss over the same period is considered severe (Harrison 22e p.2617).","Albumin and prealbumin are negative acute-phase reactants and fall with injury, disease, or inflammation; they have poor sensitivity and specificity for malnutrition, though they retain value for predicting clinical outcomes (Harrison 22e pp.2617-2618).","C-reactive protein is a positive acute-phase reactant useful for confirming inflammation, with the chapter noting supportive thresholds of mild 3.0-9.9 mg/L, moderate 10-50 mg/L, and severe >50 mg/L (Harrison 22e p.2616).","Calf circumference is a practical, valid estimate of skeletal muscle mass that requires less training than mid-upper arm circumference, with ethnicity-specific reference ranges that should be adjusted for BMI extremes (Harrison 22e p.2618).","When inflammation is severe, nutrition care tends to be supportive only; at low-to-moderate inflammation, nutrition care may be therapeutic and improve clinical outcomes (Harrison 22e pp.2615-2616).","Calf circumference, BMI, and other GLIM parameters make malnutrition diagnosis feasible even in the outpatient/ambulatory setting where dietitian access may be limited (Harrison 22e p.2617)."],"pathophysiology":"Malnutrition arises from an imbalance between nutrient intake/assimilation and metabolic demand, and acute or chronic inflammation is now recognized as central to its pathophysiology. Inflammatory mediators raise resting energy expenditure, promote anabolic resistance and muscle catabolism, and cause anorexia that further reduces intake. Severe acute inflammation (e.g., critical illness, sepsis, burns, trauma, closed head injury) may be superimposed on a mild-to-moderate chronic inflammatory state (e.g., malignancy, COPD, congestive heart failure, chronic renal disease, chronic GI disease). The resulting deficits impair wound healing, immune function, and organ function. Because albumin and prealbumin behave as negative acute-phase reactants, the inflammatory milieu also distorts these traditional nutrition biomarkers (Harrison 22e pp.2615-2617).","additionalDifferentials":["Sarcopenia (progressive generalized loss of skeletal muscle mass and strength; screened with SARC-F)","Sarcopenic obesity","Cachexia of chronic disease (malignancy, organ failure)","Frailty syndrome","Dehydration / fluid shifts mimicking weight change","Micronutrient deficiency states"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Edema or fluid overload masking true weight loss (dry weight estimation may be needed)","Hypoalbuminemia from systemic inflammation, hepatic or renal insufficiency, protein-losing enteropathy, or corticosteroid use rather than nutrition itself","Low BUN/creatinine from renal or hepatic disease falsely suggesting reduced muscle mass","Hypocholesterolemia related to acute illness rather than malnutrition per se"],"additionalInvestigations":["Validated risk-screening tools: Malnutrition Screening Tool, Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool (MUST), Nutritional Risk Screening 2002, Mini Nutritional Assessment, Subjective Global Assessment","Anthropometrics: serial body weight (without shoes/heavy garments), BMI, calf circumference, mid-upper arm circumference; height estimated by arm span or knee-height caliper when needed","Body composition: bioelectrical impedance analysis (BIA, portable), dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry (DXA), air displacement plethysmography, and opportunistic CT/MRI muscle measurement","Laboratory: complete blood count with differential (screen for iron, B12, folate, vitamin C deficiencies), C-reactive protein for inflammation, specific micronutrient levels","Indirect calorimetry for resting energy expenditure; 24-h urine urea nitrogen and nitrogen balance to gauge catabolism and protein adequacy (normal renal/hepatic function required)","Functional tests: handgrip strength, Sit-to-Stand test, 6-Meter Walk Test, Clinical Frailty Scale, SARC-F","Nutrition-focused physical examination for edema, subcutaneous fat and muscle loss; diet history including oral supplements, enteral and parenteral intake"],"redFlags":["Nonvolitional weight loss >10% within 6 months (or >20% beyond 6 months) indicating severe malnutrition","BMI <18.5 kg/m2 in adults <70 years (or <20 if >=70 years) indicating severe malnutrition by GLIM","Severe acute inflammatory state with negative nitrogen balance and elevated resting energy expenditure","Low handgrip strength predicting poorer clinical outcomes","Hypocholesterolemia (<120 mg/dL) developing during hospitalization, linked to worse outcomes","Reduced food intake/assimilation (<=50% of energy requirement for >1 week, any reduction >2 weeks, or chronic GI condition impairing absorption)"],"prognosis":"Malnutrition is associated with poor wound healing, compromised immunity, and impaired organ function, leading to longer hospital stays, more readmissions, higher mortality, and increased health care costs. Severe inflammation may limit nutritional interventions to a supportive rather than therapeutic role, and the associated malnutrition can diminish the effectiveness of medical therapies. Robust evidence indicates that a multipronged approach to malnutrition intervention reduces mortality and holds promise for improving quality of life, so timely diagnosis and treatment are important (Harrison 22e pp.2615, 2618).","pitfalls":["Relying on albumin or prealbumin to diagnose malnutrition or judge response to nutrition therapy; they reflect inflammation, hepatic/renal status, hydration, and other factors and have poor specificity (Harrison 22e pp.2617-2618).","Assuming a normal or high BMI excludes malnutrition; sarcopenic obesity and muscle loss can occur at any BMI (Harrison 22e p.2617).","Overlooking edema, which can mask true weight loss unless dry weight is estimated (Harrison 22e p.2617).","Using BUN/creatinine, total lymphocyte count, cholesterol, creatinine-height index, cytokines, ECG, recall-antigen skin testing, or urine 3-methylhistidine as malnutrition indicators; the chapter lists these as NOT recommended for diagnosis (Harrison 22e pp.2618-2619).","Comparing malnutrition prevalence across studies without accounting for differences between diagnostic tools, which yield incomparable rates (Harrison 22e p.2616).","Failing to recognize prolonged pre-admission decreases in intake when an acute event is superimposed on chronic disease (Harrison 22e p.2617)."],"management":["Screen with a validated tool such as the Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool and diagnose using GLIM phenotypic and aetiologic criteria","Deliver an individualised plan prioritising the oral route with dietary counselling, food fortification and oral nutritional supplements","Provide enteral tube feeding when oral intake is inadequate and the gut functions, reserving parenteral nutrition for an unusable gastrointestinal tract","Identify patients at risk of refeeding syndrome, reintroduce nutrition cautiously with thiamine and replace phosphate, potassium and magnesium","Correct micronutrient and vitamin deficiencies including iron, vitamin B12, folate, vitamin C and vitamin D","Treat the underlying disease and inflammatory burden, recognising that severe inflammation limits the response to nutritional support","Monitor weight, dietary intake, biochemistry and functional measures such as handgrip strength to gauge response","Coordinate dietitian-led multidisciplinary care, involving speech and language therapy where dysphagia contributes"],"severityClassification":"GLIM severity grading by phenotypic criteria. Stage 1 (moderate): weight loss 5-10% within 6 months or 10-20% beyond 6 months; BMI <20 (<70 years) or <22 (>=70 years); mild-to-moderate muscle mass deficit. Stage 2 (severe): weight loss >10% within 6 months or >20% beyond 6 months; BMI <18.5 (<70 years) or <20 (>=70 years); severe muscle mass deficit. WHO/NIH BMI categories: <18.5 underweight, 18.5-24.9 desirable, 25.0-29.9 overweight, >=30 obese (Harrison 22e pp.2616-2617).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2615","Harrison 22e p.2616","Harrison 22e p.2617","Harrison 22e p.2618","Harrison 22e p.2619"],"crossLinks":[]},"malonic_aciduria":{"name":"Malonic aciduria (malonyl-CoA decarboxylase deficiency)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MLYCD deficiency","malonyl-CoA decarboxylase deficiency","malonic acidaemia","combined malonic and methylmalonic aciduria (distinct entity)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare organic aciduria caused by deficiency of malonyl-CoA decarboxylase (MLYCD), which converts malonyl-CoA to acetyl-CoA and regulates fatty acid synthesis and oxidation","Presents in infancy or childhood with developmental delay, hypotonia, seizures and cardiomyopathy","Metabolic decompensation with hypoglycaemia, metabolic acidosis and ketosis can occur, sometimes triggered by fasting or illness","The biochemical hallmark is elevated urinary malonic acid, often with a raised malonic to methylmalonic acid ratio","Cardiomyopathy is an important feature and a major determinant of outcome","Management centres on avoiding fasting, a modified diet and supportive metabolic care; carnitine supplementation may be used adjunctively","Diagnosis is supported by urine organic acids and acylcarnitine profile and confirmed by MLYCD molecular testing"],"pathophysiology":"Malonyl-CoA decarboxylase converts malonyl-CoA to acetyl-CoA. Malonyl-CoA both provides substrate for fatty acid synthesis and inhibits carnitine palmitoyltransferase 1, the gatekeeper of mitochondrial fatty acid oxidation. Loss of the enzyme causes malonyl-CoA accumulation, which impairs long-chain fatty acid oxidation and disturbs the balance of energy metabolism, particularly in heart and brain. Accumulated malonic acid is excreted in urine, giving the characteristic organic aciduria, while impaired energy supply underlies the cardiomyopathy, neurological features and susceptibility to metabolic crises.","additionalDifferentials":["Methylmalonic and other classic organic acidaemias","Fatty acid oxidation disorders with cardiomyopathy and hypoglycaemia","Combined malonic and methylmalonic aciduria (ACSF3-related), a distinct entity","Mitochondrial disease presenting with cardiomyopathy","Glycogen storage disorders with hypoglycaemia","Other causes of infantile hypotonia with developmental delay"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine organic acids showing elevated malonic acid and the malonic to methylmalonic acid ratio","Plasma acylcarnitine profile","MLYCD molecular genetic testing","Echocardiography for cardiomyopathy","Blood glucose, blood gas and ketone measurement during illness","Neuroimaging and developmental assessment"],"redFlags":["Acute metabolic decompensation with hypoglycaemia and acidosis","Signs of heart failure from cardiomyopathy","New or worsening seizures","Cardiac arrhythmia"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable and strongly influenced by the severity of cardiomyopathy; with dietary management and avoidance of fasting, some children stabilise, but significant cardiac or neurological disability can persist.","pitfalls":["Confusing malonyl-CoA decarboxylase deficiency with the distinct combined malonic and methylmalonic aciduria","Overlooking cardiomyopathy, which drives prognosis","Failing to prevent catabolism during intercurrent illness","Misreading a mildly abnormal organic acid profile without confirmatory genetics"],"management":["Avoid prolonged fasting with regular feeds and an emergency regimen of intravenous glucose during intercurrent illness to prevent catabolic decompensation","A modified diet restricting long-chain fat with generous carbohydrate, and medium-chain triglyceride supplementation where appropriate, to limit reliance on fatty acid oxidation","Adjunctive carnitine supplementation to support fatty acid metabolism and prevent secondary deficiency","Echocardiographic surveillance for cardiomyopathy with heart failure therapy as needed, given its major influence on outcome","Monitor glucose, acid-base status and ketones during illness and treat metabolic acidosis and hypoglycaemia promptly","Neurodevelopmental support and management of seizures with input from paediatric neurology","Coordinate care through a specialist metabolic team including a metabolic dietitian","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance with testing of at-risk family members"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID, organic acidaemias","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics, organic acid disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"malonyl_coa_decarboxylase_deficiency":{"name":"Malonyl-CoA Decarboxylase Deficiency (Malonic Aciduria)","system":"Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MLYCD deficiency","malonic aciduria","MCD deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Inborn errors of metabolism references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare autosomal recessive organic aciduria caused by biallelic MLYCD variants encoding malonyl-CoA decarboxylase.","The biochemical hallmark is malonic aciduria in which malonic acid characteristically exceeds methylmalonic acid, with raised malonylcarnitine (C3-DC) on acylcarnitine profiling.","Cardiomyopathy, often dilated, is a characteristic and prognostically dominant feature that is unusual among the organic acidurias.","Neurodevelopmental delay, hypotonia and seizures are common, and developmental regression may occur.","Metabolic decompensation presents with hypoglycaemia, metabolic acidosis and ketosis, often with gastrointestinal symptoms such as vomiting, diarrhoea and abdominal distension.","Malonyl-CoA is a key regulator of fatty-acid oxidation through inhibition of carnitine palmitoyltransferase 1, so its accumulation disturbs cellular energy metabolism.","It may be detected on newborn screening through the elevated malonylcarnitine (C3-DC) marker."],"pathophysiology":"MLYCD encodes malonyl-CoA decarboxylase, the enzyme that converts malonyl-CoA to acetyl-CoA and thereby sets the cellular concentration of malonyl-CoA. Malonyl-CoA is both a substrate for fatty-acid synthesis and a potent inhibitor of carnitine palmitoyltransferase 1, the gatekeeper of mitochondrial long-chain fatty-acid oxidation. Enzyme deficiency causes malonyl-CoA to accumulate, and it is hydrolysed to malonic acid, producing malonic acidaemia and aciduria, while the raised malonyl-CoA suppresses fatty-acid oxidation; the combination of impaired fatty-acid oxidation and disordered malonate metabolism deprives the heart and brain of energy substrate and explains the cardiomyopathy and neurological features. Malonate additionally inhibits succinate dehydrogenase (mitochondrial complex II), contributing to secondary mitochondrial dysfunction.","additionalDifferentials":["Combined malonic and methylmalonic aciduria (ACSF3 deficiency), the key sibling disorder, but there methylmalonate usually exceeds malonate","Classic methylmalonic acidaemias (MMUT and cobalamin-related defects)","Fatty-acid oxidation disorders, Barth syndrome and mitochondrial disease presenting with cardiomyopathy","Primary carnitine deficiency and other carnitine-cycle disorders","Glutaric aciduria and other organic acidurias presenting with encephalopathy","Malonate elevation secondary to other mitochondrial dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine organic acids (malonate predominant over methylmalonate)","Plasma acylcarnitine profile (raised malonylcarnitine, C3-DC)","Echocardiography and ECG for cardiomyopathy surveillance","Molecular testing of MLYCD or an organic-aciduria gene panel","Blood glucose, gas, lactate and ketone measurement during illness","Enzyme assay in fibroblasts or leucocytes where available","Review of newborn-screening acylcarnitine results"],"redFlags":["New or worsening heart failure from dilated cardiomyopathy","Acute metabolic decompensation with hypoglycaemia and acidosis during illness or fasting","Arrhythmia in the context of cardiomyopathy","Rapid neurological deterioration during catabolic stress"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is largely determined by the cardiomyopathy; with early diagnosis, avoidance of fasting, dietary measures and carnitine support some children do well, whereas progressive cardiac disease or severe decompensation carries significant risk.","pitfalls":["Assuming malonic aciduria is always MLYCD deficiency, when combined malonic and methylmalonic aciduria (ACSF3) must be distinguished by the malonate-to-methylmalonate ratio.","Overlooking cardiac surveillance, since cardiomyopathy may dominate the outcome.","Attributing the dilated cardiomyopathy to a primary cardiac cause and missing the underlying metabolic diagnosis.","Under-recognising the risk of decompensation during fasting or intercurrent illness."],"management":["Institute a low-fat, high-carbohydrate diet with strict avoidance of fasting and frequent feeds to reduce reliance on impaired long-chain fatty-acid oxidation","Supplement with L-carnitine to promote excretion of accumulated acyl-CoA species and correct secondary carnitine depletion","Provide an emergency regimen during intercurrent illness with increased carbohydrate intake or intravenous glucose to reverse catabolism and prevent decompensation","Treat acute metabolic decompensation with intravenous dextrose and fluids to correct hypoglycaemia and metabolic acidosis, temporarily withholding fat load","Undertake cardiac surveillance with serial echocardiography and ECG, treating dilated cardiomyopathy with standard heart-failure therapy","Support neurodevelopment with developmental assessment, physiotherapy and anticonvulsants for seizures","Coordinate care through a metabolic multidisciplinary team including metabolic physician, dietitian and cardiologist","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance and biallelic MLYCD variants, with testing of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Physician's Guide to the Diagnosis, Treatment and Follow-up of Inherited Metabolic Diseases","Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment","Organic acidurias and fatty-acid metabolism reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"malrotation_volvulus":{"name":"Intestinal malrotation with midgut volvulus","system":"Paediatric surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["malrotation","midgut volvulus","intestinal nonrotation","Ladd band obstruction","malrotation with volvulus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Ashcraft Pediatric Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Malrotation is incomplete or abnormal rotation and fixation of the midgut, leaving a narrow mesenteric base that predisposes to catastrophic volvulus","Bilious vomiting in a previously well neonate or infant is midgut volvulus until proven otherwise and demands emergency evaluation","Ladd bands may cross and obstruct the duodenum even without volvulus, causing intermittent bilious vomiting","The upper gastrointestinal contrast study is the reference investigation, showing an abnormally positioned duodenojejunal junction and a corkscrew appearance of the proximal jejunum in volvulus","Ultrasound may reveal a whirlpool sign of the superior mesenteric vein wrapping around the artery and inversion of the normal superior mesenteric artery/vein relationship","Volvulus twists the bowel around the superior mesenteric vessels, threatening ischaemia and loss of the entire midgut within hours","Treatment of volvulus is urgent laparotomy with counterclockwise detorsion and a Ladd procedure to broaden the mesenteric base"],"pathophysiology":"During normal development the midgut herniates, rotates 270 degrees counterclockwise around the superior mesenteric artery, and returns to the abdomen with broad retroperitoneal fixation of the duodenojejunal junction and caecum. In malrotation this sequence is arrested or aberrant, so the mesenteric root remains narrow and points of fixation are abnormal, often with peritoneal (Ladd) bands tethering the caecum across the duodenum. The narrow pedicle allows the midgut to twist around the superior mesenteric vessels, producing both luminal obstruction and vascular compromise; sustained torsion leads to venous congestion, arterial occlusion, and infarction of bowel supplied by the superior mesenteric artery.","additionalDifferentials":["Duodenal atresia or stenosis","Jejunoileal atresia","Necrotising enterocolitis","Hirschsprung disease","Incarcerated inguinal hernia","Sepsis or metabolic disease causing bilious vomiting"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper gastrointestinal contrast study to define the position of the duodenojejunal junction and detect a corkscrew pattern","Abdominal ultrasound for the whirlpool sign and superior mesenteric artery/vein orientation","Abdominal radiograph, which may show a paucity of distal gas or a double-bubble-like proximal obstruction","Contrast enema to assess caecal position when other studies are equivocal","Blood gas and lactate to detect ischaemia and guide resuscitation","Baseline bloods and group-and-save in preparation for emergency surgery"],"redFlags":["Bilious vomiting in a neonate or infant, mandating emergency assessment for volvulus","Abdominal distension with tenderness, guarding, or discoloration indicating ischaemia","Blood per rectum, a late and ominous sign of bowel infarction","Haemodynamic instability, acidosis, or rising lactate","Rapid clinical deterioration where imaging should not delay laparotomy"],"prognosis":"When volvulus is recognised and untwisted promptly the outcome is usually excellent, but delayed diagnosis can lead to massive bowel loss, short-bowel syndrome, or death. Elective Ladd procedure for asymptomatic malrotation carries low risk and prevents future volvulus.","pitfalls":["Dismissing bilious vomiting as benign feed intolerance and delaying imaging or surgery","Awaiting non-urgent tests in a compromised infant instead of proceeding to laparotomy","Relying on a normal abdominal radiograph to exclude volvulus","Misinterpreting a normally positioned duodenojejunal junction variant and overlooking subtle malrotation","Failing to perform appendicectomy and full Ladd manoeuvres, leaving the patient at future risk"],"management":["Perform emergency Ladd procedure with detorsion of the volvulus, division of Ladd bands, broadening of the mesenteric base, appendicectomy and placement of bowel in a non-rotation position","Resuscitate preoperatively with nil by mouth, nasogastric decompression, intravenous fluids and correction of electrolyte and acid-base derangement","Give broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics where ischaemia or perforation is suspected","Assess bowel viability intraoperatively, resect frankly necrotic segments and plan a second-look laparotomy for borderline bowel","Offer elective Ladd procedure for incidentally detected asymptomatic malrotation to prevent future volvulus","Manage short-bowel syndrome after extensive resection with parenteral nutrition and gradual enteral advancement","Escalate immediately for any bilious vomiting in a neonate or infant, treating as midgut volvulus until proven otherwise given the risk of losing the entire midgut within hours"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Ashcraft's Pediatric Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"malt_lymphoma":{"name":"Extranodal marginal zone (MALT) lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MALT lymphoma","extranodal marginal zone lymphoma of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue","MALToma","t(11;18) API2-MALT1 lymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An indolent marginal-zone B-cell lymphoma arising in extranodal mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, most commonly the stomach but also lung, salivary gland, thyroid, ocular adnexa and skin","Chronic antigenic stimulation drives the disease, classically Helicobacter pylori in gastric MALT lymphoma and autoimmune conditions such as Sjogren syndrome or Hashimoto thyroiditis at other sites","Early-stage H. pylori-positive gastric disease frequently regresses with eradication therapy alone, making this a lymphoma treatable with antibiotics","The translocation t(11;18) fusing API2 and MALT1 predicts a lower likelihood of response to H. pylori eradication and tends to remain localised","Lymphoepithelial lesions, where neoplastic B cells infiltrate and disrupt glandular epithelium, are a histological hallmark","The immunophenotype is CD20-positive with CD5, CD10, CD23 and cyclin D1 negativity, helping to distinguish it from other small B-cell lymphomas","Localised H. pylori-negative or non-responding disease is managed with radiotherapy or anti-CD20 targeted therapy, and disseminated disease with chemoimmunotherapy"],"pathophysiology":"MALT lymphoma develops at sites of acquired lymphoid tissue generated by chronic infection or autoimmune inflammation. Persistent antigenic drive, exemplified by Helicobacter pylori in the stomach, sustains T-cell-dependent proliferation of marginal-zone B cells; early in the disease this dependence explains why eradicating the antigenic stimulus can lead to regression. Over time the acquisition of genetic changes, notably translocations such as t(11;18) that generate the API2-MALT1 fusion and constitutively activate NF-kappaB signalling, renders the lymphoma antigen-independent and unlikely to respond to eradication. The infiltrate expands the marginal zone, colonises follicles and produces lymphoepithelial lesions within glandular structures.","additionalDifferentials":["Reactive lymphoid hyperplasia or chronic gastritis with lymphoid follicles","Follicular lymphoma involving an extranodal site","Mantle cell lymphoma (including lymphomatous polyposis of the gut)","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (including transformation)","Nodal or splenic marginal zone lymphoma with extranodal spread","Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia/small lymphocytic lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Endoscopy with multiple mucosal biopsies for gastric disease, including testing for Helicobacter pylori","Immunohistochemistry showing CD20-positive, CD5/CD10/CD23/cyclin D1-negative marginal-zone B cells with lymphoepithelial lesions","FISH or molecular testing for t(11;18) API2-MALT1 to guide therapy","Staging imaging and, for gastric disease, assessment of depth of invasion and regional nodes","Autoimmune serology (e.g. for Sjogren syndrome or Hashimoto thyroiditis) at relevant sites","Bone marrow examination and blood counts for staging"],"redFlags":["Transformation to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma indicated by a rapidly enlarging mass, ulceration or rising lactate dehydrogenase","Gastrointestinal bleeding, obstruction or perforation from gastric disease","Failure of gastric lymphoma to regress after documented H. pylori eradication","Orbital or airway compromise from bulky extranodal disease"],"prognosis":"The overall prognosis is excellent with prolonged survival; early gastric disease often achieves durable remission after H. pylori eradication, while t(11;18)-positive tumours are less likely to respond to eradication but usually remain localised, and the main adverse event is transformation to aggressive large B-cell lymphoma.","pitfalls":["Failing to test for and eradicate Helicobacter pylori in gastric disease","Expecting eradication to work in t(11;18)-positive tumours","Discontinuing surveillance too early, as regression after eradication can be slow","Missing multifocal or disseminated disease by not staging adequately","Overlooking transformation to aggressive lymphoma in a changing lesion"],"management":["Give Helicobacter pylori eradication with a proton-pump inhibitor plus antibiotic combination as first-line therapy for early-stage gastric disease, achieving regression in many cases","Undertake endoscopic surveillance with repeat biopsy to confirm histological remission after eradication","Use involved-site radiotherapy for localised t(11;18)-positive, eradication-refractory or H. pylori-negative disease","Treat advanced or disseminated disease with an anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody (rituximab) with or without chemotherapy","Address the underlying autoimmune driver at non-gastric sites such as Sjogren syndrome or Hashimoto thyroiditis","Monitor for transformation to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, prompting biopsy of any changing or symptomatic lesion","Manage through a haemato-oncology multidisciplinary team with site-specific specialist input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","British Society for Haematology guidance on marginal zone lymphomas"],"crossLinks":[]},"mammary_duct_ectasia":{"name":"Mammary duct ectasia","system":"Breast","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Duct ectasia","Plasma cell mastitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"ABC of Breast Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Dilatation and shortening of the major subareolar (lactiferous) ducts, usually affecting peri- and postmenopausal women.","Classic presentation is thick, cheesy, multicoloured nipple discharge (creamy, green or brown) from multiple ducts, often bilateral.","Slit-like or transverse nipple retraction may develop as periductal fibrosis shortens the ducts and can mimic malignant retraction.","A firm subareolar mass or thickening can form and, with associated calcification, may raise concern on mammography.","Distinct from periductal mastitis, which affects younger smokers, although the two overlap within the duct ectasia-periductal mastitis spectrum.","Coarse, rod-like ('needle-shaped') secretory calcifications aligned toward the nipple are a characteristic benign mammographic feature.","Most cases need only reassurance once malignancy is excluded; total duct excision is reserved for troublesome discharge or recurrent problems.","Blood-stained single-duct discharge should not be assumed to be duct ectasia and warrants investigation for papilloma or carcinoma."],"pathophysiology":"Mammary duct ectasia is a benign involutional process in which the large subareolar ducts dilate and fill with inspissated proteinaceous and lipid-rich secretions. Stagnant secretions may leak into the periductal tissue, provoking a chronic inflammatory reaction rich in plasma cells and macrophages (giving the alternative term plasma cell mastitis) and progressive periductal fibrosis. This fibrosis shortens the ducts, producing nipple retraction, while the retained secretions calcify to form the characteristic coarse ductal calcifications. The condition sits at the involutional end of the duct ectasia-periductal mastitis spectrum and is more prevalent with advancing age.","additionalDifferentials":["Periductal mastitis","Intraductal papilloma","Ductal carcinoma in situ presenting with discharge or calcification","Invasive carcinoma causing nipple retraction","Paget's disease of the nipple","Breast abscess","Galactorrhoea from hyperprolactinaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Triple assessment with examination, ultrasound and mammography","Assessment of discharge for single- versus multiple-duct and colour","Mammography to characterise secretory calcification and exclude malignancy","Ultrasound of any subareolar mass or dilated duct","Core biopsy of a discrete or suspicious mass","Ductography or ductoscopy in selected discharge cases","Serum prolactin and thyroid function if galactorrhoea is suspected"],"redFlags":["Blood-stained or single-duct nipple discharge","A hard, irregular or fixed subareolar mass","Fine pleomorphic or branching microcalcification rather than coarse secretory calcification","Eczematous or ulcerated nipple change suggesting Paget's disease","Progressive unilateral nipple retraction with skin distortion"],"prognosis":"Duct ectasia is a benign, self-limiting condition that does not increase breast cancer risk. Symptoms often settle spontaneously, and surgery, when required for persistent discharge or retraction, is generally curative.","pitfalls":["Assuming all nipple retraction is malignant, or conversely dismissing malignant retraction as benign ectasia","Failing to distinguish benign coarse secretory calcification from suspicious pleomorphic microcalcification","Attributing blood-stained single-duct discharge to ectasia without excluding papilloma or carcinoma","Confusing duct ectasia with periductal mastitis and mismanaging the younger smoker","Overlooking hyperprolactinaemia when discharge is milky and bilateral"],"management":["Complete triple assessment to exclude malignancy, then reassure that this is a benign, self-limiting involutional condition that does not raise breast cancer risk","Manage conservatively with warm compresses, good nipple hygiene and a well-fitting supportive bra","Reserve antibiotics for superimposed periductal infection, covering anaerobes and skin flora","Advise smoking cessation given its association within the duct ectasia-periductal mastitis spectrum","Offer surgical duct excision (microdochectomy or total duct excision) for persistent troublesome discharge or nipple retraction","Re-biopsy or investigate any suspicious mass or bloody single-duct discharge to exclude underlying carcinoma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ABC of Breast Diseases (current edition)","Standard textbook of breast surgery on benign duct disorders","National guidance on investigation of nipple discharge"],"crossLinks":[]},"mandibular_fracture":{"name":"Mandibular fracture","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fractured mandible","broken jaw","lower jaw fracture","condylar fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The mandible commonly fractures at more than one site because of its arch shape, so a fracture at one point should prompt a search for a second, often contralateral, fracture such as the condyle","Malocclusion, a change in the patient's usual bite, is a sensitive sign of mandibular fracture and must be specifically asked about and examined","Other clinical signs include pain on jaw movement, trismus, a step in the dental arch, sublingual haematoma, and numbness of the lower lip from inferior alveolar nerve injury","Panoramic radiography and CT are used to define fracture site, displacement, and the relationship to tooth roots and the inferior alveolar nerve","Fractures involving tooth-bearing bone communicate with the mouth and are effectively open, requiring antibiotics and timely management to reduce infection risk","Restoring the pre-injury occlusion is the central goal, achieved with maxillomandibular fixation and/or open reduction with internal fixation depending on the pattern","Many condylar fractures, particularly in children and minimally displaced cases, can be managed conservatively with a short period of fixation and early mobilisation to avoid ankylosis","Bilateral anterior (parasymphyseal) fractures can allow the tongue and anterior segment to fall backward and threaten the airway"],"pathophysiology":"The mandible is a mobile, U-shaped bone whose weaker regions - the condylar neck, angle (often at an unerupted third molar), and parasymphysis - are prone to fracture. Because it forms a ring-like structure articulating at both temporomandibular joints, force at one point is frequently transmitted to produce a second fracture elsewhere. Displacement is influenced by the pull of the muscles of mastication, which can distract or override fracture segments depending on their orientation (favourable versus unfavourable patterns). Fractures through tooth-bearing segments breach the oral mucosa, creating a contaminated, effectively open injury, and disruption of the inferior alveolar nerve within the canal causes lower lip and chin numbness.","additionalDifferentials":["Temporomandibular joint dislocation without fracture","Dentoalveolar fracture or isolated dental trauma","Zygomatic or midface fracture causing trismus","Mandibular contusion or muscle injury","Pathological fracture through a cyst or tumour","Temporomandibular joint dysfunction unrelated to trauma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Panoramic radiograph (orthopantomogram) as an initial survey of the mandible","CT for detailed assessment of condyle, displacement, and comminution","Careful occlusal and dental examination to detect malocclusion and tooth injury","Inferior alveolar nerve sensory testing of the lower lip and chin","Assessment for a second fracture given the ring anatomy of the mandible"],"redFlags":["Airway compromise from bilateral parasymphyseal fractures with posterior displacement of the tongue (emergency)","Displaced open fracture through tooth-bearing bone with infection risk","Signs of associated cervical spine or head injury in high-energy trauma","Progressive infection or abscess from an untreated open mandibular fracture"],"prognosis":"Most mandibular fractures heal well with restored occlusion and function when reduction and fixation are appropriate, and many condylar fractures do well with conservative management. Poorer outcomes - malunion, malocclusion, nonunion, infection, temporomandibular joint stiffness or ankylosis, and persistent nerve numbness - are associated with delayed treatment, inadequate reduction, open contaminated fractures, or prolonged immobilisation.","pitfalls":["Missing a second (often condylar) fracture after identifying the first","Failing to recognise malocclusion as a key diagnostic sign","Underestimating infection risk in fractures communicating with the oral cavity","Overlooking airway risk in bilateral anterior fractures","Prolonged rigid immobilisation of condylar fractures leading to stiffness or ankylosis","Neglecting to document inferior alveolar nerve deficit before intervention"],"management":["Restore pre-injury occlusion as the central goal using maxillomandibular fixation and/or open reduction with internal fixation according to the fracture pattern","Manage minimally displaced fractures and many condylar fractures conservatively with a soft diet, analgesia and close review","Give antibiotics for fractures through tooth-bearing bone, which communicate with the mouth and are effectively open, together with tetanus prophylaxis","Provide analgesia and oral hygiene measures with antiseptic mouthwash","Search for and treat a second fracture, often contralateral condylar, given the ring anatomy of the mandible","Document and follow inferior alveolar nerve function through lower-lip and chin sensation testing","Protect the airway in bilateral or comminuted fractures where loss of tongue support threatens obstruction","Refer to maxillofacial or plastic surgery with dental input for associated tooth injuries"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"mandibuloacral_dysplasia":{"name":"Mandibuloacral dysplasia (LMNA/ZMPSTE24)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAD","MADA (LMNA)","MADB (ZMPSTE24)","mandibuloacral dysplasia with lipodystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Endocrine Society clinical guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare autosomal recessive progeroid disorder combining skeletal dysplasia with partial or generalised lipodystrophy","Type A (MADA) arises from LMNA (lamin A/C) mutations, while type B (MADB) results from ZMPSTE24 mutations affecting prelamin A processing","Skeletal hallmarks include mandibular and clavicular hypoplasia, acro-osteolysis (resorption of the terminal finger phalanges), delayed cranial suture closure, and joint contractures","The MADA fat pattern is typically partial with limb fat loss and relative truncal sparing, whereas MADB tends toward more generalised fat loss with earlier, more severe features","Progeroid skin and features, mottled pigmentation, alopecia, and a beaked nose accompany the phenotype","Loss of subcutaneous fat produces insulin resistance, acanthosis nigricans, and a predisposition to hypertriglyceridaemia and diabetes","Management targets the metabolic complications with insulin sensitisers and lipid control, with leptin (metreleptin) replacement considered when leptin is low","Diagnosis integrates the skeletal-progeroid-lipodystrophy triad with LMNA and ZMPSTE24 genetics"],"pathophysiology":"Mandibuloacral dysplasia is a laminopathy-spectrum disorder caused by biallelic mutations in LMNA, which encodes the nuclear-lamina proteins lamin A/C, or in ZMPSTE24, the metalloprotease that cleaves prelamin A to mature lamin A. Defective lamin processing destabilises the nuclear envelope, impairing the function and survival of mesenchymal-derived tissues including bone and adipocytes. This produces the characteristic mandibular and acral skeletal defects, progeroid connective-tissue changes, and loss of subcutaneous fat. As with other lipodystrophies, reduced fat-storage capacity drives ectopic lipid deposition, insulin resistance, acanthosis nigricans, and a tendency to hypertriglyceridaemia and diabetes.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial partial lipodystrophy (LMNA-related)","Hutchinson-Gilford progeria and other progeroid syndromes","Congenital generalised lipodystrophy (for MADB)","Acro-osteolysis syndromes such as Hajdu-Cheney","Cleidocranial dysplasia (for clavicular/cranial features)","Werner syndrome and adult progeroid disorders","Other skeletal dysplasias with metabolic features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey demonstrating mandibular/clavicular hypoplasia and acro-osteolysis","Regional fat imaging (DEXA/MRI) to characterise the lipodystrophy pattern","Fasting glucose/HbA1c, insulin, and lipid profile","Serum leptin to guide consideration of leptin replacement","Genetic testing of LMNA and ZMPSTE24","Liver function tests and hepatic imaging for steatosis"],"redFlags":["Severe hypertriglyceridaemia risking pancreatitis","Progressive skeletal deformity and joint contractures limiting function","Rapidly advancing progeroid features in the more severe MADB form","Poorly controlled insulin-resistant diabetes"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies with subtype and severity; the LMNA-related type A tends to be milder with a partial lipodystrophy phenotype, whereas the ZMPSTE24-related type B is more severe and progeroid, and long-term outlook is shaped by metabolic and skeletal complications.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the disorder for isolated familial partial lipodystrophy and missing the skeletal features","Overlooking acro-osteolysis on hand radiographs","Failing to distinguish the milder LMNA type from the more severe ZMPSTE24 type","Neglecting metabolic screening for diabetes and hypertriglyceridaemia","Not arranging genetic confirmation and counselling"],"management":["Manage insulin resistance and diabetes with lifestyle measures and insulin-sensitising therapy such as metformin, adding insulin where needed","Treat hypertriglyceridaemia with a low-fat diet plus fibrates and/or statins to reduce pancreatitis and cardiovascular risk","Consider recombinant leptin (metreleptin) replacement in significant lipodystrophy with metabolic complications and low leptin","Undertake metabolic surveillance with fasting glucose, HbA1c, lipid profile and monitoring for hepatic steatosis","Provide orthopaedic and physiotherapy input for joint contractures and monitor acro-osteolysis and skeletal dysplasia","Coordinate care across endocrinology, clinical genetics and orthopaedics","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive LMNA or ZMPSTE24 disease with testing of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society clinical practice guidance on lipodystrophy"],"crossLinks":[]},"mandibulofacial_dysostosis_guion_almeida":{"name":"Mandibulofacial dysostosis with microcephaly","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mandibulofacial dysostosis, Guion-Almeida type","MFDM","MFDGA","EFTUD2-related mandibulofacial dysostosis","acrofacial dysostosis, Guion-Almeida type"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nature Reviews chromatinopathy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from haploinsufficiency of EFTUD2, which encodes a GTPase subunit of the U5 small nuclear ribonucleoprotein within the major spliceosome, placing the condition among the spliceosomopathies rather than the classic chromatin disorders","The cardinal combination is malar and mandibular hypoplasia together with progressive microcephaly, which helps separate it from Treacher Collins syndrome where head circumference is usually preserved","External ear anomalies such as microtia and atresia of the external auditory canal are common and frequently accompanied by conductive hearing loss requiring early audiological input","Oesophageal atresia with or without tracheo-oesophageal fistula and choanal atresia are recognised associations that may declare themselves in the neonatal period","Supporting features include cleft palate, congenital heart defects, thumb and radial ray anomalies, epibulbar dermoids and clefts of the zygomatic arch","Developmental delay and intellectual disability of variable degree are usual, so neurodevelopmental follow-up should be arranged from infancy","Diagnosis is confirmed by finding a heterozygous, usually de novo, loss-of-function EFTUD2 variant or a 17q21.31 deletion encompassing the gene"],"pathophysiology":"EFTUD2 encodes a conserved GTPase that forms part of the U5 small nuclear ribonucleoprotein of the major spliceosome, the molecular machine that removes introns from pre-messenger RNA. Loss-of-function variants reduce the amount of functional protein (haploinsufficiency), impairing efficient and accurate splicing during a critical window of embryonic development. Tissues derived from the neural crest, which give rise to much of the facial skeleton and ears, appear particularly sensitive to this reduction, producing the characteristic mandibulofacial dysostosis. Concurrent disturbance of neuronal proliferation is thought to underlie the microcephaly and intellectual disability, while disrupted patterning of the foregut and other structures accounts for the oesophageal and cardiac anomalies.","additionalDifferentials":["Treacher Collins syndrome (TCOF1, POLR1C, POLR1D)","Nager acrofacial dysostosis (SF3B4, a related spliceosomopathy)","Miller postaxial acrofacial dysostosis (DHODH)","CHARGE syndrome","Oculoauriculovertebral spectrum / Goldenhar syndrome","Burn-McKeown syndrome","Craniofacial microsomia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or genome sequencing to detect EFTUD2 sequence variants, including small intragenic deletions","Chromosomal microarray to identify 17q21.31 deletions that encompass EFTUD2","Formal audiological assessment with temporal bone imaging where hearing loss is suspected","Neonatal evaluation for oesophageal atresia and tracheo-oesophageal fistula when feeding difficulty or excess secretions occur","Echocardiography to screen for congenital heart disease","Brain MRI to characterise structural anomalies underlying the microcephaly","Ophthalmological review for epibulbar dermoids and assessment of choanal patency"],"redFlags":["Neonatal respiratory distress or choking on feeds suggesting choanal atresia or oesophageal atresia with tracheo-oesophageal fistula","Upper airway obstruction from severe mandibular hypoplasia and glossoptosis","Recurrent aspiration with faltering growth","Unrecognised conductive hearing loss compromising early language acquisition"],"prognosis":"Outcome is highly variable and is driven chiefly by the severity of airway compromise, feeding difficulty and cardiac disease in infancy; with coordinated multidisciplinary care these can often be managed, after which intellectual ability determines longer-term function across a wide spectrum from mild to severe impairment.","pitfalls":["Labelling the child as Treacher Collins syndrome and thereby overlooking the microcephaly and intellectual disability that point to EFTUD2","Failing to look for oesophageal atresia or tracheo-oesophageal fistula in a neonate with excessive secretions","Delaying audiological assessment so that treatable conductive hearing loss impairs speech development","Assuming a normal karyotype excludes the diagnosis when microdeletion and single-gene testing are required"],"management":["Assess and secure the neonatal airway given micrognathia and choanal atresia, using positioning, nasopharyngeal airway or tracheostomy as required","Arrange early surgical repair of oesophageal atresia with or without tracheo-oesophageal fistula and of choanal atresia","Support feeding and growth with nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding and dietitian input","Provide audiological assessment and hearing habilitation with hearing aids or bone-conduction devices for conductive loss from microtia and canal atresia","Plan staged craniofacial reconstruction for malar and mandibular hypoplasia and microtia, with cleft palate repair","Screen with echocardiography and manage any congenital heart defect","Arrange neurodevelopmental follow-up and early-intervention support given variable intellectual disability","Offer genetic counselling for usually de novo, dominantly acting EFTUD2 haploinsufficiency with family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews — Mandibulofacial Dysostosis with Microcephaly","Nature Reviews Genetics — spliceosomopathies and craniofacial development"],"crossLinks":[]},"manganese_toxicity":{"name":"Manganese toxicity","system":"Toxicology / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["manganism","manganese poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A neurotoxic syndrome from excess manganese exposure, classically in welders, miners, smelters and battery workers","Also occurs with chronic liver failure (impaired biliary excretion) and prolonged parenteral nutrition, particularly in infants","Produces an extrapyramidal, parkinsonism-like picture that is often poorly responsive to levodopa","Early features include behavioural change, irritability and psychiatric symptoms (historically termed manganese madness)","A characteristic gait with retropulsion and dystonia (cock-walk) may develop","MRI classically shows symmetrical high signal in the basal ganglia (globus pallidus) on T1-weighted images","Management focuses on removing the exposure source; chelation has limited benefit"],"pathophysiology":"Manganese is an essential trace element normally regulated by intestinal absorption and biliary excretion. Excess exposure, or reduced clearance in liver failure, allows accumulation in the brain, especially the basal ganglia and globus pallidus, where manganese is paramagnetic and produces the characteristic T1 hyperintensity. Accumulated manganese impairs mitochondrial function, promotes oxidative stress and disrupts dopaminergic and other neurotransmitter systems, producing an extrapyramidal movement disorder distinct from idiopathic Parkinson's disease and typically levodopa-unresponsive.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic Parkinson's disease","Drug-induced parkinsonism","Wilson disease","Other causes of basal ganglia T1 hyperintensity (chronic liver disease, parenteral nutrition)","Progressive supranuclear palsy and other atypical parkinsonism","Neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Whole-blood manganese concentration (imperfect marker of brain burden)","Brain MRI looking for symmetrical pallidal T1 hyperintensity","Occupational and nutritional exposure history","Liver function assessment","Review of parenteral nutrition manganese content where relevant","Exclusion of Wilson disease"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive parkinsonism or dystonia","Significant psychiatric disturbance","Ongoing high-level occupational exposure","Infant on long-term parenteral nutrition with neurological change"],"prognosis":"Neurological deficits may stabilise or partially improve after exposure ceases but can be persistent or progressive; early recognition and source removal give the best outcome.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as idiopathic Parkinson's disease and expecting a levodopa response","Overlooking parenteral nutrition or chronic liver disease as non-occupational sources","Relying on blood manganese alone, which correlates poorly with brain accumulation","Failing to take an occupational history"],"management":["Identify and remove the source of exposure, whether occupational, by reducing manganese content of parenteral nutrition, or by treating the underlying liver failure impairing biliary excretion","Consider chelation therapy such as calcium disodium EDTA to enhance urinary excretion, recognising limited neurological benefit","Trial levodopa while noting the extrapyramidal syndrome is often poorly responsive, and treat parkinsonism and dystonia symptomatically","Manage hepatic dysfunction contributing to impaired manganese clearance","Provide psychiatric and behavioural support for irritability and mood disturbance","Implement occupational health measures with workplace exposure limits and biological monitoring","Follow up with neurology and toxicology, monitoring whole-blood manganese and pallidal signal on brain MRI"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies (standard reference)","Occupational neurology references"],"crossLinks":[]},"mannose_binding_lectin_deficiency":{"name":"Mannose-binding lectin deficiency","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MBL deficiency","MBL2","mannan-binding lectin deficiency","lectin pathway deficiency","MBL insufficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies / Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["One of the most common inherited immune variants, with low functional mannose-binding lectin found in a substantial minority of healthy people","Usually clinically silent and best regarded as a susceptibility modifier rather than a stand-alone immunodeficiency","Any predisposition is most relevant in early childhood before adaptive immunity matures, or when combined with another immune defect or immunosuppression","Reported associations include increased frequency or severity of pyogenic and respiratory infections and worse outcomes in some inflammatory or septic states","Low levels arise from common polymorphisms in the MBL2 structural gene and its promoter region","Diagnosis requires demonstrating low MBL level or impaired lectin pathway function, and low levels alone do not establish causation for a given infection","Management is supportive and directed at any co-existing immune defect, since no specific MBL replacement is in routine clinical use"],"pathophysiology":"Mannose-binding lectin is a circulating pattern-recognition molecule of the innate immune system that binds carbohydrate arrays on microbial surfaces and activates the lectin pathway of complement through MBL-associated serine proteases, promoting opsonisation and complement deposition independently of antibody. Common polymorphisms in the MBL2 gene reduce the amount of functional, correctly oligomerised protein, lowering lectin-pathway activity. Because this pathway provides an antibody-independent first line of defence, deficiency may leave a window of increased vulnerability, particularly in infancy or when other arms of immunity are compromised; however, redundancy with the classical and alternative complement pathways and with adaptive immunity means most deficient individuals remain healthy.","additionalDifferentials":["Antibody deficiency syndromes (e.g. IgG subclass or specific antibody deficiency)","Other complement pathway deficiencies","Physiological immune immaturity of infancy","Secondary immunosuppression (drugs, malignancy, chemotherapy)","Ficolin or MASP2 lectin-pathway component deficiencies","Normal host with recurrent common childhood infections"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum mannose-binding lectin concentration","Functional lectin pathway complement assay","MBL2 genotyping for structural and promoter variants","Broader immune workup (immunoglobulins, specific antibodies, complement) to exclude co-existing defects","MASP2 and ficolin assessment where available if lectin-pathway defect suspected","Clinical correlation of infection history rather than reliance on the level alone"],"redFlags":["Severe, recurrent or unusually invasive infection out of proportion to a minor MBL variant, prompting search for another immune defect","Deteriorating course of sepsis in a critically ill or immunosuppressed patient","Recurrent infection in an infant that persists beyond expected immune maturation","Concurrent features suggesting a more significant primary immunodeficiency"],"prognosis":"The great majority of individuals with low mannose-binding lectin are healthy and have a normal outlook; any excess infection risk is modest and mainly emerges in combination with immune immaturity, another immune defect, or immunosuppression.","pitfalls":["Over-attributing a patient's recurrent infections to MBL deficiency and stopping the workup prematurely","Treating a common benign polymorphism as a serious primary immunodeficiency","Failing to look for a co-existing, more clinically important immune defect","Interpreting a single low MBL level without a functional lectin pathway assessment"],"management":["Reassure that low mannose-binding lectin is usually clinically silent and is a susceptibility modifier rather than a stand-alone immunodeficiency","Treat intercurrent infections promptly with appropriate antimicrobials, as no routine replacement therapy is indicated","Ensure full immunisation, including pneumococcal and other polysaccharide-conjugate vaccines","Investigate for and manage any co-existing immune defect when infections are recurrent or unusually severe","Maintain heightened vigilance during infancy, chemotherapy or other immunosuppression when redundancy of immune defences is reduced","Involve clinical immunology and correlate the infection history with the deficiency rather than attributing causation to a low level alone"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"mansonelliasis":{"name":"Mansonelliasis (Mansonella filariasis)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mansonella perstans","Mansonella ozzardi","Mansonella streptocerca","Mansonella filariasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Mansonelliasis is a filarial infection caused by Mansonella species (M. perstans, M. ozzardi, M. streptocerca) transmitted by biting midges and blackflies","It is endemic across parts of sub-Saharan Africa, and the Caribbean and Latin America, often coexisting with other filariases","Most infections are asymptomatic or cause mild, non-specific complaints, so the parasite is frequently an incidental finding","When symptomatic, features may include itching, transient swellings, arthralgia, serous-cavity involvement and, with M. streptocerca, skin changes","Peripheral eosinophilia is common and may be the clue that prompts investigation","Diagnosis rests on detecting microfilariae in blood or skin, depending on species; M. perstans and M. ozzardi produce blood microfilariae while M. streptocerca is found in skin","Treatment differs by species and often involves agents such as diethylcarbamazine or ivermectin, with doxycycline used against Wolbachia-harbouring species"],"pathophysiology":"Mansonella infection begins when infective larvae are inoculated by biting midges (Culicoides) or blackflies during a blood meal. The larvae mature into adult worms that reside in serous cavities, mesentery or subcutaneous tissue depending on the species, and release microfilariae into the blood or skin. Many species harbour Wolbachia endosymbiotic bacteria that contribute to inflammatory responses. Because the parasite provokes relatively little tissue reaction in most hosts, infection is often clinically silent; symptomatic disease reflects localised inflammatory and hypersensitivity responses to the worms and microfilariae, frequently accompanied by eosinophilia.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphatic filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti, Brugia species)","Onchocerciasis","Loiasis","Other causes of eosinophilia such as strongyloidiasis and toxocariasis","Allergic and hypersensitivity disorders","Reactive or inflammatory arthropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood films (including concentration techniques) for microfilariae","Skin snip examination for M. streptocerca microfilariae","Full blood count to detect eosinophilia","Species identification of microfilariae by morphology or molecular methods","Screening for co-infection with other filariae, particularly Loa loa before treatment","Serological or antigen tests where available to support diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Marked eosinophilia with systemic symptoms warranting exclusion of other pathology","Co-infection with Loa loa raising the risk of treatment-related reactions","Serositis or effusions attributable to the parasite","Persistent symptoms despite therapy suggesting alternative or additional diagnoses"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally very good, as most infections are benign and asymptomatic; symptomatic disease usually responds to appropriate species-directed therapy, though clearance of adult worms can be slow.","pitfalls":["Attributing all symptoms to Mansonella when co-infection with other filariae is common","Treating without first excluding heavy Loa loa co-infection","Missing the diagnosis because microfilariae require the correct sampling method and timing","Over-treating an incidental, asymptomatic infection"],"management":["Direct antiparasitic therapy by species, using diethylcarbamazine for M. streptocerca and M. ozzardi where appropriate","Use doxycycline to target Wolbachia endosymbionts for M. perstans, which responds poorly to diethylcarbamazine","Consider ivermectin for its activity against M. ozzardi microfilariae","Screen for and exclude Loa loa co-infection before microfilaricidal treatment to avoid severe reactions","Provide symptomatic relief with antihistamines and analgesia for pruritus, transient swellings and arthralgia","Reassure and observe asymptomatic individuals in whom microfilariae are an incidental finding","Refer to infectious diseases or tropical medicine for species confirmation and treatment planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mantle_cell_lymphoma":{"name":"Mantle cell lymphoma (CCND1/t(11;14))","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCL","t(11;14) CCND1-IGH lymphoma","cyclin D1-positive B-cell lymphoma","blastoid mantle cell lymphoma","leukaemic non-nodal mantle cell lymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mature B-cell lymphoma of pre-germinal-centre origin defined by the translocation t(11;14) that juxtaposes CCND1 with the immunoglobulin heavy-chain locus and overexpresses cyclin D1","The neoplastic cells are typically CD20-positive and CD5-positive with SOX11 expression, but unlike chronic lymphocytic leukaemia are CD23-negative","Often presents at an advanced stage in older adults with widespread lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, marrow and blood involvement, and sometimes gastrointestinal lymphomatous polyposis","The blastoid and pleomorphic variants are morphologically aggressive and carry a worse prognosis, especially with high proliferation index or TP53 abnormalities","A leukaemic non-nodal variant, often SOX11-negative, behaves more indolently and may be observed initially","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating cyclin D1 overexpression and/or t(11;14), with SOX11 useful in the rare cyclin D1-negative cases","Management uses chemoimmunotherapy incorporating anti-CD20 therapy, often with autologous HSCT in fit patients and BTK-inhibitor targeted agents in relapse"],"pathophysiology":"Mantle cell lymphoma arises from a mature B cell of the follicle mantle zone, generally before germinal-centre transit. The defining event is the t(11;14) translocation, which places the CCND1 gene under the control of the immunoglobulin heavy-chain enhancer and leads to constitutive overexpression of cyclin D1, dysregulating the cell cycle at the G1-S transition. The transcription factor SOX11 is commonly expressed and contributes to the tumour phenotype. Additional genomic lesions affecting DNA-damage response and cell-cycle pathways, notably TP53 and CDKN2A alterations, drive more aggressive blastoid disease. The tumour disseminates widely, colonising the mantle zones of follicles and infiltrating nodes, spleen, marrow, blood and the gastrointestinal tract.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia/small lymphocytic lymphoma","Marginal zone lymphoma","Follicular lymphoma","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (for blastoid variant)","Lymphoblastic lymphoma (for blastoid variant)","Prolymphocytic leukaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lymph node or tissue biopsy with immunohistochemistry showing cyclin D1 and SOX11 positivity","FISH or molecular testing to confirm t(11;14) CCND1-IGH","Flow cytometry demonstrating CD20-positive, CD5-positive, CD23-negative B cells","Assessment of proliferation index (Ki-67) for prognostic stratification","TP53 mutation and deletion analysis given its strong prognostic impact","PET-CT and bone marrow examination for staging, with gastrointestinal endoscopy where indicated"],"redFlags":["Blastoid or pleomorphic morphology with high proliferation index indicating aggressive disease","TP53 abnormality predicting resistance to standard chemoimmunotherapy","Rapidly progressive bulky disease with B symptoms and rising lactate dehydrogenase","Central nervous system involvement in aggressive variants","Tumour lysis risk with high tumour burden at treatment initiation"],"prognosis":"Classic mantle cell lymphoma is generally incurable with a relapsing course, though modern chemoimmunotherapy, HSCT and targeted agents have improved survival; blastoid morphology, high Ki-67 and TP53 abnormalities predict poor outcomes, whereas the leukaemic non-nodal SOX11-negative variant can follow an indolent course.","pitfalls":["Confusing the disease with chronic lymphocytic leukaemia, both CD5-positive, without checking CD23 and cyclin D1","Missing the rare cyclin D1-negative variant, where SOX11 and t(11;14) alternatives aid diagnosis","Treating the indolent leukaemic non-nodal variant as aggressively as classic disease","Overlooking TP53 status, which should influence treatment choice","Failing to consider gastrointestinal involvement, which is common even when asymptomatic"],"management":["Treat fit patients with intensive induction chemoimmunotherapy combining an anti-CD20 antibody (rituximab) with a cytarabine-containing regimen, followed by autologous haematopoietic stem-cell transplant consolidation","Give rituximab maintenance to prolong remission after induction","Use a less intensive rituximab-bendamustine-based regimen for older or less fit patients","Employ Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as ibrutinib at relapse and increasingly earlier in the disease course","Adopt active surveillance for the indolent leukaemic non-nodal SOX11-negative variant","Recognise TP53-aberrant disease as chemo-resistant and prioritise novel agents, CAR-T therapy or clinical trials","Stage and monitor with PET-CT and bone marrow examination, managing tumour lysis risk during treatment","Coordinate through haemato-oncology, considering allogeneic transplant or CAR-T in refractory disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","British Society for Haematology guidance on mantle cell lymphoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"map_dot_fingerprint_dystrophy":{"name":"Map-dot-fingerprint dystrophy","system":"Eye / Cornea","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["epithelial basement membrane dystrophy","Cogan microcystic dystrophy","anterior basement membrane dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / corneal dystrophy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest anterior corneal dystrophy, involving abnormal epithelial basement membrane","Slit-lamp examination shows map-like patches, dot-like microcysts and fingerprint lines in the epithelium","Many patients are asymptomatic, but it is a leading cause of recurrent corneal erosion syndrome","Recurrent erosions cause episodes of sharp pain, watering and photophobia, classically on waking","It can transiently blur vision and cause irregular astigmatism","Management targets recurrent erosions with lubrication and, if needed, procedures such as epithelial debridement or stromal micropuncture"],"pathophysiology":"Map-dot-fingerprint dystrophy is characterised by abnormal production and organisation of the epithelial basement membrane, with reduplicated membrane and intraepithelial cysts. Poor adhesion of the corneal epithelium to this faulty basement membrane predisposes to recurrent breakdown, so the epithelium detaches, producing recurrent corneal erosions. The abnormal membrane and microcysts create the characteristic map, dot and fingerprint patterns and can distort the overlying epithelial surface.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent corneal erosion from prior trauma","Meesmann corneal dystrophy","Dry eye disease","Epithelial infectious keratitis (during an acute erosion)","Other anterior corneal dystrophies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination, including retroillumination, for the characteristic patterns","Fluorescein staining to identify epithelial defects during erosions","Corneal topography if irregular astigmatism affects vision","History focused on recurrent erosion episodes","Exclusion of infection during an acute painful episode"],"redFlags":["Signs of infective keratitis complicating an erosion","Persistent or non-healing epithelial defect","Significant visual loss from irregular astigmatism or scarring","Frequent disabling erosion episodes"],"prognosis":"The dystrophy is benign and often asymptomatic; recurrent erosions can be troublesome but are usually controllable, and vision is generally preserved.","pitfalls":["Missing an infective keratitis during an acute erosion","Overlooking the dystrophy as the underlying cause of recurrent erosions","Under-treating erosions and allowing recurrent episodes","Attributing visual blur solely to refractive error"],"management":["Lubricate the ocular surface with topical tear substitutes and a hypertonic saline ointment at night to reduce recurrent erosions","Apply a bandage contact lens to promote healing during an acute recurrent erosion","Manage the acute erosion with lubrication, cycloplegia for pain and topical antibiotic prophylaxis, while excluding infection","Offer procedural treatment for refractory erosions such as epithelial debridement, anterior stromal micropuncture or phototherapeutic keratectomy","Reassure asymptomatic patients that the dystrophy is benign with generally preserved vision","Correct irregular astigmatism where it degrades vision","Follow up under ophthalmology and cornea services for recurrent erosion syndrome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","IC3D classification of corneal dystrophies"],"crossLinks":[]},"maple_syrup_urine_disease":{"name":"Maple Syrup Urine Disease","system":"Genetic-metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MSUD","branched-chain ketoaciduria","BCKD deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder caused by deficiency of the branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex, impairing breakdown of the branched-chain amino acids leucine, isoleucine, and valine.","The classic severe form presents in the first week of life with poor feeding, vomiting, lethargy, and worsening encephalopathy after protein feeding begins.","Neurological signs may include fluctuating tone, dystonic posturing, a boxing or bicycling movement pattern, and progression to seizures and coma.","The urine, sweat, or cerumen may carry a characteristic sweet, maple-syrup or burnt-sugar odour.","Leucine and its ketoacid accumulation is the principal driver of neurotoxicity and cerebral oedema.","Newborn screening detects elevated branched-chain amino acids; diagnosis is confirmed by the plasma amino acid pattern (notably raised leucine and the presence of alloisoleucine) and molecular testing.","Acute management centres on halting protein intake, promoting anabolism with adequate energy, and rapidly lowering branched-chain amino acid levels, with dialysis or haemofiltration in severe crises.","Long-term treatment is a carefully monitored diet restricting branched-chain amino acids while providing enough protein and energy for growth.","Intercurrent illness, fasting, or catabolic stress can precipitate metabolic crises even in previously stable, treated children."],"pathophysiology":"A defect in the branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex blocks the oxidative decarboxylation of the ketoacids derived from leucine, isoleucine, and valine. As a result these branched-chain amino acids and their corresponding ketoacids accumulate in blood, urine, and tissues. Leucine and its ketoacid are particularly neurotoxic, disturbing neurotransmitter balance, cerebral amino acid transport, and cellular osmolarity, which produces cerebral oedema and encephalopathy. During catabolic states, endogenous protein breakdown releases further branched-chain amino acids, precipitating acute, potentially life-threatening metabolic decompensation.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis","Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Urea cycle disorders","Organic acidemias","Nonketotic hyperglycinemia","Other causes of neonatal encephalopathy and seizures"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Newborn screening result and confirmatory plasma amino acid profile (elevated leucine, presence of alloisoleucine)","Urine organic acids and detection of branched-chain ketoacids","Molecular genetic testing of the BCKD complex genes","Blood gas, glucose, ammonia, and ketone assessment during crises","Neuroimaging if cerebral oedema is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive encephalopathy with lethargy, abnormal movements, and coma in a neonate","Signs of cerebral oedema and raised intracranial pressure","Metabolic crisis precipitated by intercurrent illness or fasting","Marked elevation of leucine requiring emergency clearance","Seizures or apnoea in a decompensating infant"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on how quickly the diagnosis is made and metabolic control is achieved; early treatment and avoidance of crises support near-normal development, whereas repeated or severe decompensations and prolonged high leucine levels risk permanent neurological damage. Lifelong dietary management and prompt sick-day protocols are essential.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early presentation for neonatal sepsis and delaying metabolic evaluation","Not recognising the characteristic sweet odour as a diagnostic clue","Continuing protein feeds during an acute crisis instead of stopping them and driving anabolism","Underestimating how rapidly catabolic stress can trigger dangerous decompensation","Failing to establish sick-day plans and long-term dietary monitoring"],"management":["Institute lifelong dietary management with branched-chain amino acid restriction using a leucine-restricted diet and a branched-chain-free medical formula, while providing sufficient isoleucine and valine","Provide an emergency regimen during illness or catabolism with high-energy, branched-chain-free intake to promote anabolism and prevent a leucine surge","Treat acute crisis by reversing catabolism with intravenous glucose and insulin, removing leucine, and using haemodialysis or haemofiltration for markedly raised leucine with encephalopathy","Manage cerebral oedema actively during decompensation","Monitor plasma amino acids, particularly leucine, to guide dietary adjustment","Trial thiamine in the thiamine-responsive variant","Coordinate care with a metabolic multidisciplinary team and dietitian, considering liver transplantation for difficult classic disease","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance with newborn screening and family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"marasmus":{"name":"Marasmus","system":"Nutrition / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["severe wasting","protein-energy malnutrition (non-oedematous)","energy malnutrition"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO nutrition guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Marked wasting of muscle and subcutaneous fat gives an emaciated, wizened appearance with an apparently large head and loose skin folds","Unlike kwashiorkor there is no oedema, and the child is often alert but irritable and ravenously hungry","It results from severe, prolonged deficiency of total energy intake, frequently compounded by chronic infection or feeding difficulties","It remains a significant problem among food-insecure young children in India and other low-resource settings","Hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, dehydration, and infection are common and dangerous accompaniments","Assessment relies on anthropometry, especially low weight-for-height and reduced mid-upper-arm circumference","Nutritional rehabilitation must be gradual to avoid refeeding complications"],"pathophysiology":"Marasmus is a form of severe acute malnutrition dominated by deficiency of overall energy intake. The body adapts to prolonged starvation by mobilising fat and muscle for fuel, lowering metabolic rate, and preserving visceral protein and albumin, which is why oedema is characteristically absent. Depletion of energy stores leads to profound wasting, immune suppression, gut mucosal atrophy, and impaired thermoregulation and glucose homeostasis. As with other forms of severe malnutrition, the depleted state creates vulnerability to hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, infection, and to metabolic disturbance during refeeding.","additionalDifferentials":["Kwashiorkor and marasmic-kwashiorkor","Wasting from chronic infection such as tuberculosis or HIV","Malabsorptive disorders","Malignancy and other chronic wasting illnesses","Congenital or metabolic disease causing failure to thrive"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anthropometry with weight-for-height and mid-upper-arm circumference","Blood glucose and body temperature to detect hypoglycaemia and hypothermia","Electrolytes including potassium, magnesium, and phosphate","Screening for underlying and intercurrent infection","Assessment for micronutrient deficiencies and anaemia"],"redFlags":["Hypoglycaemia and hypothermia in a severely wasted child","Signs of sepsis, which may be subtle","Circulatory compromise from severe dehydration","Refeeding-related electrolyte shifts during nutritional rehabilitation"],"prognosis":"Gradual nutritional rehabilitation with treatment of infection allows recovery, but delayed presentation, sepsis, or mismanaged refeeding carry a high mortality, and prolonged early malnutrition can impair growth and development.","pitfalls":["Refeeding too rapidly and precipitating refeeding syndrome or cardiac failure","Failing to detect and correct hypoglycaemia and hypothermia early","Overlooking an underlying chronic infection such as tuberculosis","Rehydrating aggressively and causing fluid and electrolyte imbalance"],"management":["Undertake cautious phased nutritional rehabilitation, beginning with low-energy stabilisation feeds before catch-up feeding using ready-to-use therapeutic food","Prevent and treat hypoglycaemia and hypothermia with frequent feeds and active warming","Rehydrate cautiously using an oral rehydration solution formulated for malnutrition, avoiding fluid overload and cardiac failure","Correct electrolyte deficits including potassium and magnesium, and provide micronutrients, vitamins and treatment of anaemia","Escalate feeds gradually and monitor for refeeding syndrome with attention to falling phosphate","Give empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics and screen for and treat intercurrent infection","Arrange multidisciplinary follow-up addressing the underlying cause, feeding practices and family food security"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO nutrition guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"marburg_multiple_sclerosis":{"name":"Marburg variant multiple sclerosis","system":"Nervous system / Neuroimmunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Marburg variant MS","acute malignant multiple sclerosis","fulminant multiple sclerosis","Marburg disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare fulminant demyelinating disorder causing rapidly progressive, often monophasic neurological decline over days to weeks","Widespread confluent lesions can produce hemiparesis, brainstem dysfunction, encephalopathy, and reduced consciousness","Tumefactive, mass-like lesions with oedema may raise concern for neoplasm on imaging","Considered the most aggressive end of the multiple sclerosis spectrum, with severe, destructive demyelination and axonal loss","Aggressive treatment with high-dose corticosteroids and plasma exchange is used, with escalation to potent immunotherapy in refractory disease","Antibody testing (aquaporin-4, MOG) helps distinguish it from other severe demyelinating disorders","Distinction from acute disseminated encephalomyelitis can be difficult, particularly early in the illness"],"pathophysiology":"The Marburg variant represents an especially severe form of inflammatory demyelination in which intense macrophage-rich inflammation causes extensive, confluent myelin destruction accompanied by prominent acute axonal injury and necrosis. The severity and rapidity of tissue damage exceed that of typical multiple sclerosis, and lesions are often large and swollen with surrounding oedema. The process is usually monophasic and can involve the hemispheres and brainstem, with brainstem involvement contributing to the potential for a fatal outcome.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Tumefactive demyelination","High-grade glioma or central nervous system lymphoma","Cerebral abscess or other infection","Aquaporin-4 or MOG antibody disease","Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain and spinal cord with contrast to define the extent and activity of lesions","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis including cell count and oligoclonal bands","Aquaporin-4 and MOG antibody testing","Brain biopsy when imaging cannot exclude tumour","Advanced MRI (spectroscopy, perfusion) to help separate demyelination from neoplasm","Screening for infective causes before intensive immunosuppression"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining consciousness or brainstem dysfunction","Large lesions with mass effect and raised intracranial pressure","Respiratory compromise from brainstem involvement","Failure to respond to corticosteroids requiring urgent escalation"],"prognosis":"The variant carries a guarded prognosis, historically often fatal within months, though aggressive modern immunotherapy has allowed survival and partial recovery in some patients.","pitfalls":["Delaying treatment while pursuing an assumed tumour diagnosis","Confusing it with monophasic acute disseminated encephalomyelitis and under-treating","Omitting antibody testing that would reclassify the disorder","Underestimating the risk of brainstem involvement and respiratory failure"],"management":["Treat acutely with high-dose intravenous corticosteroids such as methylprednisolone as first-line immunotherapy","Add plasma exchange for steroid-refractory fulminant disease","Escalate to potent immunosuppression such as cyclophosphamide in refractory cases","Provide neurocritical supportive care with airway and ventilatory support for brainstem involvement and management of raised intracranial pressure and oedema","Exclude infection and neoplasm before and during intensive immunosuppression, using brain biopsy where imaging cannot distinguish tumour","Deliver multidisciplinary neurorehabilitation with physiotherapy and occupational therapy","Involve neurology and neuroimmunology, testing aquaporin-4 and MOG antibodies to distinguish alternative demyelinating disorders and guide therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Continuum: Multiple Sclerosis and Related Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"marcus_gunn_jaw_winking":{"name":"Marcus Gunn jaw-winking synkinesis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Marcus Gunn phenomenon","jaw-winking ptosis","trigemino-oculomotor synkinesis","Marcus Gunn syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski / Taylor & Hoyt Paediatric Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital synkinesis in which a ptotic upper eyelid elevates on movement of the jaw, classically when the mouth opens or the jaw moves to the opposite side","Reflects anomalous connections between the motor branch of the trigeminal nerve supplying the pterygoid muscles and the levator palpebrae superioris","Usually unilateral and present from birth, often noticed during bottle or breast feeding as the lid flickers with sucking","The underlying ptosis in primary position must be assessed separately, as it determines amblyogenic risk and surgical planning","Amblyopia can arise from the ptosis itself, associated anisometropia or strabismus rather than the winking","The winking movement tends to persist even after ptosis surgery unless the synkinetic levator is addressed","For significant cases, surgery may involve levator disinsertion with frontalis suspension, sometimes bilaterally for symmetry"],"pathophysiology":"Marcus Gunn jaw-winking is a congenital trigemino-oculomotor synkinesis caused by aberrant innervation, in which fibres of the motor division of the trigeminal nerve destined for the pterygoid muscles are misdirected to the levator palpebrae superioris. As a result, activation of the jaw muscles simultaneously produces contraction of the levator, so the ptotic lid rises when the mouth opens or the jaw deviates. The baseline ptosis reflects the abnormal levator innervation, and the synkinetic elevation is an involuntary, stimulus-linked movement. Because the anomaly is at the level of nerve wiring, the winking is not abolished by conventional levator-strengthening surgery alone.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated congenital ptosis","Congenital third nerve palsy with aberrant regeneration","Congenital Horner syndrome","Inverse (Marin-Amat) synkinesis with lid closure on jaw movement","Myasthenic ptosis","Blepharophimosis syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Observation of lid movement during sucking, chewing or lateral jaw excursion","Measurement of levator function and ptosis in primary position","Cycloplegic refraction and amblyopia and strabismus assessment","Orthoptic evaluation for associated ocular deviation","Assessment of the excursion and stimulus of the wink to gauge severity","Preoperative planning of the affected levator and any bilateral surgery"],"redFlags":["Amblyopia from ptosis, anisometropia or strabismus needing prompt treatment","A large or cosmetically and socially troublesome wink causing functional impact","Associated ophthalmoplegia or pupil signs suggesting a third nerve process","Fatigable ptosis raising the possibility of myasthenia"],"prognosis":"The synkinesis is stable and benign, and outcomes are generally good when amblyopia is managed and surgery is appropriately selected, though residual asymmetry or persistence of the wink can occur if the synkinetic levator is not addressed.","pitfalls":["Correcting the ptosis without addressing the synkinetic levator, leaving a persistent wink","Missing associated amblyopia, anisometropia or strabismus","Failing to observe feeding or jaw movement and overlooking the diagnosis","Confusing it with aberrant third nerve regeneration"],"management":["Detect and treat amblyopia with refractive correction and occlusion therapy, as it drives long-term visual prognosis","Observe mild ptosis and winking where visual development is normal","Offer surgery for amblyogenic or cosmetically significant disease, typically levator excision or disinsection with a frontalis sling to abolish the synkinesis, often performed bilaterally for symmetry","Address the underlying ptosis in primary position and any associated strabismus or anisometropia","Perform cycloplegic refraction and orthoptic assessment to characterise associated deviation","Follow up under paediatric ophthalmology, recognising the wink persists after conventional ptosis surgery unless the synkinetic levator is addressed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Taylor & Hoyt's Paediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"marfan_syndrome":{"name":"Marfan syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FBN1-related Marfan syndrome","fibrillin-1 disorder","MFS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant disorder caused by pathogenic variants in FBN1, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-1; roughly a quarter of cases arise from de novo variants","Diagnosis rests on the revised Ghent criteria, which weight aortic root dilatation and ectopia lentis as cardinal features alongside a systemic score and family history","Skeletal features include disproportionately long limbs and digits (arachnodactyly), tall stature, pectus deformity, scoliosis, joint hypermobility, and a high-arched palate","Ectopia lentis (typically superotemporal lens dislocation) is a hallmark ocular finding and helps distinguish Marfan syndrome from Loeys-Dietz syndrome","Progressive aortic root dilatation at the sinuses of Valsalva predisposes to aortic dissection and is the principal cause of premature death","Lifelong surveillance with transthoracic echocardiography, blood-pressure control, and medical aortic protection (beta-blockers and/or angiotensin-receptor blockers) are central to management","Prophylactic aortic root surgery is considered once the aortic diameter reaches an at-risk threshold or shows rapid progression, individualised to body size and family history","Activity advice usually restricts high-intensity isometric exercise, competitive contact sport, and heavy lifting to reduce aortic wall stress"],"pathophysiology":"Marfan syndrome results from loss of normal fibrillin-1, a glycoprotein that assembles into microfibrils forming the scaffold of elastic fibres in the aorta, suspensory ligament of the lens, skin, and periosteum. Deficient or abnormal microfibrils weaken these connective tissues and also dysregulate transforming growth factor beta signalling, since microfibrils normally sequester latent TGF-beta. Excess TGF-beta activity contributes to progressive weakening and dilatation of the aortic media, degeneration of the zonular fibres causing lens subluxation, and overgrowth of the long bones. The combined structural and signalling defects explain the cardiovascular, ocular, skeletal, and dural manifestations.","additionalDifferentials":["Loeys-Dietz syndrome","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Homocystinuria (which can also cause ectopia lentis and a marfanoid habitus)","Congenital contractural arachnodactyly (Beals syndrome)","MASS phenotype and isolated familial thoracic aortic aneurysm","Familial ectopia lentis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography with aortic root measurement indexed to body size","Slit-lamp examination through a dilated pupil to detect ectopia lentis","Cross-sectional aortic imaging (CT or MR angiography) to assess the whole aorta","Molecular genetic testing of FBN1 and, where indicated, a wider aortopathy gene panel","Plasma homocysteine or urine amino acids to exclude homocystinuria","Imaging for dural ectasia and skeletal review for scoliosis and protrusio acetabuli"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe chest, back, or abdominal pain suggesting acute aortic dissection","Rapidly enlarging aortic root or diameter approaching the surgical threshold","New aortic regurgitation or signs of cardiac failure","Sudden painless visual loss suggesting retinal detachment or acute lens dislocation","Symptoms of pneumothorax such as acute breathlessness and pleuritic pain"],"prognosis":"With regular cardiovascular surveillance, medical aortic protection, and timely prophylactic surgery, life expectancy now approaches that of the general population. Untreated aortic disease carries a high risk of dissection and premature death, so adherence to monitoring and lifestyle advice is critical.","pitfalls":["Attributing tall marfanoid habitus to Marfan syndrome without excluding homocystinuria, which needs different management","Relying on a single aortic measurement rather than tracking the trend over time","Overlooking non-aortic vascular and ocular complications during follow-up","Failing to offer cascade family screening and genetic counselling","Assuming a normal childhood echocardiogram excludes future aortic disease"],"management":["Provide medical aortic protection with beta-blockers and/or angiotensin-receptor blockers to slow aortic root dilatation","Maintain lifelong cardiovascular surveillance with serial transthoracic echocardiography, blood-pressure control and periodic whole-aorta cross-sectional imaging","Advise activity modification, avoiding isometric exertion and high-intensity contact or competitive sport","Undertake prophylactic aortic root surgery when the root reaches the surgical threshold or shows rapid growth or a family history of dissection","Manage acute aortic dissection as an emergency with heart-rate and blood-pressure control and immediate surgery for type A dissection","Provide ophthalmic care for ectopia lentis with refractive correction and amblyopia management","Monitor and manage skeletal features including scoliosis and pectus deformity and treat symptomatic dural ectasia","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant FBN1 disease with cascade family screening and pre-pregnancy counselling with close monitoring in pregnancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Marfan syndrome","Revised Ghent nosology for the Marfan syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"marine_ciguatera_poisoning":{"name":"Ciguatera fish poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ciguatera","Ciguatoxin poisoning","CFP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest fish-borne, non-bacterial poisoning worldwide, acquired by eating tropical and subtropical reef fish.","Ciguatoxins originate in the dinoflagellate Gambierdiscus and bioaccumulate up the food chain, so large predatory reef fish such as barracuda, grouper, snapper and moray eel carry the highest risk.","The toxins are heat- and acid-stable, so cooking does not make the fish safe, and affected fish look, smell and taste normal.","Illness typically evolves through early gastrointestinal upset, then neurological and, in severe cases, cardiovascular features.","A characteristic symptom is altered temperature sensation, often described as cold allodynia or paradoxical temperature reversal, with dysaesthesiae of the lips and extremities.","Bradycardia and hypotension may occur in severe poisoning and warrant close cardiovascular monitoring.","Symptoms can persist for weeks to months and characteristically recur with alcohol, caffeine, exercise or further fish consumption.","Diagnosis is clinical and dietary; ciguatoxin is not detected by routine laboratory tests."],"pathophysiology":"Ciguatoxins are lipid-soluble polyether toxins that bind a neurotoxin receptor on the voltage-gated sodium channel, shifting activation towards more negative potentials and impairing inactivation, so channels open at or near the resting membrane potential. The resulting sustained depolarisation with spontaneous, repetitive firing across sensory, motor and autonomic fibres — together with nodal and Schwann cell oedema — underlies the paraesthesiae, temperature dysperception, autonomic instability and gastrointestinal hypermotility. A related dinoflagellate toxin, maitotoxin, promotes calcium influx and may contribute to toxicity. Because the toxins are heat- and acid-stable and fat-soluble, they survive cooking and gastric acid and accumulate in fish tissue, biomagnifying in larger, older, predatory reef species that sit high in the food chain.","additionalDifferentials":["Scombroid (histamine) fish poisoning","Paralytic shellfish poisoning from saxitoxin","Neurotoxic shellfish poisoning from brevetoxin","Pufferfish tetrodotoxin poisoning","Organophosphate poisoning","Guillain-Barre syndrome","Acute viral or bacterial gastroenteritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful dietary and travel history, including the species and source of the fish","Electrocardiography and cardiac monitoring for bradyarrhythmia","Serum electrolytes and renal function","Assessment of hydration status after vomiting and diarrhoea","Clinical exclusion of other seafood toxin syndromes","Specialised assay of any leftover fish for ciguatoxin where available"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic bradycardia or hypotension","Respiratory difficulty or progressive weakness","Marked dehydration from vomiting and diarrhoea","Neurological deterioration"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover over days to weeks, although sensory symptoms may linger for months and relapse with dietary and other triggers; fatalities are rare.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the illness as ordinary gastroenteritis and missing the neurological clues.","Confusing it with scombroid poisoning, which is histamine-mediated and responds to antihistamines.","Assuming that cooking or freezing the fish removes the risk.","Failing to counsel patients to avoid alcohol, caffeine and fish, which can precipitate relapse."],"management":["Provide supportive care with rehydration and correction of electrolyte disturbance following vomiting and diarrhoea","Monitor cardiovascular status and treat bradycardia and hypotension with atropine and fluids or vasopressors","Consider intravenous mannitol for acute neurological symptoms, recognising the evidence is debated and benefit greatest early","Relieve painful dysaesthesiae and cold allodynia symptomatically with agents such as gabapentinoids or amitriptyline","Advise avoidance of fish, alcohol, caffeine and nuts during recovery to prevent relapse of symptoms","Note there is no specific antitoxin, report to public health and identify the implicated fish","Involve toxicology and emergency medicine for severe or protracted cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical toxicology references","Marine poisoning and envenomation references","Emergency medicine references"],"crossLinks":[]},"marine_envenomation_general":{"name":"Marine envenomation (general approach)","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["marine sting general management","aquatic envenomation approach","sea creature sting","venomous marine animal injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / WHO envenoming guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Broadly divide marine injuries into nematocyst stings (jellyfish and relatives) and venomous puncture wounds (fish spines, stingrays, urchins, cone snails)","For heat-stable venoms of many venomous fish, sea urchins and stingrays, hot-water immersion at a tolerable temperature relieves pain","For jellyfish stings, decontamination depends on species: vinegar is used for tropical box jellyfish/cubozoans, whereas indiscriminate use in other species may be unhelpful, and fresh water and rubbing should be avoided","Puncture wounds commonly retain spine or barb fragments, so imaging and wound exploration and irrigation are important","Marine wounds are prone to infection by marine organisms such as Vibrio species, so wound care, tetanus prophylaxis and vigilance for infection are essential","A small number of syndromes are rapidly life-threatening (Chironex cardiotoxicity, Irukandji cardiac failure, tetrodotoxin paralysis from blue-ringed octopus, sea snake neuro/myotoxicity), so resuscitation and monitoring come first","Specific antivenoms exist for a few marine species (box jellyfish, stonefish, sea snake); most stings are managed supportively"],"pathophysiology":"Marine venoms are delivered by two main mechanisms. Cnidarians (jellyfish, anemones, corals) fire nematocysts that inject venom on contact, with effects ranging from local pain and dermatitis to systemic cardiotoxicity or catecholamine-driven syndromes. Venomous fish, stingrays, sea urchins and cone snails introduce venom through spines, barbs or a radular tooth, causing intense local pain, tissue injury and, in some species, systemic neurotoxic or cardiovascular effects. Many fish and echinoderm venoms are heat-labile proteins, explaining the benefit of hot-water immersion, whereas cnidarian nematocyst discharge is influenced by osmotic and chemical changes, which underpins species-specific decontamination.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-venomous marine laceration or abrasion with infection","Allergic or anaphylactic reaction to marine antigen","Retained foreign body without envenomation","Decompression illness or immersion-related collapse in a diver","Seafood-borne toxin illness (ciguatera, scombroid, shellfish toxins)","Skin infection such as Vibrio, Mycobacterium marinum or Erysipelothrix"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wound imaging (ultrasound or radiography) for retained spines or barbs","Neurovascular assessment of the affected part","Electrocardiography and monitoring where systemic toxicity is possible","Inflammatory markers and wound cultures if infection is suspected","Creatine kinase, renal function and haemolysis markers in significant systemic envenomation","Identification of the responsible species where possible to guide specific therapy"],"redFlags":["Cardiovascular collapse or arrhythmia after a jellyfish sting","Progressive paralysis or respiratory failure","Delayed severe systemic syndrome after a trivial sting (Irukandji)","Spreading soft-tissue infection or systemic sepsis","Compartment syndrome or extensive necrosis around a puncture wound"],"prognosis":"Most marine envenomations cause self-limiting local pain and heal well with first aid and wound care. A minority of species cause life-threatening systemic toxicity, where outcome depends on prompt resuscitation, appropriate decontamination and, where available, species-specific antivenom.","pitfalls":["Applying a single decontamination method to all jellyfish stings rather than species-appropriate treatment","Rinsing cnidarian stings with fresh water or rubbing them, provoking further nematocyst discharge","Missing retained spine fragments and marine wound infection during follow-up","Focusing on local wound care while overlooking rapidly evolving systemic toxicity"],"management":["Prioritise resuscitation and monitoring for rapidly life-threatening syndromes such as box jellyfish cardiotoxicity, Irukandji syndrome, tetrodotoxin paralysis and sea snake envenomation, securing airway, breathing and circulation first","Apply species-specific decontamination, using vinegar to inhibit cubozoan nematocyst discharge while avoiding fresh water and rubbing","Immerse in hot water at a tolerable temperature for the heat-labile venoms of venomous fish, stingrays and sea urchins","Explore, irrigate and image puncture wounds for retained spines or barbs and remove foreign material","Administer specific antivenom where available and indicated, as for box jellyfish, stonefish and sea snake envenomation","Prevent infection with wound care covering marine organisms including Vibrio species, and give tetanus prophylaxis","Provide analgesia and escalate to critical care for systemic toxicity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Australian/international marine envenomation management guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"marjolin_ulcer":{"name":"Marjolin ulcer","system":"Skin and subcutaneous tissue / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Marjolin's ulcer","malignant transformation of a chronic wound","scar carcinoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Dermatology / plastic surgery oncology texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Marjolin ulcer is a malignancy arising within chronically inflamed, scarred or ulcerated skin, most commonly a well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma, though basal cell carcinoma, melanoma and sarcoma are also reported","Classic settings include old burn scars, chronic venous or pressure ulcers, and the sinus tracts of chronic osteomyelitis, typically after a long latency of years to decades","Malignant transformation should be suspected when a chronic wound changes, developing everted or rolled margins, exuberant granulation, new nodularity, increased pain, bleeding, malodour or failure to heal despite optimal treatment","Because malignant change may be focal, diagnosis requires multiple deep biopsies taken from the margin and base of the suspicious wound","Squamous cell carcinomas arising in burn scars tend to behave more aggressively, with a higher risk of nodal metastasis than typical sun-related cutaneous carcinoma","Staging includes careful examination of the regional lymph nodes and cross-sectional imaging where deep invasion or metastasis is suspected","Treatment is wide local excision to clear margins with management of regional nodes as indicated and appropriate reconstruction, with radiotherapy or systemic therapy as adjuncts in advanced disease","Long-standing wounds and unstable scars should be kept under surveillance and biopsied promptly if they change, since delayed diagnosis worsens outcome"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic tissue injury with repeated cycles of ulceration and repair creates an environment of persistent inflammation, sustained cellular proliferation and impaired local immune surveillance within scarred, poorly vascularised tissue. Over a prolonged latent period, accumulated genetic damage in chronically regenerating keratinocytes, together with the tumour-permissive inflammatory milieu and relative isolation of the scar from immune effector cells, drives malignant transformation, most often to squamous cell carcinoma. The dense scar may also facilitate invasion once malignancy develops and can contribute to the aggressive behaviour seen in some lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic non-healing venous, arterial, pressure or neuropathic ulcer without malignancy","Chronic osteomyelitis with a discharging sinus","Pyoderma gangrenosum","Hypertrophic scar or keloid","Primary cutaneous squamous or basal cell carcinoma unrelated to a wound","Chronic cutaneous infection, including atypical mycobacterial or deep fungal disease","Other cutaneous malignancy such as melanoma or sarcoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Multiple deep incisional or wedge biopsies from the ulcer margin and base for histology","Regional lymph node examination and ultrasound with fine-needle aspiration or core biopsy of abnormal nodes","Cross-sectional imaging with CT or MRI to assess local invasion and metastasis","Imaging for underlying osteomyelitis where a sinus is present","Histopathological assessment of excision margins","Staging investigations as directed by the multidisciplinary team"],"redFlags":["A chronic wound that fails to heal despite optimal care","New nodularity, everted edges, bleeding or rapid change in a long-standing ulcer or scar","Palpable regional lymphadenopathy","A wound arising within an old burn scar or a chronic osteomyelitis sinus"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on tumour type, differentiation, depth of invasion and nodal status; well-differentiated lesions detected early can be cured by excision, but nodal metastasis, notably with burn-scar carcinomas, markedly worsens outcome.","pitfalls":["Attributing non-healing solely to the underlying ulcer and delaying biopsy","Taking a single superficial biopsy that misses focal malignancy","Underestimating the aggressiveness of burn-scar carcinomas","Overlooking the regional lymph nodes","Performing an excision with inadequate margins"],"management":["Confirm malignancy with multiple deep biopsies from the margin and base of the wound before definitive treatment","Undertake wide local excision of the tumour, most often a squamous cell carcinoma, with clear margins and appropriate reconstruction","Assess regional lymph nodes and perform node dissection for proven or suspected nodal metastasis, given the aggressive behaviour of burn-scar carcinoma","Give adjuvant radiotherapy for high-risk features, positive margins or nodal disease","Consider amputation for extensive limb tumours or malignant transformation complicating chronic osteomyelitis","Maintain surveillance for local recurrence and metastasis after treatment","Prevent progression by managing chronic non-healing wounds and biopsying long-standing scars or ulcers that change","Direct staging and treatment through an oncology multidisciplinary team spanning plastic surgery, oncology and dermatology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Plastic and reconstructive surgery oncology reference texts","National guidance on the management of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"markesbery_griggs_myopathy":{"name":"Markesbery-Griggs distal myopathy (ZASP/LDB3)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Markesbery-Griggs late-onset distal myopathy","ZASP-related myopathy","LDB3 myopathy","zaspopathy","myofibrillar myopathy type 4","MFM4"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant late-onset distal myopathy caused by variants in ZASP (encoded by LDB3), a Z-disc protein, and classified within the myofibrillar myopathies","Onset is typically after the fourth or fifth decade, with distal weakness commonly beginning in the lower legs, and both anterior and posterior compartment involvement may occur","As a myofibrillar myopathy it may be accompanied by cardiomyopathy, cardiac conduction disease, and peripheral neuropathy, so cardiac and neurological assessment is important","Weakness gradually extends to more proximal muscles over time, but progression is generally slow","Creatine kinase is normal or mildly to moderately elevated and electromyography shows myopathic changes, sometimes with irritable or neurogenic-appearing features","Muscle biopsy shows the myofibrillar pathology hallmark of Z-disc streaming with abnormal accumulation of proteins such as desmin and myotilin, often with rimmed vacuoles","The ZASP/LDB3 gene overlaps phenotypically with other myofibrillar myopathy genes, so a broad panel is often needed","Diagnosis integrates the distal myopathy pattern, myofibrillar biopsy features, and LDB3 genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Markesbery-Griggs myopathy is a myofibrillar myopathy caused by dominant variants in LDB3, which encodes the Z-band alternatively spliced PDZ-motif protein (ZASP). ZASP stabilises the sarcomeric Z-disc through interactions with alpha-actinin and other cytoskeletal proteins. Mutant ZASP disrupts Z-disc integrity, leading to disintegration of myofibrils, streaming of Z-disc material, and abnormal aggregation of cytoskeletal proteins including desmin. This myofibrillar disorganisation produces progressive muscle weakness and, because the same proteins are important in cardiac muscle and nerve, predisposes to associated cardiomyopathy, conduction disease, and neuropathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Other myofibrillar myopathies (desmin, myotilin, filamin C, BAG3, crystallin-related)","Inclusion body myositis","Other distal myopathies (Udd, Welander, Laing)","Myotonic dystrophy","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","Chronic acquired neuropathy contributing to distal weakness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography","Muscle biopsy with immunohistochemistry for desmin, myotilin, and other myofibrillar markers","Muscle MRI to map the pattern of involvement","Cardiac evaluation with ECG and echocardiography given the myofibrillar cardiac risk","Genetic testing with an LDB3-inclusive myofibrillar myopathy panel"],"redFlags":["Cardiomyopathy or conduction block requiring cardiac monitoring and possible device therapy","Respiratory muscle weakness with declining vital capacity","Rapidly progressive weakness suggesting an alternative or overlapping diagnosis","New arrhythmia or syncope in a patient or affected relative"],"prognosis":"Skeletal muscle weakness progresses slowly over years, but overall prognosis depends heavily on associated cardiac involvement; unrecognised cardiomyopathy or conduction disease can be life-limiting, making cardiac surveillance central to management.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for and monitor the cardiac complications typical of myofibrillar myopathies","Misdiagnosing it as inclusion body myositis on the basis of rimmed vacuoles alone","Relying on a narrow genetic test rather than a broad myofibrillar panel given phenotypic overlap","Overlooking respiratory involvement in later disease"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists; care centres on regular physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain function and prevent contractures in the progressively weak distal muscles","Ankle-foot orthoses and mobility aids for foot drop and distal lower-limb weakness","Regular cardiac surveillance with ECG and echocardiography for the myofibrillar cardiomyopathy and conduction disease, with timely pacemaker or implantable defibrillator for conduction block or arrhythmia","Respiratory function monitoring with non-invasive ventilation if respiratory muscle involvement develops","Screen for and manage the associated peripheral neuropathy","Autosomal dominant inheritance: offer genetic counselling and cascade screening, including cardiac screening, of at-risk relatives","Coordinate care through a specialist neuromuscular multidisciplinary team with cardiology input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: Myofibrillar myopathy (LDB3/ZASP-related)"],"crossLinks":[]},"maroteaux_lamy_syndrome":{"name":"Maroteaux-Lamy syndrome (MPS VI)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPS VI","mucopolysaccharidosis type VI","arylsulfatase B deficiency","N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase deficiency","ARSB deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Scriver's OMMBID","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Produces prominent somatic disease, including dysostosis multiplex, coarse facies, and organomegaly, while intelligence is characteristically preserved","Caused by deficiency of arylsulfatase B (N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase), impairing dermatan sulfate degradation","Dermatan sulfate is the main glycosaminoglycan that accumulates and is excreted, with no heparan sulfate elevation","Corneal clouding, cardiac valve disease, and carpal tunnel syndrome are common","Severity spans a wide spectrum from rapidly progressive infantile disease to slowly progressive attenuated forms","Cervical cord compression from dural thickening and atlantoaxial changes is an important complication","Diagnosis rests on deficient arylsulfatase B activity with isolated urinary dermatan sulfate and ARSB genotyping"],"pathophysiology":"Maroteaux-Lamy syndrome results from biallelic pathogenic variants in ARSB, causing deficiency of arylsulfatase B (also called N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase). This enzyme is required for the lysosomal degradation of dermatan sulfate, so its deficiency leads to accumulation of dermatan sulfate within lysosomes of connective tissue, cartilage, bone, cornea, heart valves, and meninges. The resulting multisystem storage produces the characteristic skeletal, ocular, and cardiac disease. Because heparan sulfate metabolism is unaffected and the brain is relatively spared, cognition is typically normal despite severe somatic involvement. Excess dermatan sulfate is excreted in the urine. Inheritance is autosomal recessive, and residual enzyme activity correlates broadly with disease severity.","additionalDifferentials":["Hurler and Hurler-Scheie syndromes (MPS I)","Hunter syndrome (MPS II)","Morquio syndrome (MPS IV)","Mucolipidoses with skeletal and connective tissue features","Multiple sulfatase deficiency","Other storage disorders with coarse facies and organomegaly"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Leucocyte arylsulfatase B enzyme assay with confirmation against another sulfatase to exclude multiple sulfatase deficiency","Quantitative urinary glycosaminoglycan analysis showing isolated dermatan sulfate","ARSB gene sequencing","Echocardiography for valvular assessment","Cervical spine imaging for atlantoaxial instability and dural thickening","Slit-lamp examination and audiology for ocular and hearing involvement"],"redFlags":["Cervical cord compression from dural thickening or atlantoaxial instability","Progressive cardiac valve dysfunction and heart failure","Airway obstruction and anaesthetic difficulty","Rapid corneal clouding threatening vision"],"prognosis":"Outlook varies widely with disease severity; rapidly progressive forms cause early cardiorespiratory and skeletal complications, while attenuated forms allow survival into adulthood. Enzyme replacement therapy improves endurance and some somatic manifestations but does not reverse established skeletal or corneal disease.","pitfalls":["Confusing the enzyme deficiency with multiple sulfatase deficiency without confirmatory testing","Failing to evaluate the cervical spine before anaesthesia","Assuming preserved intellect excludes a mucopolysaccharidosis","Overlooking silent valvular heart disease requiring surveillance"],"management":["Enzyme replacement therapy with recombinant arylsulfatase B (galsulfase) as the disease-specific mainstay to improve endurance and reduce dermatan sulfate storage","Consider haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in selected severe early-onset cases","Surveillance echocardiography for valvular heart disease, with valve repair or replacement when indicated","Cervical spine imaging and monitoring for atlantoaxial instability and dural thickening, with decompression or stabilisation surgery for cervical cord compression","Manage somatic complications: carpal tunnel release, hip and other orthopaedic surgery, corneal grafting for visual loss, and hearing aids or grommets for hearing loss","Careful peri-operative airway assessment given difficult intubation and cervical instability","Autosomal recessive: offer genetic counselling and family screening","Coordinate multidisciplinary metabolic, cardiac, orthopaedic, ophthalmic and ENT care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"marshall_smith_syndrome":{"name":"Marshall-Smith syndrome (NFIX)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NFIX","MSS","Marshall-Smith","nuclear factor I X-related syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare disorder marked by strikingly accelerated osseous (bone) maturation from early infancy despite failure to thrive","Respiratory compromise from a small, unstable upper airway (choanal atresia, laryngomalacia, glossoptosis) is the leading cause of morbidity","Facial features include a prominent forehead, proptosis with shallow orbits, a depressed nasal bridge and micrognathia","Developmental impairment is usually severe, with hypotonia and later kyphoscoliosis","Caused by de novo heterozygous variants in NFIX, a transcription factor of the nuclear factor I family regulating skeletal and neural development","Distinct NFIX variants cause Malan (Sotos-like overgrowth) syndrome, so the same gene produces two contrasting phenotypes","Advanced bone age with poor somatic growth is a paradoxical and characteristic clue"],"pathophysiology":"NFIX encodes a nuclear factor I transcription factor that regulates the expression of genes controlling the proliferation and differentiation of skeletal and neural progenitor cells during development. In Marshall-Smith syndrome, specific variants (often affecting one region of the gene) are thought to escape the normal degradation of aberrant transcripts and exert a dominant effect, dysregulating skeletal maturation and neural development. This produces the paradoxical combination of markedly advanced bone age with poor growth, together with severe developmental impairment and craniofacial and airway anomalies. Different NFIX variants that cause loss of function instead produce the overgrowth-type Malan syndrome, illustrating variant-specific mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Malan (NFIX-related overgrowth) syndrome","Weaver syndrome","Sotos syndrome","Marshall syndrome (distinct, COL11A1-related)","Other syndromes with advanced bone age","Congenital airway malformation syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bone-age radiograph and skeletal survey demonstrating advanced maturation","Airway evaluation including endoscopy for laryngomalacia and choanal patency","Sleep and respiratory assessment","Brain MRI and developmental evaluation","Targeted NFIX sequencing or a gene panel or exome sequencing"],"redFlags":["Upper airway obstruction and respiratory failure","Recurrent aspiration and feeding failure","Apnoea or desaturation events","Progressive kyphoscoliosis affecting respiration"],"prognosis":"Marshall-Smith syndrome carries a guarded prognosis, with early mortality historically driven by airway and respiratory complications; survivors have severe developmental impairment. Proactive airway and respiratory management can improve survival, and care is supportive and multidisciplinary.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the airway risk and its impact on survival","Confusing it with the overgrowth-type Malan syndrome caused by other NFIX variants","Mislabelling advanced bone age as an overgrowth disorder despite poor somatic growth","Delaying respiratory assessment in an infant with failure to thrive"],"management":["No disease-specific therapy; care is supportive and centred on securing the airway","Proactive upper-airway management for choanal atresia, laryngomalacia and glossoptosis, including choanal surgery, CPAP or tracheostomy where needed","Respiratory support and treatment of recurrent respiratory infections, with surveillance for sleep-disordered breathing","Nutritional support for failure to thrive, including gastrostomy feeding where oral intake is inadequate","Early developmental support and therapies for the severe developmental impairment and hypotonia","Orthopaedic monitoring and management of kyphoscoliosis","Usually de novo: offer genetic counselling with parental testing, as recurrence risk is generally low","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary team including ENT, respiratory, genetics and developmental paediatrics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: NFIX-Related Disorders","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"martorell_ulcer":{"name":"Martorell ulcer","system":"Skin and subcutaneous tissue / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Martorell hypertensive ischaemic leg ulcer","hypertensive ischaemic leg ulcer","Martorell's ulcer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Dermatology / EWMA wound guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Martorell ulcer is a painful ischaemic skin ulcer caused by subcutaneous arteriolosclerosis, arising almost exclusively in patients with long-standing, often poorly controlled arterial hypertension and frequently coexisting type 2 diabetes","It characteristically affects the dorsolateral aspect of the lower leg or the region overlying the Achilles tendon, and is bilateral in a substantial proportion of cases","The pain is severe and disproportionate to ulcer size; lesions begin as a livid or purpuric patch that rapidly breaks down into a necrotic ulcer with an irregular, often serpiginous, advancing border","Peripheral pulses are typically preserved and the ankle-brachial index is normal, which helps distinguish it from large-vessel atherosclerotic arterial ulceration","Diagnosis rests on the combination of hypertension or diabetes, the characteristic site and disproportionate pain, and a deep wedge biopsy from the ulcer edge showing arteriolosclerosis with medial calcification and subintimal proliferation","It is frequently mistaken for pyoderma gangrenosum, and inappropriate immunosuppression may worsen the wound and delay correct treatment","Management centres on optimising blood pressure control, meticulous wound care with debridement of necrotic tissue, and adequate analgesia, while avoiding strongly vasoconstricting agents where feasible","Early split-skin grafting, sometimes aided by negative-pressure wound therapy, can markedly reduce pain and accelerate healing"],"pathophysiology":"The primary lesion in Martorell ulcer lies in the subcutaneous small arteries and arterioles, which develop medial hypertrophy and calcification together with subintimal (myointimal) proliferation as a consequence of chronic arterial hypertension and diabetic microvascular disease. Progressive luminal narrowing of these vessels critically reduces dermal and subcutaneous perfusion, so that a comparatively minor rise in local tissue pressure or trivial trauma produces a cutaneous infarct. Because the occlusive process is confined to the microcirculation, the large conduit arteries remain patent and pulses are preserved, yet the ischaemic skin necroses and ulcerates, extending as the surrounding arterioles are progressively compromised.","additionalDifferentials":["Pyoderma gangrenosum","Atherosclerotic (large-vessel) arterial ulcer","Calciphylaxis (calcific uraemic arteriolopathy)","Vasculitic ulcer, such as in polyarteritis nodosa or other systemic vasculitis","Venous leg ulcer","Cholesterol (atheroembolic) embolisation to the skin","Ulcerated necrobiosis lipoidica"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Deep incisional or wedge biopsy taken from the ulcer edge for histology demonstrating subcutaneous arteriolosclerosis","Ankle-brachial index and arterial duplex ultrasound to exclude significant large-vessel disease","Blood pressure assessment, including ambulatory monitoring, to gauge control","Glycaemic assessment with glucose and glycated haemoglobin","Screen for calciphylaxis with calcium, phosphate, parathyroid hormone and renal function","Autoimmune and vasculitis screen to exclude mimics","Wound tissue culture if secondary infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging necrotic ulcer with pain out of proportion to its appearance","Signs of secondary wound infection or systemic sepsis","Features suggesting calciphylaxis, such as renal failure or dialysis dependence with painful skin necrosis","Exposure of deep structures such as tendon within the wound base"],"prognosis":"Martorell ulcers are typically slow to heal and intensely painful, but with good blood pressure control, meticulous wound care and early skin grafting many will heal; recurrence and involvement of the contralateral leg are common.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the ulcer as pyoderma gangrenosum and treating with immunosuppression","Attributing the ulcer to large-vessel arterial disease despite normal pulses and ankle pressures","Providing inadequate analgesia for a characteristically severe pain","Taking a superficial biopsy that fails to sample the deep arteriolar pathology","Relying on strongly vasoconstricting antihypertensive agents that may impair skin perfusion"],"management":["Optimise blood pressure control with antihypertensive therapy as the key disease-modifying step targeting the underlying arteriolosclerosis","Optimise glycaemic control in coexisting diabetes","Effective analgesia, often including opioids, for the characteristically severe, disproportionate pain","Meticulous moist wound care with debridement of necrotic tissue","Early split-skin grafting, which accelerates healing and provides pain relief","Avoid inappropriate immunosuppression intended for pyoderma gangrenosum, which worsens the ischaemic wound","Monitor the contralateral leg for recurrence and modify cardiovascular risk factors, with combined dermatology and vascular surgery input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","European Wound Management Association guidance on atypical wounds","Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy"],"crossLinks":[]},"massive_ovarian_edema":{"name":"Massive ovarian oedema","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["massive ovarian edema","massive oedema of the ovary","ovarian stromal oedema"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology reference textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, benign condition of young women and adolescents in which one ovary becomes markedly enlarged by stromal oedema","It is thought to result from partial or intermittent torsion around the mesovarium that obstructs venous and lymphatic drainage while sparing arterial inflow","Presentation is with pelvic or abdominal pain and a palpable mass, closely mimicking ovarian torsion or a neoplasm","Imaging shows a diffusely enlarged, oedematous ovary, often with follicles displaced to the periphery and central stromal oedema","Magnetic resonance imaging demonstrates a strikingly oedematous stroma that is high in signal on T2-weighted sequences","Tumour markers are usually normal, which helps to distinguish it from a true ovarian neoplasm","Fertility-sparing surgery, such as detorsion with ovarian fixation, is preferred over oophorectomy in these young patients"],"pathophysiology":"Partial or intermittent torsion of the ovary about its vascular pedicle in the mesovarium obstructs the lower-pressure venous and lymphatic outflow while the arterial supply continues to fill the organ. Fluid therefore accumulates within the ovarian stroma, producing progressive, sometimes massive, oedematous enlargement of the ovary. The resulting mass can closely resemble a neoplasm both clinically and radiologically, yet the underlying process is benign and potentially reversible if recognised and treated conservatively.","additionalDifferentials":["Ovarian torsion","Ovarian neoplasm such as a fibroma","Ovarian fibromatosis","Tubo-ovarian abscess","Metastatic ovarian tumour","Lymphomatous involvement of the ovary"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal or transabdominal ultrasound with Doppler showing an enlarged ovary with peripheral follicles","Magnetic resonance imaging demonstrating stromal oedema with high T2 signal","Tumour markers, which are typically normal and help exclude malignancy","Assessment of arterial and venous flow to evaluate the degree of torsion","Histological examination if the ovary is removed, confirming stromal oedema without neoplasia"],"redFlags":["Complete torsion with compromised ovarian viability","Inability to exclude malignancy, risking unnecessary oophorectomy","Bilateral involvement","Acute severe pain with signs of an ischaemic ovary"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign with a good prognosis; ovary-sparing surgery preserves ovarian function and fertility, and outcomes are excellent when the diagnosis is recognised before radical surgery is undertaken.","pitfalls":["Performing an unnecessary oophorectomy in a young woman by mistaking oedema for a tumour","Failing to consider fertility preservation when planning surgery","Overlooking intermittent torsion as the underlying mechanism","Not recognising the characteristic imaging appearance of stromal oedema"],"management":["Ovary-sparing surgery (detorsion with wedge resection or biopsy) to preserve ovarian function and fertility, avoiding unnecessary oophorectomy","Intra-operative frozen-section histology to confirm the benign oedematous process and exclude neoplasm before deciding the extent of surgery","Oophoropexy (fixation) to reduce the risk of recurrent torsion, given intermittent torsion as the underlying mechanism","Analgesia and supportive care for the acute pelvic pain","Postoperative imaging follow-up to confirm resolution and monitor ovarian recovery","Refer to gynaecology, with fertility-preservation counselling in young women and adolescents"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference textbook","Peer-reviewed gynaecology clinical reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"massive_transfusion":{"name":"Massive transfusion","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["massive transfusion protocol","MTP","massive haemorrhage protocol"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AABB Technical Manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Commonly defined as replacement of roughly one whole blood volume within about 24 hours, or transfusion of around ten or more red cell units in that period; rapid-loss definitions (several units within an hour) are also used in trauma","Modern resuscitation favours a balanced ratio of red cells, plasma, and platelets that approximates whole blood rather than red cells alone, to limit dilutional coagulopathy","The lethal triad of hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy compounds bleeding and must be actively prevented with warmed fluids and blood products","Citrate anticoagulant in stored products binds calcium, so ionised hypocalcaemia is common during rapid transfusion and should be monitored and corrected","Hyperkalaemia can occur, especially with older stored red cells and rapid infusion in neonates or patients with renal impairment","Point-of-care viscoelastic testing (thromboelastography or ROTEM) helps guide targeted product replacement in preference to fixed ratios once bleeding is controlled","Tranexamic acid given early in traumatic haemorrhage reduces mortality; early activation of an institutional protocol streamlines product delivery"],"pathophysiology":"Massive haemorrhage and its replacement produce a combination of dilutional and consumptive coagulopathy: clotting factors and platelets are lost with bleeding and diluted by crystalloid and red cell replacement. Hypoperfusion drives metabolic acidosis and, together with infusion of cold products, causes hypothermia; both impair enzymatic clotting and platelet function. Citrate load from banked products chelates ionised calcium and magnesium, further impairing coagulation and cardiac contractility, while potassium leaked from stored red cells can accumulate. The result is a self-perpetuating cycle of bleeding, impaired haemostasis, and shock unless actively interrupted.","additionalDifferentials":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Pre-existing anticoagulant or antiplatelet effect","Inherited or acquired factor deficiency","Hyperfibrinolysis","Surgical or mechanical bleeding requiring intervention"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial full blood count, prothrombin time/INR, activated partial thromboplastin time, and fibrinogen","Ionised calcium, potassium, and arterial blood gas with lactate and pH","Viscoelastic haemostatic assays (TEG/ROTEM) for goal-directed replacement","Core temperature monitoring","Type and crossmatch with repeat sampling to detect ongoing loss"],"redFlags":["Ongoing uncontrolled haemorrhage with haemodynamic instability requiring emergency surgical or interventional haemostasis","Refractory hypocalcaemia with cardiovascular compromise during rapid transfusion","Rising potassium with electrocardiographic changes","Persistent coagulopathy despite balanced product resuscitation, suggesting DIC or hyperfibrinolysis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends chiefly on control of the bleeding source and speed of resuscitation; early balanced transfusion, prevention of the lethal triad, and timely antifibrinolytic therapy improve survival, whereas delayed correction of coagulopathy and shock markedly increases mortality.","pitfalls":["Over-reliance on crystalloid resuscitation, worsening dilutional coagulopathy and tissue oedema","Neglecting patient and fluid warming, allowing hypothermia-driven coagulopathy","Forgetting to monitor and replace ionised calcium during rapid citrate exposure","Continuing fixed high-ratio product delivery after bleeding is controlled rather than switching to laboratory-guided replacement"],"management":["Activate a major haemorrhage protocol with early, balanced transfusion of red cells, plasma and platelets approximating whole blood","Achieve rapid definitive control of the bleeding source by surgical, endoscopic or interventional radiology means","Give an antifibrinolytic (tranexamic acid) early, particularly in trauma","Prevent and correct the lethal triad by warming the patient and all fluids and products and by correcting metabolic acidosis","Monitor and correct citrate-induced ionised hypocalcaemia with intravenous calcium","Monitor for and treat hyperkalaemia, especially with rapidly infused older red cell units","Transition to viscoelastic-guided (TEG/ROTEM) goal-directed component replacement once bleeding is controlled, with cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate for hypofibrinogenaemia","Involve transfusion, haematology and critical care teams"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"massive_transfusion_coagulopathy":{"name":"Massive transfusion coagulopathy","system":"Haematology / Transfusion medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Dilutional coagulopathy","Coagulopathy of massive transfusion"],"chapter":null,"source":"British Society for Haematology guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A bleeding tendency arising during large-volume resuscitation, driven by dilution and consumption of clotting factors and platelets, hypothermia, acidosis and hypocalcaemia.","The lethal triad of hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy is self-reinforcing and a leading cause of death in major haemorrhage.","Stored red cells contain little functional platelet or coagulation factor activity, so replacing large blood losses with red cells alone dilutes haemostasis.","Fibrinogen is often the first factor to fall to critical levels and requires early replacement with cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate.","Modern major haemorrhage protocols favour balanced ratios of red cells, plasma and platelets and early empirical treatment before laboratory results return.","Point-of-care viscoelastic testing can guide targeted component therapy and reduce unnecessary transfusion.","Correcting hypothermia and acidosis is essential because clotting enzymes function poorly when cold and acidotic.","In trauma, an early consumptive coagulopathy of trauma-induced coagulopathy coexists with and precedes purely dilutional effects."],"pathophysiology":"Massive transfusion coagulopathy is a multifactorial failure of haemostasis during and after large-volume blood replacement. As shed blood is replaced with red cell concentrates and crystalloid, platelets and coagulation factors are progressively diluted while ongoing bleeding and clot formation consume them. Concurrent hypothermia slows the kinetics of coagulation enzymes and impairs platelet function, and metabolic acidosis from hypoperfusion further inhibits the coagulation cascade and accelerates fibrinogen breakdown. Citrate-induced hypocalcaemia reduces calcium-dependent clotting reactions and myocardial performance. In trauma an endogenous trauma-induced coagulopathy, driven by tissue injury, shock, endothelial activation and hyperfibrinolysis, is superimposed on these dilutional and metabolic derangements. Together these processes produce diffuse microvascular bleeding that compounds the original haemorrhage.","additionalDifferentials":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Trauma-induced coagulopathy","Pre-existing anticoagulant or antiplatelet therapy","Inherited bleeding disorder unmasked by haemorrhage","Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction with coagulopathy","Hyperfibrinolysis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with platelet count","Prothrombin time and activated partial thromboplastin time","Fibrinogen concentration","Viscoelastic testing such as thromboelastography or rotational thromboelastometry","Ionised calcium, temperature and arterial blood gas for the lethal triad","D-dimer and fibrin degradation products where disseminated intravascular coagulation is suspected"],"redFlags":["Diffuse microvascular oozing from wounds, puncture sites and mucosa","Critically low fibrinogen during ongoing haemorrhage","Core hypothermia with worsening bleeding","Severe metabolic acidosis in a bleeding patient","Ionised hypocalcaemia during rapid transfusion"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on rapid haemorrhage control and correction of the lethal triad; established coagulopathy with hypothermia and acidosis carries high mortality. Structured major haemorrhage protocols with balanced component ratios and goal-directed therapy improve survival.","pitfalls":["Resuscitating with red cells and crystalloid alone and neglecting plasma, platelets and fibrinogen.","Waiting for laboratory results before treating in the face of active major haemorrhage.","Ignoring hypothermia and acidosis while transfusing components.","Overlooking hypocalcaemia from citrate during rapid transfusion.","Failing to consider trauma-induced coagulopathy and hyperfibrinolysis as distinct contributors."],"management":["Correct the coagulopathy within a major haemorrhage protocol using balanced ratios of red cells, plasma and platelets rather than red cells alone","Early empirical fibrinogen replacement with cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate, as fibrinogen falls first","Administer tranexamic acid to counter hyperfibrinolysis, especially in trauma-induced coagulopathy","Actively reverse the self-reinforcing lethal triad by warming and by restoring perfusion to correct acidosis","Monitor and replace ionised calcium to support calcium-dependent clotting","Use point-of-care viscoelastic testing (TEG/ROTEM) to target component therapy and avoid unnecessary transfusion","Achieve rapid haemorrhage source control","Involve haematology and transfusion specialists alongside critical care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["British Society for Haematology guidelines on the management of major haemorrhage","AABB Technical Manual, current edition","Mollison's Blood Transfusion in Clinical Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"massive_transfusion_syndrome":{"name":"Massive transfusion syndrome","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["massive transfusion complications","complications of massive transfusion","massive haemorrhage transfusion effects"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / AABB technical manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Describes the metabolic and haemostatic derangements that follow replacement of a large fraction of the blood volume over a short period","The lethal triad of coagulopathy, hypothermia and acidosis compounds bleeding and worsens outcome","Dilutional and consumptive coagulopathy arises as clotting factors and platelets are diluted and consumed during massive haemorrhage","Citrate anticoagulant in stored components chelates calcium, producing hypocalcaemia that impairs coagulation and cardiac function","Potassium leaks from stored red cells and can cause hyperkalaemia, especially with rapid infusion of older units","Rapid infusion of cold components causes hypothermia, which itself impairs enzymatic clotting and platelet function","Major haemorrhage protocols emphasise balanced ratios of red cells, plasma and platelets, blood warming and correction of calcium","Frequent monitoring of coagulation, ionised calcium, potassium, temperature and acid-base status guides ongoing replacement"],"pathophysiology":"Massive transfusion syndrome is the constellation of disturbances that accompanies replacement of a large proportion of the circulating blood volume within hours. Coagulopathy develops through dilution and consumption of clotting factors and platelets, compounded by fibrinolysis and by the underlying tissue injury. Stored components are anticoagulated with citrate, which binds ionised calcium and can produce hypocalcaemia that impairs both coagulation and myocardial contractility. Progressive leakage of potassium from stored red cells may cause hyperkalaemia when large volumes are infused rapidly. Cold components lower core temperature, and the resulting hypothermia slows the enzymatic clotting cascade and platelet function. Tissue hypoperfusion generates a metabolic acidosis, and together hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy form a self-reinforcing lethal triad.","additionalDifferentials":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation from the underlying injury","Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction","Transfusion-associated circulatory overload","Transfusion-related acute lung injury","Dilutional thrombocytopenia from other fluids","Primary hyperfibrinolysis in trauma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial coagulation screen, fibrinogen and platelet count, or viscoelastic testing","Ionised calcium to detect citrate-induced hypocalcaemia","Serum potassium and continuous cardiac monitoring for hyperkalaemia","Core temperature monitoring for hypothermia","Arterial blood gas and lactate for acidosis and perfusion","Full blood count to track haemoglobin and platelets during resuscitation"],"redFlags":["Diffuse microvascular oozing indicating established coagulopathy","Electrocardiographic changes or arrhythmia from hyperkalaemia or hypocalcaemia","Falling core temperature refractory to warming measures","Worsening metabolic acidosis with rising lactate despite resuscitation"],"prognosis":"Outcome is largely determined by the underlying cause of haemorrhage and the speed of control, but timely correction of coagulopathy, calcium, temperature and acidosis within a structured major haemorrhage protocol improves survival.","pitfalls":["Transfusing red cells alone without proportionate plasma and platelets","Neglecting to warm rapidly infused blood and fluids","Overlooking citrate-induced hypocalcaemia during high-volume transfusion","Failing to monitor and correct potassium and acid-base disturbances"],"management":["Manage within a structured major haemorrhage protocol with balanced component ratios to limit dilutional and consumptive coagulopathy","Warm the patient and all infused fluids and blood products to prevent hypothermia","Monitor and correct citrate-induced ionised hypocalcaemia with intravenous calcium","Monitor serum potassium with continuous cardiac monitoring and treat hyperkalaemia","Correct metabolic acidosis by restoring perfusion and controlling haemorrhage","Guide clotting-factor, platelet and fibrinogen replacement by coagulation and viscoelastic testing, with tranexamic acid where indicated","Achieve definitive control of the bleeding source","Coordinate haematology, transfusion and critical care management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"mast_cell_activation_syndrome":{"name":"Mast cell activation syndrome","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCAS","mast cell activation disorder","monoclonal mast cell activation syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A syndrome of recurrent episodes of mast cell mediator release affecting two or more organ systems, typically skin, gastrointestinal tract, cardiovascular and respiratory systems","Common features include flushing, urticaria, abdominal cramps, diarrhoea, hypotension and, in severe cases, anaphylaxis","Diagnosis rests on the clinical picture, objective evidence of mediator release, and a response to mast-cell-directed treatment","A transient rise in serum tryptase measured during and after an event, compared with a stable baseline, provides objective support","The syndrome is classified as primary (clonal, with a KIT D816V mutation or the surface marker CD25), secondary (an identifiable allergic or other trigger), or idiopathic","It overlaps with, but must be distinguished from, systemic mastocytosis, which shows a clonal expansion of mast cells","Management combines trigger avoidance with agents that block or stabilise mast cell mediators, such as antihistamines and mast cell stabilisers, and adrenaline for anaphylaxis"],"pathophysiology":"Mast cell activation syndrome arises from inappropriate or excessive release of mast cell mediators, including histamine, tryptase, prostaglandins and leukotrienes, producing multisystem symptoms. In primary (clonal) disease an acquired activating KIT D816V mutation, often with aberrant CD25 expression, lowers the threshold for mast cell degranulation. In secondary forms an identifiable trigger such as IgE-mediated allergy drives activation, while idiopathic cases have no demonstrable clone or trigger. The released mediators cause vasodilatation, increased vascular permeability, smooth muscle contraction and secretory changes that explain the flushing, hypotension, bronchospasm and gastrointestinal symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic mastocytosis","Carcinoid syndrome","Phaeochromocytoma","Hereditary or acquired angioedema","IgE-mediated allergy or idiopathic anaphylaxis","Vasoactive intestinal peptide or other neuroendocrine tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial serum tryptase with an event-related rise above baseline","Urinary mediators such as N-methylhistamine and prostaglandin metabolites","KIT D816V mutation testing in blood or marrow","Bone marrow examination with mast cell immunophenotyping when clonal disease is suspected","Allergy evaluation for secondary triggers","Exclusion tests for carcinoid and phaeochromocytoma where indicated"],"redFlags":["Recurrent anaphylaxis or unexplained hypotensive collapse","A persistently and markedly elevated baseline tryptase suggesting clonal mast cell disease","Features of systemic mastocytosis such as organomegaly or cytopenias","Failure to respond to standard mast-cell-directed therapy"],"prognosis":"Most patients achieve good symptom control with trigger avoidance and mediator-directed therapy and have a normal life expectancy; prognosis is less favourable when there is an underlying advanced clonal mast cell disorder.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing the syndrome without objective evidence of mediator release","Failing to check a baseline tryptase, so an event-related rise cannot be interpreted","Missing an underlying clonal mast cell disorder or systemic mastocytosis","Overlooking mimics such as carcinoid or phaeochromocytoma"],"management":["Identify and avoid individual triggers (foods, drugs, physical stimuli) that precipitate mediator release","First-line H1-antihistamines, adding H2-antihistamines for gastrointestinal and cutaneous symptoms","Add mast cell stabilisers (oral sodium cromoglicate) and leukotriene receptor antagonists for incomplete control","Anti-IgE therapy (omalizumab) for refractory symptoms or recurrent anaphylaxis","Prescribe and train the patient in self-injectable adrenaline with a written anaphylaxis action plan","Identify and treat secondary allergic triggers, and evaluate for an underlying clonal mast cell disorder","Refer to allergy and immunology or haematology; escalate where anaphylaxis or clonal disease is present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"mast_cell_leukaemia":{"name":"Mast cell leukaemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCL"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO haematolymphoid classification / mastocytosis literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, aggressive form of systemic mastocytosis defined by a high proportion of atypical mast cells in the bone marrow","Circulating mast cells may or may not be present (classical versus aleukaemic variants)","Presents with rapidly progressive organ dysfunction, cytopenias, hepatosplenomegaly and severe mediator symptoms","Serum tryptase is typically very high","It carries a poor prognosis with short survival in many cases","Management involves cytoreductive and KIT-targeted therapies and consideration of stem cell transplantation, alongside aggressive mediator control"],"pathophysiology":"Mast cell leukaemia is the most aggressive category of systemic mastocytosis, characterised by extensive proliferation of atypical, often immature, clonal mast cells that heavily infiltrate the bone marrow and impair normal haematopoiesis. Although KIT mutations are common, the mutation profile can differ from indolent disease and influences response to targeted therapy. The massive mast cell burden causes both marrow failure with cytopenias and organ infiltration, and profuse mediator release producing severe systemic symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Aggressive systemic mastocytosis","Acute myeloid leukaemia","Other systemic mastocytosis with an associated haematological neoplasm","Basophilic leukaemia","Reactive mast cell hyperplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bone marrow aspirate and trephine with mast cell quantification and immunophenotyping","Blood film for circulating mast cells","Serum tryptase","KIT and broader mutation analysis","Staging of organ involvement and assessment for transplant candidacy"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive cytopenias and organ failure","Severe, refractory mediator crises","Very high mast cell burden on marrow","Features of an associated haematological neoplasm"],"prognosis":"Mast cell leukaemia has a poor prognosis with short median survival in many series, though newer KIT-targeted agents and transplantation may improve outcomes in selected patients.","pitfalls":["Delaying marrow examination in rapidly progressive mastocytosis","Assuming standard KIT-targeted therapy will work regardless of mutation profile","Underestimating the risk of catastrophic mediator release","Missing an associated haematological neoplasm"],"management":["KIT-targeted tyrosine kinase inhibitors (midostaurin or avapritinib) directed by the KIT mutation profile","Cytoreductive chemotherapy such as cladribine to reduce the mast cell burden","Consider allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in eligible patients","Aggressive mediator control with H1- and H2-antihistamines, mast cell stabilisers and self-injectable adrenaline for anaphylaxis","Supportive care for marrow failure, including transfusion of blood products and treatment of infection","Osteoporosis surveillance with bone-protective therapy","Manage through a specialist haematology multidisciplinary team with transplant assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","Mastocytosis clinical guidelines (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"mast_cell_sarcoma":{"name":"Mast cell sarcoma","system":"Haematology / Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCS"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO haematolymphoid classification / mastocytosis literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An extremely rare, highly aggressive localised tumour of atypical mast cells with destructive growth","It presents as a solitary mass that invades and destroys local tissue","Despite an initially localised presentation, it has a strong tendency to disseminate","It may progress to a mast cell leukaemia-like picture","Diagnosis requires histology with mast cell markers and exclusion of systemic mastocytosis at presentation","Prognosis is poor, and management uses surgery, cytoreductive and targeted therapy with transplantation considered"],"pathophysiology":"Mast cell sarcoma is a malignant neoplasm of mast cells forming a destructive solid tumour, in contrast to the marrow-based infiltration of mast cell leukaemia. The atypical mast cells show high-grade cytological features and invade surrounding tissues. Although localised at diagnosis, the tumour behaves aggressively with a marked propensity for systemic spread, and terminal disease can resemble mast cell leukaemia. KIT and other mutations may be present and influence therapy.","additionalDifferentials":["Mast cell leukaemia","Extramedullary myeloid sarcoma","Anaplastic large cell or other lymphoma","Poorly differentiated carcinoma or sarcoma","Extracutaneous mastocytoma (benign)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Tissue biopsy with mast cell immunohistochemistry","Assessment for systemic mastocytosis (marrow, tryptase)","KIT and molecular studies","Staging imaging for local extent and dissemination","Multidisciplinary review for treatment planning"],"redFlags":["Rapid local growth and tissue destruction","Evidence of systemic dissemination","Transformation toward a leukaemic picture","Severe mediator symptoms"],"prognosis":"Mast cell sarcoma has a poor prognosis with frequent dissemination and progression despite treatment, although outcomes may improve with targeted therapy and transplantation in selected cases.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for a benign extracutaneous mastocytoma","Underestimating its propensity to disseminate","Delaying molecular characterisation that could guide targeted therapy","Failing to assess for systemic mast cell disease"],"management":["Complete surgical resection of the localised destructive tumour where feasible","Local radiotherapy for unresectable or residual disease","KIT-targeted tyrosine kinase inhibitors and cytoreductive therapy, guided by mutation analysis, given the strong tendency to disseminate","Consider allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in selected patients","Mediator-directed therapy with antihistamines and self-injectable adrenaline for mediator release and anaphylaxis","Surveillance for systemic dissemination and progression to a mast cell leukaemia-like picture","Coordinate management through a specialist multidisciplinary sarcoma and haematology team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","Mastocytosis clinical guidelines (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"mast_syndrome_spg21":{"name":"MAST syndrome (SPG21)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPG21","Mast syndrome","ACP33-related spastic paraplegia","maspardin deficiency","locus 15q22.31"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive complicated hereditary spastic paraplegia caused by biallelic loss of maspardin, encoded by SPG21 (ACP33)","Classically described in the Old Order Amish, in whom a founder frameshift variant is responsible","Onset is usually in early adulthood with slowly progressive spastic paraparesis followed by cognitive decline and dementia","Additional features include cerebellar and extrapyramidal signs, dysarthria and sometimes psychiatric disturbance","Neuroimaging characteristically shows a thin corpus callosum and periventricular white-matter change","Maspardin is thought to regulate endosomal trafficking and receptor recycling, linking SPG21 to other trafficking-related complicated HSPs","Management is symptomatic and supportive, with physiotherapy, spasticity control and cognitive and psychiatric support"],"pathophysiology":"MAST syndrome (SPG21) is caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in the gene encoding maspardin, a protein associated with endosomal and trans-Golgi membranes that appears to regulate intracellular trafficking and receptor recycling, including modulation of CD4-related signalling. Loss of maspardin is thought to disturb endosomal sorting and membrane homeostasis in neurons, leading to a diffuse neurodegenerative process. This affects the corticospinal tracts, cerebral white matter (with the characteristic thin corpus callosum), cerebellum and basal ganglia, producing the combination of spastic paraparesis, dementia and mixed cerebellar and extrapyramidal features that defines the syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["SPG11 and SPG15 complicated hereditary spastic paraplegia with thin corpus callosum","Other autosomal recessive hereditary spastic paraplegias","Early-onset dementias with pyramidal features","Metabolic leukodystrophies","Wilson disease and other treatable movement-disorder mimics","Frontotemporal dementia with motor features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SPG21 sequencing within a hereditary spastic paraplegia or complicated-HSP panel","MRI of brain showing thin corpus callosum and white-matter change","Neuropsychological assessment to characterise cognitive decline","Nerve conduction studies to identify any peripheral involvement","Metabolic screen and copper studies to exclude treatable mimics","Family history and consanguinity assessment supporting recessive inheritance"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive cognitive decline requiring exclusion of treatable dementias","New extrapyramidal signs suggesting an alternative or coexisting movement disorder","Acute behavioural or psychiatric deterioration needing urgent support","Loss of safe swallowing or mobility indicating advancing disease"],"prognosis":"MAST syndrome progresses slowly but is a multisystem neurodegenerative disorder, and over years affected individuals develop increasing motor disability and dementia with substantial dependence.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis outside the Amish population, where it is rarer and less expected","Attributing early spastic gait to a pure HSP and missing evolving cognitive decline","Failing to recognise thin corpus callosum as a clue to a complicated recessive HSP","Neglecting psychiatric and cognitive needs while focusing only on spasticity"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy; management is supportive with regular physiotherapy to maintain mobility and prevent contractures in the spastic paraparesis","Antispasticity treatment with agents such as baclofen or tizanidine, and focal botulinum toxin injection for troublesome spasticity","Mobility aids, orthoses and occupational therapy as disability progresses","Cognitive assessment and dementia support, with management of psychiatric and behavioural disturbance","Speech and language therapy for dysarthria and swallowing safety","Autosomal recessive with founder variants: offer genetic counselling and family screening, addressing consanguinity","Coordinate care through a neurology-led multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Hereditary Spastic Paraplegia Overview","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"mastocytosis":{"name":"Mastocytosis","system":"Immunology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mast cell disease","systemic mastocytosis"],"chapter":366,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2819-2821","clinicalPearls":["Mastocytosis is defined by accumulation of clonally expanded mast cells in tissues (skin, bone marrow, liver, spleen, gut); mast cell expansion is most readily identified in skin and/or bone marrow (p.2819).","More than 90% of patients carry a somatic gain-of-function KIT D816V mutation in the stem cell factor receptor gene (p.2819).","Classification recognizes cutaneous mastocytosis (with variants), systemic mastocytosis (SM, five variants), and mast cell sarcoma; cutaneous mastocytosis is the most common diagnosis in children and is usually diagnosed within the first year of life (p.2819-2820).","Systemic mastocytosis denotes involvement of an extracutaneous site, most often bone marrow; indolent systemic mastocytosis (ISM) accounts for >70% of adult patients and is associated with a normal life expectancy (p.2820).","Cutaneous lesions of maculopapular cutaneous mastocytosis (MPCM, also called urticaria pigmentosa) are reddish-brown macules, papules, or plaques that urticate and become erythematous on rubbing or trauma (Darier's sign) (p.2820).","Clinical manifestations of SM reflect release of bioactive mast cell mediators plus tissue infiltration: intermittent urticaria, flushing, tachycardia and vascular collapse, gastric acid hypersecretion, crampy lower abdominal pain, and diarrhea (p.2820).","Anaphylaxis in mastocytosis is most commonly triggered by Hymenoptera stings and NSAIDs; symptom flares may also follow alcohol, temperature change, stress, and mast cell-interactive opioids (p.2820).","A basal serum tryptase level is a useful noninvasive test to consider before bone marrow biopsy; total serum tryptase >20 ng/mL is a minor diagnostic criterion for SM (p.2821).","Diagnosis of SM requires tissue confirmation (lesional/bone marrow biopsy): one major plus one minor, or three minor criteria. Major = multifocal dense mast cell aggregates (>15 mast cells per aggregate) in extracutaneous tissue; minor = spindled/atypical morphology, aberrant CD25/CD2/CD30 phenotype, activating KIT mutation, or tryptase >20 ng/mL (p.2821).","Patients with recurrent mast cell activation (especially anaphylaxis with hypotensive syncope) who carry KIT D816V but do not meet full SM criteria are termed monoclonal mast cell activation syndrome (p.2821)."],"pathophysiology":"Mastocytosis is a clonal mast cell disorder driven by somatic gain-of-function mutations in the KIT (stem cell factor receptor) gene, with KIT D816V present in >90% of patients. The mutation produces ligand-independent KIT activation and clonal mast cell proliferation/accumulation in skin, bone marrow, liver, spleen, and gut. KIT mutations occur across all forms of SM and in lesional skin of some children with cutaneous disease; additional mutations (TET2, SRSF2, ASXL1, RUNX1) seen in other hematologic neoplasms can appear in advanced SM variants. Disease manifestations arise from release of bioactive mast cell mediators acting locally and distally, tissue infiltration by mast cells, and the tissue response to that infiltrate; mast cell-mediated fibrosis may affect liver, spleen, and bone marrow but not gastrointestinal tissue or skin (p.2819-2820).","additionalDifferentials":["Carcinoid syndrome (exclude with 24-h urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid)","Pheochromocytoma (exclude with 24-h urine/plasma metanephrines)","Hereditary alpha-tryptasemia (autosomal dominant extra copies of the alpha-tryptase gene at TPSAB1; elevated baseline serum tryptase with multisystem mast cell activation symptoms)","Recurrent IgE-mediated or idiopathic anaphylaxis (usually presents with urticaria, angioedema and/or bronchospasm, which are not typical manifestations of anaphylaxis in SM)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Carcinoid syndrome (flushing, diarrhea)","Pheochromocytoma (flushing, tachycardia, vascular collapse)","Hereditary alpha-tryptasemia","Idiopathic or IgE-mediated anaphylaxis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Basal (baseline) serum tryptase as a noninvasive screen before bone marrow biopsy","Bone marrow biopsy with mast cell morphology, immunophenotype (CD25, CD2, CD30), and KIT D816V mutation testing for systemic disease","Skin biopsy to confirm diffuse cutaneous and bullous mastocytosis subvariants","24-h urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (carcinoid) and metanephrines (pheochromocytoma) to exclude mimics","Bone densitometry, bone scan, or skeletal survey (osteoporosis is increased and may cause pathologic fractures)","CT scan or endoscopy and neuropsychiatric evaluation as directed by presentation"],"redFlags":["Anaphylaxis with rapid, life-threatening vascular collapse (most often after Hymenoptera stings or NSAIDs)","Hypotensive syncope from mast cell activation","C findings of aggressive disease: cytopenias, hepatomegaly with ascites and impaired liver function, splenomegaly with hypersplenism, malabsorption with hypoalbuminemia and weight loss, or osteolytic skeletal lesions with pathologic fractures","Circulating atypical mast cells suggesting mast cell leukemia"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is variant-dependent. Life expectancy is normal for cutaneous mastocytosis and most ISM; progression from ISM to a more advanced form is uncommon (~5% lifetime risk). In SM-AHNMD, outcome is determined by the associated non-mast cell hematologic disorder. SSM carries an intermediate prognosis. Aggressive SM and mast cell leukemia have a poor prognosis, and mast cell leukemia is invariably fatal at present. In infants and children with cutaneous disease, visceral involvement is usually absent and spontaneous resolution before adolescence is common; progression from cutaneous mastocytosis to ISM occurs in ~10% of children, especially with high mast cell burden or hematologic abnormalities (p.2820).","pitfalls":["Atopy is not increased in mastocytosis compared with the general population (p.2819).","Imatinib is not effective in most cases because the KIT D816V mutation confers resistance (p.2821).","Osteoporosis alone without osteolytic lesions does not satisfy the C-finding skeletal criterion for aggressive SM (p.2821).","SM cannot be confirmed on clinical and laboratory grounds alone; tissue diagnosis is required (p.2821).","Anaphylaxis in SM typically lacks the urticaria, angioedema, and bronchospasm seen in IgE-mediated or idiopathic anaphylaxis (p.2821).","Baseline serum tryptase elevation must be interpreted with caution where hereditary alpha-tryptasemia (extra TPSAB1 copies) coexists (p.2821)."],"management":["Avoid known mediator-release triggers and provide self-injectable adrenaline with a written anaphylaxis plan","Symptom control with H1- and H2-antihistamines, mast cell stabilisers (sodium cromoglicate) and leukotriene antagonists, with H2-blockers or proton pump inhibitors for gastric acid hypersecretion","Topical corticosteroids or phototherapy for symptomatic cutaneous disease","Bone density surveillance with bisphosphonate therapy for the associated osteoporosis and fracture risk","For advanced systemic disease, KIT-targeted tyrosine kinase inhibitors (midostaurin, avapritinib) and cytoreductive therapy, guided by KIT D816V status","In systemic mastocytosis with an associated haematological neoplasm, treat the associated disorder, which determines outcome","Monitor with serial tryptase and assessment for progression, involving haematology and allergy specialists"],"severityClassification":"Consensus classification: Cutaneous mastocytosis (CM) - maculopapular cutaneous mastocytosis (MPCM/urticaria pigmentosa), solitary mastocytoma of skin, diffuse cutaneous mastocytosis. Systemic mastocytosis (SM) - indolent SM (ISM, >70% of adults, normal life expectancy), smoldering SM (SSM; >=2 B findings, no C findings: high mast cell burden with marrow infiltration >30%, basal tryptase >200 ng/mL, and/or KIT D816V VAF >10%, dysmyelopoiesis, organomegaly), SM with associated clonal hematologic non-mast cell lineage disease (SM-AHNMD), aggressive SM (ASM; >=1 C finding - cytopenias, hepatic dysfunction with ascites, hypersplenism, malabsorption with weight loss, osteolytic fractures), mast cell leukemia (MCL, rarest, invariably fatal). Mast cell sarcoma (MCS) is a rare invasive solid mast cell tumor (Tables 366-1, 366-2; p.2820).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2819-2821"],"crossLinks":["anaphylaxis"]},"mastoiditis":{"name":"Acute mastoiditis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute coalescent mastoiditis","mastoid abscess","suppurative mastoiditis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Usually arises as a complication of acute otitis media when infection spreads into the air cell system of the mastoid and pus fails to drain","Suspect it when a child with a middle ear infection develops worsening pain, fever and swelling behind the ear despite treatment","The classic sign is a boggy, tender postauricular swelling that pushes the pinna forward and downward, often obliterating the retroauricular groove","Otoscopy commonly shows sagging of the posterosuperior canal wall and a bulging or discharging drum","Progressive breakdown of the bony walls between air cells (coalescence) distinguishes surgical disease from simple mastoid mucosal inflammation seen in every acute otitis media","Contrast CT of the temporal bone defines the extent of coalescence, subperiosteal abscess and any early intracranial extension","Management combines aggressive parenteral antibiotics with drainage; cortical mastoidectomy is indicated when there is a subperiosteal abscess, no response to medical therapy, or a suspected complication","Pus should be sent for culture, and common organisms include Streptococcus pneumoniae, Streptococcus pyogenes, Staphylococcus and Pseudomonas in chronic cases"],"pathophysiology":"The mastoid air cells are continuous with the middle ear, so mucosal inflammation accompanies most episodes of acute otitis media. Mastoiditis develops when purulent material is trapped because the aditus ad antrum becomes blocked by oedema, allowing pressure and infection to build within the air cells. Persistent suppuration decalcifies and destroys the thin bony septa between cells, which fuse into an abscess cavity (coalescent mastoiditis). Pus may then track outward through the cortex to form a subperiosteal abscess, along fascial planes into the neck (Bezold abscess), or inward toward the dura, sigmoid sinus and posterior/middle cranial fossae, producing serious intracranial complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe acute otitis media without true mastoid coalescence","Postauricular lymphadenitis or infected sebaceous cyst","Suppurating postauricular lymph node from scalp infection","Furunculosis of the external canal with periauricular swelling","Histiocytosis or malignancy involving the temporal bone","Otitis externa with surrounding cellulitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the temporal bone to assess air cell coalescence and abscess formation","MRI with venography when intracranial extension or sinus thrombosis is suspected","Otoscopy for sagging canal wall, drum changes and discharge","Culture and sensitivity of ear or abscess pus to guide antibiotics","Full blood count and inflammatory markers to gauge severity and monitor response","Audiometry once the acute phase settles to document hearing"],"redFlags":["Headache, high fever, neck stiffness or altered consciousness suggesting meningitis or brain abscess (intracranial spread is an emergency)","Swelling and tenderness over the sigmoid sinus region with swinging fever pointing to lateral sinus thrombophlebitis","Neck swelling and torticollis suggesting a Bezold abscess tracking into the neck","Facial nerve weakness or vertigo indicating deeper temporal bone involvement","Rapid clinical deterioration or failure to respond to intravenous antibiotics"],"prognosis":"With early recognition, appropriate antibiotics and timely surgical drainage the outlook is good and hearing is usually preserved, but delayed treatment allows intracranial and intratemporal complications that carry significant morbidity and can be life-threatening.","pitfalls":["Attributing postauricular swelling to simple lymphadenopathy and delaying imaging","Assuming any mastoid opacity on imaging equals surgical mastoiditis without correlating clinical signs and bony coalescence","Under-treating with oral antibiotics when parenteral therapy and drainage are required","Missing evolving intracranial complications by not reassessing the non-improving patient","Overlooking that the pinna is pushed forward, mistaking the picture for external ear pathology"],"management":["Prompt intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics covering common acute otitis media pathogens, refined by culture and sensitivity","Myringotomy with or without grommet insertion to drain the middle ear and obtain pus for culture","Cortical mastoidectomy for coalescent disease, subperiosteal abscess or failure to respond to medical therapy","Surgical drainage of a subperiosteal or Bezold abscess","Analgesia, antipyretics and supportive care","Contrast imaging and urgent neurosurgical and ENT input for intracranial extension or sigmoid sinus thrombosis, with anticoagulation considered for sinus thrombosis","Audiometry follow-up once the acute phase settles to document hearing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"maternal_uniparental_disomy_15":{"name":"Maternal uniparental disomy 15","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["maternal UPD15","UPD(15)mat","chromosome 15 maternal uniparental disomy"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Maternal uniparental disomy 15 is one mechanism of Prader-Willi syndrome, since two maternal copies mean absent paternal expression at 15q11-q13, and it accounts for roughly a quarter of cases","Prader-Willi syndrome presents in infancy with profound hypotonia, poor suck, and feeding difficulty or failure to thrive","In early childhood the picture switches to hyperphagia and progressive obesity if intake is not controlled","Hypogonadism, short stature from growth-hormone deficiency, small hands and feet, and a characteristic facial appearance are typical","Intellectual disability is usually mild-to-moderate, with behavioural difficulties and a characteristic temper and obsessive profile","The UPD subtype carries a lower recurrence risk than an imprinting defect but a slightly higher reported rate of autism spectrum disorder and psychosis than the deletion subtype","Maternal UPD15 typically arises via trisomy 15 rescue, so it is associated with advanced maternal age and confined placental mosaicism"],"pathophysiology":"The Prader-Willi region at 15q11-q13 contains paternally expressed genes that are silenced on the maternal chromosome. In maternal uniparental disomy 15 both chromosome 15s come from the mother, so there is no paternal contribution and the paternally expressed genes are not transcribed, producing Prader-Willi syndrome. This uniparental disomy most often results from rescue of an initial trisomy 15 through loss of the paternal chromosome, which is why it is associated with advanced maternal age; the same clinical picture can also follow a paternal deletion or an imprinting defect.","additionalDifferentials":["Deletion or imprinting-defect Prader-Willi syndrome, the same phenotype with a different mechanism","Other causes of neonatal hypotonia, whether neuromuscular or central","Other syndromic obesity such as Bardet-Biedl and Cohen syndromes","Angelman syndrome, the reciprocal disorder of paternal UPD15","Hypothalamic or acquired causes of hyperphagia and obesity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Methylation analysis of the 15q11-q13 locus as the first-line test, abnormal in all Prader-Willi syndrome regardless of mechanism","Follow-up testing by microsatellite or SNP analysis, FISH, and microarray to distinguish maternal disomy from deletion or imprinting defect","Chromosomal microarray, which detects deletions but not uniparental disomy","Growth and endocrine assessment for growth-hormone status and hypogonadism","Developmental, behavioural, and feeding assessment","Monitoring for obesity-related complications"],"redFlags":["Severe neonatal hypotonia with respiratory or feeding compromise","Rapid weight gain with complications of morbid obesity","A behavioural or psychiatric crisis, given the higher psychosis risk in the disomy subtype"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis, growth-hormone therapy, and strict dietary and behavioural management, outcomes have improved, but morbid obesity and its complications remain the main threat to health and life expectancy, and a higher rate of psychiatric illness is noted in the uniparental disomy subtype.","pitfalls":["Relying on microarray or FISH, which detect deletions but miss uniparental disomy, when a methylation test is needed first","Not subtyping after a positive methylation test, which affects recurrence-risk counselling","Missing the infantile diagnosis because early hypotonia is non-specific","Underestimating the raised psychiatric risk in maternal UPD15 cases"],"management":["Growth hormone replacement therapy to improve stature, body composition and muscle tone","Strict lifelong dietary energy restriction with a supervised, controlled food environment to prevent hyperphagia-driven obesity","Sex hormone replacement for hypogonadism and to support pubertal development and bone health","Early physiotherapy and feeding support for the infantile hypotonia, poor suck and failure to thrive","Behavioural and psychological or psychiatric support, with heightened vigilance for autism spectrum disorder and psychosis in the uniparental disomy subtype","Surveillance for obesity-related complications including obstructive sleep apnoea, type 2 diabetes and scoliosis","Genetic counselling noting the lower recurrence risk of the uniparental disomy subtype compared with an imprinting defect","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across endocrinology, dietetics, genetics and developmental and behavioural services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Cassidy and Allanson's Management of Genetic Syndromes"],"crossLinks":[]},"maternally_inherited_diabetes_and_deafness":{"name":"Maternally Inherited Diabetes and Deafness","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MIDD","m.3243A>G diabetes","MT-TL1-related diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mitochondrial medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by a heteroplasmic point mutation in mitochondrial DNA, classically m.3243A>G in MT-TL1; inheritance is strictly maternal with no male-to-offspring transmission.","Sensorineural hearing loss is typically bilateral and high-frequency and often precedes the onset of diabetes by years.","Diabetes results from progressive beta-cell failure rather than insulin resistance; patients are usually lean and may transition from oral agents to insulin over time.","A macular pattern dystrophy with perifoveal retinal pigment change is characteristic and often spares central vision early on.","The same mutation underlies MELAS, so remain alert to overlapping features such as stroke-like episodes, myopathy and raised lactate.","Metformin is generally avoided because of the theoretical risk of precipitating lactic acidosis in mitochondrial disease.","Systemic involvement may include cardiomyopathy, renal disease with a focal segmental glomerulosclerosis picture, and short stature.","Heteroplasmy level and its tissue distribution largely determine severity and the spectrum of organ involvement."],"pathophysiology":"The disorder arises from a heteroplasmic pathogenic variant in mitochondrial DNA, most commonly m.3243A>G within MT-TL1, the gene encoding the mitochondrial transfer RNA for leucine (UUR). Impaired mitochondrial tRNA function disrupts intramitochondrial protein synthesis and oxidative phosphorylation, so tissues with high metabolic demand — pancreatic beta cells, the cochlea, the retina, myocardium, skeletal muscle and kidney — are preferentially affected. Because mitochondria are transmitted exclusively through the oocyte, inheritance is maternal, and the proportion of mutant to wild-type genomes (heteroplasmy) within a given tissue, together with a threshold effect, explains both the wide phenotypic variability and the shared genetic basis with MELAS.","additionalDifferentials":["Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY)","Wolfram syndrome (DIDMOAD)","MELAS and other mitochondrial cytopathies","Kearns-Sayre syndrome","Latent autoimmune diabetes of adults"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted molecular testing for m.3243A>G, preferably on urinary epithelial cells or buccal mucosa rather than blood","Pure-tone audiometry","Dilated fundoscopy with optical coherence tomography for pattern dystrophy","Echocardiography to screen for cardiomyopathy","Renal function and urinalysis for proteinuria","Islet autoantibodies, expected to be negative","Resting and post-exertional lactate"],"redFlags":["Lactic acidosis","New or progressive cardiomyopathy and heart failure","Declining renal function or nephrotic-range proteinuria","Stroke-like episodes suggesting MELAS overlap","Rapidly progressive visual loss"],"prognosis":"A chronic, progressive multisystem disorder in which outcome depends on heteroplasmy and the organs involved. Cardiac and renal complications are the main determinants of premature mortality.","pitfalls":["Frequently mislabelled as type 1 or type 2 diabetes, delaying recognition of the maternal pedigree and associated deafness.","Blood testing may miss the mutation because leucocyte heteroplasmy declines with age, so urinary sediment is the preferred sample.","Islet autoantibodies are negative, which should reinforce rather than exclude the diagnosis in the right context.","The risk of provoking lactic acidosis with certain oral agents is easily overlooked in day-to-day management."],"management":["Treat the diabetes as progressive beta-cell failure, favouring early insulin, while avoiding metformin because of the lactic-acidosis risk in mitochondrial disease","Hearing rehabilitation with hearing aids, and cochlear implantation for advanced sensorineural hearing loss","Consider coenzyme Q10 supplementation and avoid mitochondrial-toxic drugs such as aminoglycosides","Regular retinal review for the macular pattern dystrophy","Cardiac surveillance with echocardiography for cardiomyopathy and renal monitoring for proteinuria and nephropathy, the main determinants of prognosis","Remain alert to MELAS-overlap features such as stroke-like episodes, myopathy and lactic acidosis","Genetic counselling explaining strictly maternal inheritance with variable heteroplasmy, offering family screening","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across endocrinology, audiology, ophthalmology, cardiology and genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mitochondrial medicine references","Clinical endocrinology and diabetes references","Medical genetics references"],"crossLinks":[]},"mature_cystic_teratoma_ovary":{"name":"Mature cystic teratoma of the ovary","system":"Gynaecology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dermoid cyst","benign cystic teratoma","ovarian dermoid"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Commonest ovarian germ-cell tumour and the most frequent ovarian neoplasm in young women of reproductive age; usually benign","Contains mature tissue derived from all three germ layers, so cut sections typically reveal sebaceous material, hair, and sometimes teeth or cartilage","Often an incidental finding; when symptomatic it causes vague pelvic discomfort, a palpable mass, or presents acutely with torsion","Ultrasound features are characteristic, including echogenic mural nodules (Rokitansky protuberance), fat-fluid levels, and dense shadowing from hair and sebum","Torsion is the most common complication because of the fatty content and long pedicle; rupture spills irritant sebaceous material and provokes chemical peritonitis","Malignant transformation is rare and almost always squamous cell carcinoma arising in the ectodermal component, seen mainly in older women with larger lesions","Ovarian cystectomy with preservation of normal ovarian tissue is the usual fertility-sparing approach; bilateral disease occurs in a minority","May rarely trigger anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis, so a paraneoplastic neurological presentation should prompt a search for a dermoid"],"pathophysiology":"A mature cystic teratoma arises from a single germ cell that undergoes parthenogenetic development after the first meiotic division, generating well-differentiated tissues from ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm. Ectodermal elements usually predominate, which is why skin, sebaceous glands, hair follicles, and neural tissue are conspicuous, giving the lesion its dermoid character. The tumour is typically a unilocular cyst lined by squamous epithelium and filled with keratin and sebum, with a raised nodule (the Rokitansky protuberance) from which hair, teeth, and other solid tissues project. Because the components are mature, the lesion behaves benignly, with complications arising chiefly from its physical bulk and fatty content rather than from invasive growth.","additionalDifferentials":["Endometrioma","Haemorrhagic ovarian cyst","Ovarian fibroma or other solid stromal tumour","Mucinous cystadenoma","Immature (malignant) teratoma","Struma ovarii","Pedunculated uterine leiomyoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound to identify fat, hair, and the Rokitansky nodule","Pelvic MRI with fat-suppression sequences when imaging is equivocal","Tumour markers (CA125, AFP, beta-hCG, LDH) mainly to help exclude a malignant germ-cell component","Doppler assessment of ovarian blood flow if torsion is suspected","Histological examination of the excised specimen to confirm maturity of all elements","Thyroid function testing if a struma ovarii component is suspected"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe unilateral pelvic pain with nausea suggesting ovarian torsion","Signs of chemical peritonitis following cyst rupture","Rapid enlargement or solid vascularised areas raising concern for malignant transformation","New neuropsychiatric or seizure symptoms suggesting associated anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis"],"prognosis":"Excellent for the great majority, which are benign and cured by complete cystectomy; recurrence is uncommon and usually reflects a new primary. Prognosis worsens markedly if squamous carcinoma has developed within the lesion, particularly when detected late.","pitfalls":["Attributing acute pain solely to the mass and missing torsion, which threatens ovarian viability","Rupturing the cyst intraoperatively and spilling contents, provoking granulomatous peritonitis","Assuming all teratomas are benign and overlooking an immature or malignantly transformed component","Failing to consider a dermoid as the cause of a paraneoplastic encephalitis"],"management":["Ovary-sparing cystectomy, preferably laparoscopic, as the definitive treatment preserving fertility","Careful surgical technique to avoid intraperitoneal spillage of sebaceous contents and consequent chemical peritonitis, with copious irrigation if rupture occurs","Urgent surgery for torsion, with detorsion and ovarian conservation where the ovary is viable","Salpingo-oophorectomy and gynaecological oncology referral if malignant (squamous) transformation is suspected, particularly in older women with larger lesions","Consider expectant management with surveillance imaging for small, asymptomatic lesions","Histological confirmation of maturity of all elements, with thyroid assessment if a struma ovarii component is present","Postoperative follow-up for recurrence or a contralateral lesion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","Blaustein's Pathology of the Female Genital Tract"],"crossLinks":[]},"mauriac_syndrome":{"name":"Mauriac Syndrome","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Diabetic dwarfism","Mauriac's syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric endocrinology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A complication of chronically poorly controlled, insulin-deficient type 1 diabetes mellitus, described historically in children and adolescents.","Classic constellation of growth failure with short stature, tender hepatomegaly from hepatic glycogen loading (glycogenic hepatopathy), a cushingoid habitus and delayed puberty.","Glycogenic hepatopathy is the hepatic hallmark — reversible glycogen overloading of hepatocytes that is distinct from non-alcoholic fatty liver disease.","Transaminases may be strikingly raised yet typically normalise once stable glycaemic control is restored.","Wide swings between hyperglycaemia and periods of insulin excess drive the hepatic glycogen accumulation.","Growth retardation is linked to disruption of the growth hormone–IGF-1 axis with hepatic growth hormone resistance despite adequate or high growth hormone.","Now uncommon where modern insulin regimens are available, but still seen with poor adherence, insulin omission or limited access to care.","The disorder is largely reversible, underscoring the value of restoring consistent glycaemic control."],"pathophysiology":"Mauriac syndrome arises in the setting of chronic insulin deficiency with erratic glycaemic control, classically in a child or adolescent with type 1 diabetes who oscillates between prolonged hyperglycaemia and episodes of insulin excess. Fluctuating glucose and insulin availability drive excessive hepatic uptake and storage of glucose as glycogen, producing the reversible glycogen overloading of hepatocytes known as glycogenic hepatopathy, which manifests as tender hepatomegaly and often striking transaminase elevation. Persistent metabolic derangement disturbs the growth hormone–IGF-1 axis, with hepatic growth hormone resistance and low IGF-1 despite adequate or elevated growth hormone, explaining the growth failure and delayed puberty, while counter-regulatory cortisol excess and altered fat distribution contribute the cushingoid habitus. Because the underlying lesion is functional rather than structural, restoration of stable glycaemic control typically reverses the hepatic, growth and pubertal abnormalities.","additionalDifferentials":["Hepatic glycogen storage disorders (e.g. glycogen storage disease type I)","Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease and steatohepatitis","Cushing syndrome","Growth hormone deficiency","Hypothyroidism","Coeliac disease as a cause of growth failure in type 1 diabetes","Constitutional delay of growth and puberty"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Glycated haemoglobin and review of continuous or capillary glucose monitoring data","Liver function tests with serial transaminases to demonstrate reversibility","Liver ultrasound to characterise hepatomegaly and hepatic echogenicity","Liver biopsy in diagnostic doubt, with special stains to distinguish glycogen from steatosis","IGF-1, IGFBP-3 and assessment of the growth hormone axis","Bone age radiograph to quantify skeletal maturation","Coeliac and thyroid autoimmunity screening given associated conditions"],"redFlags":["Recurrent diabetic ketoacidosis or severe hypoglycaemia indicating dangerously unstable control","Rapidly enlarging tender liver or marked, unexplained rise in transaminases","Progressive growth arrest with falling height velocity","Clues to insulin omission or a psychosocial crisis underlying poor adherence"],"prognosis":"The syndrome is largely reversible, and hepatomegaly, transaminitis and growth usually improve with sustained, stable glycaemic control. Persistently poor control risks permanent short stature and the accrual of long-term microvascular complications.","pitfalls":["Mistaking glycogenic hepatopathy for fatty liver disease, which carries different management and prognosis.","Attributing the transaminitis to unrelated liver disease and overlooking the glycaemic cause.","Over-aggressive insulin correction that worsens hepatic glycogen loading and provokes hypoglycaemia.","Overlooking associated autoimmune conditions such as coeliac disease and hypothyroidism as contributors to growth failure."],"management":["Restore stable, sustained glycaemic control by optimising the insulin regimen (basal-bolus, insulin pump or continuous glucose monitoring), which reverses the syndrome","Structured diabetes education and adherence support to eliminate the wide swings between hyperglycaemia and insulin excess","Dietetic input and management of the eating and behavioural factors contributing to erratic control","Serial monitoring of liver function to confirm resolution of the glycogenic hepatopathy and transaminitis","Monitor growth, pubertal development and the growth hormone-IGF-1 axis, expecting catch-up with improved control","Screen for and manage emerging microvascular complications of diabetes","Psychosocial and family support, involving a paediatric diabetes multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard paediatric endocrinology textbooks","Clinical references on diabetes mellitus","Clinical hepatology references"],"crossLinks":[]},"may_hegglin_anomaly":{"name":"May-Hegglin anomaly","system":"Haematological system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MYH9-related disorder","Macrothrombocytopenia with Dohle-like bodies","May-Hegglin anomaly"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant disorder within the MYH9-related disease spectrum caused by mutations in the gene for non-muscle myosin heavy chain IIA.","Defined by the triad of macrothrombocytopenia, characteristic Dohle-like leucocyte inclusion bodies and generally mild bleeding.","The pale-blue cytoplasmic inclusions in neutrophils are aggregates of mutant myosin and can be confirmed by immunofluorescence for MYH9 protein.","Platelets are large or giant, and automated counters often underestimate the count by miscategorising platelets as red cells, so a manual film review is essential.","Many patients are asymptomatic or have only mild mucocutaneous bleeding disproportionately milder than the platelet count suggests.","The wider MYH9 spectrum (Epstein, Fechtner, Sebastian syndromes) may add sensorineural deafness, nephropathy and cataract, which can appear over time.","Bleeding management uses antifibrinolytics and desmopressin for minor procedures, reserving platelet transfusion for major haemorrhage.","Genotype broadly predicts risk of non-haematological features, guiding surveillance for renal and auditory complications."],"pathophysiology":"May-Hegglin anomaly is one phenotype of MYH9-related disease, caused by heterozygous mutations in MYH9, which encodes non-muscle myosin heavy chain IIA. This cytoskeletal motor protein is essential for cytokinesis, cell shape and migration, and for the terminal stages of megakaryocyte maturation and proplatelet formation. Mutant myosin disrupts orderly platelet release, yielding reduced numbers of abnormally large platelets, and aggregates within neutrophil cytoplasm to form the Dohle-like inclusion bodies. Because myosin IIA is also expressed in the cochlea, kidney podocytes and ocular lens, certain mutations additionally cause the sensorineural deafness, glomerular nephropathy and cataract seen across the MYH9 disease spectrum. Platelet function is largely preserved, explaining bleeding that is milder than the platelet count implies.","additionalDifferentials":["Immune thrombocytopenia","Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Other MYH9-related syndromes (Epstein, Fechtner, Sebastian)","Grey platelet syndrome","Type 2B or platelet-type von Willebrand disease with macrothrombocytopenia","Congenital macrothrombocytopenias (for example ACTN1, TUBB1, GP1BA/B)","Pseudothrombocytopenia from platelet clumping"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with mean platelet volume and manual platelet estimate","Peripheral blood film for giant platelets and neutrophil Dohle-like inclusions","Immunofluorescence staining for MYH9 protein distribution in neutrophils","MYH9 gene sequencing","Audiometry to detect sensorineural hearing loss","Urinalysis and renal function for proteinuria and nephropathy","Ophthalmological assessment for cataract"],"redFlags":["Significant haemorrhage after major surgery, trauma or delivery","Progressive proteinuria or renal impairment indicating MYH9 nephropathy","Progressive sensorineural hearing loss","Severe unexplained thrombocytopenia prompting inappropriate immunosuppression or splenectomy","Visual deterioration from cataract"],"prognosis":"Isolated May-Hegglin anomaly carries an excellent prognosis with normal life expectancy and usually only mild bleeding. Long-term outlook depends on the specific MYH9 mutation and the development of renal, auditory or ocular complications, which warrant lifelong monitoring.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the macrothrombocytopenia as immune thrombocytopenia and treating with steroids, immunoglobulin or splenectomy.","Accepting a falsely low automated platelet count without manual film review.","Overlooking the Dohle-like inclusions or dismissing them as reactive change in sepsis.","Failing to screen for the renal, auditory and ocular features of the wider MYH9 spectrum.","Transfusing platelets unnecessarily in a patient with mild bleeding and preserved platelet function."],"management":["Recognise the macrothrombocytopenia as MYH9-related to avoid misdiagnosis as immune thrombocytopenia and inappropriate corticosteroids, immunosuppression or splenectomy","No routine treatment is needed for the usually mild bleeding; use local haemostatic measures, antifibrinolytics (tranexamic acid) and desmopressin for minor bleeding or procedures","Reserve platelet transfusion for significant bleeding or major surgery","Monitor renal function and urinalysis for MYH9 nephropathy, with an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker for proteinuria","Audiometry surveillance for sensorineural hearing loss, with hearing aids or cochlear implantation as needed","Ophthalmology review for cataract","Autosomal dominant: offer genetic counselling and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology, current edition","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood, current edition","International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis guidance on inherited platelet disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"may_thurner_syndrome":{"name":"May-Thurner syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["iliac vein compression syndrome","Cockett syndrome","iliocaval compression syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Compression of the left common iliac vein between the overlying right common iliac artery and the underlying lumbar vertebra, typically at L5","Chronic pulsatile compression produces intraluminal venous spurs or webs and predisposes to left iliofemoral deep vein thrombosis and left-sided venous hypertension","Classically presents in young to middle-aged women with left leg swelling, pain, varicosities, or an unprovoked left iliofemoral DVT","Should be suspected in left-sided iliofemoral DVT, especially when unprovoked or recurrent","Cross-sectional venography (CT or MR) demonstrates the compression, while intravascular ultrasound best quantifies the stenosis and any post-thrombotic change","Management of the acute thrombotic presentation combines anticoagulation with catheter-directed thrombolysis or thrombectomy in selected patients, followed by angioplasty and stenting of the compressed segment","Correcting the underlying compression with venous stenting reduces post-thrombotic morbidity and rethrombosis"],"pathophysiology":"May-Thurner syndrome arises from the anatomical position of the right common iliac artery crossing over and compressing the left common iliac vein against the fifth lumbar vertebra. Repetitive arterial pulsation and mechanical compression cause endothelial injury and the development of intraluminal venous spurs or webs, producing a fixed venous stenosis. This impairs venous outflow from the left leg, causing venous hypertension and greatly increasing the risk of left iliofemoral deep vein thrombosis, which may present acutely or lead to chronic post-thrombotic sequelae.","additionalDifferentials":["Deep vein thrombosis from other causes","Post-thrombotic syndrome","Lymphoedema","Chronic venous insufficiency from superficial reflux","Extrinsic iliac vein compression by tumour or other mass","Cellulitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Venous duplex ultrasound as an initial assessment, though it visualises the iliac segments poorly","CT or MR venography to demonstrate iliac vein compression and collaterals","Intravascular ultrasound to quantify the stenosis and guide stenting","Contrast venography during intervention","Thrombophilia screen in unprovoked or recurrent thrombosis where appropriate","Assessment for post-thrombotic changes in chronic presentations"],"redFlags":["Acute massive iliofemoral DVT with limb compromise (phlegmasia)","Recurrent thrombosis despite adequate anticoagulation","Signs of pulmonary embolism","An extrinsic pelvic mass causing the compression"],"prognosis":"With recognition and treatment of the underlying compression by stenting, alongside thrombus removal and anticoagulation, outcomes are good with high stent patency; untreated compression predisposes to recurrent thrombosis and post-thrombotic syndrome.","pitfalls":["Treating left iliofemoral DVT with anticoagulation alone and missing the underlying compression","Relying on duplex alone, which visualises the iliac veins poorly","Under-recognising the syndrome in young women with left leg swelling","Failing to use intravascular ultrasound and thereby underestimating the degree of stenosis"],"management":["Anticoagulation for acute left iliofemoral deep vein thrombosis","Catheter-directed thrombolysis or pharmacomechanical thrombectomy for extensive acute iliofemoral thrombosis in selected patients","Endovascular angioplasty and stenting of the compressed iliac vein segment as the definitive treatment of the underlying compression","Graduated compression therapy for limb swelling and post-thrombotic syndrome","Surveillance of stent patency with duplex imaging and continuation of antithrombotic therapy after stenting","Thrombophilia evaluation in unprovoked or recurrent thrombosis","Refer to vascular and interventional specialists for assessment and intervention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","ESVS Clinical Practice Guidelines on Venous Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"mayer_rokitansky_kuster_hauser":{"name":"Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome","system":"Reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MRKH syndrome","müllerian aplasia","congenital absence of the uterus and vagina"],"chapter":null,"source":"Speroff's Clinical Gynecologic Endocrinology and Infertility","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["MRKH is congenital aplasia of the uterus and the upper (proximal) two-thirds of the vagina resulting from müllerian (paramesonephric) duct agenesis in a phenotypically normal 46,XX woman with functioning ovaries.","It is the second most common cause of primary amenorrhoea after gonadal dysgenesis and typically presents in adolescence with primary amenorrhoea despite normal breast and pubic-hair development.","Because the ovaries develop independently of the müllerian ducts and function normally, secondary sexual characteristics, cyclical hormone production and ovulation are preserved, with normal gonadotrophins and oestradiol.","Examination shows normal external genitalia with a short, blind-ending vaginal dimple or pouch and an absent or rudimentary uterus.","Type 1 (typical, isolated) is symmetrical uterovaginal aplasia with normal fallopian tubes and no extragenital anomaly; Type 2 (atypical) and the MURCS association add renal, skeletal (vertebral, Klippel-Feil) and, less often, cardiac and auditory anomalies.","Renal tract anomalies such as unilateral renal agenesis and ectopic or horseshoe kidney occur in a substantial proportion, so upper urinary tract imaging is mandatory.","First-line creation of a functional vagina is non-surgical self-dilation (Frank technique); vaginoplasty (Vecchietti, McIndoe, Davydov or bowel) is reserved for those who fail or decline dilation.","Ovulation is intact, so genetic parenthood is achievable through oocyte retrieval with gestational surrogacy and, increasingly, uterus transplantation; functioning endometrium in a rudimentary horn can rarely cause cyclical pain and haematometra."],"pathophysiology":"MRKH arises from failure of development of the müllerian (paramesonephric) ducts during embryogenesis, when these ducts would normally fuse and canalise to form the uterus, cervix and upper two-thirds of the vagina; the result is aplasia or hypoplasia of these structures, while the lower vagina, derived from the urogenital sinus, persists as a short pouch. Ovaries and fallopian tubes develop from separate lineages that are independent of the müllerian ducts, so ovarian endocrine and germ-cell function is retained and the karyotype is 46,XX with a normal female hormonal milieu. The aetiology is heterogeneous and largely sporadic, with familial clustering in some kindreds suggesting polygenic or multifactorial inheritance and implicating genes that govern müllerian duct and mesodermal development. The frequent coexistence of renal and skeletal anomalies reflects the shared intermediate-mesoderm origin of the urogenital and axial skeletal systems, which underlies the MURCS association.","additionalDifferentials":["Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (46,XY, absent or scant pubic hair, testes, absent uterus)","Transverse vaginal septum","Imperforate hymen with haematocolpos","Isolated distal vaginal agenesis with an intact uterus","Cervical agenesis or dysgenesis with a functioning uterus","Gonadal dysgenesis (e.g., Turner or Swyer syndrome)","Functional hypothalamic or constitutional causes of primary amenorrhoea with normal anatomy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype to confirm 46,XX and exclude androgen insensitivity and gonadal dysgenesis","Pelvic ultrasound and, definitively, pelvic MRI to assess uterine remnants, functioning endometrium and vaginal length","Renal tract imaging (ultrasound and MR urogram) for associated anomalies","Serum FSH, LH and oestradiol (expected normal) and serum testosterone to distinguish androgen insensitivity","Spinal and skeletal imaging where vertebral or Klippel-Feil anomalies are suspected","Audiological assessment and echocardiography in atypical or MURCS presentations","Ovarian reserve assessment (antral follicle count and anti-müllerian hormone) if assisted reproduction is planned"],"redFlags":["Cyclical or progressive pelvic pain from an obstructed functioning uterine remnant or haematometra requiring surgical excision","Undiagnosed renal anomaly such as a solitary or ectopic kidney affecting anaesthetic and surgical planning","Severe psychological distress, low mood or suicidal ideation following the diagnosis","Skeletal anomalies causing spinal deformity or, in MURCS, cardiac lesions needing evaluation"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is normal and, with vaginal dilation or vaginoplasty, satisfactory sexual function is achievable for most women. Fertility requires assisted reproduction with surrogacy or uterus transplantation, and the long-term outcome is strongly influenced by psychological support around the diagnosis.","pitfalls":["Confusing MRKH with complete androgen insensitivity, when pubic and axillary hair with a 46,XX karyotype distinguish MRKH and gonadal management differs entirely","Omitting renal tract imaging and missing a clinically important solitary or ectopic kidney","Overlooking a functioning rudimentary uterine horn as a treatable cause of cyclical pain","Proceeding to surgical vaginoplasty before offering first-line dilation therapy","Neglecting the profound psychological impact and failing to arrange specialist counselling and peer support"],"management":["First-line graduated vaginal self-dilation (Frank technique) to create a functional neovagina","Surgical vaginoplasty (Vecchietti, McIndoe skin-graft or bowel vaginoplasty) reserved for those in whom dilation fails or is declined","Structured psychological support and peer counselling addressing diagnosis disclosure, body image and sexual identity","Fertility counselling offering assisted reproduction with the woman's own retrieved oocytes via gestational surrogacy, or uterus transplantation in selected centres","Mandatory upper urinary tract imaging with urology referral for associated renal anomalies, plus skeletal and cardiac assessment in atypical or MURCS forms","Multidisciplinary follow-up spanning gynaecology, adolescent medicine, urology and clinical psychology into adulthood","Genetic counselling for the family, acknowledging the usually sporadic occurrence with occasional familial clustering"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Speroff's Clinical Gynecologic Endocrinology and Infertility","Berek & Novak's Gynecology","ACOG Committee Opinion: Müllerian Agenesis — Diagnosis, Management, and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"mayer_rokitansky_kuster_hauser_syndrome":{"name":"Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome","system":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MRKH syndrome","mullerian agenesis","mullerian aplasia","congenital absence of the uterus and vagina"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology texts / international guidance on mullerian anomalies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["MRKH is congenital agenesis or hypoplasia of the uterus and upper vagina from failure of mullerian duct development in a genetically and phenotypically female individual","It typically presents as primary amenorrhoea with normal secondary sexual characteristics and normal female external genitalia","The karyotype is 46,XX and the ovaries are present and functional, so pubertal development and hormone production are normal","The vagina is short or absent, which may also present as difficulty with intercourse","The type 1 form is isolated to the reproductive tract, while type 2 is associated with renal, skeletal, and other anomalies","Renal anomalies such as a solitary, pelvic, or absent kidney and vertebral or other skeletal defects are important associations to screen for","Management combines psychological support with creation of a functional vagina, using vaginal dilation as first-line and surgery when needed","Because the ovaries are normal, biological parenthood may be possible through assisted reproduction with a gestational carrier"],"pathophysiology":"Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome results from failure of development of the mullerian (paramesonephric) ducts, which normally form the uterus, cervix, and upper vagina. The defect leaves absent or rudimentary uterine structures and a shortened or absent upper vagina, while the lower vagina, derived separately, and the external genitalia are normal. Because ovarian tissue arises independently and is unaffected, the ovaries function normally with a 46,XX karyotype, producing normal secondary sexual characteristics. Shared embryological timing with the developing urinary and axial skeletal systems explains the frequent association with renal and vertebral anomalies in the type 2 form.","additionalDifferentials":["Androgen insensitivity syndrome","Transverse vaginal septum or imperforate hymen with outflow obstruction","Isolated vaginal agenesis with a functioning uterus","Gonadal dysgenesis","Other causes of primary amenorrhoea including hypothalamic and pituitary disorders","Complex disorders of sex development"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pelvic ultrasound to assess uterus, vagina, and ovaries","Pelvic magnetic resonance imaging to define mullerian and any rudimentary uterine structures","Karyotype to confirm 46,XX and distinguish from androgen insensitivity","Serum hormone profile confirming normal ovarian function","Renal tract imaging to detect associated urinary anomalies","Spinal and skeletal imaging where indicated for associated anomalies"],"redFlags":["Cyclical pelvic pain suggesting a functioning rudimentary uterine horn with obstructed menstruation","Undiagnosed associated renal anomaly affecting overall health","Significant psychological distress requiring dedicated support","Diagnostic uncertainty needing distinction from androgen insensitivity or a disorder of sex development"],"prognosis":"MRKH does not affect general health or life expectancy, and normal ovarian function preserves endocrine health and the potential for genetic offspring through assisted reproduction with a surrogate. With dilation or surgery a functional vagina can usually be achieved, and psychological support is central to long-term wellbeing.","pitfalls":["Confusing MRKH with androgen insensitivity by not checking karyotype and hormone profile","Failing to screen for associated renal and skeletal anomalies","Missing a functioning rudimentary uterine horn causing cyclical obstructed pain","Neglecting the psychological impact and support needs of the diagnosis"],"management":["Graduated vaginal dilation therapy as first-line creation of a functional vagina","Vaginoplasty (skin-graft, bowel or traction techniques) when dilation is unsuccessful or not chosen","Psychological support and counselling around the diagnosis, sexual function and future fertility","Assisted reproduction using the woman's own oocytes with a gestational carrier, or uterus transplantation where available","Screen for and manage associated renal tract, vertebral and skeletal anomalies with appropriate imaging and specialist referral","Coordinated multidisciplinary care and long-term follow-up through gynaecology, urology and psychology","Genetic counselling for the individual and family given occasional familial clustering"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology texts","International consensus and ESHRE/ESGE classification of female genital anomalies","Guidance on management of mullerian agenesis"],"crossLinks":[]},"mbl_deficiency":{"name":"Mannose-binding lectin deficiency","system":"Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MBL deficiency","mannan-binding lectin deficiency","MBL2 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Mannose-binding lectin is a pattern-recognition protein that initiates the lectin pathway of complement by binding carbohydrate structures on microbial surfaces","Low MBL levels are common in the general population, arising from polymorphisms in the MBL2 gene, and many people with low levels are entirely healthy","MBL deficiency is therefore best regarded as a susceptibility factor or disease modifier rather than a strong stand-alone immunodeficiency","Any increased infection risk is generally modest and most apparent in infancy, when adaptive immunity is still developing, or when combined with another immune defect or immunosuppression","Reported associations include recurrent respiratory infections in young children and a greater burden of infection in patients undergoing chemotherapy or with other coexisting deficiencies","The organisms involved are typically pyogenic bacteria, reflecting impaired complement-mediated opsonisation","Diagnosis relies on measuring serum MBL levels and, where relevant, MBL2 genotyping, while interpreting results cautiously in the light of the high population prevalence of low levels","There is no specific replacement therapy in routine use; management is supportive, with attention to vaccination, prompt treatment of infections, and evaluation for additional or more significant immune defects"],"pathophysiology":"Mannose-binding lectin is a serum collectin that recognises repeating sugar patterns on the surface of many microorganisms. On binding, it activates associated serine proteases (MASPs) to trigger the lectin pathway of complement, promoting opsonisation and clearance of pathogens independently of antibody. Common MBL2 polymorphisms reduce circulating MBL levels or impair its function, modestly weakening this early, antibody-independent arm of innate defence. Because other complement pathways and adaptive immunity usually compensate, the clinical impact is limited in most individuals; susceptibility becomes more evident when MBL deficiency coexists with immaturity of the immune system, immunosuppression, or another immune defect.","additionalDifferentials":["Antibody deficiencies (for example IgA deficiency or common variable immunodeficiency)","Complement component deficiencies affecting other pathways","Other causes of recurrent childhood respiratory infection","Secondary immunodeficiency from immunosuppression or malignancy","Normal variation in a healthy individual with incidental low MBL"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum mannose-binding lectin level and functional lectin pathway assay","MBL2 genotyping where clinically indicated","Broader immune workup (immunoglobulins, specific antibody responses, complement pathways) to exclude a more significant coexisting defect","Assessment of infection frequency and severity in clinical context","Review of concurrent immunosuppressive factors"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or severe infection that is disproportionate to isolated MBL deficiency, prompting a search for an additional immune defect","Serious infection during chemotherapy or other immunosuppression where MBL deficiency may compound risk","Invasive pyogenic infection needing prompt treatment","Recurrent infection in infancy warranting follow-up as adaptive immunity matures"],"prognosis":"The prognosis for isolated MBL deficiency is generally excellent, as most affected individuals have no significant clinical consequences; the main relevance is as a modifier that can worsen outcomes when combined with immune immaturity, immunosuppression, or another immunodeficiency.","pitfalls":["Over-attributing recurrent infections to MBL deficiency and stopping the workup prematurely, missing a more important coexisting defect","Treating an incidental low MBL level in a healthy person as a clinically significant disease","Failing to interpret MBL levels against the high population prevalence of low values","Neglecting standard vaccination and infection-prevention measures","Assuming a specific replacement therapy exists when management is essentially supportive"],"management":["No specific replacement therapy exists; care centres on prompt recognition and treatment of infection","Early empirical antibacterial therapy targeting pyogenic bacteria during intercurrent infections","Ensure full routine immunisation, including pneumococcal and other encapsulated-organism vaccines","Identify and mitigate coexisting immunosuppression or additional immune defects that unmask susceptibility","Heightened surveillance during infancy and periods of chemotherapy or immunosuppression","Investigate for a more significant coexisting immunodeficiency where infections are recurrent or severe","Patient education and reassurance given the generally benign course of isolated deficiency"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"mcad_deficiency":{"name":"Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCAD deficiency","MCADD","ACADM deficiency","medium-chain acyl-coenzyme A dehydrogenase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Commonest inherited disorder of fatty-acid oxidation, caused by biallelic ACADM variants; the c.985A>G (p.Lys304Glu) allele predominates in populations of northern European ancestry","The defective enzyme cannot dehydrogenate medium-chain (C6-C10) acyl-CoA substrates, so beta-oxidation stalls once glycogen stores are exhausted during fasting or intercurrent illness","Classic decompensation is hypoketotic hypoglycaemia with lethargy, vomiting and encephalopathy, often after a period of poor feeding or a viral illness","Newborn screening detects a characteristic acylcarnitine profile with elevated octanoylcarnitine (C8) and a raised C8:C10 ratio","Urine organic acids during crisis show medium-chain dicarboxylic aciduria with inappropriately low ketones","Cornerstone of management is avoidance of prolonged fasting, an emergency regimen with oral or intravenous glucose during illness, and prompt presentation when unwell","Many diagnosed on screening remain asymptomatic if fasting is avoided, but sudden unexpected death and Reye-like presentations occurred historically before screening"],"pathophysiology":"Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase catalyses the first, rate-limiting dehydrogenation step of mitochondrial beta-oxidation for medium-chain fatty acyl-CoA esters. When the enzyme is deficient, fatty acids cannot be fully oxidised to supply acetyl-CoA and hepatic ketone bodies during fasting, so tissues that depend on ketones and fatty-acid-derived energy are compromised and hypoglycaemia develops once hepatic glycogen is depleted. Accumulating medium-chain acyl-CoA intermediates are diverted to carnitine esters and dicarboxylic acids, and the shortage of acetyl-CoA impairs ureagenesis, producing hyperammonaemia in severe crises.","additionalDifferentials":["Other fatty-acid oxidation defects (VLCAD, LCHAD/trifunctional protein, SCAD deficiencies)","Carnitine cycle disorders (CPT1, CPT2, translocase, primary carnitine deficiency)","Reye syndrome and salicylate-associated encephalopathy","Ketotic hypoglycaemia and glycogen storage disorders","Hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia","Sepsis or acute viral encephalopathy in an unwell infant"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma acylcarnitine profile by tandem mass spectrometry (elevated C8, raised C8:C10)","Urine organic acids seeking medium-chain dicarboxylic aciduria and hexanoylglycine/suberylglycine","Blood glucose, ketones (beta-hydroxybutyrate), ammonia and lactate during acute illness","Plasma free and total carnitine","ACADM molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Enzyme assay in fibroblasts or leucocytes where genotype is inconclusive"],"redFlags":["Reduced consciousness or seizures with documented hypoketotic hypoglycaemia","Vomiting and refusal to feed during intercurrent illness in a known or at-risk infant","Hyperammonaemia or hepatic dysfunction suggesting metabolic decompensation","Apparent life-threatening event or sudden unexpected collapse in a young infant"],"prognosis":"With newborn screening, education on fasting avoidance and a written emergency regimen, the long-term outlook is excellent and most individuals develop normally. Risk of morbidity and death concentrates in the first decompensation, so prevention and early recognition are decisive.","pitfalls":["Attributing hypoglycaemia to a simple viral illness and missing the underlying oxidation defect","Expecting ketosis to accompany hypoglycaemia, when the hallmark is that ketones are inappropriately low","Allowing prolonged fasting for procedures or investigations without a glucose-containing infusion","Assuming an asymptomatic screen-detected infant needs no emergency plan"],"management":["Strict avoidance of prolonged fasting with regular carbohydrate-rich feeds and age-appropriate maximum fasting intervals","Written emergency regimen using oral glucose polymer during intercurrent illness, escalating to intravenous glucose if oral intake fails","During decompensation, intravenous dextrose to reverse hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and suppress lipolysis, with correction of hyperammonaemia","Low threshold for prompt hospital attendance during any febrile or vomiting illness","Avoid medium-chain triglyceride supplements and fat-loading, with metabolic dietitian involvement","Consider L-carnitine supplementation where secondary carnitine depletion is documented","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, with sibling testing and newborn-screening cascade","Provide emergency alert documentation identifying the metabolic diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"mcardle_disease":{"name":"McArdle disease (glycogen storage disease type V)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["glycogen storage disease type V","GSD V","myophosphorylase deficiency","muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency","PYGM deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Scriver's Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive myopathy caused by deficiency of muscle glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase, PYGM gene), impairing glycogen breakdown in skeletal muscle","Presents with exercise intolerance, painful muscle cramps, and early fatigue during brief high-intensity or sustained exertion","The classic 'second wind' phenomenon is a hallmark: symptoms ease if exercise is paused and then continued at a lower intensity as blood-borne fuels take over","Recurrent rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria (pigmenturia) can occur after strenuous activity and may precipitate acute kidney injury","The non-ischaemic (or ischaemic) forearm exercise test shows a flat or blunted rise in venous lactate with an exaggerated rise in ammonia","Serum creatine kinase is often persistently elevated at rest and rises further with exertion","Management is largely non-pharmacological: avoiding intense exertion, aerobic conditioning, and pre-exercise carbohydrate to reduce symptoms and rhabdomyolysis risk"],"pathophysiology":"McArdle disease is caused by deficiency of myophosphorylase, the muscle isoform of glycogen phosphorylase encoded by PYGM, which initiates glycogen breakdown by cleaving glucose units from glycogen in skeletal muscle. Without functional enzyme, muscle cannot mobilise its glycogen stores to fuel anaerobic and early aerobic exercise, so ATP generation fails during intense or sustained activity, producing cramps, contractures, and fatigue. Because glycolysis downstream cannot be fed from glycogen, blood lactate does not rise normally with exercise. Energy-depleted muscle fibres are prone to injury and breakdown, causing rhabdomyolysis and myoglobinuria. The 'second wind' reflects the delayed availability of blood glucose and free fatty acids as alternative fuels once the circulation delivers them to working muscle.","additionalDifferentials":["Other muscle glycogenoses (e.g. Tarui disease, phosphorylase b kinase deficiency)","Fatty acid oxidation disorders (e.g. CPT II deficiency)","Mitochondrial myopathies","Inflammatory or metabolic myopathies with rhabdomyolysis","Muscular dystrophies","Exertional rhabdomyolysis from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Forearm exercise test showing blunted lactate rise with exaggerated ammonia rise","Resting and post-exercise creatine kinase","Urine myoglobin testing after episodes of exertion","Molecular genetic testing of the PYGM gene","Muscle biopsy with histochemical staining for myophosphorylase activity","Renal function monitoring during episodes of rhabdomyolysis"],"redFlags":["Severe rhabdomyolysis with markedly elevated creatine kinase and pigmenturia","Acute kidney injury following an episode of myoglobinuria","Fixed muscle contractures or severe cramps during exertion"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is usually normal and most patients remain functional with lifestyle adaptation; the principal risks are recurrent rhabdomyolysis and consequent acute kidney injury, and a minority develop fixed muscle weakness with age.","pitfalls":["Dismissing exertional symptoms as deconditioning and missing the diagnosis for years","Performing an ischaemic forearm test with a tourniquet when a non-ischaemic protocol is safer and adequate","Advising complete avoidance of activity rather than encouraging graded aerobic conditioning","Overlooking the risk of acute kidney injury during rhabdomyolysis"],"management":["Structured low-to-moderate aerobic conditioning to raise oxidative capacity, avoiding maximal or sustained isometric exertion that triggers muscle injury","Pre-exercise oral sucrose or glucose to improve early exercise tolerance","Teach graded warm-up and pacing to exploit the 'second wind' as blood-borne fuels take over","Prompt recognition of rhabdomyolysis with early hydration and monitoring to prevent myoglobinuric acute kidney injury","Avoid myotoxic agents such as statins where possible","Dietary carbohydrate strategies under metabolic supervision to support exercise","Periodic monitoring of muscle symptoms, creatine kinase and renal function","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive condition with family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Scriver's The Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"mccune_albright":{"name":"McCune-Albright syndrome","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAS","polyostotic fibrous dysplasia with endocrinopathy","McCune-Albright-Sternberg syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically defined by the triad of polyostotic fibrous dysplasia, café-au-lait skin macules, and autonomous endocrine hyperfunction, though not all features are always present","Peripheral (gonadotropin-independent) precocious puberty is the most common endocrine manifestation, frequently the presenting problem in young girls","The café-au-lait patches typically have irregular jagged borders (often likened to the coast of Maine) and tend to follow the midline","Fibrous dysplasia weakens bone and can cause pain, deformity, and pathological fractures, and skull base involvement may compress cranial nerves","Additional autonomous endocrinopathies include hyperthyroidism, growth hormone excess, and Cushing syndrome in infancy","Renal phosphate wasting from excess FGF23 may produce hypophosphataemia and contribute to bone disease","The disorder is caused by a post-zygotic activating GNAS mutation, so it is mosaic and never inherited, with features reflecting the distribution of affected cells","Because endocrine overactivity is autonomous, treatment targets hormone excess at the gland or peripheral level rather than the hypothalamic-pituitary axis"],"pathophysiology":"McCune-Albright syndrome results from a somatic, post-zygotic activating mutation of GNAS encoding the stimulatory G protein alpha subunit. Constitutive activation of this signalling pathway causes cyclic AMP-dependent hormone-secreting cells to function autonomously, independent of normal trophic control, producing precocious puberty, hyperthyroidism, GH excess, or hypercortisolism. In bone, the mutation drives replacement of normal marrow and trabeculae with fibrous tissue (fibrous dysplasia), while in skin it produces melanocytic café-au-lait pigmentation. Because the mutation occurs after conception, the syndrome is mosaic, and the clinical pattern depends on which tissues carry the mutant clone.","additionalDifferentials":["Central (gonadotropin-dependent) precocious puberty","Neurofibromatosis type 1 (café-au-lait macules)","Isolated polyostotic fibrous dysplasia","Other causes of childhood hyperthyroidism such as Graves disease","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia and other causes of virilisation","Tumour-induced osteomalacia from FGF23 excess"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sex steroid levels with suppressed gonadotropins to confirm peripheral precocious puberty","Thyroid function tests and thyroid ultrasound","IGF-1 and growth hormone assessment for acromegaly/gigantism","Serum phosphate and FGF23 for renal phosphate wasting","Skeletal survey or bone scan to map fibrous dysplasia","Cortisol studies where infantile Cushing syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["Skull base fibrous dysplasia causing visual loss, hearing loss, or cranial nerve compression","Pathological fractures or severe progressive bone deformity","Infantile Cushing syndrome, which can be life-threatening and needs urgent evaluation","Severe hyperthyroidism or high-output complications from autonomous glandular overactivity"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the extent of skeletal disease and the number of endocrinopathies; many patients have manageable disease, but extensive fibrous dysplasia can cause chronic disability, and severe neonatal endocrine involvement carries higher risk.","pitfalls":["Treating precocious puberty as central and using GnRH analogues, which are ineffective for the peripheral mechanism","Overlooking additional autonomous endocrinopathies once one is found","Missing craniofacial fibrous dysplasia threatening vision or hearing","Attributing café-au-lait macules solely to neurofibromatosis","Ignoring hypophosphataemia as a contributor to bone fragility"],"management":["Peripheral precocious puberty controlled with aromatase inhibitors (letrozole) or an oestrogen-receptor modulator (tamoxifen) in girls, avoiding ineffective GnRH analogues","Autonomous hyperthyroidism treated with thionamide antithyroid drugs, progressing to thyroidectomy for definitive control","Growth hormone excess managed with somatostatin analogues and/or the GH-receptor antagonist pegvisomant","Bisphosphonate therapy for fibrous dysplasia bone pain, with orthopaedic fixation of fractures and correction of deformity","Correct FGF23-driven renal phosphate wasting with oral phosphate and active vitamin D (calcitriol)","Regular endocrine, skeletal and ophthalmological surveillance, including for skull-base cranial nerve compression","Multidisciplinary care with reassurance that the post-zygotic mosaic mutation is not inherited"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"mccune_albright_syndrome":{"name":"McCune-Albright syndrome","system":"Genetics / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAS","GNAS","polyostotic fibrous dysplasia with endocrinopathy","fibrous dysplasia/McCune-Albright syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / relevant specialty textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by a postzygotic activating (gain-of-function) variant in GNAS, producing somatic mosaicism, so the condition is not inherited and severity depends on the distribution of affected tissues","Classic triad comprises fibrous dysplasia of bone, cafe-au-lait skin macules and one or more hyperfunctioning endocrinopathies","Cafe-au-lait patches characteristically have irregular jagged (coast-of-Maine) borders and often respect the midline","Gonadotropin-independent precocious puberty is a common early presentation, frequently as the first sign in girls","Other endocrine features include hyperthyroidism, growth hormone excess, Cushing syndrome and phosphaturic renal phosphate wasting","Fibrous dysplasia can be monostotic or polyostotic, causing bone pain, deformity, fracture and, in the skull base, nerve compression","The GNAS variant constitutively activates the alpha subunit of the stimulatory G protein, driving cyclic-AMP overproduction and autonomous hormone secretion and cell proliferation"],"pathophysiology":"McCune-Albright syndrome results from an activating variant in GNAS occurring after fertilisation, producing a mosaic distribution of affected cells; a germline variant would be lethal, which explains why the condition arises only as somatic mosaicism. The variant renders the alpha subunit of the stimulatory G protein constitutively active, leading to unregulated adenylate cyclase activity and elevated intracellular cyclic AMP. In hormone-responsive endocrine tissues this drives autonomous hormone secretion independent of normal control, while in bone-forming cells it produces the fibrous dysplasia lesions. The pattern and severity of disease reflect which tissues carry the variant and in what proportion.","additionalDifferentials":["Neurofibromatosis type 1 with cafe-au-lait macules (smooth coast-of-California borders)","Central (gonadotropin-dependent) precocious puberty from other causes","Isolated fibrous dysplasia without endocrine features","Other causes of hyperthyroidism, Cushing syndrome or acromegaly","Hereditary hypophosphataemic rickets","Other mosaic overgrowth or RASopathy syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing for the GNAS activating variant, ideally on affected tissue given mosaicism","Skeletal survey or radionuclide bone scan to map fibrous dysplasia","Endocrine biochemistry for precocious puberty, thyroid function, growth hormone/IGF-1 and cortisol","Serum phosphate and markers of renal phosphate wasting (including FGF23-related studies)","Cross-sectional imaging of skull base for optic or auditory canal involvement","Bone-specific imaging and biopsy where malignant transformation is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive skull-base fibrous dysplasia threatening the optic nerve or hearing","Pathological fracture or severe progressive bone deformity","Rapid onset of precocious puberty or signs of severe endocrine excess","New pain, swelling or radiographic change suggesting rare malignant transformation of fibrous dysplasia"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is highly variable and depends on the extent and location of affected tissues, ranging from mild monostotic disease to severe polyostotic bone disease with multiple endocrinopathies. Malignant transformation of fibrous dysplasia is rare, and most morbidity relates to skeletal complications and hormonal excess, which are managed rather than cured.","pitfalls":["Testing blood alone and missing a low-level mosaic GNAS variant that is detectable in affected tissue","Confusing the coast-of-Maine cafe-au-lait borders with the smoother lesions of neurofibromatosis type 1","Overlooking phosphate wasting as a treatable contributor to bone disease","Assuming precocious puberty is centrally driven and giving inappropriate GnRH-analogue therapy"],"management":["Treat gonadotropin-independent precocious puberty with aromatase inhibitors (letrozole) or tamoxifen, as GnRH analogues are ineffective in the peripheral form","Manage autonomous hyperthyroidism with thionamides, progressing to thyroidectomy, and treat growth hormone excess with somatostatin analogues or pegvisomant","Bisphosphonates for fibrous dysplasia bone pain, with orthopaedic fixation and deformity correction","Replace phosphate and give active vitamin D for FGF23-mediated renal phosphate wasting","Surveillance for skull-base fibrous dysplasia causing optic or auditory canal compression, and rare malignant transformation","Explain that the postzygotic GNAS mosaicism is sporadic with negligible recurrence and transmission risk, so routine family screening is not required","Coordinated multidisciplinary follow-up across endocrinology, orthopaedics and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","GeneReviews: Fibrous Dysplasia/McCune-Albright Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"mckusick_kaufman_syndrome":{"name":"McKusick-Kaufman syndrome (MKKS)","system":"Genetics / Ciliopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MKKS","hydrometrocolpos-postaxial polydactyly-congenital heart disease syndrome","Kaufman-McKusick syndrome","BBS6"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad of hydrometrocolpos (in females), postaxial polydactyly and congenital heart disease","Hydrometrocolpos results from vaginal obstruction with fluid accumulation, presenting as a lower abdominal or pelvic mass in the newborn girl","Affected boys may have genital anomalies such as hypospadias","Caused by biallelic variants in MKKS, the same gene responsible for Bardet-Biedl syndrome type 6","Distinguishing it from Bardet-Biedl in infancy is difficult, because the retinal dystrophy, obesity, learning difficulties and renal disease of Bardet-Biedl emerge only later","Long-term follow-up is essential to detect emerging Bardet-Biedl features and revise the diagnosis if needed","MKKS encodes a chaperonin-like protein contributing to assembly of the BBSome and normal ciliary function"],"pathophysiology":"McKusick-Kaufman syndrome is caused by biallelic variants in MKKS, which encodes a chaperonin-like protein that helps assemble the BBSome, a complex trafficking membrane proteins into and along the primary cilium. Impaired ciliary function during development disturbs the patterning and morphogenesis of the genital tract, limbs and heart, producing hydrometrocolpos, postaxial polydactyly and congenital heart disease. Because the same gene, when more severely affected, causes Bardet-Biedl syndrome type 6, the two disorders lie on a continuum: the additional Bardet-Biedl features of retinal dystrophy, obesity, renal disease and learning difficulties depend on wider ciliary dysfunction and typically appear only with time, which is why the diagnosis may need revision during follow-up.","additionalDifferentials":["Bardet-Biedl syndrome (especially the BBS6 subtype)","Pallister-Hall syndrome","Ellis-van Creveld syndrome","Isolated hydrometrocolpos (imperforate hymen or vaginal atresia)","Isolated postaxial polydactyly","Other short-rib / polydactyly ciliopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pelvic and abdominal ultrasound for hydrometrocolpos and renal anomalies","Echocardiography for congenital heart disease","Radiographs of the hands and feet for polydactyly","Long-term ophthalmological monitoring, including electroretinography, for emerging retinal dystrophy","Molecular testing of MKKS with a Bardet-Biedl / ciliopathy gene panel"],"redFlags":["A tense abdominal or pelvic mass from hydrometrocolpos causing urinary obstruction or respiratory compromise","Haemodynamically significant congenital heart disease","Later emergence of retinal dystrophy or renal impairment indicating Bardet-Biedl syndrome"],"prognosis":"With timely decompression of hydrometrocolpos and management of any cardiac lesion the early outlook is good, but if the phenotype evolves into Bardet-Biedl syndrome the long-term prognosis follows that of the retinal and renal disease.","pitfalls":["Prematurely excluding Bardet-Biedl syndrome, which shares the MKKS gene and emerges later","Mistaking hydrometrocolpos for other pelvic masses and delaying decompression","Overlooking associated renal or cardiac anomalies","Failing to arrange long-term retinal and renal surveillance"],"management":["Prompt drainage and surgical relief of hydrometrocolpos in the newborn girl to correct vaginal outflow obstruction","Cardiological assessment with surgical repair of congenital heart lesions as indicated","Surgical excision of postaxial polydactyly and repair of genital anomalies such as hypospadias in boys","Long-term ophthalmological surveillance with electroretinography for emerging retinal dystrophy","Renal monitoring and attention to obesity and metabolic features should the phenotype evolve towards Bardet-Biedl syndrome","Long-term multidisciplinary follow-up to detect evolving Bardet-Biedl features and revise the diagnosis","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive condition, with testing of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews / OMIM: McKusick-Kaufman Syndrome","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"mcleod_syndrome":{"name":"McLeod syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["McLeod neuroacanthocytosis syndrome","XK disease","MLS","Kell/XK-related neuroacanthocytosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked recessive neuroacanthocytosis caused by variants in the XK gene, so affected patients are almost always male with maternally related pedigrees","Loss of the XK protein reduces expression of the Kell blood-group antigens, defining the McLeod red-cell phenotype detectable on blood-group serology","Presents in mid-adult life with chorea, dystonia and a subcortical movement disorder, often with psychiatric and cognitive change resembling other neuroacanthocytoses","Cardiomyopathy and cardiac arrhythmia are important systemic features that may dominate the prognosis","A myopathy with raised creatine kinase and a chronic axonal neuropathy with reduced or absent reflexes are common","Acanthocytes are present on blood film and haemolysis with compensated anaemia may occur","Recognition matters for transfusion safety, as these patients can develop antibodies to Kell antigens and require carefully matched or autologous blood"],"pathophysiology":"McLeod syndrome arises from pathogenic variants in the XK gene on the X chromosome, which encodes a membrane transport-related protein that is covalently linked to the Kell glycoprotein on the red-cell surface. Loss of XK weakens the erythrocyte membrane, producing acanthocytes and the McLeod blood-group phenotype with depressed Kell antigen expression. The same protein is expressed in brain, muscle and heart, and its deficiency drives striatal neurodegeneration, skeletal myopathy, peripheral neuropathy and a dilated cardiomyopathy, giving the characteristic multisystem picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Chorea-acanthocytosis (VPS13A disease)","Huntington disease and Huntington disease-like disorders","Neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation","Wilson disease","Acquired or drug-induced chorea","Other causes of dilated cardiomyopathy with neurological features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood film for acanthocytes using incubated wet-film technique","Kell blood-group phenotyping to identify the McLeod red-cell phenotype","Serum creatine kinase and markers of haemolysis","XK molecular genetic testing","Cardiac assessment with electrocardiography and echocardiography for cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia","Brain MRI for caudate atrophy and nerve conduction studies for neuropathy"],"redFlags":["Cardiac symptoms, syncope or arrhythmia suggesting cardiomyopathy that may be fatal","Need for blood transfusion, where mismatched Kell-positive blood risks a severe reaction","Progressive cognitive and psychiatric deterioration","New seizures or rapid neurological decline"],"prognosis":"McLeod syndrome is a slowly progressive multisystem disorder; neurological disability accrues over years, but cardiac involvement with cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia is a leading cause of morbidity and premature death, so cardiac surveillance is central to care.","pitfalls":["Transfusing standard donor blood without recognising the McLeod phenotype, risking alloimmune reactions","Attributing the movement disorder solely to Huntington disease and missing the X-linked pattern","Failing to screen for cardiomyopathy in a patient labelled as a pure movement disorder","Assuming a normal chorein result excludes neuroacanthocytosis, since McLeod syndrome requires XK and Kell testing"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy; symptomatic control of chorea with dopamine-depleting agents (tetrabenazine) or dopamine-receptor blockers","Psychiatric treatment of associated mood, psychotic and cognitive features","Cardiac surveillance with management of dilated cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia, including heart-failure therapy and device implantation where indicated","Transfusion planning using Kell-compatible (McLeod-phenotype) or pre-deposited autologous blood to avoid dangerous alloimmunisation","Multidisciplinary rehabilitation with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and swallow assessment","Monitor for compensated haemolytic anaemia and progressive myopathy","Genetic counselling for this X-linked disorder, with carrier testing of maternal relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"mdr_tuberculosis":{"name":"Multidrug-resistant tuberculosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MDR-TB","drug-resistant TB","DR-TB","pre-XDR-TB"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India NTEP","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Multidrug-resistant tuberculosis is defined by resistance to at least isoniazid and rifampicin, the two most important first-line drugs","Rifampicin resistance detected on rapid molecular testing is treated as a marker for probable MDR-TB and triggers a drug-resistant TB treatment pathway","Resistance may be primary, from transmission of a resistant strain, or acquired through inadequate, interrupted or poorly supervised prior treatment","Further resistance to fluoroquinolones and other key agents defines pre-XDR and extensively drug-resistant tuberculosis, which are harder to treat","Rapid molecular tests such as cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification and line-probe assays allow early detection of resistance and should be used up front","Modern treatment favours all-oral, shorter, drug-susceptibility-guided regimens including newer agents such as bedaquiline and linezolid, delivered under programmatic support","India's NTEP provides free universal drug-susceptibility testing and standardised drug-resistant TB regimens, with active follow-up to improve adherence and outcomes"],"pathophysiology":"Drug resistance in Mycobacterium tuberculosis arises from spontaneous chromosomal mutations in genes encoding drug targets or activating enzymes, for example rpoB mutations conferring rifampicin resistance and katG or inhA mutations conferring isoniazid resistance. Selective pressure from inadequate drug exposure, poor adherence, or malabsorption allows resistant mutants to become the dominant population. Sequential accumulation of mutations produces multidrug and extensively drug-resistant strains. These resistant organisms can be transmitted directly to new hosts, so resistance is propagated both by acquisition during treatment and by primary transmission within communities.","additionalDifferentials":["Drug-susceptible tuberculosis with treatment failure from non-adherence","Malabsorption of anti-tuberculosis drugs mimicking resistance","Non-tuberculous mycobacterial disease","Paradoxical reaction or immune reconstitution rather than true failure","Reinfection with a new strain"],"infectionMimics":["Non-tuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary disease","Drug-susceptible TB failing due to poor adherence","Chronic fungal lung infection","Post-tuberculous lung damage with secondary infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Rapid molecular testing (cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification) for rifampicin resistance at diagnosis","Line-probe assays and phenotypic drug-susceptibility testing to define the resistance pattern","Culture-based monitoring of treatment response","Baseline and monitoring investigations for drug toxicity (audiometry, ECG for QT, liver and renal function)","HIV testing and screening of close contacts"],"redFlags":["Rifampicin resistance on molecular testing requiring urgent switch to a drug-resistant TB pathway","Extensive or progressive disease with respiratory compromise","Emergence of additional resistance (pre-XDR or XDR) narrowing treatment options","Public health emergency of transmission of resistant strains, especially in congregate or HIV settings"],"prognosis":"Outcomes have improved substantially with all-oral shorter regimens containing newer agents, but MDR-TB still carries higher treatment failure, relapse and mortality than drug-susceptible disease, with worse outcomes as resistance broadens or when treatment is delayed.","pitfalls":["Adding a single new drug to a failing regimen, which amplifies resistance","Delaying drug-susceptibility testing and treating empirically as drug-susceptible disease","Under-recognising drug toxicities such as QT prolongation and peripheral neuropathy during monitoring","Neglecting adherence support and contact tracing, allowing transmission"],"management":["All-oral, shorter, susceptibility-guided regimen built around bedaquiline with companion agents such as pretomanid, linezolid and a later-generation fluoroquinolone (BPaLM)","Tailor the regimen to line-probe and phenotypic drug-susceptibility results, substituting agents as resistance broadens","Deliver treatment with programmatic or directly observed support and adherence measures to prevent amplification of resistance","Baseline and serial toxicity monitoring: ECG for QT prolongation, full blood count for linezolid myelosuppression, visual acuity, and liver and renal function","Culture-conversion monitoring to confirm treatment response","Airborne infection control with isolation and screening of close contacts","HIV testing with antiretroviral co-management and nutritional support","Refer to a specialist drug-resistant tuberculosis service for multidisciplinary management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/NTEP guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"measles":{"name":"Measles (Rubeola)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rubeola"],"chapter":211,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1638","clinicalPearls":["Caused by measles virus, a paramyxovirus; transmitted primarily by respiratory droplets and, less commonly, by small-particle aerosols that can remain suspended in air, making it highly transmissible (Harrison 22e p.1639).","Incubation is ~10 days to fever onset and ~14 days to rash onset; this period may be shorter in infants and longer (up to 3 weeks) in adults (Harrison 22e p.1640).","Classic prodrome is fever and malaise beginning ~10 days after exposure, followed by the three Cs: cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis, increasing in severity over ~4 days (Harrison 22e p.1640).","Koplik's spots (bluish-white ~1 mm dots surrounded by erythema on the buccal mucosa opposite the lower molars) are pathognomonic, appear ~2 days before the rash, and fade with rash onset (Harrison 22e p.1641).","The exanthem is an erythematous maculopapular rash beginning behind the ears and on the neck/hairline, spreading cephalocaudally to face, trunk, arms, and then legs/feet by the end of the second day; it fades in the same order and may desquamate, especially in undernourished children (Harrison 22e p.1641).","Care is largely supportive; vitamin A is recommended for children with measles and can markedly reduce morbidity and mortality (Harrison 22e p.1641).","Two doses of live attenuated measles vaccine (commonly as MMR or MMRV) are highly protective; ~85% develop protective antibody after one dose at 9 months and ~95% at 12 months (Harrison 22e p.1642).","Post-exposure human immunoglobulin within 72 h usually prevents infection and, given up to 6 days after exposure, can still prevent or modify disease in susceptible high-risk contacts (Harrison 22e p.1642).","Endemic transmission was eliminated in the United States in 2000, but outbreaks recur from imported cases amplified in under- and unvaccinated communities; vaccine hesitancy and pandemic-related disruption of immunization programs have driven a global resurgence (Harrison 22e p.1638, p.1369)."],"pathophysiology":"Measles virus is deposited in the respiratory tract, oropharynx, or conjunctivae, then proliferates locally in dendritic cells and lymphocytes and spreads to draining lymph nodes. It enters the bloodstream by budding from infected lymphocytes, producing a viremia that disseminates infection throughout the body (respiratory epithelium, lymphatic tissue, spleen, lung, thymus, liver, skin). Signs and symptoms appear 8-12 days after infection as virus replicates in target organs and the host immune response develops; the characteristic rash is itself a consequence of the cellular immune response. TH1 (interferon-gamma) responses are essential for viral clearance and later TH2 (interleukin-4) responses drive protective antibody. Paradoxically, measles induces a state of immunosuppression lasting weeks to months that depresses responses to unrelated antigens, reduces the magnitude and diversity of pre-existing antibody (\"immune amnesia\"), and increases susceptibility to secondary bacterial and viral infections, accounting for much measles-related morbidity and death.","additionalDifferentials":["Rubella (German measles) - milder, no cough, with posterior auricular/suboccipital lymphadenopathy","Kawasaki disease","Infectious mononucleosis (atypical lymphocytosis, in contrast to the leukopenia common in measles)","Roseola (exanthem subitum) - rash appears after fever subsides","Scarlet fever","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Enterovirus or adenovirus infection","Drug sensitivity/eruption"],"infectionMimics":["Rubella","Infectious mononucleosis (Epstein-Barr virus)","Roseola/exanthem subitum (HHV-6/HHV-7)","Scarlet fever","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Enterovirus (echovirus, coxsackievirus) infection","Adenovirus infection","Early meningococcemia or rickettsial infection (morbilliform before petechiae/purpura)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Kawasaki disease","Drug eruption / drug sensitivity","Acute graft-versus-host disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Detection of measles virus-specific IgM in a single serum or oral fluid specimen is diagnostic of acute infection","Fourfold or greater rise in measles virus-specific IgG between acute- and convalescent-phase sera","Note timing pitfall: IgM may not be detectable until 4-5 days after rash onset (false-negatives if drawn too early) and usually falls to undetectable within 4-8 weeks","Reverse-transcription PCR for measles virus RNA (sensitive/specific; can genotype and distinguish wild-type from vaccine strain)","Virus isolation in cell culture from respiratory secretions, nasopharyngeal/conjunctival swabs, blood, or urine","Direct detection of giant cells in respiratory secretions, urine, or biopsy tissue","Neutralization tests (highly correlated with protective immunity but expensive and laborious)"],"redFlags":["Pneumonia - secondary bacterial bronchopneumonia or measles giant cell pneumonitis (the latter in immunocompromised persons); recurrence or persistence of fever after the rash suggests secondary bacterial infection","Acute laryngotracheobronchitis (croup) with risk of airway obstruction, particularly in young children","Post-measles encephalomyelitis (~1 in 1000 cases) within 2 weeks of rash onset with fever, seizures, and neurologic abnormalities - thought to be autoimmune","Measles inclusion body encephalitis (MIBE) - rare, fatal, in those with defective cellular immunity, months after infection","Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) - slowly progressive, fatal, 5-15 years after infection (mostly in those infected at <2 years)","Diarrhea worsening undernutrition","Absent or delayed rash in immunocompromised or severely undernourished children with impaired cellular immunity - associated with a high case-fatality rate"],"prognosis":"Most persons recover and develop long-term (generally lifelong) protective immunity. Case-fatality varies with age at infection, nutritional and immunologic status, vaccine coverage, and access to care. In most developed countries the case-fatality rate is 0.01-0.1%, but in endemic areas of sub-Saharan Africa it may be 5-10% or higher, and in refugee camps and internally displaced populations rates have reached 20-30%. Previously vaccinated persons who become infected have less severe disease and significantly lower mortality. Measles still causes more than 100,000 deaths per year worldwide.","pitfalls":["Patients are contagious before recognizable disease appears, which undermines isolation measures; nosocomial transmission in waiting rooms, hospitals, and physicians' offices is well recognized.","Clinical diagnosis is difficult during the prodrome, when the rash is attenuated by passively acquired antibody or prior immunization, when the rash is absent/delayed in immunocompromised or severely undernourished children, and in low-incidence regions.","Measles virus-specific IgM may be falsely negative if serum is obtained too early (within the first few days of rash onset).","Persons with impaired cellular immunity may not develop the characteristic rash, may develop giant cell pneumonitis, and have a high case-fatality rate.","There is no specific antiviral therapy; ribavirin benefit has not been conclusively demonstrated in trials.","The 1998 report hypothesizing an MMR-autism link was a misinterpreted temporal association, has been refuted by numerous studies, and was retracted - there is no causal link."],"management":["Supportive care with antipyretics, oral or intravenous rehydration and rest, as there is no specific licensed antiviral","Vitamin A supplementation for affected children to reduce morbidity and mortality","Treat secondary bacterial complications such as pneumonia and otitis media with appropriate antibacterials, which are not given routinely","Airborne and contact isolation to limit the high transmissibility, with statutory public-health notification","Post-exposure prophylaxis for susceptible contacts using MMR vaccine early after exposure, or human immunoglobulin where vaccine is contraindicated","Monitor for and manage complications including pneumonia, croup, encephalitis and dehydration","Consider ribavirin only in severe disease or immunocompromised patients, acknowledging limited evidence","Primary prevention through MMR immunisation of the population"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal grading scale. Severity is driven by host and population factors: severe or fatal disease occurs in those with impaired/deficient T-lymphocyte (cellular) immunity and in malnourished/undernourished children (e.g., vitamin A deficiency), among whom the rash may be absent and giant cell pneumonitis and high case-fatality occur; pregnant women, immunocompromised persons, and infants <1 year are at risk of severe measles and are prioritized for immunoglobulin prophylaxis.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1638","Harrison 22e p.1639","Harrison 22e p.1640","Harrison 22e p.1641","Harrison 22e p.1642","Harrison 22e p.1117 (SSPE)","Harrison 22e p.1369 (travel/outbreaks)"],"crossLinks":["ENCEPHALITIS"]},"measles_inclusion_body_encephalitis":{"name":"Measles inclusion body encephalitis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MIBE","measles inclusion-body encephalitis","subacute measles encephalitis","immunosuppressive measles encephalitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A subacute encephalitis caused by persistent measles virus infection of the brain occurring specifically in immunocompromised patients","It is seen in children and adults with impaired cell-mediated immunity, such as leukaemia and lymphoma, chemotherapy, HIV, or transplantation","Unlike SSPE, the latent interval is short, typically weeks to a few months after measles exposure or infection, and the illness may lack a preceding rash","Presentation is with altered mental status, focal neurological deficits, and, very characteristically, refractory focal seizures including epilepsia partialis continua","Because antibody responses are blunted by immunosuppression, cerebrospinal fluid measles antibody may be low or absent, unlike the high titres of SSPE","Diagnosis rests on detecting measles virus RNA or antigen and viral inclusions in brain tissue, supported by imaging, since serology is unreliable in these hosts","The prognosis is poor; there is no established effective therapy, and prevention through avoiding measles exposure and ensuring immunity in contacts is essential"],"pathophysiology":"Measles inclusion body encephalitis develops when the measles virus persists and replicates within the central nervous system of a host whose cell-mediated immunity is too impaired to clear it. In contrast to subacute sclerosing panencephalitis, where a defective virus smoulders for years and provokes intense antibody production, here the immunosuppressed brain permits relatively active viral replication over a much shorter period with only a weak immune response. Neurones and glia contain intranuclear and intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies packed with viral nucleocapsids, and there is neuronal loss with comparatively little inflammatory infiltrate because the immune response is suppressed. The rapidly progressive cortical involvement produces the characteristic intractable focal seizures and focal deficits, and the blunted humoral response explains why CSF measles antibody is often unhelpful for diagnosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex and other viral encephalitides in the immunocompromised","Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy","Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis","Cerebral toxoplasmosis or CNS lymphoma","Metabolic or drug-related encephalopathy","Autoimmune or paraneoplastic encephalitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detection of measles virus RNA by PCR or antigen in cerebrospinal fluid or brain tissue","Brain biopsy showing intranuclear and intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies with immunostaining for measles antigen","MRI of the brain to identify cortical and subcortical signal change","Electroencephalography, often showing focal epileptiform activity rather than the periodic complexes of SSPE","Assessment of immune status including HIV testing, CD4 count, and chemotherapy or transplant history","Measles exposure and immunisation history of the patient and close contacts"],"redFlags":["Refractory focal seizures or epilepsia partialis continua in an immunocompromised patient","Progressive decline in conscious level or new focal deficits","Recent measles exposure or infection in a severely immunosuppressed host","Status epilepticus or rapid neurological deterioration"],"prognosis":"The outlook is poor, with most patients deteriorating over weeks to months and high mortality; survival and any recovery depend heavily on restoring immune function, and no antiviral regimen is reliably effective.","pitfalls":["Excluding measles because CSF antibody is low, when the immunosuppressed host cannot mount a strong response","Confusing MIBE with SSPE despite their different time course, immune context, and EEG findings","Failing to obtain brain tissue or molecular testing when serology is unrevealing","Overlooking measles exposure in an immunocompromised patient with new refractory seizures"],"management":["Restore cell-mediated immunity where possible by reducing immunosuppression, treating HIV with antiretroviral therapy, or achieving immune reconstitution","Attempt antiviral therapy with ribavirin (systemic and/or intraventricular), with or without interferon, recognising the limited anecdotal evidence","Aggressive control of refractory focal seizures and epilepsia partialis continua with combination antiepileptic drugs","Neurocritical supportive care addressing airway protection, nutrition and raised intracranial pressure","Multidisciplinary input from neurology, infectious diseases and the primary immunosuppressing service","Early discussion of the grave prognosis with palliative and family support where deterioration is relentless","Prevention through measles immunisation of susceptible contacts and immunoglobulin prophylaxis after exposure in immunocompromised patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","UpToDate: Measles-associated neurological disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"mechanical_low_back_pain":{"name":"Mechanical low back pain","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["non-specific low back pain","lumbar strain","simple backache","lumbago"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon / standard sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most low back pain is non-specific and mechanical, varying with posture and movement, often eased by rest and lacking a single identifiable structural cause","The clinical priority is to triage into non-specific mechanical pain, radicular pain, or serious pathology using red-flag screening","Imaging is not indicated for acute non-specific back pain without red flags because findings correlate poorly with symptoms and may lead to unnecessary intervention","Advise patients to stay active and avoid bed rest, as continued activity improves recovery","Management centres on reassurance, education, analgesia by principle, and early return to normal activity and exercise","Psychosocial yellow flags such as fear-avoidance beliefs, low mood, and job dissatisfaction predict progression to chronic disability and should be addressed","Exercise therapy and, where appropriate, manual therapy or a structured self-management programme help recovery and prevent recurrence","The natural history is generally favourable, with most acute episodes settling within a few weeks, though recurrence is common","Persistent or worsening pain, or the emergence of red-flag features, warrants reassessment and targeted investigation"],"pathophysiology":"Mechanical low back pain arises from the spinal structures under load, including the intervertebral discs, facet joints, ligaments, and paraspinal muscles, without a single identifiable lesion in most cases. Age-related disc and facet degeneration, muscular strain, and altered movement patterns contribute. Pain is influenced by biomechanical factors together with central pain processing and psychosocial factors, which can drive the transition from acute to chronic pain.","additionalDifferentials":["Lumbar radiculopathy or sciatica from disc herniation or stenosis","Vertebral compression or other fracture","Spondyloarthropathy (inflammatory back pain)","Vertebral infection (discitis or osteomyelitis)","Malignancy or metastatic disease","Cauda equina syndrome","Referred pain from abdominal, pelvic, or vascular pathology such as aortic aneurysm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["No imaging for acute non-specific back pain in the absence of red flags","MRI when serious pathology is suspected or radicular/neurological features persist and intervention is considered","Plain radiographs for suspected fracture or structural deformity","Blood tests such as inflammatory markers and appropriate screening if infection, malignancy, or inflammatory disease is suspected","Bone imaging or specialist referral guided by the clinical picture"],"redFlags":["Cauda equina features: saddle anaesthesia, bladder or bowel dysfunction (retention or incontinence), and bilateral or progressive leg weakness requiring emergency assessment","Features suggesting malignancy: age over 50, past cancer, unexplained weight loss, and progressive unremitting or night pain","Features suggesting infection: fever, intravenous drug use, immunosuppression, and severe night pain","Significant trauma or possible osteoporotic fracture, and inflammatory features such as morning stiffness in a young adult"],"prognosis":"The majority of acute episodes improve substantially within a few weeks, but recurrences are common and a minority develop persistent disabling chronic pain, strongly influenced by psychosocial factors.","pitfalls":["Requesting early imaging for non-specific pain, leading to incidental findings and overtreatment","Missing red flags for cauda equina, malignancy, or infection","Recommending bed rest instead of maintaining activity","Ignoring yellow flags, allowing progression to chronic disability"],"management":["Reassurance and education emphasising the benign course, staying active and avoiding bed rest","Analgesia by principle with NSAIDs first-line and short-term weak opioids only if needed, avoiding long-term opioid use","Structured exercise and physiotherapy with graded return to normal activity and work","Identify and address psychosocial yellow flags, using cognitive behavioural approaches for those at risk of chronicity","Multidisciplinary rehabilitation and manual therapy for persistent or recurrent pain","Avoid imaging in acute non-specific pain without red flags","Screen for red flags and escalate promptly for suspected serious pathology or progressive neurological deficit"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"meckel_gruber_syndrome":{"name":"Meckel-Gruber syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Meckel syndrome","dysencephalia splanchnocystica","MKS","MKS1","TMEM67","CEP290","B9D1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A lethal autosomal recessive ciliopathy classically defined by the triad of occipital encephalocele, bilateral enlarged cystic (polycystic) kidneys, and postaxial polydactyly","Hepatic involvement with ductal plate malformation and fibrosis is a further consistent feature, and many consider the hepatic changes near-universal","It sits at the severe end of the ciliopathy spectrum and shares causative genes with Joubert syndrome and other ciliopathies, illustrating phenotype overlap","The massively enlarged kidneys cause oligohydramnios, leading to pulmonary hypoplasia and the Potter sequence of features","Prenatal diagnosis is often possible in the first or early second trimester from the combination of encephalocele, large echogenic cystic kidneys, and polydactyly on ultrasound","Additional malformations may include other posterior fossa or neural tube defects, cleft lip or palate, and genital anomalies","Diagnosis is clinical and radiological, supported by molecular testing, and is important for recurrence-risk counselling given the twenty-five percent recurrence in future pregnancies"],"pathophysiology":"Meckel-Gruber syndrome is a severe ciliopathy caused by biallelic variants in genes encoding proteins of the ciliary transition zone and basal body. Loss of normal primary cilium function disrupts developmental signalling across multiple organs during embryogenesis, producing central nervous system malformation (typically an occipital encephalocele), cystic dysplasia of the renal collecting system, ductal plate malformation of the liver, and limb patterning defects manifesting as polydactyly. The renal enlargement reduces amniotic fluid, and the consequent oligohydramnios impairs lung development.","additionalDifferentials":["Joubert syndrome and related disorders at the milder end of the ciliopathy spectrum","Trisomy 13 with overlapping midline and polydactyly features","Bardet-Biedl syndrome","Hydrolethalus syndrome","Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease with encephalocele","Isolated neural tube defect with incidental renal anomaly"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed foetal ultrasound and, where available, foetal MRI to define the encephalocele and renal cystic disease","Postnatal or post-mortem examination and imaging to document the classic triad","Molecular genetic testing using a ciliopathy panel or exome sequencing","Renal ultrasound to characterise cystic dysplasia and measure kidney size","Liver imaging and histology for ductal plate malformation and fibrosis","Karyotype or microarray to exclude chromosomal differentials such as trisomy 13"],"redFlags":["Severe oligohydramnios with pulmonary hypoplasia incompatible with sustained postnatal breathing","Large occipital encephalocele with neural tissue herniation","Rapidly enlarging cystic kidneys causing abdominal distension and renal failure","Multi-organ malformation indicating a lethal prognosis requiring sensitive counselling"],"prognosis":"Meckel-Gruber syndrome is uniformly lethal, with death occurring before birth or within the neonatal period, chiefly from pulmonary hypoplasia and renal failure. Management is directed at prenatal diagnosis, compassionate perinatal care, and genetic counselling for recurrence risk.","pitfalls":["Confusing milder ciliopathies such as Joubert syndrome with the lethal Meckel-Gruber phenotype because of shared genes","Overlooking hepatic ductal plate malformation, which may be less clinically obvious than the renal and CNS features","Failing to offer post-mortem examination and genetic testing, losing the opportunity for accurate recurrence counselling","Attributing the pulmonary hypoplasia to a primary lung problem rather than to oligohydramnios from renal disease"],"management":["No curative therapy exists; care is directed at prenatal diagnosis, compassionate perinatal management and family support","Offer detailed prenatal counselling with the option of termination once the diagnosis is established on ultrasound and molecular testing","Provide compassionate perinatal palliative care for liveborn infants given the uniformly lethal pulmonary hypoplasia and renal failure","Confirm the diagnosis with foetal imaging, postnatal or post-mortem examination and ciliopathy-panel genetic testing","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive condition, quantifying the recurrence risk to parents","Offer prenatal or preimplantation genetic testing in subsequent pregnancies once the familial variants are known","Multidisciplinary involvement of foetal medicine, neonatology, clinical genetics and bereavement support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","GeneReviews: Meckel Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"meconium_aspiration_syndrome":{"name":"Meconium Aspiration Syndrome","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAS","meconium aspiration"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Respiratory distress in a newborn born through meconium-stained amniotic fluid, when meconium is aspirated into the airways around the time of birth.","It occurs mainly in term and post-term infants and is associated with fetal distress and intrauterine hypoxia.","Affected infants may be meconium-stained, have distress from birth with tachypnoea and recession, and show a barrel-shaped, hyperinflated chest.","The chest radiograph typically shows patchy or coarse infiltrates with areas of hyperinflation and atelectasis, and air leaks are common.","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn frequently complicates the syndrome and contributes to severe hypoxaemia.","Routine intrapartum suctioning of the airway and routine tracheal suction of vigorous meconium-stained infants are no longer recommended.","Non-vigorous infants born through meconium may need resuscitation, and management is largely supportive with oxygen and respiratory support.","Severe cases may require mechanical ventilation, surfactant, inhaled nitric oxide for pulmonary hypertension, and occasionally extracorporeal membrane oxygenation.","Air leaks such as pneumothorax and pneumomediastinum are important and potentially sudden complications."],"pathophysiology":"Intrauterine stress and hypoxia can provoke passage of meconium into the amniotic fluid and gasping that draws it into the airways. Aspirated meconium causes mechanical airway obstruction with a ball-valve effect producing air trapping and air leaks, a chemical pneumonitis with inflammation, and inactivation of surfactant. These changes cause ventilation-perfusion mismatch and hypoxaemia, and, combined with hypoxia-driven pulmonary vasoconstriction, promote persistent pulmonary hypertension with right-to-left shunting.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal pneumonia or sepsis","Transient tachypnoea of the newborn","Respiratory distress syndrome (in less mature infants)","Persistent pulmonary hypertension from other causes","Air-leak syndromes","Congenital heart disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph for patchy infiltrates, hyperinflation, and air leaks","Pulse oximetry (pre- and post-ductal) and blood gas analysis","Echocardiography to assess for pulmonary hypertension","Sepsis screen when infection cannot be excluded","Continuous cardiorespiratory monitoring"],"redFlags":["Severe or refractory hypoxaemia despite respiratory support","Large pre- to post-ductal saturation gradient suggesting pulmonary hypertension","Sudden deterioration indicating pneumothorax or other air leak","Signs of hypoxic-ischaemic injury to other organs","Escalating ventilatory requirements or shock"],"prognosis":"Most infants with mild to moderate disease recover with supportive care, but severe disease complicated by persistent pulmonary hypertension or air leaks carries significant morbidity and mortality; survivors may have transient respiratory sequelae.","pitfalls":["Performing routine intrapartum or tracheal suctioning that is no longer recommended","Underestimating the risk of pneumothorax and missing a sudden air leak","Failing to recognise coexisting persistent pulmonary hypertension","Overlooking associated hypoxic-ischaemic injury to other organs","Delaying escalation of respiratory support in rapidly deteriorating infants"],"management":["Respiratory support graded to severity, from supplemental oxygen and CPAP to mechanical ventilation","Exogenous surfactant replacement, or surfactant lavage, to counter meconium-induced surfactant inactivation","Treat persistent pulmonary hypertension with inhaled nitric oxide, optimal oxygenation and avoidance of acidosis","Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation for refractory hypoxaemic respiratory failure","Vigilance for and prompt drainage of air leaks such as pneumothorax","Empirical antibacterials when sepsis cannot be excluded, alongside continuous cardiorespiratory monitoring and supportive neonatal intensive care","Follow current resuscitation guidance that no longer recommends routine intrapartum or tracheal suctioning of vigorous meconium-stained infants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"meconium_ileus":{"name":"Meconium ileus","system":"Paediatric surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neonatal meconium obstruction","inspissated meconium obstruction","simple meconium ileus","complicated meconium ileus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Ashcraft Pediatric Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A neonatal distal small-bowel obstruction caused by abnormally thick, tenacious meconium impacting the terminal ileum","It is the earliest clinical manifestation of cystic fibrosis in a large proportion of affected infants, so all cases warrant sweat testing and CFTR genetic evaluation","Presents in the first days of life with abdominal distension, bilious vomiting, and failure to pass meconium","Abdominal radiographs show dilated small-bowel loops with a soap-bubble or ground-glass appearance in the right lower quadrant and characteristically few air-fluid levels","Divided into simple obstruction and complicated forms with volvulus, atresia, perforation, or meconium peritonitis with calcification","A water-soluble contrast (gastrograffin-type) enema is both diagnostic, showing a microcolon and inspissated pellets, and therapeutic in uncomplicated cases","Surgery is reserved for enemas that fail or for complicated disease, and long-term management addresses the underlying cystic fibrosis"],"pathophysiology":"In meconium ileus, deficient CFTR chloride channel function reduces luminal fluid secretion and, together with abnormal pancreatic enzyme delivery, produces meconium that is dehydrated, viscid, and adherent. This inspissated meconium obstructs the distal ileum, causing proximal small-bowel dilatation and an unused microcolon distally. In complicated disease, the heavy, obstructed loops may twist (volvulus), lose their blood supply, become atretic, or perforate in utero, spilling sterile meconium into the peritoneum and producing meconium peritonitis with dystrophic calcification.","additionalDifferentials":["Ileal or jejunal atresia","Hirschsprung disease","Meconium plug syndrome and small left colon syndrome","Anorectal malformation with distal obstruction","Neonatal small left colon in an infant of a diabetic mother","Malrotation with midgut volvulus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal radiograph for dilated loops, soap-bubble sign, and any peritoneal calcification suggesting perforation","Water-soluble contrast enema to confirm microcolon and locate inspissated pellets, with therapeutic intent","Sweat chloride testing and CFTR mutation analysis to confirm cystic fibrosis","Newborn screening (immunoreactive trypsinogen) follow-up","Serial examination and imaging to detect complicated features such as volvulus or perforation","Prenatal ultrasound review for echogenic bowel, dilated loops, or intra-abdominal calcification"],"redFlags":["Signs of peritonitis, marked tenderness, or abdominal wall erythema indicating perforation or meconium peritonitis","Rapid clinical deterioration or haemodynamic instability suggesting volvulus or ischaemia","Failure of contrast enema to relieve obstruction, mandating operative management","Free intraperitoneal air or a pseudocyst on imaging","Respiratory compromise from severe distension"],"prognosis":"With prompt relief of obstruction, outcomes for the bowel are generally good, but overall prognosis is dominated by the underlying cystic fibrosis and its pulmonary and pancreatic manifestations. Complicated meconium ileus carries higher perioperative morbidity but survival with modern care remains favourable.","pitfalls":["Failing to test for cystic fibrosis in every infant with meconium ileus","Attempting contrast enema treatment when perforation or a complicated lesion is present","Missing an associated atresia or volvulus behind the obstruction","Inadequate attention to hydration status during hyperosmolar contrast enema","Overlooking evolving meconium peritonitis when abdominal calcification is present"],"management":["In uncomplicated cases, therapeutic water-soluble (gastrografin-type) contrast enema, sometimes repeated, to disimpact inspissated meconium, with careful concurrent intravenous rehydration","Nasogastric decompression with fluid and electrolyte correction as initial resuscitation","Surgical intervention for complicated disease with volvulus, atresia, perforation or meconium peritonitis, using enterotomy and irrigation, resection or a temporary stoma","Adjunctive N-acetylcysteine to soften and dissolve tenacious meconium","Confirm underlying cystic fibrosis by sweat chloride and CFTR analysis, and initiate pancreatic enzyme replacement, fat-soluble vitamins, CFTR-modulator and respiratory care through the CF team","Genetic counselling for cystic fibrosis, an autosomal recessive condition, with family testing","Multidisciplinary follow-up combining paediatric surgery and the cystic fibrosis service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Ashcraft's Pediatric Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"medial_collateral_ligament_injury":{"name":"Medial collateral ligament injury","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCL sprain","medial collateral ligament tear","tibial collateral ligament injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Review of Orthopaedics / Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most commonly injured knee ligament, typically from a valgus force to the lateral knee","Graded I-III by the degree of medial laxity on valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion","Pain and tenderness localise along the medial joint line and femoral or tibial attachment","The great majority of isolated MCL injuries heal without surgery owing to the ligament's good blood supply","Valgus laxity in full extension implies a more severe injury with cruciate or capsular involvement","Part of the 'unhappy triad' alongside ACL and medial meniscus injury","A Pellegrini-Stieda lesion (calcification at the femoral origin) can follow chronic proximal MCL injury"],"pathophysiology":"The superficial MCL is the primary restraint to valgus stress. A laterally directed blow or valgus load stretches or tears its fibres, most often at the femoral origin. Because the ligament is extra-articular with a rich blood supply, isolated tears typically heal with conservative management; combined injury to the cruciates or posteromedial corner produces greater instability and worse healing.","additionalDifferentials":["Medial meniscal tear","ACL injury (unhappy triad)","Pes anserine bursitis","Medial patellofemoral ligament injury after patellar dislocation","Physeal injury in skeletally immature patients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Valgus stress testing at 0 and 30 degrees","MRI to grade the tear and detect associated injuries","Radiographs to exclude fracture and detect Pellegrini-Stieda calcification or physeal injury","Stress radiographs in skeletally immature patients to distinguish physeal fracture"],"redFlags":["Valgus laxity in full extension indicating combined ligamentous injury","Multiligament injury or knee dislocation","Physeal fracture mimicking ligament injury in children"],"prognosis":"Isolated MCL injuries of all grades usually recover well with bracing and rehabilitation; outcomes are less predictable when the injury is combined with cruciate or posteromedial corner damage.","pitfalls":["Assuming an isolated MCL injury when valgus laxity in extension signals a combined injury","Missing a paediatric distal femoral physeal fracture that mimics MCL laxity","Overlooking a concurrent ACL or meniscal tear","Immobilising rigidly when early protected motion aids healing"],"management":["Non-operative management for isolated tears with a hinged knee brace, early protected weight-bearing and initial rest, ice and analgesia","Progressive physiotherapy to restore range of motion, quadriceps and hamstring strength and proprioception","Graded, criteria-based return to sport once stability and strength are regained","Surgical repair or reconstruction reserved for combined cruciate or posteromedial-corner injuries or persistent high-grade valgus instability","Grade the injury and identify associated ligament or meniscal damage with valgus stress testing and MRI","In skeletally immature patients, distinguish and manage physeal injury with stress radiographs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Review of Orthopaedics","Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"medial_epicondylitis":{"name":"Medial epicondylitis (golfer elbow)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["golfer's elbow","medial epicondylalgia","common flexor origin tendinopathy","medial elbow tendinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Medial elbow pain from the common flexor-pronator origin, a degenerative tendinopathy rather than a true inflammation","Provoked by resisted wrist flexion and forearm pronation, and by gripping","Tenderness localises to the medial epicondyle and just distal to it","Less common than lateral epicondylitis","The ulnar nerve lies close by and may be irritated or entrapped, so ulnar nerve symptoms should be sought","Diagnosis is largely clinical, with imaging (ultrasound or MRI) reserved for uncertain or refractory cases","Management mirrors lateral epicondylitis with activity modification, progressive or eccentric loading and physiotherapy, and cautious use of corticosteroid injection given tendon and nearby ulnar nerve considerations"],"pathophysiology":"Medial epicondylitis is an overuse tendinopathy of the common flexor-pronator origin at the medial humeral epicondyle, arising from repetitive wrist flexion, forearm pronation and gripping loads, as in golf and throwing or racquet sports. As with other chronic tendinopathies the pathology is degenerative, with disorganised collagen, mucoid change and neovascularisation and little inflammation, reflecting failed tendon healing under repetitive load. The proximity of the ulnar nerve within the cubital tunnel means concurrent ulnar neuropathy is not uncommon.","additionalDifferentials":["Ulnar neuropathy at the elbow (cubital tunnel syndrome)","Ulnar collateral ligament injury or insufficiency","Medial elbow joint arthritis","Cervical radiculopathy (C8 or T1)","Flexor-pronator muscle strain","Referred pain from the shoulder or neck"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical provocative tests (resisted wrist flexion and pronation, palpation of the medial epicondyle)","Assessment of ulnar nerve function (Tinel's sign over the cubital tunnel, sensory testing)","Ultrasound to confirm common flexor tendinopathy","MRI in unclear or refractory cases and to assess the ulnar collateral ligament","Nerve conduction studies if ulnar neuropathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Ulnar nerve symptoms such as little and ring finger numbness or intrinsic weakness suggesting neuropathy","Valgus instability suggesting collateral ligament failure","Systemic features or night pain suggesting other pathology","Failure to respond, prompting review of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Most improve with load management and progressive rehabilitation, although recovery may be slow; coexisting ulnar neuropathy or ligament injury can complicate the course.","pitfalls":["Overlooking coexisting ulnar neuropathy","Corticosteroid injection risking tendon harm and ulnar nerve injury if poorly placed","Treating as inflammatory rather than a loading problem","Missing ulnar collateral ligament injury in throwing athletes"],"management":["Activity modification and load management to offload the flexor-pronator origin","Progressive loading and eccentric rehabilitation of the flexor-pronator muscles as the mainstay","Short-term analgesia with NSAIDs and ice, plus counterforce bracing and correction of technique and ergonomics","Assess for and manage coexisting ulnar neuropathy","Consider a corticosteroid injection for short-term symptom relief in refractory cases, weighing its limited long-term benefit","Surgical debridement of the degenerate tendon reserved for cases refractory to prolonged conservative care","Counsel the patient that recovery is often slow"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","British Journal of Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"medial_medullary_syndrome":{"name":"Medial medullary (Dejerine) syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Dejerine syndrome","anterior bulbar syndrome","medial bulbar syndrome","inferior alternating hemiplegia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by infarction of the medial medulla, usually from occlusion of paramedian branches of the anterior spinal artery or the distal vertebral artery","Presents as a classic crossed picture: contralateral limb weakness with ipsilateral tongue deviation","Contralateral hemiparesis spares the face because the lesion lies below the facial nucleus and affects the pyramid","Ipsilateral tongue wasting and deviation towards the weak side reflect hypoglossal nerve involvement","Contralateral loss of proprioception and vibration follows medial lemniscus involvement, while pain and temperature are typically preserved","Bilateral medial medullary infarction is rare but can cause quadriparesis and mimic more caudal spinal cord disease","Upbeat nystagmus may occur from involvement of medial longitudinal fasciculus fibres near the midline"],"pathophysiology":"Medial medullary infarction results from ischaemia in the paramedian territory supplied by penetrating branches of the anterior spinal artery and distal vertebral artery. Three vertically oriented midline structures are affected: the pyramid carrying the corticospinal tract (contralateral limb weakness with facial sparing), the medial lemniscus (contralateral loss of dorsal-column sensation), and the emerging hypoglossal nerve fibres and nucleus (ipsilateral tongue weakness with deviation to the paretic side). Because the spinothalamic tract and the lateral medullary structures lie outside this territory, pain and temperature sensation and cranial nerves nine and ten are generally spared, giving the characteristic dissociated, crossed deficit.","additionalDifferentials":["Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome","Anterior spinal artery syndrome of the cervical cord","Cervical cord compression or myelopathy","Demyelinating brainstem lesion in multiple sclerosis","Motor neurone disease with bulbar involvement","Vertebral artery dissection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diffusion-weighted MRI showing a paramedian medullary infarct, sometimes with a heart-shaped appearance","MR or CT angiography of the vertebral and anterior spinal circulation","Assessment of tongue movement, articulation, and swallowing safety","Vessel wall imaging when dissection or atherosclerosis is suspected","Cardiovascular risk workup including ECG, echocardiography where indicated, and metabolic profiling","Somatosensory testing to document dorsal-column involvement"],"redFlags":["Bilateral involvement producing quadriparesis and respiratory compromise","Rostral extension towards the pons threatening consciousness and airway","Vertebral dissection with risk of recurrent posterior circulation embolism","Rapidly progressive weakness suggesting an evolving basilar territory event"],"prognosis":"Unilateral cases often show meaningful motor recovery with rehabilitation, but bilateral or extensive infarcts causing quadriparesis and bulbar failure carry a substantially worse outcome.","pitfalls":["Confusing the facial-sparing hemiparesis with a hemispheric stroke and misdirecting imaging","Overlooking ipsilateral tongue deviation that localises the lesion to the medial medulla","Missing evolving bilateral infarction that can precipitate respiratory failure","Assuming intact pain and temperature sensation excludes a brainstem stroke"],"management":["Hyperacute reperfusion with intravenous thrombolysis within the licensed window, and mechanical thrombectomy where an accessible vertebral or basilar large-vessel occlusion is present","Early antiplatelet therapy (aspirin) for secondary prevention, using anticoagulation instead where cardioembolism or arterial dissection is confirmed","Aggressive vascular risk-factor control with a statin, blood-pressure management, glycaemic control and smoking cessation","Swallow assessment with dysphagia management and nutritional support to prevent aspiration","Multidisciplinary stroke-unit rehabilitation with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language therapy","Monitor for neurological deterioration, including bilateral infarct progression causing quadriparesis and bulbar or respiratory failure, and escalate to critical care as needed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":["lateral_medullary_syndrome","locked_in_syndrome"]},"medial_tibial_stress_syndrome":{"name":"Medial tibial stress syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["shin splints","tibial periostitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Exercise-induced pain along the posteromedial border of the tibia, typically over a diffuse length rather than a focal point","Pain characteristically eases with rest early on but can become more persistent with continued activity","It is an overuse injury common in runners, dancers and military recruits, often after a change in training load or surface","A key differential is tibial stress fracture, which causes more focal tenderness and pain, and chronic exertional compartment syndrome","Tenderness is spread over several centimetres of the distal medial tibia, unlike the pinpoint tenderness of a stress fracture","Management centres on relative rest, load modification, addressing biomechanical and training factors, and graded return"],"pathophysiology":"Medial tibial stress syndrome represents a bone-stress and periosteal overload injury along the posteromedial tibia, sitting on a continuum that can progress to tibial stress fracture. Repetitive loading exceeds the bone's capacity to remodel, producing periosteal and cortical stress and traction from the muscles attaching along the tibial border. Contributing factors include training errors, poor footwear, hard surfaces and biomechanical abnormalities such as overpronation, which concentrate load on the medial tibia.","additionalDifferentials":["Tibial stress fracture","Chronic exertional compartment syndrome","Popliteal artery entrapment syndrome","Tendinopathy of tibialis posterior or soleus","Nerve entrapment causing exertional leg pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the diffuse posteromedial tibial tenderness","Plain radiographs, often normal, to exclude other bony pathology","MRI or bone scan where stress fracture needs to be distinguished","Assessment of training load, footwear and biomechanics","Compartment pressure testing if exertional compartment syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["Focal tenderness and night or rest pain suggesting stress fracture","Neurovascular symptoms suggesting compartment syndrome or arterial entrapment","Failure to improve with appropriate load management","Features suggesting bone pathology other than stress injury"],"prognosis":"The condition usually resolves with load modification and correction of contributing factors, though recovery can be slow and recurrence is common if training errors persist.","pitfalls":["Missing a progressing tibial stress fracture","Overlooking chronic exertional compartment syndrome as the true cause","Returning to full training too quickly","Failing to correct biomechanical and training-load factors"],"management":["Relative rest and load modification, reducing the aggravating running or impact volume","Correct contributing factors: training errors, running surface, appropriate footwear and orthoses for overpronation","Symptomatic relief with ice and simple analgesia","Rehabilitation with calf and lower-limb strengthening and gait retraining","Graded, progressive return to impact loading once symptoms settle","Monitor for progression to tibial stress fracture and image if focal pain develops","Refer for specialist assessment in refractory cases, with surgery such as posterior fasciotomy or periosteal release rarely required"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sports medicine references (standard reference)","Orthopaedic reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"median_arcuate_ligament_syndrome":{"name":"Median arcuate ligament syndrome (Dunbar)","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MALS","Dunbar syndrome","coeliac artery compression syndrome","coeliac axis compression syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically presents with postprandial epigastric pain, food-fear-related weight loss, and sometimes an epigastric bruit that varies with respiration","Caused by extrinsic compression of the coeliac axis (and often its neural plexus) by a low-lying median arcuate ligament","Compression is typically worse in expiration, when the coeliac artery is drawn upward against the ligament","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring careful evaluation for other causes of chronic abdominal pain","Duplex ultrasound with respiratory manoeuvres shows increased coeliac artery velocities that worsen on expiration","Cross-sectional angiography demonstrates the characteristic focal hooked narrowing of the proximal coeliac artery","Definitive treatment is ligament release (often laparoscopic or robotic) to decompress the artery and coeliac plexus"],"pathophysiology":"In median arcuate ligament syndrome, a low-lying or hypertrophied median arcuate ligament (the fibrous arch connecting the diaphragmatic crura) compresses the proximal coeliac artery and the surrounding coeliac neural plexus. The compression is dynamic, tightening during expiration as the artery rides upward. Symptoms are attributed both to a degree of foregut ischaemia or flow limitation and, importantly, to irritation of the compressed coeliac plexus, which may explain the disproportionate, meal-related pain despite generally rich collateral mesenteric circulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic mesenteric ischaemia from atherosclerotic disease","Peptic ulcer disease","Chronic pancreatitis","Biliary colic or gallbladder disease","Functional dyspepsia or other functional gut-brain disorders","Superior mesenteric artery syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Mesenteric duplex ultrasound with inspiratory and expiratory measurements of coeliac velocities","CT or MR angiography to show the hooked coeliac stenosis and exclude other pathology","Upper endoscopy to exclude peptic disease","Cross-sectional abdominal imaging to evaluate pancreas and biliary tree","Coeliac plexus block as a diagnostic and prognostic manoeuvre in selected patients"],"redFlags":["Weight loss and food avoidance raising concern for malignancy or true mesenteric ischaemia","Acute severe abdominal pain suggesting an alternative vascular emergency","Persistent symptoms after other causes have been excluded","Signs of gastrointestinal bleeding pointing to another diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Outcomes after ligament release are variable but often favourable in carefully selected patients, particularly younger patients with classic postprandial pain and confirmed dynamic compression; symptom persistence can occur when the diagnosis is misapplied.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing MALS from an incidental coeliac indentation on imaging in an asymptomatic patient","Failing to perform respiratory-phase imaging to demonstrate dynamic compression","Overlooking other treatable causes of chronic abdominal pain before intervention","Attributing all postoperative symptom relief to arterial rather than neural decompression"],"management":["Confirm the diagnosis by excluding other causes of chronic abdominal pain before intervention, as it is a diagnosis of exclusion","Surgical division (release) of the median arcuate ligament to decompress the coeliac axis, by laparoscopic, robotic or open approach, as the definitive treatment","Coeliac plexus neurolysis or ganglionectomy at the time of release to address the neurogenic component of the pain","Coeliac artery revascularisation with angioplasty and stenting or bypass grafting where a fixed residual stenosis persists after ligament release","Nutritional support and dietary optimisation to reverse food-fear-related weight loss","Coeliac plexus block as a diagnostic and prognostic step and for symptomatic relief in selected patients","Manage through a multidisciplinary vascular surgery, gastroenterology and pain team, reserving surgery for carefully selected patients","Postoperative duplex surveillance of coeliac flow with monitoring for symptom recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy"],"crossLinks":[]},"median_nail_dystrophy":{"name":"Median canaliform nail dystrophy","system":"Dermatology / Nail","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["median canaliform dystrophy of Heller","dystrophia unguis mediana canaliformis","median nail dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Median canaliform dystrophy shows a longitudinal split or canal running down the centre of the nail, most often the thumb","Fine ridges angle outwards from the central groove towards the sides, producing the classic inverted fir-tree or Christmas-tree pattern","An enlarged lunula is a frequently associated feature","It commonly affects both thumbs symmetrically and can wax and wane over time","Repetitive trauma or pressure on the proximal nail fold is thought to contribute, and it overlaps clinically with habit-tic deformity","It is a benign condition, and the diagnosis is usually made clinically from the characteristic pattern","Management is conservative, centring on avoiding trauma and protecting the nail, as many cases improve spontaneously though relapse is common"],"pathophysiology":"Median canaliform dystrophy is thought to arise from a temporary defect in nail formation at the centre of the matrix, producing a longitudinal split or canal down the middle of the plate with oblique ridges branching towards the edges. The cause is not fully understood, but repetitive trauma or pressure transmitted to the proximal nail matrix is considered an important contributor, which is why it overlaps with self-induced habit-tic deformity. Because the matrix is disturbed rather than destroyed, the change is generally reversible and can fluctuate, with the nail capable of recovering a normal surface once any trauma ceases.","additionalDifferentials":["Habit-tic nail deformity","Myxoid (mucous) cyst producing a longitudinal groove","Nail changes of lichen planus","Traumatic nail splitting","Onychotillomania and other self-induced dystrophies","Congenital or hereditary nail canal variants"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical recognition of the central canal with the inverted fir-tree ridge pattern","History for repetitive trauma, pressure, or manipulation of the nail fold","Examination of the proximal nail fold and lunula","Onychoscopy to define the ridging pattern","Assessment to exclude a proximal myxoid cyst causing a groove","Photographic follow-up to monitor the characteristic fluctuation"],"redFlags":["A persistent groove localised to one nail with a swelling over the proximal fold suggesting a myxoid cyst","Features of scarring nail disease such as lichen planus that require specific treatment","Atypical or destructive change inconsistent with a benign central dystrophy","Evidence of significant self-injurious behaviour requiring psychological support"],"prognosis":"Median canaliform dystrophy is benign and often improves spontaneously, particularly when trauma is avoided, but it characteristically fluctuates and may recur, and complete permanent resolution cannot be guaranteed.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with a myxoid cyst, which needs different management of the underlying cyst","Overlooking a contributory habit or repetitive trauma","Over-treating a benign, self-limiting condition","Assuming permanent cure when the course is typically relapsing and remitting"],"management":["Reassure the patient that the condition is benign and often reverses once the underlying cause is removed","Identify and stop repetitive trauma, pressure or manipulation of the proximal nail fold, including habit-tic behaviour","Habit-reversal behavioural measures, with an occlusive dressing or tape over the nail fold to interrupt picking","Emollients and protective nail care to shield the matrix and nail plate","Consider a trial of topical calcineurin inhibitor (tacrolimus) or intralesional corticosteroid around the proximal fold in persistent cases","Exclude a proximal myxoid cyst as a treatable cause of a longitudinal groove","Photographic follow-up to track the characteristic fluctuation and confirm recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"median_rhomboid_glossitis":{"name":"Median rhomboid glossitis","system":"Oral cavity","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["central papillary atrophy of the tongue"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oral medicine reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A well-demarcated rhomboid or oval area of papillary atrophy in the midline of the tongue dorsum, just anterior to the circumvallate papillae","It is usually asymptomatic and found incidentally, appearing smooth, red and depapillated","There is a strong association with Candida colonisation, and it is often regarded as a form of chronic mucosal candidosis","Smoking, denture use, inhaled corticosteroids and immunosuppression are recognised associations","A matching (kissing) lesion on the opposing palate suggests more extensive candidal involvement","Management includes antifungal therapy where Candida is implicated and addressing predisposing factors"],"pathophysiology":"Median rhomboid glossitis is a localised area of atrophy of the filiform papillae in the midline dorsal tongue. Although historically attributed to a developmental defect, it is now strongly linked to chronic Candida colonisation of the site, with the yeast contributing to the persistent papillary atrophy. Local factors that favour candidal overgrowth, such as smoking, corticosteroid inhalers and immunosuppression, promote the lesion, and the condition is considered a chronic candidal-associated process rather than a primary invasive infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Geographic tongue","Erythematous candidosis elsewhere on the tongue","Squamous cell carcinoma or other tongue neoplasm (if indurated)","Vitamin deficiency glossitis","Lingual thyroid or other midline swelling"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the characteristic midline site and appearance","Mycological assessment for Candida where indicated","Review of predisposing factors (smoking, inhaled steroids, immune status)","Biopsy if there is induration, ulceration or diagnostic doubt"],"redFlags":["Induration, ulceration or a mass suggesting malignancy","Failure to respond to antifungal treatment","Associated widespread candidosis suggesting immunosuppression","Rapid change in the lesion"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign; it often improves with antifungal treatment and control of predisposing factors, though the depapillated area may persist.","pitfalls":["Mistaking an indurated lesion for the benign condition and missing carcinoma","Overlooking the candidal association and predisposing factors","Confusing it with geographic tongue","Failing to examine the opposing palate for a kissing lesion"],"management":["Topical antifungal therapy with an azole such as miconazole gel or with nystatin where Candida is implicated","Systemic azole antifungal such as fluconazole for extensive, symptomatic or refractory candidal involvement, including a kissing palatal lesion","Smoking cessation as a key modifiable predisposing factor","Optimise inhaler technique with mouth-rinsing after inhaled corticosteroids and attend to denture hygiene","Address underlying immunosuppression and glycaemic control where relevant","Reassure that the lesion is benign, though the depapillated area may persist","Biopsy if there is induration, ulceration or diagnostic uncertainty to exclude other pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Oral medicine reference texts (standard reference)","Dermatology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"mediastinal_germ_cell_tumor":{"name":"Mediastinal germ cell tumour","system":"Respiratory / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["primary mediastinal germ cell tumour","mediastinal seminoma","mediastinal non-seminomatous germ cell tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Shields' General Thoracic Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest extragonadal site for germ cell tumours, arising in the anterior mediastinum, and predominantly affecting young men","Divided into seminomatous and non-seminomatous types, which differ markedly in treatment and outlook","Non-seminomatous tumours characteristically raise serum alpha-fetoprotein and/or beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin, whereas pure seminoma may show a modestly raised beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin but a normal alpha-fetoprotein","There is a recognised association with Klinefelter syndrome, so karyotype should be considered in young men","Testicular examination and imaging are needed to confirm a primary mediastinal origin rather than a metastasis from a testicular primary","Seminomas are highly chemosensitive and radiosensitive with a good prognosis, while non-seminomatous tumours require cisplatin-based chemotherapy often followed by resection of residual disease","A raised alpha-fetoprotein essentially excludes pure seminoma and mandates management as a non-seminomatous tumour"],"pathophysiology":"Primary mediastinal germ cell tumours are believed to originate from primordial germ cells that became arrested in the mediastinum during their embryonic migration along the midline. These pluripotent cells can give rise to the full spectrum of germ cell tumours, from mature teratoma through seminoma to malignant non-seminomatous types such as yolk sac tumour, embryonal carcinoma, and choriocarcinoma. Malignant elements secrete alpha-fetoprotein or beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin, which serve as diagnostic and monitoring markers. The association with Klinefelter syndrome implicates the extra X chromosome in the pathogenesis of mediastinal disease in affected young men.","additionalDifferentials":["Mature mediastinal teratoma","Thymoma or thymic carcinoma","Lymphoma involving the anterior mediastinum","Metastasis from a testicular germ cell primary","Substernal thyroid mass","Other anterior mediastinal malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum alpha-fetoprotein and beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin, with lactate dehydrogenase","Contrast-enhanced CT of the thorax, abdomen, and pelvis for staging","Testicular examination and scrotal ultrasound to exclude a testicular primary","Biopsy with immunohistochemistry when markers are non-diagnostic","Karyotype to evaluate for Klinefelter syndrome in young men"],"redFlags":["Superior vena caval obstruction or airway compromise from a bulky mass","Markedly elevated tumour markers indicating aggressive non-seminomatous disease","Rapid growth with distant metastases at presentation","Development of a somatic malignancy within a teratomatous component"],"prognosis":"Mediastinal seminoma has an excellent prognosis with chemotherapy and radiotherapy, whereas primary mediastinal non-seminomatous tumours carry a poorer outlook than their gonadal counterparts and require intensive chemotherapy with resection of residual masses.","pitfalls":["Failing to measure tumour markers before treatment, losing baseline and diagnostic information","Treating a marker-positive tumour as a seminoma when a raised alpha-fetoprotein indicates non-seminomatous disease","Overlooking a testicular primary and mislabelling the tumour as extragonadal","Not considering Klinefelter syndrome in a young man with a mediastinal germ cell tumour"],"management":["Cisplatin-based combination chemotherapy, such as bleomycin, etoposide and cisplatin, as the systemic mainstay for both seminomatous and non-seminomatous tumours","Surgical resection of residual masses after chemotherapy for non-seminomatous tumours, given their poorer response","Serial alpha-fetoprotein, beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin and lactate dehydrogenase to guide treatment and monitor response","Sperm banking and fertility counselling before gonadotoxic chemotherapy","Exclude a testicular primary and offer karyotyping for Klinefelter syndrome in young men","Manage superior vena cava obstruction and airway compromise from mass effect as acute complications","Coordinate care through a germ-cell and thoracic oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Shields' General Thoracic Surgery","Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"mediastinal_large_b_cell_lymphoma":{"name":"Primary mediastinal large B-cell lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMBCL","PMBL","thymic large B-cell lymphoma","mediastinal (thymic) large B-cell lymphoma","CD30-positive large B-cell lymphoma of the mediastinum"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Arises from a thymic medullary B cell and characteristically presents in young adults, with a marked predominance in women","Typically presents as a bulky anterior mediastinal mass causing cough, dyspnoea, chest discomfort or superior vena cava obstruction","The tumour cells are CD20-positive but often show weak or absent surface immunoglobulin and frequently express CD23 and CD30 (usually weaker and more variable than in Hodgkin lymphoma)","There is biological and clinical overlap with classic Hodgkin lymphoma, reflected in shared molecular features such as amplification of chromosome 9p24 involving the JAK2 and PD-L1/PD-L2 loci","Compartmentalising sclerosis producing fibrous bands is a common histological feature that can complicate biopsy interpretation","Managed with intensive chemoimmunotherapy incorporating anti-CD20 targeted therapy, with radiotherapy considered in selected cases and PD-1 checkpoint blockade active in relapse","End-of-treatment PET-CT is important, as a residual mediastinal mass is common and requires functional imaging to distinguish fibrosis from active disease"],"pathophysiology":"Primary mediastinal large B-cell lymphoma derives from a specialised thymic medullary B cell, giving it a distinct gene-expression signature that overlaps substantially with classic Hodgkin lymphoma. Recurrent genetic events, notably gains and amplifications of the 9p24 region, upregulate JAK-STAT signalling and the programmed death-ligands PD-L1 and PD-L2, promoting proliferation and immune evasion within the tumour microenvironment. Constitutive NF-kappaB activation further supports cell survival. The neoplasm grows as sheets of large clear cells within the anterior mediastinum, accompanied by fine compartmentalising fibrosis, and tends to spread locally to adjacent thoracic structures before distant dissemination.","additionalDifferentials":["Classic Hodgkin lymphoma of the mediastinum, nodular sclerosis type","Grey zone lymphoma with intermediate features","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma involving the mediastinum","Lymphoblastic lymphoma/leukaemia (T-cell) in young patients","Thymoma or thymic carcinoma","Germ cell tumour of the mediastinum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Core or surgical biopsy of the mediastinal mass with immunophenotyping, as adequate tissue is essential given the fibrosis","Immunohistochemistry showing CD20, CD23 and variable CD30 with weak surface immunoglobulin","PET-CT for staging and end-of-treatment response assessment of residual masses","Assessment for superior vena cava obstruction and airway compromise","Blood counts, lactate dehydrogenase and organ function before intensive therapy","Molecular or FISH studies for 9p24 abnormalities where available"],"redFlags":["Superior vena cava obstruction with facial and upper-limb swelling or airway compromise requiring urgent assessment","Rapidly enlarging bulky mediastinal mass with respiratory distress","Pericardial or pleural effusion causing haemodynamic compromise","Central nervous system symptoms suggesting extranodal spread in advanced disease"],"prognosis":"With intensive chemoimmunotherapy the majority of patients achieve durable remission and cure, and outcomes in young patients are generally favourable; checkpoint inhibition offers meaningful salvage in relapsed or refractory disease, though bulky presentation and treatment-related toxicity remain concerns.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the tumour for classic Hodgkin lymphoma or diffuse large B-cell lymphoma without integrating clinical, morphological and molecular features","Obtaining insufficient tissue because of extensive fibrosis, leading to non-diagnostic biopsy","Interpreting a residual post-treatment mediastinal mass as persistent disease without functional imaging","Delaying management of superior vena cava obstruction while awaiting definitive staging","Overlooking the young age and female predominance that should raise the diagnosis"],"management":["Intensive anti-CD20-based chemoimmunotherapy, with rituximab combined with a regimen such as dose-adjusted EPOCH or CHOP, as first-line curative treatment","Consolidation mediastinal radiotherapy in selected patients with residual or PET-positive disease","PD-1 checkpoint blockade and CD19-directed CAR T-cell therapy for relapsed or refractory disease","Urgent management of superior vena cava obstruction and airway compromise from the bulky mediastinal mass","Tumour lysis syndrome prophylaxis and monitoring at the start of therapy","PET-CT for staging and end-of-treatment response assessment of residual masses","Fertility preservation counselling before gonadotoxic therapy, with long-term surveillance for radiotherapy-related cardiac and breast complications in young women","Manage through a haemato-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","British Society for Haematology guidance on aggressive B-cell lymphomas"],"crossLinks":[]},"mediastinal_mass":{"name":"Mediastinal mass","system":"Paediatric surgery / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mediastinal tumour","paediatric mediastinal mass"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Bailey & Love (paediatric surgery)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The likely diagnosis is best predicted by which mediastinal compartment is involved, so localisation to the anterior, middle, or posterior mediastinum guides the differential","Anterior masses often reflect the 'terrible T's', notably lymphoma and germ cell tumours; posterior masses in children are frequently neurogenic tumours such as neuroblastoma","Middle mediastinal lesions include lymphadenopathy, foregut duplication cysts, and bronchogenic cysts","Many masses are found incidentally on chest imaging, but they can cause cough, wheeze, stridor, superior vena cava obstruction, or dysphagia through mass effect","A large anterior mediastinal mass is a serious anaesthetic hazard because it can compress the airway and great vessels, particularly when the child lies supine","Whenever possible a tissue diagnosis should be obtained by the least invasive and safest route, and general anaesthesia may need to be avoided in high-risk airway compression","Investigation includes cross-sectional imaging, and, depending on suspected pathology, tumour markers and marrow or nodal sampling","Neurogenic posterior tumours require spinal imaging to detect intraspinal (dumbbell) extension that may threaten the cord"],"pathophysiology":"A mediastinal mass is any space-occupying lesion within the mediastinum, and its nature relates strongly to the compartment of origin because different tissues predominate in each. Anterior lesions arise from thymic, lymphoid, germ cell, or thyroid tissue; middle lesions from lymph nodes, the airway, and foregut-derived cysts; and posterior lesions largely from neural tissue along the sympathetic chain. Symptoms result mainly from compression or invasion of adjacent structures such as the trachea, bronchi, great vessels, oesophagus, and spinal cord, and systemic effects may occur with malignant or hormonally active tumours.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphoma (Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin)","Germ cell tumour (including teratoma)","Neuroblastoma, ganglioneuroblastoma, or ganglioneuroma","Foregut duplication or bronchogenic cyst","Thymic lesions and reactive or infective lymphadenopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph followed by CT or MRI to localise and characterise the mass","MRI of the spine for posterior masses to assess intraspinal extension","Tumour markers such as alpha-fetoprotein and beta-hCG for germ cell tumours, and urinary catecholamine metabolites for neuroblastoma","Tissue sampling by the safest feasible route, with bone marrow examination where relevant","Airway assessment and echocardiography before anaesthesia in large anterior masses"],"redFlags":["A large anterior mediastinal mass causing airway or great-vessel compression, which is a critical anaesthetic emergency and can cause cardiorespiratory collapse under sedation or when supine","Superior vena cava obstruction with facial swelling, distended veins, and respiratory distress","Stridor, orthopnoea, or inability to lie flat indicating significant tracheal compression","Posterior tumour with intraspinal extension threatening spinal cord compression"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends entirely on the underlying pathology, ranging from excellent for benign cysts and localised resectable tumours to highly variable outcomes for lymphomas and neuroblastoma, which depend on stage, histology, and biology. Early recognition of airway compromise is critical to avoid procedure-related mortality.","pitfalls":["Inducing general anaesthesia in a child with a large anterior mass without assessing airway and cardiovascular compression risk","Delaying diagnosis of superior vena cava obstruction or airway compromise","Failing to image the spine in posterior neurogenic tumours and missing cord compression","Pursuing an invasive biopsy when a diagnosis could be obtained more safely by markers or peripheral sampling"],"management":["Direct definitive treatment by the underlying pathology once identified, with chemotherapy for lymphoma and surgical resection for cysts, neurogenic tumours and teratomas","Obtain a tissue diagnosis by the least invasive and safest feasible route, using peripheral samples such as bone marrow or effusion cytology where possible","Assess airway and cardiovascular compression before any sedation, avoiding general anaesthesia in high-risk anterior masses and keeping the child upright","Manage acute airway and superior vena cava obstruction as emergencies, using positioning and empirical corticosteroids or radiotherapy to shrink a compressing tumour","Cross-sectional imaging with CT or MRI to localise the compartment and guide the differential, with spinal MRI for posterior masses","Coordinate care through a paediatric surgery, oncology and anaesthesia multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"mediastinal_teratoma":{"name":"Mediastinal teratoma","system":"Respiratory / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mature mediastinal teratoma","mediastinal dermoid cyst","benign mediastinal germ cell tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Shields' General Thoracic Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest germ cell tumour of the anterior mediastinum, usually a mature (benign) lesion containing tissues from more than one germ layer","Sits within the classic anterior-mediastinal 'four Ts' differential alongside thymoma, thyroid masses, and 'terrible' lymphoma","Often asymptomatic and found incidentally, but may cause chest pain, cough, or breathlessness through mass effect","Cross-sectional imaging typically shows a heterogeneous mass containing fat, fluid, soft tissue, and calcification, sometimes with a recognisable tooth","Coughing up hair or sebaceous material (trichoptysis) is a rare but essentially diagnostic feature when a teratoma erodes into an airway","Mature teratomas are benign, whereas immature or malignant germ cell elements alter management and prognosis","Complete surgical resection is both diagnostic and curative for mature lesions"],"pathophysiology":"Mediastinal teratomas are thought to arise from primordial germ cells that fail to complete their migration and become mislocated within the mediastinum during embryogenesis. These pluripotent cells give rise to tissues derived from all three germ layers, so the resulting mass typically contains a mixture of ectodermal skin and hair, mesodermal fat and cartilage, and endodermal glandular elements, accounting for the characteristic heterogeneous imaging appearance with fat and calcification. Mature teratomas are well differentiated and benign, but tumours containing immature tissue or other malignant germ cell components behave more aggressively and may secrete tumour markers.","additionalDifferentials":["Thymoma or thymic carcinoma","Malignant mediastinal germ cell tumour (seminomatous or non-seminomatous)","Lymphoma involving the anterior mediastinum","Substernal (retrosternal) thyroid goitre","Thymic cyst or other foregut cyst","Bronchogenic cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the thorax showing fat, fluid, and calcific components","MRI to further characterise tissue components and delineate adjacent structures","Serum alpha-fetoprotein and beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin to help exclude malignant germ cell elements","Biopsy in selected cases where imaging is indeterminate or malignancy is suspected","PET-CT where a malignant or immature component is a concern"],"redFlags":["Raised alpha-fetoprotein or beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin suggesting a malignant germ cell component","Rapid growth or invasion of adjacent structures on imaging","Superior vena caval obstruction or airway compromise from mass effect","Trichoptysis or rupture into an airway, pleura, or pericardium"],"prognosis":"Completely resected mature teratomas have an excellent prognosis and are effectively cured, whereas immature or malignant germ cell tumours require multimodality treatment and carry a more guarded outlook.","pitfalls":["Assuming any anterior mediastinal mass is a thymoma without considering a teratoma","Failing to measure germ cell tumour markers before treatment planning","Mistaking a malignant germ cell tumour for a benign teratoma on limited sampling","Underestimating the technical difficulty of resecting a mass densely adherent to great vessels"],"management":["Complete surgical resection as the definitive, curative treatment for mature (benign) teratoma","Measure alpha-fetoprotein and beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin to detect malignant germ-cell elements that change management","Cisplatin-based chemotherapy followed by resection of residual disease where immature or malignant components are present","Manage local mass effect on the airway, great vessels and oesophagus, including erosion into an airway causing trichoptysis","Post-resection surveillance imaging to detect recurrence, particularly with immature or malignant elements","Coordinate care through a thoracic surgery and oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Shields' General Thoracic Surgery","Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"medication_overuse_headache":{"name":"Medication-overuse headache","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rebound headache","analgesic overuse headache","drug-induced headache"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A secondary headache developing in a person with a pre-existing primary headache, usually migraine or tension-type headache, who overuses acute or symptomatic headache medication","ICHD-3 defines it as headache on 15 or more days per month with regular overuse for more than 3 months: 10 or more days per month for triptans, ergots, opioids or combination analgesics, and 15 or more days per month for simple analgesics such as paracetamol, NSAIDs or aspirin","The clinical picture is often a daily or near-daily, dull, diffuse headache present on waking, superimposed on the features of the underlying primary headache","Opioids, combination analgesics containing codeine or caffeine, and triptans carry the highest risk, whereas simple analgesics carry lower risk and higher thresholds","It is a clinical diagnosis, and the key step is a careful drug history that quantifies acute medication use including over-the-counter and non-headache analgesics","Management is withdrawal of the overused medication, abrupt or gradual depending on the drug and setting, with education that acute treatments must be limited to avoid recurrence","Introducing or optimising migraine preventive therapy around the time of withdrawal improves outcomes, and a transient worsening of headache with withdrawal symptoms is common in the first days to weeks","Prevention through early counselling on acute-medication frequency limits is essential, as the condition is common and largely avoidable"],"pathophysiology":"Medication-overuse headache develops in predisposed individuals with a primary headache disorder through medication-induced changes in central pain processing. Chronic exposure to acute headache drugs is thought to cause neuroadaptive changes, including sensitisation of trigeminal and central pain pathways, altered descending pain modulation, receptor changes in serotonergic and opioid systems, and cortical hyperexcitability, which lower the threshold for headache. The result is a self-perpetuating cycle in which increasing medication use drives more frequent headache. Genetic and behavioural factors, including dependence-related mechanisms, contribute, and the disorder characteristically reverses after withdrawal.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic migraine without medication overuse","Chronic tension-type headache","New daily persistent headache","Secondary headache from raised or low intracranial pressure or a mass lesion if atypical","Caffeine or analgesic withdrawal headache","Hemicrania continua, which is indometacin-responsive, masquerading as chronic daily headache"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed headache and medication diary quantifying days per month of each acute drug","Clinical diagnosis per ICHD-3, as no specific test confirms the condition","Review of over-the-counter and prescribed analgesics including opioids and combination products","Neuroimaging with MRI only if red flags or atypical features suggest a secondary cause","Screening for medication dependence, anxiety, depression and other comorbidities","Baseline bloods where indicated, for example to assess NSAID or paracetamol-related organ effects"],"redFlags":["New neurological signs, papilloedema or features of raised intracranial pressure","Thunderclap onset or a first or worst headache, or a clear change in headache character","Systemic symptoms such as fever or weight loss, immunosuppression or malignancy","Headache that does not improve after appropriate withdrawal, prompting reconsideration of a secondary cause","New headache with onset over the age of 50"],"prognosis":"With withdrawal of the overused medication and optimisation of preventive treatment, a large proportion of patients improve substantially and revert to their underlying episodic pattern, usually within weeks to a couple of months. Relapse is common, however, so ongoing education and limitation of acute-medication use are needed.","pitfalls":["Not taking a thorough medication history including over-the-counter and non-headache analgesics","Overlooking opioid and combination-analgesic overuse as major drivers","Escalating acute treatment instead of recognising and treating the overuse","Failing to start preventive therapy and support around withdrawal, increasing the risk of relapse","Assuming that persistent headache after withdrawal is still medication overuse and missing a secondary cause"],"management":["Withdraw the overused acute medication, abruptly for simple analgesics and triptans or with a supervised taper for opioids and barbiturate-containing combinations","Start or optimise preventive therapy for the underlying primary headache, such as topiramate, amitriptyline, a beta-blocker or a CGRP monoclonal antibody","Provide bridging therapy to ease withdrawal, using a longer-acting NSAID, a short corticosteroid course or antiemetics","Educate the patient and use a headache diary to limit acute treatment to a safe number of days and prevent recurrence","Screen for and manage comorbid anxiety, depression and medication dependence","Arrange follow-up focused on relapse prevention, as recurrence is common","Refer for specialist or inpatient supervised withdrawal when outpatient treatment fails or with opioid or barbiturate overuse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (ICHD-3)","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"mediterranean_macrothrombocytopenia":{"name":"Mediterranean macrothrombocytopenia (GP1BA)","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GP1BA","monoallelic Bernard-Soulier","Mediterranean macrothrombocytopenia","GPIb-alpha related macrothrombocytopenia","benign Mediterranean macrothrombocytopenia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / Nathan and Oski","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A generally benign autosomal dominant macrothrombocytopenia associated with heterozygous (monoallelic) mutations in GP1BA, part of the platelet GPIb-IX-V complex","Represents the heterozygous carrier state of Bernard-Soulier syndrome, whose biallelic form is far more severe","Characterised by mild thrombocytopenia with large platelets and usually little or no bleeding tendency","Often discovered incidentally on a routine full blood count or misclassified by automated analysers that miscount giant platelets","Platelet aggregation to ristocetin may be mildly reduced but is far less abnormal than in biallelic Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Recognition prevents inappropriate treatment for presumed immune thrombocytopenia","It is particularly described in Mediterranean populations, hence the name","Diagnosis rests on film review of platelet morphology, flow cytometry of GPIb, and GP1BA genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"GP1BA encodes GPIb-alpha, a subunit of the platelet GPIb-IX-V receptor complex that binds von Willebrand factor and mediates platelet adhesion. In the biallelic state, loss of the complex causes Bernard-Soulier syndrome with giant platelets and severe bleeding. A single mutant allele (monoallelic) produces a milder phenotype in which platelet production and size regulation are partially disturbed, giving mild macrothrombocytopenia with largely preserved haemostatic function. The enlarged platelets and modestly reduced counts reflect abnormal megakaryocyte fragmentation rather than peripheral destruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Biallelic Bernard-Soulier syndrome","MYH9-related disorder","Immune thrombocytopenia (acquired)","Other inherited macrothrombocytopenias (for example GP9, GP1BB variants)","Grey platelet syndrome","Pseudothrombocytopenia from platelet clumping"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with manual film review to confirm large platelets and exclude clumping","Flow cytometry for GPIb-IX-V complex expression","Ristocetin-induced platelet agglutination studies","GP1BA (and related complex genes) sequencing","Family studies given autosomal dominant inheritance","Exclusion of secondary and immune causes of thrombocytopenia"],"redFlags":["Bleeding that is more severe than expected, prompting reassessment for biallelic Bernard-Soulier syndrome or an additional disorder","Progressive fall in platelet count suggesting a superimposed acquired process","Additional cytopenias or systemic features indicating another diagnosis","Marked haemorrhage during surgery or trauma requiring platelet support"],"prognosis":"The condition is usually benign with a normal life expectancy and minimal bleeding, and its main clinical importance lies in correct recognition to avoid unnecessary investigation and treatment.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosis as immune thrombocytopenia leading to steroids, immunoglobulin, or splenectomy","Accepting a falsely low automated platelet count when giant platelets are miscounted","Confusing the mild heterozygous phenotype with severe biallelic Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Overlooking pseudothrombocytopenia from in-vitro platelet clumping"],"management":["Recognise the condition to avoid inappropriate immune thrombocytopenia treatment such as corticosteroids, immunoglobulin or splenectomy","No specific therapy is needed, as the disorder is benign with preserved haemostasis","Cover haemostatic challenges such as surgery, dental extraction and childbirth with an antifibrinolytic such as tranexamic acid, reserving platelet transfusion for significant bleeding","Use caution with antiplatelet and anticoagulant drugs and counsel on their bleeding risk","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and offer family testing","Give the patient documentation of the diagnosis to prevent repeated investigation and misdiagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"mediterranean_spotted_fever":{"name":"Mediterranean spotted fever","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["boutonneuse fever","Rickettsia conorii infection","Marseilles fever","tick typhus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by Rickettsia conorii and transmitted by the brown dog tick (Rhipicephalus sanguineus), with dogs acting as an important reservoir and amplifier","Endemic around the Mediterranean basin, in southern Europe, Africa and parts of Asia, with a summer peak reflecting tick activity","After an incubation of about 5-7 days there is abrupt fever, headache and myalgia followed within a few days by a maculopapular rash","A black necrotic eschar at the bite site, the tache noire, is a highly suggestive sign and is often single; careful search of skin folds may reveal it","Unlike many exanthems the rash frequently involves the palms and soles and can become purpuric in severe disease","Doxycycline is the treatment of choice and should be given empirically on clinical suspicion rather than awaiting confirmatory serology","Most cases are self-limiting but a severe malignant form with vasculitic complications occurs, particularly in older patients, those with alcohol-related liver disease, diabetes or glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency"],"pathophysiology":"Rickettsia conorii is an obligate intracellular bacterium inoculated during tick feeding that targets vascular endothelial cells. Local replication at the inoculation site produces the characteristic necrotic eschar (tache noire) through focal vasculitis and dermal necrosis, while dissemination causes a generalised small-vessel vasculitis analogous to other spotted fever rickettsioses. Endothelial injury increases vascular permeability and activates coagulation, producing the rash and, in severe cases, organ dysfunction affecting the brain, kidneys and lungs.","additionalDifferentials":["Other spotted fever group rickettsioses including Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Meningococcaemia","Murine or epidemic typhus","Leptospirosis","Viral exanthems","Drug eruption"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Indirect immunofluorescence antibody serology on paired sera (confirmatory but often negative in the first week)","PCR of blood or of an eschar or skin biopsy","Immunohistochemistry of an eschar biopsy","Full blood count looking for thrombocytopenia and leucopenia","Liver enzymes and renal function","Serum sodium to detect hyponatraemia"],"redFlags":["Confusion, meningism or focal neurology suggesting encephalitic involvement","Purpuric rash with hypotension indicating the malignant form","Acute kidney injury or respiratory compromise","Underlying immunosuppression, glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency or chronic liver disease predisposing to severe illness"],"prognosis":"The majority of cases resolve fully with timely doxycycline, but the severe malignant form carries appreciable mortality, especially in older or comorbid patients, so early empirical treatment is important.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the tache noire because it is painless and hidden in a skin fold, scalp or groin","Waiting for serological confirmation before starting doxycycline","Attributing the illness to a simple viral exanthem and missing palm and sole involvement","Failing to recognise host factors that predict the malignant form","Using an ineffective beta-lactam antibiotic"],"management":["Empirical doxycycline, a tetracycline, started on clinical suspicion without waiting for serological confirmation","A macrolide such as azithromycin or clarithromycin as an alternative in mild disease or where tetracyclines are contraindicated","Supportive care with fluids and antipyretics","Monitor for the severe malignant form with neurological, renal and respiratory organ dysfunction, escalating to intensive care when needed","Advise tick-bite avoidance and control of the brown dog tick reservoir to prevent reinfection","Heightened vigilance and prompt treatment in older or comorbid patients, who are at greatest risk of severe disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"medium_chain_acyl_coa_deficiency":{"name":"Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCAD deficiency","MCADD","medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","ACADM deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common inherited disorder of mitochondrial fatty acid beta-oxidation, caused by deficiency of medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase","Classically presents in infancy or early childhood with hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and lethargy or coma precipitated by fasting or an intercurrent illness","Vomiting, hepatomegaly and encephalopathy may accompany crises, and sudden unexpected death can occur, sometimes mimicking sudden infant death syndrome","The key clue is inappropriately low or absent ketones despite hypoglycaemia, since fat cannot be used as fuel during fasting","The acylcarnitine profile shows elevated medium-chain species (notably octanoylcarnitine, C8) with a raised C8:C10 ratio, and urine shows medium-chain dicarboxylic aciduria","Between episodes children are typically well, and the disorder can be clinically silent until a catabolic stress unmasks it","Management is essentially preventive: avoidance of fasting, prompt provision of glucose during illness, and an emergency regimen, with generally no need for severe dietary fat restriction","Now widely detected on newborn screening by elevated C8 acylcarnitine, which has markedly reduced deaths from unrecognised disease"],"pathophysiology":"MCAD deficiency is an autosomal recessive disorder, usually due to a common ACADM gene variant, that impairs the beta-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acids. During fasting the body normally shifts to oxidising fatty acids for energy and generating ketones for the brain; when medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase is deficient, this pathway stalls, so energy production fails and ketogenesis is inadequate. Glucose is consumed without adequate fat-derived backup, producing hypoketotic hypoglycaemia. Accumulated medium-chain fatty acids and their carnitine and glycine conjugates are toxic and contribute to encephalopathy and hepatic dysfunction. Secondary carnitine depletion is common.","additionalDifferentials":["Other fatty acid oxidation defects (LCHAD, VLCAD, carnitine transport/CPT defects)","Ketotic hypoglycaemia and other causes of childhood hypoglycaemia","Hyperinsulinism","Reye syndrome","Glycogen storage disorders","Sepsis and sudden unexpected death in infancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood glucose, ketones (inappropriately low), and blood gas during an episode","Plasma acylcarnitine profile (elevated C8 with raised C8:C10 ratio)","Urine organic acids showing medium-chain dicarboxylic aciduria","Newborn screening acylcarnitine analysis","Plasma free and total carnitine (often low)","Molecular testing of the ACADM gene"],"redFlags":["Hypoketotic hypoglycaemia with encephalopathy or coma during fasting or illness is a life-threatening emergency","Sudden collapse or unexpected death, particularly in a previously well infant","Prolonged fasting, vomiting or poor intake during intercurrent illness without glucose provision","First presentation of an undiagnosed child with unexplained hypoglycaemic coma"],"prognosis":"With diagnosis (especially via newborn screening) and simple preventive measures the prognosis is excellent and most individuals remain healthy; however, an unrecognised metabolic crisis can cause sudden death or permanent neurological injury, so the main determinant of outcome is prevention of fasting-induced decompensation.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the disorder because the child is entirely well between episodes","Failing to recognise that ketones are inappropriately low for the degree of hypoglycaemia","Not providing an emergency glucose plan for periods of illness or fasting","Assuming SIDS in an infant death without considering an underlying fatty acid oxidation defect"],"management":["Avoid prolonged fasting with regular carbohydrate-containing feeds as the cornerstone of prevention","Provide an emergency regimen for intercurrent illness, escalating to intravenous dextrose to reverse catabolism and prevent hypoketotic hypoglycaemia","Avoid medium-chain triglyceride oil, which bypasses the enzymatic block and can precipitate crises","Consider L-carnitine supplementation where secondary carnitine deficiency is documented","Manage acute metabolic decompensation urgently with intravenous glucose and correction of hypoglycaemia and encephalopathy","Identify cases early through newborn screening and involve a metabolic specialist and dietitian","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance and test at-risk siblings","Provide a written emergency care plan and family education, as unrecognised crises can be fatal"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"mednik_syndrome":{"name":"MEDNIK syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mental retardation-enteropathy-deafness-neuropathy-ichthyosis-keratodermia syndrome","Kamouraska syndrome","AP1S1-related disorder","AP1S1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / Journal of Inherited Metabolic Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The acronym summarises the core features: mental retardation, enteropathy, deafness, peripheral neuropathy, ichthyosis, and keratodermia","An autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic variants in AP1S1, encoding a subunit of the adaptor protein complex 1 (AP-1) that governs intracellular vesicular trafficking","Copper indices resemble those of Wilson disease and Menkes disease combined: low serum copper and caeruloplasmin alongside high hepatic copper, reflecting mislocalisation of the copper ATPases ATP7A and ATP7B","Congenital or infantile diarrhoea (enteropathy) may be severe and life-threatening in early life","Progressive sensorineural hearing loss and a length-dependent sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy develop over time","Skin findings include congenital ichthyosis and palmoplantar keratoderma reflecting disturbed epidermal trafficking","Cholestatic liver disease with copper accumulation can occur, and copper-modulating therapy has been explored","Diagnosis rests on the characteristic multisystem pattern confirmed by AP1S1 sequencing"],"pathophysiology":"MEDNIK syndrome results from loss-of-function variants in AP1S1, which encodes the sigma-1A subunit of the heterotetrameric adaptor protein complex 1. AP-1 mediates clathrin-dependent sorting of transmembrane cargo between the trans-Golgi network and endosomes. Disrupted AP-1 function impairs the correct trafficking and localisation of the copper-transporting ATPases ATP7A and ATP7B, producing a mixed copper-handling defect that biochemically overlaps both Menkes and Wilson disease. The same trafficking failure perturbs epidermal keratinocyte differentiation, enterocyte function, myelination, and neuronal development, generating the combined dermatological, gastrointestinal, auditory, neurological, and cognitive phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Wilson disease (hepatic copper accumulation)","Menkes disease and occipital horn syndrome (ATP7A defects)","Congenital ichthyosis syndromes (e.g. Netherton syndrome)","Congenital diarrhoeal and enteropathy syndromes","Peripheral neuropathies with deafness (e.g. some Charcot-Marie-Tooth variants)","Other congenital disorders of glycosylation and trafficking"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum copper and caeruloplasmin (typically low)","Hepatic copper quantification and liver function tests","AP1S1 molecular genetic testing","Audiometry for sensorineural hearing loss","Nerve conduction studies for peripheral neuropathy","Skin examination and, where needed, biopsy for ichthyosis and keratoderma"],"redFlags":["Severe infantile enteropathy with dehydration and failure to thrive","Progressive cholestatic liver disease with copper overload","Rapidly progressive hearing loss or neuropathy","Sepsis complicating protracted diarrhoea in infancy"],"prognosis":"A chronic multisystem disorder; early enteropathy can be life-threatening, while survivors face progressive hearing loss, neuropathy, and variable cognitive impairment, with liver involvement contributing to long-term morbidity.","pitfalls":["Interpreting the copper indices as isolated Wilson or Menkes disease and missing the trafficking diagnosis","Attributing infantile diarrhoea solely to infection or allergy","Overlooking the syndromic significance of combined skin, hearing, and nerve findings","Assuming copper chelation alone addresses the underlying multisystem defect"],"management":["Zinc acetate, with copper-modulating therapy as used in Wilson disease, to correct the disturbed copper handling and limit hepatic copper accumulation","Nutritional support for the enteropathy, including parenteral nutrition during severe infantile diarrhoea","Emollients, keratolytics and topical or systemic retinoids for ichthyosis and palmoplantar keratoderma","Hearing aids or cochlear implantation for progressive sensorineural hearing loss, with physiotherapy and supportive care for the peripheral neuropathy","Developmental, educational and communication support for cognitive impairment","Monitor liver function and hepatic copper, with periodic audiometry and nerve conduction studies","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance and test at-risk family members","Coordinate care through a metabolic, dermatology, neurology and audiology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Journal of Inherited Metabolic Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"medullary_cystic_kidney":{"name":"Medullary cystic kidney disease","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCKD","autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease","ADTKD","nephronophthisis-medullary cystic kidney disease complex"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An inherited tubulointerstitial disease, now largely reclassified within autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease (ADTKD), that causes slowly progressive chronic kidney disease","Associated genes include UMOD (uromodulin), MUC1, REN (renin), and HNF1B, each with characteristic features","UMOD-related disease is notable for hyperuricaemia and early-onset gout, often out of proportion to kidney function","Small cysts, when present, arise at the corticomedullary junction, but cysts may be absent and the kidneys are often normal or small in size","Urinalysis is typically bland with little proteinuria or haematuria, reflecting the tubulointerstitial rather than glomerular nature of the disease","It is closely related to nephronophthisis, which is the autosomal recessive childhood-onset counterpart within the same disease complex","Diagnosis rests on family history, the bland urine, characteristic extrarenal clues, and genetic testing rather than on imaging alone","Care is supportive, focusing on slowing progression, managing gout and complications, and planning for kidney replacement therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Medullary cystic kidney disease, part of the autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease group, results from mutations in genes expressed in the distal tubule such as UMOD, MUC1, REN, and HNF1B. Defective or misfolded proteins (for example mutant uromodulin retained in tubular cells) trigger tubular injury, interstitial fibrosis, and tubular atrophy, sometimes with small corticomedullary cysts. The gradual loss of functioning nephrons produces progressive chronic kidney disease. In UMOD disease, impaired uromodulin handling also reduces urate excretion, causing hyperuricaemia and gout; REN mutations may cause hyperkalaemia, mild anaemia, and hypotension.","additionalDifferentials":["Nephronophthisis (recessive juvenile form)","Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease","Chronic interstitial nephritis from other causes","Familial juvenile hyperuricaemic nephropathy","Reflux or obstructive nephropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum urate, particularly where early gout is present","Renal ultrasound to assess kidney size and corticomedullary cysts","Urinalysis to confirm a bland sediment with minimal proteinuria","Detailed family history and pedigree analysis","Genetic testing for UMOD, MUC1, REN, and HNF1B mutations"],"redFlags":["Progressive decline toward kidney failure","Refractory gout with joint destruction","Hyperkalaemia or anaemia suggesting REN-related disease","Family clustering of unexplained kidney failure"],"prognosis":"The disease progresses slowly but relentlessly to kidney failure, commonly between the third and sixth decades depending on the gene involved; extrarenal manifestations such as gout are manageable, and kidney transplantation is not affected by disease recurrence.","pitfalls":["Expecting cysts on imaging and dismissing the diagnosis when none are seen","Overlooking early gout as a clue to UMOD-related disease","Misclassifying it as generic chronic interstitial nephritis without seeking a genetic cause","Failing to take a thorough family history in unexplained progressive CKD"],"management":["Manage as chronic kidney disease with blood pressure control and avoidance of nephrotoxins, since there is no disease-specific therapy","Treat hyperuricaemia and gout in UMOD disease with a xanthine oxidase inhibitor such as allopurinol","In REN-related disease avoid renin-angiotensin blockade, NSAIDs and volume depletion, and use fludrocortisone for salt-wasting hypotension and hyperkalaemia","Monitor eGFR trajectory and urate, with potassium and haemoglobin in REN disease","Plan timely renal replacement therapy and transplantation, which is curative as the disease does not recur in the graft","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance with cascade family screening and careful living-donor evaluation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"medullary_cystic_kidney_disease":{"name":"Medullary cystic kidney disease","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCKD","Autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Now largely reclassified as autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease (ADTKD), with UMOD and MUC1 the commonest causative genes.","Inheritance is autosomal dominant, with slowly progressive chronic kidney disease and characteristically bland urine showing little or no proteinuria or haematuria.","UMOD (uromodulin) disease typically causes hyperuricaemia and early-onset gout, often preceding renal impairment.","Cysts are few, small and corticomedullary and are frequently absent, so the historical name is a misnomer and the diagnosis is not made on imaging.","Kidneys are normal or small in size and imaging is usually non-specific.","Distinguish it from nephronophthisis, which is autosomal recessive, causes end-stage disease in childhood or adolescence and has retinal, hepatic and skeletal associations.","Diagnosis rests on family history, clinical phenotype and genetic testing; biopsy shows tubulointerstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy.","Management is supportive, progressing over decades to end-stage renal disease requiring renal replacement or transplantation."],"pathophysiology":"Medullary cystic kidney disease belongs to a group of autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial diseases caused by mutations in genes expressed in the distal nephron. UMOD mutations cause misfolding and intracellular retention of uromodulin within thick ascending limb cells, provoking endoplasmic reticulum stress and tubular injury, whereas MUC1 frameshift mutations generate a toxic mucin-1 neoprotein; HNF1B and REN mutations account for further cases. The common pathway is progressive tubulointerstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy with relatively bland urine, and corticomedullary cysts, when present, are a secondary and inconstant feature.","additionalDifferentials":["Nephronophthisis (autosomal recessive)","Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease","Chronic interstitial nephritis (analgesic or obstructive)","Familial juvenile hyperuricaemic nephropathy (part of the UMOD spectrum)","Reflux nephropathy","Alport syndrome or thin basement membrane disease","Medullary sponge kidney"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed family pedigree and inheritance assessment","Serum urate (hyperuricaemia in UMOD disease)","Urinalysis (characteristically bland)","Renal ultrasound (normal or small kidneys; cysts inconstant)","Serial renal function and eGFR trajectory","Genetic testing (UMOD, MUC1, HNF1B, REN)","Renal biopsy where genetics are unavailable, showing tubulointerstitial fibrosis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining renal function","Uraemic symptoms indicating advanced chronic kidney disease","Refractory or tophaceous gout","Extrarenal features suggesting an alternative genetic syndrome"],"prognosis":"The disease progresses slowly over decades, with most affected individuals reaching end-stage renal disease in adulthood; transplantation is successful because the condition does not recur in the graft.","pitfalls":["Expecting imaging cysts to confirm the diagnosis when they are often absent","Confusing autosomal recessive nephronophthisis with autosomal dominant ADTKD","Missing UMOD disease in young patients presenting with gout and chronic kidney disease","Overlooking the family history because of variable expression"],"management":["Provide supportive chronic kidney disease care with blood pressure optimisation and avoidance of nephrotoxic drugs, as no targeted therapy exists","Lower urate with a xanthine oxidase inhibitor such as allopurinol to control the early gout of UMOD-related disease","Where REN is implicated, avoid renin-angiotensin inhibitors, NSAIDs and dehydration, and correct hypotension and hyperkalaemia with fludrocortisone","Track eGFR decline and serum urate to anticipate progression to kidney failure","Prepare for dialysis and kidney transplantation, which succeeds because the condition does not recur in the allograft","Confirm the causative gene by genetic testing and offer counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance with family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Oxford Textbook of Medicine","KDIGO consensus report on autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"medullary_sponge_kidney":{"name":"Medullary sponge kidney","system":"Renal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MSK","Cacchi-Ricci disease","medullary tubular ectasia","precalyceal canalicular ectasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard nephrology and urology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A developmental disorder characterised by dilatation (ectasia) of the terminal collecting ducts in the renal medullary pyramids","Often asymptomatic and discovered incidentally, but classically presents with recurrent kidney stones, haematuria or urinary tract infections","Nephrocalcinosis with clusters of small medullary calcifications is a hallmark imaging finding","Associated tubular defects include a urinary acidification defect (distal renal tubular acidosis) and hypercalciuria, which promote stone formation","Most cases are sporadic, though familial clustering and associations with hemihypertrophy and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome are reported","Overall renal function is usually preserved, and progression to kidney failure is uncommon","Contrast imaging characteristically shows a 'paintbrush' or 'bouquet of flowers' appearance of contrast within the ectatic ducts"],"pathophysiology":"Medullary sponge kidney is a congenital abnormality in which the precalyceal segments of the collecting ducts within the renal pyramids are cystically dilated, producing a sponge-like cut surface. The stagnant urine within these ectatic ducts, combined with frequently coexisting metabolic tubular abnormalities such as hypercalciuria, hypocitraturia and impaired distal urinary acidification, favours crystal precipitation, medullary nephrocalcinosis and recurrent calcium stone formation. Urinary stasis also predisposes to infection. Although the defect is structural and lifelong, glomerular function is generally maintained because the process is confined to the distal tubular architecture.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic recurrent calcium nephrolithiasis","Distal renal tubular acidosis of other cause","Papillary necrosis","Nephrocalcinosis from primary hyperparathyroidism or hypervitaminosis D","Autosomal dominant polycystic or medullary cystic kidney disease","Renal tuberculosis causing calcification"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cross-sectional or contrast urography demonstrating medullary tubular ectasia and nephrocalcinosis","Non-contrast CT to characterise stone burden and calcification","24-hour urine metabolic stone screen (calcium, citrate, oxalate, urate, volume)","Urinary pH and assessment for distal renal tubular acidosis","Serum calcium, phosphate and PTH to exclude systemic causes of nephrocalcinosis","Urinalysis and culture given the risk of recurrent infection"],"redFlags":["Obstructing stone with infection (obstructive pyelonephritis) requiring urgent decompression","Recurrent urosepsis","Progressive nephrocalcinosis with declining renal function","Persistent haematuria warranting exclusion of another urinary tract pathology"],"prognosis":"The long-term outlook is generally good, with renal function usually preserved; morbidity arises chiefly from recurrent stones and infections, which are reduced by metabolic evaluation, high fluid intake and correction of underlying tubular abnormalities.","pitfalls":["Mistaking medullary nephrocalcinosis for other systemic causes without a metabolic workup","Failing to screen for and treat distal renal tubular acidosis and hypercalciuria that drive stones","Overlooking infection behind an obstructing calculus","Attributing all cysts to polycystic disease rather than recognising tubular ectasia"],"management":["Maintain high fluid intake to reduce urinary stasis and stone formation as the mainstay of prevention","Potassium citrate alkali therapy to correct hypocitraturia and the distal renal tubular acidification defect","A thiazide diuretic to reduce hypercalciuria and calcium stone recurrence","Prompt culture-directed antimicrobial treatment of urinary tract infections, to which urinary stasis predisposes","Manage acute stone episodes with analgesia and urological intervention such as ureteroscopy or shock-wave lithotripsy","A metabolic stone screen and periodic monitoring of renal function","Reassure that renal function is usually preserved, and screen for associated hemihypertrophy or Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome where relevant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"medullary_thyroid_carcinoma":{"name":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MTC","medullary carcinoma of thyroid","C-cell carcinoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / WHO Classification of Endocrine Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Arises from calcitonin-secreting parafollicular C cells rather than follicular epithelium, so it does not concentrate iodine and radioactive iodine therapy is ineffective","Serum calcitonin is the key tumour marker for diagnosis and follow-up, with carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) as a complementary marker; both track tumour burden","Occurs sporadically or as part of hereditary MEN2 syndromes, so a germline RET mutation should be sought in every patient and appropriate family screening offered","Cytology shows dispersed spindle or plasmacytoid cells with amyloid stroma derived from calcitonin, and immunostaining is positive for calcitonin","Diarrhoea or flushing may occur with advanced disease owing to high circulating calcitonin and related peptides","Total thyroidectomy with central and often lateral nodal clearance is the mainstay, as the tumour spreads early to cervical nodes and the disease is not radioiodine-avid","RET and multikinase inhibitors are used in progressive or metastatic disease, and selective RET inhibitors are effective where a RET alteration is present"],"pathophysiology":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma is a neuroendocrine tumour of the parafollicular C cells, which normally secrete calcitonin. Because it does not originate from follicular epithelium, it neither takes up iodine nor produces thyroglobulin, so calcitonin and CEA serve as its markers and radioactive iodine has no role. Sporadic tumours and the hereditary MEN2 syndromes are both driven by activating RET proto-oncogene mutations; germline RET mutations cause the familial forms and, in MEN2, associate with phaeochromocytoma and, in MEN2A, primary hyperparathyroidism. The tumour deposits amyloid derived from calcitonin and metastasises early to regional cervical lymph nodes.","additionalDifferentials":["Papillary or follicular thyroid carcinoma","Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma","Primary thyroid lymphoma","Parathyroid tumour presenting as a neck mass","Metastasis or neuroendocrine tumour involving the thyroid","Benign thyroid nodule"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen as diagnostic and monitoring markers","Germline RET mutation testing with genetic counselling and family screening","Biochemical exclusion of phaeochromocytoma (plasma or urinary metanephrines) before any surgery","Serum calcium and PTH to screen for associated primary hyperparathyroidism in MEN2A","Neck ultrasound with fine-needle aspiration and calcitonin immunostaining","Cross-sectional imaging and functional imaging to stage nodal and distant disease when calcitonin is markedly elevated"],"redFlags":["Coexisting phaeochromocytoma, which must be identified and treated before thyroid surgery to avoid intraoperative crisis","Very high calcitonin with secretory diarrhoea or flushing indicating extensive metastatic disease","Rapidly rising calcitonin or CEA doubling times signalling aggressive progression","Airway or recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement from bulky local disease"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on stage: node-negative disease confined to the thyroid can be cured surgically, whereas extensive nodal or distant metastasis and short calcitonin/CEA doubling times predict poorer survival.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to thyroid surgery without first excluding a phaeochromocytoma in a potential MEN2 patient","Forgetting germline RET testing, thereby missing an inherited syndrome and the chance for family screening","Expecting benefit from radioactive iodine, which is ineffective as C cells do not concentrate iodine","Attributing chronic diarrhoea to gastrointestinal causes and overlooking a calcitonin-secreting tumour"],"management":["Total thyroidectomy with central and, where indicated, lateral cervical lymph node dissection as the surgical mainstay","Exclude and treat any phaeochromocytoma first with alpha-adrenergic blockade and adrenalectomy before thyroid surgery","Lifelong levothyroxine replacement postoperatively, noting the tumour is not radioiodine-avid","RET-selective inhibitors such as selpercatinib or pralsetinib, or multikinase inhibitors such as cabozantinib or vandetanib, for progressive advanced or metastatic disease","Serial calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen with doubling-time assessment to detect residual or recurrent disease","Manage hormone-mediated diarrhoea and flushing with antidiarrhoeals and somatostatin analogues in advanced disease","Germline RET testing with genetic counselling, cascade family screening, prophylactic thyroidectomy in gene carriers and surveillance for hyperparathyroidism in MEN2A"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","WHO Classification of Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"medulloblastoma":{"name":"Medulloblastoma","system":"Oncology / Neuro-oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cerebellar medulloblastoma","WNT-activated medulloblastoma","SHH-activated medulloblastoma","group 3 medulloblastoma","group 4 medulloblastoma","CNS WHO grade 4 embryonal tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of the CNS","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest malignant embryonal brain tumour of childhood, typically arising in the cerebellum and presenting with signs of raised intracranial pressure and truncal ataxia","Now classified into four molecular groups: WNT-activated, SHH-activated, and the provisional non-WNT/non-SHH group 3 and group 4, each with distinct biology and outcome","WNT-activated tumours carry the most favourable prognosis, whereas group 3 (often MYC-amplified) tends to behave most aggressively","Leptomeningeal (CSF) dissemination is common, so craniospinal MRI and CSF cytology are essential for accurate staging before treatment","Histological patterns include classic, desmoplastic/nodular, extensive nodularity, and large-cell/anaplastic forms, the last carrying a worse outlook","Desmoplastic/nodular and extensive-nodularity morphology is enriched in the SHH group, including infants and germline predisposition syndromes","Standard management combines maximal safe surgical resection, craniospinal radiotherapy (age-permitting), and multi-agent chemotherapy, stratified by risk"],"pathophysiology":"Medulloblastoma is an embryonal tumour thought to arise from progenitor cells of the cerebellar and brainstem region, including granule-cell precursors of the external granular layer and cells of the rhombic lip. Distinct molecular groups reflect dysregulation of key developmental signalling pathways: constitutive WNT signalling, aberrant sonic-hedgehog (SHH) pathway activation, and the less well-defined group 3 and group 4 tumours characterised by transcriptional and epigenetic changes with frequent MYC or MYCN amplification. Germline predisposition may underlie some cases, such as APC mutation in WNT tumours and PTCH1, SUFU or TP53 alterations in SHH tumours. The tumour has a strong tendency to seed the cerebrospinal fluid pathways, producing leptomeningeal metastases along the neuraxis.","additionalDifferentials":["Pilocytic astrocytoma of the cerebellum","Ependymoma of the fourth ventricle","Atypical teratoid/rhabdoid tumour","Other CNS embryonal tumours","Cerebellar haemangioblastoma","Metastatic small round blue cell tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced MRI of the brain to characterise the posterior fossa mass","Craniospinal MRI to assess for leptomeningeal spread before surgery where feasible","Lumbar CSF cytology (timed appropriately after surgery) for staging","Histopathology with molecular subgrouping (WNT, SHH, group 3, group 4)","Assessment for MYC/MYCN amplification and TP53 status","Germline testing for predisposition syndromes when clinically suggested"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive raised intracranial pressure with obstructive hydrocephalus requiring urgent decompression","Reduced conscious level or signs of impending tonsillar herniation","Radiological or cytological evidence of leptomeningeal dissemination","Large-cell/anaplastic histology or MYC amplification indicating high-risk disease"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on molecular group, extent of resection, metastatic stage, and age. WNT-activated disease has an excellent prognosis, standard-risk non-WNT/non-SHH disease does moderately well with combined treatment, while metastatic, MYC-amplified group 3 and anaplastic tumours carry a poorer outlook. Long-term survivors frequently experience treatment-related neurocognitive, endocrine and growth sequelae.","pitfalls":["Treating all medulloblastomas uniformly without molecular subgrouping, which now guides risk stratification and therapy de-escalation","Omitting craniospinal imaging and CSF cytology, leading to understaging of metastatic disease","Performing lumbar puncture before imaging in the presence of a posterior fossa mass and hydrocephalus","Underestimating the long-term cognitive and endocrine harm of craniospinal radiotherapy in young children"],"management":["Maximal safe surgical resection of the posterior fossa tumour as the initial definitive step","Craniospinal radiotherapy with a tumour-bed boost in children beyond infancy, intensity tailored to molecular group and risk stratification","Adjuvant multi-agent chemotherapy with platinum-based regimens combined with vincristine and an alkylating agent, used to defer or reduce radiotherapy in infants","Relieve raised intracranial pressure and hydrocephalus with corticosteroids and CSF diversion","Craniospinal MRI and CSF cytology for staging, with molecular subgrouping to guide risk-adapted therapy","Long-term surveillance imaging for relapse and management of neurocognitive, endocrine and growth late-effects, including hormone replacement","Genetic counselling and testing for predisposition syndromes such as APC in WNT and TP53, PTCH1 or SUFU in SHH tumours","Coordinate multidisciplinary neuro-oncology, neurosurgery, radiation oncology and rehabilitation care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System","Louis DN et al. The 2021 WHO Classification of Tumors of the Central Nervous System"],"crossLinks":[]},"meesmann_corneal_dystrophy":{"name":"Meesmann epithelial corneal dystrophy","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MECD","juvenile epithelial dystrophy of Meesmann","KRT3/KRT12-related epithelial dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology / Cornea by Krachmer","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal dominant epithelial dystrophy presenting in early childhood with myriad tiny intraepithelial cysts best seen on retroillumination","Caused by mutations in the cornea-specific keratin genes KRT12 or KRT3, which weaken the epithelial cytoskeleton","The cysts are most numerous in the interpalpebral zone and appear as fine clear vesicles extending toward the limbus","Many patients are asymptomatic, but cyst rupture can cause recurrent erosions with irritation, photophobia, and intermittent blurred vision","Vision is usually well preserved because the epithelium remains relatively transparent despite the cysts","Histology shows a thickened, fragile epithelium with intraepithelial cysts and a characteristic PAS-positive material (peculiar substance)","Management is conservative with lubrication and bandage contact lenses, reserving epithelial debridement or phototherapeutic keratectomy for troublesome erosions","Symptoms and cysts frequently recur after superficial treatment because the underlying keratin defect persists"],"pathophysiology":"Meesmann epithelial corneal dystrophy is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in the cornea-specific keratin genes KRT12 and KRT3. These keratins form the intermediate filament cytoskeleton of the corneal epithelium, and mutations weaken epithelial cell integrity. The fragile epithelium develops numerous small intraepithelial cysts containing degenerated cellular material, and the compromised cells are prone to breakdown. Cyst rupture and epithelial fragility give rise to recurrent surface disruption and irritation, while the overall epithelial transparency is largely maintained.","additionalDifferentials":["Epithelial basement membrane (map-dot-fingerprint) dystrophy","Lisch epithelial corneal dystrophy","Recurrent corneal erosion syndrome of other cause","Punctate epithelial keratopathy from dry eye or toxicity","Viral epithelial keratitis","Contact-lens-related epithelial microcysts"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp biomicroscopy with retroillumination to reveal intraepithelial cysts","Genetic testing for KRT12 and KRT3 mutations","In vivo confocal microscopy to visualise epithelial cysts","Anterior segment optical coherence tomography of the epithelial layer","Histology of debrided epithelium showing cysts and PAS-positive material","Fluorescein staining to document epithelial breakdown during symptomatic episodes"],"redFlags":["Frequent painful erosions disrupting daily function","Non-healing epithelial defect raising concern for secondary infection","Unexpected stromal involvement or scarring suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Progressive visual loss that is atypical for this predominantly epithelial dystrophy"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally good, with vision usually well preserved. Recurrent erosions can cause chronic irritation, and symptoms tend to recur after superficial treatment because the underlying keratin defect is not corrected.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the epithelial microcysts for contact-lens changes or viral keratitis","Expecting a permanent cure from epithelial debridement despite the genetic basis","Overtreating asymptomatic cysts that do not threaten vision","Failing to counsel about the autosomal dominant inheritance"],"management":["Ocular lubricants with artificial tears and lubricating ointment for surface irritation as first-line symptomatic care","Bandage contact lens and hypertonic saline for recurrent epithelial erosions","Reassure and observe asymptomatic patients, as vision is usually preserved","Epithelial debridement or phototherapeutic keratectomy for troublesome erosions, counselling that recurrence is expected because the underlying keratin defect persists","Fluorescein assessment during symptomatic flares to document epithelial breakdown","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and examine at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Cornea by Krachmer, Mannis and Holland"],"crossLinks":[]},"meester_loeys_syndrome":{"name":"Meester-Loeys syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MRLS","BGN","biglycan-related aortopathy","X-linked thoracic aortic aneurysm syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews / Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked heritable aortopathy: consider it particularly in males with early-onset aortic root dilatation, aneurysm and dissection","Caused by pathogenic variants in BGN, which encodes biglycan, a small leucine-rich proteoglycan of the extracellular matrix, on the X chromosome","Because inheritance is X-linked, affected males are typically more severely affected while carrier females can show milder or variable features","Aortic and arterial tortuosity and aneurysms can extend beyond the aortic root, so arterial imaging should not be confined to the heart","Connective-tissue and skeletal features overlap with other aortopathies: pectus deformity, scoliosis, joint hypermobility and a marfanoid or dysmorphic appearance","Aortic surveillance imaging is essential, with medical therapy and timely surgical repair to reduce dissection risk","Blood-pressure-lowering agents such as beta-blockers or angiotensin-pathway blockers are used as in related aortopathies","Family screening should account for the X-linked pattern when counselling relatives"],"pathophysiology":"Meester-Loeys syndrome is an X-linked heritable connective-tissue aortopathy caused by loss-of-function variants in BGN, encoding biglycan. Biglycan is a small leucine-rich proteoglycan of the extracellular matrix that helps organise collagen and elastic fibres and modulates transforming growth factor beta signalling in the arterial wall. Deficiency of functional biglycan weakens the structural integrity of the aortic media and dysregulates growth-factor signalling, predisposing to aortic root and more widespread arterial dilatation, tortuosity, aneurysm and dissection. The same matrix disturbance produces the skeletal and connective-tissue features. Because the gene is X-linked, hemizygous males are generally affected more severely than heterozygous females.","additionalDifferentials":["Marfan syndrome","Loeys-Dietz syndrome","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Other familial thoracic aortic aneurysm and dissection syndromes","Arterial tortuosity syndrome","Shprintzen-Goldberg syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["BGN molecular genetic testing","Echocardiography for aortic root and cardiac assessment","Whole-body magnetic resonance or CT angiography for aneurysms and tortuosity","Skeletal survey for scoliosis and chest-wall deformity","Ophthalmological review for connective-tissue features","Cascade family screening informed by X-linked inheritance"],"redFlags":["Acute chest, back or abdominal pain suggesting aortic dissection or rupture","Rapidly enlarging aortic root or other aneurysm","Signs of aortic regurgitation or cardiac decompensation","New arterial aneurysms on surveillance imaging"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is dominated by the risk of aortic aneurysm and dissection, which can be life-threatening, particularly in males; structured surveillance, medical therapy and timely surgery substantially improve outcome.","pitfalls":["Restricting imaging to the aortic root and missing more distal arterial disease","Failing to recognise the X-linked inheritance when counselling and screening families","Assuming carrier females are unaffected","Delaying surgical referral for an enlarging aneurysm"],"management":["Beta-blockade and/or an angiotensin receptor blocker such as losartan to reduce aortic wall stress and slow aneurysm growth","Regular aortic and whole-body arterial surveillance with echocardiography and MR or CT angiography, as aneurysms extend beyond the root","Prophylactic surgical repair of the aortic root or aneurysms once size or growth thresholds are reached, ahead of dissection","Activity modification avoiding isometric straining and contact or competitive sports","Emergency recognition and management of acute aortic dissection","Manage skeletal and connective-tissue features such as scoliosis, pectus deformity and joint hypermobility with orthopaedic and physiotherapy input","Genetic counselling for X-linked inheritance with cascade family screening, noting more severe disease in males","Coordinate care through a cardiology, cardiothoracic surgery and clinical genetics multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["OMIM entry for Meester-Loeys Syndrome (BGN)","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Consensus statements on heritable thoracic aortic disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"mef2c_haploinsufficiency":{"name":"MEF2C Haploinsufficiency Syndrome","system":"Neurology / Neurodevelopmental disorder","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MEF2C-related disorder","5q14.3 microdeletion syndrome","MRD20"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["MEF2C encodes a MADS-box (MEF2) transcription factor essential for neuronal differentiation, migration and activity-dependent synapse regulation.","Disease arises from heterozygous intragenic variants or 5q14.3 microdeletions encompassing the gene; both produce haploinsufficiency.","Core features are severe to profound intellectual disability, absent or minimal speech and marked early hypotonia.","Epilepsy is frequent, with variable seizure types and onset commonly in infancy.","Stereotypic movements (including hand stereotypies), poor eye contact and autistic features produce a Rett-like phenotype.","Most affected children do not achieve independent walking, and feeding difficulty and constipation are common.","Neuroimaging may show mildly enlarged ventricles, abnormal periventricular white matter or a hypoplastic corpus callosum.","Larger contiguous 5q14.3 deletions may involve neighbouring genes and broaden the phenotype."],"pathophysiology":"MEF2C at chromosome 5q14.3 encodes myocyte enhancer factor 2C, a MADS-box transcription factor that acts as a master regulator of activity-dependent neuronal development, directing neurogenesis, neuronal migration and dendritic morphology while restraining excitatory synapse number. The disorder results from heterozygous loss-of-function point variants or 5q14.3 microdeletions that remove or disrupt one MEF2C allele, producing haploinsufficiency; contiguous deletions extending beyond the gene may add features from adjacent loci. Reduced MEF2C dosage impairs the downstream transcriptional programmes (including targets such as BDNF and immediate-early genes) required for cortical network maturation, yielding severe neurodevelopmental impairment, epilepsy and a Rett-like clinical picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Rett syndrome (MECP2)","CDKL5 deficiency disorder","FOXG1 syndrome (congenital Rett variant)","Angelman syndrome","Pitt-Hopkins syndrome (TCF4)","MECP2 duplication syndrome","Other 5q14.3 contiguous-gene deletion phenotypes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal microarray (detects 5q14.3 deletions)","MEF2C sequencing via targeted gene, panel or exome testing","MRI brain","EEG for seizure characterisation","Developmental, feeding and swallowing assessment","MECP2, CDKL5 and FOXG1 testing if the Rett spectrum is considered"],"redFlags":["Regression or loss of previously acquired skills","Poorly controlled or evolving epilepsy","Feeding difficulty with aspiration or faltering growth","New focal neurological signs prompting repeat imaging"],"prognosis":"A lifelong severe neurodevelopmental disorder; most individuals remain non-verbal with limited mobility and need full daily support, although seizures may be partially controllable and survival into adulthood is possible.","pitfalls":["Clinically indistinguishable from Rett syndrome, so negative MECP2 testing should prompt MEF2C and microarray analysis","Small intragenic variants missed by microarray require sequence-based testing","Attributing stereotypies to idiopathic autism without genetic workup","Assuming a normal microarray excludes the diagnosis when a point mutation is responsible"],"management":["Antiseizure medication tailored to seizure type to control the frequently associated epilepsy","Early developmental intervention with physiotherapy and occupational therapy for hypotonia and motor delay","Speech and language therapy with augmentative and alternative communication for absent or minimal speech","Feeding and swallowing assessment with nutritional support, and gastrostomy where feeding difficulty is severe","Manage constipation with laxatives and provide behavioural support for autistic features and stereotypies","EEG and brain MRI to characterise seizures and structural change, with ongoing developmental monitoring","Genetic counselling, noting most cases are de novo, with confirmatory testing and recurrence-risk assessment","Coordinate multidisciplinary paediatric neurology, developmental and therapy support for lifelong care needs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical dysmorphology and neurogenetics reference texts","Paediatric neurology reference texts","Neurodevelopmental genetics review literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"megacystis_microcolon_intestinal_hypoperistalsis":{"name":"Megacystis-microcolon-intestinal hypoperistalsis syndrome","system":"Gastrointestinal / Genitourinary (visceral smooth muscle)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MMIHS","Berdon syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and paediatric surgery references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare, severe congenital visceral myopathy defined by a massively distended, non-obstructed urinary bladder (megacystis), a narrow unused microcolon and intestinal hypoperistalsis.","In contrast to most neonatal obstructive disorders, it shows a female predominance.","The commonest cause is a heterozygous, usually de novo, variant in ACTG2, encoding smooth-muscle gamma-actin; autosomal recessive forms involve other contractile-apparatus genes such as MYH11, LMOD1, MYLK and MYL9.","Antenatal clues include an enlarged fetal bladder with upper urinary tract dilatation.","Neonates present with abdominal distension, bilious vomiting, failure to pass meconium and functional urinary retention requiring catheterisation.","Enteric ganglion cells are present, which distinguishes the condition from Hirschsprung disease.","Malrotation is a frequent association.","Most affected infants develop intestinal failure and become dependent on parenteral nutrition."],"pathophysiology":"The syndrome is a congenital visceral myopathy in which the smooth muscle of the bladder and gastrointestinal tract fails to generate effective contraction despite the absence of any mechanical obstruction. The commonest genetic cause is a heterozygous, usually de novo, variant in ACTG2, which encodes enteric smooth-muscle gamma-actin; such variants act in a dominant-negative fashion, impairing actin polymerisation and thus force generation. Autosomal recessive forms arise from biallelic variants in other genes of the contractile apparatus and its regulation, such as MYH11, LMOD1, MYLK and MYL9. The consequence is ineffective peristalsis: the bladder becomes massively distended and thin-walled with functional urinary retention and secondary upper-tract dilatation, while the small intestine shows hypoperistalsis or aperistalsis and the unused distal bowel remains narrow as a microcolon. Histology may reveal vacuolar degeneration of smooth muscle, at times with fibrosis, but enteric ganglion cells are characteristically preserved, which is central to distinguishing the condition from Hirschsprung disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Hirschsprung disease (aganglionosis, whereas ganglion cells are present here)","Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction of other cause","Prune belly (Eagle-Barrett) syndrome","Posterior urethral valves and other mechanical bladder outlet obstruction","Meconium ileus and cystic fibrosis","Intestinal atresia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antenatal and postnatal ultrasound of the bladder and urinary tract","Contrast studies demonstrating microcolon, malrotation and a functional, non-mechanical obstruction","Genetic testing beginning with ACTG2, followed by a panel for recessive visceral-myopathy genes","Full-thickness intestinal biopsy showing visceral myopathy with preserved ganglion cells","Rectal biopsy to exclude Hirschsprung disease","Bladder catheterisation and urodynamic assessment"],"redFlags":["An antenatally enlarged fetal bladder (megacystis)","Bilious vomiting with abdominal distension and failure to pass meconium","Inability to establish enteral feeding and wean from parenteral nutrition","Recurrent central venous line sepsis and intestinal-failure-associated liver disease","Progressive upper urinary tract dilatation with declining renal function"],"prognosis":"Historically associated with high early mortality from intestinal failure, sepsis and complications of long-term parenteral nutrition. Survival has improved with contemporary intestinal-failure care and intestinal or multivisceral transplantation, although morbidity remains substantial.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the functional obstruction for a mechanical one and performing futile bowel resections","Confusing the condition with Hirschsprung disease despite the presence of ganglion cells","Attributing the megacystis solely to urological outlet obstruction","Overlooking associated malrotation","Underusing antenatal bladder findings as an early diagnostic clue"],"management":["Decompress the massively distended bladder with intermittent catheterisation or vesicostomy to relieve functional urinary retention and protect the upper renal tracts","Manage intestinal failure with total parenteral nutrition, attempting cautious enteral feeding as tolerated since prokinetic agents are generally ineffective in this visceral myopathy","Maintain meticulous central venous catheter care and treat line-associated and urinary sepsis promptly with appropriate antimicrobials","Use surgical measures such as decompressive or diverting stomas for the functional obstruction rather than expecting mechanical relief","Refer early to an intestinal-failure centre for consideration of isolated intestinal or multivisceral transplantation","Coordinate multidisciplinary paediatric surgical, urological, nutritional and gastroenterological care","Offer genetic counselling and family testing given the dominant ACTG2 and recessive contractile-apparatus gene causes","Escalate promptly for sepsis, upper urinary tract deterioration or parenteral-nutrition-associated liver disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics references","Paediatric surgery reference texts","Paediatric gastroenterology and intestinal failure references"],"crossLinks":[]},"megalencephalic_leukoencephalopathy":{"name":"Megalencephalic leukoencephalopathy with subcortical cysts","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MLC","van der Knaap disease","MLC1","GLIALCAM","HEPACAM","vacuolating megalencephalic leukoencephalopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurology textbooks and peer-reviewed leukodystrophy reviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically caused by biallelic variants in MLC1; a subset arises from variants in GLIALCAM (HEPACAM), which can produce both recessive disease and a remitting dominant form","Presents in infancy with macrocephaly that develops within the first year, often the earliest and most striking sign","Early motor and cognitive development is often near-normal, with slow deterioration of gait, ataxia, and spasticity over years","Seizures are common and may be precipitated by minor head trauma; epilepsy is a frequent feature","Cognitive decline is usually mild and late relative to the severity of the white-matter changes on imaging","MRI shows diffusely swollen, abnormal cerebral white matter with characteristic subcortical cysts, typically in the anterior temporal and frontoparietal regions","The dominant GLIALCAM-related variant may show improving imaging and a milder, sometimes reversible course","Diagnosis rests on the combination of macrocephaly, the typical MRI pattern, and confirmatory genetic testing; there is no disease-modifying therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Megalencephalic leukoencephalopathy with subcortical cysts is a disorder of brain water and ion homeostasis. MLC1 and GlialCAM are proteins expressed at astrocytic end-feet and cell-cell contacts, where they regulate ion and water flux (including chloride channel activity via ClC-2) across the blood-brain and glial-limiting barriers. Defective function impairs the astrocytic handling of fluid, leading to chronic intramyelinic and extracellular oedema. Histologically the white matter is vacuolated, with splitting of myelin lamellae, and fluid accumulates to form the subcortical cysts and diffuse swelling characteristic of the disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of macrocephaly with leukoencephalopathy (Canavan disease, Alexander disease)","Vanishing white matter disease","Glutaric aciduria type 1","L-2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria","Infantile-onset lysosomal storage leukodystrophies","Benign familial macrocephaly (as a differential for the head size alone)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to demonstrate diffusely swollen white matter with anterior temporal and frontoparietal subcortical cysts","Serial head circumference measurement documenting infantile-onset macrocephaly","Molecular genetic testing of MLC1 and GLIALCAM (HEPACAM)","Metabolic screening to exclude organic acidurias and other leukodystrophies","EEG when seizures occur to characterise the epilepsy"],"redFlags":["Acute neurological deterioration or seizures following minor head trauma","Rapidly progressive loss of ambulation or bulbar function","Status epilepticus","Signs of raised intracranial pressure disproportionate to the known chronic swelling"],"prognosis":"The classic recessive form follows a slowly progressive course over years to decades, with preserved cognition until late and gradual loss of motor function; the dominant GLIALCAM-related form is milder and may partially remit. Life expectancy varies but many patients survive into adulthood.","pitfalls":["Attributing infantile macrocephaly to benign familial causes and delaying imaging","Interpreting the extensive MRI white-matter change as indicating imminent severe cognitive collapse, which is usually late","Missing the distinct improving-imaging pattern of the dominant GLIALCAM variant","Overlooking head-trauma-precipitated seizures as a recognised feature"],"management":["Recognise that no disease-modifying therapy exists, so care centres on supportive and symptomatic management of the leukoencephalopathy","Control the frequently associated epilepsy with antiseizure medication","Provide physiotherapy, occupational therapy and antispasticity measures for the slowly progressive gait disturbance, ataxia and spasticity","Advise avoidance of and prompt assessment after minor head trauma, which can precipitate seizures or acute deterioration","Offer educational support and monitor cognition, given the usually mild and late cognitive decline","Undertake regular neurological review with serial MRI to track white-matter swelling and cyst evolution","Provide genetic counselling for the recessive MLC1 and GLIALCAM forms and the milder remitting dominant GLIALCAM form"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Megalencephalic Leukoencephalopathy with Subcortical Cysts","van der Knaap and Valk, Magnetic Resonance of Myelination and Myelin Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"megalencephaly_capillary_malformation":{"name":"Megalencephaly-capillary malformation syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCAP","M-CM","megalencephaly-capillary malformation-polymicrogyria syndrome","PIK3CA-related overgrowth spectrum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Progressive megalencephaly with somatic and often asymmetric (segmental) overgrowth and cutaneous capillary malformations, classically a midline facial or philtral stain","Neurological features include developmental delay, hypotonia, seizures, and a risk of cerebellar tonsillar herniation from the disproportionate brain growth","Connective tissue laxity, cutaneous syndactyly, polydactyly, and frontal bossing are supportive features","Part of the PIK3CA-related overgrowth spectrum caused by post-zygotic (mosaic) activating PIK3CA variants","Because the variant is mosaic the condition is sporadic and not inherited, with the pattern of overgrowth reflecting the tissues carrying the variant","Brain imaging may show polymicrogyria, ventriculomegaly, cerebellar tonsillar ectopia, and white matter changes, so neuroimaging surveillance is important","Diagnosis often needs high-sensitivity sequencing of affected tissue because the mosaic variant may not be detectable in blood"],"pathophysiology":"Megalencephaly-capillary malformation syndrome is part of the PIK3CA-related overgrowth spectrum and results from post-zygotic mosaic activating variants in PIK3CA, which constitutively activate the PI3K/AKT/mTOR pathway. Excessive signalling promotes unregulated growth and proliferation, producing brain overgrowth (megalencephaly with cortical malformation such as polymicrogyria), segmental somatic overgrowth, and vascular anomalies. Because the mutation is mosaic, disease burden and distribution depend on when and where the variant arose during development.","additionalDifferentials":["CLOVES syndrome and other PIK3CA-related overgrowth phenotypes","Sotos syndrome and other macrocephalic overgrowth syndromes","PTEN hamartoma tumour syndrome","Proteus syndrome","Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome","Benign familial macrocephaly and hydrocephalus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-sensitivity sequencing of affected tissue for mosaic PIK3CA variants","Brain MRI to assess megalencephaly, polymicrogyria, ventriculomegaly, and cerebellar tonsillar ectopia","Serial head circumference measurement and neuroimaging surveillance for progressive changes","EEG where seizures are suspected","Developmental assessment to guide intervention","Echocardiography and evaluation for arrhythmia given reported cardiac associations"],"redFlags":["Signs of raised intracranial pressure or symptomatic cerebellar tonsillar herniation","New or worsening seizures","Rapidly progressive macrocephaly suggesting hydrocephalus","Cardiac arrhythmia or structural heart disease requiring assessment"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable and largely determined by the severity of brain involvement, with many individuals having developmental delay, epilepsy, and a risk of hindbrain herniation requiring neurosurgical monitoring. Somatic overgrowth and vascular features are managed supportively, and targeted pathway inhibition is being explored for severe disease.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis after normal blood testing rather than sequencing affected tissue","Overlooking evolving cerebellar tonsillar herniation on follow-up imaging","Attributing macrocephaly to benign familial causes and missing associated capillary malformation and overgrowth","Failing to recognise the sporadic mosaic nature and giving incorrect recurrence-risk counselling"],"management":["Consider targeted inhibition of the overactive PI3K/AKT/mTOR pathway with an mTOR inhibitor such as sirolimus or a PI3K inhibitor as emerging disease-directed therapy","Undertake neuroimaging surveillance with neurosurgical decompression for cerebellar tonsillar herniation and management of hydrocephalus from disproportionate brain overgrowth","Control the associated epilepsy with antiseizure medication","Treat symptomatic or disfiguring capillary malformations with pulsed-dye laser","Provide developmental therapies and management of hypotonia and developmental delay","Arrange cardiac evaluation and monitoring for arrhythmia given reported cardiac associations","Reassure that the condition arises from a post-zygotic mosaic PIK3CA variant and is sporadic rather than inherited","Coordinate multidisciplinary clinical genetics, neurology and neurosurgery input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: PIK3CA-Related Overgrowth Spectrum"],"crossLinks":[]},"megaloblastic_anemias":{"name":"Megaloblastic Anemias","system":"Hematologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Vitamin B12 deficiency anemia","Cobalamin deficiency anemia","Folate deficiency anemia","Pernicious anemia"],"chapter":104,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.780","clinicalPearls":["Megaloblastic anemias share a defect in DNA synthesis affecting rapidly dividing marrow cells, producing characteristic morphology with a hypercellular marrow and ineffective erythropoiesis (Harrison 22e p.780).","The usual cause is deficiency of cobalamin (vitamin B12) or folate, but the picture can also arise from genetic or acquired abnormalities of their metabolism or from DNA-synthesis defects unrelated to either vitamin (Harrison 22e p.780).","Body cobalamin stores (about 2-3 mg) are large enough to last 3-4 years if intake stops, whereas folate stores (about 10 mg total, mostly hepatic) suffice for only 3-4 months, so severe folate deficiency can develop rapidly (Harrison 22e p.780, p.781).","Cobalamin is made only by microorganisms; the sole human source is animal-derived food, so strict vegans are at risk of dietary deficiency (Harrison 22e p.781, p.786).","Pernicious anemia, the dominant cause of severe cobalamin deficiency in Western countries, is gastric atrophy with severe lack of intrinsic factor; median onset is age 70-80, it clusters with other organ-specific autoimmune diseases (thyroid disease, vitiligo, type 1 diabetes, Addison disease), and gastric output of acid, pepsin, and intrinsic factor is severely reduced with raised serum gastrin and low pepsinogen I (Harrison 22e p.785, p.786).","Cobalamin deficiency can cause neurologic disease (subacute combined degeneration) with demyelination of posterior and lateral spinal columns, peripheral neuropathy, optic atrophy, and cerebral changes including dementia and psychosis; patients (more often male) present with paresthesias, weakness, or gait difficulty, often with positive Romberg and Lhermitte signs (Harrison 22e p.783).","Peripheral blood shows oval macrocytes with marked aniso-poikilocytosis, an MCV usually >100 fL (unless coexisting microcytosis), and hypersegmented neutrophils (more than five nuclear lobes) (Harrison 22e p.784).","In a nonanemic patient, a few macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils may be the only clue to underlying deficiency, and serum cobalamin may be borderline; supporting tests include MMA, IF/parietal-cell antibodies, and serum gastrin (Harrison 22e p.783, p.784).","Periconceptional folic acid reduces neural tube defects (anencephaly, meningomyelocele, encephalocele, spina bifida) by roughly 80%; the lower the maternal folate, the greater the fetal risk (Harrison 22e p.783).","Older patients with iron deficiency should also be assessed for cobalamin deficiency and vice versa, since coexistence is common (15-20% over age 60) and iron deficiency may blunt the macrocytosis (Harrison 22e p.789).","Removal of 1.2 m or more of terminal ileum, total gastrectomy, fish tapeworm (Diphyllobothrium latum) infestation, and intestinal stagnant-loop syndrome are recognized malabsorptive causes of cobalamin deficiency (Harrison 22e p.786, p.787)."],"pathophysiology":"The common defect is impaired DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing cells. In folate or cobalamin deficiency, conversion of deoxyuridine monophosphate (dUMP) to deoxythymidine monophosphate (dTMP) fails because the folate coenzyme 5,10-methylene-tetrahydrofolate, needed for this step, becomes scarce. The shortage of dTTP precursor slows DNA replication, leaving single-stranded breaks; an alternative theory invokes misincorporation of uracil into DNA. Cobalamin is a cofactor for two reactions: methylmalonyl-CoA mutase and the methionine-synthase-mediated methylation of homocysteine to methionine, which also requires 5-methyl-THF. In cobalamin deficiency, 5-methyl-THF accumulates in plasma while intracellular THF (the substrate for folate polyglutamate formation) falls — the methylfolate trap (THF starvation) — explaining high serum folate but low cell folate, and why the anemia of cobalamin deficiency can respond to large folic acid doses. Death of nucleated red cells in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis) produces unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, raised LDH, raised urine urobilinogen, and reduced haptoglobins (Harrison 22e p.782, p.783, p.785).","additionalDifferentials":["Megaloblastic anemia from antifolate/antimetabolite drugs (methotrexate, pyrimethamine, trimethoprim; hydroxyurea, cytarabine, mercaptopurine, thioguanine, AZT) (Harrison 22e p.780, p.790)","Orotic aciduria (responds to uridine) (Harrison 22e p.780, p.790)","Thiamine-responsive megaloblastic anemia (SLC19A2 transporter defect; may have diabetes, deafness, ringed sideroblasts) (Harrison 22e p.780, p.790)","Megaloblastic changes in acute myeloid leukemia and myelodysplasia (refractory to folate/cobalamin) (Harrison 22e p.780, p.790)","Macrocytosis without true megaloblastic change: alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, pregnancy, myelodysplasia, myeloma/paraproteinemia, aplastic anemia, smoking (Harrison 22e p.790)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Myelodysplasia (megaloblastoid marrow may mimic megaloblastic anemia and, in severe cases, accumulation of blasts may mimic acute myeloid leukemia) (Harrison 22e p.785)","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (a weakly positive direct antiglobulin test due to complement from ineffective erythropoiesis can lead to a false diagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.785)","Other causes of macrocytosis: alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, pregnancy (Harrison 22e p.790)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum cobalamin (ELISA or competitive-binding luminescence assay); note IF antibodies can cause falsely normal results in up to 50% of pernicious anemia cases (Harrison 22e p.788)","Serum methylmalonic acid (raised in cobalamin deficiency sufficient for anemia/neuropathy; fluctuates in renal failure) (Harrison 22e p.788, p.789)","Serum homocysteine (raised in early cobalamin and folate deficiency but nonspecific) (Harrison 22e p.789)","Serum folate and red cell folate (red cell folate reflects body stores; false-normal after transfusion or with high reticulocytes) (Harrison 22e p.789)","Serum gastrin (raised in pernicious anemia) and pepsinogen I (low) (Harrison 22e p.785, p.789)","Intrinsic factor and parietal cell antibodies; gastric endoscopy/biopsy (Harrison 22e p.786, p.789)","Bone marrow examination in selected severely anemic patients (Harrison 22e p.784)","Transglutaminase antibodies to exclude gluten-induced enteropathy in folate deficiency (Harrison 22e p.789)"],"redFlags":["Neurologic signs of subacute combined degeneration: loss of proprioception/vibration, positive Romberg and Lhermitte signs, ataxic/spastic gait, hyperreflexia (Harrison 22e p.783)","Cerebral involvement: dementia, depression, psychosis, cognitive impairment, visual impairment from optic atrophy (Harrison 22e p.783)","Nonanemic patient with neurologic/psychiatric features and low or borderline serum cobalamin (deficiency must be actively confirmed) (Harrison 22e p.783)","Severe thrombocytopenia (rarely <40 x 10^9/L) with bruising (Harrison 22e p.783, p.784)","Marked marrow blast accumulation in severe cases mimicking acute leukemia (Harrison 22e p.785)"],"prognosis":"With regular treatment of pernicious anemia, life expectancy is normal in women; men historically had slightly reduced life expectancy due to a higher incidence of gastric carcinoma, with current data unavailable. Neurologic recovery depends on duration before treatment, and a therapeutic trial of at least 3 months may be needed to judge improvement in neuropsychiatric cases. Untreated severe deficiency causes progressive anemia, neurologic deterioration, and infertility (Harrison 22e p.783, p.786).","pitfalls":["Intrinsic-factor antibodies can produce falsely normal serum cobalamin in up to 50% of pernicious anemia patients tested by certain assays — a normal level does not exclude pernicious anemia when clinical suspicion is strong (Harrison 22e p.788).","Giving large doses of folic acid before excluding/correcting cobalamin deficiency can allow cobalamin neuropathy to progress despite hematologic response (Harrison 22e p.789, p.790).","Serum folate falls with recent poor diet but rises in severe cobalamin deficiency (methylfolate trap) and in stagnant-loop syndrome, so it can mislead (Harrison 22e p.789).","Mildly elevated MMA and/or homocysteine occur in up to 30% of healthy volunteers and in 15% of elderly with normal cobalamin, so cutoffs and clinical significance are uncertain (Harrison 22e p.789).","Coexisting iron deficiency may mask macrocytosis; the term 'megaloblastoid' is best avoided as it refers to myelodysplastic change refractory to vitamin therapy (Harrison 22e p.785, p.789).","A weakly positive direct antiglobulin test from ineffective erythropoiesis can be misread as autoimmune hemolytic anemia (Harrison 22e p.785)."],"management":["Give parenteral cobalamin (hydroxocobalamin) replacement for cobalamin deficiency, with lifelong maintenance in pernicious anaemia","Replace folate with oral folic acid for folate deficiency while correcting dietary and malabsorptive causes","Correct cobalamin before giving folate alone, since folate can precipitate or worsen subacute combined degeneration","Treat the underlying cause, with dietary cobalamin advice for vegans and management of malabsorption or autoimmune gastritis","Monitor for hypokalaemia and rebound thrombocytosis during the brisk haematological response to replacement","Screen for and manage associated organ-specific autoimmune disease such as thyroid disease, type 1 diabetes, Addison disease and vitiligo","Provide endoscopic surveillance in pernicious anaemia given the increased risk of gastric carcinoma","Arrange neurological review, recognising that recovery depends on the duration of deficiency before treatment"],"severityClassification":"Severity is gauged by the degree of anemia and parallels the morphologic changes; marrow involvement is described as severe, intermediate, mild, or early. In megaloblastic anemia due to cobalamin deficiency the serum cobalamin is usually <74 pmol/L (100 ng/L), with 74-148 pmol/L (100-200 ng/L) regarded as borderline; the more severe the deficiency, the lower the level, and very low levels occur with spinal cord involvement even without anemia (Harrison 22e p.785, p.788).","references":["Harrison 22e p.780","Harrison 22e p.781","Harrison 22e p.782","Harrison 22e p.783","Harrison 22e p.784","Harrison 22e p.785","Harrison 22e p.786","Harrison 22e p.787","Harrison 22e p.788","Harrison 22e p.789","Harrison 22e p.790"],"crossLinks":[]},"megalocornea":{"name":"Megalocornea","system":"Eye / Anterior segment","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anterior megalophthalmos"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ophthalmology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A non-progressive, bilaterally symmetrical enlargement of the cornea with a normal intraocular pressure","The corneal diameter is enlarged beyond the normal range while the cornea remains clear","It is most often X-linked recessive, so it predominantly affects males","The crucial differential is primary congenital glaucoma (buphthalmos), which is distinguished by raised pressure, corneal oedema and progression","Associated features can include high myopia, lens subluxation, cataract and pigment dispersion","Management is largely refractive and monitoring, with attention to lens-related complications later in life"],"pathophysiology":"Megalocornea is a developmental enlargement of the cornea, thought to result from either an oversized optic cup or failure of the developing anterior segment to close normally, producing a large but structurally clear cornea with a deep anterior chamber. In the common X-linked form it is caused by mutations affecting anterior segment development. Unlike buphthalmos, the enlargement is not driven by raised intraocular pressure, so the cornea stays clear; however, the enlarged anterior segment can be associated with zonular weakness leading to lens subluxation and cataract.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary congenital glaucoma (buphthalmos)","Keratoglobus","High axial myopia","Marfan and other connective tissue disorders (for lens subluxation)","Anterior megalophthalmos as part of a syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Measurement of corneal diameter and intraocular pressure","Assessment for corneal clarity and Descemet tears (to exclude glaucoma)","Lens and zonule assessment","Refraction","Family history and, where appropriate, genetic evaluation"],"redFlags":["Raised intraocular pressure or corneal oedema indicating glaucoma","Progressive corneal enlargement","Lens subluxation or cataract","Features of an underlying connective tissue disorder"],"prognosis":"Megalocornea itself is benign and non-progressive with generally good vision when refractive error is corrected; later lens complications may need intervention.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for primary congenital glaucoma and vice versa","Overlooking later lens subluxation or cataract","Attributing reduced vision solely to refractive error","Missing an associated systemic disorder"],"management":["Correct the associated myopia and astigmatism with spectacles or contact lenses as the mainstay of care","Measure intraocular pressure and monitor serially to distinguish the benign condition from primary congenital glaucoma","Survey the lens and zonules for later subluxation and cataract","Undertake cataract and lens surgery when visually significant complications develop, anticipating zonular weakness","Reassure that the corneal enlargement is non-progressive with a generally good visual prognosis","Offer genetic counselling for the X-linked inheritance pattern that predominantly affects males"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"megalopapilla":{"name":"megalopapilla","system":"ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["megalopapilla","abnormally large optic disc"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital anomaly defined by an abnormally large optic disc, conventionally with a horizontal or vertical diameter of roughly 2.1 mm or more.","Two forms are described: a common form with generalised disc enlargement and a large but round cup, and a form with a superotemporal or inferonasal enlargement resembling a coloboma.","The enlarged cup produces a raised cup-to-disc ratio that can be mistaken for glaucomatous cupping.","The neuroretinal rim remains healthy and of normal colour, and the cup shape is typically round and central rather than notched.","Frequently bilateral and usually associated with normal or near-normal visual acuity.","May show an enlarged blind spot on perimetry but generally without progressive glaucomatous field loss.","Distinguished from glaucoma by a normal intraocular pressure, an intact rim, and a stable, non-progressive course.","Rarely associated with midline anomalies such as basal encephalocoele, so imaging is considered when other features are present."],"pathophysiology":"Megalopapilla reflects a congenital enlargement of the scleral canal and optic nerve head, resulting in an abnormally large disc with a correspondingly large optic cup. The normal complement of retinal ganglion cell axons is distributed across a wider canal, so the cup-to-disc ratio increases while the neuroretinal rim tissue remains structurally and functionally intact. Because the enlargement is a primary developmental variant of canal size rather than a loss of axons, there is no progressive excavation or rim thinning as seen in glaucoma. The larger disc surface projects a correspondingly larger blind spot, but ganglion cell function and the visual field are otherwise typically preserved.","additionalDifferentials":["Glaucomatous optic disc cupping","Optic nerve coloboma","Morning glory disc anomaly","Physiological large cup","Peripapillary staphyloma","Optic disc pit","Tilted disc syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Stereoscopic disc examination and disc photography to assess rim integrity","Optical coherence tomography of the disc and retinal nerve fibre layer","Intraocular pressure measurement to exclude glaucoma","Automated perimetry to document any enlarged blind spot and exclude glaucomatous loss","Measurement of disc diameter and cup-to-disc ratio","Neuroimaging when a basal encephalocoele or midline anomaly is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive cup enlargement or neuroretinal rim thinning suggesting coexisting glaucoma","Raised intraocular pressure with a suspicious disc","Midline facial anomalies or features suggesting basal encephalocoele","Development of a glaucomatous or arcuate visual field defect over time"],"prognosis":"Megalopapilla is a stationary developmental variant with an excellent visual prognosis and normal acuity in most cases. Recognising it prevents overdiagnosis and unnecessary treatment for glaucoma.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the large physiological cup as glaucomatous cupping and starting pressure-lowering therapy unnecessarily.","Failing to appreciate that the neuroretinal rim is intact when interpreting a high cup-to-disc ratio.","Overlooking coexisting glaucoma in a large disc by assuming all cupping is congenital.","Neglecting to consider a midline encephalocoele in atypical or syndromic presentations."],"management":["Reassure and observe, as this is a stationary developmental disc variant requiring no active treatment","Measure intraocular pressure to exclude glaucoma masquerading behind the raised cup-to-disc ratio","Perform stereoscopic disc assessment and optical coherence tomography of the retinal nerve fibre layer to confirm a healthy neuroretinal rim","Obtain baseline and interval perimetry to document the enlarged blind spot and exclude progressive glaucomatous field loss","Arrange neuroimaging where a basal encephalocoele or midline anomaly is suspected","Avoid unnecessary anti-glaucoma treatment through correct recognition of the variant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"megdel_syndrome":{"name":"MEGDEL syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SERAC1 deficiency","3-methylglutaconic aciduria with deafness, encephalopathy and Leigh-like syndrome","MEGD(H)EL syndrome","3-methylglutaconic aciduria type IV / SERAC1-related"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID) / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder caused by SERAC1 mutations affecting a protein involved in phospholipid remodelling and cholesterol trafficking at the mitochondria-associated membranes","The acronym captures the core features: 3-Methylglutaconic aciduria, Deafness (sensorineural), Encephalopathy, and Leigh-like disease","Neonates often have transient hepatic dysfunction and hypoglycaemia in the newborn period that resolves, followed by developmental regression and a dystonia-spasticity movement disorder","Persistent 3-methylglutaconic aciduria on urine organic acids is the biochemical hallmark and a key screening clue in an infant with deafness and encephalopathy","Brain MRI evolves to show basal ganglia (especially putaminal) changes in a Leigh-like pattern, correlating with the extrapyramidal movement disorder","Diagnosis combines the biochemical and MRI findings with SERAC1 sequencing; management is supportive, including hearing rehabilitation and movement-disorder care"],"pathophysiology":"MEGDEL syndrome results from biallelic SERAC1 mutations. SERAC1 localises to mitochondria-associated endoplasmic reticulum membranes and participates in phosphatidylglycerol remodelling, a step needed for mature cardiolipin synthesis, and in intracellular cholesterol trafficking. Disturbed phospholipid composition impairs mitochondrial membrane function and oxidative metabolism, producing the multisystem picture of neonatal hepatopathy and hypoglycaemia, sensorineural deafness, and a progressive Leigh-like encephalopathy with dystonia. The disrupted mitochondrial lipid metabolism underlies the characteristic and persistent 3-methylglutaconic aciduria.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of 3-methylglutaconic aciduria (Barth syndrome, TMEM70 defect, DNAJC19-related disease)","Leigh syndrome from other nuclear or mitochondrial DNA mutations","Neonatal mitochondrial hepatopathies","Dystonic cerebral palsy in a child with sensorineural hearing loss","Congenital or acquired causes of neonatal hypoglycaemia with liver dysfunction","Other progressive encephalopathies with movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine organic acids demonstrating persistent 3-methylglutaconic aciduria","SERAC1 gene sequencing to confirm the diagnosis","Brain MRI to document evolving basal ganglia (putaminal) and Leigh-like changes","Audiological assessment for sensorineural hearing loss","Liver function tests and glucose monitoring in the neonatal period","Blood and CSF lactate as markers of mitochondrial dysfunction"],"redFlags":["Neonatal hypoglycaemia with hepatic dysfunction requiring urgent stabilisation","Developmental regression with an emerging severe dystonic movement disorder","Progressive sensorineural deafness affecting communication and development","Acute neurological deterioration during intercurrent illness"],"prognosis":"MEGDEL syndrome is a progressive disorder; the neonatal hepatopathy and hypoglycaemia are often transient, but the neurological course leads to significant intellectual and motor disability with a disabling dystonia and deafness, and life expectancy is reduced. Care is supportive and multidisciplinary.","pitfalls":["Attributing transient neonatal liver disease and hypoglycaemia to sepsis or perinatal stress and missing the metabolic diagnosis","Not testing urine organic acids, thereby overlooking the diagnostic 3-methylglutaconic aciduria","Labelling the child as dystonic cerebral palsy without investigating a progressive mitochondrial disorder","Failing to identify and rehabilitate sensorineural hearing loss early"],"management":["Recognise that no disease-modifying therapy exists, so management is supportive across the multisystem SERAC1 disorder","Treat the disabling dystonia with movement-disorder agents such as trihexyphenidyl, baclofen or levodopa, with botulinum toxin for focal dystonia","Provide hearing rehabilitation with hearing aids or cochlear implantation for the sensorineural deafness","Recognise and correct neonatal hypoglycaemia and support the infant through the transient hepatopathy","Give nutritional and feeding support with attention to swallowing safety","Use antiseizure medication and neurological monitoring as the encephalopathy progresses","Offer genetic counselling for the autosomal recessive inheritance with family testing","Coordinate multidisciplinary metabolic, neurology, audiology and rehabilitation care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"meier_gorlin_syndrome":{"name":"Meier-Gorlin syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ear-patella-short stature syndrome","microtia-absent patellae-micrognathia syndrome","MGORS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A microcephalic primordial dwarfism defined by the triad of bilateral microtia, absent or hypoplastic patellae, and severe short stature","Growth restriction begins prenatally and continues postnatally, with microcephaly usually present but cognition often relatively preserved","Additional features include micrognathia, a small mouth, full lips, and mammary hypoplasia in affected females","Caused by biallelic variants in genes of the pre-replication complex that licenses DNA replication origins, including ORC1, ORC4, ORC6, CDT1, CDC6, and related genes","Inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern, although some genotypes produce a dominant form, so accurate molecular diagnosis refines counselling","Feeding difficulties and recurrent respiratory or airway problems may complicate infancy","Genital and pubertal abnormalities and, in some genotypes, a degree of learning difficulty may be present"],"pathophysiology":"Meier-Gorlin syndrome results from variants in components of the origin recognition complex and other pre-replication complex proteins that license origins of DNA replication before each cell division. Impaired replication licensing limits the proliferative capacity of cells during development, reducing overall growth and producing severe short stature, microcephaly, and the characteristic skeletal and ear anomalies. The tissue-specific hallmarks such as absent patellae reflect the sensitivity of particular developmental programmes to reduced cell proliferation.","additionalDifferentials":["Seckel syndrome and other microcephalic primordial dwarfisms","Microcephalic osteodysplastic primordial dwarfism (MOPD types)","Silver-Russell syndrome","Nager and other acrofacial dysostoses with microtia","3-M syndrome","CHARGE syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gene panel or exome sequencing covering ORC1, ORC4, ORC6, CDT1, CDC6, GMNN and related pre-replication complex genes","Chromosomal microarray to exclude copy-number causes","Radiographs of the knees to confirm absent or hypoplastic patellae and assess the skeleton","Audiological and ENT assessment for microtia and hearing","Growth and endocrine evaluation including growth axis assessment","Airway and feeding assessment in infancy"],"redFlags":["Airway obstruction or apnoea related to micrognathia and small airways","Severe faltering growth requiring nutritional intervention","Recurrent aspiration or respiratory compromise","Hearing loss impairing communication and development"],"prognosis":"Short stature is lifelong and remains marked despite endocrine input, but cognition is frequently within or near the normal range and most individuals do well with orthopaedic, ENT, and supportive care. Airway and feeding difficulties in infancy are the main early risks.","pitfalls":["Missing absent patellae because they are not radiologically visible until the knees ossify in later childhood","Confusing the disorder with Seckel syndrome and other primordial dwarfisms without molecular testing","Underestimating airway compromise from micrognathia in the newborn","Assuming intellectual disability when cognition is often relatively preserved"],"management":["Offer a trial of growth hormone therapy where indicated, recognising the response in this primordial dwarfism is often limited","Assess and support the airway and feeding in infancy, the main early risks","Manage microtia with ENT and reconstructive input and provide audiological rehabilitation for hearing loss","Arrange orthopaedic assessment and management of absent or hypoplastic patellae and other skeletal problems","Monitor development, noting that cognition is frequently near-normal","Provide genetic counselling reflecting the autosomal recessive inheritance and rarer dominant genotypes","Coordinate multidisciplinary paediatric, endocrine, ENT and orthopaedic care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Meier-Gorlin Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"meige_lymphoedema":{"name":"meige lymphoedema","system":"lymphatic and vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lymphoedema praecox","hereditary lymphoedema type II"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common form of primary lymphoedema, defined by onset around puberty and before the age of 35 years, hence the alternative term lymphoedema praecox.","It shows a clear female predominance and typically presents as bilateral, often asymmetrical, lower-limb swelling.","Inheritance is usually autosomal dominant with variable penetrance and expressivity, so an affected first-degree relative supports the diagnosis.","It is distinguished from Milroy disease, which is congenital, type I and classically linked to FLT4 (VEGFR3) mutations, whereas Meige disease presents later and is genetically heterogeneous.","By definition Meige disease is isolated lymphoedema; the presence of distichiasis points instead to FOXC2-related lymphoedema-distichiasis and warrants ophthalmic examination.","The Stemmer sign is positive and the swelling begins distally before ascending, becoming progressively less pitting over time.","As with all chronic lymphoedema, recurrent cellulitis is common and both worsens and is worsened by the underlying lymphatic failure.","Lymphoscintigraphy confirms impaired lymphatic transport and helps exclude a secondary cause when onset is unusually asymmetrical or abrupt."],"pathophysiology":"Meige lymphoedema arises from a constitutional abnormality of the peripheral lymphatic vessels, most often hypoplasia or aplasia of the collecting lymphatics, which becomes clinically manifest around puberty. The peripubertal timing suggests a hormonal trigger that unmasks marginal lymphatic reserve. Deficient lymphatic drainage causes accumulation of protein-rich interstitial fluid, and the resulting chronic inflammation drives adipose deposition and dermal fibrosis in the same self-perpetuating manner seen across primary lymphoedema. The condition is genetically heterogeneous, and it must be separated from defined single-gene entities such as FOXC2-related lymphoedema-distichiasis and GJC2-associated disease, which have distinct features and inheritance.","additionalDifferentials":["Milroy disease (congenital hereditary lymphoedema type I)","lymphoedema-distichiasis syndrome (FOXC2)","lymphoedema tarda with onset after 35 years","secondary lymphoedema from infection, malignancy or trauma","lipoedema","chronic venous insufficiency","systemic oedema from cardiac, renal or hepatic disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["lymphoscintigraphy to demonstrate impaired lymphatic transport","detailed family pedigree and targeted genetic testing","venous duplex ultrasonography to exclude venous disease","MR or CT lymphangiography for anatomical assessment","slit-lamp ophthalmic examination to exclude distichiasis","thyroid, renal, hepatic and cardiac evaluation to exclude systemic oedema"],"redFlags":["recurrent cellulitis with systemic sepsis","new violaceous or haemorrhagic cutaneous nodules suggesting angiosarcoma","rapidly progressive or newly asymmetrical swelling prompting exclusion of malignancy","syndromic features such as distichiasis, ptosis or yellow nails indicating an alternative diagnosis","skin ulceration or lymphorrhoea"],"prognosis":"Meige lymphoedema is a lifelong condition, but with compression, skin care, exercise and infection prevention most patients maintain good limb function and a favourable quality of life. The outlook worsens with recurrent cellulitis and advanced fibrosis, and the small long-term risk of angiosarcoma persists.","pitfalls":["labelling all pubertal-onset lymphoedema as isolated Meige disease without checking for distichiasis or other syndromic features","failing to take a family history and thereby missing the hereditary pattern","confusing early presentation with lipoedema","undertreating cellulitis and accelerating lymphatic damage","attributing swelling to venous disease and delaying appropriate lymphatic management"],"management":["Deliver complete decongestive therapy with multilayer bandaging and graduated compression garments as the mainstay","Maintain meticulous skin care and hygiene to preserve the skin barrier and reduce infection risk","Treat cellulitis promptly with antibiotics and offer antibiotic prophylaxis for recurrent episodes","Encourage exercise, limb elevation and weight optimisation to support lymphatic drainage","Provide manual lymphatic drainage and consider surgery such as lymphaticovenous anastomosis, vascularised lymph node transfer or debulking in selected refractory cases","Exclude systemic causes of oedema and defined single-gene entities such as FOXC2-related lymphoedema-distichiasis","Offer genetic counselling given the autosomal dominant inheritance with variable penetrance","Undertake long-term surveillance for advancing fibrosis and the rare long-term risk of angiosarcoma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Browse's Introduction to the Symptoms and Signs of Surgical Disease","International Society of Lymphology Consensus Document on the diagnosis and treatment of peripheral lymphoedema"],"crossLinks":[]},"meige_syndrome":{"name":"Meige syndrome","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cranial dystonia","blepharospasm-oromandibular dystonia","Brueghel syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / movement disorder literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A segmental cranial dystonia combining blepharospasm with oromandibular dystonia affecting the jaw, tongue and lower face","Blepharospasm causes involuntary forced eyelid closure that can be functionally blinding","Oromandibular dystonia produces involuntary jaw opening or closing, lip and tongue movements, and can impair speech and eating","It typically begins in mid to late adult life and is more common in women","Sensory tricks (geste antagoniste) such as touching the face may transiently relieve the spasms","Botulinum toxin injection into the affected muscles is the mainstay of treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Meige syndrome is a focal or segmental dystonia of the cranial muscles arising from dysfunction within basal ganglia motor circuits and their control of facial and jaw musculature. As in other dystonias, abnormal patterns of muscle co-contraction produce sustained or repetitive involuntary movements, and altered sensorimotor integration underlies both the dystonic postures and the benefit from sensory tricks. Most cases are idiopathic, though secondary cranial dystonia can follow certain drug exposures or brain lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated blepharospasm","Hemifacial spasm","Tardive dyskinesia or tardive dystonia","Functional facial movements","Facial tics","Temporomandibular joint disorder (for jaw symptoms)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the characteristic combination of signs","Medication history to identify drug-induced tardive syndromes","MRI of the brain if features suggest a secondary cause","Assessment of functional impact on vision, speech and eating","Evaluation for sensory tricks supporting a dystonic diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression or additional neurological signs suggesting secondary dystonia","History of dopamine-receptor-blocking drug use","Functional blindness from severe blepharospasm","Marked speech or swallowing impairment"],"prognosis":"The condition is usually chronic, but repeated botulinum toxin treatment provides substantial symptomatic relief for most patients and can preserve function.","pitfalls":["Mistaking bilateral cranial dystonia for unilateral hemifacial spasm","Overlooking a tardive syndrome from previous neuroleptic exposure","Underestimating the functional impact of severe blepharospasm","Failing to recognise sensory tricks that confirm dystonia"],"management":["Inject botulinum toxin into the affected periocular and oromandibular muscles as the mainstay of treatment","Use oral agents such as anticholinergics, benzodiazepines, baclofen or tetrabenazine for adjunctive or wider symptom control","Encourage sensory tricks (geste antagoniste) that transiently relieve the spasms","Review and withdraw causative drugs where a tardive or drug-induced cranial dystonia is identified","Consider deep brain stimulation of the globus pallidus for severe, refractory disease","Provide supportive measures for vision, speech and swallowing with multidisciplinary neurology, ophthalmology and speech therapy input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Movement Disorders textbook (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"meigs_syndrome":{"name":"Meigs syndrome","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Meigs' syndrome","Meigs-Salmon syndrome","ovarian fibroma with ascites and pleural effusion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology reference textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the triad of a benign solid ovarian tumour, ascites, and a pleural effusion, all of which resolve after the tumour is removed","The classic tumour is an ovarian fibroma, though thecomas, Brenner tumours, and granulosa cell tumours may produce the same picture","The pleural effusion is more often right-sided, and both effusions are transudative and benign","CA-125 may be raised, which together with ascites and an adnexal mass can closely mimic advanced ovarian malignancy","The term pseudo-Meigs syndrome is used when a similar picture accompanies other ovarian or pelvic tumours","Recognising the syndrome avoids the assumption of disseminated malignancy and the inappropriate management that would follow","Complete surgical removal of the tumour is curative, with resolution of the ascites and pleural effusion"],"pathophysiology":"A benign solid ovarian tumour, most commonly a fibroma, becomes associated with the accumulation of ascitic fluid and a pleural effusion. The fluid is thought to arise from transudation or leakage from the surface of the tumour or its lymphatics into the peritoneal cavity, from where it passes to the pleural space through diaphragmatic lymphatic channels and defects. Because the effusions are secondary to the presence of the tumour rather than to malignant spread, they resolve once it is excised.","additionalDifferentials":["Ovarian malignancy with malignant ascites and pleural effusions","Pseudo-Meigs syndrome from other pelvic tumours","Cirrhosis or heart failure causing ascites and effusion","Tuberculous peritonitis","Other causes of an exudative or transudative pleural effusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal and pelvic ultrasound demonstrating a solid ovarian mass","Computed tomography of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis to define the mass and effusions","Serum CA-125, interpreted cautiously given its poor specificity in this setting","Analysis of pleural and ascitic fluid to confirm a benign, non-malignant nature","Histological examination of the excised tumour for definitive diagnosis"],"redFlags":["A clinical and radiological picture indistinguishable from disseminated ovarian malignancy","A markedly raised CA-125 heightening concern for cancer","A large pleural effusion causing breathlessness or respiratory compromise","Any histological or cytological feature suggesting true malignancy"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; because the tumour is benign, complete surgical removal cures the condition and leads to full resolution of the ascites and pleural effusion.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the syndrome as advanced ovarian malignancy and instituting inappropriate treatment","Placing undue reliance on a raised CA-125 to infer malignancy","Failing to obtain fluid cytology to confirm the benign nature of the effusions","Not recognising that resolution of effusions after surgery supports the diagnosis"],"management":["Perform complete surgical excision of the benign ovarian tumour, which is curative and resolves the effusions","Confirm the benign diagnosis by histological examination of the excised tumour","Provide therapeutic drainage of symptomatic ascites or pleural effusion for relief while awaiting surgery","Interpret a raised CA-125 cautiously and use imaging to avoid mistaking the syndrome for disseminated ovarian malignancy","Analyse pleural and ascitic fluid to confirm its benign, transudative nature","Confirm resolution of the ascites and pleural effusion after tumour removal"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference textbook","Gynaecological oncology reference text"],"crossLinks":[]},"melas_syndrome":{"name":"MELAS syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mitochondrial encephalopathy, lactic acidosis and stroke-like episodes","m.3243A>G","MT-TL1","MELAS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / GeneReviews mitochondrial disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The hallmark is recurrent stroke-like episodes producing focal neurological deficits that characteristically do not respect classical vascular territories, often affecting the posterior (parieto-occipital) cortex","The great majority of cases carry the maternally inherited m.3243A>G point mutation in the MT-TL1 gene encoding the mitochondrial transfer RNA for leucine","Expression is governed by heteroplasmy and a tissue threshold effect, so the mutant load, clinical severity and age of onset vary widely within a maternal lineage","Elevated blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate, worsening with exertion, is typical, and MR spectroscopy often shows a lactate doublet within affected cortex","Common systemic features include short stature, sensorineural hearing loss, migraine-like headaches, seizures, diabetes and cardiomyopathy","Muscle biopsy classically shows ragged-red fibres on modified Gomori trichrome staining and strong succinate dehydrogenase but cytochrome c oxidase deficiency in a subset of fibres","Management is supportive, favouring aerobic activity as tolerated, avoidance of mitochondrial toxins, and generic use of cofactor and antioxidant supplements while avoiding agents that impair respiratory-chain function"],"pathophysiology":"MELAS results from a defect of oxidative phosphorylation, most often the m.3243A>G substitution in the mitochondrial transfer RNA(Leu) gene, which impairs mitochondrial protein synthesis and reduces the activity of respiratory-chain complexes. Because mitochondrial DNA is maternally transmitted and present in many copies per cell, a mixture of mutant and normal genomes (heteroplasmy) exists, and disease appears only once the mutant proportion exceeds a tissue-specific threshold. Energy-demanding tissues such as brain, muscle and the endocrine pancreas are preferentially affected. The stroke-like episodes are thought to reflect a combination of neuronal hyperexcitability, an angiopathy of small cerebral vessels with dysfunctional mitochondria in endothelium and smooth muscle, and a mismatch between metabolic demand and impaired energy supply rather than true thromboembolic occlusion.","additionalDifferentials":["Ischaemic arterial stroke or cerebral venous thrombosis","Other mitochondrial encephalomyopathies such as MERRF or Leigh syndrome","Viral or autoimmune encephalitis","Complex partial or focal status epilepticus with postictal deficit","Hemiplegic migraine","CADASIL and other hereditary small-vessel arteriopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Resting and post-exercise blood lactate and pyruvate, with cerebrospinal fluid lactate when indicated","Brain MRI showing cortical stroke-like lesions crossing vascular boundaries, plus MR spectroscopy for a lactate peak","Targeted molecular testing for m.3243A>G with heteroplasmy quantification, ideally in urine sediment or muscle rather than blood","Skeletal muscle biopsy for histochemistry (ragged-red fibres) and respiratory-chain enzyme assays","Echocardiography and ECG to screen for cardiomyopathy and conduction disease","Audiometry and fasting glucose to detect associated hearing loss and diabetes"],"redFlags":["Acute stroke-like episode with encephalopathy, seizures or status epilepticus requiring urgent neurological support","New or decompensating cardiomyopathy or cardiac conduction block","Rapidly rising lactic acidosis with metabolic decompensation","Exposure to mitochondrial toxins that may precipitate deterioration"],"prognosis":"MELAS is a progressive multisystem disorder; recurrent stroke-like episodes tend to accumulate neurological disability, dementia and, in many patients, a shortened lifespan, though the course is highly variable and depends on mutant load and organ involvement.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing stroke-like episodes as conventional ischaemic stroke and overlooking the underlying mitochondrial disease","Testing only blood, where the m.3243A>G mutation may be undetectable or at low level; urine or muscle sampling improves yield","Using agents that impair mitochondrial function, which can trigger decompensation","Attributing hearing loss, diabetes or short stature to unrelated causes rather than recognising a unifying maternal disorder"],"management":["Give L-arginine, intravenously during acute stroke-like episodes and orally for prophylaxis, to improve endothelial function and reduce episode severity","Provide mitochondrial supportive supplements such as coenzyme Q10 and a cofactor cocktail including riboflavin and L-carnitine","Avoid mitochondrial toxins, notably sodium valproate, metformin, aminoglycosides and prolonged propofol","Control seizures with antiseizure medication, avoiding valproate where possible","Manage systemic involvement including diabetes, cardiomyopathy with cardiac surveillance, and sensorineural hearing loss with hearing aids or cochlear implants","Provide nutritional support and avoid prolonged fasting and other metabolic stressors","Offer maternal-lineage genetic counselling reflecting mitochondrial inheritance and heteroplasmy","Coordinate multidisciplinary metabolic, neurology, cardiology and audiology care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: MELAS","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"melasma":{"name":"Melasma","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chloasma","mask of pregnancy","melasma faciei"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as symmetrical, poorly defined brown to grey-brown patches on sun-exposed facial skin, typically the cheeks, forehead, upper lip, and chin","It is far more common in women and in people with darker skin phototypes, and often follows pregnancy or hormonal exposure","Recognised patterns are centrofacial, malar, and mandibular; a similar eruption can also occur on the forearms","Wood's lamp examination can help gauge the depth of pigment, with epidermal pigment enhanced and dermal pigment less so, which influences treatment expectations","Ultraviolet and visible light, including screen and heat exposure, are major drivers, so broad-spectrum photoprotection with a tinted (iron-oxide-containing) product is the cornerstone of care","Combination topical lightening agents and gentle chemical peels can help, but aggressive procedures risk post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, especially in darker skin","Review and, where feasible, stop contributory hormonal exposures such as combined oral contraception","Set realistic expectations: recurrence is common and lifelong sun protection and maintenance are usually needed"],"pathophysiology":"Melasma reflects an acquired disorder of localised hyperpigmentation in which functionally hyperactive melanocytes deposit excess melanin in the epidermis, dermis, or both. Ultraviolet and visible light, hormonal influences (pregnancy, oestrogen), and a genetic predisposition upregulate melanogenic signalling, while an altered dermal environment with solar elastosis, increased vascularity, and a disrupted basement membrane allows pigment to drop into the dermis. This basement-membrane damage helps explain the chronic, relapsing nature and the resistance of dermal pigment to topical therapy.","additionalDifferentials":["Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation following acne or dermatitis","Solar lentigines and other photo-induced pigmentation","Drug-induced pigmentation","Ochronosis (which can be exogenous, from overuse of hydroquinone)","Riehl's melanosis / pigmented contact dermatitis","Naevus of Ota or acquired dermal melanocytosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Primarily a clinical diagnosis supported by dermoscopy","Wood's lamp examination to help assess pigment depth","Skin biopsy only in atypical or treatment-resistant cases","Review of hormonal and drug history as part of assessment"],"redFlags":["Asymmetric, irregular, or evolving pigmented lesions raising concern for melanoma rather than melasma","Rapidly darkening or nodular change within a pigmented area","Paradoxical darkening or blue-black discolouration suggesting exogenous ochronosis from prolonged skin-lightening use"],"prognosis":"Melasma is typically chronic and relapsing; pregnancy-related disease may fade after delivery, but many patients experience persistence or recurrence, and long-term photoprotection with maintenance therapy is usually required to sustain improvement.","pitfalls":["Neglecting visible-light protection and relying only on conventional UV sunscreen","Provoking post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation with overly aggressive peels or lasers in darker skin","Causing exogenous ochronosis through prolonged, unsupervised use of skin-lightening agents","Promising a permanent cure rather than emphasising control and maintenance"],"management":["Apply rigorous broad-spectrum photoprotection with a tinted iron-oxide-containing sunscreen shielding against both ultraviolet and visible light as the cornerstone","Prescribe topical lightening therapy with hydroquinone, or a triple combination of hydroquinone, a retinoid and a corticosteroid, with alternatives such as azelaic acid or cysteamine","Use oral tranexamic acid for resistant or extensive disease","Add gentle superficial chemical peels as an adjunct, avoiding aggressive procedures that risk post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation","Identify and remove hormonal triggers such as combined oral contraceptives where feasible","Use laser and energy devices cautiously, particularly in darker skin phototypes","Continue long-term maintenance therapy and sustained photoprotection given the chronic relapsing course"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"melkersson_rosenthal_syndrome":{"name":"Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MRS","orofacial granulomatosis with facial palsy","Miescher cheilitis (monosymptomatic form)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically a triad of recurrent orofacial oedema, recurrent (often alternating) peripheral facial nerve palsy, and a fissured (scrotal/plicated) tongue, though the complete triad is uncommon","Most patients present with only one or two features, and the lip swelling (granulomatous cheilitis of Miescher) is frequently the first and most persistent sign","Facial swelling is typically non-pitting, painless, and initially transient but tends to become firm and permanent with recurrent attacks","Lip biopsy may show non-caseating granulomas, but a normal histology does not exclude the diagnosis","The condition sits within the spectrum of orofacial granulomatosis and can overlap with Crohn disease and sarcoidosis, which should be considered particularly in younger patients","Facial palsy may precede, accompany, or follow the swelling and can be recurrent, mimicking repeated Bell palsy","Management is largely supportive and anti-inflammatory (intralesional or systemic corticosteroids); refractory persistent lip swelling may be reduced with reduction cheiloplasty"],"pathophysiology":"The syndrome is characterised by non-caseating granulomatous inflammation affecting the orofacial soft tissues, with lymphoedema and perivascular granulomas obstructing lymphatic drainage and producing recurrent, eventually persistent, oedema. The facial nerve is thought to be compromised by granulomatous or oedematous swelling within its bony canal, producing lower motor neurone palsy that may recur. The underlying trigger is unclear but is presumed to be an aberrant delayed-type immune response, possibly to dietary, dental, or microbial antigens, and the disorder overlaps mechanistically with other granulomatous conditions such as Crohn disease and sarcoidosis. The fissured tongue is regarded as a developmental/constitutional feature associated with the syndrome rather than a granulomatous lesion.","additionalDifferentials":["Angio-oedema (allergic, hereditary, or ACE-inhibitor related)","Orofacial granulomatosis secondary to Crohn disease","Sarcoidosis","Contact or allergic cheilitis","Cheilitis glandularis","Recurrent isolated Bell palsy","Cutaneous or oral lymphoma infiltration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Labial or oral mucosal biopsy seeking non-caseating granulomas","Faecal calprotectin, and gastrointestinal endoscopy where Crohn disease is suspected","Serum angiotensin-converting enzyme and chest imaging to screen for sarcoidosis","Patch testing and dietary/allergen assessment for contactants such as cinnamon and benzoates","C1 esterase inhibitor level and complement studies to exclude hereditary angio-oedema","MRI of the facial nerve if palsy is atypical or fails to recover"],"redFlags":["Systemic features (weight loss, chronic diarrhoea, respiratory symptoms) suggesting Crohn disease or sarcoidosis","Progressive or incomplete recovery of facial palsy raising concern for a compressive or neoplastic lesion","Rapidly evolving airway or tongue swelling suggesting angio-oedema rather than granulomatous oedema","Unilateral fixed mass rather than diffuse recurrent swelling"],"prognosis":"The course is chronic and relapsing; early oedema is transient but repeated attacks can lead to permanent disfiguring lip swelling. Facial palsy often recovers but may be incomplete after recurrent episodes. The syndrome itself is benign, though associated systemic granulomatous disease influences the overall outlook.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing recurrent orofacial oedema as allergic angio-oedema and treating only with antihistamines","Assuming a normal lip biopsy excludes the diagnosis, since granulomas are patchy","Overlooking underlying Crohn disease or sarcoidosis, especially in children and young adults","Treating recurrent facial palsy as simple Bell palsy without recognising the wider syndrome","Attributing the fissured tongue to poor hygiene rather than a syndromic feature"],"management":["Reduce the granulomatous orofacial swelling with corticosteroids given intralesionally or systemically","Use steroid-sparing immunomodulators such as clofazimine, methotrexate or a TNF-alpha inhibitor for persistent or refractory disease","Investigate for and treat overlapping Crohn disease and sarcoidosis within the orofacial granulomatosis spectrum","Undertake dietary exclusion and avoidance of implicated contactants such as cinnamon and benzoates identified on patch testing","Provide supportive facial-palsy care with eye protection, and consider reduction cheiloplasty or facial-nerve decompression for disfiguring persistent swelling","Coordinate multidisciplinary oral medicine, neurology, gastroenterology and dermatology input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"melnick_needles_syndrome":{"name":"Melnick-Needles syndrome (FLNA)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MNS","osteodysplasty","FLNA","Melnick-Needles osteodysplasty"],"chapter":null,"source":"Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most severe of the X-linked FLNA otopalatodigital-spectrum disorders, caused by gain-of-function variants in filamin A","Distinctive facial appearance includes exophthalmos, full cheeks, micrognathia and a high forehead","Radiographs show characteristic ribbon-like (wavy) ribs, S-shaped bowing of the long bones, and irregular cortices","Marked X-linked sex difference: heterozygous females typically survive with the characteristic phenotype, whereas hemizygous males are usually severely affected and the condition is frequently lethal in males","Skeletal features include a small thorax, sclerosis of the skull base, delayed cranial ossification and joint problems","Extraskeletal complications such as airway narrowing, urinary tract obstruction and cardiac anomalies can occur and contribute to morbidity","Management is supportive and multidisciplinary, with respiratory and airway monitoring, orthopaedic care and hearing assessment, alongside genetic counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Melnick-Needles syndrome results from gain-of-function variants in the X-linked FLNA gene encoding filamin A, a cytoskeletal actin-cross-linking protein integral to cell shape, migration and mechanical signalling in developing bone and connective tissue. Disordered filamin A activity yields grossly abnormal bone modelling, producing the wavy ribbon-like ribs, bowed long bones with irregular cortices and the characteristic facial gestalt. The strong sex bias reflects X-linked inheritance: heterozygous females survive with the full phenotype because of X-inactivation mosaicism, whereas hemizygous males, lacking a normal allele, generally have severe, often lethal disease. Involvement of the airway, urinary tract and heart underlies the extraskeletal complications that determine outcome.","additionalDifferentials":["Otopalatodigital syndrome (FLNA spectrum)","Frontometaphyseal dysplasia (FLNA spectrum)","Osteogenesis imperfecta with bowing and abnormal ribs","Serpentine fibula-polycystic kidney syndrome","Other skeletal dysplasias with wavy ribs and bowed long bones","Cleidocranial-type dysplasias with delayed cranial ossification"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey demonstrating ribbon-like ribs, bowed long bones and irregular cortices","Chest imaging and respiratory assessment given the small thorax and airway risk","Renal tract imaging to detect urinary obstruction","Cardiac evaluation for associated anomalies","FLNA molecular genetic testing with X-linked segregation analysis","Audiometry and cranial imaging where hearing or foraminal concerns arise"],"redFlags":["Respiratory compromise from a small thorax or airway narrowing, especially in neonates","Severe presentation in a male fetus or infant, which is often life-threatening","Urinary tract obstruction threatening renal function","Significant cardiac anomaly"],"prognosis":"Prognosis differs sharply by sex: affected females usually survive into adulthood with the characteristic phenotype and variable morbidity, whereas affected males typically have severe disease that is frequently lethal in the perinatal period.","pitfalls":["Failing to place the disorder within the FLNA otopalatodigital spectrum and appreciate its X-linked inheritance","Underestimating the perinatal severity and lethality in affected males","Overlooking extraskeletal airway, renal or cardiac complications","Attributing the bowed bones and abnormal ribs to osteogenesis imperfecta without genetic confirmation"],"management":["Recognise that management is supportive, directed at the skeletal and extraskeletal complications of this FLNA disorder","Assess and support the airway for narrowing and the small thorax, a key determinant of outcome in affected males","Image the renal tract and relieve urinary tract obstruction where present","Evaluate and manage associated cardiac anomalies","Provide orthopaedic care for long-bone bowing, joint problems and skeletal deformity","Undertake audiological and cranial assessment where hearing loss or foraminal narrowing arise","Offer genetic counselling for the X-linked inheritance, with severe often perinatally lethal disease in males and survival with the phenotype in females","Coordinate multidisciplinary clinical genetics, respiratory, orthopaedic and cardiology care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders","GeneReviews: FLNA-related otopalatodigital spectrum disorders"],"crossLinks":["otopalatodigital_syndrome","frontometaphyseal_dysplasia"]},"melorheostosis":{"name":"Melorheostosis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Leri disease","flowing hyperostosis","candle wax bone disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism (ASBMR)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, usually sporadic sclerosing dysplasia in which dense cortical hyperostosis appears to flow along the length of a bone","The radiographic hallmark is irregular, wavy cortical thickening likened to dripping candle wax down one side of a long bone","Lesions frequently follow a sclerotomal distribution, tending to affect one limb along the territory of a spinal sensory nerve","Presents with limb pain, stiffness, restricted joint movement, and sometimes limb-length discrepancy or deformity, often beginning in adulthood","Overlying soft-tissue changes such as fibrosis, contractures, oedema, and skin thickening can accompany the bony disease","Somatic mosaic mutations, notably in MAP2K1 and related pathway genes, have been implicated in the endosteal and soft-tissue components","Management is symptomatic, addressing pain and function, with orthopaedic surgery reserved for disabling deformity or contracture"],"pathophysiology":"Melorheostosis is characterised by localised, progressive hyperostosis affecting the cortex of long bones and adjacent soft tissues. It is generally sporadic and non-hereditary, and recent work has identified somatic mosaic mutations, including activating changes in MAP2K1 within the MAP-kinase pathway, in affected lesional tissue, with other genetic changes reported in the endosteal component. Enhanced local signalling drives excess bone formation confined to affected regions, often distributed along a sclerotome, producing the characteristic asymmetrical flowing cortical thickening. Associated fibrosis and contracture of surrounding soft tissue contribute to the pain and functional limitation.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteoma and parosteal osteosarcoma","Myositis ossificans","Osteopoikilosis and osteopathia striata (mixed sclerosing bone dystrophy)","Chronic osteomyelitis with sclerosis","Paget disease of bone","Reflex or post-traumatic periosteal reaction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs demonstrating the characteristic flowing candle-wax cortical hyperostosis","CT to define the extent and cortical involvement","MRI to assess soft-tissue fibrosis, contracture, and marrow","Bone scintigraphy showing increased uptake in active lesions","Assessment of joint range, limb length, and function","Biopsy or genetic analysis of lesional tissue in atypical cases to exclude malignancy"],"redFlags":["New rapidly enlarging mass or pain raising concern for a surface osteosarcoma","Progressive contracture or neurovascular compromise of a limb","Significant limb-length discrepancy or deformity in a growing child","Severe intractable pain limiting function"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and slowly progressive with a normal lifespan, but it can cause chronic pain, joint contracture, and functional impairment; outcomes depend on lesion location and the degree of soft-tissue involvement.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the flowing hyperostosis for an aggressive surface bone tumour and vice versa","Assuming the condition is purely osseous and overlooking disabling soft-tissue fibrosis and contracture","Expecting a hereditary pattern when the disorder is typically sporadic and mosaic","Undertaking extensive surgery that may be complicated by recurrence and poor soft-tissue healing"],"management":["Control the chronic bone and soft-tissue pain with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and other analgesics","Maintain joint range and limit contracture with physiotherapy and stretching","Consider bisphosphonates in selected patients for pain associated with active hyperostosis","Undertake surgery such as contracture release, deformity correction or excision of excess bone for functional limitation and limb-length discrepancy","Consider MEK-pathway-directed therapy as an emerging targeted approach given the somatic MAP2K1 mutations in lesional tissue","Biopsy or genetically analyse lesional tissue in atypical cases to exclude malignancy","Monitor limb length, deformity and function over the long term"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism (ASBMR)","Peer-reviewed reviews of melorheostosis and its somatic mosaicism"],"crossLinks":[]},"membranoproliferative_glomerulonephritis":{"name":"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","system":"Renal / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPGN","mesangiocapillary glomerulonephritis","membranoproliferative GN","C3 glomerulopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Brenner and Rector's The Kidney","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["MPGN is a histological pattern of mesangial and endocapillary hypercellularity with capillary wall remodelling, not a single disease","Light microscopy shows a lobular glomerular architecture with double-contour ('tram-track') basement membranes from subendothelial deposits and mesangial interposition","Modern classification is based on immunofluorescence: immune-complex/immunoglobulin-mediated MPGN versus complement-mediated C3 glomerulopathy (C3 glomerulonephritis and dense deposit disease)","Immunoglobulin-positive patterns point to infection, autoimmune disease or a monoclonal gammopathy, so a paraprotein screen is essential in adults","C3-dominant staining with scanty immunoglobulin indicates dysregulation of the alternative complement pathway, sometimes with C3 nephritic factor or complement gene abnormalities","Clinical presentation spans nephrotic syndrome, nephritic features with haematuria, or a mixed picture, frequently with persistently low serum complement","Treatment targets the underlying cause: eradicate infection, treat autoimmune disease or the paraprotein-producing clone, and consider complement-directed therapy in refractory C3 glomerulopathy"],"pathophysiology":"The MPGN pattern results from deposition of immune complexes or complement components in the mesangium and subendothelial space, which activates complement and recruits inflammatory cells. Mesangial cells proliferate and interpose their cytoplasm between the endothelium and the glomerular basement membrane while laying down new basement membrane material, producing the characteristic double-contour appearance. In immune-complex disease the trigger is chronic antigenaemia from infection, autoimmunity or a monoclonal immunoglobulin; in C3 glomerulopathy uncontrolled activation of the alternative complement pathway, from acquired autoantibodies (such as C3 nephritic factor) or inherited regulator defects, drives predominant C3 deposition, with dense osmiophilic transformation of the basement membrane in dense deposit disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Postinfectious glomerulonephritis","Lupus nephritis","Cryoglobulinaemic glomerulonephritis","Monoclonal gammopathy of renal significance","Thrombotic microangiopathy with double-contour changes","Diabetic nodular glomerulosclerosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal biopsy with immunofluorescence and electron microscopy to separate immune-complex from complement-mediated disease","Serum C3 and C4 with an alternative-pathway complement assessment","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis and serum free light chains to detect a paraprotein","Autoimmune serology including antinuclear and anti-DNA antibodies and cryoglobulins","Complement functional assays, C3 nephritic factor and complement regulator/gene testing in C3 glomerulopathy","Screening for chronic infection where an immune-complex pattern is found"],"redFlags":["Crescentic transformation with rapidly progressive renal impairment","Nephrotic syndrome with thromboembolic complications","Persistently low complement with systemic features suggesting active autoimmune or cryoglobulinaemic disease","Detection of a monoclonal protein indicating an underlying haematological clone"],"prognosis":"The MPGN pattern is often chronic and progressive, with a substantial proportion reaching end-stage kidney disease, though outcome is strongly influenced by the underlying cause and its treatment. Dense deposit disease and crescentic forms carry a poorer prognosis, and both immune-complex and complement-mediated disease can recur after transplantation.","pitfalls":["Treating the pattern as a single entity instead of pursuing the immune-complex versus complement cause","Failing to screen adults for a monoclonal gammopathy driving the disease","Overlooking chronic infection as a reversible trigger","Assuming normal complement excludes the diagnosis","Giving broad immunosuppression in complement-mediated disease without addressing the pathway dysregulation"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying cause, whether chronic infection, autoimmune disease or a monoclonal gammopathy in immune-complex disease","Use renin-angiotensin system blockade with an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker to reduce proteinuria and control blood pressure","Give immunosuppression such as corticosteroids with mycophenolate or a calcineurin inhibitor for idiopathic immune-complex disease with heavy proteinuria or declining function","Consider complement inhibition with a terminal complement C5 inhibitor such as eculizumab for progressive C3 glomerulopathy","Direct clone-targeted therapy where a monoclonal immunoglobulin is driving the disease","Provide general nephroprotection with lipid control, oedema management and dietary sodium restriction","Consider transplantation with counselling on the risk of disease recurrence in the graft"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Brenner and Rector's The Kidney"],"crossLinks":[]},"membranoproliferative_gn":{"name":"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPGN","mesangiocapillary glomerulonephritis","membranoproliferative GN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A histological pattern rather than a single disease, defined by mesangial proliferation, thickened capillary walls, and a characteristic double-contour ('tram-track') appearance of the basement membrane","Modern classification is based on immunofluorescence, dividing it into immunoglobulin/immune-complex-mediated disease and complement-mediated C3 glomerulopathy","Immune-complex MPGN is driven by chronic antigenaemia, such as hepatitis C (often with cryoglobulins), chronic infections, autoimmune disease, or monoclonal gammopathy","Complement-mediated forms reflect dysregulation of the alternative pathway and overlap with C3 glomerulopathy","Presentations range from asymptomatic urinary abnormalities to nephrotic syndrome, nephritic features, or a mixed nephritic-nephrotic picture, often with low serum complement","Because it is a pattern of injury, the essential step is a thorough search for the underlying cause","Treatment is directed at the underlying driver, for example antiviral therapy for hepatitis C or addressing a monoclonal clone, alongside supportive renal care","Prognosis and specific therapy differ substantially between the immune-complex and complement-mediated subtypes"],"pathophysiology":"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis describes a pattern of glomerular injury in which deposits accumulate in the mesangium and subendothelial space, prompting mesangial cell proliferation and interposition into the capillary wall. This remodelling produces basement membrane duplication (double contours) and capillary wall thickening. The deposits may consist of immune complexes formed against persistent antigens (chronic infection, autoimmune disease, or monoclonal immunoglobulin) in immune-complex MPGN, or of complement components in complement-mediated disease due to alternative pathway dysregulation. Both routes activate complement and inflammatory cascades, resulting in proteinuria, haematuria, and progressive nephron loss.","additionalDifferentials":["C3 glomerulopathy (as a related complement-mediated entity)","Cryoglobulinaemic glomerulonephritis (often hepatitis C associated)","Lupus nephritis","Monoclonal gammopathy of renal significance","Chronic thrombotic microangiopathy (which can mimic the pattern)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum complement (C3, C4) to help separate immune-complex from complement-mediated disease","Hepatitis B and C serology and cryoglobulin testing","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis with free light chains","Autoimmune serology (e.g. ANA) where clinically indicated","Kidney biopsy with immunofluorescence and electron microscopy to subclassify"],"redFlags":["Nephrotic-range proteinuria with falling kidney function","Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis with crescents","Systemic features of cryoglobulinaemia or vasculitis","Findings suggesting an occult malignancy or monoclonal process"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on the underlying cause and subtype; treating a reversible driver such as hepatitis C can improve outcomes, whereas complement-mediated and idiopathic forms often progress to kidney failure over years.","pitfalls":["Treating the biopsy pattern without identifying and addressing the underlying cause","Failing to test for hepatitis C, cryoglobulins, or a monoclonal gammopathy","Not using immunofluorescence to distinguish immune-complex from C3-dominant disease","Assuming a uniform prognosis across all MPGN subtypes"],"management":["Search thoroughly for and treat the underlying cause, since this is a pattern of injury rather than a single disease","Give direct-acting antiviral therapy for hepatitis C, including where cryoglobulinaemia is present","Use renin-angiotensin system blockade to reduce proteinuria and control blood pressure","Provide immunosuppression for idiopathic or autoimmune immune-complex disease with significant proteinuria or renal impairment","Consider complement inhibition with a C5 inhibitor for progressive complement-mediated C3 glomerulopathy","Deliver supportive nephroprotective care with oedema, lipid and blood-pressure management","Maintain nephrology follow-up, monitoring for progression to kidney failure and counselling on recurrence risk at transplantation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"men1":{"name":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1","system":"Oncology / Genetic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MEN1","Wermer syndrome","multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / NCCN & genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by germline variants in the MEN1 tumour suppressor gene (encoding menin), inherited autosomal dominantly with high penetrance","Classically characterised by tumours of the parathyroid glands, the anterior pituitary, and the pancreatic islets and duodenum (the three Ps)","Primary hyperparathyroidism from multigland parathyroid hyperplasia is usually the earliest and most common manifestation, causing hypercalcaemia","Duodenopancreatic neuroendocrine tumours include gastrinomas (causing Zollinger-Ellison syndrome), insulinomas, and often multiple non-functioning tumours, and are a major cause of mortality","Pituitary tumours, most frequently prolactinomas, may cause hormonal excess or mass effects","Additional features include foregut carcinoids (thymic, bronchial, gastric), adrenocortical tumours, lipomas, angiofibromas, and collagenomas","Biochemical and imaging surveillance from childhood or early adulthood allows early detection of hormone excess and tumour growth","Because parathyroid disease is multiglandular, surgical strategy differs from sporadic single-adenoma disease and is planned in specialist centres"],"pathophysiology":"MEN1 encodes menin, a nuclear tumour suppressor involved in transcriptional regulation, chromatin modification, and control of cell proliferation. A germline loss-of-function variant combined with somatic loss of the remaining allele in susceptible endocrine tissues removes this growth restraint, leading to hyperplasia and tumour formation in the parathyroids, pituitary, and pancreatic/duodenal neuroendocrine cells. The resulting autonomous hormone secretion produces the characteristic endocrine syndromes, while tumour bulk and malignant potential drive later morbidity.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2","MEN4 (CDKN1B-related) syndrome","Sporadic primary hyperparathyroidism or single endocrine tumour","Familial isolated hyperparathyroidism and hyperparathyroidism-jaw tumour syndrome","Von Hippel-Lindau disease (pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Germline MEN1 sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis with genetic counselling","Biochemical surveillance including calcium and parathyroid hormone, and pituitary and gut hormone panels (e.g. prolactin, fasting gastrin, glucose/insulin)","Cross-sectional or functional imaging (MRI/CT and somatostatin receptor imaging) for pancreatic, duodenal, and thoracic neuroendocrine tumours","Pituitary MRI for suspected adenomas","Localisation studies before parathyroid or pancreatic surgery"],"redFlags":["Severe or symptomatic hypercalcaemia from primary hyperparathyroidism","Refractory peptic ulceration or diarrhoea suggesting a gastrinoma","Recurrent hypoglycaemia indicating an insulinoma","Enlarging or metastasising duodenopancreatic or thymic neuroendocrine tumour"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is reduced mainly by malignant duodenopancreatic and thymic neuroendocrine tumours, whereas hyperparathyroidism and prolactinomas are generally manageable. Early diagnosis through family screening and structured surveillance improves outcomes by enabling timely, planned intervention.","pitfalls":["Managing MEN1 hyperparathyroidism as sporadic single-gland disease and underestimating the risk of persistent or recurrent hypercalcaemia","Overlooking the diagnosis in a young patient presenting with an apparently isolated endocrine tumour","Failing to institute lifelong multi-organ surveillance and cascade testing of relatives","Neglecting thymic and bronchial carcinoids, which can be aggressive and easily missed"],"management":["Undertake subtotal or total parathyroidectomy for the multigland primary hyperparathyroidism causing hypercalcaemia","Control gastric acid hypersecretion in gastrinoma-associated Zollinger-Ellison syndrome with proton pump inhibitors","Resect functioning and larger duodenopancreatic neuroendocrine tumours surgically, using somatostatin analogues for hormone control and tumour stabilisation","Treat prolactinomas with dopamine agonists such as cabergoline, reserving trans-sphenoidal surgery for other or resistant pituitary tumours","Maintain lifelong biochemical and cross-sectional and functional imaging surveillance of the parathyroid, pituitary and duodenopancreatic and thoracic tumours","Provide genetic counselling with germline MEN1 testing and structured screening of at-risk relatives","Coordinate multidisciplinary endocrine, surgical and oncological care with early planned intervention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","NCCN guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"men2":{"name":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2","system":"Oncology / Genetic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MEN2","MEN2A","MEN2B","Sipple syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / NCCN & genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by activating germline variants in the RET proto-oncogene, inherited autosomal dominantly, and subclassified into MEN2A, MEN2B, and familial medullary thyroid carcinoma","Medullary thyroid carcinoma is the near-universal and often first manifestation across all subtypes and is the principal cause of death","Phaeochromocytoma occurs in a substantial proportion of MEN2A and MEN2B and must be excluded biochemically before any surgery, including thyroidectomy, and during pregnancy","MEN2A additionally features primary hyperparathyroidism and may be associated with cutaneous lichen amyloidosis or Hirschsprung disease","MEN2B is the most aggressive subtype, with early medullary thyroid carcinoma, mucosal neuromas, intestinal ganglioneuromatosis, and a marfanoid habitus, but without hyperparathyroidism","The specific RET variant correlates with medullary thyroid carcinoma risk and thus guides the recommended timing of prophylactic thyroidectomy in gene carriers","Prophylactic total thyroidectomy in early childhood can prevent or cure medullary thyroid carcinoma when performed before malignant spread","Calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen serve as tumour markers for medullary thyroid carcinoma diagnosis and follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"RET encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase that regulates growth and differentiation of neural crest-derived cells. Germline gain-of-function variants cause constitutive, ligand-independent kinase activation, driving proliferation of thyroid C cells (medullary thyroid carcinoma), adrenal medullary cells (phaeochromocytoma), and parathyroid tissue. The particular codon affected determines the strength of oncogenic activation and hence the aggressiveness of medullary thyroid carcinoma, forming the basis of genotype-directed surgical timing.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1","Hereditary phaeochromocytoma-paraganglioma syndromes (SDHx, VHL, NF1)","Sporadic medullary thyroid carcinoma","Familial isolated hyperparathyroidism","Marfan syndrome (for the marfanoid habitus of MEN2B)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Germline RET analysis with genetic counselling, including subtype-defining codon identification","Serum calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen for medullary thyroid carcinoma","Plasma or urinary metanephrines and adrenal imaging to detect phaeochromocytoma","Serum calcium and parathyroid hormone for hyperparathyroidism (MEN2A)","Neck ultrasound and appropriate staging imaging for medullary thyroid carcinoma"],"redFlags":["Unrecognised phaeochromocytoma provoking a hypertensive crisis during surgery, anaesthesia, or pregnancy","Rising calcitonin or carcinoembryonic antigen indicating progressive or metastatic medullary thyroid carcinoma","Delay in prophylactic thyroidectomy in a high-risk RET carrier","Features of MEN2B in a child (mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus) mandating urgent evaluation"],"prognosis":"Outcome is dominated by medullary thyroid carcinoma; when prophylactic thyroidectomy is performed early, cure is achievable, whereas late diagnosis with nodal or distant spread carries a worse prognosis. Phaeochromocytoma is usually benign but life-threatening if unrecognised, and hyperparathyroidism is generally manageable.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to thyroidectomy or other surgery without first excluding phaeochromocytoma","Delaying prophylactic thyroidectomy beyond the window indicated by the RET genotype","Missing MEN2B in a child by attributing mucosal neuromas or bowel symptoms to other causes","Treating medullary thyroid carcinoma with strategies designed for differentiated thyroid cancer, which do not apply"],"management":["Perform prophylactic total thyroidectomy in RET carriers, with timing directed by the specific codon's medullary thyroid carcinoma risk","Exclude and treat phaeochromocytoma biochemically before any surgery or pregnancy, using alpha-adrenergic then beta-adrenergic blockade followed by adrenalectomy, adrenal-sparing where feasible","Undertake parathyroidectomy for the primary hyperparathyroidism seen in MEN2A","Use tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as vandetanib, cabozantinib or a selective RET inhibitor like selpercatinib for advanced or metastatic medullary thyroid carcinoma","Monitor with serum calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen, neck ultrasound and staging imaging for medullary thyroid carcinoma","Provide genetic counselling with germline RET testing and cascade screening of relatives to enable early prophylactic surgery","Coordinate multidisciplinary endocrine, surgical and oncology care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","NCCN guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"mendelian_susceptibility_mycobacterial_disease":{"name":"Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease (IFN-gamma axis)","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MSMD","familial disseminated atypical mycobacterial infection","IL12RB1 deficiency","IFNGR1 deficiency","IFNGR2 deficiency","STAT1 deficiency","IL12B deficiency","ISG15 deficiency","SPPL2A deficiency","IRF8 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of inherited disorders in which defects along the interleukin-12/interleukin-23 to interferon-gamma circuit cripple the immune response to intracellular pathogens, leaving affected individuals uniquely vulnerable to weakly virulent mycobacteria","The hallmark is disseminated or recurrent infection with the BCG vaccine strain, non-tuberculous (environmental) mycobacteria, and non-typhoidal Salmonella, often in an otherwise healthy-looking child","Disease severity tracks the underlying genetic lesion: complete receptor defects present in infancy with overwhelming, poorly granuloma-forming disease, whereas partial defects present later with more contained, granulomatous illness","Severe reactions to BCG immunisation (BCGitis or disseminated BCGosis) in a previously well infant should trigger evaluation for this pathway","Susceptibility to intramacrophage organisms including certain fungi (histoplasma, coccidioides), and in some genotypes an added vulnerability to severe viral infection, may accompany the mycobacterial phenotype","Diagnosis rests on functional testing of the interferon-gamma axis together with molecular genetic confirmation, as several distinct genes converge on the same clinical picture","Management combines prolonged, tailored antimicrobial therapy against the offending organism with adjunctive interferon-gamma in receptor-competent forms, and consideration of haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for the most severe complete defects"],"pathophysiology":"The control of intracellular pathogens such as mycobacteria and Salmonella depends on a self-reinforcing loop between phagocytes and lymphocytes. Infected macrophages and dendritic cells release interleukin-12 and interleukin-23, which act on natural killer and T cells to drive production of interferon-gamma; that interferon-gamma in turn binds its receptor on the phagocyte, signals through the JAK-STAT1 pathway, and switches on the microbicidal machinery needed to kill the organism. Inherited defects at any node of this circuit (the interferon-gamma receptor chains, STAT1, the interleukin-12/23 subunit or its receptor, and several regulatory or transcription-factor genes) interrupt the loop, so phagocytes fail to be fully activated and organisms of low intrinsic virulence persist and spread. The depth of the defect determines whether granulomas form poorly and disease disseminates or whether infection remains comparatively localised.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic granulomatous disease and other phagocyte oxidative-burst defects","Severe combined immunodeficiency and other combined T-cell defects","GATA2 deficiency with mononuclear cytopenia and mycobacterial susceptibility","Anti-interferon-gamma autoantibody syndrome (acquired adult mycobacterial susceptibility)","HIV-associated disseminated mycobacterial disease","Idiopathic CD4 lymphopenia","Tuberculosis or leprosy in an immunocompetent host"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Functional assays of the interferon-gamma axis, including whole-blood interferon-gamma and interleukin-12 production after stimulation","Flow cytometry for interferon-gamma receptor and interleukin-12 receptor beta-1 surface expression and STAT1 phosphorylation","Targeted or panel-based genetic sequencing of the known MSMD genes","Mycobacterial and Salmonella cultures with species identification and drug-susceptibility testing","Testing for anti-interferon-gamma neutralising autoantibodies to exclude the acquired phenocopy","Assessment for HIV and other secondary immunodeficiency","Imaging to define the extent of disseminated infection and organ involvement"],"redFlags":["Disseminated illness following BCG vaccination in an infant","Recurrent or multifocal non-tuberculous mycobacterial or non-typhoidal Salmonella infection","A family history of childhood death from disseminated mycobacterial disease","Failure of poorly formed granulomas with progressive organ infiltration in complete defects","Concurrent severe viral or intracellular fungal infection suggesting a broader signalling defect"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on genotype: partial defects and interleukin-12/23-pathway lesions often do well with prolonged antimicrobial therapy and adjunctive interferon-gamma, whereas complete interferon-gamma receptor defects carry a poor prognosis with recurrent life-threatening infection unless haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is undertaken, itself difficult in the face of active infection.","pitfalls":["Attributing severe BCG or environmental mycobacterial disease to poor technique or contamination rather than an underlying host defect","Stopping antimicrobial therapy too early, as relapse is common while the immune defect persists","Giving live BCG or other live vaccines before the diagnosis is excluded in a suggestive family","Assuming a normal routine immunology screen excludes the disorder, since antibody and lymphocyte counts are typically normal","Overlooking the acquired anti-interferon-gamma autoantibody syndrome as a treatable adult mimic"],"management":["Prolonged, species-directed combination antimycobacterial therapy guided by culture and drug-susceptibility testing, continued until immune reconstitution or lifelong in severe defects","Adjunctive recombinant interferon-gamma in interleukin-12/23-axis and partial receptor defects, recognising it is futile in complete interferon-gamma receptor deficiency or STAT1 loss-of-function","Strict avoidance of BCG and other live vaccines, with prompt treatment of any BCG-related disease","Antimicrobial and antifungal cover for accompanying non-typhoidal Salmonella and intramacrophage fungal infection","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as potentially curative for complete interferon-gamma receptor deficiency","Ongoing surveillance for recurrent or disseminated infection and for toxicity of prolonged antimicrobials","Coordinated clinical immunology, infectious disease and specialist microbiology input","Genetic counselling and testing of at-risk relatives, with exclusion of the acquired anti-interferon-gamma autoantibody phenocopy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"mendelian_susceptibility_to_mycobacterial_disease":{"name":"Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease","system":"Immunology / Inborn errors of immunity","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MSMD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Selective vulnerability to weakly virulent mycobacteria (the BCG vaccine strain and environmental non-tuberculous mycobacteria) and to non-typhoidal Salmonella in a child who otherwise appears immunologically well.","An umbrella term for genetically heterogeneous defects along the IL-12/IL-23-interferon-gamma circuit that couples mononuclear phagocytes with T and natural killer cells.","Disseminated BCG disease following neonatal vaccination is a classic sentinel presentation.","The specific genotype matters: complete interferon-gamma receptor defects behave very differently from IL-12/IL-23 axis defects in severity and treatment response.","Recombinant interferon-gamma helps in IL-12/IL-23 axis and partial receptor defects but is futile in complete interferon-gamma receptor deficiency or STAT1 loss-of-function.","Granuloma quality is informative: poorly formed, multibacillary, lepromatous-like lesions suggest severe interferon-gamma signalling failure.","Consider the diagnosis in any child with recurrent or disseminated environmental mycobacterial or salmonella infection, even without a family history.","Live BCG and other live vaccines are contraindicated once the diagnosis is suspected."],"pathophysiology":"MSMD arises from inborn errors that disrupt the interleukin-12/interleukin-23-dependent production of interferon-gamma and the interferon-gamma-mediated activation of mononuclear phagocytes. Myeloid cells secrete IL-12 and IL-23, which act through receptors sharing the IL-12 receptor beta-1 chain on T and natural killer cells to drive interferon-gamma release; interferon-gamma then signals through the IFNGR1/IFNGR2 receptor and STAT1 to license macrophages to kill intracellular organisms. Autosomal recessive or dominant defects in IFNGR1, IFNGR2, IL12B, IL12RB1, IL12RB2, IL23R, STAT1, IRF8, ISG15, TYK2, JAK1, RORC, TBX21, SPPL2A and the macrophage-specific respiratory-burst component CYBB each interrupt this circuit at a different point, producing a shared phenotype of impaired containment of poorly virulent mycobacteria and Salmonella. The amount of residual pathway function determines whether disease is mild and localised or severe and disseminated.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe combined and other combined immunodeficiencies","Chronic granulomatous disease","HIV infection and other causes of acquired CD4 lymphopenia","Anti-interferon-gamma autoantibody syndrome (an acquired adult phenocopy)","GATA2 deficiency with disseminated non-tuberculous mycobacterial disease","Disseminated tuberculosis in an apparently immunocompetent host","NEMO/IKK-gamma deficiency and other syndromic immunodeficiencies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Whole-exome or targeted inborn-errors-of-immunity gene panel sequencing","Flow cytometry for interferon-gamma receptor and IL-12 receptor beta-1 surface expression","Functional whole-blood assays of interferon-gamma production after BCG/IL-12 stimulation and of STAT1 phosphorylation","Plasma interferon-gamma and IL-12 concentrations, whose pattern helps localise the defect","Mycobacterial culture with species identification and molecular typing to distinguish BCG from environmental strains","Screening for anti-interferon-gamma autoantibodies in older patients to exclude the acquired phenocopy","Histology of affected tissue for granuloma morphology and bacillary load"],"redFlags":["Disseminated or persistent infection following live BCG vaccination","Recurrent non-tuberculous mycobacterial or invasive non-typhoidal Salmonella disease in a child","Poorly formed, multibacillary granulomata on biopsy","Failure of appropriately treated mycobacterial infection to respond to standard antimicrobials","Family history of childhood death from disseminated mycobacterial or salmonella infection"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on the specific genetic lesion and residual pathway function: IL-12/IL-23 axis and partial receptor defects often do well with prolonged antimicrobials and adjunctive interferon-gamma, whereas complete interferon-gamma receptor deficiency carries high mortality and may require haematopoietic stem cell transplantation.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal basic immune screen (immunoglobulins, lymphocyte subsets, neutrophil oxidative burst) excludes the diagnosis, since these are typically normal.","Giving recombinant interferon-gamma to a child with complete receptor deficiency, in whom it cannot work.","Labelling BCG-derived disease as tuberculosis without species typing.","Dismissing the diagnosis because there is no family history, as many cases are sporadic or arise from consanguineous but unaffected parents.","Administering further live vaccines before immunological evaluation."],"management":["Prolonged combination antimycobacterial therapy directed by species identification and susceptibility testing, with duration extended in poorly controlled disease","Adjunctive recombinant interferon-gamma for interleukin-12/interleukin-23-axis and partial receptor defects, avoided as ineffective in complete interferon-gamma receptor deficiency and STAT1 loss-of-function","Avoidance of live vaccines including BCG, with active treatment of disseminated BCG disease","Antimicrobial cover for non-typhoidal Salmonella and antifungal therapy for susceptible intramacrophage fungi when they arise","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for complete interferon-gamma receptor deficiency, where mortality is otherwise high","Regular monitoring for relapse, new intracellular infection and cumulative drug toxicity","Genetic counselling and cascade testing of relatives given the Mendelian inheritance","Multidisciplinary care through clinical immunology and infectious disease services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Union of Immunological Societies (IUIS) inborn errors of immunity classification","Clinical immunology reference texts","Paediatric infectious diseases reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"menetrier_disease":{"name":"Menetrier disease (hyperplastic gastropathy)","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hyperplastic gastropathy","giant hypertrophic gastritis","protein-losing hypertrophic gastropathy","Menetrier's disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by giant, cerebriform rugal folds in the gastric body and fundus that typically spare the antrum","Foveolar (mucous cell) hyperplasia with cystic dilatation of glands and loss of parietal cells produces a thickened, boggy mucosa and excess mucus secretion","A protein-losing gastropathy causes hypoalbuminaemia, which may present with peripheral oedema and anasarca","Acid output is characteristically reduced (hypochlorhydria or achlorhydria) because of parietal cell loss","Overexpression of transforming growth factor-alpha (TGF-alpha) with excessive epidermal growth factor receptor signalling is implicated in the adult form","In children the disorder is frequently self-limiting and associated with cytomegalovirus infection","Management is largely supportive, with a high-protein diet and protein replacement; anti-epidermal growth factor receptor antibody therapy has been used in refractory adult disease and gastrectomy is reserved for severe or premalignant cases","The adult form carries an increased long-term risk of gastric adenocarcinoma, so surveillance is prudent"],"pathophysiology":"Menetrier disease is a rare hyperplastic gastropathy in which marked expansion of surface mucous (foveolar) cells with cystic gland dilatation and progressive loss of acid-secreting parietal cells thickens the gastric body and fundus into giant folds. Excessive epidermal growth factor receptor activation, driven by overexpression of transforming growth factor-alpha, is thought to underlie the mucous cell hyperplasia and to suppress acid secretion in the adult form, whereas the paediatric form is often triggered by cytomegalovirus. The abnormal mucosa is leaky, allowing plasma proteins to escape into the gastric lumen, which produces a protein-losing gastropathy with hypoalbuminaemia and dependent oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Zollinger-Ellison syndrome with hypertrophic gastric folds","Gastric lymphoma including mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma","Infiltrative gastric adenocarcinoma (linitis plastica)","Hypertrophic lymphocytic gastritis","Gastric involvement by sarcoidosis or eosinophilic gastritis","Polyposis syndromes affecting the stomach"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy demonstrating enlarged folds, with deep (well or jumbo) biopsies to sample the full mucosal thickness","Serum albumin and total protein to document the protein-losing state","Cross-sectional imaging to assess fold thickness and help exclude malignancy","Gastric acid studies typically showing hypochlorhydria","Testing for cytomegalovirus, particularly in children","Stool alpha-1 antitrypsin clearance to confirm gastrointestinal protein loss","Fasting serum gastrin to exclude a gastrinoma"],"redFlags":["Progressive weight loss, anaemia, or a focal mass raising concern for underlying malignancy","Severe hypoalbuminaemia with anasarca and effusions","Biopsy or imaging features overlapping with infiltrative carcinoma or lymphoma","Persistent bleeding or obstructive symptoms"],"prognosis":"The adult form is usually chronic and may be complicated by disabling protein loss and an elevated risk of gastric adenocarcinoma, whereas the childhood form associated with cytomegalovirus is typically self-limiting and resolves over weeks to months.","pitfalls":["Taking superficial biopsies that miss the diagnosis because the characteristic changes lie deep within the mucosa","Attributing hypoalbuminaemia to hepatic or renal disease and overlooking a protein-losing gastropathy","Failing to distinguish giant folds from malignant infiltration or a gastrinoma","Neglecting surveillance for gastric cancer in long-standing adult disease"],"management":["Targeted anti-epidermal growth factor receptor therapy with the monoclonal antibody cetuximab in symptomatic adult disease to reduce foveolar hyperplasia and protein loss","High-protein diet with intravenous albumin replacement as needed to counter the protein-losing gastropathy and dependent oedema","Symptomatic acid suppression, anticholinergics or octreotide to lessen mucus and protein losses","Eradication of Helicobacter pylori where detected, and antiviral consideration for cytomegalovirus-associated childhood disease, which is often self-limiting","Endoscopic surveillance with deep biopsies for the recognised risk of gastric adenocarcinoma","Total or partial gastrectomy reserved for intractable protein loss, uncontrollable symptoms or malignant transformation","Gastroenterology-led multidisciplinary follow-up with monitoring of serum albumin and nutritional status"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Yamada's Textbook of Gastroenterology"],"crossLinks":[]},"menieres_disease":{"name":"Meniere's disease","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Meniere disease","endolymphatic hydrops","Meniere's syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by a triad of episodic vertigo, fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus, often with a sense of aural fullness","Vertigo attacks typically last from minutes to hours, distinguishing them from the seconds-long spells of positional vertigo","Hearing loss initially affects lower frequencies and fluctuates, but tends to become permanent and progressive over time","Usually begins unilaterally, though the second ear can become involved in a proportion of patients","The underlying disorder is endolymphatic hydrops, an increase in endolymph volume within the membranous labyrinth","Management principles include dietary salt restriction, diuretics and vestibular sedatives for acute attacks, with lifestyle measures to reduce triggers","Escalating options for intractable disease range from intratympanic therapy to surgical procedures such as endolymphatic sac decompression or labyrinthine ablation","Sudden drop attacks (Tumarkin crises) can cause falls without warning and pose an injury risk"],"pathophysiology":"Meniere's disease is attributed to endolymphatic hydrops, an abnormal accumulation of endolymph that distends the membranous labyrinth. Impaired production, circulation or absorption of endolymph, potentially involving the endolymphatic sac, raises pressure within the scala media. Episodic rupture or dysfunction of the membranes allows mixing of endolymph and perilymph, disturbing the ionic environment of the sensory hair cells and producing acute vertigo, fluctuating hearing loss and tinnitus. Repeated insults cause progressive damage to cochlear and vestibular structures, leading to permanent hearing loss and vestibular hypofunction.","additionalDifferentials":["Vestibular migraine","Vestibular neuritis or labyrinthitis","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Acoustic neuroma (vestibular schwannoma)","Autoimmune inner ear disease","Perilymph fistula"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure tone audiometry to document fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural loss","Tests of vestibular function such as caloric testing and video head impulse testing","Electrocochleography and other specialised tests where available","MRI of the internal auditory meatus to exclude a vestibular schwannoma","Blood tests to exclude alternative causes when clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Progressive unilateral hearing loss with tinnitus raising concern for vestibular schwannoma","Neurological signs suggesting a central cause","Sudden severe sensorineural hearing loss requiring urgent assessment","Recurrent drop attacks causing injury"],"prognosis":"The course is variable and unpredictable, with clustered attacks and remissions; vertigo often diminishes over years as vestibular function declines, but hearing loss tends to be progressive and permanent.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing Meniere's without excluding a retrocochlear lesion such as acoustic neuroma","Confusing it with vestibular migraine, which can present similarly","Overusing vestibular sedatives beyond acute attacks and impairing compensation","Underestimating the psychosocial and safety impact of unpredictable attacks"],"management":["Dietary salt restriction with reduction of caffeine and alcohol as first-line lifestyle measures to limit endolymphatic hydrops","Maintenance diuretic therapy and betahistine to reduce attack frequency","Vestibular sedatives and antiemetics such as prochlorperazine or antihistamines for acute vertiginous attacks","Intratympanic corticosteroid, or ablative intratympanic gentamicin for disabling refractory unilateral disease","Vestibular rehabilitation and hearing aids to manage residual vestibular hypofunction and progressive sensorineural loss","Surgical options such as endolymphatic sac decompression, vestibular neurectomy or labyrinthectomy reserved for intractable vertigo","Audiological monitoring and safety counselling regarding sudden attacks, with ENT specialist follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"meningioma":{"name":"Meningioma","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["meningeal tumour","dural-based tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Meningiomas are the most common primary intracranial tumours and are usually slow-growing and benign, arising from the meningeal covering of the brain and spinal cord","Many are asymptomatic and discovered incidentally on imaging performed for other reasons","When symptomatic, features depend on location and result from gradual compression, producing focal deficits, seizures, or symptoms of raised intracranial pressure","They are more common in women and their growth can be influenced by hormonal factors; some carry hormone receptors","On imaging they are typically well-defined, extra-axial, dural-based, avidly and homogeneously enhancing masses, often with a dural tail and sometimes adjacent bony change","Small asymptomatic meningiomas are frequently managed by surveillance imaging, reserving intervention for growth or symptoms","Symptomatic or enlarging tumours are usually treated by surgical resection, with radiotherapy or radiosurgery for residual, recurrent, higher-grade, or surgically inaccessible lesions","Although most are low grade, a minority are atypical or malignant with a greater tendency to recur, so histological grading guides follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"Meningiomas arise from meningothelial (arachnoid cap) cells of the meninges and are therefore extra-axial, growing outside the brain parenchyma and compressing it from the surface. They are typically slow-growing and attached to the dura, and their growth can distort adjacent brain, cranial nerves, and vessels, provoke surrounding oedema, and erode or hyperostose overlying bone. Symptoms arise from mass effect at the specific location rather than infiltration. Most are histologically low grade, but atypical and anaplastic variants show more aggressive behaviour and higher recurrence rates. Prior cranial irradiation and certain genetic conditions increase risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Dural metastasis","Solitary fibrous tumour / haemangiopericytoma","Schwannoma (at skull base sites)","Dural-based lymphoma or granulomatous disease","Extra-axial abscess or empyema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI brain or spine with contrast to characterise the extra-axial dural-based mass and dural tail","CT to assess associated hyperostosis, calcification, or bony erosion","Serial imaging to monitor growth of incidental or conservatively managed lesions","Histopathology and grading after biopsy or resection","Vascular imaging when preoperative embolisation of a highly vascular tumour is considered"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth or atypical imaging features suggesting a higher-grade tumour","Progressive neurological deficit, new seizures, or cranial nerve compromise","Signs of raised intracranial pressure from a large lesion with oedema and mass effect","Acute deterioration from peritumoural oedema or, rarely, haemorrhage"],"prognosis":"Most meningiomas are benign and, when completely resected, have an excellent prognosis with low recurrence; incompletely removed, atypical, or malignant tumours recur more often and may need adjuvant radiotherapy and closer surveillance.","pitfalls":["Assuming every dural-based enhancing mass is a benign meningioma without considering metastasis or other lesions","Over-treating small, stable, asymptomatic incidental tumours","Underestimating the recurrence risk of atypical or malignant subtypes","Overlooking peritumoural oedema as a treatable cause of symptoms"],"management":["Active surveillance with serial contrast MRI for small, asymptomatic, incidentally found tumours","Maximal safe surgical resection as the mainstay for symptomatic or enlarging lesions, aiming for complete removal of tumour and dural attachment","Stereotactic radiosurgery or fractionated radiotherapy for residual, recurrent, atypical or anaplastic tumours and surgically inaccessible sites","Corticosteroids to control symptomatic peritumoural oedema and antiseizure medication for tumour-related seizures","Preoperative embolisation of highly vascular tumours to reduce operative haemorrhage","Histological grading to guide the need for adjuvant radiotherapy and the intensity of follow-up","Neuro-oncology multidisciplinary management with long-term surveillance imaging for recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"meningococcal_disease":{"name":"Meningococcal Disease","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["meningococcemia","Neisseria meningitidis infection"],"chapter":160,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1244-1253","clinicalPearls":["Neisseria meningitidis most commonly causes asymptomatic nasopharyngeal colonization; invasive disease is rare and usually presents as either bacterial meningitis or meningococcal septicemia (p.1244).","A nonblanching petechial or purpuric rash develops in over 80% of cases, but it is often absent early in the illness and may begin as a blanching maculopapular or urticarial eruption indistinguishable from common viral rashes before becoming petechial/purpuric over hours; some patients, including those with overwhelming sepsis, have no rash at all (p.1248).","In the most severe cases large purpuric lesions develop (purpura fulminans) with signs of peripheral ischemia (p.1248).","Roughly 30-50% present with a meningitis syndrome alone, up to 40% of meningitis patients also have features of septicemia, and meningococcal septicemia alone accounts for up to 20% of cases (p.1248).","In fulminant disease death may occur within hours of the first symptoms; early septicemic symptoms are nonspecific and influenza-like (fever, headache, myalgia, vomiting, abdominal pain) (p.1248).","In children, hypotension is a late sign; they more commonly present with compensated shock (tachycardia, poor peripheral perfusion, normal blood pressure) (p.1248).","Terminal complement component (C5-9), properdin or factor D deficiency, and treatment with complement inhibitors such as eculizumab, increase the risk of meningococcal disease (up to ~600-fold) and may cause recurrent attacks (p.1247).","Chronic meningococcemia is rarely recognized and presents as recurrent episodes of petechial rash with fever, joint pain/arthritis, and splenomegaly, with bacteremia that characteristically clears without treatment then recurs (p.1248-1249).","Tobacco smoking (and passive smoke exposure) is an important risk factor that damages the nasopharyngeal epithelium and increases both carriage and invasive disease (p.1247).","Immunization is regarded as the only rational approach to prevention at a population level; chemoprophylaxis of close contacts aims to eradicate carriage of the invasive strain (p.1250-1253)."],"pathophysiology":"N. meningitidis colonizes the human nasopharynx, then in a susceptible individual may invade the mucosa into the bloodstream, usually within a few days of acquisition. The polysaccharide capsule is a key virulence factor providing resistance to phagocytosis; acapsular strains rarely cause invasive disease. During growth meningococci shed outer-membrane blebs containing lipopolysaccharide (LPS, endotoxin), which binds CD14/TLR4 to trigger an inflammatory cascade (TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6, IL-8, IL-10, PAI-1 and others). Disease severity relates to both endotoxin levels and the magnitude of the inflammatory response. Endothelial injury is central to meningococcemia, causing increased vascular permeability (capillary leak with hypovolemia, tissue and pulmonary edema), altered vascular tone, loss of thromboresistance, intravascular coagulation, and myocardial dysfunction. Procoagulant activation with tissue factor upregulation, downregulation of anticoagulant pathways (loss of thrombomodulin/protein C receptors; reduced antithrombin III, protein C, protein S, tissue factor pathway inhibitor) and impaired thrombolysis from high PAI-1 produce purpura fulminans and skin/limb infarction. Shock results from hypovolemia plus myocardial depression (hypoxia, metabolic derangements such as hypocalcemia, cytokines). Bacteria reaching the meninges provoke a local cytokine-driven inflammatory response that can cause cerebral edema and raised intracranial pressure (p.1247-1248).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of bacterial meningitis (presentation of meningococcal meningitis is indistinguishable unless an associated petechial/purpuric rash or gram-negative diplococci are present) (p.1248-1249)","Differential of chronic meningococcemia: bacterial endocarditis, acute rheumatic fever, Henoch-Schonlein purpura, infectious mononucleosis, disseminated gonococcal infection, immune-mediated vasculitis (p.1248-1249)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral infections (enteroviruses, influenza and other respiratory viruses, measles, Epstein-Barr virus, cytomegalovirus, parvovirus) - most patients presenting with a petechial/purpuric rash have a viral infection (p.1248)","Pneumococcal, streptococcal, staphylococcal, or other gram-negative bacterial sepsis (p.1248)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Protein C or S deficiency (including post-varicella protein S deficiency) (p.1248)","Platelet disorders (e.g., idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura, drug effects, bone marrow infiltration) (p.1248)","Henoch-Schonlein purpura, connective tissue disorders, and trauma (including nonaccidental injury in children) (p.1248)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood culture for confirmation and public health investigation (positive in up to ~75% of cases); avoid media containing sodium polyanethol sulfonate, which may inhibit meningococcal growth, and avoid delay in transport (p.1249)","Real-time PCR of whole blood increases diagnostic yield by more than 40% and may remain positive for several days after antibiotics (p.1249)","Lumbar puncture for CSF in suspected meningitis (unless contraindicated); CSF Gram stain is up to ~80% sensitive, CSF culture sensitivity ~90%, plus PCR where available; CSF latex agglutination antigen testing is insensitive and should be replaced by molecular diagnosis (p.1249)","Inflammatory markers (WBC count, C-reactive protein, procalcitonin, ESR) may support the diagnosis but can be normal or low in rapidly progressive disease and do not exclude it (p.1249)","Severe septicemia laboratory findings: hypoglycemia, acidosis, hypokalemia, hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia, hypophosphatemia, anemia, coagulopathy (p.1249)","Measure total hemolytic complement activity to assess for complement deficiency in patients with recurrent disease, particularly non-B capsular groups (p.1247)"],"redFlags":["A nonblanching (petechial or purpuric) rash in a febrile patient should be treated as a medical emergency suggestive of meningococcal disease (p.1248)","Purpura fulminans / large purpuric lesions with peripheral ischemia (p.1248)","Signs of shock: tachycardia, poor peripheral perfusion, tachypnea, oliguria, cold mottled extremities, limb pain (hypotension is a late sign in children) (p.1248)","Reduced level of consciousness, agitation, or confusion (decreased cerebral perfusion or raised intracranial pressure) (p.1248)","Features of raised intracranial pressure: reduced consciousness, relative bradycardia and hypertension, focal neurologic signs, abnormal posturing, unequal/dilated/poorly reactive pupils (p.1248)","Rapid progression - death may occur within hours of first symptoms in fulminant cases (p.1248)"],"prognosis":"About 10% of patients with meningococcal disease die despite antimicrobial therapy and intensive care. Fulminant meningococcemia is more likely to cause death or ischemic skin loss than meningitis alone. Factors associated with poorer prognosis include shock; extremes of age (infancy and old age) and adolescence; coma; purpura fulminans; disseminated intravascular coagulation; thrombocytopenia; leukopenia; absence of meningitis; metabolic acidosis; low antithrombin and protein S/C levels; high PAI-1; and a low ESR or C-reactive protein. The Glasgow Meningococcal Septicaemia Prognostic Score (GMSPS) performs well for severity assessment, though scoring systems do not direct specific interventions; the management priority remains recognition and urgent treatment of compromised airway, breathing, or circulation. Most patients improve rapidly with appropriate antibiotics and supportive care. Complications include scarring after purpuric skin necrosis (~10%, sometimes requiring skin grafting), amputation in 1-2% of survivors after peripheral ischemia or compartment syndrome, hearing loss (~5%), neurologic complications (~7%), chronic pain, and psychological/psychosocial sequelae (p.1249-1250).","pitfalls":["A normal or low WBC count and inflammatory markers do not exclude meningococcal disease and may occur in rapidly progressive disease (p.1249)","The rash may be absent early or absent altogether (including in overwhelming sepsis), and may initially appear blanching/viral before becoming petechial or purpuric (p.1248)","Most patients with a petechial/purpuric rash and fever do NOT have meningococcal disease - fewer than 10% of children (and in some settings under 1%) with this presentation are found to have it, so most have a viral cause (p.1248)","Lumbar puncture should generally be avoided in meningococcal septicemia because positioning may critically compromise circulation in hypovolemic shock; it is also contraindicated with raised intracranial pressure, uncorrected shock, coagulopathy, thrombocytopenia, respiratory insufficiency, local infection, or ongoing convulsions (p.1249)","A normal CT brain scan does not exclude raised intracranial pressure in meningococcal meningitis (p.1249)","CSF latex agglutination antigen testing and urinary antigen testing are insensitive; throat-swab identification has limited diagnostic value because N. meningitidis is normal nasopharyngeal flora (p.1249)","Postmeningococcal reactive (immune-complex) disease arising ~4-10 days after onset (rash, arthritis, iritis, pericarditis/polyserositis with fever) can be mistaken for relapse and lead to unnecessarily prolonged antibiotics; it resolves spontaneously (p.1249)","Aggressive fluid resuscitation with unbuffered electrolyte solutions was found to increase mortality in febrile African children (p.1249-1250)"],"management":["Immediate empirical parenteral third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone or cefotaxime, with pre-hospital benzylpenicillin, without delaying for lumbar puncture","Aggressive fluid resuscitation with vasoactive and inotropic support and intensive care admission for septic shock","Correction of coagulopathy and metabolic derangements such as hypoglycaemia, acidosis and electrolyte disturbance","Airway protection and ventilatory support for capillary leak, pulmonary oedema and respiratory failure","Surgical and wound care for purpura fulminans and peripheral ischaemic necrosis","Urgent chemoprophylaxis of close contacts with rifampicin, ciprofloxacin or ceftriaxone, and meningococcal vaccination for prevention","Statutory public health notification and outbreak investigation","Complement activity screening in recurrent disease with referral to immunology"],"severityClassification":"Clinical spectrum spans asymptomatic nasopharyngeal carriage, occult bacteremia, localized/focal infection (pneumonia, arthritis, osteomyelitis, pericarditis, conjunctivitis), meningitis, and meningococcal septicemia, with chronic meningococcemia as a relapsing form. The Glasgow Meningococcal Septicaemia Prognostic Score (GMSPS) is used for severity assessment. Fulminant meningococcemia with purpura fulminans and Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome (overwhelming meningococcal sepsis) represents the most severe end of the spectrum (p.1244, p.1248-1249).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1244","Harrison 22e p.1247","Harrison 22e p.1248","Harrison 22e p.1249","Harrison 22e p.1250","Harrison 22e p.1253"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","SEPSIS","SEPTIC_SHOCK"]},"meniscal_injury":{"name":"Meniscal injury","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["meniscal tear","torn meniscus","cartilage tear of the knee","bucket-handle tear"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["In younger patients tears usually follow a twisting injury on a loaded, flexed knee; in older patients they are often degenerative and may occur with minimal trauma","Typical symptoms are joint-line pain, an effusion developing over a day or so, and mechanical features such as clicking, catching, or locking","A knee locked in flexion that cannot be fully extended suggests a displaced bucket-handle tear and warrants prompt treatment","Joint-line tenderness and provocative rotation tests (e.g. McMurray's) support the diagnosis, and MRI is the investigation of choice","The peripheral (outer) third of the meniscus is vascular and can heal, favouring repair, whereas the avascular inner portion has poor healing potential","Repair is preferred where feasible, especially in younger patients with peripheral tears, to preserve meniscal function; partial meniscectomy is reserved for irreparable tears","Meniscus preservation is important because meniscectomy increases contact stress and the long-term risk of osteoarthritis","Degenerative tears in older knees with early arthritis often respond as well to conservative management and rehabilitation as to arthroscopic surgery"],"pathophysiology":"The menisci are fibrocartilaginous, wedge-shaped structures that deepen the tibial articular surface, distribute load, absorb shock, and contribute to joint stability and lubrication. A twisting force on a weight-bearing flexed knee shears the meniscus between the femoral and tibial condyles, producing longitudinal, radial, horizontal, or complex tears; a longitudinal tear whose free fragment displaces into the notch forms a bucket-handle lesion that can block extension. With age the meniscal tissue degenerates and tears more readily under low loads. Only the peripheral third has a blood supply capable of healing, so the location of a tear largely determines whether repair or excision is appropriate. Loss of meniscal tissue raises articular contact pressures and predisposes to degenerative change.","additionalDifferentials":["Anterior cruciate ligament tear","Collateral ligament injury","Osteochondral injury or loose body","Patellofemoral pain or patellar instability","Osteoarthritis of the knee","Plica syndrome","Osteochondritis dissecans"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the knee to characterise the tear and assess associated ligament and cartilage injury","Clinical joint-line palpation and provocative tests (e.g. McMurray's, Apley's grind)","Plain radiographs to assess for degenerative change or loose bodies and to exclude fracture","Diagnostic arthroscopy in selected cases where imaging is equivocal and intervention is planned"],"redFlags":["A truly locked knee unable to fully extend, suggesting a displaced bucket-handle tear needing prompt surgery","Acute tense haemarthrosis suggesting a significant associated injury such as an ACL rupture","Hot, swollen, systemically unwell knee raising concern for septic arthritis","Signs of associated ligamentous instability or fracture"],"prognosis":"Peripheral tears repaired successfully can restore durable meniscal function, while small stable tears may settle with conservative care; extensive meniscectomy relieves mechanical symptoms but increases the long-term likelihood of osteoarthritis, and degenerative tears in already arthritic knees have a more limited response to surgery.","pitfalls":["Performing meniscectomy on a degenerative tear in an arthritic knee that would do as well with rehabilitation","Removing repairable peripheral tissue rather than attempting repair, accelerating arthritis","Missing a locked knee from a displaced bucket-handle tear","Overlooking a concurrent ACL injury that leaves the meniscal repair at risk of re-tear"],"management":["Conservative management with activity modification, analgesia and physiotherapy including quadriceps strengthening for small, stable and degenerate tears","Arthroscopic meniscal repair for peripheral vascular-zone tears, especially in younger patients, to preserve meniscal function","Partial meniscectomy limited to irreparable tears in the avascular inner zone","Prompt arthroscopy for a locked, displaced bucket-handle tear blocking extension","Aspiration of a tense effusion and treatment of any associated ligament or chondral injury","Structured rehabilitation with graduated return to loading and weight optimisation","Long-term follow-up for degenerative change, given the raised osteoarthritis risk after meniscal loss"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"meniscal_tear":{"name":"Meniscal tear","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["torn meniscus","bucket-handle meniscal tear","degenerate meniscal tear"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Review of Orthopaedics / Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Acute traumatic tears follow a twisting injury on a loaded flexed knee, often in younger athletes","Degenerate tears occur in older knees, frequently with osteoarthritis, and may have minimal trauma","Symptoms include joint-line pain, effusion, catching or locking, and a sense of giving way","True locking (a fixed block to extension) suggests a displaced bucket-handle fragment","Joint-line tenderness with positive McMurray or Thessaly tests supports the diagnosis","Only the peripheral (red) zone has a blood supply, so peripheral tears can be repaired whereas central tears are usually debrided","In degenerate tears with osteoarthritis, exercise therapy is generally preferred to arthroscopic meniscectomy"],"pathophysiology":"The menisci are fibrocartilaginous shock absorbers that distribute load and stabilise the knee. Shear and compressive forces on a rotating flexed knee, or cumulative degeneration, tear the collagen architecture in vertical, horizontal, radial, flap or bucket-handle patterns. Because only the outer third is vascularised, healing potential depends on tear location; loss of meniscal function increases contact stress and accelerates cartilage degeneration.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteoarthritis of the knee","Ligamentous injury (ACL, MCL)","Osteochondral lesion or loose body","Patellofemoral pain syndrome","Plica syndrome","Discoid meniscus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Weight-bearing radiographs to assess osteoarthritis and alignment","MRI of the knee as the primary imaging test","Diagnostic arthroscopy where imaging is equivocal and mechanical symptoms persist","Aspiration if a tense effusion or haemarthrosis needs characterisation"],"redFlags":["A truly locked knee that cannot fully extend","Tense haemarthrosis suggesting associated ligament or osteochondral injury","Signs of septic arthritis (hot swollen knee with fever) mimicking a mechanical effusion"],"prognosis":"Peripheral repairable tears in stable knees heal well; degenerate tears often improve with rehabilitation, and outcomes after meniscectomy are limited by the long-term risk of accelerated osteoarthritis, especially with larger resections.","pitfalls":["Recommending arthroscopic meniscectomy for degenerate tears with established osteoarthritis","Missing an associated ACL rupture in an acute haemarthrosis","Interpreting incidental MRI meniscal signal as the pain source in older patients","Overlooking a reducible bucket-handle tear as the cause of a locked knee"],"management":["Initial conservative care with analgesia, activity modification and physiotherapy-led rehabilitation, particularly for degenerate tears in osteoarthritic knees","Arthroscopic repair of peripheral red-zone tears in stable knees to retain load-distributing function","Arthroscopic partial meniscectomy or debridement for symptomatic, irreparable central tears","Urgent surgical intervention for a true locked knee from a displaced bucket-handle fragment","Aspiration to characterise a tense effusion or haemarthrosis when required","Progressive strengthening and neuromuscular rehabilitation with staged return to sport","Sports medicine and orthopaedic follow-up, counselling on long-term osteoarthritis risk after larger resections"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Review of Orthopaedics","Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"menke_hennekam_syndrome":{"name":"Menke-Hennekam syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MKHK","Menke-Hennekam syndrome 1 (CREBBP)","CREBBP exon 30-31-related syndrome","atypical CREBBP-related disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nature Reviews chromatinopathy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by missense variants clustered in exons 30 and 31 of CREBBP, the gene encoding the histone acetyltransferase CBP, and is regarded as distinct from classic Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome despite involving the same gene","Unlike Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome, it lacks the hallmark broad and often angulated thumbs and great toes","Typical features include short stature, microcephaly, intellectual disability and a characteristic face with short palpebral fissures, telecanthus, a depressed nasal bridge and a short nose","Recurrent upper airway and ear infections and hearing loss are frequently reported","Feeding difficulties, autistic features and behavioural problems are common","The equivalent phenotype arising from variants in the corresponding region of EP300 is designated Menke-Hennekam syndrome type 2","Because the variants affect a specific domain of the acetyltransferase, the disorder illustrates how domain-specific changes in one chromatin modifier can produce a phenotype separate from the classic syndrome"],"pathophysiology":"CREBBP encodes CBP, a histone acetyltransferase and transcriptional co-activator that acetylates histones and numerous non-histone proteins, opening chromatin and coordinating context-dependent gene expression. Whereas most Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome variants cause loss of function across the protein, the missense changes clustered in exons 30 and 31 alter a specific region and are thought to disturb the protein's function in a distinct, possibly localised manner rather than simply reducing its dose. This domain-specific perturbation of acetylation-dependent transcriptional control produces a recognisable pattern of growth restriction, microcephaly, intellectual disability and characteristic facial features that differs from the classic Rubinstein-Taybi phenotype, and an analogous mechanism applies to the EP300-related form.","additionalDifferentials":["Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome (classic CREBBP or EP300 variants)","Menke-Hennekam syndrome type 2 (EP300)","Cornelia de Lange syndrome","Kabuki syndrome (KMT2D, KDM6A)","Coffin-Siris syndrome (BAF/SWI-SNF genes)","Other syndromic intellectual disability with microcephaly and short stature","Wiedemann-Steiner syndrome (KMT2A)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or genome sequencing with careful attention to the exon 30-31 region of CREBBP (and EP300)","DNA methylation episignature analysis where available to help classify variants","Audiological assessment for hearing loss","Evaluation of recurrent infections and airway problems","Neurodevelopmental and behavioural assessment including autism screening","Growth monitoring and evaluation of short stature and microcephaly","Ophthalmological review"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or severe respiratory and ear infections requiring assessment","Feeding difficulty with faltering growth in infancy","Unrecognised hearing loss impairing language development","Developmental regression prompting reassessment"],"prognosis":"The disorder is generally compatible with survival into adulthood; intellectual disability is usually present and variable in degree, and outcome is aided by treating hearing loss and recurrent infections and by sustained developmental and educational support.","pitfalls":["Assuming any CREBBP variant means Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome and thereby misjudging prognosis and management","Discounting the diagnosis because the broad thumbs of Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome are absent","Overlooking treatable hearing loss and recurrent infections","Failing to consider the EP300-related type 2 form in a similar clinical picture"],"management":["Symptom-directed care as no disease-specific therapy exists, coordinated through a multidisciplinary team","Audiological management of hearing loss with hearing aids and ENT review","Prompt treatment and prevention of recurrent upper airway and ear infections","Nutritional and feeding support for feeding difficulties with paediatric dietetic input","Early developmental intervention with speech, occupational and physiotherapy plus tailored educational support for intellectual disability","Behavioural support and autism-focused strategies for behavioural difficulties","Growth monitoring with evaluation of short stature and microcephaly","Genetic counselling for the family with confirmation of the CREBBP or EP300 variant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews — CREBBP/EP300-related disorders and Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome","Nature Reviews — histone acetyltransferases and domain-specific chromatinopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"menkes_disease":{"name":"Menkes disease","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ATP7A deficiency","kinky hair disease","trichopoliodystrophy","Menkes kinky hair syndrome","copper transport ATPase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked recessive disorder caused by pathogenic variants in ATP7A, encoding a copper-transporting P-type ATPase, so affected infants are almost always male","The defect impairs intestinal copper absorption and delivery of copper to cuproenzymes, producing low serum copper and low caeruloplasmin","Presents in early infancy with progressive neurodegeneration, hypotonia, seizures and failure to thrive after an initially unremarkable neonatal period","The characteristic sparse, depigmented, brittle hair shows pili torti (twisted shafts) on microscopy, giving the 'kinky hair' appearance","Connective tissue and vascular abnormalities arise from deficient lysyl oxidase activity, causing tortuous cerebral and systemic vessels, bladder diverticula and joint laxity","Parenteral copper-histidine may help if started very early, but neurological outcome remains poor once degeneration is established","Occipital horn syndrome is an allelic, milder ATP7A phenotype dominated by connective tissue features","Neonatal screening pitfalls exist because serum copper and caeruloplasmin are physiologically low in healthy newborns"],"pathophysiology":"Menkes disease results from loss of function of the copper-transporting ATPase ATP7A, which normally moves copper across the enterocyte basolateral membrane and delivers it within cells to copper-dependent enzymes. The defect traps dietary copper in intestinal and renal cells while depriving other tissues, so despite total-body copper accumulation in some sites, functional copper deficiency dominates. Reduced activity of cuproenzymes explains the phenotype: deficient lysyl oxidase impairs collagen and elastin cross-linking (vascular tortuosity, connective tissue laxity), deficient dopamine beta-hydroxylase disturbs catecholamine metabolism, and reduced cytochrome c oxidase and other enzymes contribute to the neurodegeneration and hair changes.","additionalDifferentials":["Child abuse or non-accidental injury (because of fractures and subdural collections)","Other neonatal-onset epileptic encephalopathies","Mitochondrial disorders with early neurodegeneration","Ehlers-Danlos syndrome and other connective tissue disorders","Nutritional copper deficiency","Trichothiodystrophy and other hair-shaft disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum copper and caeruloplasmin (characteristically low, interpreted cautiously in neonates)","Plasma and cerebrospinal fluid catecholamine ratios reflecting dopamine beta-hydroxylase deficiency","Light microscopy of hair demonstrating pili torti","Molecular genetic testing of ATP7A to confirm the diagnosis and enable carrier testing","Neuroimaging showing atrophy, subdural collections and tortuous intracranial vessels","Skeletal survey for metaphyseal changes and wormian bones"],"redFlags":["Progressive seizures and developmental regression in a young male infant","Unexplained fractures or subdural haemorrhage that may mimic non-accidental injury","Vascular tortuosity with risk of aneurysm or haemorrhage","Rapid neurological deterioration signalling advanced disease"],"prognosis":"The classic form is progressive and usually fatal in early childhood, with severe neurodevelopmental impairment. Very early parenteral copper replacement, particularly with certain ATP7A variants retaining residual function, may improve survival and neurological outcome, but many children remain profoundly affected.","pitfalls":["Interpreting low neonatal serum copper and caeruloplasmin as normal and delaying diagnosis","Attributing fractures and subdural collections solely to non-accidental injury","Starting copper replacement too late, after irreversible neurodegeneration","Overlooking the diagnosis because early neonatal development can appear normal"],"management":["Early parenteral copper-histidine replacement, most beneficial when started neonatally and in ATP7A variants retaining residual transporter function","Anticonvulsant therapy for seizures and management of hypotonia","Nutritional support for failure to thrive and feeding difficulty","Surveillance and management of connective-tissue and vascular complications, including bladder diverticula with attention to urinary tract infection","Neuroimaging follow-up for subdural collections alongside supportive neurodevelopmental therapies","Genetic counselling with maternal carrier testing and prenatal or pre-symptomatic diagnosis given X-linked inheritance","Coordinated metabolic medicine and clinical genetics multidisciplinary care with palliative input in advanced disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"menopause_and_postmenopausal_hormone_thera":{"name":"Menopause and Postmenopausal Hormone Therapy","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Menopause","Perimenopause","Postmenopausal hormone therapy","Climacteric","Menopausal transition"],"chapter":407,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3142","clinicalPearls":["Menopause is the permanent cessation of menstruation from loss of ovarian follicular function and is diagnosed retrospectively after 12 months of amenorrhea; the average age in U.S. women is about 51 years.","Perimenopause is the transition preceding menopause when fertility wanes and cycles become irregular, typically beginning 2-8 years before the final menses (mean duration about 4 years); smoking advances the transition by roughly 2 years.","Perimenopause is marked by 'irregularly irregular' hormone levels rather than the consistently high FSH and low estradiol of established menopause; intermenstrual intervals often shorten (by about 3 days) due to an accelerated follicular phase.","FSH rises before LH during the transition, attributed to loss of inhibin and estrogen feedback; after menopause estradiol falls markedly while estrone is relatively preserved through peripheral aromatization of adrenal and ovarian androgens.","Vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes, night sweats), irregular bleeding, and vaginal dryness are strongly attributable to the transition; sleep disturbance has moderate supporting evidence, while mood swings, memory/concentration changes, incontinence, and sexual dysfunction are not clearly caused by ovarian aging alone.","Nearly 60% of women in one U.S. study reported hot flashes in the 2 years before their final menses; symptom intensity, frequency, duration, and quality-of-life impact are highly variable.","Estrogen therapy is the most effective treatment for vasomotor and genitourinary symptoms; randomized trial data suggest hormone therapy reduces menopausal symptoms by roughly 65-90%.","The 'timing hypothesis' holds that estrogen may slow early atherosclerosis but adversely affect advanced lesions, so women initiating hormone therapy early after menopause may differ in cardiovascular outcome from those starting many years later.","Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease should NOT be considered an expected benefit of hormone therapy; an increased stroke risk and a small early increase in coronary risk should be weighed.","A 1-year absence of spontaneous menses reliably indicates ovulation has ceased, but natural menstrual pattern cannot be assessed while a woman is taking oral contraceptives.","Estrogen reduces bone turnover and resorption; postmenopausal estrogen (with or without a progestogen) increases spine bone density by about 4-6% and hip density by about 2-3%, with WHI showing roughly a 33% reduction in hip fractures.","Unopposed estrogen markedly raises endometrial cancer risk; adding a progestogen opposes this effect and can even reduce endometrial cancer risk, which is why estrogen-only therapy is reserved for women without a uterus."],"pathophysiology":"Ovarian mass and fertility decline sharply after age 35 and more steeply during perimenopause as the pool of primary follicles, depleting since before birth, runs down. Altered folliculogenesis and reduced inhibin secretion cause FSH to rise; the propensity for anovulatory cycles produces a relatively hyperestrogenic, hypoprogestagenic milieu that may underlie the increased perimenopausal incidence of endometrial hyperplasia/carcinoma, uterine polyps, and leiomyomata. With transition into menopause, estradiol falls markedly while estrone is comparatively preserved through peripheral aromatization of adrenal and ovarian androgens; FSH rises more than LH owing to loss of inhibin plus estrogen feedback. Exogenous estrogen lowers LDL and raises HDL cholesterol by about 10-15% and favorably affects lipoprotein(a), LDL oxidation, and endothelial function, but it also raises triglycerides, promotes coagulation (factor VII, prothrombin fragments, fibrinopeptide A), and elevates C-reactive protein, providing a mechanistic basis for both potential benefits and thrombotic/inflammatory harms.","additionalDifferentials":["Pregnancy as a cause of amenorrhea in perimenopausal women (unintentional pregnancy rate in the forties rivals that of adolescents)","Premature ovarian insufficiency / primary ovarian insufficiency when ovarian failure occurs well before the usual age","Thyroid dysfunction or other endocrine causes of menstrual irregularity and vasomotor-like symptoms","Uterine structural lesions (leiomyomata, endometrial polyps, hyperplasia or carcinoma) presenting as abnormal/heavy bleeding","Other age-related conditions causing midlife mood, sleep, cognitive, or sexual complaints not attributable to ovarian senescence"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Thyroid disease producing vasomotor-like flushing and menstrual change","Anxiety or panic disorder mimicking hot flashes","Endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma presenting as abnormal bleeding rather than physiologic transition","Medication effects or other causes of secondary amenorrhea"],"additionalInvestigations":["Menstrual cycle history as the principal STRAW+10 criterion for staging reproductive aging, with biomarkers as supportive criteria","FSH measurement: random level >25 IU/L is characteristic of the late menopausal transition; day-3 levels <20, 20 to <30, and >=30 IU/L indicate good, fair, and poor pregnancy likelihood respectively","Estradiol levels, recognizing that FSH and estradiol are imperfect diagnostic indicators in still-menstruating perimenopausal women due to marked intraindividual variability","Anti-mullerian hormone and inhibin B as additional markers of reproductive aging","Transvaginal ultrasound (with saline enhancement) to detect leiomyomata or polyps when bleeding is abnormal","Endometrial aspiration/biopsy to identify hyperplastic or premalignant changes when menorrhagia or abnormal bleeding occurs","Bone mineral density assessment and FRAX 10-year fracture risk estimation to guide osteoporosis decisions; 25-hydroxyvitamin D level >=50 nmol/L considered sufficient for bone maintenance"],"redFlags":["Unexplained or undiagnosed vaginal bleeding requires evaluation to exclude endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma before attributing it to the transition","An acute coronary event or stroke while on hormone therapy mandates immediate discontinuation of therapy","New or worsening vasomotor symptoms accompanied by features suggesting thromboembolism, given the increased VTE risk with oral estrogen","Heavy menstrual bleeding (menorrhagia) in perimenopause warrants evaluation for uterine disorders rather than assumption of benign cause","Symptoms or signs of estrogen-dependent malignancy in a woman considering or using hormone therapy"],"prognosis":"Most peri- and postmenopausal symptoms are self-limited though variable in duration and intensity. In the WHI, many risks and benefits of hormone therapy dissipated within 5-7 years after stopping, though some persisted: with estrogen-progestin an elevated breast cancer risk continued over long follow-up and a reduced hip fracture risk and reduced endometrial cancer risk persisted; with estrogen alone the breast cancer risk reduction became significant on extended follow-up. Estrogen-progestin carried an overall unfavorable global benefit-risk profile that did not vary by 10-year age group, whereas estrogen-only therapy had a roughly neutral profile overall with a more favorable balance in younger women (relative risks for the global index of about 0.84, 0.99, and 1.17 in ages 50-59, 60-69, and 70-79). Over extended follow-up estrogen alone was associated with reduced all-cause mortality in women aged 50-59 (notably those with bilateral oophorectomy), while estrogen-progestin showed elevated breast cancer risk and a suggestion of increased breast cancer mortality.","pitfalls":["Observational studies overstated cardiovascular benefit because women choosing hormone therapy tend to be healthier and more health-conscious (healthy-user confounding); randomized trials did not confirm the benefit.","Relying on a single FSH or estradiol value to diagnose perimenopause in a menstruating woman is unreliable given extreme intraindividual variability.","Prescribing unopposed estrogen to a woman with an intact uterus markedly raises endometrial cancer and atypical hyperplasia risk; a progestogen is needed for endometrial protection.","Attributing midlife mood, cognitive, urinary, or sexual complaints to menopause when evidence does not support ovarian aging as the principal cause.","Counseling should emphasize absolute rather than relative risk measures, since absolute risks rise with age even when relative risks are similar.","Apparent excess breast cancer detection in early hormone therapy users may partly reflect more frequent screening mammography rather than a true biologic effect.","Assuming the cardiovascular and other effects of one formulation/route apply to all, since transdermal estrogen may carry lower thrombotic and gallbladder risk than oral estrogen."],"management":["Systemic menopausal hormone therapy with oestrogen, combined with a progestogen when the uterus is intact, as the most effective treatment for vasomotor symptoms","Low-dose vaginal oestrogen for genitourinary syndrome of menopause and vaginal dryness","Non-hormonal alternatives such as SSRIs or SNRIs, gabapentin, clonidine or a neurokinin-3 receptor antagonist for women who cannot or prefer not to take hormones","Individualised shared decision-making that weighs breast cancer and venous thromboembolism risk, favouring transdermal oestrogen where thrombotic risk is a concern","Bone protection with weight-bearing exercise, calcium and vitamin D, and antiresorptive therapy such as bisphosphonates for osteoporosis","Cardiovascular risk factor optimisation and lifestyle measures including smoking cessation","Endometrial assessment for abnormal or unscheduled bleeding, with periodic review of the ongoing need for hormone therapy"],"severityClassification":"The Stages of Reproductive Aging Workshop +10 (STRAW+10) system stages reproductive aging using menstrual cycle characteristics as principal criteria (e.g., persistent >=7-day differences in consecutive cycle length in the early transition; an interval of amenorrhea of >=60 days in the late transition) with endocrine markers (FSH, AMH, inhibin B, antral follicle count) as supportive criteria, spanning reproductive, menopausal transition (perimenopause), and postmenopause stages around the final menstrual period.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3142","Harrison 22e p.3143","Harrison 22e p.3144","Harrison 22e p.3145","Harrison 22e p.3146","Harrison 22e p.3147","Harrison 22e p.3148"],"crossLinks":[]},"menstrual_disorders_and_pelvic_pain":{"name":"Menstrual Disorders and Pelvic Pain","system":"Reproductive / Gynecologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Amenorrhea","Abnormal uterine bleeding","Dysfunctional uterine bleeding","Oligomenorrhea","Dysmenorrhea","Pelvic pain"],"chapter":405,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3132","clinicalPearls":["Amenorrhea is the absence of menses: primary when bleeding has never occurred without hormonal treatment, secondary when established cycles stop for 3-6 months; primary amenorrhea is rare (<1% of women), whereas roughly 3-5% of women have at least 3 months of secondary amenorrhea in a given year.","Pregnancy is the single most common cause of amenorrhea and must be excluded in every case with a beta-hCG, regardless of the history offered.","A normal cycle recurs every 21-35 days, lasts 4-7 days, and involves up to about 80 mL of blood loss; the term abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB) has replaced dysfunctional uterine bleeding and is categorized by the PALM-COEIN scheme (structural: polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, malignancy/hyperplasia; non-structural: coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, endometrial, iatrogenic, not yet classified).","Evaluation of ovulatory disorders rests on three initial tests: a pregnancy test, an FSH level (to localize the problem to the ovary versus a central cause), and assessment for hyperandrogenism.","FSH is a better marker of ovarian failure than LH because it reflects loss of negative feedback from both estradiol and the inhibins and is less variable; anti-mullerian hormone (AMH) is also low in primary ovarian insufficiency.","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea is a diagnosis of exclusion, typically driven by a mismatch between energy expenditure and intake and/or significant stress, with suppressed GnRH, low gonadotropins, and normal prolactin; eating disorders, excessive exercise, and chronic disease must be specifically ruled out.","Isolated hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (IHH) is three times more common in men; about half of affected women have anosmia (Kallmann syndrome), and roughly half have an identifiable genetic cause.","Primary ovarian insufficiency (POI) - the preferred term over premature menopause/ovarian failure - denotes ovarian failure before age 40, accounts for about 10% of secondary amenorrhea, and can wax and wane so serial measurements may be needed.","Roughly up to one-third of women between menarche and menopause experience abnormal uterine bleeding at some point.","Up to 60% of pelvic pain is attributable to gastrointestinal causes (appendicitis, cholecystitis, infection, obstruction, diverticulitis, inflammatory bowel disease); urinary and musculoskeletal disorders are also common, so pelvic pain is frequently not gynecologic in origin.","Beta-hCG normally roughly doubles every 48 h in the early first trimester, and 99% of viable intrauterine pregnancies show at least a 53% rise over 2 days; the discriminatory zone (about 1500-3000 IU/mL) is the level above which a normal intrauterine pregnancy should be visible on ultrasound.","Dysmenorrhea begins with menstrual flow and eases over 12-72 h; primary dysmenorrhea is largely prostaglandin-mediated, while secondary dysmenorrhea reflects underlying pelvic pathology such as endometriosis or adenomyosis."],"pathophysiology":"Menstrual function depends on coordinated signaling among four compartments - hypothalamus, pituitary, ovary, and uterus/outflow tract - linked by feedback loops involving GnRH, the gonadotropins FSH and LH, and the ovarian products estradiol, progesterone, and the inhibins (inhibin A and B). Disorders divide into outflow-tract/uterine problems and ovulatory disorders. Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism arises when anatomic, genetic, or functional disturbances impair hypothalamic GnRH output or pituitary responsiveness, producing low estrogen with normal or low LH/FSH; in functional hypothalamic amenorrhea, energy deficit and stress raise CRH, suppress GnRH, and lower TRH input, with leptin and hypothalamic kisspeptin/neurokinin B/dynorphin signaling transducing metabolic and stress cues to the GnRH pulse generator. Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism (ovarian insufficiency) removes negative feedback, raising FSH and LH. In PCOS, abnormal GnRH pulsatility elevates LH and drives ovarian androgen output, and insulin resistance in muscle and fat further augments insulin-stimulated ovarian androgen production; frequent anovulation yields irregular menses and unopposed estrogen effect on the endometrium. Pelvic pain shares mechanisms with abdominal pain - parietal peritoneal inflammation, hollow-viscus obstruction, vascular compromise (e.g., ovarian torsion), and abdominal-wall pain - and may be referred from extrapelvic sources. Primary dysmenorrhea is mediated chiefly by prostaglandin pathways causing intense uterine contractions, reduced blood flow, and peripheral nerve hypersensitivity, with PG-independent mediators such as platelet-activating factor also contributing. Endometriosis reflects ectopic endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterus that respond to hormonal stimulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Pregnancy (must be excluded first in amenorrhea and in any reproductive-age woman with pelvic pain or bleeding)","Outflow-tract obstruction: transverse vaginal septum, imperforate hymen","Mullerian agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome; WNT4 mutations)","Androgen insensitivity syndrome (about 10% of primary amenorrhea; 46,XY karyotype, female external genitalia, absent pubic/axillary hair, male-range testosterone)","5-alpha reductase type 2 deficiency","Asherman's syndrome (intrauterine adhesions, usually post-curettage; genital tuberculosis in endemic areas)","Hypothalamic/pituitary tumors and infiltrative disease, hyperprolactinemia, Sheehan's syndrome, lymphocytic hypophysitis, prior cranial irradiation","Isolated hypogonadotropic hypogonadism / Kallmann syndrome","Primary ovarian insufficiency (idiopathic, Turner syndrome, autoimmune polyglandular failure, fragile X premutation, post-chemo/radiotherapy)","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Rarer causes of hypergonadotropic hypogonadism: FSH/LH receptor mutations, aromatase deficiency, 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency","Pelvic pain mimics outside the reproductive tract: appendicitis, diverticulitis, inflammatory bowel disease, urinary tract disorders, musculoskeletal/neuromuscular and psychosomatic causes"],"infectionMimics":["Pelvic inflammatory disease (upper genital tract infection, often Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae)","Tubo-ovarian abscess","Endometritis (post-instrumentation, postpartum, or STI-associated)","Tuberculous salpingitis / genital tuberculosis (also a cause of Asherman's syndrome in endemic regions)","Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome (perihepatitis complicating PID, presenting with right upper quadrant pain)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ectopic pregnancy","Threatened abortion","Ruptured or hemorrhagic ovarian cyst","Ovarian (or rarely tubal) torsion","Mittelschmerz (ovulation pain)","Endometriosis and endometrioma","Uterine fibroids (including acutely degenerating leiomyoma) and adenomyosis","Pelvic adhesions","Pelvic congestion syndrome","Vulvodynia","Pelvic malignancy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Beta-hCG to exclude pregnancy in every amenorrhea and acute pelvic pain evaluation","FSH (and LH) to localize ovulatory failure to ovary versus central cause; serial/repeat sampling in suspected POI","Prolactin in all patients with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Estradiol, testosterone and androgen assessment for hyperandrogenism; AMH (low in POI, may be elevated in PCOS)","Karyotype in primary amenorrhea with suspected chromosomal cause and in all POI patients","POI work-up: anti-cortical and 21-hydroxylase antibodies (specific but not sensitive for future adrenal insufficiency), thyroid function and thyroid peroxidase antibodies, FMR1 premutation screening, and bone mineral density (ovarian biopsy is NOT indicated)","Pelvic ultrasound (transvaginal) as first-line imaging for amenorrhea work-up, pelvic pain, ovarian cysts, torsion (with Doppler for ovarian blood flow), endometrioma, and fibroids","MRI of the pelvis for better delineation of reproductive-tract anomalies before surgery and for higher sensitivity/specificity in endometriosis","Cranial MRI when history is atypical, with headache, other hypothalamic dysfunction, or hyperprolactinemia","Wet-mount of vaginal discharge plus nucleic acid amplification testing for C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae in suspected PID","CA-125 may be elevated in endometriosis but has low negative predictive value; diagnostic laparoscopy is the gold standard for endometriosis when empiric treatment fails"],"redFlags":["Shock or peritoneal signs in a woman with pelvic pain - identify life-threatening conditions first as they may need emergent surgery","Reproductive-age woman with abdominal pain or vaginal bleeding - maintain high suspicion for ectopic pregnancy regardless of contraceptive use; tubal rupture is a life-threatening emergency","Severe pain with fever and peritoneal signs in PID suggests tubo-ovarian abscess or peritonitis","Right upper quadrant pain in PID suggests perihepatitis (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome)","Acute unilateral intermittent pain raising concern for ovarian torsion (confirm by absent Doppler flow)","Atypical amenorrhea history with headache, signs of hypothalamic dysfunction, short stature, diabetes insipidus, or galactorrhea - evaluate for a neuroanatomic lesion","Heavy or prolonged bleeding warrants prompt attention, but infrequent or absent bleeding is often under-reported and should be actively elicited"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on cause. Many causes of functional hypothalamic amenorrhea are reversible with restoration of energy balance, though bone density may not fully recover with hormone replacement alone. Amenorrhea of any cause is usually associated with chronically low estrogen, carrying risk to bone and cardiovascular health if untreated. PID untreated risks infertility and ectopic pregnancy, and subclinical PID remains a major worldwide cause of these complications. PCOS carries elevated long-term risk of metabolic syndrome, type 2 diabetes, dyslipidemia, hypertension, cardiovascular disease, obstructive sleep apnea, steatotic liver disease, and a two- to sixfold increase in endometrial hyperplasia/cancer from chronic anovulation; symptoms often improve with age, with most women regaining regular menses and normal androgens by the fourth decade. POI is associated with additional health problems and infertility, though oocyte donation has high success. Ectopic pregnancy with tubal rupture can be fatal.","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude pregnancy before pursuing an amenorrhea or pelvic-pain work-up.","Overlooking infrequent or absent menses because the patient does not volunteer it - a focused menstrual history is needed at every encounter.","Attributing pelvic pain to the reproductive tract when up to 60% of cases are gastrointestinal and urinary/musculoskeletal causes are common.","Waiting for positive microbiology before treating suspected PID - a presumptive clinical diagnosis is sufficient to begin treatment, and negative tests do not exclude it.","Confusing androgen insensitivity syndrome with mullerian agenesis - absent pubic/axillary hair and a male-range testosterone distinguish AIS.","Diagnosing PCOS without excluding mimics (hypothyroidism, hyperprolactinemia, adrenal hyperandrogenism); it is a diagnosis of exclusion, and in adolescents one should wait at least 3 years after menarche and not rely on ultrasound/AMH criteria.","Performing ovarian biopsy in POI - it is not indicated; routine genetic testing beyond FMR1 is also not currently recommended.","Assuming endometriosis severity at laparoscopy correlates with pain - it does not always; a normal CA-125 does not exclude endometriosis.","Delaying surgery for outflow-tract obstruction - early correction reduces the risk of endometriosis from retrograde menstruation."],"management":["Exclude pregnancy with beta-hCG and direct treatment at the underlying cause identified through the PALM-COEIN framework","Restore energy balance and address disordered eating or excessive exercise in functional hypothalamic amenorrhoea, with oestrogen replacement to protect bone","Medical management of heavy or abnormal uterine bleeding with the levonorgestrel intrauterine system, combined hormonal contraception, progestogens, tranexamic acid or NSAIDs","Surgical treatment of structural lesions such as polypectomy or myomectomy where indicated","Hormone replacement therapy until the usual age of menopause in primary ovarian insufficiency, with fertility and oocyte-donation counselling","Dopamine agonist therapy for hyperprolactinaemic amenorrhoea","Bone mineral density surveillance in sustained hypo-oestrogenism, with gynaecology and reproductive endocrinology input and psychological support"],"severityClassification":"Abnormal uterine bleeding is categorized etiologically by the PALM-COEIN system (structural causes: Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma, Malignancy/hyperplasia; non-structural: Coagulopathy, Ovulatory dysfunction, Endometrial, Iatrogenic, Not yet classified). PCOS is diagnosed by the updated Rotterdam criteria (2023 international guidelines): two of three among irregular menses (fewer than 8 per year), clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound (>=20 antral follicles or ovarian volume >=10 cm3 in one ovary) or elevated AMH, yielding hyperandrogenic and non-hyperandrogenic phenotypes. Pelvic pain is classified as acute versus chronic and cyclic versus noncyclic to guide evaluation. Endometriosis is staged by laparoscopy.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3132","Harrison 22e p.3133","Harrison 22e p.3134","Harrison 22e p.3135","Harrison 22e p.3136","Harrison 22e p.3137"],"crossLinks":[]},"meralgia_paraesthetica":{"name":"meralgia paraesthetica","system":"Nervous system / Peripheral nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lateral femoral cutaneous nerve entrapment","Bernhardt-Roth syndrome","lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh neuralgia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A purely sensory mononeuropathy of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve, typically entrapped where it passes beneath or through the inguinal ligament near the anterior superior iliac spine","Presents with burning, tingling, numbness or hypersensitivity over the anterolateral thigh, often worsened by standing, walking or hip extension and relieved by sitting","There is by definition NO motor weakness and NO loss of the patellar reflex, which distinguishes it from femoral neuropathy and L2-L3 radiculopathy","Common precipitants include obesity, pregnancy, tight belts or waistbands, body armour or tool belts, rapid weight change and diabetes","Iatrogenic cases follow iliac crest bone-graft harvest, anterior hip surgery, hernia repair and prolonged prone positioning","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by a positive Tinel sign medial to the anterior superior iliac spine and by symptom relief after a diagnostic local anaesthetic nerve block","First-line management is conservative: removing external compression, weight reduction and neuropathic analgesic agents, with local anaesthetic and corticosteroid injection or surgical decompression or neurectomy reserved for refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve arises from the L2-L3 roots, courses across the iliacus and typically exits the pelvis just medial to the anterior superior iliac spine, passing under, through or over the inguinal ligament before supplying sensation to the anterolateral thigh. Its sharp angulation at this fibro-osseous point makes it vulnerable to focal compression and repetitive mechanical or traction injury, particularly where an acute course, external pressure, raised intra-abdominal pressure or perineurial fibrosis is present. Chronic compression produces focal demyelination and, if sustained, axonal loss confined to this sensory nerve, generating the characteristic dysaesthesia without any motor deficit.","additionalDifferentials":["Upper lumbar (L2-L3) radiculopathy","Femoral neuropathy","Lumbar plexopathy including diabetic amyotrophy","Trochanteric bursitis or hip joint pathology","Referred pain from intra-abdominal or retroperitoneal mass compressing the nerve","Iliopsoas or retroperitoneal haematoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical sensory mapping of the anterolateral thigh with pelvic compression test","Diagnostic local anaesthetic block of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve near the anterior superior iliac spine","Nerve conduction studies of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (technically difficult, side-to-side comparison)","High-resolution ultrasound of the nerve at the inguinal ligament","Pelvic or retroperitoneal imaging if a compressive mass or secondary cause is suspected","Screening for diabetes mellitus in atypical or bilateral presentations"],"redFlags":["Any motor weakness, quadriceps wasting or depressed patellar reflex suggesting femoral nerve or root involvement","Bilateral or rapidly progressive symptoms raising concern for a retroperitoneal mass or infiltrative process","Associated weight loss, night pain or systemic features","New symptoms with abdominal or back pain suggesting haematoma or malignancy"],"prognosis":"The majority of cases resolve with conservative measures and removal of the precipitating compression, and pregnancy-related cases usually settle after delivery. A minority with persistent axonal injury develop chronic dysaesthesia that may require injection therapy or surgical decompression or neurectomy.","pitfalls":["Attributing anterolateral thigh numbness to a lumbar disc without noting the absence of motor and reflex signs","Missing an iatrogenic or compressive cause such as a recently applied tight garment, belt or surgical positioning","Overlooking a retroperitoneal or pelvic mass in bilateral or progressive disease","Performing surgery before an adequate trial of conservative management and a confirmatory diagnostic block","Confusing the purely sensory distribution with the broader deficit of a lumbar plexopathy"],"management":["Conservative first-line management by removing the source of compression, loosening belts and tight garments, and weight reduction","Neuropathic pain relief with gabapentinoids, tricyclic agents or topical therapy for troublesome dysaesthesia","Local anaesthetic with or without corticosteroid nerve block near the anterior superior iliac spine, both diagnostic and therapeutic","Address precipitating factors including glycaemic control in diabetes, with reassurance that pregnancy-related cases usually settle after delivery","Surgical decompression or neurectomy reserved for persistent, disabling symptoms with axonal injury","Referral to neurology or a pain service for refractory cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"meralgia_paresthetica":{"name":"Meralgia paraesthetica (lateral femoral cutaneous nerve)","system":"Nervous system / Peripheral nerve","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["meralgia paraesthetica","lateral femoral cutaneous nerve entrapment","Bernhardt-Roth syndrome","LFCN neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A focal mononeuropathy of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve, which arises from the L2-L3 roots and is a purely sensory nerve","Entrapment typically occurs as the nerve passes beneath or through the inguinal ligament near the anterior superior iliac spine","Presents with burning, tingling, numbness, and hypersensitivity over the anterolateral thigh in the nerve's territory, without motor weakness","Symptoms are often worsened by standing, walking, or hip extension and eased by sitting, which relaxes tension on the nerve","Common contributors include obesity, pregnancy, tight belts or waistbands, tool belts, and abdominal or pelvic surgery or scarring","Because the nerve is purely sensory, the reflexes and power are normal, and a positive Tinel sign near the inguinal ligament supports the diagnosis","First-line management is conservative with weight loss, avoiding constrictive clothing, and activity modification, with neuropathic-pain agents or a local anaesthetic/corticosteroid nerve block for persistent symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve is a purely sensory nerve derived from the L2 and L3 roots that supplies the skin of the anterolateral thigh. As it courses toward the thigh it passes near the anterior superior iliac spine and beneath, through, or over the inguinal ligament, where its relatively fixed and angulated path makes it vulnerable to mechanical compression or stretch. Increased intra-abdominal pressure or local tension from obesity, pregnancy, tight garments, or postoperative scarring compresses the nerve, causing focal demyelination and axonal irritation that produce the characteristic burning dysaesthesia and numbness. Because the nerve carries no motor fibres, there is no weakness or reflex change, and the deficit is confined to a well-defined cutaneous patch.","additionalDifferentials":["Lumbar radiculopathy affecting the L2 or L3 root","Femoral neuropathy with motor involvement","Lumbar plexopathy","Trochanteric or hip joint pathology referring to the lateral thigh","Diabetic or other generalised polyneuropathy","Retroperitoneal or pelvic mass compressing the nerve proximally"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination confirming purely sensory loss over the anterolateral thigh with preserved power and reflexes","Tinel sign elicited near the anterior superior iliac spine/inguinal ligament","Diagnostic local anaesthetic nerve block to confirm the source of pain","Nerve conduction studies of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve where diagnosis is uncertain","Ultrasound of the nerve at the inguinal ligament to identify entrapment or a structural cause","Imaging of the lumbar spine or pelvis if a proximal or radicular cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Motor weakness, which indicates a different or more proximal lesion","Bilateral or rapidly progressive symptoms suggesting polyneuropathy or a central cause","Features of a retroperitoneal or pelvic mass compressing the nerve","Bowel, bladder, or saddle symptoms pointing to a cauda equina or radicular process"],"prognosis":"Most cases are benign and resolve or substantially improve with conservative measures, particularly weight loss and removing constrictive pressure; refractory cases may respond to nerve blocks, and surgical decompression or neurectomy is reserved for the few with persistent disabling symptoms.","pitfalls":["Attributing purely sensory anterolateral thigh symptoms to hip or spinal disease and missing the entrapment","Overlooking modifiable contributors such as tight belts, rapid weight gain, or pregnancy","Investigating for motor deficits that should not occur in this purely sensory neuropathy","Proceeding to surgery before an adequate trial of conservative therapy and a confirmatory nerve block"],"management":["Removal of constrictive pressure from belts, waistbands or tool belts and weight loss as primary conservative measures","Symptomatic relief with neuropathic analgesics such as gabapentinoids or tricyclic antidepressants","Diagnostic and therapeutic local anaesthetic nerve block of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve at the inguinal ligament","Correction of contributing factors including glycaemic control, with expectant management of pregnancy-related cases that resolve postpartum","Surgical decompression or neurectomy for the minority with refractory disabling symptoms","Neurology referral when the diagnosis is uncertain or symptoms persist despite conservative care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"mercury_poisoning":{"name":"Mercury poisoning","system":"Occupational / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mercurialism","mercury toxicity","hydrargyria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hunter's Diseases of Occupations / WHO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The clinical picture depends on the chemical form: elemental mercury vapour, inorganic mercury salts, and organic mercury (such as methylmercury) each cause distinct patterns","Inhaled elemental mercury vapour, seen in industries such as chloralkali and instrument work, chiefly affects the central nervous system and can cause an acute chemical pneumonitis at high concentrations","Chronic elemental and inorganic exposure produces the classic triad of tremor, gingivostomatitis and neuropsychiatric changes ('erethism') with irritability, shyness and memory impairment","Ingested inorganic mercury salts are corrosive, causing gastrointestinal burning and haemorrhage and acute kidney injury","Organic methylmercury, the cause of environmental epidemics from contaminated fish, targets the nervous system with sensory disturbance, ataxia, visual field constriction and hearing loss, and crosses the placenta to harm the developing fetus","Diagnosis uses blood and urine mercury levels, with the appropriate specimen depending on the form of exposure, interpreted with the exposure history","Management centres on removing exposure and supportive care, with a chelating agent used in significant inorganic or elemental poisoning under specialist advice, while chelation is less effective for organic mercury","Acrodynia (pink disease) is a distinctive reaction to mercury seen particularly in children, with painful pink extremities, irritability and desquamation"],"pathophysiology":"Mercury binds avidly to sulfhydryl groups, disrupting numerous enzymes and cellular proteins. Lipophilic elemental vapour and organic mercury readily cross the blood-brain barrier (and placenta), producing neuronal injury and the neurological and neuropsychiatric features, while inorganic salts concentrate in and damage the renal tubules and are locally corrosive to the gut. Oxidative stress and immune-mediated renal effects contribute to the tissue damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Essential or other causes of tremor","Primary psychiatric or neurodegenerative disease","Other heavy metal poisoning","Peripheral neuropathy of other cause","Acute corrosive ingestion of other agents","Acute kidney injury from other nephrotoxins"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood mercury (more useful for recent organic exposure) and urinary mercury (for elemental and inorganic exposure)","Renal function and urinalysis to detect tubular injury","Neurological and neuropsychological assessment","Chest imaging in acute vapour inhalation for pneumonitis","Exposure history covering occupation, dental amalgam handling, and dietary fish intake"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: acute high-level vapour inhalation causing respiratory distress and chemical pneumonitis","Corrosive ingestion of inorganic salts with gastrointestinal haemorrhage and shock","Acute kidney injury","Pregnancy with organic mercury exposure given the risk of fetal neurological harm"],"prognosis":"Removal from exposure often allows gradual improvement of inorganic and elemental effects, but severe neurological injury, especially from organic methylmercury, may be permanent, and prenatal exposure can cause lasting developmental impairment.","pitfalls":["Using the wrong specimen (for example relying on blood levels for chronic elemental exposure)","Expecting chelation to reverse established organic mercury neurotoxicity","Attributing neuropsychiatric change to primary psychiatric illness without an exposure history","Overlooking dietary fish as a source of methylmercury","Missing the risk to the fetus in exposed pregnant women"],"management":["Immediate removal from the source of exposure as the cornerstone of management","Chelation with succimer, DMPS or dimercaprol for symptomatic elemental or inorganic poisoning, noting dimercaprol is avoided in methylmercury exposure as it may worsen central nervous system toxicity","Supportive care for acute vapour pneumonitis with oxygen and ventilatory support, and management of corrosive gastrointestinal injury from ingested salts","Renal support including haemodialysis for acute kidney injury from inorganic mercury","Gut decontamination after significant ingestion where appropriate","Neurological and neuropsychological rehabilitation for persistent deficits","Occupational hygiene, source control and public health involvement, with dietary fish advice and counselling in pregnancy to protect the foetus"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Hunter's Diseases of Occupations","WHO guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"merkel_cell_carcinoma":{"name":"Merkel cell carcinoma","system":"Dermatology / Oncology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCC","cutaneous neuroendocrine carcinoma","trabecular carcinoma of skin","primary neuroendocrine carcinoma of skin"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / WHO Classification of Skin Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents as a rapidly enlarging, painless, firm violaceous or red nodule on sun-exposed skin of elderly or immunosuppressed patients","The acronym AEIOU (Asymptomatic, Expanding rapidly, Immunosuppression, Older than fifty, UV-exposed fair skin) captures the classic clinical profile","Two distinct pathways exist: a Merkel-cell polyomavirus-driven form and a UV-mutagenesis-driven form with high tumour mutational burden","Histology shows small round blue cells with scant cytoplasm, salt-and-pepper chromatin, and a high mitotic and apoptotic rate","Immunohistochemistry demonstrates a characteristic paranuclear dot pattern of cytokeratin 20 and neuroendocrine markers, with negativity for TTF-1 helping exclude metastatic small-cell lung carcinoma","Early regional nodal spread is common, so sentinel lymph node biopsy is central to staging even in clinically node-negative disease","Management combines wide local excision (or Mohs micrographic surgery) with adjuvant radiotherapy, and immune-checkpoint inhibition has transformed treatment of advanced disease","The tumour is markedly more common and more aggressive in the immunosuppressed, including transplant recipients and those with HIV or haematological malignancy"],"pathophysiology":"Merkel cell carcinoma is an aggressive cutaneous neuroendocrine malignancy that arises through two convergent routes. In roughly the majority of cases in many populations, clonal integration of Merkel-cell polyomavirus into the host genome, together with truncating mutations of the viral large T antigen, drives persistent expression of viral oncoproteins that inactivate tumour-suppressor pathways such as retinoblastoma protein signalling. The alternative route is chronic ultraviolet damage producing a very high burden of somatic mutations, typically in virus-negative tumours. Both pathways yield a proliferation of small cells with neuroendocrine differentiation that infiltrate the dermis, invade lymphatics readily, and metastasise early to regional nodes and beyond. The strong influence of immune surveillance explains both the excess incidence in immunosuppression and the notable responsiveness to checkpoint blockade.","additionalDifferentials":["Metastatic small-cell lung carcinoma to skin","Cutaneous lymphoma (including lymphoblastic and other small round cell infiltrates)","Basal cell carcinoma, particularly nodular type","Amelanotic melanoma","Cutaneous metastasis from other neuroendocrine primaries","Poorly differentiated squamous cell carcinoma","Ewing sarcoma / peripheral primitive neuroectodermal tumour of skin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with histopathology for small round blue cell morphology","Immunohistochemistry panel including cytokeratin 20 (paranuclear dot), neuroendocrine markers, and TTF-1 to exclude pulmonary origin","Sentinel lymph node biopsy for regional staging in clinically node-negative disease","Cross-sectional imaging or functional imaging to assess nodal and distant metastasis","Merkel-cell polyomavirus status or oncoprotein antibody testing where available for prognostication and surveillance","Assessment of immune status including consideration of underlying immunosuppression or occult malignancy"],"redFlags":["Rapidly growing painless nodule on sun-exposed skin of an older or immunosuppressed patient","Palpable regional lymphadenopathy suggesting nodal metastasis","In-transit cutaneous satellite lesions indicating aggressive lymphatic spread","Systemic symptoms or imaging findings suggesting distant metastatic disease","New aggressive skin tumour in a transplant recipient or other profoundly immunosuppressed individual"],"prognosis":"Merkel cell carcinoma is aggressive with a substantial risk of nodal and distant metastasis; outcome is strongly stage-dependent, being relatively favourable for small localised node-negative lesions but poor once regional or distant spread occurs. Immunosuppression worsens prognosis, while immune-checkpoint inhibition has meaningfully improved survival in advanced disease.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the bland-looking nodule for a benign cyst or lipoma and delaying biopsy","Failing to perform immunohistochemistry to distinguish it from metastatic small-cell lung carcinoma and other small round cell tumours","Omitting sentinel lymph node biopsy in clinically node-negative disease and thereby understaging","Underestimating aggressiveness in immunosuppressed patients or attributing rapid growth to a benign process"],"management":["Wide local excision of the primary tumour with adequate margins as the mainstay for localised disease","Sentinel lymph node biopsy for staging, with completion nodal dissection or nodal radiotherapy for involved nodes","Adjuvant radiotherapy to the primary site and regional nodal basin, exploiting the tumour's radiosensitivity","Immune checkpoint inhibition with anti-PD-1/PD-L1 agents such as avelumab or pembrolizumab as first-line systemic therapy for advanced or metastatic disease","Reduction of immunosuppression where feasible and evaluation for occult immunosuppressive conditions","Close surveillance with skin and nodal examination, cross-sectional imaging and Merkel-cell polyomavirus oncoprotein antibody monitoring for early relapse","Multidisciplinary skin oncology management coordinating surgery, radiotherapy and systemic therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","WHO Classification of Skin Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"merosin_deficient_congenital_muscular_dystrophy":{"name":"Merosin-deficient congenital muscular dystrophy (LAMA2-related)","system":"Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MDC1A","LAMA2-related congenital muscular dystrophy","Laminin-alpha2 deficiency","Merosin-deficient CMD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neuromuscular and paediatric neurology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic LAMA2 variants affecting the laminin alpha-2 chain (merosin) of laminin-211; it is the most common congenital muscular dystrophy in many Western populations.","Complete merosin deficiency presents in the neonatal period with profound hypotonia, weakness, poor antigravity movement and feeding and respiratory difficulty (the floppy infant).","Children with complete deficiency often achieve sitting but rarely walk independently, whereas partial deficiency causes a milder, later-onset, sometimes limb-girdle-like course.","A characteristic diffuse white-matter signal change on brain MRI becomes apparent after the early months of life and is usually accompanied by normal cognition.","Epilepsy affects a substantial minority despite the white-matter change, and occipital cortical malformation (such as polymicrogyria) is seen in some.","A demyelinating peripheral neuropathy is frequently present, since merosin is also expressed in the Schwann-cell basal lamina.","Serum creatine kinase is markedly elevated, particularly in early disease.","Joint contractures, progressive scoliosis and respiratory insufficiency dominate long-term management."],"pathophysiology":"LAMA2 encodes the laminin alpha-2 chain, which assembles with beta-1 and gamma-1 chains to form laminin-211 (merosin), the principal laminin of the skeletal-muscle basement membrane and a component of the peripheral-nerve basal lamina and cerebral vasculature. Laminin-211 bridges alpha-dystroglycan and the integrin alpha7-beta1 complex on the sarcolemma to the extracellular matrix, stabilising the fibre and transmitting contractile force. Absent or reduced merosin destabilises this linkage, causing sarcolemmal vulnerability, myofibre degeneration, impaired regeneration and fibrosis. The same deficiency in peripheral nerve produces dysmyelination, while an abnormal basement membrane at the blood-brain barrier underlies the characteristic cerebral white-matter change, which typically does not by itself impair cognition.","additionalDifferentials":["Dystroglycanopathy-type congenital muscular dystrophies (Fukuyama, muscle-eye-brain disease, Walker-Warburg syndrome)","Collagen VI-related congenital muscular dystrophy (Ullrich congenital muscular dystrophy)","SELENON- and RYR1-related congenital myopathies","Congenital myotonic dystrophy","Spinal muscular atrophy and other causes of the floppy infant","Congenital myasthenic syndromes","Leukodystrophies and other causes of infantile white-matter change when MRI is the presenting finding"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Brain MRI to demonstrate the white-matter signal change and to assess for cortical malformation","LAMA2 sequencing with deletion and duplication analysis, or a congenital muscular dystrophy gene panel","Muscle biopsy with laminin-alpha2 (merosin) immunostaining to distinguish complete from partial deficiency","Skin biopsy immunostaining for laminin-alpha2 as a less invasive alternative","Nerve conduction studies to detect the associated demyelinating neuropathy","Respiratory assessment including overnight studies, and a swallowing and feeding evaluation"],"redFlags":["Neonatal respiratory distress or feeding failure with aspiration risk","Nocturnal hypoventilation as respiratory muscle strength and chest-wall compliance decline","New-onset seizures","Rapidly progressive scoliosis compromising respiration","Failure to thrive arising from feeding difficulty"],"prognosis":"Complete merosin deficiency causes severe, early-onset disability, with most children never walking and with substantial respiratory morbidity; survival depends heavily on respiratory and nutritional support. Partial deficiency is generally milder, with later onset and better motor attainment.","pitfalls":["Interpreting the striking white-matter MRI change as a primary leukodystrophy and missing the underlying muscle disorder.","Assuming intellectual impairment from the MRI appearance, when cognition is usually normal.","Overlooking the associated demyelinating peripheral neuropathy.","Missing partial deficiency that presents later as a limb-girdle phenotype.","Deferring respiratory surveillance because the child is young and not yet overtly symptomatic."],"management":["Supportive, multidisciplinary care as no disease-modifying therapy currently exists","Proactive respiratory surveillance with non-invasive ventilation and cough-assist for hypoventilation and respiratory insufficiency","Nutritional and feeding support, including gastrostomy where feeding difficulty is severe","Physiotherapy, stretching, orthoses and management of contractures, with surgical correction of scoliosis","Anticonvulsant therapy for epilepsy, which affects a substantial minority","Surveillance for cardiac involvement and the associated demyelinating neuropathy","Coordinated neuromuscular multidisciplinary team and genetic counselling for the family given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular medicine references","Congenital muscular dystrophy consensus guidelines","Paediatric neurology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"merrf_syndrome":{"name":"MERRF syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Myoclonic epilepsy with ragged-red fibres","m.8344A>G","MT-TK","MERRF"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / GeneReviews mitochondrial disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The defining tetrad is myoclonus, generalised epilepsy, cerebellar ataxia and a myopathy with ragged-red fibres on muscle biopsy","Most cases are due to the maternally inherited m.8344A>G point mutation in the MT-TK gene encoding the mitochondrial transfer RNA for lysine","As with other mitochondrial tRNA disorders, heteroplasmy and a threshold effect explain the variable severity and age of onset across a maternal lineage","Additional features may include sensorineural deafness, optic atrophy, peripheral neuropathy, dementia and multiple symmetrical lipomatosis of the neck and trunk","Elevated resting or exercise-induced lactate supports the diagnosis but is neither universal nor specific","Myoclonus is frequently stimulus-sensitive and can be difficult to control; some anticonvulsants worsen mitochondrial function and are best avoided","Muscle histochemistry shows ragged-red fibres that are typically cytochrome c oxidase deficient, reflecting the tRNA translation defect"],"pathophysiology":"MERRF is caused by a mitochondrial tRNA mutation, usually m.8344A>G in MT-TK, that impairs mitochondrial translation and thereby the synthesis of respiratory-chain subunits, reducing oxidative phosphorylation capacity. The mutation is maternally inherited and heteroplasmic, so clinical expression depends on the proportion of mutant genomes and the energy threshold of each tissue. Neurons of the cerebellum, brainstem and cerebral cortex, together with skeletal muscle, are especially vulnerable to the resulting energy deficit, producing the characteristic myoclonus, seizures, ataxia and myopathy. Accumulation of abnormal mitochondria beneath the sarcolemma gives the ragged-red appearance on trichrome staining.","additionalDifferentials":["Other progressive myoclonic epilepsies such as Unverricht-Lundborg and Lafora disease","MELAS and overlap mitochondrial syndromes","Sialidosis and neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis","Spinocerebellar ataxias with myoclonus","Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy","Coeliac-associated or paraneoplastic cerebellar ataxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Resting and post-exercise blood lactate and pyruvate","Electroencephalography to characterise generalised epileptiform and photosensitive discharges","Brain MRI, which may show cerebellar and brainstem atrophy or basal ganglia calcification","Targeted molecular testing for m.8344A>G with heteroplasmy quantification in appropriate tissue","Skeletal muscle biopsy for ragged-red fibres and respiratory-chain enzymology","Audiometry and nerve conduction studies for associated deafness and neuropathy"],"redFlags":["Poorly controlled myoclonic seizures or status epilepticus","Rapid neurological deterioration or new cardiorespiratory compromise","Cardiac conduction abnormality or cardiomyopathy on surveillance","Metabolic decompensation with rising lactic acidosis"],"prognosis":"MERRF is slowly progressive with a broad spectrum of severity; many patients have a normal early childhood before symptoms emerge, but accumulating myoclonus, ataxia and dementia can be disabling, and lifespan may be reduced in severely affected individuals.","pitfalls":["Labelling the disorder an idiopathic progressive myoclonic epilepsy without pursuing mitochondrial testing","Relying on blood alone for genetic testing, since low-level heteroplasmy may be missed","Prescribing anticonvulsants that impair respiratory-chain or mitochondrial function","Overlooking cardiac and audiological involvement that warrants surveillance"],"management":["Antiepileptic therapy for seizures and myoclonus, favouring agents such as levetiracetam and clonazepam while avoiding sodium valproate, which is mitochondrially toxic","Supportive mitochondrial supplementation such as coenzyme Q10, riboflavin and L-carnitine, recognising the limited evidence base","Management of complications including hearing aids or cochlear implantation for sensorineural deafness and rehabilitation for cerebellar ataxia","Avoidance of mitochondrial toxins including aminoglycosides and valproate","Surveillance for cardiac conduction disease, endocrinopathy and progressive neurological decline","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and multidisciplinary neurological support","Genetic counselling emphasising maternal mitochondrial inheritance and heteroplasmy, with assessment of the maternal lineage"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: MERRF","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"mesenteric_cyst":{"name":"mesenteric cyst","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mesenteric lymphangioma","chylolymphatic cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Schwartz's Principles of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Mesenteric cysts are rare intra-abdominal cysts arising within the mesentery, most commonly in the small bowel mesentery and particularly the ileum.","The de Perrot classification groups them by origin into lymphatic, mesothelial, enteric, urogenital, dermoid, and non-pancreatic pseudocyst types, with lymphatic (lymphangiomatous) lesions predominating.","Many are asymptomatic incidental findings; symptomatic lesions cause abdominal distension, vague pain, or a palpable mass.","The classic Tillaux sign is a mass that moves freely in a plane perpendicular to the axis of the mesentery but is restricted along it.","Complications include torsion, intracystic haemorrhage, infection, rupture, and bowel obstruction, any of which can produce an acute abdomen.","Ultrasound and CT typically show a thin-walled, well-defined, fluid-filled lesion; internal septation or chylous fluid suggests a lymphatic origin.","Complete surgical excision is the treatment of choice and may require resection of intimately adherent bowel to ensure clearance.","Chylous ascites can accompany or follow lymphatic cysts and complicate surgical management."],"pathophysiology":"Mesenteric cysts are benign lesions most often thought to result from proliferation of ectopic or maldeveloped lymphatic tissue that fails to establish communication with the normal lymphatic system, leading to progressive accumulation of lymph or chyle. Other histogenetic origins account for the mesothelial, enteric, and urogenital variants, reflecting entrapped embryonic tissues within the mesenteric leaves. The cyst enlarges as fluid accumulates, exerting mass effect on adjacent bowel and mesenteric vessels; because the lesion lies between the mesenteric leaves it can twist on its pedicle, bleed, become infected, or rupture. The lining varies with origin — endothelial in lymphatic cysts and mesothelial in mesothelial cysts — a distinction confirmed histologically and with immunohistochemistry.","additionalDifferentials":["omental cyst","intestinal duplication cyst","ovarian cyst","pancreatic pseudocyst","hydatid cyst","cystic mesothelioma","choledochal cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["abdominal ultrasound to characterise the cyst and its septa","contrast-enhanced CT to define anatomy and vascular relationships","MRI for soft-tissue characterisation and surgical planning","analysis of aspirated fluid for chyle versus serous content","histopathology with endothelial and mesothelial immunohistochemical markers","diagnostic laparoscopy in equivocal cases"],"redFlags":["acute abdomen from torsion, intracystic haemorrhage, or rupture","features of bowel obstruction","rapid enlargement or systemic signs suggesting infection","development of chylous ascites","solid or thickened components raising concern for cystic mesothelioma"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent after complete excision, with cure and low recurrence. Incomplete removal of lymphatic lesions may be followed by recurrence.","pitfalls":["misinterpreting a large cyst as free ascites on imaging","confusing the lesion with an ovarian cyst in female patients","incomplete excision of a lymphatic cyst leading to recurrence","underestimating the need for adjacent bowel resection when the cyst is inseparable from the mesenteric vessels","overlooking postoperative chylous leak after excision of a lymphatic cyst"],"management":["Complete surgical excision as the definitive treatment, by laparoscopy or laparotomy, with enucleation where possible","Segmental bowel resection when the cyst is inseparable from adjacent bowel or its blood supply","Urgent surgery for acute complications such as torsion, intracystic haemorrhage, infection, rupture or bowel obstruction","Histopathological confirmation with immunohistochemistry to define origin and exclude malignancy","Marsupialisation or sclerotherapy only when complete excision is not feasible, accepting a higher recurrence risk","Follow-up for recurrence, particularly after incomplete removal of lymphatic lesions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Schwartz's Principles of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","Ashcraft's Pediatric Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"mesenteric_vascular_insufficiency":{"name":"Mesenteric Vascular Insufficiency","system":"Gastrointestinal / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Intestinal ischemia","Mesenteric ischemia","Chronic mesenteric ischemia (CMI)","Acute mesenteric ischemia (AMI)","Intestinal angina","Nonocclusive mesenteric ischemia (NOMI)","Mesenteric venous thrombosis","Ischemic colitis"],"chapter":340,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2586","clinicalPearls":["Intestinal ischemia arises when splanchnic perfusion cannot meet the metabolic demands of the gut; it is broadly divided into chronic mesenteric ischemia (CMI) and acute mesenteric ischemia (AMI), with the latter carrying a high mortality (~50-80%) and depending critically on early diagnosis and prompt intervention (p.2586).","AMI is mechanistically categorized into three patterns: arterio-occlusive (embolic or thrombotic), nonocclusive (vasospastic/hypoperfusion), and mesenteric venous thrombosis (p.2587).","Roughly half of AMI cases are embolic, with emboli typically lodging in the mid-to-distal superior mesenteric artery (SMA) just beyond the origin of the middle colic artery; about 25-30% are acute-on-chronic thrombosis on preexisting mesenteric atherosclerosis (p.2586-2587).","The hallmark of acute arterial mesenteric ischemia is severe, abrupt, nonremitting abdominal pain that is strikingly out of proportion to physical examination findings, present in roughly 95% of cases (p.2588).","Pain is disproportionate to exam because ischemia begins in the mucosal layer and advances outward toward the serosa, so peritoneal signs and cardiovascular collapse are late developments (p.2588).","CMI classically presents with insidious, recurrent dull crampy postprandial epigastric pain ('intestinal angina'), fear of eating, and consequent weight loss, often over 6-12 months, in a malnourished patient with other signs of atherosclerosis (p.2587).","CT angiography with 1-mm or thinner cuts is the gold-standard diagnostic test for both acute and chronic mesenteric arterial occlusive disease; a venous-phase study aids detection of mesenteric venous thrombosis (p.2587-2588).","Fasting mesenteric duplex ultrasound is a useful screening tool: a high SMA peak flow velocity carries about an 80% positive predictive value, and a negative duplex study virtually excludes mesenteric ischemia (p.2587).","In suspected AMI the decision to operate is frequently driven by a high index of suspicion from history and exam despite normal laboratory values, and surgical consultation should not be delayed (p.2588).","Mesenteric venous thrombosis (<10% of cases) tends to be associated with hypercoagulable states; documented inherited thrombophilia warrants long-term anticoagulation (p.2587, p.2588-2589).","Severity of ischemic colonic mucosal injury is graded by colonoscopy from mild (minimal erythema) to moderate (pale ulcerations extending into muscularis) to severe (black/green discoloration indicating full-thickness necrosis) (p.2589)."],"pathophysiology":"The intestine is perfused by the celiac artery, superior mesenteric artery (SMA), and inferior mesenteric artery (IMA), with extensive collateralization through the mesenteric arcades; small-bowel collaterals converge at the duodenum and pancreatic bed, while colonic collaterals meet at the splenic flexure (Griffiths' point) and the descending/sigmoid colon (Sudeck's point), the watershed zones most prone to colonic ischemia. The splanchnic bed can receive up to ~30% of cardiac output, and protective mechanisms include abundant collaterals, autoregulation of flow, and the capacity to increase oxygen extraction. Occlusive ischemia results from embolic or thrombotic interruption of a major mesenteric artery (emboli, originating from the heart in >75% of cases, preferentially lodge in the SMA distal to the middle colic origin); development of chronic intestinal angina generally requires significant stenosis of two of the three major vessels, with SMA involvement most concerning. CMI reflects failure to mount the normal postprandial hyperemic increase in intestinal blood flow, analogous to cardiac angina, due to atherosclerotic narrowing of the SMA and/or celiac origins. Nonocclusive ischemia represents disproportionate mesenteric arteriolar vasospasm in response to severe physiologic stress such as shock; if untreated, early mucosal stress ulceration progresses to full-thickness injury. Mesenteric venous thrombosis arises from inherited or acquired hypercoagulable states (p.2587).","additionalDifferentials":["Gastrointestinal malignancy (must be excluded in CMI before intervention)","Peptic ulcer disease and other causes of epigastric/postprandial pain","Biliary colic / biliary tract disease","Acute pancreatitis","Inflammatory bowel disease (ischemic colitis is commonly confused with IBD)","Acute intestinal obstruction (especially strangulated obstruction with vascular insufficiency)"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious colitis","Infectious gastroenteritis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease","Diverticular disease / diverticulitis","Colonic neoplasm","Peptic ulcer disease","Biliary tract disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT angiography with thin (1-mm or finer) cuts, including venous phase for venous thrombosis (gold standard)","Fasting mesenteric duplex ultrasound (screening; SMA peak velocity)","Esophagogastroduodenoscopy and colonoscopy (to exclude malignancy in CMI and assess colonic mucosa in ischemic colitis)","Abdominal CT and abdominal ultrasound","Electrocardiogram and echocardiogram (to identify embolic source such as atrial fibrillation; should not delay surgery)","Laboratory markers: leukocytosis, metabolic acidosis, lactic acidosis (support advanced ischemia but do not reliably distinguish reversible ischemia from necrosis)","Hypercoagulability workup in mesenteric venous thrombosis"],"redFlags":["Severe abdominal pain out of proportion to physical findings","Blood per rectum / bloody stools","Peritonitis and cardiovascular collapse (late findings indicating advanced ischemia)","Leukocytosis with metabolic/lactic acidosis suggesting advanced ischemia or necrosis","Black or green colonic mucosal discoloration on colonoscopy indicating full-thickness necrosis","Atrial fibrillation, recent myocardial infarction, or infective endocarditis in a patient with acute abdominal pain (embolic source)"],"prognosis":"Acute mesenteric ischemia carries a mortality rate exceeding 50% and is among the most challenging diagnoses; the timeliness of diagnosis and treatment is the single most important determinant of survival. Chronic mesenteric ischemia managed with revascularization has favorable long-term outcomes, with endovascular stenting associated with about an 80% long-term success rate. Outcomes hinge on early recognition before progression to full-thickness bowel necrosis and peritonitis (p.2587-2588).","pitfalls":["Anchoring on a benign cause and underestimating severity because abdominal pain is markedly out of proportion to a relatively unremarkable exam (p.2588).","Waiting for abnormal laboratory values before acting; lactate, leukocytosis, and acidosis may be normal early or fail to distinguish reversible ischemia from necrosis (p.2588-2589).","Performing mesenteric duplex without fasting, since bowel gas obscures flow and the postprandial vasodilation response (p.2587).","Mistaking ischemic colitis for inflammatory bowel disease (p.2589).","Overlooking nonocclusive ischemia in obtunded, critically ill patients in whom physical findings are obscured by the underlying illness (p.2589).","Failing to recognize that the proximal jejunum may be spared in SMA embolism while the rest of the small bowel to the transverse colon becomes ischemic, leading to incomplete bowel assessment (p.2589)."],"management":["Emergency resuscitation, broad-spectrum antibiotics and systemic heparin anticoagulation for suspected acute mesenteric ischaemia","Urgent revascularisation by surgical embolectomy, endovascular thrombectomy or stenting, or mesenteric bypass, to restore splanchnic flow","Laparotomy with resection of frankly necrotic bowel and a planned second-look procedure to reassess viability","Intra-arterial vasodilator such as papaverine and correction of the low-flow state, avoiding vasoconstrictors, in nonocclusive ischaemia","Anticoagulation as the mainstay for mesenteric venous thrombosis","Elective endovascular stenting or surgical bypass for chronic mesenteric ischaemia, with nutritional repletion of the malnourished patient","Cardiovascular risk factor modification, antiplatelet therapy and treatment of an embolic source such as atrial fibrillation","Vascular surgery-led multidisciplinary care with monitoring for recurrent ischaemia after revascularisation"],"severityClassification":"Ischemic colonic mucosal injury is graded by colonoscopy: mild = minimal mucosal erythema; moderate = pale mucosal ulcerations with extension into the muscular layer; severe = severe ulcerations with black or green mucosal discoloration consistent with full-thickness bowel-wall necrosis. Mechanically, mesenteric ischemia is classified as chronic (CMI) versus acute (AMI), and AMI is subclassified as arterio-occlusive (embolic or thrombotic), nonocclusive (vasospastic/hypoperfusion), or mesenteric venous thrombosis (p.2587, p.2589).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2586","Harrison 22e p.2587","Harrison 22e p.2588","Harrison 22e p.2589"],"crossLinks":[]},"mesenteric_venous_thrombosis":{"name":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MVT","mesenteric vein thrombosis","superior mesenteric vein thrombosis","splanchnic venous thrombosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A less common cause of mesenteric ischaemia, most often involving the superior mesenteric vein and its tributaries","Presentation is frequently subacute with vague, poorly localised abdominal pain evolving over days, in contrast to the abrupt onset of arterial occlusion","The classic teaching is pain out of proportion to abdominal findings, at least early before infarction and peritonitis develop","A hypercoagulable state, whether inherited thrombophilia, malignancy, or acquired prothrombotic conditions, underlies many cases and should be sought","Local factors such as portal hypertension, intra-abdominal inflammation or infection, recent surgery, and pancreatitis also predispose","Contrast-enhanced CT is the investigation of choice, showing thrombus in the mesenteric or portal veins, bowel wall oedema, and signs of compromised perfusion","Prompt systemic anticoagulation is the mainstay when there is no infarction, and it improves outcomes and limits propagation","Bowel infarction, peritonitis, or clinical deterioration mandates surgical exploration with resection of non-viable bowel"],"pathophysiology":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis occurs when thrombus obstructs venous outflow from the bowel, most commonly in the superior mesenteric vein. Impaired venous drainage raises intestinal capillary and interstitial pressure, producing bowel wall oedema, arterial inflow compromise, and progressive congestion. If flow is not restored, transmural ischaemia and infarction ensue, with translocation of bacteria, peritonitis, and sepsis. It is usually driven by hypercoagulable states (primary thrombophilia, malignancy) or local factors such as portal hypertension, abdominal sepsis or inflammation, and recent surgery. The extent of collateral drainage and the speed of thrombus propagation determine whether ischaemia remains reversible.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute arterial mesenteric ischaemia (embolic or thrombotic)","Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia","Bowel obstruction","Perforated viscus","Acute pancreatitis","Other causes of acute abdomen (e.g. appendicitis, diverticulitis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen with venous phase to identify venous thrombus and bowel compromise","Duplex ultrasound of the portal and mesenteric veins in selected cases","Laboratory markers of ischaemia including lactate and acid-base status","Thrombophilia screen and evaluation for underlying malignancy","Assessment for portal hypertension and intra-abdominal inflammatory sources"],"redFlags":["Peritonism, rising lactate, or haemodynamic instability indicating bowel infarction and the need for emergency surgery","Pain out of proportion to examination raising early suspicion before overt signs appear","Gastrointestinal bleeding or clinical deterioration during treatment","Extension into the portal vein or evidence of extensive bowel involvement"],"prognosis":"When diagnosed before infarction and treated promptly with anticoagulation, mesenteric venous thrombosis has a better prognosis than acute arterial mesenteric ischaemia. Delayed diagnosis with established bowel necrosis carries high morbidity and mortality, and recurrence risk depends on the underlying prothrombotic cause and duration of anticoagulation.","pitfalls":["Attributing subacute nonspecific abdominal pain to benign causes and delaying imaging","Failing to obtain venous-phase CT and missing the venous thrombus","Delaying anticoagulation in the absence of infarction","Not investigating for an underlying thrombophilia or malignancy after the acute event"],"management":["Start prompt systemic anticoagulation with therapeutic heparin to arrest thrombus propagation and allow venous recanalisation, transitioning to oral anticoagulation","Institute bowel rest with nasogastric decompression, intravenous fluid resuscitation, and correction of electrolyte and acid-base derangement","Give broad-spectrum antibiotics covering enteric organisms when transmural ischaemia, bacterial translocation, or peritonitis is suspected","Monitor with serial abdominal examination and lactate/acid-base trends to detect progression to infarction","Proceed to laparotomy with resection of frankly infarcted bowel, with second-look laparotomy or catheter-directed thrombolysis/thrombectomy in selected extensive cases","Investigate and treat the underlying driver (thrombophilia, occult malignancy, portal hypertension, intra-abdominal sepsis), tailoring anticoagulation duration to the prothrombotic state","Escalate urgently for surgery once peritonism or established bowel necrosis develops, with vascular surgery and haematology input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"mesothelioma":{"name":"Mesothelioma","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["malignant mesothelioma","malignant pleural mesothelioma","MPM"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Asbestos exposure is the dominant cause, with a characteristically long latency of several decades between exposure and disease onset","The pleura is the most common site, presenting with breathlessness and chest pain, often accompanied by a unilateral pleural effusion; the peritoneum is a less common site","A detailed occupational and environmental exposure history is essential, since exposure may have been remote, indirect, or through household contact","Pleural fluid cytology alone is often insufficient, and definitive diagnosis usually requires tissue biopsy, best obtained by thoracoscopy with immunohistochemical confirmation","The epithelioid subtype carries a better prognosis than the sarcomatoid or biphasic subtypes, which are more aggressive","The disease tends to spread by local extension over the pleural surfaces and encases the lung rather than forming discrete masses, making complete surgical clearance difficult","Management is usually multimodal and largely palliative, combining platinum-based chemotherapy, increasingly with immunotherapy, alongside radiotherapy and surgery in selected fit patients","Effusion control with pleurodesis or an indwelling catheter and good symptom management are central to care, and cases often carry medicolegal and compensation implications"],"pathophysiology":"Malignant mesothelioma arises from the mesothelial cells lining the pleural, peritoneal, or, rarely, pericardial cavities. Inhaled asbestos fibres, particularly amphibole types, are retained in tissues and provoke chronic inflammation, oxidative injury, and DNA damage over many years, leading to malignant transformation. The tumour characteristically grows as a diffuse rind along serosal surfaces, encasing the lung and invading the chest wall, diaphragm, and mediastinum. Histological subtypes range from epithelioid through biphasic to the more aggressive sarcomatoid form.","additionalDifferentials":["Metastatic adenocarcinoma to the pleura","Primary lung carcinoma with pleural involvement","Benign asbestos-related pleural disease and pleural plaques","Reactive pleuritis or empyema","Pleural lymphoma or other rare pleural tumours","Peritoneal carcinomatosis for peritoneal presentations"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph and contrast-enhanced CT of the chest to assess pleural thickening and effusion","Pleural fluid aspiration for cytology, recognising its limited sensitivity","Thoracoscopic or image-guided pleural biopsy with immunohistochemistry for definitive diagnosis","MRI or PET-CT to define local extent and staging in surgical candidates","Detailed documentation of asbestos exposure history for clinical and medicolegal purposes"],"redFlags":["Progressive breathlessness with a large or recurrent unilateral pleural effusion","Persistent chest wall pain suggesting local tumour invasion","Rapid clinical deterioration or weight loss indicating aggressive disease","Superior vena cava obstruction or extensive chest wall involvement"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally poor with limited long-term survival; the epithelioid subtype, early stage, and good performance status favour longer survival, while sarcomatoid histology and advanced disease predict rapid progression.","pitfalls":["Relying on pleural fluid cytology alone and delaying tissue biopsy","Failing to elicit a remote or indirect asbestos exposure history","Overlooking the medicolegal and compensation dimensions of the diagnosis","Offering aggressive surgery to patients unlikely to benefit given subtype or stage"],"management":["Offer systemic therapy with platinum-pemetrexed chemotherapy, increasingly combined with immune checkpoint inhibition (nivolumab plus ipilimumab) as first-line treatment","Consider lung-preserving surgery (pleurectomy/decortication) within a multimodality plan for selected early epithelioid disease, with radiotherapy for local control or symptom palliation","Control the recurrent pleural effusion and breathlessness with indwelling pleural catheter drainage or talc pleurodesis","Provide early palliative care with opioid analgesia and neuropathic agents for chest-wall pain and breathlessness","Monitor disease with interval contrast-enhanced CT to detect progression and guide further lines of therapy","Manage through a thoracic oncology multidisciplinary team including surgery, oncology, respiratory medicine, and palliative care","Document the asbestos exposure history and advise the patient on compensation and medicolegal entitlements"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology"],"crossLinks":[]},"metabolic_alkalosis":{"name":"Metabolic alkalosis","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["metabolic alkalaemia","chloride-responsive alkalosis","chloride-resistant alkalosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Metabolic alkalosis is a primary rise in serum bicarbonate with a compensatory increase in carbon dioxide from reduced ventilation","Understanding it requires separating the process that generates the alkalosis from the factors that allow the kidney to maintain it","Urine chloride is the single most useful test for classification, dividing cases into chloride-responsive (low urine chloride) and chloride-resistant (high urine chloride) forms","Chloride-responsive alkalosis, from vomiting, nasogastric suction, or diuretics, is accompanied by volume and chloride depletion and corrects with saline and potassium repletion","Chloride-resistant alkalosis reflects mineralocorticoid excess or severe potassium depletion and does not respond to saline, so the underlying disorder must be treated","Hypokalaemia both contributes to and is worsened by alkalosis, and the two often need correcting together","Maintenance of the alkalosis depends on factors such as volume contraction, chloride depletion, potassium depletion, and mineralocorticoid activity that impair renal bicarbonate excretion","Severe alkalaemia can cause neuromuscular irritability, arrhythmias, and reduced tissue oxygen delivery, and may need more aggressive correction"],"pathophysiology":"Metabolic alkalosis arises when there is a net gain of bicarbonate or a loss of hydrogen ions, but its persistence depends on the kidney's inability to excrete the excess bicarbonate. Normally the kidney can excrete a large bicarbonate load rapidly, so an alkalosis is only sustained when a maintenance factor is present. Generation occurs through loss of acid from the upper gastrointestinal tract or the kidney, or from a gain of alkali. Maintenance results from conditions that increase renal bicarbonate reabsorption, including reduced effective circulating volume, chloride depletion, potassium depletion, and excess mineralocorticoid activity, all of which enhance sodium and bicarbonate reabsorption and hydrogen secretion. In chloride-responsive states, volume and chloride depletion drive the maintenance and are corrected by saline; in chloride-resistant states, ongoing mineralocorticoid excess or profound potassium loss sustains the alkalosis despite adequate volume.","additionalDifferentials":["Vomiting or nasogastric suction","Diuretic therapy","Primary hyperaldosteronism and other mineralocorticoid excess states","Cushing syndrome","Bartter and Gitelman syndromes","Milk-alkali syndrome or excessive alkali administration","Severe potassium or magnesium depletion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial or venous blood gas with serum bicarbonate to confirm the primary alkalosis and assess compensation","Serum electrolytes including potassium, chloride, and magnesium","Urine chloride to classify chloride-responsive versus chloride-resistant disease","Assessment of volume status and blood pressure","Plasma renin and aldosterone when a mineralocorticoid cause is suspected","Review of medications and history for vomiting, diuretic, or laxative use"],"redFlags":["Severe alkalaemia causing tetany, seizures, or cardiac arrhythmia","Profound hypokalaemia with muscle weakness or arrhythmia risk","Marked volume depletion with haemodynamic compromise","Hypertension with hypokalaemic alkalosis suggesting mineralocorticoid excess"],"prognosis":"Most cases resolve readily once the generating cause is removed and the maintenance factors (volume, chloride, and potassium depletion) are corrected. Chloride-resistant forms require treatment of the underlying disorder and may persist otherwise. Severe alkalaemia is dangerous but usually reversible with appropriate management, and overall outcome is governed by the underlying condition.","pitfalls":["Giving saline to a chloride-resistant alkalosis that will not respond","Failing to measure urine chloride and thereby misclassifying the disorder","Neglecting concurrent potassium and magnesium deficits that perpetuate the alkalosis","Overlooking surreptitious vomiting or diuretic use as the underlying cause"],"management":["Identify and remove the generating cause, stopping loop or thiazide diuretics and controlling vomiting or nasogastric losses with antiemetics and acid-suppression","Correct chloride-responsive disease by restoring volume and chloride with sodium chloride solution alongside potassium chloride repletion","Replace potassium and magnesium, as hypokalaemia both drives and worsens the alkalosis","Treat chloride-resistant disease by addressing the underlying mineralocorticoid excess, using an aldosterone antagonist or definitive management of an adrenal source","Use the carbonic anhydrase inhibitor acetazolamide in selected volume-overloaded patients in whom saline loading is undesirable","Reserve haemodialysis or dilute hydrochloric acid infusion for severe alkalaemia with renal impairment unresponsive to other measures","Monitor blood gases and electrolytes, watching for arrhythmia and compensatory hypoventilation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"metachromatic_leukodystrophy":{"name":"Metachromatic leukodystrophy","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MLD","arylsulfatase A deficiency","sulfatide lipidosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard metabolic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive leukodystrophy caused by deficiency of the enzyme arylsulfatase A, leading to accumulation of sulfatides and progressive demyelination of central and peripheral nervous systems","The late-infantile form is most common, presenting in the second year with gait disturbance, weakness, and loss of previously acquired motor skills","Peripheral neuropathy with depressed or absent deep tendon reflexes is characteristic and can accompany central signs","Juvenile and adult forms present later, often with behavioural change, cognitive decline, or psychiatric symptoms before motor deterioration","MRI shows symmetrical confluent white matter signal change, sometimes with a tigroid or leopard-skin pattern of spared perivascular regions","Elevated cerebrospinal fluid protein and slowed nerve conduction reflect the demyelinating process","A rare subset is due to deficiency of the sphingolipid activator protein saposin B rather than the enzyme itself","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation and, in selected presymptomatic patients, gene therapy may modify the course, while established neurological damage is not reversed"],"pathophysiology":"Metachromatic leukodystrophy results from biallelic pathogenic variants in ARSA, encoding the lysosomal enzyme arylsulfatase A, which desulfates sulfatide (cerebroside sulfate). Deficient activity causes sulfatides to accumulate within lysosomes of myelin-forming cells (oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells) and other tissues. The accumulated sulfatide is toxic to myelin-producing cells, driving progressive demyelination of the central and peripheral nervous system and the resulting motor, cognitive, and neuropathic decline. Stored material stains metachromatically, giving the disease its name.","additionalDifferentials":["Krabbe disease","Other leukodystrophies such as X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy","GM1 and GM2 gangliosidoses","Acquired demyelinating disorders","Multiple sulfatase deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assay of arylsulfatase A enzyme activity, interpreted with awareness of pseudodeficiency alleles","ARSA molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Urinary sulfatide measurement to support diagnosis","Brain MRI to characterise white matter involvement","Nerve conduction studies and cerebrospinal fluid protein assessment"],"redFlags":["Progressive loss of motor milestones in a previously well toddler","Combined central and peripheral neurological signs","Rapid cognitive or psychiatric deterioration in older patients","Feeding and swallowing difficulty with aspiration risk"],"prognosis":"The late-infantile form progresses rapidly to severe disability and death within a few years of onset. Juvenile and adult forms progress more slowly and variably. Presymptomatic or very early treatment with stem cell transplantation or gene therapy offers the best chance of altering the course, but symptomatic disease responds poorly.","pitfalls":["Being misled by pseudodeficiency alleles that lower measured enzyme activity without causing disease","Attributing early behavioural or psychiatric changes in adults to a primary psychiatric disorder","Missing the peripheral neuropathy that helps distinguish it from other leukodystrophies","Delaying assessment for disease-modifying therapy until symptoms are advanced"],"management":["Offer disease-modifying therapy to presymptomatic or very early patients: autologous ex-vivo ARSA gene-corrected haematopoietic stem cell gene therapy (atidarsagene autotemcel) or allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in slower-progressing juvenile and adult forms","Manage spasticity with antispasticity agents such as baclofen supplemented by botulinum toxin, alongside physiotherapy and positioning","Control seizures with antiepileptic drugs and treat neuropathic pain and dystonia","Provide feeding support with gastrostomy, secretion management, and respiratory care as bulbar and motor function decline","Undertake surveillance with serial brain MRI, developmental and neurological review, and monitoring for gallbladder disease","Coordinate care through a metabolic, neurology, rehabilitation, and palliative care multidisciplinary team","Offer genetic counselling and test at-risk siblings for ARSA variants and enzyme deficiency, since presymptomatic identification enables curative early treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"metachromatic_variant_leukodystrophy":{"name":"Multiple sulfatase deficiency / metachromatic-spectrum leukodystrophy variant","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["multiple sulfatase deficiency","MSD","Austin disease","SUMF1","metachromatic-spectrum leukodystrophy variant","sulfatase-modifying factor 1 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurology and inborn-errors-of-metabolism textbooks with peer-reviewed reviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic variants in SUMF1, encoding the formylglycine-generating enzyme required to activate all sulfatases","Because every sulfatase is deficient, the phenotype combines metachromatic leukodystrophy with features of several mucopolysaccharidoses and X-linked ichthyosis","Neurological picture overlaps late-infantile metachromatic leukodystrophy, with regression of milestones, spasticity, and peripheral neuropathy","Somatic clues include coarse facial features, skeletal dysostosis, organomegaly, and ichthyotic skin, distinguishing it from isolated arylsulfatase A deficiency","Peripheral demyelinating neuropathy with slowed nerve conduction is a useful supportive finding, mirroring classic metachromatic leukodystrophy","MRI demonstrates symmetrical, confluent periventricular white-matter signal change, sometimes with a tigroid or leopard-skin sparing pattern within the affected white matter","Biochemical hallmark is combined deficiency of multiple sulfatases with raised urinary glycosaminoglycans and sulfatides","Diagnosis is confirmed by demonstrating deficient activity of several sulfatases and by SUMF1 genetic testing; no curative therapy exists"],"pathophysiology":"In multiple sulfatase deficiency, biallelic variants in SUMF1 impair the formylglycine-generating enzyme that performs a post-translational modification essential for the catalytic activity of every sulfatase. As a result all sulfatases are simultaneously deficient, so sulfated substrates accumulate across multiple pathways: sulfatides build up in myelin (as in metachromatic leukodystrophy), glycosaminoglycans accumulate (as in mucopolysaccharidoses), and cholesterol sulfate accumulates in skin (as in X-linked ichthyosis). The combined storage produces central and peripheral demyelination alongside the somatic, skeletal, and dermatological features of the overlapping disorders.","additionalDifferentials":["Classic metachromatic leukodystrophy from isolated arylsulfatase A deficiency","Mucopolysaccharidoses (particularly those with neurological involvement)","Krabbe disease (globoid cell leukodystrophy)","GM1/GM2 gangliosidoses","X-linked ichthyosis considered in isolation for the skin findings","Other late-infantile neurodegenerative regression syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assay of multiple sulfatase activities demonstrating combined deficiency","Urinary glycosaminoglycan and sulfatide analysis","Nerve conduction studies to document demyelinating peripheral neuropathy","Brain MRI to characterise the confluent periventricular leukodystrophy pattern","SUMF1 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Rapid developmental regression with loss of previously acquired skills","Progressive bulbar dysfunction with aspiration risk","New-onset seizures or status epilepticus","Airway compromise from combined storage-related upper-airway and skeletal changes"],"prognosis":"The disorder is progressive with a poor prognosis, especially in neonatal and late-infantile presentations, which typically lead to severe disability and early death; later-onset forms progress more slowly but remain relentless. No disease-modifying treatment is currently available.","pitfalls":["Testing only arylsulfatase A and diagnosing isolated metachromatic leukodystrophy while missing the combined deficiency","Overlooking the somatic and dermatological clues (coarse features, ichthyosis, dysostosis) that point away from an isolated leukodystrophy","Attributing the neuropathy solely to a central process and not testing nerve conduction","Failing to recognise the multisystem nature when planning surveillance and supportive care"],"management":["Provide supportive care as no therapy corrects the combined sulfatase deficiency, directing treatment at the individual manifestations","Control seizures with antiepileptic drugs and manage spasticity with antispasticity agents and physiotherapy","Support feeding and nutrition and manage respiratory secretions as neurological regression advances","Treat the ichthyotic skin with emollients and keratolytic agents","Undertake surveillance for the mucopolysaccharidosis-type complications, including skeletal, cardiac valve, hearing, ophthalmic, and organomegaly monitoring","Coordinate multidisciplinary care with early involvement of palliative services","Offer genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance of SUMF1 variants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews / standard inborn-errors-of-metabolism references on Multiple Sulfatase Deficiency","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"metagonimiasis":{"name":"Metagonimiasis (intestinal fluke)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Metagonimus yokogawai infection","Yokogawa's fluke","minute intestinal fluke infection","heterophyid intestinal fluke disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / Mandell","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["One of the smallest human intestinal flukes, acquired by eating raw or undercooked freshwater fish carrying metacercariae, particularly in East Asia","Light infection is usually asymptomatic; heavier burdens cause colicky abdominal pain, intermittent diarrhoea, nausea and lethargy","Adult flukes attach to the mucosa of the small intestine and provoke local inflammation and mild malabsorption","Diagnosis relies on finding small operculated eggs in stool that closely resemble those of Opisthorchis, Clonorchis and other heterophyids","Rarely, eggs or flukes enter the circulation and embolise to the heart or brain, causing ectopic disease","Praziquantel is highly effective and the treatment of choice","Prevention centres on avoiding raw freshwater fish and improving sanitation to reduce egg contamination"],"pathophysiology":"Metagonimus yokogawai is a minute intestinal trematode whose life cycle passes through freshwater snails and then freshwater fish, in which infective metacercariae encyst. Humans and other fish-eating animals acquire infection by consuming raw or undercooked fish. The larvae excyst in the small intestine and mature into tiny adult flukes that burrow among the villi of the jejunum, causing localised mucosal inflammation, villous flattening and superficial ulceration. Heavy infection can impair absorption and produce chronic diarrhoea. Occasionally eggs deposited in the mucosa penetrate vessels and are carried to distant organs such as the myocardium or central nervous system, where they excite a granulomatous reaction. Eggs excreted in faeces continue the cycle by contaminating freshwater.","additionalDifferentials":["Heterophyiasis and other heterophyid intestinal fluke infection","Fasciolopsiasis and other intestinal trematode disease","Giardiasis and other protozoal enteritis","Opisthorchiasis or clonorchiasis (from egg morphology overlap)","Irritable bowel syndrome or functional dyspepsia","Chronic bacterial or viral gastroenteritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy for ova and parasites with concentration techniques","Repeated stool examination given intermittent and low egg output","Full blood count for eosinophilia","Careful egg morphometry to distinguish from other small trematode eggs","Imaging or echocardiography only if ectopic (cardiac or cerebral) disease is suspected","Serology where available to support the diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Neurological deficit or seizures suggesting cerebral egg embolisation","Cardiac symptoms raising concern for myocardial involvement","Persistent diarrhoea with weight loss and evidence of malabsorption","Failure to respond to treatment, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent for intestinal disease, which resolves promptly with anthelmintic therapy; the rare ectopic cardiac or cerebral forms can be serious depending on the site affected.","pitfalls":["Misidentifying the eggs as those of opisthorchiid liver flukes because of near-identical morphology","Overlooking light infections when only a single stool sample is examined","Attributing non-specific gastrointestinal symptoms to functional disease in endemic settings","Failing to consider ectopic embolisation when unexplained cardiac or neurological signs appear"],"management":["Treat with the anthelmintic praziquantel, which is highly effective and the agent of choice for intestinal fluke infection","Provide supportive rehydration and correct any malabsorption or nutritional deficit from heavy infection","Confirm cure with follow-up stool microscopy, allowing for intermittent low egg output","Treat household members and others who shared the same raw or undercooked freshwater fish source","Manage rare ectopic cardiac or cerebral disease with anthelmintic therapy plus organ-specific supportive or surgical care and relevant specialist referral","Advise on prevention through thorough cooking of freshwater fish and avoidance of raw preparations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"metaphyseal_dysplasia_braun_tinschert":{"name":"Metaphyseal dysplasia, Braun-Tinschert type","system":"Skeletal dysplasia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Braun-Tinschert metaphyseal dysplasia","Metaphyseal dysplasia with exostosis-like changes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Skeletal dysplasia references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare autosomal dominant metaphyseal undermodelling disorder first delineated by Braun and Tinschert in an affected father and son, confirming vertical (dominant) transmission.","Hallmark is symmetrical broadening (undermodelling) of the metaphyses of the long tubular bones, most conspicuous around the knees, where the normal funnelling of metaphysis into diaphysis fails.","Exostosis-like bony projections arise from the affected metaphyses and are readily mistaken for hereditary multiple exostoses.","Bowing of the forearm bones occurs, with a tendency to radial head subluxation or dislocation and limitation of forearm rotation.","Stature is usually normal or near-normal, which helps separate it from many other metaphyseal dysplasias that cause disproportionate short stature.","Diaphyses and epiphyses are relatively spared and the spine is typically uninvolved, so involvement is essentially confined to the metaphyses.","Presentation is in childhood with limb deformity, a bony lump, or an incidental radiographic finding; intellect and life expectancy are normal.","Diagnosis is largely clinical and radiographic, made by careful exclusion of the better-defined metaphyseal and exostosing disorders."],"pathophysiology":"Metaphyseal dysplasia of the Braun-Tinschert type is an autosomal dominant skeletal dysplasia characterised by defective metaphyseal remodelling, so that the tubulation (funnelling) of the metaphysis towards the diaphysis is incomplete and the metaphyses remain broad and undermodelled. It was delineated as a distinct entity by Braun and Tinschert on the basis of a father-and-son pair, indicating dominant inheritance. The exostosis-like outgrowths at the metaphyseal margins and the bowing of the forearm bones point to a disturbance of perichondrial and metaphyseal osteogenesis and of cortical modelling at the periphery of the growth plate. Unlike several sibling metaphyseal disorders, no single causative gene has been conclusively established for this rare condition; it is defined on clinical and radiographic grounds and separated from the metaphyseal chondrodysplasias (such as the Schmid, Jansen and McKusick types) and from disorders of osteoclastic remodelling such as Pyle disease. The absence of significant epiphyseal or spinal involvement and the preservation of stature distinguish it from the spondylometaphyseal and spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasias.","additionalDifferentials":["Hereditary multiple exostoses (EXT1/EXT2) — the closest clinical mimic because of the exostosis-like projections","Pyle metaphyseal dysplasia (SFRP4) — marked metaphyseal flaring but different distribution","Metaphyseal chondrodysplasia, Schmid type (COL10A1)","Craniometaphyseal dysplasia (ANKH) — metaphyseal undermodelling with cranial sclerosis","Dysplasia epiphysealis hemimelica (Trevor disease)","Metachondromatosis (PTPN11) — combined exostoses and enchondromas","Enchondromatosis (Ollier disease) affecting the metaphyses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full skeletal survey with dedicated views of the knees, forearms and wrists to map the metaphyseal changes","Comparison radiographs of affected relatives to confirm the dominant pattern and characteristic phenotype","Elbow and forearm imaging to assess radial head position and forearm rotation","Serum calcium, phosphate and alkaline phosphatase to exclude a metabolic bone disorder (interpreted qualitatively)","Skeletal dysplasia gene panel or targeted testing to exclude defined disorders such as hereditary multiple exostoses","Three-generation family pedigree and clinical examination of first-degree relatives","Orthopaedic assessment of joint alignment, deformity and range of movement"],"redFlags":["Progressive forearm deformity with restricted rotation or radial head dislocation","A rapidly enlarging bony mass raising concern for malignant change (rare) and warranting cross-sectional imaging","Neurovascular compromise from an expanding or projecting metaphyseal lesion"],"prognosis":"The outlook is generally favourable, with normal stature, intellect and life expectancy. Morbidity is largely orthopaedic, relating to forearm deformity, limitation of movement and cosmetic concern from the metaphyseal outgrowths.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosis as hereditary multiple exostoses, leading to inappropriate surveillance and inaccurate genetic counselling","Assuming an underlying metabolic bone disease when biochemistry is normal","Overlooking the autosomal dominant inheritance and failing to examine and counsel relatives","Using preserved stature to wrongly exclude a skeletal dysplasia"],"management":["Manage supportively as there is no disease-specific drug therapy, centring care on the orthopaedic manifestations","Observe with periodic clinical and radiographic monitoring of the metaphyseal outgrowths, forearm bowing, and radial head position","Undertake orthopaedic surgery for symptomatic exostosis-like projections and for forearm deformity or radial head subluxation limiting rotation","Use physiotherapy to preserve forearm rotation and upper-limb function, with analgesia for discomfort","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance, examining and imaging first-degree relatives","Refer to clinical genetics and orthopaedics for coordinated assessment and management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nosology and classification of genetic skeletal disorders","Comprehensive reference texts of skeletal dysplasias","Clinical genetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"metatarsalgia":{"name":"Metatarsalgia","system":"Musculoskeletal / Foot & Ankle","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["forefoot pain","metatarsal head pain","forefoot overload"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Metatarsalgia is a symptom of pain under the metatarsal heads from forefoot overload rather than a single diagnosis, so the underlying cause must be sought","Pain is typically felt on the plantar aspect of the forefoot, worse on weight bearing and in hard or high-heeled shoes, and often accompanied by plantar callosities under the affected heads","Mechanical contributors include a tight gastrocnemius, cavus or splayed forefoot, hallux disorders that transfer load laterally, and lesser-toe deformities that plantarflex the metatarsal heads","Transfer metatarsalgia describes overload of neighbouring metatarsals when one ray is short, elevated, or has been altered by hallux surgery","Distinguish diffuse mechanical metatarsalgia from focal causes such as interdigital neuroma, plantar plate tear, Freiberg's infraction, stress fracture, and sesamoid pathology","Careful palpation localises tenderness to the metatarsal head, the intermetatarsal space, or the metatarsophalangeal joint, guiding the differential","Weight-bearing radiographs assess metatarsal length and alignment, while ultrasound or MRI evaluates soft-tissue and bony causes","Management is directed at the cause and includes metatarsal offloading orthoses and bars, cushioned footwear, calf stretching, activity modification, and correction of contributing deformity"],"pathophysiology":"Metatarsalgia arises when load beneath the lesser metatarsal heads exceeds what the forefoot soft tissues can tolerate, causing pain, inflammation, and protective callosity. Normal forefoot loading depends on even metatarsal length and alignment, a mobile first ray that shares weight, an adequate plantar fat pad, and balanced toe function. Disturbance of any of these concentrates pressure: a tight gastrocnemius increases forefoot load throughout stance, hallux valgus or hallux rigidus shifts weight laterally, lesser-toe clawing or hammering plantarflexes the metatarsal heads, and a long or plantarflexed metatarsal bears excess load. Distal migration or atrophy of the protective plantar fat pad, common with age, removes cushioning. When one ray is shortened, elevated, or surgically altered, adjacent rays absorb the redistributed force, producing transfer metatarsalgia. The result is localised or diffuse plantar forefoot pain that reflects the specific mechanical derangement.","additionalDifferentials":["Interdigital (Morton's) neuroma","Plantar plate tear or metatarsophalangeal joint instability","Freiberg's infraction of a metatarsal head","Metatarsal stress fracture","Sesamoiditis and other sesamoid disorders","Inflammatory arthritis of the metatarsophalangeal joints","Plantar fibroma or other soft-tissue mass"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Standing clinical examination with palpation of metatarsal heads, intermetatarsal spaces, and joints","Assessment of gastrocnemius tightness, forefoot alignment, and associated hallux and lesser-toe deformity","Weight-bearing foot radiographs to assess metatarsal length, alignment, and joint change","Ultrasound to evaluate for interdigital neuroma or plantar plate pathology","MRI where stress fracture, Freiberg's infraction, or occult soft-tissue lesion is suspected","Pedobarography to map the distribution of forefoot loading","Inflammatory markers and serology if inflammatory arthropathy is considered"],"redFlags":["Focal bony tenderness and swelling suggesting a metatarsal stress fracture, particularly with a change in activity","Rest or night pain, or a destructive lesion on imaging, suggesting infection or tumour","Systemic or polyarticular features indicating inflammatory arthritis","Plantar ulceration or sensory loss in a neuropathic or diabetic foot","Progressive lesser-toe deformity with instability indicating an evolving plantar plate tear"],"prognosis":"Most mechanical metatarsalgia responds well to identifying and correcting the underlying cause with footwear, orthoses, calf stretching, and activity modification. Outcomes are good when contributing deformity is addressed, whereas symptoms persist or recur if the mechanical driver, such as a tight gastrocnemius or uncorrected toe deformity, is overlooked. Focal causes have their own prognosis and may require targeted intervention.","pitfalls":["Treating metatarsalgia as a diagnosis rather than identifying the specific underlying cause","Missing a focal cause such as stress fracture, neuroma, or plantar plate tear behind diffuse forefoot pain","Overlooking a tight gastrocnemius that maintains forefoot overload","Failing to recognise transfer metatarsalgia following hallux surgery or with a short or elevated ray","Neglecting fat pad atrophy and footwear factors in older patients"],"management":["Identify and correct the underlying mechanical driver rather than treating pain alone, as metatarsalgia is a symptom of forefoot overload","Modify footwear to cushioned, wide, low-heeled shoes with a stiff or rocker sole to reduce forefoot pressure","Prescribe orthoses with metatarsal pads, domes, or bars and custom insoles to offload the affected metatarsal heads","Advise gastrocnemius and calf stretching, activity modification, weight optimisation, and callus care","Target specific focal causes appropriately, such as interdigital neuroma, plantar plate tear, Freiberg's infraction, or stress fracture","Consider corrective surgery such as metatarsal osteotomy or correction of hallux and lesser-toe deformity for refractory structural cases","Refer to podiatry or orthopaedics when symptoms persist despite conservative measures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"metatarsus_adductus":{"name":"Metatarsus adductus","system":"Paediatric orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["metatarsus varus","forefoot adduction","MTA","hooked forefoot"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest congenital foot deformity of infancy, thought to reflect intrauterine moulding, giving a foot that curves inward at the forefoot while the hindfoot stays neutral","The forefoot is adducted and sometimes supinated, producing a C-shaped or bean-shaped lateral border with a prominent base of the fifth metatarsal","The heel bisector line normally passes through the second or third toe web; in metatarsus adductus it points more laterally toward the third to fifth toes, grading severity","Flexibility is the key clinical distinction: most feet can be passively abducted past the midline (mild) or to the midline (moderate), and only rigid feet resist correction","The vast majority resolve spontaneously without any intervention, so reassurance and observation are appropriate for flexible feet","There is a recognised association with developmental dysplasia of the hip, so the hips should always be examined and imaged if clinically indicated","Serial casting or reverse-last footwear is reserved for feet that remain rigid or fail to improve, and surgery is rarely needed"],"pathophysiology":"Metatarsus adductus is a transverse-plane deformity in which the metatarsals deviate medially at the tarsometatarsal joints while the hindfoot remains in normal alignment, distinguishing it from clubfoot which also involves hindfoot equinus and varus. It is generally regarded as a positional or packaging deformity resulting from crowding in utero, although imbalance of the medial soft tissues and abnormal insertion of the tibialis anterior or posterior have been implicated in more rigid forms. The medial soft tissues and the abductor hallucis may be tight, and in persistent cases bony adaptation of the medial cuneiform and metatarsal bases can develop, but the subtalar and ankle joints are typically unaffected.","additionalDifferentials":["Clubfoot (congenital talipes equinovarus) with associated hindfoot equinus and varus","Skewfoot (serpentine foot) with forefoot adduction plus hindfoot valgus","Residual forefoot adduction following treated clubfoot","Metatarsus primus varus contributing to juvenile hallux valgus","Internal tibial torsion producing an in-toeing appearance without a true foot deformity","Neuromuscular forefoot deformity in conditions such as cerebral palsy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of forefoot flexibility and the heel bisector angle to grade severity","Examination of the hindfoot to exclude equinus and confirm a supple heel","Hip examination, with ultrasound or radiography where developmental dysplasia is suspected","Standing or simulated weight-bearing foot radiographs reserved for rigid or atypical deformity","Assessment of the contralateral foot and lower-limb rotational profile","Neurological examination to exclude an underlying neuromuscular cause in rigid or asymmetrical cases"],"redFlags":["A rigid deformity that cannot be passively corrected, suggesting a structural rather than positional cause","Associated hindfoot equinus or varus indicating clubfoot rather than isolated forefoot adduction","Concurrent signs of hip dysplasia such as limited abduction or leg-length discrepancy","Progressive, painful, or markedly asymmetrical deformity, or features suggesting an underlying neuromuscular disorder"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent, as most flexible cases correct spontaneously within the first year or two of life; even moderate deformity usually responds well to serial casting, and long-term function and comfort are rarely impaired.","pitfalls":["Mistaking metatarsus adductus for clubfoot by overlooking the normal, supple hindfoot","Failing to examine the hips and thereby missing coexisting developmental dysplasia","Overtreating flexible feet that would have resolved with simple observation","Delaying casting in the rare truly rigid foot, when earlier intervention is more effective","Confusing the condition with skewfoot, in which uncorrected hindfoot valgus needs recognition"],"management":["Reassure and observe flexible feet, as the large majority correct spontaneously within the first years of life","Teach parents passive stretching exercises for mild to moderate flexible deformity","Apply serial casting for rigid or persistent deformity, with corrective footwear or orthoses following cast treatment","Reserve surgery for the rare severe rigid deformity persisting into later childhood","Examine the hips and image with ultrasound or radiography given the association with developmental dysplasia of the hip","Refer rigid, atypical, or asymmetrical cases to paediatric orthopaedics and exclude an underlying neuromuscular cause","Monitor foot alignment and growth over time"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Lovell and Winter's Pediatric Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"metatropic_dysplasia":{"name":"Metatropic dysplasia","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TRPV4","metatropic dwarfism","hyperplastic dysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by gain-of-function variants in TRPV4, a calcium-permeable cation channel; most cases are autosomal dominant, frequently arising de novo","The name reflects a 'changing' body proportion: relatively long trunk with short limbs at birth that evolves into a short trunk as progressive kyphoscoliosis develops","A small coccygeal skin-covered 'tail' overlying an elongated coccyx is a characteristic and highly suggestive external feature","Radiographs show severe platyspondyly with dumbbell-shaped shortened long bones due to marked metaphyseal flaring","Progressive kyphoscoliosis and cervical spine instability (odontoid hypoplasia, atlantoaxial subluxation) are the dominant clinical problems","Presents with a large joints that appear prominent, contractures, and restricted movement; joint hypermobility may coexist","The TRPV4 channelopathy spectrum overlaps other skeletal dysplasias (spondylometaphyseal dysplasia Kozlowski type) and neuropathies, so phenotype varies widely"],"pathophysiology":"Metatropic dysplasia results from gain-of-function variants in TRPV4, which encodes a widely expressed calcium-permeable transient receptor potential channel important in chondrocyte and osteoblast signalling. Abnormal channel gating disturbs endochondral ossification, producing severe platyspondyly and dumbbell-shaped long bones with exuberant metaphyseal flaring. The progressive nature reflects worsening vertebral collapse and kyphoscoliosis over time, which transforms an initially long trunk into a short trunk. Cervical instability arises from odontoid hypoplasia and ligamentous laxity, posing a risk of cord compression.","additionalDifferentials":["Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita","Kniest dysplasia","Diastrophic dysplasia","Spondylometaphyseal dysplasia (Kozlowski type)","Morquio syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis IV)","Fibrochondrogenesis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full skeletal survey to document platyspondyly, dumbbell long bones and metaphyseal flaring","TRPV4 molecular genetic testing (targeted or via a skeletal dysplasia panel)","Flexion-extension cervical spine imaging to assess atlantoaxial instability","Whole-spine imaging and monitoring for progressive kyphoscoliosis","Respiratory and sleep assessment where thoracic or cervical involvement threatens ventilation"],"redFlags":["Cervical myelopathy or atlantoaxial instability threatening the spinal cord","Rapidly progressive kyphoscoliosis with thoracic insufficiency and respiratory compromise","New neurological deficit in limbs suggesting cord compression","Neonatal respiratory distress in severe early-presenting forms"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on severity: milder cases have near-normal survival with orthopaedic morbidity, whereas severe forms cause significant respiratory and neurological compromise from thoracic restriction and cervical instability. Lifelong spinal surveillance is required.","pitfalls":["Overlooking cervical instability before sedation, anaesthesia or manipulation, risking cord injury","Attributing the changing body proportions to a different evolving dysplasia and missing the diagnosis","Failing to recognise the coccygeal appendage as a diagnostic clue","Underestimating the pace of scoliosis progression and delaying orthopaedic referral"],"management":["Manage supportively as no drug corrects the TRPV4 channel defect, focusing on spinal, orthopaedic, and respiratory complications","Undertake cervical spine surveillance with flexion-extension imaging and proceed to surgical stabilisation or decompression for atlantoaxial instability or cord compression","Manage progressive kyphoscoliosis with bracing and spinal fusion surgery","Provide respiratory support for thoracic restriction, including ventilatory assistance and sleep assessment","Offer physiotherapy for joint contractures and mobility aids, with anaesthetic precautions to protect the unstable cervical cord","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary team of clinical genetics, spinal and orthopaedic surgery, and respiratory medicine","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance of TRPV4 variants, noting that many cases arise de novo"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Bone Dysplasias: An Atlas of Genetic Disorders of Skeletal Development (Spranger)"],"crossLinks":[]},"methaemoglobinaemia_acquired":{"name":"Acquired methaemoglobinaemia","system":"Toxicology / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["toxic methaemoglobinaemia","drug-induced methaemoglobinaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs when an oxidising drug or chemical converts haemoglobin iron to the ferric state, forming methaemoglobin which cannot carry oxygen","Common precipitants include topical/local anaesthetics (benzocaine, prilocaine), dapsone, nitrites and nitrates, and aniline dyes","Classic clue is cyanosis that does NOT improve with supplemental oxygen, with a normal arterial oxygen tension","Blood appears characteristically chocolate-brown and does not redden on exposure to air","Pulse oximetry is unreliable, often reading around the mid-80s regardless of true saturation; co-oximetry is required","Severity ranges from asymptomatic through headache and fatigue to obtundation, arrhythmia and death as levels rise","Methylthioninium chloride (methylene blue) is the specific antidote for significant or symptomatic cases"],"pathophysiology":"Haemoglobin normally contains iron in the ferrous state, which binds oxygen. Oxidising agents convert some iron to the ferric (methaemoglobin) form, which cannot bind oxygen and also shifts the oxygen affinity of the remaining ferrous haem, impairing oxygen delivery further. Endogenous cytochrome-b5 reductase normally reconverts methaemoglobin, but this capacity is overwhelmed by oxidant stress. Methylthioninium chloride acts as an artificial electron acceptor via NADPH-methaemoglobin reductase to accelerate reduction back to functional haemoglobin; it is ineffective and potentially harmful in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypoxaemia from cardiorespiratory disease","Sulfhaemoglobinaemia","Congenital methaemoglobinaemia (cytochrome-b5 reductase deficiency or HbM)","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Cyanide poisoning","Shock or poor peripheral perfusion causing cyanosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Co-oximetry to measure the methaemoglobin fraction","Arterial blood gas (oxygen tension typically normal)","Exposure and medication history","Full blood count and assessment for haemolysis","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status before giving methylthioninium chloride"],"redFlags":["Depressed consciousness, seizures or arrhythmia","Cyanosis unresponsive to oxygen with a high methaemoglobin fraction","Coexisting anaemia amplifying the impact on oxygen delivery","Ongoing exposure to the causative oxidant"],"prognosis":"Most cases respond rapidly to removal of the oxidant and, where indicated, methylthioninium chloride; severe or unrecognised cases, or those with G6PD deficiency limiting antidote use, carry higher risk.","pitfalls":["Trusting a normal-looking pulse oximeter reading, which is unreliable in methaemoglobinaemia","Giving methylthioninium chloride in G6PD deficiency, which can precipitate haemolysis and is ineffective","Missing the diagnosis when cyanosis fails to respond to oxygen","Overlooking topical anaesthetic sprays as a common precipitant"],"management":["Immediately identify and discontinue the causative oxidant, such as benzocaine, prilocaine, dapsone, nitrites, or aniline dyes","Give the antidote methylthioninium chloride (methylene blue), which accelerates enzymatic reduction of methaemoglobin, for symptomatic or significant elevation","Check glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status and avoid methylthioninium chloride in deficiency, using ascorbic acid, exchange transfusion, or hyperbaric oxygen instead","Provide supplemental oxygen and airway and haemodynamic support while correcting the underlying process","Anticipate rebound from slowly absorbed agents such as dapsone, considering repeat dosing and adjunctive cimetidine to reduce toxic metabolite formation","Monitor response with co-oximetry rather than pulse oximetry","Escalate to critical care with exchange transfusion for life-threatening or refractory methaemoglobinaemia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies (standard reference)","Emergency toxicology references"],"crossLinks":[]},"methanol_poisoning":{"name":"Methanol poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["methyl alcohol poisoning","wood alcohol poisoning","methanol toxicity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Methanol itself is relatively inert; toxicity comes from hepatic metabolism to formaldehyde and then formic acid, which produces severe high anion-gap metabolic acidosis and target-organ injury","There is a characteristic latent period of hours (longer if ethanol is co-ingested) before symptoms appear, so early presentation can look deceptively mild","Visual symptoms are a hallmark - blurred vision, photophobia, a snowfield or blizzard-like whiteout, and potentially permanent blindness from optic nerve injury","Look for a wide anion-gap metabolic acidosis together with a raised osmolal gap; the osmolal gap falls as methanol is metabolised while the anion gap rises","Fomepizole is the preferred antidote as it inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase and blocks formation of toxic metabolites; ethanol is an alternative competitive substrate where fomepizole is unavailable","Folinic acid (or folic acid) is given to accelerate metabolism of formic acid to carbon dioxide and water","Haemodialysis removes methanol and formate and corrects acidosis, and is indicated in severe acidosis, visual impairment, renal failure, or high methanol levels","Consider methanol in any unexplained high anion-gap acidosis with visual complaints, particularly after consumption of illicit or adulterated spirits"],"pathophysiology":"Methanol is oxidised by alcohol dehydrogenase to formaldehyde, which is rapidly converted by aldehyde dehydrogenase to formic acid. Formic acid is the principal toxin: it inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome oxidase, causing cellular hypoxia and lactate accumulation, and produces a profound high anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Formate is directly toxic to the retina and optic nerve, causing oedema and demyelination that can lead to irreversible blindness, and can injure the basal ganglia (putaminal necrosis). Because the parent alcohol elevates serum osmolality, an early osmolal gap precedes the later anion-gap acidosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Ethylene glycol poisoning","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Lactic acidosis from other causes","Salicylate poisoning","Uraemia and other high anion-gap acidoses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum methanol and formate levels where available","Arterial blood gas and calculated anion gap","Serum osmolality with calculated osmolal gap","Serum electrolytes, urea, creatinine, and glucose","Fundoscopy and formal visual assessment for optic involvement"],"redFlags":["Visual disturbance or reduced acuity signalling optic nerve toxicity and impending blindness","Severe high anion-gap metabolic acidosis","Reduced consciousness, seizures, or coma","Rising anion gap with a narrowing osmolal gap indicating ongoing conversion to toxic metabolites","Consumption of illicit or home-distilled alcohol in a cluster of patients"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the interval to treatment and the depth of acidosis at presentation; early antidote and dialysis can be curative, but delayed treatment risks permanent blindness, neurological deficits, and death.","pitfalls":["Being falsely reassured by the asymptomatic latent period, especially with co-ingested ethanol","Waiting for a confirmatory methanol level before starting antidote in a strongly suggestive case","Overlooking the diagnosis when the osmolal gap has already normalised late in the course","Failing to arrange haemodialysis for severe acidosis or visual symptoms"],"management":["Give the antidote fomepizole to inhibit alcohol dehydrogenase and block formation of toxic formic acid, using ethanol as a competitive alternative where fomepizole is unavailable","Administer folinic or folic acid to accelerate metabolism of formate to carbon dioxide and water","Correct the high anion-gap metabolic acidosis with intravenous sodium bicarbonate","Institute haemodialysis to clear methanol and formate and correct acidosis in severe poisoning, visual toxicity, or renal impairment","Provide supportive airway protection, ventilation, and fluid resuscitation","Obtain ophthalmology assessment and monitor for optic nerve and retinal toxicity","Involve toxicology or the poisons centre early and screen for co-ingested toxic alcohols"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"methemoglobinemia":{"name":"Methaemoglobinaemia","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["methemoglobinemia","MetHb","acquired methaemoglobinaemia","congenital methaemoglobinaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AABB Technical Manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs when haemoglobin iron is oxidised from the ferrous to the ferric state, producing methaemoglobin that cannot bind oxygen and shifts the dissociation curve so remaining oxygen is released poorly","Classic clue is cyanosis, often with chocolate-brown blood, that does not improve with supplemental oxygen","A characteristic saturation gap exists, with pulse oximetry reading falsely and tending toward the mid-80s while arterial blood gas calculates a normal partial pressure of oxygen","Co-oximetry, not standard oximetry or blood gas calculation, is required to measure the methaemoglobin fraction directly","Acquired causes predominate and include oxidant drugs and chemicals such as topical and local anaesthetics, dapsone, nitrites and nitrates, and aniline compounds","Congenital forms arise from cytochrome b5 reductase deficiency or from haemoglobin M variants and are usually better tolerated because of chronic adaptation","First-line treatment of significant symptomatic acquired disease is methylene blue, which is ineffective and potentially harmful in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, where exchange transfusion or ascorbic acid may be considered"],"pathophysiology":"Haemoglobin normally keeps its iron in the reduced ferrous state, which binds oxygen; a small fraction is continually oxidised to the ferric methaemoglobin form and reduced again, mainly by the cytochrome b5 reductase (NADH-dependent) system. Methaemoglobinaemia develops when oxidative stress from drugs or chemicals overwhelms this reducing capacity, or when the reductase enzyme or the globin chain is congenitally abnormal. Ferric haem cannot carry oxygen, and its presence increases the oxygen affinity of the remaining ferrous haem, impairing oxygen delivery to tissues. The NADPH-dependent methaemoglobin reductase pathway becomes relevant therapeutically because methylene blue acts through it, which is why it fails when glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase is deficient.","additionalDifferentials":["Sulfhaemoglobinaemia","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Cyanotic congenital heart disease","Hypoxaemic respiratory failure","Shock or peripheral cyanosis from low cardiac output"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Co-oximetry to quantify the methaemoglobin fraction","Arterial blood gas alongside pulse oximetry to demonstrate the saturation gap","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase assay before or alongside methylene blue use","Review of drug, chemical, and occupational exposures","Enzyme assay or haemoglobin analysis when a congenital form is suspected"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic hypoxia with high methaemoglobin levels causing altered consciousness, seizures, arrhythmia, or cardiovascular collapse, which is an emergency","Cyanosis unresponsive to high-flow oxygen","Suspected methaemoglobinaemia in a patient with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, where methylene blue is hazardous","Rapidly rising levels after a large oxidant exposure"],"prognosis":"Acquired disease usually resolves well once the offending agent is removed and, where needed, methylene blue is given; congenital reductase deficiency is generally compatible with normal life, whereas very high acute levels can be fatal without prompt treatment.","pitfalls":["Trusting pulse oximetry or a calculated blood gas saturation instead of measuring methaemoglobin by co-oximetry","Persisting with oxygen alone when cyanosis fails to respond","Giving methylene blue in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, risking haemolysis and worsening","Failing to identify and stop the causative oxidant exposure"],"management":["For acquired disease, remove the offending oxidant agent and give methylthioninium chloride (methylene blue) for symptomatic elevation","Check glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status before methylthioninium chloride and use ascorbic acid, exchange transfusion, or hyperbaric oxygen if deficient","Treat congenital cytochrome b5 reductase deficiency, when troublesome, with chronic oral methylthioninium chloride or ascorbic acid, though many need no treatment","Provide supplemental oxygen and monitor the methaemoglobin fraction with co-oximetry","Educate susceptible and congenital patients to avoid oxidant drugs and chemicals","Reserve exchange transfusion and critical care for severe or refractory acute elevations","Offer genetic counselling for congenital reductase deficiency and haemoglobin M variants, the latter usually requiring no therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"methionine_adenosyltransferase_deficiency":{"name":"Methionine adenosyltransferase I/III deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAT I/III deficiency","MAT deficiency","hepatic methionine adenosyltransferase deficiency","S-adenosylmethionine synthetase deficiency","isolated persistent hypermethioninaemia","MAT1A"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in MAT1A affecting the liver-specific isoenzymes (MAT I and MAT III) that convert methionine to S-adenosylmethionine (SAM)","The commonest cause of isolated persistent hypermethioninaemia and a frequent incidental finding on newborn screening","Most individuals are clinically well; the biochemical abnormality is usually benign and does not require intervention","Reduced SAM production, with an elevated methionine but normal or low SAM, helps separate it from GNMT deficiency where SAM is high","A distinctive boiled-cabbage or sulphurous breath odour may occur from dimethyl sulfide when methionine is very high","Rare individuals with severe biallelic disease can develop neurological signs and central demyelination, attributed to SAM deficiency","Inheritance is usually autosomal recessive for the severe forms and autosomal dominant for the common mild elevation","Where treatment is considered in severe cases it is generic, such as a moderately methionine-restricted diet or SAM supplementation"],"pathophysiology":"Methionine adenosyltransferase catalyses the first step of methionine metabolism, forming S-adenosylmethionine, the principal biological methyl donor. MAT1A encodes the isoenzymes expressed mainly in the liver; when their activity is reduced, methionine is not efficiently converted and accumulates in blood, while hepatic SAM formation falls. Because MAT II activity in other tissues is preserved, most methylation reactions continue and patients are typically asymptomatic. In the uncommon severe forms, marked SAM deficiency in the nervous system is thought to impair myelin methylation reactions, giving rise to demyelination and neurological features.","additionalDifferentials":["Classical homocystinuria (cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency), which also raises methionine but with homocystinuria","Glycine N-methyltransferase deficiency","S-adenosylhomocysteine hydrolase deficiency","Adenosine kinase deficiency","Hypermethioninaemia secondary to liver disease or high methionine/protein intake","Tyrosinaemia type 1 with secondary hypermethioninaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma amino acids demonstrating isolated raised methionine","Plasma total homocysteine to exclude homocystinuria","S-adenosylmethionine and S-adenosylhomocysteine measurement to distinguish related disorders","Liver function tests to exclude hepatic causes","MAT1A gene sequencing for confirmation","Brain MRI in the rare cases with neurological signs to assess for demyelination"],"redFlags":["Emerging neurological signs or developmental regression suggesting a severe biallelic form with demyelination","Very high plasma methionine sustained over time","Failure of methionine to normalise, prompting search for an alternative diagnosis","Coexisting hepatic dysfunction pointing to a different or additional cause"],"prognosis":"The great majority have an entirely benign course with normal development and lifespan; only the rare severe biallelic forms carry a risk of neurological impairment, which may improve with dietary or SAM treatment.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling isolated hypermethioninaemia as classical homocystinuria and imposing unnecessary treatment","Failing to check homocysteine and SAM/SAH, which are needed to place the disorder correctly","Over-restricting methionine in a well child, risking deficiency of an essential amino acid","Assuming all cases are benign without excluding the rare severe form when neurological signs appear"],"management":["Reassure and avoid intervention in the great majority, in whom isolated persistent hypermethioninaemia is a benign incidental finding","Confirm the diagnosis by excluding homocystinuria and other causes of hypermethioninaemia through homocysteine and metabolite measurement","For the rare severe biallelic disease with neurological involvement, institute a methionine-restricted diet and consider oral S-adenosylmethionine supplementation","Monitor plasma methionine and undertake neurological review and brain MRI where central demyelination is suspected","Involve a metabolic dietitian in symptomatic or severe cases","Offer genetic counselling for MAT1A variants, recognising both dominant and recessive inheritance patterns"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID","Mudd SH. Hypermethioninemias of genetic and non-genetic origin","Blau N. Physician's Guide to the Diagnosis, Treatment, and Follow-up of Inherited Metabolic Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"methylcrotonyl_coa_carboxylase_deficiency":{"name":"3-Methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["3-MCC deficiency","methylcrotonylglycinuria","MCCC1 deficiency","MCCC2 deficiency","isolated 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Disorder of leucine catabolism caused by biallelic variants in MCCC1 or MCCC2 encoding the two subunits of the enzyme","One of the most frequently detected conditions on expanded newborn screening, yet most identified individuals remain asymptomatic","When symptomatic, presents with episodes of metabolic acidosis, hypoglycaemia, hyperammonaemia, vomiting and lethargy, often during catabolic illness","Characteristic acylcarnitine finding is elevated 3-hydroxyisovalerylcarnitine (C5-OH), which flags it on screening","Urine organic acids show raised 3-methylcrotonylglycine and 3-hydroxyisovalerate","A low free carnitine state is common and may be the presenting biochemical abnormality","Maternal 3-MCC deficiency can be unmasked by an abnormal newborn screen in an unaffected infant","Management is often just avoidance of prolonged fasting and carnitine supplementation, with mild leucine restriction and an emergency regimen in symptomatic cases"],"pathophysiology":"3-Methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase is a biotin-dependent mitochondrial enzyme that catalyses a step in the degradation of leucine, converting 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA to 3-methylglutaconyl-CoA. Deficiency, from defects in either the alpha (MCCC1) or beta (MCCC2) subunit, blocks leucine catabolism so that 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA is diverted to 3-methylcrotonylglycine and 3-hydroxyisovaleric acid, which accumulate and are excreted. The conjugation of these metabolites with carnitine and glycine can deplete free carnitine. Although the biochemical defect is consistent, clinical expression is highly variable and often benign, so it is regarded as a condition of low and unpredictable penetrance. During catabolic stress a minority develop a Reye-like metabolic crisis with acidosis and hyperammonaemia. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Holocarboxylase synthetase or biotinidase deficiency (multiple carboxylase defects)","Other organic acidaemias (isovaleric, methylmalonic, propionic)","3-methylglutaconic aciduria and HMG-CoA lyase deficiency","Primary carnitine transporter deficiency (for low free carnitine)","Fatty acid oxidation disorders presenting with hypoketotic hypoglycaemia","Maternal 3-MCC deficiency detected through the infant's screen"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma acylcarnitine profile showing elevated C5-OH (3-hydroxyisovalerylcarnitine)","Urine organic acids for 3-methylcrotonylglycine and 3-hydroxyisovalerate","Plasma free and total carnitine levels","Blood gas, glucose and ammonia during suspected crises","MCCC1 and MCCC2 gene sequencing for confirmation","Enzyme activity assay in fibroblasts/leukocytes where needed","Maternal biochemical testing when a newborn screen is abnormal but the infant is well"],"redFlags":["Metabolic acidosis with hyperammonaemia and encephalopathy during illness","Hypoglycaemia with lethargy in a catabolic child","Severe carnitine depletion with hypotonia or cardiac concern","Reye-like deterioration during intercurrent infection or fasting","Recurrent unexplained vomiting and decompensation"],"prognosis":"The great majority of individuals identified are asymptomatic with a normal outlook; a minority experience metabolic crises, and with fasting avoidance and carnitine supplementation the prognosis is generally good.","pitfalls":["Over-treating or over-restricting asymptomatic screen-positive infants given the low penetrance","Failing to consider maternal 3-MCC deficiency when a healthy infant screens positive","Missing symptomatic decompensation by assuming the condition is always benign","Overlooking secondary carnitine depletion","Allowing prolonged perioperative or illness-related fasting without an emergency plan"],"management":["Recognise that most identified individuals are asymptomatic and need only an emergency sick-day plan rather than routine treatment","Avoid prolonged fasting and catabolism, using a glucose-based emergency regimen during intercurrent illness to prevent metabolic decompensation","Supplement with L-carnitine to correct the secondary carnitine depletion","Apply modest leucine or protein restriction only in symptomatic individuals, avoiding unnecessary over-restriction","Treat acute crises with intravenous glucose and supportive correction of acidosis, hypoglycaemia, and hyperammonaemia","Monitor free and total carnitine, acylcarnitine profile, and growth through metabolic follow-up","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance of MCCC1 and MCCC2 variants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","Saudubray, Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"methylmalonic_acidemia":{"name":"Methylmalonic acidemia","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MMA","methylmalonic aciduria","methylmalonyl-CoA mutase deficiency","cobalamin-related methylmalonic acidemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An organic acidaemia caused by deficient methylmalonyl-CoA mutase activity or by defects in its adenosylcobalamin (vitamin B12) cofactor synthesis","Typical neonatal presentation is metabolic decompensation with poor feeding, vomiting, lethargy, hypotonia and a severe high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","Characteristic laboratory findings include ketosis, hyperammonaemia, hypoglycaemia, and cytopenias such as neutropenia and thrombocytopenia","Elevated methylmalonic acid in blood and urine with raised propionylcarnitine (C3) on acylcarnitine profile establishes the diagnosis","Long-term complications include chronic kidney disease (tubulointerstitial nephropathy), a movement disorder from basal ganglia injury, growth failure and pancreatitis","Some cobalamin-responsive forms improve with vitamin B12 (hydroxocobalamin), so a cofactor trial is standard","Chronic management principles are a protein-restricted diet limiting propiogenic amino acids, carnitine supplementation and avoidance of catabolism, with metronidazole sometimes used to reduce gut propionate production","Detectable through newborn screening by elevated C3 acylcarnitine, prompting confirmatory testing"],"pathophysiology":"Methylmalonic acidemia results from a block in the conversion of methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA, a step catalysed by methylmalonyl-CoA mutase using adenosylcobalamin. This reaction lies on the final common pathway for catabolism of the branched-chain amino acids isoleucine and valine, methionine, threonine, odd-chain fatty acids and cholesterol side chains. Enzyme deficiency or impaired cofactor synthesis causes methylmalonic acid and related organic acids to accumulate, producing severe metabolic acidosis. These metabolites inhibit ureagenesis (causing secondary hyperammonaemia) and mitochondrial energy metabolism, are toxic to bone marrow and renal tubules, and injure the basal ganglia during decompensation.","additionalDifferentials":["Propionic acidemia and other organic acidaemias","Isovaleric acidemia","Urea cycle disorders (hyperammonaemia without high anion gap acidosis)","Maple syrup urine disease","Neonatal sepsis and diabetic ketoacidosis","Acquired vitamin B12 deficiency (in later-onset or maternal cases)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood gas and anion gap, blood glucose, lactate and ammonia","Plasma and urine organic acids showing elevated methylmalonic acid","Plasma acylcarnitine profile (elevated propionylcarnitine, C3)","Newborn screening acylcarnitine analysis","Vitamin B12 responsiveness testing and complementation/molecular studies to subtype","Renal function monitoring and neuroimaging after metabolic crises"],"redFlags":["Acute metabolic decompensation with severe high-anion-gap acidosis is a life-threatening emergency","Hyperammonaemia with encephalopathy accompanying an acidotic crisis","Rapid deterioration during intercurrent illness, fasting or increased protein catabolism","Progressive renal impairment or acute basal ganglia injury with a new movement disorder"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies by subtype; cobalamin-responsive forms generally do better, whereas mutase-deficient disease carries significant risk of recurrent metabolic crises, chronic kidney disease and neurological injury, and severe cases may require kidney or combined liver-kidney transplantation. Early screening detection and meticulous metabolic control improve outcomes.","pitfalls":["Attributing an acidotic, unwell neonate to sepsis and delaying metabolic investigation","Failing to trial and document vitamin B12 responsiveness","Overlooking evolving chronic kidney disease during long-term follow-up","Under-treating catabolism during intercurrent illness, allowing preventable decompensation"],"management":["Institute a protein-restricted diet limiting the propiogenic precursors isoleucine, valine, methionine, and threonine, supplemented with a specialised metabolic formula","Trial parenteral hydroxocobalamin and continue it long-term in cobalamin-responsive forms","Supplement with L-carnitine and consider intermittent gut-flora suppression with metronidazole to reduce propionate production","Manage acute decompensation by stopping protein, giving intravenous glucose with insulin to reverse catabolism, correcting acidosis, and using ammonia scavengers with haemofiltration or dialysis for severe hyperammonaemia","Provide an emergency sick-day regimen and strict fasting avoidance","Undertake surveillance for chronic kidney disease, basal ganglia injury and movement disorder, growth failure, and pancreatitis","Consider liver or combined liver-kidney transplantation in severe or recurrently decompensating disease, with genetic counselling and metabolic multidisciplinary care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"mevalonate_kinase_deficiency":{"name":"Mevalonate kinase deficiency","system":"Genetics / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MKD","hyper-IgD syndrome","HIDS","MVK","mevalonic aciduria"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews (NCBI) and autoinflammatory disease consensus guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive autoinflammatory disorder caused by biallelic variants in MVK, reducing mevalonate kinase activity in the cholesterol and isoprenoid pathway","Presents in infancy or early childhood with recurrent febrile episodes lasting several days, often triggered by vaccination, infection or stress","Attacks feature high fever with cervical lymphadenopathy, abdominal pain, vomiting and diarrhoea, arthralgia and a polymorphous rash","Aphthous oral ulceration and painful hepatosplenomegaly are common during flares","Serum IgD is often elevated, giving the historical name hyper-IgD syndrome, though a normal IgD does not exclude the diagnosis","The severe end of the spectrum, mevalonic aciduria, adds developmental delay, dysmorphism, ataxia, cataract and failure to thrive","Urinary mevalonic acid is markedly raised during attacks, especially in the severe form","IL-1 inhibition is the mainstay of treatment for frequent or severe flares, with enzyme activity and genetic testing confirming the diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Mevalonate kinase deficiency results from biallelic variants in MVK that reduce the activity of mevalonate kinase, an enzyme of the isoprenoid biosynthetic pathway downstream of HMG-CoA reductase. Impaired production of non-sterol isoprenoids, particularly geranylgeranyl pyrophosphate, disrupts prenylation of small GTPases and activates the inflammasome, driving excess interleukin-1 beta release. The degree of residual enzyme activity determines whether the phenotype is the milder periodic fever (hyper-IgD) syndrome or severe mevalonic aciduria.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial Mediterranean fever","TNF-receptor-associated periodic syndrome (TRAPS)","Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes","PFAPA (periodic fever, aphthous stomatitis, pharyngitis, adenitis) syndrome","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Cyclic neutropenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinary organic acids for mevalonic acid, ideally during an attack","Mevalonate kinase enzyme activity assay","Molecular genetic testing of MVK","Serum immunoglobulins including IgD and IgA","Acute-phase reactants and full blood count during and between flares","Ophthalmological and developmental assessment in suspected severe disease"],"redFlags":["Features of secondary amyloidosis such as proteinuria in long-standing uncontrolled disease","Severe neurological impairment, ataxia or visual loss suggesting mevalonic aciduria","Failure to thrive with recurrent crises in infancy","Signs of macrophage activation syndrome during a severe flare"],"prognosis":"The milder periodic fever form tends to improve with age but can be complicated by secondary amyloidosis if inflammation is poorly controlled. Mevalonic aciduria carries a poor prognosis with significant neurodevelopmental disability and early mortality; IL-1 blockade can substantially reduce flare frequency.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because serum IgD is normal","Missing the raised urinary mevalonic acid by sampling only between attacks","Overlooking the risk of secondary amyloidosis with chronic inflammation","Failing to distinguish the severe (mevalonic aciduria) form, which needs broader multisystem care"],"management":["Control the autoinflammatory febrile attacks with interleukin-1 blockade using anakinra or canakinumab as the mainstay of disease-specific therapy","Use TNF inhibition or interleukin-6 blockade with tocilizumab in selected cases refractory to interleukin-1 blockade","Treat acute flares symptomatically with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs or short corticosteroid courses","Undertake surveillance for secondary AA amyloidosis through proteinuria monitoring, maintaining tight control of inflammation","Support the severe mevalonic aciduria end of the spectrum with neurodevelopmental, ophthalmological, and physiotherapy care, considering haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in refractory disease","Manage triggers by treating intercurrent infection and anticipating vaccination-related flares","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance of MVK variants and manage through rheumatology and immunology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (NCBI): Mevalonate kinase deficiency","OMIM: Hyper-IgD syndrome and mevalonic aciduria"],"crossLinks":[]},"mevalonic_aciduria":{"name":"Mevalonic aciduria (severe mevalonate kinase deficiency)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mevalonic aciduria","severe mevalonate kinase deficiency","MVK deficiency","mevalonate kinase deficiency","MKD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Represents the severe end of mevalonate kinase deficiency, in which the enzyme mevalonate kinase (MVK) in the cholesterol/isoprenoid biosynthesis pathway is markedly impaired","The milder allelic phenotype is hyperimmunoglobulinaemia D syndrome (HIDS), a hereditary periodic fever syndrome, so both share recurrent inflammatory febrile attacks","Severe disease features recurrent fevers with lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, rash, and arthralgia, plus failure to thrive, developmental delay, ataxia, and dysmorphism","The biochemical hallmark is markedly elevated urinary mevalonic acid, especially during crises, unlike most organic acidurias where the diagnostic metabolite is downstream","The autoinflammatory phenotype is thought to stem from deficient production of isoprenoid intermediates such as geranylgeranyl pyrophosphate, impairing protein prenylation and dysregulating inflammatory signalling","Attacks may be provoked by vaccination, infection, or stress, and management involves anti-inflammatory and interleukin-1-directed strategies alongside supportive care","Inheritance is autosomal recessive, and cataracts and progressive neurological impairment can occur in severe cases","British spelling applies (hyperimmunoglobulinaemia, tumour)"],"pathophysiology":"Mevalonate kinase phosphorylates mevalonate, an early committed step in the biosynthesis of cholesterol and non-sterol isoprenoids. Severe deficiency causes mevalonic acid to accumulate and be excreted in large amounts, while downstream isoprenoid products, including those needed for protein prenylation (such as geranylgeranyl pyrophosphate), are relatively depleted. Reduced prenylation of small regulatory proteins is believed to activate inflammasome-driven interleukin-1 production, explaining the recurrent autoinflammatory fevers. The combination of substrate accumulation and deficient isoprenoid end-products underlies both the systemic inflammation and the neurodevelopmental and dysmorphic features of the severe form.","additionalDifferentials":["Other hereditary periodic fever syndromes (familial Mediterranean fever, TRAPS, cryopyrin-associated syndromes)","Systemic-onset juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Recurrent infection or occult sepsis","Other organic acidurias presenting with encephalopathy and failure to thrive","Lymphoproliferative or malignant disease causing fevers and hepatosplenomegaly","Congenital infection in the neonatal period"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine organic acids demonstrating raised mevalonic acid, especially during febrile crises","Serum immunoglobulin D and A levels (often elevated in the milder phenotype)","Acute-phase inflammatory markers during attacks","Mevalonate kinase enzyme activity assay","Molecular analysis of the MVK gene","Neuroimaging and ophthalmological assessment for cerebellar and lens involvement"],"redFlags":["Severe recurrent inflammatory crises with dehydration and systemic compromise","Progressive neurological deterioration, ataxia, or developmental regression","Failure to thrive with hepatosplenomegaly in infancy","Visual impairment from cataract formation"],"prognosis":"The severe mevalonic aciduria form carries significant morbidity and mortality, with recurrent crises, neurological impairment, and failure to thrive, whereas the milder periodic fever phenotype has a better outlook. Interleukin-1-directed therapy has improved control of inflammatory attacks.","pitfalls":["Attributing recurrent fevers to infection alone and missing an autoinflammatory metabolic cause","Not sampling urine organic acids during a crisis when mevalonic acid excretion peaks","Overlooking the neurodevelopmental and ophthalmological complications of severe disease","Failing to anticipate vaccination or infection as attack triggers"],"management":["Control the recurrent inflammatory attacks with interleukin-1-directed therapy using anakinra or canakinumab","Treat acute flares with corticosteroids or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs alongside supportive care during crises","Provide supportive neurodevelopmental care with physiotherapy, developmental support, and nutritional and feeding support for failure to thrive","Manage the ophthalmological and cerebellar features, including cataract and ataxia","Undertake surveillance for secondary AA amyloidosis with sustained control of inflammation","Consider haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in severe refractory disease","Coordinate metabolic, immunology, and neurology multidisciplinary care and offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive MVK inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: mevalonate kinase deficiency and mevalonic aciduria","Nyhan, Atlas of Inherited Metabolic Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mgat2_cdg":{"name":"MGAT2-CDG (CDG-IIa)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MGAT2","GnT-II deficiency","N-acetylglucosaminyltransferase II deficiency","congenital disorder of glycosylation type IIa","CDG-IIa"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A prototypical N-linked type II CDG caused by deficiency of Golgi N-acetylglucosaminyltransferase II, which blocks conversion of hybrid to complex N-glycans","Presents in infancy with dysmorphism, developmental delay, hypotonia and seizures rather than the peripheral neuropathy and cerebellar hypoplasia characteristic of type I disease","Recurrent infections and a bleeding tendency reflect hypoglycosylation of immune and coagulation proteins","Skeletal and facial dysmorphic features are common, and gastrointestinal problems may occur","Because the defect is in glycan processing, transferrin isoelectric focusing shows a type II pattern (increased mono- and asialotransferrin from truncated antennae)","Management is supportive and multidisciplinary; no specific dietary or enzyme therapy alters the underlying defect","Confirmed by MGAT2 sequencing supported by enzyme assay in leucocytes or fibroblasts"],"pathophysiology":"MGAT2 encodes N-acetylglucosaminyltransferase II, a Golgi enzyme that adds a second N-acetylglucosamine branch during maturation of N-glycans from hybrid to biantennary complex structures. Deficiency arrests processing after the oligosaccharide has already been transferred to protein, so the glycan chains are structurally truncated rather than absent. This defines a type II disorder, distinguishable from type I assembly defects by the transferrin isoform pattern on isoelectric focusing or mass spectrometry. Truncated complex glycans impair function of many secreted and membrane glycoproteins, producing the multisystem phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Other type II CDG subtypes (e.g. TMEM165-CDG, B4GALT1-CDG, COG-complex CDG, SLC35A2-CDG)","Type I CDG subtypes when the transferrin pattern is ambiguous","Chromosomal and other syndromic causes of dysmorphism with developmental delay","Primary immunodeficiency with recurrent infection","Inherited coagulation disorders","Mitochondrial disease in infancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transferrin isoelectric focusing or mass spectrometry demonstrating a type II pattern","MGAT2 molecular genetic testing","N-acetylglucosaminyltransferase II enzyme activity assay","Coagulation profile and immunoglobulin levels","Neuroimaging and electroencephalography","Skeletal survey and dysmorphology assessment"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or severe infections suggesting significant immune dysfunction","Unexplained bleeding from coagulation factor hypoglycosylation","Progressive encephalopathy or refractory seizures","Failure to thrive with multi-organ involvement"],"prognosis":"MGAT2-CDG causes significant and lasting neurodevelopmental disability; life expectancy is variable and depends on the severity of infections, bleeding and other systemic complications. There is no treatment that corrects the enzymatic defect.","pitfalls":["Assuming a type II transferrin pattern excludes CDG when the clinical picture differs from classic PMM2-CDG","Failing to distinguish type I from type II defects, which alters the candidate gene list","Overlooking immunodeficiency and bleeding risk in perioperative planning","Interpreting an isolated enzyme result without molecular confirmation"],"management":["Manage supportively as no therapy corrects the underlying glycosylation defect, directing care at the multisystem manifestations","Control seizures with antiepileptic drugs and provide developmental therapies for delay and hypotonia","Manage the bleeding tendency with coagulation factor support around surgery and invasive procedures","Treat and prevent recurrent infections aggressively, providing immunoglobulin support where humoral deficiency is demonstrated","Provide nutritional and feeding support and manage gastrointestinal problems","Undertake surveillance of coagulation, immune function, growth, and neurological status with electroencephalography and neuroimaging","Coordinate multidisciplinary care and offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance of MGAT2 variants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID, congenital disorders of glycosylation","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics, disorders of glycosylation"],"crossLinks":[]},"mhc_class_ii_deficiency":{"name":"MHC class II deficiency (bare lymphocyte syndrome type II)","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bare lymphocyte syndrome type II","BLS type II","CIITA","RFX5","RFXAP","RFXANK","HLA class II deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification of inborn errors of immunity","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents in infancy or early childhood with a combined immunodeficiency, recurrent respiratory and gastrointestinal infection, chronic diarrhoea and failure to thrive","The defining feature is absent HLA class II (HLA-DR, DP, DQ) expression on antigen-presenting cells and other cells, demonstrated by flow cytometry","It is not caused by defects in the HLA genes themselves but by mutations in the transcription factors that regulate their expression, principally CIITA, RFX5, RFXAP and RFXANK","Because positive selection of CD4 cells depends on class II, there is marked CD4 T-cell lymphopenia with a reduced CD4:CD8 ratio, while CD8 numbers are relatively preserved","Antibody responses are impaired owing to defective CD4-dependent T-cell help despite normal B-cell numbers, producing hypogammaglobulinaemia","Sclerosing cholangitis, often linked to chronic Cryptosporidium infection, and other opportunistic infections are important complications","Inheritance is autosomal recessive and is more prevalent in populations with high consanguinity, notably of North African origin","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is the only curative treatment but outcomes are less favourable than in some other combined immunodeficiencies"],"pathophysiology":"MHC class II deficiency results from loss-of-function variants in the transcriptional regulators that drive HLA class II gene expression, most often CIITA, RFX5, RFXAP or RFXANK, rather than from defects in the HLA loci themselves. Without these regulators, class II molecules are not expressed on antigen-presenting cells, so peptides cannot be presented to CD4 T cells; positive selection of CD4 thymocytes fails, causing profound CD4 lymphopenia, and mature CD4 cells cannot be activated. The lack of effective CD4 help cripples antibody production and cell-mediated immunity, yielding a combined immunodeficiency with susceptibility to a broad range of viral, bacterial, fungal and protozoal pathogens.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe combined immunodeficiency of other genotype","MHC class I deficiency (bare lymphocyte syndrome type I)","CD4 lymphopenia from other T-cell defects","Idiopathic CD4 lymphopenia","HIV infection","Hyper-IgM syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flow cytometry for HLA class II (HLA-DR) expression on monocytes and B cells","Lymphocyte subsets showing CD4 lymphopenia and a reduced CD4:CD8 ratio","Serum immunoglobulins and specific antibody responses","Lymphocyte proliferation assays","Sequencing of CIITA, RFX5, RFXAP and RFXANK","Investigation for opportunistic infection including Cryptosporidium and hepatobiliary imaging for sclerosing cholangitis"],"redFlags":["Progressive cholestasis suggesting sclerosing cholangitis with cryptosporidial infection","Opportunistic or disseminated infection in an infant with CD4 lymphopenia","Failure to thrive with chronic intractable diarrhoea","Complications following live vaccination or non-irradiated blood products","Rapid clinical deterioration awaiting transplantation"],"prognosis":"The natural history is poor with early mortality from infection and hepatobiliary disease; allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation offers the only cure but survival is lower than for several other combined immunodeficiencies, underlining the value of early diagnosis.","pitfalls":["Assuming the defect lies in the HLA genes rather than their transcriptional regulators","Overlooking the diagnosis because B-cell numbers and total lymphocyte count can be normal","Not measuring HLA class II expression directly by flow cytometry","Missing cryptosporidial infection and evolving sclerosing cholangitis","Giving live vaccines or non-irradiated blood products before diagnosis"],"management":["Offer early allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the only curative treatment for this combined immunodeficiency","Give infection prophylaxis with co-trimoxazole against Pneumocystis alongside antifungal and antiviral prophylaxis","Provide immunoglobulin replacement therapy for the associated antibody deficiency","Treat intercurrent and opportunistic infections aggressively, using only irradiated cytomegalovirus-safe blood products and avoiding live vaccines","Prevent Cryptosporidium exposure with boiled or filtered water and undertake hepatobiliary surveillance for sclerosing cholangitis","Provide nutritional support for chronic diarrhoea and failure to thrive through immunology, transplant, and hepatology teams","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for the causative transcription-factor variants (CIITA, RFX5, RFXAP, RFXANK) and HLA-type siblings as potential donors"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"microangiopathic_haemolytic_anaemia":{"name":"Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAHA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mechanical (non-immune) haemolysis in which red cells are fragmented as they pass through abnormal small vessels","The blood film hallmark is schistocytes (red cell fragments) with features of haemolysis and often thrombocytopenia","The direct antiglobulin test is negative, distinguishing it from immune haemolysis","It is a syndrome, not a single disease, and the key task is to identify the underlying cause","Thrombotic microangiopathies (TTP, haemolytic uraemic syndrome) are urgent causes requiring specific treatment","Other causes include disseminated intravascular coagulation, malignant hypertension, pre-eclampsia/HELLP, disseminated cancer and mechanical heart valves"],"pathophysiology":"Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia results from physical shearing of red cells within the microcirculation or across abnormal surfaces. In thrombotic microangiopathies, platelet-rich or fibrin thrombi partially occlude small vessels, and red cells passing through are fragmented into schistocytes. Similar fragmentation occurs across fibrin strands in disseminated intravascular coagulation, in vessels damaged by malignant hypertension or vasculitis, or across prosthetic valves. Because the process is mechanical, the antiglobulin test is negative, and consumption of platelets is common in thrombotic causes.","additionalDifferentials":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Haemolytic uraemic syndrome (including atypical)","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Malignant hypertension","Pre-eclampsia and HELLP syndrome","Disseminated malignancy or mechanical valve haemolysis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood film for schistocytes and platelet count","Direct antiglobulin test (negative in MAHA)","Coagulation screen and fibrinogen to assess for DIC","Renal function and blood pressure","Cause-specific tests (ADAMTS13, complement studies, pregnancy assessment)"],"redFlags":["Thrombocytopenia with neurological or renal features suggesting a thrombotic microangiopathy","Rapidly rising creatinine","Pregnancy with hypertension and haemolysis (HELLP)","Evidence of disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends entirely on the cause; thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura is rapidly fatal untreated but responds to prompt plasma exchange, whereas other causes require treatment of the underlying process.","pitfalls":["Missing thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, a medical emergency, by delaying ADAMTS13 testing and plasma exchange","Attributing MAHA to immune haemolysis despite a negative antiglobulin test","Overlooking pregnancy-related causes","Failing to search systematically for the underlying cause"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying cause, as this is a syndrome rather than a single disease","Initiate urgent therapeutic plasma exchange when thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura is suspected, alongside corticosteroids, with caplacizumab and rituximab as adjuncts","Treat complement-mediated (atypical) haemolytic uraemic syndrome with a complement C5 inhibitor such as eculizumab, and manage shiga-toxin-associated HUS supportively","In disseminated intravascular coagulation, treat the precipitating illness and replace fibrinogen and platelets guided by bleeding","Control accelerated (malignant) hypertension with parenteral antihypertensives, and expedite delivery in pre-eclampsia or HELLP syndrome","Transfuse red cells for symptomatic anaemia but avoid platelet transfusion in TTP unless there is life-threatening haemorrhage","Monitor the blood film, platelet count and renal function to track response and detect relapse","Refer urgently to haematology, with nephrology or obstetric input as relevant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Haematology reference texts (standard reference)","Thrombotic microangiopathy guidelines (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"microcornea":{"name":"Microcornea","system":"Eye / Anterior segment","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["small cornea"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric ophthalmology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenitally small cornea (reduced horizontal diameter) in an eye that is otherwise of relatively normal size","It is distinct from microphthalmos, in which the whole globe is small, and from nanophthalmos","The cornea is usually clear and often flatter than normal, tending toward hyperopia","It may be isolated or associated with other anterior segment anomalies and syndromes","There is an increased long-term risk of angle-closure and other glaucoma","Management centres on refractive correction, amblyopia treatment and lifelong glaucoma surveillance"],"pathophysiology":"Microcornea arises from arrest of corneal growth in the fifth month of gestation or from overgrowth of the surrounding optic cup rim, producing a cornea of reduced diameter. The small, often flat cornea reduces refractive power, typically causing hyperopia, and the associated crowded anterior segment anatomy predisposes to angle-closure glaucoma. It can occur in isolation or as part of anterior segment dysgenesis and various systemic syndromes, reflecting shared developmental pathways.","additionalDifferentials":["Microphthalmos or nanophthalmos","Anterior segment dysgenesis syndromes","Congenital glaucoma (which enlarges the cornea, the opposite finding)","Sclerocornea","Syndromic associations (for example with cataract or coloboma)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Measurement of corneal diameter and axial length to distinguish from microphthalmos","Refraction and cycloplegic assessment","Gonioscopy and intraocular pressure for glaucoma risk","Assessment for associated ocular and systemic anomalies","Amblyopia evaluation in children"],"redFlags":["Signs of angle-closure or other glaucoma","Associated cataract, coloboma or dysgenesis","Amblyopia in childhood","Syndromic features"],"prognosis":"Isolated microcornea generally has good vision with refractive correction; the main long-term concern is glaucoma, and prognosis worsens with associated anomalies.","pitfalls":["Confusing microcornea with microphthalmos","Overlooking the elevated glaucoma risk","Neglecting amblyopia management","Missing associated anterior segment or systemic anomalies"],"management":["Correct the associated refractive error, commonly hyperopia, with spectacles or contact lenses","Detect and treat amblyopia early in children with refractive correction and occlusion (patching) therapy","Undertake lifelong glaucoma surveillance with intraocular pressure measurement and gonioscopy given the angle-closure risk","Treat established glaucoma with topical pressure-lowering agents, and consider laser peripheral iridotomy or surgery for angle closure","Screen for and manage associated anterior segment dysgenesis and systemic syndromic features","Provide long-term paediatric ophthalmology follow-up to safeguard visual development"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"microcystic_adnexal_carcinoma":{"name":"Microcystic adnexal carcinoma","system":"Dermatology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAC","sclerosing sweat duct carcinoma","malignant syringoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / WHO Classification of Skin Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A locally aggressive adnexal carcinoma with both follicular and eccrine/sweat-duct differentiation, favouring the central face and upper lip","Clinically deceptive, presenting as a slowly enlarging indurated flesh-coloured plaque or nodule that appears far more innocuous than its infiltrative behaviour","Histology reveals a paradox of deceptively bland superficial keratocysts and cords overlying a deeply infiltrative sclerosing component with marked perineural invasion","Perineural invasion is a hallmark and may cause paraesthesiae, numbness, or a burning sensation and contributes to deep subclinical extension","Superficial or shave biopsy is frequently non-diagnostic because it samples only the bland upper portion, so deep incisional biopsy is preferred","Because subclinical spread greatly exceeds clinical margins, Mohs micrographic surgery is the preferred treatment to achieve complete clearance","Distant metastasis is uncommon, but local recurrence after inadequate excision is frequent owing to the infiltrative and perineural growth"],"pathophysiology":"Microcystic adnexal carcinoma is a malignant adnexal tumour displaying dual follicular and sweat-duct (eccrine) differentiation. It is characterised by a striking zonation: the superficial portion comprises bland keratin-filled microcysts and small basaloid nests, while the deeper portion consists of infiltrating cords and ductal structures set in a dense sclerotic stroma. The tumour extends far beyond its apparent clinical borders, tracks along nerves (perineural invasion), and may invade underlying muscle, cartilage, and bone. This discrepancy between an indolent appearance and aggressive infiltrative local behaviour underlies both the frequent misdiagnosis on superficial biopsy and the high rate of local recurrence when margins are inadequate.","additionalDifferentials":["Desmoplastic trichoepithelioma","Morphoeic (sclerosing) basal cell carcinoma","Syringoma","Desmoplastic trichilemmoma","Squamous cell carcinoma with ductal features","Metastatic scirrhous adenocarcinoma to skin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Deep incisional biopsy reaching the reticular dermis or subcutis rather than a superficial shave","Histopathology assessing depth of infiltration and perineural invasion","Immunohistochemistry to help distinguish from morphoeic basal cell carcinoma and desmoplastic trichoepithelioma","Mohs micrographic surgery with intraoperative margin mapping","Imaging to evaluate deep, bony, or perineural extension in advanced lesions","Neurological assessment when perineural symptoms are present"],"redFlags":["Indurated facial plaque with sensory symptoms suggesting perineural invasion","Persistent or recurrent lesion after apparently adequate excision","Fixation to deeper structures suggesting muscle, cartilage, or bone involvement","Progressive facial numbness, pain, or motor deficit indicating nerve involvement"],"prognosis":"The tumour rarely metastasises but is notorious for extensive subclinical local infiltration and perineural spread, giving a high local recurrence rate after conventional excision; margin-controlled surgery substantially reduces recurrence, and long-term follow-up is required.","pitfalls":["Relying on a superficial biopsy that samples only the bland upper tumour and misdiagnosing a benign lesion","Confusing it histologically with desmoplastic trichoepithelioma or morphoeic basal cell carcinoma","Underestimating margins and performing narrow excision, leading to recurrence","Ignoring perineural symptoms that signal deep nerve involvement"],"management":["Achieve complete clearance with Mohs micrographic surgery, the preferred treatment given extensive subclinical infiltration","Use wide local excision with comprehensive margin assessment where Mohs is unavailable","Consider adjuvant radiotherapy for positive or inaccessible margins or extensive perineural invasion","Obtain a deep incisional biopsy reaching the reticular dermis or subcutis rather than a shave, to avoid a falsely bland sample","Image with MRI to map perineural, deep soft-tissue or bony extension in advanced lesions","Manage through a multidisciplinary team including dermatopathology, head and neck or oculoplastic surgery and oncology","Provide prolonged clinical follow-up because of the high rate of local recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","WHO Classification of Skin Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"micropenis":{"name":"Micropenis","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["microphallus","micropenis (stretched length below normal)","congenital micropenis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as a stretched penile length more than 2.5 standard deviations below the mean for age in an otherwise normally formed penis with a correctly positioned urethral meatus","The normal structure but small size implies that androgen action was adequate during early urethral formation but deficient in the later gestational phase of penile growth","It is a sign rather than a diagnosis, most often reflecting hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, primary (hypergonadotropic) testicular failure, or partial defects in androgen synthesis or action","Measurement technique matters: the penis must be gently stretched with the suprapubic fat pad depressed, as a buried or concealed penis can mimic true micropenis","Associated hypoglycaemia or midline defects should raise concern for congenital hypopituitarism with multiple hormone deficiencies","A short trial of androgen therapy in infancy can both promote penile growth and provide diagnostic information about androgen responsiveness","Marked ambiguity of the genitalia points away from isolated micropenis and towards a disorder of sex development requiring karyotype and specialist assessment"],"pathophysiology":"Penile growth in the second half of gestation depends on testosterone secretion driven by fetal and placental gonadotropins and its conversion to dihydrotestosterone acting on androgen receptors. Because the urethra and overall penile architecture form earlier under androgen influence, a normally shaped but undersized penis indicates that androgen exposure was sufficient for structural development but inadequate during the later growth phase. The underlying defect may lie anywhere along the axis: deficient hypothalamic-pituitary gonadotropin drive (hypogonadotropic hypogonadism), primary testicular failure with impaired testosterone production (hypergonadotropic hypogonadism), or partial defects of testosterone biosynthesis or androgen-receptor signalling. In some cases no cause is identified and the finding is idiopathic.","additionalDifferentials":["Buried or concealed penis (normal size obscured by suprapubic fat or skin)","Webbed or trapped penis","Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, including congenital GnRH deficiency and hypopituitarism","Primary testicular failure (hypergonadotropic hypogonadism)","Partial androgen insensitivity syndrome","Defects of testosterone biosynthesis or 5-alpha-reductase deficiency","A more complex disorder of sex development"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Accurate stretched penile length measurement compared with age-based normative data","LH, FSH, and testosterone, ideally sampled during the neonatal minipuberty window","Assessment of the wider anterior pituitary axis (including cortisol, thyroid, and growth hormone) where hypopituitarism is suspected","hCG stimulation test to assess testicular testosterone production capacity","Karyotype and disorder-of-sex-development workup if there is any genital ambiguity","Pelvic and adrenal imaging where indicated by the hormonal findings"],"redFlags":["Neonatal hypoglycaemia, prolonged jaundice, or midline facial defects suggesting congenital hypopituitarism","Bilateral non-palpable gonads or genital ambiguity indicating a possible disorder of sex development","Failure of any response to an appropriate androgen trial","Features suggesting adrenal insufficiency in a hypopituitary infant"],"prognosis":"Many infants respond to early androgen therapy with satisfactory penile growth and can expect adequate adult size and function; the overall outlook is dictated by the underlying cause, with fertility potential depending on whether the defect is central, gonadal, or in androgen action.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing micropenis without a properly performed stretched measurement, mistaking a buried penis for a small one","Failing to evaluate the whole pituitary axis and missing life-threatening cortisol deficiency in hypopituitarism","Missing the neonatal minipuberty window, when hormonal evaluation is most informative","Overlooking an underlying disorder of sex development in the presence of subtle ambiguity","Attributing the finding to idiopathic causes before completing the endocrine assessment"],"management":["Give a trial of androgen therapy (testosterone) in infancy to promote penile growth and gauge androgen responsiveness","Treat the underlying cause: gonadotrophin or GnRH therapy for hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, and testosterone induction of puberty for testicular failure","Where congenital hypopituitarism is present, replace deficient anterior pituitary hormones (glucocorticoid, thyroxine, growth hormone) and treat associated hypoglycaemia promptly","Monitor penile growth, overall growth pattern and pubertal progression over time","Provide psychological support to the family and, in due course, to the patient","Manage within a multidisciplinary paediatric and reproductive endocrinology and urology team, with disorder-of-sex-development input where there is genital ambiguity","Offer genetic counselling and karyotype or DSD assessment, and discuss fertility potential according to whether the defect is central, gonadal or in androgen action"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"microphthalmia_anophthalmia":{"name":"Microphthalmia and anophthalmia","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAC spectrum","microphthalmia-anophthalmia-coloboma","SOX2","OTX2","clinical anophthalmia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Microphthalmia is an abnormally small, disorganised eye, whereas anophthalmia is the complete absence of ocular tissue; the two lie on a continuous spectrum","Both often coexist with coloboma as part of the microphthalmia-anophthalmia-coloboma (MAC) spectrum","Presentation ranges from an obviously small or absent eye to a clinically normal-looking eye with reduced axial length (nanophthalmos or posterior microphthalmos)","Frequently syndromic, with SOX2 and OTX2 among the most important causative genes, and association with chromosomal and multisystem disorders","Can be caused by genetic, chromosomal or environmental (including gestational infective and teratogenic) factors, so a broad aetiological workup is needed","True clinical anophthalmia is confirmed only when imaging shows no ocular tissue, as a rudimentary globe may be hidden","Early involvement of ocularists and orbital specialists supports socket and facial growth in severe cases"],"pathophysiology":"Microphthalmia and anophthalmia arise from disturbance of early eye morphogenesis. Normal eye formation depends on outgrowth of the optic vesicle from the developing forebrain and its invagination to form the optic cup, guided by a network of transcription factors including SOX2 and OTX2. Mutations in these developmental genes, chromosomal abnormalities, or environmental insults during embryogenesis can halt or distort this process, so the eye fails to grow to normal size (microphthalmia) or fails to develop at all (anophthalmia). Because the same signalling controls closure of the optic fissure, coloboma frequently coexists, and because these genes act in other tissues, associated brain, pituitary and systemic anomalies are common.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated coloboma at the milder end of the MAC spectrum","Cryptophthalmos and other eyelid or orbital malformations","Congenital cystic eye or orbital cyst","Phthisis bulbi from an acquired process mimicking a small eye","Nanophthalmos and posterior microphthalmos with a normal external appearance","Syndromic associations such as CHARGE syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed ophthalmic examination and measurement of axial length by ocular biometry","Orbital and cranial imaging (ultrasound, CT or MRI) to confirm the presence and size of ocular tissue","Systemic and neurodevelopmental assessment, including for pituitary and brain anomalies","Chromosomal and molecular genetic testing including SOX2 and OTX2","Assessment of the fellow eye and orbit to guide prosthetic and reconstructive planning"],"redFlags":["Bilateral severe microphthalmia or anophthalmia implying profound visual impairment","Associated neurological, endocrine or systemic anomalies requiring urgent evaluation","Progressive orbital or facial asymmetry from impaired socket growth","Family history indicating a heritable cause needing genetic counselling"],"prognosis":"Visual prognosis depends on the severity and laterality of the malformation, ranging from useful vision in mild unilateral microphthalmia to no vision in bilateral anophthalmia; in severe cases the focus shifts to promoting orbital and facial growth with expanders and prostheses, and overall outcome is strongly influenced by associated systemic conditions.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing anophthalmia clinically without imaging to exclude hidden rudimentary ocular tissue","Overlooking associated brain, pituitary or systemic anomalies","Delaying socket expansion and prosthetic management, impairing orbital and facial development","Assuming a normal-appearing eye excludes posterior microphthalmos without biometry"],"management":["Promote orbital and mid-facial growth from infancy with progressively enlarging conformers, orbital expanders and ocular prostheses","Maximise and protect vision in any seeing eye with refractive correction, amblyopia therapy and protective eyewear","Manage coexisting ocular anomalies such as coloboma or cataract, and monitor for glaucoma and retinal detachment","Undertake systemic and neurodevelopmental assessment, including pituitary and brain evaluation, and treat any endocrine deficiency","Arrange molecular genetic testing (including SOX2 and OTX2) with genetic counselling and family assessment","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary team of ophthalmology, oculoplastic surgery, clinical genetics and paediatrics","Provide low-vision rehabilitation and educational support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"microphthalmia_coloboma":{"name":"Microphthalmia-anophthalmia-coloboma spectrum","system":"Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAC spectrum","Ocular coloboma","Uveal coloboma","Anophthalmia-microphthalmia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical ophthalmology and genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Coloboma results from failure of the embryonic optic (choroidal) fissure to close, so defects lie in a characteristic inferonasal distribution.","The malformations form a continuum: isolated iris coloboma, chorioretinal and optic disc coloboma, microphthalmia (a small but structured eye) and anophthalmia (a clinically absent eye).","Visual prognosis depends chiefly on whether the macula and optic nerve are involved; an iris coloboma alone producing a keyhole pupil may spare vision.","A substantial proportion are syndromic; CHARGE association (CHD7) is the classic link, while SOX2 and OTX2 are leading causes of anophthalmia and severe microphthalmia.","Chorioretinal colobomas carry a lifelong risk of retinal detachment through the thinned coloboma bed.","Severe microphthalmia and anophthalmia require early orbital conformers and expanders to stimulate orbital and mid-facial growth.","Bilateral disease and midline brain or pituitary anomalies (SOX2, OTX2) mandate neuro-imaging and endocrine review.","A colobomatous keyhole iris should prompt a search for posterior segment and systemic involvement rather than being dismissed as cosmetic."],"pathophysiology":"During early embryonic development the optic vesicle invaginates to form the optic cup, and the choroidal (optic) fissure on its inferonasal aspect must fuse. Failure of this fusion produces coloboma, which characteristically affects the structures lying along the fissure line, namely the iris, ciliary body, retina and choroid and the optic disc, in an inferonasal distribution. Disruption of the transcriptional network that governs ocular morphogenesis, including SOX2, OTX2, PAX6, RAX and downstream regulators, at earlier stages arrests growth of the globe to produce microphthalmia, or prevents optic vesicle formation altogether to produce anophthalmia; the three phenotypes therefore represent a graded severity of the same developmental programme. Environmental insults such as maternal vitamin A deficiency or excess, gestational infection or teratogen exposure can phenocopy the genetic forms, and inheritance in familial disease is most often autosomal dominant with marked variable expressivity.","additionalDifferentials":["CHARGE syndrome (coloboma, heart defects, choanal atresia, growth retardation, genital and ear anomalies)","Congenital infection with chorioretinal scarring mimicking chorioretinal coloboma","Retinoblastoma or other cause of leukocoria when microphthalmia has an intraocular mass","Persistent fetal vasculature (persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous)","Morning glory disc anomaly, distinct from a true optic disc coloboma","Isolated congenital cataract and anterior segment dysgenesis","Nanophthalmos or posterior microphthalmos"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed dilated fundus examination and slit-lamp assessment of both eyes","Ocular biometry and B-scan ultrasound to document axial length in microphthalmia","Orbital and neuro-imaging (MRI) of optic nerves, chiasm, pituitary and brain","Chromosomal microarray with a targeted gene panel (SOX2, OTX2, PAX6, CHD7 and others)","Systemic evaluation for CHARGE including echocardiography, hearing assessment and endocrine review","Examination of parents and relatives for subtle iris coloboma in dominant families"],"redFlags":["Sudden visual loss or a new field defect indicating retinal detachment through a chorioretinal coloboma","Leukocoria that may represent retinoblastoma rather than coloboma","Cyanosis or feeding difficulty from choanal atresia suggesting CHARGE association","Midline brain or pituitary anomaly with hypopituitarism in SOX2 or OTX2 disease"],"prognosis":"Visual outcome ranges from normal in isolated iris coloboma to severe bilateral impairment with macular or optic nerve coloboma and with anophthalmia. Overall systemic prognosis is governed by any underlying syndrome rather than by the eye malformation alone.","pitfalls":["Assuming an isolated eye finding when it is the presenting sign of a syndrome, so failing to screen for CHARGE or midline defects.","Mistaking a chorioretinal coloboma for old chorioretinitis scarring.","Under-recognising the lifelong risk of retinal detachment.","Forgetting early socket expansion with conformers in infants with anophthalmia, which is needed to promote orbital and facial growth.","Neglecting amblyopia therapy in unilateral cases with useful residual vision."],"management":["Correct refractive error and treat amblyopia in eyes with useful visual potential","Monitor chorioretinal colobomas lifelong for retinal detachment and treat detachment promptly with vitreoretinal surgery","Fit orbital conformers and expanders early in severe microphthalmia and anophthalmia to stimulate orbital and mid-facial growth, followed by prostheses","Manage associated glaucoma or cataract as they arise","Investigate for syndromic associations, notably CHARGE, with systemic evaluation including cardiac, hearing and endocrine assessment","Arrange chromosomal microarray and a targeted gene panel (SOX2, OTX2, PAX6, CHD7) with genetic counselling and examination of relatives for subtle iris coloboma","Provide low-vision aids and multidisciplinary ophthalmology, genetics and paediatric support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical ophthalmology reference texts","Paediatric ophthalmology and strabismus reference texts","Principles and practice of medical genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"microscopic_polyangiitis":{"name":"Microscopic polyangiitis","system":"Rheumatology / Vasculitis","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPA","microscopic polyarteritis","ANCA-associated vasculitis, MPO type"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology / ACR-EULAR classification criteria","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A pauci-immune, non-granulomatous necrotising small-vessel vasculitis, most strongly associated with perinuclear ANCA directed against myeloperoxidase","The classic presentation is a pulmonary-renal syndrome with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis and, in severe cases, diffuse alveolar haemorrhage","Unlike granulomatosis with polyangiitis, it lacks destructive upper-airway granulomas, and unlike EGPA it lacks asthma and eosinophilia","Palpable purpura, mononeuritis multiplex, and constitutional symptoms such as weight loss and fever are common accompaniments","Renal biopsy typically shows focal necrotising crescentic glomerulonephritis with scant immune deposits (pauci-immune)","Remission induction uses glucocorticoids combined with rituximab or cyclophosphamide, with plasma exchange reserved for selected severe presentations","Relapse risk is lower than in PR3-associated disease but chronic kidney injury frequently persists"],"pathophysiology":"Microscopic polyangiitis is a systemic pauci-immune small-vessel vasculitis within the ANCA-associated spectrum. Circulating anti-myeloperoxidase antibodies prime and activate neutrophils, which adhere to and degranulate against endothelium, releasing reactive oxygen species and proteolytic enzymes that produce necrotising inflammation of capillaries, venules, and arterioles. In the kidney this yields a focal segmental necrotising and crescentic glomerulonephritis with minimal immunoglobulin deposition, and in the lung it can cause alveolar capillaritis with haemorrhage. Unlike granulomatosis with polyangiitis, granuloma formation is characteristically absent.","additionalDifferentials":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Anti-glomerular basement membrane disease","IgA vasculitis","Cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis","Polyarteritis nodosa","Lupus nephritis with systemic features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["ANCA testing with anti-myeloperoxidase and anti-proteinase-3 specificities","Urinalysis and microscopy for dysmorphic haematuria and red cell casts","Renal function and urine protein quantification","Chest imaging and, where indicated, bronchoscopy for alveolar haemorrhage","Renal or other tissue biopsy demonstrating pauci-immune necrotising vasculitis","Anti-GBM antibody testing to exclude overlapping disease"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising creatinine with active urinary sediment indicating crescentic glomerulonephritis","Haemoptysis and hypoxaemia suggesting diffuse alveolar haemorrhage","Progressive sensorimotor deficits from mononeuritis multiplex","Oliguria or dialysis-requiring acute kidney injury","Widespread purpura with systemic deterioration"],"prognosis":"Prompt immunosuppression induces remission in most patients, but many are left with chronic kidney disease and some progress to end-stage renal failure. Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage and advanced renal impairment at presentation predict worse outcomes.","pitfalls":["Delaying biopsy and treatment while awaiting ANCA serology in a deteriorating patient","Failing to distinguish MPA from anti-GBM disease, which alters urgency and use of plasma exchange","Overlooking alveolar haemorrhage when haemoptysis is minimal but haemoglobin falls","Attributing pauci-immune crescentic nephritis to another cause without checking ANCA"],"management":["Induce remission with high-dose glucocorticoids combined with rituximab or cyclophosphamide","Add the C5a-receptor antagonist avacopan to reduce cumulative glucocorticoid exposure","Reserve plasma exchange for severe presentations such as diffuse alveolar haemorrhage or severe, advancing renal failure","Maintain remission with rituximab or azathioprine to prevent relapse","Support failing organs with renal replacement therapy for severe kidney injury and respiratory support for alveolar haemorrhage","Give prophylaxis against Pneumocystis, bone protection and vaccination while immunosuppressed","Monitor renal function, urinalysis, ANCA titre and drug toxicity to detect relapse and complications","Manage jointly with rheumatology, nephrology and respiratory teams"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","ACR-EULAR classification criteria for microscopic polyangiitis"],"crossLinks":[]},"microspherophakia":{"name":"Microspherophakia","system":"Eye / Anterior segment","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["spherophakia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ophthalmology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A developmental anomaly in which the crystalline lens is small and abnormally spherical","The increased lens curvature causes marked lenticular myopia","The spherical lens and weak zonules predispose to lens subluxation and to pupillary-block glaucoma","It may be isolated or part of Weill-Marchesani syndrome and other connective tissue disorders","Pupillary block can be paradoxically worsened by miotic drugs and improved by cycloplegics, which is important for management","Careful glaucoma management and lens surgery in selected cases are the mainstays"],"pathophysiology":"Microspherophakia results from arrested lens development leaving a small, highly curved (spherical) lens with attenuated or abnormal zonules. The increased curvature produces high myopia, and the lax zonules allow the lens to move anteriorly. Anterior movement of the spherical lens can obstruct the pupil, causing pupillary-block angle-closure glaucoma; miotics worsen this by relaxing the zonule and allowing further forward lens movement, whereas cycloplegics tighten the zonule and pull the lens back. Zonular weakness also predisposes to frank lens subluxation.","additionalDifferentials":["Weill-Marchesani syndrome","Marfan syndrome and other causes of ectopia lentis","Homocystinuria","Isolated ectopia lentis","Primary angle-closure glaucoma of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp assessment of lens size, shape and position","Refraction demonstrating lenticular myopia","Gonioscopy and intraocular pressure for angle-closure risk","Assessment for systemic features of Weill-Marchesani or other syndromes","Family history and genetic evaluation where indicated"],"redFlags":["Acute angle-closure (pupillary block) glaucoma","Lens subluxation or dislocation","Worsening pressure after miotic use","Systemic features of a connective tissue syndrome"],"prognosis":"With appropriate refractive correction and glaucoma management the prognosis can be reasonable, but recurrent pupillary block and lens dislocation may necessitate surgery and carry risk to vision.","pitfalls":["Giving miotics for glaucoma, which worsen pupillary block in spherophakia","Missing associated Weill-Marchesani or homocystinuria","Overlooking lens subluxation","Attributing high myopia solely to axial length"],"management":["Correct the lenticular myopia with spectacles or contact lenses","Relieve and prevent pupillary-block angle closure with cycloplegic (mydriatic) agents and laser peripheral iridotomy, avoiding miotics which worsen the block","Lower intraocular pressure with topical pressure-lowering agents when glaucoma is present","Undertake lens extraction in selected eyes with recurrent pupillary block or lens subluxation and dislocation","Monitor lifelong for lens subluxation, angle closure and progressive glaucomatous optic nerve damage","Assess for Weill-Marchesani and other connective tissue syndromes, with genetic evaluation and family review"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"microsporidiosis":{"name":"Microsporidiosis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Enterocytozoon bieneusi infection","Encephalitozoon intestinalis infection","microsporidia infection","microsporidian enteritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell/Manson's Tropical Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Microsporidia are spore-forming, obligate intracellular organisms now classified with the fungi, transmitted by ingesting or inhaling environmentally resistant spores","Enterocytozoon bieneusi and Encephalitozoon intestinalis are the commonest species causing chronic, watery, non-bloody diarrhoea and wasting in people with advanced HIV","The infection came to prominence as an AIDS-defining cause of intractable diarrhoea before widespread antiretroviral therapy","Encephalitozoon species can disseminate beyond the gut to cause keratoconjunctivitis, sinusitis, hepatitis, nephritis and encephalitis, unlike the largely enteric E. bieneusi","Diagnosis relies on detecting the small spores in stool or biopsy using chromotrope-based (modified trichrome) stains that colour the spores pinkish-red against a pale background, or with calcofluor and immunofluorescence","Albendazole is effective against Encephalitozoon species, whereas E. bieneusi responds poorly and is better managed with fumagillin where available","Immune reconstitution with antiretroviral therapy is the most important measure for durable resolution in HIV-associated disease"],"pathophysiology":"Microsporidia are unicellular, obligate intracellular organisms that infect host cells by explosively everting a coiled polar tubule, through which the sporoplasm is injected into the cytoplasm. Intracellular multiplication culminates in the release of resistant spores that are shed in stool, urine or respiratory secretions and transmit infection. In the gut, infection of enterocytes causes villous atrophy, epithelial disarray and malabsorption with a secretory diarrhoea. Effective control depends on cell-mediated immunity, so heavy chronic infection and dissemination to eye, sinuses, liver, kidney and central nervous system occur predominantly in profound immunosuppression such as advanced HIV.","additionalDifferentials":["Cryptosporidiosis","Cystoisosporiasis","Cyclosporiasis","Giardiasis","HIV enteropathy","Cytomegalovirus enterocolitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy with chromotrope (modified trichrome) staining for spores","Calcofluor white or immunofluorescent staining of faecal smears","Molecular PCR with species identification","Small-bowel biopsy with electron microscopy when stool testing is inconclusive","HIV testing and CD4 count","Urine, corneal scrapings or other specimens when dissemination is suspected"],"redFlags":["Chronic dehydrating diarrhoea with severe wasting in advanced HIV","Visual symptoms suggesting microsporidial keratoconjunctivitis","Features of disseminated disease such as renal, hepatic or neurological involvement","Failure of enteric symptoms to settle despite species-directed therapy"],"prognosis":"In immunocompetent hosts infection is usually mild and self-limiting; in advanced HIV it can be chronic and disabling, though outcomes improved markedly with immune reconstitution and species-directed therapy.","pitfalls":["Missing the tiny spores because routine stool microscopy lacks a chromotrope or fluorescent stain","Treating all species with albendazole and expecting response, when Enterocytozoon bieneusi responds poorly","Overlooking extraintestinal sites such as the eye in disseminated Encephalitozoon infection","Neglecting antiretroviral therapy, without which relapse is likely"],"management":["Treat Encephalitozoon species with albendazole, and Enterocytozoon bieneusi with fumagillin where available","Restore cell-mediated immunity with antiretroviral therapy in HIV, which is central to clearing and preventing relapse of infection","Provide supportive care with rehydration, electrolyte correction, nutritional support and antidiarrhoeal agents for the secretory diarrhoea","Treat microsporidial keratoconjunctivitis with topical fumagillin alongside systemic therapy","Monitor the CD4 count and clinical response, remaining alert to dissemination to eye, sinuses, liver, kidney and central nervous system","Advise on prevention through safe water and hand hygiene, particularly in the immunocompromised"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"microvillus_inclusion_disease":{"name":"Microvillus inclusion disease","system":"Gastroenterology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MYO5B","MVID","microvillus atrophy","congenital microvillus atrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive congenital enteropathy, most often caused by biallelic MYO5B variants, presenting in the first days of life with severe watery diarrhoea","The diarrhoea is predominantly secretory and persists during fasting, producing profound dehydration and electrolyte loss","Small-bowel biopsy shows villus atrophy with absent or shortened apical microvilli and characteristic intracytoplasmic microvillus inclusions on electron microscopy","PAS and CD10 immunostaining reveal abnormal apical accumulation of brush-border material within enterocytes","A milder late-onset variant exists, and some patients develop cholestatic liver disease reflecting the wider role of MYO5B in apical trafficking","Affected infants are dependent on total parenteral nutrition, and definitive treatment for intractable cases is intestinal transplantation","Antenatal or early genetic diagnosis is valuable given the severity and lifelong nutritional dependence"],"pathophysiology":"Microvillus inclusion disease arises from defective apical trafficking within the enterocyte. Loss-of-function variants in MYO5B (and less often STX3 or STXBP2) disrupt the myosin Vb-mediated recycling of apical membrane and transporter proteins to the brush border. The result is failure to assemble a normal apical microvillus surface, with brush-border components mislocalised into cytoplasmic vesicles and microvillus inclusions. Because functional transporters and the absorptive surface are lost, the intestine cannot reabsorb fluid and electrolytes, producing a severe secretory diarrhoea that continues even when enteral feeds are withheld.","additionalDifferentials":["Tufting enteropathy (congenital tufting enteropathy, EPCAM)","Congenital chloride diarrhoea","Congenital sodium diarrhoea","Autoimmune enteropathy and IPEX syndrome","Enteroendocrine cell dysgenesis (NEUROG3)","Congenital secretory diarrhoea from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool electrolytes and calculation of the faecal osmotic gap to confirm a secretory pattern","Small-bowel mucosal biopsy with light microscopy and electron microscopy","PAS and CD10 immunohistochemistry to demonstrate abnormal apical brush-border accumulation","Molecular genetic testing for MYO5B, STX3 and STXBP2 variants","Serial fluid balance, serum electrolytes and acid-base assessment during acute management"],"redFlags":["Profound dehydration and haemodynamic instability from massive fluid loss in the neonatal period","Severe electrolyte and acid-base derangement requiring intensive correction","Failure of diarrhoea to abate on complete bowel rest, indicating a secretory process","Emerging cholestatic liver disease during long-term parenteral nutrition"],"prognosis":"The classic early-onset form carries a guarded prognosis with lifelong dependence on parenteral nutrition and its complications; intestinal or combined transplantation offers the main prospect of enteral autonomy. Milder late-onset phenotypes may be managed with prolonged nutritional support.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the persistent secretory diarrhoea for infection and delaying biopsy and genetic testing","Attributing the picture to feed intolerance and repeatedly changing formulas rather than investigating for a congenital enteropathy","Failing to obtain electron microscopy, which is often required to identify the diagnostic inclusions","Overlooking parenteral-nutrition-associated cholestasis and catheter-related complications during long-term care"],"management":["Sustain nutrition and hydration with total parenteral nutrition, on which affected infants are dependent from birth","Meticulously replace the fluid and electrolyte losses of the secretory diarrhoea, with careful acid-base management","Prevent and manage parenteral-nutrition complications, including catheter-related sepsis and intestinal failure-associated (cholestatic) liver disease, through careful line care and lipid strategies","Offer intestinal, or combined liver-intestine, transplantation for intractable disease to achieve enteral autonomy","Monitor growth, nutritional status and hepatic function, watching for cholestasis","Provide genetic counselling and testing for MYO5B and related genes in this autosomal recessive disorder, with family screening","Coordinate a multidisciplinary team of paediatric gastroenterology, specialist nutrition and intestinal transplantation services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"middle_lobe_syndrome":{"name":"middle lobe syndrome","system":"respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["right middle lobe syndrome","Brock syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray and Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Middle lobe syndrome denotes recurrent or chronic collapse, consolidation or bronchiectasis of the right middle lobe, and by extension the lingula.","Anatomical predisposition is central: the right middle lobe bronchus is long and narrow, arises at an acute angle, and is surrounded by a collar of lymph nodes, with relatively poor collateral ventilation to the lobe.","It is classified into obstructive forms (endobronchial tumour, broncholith, extrinsic nodal compression, foreign body) and non-obstructive forms (recurrent infection, asthma, impaired clearance).","In adults, a persistently collapsed middle lobe must prompt exclusion of an endobronchial neoplasm before it is assumed to be benign.","In children and non-smokers, asthma, recurrent infection and impaired mucociliary clearance are common non-obstructive causes.","Presentation includes chronic cough, recurrent focal pneumonia, wheeze and haemoptysis, or it may be an incidental radiographic finding.","The lateral chest radiograph shows a characteristic thin, wedge-shaped opacity overlying the cardiac shadow; CT delineates bronchiectasis, volume loss and any obstructing lesion.","Bronchoscopy is central to excluding an obstructing lesion and obtaining microbiological and cytological samples; non-tuberculous mycobacterial infection (the 'Lady Windermere' pattern) is an important association."],"pathophysiology":"The right middle lobe is anatomically vulnerable: its bronchus is comparatively long and slender, branches at an acute angle from the bronchus intermedius, and is encircled by hilar and interlobar lymph nodes, while collateral ventilation from adjacent lobes is limited because the lobe is largely surrounded by fissures. Any process that narrows the bronchus — an endobronchial tumour, broncholith, aspirated foreign body, or extrinsic compression by enlarged nodes — readily produces atelectasis with impaired clearance and post-obstructive infection. In non-obstructive disease, recurrent infection, asthma or ciliary and clearance defects lead to mucus retention and repeated inflammation; because collateral ventilation cannot easily re-expand the lobe, atelectasis persists and a cycle of infection, inflammation and bronchial wall damage ensues, culminating in bronchiectasis and chronic volume loss. Non-tuberculous mycobacterial infection may both result from and perpetuate this process, classically in the middle lobe and lingula.","additionalDifferentials":["endobronchial carcinoma or carcinoid tumour","broncholithiasis obstructing the middle lobe bronchus","aspirated foreign body","non-tuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary infection","bronchiectasis from other causes","extrinsic nodal compression from sarcoidosis, tuberculosis or malignancy","recurrent aspiration or asthma-related mucus plugging"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["chest CT with attention to the middle lobe bronchus, nodes and bronchiectasis","flexible bronchoscopy to exclude obstruction and obtain samples","sputum and bronchoalveolar lavage for bacteria, mycobacteria and fungi","cytology or biopsy of any endobronchial lesion","pulmonary function testing and assessment for asthma","evaluation for impaired mucociliary clearance or immunodeficiency in recurrent disease","tuberculin or interferon-gamma release testing where granulomatous disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["haemoptysis or an endobronchial lesion suggesting malignancy","persistent lobar collapse in an adult smoker","systemic features such as weight loss or night sweats","recurrent pneumonia confined to the same lobe despite treatment","progressive bronchiectasis with worsening sputum burden"],"prognosis":"Non-obstructive disease often improves with treatment of infection, airway inflammation and clearance, whereas established bronchiectasis or an obstructing lesion may require targeted therapy or surgical resection; outcome is generally good once malignancy is excluded and the underlying cause addressed.","pitfalls":["Assuming benign disease and omitting bronchoscopy in an adult with a persistently collapsed middle lobe.","Overlooking non-tuberculous mycobacterial infection as both cause and consequence of the syndrome.","Missing a small obstructing broncholith or foreign body on low-resolution imaging.","Attributing recurrent focal pneumonia to simple community infection without pursuing the anatomical explanation.","Proceeding to resection without adequately characterising and treating a reversible infective or inflammatory cause."],"management":["Exclude and relieve endobronchial obstruction bronchoscopically, removing a foreign body or broncholith and treating any endobronchial tumour","Treat non-obstructive causes by optimising asthma control with inhaled corticosteroids and treating infection with appropriate antibiotics","Promote airway clearance with chest physiotherapy and secretion-clearance techniques, especially where bronchiectasis is present","Treat recurrent or post-obstructive infection with targeted antibiotic therapy guided by sputum and lavage cultures","Consider surgical resection (middle lobectomy) for refractory disease, established localised bronchiectasis or a resectable obstructing lesion","Investigate and manage underlying impaired mucociliary clearance or immunodeficiency in recurrent disease","Follow up with imaging to confirm resolution and ensure malignancy has been excluded"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray and Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine, current edition","Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders, current edition","Contemporary reviews of right middle lobe syndrome and non-tuberculous mycobacterial lung disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"miliaria":{"name":"Miliaria","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["heat rash","prickly heat","sweat rash","miliaria rubra","miliaria crystallina"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Miliaria arises from blockage of eccrine sweat ducts, so it favours hot, humid conditions, febrile illness, occlusion, and overdressing, and is common in neonates and in the newly bedridden","The subtype reflects the depth of duct obstruction: crystallina shows tiny clear, easily ruptured superficial vesicles; rubra produces itchy or prickly erythematous papulovesicles; and the deeper profunda presents as firm, skin-coloured papules","Typical sites are areas of occlusion and friction such as the trunk, flexures, and skin under clothing or dressings","In infants it commonly affects the neck, groin, and axillae and is easily provoked by warm environments and heavy wrapping","The cornerstone of management is cooling and reducing sweating: a cooler environment, loose breathable clothing, and avoiding occlusion, which allow the ducts to reopen","It is generally self-limiting once the provoking heat and occlusion are removed, and specific drug treatment is rarely needed","Widespread deep miliaria can impair sweating and predispose to heat intolerance and, in extreme heat, heat exhaustion","Secondary bacterial infection (miliaria pustulosa) can complicate the picture if lesions are scratched or persist"],"pathophysiology":"Miliaria results from obstruction of the eccrine sweat ducts, which causes sweat to leak into the surrounding skin rather than reach the surface. Keratin plugging of the duct, often aggravated by an overgrowth of skin flora producing occlusive material, blocks flow at varying levels. The level of the block determines the clinical form: obstruction within the stratum corneum causes the superficial vesicles of crystallina, blockage within the epidermis produces the inflammatory papulovesicles of rubra, and obstruction at the dermo-epidermal junction gives the deeper papules of profunda. The extravasated sweat provokes local inflammation and the sensation of prickling, while widespread duct blockage can compromise thermoregulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Folliculitis","Acute eczema or contact dermatitis","Candidal intertrigo in flexures","Viral exanthem or drug eruption","Neonatal erythema toxicum or transient neonatal pustular melanosis","Grover's disease (transient acantholytic dermatosis) in adults"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical, based on morphology and a history of heat, occlusion, or fever","Skin swab if secondary bacterial infection (pustular miliaria) is suspected","Investigations are seldom required, and biopsy is reserved for atypical or persistent cases","Assessment of the environment and clothing or dressing practices, especially in infants and bedridden patients"],"redFlags":["Extensive deep (profunda) involvement impairing sweating with symptoms of heat intolerance or heat exhaustion","Signs of secondary bacterial infection such as pustules, increasing pain, or spreading erythema","Systemic upset or fever suggesting an alternative or complicating diagnosis","Failure to settle after removing heat and occlusion, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Miliaria is benign and typically resolves quickly once overheating and occlusion are addressed; recurrence is common with repeated heat exposure, and only extensive deep disease or secondary infection causes significant problems.","pitfalls":["Treating with occlusive creams or heavy dressings that worsen duct blockage","Overwrapping or overdressing infants and perpetuating the rash","Mistaking pustular miliaria with secondary infection for a primary bacterial folliculitis or vice versa","Overlooking impaired thermoregulation in extensive deep disease during hot conditions"],"management":["Cool the patient and reduce sweating with a cooler, less humid environment and air conditioning to allow the ducts to reopen","Wear loose, breathable clothing and avoid occlusive fabrics, dressings and overdressing","In infants, avoid heavy wrapping and keep the neck, groin and axillae cool and dry","Relieve the itch and prickling of miliaria rubra with cooling measures and, if needed, a short course of a topical corticosteroid","Treat secondary bacterial infection (pustular miliaria) with topical or systemic antibiotics guided by swab","Reassure that the condition is self-limiting once overheating and occlusion are removed, and advise avoiding future heat exposure to prevent recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"milk_alkali_syndrome":{"name":"Milk-alkali syndrome","system":"Renal / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["calcium-alkali syndrome","milk alkali syndrome","Burnett syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A triad of hypercalcaemia, metabolic alkalosis and acute kidney injury caused by ingesting large amounts of calcium together with absorbable alkali","Historically from milk and antacids taken for peptic ulcer; now more often from calcium carbonate used for osteoporosis prevention, dyspepsia or as a phosphate binder","Increasingly recognised in older women and in patients with chronic kidney disease, who handle a calcium load less well","It presents with symptoms of hypercalcaemia such as nausea, vomiting, polyuria and confusion, alongside features of volume depletion and renal impairment","Hypercalcaemia causes renal vasoconstriction and a natriuresis that worsens volume depletion, which further impairs calcium excretion in a self-reinforcing cycle","Unlike primary hyperparathyroidism, parathyroid hormone is appropriately suppressed and the acid-base picture is a metabolic alkalosis rather than acidosis","Nephrocalcinosis may develop with prolonged exposure","Management is to stop the calcium and alkali and restore volume with intravenous fluids, after which the hypercalcaemia and renal function usually improve"],"pathophysiology":"A high intake of calcium plus absorbable alkali raises the serum calcium, which acts on the calcium-sensing receptor to reduce sodium and water reabsorption, producing a natriuresis and volume depletion. Hypercalcaemia also causes renal afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction, lowering the glomerular filtration rate. The fall in filtration and in circulating volume reduces excretion of both calcium and bicarbonate, so calcium and alkali accumulate, generating a metabolic alkalosis that perpetuates the hypercalcaemia. Alkalosis itself enhances distal tubular calcium reabsorption, closing the self-reinforcing loop, and prolonged hypercalcaemia can lead to calcium deposition within the kidney.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperparathyroidism","Malignancy-associated hypercalcaemia","Vitamin D intoxication","Sarcoidosis and other granulomatous diseases","Thiazide-induced hypercalcaemia","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium and parathyroid hormone, the latter suppressed in milk-alkali syndrome","Venous blood gas or bicarbonate demonstrating a metabolic alkalosis","Renal function and electrolytes","A careful history of calcium, antacid and supplement intake","Serum phosphate and vitamin D to exclude other causes of hypercalcaemia","Renal imaging for nephrocalcinosis if the exposure has been chronic"],"redFlags":["Severe symptomatic hypercalcaemia with marked confusion or arrhythmia risk","Significant acute kidney injury or oliguria","Profound volume depletion","Persisting hypercalcaemia after intake has stopped, suggesting another cause"],"prognosis":"Usually reversible once the calcium and alkali are withdrawn and volume is restored, though repeated or prolonged episodes can leave residual chronic kidney disease and nephrocalcinosis.","pitfalls":["Not asking about over-the-counter calcium supplements and antacids","Misdiagnosing as hyperparathyroidism despite a suppressed PTH and an alkalosis","Overlooking the syndrome in CKD patients taking calcium-based phosphate binders","Failing to correct volume depletion promptly"],"management":["Withdraw the causative calcium and absorbable alkali, such as calcium carbonate, milk and antacids","Restore intravascular volume with intravenous isotonic saline, which also promotes urinary calcium excretion","Add a loop diuretic only after volume repletion if hypercalcaemia persists, and use bisphosphonates sparingly because of the risk of rebound hypocalcaemia","Allow the metabolic alkalosis to correct as volume and electrolytes are restored","Monitor serum calcium, renal function and electrolytes closely, watching for rebound hypocalcaemia","Review long-term supplement and antacid use and substitute alternative osteoporosis or dyspepsia therapy to prevent recurrence","Refer to nephrology if renal impairment persists or nephrocalcinosis is present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (Feehally et al.)","Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"millard_gubler_syndrome":{"name":"Millard-Gubler syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ventral pontine syndrome","ventrocaudal pontine syndrome","facial-abducens alternating hemiplegia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from a lesion of the ventral caudal pons, usually ischaemic, in the territory of paramedian and short circumferential branches of the basilar artery","Combines ipsilateral facial (seventh nerve) and abducens (sixth nerve) palsies with contralateral hemiparesis","The facial weakness is of lower motor neurone type, affecting the whole ipsilateral face including the forehead","The sixth nerve palsy causes failure of ipsilateral eye abduction and horizontal diplopia","Contralateral limb weakness reflects involvement of the corticospinal fibres in the basis pontis before they decussate","It is a lower alternating hemiplegia, contrasting with the midbrain (Weber) upper alternating hemiplegia","Adjacent involvement of horizontal gaze structures may add a gaze palsy, overlapping with Foville syndrome"],"pathophysiology":"Millard-Gubler syndrome follows a lesion of the ventral caudal pons supplied by paramedian and short circumferential branches of the basilar artery. The infarct or haemorrhage damages the intrapontine fascicles of the facial and abducens nerves as they course towards their exit, producing ipsilateral lower motor neurone facial weakness and a lateral rectus palsy, together with the descending corticospinal fibres in the basis pontis, producing contralateral hemiparesis with facial sparing on the weak side. Because the cranial nerve deficits are ipsilateral and the limb weakness contralateral, the picture is that of a caudal alternating hemiplegia; extension towards the paramedian pontine reticular formation can add an ipsilateral horizontal gaze palsy.","additionalDifferentials":["Foville syndrome (with conjugate gaze palsy)","Lateral pontine (Marie-Foix) syndrome","Locked-in syndrome from extensive ventral pontine infarction","Pontine demyelination or tumour","Cerebellopontine angle lesion affecting the facial and abducens nerves","Basilar artery thrombosis with evolving brainstem signs"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diffusion-weighted MRI of the pons to confirm a ventral caudal pontine lesion","MR or CT angiography of the basilar artery and posterior circulation","Assessment of facial nerve function distinguishing upper from lower motor neurone weakness","Ocular motility testing for abduction deficit and horizontal gaze","Cardiovascular and stroke risk workup including ECG and metabolic profiling","Vessel wall imaging where basilar atherosclerosis or dissection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive drowsiness or quadriparesis suggesting evolving basilar thrombosis","Bilateral pontine signs raising the possibility of a locked-in state","Rapidly worsening deficit indicating an expanding pontine haemorrhage","New respiratory or swallowing compromise from brainstem extension"],"prognosis":"Isolated small ventral pontine infarcts may show useful recovery of limb power and facial function, but larger lesions or those heralding basilar thrombosis carry a poor prognosis with risk of progression to a locked-in state.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the lower motor neurone facial palsy for a peripheral Bell palsy in isolation","Overlooking the contralateral hemiparesis and missing the brainstem localisation","Failing to recognise an evolving basilar occlusion behind apparently limited signs","Confusing an added gaze palsy with a hemispheric gaze deviation"],"management":["Treat the acute pontine infarct as a stroke, giving intravenous thrombolysis within the therapeutic window and considering endovascular thrombectomy for basilar artery occlusion","Admit to a stroke unit and monitor closely for progression to basilar thrombosis or a locked-in state","Institute secondary prevention with antiplatelet therapy, a statin and control of blood pressure and other vascular risk factors, using anticoagulation if a cardioembolic source is found","Protect the eye affected by facial weakness with lubricants and lid taping, and manage horizontal diplopia with occlusion or prisms","Provide multidisciplinary rehabilitation with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language therapy","Investigate and address the underlying cause, such as basilar atherosclerosis or arterial dissection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":["foville_syndrome","lateral_pontine_syndrome","locked_in_syndrome"]},"miller_fisher_syndrome":{"name":"Miller Fisher syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neuroimmunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Fisher syndrome","Miller-Fisher variant of Guillain-Barre syndrome","anti-GQ1b antibody syndrome (ophthalmoplegic variant)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically presents with the triad of ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and areflexia, often after a preceding respiratory or gastrointestinal infection","Regarded as a variant of Guillain-Barre syndrome and part of the anti-GQ1b antibody spectrum","Serum anti-GQ1b antibodies are positive in the great majority of cases and strongly support the diagnosis","Consciousness is preserved, distinguishing it from Bickerstaff brainstem encephalitis, which shares the anti-GQ1b mechanism but adds encephalopathy","Cerebrospinal fluid often shows albuminocytological dissociation, though this may be absent early","Overlap with limb-weakness Guillain-Barre syndrome can occur, so monitoring for respiratory and bulbar involvement is important","Treatment mirrors Guillain-Barre syndrome using intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange, with many patients also recovering spontaneously"],"pathophysiology":"Miller Fisher syndrome is an acute immune-mediated neuropathy triggered by infection, in which antibodies against the GQ1b ganglioside are generated through molecular mimicry with microbial (often Campylobacter jejuni) lipo-oligosaccharide. GQ1b is densely expressed in the oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nerves and in muscle-spindle afferents, so antibody binding there produces ophthalmoplegia and sensory ataxia, while impaired large-fibre afferent function causes areflexia. The peripheral, largely nodal-paranodal site of injury and the self-limiting immune response underlie the generally favourable outcome.","additionalDifferentials":["Bickerstaff brainstem encephalitis","Guillain-Barre syndrome (classic form)","Myasthenia gravis","Brainstem stroke","Wernicke encephalopathy","Botulism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum anti-GQ1b ganglioside antibody testing","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis for albuminocytological dissociation","Nerve conduction studies, which may show reduced sensory responses or subtle changes","MRI of the brain to exclude brainstem lesions when the picture is atypical","Screening for preceding Campylobacter or other infection","Respiratory function monitoring where overlap weakness is suspected"],"redFlags":["Evolving limb weakness or respiratory compromise indicating overlap with Guillain-Barre syndrome","Bulbar dysfunction threatening swallowing and the airway","Declining consciousness suggesting Bickerstaff brainstem encephalitis","Rapid deterioration requiring escalation of care"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally excellent, with most patients recovering fully over weeks to months, though those with overlapping Guillain-Barre features may have a more prolonged course.","pitfalls":["Assuming a benign course and missing evolving respiratory or bulbar weakness","Attributing ophthalmoplegia to myasthenia or stroke","Excluding the diagnosis when early cerebrospinal fluid protein is normal","Overlooking the encephalopathy that would reclassify the case as Bickerstaff brainstem encephalitis"],"management":["Treat with immunotherapy, either intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange, particularly where there is significant disability or Guillain-Barre overlap","Monitor respiratory and bulbar function closely for overlapping Guillain-Barre weakness, escalating to ventilatory support if required","Provide supportive care including venous thromboembolism prophylaxis and physiotherapy during recovery","Manage the ophthalmoplegia symptomatically with monocular occlusion for diplopia and ocular lubrication","Watch for and treat autonomic instability","Manage under neurology and neuroimmunology, reassuring that recovery is usually complete over weeks to months"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Continuum: Peripheral Nervous System Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"miller_syndrome":{"name":"Miller syndrome (postaxial acrofacial dysostosis, DHODH)","system":"Genetics / Craniofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["postaxial acrofacial dysostosis","Genee-Wiedemann syndrome","Wildervanck-Smith syndrome","acrofacial dysostosis, Miller type"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Combines a mandibulofacial dysostosis resembling Treacher Collins with postaxial (little-finger side) limb defects","Facial features include malar and mandibular hypoplasia, down-slanting palpebral fissures, lower eyelid ectropion or coloboma, and cup-shaped ears","The distinguishing limb finding is postaxial deficiency, with absence or hypoplasia of the fifth digits and forearm bones","Cleft lip and palate are common and add to feeding and speech difficulties","It is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner and caused by variants in DHODH, an enzyme of pyrimidine synthesis","Cognition is typically normal, distinguishing it from many other multiple-anomaly syndromes","The postaxial limb pattern is the key clinical feature separating it from the preaxial defects of Nager syndrome"],"pathophysiology":"Miller syndrome is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by variants in DHODH, which encodes dihydroorotate dehydrogenase, a mitochondrial enzyme catalysing a rate-limiting step in de novo pyrimidine biosynthesis. Reduced enzyme activity is thought to impair nucleotide supply for rapidly proliferating tissues during embryogenesis, particularly the cranial neural crest cells forming the first and second branchial arches and the developing limb buds. The resulting disturbance produces the mandibulofacial dysostosis together with postaxial (ulnar-side) limb reduction defects. Its placement among the acrofacial dysostoses, and its clinical resemblance to Nager syndrome, reflect the shared vulnerability of neural crest and limb development, with the preaxial versus postaxial limb pattern separating the two conditions.","additionalDifferentials":["Nager acrofacial dysostosis (preaxial limb involvement)","Treacher Collins syndrome (facial features without postaxial limb defects)","Acrofacial dysostosis, Rodriguez type","Postaxial polydactyly-associated syndromes","Cornelia de Lange syndrome","Ulnar-mammary syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing for biallelic DHODH variants","Radiographs of the limbs to characterise postaxial and forearm defects","Audiological assessment for conductive hearing loss","Craniofacial imaging to plan reconstruction and airway management","Feeding and swallowing assessment in infancy","Ophthalmological review for eyelid and ocular surface protection"],"redFlags":["Neonatal airway obstruction from severe micrognathia","Feeding failure requiring nutritional support","Recurrent aspiration from combined cleft and airway abnormality","Exposure keratopathy from eyelid coloboma or ectropion","Anticipated difficult intubation at any procedure"],"prognosis":"With airway stabilisation, feeding support, hearing rehabilitation, and staged craniofacial and limb reconstruction, most affected individuals have normal intellect and a reasonable functional outcome, the main early risk being airway compromise.","pitfalls":["Confusing Miller (postaxial) with Nager (preaxial) limb involvement","Assuming intellectual disability when cognition is typically preserved","Underestimating neonatal airway risk from severe micrognathia","Neglecting corneal protection where eyelid anomalies are present","Overlooking the autosomal recessive recurrence risk in counselling"],"management":["Secure the airway in infancy, from positioning and a nasopharyngeal airway to mandibular distraction osteogenesis or tracheostomy in severe micrognathia","Support feeding and growth with specialised cleft feeding techniques and nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding as needed","Undertake staged craniofacial reconstruction, including cleft lip and palate repair and malar and mandibular reconstruction","Provide orthopaedic and hand-surgical reconstruction for the postaxial limb and forearm defects","Rehabilitate conductive hearing loss with hearing aids and audiological follow-up to protect speech and language development","Protect the ocular surface where there is lower-lid ectropion or coloboma with lubrication and eyelid surgery","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive DHODH-related disorder with family testing","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary craniofacial team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: DHODH-Related Miller Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"milroy_disease":{"name":"Milroy disease","system":"Vascular anomalies / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Milroy's disease","congenital hereditary lymphoedema type I","FLT4/VEGFR3-related primary lymphoedema","Nonne-Milroy lymphoedema"],"chapter":null,"source":"ISSVA classification / Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant primary lymphoedema caused by variants in FLT4, which encodes the lymphatic growth factor receptor VEGFR3","It presents as congenital, usually bilateral, lower-limb lymphoedema, characteristically confined below the knee and often affecting the dorsum of the feet","Suggestive signs include upturned toenails, deep skin creases at the toes, prominent leg veins and, in males, hydrocele","Onset at or shortly after birth distinguishes it from FOXC2-related lymphoedema-distichiasis, which typically begins around puberty","The underlying defect is hypoplasia and functional failure of the initial lymphatics due to impaired VEGFR3 signalling","Recurrent cellulitis is a common and important complication of the affected limbs","Diagnosis rests on the congenital clinical picture, family history and FLT4 genetic testing, with lymphoscintigraphy showing impaired uptake","Management is lifelong supportive lymphoedema care with compression, meticulous skin care and prompt treatment of infection"],"pathophysiology":"Milroy disease is an autosomal dominant primary lymphoedema arising from loss-of-function variants in FLT4 (VEGFR3), the receptor for vascular endothelial growth factors C and D that drives development of the lymphatic vasculature. Defective VEGFR3 signalling causes hypoplasia and functional failure of the initial (peripheral) lymphatic vessels, so interstitial fluid cannot be adequately drained from the lower limbs, producing congenital, typically bilateral, below-knee lymphoedema. The chronic protein-rich oedema predisposes to skin changes and recurrent infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphoedema-distichiasis syndrome (FOXC2, later onset, distichiasis)","Other primary lymphoedemas (Meige/late-onset type)","Secondary lymphoedema (infective, filarial, post-surgical)","Congenital venous malformation with limb swelling","Lipoedema","Systemic causes of neonatal oedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["FLT4 (VEGFR3) molecular genetic testing","Lymphoscintigraphy demonstrating reduced or absent lymphatic uptake","Clinical examination for toenail changes, toe creases and hydrocele","Duplex ultrasound to exclude a venous cause of swelling","Assessment for and monitoring of recurrent cellulitis","Family history review and cascade genetic screening"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or severe cellulitis of the affected limb","Rapidly progressive or asymmetric swelling suggesting an additional secondary cause","Skin breakdown, ulceration or lymphorrhoea","Signs of chronic infection or systemic sepsis","New unilateral swelling in a previously stable patient warranting further evaluation"],"prognosis":"Milroy disease is a chronic lifelong condition but is compatible with normal life expectancy; with consistent conservative lymphoedema management most patients maintain good limb function, though recurrent cellulitis and progressive skin changes are the main sources of morbidity.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with the later-onset FOXC2 lymphoedema-distichiasis syndrome","Overlooking supportive clues such as upturned toenails and hydrocele","Delaying treatment of cellulitis, which accelerates lymphatic damage","Failing to provide genetic counselling for an autosomal dominant disorder"],"management":["Manage the lymphoedema conservatively with graduated compression garments, bandaging and complex decongestive therapy including manual lymphatic drainage and exercise","Maintain meticulous skin care and limb elevation to limit progression and protect the skin","Treat episodes of cellulitis promptly with antibiotics and offer prophylactic antibiotics for recurrent attacks","Consider microsurgical options such as lymphovenous anastomosis in carefully selected patients","Manage associated features such as hydrocele surgically where symptomatic","Provide genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant FLT4/VEGFR3 disorder and offer cascade family screening","Follow up long-term through a specialist lymphoedema service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ISSVA Classification of Vascular Anomalies","Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies: Haemangiomas and Malformations"],"crossLinks":[]},"milwaukee_shoulder":{"name":"Milwaukee shoulder syndrome","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Milwaukee shoulder","apatite-associated destructive arthritis","BCP destructive arthropathy of the shoulder","rotator cuff crystal arthropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Milwaukee shoulder is a rapidly destructive arthropathy of the shoulder driven by basic calcium phosphate (hydroxyapatite) crystals, typically affecting elderly women","It presents with a large, often blood-tinged, minimally inflammatory joint effusion together with shoulder pain and marked loss of function","There is extensive rotator cuff deficiency (a large or massive tear) that allows superior migration of the humeral head towards the acromion","Radiographs show glenohumeral joint destruction, superior subluxation of the humeral head into a high-riding position, and soft-tissue calcification","Synovial fluid contains relatively few cells (non-inflammatory to only mildly inflammatory) but harbours aggregates of BCP crystals, which are non-birefringent and require special staining such as alizarin red to suggest","The crystals stimulate synovial release of proteolytic enzymes, including collagenases and other proteases, which degrade cartilage and the rotator cuff in a self-perpetuating destructive cycle","Treatment is largely palliative, using analgesia, anti-inflammatory therapy, joint aspiration, activity modification, and physiotherapy, with surgery including arthroplasty options reserved for selected patients"],"pathophysiology":"In Milwaukee shoulder, basic calcium phosphate (hydroxyapatite) crystals within the joint are phagocytosed by synovial lining cells, which respond by secreting matrix metalloproteinases and other proteolytic enzymes. These enzymes degrade articular cartilage and the rotator cuff tendons; the resulting cuff deficiency allows the humeral head to migrate superiorly, and continued crystal shedding perpetuates enzyme release and progressive joint destruction. The effusions are large but contain few inflammatory cells, which helps distinguish the condition from septic or highly inflammatory arthritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Rotator cuff tear arthropathy","Septic arthritis","Gout or CPP crystal arthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis of the shoulder","Neuropathic (Charcot) shoulder","Rapidly progressive osteoarthritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Joint aspiration showing a large, blood-tinged, low-cell-count effusion, with culture to exclude infection","Alizarin red staining or electron microscopy to suggest or confirm BCP crystals, which are non-birefringent on polarised light","Radiographs showing glenohumeral destruction and superior humeral head migration","MRI or ultrasound to define the rotator cuff tear","Cross-sectional CT for bony destruction and surgical planning","Synovial fluid cell count and Gram stain to differentiate from sepsis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive joint destruction with loss of function","Features that could indicate septic arthritis superimposed on a destroyed joint","Large recurrent haemorrhagic effusions"],"prognosis":"The condition is typically progressive with a limited response to medical treatment; joint destruction and functional impairment tend to continue, so management is largely aimed at symptom control, with surgery considered only in selected patients because rotator cuff deficiency complicates reconstruction.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the large, low-inflammatory effusion for a benign process, or conversely over-diagnosing sepsis","Failing to detect the BCP crystals because they are non-birefringent","Overlooking the massive rotator cuff tear that drives the deformity","Assuming a standard arthroplasty will behave as it would in routine osteoarthritis, when cuff deficiency alters the options and outcome"],"management":["Focus on symptom control with simple analgesia and cautious use of NSAIDs, given the typically elderly patients","Perform therapeutic joint aspiration of large effusions, always with culture to exclude infection","Give intra-articular corticosteroid injection to reduce pain and effusion","Maintain range of movement and function with physiotherapy and activity modification","Reserve surgery for selected patients, with reverse total shoulder arthroplasty an option since rotator cuff deficiency precludes conventional repair","Monitor the joint and remain alert to superimposed septic arthritis given the destructive course"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Oxford Textbook of Rheumatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"mineralocorticoid_resistance":{"name":"Mineralocorticoid resistance (pseudohypoaldosteronism spectrum overview)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pseudohypoaldosteronism","PHA","pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1","aldosterone resistance"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of disorders in which target tissues resist aldosterone despite high aldosterone and renin, causing salt wasting rather than salt retention","Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1 has a renal autosomal dominant form (NR3C2 mineralocorticoid receptor) that is milder and often improves with age, and a systemic autosomal recessive form (epithelial sodium channel subunits SCNN1A, SCNN1B, SCNN1G) that is severe and lifelong","Typically presents in the neonate with failure to thrive, vomiting, dehydration, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia and metabolic acidosis","The systemic recessive form also affects sweat glands, colon and airways and can mimic cystic fibrosis with salty sweat and respiratory symptoms","Biochemistry shows hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, high plasma renin and markedly elevated aldosterone, in contrast to adrenal insufficiency where aldosterone is low","Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 2 (Gordon syndrome, WNK/KLHL3/CUL3) is the mirror image, with hyperkalaemia, hypertension and low renin","Management centres on sodium supplementation and control of hyperkalaemia, since mineralocorticoids are ineffective because the defect is downstream of the hormone"],"pathophysiology":"Mineralocorticoid resistance encompasses the pseudohypoaldosteronism syndromes, in which the distal nephron fails to respond to aldosterone despite elevated hormone levels. In the autosomal dominant renal form of type 1, heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in NR3C2 (the mineralocorticoid receptor) blunt renal sodium reabsorption. In the autosomal recessive systemic form, mutations in the epithelial sodium channel subunits abolish sodium transport across multiple aldosterone-responsive epithelia including kidney, colon, sweat and salivary glands, and lung. Impaired sodium reabsorption drives salt wasting, volume depletion and secondary activation of renin and aldosterone, while reduced electrogenic sodium uptake impairs potassium and hydrogen secretion, producing hyperkalaemia and metabolic acidosis. Type 2 (Gordon syndrome) instead reflects overactive distal sodium chloride reabsorption from mutations in the WNK-kinase pathway.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary adrenal insufficiency or salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia, where aldosterone is low","Aldosterone synthase deficiency","Cystic fibrosis, for the systemic form with salty sweat and respiratory features","Secondary or transient pseudohypoaldosteronism from urinary tract infection or obstructive uropathy in infants","Renal tubular acidosis","Gordon syndrome (pseudohypoaldosteronism type 2) for the hyperkalaemic hypertensive phenotype"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum electrolytes, urea, creatinine and venous blood gas showing hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia and metabolic acidosis","Plasma renin activity and aldosterone, both high in type 1","Urinary sodium and potassium to demonstrate renal salt wasting","Sweat chloride testing to help distinguish the systemic form from cystic fibrosis, noting both can raise sweat chloride","Renal tract imaging to exclude secondary or transient pseudohypoaldosteronism","Genetic testing of NR3C2, the epithelial sodium channel subunit genes, and the WNK pathway genes for type 2"],"redFlags":["Neonatal salt-wasting crisis with severe hyperkalaemia and cardiac arrhythmia","Failure to thrive and dehydration unresponsive to mineralocorticoid replacement","Respiratory compromise in the systemic form mimicking cystic fibrosis"],"prognosis":"The autosomal dominant renal form of type 1 is generally mild and tends to improve with age as dietary sodium intake rises, whereas the recessive systemic form requires lifelong aggressive salt supplementation and carries greater morbidity; with careful electrolyte management most patients grow and develop normally.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with adrenal insufficiency and giving mineralocorticoid, which is ineffective because resistance lies downstream","Missing transient or secondary pseudohypoaldosteronism due to infant urinary tract infection or obstruction","Mislabelling the systemic form as cystic fibrosis on the basis of a raised sweat chloride","Failing to distinguish salt-wasting type 1 from hypertensive type 2 (Gordon syndrome)"],"management":["Replace sodium generously with oral sodium chloride supplementation, the mainstay for salt-wasting pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1","Control hyperkalaemia with dietary potassium restriction and potassium-binding cation-exchange resins","Correct the metabolic acidosis with sodium bicarbonate or citrate","Treat acute salt-wasting crises with intravenous saline and emergency measures for severe hyperkalaemia","In the systemic recessive form, provide skin care and airway and respiratory management for the cystic-fibrosis-like manifestations","Manage pseudohypoaldosteronism type 2 (Gordon syndrome) differently, with dietary sodium restriction and a thiazide diuretic","Monitor growth, electrolytes and acid-base status regularly, expecting the dominant renal form to ease with age","Offer genetic testing and counselling to define the subtype and guide family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","DeGroot's Endocrinology","Sperling Pediatric Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"minimal_change_nephrotic_child":{"name":"Minimal Change Nephrotic Syndrome (Child)","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["minimal change disease","MCNS","idiopathic nephrotic syndrome of childhood","steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Minimal change disease accounts for the large majority of idiopathic nephrotic syndrome in children, most commonly presenting between about 1 and 6 years of age.","The defining features are heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminaemia, oedema, and hyperlipidaemia.","Oedema is typically dependent and periorbital and can progress to generalised swelling with ascites and pleural effusions.","Most children are steroid-responsive, and a favourable response to corticosteroids is both diagnostic and therapeutic in the typical age group.","Renal biopsy is generally reserved for atypical features such as age under 1 or over 10 years, persistent hypertension, gross haematuria, low complement, impaired renal function, or steroid resistance.","Children are at increased risk of infection, particularly spontaneous bacterial peritonitis from encapsulated organisms, because of urinary immunoglobulin losses.","A hypercoagulable state develops from urinary loss of anticoagulant proteins, increasing the risk of venous and arterial thrombosis.","Relapses are common, and some children become frequent relapsers or steroid-dependent, requiring steroid-sparing agents."],"pathophysiology":"A poorly understood immune dysregulation, involving circulating permeability factors and T-cell mediated signalling, injures the glomerular podocytes. On light microscopy the glomeruli appear normal, but electron microscopy reveals diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes. Loss of the size- and charge-selective filtration barrier permits massive albumin leakage into the urine, producing hypoalbuminaemia, reduced oncotic pressure, oedema, and compensatory hepatic lipoprotein synthesis causing hyperlipidaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","Membranous nephropathy","Post-infectious or membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Lupus nephritis","Congenital nephrotic syndrome (in infants)","Secondary nephrotic syndrome (e.g. Henoch-Schonlein, infection-related)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and quantification of proteinuria (protein:creatinine ratio or 24-hour protein)","Serum albumin, lipid profile, and renal function","Serum complement (C3, C4)","Autoimmune serology when secondary causes are suspected","Assessment for infection and, in selected cases, screening for thrombosis","Renal biopsy in atypical presentations or steroid resistance"],"redFlags":["Signs of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (fever, abdominal pain, tenderness)","Features of venous or arterial thrombosis, including limb swelling, respiratory compromise, or neurological signs","Hypovolaemic shock during aggressive diuresis","Severe respiratory distress from large pleural effusions or ascites","Steroid resistance or deteriorating renal function suggesting a more aggressive lesion"],"prognosis":"Children with steroid-sensitive minimal change disease generally have an excellent long-term renal prognosis, though many experience relapses over several years before remitting; steroid-resistant disease, often reflecting focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, carries a higher risk of progression to chronic kidney disease.","pitfalls":["Missing spontaneous bacterial peritonitis because ascitic signs can be subtle in an oedematous child","Overlooking thrombosis risk during periods of heavy proteinuria and diuresis","Assuming all childhood nephrotic syndrome is minimal change and delaying biopsy in atypical cases","Over-diuresing and precipitating hypovolaemia in a child with low intravascular volume"],"management":["Induce remission with oral corticosteroids (prednisolone), which are both diagnostic and therapeutic in the typical age group","For frequent relapses or steroid dependence, add steroid-sparing agents such as levamisole, calcineurin inhibitors (ciclosporin or tacrolimus), mycophenolate mofetil, cyclophosphamide or rituximab","Manage oedema with dietary sodium restriction and diuretics, reserving intravenous albumin for severe symptomatic oedema","Treat infections promptly, remaining alert to spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, and ensure pneumococcal and other vaccination","Consider thromboprophylaxis in severe nephrotic states given the hypercoagulable risk","Monitor for relapse with home urine dipstick testing, and track growth and corticosteroid side effects","Refer for renal biopsy and specialist input in atypical presentations or steroid-resistant disease, which may reflect focal segmental glomerulosclerosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"mirizzi_syndrome":{"name":"Mirizzi syndrome","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mirizzi's syndrome","extrinsic bile duct compression by cystic duct stone","functional hepatic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Obstructive jaundice caused by extrinsic compression of the common hepatic duct by a gallstone impacted in the cystic duct or gallbladder neck","Presents with jaundice, right upper quadrant pain, and often features of cholangitis, mimicking choledocholithiasis or a biliary malignancy","Classified by the presence and extent of a cholecystobiliary fistula, which develops as chronic pressure erodes between the gallbladder and the bile duct","Requires a low threshold of suspicion, as it is frequently unrecognised before surgery and increases the risk of bile duct injury","MRCP is valuable for demonstrating the impacted stone, the level of compression, and any fistula","There is an association with gallbladder carcinoma, so malignancy should be considered during evaluation","Management depends on type: cholecystectomy for simple compression, and biliary reconstruction or repair when a fistula is present, with ERCP useful for decompression, stone clearance, and stenting"],"pathophysiology":"Mirizzi syndrome arises when a gallstone becomes impacted in the cystic duct or the neck (Hartmann's pouch) of the gallbladder, usually where the cystic duct runs a long parallel course alongside the common hepatic duct. The stone and the surrounding inflammatory reaction compress the adjacent common hepatic duct extrinsically, obstructing bile flow and causing jaundice. Persistent pressure and chronic inflammation can erode the wall separating the gallbladder from the bile duct, producing a cholecystobiliary fistula of varying size; in advanced disease the stone may migrate into the common duct. The dense inflammation and distorted anatomy in Calot's triangle obscure normal landmarks and predispose to iatrogenic bile duct injury during cholecystectomy.","additionalDifferentials":["Choledocholithiasis","Cholangiocarcinoma","Gallbladder carcinoma","Pancreatic head adenocarcinoma","Benign biliary stricture","IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI with MRCP to localise the impacted stone, level of obstruction, and any fistula","Abdominal ultrasound to detect gallstones, a contracted gallbladder, and duct dilatation","ERCP for diagnosis, biliary decompression, stone clearance, and stenting","Liver function tests to characterise the cholestatic picture","Serum CA 19-9 and cross-sectional imaging when malignancy is a concern","Endoscopic ultrasound to further assess the stone and exclude a mass"],"redFlags":["Ascending cholangitis with fever, rigors, and sepsis","Features suggesting coexisting gallbladder carcinoma or cholangiocarcinoma","A large cholecystobiliary fistula complicating operative management","Rising bilirubin with worsening liver function","Intraoperative loss of normal anatomical landmarks in Calot's triangle"],"prognosis":"With timely recognition and appropriate surgery the outlook is generally good, but the syndrome carries a higher risk of bile duct injury and operative complications than routine gallstone disease, and the associated gallbladder carcinoma, if present, worsens prognosis considerably.","pitfalls":["Failing to anticipate the diagnosis preoperatively, increasing the risk of common duct injury during cholecystectomy","Attempting a straightforward laparoscopic cholecystectomy in dense inflammation without recognising distorted anatomy","Overlooking a cholecystobiliary fistula that alters the required operation","Missing coexisting gallbladder carcinoma","Inadequate biliary decompression in the presence of cholangitis"],"management":["Definitive treatment is cholecystectomy with clearance of the impacted stone; a fistula-free duct allows standard cholecystectomy, whereas a cholecystobiliary fistula requires subtotal cholecystectomy with fistula closure or biliary reconstruction such as choledochoplasty or hepaticojejunostomy","Preoperative ERCP for biliary decompression, stone extraction and temporary stent placement to relieve obstructive jaundice and cholangitis before surgery","Broad-spectrum antibiotics covering enteric Gram-negative organisms and anaerobes when cholangitis is present, with correction of coagulopathy from cholestasis","Intraoperative cholangiography and careful dissection of Calot's triangle to define the distorted anatomy and minimise the heightened risk of iatrogenic bile duct injury","Actively exclude coexisting gallbladder carcinoma during evaluation and submit the resected gallbladder for histology","Refer to a hepatobiliary surgical unit for complex fistulating disease, with a low threshold for conversion from laparoscopic to open surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Csendes classification of Mirizzi syndrome","Reviews of the diagnosis and surgical management of Mirizzi syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"misophonia":{"name":"Misophonia","system":"Nervous system / Psychiatry","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Selective sound sensitivity syndrome","Sound-rage"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan and Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A strong, disproportionate aversive emotional and physiological reaction, typically anger, disgust, or anxiety, to specific trigger sounds that are often human-generated.","Common triggers include chewing, lip-smacking, slurping, breathing, sniffing, pen-clicking, and keyboard tapping.","The reaction includes autonomic arousal, an urge to escape, irritability or rage, and sometimes aggression, frequently followed by guilt.","Onset is often in childhood or adolescence, and early triggers frequently involve family members.","It is not currently a formal DSM-5-TR or ICD-11 disorder but is increasingly recognised, and should be distinguished from hyperacusis (loudness intolerance) and phonophobia (fear of sound).","Causes significant functional and relational impairment, with avoidance of shared meals and social situations.","High comorbidity is seen with anxiety, obsessive-compulsive traits, and sometimes attention-deficit or autistic traits.","Management is largely psychological, particularly cognitive-behavioural therapy with coping and sound-based strategies, and no established pharmacotherapy exists."],"pathophysiology":"Misophonia is proposed to reflect abnormal salience and connectivity between the auditory system and limbic, autonomic, and interoceptive networks, such that specific learned trigger sounds evoke exaggerated activation of the anterior insula and associated emotional-processing regions, with heightened autonomic responses. Enhanced functional connectivity between auditory cortex and salience and limbic structures, together with conditioned associations formed early in life, is thought to underlie the automatic aversive response. It is distinct from peripheral auditory pathology, since audiometric function is typically normal, and it is best regarded as a disorder of emotional and attentional processing of sound rather than of hearing itself.","additionalDifferentials":["Hyperacusis with reduced tolerance to sound loudness","Phonophobia with fear or anxiety about specific sounds","Tinnitus-related distress","Obsessive-compulsive disorder","Anxiety disorders","Autism spectrum sensory sensitivities","Post-traumatic reactions to specific sounds"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed clinical history of triggers, reactions, and impairment","Validated questionnaires such as the Misophonia Questionnaire or the Amsterdam Misophonia Scale","Audiological assessment including audiometry and loudness discomfort levels to exclude hyperacusis","Screening for anxiety, obsessive-compulsive disorder, and autistic traits","Assessment of functional and relational impact","ENT or audiology referral where auditory pathology is suspected","Mood and risk assessment where aggression or guilt is prominent"],"redFlags":["Aggression or violence toward others during trigger exposure","Severe avoidance causing social or occupational collapse or nutritional impact","Comorbid depression with suicidal ideation","Self-harm related to the distress"],"prognosis":"Misophonia tends to be chronic, but cognitive-behavioural approaches and coping and sound strategies can meaningfully reduce distress and functional impact, although complete resolution is uncommon.","pitfalls":["Confusing misophonia with hyperacusis or phonophobia and mismanaging accordingly","Dismissing it as mere irritability or fussiness","Overlooking comorbid anxiety, obsessive-compulsive disorder, or autistic traits","Recommending indefinite avoidance, which can reinforce the response"],"management":["Cognitive-behavioural therapy tailored to misophonia is the mainstay, restructuring the conditioned aversive response to trigger sounds and reducing avoidance","Sound-based coping strategies such as background noise, masking or music through earphones, alongside tinnitus-retraining-style counselling to lower the salience of triggers","Psychoeducation for the patient and family, with environmental adjustments at home, school or work to limit exposure to distressing sounds","Identify and treat comorbid anxiety, obsessive-compulsive disorder or autistic traits, which may need their own targeted psychological or pharmacological therapy","Audiological assessment to distinguish and exclude hyperacusis and phonophobia, avoiding habitual ear protection that can worsen sound tolerance","Multidisciplinary care across psychiatry, psychology and audiology, with referral when functional or relational impairment is severe"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan and Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","New Oxford Textbook of Psychiatry","Consensus definition of misophonia (Delphi consensus study)"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitochondrial_complex_i_deficiency":{"name":"Mitochondrial Complex I Deficiency (NADH:Ubiquinone Oxidoreductase Deficiency)","system":"Metabolic / Mitochondrial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NADH dehydrogenase deficiency","NDUFS/NDUFV deficiency","MT-ND deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Complex I deficiency is the single most common defect of the mitochondrial respiratory chain and the commonest biochemical cause of Leigh syndrome.","Complex I is the largest respiratory chain enzyme, built from dozens of subunits; seven core subunits are encoded by mitochondrial DNA (the MT-ND genes) and the remainder plus assembly factors are nuclear-encoded, so inheritance may be autosomal recessive, maternal or (rarely) X-linked.","Nuclear structural or assembly-factor defects (e.g. NDUFS1, NDUFS4, NDUFS7, NDUFS8, NDUFV1, NDUFA1, NDUFAF1-8, ACAD9, FOXRED1, NUBPL) typically present in the neonatal period or infancy with encephalopathy, hypotonia and lactic acidosis.","The distinctive mtDNA-related phenotypes arise almost exclusively at Complex I: MT-ND point variants underlie the majority of Leber hereditary optic neuropathy, and ND-gene variants are a recognised cause of MELAS and of maternally inherited Leigh syndrome.","ACAD9 deficiency is a treatable Complex I assembly disorder in which riboflavin supplementation may improve the exercise-intolerant cardiomyopathy/myopathy phenotype, so it should be actively sought.","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, leukoencephalopathy with characteristic cavitating white-matter change, and Leigh-type brainstem/basal ganglia signal abnormality are recurring imaging and organ patterns.","Raised lactate in blood and cerebrospinal fluid with an elevated lactate-to-pyruvate ratio suggests a respiratory chain lesion, but normal lactate does not exclude Complex I deficiency.","Isolated Complex I deficiency on muscle or fibroblast respiratory chain enzymology, with normal activity of the other complexes, narrows the genetic search to Complex I structural, assembly and MT-ND genes."],"pathophysiology":"Complex I (NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase) is the entry point of the electron transport chain, oxidising NADH generated by the Krebs cycle and beta-oxidation, transferring electrons via flavin mononucleotide and a chain of iron-sulphur clusters to ubiquinone, and pumping protons to build the transmembrane electrochemical gradient that powers ATP synthesis. It is the largest of the oxidative phosphorylation enzymes, an L-shaped assembly of a membrane arm and a peripheral matrix arm comprising many subunits; seven hydrophobic core subunits (ND1-ND6 and ND4L) are encoded by mitochondrial DNA (the MT-ND genes) while the remaining structural subunits and a large set of dedicated assembly factors are nuclear-encoded. Pathogenic variants in nuclear genes such as NDUFS1, NDUFS4, NDUFS7, NDUFS8, NDUFV1, NDUFA1 (X-linked), the assembly factors NDUFAF1-NDUFAF8, ACAD9, FOXRED1 and NUBPL, or maternally transmitted MT-ND variants, destabilise the holoenzyme or impair its assembly, causing loss of NADH oxidation, a bottleneck in electron flow, secondary lactic acidosis from redirected pyruvate, and energy failure that most severely affects high-demand tissues such as brain, heart and skeletal muscle.","additionalDifferentials":["Mitochondrial Complex III deficiency (ubiquinol-cytochrome c oxidoreductase defect, including BCS1L/GRACILE)","Mitochondrial Complex IV deficiency (cytochrome c oxidase / COX defect, e.g. SURF1 Leigh syndrome)","Mitochondrial Complex V deficiency (ATP synthase defect, e.g. TMEM70, MT-ATP6 NARP/MILS)","Combined oxidative phosphorylation deficiencies from mtDNA depletion or maintenance defects and mitochondrial translation defects","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex deficiency and other primary lactic acidoses","Biotinidase deficiency and other treatable causes of Leigh-like brainstem encephalopathy","Leber hereditary optic neuropathy from other causes and dominant optic atrophy for the isolated optic phenotype"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate and pyruvate with lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","Plasma amino acids (alanine) and urine organic acids","Spectrophotometric respiratory chain enzyme activities in skeletal muscle or cultured fibroblasts showing isolated Complex I reduction","Neuroimaging (magnetic resonance imaging and spectroscopy) for symmetrical basal ganglia/brainstem lesions and a lactate peak","Molecular genetic testing including a mitochondrial nuclear gene panel or exome plus whole mitochondrial genome sequencing","Echocardiography and electrocardiography to detect hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and conduction disease","Blue-native gel electrophoresis and immunodetection of Complex I assembly in tissue or fibroblasts"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive brainstem dysfunction with respiratory irregularity, apnoea or swallowing failure in an encephalopathic infant","Decompensated lactic acidosis with cardiorespiratory instability during intercurrent illness or fasting","New or worsening hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with heart failure or arrhythmia","Acute stroke-like episodes with seizures suggesting a MELAS-type presentation","Sudden bilateral painless visual loss suggesting Leber hereditary optic neuropathy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is heterogeneous and gene- and phenotype-dependent: severe neonatal and Leigh-type presentations often progress to early death, whereas some later-onset myopathic, optic or riboflavin-responsive ACAD9 phenotypes are more indolent. Overall the disorder is chronic and frequently life-limiting, with course punctuated by metabolic decompensation during catabolic stress.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal blood lactate excludes the diagnosis, when cerebrospinal fluid lactate or an elevated lactate-to-pyruvate ratio may be the only clue.","Overlooking the treatable riboflavin-responsive ACAD9-related Complex I deficiency and other cofactor-responsive forms.","Attributing an isolated optic or stroke-like presentation to a non-mitochondrial cause and missing an MT-ND variant.","Failing to appreciate that maternal, autosomal recessive and X-linked inheritance all occur, which misdirects counselling if only one mode is considered.","Using sodium valproate or other mitochondrial toxins, or unbuffered fasting/anaesthesia, which can precipitate decompensation."],"management":["No curative therapy; supportive treatment uses a mitochondrial cofactor regimen of riboflavin, coenzyme Q10 and antioxidants, and a riboflavin trial should be actively pursued in ACAD9-related disease as this subtype can be riboflavin-responsive","Consider idebenone for the Leber hereditary optic neuropathy phenotype caused by MT-ND variants","Treat acute metabolic decompensation and lactic acidosis promptly with intravenous dextrose and fluids, and avoid prolonged fasting and catabolic stress","Avoid mitochondrial toxins such as sodium valproate and aminoglycosides that can precipitate deterioration","Symptomatic control of seizures, dystonia and spasticity, with anticonvulsant choice mindful of hepatotoxicity","Surveillance for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and conduction disease with echocardiography and ECG, alongside monitoring of neurological, feeding and respiratory status","Multidisciplinary care and genetic counselling reflecting the relevant autosomal recessive, maternal or X-linked inheritance, with prenatal and reproductive options"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of inherited metabolic disease (mitochondrial disorders chapter)","Neurology reference on mitochondrial and respiratory chain disease","International consensus guidance on diagnosis and management of mitochondrial disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitochondrial_complex_iii_deficiency":{"name":"Mitochondrial Complex III Deficiency (Ubiquinol-Cytochrome c Oxidoreductase Deficiency)","system":"Metabolic / Mitochondrial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cytochrome bc1 complex deficiency","BCS1L deficiency","MT-CYB deficiency","GRACILE syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Complex III (the cytochrome bc1 complex) transfers electrons from reduced ubiquinone to cytochrome c through the proton-translocating Q cycle; isolated deficiency is the rarest of the isolated respiratory chain defects.","Cytochrome b, encoded by the mitochondrial MT-CYB gene, is the only mtDNA-encoded subunit; sporadic somatic or maternally transmitted MT-CYB variants classically cause exercise intolerance and myopathy, sometimes with lactic acidosis or myoglobinuria.","BCS1L is the commonest nuclear cause: it is an assembly factor that inserts the Rieske iron-sulphur protein, and its variants produce a spectrum from the severe GRACILE syndrome to Björnstad syndrome.","GRACILE syndrome (Growth Retardation, Aminoaciduria, Cholestasis, Iron overload, Lactic acidosis and Early death) is a distinctive BCS1L phenotype with neonatal iron overload and hepatic involvement, prevalent in Finnish families.","Björnstad syndrome couples sensorineural hearing loss with pili torti (twisted, brittle hair), and is also caused by BCS1L variants at the milder end of the spectrum.","TTC19 deficiency is a recognised nuclear Complex III cause producing a later-onset, slowly progressive neurological picture with a Leigh-like or spinocerebellar phenotype and psychiatric features.","Other nuclear subunit and assembly genes include UQCRB, UQCRQ, CYC1, UQCRC2 and UQCC2/UQCC3, giving hypoglycaemia, ketoacidosis or encephalomyopathy.","Respiratory chain enzymology showing isolated reduction of Complex III activity (rotenone-sensitive or antimycin-sensitive assays), with preserved Complexes I, IV and V, focuses the genetic workup."],"pathophysiology":"Complex III (ubiquinol-cytochrome c oxidoreductase, the cytochrome bc1 complex) sits at the centre of the electron transport chain, accepting electrons from ubiquinol and passing them to cytochrome c through the bifurcated Q cycle, which couples electron transfer to proton pumping across the inner mitochondrial membrane. The functional dimeric enzyme contains three redox-active catalytic subunits: cytochrome b (encoded by mitochondrial MT-CYB), cytochrome c1 (CYC1) and the Rieske iron-sulphur protein (UQCRFS1), together with supernumerary nuclear subunits and dedicated assembly factors. Disease arises from maternally inherited or somatic MT-CYB variants, or from autosomal recessive variants in nuclear structural subunits (CYC1, UQCRB, UQCRQ, UQCRC2) and assembly factors, most importantly BCS1L, which chaperones incorporation of the Rieske protein, and TTC19, which is required for clearance of assembly intermediates. Impaired bc1 function blocks electron flow between the ubiquinone pool and cytochrome c, collapses proton-motive force, diverts pyruvate to lactate, and, in the BCS1L GRACILE phenotype, additionally deranges iron handling to cause hepatic iron overload; energy failure again targets liver, brain, muscle, kidney tubule and cochlea.","additionalDifferentials":["Mitochondrial Complex I deficiency (NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase defect)","Mitochondrial Complex IV deficiency (cytochrome c oxidase / COX defect)","Mitochondrial Complex V deficiency (ATP synthase defect)","Coenzyme Q10 (ubiquinone) biosynthesis deficiency, which impairs electron delivery to Complex III and is treatable","Neonatal haemochromatosis and other causes of neonatal iron overload and cholestasis (for the GRACILE presentation)","Other syndromic sensorineural hearing loss with hair-shaft abnormality (for the Björnstad presentation)","Combined oxidative phosphorylation and mitochondrial translation defects"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate and pyruvate with lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","Urine organic acids and generalised aminoaciduria screen (renal Fanconi-type involvement)","Iron studies, ferritin and liver function tests, with liver imaging where GRACILE is suspected","Spectrophotometric respiratory chain enzymology demonstrating isolated Complex III deficiency","Molecular testing of MT-CYB and nuclear genes (BCS1L, TTC19, CYC1, UQCRB, UQCRQ, UQCRC2, UQCC2/UQCC3)","Coenzyme Q10 measurement in muscle or fibroblasts to exclude a treatable ubiquinone biosynthesis defect","Audiometry and hair-shaft microscopy for pili torti when Björnstad syndrome is considered"],"redFlags":["Neonatal lactic acidosis with cholestasis and iron overload suggesting GRACILE syndrome","Fulminant hepatic dysfunction or intractable metabolic acidosis in a neonate","Recurrent rhabdomyolysis or myoglobinuria with exercise in MT-CYB-related myopathy","Rapid neurological regression or brainstem dysfunction in a Leigh-like TTC19 presentation","Progressive hearing loss with metabolic decompensation during intercurrent illness"],"prognosis":"Outcome ranges from lethal in early infancy (GRACILE and severe neonatal encephalohepatopathic forms) to slowly progressive in later-onset TTC19 neurological disease and relatively benign in some isolated MT-CYB myopathies. A treatable coenzyme Q10 biosynthesis defect must be excluded because it materially changes prognosis.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing GRACILE-related neonatal iron overload as primary neonatal haemochromatosis and missing the BCS1L cause.","Overlooking a treatable coenzyme Q10 biosynthesis defect, which can mimic Complex III deficiency biochemically.","Assaying Complex III without appropriate specific inhibitors, giving spuriously normal or misleading activity.","Attributing isolated exercise intolerance or recurrent myoglobinuria to a muscle glycogenosis and missing an MT-CYB variant.","Failing to examine the hair shaft and hearing in a child with unexplained respiratory chain disease, missing Björnstad syndrome."],"management":["No cure; management is supportive, but a treatable coenzyme Q10 biosynthesis defect must be excluded because ubiquinone (coenzyme Q10) supplementation can benefit that subgroup","Supportive mitochondrial cofactor supplementation with avoidance of prolonged fasting and catabolic stress to limit metabolic decompensation","Prompt correction of lactic acidosis and hypoglycaemia during metabolic crises with intravenous dextrose and fluids","In GRACILE and hepatopathic forms, manage cholestasis, iron overload and liver failure with nutritional and hepatology support","Manage the sensorineural hearing loss of Bjornstad syndrome with amplification, and treat the neurological and psychiatric features of TTC19 disease symptomatically","Avoid mitochondrial toxins, and manage exercise intolerance and myopathy in MT-CYB disease with activity pacing","Multidisciplinary follow-up and genetic counselling for autosomal recessive nuclear disease or maternally inherited MT-CYB variants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of inherited metabolic disease (mitochondrial disorders chapter)","Neurology reference on mitochondrial and respiratory chain disease","International consensus guidance on diagnosis and management of mitochondrial disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitochondrial_complex_iv_deficiency":{"name":"Mitochondrial Complex IV Deficiency (Cytochrome c Oxidase Deficiency)","system":"Metabolic / Mitochondrial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["COX deficiency","SURF1 Leigh syndrome","LRPPRC / Leigh syndrome French-Canadian type","SCO2 cardioencephalomyopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase, COX) is the terminal enzyme of the electron transport chain, transferring electrons from cytochrome c to molecular oxygen using haem a/a3 and copper centres while pumping protons.","Isolated COX deficiency is one of the commonest respiratory chain defects in childhood and a frequent biochemical cause of Leigh syndrome; most cases are due to nuclear assembly-factor defects rather than the mtDNA-encoded catalytic subunits MT-CO1, MT-CO2 and MT-CO3.","SURF1 is the prototypical nuclear cause of COX-deficient Leigh syndrome, producing symmetrical brainstem and basal ganglia lesions, hypertrichosis and progressive neurological regression.","SCO2 and COX15 defects cause a fatal infantile hypertrophic cardioencephalomyopathy, and SCO1 and COX10 cause hepatopathic or tubulopathic (renal Fanconi) presentations, reflecting the copper-handling and haem a-synthesis roles of these factors.","LRPPRC variants cause the Leigh syndrome French-Canadian (Saguenay-Lac-Saint-Jean) type, with a distinctive tendency to lethal, acidotic and hyperlactataemic metabolic crises and facial dysmorphism.","Histochemistry classically shows COX-negative fibres, and ragged-red or COX-deficient fibres support a mitochondrial myopathy; enzymology confirms isolated reduction of Complex IV activity.","TACO1 (a translational activator of COX1) gives a later-onset Leigh-like syndrome, illustrating that defects of mtDNA translation specific to COX subunits also present as isolated COX deficiency.","Benign infantile mitochondrial myopathy with a reversible COX deficiency (associated with a homoplasmic MT-TE variant) can recover spontaneously, so a poor prognosis should not be assumed prematurely."],"pathophysiology":"Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase) is the final electron acceptor of the respiratory chain, receiving electrons from reduced cytochrome c and using them to reduce molecular oxygen to water at a binuclear haem a3-copperB centre, while translocating protons to sustain the membrane potential that drives ATP synthase. Its three catalytic core subunits (COX1, COX2 and COX3) are encoded by mitochondrial DNA (MT-CO1, MT-CO2, MT-CO3) and carry the haem and copper redox centres, whereas the numerous supernumerary subunits and, critically, a large network of nuclear-encoded assembly and metallation factors are required to build the mature enzyme. Autosomal recessive variants in these nuclear factors are the predominant cause: SURF1 (a general assembly factor), SCO1 and SCO2 (mitochondrial copper delivery), COX10 and COX15 (haem a biosynthesis), COX6B1, PET100, the translational activator TACO1, and LRPPRC (which stabilises and polyadenylates mitochondrial mRNAs); maternally inherited MT-CO variants also occur. Loss of terminal oxidase activity halts electron flow to oxygen, collapses proton-motive force and oxidative phosphorylation, and generates lactic acidosis, with tissue-specific damage in brain (Leigh syndrome), heart (SCO2/COX15 cardiomyopathy), liver and renal tubule reflecting the differing roles of each assembly factor.","additionalDifferentials":["Mitochondrial Complex I deficiency (NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase defect)","Mitochondrial Complex III deficiency (cytochrome bc1 / BCS1L defect)","Mitochondrial Complex V deficiency (ATP synthase defect)","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex deficiency and biotinidase deficiency (other treatable Leigh-like disorders)","Combined oxidative phosphorylation deficiencies from mtDNA depletion and translation defects","Other causes of infantile hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (Pompe disease, other mitochondrial and metabolic cardiomyopathies)","Fatal and benign infantile mitochondrial myopathy (reversible COX deficiency associated with the MT-TE variant)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate and pyruvate with lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","Urine organic acids and renal tubular function screen for Fanconi syndrome","Muscle histochemistry for COX-negative fibres and ragged-red fibres, with combined COX/SDH staining","Spectrophotometric respiratory chain enzymology showing isolated Complex IV deficiency","Neuroimaging for symmetrical basal ganglia and brainstem lesions typical of Leigh syndrome","Molecular testing of nuclear genes (SURF1, SCO1, SCO2, COX10, COX15, COX6B1, LRPPRC, TACO1, PET100) and MT-CO genes","Echocardiography for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and liver function tests where hepatopathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive brainstem dysfunction with abnormal breathing, apnoea or bulbar failure in Leigh syndrome","Fatal infantile hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with heart failure (SCO2/COX15 phenotype)","Severe lactic acidotic crises in the French-Canadian LRPPRC phenotype, often triggered by illness or fever","Hepatic failure or refractory metabolic acidosis in the neonatal period","Rapid neurological regression with loss of acquired milestones"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally poor for SURF1-related Leigh syndrome, the SCO2/COX15 cardioencephalomyopathies and LRPPRC French-Canadian disease, which are frequently fatal in infancy or early childhood; however, the reversible infantile COX-deficient myopathy can recover, and milder later-onset forms follow a more protracted course.","pitfalls":["Assuming all infantile COX deficiency is fatal and missing the reversible MT-TE-associated benign infantile myopathy.","Failing to recognise the French-Canadian LRPPRC phenotype and its propensity for sudden lethal acidotic crises.","Overlooking treatable mimics such as biotinidase deficiency and pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency before concluding COX deficiency.","Interpreting patchy COX-negative fibres as normal when only a proportion of fibres are affected.","Not screening the heart and renal tubule, missing the cardiomyopathic and Fanconi phenotypes tied to specific assembly-factor genes."],"management":["No curative treatment; care is supportive with a mitochondrial cofactor regimen of coenzyme Q10, riboflavin and antioxidants while a treatable coenzyme Q10 defect is excluded","Aggressive management of acidotic, hyperlactataemic metabolic crises, notably in LRPPRC French-Canadian disease, with intravenous dextrose and fluids and correction of acidosis, and avoidance of fasting","Anticonvulsants and neurological symptomatic care for Leigh-type encephalopathy in SURF1 disease, including spasticity and movement-disorder management","Cardiac surveillance with echocardiography and ECG and heart-failure treatment for the infantile hypertrophic cardioencephalomyopathy of SCO2 and COX15 defects","Monitor and support renal tubular (Fanconi) and hepatic involvement in SCO1 and COX10 disease with electrolyte and bicarbonate replacement","Recognise the reversible infantile COX-deficient myopathy, which may recover with supportive respiratory and nutritional care","Multidisciplinary care with autosomal recessive genetic counselling and prenatal testing offered to the family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of inherited metabolic disease (mitochondrial disorders chapter)","Neurology reference on mitochondrial and respiratory chain disease","International consensus guidance on diagnosis and management of mitochondrial disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitochondrial_complex_v_deficiency":{"name":"Mitochondrial Complex V Deficiency (ATP Synthase Deficiency)","system":"Metabolic / Mitochondrial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ATP synthase deficiency","TMEM70 deficiency","MT-ATP6 deficiency","NARP / maternally inherited Leigh syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Complex V (F1Fo ATP synthase) is the terminal step of oxidative phosphorylation, using the proton gradient generated by Complexes I, III and IV to phosphorylate ADP to ATP; its deficiency uniquely tends to raise 3-methylglutaconic acid in the urine.","TMEM70 deficiency is the commonest nuclear cause and a defect of ATP synthase biogenesis; it produces a recognisable neonatal encephalo-cardiomyopathy with lactic acidosis, hyperammonaemia, 3-methylglutaconic aciduria and often facial dysmorphism, and is prevalent in the Roma population owing to a founder variant.","The mtDNA subunit gene MT-ATP6 is the classic maternally inherited cause: high heteroplasmy produces maternally inherited Leigh syndrome, while a somewhat lower load produces NARP (Neuropathy, Ataxia and Retinitis Pigmentosa).","NARP characteristically combines sensory neuropathy, cerebellar ataxia and pigmentary retinopathy, sometimes with seizures, dementia and proximal weakness, and severity correlates with the mutant heteroplasmy level.","MT-ATP8 variants and nuclear structural or assembly genes such as ATP5F1A, ATP5F1D, ATP5F1E and ATPAF2 are rarer causes of Complex V deficiency.","Neonatal presentations feature severe lactic acidosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, hypotonia and encephalopathy, with the combination of 3-methylglutaconic aciduria and hyperammonaemia being a strong pointer to TMEM70.","In isolated Complex V deficiency the activities of the other respiratory chain complexes are typically preserved, and blue-native studies show reduced assembled ATP synthase.","Testing MT-ATP6 heteroplasmy across tissues and in maternal relatives is important for diagnosis and genetic counselling, as blood levels may underestimate the burden."],"pathophysiology":"Complex V (F1Fo ATP synthase) is the molecular motor that completes oxidative phosphorylation: protons pumped into the intermembrane space by Complexes I, III and IV flow back through the membrane-embedded Fo domain, driving rotation that the catalytic F1 head uses to condense ADP and inorganic phosphate into ATP. The membrane proton channel is formed largely by the mtDNA-encoded subunits ATP6 and ATP8 (genes MT-ATP6 and MT-ATP8), while the F1 catalytic subunits and the many structural and biogenesis factors are nuclear-encoded. Disease results either from maternally inherited MT-ATP6 (and rarely MT-ATP8) variants that impair proton translocation and coupling, or from autosomal recessive nuclear variants in structural subunits (ATP5F1A, ATP5F1D, ATP5F1E) and biogenesis/assembly factors, most importantly TMEM70 and ATPAF2. Failure of ATP synthase both starves the cell of ATP and, by preventing dissipation of the proton gradient, hyperpolarises the inner membrane and promotes reactive oxygen species; the impaired terminal phosphorylation and disturbed intramitochondrial metabolism characteristically generate 3-methylglutaconic aciduria, and energy deficit again strikes brain, heart and muscle, with the heteroplasmy level of mtDNA variants determining whether the phenotype is NARP or the more severe maternally inherited Leigh syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Mitochondrial Complex I deficiency (NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase defect)","Mitochondrial Complex III deficiency (cytochrome bc1 / BCS1L defect)","Mitochondrial Complex IV deficiency (cytochrome c oxidase / COX defect)","Other 3-methylglutaconic aciduria syndromes (Barth syndrome/TAZ, OPA3/Costeff, DNAJC19, SERAC1/MEGDEL)","Combined oxidative phosphorylation deficiencies and mtDNA maintenance defects","Other causes of neonatal lactic acidosis with hyperammonaemia (urea cycle and organic acid disorders)","Retinitis pigmentosa with ataxia from other causes for the NARP phenotype"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate and pyruvate with lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","Urine organic acids specifically seeking 3-methylglutaconic acid, and plasma ammonia","Echocardiography and electrocardiography for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Neuroimaging for Leigh-type basal ganglia and brainstem lesions","MT-ATP6/MT-ATP8 sequencing with heteroplasmy quantification across multiple tissues and in maternal relatives","Nuclear gene testing (TMEM70, ATPAF2, ATP5F1A, ATP5F1D, ATP5F1E) by panel or exome","Respiratory chain enzymology and blue-native electrophoresis showing isolated Complex V deficiency with reduced assembled enzyme"],"redFlags":["Severe neonatal lactic acidosis with hyperammonaemia and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy suggesting TMEM70 deficiency","Rapidly progressive brainstem dysfunction and regression in maternally inherited Leigh syndrome","Cardiorespiratory decompensation during intercurrent illness in an affected infant","Progressive visual loss with ataxia and neuropathy indicating advancing NARP","Encephalopathy with intractable metabolic acidosis in the newborn period"],"prognosis":"Neonatal TMEM70 and severe nuclear or high-heteroplasmy MT-ATP6 (maternally inherited Leigh) presentations carry a high early mortality, though some TMEM70 survivors stabilise with residual cardiac and neurological disability; NARP tends to follow a slower, stepwise course whose severity tracks the mutant heteroplasmy level.","pitfalls":["Missing 3-methylglutaconic aciduria because it is not specifically sought on the organic acid profile, thereby overlooking a key clue to Complex V deficiency.","Relying on blood-only MT-ATP6 heteroplasmy, which can underestimate the mutant load present in clinically affected tissues.","Confusing the NARP retinopathy-ataxia picture with a primary retinal dystrophy or a spinocerebellar ataxia.","Not considering TMEM70 in a dysmorphic neonate with cardiomyopathy, lactic acidosis and hyperammonaemia, particularly in at-risk founder populations.","Overlooking the other 3-methylglutaconic aciduria syndromes (such as Barth syndrome) in the differential of a boy with cardiomyopathy and neutropenia."],"management":["No cure; supportive mitochondrial cofactor supplementation with avoidance of fasting and catabolic stress","Treat neonatal metabolic crises with intravenous dextrose and fluids and correction of lactic acidosis, and manage the hyperammonaemia of TMEM70 disease with ammonia-scavenging agents and protein modulation","Cardiac surveillance with echocardiography and ECG and heart-failure therapy for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Anticonvulsants and neurological symptomatic care for Leigh-type encephalopathy and NARP-associated seizures","Support NARP manifestations with visual rehabilitation for the retinitis pigmentosa and physiotherapy for ataxia and neuropathy","Multidisciplinary follow-up spanning dietetics, cardiology, neurology and ophthalmology","Genetic counselling distinguishing autosomal recessive nuclear disease such as the TMEM70 founder variant from maternally inherited MT-ATP6 disease with heteroplasmy-dependent recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of inherited metabolic disease (mitochondrial disorders chapter)","Neurology reference on mitochondrial and respiratory chain disease","International consensus guidance on diagnosis and management of mitochondrial disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitochondrial_disease":{"name":"Mitochondrial disease","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mitochondrial cytopathy","mitochondrial encephalomyopathy","oxidative phosphorylation disorder","respiratory chain disorder","MELAS/MERRF/Leigh syndrome (subtypes)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A heterogeneous group of disorders of oxidative phosphorylation caused by mutations in either mitochondrial DNA or nuclear genes affecting the respiratory chain","Should be suspected when unexplained multisystem disease affects high-energy organs such as brain, muscle, heart, eye, endocrine glands, liver and kidney in unusual combinations","Mitochondrial DNA disorders are maternally inherited and show heteroplasmy, so severity and tissue involvement vary with the proportion of mutant genomes (threshold effect)","Recognisable syndromes include MELAS (stroke-like episodes and lactic acidosis), MERRF (myoclonic epilepsy with ragged-red fibres), Leigh syndrome (subacute necrotising encephalopathy), Kearns-Sayre and chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia, and Leber hereditary optic neuropathy","Elevated lactate in blood or CSF, and a raised lactate-to-pyruvate ratio, support the diagnosis but are neither fully sensitive nor specific","Muscle biopsy may show ragged-red fibres and cytochrome-c-oxidase-deficient fibres, and definitive diagnosis increasingly relies on genetic testing of mitochondrial and nuclear genomes","There is no curative therapy; management is largely supportive with cofactor and antioxidant cocktails, avoidance of mitochondrial toxins, and treatment of organ-specific complications","Certain metabolic markers may appear on newborn screening, but most mitochondrial disorders are not reliably detected by standard screening"],"pathophysiology":"Mitochondrial diseases arise from impaired oxidative phosphorylation, the process by which the electron transport chain generates ATP. Because mitochondria contain their own DNA encoding some respiratory chain subunits, while most subunits and assembly factors are nuclear-encoded, disease can follow maternal, autosomal or X-linked inheritance. Mutations reduce ATP production and may increase reactive oxygen species and lactate. Tissues with high energy demand are preferentially affected, and in mitochondrial DNA disorders the mixture of normal and mutant genomes within cells (heteroplasmy) and a threshold of mutant load determine which tissues manifest disease and how severely, explaining the marked clinical variability.","additionalDifferentials":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency and other primary lactic acidoses","Organic acidaemias and fatty acid oxidation defects","Inflammatory or infectious encephalopathy and stroke (for stroke-like episodes)","Muscular dystrophies and inflammatory myopathies","Neurodegenerative and demyelinating disorders","Acquired causes of raised lactate (hypoperfusion, sepsis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and CSF lactate and pyruvate with lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","Plasma amino acids (alanine) and urine organic acids","Neuroimaging (MRI and MR spectroscopy showing a lactate peak; symmetrical basal ganglia or brainstem lesions in Leigh syndrome)","Muscle biopsy with histochemistry (ragged-red and COX-deficient fibres) and respiratory chain enzyme assays","Molecular testing of mitochondrial DNA and nuclear gene panels","Organ-specific assessment (echocardiography, audiometry, endocrine and ophthalmological review)"],"redFlags":["Stroke-like episodes with encephalopathy, seizures and lactic acidosis (as in MELAS)","Acute metabolic decompensation with severe lactic acidosis","Rapidly progressive encephalopathy, respiratory failure or brainstem dysfunction in Leigh syndrome","Cardiac conduction block (e.g. in Kearns-Sayre) with risk of sudden death, and status epilepticus"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is highly variable, ranging from severe fatal infantile encephalopathy to slowly progressive or relatively stable adult disease, depending on the genetic defect, mutant load and organs involved; most forms are progressive with no cure, and management aims to preserve function and prevent decompensation.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis on the basis of a normal blood lactate, which can be intermittent","Using medications toxic to mitochondria (e.g. valproate in some contexts, certain antivirals) without caution","Failing to screen for treatable organ complications such as cardiac conduction disease and endocrinopathy","Overlooking maternal inheritance and the implications of heteroplasmy for family counselling"],"management":["No curative therapy; supportive care uses a mitochondrial cofactor regimen of coenzyme Q10, riboflavin and other antioxidants with optimisation of nutrition and energy intake","Avoid mitochondrial toxins, especially sodium valproate (particularly in POLG disease) and aminoglycosides, and avoid prolonged fasting","Treat organ-specific manifestations: anticonvulsants for seizures avoiding valproate, heart-failure and pacing therapy for cardiomyopathy and conduction disease, and hormone replacement for endocrinopathies such as diabetes","Manage MELAS stroke-like episodes including consideration of L-arginine, and treat lactic acidosis and metabolic decompensation with intravenous dextrose and fluids","Structured surveillance across affected systems with echocardiography, ECG, audiometry, ophthalmology and endocrine review","Multidisciplinary specialist care coordinated through a mitochondrial disease centre, with physiotherapy, dietetics and hearing and visual rehabilitation","Genetic counselling tailored to maternal, autosomal or X-linked inheritance, with reproductive options including preimplantation genetic testing and, for mitochondrial DNA disease, mitochondrial donation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitochondrial_dna_depletion_hepatocerebral":{"name":"Hepatocerebral mitochondrial DNA depletion syndrome","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hepatocerebral MDDS","DGUOK deficiency","MPV17-related hepatocerebral MDDS","MDS","mitochondrial DNA depletion syndrome hepatocerebral form"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / Suchy Liver Disease in Children","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of autosomal recessive disorders presenting with infantile liver failure and neurological involvement due to quantitative depletion of mitochondrial DNA","Commonly caused by mutations in DGUOK and MPV17, and also POLG, which impair maintenance of the mitochondrial genome","Typically presents in the first weeks to months of life with cholestasis, hypoglycaemia, coagulopathy, and lactic acidosis progressing to liver failure","Neurological features include hypotonia, developmental delay, nystagmus, and, in some forms, a hepatocerebral phenotype with encephalopathy","Diagnosis relies on demonstrating reduced mitochondrial DNA content in liver tissue and reduced respiratory chain enzyme activity","A raised blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate supports a mitochondrial cause","Management is supportive; liver transplantation is controversial because of extrahepatic (neurological) disease and must be considered case by case"],"pathophysiology":"Hepatocerebral mitochondrial DNA depletion syndromes result from defects in nuclear genes that maintain the mitochondrial genome or supply deoxyribonucleotides for its replication. Mutations in DGUOK (deoxyguanosine kinase) disturb the mitochondrial nucleotide pool, MPV17 encodes an inner-membrane protein needed for genome maintenance, and POLG encodes the mitochondrial DNA polymerase. The resulting depletion of mitochondrial DNA cripples oxidative phosphorylation in high-demand tissues, producing early hepatocellular failure with cholestasis, hypoglycaemia and lactic acidosis, together with progressive neurological dysfunction.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal haemochromatosis (gestational alloimmune liver disease)","GRACILE syndrome (BCS1L)","Navajo neurohepatopathy (MPV17)","Galactosaemia and hereditary fructose intolerance","Tyrosinaemia type 1","Other respiratory chain disorders and Alpers syndrome (POLG)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood lactate, lactate/pyruvate ratio, and glucose","Liver function tests, serum bile acids, and coagulation profile","Liver biopsy with quantitative mitochondrial DNA content","Respiratory chain enzyme analysis in liver or muscle","Brain MRI and cerebrospinal fluid lactate where encephalopathy is present","Gene panel testing including DGUOK, MPV17, and POLG"],"redFlags":["Acute infantile liver failure with coagulopathy and hypoglycaemia","Refractory lactic acidosis","Progressive encephalopathy or intractable seizures (especially POLG)","Multisystem deterioration in early infancy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally poor, with many infants dying of liver failure or neurological disease in the first years of life; liver transplantation may help isolated hepatic forms but does not address neurological involvement and carries a worse outcome when extrahepatic disease is present.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to liver transplantation without assessing for significant neurological involvement","Missing lactic acidosis and hypoglycaemia as pointers to a mitochondrial cause","Confusing the picture with neonatal haemochromatosis or metabolic liver disease","Using sodium valproate in suspected POLG disease, which can precipitate liver failure"],"management":["No specific cure; supportive management of hepatic failure with correction of coagulopathy, treatment of hypoglycaemia by regular feeding and intravenous dextrose, and relief of cholestasis","Nutritional support to prevent catabolism, with fat-soluble vitamin supplementation in cholestasis","Treat lactic acidosis and metabolic decompensation with intravenous dextrose and fluids","Avoid hepatotoxic and mitochondrial-toxic drugs, particularly sodium valproate, which can precipitate fatal liver failure especially in POLG-related disease","Liver transplantation considered for isolated hepatic disease but weighed carefully, as it does not correct neurological involvement and outcomes are worse with extrahepatic disease","Neurological monitoring with symptomatic care such as anticonvulsants and developmental support for the hepatocerebral phenotype","Autosomal recessive genetic counselling with carrier testing and prenatal options for the family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Suchy, Sokol & Balistreri, Liver Disease in Children"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitochondrial_dna_depletion_syndrome":{"name":"Mitochondrial DNA depletion syndromes","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MDS","MDDS","mtDNA depletion syndrome","TK2","DGUOK","MPV17","SUCLA2","RRM2B"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews mitochondrial DNA depletion / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is a group of autosomal recessive nuclear disorders in which the amount, rather than the sequence, of mitochondrial DNA is reduced in affected tissues","They are broadly grouped by phenotype into myopathic (for example TK2), encephalomyopathic (for example SUCLA2, RRM2B), hepatocerebral (for example DGUOK, MPV17, POLG) and neurogastrointestinal forms","Causative genes maintain the mitochondrial nucleotide pool or replicate mitochondrial DNA, so their failure starves post-mitotic tissues of intact mitochondrial genomes","Because inheritance is Mendelian and nuclear, recurrence risk follows autosomal recessive expectations rather than the maternal transmission seen with primary mitochondrial DNA mutations","Hepatocerebral forms present in infancy with liver failure and neurological features, while myopathic forms present with progressive weakness and hypotonia","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating reduced mitochondrial DNA copy number in affected tissue alongside targeted or panel nuclear gene testing","Sodium valproate is avoided where POLG or a hepatocerebral depletion disorder is suspected because of hepatotoxicity risk, and care is otherwise supportive"],"pathophysiology":"Mitochondrial DNA depletion syndromes are caused by recessive mutations in nuclear genes that either supply the deoxyribonucleotide building blocks for mitochondrial DNA (such as TK2, DGUOK, SUCLA2, SUCLG1 and RRM2B), maintain the mitochondrial environment (such as MPV17), or replicate the genome directly (POLG, TWNK). Impairment of any of these functions reduces the number of intact mitochondrial genomes per cell, and because mitochondrial DNA encodes essential respiratory-chain subunits, oxidative phosphorylation fails in a tissue-specific pattern that reflects each gene's expression and each tissue's energy demand. Post-mitotic, energy-hungry tissues such as muscle, brain and liver are most vulnerable, giving the myopathic, encephalomyopathic and hepatocerebral phenotypes.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary mitochondrial DNA point mutation or deletion syndromes","Spinal muscular atrophy and congenital myopathies (for myopathic forms)","Neonatal haemochromatosis and other infantile liver failure syndromes","Glycogen storage and fatty acid oxidation disorders","Leigh syndrome","Congenital disorders of glycosylation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantification of mitochondrial DNA copy number in muscle or liver","Nuclear gene panel or exome sequencing covering TK2, DGUOK, MPV17, SUCLA2, RRM2B, POLG and related genes","Respiratory-chain enzyme assays in affected tissue showing combined complex deficiencies","Liver function and coagulation studies in hepatocerebral presentations","Blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate","Plasma amino acids and urine organic acids (for example methylmalonic aciduria in SUCLA2/SUCLG1 defects)"],"redFlags":["Infantile hepatic failure with coagulopathy","Rapidly progressive weakness with respiratory muscle involvement","Encephalopathy or refractory seizures","Metabolic decompensation with severe lactic acidosis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies by subtype and gene: severe infantile hepatocerebral and encephalomyopathic forms often carry a poor outlook with early mortality, whereas some myopathic forms are more slowly progressive, and outcome depends heavily on the specific genetic defect and organ involvement.","pitfalls":["Confusing these nuclear, Mendelian disorders with maternally inherited mitochondrial DNA diseases when counselling families","Failing to sample the clinically affected tissue, so depletion is missed in blood","Administering valproate in POLG or hepatocerebral forms and precipitating liver failure","Not recognising specific biochemical clues such as mild methylmalonic aciduria pointing to SUCLA2 or SUCLG1 defects"],"management":["No universal cure, but TK2 deficiency has disease-specific nucleoside substrate therapy using deoxycytidine and deoxythymidine (pyrimidine nucleoside) supplementation that can improve the myopathic phenotype","Supportive mitochondrial cofactor supplementation, nutritional support and avoidance of fasting and catabolic stress","Avoid mitochondrial toxins, especially sodium valproate in POLG-related disease","Phenotype-directed care: ventilatory and physiotherapy support for myopathic weakness, management of liver failure in hepatocerebral forms, and anticonvulsants for encephalomyopathic seizures","Treat metabolic decompensation and lactic acidosis with intravenous dextrose and fluids","Surveillance of respiratory function, cardiac status, growth and neurodevelopment across the subtypes","Multidisciplinary care with autosomal recessive genetic counselling and prenatal and carrier testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Mitochondrial DNA Depletion Syndromes","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitochondrial_membrane_protein_associated_neurodegeneration":{"name":"Mitochondrial membrane protein-associated neurodegeneration","system":"Neurology / Neurodegeneration","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPAN","NBIA4","C19orf12-related neurodegeneration"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurology and neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Usually autosomal recessive (occasionally autosomal dominant) NBIA caused by C19orf12 variants, with onset ranging from childhood to early adulthood.","A distinctive mix of upper and lower motor neuron signs, spastic paraparesis combined with a motor axonal neuropathy and amyotrophy, can closely mimic a motor neuron disorder.","Optic atrophy with progressive visual loss is a hallmark and is a key feature separating MPAN from PKAN.","Prominent neuropsychiatric features (behavioural change, emotional lability, impulsive or obsessive behaviour, and later psychosis) accompany a progressive dementia.","Parkinsonism and dystonia, often oromandibular, develop as the disease advances.","MRI shows T2 hypointensity (iron) in the globus pallidus and substantia nigra; a T2-hyperintense streak of the medial medullary lamina between the internal and external pallidum is characteristic, and the eye-of-the-tiger sign is usually absent.","Neuropathology combines iron deposition with Lewy bodies, hyperphosphorylated tau and axonal spheroids, so MPAN is both a synucleinopathy and a tauopathy.","C19orf12 encodes a small mitochondrial membrane protein implicated in lipid and fatty-acid metabolism, placing MPAN mechanistically near the coenzyme A pathway of PKAN."],"pathophysiology":"C19orf12 encodes a small, highly conserved mitochondrial membrane protein whose precise function remains incompletely defined but which is implicated in lipid and fatty-acid homeostasis and, indirectly, in coenzyme A-related metabolism, linking it mechanistically to the pathway disrupted in pantothenate kinase-associated neurodegeneration. Loss of functional protein produces mitochondrial dysfunction and disturbed lipid handling, and is followed by progressive iron accumulation in the globus pallidus and substantia nigra together with widespread neurodegeneration. The neuropathology is notable for co-existing alpha-synuclein-positive Lewy bodies, hyperphosphorylated-tau inclusions and axonal spheroids, reflecting combined failure of protein aggregation clearance and axonal integrity. This broad pathology explains the clinical convergence of pyramidal, extrapyramidal, lower motor neuron, optic and neuropsychiatric features, and inheritance is predominantly autosomal recessive with rare dominant kindreds described.","additionalDifferentials":["Other NBIA subtypes (PKAN, BPAN, PLA2G6-associated neurodegeneration, FAHN)","Hereditary spastic paraplegia","Juvenile amyotrophic lateral sclerosis / motor neuron disease","Young-onset Parkinson disease and atypical parkinsonian syndromes","Wilson disease","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis","Mitochondrial disease with optic atrophy (dominant optic atrophy spectrum)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI with iron-sensitive sequences (susceptibility-weighted/T2*) to demonstrate pallidal and nigral iron and the medial medullary lamina streak","C19orf12 testing, ideally within an NBIA gene panel","Nerve conduction studies and EMG to characterise the motor axonal neuropathy and denervation","Ophthalmological assessment with optical coherence tomography and visual evoked potentials for optic atrophy","Neuropsychiatric and cognitive evaluation","Serum caeruloplasmin and copper studies to exclude Wilson disease"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive weakness with bulbar or respiratory compromise","New psychosis or severe behavioural disturbance","Progressive visual deterioration","Frequent falls and loss of ambulation from combined spasticity and neuropathy"],"prognosis":"MPAN is slowly progressive over one to several decades, leading to severe combined motor and cognitive disability and reduced life expectancy. Treatment is supportive only.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosis as hereditary spastic paraplegia or motor neuron disease because of the mixed pyramidal and neuropathic picture.","Expecting the eye-of-the-tiger sign, which is characteristic of PKAN rather than MPAN, and discounting the diagnosis when it is absent.","Overlooking optic atrophy, one of the most useful distinguishing clues.","Attributing the neuropsychiatric features to a primary psychiatric illness."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy; management is supportive and symptom-directed","Treat dystonia with anticholinergics, baclofen or botulinum toxin injections, with deep brain stimulation considered for refractory dystonia","Manage spasticity with antispasticity agents and physiotherapy, and address the motor axonal neuropathy, weakness and amyotrophy with rehabilitation, orthoses and mobility aids","Trial of levodopa for parkinsonian features, recognising that response is often limited","Psychiatric management of behavioural change, impulsivity and psychosis, with cognitive support for the progressive dementia","Low-vision support and ophthalmology follow-up for the progressive optic atrophy","Multidisciplinary neurology-led care with speech and language therapy, dietetics and palliative planning as disability advances","Autosomal recessive (occasionally dominant) genetic counselling and family testing for C19orf12 variants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation (NBIA) clinical references","Movement disorders neurology references","Neurogenetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitochondrial_membrane_protein_neurodegeneration":{"name":"Mitochondrial membrane protein-associated neurodegeneration","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPAN","neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation type 4","NBIA4","C19orf12-related neurodegeneration","C19orf12"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation (NBIA) caused by variants in C19orf12, most often autosomal recessive but occasionally dominant","Typically presents in childhood or early adulthood with progressive spastic paraparesis, dystonia, parkinsonism, and motor axonal neuropathy","Optic atrophy and progressive cognitive decline with neuropsychiatric features are characteristic","Brain MRI shows iron deposition (T2 hypointensity) in the globus pallidus and substantia nigra, sometimes with a hyperintense streak in the medial medullary lamina","Neuropathology demonstrates iron accumulation, Lewy-body-like alpha-synuclein pathology, and axonal spheroids","C19orf12 encodes a small mitochondrial membrane protein implicated in lipid metabolism and mitochondrial function","The combination of pyramidal, extrapyramidal, and lower-motor-neuron signs with optic atrophy is a useful diagnostic clue","No disease-modifying therapy exists; care is supportive, with management of dystonia, spasticity, and neuropsychiatric symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"MPAN belongs to the NBIA group and is caused by variants in C19orf12, which encodes a small protein localised to mitochondrial membranes and implicated in lipid homeostasis and mitochondrial function. Loss of normal protein function disturbs mitochondrial and lipid metabolism in vulnerable neurons, leading to progressive neurodegeneration accompanied by pathological iron deposition in the basal ganglia, particularly the globus pallidus and substantia nigra. The neuropathology characteristically shows axonal spheroids and alpha-synuclein-positive Lewy-body-like inclusions in addition to iron, linking the disorder to both iron-accumulation and synucleinopathy mechanisms. This produces the mixed clinical picture of upper motor neuron, extrapyramidal, and peripheral nerve involvement with optic atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Other NBIA disorders (PKAN, PLAN, BPAN, and related conditions)","Wilson disease","Hereditary spastic paraplegias","Juvenile parkinsonism and dystonia syndromes","Mitochondrial disorders with basal-ganglia involvement","Hereditary motor and sensory neuropathies with central features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI including iron-sensitive sequences of the basal ganglia","C19orf12 molecular genetic testing (often within an NBIA gene panel)","Nerve conduction studies for motor axonal neuropathy","Ophthalmological assessment for optic atrophy","Neuropsychological and psychiatric evaluation","Serum caeruloplasmin and copper studies to exclude Wilson disease"],"redFlags":["Rapid loss of ambulation from progressive spasticity and dystonia","Severe dystonic crisis requiring urgent management","Marked neuropsychiatric deterioration with safety concerns","Progressive visual loss from optic atrophy"],"prognosis":"A progressive neurodegenerative disorder leading to increasing motor and cognitive disability over years to decades; no treatment halts progression and care is supportive.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise the diagnostic MRI iron pattern and pursuing broader neurology unnecessarily","Attributing the peripheral neuropathy to a separate cause rather than the same disorder","Overlooking the neuropsychiatric component of the phenotype","Not excluding treatable mimics such as Wilson disease before settling on the diagnosis"],"management":["No disease-modifying treatment for this C19orf12-related NBIA; care is supportive and symptom-directed","Treat dystonia with anticholinergics, baclofen or botulinum toxin injections, reserving deep brain stimulation for refractory disabling dystonia","Manage spasticity and parkinsonism with antispasticity agents, physiotherapy and a levodopa trial, and support the motor axonal neuropathy with orthoses and mobility aids","Psychiatric and cognitive management of the neuropsychiatric features and progressive dementia","Low-vision rehabilitation and ophthalmology follow-up for the progressive optic atrophy","Multidisciplinary neurology-led care with speech and language therapy, dietetic and swallowing support, and palliative planning as disability progresses","Autosomal recessive (occasionally dominant) genetic counselling and cascade family testing for C19orf12 variants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitochondrial_nonsyndromic_hearing_loss":{"name":"Mitochondrial non-syndromic hearing loss (MT-RNR1 aminoglycoside sensitivity)","system":"Ear / Audiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["m.1555A>G","MT-RNR1-related deafness","aminoglycoside-induced deafness","maternally inherited hearing loss","MT-TS1 m.7445A>G"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Maternally inherited variants in the mitochondrial genome, classically m.1555A>G in MT-RNR1, predispose to sensorineural hearing loss","Affected individuals are exquisitely susceptible to aminoglycoside ototoxicity, which can trigger severe irreversible loss even after a single standard course","Baseline hearing may be normal, mild, or progressive, and susceptibility can be silent until an aminoglycoside is given","The pattern of maternal transmission with no father-to-child inheritance is a key pedigree clue","Genotype knowledge allows aminoglycoside avoidance across an entire maternal lineage, a highly preventable cause of deafness","Rapid point-of-care or pre-prescription testing is increasingly used to avoid ototoxic exposure in acute settings","MT-TS1 variants such as m.7445A>G are additional maternally inherited causes"],"pathophysiology":"The mitochondrial 12S ribosomal RNA gene MT-RNR1 forms part of the mitochondrial ribosome. The m.1555A>G variant alters the RNA so that it more closely resembles the bacterial ribosomal binding site targeted by aminoglycosides, increasing drug binding and impairing mitochondrial protein synthesis. Because cochlear hair cells are highly dependent on oxidative metabolism, this susceptibility renders them vulnerable to aminoglycoside-induced injury, producing sensorineural hearing loss. Even without drug exposure some carriers develop progressive loss, reflecting a milder underlying deficit in mitochondrial function; the mitochondrial genome is inherited exclusively through the maternal line, explaining the pattern of transmission.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-mitochondrial hereditary sensorineural hearing loss (DFNA and DFNB)","Acquired aminoglycoside ototoxicity without a predisposing variant","Presbyacusis and noise-induced loss","Syndromic mitochondrial disease with additional systemic features","Autoimmune inner-ear disease","Enlarged vestibular aqueduct with progression"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure-tone audiometry to establish baseline thresholds and monitor progression","Otoacoustic emissions and brainstem response to characterise cochlear function","Mitochondrial genetic testing for m.1555A>G and related MT-RNR1 and MT-TS1 variants","Detailed maternal-lineage pedigree to identify at-risk relatives","Review of prior and planned aminoglycoside exposure","Assessment for wider mitochondrial disease when systemic features are present"],"redFlags":["Planned or ongoing aminoglycoside therapy in a person with the variant or a suggestive maternal history","Sudden severe hearing loss following aminoglycoside exposure","Progressive bilateral loss threatening communication","Systemic features suggesting a broader mitochondrial disorder"],"prognosis":"Aminoglycoside-triggered loss is typically severe and irreversible, but the disorder is highly preventable through drug avoidance; baseline and progressive loss are managed with amplification or cochlear implantation.","pitfalls":["Prescribing an aminoglycoside without eliciting a maternal deafness history or checking genotype where feasible","Assuming ototoxicity requires prolonged high exposure when a single course can suffice in carriers","Failing to warn and test maternal relatives after diagnosis","Overlooking the diagnosis in progressive loss with a maternal inheritance pattern"],"management":["The central intervention is lifelong avoidance of aminoglycoside antibiotics using alternative antimicrobial classes, since a single standard course can trigger severe irreversible deafness","Flag the diagnosis prominently in medical records and on alert cards or bracelets, and extend aminoglycoside-avoidance advice across the entire maternal lineage","Amplification with hearing aids for established loss, and cochlear implantation for severe-to-profound sensorineural hearing loss","Baseline and serial pure-tone audiometry to establish thresholds and monitor progression","Point-of-care or pre-prescription genotyping to guide safe antibiotic choice in acute settings","Maternal-lineage cascade genetic testing and counselling to identify at-risk relatives, with speech and language support for affected children"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology","GeneReviews: Mitochondrial Nonsyndromic Hearing Loss and Deafness"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitochondrial_trifunctional_protein_deficiency":{"name":"Mitochondrial trifunctional protein deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MTP deficiency","TFP deficiency","HADHA deficiency","HADHB deficiency","long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase (LCHAD) deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive defect of the mitochondrial trifunctional protein (HADHA/HADHB genes) that carries out three steps of long-chain fatty-acid beta-oxidation; isolated LCHAD deficiency is a common subtype","Presents across a spectrum from severe neonatal cardiomyopathy and hypoketotic hypoglycaemia to a milder later-onset phenotype with episodic rhabdomyolysis and a progressive peripheral neuropathy","Fasting or intercurrent illness precipitates hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, liver dysfunction and metabolic decompensation","LCHAD deficiency is distinctively associated with a progressive pigmentary retinopathy and peripheral neuropathy not usually seen in other fatty-acid oxidation defects","Carrying an affected fetus is linked to maternal HELLP syndrome and acute fatty liver of pregnancy, so a maternal obstetric history is an important clue","Blood acylcarnitines show elevated long-chain hydroxyacylcarnitines, detectable on newborn screening","Management centres on avoidance of fasting, a low long-chain-fat diet with medium-chain triglyceride substitution, and prompt glucose during catabolic stress"],"pathophysiology":"The mitochondrial trifunctional protein is a hetero-octamer of alpha (HADHA) and beta (HADHB) subunits that performs the last three enzymatic steps of long-chain fatty-acid beta-oxidation: long-chain enoyl-CoA hydratase, long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase (LCHAD) and long-chain 3-ketoacyl-CoA thiolase activities. Deficiency blocks energy production from long-chain fats, so during fasting or illness tissues cannot generate adequate ATP or ketones, producing hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and accumulation of toxic long-chain hydroxyacyl intermediates. These intermediates injure the heart, skeletal muscle, liver, peripheral nerves and retina. Reduced fatty-acid oxidation in the fetus and placenta also underlies the associated maternal liver disease of pregnancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Very-long-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (VLCAD) deficiency","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency","Other long-chain fatty-acid oxidation disorders","Primary dilated or hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Inherited causes of recurrent rhabdomyolysis","Mitochondrial respiratory chain disorders","Hereditary sensorimotor neuropathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma acylcarnitine profile showing elevated long-chain 3-hydroxyacylcarnitines","Blood glucose, ketones and free fatty acids during acute episodes to demonstrate hypoketotic hypoglycaemia","Creatine kinase and myoglobinuria assessment during suspected rhabdomyolysis","Echocardiography and ECG to evaluate cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia","Ophthalmological review and nerve conduction studies for retinopathy and neuropathy","HADHA/HADHB molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Neonatal cardiomyopathy with hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and collapse","Acute rhabdomyolysis with risk of renal impairment during illness or exertion","Arrhythmia or cardiac decompensation during metabolic crises","Maternal HELLP syndrome or acute fatty liver of pregnancy prompting evaluation of the infant"],"prognosis":"The severe neonatal cardiomyopathic form carries high mortality, whereas milder phenotypes may be managed with diet and fasting avoidance; however, progressive retinopathy and peripheral neuropathy can cause long-term morbidity even with good metabolic control.","pitfalls":["Missing hypoketotic hypoglycaemia by not measuring ketones alongside glucose","Failing to consider the diagnosis in a mother with HELLP or acute fatty liver of pregnancy","Overlooking the evolving retinopathy and neuropathy that distinguish LCHAD/MTP deficiency","Allowing prolonged fasting or high long-chain-fat intake that precipitates decompensation"],"management":["Dietary therapy is the mainstay: a low long-chain-fat diet supplemented with medium-chain triglycerides that bypass the enzyme block, together with a high complex-carbohydrate intake","Strict avoidance of fasting with frequent feeds and an emergency regimen of extra carbohydrate during intercurrent illness, and intravenous dextrose when oral intake fails","Docosahexaenoic acid supplementation to slow the LCHAD-associated pigmentary retinopathy, with carnitine used cautiously only if deficient","Prompt treatment of acute rhabdomyolysis with intravenous fluids and hydration to protect renal function","Cardiac surveillance with echocardiography and ECG and heart-failure or arrhythmia treatment for the cardiomyopathic form","Regular ophthalmological review for retinopathy and nerve conduction studies for peripheral neuropathy","Multidisciplinary metabolic team follow-up with a specialist dietitian, autosomal recessive genetic counselling with newborn screening and family testing, and awareness of the maternal risk of HELLP and acute fatty liver of pregnancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: Disorders of Mitochondrial Fatty Acid Oxidation","Saudubray, Baumgartner & Walter, Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitral_annular_calcification":{"name":"Mitral annular calcification","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAC","mitral annulus calcification","calcification of the mitral annulus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic degenerative deposition of calcium in the fibrous ring supporting the mitral valve, most often affecting the posterior annulus","Behaves as a marker of the same risk profile as atherosclerosis, being commoner with advancing age, hypertension, diabetes, and chronic kidney disease with disordered calcium-phosphate handling","Usually an incidental echocardiographic or chest radiograph finding, but extensive calcification can cause functional mitral regurgitation or, less often, mitral stenosis","Associated with an excess of atrial fibrillation, conduction disturbance when calcium encroaches on the atrioventricular node, and systemic embolism","Occasionally the calcified mass liquefies centrally (caseous MAC), which can be mistaken for a tumour or abscess on imaging","Extensive annular calcium complicates surgical and transcatheter mitral intervention and raises procedural risk","Management is directed at the underlying vascular risk factors and any consequent valve dysfunction or arrhythmia rather than the calcium itself"],"pathophysiology":"Mitral annular calcification is a degenerative process in which calcium and phosphate are deposited within the fibrous annulus of the mitral valve, sharing mechanisms with atherosclerosis and with the disordered mineral metabolism of chronic kidney disease. The rigid, calcified ring loses its normal systolic contraction and can prevent adequate leaflet coaptation, producing regurgitation, while heavy calcium extending onto the leaflet bases narrows the inflow and mimics rheumatic mitral stenosis. Encroachment of calcium on the conduction tissue near the atrioventricular node underlies the associated bradyarrhythmias, and the calcified surface may act as a nidus for thrombus or, rarely, infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Rheumatic mitral valve disease","Degenerative aortic valve calcification with annular extension","Infective endocarditis or annular abscess","Cardiac tumour or thrombus (caseous MAC mimic)","Papillary muscle or chordal calcification","Left atrial calcification"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography to localise the calcification and quantify any stenosis or regurgitation","Transoesophageal echocardiography for clearer annular and leaflet detail and to exclude endocarditis","Cardiac CT to characterise the extent and distribution of calcium and clarify a caseous mass","Cardiac MRI to distinguish caseous calcification from tumour or thrombus","Assessment of renal function and calcium-phosphate balance and of cardiovascular risk factors"],"redFlags":["Haemodynamically significant mitral stenosis or regurgitation causing heart failure","High-grade atrioventricular block from calcium involving the conduction system","Systemic embolism or stroke arising from the calcified valve","A mobile mass or new regurgitation with fever suggesting superimposed endocarditis"],"prognosis":"The calcification itself is usually benign and slowly progressive, but its presence signals a heightened cardiovascular and mortality risk, and extensive disease can produce valve dysfunction, conduction block, and difficult mitral intervention.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing caseous mitral annular calcification as a tumour or abscess and pursuing unnecessary surgery","Overlooking conduction disease when calcium extends towards the atrioventricular node","Attributing all mitral stenosis to rheumatic disease and missing heavy annular calcium","Underestimating the added risk that annular calcium brings to mitral valve surgery or transcatheter repair"],"management":["No specific therapy for the calcification itself; manage the shared cardiovascular risk profile by treating hypertension, diabetes and dyslipidaemia and optimising calcium-phosphate handling and renal disease","Asymptomatic incidental disease needs only echocardiographic surveillance to track progression of any valve dysfunction","Treat significant secondary mitral regurgitation or stenosis with heart-failure medical therapy and, where needed, surgical or transcatheter mitral intervention, recognising that heavy annular calcium raises procedural risk","Anticoagulation for associated atrial fibrillation to reduce embolic stroke, with rate or rhythm control","Monitor for conduction disturbance and provide permanent pacing if high-grade atrioventricular block develops","Use cardiac CT or MRI to characterise caseous calcification and avoid misdiagnosis as tumour or abscess","Refer to a valve and structural heart team for intervention planning in extensive disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitral_arcade":{"name":"Mitral arcade (hammock valve)","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hammock mitral valve","anomalous mitral arcade","arcade of the mitral valve"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare congenital malformation of the mitral subvalvar apparatus in which papillary muscles connect directly, or through short thickened chordae, to a bridge of fibrous tissue along the leaflet edge","The resulting 'hammock' arrangement restricts leaflet motion and disrupts normal coaptation, causing mitral stenosis, regurgitation, or a mixed lesion","Part of the spectrum of congenital mitral inflow anomalies and often associated with other left heart obstructive lesions","Typically presents in infancy or childhood with breathlessness, poor feeding or failure to thrive, and features of pulmonary venous congestion, though milder forms may present later","Careful echocardiographic assessment of the whole mitral apparatus, including papillary muscles and chordae, is essential to define the mechanism","Distinguishing arcade from other congenital mitral lesions such as parachute valve and supravalvar ring guides surgical planning","Management is surgical when the lesion is significant, favouring valve repair where feasible, with replacement reserved for valves not amenable to repair"],"pathophysiology":"In a mitral arcade the normal delicate chordal support of the mitral leaflets is replaced by direct or near-direct insertion of the papillary muscles into a thickened fibrous ridge running along the free edge of the leaflets. This tethering fixes the leaflets in a curtain-like or hammock configuration that limits their excursion and prevents proper coaptation, so blood flow across the valve is obstructed and, or, the valve leaks. The consequent rise in left atrial pressure is transmitted to the pulmonary circulation, producing pulmonary venous congestion and, if long-standing, pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain.","additionalDifferentials":["Parachute mitral valve","Supravalvar mitral ring","Congenital mitral stenosis from other causes","Cor triatriatum sinistrum","Rheumatic mitral valve disease","Shone complex with multilevel left heart obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography with detailed assessment of leaflets, chordae, and papillary muscles","Transoesophageal echocardiography for finer definition of the subvalvar apparatus","Three-dimensional echocardiography to display the arcade morphology for surgical planning","Cardiac MRI or CT to clarify anatomy and associated left heart lesions when needed","Assessment of pulmonary artery pressure by echocardiography or right heart catheterisation"],"redFlags":["Heart failure or failure to thrive in an infant with a congenital mitral lesion","Severe mitral stenosis or regurgitation with pulmonary venous congestion","Established pulmonary hypertension from chronic obstruction","Multilevel left heart obstruction indicating a Shone-type complex"],"prognosis":"The outlook depends on the severity of valve dysfunction and associated lesions; timely repair can give good results, but the abnormal apparatus makes durable repair challenging and some patients ultimately require valve replacement.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing mitral stenosis without recognising the abnormal subvalvar arcade responsible","Confusing an arcade with a parachute valve or supravalvar ring, which alters surgical strategy","Failing to look for associated left heart obstructive lesions","Underestimating the difficulty of achieving a durable repair of the malformed apparatus"],"management":["Definitive treatment is surgical, with mitral valve repair of the abnormal subvalvar apparatus where feasible or valve replacement when durable repair is not possible","Medical therapy of pulmonary venous congestion and heart failure with diuretics and afterload reduction to stabilise symptoms before surgery","Nutritional support for failure to thrive in infants with feeding difficulty","Detailed echocardiographic assessment of the whole mitral apparatus to define the mechanism and distinguish arcade from parachute valve and supravalvar ring, guiding surgical planning","Monitor for and manage pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain","Screen for associated left-heart obstructive lesions and attend to oral health and endocarditis prevention","Paediatric cardiology and congenital cardiac surgical referral with long-term follow-up for recurrent or residual valve dysfunction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC Guidelines for the management of adult congenital heart disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitral_regurgitation":{"name":"Mitral Regurgitation","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MR"],"chapter":275,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2052","clinicalPearls":["Distinguish primary (degenerative) MR, where the leaflets and/or chordae are themselves abnormal, from secondary (functional) MR, where leaflets and chordae are usually normal and regurgitation is driven by LV/LA remodeling, annular dilation, papillary muscle displacement, dyssynchrony, or leaflet tethering; assessment, treatment, and prognosis differ significantly between the two (p.2052).","The characteristic murmur of chronic severe MR is a holosystolic, plateau-configuration systolic murmur of at least grade III/VI, most prominent at the apex and radiating to the axilla (p.2053-2054).","Murmur radiation reflects jet direction: with posterior leaflet prolapse/flail the eccentric anteriorly-directed jet strikes the LA wall near the aortic root and the murmur radiates to the base (can mimic aortic stenosis), whereas anterior leaflet prolapse/flail directs the jet and murmur toward the axilla (p.2054).","Ruptured chordae tendineae may give the systolic murmur a cooing or 'seagull' quality; a flail leaflet may produce a musical murmur (p.2054).","The murmur of chronic MR not due to MVP is intensified by isometric handgrip and reduced during the strain phase of Valsalva (decreased LV preload) (p.2054).","In acute severe MR the murmur is early in timing and decrescendo, ending well before S2, reflecting the rapidly rising LA pressure and diminishing LV-LA gradient — in contrast to the holosystolic plateau murmur of chronic MR (p.2053-2054).","Because EF rises in severe MR when LV function is normal, even a modest fall in EF (below 60%) reflects significant contractile dysfunction (p.2052-2053).","Atrial fibrillation is almost invariably present once the LA dilates significantly in chronic severe MR; LA enlargement and AF support the diagnosis on ECG (p.2053).","'MR begets MR': LA enlargement tensions the posterior leaflet pulling it away from the orifice, and LV dilation increases regurgitation, enlarging LA and LV further in a vicious cycle (p.2052).","With post-MI papillary muscle rupture the posteromedial papillary muscle is involved far more often than the anterolateral muscle because of its single blood supply (p.2052)."],"pathophysiology":"MR can arise from disease of any of the five components of the mitral apparatus: leaflets, annulus, chordae tendineae, papillary muscles, and subjacent myocardium (p.2052). MR reduces resistance to LV emptying (lowers afterload); the LV decompresses into the LA during ejection, so initial compensation is more complete LV emptying. Over time LV volume rises as regurgitation worsens and contractile function deteriorates, and forward cardiac output falls; LV compliance is often increased so LV diastolic pressure does not rise until late (p.2052-2053). The regurgitant volume varies directly with LV systolic pressure and the size of the regurgitant orifice (influenced by LV and annular dilation and leaflet morphology). LA compliance governs the clinical picture: in acute severe MR the regurgitant volume enters a normal-sized, noncompliant LA so LA and pulmonary venous pressures rise sharply with a prominent v wave and pulmonary edema is common; in chronic severe MR the LA enlarges and becomes more compliant, buffering pressure so pulmonary congestion is initially less prominent and fatigue from low forward output predominates (p.2053). Chronic secondary MR results from annular dilation, ventricular remodeling, papillary muscle displacement, and leaflet tethering (e.g., after MI, in dilated cardiomyopathy once LV end-diastolic dimension reaches ~6 cm, or atrial functional MR from chronic AF with annular dilation) (p.2052).","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic stenosis (basally-radiating MR murmur from posterior leaflet prolapse/flail can be confused with AS) (p.2054)","Mitral stenosis (a brief early-diastolic LA-LV gradient with rapid filling sound and mid-diastolic rumble in pure severe MR can masquerade as MS) (p.2053-2054)","Ventricular septal defect and other causes of a holosystolic murmur"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) with Doppler to define mechanism and severity (jet width/area, regurgitant volume, regurgitant fraction, effective regurgitant orifice area, LV volumes/EF, LA/LV size, chordal integrity, annular calcification) (p.2053)","Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) for greater anatomic detail (p.2053-2054)","Estimation of pulmonary artery systolic pressure from the tricuspid regurgitation jet velocity (p.2053)","Exercise (stress) echocardiography to assess exercise capacity and dynamic change in MR severity, PA pressures, and biventricular function when clinical and resting findings disagree (p.2053)","Cardiac magnetic resonance (CMR) for quantitative regurgitant measures when TTE is discordant or insufficient (noted as not widely available) (p.2052-2053)","ECG (LA enlargement, possible RA enlargement with pulmonary hypertension, AF, eccentric LVH) (p.2053)","Chest X-ray (LA/LV enlargement, pulmonary venous congestion, Kerley B lines, mitral/annular calcification; asymmetric pulmonary edema possible in acute MR) (p.2053-2054)","Left and right heart catheterization with contrast ventriculography and coronary angiography when noninvasive findings are discrepant or to identify coronary disease before surgery (p.2053, p.2056)"],"redFlags":["Acute severe MR is a surgical emergency: papillary muscle rupture post-MI or leaflet destruction/perforation in infective endocarditis can cause sudden pulmonary edema and hemodynamic collapse, requiring urgent stabilization and emergent surgery (p.2052, p.2055-2056)","Acute pulmonary edema, hypotension with narrow pulse pressure, and prominent signs of pulmonary congestion with a non-displaced apex point to acute severe MR (p.2053)","Onset of palpitations or atrial fibrillation in chronic severe MR (p.2053)","Late-onset right heart failure with hepatic congestion, ankle edema, distended neck veins, ascites, and secondary tricuspid regurgitation, indicating pulmonary vascular disease and pulmonary hypertension (p.2053)","Falling LVEF (to 60% or below) or rising LV end-systolic dimension (40 mm or more), even when asymptomatic, signals contractile dysfunction warranting surgical referral (p.2053, p.2056)"],"prognosis":"Chronic mild-to-moderate isolated MR is well tolerated and usually asymptomatic. Chronic severe MR tends to be progressive ('MR begets MR') and, when secondary, severity correlates inversely with prognosis, with adverse outcome tied to the degree of LV remodeling and myocardial dysfunction (p.2052-2053). With surgery, repair carries substantially lower risk than replacement; a 2023 STS database analysis reported mean perioperative mortality of about 1.16% (under 0.5% in patients younger than 65) for repair of primary MR, durable repair is feasible in up to 95% of degenerative cases at high-volume centers, and reoperation runs about 1% per year over the first 10 years. In ischemic MR with severely impaired LV function (EF below 30%), surgical risk is higher, recovery of LV performance is incomplete, and long-term survival is reduced (p.2055-2056).","pitfalls":["In secondary MR the severity can be underappreciated by echocardiographic/Doppler techniques, and lesser degrees of regurgitation may carry relatively greater prognostic weight (p.2053).","An S3 in MR may reflect rapid LV filling, but in ischemic or dilated cardiomyopathy an S3 may instead signify ventricular dysfunction (p.2054).","Because EF is artifactually elevated in severe MR with normal myocardium, a 'low-normal' EF may already represent meaningful contractile dysfunction (p.2052-2053).","Routine surgical valve repair for significant secondary MR due to dilated cardiomyopathy has NOT been shown to improve long-term survival versus optimal guideline-directed medical therapy, especially in the era of quadruple HFrEF therapy (p.2056).","Direct oral anticoagulants should NOT be used if moderate or severe rheumatic mitral stenosis coexists, and are NOT approved for mechanical prosthetic valves (p.2055).","A presystolic murmur is not ordinarily heard with isolated MR (p.2054)."],"management":["For primary degenerative severe MR, mitral valve repair (preferred over replacement) is the definitive treatment, indicated once symptoms develop or with left-ventricular enlargement or dysfunction, new atrial fibrillation or pulmonary hypertension","For secondary functional MR, first optimise guideline-directed heart-failure therapy with beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors or angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitors, mineralocorticoid-receptor antagonists and SGLT2 inhibitors, adding cardiac resynchronisation where indicated","Transcatheter edge-to-edge mitral repair for selected patients with persistent secondary MR despite optimal medical therapy or who are at high surgical risk","Acute severe MR is a surgical emergency: stabilise with afterload reduction using vasodilators or intravenous nitrates and mechanical circulatory support such as an intra-aortic balloon pump as a bridge to urgent surgery","Manage atrial fibrillation with rate or rhythm control and anticoagulation for thromboembolic prevention","Serial echocardiographic surveillance of regurgitant severity, left-ventricular size and function and pulmonary pressures to time intervention","Refer to a multidisciplinary heart-valve team for mechanism assessment and intervention planning"],"severityClassification":"Chronic severe MR (Doppler/CMR quantitative criteria) is defined by a regurgitant volume of at least 60 mL/beat, regurgitant fraction of at least 50%, and effective regurgitant orifice area of at least 0.40 cm2 (p.2053). ACC/AHA disease staging (Stages B, C1, C2, D) is applied: Stage C is asymptomatic severe MR (C1 normal LV systolic function with LVEF above 60% and ESD below 40 mm; C2 LV systolic dysfunction with LVEF at or below 60% or ESD at or above 40 mm) and Stage D is symptomatic severe MR (p.2054-2055).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2052"],"crossLinks":["IE","heart_failure","atrial_fib"]},"mitral_stenosis":{"name":"Mitral Stenosis","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MS"],"chapter":274,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2048","clinicalPearls":["Rheumatic fever is the leading cause of mitral stenosis; pure or predominant MS occurs in roughly 40% of patients with rheumatic heart disease and a history of rheumatic fever (p.2048).","Although declining in temperate, middle- to high-income climates, MS remains a major problem in low-income countries, especially sub-Saharan Africa, India, Southeast Asia, and Oceania (p.2048).","Auscultation classically reveals an accentuated first heart sound (S1), an opening snap (OS) of the mitral valve heard best in expiration at or just medial to the apex, followed by a low-pitched rumbling diastolic murmur heard best at the apex in the left lateral recumbent position (p.2049).","The A2-to-opening-snap interval varies inversely with severity of MS: a shorter interval indicates more severe stenosis (p.2049).","In patients in sinus rhythm, the diastolic murmur often reappears or becomes louder during atrial systole (presystolic accentuation) (p.2049).","Development of persistent atrial fibrillation often marks a turning point in the patient's course and is associated with acceleration of symptom progression (p.2048).","Echocardiography (TTE with Doppler) provides mitral orifice area, transvalvular gradients, leaflet calcification/restriction, and suitability for percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy; TEE is especially indicated to exclude LA thrombus before intervention (p.2049).","Mitral valve area 1-1.5 cm2 denotes severe MS; an LA pressure of about 25 mmHg is needed to maintain normal cardiac output when the orifice falls below 1.5 cm2 (p.2048-2049)."],"pathophysiology":"In normal adults the mitral valve orifice area is 4-6 cm2. Significant obstruction occurs when the orifice is reduced to less than about 2 cm2, so blood can only flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle if propelled by an abnormally elevated left atrioventricular pressure gradient, the hemodynamic hallmark of MS. When the orifice is below 1.5 cm2 (severe MS), an LA pressure of about 25 mmHg is required to maintain normal cardiac output. Elevated pulmonary venous and PA wedge pressures reduce pulmonary compliance and cause exertional dyspnea. Because tachycardia shortens diastole proportionately more than systole, an increased heart rate (including rapid AF) augments the transvalvular gradient and further elevates LA pressure. Chronic LA pressure elevation leads to LA enlargement, predisposing to AF and to thrombus formation (particularly in the LA appendage) with risk of systemic embolization. Pulmonary hypertension develops from passive backward transmission of elevated LA pressure, reactive pulmonary arteriolar constriction (the so-called second stenosis), interstitial edema of small pulmonary vessels, and, at end stage, obliterative pulmonary vascular changes; severe pulmonary hypertension produces RV enlargement, secondary tricuspid and pulmonic regurgitation, and right-sided heart failure (p.2048-2049).","additionalDifferentials":["Mitral regurgitation (may have an apical diastolic flow murmur, but it begins slightly later, S1 is soft or absent, and there is no opening snap; LV enlargement is common) (p.2049)","Severe aortic regurgitation with an Austin Flint murmur (apical mid-diastolic murmur not intensified in presystole and softened by amyl nitrite/arterial vasodilators) (p.2049)","Tricuspid stenosis (rare without MS; its diastolic murmur increases with inspiration and the jugular venous y descent is delayed) (p.2049-2050)","Atrial septal defect (RV enlargement and increased pulmonary vascularity, but no LA enlargement or Kerley B lines, with fixed splitting of S2) (p.2050)","Left atrial myxoma (obstructs LA emptying causing a positional diastolic murmur; often with systemic features such as weight loss, fever, anemia, emboli, elevated IgG/IL-6) (p.2050)"],"infectionMimics":["Infective endocarditis with large vegetations is itself a recognized (uncommon) cause of left ventricular inflow obstruction mimicking MS (p.2048)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Congenital mitral valve stenosis (parachute valve, cor triatriatum) (p.2048)","Severe mitral annular calcification with extension onto the leaflets (p.2048)","Systemic lupus erythematosus and rheumatoid arthritis involving the valve (p.2048)","Left atrial myxoma (p.2048)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography with color flow and spectral Doppler for mitral inflow velocities, peak/mean gradients, mitral orifice area, leaflet calcification and restriction, and suitability for percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy (p.2049)","Transesophageal echocardiography when TTE is inadequate, and specifically to exclude left atrial thrombus before commissurotomy (p.2049)","Exercise (stress) echocardiography to assess mean mitral diastolic gradient, PA pressures, and RV function when clinical and resting hemodynamic findings are discrepant (p.2049)","ECG showing left atrial enlargement (P-wave changes), and right axis deviation/RV hypertrophy when severe pulmonary hypertension supervenes (p.2049)","Chest X-ray showing straightening of the left cardiac border, prominent main PAs, upper-lobe pulmonary venous dilation, an enlarged LA, and Kerley B lines when mean LA pressure exceeds about 20 mmHg (p.2049)","Left and right heart catheterization when clinical and noninvasive findings are discrepant; CT coronary angiography to screen for coronary disease before valve surgery (p.2050)"],"redFlags":["Atrial fibrillation, which accelerates symptom progression and markedly increases thromboembolic risk (p.2048)","Systemic embolization (incidence 10-20%), more frequent with AF, age over 65, and reduced cardiac output; may be the presenting feature even in mild MS (p.2049)","Hemoptysis from rupture of pulmonary-bronchial venous connections due to pulmonary venous hypertension (p.2048)","Pulmonary hypertension and right-sided heart failure (hepatomegaly, ankle edema, ascites, pleural effusion) (p.2048-2049)","Recurrent pulmonary emboli, sometimes with infarction, an important late cause of morbidity and mortality (p.2048)","Decompensation precipitated by tachycardia, fever, severe anemia, paroxysmal AF, thyrotoxicosis, exertion, or pregnancy (p.2048)","In pregnancy, increased blood volume, cardiac output, and tachycardia can raise the transmitral gradient and cause pulmonary edema or tachyarrhythmias, with the immediate postpartum period a time of particular concern due to rapid volume shifts (p.3900)"],"prognosis":"In temperate climates the latent period between the initial attack of rheumatic carditis and symptomatic MS is generally about two decades, with disability typically beginning in the fourth decade. Historical studies before surgical valvotomy showed that once a patient became seriously symptomatic, the disease progressed inexorably to death within 2-5 years. Onset of persistent AF generally marks acceleration of symptom progression (p.2048).","pitfalls":["When cardiac output is markedly reduced, the typical auscultatory findings including the diastolic rumble may be undetectable (silent MS) and may reappear only as compensation is restored (p.2049)","Systemic embolization may be the presenting feature in otherwise asymptomatic patients with only mild MS (p.2049)","The Graham Steell murmur of pulmonic regurgitation can be indistinguishable from aortic regurgitation, though it may increase with inspiration and is accompanied by a loud, often palpable P2 (p.2049)","A history of rheumatic carditis is increasingly rare and often cannot be elicited (p.2048)"],"management":["Percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy is the treatment of choice for symptomatic severe rheumatic MS with pliable, non-calcified valve anatomy and no left atrial thrombus","Surgical mitral commissurotomy or valve replacement for patients unsuitable for balloon commissurotomy or with heavily calcified or regurgitant valves","Medical therapy to relieve congestion with diuretics, and heart-rate control with beta-blockers, rate-limiting calcium-channel blockers or digoxin to lengthen diastolic filling","Anticoagulation with a vitamin K antagonist for atrial fibrillation, prior embolism or left atrial thrombus to prevent systemic embolism","Secondary antibiotic prophylaxis with long-acting penicillin to prevent recurrent rheumatic fever in rheumatic heart disease","Transoesophageal echocardiography to exclude left atrial thrombus before commissurotomy, with serial echocardiographic surveillance of valve area and pulmonary pressures","Refer to a heart-valve team for intervention timing, especially at the onset of atrial fibrillation or pulmonary hypertension"],"severityClassification":"Severity is graded by mitral valve area. The normal orifice is 4-6 cm2; significant obstruction begins below about 2 cm2. A valve area below 1.5 cm2 is termed severe MS (with an LA pressure of about 25 mmHg required to maintain normal cardiac output), and an area below 1 cm2 is very severe, at which cardiac output is subnormal at rest. Echocardiographic measurement of mitral orifice area and transvalvular gradient is the basis for grading; commissurotomy is generally indicated for symptomatic patients with valve area below about 1 cm2/m2 body surface area or below 1.5 cm2 in normal-sized adults (p.2048-2051).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2048"],"crossLinks":["atrial_fib","acute_rheumatic_fever","ischemic_stroke","aortic_stenosis"]},"mitral_valve_arcade":{"name":"mitral valve arcade","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anomalous mitral arcade","hammock mitral valve"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children, and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Anomalous mitral arcade, or hammock valve, is a rare congenital dysplasia in which the papillary muscles attach directly to the leaflet free edge through a bridging band of fibrous tissue, obliterating the normal chordae.","It causes mixed mitral dysfunction, with regurgitation from restricted coaptation and stenosis from limited leaflet opening.","It often forms part of the congenital mitral dysplasia spectrum and may coexist with parachute valve, supravalvar mitral ring and subaortic stenosis within Shone complex.","Echocardiography shows papillary muscles displaced towards the annulus with a hammock-like subvalvar apparatus and thickened, immobile leaflet edges.","It is distinguished from parachute mitral valve, in which chordae converge on a single papillary muscle, whereas the arcade shows direct muscular or fibrous attachment to the leaflet margin.","Because chordal shortening tethers the leaflet tips while the body may still billow, the appearance should not be mistaken for prolapse.","Surgical repair is difficult owing to the deficient subvalvar apparatus, and valve replacement is sometimes required."],"pathophysiology":"The lesion results from abnormal development of the mitral tension apparatus in which the interchordal spaces fail to form. The papillary muscles insert directly onto the free margin of the anterior leaflet, or a continuous fibrous arcade bridges the papillary muscle tips along the leaflet edge, so that discrete chordae are effectively absent. The resulting tethering restricts leaflet excursion, producing regurgitation through failure of coaptation and stenosis through limited diastolic opening. The abnormality lies within the wider spectrum of congenital mitral valve dysplasia and commonly accompanies other left-heart obstructive lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["Parachute mitral valve","Congenital mitral stenosis with supravalvar mitral ring","Rheumatic mitral valve disease","Mitral valve prolapse","Cleft mitral valve or atrioventricular septal defect","Ischaemic or dysplastic papillary muscle dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography with focused subvalvar apparatus imaging","Transoesophageal and 3D echocardiography","Cardiac MRI","Cardiac catheterisation with transvalvar pressure measurement","Electrocardiography","Screening for associated Shone-complex left-heart obstructive lesions"],"redFlags":["Heart failure or pulmonary oedema from severe mitral dysfunction","Pulmonary hypertension","Failure to thrive in infants","Atrial arrhythmia with haemodynamic compromise"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on severity and associated lesions; mild disease may remain stable for years, but significant mixed mitral disease often needs repair or replacement, and the deficient subvalvar apparatus makes durable repair difficult.","pitfalls":["Confusing arcade with parachute valve or rheumatic disease","Missing associated Shone-complex lesions","Mistaking tethered leaflet tips with a billowing body for prolapse","Underestimating the stenosis component when regurgitation dominates the clinical picture"],"management":["Definitive management is surgical: repair of the dysplastic subvalvar apparatus where anatomy allows, or mitral valve replacement when the deficient chordal support precludes durable repair","Medical control of pulmonary congestion and heart failure with diuretics and afterload reduction ahead of surgery","Precise echocardiographic delineation of papillary muscles, chordae and leaflets to define the mixed stenosis and regurgitation mechanism and distinguish it from parachute valve and prolapse","Screen for and manage associated Shone-complex lesions including parachute valve, supravalvar mitral ring and subaortic or aortic obstruction","Monitor for and treat pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain","Nutritional support for infants with failure to thrive and feeding difficulty","Paediatric cardiology and congenital cardiac surgical follow-up for residual or recurrent valve dysfunction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children, and Adolescents","Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","Feigenbaum's Echocardiography"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitral_valve_prolapse":{"name":"Mitral Valve Prolapse","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MVP","Systolic click-murmur syndrome","Barlow's syndrome","Floppy-valve syndrome","Billowing mitral leaflet syndrome"],"chapter":276,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2057-2059","clinicalPearls":["MVP is a clinically variable syndrome arising from diverse pathologic mechanisms affecting the mitral valve apparatus, commonly excessive or redundant leaflet tissue associated with myxomatous degeneration and increased glycosaminoglycan concentrations (p.2057).","It is the most common abnormality leading to primary mitral regurgitation and to mitral valve repair surgery, and in North America is now the most common cause of isolated severe MR requiring surgery (p.2057-2058).","MVP is more common in women than men and typically presents between ages 15 and 30 with a generally benign course; in older patients (>50 years, often men) MR tends to be more severe because of chordal rupture (p.2058).","The posterior leaflet is usually more affected than the anterior, the annulus is often dilated, and elongated, redundant, or ruptured chordae tendineae frequently cause or contribute to regurgitation (p.2058).","The hallmark auscultatory finding is a mid- or late nonejection systolic click occurring 0.14 s or more after S1, often followed by a high-pitched mid-late systolic crescendo-decrescendo murmur best heard at the apex, sometimes described as whooping or honking (p.2058).","Dynamic auscultation aids diagnosis: standing, the strain phase of Valsalva, and any maneuver decreasing LV volume make the click and murmur occur earlier, whereas squatting and isometric exercise (increasing LV volume) delay the click-murmur complex and may make it disappear (p.2058).","Murmur radiation depends on the leaflet involved: posterior leaflet prolapse directs the jet anteriorly with radiation toward the base, while anterior leaflet involvement directs the jet posteriorly with radiation to the axilla and back (p.2058).","A useful echocardiographic definition is systolic displacement of the belly of the mitral leaflets by at least 2 mm into the left atrium above the plane of the mitral annulus in the parasternal long-axis view (p.2058).","Chest pain in MVP is often substernal, prolonged, and unrelated to exertion, and only rarely resembles angina pectoris (p.2058).","Most patients are asymptomatic and remain so for life; the disease may progress slowly over years to decades or worsen abruptly from chordal rupture or endocarditis (p.2058).","MVP can occur as a rare sequel to acute rheumatic fever, in ischemic heart disease, in various cardiomyopathies, and in about 20% of patients with ostium secundum atrial septal defect (p.2058)."],"pathophysiology":"MVP results from myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve apparatus with excessive or redundant leaflet tissue and markedly increased glycosaminoglycan content. In many cases the cause is unknown, but some forms are genetically determined; reduced type III collagen production has been implicated, and electron microscopy shows fragmentation of collagen fibrils. Genome-wide association work has identified multiple genetic loci (candidate genes include LMCD1, SPTBN1, LTBP2, TGFB2, NMB, and ALPK3), and associations with LMNA, FLNC, and FLNA that are often expressed as cardiomyopathies. The posterior leaflet is typically more affected, the annulus is often dilated, and elongated, redundant, or ruptured chordae contribute to regurgitation. Prolapse may impose excessive stress on the papillary muscles, producing localized ischemia, infarction, and replacement fibrosis that can serve as a nidus for ventricular arrhythmias (visible as late gadolinium enhancement on cardiac MRI even without coronary disease). Chordal rupture along with progressive annular dilation and calcification worsen regurgitation, placing further stress on the diseased apparatus in a self-perpetuating cycle (p.2057-2058).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of primary mitral regurgitation","Rheumatic mitral valve disease (chordae thickened but not fused in MVP, unlike rheumatic disease)","Innocent or functional systolic murmurs","Angina pectoris (when atypical chest pain is present)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["ECG (often normal; may show biphasic or inverted T waves in leads II, III, aVF and supraventricular or ventricular premature beats)","Transthoracic echocardiography to identify abnormal leaflet position and prolapse and define the >=2 mm displacement criterion","Color flow and continuous-wave Doppler to assess associated MR and estimate severity","Three-dimensional echocardiography and cardiac MRI for more precise LV volumes and detection of papillary muscle fibrosis/late gadolinium enhancement","Transesophageal echocardiography when detailed anatomy is needed and for intraoperative or transcatheter procedural guidance","Exercise testing (often with rest and immediate poststress TTE) to assess functional capacity, LV/RV function, dynamic MR, and pulmonary pressures","Left ventriculography during catheterization (rarely necessary)"],"redFlags":["Severe MR with depressed LV systolic function (highest risk for sudden death)","Flail leaflet, which carries an excess risk of sudden death and usually accompanies severe MR","High-grade ventricular ectopy with inferior-apical T-wave abnormalities, high-density premature ventricular complexes at rest, mitral annular disjunction, and papillary muscle fibrosis on cardiac MRI","Syncope or light-headedness from arrhythmias","Transient ischemic attacks from emboli due to endothelial disruption on the valve","Features suggesting infective endocarditis in patients with MR and/or leaflet thickening"],"prognosis":"The clinical course is most often benign, and the majority of patients remain asymptomatic for their entire lives. In many the condition progresses gradually over years to decades, while in others it worsens rapidly from chordal rupture or endocarditis. Sudden death is a very rare complication, occurring most often with severe MR and depressed LV systolic function but possible even with normal LV size and function; a small high-risk subset has high-grade ventricular ectopy with characteristic imaging features, and patients with a flail leaflet carry an excess sudden-death risk (p.2058).","pitfalls":["Relying on a static exam can miss MVP; the click and murmur are dynamic and shift with maneuvers that alter LV volume (Valsalva/standing exaggerate, squatting/isometrics diminish them).","Presentations vary: some patients have a mid-systolic click without a murmur, others a murmur without a click, and still others both at different times.","The eccentric MR jet typical of MVP makes effective regurgitant orifice area and regurgitant volume difficult to assess with standard techniques.","Atypical, prolonged, non-exertional chest pain may be misattributed to coronary disease and is often difficult to evaluate.","Sudden death and significant arrhythmias can occur even in patients with normal LV size and function, so apparently low-risk patients are not entirely without risk."],"management":["Reassurance and periodic observation for the majority with benign asymptomatic prolapse and no significant regurgitation","Beta-blockers for symptomatic palpitations, chest pain and autonomic symptoms","Mitral valve repair for severe primary MR complicating prolapse, particularly after chordal rupture, following the same timing principles as degenerative MR","Surveillance for and management of ventricular arrhythmias in arrhythmic mitral valve prolapse with mitral annular disjunction and papillary muscle fibrosis, using antiarrhythmic drugs or an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator in those at high risk of sudden death","Serial echocardiography to monitor leaflet morphology, progression of regurgitation and left-ventricular size and function, at intervals guided by severity","Attention to dental and skin hygiene for endocarditis prevention, with antibiotic prophylaxis reserved for the highest-risk subgroups per current guidance","Genetic evaluation and family screening where a heritable syndromic or familial form is suspected"],"severityClassification":"Severity is anchored on the associated mitral regurgitation, ranging from a benign isolated systolic click with mild posterior-leaflet prolapse to severe MR from chordal rupture and leaflet flail. Echocardiographic prolapse is defined as >=2 mm systolic displacement of the leaflet belly into the left atrium above the annular plane (parasternal long-axis), with Doppler used to grade MR severity (p.2058).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2057-2059"],"crossLinks":[]},"mittelschmerz":{"name":"Mittelschmerz","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mid-cycle pain","ovulation pain","ovulatory pain","midcycle ovulatory pain"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology reference textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as recurrent unilateral lower abdominal or pelvic pain occurring around the middle of the menstrual cycle, roughly coinciding with ovulation","Pain classically alternates sides between cycles depending on which ovary ovulates, and typically lasts from a few hours up to a couple of days","The discomfort is usually mild to moderate and self-limiting, sometimes accompanied by light spotting or a small amount of physiological vaginal discharge","It is fundamentally a clinical diagnosis of exclusion made from a characteristic cyclical history in an otherwise well woman","Correlating symptoms with a menstrual diary and confirming the timing relative to ovulation supports the diagnosis and reassures the patient","Management is generally conservative with reassurance and simple analgesia; suppression of ovulation with a combined oral contraceptive abolishes the pain in troublesome cases","A positive pregnancy test in a woman with mid-cycle-type pain should always prompt exclusion of ectopic pregnancy rather than attribution to mittelschmerz"],"pathophysiology":"The pain is attributed to the physiological events of ovulation. Rapid stretching of the ovarian capsule by the enlarging dominant follicle, followed by rupture of the follicle, releases follicular fluid together with a small quantity of blood into the peritoneal cavity. This fluid irritates the adjacent peritoneum and may provoke localised contraction of the fallopian tube and surrounding smooth muscle, producing transient unilateral pain on the side of the ovulating ovary.","additionalDifferentials":["Ectopic pregnancy","Acute appendicitis","Ovarian torsion","Ruptured ovarian cyst","Pelvic inflammatory disease","Endometriosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinary or serum pregnancy test to exclude early or ectopic pregnancy","Transvaginal ultrasound to assess the ovaries and exclude a cyst, torsion, or free fluid","Full blood count and inflammatory markers if an infective or inflammatory cause is being considered","Urinalysis to help exclude urinary tract infection or renal tract pathology","Menstrual symptom diary correlating pain with the timing of ovulation"],"redFlags":["A positive pregnancy test with pain, raising the possibility of ectopic pregnancy","Signs of peritonitis, haemodynamic instability, or a tender palpable mass","Fever or systemic upset suggesting infection rather than physiological pain","Progressive, severe, or atypical pain that does not follow the expected mid-cycle pattern"],"prognosis":"Mittelschmerz is a benign, self-limiting condition with an excellent outlook; symptoms resolve within a day or two each cycle and are readily controlled by ovulation suppression when they interfere with daily life.","pitfalls":["Attributing pain to mittelschmerz and thereby missing an ectopic pregnancy, torsion, or appendicitis","Failing to confirm the cyclical mid-cycle pattern before making the diagnosis","Over-investigating a clear, characteristic history in an otherwise well woman","Overlooking that ovulation pain cannot occur when ovulation is suppressed, prompting a review of alternative causes"],"management":["Reassurance and explanation of the benign, self-limiting nature of the mid-cycle ovulatory pain","Simple analgesia with paracetamol or a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug for the transient discomfort","Suppression of ovulation with the combined oral contraceptive pill to abolish recurrent troublesome pain","Local heat and a menstrual symptom diary correlating pain with ovulation to confirm the pattern and reassure","Exclude alternative pathology with a pregnancy test and pelvic ultrasound for cyst, torsion or free fluid when features are atypical","Refer to gynaecology if pain is severe, persistent or atypical, or if diagnostic doubt remains"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference textbook","Peer-reviewed gynaecology clinical reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"mixed_connective_tissue":{"name":"Mixed connective tissue disease","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCTD","Sharp syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An overlap syndrome combining features of systemic lupus erythematosus, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis, defined serologically by high-titre anti-U1 RNP antibodies","Raynaud phenomenon and swollen 'puffy' hands or fingers are common early manifestations","Typical features include arthritis or arthralgia, inflammatory myopathy, oesophageal dysmotility, and sclerodermatous skin changes","Pulmonary arterial hypertension is a leading cause of death and must be actively sought even when patients are relatively well","Interstitial lung disease is frequent and warrants baseline and periodic monitoring","By definition antibodies to U1 RNP are present, usually in high titre, while antibodies more specific for lupus or scleroderma may be absent","Renal disease tends to be less severe than in lupus, but membranous nephropathy and other lesions can occur","Management is directed at the dominant organ involvement, borrowing treatment principles from lupus, scleroderma, and myositis"],"pathophysiology":"Mixed connective tissue disease is a systemic autoimmune disorder unified by the presence of high-titre autoantibodies against the U1 small nuclear ribonucleoprotein (U1 RNP). B-cell autoreactivity, T-cell involvement, and immune complex formation contribute to widespread inflammation and vasculopathy. Intimal proliferation and medial hypertrophy of small pulmonary arteries underlie the characteristic proliferative pulmonary vasculopathy and pulmonary arterial hypertension. Overlapping mechanisms account for the mixed features of lupus, systemic sclerosis, and inflammatory myopathy, and some patients evolve toward one of these defined diseases over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Systemic sclerosis","Polymyositis or dermatomyositis","Undifferentiated connective tissue disease","Rheumatoid arthritis with overlap features","Primary Raynaud phenomenon"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-U1 RNP antibodies and a full connective tissue antibody panel (ANA, anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm, scleroderma and myositis antibodies)","Echocardiography with estimation of pulmonary pressures, escalating to right heart catheterisation when pulmonary hypertension is suspected","Pulmonary function tests with diffusing capacity and high-resolution CT chest for interstitial lung disease","Creatine kinase and inflammatory markers to assess myositis and disease activity","Renal function, urinalysis, and nailfold capillaroscopy"],"redFlags":["Exertional dyspnoea, syncope, or right heart strain suggesting pulmonary arterial hypertension","Progressive breathlessness or hypoxia from interstitial lung disease","Severe myositis with respiratory or bulbar weakness","Scleroderma renal crisis with accelerated hypertension and renal impairment","Digital ischaemia or ulceration from severe Raynaud phenomenon"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally better than for lupus or diffuse systemic sclerosis, but pulmonary arterial hypertension and progressive interstitial lung disease are the principal determinants of mortality. Some patients maintain a mild course while others evolve into a defined connective tissue disease.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for pulmonary arterial hypertension until symptoms are advanced","Assuming benign course and neglecting periodic cardiopulmonary surveillance","Overlooking evolution into lupus or systemic sclerosis over time","Attributing puffy hands and Raynaud phenomenon to a benign cause","Treating with a single-disease framework rather than targeting dominant organ involvement"],"management":["Use corticosteroids as the backbone for inflammatory arthritis, myositis and serositis, titrated to organ involvement","Introduce steroid-sparing immunosuppressants such as methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate for persistent myositis, arthritis or interstitial lung disease","Manage Raynaud phenomenon with cold avoidance and vasodilators (dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers), escalating to phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors or prostanoids for digital ischaemia","Screen actively and repeatedly for pulmonary arterial hypertension with echocardiography, treating confirmed disease with endothelin receptor antagonists, phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors or prostacyclin analogues via a pulmonary hypertension service","Monitor for interstitial lung disease with pulmonary function tests and high-resolution CT, treating progressive disease with immunosuppression such as mycophenolate","Control oesophageal dysmotility and reflux with proton pump inhibitors and prokinetic measures","Follow up in rheumatology with multidisciplinary input, escalating urgently for pulmonary arterial hypertension, rapidly progressive lung disease or scleroderma renal crisis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"mixed_connective_tissue_disease":{"name":"Mixed connective tissue disease (anti-U1-RNP)","system":"Rheumatology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCTD","Sharp syndrome","anti-U1-RNP antibody","anti-ribonucleoprotein"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology / international connective tissue disease guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Overlaps features of systemic lupus erythematosus, systemic sclerosis, and inflammatory myopathy in the same patient","High-titre anti-U1-ribonucleoprotein antibody is the defining serological hallmark","Raynaud phenomenon and puffy, swollen (sausage) hands are characteristic early features","Inflammatory myositis, arthritis, and oesophageal dysmotility are common overlapping manifestations","Pulmonary arterial hypertension is a major cause of mortality and warrants active screening","A speckled antinuclear antibody pattern with negative anti-double-stranded DNA and anti-Smith is typical","Over time some patients evolve toward a more clearly defined single connective tissue disease"],"pathophysiology":"Mixed connective tissue disease is an autoimmune overlap disorder unified by high-titre antibodies to the U1 small nuclear ribonucleoprotein. Immune responses against this ribonucleoprotein complex, together with B-cell activation and immune complex formation, produce inflammation and vascular injury that combine features of lupus, scleroderma, and myositis. Proliferative vasculopathy of the pulmonary arteries underlies the characteristic pulmonary arterial hypertension, while the same immune dysregulation drives synovitis, myositis, and oesophageal dysmotility.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Systemic sclerosis","Idiopathic inflammatory myopathy","Undifferentiated connective tissue disease","Rheumatological overlap syndrome","Sjogren syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antinuclear antibody with high-titre anti-U1-ribonucleoprotein testing","Anti-double-stranded DNA and anti-Smith to help distinguish from lupus","Creatine kinase and inflammatory markers for myositis","Transthoracic echocardiography to screen for pulmonary arterial hypertension","Pulmonary function tests and high-resolution CT for lung involvement","Nailfold capillaroscopy for scleroderma-pattern microangiopathy"],"redFlags":["Progressive breathlessness suggesting pulmonary arterial hypertension","Severe myositis with respiratory or bulbar muscle weakness","Renal involvement raising concern for lupus-like nephritis","Critical digital ischaemia"],"prognosis":"Many patients have a relatively favourable course with immunosuppressive control of inflammatory features, but pulmonary arterial hypertension is the principal determinant of mortality. Disease may remain stable, remit, or evolve into a defined connective tissue disorder over years.","pitfalls":["Dismissing puffy hands and Raynaud as trivial and missing early disease","Failing to screen periodically for pulmonary arterial hypertension","Assuming a benign course and under-treating significant organ involvement","Overlooking evolution into lupus or systemic sclerosis during follow-up"],"management":["Suppress inflammatory features (synovitis, myositis, serositis) with corticosteroids, dosed to the severity of organ involvement","Add disease-modifying immunosuppression such as methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate to spare steroids and control myositis, arthritis or lung disease","Treat Raynaud phenomenon with warmth, cold avoidance and calcium-channel blockers, adding phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors or prostanoids for critical digital ischaemia","Screen for pulmonary arterial hypertension with transthoracic echocardiography and confirm by right heart catheterisation, treating with pulmonary vasodilator therapy under a specialist service","Surveil for interstitial lung disease with serial pulmonary function tests and high-resolution CT, using immunosuppression for progressive disease","Relieve oesophageal dysmotility and reflux with proton pump inhibitors and prokinetic measures","Coordinate rheumatology and vascular medicine follow-up, escalating urgently for pulmonary arterial hypertension or progressive lung fibrosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology","Sharp diagnostic criteria and international MCTD literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"mixed_gonadal_dysgenesis":{"name":"Mixed gonadal dysgenesis (45,X/46,XY)","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MGD","45,X/46,XY mosaicism","asymmetric gonadal dysgenesis","X/XY mosaic gonadal dysgenesis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Endocrine Society clinical guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by asymmetric gonads, classically a dysgenetic testis on one side and a streak gonad on the other, in the context of 45,X/46,XY mosaicism","The phenotype is highly variable, ranging from apparently female through ambiguous genitalia to apparently male, depending on the functional testicular tissue present","Mullerian structures may persist on the side of the streak gonad because local anti-mullerian hormone is deficient there","Individuals may share features of Turner syndrome, such as short stature and cardiac or renal anomalies, when the 45,X line predominates","Dysgenetic gonads and streak gonads containing Y material carry a significant risk of gonadoblastoma, so gonadectomy of dysgenetic gonadal tissue is generally recommended","Prenatal detection of the mosaic karyotype often reveals a phenotypically normal male, illustrating the phenotypic breadth","Management addresses sex assignment, hormone replacement, growth, associated Turner-related anomalies and tumour prevention"],"pathophysiology":"Mixed gonadal dysgenesis arises from a 45,X/46,XY mosaic karyotype in which the proportion and distribution of the two cell lines vary between tissues. Where sufficient 46,XY cells populate the gonadal ridge, partial testicular differentiation occurs, whereas predominance of the 45,X line produces a streak gonad. The asymmetric hormone output determines internal and external genital development locally: the side with functional testicular tissue may show wolffian development and partial virilisation with mullerian regression, while the streak side retains mullerian structures. The retained Y-bearing dysgenetic and streak gonads are predisposed to gonadoblastoma and other germ cell tumours, and coexisting features of Turner syndrome may occur when the 45,X line is widespread.","additionalDifferentials":["Turner syndrome and its mosaic variants","Ovotesticular disorder of sex development","Partial gonadal dysgenesis (46,XY)","Partial androgen insensitivity syndrome","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (in a virilised infant)","5-alpha-reductase deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype on multiple tissues to detect and quantify 45,X/46,XY mosaicism","Pelvic and gonadal imaging by ultrasound or MRI to define gonads and mullerian structures","Serum gonadotropins, testosterone and anti-mullerian hormone, with hCG stimulation as needed","Gonadal biopsy and histology to characterise dysgenetic and streak tissue","Cardiac and renal imaging for Turner-associated anomalies","Germ cell tumour surveillance of retained gonadal tissue"],"redFlags":["An enlarging gonadal mass suggesting gonadoblastoma or dysgerminoma","Undiagnosed cardiac or renal malformation associated with the 45,X line","Ambiguous genitalia in a newborn requiring multidisciplinary evaluation","Failure of pubertal progression from gonadal insufficiency"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the degree of gonadal function, the presence of Turner-related anomalies and the timing of gonadectomy; short stature and gonadal insufficiency are common. Early removal of dysgenetic Y-bearing gonads markedly reduces the substantial germ cell tumour risk.","pitfalls":["Assuming a single karyotype excludes mosaicism when multiple tissues should be sampled","Leaving dysgenetic or streak gonads with Y material in situ and overlooking tumour risk","Missing associated cardiac and renal anomalies linked to the 45,X line","Predicting phenotype from karyotype alone rather than assessing gonadal function"],"management":["Determine sex of rearing through multidisciplinary discussion with the family, involving paediatric endocrinology, urology, clinical genetics and psychology","Perform gonadectomy of dysgenetic and streak Y-bearing gonads to remove the substantial gonadoblastoma and germ cell tumour risk","Induce and maintain puberty and secondary sexual characteristics with sex-steroid replacement matched to the assigned sex","Consider growth-promoting therapy such as growth hormone where Turner-associated short stature is present","Undertake reconstructive genital surgery where indicated, timed and decided jointly with the family","Screen for and manage Turner-associated cardiac and renal anomalies with echocardiography and renal imaging","Provide genetic counselling on the 45,X/46,XY mosaic karyotype together with long-term fertility, hormonal and psychological support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society clinical practice guidance on disorders of sex development"],"crossLinks":[]},"mixed_phenotype_acute_leukaemia":{"name":"Mixed phenotype acute leukaemia","system":"Haematological / Bone marrow","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPAL","Biphenotypic acute leukaemia","Acute leukaemia of ambiguous lineage"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acute leukaemia of ambiguous lineage in which the blasts express antigens of more than one lineage, so they cannot be assigned to acute myeloid or acute lymphoblastic leukaemia alone.","Diagnosis follows strict immunophenotypic criteria: myeloid assignment requires myeloperoxidase or monocytic differentiation, T-lineage requires cytoplasmic CD3, and B-lineage requires strong CD19 with other B markers.","May present as a single blast population co-expressing markers (biphenotypic) or as two distinct blast populations (bilineal); both fall under the same category.","The commonest combinations are B-myeloid and T-myeloid; trilineage and T/B forms are rare.","Two recurrent genetic subtypes are recognised by definition: MPAL with BCR-ABL1 and MPAL with KMT2A (MLL) rearrangement, both of which must be actively excluded.","A firm diagnosis requires flow cytometry and cytochemistry across an adequate marker panel plus cytogenetic and molecular testing; misassignment changes treatment entirely.","Optimal therapy is not standardised, but ALL-based induction regimens are often favoured, with allogeneic stem-cell transplantation considered in first remission for many patients.","Presentation resembles other acute leukaemias, with marrow failure causing anaemia, bleeding, infection and often high blast counts."],"pathophysiology":"Mixed phenotype acute leukaemia arises from transformation of an early haematopoietic progenitor, or from a leukaemic stem cell with lineage plasticity, before firm commitment to the myeloid, T-lymphoid or B-lymphoid programme. As a result the malignant clone co-expresses lineage-defining antigens of more than one pathway, either within a single blast population or as separate coexisting populations. Underlying this promiscuous phenotype are recurrent genetic lesions: BCR-ABL1 (the Philadelphia chromosome) produces a constitutively active tyrosine kinase, while KMT2A (MLL) rearrangements dysregulate developmental transcription programmes that maintain progenitors in a lineage-ambiguous state; other cases carry alterations in genes such as ZNF384, PHF6, WT1, RUNX1 and various epigenetic regulators. These events lock cells at a stage where the transcriptional networks that normally enforce a single lineage identity are disrupted, permitting simultaneous myeloid and lymphoid gene expression. Uncontrolled proliferation of these blasts replaces normal marrow, producing pancytopenia, and the tumour's mixed identity is what defies classification into a single conventional acute leukaemia category and complicates treatment selection.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myeloid leukaemia with aberrant lymphoid antigen expression","Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (B or T) with aberrant myeloid marker expression","Acute undifferentiated leukaemia","Early T-cell precursor acute lymphoblastic leukaemia","Blast phase of chronic myeloid leukaemia (especially with BCR-ABL1)","Lineage switch at relapse of a previously assigned acute leukaemia","Blastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive multiparameter flow cytometry covering myeloid, B- and T-lineage markers","Cytochemistry including myeloperoxidase and non-specific esterase","Cytogenetics and FISH for BCR-ABL1 and KMT2A (MLL) rearrangements","Molecular studies including BCR-ABL1 transcript and broad myeloid/lymphoid gene panels","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine biopsy with immunohistochemistry","Cerebrospinal fluid examination for central nervous system involvement","HLA typing and early referral for allogeneic transplant assessment"],"redFlags":["Very high circulating blast count with leukostasis symptoms (breathlessness, visual or neurological changes)","Tumour lysis syndrome with metabolic derangement at presentation","Severe cytopenias causing bleeding or serious infection","Central nervous system symptoms suggesting leukaemic infiltration","Detection of BCR-ABL1, which changes management and prognosis"],"prognosis":"Mixed phenotype acute leukaemia generally carries a less favourable prognosis than lineage-defined acute leukaemias, particularly in adults and in cases with adverse cytogenetics such as BCR-ABL1 or KMT2A rearrangement. Outcomes are better in children, and allogeneic stem-cell transplantation in first remission improves survival for many patients.","pitfalls":["Forcing the leukaemia into a single AML or ALL category and missing the mixed phenotype, leading to suboptimal therapy.","Over-diagnosing MPAL from minor aberrant antigen expression without meeting the strict lineage-assignment criteria.","Failing to test for BCR-ABL1 and KMT2A rearrangements, which are defining and treatment-altering.","Overlooking a lineage switch at relapse, mistaking it for a new second leukaemia.","Delaying transplant referral and HLA typing, given that many patients benefit from transplantation in first remission."],"management":["Deliver intensive multi-agent chemotherapy, with evidence favouring a lymphoid (ALL-directed) induction regimen over myeloid regimens in most cases","Add a tyrosine kinase inhibitor such as imatinib or dasatinib when BCR-ABL1 is present","Give central nervous system-directed prophylaxis with intrathecal chemotherapy","Consolidate with allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, particularly in adults and in adverse-cytogenetic disease such as BCR-ABL1 or KMT2A rearrangement","Provide supportive care with transfusion support, neutropenic infection prophylaxis and prevention of tumour lysis syndrome","Manage in a specialist haemato-oncology centre, reassessing lineage and switching regimen if response is inadequate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","International consensus classification of acute leukaemias","Standard adult and paediatric haematology-oncology references"],"crossLinks":[]},"mixed_renal_tubular_acidosis":{"name":"Mixed Renal Tubular Acidosis (Type 3)","system":"Renal / Skeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Type 3 renal tubular acidosis","Combined proximal-distal RTA","Carbonic anhydrase II deficiency syndrome","Marble brain disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nephrology and metabolic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Type 3 (mixed) RTA combines proximal bicarbonate wasting with a distal defect in urinary acidification, so the acidosis tends to be more severe than either isolated form.","The classic inherited cause is carbonic anhydrase II (CA II) deficiency, an autosomal recessive disorder producing the triad of osteopetrosis, renal tubular acidosis and cerebral calcification (Guibaud-Vainsel or marble brain syndrome).","Carbonic anhydrase II is expressed in both proximal and distal tubular cells, in osteoclasts and in the brain, which explains the combined renal, skeletal and neurological phenotype.","Osteopetrosis arises because osteoclasts cannot acidify their resorption lacunae, giving dense but brittle bones prone to fracture, cranial nerve compression and marrow encroachment.","A transient developmental form of mixed RTA occurs in infants owing to tubular immaturity and usually resolves with growth; the label 'type 3' is now rarely applied to adults.","Cerebral calcification, typically of the basal ganglia and periventricular regions, with developmental delay and cognitive impairment is characteristic of the CA II-deficient form.","Both a raised fractional excretion of bicarbonate and an inability to maximally lower urine pH can be demonstrated, reflecting the dual tubular lesion.","Dental anomalies, growth retardation and visual or hearing impairment from narrowing of bony foramina may accompany the syndrome."],"pathophysiology":"Carbonic anhydrase II catalyses the reversible hydration of carbon dioxide to carbonic acid and is central to hydrogen ion and bicarbonate handling in several tissues. In the proximal tubule its loss impairs reclamation of filtered bicarbonate, producing bicarbonate wasting, while in the distal nephron it limits the generation of protons required for maximal urinary acidification; the coexistence of these lesions yields a mixed pattern with features of both proximal and distal RTA. The same enzyme deficiency cripples osteoclasts, which depend on acidification of the sealed resorption zone to dissolve bone mineral, so bone turnover falls and the skeleton becomes abnormally dense yet mechanically weak, causing fractures and encroachment on marrow and cranial foramina. In the central nervous system enzyme deficiency is associated with progressive intracranial calcification and neurodevelopmental impairment. The autosomal recessive CA2 gene defect therefore unites nephrocalcinosis-sparing acidosis, osteopetrosis and cerebral calcification within a single disorder, whereas the separate transient infantile form reflects physiological immaturity of tubular bicarbonate handling rather than a fixed genetic lesion.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis","Isolated distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis","Other inherited osteopetroses (for example TCIRG1 or CLCN7 forms) without renal tubular acidosis","Hyperkalaemic (type 4) renal tubular acidosis","Idiopathic basal ganglia calcification and other causes of cerebral calcification such as hypoparathyroidism","Other skeletal dysplasias with generalised osteosclerosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood gas and serum electrolytes confirming a hyperchloraemic normal anion gap acidosis","Urine pH and urinary acidification testing together with fractional excretion of bicarbonate","Skeletal survey demonstrating generalised osteosclerosis or osteopetrosis","Cranial computed tomography to detect basal ganglia and periventricular calcification","Red-cell carbonic anhydrase II activity assay","CA2 gene sequencing","Assessment of growth, neurodevelopment, vision and hearing"],"redFlags":["Recurrent fractures occurring despite radiologically dense bones","Progressive developmental delay or cognitive decline with intracranial calcification","Cranial nerve deficits such as visual loss, facial weakness or deafness from bony foramen compression","Severe acidosis with growth failure in infancy","Cytopenias suggesting marrow encroachment"],"prognosis":"The inherited CA II-deficient form is a lifelong disorder in which alkali therapy corrects the acidosis and may support growth, but the osteopetrosis, fracture tendency and neurological impairment persist and vary in severity; the transient infantile form resolves with maturation.","pitfalls":["Labelling a patient as 'type 3' without recognising the underlying carbonic anhydrase II deficiency and its systemic features.","Missing osteopetrosis because dense bones remain silent until a fracture or nerve compression occurs.","Attributing cerebral calcification to hypoparathyroidism without checking calcium and parathyroid status.","Forgetting that the bones are fragile despite their increased radiographic density.","Focusing on the skeletal disease while overlooking the readily treatable acidosis."],"management":["Correct the metabolic acidosis with oral alkali replacement using sodium bicarbonate or citrate preparations","Optimise alkali therapy to support linear growth, monitoring growth parameters over time","Manage the osteopetrotic skeleton with fracture prevention and orthopaedic care for dense but brittle bones","Surveil for and surgically decompress cranial nerve compression (optic and facial) and marrow encroachment","Provide neurodevelopmental and educational support for cerebral calcification-related developmental delay and cognitive impairment","Monitor periodically with blood gases, serum electrolytes and urinary acidification testing","Offer genetic counselling and family testing given autosomal recessive CA2 (carbonic anhydrase II) inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Oxford Textbook of Medicine","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"miyoshi_myopathy":{"name":"Miyoshi myopathy","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DYSF","dysferlinopathy","Miyoshi muscular dystrophy","distal myopathy, posterior compartment","MMD1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distal myopathy typically presenting in young adulthood with weakness of the posterior calf, so that patients notice difficulty standing on tiptoe or running","Wasting begins in the gastrocnemius and soleus, in contrast to the anterior distal weakness of several other distal myopathies","Caused by mutations in DYSF encoding dysferlin, and inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern, part of the dysferlinopathy spectrum that also includes limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","Serum creatine kinase is characteristically very high, often markedly so, which is a useful pointer","Weakness spreads over time to involve the thighs and upper limbs, and the same DYSF mutations can produce a proximal limb-girdle phenotype even within one family","Muscle biopsy shows dystrophic changes with reduced or absent dysferlin on immunostaining, sometimes with an inflammatory infiltrate that can mimic myositis","Immunosuppression is ineffective, so distinguishing it from inflammatory myopathy avoids inappropriate treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Miyoshi myopathy is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in DYSF, the gene encoding dysferlin, a membrane protein concentrated at the sarcolemma that is essential for the calcium-dependent resealing of the muscle-fibre membrane after injury. When dysferlin is deficient, small membrane disruptions that occur with normal muscle activity are not repaired efficiently, leading to progressive membrane damage, leakage of muscle enzymes and fibre degeneration. The resulting dystrophic process preferentially begins in the posterior lower-leg muscles in the Miyoshi phenotype, and impaired membrane repair may also provoke a secondary inflammatory response that can be mistaken histologically for an inflammatory myopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (including other dysferlinopathy phenotypes)","Other distal myopathies such as GNE and tibial (anterior) distal myopathies","Inflammatory myopathies such as polymyositis","Myofibrillar myopathies","Metabolic myopathies causing exercise intolerance and high creatine kinase","Chronic neuropathies causing distal wasting"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase, characteristically markedly elevated","Electromyography showing a myopathic pattern","Muscle magnetic resonance imaging demonstrating posterior calf and later thigh involvement","Muscle biopsy with dysferlin immunostaining and evaluation of monocyte dysferlin expression","Targeted DYSF genetic testing","Assessment to exclude an inflammatory myopathy when the biopsy shows inflammation"],"redFlags":["Being started on immunosuppression for a presumed inflammatory myopathy","Rapidly progressive proximal weakness suggesting a limb-girdle phenotype","Respiratory involvement in advanced disease","Loss of ambulation requiring rehabilitation input"],"prognosis":"Miyoshi myopathy is slowly progressive, with weakness spreading from the calves to more proximal muscles over years to decades; many patients eventually need walking aids, but respiratory and cardiac involvement are relatively uncommon and lifespan is often near normal.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the inflammatory biopsy findings for polymyositis and giving ineffective immunosuppression","Overlooking the very high creatine kinase as a clue to a dysferlinopathy","Assuming a purely distal disorder, when proximal limb-girdle phenotypes share the same gene","Attributing early calf weakness to a neuropathy rather than a myopathy"],"management":["Recognise there is no disease-modifying drug and base care on physiotherapy with a graded exercise and stretching programme to preserve function and prevent contractures","Avoid corticosteroids, which do not help dysferlinopathy and may worsen it despite biopsy inflammation mimicking myositis","Provide mobility aids, orthoses and occupational therapy as distal then proximal weakness progresses","Undertake periodic respiratory and cardiac assessment, although these are relatively uncommon in this phenotype","Support weight and metabolic health to reduce load on weakening muscle","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive DYSF inheritance and test at-risk relatives","Refer to a specialist neuromuscular service for multidisciplinary care and access to dysferlinopathy trials"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: Dysferlinopathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"mngie_syndrome":{"name":"MNGIE","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mitochondrial neurogastrointestinal encephalomyopathy","Thymidine phosphorylase deficiency","TYMP","MNGIE"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews MNGIE / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Suspect MNGIE in a young adult with severe gastrointestinal dysmotility, marked cachexia, ptosis and progressive external ophthalmoplegia, and a peripheral neuropathy","It is an autosomal recessive nuclear disorder caused by mutations in TYMP, which encodes thymidine phosphorylase, leading indirectly to mitochondrial DNA instability","Loss of thymidine phosphorylase activity causes systemic accumulation of thymidine and deoxyuridine, which unbalances the mitochondrial nucleotide pool and produces depletion, deletions and point mutations of mitochondrial DNA","Brain MRI characteristically shows a diffuse, often asymptomatic leukoencephalopathy","Gastrointestinal features (early satiety, dysphagia, gastroparesis, pseudo-obstruction, diarrhoea and diverticulosis) dominate and drive the profound weight loss","Diagnosis is supported by grossly elevated plasma thymidine and deoxyuridine and by low or absent thymidine phosphorylase activity in leucocytes, confirmed by TYMP genetics","Management is largely supportive with nutritional support; disease-modifying strategies that lower toxic nucleosides are handled in specialist centres"],"pathophysiology":"MNGIE is caused by recessive loss-of-function mutations in TYMP, the gene encoding cytosolic thymidine phosphorylase. Without this enzyme, the pyrimidine nucleosides thymidine and deoxyuridine accumulate in blood and tissues. The resulting imbalance of the mitochondrial deoxyribonucleotide pool disturbs mitochondrial DNA replication, causing depletion, multiple deletions and point mutations and thereby a secondary failure of oxidative phosphorylation. The smooth muscle of the gut, peripheral nerves, extraocular muscles and cerebral white matter are particularly affected, producing the visceral myopathy and dysmotility, neuropathy, ophthalmoplegia and leukoencephalopathy that define the disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction from other causes","Anorexia nervosa or other causes of severe cachexia","Coeliac disease and other malabsorptive states","Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia from other mitochondrial causes","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy","Systemic sclerosis with gastrointestinal involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma or urine thymidine and deoxyuridine levels, which are markedly elevated","Leucocyte thymidine phosphorylase enzyme activity assay","TYMP molecular genetic testing for confirmation","Brain MRI demonstrating diffuse leukoencephalopathy","Nerve conduction studies characterising a demyelinating or mixed neuropathy","Gastrointestinal motility studies and nutritional assessment"],"redFlags":["Intestinal pseudo-obstruction or perforation from diverticular disease","Severe malnutrition and cachexia requiring nutritional rescue","Aspiration from dysphagia and gastroparesis","Rapidly progressive neuropathy causing loss of ambulation"],"prognosis":"MNGIE is a progressive, life-limiting disorder; the relentless gastrointestinal dysmotility and cachexia are major contributors to morbidity and premature death, typically in early to mid adulthood without disease-modifying therapy.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the cachexia and gastrointestinal symptoms as an eating disorder or functional gut disorder","Overlooking the diagnosis because the leukoencephalopathy is often clinically silent","Attributing ophthalmoplegia to an isolated ocular myopathy rather than the systemic disease","Not measuring plasma nucleosides, the most accessible and specific biochemical clue"],"management":["Restore thymidine phosphorylase activity to lower toxic thymidine and deoxyuridine, using allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the principal disease-modifying option in selected patients","Consider orthotopic liver transplantation as an alternative enzyme source, or erythrocyte-encapsulated thymidine phosphorylase enzyme replacement where available in trials","Provide aggressive nutritional support, frequently parenteral nutrition, to counter cachexia and intestinal failure","Manage gastrointestinal dysmotility, gastroparesis and pseudo-obstruction with prokinetics, bowel rest and treatment of small-bowel bacterial overgrowth","Give symptomatic care of the peripheral neuropathy, ptosis and ophthalmoplegia","Monitor for and correct nutritional deficiencies and the metabolic consequences of dysmotility","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive TYMP inheritance with family testing","Coordinate care through a specialist mitochondrial and metabolic medicine centre"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Mitochondrial Neurogastrointestinal Encephalopathy Disease","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"mobius_syndrome":{"name":"Mobius syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Moebius syndrome","congenital facial diplegia with abducens palsy","Mobius sequence"],"chapter":null,"source":"Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital, non-progressive disorder defined by bilateral (often asymmetric) facial (VII) and abducens (VI) nerve palsies, producing a characteristic mask-like, expressionless face with impaired horizontal gaze and abduction","Presents in the neonate with poor feeding, incomplete eye closure (lagophthalmos), inability to smile and lack of facial expression; drooling and poor lip seal are common","Additional cranial nerve involvement is frequent, especially hypoglossal (XII) causing tongue hypoplasia or fasciculation, and lower bulbar nerves (IX, X) producing dysphagia and aspiration risk","Orofacial and limb anomalies co-occur, including micrognathia, high-arched or cleft palate, talipes equinovarus and terminal transverse limb reduction, with overlap of Poland anomaly and arthrogryposis","Esotropia is common and the horizontal gaze palsy means the eyes cannot abduct past the midline, although convergence and vertical movements are relatively spared","Lagophthalmos with a variably protective Bell's phenomenon places the cornea at risk of exposure keratopathy, so ocular surface lubrication is essential","Now classified within the congenital cranial dysinnervation disorders, reflecting maldevelopment or agenesis of brainstem motor nuclei rather than a primary muscle disease","Cognition is usually normal, but a subset show neurodevelopmental problems including autism spectrum features"],"pathophysiology":"Mobius syndrome is a congenital cranial dysinnervation disorder resulting from maldevelopment or hypoplasia of the sixth and seventh cranial nerve nuclei in the brainstem, with variable involvement of adjacent nuclei (notably XII, IX and X). Both disruptive/vascular and genetic mechanisms are implicated: proposed hypoxic-ischaemic interruption of the developing rhombencephalic vascular supply (associated with misoprostol exposure and, historically, chorionic villus sampling) and, less commonly, defined genetic causes. The end result is absent or deficient innervation of the muscles of facial expression and the lateral recti, on a background of rhombencephalic maldevelopment. Most cases are sporadic.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital fibrosis of the extraocular muscles and other congenital cranial dysinnervation disorders","Neonatal (transient) myasthenia and congenital myasthenic syndromes","Congenital myotonic dystrophy and other congenital myopathies","Birth-trauma facial nerve palsy (usually unilateral)","Hereditary congenital facial paresis without an abduction deficit","Bilateral Duane retraction syndrome","CHARGE syndrome and other syndromic cranial neuropathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thin-section brain MRI to assess the brainstem, cranial nerve nuclei and cerebellum","Orthoptic assessment of ocular alignment, ductions and horizontal gaze","Corneal and ocular surface examination for exposure keratopathy","Feeding and swallowing evaluation, including videofluoroscopy, for aspiration risk","Genetic evaluation and counselling, with chromosomal or exome testing if syndromic","Audiology and neurodevelopmental assessment","Polysomnography if central hypoventilation or sleep-disordered breathing is suspected"],"redFlags":["Aspiration and recurrent chest infections from bulbar dysfunction","Central apnoea or hypoventilation and failure to thrive in the neonatal period","Corneal ulceration from exposure keratopathy","Severe feeding difficulty with poor weight gain"],"prognosis":"The disorder is non-progressive and life expectancy is usually normal once feeding and airway issues are managed, with many patients functioning well given multidisciplinary support. Facial diplegia is permanent, but smile-reanimation surgery (such as gracilis free-muscle transfer) and strabismus surgery can improve function and appearance.","pitfalls":["Attributing the expressionless face to intellectual disability rather than to facial palsy","Missing corneal exposure because an intact Bell's phenomenon masks the lagophthalmos","Overlooking aspiration risk and airway compromise in the neonate","Assuming a purely muscular problem and forgetting the brainstem dysinnervation basis","Failing to screen for associated limb, orofacial and neurodevelopmental anomalies"],"management":["Protect the cornea from exposure keratopathy with intensive ocular surface lubrication and, where needed, eyelid surgery","Secure feeding and airway safety with modified feeding and formal swallowing assessment, using nasogastric or gastrostomy support and addressing aspiration risk","Correct esotropia and improve ocular alignment with strabismus surgery","Restore a voluntary smile and oral competence with facial reanimation surgery such as gracilis free-muscle transfer","Provide speech and language therapy and orthodontic or craniofacial care for palatal and jaw anomalies","Manage associated limb anomalies such as talipes and reduction defects orthopaedically","Follow up through a multidisciplinary team with audiology, neurodevelopmental support and genetic counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"modic_changes":{"name":"Modic changes","system":"Musculoskeletal system / Spine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vertebral endplate signal changes","vertebral endplate marrow changes","Modic endplate changes"],"chapter":null,"source":"The Adult and Pediatric Spine (Rothman-Simeone) / Grainger and Allison's Diagnostic Radiology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Modic changes are MRI signal changes in the vertebral endplate and adjacent subchondral marrow that accompany degenerative disc disease, most often in the lower lumbar spine","Type 1 represents oedema and fibrovascular marrow, appearing low on T1 and high on T2, and is the type most strongly associated with active low back pain and can enhance with contrast","Type 2 represents fatty marrow replacement, appearing high on T1 and iso- to slightly hyperintense on T2, and is generally regarded as a more stable, less painful state","Type 3 represents subchondral bony sclerosis, appearing low on both T1 and T2 and corresponding to endplate sclerosis on radiographs","The types are dynamic and can convert into one another over time or coexist as mixed patterns, reflecting an evolving degenerative and reparative process","The key differential for a painful type 1 change is infective spondylodiscitis; disc-space and endplate destruction, high T2 disc signal and paravertebral or epidural collections favour infection, whereas Modic changes spare the disc and lack these features","There is no specific treatment for the changes themselves; management targets the underlying discogenic pain and the changes are one factor among many in surgical decision-making"],"pathophysiology":"Modic changes arise from disruption of the vertebral endplate in the setting of degenerative disc disease. Microfractures and increased permeability of the endplate allow mechanical overload and biochemical exposure of subchondral marrow to disc material, provoking a local inflammatory and reparative response. In the acute type 1 pattern there is oedema, granulation tissue and fibrovascular marrow with an inflammatory infiltrate; over time this may resolve into type 2 fatty marrow replacement or type 3 subchondral sclerosis. Proposed drivers include mechanical stress, an autoimmune reaction to sequestered nucleus pulposus, and a contested hypothesis of low-grade colonisation by anaerobic skin organisms, though the changes are classified as a degenerative endplate and marrow phenomenon rather than an infective process. Associated inflammatory mediators sensitise nociceptive fibres in the endplate and outer disc, linking type 1 changes in particular to low back pain.","additionalDifferentials":["Infective spondylodiscitis or vertebral osteomyelitis","Metastatic marrow infiltration or myeloma","Traumatic or insufficiency endplate fracture","Seronegative spondyloarthropathy with Andersson lesions or corner inflammation","Haemangioma or focal fatty marrow of the vertebral body","Neuropathic (Charcot) spinal arthropathy","Calcium pyrophosphate or crystal-related endplate change"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI including T1, T2 and fat-suppressed sequences to classify the change and assess the disc and marrow","Contrast-enhanced MRI when infection or tumour is a concern","Radiographs to demonstrate disc-space narrowing and endplate sclerosis","Inflammatory markers and blood cultures if spondylodiscitis is suspected","Image-guided biopsy or aspiration where infection or malignancy must be excluded","Provocative discography only in selected cases when confirming a discogenic pain source"],"redFlags":["Fever, night pain, raised inflammatory markers or disc-space destruction suggesting spondylodiscitis","Known malignancy, weight loss or a destructive marrow lesion suggesting metastasis or myeloma","Progressive neurological deficit or cauda equina features","An epidural or paravertebral collection on imaging"],"prognosis":"Modic changes are common incidental findings whose clinical significance is variable; type 1 changes correlate best with low back pain and may fluctuate or convert to the more quiescent type 2, and their presence is only one modest factor in the overall prognosis of degenerative disc disease.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting a painful type 1 change as infective spondylodiscitis, or vice versa, and mismanaging the patient","Attributing all of a patient's back pain to Modic changes when they are a frequent incidental finding","Overlooking a destructive or neoplastic marrow lesion by labelling atypical signal as degenerative","Basing surgical decisions on the endplate changes alone without correlating clinical and other imaging findings"],"management":["Manage the associated low back pain conservatively first with education, activity modification, structured exercise and physiotherapy","Use simple analgesia and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for symptom control, adding neuropathic agents if there is radicular pain","Exclude infective spondylodiscitis before attributing pain to Modic change, using contrast-enhanced MRI, inflammatory markers and image-guided biopsy where doubt exists","Consider image-guided intradiscal or epidural corticosteroid injection in selected refractory type 1 change","Reserve surgery such as spinal fusion for carefully selected patients with concordant, disabling discogenic pain unresponsive to conservative care","Avoid unproven antibiotic therapy outside research settings, since a bacterial cause remains contested","Monitor clinically, recognising the dynamic natural history whereby type 1 change often converts to quiescent type 2"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","Grainger and Allison's Diagnostic Radiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"mody":{"name":"Maturity-onset diabetes of the young","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MODY","monogenic diabetes","familial young-onset diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of monogenic, autosomal dominant forms of diabetes caused by single-gene defects impairing beta-cell function rather than autoimmunity or insulin resistance","Suspect it in young, non-obese patients with diabetes across several generations and a strong family history consistent with dominant inheritance","Patients are typically negative for islet autoantibodies and retain measurable endogenous insulin (detectable C-peptide) beyond the honeymoon period","It is frequently misclassified as type 1 or type 2 diabetes, so a high index of suspicion and genetic testing are key to correct diagnosis","The specific gene determines the phenotype and treatment: some forms are mild and stable, others progressive","One common subtype often causes mild, stable, non-progressive fasting hyperglycaemia that may need no pharmacological treatment","Another common subtype is characteristically sensitive to sulfonylureas, so accurate genetic diagnosis can allow a change away from insulin","Correct diagnosis has implications for prognosis, treatment choice, family screening, and, in some subtypes, pregnancy management"],"pathophysiology":"Maturity-onset diabetes of the young comprises several monogenic disorders in which a mutation in a single gene critical to pancreatic beta-cell development or function impairs insulin secretion. Inheritance is autosomal dominant, so an affected parent transmits the condition to roughly half of offspring, producing diabetes across successive generations at a young age. Unlike type 1 diabetes, there is no autoimmune beta-cell destruction, and unlike type 2 diabetes, obesity and insulin resistance are not central. Different causative genes give distinct clinical pictures, ranging from mild stable hyperglycaemia due to an altered glucose-sensing threshold, to progressive insulin secretory failure with risk of the usual diabetes complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Type 2 diabetes mellitus in the young","Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults","Neonatal diabetes and other monogenic beta-cell disorders","Secondary diabetes from pancreatic disease","Gestational diabetes when first detected in pregnancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Islet autoantibody panel to help exclude autoimmune diabetes","C-peptide to demonstrate preserved endogenous insulin secretion","Detailed multigenerational family history of diabetes","Genetic testing of the relevant MODY genes to confirm the subtype","Assessment for extra-pancreatic features associated with certain subtypes such as renal cysts","Cascade genetic screening of at-risk family members"],"redFlags":["Presentation with ketoacidosis, which is atypical and suggests type 1 diabetes instead","Rapidly progressive hyperglycaemia inconsistent with a mild stable subtype","Associated renal or genitourinary abnormalities suggesting a specific syndromic subtype","Diagnostic uncertainty leading to inappropriate lifelong insulin in a sulfonylurea-responsive form"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies by subtype: the mild glucose-sensing form is typically stable with low complication risk and may need no treatment, whereas transcription-factor subtypes are progressive and can develop classic microvascular and macrovascular complications if hyperglycaemia is uncontrolled; accurate genetic diagnosis enables tailored, often simpler, management.","pitfalls":["Automatically labelling young-onset diabetes as type 1 and committing to lifelong insulin without considering monogenic causes","Overlooking the family history that suggests dominant inheritance","Failing to check autoantibodies and C-peptide before classification","Not offering genetic testing that could change treatment and enable family screening"],"management":["Confirm the subtype by genetic testing, since it dictates treatment and avoids mislabelling as type 1 or type 2 diabetes","Tailor therapy to genotype, using low-dose sulfonylureas for the transcription-factor subtypes (HNF1A, HNF4A), often in place of insulin","Recognise the glucokinase (GCK) subtype needs no pharmacological treatment outside pregnancy, managed by lifestyle measures alone","Transition suitable insulin-treated patients to sulfonylureas once a responsive subtype is confirmed","Screen for and manage microvascular and macrovascular complications in the progressive transcription-factor subtypes","Provide genetic counselling and cascade testing of at-risk relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance","Refer to endocrinology and clinical genetics, with specialist input during pregnancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"mody_type_1":{"name":"Maturity-Onset Diabetes of the Young Type 1 (HNF4A-MODY)","system":"Endocrine / Monogenic diabetes","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HNF4A-MODY","MODY1","HNF4-alpha diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrinology and clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in HNF4A, a transcription factor upstream of HNF1A that regulates beta-cell gene expression; inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with high penetrance.","Clinically resembles HNF1A-MODY with progressive insulin secretory failure and marked sensitivity to low-dose sulfonylureas, which are first-line therapy in preference to insulin.","A distinctive feature is fetal and neonatal macrosomia with transient diabetic-range neonatal hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia in a subset of carriers, reflecting a biphasic effect of HNF4A on beta-cell function.","Unlike HNF1A-MODY, the renal threshold for glucose is normal, so glycosuria disproportionate to blood glucose is absent.","HNF4A carriers tend to have a lower serum HDL cholesterol and altered apolipoprotein profile compared with HNF1A-MODY, which can offer a discriminating clue.","Diagnosis should be considered in young non-obese patients with autoantibody-negative, C-peptide-positive diabetes and a strong multigenerational family history.","A macrosomic neonate with transient hyperinsulinism born to a parent with young-onset diabetes should prompt HNF4A testing of the family.","Genetic confirmation allows rational transfer from insulin to sulfonylurea in many patients and informs cascade testing of relatives."],"pathophysiology":"MODY1 arises from heterozygous inactivating mutations in HNF4A, which encodes hepatocyte nuclear factor 4-alpha, a nuclear receptor transcription factor expressed in pancreatic islets, liver, kidney and gut. Within the beta cell, HNF4A sits within a transcriptional network that includes HNF1A and regulates genes governing glucose metabolism, mitochondrial function and insulin secretion; haploinsufficiency progressively impairs glucose-stimulated insulin release. The phenotype is characteristically biphasic: in fetal and neonatal life, altered regulation of the KATP-channel pathway can produce hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia and consequent macrosomia, whereas from adolescence or early adulthood beta-cell function declines and diabetes emerges and worsens over time. Because HNF4A also drives hepatic lipoprotein synthesis, carriers display characteristic reductions in HDL and apolipoprotein levels. Unlike HNF1A-MODY, tubular glucose handling is preserved, so the low renal threshold seen in MODY3 does not occur. Retained sensitivity of residual beta cells to sulfonylurea-mediated closure of the KATP channel underlies the strong therapeutic response to these agents.","additionalDifferentials":["HNF1A-MODY (MODY3), the closest phenotypic mimic distinguished by low renal glucose threshold and normal birthweight","Type 1 diabetes mellitus (autoantibody positive, C-peptide low or undetectable)","Young-onset type 2 diabetes mellitus with obesity and insulin resistance","Glucokinase-MODY (MODY2) with mild stable non-progressive fasting hyperglycaemia","Congenital hyperinsulinism from other causes in the neonate with hypoglycaemia","Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of HNF4A (sequencing and dosage analysis), ideally within a MODY gene panel","Pancreatic islet autoantibodies (GAD, IA-2, ZnT8) to exclude autoimmune diabetes","Fasting and stimulated C-peptide to demonstrate preserved endogenous insulin secretion","Fasting lipid profile looking for reduced HDL cholesterol","Documentation of birthweight and any neonatal hypoglycaemia in the proband and relatives","Detailed three-generation family pedigree for diabetes","HbA1c and self-monitored glucose to assess progression and treatment response"],"redFlags":["Severe or recurrent neonatal hypoglycaemia in a macrosomic infant requiring urgent glucose support","Development of chronic microvascular complications, as the diabetes is progressive and confers standard complication risk","Abrupt loss of glycaemic control suggesting exhaustion of sulfonylurea responsiveness and need for insulin","Diabetic ketoacidosis, which should prompt reconsideration of type 1 diabetes","Pregnancy in an affected woman, requiring specialist planning for maternal glycaemia and fetal macrosomia"],"prognosis":"With genetic diagnosis and appropriate low-dose sulfonylurea therapy many patients achieve durable glycaemic control for years, though beta-cell function declines progressively and some eventually require insulin. Microvascular complication risk mirrors that of other forms of diabetes and depends on long-term glycaemic control.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying the patient as type 1 diabetes and committing them to lifelong insulin when a sulfonylurea would suffice","Overlooking the diagnosis because glycosuria out of proportion to glycaemia (a MODY3 clue) is absent","Failing to recognise transient neonatal hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia as an early manifestation in mutation carriers","Attributing macrosomia solely to maternal gestational diabetes and missing the underlying monogenic cause","Not offering cascade genetic testing and counselling to at-risk relatives after a diagnosis is confirmed"],"management":["Use low-dose sulfonylureas as first-line therapy in preference to insulin, exploiting the marked sensitivity of HNF4A-MODY","Transition many insulin-treated patients to sulfonylureas after genetic confirmation","Add or switch to insulin as beta-cell secretory function declines over time","Anticipate and manage neonatal hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia in carriers (for example with diazoxide) and associated macrosomia, monitoring at-risk newborns","Manage affected pregnancies jointly with specialists, with maternal glucose and foetal growth surveillance","Screen for diabetic microvascular and macrovascular complications with ongoing glycaemic monitoring","Provide genetic counselling and cascade testing of relatives for autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard endocrinology reference texts","International monogenic diabetes clinical guidelines","Clinical genetics reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"mody_type_2":{"name":"Maturity-Onset Diabetes of the Young Type 2 (GCK-MODY)","system":"Endocrine / Monogenic diabetes","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GCK-MODY","MODY2","Glucokinase MODY"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrinology and clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by heterozygous inactivating variants in GCK, which encodes glucokinase, the beta-cell glucose sensor; inheritance is autosomal dominant with near-complete penetrance.","Produces mild, stable, non-progressive fasting hyperglycaemia present from birth rather than diabetes that worsens over time.","The glucose increment during an oral glucose tolerance test is characteristically small because the defect resets the glucose set-point rather than abolishing insulin secretion.","HbA1c is typically only mildly raised and remains stable across decades, distinguishing it from progressive forms of MODY.","Most non-pregnant patients require no pharmacological treatment; diet and lifestyle suffice and drugs rarely improve the fasting level.","Microvascular and macrovascular complications are uncommon, so aggressive glucose-lowering is generally unnecessary and unhelpful.","In pregnancy, management hinges on whether the fetus has inherited the mutation: an unaffected fetus of an affected mother may overgrow and may warrant maternal treatment.","Frequently discovered incidentally on routine screening, in pregnancy, or during family testing rather than through symptomatic presentation."],"pathophysiology":"MODY2 results from heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in GCK, the gene encoding glucokinase (hexokinase IV). Glucokinase catalyses the rate-limiting phosphorylation of glucose in the pancreatic beta cell and hepatocyte and functions as the principal glucose sensor that couples ambient glucose concentration to insulin secretion. A partial reduction in glucokinase activity raises the threshold at which glucose triggers insulin release, effectively resetting the glucostat to a higher level; the beta cell still secretes insulin appropriately, but only once a slightly higher glucose concentration is reached. The consequence is a modest, lifelong elevation of fasting glucose that is tightly regulated and does not progress, because the underlying secretory machinery and beta-cell mass are intact. Reduced hepatic glucokinase activity also blunts postprandial glycogen synthesis, contributing to the small glucose excursion after a carbohydrate load. Because regulation around the new set-point remains physiological, chronic hyperglycaemia is mild and the long-term risk of diabetic complications is low. Rare biallelic inactivating mutations instead cause permanent neonatal diabetes, while activating GCK mutations cause congenital hyperinsulinism, illustrating the gene's dose-dependent role.","additionalDifferentials":["Impaired fasting glucose or early type 2 diabetes mellitus","HNF1A-MODY and HNF4A-MODY, which are progressive and treatment-responsive rather than stable","Gestational diabetes mellitus when first detected in pregnancy","Type 1 diabetes mellitus (autoantibody positive) when hyperglycaemia is mistakenly thought significant","Stress or steroid-induced hyperglycaemia","Laboratory artefact or non-fasting sampling causing apparent hyperglycaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of GCK, typically within a MODY panel","Fasting plasma glucose demonstrating mild, stable elevation","Oral glucose tolerance test showing a characteristically small glucose increment","Serial HbA1c confirming a persistently mild and stable value over time","Pancreatic autoantibodies to exclude autoimmune diabetes","Fetal growth ultrasound surveillance in affected pregnancies","Family screening of first-degree relatives with fasting glucose"],"redFlags":["HbA1c or fasting glucose rising well beyond the expected mild range, suggesting a coexisting second cause of diabetes","Development of significant microvascular complications, which is atypical and warrants re-evaluation","Fetal macrosomia in an affected mother carrying an unaffected fetus, indicating need for maternal glucose management","Symptomatic osmotic symptoms such as marked polyuria or polydipsia, which are inconsistent with isolated GCK-MODY"],"prognosis":"Excellent: fasting hyperglycaemia is mild and stable lifelong, treatment is usually unnecessary outside pregnancy, and the risk of clinically significant diabetic complications is low. Correct genetic diagnosis spares patients unnecessary medication, monitoring and insurance implications.","pitfalls":["Escalating glucose-lowering therapy in pursuit of normoglycaemia, which is ineffective and exposes patients to hypoglycaemia and side effects","Misdiagnosing the condition as type 1 or type 2 diabetes and applying inappropriate lifelong treatment","In pregnancy, treating the mother without considering fetal genotype, risking impaired fetal growth if the fetus is also affected","Assuming the mild hyperglycaemia will inevitably progress and over-monitoring the patient","Overlooking the strongly hereditary pattern and failing to test and reassure relatives"],"management":["Recognise that most non-pregnant patients need no glucose-lowering drugs and manage with diet and lifestyle alone","Avoid unnecessary pharmacotherapy, monitoring and the diagnostic label burden once GCK-MODY is genetically confirmed","In pregnancy, guide treatment by foetal genotype, considering insulin if an unaffected foetus shows accelerated growth but withholding intensive treatment if the foetus is affected","Undertake foetal growth ultrasound surveillance in affected pregnancies","Reassure patients that microvascular and macrovascular complication risk is low and aggressive glucose-lowering is unhelpful","Provide genetic counselling and family glucose and genetic screening for autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard endocrinology reference texts","International monogenic diabetes clinical guidelines","Clinical genetics reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"mody_type_2_glucokinase":{"name":"MODY type 2 (GCK)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GCK-MODY","glucokinase MODY","MODY2","glucokinase-related maturity-onset diabetes of the young"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by heterozygous inactivating mutations in GCK, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with high penetrance","Characterised by mild, stable, non-progressive fasting hyperglycaemia that is usually present from birth and often discovered incidentally","Fasting glucose typically sits in a narrow, modestly elevated range and HbA1c remains only slightly above the reference limit","The glucose increment during an oral glucose tolerance test is characteristically small because the glucose sensor is merely reset to a higher threshold","Most individuals do not require pharmacological treatment and are managed by observation rather than glucose-lowering therapy","Microvascular and macrovascular complications are rare, reflecting the mild lifelong glycaemic elevation","Pregnancy is the key exception: management depends on whether the fetus has inherited the mutation, which influences fetal growth"],"pathophysiology":"Glucokinase acts as the glucose sensor of the pancreatic beta cell, phosphorylating glucose and thereby setting the threshold at which insulin secretion is triggered. Heterozygous inactivating GCK mutations raise this set point, so insulin release occurs at a higher-than-normal glucose concentration. The result is a regulated, mildly elevated fasting glucose that the body defends around a new equilibrium rather than a progressive failure of insulin secretion. Because glucose homeostasis is otherwise intact, hyperglycaemia remains stable across life and complications are uncommon.","additionalDifferentials":["Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Early type 2 diabetes mellitus","Other MODY subtypes, particularly HNF1A-MODY","Gestational diabetes when first detected in pregnancy","Impaired fasting glucose of multifactorial origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting plasma glucose and HbA1c demonstrating mild, stable elevation","Oral glucose tolerance test showing a small glucose increment","Molecular genetic testing of GCK to confirm the diagnosis","Negative pancreatic autoantibodies to help exclude type 1 diabetes","Family glucose screening across generations to demonstrate the dominant pattern"],"redFlags":["Rapidly worsening hyperglycaemia or ketosis, which is inconsistent with GCK-MODY and suggests an alternative diagnosis","Marked postprandial glucose excursions pointing away from the stable phenotype","Symptoms of osmotic diuresis or weight loss indicating another cause of diabetes","Emergence of significant complications, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent, with lifelong mild stable hyperglycaemia that rarely leads to diabetes-related complications and generally does not require pharmacological treatment.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying the condition as type 1 or type 2 diabetes and starting unnecessary insulin or oral agents","Interpreting a persistently mild HbA1c as poorly controlled diabetes requiring escalation","Overlooking the autosomal dominant family history that points to a monogenic cause","Failing to consider fetal genotype when planning management in pregnancy"],"management":["Manage most non-pregnant patients with lifestyle measures alone, as no glucose-lowering drug is required for this reset glucose set-point","Withhold and, where started, withdraw unnecessary pharmacotherapy and intensive monitoring after GCK-MODY is genetically confirmed","In pregnancy, base treatment on foetal genotype, offering insulin for an unaffected macrosomic foetus and avoiding intensive treatment when the foetus is affected","Perform serial foetal growth ultrasound in affected pregnancies","Reassure that clinically significant diabetic complications are rare and tight glycaemic targets are unnecessary","Offer genetic counselling and cross-generational family screening for autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"mody_type_3":{"name":"Maturity-Onset Diabetes of the Young Type 3 (HNF1A-MODY)","system":"Endocrine / Monogenic diabetes","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HNF1A-MODY","MODY3","HNF1-alpha diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrinology and clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in HNF1A and is the most common form of MODY in many populations; inheritance is autosomal dominant with high, age-dependent penetrance.","Presents typically in adolescence or young adulthood with progressive hyperglycaemia and a striking sensitivity to low-dose sulfonylureas, which are the preferred first-line therapy.","A characteristic low renal threshold for glucose causes glycosuria at near-normal or only mildly elevated blood glucose, an important diagnostic clue.","The oral glucose tolerance test often shows a large glucose increment, contrasting with the small increment of GCK-MODY.","Highly sensitive C-reactive protein tends to be low in HNF1A-MODY, a feature that can help discriminate it from type 2 diabetes.","Diabetes is progressive: beta-cell function declines over years and some patients ultimately need insulin despite an initial sulfonylurea response.","Microvascular and macrovascular complications occur at rates comparable to other diabetes, so good glycaemic control remains important.","Consider the diagnosis in young, lean, autoantibody-negative, C-peptide-positive patients with a strong multigenerational family history of diabetes."],"pathophysiology":"MODY3 is caused by heterozygous inactivating mutations in HNF1A, encoding hepatocyte nuclear factor 1-alpha, a transcription factor that regulates the expression of numerous genes essential for beta-cell development and function, including those controlling glucose transport, mitochondrial metabolism and insulin gene expression. Haploinsufficiency, sometimes compounded by a dominant-negative effect, leads to progressive failure of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion; beta-cell function is often near-normal in childhood but deteriorates through adolescence and adult life, producing worsening hyperglycaemia. HNF1A is also expressed in the renal proximal tubule where it drives expression of the sodium-glucose co-transporter, so mutation lowers the renal threshold for glucose and produces glycosuria disproportionate to the blood glucose. The transcription factor additionally influences hepatic synthesis of C-reactive protein and certain lipids, accounting for the characteristically low high-sensitivity CRP and altered lipid pattern. Residual beta cells retain a functional KATP-channel pathway, which explains their pronounced responsiveness to sulfonylureas that close this channel and augment insulin release, often at doses far lower than used in type 2 diabetes.","additionalDifferentials":["HNF4A-MODY (MODY1), which is closely similar but has a normal renal glucose threshold and neonatal macrosomia","Type 1 diabetes mellitus (autoantibody positive, low C-peptide)","Young-onset type 2 diabetes mellitus with obesity and insulin resistance","Glucokinase-MODY (MODY2) with mild stable fasting hyperglycaemia and small OGTT increment","Renal glycosuria from SGLT2 (SLC5A2) mutations when glycosuria is the dominant finding","Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of HNF1A (sequencing and dosage), usually within a MODY gene panel","Pancreatic islet autoantibodies (GAD, IA-2, ZnT8) to exclude type 1 diabetes","Fasting and stimulated C-peptide to confirm preserved endogenous insulin secretion","Urinalysis for glycosuria relative to concurrent blood glucose","High-sensitivity C-reactive protein, which is characteristically low","Oral glucose tolerance test to demonstrate a large glucose increment","Three-generation family pedigree documenting autosomal dominant diabetes"],"redFlags":["Progressive loss of glycaemic control indicating declining beta-cell reserve and possible need for insulin","Emergence of retinopathy, nephropathy or neuropathy, since complication risk is substantial","Recurrent hypoglycaemia after starting sulfonylurea, reflecting the exquisite drug sensitivity and need for careful dose titration","Diabetic ketoacidosis, which should prompt reassessment for type 1 diabetes","Pregnancy, requiring specialist review of therapy and glycaemic targets"],"prognosis":"The diabetes is progressive, but genetic diagnosis allows a shift from insulin to low-dose sulfonylurea in many patients with durable benefit for years; beta-cell function nonetheless declines and some ultimately need insulin. Long-term outcome depends on glycaemic control because complication risk is comparable to other diabetes.","pitfalls":["Labelling the patient as type 1 diabetes and using unnecessary insulin when sulfonylurea would achieve control","Starting a standard rather than a low sulfonylurea approach and precipitating hypoglycaemia due to marked drug sensitivity","Misclassifying as type 2 diabetes and missing the opportunity for targeted therapy and family testing","Overlooking glycosuria out of proportion to blood glucose as a diagnostic pointer","Assuming benign non-progressive disease and neglecting complication screening"],"management":["Use low-dose sulfonylureas as first-line therapy, exploiting the pronounced sulfonylurea sensitivity of HNF1A-MODY and often allowing insulin to be stopped","Start at a cautious dose to avoid hypoglycaemia given the heightened responsiveness","Progress to additional agents or insulin as beta-cell function declines over the years","Maintain good glycaemic control and screen for microvascular and macrovascular complications, which do occur","Manage pregnancy with specialist input and appropriate maternal glucose surveillance","Provide genetic counselling and cascade testing of relatives for autosomal dominant inheritance","Refer to endocrinology and clinical genetics for confirmation and ongoing care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard endocrinology reference texts","International monogenic diabetes clinical guidelines","Clinical genetics reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"mody_type_3_hnf1a":{"name":"MODY type 3 (HNF1A)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HNF1A-MODY","MODY3","hepatocyte nuclear factor 1-alpha MODY","TCF1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by heterozygous mutations in HNF1A with autosomal dominant inheritance and a strong multigenerational history of young-onset diabetes","Typically presents in adolescence or young adulthood with progressive hyperglycaemia rather than the stable elevation seen in GCK-MODY","A hallmark is marked sensitivity to sulfonylureas, which are often the treatment of choice and can allow insulin to be stopped","A low renal threshold for glucose can produce glycosuria at near-normal plasma glucose levels, an early diagnostic clue","Patients are typically lean and pancreatic autoantibody negative, distinguishing them from type 1 diabetes","Microvascular complications can develop over time, so glycaemic control and surveillance remain important","High-sensitivity C-reactive protein tends to be low, which can support the diagnosis alongside genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"HNF1A is a transcription factor essential for normal beta-cell development and function, regulating genes involved in glucose sensing and insulin secretion. Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations cause a progressive decline in insulin secretory capacity, so hyperglycaemia worsens over time in contrast to the stable defect of GCK-MODY. The transcription factor also governs renal glucose reabsorption, explaining the characteristically low renal threshold. The residual beta-cell KATP channel responsiveness underlies the pronounced clinical sensitivity to sulfonylureas.","additionalDifferentials":["Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Type 2 diabetes mellitus in a young lean adult","GCK-MODY and other MODY subtypes","HNF4A-MODY, which has an overlapping phenotype","Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting glucose and HbA1c documenting progressive hyperglycaemia","Molecular genetic testing of HNF1A to confirm the diagnosis","Negative pancreatic autoantibodies to exclude type 1 diabetes","Urinalysis or paired glucose testing demonstrating a low renal glucose threshold","High-sensitivity C-reactive protein as a supportive biomarker where available"],"redFlags":["Ketoacidosis or rapid decompensation, which is atypical and warrants reassessment","Progressive rise in glycaemia despite therapy, indicating declining beta-cell reserve","Development of microvascular complications requiring intensified surveillance","Severe hypoglycaemia after starting sulfonylureas because of exaggerated drug sensitivity"],"prognosis":"With correct diagnosis many patients achieve good control on low-dose sulfonylureas for years, but insulin secretion declines progressively and microvascular complications can occur, so long-term monitoring is required.","pitfalls":["Defaulting to insulin when a trial of sulfonylurea would be more appropriate and effective","Misdiagnosing the condition as type 1 diabetes because of young age at onset","Overlooking the family history that signals a dominant monogenic cause","Failing to anticipate exaggerated sulfonylurea sensitivity and consequent hypoglycaemia"],"management":["Treat first-line with low-dose sulfonylureas, harnessing the marked sulfonylurea sensitivity of HNF1A-MODY, which often lets insulin be discontinued","Titrate the sulfonylurea cautiously from a low starting dose to avoid hypoglycaemia","Escalate to additional oral agents or insulin as insulin secretory capacity declines progressively","Sustain glycaemic control and undertake regular surveillance for microvascular and macrovascular complications","Coordinate pregnancy care with specialists, monitoring maternal glucose","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing of relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance","Refer to endocrinology and clinical genetics for genetic confirmation and long-term follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"mody_type_5":{"name":"Maturity-Onset Diabetes of the Young Type 5 (HNF1B-MODY)","system":"Endocrine / Monogenic diabetes","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HNF1B-MODY","MODY5","Renal cysts and diabetes syndrome","RCAD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrinology and clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by heterozygous mutations or, very commonly, whole-gene deletions of HNF1B; a large proportion of cases arise de novo, so absence of a family history does not exclude it.","It is a multisystem disorder rather than isolated diabetes, classically the renal cysts and diabetes (RCAD) syndrome, with kidney disease often preceding or dominating the picture.","Renal features include renal cysts, renal hypoplasia or dysplasia, and progressive non-diabetic chronic kidney disease that can appear from fetal life onward.","Pancreatic involvement extends beyond beta-cell failure to hypoplasia of the pancreatic body and tail, causing exocrine insufficiency with low faecal elastase.","Unlike HNF1A and HNF4A MODY, patients are usually NOT sulfonylurea-sensitive and typically require insulin relatively early.","Associated features include genital tract malformations (for example uterine anomalies), hypomagnesaemia from renal magnesium wasting, hyperuricaemia with early gout, and deranged liver enzymes.","Consider HNF1B testing and dosage analysis in any young patient with diabetes plus unexplained renal cysts, renal dysplasia or a low serum magnesium.","The 17q12 microdeletion encompassing HNF1B may additionally be associated with neurodevelopmental and psychiatric features."],"pathophysiology":"MODY5 is caused by heterozygous inactivating point mutations or, frequently, by heterozygous deletion of the entire HNF1B gene at chromosome 17q12; a high proportion of cases occur de novo. HNF1B encodes hepatocyte nuclear factor 1-beta, a transcription factor critical for the embryonic development and function of the kidney, pancreas, liver, biliary tract and genital tract, which explains the multisystem phenotype. In the developing kidney, HNF1B governs nephrogenesis and tubular differentiation, so haploinsufficiency produces structural anomalies such as cysts, hypoplasia and dysplasia together with tubular dysfunction including renal magnesium wasting and impaired urate handling. In the pancreas it directs both endocrine and exocrine development; its loss causes pancreatic hypoplasia with reduced beta-cell mass and progressive insulin secretory failure alongside exocrine insufficiency. Because the defect reflects reduced beta-cell mass and impaired development rather than a reversible functional channel defect, residual insulin secretion is limited and the pronounced sulfonylurea sensitivity typical of HNF1A and HNF4A MODY is generally absent, so patients tend to need insulin. Additional expression in liver and genital tract accounts for hepatic enzyme derangement and Müllerian or other genital malformations.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease and other inherited cystic kidney diseases","HNF1A-MODY and HNF4A-MODY, which lack the renal structural and electrolyte features and are sulfonylurea-sensitive","Type 1 diabetes mellitus with coincidental renal disease","Type 2 diabetes mellitus with diabetic nephropathy (here nephropathy is non-diabetic and often precedes diabetes)","Other syndromic causes of renal dysplasia with diabetes","Congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract from other genetic causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of HNF1B including dosage/deletion analysis (for example MLPA) to detect whole-gene deletions","Renal imaging (ultrasound or MRI) for cysts, hypoplasia or dysplasia","Serum magnesium and fractional excretion of magnesium for renal wasting","Serum urate and renal function with estimated GFR to stage kidney disease","Faecal elastase and liver enzymes to assess exocrine pancreatic and hepatic involvement","Pelvic imaging for uterine and genital tract anomalies where relevant","Pancreatic autoantibodies and C-peptide to exclude autoimmune diabetes"],"redFlags":["Progressive chronic kidney disease advancing toward established renal failure and need for renal replacement","Antenatally detected hyperechogenic or cystic dysplastic kidneys, prompting evaluation for HNF1B","Symptomatic hypomagnesaemia or early-onset gout from renal tubular dysfunction","Diabetes that fails to respond to oral agents and requires early insulin","Neurodevelopmental or psychiatric features in patients with the 17q12 microdeletion"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is largely determined by the renal phenotype, which can progress to chronic kidney disease and end-stage renal failure independently of glycaemic control; diabetes usually requires insulin, and the multisystem nature necessitates lifelong multidisciplinary follow-up. Outcomes vary widely depending on the severity of renal and pancreatic involvement.","pitfalls":["Relying on sequencing alone and missing the common whole-gene deletion, which requires dosage analysis to detect","Excluding the diagnosis because there is no family history, when many cases are de novo","Expecting a sulfonylurea response as in HNF1A/HNF4A MODY and delaying necessary insulin","Attributing the renal disease to diabetic nephropathy when it is a primary developmental kidney disorder often predating diabetes","Overlooking hypomagnesaemia, hyperuricaemia, genital tract anomalies or exocrine insufficiency that point to the multisystem syndrome"],"management":["Treat the diabetes with insulin relatively early, as patients are typically not sulfonylurea-sensitive unlike other MODY subtypes","Manage the renal phenotype (cysts, hypoplasia or dysplasia and progressive non-diabetic chronic kidney disease) with nephrology follow-up and planning for renal replacement where it progresses","Replace magnesium for renal magnesium wasting and hypomagnesaemia","Treat hyperuricaemia and early gout with urate-lowering therapy and anti-inflammatory agents for acute attacks","Give pancreatic enzyme replacement for exocrine insufficiency indicated by low faecal elastase","Monitor liver enzymes and screen for uterine and genital tract anomalies","Provide genetic counselling noting frequent de novo mutations and whole-gene deletions, with lifelong multidisciplinary follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard endocrinology reference texts","International monogenic diabetes clinical guidelines","Clinical genetics reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"moebius_syndrome":{"name":"Moebius syndrome","system":"Genetics / Craniofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mobius syndrome","Moebius sequence","congenital facial diplegia","sixth and seventh nerve palsy syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by congenital, usually bilateral, palsy of the facial (seventh) and abducens (sixth) cranial nerves, producing a characteristic mask-like face and impaired eye abduction","Infants present with poor feeding, incomplete eye closure, drooling, and a lack of facial expression, often noted as failure to smile","Because the eyes cannot abduct, affected individuals turn the head to look sideways rather than moving the eyes","Other lower cranial nerves may be involved, causing swallowing and tongue difficulties and increasing aspiration risk","Associated limb anomalies such as talipes and reduction defects, and chest wall defects overlapping with Poland anomaly, are recognised","Cognition is normal in most individuals, though the flat facial affect can lead observers to underestimate ability","Most cases are sporadic, and a vascular disruption sequence affecting the brainstem is a favoured mechanism"],"pathophysiology":"Moebius syndrome is best understood as a disruption sequence affecting the developing brainstem, particularly the nuclei of the sixth and seventh cranial nerves in the pons. A leading hypothesis is transient interruption of blood flow (ischaemia) to the brainstem during a critical period of gestation, which may damage the cranial nerve nuclei and nearby structures; environmental exposures and uterine events causing vascular compromise have been implicated in some cases. The resulting loss of facial and abducens nerve function produces facial diplegia and impaired horizontal eye movement, and involvement of adjacent regions accounts for the additional cranial nerve, limb, and orofacial findings. Most cases are sporadic, and the associated limb defects are thought to share the vascular disruption mechanism.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital myasthenic syndromes","Congenital myotonic dystrophy","Congenital muscular dystrophies and myopathies","Hereditary congenital facial paresis","Birth-related facial nerve injury","Poland anomaly with facial involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cranial magnetic resonance imaging to assess brainstem and cranial nerve nuclei","Ophthalmological assessment of eye movements and corneal protection","Feeding and swallowing evaluation with aspiration screening","Audiological assessment","Neuromuscular investigation where a myopathic or neuromuscular differential is considered","Genetic evaluation to exclude specific mimicking disorders"],"redFlags":["Aspiration and recurrent respiratory infection from bulbar involvement","Feeding failure with poor weight gain in infancy","Exposure keratopathy from incomplete eye closure threatening the cornea","Airway compromise where multiple lower cranial nerves are affected","Obstructive breathing or apnoea requiring monitoring"],"prognosis":"The cranial nerve deficits are static and non-progressive; with feeding support, corneal protection, and later facial reanimation surgery, many individuals do well and have normal intellect, though social and communication challenges from facial immobility persist.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the expressionless face for intellectual disability or autism","Overlooking corneal exposure and risking sight-threatening keratopathy","Underestimating aspiration risk from lower cranial nerve involvement","Failing to recognise associated limb and chest wall anomalies","Attributing feeding difficulty solely to prematurity or reflux"],"management":["Prevent exposure keratopathy from incomplete eye closure with regular ocular lubricants and eyelid surgery where the cornea is at risk","Ensure safe feeding and protect the airway with feeding support, swallowing assessment and gastrostomy where aspiration risk is high","Correct strabismus surgically to improve ocular alignment given the impaired abduction","Offer facial reanimation surgery, such as gracilis free-muscle transfer, to restore a smile and oral competence","Support communication and feeding with speech and language therapy and craniofacial or orthodontic input","Address associated limb and chest-wall anomalies such as talipes and reduction defects with orthopaedic care","Provide multidisciplinary follow-up with audiology, developmental support and genetic counselling to exclude mimicking disorders"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Gorlin's Syndromes of the Head and Neck"],"crossLinks":[]},"moesin_deficiency":{"name":"Moesin (MSN) deficiency","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["moesin deficiency","MSN deficiency","X-linked moesin-associated immunodeficiency","MAID"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked combined immunodeficiency of males caused by mutations in MSN, which encodes moesin, a protein linking the plasma membrane to the actin cytoskeleton","The characteristic laboratory picture is profound lymphopenia affecting T cells, B cells, and natural killer cells","Presents with recurrent bacterial and viral infections, including varicella-zoster reactivation and other herpesvirus disease","Neutropenia may accompany the lymphopenia, broadening the infective susceptibility","Despite low lymphocyte numbers, some antibody production is retained, and the clinical severity is variable","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating the combined lymphopenia and impaired lymphocyte migration together with MSN sequencing","Management uses immunoglobulin replacement and antimicrobial prophylaxis, with some patients improving over time and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation reserved for severe disease"],"pathophysiology":"Moesin belongs to the ezrin-radixin-moesin family of proteins that tether the cortical actin cytoskeleton to the cell membrane and thereby govern cell shape, membrane dynamics, and movement. In lymphocytes this linkage is required for the cytoskeletal rearrangements underlying migration through tissues, adhesion, and the formation of the immunological synapse during activation. Loss of moesin impairs these processes, so lymphocytes migrate and proliferate poorly and fail to survive in normal numbers, producing a combined lymphopenia of T, B, and natural killer cells. The defective leucocyte trafficking and activation leave the host prone to recurrent bacterial and viral infections, with herpesvirus reactivation reflecting the weakened cellular immunity.","additionalDifferentials":["Typical severe combined immunodeficiency of other genetic causes","Other cytoskeletal immune defects such as DOCK2, DOCK8, and coronin-1A deficiency","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","Idiopathic CD4 lymphopenia","Leucocyte adhesion deficiency","Acquired lymphopenia including HIV"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lymphocyte subset enumeration showing combined T, B, and natural killer cell lymphopenia","Full blood count to detect associated neutropenia","T-cell proliferation and lymphocyte migration or adhesion assays","Quantitative immunoglobulins and specific antibody responses","Genetic sequencing of MSN","Viral studies including varicella-zoster and other herpesviruses","Newborn screening T-cell receptor excision circle results where available"],"redFlags":["Profound combined lymphopenia in a male infant or child","Severe or reactivated herpesvirus infection such as varicella-zoster","Recurrent bacterial infection with accompanying neutropenia","Failure to thrive with early recurrent infection","A maternal family history suggesting X-linked inheritance"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is variable; some patients stabilise or improve on supportive treatment with immunoglobulin replacement and prophylaxis, while those with severe, persistent immunodeficiency may require haematopoietic stem cell transplantation.","pitfalls":["Assuming profound lymphopenia always denotes classic severe combined immunodeficiency","Overlooking associated neutropenia as a contributor to infection","Giving live vaccines before the immune defect is excluded","Failing to consider X-linked inheritance in an affected male","Underestimating the variability in severity between patients"],"management":["Provide immunoglobulin replacement therapy to compensate for impaired antibody responses","Give antibacterial prophylaxis and antiviral prophylaxis or treatment for herpesviruses such as varicella-zoster with aciclovir-class agents","Treat intercurrent bacterial and viral infections promptly and aggressively","Consider haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the definitive option in severe, persistent combined immunodeficiency","Avoid live vaccines and use irradiated, leucodepleted blood products given the T-cell defect","Monitor lymphocyte subsets, neutrophil counts and for herpesvirus reactivation","Offer genetic counselling for X-linked inheritance and test at-risk male relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"mog_antibody_disease":{"name":"MOG antibody-associated disease (MOGAD)","system":"Nervous system / Neuroimmunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MOGAD","myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein antibody disease","anti-MOG associated demyelination","MOG-IgG associated disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distinct demyelinating disorder recognised as separate from multiple sclerosis and from aquaporin-4-positive neuromyelitis optica","Common phenotypes include optic neuritis (often bilateral with marked disc swelling), transverse myelitis, and, in children particularly, acute disseminated encephalomyelitis (ADEM)","Antibodies target myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein on the outer myelin surface, and patients are characteristically aquaporin-4 antibody negative","Attacks are frequently steroid-responsive but relapse on steroid withdrawal, so tapering must be cautious","Optic neuritis tends to show longitudinally extensive anterior optic nerve involvement with perineural enhancement","Antibody testing should use a live cell-based assay, and low-positive titres require cautious interpretation","Acute attacks are treated with high-dose corticosteroids, with intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange for severe or refractory episodes, and relapsing disease may need longer-term immunotherapy"],"pathophysiology":"The disorder is associated with immunoglobulin G antibodies against myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein (MOG), a protein expressed on the outermost lamellae of central nervous system myelin and the oligodendrocyte surface. Antibody binding, together with complement and cellular effector mechanisms, produces inflammatory demyelination that preferentially affects the optic nerves, spinal cord, and, in children, the cerebral white matter and deep grey structures. In contrast to aquaporin-4 disease, astrocytes are relatively spared and demyelination can be more reversible, which correlates with the frequently good recovery from individual attacks.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple sclerosis","Aquaporin-4-positive neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder","Idiopathic or infective optic neuritis","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis of other cause","Neurosarcoidosis","Infective or compressive myelopathy","Susac syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum MOG-IgG by a live cell-based assay with attention to titre","Aquaporin-4 antibody testing to distinguish neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder","MRI of the brain, orbits, and spinal cord to characterise lesions and enhancement","Optical coherence tomography and visual evoked potentials for optic nerve involvement","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis, which shows oligoclonal bands less often than in multiple sclerosis","Screening for infective mimics before immunosuppression"],"redFlags":["Severe or bilateral visual loss from optic neuritis","Longitudinally extensive myelitis with sphincter or respiratory compromise","Encephalopathy or seizures in an ADEM-like presentation","Frequent relapses on steroid tapering indicating need for maintenance therapy"],"prognosis":"Recovery from individual attacks is often good, but a relapsing course occurs in a substantial minority and repeated attacks can accumulate visual or spinal disability. Prognosis is generally more favourable than aquaporin-4 disease when relapses are prevented.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying the disorder as multiple sclerosis and starting inappropriate disease-modifying drugs","Over-interpreting low-positive antibody titres, especially with an incompatible phenotype","Tapering corticosteroids too quickly and precipitating relapse","Assuming aquaporin-4 disease without confirming antibody status"],"management":["Treat acute attacks with high-dose intravenous corticosteroids, escalating to plasma exchange or intravenous immunoglobulin for severe or steroid-refractory episodes","Taper corticosteroids slowly, since abrupt withdrawal precipitates relapse","Prevent relapses in relapsing disease with maintenance immunotherapy such as intravenous immunoglobulin, rituximab, mycophenolate mofetil or azathioprine","Avoid multiple sclerosis disease-modifying drugs, which are ineffective or harmful in MOGAD","Monitor with serial MOG-IgG titres, MRI and optical coherence tomography or visual evoked potentials for optic nerve involvement","Exclude infective mimics before starting immunosuppression and give appropriate infection prophylaxis","Refer to neuroimmunology with ophthalmology input for optic neuritis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Continuum: Multiple Sclerosis and Related Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"mogs_cdg":{"name":"MOGS-CDG (congenital disorder of glycosylation type IIb)","system":"Metabolic / Glycosylation & peroxisomal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CDG-IIb","Glucosidase I deficiency","GCS1 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["MOGS encodes endoplasmic reticulum mannosyl-oligosaccharide glucosidase (glucosidase I), which removes the outermost terminal alpha-1,2-linked glucose from the newly transferred N-glycan, the first trimming step of glycan processing.","It is therefore classified as a type II CDG (a processing or trimming defect) rather than an assembly defect.","A crucial diagnostic trap is that serum transferrin isoelectric focusing is typically normal, because the defect does not alter the sialylation-dependent charge that this test detects.","Diagnosis instead rests on urine oligosaccharide analysis, which shows a characteristic glucose-rich tetrasaccharide derived from the unprocessed precursor.","The clinical picture is a severe neonatal multisystem disorder with dysmorphism, generalised hypotonia, seizures, feeding difficulty and hepatomegaly.","Hypogammaglobulinaemia is characteristic, yet a paradoxical relative resistance to certain enveloped viruses has been described because their envelope glycoproteins depend on glucosylated N-glycans.","Multisystem neurological and hepatic involvement usually dominates the clinical course despite the immunological abnormality."],"pathophysiology":"MOGS (glucosidase I) catalyses the first step in processing of the N-linked oligosaccharide after it is transferred en bloc onto nascent glycoproteins in the endoplasmic reticulum, cleaving the terminal alpha-1,2-glucose from the triglucosylated precursor Glc3Man9GlcNAc2. This trimming is required for entry into the calnexin and calreticulin quality-control cycle and for orderly glycoprotein folding and onward processing. Loss of function leaves glycoproteins bearing unprocessed, still-glucosylated glycans, disrupts protein folding and quality control, and causes accumulation and urinary excretion of glucose-rich oligosaccharides. Because the sialylation of transferrin is unaffected, the standard transferrin screening test remains normal, which is the principal reason the disorder is missed. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Other type II CDGs (for example MGAT2, B4GALT1, SLC35 transporter and COG-complex defects)","Type I CDGs presenting with neonatal hypotonia and seizures","Primary hypogammaglobulinaemia or combined immunodeficiency","Lysosomal oligosaccharidoses (overlapping urine oligosaccharide findings)","Peroxisomal disorders with neonatal hypotonia and hepatopathy","Other syndromic neonatal encephalopathies with dysmorphism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine oligosaccharide analysis for the characteristic glucose-rich tetrasaccharide (key confirmatory test)","Serum transferrin isoelectric focusing (characteristically normal, which supports rather than excludes the diagnosis)","MOGS molecular genetic testing via a CDG panel or exome sequencing","Immunoglobulin levels and functional immune assessment","Liver function tests and hepatic imaging","Glucosidase I enzyme activity assay in a specialist laboratory","Electroencephalography and neuroimaging"],"redFlags":["Neonatal seizures with encephalopathy and feeding failure","Progressive hepatic dysfunction","Serious or recurrent bacterial infection in the setting of hypogammaglobulinaemia","Apnoea or respiratory compromise in a hypotonic neonate"],"prognosis":"Reported cases have generally followed a severe course with death in early infancy, although the full phenotypic range is still being defined as more affected individuals are identified.","pitfalls":["Excluding a CDG because transferrin isoelectric focusing is normal, the classic way MOGS-CDG is overlooked","Failing to request urine oligosaccharide analysis in a hypotonic dysmorphic neonate","Attributing recurrent infection solely to a primary immunodeficiency without considering a glycosylation disorder","Misinterpreting the urinary oligosaccharide pattern as a lysosomal storage disorder"],"management":["Recognise there is no disease-specific therapy and provide supportive, multidisciplinary care for this severe neonatal multisystem disorder","Control seizures with antiepileptic drugs","Support feeding and nutrition for feeding difficulty and failure to thrive, using tube feeding where needed","Address the generalised hypotonia with physiotherapy and developmental input","Give immunoglobulin replacement and infection prevention for the characteristic hypogammaglobulinaemia","Monitor hepatic involvement and hepatomegaly with liver function tests and imaging","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive MOGS inheritance with family testing","Involve palliative care given the frequently life-limiting course, alongside metabolic specialist coordination"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited metabolic disease textbooks","Congenital disorders of glycosylation review literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"mohr_tranebjaerg_syndrome":{"name":"Mohr-Tranebjaerg syndrome","system":"Multisystem genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Deafness-dystonia-optic neuronopathy syndrome","DDON syndrome","TIMM8A","DFN-1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked recessive neurodegenerative syndrome in which affected males develop early progressive sensorineural hearing loss followed by dystonia, visual failure from optic atrophy and later cognitive decline.","Hearing loss is usually the first manifestation, frequently the only sign in infancy, and is of an auditory-neuropathy type in which otoacoustic emissions may be preserved while auditory brainstem responses are abnormal.","The movement disorder (dystonia, ataxia or parkinsonism) typically emerges in later childhood or adolescence and is slowly progressive.","Progressive visual loss from optic atrophy generally begins in the teenage years or early adulthood.","Neuropsychiatric change and dementia often appear in adulthood, sometimes with behavioural disturbance and paranoia.","Female carriers are usually asymptomatic but may show mild focal dystonia or subtle hearing changes owing to skewed X-inactivation.","Caused by loss-of-function variants in TIMM8A; a contiguous deletion involving TIMM8A and the neighbouring BTK gene produces a combined phenotype with agammaglobulinaemia.","Cochlear implantation can aid auditory rehabilitation given the neural pattern of the deafness."],"pathophysiology":"TIMM8A on Xq22 encodes deafness-dystonia protein 1 (DDP1, also called TIM8a), a small mitochondrial intermembrane-space protein that partners with TIM13 to form the TIM8-TIM13 chaperone complex. This complex escorts hydrophobic inner-membrane carrier precursors across the intermembrane space to the TIM22 insertion machinery. Loss-of-function variants impair mitochondrial protein import, producing a progressive neurodegenerative disorder that affects the auditory nerve, basal ganglia, optic pathways and cortex; it is therefore regarded as a disease of mitochondrial protein import rather than a primary defect of oxidative phosphorylation. Because TIMM8A lies immediately adjacent to BTK, larger contiguous deletions additionally cause X-linked agammaglobulinaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Mitochondrial optic neuropathies (Leber hereditary optic neuropathy; dominant optic atrophy from OPA1)","Wolfram syndrome (optic atrophy with diabetes mellitus and deafness)","Friedreich ataxia and other hereditary ataxias with sensory involvement","X-linked dystonia-parkinsonism (Lubag)","Auditory neuropathy spectrum disorder from OTOF or other causes","Mitochondrial encephalomyopathies (e.g., MELAS, Leigh syndrome)","Contiguous gene deletion syndrome with agammaglobulinaemia (BTK involvement)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of TIMM8A including sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis, with assessment for a contiguous BTK deletion","Audiological assessment with otoacoustic emissions and auditory brainstem responses to confirm the auditory-neuropathy pattern","Ophthalmological evaluation with optical coherence tomography and visual evoked potentials","Brain MRI, principally to exclude alternative structural or metabolic causes","Immunoglobulin quantification if a contiguous deletion is suspected","Serial neuropsychological assessment to track cognition","Family segregation studies and carrier testing of at-risk female relatives"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual loss superimposed on established childhood deafness, indicating neurodegeneration rather than isolated hearing impairment","Recurrent serious infections in a deaf boy, suggesting a contiguous BTK deletion with agammaglobulinaemia","Emerging swallowing difficulty or airway compromise from advancing dystonia","Rapid behavioural or cognitive deterioration"],"prognosis":"The disorder is progressive and disabling: hearing loss appears in childhood and dystonia, visual failure and dementia accumulate over subsequent decades, with reduced life expectancy in advanced disease. Supportive care and auditory rehabilitation improve function but do not halt neurodegeneration.","pitfalls":["Labelling the deafness as ordinary sensorineural loss and missing the later neurological trajectory","Overlooking the auditory-neuropathy pattern, leading to unrealistic expectations of hearing-aid benefit","Failing to test for a contiguous BTK deletion in a boy with deafness and recurrent infections","Assuming female carriers are always unaffected"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so care centres on auditory rehabilitation for the progressive auditory-neuropathy hearing loss, with early communication support and cochlear implantation where appropriate rather than conventional aids alone","Symptomatic control of dystonia with anticholinergics, benzodiazepines or botulinum toxin for focal dystonia, and consideration of deep brain stimulation in selected disabling cases","Low-vision rehabilitation and visual aids for progressive optic atrophy","Neuropsychiatric and behavioural support with dementia care as cognitive decline emerges in adulthood","Screen for a contiguous BTK deletion, and if X-linked agammaglobulinaemia coexists provide immunoglobulin replacement and infection prophylaxis","Coordinated multidisciplinary input from neurology, audiovestibular medicine, ophthalmology, psychiatry, physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language therapy","Genetic counselling for X-linked recessive inheritance, with carrier testing of female relatives and reproductive and prenatal options"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics reference texts","Mitochondrial disease reference texts","Clinical genetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"molluscum_contagiosum":{"name":"Molluscum Contagiosum","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["molluscum","MC","water warts"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characteristic lesions are small, firm, dome-shaped papules with a central dimple (umbilication), often pearly or skin-coloured","Spread occurs by direct skin contact, autoinoculation from scratching, and shared items such as towels; in adults, involvement of the genital area can indicate sexual transmission","It is common and typically self-limiting in immunocompetent children, so reassurance and expectant management are often appropriate","A surrounding patch of eczema (molluscum dermatitis) or an inflamed, tender lesion (the beginning-of-the-end sign) often heralds resolution rather than bacterial infection","Extensive, giant, or facial lesions in an adult should prompt consideration of underlying immunosuppression, including HIV","Physical treatments (such as cryotherapy or curettage) and topical agents can be offered when lesions are troublesome, but must be weighed against discomfort and scarring, especially in young children","Advise measures to limit spread: avoid sharing towels, discourage scratching and shaving over lesions, and cover lesions where practical","Reassure that most cases clear spontaneously over months, though this can take a year or more"],"pathophysiology":"Molluscum contagiosum is caused by a poxvirus (molluscum contagiosum virus) that replicates within epidermal keratinocytes. The virus induces localised proliferation of the epidermis and produces large intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies (Henderson-Paterson bodies) packed with viral particles, which form the soft core expressed from the umbilicated centre. The virus encodes proteins that dampen the local host immune response, allowing lesions to persist; eventual immune recognition produces the inflammatory reaction that precedes clearance. Transmission is by direct contact, fomites, and autoinoculation, and impaired cell-mediated immunity permits widespread or atypical disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Common or flat warts","Milia","Closed comedones or early acne papules","Basal cell carcinoma (for a solitary lesion in an adult)","Disseminated cutaneous cryptococcosis or other opportunistic infections in the immunocompromised","Ectopic sebaceous glands or adnexal tumours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Usually a clinical diagnosis supported by dermoscopy showing central white-yellow structures with surrounding vessels","Consideration of HIV or other immunodeficiency testing when disease is extensive, facial, or atypical in an adult","Screening for other sexually transmitted infections when genital lesions suggest sexual transmission","Biopsy or expression of the core for microscopy in diagnostically uncertain cases"],"redFlags":["Numerous, giant, or facial lesions in an adult suggesting significant immunosuppression such as advanced HIV","A solitary firm nodule in an adult that could represent basal cell carcinoma or an opportunistic infection mimic","Marked periocular involvement risking conjunctivitis or keratitis"],"prognosis":"In immunocompetent individuals the condition is benign and self-limiting, usually resolving spontaneously over several months to a couple of years without scarring; recovery is slower and lesions more extensive when cell-mediated immunity is impaired.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the inflamed, resolving lesion for bacterial infection and treating with unnecessary antibiotics","Subjecting young children to painful, scarring procedures for a self-limiting condition","Missing underlying immunosuppression when an adult presents with widespread or atypical lesions","Overlooking sexual transmission and associated infections in adults with genital involvement"],"management":["Reassurance and expectant management for immunocompetent children, since lesions are benign and self-limiting","Offer physical treatments such as cryotherapy or curettage, or topical agents such as potassium hydroxide, cantharidin or a topical retinoid, when lesions are troublesome, weighing discomfort and scarring especially in young children","Manage surrounding molluscum dermatitis with emollients and a topical corticosteroid, and treat any secondary bacterial infection","Advise on reducing spread and autoinoculation by avoiding scratching and shared towels and by covering lesions","Investigate for underlying immunosuppression including HIV when disease is extensive, facial or atypical in an adult, and optimise immune reconstitution with antiretroviral therapy where relevant","Screen for other sexually transmitted infections and address partners when genital lesions suggest sexual transmission"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"molybdenum_cofactor_deficiency":{"name":"Molybdenum cofactor deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MoCD","MOCS1 deficiency","MOCS2 deficiency","GPHN gephyrin deficiency","combined deficiency of sulphite oxidase, xanthine oxidase and aldehyde oxidase"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder of molybdenum cofactor biosynthesis, most commonly due to MOCS1 variants, with MOCS2 and GPHN also implicated","The molybdenum cofactor is required by sulphite oxidase, xanthine oxidase/dehydrogenase and aldehyde oxidase, so all three enzyme activities are lost together","Loss of sulphite oxidase activity drives the severe neurological phenotype through accumulation of neurotoxic sulphite","Presents in the neonatal period with intractable seizures, feeding difficulties, marked hypotonia progressing to spasticity and rapid encephalopathy, often mimicking hypoxic-ischaemic injury","Biochemical clues include raised urinary sulphite and S-sulphocysteine with low plasma uric acid and low urinary urate reflecting xanthine oxidase deficiency","Lens dislocation (ectopia lentis) develops over time, as in isolated sulphite oxidase deficiency","Type A disease (MOCS1) can respond to substrate replacement with cyclic pyranopterin monophosphate if started very early","It is clinically and biochemically indistinguishable from isolated sulphite oxidase deficiency except for the low uric acid"],"pathophysiology":"Molybdenum cofactor is a pterin-based cofactor synthesised through a multistep pathway (involving MOCS1, MOCS2 and gephyrin) that is essential for the activity of the molybdoenzymes sulphite oxidase, xanthine oxidase/dehydrogenase and aldehyde oxidase. When cofactor synthesis fails, all three enzymes are inactive. The most damaging consequence is loss of sulphite oxidase, which normally converts sulphite to sulphate in cysteine catabolism; sulphite accumulates and is directly neurotoxic, while S-sulphocysteine builds up and may act as an excitotoxin. Xanthine oxidase deficiency additionally causes low uric acid and elevated hypoxanthine and xanthine. The combined effect is severe, progressive neonatal brain injury with cystic encephalomalacia.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated sulphite oxidase deficiency","Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Neonatal epileptic encephalopathies of other causes","Non-ketotic hyperglycinaemia","Pyridoxine-dependent and other vitamin-responsive epilepsies","Neonatal-onset organic acidaemias or urea cycle disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fresh urine sulphite testing (dipstick) and quantitation of urinary S-sulphocysteine","Plasma uric acid, which is characteristically low (distinguishing it from isolated sulphite oxidase deficiency)","Plasma total homocysteine, which tends to be low","Neuroimaging showing diffuse injury, cystic change and later cerebral atrophy","Molecular genetic testing of MOCS1, MOCS2 and GPHN","Ophthalmological assessment for lens dislocation"],"redFlags":["Intractable neonatal seizures with rapidly progressive encephalopathy","Imaging suggesting hypoxic-ischaemic injury without a supporting perinatal history","Low plasma uric acid in an encephalopathic neonate","Progressive spasticity and microcephaly"],"prognosis":"The classic neonatal form is usually devastating, with profound neurodevelopmental impairment and early death. Type A (MOCS1) disease may respond to early substrate replacement therapy, so prognosis can improve markedly when treatment is started before irreversible brain injury; type B and other genetic forms currently lack specific treatment.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the presentation for hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy and not testing for the metabolic cause","Missing the treatable MOCS1 form by delaying genetic and biochemical diagnosis","Relying on delayed urine sulphite testing, as sulphite degrades rapidly in stored samples","Failing to distinguish it from isolated sulphite oxidase deficiency by checking uric acid"],"management":["For type A (MOCS1) disease, substrate replacement with cyclic pyranopterin monophosphate (fosdenopterin) is disease-specific and should be started as early as possible, ideally before irreversible brain injury","Type B and other genetic subtypes have no available substrate therapy and are managed supportively","Dietary reduction of sulphur-containing amino acids as an adjunct to limit accumulation of neurotoxic sulphite and S-sulphocysteine","Seizure control with antiepileptic drugs, and management of feeding difficulty with nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding alongside management of hypotonia and spasticity","Biochemical monitoring of urinary sulphite and S-sulphocysteine, plasma urate and homocysteine to track treatment response, with ophthalmological review for ectopia lentis","Multidisciplinary input from metabolic medicine, neurology, dietetics and, where the phenotype is severe, palliative care","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance, with family screening and prenatal diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"mondini_dysplasia":{"name":"Mondini dysplasia (cochlear incomplete partition)","system":"Ear / Audiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mondini malformation","incomplete partition type II","IP-II","cochlear incomplete partition","Mondini deformity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital cochleovestibular malformation in which the cochlea has fewer than the normal turns with a deficient interscalar septum at the apex","In the classic incomplete partition type II it is often accompanied by a dilated vestibule and an enlarged vestibular aqueduct","Hearing loss is sensorineural or mixed and may be stable, fluctuating, or progressive","There is an increased risk of recurrent meningitis and cerebrospinal-fluid leak because of an abnormal communication with the subarachnoid space","It is frequently associated with SLC26A4 variants and overlaps clinically with Pendred syndrome","Imaging is essential both to make the diagnosis and to anticipate a perilymph or cerebrospinal-fluid gusher at surgery","Cochlear implantation is often feasible and effective with appropriate surgical planning"],"pathophysiology":"Mondini dysplasia arises from arrested cochlear development during embryogenesis, leaving a cochlea with incomplete coiling and a deficient modiolus and interscalar septum, most typically the incomplete partition type II pattern combined with a dilated vestibule and enlarged vestibular aqueduct. The malformed and often incompletely separated fluid compartments impair normal cochlear mechanics and endolymph homeostasis, producing sensorineural or mixed hearing loss. A deficient bony partition can create an abnormal communication with the cerebrospinal fluid, predisposing to fluid gusher during surgery and to recurrent meningitis, and many cases share the SLC26A4-related mechanism seen in Pendred syndrome and isolated enlarged vestibular aqueduct.","additionalDifferentials":["Enlarged vestibular aqueduct without cochlear partition defect","Incomplete partition types I and III","Common cavity and other cochlear aplasia or hypoplasia","Pendred syndrome","Cochlear nerve deficiency","Acquired sensorineural hearing loss with normal anatomy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution temporal bone imaging to define cochlear turns and the interscalar septum","Magnetic resonance imaging to assess membranous structures and the cochlear nerve","Pure-tone audiometry to characterise the type and progression of loss","SLC26A4 genetic testing and assessment for Pendred features","Thyroid evaluation when Pendred syndrome is suspected","Vestibular testing when balance symptoms are present"],"redFlags":["Recurrent meningitis or cerebrospinal-fluid rhinorrhoea indicating an abnormal fluid communication","Rapid or fluctuating hearing decline","Radiological features predicting a gusher at implantation","Coexisting thyroid or systemic features suggesting a syndrome"],"prognosis":"Hearing outcomes vary with the severity of the malformation, but with careful surgical planning cochlear implantation generally provides good rehabilitation, while the meningitis risk warrants vaccination and prompt treatment of leaks.","pitfalls":["Missing the malformation by not obtaining dedicated temporal bone imaging","Overlooking recurrent meningitis as a marker of an inner-ear fluid communication","Failing to anticipate a gusher during cochlear implantation","Not investigating for coexisting Pendred syndrome"],"management":["Auditory rehabilitation tailored to severity, with hearing aids for milder loss and cochlear implantation for severe-to-profound loss, planning surgery to anticipate and control a perilymph or cerebrospinal-fluid gusher","Pneumococcal and other indicated vaccination to reduce the risk of recurrent meningitis","Prompt surgical repair of any cerebrospinal-fluid leak and urgent antimicrobial treatment of meningitis","Serial pure-tone audiometry to monitor fluctuating or progressive loss, with counselling to avoid head trauma and barotrauma","SLC26A4 genetic testing and thyroid assessment where Pendred syndrome features are present","Multidisciplinary otology and audiovestibular care with speech and language therapy for communication development","Genetic counselling and family screening where an SLC26A4-related mechanism is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology","Sennaroglu Classification of Inner Ear Malformations"],"crossLinks":[]},"mondor_disease":{"name":"Mondor disease of the breast","system":"Breast","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mondor disease","Mondor's disease","superficial thrombophlebitis of the breast","thrombophlebitis of the thoracoepigastric vein"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard breast surgery textbook and benign breast disease guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Mondor disease is superficial thrombophlebitis of the subcutaneous veins of the breast and anterior chest wall, most often the thoracoepigastric or lateral thoracic vein","It typically presents as a sudden, tender, cord-like subcutaneous structure that becomes more visible when the skin is stretched or the arm is raised","A characteristic skin dimple or groove may form along the line of the thrombosed vein, which can raise concern for underlying malignancy","The condition is usually self-limiting and benign, and recognising the palpable cord is key to avoiding unnecessary alarm","Recognised precipitants include local trauma, breast surgery, tight garments, strenuous exertion, and dehydration, although many cases are idiopathic","Because the tethering and dimpling can mimic carcinoma, imaging as part of triple assessment is used to reassure and exclude an underlying cancer","Management is conservative with reassurance, warmth, and simple analgesia, and the cord typically resolves over several weeks","An associated breast lump or atypical features should prompt full assessment, as Mondor disease can occasionally accompany an underlying malignancy"],"pathophysiology":"Mondor disease is a localised thrombophlebitis affecting the superficial veins that drain the breast and adjacent chest wall. Thrombosis of the vein, together with an inflammatory reaction in and around the vessel wall, converts the vein into a firm, tender, palpable cord. As the process organises and the vein retracts, traction on the overlying skin produces the characteristic linear groove or dimple. The trigger is often venous stasis or endothelial injury from trauma, surgery, or compression, in keeping with the classic contributors to thrombosis, though a proportion of cases have no identifiable cause.","additionalDifferentials":["Breast carcinoma with skin tethering","Lymphatic obstruction or lymphangitis","Duct ectasia with skin change","Cellulitis or breast abscess","Traumatic fat necrosis","Superficial thrombophlebitis from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination demonstrating a tender palpable cord and skin groove","Ultrasound to confirm a thrombosed superficial vein and exclude an underlying mass","Mammography in women of appropriate age as part of triple assessment","Core biopsy of any associated discrete lump","Assessment for precipitating factors such as recent trauma or surgery"],"redFlags":["An associated discrete breast lump or suspicious imaging finding","Skin changes beyond a simple linear groove, such as thickening or peau d'orange","Failure of the cord to resolve within the expected timeframe","Recurrent or migratory thrombophlebitis suggesting an underlying systemic or thrombophilic cause"],"prognosis":"Mondor disease is benign and self-limiting, with the thrombosed cord typically resolving spontaneously over a few weeks with conservative measures, and recurrence is uncommon.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the skin dimpling for a sign of carcinoma and alarming the patient without imaging","Failing to exclude an underlying malignancy, which can rarely coexist","Over-treating a self-limiting condition with anticoagulation or surgery","Overlooking an underlying thrombophilia in recurrent or migratory cases","Not addressing modifiable precipitants such as tight garments"],"management":["Conservative supportive care as the condition is benign and self-limiting, with reassurance, simple analgesia or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and warm compresses","Identify and address precipitants such as local trauma, tight garments, strenuous exertion and dehydration","Complete triple assessment with clinical examination, ultrasound and age-appropriate mammography to exclude an underlying breast carcinoma, since tethering and dimpling can mimic malignancy","Advise temporary restriction of strenuous upper-limb activity during the acute phase to aid resolution of the thrombosed cord","Monitor for spontaneous resolution and consider evaluation for an underlying thrombophilia or malignancy if the condition is recurrent or atypical"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard breast surgery reference text on benign breast disease","Guidance on evaluation of breast skin changes"],"crossLinks":[]},"mongolian_spot":{"name":"Mongolian spot (congenital dermal melanocytosis)","system":"Dermatology / Pigmentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["congenital dermal melanocytosis","dermal melanocytosis","blue-grey macule of infancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Congenital blue-grey macule or patch, most often over the lumbosacral area and buttocks, present at or shortly after birth","Very common in infants of Asian, African, Hispanic and other darker-skinned backgrounds","Represents dermal melanocytosis, the blue-grey colour arising from a Tyndall effect of deep dermal melanin","Usually fades spontaneously during early childhood and needs no treatment","Can be mistaken for a bruise, so accurate documentation is important to avoid inappropriate concern about non-accidental injury","Extensive, widespread or persistent lesions, or those combined with vascular birthmarks (phakomatosis pigmentovascularis), may occasionally signal an inborn error such as GM1 gangliosidosis or a mucopolysaccharidosis","Aberrant lesions over unusual sites such as the shoulders or face overlap with naevus of Ito and naevus of Ota"],"pathophysiology":"During embryogenesis melanocytes derived from the neural crest normally migrate to the epidermis, but in dermal melanocytosis some are arrested within the dermis. Light scattering by this deep melanin produces the blue-grey colour, and most lesions involute as the ectopic melanocytes gradually disappear during childhood. Extensive or persistent lesions can rarely accompany lysosomal storage diseases, in which impaired clearance is thought to allow the melanocytosis to persist.","additionalDifferentials":["Bruise or non-accidental injury","Naevus of Ota","Naevus of Ito","Blue naevus","Congenital melanocytic naevus","Phakomatosis pigmentovascularis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on typical appearance and site","Careful documentation and photography for medico-legal and safeguarding purposes","Dermoscopy where the diagnosis is uncertain","Assessment for dysmorphism or organomegaly with metabolic screening if lesions are extensive or persistent","Mapping of the distribution and review for associated vascular malformation"],"redFlags":["Lesions mistaken for bruising that raise safeguarding concerns, requiring careful documentation","Extensive or persistent melanocytosis with dysmorphic features or organomegaly suggesting a lysosomal storage disease","An associated vascular malformation indicating phakomatosis pigmentovascularis","Atypical siting or nodular change prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Typical lumbosacral lesions are benign and usually fade by mid-childhood without treatment; aberrant, extensive or syndrome-associated lesions may persist and occasionally point to underlying systemic disease.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the blue-grey patch for a bruise and misattributing it to non-accidental injury","Investigating or treating typical, self-limiting lesions unnecessarily","Missing an underlying metabolic disorder in extensive, persistent melanocytosis","Overlooking overlap with naevus of Ito or Ota when lesions occur at unusual sites"],"management":["Reassurance and observation, since typical lumbosacral lesions are benign and fade spontaneously during early childhood without treatment","Accurate documentation and photography of site and extent to avoid misinterpretation as a bruise and inappropriate concern about non-accidental injury","Consider laser therapy with a pigment-specific laser only for persistent, cosmetically significant lesions that do not resolve","Assess extensive, widespread or aberrant lesions, or those with an associated vascular birthmark, for an underlying inborn error such as GM1 gangliosidosis or a mucopolysaccharidosis, with metabolic screening and evaluation for dysmorphism or organomegaly","Refer to dermatology and metabolic or genetics services when a syndromic association is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"monilethrix":{"name":"Monilethrix","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["beaded hair","moniliform hair","KRT81","KRT83","KRT86","DSG4"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A hair-shaft disorder in which hairs show regular beading, with elliptical nodes and narrowed internodes, and fracture at the constrictions","Presents with sparse, short, brittle hair and patchy alopecia, often with prominent follicular keratosis (keratosis pilaris), especially on the occiput and nape","Onset is typically in infancy or early childhood after normal lanugo is shed","Most cases are autosomal dominant due to mutations in hair-cortex keratin genes (KRT81, KRT83, KRT86); a recessive form is caused by DSG4 mutations","Diagnosis is confirmed by light microscopy of hair showing the characteristic beaded (moniliform) shafts","Severity varies and some improvement may occur with age, during summer, or in pregnancy","Management is supportive with gentle hair care, emollients/keratolytics for follicular hyperkeratosis, and avoidance of trauma; oral retinoids have been tried in severe cases"],"pathophysiology":"Monilethrix is usually an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in the type II hair-cortex keratin genes KRT81, KRT83 and KRT86; a rarer autosomal recessive form results from mutations in DSG4 encoding desmoglein 4. These genes are essential for normal assembly of the keratin intermediate-filament network and cell adhesion within the hair cortex. The mutations produce periodic weakness along the hair shaft, giving alternating nodes (of normal calibre) and internodes (constricted, medulla-deficient segments). The narrowed internodes are mechanically fragile and fracture readily, so hairs break before reaching any length, producing the clinical picture of short, sparse, brittle hair with follicular hyperkeratosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Pseudomonilethrix (an artefactual beading)","Pili torti","Trichorrhexis nodosa","Netherton syndrome (trichorrhexis invaginata)","Trichothiodystrophy","Keratosis pilaris atrophicans / other follicular keratoses with alopecia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Light microscopy of plucked or cut hairs showing regular beading","Trichoscopy (dermoscopy) demonstrating the regularly bent, beaded shafts","Molecular genetic testing of KRT81, KRT83, KRT86 and DSG4","Scalp examination for follicular keratosis and pattern of alopecia","Family history and pedigree analysis","Skin biopsy of scalp where the diagnosis is uncertain"],"redFlags":["Extensive alopecia causing significant psychosocial distress","Associated features suggesting a broader ectodermal or syndromic disorder","Secondary infection or inflammation of hyperkeratotic follicles","Atypical hair-shaft findings prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Monilethrix is a chronic condition, but it is compatible with normal health and often improves spontaneously with age, in summer months, or during pregnancy. Complete resolution is uncommon and hair fragility tends to persist, though severity fluctuates.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the alopecia for alopecia areata or tinea and missing the diagnostic hair-shaft beading","Failing to examine hairs microscopically, which is the key diagnostic step","Confusing genuine monilethrix with artefactual pseudomonilethrix","Aggressive cosmetic or chemical hair treatment that worsens breakage"],"management":["Gentle hair care to minimise mechanical fracture at the constricted internodes, avoiding traction, vigorous brushing, heat styling and chemical treatments","A trial of topical minoxidil, which can improve hair growth and length in some patients","Topical keratolytics such as urea or salicylic acid, emollients and topical retinoids for the associated follicular keratosis","Reassurance that the disorder is compatible with normal health and often improves with age, during summer months or in pregnancy","Trichoscopy and light microscopy of hair shafts to confirm the diagnosis and monitor severity over time","Genetic counselling addressing the usual autosomal dominant hair-cortex keratin inheritance and the rarer autosomal recessive DSG4 form, with assessment of affected relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"monkeypox":{"name":"Mpox (Monkeypox) & Poxvirus Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mpox","monkeypox","molluscum contagiosum"],"chapter":201,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1517","clinicalPearls":["Classic mpox/smallpox prodrome: fever first, followed days later by rash; other prodromal features include malaise, sore throat, and headache (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Lymphadenopathy is a key historical feature distinguishing mpox (present) from smallpox (absent) in endemic countries (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Classic systemic orthopoxvirus rash evolves through macular, papular, vesicular, and pustular phases (the pustular stage with central umbilication), each stage lasting 2-3 days, with lesions in the same anatomic location typically at the same stage of development (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Classic distribution is diffuse, well-circumscribed, and centrifugal: lesions more prominent on face and extremities (including palms and soles) than on the trunk; lesions are painful as they emerge and become pruritic in later stages (Harrison 22e p.1519).","In the 2022 clade IIb outbreak, transmission was primarily through close, intimate (e.g., sexual) contact and predominantly affected gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men (MSM) (Harrison 22e p.1518).","Clade IIb mpox often departed from the classic picture: rash was frequently the first or only sign (prodrome absent or concurrent), lesions were often localized to the anogenital region, smaller, fewer (often <10), and could be at different stages at the same anatomic site (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Up to 50% of those diagnosed during the 2022 outbreak in the U.S. context were also living with HIV (Harrison 22e p.1518).","Molluscum contagiosum (MCV) has a classic pearly appearance that may umbilicate as it matures, with little surrounding inflammation and painless lesions that persist for months but gradually regress over 6-12 months (Harrison 22e p.1519).","The infectious period for orthopoxvirus infections lasts from symptom onset until all lesions have crusted, the crusts have separated, and fresh healthy skin has formed underneath; lesion scabs contain viable virus (Harrison 22e p.1518).","Two distinct MPXV clades exist: Clade I (formerly Congo Basin, Central Africa) causes a higher proportion of severe disease than Clade II (formerly West African); Clade II subdivides into IIa (endemic West Africa) and IIb (spread worldwide in 2022) (Harrison 22e p.1517)."],"pathophysiology":"Poxviruses are large double-stranded DNA viruses that replicate entirely in the cytoplasm. Orthopoxvirus infection (variola, MPXV) is thought to involve systemic spread from the inoculation site to local lymph nodes, seeding of lymphoreticular tissue, and then symptomatic (febrile) viremia with viral skin tropism; disease severity reflects how well innate immune and interferon responses control early infection. Immunocompetent hosts develop lesions (often at the exposure site) about 1 week after exposure, progressing through staged phases over 7-14 days with scabbing and resolution by 14-21 days after rash onset. In severe immunocompromise (e.g., advanced HIV with CD4 <200 cells/mm3, organ transplantation), MPXV spread/growth goes unchecked, producing wide dissemination with large confluent lesions persisting for months and additional organ involvement. Most other poxvirus infections (e.g., parapoxviruses, MCV) involve only local viral growth at the inoculation site; yatapoxvirus (tanapox) pathogenesis is poorly understood (Harrison 22e p.1518).","additionalDifferentials":["Varicella (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Herpes simplex virus infection (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Syphilis (anogenital mpox lesions may resemble STIs) (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Gonorrhea (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Chlamydia (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Papillomavirus infection (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Yaws (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Cutaneous anthrax (particularly with parapoxvirus infections) (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Smallpox (variola) - distinguished from mpox classically by absence of lymphadenopathy (Harrison 22e p.1519)"],"infectionMimics":["Varicella (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Herpes simplex virus (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Syphilis and other STIs (gonorrhea, chlamydia) in anogenital presentations (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Yaws (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Papillomavirus infection (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Cutaneous anthrax (with parapoxvirus infections) (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Malaria (tanapox initially presents with very high fever and may be mistaken for malaria in endemic areas) (Harrison 22e p.1519)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nucleic acid (molecular) testing is the most common diagnostic tool: PCR assays for non-variola orthopoxvirus (NVO), orthopoxvirus generic, generic mpox, and mpox clade II; sequencing for full isolate characterization (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Multiplex assays combining NVO- and MPXV-specific targets, and clade-specific testing (U.S. Government and Public Health Laboratories), are available (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Viral culture: orthopoxviruses grow well in standard clinical tissue culture; parapoxviruses are difficult to isolate (primary cells best); MCV cannot be cultured (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Electron microscopy: characteristic large brick-shaped particles on negative stain for orthopoxvirus, yatapoxvirus, or MCV; parapoxviruses show an ovoid structure with crisscross spicules (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Histopathology of biopsy: MCV shows classic Henderson-Patterson bodies (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Serology can demonstrate orthopoxvirus reactivity but most assays cannot distinguish between orthopoxvirus species due to broad antigenic similarity (Harrison 22e p.1519)."],"redFlags":["Severe immunocompromise (advanced HIV with CD4 <200 cells/mm3, especially <50; organ transplantation) predisposes to severe, disseminated, prolonged mpox with numerous large coalescing or necrotic lesions and multi-organ involvement (GI tract, liver, lungs, brain) (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Ocular involvement: eye pain, redness, vision changes/loss, periorbital swelling, blepharitis, conjunctivitis, keratitis - can result in vision loss (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Neurologic complications: encephalitis, myelitis - severe headache, neck pain, altered mentation, focal deficits (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Myopericarditis: dyspnea or palpitations with elevated cardiac biomarkers and ECG changes (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Mucosal complications affecting alimentation, urination, or defecation due to painful or obstructive lesions; may lead to strictures, edema, and severe lymphadenopathy (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Most deaths have occurred predominantly among people with advanced HIV and CD4 <50 cells/mm3 (Harrison 22e p.1519)."],"prognosis":"In immunocompetent hosts most poxvirus infections are self-limited, resolving in weeks (or months for molluscum contagiosum). Exceptions are the generalized orthopoxvirus infections caused by MPXV and variola, which carry higher case-fatality. In unvaccinated individuals smallpox mortality is up to 30%; MPXV mortality is approximately 1.4-10% (clade I), ~1% (clade IIa), and <1% (clade IIb). Immunocompromised hosts may have more severe orthopoxvirus and parapoxvirus disease (severe mpox, progressive vaccinia, eczema vaccinatum, severe borealpox) with higher mortality, or atypical presentations (e.g., giant orf). In advanced HIV, effective antiretroviral therapy is essential to favorable outcomes. MCV can be diffuse in the immunocompromised, and immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) has been associated with recrudescence of MCV. Poxvirus reinfections are seldom reported (Harrison 22e p.1521).","pitfalls":["Do not assume a classic systemic presentation: during the 2022 clade IIb outbreak rash was often the first/only sign with prodrome absent, lesions localized to the anogenital area, smaller, fewer (<10), and could be at different stages at the same site - contrary to the classic rule that lesions in the same location are at the same stage (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Anogenital mpox lesions can be mistaken for common STIs (gonorrhea, chlamydia, syphilis); up to 10% of mpox patients may be coinfected with an STI, so a positive STI test does not exclude mpox (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Characteristic lesions plus exposure history narrow the differential, but laboratory testing is required to confirm the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Distinguish mpox from varicella (VZV), herpes simplex, and syphilis - overlapping vesiculopustular/anogenital presentations are a recognized diagnostic trap (Harrison 22e p.1519).","MCV cannot be cultured, so reliance on culture for diagnosis of molluscum is futile; biopsy (Henderson-Patterson bodies) or clinical/EM findings are needed (Harrison 22e p.1519)."],"management":["Supportive care is the mainstay for most immunocompetent cases, with analgesia, skin and wound care, hydration and management of painful proctitis or pharyngitis","Antiviral therapy for severe disease or those at high risk, with tecovirimat as the usual first choice and cidofovir, brincidofovir or vaccinia immune globulin as alternatives","Isolation and infection-control precautions until lesions have crusted and re-epithelialised, together with contact tracing","Pre-exposure and post-exposure vaccination of contacts and at-risk groups with a modified vaccinia Ankara vaccine","Test for and manage co-infections including HIV and other sexually transmitted infections, optimising antiretroviral therapy in advanced HIV given the risk of unchecked dissemination","Monitor for and escalate complications such as secondary bacterial infection, ocular involvement, encephalitis and dehydration, hospitalising severe or immunocompromised patients"],"severityClassification":"Mpox ranges from typical self-limited disease (immunocompetent, including HIV with CD4 >200 cells/mm3, generally without increased severity) to severe mpox. Severe manifestations include ocular infection, neurologic complications (encephalitis, myelitis), myopericarditis, obstructive mucosal disease, and uncontrolled viral replication with disseminated/necrotic lesions and multi-organ involvement, occurring chiefly in severely immunocompromised patients (advanced HIV, organ transplantation). Clade I causes a higher proportion of severe disease than clade II (Harrison 22e p.1519).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1517","Harrison 22e p.1518","Harrison 22e p.1519","Harrison 22e p.1521"],"crossLinks":["varicella_zoster","syphilis","hiv_aids"]},"monoclonal_gammopathy_renal_significance":{"name":"Monoclonal gammopathy of renal significance","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MGRS","renal monoclonal gammopathy","monoclonal immunoglobulin-associated kidney disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of kidney disorders caused by a nephrotoxic monoclonal immunoglobulin produced by a B-cell or plasma-cell clone that is itself too small to meet criteria for myeloma or lymphoma","The clone would otherwise be labelled a monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance, but the renal damage reclassifies it as clinically significant","Patients present with proteinuria, haematuria, progressive renal impairment or nephrotic syndrome without another clear cause","The kidney lesions are diverse and include monoclonal immunoglobulin deposition disease, proliferative glomerulonephritis with monoclonal deposits, light chain proximal tubulopathy and AL amyloidosis","A renal biopsy with immunofluorescence and electron microscopy is central to demonstrating the monoclonal immunoglobulin in the kidney","The paraprotein detected in serum or urine must be shown to match the deposited immunoglobulin to establish causation","Treatment is directed against the underlying clone to remove the pathogenic protein and preserve kidney function, and disease can recur after transplantation if the clone persists"],"pathophysiology":"Monoclonal gammopathy of renal significance describes kidney injury caused by a monoclonal immunoglobulin or its fragments secreted by a small, non-malignant B-cell or plasma-cell clone. Despite a low tumour burden, the abnormal protein has physicochemical properties that make it nephrotoxic: it may deposit in glomerular or tubular basement membranes, form organised fibrils or crystals, aggregate within proximal tubular cells, or activate complement, depending on its structure. These mechanisms produce a spectrum of glomerular and tubulointerstitial lesions. Because damage stems from the protein rather than tumour bulk, effective treatment must suppress the clone that produces it.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple myeloma with cast nephropathy","AL amyloidosis with systemic involvement","Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance without renal disease","Diabetic or hypertensive nephropathy","Immune-complex glomerulonephritis of other cause","Cryoglobulinaemic renal disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation and serum free light chains","Renal biopsy with immunofluorescence and electron microscopy","Matching of the deposited immunoglobulin with the circulating paraprotein","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine to characterise the clone","Quantification of proteinuria and assessment of renal function","Cross-sectional imaging or further staging to exclude overt myeloma or lymphoma"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive renal impairment threatening dialysis dependence","Nephrotic-range proteinuria with hypoalbuminaemia","Features suggesting systemic amyloidosis or evolving overt malignancy","Recurrence of disease in a kidney transplant"],"prognosis":"Kidney outcome depends on early recognition and effective clone-directed therapy; timely treatment can stabilise or improve renal function, whereas delayed diagnosis often leads to irreversible kidney failure, and the disease may recur after transplantation if the clone is not controlled.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a small monoclonal protein as benign despite unexplained kidney disease","Not performing a renal biopsy to define the specific lesion","Failing to confirm that the renal deposits match the circulating paraprotein","Transplanting the kidney without controlling the underlying clone, risking recurrence"],"management":["Clone-directed therapy is the definitive treatment, targeting the responsible clone even though its burden is low, with proteasome-inhibitor (bortezomib-based) regimens and daratumumab or autologous stem cell transplantation for plasma-cell clones and rituximab-based regimens for B-cell or lymphoid clones","Renal supportive care with blood-pressure control using renin-angiotensin system blockade, reduction of proteinuria and avoidance of nephrotoxins","Renal biopsy with immunofluorescence and electron microscopy to characterise the lesion and match the deposited immunoglobulin to the circulating paraprotein, directing therapy","Monitor haematological response by serum free light chains and paraprotein and renal response by proteinuria and renal function","Coordinated multidisciplinary haematology and nephrology management","Defer kidney transplantation until the clone is controlled and counsel on the high risk of recurrence in the graft if it is not"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"monoclonal_mast_cell_activation_syndrome":{"name":"Monoclonal mast cell activation syndrome","system":"Haematology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MMAS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mastocytosis and mast cell activation literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mast cell activation syndrome in which there is evidence of a clonal mast cell population but insufficient criteria for systemic mastocytosis","Patients have recurrent episodes of mast cell mediator release, often including anaphylaxis","Hymenoptera venom anaphylaxis with hypotension is a classic presentation","Clonality is shown by a KIT D816V mutation and/or aberrant mast cell markers, without meeting full systemic mastocytosis criteria","It is distinguished from non-clonal (idiopathic or secondary) mast cell activation syndrome by the demonstration of clonality","Management centres on mediator control, trigger avoidance and anaphylaxis preparedness"],"pathophysiology":"Monoclonal mast cell activation syndrome occupies a middle ground between mast cell activation syndrome and systemic mastocytosis. A clonal mast cell population, frequently harbouring the KIT D816V mutation, is present but the burden and features fall short of the diagnostic threshold for systemic mastocytosis. These clonal mast cells are prone to activation and mediator release, producing episodic symptoms such as flushing, hypotension and anaphylaxis, often with an identifiable trigger such as insect venom.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic mastocytosis","Non-clonal (idiopathic) mast cell activation syndrome","Idiopathic anaphylaxis","Hereditary alpha-tryptasaemia","Carcinoid syndrome or phaeochromocytoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum tryptase, including during and between episodes","KIT D816V mutation analysis (often on blood or marrow)","Bone marrow examination with mast cell immunophenotyping","Assessment against systemic mastocytosis criteria","Allergy and trigger evaluation, including venom testing"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or severe anaphylaxis","Evolving features suggesting progression to systemic mastocytosis","Cardiovascular collapse during episodes","Cytopenias or organ involvement"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally favourable with mediator-directed therapy and anaphylaxis management, though patients remain at risk of severe reactions and require monitoring for progression.","pitfalls":["Failing to test for clonality (KIT mutation) in unexplained anaphylaxis","Not distinguishing it from full systemic mastocytosis","Overlooking venom allergy as a trigger","Omitting rescue adrenaline provision"],"management":["Mediator-directed therapy as the foundation, with H1 and H2 antihistamines, a mast cell stabiliser such as sodium cromoglicate and a leukotriene receptor antagonist, escalating to anti-IgE therapy with omalizumab for refractory symptoms","Identification and avoidance of triggers, notably Hymenoptera venom and other precipitants of mediator release","Anaphylaxis preparedness with an adrenaline auto-injector, a personalised emergency action plan and patient education","Venom immunotherapy for Hymenoptera venom anaphylaxis, often continued long term given the underlying clonality","Monitor serum tryptase and periodically reassess against systemic mastocytosis criteria to detect progression","Consider KIT-directed therapy and specialist mastocytosis input if the clonal disease progresses, within coordinated allergy and haematology care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mastocytosis clinical guidelines (standard reference)","Allergy reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"monocular_elevation_deficiency":{"name":"monocular elevation deficiency","system":"Visual system / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["double elevator palsy","monocular elevation deficit"],"chapter":null,"source":"Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A unilateral limitation of elevation of one eye in all horizontal positions, affecting the action of both elevators (superior rectus and inferior oblique), hence the older term double elevator palsy","Usually congenital: the affected eye is hypotropic in the primary position and the patient adopts a chin-up head posture to maintain binocular single vision","Ptosis or pseudoptosis is common; true ptosis occurs in roughly half of cases, and pseudoptosis from the hypotropic globe resolves when the eye elevates to fixate","Three mechanisms are recognised: restriction of the inferior rectus (positive forced duction, the commonest), primary elevator weakness (superior rectus and inferior oblique paresis), and a supranuclear cause","Forced duction and force generation testing differentiate a tight (restrictive) inferior rectus from a paretic cause and guide the choice of operation","If the patient fixates with the affected hypotropic eye, the sound eye shows a secondary hypertropia; a preserved Bell's phenomenon points to a supranuclear or paretic rather than restrictive mechanism","Marcus Gunn jaw-winking and other synkineses should be excluded when ptosis is present","Surgical choice depends on mechanism: inferior rectus recession for restriction and a Knapp procedure (transposition of the horizontal recti towards the superior rectus) for elevator weakness"],"pathophysiology":"Monocular elevation deficiency is a heterogeneous condition characterised by defective elevation of one eye. The commonest mechanism is a restrictive, tight inferior rectus (fibrosis) that mechanically prevents elevation; alternatively there may be a primary paresis of the elevator muscles (superior rectus and inferior oblique) or, less often, a supranuclear lesion of the elevation pathway in the pretectal region, in which case Bell's phenomenon is preserved. Many cases are congenital and may fall within the congenital cranial dysinnervation disorder spectrum. The resulting hypotropia in the primary position drives the compensatory chin-up head posture and, where the visual axes are not aligned, amblyopia of the affected eye.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital fibrosis of the extraocular muscles","Brown syndrome, which limits elevation only in adduction (key distinction)","Inferior rectus restriction from thyroid eye disease or an orbital floor fracture with entrapment","Congenital superior-division third nerve palsy","Superior oblique overaction and other causes of vertical strabismus","Skew deviation and supranuclear vertical gaze disturbance","Congenital ptosis with an apparent (pseudo) motility defect"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Forced duction testing for inferior rectus restriction and force generation testing","Orthoptic assessment across all positions of gaze with measurement of head posture","Assessment of Bell's phenomenon and saccadic velocities","Cycloplegic refraction and amblyopia evaluation","Distinguishing true ptosis from pseudoptosis by rechecking lid height on fixation with the affected eye","Orbital CT or MRI if restriction from trauma, thyroid disease or a mass is suspected","Neuro-imaging if a supranuclear cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["An acquired (rather than congenital) elevation deficit suggesting orbital, neurological or thyroid pathology","Signs of an orbital floor fracture with inferior rectus entrapment, including recent trauma and an oculocardiac reflex","Pupillary involvement or other neurological signs suggesting a third nerve or midbrain lesion","Amblyopia in a young child requiring prompt treatment"],"prognosis":"Congenital cases are non-progressive, and surgery to correct the hypotropia and abnormal head posture, combined with amblyopia treatment and ptosis repair where needed, generally gives good functional and cosmetic outcomes. Some limitation of elevation typically persists.","pitfalls":["Confusing the condition with Brown syndrome, which limits elevation only in adduction","Operating on ptosis before correcting the hypotropia, when pseudoptosis may resolve once the eye is realigned","Omitting forced ductions and thereby missing a restrictive inferior rectus, leading to the wrong operation","Overlooking amblyopia in the hypotropic eye","Assuming every case is paretic and missing a supranuclear cause by not checking Bell's phenomenon"],"management":["Treat amblyopia first with cycloplegic refractive correction and occlusion or penalisation of the sound eye","Surgical correction of the hypotropia and abnormal chin-up head posture, with the procedure guided by forced duction testing, using inferior rectus recession for a restrictive tight muscle or a vertical transposition such as the Knapp procedure for elevator weakness","Repair true ptosis after ocular alignment, recognising that pseudoptosis from the hypotropic globe resolves once the eye is realigned","Distinguish restrictive, paretic and supranuclear mechanisms preoperatively to plan the appropriate operation","Neuroimaging and systemic evaluation where an acquired cause such as trauma, thyroid eye disease or an orbital mass is suspected","Ongoing orthoptic follow-up to monitor alignment and binocular function"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","von Noorden's Binocular Vision and Ocular Motility"],"crossLinks":["congenital_fibrosis_of_extraocular_muscles"]},"monofixation_syndrome":{"name":"Monofixation syndrome","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["monofixation","microtropia","monofixational phoria","subnormal binocular vision"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Von Noorden's Binocular Vision and Ocular Motility","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by a small central suppression scotoma in the deviating eye with intact peripheral fusion, giving gross but subnormal stereopsis","The manifest deviation (microtropia) is typically small, often 8 prism dioptres or less, and can be easy to miss on cover testing","Frequently represents the best achievable sensory outcome after surgical alignment of infantile esotropia","May arise primarily or secondary to anisometropia, a small unilateral macular lesion, or residual misalignment after strabismus surgery","The 4-prism-dioptre base-out test helps demonstrate the central scotoma by showing absence of a refixation movement in the affected eye","Worth four-dot testing shows fusion at near (peripheral field) but suppression at distance (central field)","Peripheral fusion preserves some motor fusion amplitude and binocular cooperation despite the central scotoma","Amblyopia is common in the non-fixating eye and should be actively sought and treated"],"pathophysiology":"Monofixation syndrome reflects a compromise in binocular sensory function in which a small central suppression scotoma develops in one eye to eliminate diplopia and confusion, while peripheral retinal correspondence and fusion are retained. This produces a stable small-angle deviation. Recognised triggers include anisometropia (unequal refraction), a small unilateral macular lesion, and residual misalignment after correction of a larger childhood strabismus. Because high-grade central stereopsis is lost but peripheral fusion persists, stereoacuity is reduced but not abolished, and the patient retains a degree of comfortable binocular vision.","additionalDifferentials":["Orthotropia with normal binocular single vision","Intermittent exotropia or esotropia","Accommodative esotropia","Amblyopia without a manifest deviation","Dissociated vertical deviation","Small vertical tropia or phoria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cover/uncover and alternate prism cover testing to detect and quantify the small deviation","4-prism-dioptre base-out test to demonstrate the central suppression scotoma","Worth four-dot test to distinguish peripheral fusion from central suppression","Stereoacuity testing (e.g. Titmus or TNO) showing gross but subnormal stereopsis","Best-corrected visual acuity in each eye to detect amblyopia","Cycloplegic refraction to identify underlying anisometropia"],"redFlags":["New-onset or increasing deviation suggesting decompensation","Dense amblyopia threatening visual development in a child","An unexplained macular lesion found on examination requiring further assessment","Diplopia developing where none was present, indicating loss of suppression"],"prognosis":"Monofixation syndrome is a stable and often favourable sensory state that provides peripheral fusion and gross stereopsis; many patients remain comfortably aligned long term, although associated amblyopia needs monitoring and the small deviation may occasionally decompensate.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the small deviation on standard cover testing and labelling the patient orthotropic","Missing coexisting amblyopia in the non-fixating eye","Attempting to eliminate the small residual angle surgically and inducing intractable diplopia","Failing to correct anisometropia that underlies the condition"],"management":["Optical correction of any underlying anisometropia with spectacles or contact lenses","Treat associated amblyopia with occlusion or penalisation and monitor visual acuity","Reassure that the microtropia is a stable and often favourable sensory state providing peripheral fusion and gross stereopsis, so many patients need no intervention beyond refraction","Reserve prism correction or strabismus surgery for the minority in whom the small deviation decompensates into a symptomatic larger-angle strabismus","Periodic orthoptic monitoring of the deviation and stereoacuity to detect decompensation or worsening amblyopia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Von Noorden's Binocular Vision and Ocular Motility"],"crossLinks":[]},"mononeuritis_multiplex":{"name":"Mononeuritis multiplex","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mononeuropathy multiplex","multiple mononeuropathy","multifocal neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the sequential or simultaneous involvement of two or more named peripheral nerves in different limbs, producing an asymmetric, patchy deficit","The stepwise, painful onset of individual nerve territories is an important clue that separates it from length-dependent symmetrical neuropathy","Vasculitis is a leading cause, so systemic features such as weight loss, rash, arthralgia, and renal involvement should be sought","Diabetes mellitus is a common metabolic cause and may combine several mechanisms","As lesions accumulate they can become confluent and mimic a symmetrical polyneuropathy, obscuring the underlying pattern","Nerve conduction studies show a multifocal, often axonal process with asymmetry between nerves","Nerve biopsy, typically of the sural nerve, may be needed to confirm vasculitis and guide immunosuppression","Because vasculitic neuropathy reflects ischaemic nerve infarction, prompt recognition and treatment can prevent irreversible axonal loss"],"pathophysiology":"Mononeuritis multiplex arises from multifocal damage to individual peripheral nerves, most classically through occlusion or inflammation of the vasa nervorum (nerve ischaemia) in systemic or non-systemic vasculitis. Ischaemic infarction of nerve fascicles produces predominantly axonal loss in the territories supplied by affected vessels, explaining the patchy, asymmetric deficits. Other mechanisms include direct infiltration, compression at multiple sites, immune-mediated demyelination, and metabolic injury as in diabetes. The random distribution reflects the scattered nature of the underlying microvascular or infiltrative lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic vasculitides (polyarteritis nodosa, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis)","Diabetic neuropathy and diabetic amyotrophy","Multiple compressive mononeuropathies (including hereditary neuropathy with pressure palsies)","Sarcoidosis and neoplastic infiltration","Infective causes such as leprosy, HIV, and Lyme disease","Cryoglobulinaemia and connective tissue disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to map the multifocal, asymmetric pattern","Inflammatory and autoimmune screen (ESR, CRP, ANCA, ANA, complement, cryoglobulins)","Testing for diabetes, HIV, hepatitis B and C, and other infective causes","Nerve and muscle biopsy to confirm vasculitis when systemic markers are inconclusive","Assessment for systemic organ involvement including renal function and urinalysis","Imaging or further workup for sarcoidosis or malignancy when suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive multifocal deficits with systemic features suggesting active vasculitis requiring urgent immunosuppression","Evidence of multi-organ involvement such as renal impairment, pulmonary haemorrhage, or gut ischaemia","Severe constitutional symptoms indicating an aggressive systemic process","New motor deficits threatening limb function that demand expedited diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the underlying cause and the speed of treatment; vasculitic cases can recover substantially if immunosuppression is started before extensive axonal infarction, but delayed treatment or severe systemic disease leaves persistent deficits.","pitfalls":["Waiting for a symmetrical pattern to emerge and missing the early asymmetric vasculitic phase","Attributing the picture to diabetes alone without excluding treatable vasculitis","Delaying nerve biopsy and immunosuppression when vasculitis is clinically likely","Overlooking systemic organ involvement that alters urgency and prognosis","Failing to consider infective causes such as leprosy in appropriate settings"],"management":["Treat the underlying cause, with corticosteroids plus immunosuppression such as cyclophosphamide or rituximab for vasculitis, tight glycaemic control for diabetic disease, and targeted antimicrobial or antiviral therapy for infective causes","Tailor immunomodulation to aetiology, for example intravenous immunoglobulin for immune-mediated disease and treatment of cryoglobulinaemia or underlying hepatitis","Neuropathic pain management with agents such as gabapentinoids or tricyclic antidepressants","Rehabilitation with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, orthoses such as a foot-drop splint, and falls prevention","Monitor disease activity, treatment toxicity and end-organ involvement, with surveillance for relapse","Escalate immunosuppression promptly for progressive deficit to prevent irreversible axonal infarction, within multidisciplinary neurology and rheumatology care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"monteggia_fracture":{"name":"Monteggia fracture-dislocation","system":"Musculoskeletal / Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["proximal ulna fracture with radial head dislocation","Monteggia lesion","Bado fracture-dislocation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fracture of the proximal ulna combined with dislocation of the radial head","The radial head typically dislocates in the direction the apex of the ulnar fracture points, described by the Bado classification (anterior, posterior, lateral, or both-bone types)","A useful memory aid contrasts Monteggia (ulna fracture with radial head dislocation) against Galeazzi (radius fracture with distal radioulnar joint injury)","The radiocapitellar line should pass through the centre of the capitellum on every view; if it does not, the radial head is dislocated","In adults the ulna requires open reduction and internal fixation restoring its length and bow, which usually reduces the radial head","A missed radial head dislocation is a common and serious error, especially in children","Posterior interosseous nerve palsy may accompany the injury, particularly in lateral and posterior patterns"],"pathophysiology":"A fracture of the proximal ulna alters the length and alignment of the bone. Because the radius and ulna are coupled proximally through the annular ligament and the proximal radioulnar joint, ulnar deformity displaces the radial head from its articulation with the capitellum. Restoring anatomical ulnar length and curvature is therefore essential, as residual ulnar malalignment leaves the radial head subluxed or dislocated. The dislocating radial head can also stretch or injure the posterior interosseous nerve as it passes around the radial neck.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated proximal ulna fracture","Isolated traumatic radial head dislocation","Radial head or neck fracture","Both-bone forearm fracture","Elbow dislocation","Isolated (nightstick) ulnar shaft fracture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anteroposterior and lateral radiographs of the elbow and the entire forearm","Confirmation that the radiocapitellar line intersects the capitellum on all views","Contralateral comparison radiographs, particularly in children","CT for complex or comminuted proximal ulna fractures","Careful documentation of posterior interosseous nerve function"],"redFlags":["Missed dislocation of the radial head","Posterior interosseous nerve palsy","Compartment syndrome after high-energy injury","Open fracture requiring urgent care"],"prognosis":"Anatomical restoration of ulnar length and alignment usually re-establishes radiocapitellar congruity with a good outcome; missed dislocation, ulnar malreduction or nerve injury worsen the result.","pitfalls":["Failing to check the radiocapitellar line and thereby missing the radial head dislocation","Inadequate restoration of ulnar length and bow, leaving the radial head subluxed","Overlooking a posterior interosseous nerve palsy","Not imaging the full forearm including the elbow"],"management":["In adults, open reduction and internal fixation of the proximal ulna restoring its anatomical length and bow, which usually reduces the dislocated radial head","In children, closed reduction with restoration of ulnar alignment and confirmation of radiocapitellar reduction, reserving surgery for unstable or irreducible injuries","Confirm radial head reduction using the radiocapitellar line intra-operatively and afterwards, with open reduction and annular ligament reconstruction if the radial head remains irreducible","Assess and document posterior interosseous nerve function, observing expectantly as most palsies are neurapraxias that recover spontaneously","Immobilisation followed by graded rehabilitation to restore elbow motion and forearm rotation","Follow-up radiographs to detect late radial head subluxation, malunion or nerve recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","AO Principles of Fracture Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"mooren_ulcer":{"name":"Mooren ulcer","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic serpiginous ulcer of the cornea","rodent ulcer of the cornea","idiopathic peripheral ulcerative keratitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology / Cornea by Krachmer","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A painful, progressive, idiopathic peripheral ulcerative keratitis that spreads circumferentially and centrally from the limbus","The ulcer has a characteristic overhanging, undermined leading edge with an intact epithelium beyond the advancing margin","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, made only after autoimmune and infective causes of peripheral ulcerative keratitis have been ruled out","A limited unilateral form typically affects older patients, while an aggressive bilateral form is more common in younger patients and can be relentless","Severe pain, out of proportion to signs, together with progressive thinning is typical","There is no scleral involvement, which helps distinguish it from Terrien degeneration and from scleritis-associated peripheral keratitis","Management follows an immunosuppressive ladder from topical corticosteroids to systemic immunomodulators, with conjunctival resection and surgical repair for perforation","An association with hepatitis C has been described in some aggressive cases and is worth considering"],"pathophysiology":"Mooren ulcer is thought to be an autoimmune peripheral ulcerative keratitis in which an immune response against corneal stromal antigens leads to progressive destruction. Autoantibodies and cell-mediated inflammation recruit neutrophils and activate collagenolytic and proteolytic enzymes from the limbal vessels and adjacent conjunctiva, degrading peripheral corneal stroma. The ulcer advances circumferentially and centrally with a characteristic undermined edge, and the sclera is spared. Progressive stromal melting can lead to marked thinning, irregular astigmatism, and perforation. Because the process is inflammatory rather than infective, treatment is directed at suppressing the immune response.","additionalDifferentials":["Peripheral ulcerative keratitis from systemic vasculitis (e.g. granulomatosis with polyangiitis, rheumatoid arthritis)","Terrien marginal degeneration","Marginal (staphylococcal hypersensitivity) keratitis","Infective peripheral keratitis (bacterial, herpetic, fungal, acanthamoeba)","Scleritis with associated keratolysis","Dellen and other non-inflammatory peripheral thinning"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination documenting the undermined advancing edge and degree of thinning","Corneal scrapings and cultures to exclude infective keratitis","Systemic autoimmune and vasculitis workup (including inflammatory markers and autoantibodies) to exclude secondary peripheral ulcerative keratitis","Hepatitis C serology in aggressive or bilateral disease","Anterior segment optical coherence tomography or pachymetry to monitor thinning","Corneal topography to assess induced astigmatism"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive thinning threatening or causing perforation","Severe unremitting pain with an advancing undermined edge","Bilateral aggressive disease in a younger patient","Features suggesting an underlying systemic vasculitis requiring urgent systemic treatment"],"prognosis":"The limited unilateral form in older patients often responds to treatment, whereas the bilateral aggressive form can be relentless, causing severe thinning, perforation, and significant visual loss despite immunosuppression.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing Mooren ulcer without first excluding systemic vasculitis and infection","Missing scleral involvement that would point away from Mooren ulcer","Undertreating aggressive disease and allowing progression to perforation","Overlooking an associated systemic condition such as hepatitis C"],"management":["Suppress the autoimmune process, beginning with intensive topical corticosteroids and escalating to systemic corticosteroids and steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as ciclosporin, methotrexate or cyclophosphamide for aggressive or bilateral disease","Limit stromal melting with collagenase inhibitors and generous lubrication, and avoid topical anaesthetic misuse","Control the severe pain and support the ocular surface","Surgical and adjunctive measures for progressive thinning or perforation, including conjunctival resection or recession, tissue adhesive, amniotic membrane grafting or tectonic corneal grafting","Investigate and treat associated systemic disease, including hepatitis C, where antiviral therapy can improve virus-associated cases","Regular monitoring of thinning with anterior segment optical coherence tomography or pachymetry and vigilance for perforation, under corneal specialist care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Cornea by Krachmer, Mannis and Holland"],"crossLinks":[]},"morgagni_hernia":{"name":"Morgagni hernia","system":"Respiratory / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["retrosternal diaphragmatic hernia","anterior diaphragmatic hernia","foramen of Morgagni hernia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Shields' General Thoracic Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital anterior diaphragmatic hernia through the retrosternal foramen of Morgagni, usually on the right side","The least common type of congenital diaphragmatic hernia and often presents late in adulthood, frequently as an incidental finding","May contain omentum, transverse colon, stomach, or liver within a peritoneal sac","Appears on a chest radiograph as a well-defined right cardiophrenic angle mass or opacity","CT confirms the diagnosis by showing herniated abdominal contents, often with a characteristic band of omental fat and vessels crossing the defect","Symptoms, when present, include vague chest or upper abdominal discomfort, cough, or gastrointestinal symptoms from herniated bowel","Surgical repair is generally recommended even when asymptomatic because of the risk of incarceration or strangulation of contents"],"pathophysiology":"The foramen of Morgagni is a small anterior triangular gap between the sternal and costal muscular origins of the diaphragm, where the superior epigastric vessels pass. A congenital weakness or failure of muscular fusion at this point leaves a retrosternal defect through which abdominal contents, carried within a peritoneal sac, can herniate into the thorax. Raised intra-abdominal pressure from obesity, pregnancy, constipation, or exertion promotes progressive herniation, which is why many cases only become apparent in adult life. Herniated omentum or bowel is usually asymptomatic but can become obstructed, incarcerated, or strangulated.","additionalDifferentials":["Pericardial fat pad","Pericardial cyst","Anterior mediastinal mass or thymic lesion","Bochdalek (posterolateral) diaphragmatic hernia","Diaphragmatic eventration","Lung or pleural-based mass at the cardiophrenic angle"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph showing a right cardiophrenic angle opacity","Contrast-enhanced CT of the thorax and abdomen to confirm herniated contents and the fatty band sign","Contrast studies of the gastrointestinal tract when bowel is thought to be involved","MRI in selected cases to characterise soft-tissue contents","Assessment for associated congenital anomalies in younger patients"],"redFlags":["Acute obstruction, incarceration, or strangulation of herniated bowel with pain and vomiting","Signs of ischaemia of herniated contents","Respiratory compromise from a large hernia","Rapidly increasing size of a cardiophrenic mass"],"prognosis":"The outlook after elective repair is excellent with low recurrence, whereas emergency presentation with incarceration or strangulation carries higher morbidity, which is the rationale for repairing even asymptomatic hernias.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the hernia for a benign pericardial fat pad or cyst and leaving it unassessed","Failing to recognise herniated bowel at risk of obstruction","Overlooking the diagnosis on a plain radiograph and attributing the opacity to a mediastinal mass","Underestimating the risk of complications when advising conservative management"],"management":["Surgical repair is the definitive treatment and is generally recommended even when asymptomatic because of the risk of obstruction and strangulation, comprising reduction of contents, excision of the peritoneal sac and closure of the retrosternal defect by an open or minimally invasive laparoscopic or thoracoscopic approach, with mesh reinforcement for large defects","Emergency surgery for incarceration, strangulation or bowel obstruction","Preoperative confirmation and operative planning with contrast-enhanced CT of the thorax and abdomen","Assess for associated congenital anomalies in paediatric presentations","Perioperative supportive care with postoperative follow-up to detect the uncommon recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Shields' General Thoracic Surgery","Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"morning_glory_disc_anomaly":{"name":"Morning glory disc anomaly","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["morning glory syndrome","morning glory optic disc anomaly","MGDA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski / Taylor & Hoyt Paediatric Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital, usually unilateral optic disc dysplasia resembling the morning glory flower, with a funnel-shaped excavated disc","The enlarged disc has a central glial tuft, an elevated surrounding annulus of chorioretinal pigment, and radially arranged vessels emerging like spokes","Often detected on investigation of poor vision, strabismus or leukocoria in early childhood","Associated with midline cranial and central nervous system anomalies, including basal encephalocoele, absent corpus callosum and pituitary abnormalities","Part of the PHACE association and linked to moyamoya-type cerebral vasculopathy, warranting cerebral vascular imaging","Serous retinal detachment is a recognised complication that can further reduce vision","Midfacial anomalies (hypertelorism, cleft lip/palate) should prompt a search for a basal encephalocoele"],"pathophysiology":"Morning glory disc anomaly is thought to arise from defective closure of the posterior scleral wall and abnormal development of the optic nerve head and surrounding neuroectodermal and mesodermal tissues. The result is a large, funnel-shaped excavation of the disc with a central glial remnant, a raised pigmented peripapillary ring and anomalous radiating retinal vessels. Because these structures develop alongside midline forebrain and vascular elements, the anomaly is frequently accompanied by midline brain defects, hypothalamic-pituitary dysfunction and cerebral arterial anomalies such as moyamoya. Traction and abnormal tissue at the disc predispose to serous (often non-rhegmatogenous) retinal detachment.","additionalDifferentials":["Optic disc coloboma","Peripapillary staphyloma","Optic nerve hypoplasia","Megalopapilla","Optic pit with serous maculopathy","Advanced glaucomatous cupping (in older children)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain and orbits to detect midline anomalies and encephalocoele","MR or CT angiography for moyamoya-type cerebral vasculopathy","Endocrine assessment of pituitary function","OCT and fundus imaging of the disc and macula","Assessment for serous retinal detachment with dilated examination","Paediatric and, where indicated, neurosurgical review"],"redFlags":["Midfacial dysmorphism suggesting a transsphenoidal basal encephalocoele","Neurological symptoms or signs of cerebral ischaemia from moyamoya vasculopathy","Features of hypopituitarism such as hypoglycaemia or growth failure","New serous retinal detachment causing acute visual decline","Nasal mass or CSF rhinorrhoea indicating an encephalocoele"],"prognosis":"Vision in the affected eye is usually poor and often does not improve, and serous detachment can worsen it; systemic outcome depends on the associated central nervous system, vascular and endocrine anomalies.","pitfalls":["Treating the disc anomaly as an isolated ocular finding and omitting neuroimaging","Missing moyamoya vasculopathy that carries a stroke risk","Overlooking pituitary dysfunction and endocrine deficits","Mistaking a serous detachment for a rhegmatogenous one and mismanaging it"],"management":["Cerebral vascular imaging with MR or CT angiography and ongoing surveillance for moyamoya-type vasculopathy, with neurosurgical revascularisation if it progresses","Neuroimaging for midline and central nervous system anomalies such as basal encephalocoele and agenesis of the corpus callosum, with neurosurgical management of an encephalocoele","Endocrine assessment of pituitary function with hormone replacement for hypothalamic-pituitary dysfunction","Amblyopia management with refractive correction and occlusion, and treatment of associated strabismus","Monitor for and treat serous retinal detachment, which may require laser or surgical intervention","Low-vision support given the typically poor and non-improving vision in the affected eye","Multidisciplinary paediatric ophthalmology, neurology and neurosurgery and endocrinology, with genetic assessment for associated conditions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Taylor & Hoyt's Paediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"morphea":{"name":"Morphea","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["localised scleroderma","circumscribed scleroderma","morphoea"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Morphea is a localised sclerosing disorder of the skin that, unlike systemic sclerosis, spares the internal organs and is not accompanied by Raynaud phenomenon or sclerodactyly","Early plaques are erythematous or violaceous and indurated, evolving into firm, waxy, ivory-coloured centres often surrounded by a lilac inflammatory ring that signals active disease","Subtypes include circumscribed (plaque) morphea, generalised, linear, and deep forms, each with different distribution and complication profiles","Linear morphea in children can cross joints and, when it affects the limbs or face, may impair growth, cause contractures, or produce the en coup de sabre pattern on the forehead","Facial and periorbital linear disease warrants assessment for underlying neurological and ophthalmological involvement","Treatment aims to halt inflammation during the active phase, since established sclerosis and atrophy are difficult to reverse; options range from topical and phototherapy approaches to systemic immunomodulation in extensive or deep disease","Physiotherapy and monitoring for joint contractures are important in linear and deep subtypes","Distinguish morphea from systemic sclerosis clinically and, if needed, serologically, to avoid unnecessary alarm and inappropriate investigation"],"pathophysiology":"Morphea is an immune-mediated fibrosing process in which an initial inflammatory phase, characterised by a perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate and endothelial activation, is followed by excessive deposition of collagen in the dermis and, in deeper subtypes, the subcutis and fascia. Dysregulated production of pro-fibrotic cytokines and growth factors drives fibroblasts to lay down dense, thickened collagen bundles, with loss of adnexal structures and normal fat. Vascular changes and, in some cases, autoimmune associations contribute. The result is progressive induration and, later, atrophy; when fibrosis extends across joints or affects growing tissues in children, functional and cosmetic sequelae follow.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic sclerosis (must be distinguished as it involves internal organs)","Lichen sclerosus (can coexist and overlap clinically)","Eosinophilic fasciitis","Scleredema and scleromyxoedema","Chronic graft-versus-host disease with sclerodermatous features","Lipodermatosclerosis and post-radiation fibrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy, ideally deep enough to assess dermis, subcutis, and fascia, to confirm the diagnosis and subtype","Clinical assessment for systemic sclerosis features, with autoantibody testing where indicated","Imaging (including MRI) in linear or deep disease to define extent and detect muscle, bone, or CNS involvement","Ophthalmology and neurology review for craniofacial linear disease","Serial mapping or scoring of lesions to monitor activity and progression"],"redFlags":["Craniofacial linear morphea (en coup de sabre / Parry-Romberg) with neurological symptoms, seizures, or eye involvement","Rapidly extending or deep lesions crossing joints and threatening contractures","Growth disturbance or limb length discrepancy in affected children","Extensive generalised disease causing functional impairment","Diagnostic uncertainty where systemic sclerosis cannot be confidently excluded"],"prognosis":"Many plaque-type lesions eventually become inactive over several years, leaving pigmentary change and atrophy, whereas linear and deep subtypes, particularly in children, carry a higher risk of lasting functional and cosmetic sequelae; early treatment of the inflammatory phase improves outcomes.","pitfalls":["Confusing morphea with systemic sclerosis and triggering unnecessary anxiety or investigation","Missing the active inflammatory (lilac ring) phase when treatment is most effective","Underappreciating growth and contracture risk in paediatric linear disease","Failing to screen for neurological and ocular involvement in craniofacial disease","Assuming established sclerosis will respond to anti-inflammatory therapy"],"management":["Halt inflammation during the active phase, since established sclerosis and atrophy are hard to reverse, using topical corticosteroids, topical calcineurin inhibitors or calcipotriol for limited disease and phototherapy such as UVA1 or narrowband UVB for widespread superficial disease","Systemic immunomodulation for extensive, linear, deep or functionally threatening disease, typically methotrexate with a corticosteroid bridge, or mycophenolate as an alternative","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to prevent and manage contractures, particularly in linear disease crossing joints in children","Ophthalmology and neurology assessment for craniofacial and en coup de sabre disease","Emollients, management of atrophy and scarring, and cosmetic or reconstructive input for established sequelae","Serial lesion mapping and scoring to monitor activity and detect relapse","Multidisciplinary dermatology, rheumatology and paediatric care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"morquio_syndrome":{"name":"Morquio syndrome (MPS IV)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPS IV","mucopolysaccharidosis type IV","Morquio A (GALNS deficiency)","Morquio B (beta-galactosidase deficiency)","keratan sulfate storage disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Scriver's OMMBID","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Distinguished by a severe skeletal dysplasia with markedly short trunk and normal intelligence, setting it apart from most other mucopolysaccharidoses","Two subtypes exist: type A from N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase deficiency and type B from beta-galactosidase deficiency","Atlantoaxial instability with odontoid hypoplasia is a hallmark and poses a major risk of cervical cord compression","Keratan sulfate is the principal accumulated and excreted glycosaminoglycan","Genu valgum, pectus carinatum, kyphoscoliosis, and ligamentous laxity are characteristic","Corneal clouding and hearing loss occur, but cognition is preserved","Diagnosis relies on the specific enzyme assay supported by urinary keratan sulfate, with molecular confirmation"],"pathophysiology":"Morquio syndrome is caused by deficiency of enzymes needed to degrade keratan sulfate. In type A, biallelic GALNS variants cause deficiency of N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase; in type B, GLB1 variants cause deficiency of beta-galactosidase. The resulting block in keratan sulfate (and, in type A, also chondroitin-6-sulfate) degradation leads to lysosomal accumulation, which disproportionately affects cartilage and bone and produces the distinctive spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia. Because keratan sulfate is concentrated in cartilage and cornea rather than the brain, intelligence is characteristically normal. Excess keratan sulfate is excreted in the urine, though levels can normalise with age. Both subtypes are inherited in an autosomal recessive manner.","additionalDifferentials":["Other mucopolysaccharidoses, particularly Maroteaux-Lamy syndrome (MPS VI)","Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita","Other primary skeletal dysplasias with short trunk","GM1 gangliosidosis (allelic with Morquio B through GLB1)","Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia","Achondroplasia and other disproportionate short-stature syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Enzyme assay for N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase or beta-galactosidase","Urinary keratan sulfate quantification","GALNS or GLB1 gene sequencing","Cervical spine imaging including flexion-extension views to assess atlantoaxial instability and odontoid hypoplasia","Skeletal survey to characterise the dysplasia","Echocardiography, ophthalmology, and audiology for extraskeletal involvement"],"redFlags":["Atlantoaxial instability with signs of cervical myelopathy","Respiratory compromise from thoracic cage deformity and airway narrowing","Anaesthetic risk from a difficult airway and unstable cervical spine","Progressive cardiac valve disease"],"prognosis":"Intelligence is preserved and survival into adulthood is usual, but skeletal complications, especially cervical instability and restrictive respiratory disease, cause significant morbidity and can be life-threatening if unrecognised. Enzyme replacement therapy is available for type A and may improve endurance and some somatic features.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for atlantoaxial instability before any anaesthetic or surgical procedure","Mistaking the disorder for a primary skeletal dysplasia without measuring glycosaminoglycans","Missing Morquio B because of overlap with GM1 gangliosidosis phenotypes","Assuming normal urinary keratan sulfate excludes the diagnosis in older patients"],"management":["Disease-specific enzyme replacement therapy with elosulfase alfa for type A disease","Cervical spine surveillance with flexion-extension imaging and timely surgical stabilisation or decompression for atlantoaxial instability and odontoid hypoplasia, given the risk of cervical cord compression","Respiratory monitoring for restrictive and obstructive disease and sleep-disordered breathing, with airway and ventilatory support as needed","Careful anaesthetic planning for any procedure because of cervical instability and a difficult airway","Orthopaedic management of genu valgum, kyphoscoliosis and joint problems, with physiotherapy and mobility aids","Regular ophthalmology for corneal clouding, audiology for hearing loss and cardiac surveillance for valve disease","Multidisciplinary metabolic and genetics-led care with genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance and family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"mortons_neuroma":{"name":"Morton neuroma","system":"Musculoskeletal / Hand & Peripheral nerve","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["interdigital neuroma","Morton metatarsalgia","intermetatarsal neuroma","plantar interdigital neuroma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Despite its name it is not a true neoplasm but a perineural fibrosis of a common plantar digital nerve","The third web space, between the third and fourth metatarsal heads, is most commonly affected, followed by the second web space","Patients describe burning plantar forefoot pain radiating into the adjacent toes and often the sensation of a pebble or lump within the shoe","Symptoms are aggravated by tight or high-heeled footwear and relieved by removing the shoe and massaging the foot","The Mulder click is a palpable and sometimes audible click produced by mediolateral compression of the forefoot combined with dorsoplantar pressure over the web space","There is tenderness in the affected web space and sensory disturbance in the contiguous borders of the involved toes","The condition is markedly more common in women, in keeping with the association with constrictive footwear","Generic management includes footwear modification and metatarsal pads or orthoses, corticosteroid injection, and surgical excision or neurolysis for refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"A Morton neuroma is a mechanically induced degenerative process of a common plantar interdigital nerve rather than a genuine tumour. The nerve is irritated where it passes beneath the deep transverse intermetatarsal ligament, and the third web space is especially vulnerable where medial and lateral plantar nerve contributions converge. Repetitive dorsiflexion in narrow footwear causes chronic compression and traction, leading to perineural fibrosis, endoneurial oedema and thickening of the nerve, which generate the characteristic neuritic pain.","additionalDifferentials":["Metatarsophalangeal joint synovitis or plantar plate instability, particularly of the second toe","Metatarsal stress fracture","Freiberg disease (osteochondrosis of a metatarsal head)","Metatarsophalangeal joint osteoarthritis","Tarsal tunnel syndrome or a more proximal plantar nerve entrapment","Intermetatarsal bursitis","Soft-tissue ganglion or tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination including the Mulder click and web space compression test","Ultrasound as the usual first-line imaging to confirm and size the lesion","MRI when ultrasound is equivocal or other pathology is suspected","Weightbearing plain radiographs to exclude a bony cause such as stress fracture or Freiberg disease","A diagnostic local anaesthetic injection into the web space to confirm the pain source"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting a soft-tissue tumour rather than an interdigital neuroma","Bilateral or multiple lesions prompting consideration of an alternative diagnosis","An underlying systemic inflammatory arthropathy","A progressive neurological deficit pointing to a more proximal cause"],"prognosis":"Many patients improve with footwear modification and injection, and surgical excision gives good relief, though it carries a risk of a recurrent stump neuroma and a permanent patch of sensory loss between the toes.","pitfalls":["Attributing pain to a neuroma when the true source is metatarsophalangeal joint synovitis or plantar plate instability","Overlooking coexisting forefoot pathology","A painful stump neuroma developing after excision","Repeated corticosteroid injection causing plantar fat atrophy or plantar plate attenuation"],"management":["Conservative first-line care with footwear modification into wide, low-heeled shoes and metatarsal pads or orthoses to offload the affected web space","Corticosteroid injection, often combined with local anaesthetic, into the symptomatic web space","Adjunctive measures such as activity modification and analgesia, with alcohol or sclerosing injections or radiofrequency ablation in selected refractory cases","Surgical excision by neurectomy or nerve decompression for symptoms resistant to conservative treatment, counselling on the risk of a recurrent stump neuroma and a permanent patch of sensory loss between the toes","Confirm the diagnosis and expected response with a diagnostic local anaesthetic injection into the web space, supported by ultrasound imaging"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","Green's Operative Hand Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"morvan_syndrome":{"name":"Morvan syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Morvan's fibrillary chorea","CASPR2 antibody syndrome","contactin-associated protein-like 2 antibody disease","peripheral nerve hyperexcitability with encephalopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / peer-reviewed autoimmune neurology literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad combines peripheral nerve hyperexcitability (neuromyotonia), dysautonomia, and an encephalopathy with severe insomnia","Muscle twitching, cramps, and undulating myokymia reflect continuous motor unit activity often visible as rippling movements","Profound agrypnia (near-total loss of sleep) with confusion, hallucinations, and fluctuating consciousness is a hallmark feature","Autonomic disturbance manifests as hyperhidrosis, tachycardia, labile blood pressure, and constipation","Antibodies target the voltage-gated potassium channel complex, chiefly CASPR2 and sometimes contactin-2 or LGI1","A significant proportion have an underlying thymoma or other malignancy, so tumour screening is essential","Immunotherapy for autoimmune encephalitis and treatment of any associated tumour usually improve symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"Morvan syndrome is an autoimmune disorder in which antibodies against the voltage-gated potassium channel complex, most often the CASPR2 protein, disrupt neuronal excitability. CASPR2 is expressed at juxtaparanodal regions of peripheral nerves and within limbic and thalamic circuits, so loss of its function produces both continuous peripheral motor nerve firing and central nervous system dysfunction. The result is peripheral nerve hyperexcitability with neuromyotonia, autonomic overactivity, and an encephalopathy dominated by intractable insomnia and fluctuating cognition. A subset is paraneoplastic, driven by thymoma, which sustains the aberrant immune response.","additionalDifferentials":["Isaacs syndrome (acquired neuromyotonia without prominent encephalopathy)","Limbic encephalitis from LGI1 or other autoimmune causes","Fatal familial insomnia and other prion disorders","Serotonin syndrome or neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Thyrotoxicosis with tremor and autonomic overdrive","Delirium from toxic, metabolic, or withdrawal states"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and CSF testing for CASPR2 and associated VGKC-complex antibodies","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography showing fasciculations, myokymic and neuromyotonic discharges","Cross-sectional chest imaging to detect thymoma and wider malignancy screening","Brain MRI to assess for limbic or thalamic signal change","Autonomic function testing and polysomnography to characterise dysautonomia and sleep loss"],"redFlags":["Severe agrypnia with agitation, hallucinations, and cardiovascular instability","Autonomic crisis with malignant arrhythmia or extreme blood pressure swings","Rapidly progressive encephalopathy suggesting a paraneoplastic driver","Discovery of an underlying thymoma or occult malignancy"],"prognosis":"With immunotherapy and removal of any associated tumour many patients improve substantially, but relapses occur and paraneoplastic cases carry the prognosis of the underlying malignancy. Autonomic instability can be life-threatening during the acute phase.","pitfalls":["Attributing the insomnia and confusion to a primary psychiatric disorder and delaying immunotherapy","Failing to screen for thymoma once the antibody syndrome is recognised","Overlooking subtle neuromyotonia because myokymia is mistaken for benign fasciculation","Not monitoring for autonomic and cardiac complications during acute illness"],"management":["Immunotherapy is the mainstay: corticosteroids combined with intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange to remove the pathogenic CASPR2/VGKC-complex antibodies","Escalate to second-line immunosuppression such as rituximab or cyclophosphamide in refractory or relapsing disease","Screen for and resect an underlying thymoma, as thymectomy is essential in paraneoplastic cases and can drive remission","Relieve neuromyotonia symptomatically with membrane-stabilising sodium-channel blockers such as carbamazepine or phenytoin","Manage autonomic instability and profound insomnia in a high-dependency setting, as labile blood pressure and cardiac dysrhythmia can be life-threatening","Monitor for relapse and continue malignancy surveillance after treatment","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across neurology, oncology and autonomic and sleep specialists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"mosaic_variegated_aneuploidy":{"name":"Mosaic variegated aneuploidy syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MVA","MVA syndrome","BUB1B-related mosaic variegated aneuploidy","mosaic variegated aneuploidy syndrome 1"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The defining laboratory feature is constitutional mosaic aneuploidy, with different cells carrying gains or losses of various chromosomes (variegated), typically involving a substantial proportion of cells on cytogenetic study","Clinically it presents with severe microcephaly, pre- and postnatal growth restriction, and developmental delay","There is a strong predisposition to childhood cancers, particularly Wilms tumour, rhabdomyosarcoma, and leukaemia, reflecting genome instability","The most recognised cause is biallelic variants in BUB1B, which encodes BUBR1, a core component of the mitotic spindle assembly checkpoint; other causes include CEP57 and TRIP13","Additional features can include Dandy-Walker malformation and other brain anomalies, cataracts and other eye abnormalities, and seizures","Diagnosis combines cytogenetic demonstration of multiple aneuploidies across cells with molecular confirmation of the causative gene","Management is supportive with heightened tumour surveillance, since early detection of embryonal tumours is central to care"],"pathophysiology":"The syndrome is a chromosome-instability disorder arising, in its classic form, from biallelic loss-of-function variants in BUB1B, which encodes BUBR1, an essential protein of the spindle assembly checkpoint. This checkpoint normally delays progression through mitosis until all chromosomes are correctly attached to the spindle, preventing missegregation. When checkpoint function is impaired, chromosomes are distributed unequally to daughter cells, generating the characteristic mosaic pattern of gains and losses across many different chromosomes. The resulting aneuploidy and genomic instability drive the growth failure, microcephaly, neurodevelopmental impairment, and the marked predisposition to embryonal tumours. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Constitutional mismatch repair deficiency","Fanconi anaemia and other chromosome-breakage syndromes","Nijmegen breakage syndrome","Primary microcephaly syndromes without aneuploidy","Seckel syndrome and microcephalic primordial dwarfism","Other cancer-predisposition syndromes presenting in early childhood"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cytogenetic karyotyping of multiple cells to demonstrate variegated mosaic aneuploidy","Molecular genetic testing of BUB1B, CEP57, and TRIP13","MRI of the brain to assess microcephaly and posterior fossa anomalies such as Dandy-Walker malformation","Tumour surveillance imaging, particularly abdominal ultrasound for Wilms tumour","Ophthalmological examination for cataract and other anomalies","Developmental assessment and, where relevant, EEG for seizures"],"redFlags":["An abdominal mass, haematuria, or other features suggesting Wilms tumour or another embryonal malignancy","Pancytopenia or other haematological abnormalities suggesting leukaemia","Rapidly enlarging head or signs of raised intracranial pressure","New seizures or acute neurological deterioration"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally guarded, reflecting the combination of severe growth and neurodevelopmental impairment and a high risk of early childhood malignancy. Outcomes depend substantially on the specific genotype and on whether tumours are detected and treated early through surveillance.","pitfalls":["Dismissing an isolated aneuploid cell on karyotype without recognising the pattern of variegated mosaic aneuploidy","Failing to institute tumour surveillance and thereby missing early, treatable embryonal tumours","Overlooking posterior fossa malformations on neuroimaging","Not recognising the autosomal recessive inheritance and its implications for recurrence risk"],"management":["Institute intensive childhood tumour surveillance for Wilms tumour, rhabdomyosarcoma and leukaemia with regular abdominal ultrasound, clinical examination and blood counts","Refer promptly for paediatric oncology treatment when an embryonal tumour or leukaemia is detected","Provide neurodevelopmental support with early intervention, physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy for the developmental impairment","Treat seizures with anticonvulsants and monitor with electroencephalography","Arrange ophthalmological review with cataract surgery, and optimise growth and nutrition given the severe growth restriction","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive condition, with testing of parents and at-risk relatives","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across clinical genetics, paediatric oncology, neurology and ophthalmology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (University of Washington)","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"motion_sickness":{"name":"Motion sickness","system":"Environmental / Emergency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["kinetosis","travel sickness","seasickness","car sickness"],"chapter":null,"source":"Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine / Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Provoked by real or perceived motion, producing nausea, pallor, cold sweating, increased salivation, and eventually vomiting","Best explained by sensory conflict, where the motion signals from the vestibular system, eyes, and proprioceptors disagree with one another or with expectation","Common triggers include sea travel, car and air travel, and reading or using a screen while in motion","Early warning symptoms such as yawning, drowsiness, and epigastric discomfort often precede overt nausea","Non-drug measures include fixing the gaze on the horizon, sitting where motion is least, improving ventilation, and reclining the head against a support","Medications, chiefly antihistamines and anticholinergic agents, can prevent or reduce symptoms, ideally taken before the journey","Habituation with repeated exposure and adaptation reduces susceptibility over time"],"pathophysiology":"Motion sickness is attributed to a mismatch between the movement information the brain receives from the vestibular apparatus, the visual system, and proprioceptors, and the pattern it expects from prior experience. This sensory conflict is processed in the vestibular and brainstem pathways and activates the emetic centres, producing autonomic features such as pallor, sweating, salivation, and nausea culminating in vomiting. A classic example is reading in a moving vehicle, where the eyes signal a stable scene while the vestibular system detects motion. Repeated exposure allows central adaptation and habituation.","additionalDifferentials":["Peripheral vestibular disorders (e.g. benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, vestibular neuritis)","Vestibular migraine","Mal de debarquement syndrome","Gastroenteritis or other cause of nausea and vomiting","Alcohol intoxication or medication side effect","Anxiety-related symptoms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the temporal relationship of symptoms to motion exposure","Vestibular and neurological examination to exclude other causes when symptoms occur without motion","Assessment for vestibular migraine in recurrent or atypical cases","Review of medications and substances that may provoke nausea","Vestibular function testing where an underlying disorder is suspected"],"redFlags":["Symptoms occurring in the absence of any motion stimulus","Associated true vertigo, hearing loss, or focal neurological signs","Severe or protracted vomiting risking dehydration","Headache with neurological features suggesting migraine or other pathology"],"prognosis":"Motion sickness resolves once the provoking motion ceases and susceptibility often lessens with habituation; it is benign, though it can be disabling during travel and, in prolonged exposure such as at sea, may cause dehydration.","pitfalls":["Giving preventive medication after symptoms are already established, when it is less effective","Overlooking an underlying vestibular disorder or migraine in atypical cases","Ignoring sedation from antihistamine or anticholinergic drugs in those who must remain alert","Failing to advise simple positional and visual strategies alongside medication"],"management":["Emphasise non-drug measures: fix the gaze on the horizon, sit where motion is least, improve ventilation, recline the head against a support and avoid reading or screen use","Encourage habituation through graded, repeated exposure to the provoking motion","Offer the anticholinergic hyoscine (scopolamine) as prophylaxis, taken before the journey","Use sedating antihistamines such as cinnarizine, cyclizine or promethazine to prevent and relieve symptoms","Maintain hydration and light bland meals, and treat dehydration during prolonged exposure such as sea travel","Reassess for vestibular migraine or an underlying vestibular disorder when symptoms are recurrent, atypical or occur without motion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine","Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"mounier_kuhn":{"name":"Mounier-Kuhn syndrome","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mounier-Kuhn syndrome","tracheobronchomegaly","congenital tracheobronchomegaly"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ATS-ERS","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare disorder characterised by marked dilatation of the trachea and main bronchi (tracheobronchomegaly) due to atrophy of the airway wall elastic and smooth muscle","Presents with chronic productive cough, recurrent respiratory infections, and breathlessness, often after years of being labelled as bronchitis or bronchiectasis","The redundant, flaccid airway wall collapses on expiration and forms outpouchings (tracheal diverticula) between cartilage rings","Impaired mucociliary clearance from the dilated, dynamically collapsing airways predisposes to recurrent infection and secondary bronchiectasis","Diagnosis is based on measuring enlarged tracheal and bronchial diameters on CT against established thresholds","Bronchoscopy may show the widened lumen, dynamic expiratory collapse, and mucosal herniation between cartilaginous rings","It predominantly affects men and often presents in the third to fifth decades","Management is supportive, centred on airway clearance techniques, prompt treatment of infections, and treating associated bronchiectasis, as there is no cure"],"pathophysiology":"Mounier-Kuhn syndrome results from deficiency or atrophy of the elastic fibres and smooth muscle within the walls of the trachea and central bronchi, causing progressive dilatation (tracheobronchomegaly). The weakened, flaccid airway walls balloon between the cartilage rings to form diverticula and collapse dynamically during forced expiration and coughing. This dynamic collapse and enlargement markedly impairs the effectiveness of cough and mucociliary clearance, leading to retained secretions, recurrent lower respiratory infection, and secondary bronchiectasis. The underlying wall defect is generally considered congenital, though it may not become clinically apparent until adulthood.","additionalDifferentials":["Bronchiectasis from other causes","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Tracheomalacia and tracheobronchomalacia of other aetiology","Cystic fibrosis","Ehlers-Danlos and other connective tissue disorders affecting the airway"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT of the chest with measurement of tracheal and bronchial diameters against diagnostic thresholds","Dynamic (inspiratory and expiratory) imaging or bronchoscopy to demonstrate airway collapse","Bronchoscopy to visualise luminal enlargement and mucosal herniation","Pulmonary function testing and assessment for associated bronchiectasis","Sputum microbiology during infective exacerbations"],"redFlags":["Severe or recurrent lower respiratory infection with clinical deterioration","Massive or recurrent haemoptysis from secondary bronchiectasis","Progressive respiratory failure in advanced disease","Acute worsening of airway collapse causing severe breathlessness"],"prognosis":"The course is variable; many patients remain relatively stable with good airway clearance, but recurrent infection and progressive bronchiectasis can lead to declining lung function and respiratory failure in advanced cases.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis by not measuring central airway diameters on CT","Attributing recurrent infections to ordinary bronchiectasis without recognising the underlying tracheobronchomegaly","Failing to demonstrate dynamic expiratory collapse that explains poor secretion clearance","Neglecting structured airway clearance, the mainstay of management"],"management":["Airway clearance is the mainstay: regular chest physiotherapy, postural drainage and mucoactive agents such as nebulised saline to counter impaired mucociliary clearance","Treat infective exacerbations promptly with antibiotics guided by sputum microbiology","Vaccinate against influenza and pneumococcus and encourage smoking cessation to reduce the infection burden","Manage secondary bronchiectasis and provide bronchodilators with pulmonary rehabilitation as needed","Consider continuous positive airway pressure to stent the flaccid airway on expiration, reserving tracheobronchial stenting or surgery for selected refractory cases","Monitor lung function and imaging for progressive airflow obstruction and decline","Escalate to non-invasive or invasive ventilation for respiratory failure and consider lung transplantation in advanced disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ATS/ERS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"mounier_kuhn_syndrome":{"name":"Mounier-Kuhn syndrome","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tracheobronchomegaly","tracheobronchomalacia with megatrachea","Mounier-Kuhn disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Respiratory Medicine and Bronchoscopy reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare condition of marked dilatation of the trachea and main bronchi (tracheobronchomegaly) due to atrophy or deficiency of elastic and smooth-muscle tissue in the airway wall","Redundant airway mucosa herniates between cartilage rings, producing characteristic tracheal diverticulosis and a corrugated appearance","Presents typically in middle-aged men with chronic productive cough, recurrent lower respiratory infections, and bronchiectasis","The enlarged, flaccid airways collapse on expiration and impair secretion clearance, predisposing to infection and bronchiectasis","Diagnosis rests on measuring tracheal and bronchial diameters on CT above defined thresholds, alongside the typical clinical picture","Bronchoscopy shows a widened, flaccid airway with mucosal outpouchings between cartilaginous rings","Management is supportive, centred on airway clearance, treatment of infections, and management of associated bronchiectasis"],"pathophysiology":"Mounier-Kuhn syndrome is characterised by atrophy or congenital deficiency of the elastic fibres and smooth muscle of the tracheal and bronchial walls, which causes progressive dilatation of these large airways. The weakened wall allows the mucous membrane to bulge outward between the cartilage rings, forming diverticula and giving a scalloped contour, and the flaccid airway collapses excessively during expiration and coughing. This dynamic collapse and enlargement impair the effectiveness of cough and mucociliary clearance, leading to retained secretions, recurrent infection, and secondary bronchiectasis. Most cases are considered congenital, and associations with connective tissue disorders have been described.","additionalDifferentials":["Bronchiectasis of other aetiology","Tracheobronchomalacia without megatrachea","Cystic fibrosis","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Tracheal dilatation from traction by pulmonary fibrosis","Ehlers-Danlos or other connective tissue disorders affecting the airway"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT of the thorax measuring tracheal and main bronchial diameters and demonstrating diverticulosis","Dynamic expiratory imaging or bronchoscopy to assess airway collapse","Bronchoscopy showing the widened flaccid airway and mucosal outpouchings","Pulmonary function tests including flow-volume loops","Sputum microbiology in recurrent infection","Assessment for associated bronchiectasis and connective tissue disease"],"redFlags":["Recurrent severe pneumonia or respiratory failure from secretion retention","Massive or extensive bronchiectasis with declining lung function","Life-threatening dynamic airway collapse","Haemoptysis requiring evaluation in the context of bronchiectasis"],"prognosis":"The course is variable; some patients remain relatively stable with airway clearance and prompt treatment of infections, while others develop progressive bronchiectasis and respiratory impairment, and prognosis is largely determined by the extent of secondary lung damage.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis and attributing recurrent infection to ordinary bronchiectasis without noting the megatrachea","Failing to measure airway diameters on CT","Missing the diagnostic clue of tracheal diverticulosis and a corrugated airway","Not addressing dynamic airway collapse contributing to poor secretion clearance"],"management":["Prioritise airway clearance with chest physiotherapy, postural drainage and mucolytics or nebulised hypertonic saline to overcome poor cough and secretion retention","Give targeted antibiotics for recurrent lower respiratory infections, directed by sputum culture","Immunise against influenza and pneumococcal disease and support smoking cessation","Treat associated bronchiectasis and use bronchodilators alongside pulmonary rehabilitation","Trial continuous positive airway pressure to splint the collapsing large airways, reserving tracheobronchial stenting or reconstructive surgery for selected refractory cases","Screen for and manage any associated connective tissue disorder","Monitor pulmonary function and escalate to non-invasive ventilation, considering lung transplantation in end-stage respiratory failure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Flexible Bronchoscopy (Wang, Mehta, Turner)"],"crossLinks":[]},"movement_disorders":{"name":"Movement Disorders (Tremor, Chorea, Dystonia)","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["essential tremor","Huntington disease","dystonia","chorea"],"chapter":447,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3508","clinicalPearls":["Movement disorders are classified as hypokinetic (poverty of movement, e.g. parkinsonism with rest tremor, cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia) versus hyperkinetic (excess movement, e.g. chorea, dystonia, tremor); the rest tremor and cogwheel rigidity of Parkinson's disease are extrapyramidal signs distinct from the action tremor of essential tremor (Harrison 22e p.3508; cf. p.197 ch.31).","Essential tremor is an action/postural tremor; a key pitfall is mislabeling it as Parkinson's disease, but Parkinson tremor is characteristically a rest tremor accompanied by cogwheel rigidity and bradykinesia, whereas essential tremor lacks these and worsens with sustained posture or movement (Harrison 22e p.3508).","Huntington's disease is an autosomal dominant degenerative disorder producing the triad of chorea, psychiatric/behavioral disturbance (mood, anxiety, psychosis), and progressive dementia; the unpredictable choreic movements give the gait a characteristic dancing quality (Harrison 22e p.3508; gait described p.~3535 ch.32; HD as autosomal-dominant dementia p.198 ch.31).","Dystonia is defined as sustained muscle contractions producing repetitive twisting movements and abnormal posture; it often has a genetic basis, but new asymmetric dystonia—particularly involving gait—in a younger adult may be an early sign of Parkinson's disease (Harrison 22e p.3508; cf. gait chapter p.~3535).","Sydenham's chorea (a manifestation of acute rheumatic fever after group A streptococcal infection) is the prototypical post-infectious chorea: it occurs mainly in females after a prolonged latent period, affects especially the head (darting tongue movements) and upper limbs, may be generalized or hemichoreic, and is frequently accompanied by emotional lability or obsessive-compulsive traits; >50% of patients presenting with chorea have carditis, so echocardiography is warranted (Harrison 22e p.3508; ch. on rheumatic fever p.~2992).","Tardive dyskinesia is a drug-induced hyperkinetic disorder seen with chronic exposure to antipsychotics and other agents that block the D2 dopamine receptor, and is a common cause of stereotypic movement and gait disturbances (Harrison 22e p.3508)."],"pathophysiology":"Movement disorders arise predominantly from dysfunction of the basal ganglia (striatum = caudate plus putamen) and their cortical and thalamic connections, which modulate motor output rather than directly generating it; disruption of these circuits yields either hypokinetic syndromes (parkinsonism) or hyperkinetic syndromes (chorea, dystonia, tremor). Huntington's disease is caused by an expanded CAG trinucleotide repeat (autosomal dominant) with degeneration of the striatum. Wilson's disease results from impaired copper excretion with copper accumulation in liver and brain. Tardive dyskinesia reflects chronic blockade of striatal D2 dopamine receptors (Harrison 22e p.3508; basal ganglia/striatum anatomy p.~199-200 ch.31).","additionalDifferentials":["Parkinson's disease (rest tremor, cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia) — see parkinsons_disease","Progressive supranuclear palsy (axial rigidity, vertical gaze palsy, early falls)","Multiple-system atrophy and corticobasal syndrome (asymmetric akinesia, rigidity, dystonia, myoclonus, alien limb)","Orthostatic tremor (high-frequency low-amplitude leg tremor, shakiness on standing relieved by sitting/walking)","Drug-induced/tardive dyskinesia from D2-blocking agents","Sydenham's chorea / post-streptococcal autoimmune chorea"],"infectionMimics":["Sydenham's chorea following group A streptococcal infection (acute rheumatic fever)","Other post-infectious or autoimmune (paraneoplastic) chorea and movement disorders"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Wilson's disease (copper accumulation; young patient with chorea, dystonia, parkinsonism or tremor)","Drug-induced movements from dopamine-receptor blockers (tardive dyskinesia, acute dystonia)","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder","Hyperthyroidism (fine tremor; chorea rare)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for the CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion when Huntington's disease is suspected","Serum ceruloplasmin (low), 24-h urinary copper (elevated), slit-lamp exam for Kayser-Fleischer rings, and liver copper to evaluate for Wilson's disease in any young patient with unexplained chorea, dystonia, tremor or parkinsonism","Echocardiography when chorea suggests acute rheumatic fever (>50% have carditis); anti-streptolysin O and anti-DNase B titers for preceding streptococcal infection","MRI brain to assess for basal ganglia and other structural/degenerative changes"],"redFlags":["New chorea, dystonia, tremor or parkinsonism in an adolescent or young adult — exclude Wilson's disease (treatable; look for Kayser-Fleischer rings, low ceruloplasmin, high urinary copper) before manifestations become irreversible","Chorea with autosomal dominant family history plus psychiatric change and cognitive decline — consider Huntington's disease","Acute chorea (especially in a young female) — evaluate for acute rheumatic fever and carditis with echocardiography","Abnormal movements in a patient on long-term antipsychotics/D2 blockers — recognize tardive dyskinesia and acute dystonic reactions"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is disorder-specific. Essential tremor is typically slowly progressive and non-degenerative. Huntington's disease is relentlessly progressive and degenerative. Wilson's disease can improve substantially with prompt diagnosis and copper-chelating treatment before end-stage manifestations become irreversible. Sydenham's chorea is usually self-limited, with choreiform movements resolving within about 6 weeks, occasionally up to 6 months, though associated obsessive-compulsive or emotional symptoms may persist longer (Harrison 22e p.3508; Wilson p.~2713 ch.355; chorea p.~2992).","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing essential (action) tremor as Parkinson's disease, or vice versa — Parkinson tremor is a rest tremor with cogwheel rigidity and bradykinesia","Failing to test for Wilson's disease in a young patient with new movement disorder; Wilson's may present with chronic hepatitis long before neurologic signs or Kayser-Fleischer rings appear","Overlooking carditis in a patient presenting with chorea (Sydenham's), since chorea may be the sole or delayed manifestation of rheumatic fever","Not recognizing drug-induced/tardive movements from D2-blocking medications"],"management":["Suppress essential tremor with beta-blockers such as propranolol or with primidone, reserving deep brain stimulation for medically refractory cases","Treat parkinsonism with dopaminergic therapy using levodopa and dopamine agonists","Control Huntington's chorea with VMAT2 inhibitors such as tetrabenazine or with dopamine-blocking agents, and manage the accompanying psychiatric and cognitive features","Treat Wilson's disease with copper chelation using penicillamine or trientine plus zinc and dietary copper restriction, which can reverse deficits if started early","Manage focal dystonia with botulinum toxin injections and anticholinergics, using deep brain stimulation for generalised or refractory dystonia","For tardive dyskinesia withdraw or reduce the offending dopamine-receptor-blocking drug and add a VMAT2 inhibitor, and treat Sydenham's chorea with streptococcal eradication and rheumatic fever management","Offer genetic counselling and family testing for heritable causes such as autosomal dominant Huntington's disease and autosomal recessive Wilson's disease","Coordinate multidisciplinary support with physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy and psychiatry"],"severityClassification":"No single unified severity scale spans all movement disorders; assessment is disorder-specific and functional. Chorea, for example, ranges from movements evident only on careful examination in mild cases to inability to perform activities of daily living in severe cases (Harrison 22e p.3508).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3508"],"crossLinks":["parkinsons_disease"]},"mowat_wilson_syndrome":{"name":"Mowat-Wilson syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MWS","Hirschsprung disease-intellectual disability syndrome","ZEB2","ZFHX1B","SIP1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distinctive face with a broad prominent forehead, deep-set widely spaced eyes, a saddle nose with a rounded tip, and uplifted fleshy earlobes (the classic 'orecchiette' or medially raised lobes) is highly recognisable","Hirschsprung disease or severe chronic constipation is a major and characteristic association","Caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants or deletions of ZEB2, following autosomal dominant inheritance and almost always de novo","Severe intellectual disability with absent or very limited speech but a notably happy, sociable demeanour is typical","Microcephaly, epilepsy and agenesis or hypoplasia of the corpus callosum are frequent","Congenital heart disease and genitourinary anomalies including hypospadias are common","The combination of the facial gestalt with Hirschsprung disease should prompt ZEB2 testing"],"pathophysiology":"Mowat-Wilson syndrome is caused by haploinsufficiency of ZEB2, a transcription factor (also called SIP1) that represses target genes and is essential for the development and migration of neural crest cells and for central nervous system patterning. Loss-of-function point variants and whole-gene deletions reduce ZEB2 activity, disrupting neural crest derivatives, which explains the enteric nervous system defect underlying Hirschsprung disease as well as the craniofacial, cardiac and genitourinary malformations, together with the neurodevelopmental impairment from disturbed brain development.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated Hirschsprung disease","Goldberg-Shprintzen syndrome","Pitt-Hopkins syndrome","Angelman syndrome","Rett syndrome","22q11.2 deletion syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis of ZEB2, or chromosomal microarray to detect deletions","Rectal suction biopsy and contrast studies to confirm Hirschsprung disease when suspected","Brain MRI to assess the corpus callosum and other structural anomalies","Electroencephalography for seizures","Echocardiography for congenital heart disease","Renal tract ultrasound and genitourinary assessment"],"redFlags":["Bilious vomiting, abdominal distension or enterocolitis suggesting Hirschsprung-related obstruction","Prolonged or refractory seizures","Undiagnosed congenital heart disease causing cardiorespiratory compromise","Signs of Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis, a potentially life-threatening complication"],"prognosis":"Individuals typically have severe intellectual disability with lifelong dependency but a characteristically cheerful temperament; survival into adulthood is common with appropriate management of bowel, cardiac and neurological complications. Enterocolitis and cardiac disease are the main threats to life.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing isolated Hirschsprung disease without recognising an associated syndrome","Missing corpus callosum agenesis that supports the diagnosis","Overlooking genitourinary anomalies such as hypospadias","Failing to consider ZEB2 testing when the facial gestalt is present without overt bowel disease"],"management":["Manage Hirschsprung disease surgically by resecting the aganglionic segment, and treat severe chronic constipation with an intensive bowel regimen","Maintain vigilance for enterocolitis, a leading and potentially fatal complication requiring prompt treatment","Control epilepsy with anticonvulsants and monitor with electroencephalography","Assess and surgically repair congenital heart disease and genitourinary anomalies such as hypospadias","Provide neurodevelopmental and communication support with early intervention, special education, alternative communication and physiotherapy","Offer genetic counselling, noting the autosomal dominant and almost always de novo nature with a small recurrence risk from germline mosaicism","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across paediatric surgery, cardiology, neurology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","GeneReviews: Mowat-Wilson syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"moyamoya_disease":{"name":"Moyamoya disease","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["moyamoya arteriopathy","spontaneous occlusion of the circle of Willis","RNF213-associated moyamoya"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / cerebrovascular surgery literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Progressive stenosis and occlusion of the terminal internal carotid arteries and proximal circle of Willis","A fragile network of dilated basal collateral vessels produces the characteristic angiographic puff of smoke appearance","Bimodal presentation: children typically present with ischaemia and adults may present with intracranial haemorrhage from fragile collaterals","Hyperventilation, as with crying in children, can precipitate transient ischaemic symptoms by lowering carbon dioxide","The RNF213 susceptibility variant is strongly associated, particularly in East Asian populations","Moyamoya syndrome describes the same angiographic pattern secondary to conditions such as sickle cell disease, neurofibromatosis, or prior radiation","Surgical revascularisation to augment collateral flow is the mainstay of definitive treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Moyamoya disease is a chronic, progressive, non-atherosclerotic, non-inflammatory steno-occlusive arteriopathy of the distal internal carotid arteries and their proximal branches. Intimal thickening from smooth muscle cell proliferation and luminal narrowing reduce anterior circulation flow, prompting the growth of fragile lenticulostriate and leptomeningeal collateral networks that give the smoky angiographic appearance. These collaterals are prone to ischaemic failure and, being thin-walled and under haemodynamic stress, to rupture and haemorrhage. Genetic susceptibility, notably RNF213 variants, underlies many idiopathic cases.","additionalDifferentials":["Atherosclerotic intracranial stenosis","Intracranial vasculitis or primary angiitis of the CNS","Sickle cell cerebral vasculopathy","Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome","Radiation-induced arteriopathy","Fibromuscular dysplasia affecting intracranial vessels"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Digital subtraction angiography as the gold standard to demonstrate steno-occlusion and collateral networks","MRI and MR angiography showing basal collaterals and ischaemic or haemorrhagic lesions","Cerebral perfusion and vascular reserve studies to guide revascularisation","Testing for secondary causes such as sickle cell disease and RNF213 genotyping where appropriate","Assessment for associated conditions like neurofibromatosis or prior cranial irradiation"],"redFlags":["Intracranial haemorrhage from rupture of fragile collateral vessels","Recurrent transient ischaemic attacks or completed strokes","Ischaemic symptoms provoked by hyperventilation, especially in children","Rapid clinical or angiographic progression"],"prognosis":"Untreated moyamoya tends to progress with recurrent ischaemic or haemorrhagic events and cumulative neurological deficit; surgical revascularisation can reduce stroke risk and stabilise the disease, though outcome depends on the stage at diagnosis.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the arteriopathy for atherosclerosis and withholding revascularisation assessment","Aggressive blood pressure lowering that compromises collateral-dependent perfusion","Overlooking secondary causes when labelling the pattern as idiopathic disease","Failing to protect against hypocapnia during anaesthesia, which can precipitate ischaemia"],"management":["Surgical revascularisation, by direct superficial-temporal-to-middle-cerebral-artery bypass or indirect procedures such as encephaloduroarteriosynangiosis, is the definitive treatment to restore perfusion and reduce stroke risk","Give antiplatelet therapy such as aspirin to reduce ischaemic events in the ischaemic-predominant form","Avoid precipitants of ischaemia including hyperventilation, hypotension, hypocapnia and dehydration, and maintain euvolaemia and normocapnia in the perioperative period","Identify and treat secondary (moyamoya syndrome) causes, for example transfusion for sickle cell disease","Provide stroke rehabilitation with physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy after ischaemic or haemorrhagic events","Monitor with serial angiographic and cerebral perfusion imaging for disease progression","Consider RNF213 genotyping and counselling, particularly in East Asian patients, and assess relatives","Escalate urgently for new transient ischaemic attacks, stroke or intracranial haemorrhage"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"mpi_cdg":{"name":"MPI-CDG (CDG type Ib)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["phosphomannose isomerase deficiency","mannose phosphate isomerase deficiency","CDG-Ib","MPI"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["One of the few treatable congenital disorders of glycosylation, caused by biallelic MPI variants","Distinctive in that intellect and neurological development are usually spared, contrasting with PMM2-CDG","Dominant features are hepatic-intestinal: protein-losing enteropathy, hepatic fibrosis, chronic diarrhoea and failure to thrive","Coagulation disturbance produces both bleeding and thrombotic risk, with low antithrombin, protein C and protein S","Hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia can occur and may be a presenting problem","Serum transferrin analysis shows a type 1 (whole-glycan loss) pattern, indistinguishable from PMM2-CDG on screening","Confirmation is by mannose phosphate isomerase enzyme assay and MPI gene sequencing","Oral mannose supplementation bypasses the enzymatic block and can markedly improve the enteropathy and coagulopathy"],"pathophysiology":"MPI-CDG is caused by deficiency of phosphomannose isomerase, which interconverts fructose-6-phosphate and mannose-6-phosphate. Because mannose-6-phosphate is the precursor for GDP-mannose and the dolichol-linked oligosaccharide used in N-glycosylation, enzyme deficiency limits mannose supply and impairs assembly of the lipid-linked glycan precursor, producing hypoglycosylation of serum and membrane glycoproteins similar to PMM2-CDG. The block lies upstream of the point where free mannose enters the pathway, so providing exogenous mannose (phosphorylated directly to mannose-6-phosphate by hexokinase) restores substrate and largely corrects glycosylation, explaining the disorder's responsiveness to dietary mannose. Inheritance is autosomal recessive, and the phenotype centres on the gut, liver and coagulation rather than the brain.","additionalDifferentials":["PMM2-CDG and other type 1 CDGs (identical transferrin screening pattern)","Congenital enteropathies and protein-losing enteropathy of other cause","Congenital hepatic fibrosis or neonatal cholestatic liver disease","Congenital hyperinsulinism from channelopathies","Inherited thrombophilias and coagulation factor deficiencies","Coeliac disease and other malabsorptive states"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum transferrin isoelectric focusing/mass spectrometry (type 1 pattern)","Phosphomannose isomerase enzyme activity assay","MPI gene sequencing for confirmation","Coagulation panel with antithrombin, protein C, protein S","Serum albumin and stool alpha-1-antitrypsin for protein-losing enteropathy","Liver ultrasound/elastography and transaminases for fibrosis","Fasting glucose and insulin studies for hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia"],"redFlags":["Life-threatening gastrointestinal or surgical bleeding from coagulopathy","Venous thrombosis, including portal or cerebral sinus thrombosis","Severe hypoalbuminaemia with oedema from protein-losing enteropathy","Recurrent hypoglycaemia causing seizures or neurological injury","Progressive hepatic fibrosis leading to portal hypertension"],"prognosis":"With early recognition and dietary mannose supplementation the gastrointestinal and coagulation abnormalities often improve substantially and neurodevelopment is typically preserved, although hepatic fibrosis may progress and thrombotic risk persists.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish MPI-CDG from other type 1 CDGs, thereby missing a treatable disorder","Not trialling mannose supplementation because the transferrin screen looks like PMM2-CDG","Overlooking combined bleeding and thrombotic risk when planning procedures","Attributing chronic diarrhoea and hypoalbuminaemia to a primary gut disease alone","Missing hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia as a treatable complication"],"management":["Oral D-mannose supplementation is the specific, disease-modifying therapy, bypassing the enzyme block to restore glycosylation and correct the gastrointestinal and coagulation abnormalities","Support the protein-losing enteropathy with nutritional optimisation and albumin replacement, and treat chronic diarrhoea and failure to thrive","Correct the coagulopathy by replacing antithrombin and managing both bleeding and thrombotic complications, anticoagulating for thrombosis","Treat hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia, for example with diazoxide, and maintain glucose supply","Monitor hepatic fibrosis with liver imaging and elastography and manage portal complications","Give thromboprophylaxis around surgery and periods of immobility given the persistent thrombotic risk","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder and test at-risk relatives","Coordinate multidisciplinary metabolic, gastroenterology and haematology care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","Jaeken J, Inborn Errors of Metabolism: Diagnosis and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"mtdna_depletion_syndrome":{"name":"Mitochondrial DNA depletion syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Mitochondrial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MDS","MDDS","mtDNA depletion syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mitochondrial medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A genetically heterogeneous group of autosomal recessive disorders defined by a quantitative reduction in mitochondrial DNA copy number in affected tissues, causing multiple respiratory-chain enzyme deficiencies.","Caused by defects in nuclear genes that maintain mtDNA replication or the mitochondrial nucleotide pool, including POLG, TWNK, TK2, DGUOK, MPV17, SUCLA2, SUCLG1, RRM2B and TYMP.","Clinical forms are grouped as myopathic (TK2), encephalomyopathic (SUCLA2, SUCLG1, RRM2B), hepatocerebral (DGUOK, MPV17, POLG, TWNK) and neurogastrointestinal (TYMP, causing MNGIE).","Because complex II is entirely nuclear-encoded it is typically spared, whereas complexes I, III and IV that contain mtDNA-encoded subunits are deficient, a useful biochemical clue.","Alpers-Huttenlocher syndrome (POLG) combines intractable epilepsy, developmental regression and liver failure; valproate can precipitate fatal hepatotoxicity and should be avoided.","MNGIE (thymidine phosphorylase deficiency) features ptosis, external ophthalmoplegia, severe gastrointestinal dysmotility with cachexia, peripheral neuropathy and leukoencephalopathy.","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating reduced mtDNA content in muscle or liver alongside targeted gene or panel testing; the encephalomyopathic SUCLA2 and SUCLG1 forms show mild methylmalonic aciduria.","Management is largely supportive, but TK2 deficiency may respond to nucleoside or nucleotide substitution and MNGIE to therapies that lower circulating thymidine."],"pathophysiology":"Faithful maintenance of the mitochondrial genome depends on nuclear-encoded replication machinery, including the polymerase POLG and the TWNK helicase, and on a balanced supply of deoxyribonucleotides delivered by mitochondrial salvage and cytosolic pathways involving TK2, DGUOK, RRM2B, TYMP and SUCLA2/SUCLG1, with MPV17 supporting nucleotide homeostasis. Pathogenic variants either cripple mtDNA replication directly or distort the mitochondrial deoxyribonucleotide pool, leading to a progressive fall in mtDNA copy number. Because several essential respiratory-chain subunits are mtDNA-encoded, depletion produces a combined deficiency of complexes I, III, IV and V with relative preservation of the wholly nuclear-encoded complex II, impairing oxidative phosphorylation in the most energy-dependent tissues such as brain, muscle, liver and gut. The specific gene defect and the tissues in which depletion is most severe determine the clinical subtype.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary mtDNA point-mutation or deletion syndromes (Leigh, MELAS, Kearns-Sayre)","Other nuclear respiratory-chain assembly or subunit defects","Congenital myopathies and spinal muscular atrophy for the myopathic form","Neonatal or infantile liver failure of other cause (tyrosinaemia, galactosaemia, viral hepatitis)","Organic acidaemias, especially methylmalonic aciduria mimicking SUCLA2/SUCLG1 disease","Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction of other cause for MNGIE","Peripheral neuropathies and leukodystrophies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative mtDNA content (copy number) in muscle or liver","Respiratory-chain enzyme assays showing combined complex I, III and IV deficiency","Nuclear gene panel or exome for mtDNA-maintenance genes","Blood and CSF lactate, plasma amino acids and urine organic acids including methylmalonate","Plasma thymidine and deoxyuridine when MNGIE is suspected","Liver function and synthetic tests, with muscle biopsy and histochemistry","Brain MRI and nerve conduction studies as the phenotype dictates"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive infantile liver failure with encephalopathy","Intractable seizures or status epilepticus with regression (Alpers-POLG)","Progressive respiratory muscle weakness and ventilatory failure (TK2)","Severe gastrointestinal dysmotility with cachexia (MNGIE)","Exposure to valproate in a child with possible POLG-related disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies by subtype and gene: hepatocerebral and severe myopathic forms are frequently fatal in infancy or early childhood, whereas some presentations, including certain TK2 and later-onset forms, survive longer and are increasingly influenced by emerging targeted therapies.","pitfalls":["Giving valproate for seizures before excluding POLG-related disease, risking fatal liver failure.","Relying on blood mtDNA levels, which may be normal despite marked tissue depletion.","Overlooking the sparing of complex II as a diagnostic pointer.","Missing the mild methylmalonic aciduria that flags SUCLA2 and SUCLG1 forms.","Attributing MNGIE cachexia to a primary gastrointestinal disorder and delaying metabolic work-up."],"management":["For TK2-related myopathic disease use nucleoside/nucleotide substrate therapy with deoxycytidine and deoxythymidine as an emerging targeted treatment","Avoid mitochondrial toxins, critically avoiding sodium valproate in POLG-related Alpers-Huttenlocher disease because it can precipitate fatal hepatotoxicity, and use aminoglycosides cautiously","In MNGIE from thymidine phosphorylase deficiency, consider allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell or liver transplantation to restore enzyme activity, with aggressive, often parenteral, nutritional support for dysmotility and cachexia","Provide supportive care for epilepsy, nutrition and mobility, using non-hepatotoxic anticonvulsants and mitochondrial cofactors such as coenzyme Q10 and riboflavin","Consider liver transplantation selectively in hepatocerebral forms, weighing any neurological involvement","Monitor for progressive hepatic, neurological, gastrointestinal and cardiac decline","Offer genetic counselling for these autosomal recessive disorders with family testing","Coordinate multidisciplinary specialist care across metabolic medicine, neurology and hepatology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mitochondrial medicine reference texts","Inborn errors of metabolism reference texts","Paediatric neurology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"mthfr_deficiency":{"name":"Severe methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["severe MTHFR deficiency","methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase deficiency","homocystinuria due to MTHFR deficiency","MTHFR","5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive deficiency of methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase, the commonest inborn error of folate metabolism and a remethylation defect","Produces homocystinuria and raised plasma total homocysteine with a low or normal methionine, the opposite of classical cystathionine beta-synthase homocystinuria where methionine is high","There is no methylmalonic aciduria, which helps distinguish it from cobalamin remethylation defects such as cblC","Severe neonatal and infantile forms present with encephalopathy, apnoea, seizures, feeding difficulty, microcephaly and developmental delay","Later-onset disease can present with neurological deterioration, psychiatric features and thromboembolism","This severe monogenic deficiency is entirely distinct from the common thermolabile C677T polymorphism, which is not a metabolic disease","Betaine is the mainstay of treatment because it drives an alternative remethylation of homocysteine to methionine through betaine-homocysteine methyltransferase","Additional generic measures include folinic acid, vitamin B12 and, in some cases, methionine supplementation, with early treatment giving the best results"],"pathophysiology":"Methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase converts 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate to 5-methyltetrahydrofolate, the methyl donor required for the vitamin B12-dependent remethylation of homocysteine to methionine. When the enzyme is severely deficient, this methyl supply fails, so homocysteine accumulates and is excreted (homocystinuria) while methionine and S-adenosylmethionine, needed for methylation in the nervous system, fall. The combination of homocysteine toxicity, impaired methylation and reduced methionine is thought to drive the neurological injury, and the raised homocysteine also predisposes to vascular thrombosis. Betaine treatment provides an alternative route to regenerate methionine and lower homocysteine.","additionalDifferentials":["Classical homocystinuria (cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency)","Cobalamin remethylation defects such as cblC, cblD and cblE","Transcobalamin and dietary vitamin B12 deficiency","Cerebral folate deficiency of other cause","Other neonatal metabolic encephalopathies","Acquired hyperhomocysteinaemia from nutritional deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma total homocysteine (markedly raised)","Plasma amino acids showing low or normal methionine with the raised homocysteine","Urine organic acids to confirm absence of methylmalonic acid","Vitamin B12 and folate status to exclude nutritional causes","MTHFR gene sequencing for confirmation","Brain MRI and electroencephalography in the encephalopathic presentation"],"redFlags":["Neonatal encephalopathy with apnoea and seizures","Progressive neurological deterioration or psychiatric change","Thromboembolic events, including in unusual sites","Rapid decompensation during intercurrent catabolic illness"],"prognosis":"Outcome is strongly determined by how early betaine and adjunctive treatment begin; presymptomatic or early therapy can markedly improve neurological outcome, while late-treated severe disease often leaves significant impairment and carries thrombotic risk.","pitfalls":["Confusing the severe enzyme deficiency with the benign common C677T polymorphism","Expecting a high methionine as in classical homocystinuria and dismissing the diagnosis when it is low or normal","Not excluding cobalamin defects by checking for methylmalonic aciduria and B12 status","Delaying betaine, which is time-critical for neurological protection","Overlooking thromboembolism as a presenting feature in older patients"],"management":["Betaine is the mainstay, providing an alternative pathway to remethylate homocysteine to methionine, lowering toxic homocysteine and restoring methylation","Add adjunctive folate as folinic acid or 5-methyltetrahydrofolate, hydroxocobalamin and, where appropriate, methionine supplementation","Begin treatment as early as possible, ideally presymptomatically, as timing strongly determines neurological outcome","Manage the acute neonatal encephalopathy with treatment of seizures and apnoea and metabolic stabilisation","Address the raised thrombotic risk with prophylaxis during high-risk periods and anticoagulation for thromboembolism","Monitor plasma total homocysteine and methionine to titrate treatment","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, with family testing and early testing of at-risk newborns","Coordinate multidisciplinary metabolic and neurology care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID","Huemer M et al. Guidelines for remethylation disorders and severe MTHFR deficiency","Blau N. Physician's Guide to the Diagnosis, Treatment, and Follow-up of Inherited Metabolic Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mucinous_cystadenoma_ovary":{"name":"Ovarian mucinous cystadenoma","system":"Gynaecology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["benign mucinous ovarian tumour","mucinous cystadenoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign epithelial ovarian tumour lined by mucin-secreting, gastrointestinal-type columnar epithelium","Characteristically multiloculated and can grow very large, sometimes filling the abdomen, and is usually unilateral","Ultrasound typically shows a multilocular cyst with thin septations and mucinous fluid of varying echogenicity","Distinguished from borderline and malignant mucinous tumours by the absence of proliferation, atypia, and invasion; extensive sampling is needed because these features can be focal","Bilateral mucinous tumours should prompt a search for a gastrointestinal primary, as metastatic mucinous carcinoma can mimic a primary ovarian lesion","Rupture or spillage of mucinous contents is a concern given the association of mucinous tumours with pseudomyxoma peritonei, although most true pseudomyxoma arises from an appendiceal source","Managed by cystectomy or oophorectomy with care to avoid intraoperative rupture"],"pathophysiology":"Mucinous cystadenomas are benign surface-epithelial tumours lined by tall columnar cells containing abundant mucin, resembling endocervical or, more often, gastrointestinal epithelium. The epithelium is a single orderly layer without stratification, atypia, or stromal invasion, and copious mucin secretion generates a characteristically multiloculated cyst that can reach a very large size. Because malignant or borderline change may be focal within an otherwise benign tumour, thorough histological sampling is essential. Spillage of mucin-secreting epithelium can theoretically seed the peritoneum, but classic pseudomyxoma peritonei is now recognised to originate predominantly from a low-grade appendiceal mucinous neoplasm rather than from the ovary itself.","additionalDifferentials":["Mucinous borderline tumour or mucinous carcinoma","Metastatic gastrointestinal or appendiceal mucinous tumour","Serous cystadenoma","Mature cystic teratoma with a mucinous component","Endometrioma","Paraovarian cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal and abdominal ultrasound to demonstrate a multilocular mucinous cyst","Pelvic and abdominal cross-sectional imaging for large lesions and to assess the appendix and bowel","Tumour markers including CA125, CEA, and CA19-9 to aid stratification","Bowel evaluation when a metastatic mucinous tumour is suspected, especially if bilateral","Histology with extensive sampling to exclude borderline or malignant foci","Assessment of the appendix if pseudomyxoma or mucinous ascites is found"],"redFlags":["Solid areas, thick irregular septations, or ascites suggesting borderline or malignant change","Bilateral mucinous tumours raising concern for metastatic gastrointestinal disease","Mucinous ascites or peritoneal deposits suggesting pseudomyxoma peritonei","Acute pain from torsion or rupture of a large cyst"],"prognosis":"Excellent for genuinely benign lesions, which are cured by complete excision; the main caveats are the need for adequate sampling to exclude malignancy and avoidance of rupture given the association of mucinous disease with peritoneal spread.","pitfalls":["Inadequate sampling that misses a focal borderline or malignant component","Assuming a bilateral mucinous tumour is a primary ovarian lesion rather than a metastasis","Causing intraoperative rupture and spillage of mucinous contents","Attributing pseudomyxoma peritonei to the ovary without evaluating the appendix"],"management":["Complete surgical excision, by ovarian cystectomy or unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy, is the definitive and curative treatment","Remove the cyst intact and avoid rupture or spillage of mucinous contents to prevent peritoneal seeding","Submit the specimen for extensive histological sampling to exclude focal borderline or malignant change","Offer fertility-sparing surgery in younger women when the contralateral ovary and uterus can be preserved","If the tumour is bilateral or mucinous ascites is found, evaluate the appendix and bowel for a primary gastrointestinal mucinous neoplasm","Refer to gynaecological oncology if borderline or invasive features are identified","Arrange appropriate clinical and imaging follow-up after excision"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","Blaustein's Pathology of the Female Genital Tract"],"crossLinks":[]},"mucinous_cystic_neoplasm_pancreas":{"name":"Mucinous cystic neoplasm of the pancreas","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCN","mucinous cystadenoma of the pancreas","pancreatic mucinous cystic neoplasm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mucin-producing cystic neoplasm that arises almost exclusively in women, typically in middle age","Characteristically located in the body or tail of the pancreas and, unlike IPMN, does not communicate with the pancreatic duct","Defined histologically by an ovarian-type stroma beneath the mucinous epithelium, a hallmark distinguishing it from other cystic lesions","Usually a single, large, thick-walled cyst that may contain septa and peripheral (eggshell) calcification","Considered a premalignant lesion with potential for progression to invasive mucinous cystadenocarcinoma","Cyst fluid typically shows a raised CEA reflecting its mucinous nature","Because of malignant potential and the generally younger, surgically fit population, resection is commonly recommended, particularly for larger or symptomatic lesions or those with worrisome features"],"pathophysiology":"Mucinous cystic neoplasms are thought to develop from primitive stroma, giving rise to the characteristic ovarian-type stroma that supports an overlying mucin-secreting columnar epithelium. This distinctive stroma expresses hormone receptors, in keeping with the striking female predominance. The lesions form solitary, septated cysts that, in contrast to intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms, do not connect with the ductal system. The epithelium can progress through increasing grades of dysplasia to invasive cystadenocarcinoma, so the presence and extent of any invasive component determines behaviour; non-invasive lesions are cured by complete resection.","additionalDifferentials":["Branch-duct intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm","Serous cystadenoma","Pancreatic pseudocyst","Solid pseudopapillary neoplasm with cystic change","Cystic pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour","Lymphoepithelial cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI with MRCP to assess cyst morphology and confirm absence of ductal communication","Endoscopic ultrasound with fine-needle aspiration for fluid analysis and wall assessment","Cyst fluid CEA (typically elevated) and cytology","Cyst fluid molecular testing for KRAS mutation to support a mucinous diagnosis","Contrast-enhanced CT to detect mural nodules, solid components, and calcification","Serum CA 19-9 when malignancy is a concern"],"redFlags":["An enhancing mural nodule or solid component within the cyst","Rapid increase in size","Associated pancreatic duct dilatation or a discrete mass suggesting invasion","New symptoms such as pain, weight loss, or jaundice","Positive or atypical cytology"],"prognosis":"Non-invasive mucinous cystic neoplasms are cured by complete surgical resection with an excellent long-term outlook; once invasive cystadenocarcinoma has developed, prognosis depends on stage and resembles that of pancreatic adenocarcinoma.","pitfalls":["Confusing a mucinous cystic neoplasm with a benign serous cystadenoma or a pseudocyst","Assuming a body or tail cyst in a woman is benign without appropriate characterisation","Attributing the lesion to prior pancreatitis and mislabelling it a pseudocyst, delaying resection","Incomplete resection leaving residual neoplastic epithelium","Failing to appreciate that the absence of ductal communication helps separate it from IPMN"],"management":["Surgical resection, typically distal pancreatectomy for these body and tail lesions, is the definitive treatment and is curative when the lesion is non-invasive","Preserve the spleen where oncologically appropriate during distal pancreatectomy","Risk-stratify before surgery with cross-sectional imaging and endoscopic ultrasound with fine-needle aspiration, using cyst fluid CEA, cytology and KRAS testing","Consider imaging surveillance for small, low-risk lesions that do not meet resection criteria","When invasive cystadenocarcinoma is present, add adjuvant chemotherapy and manage as for pancreatic adenocarcinoma","Monitor for postoperative exocrine insufficiency and diabetes after pancreatic resection","Manage within a hepatobiliary and pancreatic multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","European evidence-based guidelines on pancreatic cystic neoplasms","WHO classification of tumours of the digestive system"],"crossLinks":[]},"muckle_wells_syndrome":{"name":"muckle wells syndrome","system":"Immunological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MWS","urticaria-deafness-amyloidosis syndrome","NLRP3-associated CAPS (intermediate)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Represents the intermediate-severity phenotype within the cryopyrin-associated periodic syndrome spectrum, sitting between familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome and neonatal-onset multisystem inflammatory disease.","Caused by autosomal dominant gain-of-function variants in NLRP3 encoding cryopyrin; de novo variants and somatic mosaicism both occur.","The rash is a neutrophilic urticarial dermatosis: migrating wheals that burn or sting rather than itch, are not relieved by antihistamines and are not consistently cold-provoked.","Progressive sensorineural hearing loss is a hallmark and a key reason for early recognition, as it can be halted by timely treatment.","Recurrent low-grade fever, fatigue, conjunctivitis, headache and large-joint arthralgia accompany the cutaneous features.","Reactive AA amyloidosis, presenting with proteinuria and progressive renal impairment, is the most serious long-term complication.","Interleukin-1 blockade produces a rapid and often dramatic response and can prevent progression of both hearing loss and amyloidosis when started early.","A raised neutrophil count and persistently elevated acute-phase reactants with serum amyloid A are typical even between overt flares."],"pathophysiology":"Gain-of-function variants in NLRP3 render the cryopyrin inflammasome hypersensitive so that it assembles with minimal or no exogenous trigger. Constitutive activation of caspase-1 drives excessive maturation and secretion of interleukin-1 beta and interleukin-18, producing systemic and neutrophil-rich tissue inflammation. In the cochlea, chronic interleukin-1-mediated inflammation damages the organ of hearing, accounting for progressive sensorineural deafness, while sustained hepatic serum amyloid A production leads to AA amyloid deposition, particularly in the kidney. Somatic mosaicism of NLRP3 can produce the phenotype despite a negative conventional germline genetic test.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome","Neonatal-onset multisystem inflammatory disease (CINCA)","Chronic spontaneous urticaria","Schnitzler syndrome","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis and adult-onset Still's disease","Other hereditary recurrent fevers such as familial Mediterranean fever"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["NLRP3 gene analysis including testing for somatic mosaicism when germline sequencing is negative","Pure-tone audiometry with periodic monitoring for sensorineural hearing loss","Skin biopsy demonstrating a perivascular and interstitial neutrophilic infiltrate","Acute-phase reactants including C-reactive protein and serum amyloid A","Urinalysis for proteinuria and tissue biopsy with Congo red staining if amyloidosis is suspected","Ophthalmological review for conjunctivitis and optic disc changes"],"redFlags":["New or worsening sensorineural hearing loss indicating active cochlear inflammation","Proteinuria heralding renal AA amyloidosis","Persistently elevated inflammatory markers despite apparent clinical quiescence","Papilloedema or visual symptoms suggesting evolution towards the severe end of the spectrum"],"prognosis":"Early and sustained interleukin-1 blockade controls inflammation, preserves hearing and markedly reduces the risk of amyloidosis, allowing a good quality of life. Delayed treatment risks irreversible deafness and renal failure from amyloidosis.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the neutrophilic urticarial rash for allergic urticaria and persisting with ineffective antihistamines","Attributing insidious hearing loss to unrelated causes and missing the therapeutic window","Concluding the diagnosis is excluded by a negative germline NLRP3 test when somatic mosaicism is possible","Underestimating amyloidosis risk because overt flares appear infrequent"],"management":["Interleukin-1 blockade is the mainstay, using anakinra, canakinumab or rilonacept to suppress the constitutively active inflammasome, control symptoms and preserve hearing","Start and sustain treatment early, before irreversible cochlear damage or amyloid deposition occurs","Monitor hearing with serial pure-tone audiometry and provide hearing aids or cochlear implants for established sensorineural loss","Surveil for reactive AA amyloidosis with urinalysis for proteinuria, renal function and serum amyloid A","Titrate the interleukin-1 inhibitor to keep acute-phase reactants suppressed, escalating dose if inflammation persists","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant NLRP3 disorder, testing for somatic mosaicism if germline sequencing is negative","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across rheumatology, audiology and nephrology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology (current edition)","SHARE recommendations for the management of autoinflammatory diseases","Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology, Cassidy (current edition)"],"crossLinks":[]},"mucolipidosis_type_2":{"name":"Mucolipidosis Type II (I-Cell Disease)","system":"Metabolic / Lysosomal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["I-cell disease","ML II alpha/beta","Inclusion-cell disease","GlcNAc-1-phosphotransferase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Mucolipidosis II is a severe autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disorder caused by biallelic GNPTAB mutations that abolish the activity of GlcNAc-1-phosphotransferase (its alpha and beta subunits).","Loss of this enzyme prevents attachment of the mannose-6-phosphate recognition marker to newly made lysosomal hydrolases, so the enzymes are mis-secreted into the circulation instead of being delivered to lysosomes.","The hallmark biochemical signature is markedly raised activity of multiple lysosomal enzymes in serum together with correspondingly deficient activity inside cultured fibroblasts.","Cultured fibroblasts contain dense phase-bright cytoplasmic inclusions, the 'inclusion cells' or I-cells that give the disorder its name.","The clinical picture resembles Hurler syndrome but begins prenatally or at birth, with coarse facial features, marked gingival hypertrophy, congenital hip dislocation, joint contractures, hernias and severe growth failure.","Unlike the mucopolysaccharidoses, urinary glycosaminoglycan excretion is normal or only slightly raised, which helps separate the two groups.","Dysostosis multiplex, thoracic deformity, cardiac valve thickening and recurrent respiratory infection are usual, and psychomotor development is severely impaired.","The prognosis is poor, with death commonly occurring in early childhood from cardiorespiratory complications."],"pathophysiology":"Newly synthesised soluble lysosomal hydrolases are normally tagged in the Golgi with mannose-6-phosphate, a marker recognised by receptors that divert the enzymes into the lysosomal pathway. The first and rate-limiting step in generating this tag is performed by the hexameric enzyme GlcNAc-1-phosphotransferase, whose catalytic alpha and beta subunits are encoded by GNPTAB. In mucolipidosis II biallelic loss-of-function mutations abolish transferase activity, so the recognition marker cannot be added; the untagged hydrolases default to the constitutive secretory pathway and are released into the extracellular space and bloodstream. The lysosome is consequently starved of numerous hydrolases at once, and undegraded substrates accumulate as heterogeneous storage material within enlarged lysosomes throughout mesenchymal and other tissues. Because many enzymes are affected simultaneously, the disorder produces a widespread multisystem phenotype affecting bone, connective tissue, heart, airway and the developing nervous system, and the mislocalised enzymes can be measured at high levels in plasma, providing a diagnostic clue.","additionalDifferentials":["Hurler syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis I) and other mucopolysaccharidoses","Infantile GM1 gangliosidosis","Sialidosis and galactosialidosis","Mucolipidosis III (the attenuated allelic disorder)","Congenital hypothyroidism causing coarse features","Infantile free sialic acid storage disease and other causes of neonatal dysostosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma or serum lysosomal enzyme panel showing multiple hydrolases markedly elevated","Lysosomal enzyme assays in cultured skin fibroblasts showing reduced intracellular activity","Urinary glycosaminoglycan and oligosaccharide or sialyl-oligosaccharide screen","Skeletal survey for dysostosis multiplex","Echocardiography for valvular and myocardial involvement","GNPTAB molecular genetic testing","Prenatal enzyme or molecular testing in at-risk pregnancies"],"redFlags":["Congenital hip dislocation, joint contractures and coarse facial features present at birth","Failure to thrive accompanied by recurrent respiratory infection","Signs of cardiac valve disease or heart failure in infancy","Progressive respiratory compromise from thoracic and airway involvement","Severe developmental stagnation or regression"],"prognosis":"Mucolipidosis II is relentlessly progressive and life-limiting, with most affected children dying within the first years of life from cardiorespiratory failure; management is supportive.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the disorder as a mucopolysaccharidosis and being falsely reassured by normal urinary glycosaminoglycans.","Overlooking the diagnosis because it presents at birth, earlier than classic Hurler syndrome.","Failing to send both serum (elevated) and fibroblast (deficient) enzyme studies, which together are diagnostic.","Attributing the coarse features to congenital hypothyroidism alone.","Assuming that enzyme replacement or haematopoietic stem cell transplantation offers the benefit seen in some mucopolysaccharidoses, as evidence here is very limited."],"management":["Care is supportive and palliative, as there is no disease-modifying therapy for this rapidly progressive disorder","Manage the respiratory burden with airway support, chest physiotherapy and prompt treatment of recurrent respiratory infections","Undertake cardiac surveillance with echocardiography for valvular and myocardial disease and treat cardiorespiratory failure","Provide orthopaedic and physiotherapy management for joint contractures and congenital hip dislocation, and surgical repair of hernias","Optimise nutrition and feeding for severe growth failure and manage gingival hypertrophy","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder with carrier testing and prenatal or preimplantation options","Coordinate multidisciplinary and palliative care with early involvement of the family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Oxford Textbook of Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"mucolipidosis_type_3":{"name":"Mucolipidosis Type III (Pseudo-Hurler Polydystrophy)","system":"Metabolic / Lysosomal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pseudo-Hurler polydystrophy","ML III alpha/beta","ML III gamma","GlcNAc-1-phosphotransferase partial deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Mucolipidosis III is the attenuated counterpart of I-cell disease, caused by partial deficiency of the same GlcNAc-1-phosphotransferase; the alpha/beta form results from GNPTAB mutations and the milder gamma form from GNPTG mutations.","Residual enzyme activity permits some mannose-6-phosphate tagging, so onset is later, usually in childhood, progression is slower and survival into adulthood is typical.","The presentation is dominated by progressive joint stiffness, particularly of the shoulders, hands and hips, with claw-hand deformity, short stature and slowly coarsening features.","Destructive hip dysplasia causing pain and early degenerative change is a prominent and often disabling feature.","Carpal tunnel syndrome, scoliosis and thickening of the aortic and sometimes mitral valves are common.","Intelligence is often normal or only mildly impaired, in contrast to the severe neurological involvement of mucolipidosis II.","As in mucolipidosis II, serum lysosomal enzyme activities are elevated while fibroblast activities are reduced, and urinary glycosaminoglycans are normal.","The GNPTG (gamma-subunit) form tends to be the mildest, sometimes presenting only with joint symptoms in later childhood or adolescence."],"pathophysiology":"Mucolipidosis III shares the same fundamental lesion as mucolipidosis II, namely deficient activity of GlcNAc-1-phosphotransferase, the Golgi enzyme that initiates synthesis of the mannose-6-phosphate marker used to route soluble hydrolases to the lysosome. Here, however, the mutations in GNPTAB or in the gamma-subunit gene GNPTG preserve partial catalytic activity, so a proportion of lysosomal enzymes is still correctly tagged and delivered, and the remainder is mis-secreted into the circulation. The consequence is a milder and more slowly evolving storage phenotype in which mesenchymal tissues, especially cartilage, bone, joint capsule, tendon and cardiac valves, accumulate undegraded material over years rather than months. This connective-tissue predominance accounts for the characteristic progressive joint stiffness, destructive hip disease, short stature and valvular thickening, while the relative sparing of the nervous system reflects the residual enzyme activity. The gamma subunit contributes mainly to substrate recognition rather than catalysis, which is why GNPTG-related disease is generally the least severe within the spectrum.","additionalDifferentials":["Mucolipidosis II (the severe allelic disorder)","Attenuated mucopolysaccharidoses such as MPS I-S (Scheie) or MPS VI","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis, because of the joint stiffness","Progressive pseudorheumatoid dysplasia","Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia and other skeletal dysplasias with hip involvement","Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum lysosomal enzyme panel (elevated) alongside fibroblast enzyme assays (reduced)","Urinary glycosaminoglycan and oligosaccharide screen","Radiographs or skeletal survey of the hips, spine and hands","Echocardiography for valvular disease","Nerve conduction studies where carpal tunnel syndrome is suspected","GNPTAB and GNPTG molecular genetic testing","Ophthalmological assessment for corneal changes and retinopathy"],"redFlags":["Progressive, painful hip destruction limiting mobility","Rapidly worsening cardiac valve disease","Median nerve symptoms from carpal tunnel compression","Respiratory restriction from thoracic or spinal deformity","Growth failure in a child with unexplained joint contractures"],"prognosis":"Mucolipidosis III progresses slowly; many patients survive into adulthood but develop significant joint disability, short stature and, in some, cardiac valve disease, so care is supportive and multidisciplinary.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the early joint stiffness for juvenile idiopathic arthritis and treating with immunosuppression.","Being falsely reassured by normal urinary glycosaminoglycans and preserved intellect.","Overlooking cardiac valve involvement that warrants periodic surveillance.","Failing to distinguish the milder GNPTG form from the alpha/beta form, which carries prognostic relevance.","Attributing carpal tunnel syndrome to occupation rather than the underlying storage disorder."],"management":["Management is supportive, as there is no disease-modifying therapy, and centres on the progressive connective-tissue and skeletal disease","Provide orthopaedic care for destructive hip dysplasia, including joint replacement, alongside analgesia for pain","Use physiotherapy and occupational therapy to preserve joint mobility and function against progressive stiffness and contractures","Perform carpal tunnel decompression for median nerve entrapment and manage scoliosis with bracing or surgery","Undertake echocardiographic surveillance and manage aortic and mitral valve disease","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder from GNPTAB or GNPTG variants, with family testing","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across orthopaedics, cardiology, rehabilitation and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Oxford Textbook of Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"mucolipidosis_type_4":{"name":"Mucolipidosis type IV","system":"Metabolic / Neurometabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MLIV","ML4","Mucolipin-1 deficiency","MCOLN1-related disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Inborn errors of metabolism references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive lysosomal disorder caused by biallelic MCOLN1 variants encoding the endolysosomal cation channel mucolipin-1 (TRPML1); markedly enriched in the Ashkenazi Jewish population through founder alleles.","Presents in infancy with severe global developmental delay that plateaus early; most affected children never achieve independent walking or spoken language in the classic form.","Progressive visual impairment from corneal clouding combined with retinal degeneration and optic atrophy is a defining feature and often the earliest recognised sign.","Constitutive achlorhydria drives a compensatory rise in plasma gastrin and secondary iron-deficiency anaemia — a highly characteristic and easily overlooked biochemical clue.","Unlike mucolipidoses II/III and the mucopolysaccharidoses, there is no organomegaly, no coarse facies and no dysostosis multiplex, which is a valuable discriminator.","Blood lysosomal enzyme activities are normal, so a standard enzyme panel is falsely reassuring; diagnosis rests on MCOLN1 sequencing.","Brain MRI typically shows hypomyelination with a thin corpus callosum, later cerebellar and cerebral atrophy, and sometimes iron-related signal change.","Attenuated variants with residual channel function produce milder, later-evolving disease and can be mistaken for cerebral palsy."],"pathophysiology":"Loss-of-function variants in MCOLN1 abolish mucolipin-1 (TRPML1), a transient receptor potential cation channel situated in the membranes of late endosomes and lysosomes that conducts calcium, iron and zinc. Deficient channel activity disrupts lysosomal calcium signalling, membrane trafficking, autophagosome-lysosome fusion and lysosomal reformation, so autophagic clearance stalls and heterogeneous material accumulates within the endolysosomal compartment. The stored substrate is characteristically mixed — gangliosides and other phospholipids together with mucopolysaccharides — producing autofluorescent granular and lamellar inclusions in many cell types including conjunctival and neural tissue. The same channel is required for normal gastric parietal cell secretory function, so its loss produces the constitutive achlorhydria that underlies hypergastrinaemia and iron malabsorption. Progressive neuronal and glial dysfunction, impaired myelination and retinal photoreceptor loss follow, generating the neurodevelopmental and ophthalmological phenotype without the visceromegaly or skeletal changes typical of other storage disorders.","additionalDifferentials":["Cerebral palsy (static encephalopathy) — but mucolipidosis type IV is neurodegenerative rather than static","GM1 and GM2 gangliosidoses and other lysosomal storage disorders","Hypomyelinating leukodystrophies including Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease","Neuroaxonal dystrophies and neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation","Leigh syndrome and other mitochondrial encephalopathies","Other causes of infantile corneal clouding (mucopolysaccharidoses, congenital glaucoma, congenital corneal dystrophy)","Congenital retinal dystrophies such as Leber congenital amaurosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MCOLN1 molecular genetic testing, including targeted founder-variant analysis in individuals of Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry","Plasma gastrin (characteristically raised) as a screening marker of the constitutive achlorhydria","Full blood count and iron studies demonstrating iron-deficiency anaemia","Brain MRI for hypomyelination, thin corpus callosum, cerebellar/cerebral atrophy and iron-related signal","Ophthalmological assessment with slit-lamp examination, electroretinography and optic nerve evaluation","Electron microscopy of conjunctival or skin biopsy showing autofluorescent lamellar/granular inclusions (historical confirmatory approach)","Standard blood lysosomal enzyme panel (expected normal) to exclude mimicking storage disorders"],"redFlags":["Progressive loss of vision with corneal clouding in an infant or young child","Developmental regression or failure to progress beyond an early plateau","Refractory iron-deficiency anaemia unresponsive to oral iron","Emerging spasticity, dystonia or new movement disorder signalling neurological progression"],"prognosis":"The classic form follows a slowly progressive course with profound lifelong neurodevelopmental disability and visual loss, though many patients survive into adulthood; later cognitive and motor decline may occur. No disease-modifying therapy exists, so management is supportive and directed at vision, nutrition, anaemia and neurodisability.","pitfalls":["Labelling the child as cerebral palsy and missing a progressive, genetically defined disorder","Expecting organomegaly, coarse features or bony changes, whose absence is actually typical and should not exclude the diagnosis","Being falsely reassured by a normal blood lysosomal enzyme panel","Overlooking the hypergastrinaemia and iron-deficiency anaemia that provide an important diagnostic clue","Missing subtle corneal clouding that requires formal slit-lamp examination to detect"],"management":["Care is supportive and multidisciplinary, as no disease-modifying therapy exists","Provide ophthalmological management for corneal clouding with lubrication, monitor progressive retinal degeneration and supply low-vision aids and support","Treat the iron-deficiency anaemia arising from achlorhydria with iron supplementation","Support neurodevelopment with physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy, manage spasticity with agents such as baclofen and provide feeding support","Treat seizures with anticonvulsants where they occur","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, including carrier screening in those of Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across neurology, ophthalmology and rehabilitation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inborn errors of metabolism reference texts (standard reference)","Paediatric neurology reference texts (standard reference)","Lysosomal storage disorder clinical references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"mucolipidosis_type_iv":{"name":"Mucolipidosis type IV","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MLIV","MCOLN1-related mucolipidosis","mucolipidin-1 deficiency","TRPML1 deficiency","sialolipidosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID) / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disorder caused by MCOLN1 mutations affecting the endolysosomal cation channel mucolipin-1 (TRPML1), with high carrier frequency in the Ashkenazi Jewish population","Presents in infancy with delayed psychomotor development, hypotonia progressing to spasticity, and corneal clouding that may be mistaken for congenital infection or metabolic keratopathy","Progressive retinal degeneration leads to visual impairment over the first two decades, distinguishing it from static causes of infantile visual loss","A characteristic biochemical clue is constitutively elevated serum gastrin due to defective gastric parietal cell acid secretion (achlorhydria), often with a raised iron-deficiency picture","Unlike most storage disorders there is usually no organomegaly, coarse facies, or skeletal dysostosis, so it can be overlooked in the mucolipidosis/mucopolysaccharidosis differential","Diagnosis rests on MCOLN1 molecular testing supported by suggestive biochemistry; management is supportive and multidisciplinary rather than disease-modifying"],"pathophysiology":"Mucolipidosis type IV results from loss-of-function mutations in MCOLN1, encoding mucolipin-1 (TRPML1), a transient receptor potential cation channel located in late endosomes and lysosomes. Impaired channel function disrupts lysosomal calcium and iron handling, membrane trafficking, and autophagosome-lysosome fusion, so heterogeneous lipids and mucopolysaccharide-like material accumulate within enlarged storage vacuoles. Neurons, retinal cells, and gastric parietal cells are particularly vulnerable, producing the combination of neurodevelopmental impairment, retinal degeneration, corneal clouding, and achlorhydria with reactive hypergastrinaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Other lysosomal storage disorders (mucolipidosis II/III, mucopolysaccharidoses)","Congenital corneal clouding from congenital infection or metabolic keratopathy","Leukodystrophies and other causes of infantile spastic tetraparesis","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses causing progressive visual and neurological decline","Cerebral palsy of perinatal origin (static rather than progressive)","Congenital retinal dystrophies such as Leber congenital amaurosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MCOLN1 gene sequencing and targeted variant testing (including common Ashkenazi founder mutations)","Serum gastrin (characteristically elevated) with gastric pH/acid studies suggesting achlorhydria","Full ophthalmological assessment with electroretinography and slit-lamp examination for corneal clouding","Brain MRI, which may show hypoplasia or dysgenesis of the corpus callosum and white-matter changes","Iron studies for associated iron-deficiency anaemia","Electron microscopy of conjunctival, skin, or other cells showing lamellar and granular storage inclusions"],"redFlags":["Progressive rather than static loss of vision or motor skills, indicating a neurodegenerative process","Corneal clouding in an infant that mandates urgent metabolic and genetic evaluation","Unexplained iron-deficiency anaemia with markedly raised gastrin prompting achlorhydria workup","Developmental regression signalling advancing neurological disease"],"prognosis":"The disorder is slowly progressive; most affected individuals never develop speech beyond a few words and reach a developmental plateau in childhood, with visual function declining to severe impairment or blindness by early adulthood. Life expectancy can extend into adulthood with supportive care, though severe neurological disability persists.","pitfalls":["Attributing early hypotonia and developmental delay to cerebral palsy and missing the progressive storage disorder","Overlooking the diagnosis because the classic storage-disease features of organomegaly and coarse facies are absent","Not recognising raised gastrin and iron-deficiency anaemia as diagnostic pointers","Delaying ophthalmological review so that treatable contributors to visual loss are not addressed"],"management":["Management is supportive and multidisciplinary, with no disease-modifying treatment available","Manage visual impairment with ophthalmology input for corneal clouding and progressive retinal degeneration, providing low-vision rehabilitation","Correct the iron-deficiency anaemia due to achlorhydria with iron replacement","Provide developmental support and manage evolving spasticity and hypotonia with physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy and antispasticity agents such as baclofen","Give feeding and nutritional support, and treat seizures with anticonvulsants if present","Offer genetic counselling and carrier testing for this autosomal recessive disorder, with founder-variant screening in Ashkenazi Jewish families","Coordinate care across neurology, ophthalmology, rehabilitation and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"mucopolysaccharidosis":{"name":"Mucopolysaccharidosis","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPS","mucopolysaccharidoses","glycosaminoglycan storage disorders"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard metabolic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of lysosomal storage disorders caused by deficiency of specific enzymes needed to degrade glycosaminoglycans (mucopolysaccharides), leading to their accumulation in tissues and excretion in urine","Most subtypes are inherited in an autosomal recessive manner; the notable exception is MPS II (Hunter syndrome), which is X-linked recessive","Shared features across many subtypes include coarse facial features, short stature, joint stiffness or contractures, hepatosplenomegaly, and dysostosis multiplex on radiographs","Corneal clouding is prominent in several subtypes (for example MPS I and MPS VI) but characteristically absent in MPS II and in the neuronopathic MPS III","Neurocognitive decline is a major feature of severe MPS I, MPS II, and especially MPS III (Sanfilippo), whereas MPS IV (Morquio) primarily affects the skeleton with preserved intellect","Cardiac valve disease, airway narrowing, hearing loss, and carpal tunnel syndrome are common and progressive complications","Cervical spine instability and cord compression are important risks, particularly in Morquio syndrome","Enzyme replacement therapy exists for several subtypes, and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation can benefit selected patients, most notably severe MPS I when performed early"],"pathophysiology":"The mucopolysaccharidoses arise from deficiency of one of the lysosomal enzymes required for the stepwise degradation of glycosaminoglycans such as dermatan sulfate, heparan sulfate, keratan sulfate, and chondroitin sulfate. Incomplete breakdown leads to progressive accumulation of partially degraded glycosaminoglycans within lysosomes across many tissues, including connective tissue, cartilage, bone, cornea, heart valves, airways, and the central nervous system. This storage produces the progressive, multisystem, and often neurodegenerative phenotypes. The specific enzyme deficiency and the glycosaminoglycans that accumulate determine the clinical subtype.","additionalDifferentials":["Other lysosomal storage disorders such as the oligosaccharidoses and mucolipidoses","GM1 gangliosidosis with coarse features and skeletal changes","Skeletal dysplasias from other causes","Congenital hypothyroidism with coarse features","Multiple sulfatase deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinary glycosaminoglycan quantification and electrophoresis as a screening step","Specific lysosomal enzyme assays to identify the subtype","Molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis and guide counselling","Skeletal survey to demonstrate dysostosis multiplex and assess spine stability","Echocardiography, audiology, ophthalmology, and airway assessment for multisystem involvement"],"redFlags":["Signs of cervical spine instability or spinal cord compression","Progressive airway obstruction or obstructive sleep apnoea","Cardiac valve disease or heart failure","Rapid neurocognitive regression in neuronopathic subtypes"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies widely by subtype and severity. Severe neuronopathic forms progress to early disability and death, while attenuated forms are compatible with longer survival. Enzyme replacement and, for selected patients, stem cell transplantation can slow somatic disease, but established skeletal and neurological damage is often irreversible and does not fully respond to enzyme therapy.","pitfalls":["Missing early disease because features can be subtle in infancy and evolve over time","Overlooking cervical spine risk before anaesthesia or intubation","Assuming enzyme replacement will reverse neurological or advanced skeletal disease","Not recognising the X-linked inheritance of MPS II when counselling families"],"management":["Give enzyme replacement therapy for the subtypes where it exists, using laronidase for MPS I, idursulfase for MPS II, galsulfase for MPS VI and elosulfase alfa for MPS IVA, to slow somatic disease","Consider haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for severe MPS I (Hurler) in early infancy to preserve neurocognition","Manage the airway carefully, treating obstructive disease and anticipating a difficult airway and cervical instability during anaesthesia","Undertake cardiac surveillance with echocardiography and manage progressive valve disease","Provide orthopaedic and spinal surgery for dysostosis multiplex, cervical cord compression and carpal tunnel syndrome","Address hearing loss with aids, treat hydrocephalus with shunting and provide ophthalmological care for corneal clouding","Offer genetic counselling, noting most subtypes are autosomal recessive while MPS II (Hunter) is X-linked recessive","Coordinate lifelong multidisciplinary specialist care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"mucopolysaccharidosis_type_9":{"name":"Mucopolysaccharidosis type IX","system":"Metabolic / Lysosomal storage disorder","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPS IX","Natowicz syndrome","Hyaluronidase deficiency","HYAL1 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The rarest of the mucopolysaccharidoses, caused by deficiency of lysosomal hyaluronidase (HYAL1), leading to accumulation of hyaluronan rather than the sulfated glycosaminoglycans that accumulate in the other MPS subtypes.","The hallmark is nodular periarticular soft-tissue masses around large joints, which may wax and wane with episodes of painful swelling.","Unlike Hurler, Hunter and most other MPS types, cognition and neurodevelopment are characteristically preserved, and there is typically no corneal clouding or coarse facies.","Mild short stature and only subtle facial changes may occur; visceromegaly is characteristically absent.","Because standard urinary screening detects sulfated glycosaminoglycans, conventional MPS urine screens can be normal, which is a major diagnostic pitfall.","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating deficient hyaluronidase activity together with raised circulating hyaluronan, confirmed by HYAL1 molecular testing.","Excised nodules show storage-laden histiocytes and macrophages, and lesions may be mistaken for tumour or inflammatory arthritis.","No specific enzyme replacement therapy is available; care is supportive and multidisciplinary."],"pathophysiology":"Mucopolysaccharidosis type IX is an autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disorder caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in HYAL1 on chromosome 3p21.3, which encodes lysosomal hyaluronidase 1. Deficient enzyme activity impairs degradation of hyaluronan (hyaluronic acid), a large non-sulfated glycosaminoglycan of the extracellular matrix, causing its accumulation within lysosomes and periarticular connective tissue. The resulting histiocytic and macrophage storage produces nodular soft-tissue masses, predominantly around large joints, with relatively little visceral, skeletal or neurological involvement. Crucially, because hyaluronan is not sulfated, it escapes detection by the conventional urinary glycosaminoglycan electrophoresis used to screen for other MPS subtypes, explaining why routine screening may appear falsely normal.","additionalDifferentials":["Other mucopolysaccharidoses (types I to VII)","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Multicentric reticulohistiocytosis","Tumoral calcinosis","Gouty tophi","Synovial osteochondromatosis","Other lysosomal storage disorders with soft-tissue deposits"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma or serum hyaluronan concentration","Leukocyte or fibroblast hyaluronidase enzyme assay","HYAL1 molecular genetic testing","Ultrasound and MRI of periarticular masses","Histology of an excised nodule looking for storage-laden histiocytes","Urinary glycosaminoglycan analysis to exclude other MPS subtypes","Skeletal survey to assess acetabular and periarticular changes"],"redFlags":["Recurrent painful periarticular swellings misinterpreted as an inflammatory arthropathy","A normal urinary glycosaminoglycan screen giving false reassurance","Soft-tissue masses prompting biopsy under suspicion of malignancy","Short stature accompanied by preserved cognition and no coarse facies"],"prognosis":"Generally a mild mucopolysaccharidosis with preserved cognition and a near-normal lifespan, morbidity arising mainly from recurrent periarticular masses and joint symptoms. Because so few cases have been described, long-term outcome data remain limited.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal urinary glycosaminoglycan screen excludes an MPS","Misdiagnosing periarticular masses as neoplasm or juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Overlooking the diagnosis because it is exceptionally rare","Expecting corneal clouding, coarse facies or organomegaly, which are usually absent"],"management":["Management is supportive, as no enzyme replacement therapy is available for hyaluronidase deficiency","Treat painful periarticular soft-tissue masses with analgesia and anti-inflammatory agents, and surgically excise symptomatic or disabling nodules","Provide physiotherapy and joint-directed care for the recurrent painful swelling around large joints","Monitor for recurrence of periarticular masses after excision","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder with family testing","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across clinical genetics, rheumatology and orthopaedics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Lysosomal storage disorder reference texts","Paediatric metabolic medicine references","Clinical genetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"mucormycosis":{"name":"Mucormycosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["zygomycosis","rhinocerebral mucormycosis"],"chapter":224,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1717","clinicalPearls":["Mucormycosis is a group of life-threatening infections caused by fungi of the order Mucorales (subphylum Mucoromycotina); it is highly invasive and relentlessly progressive, with morbidity/mortality higher than many other infections (Harrison 22e p.1717).","Rhizopus oryzae and R. delemar (family Mucoraceae) are by far the most common causes in the Western Hemisphere; Apophysomyces elegans is a notable cause in India and Mucor irregularis in China (Harrison 22e p.1718).","Five well-defined clinical syndromes: rhino-orbital-cerebral, pulmonary, cutaneous, gastrointestinal, and disseminated; specific host defects predispose to specific syndromes (diabetes/DKA to rhino-orbital-cerebral; leukemia/HSCT to pulmonary) (Harrison 22e p.1718).","Rhino-orbital-cerebral disease is the most common form worldwide; early symptoms are nonspecific (eye/facial pain, facial numbness) progressing to conjunctival suffusion, blurry vision, proptosis, and chemosis (Harrison 22e p.1718-1719).","Biopsy with histopathology is the most sensitive and specific modality: wide (>=6-30 microm), thick-walled, ribbon-like, ASEPTATE hyphae branching at RIGHT angles, in contrast to thinner, septate, acute-angle-branching Aspergillus/Fusarium/Scedosporium (Harrison 22e p.1719).","Mucorales are best visualized with periodic acid-Schiff or H&E; methenamine silver may NOT stain them optimally (Harrison 22e p.1719).","Tissue for culture should be cut into sections and placed in the center of culture media, NOT homogenized, because homogenization kills the long filamentous hyphae and lowers culture yield; the lab should be alerted when mucormycosis is suspected (Harrison 22e p.1720).","It is important to distinguish mucormycosis from aspergillosis because treatments differ: polyenes for Mucorales versus expanded-spectrum triazoles for most septate molds; voriconazole is the first-line treatment for aspergillosis but EXACERBATES mucormycosis in animal models (Harrison 22e p.1719-1720)."],"pathophysiology":"Mucorales are ubiquitous environmental fungi to which humans are constantly exposed; they cause invasive, angioinvasive infection primarily in hosts with uncontrolled diabetes, phagocytic defects (neutropenia or high-dose corticosteroids), and/or elevated free iron, which supports fungal growth in serum and tissue. Iron-overloaded end-stage renal failure patients treated with the iron chelator deferoxamine had high risk of rapidly fatal disseminated disease, because deferoxamine acts as a fungal xenosiderophore that delivers iron directly to the Mucorales. In diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), acidosis dissociates iron from sequestering proteins (enhancing fungal survival/virulence) and the ketoacid beta-hydroxybutyrate upregulates host and fungal receptors that increase fungal adherence and tissue penetration; however the majority of diabetic patients with mucormycosis are NOT acidotic on presentation. Even without acidosis, hyperglycemia raises risk via hyperglycation of iron-sequestering proteins, upregulation of the host receptor GRP78 (which binds Mucorales and enables tissue penetration), poorly characterized phagocytic defects, and enhanced expression of the Mucorales-specific invasin CotH (which binds GRP78). Blood vessel thrombosis and tissue necrosis impair antifungal penetration to the site of infection. (Harrison 22e p.1717-1718)","additionalDifferentials":["Aspergillosis","Scedosporiosis (Scedosporium species)","Fusariosis (Fusarium species)","Dematiaceous (brown-pigmented) mold infections","Entomophthoromycosis (Basidiobolus and Conidiobolus)","Bacterial orbital cellulitis / cavernous sinus thrombosis (Staphylococcus aureus, streptococci, gram-negative species)","Rhinoscleroma (Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis)","Tolosa-Hunt syndrome"],"infectionMimics":["Aspergillosis (septate mold, may co-infect with Mucorales)","Scedosporiosis","Fusariosis","Dematiaceous mold infection","Entomophthoromycosis (Basidiobolus, Conidiobolus)","Bacterial orbital cellulitis / cavernous sinus thrombosis (S. aureus, streptococci, gram-negatives)","Rhinoscleroma (Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Tolosa-Hunt syndrome (painful ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, headache, cavernous sinus inflammation; lacks progression)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy with histopathologic examination (most sensitive/specific; PAS or H&E staining)","Culture from a sterile site (needle aspirate, tissue biopsy, pleural fluid) for definitive species identification; tissue sectioned NOT homogenized, incubated at both room temperature and 37C","CT of the chest (best method to define extent of pulmonary disease; may detect infection before chest x-ray)","MRI of head/sinuses (more sensitive ~80% than CT for orbital and CNS extension)","Endoscopy and/or surgical exploration with biopsy of suspected areas; blind biopsy of normal-appearing sinus tissue when suspicion is high","Mucorales-specific PCR (useful but no FDA-approved assay)"],"redFlags":["Rhino-orbital-cerebral involvement with a black necrotic eschar and/or painful necrotic ulceration of the hard palate (late finding indicating extensive, well-established angioinvasive infection) is a surgical emergency requiring urgent debridement.","Bilateral proptosis, chemosis, vision loss, and ophthalmoplegia (contralateral eye involvement) suggest cavernous sinus thrombosis and are ominous.","Hyperglycemia or DKA with sinusitis and possible orbital extension, even without a known diabetes history, should raise high suspicion (presentation may be the first recognition of diabetes, often unmasked by recent glucocorticoids).","Necrotizing fasciitis from cutaneous mucormycosis (mortality approaching 80%) and necrotic skin lesions from hematogenous dissemination.","Disseminated disease (most lethal form), seen with antifungal prophylaxis breakthrough and in highly immunocompromised patients."],"prognosis":"Overall mortality remains high though it has declined with earlier initiation of effective antifungal therapy; outcome is often driven by progression of the underlying predisposing condition. Necrotizing fasciitis from cutaneous disease carries mortality approaching 80%, gastrointestinal disease progressing to perforation has extremely high mortality, and widely disseminated disease exceeds 90% mortality. In contrast, isolated nonnecrotizing cutaneous mucormycosis treated with prompt aggressive surgical debridement has a favorable prognosis and low mortality. Rate of progression is highly variable (days to a year or more), not invariably rapid. (Harrison 22e p.1717-1720)","pitfalls":["Misperception that mucormycosis is always rapidly progressive; rate of progression is in fact highly variable (days to a year or more).","Cultures are positive in fewer than half of cases, and up to half of cases are diagnosed only at autopsy; a negative culture does NOT exclude the diagnosis.","Standard tissue homogenization for culture kills the filamentous Mucorales and lowers yield; tissue must be sectioned and not homogenized, and the lab alerted.","Imaging underestimates disease: sinus CT may look like ordinary bacterial sinusitis, and bony erosion into the orbit is rarely seen on CT even when clinically present.","Treating with voriconazole (first-line for aspergillosis) can exacerbate mucormycosis; distinguish the two molds because preferred treatments differ, and consider dual infection in highly compromised patients.","Delaying treatment of the underlying malignancy until mucormycosis is 'cured' is a common error; antifungals provide only a holding action until near-normal immunity is restored, and untreated underlying disease is the typical primary driver of death.","Iron administration and liberal blood transfusion (free iron from hemolysis) should be avoided as iron exacerbates infection.","Therapy should not be delayed for diagnostic confirmation: when suspicion is reasonable, empirical lipid polyene therapy should be started early because earlier polyene therapy improves survival."],"management":["Urgent liposomal amphotericin B as first-line antifungal, with isavuconazole or posaconazole for step-down or salvage","Early and repeated surgical debridement of all necrotic, devitalised tissue","Aggressive reversal of predisposing factors: correct diabetic ketoacidosis and hyperglycaemia, discontinue deferoxamine, taper corticosteroids and immunosuppression, and reverse neutropenia","Supportive care with glycaemic control and correction of acidosis and electrolyte derangement","Serial cross-sectional imaging (MRI and CT) to define extent and monitor response, with prolonged antifungal therapy guided by clinical and radiological resolution","Multidisciplinary management involving infectious diseases, ENT and surgical specialties, ophthalmology and haematology","Escalate to combined medical and surgical intensification if disease progresses despite therapy"],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies mucormycosis by clinical syndrome rather than a numeric severity score: rhino-orbital-cerebral, pulmonary, cutaneous (ranging from isolated nonnecrotizing cutaneous disease with favorable prognosis to necrotizing fasciitis), gastrointestinal, and disseminated/miscellaneous disease, with mortality rising steeply from localized cutaneous disease to disseminated disease (>90%). (Harrison 22e p.1718-1720)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1717"],"crossLinks":[]},"mucous_cyst_finger":{"name":"Mucous cyst of the finger","system":"musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["digital mucous cyst","distal interphalangeal ganglion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mucous cyst is a ganglion arising from the distal interphalangeal joint and is intimately associated with underlying osteoarthritis of that joint.","It typically presents in older adults as a small, dome-shaped, translucent swelling on the dorsum of the finger between the DIP joint and the nail fold.","Compression of the germinal nail matrix by the cyst commonly produces a longitudinal groove or ridge in the nail plate, a useful diagnostic clue.","An osteophyte at the DIP joint is almost always present and is considered a key driver; addressing it at surgery reduces recurrence.","Progressive attenuation of the overlying skin risks spontaneous rupture and a direct communication to the joint, creating a portal for septic arthritis or osteomyelitis.","Management ranges from observation to surgical excision with osteophyte debridement; excision that includes the joint pedicle and osteophyte offers the most durable results.","When skin over the cyst is thin or ulcerated, a local rotation or advancement flap may be needed to achieve stable soft-tissue cover.","Repeated aspiration or needling is associated with recurrence and carries a risk of introducing infection into the DIP joint, so it is generally discouraged."],"pathophysiology":"A digital mucous cyst is a ganglion of the distal interphalangeal joint that develops in the setting of osteoarthritis. Marginal osteophytes and degenerative capsular changes are thought to promote herniation of mucin-rich synovial fluid through a capsular defect, forming a mucin-filled pseudocyst connected to the joint by a pedicle. The cyst tracks dorsally between the extensor terminal tendon and the collateral structures towards the nail fold, where pressure on the germinal matrix disturbs nail growth. As with other ganglia the lesion lacks a true epithelial lining and recurs if the underlying joint communication and osteophyte are not addressed.","additionalDifferentials":["Heberden node of distal interphalangeal osteoarthritis","Dorsal wrist or digital ganglion arising elsewhere","Giant cell tumour of the tendon sheath","Epidermal inclusion cyst","Gouty tophus at the distal interphalangeal joint","Subungual or periungual tumour deforming the nail","Rheumatoid nodule"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical from the typical site, translucency and associated nail groove","Plain radiographs of the digit to demonstrate distal interphalangeal osteoarthritis and osteophytes","Ultrasound to confirm a cystic lesion and its joint connection when uncertain","MRI reserved for atypical or recurrent lesions","Transillumination of the cyst at the bedside","Assessment of overlying skin integrity to plan soft-tissue cover","Microbiological sampling if there is discharge or suspected secondary infection"],"redFlags":["Skin breakdown or discharge with signs of septic arthritis or osteomyelitis of the distal interphalangeal joint","Cellulitis or ascending infection of the digit","Rapidly enlarging or atypical solid mass suggesting alternative pathology","Marked nail plate destruction raising concern for a subungual tumour"],"prognosis":"Benign but linked to progressive distal interphalangeal osteoarthritis; surgical excision with osteophyte removal gives good durable results, whereas simple aspiration frequently recurs and the associated arthritis continues to progress.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly needling or aspirating the cyst, risking recurrence and inoculation of the DIP joint","Failing to remove the associated osteophyte, the principal cause of recurrence","Not recognising thin or ulcerated overlying skin that requires flap cover rather than simple closure","Overlooking an established communication with the joint that predisposes to septic arthritis","Attributing a nail deformity to a primary nail disorder rather than matrix compression by the cyst"],"management":["Observation for small, asymptomatic cysts with intact overlying skin","Surgical excision of the cyst with debridement of the distal interphalangeal osteophyte and ligation of the joint pedicle for a durable cure","Local rotation or advancement flap for soft-tissue cover where the overlying skin is attenuated","Avoid repeated needle aspiration or sclerotherapy, which frequently recur and risk seeding septic arthritis","Symptomatic management of the underlying distal interphalangeal osteoarthritis with analgesia and splinting","Monitor skin integrity and counsel prompt review if the cyst ruptures, given the risk of septic arthritis or osteomyelitis","Refer to hand surgery for excision when there is nail deformity, persistent pain or threatened skin"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","British Society for Surgery of the Hand guidance on digital ganglia"],"crossLinks":[]},"mucous_membrane_pemphigoid":{"name":"mucous membrane pemphigoid","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cicatricial pemphigoid","ocular cicatricial pemphigoid"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A heterogeneous group of subepithelial autoimmune blistering diseases with a predilection for mucous membranes and a marked tendency to scar.","The oral mucosa is most frequently involved (desquamative gingivitis is a common presenting sign), followed by ocular, nasopharyngeal, oesophageal, laryngeal and anogenital sites.","Ocular disease progresses through chronic conjunctivitis, subconjunctival fibrosis, symblepharon, forniceal shortening, entropion, trichiasis and ultimately corneal opacification and blindness.","Autoantibodies target multiple basement-membrane antigens, most commonly BP180 (particularly its C-terminus) and laminin-332; anti-laminin-332 disease carries an association with underlying malignancy.","Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional mucosa showing linear IgG, IgA and/or C3 along the basement-membrane zone is the diagnostic cornerstone; sensitivity rises with multiple biopsies.","The Nikolsky sign may be positive; healing with scarring distinguishes it clinically from bullous pemphigoid, which typically heals without scars.","Management is stratified by site and severity; high-risk (ocular, laryngeal, oesophageal, severe oral) disease warrants aggressive systemic immunosuppression to prevent irreversible scarring.","A multidisciplinary approach involving dermatology, ophthalmology, ENT, oral medicine and gastroenterology is essential for organ-threatening disease."],"pathophysiology":"Mucous membrane pemphigoid results from autoantibodies, predominantly IgG and IgA, directed against structural components of the epithelial basement-membrane zone, chiefly the BP180 (type XVII collagen) antigen and laminin-332 (epiligrin), and less often BP230, type VII collagen and the integrin beta-4 subunit. Antibody binding activates complement and recruits inflammatory cells, causing dermo-epidermal separation within the lamina lucida. Unlike bullous pemphigoid, the inflammatory infiltrate and cytokine milieu at mucosal sites drive a fibrotic reparative response, so repeated cycles of blistering and healing produce progressive submucosal scarring. The predilection for particular target antigens correlates loosely with clinical phenotype, and anti-laminin-332 reactivity is linked to paraneoplastic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["bullous pemphigoid","pemphigus vulgaris","erosive lichen planus","epidermolysis bullosa acquisita","linear IgA bullous dermatosis","paraneoplastic pemphigus","Stevens-Johnson syndrome / toxic epidermal necrolysis (chronic ocular sequelae)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["perilesional biopsy for direct immunofluorescence (linear IgG/IgA/C3 at the basement-membrane zone)","lesional biopsy for histopathology (subepithelial split with mixed infiltrate)","indirect immunofluorescence on salt-split skin (epidermal vs dermal binding)","serum ELISA/immunoblot for BP180, BP230 and laminin-332 antibodies","slit-lamp ophthalmological assessment and conjunctival forniceal measurement","nasendoscopy and laryngoscopy for upper-airway involvement","age-appropriate malignancy screening when anti-laminin-332 antibodies are detected"],"redFlags":["ocular involvement with progressive symblepharon or forniceal shortening threatening sight","laryngeal or supraglottic disease causing stridor or airway compromise","oesophageal stricture with dysphagia and risk of aspiration or malnutrition","anti-laminin-332 antibodies signalling possible occult malignancy","rapidly progressive scarring despite first-line therapy"],"prognosis":"The condition is chronic and relapsing; with early aggressive immunosuppression, scarring can often be arrested, but established fibrosis is irreversible and untreated organ-threatening disease may lead to blindness, airway stenosis or oesophageal stricture.","pitfalls":["Attributing desquamative gingivitis to poor oral hygiene or dental disease and delaying immunofluorescence biopsy.","Taking the biopsy from an ulcerated (rather than perilesional) site, yielding a false-negative direct immunofluorescence.","Overlooking asymptomatic early ocular disease because cutaneous involvement is minimal or absent.","Failing to screen for malignancy when laminin-332 antibodies are identified.","Under-treating so-called low-risk oral disease that later extends to high-risk sites."],"management":["Risk-stratified immunosuppression: topical or intralesional corticosteroids for limited oral disease, escalating to systemic corticosteroids with a steroid-sparing agent such as dapsone, mycophenolate mofetil or azathioprine","Cyclophosphamide with corticosteroids, or rituximab and intravenous immunoglobulin, for organ-threatening ocular, laryngeal or oesophageal involvement","Screen for glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency before dapsone and monitor blood counts during treatment","Meticulous oral hygiene and topical care to control desquamative gingivitis, with ocular surface lubrication and management of trichiasis and symblepharon","Investigate for occult malignancy where anti-laminin-332 antibodies are present","Surveillance for scarring complications, including ocular fibrosis and airway and oesophageal stricture, by slit-lamp, nasendoscopy, laryngoscopy and endoscopy","Multidisciplinary care coordinating dermatology, ophthalmology, ENT, oral medicine and gastroenterology","Early escalation of immunosuppression for progressive scarring to prevent irreversible blindness or airway and oesophageal stenosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine, current edition","International consensus on diagnosis and management of mucous membrane pemphigoid"],"crossLinks":[]},"muehrcke_lines":{"name":"Muehrcke lines","system":"Skin / Hair and nails","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Muehrcke's lines","Muehrcke bands"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Muehrcke lines are paired transverse white bands running parallel to the lunula across the nail, classically separated by strips of normal pink nail.","They are an apparent leukonychia arising in the nail bed, not the plate, so the bands do not move distally as the nail grows and disappear when the nail bed is compressed or a tourniquet is applied.","The classic association is hypoalbuminaemia, particularly nephrotic syndrome, and the lines may resolve if the serum albumin normalises.","They are also described with chemotherapy, malnutrition, chronic liver disease and other systemic illnesses that lower albumin or disturb nail bed vasculature.","Because the abnormality is in the bed rather than the plate, blanching with pressure is a useful bedside test that distinguishes them from true leukonychia and from Mees lines.","Mees lines, by contrast, are true plate leukonychia that do migrate distally and are linked to arsenic and thallium poisoning and severe systemic insults.","Muehrcke lines are asymptomatic and are a clinical sign to investigate rather than a disease to treat.","They typically affect several nails symmetrically, most often the second, third and fourth fingers."],"pathophysiology":"Muehrcke lines are a form of apparent leukonychia, meaning the whiteness originates in the nail bed rather than in the keratin of the nail plate. The prevailing explanation is a localised oedematous or vascular change in the nail bed, associated with hypoalbuminaemia, that alters the way light is transmitted through the normally translucent plate and produces the appearance of pale transverse bands. Because the plate itself is structurally normal, the bands remain fixed in position while the nail grows and vanish transiently when pressure empties the underlying vascular bed. When the provoking state, most commonly a low serum albumin, is corrected, the abnormal transmission of light resolves and the lines fade. This mechanism cleanly separates them from true leukonychia, in which the plate keratin is abnormal and the white mark migrates distally.","additionalDifferentials":["Mees lines (true transverse leukonychia)","Beau lines (transverse grooves)","Half-and-half (Lindsay) nails","Terry nails","True punctate or transverse leukonychia","Transverse traumatic leukonychia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum albumin and total protein","Renal function and urinalysis for proteinuria/nephrotic syndrome","Liver function tests","Full nutritional assessment","Blanch test with digital pressure to confirm bed origin","Review of recent chemotherapy or systemic illness"],"redFlags":["Newly appearing lines in the context of unexplained weight loss or systemic illness","Associated heavy proteinuria or oedema suggesting nephrotic syndrome","Signs of decompensated liver disease alongside the nail changes","Coexisting features that might instead indicate Mees lines and heavy-metal exposure"],"prognosis":"Muehrcke lines are themselves benign and often reversible, fading when the underlying hypoalbuminaemia or systemic illness is corrected. Their significance lies in flagging the underlying disorder, whose prognosis they follow.","pitfalls":["Confusing Muehrcke lines with Mees lines and missing heavy-metal poisoning, or vice versa","Failing to perform the blanch test that distinguishes bed-based from plate-based whiteness","Treating the nail sign rather than investigating the underlying cause","Assuming the lines will migrate out with the nail, which they do not"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying cause, most often correcting the hypoalbuminaemia that produces the bands","Investigate and manage nephrotic syndrome, chronic liver disease or malnutrition responsible for the low albumin","Nutritional support to optimise protein and albumin status","Review recent chemotherapy or systemic illness as a contributing factor","Reassure that the nail changes are themselves benign and often fade once serum albumin normalises","Monitor the lines as a clinical marker that parallels the course of the underlying disorder","Refer to the relevant specialty, such as nephrology or hepatology, according to the identified cause"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"mueller_weiss_syndrome":{"name":"Mueller-Weiss syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Muller-Weiss disease","Brailsford disease","adult tarsal navicular osteonecrosis","spontaneous osteonecrosis of the adult navicular"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A true osteonecrosis of the tarsal navicular occurring in adults, distinct from the benign childhood Kohler disease and from a traction apophysitis","Affected bone is the tarsal navicular, with the lateral portion characteristically compressed and fragmented","Typically presents in middle-aged adults and is more common in women","Presents with chronic midfoot and hindfoot pain and, characteristically, a paradoxical hindfoot varus rather than the flatfoot expected with medial column collapse","The radiographic hallmark is lateral compression and fragmentation of the navicular, producing a flattened, comma-shaped bone","In contrast to Kohler disease, the adult navicular does not spontaneously reconstitute and tends to progress","The altered mechanics predispose to talonavicular and naviculocuneiform degenerative change","Management ranges from orthoses, offloading and activity modification to arthrodesis for advanced or refractory disease as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Mueller-Weiss syndrome is an osteonecrosis of the tarsal navicular in the mature skeleton, thought to result from a combination of abnormal loading and impaired vascularity of the bone. Disordered ossification or delayed maturation of the navicular is believed to leave it mechanically vulnerable, and abnormal distribution of load across the midfoot then concentrates compressive stress on its lateral portion. The resulting subchondral insufficiency and osteonecrosis lead to lateral compression, fragmentation and a characteristic comma-shaped deformity of the navicular. As the lateral navicular collapses, the talar head is displaced and the hindfoot paradoxically drifts into varus rather than the valgus seen with typical medial column failure, and secondary degeneration of the surrounding tarsal joints follows.","additionalDifferentials":["Kohler disease (benign childhood navicular osteonecrosis)","Tarsal navicular stress fracture","Midfoot (talonavicular or naviculocuneiform) osteoarthritis","Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction","Inflammatory arthritis of the midfoot","Charcot neuroarthropathy of the midfoot"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Weight-bearing foot radiographs to demonstrate lateral navicular compression and the comma-shaped deformity","Assessment of hindfoot alignment for paradoxical varus","Computed tomography to define fragmentation and secondary joint involvement","Magnetic resonance imaging to confirm osteonecrosis and assess adjacent joints","Evaluation for a navicular stress fracture as an alternative cause of midfoot pain","Screening for contributing factors such as inflammatory arthropathy where relevant"],"redFlags":["Progressive navicular collapse with fixed hindfoot deformity","Established talonavicular or naviculocuneiform arthritis with persistent pain","Features suggesting Charcot neuroarthropathy in a patient with peripheral neuropathy","Rapidly worsening pain or swelling prompting exclusion of infection or another destructive process"],"prognosis":"Unlike the self-limiting childhood Kohler disease, adult Mueller-Weiss disease tends to progress; conservative measures may control symptoms for a time, but many patients ultimately develop midfoot arthritis and deformity that require arthrodesis, with outcomes depending on the extent of collapse and secondary joint involvement.","pitfalls":["Confusing the progressive adult disease with the benign, self-limiting childhood Kohler disease","Expecting a flatfoot and overlooking the characteristic paradoxical hindfoot varus","Mistaking the fragmentation for a simple navicular stress fracture","Delaying recognition of secondary midfoot arthritis until deformity is fixed"],"management":["Initial conservative management with custom orthoses, rigid-soled footwear and activity modification to offload the midfoot","Analgesia with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and periods of immobilisation during painful flares","Corrective arthrodesis (talonavicular, naviculocuneiform or triple fusion) for progressive collapse, restoring the medial column and correcting the paradoxical hindfoot varus","Surgical restoration of hindfoot alignment as part of reconstruction","Assess and treat contributing factors such as inflammatory arthropathy","Serial weight-bearing radiographs to monitor progression of navicular collapse and secondary midfoot arthritis","Referral to a foot and ankle orthopaedic surgeon when conservative measures fail"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle"],"crossLinks":[]},"muenke_syndrome":{"name":"Muenke syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FGFR3-related coronal synostosis","FGFR3 P250R craniosynostosis","Muenke-type craniosynostosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined at the molecular level by a single recurrent FGFR3 mutation, the p.Pro250Arg (P250R) substitution, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern","Classically causes uni- or bicoronal synostosis, but the phenotype is highly variable and some carriers are essentially unaffected","Because it is defined by genotype rather than a fixed appearance, molecular testing is essential and clinical overlap with Saethre-Chotzen syndrome is considerable","Sensorineural hearing loss is an important and frequently under-recognised associated feature that should be actively screened for","Macrocephaly, mild developmental or learning difficulties and subtle limb radiographic changes (such as carpal or tarsal fusions) may be present","Females and those with bicoronal involvement tend to be more severely affected than males or those with unilateral synostosis","The recurrent mutation means testing is straightforward and should be offered when coronal synostosis is found without another cause","Craniofacial surgical management follows the pattern and severity of suture fusion, with monitoring for raised intracranial pressure"],"pathophysiology":"Muenke syndrome is caused by a specific gain-of-function change in fibroblast growth factor receptor 3 (the P250R substitution) that increases ligand-independent or ligand-enhanced receptor signalling. The heightened FGFR3 activity in the coronal suture drives premature osteogenic differentiation and suture fusion. The same receptor is expressed in the cochlea, which accounts for the associated sensorineural hearing loss, and its wide expression contributes to the marked variability in expression, including non-penetrant carriers.","additionalDifferentials":["Saethre-Chotzen syndrome (TWIST1, overlapping coronal synostosis)","Crouzon syndrome (FGFR2)","Pfeiffer syndrome (broad thumbs and toes)","Non-syndromic uni- or bicoronal craniosynostosis","Craniofrontonasal syndrome (EFNB1)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted molecular testing for the recurrent FGFR3 P250R mutation","Audiological assessment to detect sensorineural hearing loss","Three-dimensional cranial CT to define coronal suture involvement","Developmental and neuropsychological assessment where indicated","Radiographs of the hands and feet to detect carpal or tarsal fusion","MRI of the brain if raised intracranial pressure is suspected"],"redFlags":["Signs of raised intracranial pressure, more likely with bicoronal fusion","Undiagnosed sensorineural hearing loss impairing speech and development","Developmental delay warranting additional evaluation","Progressive craniofacial deformity requiring surgical revision"],"prognosis":"Outcome ranges from unaffected carriers to children needing craniofacial surgery; cognition is usually normal or only mildly affected, but unrecognised hearing loss can compromise development, so audiological follow-up is important.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing Saethre-Chotzen syndrome clinically without molecular testing, given the overlap","Failing to screen for and treat sensorineural hearing loss","Assuming an affected parent excludes the diagnosis, since expression is highly variable","Neglecting intracranial pressure monitoring in bicoronal cases"],"management":["Cranial vault remodelling and suture release by a craniofacial surgical team for coronal synostosis and any raised intracranial pressure","Audiological management of sensorineural hearing loss, including hearing aids and speech and language support","Developmental and educational support with early intervention for learning difficulties","Surveillance for raised intracranial pressure with clinical review, fundoscopy and neuroimaging where indicated","Coordinated multidisciplinary craniofacial follow-up spanning surgery, ENT and audiology, ophthalmology and neurodevelopment","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant FGFR3 P250R condition, with testing of at-risk relatives given its variable penetrance","Regular audiological screening, because unrecognised hearing loss can impair development"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: FGFR-related craniosynostosis syndromes"],"crossLinks":[]},"mulibrey_nanism":{"name":"Mulibrey nanism","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["muscle-liver-brain-eye nanism","TRIM37-related disorder","Perheentupa syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The name is an acronym for the tissues involved: muscle, liver, brain, and eye, with nanism denoting severe growth restriction","Presents with prenatal-onset growth failure, a triangular face with frontal bossing, and a high-pitched voice, alongside normal or near-normal cognition","Constrictive pericarditis is a hallmark and a major determinant of prognosis, causing hepatomegaly, ascites, and signs of chronic heart failure","Yellowish dots and pigment dispersion in the ocular fundus are a characteristic ophthalmological finding","Caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in TRIM37 and inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern, with relatively high prevalence in the Finnish population","There is an increased risk of tumours, including Wilms tumour and other neoplasms, so surveillance is warranted","Endocrine problems such as hypogonadism, insulin resistance, and metabolic syndrome features may emerge with age"],"pathophysiology":"Mulibrey nanism is caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in TRIM37, which encodes a peroxisomal and nuclear E3 ubiquitin ligase involved in protein turnover, chromatin regulation, and cell proliferation control. Loss of TRIM37 function disturbs these processes across multiple tissues, producing severe growth restriction, fibrous change in the pericardium leading to constriction, characteristic craniofacial and ocular features, and a predisposition to tumours. The multisystem involvement reflects the broad expression and regulatory roles of the protein.","additionalDifferentials":["Silver-Russell syndrome","3-M syndrome and other primordial short-stature disorders","Seckel syndrome","Constrictive pericarditis of other cause","Noonan syndrome","Fetal growth restriction of environmental cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["TRIM37 gene sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis","Echocardiography and cardiac imaging to detect constrictive pericarditis","Ophthalmological examination for characteristic fundal pigment changes","Abdominal and renal ultrasound for tumour surveillance including Wilms tumour","Endocrine evaluation for hypogonadism, glucose intolerance, and growth axis","Liver assessment for hepatomegaly and congestion"],"redFlags":["Signs of constrictive pericarditis with heart failure, ascites, and hepatic congestion","New abdominal mass or haematuria suggesting Wilms tumour","Progressive metabolic derangement with insulin resistance","Failure to thrive with cardiac decompensation in infancy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is largely dictated by cardiac involvement, and constrictive pericarditis can cause progressive heart failure that may require pericardiectomy. With cardiac management and tumour surveillance many individuals survive into adulthood, though short stature and endocrine complications persist.","pitfalls":["Overlooking constrictive pericarditis as the cause of hepatomegaly and ascites in a child with growth failure","Failing to institute tumour surveillance for Wilms tumour and other neoplasms","Mistaking the disorder for Silver-Russell or Seckel syndrome given overlapping growth restriction","Missing the emergence of metabolic and endocrine complications in later childhood and adulthood"],"management":["Pericardiectomy for constrictive pericarditis, the principal determinant of prognosis","Diuretic therapy and supportive management of congestive heart failure features such as hepatomegaly, ascites and oedema","Tumour surveillance with abdominal and renal ultrasound for Wilms tumour and other neoplasms","Endocrine management including hormone replacement for hypogonadism and monitoring and treatment of glucose intolerance and the growth axis","Regular ophthalmological review for the characteristic fundal pigment changes","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive TRIM37 disorder, with testing of at-risk relatives","Multidisciplinary follow-up coordinating cardiology, endocrinology, oncology and ophthalmology","Escalate to cardiac surgical assessment when heart failure progresses despite medical therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Mulibrey Nanism"],"crossLinks":[]},"mullerian_duct_cyst":{"name":"Mullerian duct cyst","system":"Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mullerian duct cyst","paramesonephric duct cyst","midline prostatic cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / EAU Guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A midline cystic remnant of the paramesonephric (mullerian) duct, situated in or above the prostate near the midline","It does not communicate with the urethra and does not contain spermatozoa, in contrast to a prostatic utricle cyst","Often asymptomatic and found incidentally; larger cysts may cause voiding symptoms, perineal pain, ejaculatory dysfunction, haematospermia or infertility","It typically presents in the third to fourth decades as the cyst enlarges, unlike utricle cysts, which more often present in childhood","Its midline position distinguishes it from laterally placed seminal vesicle cysts","MRI and transrectal ultrasound define the midline location and the relationship to the verumontanum","Small asymptomatic cysts are observed, whereas symptomatic cysts may be aspirated, marsupialised or excised"],"pathophysiology":"Mullerian duct cysts arise from incomplete regression of the paramesonephric ducts, which in males normally involute under the influence of anti-mullerian hormone, leaving only the prostatic utricle and the appendix testis. A retained segment can dilate into a midline cyst behind the prostate that lies above the verumontanum and, unlike the utricle, does not open into the prostatic urethra.","additionalDifferentials":["Prostatic utricle cyst, which is midline and communicates with the urethra","Seminal vesicle cyst, which is laterally placed","Ejaculatory duct cyst","Prostatic retention cyst","Cystic prostatic neoplasm","Zinner syndrome when renal agenesis is present"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pelvic MRI to confirm the midline location and characterise the contents","Transrectal ultrasound for size and relationship to the prostate and ejaculatory ducts","Cyst fluid analysis, where absence of spermatozoa favours a mullerian rather than utricle or ejaculatory duct origin","Semen analysis if infertility is present","Cross-sectional imaging to exclude solid components or malignancy","Renal imaging to assess for associated anomalies"],"redFlags":["Solid components or rapid growth suggesting rare malignant transformation","Infection or abscess formation","Obstructive voiding symptoms or infertility","Recurrent haematospermia"],"prognosis":"Benign with a favourable outcome; most remain asymptomatic and symptomatic lesions respond well to drainage or excision, with only rare reports of malignancy arising within long-standing cysts.","pitfalls":["Confusing a mullerian duct cyst with a prostatic utricle cyst, when fluid spermatozoa and urethral communication help distinguish them","Mislabelling a midline cyst as a lateral seminal vesicle cyst","Overlooking rare malignant change in long-standing cysts","Attributing symptoms to benign prostatic enlargement"],"management":["Conservative observation for small, asymptomatic cysts","Surgical excision or deroofing by a transurethral, transrectal or laparoscopic/robotic approach for symptomatic or enlarging cysts","Aspiration or drainage as a less invasive option for symptomatic relief","Management of associated voiding symptoms, ejaculatory dysfunction or haematospermia","Fertility assessment and assisted reproductive input where infertility is present","Surveillance imaging for recurrence and for the rare development of malignancy within long-standing cysts","Referral to urology and evaluation for associated renal tract anomalies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","EAU Guidelines"],"crossLinks":["utricle_cyst","seminal_vesicle_cyst","zinner_syndrome"]},"multicentric_reticulohistiocytosis":{"name":"Multicentric reticulohistiocytosis","system":"Rheumatology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MRH","lipoid dermatoarthritis","reticulohistiocytoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare non-Langerhans histiocytosis causing a destructive polyarthritis together with characteristic skin and mucosal papules and nodules","The arthritis is typically symmetrical and can rapidly progress to a mutilating, resorptive deformity (arthritis mutilans)","Firm, reddish-brown papulonodules cluster around the nail folds ('coral bead' sign) and over the hands, face, and ears","It is a recognised paraneoplastic association, so a search for occult malignancy is essential at diagnosis","Associations also include autoimmune disease, hyperlipidaemia, and a positive tuberculin response in some patients","Histology shows infiltration by multinucleated giant cells and histiocytes with abundant eosinophilic 'ground-glass' cytoplasm","Treatment combines systemic immunosuppression, sometimes with bisphosphonates and TNF or other biologic agents, to halt joint destruction"],"pathophysiology":"Multicentric reticulohistiocytosis is a systemic histiocytic disorder in which histiocytes and multinucleated giant cells with characteristic ground-glass eosinophilic cytoplasm infiltrate the synovium, skin, and other tissues. Overexpression of proinflammatory cytokines, particularly tumour necrosis factor and interleukin-1 and interleukin-6, and osteoclast-activating pathways (including RANK ligand signalling) drives the aggressive, erosive joint destruction. The infiltrating cells are of monocyte-macrophage lineage. The frequent association with malignancy and autoimmune disease suggests an aberrant reactive immune process rather than a purely clonal neoplasm.","additionalDifferentials":["Rheumatoid arthritis, particularly erosive disease","Psoriatic arthritis with arthritis mutilans","Gout and other crystal arthropathies with tophi","Dermatomyositis","Other histiocytoses (Langerhans cell, Erdheim-Chester, Rosai-Dorfman)","Sarcoidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin or synovial biopsy showing giant cells and histiocytes with ground-glass eosinophilic cytoplasm","Radiographs of affected joints demonstrating well-marginated erosions and joint destruction","Inflammatory markers and autoimmune serology to assess associated conditions","Fasting lipid profile given the association with hyperlipidaemia","Age-appropriate malignancy screening given the paraneoplastic association","Tuberculin testing where clinically relevant"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive joint destruction threatening arthritis mutilans","Features suggesting an underlying occult malignancy","Extensive mucocutaneous or visceral involvement","Systemic symptoms such as weight loss and fever prompting malignancy evaluation"],"prognosis":"The skin lesions may eventually regress, but untreated joint disease can progress rapidly to severe, disabling arthritis mutilans; overall outlook is heavily influenced by any associated malignancy.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for rheumatoid or psoriatic arthritis and missing the diagnostic skin nodules","Failing to screen for an underlying malignancy","Delaying aggressive treatment and allowing irreversible joint destruction","Overlooking the periungual 'coral bead' papules","Attributing the nodules to gouty tophi without biopsy"],"management":["Immunosuppression with corticosteroids combined with a disease-modifying agent such as methotrexate, with leflunomide or cyclophosphamide for resistant disease","Biologic therapy targeting tumour necrosis factor, and bisphosphonates to inhibit the osteoclast and RANK-ligand-driven bone destruction, in refractory or aggressive arthritis","Investigate for and treat any associated occult malignancy, given the paraneoplastic association","Symptomatic joint care with analgesia and physiotherapy to preserve function","Screen for and manage associated hyperlipidaemia and autoimmune disease","Monitor joint radiographs and function to detect progression towards arthritis mutilans and to guide treatment intensification","Rheumatology-led multidisciplinary management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"multicystic_dysplastic_kidney":{"name":"Multicystic dysplastic kidney","system":"Renal / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCDK","multicystic kidney","multicystic renal dysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital dysplastic kidney replaced by multiple non-communicating cysts of varying size with intervening dysplastic tissue and no functioning nephrons, giving a non-functioning renal unit","Usually unilateral and detected on antenatal ultrasound; bilateral disease is incompatible with life","On ultrasound the cysts do not communicate and there is no identifiable normal renal parenchyma or a reniform pelvicalyceal pattern, distinguishing it from hydronephrosis","A DMSA or MAG3 scan confirms absent function in the affected kidney","The natural history is progressive involution, with many kidneys shrinking or disappearing over months to years","The contralateral kidney should be assessed carefully, as compensatory hypertrophy is expected and coexisting anomalies such as reflux or ureteropelvic junction obstruction on the other side carry the main functional prognosis","Most cases are managed conservatively with surveillance; nephrectomy is reserved for the rare complications of a very large lesion, symptoms, hypertension, or diagnostic uncertainty (concern for malignancy)"],"pathophysiology":"Multicystic dysplastic kidney is a severe form of renal dysplasia thought to result from an early developmental insult, such as complete ureteric or ureteropelvic obstruction/atresia, that disrupts the normal inductive interaction between the ureteric bud and the metanephric blastema. The affected kidney develops as a disorganised collection of non-communicating cysts with dysplastic stroma and cartilage and no functioning nephron mass. Because the collecting system does not connect, the unit produces no urine and typically involutes over time. Unlike hydronephrosis, there is no central dilated pelvis draining the cysts.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe hydronephrosis from ureteropelvic junction obstruction","Autosomal recessive or dominant polycystic kidney disease","Cystic renal tumour (e.g. cystic nephroma, cystic Wilms)","Segmental multicystic dysplasia in a duplex system","Simple or complex renal cysts","Obstructed dysplastic kidney with retained function"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasound to demonstrate non-communicating cysts and absent normal parenchyma","DMSA or MAG3 scintigraphy to confirm the kidney is non-functioning","Micturating cystourethrogram (MCUG) if contralateral or ipsilateral reflux is suspected","Serial ultrasound to monitor involution and the contralateral kidney","Blood pressure monitoring during follow-up","MRI in cases of diagnostic uncertainty"],"redFlags":["Any abnormality of the contralateral solitary functioning kidney","New-onset hypertension attributable to the dysplastic kidney","Enlarging or solid components within the lesion raising concern for malignancy","Respiratory or renal compromise suggesting bilateral disease in the neonate"],"prognosis":"For unilateral disease with a normal contralateral kidney the outlook is generally excellent, with progressive involution and good overall renal function; prognosis depends chiefly on the health of the contralateral kidney, and rare complications such as hypertension are the main reasons for intervention.","pitfalls":["Confusing multicystic dysplastic kidney with severe hydronephrosis and mismanaging accordingly","Neglecting thorough evaluation and follow-up of the contralateral kidney","Performing unnecessary nephrectomy on a lesion that would involute","Overlooking rare malignant transformation or a coexisting tumour"],"management":["Conservative management with serial renal ultrasound to follow the expected involution of the affected kidney","Regular blood pressure monitoring with antihypertensive treatment if hypertension develops","Careful assessment and protection of the contralateral kidney, managing any coexisting vesicoureteric reflux or pelviureteric junction obstruction","Nephrectomy reserved for complications such as uncontrolled hypertension, mass effect or diagnostic uncertainty regarding malignancy","Micturating cystourethrogram where reflux is suspected","Longitudinal monitoring of renal function, since prognosis depends chiefly on the healthy contralateral kidney","Referral to paediatric nephrology and urology for surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Gearhart, Rink & Mouriquand: Pediatric Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"multifocal_atrial_tachycardia":{"name":"multifocal atrial tachycardia","system":"cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAT","chaotic atrial tachycardia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["MAT is defined by an irregular atrial tachycardia with at least three distinct P-wave morphologies and varying PP, PR and RR intervals at a rate above 100 per minute.","It is strongly associated with decompensated chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and other causes of hypoxaemia and pulmonary hypertension.","An isoelectric baseline between clearly discernible P waves distinguishes MAT from atrial fibrillation, which lacks organised atrial activity.","Other precipitants include theophylline toxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, sepsis and the postoperative state.","Treatment centres on correcting the underlying pulmonary and metabolic disturbance rather than on antiarrhythmic drugs.","Magnesium and potassium repletion can reduce ectopy even when baseline levels appear within the reference range.","Beta-blockers are effective for rate control but are frequently limited by bronchospastic disease, so a non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker is often preferred.","Direct-current cardioversion is ineffective because the mechanism is enhanced automaticity from multiple foci rather than reentry."],"pathophysiology":"MAT results from abnormal automaticity or triggered activity arising from multiple ectopic atrial foci, producing P waves of differing morphology and timing. It typically occurs in the setting of atrial stretch, hypoxaemia, catecholamine excess and metabolic derangement, conditions epitomised by decompensated chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with right atrial enlargement and elevated pulmonary pressures. Fluctuating autonomic tone, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and drugs such as theophylline and beta-agonists further destabilise atrial membrane potentials and promote ectopic firing. Because the arrhythmia is driven by automatic foci rather than a fixed reentrant circuit, it does not respond to cardioversion and resolves chiefly with correction of the underlying disturbance.","additionalDifferentials":["atrial fibrillation","atrial flutter with variable conduction","frequent atrial premature complexes with wandering atrial pacemaker","focal atrial tachycardia","sinus tachycardia with premature atrial beats","atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["twelve-lead ECG demonstrating three or more P-wave morphologies with an isoelectric baseline","arterial blood gas analysis to assess hypoxaemia and acid-base status","serum potassium and magnesium concentrations","theophylline level where the drug is in use","chest imaging to evaluate underlying pulmonary disease","echocardiography to assess right heart size, pulmonary pressures and left ventricular function"],"redFlags":["severe hypoxaemia or respiratory failure driving the arrhythmia","haemodynamic instability from a rapid ventricular response","theophylline toxicity requiring urgent management","profound electrolyte depletion","features of decompensated cor pulmonale or right heart failure"],"prognosis":"MAT itself is rarely dangerous, but it signals serious underlying illness and its prognosis reflects the severity of the associated pulmonary or systemic disease. The arrhythmia usually resolves once the precipitating condition is treated.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing MAT as atrial fibrillation and pursuing rate or rhythm strategies aimed at the wrong mechanism.","Attempting electrical cardioversion, which does not work for an automatic multifocal rhythm.","Prescribing non-selective beta-blockers in bronchospastic disease and worsening respiratory status.","Overlooking magnesium deficiency because the serum level appears normal.","Focusing on antiarrhythmic therapy while neglecting the underlying hypoxaemia and metabolic derangement."],"management":["Treat the underlying precipitant by correcting hypoxaemia and optimising decompensated COPD with bronchodilators and treatment of infection","Correct metabolic derangement with magnesium and potassium repletion, which reduces atrial ectopy","Review and reduce arrhythmogenic drugs, particularly theophylline and beta-agonists","Rate control with a non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker such as verapamil or diltiazem, reserving beta-blockers for those without significant bronchospasm","Avoid direct-current cardioversion, which is ineffective against an automatically driven, non-reentrant rhythm","Monitor with continuous ECG and repeat electrolytes, escalating care according to the severity of the underlying pulmonary or systemic illness"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with supraventricular tachycardia","Chou's Electrocardiography in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"multifocal_choroiditis":{"name":"multifocal choroiditis","system":"ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["multifocal choroiditis and panuveitis","MCP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically affects young to middle-aged, moderately myopic women and is often bilateral though frequently asymmetric.","Fundoscopy shows multiple small, round, yellow-grey lesions at the level of the choroid and retinal pigment epithelium in the posterior pole and mid-periphery that evolve into punched-out atrophic scars with pigmented margins.","When accompanied by anterior chamber and vitreous inflammation it is termed multifocal choroiditis and panuveitis, distinguishing it from the non-inflammatory punctate inner choroidopathy.","Choroidal neovascular membranes are a major sight-threatening complication and a common cause of central visual loss.","It is an important cause of the enlarged blind spot syndrome and can produce photopsia and progressive scotomata.","Infective mimics, especially ocular tuberculosis, sarcoidosis and syphilis, must be excluded before attributing lesions to idiopathic disease.","Active inflammatory lesions respond to corticosteroids and steroid-sparing immunosuppression, whereas choroidal neovascularisation is treated with anti-vascular endothelial growth factor therapy.","Optical coherence tomography and fundus autofluorescence help distinguish active inflammatory lesions from inactive atrophic scars and detect new neovascular activity."],"pathophysiology":"Multifocal choroiditis is considered an idiopathic inflammatory disorder within the spectrum of white dot syndromes, in which an aberrant immune response targets the outer retina, retinal pigment epithelium and choriocapillaris. Focal collections of lymphocytes accumulate at the level of the retinal pigment epithelium and inner choroid, producing the characteristic discrete lesions. Repeated or ongoing inflammation disrupts Bruch's membrane and the retinal pigment epithelium, leaving punched-out chorioretinal scars and predisposing to the ingrowth of choroidal neovascular membranes through the breaks in Bruch's membrane. An autoimmune reaction against retinal or retinal pigment epithelial antigens, in a genetically susceptible host, is the leading hypothesis, and the disorder frequently coexists with intraocular inflammation in the anterior chamber and vitreous.","additionalDifferentials":["punctate inner choroidopathy","presumed ocular histoplasmosis syndrome","ocular sarcoidosis","ocular tuberculosis","syphilitic chorioretinitis","birdshot chorioretinopathy","primary vitreoretinal lymphoma masquerade"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["fundus fluorescein angiography to characterise lesions and detect choroidal neovascularisation","indocyanine green angiography showing hypofluorescent spots","optical coherence tomography for sub-retinal fluid, neovascular membranes and outer retinal disruption","fundus autofluorescence to map active and atrophic lesions","chest imaging and interferon-gamma release assay to exclude tuberculosis and sarcoidosis","serum angiotensin-converting enzyme and syphilis serology","visual field testing for blind spot enlargement and scotomata"],"redFlags":["sudden central visual loss or metamorphopsia suggesting choroidal neovascularisation","rapidly progressive or confluent lesions threatening the fovea","features suggesting an infective cause such as tuberculosis or syphilis","unilateral progressive vitritis in an older patient raising concern for masquerade lymphoma"],"prognosis":"Visual outcome is variable and depends largely on foveal involvement and the development of choroidal neovascularisation; many retain good vision with prompt control of inflammation and neovascular activity, but recurrent disease and macular scarring can cause permanent central loss.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing idiopathic disease without excluding tuberculosis, sarcoidosis and syphilis.","Missing a choroidal neovascular membrane as the cause of new central visual symptoms.","Confusing inflammatory reactivation with a new neovascular membrane, leading to inappropriate therapy.","Overlooking a masquerade syndrome such as vitreoretinal lymphoma in older or atypical patients.","Treating with corticosteroids alone in aggressive disease when steroid-sparing immunosuppression is needed."],"management":["Corticosteroid therapy (systemic, periocular or intravitreal) to control active intraocular inflammation, with steroid-sparing immunosuppressants for chronic or recurrent disease","Intravitreal anti-VEGF injections for sight-threatening choroidal neovascular membranes","Exclude infective mimics such as tuberculosis, sarcoidosis and syphilis before committing to immunosuppression","Regular monitoring with optical coherence tomography, angiography and fundus autofluorescence to detect neovascular activity and new lesions, with Amsler grid home self-monitoring","Management by an ophthalmologist or uveitis specialist","Prompt escalation of anti-inflammatory and anti-VEGF treatment when the fovea is threatened or neovascularisation appears"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Uveitis and Ocular Inflammation","Standardisation of Uveitis Nomenclature classification criteria"],"crossLinks":[]},"multifocal_motor_neuropathy":{"name":"Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block","system":"Nervous system / Neuroimmunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MMN","multifocal motor neuropathy","MMNCB"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Causes slowly progressive, asymmetrical, distal, upper-limb-predominant weakness in the distribution of individual nerves","Sensation is characteristically preserved, an important feature separating it from other polyneuropathies","The electrophysiological hallmark is motor conduction block at sites not prone to compression, with normal sensory conduction across the same segments","Serum anti-GM1 ganglioside antibodies are positive in a substantial proportion and support the diagnosis","It is often mistaken for motor neuron disease, but the pure motor picture is potentially treatable","Intravenous immunoglobulin is the mainstay of treatment, whereas corticosteroids and plasma exchange are typically unhelpful or may worsen the condition","Cerebrospinal fluid protein is usually normal or only mildly raised, unlike CIDP"],"pathophysiology":"Multifocal motor neuropathy is an immune-mediated disorder of motor axons in which antibodies, frequently against the GM1 ganglioside enriched at the node of Ranvier, disrupt nodal sodium-channel clusters and paranodal architecture. This produces persistent focal conduction block along motor fibres while sensory fibres are spared, generating the pattern of weakness confined to individual nerve territories without sensory loss. Over time some axonal degeneration occurs, but the primary and treatment-responsive abnormality is the focal motor conduction block, which explains both the clinical picture and the benefit from immunoglobulin therapy.","additionalDifferentials":["Motor neuron disease (amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, progressive muscular atrophy)","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy (Lewis-Sumner variant)","Hereditary neuropathy with liability to pressure palsies","Compressive mononeuropathies and entrapment","Inclusion body myositis","Vasculitic (multiple mononeuropathy) neuropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies to demonstrate motor conduction block with preserved sensory conduction","Serum anti-GM1 IgM antibody testing","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis to help distinguish from CIDP","MRI or ultrasound of nerves and plexus to show focal enlargement or signal change","Electromyography to assess for denervation and exclude anterior horn cell disease","Assessment of treatment response to intravenous immunoglobulin as supportive evidence"],"redFlags":["Emerging upper motor neuron signs suggesting motor neuron disease rather than MMN","Development of sensory involvement prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Rapid axonal loss with wasting threatening irreversible weakness","Loss of previously good response to immunoglobulin therapy"],"prognosis":"Many patients maintain function with regular intravenous immunoglobulin, though the disorder is usually chronic and requires ongoing treatment; progressive axonal loss can cause slowly accumulating disability over years.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing motor neuron disease and missing a treatable neuropathy","Treating with corticosteroids or plasma exchange, which may be ineffective or harmful","Excluding the diagnosis when anti-GM1 antibodies are negative","Failing to search adequately for conduction block outside common entrapment sites"],"management":["Intravenous immunoglobulin as first-line and maintenance therapy, with subcutaneous immunoglobulin as an alternative for maintenance","Immunosuppressants such as cyclophosphamide or rituximab reserved for immunoglobulin-refractory disease","Avoid corticosteroids and plasma exchange, which are typically ineffective and may worsen the condition","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and orthotic support to maintain function","Regular assessment of strength, disability and treatment response to titrate the immunoglobulin dosing interval","Neuromuscular specialist management that distinguishes the disorder from motor neuron disease so that treatable disease is not missed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Continuum: Peripheral Nervous System Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiminicore_disease":{"name":"Multiminicore disease (SELENON/RYR1)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["minicore myopathy","multicore myopathy","MmD","SELENON-related myopathy","SEPN1 myopathy","RYR1-related multiminicore disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital myopathy defined by multiple small cores (minicores) lacking oxidative enzyme activity, most often caused by recessive variants in SELENON (SEPN1) or RYR1","The classic SELENON-related form features axial and neck weakness, early spinal rigidity, scoliosis, and prominent respiratory muscle involvement, sometimes with respiratory failure while ambulation is preserved","RYR1-related forms may show more limb-girdle weakness, external ophthalmoplegia, or overlap with core and centronuclear myopathies","RYR1-related disease carries a risk of malignant hyperthermia susceptibility, so anaesthetic precautions are essential","Onset is typically in infancy or early childhood with hypotonia and delayed motor milestones, and progression is usually slow","Creatine kinase is normal or only mildly elevated and electromyography shows a myopathic pattern","Muscle biopsy shows multiple small cores of reduced oxidative staining that do not extend the length of the fibre, distinguishing them from the long central cores of central core disease","Diagnosis integrates the minicore biopsy findings, clinical phenotype, and SELENON or RYR1 genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Multiminicore disease is a genetically heterogeneous congenital myopathy. SELENON encodes selenoprotein N, an endoplasmic reticulum protein involved in redox regulation and calcium handling, and its loss impairs protection against oxidative stress and calcium homeostasis in muscle. RYR1 encodes the skeletal muscle ryanodine receptor, the calcium-release channel of the sarcoplasmic reticulum central to excitation-contraction coupling; variants disturb calcium release and can also confer malignant hyperthermia susceptibility. In both, focal areas of mitochondrial depletion and disrupted oxidative metabolism form the multiple minicores seen histologically, and the resulting impaired contraction produces weakness, with axial and respiratory muscles particularly affected in the SELENON-related form.","additionalDifferentials":["Central core disease","Other congenital myopathies (centronuclear, nemaline, congenital fibre-type disproportion)","Congenital muscular dystrophy including rigid spine syndrome","Spinal muscular atrophy and other causes of infantile hypotonia","Mitochondrial myopathy","Congenital myasthenic syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography","Muscle biopsy with oxidative enzyme histochemistry and electron microscopy to demonstrate minicores","Muscle MRI to define the pattern of muscle involvement","Respiratory function testing including lying and sitting vital capacity, given the risk of respiratory failure","Genetic testing of SELENON and RYR1 and a broader congenital myopathy panel"],"redFlags":["Respiratory insufficiency or nocturnal hypoventilation, which can occur even in ambulant patients","Progressive scoliosis or spinal rigidity compromising respiration","Personal or family history of adverse anaesthetic reaction suggesting malignant hyperthermia in RYR1 disease","Cardiac symptoms warranting evaluation","Rapid deterioration atypical for a slowly progressive congenital myopathy"],"prognosis":"Many patients remain ambulant with slow progression, but respiratory muscle involvement, especially in the SELENON-related form, can lead to ventilatory failure requiring support and is a major determinant of prognosis; proactive respiratory and orthopaedic surveillance improves outcomes.","pitfalls":["Failing to monitor respiratory function because limb strength and walking are relatively preserved","Overlooking malignant hyperthermia susceptibility in RYR1-related disease when planning anaesthesia","Not flagging avoidance of volatile anaesthetic agents and depolarising muscle relaxants and ensuring dantrolene availability where RYR1 variants are implicated","Confusing minicores with the long central cores of central core disease on biopsy"],"management":["Proactive respiratory surveillance with lying and sitting vital capacity and nocturnal non-invasive ventilation for respiratory muscle weakness","Orthopaedic management of scoliosis and spinal rigidity, including bracing and spinal fusion where indicated","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain mobility and prevent contractures","Malignant hyperthermia precautions in RYR1-related disease, avoiding volatile anaesthetics and depolarising muscle relaxants and using total intravenous anaesthesia","Multidisciplinary neuromuscular care coordinating neurology, respiratory medicine, orthopaedics and physiotherapy","Genetic counselling and family testing that accounts for recessive SELENON and dominant or recessive RYR1 inheritance","Escalate respiratory support as ventilatory function declines, since respiratory failure is the main determinant of prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: Multiminicore disease and RYR1-related myopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"multinodular_goitre":{"name":"Multinodular goitre","system":"Endocrine surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MNG","nodular goitre","multinodular goiter","toxic multinodular goitre","Plummer disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents as a painless, gradually enlarging anterior neck swelling that moves upward on swallowing, reflecting attachment to the thyroid cartilage","Most patients are euthyroid, but longstanding disease can develop autonomous nodules and progress to a toxic (hyperfunctioning) goitre with thyrotoxic features","Retrosternal extension may cause tracheal or oesophageal compression, producing dyspnoea, stridor, or dysphagia, and Pemberton sign (facial congestion on raising the arms) suggests thoracic inlet obstruction","Ultrasound is the first-line imaging to characterise nodules; a dominant or suspicious nodule should be sampled by fine-needle aspiration to exclude malignancy","Thyroid function tests and, where autonomy is suspected, a radionuclide uptake scan help distinguish hot (autonomous) from cold nodules","Surgical management is usually total or near-total thyroidectomy for compressive symptoms, cosmetic concern, suspected malignancy, or toxicity not controlled medically","Sudden painful enlargement of a single nodule often represents haemorrhage into a cyst rather than malignant change"],"pathophysiology":"A multinodular goitre develops through cycles of thyroid follicular hyperplasia and involution, driven by relative iodine deficiency, fluctuating TSH stimulation, and heterogeneous growth responses among follicular cells. Over time the gland becomes structurally heterogeneous, with colloid nodules, cystic degeneration, haemorrhage, fibrosis, and calcification. Some clones of cells acquire autonomous function independent of TSH, which can lead to subclinical or overt hyperthyroidism (toxic multinodular goitre). Progressive enlargement, particularly with retrosternal growth, can compress the trachea and oesophagus and displace or narrow the airway.","additionalDifferentials":["Solitary thyroid nodule or thyroid adenoma","Differentiated thyroid carcinoma (papillary or follicular)","Graves disease with diffuse goitre","Thyroiditis (Hashimoto or subacute)","Thyroglossal cyst or other midline neck mass","Lymphoma of the thyroid"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum TSH with free T4/T3 to define thyroid status","Neck ultrasound to map nodules and identify suspicious features","Ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration cytology of dominant or sonographically suspicious nodules","Radioisotope thyroid scan when autonomy or a toxic nodule is suspected","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI without iodinated contrast where feasible) for retrosternal extension and airway assessment","Flow-volume loops or laryngoscopy to assess airway and vocal cord function before surgery"],"redFlags":["Stridor, orthopnoea, or acute airway compromise from tracheal compression","Rapid enlargement, fixation, hoarseness, or cervical lymphadenopathy suggesting malignancy","Symptoms of severe thyrotoxicosis or impending thyroid storm in a toxic goitre","Superior vena cava obstruction features from a large retrosternal goitre"],"prognosis":"The outlook is generally good; benign euthyroid goitres often remain stable or are cured by thyroidectomy, and toxic goitres respond to definitive treatment. Recurrence can occur after subtotal resection, and lifelong thyroid hormone replacement is expected after total thyroidectomy.","pitfalls":["Assuming all nodular goitres are benign and omitting cytology of a dominant or suspicious nodule","Overlooking retrosternal extension and airway narrowing before anaesthesia","Failing to render a toxic goitre biochemically controlled before surgery, risking perioperative thyrotoxic crisis","Not documenting preoperative vocal cord status, obscuring the cause of any postoperative hoarseness","Missing postoperative hypocalcaemia or recurrent laryngeal nerve injury after total thyroidectomy"],"management":["Total or near-total thyroidectomy for compressive symptoms, retrosternal extension, cosmetic concern, suspected malignancy or toxicity not controlled medically","Radioactive iodine as an alternative to reduce gland volume or treat a toxic goitre in those unsuitable for surgery","Antithyroid thionamides such as carbimazole to restore euthyroidism before definitive treatment of a toxic goitre","Fine-needle aspiration cytology of dominant or sonographically suspicious nodules to exclude malignancy","Lifelong levothyroxine replacement after total thyroidectomy, with monitoring of thyroid function","Surveillance of a euthyroid goitre with periodic ultrasound and thyroid function tests, and preoperative airway and vocal cord assessment","Referral to endocrine surgery and endocrinology, avoiding iodinated contrast where autonomous nodules risk precipitating thyrotoxicosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_acyl_coa_dehydrogenase_deficiency":{"name":"Multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (glutaric aciduria type II)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MADD","glutaric aciduria type II","GA2","ETFA/ETFB/ETFDH deficiency","electron transfer flavoprotein deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by defects in electron transfer flavoprotein or its dehydrogenase (ETFA, ETFB, ETFDH), impairing many flavin-dependent dehydrogenases at once","Presents as a spectrum: severe neonatal forms with or without congenital anomalies, and milder late-onset (riboflavin-responsive) disease","Severe neonatal disease features hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, metabolic acidosis, hyperammonaemia and a distinctive 'sweaty feet' odour","Congenital anomalies in the severe form include large cystic (dysplastic) kidneys, facial dysmorphism and hypospadias","Late-onset MADD often manifests as a lipid-storage myopathy with exercise intolerance, muscle weakness and recurrent rhabdomyolysis","Urine organic acids show glutaric acid plus a broad range of dicarboxylic and other acids reflecting the multiple enzyme block","Acylcarnitine profile shows elevation of short-, medium- and long-chain species together, a clue to a global fatty acid oxidation defect","Many late-onset (and some ETFDH) cases respond well to riboflavin; treatment also includes fasting avoidance, glucose during crises and carnitine"],"pathophysiology":"MADD results from deficiency of electron transfer flavoprotein (ETF) or ETF-ubiquinone oxidoreductase (ETF dehydrogenase), which shuttle electrons from numerous mitochondrial flavin-adenine-dinucleotide-dependent dehydrogenases into the respiratory chain. Because these carriers serve the acyl-CoA dehydrogenases of fatty acid beta-oxidation as well as several dehydrogenases of amino acid catabolism, a single defect simultaneously impairs oxidation of fatty acids of all chain lengths, and of glutaryl-, isovaleryl- and sarcosine-related substrates. The consequence during fasting or catabolic stress is failure of fatty acid oxidation and ketogenesis, producing hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and energy deficiency, with accumulation of glutaric and dicarboxylic acids and secondary hyperammonaemia. ETFDH variants that destabilise the flavoprotein can be stabilised by riboflavin (the FAD precursor), which underlies the riboflavin-responsive phenotype. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated fatty acid oxidation disorders (MCAD, VLCAD, LCHAD)","Isolated glutaric aciduria type I (glutaryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency)","Primary carnitine deficiency and CPT disorders","Mitochondrial respiratory chain disorders","Congenital lactic acidoses","Polycystic/dysplastic kidney disease of other cause (in the neonatal form)","Inflammatory or metabolic myopathies (in late-onset disease)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma acylcarnitine profile showing multiple chain-length elevations","Urine organic acids with glutaric and dicarboxylic aciduria","Blood gas, glucose, ketones and ammonia during crises","Creatine kinase in suspected lipid-storage myopathy/rhabdomyolysis","ETFA, ETFB and ETFDH gene sequencing","Renal ultrasound in neonates for cystic dysplastic kidneys","Trial of riboflavin with clinical/biochemical response assessment"],"redFlags":["Neonatal hypoketotic hypoglycaemia with severe acidosis and cardiomyopathy","Hyperammonaemic encephalopathy during an acute crisis","Rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria and risk of renal failure","Cardiac arrhythmia or heart failure from lipid accumulation","Rapid decompensation with fasting or intercurrent illness"],"prognosis":"The severe neonatal form, especially with congenital anomalies, carries a poor prognosis with high early mortality; late-onset disease is often milder and many riboflavin-responsive patients improve markedly, though relapses can occur with catabolic stress.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the multiple acylcarnitine elevations for an isolated fatty acid oxidation defect","Not trialling riboflavin in a myopathic or ETFDH-related case","Allowing fasting for procedures or illness without a glucose-based emergency plan","Overlooking cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia in the acute setting","Failing to monitor for rhabdomyolysis and renal compromise in late-onset disease"],"management":["Trial of riboflavin, to which many late-onset patients respond markedly, with coenzyme Q10 and L-carnitine supplementation","Dietary management avoiding fasting, with a low-fat, low-protein, high-carbohydrate diet and frequent feeds","Emergency regimen during intercurrent illness or catabolic stress using intravenous glucose to suppress catabolism and prevent decompensation","Acute management of metabolic crises by correcting hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, acidosis and hyperammonaemia","Monitoring of acylcarnitine and organic acid profiles, growth, and creatine kinase where there is myopathy or rhabdomyolysis","Multidisciplinary metabolic care with a specialist dietitian","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive ETFA/ETFB/ETFDH disorder, with family screening and antenatal or newborn testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","Saudubray, Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_and_mixed_valvular_heart":{"name":"Multiple and Mixed Valvular Heart Disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Multivalvular heart disease","Mixed valve disease","Combined valvular heart disease"],"chapter":279,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2065","clinicalPearls":["Multiple acquired and congenital lesions can produce stenosis and/or regurgitation across one or more valves; rheumatic disease classically affects mitral, aortic, and tricuspid valves alone or in combination.","In multivalvular disease, derangements from the more proximal valve lesion can mask the full hemodynamic expression of a more distal lesion.","Reduced cardiac output from mitral valve disease lowers transaortic flow and can lead to underestimation of the severity of a coexisting aortic lesion.","Functional tricuspid regurgitation accompanying significant mitral valve disease is one of the most common forms of multivalve disease; the tricuspid leaflets are morphologically normal and TR arises from right ventricular and annular dilation.","Onset of atrial fibrillation can be especially deleterious in rheumatic mitral plus aortic disease: loss of atrial systole can critically reduce cardiac output and raise left atrial and LV diastolic pressures.","When AR or MR is the dominant lesion in mixed aortic or mitral disease, the LV is dilated; when AS or MS predominates, LV chamber size is normal or small.","Secondary (functional) MR complicating severe AS reflects altered LV geometry and systolic leaflet tethering with normal leaflets and chordae; it may improve after relief of afterload with valve replacement.","Persistent significant secondary MR after aortic valve replacement is associated with impaired functional outcomes and reduced survival.","Severe mitral annular calcification can cause both regurgitation (reduced systolic annular shortening) and mild-to-moderate stenosis (calcium extending onto the leaflets restricting opening).","An early ejection click, typical of bicuspid aortic valve in young adults, is often absent in congenital mixed AS and AR.","Patients with multiple or mixed valve disease may become symptomatic earlier than those with isolated single-valve lesions, sometimes even with only moderate individual lesions.","Palpitations suggesting AF can flag clinically unsuspected mitral valve disease as an important component of the presentation."],"pathophysiology":"In multivalvular heart disease the hemodynamic effects of a proximal valve lesion can mask those of a more distal lesion. With combined rheumatic mitral and aortic disease, the reduced cardiac output imposed by mitral disease lessens the apparent severity of the aortic lesion, while AF arising during mitral stenosis can abruptly worsen previously silent aortic disease. Reactive pulmonary vascular disease acts as a secondary obstructive lesion in series, and as cardiac output falls with progressive tricuspid disease, associated mitral or aortic disease may be underestimated. Functional TR follows right ventricular and annular dilation with morphologically normal leaflets, producing large systolic c-v waves in the RA pressure pulse whose height depends on RA compliance and regurgitant volume; advanced chronic severe TR ventricularizes the RA waveform. In MS with concomitant AR, enhanced LV filling raises diastolic pressure depending on chamber compliance. Secondary MR in severe AS arises from LV remodeling and leaflet tethering under high systolic pressures. In mixed AS/AR, augmented transvalvular systolic velocities inflate the Doppler gradient and murmur intensity relative to true orifice size, and the Gorlin formula is inaccurate. In mixed mitral disease, the elevated mitral E-wave velocity in severe MR may not reflect any contribution of associated MS to LA hypertension. Noncompliant, nondilated, concentrically hypertrophied ventricles (as in significant AS, radiation heart disease, cardiac amyloid, or obesity/diabetes cardiomyopathy) tolerate acute volume loads poorly, so sudden AR causes rapid rise in LV diastolic pressure and severe heart failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Patent ductus arteriosus (continuous murmur mimicking combined AS and AR systolic-diastolic murmurs)","Ruptured sinus of Valsalva aneurysm (continuous murmur, often after acute chest pain)","Isolated single-valve lesion with falsely overestimated or underestimated severity due to a masked second lesion","Pulmonary hypertension or right-sided valve disease underlying ECG signs of right heart abnormality in a left-sided valve lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Patent ductus arteriosus","Ruptured sinus of Valsalva aneurysm"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography as the principal modality, assessing valve morphology and function, calcification, chamber size, wall thickness, biventricular function, estimated PA systolic pressure, and great-vessel dimensions","Transesophageal echocardiography for more accurate valve (especially mitral) assessment and when infective endocarditis is suspected","Exercise testing with or without echocardiography when symptoms exceed resting TTE findings","Cardiac MR for additional anatomic and physiologic data when echocardiography is suboptimal (less suited to valve morphology)","Cardiac CT for intracardiac structures in complicated IE and for planning transcatheter valve implantation; coronary CT angiography as a noninvasive coronary assessment","Right and left heart catheterization to characterize individual lesion contributions, measure PA pressures, and calculate pulmonary vascular resistance, strongly recommended when clinical and noninvasive findings are discrepant in a symptomatic patient","ECG for ventricular hypertrophy/atrial enlargement and right-sided signs; chest x-ray for chamber enlargement, valve/annular calcification, pulmonary vascular changes (Kerley B lines, enlarged azygos vein indicating RA hypertension)"],"redFlags":["Abrupt development of acute aortic regurgitation (e.g., from IE or paravalvular leak after AVR/TAVI) in a noncompliant, nondilated, hypertrophied LV, causing rapid rise in diastolic pressure and severe heart failure","New atrial fibrillation in moderate mitral disease precipitating heart failure when severe aortic disease coexists","Significant paravalvular regurgitation after transcatheter AVR (risk factor for short- to intermediate-term death)","Symptoms of right heart failure (abdominal fullness/bloating, edema, ascites) signaling advanced disease","ECG or chest x-ray signs of pulmonary hypertension or RA hypertension in a patient with left-sided valve disease"],"prognosis":"Natural history is usually driven by the dominant valve lesion; for moderate mixed AS/AR in which stenosis dominates, the course tends to parallel isolated severe AS. Patients with multiple or mixed disease may develop limiting symptoms or physiologic impairment even with only moderate lesions. Persistent significant secondary MR after AVR carries worse functional outcomes and survival, and significant paravalvular regurgitation after transcatheter AVR is a notable risk factor for short- to intermediate-term death.","pitfalls":["The Gorlin formula is inaccurate in mixed aortic valve disease because augmented transvalvular flow from AR inflates the gradient relative to true orifice size.","Doppler-derived LV-aortic gradients and systolic murmur intensity overestimate true valve orifice severity in mixed AS/AR with preserved LV function.","A high mitral E-wave velocity in severe MR may be misread as indicating significant MS contribution to LA hypertension.","Low cardiac output from a proximal lesion (or AF) can mask the true severity of a more distal aortic or tricuspid lesion, leading to underestimation.","A mid-systolic outflow murmur is common in significant AR even without valve sclerosis/stenosis, so other signs of AS must be sought rather than assuming combined disease.","Combined aortic and mitral valve replacement carries significantly higher perioperative mortality than single-valve surgery, so the decision must be made carefully."],"management":["Valve intervention directed by the dominant lesion, whether surgical repair or replacement or a transcatheter approach such as TAVI or transcatheter mitral procedures, addressing multiple valves at one operation where appropriate","Percutaneous balloon valvotomy for suitable rheumatic mitral stenosis","Medical heart-failure therapy with diuretics and afterload reduction, plus rate or rhythm control and anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation, whose onset can be critically deleterious","Secondary antibiotic prophylaxis against rheumatic recurrence and vigilance for infective endocarditis","Serial echocardiographic surveillance of all valves, chamber size and pulmonary pressures, recognising that a proximal lesion may mask a distal one","Heart-team multidisciplinary assessment to determine the timing and mode of intervention, including right and left heart catheterisation when noninvasive data are discrepant","Management of pulmonary hypertension and referral for surgery before irreversible ventricular dysfunction develops"],"severityClassification":"No discrete severity grading scheme for combined disease; severity is determined by an integrated clinical and noninvasive assessment that identifies the dominant lesion, whose grading and management recommendations are then applied, with attention to lesion severity, ventricular remodeling, functional capacity, and PA pressures.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2065","Harrison 22e p.2066","Harrison 22e p.2067"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_endocrine_neoplasia":{"name":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MEN1","MEN2","MEN syndromes"],"chapter":400,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3079","clinicalPearls":["MEN1 is characterized primarily by the triad of parathyroid, pancreatic islet, and pituitary tumors (the '3 Ps'); it is an autosomal dominant syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.392/ch.388).","MEN2 predisposes to medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC), pheochromocytoma, and hyperparathyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3079).","There are three familial forms of MTC: MEN 2A, MEN 2B, and familial MTC without other features of MEN; MTC is more aggressive in MEN 2B than in MEN 2A, and familial MTC is more aggressive than sporadic MTC (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","MEN1 is caused by inactivating germline mutations of the MEN1 (menin) tumor-suppressor gene on chromosome 11q13; about half of affected patients develop prolactinomas, while acromegaly and Cushing's disease are less common (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.392).","MEN2 is caused by activating point mutations of the RET protooncogene (a receptor tyrosine kinase) in a single allele, in contrast to the tumor-suppressor loss-of-function mechanism of MEN1 (Harrison 22e p.3079).","Early genetic screening for RET mutations identifies individuals at risk for MEN2 who may benefit from prophylactic thyroidectomy and biochemical screening for pheochromocytoma and hyperparathyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3079).","All patients with MTC should be tested for RET mutations, because genetic counseling and cascade testing of family members can then be offered to those who test positive (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397)."],"pathophysiology":"MEN1 follows the two-hit tumor-suppressor paradigm: the affected individual inherits a mutant copy of the MEN1 gene (menin, chromosome 11q13), and tumorigenesis follows a somatic 'second hit' (deletion or point mutation) that inactivates the remaining normal allele. In contrast, MEN2 is caused by activating point mutations in a single allele of the RET protooncogene, encoding a receptor tyrosine kinase; constitutive RET activity leads to thyroid C-cell hyperplasia in childhood that precedes the development of medullary thyroid carcinoma (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.388/ch.397).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Other familial pituitary tumor / endocrine neoplasia syndromes: MEN 4 (CDKN1B mutation; hyperparathyroidism and pituitary adenomas), Carney complex (PRKAR1A; spotty skin pigmentation, myxomas, endocrine tumors), McCune-Albright syndrome, and familial isolated pituitary adenomas (AIP) (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.392).","Other inherited predispositions to pheochromocytoma that overlap with MEN2: von Hippel-Lindau disease, neurofibromatosis, and hereditary paraganglioma syndromes (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.247)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Germline RET mutation testing in all patients with medullary thyroid carcinoma, with cascade screening of relatives (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","MEN1 (menin) gene testing in patients with the parathyroid/pancreatic-islet/pituitary tumor pattern or relevant family history (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.392/ch.376).","Biochemical screening for pheochromocytoma and hyperparathyroidism in RET-mutation carriers identified as at risk for MEN2 (Harrison 22e p.3079)."],"redFlags":["Exclude pheochromocytoma BEFORE any surgery: prior to surgery, pheochromocytoma should be excluded in all patients with a RET mutation (failure to do so risks an intraoperative hypertensive crisis) (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","MTC in the context of MEN 2B carries a more aggressive course than MEN 2A and warrants early/prophylactic intervention (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397)."],"prognosis":"Prognosis is driven by the component tumors and genotype. MTC is more aggressive in MEN 2B than in MEN 2A, and familial MTC is more aggressive than sporadic MTC; early genetic identification permits prophylactic thyroidectomy before malignant transformation, improving outcome (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","pitfalls":["Proceeding to surgery (e.g., thyroidectomy or parathyroid surgery) without first excluding an unsuspected pheochromocytoma in a RET-mutation carrier (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","Failing to perform RET testing in all MTC patients and thereby missing cascade screening opportunities for at-risk family members (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397)."],"management":["Genotype-directed prophylactic total thyroidectomy in RET-mutation carriers, with total thyroidectomy and central neck dissection for established medullary thyroid carcinoma and tyrosine-kinase inhibitors such as vandetanib or cabozantinib for advanced disease","Exclude and treat phaeochromocytoma before any surgery, using alpha- then beta-adrenergic blockade followed by adrenalectomy","In MEN1, parathyroidectomy for hyperparathyroidism, proton-pump inhibitors for gastrinoma-driven Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, and surgical or medical treatment of pancreatic neuroendocrine and pituitary tumours including dopamine agonists for prolactinoma","Germline RET or MEN1 mutation testing of the index case with cascade genetic screening of at-risk relatives","Lifelong biochemical and imaging surveillance for the syndrome's component tumours","Genetic counselling for these autosomal dominant conditions","Coordinated multidisciplinary endocrine, surgical and oncological care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3079"],"crossLinks":["pheo","thyroid_cancer"]},"multiple_endocrine_neoplasia_type_1":{"name":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MEN1","Wermer syndrome","menin-related MEN","MEN type 1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant tumour syndrome caused by inactivating mutations in the MEN1 gene encoding menin","Classically involves the three P organs: parathyroid glands, pancreatic islets and enteroendocrine cells, and the anterior pituitary","Primary hyperparathyroidism is the most common and usually earliest manifestation, typically from multigland parathyroid hyperplasia","Pancreatic and duodenal neuroendocrine tumours include gastrinomas, which can cause Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, and insulinomas","Pituitary adenomas, most often prolactinomas, complete the classic triad","Other associated lesions include foregut carcinoids, adrenal cortical tumours and cutaneous features such as angiofibromas and collagenomas","Gene carriers require lifelong biochemical and imaging surveillance to detect tumours early"],"pathophysiology":"MEN1 encodes menin, a nuclear protein acting as a tumour suppressor involved in transcriptional regulation and genome stability. Affected individuals inherit one defective allele, and tumours arise when the remaining functional allele is lost within an endocrine cell, consistent with a two-hit mechanism. Loss of menin function removes a brake on proliferation in susceptible endocrine tissues, giving rise to hyperplasia and multiple, often synchronous or metachronous, neoplasms across the parathyroids, pancreatic islets and pituitary. The multiglandular and recurrent nature of disease reflects this germline predisposition throughout the endocrine system.","additionalDifferentials":["Sporadic primary hyperparathyroidism","Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2","Sporadic pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour","Sporadic pituitary adenoma","Other hereditary endocrine tumour syndromes such as MEN4 or hyperparathyroidism-jaw tumour syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium and parathyroid hormone to detect primary hyperparathyroidism","Gut hormone profile including fasting gastrin and other pancreatic peptides","Prolactin and anterior pituitary hormone assessment","Cross-sectional and functional imaging of the pancreas and pituitary for tumour surveillance","Molecular genetic testing of MEN1 for the index case and at-risk relatives"],"redFlags":["Severe hypercalcaemia or peptic ulceration and diarrhoea suggesting a functioning gastrinoma","Neuroglycopenic symptoms indicating an insulinoma","Rapidly enlarging or metastatic pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour","Visual field loss or hormone excess from an expanding pituitary adenoma"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends largely on pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours, which are the leading disease-related cause of death, so structured lifelong surveillance and timely intervention are central to improving long-term outcomes.","pitfalls":["Treating hyperparathyroidism as single-gland disease and overlooking multigland involvement","Failing to screen for MEN1 in a young patient with primary hyperparathyroidism or multiple endocrine tumours","Not offering genetic testing and surveillance to at-risk family members","Attributing peptic ulcer disease to common causes and missing an underlying gastrinoma"],"management":["Subtotal or total parathyroidectomy for the multigland primary hyperparathyroidism, with cinacalcet where surgery is unsuitable","Proton-pump inhibitors to control gastrinoma-related Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, with surgical resection of insulinomas and larger pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours and somatostatin analogues where appropriate","Dopamine agonists for prolactinoma and trans-sphenoidal surgery for other functioning or compressive pituitary adenomas","Structured lifelong biochemical and cross-sectional and functional imaging surveillance, since pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours are the leading cause of disease-related death","Molecular MEN1 testing of the index case with cascade screening of at-risk relatives","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant syndrome","Multidisciplinary endocrine and surgical management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_endocrine_neoplasia_type_2":{"name":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 (RET)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MEN2","MEN2A","MEN2B","Sipple syndrome","RET-related MEN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant syndrome caused by activating mutations in the RET proto-oncogene","Medullary thyroid carcinoma, arising from calcitonin-producing C cells, is the defining and most consequential feature","Phaeochromocytoma occurs in both MEN2A and MEN2B and must be excluded before any surgery","MEN2A additionally features primary hyperparathyroidism, whereas MEN2B features a marfanoid habitus and mucosal neuromas but not hyperparathyroidism","The specific RET codon mutation stratifies medullary thyroid carcinoma risk and guides the timing of prophylactic thyroidectomy","Calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen serve as tumour markers for medullary thyroid carcinoma","Genetic testing enables presymptomatic diagnosis and risk-directed preventive thyroid surgery in gene carriers"],"pathophysiology":"RET encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase important for the development of neural crest-derived tissues, including thyroid C cells and the adrenal medulla. Germline activating mutations drive constitutive kinase signalling that promotes proliferation, so C cells progress through hyperplasia to medullary thyroid carcinoma and chromaffin cells give rise to phaeochromocytoma. The particular mutated codon correlates with how aggressive and early the medullary carcinoma is, which underpins genotype-directed decisions about the age at which prophylactic thyroidectomy is undertaken. Distinct mutation patterns account for the differing feature sets of MEN2A and MEN2B.","additionalDifferentials":["Sporadic medullary thyroid carcinoma","Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1","Sporadic or other hereditary phaeochromocytoma syndromes","Von Hippel-Lindau disease and neurofibromatosis type 1","Familial isolated medullary thyroid carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen as medullary thyroid carcinoma markers","Plasma or urinary metanephrines to screen for phaeochromocytoma","Serum calcium and parathyroid hormone to detect hyperparathyroidism in MEN2A","Neck ultrasound and adrenal imaging for tumour localisation","Molecular genetic testing of RET with codon-level characterisation"],"redFlags":["Unrecognised phaeochromocytoma before surgery risking a hypertensive crisis","Rising calcitonin indicating progressive or metastatic medullary thyroid carcinoma","Aggressive early-onset medullary carcinoma associated with high-risk RET mutations","Marfanoid features and mucosal neuromas in a child suggesting MEN2B needing urgent evaluation"],"prognosis":"Outcome is dominated by medullary thyroid carcinoma; presymptomatic diagnosis with genotype-directed prophylactic thyroidectomy can be curative, whereas late diagnosis with metastatic disease carries a poorer prognosis.","pitfalls":["Operating on the thyroid without first excluding a phaeochromocytoma","Missing the diagnosis in apparently sporadic medullary thyroid carcinoma that is actually hereditary","Delaying prophylactic thyroidectomy beyond the window indicated by the RET genotype","Failing to offer cascade genetic testing to at-risk relatives"],"management":["Genotype-directed prophylactic total thyroidectomy timed by the specific RET codon mutation, with total thyroidectomy and central neck dissection for established medullary thyroid carcinoma","Tyrosine-kinase inhibitors such as vandetanib or cabozantinib for advanced or metastatic medullary thyroid carcinoma, monitoring calcitonin and carcinoembryonic antigen as tumour markers","Exclude phaeochromocytoma before any surgery and treat with alpha- then beta-adrenergic blockade followed by adrenal-sparing adrenalectomy","Parathyroidectomy for primary hyperparathyroidism in MEN2A","Germline RET testing with codon characterisation and cascade screening of at-risk relatives","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant syndrome","Lifelong biochemical and imaging surveillance for the component tumours"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_endocrine_neoplasia_type_4":{"name":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 4 (CDKN1B)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["multiple endocrine neoplasia type 4","MEN4","MENX","CDKN1B","p27 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / OMIM","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare autosomal dominant syndrome caused by variants in CDKN1B, encoding the cell-cycle inhibitor p27","Clinically resembles MEN1, with primary hyperparathyroidism and anterior pituitary tumours as the commonest features","It is an important consideration in patients with a MEN1-like phenotype who test negative for MEN1 mutations","Parathyroid disease is usually the earliest and most frequent manifestation, causing hypercalcaemia","Pituitary tumours may be functioning (for example somatotroph or corticotroph) or non-functioning, and other neuroendocrine and reproductive-tract tumours are reported","Management mirrors MEN1: biochemical and imaging surveillance for parathyroid, pituitary, and neuroendocrine tumours with tumour-directed treatment","Test CDKN1B in a MEN1-like presentation that is MEN1 mutation-negative"],"pathophysiology":"CDKN1B encodes p27, a cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor that restrains progression through the cell cycle and thus functions as a tumour suppressor in endocrine tissues. Germline loss-of-function variants reduce p27 activity, releasing this brake on proliferation and predisposing to tumour development in the parathyroid glands, anterior pituitary, and other neuroendocrine tissues. The resulting phenotype overlaps closely with MEN1 because both disorders disturb pathways controlling endocrine cell growth, producing primary hyperparathyroidism and pituitary adenomas as the leading manifestations. Because the syndrome is rare and clinically similar to MEN1, molecular testing is needed to distinguish the two.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1","Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2","Sporadic primary hyperparathyroidism","Familial isolated pituitary adenoma","Hyperparathyroidism-jaw tumour syndrome","Familial isolated hyperparathyroidism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium and parathyroid hormone for primary hyperparathyroidism","Anterior pituitary hormone profile including prolactin, IGF-1, and cortisol axis testing","Pituitary MRI for adenomas","Gut hormone and neuroendocrine tumour screening as clinically indicated","MEN1 sequencing that is negative, prompting further testing","CDKN1B gene sequencing"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic hypercalcaemia from hyperparathyroidism","Visual field loss or hormonal excess from a pituitary macroadenoma","Features of a functioning neuroendocrine tumour","Progressive disease across multiple endocrine organs"],"prognosis":"The outlook depends on the specific tumours that arise; with regular surveillance and timely treatment of parathyroid and pituitary disease most patients do well, though the syndrome is too rare for precise long-term risk figures.","pitfalls":["Stopping the diagnostic workup at a negative MEN1 test in a MEN1-like patient","Underestimating the need for lifelong multi-organ surveillance","Attributing isolated hyperparathyroidism to sporadic disease without a family assessment","Overlooking non-functioning pituitary tumours that are clinically silent"],"management":["Tumour-directed definitive therapy: parathyroidectomy for primary hyperparathyroidism, and transsphenoidal surgery or targeted medical therapy (dopamine agonists for prolactinomas, somatostatin analogues for growth-hormone-secreting adenomas) for pituitary tumours","Correct hypercalcaemia with rehydration and, where required, bisphosphonates or the calcimimetic cinacalcet while awaiting surgery","Lifelong biochemical surveillance of serum calcium, parathyroid hormone and anterior pituitary hormones to detect recurrent or new endocrine tumours","Periodic pituitary MRI and screening for gastro-entero-pancreatic and other neuroendocrine tumours as clinically indicated","Manage within a combined endocrinology, endocrine-surgery and neurosurgery multidisciplinary team","Offer genetic counselling and cascade CDKN1B testing to at-risk relatives, with structured surveillance of confirmed carriers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","OMIM entry for multiple endocrine neoplasia type 4"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_epiphyseal_dysplasia":{"name":"Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MED","Fairbank disease","COMP","COL9A1/COL9A2/COL9A3","MATN3","epiphyseal dysplasia multiplex"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A genetically heterogeneous disorder of epiphyseal cartilage; dominant forms arise from variants in COMP, COL9A1/2/3 or MATN3, and a recessive form from DTDST/SLC26A2 (also causing club feet)","Presents in childhood with joint pain (especially hips and knees), stiffness, a waddling gait and mildly short or normal stature","Radiographs show small, irregular, fragmented or flattened epiphyses that ossify late, most evident at the hips, knees and ankles","The metaphyses and spine are relatively normal, distinguishing it from spondyloepiphyseal and metaphyseal dysplasias","Early-onset osteoarthritis, particularly of the hips, is the major long-term consequence","A crucial mimic is the epiphyseal dysgenesis of untreated congenital hypothyroidism, which must be excluded biochemically","COMP variants also cause pseudoachondroplasia, so MED sits on a phenotypic spectrum with it"],"pathophysiology":"Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia results from defects in structural proteins of the cartilage extracellular matrix, most commonly cartilage oligomeric matrix protein (COMP), type IX collagen or matrilin-3. Mutant proteins misfold and are retained within chondrocytes, causing endoplasmic reticulum stress and impaired secretion of a properly organised matrix. This preferentially disturbs the epiphyseal (articular and growth) cartilage, producing delayed and irregular epiphyseal ossification. The resulting incongruent, poorly formed joint surfaces predispose to premature degenerative osteoarthritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Epiphyseal dysgenesis of congenital hypothyroidism","Bilateral Perthes disease (avascular necrosis of the hips)","Pseudoachondroplasia","Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia tarda","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Meyer dysplasia of the femoral head"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey demonstrating symmetrical epiphyseal irregularity with normal metaphyses and spine","Thyroid function tests to exclude hypothyroid epiphyseal dysgenesis","Molecular genetic testing (COMP, COL9A1/2/3, MATN3, SLC26A2)","Hip and knee imaging to assess joint congruity and early osteoarthritic change","Assessment of feet and family members, particularly for the recessive SLC26A2 form"],"redFlags":["Progressive hip pain and stiffness heralding early osteoarthritis needing orthopaedic input","Bilateral symmetrical femoral head changes that could be mistaken for Perthes disease","Untreated hypothyroidism as a reversible mimic with wider developmental consequences","Significant functional limitation or joint deformity in a growing child"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is normal and stature is often only mildly reduced, but premature osteoarthritis, especially of the hips, is common and may lead to joint replacement in adulthood.","pitfalls":["Failing to check thyroid function and missing treatable hypothyroid epiphyseal dysgenesis","Misdiagnosing symmetrical hip changes as bilateral Perthes disease","Overlooking the milder end of the pseudoachondroplasia spectrum","Not counselling about recessive versus dominant inheritance depending on the causative gene"],"management":["No disease-modifying drug therapy exists; care centres on protecting joints and preserving function through physiotherapy and activity modification to reduce load on the hips and knees","Analgesia for joint pain using simple analgesics and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents for symptomatic osteoarthritis","Weight management and low-impact exercise to limit accelerated wear of incongruent joint surfaces","Orthopaedic surgery for symptomatic deformity or early osteoarthritis, including realignment osteotomy and eventual joint (particularly hip) replacement","Exclude and biochemically treat coexisting hypothyroidism, since untreated congenital hypothyroidism mimics the epiphyseal changes","Radiographic monitoring of the hips and knees for progressive joint incongruity and degenerative change","Genetic counselling for the dominant (COMP, COL9A, MATN3) and recessive (SLC26A2) forms, with clinical assessment of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Multiple Epiphyseal Dysplasia, Dominant"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_evanescent_white_dot_syndrome":{"name":"Multiple evanescent white dot syndrome (MEWDS)","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MEWDS","evanescent white dot syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characteristically affects young women, often after a viral prodrome","Presents with acute, painless, unilateral visual loss with photopsia and a temporal or paracentral scotoma","Multiple small grey-white dots appear at the level of the outer retina and retinal pigment epithelium across the posterior pole and mid-periphery","A characteristic granularity of the fovea is a helpful diagnostic clue","An enlarged blind spot is frequently found on visual field testing","Fluorescein angiography shows a wreath-like pattern of hyperfluorescence and indocyanine green angiography shows hypofluorescent spots","The condition is self-limiting, so management is observation with recovery of vision over weeks"],"pathophysiology":"MEWDS is thought to represent a transient inflammatory disturbance of the photoreceptor and outer retinal complex together with the choriocapillaris, frequently following a viral illness. Disruption of the ellipsoid (photoreceptor) zone corresponds to the white dots and to the patient's visual symptoms. The process resolves spontaneously as the photoreceptor layer recovers, and vision returns to normal in most cases.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute posterior multifocal placoid pigment epitheliopathy","Multifocal choroiditis","Punctate inner choroidopathy","Acute zonal occult outer retinopathy","Birdshot chorioretinopathy","Acute idiopathic blind spot enlargement","Diffuse unilateral subacute neuroretinitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination looking for the white dots and foveal granularity","Fundus autofluorescence showing hyperautofluorescent dots","Optical coherence tomography demonstrating ellipsoid zone disruption","Fluorescein angiography showing wreath-like hyperfluorescence","Indocyanine green angiography showing hypofluorescent spots","Visual field testing to document an enlarged blind spot"],"redFlags":["Bilateral or recurrent disease, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Progressive or persistent visual loss suggesting the acute zonal occult outer retinopathy spectrum","Features suggesting an infective cause such as syphilis","Profound loss that fails to recover"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; almost all patients recover good vision spontaneously over weeks to a few months, although mild residual symptoms occasionally persist.","pitfalls":["Over-investigating or treating a self-limiting condition","Confusing it with other white-dot syndromes","Missing occasional recurrence or overlap with the acute zonal occult outer retinopathy spectrum","Failing to exclude syphilis in atypical cases"],"management":["Reassurance and observation, as the condition is self-limiting and vision recovers spontaneously in most patients over weeks to months","No specific pharmacological therapy is required, and corticosteroids are not routinely indicated given spontaneous resolution","Serial optical coherence tomography and fundus autofluorescence to document recovery of the ellipsoid (photoreceptor) zone","Visual field testing to monitor resolution of the enlarged blind spot and scotoma","Exclude alternative white-dot syndromes and infective or inflammatory mimics before settling on the diagnosis","Ophthalmology (retina) follow-up to confirm recovery and identify the rare recurrence or progression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course: Uveitis and Ocular Inflammation"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_hereditary_exostoses":{"name":"Multiple hereditary exostoses","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["EXT1","EXT2","hereditary multiple osteochondromas","diaphyseal aclasis","MHE","MO"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant disorder caused by loss-of-function variants in EXT1 or EXT2, encoding enzymes for heparan sulphate biosynthesis","Characterised by multiple osteochondromas (exostoses) arising from the metaphyses of long bones, ribs, scapulae and pelvis, typically capped by cartilage and pointing away from the joint","Lesions appear in childhood, enlarge during growth and characteristically stop growing after skeletal maturity","Common complications include limb-length discrepancy, forearm deformity (ulnar shortening/bowing with radial head dislocation), genu valgum and coxa valga","Osteochondromas can cause pain, mechanical restriction, bursitis or compression of adjacent nerves, vessels or tendons","The most important long-term risk is malignant transformation of an osteochondroma to secondary chondrosarcoma, more likely with axial/proximal lesions","New pain, growth of a lesion after skeletal maturity, or a thickened cartilage cap should prompt evaluation for malignant transformation"],"pathophysiology":"Multiple hereditary exostoses is caused by inactivating variants in EXT1 or EXT2, which encode glycosyltransferases that synthesise heparan sulphate. Heparan sulphate proteoglycans regulate the diffusion and signalling of morphogens such as Indian hedgehog in the growth plate. Deficient heparan sulphate disrupts the normal polarity and containment of proliferating chondrocytes at the perichondrium, so ectopic cartilage-capped bony outgrowths (osteochondromas) form at the metaphyses and grow by endochondral ossification. Malignant transformation reflects additional somatic events within the cartilage cap.","additionalDifferentials":["Solitary (sporadic) osteochondroma","Ollier disease (multiple enchondromas)","Maffucci syndrome","Metachondromatosis","Trevor disease (dysplasia epiphysealis hemimelica)","Secondary chondrosarcoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey to map the number, size and distribution of osteochondromas","EXT1/EXT2 molecular genetic testing","Cross-sectional imaging (MRI) to measure cartilage cap thickness when transformation is suspected","Targeted imaging of forearm, knees and hips to assess deformity and joint mechanics","Neurovascular assessment where a lesion compresses adjacent structures"],"redFlags":["Growth of a lesion or new pain after skeletal maturity suggesting malignant transformation","A thickened cartilage cap on imaging concerning for secondary chondrosarcoma","Neurovascular compromise or spinal cord compression from an osteochondroma","Rapidly enlarging axial or pelvic lesions with the highest transformation risk"],"prognosis":"Most people have a normal lifespan with orthopaedic morbidity from deformity and mechanical complications; the principal serious risk is secondary chondrosarcoma in a minority, making lifelong surveillance for malignant transformation important.","pitfalls":["Reassuring a patient about a lesion that has grown after skeletal maturity, when this is a warning sign","Failing to arrange surveillance for malignant transformation, especially of axial lesions","Overlooking forearm deformity and radial head dislocation that benefit from timely orthopaedic correction","Confusing the diagnosis with enchondromatosis, which involves intramedullary rather than surface cartilage lesions"],"management":["No drug therapy alters osteochondroma formation; management is orthopaedic, with surgical excision of symptomatic lesions causing pain, mechanical restriction or neurovascular and tendon compression","Corrective surgery for forearm deformity, limb-length discrepancy and angular deformity such as genu valgum, using osteotomy, guided growth or limb reconstruction","Lifelong surveillance for malignant transformation to secondary chondrosarcoma, with particular attention to axial and proximal lesions","Cross-sectional MRI to measure cartilage-cap thickness, prompting excision when a lesion grows after skeletal maturity or becomes painful","Neurovascular assessment and decompression where an exostosis compresses adjacent nerves or vessels","Genetic counselling for autosomal dominant EXT1/EXT2 inheritance, with cascade testing and clinical assessment of relatives","Escalate to a specialist bone-tumour (sarcoma) service when malignant transformation is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Hereditary Multiple Osteochondromas"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_sulfatase_deficiency":{"name":"Multiple sulfatase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Neurometabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MSD","Austin disease","Mucosulfatidosis","SUMF1 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Inborn errors of metabolism references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic SUMF1 variants; SUMF1 encodes the formylglycine-generating enzyme (FGE) that post-translationally activates every human sulfatase.","Because a single defect cripples all sulfatases simultaneously, the phenotype blends metachromatic leukodystrophy, several mucopolysaccharidoses, X-linked ichthyosis and chondrodysplasia punctata.","Suspect the diagnosis whenever a child shows features of more than one sulfatase-deficiency disorder at once — for example neuroregression together with coarse features, skeletal change and ichthyosis.","Ichthyosis (from steroid sulfatase deficiency) coexisting with a leukodystrophy-like decline is a particularly useful pointer away from isolated metachromatic leukodystrophy.","The neurological picture resembles metachromatic leukodystrophy — developmental regression, spasticity and a demyelinating peripheral neuropathy — but is broader.","Additional features include hepatosplenomegaly, dysostosis multiplex, coarse facies, deafness and, in some, corneal clouding and cardiac involvement.","Diagnosis is supported by finding reduced activity across several sulfatases in leucocytes or fibroblasts, then confirmed by SUMF1 sequencing.","A clinical spectrum exists from a severe neonatal MPS-like form to late-infantile (most common) and juvenile presentations."],"pathophysiology":"SUMF1 encodes the formylglycine-generating enzyme (FGE), which resides in the endoplasmic reticulum and carries out an obligatory post-translational modification of newly synthesised sulfatases, oxidising a conserved cysteine residue within their active site to Cα-formylglycine. This modified residue is essential for catalytic activity, so a single deficient FGE inactivates the entire family of human sulfatases. The resulting global loss of sulfatase function impairs degradation of sulfated substrates throughout the body: sulfatides accumulate in central and peripheral myelin (as in metachromatic leukodystrophy), glycosaminoglycans accumulate in lysosomes and connective tissue (as in the mucopolysaccharidoses), cholesterol sulfate accumulates in the epidermis (producing ichthyosis), and defective arylsulfatase E activity disturbs cartilage and bone development (chondrodysplasia punctata). The convergence of lysosomal storage, dysmyelination and impaired epidermal and skeletal turnover produces the combined multisystem, neurodegenerative phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated metachromatic leukodystrophy (arylsulfatase A deficiency)","Individual mucopolysaccharidoses, particularly types II, III, IVA and VI","X-linked ichthyosis occurring in isolation","Krabbe disease and other leukodystrophies","GM1 gangliosidosis and other lysosomal storage disorders with coarse features","Chondrodysplasia punctata of other cause","Peroxisomal biogenesis disorders with combined neurological and skeletal features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Enzyme assays of multiple sulfatases in leucocytes or cultured fibroblasts, showing generalised reduction","SUMF1 gene sequencing for definitive confirmation","Urinary glycosaminoglycan quantification and electrophoresis (raised)","Urinary sulfatide analysis (raised)","Nerve conduction studies demonstrating a demyelinating peripheral neuropathy","Brain MRI showing a leukodystrophy pattern with white-matter signal change","Skeletal survey for dysostosis multiplex and chondrodysplasia punctata"],"redFlags":["Developmental regression with loss of previously acquired milestones","Neurological decline occurring together with ichthyosis or coarsening facial features","Rapidly progressive spasticity or bulbar dysfunction","New sensorineural hearing loss alongside multisystem involvement"],"prognosis":"The disorder is progressive with a generally poor outlook; the severe neonatal form is often fatal in early childhood, and the more common late-infantile form leads to relentless neurological deterioration over a few years after onset. No curative therapy is available and care is supportive.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing only one component such as metachromatic leukodystrophy and missing the unifying combined disorder","Assuming several independent diagnoses rather than a single SUMF1 defect","Confusing reduced arylsulfatase A activity with common arylsulfatase A pseudodeficiency","Overlooking subtle ichthyosis or skeletal changes that would point to the combined phenotype","Relying on a single sulfatase assay rather than testing several enzymes when the disorder is suspected"],"management":["No curative or enzyme-replacement therapy exists; care is supportive and directed at the individual manifestations","Neurological supportive care mirroring metachromatic leukodystrophy, with antispasticity agents such as baclofen, management of the demyelinating neuropathy and physiotherapy for progressive spasticity","Seizure control with antiepileptic drugs where epilepsy develops","Skin care with emollients and keratolytics for the ichthyosis arising from steroid sulfatase deficiency","Nutritional support, including gastrostomy feeding for dysphagia, alongside airway and respiratory care","Surveillance and management of the mucopolysaccharidosis-like features, with hearing aids for deafness, ophthalmic review for corneal clouding and cardiac assessment","Autosomal recessive SUMF1 genetic counselling with family testing, and early palliative-care involvement given the progressive course"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inborn errors of metabolism reference texts (standard reference)","Paediatric neurology reference texts (standard reference)","Lysosomal storage disorder clinical references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_system_atrophy":{"name":"Multiple system atrophy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MSA","MSA-P","MSA-C","Shy-Drager syndrome","olivopontocerebellar atrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A sporadic alpha-synucleinopathy combining autonomic failure with either parkinsonism (MSA-P) or cerebellar ataxia (MSA-C)","Prominent, early autonomic dysfunction such as orthostatic hypotension, urinary retention or incontinence, and erectile dysfunction is a defining feature","The parkinsonism is usually symmetric, rapidly progressive, and responds poorly or only briefly to dopaminergic therapy","MSA-C presents with gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria, and eye movement abnormalities","Pyramidal signs, marked dysphonia or dysarthria, and inspiratory stridor may develop and stridor carries prognostic and airway significance","Early falls, antecollis (marked neck flexion), and cold, dusky hands can be supportive clinical clues","MRI may show putaminal, pontine (hot cross bun sign), or cerebellar atrophy","Distinguishing MSA from Parkinson disease rests on early severe autonomic failure, poor levodopa response, and cerebellar or pyramidal signs"],"pathophysiology":"MSA is characterised by abnormal aggregation of alpha-synuclein in oligodendrocytes as glial cytoplasmic inclusions, with associated neuronal loss and gliosis. Degeneration involves the striatonigral system (producing parkinsonism), the olivopontocerebellar system (producing ataxia), and central autonomic pathways including brainstem and intermediolateral spinal cord neurons (producing autonomic failure). The distribution of pathology determines whether the parkinsonian or cerebellar phenotype predominates, although most patients have overlapping features and prominent dysautonomia.","additionalDifferentials":["Parkinson disease","Progressive supranuclear palsy","Corticobasal degeneration","Pure autonomic failure","Sporadic and hereditary cerebellar ataxias","Dementia with Lewy bodies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain for putaminal, pontine, and cerebellar atrophy and the hot cross bun sign","Autonomic function testing including orthostatic blood pressure measurement","Assessment of bladder function and post-void residual volume","Trial of dopaminergic therapy to document the limited response","Sleep evaluation for stridor and REM sleep behaviour disorder","Exclusion of secondary causes of ataxia or parkinsonism"],"redFlags":["Inspiratory stridor or sleep-related airway obstruction, which may cause sudden deterioration","Severe symptomatic orthostatic hypotension causing syncope and falls","Rapid progression with loss of mobility over a short period","Significant dysphagia with aspiration risk"],"prognosis":"MSA progresses faster than Parkinson disease, with loss of independent walking usually within a few years and median survival of roughly a decade; stridor, aspiration, and autonomic complications drive mortality.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing Parkinson disease and missing early prominent autonomic failure","Overtreating with dopaminergic drugs despite poor benefit and worsening hypotension","Failing to recognise stridor as a potentially life-threatening sign","Overlooking urinary retention and its complications","Attributing early falls and ataxia to ageing rather than the disease"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists; a supervised trial of levodopa is warranted since parkinsonism may respond briefly, though the response is usually poor and transient","Treat orthostatic hypotension with non-pharmacological measures (compression garments, head-up tilt of the bed, increased salt and fluid) and pressor agents such as fludrocortisone, midodrine or droxidopa","Manage the neurogenic bladder with intermittent catheterisation for retention and appropriate agents for detrusor overactivity, with monitoring of post-void residual volume","Address inspiratory stridor and respiratory compromise with sleep evaluation, continuous positive airway pressure and consideration of tracheostomy, as stridor carries airway and prognostic significance","Supportive multidisciplinary care with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech and language therapy for dysarthria and dysphagia, and falls prevention","Manage REM sleep behaviour disorder and coexisting depression","Undertake early advance-care planning and involve palliative care given the rapid progression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"multisystem_inflammatory_syndrome_children":{"name":"Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C)","system":"Rheumatology / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MIS-C","PIMS-TS","paediatric inflammatory multisystem syndrome","post-COVID hyperinflammatory syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology / ILAR & PRINTO criteria","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A post-infectious hyperinflammatory syndrome occurring weeks after SARS-CoV-2 infection, with features overlapping Kawasaki disease and toxic shock","Presents with persistent high fever plus multisystem involvement, commonly gastrointestinal (abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhoea that can mimic an acute abdomen), mucocutaneous, and cardiovascular","Cardiac involvement is prominent, including myocardial dysfunction, shock, coronary artery dilatation or aneurysm and conduction abnormalities","Markedly raised inflammatory markers, along with elevated ferritin, D-dimer, natriuretic peptides and cardiac troponin, are typical","Evidence of recent or current SARS-CoV-2 infection (positive serology, PCR or a clear exposure) supports the diagnosis","Distinguishing MIS-C from severe acute COVID-19, Kawasaki disease and bacterial sepsis is essential and time-critical","Treatment centres on immunomodulation with IVIG and corticosteroids, with biologic therapy for refractory cases and supportive intensive care for shock"],"pathophysiology":"Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children is a delayed, dysregulated immune response following SARS-CoV-2 infection. Weeks after exposure, an exaggerated adaptive and innate immune reaction, with widespread cytokine release, endothelial activation and, in some, superantigen-like T-cell stimulation, produces a hyperinflammatory, vasculitic state. This drives multiorgan injury, most notably myocardial dysfunction, vasodilatory and cardiogenic shock, and coronary artery involvement reminiscent of Kawasaki disease, together with a systemic inflammatory and prothrombotic milieu.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe acute COVID-19","Kawasaki disease","Bacterial sepsis and toxic shock syndrome","Appendicitis or other surgical acute abdomen","Macrophage activation syndrome or systemic JIA flare","Myocarditis of other aetiology","Other viral or rickettsial infections"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Inflammatory markers (erythrocyte sedimentation rate, C-reactive protein), ferritin and full blood count","Cardiac troponin and natriuretic peptides with electrocardiography and echocardiography for cardiac assessment and coronary imaging","SARS-CoV-2 PCR and serology plus documentation of exposure","Coagulation studies including D-dimer and fibrinogen","Blood cultures and infection screen to exclude sepsis","Renal and liver function and electrolytes","Imaging of the abdomen when a surgical cause is being considered"],"redFlags":["Signs of shock, hypotension or poor perfusion requiring urgent resuscitation","Myocardial dysfunction, arrhythmia or coronary artery aneurysm on echocardiography","Rapid clinical deterioration despite initial treatment","Features suggesting macrophage activation or disseminated intravascular coagulation","Altered consciousness or other severe organ dysfunction"],"prognosis":"With prompt immunomodulation and intensive supportive care, most children recover, including recovery of myocardial function, though a minority sustain persistent cardiac abnormalities such as coronary aneurysms. Early recognition and treatment are the main determinants of outcome, and structured cardiology follow-up is recommended.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the prominent gastrointestinal presentation for a purely surgical abdomen","Anchoring on acute COVID-19 and missing the delayed hyperinflammatory picture","Underestimating cardiac involvement by not obtaining early echocardiography and cardiac biomarkers","Delaying immunomodulatory treatment in a deteriorating child","Failing to arrange cardiac follow-up after recovery"],"management":["First-line immunomodulation with intravenous immunoglobulin, usually combined with corticosteroids, to dampen the hyperinflammatory response","Escalation to biologic therapy with interleukin-1 or interleukin-6 antagonists such as anakinra or tocilizumab for refractory or severe disease","Haemodynamic support for vasodilatory and cardiogenic shock, with fluid resuscitation, vasoactive and inotropic agents and intensive-care monitoring","Antithrombotic therapy with antiplatelet aspirin and anticoagulation guided by coronary involvement and thrombotic risk","Empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics until bacterial sepsis and toxic shock syndrome are excluded","Cardiac surveillance with serial echocardiography, electrocardiography and cardiac biomarkers for myocardial dysfunction, arrhythmia and coronary artery aneurysms","Manage within a paediatric multidisciplinary team spanning rheumatology, cardiology, infectious diseases and intensive care, with structured cardiology follow-up after discharge"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology","WHO and RCPCH case definitions for MIS-C/PIMS-TS","ACR clinical guidance on MIS-C"],"crossLinks":[]},"mulvihill_smith_syndrome":{"name":"Mulvihill-Smith syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mulvihill-Smith progeroid syndrome","progeroid syndrome with pigmented naevi and immunodeficiency","MSS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / clinical dysmorphology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare progeroid (premature-ageing) syndrome combining a prematurely aged facial appearance, short stature, and low birth weight","Multiple pigmented naevi are a characteristic dermatological feature","Patients typically have a high-pitched voice, microcephaly, sensorineural hearing loss, and lack of subcutaneous fat contributing to the aged look","Progressive cognitive impairment or intellectual disability is often reported","An associated immunodeficiency predisposes to recurrent infections","Metabolic and endocrine disturbances, including features of insulin resistance and impaired glucose handling, may occur","The molecular basis remains incompletely defined, so diagnosis is clinical, based on the recognisable constellation of findings","There is a reported predisposition to neoplasia, warranting clinical surveillance; care is otherwise supportive"],"pathophysiology":"Mulvihill-Smith syndrome is a segmental progeroid disorder in which affected individuals display features of accelerated ageing alongside growth failure, immune dysfunction, and multiple pigmented naevi. The underlying molecular defect has not been firmly established, and the condition is regarded as clinically defined rather than genetically confirmed. The phenotype suggests disturbance of pathways governing growth, tissue maintenance, and genome or cellular stability, producing loss of subcutaneous fat, a prematurely aged appearance, immunodeficiency with susceptibility to infection, and a reported increased risk of malignancy. Endocrine and metabolic abnormalities such as insulin resistance are described, consistent with the broader tendency of progeroid syndromes to disturb metabolic homeostasis.","additionalDifferentials":["Werner syndrome and other adult progeroid syndromes","Hutchinson-Gilford progeria syndrome","Cockayne syndrome","Bloom syndrome and other genome-instability disorders","Neonatal progeroid (Wiedemann-Rautenstrauch) syndrome","Other syndromic short-stature disorders with lipodystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed clinical and dysmorphological assessment","Immunological evaluation for humoral and cellular immunodeficiency","Metabolic and endocrine screen including glucose and insulin studies","Audiometry for sensorineural hearing loss","Dermatological review and monitoring of pigmented naevi","Genetic testing to exclude defined progeroid and genome-instability syndromes"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or severe infection reflecting significant immunodeficiency","New or changing pigmented lesions or other features suggesting malignancy","Marked failure to thrive and growth arrest","Progressive neurological or cognitive decline"],"prognosis":"A chronic disorder with significant morbidity from immunodeficiency, growth failure, and reported cancer predisposition; prognosis is guarded and depends on the severity of complications.","pitfalls":["Assuming a confirmed molecular diagnosis when the condition remains clinically defined","Overlooking immunodeficiency as a treatable contributor to recurrent infection","Failing to arrange dermatological and malignancy surveillance","Confusing it with other progeroid syndromes that have distinct management and prognosis"],"management":["No specific or curative therapy exists; management is supportive and directed at the individual features","Immunological management of the associated immunodeficiency, with prompt treatment of infections, appropriate immunisation and immunoglobulin replacement where humoral deficiency is demonstrated","Nutritional support and growth monitoring for growth failure and loss of subcutaneous fat","Metabolic management of insulin resistance and impaired glucose handling with lifestyle measures and glucose-lowering therapy as required","Hearing rehabilitation with aids for sensorineural hearing loss, and developmental and educational support for cognitive impairment","Dermatological surveillance of the multiple pigmented naevi with vigilance for malignancy given the reported cancer predisposition","Genetic counselling and coordinated specialist multidisciplinary follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Clinical dysmorphology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"mumps":{"name":"Mumps","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["epidemic parotitis"],"chapter":213,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1646-1649","clinicalPearls":["Acute, self-limited systemic viral illness typically characterized by parotitis or other salivary gland swelling; suspect in any patient with parotitis or mumps complications regardless of age, vaccination status, or travel history (p.1646).","Parotitis is generally bilateral but may not be synchronous on both sides; unilateral involvement occurs in about one-third of cases, with tenderness and obliteration of the space between the earlobe and angle of the mandible (p.1647).","Parotitis is often preceded by a several-day prodrome of low-grade fever, malaise, myalgia, headache, and anorexia; it typically lasts ~5 days (range 3-7) with most cases resolving within 10 days (p.1647).","Infection is asymptomatic in ~20% of unvaccinated patients; it can also present as nonspecific respiratory symptoms (p.1647).","Fully vaccinated persons can still contract mumps, but are at lower risk for mumps and for mumps complications (p.1647).","Buccal swabs are the best specimens for virus detection; the parotid gland should be massaged for 30 s before collection, and specimens collected as close to parotitis onset as possible (maximal shedding within 5 days of symptom onset) (p.1649).","Complications can occur in the absence of parotitis and are more common in adults than children and in males than females (largely due to orchitis) (p.1648)."],"pathophysiology":"Caused by mumps virus, a single-stranded negative-sense enveloped RNA paramyxovirus (Rubulavirus genus, Paramyxoviridae); there is only one serotype (p.1646). Humans are the only known natural reservoir; transmission is via direct contact with respiratory droplets or saliva of an infected person. Incubation averages 16-18 days (range 12-25). A person is most infectious from 2 days before until 5 days after onset of parotitis (virus detected in saliva as early as 7 days before to as late as 9 days after onset) (p.1647). Primary replication likely occurs in the nasal/upper respiratory mucosal epithelium, with spread to regional lymph nodes and infection of mononuclear cells facilitating viremia lasting ~3-5 days. Viremia produces acute inflammatory reactions most commonly in the salivary glands (parotitis) and testes (orchitis), with dissemination also to kidneys, CNS, pancreas, heart, ovaries, mammary glands, cochlear perilymph, and (in pregnancy) the fetus. Mumps encephalitis in many cases appears to be a para- or postinfectious demyelinating process rather than direct viral cytotoxicity, though rare primary encephalitis with viral isolation from brain tissue does occur (p.1647-1648).","additionalDifferentials":["Influenza A H3N2 (can increase parotitis cases)","Parainfluenza virus types 1-3","Epstein-Barr virus","Human herpesviruses 6A and 6B","Herpes simplex viruses types 1 and 2","Coxsackievirus A","Adenovirus","Parvovirus B19","Echovirus","Lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus","HIV","Sarcoidosis","Sjogren's syndrome","Mikulicz's syndrome","Parinaud's oculoglandular syndrome","Uremia","Diabetes mellitus","Laundry starch ingestion / malnutrition / cirrhosis and some drug treatments","Salivary duct obstruction, cysts, or tumors (cause of unilateral parotitis)","Testicular torsion (mimics mumps orchitis)","Bacterial prostatic/urinary tract infection, chlamydia, gonorrhea, and viral orchitis (coxsackievirus, varicella, echovirus, CMV)"],"infectionMimics":["Suppurative (bacterial) parotitis","Parainfluenza virus types 1-3","Epstein-Barr virus","Human herpesviruses 6A/6B","Herpes simplex viruses 1 and 2","Coxsackievirus A","Adenovirus","Parvovirus B19","Echovirus","Lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus","HIV","Influenza A H3N2","Enteroviral aseptic meningitis (clinically indistinguishable from mumps meningitis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis","Sjogren's syndrome","Mikulicz's syndrome","Parinaud's oculoglandular syndrome","Uremia","Diabetes mellitus","Malnutrition / laundry starch ingestion / cirrhosis","Drug-induced parotid swelling","Salivary ductal obstruction, cysts, or tumors","Testicular torsion (mimicking orchitis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR (preferred virologic confirmation) on blood, saliva, urine, or CSF; buccal swab is the best specimen","Viral culture","Mumps-specific IgM serology (note: often undetectable, transient, or delayed in vaccinated patients, and can give false positives from cross-reactions)","Paired acute- and convalescent-phase IgG for a fourfold rise (IgG testing generally not recommended, as titers may already be elevated in vaccinated/previously infected patients)","Serum amylase and lipase (both elevated suggests pancreatitis in addition to parotitis)"],"redFlags":["Orchitis (most common complication; up to 30% of unvaccinated and 6% of vaccinated postpubertal males) with possible testicular atrophy and temporary subfertility","Aseptic meningitis (up to 10% of unvaccinated, <=1% of vaccinated patients)","Encephalitis (<=1% of patients; high fever, altered consciousness, seizures, focal neurologic signs; mortality ~1.5%)","Sensorineural hearing loss / deafness (usually sudden, unilateral, transient; bilateral and permanent loss are rare)","Oophoritis (~7% unvaccinated, <=1% vaccinated postpubertal women)","Pancreatitis (~4% unvaccinated, <1% vaccinated)","Supraglottic edema (rare, life-threatening) from obstructed lymphatic drainage","Other CNS complications: cerebellar ataxia, facial palsy, transverse myelitis, hydrocephalus, Guillain-Barre syndrome, flaccid paralysis","Myocarditis and endocardial fibroelastosis (rare but potentially severe)"],"prognosis":"Generally a benign, self-resolving illness; death due to mumps is exceedingly rare (p.1648). Aseptic meningitis is self-limited without significant risk of death or long-term sequelae. Encephalitis carries a mortality of ~1.5%, may leave permanent sequelae, and has poorer outcomes in adults than children. Orchitis can cause testicular atrophy in ~30-50% of affected testicles and temporary sterility/subfertility, but no studies have assessed the risk of permanent infertility. Mumps-related hearing loss is usually transient; bilateral permanent loss is rare. Mumps in pregnancy is generally benign and no more severe than in non-pregnant women, and evidence for an association with increased spontaneous abortion or fetal death is inconclusive (p.1648).","pitfalls":["A negative virologic or serologic result does NOT rule out mumps in a person with clinical signs, especially in vaccinated patients (p.1649).","Failure to detect mumps IgM is very common in vaccinated patients (IgM response often undetectable, transient, or delayed); IgM can also yield false positives from cross-reactions (p.1649).","Presence of parotitis, orchitis, or oophoritis supports a mumps diagnosis but their absence does NOT exclude it; complications can occur without parotitis (p.1648).","A positive IgG titer indicates only prior exposure (vaccination or natural infection) and does NOT predict protection; IgG titers should NOT be used to infer immunity in close contacts since they may instead reflect acute infection (p.1649).","Elevated serum amylase can arise from parotitis OR pancreatitis; lipase is needed to distinguish (p.1648).","Mumps reinfection and recurrent infection can occur and may be more common than previously thought (p.1648).","MMR vaccine has NOT been shown to prevent illness or alter severity in persons already infected and is NOT recommended as postexposure prophylaxis; mumps immune globulin is also NOT recommended for postexposure prophylaxis or treatment (p.1649)."],"management":["No specific antiviral therapy; management is supportive with analgesia, antipyretics, hydration and rest during the self-limiting illness","Local supportive measures for parotitis, including analgesia and avoidance of acidic foods that stimulate painful salivary secretion","Supportive care for orchitis with scrotal support and anti-inflammatory agents, and monitoring for pancreatitis, oophoritis and mastitis","Assess and manage complications such as aseptic meningitis and encephalitis with supportive care and hospital admission where indicated","Isolate the patient with droplet precautions for the infectious period to limit transmission","Notify public health authorities and arrange post-exposure MMR vaccination of susceptible contacts, since prevention rests on immunisation"],"severityClassification":"No formal severity classification in the source. Spectrum ranges from asymptomatic infection (~20% of unvaccinated) through classic self-limited parotitis to complicated disease (orchitis, oophoritis, pancreatitis, hearing loss, aseptic meningitis, encephalitis); subclinical CNS involvement (CSF pleocytosis) occurs in up to 55% of patients.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1646-1649"],"crossLinks":[]},"murine_typhus":{"name":"Murine (endemic) typhus","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["endemic typhus","flea-borne typhus","Rickettsia typhi infection","shop typhus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by Rickettsia typhi and transmitted mainly by the rat flea (Xenopsylla cheopis), with rats as the classic reservoir and cat fleas implicated in some urban and suburban settings","Infection follows contamination of the bite site or mucosae with infected flea faeces rather than the flea bite itself","Distributed worldwide in warm coastal and port regions, and often linked to rodent activity around homes and businesses","After an incubation of roughly 1-2 weeks there is fever, headache and myalgia, with a rash appearing in a variable minority of patients","When present the rash is often faint and macular, starting on the trunk, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis","The illness is generally milder than epidemic typhus but can cause pneumonitis, hepatitis, renal impairment and, uncommonly, severe multi-organ disease","Doxycycline is the treatment of choice and should be started empirically, as serology is usually negative early in the illness"],"pathophysiology":"Rickettsia typhi is an obligate intracellular bacterium maintained in a rat-flea-rat cycle. Humans are infected when flea faeces containing viable organisms are inoculated through the bite wound, abraded skin or mucous membranes. The bacteria invade and proliferate within vascular endothelial cells, producing a widespread small-vessel vasculitis with increased vascular permeability and perivascular inflammation. This endothelial injury underlies the fever, rash and, in more severe disease, involvement of the lungs, liver, kidneys and central nervous system.","additionalDifferentials":["Epidemic (louse-borne) typhus","Spotted fever group rickettsioses","Dengue and other arboviral fevers","Typhoid fever","Leptospirosis","Undifferentiated viral febrile illness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Indirect immunofluorescence antibody serology on paired acute and convalescent sera","PCR of blood where available","Full blood count, which may show thrombocytopenia","Liver enzymes, often mildly elevated","Renal function and serum sodium","Chest imaging if pneumonitis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Confusion or other neurological signs suggesting meningoencephalitis","Respiratory distress from pneumonitis or acute respiratory distress syndrome","Jaundice or marked hepatic dysfunction","Acute kidney injury","Failure to improve on empirical antibiotics, prompting review of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Murine typhus is usually a self-limiting febrile illness with low mortality and responds rapidly to doxycycline, although severe complications and occasional deaths occur, particularly with delayed treatment or in older or comorbid patients.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because there is no rash, which is frequently absent or subtle","Waiting for confirmatory serology before starting treatment","Failing to elicit rodent or flea exposure in the history","Attributing the illness to a nonspecific viral fever and missing a treatable rickettsiosis","Overlooking the possibility of severe organ involvement in apparently mild cases"],"management":["Prompt empirical antibiotic therapy with doxycycline, started on clinical suspicion without awaiting serological confirmation, as it produces rapid defervescence","Use a tetracycline-class agent as the mainstay, reserving alternatives for situations where tetracyclines are contraindicated","Supportive care with fluids, antipyretics and analgesia","Monitor for and manage complications such as pneumonitis, hepatitis and renal impairment, with organ support in severe multi-organ disease","Monitor full blood count, liver enzymes, renal function and sodium for thrombocytopenia, transaminitis, renal impairment and hyponatraemia","Environmental rodent and flea control to prevent reinfection and protect the household","Escalate to inpatient or critical care for severe or complicated disease, particularly in older or comorbid patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"muscarine_poisoning":{"name":"Muscarine poisoning","system":"Autonomic nervous system / Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Muscarinic mushroom poisoning","SLUDGE syndrome from mushrooms"],"chapter":null,"source":"Toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by muscarine-containing mushrooms, chiefly Inocybe and Clitocybe species, and produces a pure cholinergic (muscarinic) toxidrome.","Onset is rapid, typically within a short time of ingestion, distinguishing it from the dangerous delayed-onset amatoxin and gyromitrin poisonings.","Features follow the SLUDGE pattern: salivation, lacrimation, urination, defaecation, gastrointestinal cramping and emesis, together with sweating, bronchorrhoea, bronchospasm, miosis, blurred vision and bradycardia.","Muscarine directly stimulates peripheral muscarinic acetylcholine receptors and, unlike acetylcholine, is not degraded by acetylcholinesterase, so effects are sustained.","Because muscarine is a quaternary amine it does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so central nervous system effects are absent, in contrast to the delirium of Amanita muscaria (which paradoxically contains little muscarine).","Atropine is the specific antidote, titrated against secretions and bradycardia rather than pupil size.","Profuse secretions and bronchorrhoea, not bronchospasm alone, are the main threat to the airway and gas exchange in severe cases.","Illness is usually self-limiting over hours, and full recovery is the rule with prompt supportive care and antimuscarinic therapy where needed."],"pathophysiology":"Muscarine poisoning follows ingestion of mushrooms rich in muscarine, a quaternary ammonium alkaloid found in high concentration in many Inocybe and Clitocybe species. Muscarine is a direct agonist at muscarinic acetylcholine receptors on the effector organs of the parasympathetic nervous system and on sweat glands. Because it is structurally resistant to hydrolysis by acetylcholinesterase, its receptor stimulation is prolonged compared with endogenous acetylcholine. Activation of these receptors increases exocrine secretion (salivary, lacrimal, sweat and bronchial glands), stimulates smooth muscle of the gut and bronchi, contracts the detrusor and pupillary sphincter, and slows the heart through vagal effects on the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes. The resulting clinical picture is the classic muscarinic toxidrome. Crucially, the fixed positive charge of the quaternary amine prevents muscarine from crossing the blood-brain barrier, so central effects such as agitation and hallucination are absent; this pharmacology also explains why the antidote atropine, a tertiary amine that does cross into the central nervous system, effectively blocks the peripheral receptors.","additionalDifferentials":["Organophosphate or carbamate insecticide poisoning","Amanita muscaria/pantherina (ibotenic acid and muscimol) poisoning with central features","Nerve agent exposure","Cholinergic effects of medicinal cholinesterase inhibitors","Other early-onset gastrointestinal mushroom poisonings","Acute asthma or other cause of bronchospasm","Vasovagal episode causing bradycardia and sweating"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of the cholinergic toxidrome including heart rate, secretions and pupils","Electrocardiogram and continuous cardiac monitoring for bradyarrhythmias","Pulse oximetry and respiratory assessment for bronchorrhoea and bronchospasm","Serum electrolytes and renal function given fluid losses from secretions and diarrhoea","Red cell and plasma cholinesterase activity to help exclude organophosphate poisoning","Blood glucose and basic metabolic screen to exclude mimics","Mycological identification of the ingested mushrooms"],"redFlags":["Copious bronchial secretions or bronchorrhoea threatening the airway","Symptomatic or profound bradycardia","Respiratory distress or hypoxia","Marked volume depletion from combined secretory losses","Uncertainty distinguishing it from organophosphate poisoning"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; symptoms are usually self-limiting over several hours and recovery is complete, with deaths being very rare when supportive care and atropine are available. Severe cases are typically those with delayed treatment of profuse secretions and bradycardia.","pitfalls":["Assuming Amanita muscaria is the culprit for muscarinic symptoms, when Inocybe and Clitocybe are the usual high-muscarine sources","Titrating atropine to pupil size rather than to drying of secretions and heart rate","Missing coexisting organophosphate poisoning that produces an identical peripheral picture","Underestimating fluid loss from combined salivation, sweating, vomiting and diarrhoea","Expecting central nervous system features, whose absence is characteristic of pure muscarine poisoning"],"management":["Atropine is the specific antidote, titrated against secretions and bradycardia rather than pupil size and repeated as needed to control the cholinergic crisis","Airway protection and respiratory support for bronchorrhoea and bronchospasm, with suctioning and supplemental oxygen","Intravenous fluid resuscitation to replace losses from salivation, sweating, vomiting and diarrhoea","Continuous cardiac monitoring with treatment of symptomatic bradyarrhythmias","Supportive care with electrolyte correction, the illness otherwise being self-limiting over hours","Identify the mushroom species and exclude co-ingestion of delayed-onset amatoxin or gyromitrin poisoning, involving toxicology and poisons services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical toxicology textbooks","Mushroom poisoning and poisons-centre reference materials","Emergency medicine reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"muscle_eye_brain_disease":{"name":"Muscle-eye-brain disease","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MEB","muscle-eye-brain disease of Santavuori","dystroglycanopathy","POMGNT1","cobblestone malformation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital muscular dystrophy-dystroglycanopathy that, like Walker-Warburg syndrome, combines brain malformation, eye anomalies, and muscular dystrophy, but is generally less severe","It was classically described in the Finnish population and is often linked to variants in POMGNT1, though other glycosylation genes can cause an overlapping phenotype","Brain imaging shows a cobblestone-type cortical malformation, typically with frontoparietal predominance, cerebellar hypoplasia or cysts, brainstem hypoplasia, and often hydrocephalus and flat pons","Ocular features include severe myopia, glaucoma, retinal dysplasia, and progressive visual impairment","Affected children have congenital hypotonia and weakness with raised serum creatine kinase, and many achieve some developmental milestones unlike the more severe Walker-Warburg phenotype","It sits on a clinical continuum between Fukuyama congenital muscular dystrophy and Walker-Warburg syndrome within the dystroglycanopathies","Diagnosis combines the MRI pattern, ophthalmological findings, elevated creatine kinase, and molecular testing"],"pathophysiology":"Muscle-eye-brain disease is a dystroglycanopathy caused by biallelic variants in genes required for O-glycosylation of alpha-dystroglycan, classically POMGNT1. Deficient glycosylation impairs the binding of alpha-dystroglycan to extracellular matrix components, weakening basement membranes in brain, eye, and muscle. Disruption of the pial limiting membrane during corticogenesis permits neuronal over-migration and cobblestone cortical malformation, while the same molecular defect produces retinal and anterior segment anomalies and a congenital muscular dystrophy. The phenotype is intermediate in severity within the dystroglycanopathy spectrum.","additionalDifferentials":["Walker-Warburg syndrome at the severe end of the spectrum","Fukuyama congenital muscular dystrophy","Classic (type I) lissencephaly","Other congenital muscular dystrophies with eye involvement","Congenital glaucoma or high myopia of other cause","Congenital infection with brain and eye malformation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI for cobblestone cortex, cerebellar and brainstem anomalies, and hydrocephalus","Serum creatine kinase, characteristically elevated","Ophthalmological assessment for myopia, glaucoma, and retinal dysplasia","Molecular genetic testing including POMGNT1 and a dystroglycanopathy panel","Muscle biopsy with alpha-dystroglycan glycosylation studies if needed","Developmental assessment and electroencephalography where seizures occur"],"redFlags":["Progressive hydrocephalus requiring neurosurgical evaluation","Raised intraocular pressure from glaucoma threatening vision","Respiratory or feeding compromise from muscle weakness","Seizures requiring treatment"],"prognosis":"Muscle-eye-brain disease is generally less severe than Walker-Warburg syndrome, and many children survive into later childhood or adulthood with variable intellectual disability, motor impairment, epilepsy, and progressive visual loss. Prognosis depends on the severity of the brain malformation and systemic complications.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish it from Walker-Warburg syndrome, leading to inaccurate prognostic counselling","Overlooking treatable ocular complications such as glaucoma","Not recognising the muscular dystrophy component and its raised creatine kinase","Assuming a specific gene when the dystroglycanopathies show considerable phenotypic overlap"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists; management is supportive and multidisciplinary across the neuromuscular, brain and eye involvement","Neurological care with antiepileptic drugs for seizures and neurosurgical CSF diversion (ventriculoperitoneal shunt) for hydrocephalus","Ophthalmic management of severe myopia, glaucoma and retinal dysplasia, with refractive correction and glaucoma treatment to preserve vision","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and orthopaedic care for hypotonia, weakness and contractures from the congenital muscular dystrophy","Nutritional and respiratory support, with feeding and airway assessment as weakness progresses","Developmental support and monitoring, with electroencephalography where seizures occur","Autosomal recessive dystroglycanopathy (POMGNT1) genetic counselling and family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","GeneReviews: Congenital Muscular Dystrophy Dystroglycanopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"muscle_tension_dysphonia":{"name":"Muscle tension dysphonia","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Laryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MTD","functional dysphonia","hyperfunctional dysphonia","primary muscle tension dysphonia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disorder of excessive or maladaptive laryngeal muscle activity during voicing in the absence of a structural or neurological lesion","Fundamentally a diagnosis of exclusion, made only after organic pathology has been ruled out on laryngeal examination","Primary muscle tension dysphonia occurs with normal vocal folds, whereas the secondary form is a compensatory pattern layered on an underlying lesion or reflux","Presents with a strained, effortful, or rough voice, vocal fatigue, and often neck and laryngeal discomfort","Laryngoscopy may show supraglottic squeeze, such as anteroposterior or mediolateral compression, with a posterior glottic chink","Voice therapy is the mainstay of treatment, retraining phonation and reducing laryngeal hyperfunction","Coexisting reflux, stress, and vocally demanding occupations are common contributors that should be addressed"],"pathophysiology":"Muscle tension dysphonia reflects an imbalance in the coordination of the intrinsic and extrinsic laryngeal muscles during phonation, with excessive and inefficient recruitment producing a hyperfunctional voicing pattern. In the primary form no structural or neurological abnormality underlies the disorder, and psychological stress, vocal overuse, or learned maladaptive technique drive the excess muscular effort. In the secondary form the hyperfunction develops as a compensatory strategy to overcome an underlying problem such as a vocal fold lesion, reflux irritation, or glottic insufficiency. The resulting supraglottic constriction and abnormal glottic configuration degrade the efficiency of vibration, producing effortful, strained, and fatiguing voice.","additionalDifferentials":["Adductor spasmodic dysphonia","Vocal fold paralysis or paresis","Benign vocal fold lesions such as nodules or polyps","Laryngopharyngeal reflux-related dysphonia","Vocal tremor","Psychogenic (conversion) aphonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible fibreoptic laryngoscopy to exclude structural and neurological causes","Videostroboscopy to assess the mucosal wave and reveal subtle lesions or glottic insufficiency","Perceptual and acoustic voice assessment by a speech and language therapist","Assessment for laryngopharyngeal reflux as a contributing factor","Trial of voice therapy, with response supporting the functional diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Persistent dysphonia in a smoker or with other risk factors, mandating exclusion of malignancy before a functional label","Impaired vocal fold mobility suggesting a neurogenic cause rather than tension","Task-specific voice breaks characteristic of spasmodic dysphonia","Progressive symptoms with neurological signs beyond the larynx"],"prognosis":"The outlook is generally good, with many patients improving substantially through voice therapy, though relapse can occur if underlying vocal demands, stress, or reflux persist; secondary cases depend on treating the primary lesion.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing muscle tension dysphonia without first excluding organic disease by adequate laryngeal examination","Overlooking an underlying lesion driving a secondary compensatory pattern","Confusing it with spasmodic dysphonia and mismanaging accordingly","Neglecting contributory reflux, stress, or occupational vocal load"],"management":["Voice therapy delivered by a speech and language therapist is the mainstay, retraining phonation and reducing laryngeal hyperfunction","Vocal hygiene advice, hydration and reduction of vocal overuse and strain","In secondary muscle tension dysphonia, treat the underlying cause by managing any vocal fold lesion and treating laryngopharyngeal reflux with lifestyle measures and acid-suppressing proton pump inhibitors","Address contributing psychological stress with relaxation techniques and psychological support where appropriate","Ensure structural and neurological pathology has been excluded on laryngoscopy before confirming the functional diagnosis","Laryngology follow-up and reassessment, as relapse can occur if vocal demands, stress or reflux persist"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"muscular_dystrophies_and_other_muscle":{"name":"Muscular Dystrophies and Other Muscle Diseases","system":"Neuromuscular / Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Myopathies","Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD)","Becker muscular dystrophy (BMD)","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (LGMD)","Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy (EDMD)","Myotonic dystrophy (DM1/DM2)","Facioscapulohumeral dystrophy (FSHD)","Oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy (OPMD)","Mitochondrial myopathy","Periodic paralysis / channelopathy","Pompe disease (acid maltase deficiency)","McArdle disease"],"chapter":460,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3634","clinicalPearls":["Most myopathies cause persistent, symmetric, proximal (limb-girdle) weakness with preserved reflexes and intact sensation; associated sensory loss instead points toward a peripheral neuropathy or CNS (myelopathy) process rather than primary muscle disease.","The pattern of muscle involvement is the single most useful clue for narrowing the differential: facial weakness with scapular winging suggests FSHD, facial/distal weakness with grip myotonia is virtually diagnostic of myotonic dystrophy type 1, ptosis/ophthalmoparesis suggests oculopharyngeal dystrophy or mitochondrial myopathy, and asymmetric flexor-forearm plus quadriceps weakness suggests inclusion body myositis.","Creatine kinase (CK) is the most sensitive marker of muscle damage; the MM isoenzyme predominates in skeletal muscle while CK-MB marks cardiac muscle. CK can be elevated in healthy people after strenuous activity, trauma, prolonged cramp, or seizure.","Elevated AST, ALT, aldolase, and LDH can originate from muscle and be misattributed to liver disease; a normal gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) helps confirm a muscle (rather than hepatic) source because GGT is not present in muscle.","The Gower sign (using the arms to climb up the legs to rise from the floor) and a waddling or hyperlordotic toe-walking gait reflect proximal hip, thigh, and trunk weakness.","Calf enlargement in many limb-girdle dystrophies represents true muscle hypertrophy that stays strong late in the disease, so the term 'pseudohypertrophy' is best avoided; by contrast, early medial gastrocnemius atrophy characterizes Miyoshi myopathy (dysferlin/anoctamin-5).","Cardiac and ventilatory muscle involvement are the leading causes of mortality across the dystrophies, so cardiology follow-up, ECG monitoring, and surveillance for respiratory insufficiency are central to care.","Genetic testing is the gold standard for hereditary myopathies, but next-generation panels can miss large deletions/duplications and intronic mutations and frequently return variants of uncertain significance; muscle imaging (MRI/ultrasound) can support a specific diagnosis by showing characteristic involvement patterns.","Myotonic dystrophy type 1 is multisystem: hatchet facies, frontal balding, distal weakness, cataracts, cardiac conduction block, insulin resistance, gonadal atrophy, hypersomnia, and cognitive impairment; congenital DM1 occurs almost exclusively in infants of affected mothers.","Episodic weakness beginning after age 25 is almost never due to a periodic paralysis, with thyrotoxic periodic paralysis being the notable exception (favoring men of Asian descent despite higher thyrotoxicosis rates in women).","In McArdle disease, a slow warm-up or brief rest produces a 'second-wind' phenomenon as the muscle shifts to fatty acid utilization; the forearm exercise test shows a blunted venous lactate rise with a normal ammonia rise.","Ragged red fibers and cytochrome-oxidase (COX)-negative fibers on muscle biopsy, often with elevated serum lactate, FGF-21, and GDF-15, point toward a mitochondrial myopathy."],"pathophysiology":"Myopathies reflect structural or functional impairment intrinsic to muscle, distinguishing them from anterior horn cell, peripheral nerve, and neuromuscular junction disorders. In the dystrophies, the molecular defect lies in proteins linking the sarcolemma to the cytoskeleton and extracellular matrix: dystrophin (DMD/BMD) connects the actin cytoskeleton through the dystroglycan complex to the matrix, and mutations in sarcoglycans, dysferlin, calpain-3, anoctamin-5, glycosylation enzymes of alpha-dystroglycan, and sarcomeric/Z-disk and nuclear-envelope proteins underlie the limb-girdle, distal, and Emery-Dreifuss phenotypes. Nuclear-membrane protein defects (emerin, lamin A/C, nesprin-1/2, LUMA) destabilize the inner nuclear envelope and cause EDMD with prominent contractures and arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy. Myotonic dystrophies arise from non-coding repeat expansions (CTG in DMPK for DM1, CCTG in CNBP for DM2) acting through a toxic RNA gain of function: mutant RNA forms nuclear inclusions that sequester splicing regulators, causing widespread aberrant splicing and the multisystem phenotype, with anticipation as repeat length grows across generations. FSHD results from hypomethylation at 4q35 (contracted D4Z4 repeats in FSHD1; SMCHD1/DNMT3B/LRIF1 mutations in FSHD2) that permits toxic expression of the DUX4 transcription factor. Energy-metabolism disorders impair glycolysis (e.g., myophosphorylase/McArdle), glycogen handling (acid maltase/Pompe), or fatty-acid transport into mitochondria via carnitine palmitoyltransferase, producing either exertional cramping with rhabdomyolysis/myoglobinuria or progressive weakness. Mitochondrial myopathies stem from maternally inherited mtDNA point mutations or deletions, or nuclear-gene defects of mtDNA maintenance/replication, impairing oxidative phosphorylation. Channelopathies of the sarcolemma (calcium CACNA1S, sodium SCN4A, potassium Kir2.1, chloride CLCN1) disturb membrane excitability to produce periodic paralysis and/or myotonia. Endocrine and toxic myopathies act through hormonal or pharmacologic injury, often producing fiber atrophy rather than destruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Anterior horn cell disease (amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, spinal muscular atrophy)","Peripheral neuropathy / radiculopathy","Myelopathy and other CNS lesions causing weakness","Neuromuscular junction disorders (myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton, congenital myasthenia, botulism)","Inflammatory myopathies (polymyositis, dermatomyositis, immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, inclusion body myositis)","Fibromyalgia and polymyalgia rheumatica (pain without true weakness; normal CK/EMG/biopsy)","Stiff-person syndrome and Isaacs' (neuromyotonia) syndrome"],"infectionMimics":["Pyomyositis and other bacterial muscle infections causing focal muscle enlargement","Parasitic muscle infection (e.g., causing muscle enlargement)","SLONM occurring in the setting of HIV infection","Viral or bacterial infection-triggered episodes of rhabdomyolysis/myoglobinuria"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy mimicking limb-girdle dystrophy clinically and histopathologically (screen for anti-HMGCR and anti-SRP antibodies)","Sarcoid granulomatous infiltration or amyloid deposition producing muscle enlargement","Focal myositis or fasciitis","Hepatic disease wrongly inferred from muscle-derived transaminase/aldolase/LDH elevations","Thyrotoxic hyperreflexia with fasciculations mimicking amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Diabetic muscle infarction presenting as acute painful indurated thigh/calf swelling"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum CK (MM vs CK-MB isoenzymes); AST, ALT, aldolase, LDH with GGT to localize muscle vs liver origin","Needle EMG (short-duration small-amplitude polyphasic MUAPs firing early; myotonic discharges; irritability in active necrosis), nerve conduction studies, repetitive nerve stimulation, and single-fiber EMG","Skeletal muscle MRI/ultrasound to define involvement pattern and guide biopsy site","Targeted/next-generation genetic testing (gold standard for hereditary myopathies), with awareness of missed large deletions/intronic mutations and variants of uncertain significance","Muscle biopsy with histochemistry, immunohistochemistry (dystrophin, sarcoglycans, dysferlin, emerin, alpha-dystroglycan) and electron microscopy","Forearm exercise test (lactate and ammonia) for glycolytic defects; dried blood spot enzyme assay screening for Pompe disease","Serum lactate, FGF-21, and GDF-15 for mitochondrial disease; serum/urine potassium and thyroid studies for periodic paralyses; ECG for conduction disease across dystrophies","Endocrine workup: thyroid function tests, parathyroid hormone, vitamin D and calcium; autoantibody panels (including anti-HMGCR, anti-SRP, anti-GAD for stiff-person syndrome)"],"redFlags":["Cardiac conduction abnormalities or arrhythmias (atrial fibrillation, atrioventricular/complete heart block, atrial standstill) in EDMD and myotonic dystrophy carrying risk of sudden death","Dilated cardiomyopathy or heart failure in DMD/BMD, LGMD, and myofibrillar myopathies","Ventilatory/diaphragmatic muscle weakness causing respiratory insufficiency (may be the presenting feature in adult Pompe disease and LGMD)","Myoglobinuria with CK rising to over 100 times normal, signaling rhabdomyolysis","Life-threatening hypokalemia-related arrhythmias during hypokalemic periodic paralysis attacks; potentially serious long-QT and ventricular arrhythmias in Andersen-Tawil syndrome","Dysphagia with aspiration risk in oculopharyngeal dystrophy/OPDM and myotonic dystrophy","Complete heart block and cardiac arrest in Kearns-Sayre syndrome; stroke-like episodes in MELAS"],"prognosis":"The dystrophies generally follow a slowly progressive course, though severity varies between and within subtypes. Life expectancy is reduced in DMD and BMD, with most BMD patients surviving into the fourth or fifth decade; death is often attributable to associated cardiomyopathy. FSHD usually spares the heart, but about 20% of patients progress to pelvic-girdle involvement and possible wheelchair dependence. In Kearns-Sayre syndrome, death from heart block occurs in roughly 20% of patients, underscoring the value of cardiac surveillance and prophylactic pacing for conduction defects. MELAS in full expression can lead to dementia, a bedridden state, and a fatal outcome. Most endocrine myopathies respond to treatment of the underlying disorder. For many hereditary myopathies no specific disease-modifying therapy exists, and care centers on preserving function and managing cardiac and respiratory complications.","pitfalls":["Attributing isolated fatigue to primary muscle disease: fatigue without abnormal clinical or laboratory findings almost never indicates a myopathy, and asthenia must be distinguished from true pathologic fatigability.","Misreading muscle-derived transaminase/aldolase/LDH elevations as liver disease; checking GGT clarifies the source.","Diagnosing myositis in FSHD when biopsy shows an inflammatory infiltrate, leading to incorrect immunosuppressive treatment.","Overlooking immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy (a treatable autoimmune cause) in apparent LGMD without an identified pathogenic mutation; antibody screening is warranted.","Calling calf enlargement 'pseudohypertrophy' when it is true hypertrophy in many LGMDs.","Assuming hyperkalemia in hyperkalemic periodic paralysis; patients are often normokalemic during attacks, and the disorder is defined by precipitation with potassium loading.","Misattributing exertional cramps: cramps follow short intense bursts in glycolytic defects, whereas CPT2-deficiency pain appears only after prolonged exercise once muscle breakdown has begun.","Preferring muscle biopsy over imaging in suspected diabetic muscle infarction, where hemorrhage into the biopsy site can occur.","Relying solely on next-generation sequencing panels, which can miss large deletions/duplications and intronic mutations and may yield variants of uncertain significance."],"management":["Disease-specific therapy where available, including corticosteroids to prolong ambulation in Duchenne muscular dystrophy, mutation-targeted exon-skipping antisense oligonucleotides for eligible genotypes, and enzyme replacement for Pompe disease","Cardiac surveillance and treatment of the associated cardiomyopathy and arrhythmias with ACE inhibitors and beta-blockers, and pacing or defibrillator implantation in Emery-Dreifuss and myotonic dystrophy","Respiratory monitoring with spirometry and overnight oximetry, and non-invasive ventilation for nocturnal hypoventilation","Physiotherapy, stretching and orthoses to prevent contractures, with orthopaedic management of scoliosis","Multidisciplinary neuromuscular care coordinating physiotherapy, occupational therapy, dietetics and respiratory and cardiac specialists","Genetic counselling and family screening for the relevant inheritance pattern, including cardiac assessment of female DMD and BMD carriers","Precautions against malignant-hyperthermia-type anaesthetic reactions and rhabdomyolysis with certain agents, with vigilance for acute deterioration"],"severityClassification":"No single unifying grading system; severity is assessed disease-specifically. Functional milestones (Gower sign, gait pattern, ambulatory status, wheelchair dependence) and manual muscle testing (e.g., biopsy is best taken from a muscle around grade 4/5) gauge weakness. Mortality risk is stratified by cardiac (conduction disease, cardiomyopathy) and respiratory (ventilatory insufficiency) involvement. In rhabdomyolysis, CK over 100 times normal accompanies myoglobinuria, and for statin myotoxicity, marked CK elevation (more than 3 to 5 times baseline) with severe myalgia, weakness, or myoglobinuria is an indication to stop the drug.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3634","Harrison 22e p.3635","Harrison 22e p.3637","Harrison 22e p.3638","Harrison 22e p.3640","Harrison 22e p.3641","Harrison 22e p.3642","Harrison 22e p.3643","Harrison 22e p.3644","Harrison 22e p.3645","Harrison 22e p.3646","Harrison 22e p.3647","Harrison 22e p.3648"],"crossLinks":[]},"musculocontractural_ehlers_danlos_syndrome":{"name":"Musculocontractural Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","system":"Connective tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mcEDS","adducted thumb-clubfoot syndrome","CHST14-related EDS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Connective tissue disorder references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive, caused by biallelic variants in CHST14, encoding dermatan 4-O-sulfotransferase-1, or less often in DSE, disrupting dermatan sulfate synthesis.","Presents at birth with multiple congenital contractures, characteristically adducted thumbs and talipes equinovarus.","Distinctive craniofacial features include a large or late-closing fontanelle, hypertelorism, short downslanting palpebral fissures, blue sclerae, low-set ears and a small mouth.","Progressive connective tissue fragility produces hyperextensible, easily bruised and fragile skin with atrophic scarring.","Recurrent large subcutaneous haematomas can be severe or life-threatening, and visceral or vascular rupture is reported.","Ophthalmological complications include glaucoma, high refractive errors and retinal detachment.","Progressive kyphoscoliosis, a tall slender habitus and recurrent joint dislocations develop over time.","Lifelong multisystem surveillance is required, covering cardiac, ophthalmic, skeletal and vascular systems."],"pathophysiology":"CHST14 encodes dermatan 4-O-sulfotransferase-1, the enzyme that transfers sulfate to the dermatan sulfate glycosaminoglycan chains carried by proteoglycans such as decorin. Loss of enzyme function abolishes normal dermatan sulfate biosynthesis, so decorin bears aberrant chondroitin sulfate chains instead. Because decorin normally coats collagen fibrils and regulates their spacing and lateral assembly, this switch disorganises collagen fibril architecture throughout the extracellular matrix. The consequence is generalised connective tissue fragility affecting skin, joints, blood vessels, eyes and viscera, together with the congenital contractures and craniofacial features that define the subtype. Biallelic variants in DSE, encoding dermatan sulfate epimerase, impair the same pathway and cause a clinically similar phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Distal arthrogryposis syndromes","Kyphoscoliotic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Classical Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Spondylodysplastic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Loeys-Dietz syndrome","Cutis laxa syndromes","Congenital myopathies with contractures"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of CHST14 and DSE","Analysis of glycosaminoglycan and dermatan sulfate composition where available","Echocardiography and vascular imaging for structural and rupture risk","Comprehensive ophthalmological assessment including intraocular pressure","Spinal and skeletal imaging to monitor scoliosis and contractures","Cranial or soft-tissue imaging when a large haematoma is suspected"],"redFlags":["A large or rapidly expanding subcutaneous haematoma","Signs of visceral or arterial rupture","Raised intraocular pressure or acute visual loss","Rapidly progressive scoliosis with respiratory compromise","Haemodynamic instability after minor trauma"],"prognosis":"The course is variable, with multisystem connective tissue fragility causing significant lifelong morbidity. Life-threatening haematomas and vascular or visceral rupture can occur, and life expectancy may be reduced in severely affected individuals.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the presentation as isolated arthrogryposis and missing the wider connective tissue disorder.","Overlooking the autosomal recessive inheritance and the recurrence risk for siblings.","Underestimating the risk of large, potentially life-threatening subcutaneous haematomas.","Failing to screen for ocular complications such as glaucoma and retinal detachment."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists; management is supportive and directed at connective tissue fragility, contractures and bleeding risk","Physiotherapy, splinting and orthopaedic or orthotic management of congenital contractures, talipes and progressive scoliosis","Meticulous wound and skin care with careful surgical technique and tension-free closure given fragile, atrophic-scarring skin","Vigilance for large subcutaneous haematomas and vascular or visceral rupture, with prompt imaging and haemostatic or surgical management of significant bleeds","Cardiovascular surveillance with echocardiography and vascular imaging for structural anomalies and rupture risk","Regular ophthalmological review including intraocular pressure for glaucoma, high refractive errors and retinal detachment","Autosomal recessive genetic counselling for CHST14 and DSE variants, with family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International EDS classification consensus criteria","Standard medical genetics textbooks","Peer-reviewed connective tissue disease reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"musicians_dystonia":{"name":"Musician's dystonia","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["musician's focal dystonia","task-specific dystonia in musicians"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / movement disorder literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A task-specific focal dystonia causing loss of voluntary control of highly trained movements while playing an instrument","It typically affects the hand in instrumentalists or the embouchure (mouth and facial muscles) in wind and brass players","Symptoms appear during skilled playing but not during ordinary use of the same body part","It usually develops in dedicated professional or serious amateur musicians after years of intensive practice","There is no weakness or sensory loss, and pain is characteristically absent, distinguishing it from overuse injury","Management includes retraining, technique modification and, for some, botulinum toxin, though outcomes are variable"],"pathophysiology":"Musician's dystonia is a task-specific dystonia attributed to maladaptive cortical plasticity from prolonged, highly repetitive skilled practice, producing blurred or overlapping cortical representations of the involved digits or muscles and reduced inhibitory control. This abnormal sensorimotor organisation disrupts the finely tuned motor programmes required for the specific musical task, so involuntary co-contraction and posturing emerge selectively during playing. A predisposition, sometimes with a family history of dystonia, interacts with intensive practice to trigger the disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Writer's cramp and other task-specific dystonias","Overuse or repetitive strain injury","Peripheral nerve entrapment","Essential tremor affecting performance","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from task-specific loss of control during playing","Examination to exclude weakness, sensory loss and nerve entrapment","Observation or recording of the patient playing the instrument","Nerve conduction studies if entrapment is suspected","MRI of the brain only if a secondary cause is suggested"],"redFlags":["Weakness, wasting or sensory loss pointing to a peripheral nerve lesion","Spread of dystonia beyond the task","Pain as a dominant feature suggesting overuse injury","Additional neurological signs suggesting generalised dystonia"],"prognosis":"The disorder is often persistent and can be career-threatening for professional musicians, though technique retraining and selected treatments improve function in some; complete resolution is uncommon.","pitfalls":["Misattributing symptoms to overuse injury and delaying diagnosis","Overlooking peripheral nerve entrapment as a mimic","Failing to observe the patient actually playing","Underestimating the psychological and occupational impact"],"management":["Sensorimotor retraining and technique modification, altering fingering, instrument ergonomics and practice patterns, as the primary approach","Botulinum toxin injection into the overactive muscles in selected patients to reduce involuntary co-contraction","Trial of oral anticholinergic agents such as trihexyphenidyl in some cases, with variable benefit","Prolonged rest from the triggering task with graded reintroduction and sensory-motor retuning strategies","Reassure that there is no weakness, sensory loss or pain, and exclude nerve entrapment and overuse injury","Refer to a movement-disorder neurologist and, where available, a performing-arts medicine service, recognising outcomes are variable"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Movement Disorders textbook (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"musk_congenital_myasthenic_syndrome":{"name":"MuSK-related congenital myasthenic syndrome","system":"Neuromuscular junction","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MUSK-CMS","MuSK deficiency CMS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["MUSK encodes muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase, the postsynaptic organiser that, with agrin, LRP4 and downstream DOK7 and rapsyn, drives clustering of acetylcholine receptors at the endplate.","This is a postsynaptic congenital myasthenic syndrome, distinct from the autoimmune MuSK-antibody myasthenia gravis of adults, and is caused by biallelic MUSK variants.","Onset is usually neonatal or in early infancy with hypotonia, ptosis, variable ophthalmoparesis, bulbar and respiratory involvement, and fatigable limb weakness.","Respiratory crises and apnoeic episodes, sometimes precipitated by intercurrent infection, are an important feature and can be life-threatening in infancy.","Salbutamol/albuterol and ephedrine are generally the preferred long-term treatments, mirroring other endplate-development congenital myasthenic syndromes.","Response to anticholinesterases is unreliable and some patients worsen, so pyridostigmine should be introduced cautiously and under supervision.","Electrodiagnostic testing shows a decremental response on repetitive nerve stimulation and increased jitter on single-fibre studies, without the repetitive CMAP seen in acetylcholinesterase deficiency."],"pathophysiology":"MuSK-related congenital myasthenic syndrome arises from biallelic loss-of-function or hypomorphic variants in MUSK, which encodes muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase, the central hub of the postsynaptic acetylcholine receptor clustering pathway. Neural agrin secreted by the motor nerve terminal binds LRP4, which in turn activates MuSK; MuSK then signals through the adaptor protein DOK7 and downstream effectors including rapsyn to concentrate acetylcholine receptors precisely opposite the nerve terminal and to shape the postsynaptic membrane. Defective MuSK function impairs this agrin-LRP4-MuSK-DOK7 cascade, resulting in poorly formed, small and simplified endplates with reduced acetylcholine receptor density and diminished endplate potentials. The reduced number and abnormal geometry of receptor clusters lowers the safety margin of neuromuscular transmission, producing fatigable weakness. Because the deficit lies in endplate development and maintenance rather than in acetylcholine clearance, agents that enhance neuromuscular transmission or endplate stability, such as beta-adrenergic agonists, tend to help, whereas prolonging acetylcholine dwell time with anticholinesterases gives inconsistent and sometimes adverse results.","additionalDifferentials":["DOK7 congenital myasthenic syndrome, which shares the postsynaptic developmental mechanism and salbutamol responsiveness","Rapsyn-related and agrin-related congenital myasthenic syndromes within the same clustering pathway","Autoimmune MuSK-antibody myasthenia gravis (an acquired disorder, not the congenital form)","Spinal muscular atrophy and congenital myopathies presenting with neonatal hypotonia and respiratory weakness","Congenital muscular dystrophy and mitochondrial myopathy","Infantile botulism causing acute neuromuscular transmission failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repetitive nerve stimulation demonstrating a decremental response","Single-fibre electromyography showing increased jitter and blocking","MUSK sequencing within a congenital myasthenic syndrome gene panel with confirmation of biallelic variants","Acetylcholine receptor and MuSK autoantibody testing to exclude autoimmune myasthenia","Respiratory function assessment, including overnight oximetry and monitoring for apnoeic episodes","Creatine kinase and, where required, muscle imaging to help exclude myopathic mimics"],"redFlags":["Sudden apnoea or respiratory crisis, particularly during intercurrent illness in infancy","Progressive bulbar weakness with aspiration risk and feeding failure","Clinical deterioration following anticholinesterase exposure","Nocturnal hypoventilation or recurrent respiratory infections signalling ventilatory compromise"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is variable; recurrent respiratory crises in early life carry significant risk, but many children stabilise with beta-adrenergic agonist therapy and vigilant respiratory management, achieving useful long-term function. Careful anticipatory care around intercurrent infections is important.","pitfalls":["Confusing the recessive congenital MuSK disorder with adult autoimmune MuSK-antibody myasthenia gravis, which has an entirely different management approach.","Assuming anticholinesterases will help; they are unpredictable and can worsen this subtype.","Underestimating the risk of sudden apnoea in infants, leading to inadequate respiratory monitoring.","Attributing neonatal hypotonia to a myopathy and missing the treatable neuromuscular junction diagnosis."],"management":["Long-term treatment with beta-adrenergic agonists such as salbutamol and with ephedrine, the preferred agents for this endplate-development congenital myasthenic syndrome","Introduce the anticholinesterase pyridostigmine only cautiously and under supervision, as the response is unreliable and some patients worsen","Anticipatory respiratory management with monitoring for apnoeic episodes and crises and ready access to ventilatory support during intercurrent infection","Prompt treatment of intercurrent infections with a written emergency plan, given the risk of respiratory crises in infancy","Physiotherapy with feeding and developmental support for hypotonia, bulbar weakness and fatigable limb weakness","Neuromuscular specialist follow-up and autosomal recessive MUSK genetic counselling with family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neurology and neuromuscular textbooks","Peripheral nerve and neuromuscular junction disorder references","Congenital myasthenic syndrome clinical practice guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"mutyh_associated_polyposis":{"name":"MUTYH-associated polyposis","system":"Oncology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MUTYH","MYH-associated polyposis","MAP"],"chapter":null,"source":"DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer / NCCN genetic/familial guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic germline pathogenic variants in MUTYH, and unusually among the hereditary polyposis syndromes it is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern","MUTYH encodes a base-excision-repair glycosylase that corrects oxidative DNA damage, so its loss leads to characteristic somatic mutations, notably in the APC and KRAS genes","Typically presents with attenuated colorectal polyposis of tens to low hundreds of adenomas, though serrated and hyperplastic polyps also occur, with a high colorectal cancer risk","The phenotype can closely mimic attenuated familial adenomatous polyposis, but the recessive inheritance pattern helps distinguish the two","Extracolonic features include duodenal adenomas and an increased risk of duodenal and other cancers, so upper gastrointestinal surveillance is warranted","Management centres on colonoscopic surveillance with polypectomy, with risk-reducing colectomy reserved for uncontrollable polyp burden or cancer","Two common founder variants account for many cases in populations of European ancestry, but comprehensive gene analysis is needed to detect all pathogenic changes"],"pathophysiology":"MUTYH encodes a DNA glycosylase that is part of the base-excision-repair system responsible for correcting the mutagenic effects of oxidative DNA damage. When both alleles carry loss-of-function variants, the enzyme can no longer remove adenines misincorporated opposite the oxidised guanine lesion 8-oxoguanine, so characteristic G-to-T transversions accumulate. These mutations preferentially strike growth-regulatory genes such as APC and KRAS, driving adenoma formation through the same adenoma-carcinoma sequence seen in other colorectal polyposis syndromes.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial adenomatous polyposis and its attenuated form","Lynch syndrome","Serrated polyposis syndrome","Polymerase proofreading-associated polyposis (POLE/POLD1)","NTHL1-associated polyposis","Sporadic multiple adenomas"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Germline MUTYH sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis to confirm biallelic variants","Colonoscopy with polypectomy and histological characterisation of polyps","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with duodenal assessment","APC germline testing to exclude adenomatous polyposis when phenotype overlaps","Full blood count and iron studies for occult blood loss","Family testing that considers both parents given recessive inheritance"],"redFlags":["A discrete or advanced colorectal lesion suggesting malignancy","Advanced-histology duodenal adenoma on upper endoscopy","Iron-deficiency anaemia or overt gastrointestinal bleeding","Rapid change in polyp burden between surveillance examinations"],"prognosis":"With regular colonoscopic surveillance and polypectomy the colorectal cancer risk can be substantially reduced, and prognosis is favourable when disease is detected early. Because risk persists lifelong and extends to the duodenum, ongoing surveillance and, where required, surgical management remain important.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying the condition as dominant and failing to test both parents and siblings appropriately","Diagnosing attenuated familial adenomatous polyposis without excluding biallelic MUTYH variants","Detecting only a single founder variant and wrongly concluding the diagnosis is excluded","Neglecting upper gastrointestinal surveillance for duodenal disease"],"management":["Regular colonoscopic surveillance with polypectomy is the mainstay, clearing adenomas to reduce colorectal cancer risk","Risk-reducing colectomy or proctocolectomy reserved for an uncontrollable polyp burden or established cancer","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopic surveillance with duodenal assessment for duodenal adenomas and cancer risk","Histological characterisation and clearance of serrated and hyperplastic polyps as well as adenomas","Manage iron-deficiency anaemia from occult gastrointestinal blood loss and investigate accordingly","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance, testing both parents and offering cascade and carrier testing to relatives","Coordinate lifelong surveillance through a specialist hereditary-colorectal-cancer and genetics multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer: Principles and Practice of Oncology","NCCN Guidelines: Genetic/Familial High-Risk Assessment - Colorectal"],"crossLinks":[]},"myalgic_encephalomyelitis_chronic_fatigue":{"name":"Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/ Chronic Fatigue Syndrome","system":"Neurologic / Multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ME/CFS","Chronic Fatigue Syndrome","Myalgic Encephalomyelitis","CFS","ME"],"chapter":461,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3649","clinicalPearls":["ME/CFS is a chronic, complex, multisystem illness whose functional impact on daily functioning has been compared to that of multiple sclerosis, rheumatoid arthritis, and congestive heart failure.","The defining feature is persistent, unexplained fatigue causing substantial functional impairment, together with postexertional malaise (PEM) - worsening of symptoms after physical, mental, or emotional exertion that would not have been a problem before the illness.","PEM relapses last longer than a day and can persist for weeks; the relationship between a relapse and the preceding activity is often not apparent to the patient, so PEM may go unrecognized.","Common accompanying symptoms include pain, cognitive dysfunction (patient-described 'brain fog'), unrefreshing sleep, headache, sore throat, tender lymph nodes, muscle and joint aches, feverishness, and orthostatic intolerance.","There is no diagnostic test; diagnosis rests on a characteristic profile of patient-reported symptoms, requiring evaluation by an experienced clinician.","The 2015 Institute of Medicine criteria require substantial activity reduction with profound new-onset fatigue not relieved by rest, plus PEM, plus unrefreshing sleep, plus either cognitive impairment or orthostatic intolerance.","Routine laboratory tests are typically within normal limits in ME/CFS; their value lies in identifying alternative or contributing illnesses rather than confirming the diagnosis.","Roughly 10% of patients remain ill for 6 months or more after a variety of infections (e.g., Epstein-Barr virus, Ross River virus, Q fever, Ebola, SARS-CoV-1, Giardia); recovery is otherwise the rule.","Recognition of ME/CFS as a diagnosable component of Long COVID has increased clinical awareness, but patients still encounter stigma and misunderstanding from clinicians.","An estimated 80% or more of people who meet ME/CFS criteria have never been diagnosed by a health care provider.","Patients are reported to be more medication-sensitive than the general population, so any drug should begin at lower-than-usual doses and be increased slowly.","Patients may appear well during an office visit and then relapse afterward from the exertion of attending the consultation."],"pathophysiology":"ME/CFS is understood as a complex disorder involving alterations across multiple interrelated homeostatic systems rather than a single mechanism. A wide range of infectious agents (viral and nonviral) can trigger a postinfectious fatiguing illness resembling ME/CFS, and noninfectious stressors - toxins, physical trauma, adverse life events, and cumulative allostatic load ('wear and tear') - are also associated. Twin and family studies suggest contributions from shared environment and genetic factors. Evidence for immunologic dysfunction is inconsistent and not established in most patients; reported findings include modest antinuclear antibody elevations, reduced immunoglobulin subclasses, impaired mitogen-driven lymphocyte proliferation, decreased natural killer cell activity, altered cytokine production, and altered T-cell metabolism. A hypothesis that excess cytokines such as interleukin-1 or interferon-alpha drive the fatigue and flulike symptoms lacks compelling supporting data. Other inconsistent and nondiagnostic findings include nonspecific regional brain MRI changes, autonomic nervous system dysfunction, hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis abnormalities, altered metabolism, and gut microbiome dysbiosis. Unifying multisystem models integrate infection, immune, central and autonomic nervous system, metabolic, genetic, lifestyle, and stress factors.","additionalDifferentials":["Broad differential of fatigue (medical and psychiatric causes evaluated per chronic fatigue workup)","Thyroid dysfunction (e.g., hypothyroidism)","Anemia / iron deficiency or iron overload","Celiac disease","Depression and anxiety disorders","Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS) and other dysautonomia","Idiopathic chronic fatigue (unexplained chronic fatigue not meeting full ME/CFS criteria)"],"infectionMimics":["Postinfectious fatiguing illness following Epstein-Barr virus","Q fever (Coxiella burnetii) postinfectious fatigue","Ross River virus infection","Post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 (Long COVID) with prolonged fatiguing illness","Giardia-associated postinfectious fatigue"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Fibromyalgia","Hypothyroidism and other endocrine disorders","Depression and other psychiatric conditions presenting with fatigue","Anemia","Celiac disease","Sjogren's syndrome"],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","Electrolytes, fasting glucose, calcium, phosphate","Renal function tests (blood urea nitrogen, glomerular filtration rate)","Liver function panel (bilirubin, ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase, gamma-glutamyl transferase, total protein, albumin/globulin ratio)","Thyroid function tests (TSH, free thyroxine)","Iron studies to assess both deficiency and overload (serum iron, transferrin saturation, ferritin)","Celiac disease screening tests","Urinalysis","Lean/standing (orthostatic) test for POTS","Mental health assessment screening for depression and anxiety"],"redFlags":["Continued decline in function (should prompt evaluation for another illness rather than being attributed to ME/CFS)","New, worsening, or unusual symptoms warranting further workup for a new illness","Serious depression and increased suicide risk associated with social isolation and loss of hope","Severe disease leaving the patient essentially homebound"],"prognosis":"Severity ranges from mild or moderate (retaining varying degrees of pre-illness function) to severe (essentially homebound). Most patients experience some improvement and stabilize, but a return to the prior level of function is unusual. A continued functional decline is atypical and should trigger evaluation for other illnesses. Because of social isolation and loss of hope, serious depression and an increased risk of suicide are reported, warranting active screening and referral.","pitfalls":["Trivializing the illness or dismissing patients, contributing to stigma and underdiagnosis (most who meet criteria are never diagnosed).","Failing to recognize PEM because patients do not connect relapses to preceding exertion, and may appear well in clinic only to crash afterward.","Attributing a continued functional decline to ME/CFS rather than investigating an alternative diagnosis.","Standard-dose medication initiation in patients who are unusually drug-sensitive, increasing toxicity risk.","Encouraging overexertion on 'good days' that leads to the 'push and crash' cycle and negates functional gains.","Relying on laboratory tests for diagnosis - results are usually normal and serve only to exclude other conditions."],"management":["Energy management or pacing to keep activity within the individual's energy limits and avoid triggering post-exertional malaise, noting that graded exercise therapy is no longer recommended as it can worsen symptoms","Symptomatic treatment of pain with simple analgesics and neuropathic agents where indicated","Management of unrefreshing sleep with sleep-hygiene measures and cautious short-term use of sleep aids","Treat orthostatic intolerance with increased fluid and salt intake, compression garments and pharmacological measures where needed","Support for cognitive dysfunction with activity structuring and cognitive strategies, plus psychological support for coping with a chronic illness","Exclude and treat alternative causes of fatigue, such as thyroid disease and anaemia, before settling on the diagnosis","Multidisciplinary specialist input with re-evaluation for any atypical continued functional decline"],"severityClassification":"Severity is described clinically as mild/moderate (patients retain varying degrees of pre-illness function) versus severe (patients essentially homebound). Diagnostically, the 2015 IOM criteria require substantial activity reduction lasting more than 6 months with profound new-onset fatigue not relieved by rest, plus postexertional malaise, plus unrefreshing sleep, plus either cognitive impairment or orthostatic intolerance, with symptoms present at least half the time and of at least moderate intensity.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3649","Harrison 22e p.3650","Harrison 22e p.3651"],"crossLinks":[]},"myasthenia_gravis":{"name":"Myasthenia Gravis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MG","Lambert-Eaton syndrome"],"chapter":459,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3624-3633","clinicalPearls":["Cardinal features are fatigable, fluctuating skeletal muscle weakness that worsens with repeated use or late in the day and improves with rest or sleep (Harrison 22e p.3625-3626).","Ocular and eyelid muscles are commonly involved early; diplopia and ptosis are frequent initial symptoms, and facial weakness can produce a 'snarling' expression on attempted smiling (Harrison 22e p.3626).","Bulbar involvement causes nasal/dysarthric 'mushy' speech, dysphagia with nasal regurgitation or aspiration, and chewing weakness; neck extensor weakness can cause head drop (Harrison 22e p.3626).","If weakness stays confined to ocular muscles for 3 years, later generalization is unlikely (ocular MG), though rare patients generalize beyond 5 years (Harrison 22e p.3626).","Bulbar, neck, and ventilatory weakness can be especially prominent in MuSK antibody-positive MG (Harrison 22e p.3626).","Ice-pack test: applying ice over a ptotic eye for 2 min produces a lid rise of 2 mm or more (positive) when ptosis is due to an NMJ defect; it is a quick, very sensitive bedside test (Harrison 22e p.3626-3627).","Approximately 10-15% of MG patients have a thymoma, so chest CT or MRI to evaluate the thymus is performed at diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.3628)."],"pathophysiology":"MG is an autoimmune NMJ disorder in which the number of available postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors (AChRs) is reduced and the postsynaptic folds are flattened ('simplified'), decreasing the efficiency of neuromuscular transmission so that normally released ACh produces small end-plate potentials that may fail to trigger muscle action potentials. Anti-AChR antibodies (IgG, T-cell dependent) act by three mechanisms: accelerated AChR turnover via cross-linking and endocytosis, complement-mediated damage to the postsynaptic membrane, and blockade of the ACh-binding site. Anti-MuSK antibodies (IgG4, do not activate complement) impair MuSK-LRP4 interaction and AChR clustering; anti-LRP4 antibodies (IgG1) cause complement-mediated destruction. The normal decline in ACh release on repeated activity (presynaptic rundown) combined with reduced transmission efficiency produces myasthenic fatigue and the decremental response to repetitive nerve stimulation. The thymus is abnormal in ~75% of AChR antibody-positive patients (~65% hyperplastic, ~10% thymoma) (Harrison 22e p.3624-3626).","additionalDifferentials":["Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS)","Congenital myasthenic syndromes (nonautoimmune, >30 genes; double-seronegative MG mimic)","Drug-induced myasthenia (e.g., penicillamine)","Immune checkpoint inhibitor-related autoimmune MG (and ICI-related myositis/myocarditis mimic)","Botulism","Hyperthyroidism (Graves' disease)","Intracranial mass compressing extraocular nerves (e.g., sphenoid ridge meningioma)","Oculopharyngeal dystrophy","Progressive external ophthalmoplegia / mitochondrial myopathy (Kearns-Sayre syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":["Botulism (Clostridium botulinum toxin cleaves proteins needed for ACh release, causing myasthenia-like bulbar weakness without sensory signs; reflexes present early)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (presynaptic, P/Q-type calcium channel antibodies)","Congenital myasthenic syndromes","Drug-induced myasthenia","Immune checkpoint inhibitor-related myositis","Hyperthyroidism (Graves' disease)","Progressive external ophthalmoplegia / mitochondrial myopathy","Intracranial mass lesion causing diplopia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-AChR antibody radioimmunoassay (positive in ~85% of generalized MG, ~50% of ocular MG; a positive result is virtually diagnostic but a negative result does not exclude MG)","Anti-MuSK antibodies (present in up to ~40% of AChR antibody-negative generalized MG)","Anti-LRP4 antibodies (low specificity; only tested when phenotype/electrodiagnostics fit MG but AChR and MuSK antibodies are absent)","Repetitive nerve stimulation (decrement >10% at 2-3 Hz is highly probable for MG)","Single-fiber EMG (blocking and jitter with normal fiber density; sensitive and confirmatory but not specific)","Ice-pack test for ptosis","Chest CT or MRI to evaluate the thymus for thymoma","Thyroid function tests in all patients with suspected MG","CT or MRI of head/orbits to exclude intracranial lesions in ocular or cranial presentations","Pulmonary function tests / forced vital capacity to assess respiratory impairment"],"redFlags":["Myasthenic crisis: ventilatory weakness requiring intubation or noninvasive ventilation to avoid intubation, managed in intensive care (treat respiratory infection, attend to fluids); requires rescue therapy with IVIg or plasmapheresis (Harrison 22e p.3626, p.3628-3629).","Dysphagia with nasal regurgitation or aspiration of liquids/food (bulbar involvement) (Harrison 22e p.3626).","Enlargement of the thymus in a patient older than 40 years is highly suspicious for thymoma (Harrison 22e p.3628).","Infection or tapering of MG therapy can precipitate myasthenic exacerbation or crisis (Harrison 22e p.3626).","ICI-related MG with concurrent myocarditis carries a high fatality rate in fulminant disease (Harrison 22e p.3627)."],"prognosis":"Prognosis has improved strikingly with modern treatment, and nearly all myasthenic patients can be returned to full productive lives with proper therapy. The course is variable, with exacerbations and remissions, particularly in the first 1-3 years; in ~85% of patients weakness becomes generalized. A cure remains elusive (Harrison 22e p.3625-3628).","pitfalls":["A negative anti-AChR antibody test does not exclude MG, and the antibody level does not correlate well with disease severity (Harrison 22e p.3627).","Distinguish LEMS from MG: LEMS is presynaptic (P/Q-type calcium channel antibodies), preferentially affects proximal lower-limb muscles, typically spares prominent ocular/bulbar onset, and shows depressed/absent reflexes plus autonomic features (dry mouth, orthostasis, impotence); unlike MG, strength and CMAP amplitude increase (incremental response) after brief exercise or high-rate (20-50 Hz) stimulation. In older adults LEMS is associated with malignancy, most commonly small-cell lung cancer (Harrison 22e p.3628).","Anti-striated muscle (titin) antibodies are not pathogenic and do not confirm MG or a thymoma (Harrison 22e p.3627).","Aminoglycoside, quinolone, and macrolide antibiotics, IV magnesium, procainamide, and penicillamine can exacerbate myasthenic weakness; botulinum toxin injections should be avoided in MG patients (Harrison 22e p.3627).","Anti-MuSK MG generally responds less to anticholinesterase agents (and may worsen) and does not respond to complement inhibition because MuSK antibodies are IgG4 and do not activate complement (Harrison 22e p.3626, p.3628-3629).","Diagnosis must be confirmed definitively before treatment because other treatable conditions mimic MG and treatment carries surgical/drug risks (Harrison 22e p.3626)."],"management":["Symptomatic treatment with the acetylcholinesterase inhibitor pyridostigmine to improve neuromuscular transmission","Immunosuppression with corticosteroids and steroid-sparing agents such as azathioprine or mycophenolate for generalised disease, with targeted therapies (complement inhibitors such as eculizumab, or neonatal-Fc-receptor antagonists) for refractory disease","Thymectomy for thymoma and in appropriate AChR-antibody-positive generalised myasthenia to improve long-term outcomes","Rapid immunotherapy with plasma exchange or intravenous immunoglobulin for myasthenic crisis and severe exacerbations","Airway protection and ventilatory support in intensive care for myasthenic crisis with respiratory or bulbar failure","Avoid or use cautiously drugs that worsen transmission, including aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, magnesium and certain beta-blockers, and treat intercurrent infection promptly as a crisis trigger","Screen for and manage associated autoimmune disease, particularly thyroid, noting MuSK-antibody disease responds less well to anticholinesterases and often favours rituximab"],"severityClassification":"Weakness is classified as ocular versus generalized (the latter affecting facial, bulbar, axial, or limb muscles in ~85% of patients). Worsening requiring intubation or noninvasive ventilation defines myasthenic crisis; all other worsening is termed exacerbation. Standardized measures referenced include the Myasthenia Gravis Activities of Daily Living (MGADL) and Quantitative MG (QMG) scores (Harrison 22e p.3626, p.3631).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3624-3633"],"crossLinks":["myasthenic_crisis","gbs"]},"mycetoma":{"name":"Mycetoma","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Madura foot","maduromycosis","eumycetoma","actinomycetoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India NTEP","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic granulomatous subcutaneous infection characterised by the triad of painless swelling, multiple discharging sinuses and extrusion of grains, most often affecting the foot","It is either eumycetoma, caused by true fungi, or actinomycetoma, caused by aerobic filamentous bacteria such as Nocardia and Actinomadura, and this distinction dictates treatment","Grain colour offers a clue to aetiology: black grains suggest fungal causes, while red, yellow or white grains suggest actinomycetes","It is a neglected tropical disease of barefoot agricultural workers, endemic in the 'mycetoma belt' including parts of India, and follows traumatic inoculation","Disease spreads locally to involve subcutaneous tissue, fascia and bone, causing deformity and disability, but rarely disseminates systemically","Diagnosis combines grain microscopy and culture, histopathology, imaging for bone involvement and increasingly molecular identification of the causative organism","Actinomycetoma responds to prolonged combination antibacterial therapy, whereas eumycetoma requires prolonged antifungal therapy often combined with surgery"],"pathophysiology":"Following traumatic inoculation of soil-dwelling fungi or aerobic actinomycetes, the organisms aggregate into grains (microcolonies) within subcutaneous tissue. The host mounts a suppurative and granulomatous response, forming abscesses and sinus tracts that discharge grains to the surface. Chronic inflammation and progressive spread involve fascia, muscle and bone, producing swelling, fibrosis, deformity and osteolysis. The grain structure and, in eumycetoma, a surrounding cement substance protect organisms from host defences and antimicrobials, accounting for the chronicity and treatment difficulty.","additionalDifferentials":["Chromoblastomycosis","Chronic osteomyelitis (bacterial)","Tuberculous osteomyelitis or cold abscess","Botryomycosis (bacterial grain-forming infection)","Soft-tissue tumour of the foot"],"infectionMimics":["Chronic bacterial osteomyelitis with sinuses","Tuberculosis of bone and soft tissue","Botryomycosis","Actinomycosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination of discharged grains for colour, and microscopy after potassium hydroxide or staining","Culture of grains to distinguish fungal from actinomycetous causes","Histopathology of deep biopsy showing grains within granulomatous inflammation","Imaging (radiography, ultrasound or MRI) to assess bone and soft-tissue extent","Molecular identification of the causative species where available"],"redFlags":["Bone involvement with extensive osteolysis threatening limb function","Secondary bacterial infection or sepsis complicating chronic sinuses","Rapid progression or atypical features suggesting malignancy","Advanced disease requiring possible amputation"],"prognosis":"Actinomycetoma generally responds well to prolonged combination antibacterial therapy, whereas eumycetoma is often refractory, requires prolonged antifungals with surgery and has a higher relapse rate; late presentation with bone disease may necessitate amputation.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish eumycetoma from actinomycetoma, leading to inappropriate therapy","Underestimating treatment duration, causing relapse","Missing bone involvement by not imaging adequately","Delaying referral until advanced disease limits options to amputation"],"management":["Identify and characterise the grains (microscopy, culture, molecular typing) to separate eumycetoma from actinomycetoma, as aetiology dictates the entire treatment strategy","Treat actinomycetoma with prolonged combination antibacterial therapy, typically co-trimoxazole paired with an aminoglycoside such as amikacin or with rifampicin, given in cycles","Treat eumycetoma with prolonged oral azole antifungals such as itraconazole (or newer triazoles), combined with surgery to remove the fungal focus","Undertake surgical debridement or wide local excision of localised lesions, reserving amputation for advanced disease with extensive bone destruction and disability","Provide supportive wound care of discharging sinuses, analgesia, and treatment of any secondary bacterial infection","Monitor treatment response and bone involvement with serial ultrasound or MRI, and actively support adherence given the very long treatment duration","Refer for multidisciplinary care (infectious diseases, surgery, dermatology) and promote protective footwear to prevent traumatic inoculation in endemic communities"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/NTEP guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_abscessus_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium abscessus infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["M. abscessus infection","Mycobacterium abscessus complex infection","MABSC infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most pathogenic and most drug-resistant of the rapidly growing mycobacteria, forming visible colonies within about a week on subculture","A complex of subspecies (abscessus, massiliense, and bolletii) that is ubiquitous in water and soil and a recognised cause of health-care-associated outbreaks","Causes chronic pulmonary disease, particularly in people with bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis, or other structural lung disease","A leading cause of skin, soft-tissue, and post-procedural infection after cosmetic surgery, injections, tattooing, acupuncture, and catheter placement","Inducible macrolide resistance mediated by a functional erm(41) gene is common, so prolonged incubation is needed during susceptibility testing to detect it","Subspecies matters: massiliense usually has a non-functional erm(41) and responds better to macrolide-based therapy than abscessus with an active gene","Treatment is difficult, combining a macrolide (where active) with an initial intensive phase of intravenous agents, followed by a prolonged continuation phase","Surgical resection of localised pulmonary disease or debridement of infected tissue and removal of foreign material greatly improve cure rates"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium abscessus is an environmental rapidly growing mycobacterium found in natural and treated water and in soil. Pulmonary infection typically arises in hosts with impaired mucociliary clearance or structural lung damage, such as bronchiectasis and cystic fibrosis, where the organism establishes chronic airway infection and granulomatous inflammation. Skin and soft-tissue disease usually follows direct inoculation through trauma, surgery, injection, or contaminated water and instruments, producing nodules, abscesses, and draining sinuses. Its intrinsic resistance to many antimicrobials, a lipid-rich cell wall, biofilm formation, and an inducible macrolide-resistance gene (erm(41)) underlie the poor response to therapy; disseminated disease occurs mainly with significant immunosuppression.","additionalDifferentials":["Other rapidly growing mycobacteria (M. chelonae, M. fortuitum)","Pulmonary tuberculosis or Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease","Bacterial or fungal surgical-site infection","Bronchiectasis exacerbation from conventional pathogens","Nocardiosis","Cystic fibrosis pulmonary exacerbation from other organisms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Mycobacterial culture demonstrating rapid growth on solid media","Molecular identification to species and subspecies level (for example rpoB or hsp65 sequencing)","Extended drug-susceptibility testing including macrolide testing with prolonged incubation to detect inducible resistance","High-resolution CT of the chest for nodular-bronchiectatic or cavitary disease","Repeated respiratory cultures to satisfy diagnostic criteria and distinguish infection from colonisation","Biopsy and culture of skin or soft-tissue lesions with a history of recent procedures"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive pulmonary disease in cystic fibrosis, with implications for lung-transplantation candidacy","Post-surgical or injection-related clusters pointing to a contaminated source needing public-health investigation","Disseminated disease indicating significant immunosuppression","Catheter-related bloodstream infection requiring line removal","Failure to respond to macrolide therapy signalling inducible resistance"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are among the poorest of the nontuberculous mycobacteria. Pulmonary disease due to macrolide-resistant subspecies is frequently difficult to cure and may only be controlled rather than eradicated, whereas macrolide-susceptible massiliense and surgically resectable disease have better outcomes. Skin and soft-tissue infection generally responds to combined medical and surgical treatment.","pitfalls":["Relying on a macrolide without testing for inducible resistance, which leads to relapse","Not identifying the organism to subspecies level, obscuring the likely macrolide response","Attempting cure with antimicrobials alone when surgery or foreign-body removal is needed","Treating a single respiratory isolate without meeting diagnostic criteria","Overlooking a contaminated water or instrument source in clustered post-procedural cases"],"management":["Confirm species and subspecies and obtain extended drug-susceptibility testing, including prolonged incubation to detect inducible erm(41)-mediated macrolide resistance, before committing to a regimen","Treat with prolonged multidrug therapy comprising an intravenous initiation phase (amikacin with tigecycline, imipenem, or cefoxitin) followed by a continuation phase, adding a macrolide such as azithromycin only where erm(41) is non-functional","Recognise that eradication is often unattainable, so aim for disease control alongside airway-clearance techniques and treatment of underlying bronchiectasis or cystic fibrosis","Pursue source control with surgical resection of localised pulmonary disease and debridement of skin or soft-tissue lesions, removing any infected catheters or foreign material","Monitor for drug toxicity, including aminoglycoside-related ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity with audiometry and renal function, and track response with repeated cultures","Optimise nutrition and comorbid lung disease, including CFTR modulator therapy in eligible cystic fibrosis patients","Manage through a specialist multidisciplinary team spanning infectious diseases, respiratory or cystic fibrosis services, and surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_avium_lymphadenitis":{"name":"Non-tuberculous mycobacterial cervical lymphadenitis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NTM lymphadenitis","atypical mycobacterial lymphadenitis","nontuberculous mycobacterial cervical lymphadenitis","MAC lymphadenitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common form of nontuberculous mycobacterial disease in otherwise healthy young children, usually caused by Mycobacterium avium complex and less often M. scrofulaceum, M. haemophilum, or M. malmoense","Typically affects children aged one to five years, who are thought to acquire the organism from soil and water via the mouth","Presents as a unilateral, firm, painless, slowly enlarging node in the submandibular, cervical, or preauricular region with little systemic upset","The overlying skin often develops a characteristic violaceous discolouration and may progress to fluctuance and a chronically discharging sinus","The tuberculin skin test is usually negative or only weakly positive and interferon-gamma release assays are typically negative, helping to distinguish it from tuberculosis","Complete surgical excision of the affected nodes is the treatment of choice and offers the highest cure rate","Incision and drainage should be avoided because it commonly leads to a chronic discharging sinus","Macrolide-based antimicrobial therapy or watchful observation are alternatives when excision is incomplete, high risk to the facial nerve, or declined, as many nodes resolve slowly on their own"],"pathophysiology":"Nontuberculous mycobacterial cervical lymphadenitis develops when environmental mycobacteria, most often Mycobacterium avium complex, enter through the oral mucosa of a young child and spread to the draining submandibular and cervical lymph nodes. A granulomatous immune response with caseation forms within the node; because the child is immunocompetent, disease remains localised with minimal systemic features. Progressive necrosis can liquefy and extend to the skin, producing the typical violaceous discolouration, fluctuance, and eventual sinus formation. Multifocal, bilateral, recurrent, or unusually aggressive disease should prompt consideration of an underlying immune defect.","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculous lymphadenitis","Reactive or pyogenic bacterial lymphadenitis","Cat-scratch disease","Lymphoma","Branchial cleft cyst or other congenital neck mass","Other granulomatous disease such as sarcoidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional lymph-node biopsy, which is both diagnostic and therapeutic","Mycobacterial culture and molecular testing of tissue with speciation","Tuberculin skin test and interferon-gamma release assay to help exclude tuberculosis","Ultrasound of the neck to define nodal extent and proximity to the facial nerve","Histology showing granulomatous inflammation with or without caseation","Assessment of immune status in atypical, multifocal, or recurrent disease"],"redFlags":["Nodes near the facial nerve (preauricular or periparotid) where surgery risks nerve injury","Features suggesting tuberculosis, prompting exclusion and contact tracing","Multifocal, bilateral, or recurrent disease suggesting an underlying immunodeficiency","Rapidly enlarging or systemically symptomatic swelling suggesting malignancy","A chronically discharging sinus complicating incision and drainage"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent, particularly after complete surgical excision, which is usually curative. Untreated or incompletely treated disease tends to resolve over months but may leave scarring or a discharging sinus; recurrence is uncommon after complete excision.","pitfalls":["Performing incision and drainage, which promotes a chronic discharging sinus","Failing to exclude tuberculosis before attributing the adenitis to nontuberculous mycobacteria","Not sending tissue for mycobacterial culture and molecular testing","Risking facial-nerve injury during excision of periparotid or preauricular nodes","Overlooking an underlying immune defect in multifocal or recurrent disease"],"management":["Undertake complete surgical excision of the affected nodes as the definitive treatment of choice, offering the highest cure rate","Avoid simple incision and drainage, which risks a chronically discharging sinus or fistula","Reserve macrolide-based antimycobacterial therapy (a macrolide such as clarithromycin combined with rifampicin or ethambutol) for incompletely excised, recurrent, or surgically hazardous disease near the facial nerve","Consider watchful observation in selected cases, since many nodes resolve slowly over months, weighing this against cosmetic scarring","Define nodal extent and proximity to the facial nerve with neck ultrasound to plan safe surgery","Assess immune status where disease is atypical, multifocal, bilateral, or recurrent","Refer for multidisciplinary care involving paediatric surgery or ENT and infectious diseases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_chelonae_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium chelonae infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["M. chelonae infection","Mycobacterium chelonae soft-tissue infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rapidly growing mycobacterium widely distributed in water, causing predominantly skin, soft-tissue, and post-procedural infection","Disseminated cutaneous disease with multiple nodules on the limbs is characteristic in immunosuppressed patients, especially those on corticosteroids or after solid-organ transplantation","Associated with outbreaks linked to tattoo ink, mesotherapy, injections, and contaminated water, and with catheter-related infection","A recognised cause of keratitis following laser refractive and other eye surgery","Unlike some rapid growers it usually lacks a functional inducible macrolide-resistance gene and is often macrolide-susceptible, with tobramycin frequently more active than amikacin","Pulmonary disease is much less common than with M. abscessus","Management combines susceptibility-guided macrolide-based multidrug therapy with surgical debridement and removal of any infected foreign material"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium chelonae is an environmental rapidly growing mycobacterium found in water supplies. Disease usually follows percutaneous inoculation through trauma, surgery, injection, or contaminated solutions and devices, producing localised nodules and abscesses. In immunocompetent hosts infection tends to remain localised, whereas impaired cell-mediated immunity, particularly corticosteroid therapy and transplantation, predisposes to disseminated cutaneous disease with crops of nodules and to deeper infection. The organism forms biofilms on catheters and prosthetic material, and its relatively preserved susceptibility to macrolides distinguishes it from the more resistant M. abscessus.","additionalDifferentials":["Other rapidly growing mycobacteria (M. abscessus, M. fortuitum)","Bacterial furunculosis or abscess","Disseminated fungal infection with cutaneous nodules","Nocardiosis","Cutaneous vasculitis or panniculitis","Sporotrichosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Culture of skin or tissue showing rapid mycobacterial growth","Molecular speciation to distinguish it from other rapid growers","Drug-susceptibility testing including macrolides, tobramycin, and amikacin","Skin biopsy for histology and staining","Assessment of immune status and medication review in disseminated disease","Corneal scrape and culture in suspected post-surgical keratitis"],"redFlags":["Disseminated cutaneous nodules indicating impaired cell-mediated immunity","Catheter-related or prosthesis-related infection requiring device removal","Post-operative keratitis threatening vision","Progressive infection despite apparently appropriate therapy"],"prognosis":"Localised infection in immunocompetent hosts generally responds well to combined medical and surgical treatment. Disseminated disease in immunosuppressed patients requires prolonged therapy and reduction of immunosuppression where possible, and outcomes depend on the underlying condition; relapse can occur if infected foreign material is not removed.","pitfalls":["Assuming the drug-resistance pattern of M. abscessus and withholding a potentially effective macrolide","Continuing immunosuppression unchanged in disseminated disease","Treating device-associated infection without removing the device","Missing a common contaminated injection or cosmetic-procedure source in clustered cases"],"management":["Guide therapy by susceptibility testing, exploiting its usually preserved macrolide susceptibility with a macrolide such as clarithromycin or azithromycin, and noting that tobramycin is often more active than amikacin","Use combination antimicrobial therapy rather than monotherapy, particularly for disseminated cutaneous disease, to prevent emergent resistance","Achieve source control through surgical debridement or drainage of abscesses and removal of infected intravascular catheters or prosthetic material","Reduce immunosuppression where feasible, tapering corticosteroids or minimising transplant immunosuppression to aid clearance","Treat post-surgical keratitis with topical antimycobacterial therapy under ophthalmology guidance","Monitor response with repeat cultures and watch for drug toxicity during prolonged treatment","Refer to infectious diseases, with outcome in disseminated disease depending on control of the underlying immunocompromising condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_fortuitum_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium fortuitum infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["M. fortuitum infection","Mycobacterium fortuitum complex infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rapidly growing mycobacterium found in soil and water that most often causes localised skin, soft-tissue, and wound infection","Classically causes post-surgical wound infection, including sternal wounds after cardiac surgery and infection after breast augmentation, and catheter-related infection","A well-recognised cause of furunculosis of the lower legs and feet after nail-salon footbaths (pedicure-associated furunculosis)","Pulmonary disease is uncommon and, when it occurs, is often linked to oesophageal disorders and chronic aspiration","Although a macrolide may appear active in vitro, a functional inducible macrolide-resistance gene can emerge, so this should be tested for","Often susceptible to a broader range of oral agents than M. abscessus, including fluoroquinolones, tetracyclines, and sulfonamides, giving more treatment options","Management is with susceptibility-guided combination therapy together with surgical debridement and removal of infected foreign material"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium fortuitum is a ubiquitous rapidly growing mycobacterium of soil and water. Most infections follow inoculation through trauma, surgery, injection, catheters, or contaminated water, producing localised abscesses, wound infection, or furuncles; outbreaks have been traced to footbaths in nail salons. Pulmonary infection is unusual and is associated with conditions predisposing to aspiration, such as achalasia and gastro-oesophageal reflux. Although generally less virulent and more antibiotic-susceptible than M. abscessus, the organism can carry an inducible macrolide-resistance mechanism, and retained foreign material or undrained collections allow persistent infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Other rapidly growing mycobacteria (M. abscessus, M. chelonae)","Ordinary bacterial wound infection or abscess","Nocardiosis","Cutaneous fungal infection","Foreign-body or suture granuloma","Aspiration pneumonia from conventional pathogens"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Culture of wound, tissue, or sputum showing rapid mycobacterial growth","Molecular identification to species level","Drug-susceptibility testing including detection of inducible macrolide resistance","Imaging of the surgical site or affected area to define collections","Investigation for an oesophageal or aspiration disorder in pulmonary disease","Review for a common environmental or procedural source in clustered cases"],"redFlags":["Sternal wound or mediastinal infection after cardiac surgery","Prosthesis- or catheter-related infection requiring device removal","Clustered post-procedural cases suggesting a contaminated source","Pulmonary disease with an underlying oesophageal or aspiration disorder"],"prognosis":"Localised skin and wound infection generally has a good prognosis with combined antimicrobial and surgical treatment, aided by the organism's relatively broad susceptibility. Deep surgical-site, prosthesis-related, and pulmonary infections are more difficult and depend on adequate source control and management of predisposing conditions.","pitfalls":["Relying on apparent in vitro macrolide activity without testing for inducible resistance","Failing to achieve source control by removing infected material or draining collections","Overlooking an oesophageal or aspiration disorder in pulmonary cases","Missing a shared footbath or injection source in clustered furunculosis"],"management":["Exploit its relatively broad susceptibility with a susceptibility-guided oral combination drawn from fluoroquinolones, tetracyclines such as doxycycline or minocycline, and sulfonamides such as co-trimoxazole","Test specifically for inducible macrolide resistance before relying on a macrolide, since a functional resistance gene can emerge despite apparent in vitro activity","Achieve source control by debriding or draining infected surgical sites and removing infected catheters or prosthetic material such as breast implants","Investigate and manage an underlying oesophageal or aspiration disorder where pulmonary disease is present","Monitor treatment response and drug toxicity across the prolonged course, imaging affected sites to define collections","Investigate a common environmental or procedural source, such as nail-salon footbaths, in clustered cases","Refer to infectious diseases, recognising that deep, prosthesis-related, and pulmonary disease depend heavily on adequate source control"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_genavense_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium genavense infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["M. genavense infection","disseminated Mycobacterium genavense infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An extremely fastidious slow-growing mycobacterium that grows poorly or not at all on solid media and often requires liquid culture, mycobactin J supplementation, and prolonged incubation","Frequently identified only by molecular methods because conventional culture is so slow and insensitive","Chiefly causes disseminated disease in severely immunosuppressed patients, especially those with advanced HIV and very low CD4 counts","Presents with fever, weight loss, hepatosplenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, anaemia, and prominent gastrointestinal involvement","Clinically and pathologically mimics disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex infection","Also recognised in pet birds and in other immunosuppressed groups such as transplant recipients","Management uses macrolide-based multidrug therapy as for disseminated MAC, together with restoration of immune function such as antiretroviral therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium genavense is a highly fastidious slow-growing mycobacterium of environmental and avian origin. It causes disease almost exclusively in hosts with profound impairment of cell-mediated immunity, in whom the portal of entry is thought to be the gastrointestinal tract with subsequent widespread dissemination to liver, spleen, bone marrow, and lymph nodes. Tissues carry a heavy organism burden with a poorly formed granulomatous response, and the illness closely resembles disseminated M. avium complex disease. Because the organism requires mycobactin J and prolonged incubation, and grows poorly on solid media, diagnosis often depends on nucleic-acid sequencing of blood or tissue.","additionalDifferentials":["Disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex infection","Disseminated tuberculosis","Other disseminated NTM in advanced HIV","Visceral leishmaniasis","Lymphoma","Disseminated histoplasmosis or other endemic mycosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and tissue mycobacterial culture using liquid media with mycobactin J and prolonged incubation","Molecular identification by 16S ribosomal RNA gene sequencing, often required when culture is negative","Biopsy of liver, marrow, or lymph node showing acid-fast bacilli with a poor granulomatous response","CD4 count and HIV testing","Imaging for organomegaly and lymphadenopathy","Screening for gastrointestinal involvement"],"redFlags":["Profound immunosuppression such as advanced HIV with a very low CD4 count","Progressive wasting and organ dysfunction from high organism burden","Failure to isolate any organism despite a compatible disseminated illness, prompting molecular testing","Concurrent opportunistic infections"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is largely determined by the underlying immunodeficiency. With macrolide-based therapy and effective restoration of immunity, particularly antiretroviral therapy in HIV, outcomes have improved, but untreated or profoundly immunosuppressed patients fare poorly and diagnosis is often delayed by the organism's fastidious growth.","pitfalls":["Assuming a negative conventional culture excludes mycobacterial disease when molecular testing is required","Not supplementing media with mycobactin J or allowing sufficiently prolonged incubation","Failing to prioritise immune reconstitution alongside antimicrobials","Mislabelling the illness as disseminated MAC without species confirmation"],"management":["Treat with prolonged macrolide-based multidrug antimycobacterial therapy, typically a macrolide such as clarithromycin combined with ethambutol and a rifamycin such as rifabutin, sometimes with a fluoroquinolone","Restore cell-mediated immunity as the decisive intervention, starting antiretroviral therapy in advanced HIV and reducing immunosuppression in transplant recipients where possible","Provide supportive care for the systemic illness, including nutritional support, transfusion for anaemia, and management of gastrointestinal involvement","Anticipate and manage immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome when antiretroviral therapy is introduced","Account for interactions between rifamycins and antiretrovirals, adjusting agents and doses under specialist guidance","Continue therapy for a prolonged period given the fastidious, slow-growing organism and monitor response clinically and radiologically","Manage jointly with infectious diseases and HIV specialists, as prognosis is largely determined by the underlying immunodeficiency"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_gordonae_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium gordonae infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["M. gordonae infection","tap water bacillus infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A slow-growing scotochromogen widely known as the tap water bacillus and the least pathogenic of the commonly encountered nontuberculous mycobacteria","One of the most frequent environmental and laboratory contaminants, so a positive culture is far more often contamination than infection","Recovered readily from tap water, laboratory water, and reagents, and a recognised cause of pseudo-outbreaks traced to contaminated water or solutions","Genuine disease is very rare and largely confined to severely immunocompromised patients or infection associated with prosthetic material and foreign bodies","Reported true infections include disseminated disease, pulmonary disease, and device-related infection","Diagnosis of real disease demands repeated isolation from a normally sterile or clinically compatible site and rigorous exclusion of contamination","Most single isolates require no treatment; when genuine infection is confirmed, macrolide-based multidrug therapy and removal of infected foreign material are used"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium gordonae is a slow-growing scotochromogenic mycobacterium ubiquitous in water, including treated tap water, and a common contaminant of specimens, laboratory water, and reagents. Its very low virulence means that in immunocompetent people isolation almost always reflects colonisation or contamination rather than infection. Genuine disease is confined largely to hosts with severe impairment of cell-mediated immunity or to infection of prosthetic material and indwelling devices, where biofilm formation allows persistence. Because pseudo-outbreaks from contaminated water and solutions are well described, apparent clusters warrant investigation of the water supply and laboratory processes.","additionalDifferentials":["Specimen or laboratory contamination (by far the most common explanation)","Other nontuberculous mycobacterial infection","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Prosthesis- or catheter-related bacterial infection","Disseminated infection from another opportunistic organism","Pseudo-outbreak from a contaminated water source"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repeated cultures from a compatible or normally sterile site to establish genuine infection","Molecular identification to species level","Investigation of water supplies, reagents, and laboratory processes when a pseudo-outbreak is suspected","Assessment of immune status when true disease is considered","Imaging or device sampling in suspected prosthesis-related infection","Histological confirmation of organisms in tissue to support true infection"],"redFlags":["Genuine disease implying significant immunosuppression","Prosthesis- or device-related infection requiring removal","Clustered isolates indicating a contaminated water source or pseudo-outbreak","Repeated isolation from a normally sterile site"],"prognosis":"The clinical significance of most isolates is negligible and no treatment is needed. The rare instances of true infection occur in vulnerable hosts or around foreign material and depend on immune status and adequate source control; with these addressed, outcomes are generally acceptable.","pitfalls":["Treating a single positive culture as infection when it almost always represents contamination","Missing a laboratory or water-source pseudo-outbreak","Failing to remove infected prosthetic material when genuine infection is confirmed","Subjecting patients to unnecessary prolonged toxic therapy for a contaminant"],"management":["Recognise that most isolates represent contamination or colonisation and require no antimicrobial treatment","Establish genuine infection before treating by demanding repeated isolation from a normally sterile or clinically compatible site with histological confirmation of organisms in tissue","For confirmed true disease, use susceptibility-guided multidrug antimycobacterial therapy such as rifampicin, ethambutol, and a macrolide, sometimes with a fluoroquinolone","Achieve source control by removing infected prosthetic material or indwelling devices, where biofilm sustains infection","Investigate the water supply, reagents, and laboratory processes when a pseudo-outbreak is suspected","Assess and optimise immune status, since real disease occurs almost exclusively in the severely immunocompromised","Refer to infectious diseases, with outcomes generally acceptable once immune status and source control are addressed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_haemophilum_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium haemophilum infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["M. haemophilum infection","Mycobacterium haemophilum skin infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A slow-growing mycobacterium with fastidious requirements: it needs iron-supplemented (haemin-containing) media and a lower incubation temperature of around 30 to 32 degrees Celsius","The laboratory must be alerted to the possibility, because routine media and standard temperatures fail to recover it","Causes skin lesions, characteristically papules, nodules, and ulcers over the cooler extremities and overlying joints, in immunosuppressed patients","Septic arthritis and osteomyelitis may accompany the skin disease, and disseminated infection occurs with severe immunosuppression","Recognised in transplant recipients, patients on tumour-necrosis-factor inhibitors, and people with advanced HIV","In otherwise healthy children it is a cause of cervical lymphadenitis","Management combines multidrug antimicrobial therapy with reduction of immunosuppression where possible and surgical excision for lymphadenitis"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium haemophilum is a slow-growing mycobacterium with a distinctive requirement for iron (haemin) and for lower incubation temperatures, reflecting its tropism for the cooler superficial tissues. The presumed environmental reservoir is water. Disease overwhelmingly affects hosts with impaired cell-mediated immunity, in whom the organism produces granulomatous skin nodules and ulcers over the limbs and joints, sometimes with underlying septic arthritis or osteomyelitis and, in the most immunosuppressed, dissemination. In immunocompetent children the same organism can localise to cervical lymph nodes. Its fastidious growth requirements mean infection is frequently missed unless specifically sought.","additionalDifferentials":["Other nontuberculous mycobacterial skin infection (M. marinum, M. chelonae)","Disseminated fungal infection with skin nodules","Bacterial septic arthritis or osteomyelitis","Cutaneous tuberculosis","Nocardiosis","Cutaneous lymphoma or vasculitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin or tissue biopsy for histology and mycobacterial culture","Culture on iron-supplemented media at a lower incubation temperature after alerting the laboratory","Molecular identification to species level, sometimes required when culture fails","MRI for suspected septic arthritis or osteomyelitis","Assessment and optimisation of immune status and immunosuppressive medication","Blood cultures and staging for dissemination in severely immunosuppressed patients"],"redFlags":["Disseminated disease in a severely immunosuppressed host","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis requiring surgical management","Rapid progression in transplant or anti-TNF-treated patients","Failure to grow the organism despite strong clinical suspicion, indicating the need for specialised media"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on the degree of immunosuppression and how quickly it can be reversed. Localised lymphadenitis in children does well after excision, whereas disseminated disease in profoundly immunosuppressed patients can be difficult to control and carries a worse prognosis.","pitfalls":["Not alerting the laboratory, so appropriate media and incubation temperature are not used and cultures are falsely negative","Failing to reduce immunosuppression as part of treatment","Attributing skin nodules to another cause and delaying biopsy","Treating osteoarticular disease medically without surgical input"],"management":["Alert the laboratory to culture on iron- (haemin-) supplemented media at a lower incubation temperature, as routine conditions fail to recover the organism and delay treatment","Treat with prolonged multidrug antimycobacterial therapy, typically a macrolide such as clarithromycin combined with a rifamycin such as rifabutin and a fluoroquinolone such as ciprofloxacin","Reduce and optimise immunosuppression where possible, tapering tumour-necrosis-factor inhibitors or minimising transplant immunosuppression","Debride skin lesions surgically and manage accompanying septic arthritis or osteomyelitis with combined medical and surgical care","Treat childhood cervical lymphadenitis with complete surgical excision","Stage for dissemination with blood cultures and imaging in profoundly immunosuppressed patients and monitor treatment response","Refer to infectious diseases, recognising that outcome depends heavily on how quickly immunosuppression can be reversed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_immunogenum_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium immunogenum infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["M. immunogenum infection","Mycobacterium immunogenum hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rapidly growing mycobacterium closely related to the M. abscessus and M. chelonae group, from which it was formerly indistinguishable without molecular methods","Its best-known niche is metalworking fluid, where contamination has caused outbreaks of hypersensitivity pneumonitis among machine-shop workers","Also found in water systems and contaminated solutions, and a cause of catheter-related and other health-care-associated infection","The occupational lung disease is an immune (hypersensitivity) reaction to aerosolised organisms rather than invasive infection","True infection includes bloodstream infection related to intravascular catheters and skin or soft-tissue infection after procedures","Reliable identification requires molecular methods such as rpoB or hsp65 sequencing to separate it from other rapid growers","Like related rapid growers it is often multidrug-resistant with inducible macrolide resistance, so treatment is susceptibility-guided and combined with source removal"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium immunogenum is an environmental rapidly growing mycobacterium closely allied to the M. abscessus and M. chelonae group. Its characteristic reservoir is metalworking fluid, and inhalation of contaminated aerosols provokes hypersensitivity pneumonitis, an immune-mediated reaction to the organism rather than tissue invasion. Where it causes genuine infection, this usually follows inoculation or intravascular-device contamination, giving catheter-related bloodstream infection or post-procedural skin and soft-tissue disease, with biofilm on devices promoting persistence. Its resistance profile resembles that of related rapid growers, including inducible macrolide resistance, so cure depends on susceptibility-guided therapy and removal of the source or device.","additionalDifferentials":["Other rapidly growing mycobacteria (M. abscessus, M. chelonae)","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis from other causes","Catheter-related bloodstream infection from conventional organisms","Bacterial or fungal post-procedural soft-tissue infection","Occupational asthma or organic dust toxic syndrome","Other nontuberculous mycobacterial disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Mycobacterial culture demonstrating rapid growth","Molecular identification (rpoB or hsp65 sequencing) to distinguish it from related rapid growers","Drug-susceptibility testing including detection of inducible macrolide resistance","Occupational and exposure history with sampling of metalworking fluid in suspected outbreaks","Chest CT and lung assessment for hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Blood and device cultures in suspected catheter-related infection"],"redFlags":["Clustered respiratory illness in machine-shop workers suggesting a contaminated metalworking-fluid source","Catheter-related bloodstream infection requiring line removal","Health-care-associated clusters pointing to a contaminated solution or device","Progressive infection despite therapy, reflecting drug resistance"],"prognosis":"Occupational hypersensitivity pneumonitis usually improves with removal from the offending exposure. Genuine infection carries the guarded outlook of the related rapid growers, being difficult to eradicate with antimicrobials alone; adequate source control, including device removal, is central to a good outcome.","pitfalls":["Misidentifying it as M. abscessus or M. chelonae without molecular testing","Treating hypersensitivity pneumonitis as invasive infection instead of removing the exposure","Relying on a macrolide without testing for inducible resistance","Failing to remove an infected catheter or identify a contaminated solution"],"management":["For occupational hypersensitivity pneumonitis, remove the worker from the contaminated metalworking-fluid exposure as the primary treatment, using corticosteroids for symptomatic inflammation and remediating the fluid system","For genuine infection, treat with susceptibility-guided multidrug therapy as for related rapid growers, such as amikacin with a macrolide where susceptible, testing for inducible macrolide resistance","Achieve source control by removing infected intravascular catheters or other devices where biofilm sustains infection","Confirm the species by molecular methods such as rpoB or hsp65 sequencing to separate it from other rapid growers and guide expectations","Investigate the occupational or procedural source, sampling metalworking fluid and water systems in suspected outbreaks","Monitor treatment response and drug toxicity across the prolonged antimycobacterial course","Manage with infectious diseases and, for the lung disease, occupational medicine and respiratory input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_kansasii_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium kansasii infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["M. kansasii infection","Mycobacterium kansasii lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A slow-growing photochromogen (producing yellow pigment on exposure to light) that is one of the more virulent nontuberculous mycobacteria","Acquired chiefly from municipal tap water rather than soil, with geographical clustering in certain regions and among miners and industrial workers","Produces the most tuberculosis-like illness of the nontuberculous mycobacteria, with chronic cavitary upper-lobe pulmonary disease, cough, and haemoptysis","Most often affects middle-aged men with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, pneumoconiosis, or previous tuberculosis","Isolation is rarely due to simple contamination, so a positive respiratory culture usually reflects genuine disease","Carries the ESAT-6 and CFP-10 antigens and can therefore cause a positive interferon-gamma release assay, adding to the confusion with tuberculosis","Rifampicin is the cornerstone of therapy, combined with agents such as ethambutol and a macrolide or isoniazid, and rifampicin susceptibility should be tested","Responds well to rifampicin-based combination treatment, giving one of the better outcomes among the nontuberculous mycobacteria"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium kansasii is a slow-growing photochromogenic mycobacterium whose principal reservoir is treated water-distribution systems. Inhalation leads to pulmonary infection in hosts with pre-existing lung damage, where it produces a granulomatous, frequently cavitating process closely resembling tuberculosis. Impaired cell-mediated immunity, notably advanced HIV infection, predisposes to disseminated disease and extrapulmonary sites such as lymph nodes, skin, and joints. Because the organism expresses antigens shared with M. tuberculosis, immune-based tests can cross-react. Its comparatively predictable susceptibility to rifampicin underlies the good response to rifampicin-based regimens.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary tuberculosis","Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease","Other cavitary NTM lung disease (M. xenopi, M. malmoense)","Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis","Lung malignancy with cavitation","Bacterial lung abscess"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Mycobacterial culture with recognition of photochromogenic pigment","Molecular identification to distinguish it from M. tuberculosis and other NTM","Rifampicin susceptibility testing of the isolate","Chest radiograph and CT for upper-lobe cavitary or fibronodular disease","Repeated sputum cultures to meet diagnostic criteria","HIV testing and immune assessment, especially with disseminated disease"],"redFlags":["Extensive cavitary disease with haemoptysis","Disseminated disease indicating advanced immunosuppression such as HIV","Rifampicin resistance on susceptibility testing","Rapid clinical or radiographic progression","Diagnostic confusion leading to treatment for tuberculosis and delay in correct therapy"],"prognosis":"With rifampicin-based combination therapy the prognosis is generally favourable and relapse is uncommon in adherent patients, making M. kansasii one of the more treatable nontuberculous mycobacteria. Rifampicin resistance, extensive structural lung damage, and significant immunosuppression worsen outcomes.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the illness and a positive interferon-gamma release assay for tuberculosis","Dismissing a positive culture as a contaminant when it usually signifies real disease","Omitting rifampicin susceptibility testing","Under-treating with an inadequate or single-drug regimen"],"management":["Treat with a prolonged rifampicin-based combination regimen, typically rifampicin, ethambutol, and either isoniazid or a macrolide, which achieves reliably good outcomes","Perform rifampicin susceptibility testing to confirm the isolate remains rifampicin-susceptible and guide the regimen","Manage the underlying structural lung disease, such as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, alongside antimycobacterial treatment","Monitor for drug toxicity, including ethambutol-related ocular toxicity with visual monitoring and hepatotoxicity, and track response with repeated sputum cultures","Perform HIV testing and immune assessment, treating disseminated disease more intensively and starting antiretroviral therapy where indicated while accounting for rifamycin interactions","Refer to infectious diseases or respiratory medicine, noting the generally favourable prognosis and low relapse rate in adherent patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_malmoense_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium malmoense infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["M. malmoense infection","Mycobacterium malmoense lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A slow-growing non-chromogenic mycobacterium that can take several weeks to grow, so prolonged incubation is important","Shows striking geographical clustering, being reported particularly from northern Europe, including Scandinavia and the United Kingdom","Acquired from the environment (soil and water), it most often causes chronic pulmonary disease resembling that due to Mycobacterium avium complex or M. kansasii","Typically affects middle-aged and older adults with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, pneumoconiosis, or a smoking history, producing cavitary or fibronodular changes","In young children it is a recognised cause of cervical lymphadenitis","Its slow growth means cultures may be reported as negative if not held long enough","Treatment is with rifampicin and ethambutol, often with a macrolide or a quinolone, while childhood lymphadenitis is managed by surgical excision"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium malmoense is a slow-growing, non-pigmented environmental mycobacterium found in soil and water, with a distinct geographical distribution favouring northern Europe. Inhalation causes pulmonary infection chiefly in adults with pre-existing lung damage, producing a granulomatous, often cavitating process similar to that of M. avium complex and M. kansasii. In immunocompetent children the organism localises to cervical lymph nodes, as with other nontuberculous mycobacteria. Its very slow growth in culture can delay or prevent diagnosis unless incubation is sufficiently prolonged.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary tuberculosis","Mycobacterium avium complex or M. kansasii lung disease","Other cavitary NTM lung disease (M. xenopi)","Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis","Lung malignancy","Nontuberculous mycobacterial or tuberculous lymphadenitis in children"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Mycobacterial culture with prolonged incubation given slow growth","Molecular identification to species level","Chest radiograph and CT for cavitary or fibronodular disease","Repeated respiratory cultures to satisfy diagnostic criteria","Excisional biopsy and culture for suspected childhood lymphadenitis","Assessment of underlying lung disease and immune status"],"redFlags":["Progressive cavitary disease with constitutional symptoms or haemoptysis","Facial-nerve proximity when excising cervical nodes in children","Disseminated disease suggesting immunosuppression","Prolonged culture-negative results despite strong clinical suspicion"],"prognosis":"Pulmonary disease responds reasonably to prolonged rifampicin- and ethambutol-based combination therapy, though relapse can occur and outcomes are influenced by underlying lung disease. Childhood cervical lymphadenitis does well after complete surgical excision.","pitfalls":["Discarding cultures before slow growth can be detected","Failing to meet diagnostic criteria before treating respiratory isolates","Performing incision and drainage of a child's node, promoting a chronic sinus","Assuming the same regimen and response as for M. tuberculosis"],"management":["Treat pulmonary disease with prolonged rifampicin- and ethambutol-based combination therapy, commonly with the addition of a macrolide such as clarithromycin","Ensure sufficiently prolonged culture incubation to confirm the diagnosis, since this very slow grower may otherwise be reported as negative","Manage the underlying lung disease, such as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and support smoking cessation","Monitor treatment response with repeated respiratory cultures and watch for drug toxicity, including ethambutol-related visual changes","Treat childhood cervical lymphadenitis with complete surgical excision","Refer to infectious diseases or respiratory medicine, recognising that relapse can occur and outcomes are shaped by underlying lung disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_marinum_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium marinum infection (fish-tank granuloma)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fish-tank granuloma","swimming-pool granuloma","M. marinum infection","aquarium granuloma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A slow-growing photochromogenic mycobacterium that grows best at cooler temperatures (around 30 degrees Celsius), which explains its predilection for the skin of the cooler distal extremities","Classically acquired from aquaria, fish handling, and fresh or salt water, giving rise to the terms fish-tank and swimming-pool granuloma; a careful exposure history is the key to suspecting it","Typically presents weeks after minor skin trauma as a papulonodular or verrucous lesion on the hand or finger that may ulcerate","Can spread proximally along lymphatics as a chain of nodules (sporotrichoid pattern), mimicking sporotrichosis","Deeper extension to tenosynovitis, septic arthritis, bursitis, or osteomyelitis occurs in a minority and often causes diagnostic delay","Diagnosis rests on biopsy for histology and mycobacterial culture, with the laboratory specifically asked to incubate at a lower temperature to recover the organism","Management is with macrolide-based combination antimicrobial therapy, often for several months, with surgical debridement reserved for deep or refractory infection","It carries the ESAT-6 and CFP-10 antigens and can therefore produce a positive interferon-gamma release assay, which should not be mistaken for tuberculosis"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium marinum is a ubiquitous inhabitant of fresh and salt water, including fish tanks and swimming pools. Infection follows inoculation of the organism through a break in the skin, usually on a hand or other distal site exposed during aquarium cleaning or fish handling. Because the bacterium grows optimally at temperatures below core body temperature, disease tends to remain localised to the cooler superficial tissues and rarely disseminates in immunocompetent people. A granulomatous inflammatory response develops at the inoculation site, producing the characteristic nodular or verrucous lesion, and organisms may track along lymphatic channels to produce ascending (sporotrichoid) nodules. In immunosuppressed hosts deeper and more extensive disease, including tenosynovitis and osteomyelitis, is more likely.","additionalDifferentials":["Sporotrichosis","Cutaneous leishmaniasis","Nocardiosis with sporotrichoid spread","Cutaneous tuberculosis (lupus vulgaris or tuberculosis verrucosa cutis)","Other nontuberculous mycobacterial skin infection","Pyogenic granuloma or common viral wart"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy for histopathology demonstrating granulomatous inflammation","Mycobacterial culture of tissue with incubation at a lower temperature (around 30 degrees Celsius)","Acid-fast staining of tissue, recognising that yield is often low","Molecular identification (line-probe assay or gene sequencing) to speciate the isolate","Imaging (ultrasound or MRI) if deep structures such as tendon sheaths or bone are suspected","Assessment of immune status when disease is extensive or atypical"],"redFlags":["Deep involvement with tenosynovitis, septic arthritis, or osteomyelitis requiring surgical input","Extensive or disseminated disease suggesting significant immunosuppression","Progressive lesions despite prolonged empirical antibacterial therapy","Functional impairment of the hand from tendon-sheath involvement"],"prognosis":"Localised cutaneous disease usually responds well to prolonged combination therapy with a good outcome, although resolution is slow. Deep infection of tendons, joints, or bone often requires surgery and may leave residual functional impairment, and treatment delay is common because the diagnosis is not initially suspected.","pitfalls":["Failing to ask about aquarium, fish, or water exposure so the diagnosis is never considered","Not requesting culture at a lower incubation temperature, leading to falsely negative cultures","Mistaking sporotrichoid spread for fungal sporotrichosis and treating incorrectly","Interpreting a positive interferon-gamma release assay as tuberculosis"],"management":["Treat with prolonged combination antimycobacterial therapy, typically a macrolide such as clarithromycin with ethambutol or rifampicin, using tetracyclines such as doxycycline or co-trimoxazole as alternatives","Alert the laboratory to incubate cultures at a lower temperature so the organism can be recovered and treatment targeted","Refer for surgical debridement where infection extends to tendon sheaths, joints, or bone, as deep disease often needs operative management","Provide exposure-avoidance advice, including waterproof gloves when handling aquaria or fish, to prevent recurrence and inform others","Continue therapy well beyond clinical resolution given the characteristically slow response, monitoring for drug toxicity","Assess immune status where disease is extensive or atypical","Refer to infectious diseases or dermatology, noting the good outlook for cutaneous disease but potential residual impairment after deep infection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_scrofulaceum_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium scrofulaceum infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["M. scrofulaceum infection","scrofulaceum lymphadenitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A slow-growing scotochromogen (producing yellow-orange pigment even in the dark) named for its historical role in scrofula","Was a classic cause of cervical lymphadenitis in young children before Mycobacterium avium complex became the predominant cause","Acquired from the environment, including water, soil, and formerly raw dairy products, typically entering through the mouth and pharynx","Presents in a child of one to five years as a unilateral, firm, painless, enlarging neck node with little systemic upset","Untreated nodes may soften, discolour the overlying skin, and drain through a chronic sinus","Complete surgical excision of the affected node is the treatment of choice and is usually curative","Antimicrobial therapy is adjunctive, reserved for incomplete excision, recurrence, or extensive disease, and incision and drainage should be avoided because it promotes sinus formation"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium scrofulaceum is a slow-growing scotochromogenic environmental mycobacterium. In young children it is thought to enter through the oropharyngeal mucosa and spread to draining cervical and submandibular lymph nodes, where a granulomatous response with caseation develops. The relatively contained immune response accounts for the localised, minimally symptomatic adenitis; progressive necrosis can liquefy and track to the skin, producing discolouration, fluctuance, and a chronically discharging sinus. In older or immunocompromised patients pulmonary and disseminated disease occur uncommonly.","additionalDifferentials":["Mycobacterium avium complex lymphadenitis","Tuberculous lymphadenitis","Cat-scratch disease","Reactive or pyogenic bacterial lymphadenitis","Lymphoma","Branchial cleft cyst or other congenital neck swelling"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional lymph-node biopsy for histology and mycobacterial culture","Mycobacterial culture with recognition of scotochromogenic pigment","Molecular identification to species level","Tuberculin skin test and interferon-gamma release assay to help exclude tuberculosis","Ultrasound of the neck to define the extent of nodal disease","Assessment of immune status in atypical, extensive, or recurrent disease"],"redFlags":["Involvement near the facial nerve where surgery risks nerve injury","Features suggesting tuberculosis requiring exclusion and contact tracing","Extensive, bilateral, or recurrent disease suggesting immunodeficiency","Rapidly enlarging or systemically symptomatic swelling suggesting malignancy"],"prognosis":"Childhood cervical lymphadenitis has an excellent prognosis after complete surgical excision, which is generally curative. Incomplete excision or inappropriate incision and drainage can lead to chronic sinuses and prolonged healing. Pulmonary and disseminated disease are uncommon and carry a more guarded outlook.","pitfalls":["Performing incision and drainage, which promotes a chronic discharging sinus","Failing to exclude tuberculosis before attributing adenitis to nontuberculous mycobacteria","Delaying diagnosis by not sending tissue for mycobacterial culture and molecular testing","Risking facial-nerve injury during excision of periparotid nodes"],"management":["Undertake complete surgical excision of the affected node as the definitive treatment of choice, which is usually curative","Avoid incision and drainage, which can produce chronic sinuses and prolonged healing","Reserve macrolide-based multidrug antimycobacterial therapy for incompletely excised disease or for uncommon pulmonary and disseminated infection","Define the extent of nodal disease with neck ultrasound to plan complete excision","Assess immune status in atypical, extensive, or recurrent disease","Refer for multidisciplinary care with paediatric surgery or ENT and infectious diseases, noting the excellent prognosis of childhood adenitis after complete excision"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_simiae_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium simiae infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["M. simiae infection","Mycobacterium simiae lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A slow-growing photochromogen originally isolated from monkeys, hence its name","Its main reservoir is water, including aquifers and tap water, and it shows geographical clustering in certain regions","Very commonly isolated as an environmental contaminant or coloniser, so true disease is far less frequent than positive cultures suggest","When genuinely pathogenic it causes nodular-bronchiectatic or cavitary pulmonary disease and, in the profoundly immunosuppressed, disseminated infection","Notoriously drug-resistant, with unpredictable in vitro susceptibility and a poor response to treatment","Distinguishing colonisation from disease is a central challenge and requires strict application of diagnostic criteria","When treatment is warranted, susceptibility-guided combinations (often including a macrolide, moxifloxacin, and co-trimoxazole) are used, but results are frequently disappointing"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium simiae is a slow-growing photochromogenic mycobacterium whose reservoir is water, including groundwater aquifers and municipal supplies, giving rise to regional clusters. It is among the mycobacteria most often recovered as a contaminant or airway coloniser, and only a minority of positive cultures reflect true disease. When pathogenic it produces chronic pulmonary infection in hosts with structural lung disease and disseminated disease in the profoundly immunosuppressed. Intrinsic and unpredictable drug resistance, with poor correlation between in vitro testing and clinical response, makes it one of the more difficult nontuberculous mycobacteria to treat.","additionalDifferentials":["Colonisation or contamination without true disease","Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Other NTM lung disease","Bronchiectasis exacerbation from conventional pathogens","Disseminated NTM in advanced HIV"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repeated respiratory cultures to distinguish colonisation from genuine disease","Mycobacterial culture with recognition of photochromogenic pigment","Molecular identification to species level","Drug-susceptibility testing, recognising its limited predictive value","Chest CT for nodular-bronchiectatic or cavitary disease","Assessment of immune status, particularly for disseminated disease"],"redFlags":["Disseminated disease indicating profound immunosuppression","Progressive pulmonary disease meeting diagnostic criteria","Poor response to therapy reflecting extensive drug resistance","Clustered isolates suggesting a shared water source"],"prognosis":"Even when true disease is confirmed, response to treatment is often poor because of extensive and unpredictable drug resistance, so the prognosis is guarded. Many isolates, however, represent colonisation that does not require treatment and does not progress.","pitfalls":["Treating a coloniser or contaminant as genuine disease and exposing the patient to prolonged toxic therapy","Relying too heavily on in vitro susceptibility that correlates poorly with outcome","Failing to meet diagnostic criteria before starting treatment","Underestimating the difficulty of achieving cure"],"management":["Distinguish colonisation or contamination from genuine disease by strict diagnostic criteria and repeated respiratory cultures, since many isolates need no treatment","When true disease is confirmed, use multidrug therapy such as a macrolide with a fluoroquinolone like moxifloxacin and co-trimoxazole, recognising the poor correlation between in vitro susceptibility and response","Aim for disease control rather than reliable cure given the notorious and unpredictable drug resistance","Manage the underlying structural lung disease alongside antimycobacterial treatment","Monitor treatment response with repeated cultures and watch for drug toxicity over the prolonged course","Assess immune status, particularly where disseminated disease is suspected in profoundly immunosuppressed patients","Refer to infectious diseases or respiratory medicine given the guarded prognosis and treatment difficulty"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycobacterium_xenopi_infection":{"name":"Mycobacterium xenopi infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["M. xenopi infection","Mycobacterium xenopi lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A slow-growing thermophilic scotochromogen that grows optimally at high temperatures (around 42 to 45 degrees Celsius)","Its thermophilia explains its niche in hot-water systems, including hospital water supplies, storage tanks, and taps","Contaminated water and bronchoscopes have caused pseudo-outbreaks, so isolation does not always indicate true disease","Most often causes chronic cavitary or fibronodular upper-lobe pulmonary disease in middle-aged men with pre-existing lung disease","Distinguishing genuine infection from colonisation or laboratory pseudo-infection requires careful application of diagnostic criteria","Recovery in the laboratory is improved by incubating cultures at a higher temperature","Treatment usually combines rifampicin, ethambutol, and a macrolide, sometimes with a quinolone, though the optimal regimen is uncertain and outcomes are guarded"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium xenopi is a slow-growing, thermophilic scotochromogenic mycobacterium that thrives in hot-water systems, its principal reservoir. Inhalation of aerosols leads to pulmonary infection predominantly in hosts with structural lung disease, producing cavitary or fibronodular changes. Because the organism colonises water systems and instruments, it is frequently isolated as a contaminant or coloniser, and clusters of positive cultures may represent pseudo-outbreaks rather than disease. Disseminated infection is uncommon and occurs with significant immunosuppression. Reported mortality is relatively high, though this partly reflects the burden of underlying comorbidity.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary tuberculosis","Mycobacterium avium complex or M. kansasii lung disease","Other cavitary NTM lung disease (M. malmoense)","Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis","Lung malignancy","Colonisation or laboratory pseudo-infection without true disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Mycobacterial culture incubated at a higher temperature to aid recovery","Molecular identification to species level","Repeated respiratory cultures to distinguish disease from colonisation or contamination","Chest CT for cavitary or fibronodular disease","Review of water systems and bronchoscope decontamination in clustered cases","Assessment of underlying lung disease and immune status"],"redFlags":["Progressive cavitary disease with weight loss or haemoptysis","Clustered isolates suggesting a contaminated water source or bronchoscope needing investigation","Disseminated disease indicating significant immunosuppression","Deterioration despite therapy given the guarded response"],"prognosis":"The prognosis of M. xenopi pulmonary disease is guarded, with relatively high mortality that is influenced strongly by coexisting lung disease and comorbidity. Response to treatment is variable and the best regimen remains uncertain, so outcomes are less favourable than for M. kansasii.","pitfalls":["Treating a contaminant or coloniser as genuine disease without meeting diagnostic criteria","Overlooking a contaminated hospital water supply or bronchoscope in clustered cases","Failing to incubate cultures at a higher temperature, reducing recovery","Attributing all clinical decline to the organism rather than to underlying comorbidity"],"management":["Treat with prolonged multidrug combination therapy, typically rifampicin and ethambutol with a macrolide and/or a fluoroquinolone such as moxifloxacin, accepting that the optimal regimen remains uncertain","Distinguish genuine disease from colonisation or laboratory pseudo-infection through repeated respiratory cultures and strict diagnostic criteria before treating","Ask the laboratory to incubate cultures at a higher temperature to improve recovery of this thermophilic organism","Consider surgical resection for localised cavitary disease in suitable candidates as an adjunct to drug therapy","Manage the underlying lung disease and coexisting comorbidity, which strongly influence outcome","Investigate hospital hot-water systems and bronchoscope decontamination when clustered isolates suggest a pseudo-outbreak","Refer to infectious diseases or respiratory medicine, recognising the guarded prognosis and relatively high mortality"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycoplasma_genitalium_infection":{"name":"Mycoplasma genitalium infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mycoplasma genitalium","M. genitalium","Mgen","MG"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell / BASHH-CDC STI guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Mycoplasma genitalium is a small, slow-growing, cell-wall-deficient bacterium and an established sexually transmitted cause of non-gonococcal urethritis in men and cervicitis in women","Many infections are asymptomatic; when symptomatic, men typically report dysuria and urethral discharge while women may have discharge, intermenstrual or postcoital bleeding, or pelvic pain","It is an important cause of persistent or recurrent non-gonococcal urethritis after treatment for presumed chlamydia and is linked to cervicitis and pelvic inflammatory disease","Diagnosis relies on nucleic acid amplification testing because the organism is extremely difficult to culture; lacking a cell wall, it is not seen on Gram stain","Wherever possible, positive samples should undergo testing for macrolide-resistance-mediating mutations to guide therapy, given rapidly rising macrolide resistance","First-line treatment is often azithromycin (frequently as an extended regimen) in macrolide-susceptible infection, with doxycycline commonly used to lower bacterial load beforehand","Moxifloxacin is reserved for macrolide-resistant or persistent infection, and treatment failure with escalating resistance is an increasing problem","Test of cure and partner notification are recommended, and routine screening of asymptomatic people is discouraged to limit resistance selection"],"pathophysiology":"Mycoplasma genitalium is among the smallest self-replicating bacteria and lacks a rigid cell wall, which renders it invisible on Gram stain and intrinsically resistant to cell-wall-active antibiotics such as beta-lactams. It is transmitted sexually and attaches to and glides along genital and urethral epithelial surfaces by means of a specialised terminal adhesion organelle, provoking a local inflammatory response that manifests as urethritis, cervicitis, or endometrial and tubal inflammation. Because it grows slowly and fastidiously, infection tends to be indolent and persistent, and its capacity to acquire mutations in the 23S ribosomal RNA gene (conferring macrolide resistance) and in topoisomerase genes (conferring fluoroquinolone resistance) underlies the growing difficulty in achieving cure.","additionalDifferentials":["Chlamydia trachomatis urethritis or cervicitis","Neisseria gonorrhoeae infection","Trichomonas vaginalis infection","Ureaplasma or other genital mollicute infection","Urinary tract infection","Non-infective (mechanical or chemical) urethritis","Herpes simplex urethritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nucleic acid amplification testing of first-void urine (men) or vaginal, endocervical, or urine samples (women)","Reflex molecular testing for 23S rRNA macrolide-resistance mutations on positive specimens","Concurrent nucleic acid amplification testing for chlamydia and gonorrhoea","Microscopy of urethral or genital smears to document urethritis or cervicitis where available","Test of cure by nucleic acid amplification testing several weeks after completing treatment","Screening for other sexually transmitted infections including HIV and syphilis"],"redFlags":["Features of pelvic inflammatory disease such as pelvic pain, adnexal tenderness, or fever","Persistent or recurrent urethritis despite appropriate first-line therapy suggesting antimicrobial resistance","Signs of epididymo-orchitis in men","Repeated treatment failure indicating multidrug-resistant infection needing specialist input"],"prognosis":"Susceptible infections usually respond to appropriate antibiotics, but rising macrolide and emerging fluoroquinolone resistance make persistent and recurrent infection increasingly common; untreated or inadequately treated disease may contribute to pelvic inflammatory disease and associated reproductive sequelae.","pitfalls":["Treating with azithromycin without resistance-guided testing, which can select for macrolide resistance","Assuming persistent non-gonococcal urethritis is chlamydia and not testing for Mycoplasma genitalium","Screening asymptomatic individuals, which drives unnecessary treatment and resistance","Omitting test of cure and partner notification, allowing reinfection and onward transmission"],"management":["Adopt resistance-guided therapy, testing positive specimens for 23S rRNA macrolide-resistance mutations to direct antibiotic choice","In macrolide-susceptible infection, use doxycycline to lower organism load followed by an extended azithromycin regimen","In macrolide-resistant infection, use doxycycline followed by a fluoroquinolone such as moxifloxacin","Avoid beta-lactams entirely, as the cell-wall-deficient organism is intrinsically resistant to them","Undertake partner notification and treatment and advise abstaining from sex until treatment is completed to prevent reinfection","Arrange test of cure by nucleic acid amplification testing several weeks after treatment, and screen for coexisting sexually transmitted infections including HIV and syphilis","Manage complications such as pelvic inflammatory disease and refer to genitourinary medicine for persistent or recurrent infection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["BASHH UK national guideline for the management of infection with Mycoplasma genitalium","CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines","Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycoplasma_infection":{"name":"Mycoplasma Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mycoplasma pneumoniae","atypical pneumonia","walking pneumonia"],"chapter":193,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1464-1466","clinicalPearls":["M. pneumoniae pneumonia is also called Eaton agent pneumonia, primary atypical pneumonia, and 'walking' pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Onset of pneumonia is usually gradual over several days; the most common presenting symptom is cough, which is typically nonproductive, though headache, malaise, chills, and fever are noted in the majority of patients (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Pneumonia develops in only 3-13% of infected individuals; upper respiratory illness is far more common, with an estimated incidence up to 20 times that of pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Wheezes or rales are detected in about 80% of patients with M. pneumoniae pneumonia, but in many patients pneumonia can be diagnosed only by chest radiography (Harrison 22e p.1465).","The most common radiographic pattern is peribronchial pneumonia with thickened bronchial markings, streaks of interstitial infiltration, and subsegmental atelectasis; segmental or lobar consolidation is not uncommon (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Clinical presentation does not reliably distinguish M. pneumoniae pneumonia from other causes of community-acquired pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Many patients with extrapulmonary M. pneumoniae disease do not have respiratory disease (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Hematologic manifestations include hemolytic anemia, aplastic anemia, cold agglutinins, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and hypercoagulopathy; when anemia occurs it generally develops in the second or third week of illness (Harrison 22e p.1466).","Erythema multiforme major (Stevens-Johnson syndrome) is the most clinically significant skin eruption associated with M. pneumoniae and appears to occur more commonly with M. pneumoniae than with other infectious agents; in M. pneumoniae-induced rash and mucositis (MIRM)/reactive infectious mucocutaneous eruption (RIME) there may be minimal cutaneous involvement with prominent mucosal disease (Harrison 22e p.1466, p.591).","M. pneumoniae has been implicated as a likely etiologic agent in 5-7% of cases of encephalitis (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Diagnosis is most sensitively and rapidly made by combining PCR of respiratory tract secretions with serologic testing of paired acute- and convalescent-phase samples (Harrison 22e p.1466).","M. pneumoniae is the most frequently detected 'atypical' organism in adult community-acquired pneumonia; older pooled data gave a prevalence of about 22.7%, and more recent (2002-2015) analyses gave about 7.2% (Harrison 22e p.1465)."],"pathophysiology":"Mycoplasmas are prokaryotes of the class Mollicutes, the smallest organisms capable of independent replication; they lack a cell wall and are bound only by a cell membrane, which explains the inactivity of beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins and cephalosporins) against them. M. pneumoniae is generally an extracellular pathogen that attaches to ciliated respiratory epithelial cells via a complex terminal organelle using interactive adhesins and accessory proteins. Tissue injury is thought to be mediated by production of hydrogen peroxide and an ADP-ribosylating and vacuolating cytotoxin similar to pertussis toxin. Lacking a cell wall, mycoplasmas lack cell-wall-derived innate immune stimulators (lipopolysaccharide, lipoteichoic acid, peptidoglycan), but membrane lipoproteins act through Toll-like receptors (primarily TLR2). Innate immunity provides most lung defense, cellular immunity may be immunopathogenic, and humoral immunity protects against dissemination; patients with humoral immunodeficiency are prone to disseminated disease (arthritis, meningitis, osteomyelitis). Extrapulmonary manifestations may arise from disseminated infection, postinfectious autoimmune phenomena (e.g., Guillain-Barre syndrome), or possibly an ADP-ribosylating toxin (Harrison 22e p.1464-1466).","additionalDifferentials":["Chlamydia (Chlamydophila) pneumoniae community-acquired pneumonia","Legionella species pneumonia","Streptococcus pneumoniae and other bacterial community-acquired pneumonia","Viral respiratory tract infection / pneumonia"],"infectionMimics":["Chlamydophila pneumoniae pneumonia","Legionella pneumonia","Viral atypical pneumonia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Asthma / reactive airway disease (wheezing overlap)","Drug-induced or herpes-associated erythema multiforme major (overlapping with Mycoplasma-associated SJS/MIRM)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Respiratory PCR for M. pneumoniae (sensitivity 65-90%, specificity 90-100%) - allows rapid early diagnosis","Paired acute- and convalescent-phase serology (IgM and IgG) by enzyme-linked immunoassay; a single acute sample is inadequate because antibodies may not develop until ~2 weeks","Respiratory culture on special media (sensitivity <=60%, specificity 100%) - not recommended for routine diagnosis as it may take weeks","Chest radiography","Cerebrospinal fluid PCR for extrapulmonary (CNS) infection","Macrolide-resistance testing by culture and/or PCR when resistance is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neurologic manifestations: meningoencephalitis, encephalitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome, aseptic meningitis, transverse myelitis, acute disseminated encephalomyelitis, cerebrovascular thromboembolic events, acute psychosis, cerebellar ataxia, cranial neuropathy","Severe mucocutaneous disease: erythema multiforme major / Stevens-Johnson syndrome / MIRM","Hemolytic anemia, disseminated intravascular coagulation, hypercoagulopathy","Disseminated infection (septic arthritis, meningitis, osteomyelitis), especially in hypogammaglobulinemic / humorally immunodeficient patients","Community-acquired pneumonia failing to respond to a penicillin or cephalosporin"],"prognosis":"M. pneumoniae pneumonia is generally self-limited, with symptoms usually resolving within 2-3 weeks of onset even without treatment; appropriate antimicrobial therapy significantly shortens the duration of clinical illness. Infection uncommonly results in critical illness and only rarely in death. Long-term recurrent wheezing or reactive airway disease may follow resolution of acute pneumonia, and a possible link to asthma is under investigation. Immunity following severe infection is more protective and longer-lasting than after mild infection, and genuine second attacks of pneumonia are reported infrequently (Harrison 22e p.1465).","pitfalls":["Because mycoplasmas lack a cell wall, beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins and cephalosporins) are ineffective; consider M. pneumoniae particularly when community-acquired pneumonia fails to respond to these agents (Harrison 22e p.1464-1465).","M. pneumoniae lacks a cell wall and is therefore not visible on Gram stain (Harrison 22e p.1466).","Measurement of cold agglutinin titers is no longer recommended for diagnosis because the findings are nonspecific and specific M. pneumoniae assays are now available (Harrison 22e p.1466).","A single acute-phase serum sample is not adequate for diagnosis, and IgM antibody can persist for up to 1 year, so its presence may indicate recent rather than acute infection - paired samples are needed (Harrison 22e p.1466).","Little evidence supports the commonly held belief that M. pneumoniae is an important cause of otitis media, with or without bullous myringitis (Harrison 22e p.1465).","A test of cure for eradication is not suggested, because appropriate antibiotics do not appear to shorten the duration of detection by culture or PCR (Harrison 22e p.1466).","Ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin are not recommended for M. pneumoniae owing to high MICs and poor experimental performance, in contrast to respiratory fluoroquinolones (Harrison 22e p.1466)."],"management":["Treat with a macrolide such as azithromycin or clarithromycin as first-line therapy, avoiding beta-lactams because the wall-less organism is intrinsically resistant to them","Use a tetracycline such as doxycycline or a respiratory fluoroquinolone where macrolide resistance is suspected or in adults","Provide supportive care with antipyretics, analgesia, hydration, and rest, recognising that many infections are self-limiting","Relieve wheeze with bronchodilators and reserve hospital admission and supplemental oxygen for severe pneumonia or respiratory compromise","Identify and manage extrapulmonary manifestations such as haemolytic anaemia, neurological involvement, and severe mucocutaneous reactions","Send macrolide-resistance testing by culture or PCR when response to first-line therapy is poor"],"severityClassification":"No formal severity classification is given for Mycoplasma infection; M. pneumoniae illness is generally graded clinically from mild self-limited upper respiratory illness through pneumonia to severe/refractory or critical disease, with macrolide resistance and extrapulmonary involvement marking more severe courses (Harrison 22e p.1464-1466).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1464-1466","Harrison 22e p.591"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycosis_fungoides":{"name":"Mycosis Fungoides","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MF","cutaneous T-cell lymphoma","CTCL"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Mycosis fungoides is the commonest form of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma and usually runs an indolent course over years, evolving classically through patch, plaque, and tumour stages","Early patches are erythematous, finely scaly, and often persistent in sun-protected sites such as the buttocks and trunk (the bathing-trunk distribution), frequently mimicking eczema or psoriasis for years before diagnosis","A high index of suspicion is warranted for atypical, treatment-resistant, or variably shaped chronic dermatitis in an adult, particularly with poikilodermatous change","Diagnosis often requires multiple biopsies over time, since early histology can be subtle and non-specific; T-cell clonality studies and immunophenotyping support the diagnosis","Staging assesses skin (patch/plaque/tumour or erythroderma), lymph nodes, blood, and viscera, and drives both prognosis and treatment intensity","Skin-directed therapies (topical agents, phototherapy, radiotherapy) are used for early-stage disease, while advanced or transformed disease requires systemic and specialist haemato-oncological management","Sezary syndrome is the leukaemic, erythrodermic variant, characterised by widespread redness, circulating atypical (Sezary) cells, and lymphadenopathy, and carries a poorer prognosis","Large-cell transformation, tumour-stage disease, and extracutaneous spread mark a more aggressive course and warrant prompt reassessment"],"pathophysiology":"Mycosis fungoides is a malignant proliferation of skin-homing mature memory T-helper lymphocytes, typically expressing a CD4-positive phenotype with characteristic loss of certain T-cell surface antigens. The neoplastic cells display epidermotropism, migrating into the epidermis and forming intraepidermal collections (Pautrier microabscesses), a histological hallmark. A clonal T-cell receptor rearrangement can often be demonstrated. Over time, accumulating genetic and epigenetic alterations, altered cytokine signalling, and impaired antitumour immunity allow progression from patches and plaques to tumours, large-cell transformation, and dissemination to blood, nodes, and organs, as seen in advanced disease and Sezary syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic eczema or atopic dermatitis","Psoriasis and parapsoriasis","Drug eruptions and chronic contact dermatitis","Tinea corporis and other superficial dermatoses","Erythroderma of other causes (for the erythrodermic variant)","Other cutaneous lymphomas and lymphomatoid conditions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy, often repeated, with immunohistochemistry and T-cell receptor gene rearrangement (clonality) studies","Peripheral blood examination including flow cytometry for circulating atypical (Sezary) cells","Full staging with lymph node assessment and imaging (such as CT or PET-CT) in advanced disease","Lactate dehydrogenase and other bloods to help gauge tumour burden","Lymph node biopsy where nodal involvement is suspected"],"redFlags":["Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma is a malignancy: persistent, atypical, or treatment-resistant chronic dermatitis in an adult should prompt biopsy and specialist referral","Development of tumours, ulceration, or rapid progression suggesting large-cell transformation","Erythroderma with circulating atypical cells indicating Sezary syndrome","Lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, or systemic symptoms suggesting extracutaneous spread","Signs of secondary infection or sepsis in extensively involved or eroded skin"],"prognosis":"Early patch- and plaque-stage mycosis fungoides is often indolent with a life expectancy close to normal, but prognosis worsens markedly with tumour-stage disease, large-cell transformation, nodal or visceral involvement, and Sezary syndrome; ongoing surveillance is needed because progression can occur after years of stable disease.","pitfalls":["Labelling persistent atypical dermatitis as eczema or psoriasis for years and delaying biopsy","Relying on a single non-diagnostic biopsy to exclude the diagnosis","Overlooking Sezary syndrome as a cause of adult erythroderma","Undertreating early disease or, conversely, overtreating indolent disease with aggressive therapy","Missing large-cell transformation or extracutaneous progression during follow-up"],"management":["Match treatment intensity to stage, reserving skin-directed therapy for early patch and plaque disease and systemic therapy for advanced or transformed disease","For early-stage disease, use skin-directed measures such as topical corticosteroids, topical chlormethine (nitrogen mustard), topical retinoids or bexarotene, phototherapy with PUVA or narrowband UVB, and localised radiotherapy","For advanced, erythrodermic, or transformed disease, use systemic agents such as oral bexarotene, interferon, histone deacetylase inhibitors, brentuximab vedotin, mogamulizumab, or chemotherapy, with total skin electron beam therapy for extensive skin involvement","Treat Sezary syndrome with extracorporeal photopheresis, often combined with systemic therapy, and consider allogeneic stem-cell transplantation in selected younger patients","Provide symptomatic skin care with emollients, active management of pruritus, and treatment of secondary skin infection","Maintain surveillance with periodic restaging and monitoring for large-cell transformation, nodal or visceral progression, and lactate dehydrogenase as a marker of tumour burden","Manage through a multidisciplinary team spanning dermatology and haemato-oncology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"mycotic_aneurysm":{"name":"Mycotic (infective) aneurysm","system":"Rheumatology / Vascular","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["infective aneurysm","infected aneurysm","microbial arteritis with aneurysm","Salmonella aortitis","Staphylococcus aureus aortitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases / vascular surgery guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Refers to an infected arterial aneurysm, not a fungal one; most cases are bacterial","Salmonella species and Staphylococcus aureus are the most common causative organisms","Presents with fever, localised pain, and a tender, often rapidly enlarging pulsatile mass","Imaging may show a saccular, lobulated, or rapidly growing aneurysm with periaortic gas, fluid, or soft tissue","Positive blood cultures with a new or expanding aneurysm strongly support the diagnosis","Rupture risk is high and the process can progress quickly, so it is a surgical emergency","Treatment combines urgent surgical or endovascular repair with prolonged targeted antimicrobial therapy"],"pathophysiology":"A mycotic (infective) aneurysm forms when microorganisms infect and destroy the arterial wall. Bacteria reach the vessel through haematogenous seeding of the vasa vasorum, septic embolism (as in endocarditis), contiguous spread from adjacent infection, or direct inoculation. Organisms such as Salmonella, with its affinity for atherosclerotic and diseased intima, and Staphylococcus aureus incite suppurative inflammation that weakens and digests the wall, producing a rapidly expanding, often saccular aneurysm. The infected, structurally compromised wall is prone to early rupture and life-threatening haemorrhage.","additionalDifferentials":["Degenerative (atherosclerotic) aneurysm","Inflammatory aortic aneurysm","Non-infectious aortitis","Contained aneurysm rupture","Perivascular abscess or retroperitoneal infection","Penetrating aortic ulcer"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Multiple blood cultures before starting antimicrobials","CT angiography for aneurysm morphology and periaortic gas or fluid","PET or labelled white-cell imaging in diagnostically uncertain cases","Echocardiography to assess for infective endocarditis as a source","Inflammatory markers and full blood count","Intraoperative tissue and pus samples for microbiological culture"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe pain or haemodynamic collapse indicating rupture","Rapidly enlarging aneurysm on serial imaging","Sepsis with a pulsatile, tender mass","Periaortic gas suggesting aggressive infection"],"prognosis":"Mycotic aneurysm carries high mortality, particularly if rupture occurs, but prompt combined surgical or endovascular repair and prolonged targeted antimicrobial therapy substantially improve survival. Relapse of infection and graft complications require long-term follow-up.","pitfalls":["Attributing a rapidly enlarging aneurysm to degeneration and missing infection","Starting antimicrobials before obtaining blood cultures","Delaying surgical referral in a febrile patient with a tender pulsatile mass","Underestimating the duration of antimicrobial therapy needed after repair"],"management":["Treat as a surgical emergency with urgent open surgical or endovascular repair, involving debridement of infected tissue, aneurysm resection, and revascularisation","Take multiple blood cultures before starting antimicrobials and give prolonged targeted intravenous therapy guided by results, using anti-staphylococcal agents for Staphylococcus aureus and a fluoroquinolone or ceftriaxone for Salmonella","Start empirical broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics after cultures while awaiting microbiology, then narrow to targeted therapy","Identify and manage the source, including treating infective endocarditis where septic embolism is the origin","Resuscitate and provide haemodynamic support for rupture and haemorrhage, which is the dominant life-threatening risk","Maintain long-term follow-up with serial imaging and inflammatory markers for relapse and graft complications, using prolonged or suppressive antibiotics in selected patients","Manage through a multidisciplinary team of vascular surgery and infectious diseases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Society for Vascular Surgery guidance on infected aortic aneurysms"],"crossLinks":[]},"myd88_deficiency":{"name":"MyD88 Deficiency","system":"Immunology / Inborn errors of immunity","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MYD88","MyD88","Myeloid differentiation primary response 88 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive MyD88 deficiency produces a clinical picture essentially indistinguishable from IRAK-4 deficiency, reflecting the adjacent roles of the two proteins in the same signalling pathway.","Susceptibility is narrow: recurrent invasive pyogenic bacterial infection, particularly Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas aeruginosa.","Inflammatory responses are blunted, so fever and acute-phase reactants may be low or absent despite serious infection.","Antiviral, antifungal and antiparasitic immunity is largely intact because TLR3 and several other pathways are MyD88-independent.","Infection risk peaks in early childhood and tends to diminish as the patient grows older.","Standard immunological screening is frequently normal, requiring a high index of suspicion.","Antibiotic prophylaxis, immunoglobulin replacement and vaccination against encapsulated organisms are central to management."],"pathophysiology":"MYD88 encodes the cytosolic adaptor protein MyD88, which links Toll-like receptors (all except TLR3) and interleukin-1 receptor family members to their downstream kinases. On receptor engagement, MyD88 recruits IRAK-4 and IRAK-1 to assemble the myddosome, driving NF-kappaB and MAPK activation and proinflammatory cytokine production. Biallelic loss-of-function variants abolish this adaptor function and produce the same failure of early innate inflammatory responses seen in IRAK-4 deficiency, hence the shared susceptibility to invasive pyogenic bacteria and the characteristically muted febrile and acute-phase response. This germline loss-of-function state is mechanistically distinct from the somatic gain-of-function MYD88 variants that occur in certain lymphoid malignancies.","additionalDifferentials":["IRAK-4 deficiency (clinically identical)","Congenital or functional asplenia","Complement deficiency","Primary antibody deficiency","NEMO/IKBKG-related immunodeficiency","Severe congenital neutropenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic sequencing of MYD88","Functional Toll-like and interleukin-1 receptor stimulation assays demonstrating impaired cytokine production","Baseline immunoglobulins, complement and blood counts to exclude alternatives","Blood film for splenic-function markers such as Howell-Jolly bodies","Aggressive microbiological sampling and cultures during infective episodes","Site-directed imaging guided by the focus of infection"],"redFlags":["Invasive pyogenic infection with a disproportionately mild fever or acute-phase response","Recurrent invasive pneumococcal or staphylococcal disease in a young child","Clinical deterioration despite reassuring inflammatory markers"],"prognosis":"As with IRAK-4 deficiency, early childhood carries the highest mortality risk during invasive episodes, but prophylaxis, vaccination and prompt treatment substantially improve outcomes, with susceptibility decreasing over time.","pitfalls":["Confusing germline loss-of-function MyD88 deficiency (an immunodeficiency) with the somatic activating MYD88 mutations found in lymphomas such as Waldenstrom macroglobulinaemia, which are entirely different clinical entities.","Using fever or acute-phase markers to judge severity, which can be dangerously misleading.","Assuming that normal routine immunology excludes the disorder.","Withholding early empirical antibiotics pending confirmatory signs of inflammation."],"management":["Give long-term antibacterial prophylaxis against invasive pyogenic organisms, typically penicillin or co-trimoxazole, targeting Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Provide immunoglobulin replacement therapy to supplement humoral protection during the high-risk early childhood years","Vaccinate against encapsulated organisms, including pneumococcal, Haemophilus influenzae type b, and meningococcal vaccines","Treat suspected infection with prompt aggressive empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics at the earliest sign, since blunted fever and acute-phase responses mask severity","Educate the patient and family to seek urgent medical care with any infective symptoms, given the muted clinical picture","Undertake aggressive microbiological sampling, cultures, and site-directed imaging during infective episodes and monitor with a high index of suspicion","Offer genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance, and manage under an immunology specialist"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity","Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies","ESID clinical guidance on innate immune defects"],"crossLinks":[]},"myelinated_retinal_nerve_fibres":{"name":"myelinated retinal nerve fibres","system":"ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["medullated nerve fibres","myelinated nerve fibre layer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Appear as white or greyish-white patches with characteristic feathery, striated borders following the retinal nerve fibre bundle orientation.","Most commonly located at or contiguous with the optic disc margin, most often superiorly or inferiorly, and may obscure the underlying retinal vessels.","Represent aberrant myelination of retinal ganglion cell axons anterior to the lamina cribrosa, where myelin normally stops.","Usually an incidental, benign and non-progressive finding compatible with normal vision when isolated.","Extensive peripapillary myelination can produce a corresponding scotoma or enlarged blind spot on perimetry.","A recognised triad exists of unilateral extensive myelinated fibres, high axial myopia and dense amblyopia that responds poorly to treatment.","Rarely associated with neurofibromatosis and other neurocutaneous or developmental disorders.","Acquired myelinated fibres are uncommon but reported, sometimes following optic nerve or retinal insult."],"pathophysiology":"Myelination of the anterior visual pathway normally proceeds from the lateral geniculate body towards the eye and halts at the lamina cribrosa before birth, so that the retinal nerve fibre layer remains unmyelinated. Myelinated retinal nerve fibres result from oligodendrocytes, or oligodendrocyte-like cells, aberrantly present within the retina depositing myelin around ganglion cell axons anterior to the lamina cribrosa. The opaque myelin sheaths scatter light, producing the white feathery patches and obscuring the underlying vasculature. The myelin itself does not usually impair conduction, but dense peripapillary or macular involvement can create field defects, and when combined with high myopia it contributes to a deprivation-type amblyopia that is characteristically refractory.","additionalDifferentials":["Cotton wool spots","Retinal infiltrates or exudates","Chorioretinal scarring","Retinoblastoma or astrocytic hamartoma (in children)","Branch retinal artery occlusion with retinal whitening","Optic disc drusen with peripapillary changes","Combined hamartoma of the retina and RPE"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundoscopy and fundus photography documenting the feathery-edged patch","Optical coherence tomography showing hyper-reflective thickening of the nerve fibre layer","Automated perimetry to map any associated scotoma or enlarged blind spot","Cycloplegic refraction to detect associated high myopia","Amblyopia assessment in children, including visual acuity and fixation testing","Fundus autofluorescence or fluorescein angiography where the diagnosis is uncertain"],"redFlags":["Apparent progression or new appearance of myelination, which is atypical and warrants investigation","Associated dense amblyopia and high myopia in a child requiring prompt management","Features suggesting an alternative infiltrative or vascular lesion masquerading as myelin","Systemic signs suggesting neurofibromatosis or other syndromic association"],"prognosis":"Isolated myelinated retinal nerve fibres are benign and stable with an excellent visual prognosis. When part of the myopia–amblyopia triad, the visual outcome is poor and often resistant to conventional amblyopia therapy.","pitfalls":["Mistaking myelinated fibres for cotton wool spots or retinal infiltrates and pursuing an unnecessary systemic work-up.","In children, confusing extensive lesions with retinoblastoma or a retinal hamartoma.","Failing to detect and treat the associated high myopia and deprivation amblyopia early.","Assuming the amblyopia will respond fully to occlusion, leading to unrealistic expectations."],"management":["Reassure and observe when isolated, as the finding is benign, non-progressive and compatible with normal vision and needs no active treatment","Correct any associated high axial myopia with cycloplegic-refraction-guided spectacles or contact lenses","Treat associated amblyopia in children with full refractive correction and occlusion or pharmacological penalisation of the fellow eye, recognising the myopia-amblyopia triad often responds poorly","Perform baseline and periodic automated perimetry to document any scotoma or enlarged blind spot from dense peripapillary involvement","Monitor visual acuity, fixation and fundus appearance with serial fundus photography and optical coherence tomography","Refer children promptly to paediatric ophthalmology and orthoptics for amblyopia therapy and monitoring"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"myelodysplastic_syndrome":{"name":"Myelodysplastic syndrome","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MDS","myelodysplasia","myelodysplastic syndromes","preleukaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of clonal stem cell disorders defined by ineffective haematopoiesis, producing peripheral cytopenias despite a marrow that is usually normocellular or hypercellular","Typically affects older adults, often presenting with symptomatic anaemia; neutropenia and thrombocytopenia may also occur, sometimes with recurrent infection or bleeding","The paradox of low blood counts with a busy marrow reflects intramedullary apoptosis of dysplastic precursors","Diagnosis rests on morphological dysplasia in one or more lineages, blast percentage, and cytogenetic or molecular abnormalities; ring sideroblasts and specific mutations (e.g. SF3B1) carry prognostic meaning","Risk stratification with scoring systems that weight blast count, cytogenetics, and depth of cytopenias guides both prognosis and treatment intensity","Lower-risk disease is managed supportively with transfusions, erythropoiesis-stimulating agents, and iron chelation when transfusion burden is high; an isolated 5q deletion responds well to lenalidomide","Higher-risk disease is treated with hypomethylating agents, and allogeneic stem cell transplant offers the only potential cure for suitable patients","Progression to acute myeloid leukaemia is a defining risk, greatest in high-risk categories with excess blasts and adverse cytogenetics"],"pathophysiology":"MDS arises from an acquired mutation in a haematopoietic stem cell that gains a clonal growth advantage. Accumulating mutations in genes governing RNA splicing, epigenetic regulation, transcription, and signalling produce disordered maturation. Precursors are produced abundantly but undergo excessive apoptosis within the marrow, so mature cells are released ineffectively, giving cytopenias in the face of a cellular marrow. Over time the clone may acquire further mutations and expand, with rising blast counts marking transformation towards acute myeloid leukaemia. Prior chemotherapy or radiotherapy can cause therapy-related MDS, which typically carries adverse cytogenetics.","additionalDifferentials":["Aplastic anaemia (especially hypoplastic MDS)","Vitamin B12 or folate deficiency causing megaloblastic dysplasia","Acute myeloid leukaemia","Myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative overlap syndromes (e.g. CMML)","Reactive dysplasia from alcohol, copper deficiency, or drugs","Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count, reticulocytes, and careful blood film review for dysplastic changes","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine with blast count and assessment of dysplasia and ring sideroblasts","Cytogenetic analysis and targeted molecular/next-generation sequencing panels","Serum erythropoietin level to predict response to erythropoiesis-stimulating agents","B12, folate, and copper levels to exclude reversible mimics","Flow cytometry and PNH clone testing when the picture overlaps with marrow failure"],"redFlags":["Rising blast percentage or new adverse cytogenetics signalling transformation to acute leukaemia","Severe neutropenia with fever requiring urgent treatment for sepsis","Profound thrombocytopenia with clinically significant bleeding","Rapidly worsening pancytopenia demanding urgent haematology review and transplant assessment"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely by risk category: lower-risk patients may live many years with supportive care, whereas high-risk disease carries short survival and a substantial chance of progression to acute myeloid leukaemia. Allogeneic transplant is the only curative option but is limited by age and comorbidity.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing MDS without excluding reversible causes of dysplasia such as B12 or folate deficiency, copper deficiency, alcohol, or recent chemotherapy","Failing to obtain cytogenetics and molecular testing, which are central to risk stratification","Undertreating iron overload in chronically transfused patients","Overlooking an isolated 5q deletion that has a favourable, targeted treatment","Missing the transition to acute leukaemia by not repeating marrow assessment when counts deteriorate"],"management":["Risk-stratify with a validated prognostic scoring system that weights blast count, cytogenetics and depth of cytopenias to guide treatment intensity","Support lower-risk disease with red-cell transfusions and erythropoiesis-stimulating agents to improve symptomatic anaemia","Use lenalidomide for transfusion-dependent disease with an isolated 5q deletion, which responds well","Offer hypomethylating agents such as azacitidine or decitabine for higher-risk disease unsuitable for transplantation","Proceed to allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the only curative option in eligible higher-risk patients","Add iron chelation therapy where a high transfusion burden causes iron overload","Treat neutropenic infection promptly with antimicrobials and give platelet transfusions for bleeding or severe thrombocytopenia","Monitor blood counts and repeat marrow assessment to detect progression towards acute myeloid leukaemia, under haematology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"myeloma_cast_nephropathy":{"name":"Myeloma cast nephropathy","system":"Renal / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cast nephropathy","myeloma kidney","light chain cast nephropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Brenner and Rector's The Kidney","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest cause of acute kidney injury in multiple myeloma, driven by high circulating free light chains","Filtered monoclonal free light chains bind Tamm-Horsfall protein and precipitate as obstructing casts in the distal tubules","Biopsy shows dense, fractured, angulated tubular casts surrounded by a cellular reaction, often with giant cells and tubular injury","Presentation is typically acute or subacute kidney injury, sometimes precipitated by dehydration, hypercalcaemia, infection, contrast or nephrotoxic drugs","Urine dipstick underestimates the proteinuria because it detects albumin rather than the predominant light-chain (Bence Jones) protein","Diagnosis rests on serum free light-chain assay, serum and urine electrophoresis with immunofixation, and confirmation of the plasma-cell clone","Management combines rapid reduction of the pathogenic light chain by clone-directed therapy for the paraprotein-related disease with correction of precipitating factors and supportive care"],"pathophysiology":"In multiple myeloma a plasma-cell clone secretes large amounts of monoclonal free light chains that are filtered by the glomerulus and delivered to the distal nephron. There they co-aggregate with Tamm-Horsfall (uromodulin) protein secreted by the thick ascending limb, forming dense, waxy, often fractured casts that obstruct tubular flow and incite an inflammatory and giant-cell reaction with tubular epithelial injury. Obstruction and back-pressure reduce glomerular filtration, producing acute kidney injury, while direct proximal tubular toxicity of light chains adds further damage. Volume depletion, hypercalcaemia, loop diuretics, contrast and nephrotoxins promote cast formation by concentrating light chains and reducing flow, explaining common precipitants.","additionalDifferentials":["Light chain deposition disease","Renal amyloidosis","Hypercalcaemic nephropathy","Acute tubular injury from other causes","Contrast-associated acute kidney injury","Proximal (Fanconi) tubulopathy from light chains"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum free light-chain assay with the kappa-to-lambda ratio","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation","Bone marrow examination to confirm the plasma-cell clone","Serum calcium, renal function and full blood count","Renal biopsy where the diagnosis or reversibility is uncertain","Imaging for lytic bone lesions and assessment of infection or dehydration"],"redFlags":["Severe or dialysis-requiring acute kidney injury","Hypercalcaemic crisis compounding renal failure","Very high free light-chain burden","Concurrent infection, sepsis or hyperviscosity"],"prognosis":"Renal recovery depends on the speed and depth of light-chain reduction and on the degree of established tubular and interstitial damage at diagnosis; early, effective clone-directed therapy offers the best chance of renal recovery and improved survival. Extensive cast formation with fibrosis and a delayed light-chain response predict dialysis dependence.","pitfalls":["Relying on urine dipstick, which misses Bence Jones proteinuria","Missing precipitants such as dehydration, hypercalcaemia, contrast and loop diuretics","Delaying clone-directed therapy while awaiting biopsy in clear-cut cases","Confusing cast nephropathy with light chain deposition disease or amyloidosis","Giving nephrotoxic drugs that worsen cast formation"],"management":["Start clone-directed anti-myeloma therapy urgently, typically a bortezomib-based regimen with dexamethasone, to reduce circulating free light chains rapidly","Correct volume depletion with intravenous fluids to increase distal tubular flow and dilute light chains, limiting cast formation","Treat coexisting hypercalcaemia with hydration and a bisphosphonate","Withdraw nephrotoxins and avoid iodinated contrast, loop diuretics and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs that promote cast precipitation","Provide renal replacement therapy for refractory acute kidney injury and uraemic complications","Monitor serum free light chains and renal function to gauge depth of response and prospects for renal recovery","Manage jointly with haematology and nephrology, and consider renal biopsy where the diagnosis or reversibility is uncertain"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Brenner and Rector's The Kidney"],"crossLinks":[]},"myeloperoxidase_deficiency":{"name":"myeloperoxidase deficiency","system":"Immune system / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPO deficiency","hereditary myeloperoxidase deficiency","neutrophil peroxidase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies / Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest inherited neutrophil enzyme defect, yet most affected individuals are clinically asymptomatic because alternative oxygen-dependent and oxygen-independent killing mechanisms compensate","Caused by variants in the MPO gene and inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, producing either complete or partial enzyme deficiency","Myeloperoxidase generates hypochlorous acid and other reactive halogen species from hydrogen peroxide and chloride within the phagolysosome, a potent microbicidal system","The defect is frequently discovered incidentally when peroxidase-based automated haematology analysers flag absent neutrophil and eosinophil peroxidase activity","Clinically significant infection is uncommon, but there is a recognised predisposition to invasive candidiasis, including Candida sepsis, particularly when diabetes mellitus coexists","The NADPH oxidase respiratory burst is intact, which distinguishes myeloperoxidase deficiency from chronic granulomatous disease on nitroblue tetrazolium or dihydrorhodamine testing","Most individuals need no specific treatment, so management focuses on prompt recognition and treatment of fungal infection in those at risk"],"pathophysiology":"Myeloperoxidase is a haem-containing enzyme stored in the azurophilic (primary) granules of neutrophils and in monocyte lysosomes. After phagocytosis it is discharged into the phagosome, where it uses hydrogen peroxide generated by the NADPH oxidase respiratory burst together with chloride to form hypochlorous acid and related reactive species that kill ingested microbes. Inherited deficiency removes this halogenation system, but killing mediated by hydrogen peroxide itself, defensins and other granule constituents remains largely intact, which explains the generally mild phenotype. Killing of fungi, particularly Candida hyphae, is more dependent on the myeloperoxidase system, accounting for the specific vulnerability to invasive candidiasis, an effect amplified by hyperglycaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic granulomatous disease","Specific granule deficiency","Severe glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency with granulocyte dysfunction","Diabetes-associated neutrophil dysfunction","Other causes of chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis","Acquired myeloperoxidase deficiency due to myelodysplasia, drugs or heavy-metal exposure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peroxidase cytochemical stain on the blood film showing absent neutrophil activity","Automated analyser peroxidase channel demonstrating absent granulocyte peroxidase","Dihydrorhodamine flow cytometry or nitroblue tetrazolium test, normal results excluding chronic granulomatous disease","MPO gene sequencing","Fasting glucose and glycated haemoglobin to detect coexisting diabetes","Fungal cultures and imaging when invasive candidiasis is suspected","Neutrophil bactericidal and functional assays if the phenotype is atypical"],"redFlags":["Invasive or disseminated candidiasis, especially in a person with diabetes","Deep fungal infection or Candida bloodstream infection","Severe or recurrent infection suggesting an additional or alternative immune defect","Unexplained absence of neutrophil peroxidase in the context of active infection"],"prognosis":"The great majority of individuals have a normal life expectancy with no excess infection burden, so the overall prognosis is excellent; clinically important risk is largely confined to invasive fungal infection in those with concurrent diabetes or additional immune compromise.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting an incidental analyser flag as myelodysplasia or a laboratory artefact","Confusing myeloperoxidase deficiency with chronic granulomatous disease despite the normal respiratory burst","Assuming that every inherited neutrophil defect is clinically significant","Overlooking coexisting diabetes that heightens candidal risk","Failing to consider acquired myeloperoxidase deficiency in the setting of myelodysplasia"],"management":["Reassure asymptomatic individuals, as intact hydrogen-peroxide, defensin and NADPH-oxidase-dependent killing compensates and no specific treatment is required","Optimise glycaemic control in coexisting diabetes mellitus, since hyperglycaemia amplifies the predisposition to invasive candidiasis","Maintain a high index of suspicion for invasive candidiasis and treat promptly with antifungal agents such as azoles or echinocandins","Obtain fungal cultures and imaging when systemic or invasive candidal infection is suspected","Distinguish from chronic granulomatous disease with a dihydrorhodamine or nitroblue tetrazolium test to avoid unnecessary antimicrobial prophylaxis","Refer to clinical immunology when infections are recurrent or additional immune compromise coexists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies","Williams Hematology"],"crossLinks":["specific_granule_deficiency"]},"myh9_related_disorder":{"name":"MYH9-related disorder","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MYH9-related disease","May-Hegglin anomaly","Sebastian syndrome","Fechtner syndrome","Epstein syndrome","MYH9-RD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / Nathan and Oski's Hematology of Infancy and Childhood","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant disorder caused by MYH9 mutations affecting non-muscle myosin heavy chain IIA, presenting with macrothrombocytopenia","The blood film shows large or giant platelets together with pale blue Dohle-like cytoplasmic inclusions in neutrophils","The historical eponyms (May-Hegglin, Sebastian, Fechtner, Epstein) are now recognised as one disease spectrum defined by the MYH9 gene","Extra-haematological features may include progressive sensorineural deafness, presenile cataract, and nephropathy that can advance to renal failure","The thrombocytopenia is often mild and the bleeding tendency modest, so the low platelet count is frequently discovered incidentally","Automated counters may undercount the giant platelets and overstate the severity, so film review is essential","Diagnosis combines macrothrombocytopenia with leucocyte inclusions on immunofluorescence for myosin IIA and MYH9 sequencing"],"pathophysiology":"MYH9-related disorder results from mutations in MYH9, which encodes the heavy chain of non-muscle myosin IIA, a cytoskeletal motor protein expressed in platelets, leucocytes, cochlea, lens, and kidney. In megakaryocytes, defective myosin IIA disrupts the cytoskeletal remodelling required for orderly proplatelet formation, so platelets are released as fewer, abnormally large cells, producing macrothrombocytopenia. In neutrophils, the mutant protein aggregates into cytoplasmic inclusions visible as Dohle-like bodies. The same protein is essential in other tissues, so its dysfunction can cause sensorineural hearing loss, cataract, and a glomerular nephropathy, with the pattern and severity of extra-haematological involvement varying by the specific mutation.","additionalDifferentials":["Immune thrombocytopenia (ITP)","Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Gray platelet syndrome","Other inherited macrothrombocytopenias (for example TUBB1, ACTN1)","Type 2B von Willebrand disease","Alport syndrome (for the renal and auditory features)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood film review for giant platelets and neutrophil Dohle-like inclusions","Immunofluorescence staining for myosin IIA distribution in neutrophils","MYH9 sequencing to confirm the diagnosis and correlate genotype","Audiometry to detect and monitor sensorineural hearing loss","Urinalysis and renal function tests to screen for nephropathy","Ophthalmological assessment for cataract"],"redFlags":["Progressive proteinuria or declining renal function heralding nephropathy","Rapidly progressive hearing loss requiring audiological intervention","Significant bleeding disproportionate to the platelet count, prompting review of the diagnosis","Unnecessary immunosuppression or splenectomy after misdiagnosis as immune thrombocytopenia"],"prognosis":"The haematological course is usually benign with only a modest bleeding tendency, but long-term prognosis is shaped by extra-haematological complications, particularly progressive nephropathy and hearing loss in susceptible genotypes.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the condition as immune thrombocytopenia and giving steroids, immunoglobulin, or splenectomy","Relying on automated platelet counts that miss giant platelets","Failing to screen for and monitor renal, auditory, and ocular involvement","Overtreating a mild, incidentally found thrombocytopenia"],"management":["Recognise the inherited macrothrombocytopenia to avoid misdiagnosis as immune thrombocytopenia and inappropriate corticosteroids, immunosuppression or splenectomy","Cover bleeding and surgical or peripartum haemostasis with platelet transfusions and an antifibrinolytic such as tranexamic acid; a thrombopoietin-receptor agonist can raise counts before procedures","Monitor renal function and urinalysis for proteinuria, treating emerging nephropathy with renin-angiotensin system blockade","Perform serial audiometry and provide hearing aids or cochlear implantation for progressive sensorineural deafness","Arrange ophthalmological review with cataract surgery when visually significant","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant MYH9 disorder and testing of at-risk relatives","Refer to nephrology as chronic kidney disease advances, with consideration of dialysis or renal transplantation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"myhre_syndrome":{"name":"Myhre syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SMAD4","Myhre growth-mental deficiency syndrome","LAPS syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by specific recurrent gain-of-function variants in SMAD4, a central mediator of TGF-beta/BMP signalling","Characterised by progressive, often striking fibrosis affecting skin, joints, serosal surfaces and the cardiovascular and respiratory systems","Thick, stiff skin and progressive joint stiffness with muscular-appearing build are typical, alongside short stature and short hands","Hearing loss (conductive, sensorineural or mixed) is common and warrants audiological monitoring","A distinctive facial appearance includes a short palpebral fissures, maxillary hypoplasia, a small mouth and prognathism that evolves with age","Fibrosis can be dangerously exuberant after surgery or catheterisation, so procedures carry heightened risk of restrictive complications and pericardial or airway scarring","Cardiovascular involvement (pericardial disease, hypertension, congenital heart defects) and airway stenosis are important sources of morbidity"],"pathophysiology":"Myhre syndrome results from recurrent heterozygous variants in SMAD4 that stabilise the protein and enhance transforming growth factor beta and bone morphogenetic protein signalling. The persistently amplified signalling drives excessive deposition of extracellular matrix and progressive fibrosis across many tissues, explaining the thickened skin, joint stiffness, serosal and pericardial scarring, and airway and vascular narrowing. Because the underlying tendency is to fibrose, tissue injury from surgery or instrumentation can trigger disproportionate scarring, a key consideration in management.","additionalDifferentials":["Geleophysic dysplasia","Acromicric dysplasia","Scleroderma and other fibrosing disorders","Stiff skin syndrome","Mucopolysaccharidoses","Other acromelic dysplasias with joint stiffness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted SMAD4 analysis for the recurrent pathogenic variants","Echocardiography and cardiac imaging for pericardial disease and congenital defects","Airway and respiratory assessment for stenosis or restrictive disease","Audiological evaluation for hearing loss","Skeletal survey for short stature and hand changes","Blood pressure monitoring for hypertension"],"redFlags":["Pericardial constriction or effusion causing haemodynamic compromise","Progressive airway stenosis or restrictive respiratory failure","Exuberant fibrotic response following surgery or cardiac catheterisation","Uncontrolled hypertension or evolving cardiovascular disease"],"prognosis":"The condition is progressive and can be life-limiting, with cardiovascular and respiratory fibrosis being the principal threats; outcomes are worsened by the exaggerated scarring that may follow interventions, so a cautious, multidisciplinary approach is essential.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to elective surgery or catheterisation without anticipating the exaggerated fibrotic response","Attributing progressive stiffness to a primary rheumatological disease rather than the underlying syndrome","Overlooking pericardial disease until constriction develops","Missing hearing loss and its impact on development"],"management":["Minimise elective surgery, catheterisation and instrumentation wherever possible, as tissue injury triggers exuberant fibrosis, and plan any unavoidable procedure meticulously","Monitor cardiovascular status with serial echocardiography and cardiac imaging for pericardial thickening or constriction and valvular or vascular stenosis","Screen and manage the airway and respiratory system for stenosis and restrictive fibrotic disease","Provide audiological monitoring with hearing aids for conductive, sensorineural or mixed hearing loss","Control hypertension with pharmacotherapy and monitor blood pressure regularly","Support growth, joint stiffness and function with physiotherapy and orthopaedic input","Offer genetic counselling regarding the recurrent gain-of-function SMAD4 variant","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary team given the multi-organ fibrotic burden"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Myhre Syndrome","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"myiasis":{"name":"Myiasis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fly larval infestation","maggot infestation","Dermatobia hominis (human botfly)","Cordylobia anthropophaga (tumbu fly)","Cochliomyia hominivorax (screwworm)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Myiasis is infestation of living tissue by dipterous (fly) larvae; clinical patterns include furuncular (boil-like), wound (traumatic), migratory (creeping), and cavity/body-orifice forms","Furuncular myiasis is classically caused by the human botfly (Dermatobia hominis) in the Americas and the tumbu fly (Cordylobia anthropophaga) in sub-Saharan Africa","Dermatobia eggs are carried to skin by biting insects (phoresis), whereas tumbu fly eggs are laid on soil or drying laundry and larvae penetrate contact skin","The typical furuncular lesion is a painful nodule with a central punctum that intermittently discharges serosanguineous fluid; patients often describe movement or a sensation of crawling","A central breathing pore that bubbles, and sometimes a visible larval tip, help confirm the diagnosis; occlusion can bring the larva to the surface","Wound and cavity myiasis (e.g. screwworm) can be aggressively tissue-destructive and occurs in neglected wounds, orifices or debilitated patients","Treatment is removal of the larva (occlusion and extraction or minor surgical removal), wound care, and tetanus/antibiotic cover for secondary infection where indicated"],"pathophysiology":"Fly larvae invade host tissue by species-specific routes: some deposit larvae directly, some lay eggs transferred by biting arthropods, and others deposit eggs on soil or clothing from which larvae penetrate intact or broken skin. The larva develops within the dermis or a wound, feeding on tissue and exudate while maintaining a respiratory opening at the skin surface. Local host inflammation produces a furuncle-like nodule in obligate skin-dwelling species, whereas facultative or obligate wound feeders such as screwworm can burrow and destroy tissue extensively. Secondary bacterial infection frequently complicates the lesion.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial furuncle or abscess","Tungiasis","Infected epidermoid cyst","Cutaneous larva migrans (for migratory forms)","Retained foreign body with granuloma","Cellulitis of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination with travel/exposure history and inspection of the central punctum","Occlusion test to observe the larval breathing movements","Dermoscopy or ultrasound to visualise the embedded larva in furuncular myiasis","Identification of the extracted larva to species where relevant","Wound swab and culture if secondary bacterial infection is suspected","Assessment of tetanus immunisation status"],"redFlags":["Aggressive wound or cavity (e.g. nasal, orbital, aural) myiasis with rapid tissue destruction","Screwworm infestation requiring prompt eradication and public health notification","Secondary cellulitis, abscess or signs of systemic sepsis","Involvement of the eye, sinuses or central structures threatening deep spread","Infestation in a neglected, debilitated or cognitively impaired patient"],"prognosis":"Furuncular myiasis is generally benign and resolves completely once the larva is removed, whereas wound and cavity myiasis, especially screwworm, can cause serious tissue destruction and requires urgent intervention; secondary infection is the main complication.","pitfalls":["Rupturing or fragmenting the larva during extraction, leaving parts that provoke a foreign-body reaction","Misdiagnosing furuncular myiasis as a simple boil and incising without recognising the larva","Underestimating aggressive wound or cavity myiasis and its capacity for deep destruction","Neglecting tetanus prophylaxis and secondary infection management","Failing to notify authorities for obligate parasites such as screwworm where reportable"],"management":["Remove the larva completely: occlude the central breathing punctum to force it towards the surface, then extract it by manual expression or minor surgical removal","Debride devitalised tissue and remove all larvae surgically in wound and cavity forms, with urgent thorough intervention for tissue-destructive screwworm infestation","Treat or prevent secondary bacterial infection with antibiotics and thorough wound cleaning","Ensure tetanus immunisation is up to date","Consider systemic or topical ivermectin to aid larval killing in extensive, orbital or cavity myiasis","Advise prevention through insect-bite avoidance and ironing clothing or linen dried outdoors in tumbu-fly regions","Refer to surgery, ENT or ophthalmology for orbital, nasal or aggressive cavity involvement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"myoadenylate_deaminase_deficiency":{"name":"Myoadenylate deaminase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Skeletal genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AMPD1 deficiency","Muscle AMP deaminase deficiency","MADD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic variants in AMPD1 encoding the muscle-specific isoform of AMP deaminase, the enzyme that deaminates adenosine monophosphate to inosine monophosphate within the purine nucleotide cycle during exercise.","The commonest inherited defect of the purine nucleotide cycle in skeletal muscle; a single recurrent nonsense variant accounts for the great majority of pathogenic alleles in many populations.","Presents as exercise-induced myalgia, early fatigue and cramping, sometimes with exertional rhabdomyolysis and myoglobinuria; many homozygotes remain wholly asymptomatic, so the biochemical defect and clinical disease are often dissociated.","Distinguish the primary (inherited) form from acquired secondary deficiency, in which reduced enzyme activity is a bystander finding alongside another established neuromuscular disorder such as an inflammatory or dystrophic myopathy.","The ischaemic (or non-ischaemic) forearm exercise test characteristically shows a normal lactate rise but a blunted or absent rise in ammonia, the mirror image of the flat lactate seen in glycogenoses.","Resting serum creatine kinase may be normal or only mildly elevated between attacks, so a normal value does not exclude the diagnosis.","Confirm with muscle biopsy histochemical staining for AMP deaminase activity or biochemical assay, and with AMPD1 gene testing; management is supportive with graded activity, avoidance of exhaustive exertion and attention to hydration around exercise.","Anecdotal reports describe symptomatic benefit from oral ribose supplementation, but evidence is limited and it is not established therapy."],"pathophysiology":"Myoadenylate deaminase deficiency results from loss-of-function variants in AMPD1 on chromosome 1p13, which encodes the muscle-specific (isoform M) subunit of adenosine monophosphate deaminase. Within contracting skeletal muscle this enzyme drives the purine nucleotide cycle by deaminating AMP to inosine monophosphate with release of ammonia, a reaction that helps maintain a favourable adenylate energy charge by removing AMP and displacing the adenylate kinase equilibrium towards adenosine triphosphate regeneration, while also feeding the tricarboxylic acid cycle through anaplerotic replenishment of fumarate. When muscle AMP deaminase activity is absent, vigorous or sustained exercise leads to inefficient regeneration of high-energy phosphates and excessive loss of adenine nucleotides, manifesting as premature fatigue, cramping and, in some individuals, myofibre breakdown. A single recurrent premature stop variant is the predominant disease allele; the marked variability in whether homozygotes develop symptoms is thought to reflect residual or compensatory ammonia-generating pathways and modifier effects, explaining why the enzyme defect is frequently detected incidentally.","additionalDifferentials":["McArdle disease (myophosphorylase deficiency) and other glycogen-storage myopathies, in which the forearm test shows a flat lactate but a normal ammonia rise","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency and other fatty-acid oxidation defects causing exertional rhabdomyolysis","Mitochondrial myopathy with exercise intolerance","Secondary (acquired) AMP deaminase deficiency accompanying an inflammatory or dystrophic myopathy","Recurrent exertional rhabdomyolysis due to RYR1-related or malignant-hyperthermia-susceptible myopathy","Benign exertional cramp syndromes and simple deconditioning"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ischaemic or non-ischaemic forearm exercise test measuring paired venous lactate and ammonia responses","Muscle biopsy with histochemical staining for AMP deaminase activity, plus routine histology to exclude another myopathy","Biochemical assay of AMP deaminase activity in muscle tissue","Targeted AMPD1 sequencing, including testing for the common recurrent variant","Serum creatine kinase at rest and after exertion, with urine myoglobin during acute episodes","Broader metabolic myopathy panel (acylcarnitines, other enzyme assays) when the phenotype is atypical"],"redFlags":["Pigmented (tea-coloured) urine after exertion indicating myoglobinuria and possible rhabdomyolysis","Marked post-exercise weakness with grossly elevated creatine kinase suggesting significant muscle breakdown","Features of acute kidney injury complicating a rhabdomyolysis episode","Rapidly progressive fixed weakness, which points away from this disorder towards an alternative myopathy"],"prognosis":"Overall prognosis is good and life expectancy is normal, with many enzyme-deficient individuals remaining asymptomatic throughout life. Symptomatic patients typically experience exercise-limited myalgia and occasional rhabdomyolysis that are managed conservatively, though severe exertional episodes rarely carry the usual complications of myoglobinuric acute kidney injury.","pitfalls":["Interpreting an incidentally low muscle enzyme activity as the cause of symptoms without excluding a coexisting primary myopathy (the secondary/acquired form).","Excluding the diagnosis on the basis of a normal resting creatine kinase, which is common between episodes.","Misattributing a flat ammonia response to a technically inadequate forearm test; venous sampling and effort must be validated by the paired lactate rise.","Assuming that homozygosity for the common variant guarantees clinical disease, given the frequent asymptomatic state.","Overlooking hydration and rhabdomyolysis risk when advising patients about strenuous or competitive exercise."],"management":["Reassure, as the primary defect is frequently asymptomatic and carries a normal life expectancy","Advise graded, paced exercise with adequate warm-up and avoidance of sudden maximal or sustained exertion to limit exercise-induced myalgia and cramp","Ensure good hydration and consider carbohydrate intake before exertion to support high-energy phosphate regeneration","Recognise and manage exertional rhabdomyolysis promptly with intravenous fluids to protect against acute kidney injury","Exclude a coexisting inflammatory or dystrophic myopathy before attributing symptoms to a secondary bystander enzyme deficiency","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive AMPD1 defect"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of the metabolic and molecular bases of inherited disease","Reference textbook of clinical neuromuscular disorders and myopathies","Peer-reviewed reviews of purine metabolism disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"myocardial_bridging":{"name":"Myocardial bridging","system":"Cardiovascular / Congenital","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["myocardial bridge","coronary muscle bridge","intramural coronary artery","tunnelled coronary artery"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A segment of an epicardial coronary artery, usually the mid left anterior descending, dips into the myocardium and is compressed during systole","Angiography shows systolic narrowing that relaxes in diastole, the milking effect, since most coronary perfusion occurs in diastole","The overwhelming majority are benign incidental findings with a favourable natural history","Symptomatic cases may report exertional chest pain, and rarely arrhythmia or ischaemic events are attributed to deep or long bridges","Diastolic flow disturbance and a persistent early-diastolic delay in relaxation can occasionally impair perfusion","Tachycardia shortens diastole and can unmask or worsen symptoms by reducing perfusion time","Most patients need reassurance only; medical therapy that reduces contractility and heart rate is first-line, with intervention reserved for refractory, well-documented ischaemia"],"pathophysiology":"Myocardial bridging occurs when a portion of an epicardial coronary artery, most often the mid left anterior descending, takes an intramuscular course so that overlying myocardium compresses it during systole. Because coronary perfusion is predominantly diastolic, the systolic compression is usually of little consequence, which explains why most bridges are benign. In deeper or longer bridges the compression can persist into early diastole and a phase lag in vessel re-expansion may transiently reduce flow, and tachycardia further curtails diastolic filling time. Local wall stress at the bridge entrance may also predispose the proximal segment to atherosclerosis. The net functional effect ranges from none in the great majority to occasional demand ischaemia in a minority.","additionalDifferentials":["Obstructive coronary atherosclerosis","Coronary vasospasm","Anomalous coronary artery origin or course","Microvascular angina","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with demand ischaemia","Non-cardiac chest pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Coronary angiography demonstrating systolic compression with diastolic relaxation","Coronary CT angiography showing the intramyocardial segment","Intravascular imaging or physiological indices to assess functional significance in selected cases","Stress testing or perfusion imaging to document inducible ischaemia","Echocardiography to exclude hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Electrocardiogram during symptoms"],"redFlags":["Documented exertional ischaemia attributable to a deep or long bridge","Ventricular arrhythmia associated with the bridged segment","Persistent symptoms despite optimal rate and contractility control","Ischaemic event in a young patient without atherosclerotic risk factors"],"prognosis":"The natural history is generally benign with normal life expectancy in the majority; adverse events are rare and largely confined to deep, long bridges or those with proven ischaemia.","pitfalls":["Overtreating an incidental bridge and attributing unrelated chest pain to it","Using nitrates, which can worsen systolic compression and symptoms","Failing to control heart rate, allowing tachycardia to shorten diastolic perfusion time","Ignoring coexisting fixed atherosclerotic disease proximal to the bridge"],"management":["Reassure and observe the majority, who have a benign incidental bridge requiring no treatment","Use negative chronotropic and inotropic agents, beta-blockers or non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers, as first-line therapy for symptomatic ischaemia to prolong diastolic perfusion time","Avoid nitrates and pure vasodilators, which can accentuate systolic compression and worsen symptoms","Address atherosclerotic risk factors, as the segment proximal to the bridge is prone to plaque formation","Reserve surgical myotomy (unroofing) or coronary bypass for refractory symptoms with documented ischaemia","Investigate persistent symptoms with stress or perfusion imaging and intracoronary physiological indices to confirm functional significance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"myoclonus":{"name":"Myoclonus","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["myoclonic jerks","myoclonic movements"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as sudden, brief, shock-like involuntary movements, which may be positive (muscle contraction) or negative (a lapse of tone, as in asterixis)","Classified clinically by distribution (focal, segmental, multifocal, or generalised) and by trigger, such as action or stimulus-sensitive myoclonus","Aetiologically grouped into physiological, essential, epileptic, and symptomatic (secondary) forms, the last being the most common in clinical practice","A crucial step is to identify secondary causes, particularly metabolic and toxic encephalopathies, since these are often reversible","Asterixis (negative myoclonus) is a classic sign of hepatic, renal, and other metabolic encephalopathies","Neurophysiological localisation into cortical, subcortical, spinal, or peripheral origin helps guide investigation and treatment","Post-hypoxic action myoclonus (Lance-Adams syndrome) can follow cardiac arrest and cerebral hypoxia","Treatment principles combine correcting any underlying cause with symptomatic antimyoclonic agents chosen according to the presumed generator"],"pathophysiology":"Myoclonus results from abnormal, excessive neuronal discharge that can arise at several levels of the nervous system. Cortical myoclonus reflects hyperexcitability of the sensorimotor cortex and often coexists with epilepsy; subcortical and brainstem forms arise from deeper generators; and spinal and peripheral forms originate in the cord or nerve. Loss of normal inhibitory control, frequently involving impaired GABAergic and serotonergic mechanisms, allows brief synchronous muscle discharges. Underlying processes include metabolic derangement, drugs and toxins, neurodegeneration, hypoxia, and epilepsy.","additionalDifferentials":["Tremor","Tics","Chorea","Seizures and epileptic phenomena","Dystonic jerks and startle responses","Fasciculations and functional jerks"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Metabolic screen including renal, hepatic, glucose, electrolyte, and calcium studies to identify reversible causes","Review of medications and toxins that can provoke myoclonus","EEG, including back-averaging with EMG, to identify a cortical origin and epileptic activity","MRI of the brain and, where relevant, the spinal cord to seek structural or degenerative causes","Targeted testing for infective, autoimmune, paraneoplastic, or metabolic-genetic disorders when indicated"],"redFlags":["Myoclonus with declining consciousness suggesting a serious metabolic, toxic, or hypoxic encephalopathy","Rapidly progressive myoclonus with cognitive decline raising concern for prion disease or an autoimmune encephalitis","Myoclonus following cardiac arrest, which may indicate severe hypoxic brain injury","Myoclonus as part of a status myoclonicus or in the context of status epilepticus requiring urgent treatment"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is dictated by the underlying cause. Myoclonus due to reversible metabolic or drug causes typically resolves once the trigger is corrected, whereas myoclonus from neurodegenerative, prion, or severe hypoxic injury carries a poor outlook. Symptomatic treatment can meaningfully reduce disability in many chronic cases even when the cause cannot be cured.","pitfalls":["Failing to search for and correct a reversible metabolic or drug-induced cause","Misclassifying myoclonus as tremor, tics, or seizures and mismanaging it","Overlooking a serious progressive cause such as prion disease or autoimmune encephalitis","Not localising the myoclonus neurophysiologically, leading to poorly targeted therapy"],"management":["Identify and correct the underlying cause, especially reversible metabolic derangements and offending drugs or toxins","Localise the generator neurophysiologically as cortical, subcortical, spinal or peripheral to guide drug selection","Treat cortical myoclonus with antiseizure agents such as levetiracetam, sodium valproate or clonazepam","Manage myoclonus of other origins with clonazepam alongside cause-directed therapy","Manage acute severe myoclonus, including post-hypoxic status myoclonus, with escalating antiseizure and anaesthetic therapy and supportive care","Investigate for structural, autoimmune, paraneoplastic or neurodegenerative causes and refer to neurology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"myoclonus_dystonia":{"name":"Myoclonus-dystonia","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["myoclonus-dystonia syndrome","DYT11","SGCE","epsilon-sarcoglycan deficiency","M-D","alcohol-responsive myoclonic dystonia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Movement-disorder textbooks with peer-reviewed reviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most commonly caused by variants in SGCE (epsilon-sarcoglycan), inherited autosomal dominantly with maternal imprinting, so penetrance depends on parent of origin (usually manifest only when inherited from the father)","Core phenotype combines lightning-like myoclonic jerks, predominantly of the upper body and arms, with milder dystonia (often cervical dystonia or writer's cramp)","Onset is typically in childhood or adolescence, and the myoclonus is usually the more disabling feature","A characteristic and well-recognised feature is marked, if temporary, improvement of myoclonus with alcohol, which can lead to alcohol misuse","Psychiatric comorbidity is common, including anxiety, obsessive-compulsive features, and depression","The disorder is non-degenerative with normal cognition and structural imaging, and course is generally benign and non-progressive","Symptomatic treatment may target myoclonus and dystonia; deep brain stimulation is an option in severely affected, refractory cases","Diagnosis is supported by the clinical picture and family history and confirmed by SGCE molecular testing, keeping imprinting in mind when interpreting inheritance"],"pathophysiology":"Myoclonus-dystonia is most often caused by loss-of-function variants in SGCE, encoding epsilon-sarcoglycan, a transmembrane protein widely expressed in neurons including cerebellar Purkinje cells and monoaminergic neurons. SGCE is maternally imprinted, so only the paternally inherited allele is normally expressed, which explains why the disorder typically manifests when the variant is inherited from the father. Deficiency of epsilon-sarcoglycan is thought to disrupt cerebellothalamocortical and striatal network function, producing subcortical myoclonus and dystonia without neurodegeneration, consistent with the normal cognition and imaging.","additionalDifferentials":["Essential tremor and other tremor syndromes","Primary (isolated) dystonias such as DYT1","Progressive myoclonic epilepsies and other myoclonus syndromes","Tic disorders including Tourette syndrome","Functional (psychogenic) jerks","Cortical myoclonus from other neurological disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SGCE molecular genetic testing, interpreted with attention to maternal imprinting","Neurophysiological studies (EMG with back-averaging) to characterise the myoclonus as subcortical","EEG to distinguish myoclonic epilepsy","Brain MRI, expected to be normal, to exclude structural or degenerative causes","Serum copper and caeruloplasmin to exclude Wilson disease when the picture is atypical"],"redFlags":["Development of alcohol dependence driven by self-medication of myoclonus","Severe psychiatric symptoms including suicidal ideation","Atypical progression, cognitive decline, or additional neurological signs suggesting another diagnosis","Disabling myoclonus or dystonia refractory to medical therapy"],"prognosis":"The condition is generally benign and non-progressive, compatible with a normal lifespan and preserved cognition, though myoclonus and dystonia can cause functional and social disability and psychiatric comorbidity may be significant. Selected refractory patients benefit from deep brain stimulation.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the inheritance by ignoring maternal imprinting and wrongly excluding the diagnosis in an unaffected carrier parent","Overlooking or inadvertently reinforcing alcohol misuse arising from symptom self-treatment","Labelling the jerks as tics or a functional disorder without neurophysiology or genetic testing","Failing to address the frequently associated psychiatric comorbidity"],"management":["Treat disabling myoclonus with clonazepam or other benzodiazepines, considering anticholinergics or sodium valproate as adjuncts","Manage focal dystonia such as cervical dystonia or writer's cramp with botulinum toxin injections","Offer pallidal deep brain stimulation for medically refractory, disabling myoclonus-dystonia","Counsel against using alcohol for symptom relief and screen for and manage alcohol misuse","Identify and treat psychiatric comorbidity, including anxiety, obsessive-compulsive features and depression","Provide genetic counselling for the SGCE variant, explaining maternal imprinting and parent-of-origin penetrance","Refer to a movement-disorder specialist for diagnosis and ongoing management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: SGCE Myoclonus-Dystonia","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"myoepithelioma":{"name":"Soft-tissue myoepithelioma","system":"Oncology / Pathology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["myoepithelioma of soft tissue","soft tissue mixed tumour","parachordoma","myoepithelial tumour of soft tissue","cutaneous myoepithelioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A tumour showing myoepithelial differentiation arising in soft tissue and skin, outside the salivary glands and breast where myoepithelial tumours are more familiar","Encompasses a spectrum from benign myoepithelioma and mixed tumour (with ductal structures) to malignant myoepithelial carcinoma","Characteristically coexpresses epithelial markers (cytokeratins and/or EMA) together with S100 protein, and often GFAP, calponin, p63 or smooth muscle actin","A large proportion carry EWSR1 gene rearrangements, with fusion partners such as POU5F1, PBX1 or ZNF444; a subset show FUS rearrangement","Occurs across a wide age range in the limbs, limb girdles and trunk, with morphology ranging from epithelioid to spindled cells in a myxoid or hyaline stroma","Malignancy is chiefly determined by cytological atypia, and malignant tumours (myoepithelial carcinoma) may recur and metastasise","Parachordoma is now regarded as part of the myoepithelial tumour spectrum rather than a separate entity"],"pathophysiology":"Soft-tissue myoepithelioma is defined by differentiation towards myoepithelial cells, which normally combine epithelial and contractile (myoid) properties, explaining the dual immunophenotype of keratins, S100 and myoid markers. Many tumours are driven by EWSR1 gene fusions, in which a strong promoter is joined to a transcription-factor partner to deregulate gene expression, while a minority use FUS as the alternative partner. The genetic drivers overlap with those of other EWSR1-associated soft-tissue tumours, and the degree of nuclear atypia rather than the specific fusion best predicts clinical behaviour.","additionalDifferentials":["Extraskeletal myxoid chondrosarcoma","Ossifying fibromyxoid tumour","Epithelioid sarcoma and other epithelioid soft-tissue tumours","Chordoma (particularly for parachordoma-like lesions)","Metastatic or salivary-type carcinoma showing myoepithelial features","Myoepithelial carcinoma of the malignant end of the spectrum","Mixed tumour or myoepithelioma of skin adnexal origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Immunohistochemistry for cytokeratins, EMA and S100 as the core diagnostic panel","Additional myoepithelial markers such as GFAP, calponin, p63 and smooth muscle actin","FISH or molecular testing for EWSR1 (and FUS) rearrangement","SMARCB1 (INI1) immunohistochemistry, since loss occurs in a subset of malignant tumours","Cross-sectional imaging to define the mass and assess for metastasis in atypical cases","Careful assessment of nuclear atypia and mitotic activity to separate benign from malignant tumours"],"redFlags":["Marked nuclear atypia indicating myoepithelial carcinoma","Rapid growth, large size or infiltrative margins","Local recurrence after excision","Distant metastasis, most often to lung and bone, in malignant tumours"],"prognosis":"Benign myoepitheliomas and mixed tumours are generally cured by complete excision, though local recurrence can follow incomplete removal; malignant myoepithelial carcinomas, defined largely by significant cytological atypia, carry a real risk of recurrence and metastasis, and paediatric malignant tumours in particular may behave aggressively.","pitfalls":["Confusing myoepithelioma with extraskeletal myxoid chondrosarcoma or chordoma on limited sampling","Relying on a single marker rather than demonstrating the characteristic dual immunophenotype","Underrecognising malignancy because architecture can appear deceptively bland despite atypia","Overlooking SMARCB1 loss, which can mimic other INI1-deficient tumours","Assuming an EWSR1 rearrangement points to Ewing sarcoma rather than a myoepithelial tumour"],"management":["Achieve complete surgical excision with clear margins as the mainstay of treatment","Grade malignancy by cytological atypia, treating benign tumours by excision and atypical or malignant tumours with wider resection","Consider adjuvant radiotherapy for malignant myoepithelial carcinoma or where surgical margins are inadequate","Confirm the diagnosis with the immunohistochemical panel of cytokeratins or EMA, S100 and myoid markers, plus EWSR1 or FUS molecular testing","Surveil for local recurrence and metastasis after treatment of malignant tumours with clinical review and cross-sectional imaging","Manage through a specialist soft-tissue sarcoma multidisciplinary team, with paediatric oncology input for childhood malignant tumours"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","WHO Classification of Tumours: Skin Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"myofascial_pain_syndrome":{"name":"Myofascial pain syndrome","system":"Rehabilitation / Pain","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPS","myofascial trigger point pain","muscular trigger point syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braddom's Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation / pain medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Myofascial pain syndrome is a regional pain disorder characterised by myofascial trigger points, which are hyperirritable spots within a palpable taut band of skeletal muscle","Pressing a trigger point reproduces the patient's familiar pain and refers it in a characteristic, reproducible pattern that does not follow dermatomal boundaries","A local twitch response, elicited by snapping palpation or needling of the taut band, supports the diagnosis, which is fundamentally clinical","It is distinguished from fibromyalgia, which is a widespread pain disorder with diffuse tender points rather than regional referring trigger points","Perpetuating factors such as poor posture, ergonomic strain, muscle overload, and underlying structural or psychological stressors should be identified and corrected","Core treatment is manual and physical therapy including trigger-point release, stretching, spray-and-stretch techniques, and posture and activity correction","Trigger-point injection or dry needling can deactivate stubborn points, and botulinum toxin injection is used for selected refractory cases","Addressing sleep, mood, and contributing chronic conditions improves durability of the response, and long-term management emphasises self-directed exercise"],"pathophysiology":"Myofascial trigger points are thought to develop where sustained or repetitive muscle overload leads to localised areas of contracted sarcomeres and a taut band. A widely cited model proposes excessive acetylcholine release at dysfunctional motor endplates, producing sustained contraction, local ischaemia and energy crisis, and accumulation of sensitising biochemical mediators. This lowers the local pain threshold, sensitises peripheral nociceptors, and, through convergence in the dorsal horn, generates referred pain and contributes to central sensitisation when the process becomes chronic.","additionalDifferentials":["Fibromyalgia","Radiculopathy or peripheral nerve entrapment","Cervical or lumbar facet joint pain","Bursitis or tendinopathy","Inflammatory or crystal arthropathy","Referred visceral pain","Polymyalgia rheumatica"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination to locate taut bands, trigger points, and reproducible referred pain","Assessment of posture, ergonomics, and mechanical perpetuating factors","Investigations to exclude mimics, such as imaging or nerve conduction studies when a structural or neurological cause is suspected","Screening for contributing conditions including thyroid dysfunction, vitamin deficiency, and sleep disturbance","Blood tests such as inflammatory markers where inflammatory or systemic disease is considered"],"redFlags":["Constitutional features such as fever, weight loss, or night sweats suggesting infection or malignancy","Progressive neurological deficit indicating a radicular or cord lesion rather than muscular pain","Sudden severe pain or a mass suggesting an alternative acute pathology","Pain unresponsive to appropriate myofascial treatment prompting review of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Acute myofascial pain from an identifiable overload often resolves well when the trigger points are treated and perpetuating factors corrected. Chronic cases with central sensitisation and unaddressed contributing factors can be persistent, but most patients improve with an active, multimodal rehabilitation approach.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling widespread fibromyalgia as regional myofascial pain, or vice versa","Treating trigger points while ignoring the posture, ergonomic, or structural factors that perpetuate them","Relying on passive treatments and medication without an active exercise programme","Overlooking sleep disturbance, mood, and stress that maintain chronic muscle pain","Repeated needling of trigger points without addressing the underlying cause"],"management":["Base treatment on manual and physical therapy including trigger-point release, stretching and spray-and-stretch techniques","Perform trigger-point needling, either dry needling or local anaesthetic injection, for persistent active points","Identify and correct perpetuating factors such as poor posture, ergonomic strain, muscle overload and psychological stressors","Use simple analgesia and consider a low-dose tricyclic antidepressant or muscle relaxant for pain and associated sleep disturbance","Screen for and treat contributing conditions including thyroid dysfunction, vitamin deficiency and sleep disturbance","Maintain an active multimodal rehabilitation programme and address central sensitisation in chronic cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braddom's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation"],"crossLinks":[]},"myofibrillar_myopathy":{"name":"Myofibrillar myopathy","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MFM","desminopathy","DES","CRYAB","MYOT","desmin-related myopathy","protein aggregate myopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of genetically heterogeneous myopathies unified by a common histological picture of myofibrillar disintegration with abnormal protein aggregates","Weakness is frequently distal or mixed distal and proximal at onset, distinguishing it from most limb-girdle dystrophies","Cardiac involvement, including cardiomyopathy and conduction disease, is common and can dominate the clinical picture, sometimes preceding overt weakness","Caused by mutations in genes for Z-disc and associated proteins, with desmin (DES) and alpha-B-crystallin (CRYAB) among the classic causes, alongside myotilin, ZASP, filamin C and others","Respiratory muscle weakness and peripheral neuropathy may accompany the myopathy","Muscle biopsy shows Z-disc streaming, rimmed vacuoles and aggregates of desmin and other proteins on immunohistochemistry and electron microscopy","Because cardiac and respiratory complications drive morbidity, systematic cardiac and respiratory surveillance is central to management"],"pathophysiology":"Myofibrillar myopathies are caused by mutations in genes encoding proteins of the sarcomeric Z-disc and its associated cytoskeletal network, including desmin, alpha-B-crystallin, myotilin, ZASP and filamin C. These proteins maintain the structural integrity and mechanical linkage of the myofibrils and the intermediate-filament scaffold that connects sarcomeres to the sarcolemma and organelles. When they are defective, the Z-disc disintegrates and misfolded proteins, particularly desmin, accumulate as insoluble aggregates that disrupt myofibrillar architecture, mitochondrial positioning and protein-quality-control pathways. The resulting mechanical and metabolic failure of the fibre produces progressive weakness, and because several of these proteins are also essential in cardiac muscle, cardiomyopathy and conduction disease are frequent.","additionalDifferentials":["Limb-girdle muscular dystrophies","Distal myopathies including GNE and Miyoshi myopathy","Inclusion body myositis","Inherited cardiomyopathies with skeletal involvement","Metabolic myopathies","Chronic inflammatory or hereditary neuropathies where neuropathy coexists"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase, which is often normal to moderately elevated","Electromyography, which may show myopathic changes sometimes with myotonic or neurogenic features","Muscle biopsy with immunohistochemistry and electron microscopy showing protein aggregates and Z-disc pathology","Cardiac assessment with electrocardiogram, echocardiography and cardiac magnetic resonance imaging","Respiratory function testing","Targeted or panel genetic testing covering desmin, alpha-B-crystallin, myotilin, ZASP, filamin C and related genes"],"redFlags":["Cardiac conduction block, arrhythmia or heart failure, which can be life-threatening and may precede weakness","Respiratory muscle weakness with nocturnal hypoventilation","Rapid functional decline suggesting an aggressive subtype","Sudden cardiac events in the patient or affected relatives"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies with the causative gene, but progression is usually slow for the skeletal weakness while cardiac and respiratory complications are the main determinants of morbidity and mortality, so outcomes are substantially improved by early detection and treatment of heart and respiratory involvement.","pitfalls":["Focusing on the skeletal weakness and failing to screen for the potentially fatal cardiac disease","Mislabelling it as a limb-girdle dystrophy and missing the diagnostic aggregate pathology","Overlooking coexisting peripheral neuropathy or respiratory involvement","Assuming a single gene, when the group is genetically heterogeneous and needs broad testing"],"management":["Undertake cardiac surveillance with electrocardiography, echocardiography and cardiac magnetic resonance imaging, treating conduction disease and considering a pacemaker or implantable defibrillator to prevent sudden death","Monitor respiratory function and institute non-invasive ventilation for respiratory muscle weakness","Maintain mobility and function with physiotherapy, orthoses such as ankle-foot orthoses and occupational therapy","Manage dysphagia and nutrition with speech and language therapy and dietetic support","Confirm the causative gene, such as desmin, alpha-B-crystallin, myotilin, ZASP or filamin C, by genetic testing to refine surveillance","Offer genetic counselling and cardiac screening of at-risk relatives","Coordinate care through a neuromuscular multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: Myofibrillar Myopathy / Desmin-related myopathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"myopic_maculopathy":{"name":"Myopic maculopathy","system":"Special senses / Eye (macula)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["myopic macular degeneration","pathological myopia maculopathy","degenerative myopia maculopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ryan's Retina / Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A sight-threatening complication of pathological (degenerative) myopia driven by progressive axial elongation of the globe and formation of a posterior staphyloma","The META-PM classification grades categories from tessellated fundus through diffuse and patchy chorioretinal atrophy to macular atrophy, with three 'plus' lesions: lacquer cracks, myopic choroidal neovascularisation and Fuchs spot","Lacquer cracks are breaks in Bruch's membrane that predispose to myopic choroidal neovascularisation and subretinal haemorrhage","Myopic choroidal neovascularisation is typically small, grey and subfoveal or juxtafoveal and may leave a pigmented scar (Fuchs or Forster-Fuchs spot); anti-VEGF therapy is the mainstay of treatment","Myopic traction maculopathy, including foveoschisis, results from inward traction on a stretched, non-compliant retina overlying a staphyloma and may progress to macular hole and detachment","Dome-shaped macula and peripapillary changes such as myopic conus, peripapillary atrophy and tilted disc are recognised associated features that can complicate glaucoma assessment","OCT is essential for detecting neovascularisation, foveoschisis, macular hole and dome-shaped macula, and OCT-angiography aids detection of myopic choroidal neovascularisation","Because early myopic neovascularisation is very treatable, patients benefit from education about warning symptoms and often Amsler grid monitoring"],"pathophysiology":"Pathological myopia is characterised by excessive axial elongation of the eye and development of a posterior staphyloma, an outpouching of the wall of the globe. Progressive mechanical stretching thins the retina, retinal pigment epithelium, Bruch's membrane and choroid, producing chorioretinal atrophy and breaks in Bruch's membrane known as lacquer cracks. These breaks together with the attenuated choroid predispose to myopic choroidal neovascularisation and subsequent scarring (Fuchs spot). Over the rigid, non-compliant posterior pole, persistent vitreoretinal and epiretinal traction on a stretched retina causes myopic traction maculopathy and foveoschisis, which may progress to full-thickness macular hole and retinal detachment. The combination of atrophy, neovascularisation and traction accounts for the central visual loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Neovascular age-related macular degeneration","Angioid streaks with choroidal neovascularisation","Central serous chorioretinopathy","Choroidal neovascularisation of inflammatory or idiopathic origin","Macular hole or vitreomacular traction of other aetiology","Tilted disc syndrome with associated maculopathy","Punctate inner choroidopathy and other inflammatory chorioretinopathies in young myopes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["OCT of the macula for neovascularisation, foveoschisis, macular hole and dome-shaped macula","OCT-angiography and/or fundus fluorescein angiography to detect and characterise myopic choroidal neovascularisation","Fundus autofluorescence and colour imaging to map chorioretinal atrophy and lacquer cracks","Measurement of axial length and refraction to confirm the degree of myopia","Amsler grid for symptom self-monitoring","Wide-field imaging and peripheral retinal examination, as high myopes are also at risk of peripheral breaks and detachment"],"redFlags":["New metamorphopsia, a central scotoma or sudden drop in central vision suggesting neovascularisation or subretinal haemorrhage","Flashes, floaters or a field defect suggesting retinal detachment, to which high myopes are predisposed","Progressive distortion suggesting foveoschisis advancing towards a macular hole","Fresh subretinal haemorrhage at the fovea"],"prognosis":"Myopic maculopathy is a leading cause of irreversible central visual impairment in high myopia and tends to progress with age and increasing axial length. Nonetheless, early detection and prompt anti-VEGF treatment of myopic choroidal neovascularisation gives a relatively favourable visual outcome compared with untreated disease.","pitfalls":["Attributing reduced vision solely to refractive error and missing treatable myopic choroidal neovascularisation","Misinterpreting myopic disc and peripapillary changes, leading to over- or under-diagnosis of glaucoma","Overlooking foveoschisis on clinical examination when OCT would reveal it","Failing to examine the peripheral retina for breaks in a high myope","Confusing myopic neovascularisation with neovascular AMD, which differs in lesion size, behaviour and treatment response"],"management":["Treat myopic choroidal neovascularisation promptly with intravitreal anti-VEGF injections as the mainstay of therapy","Consider vitreoretinal surgery, such as vitrectomy with internal limiting membrane peeling, for myopic traction maculopathy, foveoschisis and macular hole","Repair associated retinal detachment surgically and treat peripheral retinal breaks","Monitor with serial optical coherence tomography and OCT-angiography, and provide an Amsler grid for symptom self-monitoring","Optimise refractive correction and provide low-vision aids and rehabilitation for irreversible central visual loss","Surveil for and manage coexisting glaucoma, allowing for the difficult optic disc assessment in high myopia","Refer to medical retina and advise prompt review of any new distortion or scotoma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ryan's Retina","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","International photographic classification and grading system for myopic maculopathy (META-PM)"],"crossLinks":[]},"myosin_storage_myopathy":{"name":"Myosin storage myopathy","system":"Musculoskeletal system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Hyaline body myopathy","MYH7-related myosin storage myopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dubowitz's Muscle Disorders in Childhood","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by mutations in MYH7 encoding slow or beta-cardiac myosin heavy chain, typically within the distal light-meromyosin rod domain.","The pathological hallmark is subsarcolemmal hyaline bodies of accumulated slow myosin that are eosinophilic on haematoxylin and eosin and stain strongly for slow-myosin ATPase.","Weakness ranges from a scapuloperoneal to a limb-girdle distribution, often with prominent scapular and foot-dorsiflexor involvement.","Cardiomyopathy, whether hypertrophic or dilated, may coexist because of the shared MYH7 gene and warrants active surveillance.","Inheritance is usually autosomal dominant, with recessive and de novo cases described.","Onset ranges from childhood to adulthood, with generally slow progression.","Clinically and genetically it overlaps with Laing distal myopathy and MYH7-related cardiomyopathies."],"pathophysiology":"MYH7 encodes the slow beta-myosin heavy chain expressed in type 1 skeletal fibres and in myocardium. Mutations, frequently in the rod domain, impair thick-filament assembly and stability, so myosin aggregates into hyaline bodies within type 1 fibres and sarcomeric contraction is disrupted. Because the same isoform is expressed in the heart, a cardiomyopathy may accompany the skeletal phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Laing distal myopathy (MYH7)","Myofibrillar myopathies (desmin, myotilin)","Scapuloperoneal muscular dystrophy","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophies","Nemaline and cap myopathy","Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MYH7 gene sequencing","Muscle biopsy with slow-myosin immunostaining and electron microscopy","Serum creatine kinase","Electrocardiography and echocardiography, with cardiac MRI if needed","Electromyography","Muscle MRI to define the selective involvement pattern","Respiratory function testing"],"redFlags":["New arrhythmia, syncope or symptoms of heart failure","Progressive respiratory muscle weakness","Family history of sudden cardiac death"],"prognosis":"Skeletal weakness is generally slowly progressive with ambulation preserved for many years. The overall outlook is strongly influenced by any associated cardiomyopathy, making cardiac surveillance essential.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for and monitor cardiomyopathy.","Mistaking hyaline bodies for other protein aggregates without slow-myosin immunostaining.","Overlooking the clinical overlap with Laing distal myopathy.","Not offering cascade cardiac and neurological screening to relatives."],"management":["Undertake cardiac surveillance with electrocardiography and echocardiography, adding cardiac magnetic resonance imaging as needed, for MYH7-related hypertrophic or dilated cardiomyopathy and treat heart failure or arrhythmia accordingly","Maintain muscle function and mobility with physiotherapy and ankle-foot orthoses for foot-drop","Provide occupational therapy and aids for scapular and scapuloperoneal weakness","Monitor respiratory function and support with non-invasive ventilation if weakness progresses","Confirm the diagnosis by MYH7 sequencing and correlate the genotype","Offer genetic counselling for the usually autosomal dominant inheritance and cardiac screening of relatives","Coordinate care through neuromuscular and cardiology specialists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dubowitz V, Sewry CA, Oldfors A. Muscle Biopsy: A Practical Approach","Standard textbook of neuromuscular disorders","International consensus guidance on the congenital myopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"myositis_ossificans":{"name":"Myositis ossificans traumatica","system":"Musculoskeletal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["myositis ossificans","heterotopic ossification","pseudomalignant osseous tumour of soft tissue"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone / Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign, self-limiting formation of heterotopic bone within skeletal muscle, usually following trauma in young active adults","Presents as a rapidly enlarging, firm, tender soft-tissue mass, often in the thigh, buttock or upper arm","The defining feature is zonal maturation, with a mineralising peripheral shell of mature bone surrounding a cellular, immature central zone","This zonal pattern is the reverse of osteosarcoma, in which the most immature and dense mineralisation tends to lie centrally","Radiographic ossification typically becomes visible several weeks after injury and matures peripherally over time","Early biopsy can be dangerously misleading, as the immature cellular centre mimics a sarcoma histologically","Management is usually conservative with observation, as the lesion matures and often partially regresses without excision"],"pathophysiology":"Myositis ossificans traumatica is a benign, reactive proliferation of fibroblasts, cartilage and bone within soft tissue, most often triggered by a single injury or repetitive microtrauma. Local tissue damage initiates a proliferative fibroblastic response that undergoes progressive metaplastic differentiation into cartilage and then bone. The lesion matures in a centripetal, zonal fashion, with the peripheral rim ossifying first and most completely, while the centre remains cellular and immature early in the course. Over weeks to months the whole lesion becomes better demarcated and ossified, and it may shrink.","additionalDifferentials":["Extraskeletal osteosarcoma","Parosteal or periosteal osteosarcoma","Soft-tissue sarcoma with metaplastic bone","Fibro-osseous pseudotumour of the digits","Tumoral calcinosis","Osteochondroma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial plain radiography to demonstrate evolving peripheral ossification over time","CT to display the characteristic peripheral mineralised rim and central lucency","MRI to assess the soft-tissue mass, noting surrounding oedema in early lesions","Correlation of imaging with time since injury to recognise the maturation sequence","Biopsy only when imaging is atypical, ideally deferred until the lesion has matured"],"redFlags":["A mass without peripheral maturation raising concern for extraskeletal osteosarcoma","Continued growth beyond the expected maturation window","Central rather than peripheral dense mineralisation on imaging","Absence of any traumatic history with atypical imaging features"],"prognosis":"Myositis ossificans is benign and self-limiting, typically maturing and stabilising over several months, often with partial resorption. Excision, if required for symptoms, is best delayed until the lesion is mature to reduce recurrence, and the outlook is excellent.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the cellular immature centre on early biopsy as a sarcoma","Reversing the zonal reasoning and mistaking it for extraskeletal osteosarcoma","Excising a lesion prematurely before maturation, increasing recurrence risk","Imaging too early before diagnostic peripheral ossification has developed"],"management":["Manage the acute phase conservatively with rest, activity modification, ice and analgesia while the lesion matures","Avoid aggressive massage and early surgery, which can aggravate heterotopic bone formation","Restore range of motion gently with physiotherapy once acute inflammation settles","Follow with serial imaging to confirm the zonal maturation pattern and distinguish it from osteosarcoma, deferring any biopsy until the lesion is mature","Reserve surgical excision for symptomatic mature lesions, delaying until ossification is complete to reduce recurrence","Reassure the patient of the benign, self-limiting natural history with frequent partial resorption"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"myotonia_congenita":{"name":"Myotonia congenita","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CLCN1","Thomsen disease","Becker myotonia","chloride channelopathy of muscle","autosomal dominant myotonia congenita","autosomal recessive generalised myotonia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by painless muscle stiffness that is worst on initiating movement after rest and eases with repeated contraction, the classic warm-up phenomenon","Caused by mutations in the CLCN1 gene encoding the skeletal-muscle chloride channel, with a dominant form (Thomsen disease) and a generally more severe recessive form (Becker myotonia)","Becker myotonia often shows transient weakness at the start of forceful exercise that then resolves, and tends to have a later onset and more marked muscle hypertrophy","Affected individuals frequently have a well-developed, athletic muscular appearance despite the functional handicap of stiffness","Grip myotonia and percussion myotonia can be demonstrated at the bedside, and eyelid myotonia may follow sustained forced closure","Cold can accentuate stiffness but, unlike paramyotonia congenita, myotonia congenita characteristically improves rather than worsens with continued activity","Symptomatic relief is achieved with sodium-channel-blocking antimyotonic agents when stiffness is disabling"],"pathophysiology":"Myotonia congenita results from loss-of-function mutations in CLCN1, the gene encoding the skeletal-muscle voltage-gated chloride channel ClC-1. This channel carries most of the resting membrane conductance and normally stabilises the sarcolemma after an action potential. When chloride conductance is reduced, potassium that accumulates in the transverse tubules during activity depolarises the membrane more readily, producing runs of repetitive after-discharges experienced as delayed muscle relaxation, or myotonia. The autosomal dominant form reflects a dominant-negative effect on the dimeric channel, whereas the recessive form arises when both alleles are severely impaired, and the transient warm-up improvement reflects redistribution of ions with repeated activity.","additionalDifferentials":["Paramyotonia congenita","Myotonic dystrophy types 1 and 2","Sodium-channel (potassium-aggravated) myotonias","Schwartz-Jampel syndrome","Brody myopathy presenting with exercise-induced stiffness","Rippling muscle disease and other non-dystrophic causes of stiffness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electromyography demonstrating myotonic discharges with their characteristic waxing and waning pattern","Serum creatine kinase, which may be mildly elevated","Short exercise electromyography protocol, with and without cooling, to distinguish it from paramyotonia congenita","Targeted CLCN1 genetic testing","Assessment to exclude myotonic dystrophy, including examination for multisystem features","Muscle biopsy only where the diagnosis remains unclear"],"redFlags":["Cardiac conduction disturbance or systemic features suggesting myotonic dystrophy rather than a pure channelopathy","Progressive fixed weakness, which is not expected and points to an alternative diagnosis","Severe stiffness compromising airway, swallowing or safe mobility","Adverse response to depolarising neuromuscular blockade during anaesthesia"],"prognosis":"Myotonia congenita is a non-progressive, non-dystrophic disorder compatible with a normal lifespan, and although stiffness can be functionally limiting it often stabilises or improves in adulthood and usually responds to antimyotonic therapy.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for myotonic dystrophy and missing the systemic surveillance the latter requires, or vice versa","Confusing the warm-up phenomenon of myotonia congenita with the paradoxical cold-worsened myotonia of paramyotonia congenita","Overlooking the transient exercise-induced weakness of Becker myotonia and labelling it a fixed myopathy","Not warning about depolarising muscle relaxants and other anaesthetic triggers of severe myotonia"],"management":["Relieve disabling stiffness with antimyotonic sodium-channel-blocking agents such as mexiletine, with lamotrigine or carbamazepine as alternatives","Advise on the warm-up phenomenon and gradual initiation of activity to reduce stiffness","Advise avoidance of cold exposure, which can accentuate the stiffness","Take anaesthetic precautions, avoiding depolarising muscle relaxants such as suxamethonium that provoke myotonia","Confirm the diagnosis with CLCN1 genetic testing and distinguish the dominant Thomsen from the recessive Becker form","Offer genetic counselling appropriate to the inheritance pattern","Reassure regarding the non-progressive course and normal lifespan"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: Myotonia Congenita"],"crossLinks":[]},"myotonic_dystrophy":{"name":"Myotonic dystrophy","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dystrophia myotonica","DM1","DM2","Steinert disease","DMPK-related / CNBP-related disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant multisystem disorder; type 1 (DM1) is caused by a CTG trinucleotide repeat expansion in DMPK, and type 2 (DM2) by a CCTG repeat expansion in CNBP","Myotonia (delayed muscle relaxation, for example a slow grip release) is a defining feature, alongside progressive weakness","DM1 shows a distinctive distal and facial weakness pattern with ptosis, temporal wasting, and neck flexor weakness, whereas DM2 tends to be more proximal","The repeat expansion is unstable and tends to enlarge across generations, producing genetic anticipation with earlier onset and greater severity in successive generations","A severe congenital form of DM1 occurs, particularly with maternal transmission, presenting with neonatal hypotonia and respiratory difficulty","Multisystem involvement includes cardiac conduction defects and arrhythmia, cataracts, insulin resistance, hypersomnolence, and gonadal and cognitive effects","Cardiac conduction disease can cause sudden death, so regular electrocardiographic surveillance is essential regardless of skeletal muscle severity","Diagnosis rests on the clinical picture, demonstration of myotonia (clinically or on electromyography), and targeted repeat-expansion genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Myotonic dystrophy is caused by expanded non-coding repeats (CTG in DMPK for DM1 and CCTG in CNBP for DM2) that are transcribed into RNA containing long repeat tracts. These toxic RNA transcripts accumulate in the nucleus and sequester RNA-binding proteins, particularly muscleblind-like proteins, disrupting the normal alternative splicing of many downstream genes. The resulting misregulated splicing of ion-channel, insulin-receptor, and other transcripts produces myotonia, progressive muscle degeneration, and the wide-ranging systemic features. Repeat instability during transmission enlarges the expansion across generations, underlying the phenomenon of anticipation.","additionalDifferentials":["Limb-girdle muscular dystrophies","Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy","Non-dystrophic myotonias (channelopathies)","Mitochondrial myopathy","Oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy","Motor neurone disease with prominent weakness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted repeat-expansion genetic testing for DMPK (DM1) and CNBP (DM2)","Electromyography demonstrating myotonic discharges","Electrocardiography and Holter monitoring for conduction disease and arrhythmia","Echocardiography where cardiomyopathy is suspected","Slit-lamp examination for characteristic cataracts","Assessment of glucose tolerance and respiratory function"],"redFlags":["Syncope, palpitations, or a high-grade conduction block indicating risk of sudden cardiac death","Respiratory failure, especially in the congenital form or during anaesthesia","Neonatal hypotonia and respiratory distress suggesting congenital DM1","Excessive daytime sleepiness with signs of hypoventilation","Heightened sensitivity to anaesthetic and sedative agents"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the type and severity: DM1 tends to be more severe than DM2, and the congenital form carries high neonatal morbidity and mortality. Cardiac conduction disease and respiratory failure are the leading causes of premature death, so lifelong cardiac and respiratory surveillance is central to care.","pitfalls":["Focusing on skeletal weakness and neglecting the potentially fatal cardiac conduction disease","Giving standard doses of anaesthetic or sedative agents to which patients are unusually sensitive","Not recognising anticipation and its implications for family counselling","Missing the diagnosis in a mildly affected parent of a floppy neonate","Overlooking treatable comorbidities such as cataracts, diabetes, and sleep-disordered breathing"],"management":["Provide lifelong cardiac surveillance with electrocardiography and Holter monitoring, implanting a pacemaker or defibrillator for conduction disease or arrhythmia to prevent sudden death","Assess respiratory function and use non-invasive ventilation for hypoventilation, taking great care with anaesthesia and sedatives given respiratory and cardiac sensitivity","Treat symptomatic myotonia with an antimyotonic agent such as mexiletine when required","Manage the multisystem features with cataract surgery, treatment of insulin resistance or diabetes, and a wakefulness-promoting agent such as modafinil for disabling hypersomnolence","Support weakness with physiotherapy, ankle-foot orthoses for foot-drop and occupational therapy","Offer genetic counselling explaining autosomal dominant inheritance and anticipation, screen at-risk relatives and monitor pregnancies for the severe congenital form","Coordinate multidisciplinary neuromuscular care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Myotonic dystrophy type 1 and type 2","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"myotonic_dystrophy_type_2":{"name":"Myotonic Dystrophy Type 2","system":"Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DM2","PROMM","Proximal myotonic myopathy","Ricker syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Weakness is proximal-predominant (hip girdle and thigh), the opposite of the distal-onset pattern of type 1 disease, hence the label 'proximal myotonic myopathy'.","Myotonia is often mild, fluctuating and may be clinically inapparent, sometimes only demonstrable on electromyography.","Myalgia and fluctuating muscle stiffness or pain are prominent and frequently the presenting complaint.","Grip and percussion myotonia may be elicited, and calf hypertrophy can occur.","It is a multisystem disorder: posterior subcapsular iridescent cataracts, cardiac conduction defects and arrhythmia, insulin resistance and diabetes, hypogonadism and mild cognitive change.","Onset is typically in adulthood and the phenotype is generally milder than type 1, with no congenital form.","Creatine kinase may be mildly elevated.","Inheritance is autosomal dominant; a family history is often present but the condition is frequently under-recognised."],"pathophysiology":"Myotonic dystrophy type 2 is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by an unstable CCTG tetranucleotide repeat expansion in intron 1 of the CNBP gene (formerly ZNF9) on chromosome 3q21. Transcription produces expanded CCTG-containing RNA that accumulates as ribonuclear foci, sequestering muscleblind-like (MBNL) splicing regulators and dysregulating CELF1. This toxic RNA gain-of-function drives widespread mis-splicing (a spliceopathy), including of the insulin receptor, the muscle chloride channel CLCN1 (producing myotonia through reduced chloride conductance) and cardiac troponin T, explaining the multisystem phenotype. In contrast to type 1 disease, repeat length correlates poorly with severity, and prominent anticipation and a congenital form are essentially absent.","additionalDifferentials":["Myotonic dystrophy type 1","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","Non-dystrophic myotonias (myotonia congenita from CLCN1; paramyotonia congenita from SCN4A)","Inflammatory myopathy (polymyositis)","Metabolic myopathy such as late-onset Pompe disease","Drug-induced myopathy including statin-associated myopathy","Fibromyalgia and other chronic pain syndromes, given the prominent myalgia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted genetic testing for the CNBP (ZNF9) CCTG repeat expansion using repeat-primed PCR or Southern blot.","Electromyography to demonstrate myotonic discharges.","Creatine kinase.","ECG and ambulatory rhythm monitoring for conduction disease and arrhythmia, with echocardiography.","Slit-lamp examination for characteristic cataracts.","Fasting glucose and glycated haemoglobin with assessment of insulin resistance.","Muscle biopsy where needed, which may show selective type 2 fibre atrophy and pyknotic nuclear clumps."],"redFlags":["Syncope, palpitations or documented conduction block, given the risk of sudden cardiac death and the need for cardiology surveillance.","New symptoms of heart failure.","Peri-operative and sedation risk, with sensitivity to anaesthetic and analgesic agents, prolonged apnoea and myotonia provoked by depolarising muscle relaxants.","Progressive proximal weakness affecting mobility and transfers."],"prognosis":"The course is generally milder and more slowly progressive than type 1 disease, with life expectancy often near normal and ambulation usually preserved for many years. Cardiac conduction disease and arrhythmia remain important causes of morbidity and of sudden death.","pitfalls":["Frequently misdiagnosed as limb-girdle dystrophy, fibromyalgia or statin myopathy because the myotonia is subtle.","Repeat size does not predict severity, so counselling should not apply the anticipation rules used for type 1 disease.","Standard sequencing panels miss the intronic repeat; specific repeat-expansion testing is needed.","Cardiac risk is easily underestimated because the skeletal weakness is comparatively mild.","Unrecognised diagnosis can lead to avoidable anaesthetic complications."],"management":["Provide cardiac surveillance with electrocardiography and ambulatory rhythm monitoring for conduction disease and arrhythmia, with device therapy where indicated","Manage prominent myalgia and fluctuating stiffness with analgesia, adding an antimyotonic agent such as mexiletine for symptomatic myotonia","Screen for and treat insulin resistance and diabetes mellitus","Arrange slit-lamp review and cataract surgery when visually significant","Support proximal muscle function with physiotherapy and graded exercise","Offer genetic counselling for the autosomal dominant CNBP CCTG repeat expansion","Coordinate multidisciplinary neurology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Consensus care guidelines for myotonic dystrophy","Standard neuromuscular disorders textbook","Neurogenetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"myotubular_myopathy":{"name":"Myotubular myopathy","system":"Musculoskeletal system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["X-linked myotubular myopathy","X-linked centronuclear myopathy","XLMTM"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dubowitz's Muscle Disorders in Childhood","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked recessive congenital myopathy caused by MTM1 mutations encoding myotubularin, a phosphoinositide phosphatase regulating membrane trafficking and excitation-contraction coupling.","Affected male neonates present with profound hypotonia, respiratory failure frequently requiring immediate ventilatory support, and feeding difficulty.","Macrocephaly, a long face, external ophthalmoplegia and undescended testes are characteristic supportive features.","Muscle biopsy shows small rounded fibres with central nuclei resembling fetal myotubes, with central aggregation of oxidative enzyme activity on NADH and SDH staining.","Antenatal clues include polyhydramnios, reduced fetal movements and increased birth length.","Female carriers are usually asymptomatic but may show variable weakness through skewed X-inactivation.","Milder and later-onset MTM1 phenotypes are increasingly recognised and should not be excluded by survival beyond infancy.","Belongs to the centronuclear myopathy family alongside autosomal DNM2- and BIN1-related forms."],"pathophysiology":"MTM1 encodes myotubularin, a lipid phosphatase that dephosphorylates phosphatidylinositol-3-phosphate and its 3,5-bisphosphate, regulating endosomal membrane trafficking, autophagy and the triad structures responsible for excitation-contraction coupling. Loss of function disorganises T-tubules and triad positioning, impairs calcium release and arrests myofibre maturation, so nuclei persist centrally in a manner reminiscent of fetal myotubes and severe congenital weakness results.","additionalDifferentials":["Spinal muscular atrophy type 1","Congenital myotonic dystrophy","Other centronuclear myopathies (DNM2, BIN1, RYR1)","Congenital myasthenic syndromes","Infantile-onset Pompe disease","Prader-Willi syndrome","Congenital muscular dystrophies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MTM1 sequencing with deletion and duplication analysis","Muscle biopsy with histochemistry (NADH, SDH, PAS)","Serum creatine kinase (usually normal or mildly raised)","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography","Overnight oximetry and capnography with respiratory function assessment","Cranial and muscle imaging where indicated","Genetic counselling and carrier testing of the mother"],"redFlags":["Neonatal respiratory failure requiring ventilation","Recurrent aspiration and feeding failure","Apnoea or hypoventilation","Hepatic peliosis or haemorrhage, a recognised MTM1 association","Unexplained bleeding complications"],"prognosis":"Classic severe disease historically carries high early mortality from respiratory failure, although survival is improving with ventilatory and nutritional support and milder variants run a more favourable course. Gene-directed therapies are under active investigation.","pitfalls":["Attributing neonatal hypotonia solely to central causes and missing a primary myopathy.","Being falsely reassured by a normal creatine kinase.","Overlooking hepatic haemorrhage or peliosis as a cause of sudden deterioration.","Failing to test and counsel the carrier mother and at-risk female relatives.","Assuming survival beyond infancy excludes an MTM1 diagnosis."],"management":["Provide ventilatory support as the mainstay, with neonatal invasive or non-invasive ventilation and early consideration of tracheostomy for chronic ventilator dependence","Secure nutrition through nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding to overcome bulbar weakness and reduce aspiration risk","Offer physiotherapy, orthoses, contracture prevention and adaptive equipment to optimise residual motor function","Enrol in specialist neuromuscular follow-up and consider emerging MTM1 gene-directed (AAV-myotubularin) therapy through clinical trials","Undertake surveillance for known complications, notably hepatic peliosis and life-threatening hepatic haemorrhage, cryptorchidism and progressive respiratory decline","Offer genetic counselling and carrier testing for the mother and at-risk female relatives, with antenatal or preimplantation options given X-linked inheritance","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across neurology, respiratory, gastroenterology, orthopaedics and palliative medicine, with advance-care planning in severe disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dubowitz V, Sewry CA, Oldfors A. Muscle Biopsy: A Practical Approach","Standard textbook of paediatric neurology","International consensus guidance on the congenital myopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"myxedema_coma":{"name":"Myxoedema coma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["myxedema coma","myxoedema crisis","decompensated hypothyroidism","severe decompensated hypothyroidism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / endocrine society guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A life-threatening decompensation of severe hypothyroidism, typically in an elderly patient during winter, precipitated by infection, cold exposure, sedatives or non-compliance","The cardinal features are hypothermia, decreased consciousness and hypoventilation, often with hyponatraemia and hypoglycaemia","Coma is frequently a misnomer, as many patients present with lethargy or confusion rather than true unconsciousness","Look for supporting signs of longstanding hypothyroidism: dry skin, non-pitting oedema, macroglossia, bradycardia, delayed reflex relaxation and a thyroidectomy scar","Diagnosis is clinical and should not await confirmatory thyroid function tests, as delay increases mortality","Coexisting adrenal insufficiency must be considered, so stress steroids are given alongside thyroid hormone replacement","Treatment combines levothyroxine and stress steroids for myxoedema coma with rewarming, ventilatory and haemodynamic support, and treatment of the precipitant"],"pathophysiology":"Myxoedema coma represents failure of physiological compensation in profound, longstanding hypothyroidism. Severe thyroid-hormone deficiency slows metabolic rate and thermogenesis, producing hypothermia, and depresses respiratory drive with carbon-dioxide retention. Reduced free-water clearance and inappropriate antidiuresis cause hyponatraemia, while impaired gluconeogenesis contributes to hypoglycaemia. Cardiovascular effects include bradycardia, reduced contractility and pericardial effusion, and mucopolysaccharide deposition in tissues produces the characteristic non-pitting oedema. A precipitating stress such as infection or sedation tips the decompensated patient into obtundation and multi-organ dysfunction.","additionalDifferentials":["Sepsis with encephalopathy","Primary hypothermia from environmental exposure","Sedative or opioid intoxication","Hyponatraemia or hypoglycaemia of other cause","Adrenal crisis","Hypercapnic respiratory failure from other causes","Stroke or other structural brain injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thyroid function tests (TSH and free thyroxine) with cortisol before steroid administration","Core temperature measurement and blood glucose","Serum sodium and full electrolyte panel","Arterial blood gas to assess hypoventilation and carbon-dioxide retention","Septic screen including cultures and chest imaging to identify a precipitant","Electrocardiogram and, where indicated, echocardiography for effusion","Creatine kinase and full blood count"],"redFlags":["Profound hypothermia unresponsive to rewarming","Type 2 respiratory failure requiring ventilatory support","Refractory hypotension or bradyarrhythmia","Severe hyponatraemia with seizures or worsening coma","Haemodynamic instability suggesting concurrent adrenal crisis or sepsis"],"prognosis":"Even with modern intensive care, mortality remains substantial; outcome depends on prompt recognition, early hormone replacement and treatment of the precipitating illness.","pitfalls":["Delaying treatment while awaiting thyroid function results","Giving thyroid hormone without covering possible adrenal insufficiency with steroids","Aggressive active external rewarming that precipitates vasodilatory collapse","Overcorrecting hyponatraemia too rapidly","Missing the precipitating infection or sedative that triggered decompensation"],"management":["Give intravenous thyroid hormone replacement (levothyroxine, with or without liothyronine) as definitive therapy without awaiting confirmatory thyroid function tests","Give empirical stress-dose glucocorticoids (hydrocortisone) alongside thyroid hormone until coexisting adrenal insufficiency is excluded","Provide supportive intensive care with airway protection and ventilatory support for hypoventilation and carbon-dioxide retention","Correct hypothermia by gradual passive rewarming and manage hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia and hypotension with cautious fluid and glucose support","Identify and treat the precipitant, particularly infection with appropriate antimicrobials, and withdraw offending sedative drugs","Monitor in critical care with cardiac monitoring for bradyarrhythmia and echocardiography for pericardial effusion","Establish maintenance oral thyroid hormone replacement on recovery and arrange endocrinology follow-up to prevent recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine society guidance on severe hypothyroidism and myxoedema coma"],"crossLinks":[]},"myxofibrosarcoma":{"name":"Myxofibrosarcoma","system":"Oncology / Sarcoma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MFS","myxoid malignant fibrous histiocytoma","myxoid MFH","myxofibrosarcoma of soft tissue"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["One of the commoner sarcomas of elderly patients, arising typically in the dermal and subcutaneous soft tissue of the limbs and limb girdles","Characteristically shows a fibroblastic tumour with variably myxoid stroma, curvilinear vessels, and a spectrum of grades from low to high","Notorious for an infiltrative growth pattern with finger-like extensions along fascial planes that extend well beyond the apparent mass, driving a high local recurrence rate","Presents as a slowly enlarging, often painless subcutaneous mass, which may be mistaken for a benign lesion, especially when low grade","MRI often reveals infiltrative tails of tumour along fascia (the tail sign), helping to plan resection margins","Genomically complex with highly aberrant karyotypes rather than a defining recurrent fusion","Wide surgical excision with generous margins is essential, frequently combined with radiotherapy to reduce local recurrence, with chemotherapy reserved for selected high-grade cases","Repeated local recurrences may be accompanied by progression to higher grade and increasing metastatic potential"],"pathophysiology":"Myxofibrosarcoma is a malignant fibroblastic tumour with a characteristically myxoid stroma and prominent curvilinear vasculature. It is genomically complex, showing marked chromosomal instability and copy-number changes rather than a single defining genetic event, which places it among the karyotypically complex sarcomas. Its striking tendency to infiltrate diffusely along fascial and subcutaneous planes, often extending microscopically far beyond the palpable or radiologically evident mass, underlies its high propensity for local recurrence. With repeated recurrence, tumours often increase in grade and cellularity, acquiring greater metastatic potential, most commonly to the lungs.","additionalDifferentials":["Myxoid liposarcoma","Low-grade fibromyxoid sarcoma","Nodular fasciitis and other benign myxoid lesions","Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma","Myxoid neurogenic tumours","Superficial soft-tissue benign masses (e.g. lipoma, ganglion)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the primary site to identify infiltrative fascial tails and define true extent","Chest CT for pulmonary metastases in higher-grade disease","Whole-body functional imaging for staging where indicated","Core biopsy with careful tract planning","Immunohistochemistry to exclude specific differentiation and other myxoid tumours","Molecular testing to exclude entities such as myxoid liposarcoma and low-grade fibromyxoid sarcoma"],"redFlags":["An enlarging subcutaneous mass in an elderly patient, particularly if previously excised locally","Repeated local recurrence at a prior excision site","Increasing size, firmness, or pain suggesting progression to higher grade","New respiratory symptoms indicating pulmonary metastases","Extensive infiltrative disease on imaging beyond the palpable mass"],"prognosis":"Prognosis relates chiefly to grade and adequacy of excision; low-grade tumours rarely metastasise but recur locally, whereas high-grade tumours carry a significant metastatic risk, and repeated recurrence with grade progression worsens the outlook.","pitfalls":["Underestimating tumour extent because infiltrative tails reach well beyond the visible mass, leading to inadequate margins","Mistaking a low-grade tumour for a benign myxoid lesion and performing marginal excision","Confusing myxofibrosarcoma with myxoid liposarcoma or low-grade fibromyxoid sarcoma without molecular testing","Neglecting adjuvant radiotherapy where indicated, contributing to local recurrence","Failing to reassess grade at recurrence, missing progression to higher-grade disease"],"management":["Achieve wide local excision with generous margins as definitive treatment, accounting for infiltrative fascial tails extending beyond the palpable mass","Use neoadjuvant or adjuvant radiotherapy to improve local control given the high recurrence rate, particularly for high-grade or marginally resected tumours","Reserve anthracycline-based systemic chemotherapy (doxorubicin with or without ifosfamide) for high-grade, large or metastatic disease","Manage all cases through a specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team with dedicated soft-tissue pathology and MRI margin planning","Undertake structured surveillance for local recurrence with clinical review and MRI, and for pulmonary metastases with chest imaging, intensified in high-grade tumours","Provide limb-preserving reconstruction and rehabilitation after resection, considering re-excision or amputation for repeated recurrence","Treat metastatic disease including metastasectomy for isolated pulmonary deposits, with palliative care input where appropriate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"myxoid_liposarcoma":{"name":"Myxoid liposarcoma","system":"Musculoskeletal / Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MLS","Myxoid/round cell liposarcoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Second most common liposarcoma subtype, arising typically in the deep soft tissues of the thigh or popliteal fossa in young and middle-aged adults.","Defined by a recurrent FUS-DDIT3 fusion from t(12;16), with a small subset showing EWSR1-DDIT3; demonstrating this fusion confirms the diagnosis.","A higher proportion of hypercellular round-cell morphology denotes higher grade and worse prognosis; the round-cell component is the aggressive part of the spectrum.","Distinctively metastasises to unusual extrapulmonary soft-tissue and bone sites (spine, other soft tissues, retroperitoneum) as well as to the lungs.","Because of the propensity for spinal and soft-tissue metastases, whole-body MRI or dedicated axial imaging is often used for staging and surveillance.","Classic histology shows uniform round to oval cells in a myxoid stroma with a delicate plexiform (chicken-wire) capillary network and signet-ring lipoblasts.","Notably radiosensitive; neoadjuvant radiotherapy can produce marked tumour response and is frequently combined with wide surgical excision.","Sensitive to certain systemic agents such as trabectedin, which targets the fusion-driven transcriptional programme."],"pathophysiology":"Myxoid liposarcoma is driven by a balanced translocation, most often t(12;16)(q13;p11), that fuses FUS with DDIT3 (CHOP); a minority carry an EWSR1-DDIT3 fusion. The chimeric protein acts as an aberrant transcription factor that dysregulates adipocytic differentiation by interfering with CCAAT/enhancer-binding proteins, arresting cells at an immature preadipocyte stage and promoting proliferation. The fusion also perturbs downstream pathways and cooperates with secondary events, notably activating alterations in the PI3K/AKT pathway (such as PIK3CA mutations and PTEN loss) and TERT promoter changes, which correlate with the transition to the hypercellular round-cell phenotype and more aggressive behaviour. The tumour's rich, delicate plexiform capillary vasculature and abundant myxoid matrix are characteristic and underlie its imaging appearance, sometimes mimicking a cyst. The distinctive tropism for extrapulmonary soft-tissue and bony metastatic sites reflects biology particular to this fusion-driven sarcoma rather than the lung-dominant spread of most sarcomas.","additionalDifferentials":["Well-differentiated / dedifferentiated liposarcoma","Pleomorphic liposarcoma","Myxofibrosarcoma","Low-grade fibromyxoid sarcoma","Extraskeletal myxoid chondrosarcoma","Intramuscular myxoma","Myxoid change within other soft-tissue sarcomas"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the primary site to assess fat content, myxoid matrix and local extent","Core needle biopsy with dedicated soft-tissue pathology review","FISH or RT-PCR for DDIT3 rearrangement (FUS-DDIT3 / EWSR1-DDIT3)","Histological quantification of the round-cell component for grading","Whole-body MRI or dedicated spinal imaging given extrapulmonary metastatic tropism","CT of the chest for pulmonary metastases","Assessment for suitability of neoadjuvant radiotherapy at a sarcoma centre"],"redFlags":["A large or rapidly growing deep thigh mass in a young or middle-aged adult","New back pain or neurological symptoms suggesting spinal metastasis","Increasing round-cell content on repeat pathology indicating higher grade","Development of metastases at unusual soft-tissue or bony sites","Local recurrence after resection during surveillance"],"prognosis":"Pure or predominantly myxoid, low-grade tumours have a relatively favourable outcome, whereas a substantial round-cell (hypercellular) component markedly worsens prognosis and metastatic risk. Overall survival depends on grade, tumour size and metastatic status, and late relapses at extrapulmonary sites can occur.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the myxoid, low-density imaging appearance for a benign cyst or myxoma and delaying biopsy.","Failing to quantify the round-cell component, which is essential for grading and prognosis.","Staging with chest CT alone and missing the characteristic spinal and soft-tissue metastases.","Overlooking the tumour's radiosensitivity, which influences the sequencing of neoadjuvant therapy.","Confusing it with other myxoid soft-tissue tumours without molecular confirmation of the DDIT3 fusion."],"management":["Perform wide surgical excision of the primary tumour with clear margins as the mainstay of curative treatment","Deliver neoadjuvant or adjuvant radiotherapy, exploiting the marked radiosensitivity of this subtype to improve local control and enable downsizing","Consider systemic therapy with anthracycline-based chemotherapy or trabectedin for round-cell-rich, large or metastatic disease","Grade by quantifying the round-cell component, which drives systemic treatment decisions and prognosis","Stage and survey with whole-body or dedicated spinal MRI plus chest CT, given the tropism for extrapulmonary soft-tissue, bone and spinal metastases","Manage through a specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team with dedicated soft-tissue pathology confirming the FUS-DDIT3 fusion","Maintain prolonged surveillance for late and extrapulmonary relapse, with metastasectomy or targeted radiotherapy for oligometastatic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Soft-tissue sarcoma clinical practice guidelines","Standard surgical and radiation oncology references for sarcoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"n_acetylglutamate_synthase_deficiency":{"name":"N-acetylglutamate synthase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NAGS deficiency","N-acetylglutamate synthetase deficiency","NAGS","hyperammonaemia due to NAGS deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rarest of the urea cycle disorders, caused by biallelic NAGS variants","NAGS makes N-acetylglutamate, the obligatory allosteric activator of carbamoyl phosphate synthetase 1, so its loss shuts down the first committed step of ureagenesis","Biochemically resembles CPS1 deficiency: hyperammonaemia with low/normal citrulline, low arginine and a characteristically low urine orotic acid","Presents as neonatal hyperammonaemic encephalopathy, or later with episodic hyperammonaemia and neurocognitive symptoms","The key distinguishing feature is its responsiveness to carglumic acid, a synthetic N-acetylglutamate analogue that restores CPS1 activity","Low or absent orotic aciduria helps separate proximal urea cycle defects (NAGS, CPS1) from OTC deficiency","Confirm with NAGS gene sequencing and by demonstrating a therapeutic response to carglumic acid","Acute management is emergency ammonia lowering (dialysis if needed) with carglumic acid, nitrogen scavengers, arginine and protein restriction"],"pathophysiology":"N-acetylglutamate synthase catalyses formation of N-acetylglutamate from glutamate and acetyl-CoA. N-acetylglutamate is the essential allosteric activator of carbamoyl phosphate synthetase 1 (CPS1), the enzyme that initiates the urea cycle by incorporating ammonia into carbamoyl phosphate. In NAGS deficiency the activator is not produced, so CPS1 remains inactive and the urea cycle cannot begin, leading to accumulation of ammonia. Because the block is at the very first step, downstream intermediates such as citrulline and arginine are low, and carbamoyl phosphate is not diverted into pyrimidine synthesis, so orotic acid excretion stays low (mirroring CPS1 deficiency and contrasting with OTC deficiency). Uniquely, the missing activator can be replaced pharmacologically by carglumic acid, which reactivates CPS1. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase 1 deficiency (biochemically near-identical)","Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency (raised orotic acid, X-linked)","Citrullinaemia type 1 and argininosuccinic aciduria","Organic acidaemias causing secondary hyperammonaemia","Fatty acid oxidation disorders with hyperammonaemia","Transient hyperammonaemia of the newborn","Neonatal sepsis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent plasma ammonia during suspected crises","Plasma amino acids (low/normal citrulline, low arginine, raised glutamine)","Urine orotic acid (characteristically low, separating from OTC deficiency)","Blood gas showing respiratory alkalosis of hyperammonaemia","NAGS gene sequencing for confirmation","Documented biochemical response to a carglumic acid trial","Plasma acylcarnitines/organic acids to exclude secondary hyperammonaemia"],"redFlags":["Neonatal hyperammonaemic coma requiring emergency ammonia removal","Cerebral oedema from severe hyperammonaemia","Rapidly rising ammonia with encephalopathy at any age","Decompensation triggered by catabolic illness, high protein load or surgery","Failure of ammonia to fall despite standard scavenger therapy (prompting carglumic acid)"],"prognosis":"NAGS deficiency is one of the few urea cycle disorders with a specific pharmacological treatment; with early diagnosis and carglumic acid therapy long-term metabolic control and outcome can be excellent, whereas delayed treatment of severe neonatal hyperammonaemia risks permanent neurological injury.","pitfalls":["Not distinguishing NAGS from CPS1 deficiency and thereby missing a treatable disorder","Failing to trial carglumic acid, which is specifically effective in NAGS deficiency","Overlooking the low orotic acid clue that points to a proximal urea cycle defect","Delaying emergency ammonia lowering while awaiting confirmatory genetics","Assuming carglumic acid is equally effective in all urea cycle disorders"],"management":["Give carglumic acid as specific disease-directed therapy, a synthetic N-acetylglutamate analogue that restores carbamoyl phosphate synthetase 1 activity and can normalise ammonia","Institute a protein-restricted diet with adequate energy intake and essential amino-acid supplementation to limit ammonia generation","Treat acute hyperammonaemic crises by stopping protein intake, giving high-calorie glucose (with insulin if needed) to reverse catabolism, and intravenous carglumic acid with nitrogen-scavenger support","Escalate to haemodialysis or haemofiltration for severe or refractory hyperammonaemia to remove ammonia rapidly","Monitor plasma ammonia, amino acids and growth long term, and avoid catabolic triggers such as fasting, infection and the perioperative state","Provide an emergency regimen and sick-day plan with prompt hospital access during intercurrent illness","Offer genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance, with antenatal or newborn diagnostic testing, and coordinate care through a metabolic medicine team with specialist dietetic input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","Saudubray, Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"nabothian_cyst":{"name":"Nabothian cyst","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nabothian cyst","nabothian follicle","cervical retention cyst","mucinous retention cyst of the cervix"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology textbook and colposcopy guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A nabothian cyst is a benign mucus-filled retention cyst on the cervix formed when the opening of an endocervical gland becomes covered by squamous epithelium during normal metaplasia","They are extremely common, usually multiple, and almost always asymptomatic incidental findings on speculum examination or pelvic imaging","Typical appearance is one or more smooth, translucent or yellowish-white raised nodules on the ectocervix, often over the transformation zone","They require no treatment when characteristic and asymptomatic, and reassurance is the mainstay of management","On ultrasound they appear as well-defined cystic spaces within the cervix and can occasionally be large or deep, prompting unnecessary concern if misinterpreted","Rarely, very large or numerous deep cysts (so-called tunnel clusters) can be mistaken on imaging for a cervical neoplasm, so correlation with the benign clinical picture is important","Intervention such as marsupialisation or excision is only considered if a cyst is unusually large, symptomatic, or the diagnosis is uncertain","Cervical screening should continue as normal, since nabothian cysts themselves carry no malignant potential"],"pathophysiology":"The cervix undergoes continuous squamous metaplasia at the transformation zone, where columnar endocervical epithelium is progressively replaced by squamous epithelium. When advancing squamous epithelium grows over the mouth of a mucus-secreting endocervical gland, the gland's outflow is obstructed and mucus is retained, distending it into a smooth cyst. The process is entirely physiological and benign, and the cysts have no relationship to infection or malignancy. Their main clinical relevance is avoiding misdiagnosis, since deep or clustered cysts can superficially resemble other cervical lesions on imaging.","additionalDifferentials":["Cervical polyp","Endometriosis of the cervix","Mesonephric or other benign cervical cyst","Adenoma malignum or minimal-deviation adenocarcinoma (rare, when deep cysts are atypical)","Cervical malignancy","Cervical fibroid"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Speculum examination to identify characteristic smooth cervical cysts","Transvaginal ultrasound where imaging characterisation is needed","Colposcopy if the appearance is atypical or screening is abnormal","Magnetic resonance imaging for unusually large or deep cystic lesions to reassure and exclude neoplasia","Cervical cytology and human papillomavirus testing as part of routine screening"],"redFlags":["Atypical, rapidly enlarging, or irregular cervical lesion that could represent malignancy","Abnormal bleeding, particularly postcoital or postmenopausal, warranting further assessment","Deep or extensive cystic change on imaging that raises the question of adenoma malignum","Abnormal cervical screening results"],"prognosis":"Nabothian cysts are benign with an excellent prognosis, requiring no treatment in the vast majority of cases and carrying no risk of malignant transformation.","pitfalls":["Over-investigating or excising a characteristic asymptomatic cyst unnecessarily","Misinterpreting deep or clustered cysts on imaging as cervical malignancy","Attributing abnormal bleeding to a nabothian cyst and failing to exclude other cervical pathology","Neglecting routine cervical screening because a benign cyst is present"],"management":["Reassure that the lesion is a benign mucus-retention cyst requiring no treatment when the appearance is characteristic and asymptomatic","Confirm the benign nature on speculum examination and correlate with routine cervical screening cytology and human papillomavirus testing","Reserve transvaginal ultrasound or MRI for unusually large, deep or clustered (tunnel-cluster) cysts to distinguish them from cervical neoplasia","Perform colposcopy if the appearance is atypical or screening is abnormal","Consider marsupialisation, electrocautery or excision only in the rare symptomatic case with discomfort or discharge","Avoid unnecessary intervention or over-investigation once the benign diagnosis is secure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference text on benign cervical lesions","Colposcopy and cervical screening guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"naegeli_franceschetti_jadassohn_syndrome":{"name":"Naegeli-Franceschetti-Jadassohn syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NFJS","KRT14","keratin 14","Naegeli syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An ectodermal dysplasia characterised by reticulate (net-like) hyperpigmentation that, unusually, is not preceded by an inflammatory or vesicular stage","Absent or markedly reduced dermatoglyphics (loss of fingerprints) is a distinctive and helpful sign","Diffuse palmoplantar keratoderma and reduced sweating (hypohidrosis) with consequent heat intolerance are typical","Dental anomalies, including yellow-brown discolouration and early tooth loss, and nail dystrophy are common","Autosomal dominant; caused by mutations in KRT14 encoding keratin 14","The reticulate pigmentation tends to fade with age, in contrast to some other pigmentary genodermatoses","Management is supportive with emollients/keratolytics, heat-avoidance measures for hypohidrosis, and dental care"],"pathophysiology":"Naegeli-Franceschetti-Jadassohn syndrome is an autosomal dominant ectodermal dysplasia caused by mutations in KRT14, which encodes keratin 14, a basal keratinocyte intermediate-filament protein that pairs with keratin 5 to maintain the mechanical integrity of the basal epidermis. The disease-causing mutations affect the non-helical head domain of keratin 14 and are thought to disturb keratin-network function and increase basal keratinocyte apoptosis, with resultant transfer of melanin/pigment into the dermis producing the reticulate hyperpigmentation. The same defect disturbs the development of epidermal appendages, giving hypohidrosis (from reduced or abnormal sweat glands), palmoplantar keratoderma, nail dystrophy, dental abnormalities and the absence of dermatoglyphics. (Mutations in KRT14 that abolish the protein instead cause the allelic dermatopathia pigmentosa reticularis.)","additionalDifferentials":["Dermatopathia pigmentosa reticularis (allelic KRT14 disorder)","Incontinentia pigmenti (which has preceding inflammatory/vesicular stages)","Dyskeratosis congenita","Franceschetti-Jadassohn variants / other reticulate pigmentary disorders","Dowling-Degos disease","Other hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of KRT14","Examination for absent dermatoglyphics (fingerprint loss)","Skin biopsy showing pigment incontinence without significant inflammation","Assessment of sweating and heat tolerance","Dental and nail evaluation","Family history and pedigree analysis"],"redFlags":["Hyperthermia or heat intolerance from hypohidrosis, particularly in infancy","Severe early dental disease requiring intervention","Functionally limiting palmoplantar keratoderma","Features suggesting an alternative diagnosis with systemic risk (e.g. dyskeratosis congenita)"],"prognosis":"The condition is compatible with a normal lifespan and general health; the reticulate pigmentation characteristically fades with age. The main ongoing issues are heat intolerance from hypohidrosis, keratoderma and dental problems, all of which are manageable with supportive measures.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with incontinentia pigmenti, which has vesicular and verrucous stages preceding pigmentation","Overlooking the diagnostically useful absence of fingerprints","Failing to warn about heat intolerance and to advise cooling measures","Not distinguishing it from the allelic dermatopathia pigmentosa reticularis"],"management":["Manage hypohidrosis and heat intolerance by avoiding overheating, using cooling measures and maintaining adequate hydration in hot conditions","Treat palmoplantar keratoderma with regular emollients and topical keratolytics such as urea- or salicylic-acid-based preparations","Provide comprehensive dental care with restorative and prosthetic management for enamel defects and early tooth loss","Address nail dystrophy and any secondary infection with supportive dermatological care","Reassure that the reticulate hyperpigmentation is benign and tends to fade with age, offering cosmetic camouflage if desired","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant KRT14 disease with confirmatory molecular testing and family assessment","Coordinate multidisciplinary follow-up across dermatology, dentistry and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"naevus_anaemicus":{"name":"naevus anaemicus","system":"Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nevus anemicus","pharmacological naevus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital vascular anomaly rather than a true pigmentary disorder, appearing as a pale, well-demarcated macule or patch usually on the upper trunk.","The pallor is functional and reflects a localised hypersensitivity of dermal vessels to catecholamines causing persistent vasoconstriction, not a deficiency of melanin.","Diascopy (pressure with a glass slide) causes the lesion to blend indistinguishably with the surrounding blanched skin, confirming the vascular basis.","Rubbing or warming the area produces reactive erythema in the normal surrounding skin but not within the naevus, sharply outlining the lesion.","Wood's lamp examination does not accentuate the border, because there is no melanin abnormality, helping distinguish it from hypomelanotic lesions.","Injection of an alpha-adrenergic blocker or heating abolishes the pallor, underscoring the pharmacological (catecholamine-mediated) mechanism.","It is associated with neurofibromatosis type 1 and may be an early cutaneous marker, and can also co-occur with tuberous sclerosis and phacomatosis pigmentovascularis.","The lesion is stable throughout life and of cosmetic significance only."],"pathophysiology":"Naevus anaemicus is a congenital functional vascular defect in which the cutaneous blood vessels within a circumscribed area exhibit exaggerated sensitivity to circulating and locally released catecholamines. This heightened alpha-adrenergic responsiveness maintains the affected vessels in a state of near-permanent vasoconstriction, reducing local blood flow and producing the characteristic pallor against normally perfused surrounding skin. The vessels are structurally normal, and there is no abnormality of melanocytes, melanin, or vessel number; the phenomenon is therefore pharmacological rather than anatomical or pigmentary. Evidence for this mechanism includes the abolition of pallor by intradermal alpha-adrenergic blockade, by warming, and by physical trauma that would normally provoke a vasodilatory flare, and its persistence despite normal vascular architecture. Because there is no melanin defect, the lesion neither fluoresces abnormally under Wood's lamp nor blanches distinctly on diascopy.","additionalDifferentials":["Naevus depigmentosus","Vitiligo","Ash-leaf macule of tuberous sclerosis","Pityriasis alba","Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation","Hypopigmented segmental lesions of mosaicism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diascopy demonstrating disappearance of the lesion margin under glass pressure","Rubbing or warming test showing surrounding erythema that spares the lesion","Wood's lamp examination confirming absence of pigmentary accentuation","Examination for café-au-lait macules, neurofibromas, and other features of neurofibromatosis type 1","Dermoscopy showing reduced or absent background vascular erythema","Skin biopsy (rarely required) confirming normal melanocytes and vasculature"],"redFlags":["Multiple café-au-lait macules or neurofibromas suggesting associated neurofibromatosis type 1","Coexisting vascular and pigmentary naevi indicating phacomatosis pigmentovascularis","Features of tuberous sclerosis accompanying the lesion","Any progressive change, which would be atypical for this stable congenital anomaly"],"prognosis":"Naevus anaemicus is entirely benign and permanent, remaining stable throughout life with no risk of malignant transformation. Its main clinical value is as a potential early cutaneous marker prompting evaluation for neurofibromatosis type 1.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the pallor as a hypomelanotic or depigmenting disorder and pursuing unnecessary pigmentary work-up","Omitting diascopy and the rub test, which are the simplest confirmatory bedside manoeuvres","Expecting Wood's lamp accentuation and doubting the diagnosis when none occurs","Failing to examine for neurofibromatosis type 1 when a naevus anaemicus is identified","Attempting active treatment of an inherently stable, benign vascular anomaly"],"management":["Reassure that the lesion is a benign, stable functional vascular anomaly requiring no active treatment","Confirm the diagnosis clinically with diascopy and the rubbing or warming test rather than resorting to biopsy","Evaluate for neurofibromatosis type 1, since naevus anaemicus can be an early cutaneous marker, examining for cafe-au-lait macules, neurofibromas and other stigmata","Arrange neurocutaneous and genetic assessment with family-history review where features of neurofibromatosis type 1 coexist","Offer cosmetic camouflage for troublesome lesions, as no medical treatment alters the pallor","Provide follow-up focused on the associated neurofibromatosis risk rather than the naevus itself"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine, current edition","Bolognia Dermatology, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"naevus_depigmentosus":{"name":"naevus depigmentosus","system":"Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["naevus achromicus","nevus depigmentosus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital, stable, well-demarcated hypopigmented (not depigmented) macule or patch that is usually solitary and often noted at or shortly after birth.","Three clinical patterns are recognised: an isolated localised form, a segmental or dermatomal form, and a rarer systematised (whorled/blaschkoid) form.","Under Wood's lamp the lesion appears off-white or dull rather than the chalky bright-white accentuation seen in vitiligo, reflecting reduced but not absent melanin.","The lesion is stable relative to body growth and does not spread or repigment, with borders that are frequently serrated or irregular.","Melanocyte numbers are normal or only slightly reduced; the defect lies in abnormal melanosome morphology and impaired transfer to keratinocytes.","Overlying hair may be hypopigmented within the patch, a helpful supportive sign.","Multiple or systematised lesions warrant evaluation for associated neurocutaneous or developmental abnormalities.","Differentiation from a single ash-leaf macule of tuberous sclerosis is critical, as management and prognosis differ substantially."],"pathophysiology":"Naevus depigmentosus is best regarded as a localised functional defect of pigmentation rather than an absence of melanocytes. Electron microscopy demonstrates a normal or near-normal number of melanocytes containing aggregated, abnormally structured melanosomes and reduced melanosome transfer to surrounding keratinocytes; the melanocytes show stunted dendrites. The result is a stable region of hypomelanosis in which some residual pigment remains, distinguishing it clinically and on Wood's lamp from the total melanocyte loss of vitiligo. The mosaic segmental and blaschkoid distributions are thought to reflect post-zygotic somatic mutation affecting a clone of melanocytic precursors, producing pigmentary mosaicism along the lines of Blaschko. Because the underlying melanocytes are present and viable, the lesion neither progresses nor responds to the immunomodulatory therapies used for vitiligo.","additionalDifferentials":["Segmental vitiligo","Ash-leaf macule of tuberous sclerosis","Naevus anaemicus","Hypomelanosis of Ito","Pityriasis alba","Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation","Piebaldism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wood's lamp examination showing off-white rather than chalky-white fluorescence","Dermoscopy demonstrating faint, ill-defined pigment network with irregular borders","Skin biopsy with electron microscopy showing normal melanocyte numbers and abnormal melanosomes (rarely needed)","Neurocutaneous evaluation and neuroimaging if systematised or associated with neurological signs","Ophthalmological and developmental assessment when pigmentary mosaicism is extensive","Serial photography to confirm stability over time"],"redFlags":["Multiple hypopigmented macules with seizures or developmental delay suggesting tuberous sclerosis","Progressive enlargement or new depigmentation inconsistent with a stable congenital naevus","Extensive systematised lesions accompanied by neurological or skeletal abnormalities","Associated ocular or dental anomalies indicating a broader mosaic syndrome"],"prognosis":"Naevus depigmentosus is a benign, lifelong, and stable condition of purely cosmetic significance in the localised form, with no risk of malignant change. The main clinical importance lies in correctly distinguishing it from tuberous sclerosis and vitiligo to avoid unnecessary anxiety and treatment.","pitfalls":["Confusing a solitary lesion with an ash-leaf macule and either missing or wrongly attributing tuberous sclerosis","Misdiagnosing it as segmental vitiligo and prescribing futile depigmentation-targeted therapy","Expecting Wood's lamp to show the bright accentuation typical of vitiligo and thereby doubting the diagnosis","Failing to recognise systematised forms as pigmentary mosaicism warranting further assessment","Reassuring about stability without documenting the lesion photographically at baseline"],"management":["Reassure that this is a benign, stable, lifelong hypomelanotic lesion of cosmetic significance only, with no malignant potential","Confirm the diagnosis clinically with Wood's-lamp examination showing off-white rather than chalky-white change, and serial photography to demonstrate stability","Distinguish from the ash-leaf macules of tuberous sclerosis and from vitiligo to avoid unnecessary anxiety and treatment","Arrange neurocutaneous evaluation, neuroimaging and developmental and ophthalmological assessment when the lesion is systematised or extensive (pigmentary mosaicism)","Offer photoprotection of the hypopigmented skin and cosmetic camouflage, discussing the limited role of pigment-cell transplantation for selected cosmetically important lesions","Provide reassurance-based follow-up without active dermatological treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine, current edition","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"nafld":{"name":"MASLD / NAFLD (Fatty Liver Disease)","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NAFLD","NASH","MASLD","MASH","fatty liver"],"chapter":354,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2700","clinicalPearls":["Steatotic liver disease (SLD) is the most common cause of chronic liver disease in the US and worldwide; metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) and steatohepatitis (MASH) now replace the older terms NAFLD and NASH, with ~98% population overlap (Harrison 22e p.2700-2701).","Diagnosis of MASLD requires hepatic steatosis plus little/no alcohol intake (<20 g/d women, <30 g/d men) and at least one cardiometabolic risk factor (overweight/obesity, hypertension, dyslipidemia, insulin resistance/T2DM) (Harrison 22e p.2700, 2703).","Most patients are asymptomatic; MASLD is typically detected incidentally via raised ALT or steatosis on imaging done for other reasons (Harrison 22e p.2702-2703).","Disease spans a dynamic, potentially reversible spectrum: simple steatosis (MASL) -> steatohepatitis (MASH) -> fibrosis -> cirrhosis/HCC; chronic steatohepatitis is the primary driver of fibrosis, and fibrosis severity is the chief predictor of liver-related morbidity/mortality (Harrison 22e p.2700-2701, 2704).","FIB-4 (age, AST, ALT, platelets) is the recommended first-line noninvasive score with excellent negative predictive value: FIB-4 <1.3 = low risk (manage in primary care), >=2.67 = moderate/high risk warranting a secondary test (elastography or ELF) (Harrison 22e p.2704-2705).","T2DM is the strongest risk factor for MASLD acquisition, fibrosis progression, and HCC; the MASLD-T2DM relationship is bidirectional, so patients with MASLD should be screened for T2DM (Harrison 22e p.2703).","Foundational treatment is lifestyle modification with weight loss: ~5% weight loss improves steatosis, while >10% improves MASH and can reverse fibrosis; cardiovascular risk reduction and treatment of metabolic comorbidities apply to all patients (Harrison 22e p.2706).","Statins are safe across the MASLD spectrum and indicated for dyslipidemia given high cardiovascular risk; reluctance to use them is not supported by evidence of liver harm (Harrison 22e p.2707)."],"pathophysiology":"MASLD arises from imbalance between energy intake and expenditure: excess caloric intake, reduced physical activity, and limited peripheral adipose expansion drive visceral adiposity, insulin resistance, and metabolic dysfunction. Hepatocyte triglyceride accumulates from increased free fatty acid delivery (dietary uptake, adipose lipolysis, de novo lipogenesis), impaired fatty acid disposal, or impaired triglyceride export; triglyceride storage itself is relatively safe, so steatosis per se does not damage the liver but is a biomarker of metabolic dysfunction. When adaptive mechanisms are overwhelmed, lipotoxicity from dysfunctional lipid handling injures and kills hepatocytes, producing steatohepatitis (MASH) with inflammation and ballooned hepatocytes. Repeated/protracted MASH triggers regenerative responses that become maladaptive, promoting progressive fibrosis (replacement of functional parenchyma by scar) and primary liver cancer; cirrhosis represents futile repair. Heritable factors (e.g., PNPLA3, TM6SF2, MBOAT7 variants), epigenetic influences, gut microbiota alterations, and environmental factors (high-fat/high-sugar diets) modify susceptibility (Harrison 22e p.2701-2702).","additionalDifferentials":["Alcohol-associated liver disease (ALD) and the intermediate MetALD category (metabolic dysfunction with social alcohol use, 20-50 g/d women, 30-60 g/d men)","Hepatitis C, particularly genotype 3","Autoimmune hepatitis","Wilson disease","Hemochromatosis / iron overload","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Inborn errors of metabolism (abetalipoproteinemia, cholesterol ester storage disease, galactosemia, glycogen storage disease, hereditary fructose intolerance, homocystinuria, systemic carnitine deficiency, tyrosinemia)","Weber-Christian syndrome"],"infectionMimics":["Chronic hepatitis C (genotype 3 causes hepatic steatosis)","Chronic hepatitis B"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Alcohol-associated liver disease (histologically can be indistinguishable; alcohol intake must be excluded)","Autoimmune hepatitis (note ~20% of steatotic liver disease patients have a positive ANA)","Medication/drug-associated steatosis or steatohepatitis","Wilson disease, hemochromatosis, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum aminotransferases (ALT/AST) - true normal ALT is ~29-33 U/L (men) and ~19-25 U/L (women); normal liver enzymes do NOT exclude MASLD","FIB-4 score (age, AST, ALT, platelet count) - first-line noninvasive fibrosis risk score","Vibration-controlled transient elastography / liver stiffness measurement (VCTE <8 kPa low, >12 kPa high; LSM >12 kPa associated with cirrhosis)","Enhanced Liver Fibrosis (ELF) blood test (ELF >=9.8 increased risk; >11.8 suspect cirrhosis)","Magnetic resonance elastography (MRE) and cT1 for refined risk stratification","Abdominal ultrasound / cross-sectional imaging for steatosis","Workup to exclude other causes: viral hepatitis serologies, autoimmune markers, iron/copper studies, alpha-1 antitrypsin","Screening for T2DM; assessment of metabolic syndrome components","Liver biopsy - historical gold standard for staging, reserved for indeterminate/discordant NITs, diagnostic uncertainty, or suspected concurrent chronic hepatitis (limited by sampling and observer variability)"],"redFlags":["FIB-4 >=2.67 (moderate/high risk for advanced fibrosis - needs secondary testing/specialist referral)","Fibrosis stage >=F2 = 'at-risk MASH' with increased risk of further progression and adverse liver outcomes","Persistently elevated AST and ALT","Stigmata of advanced disease: spider angiomata, palmar erythema, splenomegaly, thrombocytopenia, clubbing, ascites, encephalopathy","Features of decompensation/end-stage disease: jaundice, ascites, variceal hemorrhage","Clinical/radiologic suspicion of cirrhosis or ELF >11.8"],"prognosis":"Variable and stage-dependent. The 10-year probability of liver-related morbidity/mortality is negligible with steatosis alone, so this group is managed conservatively. Fibrosis severity is the primary predictor of outcome: average fibrosis progression is roughly one stage every 14 years in steatosis versus one stage every 7 years in MASH; once at stage >=F2 ('at-risk MASH') the risk of further progression and bad outcomes rises. Once MASLD-related cirrhosis develops, progression to decompensation runs 3-20% per year and annual primary liver cancer incidence can reach 1-2% per year, prompting biannual HCC surveillance and variceal screening. Cirrhosis can regress, and MASLD outcomes after liver transplantation are generally good in well-selected patients, though comorbid diabetes, obesity, and CVD limit candidacy and disease may recur post-transplant. MASLD is also a premalignant condition increasing risk of primary and extrahepatic malignancy even in noncirrhotic livers (Harrison 22e p.2701, 2704, 2708).","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude significant alcohol intake and other chronic liver diseases (viral, autoimmune, iron/copper overload, alpha-1 antitrypsin) before attributing steatosis to MASLD.","Assuming normal liver enzymes exclude MASLD - they do not; normal ALT 'reference ranges' are inflated by including people with undiagnosed liver disease.","Relying on liver tests for staging - aminotransferases, alkaline phosphatase, bilirubin, albumin, and PT may all be completely normal even with significant disease.","MASLD can occur in lean individuals (BMI <25, or <23 in Asians), especially with truncal/visceral adiposity, so a normal BMI does not exclude it.","FIB-4 has reduced accuracy in patients >65 and <35 years and is a less robust predictor in T2DM, so a secondary noninvasive test is needed in these groups.","Liver biopsy is limited by sampling error, inter/intraobserver variability (worse with cores <2 cm), and gives only a static snapshot of disease activity."],"management":["Institute lifestyle intervention as the cornerstone, with sustained weight loss through calorie restriction and increased physical activity to reverse the underlying metabolic dysfunction","Optimise cardiometabolic drivers by controlling type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia and hypertension, and treating obesity with GLP-1 receptor agonists and, in eligible patients, metabolic (bariatric) surgery","Consider disease-specific pharmacotherapy for confirmed steatohepatitis with fibrosis, notably the thyroid-hormone-receptor-beta agonist resmetirom, with GLP-1 agonists, pioglitazone or vitamin E as options in selected patients","Advise alcohol minimisation and provide hepatitis immunisation to limit additional liver injury","Risk-stratify and monitor fibrosis with FIB-4 and elastography, referring higher-risk patients from primary care to hepatology","Surveil for complications of advanced fibrosis and cirrhosis, including hepatocellular carcinoma screening and variceal surveillance, and screen for coexisting type 2 diabetes","Prioritise cardiovascular risk reduction as the leading cause of death, and refer for liver transplantation assessment in decompensated cirrhosis"],"severityClassification":"Histopathologic/fibrosis staging spectrum: simple steatosis (MASL) -> steatohepatitis (MASH) -> fibrosis -> cirrhosis. Fibrosis stages F0 (none), F1 (minimal), F2 (significant), F3 (advanced/bridging), F4 (cirrhosis, further stratified into compensated and decompensated). 'At-risk MASH' = MASH with fibrosis stage >=F2. Noninvasive risk stratification: FIB-4 <1.3 low risk, >=1.3 intermediate, >=2.67 moderate/high; secondary tests VCTE <8 kPa low / 8-12 intermediate / >12 high, ELF <7.7 low / 7.7-9.8 intermediate / >9.8 high (Harrison 22e p.2704-2705).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2700","Harrison 22e p.2701","Harrison 22e p.2702","Harrison 22e p.2703","Harrison 22e p.2704","Harrison 22e p.2705","Harrison 22e p.2706","Harrison 22e p.2707","Harrison 22e p.2708"],"crossLinks":["obesity","diabetes_mellitus","decomp_cirrhosis","alcohol_liver_disease"]},"nagashima_type_keratoderma":{"name":"Nagashima-type palmoplantar keratoderma","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NPPK","palmoplantar keratoderma Nagashima type","SERPINB7"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A relatively mild, non-progressive diffuse palmoplantar keratoderma most common in East Asian populations","Caused by biallelic variants in SERPINB7, a serine protease inhibitor expressed in the stratum corneum","A characteristic feature is transient whitening and spongy swelling of the affected skin on water immersion, resembling an exaggerated aquagenic wrinkling","The keratoderma is transgredient, extending onto the dorsa of the hands and feet, the Achilles region and the wrists, often with erythematous margins","Hyperhidrosis and malodour with secondary dermatophyte and pitted-keratolysis-type infection are common","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by the water-immersion sign and SERPINB7 molecular testing, and it is distinguished from the more severe Mal de Meleda","Management is supportive with keratolytics, emollients, antiperspirants and treatment of secondary infection"],"pathophysiology":"Nagashima-type palmoplantar keratoderma is caused by loss of function of SERPINB7, a serine protease inhibitor that helps regulate proteolytic activity within the stratum corneum. Its deficiency is thought to impair the water-barrier properties of the cornified layer, so that acral skin absorbs water abnormally and swells and whitens on immersion, and terminal keratinocyte processing is disturbed to give a diffuse transgredient keratoderma. The compromised barrier and associated hyperhidrosis favour secondary microbial colonisation and malodour.","additionalDifferentials":["Mal de Meleda","Diffuse non-epidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma (Unna-Thost)","Aquagenic palmoplantar keratoderma / aquagenic wrinkling","Cystic fibrosis-associated aquagenic wrinkling","Olmsted syndrome","Pachyonychia congenita"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of SERPINB7","Water-immersion test to demonstrate whitening and swelling","Skin biopsy showing orthohyperkeratosis","Mycological examination for dermatophyte infection","Bacterial swabs where malodour or pitting suggests secondary infection","Family history and pedigree analysis"],"redFlags":["Marked functional impairment or painful fissuring of the soles","Recurrent or resistant secondary infection","Features atypical for the mild course suggesting an alternative, more severe keratoderma","Rapidly progressive thickening warranting reassessment"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and non-progressive; with regular keratolytic and emollient care and attention to secondary infection, most patients maintain good function and quality of life.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with the more severe and potentially mutilating Mal de Meleda","Overlooking the diagnostic water-immersion whitening sign","Neglecting the treatable dermatophyte and pitted-keratolysis-type infections driving malodour","Assuming progression and prescribing aggressive systemic therapy unnecessarily"],"management":["Manage the keratoderma with regular emollients and topical keratolytics such as urea- or salicylic-acid-based preparations as the mainstay","Control hyperhidrosis and malodour with antiperspirants and meticulous skin hygiene","Treat secondary dermatophyte infection with topical or, where extensive, oral antifungals, and pitted-keratolysis-type bacterial infection with topical antibacterials","Advise on appropriate footwear, moisture management and avoidance of prolonged water immersion to reduce maceration","Confirm the diagnosis and distinguish it from the more severe Mal de Meleda using the water-immersion sign and SERPINB7 molecular testing","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance with family assessment","Reassure that the condition is benign and non-progressive, with dermatology follow-up as required"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"nager_syndrome":{"name":"Nager acrofacial dysostosis (SF3B4)","system":"Genetics / Craniofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Nager syndrome","preaxial acrofacial dysostosis","acrofacial dysostosis type 1","Nager-de Reynier syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Combines a mandibulofacial dysostosis resembling Treacher Collins with preaxial (thumb-side) upper limb defects","Facial features include malar and mandibular hypoplasia, down-slanting palpebral fissures, and external ear anomalies","The distinguishing limb finding is preaxial radial ray involvement, with hypoplastic, absent, or triphalangeal thumbs and radial deficiency","Severe micrognathia frequently causes neonatal airway obstruction and feeding difficulty","Cleft palate and velopharyngeal problems are common and contribute to speech difficulty","Most cases are caused by variants in SF3B4, a component of the spliceosome, usually arising de novo","Cognition is generally preserved, so early airway and hearing management strongly influences outcome"],"pathophysiology":"Nager syndrome is a spliceosomopathy, most often caused by loss-of-function variants in SF3B4, which encodes a protein of the U2 small nuclear ribonucleoprotein complex essential for pre-messenger RNA splicing. Disturbed splicing during embryogenesis is thought to impair the proliferation and survival of cranial neural crest cells populating the first and second branchial arches, producing the mandibulofacial dysostosis. The same molecular disturbance affects developing limb buds on the preaxial (radial) side, giving rise to thumb and radial ray malformations. This combined facial and appendicular pattern places Nager syndrome among the acrofacial dysostoses, distinguished from Miller syndrome by the preaxial rather than postaxial limb involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Treacher Collins syndrome (facial features without radial limb defects)","Miller syndrome (postaxial limb involvement)","Acrofacial dysostosis, Rodriguez type","Thrombocytopenia-absent radius syndrome","Holt-Oram syndrome (radial defects with cardiac disease)","Fanconi anaemia with radial ray anomalies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing for SF3B4 variants","Radiographs of the upper limbs to characterise radial ray and thumb anomalies","Audiological assessment for conductive hearing loss","Craniofacial imaging to plan reconstruction and airway management","Sleep study to evaluate obstructive sleep apnoea","Feeding and swallowing assessment in the neonatal period"],"redFlags":["Severe micrognathia causing life-threatening neonatal airway obstruction","Feeding failure requiring supported or non-oral nutrition","Recurrent aspiration from combined palatal and airway abnormality","Anticipated difficult intubation at any procedure","Progressive obstructive sleep apnoea"],"prognosis":"Early mortality relates chiefly to airway compromise; those who survive the neonatal period with secure airway and feeding generally have normal intellect and function reasonably well with staged craniofacial and hand reconstruction.","pitfalls":["Confusing Nager (preaxial) with Miller syndrome (postaxial) limb involvement","Underestimating neonatal airway risk in the presence of severe micrognathia","Assuming intellectual disability when cognition is typically normal","Overlooking hearing loss and its effect on language","Failing to arrange genetic counselling for de novo dominant recurrence risk"],"management":["Secure the neonatal airway as the immediate priority through positioning and, where needed, nasopharyngeal airway, mandibular distraction osteogenesis or tracheostomy for severe micrognathia","Support feeding and nutrition with specialised feeding techniques or gastrostomy given micrognathia and cleft palate","Undertake staged craniofacial reconstruction for malar and mandibular hypoplasia and repair of the cleft palate","Manage the radial ray and thumb anomalies with hand surgery such as pollicisation or thumb reconstruction, supported by occupational therapy","Assess and manage conductive hearing loss with audiology, hearing aids and speech and language therapy","Investigate and treat obstructive sleep apnoea with sleep studies and appropriate airway support","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across craniofacial, ENT, plastic and hand surgery, orthodontic and speech teams","Offer genetic counselling for SF3B4 variants (usually de novo) with recurrence-risk assessment and family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: SF3B4-Related Acrofacial Dysostosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"nail_patella_syndrome":{"name":"Nail-patella syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LMX1B","hereditary osteo-onychodysplasia","HOOD syndrome","Fong disease","iliac horn syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant disorder caused by variants in LMX1B, a transcription factor for dorsoventral limb patterning","The classic tetrad is dysplastic or absent nails, absent or hypoplastic patellae, elbow abnormalities and iliac horns","Nail changes are usually the earliest and most consistent sign: hypoplastic, ridged, or split nails, often with triangular lunulae, most marked on the thumbs","Absent or small patellae cause recurrent patellar dislocation, knee instability and early degenerative change","Iliac horns, bony spurs projecting posteriorly from the ilia, are pathognomonic and asymptomatic","Elbow involvement includes limited extension, radial head subluxation/dislocation and hypoplastic capitellum","Nephropathy and open-angle glaucoma are important extraskeletal complications that require lifelong monitoring"],"pathophysiology":"Nail-patella syndrome is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in LMX1B, a LIM-homeodomain transcription factor essential for dorsal patterning of the developing limb and for the differentiation of structures including the glomerular basement membrane and the anterior segment of the eye. Haploinsufficiency disrupts dorsalisation, producing nail hypoplasia, patellar and elbow malformations, and the iliac horns. In the kidney, defective podocyte and basement membrane development can lead to proteinuria and progressive nephropathy, while impaired anterior-segment development predisposes to glaucoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated congenital patellar dislocation or aplasia","Small patella syndrome","Other nail dysplasias (e.g. ectodermal dysplasias)","DOOR syndrome","Coffin-Siris syndrome","Other causes of hereditary nephropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal radiographs of pelvis (iliac horns), knees (patellae) and elbows","LMX1B molecular genetic testing","Urinalysis for proteinuria and monitoring of renal function for nephropathy","Ophthalmological assessment including intraocular pressure for glaucoma","Family assessment given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Proteinuria or declining renal function heralding progressive nephropathy","Raised intraocular pressure or visual field loss from glaucoma","Recurrent patellar dislocation with disabling knee instability","Signs of nephrotic-range proteinuria requiring nephrology input"],"prognosis":"Most affected individuals have a normal lifespan; morbidity comes from musculoskeletal deformity and, in a subset, from progressive nephropathy that can lead to renal failure, and from glaucoma, both of which are the main determinants of long-term outcome.","pitfalls":["Treating it as a purely orthopaedic disorder and failing to monitor for nephropathy and glaucoma","Missing the diagnosis because iliac horns are asymptomatic and not sought on imaging","Overlooking subtle nail changes as the earliest clue","Not screening family members despite clear dominant inheritance"],"management":["Manage musculoskeletal manifestations with physiotherapy, bracing and orthopaedic surgery for recurrent patellar dislocation, knee instability and elbow deformity","Monitor renal function with regular urinalysis for proteinuria and treat nephropathy with renin-angiotensin blockade using ACE inhibitors or angiotensin-receptor blockers to reduce proteinuria","Screen for progressive nephropathy and escalate to renal replacement therapy or transplantation in renal failure","Undertake lifelong ophthalmological surveillance for glaucoma with intraocular-pressure monitoring and appropriate glaucoma therapy","Provide analgesia and joint-protection strategies for degenerative joint disease","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant LMX1B disease with molecular confirmation and family assessment","Coordinate multidisciplinary follow-up across orthopaedics, nephrology, ophthalmology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Nail-Patella Syndrome (LMX1B-related)"],"crossLinks":[]},"napkin_dermatitis":{"name":"napkin dermatitis","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nappy rash","diaper dermatitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Irritant contact dermatitis is the commonest form, sparing the inguinal and gluteal skin folds because the convex surfaces in contact with the napkin bear the brunt of friction, moisture and irritants.","Fold involvement is the key clue that the picture is no longer simple irritant dermatitis and points to candidal infection, seborrhoeic dermatitis, or intertrigo.","Candidal napkin dermatitis characteristically shows beefy-red confluent erythema with peripheral scaling and satellite papules and pustules extending into the folds.","Prolonged skin wetness raises pH and activates faecal proteases and lipases, so the mainstay of prevention is frequent changing, gentle cleansing and a barrier preparation.","Jacquet erosive dermatitis is a severe irritant form with punched-out ulcers and raised papulonodules, seen with chronic diarrhoea or infrequent changing.","A napkin eruption that is erosive, purpuric, ulcerated or unresponsive to standard care should raise suspicion of Langerhans cell histiocytosis, zinc deficiency or child neglect.","Perianal streptococcal disease and staphylococcal folliculitis can mimic or complicate napkin dermatitis and should be considered with a well-demarcated painful perianal erythema.","Low-potency topical corticosteroid for short courses helps inflammatory cases, but potent fluorinated steroids under occlusion of the napkin risk atrophy and granuloma gluteale infantum."],"pathophysiology":"Napkin dermatitis is predominantly an irritant contact dermatitis arising from the occluded, humid microenvironment beneath a nappy. Sustained contact with urine and faeces increases skin hydration and surface pH, which in turn activates faecal proteolytic and lipolytic enzymes that degrade the stratum corneum barrier. Friction against the napkin, maceration and repeated wetting-drying cycles compound the barrier disruption, allowing irritants and microbes to penetrate. Overgrowth of Candida albicans from the gastrointestinal tract frequently supervenes on damaged skin, and secondary bacterial colonisation may occur. Underlying inflammatory dermatoses such as seborrhoeic dermatitis, atopic eczema and psoriasis can localise to or be exacerbated within the napkin area because of the Koebner phenomenon and occlusion.","additionalDifferentials":["candidal (monilial) dermatitis with satellite pustules","seborrhoeic dermatitis involving the folds and scalp","infantile or napkin psoriasis","atopic dermatitis with secondary napkin involvement","acrodermatitis enteropathica or acquired zinc deficiency","Langerhans cell histiocytosis","perianal streptococcal dermatitis or bullous impetigo"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["skin swab and microscopy or culture for Candida when folds are involved or satellite lesions present","bacterial swab for suspected streptococcal or staphylococcal secondary infection","serum zinc and alkaline phosphatase if an acrodermatitis-like periorificial and acral eruption coexists","skin biopsy for persistent, erosive or petechial eruptions to exclude histiocytosis","stool assessment when chronic diarrhoea is driving an erosive irritant pattern","blood glucose and immune screen where recurrent or refractory candidiasis suggests underlying immunodeficiency","patch testing in older children with suspected allergic contact dermatitis to wipes or fragrances"],"redFlags":["erosive, ulcerated or purpuric lesions suggesting Langerhans cell histiocytosis rather than simple irritant rash","periorificial and acral dermatitis with diarrhoea and alopecia indicating zinc deficiency","failure to respond to appropriate barrier and antifungal treatment","systemic upset, spreading cellulitis or blistering suggesting invasive bacterial infection","features raising concern for neglect such as a severe untreated eruption with poor general care"],"prognosis":"Simple irritant napkin dermatitis resolves rapidly with barrier protection and improved hygiene and clears completely once nappies are discontinued. Recurrence is common while in nappies, and persistent or atypical eruptions require reassessment for an alternative underlying diagnosis.","pitfalls":["treating fold-involving candidal dermatitis as pure irritant rash and omitting an antifungal","prescribing potent fluorinated corticosteroids under napkin occlusion, causing atrophy or granuloma gluteale infantum","missing acrodermatitis enteropathica by not examining periorificial and acral sites","overlooking Langerhans cell histiocytosis in an erosive, petechial or treatment-resistant eruption","attributing all perianal erythema to irritation and missing perianal streptococcal disease"],"management":["Optimise skin-barrier care as the mainstay, with frequent nappy changes, gentle cleansing, air exposure and liberal barrier preparations such as zinc oxide or petrolatum-based products","Treat candidal superinfection, suggested by fold involvement and satellite pustules, with a topical antifungal such as an imidazole or nystatin","Use a low-potency topical corticosteroid briefly for marked inflammation and treat secondary bacterial infection with appropriate topical or oral antibacterials","Identify and treat underlying dermatoses localising to the area, including seborrhoeic dermatitis, atopic eczema or psoriasis","Reassess erosive, purpuric, ulcerated or refractory eruptions for Langerhans cell histiocytosis, zinc deficiency or neglect","Advise carers on prevention and expected rapid resolution, with definitive clearance once out of nappies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Neonatal and Infant Dermatology (Eichenfield, Frieden)"],"crossLinks":[]},"narcolepsy":{"name":"Narcolepsy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["narcolepsy type 1","narcolepsy type 2","hypocretin deficiency syndrome","Gelineau syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Cardinal feature is excessive daytime sleepiness with irresistible sleep attacks, often accompanied by a classic tetrad that also includes cataplexy, sleep paralysis, and hypnagogic hallucinations","Cataplexy, sudden transient loss of muscle tone triggered by emotion such as laughter, is highly specific and defines narcolepsy type 1","The disorder reflects intrusion of REM-sleep phenomena into wakefulness, explaining cataplexy, dream-like hallucinations, and sleep paralysis","Narcolepsy type 1 is associated with low or undetectable cerebrospinal fluid hypocretin (orexin) and a strong link to the HLA-DQB1*06:02 allele","Diagnosis is supported by overnight polysomnography followed by a multiple sleep latency test showing short sleep latency and multiple sleep-onset REM periods","Management combines scheduled naps and sleep hygiene with wake-promoting agents for sleepiness and specific therapy for cataplexy","Onset is typically in adolescence or young adulthood and the condition is frequently under-recognised for years","Secondary narcolepsy can follow hypothalamic lesions, so consider imaging when there are atypical features or focal signs"],"pathophysiology":"Narcolepsy type 1 results from selective loss of hypocretin (orexin)-producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, most likely through an autoimmune process in genetically susceptible individuals carrying HLA-DQB1*06:02. Hypocretin normally stabilises wakefulness and suppresses inappropriate REM sleep; its deficiency destabilises the sleep-wake switch, producing daytime sleepiness and the intrusion of REM features such as cataplexy into wakefulness. Type 2 has preserved hypocretin levels and a less well-defined mechanism.","additionalDifferentials":["Obstructive sleep apnoea and other causes of fragmented sleep","Insufficient sleep syndrome","Idiopathic hypersomnia","Circadian rhythm sleep disorders","Syncope or seizures mistaken for cataplexy","Depression and medication-related sedation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Overnight polysomnography followed by a multiple sleep latency test","HLA typing for DQB1*06:02 as a supportive marker","Cerebrospinal fluid hypocretin (orexin) measurement in selected cases","MRI of the brain when secondary narcolepsy from a hypothalamic lesion is suspected","Sleep diaries and actigraphy to exclude insufficient sleep and circadian disorders"],"redFlags":["New hypersomnolence with focal neurological signs or endocrine disturbance suggesting a hypothalamic mass","Severe sleep attacks endangering driving or operating machinery","Status cataplecticus with prolonged, near-continuous cataplexy","Abrupt withdrawal of anticataplectic therapy precipitating rebound cataplexy"],"prognosis":"Narcolepsy is a lifelong disorder, but symptoms can usually be controlled well enough with treatment and behavioural measures to allow good function. It carries important safety implications for driving and work, and untreated disease significantly impairs quality of life, mood, and daytime performance.","pitfalls":["Attributing chronic sleepiness to lifestyle or depression and delaying diagnosis for years","Missing cataplexy because patients do not volunteer it or it is mistaken for syncope or seizures","Failing to screen for and address driving and occupational safety risks","Overlooking a secondary hypothalamic cause in atypical or late-onset presentations"],"management":["Combine behavioural measures as first-line treatment of sleepiness, with scheduled daytime naps, a regular sleep schedule and good sleep hygiene","Treat excessive daytime sleepiness with wake-promoting agents such as modafinil, armodafinil, solriamfetol or pitolisant, or with stimulants such as methylphenidate or amfetamines","Treat cataplexy and other REM-intrusion symptoms with sodium oxybate, pitolisant or antidepressants with anticataplectic effect such as SNRIs, SSRIs or tricyclics","Counsel on driving and occupational safety and manage the hazard of untreated sleep attacks","Screen for and manage comorbid mood disorder and disrupted nocturnal sleep","Confirm the diagnosis and subtype with overnight polysomnography and multiple sleep latency testing, referring to a sleep-medicine specialist","Provide long-term follow-up with treatment titration and patient support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"narcolepsy_type_1":{"name":"Narcolepsy type 1","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["narcolepsy with cataplexy","hypocretin-deficient narcolepsy"],"chapter":null,"source":"American Academy of Sleep Medicine ICSD-3","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by chronic excessive daytime sleepiness with cataplexy and/or a low cerebrospinal fluid hypocretin (orexin) level","Cataplexy is a sudden transient loss of muscle tone triggered by strong emotion, classically laughter, with preserved consciousness","Other features include sleep paralysis, hypnagogic hallucinations and disrupted nocturnal sleep","It is strongly associated with the HLA-DQB1*06:02 allele, supporting an autoimmune basis for hypocretin-neuron loss","The multiple sleep latency test typically shows a short sleep latency and two or more sleep-onset REM periods","Management combines scheduled naps and sleep hygiene with wake-promoting agents and specific anticataplectic treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Narcolepsy type 1 is caused by selective loss of the hypocretin (orexin)-producing neurons of the lateral hypothalamus, which normally stabilise wakefulness and suppress inappropriate REM sleep. The deficiency is believed to be autoimmune, occurring in genetically susceptible individuals carrying HLA-DQB1*06:02, sometimes after immune triggers. Loss of hypocretin signalling destabilises the boundaries between wake and REM sleep, producing sleepiness and intrusion of REM phenomena such as cataplexy, sleep paralysis and dream-like hallucinations into wakefulness.","additionalDifferentials":["Narcolepsy type 2","Idiopathic hypersomnia","Obstructive sleep apnoea","Insufficient sleep syndrome","Kleine-Levin syndrome","Syncope or seizures (for cataplexy-like episodes)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Overnight polysomnography followed by a multiple sleep latency test","Cerebrospinal fluid hypocretin measurement where available","HLA typing as a supportive test","Assessment to exclude other causes of hypersomnolence","Sleep diary and actigraphy to confirm adequate prior sleep"],"redFlags":["Cataplexy causing falls and injury","Sleepiness severe enough to impair driving or safety-critical tasks","Rapid weight gain or precocious features in children","Atypical features suggesting a secondary hypothalamic lesion"],"prognosis":"Narcolepsy type 1 is a lifelong condition, but most patients achieve meaningful control of sleepiness and cataplexy with combined behavioural and pharmacological treatment.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis by not recognising emotionally triggered cataplexy","Performing the multiple sleep latency test without ensuring adequate prior sleep","Overlooking coexisting obstructive sleep apnoea","Attributing cataplexy to syncope or seizures"],"management":["Establish behavioural foundations with scheduled naps, consistent sleep-wake timing and good sleep hygiene","Treat excessive daytime sleepiness with wake-promoting agents such as modafinil, armodafinil, pitolisant or solriamfetol, or with stimulants such as methylphenidate or amfetamines","Treat cataplexy specifically with sodium oxybate, pitolisant or anticataplectic antidepressants such as SNRIs, SSRIs or tricyclics, with sodium oxybate also improving disrupted nocturnal sleep","Counsel on driving safety and the occupational implications of sleep attacks and cataplexy","Screen for and manage comorbid depression, obesity and disturbed nocturnal sleep","Confirm the diagnosis with polysomnography, multiple sleep latency testing and, where needed, cerebrospinal fluid hypocretin measurement, under sleep-medicine specialist care","Provide lifelong follow-up with pharmacological titration and support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["American Academy of Sleep Medicine ICSD-3","Principles and Practice of Sleep Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"narcolepsy_type_2":{"name":"Narcolepsy type 2","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["narcolepsy without cataplexy"],"chapter":null,"source":"American Academy of Sleep Medicine ICSD-3","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Chronic excessive daytime sleepiness with objective evidence of early REM sleep but without cataplexy","Cerebrospinal fluid hypocretin levels are normal or only borderline, unlike the marked deficiency of type 1","The multiple sleep latency test shows a short sleep latency and two or more sleep-onset REM periods, as in type 1","Sleep paralysis and hypnagogic hallucinations may occur but are less consistently present","The diagnosis requires excluding other causes of sleepiness such as insufficient sleep and sleep apnoea","If cataplexy later develops or hypocretin is found to be low, the diagnosis is reclassified as type 1"],"pathophysiology":"Narcolepsy type 2 shares the core feature of REM sleep dysregulation seen in type 1 but without the profound hypocretin-neuron loss, so cerebrospinal fluid hypocretin is preserved. The underlying cause is less well understood and is likely heterogeneous, with only partial hypocretin dysfunction in some patients. The preserved hypocretin signalling is thought to explain the absence of cataplexy, while residual instability of wake-REM regulation produces the sleepiness and sleep-onset REM periods.","additionalDifferentials":["Narcolepsy type 1","Idiopathic hypersomnia","Insufficient sleep syndrome","Obstructive sleep apnoea","Circadian rhythm sleep-wake disorders","Hypersomnia due to a medical or psychiatric disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Overnight polysomnography followed by a multiple sleep latency test","Sleep diary and actigraphy to confirm sufficient prior sleep","Cerebrospinal fluid hypocretin measurement where clarification is needed","Screening for sleep apnoea and other causes of hypersomnolence","Review for medications or substances affecting REM sleep"],"redFlags":["Sleepiness impairing driving or safety-critical activity","Later emergence of cataplexy prompting reclassification","Features suggesting a secondary or medical cause of hypersomnia","Significant coexisting mood disorder"],"prognosis":"The course is generally chronic though sometimes more variable than type 1, and daytime sleepiness usually responds to behavioural measures and wake-promoting therapy.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing type 2 without excluding insufficient sleep or sleep apnoea","Performing the multiple sleep latency test after inadequate sleep, giving false-positive results","Failing to reclassify to type 1 when cataplexy or hypocretin deficiency emerges","Overlooking medication effects on REM sleep that mimic the test findings"],"management":["Establish behavioural management with scheduled naps, a regular sleep schedule and good sleep hygiene","Treat excessive daytime sleepiness with wake-promoting agents such as modafinil, armodafinil, solriamfetol or pitolisant, or with stimulants such as methylphenidate or amfetamines","Exclude and treat mimics and contributors, including insufficient sleep, obstructive sleep apnoea and REM-affecting medications, before and during treatment","Counsel on driving and occupational safety related to sleepiness","Confirm the diagnosis with overnight polysomnography and multiple sleep latency testing under sleep-medicine specialist oversight","Reassess periodically, reclassifying to type 1 if cataplexy emerges or hypocretin proves low"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["American Academy of Sleep Medicine ICSD-3","Principles and Practice of Sleep Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"narcotic_bowel_syndrome":{"name":"Narcotic bowel syndrome","system":"Gastrointestinal / Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["opioid-induced gastrointestinal hyperalgesia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rome IV criteria / gastroenterology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A paradoxical worsening of chronic abdominal pain with continued or escalating opioid use","The pain increases as opioid doses rise, in contrast to the expected analgesic effect","It is a form of opioid-induced hyperalgesia affecting the gut and is distinct from simple opioid-induced constipation","It should be suspected when abdominal pain persists or worsens despite high or increasing opioid doses without another explanation","The key intervention is a planned, supported withdrawal of opioids, often with adjunctive medication and psychological support","Pain typically flares transiently during withdrawal before improving, which must be anticipated and explained"],"pathophysiology":"Narcotic bowel syndrome is a manifestation of opioid-induced hyperalgesia, in which chronic opioid exposure produces neuroplastic changes that paradoxically enhance pain sensitivity. Mechanisms include activation of pronociceptive pathways, central sensitisation and glial activation, so that escalating opioids further amplify rather than relieve visceral pain. Opioid effects on gut motility may compound symptoms, but the defining feature is the worsening pain with increasing opioid dose, which resolves only when opioids are withdrawn.","additionalDifferentials":["Centrally mediated abdominal pain syndrome","Opioid-induced constipation with pain","Structural intra-abdominal disease","Chronic pancreatitis","Opioid withdrawal (during treatment)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful opioid and pain history documenting the dose-pain relationship","Targeted investigation to exclude structural disease rather than repeated testing","Assessment of psychological comorbidity and functional impact","Planning of a structured opioid withdrawal programme","Review of previous investigations to avoid duplication"],"redFlags":["New alarm features suggesting organic disease","Signs of a surgical abdomen","Severe psychological distress complicating withdrawal","Escalating opioid requirements without benefit"],"prognosis":"Pain usually improves substantially after supported opioid withdrawal, though relapse is common if opioids are resumed; long-term success depends on a multidisciplinary approach.","pitfalls":["Escalating opioids in response to worsening pain","Attributing the pain to undiagnosed structural disease and operating","Not preparing the patient for the transient flare during withdrawal","Failing to provide psychological and multidisciplinary support"],"management":["Undertake a planned, supported opioid withdrawal (structured taper or detoxification) as the definitive treatment, explaining the expected transient pain flare","Bridge withdrawal with adjunctive non-opioid analgesia and neuromodulators such as tricyclic antidepressants, SNRIs or gabapentinoids for visceral pain","Manage withdrawal symptoms with supportive agents such as clonidine and antiemetics, and treat associated constipation","Provide psychological support, including cognitive behavioural therapy and treatment of anxiety and depression, as an integral part of the programme","Build a firm therapeutic alliance with clear explanation and avoid repeated invasive investigations once structural disease is excluded","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across gastroenterology, pain medicine and psychology, with relapse-prevention planning since resuming opioids reproduces the syndrome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rome IV diagnostic criteria","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"narp_syndrome":{"name":"NARP syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Neuropathy, ataxia and retinitis pigmentosa","m.8993T>G","m.8993T>C","MT-ATP6","NARP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / GeneReviews mitochondrial disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The core triad is a sensory peripheral neuropathy, cerebellar ataxia and pigmentary retinopathy (retinitis pigmentosa), often with developmental delay and seizures","It is caused by maternally inherited mutations of the mitochondrial MT-ATP6 gene, most commonly m.8993T>G, which impair complex V (ATP synthase)","NARP and maternally inherited Leigh syndrome form a continuum: the same mutation produces NARP at intermediate mutant load and a Leigh phenotype when heteroplasmy is very high","The threshold effect means clinical severity tracks the proportion of mutant mitochondrial DNA in affected tissues","Retinal disease may present with night blindness and constricted visual fields, and salt-and-pepper or bone-spicule pigmentary changes are seen on fundoscopy","Lactate is often normal or only mildly elevated, unlike some other mitochondrial disorders, so a normal lactate does not exclude the diagnosis","Care is supportive, including neurorehabilitation, low-vision support, seizure management and avoidance of mitochondrial toxins"],"pathophysiology":"NARP arises from point mutations in MT-ATP6, which encodes a subunit of ATP synthase (respiratory-chain complex V). The mutations impair proton flux through the enzyme and reduce ATP generation while altering mitochondrial membrane potential. Because the gene is mitochondrial, inheritance is maternal and the mutation is heteroplasmic, so the phenotype depends on the mutant load within energy-dependent tissues. Neurons of the peripheral nerves, cerebellum and retina, which have high and sustained energy requirements, are preferentially affected, producing the neuropathy, ataxia and retinitis pigmentosa. At very high mutant proportions the same defect causes the more severe maternally inherited Leigh phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Maternally inherited Leigh syndrome (same gene, higher mutant load)","Other syndromic retinitis pigmentosa such as Bardet-Biedl or Usher syndrome","Kearns-Sayre syndrome and other mitochondrial retinopathies","Spinocerebellar ataxias with sensory neuropathy","Abetalipoproteinaemia and vitamin E deficiency","Refsum disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing for MT-ATP6 mutations (m.8993T>G / m.8993T>C) with heteroplasmy quantification","Ophthalmological assessment with fundoscopy and electroretinography","Nerve conduction studies to characterise the peripheral neuropathy","Brain MRI, which may show cerebellar atrophy or, at high mutant load, Leigh-type lesions","Resting and post-exercise lactate, recognising these are often normal","Neurodevelopmental assessment in affected children"],"redFlags":["Rapid clinical deterioration suggesting evolution towards a Leigh phenotype","New seizures or encephalopathy","Progressive visual loss impairing safety and function","Cardiorespiratory compromise during metabolic stress"],"prognosis":"NARP tends to run a slowly progressive course with episodic worsening during metabolic stress; visual, sensory and cerebellar disability accumulate over time, and prognosis is worse at high mutant loads where a Leigh-type picture may supervene.","pitfalls":["Excluding a mitochondrial disorder because lactate is normal, which is common in NARP","Failing to recognise the NARP-Leigh continuum and its dependence on heteroplasmy level","Attributing retinitis pigmentosa to an isolated retinal dystrophy and missing the systemic mitochondrial cause","Not counselling about maternal inheritance and variable transmission of mutant load to offspring"],"management":["Provide symptomatic and supportive care as no curative therapy exists, treating seizures with anticonvulsants while avoiding mitochondrial-toxic agents such as valproate where possible","Offer low-vision support and ophthalmological management for retinitis pigmentosa, with neurorehabilitation and physiotherapy for ataxia and sensory neuropathy","Consider a mitochondrial cofactor regimen such as coenzyme Q10, riboflavin and other vitamins, recognising the limited supporting evidence","Avoid metabolic decompensation triggers such as prolonged fasting and mitochondrial-toxic drugs including aminoglycosides, and treat intercurrent illness promptly","Monitor for evolving multisystem involvement and progression towards a Leigh-type phenotype, with developmental assessment in children","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across neurology, ophthalmology, metabolic medicine and rehabilitation","Offer genetic counselling for maternal (mitochondrial) inheritance, explaining heteroplasmy and the threshold effect, with reproductive options including oocyte or mitochondrial donation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Mitochondrial DNA-Associated Leigh Syndrome and NARP","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"nasal_fracture":{"name":"Nasal fracture","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fractured nose","broken nose","nasal bone fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The nasal bones are the most commonly fractured facial bones, and the diagnosis is largely clinical, based on deformity, swelling, epistaxis, tenderness, and mobility or crepitus of the nasal bridge","Plain radiographs add little to management, so imaging is reserved for suspicion of associated injuries such as naso-orbito-ethmoid or midface fractures","The nose should be examined internally with a speculum to exclude a septal haematoma, which is the key finding not to miss","A septal haematoma requires prompt drainage because untreated it can cause septal cartilage necrosis, perforation, and a saddle-nose deformity","It is important to establish the pre-injury shape of the nose, often with a photograph, to judge post-traumatic deformity accurately","Reduction, when needed, is best performed either within the first few hours before swelling develops or after several days once swelling subsides but before the fracture sets, generally within about two weeks","Persistent deformity or nasal obstruction after healing may need later formal septorhinoplasty rather than early manipulation","Clear rhinorrhoea after nasal trauma should raise concern for a CSF leak from an associated skull base or cribriform injury"],"pathophysiology":"The prominent, relatively thin nasal bones and the cartilaginous septum are readily displaced by frontal or lateral trauma. Lateral forces tend to displace the nasal bones to one side, while frontal impact can flatten and splay the bones and buckle the septum. Bleeding into the subperichondrial space of the septum forms a septal haematoma, which strips the perichondrium from the cartilage and deprives it of its blood supply; the resulting ischaemia and any superimposed infection can destroy the cartilage, leading to septal perforation and loss of dorsal support (saddle deformity). Higher-energy injuries may extend into the naso-orbito-ethmoid region or the skull base.","additionalDifferentials":["Naso-orbito-ethmoid fracture","Septal haematoma or septal fracture without nasal bone fracture","Nasal soft tissue contusion without fracture","Associated midface (Le Fort) or orbital fracture","Pre-existing nasal deformity mistaken for acute injury","Frontal sinus or skull base fracture with CSF leak"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination for external deformity, mobility, and airflow","Internal nasal examination with a speculum to exclude septal haematoma","Pre-injury photographs to assess the original nasal shape","CT when naso-orbito-ethmoid, midface, or skull base injury is suspected","Assessment for CSF rhinorrhoea if clear nasal discharge follows the injury"],"redFlags":["Septal haematoma requiring urgent drainage to prevent cartilage necrosis","Clear rhinorrhoea suggesting a CSF leak from skull base or cribriform injury","Telecanthus, medial canthal disruption, or features of a naso-orbito-ethmoid fracture","Uncontrolled epistaxis or signs of a more extensive midface fracture"],"prognosis":"Most isolated nasal fractures heal well, and timely closed reduction gives good cosmetic and functional results in many patients. A proportion are left with residual deformity or nasal obstruction that may require later septorhinoplasty. The main preventable adverse outcome is the saddle-nose deformity and septal perforation that follow an undrained septal haematoma.","pitfalls":["Failing to examine the septum and missing a septal haematoma","Relying on plain radiographs, which rarely change management","Attempting reduction during peak swelling, giving an inaccurate result","Delaying reduction beyond the window in which the fracture can be manipulated","Overlooking an associated naso-orbito-ethmoid or skull base injury","Not documenting the pre-injury appearance, leading to unrealistic expectations"],"management":["Exclude and urgently drain a septal haematoma, the key finding not to miss, to prevent cartilage necrosis and saddle-nose deformity","Provide initial care with control of epistaxis, analgesia and reduction of swelling, and establish the pre-injury nasal shape with a photograph","Perform closed reduction of displaced fractures within the appropriate window, either before swelling develops or after it subsides but before the fracture sets, reserving open reduction or septorhinoplasty for complex or late deformity","Reserve CT imaging for suspected associated naso-orbito-ethmoid, midface or skull-base injury, and assess for CSF rhinorrhoea if clear discharge follows","Manage isolated fractures without routine radiographs, since the diagnosis is clinical","Consider antibiotic cover after septal haematoma drainage or when there is an associated open injury","Arrange ENT or plastic surgery follow-up to assess residual deformity or nasal obstruction, with later septorhinoplasty if needed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"nasal_polyps":{"name":"Nasal polyps","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nasal polyposis","sinonasal polyps","ethmoidal polyps","antrochoanal polyp"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Benign oedematous outgrowths of inflamed sinonasal mucosa presenting with nasal obstruction, reduced smell and rhinorrhoea","Bilateral multiple ethmoidal polyps are typically associated with chronic rhinosinusitis, allergy and asthma","A single antrochoanal polyp usually arises from the maxillary sinus, is more common in younger patients, and extends toward the posterior nasal space","Polyps appear as pale, smooth, mobile, insensitive glistening masses on anterior rhinoscopy or endoscopy","The association of nasal polyps, asthma and aspirin sensitivity is a recognised clinical triad","Management principles combine intranasal and, where needed, short courses of systemic corticosteroids with treatment of underlying allergy and infection","Endoscopic sinus surgery is considered when medical therapy fails or obstruction is severe, though recurrence is common","Unilateral polyps, bleeding, or atypical appearance warrant biopsy and imaging to exclude malignancy or other lesions"],"pathophysiology":"Nasal polyps are pedunculated masses of oedematous, chronically inflamed sinonasal mucosa, most often stemming from persistent inflammation in the ethmoid region. Ongoing mucosal inflammation, frequently eosinophil-rich, causes fluid accumulation within the lamina propria, and gravity together with impaired lymphatic drainage encourages the mucosa to prolapse into the nasal cavity as polyps. Contributing factors include chronic rhinosinusitis, allergy, aspirin sensitivity and, in children, cystic fibrosis. The antrochoanal polyp arises from maxillary sinus mucosa, passes through its ostium and enlarges into the choana and nasopharynx.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertrophied inferior turbinate mistaken for a polyp","Inverted papilloma","Sinonasal malignancy","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma (in adolescent males)","Encephalocoele or meningoencephalocoele in children","Chronic rhinosinusitis without polyps"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasal endoscopy to characterise and localise the polyps","CT of the paranasal sinuses to define extent and plan surgery","Allergy testing where an allergic component is suspected","Histopathology of excised or atypical tissue to exclude neoplasm","Investigation for cystic fibrosis in children with polyps"],"redFlags":["Unilateral polyp with bleeding or an atypical firm appearance suggesting malignancy or angiofibroma","Orbital signs such as proptosis, diplopia or visual disturbance indicating spread","A polypoidal mass in a child that could be an encephalocoele, warranting caution before biopsy","Rapid growth, facial pain or cranial nerve involvement"],"prognosis":"Nasal polyps are benign but tend to be chronic and recurrent, particularly in association with asthma and aspirin sensitivity; symptoms are usually controllable with combined medical and surgical management, though repeated treatment is often needed.","pitfalls":["Assuming all nasal masses are benign polyps and missing a tumour or angiofibroma","Biopsying a suspected polyp in a child without first excluding an encephalocoele","Relying on surgery alone without ongoing medical control of underlying inflammation","Overlooking associated asthma and aspirin sensitivity"],"management":["First-line intranasal corticosteroids to shrink polyps and suppress mucosal inflammation","Short courses of systemic corticosteroids for severe obstruction or failure of topical therapy","Regular saline nasal irrigation alongside treatment of coexisting allergic rhinitis with antihistamines or leukotriene receptor antagonists","Endoscopic sinus surgery (polypectomy or functional endoscopic sinus surgery) for polyps refractory to medical therapy or for an obstructing antrochoanal polyp","Biologic therapy targeting type 2 inflammation, such as anti-IL-4Ralpha or anti-IL-5 agents, for severe recurrent eosinophilic disease","In aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, avoid NSAIDs and consider aspirin desensitisation","Screen children with polyps for cystic fibrosis","Long-term ENT follow-up with endoscopic surveillance for recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"nasal_septal_perforation":{"name":"Nasal septal perforation","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Laryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["septal perforation","perforated nasal septum","nasal septum perforation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A full-thickness defect through the nasal septum whose causes span iatrogenic, traumatic, inflammatory, and toxic aetiologies","Prior septal surgery is the commonest cause, followed by digital trauma, intranasal cocaine use, and topical vasoconstrictor or steroid overuse","Anterior perforations tend to produce whistling, crusting, epistaxis, and obstruction, whereas posterior perforations may be silent","Cocaine abuse and systemic vasculitis, particularly granulomatosis with polyangiitis, are important causes to exclude, along with malignancy","A saddle-nose deformity or extension beyond the septum suggests a destructive vasculitic, infective, or neoplastic process rather than simple trauma","Small symptomatic perforations may be managed conservatively with saline irrigation, emollients, and a Silastic septal button, while surgical repair is offered for suitable defects","The margins should be examined and biopsied when an underlying inflammatory, infective, or malignant cause is suspected"],"pathophysiology":"A nasal septal perforation develops when the mucoperichondrium is disrupted on both sides of the septum and the intervening cartilage or bone, deprived of its blood and nutrient supply, necroses and is lost. Iatrogenic injury during septal surgery, repetitive digital trauma, and the intense vasoconstriction produced by cocaine or overused topical decongestants all compromise mucosal perfusion. Inflammatory and granulomatous diseases such as vasculitis, and infiltrative malignancy, destroy the septal framework directly. Once a perforation is established, turbulent airflow across its edges causes drying, crusting, and repeated mucosal breakdown, which tends to enlarge the defect and produces the characteristic crusting, bleeding, whistling, and obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and other vasculitides","Cocaine-induced midline destructive lesion","Sarcoidosis of the nose","NK/T-cell or other sinonasal malignancy","Chronic granulomatous infection such as tuberculosis or syphilis","Postsurgical or septal-haematoma-related defect"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anterior rhinoscopy and nasal endoscopy to size and site the perforation and inspect the margins","Biopsy of the perforation edges where an inflammatory, infective, or neoplastic cause is suspected","Vasculitis screen including antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies","Investigations for granulomatous infection such as tuberculosis and syphilis serology","CT of the sinuses to assess bony involvement and extent of any destructive process"],"redFlags":["Progressive destruction with a developing saddle-nose deformity, suggesting vasculitis, infection, or malignancy","Systemic features of vasculitis such as pulmonary or renal involvement","An enlarging or ulcerating lesion with atypical margins raising concern for malignancy","A history of cocaine use with ongoing midfacial tissue loss"],"prognosis":"Many small perforations remain stable and are well controlled with conservative measures or a septal button, and carefully selected defects can be repaired successfully, but the outlook worsens when the cause is an ongoing destructive vasculitis, infection, cocaine use, or malignancy.","pitfalls":["Assuming a perforation is postsurgical or traumatic without excluding vasculitis, cocaine use, or malignancy","Failing to biopsy suspicious margins and missing an underlying disease","Attempting surgical repair while a destructive process or ongoing cocaine use remains active","Overlooking systemic vasculitic involvement that requires urgent medical management"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying cause, including cessation of intranasal cocaine, stopping overused topical decongestants or corticosteroids, and immunosuppression with corticosteroids plus cyclophosphamide or rituximab for granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Conservative symptom control with saline irrigation and emollient ointments to soften crusts and reduce bleeding","A Silastic septal button to occlude symptomatic perforations not suitable for repair","Surgical closure using mucosal advancement flaps with interposition grafting for selected defects","Antimicrobial therapy directed at any identified granulomatous infection, such as antituberculous drugs or penicillin for syphilis","Avoidance of ongoing digital trauma and intranasal irritants","Multidisciplinary referral to ENT and rheumatology, with biopsy of the perforation edges to exclude vasculitis and malignancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"nasolacrimal_duct_obstruction":{"name":"Congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CNLDO","congenital dacryostenosis","blocked tear duct","nasolacrimal duct obstruction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest cause of persistent watering (epiphora) and mucopurulent discharge in infants, usually from birth or the first weeks of life","Most often results from a persistent membrane at the distal duct (valve of Hasner)","The majority resolve spontaneously within the first year of life","Lacrimal sac massage (Crigler technique) and lid hygiene form first-line conservative care, with topical antibiotics reserved for secondary infection","Regurgitation of mucoid material on pressure over the lacrimal sac supports the diagnosis","Probing of the nasolacrimal duct is considered when symptoms persist beyond about the first year, with syringing, intubation, or balloon dacryoplasty for refractory cases","A tense bluish swelling at the medial canthus in a neonate suggests a dacryocele, which may become infected","Acute dacryocystitis, a red tender medial canthal swelling with systemic upset, is an important complication requiring prompt treatment"],"pathophysiology":"During development the nasolacrimal drainage system canalises from the eye toward the nose, and the last segment to open is the distal duct at the valve of Hasner. Failure of this membrane to perforate produces a functional obstruction so that tears and mucus cannot drain, causing epiphora and stagnation within the sac that predisposes to secondary infection. Retained secretions may distend the sac to form a dacryocele, and bacterial colonisation can progress to acute dacryocystitis, occasionally with surrounding cellulitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital or infantile glaucoma with tearing, photophobia, and corneal enlargement","Conjunctivitis, infective or allergic","Ophthalmia neonatorum","Corneal epithelial disease or foreign body","Punctal or canalicular agenesis or atresia","Epiblepharon or entropion causing reflex tearing","Dacryocele or lacrimal sac mucocele"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment with the fluorescein dye disappearance test","Examination for lacrimal sac regurgitation on digital pressure","Measurement of corneal diameter and intraocular pressure to exclude congenital glaucoma","Nasal endoscopy in complex or revision cases","Dacryocystography or dacryoscintigraphy in atypical or older children to define the level of obstruction","Microbiological swab of discharge if acute dacryocystitis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Corneal enlargement, cloudiness, or photophobia suggesting congenital glaucoma rather than simple duct obstruction","Acute dacryocystitis with a red, tender, swollen medial canthus and systemic illness","An expanding tense dacryocele, especially if it causes respiratory difficulty in a neonate","Orbital cellulitis with proptosis, restricted eye movements, and systemic toxicity"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent, with most cases resolving spontaneously during the first year; probing is highly successful when required, and only a minority need intubation, balloon dilatation, or dacryocystorhinostomy.","pitfalls":["Mistaking congenital glaucoma for simple duct obstruction and missing sight-threatening disease","Repeatedly treating with topical antibiotics without addressing the underlying obstruction","Delaying management of acute dacryocystitis or an infected dacryocele","Failing to recognise a neonatal dacryocele that compromises nasal breathing"],"management":["First-line conservative care with lacrimal sac massage (Crigler technique) and lid hygiene, with parental reassurance that most cases resolve spontaneously within the first year","Topical antibiotics reserved for secondary conjunctivitis or mucopurulent discharge","Systemic antibiotics and prompt ophthalmological review for acute dacryocystitis or periorbital cellulitis","Nasolacrimal duct probing when symptoms persist beyond about the first year of life","Syringing, silicone intubation or balloon dacryoplasty for probing failures or recurrent obstruction","Dacryocystorhinostomy for refractory obstruction","Referral to paediatric ophthalmology, with exclusion of congenital glaucoma when watering is atypical"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Royal College of Ophthalmologists guidance on congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction"],"crossLinks":[]},"nasopharyngeal_carcinoma":{"name":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NPC","nasopharyngeal cancer","carcinoma of the nasopharynx"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Nasopharyngeal carcinoma arises from the epithelium of the nasopharynx, most often in the fossa of Rosenmuller, and shows a strong association with Epstein-Barr virus and with certain geographic and dietary factors","A very common presentation is a painless neck lump from cervical nodal metastasis, which may be the first and only sign","Unilateral serous otitis media with hearing loss in an adult should raise strong suspicion, as the tumour blocks the Eustachian tube opening","Nasal symptoms include blockage and blood-stained nasal or postnasal discharge","Skull base and cavernous sinus invasion can cause cranial nerve palsies, producing diplopia, facial numbness or other neurological deficits","Because the tumour hides in the nasopharynx, diagnosis relies on endoscopic examination and biopsy, supported by imaging and Epstein-Barr virus serology or DNA levels","MRI best defines local and skull base extension while CT and PET assess bony involvement and distant spread","Treatment is primarily radiotherapy, often with concurrent chemotherapy, because the tumour is radiosensitive and surgery has a limited primary role"],"pathophysiology":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma develops in the epithelial lining of the nasopharynx, typically in the recess of the fossa of Rosenmuller near the Eustachian tube opening. Malignant transformation is closely linked to latent Epstein-Barr virus infection, together with genetic susceptibility and environmental exposures such as diets rich in preserved salted foods and nitrosamines. The tumour tends to spread submucosally and infiltrate early, obstructing the Eustachian tube to cause middle ear effusion, and eroding through the skull base foramina to involve cranial nerves and the cavernous sinus. Its rich lymphatic drainage leads to early and often bilateral cervical lymph node metastasis, and distant spread to bone, lung and liver can occur.","additionalDifferentials":["Reactive or tuberculous cervical lymphadenopathy","Lymphoma of the nasopharynx or neck","Adenoid hypertrophy (in younger patients)","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma","Other nasopharyngeal tumours such as minor salivary gland tumours","Chronic adenoiditis or Thornwaldt cyst","Metastasis from another primary site"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasopharyngeal endoscopy with biopsy of any suspicious lesion","MRI of the nasopharynx and skull base to assess local and perineural extension","CT to evaluate bony erosion and nodal disease","PET-CT for staging and detection of distant metastases","Epstein-Barr virus serology and plasma EBV DNA levels","Fine needle aspiration of a neck node when the primary is not yet identified","Audiometry and tympanometry to document middle ear effusion"],"redFlags":["Unilateral serous otitis media in an adult, which mandates nasopharyngeal examination to exclude this tumour","A persistent painless neck mass, especially in the upper deep cervical chain","Blood-stained nasal or postnasal discharge","Cranial nerve palsies, diplopia or facial numbness suggesting skull base invasion","Trismus, persistent headache or unexplained weight loss"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on stage at presentation; early disease treated with radiotherapy has a favourable prognosis, whereas advanced disease with skull base invasion or distant metastasis carries a poorer outlook, and the frequently late presentation makes vigilance for early signs important.","pitfalls":["Treating adult unilateral middle ear effusion as simple Eustachian tube dysfunction without examining the nasopharynx","Attributing a neck node to benign causes and delaying evaluation of the primary","Taking an inadequate or superficial biopsy of a submucosal tumour","Underestimating skull base and perineural spread by relying on CT alone rather than MRI","Overlooking cranial nerve findings as unrelated"],"management":["Radical intensity-modulated radiotherapy as the primary curative treatment for localised disease","Concurrent platinum-based chemoradiotherapy, with induction or adjuvant chemotherapy, for locoregionally advanced tumours","Platinum-based systemic chemotherapy and anti-PD-1 immune checkpoint inhibitors for recurrent or metastatic disease","Salvage nasopharyngectomy, neck dissection or re-irradiation for local or nodal recurrence","Supportive care for treatment toxicity, including pre-radiotherapy dental assessment and management of mucositis, xerostomia and nutrition","Coordination through a head and neck oncology multidisciplinary team","Post-treatment surveillance with endoscopy, imaging and plasma Epstein-Barr virus DNA monitoring for recurrence and distant metastasis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"navajo_neurohepatopathy":{"name":"Navajo neurohepatopathy","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NNH","MPV17-related hepatocerebral mitochondrial DNA depletion","Navajo neuropathy","MPV17 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / Suchy Liver Disease in Children","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder of the Navajo (Diné) population caused by a founder mutation in MPV17, producing a hepatocerebral mitochondrial DNA depletion syndrome","Combines progressive liver disease with a severe sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy and central white-matter involvement (leukoencephalopathy)","Neuropathy leads to distal weakness, sensory loss, painless injuries, acral mutilation, and corneal anaesthesia with recurrent ulceration","Hepatic presentations range from infantile liver failure to a slowly progressive childhood cirrhosis","Metabolic decompensation may be accompanied by lactic acidosis reflecting impaired oxidative phosphorylation","Diagnosis rests on the ethnic background, characteristic neurological and hepatic features, quantitative mitochondrial DNA depletion in liver, and MPV17 sequencing","Management is supportive; liver transplantation may be considered for hepatic failure but does not arrest the progressive neurological disease"],"pathophysiology":"Navajo neurohepatopathy is caused by biallelic mutations in MPV17, which encodes an inner mitochondrial membrane protein needed to maintain the deoxyribonucleotide pool and mitochondrial DNA. Loss of function leads to depletion of mitochondrial DNA in the liver and nervous system, impairing oxidative phosphorylation in tissues with high energy demand. The result is progressive hepatocellular injury alongside a demyelinating/axonal sensorimotor neuropathy and leukoencephalopathy, with lactic acidosis during metabolic stress. A founder MPV17 variant underlies the disorder in the Navajo population.","additionalDifferentials":["Other hepatocerebral mitochondrial DNA depletion syndromes (DGUOK, POLG, MPV17-related non-Navajo)","Hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathies","Alpers-Huttenlocher syndrome","Wilson disease with neurological features","Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis","Leukodystrophies of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Liver function tests, serum bile acids, and lactate/pyruvate","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography documenting sensorimotor neuropathy","Brain MRI to demonstrate leukoencephalopathy","Liver biopsy with quantitative mitochondrial DNA content showing depletion","Respiratory chain enzyme analysis in liver or muscle","MPV17 gene sequencing"],"redFlags":["Acute infantile liver failure with coagulopathy and lactic acidosis","Corneal anaesthesia leading to unnoticed ulceration and visual loss","Progressive acral mutilation from sensory loss and painless injury","Rapid neurological deterioration or encephalopathy"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is poor: the infantile hepatic form may cause early liver failure and death, and even in the more slowly progressive childhood form the neurological disability advances relentlessly; liver transplantation does not correct the neuropathy or leukoencephalopathy.","pitfalls":["Attributing painless injuries or mutilation to non-accidental causes rather than a sensory neuropathy","Expecting liver transplantation to halt neurological progression","Missing corneal anaesthesia and failing to protect the eye","Overlooking mitochondrial disease when liver dysfunction and neuropathy coexist"],"management":["No disease-specific therapy exists, so care is supportive, with nutritional support, fat-soluble vitamin supplementation and management of coagulopathy in chronic liver disease","Avoidance of hepatotoxic agents and prompt treatment of intercurrent illness, with correction of lactic acidosis during metabolic decompensation","Consideration of liver transplantation for hepatic failure, recognising it does not halt the neuropathy or leukoencephalopathy","Protective care of insensate limbs with meticulous foot and wound care to prevent painless injury and acral mutilation","Ophthalmic lubrication and corneal protection to prevent ulceration from corneal anaesthesia","Multidisciplinary input from hepatology, neurology and ophthalmology, with physiotherapy and orthoses for neuropathic weakness","Genetic counselling and family screening for the autosomal recessive MPV17 founder mutation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Suchy, Sokol & Balistreri, Liver Disease in Children"],"crossLinks":[]},"naxos_disease":{"name":"Naxos disease","system":"Cardiovascular / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JUP-related cardiocutaneous syndrome","plakoglobin cardiocutaneous syndrome","Naxos syndrome","arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy with woolly hair and palmoplantar keratoderma"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC/AHA cardiomyopathy guidelines; specialist inherited cardiomyopathy and dermatology literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive cardiocutaneous syndrome combining arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy with woolly hair and palmoplantar keratoderma","Caused by variants in the JUP gene encoding plakoglobin (junction plakoglobin), a desmosomal protein shared by heart and skin","The cutaneous features (tightly curled woolly hair from infancy and thickened palms and soles) appear in childhood, before the cardiac disease becomes evident","The cardiomyopathy resembles arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy, with fibrofatty replacement, ventricular arrhythmia and risk of sudden death, typically emerging by adolescence","The recognisable skin and hair phenotype offers an early clue to identify individuals at future cardiac risk","Cardiac management mirrors arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy, including exercise restriction, antiarrhythmic therapy and a defibrillator for high-risk patients","It clusters in families from Naxos and other Greek islands and Mediterranean regions, reflecting founder effects","A related recessive desmoplakin cardiocutaneous condition, Carvajal syndrome, features a more left-dominant cardiomyopathy"],"pathophysiology":"Naxos disease is caused by recessive variants in JUP, which encodes plakoglobin, a component of the desmosomes and adherens junctions that mechanically couple cells. Because plakoglobin is expressed in both cardiac muscle and the epidermis, its dysfunction weakens cell-cell adhesion in both tissues. In the heart, impaired desmosomal integrity under mechanical stress leads to myocyte death and fibrofatty replacement, creating an arrhythmogenic substrate akin to arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy. In the skin and hair follicles, defective adhesion produces the woolly hair and palmoplantar keratoderma that characterise the syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy from other desmosomal genes","Carvajal syndrome (desmoplakin-related cardiocutaneous disease)","Other inherited palmoplantar keratodermas","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Idiopathic woolly hair without cardiac disease","Cardiac sarcoidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dermatological assessment of hair texture and palmoplantar skin","12-lead ECG for right precordial changes and ventricular arrhythmia","Echocardiography and cardiac MRI for ventricular structure and fibrofatty change","Ambulatory monitoring and exercise testing for arrhythmia burden","Genetic testing for JUP variants","Family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"redFlags":["Syncope or sustained ventricular arrhythmia in a child or young adult with the cutaneous phenotype","Progressive heart failure","Family history of sudden death, especially in consanguineous or founder-population families","Documented ventricular tachycardia of right ventricular origin"],"prognosis":"The cutaneous features are benign, but the associated arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy is progressive and carries a significant risk of ventricular arrhythmia, heart failure and sudden death; early recognition through the skin phenotype allows timely cardiac surveillance and risk reduction.","pitfalls":["Regarding the woolly hair and keratoderma as purely cosmetic and missing the cardiac risk","Failing to institute cardiac surveillance before symptoms appear","Confusing it with dominant arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy and overlooking recessive inheritance and family risk","Permitting intense exercise, which accelerates the cardiomyopathy","Not distinguishing it from the desmoplakin-related Carvajal syndrome"],"management":["Exercise restriction, avoiding competitive and endurance sport that accelerates arrhythmogenic myocardial change","Antiarrhythmic therapy with beta-blockers, adding agents such as amiodarone or sotalol for ventricular arrhythmia","Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator for high-risk patients to prevent sudden cardiac death","Catheter ablation for recurrent or drug-refractory ventricular tachycardia","Standard heart failure therapy for ventricular dysfunction, with cardiac transplantation for end-stage disease","Topical emollients and keratolytics for the palmoplantar keratoderma","Lifelong cardiac surveillance with ECG, echocardiography or cardiac MRI and ambulatory monitoring of arrhythmia burden","Genetic counselling and cascade family screening for the autosomal recessive JUP variant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiomyopathies","GeneReviews / specialist literature on Naxos disease and arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"near_drowning":{"name":"Drowning / submersion injury","system":"Environmental / Emergency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["near drowning","near-drowning","submersion injury","drowning","immersion injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine / Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Drowning is defined as respiratory impairment from submersion or immersion in liquid, and modern terminology discourages older labels such as near drowning, wet, dry, and secondary drowning in favour of describing outcome and severity","The primary insult is hypoxia, so early and effective ventilation and oxygenation are the most important interventions and rescue breathing should begin as soon as possible","The historical distinction between wet and dry drowning and between fresh and salt water is of little practical importance; management is dictated by hypoxia and its consequences","The mammalian diving reflex, more pronounced in children and in cold water, can support survival after prolonged submersion, so aggressive resuscitation is justified","Pulmonary injury with surfactant washout and non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema can evolve over hours, so symptomatic patients need a period of observation even if they initially look well","Hypothermia frequently accompanies submersion and both complicates resuscitation and may offer neuroprotection, so cold arrested patients warrant prolonged efforts","Consider precipitating factors such as seizure, arrhythmia, intoxication, trauma including cervical spine injury, and hypoglycaemia"],"pathophysiology":"In drowning, submersion leads to breath-holding and then, as hypoxia and hypercarbia develop, involuntary inspiration and aspiration of liquid or laryngospasm. The central problem is hypoxaemia. Aspirated water and the associated washout and dysfunction of surfactant collapse alveoli, increase permeability, and cause ventilation-perfusion mismatch and non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, which may progress over subsequent hours. Systemic hypoxia damages the brain, heart, and other organs, and prolonged arrest can lead to hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy. Only small volumes are typically aspirated, and the once-emphasised electrolyte and haemolytic differences between fresh and salt water are rarely clinically significant. Concurrent hypothermia and the diving reflex modify the physiological response and can influence survival.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary cardiac arrest or arrhythmia precipitating submersion","Seizure disorder causing the submersion event","Traumatic injury, including cervical spine injury from diving","Intoxication with alcohol or drugs contributing to the event","Hypoglycaemia or other metabolic cause of collapse","Acute respiratory distress syndrome from another cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pulse oximetry and arterial blood gas to assess oxygenation and acid-base status","Chest radiograph to detect aspiration and evolving pulmonary oedema","Core temperature measurement to identify and grade hypothermia","Electrocardiogram and cardiac monitoring for arrhythmia","Blood glucose, electrolytes, and a toxicology screen where relevant","Imaging of the cervical spine and head when trauma is suspected"],"redFlags":["Apnoea, cardiac arrest, or persistent severe hypoxaemia after rescue","Deteriorating respiratory status with worsening pulmonary oedema over hours","Altered consciousness or neurological signs indicating hypoxic-ischaemic brain injury","Associated severe hypothermia or suspected cervical spine injury"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is determined mainly by the duration of submersion and hypoxia and the response to initial resuscitation. Brief submersion with prompt effective rescue often has an excellent outcome, whereas prolonged submersion with prolonged resuscitation and persistent coma carries a high risk of death or severe neurological disability. Cold-water submersion, particularly in children, occasionally allows good recovery after remarkably long submersion times.","pitfalls":["Discharging an initially well-appearing patient without observing for delayed pulmonary oedema","Overemphasising fresh versus salt water instead of treating hypoxia","Prematurely stopping resuscitation in a cold, submerged patient who may still recover","Neglecting cervical spine precautions when diving or trauma is possible","Failing to identify a precipitating cause such as seizure, arrhythmia, or intoxication"],"management":["Immediate restoration of oxygenation with early rescue breathing and supplemental oxygen, as correcting hypoxia is the overriding priority","Airway protection, intubation and mechanical ventilation with positive end-expiratory pressure for respiratory failure or non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema","Cardiopulmonary resuscitation for arrest, with prolonged efforts continued in the cold or hypothermic patient","Active rewarming and management of hypothermia","A period of in-hospital observation of symptomatic patients given the risk of delayed pulmonary oedema","Supportive intensive care with targeted temperature management and neuroprotection for hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Cervical spine immobilisation where diving or traumatic injury is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine","Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine: A Comprehensive Study Guide"],"crossLinks":[]},"necrobiosis_lipoidica":{"name":"Necrobiosis lipoidica","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["necrobiosis lipoidica diabeticorum","NLD","Oppenheim-Urbach disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic granulomatous dermatosis presenting as well-demarcated plaques with a yellow-brown, atrophic, waxy centre and a raised violaceous border","Most commonly affects the pretibial skin (shins), usually bilaterally, and is more frequent in women","Prominent telangiectasia and a shiny, atrophic surface through which underlying vessels are visible are characteristic","Classically associated with diabetes mellitus, though it occurs in non-diabetic individuals and its course does not track tightly with glycaemic control","Ulceration, often precipitated by minor trauma, is a common and troublesome complication","Histology shows layered ('palisaded') necrobiotic collagen degeneration with granulomatous inflammation throughout the dermis","Long-standing lesions carry a small risk of squamous cell carcinoma developing within them"],"pathophysiology":"Necrobiosis lipoidica is a granulomatous disorder characterised by degeneration ('necrobiosis') of dermal collagen with surrounding palisaded granulomatous inflammation. Microangiopathy with vessel-wall thickening and altered collagen, plus immune-complex-mediated vascular injury, are thought to compromise dermal blood supply and connective-tissue integrity, producing the atrophic, telangiectatic plaques. Although strongly linked with diabetes mellitus and its microvascular disease, the association is not absolute and glycaemic control does not reliably alter the skin lesions, indicating that additional immune and vascular mechanisms contribute.","additionalDifferentials":["Granuloma annulare","Sarcoidosis (cutaneous)","Stasis dermatitis and lipodermatosclerosis","Diabetic dermopathy","Morphoea (localised scleroderma)","Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma arising within a chronic plaque"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing palisaded necrobiotic granulomas (with caution given poor healing at biopsy sites)","Fasting glucose and HbA1c to screen for or monitor diabetes mellitus","Assessment for associated microvascular complications in diabetic patients","Wound assessment and swab if ulceration or secondary infection is suspected","Thyroid function and other autoimmune screening where clinically indicated","Biopsy of any non-healing or indurated area to exclude squamous cell carcinoma"],"redFlags":["Chronic non-healing ulceration with signs of secondary infection","Nodular growth or induration within a long-standing plaque suggesting squamous cell carcinoma","Newly diagnosed diabetes mellitus uncovered during evaluation","Rapidly enlarging or atypical lesions requiring reassessment"],"prognosis":"Necrobiosis lipoidica is chronic and often persistent; lesions may slowly enlarge and can ulcerate, treatment responses are variable, and only a minority resolve spontaneously, with squamous cell carcinoma a rare long-term complication.","pitfalls":["Assuming tight glycaemic control will clear the skin lesions","Biopsying pretibial lesions without anticipating poor wound healing and ulceration","Overlooking malignant transformation in a chronic non-healing plaque","Confusing it with granuloma annulare or stasis dermatitis and mismanaging accordingly"],"management":["First-line potent topical or intralesional corticosteroids applied to the active raised border, with topical calcineurin inhibitors as an alternative","Systemic agents for refractory or ulcerating disease, such as tetracyclines, antimalarials, pentoxifylline or TNF-alpha inhibitors","Optimisation of glycaemic control and cardiovascular risk factors, while recognising the skin lesions may not track with glucose control","Meticulous wound care for ulceration, protection of the shins from trauma and compression for associated oedema","Smoking cessation to improve dermal microvascular perfusion","Surveillance biopsy of any non-healing or indurated lesion to exclude squamous cell carcinoma","Dermatology referral for diagnosis and management of resistant disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"necrobiotic_xanthogranuloma":{"name":"Necrobiotic xanthogranuloma","system":"Skin / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NXG","necrobiotic xanthogranuloma with paraproteinaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare chronic non-Langerhans cell histiocytosis of adults presenting as indurated yellow to violaceous plaques and nodules that characteristically ulcerate and develop atrophic, telangiectatic centres.","The periorbital region is the most frequently affected site, though the trunk and limbs are also involved; periorbital disease may be complicated by ectropion, scarring and ocular inflammation.","A strong association with monoclonal gammopathy, most often IgG kappa, is a defining feature and may precede or follow the skin disease by years.","It is linked to underlying or subsequent lymphoproliferative and plasma cell disorders, including multiple myeloma and other B-cell dyscrasias, mandating long-term haematological surveillance.","Histology shows extensive palisading and diffuse xanthogranulomatous inflammation with broad zones of necrobiosis (hyaline collagen degeneration), numerous Touton and foreign-body giant cells and cholesterol clefts.","Cholesterol clefts and lymphoid aggregates within the necrobiotic zones help distinguish it from necrobiosis lipoidica and other palisading granulomas.","Complement abnormalities, cryoglobulinaemia and low C1 esterase inhibitor levels have been described in some patients.","Management is directed at the underlying paraproteinaemia; alkylating and other systemic immunomodulatory agents are used, and disfiguring or ulcerated lesions may require specialist care."],"pathophysiology":"Necrobiotic xanthogranuloma is a xanthogranulomatous histiocytic disorder in which lipid-laden macrophages, Touton and foreign-body giant cells accumulate within the dermis and subcutis, producing broad bands of collagen necrobiosis interspersed with cholesterol clefts and lymphoplasmacytic aggregates. Its consistent association with a monoclonal paraprotein, usually IgG kappa, has led to the hypothesis that circulating immunoglobulin, potentially acting as an antibody with lipoprotein-binding activity or forming immune complexes, drives a granulomatous foreign-body-type reaction to lipid within tissue. The resulting chronic granulomatous inflammation degrades dermal collagen and elastic tissue, giving the characteristic atrophic, telangiectatic and ulcerating plaques. The frequent coexistence of, or progression to, plasma cell and lymphoproliferative disorders underscores that the cutaneous disease is a marker of an underlying clonal immunoglobulin-producing process.","additionalDifferentials":["Necrobiosis lipoidica","Xanthelasma and other periorbital xanthomas","Sarcoidosis","Granuloma annulare","Cutaneous involvement by lymphoma or plasma cell disorder","Erdheim-Chester disease","Rheumatoid nodule"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with histology showing necrobiosis, cholesterol clefts and Touton giant cells","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation to detect a monoclonal paraprotein","Serum free light chain assay","Bone marrow examination when a plasma cell or lymphoproliferative disorder is suspected","Full blood count, renal profile and serum calcium as myeloma screen","Complement studies and cryoglobulins where indicated","Ophthalmological assessment for periorbital and ocular complications"],"redFlags":["Detection of a monoclonal paraprotein or rising paraprotein level indicating an evolving plasma cell dyscrasia","Anaemia, hypercalcaemia, renal impairment or bone pain suggesting multiple myeloma","Periorbital ulceration, ectropion or ocular inflammation threatening vision","Lymphadenopathy or organomegaly suggesting lymphoproliferative disease","Extensive ulceration with secondary infection"],"prognosis":"The cutaneous disease is chronic, progressive and relapsing, and while the skin lesions themselves are not life-threatening, prognosis is governed by the associated haematological disorder, which may evolve into multiple myeloma or lymphoma over many years and necessitates indefinite surveillance.","pitfalls":["Confusing it histologically and clinically with necrobiosis lipoidica and missing the associated paraproteinaemia.","Failing to perform and repeat serum protein electrophoresis, since the gammopathy may appear years after the skin disease.","Attributing periorbital plaques to ordinary xanthelasma and overlooking the systemic implications.","Neglecting long-term haematological monitoring after an initially negative myeloma screen.","Aggressive surgery on ulceration-prone lesions that heal poorly and may recur."],"management":["Treatment directed at the associated plasma cell dyscrasia or lymphoproliferative disorder, using alkylating agents such as chlorambucil or melphalan with corticosteroids, or thalidomide-class immunomodulators","Intravenous immunoglobulin or systemic corticosteroids for progressive cutaneous disease","Local therapy with intralesional corticosteroids, reserving surgery given poor wound healing and a tendency to recur","Ophthalmological management of periorbital complications including ectropion and ocular inflammation","Indefinite haematological surveillance with serial protein electrophoresis and free light chains for evolution to myeloma or lymphoma","Multidisciplinary care coordinated between dermatology, haematology and ophthalmology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology (current edition)","WHO Classification of Histiocytoses and revised histiocytosis classification (current)","Standard dermatology and haematology reference texts (current editions)"],"crossLinks":[]},"necrolytic_acral_erythema":{"name":"Necrolytic acral erythema","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NAE","acral necrolytic erythema"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as well-demarcated, dusky erythematous to violaceous plaques with a rim of scale, erosions and later hyperkeratosis, characteristically on the dorsa of the feet and lower legs","Lesions are often symmetrical, may be pruritic or painful, and can be mistaken for psoriasis or eczema","It is strongly associated with chronic hepatitis C virus infection and is regarded as a cutaneous marker of it","Zinc deficiency frequently coexists and zinc repletion can lead to marked improvement","It belongs to the family of necrolytic erythemas that includes necrolytic migratory erythema and nutritional deficiency dermatoses","Biopsy shows psoriasiform hyperplasia with upper epidermal pallor, necrosis of keratinocytes and parakeratosis, overlapping with other necrolytic erythemas","Management combines treatment of the underlying hepatitis C with correction of zinc and other nutritional deficiencies"],"pathophysiology":"Necrolytic acral erythema is a necrolytic erythema most consistently linked to chronic hepatitis C virus infection. Its pathogenesis is thought to involve metabolic and nutritional disturbances associated with liver disease, particularly zinc deficiency and abnormalities of amino-acid, albumin and fatty-acid metabolism, which impair epidermal keratinocyte integrity. The nutrient-deprived upper epidermis undergoes necrolysis with the characteristic pallor, vacuolisation and parakeratosis. The frequent response to zinc supplementation supports a central role for micronutrient deficiency in genetically or hepatically susceptible individuals.","additionalDifferentials":["Psoriasis","Necrolytic migratory erythema (glucagonoma)","Acrodermatitis enteropathica / zinc deficiency","Chronic eczema / lichen simplex","Pellagra","Contact dermatitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Hepatitis C serology and confirmatory viral RNA testing","Liver function tests and assessment of chronic liver disease","Serum zinc level","Plasma amino-acid profile and albumin","Skin biopsy for the necrolytic epidermal pattern","Serum glucagon if necrolytic migratory erythema is a consideration"],"redFlags":["Undiagnosed chronic hepatitis C with progression to cirrhosis or hepatocellular carcinoma","Extensive erosions with secondary bacterial infection","Failure to respond, prompting reassessment for glucagonoma or other cause","Features of decompensated liver disease"],"prognosis":"Lesions can be chronic and refractory to topical therapy alone but often improve substantially with zinc repletion and successful antiviral treatment of hepatitis C; long-term outcome is influenced by the course of the underlying liver disease.","pitfalls":["Treating it as recalcitrant psoriasis or eczema without testing for hepatitis C","Not checking or replacing zinc despite its therapeutic value","Confusing it with necrolytic migratory erythema and missing a glucagonoma, or vice versa","Overlooking progression of underlying chronic liver disease"],"management":["Oral zinc supplementation, which frequently produces marked improvement","Antiviral treatment of the underlying chronic hepatitis C with direct-acting antivirals","Correction of associated nutritional deficiencies, including amino acids and albumin","Adjunctive topical corticosteroids, recognising that topical therapy alone is often insufficient","Monitoring and management of the underlying chronic liver disease","Referral to hepatology and dermatology for coordinated care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"necrolytic_migratory_erythema":{"name":"Necrolytic migratory erythema (glucagonoma)","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NME","glucagonoma syndrome rash","necrolytic migratory erythema"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as migratory, annular or polycyclic erythematous plaques with superficial erosions, crusting and a collarette of scale, favouring the perioral area, perineum, groin and distal limbs","Individual lesions evolve over days with central clearing while new lesions appear at the periphery, giving a waxing and waning pattern","It is the classic cutaneous marker of the glucagonoma syndrome, a glucagon-secreting pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour","Associated features include weight loss, diabetes or glucose intolerance, glossitis, angular cheilitis, anaemia and venous thrombosis","Hypoaminoacidaemia is characteristic and may drive the skin changes, and similar rashes occur in other states of nutritional deficiency","Biopsy shows necrosis of the upper epidermis with pallor of the upper keratinocytes, vacuolisation and confluent parakeratosis","Treatment is resection of the glucagonoma where possible, with somatostatin analogues and amino-acid, zinc and fatty-acid repletion"],"pathophysiology":"Necrolytic migratory erythema is most often a paraneoplastic manifestation of a glucagon-secreting pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour. Sustained hyperglucagonaemia promotes catabolism with depletion of amino acids, and the resulting hypoaminoacidaemia, together with deficiencies of zinc and essential fatty acids, is thought to impair epidermal integrity. Nutrient-starved keratinocytes in the upper epidermis undergo necrolysis, producing the characteristic superficial necrosis, vacuolisation and crusting. Similar changes can arise in non-tumour states of malnutrition, malabsorption or liver disease that share these deficiencies.","additionalDifferentials":["Acrodermatitis enteropathica / zinc deficiency","Necrolytic acral erythema","Pellagra and other nutritional deficiency dermatoses","Pemphigus foliaceus","Mucocutaneous candidiasis / intertrigo","Psoriasis (especially flexural)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum glucagon level","Pancreatic imaging (CT/MRI) and functional neuroendocrine imaging to localise the tumour","Plasma amino-acid profile, serum zinc and essential fatty acid status","Fasting glucose and glycaemic assessment","Skin biopsy for the characteristic epidermal necrolysis","Full blood count for normochromic anaemia and general nutritional screen"],"redFlags":["Weight loss, new diabetes and rash together, pointing to glucagonoma","Venous thromboembolism, which is common and potentially fatal in the glucagonoma syndrome","Evidence of metastatic neuroendocrine disease at diagnosis","Severe malnutrition with extensive skin breakdown and secondary infection"],"prognosis":"When a glucagonoma is localised and resected the rash and metabolic disturbances usually resolve, but many tumours have already metastasised at diagnosis, which worsens the outlook; nutritional and somatostatin-based measures can improve the skin and symptoms even when cure is not possible.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating the rash as eczema, psoriasis or candidiasis and missing the underlying tumour","Not measuring glucagon and amino acids in a suggestive migratory erosive eruption","Overlooking the high risk of venous thrombosis in affected patients","Attributing all cases to a tumour and ignoring nutritional or hepatic causes of a similar picture"],"management":["Surgical resection of the glucagonoma, which is potentially curative and clears the rash when the tumour is localised","Somatostatin analogues such as octreotide or lanreotide to suppress glucagon secretion and improve the eruption","Correction of the underlying deficiencies with amino acid, zinc and essential fatty acid repletion","Management of diabetes or glucose intolerance and venous thromboembolism prophylaxis given the thrombotic risk","Systemic therapy for unresectable or metastatic disease, including peptide receptor radionuclide therapy, targeted agents or chemotherapy","Management through a neuroendocrine tumour multidisciplinary team with surveillance imaging"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"necrotising_enterocolitis":{"name":"Necrotising enterocolitis","system":"Neonatal / Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NEC"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neonatology and paediatric surgery references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A serious neonatal gastrointestinal emergency of bowel inflammation and necrosis, predominantly affecting preterm infants","Presents with feed intolerance, abdominal distension, bloody stools, bilious aspirates and systemic instability","Prematurity and formula feeding increase risk, while human breast milk is protective","The abdominal radiograph may show pneumatosis intestinalis, and portal venous gas or free air indicate advanced disease","The modified Bell staging system grades severity from suspected disease to established and advanced perforation","Management ranges from bowel rest, gastric decompression and antibiotics to surgery for perforation or deterioration"],"pathophysiology":"Necrotising enterocolitis is a multifactorial disorder in which immaturity of the preterm gut, in combination with enteral feeding, an abnormal microbial colonisation pattern and mucosal ischaemia, triggers an exaggerated inflammatory response and breakdown of the intestinal barrier. Bacterial translocation across the compromised mucosa amplifies inflammation, leading to variable bowel-wall necrosis, gas within the bowel wall (pneumatosis) and, in severe cases, full-thickness necrosis with perforation and peritonitis. Although bacteria contribute, it is regarded as a distinct inflammatory-ischaemic disease of the immature gut rather than a simple infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Spontaneous intestinal perforation","Neonatal sepsis with ileus","Malrotation with volvulus","Hirschsprung enterocolitis","Milk protein intolerance","Anal fissure or swallowed maternal blood (for bloody stool)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal radiographs for pneumatosis intestinalis, portal venous gas and free air","Full blood count, inflammatory markers and blood culture","Blood gas, glucose and coagulation studies","Serial abdominal examination and, where used, abdominal ultrasound","Surgical review for advanced or deteriorating disease"],"redFlags":["Pneumoperitoneum indicating perforation","Rapid clinical deterioration with shock or disseminated intravascular coagulation","Abdominal wall erythema or a fixed mass","Portal venous gas or extensive pneumatosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies with severity; medical disease often resolves, but advanced disease carries significant mortality and long-term risks including short bowel syndrome, strictures and neurodevelopmental impairment.","pitfalls":["Continuing feeds despite early warning signs","Missing perforation by not repeating imaging in a deteriorating baby","Confusing spontaneous intestinal perforation with necrotising enterocolitis","Underestimating the risk of later strictures after recovery"],"management":["Bowel rest with cessation of enteral feeds and nasogastric decompression","Broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics covering enteric organisms, with parenteral nutrition to maintain intake","Supportive care correcting acidosis, coagulopathy and thrombocytopenia, with fluid resuscitation, inotropes and ventilation for shock","Serial abdominal radiographs and examination to detect progression to portal venous gas or free air","Surgical intervention with laparotomy or peritoneal drainage for perforation or full-thickness necrosis","Prioritisation of human breast milk as protective, with cautious graded reintroduction of feeds on recovery","Paediatric surgical referral and long-term follow-up for strictures, short bowel syndrome and neurodevelopmental outcome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neonatology reference texts (standard reference)","Paediatric surgery reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"necrotising_sialometaplasia":{"name":"Necrotising sialometaplasia","system":"Oral cavity / Salivary glands","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["necrotizing sialometaplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oral pathology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign, self-limiting inflammatory condition of minor salivary glands that clinically and histologically mimics malignancy","It most often presents as a sudden crater-like ulcer of the hard palate, sometimes bilateral","The lesion is important chiefly because it can be mistaken for squamous cell carcinoma or a salivary malignancy","Preceding local ischaemic insults such as trauma, injection, surgery or smoking are commonly identified","Histology shows lobular necrosis with squamous metaplasia of ducts while preserving the lobular architecture","Recognition avoids unnecessary radical surgery, as the lesion heals spontaneously over weeks"],"pathophysiology":"Necrotising sialometaplasia is thought to result from ischaemic injury to the minor salivary glands, most often in the palate, following vascular compromise from trauma, local anaesthetic injection, surgery, pressure or smoking. The ischaemia causes acinar necrosis, and reparative squamous metaplasia of the salivary ducts follows. This combination of necrosis and metaplasia can closely resemble malignancy both clinically and microscopically, but the preserved lobular architecture indicates a benign reactive process.","additionalDifferentials":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma or other salivary malignancy","Traumatic ulcer","Deep fungal or other infective ulcer","Ulceration from vasculitis or other systemic disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Incisional biopsy with experienced histopathological assessment","History of preceding local trauma, injection or surgery","Clinical follow-up to confirm spontaneous healing","Exclusion of malignancy and infective causes on histology"],"redFlags":["Failure to heal within the expected weeks","Histological features favouring malignancy on expert review","Progressive enlargement or induration","Associated systemic features suggesting another cause"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limiting, healing spontaneously over several weeks without specific treatment.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the clinical or histological picture as carcinoma and performing radical surgery","Failing to obtain expert histopathology review","Overlooking a preceding ischaemic insult in the history","Not arranging follow-up to confirm healing"],"management":["Reassurance and conservative management, as the lesion heals spontaneously over several weeks without specific treatment","Incisional biopsy with experienced histopathological review to exclude squamous cell carcinoma or salivary malignancy and avoid unnecessary radical surgery","Symptomatic care with analgesia, gentle oral hygiene and saline mouth rinses while the ulcer heals","Identification and removal of precipitating factors such as local trauma and smoking","Clinical follow-up to confirm spontaneous resolution","Referral to oral medicine or oral and maxillofacial surgery for diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Oral pathology reference texts (standard reference)","Oral and maxillofacial surgery reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"necrotizing_enterocolitis":{"name":"Necrotizing Enterocolitis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NEC","necrotising enterocolitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common acquired gastrointestinal emergency of the newborn, occurring mainly in preterm, low-birth-weight infants and usually within the first weeks after birth.","Presentation ranges from feeding intolerance, abdominal distension, and bloody stools to systemic signs such as lethargy, temperature instability, apnoea, and shock.","The pathognomonic radiographic sign is pneumatosis intestinalis (intramural gas); portal venous gas and free intraperitoneal air are more ominous findings.","Prematurity is the dominant risk factor; enteral feeding, particularly with formula, and intestinal dysbiosis are contributory, while human milk is protective.","Initial management is medical: stop enteral feeds, decompress the bowel with a nasogastric tube, give intravenous fluids and broad-spectrum antibiotics, and provide supportive care.","Serial abdominal radiographs and clinical review guide progression; free air on imaging indicates perforation and the need for surgery.","Surgical options include peritoneal drainage or laparotomy with resection of necrotic bowel, sometimes with a stoma.","The Bell staging system grades severity from suspected to advanced disease with perforation and is used to guide management.","Extensive resection can leave short bowel syndrome with long-term dependence on parenteral nutrition."],"pathophysiology":"Necrotizing enterocolitis reflects a multifactorial injury to the immature intestine. An excessive inflammatory response of the immature gut mucosa, altered microbial colonisation, ischaemia or impaired mucosal perfusion, and enteral substrate combine to cause mucosal injury, bacterial translocation, and gas production within the bowel wall. Progression leads to full-thickness necrosis, perforation, peritonitis, and systemic inflammatory response with sepsis and shock.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis with ileus","Spontaneous intestinal perforation","Malrotation with midgut volvulus","Hirschsprung enterocolitis","Infectious gastroenteritis","Milk protein intolerance / allergic colitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Supine and lateral decubitus or cross-table lateral abdominal radiographs for pneumatosis, portal gas, or free air","Full blood count (looking for neutropenia, thrombocytopenia) and inflammatory markers","Blood culture and blood gas with lactate","Serum electrolytes and glucose, coagulation studies","Abdominal ultrasound to assess bowel wall perfusion and free fluid"],"redFlags":["Free intraperitoneal air indicating perforation","Portal venous gas","Persistent metabolic acidosis or rising lactate","Thrombocytopenia, neutropenia, or evidence of disseminated intravascular coagulation","Abdominal wall erythema, fixed mass, or clinical deterioration despite medical therapy"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on gestational age and severity; mild disease often resolves with medical management, but advanced disease requiring surgery carries substantial mortality and long-term morbidity including strictures, short bowel syndrome, and adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes.","pitfalls":["Attributing early nonspecific signs to feeding intolerance and delaying evaluation","Advancing enteral feeds too rapidly in vulnerable preterm infants","Missing a subtle pneumoperitoneum on a single supine film","Underestimating rapid clinical deterioration and delaying surgical consultation","Overlooking strictures that can develop weeks after apparent recovery"],"management":["Immediate cessation of enteral feeds with nasogastric tube decompression of the bowel","Broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics and parenteral nutrition during the period of bowel rest","Supportive intensive care with fluid resuscitation, correction of acidosis and coagulopathy, and respiratory and cardiovascular support for sepsis or shock","Serial supine and lateral decubitus abdominal radiographs with clinical review to guide progression","Surgical management with laparotomy or primary peritoneal drainage when free air indicates perforation","Promotion of human milk feeding as protective and avoidance of aggressive formula feed advancement","Paediatric surgical involvement and follow-up for strictures, short bowel syndrome and neurodevelopmental impairment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"nelson_syndrome":{"name":"Nelson syndrome","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Nelson's syndrome","corticotroph tumour progression","post-adrenalectomy corticotroph adenoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Refers to progressive growth of a corticotroph pituitary tumour after bilateral adrenalectomy performed for Cushing disease","Loss of cortisol negative feedback allows the residual corticotroph adenoma to enlarge and secrete large amounts of adrenocorticotrophic hormone","Very high circulating adrenocorticotrophic hormone drives striking skin hyperpigmentation","An expanding sellar mass can cause headache, visual field defects and hypopituitarism","Risk is greater in younger patients and when adrenalectomy is done without pituitary irradiation","Regular monitoring of adrenocorticotrophic hormone levels and pituitary imaging after bilateral adrenalectomy allows early detection","Prophylactic pituitary radiotherapy at the time of adrenalectomy reduces the likelihood of later tumour progression","Management centres on transsphenoidal surgery, with radiotherapy and medical therapy for residual or aggressive disease"],"pathophysiology":"Nelson syndrome develops when a corticotroph adenoma, previously the source of Cushing disease, continues to grow after the adrenal glands are surgically removed. With cortisol production abolished, the normal suppressive feedback on the pituitary is lost, unleashing the tumour to proliferate and to secrete high levels of adrenocorticotrophic hormone and its related pro-opiomelanocortin peptides. The excess melanocyte-stimulating activity of these peptides produces marked hyperpigmentation, while the enlarging adenoma exerts local mass effect on adjacent structures. A subset behave aggressively with invasive growth.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent or persistent Cushing disease before adrenalectomy","Other enlarging pituitary macroadenoma","Primary adrenal insufficiency hyperpigmentation without tumour progression","Ectopic adrenocorticotrophic hormone secretion","Non-functioning pituitary tumour","Hypophysitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial plasma adrenocorticotrophic hormone measurement","Magnetic resonance imaging of the pituitary to assess tumour size and invasion","Formal visual field testing","Anterior pituitary hormone profile to detect hypopituitarism","Assessment of glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement adequacy","Histological and proliferative characterisation of resected tumour"],"redFlags":["Rapid visual field loss from chiasmal compression","Aggressive or invasive tumour behaviour","New hypopituitarism from an enlarging mass","Very high and rising adrenocorticotrophic hormone with worsening pigmentation","Adrenal crisis from inadequate replacement during tumour-related stress"],"prognosis":"Early detection and effective transsphenoidal resection, with adjuvant radiotherapy where needed, give a reasonable outlook, but aggressive corticotroph tumours can be difficult to control and cause significant morbidity through mass effect and hypopituitarism.","pitfalls":["Failing to monitor adrenocorticotrophic hormone and pituitary imaging after bilateral adrenalectomy","Attributing hyperpigmentation solely to adrenal insufficiency without checking for tumour progression","Delaying intervention until visual compromise occurs","Not considering prophylactic pituitary radiotherapy in high-risk patients","Overlooking new anterior pituitary deficiencies from the enlarging adenoma"],"management":["Transsphenoidal surgical resection of the corticotroph adenoma as the primary treatment","Adjuvant pituitary radiotherapy or stereotactic radiosurgery for residual or aggressive tumour","Medical therapy to control ACTH secretion and tumour growth, such as pasireotide, cabergoline or temozolomide for aggressive disease","Optimisation of glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement following bilateral adrenalectomy","Replacement of deficient anterior pituitary hormones and monitoring of visual fields for chiasmal compression","Surveillance with serial plasma ACTH measurement and pituitary MRI for early detection of tumour growth","Management through an endocrinology and pituitary neurosurgery multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","DeGroot's Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"nelson_tumor":{"name":"Nelson syndrome corticotroph tumour","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Nelson syndrome","Nelson tumour","corticotroph tumour progression","post-adrenalectomy corticotroph adenoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Develops after bilateral adrenalectomy for Cushing disease when loss of cortisol negative feedback allows the residual corticotroph adenoma to enlarge","The classic combination is progressive growth of an ACTH-secreting pituitary tumour, very high plasma ACTH and skin hyperpigmentation","Hyperpigmentation results from markedly elevated ACTH and other pro-opiomelanocortin-derived peptides stimulating melanocytes","Mass effect may cause headache, visual field loss such as a bitemporal hemianopia, and hypopituitarism","Risk is higher in younger patients and in aggressive disease, and prophylactic pituitary irradiation at the time of adrenalectomy reduces the incidence","The condition is largely avoided by treating the pituitary source directly rather than relying on bilateral adrenalectomy alone","It is now less common as pituitary-directed therapy has become first-line for Cushing disease","Management combines transsphenoidal surgery and radiotherapy, and some tumours behave aggressively"],"pathophysiology":"Nelson syndrome arises when bilateral adrenalectomy, performed to control cortisol excess in Cushing disease, removes the cortisol that normally restrains the pituitary corticotroph adenoma. Freed from negative feedback, the corticotroph tumour grows and secretes large amounts of ACTH and other pro-opiomelanocortin derivatives. The excess ACTH and related melanocyte-stimulating peptides cause diffuse skin hyperpigmentation, while tumour enlargement produces local mass effects on the optic chiasm and the normal pituitary. Some corticotroph tumours in this setting are inherently aggressive, with rapid growth and, rarely, malignant transformation.","additionalDifferentials":["Other sellar masses causing mass effect such as non-functioning adenoma, craniopharyngioma or meningioma","Primary adrenal insufficiency, which also causes hyperpigmentation and high ACTH","Persistent or recurrent Cushing disease before adrenalectomy","Ectopic ACTH syndrome","Other causes of hyperpigmentation such as haemochromatosis or drugs"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial plasma ACTH measurement, showing marked and progressive elevation","Serial pituitary MRI to document corticotroph tumour growth","Formal visual field assessment by perimetry for chiasmal compression","Assessment of remaining anterior pituitary function for hypopituitarism","Confirmation of adequate glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement after adrenalectomy"],"redFlags":["Rapid tumour growth with visual field loss or cranial nerve involvement","Very high or steeply rising ACTH with deepening pigmentation","Features suggesting an aggressive or malignant corticotroph tumour","Adrenal crisis from inadequate replacement in an adrenalectomised patient"],"prognosis":"Variable: many corticotroph tumours are controlled with surgery and radiotherapy, but a subset behave aggressively with recurrence and rare metastasis; prophylactic radiotherapy and modern pituitary-directed treatment of Cushing disease have reduced the incidence and improved outcomes.","pitfalls":["Relying on bilateral adrenalectomy without monitoring ACTH and pituitary imaging for tumour progression","Attributing new hyperpigmentation to under-replacement alone without imaging the pituitary","Missing visual field deterioration through infrequent monitoring","Overlooking the higher risk in children and young patients"],"management":["Transsphenoidal resection of the ACTH-secreting corticotroph tumour as the mainstay of treatment","Adjuvant or prophylactic pituitary radiotherapy, given at the time of adrenalectomy in high-risk cases to reduce incidence","Medical therapy to suppress ACTH and restrain tumour growth, including pasireotide, cabergoline and temozolomide for aggressive or malignant behaviour","Lifelong glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement after bilateral adrenalectomy","Treatment of hypopituitarism with targeted hormone replacement and monitoring for bitemporal hemianopia from chiasmal compression","Serial plasma ACTH and pituitary MRI surveillance after adrenalectomy for early detection","Preferential direct pituitary-directed treatment of Cushing disease to avoid the syndrome, coordinated by an endocrine and neurosurgical team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","DeGroot's Endocrinology","Pituitary Society reviews on Nelson syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"nemaline_myopathy":{"name":"Nemaline myopathy","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rod myopathy","nemaline rod myopathy","ACTA1","NEB","TPM3"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neuromuscular Disorders reference texts / OMIM","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital myopathy with a wide severity spectrum, from a severe neonatal form with profound hypotonia and respiratory failure to milder childhood and adult-onset forms","Weakness is typically most marked in facial, bulbar, neck-flexor and proximal limb muscles, and a long thin face with a high-arched palate is characteristic","Caused by variants in several sarcomeric thin-filament genes, most commonly nebulin (NEB, usually recessive) and skeletal actin (ACTA1), with tropomyosin and troponin genes also implicated","Muscle biopsy hallmark is nemaline rods (thread-like bodies) within fibres, best seen as red-purple inclusions on the modified Gomori trichrome stain and confirmed on electron microscopy as electron-dense structures arising from the Z-disc","Respiratory muscle weakness may be disproportionate to limb weakness and can occur even when limb strength is relatively preserved","Creatine kinase is usually normal or mildly elevated","Cardiac involvement is uncommon but has been described, so periodic cardiac review is reasonable in more severe cases"],"pathophysiology":"Nemaline myopathy is a congenital myopathy caused by variants in genes encoding thin-filament and Z-disc-associated proteins of the skeletal muscle sarcomere, including nebulin, skeletal alpha-actin, and the tropomyosin and troponin genes. Disruption of thin-filament assembly and regulation impairs force generation and leads to the aggregation of Z-disc material into the characteristic rod-like inclusions. The resulting contractile dysfunction produces predominantly proximal, facial and bulbar weakness with notable respiratory muscle involvement. Inheritance may be autosomal recessive or dominant depending on the causative gene.","additionalDifferentials":["Central core disease","Centronuclear/myotubular myopathy","Congenital muscular dystrophy","Congenital myasthenic syndrome","Spinal muscular atrophy","Congenital myotonic dystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Muscle biopsy with Gomori trichrome staining and electron microscopy to demonstrate nemaline rods","Genetic panel testing covering NEB, ACTA1, TPM2, TPM3, TNNT1 and related genes","Serum creatine kinase","Respiratory function testing including assessment for nocturnal hypoventilation","Swallowing and nutritional assessment in infants and severely affected patients","Electromyography to confirm a myopathic pattern and exclude neuropathic causes"],"redFlags":["Neonatal respiratory failure or apnoea requiring ventilatory support","Feeding difficulty with recurrent aspiration","Progressive nocturnal hypoventilation with morning headaches or daytime somnolence","Rapidly worsening scoliosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends strongly on subtype: the severe neonatal form carries a high risk of early respiratory failure, whereas typical congenital and milder forms are often static or slowly progressive and compatible with prolonged survival and useful function. Respiratory surveillance is the key determinant of long-term outcome.","pitfalls":["Underestimating respiratory muscle weakness because limb strength appears adequate","Missing rods because trichrome staining or electron microscopy was not performed","Confusing the myopathic facial and bulbar weakness with a neuromuscular junction disorder","Assuming a normal creatine kinase excludes a congenital myopathy"],"management":["No disease-specific therapy exists, so management centres on respiratory surveillance with non-invasive ventilation for nocturnal hypoventilation and respiratory failure","Swallowing and nutritional assessment, with gastrostomy feeding where bulbar weakness impairs intake","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy, orthoses and mobility aids to maintain function and prevent contractures","Orthopaedic management of scoliosis and joint contractures","Anaesthetic precautions given respiratory and bulbar vulnerability","Coordinated multidisciplinary neuromuscular, respiratory, therapy and orthopaedic care","Genetic counselling and family screening tailored to autosomal recessive or dominant inheritance of the causative gene"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dubowitz Muscle Disorders in Childhood","OMIM: Nemaline myopathy (NEB/ACTA1)"],"crossLinks":[]},"nemo_deficiency":{"name":"NEMO deficiency (X-linked, IKBKG)","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NEMO deficiency","IKBKG deficiency","NF-kappaB essential modulator deficiency","X-linked anhidrotic ectodermal dysplasia with immunodeficiency","EDA-ID"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked disorder of males caused by hypomorphic mutations in IKBKG, which encodes the regulatory NEMO subunit essential for nuclear factor kappa B signalling","The classic phenotype couples anhidrotic ectodermal dysplasia (sparse hair, conical or missing teeth, reduced sweating) with a broad immunodeficiency","Susceptibility spans encapsulated pyogenic bacteria, mycobacteria (including severe BCG reactions), and often opportunistic and viral pathogens, reflecting failure across multiple immune pathways","Impaired antibody responses, notably to polysaccharide antigens, are common and may be accompanied by low or dysregulated immunoglobulin levels including raised IgM in some patients","Inflammatory bowel-like colitis and other immune dysregulation can complicate the immunodeficiency","Complete loss of NEMO is lethal in males and instead causes incontinentia pigmenti in heterozygous females, so surviving affected males carry partial-function mutations","Management combines immunoglobulin replacement, antimicrobial prophylaxis, avoidance of live vaccines, and consideration of haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in severe cases"],"pathophysiology":"NEMO, the regulatory subunit of the IkappaB kinase complex encoded by IKBKG on the X chromosome, is required to activate the transcription factor nuclear factor kappa B downstream of many immune and developmental receptors, including Toll-like receptors, the interleukin-1 and tumour necrosis factor receptor families, and the ectodysplasin receptor. Because this pathway serves both immune signalling and the development of ectodermal structures, hypomorphic mutations simultaneously impair innate and adaptive immune responses and disrupt the formation of sweat glands, hair, and teeth. Weakened Toll-like receptor and cytokine signalling blunts inflammatory and antibody responses, so the host handles encapsulated bacteria, mycobacteria, and certain viruses poorly, while dysregulated signalling can also drive autoinflammation and colitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Other combined immunodeficiencies with syndromic features","X-linked agammaglobulinaemia and other antibody deficiencies","Hyper-IgM syndromes (CD40 ligand and related defects)","IkappaB-alpha gain-of-function deficiency with a similar ectodermal-dysplasia-immunodeficiency picture","Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease","Autosomal recessive anhidrotic ectodermal dysplasia without immunodeficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic sequencing of IKBKG, including assessment for common recurrent mutations and copy-number changes","Quantitative immunoglobulins with specific antibody responses to protein and polysaccharide vaccines","Functional assays of Toll-like receptor and cytokine-driven nuclear factor kappa B activation in blood cells","Lymphocyte subset and natural killer cell function analysis","Cultures and species identification for bacterial and mycobacterial infections","Clinical and dental assessment of ectodermal features and sweating","Endoscopy and histology when inflammatory colitis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Severe or disseminated infection after BCG vaccination","Invasive infection with encapsulated bacteria in a boy with ectodermal features","Opportunistic or severe viral infection indicating broad immune failure","Chronic diarrhoea and colitis suggesting immune dysregulation","A maternal family history of male infant deaths or incontinentia pigmenti in females"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies with the specific mutation and phenotype; some patients are stabilised with immunoglobulin replacement and prophylaxis, but severe combined phenotypes carry high infective mortality, and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, while potentially curative for the immunodeficiency, is challenging and does not correct the ectodermal features.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis when ectodermal features are subtle or when immunodeficiency dominates the presentation","Giving live vaccines, including BCG, before the diagnosis is excluded","Interpreting a raised IgM as a simple hyper-IgM syndrome without recognising the wider signalling defect","Missing accompanying colitis or other immune dysregulation","Failing to counsel the family about X-linked inheritance and the incontinentia pigmenti link in females"],"management":["Immunoglobulin replacement therapy to correct impaired antibody responses","Antimicrobial prophylaxis and avoidance of live vaccines, particularly BCG given the risk of severe mycobacterial reactions","Prompt aggressive treatment of infections with targeted antibacterial and antimycobacterial agents","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the potentially curative option for the immunodeficiency","Management of immune dysregulation such as inflammatory colitis with immunomodulatory therapy","Supportive management of the ectodermal features, including dental care and avoidance of overheating due to reduced sweating","Coordinated care by clinical immunology and genetics, with prompt escalation for severe or opportunistic infection","Genetic counselling and family screening for the X-linked IKBKG mutation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_abstinence_syndrome":{"name":"Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome","system":"Neonatal / Neurological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NAS","neonatal opioid withdrawal syndrome","NOWS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A constellation of withdrawal signs in a newborn following intrauterine exposure to substances, most commonly opioids, that the infant is no longer receiving after birth.","Signs typically involve central nervous system irritability, autonomic disturbance, and gastrointestinal dysfunction.","Common features include tremors, high-pitched crying, poor sleep, irritability, hypertonia, sweating, sneezing, nasal stuffiness, feeding difficulty, vomiting, and loose stools.","The timing of onset depends on the substance and its half-life; short-acting opioids produce earlier withdrawal, while longer-acting agents may delay onset for days.","Standardised scoring tools are used to assess severity and guide whether pharmacological treatment is needed.","First-line care emphasises supportive, non-pharmacological measures such as a low-stimulation environment, swaddling, gentle handling, and rooming-in with the mother.","The Eat, Sleep, Console approach focuses on the infant's function rather than symptom counts alone.","When supportive care is insufficient, opioid-based pharmacological treatment is used with gradual weaning; polysubstance exposure and breastfeeding considerations should also be addressed."],"pathophysiology":"During pregnancy, drugs that cross the placenta reach the fetus and lead to physiological dependence, particularly with opioids acting on central opioid receptors. After delivery the sudden loss of transplacental drug supply removes this stimulation, producing a hyperexcitable state affecting the central and autonomic nervous systems and the gastrointestinal tract. The resulting neurotransmitter imbalance manifests as the characteristic irritability, autonomic instability, and feeding disturbance; the pattern and timing vary with the specific agent, dose, and its pharmacokinetics.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis or infection","Hypoglycaemia","Hypocalcaemia or other electrolyte disturbance","Hyperthyroidism (neonatal thyrotoxicosis)","Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Central nervous system haemorrhage or injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial standardised withdrawal scoring (e.g. modified Finnegan) or function-based assessment","Maternal history of substance use and prescribed medications","Toxicology testing of infant urine, meconium, or other matrices where appropriate","Blood glucose monitoring","Electrolytes and calcium if clinically indicated","Sepsis screen when infection cannot be excluded"],"redFlags":["Seizures attributable to withdrawal","Severe feeding intolerance with dehydration or poor weight gain","Excessive weight loss or failure to thrive","Marked autonomic instability or persistent high scores despite supportive care","Signs suggesting an alternative serious diagnosis such as sepsis or hypoglycaemia","Difficulty consoling with escalating pharmacological requirements"],"prognosis":"Acute withdrawal is usually self-limited and manageable, and most infants recover well with appropriate supportive and, when needed, pharmacological care; longer-term neurodevelopmental outcomes are influenced more by the broader social and environmental context, so coordinated family support and follow-up are important.","pitfalls":["Relying on medication before optimising non-pharmacological, function-focused care","Excessive handling and stimulation that worsen the infant's symptoms","Overlooking polysubstance exposure, which alters the withdrawal course","Missing concurrent problems such as sepsis or hypoglycaemia","Inconsistent scoring between assessors leading to inappropriate treatment decisions","Neglecting maternal support, breastfeeding counselling, and social care coordination"],"management":["First-line non-pharmacological care with a low-stimulation environment, swaddling, gentle handling, rooming-in with the mother and frequent small feeds","Standardised withdrawal scoring to assess severity and decide when pharmacological treatment is needed","Pharmacological treatment of opioid withdrawal with an opioid such as oral morphine or methadone when supportive measures are insufficient","Adjunctive phenobarbital or clonidine for severe, refractory or polysubstance-related withdrawal","Support of breastfeeding where maternal circumstances allow, with monitoring of weight, hydration and blood glucose","Engagement of maternal addiction services, social support and safeguarding assessment","Coordinated follow-up for feeding, growth and neurodevelopmental outcome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_adrenoleukodystrophy":{"name":"Neonatal adrenoleukodystrophy","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NALD","Peroxisome biogenesis disorder","Zellweger spectrum disorder","PEX genes"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews Zellweger Spectrum Disorder / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Neonatal adrenoleukodystrophy is a peroxisome biogenesis disorder within the Zellweger spectrum, distinct from X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy despite the similar name","It is autosomal recessive, caused by mutations in PEX genes that impair assembly of the whole peroxisome, so multiple peroxisomal functions fail simultaneously","Presentation is in infancy with hypotonia, seizures, poor feeding, hepatic dysfunction, retinopathy and sensorineural hearing loss, with a milder and later course than classic Zellweger syndrome","The generalised nature distinguishes it biochemically from single-enzyme peroxisomal disorders, with abnormalities across several peroxisomal pathways","Key biochemical findings include elevated very-long-chain fatty acids, raised phytanic and pristanic acids, abnormal bile acid intermediates and reduced plasmalogens","White-matter demyelination and adrenal insufficiency can develop, and neurodegeneration is progressive","Management is supportive, covering feeding, seizures, hepatic and adrenal support, hearing and vision aids, with monitoring for adrenal insufficiency"],"pathophysiology":"Neonatal adrenoleukodystrophy belongs to the Zellweger spectrum of peroxisome biogenesis disorders, in which recessive mutations in PEX genes prevent the normal assembly of functional peroxisomes. Because the organelle itself is defective, the many enzymatic functions it houses are lost together: beta-oxidation of very-long-chain fatty acids fails so these accumulate, alpha-oxidation of phytanic acid is impaired, bile acid synthesis is incomplete, and plasmalogen biosynthesis is deficient. The combined biochemical disturbance disrupts membrane and myelin formation and produces toxic lipid accumulation, leading to the progressive leukodystrophy, adrenocortical dysfunction, hepatic disease and multisystem involvement that characterise the disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy","Classic Zellweger syndrome and infantile Refsum disease (same spectrum, differing severity)","Single-enzyme peroxisomal defects such as isolated acyl-CoA oxidase or D-bifunctional protein deficiency","Mitochondrial encephalopathies of infancy","Congenital infections (TORCH) causing neonatal encephalopathy and hepatopathy","Neonatal seizure and hypotonia syndromes of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma very-long-chain fatty acids, the principal screening test","Plasma phytanic and pristanic acids and bile acid intermediates","Erythrocyte plasmalogen levels","Molecular genetic testing of PEX genes","Brain MRI to assess demyelination and neuronal migration abnormalities","Adrenal function testing and ophthalmological and audiological assessment"],"redFlags":["Adrenal insufficiency or adrenal crisis","Refractory neonatal or infantile seizures","Progressive liver dysfunction with coagulopathy","Aspiration and respiratory compromise from severe hypotonia"],"prognosis":"Neonatal adrenoleukodystrophy is progressive with a poor prognosis; although milder than classic Zellweger syndrome, most affected children have severe neurodevelopmental impairment and shortened survival, with outcome depending on residual peroxisomal function.","pitfalls":["Confusing this recessive peroxisome biogenesis disorder with X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy, which differs in inheritance and mechanism","Overlooking adrenal insufficiency that can be life-threatening","Ordering only very-long-chain fatty acids and not the broader peroxisomal panel needed to characterise a biogenesis defect","Missing associated hearing and vision loss that affect development and support needs"],"management":["No disease-specific therapy exists; care is supportive, with glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement for adrenal insufficiency","Anticonvulsant therapy for seizures","Nutritional support with gastrostomy feeding, fat-soluble vitamin supplementation and management of hepatic dysfunction and coagulopathy","Sensory support with hearing aids for sensorineural hearing loss and ophthalmological care for retinopathy","Palliative and developmental supportive care given the progressive neurodegeneration","Multidisciplinary coordination across metabolic medicine, neurology, ophthalmology and audiology","Genetic counselling and family screening for the autosomal recessive PEX gene disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Zellweger Spectrum Disorder","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_alloimmune_thrombocytopenia":{"name":"Neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia","system":"Neonatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NAIT","FNAIT","foetal and neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia","feto-maternal alloimmune thrombocytopenia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cloherty and Stark Manual of Neonatal Care / Nathan and Oski's Hematology of Infancy and Childhood","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia classically causes severe isolated thrombocytopenia in an otherwise well-appearing term neonate born to a healthy mother with a normal platelet count","It is the platelet equivalent of haemolytic disease of the newborn, caused by maternal antibodies against paternally inherited platelet antigens the mother lacks","In many populations the most common culprit is alloimmunisation to the human platelet antigen HPA-1a","Unlike red cell disease, a first pregnancy can be severely affected, and there may be no warning until bleeding or thrombocytopenia is discovered","The most feared complication is intracranial haemorrhage, which can occur antenatally as well as after birth","Diagnosis is supported by demonstrating maternal platelet-specific alloantibodies and incompatibility between maternal and foetal or paternal platelet antigens","Management principles include prompt platelet transfusion for significant bleeding or very low counts, with antigen-compatible or washed maternal platelets preferred, and intravenous immunoglobulin as an adjunct"],"pathophysiology":"Neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia occurs when the foetus inherits a platelet antigen from the father that is absent in the mother. Foetal platelets crossing into the maternal circulation stimulate production of maternal IgG alloantibodies, which then cross the placenta and bind foetal and neonatal platelets bearing the target antigen. These opsonised platelets are removed by the reticuloendothelial system, producing severe thrombocytopenia. Because IgG transfer and sensitisation can occur early, a first pregnancy may be affected and haemorrhage, including intracranial bleeding, can develop before birth. The mother remains unaffected because her own platelets lack the antigen, which is why she has a normal platelet count.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis or disseminated intravascular coagulation causing thrombocytopenia","Maternal immune thrombocytopenia with transplacental autoantibodies","Congenital infection such as cytomegalovirus, toxoplasmosis, or rubella","Inherited thrombocytopenia or bone marrow failure syndromes","Thrombocytopenia associated with placental insufficiency or intrauterine growth restriction","Consumptive thrombocytopenia from thrombosis or a giant haemangioma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film to confirm isolated thrombocytopenia and exclude other cytopenias","Maternal and paternal (or neonatal) platelet antigen typing to demonstrate incompatibility","Detection of maternal platelet-specific alloantibodies","Cranial ultrasound to screen for intracranial haemorrhage","Maternal platelet count to distinguish alloimmune from maternal autoimmune causes","Sepsis and congenital infection screen when the clinical picture is atypical"],"redFlags":["Signs of intracranial haemorrhage such as seizures, bulging fontanelle, apnoea, or altered consciousness","Profoundly low platelet count with active bleeding","Antenatal detection of foetal intracranial haemorrhage or ventriculomegaly","A previously affected sibling, which predicts more severe disease in subsequent pregnancies"],"prognosis":"Most affected neonates recover as maternal antibody levels fall and platelet counts normalise over days to a few weeks. Prognosis is excellent in the absence of intracranial haemorrhage, but antenatal or neonatal intracranial bleeding can cause death or lasting neurological disability. Subsequent pregnancies are typically as severe or more severe and warrant specialist antenatal management.","pitfalls":["Attributing severe thrombocytopenia in a well neonate to sepsis and missing the alloimmune diagnosis","Transfusing only random-donor platelets when antigen-negative or washed maternal platelets are more effective","Failing to arrange cranial imaging to look for occult intracranial haemorrhage","Not counselling parents about the high recurrence risk and need for specialist care in future pregnancies","Confusing alloimmune with maternal autoimmune thrombocytopenia, which differ in mechanism and management"],"management":["Transfuse antigen-negative (e.g. HPA-1a-negative), compatible platelets as first-line for severe thrombocytopenia or active bleeding, using washed maternal or matched-donor units where available","Give intravenous immunoglobulin, with or without corticosteroids, to raise the platelet count when antigen-matched platelets are unavailable or as an adjunct","Transfuse random-donor platelets without delay in an emergency if compatible units cannot be obtained","Perform serial full blood counts to track platelet recovery as maternal alloantibody wanes over days to weeks","Undertake cranial ultrasound surveillance for intracranial haemorrhage, the most feared complication","Refer to foetal medicine and give antenatal maternal intravenous immunoglobulin (with corticosteroids) in subsequent at-risk pregnancies to prevent foetal haemorrhage","Arrange parental platelet antigen typing and genetic counselling regarding the high recurrence and increasing severity in future pregnancies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cloherty and Stark Manual of Neonatal Care","Nathan and Oski's Hematology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_diabetes_mellitus":{"name":"Neonatal diabetes mellitus (KCNJ11/ABCC8/INS)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NDM","monogenic neonatal diabetes","KATP channel neonatal diabetes","permanent neonatal diabetes","transient neonatal diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as diabetes diagnosed in the first months of life, most commonly within the first six months, when autoimmune type 1 diabetes is very unlikely","Common genetic causes include activating mutations in the KATP channel genes KCNJ11 and ABCC8 and mutations in the insulin gene INS","Presents with hyperglycaemia, poor weight gain, failure to thrive and sometimes ketoacidosis or dehydration in a young infant","KATP channel forms are frequently sulfonylurea-responsive, allowing many infants to transition from insulin to oral sulfonylurea therapy","Can be permanent or transient; transient forms often relate to chromosome 6q24 abnormalities and may relapse later in life","Some KCNJ11 mutations cause the DEND syndrome with developmental delay, epilepsy and neonatal diabetes, reflecting KATP channels in neural tissue","Urgent genetic testing is essential because the result directly changes treatment and prognosis"],"pathophysiology":"In beta cells the ATP-sensitive potassium (KATP) channel links glucose metabolism to insulin release; rising ATP normally closes the channel, depolarising the cell and triggering secretion. Activating mutations in KCNJ11 or ABCC8 hold the channel open, preventing depolarisation and impairing insulin release, yet high-affinity sulfonylureas can close the channel independently of ATP and restore secretion. INS mutations instead cause misfolding of proinsulin and beta-cell stress, while chromosome 6q24 abnormalities perturb imprinted growth-regulating genes and typically produce transient disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Type 1 diabetes mellitus presenting in infancy","Stress or illness-related transient hyperglycaemia of the newborn","Neonatal diabetes as part of a syndrome such as Wolcott-Rallison or IPEX","Pancreatic agenesis or hypoplasia","Iatrogenic hyperglycaemia from glucocorticoids or dextrose infusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma glucose, ketones and blood gas to assess severity and ketoacidosis","C-peptide and insulin levels to gauge endogenous secretion","Urgent molecular genetic testing for KCNJ11, ABCC8, INS and 6q24 abnormalities","Pancreatic autoantibodies, typically negative, to help exclude autoimmune diabetes","Pancreatic imaging and syndromic assessment when structural or syndromic causes are suspected"],"redFlags":["Diabetic ketoacidosis or severe dehydration in a young infant requiring emergency stabilisation","Neurological features such as developmental delay or seizures suggesting a DEND-type presentation","Failure to thrive or intractable hyperglycaemia needing rapid diagnostic clarification","Associated syndromic features indicating a broader multisystem disorder"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies by genotype: many KATP channel cases achieve excellent control on sulfonylureas with potential neurodevelopmental benefit, transient forms may remit though relapse is common, while syndromic and INS-related forms carry a more guarded outlook.","pitfalls":["Assuming any infantile diabetes is type 1 and committing to lifelong insulin without genetic testing","Missing the opportunity to transition sulfonylurea-responsive cases off insulin","Overlooking neurodevelopmental features that indicate a KATP channel syndrome","Failing to plan follow-up for transient neonatal diabetes that can relapse in later life"],"management":["Start insulin to control hyperglycaemia at presentation and to treat any ketoacidosis or dehydration","After urgent genotyping, trial an oral sulfonylurea in KATP-channel (KCNJ11/ABCC8) disease to close the channel and transition many infants off insulin","Maintain insulin therapy for INS-gene, 6q24 and sulfonylurea-unresponsive forms","Monitor glycaemic control, feeding and growth, and screen for the neurodevelopmental features of DEND syndrome in KCNJ11 disease","Anticipate remission in transient (6q24) forms and follow up for later relapse in childhood or adolescence","Refer to paediatric endocrinology and clinical genetics, with genetic counselling for the family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_hemochromatosis":{"name":"Neonatal haemochromatosis","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["gestational alloimmune liver disease","GALD","neonatal iron storage disease","perinatal haemochromatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common recognised cause is gestational alloimmune liver disease (GALD), an alloimmune process rather than a primary genetic iron-handling defect","It presents as severe neonatal liver failure with coagulopathy, hypoalbuminaemia, jaundice, hypoglycaemia, and often intrauterine growth restriction","There is extrahepatic siderosis, with iron deposition in sites such as the pancreas, heart, and salivary glands, while sparing the reticuloendothelial system","Markedly raised ferritin with high transferrin saturation is characteristic but not specific","Diagnosis is supported by demonstrating extrahepatic iron, classically by lip (minor salivary gland) biopsy or magnetic resonance imaging","The condition has a very high recurrence rate in subsequent pregnancies of the same mother, which has important management implications","Treatment centres on exchange transfusion and intravenous immunoglobulin to remove and neutralise the offending alloantibody, with liver transplantation reserved for refractory failure"],"pathophysiology":"In gestational alloimmune liver disease, maternal immunoglobulin G antibodies directed against a fetal hepatocyte antigen cross the placenta and activate the complement cascade on fetal liver cells, causing widespread hepatocyte injury and necrosis in utero. The resulting fetal liver failure disturbs the regulation of iron transport across the placenta, leading to excessive iron loading of fetal tissues. Iron therefore accumulates in the liver and in extrahepatic parenchymal sites such as the pancreas, heart, and salivary and other glands, characteristically sparing the reticuloendothelial system. The severe hepatic dysfunction present at birth reflects damage that began during gestation, and the same maternal alloimmunity tends to recur in later pregnancies.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis with liver dysfunction","Perinatal viral hepatitis and congenital infections","Inherited metabolic disease such as tyrosinaemia and mitochondrial disorders","Bile acid synthesis defects","Haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","Other causes of neonatal acute liver failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ferritin and transferrin saturation","Coagulation studies and markers of synthetic liver function","Lip (minor salivary gland) biopsy to demonstrate extrahepatic siderosis","Magnetic resonance imaging to detect extrahepatic iron deposition","Metabolic and infective screen to exclude other causes of neonatal liver failure","Placental and, where relevant, postmortem examination to support the diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Severe coagulopathy unresponsive to vitamin K signalling advanced liver failure","Refractory hypoglycaemia and metabolic instability","Rapid clinical deterioration despite supportive care","A history of prior affected pregnancy or unexplained fetal loss indicating high recurrence risk"],"prognosis":"Untreated the condition carries a very high mortality from neonatal liver failure, but alloimmune-directed therapy and, when needed, liver transplantation have improved survival; recurrence in subsequent pregnancies is common and can be mitigated by maternal treatment during gestation.","pitfalls":["Assuming it is a primary genetic iron-overload disorder rather than an alloimmune process","Relying on raised ferritin alone, which is neither sensitive nor specific","Failing to demonstrate extrahepatic siderosis to support the diagnosis","Overlooking the high recurrence risk and the opportunity for maternal treatment in future pregnancies","Delaying alloimmune-directed therapy while awaiting confirmatory tests in a deteriorating neonate"],"management":["Treat established disease with exchange transfusion and intravenous immunoglobulin to remove and dilute the maternal alloantibody and limit ongoing hepatocyte injury","Provide intensive supportive care for neonatal liver failure, correcting coagulopathy with fresh frozen plasma and vitamin K and treating hypoglycaemia","Refer for liver transplantation when synthetic failure is irreversible despite alloimmune-directed therapy","Monitor coagulation, albumin, bilirubin and ferritin as markers of hepatic recovery or deterioration","Administer antenatal maternal intravenous immunoglobulin through subsequent pregnancies to mitigate the high recurrence risk","Coordinate care across neonatal hepatology, transplant services and clinical genetics, with counselling on recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_hypocalcaemia":{"name":"Neonatal hypocalcaemia","system":"Neonatal / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["low calcium in the newborn"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neonatology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A low serum calcium in the newborn, conventionally divided into early (first days) and late (end of the first week or later) onset","Early hypocalcaemia is associated with prematurity, maternal diabetes, growth restriction and perinatal asphyxia","Late hypocalcaemia is linked to high phosphate intake, hypomagnesaemia, vitamin D deficiency and hypoparathyroidism","It is often asymptomatic but can cause jitteriness, irritability, feeding difficulty, apnoea and seizures","Persistent or refractory hypocalcaemia should prompt consideration of hypomagnesaemia, DiGeorge syndrome and vitamin D disorders","Management corrects calcium (and any magnesium deficiency) and addresses the underlying cause"],"pathophysiology":"At birth the continuous placental calcium supply ceases, and the newborn depends on parathyroid hormone, vitamin D and calcium intake to maintain calcium. Early hypocalcaemia reflects an exaggerated postnatal fall with immature or suppressed parathyroid responses, more marked in preterm, growth-restricted and asphyxiated infants and those of diabetic mothers. Late hypocalcaemia results from factors that impair calcium handling, such as a high phosphate load, magnesium deficiency (which impairs parathyroid hormone secretion and action), vitamin D deficiency, or congenital hypoparathyroidism as in DiGeorge syndrome. Low ionised calcium increases neuromuscular excitability, causing the clinical features.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypomagnesaemia","Vitamin D deficiency or disorders of vitamin D metabolism","Hypoparathyroidism (for example DiGeorge syndrome)","Hypoglycaemia (as another cause of jitteriness and seizures)","Sepsis","Alkalosis reducing ionised calcium"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum total and ionised calcium","Magnesium, phosphate and alkaline phosphatase","Parathyroid hormone and vitamin D where persistent","Assessment for DiGeorge syndrome if features suggest it","Glucose and sepsis evaluation for symptomatic infants"],"redFlags":["Seizures or significant neuromuscular irritability","Refractory hypocalcaemia suggesting hypomagnesaemia or hypoparathyroidism","Features suggesting DiGeorge syndrome (cardiac, dysmorphic, immune)","Cardiac arrhythmia or prolonged QT interval"],"prognosis":"Early hypocalcaemia usually resolves within days with correction and treatment of contributing factors; prognosis of late or persistent hypocalcaemia depends on the underlying cause.","pitfalls":["Failing to correct coexisting hypomagnesaemia, without which calcium is hard to normalise","Overlooking DiGeorge syndrome in persistent hypocalcaemia","Confusing the cause of seizures with hypoglycaemia","Giving a high-phosphate feed that perpetuates late hypocalcaemia"],"management":["Give intravenous calcium (calcium gluconate) for symptomatic or severe hypocalcaemia and oral calcium supplementation for milder cases","Correct coexisting hypomagnesaemia with magnesium, as it impairs parathyroid hormone secretion and perpetuates refractory hypocalcaemia","Treat the underlying cause: reduce a high phosphate load, replace vitamin D in deficiency, and manage hypoparathyroidism","Deliver intravenous calcium under cardiac monitoring for seizures or apnoea, watching for bradycardia and extravasation injury","Monitor total and ionised calcium, phosphate and magnesium during correction","Investigate DiGeorge syndrome and vitamin D disorders in persistent or late hypocalcaemia, with genetics referral where a 22q11 deletion is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neonatology reference texts (standard reference)","Paediatric endocrinology references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_hypoglycaemia":{"name":"Neonatal hypoglycaemia","system":"Neonatal / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["low blood glucose in the newborn"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neonatology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A low blood glucose in the newborn period that is common, often transient, but potentially harmful to the developing brain","At-risk groups include infants of diabetic mothers, small- or large-for-gestational-age babies, preterm infants and those who are unwell or cold","It is frequently asymptomatic but can cause jitteriness, poor feeding, hypotonia, apnoea, seizures or a reduced conscious level","Screening of at-risk and symptomatic babies with reliable glucose measurement guides management","Persistent or severe hypoglycaemia beyond the transitional period suggests hyperinsulinism or a metabolic or endocrine disorder","Management ranges from supported feeding to intravenous dextrose, with investigation of refractory cases during a hypoglycaemic episode"],"pathophysiology":"After birth the newborn must transition from a continuous placental glucose supply to intermittent enteral feeding, mobilising glycogen and generating glucose through gluconeogenesis and using alternative fuels such as ketones. Hypoglycaemia arises when demand exceeds supply or counter-regulation is inadequate, for example with limited glycogen stores in growth-restricted or preterm babies, or with excess insulin in infants of diabetic mothers and in congenital hyperinsulinism. Because the brain depends on glucose, prolonged or severe hypoglycaemia, particularly when alternative fuels are lacking, risks neuronal injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Transitional hypoglycaemia of the healthy newborn","Congenital hyperinsulinism","Sepsis","Inborn errors of metabolism","Endocrine disorders (for example hypopituitarism, cortisol deficiency)","Perinatal stress or asphyxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Reliable blood glucose measurement (laboratory-confirmed for low readings)","A hypoglycaemia screen taken during a low-glucose episode in persistent cases","Assessment for sepsis and other acute illness","Metabolic and endocrine investigations for refractory or persistent disease","Monitoring of feeding and glucose response"],"redFlags":["Seizures or reduced conscious level","Persistent or recurrent hypoglycaemia beyond the transitional period","Very low glucose requiring high dextrose delivery, suggesting hyperinsulinism","Features suggesting sepsis or a metabolic disorder"],"prognosis":"Transient hypoglycaemia corrected promptly usually has an excellent outcome, but severe, prolonged or recurrent hypoglycaemia, especially with symptoms, carries a risk of neurodevelopmental injury.","pitfalls":["Relying on point-of-care meters that are inaccurate at low values","Failing to obtain a hypoglycaemia screen during the episode in persistent cases","Missing hyperinsulinism as a cause of high glucose requirement","Overlooking sepsis or a metabolic disorder"],"management":["Establish early and frequent enteral feeding as the first-line measure in at-risk infants","Give intravenous dextrose for symptomatic infants or those failing to maintain glucose on feeds, titrating to the glucose response","Treat suspected congenital hyperinsulinism with diazoxide, adding octreotide or glucagon for diazoxide-unresponsive disease","Maintain normothermia and treat concurrent illness such as sepsis that increases glucose demand","Monitor blood glucose with laboratory confirmation of low bedside readings","Take a hypoglycaemia screen during a documented low-glucose episode and investigate persistent disease for metabolic and endocrine disorders","Refer to paediatric endocrine or metabolic services for persistent hyperinsulinism or an inborn error of metabolism"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neonatology reference texts (standard reference)","British Association of Perinatal Medicine guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_hypoglycemia":{"name":"Neonatal Hypoglycaemia","system":"Endocrine/Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["low blood sugar in newborn","hypoglycaemia of the newborn","neonatal low glucose"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Low blood glucose is common in the transitional period after birth as the newborn adapts from continuous placental glucose supply to intermittent feeding.","Infants at highest risk include those of diabetic mothers, large- or small-for-gestational-age babies, preterm infants, and any newborn who is stressed, cold, or septic.","Signs are often subtle and non-specific: jitteriness, poor feeding, lethargy, hypotonia, temperature instability, apnoea, or seizures; many affected infants appear asymptomatic.","At-risk and symptomatic infants should be screened with bedside glucose testing, with low values confirmed on a laboratory sample given the imprecision of point-of-care meters.","Early and frequent feeding is the first-line measure; infants who cannot maintain adequate glucose or who are symptomatic require intravenous dextrose.","Symptomatic hypoglycaemia with a documented low glucose that resolves when glucose is normalised (Whipple triad) warrants investigation and treatment.","Persistent or recurrent hypoglycaemia beyond the first days of life suggests an underlying disorder such as hyperinsulinism, endocrine deficiency, or an inborn error of metabolism.","The clinical concern is that prolonged or severe low glucose can injure the developing brain, so prevention and prompt correction are priorities."],"pathophysiology":"After the cord is cut, the newborn loses its steady maternal glucose supply and must mobilise hepatic glycogen, activate gluconeogenesis, and use alternative fuels such as ketones and lactate, processes driven by a surge in counter-regulatory hormones and a fall in insulin. When glycogen stores are limited (prematurity, growth restriction), glucose demand is high (stress, sepsis, hypothermia), or insulin secretion is excessive (infant of a diabetic mother, hyperinsulinism), this adaptation fails and blood glucose falls. Because the neonatal brain depends heavily on glucose, inadequate supply combined with immature ketogenesis in some infants places the brain at risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Sepsis","Neonatal seizures from other causes","Hypocalcaemia or other electrolyte disturbance","Congenital hyperinsulinism","Panhypopituitarism or cortisol deficiency","Inborn errors of metabolism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Confirmatory laboratory plasma glucose","Critical sample during hypoglycaemia (insulin, cortisol, growth hormone, ketones, free fatty acids) when persistent","Serum electrolytes and calcium","Sepsis screen if clinically indicated","Metabolic screen (ammonia, lactate, acylcarnitine profile) in recurrent or refractory cases"],"redFlags":["Seizures, apnoea, or coma","Persistent hypoglycaemia despite feeding and initial glucose infusion","High glucose infusion requirement suggesting hyperinsulinism","Recurrent hypoglycaemia beyond the transitional period","Associated hypotonia, poor feeding, or encephalopathy"],"prognosis":"Brief, promptly treated transitional hypoglycaemia in an otherwise well infant usually has an excellent outcome, whereas severe, prolonged, symptomatic, or recurrent hypoglycaemia carries a risk of long-term neurodevelopmental impairment.","pitfalls":["Treating an isolated low bedside meter reading without confirming on a laboratory sample or clinical context","Assuming all at-risk infants are symptomatic, or dismissing subtle signs as normal newborn behaviour","Failing to investigate hypoglycaemia that persists or recurs beyond the first days of life","Overlooking hyperinsulinism when a high rate of glucose infusion is needed to keep glucose normal"],"management":["Initiate early, frequent feeding (breast or formula) as first-line treatment for at-risk and mildly affected newborns","Escalate to intravenous dextrose for symptomatic hypoglycaemia or when feeding fails to normalise glucose","Investigate and treat persistent hyperinsulinism with diazoxide, reserving octreotide or glucagon for unresponsive cases","Correct and prevent contributing factors such as hypothermia, sepsis and inadequate intake","Confirm low bedside glucose values on a laboratory sample given point-of-care imprecision","Draw a critical (hypoglycaemia) sample during a low-glucose episode in persistent cases and pursue a metabolic and endocrine work-up","Refer refractory or congenital hyperinsulinism to specialist endocrine and metabolic services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_ichthyosis_sclerosing_cholangitis":{"name":"Neonatal ichthyosis-sclerosing cholangitis syndrome","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NISCH syndrome","ichthyosis-sclerosing cholangitis","CLDN1 deficiency","claudin-1 deficiency","neonatal sclerosing cholangitis with ichthyosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / Suchy Liver Disease in Children","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal recessive syndrome combining scalp and skin ichthyosis with neonatal sclerosing cholangitis, caused by mutations in CLDN1 encoding claudin-1","Cholestasis presents in the neonatal period with conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia and a raised gamma-glutamyl transferase, unlike low-GGT inherited cholestases","Cholangiography demonstrates an irregular, beaded intrahepatic biliary tree typical of sclerosing cholangitis","Characteristic features include a scaly (ichthyotic) skin, scalp hypotrichosis or scarring alopecia, and sometimes oligodontia and dental anomalies","Claudin-1 is a tight-junction protein of biliary epithelium and skin; its loss disrupts the epithelial barrier in both organs","Diagnosis integrates the dermatological and cholestatic features with cholangiographic findings and CLDN1 sequencing","Management is supportive with ursodeoxycholic acid, fat-soluble vitamins, and skin care, with liver transplantation for progressive biliary cirrhosis"],"pathophysiology":"Neonatal ichthyosis-sclerosing cholangitis (NISCH) syndrome is caused by biallelic mutations in CLDN1, which encodes claudin-1, an integral tight-junction protein maintaining paracellular barriers in the biliary epithelium and epidermis. Loss of claudin-1 disrupts these junctions, producing an ichthyotic skin phenotype and a sclerosing cholangitis in which bile leaks across a defective canalicular/ductular barrier and provokes biliary inflammation, strictures, and progressive fibrosis. The combination of high-GGT neonatal cholestasis with ichthyosis and scalp involvement is characteristic.","additionalDifferentials":["Biliary atresia","Primary sclerosing cholangitis of other cause","Alagille syndrome","Other neonatal sclerosing cholangitis (e.g. DCDC2-related)","Ichthyosis syndromes without hepatic disease","Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Liver function tests and gamma-glutamyl transferase (characteristically raised)","Serum bile acids and fractionated bilirubin","Cholangiography (MRCP or intraoperative) showing irregular sclerosing bile ducts","Liver biopsy demonstrating ductular reaction, cholangitis, and fibrosis","Dermatological assessment and skin examination for ichthyosis and hypotrichosis","CLDN1 gene sequencing"],"redFlags":["Progressive biliary cirrhosis with portal hypertension","Vitamin K-dependent coagulopathy in the cholestatic neonate","Recurrent cholangitis with fever and worsening jaundice","Failure to thrive from fat and fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption"],"prognosis":"The sclerosing cholangitis is usually progressive, and many children develop biliary cirrhosis requiring liver transplantation over time; the cutaneous features persist but are managed supportively, and outcome depends chiefly on the hepatic course.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnostic significance of coexisting ichthyosis and scalp involvement in a cholestatic infant","Confusing the cholangiographic appearance with biliary atresia","Assuming a low GGT, which does not fit and can delay the correct diagnosis","Neglecting fat-soluble vitamin supplementation and skin care"],"management":["Manage the cholestasis with ursodeoxycholic acid and choleretic support to improve bile flow","Supplement fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E and K) and provide medium-chain-triglyceride-enriched nutrition to counter fat malabsorption and support growth","Control cholestatic pruritus with agents such as rifampicin or a bile-acid sequestrant","Treat the ichthyosis supportively with emollients and keratolytic skin care under dermatology","Monitor liver function and disease progression, surveying for biliary cirrhosis and portal hypertension","Refer for liver transplantation as the sclerosing cholangitis progresses to end-stage liver disease","Confirm the diagnosis with CLDN1 sequencing and provide genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Suchy, Sokol & Balistreri, Liver Disease in Children"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_intraventricular_haemorrhage":{"name":"Neonatal intraventricular haemorrhage","system":"Neonatal / Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["germinal matrix haemorrhage","IVH of prematurity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neonatology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Bleeding arising from the fragile germinal matrix, chiefly in preterm infants, with risk inversely related to gestation and birth weight","Most haemorrhages occur within the first few days of life","It is graded from small germinal matrix or intraventricular bleeding to large haemorrhage with ventricular dilatation or parenchymal involvement","Presentation ranges from clinically silent to a catastrophic decline with bulging fontanelle, seizures and shock","Cranial ultrasound is the mainstay of diagnosis and surveillance in at-risk infants","Post-haemorrhagic ventricular dilatation and parenchymal (grade IV) involvement carry the greatest risk of adverse outcome"],"pathophysiology":"In preterm infants the germinal matrix is a highly vascular, metabolically active region with fragile, poorly supported vessels. Immature cerebral autoregulation means fluctuations in cerebral blood flow and pressure, from hypotension, hypercapnia, ventilation or handling, are transmitted to these vessels and cause them to rupture. Blood may remain localised to the matrix or extend into the ventricles, and large haemorrhages can obstruct cerebrospinal fluid flow causing ventricular dilatation, or be accompanied by venous infarction of adjacent white matter, which underlies the neurological sequelae.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Neonatal seizures of other cause","Metabolic derangement (hypoglycaemia, electrolyte disturbance)","Sepsis or meningitis","Other intracranial haemorrhage (subdural, subarachnoid)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cranial ultrasound for diagnosis, grading and serial monitoring","Measurement of head circumference and fontanelle assessment","Coagulation studies and full blood count","MRI where parenchymal injury or prognosis needs clarification","Monitoring for post-haemorrhagic ventricular dilatation"],"redFlags":["Rapid neurological deterioration with a tense fontanelle","Progressive post-haemorrhagic ventricular dilatation","Parenchymal (grade IV) involvement","Seizures or cardiovascular collapse"],"prognosis":"Low-grade haemorrhages often have a good outcome, whereas high-grade haemorrhage with parenchymal involvement or progressive ventricular dilatation carries a substantial risk of cerebral palsy and developmental impairment.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal neurological examination excludes haemorrhage","Failing to monitor for evolving ventricular dilatation","Overlooking modifiable haemodynamic factors that propagate bleeding","Not arranging neurodevelopmental follow-up after high-grade haemorrhage"],"management":["Prevent haemorrhage through antenatal corticosteroids, delayed cord clamping, gentle handling and avoidance of swings in blood pressure, carbon dioxide and cerebral blood flow","Provide supportive neonatal intensive care, stabilising haemodynamics and ventilation and correcting coagulopathy and anaemia with blood products","Perform serial cranial ultrasound for diagnosis, grading and detection of progressive ventricular dilatation","Monitor head circumference and the fontanelle, and treat seizures if they occur","Manage post-haemorrhagic ventricular dilatation with temporising cerebrospinal fluid drainage (ventricular tap or reservoir) and ventriculoperitoneal shunt insertion via neurosurgery","Arrange structured neurodevelopmental follow-up given the risk of cerebral palsy and developmental impairment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neonatology reference texts (standard reference)","Paediatric neurology references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_jaundice":{"name":"Neonatal jaundice","system":"Neonatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neonatal hyperbilirubinaemia","jaundice of the newborn","physiological jaundice","icterus neonatorum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Visible yellow discolouration of skin and sclerae from raised serum bilirubin, extremely common in newborns and usually due to unconjugated (indirect) hyperbilirubinaemia.","Physiological jaundice appears after the first 24 hours, peaks around days 3-5 in term infants, and resolves without harm; it reflects normal neonatal bilirubin handling.","Jaundice in the first 24 hours of life is always pathological and demands urgent evaluation, commonly for haemolysis.","Key pathological causes include blood-group incompatibility (Rh, ABO), G6PD deficiency, sepsis, and other haemolytic conditions.","Conjugated (direct) hyperbilirubinaemia is never physiological and requires investigation for cholestasis, notably biliary atresia, which needs early surgery.","Breastfeeding-associated (early, from inadequate intake) and breast-milk (later, prolonged) jaundice are common benign patterns once other causes are excluded.","Management depends on bilirubin level plotted against age in hours and risk factors; phototherapy is first-line and exchange transfusion is reserved for dangerously high or rapidly rising levels.","The feared complication is bilirubin-induced neurological dysfunction (acute encephalopathy progressing to kernicterus), which is preventable with timely treatment."],"pathophysiology":"Newborns have increased bilirubin production from a high red cell mass with shorter red cell lifespan, combined with immature hepatic conjugation (low UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity) and enhanced enterohepatic recirculation of bilirubin. These factors produce transient physiological unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia. Pathological jaundice arises when production is excessive (haemolysis, extravasated blood), conjugation or uptake is impaired, or excretion is obstructed (cholestasis). Unbound unconjugated bilirubin is lipid-soluble and can cross the blood-brain barrier, depositing in the basal ganglia and brainstem nuclei to cause neurotoxicity.","additionalDifferentials":["Rh or ABO haemolytic disease","G6PD deficiency and other red cell enzyme/membrane defects","Sepsis or congenital infection","Biliary atresia and other causes of neonatal cholestasis","Hypothyroidism","Breastfeeding and breast-milk jaundice","Cephalohaematoma or extensive bruising"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Total and conjugated (direct) serum bilirubin, interpreted against age in hours","Transcutaneous bilirubinometry for screening","Blood group and direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test on infant and mother","Full blood count, reticulocyte count, and blood film","G6PD assay where clinically indicated","Thyroid function and septic screen when suggested by the picture","Assessment for cholestasis (e.g. pale stools, dark urine) when direct bilirubin is raised"],"redFlags":["Jaundice within the first 24 hours of life","Rapidly rising bilirubin or a level near the exchange-transfusion threshold","Signs of acute bilirubin encephalopathy: lethargy, poor feeding, hypotonia progressing to hypertonia, high-pitched cry, arching","Conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia, pale stools, and dark urine suggesting biliary atresia","Prolonged jaundice beyond two weeks (term) or three weeks (preterm) requiring evaluation"],"prognosis":"Most neonatal jaundice is benign and resolves without sequelae; with appropriate monitoring and treatment, kernicterus is largely preventable, but untreated severe hyperbilirubinaemia can cause permanent neurological injury.","pitfalls":["Dismissing jaundice in the first 24 hours as physiological","Failing to fractionate bilirubin and missing cholestasis / biliary atresia","Assessing jaundice by clinical appearance alone rather than measured bilirubin against age-specific thresholds","Overlooking dehydration from inadequate feeding as a contributor in early breastfeeding jaundice","Not investigating jaundice that persists beyond the expected period"],"management":["Treat significant unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with phototherapy, interpreting levels against age-in-hours thresholds","Perform exchange transfusion for severe or rapidly rising bilirubin or signs of acute bilirubin encephalopathy","Give intravenous immunoglobulin in isoimmune haemolytic disease (Rh or ABO) to reduce haemolysis and the need for exchange transfusion","Ensure adequate feeding and hydration, supporting breastfeeding and correcting inadequate intake","Investigate and treat the underlying cause, including antibiotics for sepsis and early surgical referral (Kasai portoenterostomy) for biliary atresia in conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia","Monitor bilirubin trends and watch for evolving neurological signs to prevent kernicterus"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_onset_multisystem_inflammatory_disease":{"name":"neonatal onset multisystem inflammatory disease","system":"Immunological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NOMID","CINCA syndrome","chronic infantile neurological cutaneous and articular syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology, Cassidy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most severe phenotype of the cryopyrin-associated periodic syndrome spectrum, caused by gain-of-function NLRP3 variants that are frequently de novo and often present as somatic mosaicism.","Presents in the neonatal period with a near-continuous migratory urticarial rash, in contrast to the episodic attacks of the milder phenotypes.","Chronic aseptic meningitis produces raised intracranial pressure, chronic headache, papilloedema, optic atrophy, sensorineural hearing loss and, if untreated, developmental delay.","A characteristic deforming arthropathy with bony overgrowth of the epiphyses, especially of the patella and long-bone growth plates, is highly suggestive and can be radiographically distinctive.","Dysmorphic features evolve, including frontal bossing, saddle-shaped nose and macrocephaly, together with growth failure.","Reactive AA amyloidosis is a serious late complication of poorly controlled disease.","Early interleukin-1 blockade transforms the natural history and can prevent irreversible neurological, ophthalmological and auditory damage.","Cerebrospinal fluid typically shows a raised opening pressure with a sterile neutrophil-predominant pleocytosis."],"pathophysiology":"Highly activating NLRP3 variants cause near-constitutive assembly of the cryopyrin inflammasome with minimal need for any trigger, driving continuous caspase-1 activation and overwhelming secretion of interleukin-1 beta and interleukin-18. The resulting unremitting innate immune activation produces persistent multisystem inflammation: neutrophilic urticarial dermatosis, chronic aseptic leptomeningitis with raised intracranial pressure, cochlear and optic nerve damage, and a growth-plate arthropathy with disordered endochondral ossification and epiphyseal overgrowth. Somatic mosaicism, in which the activating variant is present in only a proportion of cells, is common and can yield a negative conventional germline test despite classic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Muckle-Wells syndrome","Familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome","Deficiency of the interleukin-1 receptor antagonist","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis of very early onset","Congenital (TORCH) infection","Neonatal sepsis or congenital bacterial meningitis","Aicardi-Goutieres syndrome and other interferonopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["NLRP3 gene analysis with deep or next-generation sequencing to detect somatic mosaicism","Cerebrospinal fluid examination for raised opening pressure and sterile pleocytosis","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain for leptomeningeal enhancement and ventricular changes","Pure-tone audiometry and serial ophthalmological assessment including fundoscopy for papilloedema","Skeletal radiographs demonstrating epiphyseal and physeal overgrowth","Acute-phase reactants with serum amyloid A and urinalysis for amyloidosis surveillance"],"redFlags":["Signs of raised intracranial pressure such as papilloedema, bulging fontanelle or persistent vomiting","Progressive visual or hearing loss indicating ongoing neurosensory damage","Developmental regression or seizures","Proteinuria signalling renal AA amyloidosis"],"prognosis":"Without treatment the disorder causes progressive neurological, visual and auditory disability, deforming arthropathy and a shortened lifespan. Early interleukin-1 blockade substantially improves outcome and can preserve neurosensory function, though damage sustained before treatment is often irreversible.","pitfalls":["Concluding the diagnosis is excluded by a negative germline NLRP3 test when somatic mosaicism has not been sought","Misinterpreting the sterile neutrophilic cerebrospinal fluid pleocytosis as bacterial meningitis","Delaying interleukin-1 blockade and allowing irreversible neurosensory injury to accumulate","Overlooking the diagnostic value of the characteristic epiphyseal bony overgrowth on imaging"],"management":["Start interleukin-1 blockade (anakinra, canakinumab or rilonacept) as the disease-specific mainstay to suppress inflammasome-driven inflammation and preserve neurosensory function","Manage raised intracranial pressure from chronic aseptic meningitis and provide hearing and visual aids for established deficits","Monitor acute-phase reactants and serum amyloid A to guide therapy and confirm inflammatory control","Undertake serial audiometry, ophthalmological review with fundoscopy, and skeletal assessment of the deforming arthropathy","Screen for reactive AA amyloidosis with urinalysis for proteinuria as a late complication","Confirm the NLRP3 variant using deep or next-generation sequencing to detect somatic mosaicism, with genetic counselling","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across paediatric rheumatology, neurology, audiology and ophthalmology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology, Cassidy (current edition)","SHARE recommendations for the management of autoinflammatory diseases","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics (current edition)"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_ophthalmia":{"name":"Ophthalmia neonatorum","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ophthalmia neonatorum","neonatal conjunctivitis","gonococcal ophthalmia neonatorum","chlamydial ophthalmia neonatorum","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Chlamydia trachomatis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell / BASHH-CDC STI guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Ophthalmia neonatorum is conjunctivitis in the first month of life, classically acquired as the infant passes through an infected birth canal","The two most important sexually transmitted causes are Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis, alongside other bacteria, herpes simplex virus, and chemical irritation","Timing offers a clue: chemical conjunctivitis appears within the first day or two, gonococcal disease typically within the first few days, and chlamydial disease usually toward the end of the first week or later","Gonococcal ophthalmia is a sight-threatening emergency because it can rapidly cause corneal ulceration, perforation, and blindness, and it characteristically produces a hyperacute, profuse purulent discharge","Chlamydial ophthalmia is generally milder with mucopurulent discharge but is the commonest infective cause and can be accompanied by chlamydial pneumonitis","Diagnosis combines Gram stain and culture with nucleic acid amplification testing of conjunctival specimens to distinguish gonococcal, chlamydial, and other causes","Gonococcal disease requires systemic treatment with a third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone plus saline irrigation, while chlamydial disease requires systemic macrolide therapy, as topical treatment alone is inadequate","Because it reflects maternal infection, management must include testing and treatment of the mother and her partners, and antenatal screening is the key preventive measure"],"pathophysiology":"Ophthalmia neonatorum arises when pathogens colonising the maternal genital tract contaminate the neonatal conjunctiva, most often during vaginal delivery, though ascending infection after membrane rupture can also occur. Neisseria gonorrhoeae is especially virulent: it can penetrate intact corneal epithelium, and its intense neutrophilic response with copious purulent exudate can rapidly progress to corneal ulceration and perforation. Chlamydia trachomatis, an obligate intracellular organism, infects conjunctival epithelial cells to produce a more subacute follicular or papillary conjunctivitis and may seed the nasopharynx and lungs, causing an afebrile pneumonitis. Chemical conjunctivitis, by contrast, is a transient self-limiting irritant reaction to topical prophylaxis rather than an infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Gonococcal conjunctivitis","Chlamydial conjunctivitis","Other bacterial conjunctivitis (for example Staphylococcus aureus, streptococci, Gram-negative organisms)","Herpes simplex virus conjunctivitis or keratitis","Chemical conjunctivitis from topical prophylaxis","Congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction with secondary infection (dacryocystitis)","Congenital glaucoma presenting with tearing and a cloudy cornea"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gram stain of conjunctival exudate looking for Gram-negative intracellular diplococci suggesting gonococcus","Bacterial culture of conjunctival swabs, including chocolate agar for Neisseria gonorrhoeae with antimicrobial susceptibility testing","Nucleic acid amplification testing for Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Chlamydial-specific specimen collection sampling conjunctival cells for detection of the intracellular organism","Viral testing for herpes simplex when vesicles, keratitis, or systemic features are present","Maternal and partner screening for sexually transmitted infections to identify the source and guide treatment"],"redFlags":["Hyperacute copious purulent discharge with marked lid oedema suggesting gonococcal infection","Corneal haze, ulceration, or clouding indicating sight-threatening keratitis","Systemic features such as poor feeding, lethargy, or respiratory signs suggesting disseminated gonococcal infection or chlamydial pneumonitis","Vesicular skin or eye lesions or encephalopathic features raising concern for neonatal herpes simplex"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition and appropriate systemic treatment the outlook is excellent, but delayed treatment of gonococcal ophthalmia can lead to corneal perforation and permanent blindness, and untreated chlamydial infection may be complicated by pneumonitis; antenatal screening and neonatal ocular prophylaxis have greatly reduced severe outcomes.","pitfalls":["Attributing a purulent neonatal eye to harmless chemical or blocked-duct causes and missing gonococcal disease","Relying on topical antibiotics alone for gonococcal or chlamydial infection, which require systemic therapy","Failing to test for and treat coexisting infection (for example both gonorrhoea and chlamydia) and to consider chlamydial pneumonitis","Neglecting to investigate and treat the mother and her sexual partners","Overlooking herpes simplex, which needs antiviral rather than antibacterial treatment"],"management":["Treat gonococcal ophthalmia urgently with a systemic third-generation cephalosporin (such as ceftriaxone or cefotaxime), as it is a sight-threatening emergency","Treat chlamydial ophthalmia with a systemic macrolide (oral erythromycin or azithromycin), which also covers concurrent chlamydial pneumonitis","Give systemic aciclovir when herpes simplex is implicated by vesicles or keratitis","Irrigate the eye with saline to clear the copious purulent discharge in gonococcal disease","Examine for and manage corneal involvement to prevent ulceration and perforation, with ophthalmology input","Treat the mother and her sexual partners for the identified sexually transmitted infection and screen accordingly","Reinforce neonatal ocular prophylaxis at birth as prevention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["BASHH UK national guidelines on the management of gonorrhoea and of chlamydial infection","CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines","Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_polycythaemia":{"name":"Neonatal polycythaemia","system":"Neonatal / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neonatal polycythemia","hyperviscosity of the newborn"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neonatology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An abnormally high haematocrit in the newborn, conventionally a venous value above the mid-sixties percent","The clinical concern is hyperviscosity, which impairs microcirculatory flow","Causes include placental transfusion (delayed cord clamping, twin-to-twin transfusion), intrauterine hypoxia and maternal diabetes","Many infants are asymptomatic; symptoms are non-specific and include plethora, lethargy, poor feeding, jitteriness and hypoglycaemia","Capillary samples overestimate haematocrit, so a venous sample is needed to confirm","Partial exchange transfusion is considered for symptomatic infants with significant polycythaemia, alongside hydration and treating hypoglycaemia"],"pathophysiology":"Neonatal polycythaemia results from either increased red cell transfusion to the fetus or newborn (as with delayed cord clamping, cord stripping or twin-to-twin transfusion) or increased fetal red cell production in response to chronic intrauterine hypoxia (as in growth restriction, maternal diabetes or maternal smoking). As the haematocrit rises, blood viscosity increases disproportionately, impairing flow through the microcirculation and reducing oxygen and glucose delivery to tissues. This underlies complications affecting the brain, gut, kidneys and metabolic status, and increases the risk of hypoglycaemia and jaundice.","additionalDifferentials":["Dehydration causing haemoconcentration","Sepsis (as a cause of similar non-specific symptoms)","Congenital heart disease","Hypoglycaemia of other cause","Capillary sampling artefact overestimating haematocrit"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Confirmatory venous haematocrit","Blood glucose (hypoglycaemia is common)","Bilirubin monitoring","Assessment of hydration and perfusion","Evaluation for underlying cause and for complications where symptomatic"],"redFlags":["Neurological signs such as seizures or marked lethargy","Features of necrotising enterocolitis or renal compromise","Respiratory distress suggesting cardiopulmonary involvement","Severe symptomatic hyperviscosity"],"prognosis":"Asymptomatic infants generally do well with hydration and monitoring; symptomatic hyperviscosity, particularly with end-organ effects, carries a less certain neurodevelopmental prognosis.","pitfalls":["Acting on a capillary rather than venous haematocrit","Overlooking hypoglycaemia in a polycythaemic infant","Attributing symptoms to polycythaemia without excluding sepsis","Treating asymptomatic mild polycythaemia unnecessarily"],"management":["Ensure adequate hydration and fluid intake for asymptomatic infants with a modestly raised haematocrit","Perform partial exchange transfusion, replacing blood with crystalloid, for symptomatic infants with significant hyperviscosity","Confirm polycythaemia on a venous haematocrit, as capillary samples overestimate the value","Treat accompanying hypoglycaemia and monitor bilirubin for hyperbilirubinaemia","Observe for end-organ effects on the brain, gut and kidneys, including necrotising enterocolitis once enteral feeding begins","Evaluate and address the underlying cause such as maternal diabetes, intrauterine hypoxia or placental transfusion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neonatology reference texts (standard reference)","Paediatric haematology references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_rds":{"name":"Neonatal Respiratory Distress Syndrome","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hyaline membrane disease","RDS","surfactant deficiency disease","respiratory distress syndrome of the newborn"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disease of surfactant deficiency affecting mainly preterm infants; incidence rises steeply as gestational age falls, being greatest below about 28 weeks.","Distress typically begins at or soon after birth with tachypnoea, grunting, nasal flaring, intercostal and subcostal recession, and cyanosis that worsen over the first hours to days.","Chest radiograph classically shows a diffuse fine reticulogranular (ground-glass) pattern with air bronchograms and low lung volumes.","Antenatal maternal corticosteroids given before anticipated preterm delivery accelerate lung maturation and markedly reduce incidence and severity.","Exogenous surfactant replacement, delivered into the airway, is a cornerstone of treatment and improves oxygenation and survival.","Early nasal continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) helps maintain functional residual capacity and can reduce the need for intubation and mechanical ventilation.","Maternal diabetes, male sex, second-born twins, caesarean delivery without labour, and perinatal asphyxia increase risk, whereas chronic intrauterine stress and prolonged rupture of membranes may reduce it.","Untreated disease tends to worsen for about 2-3 days before recovery begins, often heralded by a spontaneous diuresis."],"pathophysiology":"Immature type II pneumocytes produce insufficient pulmonary surfactant, so alveolar surface tension is high. This causes progressive diffuse atelectasis, reduced lung compliance, and ventilation-perfusion mismatch. Hypoxaemia and hypercapnia with acidosis follow, and pulmonary vascular resistance may remain elevated with right-to-left shunting. Epithelial injury and protein-rich exudate form the hyaline membranes that give the disease its historical name, further impairing gas exchange.","additionalDifferentials":["Transient tachypnoea of the newborn","Neonatal pneumonia or early-onset sepsis (especially group B streptococcus)","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","Congenital heart disease","Air-leak syndromes (pneumothorax)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph to assess the reticulogranular pattern and lung volumes","Arterial or capillary blood gas to quantify hypoxaemia, hypercapnia, and acidosis","Pulse oximetry and continuous cardiorespiratory monitoring","Blood culture and inflammatory markers when sepsis cannot be excluded","Blood glucose and electrolyte monitoring","Echocardiography if congenital heart disease or pulmonary hypertension is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly escalating oxygen or ventilatory requirement","Sudden deterioration suggesting pneumothorax or other air leak","Persistent hypoxaemia despite surfactant and respiratory support (possible pulmonary hypertension)","Apnoea, bradycardia, or signs of impending respiratory failure","Hypotension and poor perfusion suggesting shock or sepsis"],"prognosis":"With antenatal steroids, surfactant, and modern respiratory support, survival is high, particularly in more mature preterm infants; outcome worsens with decreasing gestational age and with complications such as air leak, intraventricular haemorrhage, and progression to bronchopulmonary dysplasia.","pitfalls":["Assuming RDS in a term or near-term infant without considering sepsis, aspiration, or cardiac causes","Over-oxygenation, which increases the risk of retinopathy of prematurity and oxidative lung injury","Delaying surfactant or CPAP in an infant who is deteriorating","Failing to recognise a tension pneumothorax as the cause of abrupt worsening","Not administering antenatal corticosteroids when preterm birth is anticipated"],"management":["Give exogenous surfactant replacement into the airway as a cornerstone of treatment to reduce alveolar surface tension","Support oxygenation with early nasal continuous positive airway pressure, escalating to mechanical ventilation and supplemental oxygen as needed","Promote antenatal maternal corticosteroids before anticipated preterm delivery to accelerate lung maturation and reduce severity","Provide supportive neonatal care with thermoregulation, fluid and nutritional support, and caffeine to aid respiratory drive","Cover for sepsis with empirical antibiotics when infection cannot be excluded, since presentations overlap","Monitor blood gases, oxygen saturations and chest radiographs, watching for air leak, patent ductus arteriosus and progression to bronchopulmonary dysplasia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_seizures":{"name":"Neonatal Seizures","system":"Neurological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["seizures in the newborn","neonatal convulsions","newborn fits"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Seizures in the newborn period are usually a symptom of an acute underlying brain insult rather than a chronic epilepsy, so the priority is to find and treat the cause.","Presentations are often subtle: repetitive eye deviation or blinking, lip-smacking or chewing, cycling or boxing limb movements, and apnoea, in addition to clonic or tonic activity.","Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy is the most common cause; other important causes include stroke, intracranial haemorrhage, infection, metabolic disturbances, and inborn errors of metabolism.","Because clinical signs are unreliable and electroclinical dissociation is common, EEG or amplitude-integrated EEG monitoring is central to accurate diagnosis.","Immediate workup should exclude readily treatable causes such as hypoglycaemia and electrolyte abnormalities, correcting these before escalating anticonvulsant therapy.","Non-epileptic movements like jitteriness can mimic seizures but are stimulus-sensitive, suppressible by gentle restraint, and lack autonomic or ocular features.","Neuroimaging and, where appropriate, lumbar puncture and metabolic or genetic testing help establish aetiology and guide prognosis.","Pyridoxine-dependent and other vitamin-responsive epilepsies should be considered when seizures are refractory to standard agents."],"pathophysiology":"The immature neonatal brain is intrinsically more excitable because excitatory glutamatergic circuits mature earlier than inhibitory GABAergic systems, and GABA can be depolarising early in development. An acute insult, most often hypoxia-ischaemia, disrupts energy metabolism and ion gradients, leading to neuronal depolarisation and synchronised, excessive electrical discharges. Metabolic derangements, structural lesions, infection, or genetic and metabolic disorders lower the seizure threshold further. Frequent dissociation between electrical seizures and visible clinical signs reflects this immaturity and underlies the need for electrographic monitoring.","additionalDifferentials":["Jitteriness or benign neonatal sleep myoclonus","Apnoea of other cause","Hypoglycaemia or electrolyte disturbance","Meningitis or encephalitis","Intracranial haemorrhage or perinatal stroke","Inborn errors of metabolism and vitamin-responsive epilepsies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["EEG or amplitude-integrated EEG monitoring","Blood glucose, electrolytes, calcium, and magnesium","Cranial imaging (ultrasound and MRI)","Lumbar puncture and sepsis evaluation when infection is possible","Metabolic and genetic testing in unexplained or refractory cases"],"redFlags":["Status epilepticus or very frequent recurrent seizures","Seizures refractory to first-line therapy","Encephalopathy with depressed consciousness and abnormal tone","Signs of meningitis or sepsis","Focal neurological deficits suggesting stroke or a structural lesion"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends largely on the underlying cause and the severity of any brain injury; seizures due to transient metabolic disturbances often resolve well, whereas those from severe hypoxic-ischaemic injury, major structural lesions, or certain genetic-metabolic disorders carry a higher risk of epilepsy and neurodevelopmental impairment.","pitfalls":["Relying on clinical observation alone and missing electrographic-only seizures","Mistaking jitteriness or benign myoclonus for seizures and over-treating","Escalating anticonvulsants before correcting hypoglycaemia or electrolyte abnormalities","Failing to consider vitamin-responsive epilepsies in seizures resistant to standard drugs"],"management":["Identify and correct readily treatable causes first, giving dextrose for hypoglycaemia and correcting calcium, magnesium and electrolyte disturbances","Treat electrographic and clinical seizures with anticonvulsants, using phenobarbital first-line and adding phenytoin, levetiracetam or a benzodiazepine","Offer therapeutic hypothermia for hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy, the commonest cause","Cover suspected infection with antibiotics and aciclovir pending cerebrospinal fluid and culture results","Trial pyridoxine in refractory seizures to detect vitamin-responsive causes","Use EEG or amplitude-integrated EEG monitoring to guide diagnosis and treatment given frequent electroclinical dissociation","Investigate unexplained or refractory cases for inborn errors of metabolism and genetic causes, with neurology referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_sepsis":{"name":"Neonatal sepsis","system":"Neonatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sepsis neonatorum","newborn sepsis","early-onset neonatal sepsis","late-onset neonatal sepsis","EOS","LOS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A systemic bacterial (occasionally fungal or viral) infection in the first 28 days of life, conventionally split into early-onset (within roughly the first 72 hours, often maternally acquired) and late-onset (after 72 hours, frequently nosocomial or community-acquired).","Signs are notoriously nonspecific: temperature instability (fever or hypothermia), poor feeding, lethargy or irritability, respiratory distress, apnoea, tachycardia, and abdominal distension.","Group B Streptococcus and Escherichia coli dominate early-onset disease; coagulase-negative staphylococci, Staphylococcus aureus, and Gram-negative organisms are common in late-onset disease, especially in preterm infants with indwelling lines.","Maternal risk factors include prolonged rupture of membranes, chorioamnionitis, maternal fever, and GBS colonisation without adequate intrapartum prophylaxis.","Blood culture is the diagnostic gold standard; a lumbar puncture should be considered because meningitis frequently coexists and may be missed.","Empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics must be started promptly after cultures because deterioration can be rapid; the regimen is later narrowed to culture results.","Supportive care (thermoregulation, respiratory and haemodynamic support, glucose management) is as important as antimicrobials.","A low threshold for treatment is appropriate given nonspecific signs, but antibiotics are stopped early when cultures remain negative and the infant is well."],"pathophysiology":"Neonates are relatively immunocompromised, with immature innate and adaptive defences, low immunoglobulin levels, and impaired neutrophil function. Organisms reach the infant by ascending or intrapartum transmission from the maternal genital tract in early-onset disease, or from the postnatal environment and invasive devices in late-onset disease. Once bloodstream invasion occurs, bacterial products trigger a systemic inflammatory response with cytokine release, endothelial injury, capillary leak, and impaired perfusion that can progress to septic shock, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and multi-organ dysfunction. The immature blood-brain barrier and limited containment of infection predispose to meningitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Transient tachypnoea of the newborn","Respiratory distress syndrome","Congenital heart disease with poor perfusion","Inborn errors of metabolism","Neonatal encephalopathy / birth asphyxia","Congenital viral infection (e.g. herpes simplex, cytomegalovirus)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood culture before starting antibiotics","Complete blood count with differential and immature-to-total neutrophil ratio","C-reactive protein and/or procalcitonin, ideally serially","Lumbar puncture with CSF cell count, protein, glucose and culture","Urine culture (more relevant in late-onset disease)","Chest radiograph if respiratory signs are present","Blood glucose, electrolytes and blood gas to assess metabolic status"],"redFlags":["Septic shock: poor perfusion, hypotension, prolonged capillary refill","Apnoea or worsening respiratory failure requiring support","Bulging fontanelle, seizures, or other signs suggesting meningitis","Petechiae, purpura, or bleeding suggesting disseminated intravascular coagulation","Persistent hypoglycaemia or profound metabolic acidosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on gestational age, organism, and speed of treatment; prompt therapy usually leads to recovery, but meningitis, shock, and prematurity carry higher mortality and risk of neurodevelopmental sequelae.","pitfalls":["Attributing nonspecific signs to prematurity or feeding issues and delaying evaluation","Omitting a lumbar puncture and missing coexisting meningitis","Relying on a single normal inflammatory marker to exclude sepsis early in the illness","Failing to remove or consider infected central venous catheters in late-onset disease"],"management":["Start prompt empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics after obtaining cultures, typically a penicillin (benzylpenicillin or ampicillin) plus an aminoglycoside for early-onset disease, because deterioration can be rapid","Tailor late-onset cover to likely nosocomial organisms and local patterns, adding anti-staphylococcal or glycopeptide cover, and broaden to a cephalosporin when meningitis is suspected","Narrow the regimen to culture and sensitivity results and add antifungal therapy if invasive fungal infection is identified","Provide supportive intensive care with fluid resuscitation, inotropes for septic shock, respiratory support and correction of disseminated intravascular coagulation","Perform lumbar puncture and treat coexisting meningitis with an appropriate agent and duration","Monitor blood cultures, inflammatory markers and clinical response to guide duration and de-escalation","Reinforce prevention through intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis for maternal group B streptococcus colonisation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neoplasms_of_the_lung":{"name":"Neoplasms of the Lung","system":"Respiratory / Thoracic Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lung cancer","Bronchogenic carcinoma","Non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC)","Small-cell lung cancer (SCLC)","Pulmonary carcinoma"],"chapter":83,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.614","clinicalPearls":["Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer death worldwide and in the United States; over half of patients present with locally advanced or metastatic disease at diagnosis.","Tobacco smoking accounts for roughly two-thirds of lung cancer deaths globally, but about 65% of new U.S. diagnoses are now in former smokers or never-smokers; one in five women and one in twelve men diagnosed have never smoked.","The WHO divides epithelial lung cancers into four major types: small-cell carcinoma (SCLC) and three non-small-cell types (adenocarcinoma, squamous cell, large-cell). Together these account for about 90% of epithelial lung cancers; adenocarcinoma is now the most common subtype.","Separating SCLC from NSCLC and, within NSCLC, squamous from nonsquamous histology is essential because natural history and management differ markedly; a generic diagnosis of 'non-small-cell carcinoma, not otherwise specified' is no longer acceptable.","A chronic cough with or without hemoptysis in a current or former smoker with COPD aged 40 or older warrants thorough evaluation for lung cancer even with a normal chest x-ray; a persistent pneumonia unresponsive to repeated antibiotics also requires workup.","Tissue sampling is required to confirm diagnosis in all suspected cases; a core biopsy is preferred over fine-needle aspiration to provide adequate material for molecular and PD-L1 testing.","Central, endobronchial lesions (squamous cell, small-cell, carcinoid) are more readily reached by bronchoscopy, whereas peripheral lesions (adenocarcinoma, large-cell) are more amenable to transthoracic biopsy.","Driver oncogenes (EGFR, ALK, ROS1, RET, NTRK, BRAF, MET, HER2, KRAS) are enriched in nonsquamous NSCLC and define distinct molecular cohorts; nearly 90% of SCLCs carry TP53 and RB1 mutations.","Paraneoplastic syndromes are common, especially with SCLC, and may be the presenting feature or first sign of recurrence; they can mimic metastatic disease and lead to inappropriately palliative rather than curative management.","Hypercalcemia from ectopic PTH-related peptide is the most common life-threatening metabolic complication of malignancy and occurs primarily with squamous cell carcinoma.","On FDG PET, a standardized uptake value (SUV) above 2.5 is highly suspicious for malignancy, but PET must never be used alone to diagnose cancer, nodal involvement, or metastasis because of false positives (infection, granulomatous disease such as tuberculosis) and false negatives (lesions under 8 mm, slow-growing tumors, diabetes).","Adenocarcinoma in situ and minimally invasive adenocarcinoma (solitary lesions 3 cm or less with pure or predominant lepidic growth and 5 mm or less invasion) achieve near 100% 5-year disease-free survival after complete resection."],"pathophysiology":"Inhaled environmental carcinogens, principally those in tobacco smoke, drive transformation of bronchoepithelial cells to a malignant phenotype. Susceptibility is modulated by polymorphisms in carcinogen-metabolizing genes (e.g., CYP1A1) and by genetic loci affecting nicotinic acetylcholine receptors and telomerase (5p15, 6p21, 15q25). Tumorigenesis reflects accumulation of gain-of-function mutations in oncogenes and loss-of-function mutations in tumor-suppressor genes, with the sequence differing by histologic type. Adenocarcinomas frequently harbor targetable driver alterations: activating receptor tyrosine kinase mutations (EGFR, HER2/ERBB2, MET), intracellular signaling mutations (KRAS, BRAF), and chromosomal rearrangements activating ALK, ROS1, RET, and NTRK. Squamous tumors may show FGFR1 amplification, DDR2 mutations, PIK3CA mutations/PTEN loss, and BRAF/MET alterations. Inactivated tumor suppressors include TP53, RB1, RASSF1A, CDKN2A/B, LKB1 (STK11), and FHIT; allelic loss on chromosome 3p occurs very early, even in histologically normal smoke-damaged epithelium. Clinical manifestations arise from central/endobronchial growth (cough, hemoptysis, wheeze, postobstructive pneumonia), peripheral growth (pleural/chest-wall pain, dyspnea, cavitation), regional intrathoracic spread (recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy with hoarseness, phrenic nerve palsy, Horner syndrome, Pancoast syndrome, superior vena cava syndrome, pericardial/lymphatic involvement), distant metastasis, or paraneoplastic mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Persistent or recurrent pneumonia unresponsive to antibiotics","Metastatic carcinoma to the lung from an extrapulmonary primary","Malignant pleural mesothelioma (CK5/6, calretinin, WT-1 positive, distinguishing it from adenocarcinoma)","Pulmonary carcinoid tumor (neuroendocrine, often central/endobronchial)","Benign solitary pulmonary nodule (granuloma, hamartoma)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis and other granulomatous disease (false-positive FDG-PET uptake; can mimic malignant nodules)","Postobstructive pneumonia from an underlying endobronchial tumor","Lung abscess from tumor cavitation","Parapneumonic pleural effusion mimicking malignant effusion"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis and other granulomatous disorders","Benign pulmonary nodules (hamartoma, hamartochondroma)","Pulmonary metastases from non-lung primaries","Mesothelioma","Paraneoplastic neurologic syndromes mimicking primary neurologic disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest CT and FDG PET, preferably integrated PET-CT, for anatomic staging and detection of mediastinal nodal and extrathoracic metastases","Tissue biopsy: fiberoptic bronchoscopy with bronchial/transbronchial biopsy, image-guided percutaneous core biopsy, or EBUS-guided biopsy; core biopsy preferred for adequate molecular material","Mediastinal nodal assessment by EBUS- or EUS-guided biopsy, mediastinoscopy, mediastinotomy, thoracoscopy, or thoracotomy","Next-generation sequencing (multigene panel) for driver oncogenes and PD-L1 immunohistochemistry reported as tumor proportion score (TPS)","Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) 'liquid biopsy' when tissue is limited or inaccessible","MRI brain for suspected brain metastases (most effective method) and for superior sulcus tumors to assess brachial plexus involvement","Immunohistochemistry: TTF-1 and napsin-A for adenocarcinoma (TTF-1 positive in over 70%, napsin-A in over 90% of primary lung adenocarcinomas), p40/p63 for squamous; neuroendocrine markers (CD56/NCAM, synaptophysin, chromogranin, INSM1) for SCLC","Thoracentesis with cell block for suspected malignant pleural effusion (repeat if first sample negative)","Spinal CT/MRI for suspected cord compression; brain/spine MRI plus lumbar puncture for suspected leptomeningeal disease","Physiologic evaluation: spirometry (FEV1), DLCO, and cardiopulmonary exercise testing (VO2max) before surgical resection"],"redFlags":["Hemoptysis in a smoker over 40 with chronic cough","Hoarseness (recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement) or superior vena cava syndrome","Pancoast/superior sulcus presentation: shoulder pain radiating in ulnar distribution with destruction of first/second ribs, often with Horner syndrome","Signs of cord compression: pain, weakness, paralysis, urinary retention","Weight loss exceeding 10% of body weight (poor prognostic sign)","Hypercalcemia with nausea, vomiting, constipation, polyuria, altered mental status","Symptomatic brain metastases (headache, nausea/vomiting, seizures, focal deficits) or pathologic fractures/bone pain"],"prognosis":"Outcome is strongly stage-dependent. For NSCLC by the eighth-edition TNM system, 5-year overall survival ranges from roughly 90% for the earliest (stage IA1) disease down to about 10% for stage IVB; SEER data report a 5-year survival of about 63% for localized NSCLC. Lepidic-predominant adenocarcinoma has a favorable prognosis, acinar and papillary intermediate, and solid-predominant and micropapillary patterns poor. SCLC is more aggressive: 60-70% present with extensive-stage disease, and even in limited-stage SCLC median survival is roughly 12-20 months historically. Weight loss over 10% of body weight and thrombotic complications of malignancy are poor prognostic signs. Extrathoracic metastases are found at autopsy in over 50% of squamous, about 80% of adenocarcinoma/large-cell, and over 95% of SCLC cases.","pitfalls":["A normal chest x-ray does not exclude lung cancer in a high-risk symptomatic smoker.","Negative TTF-1 does not exclude a pulmonary primary; TTF-1 is also positive in neuroendocrine and thyroid tumors.","PET-CT cannot establish a tissue diagnosis: false positives occur with tuberculosis/granulomatous and infectious disease and false negatives with sub-8 mm, slow-growing, or carcinoid/well-differentiated tumors.","Paraneoplastic syndromes may be mistaken for metastatic disease, risking inappropriate palliative rather than curative treatment.","A single negative thoracentesis does not exclude a malignant effusion; repeat sampling is essential.","Diagnosing on small biopsy/cytology specimens may blur squamous-versus-nonsquamous distinction; repeat biopsy is advised in advanced disease for adequate molecular and PD-L1 testing.","Low-dose CT screening carries a high false-positive rate (false-positive rate about 96% among positive screens in NLST), with attendant risks of overdiagnosis, unnecessary procedures, and radiation exposure."],"management":["Base treatment on histology and stage, distinguishing small-cell from non-small-cell carcinoma and squamous from nonsquamous disease","Offer surgical resection (lobectomy with nodal sampling), often with adjuvant chemotherapy, for early-stage non-small-cell lung cancer","Direct molecularly targeted agents against driver alterations in advanced adenocarcinoma (EGFR, ALK, ROS1, RET, BRAF, MET or NTRK inhibitors) guided by next-generation sequencing","Use immune checkpoint inhibitors (PD-1/PD-L1 blockade), alone or with platinum-based chemotherapy, guided by the PD-L1 tumour proportion score","Treat small-cell and unresectable locally advanced disease with platinum-based chemotherapy and radiotherapy or concurrent chemoradiation, considering prophylactic cranial irradiation in small-cell disease","Provide early palliative care and symptom control, and support smoking cessation","Manage all cases through a thoracic oncology multidisciplinary team with surveillance imaging for recurrence"],"severityClassification":"NSCLC is staged with the eighth-edition (2018) TNM system: T1 (3 cm or less; subdivided T1a 1 cm or less, T1b over 1 to 2 cm, T1c over 2 to 3 cm), T2 (over 3 to 5 cm or visceral pleura/main bronchus involvement/atelectasis; T2a over 3 to 4 cm, T2b over 4 to 5 cm), T3 (over 5 to 7 cm or invasion of chest wall/pericardium/phrenic nerve or satellite nodule in same lobe), T4 (over 7 cm or invasion of mediastinum, heart, great vessels, trachea, recurrent laryngeal nerve, esophagus, vertebra, carina, or separate nodule in a different ipsilateral lobe). Nodes: N0 none, N1 ipsilateral peribronchial/hilar/intrapulmonary, N2 ipsilateral mediastinal/subcarinal, N3 contralateral mediastinal/hilar or scalene/supraclavicular. Metastasis: M1a contralateral nodule or pleural/pericardial nodule/malignant effusion, M1b single extrathoracic metastasis, M1c multiple extrathoracic metastases. SCLC is commonly classified by the two-stage Veterans Administration system: limited-stage (confined to ipsilateral hemithorax within a tolerable radiation port) versus extensive-stage (overt metastatic disease). Physiologic operability thresholds: FEV1 over 2 L or over 80% predicted suggests tolerance of pneumonectomy; FEV1 over 1.5 L suggests adequate reserve for lobectomy; VO2max under 15 mL/kg/min predicts higher postoperative risk; contraindications to thoracic surgery include FEV1 under 1 L, resting PCO2 over 45 mmHg, DLCO under 40%, severe pulmonary hypertension, and recent myocardial infarction.","references":["Harrison 22e p.614","Harrison 22e p.615","Harrison 22e p.616","Harrison 22e p.617","Harrison 22e p.618","Harrison 22e p.619","Harrison 22e p.620","Harrison 22e p.621","Harrison 22e p.622","Harrison 22e p.623"],"crossLinks":[]},"neovascular_glaucoma":{"name":"Neovascular glaucoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NVG","rubeotic glaucoma","haemorrhagic glaucoma","thrombotic glaucoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe secondary glaucoma driven by new vessel growth on the iris (rubeosis iridis) and in the drainage angle in response to retinal ischaemia","The commonest underlying causes are ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion, proliferative diabetic retinopathy, and ocular ischaemic syndrome from carotid disease","Early rubeosis begins as fine vessels at the pupillary margin and angle before spreading; careful undilated gonioscopy and iris examination detect it before pressure rises","A fibrovascular membrane initially blocks the open angle, then contracts to pull the peripheral iris forward, producing progressive synechial (secondary) angle closure","Late presentation is with a painful red eye, very high intraocular pressure, corneal oedema, and poor vision, often with ectropion uveae from membrane contraction","Management targets the ischaemic drive with panretinal photocoagulation and anti-VEGF injection, alongside topical pressure-lowering agents and control of pain and inflammation","Once the angle is extensively closed, drainage surgery with a tube or trabeculectomy, or a cyclodestructive procedure, is usually needed to control pressure","Identifying and treating the underlying retinal or carotid ischaemia is essential and may require systemic and vascular assessment"],"pathophysiology":"Neovascular glaucoma results from chronic retinal ischaemia, which triggers release of vascular endothelial growth factor and other angiogenic mediators into the aqueous. These stimulate growth of a fibrovascular membrane over the iris surface and across the trabecular meshwork. In the early open-angle phase this membrane physically covers and obstructs the meshwork, raising pressure while the angle still appears open on gonioscopy. As the membrane matures its myofibroblasts contract, dragging the peripheral iris anteriorly to form peripheral anterior synechiae and everting the pupil margin (ectropion uveae), which produces progressive synechial angle closure. The combination of outflow obstruction and secondary angle closure causes marked pressure elevation, corneal oedema, pain, and optic-nerve damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary acute angle-closure glaucoma","Inflammatory (uveitic) glaucoma with anterior segment vessels","Chronic angle-closure glaucoma from other causes","Fuchs heterochromic uveitis with fine iris vessels","Intraocular tumour causing secondary glaucoma","Post-traumatic or ghost cell glaucoma with a red painful eye"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Undilated slit-lamp examination and gonioscopy to detect early rubeosis and angle new vessels","Applanation tonometry to gauge the pressure rise","Dilated fundus examination and retinal imaging or fluorescein angiography to identify the ischaemic driver","Carotid Doppler or vascular imaging when ocular ischaemic syndrome is suspected","Assessment and control of systemic diabetes and cardiovascular risk factors","B-scan ultrasonography when media opacity obscures the retina"],"redFlags":["New iris or angle vessels signalling active retinal ischaemia that demands urgent treatment before the angle closes","Painful red eye with very high pressure and corneal oedema requiring emergency care","An ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion or proliferative diabetic retinopathy known to precede rubeosis","Ocular ischaemic syndrome pointing to significant carotid disease and systemic vascular risk"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on how early the neovascular drive is treated; caught at the rubeosis stage, panretinal photocoagulation and anti-VEGF therapy may regress vessels and preserve the angle, but established synechial closure often leaves a difficult-to-control eye with poor visual potential, and some eyes become painful and blind despite treatment.","pitfalls":["Dilating the pupil before examining for early rubeosis and missing angle new vessels","Treating the pressure alone without addressing the underlying retinal or carotid ischaemia","Relying on anti-VEGF injection, whose effect is temporary, without definitive panretinal photocoagulation","Delaying treatment until the angle has already closed synechially","Overlooking ocular ischaemic syndrome and an underlying carotid stenosis"],"management":["Ablate the ischaemic retinal drive with panretinal photocoagulation and intravitreal anti-VEGF injection to regress the fibrovascular membrane","Lower intraocular pressure with topical agents such as beta-blockers, alpha-agonists and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, adding systemic carbonic anhydrase inhibitors or hyperosmotics for acute rises","Relieve pain and inflammation with topical corticosteroid and cycloplegic drops","Proceed to glaucoma drainage (tube or valve) surgery or a cyclodestructive procedure for pressure uncontrolled medically once synechial angle closure is established","Identify and treat the underlying cause, whether ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion, proliferative diabetic retinopathy or carotid ocular ischaemic syndrome, including carotid intervention where indicated","Detect early rubeosis with undilated gonioscopy and iris examination and treat before pressure rises to preserve the angle","Monitor intraocular pressure and, in a blind painful eye, consider palliative measures for comfort"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"nephrocalcinosis":{"name":"Nephrocalcinosis","system":"Renal / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["renal calcinosis","medullary nephrocalcinosis","cortical nephrocalcinosis","renal parenchymal calcification"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Nephrocalcinosis is generalised deposition of calcium salts within the renal parenchyma, distinct from discrete calculi in the collecting system","The medullary pattern is far more common and usually reflects a disorder of calcium handling","The classic medullary causes are hyperparathyroidism, distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis and medullary sponge kidney","Further medullary causes include hypervitaminosis D, sarcoidosis, milk-alkali syndrome and primary hyperoxaluria","Cortical nephrocalcinosis is rarer and follows acute cortical necrosis, chronic glomerulonephritis or severe pyelonephritis","It is often found incidentally on imaging; ultrasound shows echogenic medullary pyramids in the medullary pattern","Management is directed at the underlying metabolic cause rather than at the calcification itself","Distal renal tubular acidosis is suggested when nephrocalcinosis accompanies a hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis with an inappropriately alkaline urine, and is treated with alkali replacement"],"pathophysiology":"Nephrocalcinosis denotes deposition of calcium, as oxalate or phosphate, diffusely through the renal tissue, most often in the medulla. Medullary deposition arises when there is an increased filtered calcium load, impaired urinary acidification or reduced inhibitor activity, as in hyperparathyroidism, distal renal tubular acidosis (where alkaline urine and hypocitraturia favour calcium phosphate precipitation), medullary sponge kidney with stasis in dilated collecting ducts, and states of hypercalcaemia or hyperoxaluria. Cortical nephrocalcinosis instead follows destructive processes such as acute cortical necrosis or chronic infection, where calcium is laid down in devitalised tissue as dystrophic calcification.","additionalDifferentials":["Medullary sponge kidney","Distal renal tubular acidosis","Primary hyperparathyroidism and other hypercalcaemic states","Primary or enteric hyperoxaluria","Sarcoidosis and vitamin D excess","Acute cortical necrosis or chronic pyelonephritis for the cortical pattern","Papillary calcification of medullary origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasound, supplemented by CT, to confirm and characterise a medullary versus cortical pattern","Serum calcium, phosphate and parathyroid hormone","Serum and urine pH, bicarbonate and electrolytes to assess for distal renal tubular acidosis","24-hour urine calcium, oxalate and citrate","Serum vitamin D and, where indicated, angiotensin-converting enzyme for sarcoidosis","Urinalysis and renal function tests"],"redFlags":["Progressive chronic kidney disease from an untreated underlying disorder","Severe hypercalcaemia with acute kidney injury","Nephrocalcinosis in a child suggesting primary hyperoxaluria or an inherited tubulopathy","Superimposed stones causing obstruction"],"prognosis":"The renal outlook depends on the underlying cause and how early it is treated; some tubulopathies stabilise with alkali therapy, whereas primary hyperoxaluria and progressive hypercalcaemic disorders can lead to established renal failure.","pitfalls":["Confusing nephrocalcinosis, which is parenchymal, with nephrolithiasis in the collecting system","Failing to search for a treatable metabolic cause such as distal renal tubular acidosis or hyperparathyroidism","Missing primary hyperoxaluria in a child, in whom it is progressive and serious","Attributing renal impairment to the calcification rather than the underlying disease"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying disorder of calcium handling rather than the calcification itself","Give alkali therapy (potassium citrate or bicarbonate) to correct distal renal tubular acidosis and raise urinary citrate","Manage primary hyperoxaluria with high fluid intake, citrate, pyridoxine in responsive type 1 disease and oxalate reduction, referring for consideration of liver and kidney transplantation in advanced cases","Treat hypercalcaemic drivers with parathyroidectomy for hyperparathyroidism, withdrawal of excess vitamin D, and corticosteroids for sarcoidosis","Maintain generous fluid intake and appropriate dietary measures to reduce urinary crystallisation","Monitor renal function and serial imaging alongside serum and urine calcium, oxalate and citrate","Refer for genetic testing and counselling where a hereditary tubulopathy or primary hyperoxaluria is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (Feehally et al.)","Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"nephrogenic_diabetes_insipidus":{"name":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NDI","nephrogenic arginine vasopressin resistance","vasopressin-resistant diabetes insipidus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disorder in which the kidney cannot concentrate urine because the collecting duct fails to respond to vasopressin, causing polyuria, polydipsia, and dilute urine","The hereditary X-linked form results from AVPR2 mutations affecting the vasopressin V2 receptor; rarer autosomal forms arise from AQP2 mutations in the aquaporin-2 water channel","Acquired causes are far more common and include lithium therapy, chronic hypercalcaemia, hypokalaemia, and urinary tract obstruction","Distinguished from central diabetes insipidus by the lack of urine concentration after administration of desmopressin, whereas central disease responds","The water deprivation test with subsequent desmopressin helps differentiate central, nephrogenic, and primary polydipsic causes","Patients are at risk of hypernatraemic dehydration if access to water is limited, especially infants and those who cannot signal thirst","Management emphasises adequate water intake, removing the offending cause where possible, a low-solute diet, and adjuncts such as thiazide diuretics to reduce urine volume","Lithium-associated NDI may persist even after the drug is stopped"],"pathophysiology":"In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus the collecting duct is resistant to the antidiuretic action of vasopressin. Normally vasopressin binds the V2 receptor (AVPR2), triggering insertion of aquaporin-2 (AQP2) water channels into the apical membrane to allow water reabsorption. Inherited loss-of-function mutations in AVPR2 (X-linked) or AQP2 (autosomal) block this pathway, while acquired insults such as lithium, hypercalcaemia, hypokalaemia, and obstruction interfere with aquaporin-2 expression or trafficking. The kidney therefore excretes large volumes of inappropriately dilute urine, and without sufficient water intake serum sodium and osmolality rise.","additionalDifferentials":["Central (neurogenic) diabetes insipidus","Primary (psychogenic) polydipsia","Osmotic diuresis (e.g. uncontrolled diabetes mellitus)","Solute diuresis from high protein or salt load","Chronic kidney disease with impaired concentrating ability"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Paired serum and urine osmolality with serum sodium","Water deprivation test followed by desmopressin challenge","Serum calcium, potassium, and glucose to identify reversible causes","Review of medications, particularly lithium","Genetic testing for AVPR2 or AQP2 mutations in suspected hereditary disease"],"redFlags":["Hypernatraemia with signs of severe dehydration","Altered consciousness or seizures from hyperosmolar state","Inability to access or sense the need for water (infants, elderly, impaired cognition)","Rapidly rising serum sodium"],"prognosis":"With reliable water access and treatment of reversible causes most patients maintain fluid balance, though hereditary disease is lifelong and repeated hypernatraemic episodes in childhood can affect neurodevelopment.","pitfalls":["Giving desmopressin expecting a response and missing the diagnosis of a nephrogenic (rather than central) cause","Overlooking lithium, hypercalcaemia, or hypokalaemia as reversible triggers","Restricting fluids and precipitating dangerous hypernatraemia","Assuming lithium-induced disease always resolves after the drug is withdrawn"],"management":["Ensure reliable access to free water to match urinary losses and prevent hypernatraemic dehydration","Identify and reverse acquired causes, withdrawing lithium, correcting hypercalcaemia and hypokalaemia, and relieving urinary obstruction","Reduce urine output with a low-solute (sodium- and protein-restricted) diet combined with a thiazide diuretic","Add a potassium-sparing diuretic such as amiloride, particularly in lithium-induced disease, or an NSAID such as indometacin to further curtail polyuria","Monitor serum sodium and osmolality, and growth in children","Treat acute hypernatraemic dehydration with careful rehydration, avoiding overly rapid correction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"nephrogenic_diabetes_insipidus_hereditary":{"name":"Hereditary nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (AVPR2/AQP2)","system":"Renal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["congenital nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","AVPR2","AQP2","vasopressin-resistant diabetes insipidus","arginine vasopressin resistance"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Oxford Textbook of Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hereditary nephrogenic diabetes insipidus reflects collecting-duct resistance to vasopressin, so the kidney cannot concentrate urine despite adequate or high antidiuretic hormone","The majority of inherited cases are X-linked from AVPR2 mutations affecting the V2 receptor, so affected infants are typically boys","A minority are autosomal (recessive or dominant) from AQP2 water-channel mutations and affect both sexes","Infants present with polyuria, poor feeding, unexplained fevers, irritability, vomiting and hypernatraemic dehydration","The biochemical picture is dilute urine with a rising serum sodium and osmolality when free water is not replaced","A water-deprivation test with a vasopressin analogue distinguishes the nephrogenic form (no urinary concentration after the analogue) from central diabetes insipidus","Management centres on unrestricted access to water, a low-solute diet, and measures such as thiazide diuretics with dietary sodium restriction to reduce urine volume rather than vasopressin replacement"],"pathophysiology":"In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus the principal cells of the collecting duct fail to respond to vasopressin. X-linked disease results from loss-of-function mutations in AVPR2, the gene for the V2 vasopressin receptor, so hormone binding cannot trigger the intracellular signalling that normally inserts water channels into the apical membrane. Autosomal forms result from mutations in AQP2, the aquaporin-2 water channel itself, impairing its trafficking or function. Either defect prevents water reabsorption, producing large volumes of inappropriately dilute urine, a tendency to hypernatraemia and dehydration, and compensatory polydipsia.","additionalDifferentials":["Central (neurogenic) diabetes insipidus","Primary polydipsia","Acquired nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from lithium or hypercalcaemia","Osmotic diuresis from uncontrolled diabetes mellitus","Chronic hypokalaemia impairing urinary concentration","Post-obstructive or tubulointerstitial disease causing a concentrating defect"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Paired serum and urine osmolality with serum sodium","Water-deprivation test followed by a vasopressin analogue to localise the defect","Copeptin or vasopressin measurement where available to support classification","Exclusion of hypercalcaemia, hypokalaemia and drug (lithium) causes","Renal ultrasound for hydronephrosis or a distended bladder from chronic high urine output","Genetic testing of AVPR2 and AQP2 with carrier assessment in relatives"],"redFlags":["Hypernatraemic dehydration in an infant with lethargy or seizures","Recurrent unexplained fevers and failure to thrive in a young child","Obstructive uropathy or hydronephrosis from chronic high-volume output","Rapid correction of chronic hypernatraemia risking cerebral oedema"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis, reliable access to water and volume-reducing measures, growth and development are usually preserved, but repeated episodes of severe hypernatraemic dehydration in infancy can cause lasting neurological injury.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the disorder for central diabetes insipidus and expecting a response to vasopressin replacement","Restricting fluids in an infant who depends on free access to water","Correcting chronic hypernatraemia too quickly and precipitating cerebral oedema","Overlooking a treatable acquired cause such as lithium or hypercalcaemia before labelling disease hereditary"],"management":["Guarantee unrestricted access to water, feeding infants frequently, as they cannot signal thirst and are prone to hypernatraemic dehydration","Reduce urine volume with a low-solute (low-sodium, low-protein) diet and a thiazide diuretic","Add amiloride or an NSAID such as indometacin to augment the reduction in polyuria","Monitor serum sodium and osmolality and follow growth and neurodevelopment closely through childhood","Treat hypernatraemic episodes with cautious rehydration to avoid rapid osmotic shifts","Confirm the diagnosis with AVPR2 and AQP2 genetic testing and provide counselling and carrier assessment for relatives given the X-linked and rarer autosomal inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Oxford Textbook of Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"nephrogenic_systemic_fibrosis":{"name":"nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","system":"Skin / Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nephrogenic fibrosing dermopathy","NSF"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Bolognia Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acquired fibrosing disorder of skin and internal organs occurring almost exclusively in patients with severe renal impairment exposed to gadolinium-based contrast agents","Presents with symmetrical, progressive woody induration of the skin, most often on the lower limbs and forearms, with brawny hyperpigmentation and a cobblestoned, peau d'orange surface","Fibrosis leads to painful joint contractures and marked loss of mobility, and yellow scleral plaques may be seen in younger patients","Characteristically the face is spared, an important clue distinguishing it from scleroderma and scleromyxoedema","Risk is greatest with high-risk (linear, less stable) gadolinium agents, higher doses and repeated exposures in patients with an eGFR below about thirty or on dialysis, including those with acute kidney injury","Diagnosis combines the clinical picture with a deep skin biopsy showing spindle-cell proliferation with CD34-positive fibrocytes and thickened collagen, correlated with clinicoradiological criteria","Prevention is paramount: avoid or minimise high-risk contrast agents in advanced renal disease and prefer more stable macrocyclic agents; established disease is difficult to treat and may improve if renal function is restored"],"pathophysiology":"Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis is triggered by exposure to gadolinium-based contrast agents in the setting of markedly reduced renal clearance, which prolongs circulation of gadolinium and permits dissociation of the metal from its chelate (transmetallation), particularly with less stable linear agents. Retained gadolinium deposits in tissues and, together with uraemia and possibly other cofactors, is thought to recruit circulating CD34-positive, procollagen-positive bone-marrow-derived fibrocytes into the skin and other organs. These cells, driven by profibrotic cytokines such as transforming growth factor-beta, proliferate and deposit excess collagen and mucin, producing progressive dermal and systemic fibrosis that can involve muscle, lungs, heart and diaphragm.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic sclerosis and morphoea","Scleromyxoedema","Scleroedema","Eosinophilic fasciitis","Chronic graft-versus-host disease with sclerodermatous features","Lipodermatosclerosis of chronic venous disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Deep incisional (including fascia) skin biopsy showing fibrocyte proliferation, thickened collagen bundles and mucin","Renal function including eGFR to confirm and stage kidney impairment","Review of gadolinium contrast exposure history, agent type and dose","Immunohistochemistry for CD34 highlighting spindle-cell fibrocytes","Autoimmune serology to help exclude systemic sclerosis and related disorders","Serum protein electrophoresis to exclude a paraproteinaemia mimicking scleromyxoedema"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive skin thickening with developing joint contractures","Systemic fibrosis causing restrictive lung disease or cardiac involvement","Diaphragmatic involvement leading to respiratory compromise","Profound loss of mobility with dependency and risk of complications of immobility"],"prognosis":"The course is typically chronic and progressive with substantial disability from contractures and immobility, and systemic fibrosis contributes to mortality in severe cases. Some patients stabilise or improve when renal function recovers, for example after successful transplantation, but established fibrosis often persists.","pitfalls":["Administering high-risk gadolinium agents to patients with advanced renal disease without recognising the risk","Mistaking the condition for systemic sclerosis and overlooking the characteristic sparing of the face","Taking too superficial a biopsy and missing the diagnostic deep dermal and fascial fibrosis","Failing to obtain a contrast exposure history in a dialysis patient with new skin induration","Assuming a single, low-dose exposure carries no risk in severe renal impairment"],"management":["Prevention is the mainstay: avoid gadolinium-based contrast in severe renal impairment, and where imaging is essential use the lowest dose of a stable macrocyclic agent or an alternative modality","Optimise renal clearance after unavoidable exposure, including prompt haemodialysis in dialysis-dependent patients, and pursue restoration of renal function, as recovery or successful kidney transplantation may arrest or improve the fibrosis","Preserve mobility with intensive physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain range of movement and prevent joint contractures","Symptomatic care with analgesia for pain and skin-directed measures such as emollients for the indurated skin","Trial of reported disease-modifying options such as extracorporeal photopheresis or other immunomodulatory approaches in progressive disease, recognising the evidence is limited","Surveillance for systemic fibrosis involving muscle, lungs, heart and diaphragm that may compromise function","Deliver coordinated dermatology and nephrology multidisciplinary care with early specialist referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia, Dermatology"],"crossLinks":["scleromyxoedema","scleroedema"]},"nephrolithiasis":{"name":"Nephrolithiasis","system":"Renal/Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Kidney stone disease","Renal calculi","Urolithiasis","Renal stones"],"chapter":330,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2448","clinicalPearls":["Calcium oxalate stones are by far the most common type (~75%), followed by calcium phosphate (~15%), uric acid (~8%), struvite (~1%), and cystine (<1%); many stones are mixtures and contain protein matrix (Harrison 22e p.2448).","Identifying the stone type is clinically important because it informs prognosis and the choice of preventive strategy; patients should be encouraged to retrieve and submit passed stones for composition analysis (Harrison 22e p.2451-2452).","The two classic acute presentations are renal colic and painless gross hematuria; symptom onset reflects a stone moving into the ureter and gives no clue as to when the stone actually formed, since stone growth is typically clinically silent over weeks to years (Harrison 22e p.2450).","Pain radiation depends on stone location: an upper ureteral stone may radiate anteriorly, while a lower ureteral stone may radiate to the ipsilateral testicle (men) or labium (women); a ureterovesical-junction stone can cause urinary urgency and frequency (Harrison 22e p.2450).","Absence of hematuria does not exclude a stone, particularly when urine flow is completely obstructed (Harrison 22e p.2450).","Noncontrast helical CT is the gold-standard imaging test: it detects stones as small as 1 mm and visualizes uric acid stones that are traditionally considered radiolucent on plain films (Harrison 22e p.2450).","Urine pH governs solubility of several stone types: uric acid stones form at pH consistently <=5.5, calcium phosphate stones are favored at pH >=6.5, and cystine is more soluble at higher pH; calcium oxalate stone risk is not influenced by urine pH (Harrison 22e p.2450).","Urine citrate is the most clinically important natural inhibitor of calcium-containing stones; metabolic acidosis (including from high animal-flesh intake) lowers citrate excretion by enhancing its tubular reabsorption (Harrison 22e p.2448, 2449).","Dietary calcium restriction is counterproductive: higher dietary calcium intake lowers stone risk by binding intestinal oxalate and reducing urinary oxalate, whereas low calcium intake increases stone risk and may lower bone density (Harrison 22e p.2449).","Two 24-h urine collections on the patient's usual diet are the cornerstone of preventive evaluation, measuring volume, calcium, oxalate, citrate, uric acid, sodium, potassium, phosphorus, magnesium, pH, and creatinine; two collections are needed because of substantial day-to-day variability (Harrison 22e p.2451-2452).","Urinary risk factors (calcium, oxalate, citrate) are continuous variables with no valid normal/abnormal cutoff; dichotomizing them is misleading and arbitrary thresholds should be avoided (Harrison 22e p.2449, 2452).","Pure uric acid stones can be recognized by their low Hounsfield units on CT, helping distinguish stone composition non-invasively (Harrison 22e p.2452)."],"pathophysiology":"Stones form when urine becomes supersaturated with lithogenic solutes, i.e., when the concentration product exceeds the solubility product. Most people's urine is supersaturated for one or more crystal types, but crystallization inhibitors (notably urine citrate) prevent continuous stone formation. Recent biopsy data have shifted the paradigm for stone initiation: calcium phosphate deposits in the renal interstitium, hypothesized to begin at the thin limb of the loop of Henle, then extends to and erodes through the papillary epithelium, forming Randall's plaque on which most calcium oxalate stones grow. Tubular plugs of calcium phosphate may initiate calcium phosphate stones. Thus stone formation can begin years before a stone is clinically detectable. Higher urine calcium (from greater GI absorption and/or bone turnover) and higher urine oxalate (largely dietary, with higher dietary calcium reducing GI oxalate absorption) raise calcium oxalate/phosphate risk, while low urine citrate removes a key inhibitor (Harrison 22e p.2448-2450).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute cholecystitis (right ureteropelvic-junction stone)","Acute appendicitis (stone at right pelvic brim)","Acute diverticulitis (stone at left pelvic brim)","Bacterial cystitis (ureterovesical-junction stone causing urgency/frequency)","Acute pyelonephritis (obstructing stone with proximal infection)","Musculoskeletal pain","Herpes zoster","Duodenal ulcer","Abdominal aortic aneurysm","Gynecologic conditions","Ureteral stricture","Ureteral obstruction by blood clot or sloughed papilla","Extraluminal ureteral compression (typically gradual onset, not true colic)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute pyelonephritis (an obstructing stone with proximal infection can present this way)","Bacterial cystitis (ureterovesical stone causes urgency/frequency with red and white cells in urine but a negative urine culture)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute cholecystitis","Acute appendicitis","Acute diverticulitis","Abdominal aortic aneurysm","Duodenal ulcer","Herpes zoster","Gynecologic conditions","Musculoskeletal pain"],"additionalInvestigations":["Noncontrast helical CT (gold standard; detects stones as small as 1 mm and uric acid stones)","Renal ultrasound (avoids radiation, shows hydronephrosis, but insensitive for ureteral stones)","Plain abdominal radiograph (KUB) (may miss ureteral/renal stones and gives no obstruction information)","Urinalysis with sediment examination for red/white cells and crystals (calcium oxalate dihydrate = bipyramidal; cystine = hexagonal)","Urine culture if infection is suspected","Serum electrolytes (for hypokalemia/RTA), creatinine, calcium, phosphorus, uric acid","PTH and 25-hydroxy vitamin D when serum/urine calcium is elevated or high/low-normal","At least two 24-h urine collections on usual diet (volume, calcium, oxalate, citrate, uric acid, sodium, potassium, phosphorus, magnesium, pH, creatinine; supersaturation when available)","Stone composition analysis of any passed or retrieved stone"],"redFlags":["UTI in the setting of an obstructing stone is a urologic emergency ('pus under pressure') requiring urgent restoration of drainage via ureteral stent or percutaneous nephrostomy","Complete urinary obstruction by a stone (hematuria may be absent)","Stone measuring >=6 mm or anatomic abnormality (low probability of spontaneous passage)","Intractable pain unresponsive to analgesia","Staghorn or multiple bilateral stones (suggest cystinuria or struvite/infection stones)","Infection (struvite) stones, which if untreated can lead to end-stage renal disease"],"prognosis":"Although renal colic is reported as among the worst pain patients experience, nephrolithiasis is rarely fatal. More than half of first-time stone formers have a recurrence within 10 years, but recurrence of most or all stone types can be prevented with careful evaluation and targeted, often lifelong, intervention. Untreated infection (struvite) stones can have devastating consequences and progress to end-stage renal disease, and repeated obstruction and instrumentation (e.g., in cystinuria) can reduce GFR. Primary hyperoxaluria can cause intraparenchymal calcium oxalate deposition leading to renal failure (Harrison 22e p.2448, 2450, 2451).","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal urinalysis (no hematuria) rules out a stone, which is false especially with complete obstruction","Recommending low dietary calcium, which actually increases stone risk and harms bone density","Using arbitrary normal/abnormal thresholds for urine calcium, oxalate, or citrate when risk is continuous","Basing the preventive regimen on a single 24-h urine collection despite large day-to-day variability","Mistaking a preexisting residual stone for a new stone when baseline imaging was suboptimal, leading to unnecessary changes in therapy","Misattributing flank/abdominal pain to a stone and missing cholecystitis, appendicitis, diverticulitis, AAA, or an occlusive vascular event","Failing to recognize that symptom onset does not indicate when the stone formed"],"management":["Manage acute renal colic with analgesia (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, with opioids if needed) and antiemetics, offering medical expulsive therapy with an alpha-blocker to aid passage of a ureteric stone","Urgently decompress an obstructed, infected kidney with a ureteric stent or percutaneous nephrostomy alongside antimicrobial therapy, as this is a urological emergency","Remove stones that will not pass by ureteroscopy, extracorporeal shockwave lithotripsy or percutaneous nephrolithotomy according to stone size, site and composition","Encourage high fluid intake to maintain dilute urine, with dietary measures of reduced sodium, moderate animal protein and adequate dietary calcium","Analyse the passed stone and undertake metabolic evaluation to target prevention, using a thiazide diuretic and potassium citrate for recurrent calcium stones and urinary alkalinisation with potassium citrate for uric acid stones","Use a xanthine oxidase inhibitor (allopurinol) for hyperuricosuric stone formers, and hydration with alkalinisation plus a thiol-binding agent (tiopronin or penicillamine) for cystine stones","Ensure complete stone clearance and treat the underlying infection for struvite (infection) stones","Provide follow-up imaging and biochemical monitoring to detect recurrence and guide lifelong prevention"],"severityClassification":"No formal grading; clinical risk stratification rests on likelihood of spontaneous passage (stone size and location, e.g., >=6 mm or anatomic abnormality favoring intervention) and on presence of emergency features such as obstruction with superimposed infection.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2448","Harrison 22e p.2449","Harrison 22e p.2450","Harrison 22e p.2451","Harrison 22e p.2452"],"crossLinks":[]},"nephronophthisis":{"name":"Nephronophthisis","system":"Renal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NPHP","juvenile nephronophthisis","ciliopathy","NPHP1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / OMIM","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive tubulointerstitial kidney disease and the most common genetic cause of kidney failure in children and young adults","It is a ciliopathy caused by variants in numerous NPHP genes, with a large deletion of NPHP1 being the single most frequent cause","Early features reflect impaired urinary concentration: polyuria, polydipsia, nocturia and secondary enuresis, often with salt wasting","Growth retardation and anaemia can be disproportionate to the degree of kidney impairment","Ultrasound classically shows normal or slightly reduced kidney size with increased echogenicity, loss of corticomedullary differentiation and corticomedullary cysts, distinguishing it from polycystic kidney disease with its large kidneys","Extrarenal ciliopathy features may coexist, including retinal degeneration (Senior-Loken syndrome), cerebellar vermis hypoplasia (Joubert syndrome), liver fibrosis and skeletal changes","Blood pressure is often normal until late, unlike many other progressive nephropathies"],"pathophysiology":"Nephronophthisis is an inherited ciliopathy in which variants in NPHP genes disrupt proteins (nephrocystins) that localise to the primary cilium, basal body and cell junctions of renal tubular epithelial cells. Impaired ciliary signalling and planar cell polarity disturb tubular development, cell adhesion and repair, leading to a chronic tubulointerstitial nephropathy with tubular basement membrane disruption, interstitial fibrosis and small corticomedullary cysts. Because the concentrating mechanism is affected early, the disease presents with polyuria and salt wasting before progressing to fibrosis and kidney failure. The same ciliary dysfunction in other organs accounts for the associated retinal, cerebellar, hepatic and skeletal syndromes.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal dominant and recessive polycystic kidney disease","Medullary cystic kidney disease / autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease","Other chronic tubulointerstitial nephropathies","Bardet-Biedl and other syndromic ciliopathies","Reflux or obstructive nephropathy","Alport syndrome and other hereditary nephropathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasound assessing kidney size, echogenicity, corticomedullary differentiation and cysts","Genetic testing including screening for the NPHP1 deletion and broader ciliopathy gene panels","Assessment of urinary concentrating ability and evidence of salt wasting","Serial kidney function and electrolyte monitoring","Ophthalmological review and, where indicated, brain imaging and liver assessment for extrarenal disease","Kidney biopsy in selected cases showing tubulointerstitial fibrosis with tubular basement membrane changes"],"redFlags":["Progressive decline toward kidney failure requiring planning for kidney replacement therapy","Marked polyuria with dehydration and electrolyte disturbance","Visual symptoms suggesting associated retinal degeneration","Neurological signs suggesting a syndromic ciliopathy such as Joubert syndrome"],"prognosis":"Nephronophthisis is progressive and typically leads to kidney failure, with the age of onset varying by subtype (infantile, juvenile and adolescent forms); the juvenile form commonly reaches kidney failure in the teenage years. Outcome after transplantation is generally good as the disease does not recur in the graft.","pitfalls":["Expecting enlarged kidneys and dismissing the diagnosis when ultrasound shows normal-sized echogenic kidneys","Missing the diagnosis in a child with unexplained polyuria, anaemia and growth failure","Failing to screen for retinal and neurological extrarenal manifestations","Attributing early salt wasting and polyuria to a primary concentrating defect without considering nephronophthisis"],"management":["Correct the early urinary concentrating defect and salt wasting with generous fluid and sodium replacement to avoid volume depletion","Manage progressive chronic kidney disease with control of anaemia (erythropoiesis-stimulating agents and iron), renal bone disease and electrolyte disturbance","Optimise growth and nutrition, including recombinant growth hormone where growth retardation is significant","Prepare for and deliver kidney replacement therapy with dialysis and pre-emptive transplantation, which is curative for the renal disease as it does not recur in the graft","Screen for and manage extrarenal ciliopathy manifestations with ophthalmological review for retinal degeneration and assessment for hepatic fibrosis and cerebellar involvement","Refer to paediatric nephrology and clinical genetics for coordinated multidisciplinary care","Offer genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance, including testing for the NPHP1 deletion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","OMIM: Nephronophthisis (NPHP1)"],"crossLinks":[]},"nephrotic_syndrome_paediatric":{"name":"Nephrotic syndrome (paediatric)","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["childhood nephrotic syndrome","idiopathic nephrotic syndrome","minimal change disease","paediatric nephrotic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the tetrad of heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminaemia, oedema, and hyperlipidaemia.","In children it is most often idiopathic, and minimal change disease is the predominant histology, especially in preschool children.","Oedema is the usual presenting feature, typically starting periorbitally and becoming generalised with dependent swelling and sometimes ascites.","Most childhood cases are steroid-responsive, so an empirical corticosteroid trial is standard first-line therapy without initial biopsy in the typical age group.","Renal biopsy is reserved for atypical features: older or very young age, gross haematuria, hypertension, impaired renal function, or steroid resistance.","Children are at increased risk of infection (notably pneumococcal peritonitis) from urinary immunoglobulin loss, and of thromboembolism from a hypercoagulable state.","Supportive care includes sensible salt restriction, careful management of oedema, and attention to nutrition and infection prevention including vaccination.","Relapses are common; the pattern of response guides classification into steroid-sensitive, frequently relapsing, steroid-dependent, or steroid-resistant disease."],"pathophysiology":"Injury to the glomerular filtration barrier, particularly the podocytes and their slit diaphragms, increases permeability to plasma proteins, producing heavy albuminuria. In minimal change disease, light microscopy is near-normal and electron microscopy shows diffuse podocyte foot process effacement, implicating a circulating permeability factor and immune dysregulation. Urinary protein loss lowers serum albumin, reducing plasma oncotic pressure and, together with secondary sodium retention, causing oedema. The liver increases lipoprotein synthesis in response to low oncotic pressure, producing hyperlipidaemia. Loss of immunoglobulins and complement factors predisposes to infection, and loss of anticoagulant proteins with raised procoagulants predisposes to thrombosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute glomerulonephritis (nephritic syndrome)","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","Membranous nephropathy (uncommon in children)","Henoch-Schonlein purpura nephritis","Systemic lupus erythematosus nephritis","Congenital nephrotic syndrome in infants","Oedema from cardiac, hepatic, or nutritional causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and quantification of proteinuria (urine protein-to-creatinine ratio or timed collection)","Serum albumin, total protein, and lipid profile","Renal function and electrolytes","Full blood count","Complement levels and autoimmune serology when nephritic features are present","Renal biopsy in atypical or steroid-resistant cases"],"redFlags":["Fever with abdominal pain suggesting spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Signs of thromboembolism (limb swelling, respiratory distress, neurological signs)","Hypertension, gross haematuria, or reduced renal function suggesting a nephritic process","Severe hypovolaemia from rapid fluid shifts, or tense ascites and respiratory compromise","Steroid resistance prompting reassessment and biopsy"],"prognosis":"Children with steroid-sensitive minimal change disease generally have a good long-term renal outcome despite frequent relapses; steroid resistance, often from focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, carries a higher risk of progression to chronic kidney disease.","pitfalls":["Attributing oedema to another cause and missing proteinuria on urinalysis","Overlooking infection or thrombosis, the major acute complications","Missing atypical features that warrant biopsy rather than empirical steroids","Aggressive diuresis precipitating hypovolaemia and thrombosis in an intravascularly depleted child"],"management":["First-line definitive therapy is an empirical course of oral corticosteroids, which induces remission in most children with steroid-sensitive minimal change disease","Manage relapsing or steroid-dependent disease with steroid-sparing agents such as levamisole, a calcineurin inhibitor (ciclosporin or tacrolimus), mycophenolate mofetil or rituximab","Control oedema with dietary sodium restriction and judicious loop diuretics, using cautious albumin infusion for severe symptomatic oedema","Reduce infection risk through pneumococcal and other vaccination and prompt treatment of infection, remaining alert to spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Mitigate the hypercoagulable state with attention to mobilisation and thromboprophylaxis in high-risk children","Monitor with home urine dipstick testing to detect relapse, alongside surveillance of blood pressure, growth and corticosteroid toxicity","Refer for renal biopsy and paediatric nephrology input in atypical or steroid-resistant disease, often focal segmental glomerulosclerosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"nervous_system_disorders_in_critical":{"name":"Nervous System Disorders in Critical Care","system":"Nervous System","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Neurologic critical care","Neurocritical care disorders","ICU neurologic complications"],"chapter":318,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2342-2352","clinicalPearls":["Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) equals mean arterial pressure (MAP) minus intracranial pressure (ICP); the general targets in neurocritical care are ICP below 20 mmHg and CPP at or above 60 mmHg (Harrison 22e p.2345).","Cerebral autoregulation normally keeps cerebral blood flow constant across a wide MAP range, but in severe brain injury it can be lost, making flow directly pressure-dependent so that hypotension causes ischemia and hypertension causes hyperperfusion (Harrison 22e p.2343-2344).","Cerebral blood flow rises with hypercapnia/acidosis and falls with hypocapnia/alkalosis, which is the basis for short-term hyperventilation to lower ICP; the effect is short-lived (hours) due to interstitial buffering, and abrupt discontinuation can cause rebound ICP elevation (Harrison 22e p.2344, p.2345).","In altered consciousness, the first task is to separate a diffuse/metabolic process from a focal/structural one; prominent focal signs such as pupillary asymmetry, hemiparesis, gaze palsy, or visual field deficit suggest a structural lesion and mandate urgent neuroimaging (Harrison 22e p.2344).","After cardiac arrest, poor-prognosis indicators include absent pupillary light reflex or extensor/absent motor response to pain at days 5-7, bilateral absence of the N20 somatosensory evoked potential component, a burst-suppression or nonreactive EEG, and a markedly elevated neuron-specific enolase (NSE), but sedation, metabolic derangements, and hypothermia confound these signs (Harrison 22e p.2346).","Osmotic demyelination syndrome (formerly central pontine myelinolysis) is typically precipitated by rapid correction of hyponatremia or hyperosmolar states, presenting days later with quadriplegia and pseudobulbar palsy; corrective sodium changes should be limited to roughly 8 mmol/L per 24 h and 15 mmol/L per 48 h (Harrison 22e p.2348-2349).","Wernicke's disease is a thiamine-deficiency emergency; only about one-third of patients show the classic triad of ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and global confusion, and IV glucose can precipitate or worsen it, so thiamine must precede glucose in at-risk patients (Harrison 22e p.2349).","Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) reflects hyperperfusion/vasogenic edema favoring posterior (occipital) regions; the rate of blood pressure rise rather than the absolute value is the key risk factor, and the syndrome is not always reversible or strictly posterior (Harrison 22e p.2349-2350).","Sepsis-associated encephalopathy is a diffuse brain dysfunction without focal findings, occurs in the majority of patients with sepsis and multiorgan failure, and is not simply explained by metabolic derangements; cytokines such as TNF and interleukins are implicated (Harrison 22e p.2348).","Critical illness polyneuropathy is the most common PNS complication of prolonged critical illness and sepsis; it is a distal axonal sensorimotor neuropathy and should be suspected when a patient fails to wean from the ventilator despite resolving sepsis (Harrison 22e p.2351).","Persistent neuromuscular junction weakness in the ICU is almost always drug-related, most often from nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocking agents (pancuronium, vecuronium, rocuronium, cisatracurium) and potentiated by aminoglycosides; recovery is full once the drug is cleared (Harrison 22e p.2351-2352).","In PNS weakness, intubation should be considered when maximal inspiratory force falls below -25 cmH2O or vital capacity is under 1 L, and the decision should be clinical rather than waiting for oxygen desaturation or CO2 retention (Harrison 22e p.2351)."],"pathophysiology":"Life-threatening neurologic illness in the critically ill arises from primary lesions anywhere along the neuraxis or as a consequence of systemic disorders (hepatic failure, multiorgan failure, cardiac arrest). The unifying goal is preventing secondary brain injury from ischemia, hemorrhage, edema, herniation, and elevated ICP. Brain edema is vasogenic (fluid crossing an incompetent blood-brain barrier) and/or cytotoxic (cellular swelling and death); clinically significant edema usually combines both and can raise ICP and cause tissue shifts. Inadequate oxygen/glucose delivery triggers the ischemic cascade: excitatory amino acid (glutamate) release, calcium and sodium influx, protease/lipase activation, lipid peroxidation, free-radical membrane injury, cytotoxic edema, and necrotic cell death; an alternative apoptotic pathway occurs without edema and is often not seen on imaging. Penumbra denotes still-salvageable ischemic tissue. Secondary insults (hypotension, hypoxia, fever, seizures, hyperglycemia) worsen the cascade. The skull tolerates little added volume, so once intracranial compliance is exhausted, small volume increases sharply raise ICP, lower CPP, and provoke ischemia; ischemia-driven autoregulatory vasodilation can paradoxically further raise blood volume and ICP, creating a vicious cycle.","additionalDifferentials":["Diffuse/metabolic encephalopathy: organ failure (hepatic, renal), drug overdose, sedative/analgesic effect, electrolyte disturbance (hyponatremia, hypoglycemia), hypoxia-ischemia, hypercarbia","Focal/structural lesions: ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke, traumatic brain injury with intracranial hematoma, tumor, abscess, subdural/epidural hematoma","Basilar artery thrombosis causing brainstem ischemia (may mimic generalized seizures with extensor posturing)","Nonconvulsive status epilepticus presenting as unexplained decreased consciousness","Wernicke's disease in any disoriented at-risk patient","Sepsis-associated encephalopathy"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial meningitis presenting as global encephalopathy","Encephalitis (including herpes simplex virus in immunosuppressed transplant patients)","CNS opportunistic infection in transplant patients (Nocardia, Toxoplasma, aspergillosis, CMV, HHV-6, varicella, JC virus PML)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sedative/analgesic or polypharmacy-induced delirium misattributed to depression","Postcardiac bypass microembolic encephalopathy","Calcineurin inhibitor (cyclosporine, tacrolimus) neurotoxicity / PRES, possible even at normal drug levels","Checkpoint inhibitor immune encephalitis and CAR-T neurotoxicity","Posthypoxic myoclonus / myoclonic status epilepticus after cardiac arrest","Korsakoff amnestic state evolving from Wernicke's disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Noncontrast CT as the initial study for hemorrhage, hydrocephalus, and tissue shifts; hyperdense basilar artery sign suggests thrombus","MRI including diffusion-weighted imaging for acute ischemic stroke and laminar cortical injury after hypoxia-ischemia","CT/MR angiography or venography for arterial occlusion or cerebral venous thrombosis","EEG to detect nonconvulsive status epilepticus and characterize metabolic slowing or burst-suppression/myoclonic status","Lumbar puncture to exclude infection/inflammation (after neuroimaging in coma); elevated opening pressure may indicate venous sinus thrombosis","ICP monitoring (ventriculostomy preferred as it allows CSF drainage; parenchymal device for small ventricles or coagulopathy)","Serum electrolytes (especially sodium, calcium), glucose, renal and hepatic function, CBC, coagulation studies; toxicology screen in unexplained encephalopathy","Somatosensory evoked potentials (N20) and neuron-specific enolase for post-arrest prognostication","Carboxyhemoglobin measurement for suspected carbon monoxide poisoning","Bubble study (agitated saline echocardiography) to evaluate right-to-left shunt after embolic infarction in transplant patients"],"redFlags":["Coma of sudden onset with extensor posturing and cranial nerve abnormalities suggesting basilar artery thrombosis (emergency imaging required)","Coma with unilateral pupillary change as a late sign of elevated ICP requiring immediate intervention","Decreased consciousness with prominent focal neurologic signs indicating a possible structural lesion","Systolic blood pressure below 90 mmHg even briefly, or oxygen saturation below 90%, as secondary insults worsening outcome","Maximal inspiratory force below -25 cmH2O or vital capacity below 1 L, or severe bulbar/palatal weakness, signaling impending respiratory failure","Persistently dilated nonreactive pupils and absent brainstem reflexes after hypoxic-ischemic injury","Failure to wean from mechanical ventilation despite resolving systemic illness (suggests critical illness neuropathy/myopathy or prolonged neuromuscular blockade)"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on the underlying disorder and on prevention of secondary injury. After hypoxia-ischemia, recovery may be full if circulation is restored within 3-5 minutes, while longer insults often cause permanent damage; the brain tolerates pure hypoxia better than combined hypoxia-ischemia. Reliable post-arrest prognostication requires sufficient observation time, and hypothermia/sedation delay when predictors become valid; some patients awaken after a week or longer, so premature withdrawal risks a self-fulfilling prophecy. Sepsis-associated encephalopathy carries roughly 50% mortality when severe enough to cause coma, though this mainly reflects underlying illness severity; the encephalopathy usually improves substantially with successful treatment, but long-term cognitive dysfunction is increasingly recognized, especially in older survivors. Critical illness polyneuropathy and thick-filament critical illness myopathy generally recover spontaneously if the patient survives, though recovery may take weeks to months and some residual weakness can persist. Prolonged neuromuscular blockade causes no permanent damage and resolves once the agent clears.","pitfalls":["Performing lumbar puncture before neuroimaging in a comatose or profoundly encephalopathic patient","Delaying empiric antibiotics for suspected bacterial meningitis while awaiting diagnostic studies","Attributing the hypoactive confusional state of microembolic encephalopathy after cardiac surgery to depression or sedative delirium","Giving IV glucose before thiamine in malnourished/alcoholic patients, precipitating or worsening Wernicke's disease","Correcting hyponatremia too rapidly and triggering osmotic demyelination syndrome","Assuming normal serum calcineurin inhibitor levels exclude drug-induced PRES or neurotoxicity","Prolonged or unmonitored use of nondepolarizing neuromuscular blockers leading to persistent blockade or thick-filament myopathy (use lowest effective dose with peripheral nerve stimulator monitoring)","Using glucocorticoids for ICP in head trauma or ischemic/hemorrhagic stroke, where they are not indicated (reserved for vasogenic edema from tumor or abscess)","Sustained or aggressive hyperventilation, which can worsen cerebral ischemia and cause rebound ICP on discontinuation"],"management":["Prevent secondary brain injury by maintaining adequate cerebral perfusion pressure and oxygenation while avoiding hypotension, hypoxaemia, hyperglycaemia and fever","Treat raised intracranial pressure in a stepwise manner with head elevation, sedation and analgesia, osmotherapy using mannitol or hypertonic saline, and cerebrospinal fluid drainage via ventriculostomy","Use short-term controlled hyperventilation only as a temporising measure for impending herniation, avoiding abrupt discontinuation because of rebound intracranial hypertension","Institute invasive intracranial pressure monitoring and structured neurological observation to guide therapy and detect deterioration early","Manage seizures and non-convulsive status epilepticus with antiseizure medication, guided by continuous EEG","Correct hyponatraemia slowly to avoid osmotic demyelination, and identify and treat the underlying systemic or metabolic precipitant","Escalate to neurosurgical intervention such as decompressive craniectomy or haematoma evacuation for structural lesions, within a multidisciplinary neurocritical care team"],"severityClassification":"Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score of 8 or less defines severe traumatic brain injury and is a key criterion for ICP monitoring (Harrison 22e p.2345). Operational neurocritical care targets include ICP below 20 mmHg and CPP at or above 60 mmHg, with stepwise escalation of therapy for sustained ICP above 20-25 mmHg; CAR-T cytokine release syndrome and neurotoxicity severity track with degree of fever, CRP, and ferritin elevation.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2342-2352"],"crossLinks":[]},"nervus_intermedius_neuralgia":{"name":"Nervus intermedius neuralgia","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["intermedius neuralgia","nervus intermedius neuralgia of Wrisberg","facial nerve sensory neuralgia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Causes brief paroxysms of severe pain felt deep in the ear canal, sometimes radiating to the auricle, soft palate, or over the face","A trigger zone in the posterior wall of the external auditory canal is characteristic, with attacks provoked by touching the canal or by swallowing","Because the nervus intermedius carries taste, lacrimal, and salivary fibres, attacks may be associated with altered taste, lacrimation, or salivation","It is closely related to, and often used synonymously with, geniculate neuralgia, both reflecting neuralgia of the sensory division of the facial nerve","A varicella-zoster (herpetic) origin is recognised and it may follow Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Diagnosis requires exclusion of primary ear disease, glossopharyngeal and trigeminal neuralgia, and structural posterior fossa lesions","First-line treatment is anticonvulsant medication such as carbamazepine; microvascular decompression or nervus intermedius section is reserved for refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Nervus intermedius neuralgia is a paroxysmal pain disorder of the nervus intermedius, the sensory and parasympathetic branch of the facial nerve. Its sensory fibres, relaying via the geniculate ganglion, supply a small area of the external auditory canal and auricle, which explains the deep-ear pain. Ectopic, hyperexcitable neural activity, attributed to vascular compression at the root entry zone or to prior herpetic inflammation of the ganglion, generates the lancinating paroxysms. The nerve's parasympathetic and taste functions account for accompanying lacrimation, salivation, and gustatory disturbance during attacks, and its anatomical intimacy with the facial, glossopharyngeal, and trigeminal nerves produces overlapping presentations.","additionalDifferentials":["Geniculate neuralgia","Glossopharyngeal neuralgia","Trigeminal neuralgia","Ramsay Hunt syndrome (herpes zoster oticus)","Primary otological disease (otitis externa or media)","Cerebellopontine angle tumour","Referred otalgia from a head and neck malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy and microscopy to exclude external and middle ear disease","MRI of the internal auditory meatus and cerebellopontine angle focused on the facial nerve","MR angiography to detect a compressive vascular loop","Testing of the external auditory canal for a triggerable zone","Examination of the pharynx and larynx to exclude referred otalgia from malignancy","Audiometry if hearing symptoms accompany the pain"],"redFlags":["Auricular vesicles with facial weakness suggesting Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Persistent unilateral otalgia with a normal ear prompting a search for a head and neck primary","A cerebellopontine angle mass on imaging","Progressive facial nerve weakness or additional cranial neuropathies"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign but distressing; many patients respond to anticonvulsant therapy, and surgical decompression or sectioning of the nervus intermedius provides relief in selected refractory cases.","pitfalls":["Attributing deep-ear pain to primary ear disease when the ear is normal","Not distinguishing it from, or conflating it with, glossopharyngeal neuralgia","Overlooking a herpetic (post-Ramsay Hunt) aetiology","Failing to exclude referred otalgia from an occult malignancy","Missing a treatable vascular loop or posterior fossa lesion on imaging"],"management":["First-line medical therapy is with an anticonvulsant such as carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine to suppress the neuralgic paroxysms","Add or substitute other neuropathic agents such as gabapentin, pregabalin or baclofen if response is inadequate or the first agent is not tolerated","Where a herpetic (varicella-zoster) origin or preceding Ramsay Hunt syndrome is identified, treat with antiviral therapy","Consider microvascular decompression of the nervus intermedius where a compressive vascular loop is demonstrated in drug-refractory cases","Offer sectioning of the nervus intermedius, with the geniculate ganglion, as a surgical option for disabling, treatment-resistant pain","Confirm the diagnosis by excluding primary ear disease, glossopharyngeal and trigeminal neuralgia and structural posterior fossa lesions, with neurology and otolaryngology referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"netherton_syndrome":{"name":"Netherton syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPINK5","LEKTI deficiency","ichthyosis linearis circumflexa","comel-Netherton syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in SPINK5, which encodes the serine protease inhibitor LEKTI","Classic triad of congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma, a specific hair shaft defect (trichorrhexis invaginata, or 'bamboo hair'), and atopic diathesis with very high IgE","The characteristic migratory rash, ichthyosis linearis circumflexa, shows serpiginous double-edged scaling plaques","Neonates present with generalised erythroderma and scaling and are at high risk of dehydration, hypernatraemia, infection, and failure to thrive","Hair microscopy of the eyebrows may reveal the invaginated node defect more reliably than scalp hair","Skin barrier is markedly impaired, so percutaneous absorption of topical agents (including calcineurin inhibitors) can cause systemic toxicity","Confirmation is by SPINK5 genetic testing supported by absent LEKTI staining on immunohistochemistry"],"pathophysiology":"Netherton syndrome results from loss of function of the LEKTI protein, a multidomain serine protease inhibitor expressed in the granular and uppermost spinous layers of the epidermis. Without LEKTI, kallikrein-related peptidases (notably KLK5 and KLK7) are unrestrained, leading to premature and excessive degradation of corneodesmosomes and detachment of the stratum corneum. The result is a profoundly defective epidermal barrier with abnormal desquamation. Unchecked protease activity also drives proinflammatory and pro-allergic signalling, producing the severe atopic phenotype, elevated IgE, and susceptibility to cutaneous infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Atopic dermatitis (severe, early-onset)","Congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma / other autosomal recessive congenital ichthyoses","Peeling skin syndrome","Immunodysregulation such as Omenn syndrome or other severe combined immunodeficiency presenting with erythroderma","Psoriasiform erythroderma","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome in the neonate"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Light microscopy of hair shafts (eyebrow and scalp) for trichorrhexis invaginata","SPINK5 molecular genetic testing","Skin biopsy with immunostaining for LEKTI expression","Serum IgE and eosinophil count","Serum electrolytes and hydration status in neonates","Microbiological cultures when infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neonatal hypernatraemic dehydration and thermoregulatory instability from barrier failure","Sepsis or recurrent severe skin and systemic infections","Failure to thrive and growth faltering","Signs of systemic toxicity from absorbed topical agents (particularly calcineurin inhibitors)"],"prognosis":"It is a lifelong condition; the neonatal period carries the highest mortality risk from dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, and infection, after which the phenotype tends to stabilise into ichthyosis linearis circumflexa with persistent atopy, though quality of life and growth remain affected.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as recalcitrant atopic dermatitis and delaying genetic evaluation","Applying topical calcineurin inhibitors over large areas of impaired barrier, risking systemic absorption and toxicity","Sampling only scalp hair and missing the diagnostic shaft defect best seen in eyebrows","Underestimating fluid, electrolyte, and infection risk in the erythrodermic neonate"],"management":["Neonatal stabilisation is the priority, with careful fluid and electrolyte management, temperature regulation and nutritional support to counter transepidermal water loss, dehydration and hypernatraemia","Restore the skin barrier with liberal bland emollients and gentle skin care","Use topical agents cautiously because the defective barrier permits systemic absorption and toxicity, avoiding topical calcineurin inhibitors over large areas and monitoring levels if used","Treat cutaneous and systemic infection promptly with appropriate antimicrobials, maintaining vigilance for sepsis","Manage the atopic diathesis and pruritus with antihistamines and allergen avoidance, and screen for food allergy","Monitor growth and consider anti-inflammatory or immunomodulatory therapy such as intravenous immunoglobulin or a biologic targeting the inflammatory pathway in severe disease","Provide multidisciplinary dermatology, paediatric and dietetic care, and offer genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive SPINK5 inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuroblastoma":{"name":"Neuroblastoma","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neuroblastic tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common extracranial solid tumour of childhood and the most common malignancy of infancy, arising from primitive sympathetic neural crest cells.","It can occur anywhere along the sympathetic chain; the adrenal medulla and paraspinal abdomen are the most frequent primary sites.","An abdominal primary often presents as a firm, irregular mass that crosses the midline, distinguishing it from Wilms tumour.","Presentation is highly variable and may include systemic upset, bone pain, proptosis with periorbital bruising ('raccoon eyes') from orbital metastases, or a paraspinal mass causing cord compression.","Distinctive paraneoplastic features include opsoclonus-myoclonus (dancing eyes, dancing feet) and secretory diarrhoea from vasoactive intestinal peptide.","Most tumours secrete catecholamines, so urinary homovanillic acid (HVA) and vanillylmandelic acid (VMA) are useful markers.","Behaviour spans a remarkable spectrum, from spontaneous regression in infants (including stage MS/4S disease) to aggressive, widely metastatic disease.","MYCN amplification is a key adverse biological marker that upstages risk and intensifies therapy.","Risk stratification integrating age, stage, histology, and genetics guides treatment ranging from observation or surgery to intensive multimodal therapy."],"pathophysiology":"Neuroblastoma develops from neural crest-derived precursors of the sympathetic nervous system, giving rise to a spectrum from undifferentiated neuroblastoma to more mature ganglioneuroblastoma and benign ganglioneuroma. Tumour behaviour is strongly governed by biology: MYCN amplification, segmental chromosomal changes, and DNA ploidy correlate with aggressiveness, while some infant tumours undergo differentiation or apoptosis and regress spontaneously.","additionalDifferentials":["Wilms tumour","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Lymphoma or leukaemic infiltration","Phaeochromocytoma","Ewing sarcoma / other small round blue cell tumours","Hepatoblastoma or other abdominal masses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinary catecholamine metabolites (HVA and VMA)","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) of the primary site","MIBG scintigraphy for functional imaging and metastasis detection","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine biopsy for marrow involvement","Tumour biopsy for histology and biology including MYCN status","Full blood count, renal and liver function, lactate dehydrogenase and ferritin"],"redFlags":["Signs of spinal cord compression from a paraspinal (dumbbell) tumour","Rapidly progressive or widely metastatic disease","Periorbital bruising and proptosis suggesting orbital metastases","Severe secretory diarrhoea from a VIP-secreting tumour","Systemic deterioration with bone pain and marrow failure"],"prognosis":"Outcome is extremely variable: low-risk and infant disease (including 4S/MS) often has excellent survival and may regress, whereas high-risk disease with MYCN amplification or widespread metastases has a much poorer prognosis despite intensive therapy.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with Wilms tumour when it presents as an abdominal mass","Missing spinal cord compression from a paraspinal component","Overlooking opsoclonus-myoclonus as a paraneoplastic clue to occult tumour","Failing to obtain MYCN status, which changes management","Assuming all disease is aggressive and over-treating low-risk or regressing tumours"],"management":["Stratify treatment by risk group using stage, age, MYCN status and tumour biology, as intensity ranges from observation to intensive multimodal therapy","For low-risk and infant (stage 4S/MS) disease, offer surgical resection or observation, as many such tumours regress spontaneously","Treat intermediate-risk disease with moderate-intensity chemotherapy (carboplatin, etoposide, cyclophosphamide and doxorubicin) combined with surgical resection","Manage high-risk disease with intensive induction chemotherapy, surgery, high-dose chemotherapy with autologous stem-cell rescue, radiotherapy, and maintenance anti-GD2 immunotherapy (dinutuximab) with retinoic acid differentiation therapy","Provide supportive oncology care, treat cord compression from a paraspinal mass urgently, and manage paraneoplastic opsoclonus-myoclonus with immunotherapy","Refer to a paediatric oncology multidisciplinary team and consider MIBG-directed radionuclide therapy for refractory or relapsed disease","Undertake long-term surveillance for relapse and monitoring for late effects of therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neurocutaneous_melanosis":{"name":"Neurocutaneous melanosis","system":"Dermatology / Neurocutaneous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neurocutaneous melanocytosis","NCM","leptomeningeal melanosis with congenital melanocytic naevi"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Neurocutaneous Disorders (current reference texts)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by large or multiple congenital melanocytic naevi together with abnormal melanocytic proliferation in the leptomeninges","Risk is highest with a large or giant congenital melanocytic naevus in a posterior axial (head, neck or spine) location and with numerous satellite naevi","Caused by post-zygotic activating variants, most often in NRAS, affecting neural-crest-derived melanocyte precursors","Many affected children are neurologically asymptomatic, but leptomeningeal melanosis can cause raised intracranial pressure and hydrocephalus","Presenting neurological features include seizures, developmental delay, cranial nerve palsies and signs of a mass lesion","There is a significant risk of central nervous system melanoma arising within the abnormal leptomeningeal melanocytes","Management is generic and supportive: neuroimaging surveillance, treatment of hydrocephalus and seizures, and oncological referral if melanoma develops"],"pathophysiology":"Neurocutaneous melanosis results from a post-zygotic activating variant, most commonly in NRAS, occurring in a neural-crest melanocyte precursor early in embryogenesis. The affected clone gives rise both to large or multiple congenital melanocytic naevi in the skin and to an excess of melanocytes that populate the leptomeninges. Proliferation of these leptomeningeal melanocytes can impair cerebrospinal fluid flow, producing hydrocephalus and raised intracranial pressure, and may undergo malignant transformation into central nervous system melanoma. The shared NRAS driver explains the concurrence of extensive cutaneous pigmentation and intracranial melanocytic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated large congenital melanocytic naevus without CNS involvement","Primary central nervous system melanoma","Meningeal metastatic melanoma","Other causes of infantile hydrocephalus","Leptomeningeal infiltration from other neoplasia","Sturge-Weber syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain and spine, ideally in infancy before myelination obscures melanin signal, to detect leptomeningeal melanosis","Neurological and developmental assessment","Assessment for hydrocephalus and raised intracranial pressure","Cerebrospinal fluid cytology where malignant transformation is suspected","Skin examination to map the number and distribution of congenital melanocytic naevi","Molecular testing of naevus tissue for NRAS variants where available"],"redFlags":["Signs of raised intracranial pressure or rapidly enlarging head circumference","New seizures, focal deficit or cranial nerve palsy","Developmental regression","Any feature suggesting central nervous system melanoma"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies widely: asymptomatic children may remain well, but symptomatic leptomeningeal disease carries a guarded outlook, and the development of central nervous system melanoma is associated with a poor prognosis despite treatment.","pitfalls":["Focusing only on cutaneous naevi and not arranging early MRI in high-risk patients","Delaying imaging until after infancy when myelination reduces sensitivity for melanin","Attributing hydrocephalus to other causes and missing leptomeningeal melanosis","Underestimating the melanoma risk in symptomatic disease"],"management":["Establish the extent of disease with neuraxis MRI in infancy and characterise the cutaneous naevi to guide prognosis and monitoring","Relieve symptomatic hydrocephalus and raised intracranial pressure with cerebrospinal fluid diversion such as a ventriculoperitoneal shunt","Control seizures with antiseizure medication","Undertake surveillance for malignant transformation with clinical review and neuroimaging, and cerebrospinal fluid cytology where central nervous system melanoma is suspected","Manage established central nervous system melanoma palliatively through a neuro-oncology team, as it responds poorly to treatment","Address the cutaneous naevi with dermatological monitoring and consider surgical management for melanoma risk and cosmesis","Provide multidisciplinary neurology, dermatology and neurosurgical care with developmental support and family counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Happle R. Mosaicism in Human Skin"],"crossLinks":[]},"neurocysticercosis":{"name":"Neurocysticercosis","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NCC","CNS cysticercosis","cerebral cysticercosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Central nervous system infection by larval Taenia solium and one of the leading causes of acquired epilepsy in India and other endemic regions","Seizures are the most frequent presentation, typically from a single or few degenerating parenchymal cysts","Clinical features depend on cyst number, location and stage, ranging from asymptomatic calcified lesions to disabling neurological disease","Extraparenchymal disease (intraventricular, subarachnoid or racemose forms) can obstruct cerebrospinal fluid flow and cause hydrocephalus, and behaves more aggressively","MRI and CT are central to diagnosis; the finding of a cyst with a visible scolex is highly characteristic, and calcified lesions indicate old, dead cysts","Antiparasitic therapy is combined with corticosteroids to blunt the inflammatory reaction to dying cysts, with antiseizure medication for those presenting with seizures","Treatment must be individualised: viable parenchymal cysts, calcified lesions and intraventricular cysts are managed differently, and some forms need neurosurgical intervention","Fundoscopy or ophthalmic assessment is advised before antiparasitic treatment to exclude ocular cysts"],"pathophysiology":"Ingested Taenia solium eggs release oncospheres that reach the brain and develop into cysticerci. Viable cysts in the parenchyma may remain quiescent, but immune-mediated degeneration releases antigens that provoke perilesional inflammation and oedema, generating seizures and focal deficits. As cysts die they may calcify, and these calcified foci can remain epileptogenic. In the ventricles and subarachnoid space, cysts and the associated inflammation can block cerebrospinal fluid pathways, producing raised intracranial pressure and hydrocephalus, while subarachnoid (racemose) disease may cause arachnoiditis and vasculitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculoma","Cerebral toxoplasmosis","Pyogenic or fungal brain abscess","Primary or metastatic brain tumour","Other parasitic CNS lesions (e.g. hydatid cyst)"],"infectionMimics":["CNS tuberculosis","Toxoplasma encephalitis in immunocompromised patients","Primary CNS neoplasm","Vasculitic or demyelinating lesions"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI brain to characterise cyst stage, detect a scolex and assess perilesional oedema","CT to identify calcified lesions","Serological antibody testing (e.g. EITB) as supportive evidence","Ophthalmological examination to exclude ocular cysts before therapy","CSF studies and assessment for hydrocephalus in suspected extraparenchymal disease"],"redFlags":["New-onset or breakthrough seizures, including status epilepticus, requiring urgent management","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus from intraventricular disease, a neurosurgical emergency","Rapid neurological deterioration precipitated by inflammation when antiparasitic therapy is started","Focal deficits, altered consciousness or signs of stroke from subarachnoid vasculitis"],"prognosis":"Solitary parenchymal cysts often resolve with good seizure control and a favourable outcome, though some patients develop chronic epilepsy from residual calcified lesions. Extraparenchymal, subarachnoid and heavy-burden disease carries a worse prognosis with risk of hydrocephalus, recurrent complications and higher mortality.","pitfalls":["Giving antiparasitic drugs without corticosteroid cover, risking severe inflammatory worsening and seizures","Treating without first excluding ocular or intraventricular cysts, where drug-induced inflammation is dangerous","Assuming all ring-enhancing brain lesions are neurocysticercosis and missing tuberculoma or tumour","Overlooking hydrocephalus as a cause of deterioration in extraparenchymal disease"],"management":["Give antiparasitic therapy with albendazole, with or without praziquantel, to hasten resolution of viable parenchymal cysts","Co-administer corticosteroids with antiparasitic treatment to blunt the inflammatory reaction to dying cysts and control perilesional oedema","Control seizures with antiseizure medication, a mainstay for those presenting with epilepsy","Exclude ocular cysticercosis by ophthalmological examination before starting antiparasitic therapy to avoid sight-threatening inflammation","Manage extraparenchymal disease and hydrocephalus with neurosurgical intervention such as endoscopic cyst removal or cerebrospinal fluid diversion, often with prolonged antiparasitic and corticosteroid therapy","Withhold antiparasitic drugs in calcified-only (dead) lesions and in cysticercal encephalitis with diffuse oedema, treating supportively","Institute public health measures and family screening for taeniasis, with follow-up imaging to confirm lesion resolution"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuroendocrine_tumor":{"name":"Neuroendocrine tumor","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NET","neuroendocrine neoplasm","carcinoid tumour","NEN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Neuroendocrine tumours are a heterogeneous group arising most commonly in the gastrointestinal tract, pancreas, and lungs, ranging from indolent well-differentiated tumours to highly aggressive neuroendocrine carcinomas","Tumour grade, based on proliferative markers such as mitotic count and Ki-67 index, is central to classifying behaviour and guiding treatment","Many are non-functioning and present late with mass effect or metastasis, while functioning tumours cause distinctive hormonal syndromes","The carcinoid syndrome, with flushing, diarrhoea, and wheeze, typically appears only once a midgut tumour has metastasised to the liver, allowing vasoactive mediators to bypass hepatic clearance","Chromogranin A is a widely used circulating marker, and urinary 5-HIAA supports the diagnosis of serotonin-secreting carcinoid tumours","Somatostatin receptor imaging, such as gallium-labelled PET, is valuable for localisation and staging and predicts response to somatostatin receptor-targeted therapy","Somatostatin analogues control hormonal symptoms and can slow tumour growth, and peptide receptor radionuclide therapy is an option for receptor-positive progressive disease","Poorly differentiated neuroendocrine carcinomas behave like aggressive high-grade cancers and are treated with platinum-based chemotherapy, in contrast to the more indolent management of well-differentiated tumours"],"pathophysiology":"Neuroendocrine tumours originate from cells of the diffuse neuroendocrine system distributed throughout the body, which share the capacity to synthesise and secrete peptide hormones and biogenic amines. Well-differentiated tumours retain hormone production and receptor expression, including somatostatin receptors that are exploited for imaging and therapy, and generally grow slowly. Functioning tumours produce clinical syndromes through hormone excess, such as serotonin in carcinoid syndrome or insulin, gastrin, and other peptides from pancreatic tumours. Poorly differentiated neuroendocrine carcinomas lose these features and behave aggressively with rapid proliferation and early metastasis. Some tumours occur within hereditary syndromes such as multiple endocrine neoplasia.","additionalDifferentials":["Functional causes of flushing and diarrhoea, including mastocytosis and medullary thyroid carcinoma","Non-neuroendocrine primary tumours of the same organ","Adenocarcinoma with neuroendocrine differentiation","Pheochromocytoma or paraganglioma for catecholamine-related presentations","Irritable bowel syndrome or other benign causes of chronic diarrhoea","Hepatic metastases from other primary malignancies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Histological grading with Ki-67 index and mitotic count to determine differentiation","Serum chromogranin A and, for suspected carcinoid, urinary 5-HIAA","Somatostatin receptor PET imaging for localisation and staging","Cross-sectional CT or MRI to assess primary tumour and metastatic burden","Specific hormone assays and evaluation for hereditary syndromes such as MEN when indicated"],"redFlags":["Carcinoid crisis with severe flushing, haemodynamic instability, and bronchospasm, particularly around procedures","Rapidly progressive high-grade neuroendocrine carcinoma with widespread metastases","Severe hormonal effects such as refractory hypoglycaemia from an insulinoma","Carcinoid heart disease with right-sided valve involvement in metastatic midgut tumours"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies widely by grade, differentiation, site, and stage; well-differentiated low-grade tumours can be compatible with prolonged survival even with metastases, whereas poorly differentiated neuroendocrine carcinomas have an aggressive course and poor outcome.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal-appearing indolent tumour is benign and underestimating metastatic potential","Failing to prevent carcinoid crisis with somatostatin analogue cover before surgery or interventions","Managing high-grade neuroendocrine carcinoma as if it were an indolent tumour","Overlooking an associated hereditary syndrome that alters surveillance and family screening"],"management":["Resect localised disease surgically, which offers the only curative option for well-differentiated tumours","Control hormonal syndromes and inhibit tumour growth with somatostatin analogues (octreotide or lanreotide), the mainstay for functioning and advanced well-differentiated tumours","Treat carcinoid syndrome symptoms, add telotristat for refractory diarrhoea, and give perioperative somatostatin analogue cover to prevent carcinoid crisis","Use peptide receptor radionuclide therapy with a lutetium-labelled somatostatin analogue for progressive somatostatin-receptor-positive disease","Employ targeted agents such as everolimus or sunitinib, and platinum-based chemotherapy for poorly differentiated neuroendocrine carcinoma","Reduce hepatic metastatic burden with liver-directed therapy such as transarterial embolisation or ablation","Monitor with chromogranin A, urinary 5-HIAA and somatostatin-receptor imaging, and screen for hereditary syndromes such as multiple endocrine neoplasia with genetic counselling","Coordinate care through a specialist neuroendocrine tumour multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuroferritinopathy":{"name":"Neuroferritinopathy","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FTL","ferritin light chain disease","hereditary ferritinopathy","NBIA-related ferritin disorder","adult-onset basal ganglia disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Movement-disorder and neurogenetics textbooks with peer-reviewed reviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant NBIA subtype caused by variants (typically insertions) in FTL, encoding the ferritin light chain","One of the few dominantly inherited NBIA disorders, usually presenting in adulthood","Phenotype is dominated by an extrapyramidal movement disorder: chorea, dystonia (often orofacial or action-specific), and parkinsonism, frequently asymmetrical at onset","Characteristic orolingual and facial dystonia may cause dysarthria and can resemble Huntington disease","Cognition is relatively preserved until late, in contrast to many other neurodegenerative movement disorders","Serum ferritin may be low or low-normal, particularly in affected males, providing a useful clue","MRI with iron-sensitive sequences shows iron in the basal ganglia and dentate nuclei and may reveal cystic cavitation of the putamen and globus pallidus in advanced disease","Diagnosis is confirmed by FTL molecular testing in the context of the imaging and family history; management is symptomatic"],"pathophysiology":"Neuroferritinopathy is caused by dominant variants in FTL, the gene for the ferritin light chain, most commonly small insertions that disrupt the carboxy-terminus of the protein. The abnormal ferritin has impaired capacity to sequester iron safely, so iron and abnormal ferritin aggregates accumulate within neurons and glia, particularly in the basal ganglia and cerebellar dentate nuclei. The resulting iron-mediated oxidative stress and inclusion formation drive progressive neurodegeneration, and advanced disease may lead to tissue cavitation within the affected deep grey-matter structures.","additionalDifferentials":["Huntington disease","Other NBIA subtypes (PKAN, aceruloplasminaemia)","Wilson disease","Idiopathic torsion dystonia and other adult-onset dystonias","Young-onset Parkinson disease and atypical parkinsonism","Benign hereditary chorea and other choreiform syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI with iron-sensitive sequences to demonstrate basal ganglia and dentate iron and any putaminal/pallidal cavitation","FTL molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Serum ferritin, which may be low, along with iron studies","Serum copper and caeruloplasmin to exclude Wilson disease and aceruloplasminaemia","Detailed family history and consideration of testing at-risk relatives given dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Severe or disabling generalised chorea or dystonia","Bulbar dysfunction with dysphagia and aspiration risk","Status dystonicus in advanced disease","Rapid functional decline or new significant cognitive or psychiatric change"],"prognosis":"The disorder is slowly progressive over years to decades, with worsening movement disorder and eventual loss of independence, although cognition is often comparatively spared. Prognosis and rate of progression vary between affected individuals and families.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing Huntington disease on clinical grounds without pursuing NBIA imaging and FTL testing when the huntingtin expansion is negative","Overlooking a low serum ferritin as a diagnostic clue","Failing to obtain iron-sensitive MRI sequences that reveal the deep grey-matter iron and cavitation","Not recognising the dominant inheritance and missing the opportunity for family counselling"],"management":["There is no proven disease-modifying therapy, so treatment is directed symptomatically at the extrapyramidal movement disorder","Treat chorea with a dopamine-depleting agent such as tetrabenazine or with a dopamine-receptor antagonist","Manage dystonia with agents such as anticholinergics, baclofen or clonazepam, and consider botulinum toxin for focal orolingual or facial dystonia","Offer a trial of levodopa for parkinsonian features, recognising the response is variable","Approach iron chelation cautiously as it is unproven and may worsen anaemia and neurological status","Provide speech and language therapy for dysarthria and swallowing, physiotherapy for mobility and occupational therapy support","Refer to neurology movement-disorder services and clinical genetics, offering genetic counselling and predictive testing for at-risk relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Neuroferritinopathy","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"neurofibromatosis":{"name":"Neurofibromatosis","system":"Genetic / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NF1","NF2","von Recklinghausen disease","neurofibromatosis type 1","neurofibromatosis type 2"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard medical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Neurofibromatosis type 1 is diagnosed clinically using criteria that include multiple cafe-au-lait macules, skinfold freckling, neurofibromas, Lisch nodules, optic pathway glioma, characteristic bony lesions, and an affected first-degree relative","Cafe-au-lait macules and axillary or inguinal freckling are usually the earliest features in NF1","Optic pathway gliomas are a recognised childhood complication and warrant ophthalmological and imaging surveillance","Plexiform neurofibromas can be disfiguring and carry a risk of malignant transformation","Neurofibromatosis type 2 is distinct, typically presenting with bilateral vestibular schwannomas causing hearing loss, tinnitus, and balance problems","NF1 is caused by mutations in the NF1 gene encoding neurofibromin, a tumour suppressor regulating RAS signalling; NF2 involves the NF2 gene encoding merlin","Both are autosomal dominant with high penetrance, variable expressivity, and a high de novo mutation rate","Learning difficulties, hypertension, and skeletal complications such as scoliosis are common in NF1"],"pathophysiology":"Neurofibromatosis type 1 is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the NF1 gene, which encodes neurofibromin, a GTPase-activating protein that downregulates RAS signalling; loss of neurofibromin increases RAS-pathway activity and predisposes to tumour formation in neural crest-derived tissues. Neurofibromatosis type 2 results from mutations in the NF2 gene encoding merlin (schwannomin), another tumour suppressor, leading characteristically to schwannomas and meningiomas. Both disorders are inherited in an autosomal dominant manner but frequently arise de novo, and tumour development typically follows a second somatic hit inactivating the remaining normal allele, consistent with the two-hit tumour suppressor model.","additionalDifferentials":["Legius syndrome (cafe-au-lait macules without neurofibromas)","McCune-Albright syndrome","Schwannomatosis","Constitutional mismatch repair deficiency","Multiple lipomatosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment against established diagnostic criteria, supported by NF1 or NF2 molecular testing when needed","Ophthalmological examination including slit-lamp for Lisch nodules and optic pathway assessment","Magnetic resonance imaging for suspected central or peripheral nervous system tumours","Blood pressure monitoring for hypertension including phaeochromocytoma and renal artery stenosis","Audiological evaluation and brain imaging for suspected NF2"],"redFlags":["New or rapidly enlarging, painful, or firm masses suggesting malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour and needing urgent evaluation","Visual changes indicating optic pathway glioma progression","Hypertension that may reflect phaeochromocytoma or renovascular disease","Progressive hearing loss or neurological signs from vestibular schwannomas or other tumours in NF2"],"prognosis":"Most people with NF1 have a good quality of life, but morbidity and reduced life expectancy relate to tumour complications, particularly malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumours and vascular disease; NF2 prognosis depends on the burden and location of nervous system tumours.","pitfalls":["Attributing multiple cafe-au-lait macules alone to NF1 without considering mimics such as Legius syndrome","Failing to institute malignancy surveillance for changing plexiform neurofibromas","Overlooking hypertension and its treatable secondary causes","Missing NF2 in a young adult with unilateral or bilateral hearing loss"],"management":["There is no cure, so care centres on structured lifelong surveillance for the many complications, coordinated by a specialist neurofibromatosis service","Monitor children with regular clinical review, blood pressure checks and ophthalmological and visual assessment for optic pathway glioma","Treat symptomatic or progressive inoperable plexiform neurofibromas with a MEK inhibitor (selumetinib) and refer for surgery where appropriate","Investigate and manage malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour promptly with oncological and surgical care when a plexiform lesion becomes painful or rapidly enlarging","Screen for and manage hypertension, including secondary causes such as phaeochromocytoma and renal artery stenosis","Address skeletal, learning and psychosocial complications with orthopaedic, educational and psychological support","For neurofibromatosis type 2, undertake audiological monitoring and MRI surveillance for vestibular schwannomas with hearing-preservation strategies","Offer genetic counselling and family assessment given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neurofibromatosis_type_2":{"name":"Neurofibromatosis type 2","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NF2","NF2-related schwannomatosis","merlin deficiency","central neurofibromatosis","bilateral vestibular schwannoma syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer: Principles and Practice of Oncology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The hallmark of the condition is bilateral vestibular schwannomas, which typically present with progressive sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus, and imbalance in adolescence or early adulthood","It is a distinct disorder from neurofibromatosis type 1: NF2 is dominated by schwannomas, meningiomas, and ependymomas rather than the neurofibromas, café-au-lait macules, and Lisch nodules of NF1","Additional tumours include cranial and spinal meningiomas, spinal and peripheral nerve schwannomas, and intramedullary or cauda equina ependymomas","A characteristic ophthalmological clue is juvenile posterior subcapsular or cortical cataract, which may predate tumour diagnosis","The disorder is caused by loss-of-function variants in the NF2 tumour suppressor gene, which encodes merlin (schwannomin); it is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern but a large proportion of cases are de novo and mosaicism is common","Management is delivered through specialist multidisciplinary teams, using serial MRI surveillance of the brain and spine and audiological monitoring","Treatment prioritises hearing preservation; the anti-VEGF agent bevacizumab can shrink vestibular schwannomas and improve hearing in selected cases, alongside microsurgery, stereotactic radiosurgery, and auditory implantation"],"pathophysiology":"The disorder results from inactivating germline variants in the NF2 gene on chromosome 22, which encodes merlin (also called schwannomin), a cytoskeletal linker protein that normally suppresses cell proliferation by regulating contact inhibition and several growth-signalling pathways. Loss of functional merlin, following a second somatic hit in Schwann cells, meningeal cells, and ependymal cells, removes this brake and predisposes to multiple nervous-system tumours. Inheritance is autosomal dominant with high penetrance, though many patients harbour de novo variants and somatic mosaicism, which influences severity and the recurrence risk offered during counselling.","additionalDifferentials":["Neurofibromatosis type 1","Schwannomatosis (SMARCB1/LZTR1) without vestibular tumours","Sporadic unilateral vestibular schwannoma","Multiple meningiomatosis without NF2","Von Hippel-Lindau disease (other inherited tumour predisposition)","Meningioma predisposition from prior cranial irradiation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced MRI of the brain (including internal auditory meati) and whole spine","Molecular genetic testing of the NF2 gene, with tumour testing where mosaicism is suspected","Pure-tone audiometry and speech discrimination testing with serial monitoring","Brainstem auditory evoked responses to assess auditory pathway function","Ophthalmological examination for juvenile cataract and retinal lesions","Baseline neurological and dermatological assessment for schwannomas and skin tumours"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive hearing loss or brainstem compression from an enlarging vestibular schwannoma","New or worsening myelopathy or radiculopathy from spinal tumours","Raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus from large posterior fossa lesions","Facial nerve dysfunction or lower cranial nerve palsies indicating local tumour progression"],"prognosis":"The condition is chronic and progressive, with morbidity driven by cumulative tumour burden, hearing loss, and the risks of surgery near critical neural structures. Early diagnosis, expert multidisciplinary surveillance, and hearing-preservation strategies have improved outcomes, but the disorder shortens life expectancy on average and disease is generally more aggressive when it presents in childhood.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the disorder as NF1 and expecting neurofibromas and café-au-lait macules rather than schwannomas and meningiomas","Treating a young person's vestibular schwannoma as sporadic and failing to image the contralateral side and spine","Overlooking mosaicism, which can give falsely reassuring blood genetic testing and complicate counselling","Delaying referral to a specialist centre, where hearing-preservation and multidisciplinary planning materially affect outcome"],"management":["Deliver care through a specialist multidisciplinary team with serial contrast-enhanced MRI surveillance of the brain and whole spine and regular audiological monitoring","Manage vestibular schwannomas with a hearing-preservation approach, choosing between observation, microsurgery and stereotactic radiosurgery according to tumour growth and hearing","Use bevacizumab to reduce vestibular schwannoma volume and stabilise or improve hearing in progressive disease","Treat meningiomas and ependymomas with surgery or radiosurgery as dictated by symptoms and growth","Support hearing and communication with hearing aids, and offer auditory brainstem or cochlear implantation when hearing is lost","Provide ophthalmological review for juvenile cataract and monitor for other nervous-system tumours","Offer genetic counselling, molecular testing and family screening, accounting for the high rate of de novo variants and mosaicism"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (University of Washington)","DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer: Principles and Practice of Oncology"],"crossLinks":[]},"neurogenic_bladder":{"name":"Neurogenic bladder","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neuropathic bladder","neurogenic lower urinary tract dysfunction","NLUTD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Neurogenic bladder describes bladder dysfunction due to disease or injury of the nervous system, with the pattern depending on the level of the neurological lesion","Suprapontine and suprasacral (upper motor neuron) lesions typically cause a spastic, overactive detrusor, often with detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia, whereas sacral or peripheral (lower motor neuron) lesions cause a flaccid, areflexic, poorly emptying bladder","Common causes include spinal cord injury, multiple sclerosis, spina bifida, diabetes mellitus, Parkinson's disease, stroke, and cauda equina lesions","The paramount goal of management is protection of the upper urinary tracts, because high storage pressures and detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia can cause hydronephrosis and renal failure","Video-urodynamics is central to characterising bladder behaviour, storage pressures, and sphincter coordination to guide safe management","Clean intermittent catheterisation is a cornerstone for bladders that do not empty adequately, reducing residual urine and infection risk while preserving the upper tracts","Management principles combine measures to lower storage pressure and improve emptying, such as antimuscarinics, intradetrusor botulinum toxin, catheterisation regimens, and, in selected cases, augmentation or diversion surgery","Patients are at risk of recurrent urinary infection, stones, and, in high spinal cord injuries, autonomic dysreflexia triggered by bladder distension"],"pathophysiology":"Normal micturition depends on coordinated control between the pontine micturition centre, spinal pathways, and sacral (S2-S4) reflexes governing the detrusor and urethral sphincter. Neurological lesions disrupt this coordination in patterns determined by lesion level. Upper motor neuron lesions above the sacral cord release the sacral reflex from higher inhibition, producing detrusor overactivity and often detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia, in which the sphincter contracts against a contracting bladder, generating dangerously high pressures. Lower motor neuron or peripheral lesions abolish the reflex arc, producing an acontractile, flaccid bladder with retention and overflow. Sustained high intravesical pressure and incomplete emptying threaten the upper tracts and predispose to infection and stones.","additionalDifferentials":["Detrusor overactivity from non-neurogenic causes (idiopathic overactive bladder)","Bladder outlet obstruction (BPH, stricture)","Diabetic cystopathy","Cauda equina syndrome","Multiple sclerosis or other demyelinating disease","Spinal cord injury or compression","Pelvic surgery-related denervation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Video-urodynamic studies to assess detrusor pressure, compliance, and sphincter coordination","Renal function tests and upper tract imaging (ultrasound) to monitor for hydronephrosis","Post-void residual measurement","Urinalysis and culture for infection surveillance","Neurological evaluation and imaging directed at the underlying condition","Periodic surveillance for bladder stones and, where relevant, malignancy in long-term catheterised patients"],"redFlags":["High detrusor storage pressures or detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia threatening the upper tracts and renal function","New or progressive hydronephrosis and rising creatinine","Autonomic dysreflexia in high spinal cord injury, a potentially life-threatening emergency triggered by bladder overdistension","Urosepsis in an obstructed or poorly draining neurogenic bladder","Acute cauda equina syndrome presenting with retention and neurological deficit"],"prognosis":"With structured management focused on low-pressure storage, adequate emptying, and regular upper tract surveillance, many patients maintain renal function and continence; neglected high-pressure bladders carry a significant risk of progressive renal impairment and recurrent complications.","pitfalls":["Focusing on continence while neglecting upper tract protection and storage pressures","Failing to recognise and treat detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia","Not anticipating autonomic dysreflexia during bladder distension or instrumentation in high spinal injuries","Inadequate surveillance for renal deterioration, stones, and infection","Treating overactivity with antimuscarinics without ensuring adequate emptying"],"management":["The overriding goal is protection of the upper urinary tracts by ensuring low-pressure storage and adequate bladder emptying, guided by video-urodynamics","Manage the poorly emptying or high-residual bladder with clean intermittent catheterisation as the cornerstone of drainage","Reduce detrusor overactivity and storage pressures with antimuscarinic agents or a beta-3 adrenoceptor agonist (mirabegron), and use intradetrusor botulinum toxin for refractory overactivity","Treat outlet and detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia problems with alpha-blockers or botulinum toxin to the sphincter as appropriate","Consider surgical options such as augmentation cystoplasty, sacral neuromodulation or urinary diversion when conservative measures fail","Undertake regular surveillance with renal function tests, upper tract ultrasound and post-void residual measurement, and manage urinary infections judiciously","Treat the underlying neurological condition and coordinate urology and rehabilitation care, with long-term monitoring for bladder stones and malignancy in catheterised patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuroimaging_in_neurologic_disorders":{"name":"Neuroimaging in Neurologic Disorders","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Neuroimaging","CT and MRI in neurology","Brain and spine imaging"],"chapter":434,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3383","clinicalPearls":["MRI is generally more sensitive than CT for lesions of the central and peripheral nervous system, whereas CT is faster and the pragmatic first choice for uncooperative patients and for suspected acute stroke, hemorrhage, or acute intracranial/spinal trauma (Harrison 22e p.3383).","Diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI) is the single most sensitive technique for detecting acute cerebral or spinal cord ischemia, with restricted diffusion (high DWI signal) appearing in infarction of less than about 7 days; it also lights up encephalitis, abscess, and Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (Harrison 22e p.3383, 3389).","CT is superior to MRI for fine osseous detail and is preferred for the initial workup of conductive hearing loss and skull/spine bone lesions, while MRI better detects bone-marrow infiltrative processes that CT may miss (Harrison 22e p.3383).","On unenhanced CT, denser structures such as bone or acute blood appear bright (high attenuation), whereas water, air, and soft tissue appear dark; this is why acute hemorrhage and bone are well seen on noncontrast CT (Harrison 22e p.3383-3384).","Iodinated contrast highlights vascular structures and blood-brain barrier defects; in the normal CNS only vessels and structures lacking a blood-brain barrier (pituitary, choroid plexus, dura) enhance (Harrison 22e p.3384).","Susceptibility-weighted imaging (SWI) is highly sensitive to blood, calcium, and air and is the sequence of choice for microhemorrhages seen in amyloid angiopathy, hypertension, hemorrhagic metastases, traumatic brain injury, and thrombotic states (Harrison 22e p.3387-3388).","FLAIR suppresses the bright CSF signal of T2 imaging, improving detection of edematous/water-containing lesions adjacent to CSF spaces such as periventricular plaques and cortical lesions (Harrison 22e p.3387).","Gadolinium-based contrast does not cause contrast-induced nephropathy, but linear (group 1/3) agents can cause nephrogenic systemic fibrosis in patients with severe renal impairment; macrocyclic (group 2) agents are far safer and preferred in at-risk patients (Harrison 22e p.3388-3389).","Perfusion imaging that shows a region of prolonged mean transit time with preserved cerebral blood volume identifies tissue at risk (ischemic penumbra) that may be salvageable, distinct from infarcted core where blood volume and flow are reduced (Harrison 22e p.3390).","CTA and gadolinium-enhanced MRA have largely replaced catheter angiography, which is now reserved for cases needing fine small-vessel detail or planned interventional therapy (Harrison 22e p.3383).","Vessel wall MR imaging is a high-resolution technique that interrogates the arterial wall itself and helps distinguish atherosclerosis, primary CNS vasculitis, and reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3390).","Amyloid PET can be positive in up to about 25% of cognitively normal older adults, so a positive scan does not by itself diagnose Alzheimer disease; tau PET appears more specific (Harrison 22e p.3392)."],"pathophysiology":"Neuroimaging techniques exploit differing physical tissue properties. CT reconstructs a cross-sectional map of x-ray attenuation: dense tissue (bone, acute clot) attenuates heavily and appears bright, while water and air appear dark. MRI maps the distribution and relaxation behavior of (mainly hydrogen) protons placed in a strong static magnetic field and excited by radiofrequency pulses; T1 relaxation is the time for ~63% of protons to return toward equilibrium and T2 the time for ~63% to dephase. Manipulating pulse timing (TR and TE) yields T1-weighted images (fat and subacute hemorrhage bright; CSF/edema dark) or T2-weighted images (CSF/edema/demyelination/infarct bright). Gray matter contains roughly 10-15% more water than white matter, providing intrinsic contrast. Pathology such as infarction, abscess, and dense tumor restricts microscopic water motion, producing high DWI signal. Iodinated and gadolinium contrast agents reveal blood-brain barrier breakdown by tumor, infarct, or infection. Flow phenomena underlie MR angiography, and regional changes in oxy/deoxyhemoglobin underlie the BOLD signal of functional MRI (Harrison 22e p.3383-3392).","additionalDifferentials":["Ring-enhancing lesion: cerebral abscess versus high-grade glioma or metastasis (restricted central diffusion favors abscess)","Restricted diffusion in medial temporal lobe: herpes simplex encephalitis versus acute infarction versus other limbic inflammatory processes","Multiple low-signal SWI foci: cerebral amyloid angiopathy versus chronic hypertensive microbleeds versus hemorrhagic metastases versus diffuse axonal injury","Focal arterial narrowing/wall enhancement: atherosclerosis versus primary CNS vasculitis versus reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Noncontrast CT for acute hemorrhage, trauma, and acute mental-status change","MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging for suspected ischemia, encephalitis, or abscess","CT angiography or gadolinium-enhanced/time-of-flight MR angiography for vascular assessment","Vessel wall MR imaging to characterize arteriopathies and vasculitis","Perfusion CT or perfusion MRI/ASL to assess core versus penumbra and tumor vascularity","CT myelography for failed-back syndrome, CSF fistula localization, and meningeal/arachnoid cysts","FDG-PET and amyloid/tau PET ligands for oncologic staging and neurodegenerative evaluation","Estimated GFR before contrast in at-risk patients"],"redFlags":["Acute change in mental status, new focal deficit, or head/spine trauma warranting urgent noncontrast CT","Suspected subarachnoid hemorrhage (CT then CTA, with lumbar puncture if CT negative)","Severe renal impairment (eGFR <30 mL/min per 1.73 m2) or dialysis raising concern for nephrogenic systemic fibrosis with linear gadolinium agents","Prior severe contrast (anaphylactoid) reaction, with eightfold higher recurrence risk for gadolinium","Ferromagnetic implants (some aneurysm clips, non-conditional pacemakers/defibrillators) or ocular metallic foreign body before MRI","Suspected spinal block or elevated intracranial pressure before myelography/lumbar puncture"],"prognosis":"As a diagnostic modality, neuroimaging itself is generally safe. Contrast-associated acute kidney injury from iodinated contrast is rare, usually transient with creatinine returning toward baseline within 1-2 weeks, and most strongly associated with preexisting severe renal insufficiency; gadolinium does not cause it. Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis from linear gadolinium agents in severe renal failure is rare (reported incidence ~0.19-4% in GFR <30) but can be severely disabling with multiorgan fibrosis. Severe allergic reactions are uncommon (~0.04% with nonionic iodinated agents; gadolinium severe reactions exceedingly rare). Radiation-induced malignancy from diagnostic CT is felt to be minimal but is dose-dependent (Harrison 22e p.3384-3389).","pitfalls":["Reliance on shellfish or iodine allergy to predict contrast reactions is unreliable; the strongest predictor is a prior contrast reaction (about fivefold risk), plus atopy/asthma and other drug/food allergies (Harrison 22e p.3385).","Premedication regimens shorter than 4-5 hours have no demonstrated efficacy and do not guarantee prevention of contrast reactions (Harrison 22e p.3387).","Bicarbonate and acetylcysteine have not shown protection over normal saline against contrast-associated AKI in recent meta-analyses (Harrison 22e p.3385).","MRA has lower spatial resolution than catheter angiography, is insensitive to slow flow, and may not distinguish near-complete from complete occlusion or detect small-vessel vasculitis/distal vasospasm (Harrison 22e p.3390).","Brain gadolinium deposition (dentate nuclei, globus pallidus) occurs even with normal renal/hepatic function, is dose-dependent and greater with linear agents, but to date has no proven neurotoxic histologic correlate (Harrison 22e p.3387-3388).","The MR magnet is always on; ferromagnetic missile injuries and torqued implants can be fatal, so rigorous screening of patients and staff is mandatory (Harrison 22e p.3389).","Patient motion degrades MRI; uncooperative patients should be sedated or imaged with CT, and children under ~8 years often require monitored anesthesia (Harrison 22e p.3389).","Iodinated contrast should be avoided before planned radioactive iodine thyroid therapy because it reduces radioisotope uptake (Harrison 22e p.3387)."],"management":["Select noncontrast CT as the rapid first-line study for suspected acute haemorrhage, trauma or acute change in consciousness","Use MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging as the most sensitive test for acute ischaemia and for suspected encephalitis, abscess or demyelination","Choose CT or MR angiography and venography to assess arterial occlusion, aneurysm or cerebral venous thrombosis, and perfusion imaging to distinguish infarct core from salvageable penumbra","Screen for MRI safety contraindications such as implanted devices or metallic foreign bodies before scanning","Minimise contrast-associated risk by assessing renal function before iodinated or gadolinium contrast, hydrating at-risk patients, and avoiding less stable gadolinium agents in severe renal impairment to prevent nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Limit ionising radiation through justified requesting and dose optimisation, particularly in children and pregnancy","Correlate imaging with the clinical question and involve neuroradiology to guide sequence selection and interpretation"],"severityClassification":"Contrast-associated acute kidney injury is defined per KDIGO criteria within 48 h of contrast: absolute serum creatinine rise >=0.3 mg/dL (>26.4 micromol/L), a >=50% (>=1.5-fold) increase over baseline, or urine output <=0.5 mL/kg per h for at least 6 h. Renal-risk stratification for contrast uses eGFR, with <30 mL/min per 1.73 m2 carrying the greatest evidence for risk (normal >=90 mL/min per 1.73 m2) (Harrison 22e p.3384-3386).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3383","Harrison 22e p.3384","Harrison 22e p.3385","Harrison 22e p.3386","Harrison 22e p.3387","Harrison 22e p.3388","Harrison 22e p.3389","Harrison 22e p.3390","Harrison 22e p.3391","Harrison 22e p.3392","Harrison 22e p.3393"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuroleptic_malignant_syndrome":{"name":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","system":"Clinical pharmacology / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NMS","antipsychotic malignant syndrome","neuroleptic-induced hyperthermia","dopamine antagonist malignant syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A life-threatening idiosyncratic reaction to dopamine antagonists, particularly antipsychotics, or to abrupt withdrawal of dopaminergic therapy in Parkinson's disease","The cardinal features are hyperthermia, generalised lead-pipe muscular rigidity, altered mental status and autonomic instability","Creatine kinase is characteristically markedly elevated, reflecting muscle injury, and rhabdomyolysis with renal failure may follow","Onset is typically slow, over days, in contrast to the rapid onset of serotonin syndrome","Reflexes are usually normal or reduced (hyporeflexia) with rigidity, unlike the hyperreflexia and clonus of serotonin syndrome","Risk factors include high-potency agents, rapid dose escalation, parenteral administration, dehydration and agitation","Management is immediate withdrawal of the causative drug (or reinstatement of dopaminergic therapy if withdrawal-related), aggressive cooling, fluids and supportive care","Specific pharmacological treatments include dantrolene to reduce muscle rigidity and dopamine agonists such as bromocriptine"],"pathophysiology":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is thought to result from acute central dopamine deficiency caused by blockade of D2 dopamine receptors (or by sudden loss of dopaminergic stimulation when Parkinson's medication is stopped). Reduced dopaminergic activity in the hypothalamus impairs thermoregulation, producing hyperthermia, while blockade in the basal ganglia and related motor pathways causes the severe generalised rigidity. The sustained muscle contraction generates heat and causes muscle breakdown with a very high creatine kinase, risking rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia and acute kidney injury. Loss of central autonomic regulation produces labile blood pressure, tachycardia, diaphoresis and fluctuating consciousness. The slow evolution and the pattern of rigidity with hyporeflexia help distinguish it from serotonin syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Serotonin syndrome","Malignant hyperthermia","Central nervous system infection (meningitis, encephalitis)","Lethal (malignant) catatonia","Heat stroke","Anticholinergic or sympathomimetic toxicity","Status epilepticus or non-convulsive seizures"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Creatine kinase (typically markedly raised)","Renal function and electrolytes, including potassium","Core temperature monitoring","Full blood count and inflammatory markers to help exclude infection","Coagulation profile for disseminated intravascular coagulation","Neuroimaging and lumbar puncture where central nervous system infection is possible"],"redFlags":["Severe hyperthermia","Rhabdomyolysis with rising creatine kinase and acute kidney injury","Autonomic instability with labile blood pressure or arrhythmia","Progressive decline in consciousness","Respiratory compromise from chest wall rigidity"],"prognosis":"With prompt drug withdrawal and intensive supportive care most patients recover over one to two weeks, but the condition can be fatal from hyperthermia, renal failure or cardiorespiratory complications. Cautious, delayed reintroduction of antipsychotics, preferably a lower-potency agent, is needed if treatment must resume.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with serotonin syndrome and missing the distinguishing slow onset and hyporeflexia","Failing to consider it after abrupt withdrawal of Parkinson's medication","Overlooking antiemetic dopamine antagonists as a cause","Restarting the causative antipsychotic too soon after recovery","Attributing hyperthermia and rigidity to infection alone"],"management":["Immediately withdraw the offending dopamine antagonist, or reinstate dopaminergic therapy where it was precipitated by abrupt withdrawal","Provide aggressive supportive care with active cooling for hyperthermia, intravenous fluid resuscitation and correction of electrolyte disturbance in a high-dependency or intensive care setting","Prevent and treat rhabdomyolysis-related acute kidney injury with generous fluids and urinary alkalinisation while monitoring potassium","Reduce rigidity and hypermetabolism with dantrolene, and reverse the dopaminergic blockade with a dopamine agonist such as bromocriptine","Use benzodiazepines for agitation and, in refractory or catatonic cases, electroconvulsive therapy","Monitor creatine kinase, renal function, coagulation and core temperature, watching for disseminated intravascular coagulation and respiratory failure","Reintroduce antipsychotics only cautiously after recovery, choosing a lower-potency agent and titrating slowly, with psychiatry and critical care involvement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuromyelitis_optica":{"name":"Neuromyelitis Optica","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NMO","NMOSD","Neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder","Devic's disease","Devic disease"],"chapter":456,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3590","clinicalPearls":["NMO is an aggressive antibody-mediated inflammatory CNS disorder defined by recurrent attacks of optic neuritis and myelitis; the broader term NMOSD captures partial forms and cases with additional CNS involvement (Harrison p.3590).","Strongly female-predominant (women:men roughly 9:1), with a mean onset around age 40 but possible at any age (Harrison p.3590).","Optic neuritis in NMO can be bilateral and cause severe vision loss, a pattern uncommon in MS; myelitis is often severe, transverse, and longitudinally extensive spanning three or more contiguous vertebral segments (Harrison p.3590).","Unlike MS, NMO rarely follows a progressive course; accumulated disability results from attack-related injury rather than progression (Harrison p.3590).","Brain MRI is not invariably normal: lesions may involve the area postrema (intractable hiccups or vomiting), hypothalamus (sleep disorder or endocrinopathy), or cerebral hemispheres; large hemispheric lesions can be asymptomatic, show a 'cloud-like' appearance, be non-destructive, and resolve completely (Harrison p.3590).","Spinal cord lesions are focal enhancing areas of swelling and tissue destruction over three or more segments and are centered on the gray matter on axial imaging (Harrison p.3590).","CSF typically shows greater pleocytosis than MS, sometimes with neutrophils and eosinophils in acute cases, while oligoclonal bands are uncommon (present in fewer than 20% of patients) (Harrison p.3590-3591).","A highly specific serum autoantibody against the water channel aquaporin-4 (AQP4) is present in about 90% of patients and is diagnostic when paired with a typical clinical presentation (Harrison p.3590).","In a subset of AQP4-negative cases, autoantibodies against myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein (MOG) are found (Harrison p.3591).","Up to 40% of NMO patients have a coexisting systemic autoimmune disorder (e.g., SLE, Sjögren's syndrome, p-ANCA vasculitis, myasthenia gravis, Hashimoto's thyroiditis, mixed connective tissue disease) - a feature distinguishing it from MS (Harrison p.3591).","Symptomatic onset may follow acute infection with varicella-zoster virus, Epstein-Barr virus, HIV, or tuberculosis; rare cases are paraneoplastic (breast, lung, or other cancers) (Harrison p.3592).","Asian and African ancestry populations are disproportionately affected, with the highest reported prevalence in Martinique; among white populations MS is far more common than NMO (Harrison p.3591)."],"pathophysiology":"NMO is an autoimmune astrocytopathy driven by autoantibodies against the water channel aquaporin-4 (AQP4), which is concentrated on astrocyte foot processes apposed to endothelium and at paranodal regions near nodes of Ranvier. Pathology shows loss of AQP4 staining with antibody and complement deposition, astrocyte cytolysis, thickened vessel walls, demyelination, and inflammation. AQP4 antibodies are likely pathogenic, since passive transfer reproduces disease features in animals; both complement fixation and antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity contribute to astrocyte injury, and TH17 proinflammatory T cells recognizing an immunodominant AQP4 epitope may also participate. A thymic B-cell population that constitutively expresses AQP4 normally interacts with AQP4-specific T cells to maintain tolerance, and impairment of this interaction may contribute. During acute myelitis attacks, CSF levels of IL-6 and glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP) are markedly elevated, reflecting active inflammation and astrocyte injury (Harrison p.3590-3591).","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple sclerosis (especially opticospinal MS subtype in Asian/African ancestry, which can itself be AQP4-seropositive and represent NMOSD)","MOG antibody-associated disease (AQP4-negative cases)","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Idiopathic or postinfectious transverse myelitis","Neurosarcoidosis","Systemic autoimmune myelopathy (SLE, Sjögren's syndrome, Behcet's disease)","Spinal cord infarction (longitudinally extensive anterior cord syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral myelitis (e.g., herpes simplex virus type 2 causing recurrent myelitis)","Tuberculous CNS involvement","HIV-associated myelopathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Multiple sclerosis","MOG antibody-associated disease","Neurosarcoidosis","Systemic lupus erythematosus / Sjögren's-associated myelopathy","Paraneoplastic optic neuropathy/myelopathy","Spinal cord infarction"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum AQP4-IgG testing using a cell-based assay (strongly recommended detection method)","MOG-IgG testing in AQP4-seronegative patients","Spinal cord MRI to demonstrate longitudinally extensive (>=3 segment) gray matter-centered lesions","Brain MRI to detect area postrema, periependymal brainstem, diencephalic, or cloud-like hemispheric lesions","Optic nerve MRI (T2/gadolinium-enhancing lesion over >half optic nerve length or chiasm)","Lumbar puncture for CSF pleocytosis, cell differential, and oligoclonal bands","Screening for coexisting systemic autoimmune disease"],"redFlags":["Bilateral simultaneous optic neuritis with severe vision loss","Longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis spanning three or more vertebral segments","Intractable hiccups, nausea, or vomiting (area postrema syndrome)","Cervical myelitis threatening respiratory failure","Acute brainstem or diencephalic syndrome (e.g., symptomatic narcolepsy, hypothalamic dysfunction)","Recurrent attacks, since more than half of untreated AQP4-seropositive patients relapse within 1 year"],"prognosis":"NMO is typically a relapsing disease; a monophasic course occurs in fewer than 10% of patients and is somewhat more likely in AQP4-antibody-negative individuals. Untreated disease is severely disabling: historically about one-third of patients developed respiratory failure from cervical myelitis, and by 8 years roughly 60% were blind and more than half had permanent limb paralysis. AQP4-seropositive patients carry a high relapse risk (more than half relapse within 1 year if untreated). Long-term outcomes have substantially improved with the advent of attack and relapse therapies, with reported 5-year survival rising from about 68-75% around 1999 to 91-98% by 2017 (Harrison p.3590-3591).","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal brain MRI excludes NMO - characteristic area postrema, periependymal brainstem, diencephalic, and cloud-like hemispheric lesions can be present (Harrison p.3590).","Conflating NMO with MS: bilateral severe optic neuritis, longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis, marked CSF pleocytosis, and frequent absence of oligoclonal bands favor NMO (Harrison p.3590).","Relying on oligoclonal bands, which are present in fewer than 20% of NMO patients (Harrison p.3590-3591).","Overlooking that NMO frequently coexists with systemic autoimmune disease, unlike MS (Harrison p.3591).","Failing to recognize AQP4-negative disease, where MOG antibodies may be present and where seronegative NMOSD requires both optic neuritis and longitudinally extensive myelitis to meet criteria (Harrison p.3591-3592)."],"management":["Treat acute attacks promptly with high-dose intravenous corticosteroids to limit attack-related injury","Use plasma exchange for severe or steroid-refractory attacks, which is critical because attacks drive permanent disability","Prevent relapses with long-term immunotherapy such as rituximab or an approved monoclonal agent targeting complement (eculizumab), the interleukin-6 receptor (satralizumab) or CD19 (inebilizumab)","Use oral immunosuppression with azathioprine or mycophenolate mofetil under corticosteroid cover where biologics are unavailable","Avoid multiple sclerosis disease-modifying drugs such as interferon-beta, natalizumab and fingolimod, which can worsen the disorder","Provide rehabilitation and symptomatic care for residual visual loss, spasticity, neuropathic pain and bladder dysfunction, with vigilance for respiratory compromise from cervical myelitis","Confirm the diagnosis with an AQP4-IgG cell-based assay and manage through a neuro-immunology service with long-term relapse monitoring"],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grading; diagnosis follows the 2015 International Consensus Diagnostic Criteria for NMOSD, which stratify by AQP4-IgG status. AQP4-IgG-positive disease requires at least one core clinical characteristic, a positive AQP4-IgG test, and exclusion of alternatives. AQP4-IgG-negative or unknown-status disease requires at least two core characteristics (one of which must be optic neuritis, acute myelitis with longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis, or area postrema syndrome), dissemination in space, fulfillment of additional MRI requirements, a negative or unavailable AQP4-IgG test, and exclusion of alternatives. Core clinical characteristics: optic neuritis, acute myelitis, area postrema syndrome, acute brainstem syndrome, symptomatic narcolepsy/acute diencephalic syndrome with typical MRI lesions, and symptomatic cerebral syndrome with typical brain lesions (Harrison p.3591, Table 456-1).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3590","Harrison 22e p.3591","Harrison 22e p.3592","Harrison 22e p.3565"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuromyelitis_optica_spectrum_disorder":{"name":"Neuromyelitis Optica Spectrum Disorder","system":"Neurology / Neuro-immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NMOSD","Devic disease","AQP4 antibody disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An astrocytopathy driven by antibodies to the aquaporin-4 water channel, distinct from multiple sclerosis in both mechanism and treatment.","The clinical hallmarks are severe optic neuritis (often bilateral or sequential) and longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis spanning three or more vertebral segments.","Area postrema syndrome — intractable hiccups, nausea and vomiting from a dorsal medullary lesion — is a highly characteristic presenting feature.","Attacks tend to be far more disabling than in multiple sclerosis and recovery is frequently incomplete, so aggressive acute and preventive treatment is essential.","Serum AQP4-IgG testing by cell-based assay is the key diagnostic test; a seronegative subset exists and some of these patients carry MOG antibodies instead.","Multiple sclerosis disease-modifying agents such as interferon-beta, natalizumab and fingolimod can worsen the disorder and must be avoided.","Preventive immunotherapy is started early and continued long term because relapses accumulate irreversible deficit; approved biologics target complement, the interleukin-6 receptor or CD19/CD20 B cells.","Other syndromes include brainstem, diencephalic and symptomatic narcolepsy-like presentations reflecting the periventricular distribution of aquaporin-4."],"pathophysiology":"Neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder is an autoimmune astrocytopathy in which pathogenic immunoglobulin G autoantibodies bind aquaporin-4, the dominant water channel densely expressed on astrocytic foot processes at the blood-brain barrier, in the periependymal regions, optic nerves and spinal cord grey matter. Antibody binding activates the classical complement cascade, producing astrocyte injury and deposition of complement products, followed by an inflammatory infiltrate rich in neutrophils, eosinophils and macrophages that secondarily damages oligodendrocytes and neurones and produces the characteristic necrotic, cavitating lesions. The resulting loss of astrocytic support disrupts glutamate handling and water homeostasis, and the predilection of the disease for the optic nerves, area postrema, hypothalamus and central spinal cord mirrors sites of high aquaporin-4 density and relative blood-brain barrier permeability. Unlike multiple sclerosis, demyelination is a downstream consequence rather than the primary event, and the humoral, complement-dependent mechanism explains both the severity of attacks and the rationale for complement-directed and B-cell-directed therapies. A minority of patients are AQP4-seronegative, and interleukin-6 signalling is implicated in plasmablast survival and antibody production, underpinning interleukin-6 receptor blockade as a treatment strategy.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple sclerosis","Myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein antibody-associated disease","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Spinal cord infarction or dural arteriovenous fistula","Neurosarcoidosis","Systemic lupus erythematosus or Sjogren-associated myelopathy","Subacute combined degeneration from vitamin B12 deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum AQP4-IgG by cell-based assay (preferred over tissue-based or ELISA methods)","Serum MOG-IgG cell-based assay in AQP4-seronegative cases","MRI of the spine showing a longitudinally extensive central cord lesion","MRI brain and orbits with attention to periependymal, area postrema and optic nerve lesions","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis (often pleocytosis with neutrophils; oligoclonal bands usually absent)","Optical coherence tomography and visual evoked potentials to quantify optic nerve involvement","Screening for coexisting autoimmune disease including antinuclear and anti-Ro/anti-La antibodies"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive visual loss, particularly if bilateral or with poor initial acuity","Ascending sensorimotor level with sphincter involvement suggesting extensive myelitis","Respiratory compromise from a high cervical or brainstem lesion","Intractable hiccups or vomiting heralding an area postrema attack","Reduced conscious level or autonomic instability from diencephalic or brainstem disease"],"prognosis":"The disorder is typically relapsing and each attack risks permanent visual loss or paraplegia, so untreated disease carries a poor outlook, but early sustained immunotherapy with modern biologics substantially reduces relapse frequency and disability accrual. Prognosis depends heavily on prompt attack treatment and adherence to long-term prevention.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the disorder as multiple sclerosis and starting agents that provoke severe relapses","Relying on oligoclonal bands, which are usually negative and do not exclude the diagnosis","Assuming a single negative AQP4 assay excludes disease; repeat testing and MOG testing may be needed","Underestimating attack severity and delaying escalation to plasma exchange when steroids fail","Overlooking area postrema syndrome and attributing intractable vomiting to a gastrointestinal cause"],"management":["Treat acute relapses urgently with high-dose intravenous corticosteroids, escalating early to plasma exchange for severe or steroid-unresponsive attacks","Establish long-term relapse prevention with an approved biologic such as eculizumab (complement), satralizumab (interleukin-6 receptor) or inebilizumab (CD19), or with rituximab","Use azathioprine or mycophenolate mofetil under corticosteroid cover as maintenance where monoclonal therapies are not accessible","Avoid multiple sclerosis disease-modifying agents such as interferon-beta, natalizumab and fingolimod, which can precipitate deterioration","Confirm the diagnosis by serum AQP4-IgG cell-based assay, testing for MOG-IgG in seronegative cases, to direct appropriate immunotherapy","Provide symptomatic and rehabilitative care for visual impairment, paraplegia, neuropathic pain, spasticity and bladder dysfunction, and manage area postrema symptoms","Coordinate long-term care through a specialist neuro-immunology multidisciplinary team with monitoring for relapse and immunosuppression-related infection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","International consensus diagnostic criteria for neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorders","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuronal_ceroid_lipofuscinosis":{"name":"Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses (Batten disease group)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NCL","Batten disease","CLN disorders","ceroid lipofuscinosis","Jansky-Bielschowsky and Spielmeyer-Vogt disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses are a group of inherited, mostly autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disorders characterised by accumulation of autofluorescent lipopigment (ceroid and lipofuscin) in neurones and other cells","The classical phenotype combines progressive dementia, seizures, myoclonus, motor decline, and (in most childhood forms) visual failure from retinal degeneration","Subtypes are classified by causative gene (CLN1-CLN14) and traditionally by age at onset into infantile, late-infantile, juvenile, and adult forms","Age at onset guides the differential: for example CLN1 often infantile, CLN2 late-infantile, and CLN3 juvenile (Spielmeyer-Vogt) with early visual loss","Ultrastructural inclusion patterns (granular osmiophilic deposits, curvilinear bodies, fingerprint profiles) historically help characterise subtypes","Enzyme replacement therapy is available for CLN2 (TPP1) deficiency, making early recognition of that subtype particularly important","Diagnosis now rests principally on molecular genetics supported by enzyme assays and, where needed, tissue electron microscopy"],"pathophysiology":"The neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses are lysosomal disorders in which defects in a range of genes disrupt lysosomal degradation and autophagy. Several subtypes involve deficient soluble lysosomal enzymes (such as palmitoyl-protein thioesterase in CLN1 and tripeptidyl peptidase 1 in CLN2), while others affect transmembrane or membrane-associated proteins of the lysosome and endoplasmic reticulum. Impaired turnover leads to intralysosomal accumulation of autofluorescent ceroid-lipofuscin storage material. Neurones, being long-lived and metabolically demanding, are especially vulnerable, and progressive neuronal loss with retinal degeneration produces the characteristic combination of dementia, epilepsy, motor decline, and blindness.","additionalDifferentials":["Other progressive myoclonic epilepsies (Lafora and Unverricht-Lundborg disease)","Other lysosomal storage disorders such as sialidosis and gangliosidoses","Mitochondrial encephalomyopathies","Rett syndrome and other regression syndromes in young children","Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis","Inherited retinal dystrophy when visual loss predominates early"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic panel testing across CLN genes","Lysosomal enzyme assays including PPT1 (CLN1) and TPP1 (CLN2)","EEG, which may show characteristic changes such as an exaggerated response to low-frequency photic stimulation in some subtypes","Brain MRI to assess cerebral and cerebellar atrophy","Ophthalmological assessment and electroretinography for retinal degeneration, with electron microscopy of tissue where genetics are inconclusive"],"redFlags":["Developmental regression with new seizures and visual failure in a young child","Rapidly progressive dementia and myoclonus indicating advancing disease","Bulbar dysfunction with aspiration risk in later stages","A treatable CLN2 phenotype that must not be missed given available enzyme replacement therapy"],"prognosis":"Most childhood-onset forms are progressive and life-limiting, leading to dementia, blindness, and loss of motor function, though rate of decline varies by subtype; disease-modifying enzyme replacement can slow progression in CLN2 disease.","pitfalls":["Missing treatable CLN2 disease by not measuring TPP1 activity in a late-infantile regression with seizures","Attributing early visual loss to primary eye disease rather than an NCL","Assuming a single uniform disorder rather than a genetically heterogeneous group with different onsets and prognoses","Delaying genetic testing while relying solely on historical electron microscopy"],"management":["Offer enzyme replacement therapy with intracerebroventricular cerliponase alfa for CLN2 (TPP1) deficiency, making early identification of this subtype essential","For most subtypes there is no disease-modifying treatment, so care is supportive and symptom-directed","Control seizures and myoclonus with antiseizure medication, avoiding agents that may aggravate myoclonus","Manage spasticity, movement disorder, sleep disturbance and behavioural symptoms with appropriate pharmacological and supportive measures","Support declining vision with low-vision aids and address progressive feeding difficulty with dietetic input and consideration of gastrostomy feeding","Provide multidisciplinary neurology, ophthalmology, physiotherapy, palliative care and family support as the disease progresses","Offer genetic counselling and family screening given the mostly autosomal recessive inheritance, with carrier and prenatal testing where desired"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Neuronal Ceroid-Lipofuscinoses","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuronal_ceroid_lipofuscinosis_cln1":{"name":"Neuronal Ceroid Lipofuscinosis Type 1 (CLN1 Disease, PPT1 Deficiency)","system":"Metabolic / Neurodegenerative storage","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CLN1","Infantile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis","PPT1 deficiency","Santavuori-Haltia disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme palmitoyl-protein thioesterase 1 (PPT1) due to biallelic CLN1 (PPT1) variants; a form of neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis.","Classic phenotype is infantile-onset (Santavuori-Haltia): normal early development followed by rapid psychomotor regression, visual failure and seizures within the first years of life.","CLN1 is defined by the causative gene rather than age; later-onset late-infantile, juvenile and even adult presentations occur with the same enzyme defect.","Progressive microcephaly (acquired, deceleration of head growth) is characteristic of the infantile form, contrasting with several other storage disorders.","Myoclonus, ataxia, hand stereotypies and loss of acquired speech and motor skills are prominent as the disease advances.","Electron microscopy of tissue shows granular osmiophilic deposits (GRODs), the ultrastructural hallmark distinguishing CLN1 from the curvilinear and fingerprint profiles of other NCL subtypes.","Diagnosis is confirmed by demonstrating deficient PPT1 enzyme activity (readily measured in a dried blood spot or leucocytes) and by CLN1 molecular testing.","Management is supportive, focused on seizure control, spasticity, feeding, secretions and multidisciplinary palliative care; no approved disease-modifying therapy is established for CLN1."],"pathophysiology":"CLN1 disease results from deficiency of palmitoyl-protein thioesterase 1, a soluble lysosomal enzyme that removes palmitate (a thioester-linked fatty-acyl group) from cysteine residues of palmitoylated proteins, a step required for their normal lysosomal degradation and for recycling of the lipid modification. Loss of PPT1 activity impairs the turnover of S-acylated proteins and leads to lysosomal accumulation of autofluorescent lipopigment (ceroid and lipofuscin-like material) forming granular osmiophilic deposits within neurons and other cells. Neurons are exquisitely vulnerable: storage is accompanied by disrupted synaptic vesicle recycling, endoplasmic reticulum and mitochondrial stress, neuroinflammation with microglial and astrocytic activation, and progressive apoptotic neuronal loss, most strikingly in the cerebral and cerebellar cortex and the retina. The consequence is relentless grey-matter neurodegeneration producing regression, epilepsy, retinal degeneration with blindness and profound cerebral atrophy. The severity and age of onset broadly correlate with the amount of residual enzyme activity, explaining the spectrum from severe infantile to milder later-onset phenotypes carrying the same defect.","additionalDifferentials":["Other neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses, particularly CLN2 (TPP1 deficiency) and CLN3 (juvenile)","GM1 and GM2 gangliosidoses presenting with infantile regression","Rett syndrome (given hand stereotypies and regression in girls)","Krabbe disease and metachromatic leukodystrophy","Mitochondrial encephalopathies with regression and epilepsy","Infantile-onset progressive myoclonic epilepsy syndromes","Aicardi-Goutieres syndrome with acquired microcephaly and regression"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PPT1 enzyme activity assay in dried blood spot, leucocytes or fibroblasts (deficient)","Molecular genetic testing of the CLN1 (PPT1) gene","Electron microscopy of skin, conjunctiva or lymphocytes for granular osmiophilic deposits","MRI brain showing progressive cerebral and cerebellar atrophy with white-matter and thalamic signal change","Electroencephalography for epileptiform activity and progressive background attenuation","Electroretinography and ophthalmological assessment documenting retinal degeneration and optic atrophy","Serial head circumference measurement documenting acquired microcephaly in the infantile form"],"redFlags":["Deceleration of head growth (acquired microcephaly) with developmental regression in an infant","New-onset myoclonus or drug-resistant seizures accompanying loss of skills","Progressive visual failure with retinal degeneration and optic atrophy","Rapid loss of previously acquired speech, motor and social milestones","Emergence of hand stereotypies and irritability with sleep disturbance"],"prognosis":"The classic infantile CLN1 phenotype is severe and rapidly progressive, leading to a vegetative state and death in childhood; later-onset forms progress more slowly but remain fatal neurodegenerative diseases.","pitfalls":["Assuming CLN1 always presents in infancy and overlooking later-onset (juvenile or adult) presentations of the same PPT1 defect.","Misdiagnosing girls as Rett syndrome because of regression and hand stereotypies without measuring PPT1 activity.","Failing to request electron microscopy or enzyme testing when autofluorescent storage is suspected, delaying diagnosis.","Confusing CLN1 with CLN2, which has a distinct enzyme (TPP1), different ultrastructure and an available enzyme-replacement therapy.","Attributing acquired microcephaly to a primary structural or perinatal cause rather than a neurodegenerative storage process."],"management":["No licensed disease-specific therapy exists for PPT1 deficiency, so anchor care in coordinated multidisciplinary neurodegenerative supportive management","Control seizures and myoclonus with antiepileptic drugs such as valproate, levetiracetam and benzodiazepines, avoiding agents that aggravate myoclonus","Manage progressive spasticity and dystonia with physiotherapy, positioning, splinting and antispasticity agents such as baclofen","Provide nutritional support and regular swallowing assessment, progressing to gastrostomy feeding as bulbar function fails","Give proactive respiratory care with airway secretion management, chest physiotherapy and prompt treatment of aspiration","Arrange ophthalmology review and low-vision support for progressive visual failure and optic atrophy","Integrate paediatric palliative care early for symptom control and advance care planning","Offer genetic counselling, carrier testing and prenatal or family screening for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard inborn errors of metabolism reference texts","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis consensus classification and diagnostic guidance","Paediatric neurology reference works"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuronal_ceroid_lipofuscinosis_cln10":{"name":"Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 10 (CLN10 / cathepsin D deficiency)","system":"Metabolic / Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CLN10","CTSD","Cathepsin D deficiency","Congenital NCL"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics and inherited metabolic disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["CLN10 is caused by deficiency of cathepsin D, a lysosomal aspartyl protease encoded by CTSD, and is the classic cause of congenital NCL, the earliest-onset and most severe form.","The congenital form presents at or before birth with microcephaly, refractory neonatal seizures often reaching status epilepticus, rigidity and respiratory insufficiency, frequently with death in the neonatal period.","It may be heralded antenatally by reduced foetal movements or abnormal foetal imaging.","Milder CTSD variants cause later-onset disease, including late-infantile, juvenile and even adult-onset phenotypes with retinopathy and progressive neurodegeneration.","Unlike most NCLs, which store subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase, CLN10 and CLN1 accumulate storage material rich in sphingolipid activator proteins (saposins).","Electron microscopy shows granular osmiophilic deposits, the same ultrastructure seen in CLN1.","Because it is an enzyme deficiency, CLN10 can be supported biochemically by demonstrating reduced cathepsin D activity, then confirmed by CTSD genetic testing.","Congenital CLN10 brain imaging shows severe atrophy with cerebellar and brainstem hypoplasia and a thin cortex."],"pathophysiology":"CLN10 disease is an autosomal recessive neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis caused by biallelic variants in CTSD on chromosome 11p15, which encodes cathepsin D, an abundant lysosomal aspartyl protease essential for lysosomal protein turnover and neuronal homeostasis. Deficiency of cathepsin D activity impairs degradation of lysosomal substrates, including sphingolipid activator proteins (saposins), so that, unlike most NCLs which store subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase, CLN10 accumulates storage material rich in saposins A and D, seen ultrastructurally as granular osmiophilic deposits identical to those of CLN1. The degree of residual enzyme activity governs the phenotype: complete loss of function produces congenital NCL, the earliest and most severe form with antenatal or neonatal onset, whereas partial activity yields attenuated late-infantile, juvenile or adult-onset disease with retinopathy and progressive neurodegeneration. Widespread neuronal loss with severe brain atrophy underlies the clinical picture.","additionalDifferentials":["CLN1 (PPT1) NCL, which shares granular osmiophilic deposits and saposin storage","Other causes of congenital microcephaly with neonatal seizures (congenital infection, brain malformation, other metabolic encephalopathies)","CLN2, CLN5, CLN6, CLN7 and CLN8 (for the later-onset CTSD phenotypes)","Early infantile epileptic encephalopathies of other genetic cause","Pontocerebellar hypoplasias","Neonatal peroxisomal and mitochondrial encephalopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing (CTSD sequencing, NCL gene panel or exome sequencing)","Cathepsin D enzyme activity assay in fibroblasts or leucocytes","Brain MRI for severe atrophy with cerebellar and brainstem hypoplasia in the congenital form","Electron microscopy demonstrating granular osmiophilic deposits","EEG, which may show a burst-suppression pattern with neonatal seizures in the congenital form","Electroretinography and ophthalmology for retinopathy in later-onset forms","Antenatal imaging and foetal assessment where congenital disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["Congenital microcephaly with refractory neonatal seizures and respiratory failure","Reduced foetal movements or antenatal-onset neurological abnormality","Rapid neonatal neurological deterioration","Later-onset progressive visual loss with cognitive and motor regression"],"prognosis":"The congenital cathepsin D-deficiency form is frequently lethal in the neonatal period; later-onset CTSD phenotypes progress over months to years with variable survival. No disease-modifying therapy is licensed; care is supportive, including perinatal palliative care in the congenital form.","pitfalls":["Missing CLN10 in a neonate labelled as hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy or congenital infection.","Forgetting that CLN10, like CLN1, stores saposins and shows granular osmiophilic deposits rather than the ATP-synthase-subunit-c and curvilinear pattern of most NCLs.","Assuming all NCLs are childhood-onset; CTSD has adult-onset forms.","Overlooking the cathepsin D enzyme assay that can rapidly support the diagnosis."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists for cathepsin D deficiency, so management is supportive and, in the congenital form, centred on perinatal palliative care","Provide neonatal supportive care in the congenital form, including respiratory support for respiratory insufficiency and control of refractory neonatal seizures and status epilepticus with antiepileptic drugs and benzodiazepines","Control seizures and myoclonus with antiepileptic drugs in later-onset phenotypes","Support nutrition and swallowing with tube or gastrostomy feeding as bulbar function declines","Arrange ophthalmology and low-vision support for the retinopathy of later-onset forms","Coordinate multidisciplinary metabolic medicine, neurology, rehabilitation and palliative care input","Offer genetic counselling, carrier testing and prenatal diagnosis for this autosomal recessive condition, including antenatal counselling where reduced foetal movements or abnormal foetal imaging are found"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited metabolic disease reference texts","Paediatric neurology reference texts","Lysosomal storage disorder consensus guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuronal_ceroid_lipofuscinosis_cln2":{"name":"Neuronal Ceroid Lipofuscinosis Type 2 (CLN2 Disease, TPP1 Deficiency)","system":"Metabolic / Neurodegenerative storage","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CLN2","Late-infantile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis","TPP1 deficiency","Jansky-Bielschowsky disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme tripeptidyl peptidase 1 (TPP1) due to biallelic CLN2 (TPP1) variants; the classic late-infantile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis.","Typical presentation is language delay followed by seizures (often first afebrile seizures) and ataxia in a toddler or young child, then rapid motor and cognitive regression.","The combination of new-onset epilepsy plus a history of speech delay in a young child should specifically prompt consideration of CLN2.","Visual failure due to retinal degeneration tends to appear later in the course than in CLN3, but progresses to blindness.","CLN2 is the NCL subtype with an approved intracerebroventricular enzyme-replacement therapy (recombinant TPP1), making early recognition genuinely disease-altering.","Electron microscopy classically shows curvilinear inclusion bodies, contrasting with the granular osmiophilic deposits of CLN1 and the fingerprint profiles of CLN3.","Diagnosis is confirmed by demonstrating deficient TPP1 enzyme activity (dried blood spot or leucocytes) together with CLN2 molecular testing.","A standardised clinical rating scale is used to track motor and language decline and to monitor response to therapy."],"pathophysiology":"CLN2 disease is caused by deficiency of tripeptidyl peptidase 1, a lysosomal serine protease that sequentially removes tripeptides from the unblocked N-terminus of small polypeptides and also has endopeptidase activity at acidic pH. TPP1 is synthesised as an inactive proenzyme that is autocatalytically processed within the acidic lysosome to its mature form. Loss of TPP1 activity impairs lysosomal turnover of its protein substrates and leads to accumulation of autofluorescent ceroid-lipofuscin storage material, notably including subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase, forming curvilinear inclusions within neurons and other cells. The resulting lysosomal dysfunction drives neuroinflammation, synaptic loss and progressive neuronal death predominantly affecting the cerebral cortex, cerebellum and retina. Cerebellar involvement is early and prominent, correlating with the striking ataxia and later motor collapse, while cortical and retinal degeneration produce the cognitive decline, epilepsy and blindness. Residual enzyme activity influences phenotype, with rarer variants producing later-onset or predominantly ataxic or retinal presentations from the same gene.","additionalDifferentials":["Other neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses, especially CLN1 (PPT1) and CLN3 (juvenile)","Late-infantile variant NCLs due to CLN5, CLN6, CLN7 (MFSD8) and CLN8","Progressive myoclonic epilepsies such as Lafora and Unverricht-Lundborg disease","Mitochondrial epilepsy syndromes with regression","Autoimmune or infective encephalitis presenting with new seizures and regression","Late-onset GM2 gangliosidosis and other lysosomal disorders","Idiopathic epileptic encephalopathies before the neurodegenerative course declares itself"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["TPP1 enzyme activity assay in dried blood spot, leucocytes or fibroblasts (deficient)","Molecular genetic testing of the CLN2 (TPP1) gene","Electron microscopy of skin, conjunctiva or lymphocytes for curvilinear inclusion bodies","MRI brain showing early cerebellar atrophy with later cerebral atrophy and periventricular white-matter signal change","Electroencephalography, including a characteristic photoparoxysmal response to low-frequency photic stimulation","Electroretinography and ophthalmological review documenting retinal degeneration and optic atrophy","Standardised CLN2 clinical rating scale to quantify and monitor motor and language decline"],"redFlags":["New-onset unprovoked seizures in a young child with a preceding history of language delay","Progressive ataxia and myoclonus with loss of motor skills","Rapid regression of speech and cognition in the late-infantile period","Visual deterioration with retinal degeneration developing during the illness","Photoparoxysmal electroencephalographic response to slow photic stimulation"],"prognosis":"Untreated classic CLN2 progresses rapidly to loss of ambulation, speech and vision with death in childhood; intracerebroventricular enzyme-replacement therapy can slow motor and language decline when started early, altering the natural history.","pitfalls":["Dismissing early language delay as isolated, then failing to link it to subsequent seizures and missing the CLN2 pattern.","Delaying TPP1 enzyme testing in a child with new epilepsy and regression, thereby losing the window for enzyme-replacement therapy.","Confusing CLN2 with CLN1 or CLN3, which have different enzymes, ultrastructure and management implications.","Overlooking the diagnostic value of the low-frequency photoparoxysmal electroencephalographic response.","Assuming visual failure must be early; in CLN2 it often follows the seizures and ataxia rather than preceding them."],"management":["Initiate intracerebroventricular enzyme-replacement therapy with recombinant human TPP1 (cerliponase alfa) via an implanted reservoir to slow motor and language decline, ideally started early","Monitor for and manage device-related complications such as reservoir infection or malfunction and treat infusion-related reactions during enzyme delivery","Control seizures and myoclonus with antiepileptic drugs such as valproate, levetiracetam and benzodiazepines","Provide physiotherapy, occupational therapy and antispasticity management for progressive motor deterioration and ataxia","Support nutrition and swallowing with gastrostomy feeding as bulbar function fails","Arrange ophthalmology and low-vision support for progressive retinal degeneration","Coordinate multidisciplinary metabolic medicine, neurology, rehabilitation and palliative care input","Offer genetic counselling, carrier testing and family screening for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard inborn errors of metabolism reference texts","CLN2 disease expert consensus on diagnosis and management","Paediatric neurology reference works"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuronal_ceroid_lipofuscinosis_cln3":{"name":"Neuronal Ceroid Lipofuscinosis Type 3 (CLN3 Disease, Juvenile NCL / Batten Disease)","system":"Metabolic / Neurodegenerative storage","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CLN3","Juvenile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis","Batten disease","Spielmeyer-Vogt-Sjogren disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic variants in CLN3 encoding battenin, a lysosomal/endosomal membrane protein; the classic juvenile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis, often termed Batten disease.","The presenting feature is typically rapidly progressive visual loss in a previously well school-age child, due to retinal degeneration, often initially misattributed to a primary ophthalmological problem.","Cognitive and behavioural decline, then seizures, then extrapyramidal and motor deterioration follow the visual failure over subsequent years.","Psychiatric and behavioural disturbance (anxiety, psychosis-like features, aggression) is prominent and may dominate the middle phase of the disease.","A common recurrent intragenic deletion in CLN3 accounts for a large proportion of cases and facilitates targeted molecular diagnosis.","Vacuolated lymphocytes on a peripheral blood film are a useful clue relatively specific to CLN3 among the NCLs.","Electron microscopy characteristically shows fingerprint profiles, contrasting with the granular osmiophilic deposits of CLN1 and curvilinear bodies of CLN2.","Because the enzyme-based assays used for CLN1 and CLN2 are normal in CLN3, diagnosis relies on CLN3 molecular testing supported by ophthalmology, blood film and ultrastructure."],"pathophysiology":"CLN3 disease is caused by loss of function of battenin, the CLN3-encoded multipass transmembrane protein that resides principally in lysosomal and late-endosomal membranes. Although its precise molecular role is incompletely defined, battenin participates in lysosomal membrane trafficking, endosomal-lysosomal fusion, autophagy, lysosomal pH and ion homeostasis, and membrane lipid handling. Its deficiency leads to lysosomal dysfunction and accumulation of autofluorescent ceroid-lipofuscin storage material, rich in subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase, forming characteristic fingerprint inclusion profiles within neurons and other cells. The commonest disease allele is a recurrent intragenic deletion removing part of the gene. Disturbed autophagy, oxidative and endoplasmic-reticulum stress, calcium dysregulation, neuroinflammation with glial activation and immune dysregulation contribute to progressive neuronal loss. Retinal photoreceptors and ganglion cells are affected early, producing the characteristic initial visual failure, followed by cortical, cerebellar and basal-ganglia degeneration underlying the cognitive, behavioural, epileptic and motor features. The disease is unlike CLN1 and CLN2 in that it does not arise from a deficient soluble lysosomal enzyme, so conventional enzyme assays are unrevealing.","additionalDifferentials":["Other neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses, particularly juvenile-onset CLN1, CLN2 and CLN9-associated presentations","Primary retinal dystrophies such as retinitis pigmentosa or cone-rod dystrophy without neurodegeneration","Progressive myoclonic epilepsies including Lafora and Unverricht-Lundborg disease","Childhood-onset psychiatric illness when behavioural change dominates early","Wilson disease and other treatable causes of combined neuropsychiatric and movement decline","Mitochondrial disorders with retinopathy and neurodegeneration","Juvenile-onset lysosomal storage disorders and leukodystrophies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of the CLN3 gene, including specific assessment for the common recurrent intragenic deletion","Peripheral blood film for vacuolated lymphocytes","Electron microscopy of skin, conjunctiva or lymphocytes for fingerprint profiles","Detailed ophthalmological assessment with electroretinography and optical coherence tomography documenting retinal degeneration and optic atrophy","MRI brain showing progressive cerebral and cerebellar atrophy, sometimes with thalamic and white-matter signal change","Electroencephalography for epileptiform activity as seizures emerge","Neuropsychological and behavioural assessment to characterise cognitive and psychiatric decline"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive visual loss in a previously healthy school-age child","Emergence of cognitive decline and school failure following visual deterioration","New behavioural or psychiatric disturbance with hallucinations or aggression in a child","Onset of seizures added to visual and cognitive decline","Development of parkinsonism, dystonia or other movement disorder in the later course"],"prognosis":"CLN3 disease follows a protracted but relentless course over one to two decades, with blindness, dementia, epilepsy and motor decline leading to death in early adulthood; no disease-modifying therapy is established and care is supportive.","pitfalls":["Attributing the early visual loss to an isolated primary retinal dystrophy and missing the underlying neurodegenerative disorder.","Managing the middle-phase behavioural and psychiatric features as a primary psychiatric illness without recognising the storage disease.","Excluding NCL because CLN1 and CLN2 enzyme assays are normal, when CLN3 requires molecular rather than enzymatic diagnosis.","Overlooking simple, informative bedside clues such as vacuolated lymphocytes on the blood film.","Confusing the fingerprint ultrastructure and clinical pattern of CLN3 with the distinct profiles and courses of CLN1 and CLN2."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy is established, so management is supportive and multidisciplinary","Control seizures with antiepileptic drugs such as valproate, levetiracetam and lamotrigine","Manage the prominent psychiatric and behavioural disturbance (anxiety, psychosis-like features and aggression) with psychological support and judicious psychotropic agents","Provide ophthalmology assessment and low-vision and blind-support services for the early progressive visual loss","Treat emerging parkinsonism and extrapyramidal features and give physiotherapy for motor decline","Support nutrition and swallowing with gastrostomy feeding as the disease advances","Coordinate palliative care, education and social support across the protracted course","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive disorder, using targeted testing for the common recurrent intragenic deletion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard inborn errors of metabolism reference texts","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis consensus classification and diagnostic guidance","Paediatric neurology reference works"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuronal_ceroid_lipofuscinosis_cln5":{"name":"Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 5 (CLN5 disease)","system":"Metabolic / Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CLN5","Finnish variant late-infantile NCL","CLN5-related Batten disease","vLINCL (CLN5)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics and inherited metabolic disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["CLN5 disease is a variant late-infantile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis first delineated in Finnish families but now recognised worldwide.","Onset is typically a little later than classic late-infantile (CLN2) disease, opening with motor clumsiness and concentration difficulty in mid-childhood, then visual failure, myoclonus, ataxia and progressive dementia.","It is caused by biallelic variants in CLN5, which encodes a soluble lysosomal glycoprotein, so it is not one of the enzyme-deficiency NCL subtypes.","Early pigmentary retinopathy and visual loss are consistent features, in contrast to adult Kufs-type NCLs in which the retina is spared.","Brain MRI shows progressive cerebellar and cerebral atrophy, hypointense thalami and periventricular white-matter change.","Electron microscopy of stored material shows mixed inclusions, with fingerprint, curvilinear and rectilinear profiles rather than a single pattern.","Because there is no biochemical enzyme marker, diagnosis rests on molecular genetic testing rather than a leukocyte enzyme assay.","Storage material is rich in subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase, as in most non-enzymatic NCLs."],"pathophysiology":"CLN5 disease is an autosomal recessive neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in CLN5 on chromosome 13q, which encodes CLN5, a soluble, heavily glycosylated lysosomal protein. Long regarded as functionally enigmatic, CLN5 is increasingly implicated in lysosomal lipid handling, including a proposed role in the metabolism of bis(monoacylglycero)phosphate, a lipid central to intralysosomal membrane digestion. Deficient CLN5 function disturbs lysosomal homeostasis and leads to progressive intraneuronal accumulation of autofluorescent ceroid-lipofuscin storage material that, as in most non-enzymatic NCLs, is rich in subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase. The consequence is widespread neurodegeneration with prominent cortical, cerebellar and retinal neuronal loss, producing the combination of dementia, motor decline, myoclonus, ataxia and visual failure. A Finnish founder allele accounts for most cases originally described in Finland, but pathogenic variants and disease occur worldwide.","additionalDifferentials":["CLN2 (TPP1) classic late-infantile NCL","CLN6 variant late-infantile NCL","CLN7 (MFSD8) variant late-infantile NCL","CLN8 variant late-infantile NCL and Northern epilepsy","CLN1 (PPT1) infantile and later-onset NCL","Other progressive myoclonic epilepsies (Lafora disease, Unverricht-Lundborg disease, MERRF)","Late-infantile onset lysosomal disorders such as juvenile GM2 gangliosidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing (CLN5 sequencing, NCL gene panel or exome sequencing)","Brain MRI for cerebellar and cerebral atrophy and thalamic signal change","EEG including photic stimulation, seeking a photoparoxysmal response at low flash rates","Electroretinography and formal ophthalmological review for retinal degeneration","Visual evoked potentials","Electron microscopy of skin or conjunctival biopsy showing mixed fingerprint, curvilinear and rectilinear profiles (now second-line)","Targeted exclusion of enzyme-deficient NCLs (PPT1, TPP1, cathepsin D) when the genotype is uncertain"],"redFlags":["New visual failure with pigmentary retinopathy in a child whose motor and cognitive skills are regressing","Emerging myoclonus and treatment-resistant seizures","Loss of previously acquired developmental milestones","Rapidly progressive ataxia and gait deterioration"],"prognosis":"CLN5 disease is relentlessly progressive, with loss of independent mobility, vision, speech and swallowing over years and death commonly in later childhood to adolescence. No disease-modifying therapy is licensed; management is supportive, although gene-directed approaches are under investigation.","pitfalls":["Attributing early clumsiness or inattention to a behavioural or learning problem, delaying diagnosis.","Expecting a single ultrastructural inclusion type; CLN5 shows mixed profiles.","Searching for an enzyme deficiency; CLN5 is not enzyme-based and needs genetic confirmation.","Assuming it is a Finnish-only disease; CLN5 occurs worldwide.","Offering cerliponase alfa, which is specific to CLN2 and does not treat CLN5."],"management":["No licensed disease-modifying therapy exists, so provide coordinated supportive care, with gene-directed approaches available only within research settings","Control seizures and myoclonus with antiepileptic drugs, avoiding agents that aggravate myoclonus","Provide physiotherapy, occupational therapy and antispasticity management for progressive motor decline and ataxia","Arrange ophthalmology and low-vision support for pigmentary retinopathy and visual failure","Support nutrition and swallowing with gastrostomy feeding as bulbar function declines","Coordinate multidisciplinary neurology, rehabilitation, palliative care and educational support","Offer genetic counselling, carrier testing and family screening for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited metabolic disease reference texts","Paediatric neurology reference texts","Lysosomal storage disorder consensus guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuronal_ceroid_lipofuscinosis_cln6":{"name":"Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 6 (CLN6 disease)","system":"Metabolic / Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CLN6","Variant late-infantile NCL (CLN6)","Kufs disease type A","CLN6-related Batten disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics and inherited metabolic disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["CLN6 produces two distinct clinical pictures from the same gene: a variant late-infantile NCL of childhood and adult-onset Kufs disease type A, a progressive myoclonic epilepsy.","The late-infantile variant presents in early childhood with seizures, motor and language regression, ataxia, myoclonus and progressive visual loss.","Kufs disease type A causes progressive myoclonic epilepsy with dementia and ataxia but characteristically spares the retina, so vision is preserved, unlike the childhood NCLs.","It is caused by biallelic variants in CLN6, encoding an endoplasmic-reticulum transmembrane protein rather than a lysosomal enzyme.","Stored material accumulates as curvilinear, fingerprint and rectilinear profiles on electron microscopy and is rich in subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase.","Because CLN6 is not enzyme-deficient, diagnosis depends on molecular genetics and enzyme assays are normal.","MRI shows cerebellar and then cerebral atrophy; adult Kufs type A may show predominantly cerebellar atrophy.","Kufs disease type A is CLN6-related, whereas Kufs disease type B is usually due to CTSF (CLN13), an important genetic distinction in adult NCL."],"pathophysiology":"CLN6 disease is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic variants in CLN6 on chromosome 15q23, encoding CLN6, a non-glycosylated transmembrane protein resident in the endoplasmic reticulum rather than the lysosome. CLN6 is not an enzyme; it acts together with CLN8 as part of a complex that recruits newly synthesised soluble lysosomal enzymes in the endoplasmic reticulum and relays them towards the Golgi for onward lysosomal delivery. Loss of CLN6 function therefore impairs the supply of multiple lysosomal hydrolases, disturbing lysosomal degradation and causing accumulation of autofluorescent ceroid-lipofuscin rich in subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase, seen ultrastructurally as curvilinear, fingerprint and rectilinear profiles. The allelic spectrum explains the striking clinical range: some variants produce a childhood variant late-infantile NCL with retinal degeneration, whereas others cause adult-onset Kufs disease type A, a progressive myoclonic epilepsy with dementia in which the retina is spared.","additionalDifferentials":["CLN2 (TPP1) classic late-infantile NCL","CLN5, CLN7 (MFSD8) and CLN8 variant late-infantile NCLs","CLN13 (CTSF) Kufs disease type B","Other adult progressive myoclonic epilepsies (Lafora disease, Unverricht-Lundborg disease, DRPLA, MERRF, sialidosis)","CLN3 juvenile NCL","Adult-onset ataxias and early-onset dementias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing (CLN6 sequencing, NCL gene panel or exome sequencing), including in the adult Kufs work-up","Brain MRI for cerebellar and cerebral atrophy","EEG with photic stimulation for photoparoxysmal responses","Electroretinography and ophthalmology (childhood form; expected normal in Kufs type A)","Electron microscopy of skin, conjunctival or axillary biopsy for curvilinear and fingerprint profiles","Neuropsychometric assessment to characterise dementia in the adult form","Muscle, nerve or other targeted biopsy when excluding mimics"],"redFlags":["Progressive myoclonic epilepsy with cognitive decline in a young adult, raising Kufs disease type A","Developmental regression with visual loss in a child","Drug-resistant myoclonic and generalised seizures","Rapid loss of gait and coordination"],"prognosis":"Childhood CLN6 disease is relentlessly progressive with death typically in later childhood or adolescence; adult Kufs disease type A progresses more slowly but causes disabling myoclonus, dementia and premature death. Care is supportive.","pitfalls":["Excluding NCL in an adult because vision is preserved; Kufs type A spares the retina.","Confusing Kufs type A (CLN6) with Kufs type B (CTSF/CLN13); they are genetically distinct.","Relying on enzyme assays, which are normal in CLN6.","Misattributing early childhood regression to an epileptic encephalopathy of unrelated cause."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so management is supportive across both the childhood variant and adult Kufs type A phenotypes","Control myoclonus and seizures of the progressive myoclonic epilepsy with agents such as valproate, levetiracetam, clonazepam and piracetam-type drugs, avoiding sodium-channel blockers such as carbamazepine, phenytoin and lamotrigine that worsen myoclonus","Provide physiotherapy, occupational therapy and management of ataxia and spasticity for motor decline","Arrange ophthalmology and low-vision support in the childhood form, where vision is preserved in Kufs type A","Support nutrition and swallowing with gastrostomy feeding as bulbar function fails in the childhood form","Coordinate multidisciplinary neurology, rehabilitation and palliative care, with neuropsychology input for the dementia of the adult form","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited metabolic disease reference texts","Neurology and epilepsy reference texts","Lysosomal storage disorder consensus guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuronal_ceroid_lipofuscinosis_cln7":{"name":"Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 7 (CLN7 / MFSD8 disease)","system":"Metabolic / Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CLN7","MFSD8","Turkish variant late-infantile NCL","CLN7-related Batten disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics and inherited metabolic disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["CLN7 is a variant late-infantile NCL caused by biallelic variants in MFSD8, first characterised in Turkish families.","MFSD8 encodes a lysosomal transmembrane protein of the major facilitator superfamily, a putative transporter, so CLN7 is not an enzyme-deficiency subtype.","Typical onset is in early childhood with seizures that are often myoclonic, developmental regression, ataxia, progressive visual failure and speech decline.","Certain hypomorphic MFSD8 variants cause an isolated, non-syndromic macular or retinal dystrophy without neurodegeneration, a much milder allelic phenotype.","Electron microscopy shows fingerprint, curvilinear and rectilinear inclusions, and stored material is rich in subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase.","MRI demonstrates early and prominent cerebellar atrophy with later cerebral atrophy and thalamic and white-matter change.","Diagnosis is molecular; enzyme assays are unremarkable.","A photoparoxysmal EEG response to low-rate photic stimulation is characteristic of the late-infantile NCL group, including CLN7."],"pathophysiology":"CLN7 disease is an autosomal recessive neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis caused by biallelic variants in MFSD8, also designated CLN7, on chromosome 4q28. MFSD8 encodes a polytopic lysosomal membrane protein belonging to the major facilitator superfamily, a large group of secondary active transporters; its precise transported substrate remains to be fully defined, but its loss disrupts lysosomal homeostasis. Impaired MFSD8 function leads to accumulation of autofluorescent ceroid-lipofuscin rich in subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase, with fingerprint, curvilinear and rectilinear inclusions on electron microscopy, and drives progressive neuronal and retinal degeneration. Genotype-phenotype correlation is notable: complete loss-of-function alleles produce the severe variant late-infantile neurodegenerative disease, whereas certain hypomorphic missense variants cause an isolated, non-syndromic macular and retinal dystrophy without systemic neurodegeneration.","additionalDifferentials":["CLN2 (TPP1) classic late-infantile NCL","CLN5, CLN6 and CLN8 variant late-infantile NCLs","Non-syndromic inherited retinal dystrophies (for the isolated MFSD8 retinal phenotype)","Other progressive myoclonic epilepsies","CLN1 (PPT1) NCL","Juvenile-onset lysosomal and neurodegenerative disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing (MFSD8 sequencing, NCL gene panel or exome sequencing)","Brain MRI, expecting early cerebellar atrophy","EEG with photic stimulation","Electroretinography with detailed retinal imaging (optical coherence tomography and fundus autofluorescence)","Visual evoked potentials","Electron microscopy of skin or conjunctival biopsy","Ophthalmology review, particularly for the isolated retinal phenotype"],"redFlags":["Developmental regression with new seizures in early childhood","Progressive visual loss with retinal dystrophy","Treatment-resistant myoclonus","Rapid cerebellar and gait deterioration"],"prognosis":"Neurodegenerative CLN7 is relentlessly progressive, with early loss of skills and death commonly in childhood to adolescence; the isolated MFSD8 retinal dystrophy phenotype is far milder and non-lethal. Management is supportive.","pitfalls":["Overlooking MFSD8 in an isolated retinal dystrophy that lacks neurological features.","Assuming an enzyme deficiency; CLN7 requires genetic diagnosis.","Restricting suspicion to Turkish ancestry; CLN7 occurs worldwide.","Mislabelling it as classic CLN2 and expecting cerliponase alfa to help."],"management":["No licensed disease-modifying therapy exists, so management is supportive and multidisciplinary","Control the often myoclonic seizures with antiepileptic drugs, avoiding agents that aggravate myoclonus","Provide physiotherapy, occupational therapy and antispasticity management for developmental regression and ataxia","Arrange ophthalmology and low-vision support for progressive retinal degeneration, while the isolated MFSD8 macular or retinal dystrophy phenotype needs ophthalmic surveillance without neurodegenerative care","Support nutrition and swallowing with gastrostomy feeding as bulbar function declines","Coordinate neurology, rehabilitation, palliative care and educational support","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited metabolic disease reference texts","Paediatric neurology reference texts","Lysosomal storage disorder consensus guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuronal_ceroid_lipofuscinosis_cln8":{"name":"Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 8 (CLN8 disease)","system":"Metabolic / Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CLN8","Northern epilepsy syndrome","Progressive epilepsy with intellectual disability (EPMR)","CLN8 variant late-infantile NCL"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics and inherited metabolic disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["CLN8 spans a phenotypic spectrum from the comparatively mild Finnish Northern epilepsy syndrome (progressive epilepsy with intellectual disability, EPMR) to a severe Turkish variant late-infantile NCL.","Northern epilepsy begins in the early school years with generalised tonic-clonic seizures that increase in frequency, followed by slowly progressive cognitive decline; vision is relatively preserved and survival into adulthood is usual.","The variant late-infantile CLN8 phenotype presents earlier with myoclonus, motor and cognitive regression, ataxia and visual failure, a far more aggressive course.","It is caused by biallelic variants in CLN8, encoding a transmembrane protein of the endoplasmic reticulum and ER-Golgi intermediate compartment in the TLC-domain family.","CLN8 partners with CLN6 to recruit newly synthesised lysosomal enzymes for endoplasmic-reticulum-to-Golgi transport.","Stored material shows granular, curvilinear and fingerprint inclusions and is rich in subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase.","Diagnosis is molecular; CLN8 is not an enzyme-deficiency subtype.","A specific founder variant underlies Northern epilepsy in the Finnish population, whereas other variants worldwide tend to cause the variant late-infantile phenotype."],"pathophysiology":"CLN8 disease is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic variants in CLN8 on chromosome 8p23, encoding CLN8, a multipass transmembrane protein of the endoplasmic reticulum and the ER-Golgi intermediate compartment and a member of the TLC (TRAM-LAG1-CLN8) domain family implicated in lipid and ceramide regulation. CLN8 partners with CLN6 to recruit newly synthesised lysosomal enzymes in the endoplasmic reticulum and relay them to the Golgi, so its loss reduces delivery of multiple lysosomal hydrolases and perturbs lipid homeostasis. The result is accumulation of autofluorescent ceroid-lipofuscin rich in subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase, with granular, curvilinear and fingerprint inclusions, and progressive neurodegeneration. Residual protein function determines severity: a Finnish founder variant produces the comparatively protracted Northern epilepsy syndrome with intellectual disability, while other variants cause a severe variant late-infantile NCL with retinal involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["CLN2 (TPP1) classic late-infantile NCL","CLN5, CLN6 and CLN7 (MFSD8) variant late-infantile NCLs","Other genetic epilepsies with intellectual disability (for the Northern epilepsy phenotype)","Progressive myoclonic epilepsies (Unverricht-Lundborg disease, Lafora disease)","CLN3 juvenile NCL","Other causes of childhood epileptic encephalopathy with regression"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing (CLN8 sequencing, NCL gene panel or exome sequencing)","Brain MRI for cerebellar atrophy, which may be subtle in Northern epilepsy","EEG with photic stimulation","Electroretinography and ophthalmology (retinal involvement in the variant late-infantile form; relatively spared in Northern epilepsy)","Electron microscopy of skin or conjunctival biopsy for granular and curvilinear inclusions","Neuropsychometric assessment of cognitive decline"],"redFlags":["Increasingly frequent tonic-clonic seizures with insidious cognitive decline in a school-age child","Developmental regression with visual loss in the variant late-infantile form","Emerging drug-resistant myoclonus","Loss of acquired motor skills"],"prognosis":"Northern epilepsy is comparatively protracted, with seizures often lessening after puberty but persistent cognitive impairment and survival into adulthood; the variant late-infantile CLN8 phenotype is severe and shortens life to childhood or adolescence. Care is supportive.","pitfalls":["Not recognising Northern epilepsy as an NCL because vision and lifespan are relatively preserved.","Assuming uniform severity across CLN8; the phenotype varies widely by variant.","Relying on enzyme assays, which are normal.","Missing the diagnosis where the founder-population context is absent."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so management is supportive across the spectrum from Northern epilepsy to the variant late-infantile phenotype","Control seizures with antiepileptic drugs, noting that in Northern epilepsy seizure frequency often lessens after puberty","Manage myoclonus, ataxia and spasticity in the variant late-infantile form with appropriate antiepileptic and antispasticity agents, avoiding drugs that worsen myoclonus","Provide physiotherapy, occupational therapy and neuropsychological support for progressive cognitive impairment","Arrange ophthalmology and low-vision support in the variant late-infantile form, where vision is relatively preserved in Northern epilepsy","Support nutrition and swallowing with gastrostomy feeding as bulbar function declines in the severe phenotype","Coordinate multidisciplinary neurology, rehabilitation and palliative care","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited metabolic disease reference texts","Neurology and epilepsy reference texts","Lysosomal storage disorder consensus guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuronal_intranuclear_inclusion_disease":{"name":"Neuronal intranuclear inclusion disease (NOTCH2NLC)","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NIID","neuronal intranuclear hyaline inclusion disease","NOTCH2NLC GGC repeat expansion disorder","NOTCH2NLC"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A highly variable adult-onset disorder that can present with dementia, episodic encephalopathy, parkinsonism, tremor, autonomic dysfunction or a peripheral neuropathy","Caused by a GGC trinucleotide repeat expansion in the NOTCH2NLC gene, with a growing spectrum of recognised phenotypes","The most characteristic imaging clue is a ribbon of high signal along the corticomedullary junction on diffusion-weighted imaging, corresponding to the subcortical U-fibres","Leukoencephalopathy and progressive white matter change are common as the disease advances","Skin biopsy showing eosinophilic, ubiquitin- and p62-positive intranuclear inclusions can support the diagnosis less invasively than brain biopsy","Episodes of encephalopathy with headache, altered consciousness or focal deficits may mimic stroke or encephalitis","Genetic testing for the NOTCH2NLC repeat expansion provides definitive confirmation"],"pathophysiology":"Neuronal intranuclear inclusion disease is a slowly progressive neurodegenerative disorder defined pathologically by eosinophilic hyaline intranuclear inclusions within neurones, glia and cells of many other tissues, including the skin. It is caused by an abnormal GGC repeat expansion in the NOTCH2NLC gene, which is thought to produce toxic effects through repeat-associated translation and sequestration of nuclear proteins into the inclusions. Widespread deposition affects the cerebral white matter, cortex, autonomic and peripheral nervous systems, accounting for the diverse clinical picture that ranges from dementia and encephalopathic episodes to neuropathy and autonomic failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Fragile X-associated tremor/ataxia syndrome","Leukoencephalopathies of other genetic or metabolic causes","Behavioural variant frontotemporal dementia or Alzheimer disease","Recurrent stroke or transient ischaemic attacks","Autoimmune or infective encephalitis during encephalopathic episodes","Mitochondrial encephalopathy","CADASIL and other small-vessel disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain with diffusion-weighted imaging to detect the corticomedullary junction (U-fibre) ribbon","Skin biopsy to demonstrate p62- and ubiquitin-positive intranuclear inclusions","Genetic testing for the NOTCH2NLC GGC repeat expansion","Nerve conduction studies to characterise an associated peripheral neuropathy","Autonomic function testing where dysautonomia is prominent","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis to help exclude inflammatory and infective causes during encephalopathic episodes"],"redFlags":["Acute encephalopathic episodes with reduced consciousness that may be mistaken for stroke or encephalitis","Progressive autonomic failure causing syncope or gastrointestinal and bladder dysfunction","Rapidly worsening cognition or recurrent seizures","New focal neurological deficits requiring exclusion of alternative acute pathology"],"prognosis":"The disorder is generally slowly progressive over years, though the course and rate vary widely between individuals; episodic encephalopathy, accumulating cognitive decline and autonomic involvement contribute to disability, and management is supportive.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis because the phenotype is so variable and may mimic many other disorders","Failing to review diffusion-weighted imaging for the characteristic subcortical ribbon","Not pursuing skin biopsy or NOTCH2NLC testing when the imaging pattern is suggestive","Attributing encephalopathic episodes solely to stroke or infection without considering NIID"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so management is supportive and tailored to the individual's dominant manifestations","Manage encephalopathic episodes supportively with hydration and symptomatic care while excluding the stroke and encephalitis they may mimic","Treat parkinsonism with a trial of levodopa and manage tremor with appropriate agents where these features dominate","Manage dysautonomia, treating orthostatic hypotension, neurogenic bladder and gastrointestinal dysmotility","Provide neuropathic-pain treatment and rehabilitation for the associated peripheral neuropathy","Offer cognitive assessment, dementia support and caregiver input for progressive cognitive decline","Offer genetic counselling for the NOTCH2NLC GGC repeat expansion and consider family assessment given autosomal dominant inheritance in familial cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuropathic_pain":{"name":"Neuropathic pain","system":"Rehabilitation / Pain","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nerve pain","neurogenic pain","neuralgia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braddom's Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation / pain medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Neuropathic pain arises from a lesion or disease of the somatosensory nervous system and is distinct from nociceptive pain caused by ongoing tissue damage","Typical descriptors are burning, shooting, electric-shock, or stabbing pain, often with numbness, and it characteristically maps to a recognisable neuroanatomical distribution","Hallmark abnormal sensory findings include allodynia, where a normally non-painful stimulus causes pain, and hyperalgesia, an exaggerated response to a painful stimulus","Common causes span diabetic and other polyneuropathies, postherpetic neuralgia, trigeminal neuralgia, radiculopathy, post-stroke central pain, spinal cord injury, and chemotherapy-induced neuropathy","Screening tools such as the DN4 or painDETECT can support recognition, but diagnosis rests on history, distribution, and confirmatory sensory examination","First-line pharmacological principles favour gabapentinoids, tricyclic antidepressants, and serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitors, with topical agents for localised pain and opioids generally avoided as first choice","A multimodal, multidisciplinary approach combining medication, physical therapy, psychological strategies, and treatment of the underlying cause gives the best results","Interventional options such as nerve blocks and neuromodulation may help selected patients with refractory pain","Realistic goals centre on partial pain reduction and improved function and sleep rather than complete pain relief"],"pathophysiology":"Neuropathic pain results from maladaptive changes throughout the nervous system after nerve injury or disease. Damaged neurons develop ectopic and spontaneous discharge and altered ion-channel expression, producing peripheral sensitisation. In the dorsal horn, increased excitability, loss of inhibitory interneuron function, and glial activation drive central sensitisation and wind-up. Structural and functional reorganisation extends to the brain, and descending modulatory pathways may shift toward facilitation of pain. These changes together explain the spontaneous pain, allodynia, and hyperalgesia that persist beyond any ongoing tissue injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Nociceptive musculoskeletal pain","Inflammatory or ischaemic pain","Complex regional pain syndrome","Fibromyalgia and centralised pain states","Myofascial pain syndrome","Vascular claudication mimicking limb pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed sensory examination mapping the distribution and testing for allodynia and hyperalgesia","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography where a peripheral nerve or root lesion is suspected","Screening questionnaires such as DN4 or painDETECT to support recognition","Targeted investigation of the underlying cause, for example glycaemic testing, imaging of the spine, or metabolic screening","Quantitative sensory testing or skin biopsy for small-fibre assessment in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Progressive motor weakness, sphincter disturbance, or saddle anaesthesia suggesting cord or cauda equina compression","Rapidly worsening deficit implying an evolving compressive or inflammatory lesion","Systemic features such as weight loss or night pain raising concern for malignancy or infection","New severe pain with fever suggesting an infective or inflammatory neurological process"],"prognosis":"Neuropathic pain is often chronic and only partially responsive to treatment, but many patients achieve meaningful reductions in pain intensity and improved function with a structured multimodal approach. Prognosis is better when a reversible underlying cause is identified and treated early.","pitfalls":["Treating neuropathic pain with conventional analgesics or NSAIDs alone, which are usually ineffective","Escalating opioids for a pain type that responds poorly to them and carries dependence risk","Missing a serious underlying cause such as cord compression or malignancy","Setting an expectation of complete pain relief rather than functional improvement","Neglecting the psychological and sleep dimensions that amplify chronic neuropathic pain"],"management":["Identify and treat any reversible underlying cause, such as optimising glycaemic control in diabetic neuropathy or decompressing a compressed nerve root","Start first-line pharmacotherapy with a gabapentinoid, a tricyclic antidepressant or a serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor such as duloxetine, titrating to effect","Use topical agents such as lidocaine or capsaicin for localised peripheral neuropathic pain","Reserve opioids such as tramadol as later-line options given limited long-term benefit and harms, avoiding them as first choice","Consider syndrome-specific agents, such as carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine for trigeminal neuralgia","Combine pharmacotherapy with physiotherapy, psychological therapy and a multimodal self-management approach","Refer to a specialist pain service for interventional options such as nerve blocks or neuromodulation when pain is refractory","Set realistic functional goals and review efficacy and adverse effects regularly, rotating or combining agents as needed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braddom's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuropathic_ulcer":{"name":"Neuropathic ulcer","system":"Nervous system / Skin (foot)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neurotrophic ulcer","mal perforans","trophic ulcer"],"chapter":null,"source":"IWGDF / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Neuropathic ulcers develop over pressure-bearing areas in patients with sensory, and often autonomic and motor, peripheral neuropathy, where loss of protective sensation allows repetitive unnoticed trauma","They are classically painless, punched-out ulcers rimmed by callus, located over weight-bearing sites such as the metatarsal heads, plantar surface and heel, the plantar lesion being termed mal perforans","Diabetes mellitus is the commonest cause worldwide, but leprosy, alcohol-related and nutritional neuropathies including B12 deficiency, hereditary sensory neuropathies and tabes dorsalis are also recognised","Motor neuropathy causes intrinsic muscle wasting and foot deformity such as clawing and prominent metatarsal heads, which increase focal pressure, while autonomic neuropathy produces dry, fissured skin prone to breakdown","Peripheral pulses are usually present and the foot is often warm and well perfused, distinguishing a purely neuropathic from an ischaemic ulcer","Assessment includes testing protective sensation with a monofilament and vibration, inspecting for deformity and callus, and evaluating perfusion to detect any coexisting arterial disease","Management centres on offloading pressure with total-contact casting, therapeutic footwear or orthoses, regular debridement of callus and wound, infection control and treatment of the underlying cause","Charcot neuroarthropathy should be considered in a warm, swollen, deformed neuropathic foot, as it can coexist with or mimic infection"],"pathophysiology":"Sensory neuropathy removes the pain that normally prompts a person to offload an injured area, so repetitive mechanical stress at pressure points goes unrecognised and produces callus, subkeratotic haemorrhage and eventual tissue breakdown. Motor neuropathy weakens the intrinsic foot muscles and alters the architecture of the foot, redistributing load onto the metatarsal heads and other prominences and raising focal plantar pressures. Autonomic neuropathy reduces sweating, leaving dry, fissured skin and altering local blood flow through arteriovenous shunting. Together these deficits generate a chronic ulcer over a pressure point that will not heal, and will recur, unless the mechanical load is redistributed.","additionalDifferentials":["Arterial (ischaemic) ulcer","Diabetic foot ulcer with a neuroischaemic component","Venous leg ulcer","Infected ulcer with underlying osteomyelitis","Charcot neuroarthropathy with ulceration","Malignant ulcer such as a Marjolin ulcer","Plantar callus or verruca without true ulceration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Testing of protective sensation with a monofilament and of vibration or quantitative sensory thresholds","Ankle-brachial index, toe pressures and duplex to assess perfusion","Plain radiograph and MRI to detect osteomyelitis or Charcot changes","Wound or deep tissue culture and inflammatory markers if infection is suspected","Blood glucose and glycated haemoglobin, vitamin B12 and other tests to identify the cause of neuropathy","Nerve conduction studies where the underlying cause is unclear"],"redFlags":["Signs of deep infection or osteomyelitis such as the ability to probe to bone, purulent discharge or systemic features","A warm, swollen, deformed foot suggesting Charcot neuroarthropathy","Spreading cellulitis or systemic sepsis","New ischaemic changes indicating superimposed arterial disease"],"prognosis":"With effective offloading and management of the underlying cause, neuropathic ulcers often heal, but recurrence is high because the neuropathy is usually irreversible, and deep infection can threaten the limb.","pitfalls":["Overlooking coexisting arterial disease in an apparently neuropathic ulcer","Providing inadequate offloading so the ulcer persists","Missing underlying osteomyelitis","Confusing Charcot neuroarthropathy with infection or vice versa","Failing to investigate non-diabetic causes of neuropathy, or assuming that painlessness excludes serious infection"],"management":["Redistribute mechanical load by offloading with total-contact casting, removable walker boots or bespoke footwear and orthoses, the cornerstone of healing","Debride callus and non-viable tissue and provide regular wound care with appropriate dressings and moisture balance","Treat the underlying cause of the neuropathy, optimising glycaemic control in diabetes, replacing vitamin B12 and addressing alcohol or nutritional deficiency","Assess perfusion and refer for revascularisation where coexisting arterial disease is found","Treat infection with antibiotics guided by cultures and investigate for osteomyelitis and Charcot changes when suspected","Educate on daily foot inspection, skin care and emollients for dry fissured skin, and provide correct footwear to prevent recurrence","Refer to a multidisciplinary foot-care team spanning podiatry, diabetology, vascular surgery and orthotics for ongoing surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Working Group on the Diabetic Foot (IWGDF) guidelines","NICE guidance on diabetic foot problems","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"neurosarcoidosis":{"name":"Neurosarcoidosis","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neurological sarcoidosis","CNS sarcoidosis","sarcoid neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / consensus neurosarcoidosis guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Cranial neuropathy is the commonest presentation, with facial nerve palsy the classic finding, often bilateral","Basal meningeal involvement favours the hypothalamic-pituitary axis, causing diabetes insipidus and other endocrine deficits","Presentations are protean and include aseptic meningitis, myelopathy, seizures, hydrocephalus, and peripheral neuropathy","Only a minority have isolated neurological disease; most have systemic sarcoidosis providing an accessible biopsy target","Contrast MRI often shows leptomeningeal enhancement, dural masses, or parenchymal and spinal cord lesions","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating non-caseating granulomas while rigorously excluding infection and lymphoma","Corticosteroids are first-line, with steroid-sparing immunosuppression and anti-TNF agents for refractory disease"],"pathophysiology":"Sarcoidosis is a multisystem granulomatous disorder in which an exaggerated cell-mediated immune response leads to non-caseating epithelioid granulomas. When the nervous system is affected, granulomatous inflammation tends to concentrate around the basal meninges and perivascular spaces, spreading along Virchow-Robin spaces into brain parenchyma, cranial nerves, the hypothalamic-pituitary region, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves. The inflammatory infiltrate and secondary vasculopathy produce the diverse neurological syndromes seen, and chronic meningeal involvement can obstruct cerebrospinal fluid flow to cause hydrocephalus.","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculous or fungal meningitis","CNS lymphoma or leptomeningeal carcinomatosis","Multiple sclerosis and other demyelinating disease","IgG4-related disease and other granulomatous vasculitides","Idiopathic hypertrophic pachymeningitis","Neuro-Behcet and neurosyphilis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced brain and spinal MRI to map meningeal and parenchymal involvement","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis for lymphocytic pleocytosis, raised protein, and exclusion of infection and malignancy","Serum and CSF angiotensin-converting enzyme with awareness of limited sensitivity and specificity","Whole-body imaging such as CT chest and FDG-PET to find accessible extraneural biopsy sites","Tissue biopsy demonstrating non-caseating granulomas with special stains and cultures to exclude infection"],"redFlags":["Acute hydrocephalus or raised intracranial pressure from meningeal disease","Hypothalamic-pituitary failure with hyponatraemia or adrenal insufficiency","Rapidly progressive myelopathy with sphincter involvement","Diagnostic uncertainty where lymphoma or infection has not been excluded"],"prognosis":"Many patients respond to corticosteroids, but the course is often chronic or relapsing and some develop fixed neurological deficits; parenchymal and spinal disease and hydrocephalus carry a less favourable outlook.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing neurosarcoidosis on imaging alone without histological or robust supportive evidence","Missing coexisting infection because granulomas can occur in tuberculosis and fungal disease","Under-treating and allowing relapse when corticosteroids are tapered too quickly","Overlooking neuroendocrine dysfunction in basal meningeal involvement"],"management":["Induce remission with corticosteroids, the mainstay of therapy, after rigorously excluding infection and lymphoma","Add steroid-sparing immunosuppressants such as methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate for chronic, relapsing or steroid-dependent disease","Escalate refractory disease to TNF-alpha inhibitors such as infliximab","Divert cerebrospinal fluid with a ventriculoperitoneal shunt where meningeal disease causes obstructive hydrocephalus","Replace deficient hormones for hypothalamic-pituitary involvement, including desmopressin for diabetes insipidus","Control seizures with antiepileptic drugs where present","Monitor with serial contrast-enhanced MRI, treat the underlying systemic sarcoidosis, and coordinate neurology with respiratory and multisystem specialists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuroserpin_inclusion_encephalopathy":{"name":"Familial encephalopathy with neuroserpin inclusion bodies (FENIB)","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FENIB","familial encephalopathy with neuroserpin inclusion bodies","neuroserpin inclusion encephalopathy","SERPINI1-related encephalopathy","SERPINI1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in the neuroserpin gene (SERPINI1), belonging to the serpinopathies (conformational protein-polymerisation diseases)","Presents with progressive dementia and, in many patients, myoclonus and seizures, with the age of onset and severity linked to the specific mutation","Pathology shows characteristic neuronal intracytoplasmic inclusions, called Collins bodies, composed of polymerised mutant neuroserpin","More disruptive mutations generally produce earlier onset and more severe disease, illustrating a genotype-phenotype correlation","The mechanism parallels other serpinopathies such as alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, in which mutant serpin molecules polymerise and are retained within cells","A family history of early dementia with myoclonus or epilepsy should raise suspicion","Diagnosis rests on the clinical picture, family history, SERPINI1 genetic testing and, where available, characteristic pathology"],"pathophysiology":"Familial encephalopathy with neuroserpin inclusion bodies is an autosomal dominant serpinopathy caused by mutations in SERPINI1, the gene encoding neuroserpin, a serine protease inhibitor secreted by neurones. The mutant protein is prone to aberrant conformational change and polymerisation, so that molecules link together and are retained within the endoplasmic reticulum of neurones, forming intracytoplasmic inclusions known as Collins bodies. The resulting protein overload and neuronal dysfunction cause progressive encephalopathy; the extent of polymerisation and inclusion burden, determined by the particular mutation, correlates with the age of onset and disease severity, ranging from a later-onset dementia to an early, severe encephalopathy with epilepsy.","additionalDifferentials":["Progressive myoclonic epilepsies of other cause","Alzheimer disease and other early-onset dementias","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease and other prion diseases","Lafora disease and neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses","Frontotemporal dementia","Autoimmune or infective encephalitis","Other genetic neurodegenerative encephalopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SERPINI1 gene testing to confirm the diagnosis","MRI of the brain to assess atrophy and exclude alternative pathology","Electroencephalography to characterise epileptic and myoclonic activity","Neuropsychological assessment to document the cognitive decline","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis to help exclude inflammatory, infective and prion causes","Neuropathological examination demonstrating neuroserpin inclusions where available"],"redFlags":["Frequent or poorly controlled seizures and disabling myoclonus","Rapidly progressive encephalopathy, particularly with early-onset mutations","Cognitive decline compromising safety and capacity","Family history indicating dominant inheritance with implications for relatives"],"prognosis":"The disorder is progressive, and prognosis depends strongly on the mutation, with earlier-onset forms causing severe encephalopathy and epilepsy and later-onset forms a slower dementia; management is supportive, including seizure and myoclonus control and genetic counselling.","pitfalls":["Not considering a serpinopathy in a familial dementia accompanied by myoclonus or epilepsy","Confusing the presentation with prion disease or other progressive myoclonic epilepsies without genetic testing","Overlooking the genotype-phenotype correlation when counselling about prognosis","Failing to offer genetic counselling to at-risk relatives given the dominant inheritance"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists for this serpinopathy, so management is supportive","Control seizures and myoclonus with antiepileptic drugs, avoiding agents that aggravate myoclonus","Provide cognitive assessment, dementia support and caregiver input for the progressive decline","Offer physiotherapy, occupational therapy and rehabilitation for motor and functional deterioration","Coordinate multidisciplinary neurology, palliative care and social support","Offer genetic counselling and predictive family testing for this autosomal dominant SERPINI1 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"neurotoxic_shellfish_poisoning":{"name":"Neurotoxic shellfish poisoning","system":"Nervous system / Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NSP","Brevetoxin poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by brevetoxins, lipid-soluble polyether toxins produced by the dinoflagellate Karenia brevis responsible for Florida-type and Gulf of Mexico red tides, accumulated in filter-feeding shellfish.","Characteristic feature is reversal of temperature sensation, in which cold objects feel burning hot and hot objects feel cold, alongside circumoral and distal paraesthesiae.","Brevetoxins bind voltage-gated sodium channels and hold them open, causing persistent neuronal and muscle depolarisation, in contrast to the channel-blocking saxitoxins of paralytic shellfish poisoning.","Illness combines gastrointestinal symptoms with sensory neurological features, ataxia, vertigo and myalgia, but unlike paralytic poisoning life-threatening flaccid paralysis is rare.","Aerosolised brevetoxin from surf and sea spray during a red tide causes a distinct inhalational syndrome of cough, wheeze, rhinorrhoea and eye irritation, especially troublesome in people with asthma.","The toxin is heat-stable so contaminated shellfish remain hazardous after cooking, and affected shellfish look and taste normal.","Diagnosis is clinical and epidemiological, confirmed by brevetoxin assay of shellfish or body fluids at reference laboratories; management is supportive with no specific antidote.","Symptoms usually resolve within a few days, and the reported case-fatality is very low compared with paralytic shellfish poisoning."],"pathophysiology":"Neurotoxic shellfish poisoning is produced by brevetoxins, a family of lipophilic cyclic polyether neurotoxins elaborated by the marine dinoflagellate Karenia brevis, whose proliferation generates the characteristic red tides. Filter-feeding bivalves concentrate the toxin, which is also released into sea-spray aerosols when wave action lyses the organisms. Brevetoxins bind with high affinity to a specific receptor site on voltage-gated sodium channels of excitable membranes, shifting their activation to more negative potentials and inhibiting inactivation. The channels therefore remain persistently open, causing repetitive firing, sustained depolarisation and enhanced neurotransmitter release across sensory nerves, motor nerves and autonomic fibres. This mechanism, the opposite of the sodium-channel blockade caused by saxitoxin in paralytic shellfish poisoning, explains the prominent sensory disturbances including paradoxical temperature reversal, paraesthesiae and myalgia, together with gastrointestinal and mild motor features. When inhaled, the same channel activation on airway sensory nerves and smooth muscle provokes bronchoconstriction, cough and mucosal irritation. Because the toxins are heat-stable and lipid-soluble, cooking does not detoxify contaminated shellfish.","additionalDifferentials":["Paralytic shellfish poisoning (saxitoxin) with more severe paralysis","Ciguatera fish poisoning, which also causes temperature-sensation reversal and paraesthesiae","Amnesic shellfish poisoning (domoic acid)","Scombroid (histamine) fish poisoning","Acute asthma or reactive airways from another trigger in the inhalational form","Viral or bacterial gastroenteritis","Anxiety or hyperventilation syndrome producing paraesthesiae"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brevetoxin detection in implicated shellfish and in serum or urine at a reference laboratory","Pulse oximetry, peak flow or spirometry in patients with inhalational or respiratory symptoms","Chest radiograph if lower-respiratory involvement or aspiration is suspected","Serum electrolytes and renal function to guide fluid and supportive management","Electrocardiogram and cardiac monitoring in patients with autonomic symptoms","Neurological examination documenting sensory reversal, ataxia and reflexes","Environmental and public-health confirmation of a concurrent Karenia brevis bloom"],"redFlags":["Reversal of hot and cold sensation after a shellfish meal or coastal exposure","Bronchospasm, severe wheeze or respiratory distress in the inhalational form","Ataxia, vertigo or difficulty with coordinated movement","Marked bradycardia or other autonomic disturbance","Symptoms in a person with pre-existing asthma or chronic lung disease"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally excellent, with most patients recovering fully within a few days and deaths being exceptionally rare. Inhalational symptoms settle once exposure to the aerosol ceases, although those with asthma may have a more troublesome course.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with the more dangerous paralytic shellfish poisoning and either over- or under-treating","Mistaking the inhalational syndrome for ordinary asthma or an allergic reaction and missing the red-tide link","Assuming cooking renders contaminated shellfish safe, as brevetoxins are heat-stable","Discounting temperature-sensation reversal as a functional complaint","Overlooking vulnerable patients with chronic respiratory disease exposed to sea-spray aerosol"],"management":["Provide supportive care as the mainstay, since the illness is self-limiting and has no specific antidote","Remove the source by stopping ingestion of contaminated shellfish, which remain hazardous after cooking as the toxin is heat-stable, and moving affected people away from red-tide aerosol exposure","Give antiemetics and rehydrate with fluids for gastrointestinal losses","Manage inhalational symptoms by removal from the aerosol and use of bronchodilators and inhaled therapy, with heightened vigilance in people with asthma","Monitor oxygenation and respiratory function and support ventilation in the rare severe case","Observe with cardiac monitoring where autonomic features are present, and provide analgesia and reassurance for paraesthesiae and myalgia","Notify public-health authorities to support red-tide surveillance and shellfish-bed closure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical toxicology textbooks","Marine biotoxin and harmful-algal-bloom guidance documents","Emergency medicine reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"neutropenic_enterocolitis":{"name":"neutropenic enterocolitis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["typhlitis","neutropenic colitis","ileocaecal syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oxford Handbook of Oncology / Infectious Diseases Society of America neutropenia guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A necrotising inflammation of the caecum and right colon (typhlitis) occurring in profound neutropenia, classically after intensive chemotherapy for acute leukaemia and in the post-transplant setting.","The characteristic clinical triad is fever, right iliac fossa pain and neutropenia, often with watery or bloody diarrhoea, nausea and abdominal distension.","CT of the abdomen is the imaging modality of choice, showing circumferential bowel-wall thickening (typically greater than four millimetres), caecal distension, pericolic fat stranding and, in severe disease, pneumatosis intestinalis.","Colonoscopy and contrast enema should be avoided because of the high risk of perforation in inflamed, friable, neutropenic bowel.","Management is predominantly conservative: bowel rest, nasogastric decompression if obstructed, intravenous fluids, and broad-spectrum antibiotics covering Gram-negative organisms and anaerobes, with antifungal cover added for persistent fever.","Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor and correction of thrombocytopenia and coagulopathy support recovery, which closely tracks neutrophil count recovery.","Surgery is reserved for defined indications: free perforation, uncontrolled gastrointestinal haemorrhage, persistent obstruction, or clinical deterioration despite maximal medical therapy.","Distinguish from Clostridioides difficile colitis and acute appendicitis, and continue treatment until both symptoms resolve and neutropenia recovers, as relapse can occur with subsequent chemotherapy cycles."],"pathophysiology":"Chemotherapy-induced mucosal injury combines with profound neutropenia to breach the bowel's defences, most severely in the caecum, whose distensibility and relatively poor blood supply predispose it to injury. Loss of mucosal integrity allows intramural invasion by gut flora—Gram-negative bacilli, anaerobes and fungi such as Candida—producing oedema, transmural inflammation, haemorrhage and necrosis. The absence of an effective neutrophil response permits unchecked bacterial proliferation and translocation, driving bacteraemia and sepsis; progressive wall necrosis may culminate in pneumatosis and perforation.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute appendicitis","Clostridioides difficile / pseudomembranous colitis","Ischaemic colitis","Cytomegalovirus colitis","Gastrointestinal graft-versus-host disease","Intra-abdominal abscess","Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie's syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count confirming neutropenia and assessing platelets","Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen and pelvis","Blood cultures","Stool for Clostridioides difficile toxin and enteric pathogens","Serum lactate and venous blood gas","Coagulation screen","Serial abdominal examination with erect and supine abdominal radiographs if perforation is suspected"],"redFlags":["Peritonitis or radiological free intraperitoneal air","Significant gastrointestinal haemorrhage","Haemodynamic instability or septic shock","Pneumatosis intestinalis on imaging","Rising lactate or persistent deterioration despite therapy"],"prognosis":"Outcome is closely tied to neutrophil recovery; with prompt supportive care and antimicrobials many patients recover fully. Perforation, uncontrolled haemorrhage or full-thickness necrosis markedly worsen prognosis and carry a high mortality.","pitfalls":["Performing colonoscopy or contrast enema and precipitating perforation","Delaying broad-spectrum antibiotics while awaiting imaging or cultures","Operating for presumed appendicitis when conservative management is appropriate","Failing to add antifungal cover for persistent fever","Stopping treatment once the patient is afebrile while still neutropenic"],"management":["Manage conservatively with bowel rest, nasogastric decompression if obstructed and intravenous fluid resuscitation","Start broad-spectrum antibiotics covering Gram-negative bacilli and anaerobes, adding antifungal cover such as an echinocandin or azole for persistent fever","Support neutrophil recovery with granulocyte colony-stimulating factor, since outcome tracks the returning neutrophil count","Correct thrombocytopenia and coagulopathy with blood-product support","Avoid colonoscopy and contrast enema because of the high perforation risk in friable neutropenic bowel","Monitor with serial examination, abdominal imaging and lactate for perforation, uncontrolled haemorrhage or transmural necrosis","Involve surgery for operative intervention if perforation, full-thickness necrosis or uncontrolled bleeding develops","Coordinate haematology and oncology care, including review and timing of the causative chemotherapy regimen"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Oxford Handbook of Oncology","Infectious Diseases Society of America guideline on the management of neutropenic fever","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"nevus_comedonicus_syndrome":{"name":"Naevus comedonicus syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Neurocutaneous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nevus comedonicus syndrome","comedo naevus syndrome","NCS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Neurocutaneous Disorders (current reference texts)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by a naevus comedonicus, grouped dilated follicular openings plugged with keratin resembling clustered comedones, in a segmental Blaschko-linear pattern, together with extracutaneous anomalies","It is a mosaic disorder, with post-zygotic activating NEK9 variants reported, and shares the epidermal-naevus spectrum classification","The naevus represents a hamartoma of the pilosebaceous unit rather than true acne, though it may become inflamed with cysts and abscesses","Central nervous system associations include seizures, developmental delay and structural brain anomalies","Skeletal defects such as scoliosis, vertebral and limb anomalies, and ipsilateral bony changes may occur","Ocular associations such as cataract and other anterior-segment anomalies warrant ophthalmology review","Management is generic and largely symptomatic: keratolytics and topical retinoids for the naevus, treatment of secondary infection, and surgery for troublesome or extensive lesions"],"pathophysiology":"Naevus comedonicus syndrome arises from a post-zygotic mosaic variant, with activating NEK9 variants described, affecting a clone of follicular keratinocytes during embryogenesis. This produces a hamartomatous malformation of the pilosebaceous unit in which rudimentary follicles fail to form normal hair and sebaceous structures and instead retain keratin, giving the characteristic comedo-like plugs distributed along the lines of Blaschko. Secondary inflammation, cyst formation and bacterial infection can complicate the lesion. When the mosaic clone extends to neuroectodermal, ocular and skeletal progenitors, the associated central nervous system, ocular and skeletal malformations of the syndrome develop.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated naevus comedonicus without systemic features","Acne vulgaris and acne conglobata","Dilated pore of Winer and comedonal cysts","Familial dyskeratotic comedones","Linear epidermal naevus","Steatocystoma multiplex"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy to confirm the follicular hamartoma","MRI of the brain where seizures or developmental delay are present","Spinal and limb radiographs for scoliosis and skeletal anomalies","Ophthalmological assessment for cataract and anterior-segment defects","Swabs and cultures where secondary infection or abscess is suspected","Molecular analysis of lesional tissue for mosaic NEK9 variants where available"],"redFlags":["New-onset or refractory seizures","Progressive neurological deficit or developmental regression","Recurrent abscess or deep infection within the naevus","Progressive scoliosis or skeletal deformity"],"prognosis":"The cutaneous lesion is benign but often cosmetically and symptomatically troublesome, and prognosis overall depends on the severity of any neurological, ocular or skeletal involvement, which determines the need for long-term multidisciplinary care.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the naevus for ordinary acne and missing the segmental hamartomatous pattern","Overlooking neurological or skeletal associations in extensive or facial disease","Underestimating the tendency to recurrent inflammation and secondary infection","Neglecting ophthalmological screening for cataract and anterior-segment anomalies"],"management":["Treat the cutaneous naevus with topical keratolytics and retinoids such as tretinoin, or topical vitamin D analogues, to reduce comedonal plugging","Manage inflammatory complications of cysts, abscesses and secondary infection with antibiotics guided by swabs, plus incision and drainage where needed","Consider surgical excision or ablative laser for troublesome or cosmetically distressing lesions","Control associated seizures with antiepileptic drugs and provide developmental support for central nervous system involvement","Manage skeletal anomalies such as scoliosis and limb defects with orthopaedic and physiotherapy input","Arrange ophthalmology review for cataract and other anterior-segment anomalies","Coordinate multidisciplinary dermatology, neurology, orthopaedics and ophthalmology care given the syndromic associations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Happle R. Mosaicism in Human Skin"],"crossLinks":[]},"nevus_depigmentosus":{"name":"Naevus depigmentosus","system":"Dermatology / Pigmentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nevus depigmentosus","naevus achromicus","achromic naevus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital or early-onset, well-defined, off-white hypopigmented (not fully depigmented) patch that is stable in relative size and does not progress","Often has a serrated or block-like/segmental configuration and remains fixed in its distribution","Distinguished from vitiligo, which shows chalky-white complete depigmentation and tends to spread, and in which Wood-lamp accentuation is far more stark","Differentiated from naevus anaemicus, a vascular anomaly that vanishes with diascopy and lacks true pigment loss","Reflects a functional melanocyte defect with normal or slightly reduced melanocyte numbers but impaired melanosome transfer","It is benign; management is reassurance, with cosmetic camouflage and photoprotection of the pale area if desired","The patch grows in proportion to the child rather than extending independently"],"pathophysiology":"Naevus depigmentosus is a mosaic developmental defect in which melanocytes are present in normal or slightly reduced numbers but function abnormally, with defective melanosome formation and impaired transfer of melanosomes to keratinocytes. Melanin is therefore reduced rather than absent, producing hypopigmentation rather than the complete depigmentation of vitiligo. Because it is a fixed developmental anomaly, the lesion remains stable throughout life.","additionalDifferentials":["Vitiligo, particularly the segmental type","Naevus anaemicus","Ash-leaf macule of tuberous sclerosis","Hypomelanosis of Ito","Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation","Pityriasis alba","Idiopathic guttate hypomelanosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wood-lamp examination showing off-white rather than chalky-white change","Dermoscopy of the patch","Diascopy to exclude naevus anaemicus","Examination for other features of tuberous sclerosis or mosaic disorders","Skin biopsy only rarely, when the diagnosis is uncertain"],"redFlags":["Multiple ash-leaf macules or other features suggesting tuberous sclerosis","Segmental depigmentation that is spreading, raising the possibility of vitiligo","An extensive whorled or streaky pattern with systemic features suggesting hypomelanosis of Ito"],"prognosis":"The lesion is benign, stable and permanent, enlarging only in proportion to body growth, and carries no health risk beyond its cosmetic effect.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as vitiligo, or vice versa","Overlooking tuberous sclerosis when hypopigmented macules are multiple","Confusing it with naevus anaemicus by omitting diascopy","Attempting active repigmentation of a stable developmental lesion"],"management":["Reassure that the lesion is a benign, stable developmental anomaly that needs no active medical treatment","Offer cosmetic camouflage for the hypopigmented patch where it is cosmetically troublesome","Advise photoprotection with sunscreen of the pale area, which lacks protective melanin and burns readily","Confirm the diagnosis clinically, distinguishing it from vitiligo and naevus anaemicus, reserving biopsy for uncertain cases","Examine for features of tuberous sclerosis or other mosaic disorders where the presentation is atypical","Monitor for stability, expecting the lesion to enlarge only in proportion to body growth"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"nevus_of_ito":{"name":"Naevus of Ito","system":"Dermatology / Pigmentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nevus of Ito","naevus fuscoceruleus acromiodeltoideus","acromiodeltoid dermal melanocytosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Blue-grey dermal melanocytic pigmentation over the shoulder, supraclavicular, scapular and deltoid region, following the posterior supraclavicular and lateral brachial cutaneous nerves","Analogous to naevus of Ota but affecting the shoulder girdle rather than the face, and without ocular involvement","May coexist with an ipsilateral naevus of Ota in the same individual","The pigment is dermal, so it shows no Wood-lamp accentuation and topical agents are ineffective","More common in Asian populations and in females","Pigment-specific laser is the mainstay of treatment where cosmetic improvement is desired","Malignant transformation is very rare but has been reported, so persistent change warrants review"],"pathophysiology":"As in naevus of Ota, the lesion results from melanocytes of neural crest origin that fail to complete their migration to the epidermis and remain within the dermis. These dermal melanocytes produce melanin whose deep location scatters light to give the blue-grey colour. The distribution corresponds to the shoulder-girdle cutaneous nerves rather than the trigeminal territory.","additionalDifferentials":["Mongolian spot (congenital dermal melanocytosis)","Naevus of Ota","Blue naevus","Bruise or resolving haematoma","Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation","Congenital melanocytic naevus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dermoscopy to confirm the dermal melanocytic pattern","Full skin examination to detect a coexisting naevus of Ota","Serial photography to monitor stability","Wood-lamp examination to confirm the pigment is dermal","Biopsy of any nodular or atypical area to exclude melanoma"],"redFlags":["Development of a nodule or focal change suggesting melanoma","Rapid growth or ulceration within the lesion","New symptoms such as bleeding or persistent tenderness"],"prognosis":"The lesion is benign and persists lifelong if untreated, with good cosmetic response to pigment-specific laser and only a very small risk of malignant change.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the blue-grey patch for a persistent bruise","Attempting to treat dermal pigment with topical lightening agents","Failing to look for a coexisting naevus of Ota","Overlooking a rare melanoma developing within the naevus"],"management":["Reassure that the lesion is benign, undertaking treatment only for cosmetic reasons","Offer pigment-specific Q-switched or picosecond laser as the mainstay of cosmetic treatment, noting that topical agents are ineffective on dermal pigment","Examine the whole skin for a coexisting ipsilateral naevus of Ota","Monitor with serial photography for stability","Biopsy any nodular or atypical area to exclude melanoma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"nevus_of_ota":{"name":"Naevus of Ota","system":"Dermatology / Pigmentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nevus of Ota","oculodermal melanocytosis","naevus fuscoceruleus ophthalmomaxillaris"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Unilateral, mottled blue-grey to brown pigmentation in the distribution of the ophthalmic (V1) and maxillary (V2) divisions of the trigeminal nerve","Frequently involves the sclera, conjunctiva and other ocular structures (oculodermal melanocytosis), and ipsilateral glaucoma can develop, so ophthalmology review is important","More common in individuals of Asian and African descent and in females; it may be present at birth or first appear around puberty","The pigment is dermal, so it is not accentuated by Wood lamp and topical lightening agents are ineffective","Pigment-specific Q-switched or picosecond lasers are the treatment of choice for the cutaneous component","Rarely, melanoma may arise within cutaneous, uveal or leptomeningeal melanocytes, so new nodules or visual change require prompt assessment","Bilateral involvement is uncommon but recognised and warrants consideration of associated conditions"],"pathophysiology":"During embryogenesis melanocytes derived from the neural crest normally migrate to the epidermis, but in naevus of Ota some are arrested within the dermis. These entrapped, melanin-producing dermal melanocytes scatter light to give a characteristic blue-grey (Tyndall) hue, and their distribution follows the ophthalmic and maxillary trigeminal branches. The same melanocytic population can involve ocular tissues, accounting for scleral pigmentation and the associated glaucoma and, rarely, melanoma risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Mongolian spot (congenital dermal melanocytosis)","Naevus of Ito","Blue naevus","Melasma","Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation","Minocycline or other drug-induced pigmentation","Congenital melanocytic naevus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ophthalmic examination with intraocular pressure and glaucoma screening","Dermoscopy to characterise the dermal pigment pattern","Serial photography to monitor for change","Biopsy of any nodular or clinically suspicious area to exclude melanoma","Neuroimaging if leptomeningeal melanosis or neurological features are suspected"],"redFlags":["A new nodule, thickening or focal change suggesting cutaneous or uveal melanoma","Rising intraocular pressure or visual disturbance indicating glaucoma","Neurological symptoms raising concern for leptomeningeal melanocytosis","Rapid extension or ulceration of the pigmented area"],"prognosis":"The naevus is benign and persists lifelong if untreated, with good cosmetic improvement achievable using pigment-specific lasers; the main long-term concerns are glaucoma and a small risk of ocular or cutaneous melanoma.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the need for ophthalmic review and glaucoma screening","Attempting to treat the dermal pigment with topical lightening agents","Missing an evolving melanoma within the lesion or eye","Confusing it with a Mongolian spot or acquired melasma"],"management":["Reassure that the lesion is benign and treat the cutaneous component for cosmetic reasons with pigment-specific Q-switched or picosecond lasers, as topical lightening agents are ineffective on dermal pigment","Arrange ophthalmology review with intraocular pressure measurement and glaucoma screening for oculodermal melanocytosis","Undertake long-term surveillance for melanoma arising in cutaneous, uveal or leptomeningeal melanocytes, assessing new nodules or visual change promptly","Monitor with serial photography for change","Biopsy any nodular or clinically suspicious area to exclude melanoma","Arrange neuroimaging if leptomeningeal melanosis or neurological features are suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"nevus_sebaceus":{"name":"Naevus sebaceus of Jadassohn","system":"Dermatology / Neurocutaneous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nevus sebaceus","naevus sebaceus","sebaceous naevus","organoid naevus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Neurocutaneous Disorders (current reference texts)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital organoid hamartoma of the epidermis and adnexa, typically presenting as a hairless yellow-orange plaque on the scalp or face","It is a mosaic lesion caused by post-zygotic activating RAS-pathway variants, usually HRAS and less often KRAS","The lesion evolves with age, becoming smooth and flat in infancy, then verrucous and nodular around puberty under hormonal influence","The characteristic scalp lesion is associated with overlying alopecia because normal follicles are absent","It carries a lifelong risk of secondary tumours, most commonly benign trichoblastoma and syringocystadenoma papilliferum, with basal cell carcinoma the main malignant concern","Extensive lesions may be part of Schimmelpenning syndrome with cerebral, ocular and skeletal anomalies, so wide involvement prompts systemic assessment","Management is generic: observation with clinical surveillance, and consideration of complete excision where there is cosmetic concern or a suspicious secondary growth"],"pathophysiology":"Naevus sebaceus of Jadassohn is a mosaic organoid hamartoma resulting from a post-zygotic activating variant in the RAS-MAPK pathway, most often HRAS and occasionally KRAS, arising in an epidermal progenitor during development. The affected clone produces disordered overgrowth of the epidermis together with sebaceous glands, abortive hair follicles and apocrine structures, giving the lesion its organoid character and its distribution along the lines of Blaschko. Persistent RAS-pathway activation drives the pubertal thickening of the lesion and predisposes to the development of secondary benign and, less commonly, malignant appendageal tumours over the patient's lifetime.","additionalDifferentials":["Aplasia cutis congenita","Juvenile xanthogranuloma","Epidermal (keratinocytic) naevus","Solitary mastocytoma","Syringocystadenoma papilliferum arising de novo","Trichoblastoma or basal cell carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical and dermoscopic assessment of the plaque","Skin biopsy where diagnosis is uncertain or a secondary tumour is suspected","Histopathology of any excised lesion to identify secondary neoplasia","MRI of the brain where the naevus is extensive or neurological features suggest Schimmelpenning syndrome","Ophthalmological review in extensive head-and-neck disease","Molecular testing of lesional tissue for HRAS/KRAS variants where available"],"redFlags":["New nodule, ulceration or bleeding within the plaque suggesting a secondary malignancy","Rapid change in a long-stable lesion","Extensive lesions with seizures or developmental delay indicating a neurocutaneous syndrome","Ocular or skeletal anomalies in association with a large naevus"],"prognosis":"Isolated naevus sebaceus has an excellent prognosis; the main long-term consideration is the modest risk of secondary tumours, predominantly benign, so periodic surveillance or elective excision is reasonable, while extensive lesions require assessment for associated syndromic disease.","pitfalls":["Assuming any secondary growth is malignant when benign trichoblastoma is far more common","Failing to reassess a lesion that changes in adulthood","Not screening for systemic involvement when the naevus is extensive","Excising purely for a low malignancy risk without discussing that benign tumours predominate"],"management":["Manage isolated, asymptomatic lesions by periodic clinical and dermoscopic surveillance, counselling that the mosaic RAS-pathway lesion is benign but carries a lifelong risk of secondary appendageal tumours.","Offer elective full-thickness surgical excision for cosmetic concern, symptomatic lesions or where reliable surveillance is impractical, sending the specimen for histopathology.","Promptly biopsy or excise any new nodule, ulceration, bleeding or accelerated growth to exclude secondary neoplasia, commonly benign trichoblastoma or syringocystadenoma papilliferum and less often basal cell carcinoma.","Time excision with awareness of pubertal thickening, and counsel on the associated localised alopecia over scalp lesions and on scarring or reconstruction where relevant.","Assess extensive or multiple lesions for Schimmelpenning syndrome, arranging brain MRI and neurological review where cerebral involvement or seizures are suspected.","Arrange ophthalmological review for extensive head-and-neck disease and paediatric developmental assessment as part of syndromic evaluation.","Explain the post-zygotic mosaic aetiology when counselling families: the lesion is not inherited and recurrence risk in future pregnancies is not increased."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Happle R. Mosaicism in Human Skin"],"crossLinks":[]},"new_daily_persistent_headache":{"name":"New daily persistent headache","system":"Nervous system / Headache","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NDPH"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / International Classification of Sleep Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by an abrupt onset of persistent daily headache that becomes unremitting within a day, in a patient who can often name the exact date it began","The headache is continuous from close to its onset, unlike chronic migraine which evolves from episodic attacks","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, and secondary causes with a similar abrupt daily pattern must be sought","The phenotype may resemble migraine or tension-type headache, which does not exclude the diagnosis","A preceding infection, stressful life event or minor procedure is sometimes reported around the time of onset","Important secondary mimics include low- and high-cerebrospinal-fluid-pressure headaches, cerebral venous sinus thrombosis and, in older patients, giant cell arteritis","It is often refractory to treatment, which is generally guided by the predominant migrainous or tension-type phenotype"],"pathophysiology":"The mechanism of new daily persistent headache is unclear. Proposed contributors include a post-infectious or post-inflammatory state with persistent cytokine activation, cervical or ligamentous factors, and disordered central pain processing. Because the abrupt, unremitting pattern can also be produced by disorders of cerebrospinal fluid pressure or venous drainage, these secondary processes must be excluded before the primary label is applied.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic migraine","Chronic tension-type headache","Low-cerebrospinal-fluid-pressure (spontaneous intracranial hypotension) headache","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Giant cell arteritis in older patients","Medication-overuse headache"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI brain with contrast, looking for pachymeningeal enhancement or other secondary signs","MR or CT venography to exclude cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Consideration of lumbar puncture with opening pressure when a cerebrospinal-fluid-pressure disorder is suspected","Inflammatory markers such as ESR and CRP in older patients to screen for giant cell arteritis","Review of headache-onset timing, triggers and medication use"],"redFlags":["A postural component suggesting a cerebrospinal-fluid-pressure disorder","Papilloedema, visual symptoms or pulsatile tinnitus","Focal neurological deficit, seizures or a thunderclap onset","Age over fifty with scalp tenderness or jaw claudication","Fever, neck stiffness or immunocompromise"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable: some patients remit spontaneously over months, while a persisting subgroup proves refractory to preventive treatment; the prognosis is better when a treatable secondary cause is identified.","pitfalls":["Accepting the diagnosis without excluding cerebrospinal-fluid-pressure disorders or venous thrombosis","Overlooking giant cell arteritis in an older patient","Allowing medication-overuse headache to develop during treatment","Assuming a migrainous phenotype rules out a secondary cause"],"management":["Exclude and treat secondary mimics first: cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, low- and high-cerebrospinal-fluid-pressure disorders and, in older patients, giant cell arteritis, before applying the primary label","Direct treatment at the identified secondary cause when found (for example anticoagulation for venous sinus thrombosis, an epidural blood patch for spontaneous intracranial hypotension, or corticosteroids for giant cell arteritis)","Give phenotype-directed prevention: migraine-type preventives such as amitriptyline, topiramate or a beta-blocker for a migrainous picture, and amitriptyline for a tension-type phenotype","Consider adjuncts for refractory pain such as gabapentinoids, occipital nerve blocks or a short bridging course of corticosteroids","Limit acute analgesics and triptans and counsel on sleep, mood and stress to prevent superimposed medication-overuse headache","Refer to a specialist headache service for refractory disease and consider CGRP-targeted therapy","Safety-net with clear advice on red-flag features prompting urgent reassessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (ICHD-3)","The Headaches (Olesen et al.)"],"crossLinks":["hemicrania_continua"]},"nfkb1_deficiency":{"name":"NFKB1 Deficiency","system":"Immunology / Inborn errors of immunity","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NFKB1","NF-κB1 haploinsufficiency","p50/p105 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant haploinsufficiency of NFKB1 is among the most frequently identified monogenic causes of a common variable immunodeficiency phenotype in adults.","The presentation combines antibody deficiency (recurrent sinopulmonary infection, hypogammaglobulinaemia, reduced switched memory B cells) with prominent autoimmunity and autoinflammation.","Penetrance and expressivity are highly variable, with asymptomatic carriers and severely affected relatives occurring within a single family.","Autoinflammatory or Behcet-like features such as oral and genital ulceration, inflammatory bowel disease and autoimmune cytopenias are characteristic.","There is an increased risk of lymphoid and other malignancy.","Epstein-Barr virus-driven complications and lymphoproliferation may occur.","Immunoglobulin replacement together with targeted management of autoimmune and inflammatory manifestations is the mainstay of care."],"pathophysiology":"NFKB1 encodes p105, the precursor that is processed to the p50 subunit of the canonical NF-κB transcription factor complex. Heterozygous loss-of-function variants cause haploinsufficiency, reducing availability of p50 and p105 and impairing canonical NF-κB signalling downstream of numerous immune receptors. This disturbs B-cell maturation and antibody production, accounting for the common variable immunodeficiency-like antibody defect, while simultaneously dysregulating inflammatory and tolerance pathways, producing the combined picture of immunodeficiency, autoimmunity and autoinflammation. The wide variation in penetrance and severity reflects the contribution of modifier genes and environmental factors, so that identical variants can be silent in some family members and severe in others.","additionalDifferentials":["CTLA4 haploinsufficiency","LRBA deficiency","Activated PI3K-delta syndrome (PIK3CD)","NFKB2 deficiency","Behcet disease","Autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome","Common variable immunodeficiency of undefined genetic cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum immunoglobulin quantification (IgG, IgA and IgM)","Specific antibody and vaccine response testing","B-cell subset analysis focusing on switched memory B cells","Genetic sequencing of NFKB1 and broader common variable immunodeficiency gene panels","Autoantibody screen with direct antiglobulin test and blood film for cytopenias","Cross-sectional imaging for lymphoproliferation and bronchiectasis","Malignancy surveillance guided by clinical features"],"redFlags":["Recurrent severe or opportunistic infection with hypogammaglobulinaemia","Antibody deficiency combined with autoimmune cytopenia or inflammatory bowel disease","New lymphadenopathy or constitutional symptoms suggesting lymphoma","Family history of common variable immunodeficiency or autoimmunity with markedly variable severity"],"prognosis":"The course is variable and depends on infection control with immunoglobulin replacement and on autoimmune, inflammatory and malignant complications. These complications, rather than infection alone, are the major sources of morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Confusing NFKB1 (p50/p105, canonical pathway) with NFKB2 (p52/p100, non-canonical pathway), the latter being associated with anterior pituitary and adrenocorticotrophic hormone deficiency.","Interpreting a normal or borderline immunoglobulin level early in disease as excluding the diagnosis, since overt hypogammaglobulinaemia may develop later.","Dismissing the diagnosis because asymptomatic mutation carriers exist within the family.","Misattributing autoinflammatory features to primary Behcet disease."],"management":["Replace immunoglobulin (subcutaneous or intravenous) for the antibody deficiency, with antimicrobial prophylaxis and prompt treatment of sinopulmonary infection","Institute airway clearance and treat bronchiectasis to preserve lung function","Control autoimmune and autoinflammatory features with corticosteroids and steroid-sparing immunosuppression, using TNF inhibitors or other biologics for Behcet-like ulceration and inflammatory bowel disease","Treat autoimmune cytopenias with immunosuppression and monitor Epstein-Barr virus-driven lymphoproliferation","Undertake surveillance for lymphoid and other malignancy with cross-sectional imaging as indicated","Coordinate multidisciplinary care through clinical immunology alongside relevant organ specialists, and consider haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in severe refractory disease","Offer genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal dominant inheritance with markedly variable penetrance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity","ESID diagnostic criteria for common variable immunodeficiency","Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies"],"crossLinks":[]},"nicolaides_baraitser_syndrome":{"name":"Nicolaides-Baraitser syndrome (SMARCA2)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SMARCA2","NCBRS","Nicolaides-Baraitser","BAF complex disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distinctive intellectual disability syndrome with sparse scalp hair and prominent interphalangeal joints","The fingers show prominent, thickened interphalangeal joints and distal phalanges, often becoming more evident with age","Facial features include a triangular face, sparse hair, a wide mouth with a thin upper lip and thick lower lip","Seizures are common and can be difficult to control, and severe speech impairment is typical","Short stature and a friendly but sometimes irritable behavioural profile are described","Caused by de novo heterozygous variants in SMARCA2, a component of the BAF (SWI/SNF) chromatin-remodelling complex, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern","The combination of sparse hair, distinctive digits and intellectual disability is highly suggestive"],"pathophysiology":"SMARCA2 encodes BRM, an ATPase subunit of the BAF (SWI/SNF) chromatin-remodelling complex, which uses ATP to reposition nucleosomes and thereby regulate access to DNA for transcription during development. Missense variants, typically de novo, alter the ATPase function and act through a dominant mechanism, dysregulating the expression of genes needed for normal brain, skin appendage and skeletal development. The disturbed chromatin remodelling produces the intellectual disability, epilepsy, sparse hair and characteristic digital and facial features. As a chromatin-remodelling disorder, it belongs to the broader group of BAF-complex (SWI/SNF) developmental syndromes that overlaps with Coffin-Siris syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Coffin-Siris syndrome","Cornelia de Lange syndrome","Ectodermal dysplasias with sparse hair","Cockayne syndrome","Other BAF-complex-related disorders","Mucopolysaccharidoses with joint changes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Hand and skeletal radiographs for prominent interphalangeal joints and phalangeal changes","EEG and brain MRI when seizures occur","Developmental and speech assessment","Chromosomal microarray to exclude copy-number causes","Gene panel or exome sequencing including SMARCA2 and other BAF-complex genes"],"redFlags":["Drug-resistant seizures or status epilepticus","Feeding difficulty and faltering growth","Rapid deterioration in seizure control","Severe behavioural disturbance affecting safety"],"prognosis":"Nicolaides-Baraitser syndrome causes moderate to severe lifelong intellectual disability with limited speech; epilepsy severity is a major determinant of outcome. Care is supportive, with attention to seizures, development and orthopaedic features.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling as an ectodermal dysplasia based on the sparse hair alone","Confusing it with Coffin-Siris syndrome without gene-level testing","Underestimating the severity and drug resistance of the epilepsy","Overlooking the progressive nature of the digital joint changes in young children"],"management":["Provide supportive, symptom-directed care as there is no disease-modifying therapy for this chromatin-remodelling disorder","Control seizures with antiepileptic drugs, recognising that epilepsy is often difficult to treat and may need combination regimens","Deliver early developmental support with speech and language therapy, physiotherapy, occupational therapy and tailored special educational provision for the severe speech impairment and intellectual disability","Assess and manage the hand and skeletal features through orthopaedic and hand-therapy input","Monitor growth and nutrition given short stature, and offer behavioural support for the irritable behavioural profile","Coordinate multidisciplinary follow-up across clinical genetics, paediatric neurology and paediatrics","Provide genetic counselling, noting most cases arise from de novo SMARCA2 variants with a low recurrence risk in autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Nicolaides-Baraitser Syndrome","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"nicotine_addiction":{"name":"Nicotine Addiction","system":"Neurologic / Substance Use","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tobacco dependence","Tobacco use disorder","Nicotine dependence","Cigarette addiction"],"chapter":465,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3681","clinicalPearls":["Addiction is established when nicotine in any form (combusted or heated tobacco, oral pouches, or vaped nicotine/nicotine salt) is used with sufficient intensity for a sufficient duration; addicted users self-titrate the frequency and intensity of use to achieve psychoactive effects and avoid withdrawal.","The diagnosis is clinical and hinges on loss of control over the next use of a nicotine product, evidenced by daily use, high number of episodes per day, use shortly after waking, craving during abstinence, and prior failed quit attempts.","The clinically important metric is the cumulative frequency of any nicotine dosing across all products used, not the specific product source, because it is dosing frequency that creates and sustains addiction.","Nicotine itself causes relatively little disease (notably pregnancy complications); the majority of disease burden comes from repetitive exposure to the carcinogens and toxicants carried with nicotine in various products, which addiction drives by compelling multiple daily exposures.","Withdrawal symptoms intensify with longer abstinence as blood nicotine falls and detaches from receptors, and these symptoms can persist for roughly 4-6 weeks.","Acidic aerosols (cigarettes and nicotine salt vaping devices) reduce airway irritation, permit deep inhalation, and produce rapid arterial nicotine rises reaching the brain, making them more addictive than products with slower delivery.","Cancer risk rises linearly with the number of cigarettes per day and logarithmically with duration of smoking; relative risk of lung cancer in current smokers ranges roughly 13-28 across age groups.","Cigarette smoking accounts for about 80% of COPD, about 90% of peripheral vascular disease in nondiabetics, about 50% of aortic aneurysms, about 24% of coronary artery disease, and about 11% of strokes.","Smoking induces the cytochrome P450 system, which can lower serum levels of drugs such as warfarin; conversely, levels may rise during hospitalization when a patient stops smoking, an important consideration for narrow-therapeutic-index agents.","About 40% of cigarette smokers die prematurely from smoking unless they quit; the population still smoking has shifted toward socially disadvantaged groups, and prevalence has declined more slowly in those over 40 who are most likely to develop disease.","'Lower tar/nicotine' filtered cigarettes do not reduce real exposure because smokers compensate by altering puffing behavior or number smoked; ventilation-hole designs lower only machine-measured yields, not delivered toxicants.","Several genes are associated with nicotine addiction (some reducing nicotine clearance, others increasing dependence likelihood or depression risk), but the large declines in smoking since the mid-1950s indicate non-genetic factors dominate population trends."],"pathophysiology":"Nicotine reaching the brain reversibly binds nicotinic acetylcholine receptors that are highly active in networks mediating mood, reward, joy, and excitement. With sustained high exposure, nicotinic receptors are upregulated on brain cells, so that for most people daily use is required to maintain the addicted state. As blood nicotine falls between doses, nicotine detaches from receptors and addicted individuals no longer feel 'normal,' producing a compulsive drive for the next dose. The strength and speed of addiction depend on dosing frequency and on the arterial nicotine concentration reaching the brain, which varies by product. Unprotonated (freebase) nicotine, favored at alkaline pH, is absorbed readily across mucosa but is airway-irritating at high concentration; addictive products use mildly acidic aerosols in the mouth to limit irritation and permit deep inhalation, after which alveolar pH shifts toward blood pH (~7.4), rapidly liberating absorbable unprotonated nicotine and driving fast arterial rises to the brain.","additionalDifferentials":["Other substance use disorders (alcohol use disorder, stimulant use)","Caffeine use/dependence","Primary mood or anxiety disorders presenting with cravings or restlessness","Cannabis use via vaping devices (refillable pods may aerosolize marijuana oils)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Withdrawal-related irritability and restlessness mimicking primary anxiety or depressive disorders","Chronic cough from smoking-related mucous hyperplasia mimicking other causes of chronic productive cough"],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured history of total daily frequency of all nicotine products used (cigarettes, cigars, roll-your-own, vaping, oral pouches/snuff)","Duration of regular use, prior quit experiences, and current interest in quitting","Markers of addiction intensity: episodes per day, spacing through the day, and use within 30 minutes of waking","Biomarkers of smoke-toxicant exposure (markedly lower in exclusive e-cigarette users than in cigarette smokers)"],"redFlags":["Continued smoking during chemotherapy or radiation, associated with poorer outcomes and reduced survival","Smoking in pregnancy: premature rupture of membranes, abruptio placentae, placenta previa, preterm delivery, small-for-gestational-age, increased perinatal mortality and sudden infant death syndrome","Dual use of e-cigarettes and combusted cigarettes, which negates toxicant-reduction benefits","High-frequency use of noncombusted inhaled nicotine products in patients who never used combusted tobacco","Multiplicative cardiac risk when smoking is combined with hypertension or elevated lipids","Higher vascular graft occlusion rates in smokers after bypass surgery"],"prognosis":"Cessation produces substantial reversible benefit: risk of a second coronary event falls within 6-12 months, and first myocardial infarction and coronary death rates decline within 2-4 years; after about 15 years of abstinence cardiovascular risk approaches that of never-smokers. Cancer risk declines relative to continued smoking after about 4 years, but a roughly three- to fourfold excess lung cancer risk persists even 20 years after quitting. Small-airway changes in young smokers reverse after 1-2 years of abstinence; in established airflow obstruction cessation mainly slows further decline rather than restoring lung function. Quitting is typically cyclical with multiple attempts: about 70-80% of smokers want to quit and over half try in a given year, but only about 6% remain abstinent at 6 months and about 3% at 2 years; systemwide tracking and assistance can raise 12-month abstinence to as high as 25%.","pitfalls":["Assuming cigars or pipe tobacco are lower-risk: most cigars are now made like cigarettes and most pipe tobacco is used for roll-your-own, so risks are similar; clinicians should ask about and not recommend them.","Treating 'low tar/nicotine' cigarettes as safer; compensatory smoking negates any benefit and design changes have raised adenocarcinoma rates.","Focusing only on cigarettes and missing cumulative nicotine intake from vaping, oral pouches, and other products when assessing addiction intensity.","Overlooking smoking-induced cytochrome P450 changes when adjusting drug doses around hospitalization or discharge.","Dismissing patients not currently interested in quitting; many still attempt cessation in the subsequent year and benefit from a clear personalized message.","Equating exclusive e-cigarette use (lower toxicant exposure) with cessation of addiction, since persistent nicotine dependence and relapse risk remain."],"management":["Offer behavioural support and structured cessation counselling as the foundation of every quit attempt","Provide first-line pharmacotherapy with nicotine replacement therapy, varenicline or bupropion to blunt withdrawal and craving","Combine a long-acting nicotine patch with a short-acting form (gum, lozenge, spray or inhalator) for heavier dependence to match self-titrated dosing","Anticipate and support the patient through withdrawal, which intensifies over the first weeks of abstinence, and provide relapse-prevention strategies with scheduled follow-up","Address comorbid mental illness and other substance use that undermine cessation","In pregnancy prioritise behavioural support, using nicotine replacement therapy where needed given the pregnancy risks of continued nicotine","Consider switching to lower-toxicant delivery as harm reduction when abstinence cannot be achieved, since most disease burden comes from the toxicants carried with nicotine"],"severityClassification":"No formal grading is presented; addiction intensity is gauged clinically by cumulative daily dosing frequency across all products, spacing of use through the day, use within 30 minutes of waking, craving during abstinence, and history of failed quit attempts.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3681","Harrison 22e p.3682","Harrison 22e p.3683","Harrison 22e p.3684","Harrison 22e p.3685"],"crossLinks":[]},"niemann_pick_disease":{"name":"Niemann-Pick disease","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acid sphingomyelinase deficiency","sphingomyelin lipidosis","NPD","NPC"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard metabolic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive group of storage disorders; types A and B result from deficiency of the enzyme acid sphingomyelinase, while type C is a distinct defect of intracellular cholesterol trafficking","Type A is a severe infantile neurovisceral form with hepatosplenomegaly, failure to thrive, and rapid neurodegeneration, often fatal in early childhood","Type B is a chronic, largely non-neurological visceral form with organomegaly and pulmonary involvement that is compatible with survival into adulthood","A cherry-red macular spot may be seen, particularly in the acute infantile (type A) form","Type C arises from defects in the NPC1 or NPC2 proteins, causing impaired cholesterol handling and a variable neurological course","Vertical supranuclear gaze palsy is a characteristic clinical clue to type C disease","Lipid-laden foamy macrophages (Niemann-Pick cells) infiltrate the marrow, spleen, and liver","Enzyme replacement therapy is available for the non-neurological manifestations of acid sphingomyelinase deficiency, and substrate reduction therapy is used to modify neurological progression in type C"],"pathophysiology":"In Niemann-Pick types A and B, pathogenic variants in SMPD1 reduce acid sphingomyelinase activity, so sphingomyelin cannot be hydrolysed and accumulates within lysosomes of macrophages and other cells, producing visceral organomegaly and, when severe, progressive neurodegeneration. Niemann-Pick type C is mechanistically distinct: variants in NPC1 or NPC2 disrupt the intracellular transport of unesterified cholesterol and other lipids out of the late endosomal/lysosomal compartment, leading to lipid accumulation, neuronal dysfunction, and secondary sphingomyelin storage. All forms are inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern.","additionalDifferentials":["Gaucher disease","GM1 gangliosidosis","Other lysosomal storage disorders with hepatosplenomegaly","Haematological malignancy causing organomegaly","Other causes of neurodegeneration with visceromegaly in infancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Acid sphingomyelinase enzyme activity in leukocytes for types A and B","Molecular genetic testing of SMPD1, or NPC1/NPC2 for type C","Bone marrow examination showing foamy storage cells when performed","Specialised biomarkers and cell-based cholesterol studies for type C","Ophthalmological examination for cherry-red spot and imaging for organomegaly and lung disease"],"redFlags":["Rapid neurodevelopmental regression in an infant with hepatosplenomegaly","Progressive respiratory compromise from pulmonary infiltration in type B","New vertical gaze palsy or ataxia suggesting type C","Severe hypersplenism with cytopenias or risk of splenic rupture"],"prognosis":"Type A is uniformly severe with death typically in early childhood. Type B has a more favourable course with survival into adulthood, though pulmonary and hepatic disease may limit outcomes. Type C is progressive and variable, with age of onset strongly influencing survival; disease-modifying and supportive therapies can slow but not cure neurological decline.","pitfalls":["Confusing the acid sphingomyelinase forms with type C, which requires different diagnostic tests and therapies","Attributing organomegaly solely to a haematological cause and missing a storage disorder","Overlooking vertical supranuclear gaze palsy as a specific pointer to type C","Assuming enzyme replacement will address the neurological features"],"management":["Offer enzyme replacement therapy with olipudase alfa to address the non-neurological visceral and pulmonary manifestations of acid sphingomyelinase deficiency (types A and B)","For type C, use substrate-reduction therapy with miglustat to slow neurological progression, as this form is not correctable by enzyme replacement","Provide supportive care for organomegaly and hypersplenism, respiratory support for interstitial lung disease, and nutritional support for failure to thrive","Manage complications including bleeding from thrombocytopenia and intercurrent infection","Undertake surveillance of pulmonary function, liver and spleen, growth and neurological status appropriate to the subtype","Coordinate multidisciplinary care through metabolic medicine with neurology, respiratory and hepatology input, and offer palliative care in severe infantile type A","Provide genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"niemann_pick_disease_type_c":{"name":"Niemann-Pick disease type C","system":"Metabolic / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NPC","NP-C","Neonatal cholestasis with neurodegeneration (NPC)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive lysosomal lipid-trafficking disorder caused by biallelic variants in NPC1 (the great majority) or NPC2, impairing egress of unesterified cholesterol and glycosphingolipids from late endosomes/lysosomes.","Vertical supranuclear gaze palsy, initially affecting downward saccades, is a hallmark neurological sign and should prompt consideration of NPC in any child or adult with unexplained ataxia or cognitive decline.","Presentation is strikingly age-dependent: neonatal cholestatic jaundice with hepatosplenomegaly, infantile hypotonia and developmental delay, or juvenile/adult-onset ataxia, dystonia, dysarthria, dysphagia and progressive dementia.","Gelastic cataplexy (sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by laughter) and progressive psychiatric symptoms, including psychosis in adolescents and adults, are characteristic and frequently misattributed to primary psychiatric illness.","Splenomegaly, often isolated and long-standing, may precede neurological onset by years and is a valuable diagnostic clue when reviewing the history.","Diagnosis rests on molecular confirmation of biallelic NPC1/NPC2 variants, supported by plasma oxysterol and lysosphingolipid biomarkers; filipin staining of cultured fibroblasts is a confirmatory functional test.","Substrate-reduction therapy with a glucosylceramide synthase inhibitor can slow neurological progression but does not reverse established deficits, underscoring the value of early recognition."],"pathophysiology":"Niemann-Pick disease type C is an autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disorder of intracellular lipid trafficking rather than a primary enzyme deficiency. Loss-of-function variants in NPC1 (a large polytopic late-endosomal/lysosomal membrane protein) or, less commonly, NPC2 (a small soluble luminal cholesterol-binding protein) disrupt the coordinated handover and export of unesterified cholesterol and glycosphingolipids from the endolysosomal compartment. The consequent accumulation of cholesterol, sphingosine, sphingomyelin and glycosphingolipids within neurons and reticuloendothelial cells triggers secondary derangement of calcium homeostasis, autophagy and lysosomal function. In the brain this produces progressive neuronal loss, neuroaxonal dystrophy, meganeurite and ectopic dendrite formation, and neurofibrillary tangle pathology, particularly affecting Purkinje cells and cortical and brainstem neurons, giving the characteristic combination of cerebellar, extrapyramidal, oculomotor and cognitive dysfunction alongside visceral (hepatosplenic) storage.","additionalDifferentials":["Niemann-Pick disease types A and B (acid sphingomyelinase deficiency)","Gaucher disease type 3 (with horizontal supranuclear gaze palsy)","Wilson disease presenting with movement disorder and hepatic involvement","Juvenile-onset GM1/GM2 gangliosidosis","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses","Adolescent-onset primary psychiatric disorder with neurological soft signs","Ataxia-telangiectasia and other early-onset hereditary ataxias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing for biallelic NPC1 and NPC2 variants","Plasma oxysterol profile (cholestane-triol and related species)","Plasma lysosphingolipid and bile-acid metabolite biomarkers","Filipin staining of cultured skin fibroblasts for unesterified cholesterol","Brain MRI (cerebellar and cortical atrophy, white-matter change)","Ophthalmological assessment with eye-movement recording for vertical saccadic slowing","Abdominal imaging to characterise hepatosplenomegaly"],"redFlags":["Vertical supranuclear gaze palsy with unexplained ataxia or cognitive decline","Neonatal cholestasis with hepatosplenomegaly and later neurological regression","Gelastic cataplexy or new-onset psychosis in an adolescent with neurological signs","Progressive dysphagia with aspiration risk","Rapid neurodevelopmental regression in an infant with visceromegaly"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is strongly age-dependent, with early infantile neurological forms progressing rapidly and often proving fatal in childhood, while adolescent and adult-onset disease follows a more protracted but relentlessly progressive course. Substrate-reduction therapy and meticulous supportive care can slow decline and improve quality of life but do not halt the underlying neurodegeneration.","pitfalls":["Attributing adolescent psychiatric or behavioural change to a primary psychiatric disorder and missing the underlying neurometabolic cause","Overlooking long-standing isolated splenomegaly as an early clue years before neurological onset","Failing to test for vertical (especially downward) saccadic palsy, which is easily missed on routine examination","Assuming normal biomarkers exclude disease when NPC2 or atypical variants may give subtler profiles, so molecular confirmation is essential","Delaying diagnosis and therapy, given that treatment slows but cannot reverse established neurological damage"],"management":["Start substrate-reduction therapy with miglustat to slow progression of neurological disease","Consider newer disease-modifying agents such as levacetylleucine and arimoclomol where available","Treat seizures with antiepileptic drugs and manage dystonia, tremor and gelastic cataplexy (for example with anticholinergics or a tricyclic antidepressant for cataplexy)","Manage progressive dysphagia with swallow assessment, texture modification, aspiration precautions and gastrostomy feeding when needed, alongside chest physiotherapy","Treat psychiatric and behavioural symptoms, including psychosis, that are intrinsic to the disease","Monitor neurological progression, swallowing and respiratory status, and coordinate multidisciplinary care across metabolic medicine, neurology, speech and language therapy, physiotherapy and palliative care","Provide genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of inborn errors of metabolism","International consensus guidelines on Niemann-Pick disease type C","Clinical genetics reference texts on lysosomal storage disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"nijmegen_breakage_syndrome":{"name":"Nijmegen breakage syndrome","system":"Genetics / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NBS","NBN","nibrin","Seemanova syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / relevant specialty textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive chromosomal-instability disorder caused by biallelic variants in NBN, encoding nibrin, a component of the MRE11-RAD50-NBN complex involved in DNA double-strand break repair","Progressive microcephaly is present from birth or infancy and is a cardinal feature, accompanied by a characteristic bird-like facial appearance","Combined immunodeficiency causes recurrent respiratory and other infections, with low immunoglobulins and reduced lymphocyte subsets","There is a very high risk of malignancy in childhood, predominantly lymphomas and leukaemias, but also solid tumours","Growth restriction and, in females, hypergonadotropic hypogonadism with ovarian failure are common","Cells show increased chromosomal breakage and characteristic rearrangements involving chromosomes 7 and 14, with radiosensitivity","There is a founder variant common in Slavic populations, and marked radiosensitivity means ionising radiation should be avoided wherever possible"],"pathophysiology":"Nijmegen breakage syndrome is caused by biallelic hypomorphic variants in NBN, encoding nibrin, a subunit of the MRE11-RAD50-NBN (MRN) complex that senses and initiates repair of DNA double-strand breaks and coordinates cell-cycle checkpoint signalling through the ATM pathway. Impaired nibrin function leaves cells unable to repair double-strand breaks efficiently, producing chromosomal instability, characteristic rearrangements and hypersensitivity to ionising radiation. This defect underlies the immunodeficiency (from impaired lymphocyte development requiring efficient DNA recombination), the microcephaly and the strong predisposition to lymphoid and other malignancies.","additionalDifferentials":["Ataxia-telangiectasia (ATM) and ataxia-telangiectasia-like disorder (MRE11)","Fanconi anaemia and Bloom syndrome","Other primary microcephaly syndromes","LIG4 syndrome and other DNA double-strand break repair defects","Combined immunodeficiencies of other cause","Cernunnos/XLF deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of NBN to confirm biallelic variants","Cytogenetic analysis showing chromosomal instability and rearrangements involving chromosomes 7 and 14","Radiosensitivity assays where diagnostically helpful","Immunological workup including immunoglobulin levels and lymphocyte subsets","Serial neuroimaging and growth monitoring for microcephaly and growth restriction","Symptom-directed cancer surveillance, favouring non-ionising imaging"],"redFlags":["New lymphadenopathy, organomegaly or cytopenias suggesting lymphoma or leukaemia","Severe or recurrent infections indicating significant immunodeficiency","Unexpected severe toxicity after exposure to ionising radiation","Rapid neurological or developmental deterioration"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is poor, driven principally by the very high childhood risk of lymphoid and other cancers and by complications of immunodeficiency. Radiosensitivity constrains both diagnostic imaging and cancer treatment, and management is largely supportive with infection prophylaxis and vigilant, radiation-sparing cancer monitoring.","pitfalls":["Exposing patients to avoidable ionising radiation for imaging or radiotherapy given their radiosensitivity","Ascribing microcephaly to a non-genetic cause and missing the diagnosis","Underestimating the degree of immunodeficiency and infection risk","Delaying investigation of possible lymphoma because symptoms overlap with infection"],"management":["Manage the immunodeficiency with immunoglobulin replacement and antimicrobial prophylaxis, and treat infections promptly","Minimise ionising radiation because of profound radiosensitivity: avoid diagnostic X-rays and CT and favour non-ionising ultrasound and MRI","Undertake symptom-directed cancer surveillance focused on lymphoma and leukaemia, using non-ionising imaging","Modify oncological treatment when malignancy occurs, reducing radiotherapy and radiomimetic chemotherapy doses because of radiosensitivity","Provide hormone replacement for hypergonadotropic hypogonadism and ovarian failure in affected females","Monitor growth and neurodevelopment and provide developmental support for microcephaly and growth restriction","Consider haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in selected patients","Coordinate multidisciplinary care and offer genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","GeneReviews: Nijmegen Breakage Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"nipah_virus":{"name":"Nipah virus infection","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NiV","Nipah encephalitis","Nipah virus disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A highly lethal zoonotic paramyxovirus (henipavirus) whose natural reservoir is fruit bats (Pteropus species)","In India transmission has been linked to consumption of date-palm sap contaminated by bat secretions and to close contact with infected patients, with documented person-to-person and nosocomial spread","Presents as rapidly progressive encephalitis with fever, headache, altered mental status and often prominent brainstem signs; respiratory involvement may also occur","Case fatality is very high, and survivors may suffer residual neurological deficits or relapsing/late-onset encephalitis","No licensed antiviral or vaccine is established; management is supportive with rigorous infection control","Strict barrier precautions, patient isolation and contact tracing are essential because human-to-human transmission drives outbreaks","It is classified as a priority pathogen of pandemic potential requiring immediate public-health notification","Diagnosis uses RT-PCR on respiratory secretions, CSF, urine or blood, and serology (IgM/IgG ELISA), performed in high-containment laboratories"],"pathophysiology":"Nipah virus is an enveloped RNA henipavirus that infects endothelium and neurons. After spillover from fruit bats (directly, via contaminated food, or from intermediate hosts such as pigs), the virus causes a systemic vasculitis with endothelial infection, syncytium formation, thrombosis and microinfarction, particularly in the central nervous system. This vasculitic and direct neuronal injury produces the severe encephalitis, while pulmonary endothelial and epithelial infection can cause acute respiratory distress. The capacity of the virus to persist may underlie relapsing or delayed-onset encephalitis in some survivors.","additionalDifferentials":["Japanese encephalitis and other viral encephalitides","Herpes simplex encephalitis","Cerebral malaria","Bacterial or tuberculous meningoencephalitis","Scrub typhus with CNS involvement","Leptospirosis with meningoencephalitis"],"infectionMimics":["Autoimmune encephalitis","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Severe sepsis with encephalopathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR of throat/nasal swabs, CSF, urine and blood in a biosafe reference laboratory","Nipah-specific IgM and IgG serology by ELISA","MRI brain, which may show disseminated small cortical and subcortical lesions","CSF examination when safe, typically showing lymphocytic pleocytosis","Chest imaging if respiratory involvement is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly deteriorating consciousness or brainstem dysfunction signalling life-threatening encephalitis","Any respiratory distress raising concern for airborne/droplet transmissibility and severe disease","Cluster of cases or contact with a confirmed case, mandating immediate isolation and outbreak response","Autonomic instability or seizures requiring critical-care support"],"prognosis":"Nipah virus disease is one of the most lethal human encephalitides, with case fatality frequently exceeding half of symptomatic cases. Survivors may be left with persistent neurological impairment, and a minority develop relapsing or late-onset encephalitis months to years later.","pitfalls":["Failing to institute isolation and barrier precautions early, allowing nosocomial and family transmission","Delaying notification to public-health authorities during a suspected cluster","Attributing the illness to more common encephalitides and not sending appropriate high-containment testing","Underestimating the risk to healthcare workers during aerosol-generating procedures"],"management":["Deliver intensive supportive care as there is no licensed antiviral, with mechanical ventilation and organ support for encephalitis and acute respiratory distress","Consider investigational therapies such as ribavirin or a monoclonal antibody (m102.4) within research or compassionate-use frameworks","Enforce strict infection control with patient isolation, barrier precautions and personal protective equipment, given documented person-to-person and nosocomial transmission","Manage the encephalitis with seizure control, treatment of raised intracranial pressure and meticulous neurocritical care","Notify public health authorities and undertake contact tracing and outbreak control, including avoidance of raw date-palm sap and contact with sick animals","Monitor survivors for residual neurological deficits and for relapsing or late-onset encephalitis","Coordinate care through infectious diseases and critical care within a designated high-consequence pathogen pathway"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"nipple_adenoma":{"name":"Nipple adenoma","system":"Breast","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Florid papillomatosis of the nipple","Erosive adenomatosis of the nipple"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Breast Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign proliferation of the lactiferous ducts within the nipple, presenting as erosion, crusting, ulceration or a palpable nipple nodule.","Frequently accompanied by serous or blood-stained discharge from the nipple surface.","Clinically and macroscopically mimics Paget's disease of the nipple, making biopsy essential for diagnosis.","Most commonly affects middle-aged women but can occur at any age and rarely in men.","Histology shows florid adenosis and papillomatosis of the nipple ducts with preserved myoepithelial layer, confirming its benign nature.","Management is complete surgical excision, which is curative and confirms the diagnosis.","Immunohistochemistry for myoepithelial markers helps distinguish it from tubular carcinoma and Paget's disease.","Because it can coexist with carcinoma, thorough sampling of the excised specimen is important."],"pathophysiology":"Nipple adenoma is a benign tumour arising from the collecting ducts of the nipple, characterised by a compact proliferation of ductal epithelial and myoepithelial cells forming adenotic and papillary structures. The proliferating ductal tissue may erode the overlying epidermis, producing the surface ulceration and discharge that clinically resemble Paget's disease. Retention of a continuous myoepithelial cell layer around the proliferating tubules is the key feature confirming benignity and distinguishing the lesion from invasive carcinoma, although sclerosis and epithelial crowding can create a worrying pseudoinfiltrative appearance.","additionalDifferentials":["Paget's disease of the nipple","Intraductal papilloma","Tubular carcinoma","Syringomatous adenoma of the nipple","Nipple eczema or dermatitis","Subareolar sclerosing duct hyperplasia","Squamous cell carcinoma of the nipple"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination of the nipple-areola complex","Ultrasound of the retroareolar region","Mammography as part of triple assessment","Punch or excisional biopsy of the nipple lesion","Histopathology with assessment of the myoepithelial layer","Immunohistochemistry for myoepithelial markers (p63, smooth muscle myosin)","Cytology of any nipple discharge"],"redFlags":["Failure of an eroded nipple lesion to heal, requiring biopsy to exclude Paget's disease","Associated deep breast mass or suspicious microcalcification","Blood-stained discharge with atypical cytology","Histological loss of the myoepithelial layer suggesting malignancy","Coexisting invasive or in situ carcinoma in the specimen"],"prognosis":"Nipple adenoma is benign and complete excision is curative, with recurrence uncommon and generally attributable to incomplete removal. It carries no intrinsic malignant potential, although rare coexistent carcinoma should be excluded.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the lesion for Paget's disease and vice versa without biopsy","Misinterpreting the pseudoinfiltrative pattern as tubular carcinoma on histology","Treating a persistent eroded nipple as eczema and delaying biopsy","Incomplete excision leading to local recurrence","Failing to sample the specimen adequately and missing coexistent malignancy"],"management":["Perform complete surgical excision of the lesion, which is both curative and definitively diagnostic","Obtain biopsy before or at excision to confirm the diagnosis and distinguish it from Paget's disease of the nipple","Undertake triple assessment with clinical examination, retroareolar ultrasound and mammography to exclude a coexistent carcinoma","Confirm benignity on histopathology by demonstrating a preserved myoepithelial layer, supported by immunohistochemistry (p63, smooth muscle myosin)","Reassure the patient of the benign nature and lack of intrinsic malignant potential once carcinoma is excluded","Follow up to detect the uncommon recurrence, which usually reflects incomplete removal, and manage through a breast multidisciplinary service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Breast Tumours (current edition)","Standard textbook of breast pathology on nipple lesions","National guidance on assessment of nipple lesions and discharge"],"crossLinks":[]},"nitrogen_narcosis":{"name":"Nitrogen narcosis","system":"Environmental / Emergency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["inert gas narcosis","rapture of the deep","narcosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine / Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An impairment of higher mental function caused by the anaesthetic effect of nitrogen breathed under increased pressure at depth","Onset and severity increase with depth; effects usually become noticeable at moderate depths and intensify progressively as the diver goes deeper","Symptoms resemble alcohol intoxication, with euphoria, impaired judgement and reasoning, slowed reactions, overconfidence, and reduced attention","The key feature is that it is fully and rapidly reversible on ascent to shallower depth, leaving no lasting deficit","The main danger is behavioural, as impaired judgement at depth can lead to fatal errors rather than direct physiological harm","Using helium-based breathing mixtures for deep dives reduces or avoids the narcotic effect","Prevention rests on limiting depth, careful dive planning, and adequate training and experience"],"pathophysiology":"Nitrogen narcosis reflects the anaesthetic action of inert gases dissolved in nervous tissue at raised partial pressure. As a diver descends, the partial pressure of the nitrogen in the breathing gas rises in proportion to depth, and the increasing amount of dissolved nitrogen interferes with neuronal signalling in a manner analogous to inhaled anaesthetics, producing dose-dependent central nervous system depression. Because the effect depends on the gas partial pressure and no structural injury occurs, it resolves promptly and completely once the diver ascends and the partial pressure falls.","additionalDifferentials":["Oxygen toxicity (central nervous system effects)","Carbon dioxide retention (hypercapnia)","Hypoxia","Contaminated breathing gas (e.g. carbon monoxide)","Hypothermia impairing cognition","Early decompression illness affecting the nervous system"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical recognition based on depth and the reversibility of symptoms on ascent","Review of the dive profile, depth reached, and breathing gas used","Assessment for other causes of impaired consciousness at depth (gas contamination, hypercapnia)","Analysis of the breathing gas mixture where contamination is suspected","Post-dive neurological assessment to confirm full recovery and exclude decompression illness"],"redFlags":["Severe impairment at depth threatening safe diving behaviour","Failure of symptoms to resolve fully on ascent, suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Concurrent features of oxygen toxicity or gas contamination","Loss of consciousness or seizure while diving"],"prognosis":"Nitrogen narcosis is entirely reversible and leaves no residual deficit once the diver ascends; its significance lies in the risk of impaired judgement causing accidents at depth rather than any lasting physiological harm.","pitfalls":["Underestimating impaired judgement and continuing to descend","Attributing narcosis symptoms to another cause and failing to ascend","Assuming tolerance develops, when susceptibility remains at depth","Overlooking coexisting hypercapnia or gas contamination that worsens narcosis"],"management":["Manage acutely by controlled ascent to a shallower depth, which reverses symptoms fully and rapidly as the nitrogen partial pressure falls","Prevent recurrence by limiting maximum depth and planning conservative dive profiles","Use helium-based breathing mixtures (heliox or trimix) for deep dives to reduce or avoid the narcotic effect","Maintain a buddy system and dive supervision so that impaired judgement is recognised before it causes an accident","Exclude and assess other causes of impairment at depth, including breathing-gas contamination, hypercapnia and oxygen toxicity","Perform a post-dive neurological assessment to confirm full recovery and to exclude decompression illness","Reinforce diver training on the recognition and avoidance of narcosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine","Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"nk_cell_deficiency":{"name":"Natural killer cell deficiency","system":"Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NK cell deficiency","NKD","classical and functional NK cell deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Natural killer cell deficiency is a rare primary immunodeficiency defined by absent or dysfunctional NK cells with otherwise preserved lymphocyte lineages","It is subdivided into classical deficiency (NK cells are absent or greatly reduced) and functional deficiency (NK cells are present but cannot perform cytotoxicity)","The characteristic infection susceptibility is to severe herpesvirus infections, including herpes simplex, varicella-zoster, cytomegalovirus, and Epstein-Barr virus","Severe or recurrent herpesvirus disease, sometimes disseminated or life-threatening, in the absence of another obvious immunodeficiency should raise suspicion","Human papillomavirus-related disease can also be prominent in some forms","Reported genetic causes include mutations affecting GATA2, MCM4, and other genes important for NK cell development or function","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating absent NK cells or impaired NK cytotoxic function after excluding secondary causes and other primary immunodeficiencies","Management centres on antiviral prophylaxis and prompt antiviral treatment, with haematopoietic stem cell transplantation considered in severe forms such as GATA2 deficiency"],"pathophysiology":"Natural killer cells are innate lymphocytes that provide early defence against virally infected and transformed cells through cytotoxic granule release and cytokine production, and they are particularly important in controlling herpesviruses. In classical NK cell deficiency, mutations disrupt the development or survival of the NK lineage, so few or no NK cells are present; in functional deficiency, NK cells develop but cannot kill their targets because of defects in cytotoxic machinery or activating signals. The loss of this early antiviral surveillance leaves patients unable to contain herpesvirus infections, which can become severe or disseminated. Some causative genes, such as GATA2, also affect broader haematopoiesis, producing additional cytopenias and predisposition to myeloid malignancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Combined immunodeficiencies affecting T-cell antiviral immunity","GATA2 deficiency (as a broader bone marrow failure and immunodeficiency syndrome)","Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis with impaired cytotoxicity","HIV and other causes of acquired immunodeficiency","Chediak-Higashi syndrome and other disorders of cytotoxic granules"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lymphocyte subset analysis quantifying NK cells (CD3-negative, CD56/CD16-positive)","NK cell cytotoxicity functional assay","Genetic testing (including GATA2, MCM4 and related genes)","Full blood count and bone marrow evaluation where GATA2 deficiency is suspected","Viral studies for herpesviruses during acute infection","Exclusion of secondary causes of low or impaired NK cells"],"redFlags":["Severe, disseminated, or recurrent herpesvirus infection requiring urgent antiviral therapy and immunological workup","Progressive cytopenias or evolving myeloid malignancy in GATA2-associated disease","Live vaccines should be approached with caution given impaired antiviral immunity","Features of haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis in functional cytotoxicity defects"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies with the specific defect and the severity of viral disease; isolated functional deficiencies may be managed with antiviral strategies, whereas broader defects such as GATA2 deficiency carry risks of bone marrow failure and malignancy and may require haematopoietic stem cell transplantation.","pitfalls":["Attributing severe herpesvirus infections to bad luck rather than investigating NK cell function","Relying on NK cell numbers alone and missing a functional deficiency with normal counts","Failing to recognise GATA2 deficiency as a cause with wider haematological consequences","Not excluding secondary causes of reduced NK cells before diagnosing a primary deficiency","Overlooking the need for antiviral prophylaxis"],"management":["Treat herpesvirus infections promptly and aggressively with aciclovir or valaciclovir, and ganciclovir or valganciclovir for cytomegalovirus, given the characteristic susceptibility to severe herpesvirus disease","Use long-term antiviral prophylaxis to prevent recurrent or disseminated herpesvirus infection","Manage human papillomavirus-related disease with immunisation and ablative or topical treatment where prominent","In GATA2 deficiency, monitor blood counts and marrow and undertake surveillance for bone marrow failure and myeloid malignancy","Offer allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, which can be curative in severe classical deficiency and GATA2-related disease","Coordinate multidisciplinary care through clinical immunology and haematology and keep vaccinations up to date","Provide genetic counselling and family screening for the identified causative gene"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"nlrc4_macrophage_activation":{"name":"NLRC4-associated macrophage activation syndrome","system":"Immunology / Inborn errors of immunity","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NLRC4-MAS","AIFEC","NLRC4 gain-of-function"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by heterozygous gain-of-function mutations in NLRC4, producing a constitutively over-active NLRC4 inflammasome.","Distinctive for recurrent macrophage activation syndrome / haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis together with severe, often neonatal-onset enterocolitis.","Markedly elevated interleukin-18 is a hallmark biomarker, typically far higher than in other autoinflammatory diseases.","Excess caspase-1 activation drives both interleukin-1beta and, especially, free interleukin-18, which fuels macrophage activation.","Some NLRC4 mutations instead produce a milder cold-induced urticarial phenotype (familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome type 4).","Flares may be life-threatening, with fever, cytopenias, hyperferritinaemia, coagulopathy and secretory diarrhoea.","Interleukin-1 blockade, interleukin-18 blockade with recombinant interleukin-18 binding protein, and interferon-gamma neutralisation are used; interleukin-1 inhibition alone may be insufficient given the dominant interleukin-18 and interferon-gamma drive."],"pathophysiology":"NLRC4 (NLR family CARD domain-containing protein 4) is a cytosolic pattern-recognition receptor that, on sensing bacterial flagellin and type III secretion system components via NAIP proteins, oligomerises to form an inflammasome that activates caspase-1. Germline heterozygous gain-of-function mutations, typically within the nucleotide-binding domain, lower the activation threshold or lock NLRC4 in an active conformation, causing spontaneous, ligand-independent inflammasome assembly. The resulting unrestrained caspase-1 activity generates large amounts of interleukin-1beta and, characteristically, interleukin-18, along with pyroptotic cell death; the extreme interleukin-18 excess promotes interferon-gamma-driven macrophage activation and haemophagocytosis. Overactivation of the inflammasome in intestinal epithelium and myeloid cells underlies the severe infantile enterocolitis, while systemic cytokine release produces recurrent macrophage activation syndrome; certain mutations instead confer a cold-induced urticarial phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial or primary haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (perforin-pathway and degranulation defects)","Secondary macrophage activation syndrome complicating systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis or infection","Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes (NLRP3) and other inflammasomopathies","Very-early-onset inflammatory bowel disease and monogenic enteropathies such as IL-10 or IL-10 receptor defects","Other interleukin-18-high autoinflammatory disorders such as XIAP deficiency and CDC42-related NOCARH syndrome","Neonatal sepsis and infectious enterocolitis","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sequencing of NLRC4 within a haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis / autoinflammatory gene panel, with attention to somatic mosaicism","Serum interleukin-18, characteristically very high, with interleukin-18 and CXCL9 profiling","Ferritin and other macrophage-activation markers trended qualitatively across flares","Full blood count for cytopenias, together with coagulation and triglyceride assessment interpreted qualitatively","Bone marrow examination for haemophagocytosis when macrophage activation is suspected","Endoscopy and intestinal biopsy in infantile enterocolitis","Functional inflammasome and caspase-1 activation assays where available"],"redFlags":["Neonatal or early-infantile severe secretory diarrhoea and enterocolitis with failure to thrive","Recurrent episodes of fever with cytopenias, hyperferritinaemia and coagulopathy indicating macrophage activation","Rapid clinical deterioration during flares","Extremely elevated interleukin-18","Family history of infantile enterocolitis or unexplained macrophage activation syndrome"],"prognosis":"The severe enterocolitis-plus-macrophage-activation phenotype can be fatal in infancy without prompt cytokine-directed therapy, though outcomes have improved with interleukin-1, interleukin-18 and interferon-gamma-targeted treatments; the milder cold-urticarial variants have a relatively good prognosis.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent macrophage activation as secondary to infection or systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis and missing the monogenic cause.","Relying on interleukin-1 blockade alone, which may fail because of the dominant interleukin-18 and interferon-gamma signalling.","Overlooking somatic mosaicism, which can cause disease despite a normal-appearing germline result.","Mistaking the neonatal enterocolitis for infectious or allergic colitis.","Under-recognising interleukin-18 as the key discriminating biomarker."],"management":["Use interleukin-1 blockade with anakinra as the cornerstone of cytokine-directed therapy","Add interleukin-18-targeted therapy with recombinant interleukin-18 binding protein (tadekinig alfa) given the characteristic extreme interleukin-18 excess","Treat macrophage activation syndrome and haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis flares with corticosteroids and interferon-gamma neutralisation (emapalumab), alongside intensive supportive care","Manage the neonatal enterocolitis with fluid and electrolyte correction and parenteral nutrition during severe secretory diarrhoea","Monitor for impending macrophage activation with serial ferritin, blood counts, coagulation and interleukin-18 trends","Coordinate care through clinical immunology and rheumatology with intensive care involvement for crises","Offer genetic counselling and family evaluation, noting heterozygous gain-of-function inheritance and possible somatic mosaicism"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Union of Immunological Societies (IUIS) inborn errors of immunity classification","Textbook of autoinflammatory diseases and paediatric rheumatology references","Clinical immunology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"nlrc4_macrophage_activation_syndrome":{"name":"NLRC4-associated autoinflammation with macrophage activation syndrome","system":"Immunology / Autoinflammatory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NLRC4-MAS","NLRC4 gain-of-function disease","autoinflammation with infantile enterocolitis","NLRC4"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Autoinflammation / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by gain-of-function mutations in NLRC4, which encodes an inflammasome sensor, leading to constitutive inflammasome activation","Presents in infancy with recurrent fevers, severe secretory enterocolitis, and a strong predisposition to macrophage activation syndrome","Life-threatening macrophage activation syndrome (haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis) can arise spontaneously or with intercurrent illness","Very high levels of interleukin-18 in blood are a distinctive biomarker that helps distinguish this from other inflammasomopathies","Enterocolitis may cause profuse diarrhoea, feeding intolerance, and failure to thrive in early infancy","Both interleukin-1 blockade and interleukin-18 directed strategies are used, and IL-1 blockade is a cornerstone of therapy","Prompt recognition and treatment of macrophage activation syndrome is essential given its high mortality"],"pathophysiology":"This disease is driven by gain-of-function mutations in NLRC4, a cytosolic pattern-recognition receptor that forms an inflammasome. The mutant sensor triggers constitutive inflammasome assembly and caspase-1 activation, producing excess interleukin-1 beta and, characteristically, very large amounts of interleukin-18. Pyroptotic cell death and cytokine release cause severe intestinal inflammation (infantile enterocolitis) and a strong tendency toward macrophage activation syndrome. The dominant role of interleukin-1 and interleukin-18 explains the use of IL-1 blockade and IL-18 targeted approaches.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis with macrophage activation syndrome","Very-early-onset inflammatory bowel disease","Neonatal sepsis with multi-organ dysfunction","Other inflammasomopathies such as familial Mediterranean fever","Primary immunodeficiency with enteropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for NLRC4 gain-of-function mutations","Serum interleukin-18 level, characteristically markedly elevated","Ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen, and blood counts to detect macrophage activation syndrome","Acute-phase reactants during febrile episodes","Endoscopy and intestinal biopsy for enterocolitis","Bone marrow examination for haemophagocytosis when indicated"],"redFlags":["Features of macrophage activation syndrome: rising ferritin, falling cell counts, coagulopathy","Haemodynamic instability or multi-organ failure","Severe enterocolitis with dehydration and failure to thrive","Rapid deterioration during an intercurrent infection"],"prognosis":"The disease carries significant mortality risk, principally from macrophage activation syndrome, but early recognition with interleukin-1 blockade and interleukin-18 directed therapy has improved outcomes.","pitfalls":["Missing evolving macrophage activation syndrome by not tracking ferritin and blood counts","Attributing severe infantile diarrhoea solely to inflammatory bowel disease or infection","Overlooking the diagnostic value of a markedly raised interleukin-18 level","Delaying interleukin-1 blockade during a hyperinflammatory crisis"],"management":["Establish interleukin-1 blockade with anakinra as the cornerstone of therapy for the constitutive inflammasome activation","Add interleukin-18-directed treatment with recombinant interleukin-18 binding protein (tadekinig alfa) given the hallmark very high interleukin-18","Treat life-threatening macrophage activation syndrome with corticosteroids and interferon-gamma neutralisation (emapalumab) supported by critical care","Support the infantile enterocolitis with fluid and electrolyte resuscitation and parenteral nutrition during profuse secretory diarrhoea and feeding intolerance","Monitor for macrophage activation with serial ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen and blood counts, escalating promptly on deterioration","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across clinical immunology, rheumatology and intensive care","Provide genetic counselling for the gain-of-function NLRC4 variant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Autoinflammation","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"nlrp12_autoinflammatory_syndrome":{"name":"NLRP12 autoinflammatory syndrome","system":"Immunology / Autoinflammatory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome type 2","FCAS2","NLRP12-associated periodic fever","NLRP12"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Autoinflammation / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A hereditary periodic fever syndrome caused by mutations in NLRP12, a regulator of inflammatory signalling","Also termed familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome type 2, reflecting cold-triggered attacks in many patients","Recurrent episodes feature fever, urticaria-like rash, arthralgia or myalgia, and headache","Cold exposure is a common but not universal trigger of flares","Some patients report sensorineural hearing loss and aphthous ulcers alongside the periodic features","Diagnosis combines a compatible clinical picture, family history, and NLRP12 genetic testing after excluding cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes","Anti-inflammatory therapy, including interleukin-1 directed treatment in selected cases, can reduce the frequency and severity of attacks"],"pathophysiology":"NLRP12 autoinflammatory syndrome is caused by mutations in NLRP12, a NOD-like receptor family member that normally acts as a negative regulator of inflammatory signalling pathways such as nuclear factor kappa B. Loss of this regulatory function permits excessive inflammatory signalling and cytokine production, producing recurrent systemic inflammation. Cold exposure can precipitate attacks, giving rise to the familial cold autoinflammatory phenotype. The episodic fevers, rash, and musculoskeletal symptoms reflect this intermittent innate immune activation, and interleukin-1 pathway involvement underlies the benefit seen with IL-1 directed therapy in some patients.","additionalDifferentials":["Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes (NLRP3, including familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome type 1)","Familial Mediterranean fever","TNF receptor-associated periodic syndrome (TRAPS)","Acquired cold urticaria","Systemic-onset juvenile idiopathic arthritis","PLAID with cold-triggered urticaria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for NLRP12 mutations","Acute-phase reactants during febrile episodes","Cold provocation history and, where appropriate, controlled cold challenge","Audiometry if hearing loss is suspected","Skin biopsy of the rash in uncertain cases","Screening to exclude NLRP3-related cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes"],"redFlags":["Progressive sensorineural hearing loss","Features suggesting systemic amyloidosis from chronic inflammation","Severe or frequent disabling attacks despite treatment","Neurological symptoms warranting evaluation for alternative diagnoses"],"prognosis":"NLRP12 autoinflammatory syndrome generally has a favourable prognosis with a relapsing course; attacks are often milder than in some cryopyrinopathies and can be reduced with anti-inflammatory therapy.","pitfalls":["Labelling all cold-triggered periodic fevers as NLRP3-related cryopyrinopathy","Overlooking NLRP12 when NLRP3 testing is negative in a cold-sensitive patient","Attributing episodic urticaria to chronic spontaneous urticaria alone","Failing to monitor for hearing loss and amyloidosis over time"],"management":["Counsel on avoidance of cold exposure, the common precipitant of attacks","Treat flares symptomatically with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and short courses of corticosteroids","Use interleukin-1-targeted therapy (anakinra or canakinumab) for frequent or refractory attacks given interleukin-1 pathway involvement","Manage the urticaria-like rash, arthralgia and headache of individual episodes","Undertake audiometry to detect and monitor sensorineural hearing loss","Coordinate care through clinical immunology and rheumatology","Provide genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Autoinflammation","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"nocardiosis":{"name":"Nocardiosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Nocardia infection","Actinomycetoma (Nocardia)"],"chapter":179,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1358-1362","clinicalPearls":["Nocardiosis is usually an opportunistic infection occurring primarily in hosts with impaired defenses, especially defects in cell-mediated immunity and specific phagocyte function; typical risk factors include solid organ or hematopoietic stem cell transplantation, systemic corticosteroids or Cushing syndrome, other immunosuppressive therapy, and HIV infection (p.1358-1359).","Pulmonary disease is the usual portal after inhalation of fragmented mycelia; onset is typically subacute over days to weeks (more acute in the severely immunocompromised), with prominent cough producing thick non-malodorous purulent sputum, fever, anorexia, and malaise (p.1359).","Typical chest imaging shows infiltrates, nodules, and masses in one or more lobes, and nodules frequently cavitate; pleural effusion or empyema occurs in about one-third of cases, occasionally without lung involvement (p.1359).","Dissemination occurs in up to one-third of patients with pneumonia, most often to the CNS and to skin/subcutaneous tissues; virtually any organ can be involved (p.1359-1360).","CNS involvement occurs in up to two-thirds of disseminated cases; brain imaging classically shows one or more ring-enhancing, multilobulated lesions, and multiple abscesses are seen in two-thirds of patients (p.1360).","Diagnosis starts with examining sputum or pus for branching, beaded, Gram-positive filaments (~1 um wide, up to 50 um long) that fragment into apparent cocci/coccobacilli; most nocardiae are modified (weak) acid-fast (e.g., modified Kinyoun, modified Ziehl-Neelsen, Fite-Faraco) (p.1360-1361).","Primary cutaneous nocardiosis follows transcutaneous inoculation (often thorn/gardening injury) and takes three forms: cellulitis, lymphocutaneous (sporotrichoid) syndrome, or actinomycetoma; N. brasiliensis is the usual cause of skin-limited and lymphocutaneous disease and of actinomycetoma (p.1358, p.1360).","Nocardiosis tends to remit and relapse; without definitive treatment, pulmonary disease characteristically follows a course of remissions and exacerbations over weeks to months, and prolonged therapy (months) is required (p.1359, p.1362-Table 179-1)."],"pathophysiology":"Nocardia species are saprophytic, filamentous, aerobic Gram-positive bacteria ubiquitous in soil, water, and decaying organic matter; their cell walls contain mycolic acids and trehalose (Mycobacteriales order), and they are weakly (modified) acid-fast and catalase positive. Pulmonary and systemic disease follow inhalation of fragmented bacterial mycelia, while primary cutaneous and ocular disease follow direct inoculation. Most immunocompetent people clear inhaled nocardiae without disease; invasive infection is favored by impaired host defenses. Innate and adaptive responses, with neutrophils and macrophages central to antinocardial defense (underscored by the frequency of nocardiosis in chronic granulomatous disease), normally control the organism. Nocardiae have evolved mechanisms to survive within phagocytes, including neutralization of oxidants and prevention of phagosome-lysosome fusion and phagosome acidification; neutrophils limit but do not efficiently kill them. The characteristic pathology is an abscess with neutrophilic infiltration and prominent necrosis, with little fibrosis/encapsulation, whereas actinomycetoma shows granulomatous inflammation with sinus tract formation. (p.1358-1359)","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphocutaneous (sporotrichoid) presentation overlaps with Sporothrix schenckii and Mycobacterium marinum infection (p.1360).","Mycetoma differential: actinomycetoma (filamentous bacteria, e.g., Nocardia), eumycetoma (fungal), and botryomycosis (cocci/bacilli, often Staphylococcus aureus) are difficult to separate clinically and require granule microscopy, culture, or biopsy (p.1361).","Ocular nocardiosis (keratitis, scleritis, endophthalmitis) cannot be distinguished from other ocular infections by signs and symptoms alone (p.1360)."],"infectionMimics":["Sporothrix schenckii (sporotrichosis) for lymphocutaneous disease","Mycobacterium marinum for lymphocutaneous disease","Eumycetoma (fungal mycetoma) for actinomycetoma","Botryomycosis (often Staphylococcus aureus) for actinomycetoma","Other causes of pulmonary nodules/cavitation and ring-enhancing brain lesions in immunocompromised hosts (e.g., fungi, mycobacteria, toxoplasmosis as discussed in brain abscess context)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary or cerebral malignancy/metastases presenting as nodules, masses, or ring-enhancing lesions"],"additionalInvestigations":["Sputum or pus microscopy for branching beaded Gram-positive filaments plus modified (weak-acid) acid-fast staining; organisms may also take up silver stains (p.1360-1361).","Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid or lung biopsy when sputum smears are negative, as they frequently are in pulmonary nocardiosis (p.1361).","Selective media (colistin-nalidixic acid agar, modified Thayer-Martin agar, or buffered charcoal-yeast extract agar) for mixed-flora specimens; avoid mycobacterial decontamination procedures, which can kill nocardiae; alert the laboratory to incubate cultures for prolonged periods (up to 2-4 weeks) (p.1361).","Species/complex identification by MALDI-TOF or genomic sequencing (16S rRNA, secA1, hsp65, or gyrB), with antimicrobial susceptibility testing (ideally CLSI broth dilution); susceptibility testing requires experience because nocardiae grow slowly and clump (p.1361).","Granule examination in mycetoma: wash suspect particles in saline, examine microscopically, and culture; culture is the most reliable way to identify the causative organism (p.1361).","Contrast-enhanced CNS imaging (MRI preferred, with IV gadolinium more sensitive than CT) in all patients with non-cutaneous/non-ocular nocardiosis to detect occult brain abscess (p.1360-1362).","Testing for underlying immunodeficiency in patients lacking typical risk factors, including HIV testing; test for chronic granulomatous disease in children and adults with nocardiosis who have no major risk factor (p.1359, p.1362)."],"redFlags":["CNS dissemination: brain abscess may be clinically silent, so absence of neurologic symptoms does not exclude it - one-third of patients with CNS involvement have no CNS symptoms and one-fourth have no apparent pulmonary disease at presentation (p.1360).","Disseminated nocardiosis carries a higher mortality than localized pulmonary disease, especially with CNS involvement (p.1362).","Nocardiosis in a patient without a major risk factor should prompt evaluation for an undiagnosed immunologic defect, including HIV and chronic granulomatous disease (p.1359, p.1362)."],"prognosis":"With appropriate treatment, mortality for localized pulmonary nocardiosis is less than 10%. Disseminated disease carries a higher mortality, especially with CNS involvement. Primary cutaneous and ocular disease often respond to therapy. Mortality from actinomycetoma is exceedingly low, but patients often suffer substantial morbidity, disfigurement, disability, and/or stigmatization. Prolonged therapy is required and disease tends to relapse, so secondary prophylaxis is considered by some experts for patients who remain at high risk after completing antimicrobial therapy. (p.1362)","pitfalls":["Failing to image the brain: because brain abscess may be asymptomatic, all patients with pulmonary or systemic nocardiosis should have contrast-enhanced CNS imaging (MRI preferred) regardless of neurologic symptoms (p.1360, p.1362).","Missing the diagnosis on smear: sputum smears are frequently negative in pulmonary nocardiosis, and the modified (weak) acid-fast technique must be used or organisms may be overlooked; standard mycobacterial decontamination can kill nocardiae (p.1360-1361).","Cultures lost to short incubation: nocardiae grow slowly (colonies up to 2 weeks, characteristic appearance up to 4 weeks), so the laboratory must be informed to incubate cultures for prolonged periods (p.1361).","Overcalling colonization or contamination: Nocardia can colonize the respiratory tract in bronchiectasis/structural lung disease, and isolation from a respiratory specimen does not always indicate infection; blood culture isolates may be contaminants in low-risk patients without clinical disease (p.1361).","Adjunctive topical corticosteroids should NOT be given to patients with nocardial keratitis (p.1362)."],"management":["Begin antimicrobial therapy with a sulfonamide, co-trimoxazole (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) being the mainstay agent","Use combination therapy for severe, disseminated or central nervous system disease, adding a carbapenem such as imipenem with amikacin, or ceftriaxone or linezolid","Tailor the regimen to species identification and antimicrobial susceptibility testing given variable resistance across Nocardia species","Treat for a prolonged course of many months, extending duration for cerebral or immunocompromised disease, with secondary prophylaxis where immunosuppression persists","Drain or resect abscesses and empyema surgically, including cerebral abscesses that fail to respond","Reduce immunosuppression where feasible in transplant and steroid-treated patients","Image the brain to detect occult abscess and monitor the response to treatment for relapse","Manage through an infectious diseases service"],"severityClassification":"Disease is categorized clinically and by host status to guide therapy duration: pulmonary/systemic disease in hosts with intact defenses versus deficient defenses, CNS disease, primary cutaneous disease (cellulitis, lymphocutaneous), osteomyelitis/arthritis/laryngitis/sinusitis, actinomycetoma, and keratitis - longer courses for immunocompromised and CNS disease, with indefinite therapy considered in some AIDS patients with CD4 below 200/uL or chronic granulomatous disease (p.1362). [Durations noted as classification context only; not dosing guidance.]","references":["Harrison 22e p.1358-1362"],"crossLinks":["BRAIN_ABSCESS","LUNG_ABSCESS"]},"nocturnal_enuresis":{"name":"Nocturnal Enuresis","system":"Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bedwetting","nighttime wetting","sleep enuresis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Nocturnal enuresis is involuntary voiding during sleep in a child old enough to be expected to have night-time bladder control, generally around five years and older.","It is classified as primary when the child has never achieved a sustained period of night-time dryness and secondary when wetting returns after at least six months of dryness.","Monosymptomatic enuresis involves night-time wetting alone, whereas non-monosymptomatic enuresis is accompanied by daytime lower urinary tract symptoms such as urgency, frequency, or daytime wetting.","Common contributing factors include a family history, delayed maturation of arousal and bladder control, reduced overnight bladder capacity, and relative overnight excess of urine production.","Most primary monosymptomatic enuresis is not caused by serious disease, and evaluation is mainly history, examination, and urinalysis to exclude infection and glycosuria.","Secondary enuresis or associated daytime symptoms warrant closer evaluation for causes such as urinary tract infection, constipation, diabetes, or psychosocial stress.","First-line management emphasises reassurance, education, and behavioural measures, with the enuresis alarm being the most effective long-term intervention for motivated families.","Constipation frequently coexists and, when treated, wetting often improves, so bowel habits should always be assessed."],"pathophysiology":"Night-time continence depends on the balance between overnight urine production, bladder storage capacity, and the ability to either suppress bladder contractions during sleep or wake in response to a full bladder. Enuresis arises when this balance fails: some children produce a relatively large volume of urine at night, some have a smaller functional bladder capacity or overactive bladder, and many have a high arousal threshold so a full or contracting bladder does not wake them. These factors, strongly influenced by genetic predisposition and developmental maturation, often coexist. Constipation can reduce functional bladder capacity and provoke bladder overactivity, and secondary enuresis may reflect an acquired medical or psychosocial trigger.","additionalDifferentials":["Urinary tract infection","Constipation with secondary bladder dysfunction","Diabetes mellitus or diabetes insipidus","Overactive bladder or other daytime voiding dysfunction","Obstructive sleep apnoea","Psychosocial stressors (particularly in secondary enuresis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis to screen for infection and glucosuria","Assessment of bowel habits for constipation","Bladder diary of daytime symptoms and voiding pattern","Blood glucose if polyuria or other features suggest diabetes","Renal or bladder imaging only when structural or neurological pathology is suspected"],"redFlags":["New-onset (secondary) enuresis with weight loss, polyuria, or polydipsia","Daytime incontinence, straining, or a poor urinary stream","Recurrent urinary tract infections","Neurological signs or spinal abnormalities","Significant behavioural change or evidence of psychosocial distress or abuse"],"prognosis":"Primary monosymptomatic enuresis has a high rate of spontaneous resolution with age, and treatment can accelerate dryness; the outlook is generally excellent, though relapses can occur and cases with daytime symptoms may need more prolonged management.","pitfalls":["Treating the child punitively or blaming the child for an involuntary problem","Overlooking coexisting constipation as a contributor","Failing to distinguish monosymptomatic from non-monosymptomatic enuresis before choosing therapy","Missing an underlying cause of secondary enuresis such as infection or diabetes"],"management":["Provide education, reassurance and lifestyle measures, including sensible daytime fluids, avoidance of caffeine-containing drinks and voiding before sleep","Identify and treat constipation and any urinary tract infection first, as these commonly drive wetting","Offer an enuresis alarm as first-line active treatment for motivated children and families with monosymptomatic enuresis","Use desmopressin where rapid or short-term dryness is needed or an alarm is unsuitable, targeting the overnight excess of urine production","Add an anticholinergic such as oxybutynin for overactive-bladder features or non-monosymptomatic enuresis","Manage accompanying daytime lower urinary tract symptoms and provide psychological support where wetting causes distress","Investigate and refer secondary or treatment-resistant cases for causes such as diabetes or structural or neurological pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"nodal_marginal_zone_lymphoma":{"name":"Nodal marginal zone lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NMZL","nodal marginal zone B-cell lymphoma","monocytoid B-cell lymphoma","marginal zone lymphoma, nodal type"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An indolent marginal-zone B-cell lymphoma that arises primarily within lymph nodes without the splenic or extranodal (MALT) presentation of the other marginal zone lymphomas","Typically presents in older adults with localised or generalised peripheral lymphadenopathy and is often asymptomatic","Diagnosis is one of exclusion, requiring careful demonstration that a splenic or extranodal primary is absent","The neoplastic cells are CD20-positive marginal-zone or monocytoid B cells negative for CD5, CD10, CD23 and cyclin D1","An association with autoimmune disease and, in some cases, hepatitis C virus infection is recognised","The cells may show plasmacytic differentiation, and a small monoclonal paraprotein can be present","Asymptomatic patients may be observed, with anti-CD20 targeted therapy or chemoimmunotherapy and local radiotherapy used for symptomatic or advanced disease"],"pathophysiology":"Nodal marginal zone lymphoma originates from a post-germinal-centre marginal-zone/memory B cell that proliferates within the lymph node, expanding the marginal and interfollicular zones and sometimes colonising reactive follicles. Chronic antigenic and immune stimulation, including autoimmune conditions and hepatitis C virus infection in a proportion of cases, is thought to promote clonal outgrowth. The tumour cells display heterogeneous marginal-zone, monocytoid and plasmacytic morphology and may harbour recurrent mutations affecting NF-kappaB signalling and marginal-zone differentiation. By definition the disease lacks the defining splenic or extranodal features of the other marginal zone lymphomas, although secondary spread to bone marrow or blood can occur.","additionalDifferentials":["Follicular lymphoma","Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia/small lymphocytic lymphoma","Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma","Splenic marginal zone lymphoma with nodal spread","Extranodal (MALT) marginal zone lymphoma with nodal involvement","Reactive follicular hyperplasia with monocytoid B-cell reaction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional lymph node biopsy for architecture and immunophenotyping","Immunohistochemistry and flow cytometry showing CD20-positive, CD5/CD10/CD23/cyclin D1-negative B cells","Staging imaging to confirm absence of a splenic or extranodal primary","Bone marrow examination for staging","Hepatitis C virus serology and autoimmune screen where relevant","Serum protein electrophoresis for an associated paraprotein"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging node, B symptoms or rising lactate dehydrogenase suggesting transformation to aggressive lymphoma","Bulky disease causing compressive symptoms","New extranodal or marrow involvement with cytopenias","Discordant biopsy findings between sites"],"prognosis":"The disease usually follows an indolent course with prolonged survival, though it is generally considered to have a somewhat less favourable outcome than extranodal MALT lymphoma; the principal adverse event is transformation to aggressive large B-cell lymphoma.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing nodal marginal zone lymphoma without excluding a splenic or extranodal primary","Mistaking the tumour for follicular lymphoma when reactive follicles are colonised","Missing plasmacytic differentiation and an associated paraprotein","Overlooking associated hepatitis C virus infection or autoimmune disease","Failing to recognise transformation in a patient with abrupt clinical change"],"management":["Adopt a watch-and-wait approach for asymptomatic, low-tumour-burden disease given its indolent course","Treat coexistent hepatitis C virus infection with direct-acting antiviral therapy, which can itself induce lymphoma regression","Use the anti-CD20 antibody rituximab, alone or combined with chemotherapy such as bendamustine or CHOP, for symptomatic or advanced disease","Consider involved-site radiotherapy for localised nodal disease","Manage associated autoimmune conditions and provide supportive care during treatment","Maintain surveillance for transformation to aggressive large B-cell lymphoma and re-biopsy rapidly enlarging or discordant nodes","Coordinate care through a haemato-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","British Society for Haematology guidance on marginal zone lymphomas"],"crossLinks":[]},"nodular_fasciitis":{"name":"Nodular fasciitis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nodular fasciitis","pseudosarcomatous fasciitis","USP6-rearranged nodular fasciitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone / Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign, self-limiting myofibroblastic proliferation that classically grows rapidly over a few weeks, often alarming patients and clinicians","Most common in young to middle-aged adults, typically in the forearm, trunk or head and neck region","Usually a small, solitary, sometimes tender subcutaneous or fascial nodule, rarely exceeding a few centimetres","Harbours a USP6 gene rearrangement, most often fusing MYH9 to USP6, supporting a transient self-limiting neoplastic process","Histology shows plump myofibroblasts in a loose, myxoid, tissue-culture-like stroma with extravasated red cells and mitoses that can mimic sarcoma","Despite brisk mitotic activity, atypical mitoses and necrosis are absent, helping to distinguish it from malignancy","It is self-limiting and may regress, so conservative management or simple excision is curative"],"pathophysiology":"Nodular fasciitis is a benign, transient neoplastic proliferation of myofibroblasts, now understood to be driven by USP6 gene rearrangement most commonly involving an MYH9-USP6 fusion. This fusion places USP6 under an active promoter, producing a self-limited burst of proliferation that characteristically resolves, which explains the rapid growth followed by spontaneous regression. The lesion arises in relation to fascia and is composed of spindled myofibroblasts in a loose myxoid or collagenous background with cleft-like spaces, extravasated erythrocytes and scattered giant cells. Its brisk mitotic activity and cellularity can closely imitate a sarcoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Fibrosarcoma and other spindle-cell sarcomas","Desmoid-type fibromatosis","Myxofibrosarcoma","Myxoma","Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumour","Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical correlation of rapid growth with small size favouring a benign reactive-appearing lesion","Ultrasound or MRI to define a small superficial fascia-related nodule","Core or excisional biopsy for histological assessment","Molecular testing for USP6 rearrangement in diagnostically difficult cases","Immunohistochemistry showing myofibroblastic differentiation and absence of malignant markers"],"redFlags":["Large size or deep location atypical for nodular fasciitis","Continued growth beyond the expected self-limited course","Atypical mitoses or necrosis on histology suggesting sarcoma","Recurrence after complete excision prompting diagnostic review"],"prognosis":"Nodular fasciitis is benign and self-limiting, frequently regressing spontaneously and cured by simple excision when removed. Recurrence is very rare, and a recurrence should raise doubt about the original diagnosis.","pitfalls":["Overdiagnosing sarcoma because of high cellularity and mitotic activity","Performing wide excision for a lesion that would resolve or needs only simple removal","Not recognising the characteristic rapid growth of a small lesion as reassuring","Overlooking USP6 testing when the histology is ambiguous"],"management":["Confirm the diagnosis histologically by core or excisional biopsy, distinguishing it from sarcoma by the absence of atypical mitoses and necrosis","Use molecular testing for USP6 rearrangement in diagnostically difficult cases","Reassure the patient of the benign, self-limiting nature, as many lesions regress spontaneously","Offer simple local excision where the diagnosis is uncertain or the lesion is symptomatic, which is curative","Consider intralesional corticosteroid injection as a reported non-surgical alternative","Avoid overtreatment with wide or radical excision, since this is not a malignancy","Follow up briefly, and re-review the original diagnosis if the lesion recurs, as recurrence is very rare"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"nodular_lymphocyte_predominant_hodgkin":{"name":"Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NLPHL","nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin disease","LP cell (popcorn cell) lymphoma","CD20-positive Hodgkin lymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distinct B-cell entity separate from classic Hodgkin lymphoma, characterised by scattered lymphocyte-predominant (LP) or popcorn cells within a background of small B cells and follicular dendritic meshworks","The neoplastic LP cells retain the B-cell programme and are typically CD20-positive, CD45-positive, BCL6-positive and CD15/CD30-negative, unlike the Reed-Sternberg cells of classic disease","Most patients are men presenting with localised, indolent peripheral lymphadenopathy, often cervical, axillary or inguinal, and B symptoms are uncommon at diagnosis","The clinical course is indolent with a tendency to late relapse, so prolonged follow-up is required","Early-stage disease is often managed with radiotherapy alone or limited chemoimmunotherapy, with anti-CD20 targeted agents playing a useful role given CD20 expression","There is a recognised risk of transformation to an aggressive large B-cell lymphoma, so re-biopsy of rapidly enlarging or discordant nodes is important","Histological growth patterns showing a more diffuse or T-cell-rich architecture carry a higher risk of relapse and transformation"],"pathophysiology":"The tumour arises from a germinal-centre B cell that has undergone clonal transformation, giving rise to the characteristic large LP (popcorn) cell with a multilobated nucleus. Unlike classic Hodgkin lymphoma, these cells preserve much of the mature B-cell transcriptional programme and continue to express B-cell antigens and surface immunoglobulin machinery. They reside within expanded nodular meshworks of follicular dendritic cells surrounded by small reactive B lymphocytes and a rosetting rim of T cells. This retained B-cell identity underlies both the CD20 positivity that makes the tumour amenable to anti-CD20 targeting and the propensity, in some cases, to progress to a diffuse aggressive large B-cell lymphoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Classic Hodgkin lymphoma, particularly the lymphocyte-rich subtype","T-cell/histiocyte-rich large B-cell lymphoma","Follicular lymphoma","Progressive transformation of germinal centres (reactive)","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (as transformation)","Reactive follicular hyperplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional lymph node biopsy for architecture, which is essential as fine-needle aspiration is inadequate","Immunohistochemistry demonstrating CD20-positive, CD45-positive, BCL6-positive, CD15/CD30-negative LP cells","Assessment of the background T-cell rosettes and follicular dendritic meshwork pattern","PET-CT for staging and detection of possible transformation","Bone marrow evaluation in selected advanced or symptomatic cases","Baseline blood counts, lactate dehydrogenase and organ function testing"],"redFlags":["A rapidly enlarging or fixed nodal mass suggesting transformation to aggressive large B-cell lymphoma","New B symptoms, bulky disease or extranodal spread after an indolent course","Discordant histology between sites on staging imaging warranting re-biopsy","Rising lactate dehydrogenase with clinical deterioration"],"prognosis":"Early-stage disease has an excellent long-term outlook with high cure rates, but the indolent behaviour is offset by a tendency to late relapse and a small but real risk of transformation to aggressive large B-cell lymphoma, which worsens prognosis and mandates long-term surveillance.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying the tumour as classic Hodgkin lymphoma and thereby selecting inappropriate therapy","Relying on fine-needle aspiration, which cannot demonstrate the diagnostic nodular architecture","Overlooking a variant (diffuse or T-cell-rich) growth pattern that predicts higher relapse risk","Failing to re-biopsy a discordant or rapidly growing node and missing transformation","Assuming cure after early remission and abandoning long-term follow-up"],"management":["Treat early-stage disease with involved-site radiotherapy alone, exploiting its indolent, localised behaviour","Exploit CD20 positivity of the LP cells by using the anti-CD20 antibody rituximab, alone or with chemotherapy","Manage advanced or symptomatic disease with chemoimmunotherapy such as a rituximab-containing regimen","Consider active surveillance after complete excision of limited stage IA disease in selected patients","Re-biopsy rapidly enlarging or discordant nodes to detect transformation to aggressive large B-cell lymphoma, treating transformation as an aggressive lymphoma","Maintain prolonged follow-up because of the tendency to late relapse, and monitor for late treatment effects","Coordinate care through a haemato-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","British Society for Haematology guidance on Hodgkin lymphoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"nodular_regenerative_hyperplasia":{"name":"Nodular regenerative hyperplasia of the liver","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NRH","nodular regenerative hyperplasia","non-cirrhotic nodular hyperplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / EASL vascular liver disease guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A leading cause of non-cirrhotic portal hypertension, presenting with varices, splenomegaly or ascites while synthetic liver function is often preserved","Characterised histologically by diffuse micronodular transformation of hepatocytes without the fibrous septa of true cirrhosis","Strongly associated with systemic disease including autoimmune and connective-tissue disorders, myeloproliferative and lymphoproliferative conditions, and HIV","Frequently linked to drugs and toxins, notably thiopurines, oxaliplatin-based chemotherapy and certain antiretrovirals","Liver enzymes may be near-normal or show a mild cholestatic pattern, so a normal biochemistry does not exclude the diagnosis","Reticulin staining on an adequate biopsy is often needed, as routine imaging and standard histology can miss the subtle nodularity","Management targets portal-hypertensive complications and, where possible, the underlying cause or offending drug"],"pathophysiology":"Nodular regenerative hyperplasia is thought to result from heterogeneous hepatic blood flow: obliteration or injury of small portal venules leads to atrophy of poorly perfused acini and compensatory hyperplasia of well-perfused hepatocytes. This produces diffuse regenerative micronodules unaccompanied by significant fibrosis. The altered intrahepatic vascular resistance and obliterative portal venopathy generate presinusoidal portal hypertension while hepatocellular function is relatively spared, distinguishing it from cirrhosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Cirrhosis of any cause","Idiopathic non-cirrhotic portal hypertension / porto-sinusoidal vascular disease","Schistosomal portal fibrosis","Extrahepatic portal vein obstruction","Congenital hepatic fibrosis","Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Liver biopsy with reticulin staining to demonstrate micronodular architecture without bridging fibrosis","Cross-sectional imaging to exclude cirrhosis and macroscopic focal lesions","Hepatic venous pressure gradient and portal-system imaging to characterise portal hypertension","Screening for associated autoimmune, myeloproliferative and lymphoproliferative disorders","Medication and toxin history including thiopurines and chemotherapy exposure","Upper endoscopy to assess for varices"],"redFlags":["Variceal haemorrhage from portal hypertension","Progressive ascites or refractory portal-hypertensive complications","Features suggesting an underlying occult malignancy or myeloproliferative disorder","Rapid deterioration raising the possibility of superimposed vascular thrombosis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally more favourable than cirrhosis because hepatocellular function is preserved, and outcome is largely determined by control of portal-hypertensive complications and the course of any associated systemic disease.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the condition as cirrhosis and mislabelling the patient's prognosis and management","Excluding it on the basis of normal liver enzymes or unremarkable imaging","Failing to request reticulin staining, without which the diagnosis is easily missed","Overlooking a causative drug or associated systemic disorder that should be addressed"],"management":["Withdraw implicated drugs and toxins, notably thiopurines, oxaliplatin-based chemotherapy and certain antiretrovirals","Identify and treat the associated systemic disorder, whether autoimmune, myeloproliferative, lymphoproliferative or HIV-related","Screen for and manage portal hypertension, using endoscopic variceal surveillance with non-selective beta-blockers and endoscopic band ligation for varices","Manage ascites with salt restriction and diuretics, reserving a transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt for refractory portal-hypertensive complications","Consider anticoagulation where an underlying prothrombotic state or portal vein thrombosis is present","Monitor synthetic liver function, which is typically preserved, and refer to hepatology, considering transplantation in the rare event of hepatic decompensation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on vascular diseases of the liver"],"crossLinks":[]},"noise_induced_hearing_loss":{"name":"Noise-induced hearing loss","system":"Occupational / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NIHL","occupational hearing loss","noise-induced deafness"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hunter's Diseases of Occupations / WHO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["NIHL results from prolonged exposure to hazardous noise and is typically bilateral, symmetrical and sensorineural","The audiogram classically shows a notch at around 4 kHz that later broadens to involve neighbouring frequencies as damage progresses","Both the sound intensity and the cumulative duration of exposure determine risk, and damage accumulates over years of work","Early loss is often unnoticed because speech frequencies are relatively spared at first, and tinnitus may be the first complaint","A temporary threshold shift after a noisy shift can recover, but repeated insults lead to permanent threshold shift","The loss is irreversible once established, so prevention through noise control, hearing protection and audiometric surveillance is paramount","Presbyacusis and other causes of sensorineural loss must be considered, especially in older workers, and the exposure history is central to attribution","Impulse or blast noise can cause acute acoustic trauma, sometimes with sudden asymmetric loss or eardrum rupture"],"pathophysiology":"Excessive acoustic energy causes mechanical and metabolic injury to the outer hair cells of the cochlea, initially at the basal turn that encodes higher frequencies around 3 to 6 kHz. Repeated exposure produces oxidative stress and progressive, irreversible hair cell loss, giving a sensorineural deficit that begins as the characteristic high-frequency notch and widens with continued exposure.","additionalDifferentials":["Age-related hearing loss (presbyacusis)","Ototoxic drug-induced hearing loss","Meniere's disease","Acoustic neuroma (especially if asymmetric)","Conductive loss from middle ear disease or wax","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure tone audiometry to characterise the notch and threshold shifts","Otoscopy to exclude conductive causes","Occupational noise exposure history and workplace noise measurements","Comparison with baseline and serial surveillance audiograms","Referral for imaging if hearing loss is markedly asymmetric to exclude retrocochlear pathology"],"redFlags":["Sudden or markedly asymmetric sensorineural loss requiring urgent evaluation","Unilateral tinnitus or loss suggesting acoustic neuroma","Acute acoustic trauma with pain, vertigo or tympanic membrane rupture","Rapidly progressive loss inconsistent with noise exposure alone"],"prognosis":"Established noise-induced loss does not recover and there is no curative treatment; however, further deterioration can be halted by removing or controlling exposure, and communication is aided by hearing devices, making prevention the decisive factor.","pitfalls":["Assuming all hearing loss in older workers is age-related and missing a noise contribution","Overlooking asymmetry that should prompt investigation for tumour","Relying on a single audiogram rather than serial surveillance","Neglecting to enforce hearing protection and noise control at source"],"management":["Prevent further irreversible injury through workplace noise control — engineering source reduction and enclosure, administrative limits on exposure time, and consistently worn earplugs or earmuffs","Fit and optimise hearing aids for the established sensorineural loss, with assistive listening devices and aural rehabilitation to support communication","Provide tinnitus support such as sound therapy and counselling, as tinnitus is often the presenting complaint","Undertake serial audiometric surveillance compared with baseline to detect early threshold shift and reinforce protective measures","Refer for cochlear implant assessment when loss becomes severe to profound and amplification no longer suffices","Arrange neuro-imaging and otology referral for markedly asymmetric loss to exclude retrocochlear pathology","Coordinate occupational health review for exposure reassessment, redeployment away from hazardous noise, and statutory reporting"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Hunter's Diseases of Occupations","WHO guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"non24_sleep_wake_disorder":{"name":"Non-24-hour sleep-wake rhythm disorder","system":"Sleep medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Non-24","N24","free-running disorder","hypernychthemeral syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / International Classification of Sleep Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A circadian rhythm disorder in which the internal clock fails to entrain to the 24-hour day, so sleep and wake times drift progressively later","The sleep period cycles in and out of alignment with the desired schedule, giving alternating good and bad periods","It is most common in totally blind people, who lack the light input needed to entrain the clock","In sighted individuals it is rare and may relate to reduced light sensitivity, behavioural factors or an underlying delayed sleep phase","A prolonged sleep diary or actigraphy demonstrating the steadily drifting sleep pattern is central to diagnosis","Appropriately timed melatonin or a melatonin-receptor agonist can entrain the rhythm, particularly in blind patients","In sighted patients, timed bright-light exposure combined with melatonin is used to reinforce entrainment"],"pathophysiology":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus generates an intrinsic rhythm that is on average slightly longer than 24 hours and is normally reset each day, mainly by light reaching the retina. When this entraining signal is absent, as in total blindness, or is ineffective, the clock runs free at its own period and sleep-wake timing drifts later day by day. Melatonin, secreted by the pineal gland under circadian control, provides a complementary timing signal that can be supplied exogenously to help re-entrain the rhythm.","additionalDifferentials":["Delayed sleep-wake phase disorder","Irregular sleep-wake rhythm disorder","Insomnia disorder","Behaviourally induced insufficient sleep","Mood disorder with disrupted sleep","Shift-work disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sleep diary maintained over several weeks to reveal the progressively drifting pattern","Actigraphy over an extended period to document the free-running sleep-wake timing","Assessment of visual function and light perception","Circadian phase markers such as dim-light melatonin onset where available","Review of medication, substance use and daytime light exposure"],"redFlags":["New circadian disruption with neurological or cognitive decline suggesting a central cause","Severe daytime impairment with occupational or safety risk","Coexisting untreated mood disorder or suicidal ideation","Features suggesting a structural hypothalamic or pineal lesion"],"prognosis":"With correctly timed melatonin or a melatonin-receptor agonist, and light therapy in sighted patients, many achieve entrainment and stable sleep, though relapse is common if treatment timing lapses, and management is a long-term commitment.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the drifting pattern as simple insomnia and prescribing hypnotics","Giving melatonin at the wrong circadian time, which can fail to entrain or even worsen misalignment","Not recognising total blindness as the key predisposing factor","Overlooking the need for sustained, correctly timed treatment"],"management":["Entrain the circadian clock with correctly timed exogenous melatonin or a melatonin-receptor agonist such as tasimelteon, which is particularly effective in totally blind patients","Add timed morning bright-light therapy in sighted patients to strengthen photic entrainment","Reinforce a fixed sleep-wake schedule, good sleep hygiene, and regular social and activity time cues","Track response with an ongoing sleep diary and actigraphy to confirm entrainment and fine-tune the timing of treatment","Identify and treat associated mood disturbance arising from chronic circadian misalignment","Maintain treatment as a long-term commitment, since relapse is common when the timing of melatonin lapses","Refer to a sleep medicine specialist for circadian phase assessment and to guide dosing timing relative to circadian markers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd edition (ICSD-3)","Principles and Practice of Sleep Medicine (Kryger, Roth, Dement)"],"crossLinks":[]},"non_24_hour_sleep_wake_disorder":{"name":"Non-24-hour sleep-wake rhythm disorder","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["non-24","free-running disorder","hypernychthemeral syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"American Academy of Sleep Medicine ICSD-3","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A circadian rhythm sleep-wake disorder in which the internal clock is not entrained to the 24-hour day, so sleep timing drifts progressively later each day","Patients cycle through periods of good alignment with conventional night-time sleep followed by periods of daytime sleepiness and night-time insomnia","It is most common in people who are totally blind and lack the light input needed to entrain the clock","Sighted cases occur and may relate to weak light exposure, altered clock sensitivity or delayed sleep-wake phase disorder","Diagnosis relies on a prolonged sleep diary, usually with actigraphy, showing the steady daily drift","Timed melatonin, and a melatonin-receptor agonist in some cases, can help entrain the rhythm"],"pathophysiology":"The human circadian pacemaker in the suprachiasmatic nucleus has an intrinsic period slightly longer than 24 hours and normally is reset each day mainly by light. When this entrainment fails, most often because blindness removes photic input to the pacemaker, the sleep-wake rhythm free-runs on its own longer-than-24-hour cycle, drifting out of and back into phase with the environment. Impaired or mistimed light signalling and abnormal melatonin regulation underlie the sighted cases.","additionalDifferentials":["Delayed sleep-wake phase disorder","Irregular sleep-wake rhythm disorder","Chronic insomnia disorder","Shift-work disorder","Insufficient sleep syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Extended sleep diary over several weeks","Actigraphy to document the progressive phase drift","Assessment of visual function and light exposure","Where available, markers of circadian phase such as melatonin timing","Screening for mood disorder that can accompany the misalignment"],"redFlags":["Severe functional or occupational impairment from misalignment","Coexisting significant mood disturbance","New neurological signs suggesting a broader disorder","Failure to respond to appropriately timed treatment"],"prognosis":"The disorder tends to be chronic, but appropriately timed melatonin or a melatonin-receptor agonist, and light where vision permits, can achieve and maintain entrainment in many patients.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as simple insomnia or delayed sleep-wake phase disorder","Not recognising the high prevalence among totally blind people","Giving melatonin at the wrong circadian time and worsening alignment","Relying on a single night's study rather than longitudinal monitoring"],"management":["Use appropriately timed melatonin, or a melatonin-receptor agonist such as tasimelteon, to entrain the free-running rhythm, with greatest benefit in totally blind patients","Add timed morning bright-light exposure in sighted patients to reinforce entrainment through the photic pathway","Establish a consistent sleep-wake schedule with sleep-hygiene measures and structured daytime activity and light cues","Monitor with a sleep diary and actigraphy to verify entrainment and adjust the timing of melatonin or light","Screen for and manage coexisting mood disorder that accompanies the recurrent misalignment","Commit to long-term treatment, as the disorder is chronic and relapses when dosing timing slips","Refer to sleep medicine and, for blind patients, coordinate care around light-independent entrainment strategies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["American Academy of Sleep Medicine ICSD-3","Principles and Practice of Sleep Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"non_arteritic_anterior_ischaemic_optic_neuropathy":{"name":"non arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy","system":"Ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NAION","non-arteritic AION"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most common acute optic neuropathy in patients over 50; typically painless, sudden, monocular loss often noticed on waking.","An inferior altitudinal visual field defect is the classic pattern, though any nerve-fibre-bundle defect may occur.","The structural predisposition is a small, crowded optic disc with a small or absent physiological cup, the so-called disc at risk, best appreciated in the fellow eye.","Vasculopathic risk factors predominate: hypertension, diabetes mellitus, hyperlipidaemia, obstructive sleep apnoea and nocturnal hypotension.","At onset the disc shows segmental or diffuse hyperaemic oedema with peripapillary splinter haemorrhages, later resolving to sectoral pallor.","A relative afferent pupillary defect is present unless there is bilateral or previous fellow-eye involvement.","There is no proven effective treatment; the essential immediate step is to exclude an arteritic (giant cell arteritis) cause.","Fellow-eye involvement occurs in a meaningful minority over subsequent years, so vascular risk-factor modification is advised."],"pathophysiology":"The disorder results from transient hypoperfusion or non-perfusion of the short posterior ciliary arteries supplying the laminar and prelaminar anterior optic nerve head. In a structurally crowded disc with little room for swelling, the initial ischaemic insult produces axonal oedema that raises tissue pressure within the confined scleral canal, creating a compartment-like effect that compresses capillaries and propagates further ischaemia. The process is neither embolic nor inflammatory in most cases, distinguishing it from arteritic and retinal arterial occlusive disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy from giant cell arteritis","Optic neuritis","Compressive optic neuropathy","Diabetic papillopathy","Central retinal vein occlusion with disc oedema","Papilloedema from raised intracranial pressure","Infiltrative optic neuropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate, C-reactive protein and platelet count to exclude giant cell arteritis","Blood pressure assessment including consideration of nocturnal hypotension","Fasting glucose or glycated haemoglobin and a fasting lipid profile","Optical coherence tomography of the retinal nerve fibre layer","Automated perimetry to document the field defect","Screening for obstructive sleep apnoea where clinically suggested","Neuroimaging with magnetic resonance imaging if the presentation is atypical"],"redFlags":["Symptoms of giant cell arteritis such as jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, new headache or polymyalgia rheumatica","Pain on eye movement suggesting optic neuritis rather than ischaemia","Progressive rather than static visual loss suggesting a compressive or infiltrative cause","Simultaneous bilateral involvement warranting urgent systemic evaluation"],"prognosis":"Visual acuity commonly stabilises after the acute event and a proportion of patients show modest spontaneous improvement. Recurrence in the same eye is uncommon, but fellow-eye involvement occurs in roughly one in six to one in five patients over several years.","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude giant cell arteritis and thereby missing a treatable, sight-threatening arteritic cause","Misattributing the presentation to optic neuritis in an older vasculopathic patient","Not screening for obstructive sleep apnoea and modifiable vascular risk factors","Assuming corticosteroids provide benefit, for which evidence is lacking"],"management":["Aggressively modify vasculopathic risk factors — control hypertension, diabetes and hyperlipidaemia with antihypertensives, glucose-lowering therapy and a statin","Avoid nocturnal hypotension by reviewing antihypertensive timing and cautioning against evening dosing, and advise avoidance of phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors","Urgently exclude giant cell arteritis with inflammatory markers before attributing the event to the non-arteritic form, given the sight-threatening fellow-eye risk of untreated arteritis","Investigate for and treat obstructive sleep apnoea with continuous positive airway pressure where identified","Provide low-vision rehabilitation and visual aids for the residual altitudinal field defect","Counsel on the substantial risk of fellow-eye involvement, monitor the second eye, and advise prompt review of any new visual symptoms","Refer to neuro-ophthalmology for confirmation, OCT and perimetry monitoring, and exclusion of non-arteritic mimics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course, Neuro-Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"non_hodgkin_lymphoma":{"name":"Non-Hodgkin's Lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NHL"],"chapter":113,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.857","clinicalPearls":["NHLs are cancers of mature B, T, and NK cells; ~90% are of B-cell origin. They are distinguished from Hodgkin's lymphoma by the absence of the Reed-Sternberg cell and differ from HL in biology, natural history, and the more variable prognosis (Harrison 22e p.857).","Clinically, NHL is grouped by behavior: indolent/slow-growing (e.g., follicular lymphoma, the most common indolent NHL), aggressive (DLBCL, the most common subtype overall, ~one-third of cases), and highly aggressive/most rapidly growing (Burkitt's lymphoma, doubling time <24 h). Mantle cell lymphoma is intermediate-grade (Harrison 22e p.857, 862).","The duration and pace of symptoms help distinguish aggressive from indolent disease, as does the presence or absence of 'B' symptoms — fevers, drenching night sweats, and unexplained weight loss (>10% of body weight over the 6 months before diagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.860-861).","Most common presentation of follicular lymphoma is new, painless lymphadenopathy at multiple sites; most patients lack elevated LDH or B symptoms. Asymptomatic indolent disease should be observed, as treating asymptomatic patients does not improve survival (Harrison 22e p.863-864).","Serum LDH and serum beta-2-microglobulin are important independent prognostic factors in NHL and should be measured at diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.860).","Aggressive lymphomas (DLBCL, Burkitt's) are potentially curable with combination chemotherapy, whereas most indolent lymphomas are very responsive to therapy but not ultimately curable in the vast majority of cases except in localized early-stage disease (Harrison 22e p.862-863)."],"pathophysiology":"NHLs arise from mature B, T, or NK lymphocytes, with ~90% being B-cell neoplasms; transformation typically requires several genetic events. Characteristic chromosomal translocations create fusion or deregulated oncogenes: t(14;18) with BCL2 overexpression in follicular lymphoma (>85% of cases, necessary but not sufficient for transformation); MYC rearrangement at 8q24 in >95% of Burkitt's lymphoma (most often t(8;14) fusing MYC to IgH); and t(11;18) (API2-MLT) in MALT lymphoma. Gene-expression profiling subdivides DLBCL into germinal-center B-cell (GCB)-like and activated-B-cell (ABC)-like subtypes with differing prognoses. Several NHLs are linked to chronic antigenic stimulation or infection-driven immune dysregulation, and immunodeficiency states strongly predispose to (often EBV-associated) lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.857-863).","additionalDifferentials":["Hodgkin's lymphoma (distinguished by Reed-Sternberg cells)","Reactive follicular hyperplasia (major differential for follicular lymphoma on biopsy)","Lymphoid leukemias (CLL/SLL, ALL), plasma cell disorders (multiple myeloma)","Other causes of lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, or B-symptom-like constitutional illness"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious mononucleosis / EBV infection and other viral lymphadenopathies","Reactive lymphadenopathy from systemic infection","Multicentric Castleman's disease (HHV-8), which causes diffuse lymphadenopathy with fever, malaise, and weight loss mimicking lymphoma B symptoms"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune disease with lymphadenopathy (e.g., Sjögren's syndrome, SLE, rheumatoid arthritis)","Reactive follicular hyperplasia","Sarcoidosis and other causes of constitutional symptoms with adenopathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Adequate tissue biopsy reviewed by an expert hematopathologist (follicular lymphoma and Burkitt's lymphoma can be diagnosed on morphology; immunophenotyping required) (Harrison 22e p.860, 863)","Complete blood count, routine chemistries, liver function tests, uric acid, calcium","Serum protein electrophoresis (to detect monoclonal paraproteins)","Serum LDH and serum beta-2-microglobulin (prognostic) (Harrison 22e p.860)","CT scan of chest, abdomen, and pelvis (essential for staging, especially indolent lymphomas) (Harrison 22e p.861)","FDG-PET (preferred for aggressive lymphomas such as DLBCL and Burkitt's, and to assess suspected transformation of indolent disease) (Harrison 22e p.861)","Bone marrow biopsy","Lumbar puncture in lymphoblastic, Burkitt's, and DLBCL with positive marrow biopsy or CNS-risk presentations (Harrison 22e p.861)","Screening for HIV and hepatitis B and C at diagnosis (risk factors and risk of HBV reactivation with therapy) (Harrison 22e p.861)"],"redFlags":["Burkitt's lymphoma is highly aggressive (doubling time <24 h) — treatment must be initiated quickly and the tumor carries a high risk of tumor lysis syndrome; TLS is most often associated with treatment of Burkitt's lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.862, 1014)","Rapid growth of lymph nodes with new B symptoms in known indolent (follicular) lymphoma suggests histologic transformation to DLBCL (~3% per year) (Harrison 22e p.864)","Disease involving paranasal sinuses, testes, breast, kidneys, adrenal glands, or epidural space — and highly aggressive histologies like Burkitt's — carries increased risk of CNS dissemination (Harrison 22e p.861)","Concerning neurologic signs/symptoms warrant lumbar puncture for CNS involvement (Harrison 22e p.860)","'Double-hit' high-grade B-cell lymphoma (concurrent MYC and BCL2 rearrangements) has an extremely poor prognosis (median OS ~12-18 months) (Harrison 22e p.862-863)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is highly variable and driven more by histology and clinical parameters than by anatomic stage. Overall 5-year survival for NHL is ~74%. The International Prognostic Index (IPI; age >=60, elevated LDH, ECOG performance status >=2, Ann Arbor stage III/IV, and >1 extranodal site) is the best predictor for aggressive NHL: 5-year overall survival ~73%, 51%, 43%, and 26% for 0-1, 2, 3, and 4-5 risk factors. With R-CHOP, ~65-70% of DLBCL patients are cured. The FLIPI predicts follicular lymphoma outcome; most FL patients live 15-20 years but the disease is generally not curable (except localized early-stage), and Burkitt's lymphoma is curable in ~80-90% when treated promptly and correctly (Harrison 22e p.857, 861-862).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis requires an adequate tissue sample interpreted by an expert hematopathologist; an inadequate or unrepresentative specimen risks misclassification (e.g., missing coexisting DLBCL within follicular lymphoma or confusing lymphoma with reactive follicular hyperplasia) (Harrison 22e p.860, 863).","The Ann Arbor anatomic staging system is less useful in NHL than in Hodgkin's lymphoma because NHL disseminates widely rather than in an ordered stepwise fashion; histology and the IPI matter more than stage (Harrison 22e p.861).","Treating asymptomatic indolent (follicular) lymphoma does not improve survival — appropriate patients should be observed rather than treated immediately (Harrison 22e p.864).","Failing to anticipate and prevent tumor lysis syndrome before treating rapidly proliferating Burkitt's lymphoma can be life-threatening (Harrison 22e p.862).","A residual mass on CT after therapy may represent treated fibrosis rather than active disease; PET can help distinguish, but end-of-treatment PET should be delayed (preferably 6-8 weeks after chemotherapy) to avoid false positives (Harrison 22e p.861)."],"management":["Tailor treatment to histological subtype and pace: observe asymptomatic indolent follicular lymphoma and reserve therapy for symptomatic or high-burden disease","Treat diffuse large B-cell lymphoma with anti-CD20 immunochemotherapy — rituximab combined with cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine and a corticosteroid — with curative intent","Manage highly aggressive Burkitt lymphoma with intensive multi-agent chemotherapy and central nervous system prophylaxis","Give tumour lysis syndrome prophylaxis with hydration and urate-lowering therapy such as allopurinol or rasburicase, plus antimicrobial and growth-factor support during myelosuppression","Use involved-site radiotherapy for localised disease and as consolidation where indicated","Assess response and surveil for relapse with FDG-PET/CT, and biopsy suspected transformation of previously indolent disease","Offer autologous stem-cell transplantation, CD19-directed CAR T-cell therapy or bispecific antibodies for relapsed or refractory aggressive lymphoma","Manage through a haemato-oncology multidisciplinary team and escalate urgently for oncological emergencies such as spinal cord compression or superior vena cava obstruction"],"severityClassification":"WHO (WHO-HAEM5) classification of lymphoid malignancies categorizes NHL by cell of origin (mature B-cell vs mature T/NK-cell neoplasms) and entity. Anatomic extent is described by the Ann Arbor staging system (Stage I-IV, with suffix A/B for absence/presence of B symptoms). Prognostic stratification uses the International Prognostic Index (IPI) for aggressive NHL and the FLIPI for follicular lymphoma; follicular lymphoma is also graded I-III by centroblasts per high-power field (Harrison 22e p.857-862).","references":["Harrison 22e p.857"],"crossLinks":["hodgkin_lymphoma","infectious_mononucleosis"]},"non_ossifying_fibroma":{"name":"Non-ossifying fibroma","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Fibroxanthoma","Metaphyseal fibrous defect","Fibrous cortical defect"],"chapter":null,"source":"Greenspan, Orthopedic Imaging: A Practical Approach","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["One of the most common incidental bone lesions of childhood and adolescence, typically found in the metaphysis of long bones about the knee (distal femur, proximal and distal tibia).","The small purely cortical form is termed a fibrous cortical defect, whereas the larger lesion extending into the medullary cavity is called a non-ossifying fibroma; they represent a spectrum of the same process.","Radiographs show an eccentric, cortically based, well-defined lucent lesion with a thin sclerotic scalloped or grape-cluster margin and its long axis parallel to the shaft.","It is a classic do-not-touch or leave-me-alone lesion; imaging is diagnostic and biopsy of a typical lesion is unnecessary.","Most lesions regress spontaneously and ossify from the diaphyseal side with skeletal maturation, becoming uncommon after early adulthood.","Large lesions occupying more than roughly half the transverse diameter of the bone carry a risk of pathological fracture and may warrant curettage and grafting.","Multiple non-ossifying fibromas with extraskeletal features (cafe-au-lait macules, learning difficulty, hypogonadism, ocular and cardiovascular anomalies) constitute Jaffe-Campanacci syndrome.","An association with neurofibromatosis type 1 should be considered when lesions are multiple."],"pathophysiology":"Non-ossifying fibroma is a benign, developmental, non-neoplastic fibrous lesion rather than a true tumour, believed to arise near the physis and to migrate away from the growth plate into the metaphysis and diaphysis as the bone lengthens. Histologically it consists of a cellular proliferation of bland spindle-shaped fibroblasts arranged in a storiform (whorled) pattern, admixed with lipid-laden foam cells, scattered multinucleated osteoclast-type giant cells and haemosiderin deposition, accounting for the alternative name fibroxanthoma. The lesion is self-limiting and resolves through progressive ossification and remodelling, so that most examples heal spontaneously by the end of the second decade.","additionalDifferentials":["Fibrous dysplasia","Chondromyxoid fibroma","Aneurysmal bone cyst","Simple (unicameral) bone cyst","Desmoplastic fibroma","Osteofibrous dysplasia","Giant cell tumour (in the skeletally mature)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs, which are usually diagnostic on their own","MRI reserved for atypical appearances or diagnostic uncertainty","CT to assess cortical thinning and fracture risk in large lesions","Clinical assessment for cafe-au-lait macules and other features of neurofibromatosis type 1 or Jaffe-Campanacci syndrome","Interval follow-up radiographs to confirm expected regression and ossification","Biopsy only in genuinely atypical lesions, since it is not required for classic ones"],"redFlags":["Pain in the absence of a pathological fracture","Rapid or unexpected enlargement of the lesion","Cortical breach or an associated soft-tissue mass","Size approaching the threshold for pathological fracture in a weight-bearing bone","Atypical location such as the axial skeleton or a flat bone"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent, with spontaneous resolution the rule and a very low recurrence rate after curettage of large or fractured lesions. Malignant potential is essentially absent.","pitfalls":["Performing an unnecessary biopsy of a radiographically classic lesion","Misinterpreting a benign lesion as an aggressive or malignant tumour","Underestimating the fracture risk of a large lesion in a weight-bearing bone","Overlooking a syndromic association when lesions are multiple"],"management":["Manage the typical asymptomatic lesion conservatively, as this is a classic leave-alone lesion requiring no intervention","Avoid biopsy of radiologically classic lesions, since plain radiographs alone are diagnostic","Confirm the expected spontaneous regression and ossification with interval follow-up radiographs through skeletal maturation","Assess large lesions occupying much of the transverse bone width with CT for cortical thinning and pathological fracture risk","Offer curettage and bone grafting, with internal fixation, for large lesions at fracture risk or after a pathological fracture","Advise activity modification or protected weight-bearing where a substantial lesion threatens fracture","Evaluate for café-au-lait macules and features of neurofibromatosis type 1 or Jaffe-Campanacci syndrome when lesions are multiple"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Greenspan, Orthopedic Imaging: A Practical Approach","WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone (current edition)","Resnick, Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"nonfunctioning_pancreatic_net":{"name":"Non-functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["non-functioning pancreatic NET","non-secretory islet cell tumour","clinically silent pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour","NF-pNET"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["These tumours do not cause a clinical hormone-excess syndrome, so they present late with mass-related symptoms or as incidental findings","Symptoms, when present, arise from local growth: abdominal pain, obstructive jaundice, a palpable mass or bleeding","They may still secrete peptides such as chromogranin A or pancreatic polypeptide that serve as tumour markers without producing a syndrome","An increasing proportion are discovered incidentally on cross-sectional imaging performed for other reasons","Somatostatin-receptor imaging with gallium DOTATATE is valuable for staging and detecting metastatic disease","Tumour grade, based on proliferative indices, and stage are the key determinants of behaviour and management","A significant subset are associated with multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 and other inherited syndromes","Management ranges from surveillance of small low-grade lesions to surgical resection and systemic therapy including somatostatin analogues for advanced disease"],"pathophysiology":"Non-functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours arise from the neuroendocrine cells of the pancreatic islets but do not release hormones in amounts sufficient to produce a recognisable clinical syndrome. Because they are hormonally silent, they typically remain undetected until they grow large enough to cause local effects or metastasise, most often to regional lymph nodes and the liver. Their biological behaviour spans a spectrum from indolent, well-differentiated tumours to aggressive, poorly differentiated carcinomas, largely reflected in histological grade and stage. A meaningful minority occur in the context of hereditary syndromes, particularly multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1, which favours multifocal disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour such as insulinoma or gastrinoma","Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","Solid pseudopapillary neoplasm of the pancreas","Pancreatic cystic neoplasm","Metastasis to the pancreas from another primary","Autoimmune or focal chronic pancreatitis forming a mass"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cross-sectional imaging with contrast-enhanced computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging","Endoscopic ultrasound with tissue sampling for diagnosis and grading","Somatostatin-receptor imaging such as gallium DOTATATE positron emission tomography","Chromogranin A and, where relevant, pancreatic polypeptide as tumour markers","Histological grading using proliferative markers","Screening for multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 where indicated"],"redFlags":["Obstructive jaundice or duodenal obstruction from local growth","Hepatic or extensive nodal metastases at diagnosis","High-grade or poorly differentiated histology","Gastrointestinal haemorrhage from tumour invasion","Rapid tumour growth on surveillance imaging"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends chiefly on grade and stage; small, well-differentiated, resectable tumours have an excellent outlook, whereas high-grade or metastatic disease carries a considerably poorer prognosis, though indolent metastatic tumours may still be compatible with prolonged survival.","pitfalls":["Assuming that absence of a hormone syndrome means the tumour is benign","Mistaking the lesion for pancreatic adenocarcinoma and misdirecting management","Failing to grade the tumour, which is central to prognosis and treatment","Overlooking associated multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1","Neglecting somatostatin-receptor imaging and understaging metastatic disease"],"management":["Resect localised tumours surgically by enucleation or formal pancreatectomy, while small low-grade incidental tumours may be followed with active surveillance","Use somatostatin analogues such as octreotide or lanreotide for antiproliferative control of advanced, well-differentiated, somatostatin-receptor-positive disease","Offer molecular targeted therapy — the mTOR inhibitor everolimus or the tyrosine kinase inhibitor sunitinib — for progressive advanced tumours","Consider peptide-receptor radionuclide therapy with lutetium-labelled DOTATATE for somatostatin-receptor-positive metastatic disease","Treat high-grade or poorly differentiated carcinoma with platinum-based chemotherapy, and use liver-directed approaches for hepatic metastases","Monitor with chromogranin A and serial cross-sectional and somatostatin-receptor imaging to track grade-based behaviour","Screen for multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 and offer genetic counselling and family screening where hereditary disease is suspected","Manage within a specialist neuroendocrine tumour multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","DeGroot's Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"nonketotic_hyperglycinemia":{"name":"Nonketotic hyperglycinaemia (glycine encephalopathy)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["glycine encephalopathy","NKH","glycine cleavage system deficiency","GLDC deficiency","AMT deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by deficiency of the mitochondrial glycine cleavage system, most often from GLDC or AMT variants","Classic neonatal form presents with lethargy, profound hypotonia, hiccups, apnoea, myoclonic jerks and intractable seizures within the first days of life","A characteristic EEG finding is a burst-suppression pattern evolving to hypsarrhythmia","The biochemical hallmark is a raised cerebrospinal fluid to plasma glycine ratio, more discriminating than plasma glycine alone","Crucially it is nonketotic: unlike organic acidaemias there is no ketosis or acidosis to explain the glycine elevation","Distinguish from ketotic hyperglycinaemia, which reflects secondary glycine rise in propionic/methylmalonic/isovaleric acidaemia","Confirm with molecular testing of GLDC/AMT (and rarely GCSH) and, where needed, enzyme assay in liver","Treatment is supportive with glycine reduction using sodium benzoate and NMDA-receptor antagonism (e.g. dextromethorphan); outcome is often poor"],"pathophysiology":"Nonketotic hyperglycinaemia arises from impaired activity of the glycine cleavage system, a mitochondrial multienzyme complex (comprising the P-protein/GLDC, T-protein/AMT, H-protein/GCSH and a lipoamide dehydrogenase) that degrades glycine. Loss of function causes glycine to accumulate in body fluids and especially the central nervous system. Excess glycine acts both as an inhibitory neurotransmitter in the brainstem and spinal cord, causing apnoea and hypotonia, and as a co-agonist at excitatory NMDA glutamate receptors in the forebrain, driving seizures and neuronal injury. The elevated CSF glycine relative to plasma reflects central overproduction/accumulation and is the diagnostic signature. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Ketotic hyperglycinaemia from organic acidaemias (propionic, methylmalonic, isovaleric)","Pyridoxine- or pyridoxal-phosphate-dependent epilepsy","Neonatal hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Sepsis or meningitis with neonatal encephalopathy","Sulphite oxidase/molybdenum cofactor deficiency","Mitochondrial encephalopathies presenting in the neonate","Transient neonatal hyperglycinaemia (self-limiting)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Paired CSF and plasma glycine to calculate the CSF:plasma ratio","Plasma and urine amino acids and urine organic acids (to exclude ketotic causes)","EEG looking for burst-suppression or hypsarrhythmia","Brain MRI including diffusion imaging and corpus callosum assessment","Molecular analysis of GLDC, AMT and GCSH","Blood gas and ketone/lactate measurement to confirm the nonketotic state","Liver enzyme (glycine cleavage) assay when genetics are inconclusive"],"redFlags":["Central apnoea requiring ventilatory support in the neonate","Intractable seizures and burst-suppression progressing to status","Rapidly deteriorating consciousness with profound hypotonia","Progressive microcephaly and developmental arrest","Family history of unexplained neonatal deaths"],"prognosis":"The severe neonatal form carries a poor prognosis with high early mortality and profound intellectual disability, intractable epilepsy and spasticity in survivors; attenuated forms have a milder course, and genotype and residual enzyme activity are the main determinants.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a raised plasma glycine without a paired CSF sample and missing the diagnostic ratio","Mistaking secondary (ketotic) hyperglycinaemia of an organic acidaemia for true NKH","Blood contamination of CSF invalidating the CSF glycine measurement","Assuming valproate is safe, when it can worsen hyperglycinaemia and should be avoided","Overlooking transient neonatal hyperglycinaemia, which resolves and does not need lifelong therapy"],"management":["Lower systemic glycine with sodium benzoate, which conjugates glycine to enable its renal excretion","Blunt NMDA-receptor overstimulation with an antagonist such as dextromethorphan or ketamine to reduce excitotoxic injury and seizures","Control seizures with antiepileptic drugs while avoiding sodium valproate, which further inhibits the glycine cleavage system","Provide supportive care including respiratory support for apnoea and assisted feeding for hypotonia and poor feeding","Monitor development, seizure burden and plasma glycine, coordinated through a metabolic medicine and neurology team","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, with carrier testing of relatives and prenatal or preimplantation diagnosis","Discuss palliative and anticipatory care openly, given the poor prognosis of the severe neonatal form"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","Saudubray, Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"nonossifying_fibroma":{"name":"Non-ossifying fibroma","system":"Musculoskeletal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NOF","fibrous cortical defect","metaphyseal fibrous defect","fibroxanthoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone / Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["One of the commonest incidental bone lesions in children and adolescents, usually found by chance on radiographs taken for another reason","Typically located eccentrically in the metaphysis of long bones, especially around the knee and distal tibia","Radiographs show a well-defined, lobulated, eccentric lucency with a sclerotic scalloped margin and cortical thinning","Small cortical-based lesions are termed fibrous cortical defects, while larger medullary lesions are called non-ossifying fibromas; they are histologically identical","Most are asymptomatic and regress or ossify spontaneously with skeletal maturity, so no treatment is needed","Large lesions occupying a substantial portion of the bone width may carry a pathological fracture risk and warrant follow-up or fixation","Multiple lesions with cafe-au-lait macules and other features raise the possibility of Jaffe-Campanacci syndrome"],"pathophysiology":"Non-ossifying fibroma is a benign fibrous developmental lesion rather than a true neoplasm, arising in the metaphysis of growing bone. It is composed of spindle-shaped fibroblastic cells arranged in a storiform pattern, admixed with osteoclast-type giant cells and foamy lipid-laden macrophages. The lesion represents a localised failure of normal ossification at the metaphysis and characteristically migrates towards the diaphysis and involutes as the skeleton matures, often filling in with reactive bone. The eccentric cortical location and thin sclerotic rim reflect its slow, non-aggressive growth.","additionalDifferentials":["Fibrous dysplasia","Unicameral bone cyst","Aneurysmal bone cyst","Chondromyxoid fibroma","Osteofibrous dysplasia","Langerhans cell histiocytosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiography, usually sufficient to make a confident diagnosis based on classic appearance","MRI or CT reserved for atypical features or to assess fracture risk in large lesions","Assessment of lesion size relative to bone width to estimate fracture risk","Clinical review for cafe-au-lait macules if multiple lesions are present","Biopsy only when imaging features are atypical or aggressive"],"redFlags":["Large lesion occupying more than half the bone diameter with fracture risk","Aggressive or ill-defined margins atypical for a benign fibrous lesion","Persistent pain in the absence of fracture, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Multiple lesions with extraskeletal features suggesting a syndrome"],"prognosis":"Non-ossifying fibroma is benign and self-limiting, with the overwhelming majority resolving spontaneously by early adulthood and requiring no intervention. Outcomes are excellent, and treatment is reserved for the uncommon large lesion at fracture risk.","pitfalls":["Performing unnecessary biopsy of a classic incidental lesion that could be diagnosed radiographically","Overlooking fracture risk in a large lesion of a weight-bearing bone","Failing to recognise multiple lesions as a possible syndromic association","Confusing it with more aggressive fibro-osseous lesions on limited imaging"],"management":["Observe the typical asymptomatic lesion without intervention, as no treatment is needed for this benign self-limiting entity","Rely on plain radiography for diagnosis and avoid biopsy when the appearances are classic","Follow up with interval radiographs to document spontaneous regression and ossification with skeletal maturity","Estimate pathological fracture risk from lesion size relative to bone width, using CT or MRI for large lesions","Treat large lesions at fracture risk, or those that have fractured, with curettage, bone grafting and internal fixation","Advise protected weight-bearing and activity modification while a substantial lesion remains at risk","Assess for café-au-lait macules and syndromic associations when multiple lesions are present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"nonseminomatous_germ_cell_tumor":{"name":"Non-seminomatous germ cell tumour","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NSGCT","non-seminoma","embryonal carcinoma","yolk sac tumour","choriocarcinoma","testicular teratoma","mixed germ cell tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["NSGCT includes embryonal carcinoma, yolk sac tumour, choriocarcinoma, and teratoma, frequently occurring together as a mixed germ cell tumour, and tends to present at a younger age than seminoma","AFP and/or beta-hCG are elevated in most non-seminomatous tumours: yolk sac elements raise AFP and choriocarcinoma or trophoblastic elements raise beta-hCG, while LDH reflects tumour bulk","Any germ cell tumour with a raised AFP is treated as NSGCT, even when the resected specimen appears to be pure seminoma","Radical inguinal orchidectomy provides diagnosis and local control, and post-orchidectomy marker decline is used to define the risk group","Retroperitoneal lymph node dissection has a role in selected patients, and cisplatin-based chemotherapy is central to management of metastatic disease","Post-chemotherapy residual masses may contain viable tumour or mature teratoma and often require surgical resection because teratoma is chemoresistant and can grow or transform","Sperm banking should be offered before orchidectomy, chemotherapy, or retroperitoneal surgery"],"pathophysiology":"Non-seminomatous germ cell tumours arise from germ cell neoplasia in situ but differentiate along embryonic and extra-embryonic lines. Embryonal carcinoma represents primitive undifferentiated cells, yolk sac tumour produces AFP, choriocarcinoma comprises cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast that secrete beta-hCG with a marked propensity for early haematogenous spread, and teratoma contains somatic tissues derived from multiple germ layers. Most tumours are mixed. Isochromosome 12p is characteristic. Lymphatic spread to the retroperitoneum predominates, but choriocarcinoma disseminates early through the bloodstream to lung, liver, and brain.","additionalDifferentials":["Pure seminoma","Testicular lymphoma","Sex cord-stromal tumour","Metastasis to the testis","Epididymo-orchitis with a mass","Burned-out germ cell tumour presenting with metastases and a testicular scar"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound to confirm an intratesticular solid lesion","Serum AFP, beta-hCG, and LDH before and serially after orchidectomy","Radical inguinal orchidectomy for histology","CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis for staging, with brain imaging if choriocarcinoma or neurological features","Semen analysis and sperm banking before treatment","Imaging and marker reassessment of residual masses after chemotherapy to guide resection"],"redFlags":["Very high beta-hCG with widespread visceral or cerebral metastases from choriocarcinoma","Haemoptysis or acute respiratory compromise from pulmonary metastases","Markers failing to normalise or rising after orchidectomy","Enlarging residual retroperitoneal mass after chemotherapy suggesting teratoma or viable tumour","Neurological deterioration suggesting brain metastases or haemorrhage"],"prognosis":"With stage-appropriate surgery and cisplatin-based chemotherapy the majority of patients with NSGCT are cured, including many with metastatic disease; prognosis is stratified by marker levels and metastatic sites, being least favourable with markedly elevated markers and non-pulmonary visceral metastases.","pitfalls":["Managing a marker-positive tumour as seminoma when a raised AFP mandates NSGCT treatment","Assuming a shrinking post-chemotherapy mass needs no surgery when residual teratoma is chemoresistant","Trans-scrotal biopsy risking tumour seeding","Omitting sperm banking before gonadotoxic therapy","Underestimating the early haematogenous spread of choriocarcinoma"],"management":["Perform radical inguinal orchidectomy for diagnosis and local control, using post-orchidectomy marker decline to assign the risk group","Treat metastatic disease with cisplatin-based combination chemotherapy such as bleomycin, etoposide and cisplatin","Employ retroperitoneal lymph node dissection in selected patients, including for chemoresistant teratoma","Resect post-chemotherapy residual masses, since teratoma is chemoresistant and may enlarge or transform","Follow low-stage disease with structured surveillance where appropriate, using serial AFP, beta-hCG and LDH with cross-sectional imaging","Offer semen analysis and sperm banking before orchidectomy and chemotherapy to preserve fertility","Manage within a specialist testicular cancer multidisciplinary team, stratifying therapy by marker levels and metastatic sites"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","EAU Guidelines on Testicular Cancer"],"crossLinks":[]},"nonspecific_interstitial_pneumonia":{"name":"Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NSIP","Cellular and fibrotic NSIP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The second most common idiopathic interstitial pneumonia; more often affects middle-aged women and non-smokers.","Strongly associated with connective tissue disease, particularly systemic sclerosis, polymyositis/dermatomyositis and Sjögren syndrome, and may be its first manifestation.","Idiopathic NSIP is a diagnosis of exclusion made only after a thorough search for connective tissue disease, drug exposure and hypersensitivity pneumonitis.","Two histological subtypes exist: cellular (predominantly inflammatory, better prognosis) and fibrotic (worse prognosis).","HRCT shows bilateral, symmetrical, basal-predominant ground-glass opacity with fine reticulation and traction bronchiectasis; relative subpleural sparing is characteristic.","Honeycombing is absent or minimal, which helps distinguish NSIP from the UIP pattern of IPF.","Temporal uniformity on histology, with all areas of the same apparent age, contrasts with the spatial and temporal heterogeneity of UIP.","Unlike IPF, disease commonly responds to corticosteroids with or without steroid-sparing immunosuppression."],"pathophysiology":"NSIP is characterised by a temporally uniform interstitial process combining varying proportions of chronic inflammation and interstitial fibrosis that preserves the underlying alveolar architecture, lacking the fibroblastic foci and honeycombing of UIP. In many patients it represents a pulmonary manifestation of systemic autoimmune disease, in which immune-mediated alveolar septal injury and repair produce diffuse septal thickening and ground-glass change.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis / UIP pattern","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Connective tissue disease-associated interstitial lung disease","Organising pneumonia","Drug-induced pneumonitis","Desquamative interstitial pneumonia","Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis presenting with ground-glass change"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["HRCT thorax assessing distribution and degree of ground-glass and reticulation","Pulmonary function tests with transfer factor (restrictive defect with reduced DLCO)","Comprehensive autoimmune and myositis serology including antisynthetase antibodies","Detailed environmental, occupational and drug exposure history","Bronchoalveolar lavage where lymphocytosis may support cellular NSIP or an alternative diagnosis","Surgical lung biopsy when the diagnosis remains unclear after multidisciplinary review","Assessment for extrapulmonary connective tissue disease including nailfold capillaroscopy, creatine kinase and rheumatology referral"],"redFlags":["Rapid clinical decline or an acute exacerbation","Features of associated connective tissue disease requiring systemic treatment, such as scleroderma renal or cardiac involvement","Progressive hypoxaemia","Clinical signs of pulmonary hypertension"],"prognosis":"Cellular NSIP has an excellent outlook with treatment, whereas fibrotic NSIP behaves intermediately between cellular NSIP and IPF, and a subset progresses despite therapy.","pitfalls":["Labelling disease as idiopathic without a thorough connective tissue disease and exposure work-up","Confusing fibrotic NSIP with UIP, leading to incorrect prognosis and management","Withholding immunosuppression that could benefit inflammatory disease","Missing antisynthetase syndrome presenting initially as interstitial lung disease"],"management":["Treat with corticosteroids, adding a steroid-sparing immunosuppressant such as mycophenolate or azathioprine, particularly for the cellular inflammatory subtype which responds well","Identify and treat any underlying connective tissue disease, coordinating immunosuppression including rituximab in selected cases with rheumatology","Consider antifibrotic therapy with nintedanib for a progressive fibrotic phenotype behaving like fibrotic disease","Provide supportive care with supplemental oxygen for hypoxaemia, pulmonary rehabilitation and vaccination against respiratory pathogens","Remove ongoing drug or environmental exposures identified as contributory","Monitor with serial lung function including transfer factor and interval HRCT to detect progression","Refer progressive treatment-refractory disease for lung transplantation assessment and manage complex cases through a multidisciplinary interstitial lung disease team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","ATS/ERS international multidisciplinary classification of the idiopathic interstitial pneumonias"],"crossLinks":[]},"nonsyndromic_hearing_loss_gjb2":{"name":"Non-syndromic hearing loss (GJB2/connexin 26, DFNB1)","system":"Ear / Audiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DFNB1","connexin 26 deafness","GJB2-related hearing loss","GJB6 deletion deafness","connexin-related SNHL"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Biallelic GJB2 (connexin 26) variants at the DFNB1 locus are the commonest single cause of hereditary sensorineural hearing loss in many populations","Hearing loss is typically congenital, bilateral, symmetrical, and non-progressive, ranging from mild to profound with no associated systemic features","The 35delG frameshift variant predominates in European-derived populations, while other founder variants (such as 235delC) are common elsewhere","A common cause of missed genotyping is a large GJB6 deletion in trans with a single GJB2 variant, so testing must consider the wider DFNB1 region","Inheritance is usually autosomal recessive with unaffected carrier parents, though rare dominant GJB2 variants exist and may co-segregate with skin phenotypes","Vestibular function is generally preserved, and imaging is usually normal, distinguishing it from malformation-related or syndromic causes","Cochlear implantation outcomes are typically excellent because the lesion is cochlear with an intact auditory nerve and central pathway"],"pathophysiology":"Connexin 26, encoded by GJB2, is a gap-junction protein expressed in the supporting cells and fibrocytes of the cochlea. Connexins assemble into intercellular channels that permit potassium and small-molecule movement, supporting recycling of potassium away from hair cells back to the endolymph and maintaining the endocochlear potential. Loss-of-function variants disrupt this gap-junction network, impairing potassium homeostasis and cochlear amplification, which produces sensorineural hearing loss without structural malformation. Because the deficit lies within the cochlear support apparatus rather than the auditory nerve, the phenotype is a pure cochlear sensorineural loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Enlarged vestibular aqueduct and other inner-ear malformations","Congenital cytomegalovirus-related hearing loss","Syndromic hearing loss (Pendred, Usher, Waardenburg)","Auditory neuropathy spectrum disorder","Mitochondrial non-syndromic hearing loss","Acquired perinatal ototoxic or hypoxic injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Newborn hearing screening followed by diagnostic audiometry (age-appropriate behavioural or objective testing)","Otoacoustic emissions and auditory brainstem response to characterise the site and severity of loss","Targeted GJB2 sequencing and assessment for GJB6 deletions across the DFNB1 locus","Comprehensive deafness gene panel when DFNB1 testing is uninformative","Cross-sectional temporal bone imaging to exclude structural anomalies before implantation","Congenital cytomegalovirus testing where the loss may be acquired"],"redFlags":["Progressive or fluctuating loss, which is atypical and should prompt review for another cause such as EVA","Asymmetry or unilateral loss suggesting a structural or acquired lesion","Any associated dysmorphism, pigmentary, thyroid, renal, or visual features indicating a syndrome","Delayed speech and language development from unrecognised or under-amplified hearing loss"],"prognosis":"The hearing loss is usually stable over time, and because the auditory nerve and central pathways are intact, most children achieve good outcomes with early amplification or cochlear implantation and structured habilitation.","pitfalls":["Assuming a single heterozygous GJB2 variant explains the phenotype without excluding a GJB6 deletion in trans","Overlooking congenital cytomegalovirus as a mimicking or coexisting cause","Delaying intervention while awaiting genetic results and missing the critical window for language acquisition","Attributing later progression solely to DFNB1 rather than reassessing for another disorder"],"management":["Fit hearing aids early after diagnosis to provide auditory access during the critical period of language development","Refer for cochlear implantation when loss is severe to profound, since the intact auditory nerve predicts good outcomes","Provide structured habilitation with speech and language therapy and family-centred communication support","Undertake audiological surveillance to confirm the usually stable loss and adjust amplification as the child grows","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive condition, testing relatives and considering GJB6 deletions across the DFNB1 locus","Exclude structural anomalies with temporal bone imaging before implantation and confirm the loss is genetic rather than acquired","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary audiovestibular, otology, speech therapy and educational team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology","GeneReviews: Nonsyndromic Hearing Loss and Deafness, DFNB1"],"crossLinks":[]},"nontuberculous_mycobacterial_infection":{"name":"Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NTM","MAC","Mycobacterium avium complex"],"chapter":185,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1414-1418","clinicalPearls":["NTM (also termed atypical, environmental, or mycobacteria other than tuberculosis) are ubiquitous in soil and water and refer to mycobacteria other than M. tuberculosis and its close relatives and M. leprae; over 199 species are recognized (p.1414).","Most NTM cause human disease only when host defense is impaired (e.g., bronchiectasis) or breached by inoculation (liposuction, trauma, cardiac surgery); human-to-human transmission is rare and occurs almost exclusively in cystic fibrosis (p.1414).","Pulmonary disease is by far the most common form in North America and is highly associated with pulmonary epithelial defects but NOT with systemic immunodeficiency; disseminated disease instead denotes significant immune dysfunction such as advanced HIV (p.1414, 1416).","Pulmonary MAC most commonly presents in the 6th-7th decade in women with months to years of nagging intermittent cough and fatigue; pulmonary NTM in a tall, thin woman with chest-wall abnormalities is termed 'Lady Windermere syndrome' (p.1416).","Pulmonary MAC affects older nonsmoking white women more than men (onset ~60 years); such patients tend to be taller and thinner with high rates of scoliosis, mitral valve prolapse, and pectus anomalies (p.1416).","Male smokers with upper-lobe cavitary disease tend to carry the same single MAC strain indefinitely, whereas nonsmoking women with nodular bronchiectasis carry several MAC strains simultaneously that change over the disease course (p.1416).","M. kansasii can produce a clinical syndrome strongly resembling tuberculosis with hemoptysis, chest pain, and cavitary lung disease (p.1416).","Disseminated MAC (or M. kansasii) in advanced HIV is now uncommon in North America owing to effective prophylaxis and improved HIV treatment; when common, the portal of entry was the bowel with spread to bone marrow and bloodstream (p.1415).","Disseminated NTM is often indolent and progressive over weeks to months with malaise, fever, weight loss, organomegaly, lymphadenopathy, and anemia; blood cultures may be negative even though involved organs carry heavy organism burdens, sometimes with grossly impaired granulomatous response (p.1415).","Isolated cervical lymphadenopathy is the most common form of NTM infection in young children in North America (usually MAC); the swelling is firm and relatively painless with few systemic signs and is often diagnosed inadvertently at biopsy for suspected malignancy (p.1416).","Children with isolated cervical or intrathoracic NTM infection do NOT commonly have underlying immune defects and usually do NOT go on to develop other opportunistic infections (p.1416).","Cutaneous NTM usually requires a break in the skin; rapidly growing NTM (M. abscessus, M. fortuitum, M. chelonae) cause outbreaks via contaminated surgical instruments/injections, with painful erythematous draining subcutaneous nodules typically WITHOUT fever or systemic symptoms (p.1416).","M. marinum (fish tanks, pools, fish handling) causes 'fish-tank granuloma' and can spread up the arm in a sporotrichoid pattern; M. ulcerans causes painless Buruli ulcer via the toxin mycolactone (p.1416).","'Hot-tub lung' is a pulmonary hypersensitivity reaction to NTM (most often MAC) growing in underchlorinated water, distinct from true pulmonary or disseminated infection (p.1415).","For susceptibility testing, only MAC testing for clarithromycin and M. kansasii testing for rifampin is indicated; few data support other in vitro susceptibility tests, and MAC isolates not previously exposed to macrolides are almost always susceptible (p.1417)."],"pathophysiology":"NTM are ubiquitous environmental organisms (soil and water) with specific niches (e.g., M. simiae in aquifers, M. fortuitum in pedicure baths, M. immunogenum in metalworking fluids). Because exposure is essentially universal yet disease is rare, normal host defenses are strong and significant disease implies a specific susceptibility. Mycobacteria are phagocytosed by macrophages, which release IL-12; IL-12 activates T and NK cells (via STAT4) to secrete IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages/neutrophils (via STAT1) and upregulates TNF-alpha, forming a positive IFN-gamma/IL-12 feedback loop critical for mycobacterial control. Disseminated NTM correlates with declining CD4+ T cells (advanced HIV, idiopathic CD4 lymphocytopenia), TNF-alpha inhibitors (which impair granuloma formation), Mendelian mutations in the IFN-gamma/IL-12 pathway (IFNG, IFNGR1, IFNGR2, STAT1, GATA2, ISG15, IRF8, IL-12A, IL-12RB1, IL-12RB2, CYBB, IKBKG/NEMO and others), or neutralizing anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies. Only ~70% of non-HIV disseminated cases have a genetic diagnosis. In contrast, the best-recognized substrate for pulmonary NTM is bronchiectasis (cystic fibrosis, primary ciliary dyskinesia, STAT3 hyper-IgE/Job's syndrome, idiopathic), but the mechanism by which bronchiectasis predisposes to locally destructive yet non-systemic disease is unknown (p.1414-1415).","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary tuberculosis (M. kansasii closely mimics TB; PPD/IGRA cross-reactivity)","Malignancy (painless cervical adenopathy in children; isolated intrathoracic NTM mistaken for cancer)","Bronchiectasis exacerbation / underlying structural lung disease","Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (predisposes to NTM and Nocardia)","Sporotrichosis (sporotrichoid spread mimicked by M. marinum)"],"infectionMimics":["M. tuberculosis (clinical, radiographic, and AFB-smear overlap)","Nocardia (gram-positive beaded filamentous rods; alveolar proteinosis predisposes to both)","Sporothrix schenckii (sporotrichoid ascending nodules akin to M. marinum)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Malignancy presenting as painless cervical or intrathoracic adenopathy","Hot-tub lung (hypersensitivity pneumonitis-type reaction to NTM rather than invasive infection)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Acid-fast or fluorochrome smears of sputum or body fluids (organisms may appear as beaded gram-positive rods at high burden, but this is unreliable)","Mycobacterial culture using separate processing and dedicated blood culture media; differentiate rapidly growing (<7 days) from slowly growing (>=7 days) species; some labs hold cultures 6 weeks","Liquid culture media for faster isolation; lower incubation temperature (30C) for M. marinum, M. haemophilum, M. ulcerans; special additives (iron/blood for M. haemophilum, mycobactin J for M. genavense)","Species-level identification by biochemical tests, nucleic acid probes/sequencing, HPLC or mass spectrometry; molecular detection for noncultivable species (e.g., M. tilburgii)","Chest CT (tree-in-bud bronchiolar pattern, lingular and right-middle-lobe involvement, bronchiectasis, cavitation)","IGRA (note M. marinum, M. kansasii, M. szulgai carry ESAT6/CFP10 and may cause false-positive IGRA; IGRA is indeterminate with anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies)","Genetic evaluation of the IFN-gamma/IL-12 pathway and screening for anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies in disseminated disease without iatrogenic cause; GATA2 evaluation when myelodysplasia coexists"],"redFlags":["Disseminated involvement of two or more organs without an iatrogenic cause (prompts IFN-gamma/IL-12 pathway genetic investigation)","Disseminated NTM with bacterial/viral infections plus conical/peg/missing teeth or abnormal hair pattern in males (suggests NEMO/IKBKG NF-kB defect, possibly with immunoglobulin defects)","Mycobacterial disease with myelodysplasia (investigate GATA2 deficiency)","Disseminated MAC and rapidly growing NTM with other opportunistic infections (e.g., Talaromyces), especially in East Asian women (anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies)","Infected indwelling IV catheters/lines (M. abscessus, M. fortuitum from contaminated water) requiring catheter removal"],"prognosis":"NTM cause chronic infections evolving slowly over weeks to years, so emergent therapy before diagnosis and speciation is rarely needed. Treatment is complex, often poorly tolerated, and potentially toxic; inadequate single-drug therapy is almost always associated with emergence of resistance and relapse. Pulmonary MAC therapy is prolonged, generally continued for 12 months after culture conversion (typically at least 18 months total). The typical lag between symptom onset and diagnosis is about 5 years in older women (p.1416-1418).","pitfalls":["Not all NTM isolations, especially from the lung, reflect disease or require treatment; distinguishing colonization/contamination from true infection requires an appropriate clinical and radiographic setting (nodules, bronchiectasis, cavities) plus repeated isolation per American Thoracic Society criteria (p.1417).","The single most critical and sensitive step in diagnosis is simply to suspect NTM and request the correct, separately processed mycobacterial smears, cultures, and dedicated blood culture media; many cases go undiagnosed because the appropriate test was never ordered (p.1416-1417).","M. gordonae is frequently recovered from respiratory samples but is almost never a pathogen (p.1417).","PPD does not differentiate NTM from M. tuberculosis well; intermediate (~10 mm) reactions overlap, BCG cross-reacts, but large reactions (>15 mm) most commonly signify TB (p.1416-1417).","Slow-growing NTM typically do NOT proliferate in routine blood culture media, so suspicion and correct bottles are essential; biopsy isolation is strong evidence but laboratory contamination does occur (confirm with organisms on stained sections) (p.1417)."],"management":["Treat with prolonged multidrug antimycobacterial therapy chosen by species and susceptibility — for Mycobacterium avium complex a macrolide combined with rifampicin and ethambutol","Never rely on single-drug therapy, since monotherapy reliably selects for resistance","Use amikacin- and macrolide-containing regimens for rapidly growing species such as M. abscessus, and rifampicin-based therapy for M. kansasii","Address the underlying susceptibility — optimise antiretroviral therapy in advanced HIV and reconsider TNF-alpha inhibitors that impair granuloma formation","Add surgical resection or debridement for localised disease and remove infected foreign material or prostheses","Monitor for culture conversion and for drug toxicity such as aminoglycoside ototoxicity and ethambutol ocular effects","Manage through an infectious diseases specialist, reserving urgent empirical therapy for the rare fulminant case given the generally chronic course"],"severityClassification":"American Thoracic Society criteria for pulmonary NTM diagnosis: growth of NTM from two of three sputum samples (regardless of smear), OR a positive bronchoalveolar lavage sample (regardless of smear), OR a lung parenchymal biopsy with granulomatous inflammation or mycobacteria on section plus NTM on culture, in an appropriate clinical and radiographic setting. Isolation of NTM from blood or organ biopsy in a compatible clinical setting is considered diagnostic. NTM are categorized as rapidly growing (<7 days) versus slowly growing (>=7 days) (p.1417).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1414-1418"],"crossLinks":["PULMONARY_TB","BRONCHIECTASIS_EXACERBATION"]},"nontuberculous_mycobacterial_lung_disease":{"name":"Non-tuberculous mycobacterial lung disease","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NTM pulmonary disease","NTM lung disease","NTM-PD","atypical mycobacterial lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / ATS-IDSA NTM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common form of nontuberculous mycobacterial disease in many regions, caused predominantly by Mycobacterium avium complex, followed by M. kansasii, M. xenopi, M. malmoense, and M. abscessus","Two classic radiological phenotypes are recognised: fibrocavitary upper-lobe disease and nodular-bronchiectatic disease","Fibrocavitary disease typically affects older male smokers with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and resembles tuberculosis","Nodular-bronchiectatic disease classically affects older non-smoking women, often tall and slender, with right-middle-lobe and lingular involvement (so-called Lady Windermere syndrome)","Hot-tub lung is a hypersensitivity reaction to inhaled mycobacteria in aerosolised water and is distinct from invasive infection","Diagnosis requires compatible symptoms, characteristic imaging, exclusion of other causes, and defined microbiological criteria, since a single positive culture may reflect colonisation","Treatment is prolonged macrolide-based multidrug therapy, continued for about a year beyond culture conversion, with the regimen tailored to the species","Not every isolate needs treatment; the decision weighs disease severity, progression, species, and the risks of long, poorly tolerated therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Nontuberculous mycobacterial lung disease arises when ubiquitous environmental mycobacteria from water and soil are inhaled and establish infection in a susceptible airway. The main predisposing factor is impaired local defence from structural lung disease such as bronchiectasis, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, cystic fibrosis, or prior tuberculosis, rather than systemic immunodeficiency. The organisms provoke a granulomatous inflammatory response that produces either upper-lobe cavitation resembling tuberculosis or a nodular-bronchiectatic pattern with tree-in-bud change, particularly in the right middle lobe and lingula. Because exposure is universal but disease is focal and typically non-disseminating, host airway factors and body habitus are thought to be central; a separate hypersensitivity response to aerosolised organisms underlies hot-tub lung.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary tuberculosis","Bronchiectasis exacerbation from conventional pathogens","Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis","Lung malignancy","Airway colonisation without true disease","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (including hot-tub lung)","Other chronic pulmonary infection such as nocardiosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repeated sputum mycobacterial cultures (at least two separate positive samples) to meet diagnostic criteria","Bronchoalveolar lavage culture when sputum is unobtainable or non-diagnostic","High-resolution CT of the chest for cavitation, nodules, and bronchiectasis","Molecular identification to species (and, for M. abscessus, subspecies) level","Species-specific susceptibility testing, notably macrolide for MAC and rifampicin for M. kansasii","Lung biopsy with granulomatous inflammation plus culture when criteria are otherwise unmet","Assessment of underlying lung disease and, in atypical cases, immune status"],"redFlags":["Extensive cavitary disease with haemoptysis and weight loss","Rapid radiographic or clinical progression","Infection with a drug-resistant species such as M. abscessus with inducible macrolide resistance","Disease in cystic fibrosis affecting transplantation candidacy","Deterioration or macrolide resistance during treatment indicating inadequate therapy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies by species and phenotype: M. kansasii responds well to rifampicin-based therapy, nodular-bronchiectatic MAC is often controllable though relapse and reinfection are common, and fibrocavitary or M. abscessus disease carries a worse outlook with lower cure rates. Underlying lung disease and treatment tolerance strongly influence long-term outcomes.","pitfalls":["Treating a single positive culture without meeting diagnostic criteria, mistaking colonisation for disease","Using macrolide-containing regimens without recognising or testing for inducible macrolide resistance in rapid growers","Adding a single new drug to a failing regimen and thereby selecting resistance","Failing to identify the species and subspecies, which determines the regimen and expected response","Underestimating the toxicity and duration of therapy and losing adherence"],"management":["Treat Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease with a macrolide plus rifampicin and ethambutol, continued well beyond sputum culture conversion","Add intravenous or nebulised amikacin for cavitary, extensive or refractory MAC disease","Use rifampicin-based multidrug therapy for M. kansasii and macrolide plus intravenous agents such as amikacin, cefoxitin or imipenem for M. abscessus","Manage hot-tub lung by removing the aerosolised-water exposure, using corticosteroids rather than prolonged antimycobacterials","Optimise the underlying lung disease with airway clearance and physiotherapy for bronchiectasis and appropriate bronchodilator therapy","Consider watchful waiting for mild nodular-bronchiectatic disease and surgical resection for localised refractory or cavitary disease","Monitor serial sputum cultures for conversion and screen for drug toxicity, managing through a specialist multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ATS/IDSA guideline on nontuberculous mycobacterial diseases","British Thoracic Society guideline for the management of nontuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"noonan_syndrome":{"name":"Noonan syndrome","system":"Genetic / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NS","male Turner syndrome (historical, inaccurate)","RASopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard medical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Common features include short stature, characteristic facies, congenital heart disease, and a broad or webbed neck","Facial features evolve with age but classically include hypertelorism, downslanting palpebral fissures, ptosis, and low-set posteriorly rotated ears","Pulmonary valve stenosis is the most characteristic cardiac lesion, and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy also occurs","A pectus deformity (carinatum superiorly and excavatum inferiorly) and cubitus valgus are typical skeletal findings","A bleeding tendency from coagulation factor deficiencies or platelet dysfunction may complicate surgery","Belongs to the RASopathy group caused by mutations that dysregulate the RAS-MAPK signalling pathway, most commonly in PTPN11","Cryptorchidism is common in affected males and may affect fertility","Development is usually normal or only mildly affected, distinguishing it from many other syndromes"],"pathophysiology":"Noonan syndrome is one of the RASopathies, a family of disorders caused by germline mutations that increase signalling through the RAS-mitogen-activated protein kinase (RAS-MAPK) pathway, which governs cell proliferation, differentiation, and survival. The most frequent gene involved is PTPN11, with additional cases attributed to SOS1, RAF1, RIT1, KRAS, and others. Excess pathway activity during development produces the cardiac defects, characteristic facial and skeletal features, growth restriction, and haematological abnormalities. Inheritance is autosomal dominant with variable expressivity, and many cases arise de novo.","additionalDifferentials":["Turner syndrome","Other RASopathies such as cardiofaciocutaneous and Costello syndromes","Neurofibromatosis-Noonan overlap phenotype","Williams syndrome","Fetal alcohol spectrum disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic panel testing for RASopathy-associated genes","Echocardiography and electrocardiography to characterise cardiac involvement","Coagulation screen and platelet studies, particularly before surgery","Growth monitoring and endocrine assessment for short stature","Renal ultrasound and developmental assessment as indicated"],"redFlags":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy that may progress and cause heart failure or arrhythmia","Unexpected bleeding during surgery or trauma from an underlying coagulopathy","Rare association with juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia, especially in certain genotypes","Significant feeding difficulties and failure to thrive in infancy"],"prognosis":"Most individuals have a good long-term outlook with normal or near-normal cognition, though prognosis is influenced by the severity of cardiac disease, particularly hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis in a child with short stature and pulmonary stenosis","Failing to check coagulation before invasive procedures","Attributing the phenotype in a female to Turner syndrome without genetic testing","Not monitoring for evolving hypertrophic cardiomyopathy over time"],"management":["Treat the characteristic pulmonary valve stenosis with balloon valvuloplasty or surgical repair, and monitor for and manage hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Consider growth hormone therapy for short stature after endocrine assessment","Screen coagulation and platelet function before any surgery and manage the bleeding tendency proactively","Provide developmental support and educational intervention for learning and motor delays","Undertake regular cardiac surveillance with echocardiography and electrocardiography given the risk from hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant RASopathy, with molecular confirmation and assessment of relatives","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across cardiology, endocrinology, clinical genetics and developmental services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"noonan_syndrome_cbl":{"name":"Noonan syndrome-like disorder with CBL mutation","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CBL syndrome","Noonan syndrome-like disorder with or without juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia","NSLL","CBL-related RASopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nature Reviews chromatinopathy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by germline variants in CBL, an E3 ubiquitin ligase that normally restrains RAS/MAPK signalling, placing the condition among the RASopathies rather than the chromatin-modifier disorders","The phenotype overlaps Noonan syndrome, with facial dysmorphism, short stature, a broad or webbed neck and variable developmental delay","There is a notable predisposition to juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia (JMML), a myeloproliferative disorder of early childhood","Some CBL-associated JMML behaves indolently and may regress spontaneously, though close haematological monitoring is essential","Cardiac involvement and features overlapping other RASopathies may be present but are often milder than in classic Noonan syndrome","A predisposition to vasculitis and other RAS-pathway-related complications has been described","Diagnosis is molecular, distinguishing germline CBL variants from the somatic CBL changes acquired within leukaemic clones"],"pathophysiology":"CBL encodes an E3 ubiquitin ligase that tags activated receptor tyrosine kinases for degradation, thereby switching off upstream signals that feed the RAS/MAPK cascade controlling cell growth and proliferation. Loss-of-function or dominant-negative germline variants impair this braking function, so RAS/MAPK signalling remains inappropriately active, a mechanism shared with the wider RASopathy group. The persistent signalling produces the Noonan-like developmental and craniofacial features and, in the haematopoietic compartment, drives the excessive myeloid proliferation that underlies juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia; the same overactivity may contribute to vascular complications, and loss of the remaining normal allele in blood cells can promote clonal expansion.","additionalDifferentials":["Noonan syndrome (PTPN11 and other RASopathy genes)","Noonan syndrome with multiple lentigines","Cardiofaciocutaneous syndrome","Costello syndrome (HRAS)","Neurofibromatosis type 1 (with its own JMML risk)","Noonan syndrome-like disorder with loose anagen hair (SHOC2)","Other causes of juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or targeted RASopathy panel sequencing to identify germline CBL variants","Full blood count with film and haematology review to screen for myeloproliferation","Bone marrow examination and specialist assessment if JMML is suspected","Echocardiography for congenital heart disease","Growth monitoring and endocrine assessment of short stature","Neurodevelopmental assessment","Monitoring for vasculitis and other RAS-pathway complications"],"redFlags":["Hepatosplenomegaly, rising monocytosis or leukocytosis suggesting juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia","Unexplained fever, pallor, bruising or bleeding indicating marrow dysfunction","Features of vasculitis requiring urgent evaluation","Significant cardiac disease requiring intervention"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is shaped chiefly by the haematological course: many children do well, and some CBL-associated juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia follows an indolent or self-limiting course, but progressive or aggressive disease can occur and requires specialist haemato-oncology management, so long-term monitoring is essential while developmental and cardiac outcomes are often relatively favourable.","pitfalls":["Confusing germline CBL variants with the somatic CBL changes found within leukaemic clones","Assuming all CBL-related juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia is aggressive and over-treating disease that might regress","Overlooking the leukaemia predisposition when the picture is dominated by Noonan-like features","Failing to arrange haematological surveillance after a molecular diagnosis"],"management":["Undertake close haematological surveillance with full blood count and blood film for juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia, the key complication","Adopt watchful monitoring for CBL-associated JMML that may follow an indolent or self-limiting course, reserving chemotherapy and haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation for progressive or aggressive disease","Assess and monitor cardiac involvement with echocardiography, though it is often milder than in classic Noonan syndrome","Monitor growth with endocrine assessment of short stature","Provide neurodevelopmental assessment and support for delay","Remain alert to RAS-pathway complications such as vasculitis","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant germline CBL RASopathy, with testing of relatives","Coordinate care through clinical genetics and paediatric haemato-oncology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews — Noonan Syndrome and related RASopathies (CBL-related disorder)","Nature Reviews Cancer — RAS/MAPK signalling and juvenile myelomonocytic leukaemia"],"crossLinks":[]},"noonan_syndrome_multiple_lentigines":{"name":"Noonan syndrome with multiple lentigines","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LEOPARD syndrome","PTPN11","multiple lentigines syndrome","cardiocutaneous syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The historical LEOPARD acronym summarises the features: Lentigines, ECG conduction abnormalities, Ocular hypertelorism, Pulmonary stenosis, Abnormal genitalia, Retarded growth and Deafness","Multiple small dark lentigines appear over the face, neck and trunk during childhood and increase with age, alongside larger cafe-au-lait-like macules","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is the most important cardiac lesion and a major determinant of outcome, often accompanied by pulmonary valve stenosis and ECG conduction changes","Most cases arise from specific missense variants in PTPN11 (encoding the phosphatase SHP2), placing it within the RASopathy spectrum alongside Noonan syndrome","Sensorineural hearing loss is common and warrants formal audiological assessment","Facial features overlap with Noonan syndrome: hypertelorism, ptosis, low-set posteriorly rotated ears and a webbed or short neck","Cardiac surveillance with echocardiography and ECG is central to management given the risk of progressive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia"],"pathophysiology":"This disorder is caused chiefly by missense variants in PTPN11 that alter the SHP2 protein tyrosine phosphatase; unlike the activating variants of classic Noonan syndrome, several of these are catalytically impairing yet still dysregulate RAS-MAPK and PI3K-AKT signalling. The abnormal signalling disturbs cardiac development and myocardial growth, producing hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and valvar pulmonary stenosis, while effects on melanocytes generate the progressive lentigines. The same pathway disturbances underlie the craniofacial, auditory and growth features shared with the wider RASopathy family.","additionalDifferentials":["Noonan syndrome (classic)","Neurofibromatosis type 1","Legius syndrome","Carney complex (lentigines and cardiac myxomas)","Cardiofaciocutaneous syndrome","Costello syndrome","Peutz-Jeghers syndrome (mucocutaneous pigmentation)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["RASopathy gene panel including PTPN11, RAF1 and BRAF","Echocardiography to detect hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and pulmonary stenosis","12-lead ECG and Holter monitoring for conduction and rhythm abnormalities","Formal audiometry for sensorineural hearing loss","Growth monitoring and endocrine review where indicated","Dermatological documentation and mapping of lentigines"],"redFlags":["Progressive left ventricular outflow obstruction or symptomatic hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Ventricular arrhythmia, syncope or family history of sudden cardiac death","Rapidly worsening ECG conduction abnormalities","Severe or progressive sensorineural hearing loss affecting speech development"],"prognosis":"Outlook is largely governed by the cardiac phenotype; many patients do well with monitoring, but severe hypertrophic cardiomyopathy carries a risk of heart failure and sudden death, so lifelong cardiology follow-up is essential.","pitfalls":["Attributing the cafe-au-lait macules to neurofibromatosis and missing the cardiac and pigmentary pattern","Underestimating the risk of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, which can progress even when early echocardiography is normal","Failing to arrange hearing assessment, delaying support for sensorineural deafness","Assuming the PTPN11 variants behave like classic Noonan activating variants when counselling"],"management":["Prioritise cardiac care for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy — the major determinant of outcome — with monitoring and pharmacological therapy such as beta-blockers, alongside management of any pulmonary valve stenosis","Perform electrocardiography and Holter monitoring for conduction abnormalities and arrhythmia, with lifelong cardiology follow-up given the risk of sudden death","Assess hearing formally with audiometry and provide amplification for sensorineural hearing loss","Document and map the lentigines dermatologically, reassuring that they are cosmetic while distinguishing them from concerning pigmented lesions","Monitor growth and arrange endocrine review where short stature warrants it","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant PTPN11-associated RASopathy, with assessment of relatives","Coordinate lifelong multidisciplinary follow-up across cardiology, audiology, clinical genetics and dermatology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Noonan Syndrome with Multiple Lentigines","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"noonan_with_multiple_lentigines":{"name":"Noonan syndrome with multiple lentigines (LEOPARD syndrome)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LEOPARD syndrome","NSML","multiple lentigines syndrome","cardiocutaneous syndrome","PTPN11-related lentiginosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The historical LEOPARD acronym captures the core features: multiple Lentigines, ECG conduction abnormalities, Ocular hypertelorism, Pulmonary stenosis, Abnormal genitalia, Retardation of growth, and sensorineural Deafness","It belongs to the RASopathy family and overlaps clinically with classic Noonan syndrome, but the disproportionate cardiac and pigmentary features help separate it","Most cases arise from specific missense variants in PTPN11 that reduce SHP2 phosphatase activity, in contrast to the activating variants that typically cause classic Noonan syndrome; RAF1 and BRAF variants account for a minority","Widespread flat brown macules (lentigines) accumulate from childhood and are frequently accompanied by café-au-lait macules, whereas lentigines are not a feature of classic Noonan","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is markedly more common than in classic Noonan, while valvar pulmonary stenosis remains the characteristic right-sided lesion","Sensorineural hearing loss and genital anomalies such as cryptorchidism or hypospadias are recognised components that warrant proactive screening","Management is multidisciplinary and surveillance-based, centring on cardiology follow-up (echocardiography and ECG), audiology, growth monitoring, and developmental support"],"pathophysiology":"The condition is one of the RASopathies, a group of developmental disorders caused by dysregulation of the RAS-MAPK signalling pathway. The majority of cases result from germline missense variants in PTPN11, which encodes the protein tyrosine phosphatase SHP2. Unlike the gain-of-function variants underlying most classic Noonan syndrome, the variants seen here tend to impair the catalytic activity of SHP2, producing a distinct dominant effect on downstream signalling that shapes the characteristic pigmentary, cardiac, auditory, and craniofacial phenotype. A smaller proportion of patients carry variants in other pathway genes such as RAF1 or BRAF. Inheritance is autosomal dominant with variable expressivity, and many cases represent de novo events.","additionalDifferentials":["Classic Noonan syndrome","Neurofibromatosis type 1 (café-au-lait and cutaneous overlap)","Carney complex (lentigines with cardiac myxomas and endocrine tumours)","Peutz-Jeghers syndrome (mucocutaneous pigmentation)","Costello syndrome and cardiofaciocutaneous syndrome (other RASopathies)","Legius syndrome","Familial hypertrophic cardiomyopathy without syndromic features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted or panel-based molecular genetic testing for PTPN11 and related RASopathy genes","Baseline and serial echocardiography to detect hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and pulmonary stenosis","Electrocardiography to identify conduction abnormalities","Formal audiological assessment for sensorineural hearing loss","Growth monitoring with attention to short stature and consideration of endocrine review","Genital and developmental assessment, with genetic counselling for the family"],"redFlags":["Progressive left ventricular hypertrophy or outflow obstruction on echocardiography","Syncope, arrhythmia, or high-grade conduction block suggesting cardiac decompensation","Sudden clinical deterioration in an infant with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Rapidly changing or atypical pigmented lesions requiring dermatological review"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is largely determined by the cardiac phenotype: many patients have a normal or near-normal lifespan, but significant hypertrophic cardiomyopathy carries a risk of arrhythmia, heart failure, and sudden death, making lifelong cardiac surveillance essential. The lentigines are cosmetic and benign, and cognition is usually normal or only mildly affected.","pitfalls":["Attributing the café-au-lait and lentiginous pigmentation to neurofibromatosis and missing the underlying RASopathy","Assuming the cardiac risk mirrors classic Noonan and under-appreciating the higher burden of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Overlooking sensorineural hearing loss because it can be subtle and progressive","Failing to arrange genetic counselling given autosomal dominant inheritance and variable expressivity"],"management":["Centre management on the cardiac phenotype, monitoring and treating hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with agents such as beta-blockers and managing the characteristic valvar pulmonary stenosis","Screen for conduction abnormalities and arrhythmia with electrocardiography and ambulatory monitoring, maintaining lifelong cardiac surveillance for the risk of heart failure and sudden death","Arrange formal audiological assessment and hearing rehabilitation for sensorineural hearing loss","Screen for and manage genital anomalies such as cryptorchidism or hypospadias","Monitor growth and consider endocrine review for short stature","Document the accumulating lentigines and café-au-lait macules dermatologically, reassuring on their benign cosmetic nature","Provide genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant RASopathy and offer testing of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (University of Washington)","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"normal_pressure_hydrocephalus":{"name":"Normal pressure hydrocephalus","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NPH","idiopathic normal pressure hydrocephalus","Hakim-Adams syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically presents with the triad of gait disturbance, cognitive impairment, and urinary incontinence, though not all three are always present","Gait difficulty is usually the earliest and most prominent feature and is often described as magnetic, broad-based and shuffling, sometimes termed a gait apraxia","It is one of the few potentially reversible causes of dementia, so recognition matters because shunting can help selected patients","Imaging shows ventricular enlargement out of proportion to cortical atrophy, and disproportionate widening of the Sylvian fissures relative to compressed high-convexity sulci is characteristic","Cerebrospinal fluid opening pressure is within the normal range, distinguishing it from obstructive high-pressure hydrocephalus","A high-volume lumbar puncture (tap test) or extended lumbar drainage that transiently improves gait helps predict a favourable response to shunting","Gait tends to respond best to shunting, cognition variably, and long-standing incontinence least reliably","The condition predominantly affects older adults and is frequently misattributed to ageing or Parkinson disease"],"pathophysiology":"Normal pressure hydrocephalus is a communicating hydrocephalus in which impaired cerebrospinal fluid absorption leads to ventricular enlargement despite normal mean intracranial pressure. Intermittent pressure waves and altered periventricular compliance are thought to distort the corona radiata fibres, particularly those serving the legs and bladder, producing the characteristic clinical triad. It may be idiopathic or secondary to prior subarachnoid haemorrhage, meningitis, or head injury that scars the arachnoid granulations.","additionalDifferentials":["Alzheimer disease and other neurodegenerative dementias","Parkinson disease and vascular parkinsonism","Cerebral small vessel disease with vascular cognitive impairment","Obstructive hydrocephalus from a mass lesion","Cervical or lumbar spondylotic myelopathy affecting gait"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain to assess ventricular size, callosal angle, and the DESH pattern of disproportionate CSF distribution","Large-volume lumbar puncture with pre- and post-tap gait assessment","Extended external lumbar drainage in equivocal cases to predict shunt responsiveness","Neuropsychological testing to characterise the cognitive profile","Assessment for comorbid neurodegenerative or vascular disease that may limit surgical benefit"],"redFlags":["The full gait, cognitive and continence triad in an older adult should prompt evaluation for a treatable, potentially reversible cause rather than being dismissed as ageing","Rapidly progressive deterioration or focal deficits suggesting an obstructing mass or alternative diagnosis","Headache, vomiting or reduced consciousness indicating raised pressure and possible acute obstruction","Shunt malfunction or infection after surgery presenting with fever or acute neurological decline"],"prognosis":"Carefully selected patients, especially those with predominant early gait impairment and a positive tap or drainage test, often gain meaningful and sometimes durable benefit from ventriculoperitoneal shunting. Prognosis is worse when significant coexisting neurodegenerative or vascular disease is present or when symptoms are long-standing.","pitfalls":["Attributing the syndrome to normal ageing or a primary dementia and missing a reversible cause","Selecting patients for shunting on imaging alone without supportive CSF drainage testing","Underestimating the surgical risks of shunting, including subdural collections, infection, and over- or under-drainage","Expecting equal improvement in all three symptoms when gait typically responds best and incontinence least"],"management":["Offer ventriculoperitoneal shunting to carefully selected patients, especially those with predominant early gait impairment, as the definitive treatment","Select candidates with a large-volume lumbar puncture tap test or extended external lumbar drainage assessing gait response before committing to surgery","Use a programmable shunt valve and adjust it to manage overdrainage complications","Provide supportive gait and physiotherapy rehabilitation, continence management and cognitive support","Monitor after shunting for complications including subdural collections, infection and shunt malfunction","Assess for coexisting neurodegenerative or vascular disease that limits surgical benefit and informs prognosis","Manage jointly through neurology and neurosurgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"normal_tension_glaucoma":{"name":"Normal tension glaucoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NTG","normal-pressure glaucoma","low-tension glaucoma","normotensive glaucoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of open-angle glaucoma with characteristic glaucomatous optic-nerve cupping and visual field loss despite intraocular pressure that remains within the statistically normal range","The optic disc often shows more focal notching, acquired pits, and a higher frequency of splinter disc haemorrhages at the neuroretinal rim than typical high-pressure glaucoma","Field defects tend to be deeper, more localised, and closer to fixation, so central vision may be threatened earlier","Vascular dysregulation is common, with associations such as migraine, Raynaud phenomenon, nocturnal hypotension, and obstructive sleep apnoea","Diagnosis requires excluding secondary and previously higher-pressure causes, including diurnal pressure spikes missed on single readings and thin corneas that underestimate true pressure","Management still centres on lowering intraocular pressure, since a substantial pressure reduction slows progression even when the baseline pressure appears normal","A disc haemorrhage is an important warning sign of ongoing damage and warrants closer monitoring or intensified treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Normal tension glaucoma is a progressive optic neuropathy showing the structural and functional hallmarks of glaucoma while intraocular pressure stays within the normal statistical range. Damage is thought to arise from a combination of pressure-dependent stress on a susceptible optic nerve head and pressure-independent factors, particularly impaired optic-nerve blood flow from vascular dysregulation, systemic and nocturnal hypotension, and possibly a low cerebrospinal-fluid pressure that increases the translaminar pressure gradient. The result is loss of retinal ganglion cells and their axons, producing focal rim thinning, disc haemorrhages, and corresponding nerve-fibre-bundle field defects that often lie nearer fixation than in high-pressure disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary open-angle glaucoma with unrecognised pressure spikes or thin corneas","Previously elevated (burnt-out) glaucoma such as a resolved pressure-raising episode","Compressive or infiltrative optic neuropathy mimicking glaucomatous cupping","Ischaemic optic neuropathy with pallor and field loss","Congenital optic disc anomalies such as pits or colobomas","Old optic neuritis or other non-glaucomatous optic atrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diurnal intraocular pressure phasing to detect peaks missed on isolated measurements","Central corneal thickness measurement to correct apparent pressure readings","OCT of the retinal nerve fibre layer and ganglion cell complex to quantify structural loss","Automated perimetry to characterise and monitor field defects near fixation","Neuroimaging when disc pallor, atypical field loss, or rapid progression suggests a non-glaucomatous cause","Assessment for vascular risk factors including blood pressure profile and sleep apnoea when indicated"],"redFlags":["Optic disc pallor out of proportion to cupping or field loss respecting the vertical midline, suggesting a compressive or neurological cause needing imaging","Rapidly progressive field loss despite apparently normal pressure","Recurrent disc haemorrhages signalling continued optic-nerve damage","Central or paracentral defects threatening fixation and reading vision"],"prognosis":"Many eyes progress slowly, and lowering intraocular pressure substantially reduces the rate of visual field deterioration, but a subset with marked vascular dysregulation or recurrent disc haemorrhages progress despite treatment and risk central visual loss.","pitfalls":["Withholding treatment because the pressure looks normal, ignoring evidence of progression","Missing diurnal pressure spikes or overestimating true pressure in eyes with thick corneas and underestimating it in thin corneas","Failing to image the brain and orbit when disc or field features are atypical for glaucoma","Overlooking nocturnal hypotension, sometimes iatrogenic from evening antihypertensives, as a driver of progression","Interpreting any normal-pressure cupping as glaucoma without excluding neurological causes"],"management":["Lower intraocular pressure substantially with topical medical therapy led by a prostaglandin analogue, since pressure reduction slows progression even from a normal baseline","Escalate pressure-lowering with additional classes such as beta-blockers, alpha-agonists or carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, laser trabeculoplasty, or filtration surgery like trabeculectomy for continued progression","Address pressure-independent vascular factors — avoid excessive antihypertensive-induced nocturnal hypotension and treat obstructive sleep apnoea and migraine-related dysregulation","Monitor progression with serial OCT of the retinal nerve fibre layer and ganglion cell complex and with automated perimetry focused on defects near fixation","Detect missed pressure peaks with diurnal phasing and correct apparent readings for central corneal thickness","Exclude non-glaucomatous and neurological causes with neuroimaging when disc pallor, atypical field loss or rapid progression is present","Refer to ophthalmology for structured glaucoma surveillance and stepwise treatment escalation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"norrie_disease":{"name":"Norrie disease","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NDP","norrin deficiency","atrophia bulborum hereditaria","congenital progressive oculoacousticocerebral degeneration"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Emery's Principles of Medical Genetics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked recessive disorder causing congenital bilateral blindness in affected males, typically presenting with leukocoria","Caused by mutations in the NDP gene encoding norrin, which regulates retinal vascular development","The retina is severely dysplastic, often with a retrolental fibrovascular mass (pseudoglioma) resembling a mass lesion","Progressive ocular changes include cataract, retinal detachment and eventual shrinkage of the globe (phthisis bulbi)","About a third of patients develop progressive sensorineural hearing loss, and some have developmental or behavioural difficulties","It is an important differential for leukocoria and must be distinguished from retinoblastoma","Female carriers are usually unaffected but can transmit the condition to sons"],"pathophysiology":"Norrie disease is caused by mutations in the NDP gene, which encodes norrin, a secreted protein that activates a Wnt-signalling pathway essential for normal development of the retinal and inner-ear vasculature. Loss of norrin function disrupts retinal angiogenesis, leading to severe retinal dysplasia, failure of normal retinal vascularisation, and formation of a fibrovascular retrolental mass, with progression to retinal detachment and a blind, shrunken eye. The same signalling defect impairs cochlear vascular development, accounting for the later progressive sensorineural hearing loss, and central nervous system involvement can produce cognitive and behavioural features.","additionalDifferentials":["Retinoblastoma presenting with leukocoria","Persistent fetal vasculature (persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous)","Familial exudative vitreoretinopathy, also involving the NDP pathway","Retinopathy of prematurity in a preterm infant","Coats disease with exudative retinal detachment","Congenital cataract or other causes of leukocoria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination under anaesthesia with detailed fundoscopy in the infant","Ocular ultrasound to characterise the retrolental mass and detachment and help exclude retinoblastoma","MRI when needed to distinguish from an intraocular tumour","Audiological assessment with monitoring for progressive sensorineural hearing loss","Molecular genetic testing of the NDP gene with carrier testing of relatives"],"redFlags":["Leukocoria in an infant, which must be urgently investigated to exclude retinoblastoma","Rapid progression to retinal detachment threatening any residual vision","New or progressive hearing loss requiring audiological review","Developmental regression or behavioural change suggesting central involvement"],"prognosis":"Visual prognosis is very poor, with most affected males blind from birth or early infancy and little response to surgical intervention; sensorineural hearing loss tends to progress over time, and prognosis for hearing and development varies, so long-term multidisciplinary support is needed.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the retrolental mass for retinoblastoma or vice versa and delaying correct management","Assuming an isolated eye disorder and not monitoring for later hearing loss","Overlooking the X-linked inheritance and the need for carrier counselling","Attributing developmental problems to visual impairment alone"],"management":["No therapy restores the primary retinal dysplasia; early vitreoretinal surgery may be attempted for retinal detachment, though visual outcomes remain poor","Visual rehabilitation from infancy with low-vision support, Braille and orientation-and-mobility training given congenital blindness","Regular audiological surveillance with hearing aids and cochlear implantation for progressive sensorineural hearing loss","Neurodevelopmental and behavioural support for associated cognitive and behavioural difficulties","Coordinated multidisciplinary care across ophthalmology, audiology, developmental paediatrics and clinical genetics","Genetic counselling for this X-linked recessive NDP disorder, with carrier testing of female relatives and reproductive options"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Emery's Elements of Medical Genetics and Genomics"],"crossLinks":[]},"north_american_indian_childhood_cirrhosis":{"name":"North American Indian childhood cirrhosis","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NAIC","CIRH1A","UTP4 deficiency","cirhin deficiency","Ojibway-Cree childhood cirrhosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / Suchy Liver Disease in Children","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive cholestatic cirrhosis described in Ojibway-Cree children of northwestern Quebec, caused by a founder mutation in CIRH1A (now UTP4), encoding cirhin","Typically presents with transient neonatal jaundice that resolves, followed by insidious progression to biliary cirrhosis during childhood","Portal hypertension with splenomegaly and oesophageal varices is a common mode of later presentation","Liver biochemistry shows a cholestatic picture, and biopsy demonstrates portal fibrosis progressing to established cirrhosis with bile duct proliferation","Cirhin is a nucleolar protein involved in ribosomal RNA processing; how its dysfunction produces a liver-specific phenotype is not fully understood","A single homozygous founder variant (R565W) accounts for essentially all affected individuals in the original population","Management is supportive with ursodeoxycholic acid and fat-soluble vitamins, and liver transplantation is the definitive treatment for end-stage disease"],"pathophysiology":"North American Indian childhood cirrhosis is an autosomal recessive cholestatic disease caused by a founder missense mutation in CIRH1A (UTP4), which encodes cirhin, a nucleolar protein implicated in pre-ribosomal RNA processing. Affected children develop neonatal cholestasis that appears to remit, but progressive portal and periportal fibrosis continues silently, culminating in biliary cirrhosis and portal hypertension in childhood or adolescence. The exact mechanism by which impaired cirhin function selectively injures the liver remains uncertain, though disturbed ribosome biogenesis and biliary epithelial dysfunction are proposed.","additionalDifferentials":["Biliary atresia","Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis","Alagille syndrome","Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency","Congenital hepatic fibrosis","Autoimmune hepatitis with early cirrhosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Liver function tests and serum bile acids showing a cholestatic pattern","Abdominal ultrasound and elastography to assess liver architecture and fibrosis","Liver biopsy demonstrating portal fibrosis, ductular reaction, and evolving cirrhosis","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy to screen for varices in portal hypertension","Targeted genetic testing for the CIRH1A/UTP4 founder variant","Fat-soluble vitamin levels and coagulation profile"],"redFlags":["Variceal haemorrhage or other complications of portal hypertension","Progressive jaundice and synthetic failure indicating decompensated cirrhosis","Growth failure with severe fat-soluble vitamin deficiency","Ascites or hepatic encephalopathy signalling end-stage liver disease"],"prognosis":"Without treatment the disease progresses to cirrhosis and portal hypertension during childhood or adolescence, and many patients ultimately require liver transplantation, which is curative; outcomes after transplantation are generally good.","pitfalls":["Being reassured by the apparent resolution of neonatal jaundice while fibrosis progresses silently","Failing to consider the diagnosis outside a recognised ancestry when the phenotype fits","Delaying variceal surveillance in a child with unexplained splenomegaly","Confusing the histology with biliary atresia or other paediatric cholestatic disorders"],"management":["Ursodeoxycholic acid to improve bile flow and relieve cholestatic symptoms","Fat-soluble vitamin supplementation and nutritional support with medium-chain-triglyceride-based feeds to counter fat malabsorption and growth faltering","Antipruritic therapy for cholestatic itch using bile-acid sequestrants or rifampicin","Endoscopic surveillance for oesophageal varices with band ligation and non-selective beta-blockade for portal hypertension","Prompt resuscitation and endoscopic band ligation for acute variceal haemorrhage","Timely referral for liver transplantation, which is curative in end-stage biliary cirrhosis","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive CIRH1A/UTP4 founder disorder, with testing of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Suchy, Sokol & Balistreri, Liver Disease in Children"],"crossLinks":[]},"north_carolina_macular_dystrophy":{"name":"North Carolina macular dystrophy","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NCMD","MCDR1","PRDM13","central areolar pigment epithelial dystrophy (historic)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ryan's Retina / Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant, highly penetrant but characteristically non-progressive (or minimally progressive) macular dystrophy linked to the MCDR1 locus, involving regulatory changes affecting PRDM13","Congenital and stable, so the striking fundus appearance often contrasts with surprisingly preserved and unchanging visual acuity","Graded by severity: grade 1 shows fine drusen-like deposits at the fovea, grade 2 confluent deposits, and grade 3 a coloboma-like macular staphylomatous or excavated atrophic lesion","Despite dramatic grade 3 lesions, many patients retain reasonable acuity because the changes are longstanding and stable","Full-field electroretinography and electro-oculography are typically normal, reflecting disease confined to the macula","Choroidal neovascularisation is an uncommon but recognised complication that can cause acute visual loss","Management is largely observation and genetic counselling, with treatment reserved for secondary choroidal neovascularisation"],"pathophysiology":"North Carolina macular dystrophy is a developmental, dominantly inherited macular malformation mapping to the MCDR1 locus on chromosome 6, associated with non-coding regulatory variants affecting PRDM13, a transcription factor important in retinal development. The result is an abnormally formed central macula ranging from small drusen-like deposits to a large excavated coloboma-like lesion. Because the abnormality is congenital and developmental rather than degenerative, it is essentially stationary, which explains the mismatch between the dramatic appearance and the stable, often relatively preserved, acuity.","additionalDifferentials":["Central areolar choroidal dystrophy","Age-related macular degeneration with drusen","Stargardt disease and other flecked macular dystrophies","Torn or excavated macular lesions such as macular coloboma","Toxoplasma macular scar (acquired)","Best disease and pattern dystrophies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination and imaging to grade the macular lesion","Optical coherence tomography to characterise the drusen-like deposits or excavated lesion","Fundus autofluorescence for the pattern of macular involvement","Full-field electroretinography and electro-oculography, expected to be normal","Molecular genetic testing (MCDR1 locus / PRDM13-associated variants)","Fluorescein angiography or OCT-angiography if choroidal neovascularisation is suspected"],"redFlags":["Sudden distortion or drop in vision suggesting choroidal neovascularisation","Documented progression, which is atypical and should prompt review of the diagnosis","Subretinal fluid or haemorrhage at the macula","Affected relatives requiring examination given high-penetrance dominant inheritance"],"prognosis":"Excellent for stability, as the condition is congenital and non-progressive; many patients keep useful acuity lifelong, with the main threat to vision being occasional secondary choroidal neovascularisation.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the stable macular lesion for progressive age-related macular degeneration and over-treating","Assuming poor acuity from the dramatic fundus appearance and not measuring actual function","Missing choroidal neovascularisation as a treatable cause of sudden decline","Failing to recognise the dominant inheritance and examine relatives"],"management":["Reassurance and observation, as the malformation is congenital and non-progressive with no disease-modifying therapy required","Intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy for secondary choroidal neovascularisation causing acute visual loss","Low-vision aids and refractive correction to optimise function in those with excavated grade 3 lesions","Regular fundus review with home Amsler-grid self-monitoring to detect choroidal neovascularisation early","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant PRDM13/MCDR1-associated dystrophy, with examination of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ryan's Retina","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"notalgia_paraesthetica":{"name":"notalgia paraesthetica","system":"nervous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["notalgia paresthetica","posterior ramus sensory neuropathy of the back"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A sensory neuropathy of the dorsal rami of thoracic spinal nerves presenting as a localised, chronically itchy patch on the upper or mid back, usually just medial to the scapula.","The hallmark is a well-circumscribed area of pruritus often accompanied by burning, tingling or a localised hyperpigmented patch from chronic rubbing and scratching.","The hyperpigmentation is secondary to chronic friction and postinflammatory change rather than a primary pigmentary disorder, and biopsy may show amyloid deposition.","The distribution corresponds to the posterior rami of thoracic dermatomes, most often in the T2 to T6 range.","Symptoms may improve transiently with cooling or capsaicin and are typically resistant to conventional antihistamines, reflecting the neuropathic mechanism.","An association with degenerative changes of the thoracic spine and with multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A has been described.","It is closely analogous to brachioradial pruritus, which affects the dorsolateral forearm through a comparable neuropathic mechanism.","Diagnosis is clinical; the key is to recognise the neuropathic nature and avoid an exhaustive search for primary skin disease."],"pathophysiology":"Notalgia paraesthetica is attributed to dysfunction of the posterior primary rami of the thoracic spinal nerves supplying sensation to the paravertebral skin of the back. Proposed mechanisms include entrapment or compression of these cutaneous nerve branches as they pass through the paraspinal musculature at a near right angle, and irritation associated with degenerative thoracic spine disease. The resulting neuropathic itch and dysaesthesia provoke chronic rubbing and scratching, which produce the characteristic hyperpigmented, sometimes lichenified patch; histology may reveal dermal amyloid deposition thought to be secondary to the chronic friction rather than a primary amyloidosis.","additionalDifferentials":["brachioradial pruritus","macular or lichen amyloidosis","postinflammatory hyperpigmentation from another dermatosis","tinea or other localised cutaneous infection","contact or atopic dermatitis","thoracic radiculopathy or herpes zoster-related neuropathic itch","cutaneous T-cell lymphoma presenting as a persistent patch"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["clinical examination mapping the itch to a thoracic dermatomal distribution","thoracic spine imaging to assess degenerative or structural change when clinically indicated","skin biopsy where the diagnosis is uncertain or to exclude amyloid and other dermatoses","screening for features of multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A in selected patients","dermoscopy to characterise the pigmented patch","assessment of posture and musculoskeletal contributors","review for coexisting neuropathic itch syndromes such as brachioradial pruritus"],"redFlags":["an evolving, indurated or ulcerated plaque that could represent cutaneous malignancy","associated neurological deficit or radicular pain suggesting significant spinal pathology","features suggesting multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A such as relevant family history or endocrine symptoms","rapidly spreading or systemic symptoms inconsistent with a localised neuropathic itch"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign but typically chronic and relapsing, with symptoms that can be controlled but often persist over years. Treatment directed at the neuropathic mechanism and reduction of scratching improves symptoms and the associated pigmentation.","pitfalls":["treating the hyperpigmented patch as a primary pigmentary or infective dermatosis and missing the neuropathic cause","prescribing sedating antihistamines that are ineffective for neuropathic itch","overlooking an association with thoracic spine disease or multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A","attributing a persistent solitary patch to notalgia without considering cutaneous lymphoma when atypical"],"management":["Topical capsaicin to deplete substance P and relieve the neuropathic itch","Oral neuropathic agents such as gabapentinoids or tricyclic antidepressants when topical measures are insufficient, reflecting the neuropathic mechanism","Topical local anaesthetics and cooling for symptomatic relief, with measures to interrupt the itch-scratch cycle that drives hyperpigmentation","Physiotherapy, posture correction and treatment of underlying degenerative thoracic spine disease","Local injection therapy such as botulinum toxin in refractory cases","Screening for multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A in selected patients with suggestive features"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine","Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"notalgia_paresthetica":{"name":"Notalgia paraesthetica","system":"Nervous system / Peripheral nerve","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["notalgia paraesthetica","dorsal ramus neuropathy","posterior thoracic cutaneous neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A sensory neuropathy of the dorsal rami of the thoracic spinal nerves, classically involving the T2-T6 segments","Presents with chronic, often intense localised itch (pruritus) of the upper back, usually just medial to or below the scapula","The itch is frequently accompanied by burning, tingling, or a crawling sensation and by tenderness or hypersensitivity in the same area","A characteristic sign is a well-demarcated, often hyperpigmented patch of skin overlying the affected area, produced by chronic rubbing and scratching","It is a neuropathic itch, so it is typically poorly responsive to topical corticosteroids and antihistamines used for inflammatory itch","Underlying degenerative changes of the thoracic spine are often implicated, compressing or irritating the posterior primary rami as they traverse the paraspinal muscles","Management targets the neuropathic mechanism with agents such as topical capsaicin and neuropathic-pain medications, alongside physiotherapy and addressing any contributing spinal pathology"],"pathophysiology":"Notalgia paraesthetica is attributed to dysfunction of the posterior (dorsal) primary rami of the thoracic spinal nerves, most often at the T2-T6 levels, which supply sensation to the skin of the mid-upper back. These rami take an angulated course through the paraspinal musculature, where they are thought to be vulnerable to compression or irritation, frequently in association with degenerative or postural changes of the thoracic spine. The resulting neuropathy generates a chronic neuropathic itch and dysaesthesia confined to the nerve's cutaneous territory. Persistent scratching and rubbing produce secondary skin changes, notably a localised hyperpigmented lichenified patch, and amyloid may deposit in the chronically traumatised skin. Because the itch is neurogenic rather than inflammatory, conventional anti-itch therapy is largely ineffective.","additionalDifferentials":["Brachioradial pruritus and other neuropathic itch syndromes","Thoracic radiculopathy from disc or degenerative disease","Primary dermatological disease (contact dermatitis, tinea, lichen simplex chronicus)","Macular or lichen amyloidosis","Herpes zoster or post-herpetic neuralgia in a thoracic dermatome","Intraspinal or paraspinal mass irritating the dorsal rami"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical recognition of a well-demarcated pruritic hyperpigmented patch in the T2-T6 distribution","Skin examination to exclude a primary dermatosis","Skin biopsy if amyloid deposition or another dermatosis is suspected","Imaging of the thoracic spine when a compressive or degenerative cause is suspected","Neurological examination to exclude a radiculopathy or myelopathy","Trial of neuropathic therapy as both treatment and supportive confirmation of the mechanism"],"redFlags":["New neurological deficit or band-like sensory level suggesting a spinal cord lesion","A vesicular eruption in the affected dermatome indicating herpes zoster","Rapidly changing or ulcerating skin lesion warranting biopsy for malignancy","Progressive or bilateral symptoms suggesting an underlying structural spinal cause"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign but often chronic and relapsing; symptoms may be controlled with neuropathic therapy and by addressing spinal or postural contributors, though complete and lasting resolution can be difficult to achieve.","pitfalls":["Treating it as an inflammatory dermatosis with steroids and antihistamines, which rarely help neuropathic itch","Mistaking the hyperpigmented lichenified patch for a primary skin disease or malignancy","Overlooking associated thoracic spine degeneration that may be driving the neuropathy","Failing to distinguish it from herpes zoster or a thoracic radiculopathy with a genuine sensory level"],"management":["First-line topical capsaicin to desensitise cutaneous nerve endings and reduce the neuropathic pruritus","Oral neuropathic modulators such as gabapentinoids or amitriptyline for refractory itch, given poor response to antihistamines and topical corticosteroids","Topical anaesthetic preparations and cooling for symptomatic relief while breaking the itch-scratch cycle","Physiotherapy, exercises and postural correction addressing degenerative thoracic spine changes compressing the dorsal rami","Interventional options such as botulinum toxin injection or paravertebral nerve block in resistant cases","Neurological assessment to exclude radiculopathy or myelopathy where features are atypical"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"nstemi":{"name":"Non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NSTEMI","non-ST-segment elevation MI","NSTE-ACS","subendocardial infarction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["NSTEMI is an acute coronary syndrome defined by myocardial necrosis, shown by a rise and/or fall in cardiac troponin, without persistent ST-segment elevation","The ECG typically shows ST depression, transient ST elevation, or T-wave inversion, but may be normal early in the course","It is distinguished from unstable angina solely by the presence of detectable troponin release indicating cell death","It must be separated from STEMI, which requires immediate reperfusion, whereas NSTEMI is managed with an early risk-guided invasive strategy","Serial high-sensitivity troponin with a defined delta over time increases diagnostic accuracy and speeds rule-in or rule-out","Core therapy includes dual antiplatelet therapy, anticoagulation, and anti-ischaemic and secondary-prevention agents, with timing of angiography guided by risk","Higher-risk patients (ongoing ischaemia, haemodynamic or electrical instability, high risk scores) benefit from earlier invasive management","A rise in troponin is not specific to type 1 plaque-rupture infarction and can reflect supply-demand mismatch (type 2) or non-ischaemic myocardial injury"],"pathophysiology":"NSTEMI most commonly arises from rupture or erosion of a coronary plaque with partially occlusive thrombus, or from severe fixed stenosis with superimposed thrombosis, producing prolonged ischaemia sufficient to cause myocyte necrosis. Because flow is reduced rather than completely and persistently interrupted, injury is often subendocardial and the ECG lacks persistent ST elevation. Distal embolisation of platelet-fibrin material and dynamic vasoconstriction contribute. Type 2 infarction reflects an imbalance between oxygen supply and demand (for example from tachyarrhythmia, anaemia, hypotension, or hypertensive crisis) without acute plaque rupture.","additionalDifferentials":["Unstable angina","STEMI","Type 2 myocardial infarction from supply-demand mismatch","Myocarditis","Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy","Pulmonary embolism","Aortic dissection","Non-ischaemic causes of troponin elevation such as sepsis or renal failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial high-sensitivity cardiac troponin with interval delta measurement","Serial 12-lead ECGs to detect dynamic or evolving changes","Continuous rhythm monitoring for arrhythmia and ischaemia","Echocardiography for regional wall-motion abnormalities and ventricular function","Risk stratification scores to determine timing of invasive angiography","Coronary angiography as part of an early invasive strategy in higher-risk patients"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: NSTEMI is an acute coronary syndrome with established myocardial necrosis requiring urgent admission, monitoring, and antithrombotic therapy","Refractory or recurrent ischaemic pain despite optimal medical therapy","Haemodynamic instability, cardiogenic shock, or acute heart failure","Sustained ventricular arrhythmia or high-grade conduction block","New mechanical complications such as acute mitral regurgitation"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend on infarct size, coronary anatomy, left ventricular function, and comorbidities; timely risk-based invasive management and secondary prevention improve survival, though NSTEMI carries meaningful medium- and long-term mortality, sometimes exceeding STEMI over the longer term because of older age and comorbidity burden.","pitfalls":["Interpreting any raised troponin as type 1 infarction without considering type 2 injury or non-ischaemic causes","Mislabelling an NSTEMI as STEMI or vice versa and applying the wrong reperfusion pathway","Relying on a single early troponin or ECG to exclude infarction","Delaying dual antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy while awaiting angiography in eligible patients"],"management":["Dual antiplatelet therapy with aspirin plus a P2Y12 inhibitor","Parenteral anticoagulation such as fondaparinux or low-molecular-weight heparin during the acute phase","Anti-ischaemic therapy with beta-blockade and nitrates, using a calcium-channel blocker where beta-blockers are contraindicated","Risk-stratified early invasive coronary angiography with revascularisation by PCI or CABG, timed according to risk score","Secondary prevention with a high-intensity statin, an ACE inhibitor and cardiac rehabilitation, alongside smoking cessation and dietary modification","Continuous rhythm monitoring with management of arrhythmic and haemodynamic complications","For type 2 infarction, correction of the underlying supply-demand imbalance such as anaemia, tachyarrhythmia or hypotension"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"numb_chin_syndrome":{"name":"Numb chin syndrome","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mental neuropathy","mental nerve neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurology and oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Numbness of the chin and lower lip in the distribution of the mental (or inferior alveolar) nerve","It is an important red-flag sign because it is frequently caused by underlying malignancy","Recognised causes include metastatic breast and prostate cancer, lymphoma and leukaemic infiltration, and it may herald relapse","Benign causes exist (dental or local trauma, mandibular osteomyelitis, sickle cell disease), but malignancy must be excluded","The lesion may be at the mandible (mental nerve) or more proximally at the skull base or leptomeninges","Investigation targets the whole nerve pathway and a search for systemic malignancy"],"pathophysiology":"Numb chin syndrome arises from injury to the mental nerve or its parent inferior alveolar/mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve, anywhere from the leptomeninges and skull base to the mandibular canal and mental foramen. Malignant causes act by perineural spread, mandibular or leptomeningeal metastasis, or direct compression, which is why the sign so often reflects cancer. Benign local processes injure the nerve within the mandible. The chin and lip sensory loss reflects the terminal cutaneous territory of the affected nerve.","additionalDifferentials":["Metastatic malignancy (breast, prostate) or lymphoma/leukaemia","Leptomeningeal or skull-base disease","Dental or mandibular local pathology / trauma","Mandibular osteomyelitis","Sickle cell disease (vaso-occlusion)","Multiple sclerosis or other trigeminal pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain, skull base and mandible with attention to the trigeminal/mental nerve pathway","Orthopantomogram and dental assessment","Investigation for systemic malignancy (imaging, tumour markers) where a cause is not evident","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis if leptomeningeal disease is suspected","Full blood count and film if leukaemia is considered"],"redFlags":["Known or suspected malignancy (new numb chin may signal metastasis or relapse)","Associated other cranial neuropathies","Systemic symptoms of malignancy","Progressive or bilateral involvement"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is driven by the underlying cause; when due to malignancy it often signifies advanced or recurrent disease and a poor outlook, whereas benign causes may resolve.","pitfalls":["Dismissing chin numbness as dental without excluding malignancy","Failing to image the entire nerve pathway including the skull base","Overlooking it as a marker of cancer relapse","Not considering leptomeningeal disease"],"management":["Urgent investigation and treatment directed at the underlying cause, most importantly excluding and treating malignancy","Oncological therapy such as chemotherapy, radiotherapy and intrathecal treatment for leptomeningeal disease where metastatic breast, prostate or haematological cancer is identified","Treatment of benign causes such as dental infection, mandibular osteomyelitis or trauma once malignancy is excluded","Neuropathic analgesia for the chin and lip sensory symptoms","Multidisciplinary involvement of oncology, maxillofacial surgery and neurology","Recognition that a new numb chin in a patient with cancer may herald relapse, prompting restaging and escalation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Oncology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"nummular_headache":{"name":"Nummular headache","system":"Nervous system / Headache","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["coin-shaped headache","coin-shaped cephalgia"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / International Classification of Sleep Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pain is confined to a small, sharply demarcated rounded or elliptical area of the scalp, typically a few centimetres across","The painful patch stays fixed in shape, size and location over time","The parietal region is the most common site, though any part of the scalp may be affected","Local sensory changes such as tenderness, tingling, numbness or allodynia are often present within the zone","Pain may be continuous or intermittent and ranges from mild to moderate","It is thought to arise from terminal branches of the scalp sensory nerves rather than from an intracranial source","Examination and, when indicated, imaging should exclude a local structural or dermatological lesion"],"pathophysiology":"Nummular headache is thought to be a disorder of the terminal twigs of the scalp sensory nerves, producing pain and sensory disturbance restricted to a small, well-defined territory of the epicranial tissues. The sharply localised, fixed area of tenderness and dysaesthesia points to a peripheral, extracranial focus rather than referred or intracranial pain, although central sensitisation may contribute to its persistence.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary stabbing headache","Occipital or other scalp neuralgia","Local scalp or skull pathology such as a cyst or osteoma","Herpes zoster or post-herpetic neuralgia of the scalp","Giant cell arteritis when the temporal region of an older patient is involved","Referred pain from cervical or dental structures"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful mapping of the painful area and its associated sensory changes","Palpation for local masses and scalp or skull abnormalities","Skin examination to exclude a dermatological or zoster lesion","Neuroimaging or skull imaging when a structural cause is suspected","Inflammatory markers if giant cell arteritis is a consideration in an older patient"],"redFlags":["A palpable mass, skin change or bony abnormality beneath the painful area","Progressive enlargement of the affected zone","Features of giant cell arteritis in an older patient with temporal involvement","Systemic symptoms or a rapid change in the pain pattern"],"prognosis":"The condition is usually benign; many patients have a chronic but stable course, and symptoms often respond to simple analgesia, gabapentinoids or local injection therapy when troublesome.","pitfalls":["Assuming all fixed scalp pain is primary and missing an underlying local lesion","Overlooking giant cell arteritis when the patch overlies the temporal artery in an older patient","Not examining the skin and skull beneath the painful zone","Escalating systemic analgesics without considering targeted local treatment"],"management":["Reassurance and simple analgesia for mild, intermittent symptoms","Neuropathic agents such as gabapentinoids or tricyclic antidepressants for troublesome or persistent pain","Local infiltration with anaesthetic and corticosteroid, or botulinum toxin, into the affected scalp zone in refractory cases","Topical agents such as capsaicin or local anaesthetic as an adjunct over the fixed painful patch","Exclusion of a structural scalp or skull lesion, and of giant cell arteritis in older patients, before attributing symptoms to the primary disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (ICHD-3)","The Headaches (Olesen et al.)"],"crossLinks":[]},"nursemaids_elbow":{"name":"nursemaids elbow","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pulled elbow","Radial head subluxation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Nursemaid's elbow is a subluxation of the radial head beneath the annular ligament and is the commonest elbow injury of early childhood, most often in toddlers and pre-school children.","The classic mechanism is a sudden longitudinal (axial) pull on a pronated, extended forearm, such as lifting or swinging a child by the hand or arm.","The child typically holds the arm slightly flexed and pronated against the body and declines to use it, yet is otherwise settled with no swelling or deformity.","The absence of swelling, bruising, focal bony tenderness or deformity helps distinguish it from a fracture.","When the history and examination are classic, with no bony tenderness or swelling, radiographs are not required before attempting reduction.","Reduction is achieved by supination-flexion or by hyperpronation of the forearm; a palpable click is often felt and the child usually resumes normal use within minutes.","If the mechanism is atypical, there is significant swelling, or reduction fails, radiographs should be obtained to exclude a fracture such as a supracondylar or radial neck injury.","Recurrence is common until the radial head and annular ligament mature, so carers should be advised against pulling the child by the hand or wrist."],"pathophysiology":"Nursemaid's elbow arises when sudden axial traction is applied to an extended, pronated forearm, causing the annular ligament that encircles the radial neck to slip proximally over the head of the radius and become partially interposed within the radiohumeral joint. In young children the radial head is relatively small and incompletely ossified and the annular ligament is thin and lax, which permits this displacement; the trapped ligament produces pain and reluctance to use the arm while the joint itself remains structurally intact. As the radial head enlarges and the ligament strengthens with growth, the anatomy no longer permits the ligament to slip, and the condition ceases to recur.","additionalDifferentials":["Supracondylar fracture of the humerus","Radial head or radial neck fracture","Clavicle fracture presenting as a reluctant-to-use arm in a toddler","Elbow dislocation","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis involving the elbow","Non-accidental injury","Torus or buckle fracture of the distal forearm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis alone in classic presentations, with no imaging required","Elbow radiographs in anteroposterior and lateral views if atypical features, swelling or failed reduction","Comparison views of the contralateral elbow where there is diagnostic uncertainty","Ultrasound in selected uncertain cases","Assessment for non-accidental injury where the history is inconsistent or the injury unexplained","Observation of spontaneous, pain-free arm use after reduction as confirmation of success"],"redFlags":["Significant swelling, bruising, deformity or focal bony tenderness suggesting a fracture","An inconsistent, absent or implausible mechanism raising concern for non-accidental injury","Neurovascular compromise of the limb","Failure to regain normal use after appropriate reduction attempts","Fever, erythema or warmth around the joint suggesting infection"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; reduction is usually rapid and complete with immediate return of function and no long-term sequelae. Recurrence during the toddler years is common, but the tendency resolves with skeletal maturation.","pitfalls":["Attempting reduction when swelling or deformity suggests an unrecognised fracture","Assuming every toddler who will not use an arm has a pulled elbow and thereby missing a supracondylar fracture","Failing to consider non-accidental injury when the history is inconsistent","Neglecting to counsel carers on prevention, leading to recurrence","Obtaining radiographs in every case when the presentation is classic and reduction straightforward"],"management":["Closed reduction by supination-flexion or hyperpronation manoeuvre, without radiographs in classic presentations","Confirmation of success by observing spontaneous, pain-free use of the arm within minutes, often after a palpable click","Simple analgesia and reassurance, with no immobilisation required after successful reduction","Radiographs and reassessment if reduction fails, the presentation is atypical, or there is swelling or focal bony tenderness suggesting fracture","Parental education to avoid axial traction on the arm, such as lifting or swinging a child by the hand, as recurrence is common until skeletal maturation","Consideration of non-accidental injury where the history is inconsistent or the mechanism implausible"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Rockwood and Wilkins' Fractures in Children"],"crossLinks":[]},"nut_midline_carcinoma":{"name":"NUT carcinoma","system":"Oncology / Pathology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NUT midline carcinoma","nuclear protein in testis carcinoma","NUTM1-rearranged carcinoma","BRD4-NUT carcinoma","NMC"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by rearrangement of the NUTM1 gene, most often fused to BRD4 (less commonly BRD3 or NSD3), producing an oncogenic fusion protein that blocks squamous differentiation","Arises predominantly in midline structures such as the mediastinum, thymus, lung and head and neck, though non-midline sites are increasingly recognised","Typically affects children, adolescents and younger adults, but can occur at any age and shows no strong sex predilection","Histology shows a monotonous poorly differentiated carcinoma with abrupt (rather than gradual) foci of squamous keratinisation","Diagnosis is confirmed by the monoclonal NUT immunohistochemical antibody, which gives a characteristic speckled nuclear staining pattern in the majority of cases","Should be considered in any young patient with an aggressive undifferentiated or poorly differentiated midline carcinoma of unknown primary","Often initially misdiagnosed as another poorly differentiated squamous carcinoma, thymic carcinoma, Ewing sarcoma or germ cell tumour before molecular testing"],"pathophysiology":"NUT carcinoma is driven by a balanced rearrangement of the NUTM1 gene, most commonly generating a BRD4-NUT fusion in which a bromodomain protein is joined to nuclear protein in testis. The fusion protein sequesters histone-modifying machinery and drives aberrant chromatin acetylation across large genomic regions, locking cells in a proliferative, undifferentiated state and preventing terminal squamous maturation. This single genetic event is sufficient to produce a highly aggressive epithelial malignancy, and the resulting dependence on chromatin readers and acetylation underpins interest in bromodomain and histone-deacetylase directed therapies.","additionalDifferentials":["Poorly differentiated or basaloid squamous cell carcinoma","Thymic carcinoma and other mediastinal carcinomas","SMARCA4-deficient undifferentiated thoracic tumour","Ewing sarcoma and other small round blue cell tumours","Germ cell tumour, particularly in mediastinal disease","Sinonasal undifferentiated carcinoma","Lymphoma involving the mediastinum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["NUT immunohistochemistry showing speckled nuclear positivity as the principal screening test","Molecular confirmation of NUTM1 rearrangement by FISH break-apart probe or next-generation sequencing","Broad immunohistochemical panel including squamous, epithelial, germ cell and haematolymphoid markers to exclude mimics","Cross-sectional imaging of chest, abdomen and pelvis to define the primary site and stage disease","Biopsy with adequate tissue for ancillary molecular studies given the undifferentiated appearance","Assessment for distant metastases, which are frequently present at diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging midline mass in a child, adolescent or young adult","Airway compromise or superior vena cava obstruction from a bulky mediastinal or thoracic tumour","Widespread metastatic disease at presentation, often to bone and lung","Aggressive clinical course with rapid progression despite conventional therapy"],"prognosis":"NUT carcinoma is one of the most aggressive human solid tumours, with rapid local progression, early metastasis and generally short survival despite multimodality treatment; long-term survival is uncommon, although outcomes may be marginally better for non-thoracic primaries amenable to complete surgical resection combined with radiotherapy, and targeted agents remain investigational.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the tumour for ordinary poorly differentiated squamous carcinoma and not requesting NUT testing","Overlooking the diagnosis in older adults or non-midline sites where it is less expected","Interpreting focal keratinisation as evidence of a more differentiated, less aggressive carcinoma","Failing to preserve tissue for molecular confirmation when the initial impression is a small round cell tumour","Attributing weak or patchy NUT staining to a negative result without confirmatory molecular studies"],"management":["Aggressive multimodality treatment with complete surgical resection where feasible, particularly for non-thoracic primaries","Systemic chemotherapy and radiotherapy for local control and metastatic disease","Enrolment in clinical trials of targeted agents exploiting the fusion biology, notably BET/bromodomain and histone-deacetylase inhibitors","Early integration of palliative and supportive care given the highly aggressive course and short survival","Management coordinated by a specialist multidisciplinary oncology team with expert pathology review confirming NUT immunostaining and NUTM1 rearrangement","Staging and reassessment for distant metastases, which are frequently present at diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Thoracic Tumours","WHO Classification of Tumours: Head and Neck Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"nutcracker_syndrome":{"name":"Nutcracker syndrome","system":"Renal / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["renal vein compression syndrome","left renal vein entrapment","renal nutcracker syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by compression of the left renal vein, most often the anterior (classic) type where it is squeezed between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta, or the posterior type where a retroaortic left renal vein is compressed between the aorta and vertebral column","Nutcracker phenomenon describes the anatomical compression on imaging; nutcracker syndrome denotes compression that produces symptoms","Typical features are left-sided haematuria (micro- or macroscopic), left flank or abdominal pain, and orthostatic proteinuria","In men it may cause a left varicocele and in women pelvic congestion symptoms owing to raised gonadal vein pressure","Tends to occur in slender individuals with little retroperitoneal fat, and symptoms may worsen with upright posture or exertion","Diagnosis integrates Doppler ultrasound, cross-sectional venous imaging, and, when needed, venography with pressure gradient measurement","Management ranges from conservative observation (many young patients improve with growth and weight gain) to left renal vein transposition, stenting, or other surgical options for severe disease"],"pathophysiology":"Nutcracker syndrome results from mechanical compression of the left renal vein along its course to the inferior vena cava. In the anterior variant the vein is entrapped in the narrowed angle between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta; in the posterior variant a retroaortic vein is compressed between the aorta and the spine. Compression raises pressure within the left renal vein and its tributaries, causing venous congestion, development of collaterals, and rupture of thin-walled veins into the collecting system, which produces haematuria. Raised pressure transmitted to the gonadal vein underlies varicocele in men and pelvic congestion in women.","additionalDifferentials":["Glomerular causes of haematuria (e.g. IgA nephropathy, thin basement membrane disease)","Urolithiasis causing loin pain and haematuria","Urothelial or renal malignancy","Loin pain-haematuria syndrome","Pelvic congestion syndrome from other cause","Varicocele of primary venous incompetence"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Doppler ultrasound of the left renal vein to assess flow velocities and compression","CT or MR venography to demonstrate the compressed segment and anatomy","Retrograde venography with renocaval pressure gradient measurement in equivocal cases","Urinalysis to confirm haematuria and quantify proteinuria (including orthostatic testing)","Cystoscopy to lateralise bleeding to the left ureteric orifice when macroscopic haematuria is present","Exclusion of glomerular disease with appropriate renal workup"],"redFlags":["Severe or recurrent macroscopic haematuria causing anaemia or clot retention","Progressive proteinuria or evidence of renal impairment","Debilitating flank or pelvic pain unresponsive to conservative measures","Features suggesting an alternative serious cause such as malignancy"],"prognosis":"Many younger patients improve spontaneously as retroperitoneal fat increases with growth, and conservative management is often sufficient; those with severe, persistent symptoms generally respond well to renal vein transposition or endovascular stenting, though stent-related complications can occur.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing the syndrome from imaging compression alone without correlating symptoms (over-diagnosis of an incidental phenomenon)","Missing a coexisting glomerular cause of haematuria","Placing an endovascular stent prematurely in a young patient likely to improve conservatively","Overlooking the posterior (retroaortic) variant on imaging"],"management":["Conservative observation in younger, mildly symptomatic patients, as many improve spontaneously with growth and increasing retroperitoneal fat","Weight-gain measures where low body mass contributes, with analgesia for flank pain","Renal vein transposition or other open surgical repair for severe, persistent haematuria or pain refractory to conservative care","Endovascular stenting of the left renal vein as an alternative, with vigilance for stent migration and thrombosis","Monitoring of haematuria, proteinuria and anaemia, with treatment of an associated left varicocele or pelvic congestion","Referral to urology or vascular surgery for intervention decisions after confirmatory venographic pressure-gradient measurement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy"],"crossLinks":[]},"nutritional_optic_neuropathy":{"name":"nutritional optic neuropathy","system":"Ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["deficiency optic neuropathy","nutritional amblyopia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with bilateral, symmetric, painless and slowly progressive visual loss with a central or centrocaecal scotoma and prominent dyschromatopsia.","Key deficiencies include vitamin B12, folate, thiamine, pyridoxine, riboflavin and copper, often on a background of protein-calorie malnutrition.","Important settings are chronic alcohol misuse, bariatric surgery, malabsorption, highly restrictive diets and famine, and combined nutritional and toxic injury is common.","Red-green colour vision is disturbed early and disproportionately to the level of acuity.","The disc is normal or mildly hyperaemic early and develops temporal pallor as the papillomacular bundle atrophies.","Pupillary light responses are relatively preserved and a relative afferent pupillary defect is absent when involvement is symmetric.","Early recognition and repletion, including empirical thiamine in at-risk patients, can reverse or arrest visual loss.","A hereditary mitochondrial optic neuropathy such as Leber hereditary optic neuropathy can mimic the picture and should be considered."],"pathophysiology":"The small-calibre fibres of the papillomacular bundle have exceptionally high metabolic demand and depend on efficient mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation. B-group vitamins act as cofactors in the enzymatic pathways generating cellular energy, so their deficiency impairs adenosine triphosphate production, disrupts axonal transport and myelin maintenance, and increases oxidative stress. These fibres are therefore selectively vulnerable, producing the characteristic central and centrocaecal field loss, with copper deficiency causing an analogous metabolic injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Toxic optic neuropathy","Leber hereditary optic neuropathy","Dominant optic atrophy","Compressive optic neuropathy or chiasmal lesion","Demyelinating optic neuritis","Maculopathy such as occult macular dystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum vitamin B12 with folate and red-cell folate","Thiamine and serum copper with caeruloplasmin","Full blood count and blood film looking for macrocytosis","Magnetic resonance imaging to exclude a compressive or chiasmal lesion","Automated perimetry and colour vision testing","Optical coherence tomography of the retinal nerve fibre layer","Mitochondrial DNA testing for Leber hereditary optic neuropathy where suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or markedly asymmetric loss, prompting reconsideration of a compressive or other cause","Accompanying neurological signs or features of Wernicke encephalopathy","Failure to improve after adequate nutritional repletion","Coexisting toxic exposure that may accelerate the injury"],"prognosis":"The outcome is variable and depends on the duration of deficiency; vision may recover substantially with early repletion. Prolonged, untreated deficiency leads to optic atrophy and permanent visual impairment.","pitfalls":["Attributing loss to alcohol alone without measuring B12 and folate","Overlooking a coexisting toxic contribution to the neuropathy","Delaying thiamine replacement in at-risk or alcohol-dependent patients","Failing to consider copper deficiency, for example after bariatric surgery or with excess zinc intake"],"management":["Prompt repletion of the causative deficiency, notably vitamin B12, folate, thiamine and other B-group vitamins, and copper","Correction of underlying protein-calorie malnutrition with dietary optimisation and nutritional support","Abstinence from alcohol and elimination of contributory toxins where combined nutritional and toxic injury is suspected","Parenteral vitamin replacement in malabsorption or post-bariatric states where oral repletion is inadequate","Serial monitoring of visual acuity, colour vision and visual fields to track recovery","Multidisciplinary input from neuro-ophthalmology, dietetics and addiction services, with treatment of the primary malabsorptive or surgical cause, since delayed repletion leads to irreversible optic atrophy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course, Neuro-Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"nutritional_rickets":{"name":"Nutritional Rickets","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rickets","vitamin D deficiency rickets","calcipenic rickets"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disorder of defective mineralisation of growing bone, most commonly due to deficiency of vitamin D and/or dietary calcium.","Risk factors include exclusive breastfeeding without vitamin D supplementation, darker skin pigmentation, limited sun exposure, and prematurity.","Skeletal signs include widening of the wrists and ankles, a rachitic rosary at the costochondral junctions, frontal bossing, delayed fontanelle closure, and bowing of the legs once weight-bearing begins.","Non-skeletal features can include delayed motor milestones, hypotonia, growth faltering, and increased susceptibility to fractures.","Severe hypocalcaemia may present with tetany, carpopedal spasm, or seizures, especially in infants.","Biochemistry classically shows low or low-normal calcium and phosphate, elevated alkaline phosphatase, raised parathyroid hormone, and low 25-hydroxyvitamin D.","Radiographs of the wrist or knee reveal metaphyseal cupping, fraying, and widening of the growth plate.","Treatment replaces vitamin D and ensures adequate dietary calcium, with biochemical and radiological healing confirming response.","Prevention through routine vitamin D supplementation in infancy is highly effective, and failure to respond should prompt evaluation for inherited or renal forms."],"pathophysiology":"Vitamin D deficiency reduces intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate, lowering their availability for bone mineralisation. The falling calcium stimulates parathyroid hormone secretion (secondary hyperparathyroidism), which mobilises calcium from bone and promotes renal phosphate wasting. The combination of low calcium-phosphate product and PTH-driven bone resorption leaves the expanding cartilage of the growth plate and newly formed osteoid unmineralised, producing the soft, deformable bones and characteristic metaphyseal changes of rickets.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypophosphataemic (vitamin D-resistant) rickets","Vitamin D-dependent rickets (types 1 and 2)","Renal osteodystrophy from chronic kidney disease","Hypophosphatasia","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Skeletal dysplasias and physiologic bowing"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium, phosphate, and alkaline phosphatase","25-hydroxyvitamin D level","Parathyroid hormone","Renal function and, where indicated, urine calcium and phosphate","Wrist or knee radiographs for metaphyseal changes","1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D in atypical or treatment-resistant cases"],"redFlags":["Hypocalcaemic seizures, tetany, or laryngospasm","Cardiomyopathy associated with severe hypocalcaemia or vitamin D deficiency","Pathological fractures","Failure to respond to appropriate vitamin D and calcium therapy","Progressive deformity or signs suggesting an inherited or renal cause"],"prognosis":"With adequate treatment, biochemical recovery occurs within weeks and radiographic healing over months; most deformities remodel in young children, though severe or late-treated cases may leave residual bowing.","pitfalls":["Attributing bowed legs solely to physiologic causes and missing biochemical rickets","Overlooking dietary calcium deficiency as a cause even when vitamin D is adequate","Failing to investigate inherited or renal rickets when there is no response to treatment","Not providing routine vitamin D supplementation to exclusively breastfed infants"],"management":["Replacement with vitamin D, as cholecalciferol or ergocalciferol, to restore bone mineralisation","Adequate dietary or supplemental calcium alongside vitamin D, as deficiency of either contributes","Urgent correction of symptomatic hypocalcaemia with calcium for tetany, carpopedal spasm or seizures","Preventive advice on sunlight exposure and diet, with routine vitamin D supplementation of at-risk breastfed infants","Monitoring of calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, parathyroid hormone and 25-hydroxyvitamin D, with radiographic confirmation of healing","Orthopaedic referral for residual limb deformity that fails to remodel","Investigation for inherited or renal causes if there is no response to adequate replacement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"o_mannosylation_disorder":{"name":"O-mannosylation disorder (dystroglycanopathy-spectrum glycosylation defect)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dystroglycanopathy","alpha-dystroglycan glycosylation disorder","O-mannosylation defect","POMT1/POMT2/POMGNT1/FKTN/FKRP/LARGE-related CDG","muscle-eye-brain / Walker-Warburg spectrum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of glycosylation disorders affecting O-mannosylation of alpha-dystroglycan, presenting as congenital muscular dystrophy with variable brain and eye involvement","The clinical spectrum spans Walker-Warburg syndrome and muscle-eye-brain disease at the severe end to milder limb-girdle muscular dystrophy phenotypes","Severe forms combine congenital muscular dystrophy with cobblestone (type II) lissencephaly, cerebellar and brainstem malformations, and ophthalmological anomalies","Serum creatine kinase is typically markedly elevated, reflecting muscle involvement, in contrast to the usual N-linked CDGs","Standard serum transferrin isoelectric focusing is usually normal because these are O-linked defects, so a normal transferrin test does not exclude them","Muscle biopsy shows dystrophic change with reduced glycosylated alpha-dystroglycan on immunostaining; many genes are involved (e.g. POMT1, POMT2, POMGNT1, FKTN, FKRP, LARGE)","Management is supportive and multidisciplinary, addressing motor, respiratory, feeding, visual and neurological needs"],"pathophysiology":"Alpha-dystroglycan requires a specialised O-mannose-linked glycan to bind extracellular matrix proteins such as laminin and anchor the sarcolemma and basement membranes. Defects in the enzymes and glycosyltransferases that build this O-mannosyl glycan disrupt the link between the cytoskeleton and matrix, causing muscle fibre injury and impaired neuronal migration. The result is a dystroglycanopathy, with congenital muscular dystrophy and, in severe cases, malformations of cortical development and the eye. Because the defect is in O-linked rather than N-linked glycosylation, conventional transferrin screening is characteristically normal.","additionalDifferentials":["Merosin-deficient and other congenital muscular dystrophies","Spinal muscular atrophy and other infantile hypotonia causes","N-linked CDGs with neuromuscular features","Other causes of cobblestone lissencephaly and structural brain malformation","Congenital myopathies and myotonic dystrophy","Metabolic myopathies with raised creatine kinase"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase measurement","Muscle biopsy with immunostaining for glycosylated alpha-dystroglycan","Molecular genetic testing of the dystroglycanopathy gene panel","Brain MRI for cortical malformation, cerebellar and brainstem anomalies","Ophthalmological assessment for structural eye involvement","Respiratory and cardiac evaluation as part of muscular dystrophy surveillance"],"redFlags":["Congenital hypotonia with contractures and respiratory compromise","Structural brain malformation with seizures or profound developmental delay","Progressive loss of motor milestones or ambulation","Severe visual impairment from ocular malformation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis ranges widely: Walker-Warburg and muscle-eye-brain phenotypes carry high early mortality and severe disability, whereas milder limb-girdle dystroglycanopathies may permit prolonged ambulation and survival into adulthood. Respiratory and cardiac complications influence long-term outcome.","pitfalls":["Excluding a glycosylation disorder because transferrin testing is normal in these O-linked defects","Not measuring creatine kinase in a hypotonic infant with suspected CDG","Overlooking cardiac and respiratory surveillance in the muscular dystrophy phenotype","Assuming a single gene when the dystroglycanopathies are genetically heterogeneous"],"management":["Supportive multidisciplinary neuromuscular care, as there is no disease-specific or curative therapy","Physiotherapy, orthoses and mobility aids, with orthopaedic management of contractures and scoliosis","Respiratory surveillance with airway clearance and non-invasive ventilation for respiratory muscle weakness","Cardiac surveillance with treatment of any cardiomyopathy as part of muscular dystrophy monitoring","Anticonvulsants and neurodevelopmental support where there is cortical malformation, with ophthalmological care for structural eye involvement","Nutritional and feeding support, including gastrostomy for bulbar involvement in severe phenotypes","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive dystroglycanopathy, with dystroglycanopathy gene-panel testing and family screening","Early palliative care discussion in severe Walker-Warburg and muscle-eye-brain phenotypes carrying high early mortality"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID, disorders of O-glycosylation","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics, congenital muscular dystrophies"],"crossLinks":[]},"obesity":{"name":"Obesity","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["overweight"],"chapter":413,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3181","clinicalPearls":["Obesity is defined as a state of excess adipose tissue mass that adversely affects health; because direct measurement of fat mass is impractical in routine clinical practice, the body mass index (BMI = weight/height² in kg/m²) is used as a proxy (Harrison 22e p.3181).","WHO BMI classes: underweight <18.5; normal 18.5–24.9; overweight 25–29.9; obesity ≥30 kg/m². AACE/ACE management staging further subdivides obesity into class I (30.0–34.9), class II (35.0–39.9), and class III / extreme obesity (≥40) with correspondingly higher disease risk (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187).","BMI-based cutoffs were derived largely from white Western populations; adverse outcomes occur at lower BMI in other ethnic groups, so lower thresholds and ethnic-specific waist-circumference cutpoints (e.g., South Asians/Chinese, Japanese) are recommended (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187).","Fat distribution matters independent of overall BMI: a waist-to-hip ratio >0.9 in women and >1.0 in men, and excess abdominal (visceral) adiposity, are independently associated with type 2 diabetes, dyslipidemia, metabolic syndrome, and cardiovascular disease (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187).","Energy balance is under homeostatic control by the hypothalamus, which integrates the adipose-derived hormone leptin (rises with fat stores, falls fast with caloric restriction) with gut signals — GLP-1, CCK, GIP, PYY, oxyntomodulin, ghrelin — plus pancreatic insulin and amylin (Harrison 22e p.3181–3182).","Insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia is one of the earliest and most prominent biochemical abnormalities of obesity, is worse with central adiposity, and underlies/precedes many adverse cardiometabolic consequences (Harrison 22e p.3186).","Two leading (not mutually exclusive) hypotheses link obesity to insulin resistance: adipose-tissue inflammation (macrophage accumulation around dying adipocytes), and ectopic lipid deposition once safe adipose storage capacity is exceeded — with stronger human data supporting the ectopic-fat / limited-storage mechanism (Harrison 22e p.3186).","Obesity care begins with sensitive, non-stigmatizing language and an obesity-focused history covering diet, physical activity, sleep, stress, contributing factors, complications, and the patient's readiness/motivation to change (Harrison 22e p.3187–3188)."],"pathophysiology":"Adipose tissue evolved to store excess calories as triglyceride in unilocular lipid droplets and release free fatty acids and glycerol when needed. In an obesogenic environment (abundant energy-dense palatable food, larger portions, reduced physical activity), chronic energy intake exceeding expenditure — even by as little as ~7 kcal/day sustained over years — produces a cumulative excess of adipose triglyceride. Genetic factors strongly determine individual susceptibility (twin studies show adiposity tracks with biological, not adoptive, family), acting largely through the brain's control of food intake. Body weight is homeostatically defended by the hypothalamus, which integrates the adipose hormone leptin (whose fall below an individual's habitual level is a powerful drive to eat) with gut hormones (GLP-1, CCK, GIP, PYY, oxyntomodulin, ghrelin) and pancreatic insulin/amylin. Within the arcuate nucleus, leptin stimulates POMC neurons (whose products α-/β-MSH act via MC4R in the paraventricular nucleus to reduce intake and raise energy expenditure) and inhibits orexigenic AGRP/NPY/GABA neurons. Leptin defends strongly against fat loss but responds weakly to fat excess (\"leptin resistance\"), explaining why rising leptin does not prevent obesity. As obesity develops, the earliest abnormality is insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia, worse with central fat; this arises from adipose-tissue inflammation and/or ectopic lipid deposition in liver and muscle once safe adipose storage capacity is exceeded (the human data favouring the ectopic-fat mechanism, as illustrated by lipodystrophy). Insulin resistance/hyperinsulinemia then predisposes to the metabolic, endocrine, and cardiovascular complications of obesity (Harrison 22e p.3181–3186).","additionalDifferentials":["Hypothyroidism — may cause weight gain but is rarely the sole cause of severe obesity; check TSH (and free thyroxine for rare secondary hypothyroidism) in any patient presenting with obesity (Harrison 22e p.3185, 3188)","Cushing's syndrome / endogenous hypercortisolism — suggested by spontaneous bruising, livid striae, myopathy and marked centripetal fat distribution; confirmed via 24-h urinary free cortisol or dexamethasone suppression (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Hypothalamic obesity — from tumors (e.g., craniopharyngioma), inflammatory masses, or severe head injury disrupting hypothalamic energy-balance centres, often with anterior/posterior pituitary dysfunction and short history of hyperphagia (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Insulinoma — weight gain driven by frequent eating to avoid hypoglycemia (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Polycystic ovarian syndrome — secondary cause/association meriting evaluation (Harrison 22e p.3188)","Monogenic / syndromic obesity — leptin or leptin-receptor deficiency, POMC, PCSK1, MC4R, SIM1, BDNF/TrkB, SH2B1 mutations; syndromes such as Prader-Willi, Bardet-Biedl, Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy/pseudohypoparathyroidism, Cohen's, Carpenter's, Alström's, Tubby (Harrison 22e p.3183–3185)","Drug-induced weight gain — insulin, sulfonylureas, thiazolidinediones, glucocorticoids, antipsychotics (clozapine, olanzapine, risperidone), lithium, antidepressants (tricyclics, MAOIs, paroxetine, mirtazapine), antiepileptics (valproate, gabapentin, carbamazepine) (Harrison 22e p.3188)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Very muscular body habitus — BMI may overestimate body fat (Harrison 22e p.3181)","Peripheral edema from NSAIDs or calcium channel blockers — increases weight without increasing body fat (Harrison 22e p.3188)"],"additionalInvestigations":["BMI calculation plus waist circumference (surrogate for visceral fat; measure horizontally above the iliac crest, particularly when BMI ≤35) and/or waist-to-hip ratio (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187)","Fasting lipid profile (total, LDL, HDL cholesterol and triglycerides), chemistry panel, glycated haemoglobin, and blood pressure in all patients (Harrison 22e p.3187)","TSH ± free thyroxine to evaluate for hypothyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3185, 3188)","24-h urinary free cortisol or dexamethasone suppression test when Cushing's syndrome is suspected (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Genetic testing in severe early-onset obesity (up to ~20% of children with severe obesity have chromosomal abnormalities or highly penetrant mutations) (Harrison 22e p.3183)"],"redFlags":["Short history of hyperphagia and rapid weight gain with signs of anterior/posterior pituitary dysfunction — suggests hypothalamic damage from tumor, inflammation, or head injury (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Spontaneous bruising, livid striae, proximal myopathy, marked centripetal fat — suggests Cushing's syndrome rather than common obesity (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Severe early-onset obesity in childhood with intense hyperphagia/food-seeking — prompts screening for monogenic/syndromic causes (Harrison 22e p.3183–3185)","Severe hypertriglyceridemia secondary to insulin resistance — risk of pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.3186)"],"prognosis":"Obesity is a serious, highly heterogeneous chronic disease that increases morbidity and mortality across many organ systems. The number and severity of complications generally rise with increasing BMI, and excess abdominal/visceral fat confers added cardiometabolic risk. As BMI rises by 5 kg/m², cancer mortality increases by ~10%; in some countries obesity has overtaken smoking as the leading risk factor for cancer. People with obesity have increased morbidity and mortality from atherothrombotic vascular disease (coronary artery disease, stroke) even without diabetes, and are at increased risk of heart failure (especially diastolic dysfunction) and atrial fibrillation. Modest, sustained weight loss (an initial goal of ~8–10% over 6 months) improves obesity-related complications and quality of life; some complications, including type 2 diabetes (e.g., the DiRECT trial showed remission in nearly half of participants with intensive low-energy dietary intervention), can substantially improve or remit with weight loss (Harrison 22e p.3186–3187, 3190).","pitfalls":["Treating BMI alone: BMI only reflects body size, not body composition, fat distribution, health, or quality of life — not all patients with a high BMI need treatment, and BMI overestimates fat in very muscular individuals (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187, 3190).","Applying white-Western-derived BMI cutoffs uniformly: adverse outcomes occur at lower BMI in other ethnic groups, and ethnic-specific (e.g., Asia-Pacific) thresholds should be used (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187).","Overlooking that severe hypothyroidism is rarely the sole cause of severe obesity, yet thyroid function should still always be checked (Harrison 22e p.3185).","Mistaking the lower GH response to provocative testing in common obesity for a structural hypothalamic-pituitary lesion (Harrison 22e p.3186).","Assuming a specific macronutrient ratio is key: weight loss depends primarily on total caloric reduction and adherence, not on the proportion of carbohydrate, fat, or protein (Harrison 22e p.3190).","Using stigmatizing language ('obesity', 'morbid obesity', 'fatness') rather than neutral terms ('weight', 'excess weight', 'unhealthy weight'), which leaves patients feeling judged (Harrison 22e p.3188).","Initiating lifestyle change before assessing readiness, which leads to frustration and may hamper future efforts (Harrison 22e p.3188)."],"management":["Structured lifestyle intervention with dietary energy restriction, increased physical activity and behavioural support as the foundation of treatment","Adjunctive pharmacotherapy with agents such as GLP-1 receptor agonists, combined incretin agonists or orlistat","Bariatric (metabolic) surgery for severe obesity or obesity-related complications inadequately controlled by other means","Identification and management of comorbidities including type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia, hypertension and obstructive sleep apnoea","Screening for secondary and syndromic causes such as hypothyroidism, Cushing's syndrome and monogenic obesity in severe early-onset cases","Long-term monitoring of weight, waist circumference and cardiometabolic risk with sustained support, given the chronic relapsing nature","Referral to a specialist weight-management service for staged, intensive intervention"],"severityClassification":"BMI-based (kg/m²): underweight <18.5; healthy/normal weight 18.5–24.9; overweight 25.0–29.9; obesity class I 30.0–34.9 (high disease risk); obesity class II 35.0–39.9 (very high risk); extreme/class III obesity ≥40 (extremely high risk). Risk is refined by excess abdominal fat (ethnic-specific waist-circumference cutpoints; waist-to-hip ratio >0.9 women / >1.0 men). Disease-staging systems augment BMI: the AACE/ACE staging (stage 0 = no complications; stage 1 = mild-moderate complications; stage 2 = at least one severe complication) and the Edmonton Obesity Staging System (EOSS, grades 0–4 across medical, functional, and psychological domains, independent of BMI) (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187–3188).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3181","Harrison 22e p.3187"],"crossLinks":["diabetes_mellitus","sleep_apnea"]},"obesity_hypoventilation":{"name":"Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","system":"Nutrition / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pickwickian syndrome","obesity-related hypoventilation","OHS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO nutrition guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by obesity with chronic daytime hypercapnia and hypoxaemia that is not explained by another cause of hypoventilation","Most patients also have obstructive sleep apnoea, with loud snoring, witnessed apnoeas, and daytime sleepiness","It should be suspected in an obese patient with an unexplained raised serum bicarbonate reflecting chronic carbon dioxide retention","Untreated disease progresses to pulmonary hypertension, right heart failure (cor pulmonale), and peripheral oedema","Patients are vulnerable to acute-on-chronic respiratory failure, which can be precipitated by infection, sedatives, or high-flow uncontrolled oxygen","Positive airway pressure therapy is the cornerstone of management, alongside sustained weight reduction","It is increasingly relevant as obesity prevalence rises, including in urban India"],"pathophysiology":"Severe obesity increases the mechanical load on the respiratory system through chest wall and abdominal fat, reducing lung volumes and respiratory compliance and increasing the work of breathing. Combined with blunted central ventilatory responsiveness to carbon dioxide and, in most patients, coexisting upper airway obstruction during sleep, this leads to chronic alveolar hypoventilation. Sustained carbon dioxide retention drives renal bicarbonate retention, which further blunts the ventilatory drive. Chronic hypoxaemia causes pulmonary vasoconstriction and, over time, pulmonary hypertension and right ventricular failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and other intrinsic lung disease","Obstructive sleep apnoea without daytime hypercapnia","Neuromuscular disease causing respiratory muscle weakness","Chest wall disorders such as severe kyphoscoliosis","Central hypoventilation from other causes","Hypothyroidism contributing to hypoventilation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas demonstrating daytime hypercapnia and hypoxaemia","Serum bicarbonate as a screening clue to chronic carbon dioxide retention","Sleep study to assess for obstructive sleep apnoea and nocturnal hypoventilation","Pulmonary function tests to exclude intrinsic lung disease","Assessment for pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain (ECG and echocardiography)"],"redFlags":["Acute-on-chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure with reduced consciousness","Signs of cor pulmonale and right heart failure","Deterioration after uncontrolled high-flow oxygen or sedative drugs","Severe hypoxaemia or respiratory acidosis requiring urgent ventilatory support"],"prognosis":"Positive airway pressure therapy and weight reduction improve gas exchange, symptoms, and survival; untreated disease carries high morbidity and mortality from respiratory failure and cor pulmonale.","pitfalls":["Attributing hypercapnia to obstructive sleep apnoea alone and missing the hypoventilation syndrome","Giving uncontrolled high-flow oxygen and worsening carbon dioxide retention","Using sedatives that further depress ventilation","Overlooking a raised bicarbonate as an early clue to chronic hypercapnia"],"management":["Positive airway pressure therapy as the cornerstone: CPAP for coexisting obstructive sleep apnoea, or bilevel ventilation for persistent hypercapnia","Sustained weight reduction through lifestyle change, pharmacotherapy or bariatric surgery to reduce respiratory load","Cautious controlled oxygen therapy, avoiding uncontrolled high-flow oxygen that may worsen carbon dioxide retention","Treatment of pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale, including diuretics for right heart failure and peripheral oedema","Avoidance of sedatives and management of precipitants such as infection during acute-on-chronic respiratory failure","Non-invasive ventilation, escalating to invasive ventilatory support, for acute decompensated hypercapnic respiratory failure","Multidisciplinary follow-up with monitoring of daytime gas exchange and adherence to positive airway pressure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO nutrition guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"obsessive_compulsive_disorder":{"name":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OCD","obsessive compulsive disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by obsessions (intrusive, unwanted, distressing thoughts, images, or urges) and/or compulsions (repetitive behaviours or mental acts performed to reduce anxiety or prevent a feared outcome)","Symptoms are time-consuming or cause marked distress and functional impairment; patients often recognise them as excessive, though insight varies","Common themes include contamination with washing, doubt with checking, symmetry with ordering, and taboo intrusive thoughts","First-line treatment is cognitive behavioural therapy with exposure and response prevention, which is highly effective","Pharmacotherapy principles favour SSRIs, often needing higher doses and longer trials than in depression before judging response","Clomipramine is an effective alternative, and augmentation strategies are considered in resistant cases","Distinguish from obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, which involves ego-syntonic perfectionism and rigidity rather than distressing intrusive symptoms","Assess for comorbid depression, tic disorders, and hoarding, and ask about suicidal ideation"],"pathophysiology":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder is associated with dysfunction of cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical circuits, particularly hyperactivity involving the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate, and basal ganglia, together with serotonergic and glutamatergic dysregulation. Genetic factors contribute, and in some children symptom onset has been linked to post-streptococcal autoimmune processes.","additionalDifferentials":["Generalized anxiety disorder","Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder","Body dysmorphic disorder","Hoarding disorder","Tic disorders and Tourette syndrome","Illness anxiety disorder","Psychotic disorder when insight is absent"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Standardised OCD severity rating scales to quantify symptoms and monitor treatment","Screening for comorbid depression, anxiety, and tic disorders","Assessment of insight and degree of functional impairment","Consideration of post-streptococcal aetiology in abrupt paediatric onset"],"redFlags":["Suicidal ideation, particularly with comorbid depression","Severe functional incapacity from time-consuming rituals","Complete loss of insight suggesting a psychotic process","Self-harm from compulsions such as excessive washing causing skin breakdown"],"prognosis":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder is typically chronic with waxing and waning severity, but many patients achieve substantial improvement with exposure and response prevention and adequate pharmacotherapy. Early onset, poor insight, and comorbidity are associated with a more persistent course.","pitfalls":["Confusing OCD with obsessive-compulsive personality disorder","Judging SSRI non-response before an adequately high dose and prolonged trial","Providing reassurance that inadvertently reinforces compulsive checking","Overlooking comorbid depression and suicide risk","Failing to offer or refer for exposure and response prevention, the most effective psychotherapy"],"management":["Cognitive behavioural therapy with exposure and response prevention as the first-line, highly effective treatment","Pharmacotherapy with SSRIs, typically at higher doses and over longer trials than in depression before judging response","Clomipramine as an effective alternative for those intolerant of or unresponsive to SSRIs","Augmentation with antipsychotics or other strategies in treatment-resistant cases","Identification and treatment of comorbid depression, anxiety and tic disorders","Consideration of a post-streptococcal (PANDAS) aetiology in abrupt-onset paediatric cases","Referral to specialist services, with neuromodulation or neurosurgery considered in severe refractory disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"obstetric_cholestasis":{"name":"Obstetric Cholestasis","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy","ICP","cholestasis of pregnancy","pruritus gravidarum with cholestasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Obstetric cholestasis is a pregnancy-specific liver disorder characterised by pruritus and raised bile acids, typically presenting in the second half of pregnancy","The hallmark symptom is generalised itching, often severe and worse at night, classically involving the palms and soles, in the absence of a primary skin rash","Diagnosis rests on elevated serum bile acids with or without raised transaminases once other causes of itch and abnormal liver function are excluded","The main concern is fetal rather than maternal, as high bile acid concentrations are associated with an increased risk of stillbirth, preterm birth, and meconium-stained liquor","The risk of stillbirth rises with the degree of bile acid elevation, so severity is graded on peak bile acid levels and guides timing of delivery","Ursodeoxycholic acid is commonly used to relieve maternal itching, though its effect on fetal outcome is limited, and vitamin K may be considered if there is prolonged clotting from fat malabsorption","Symptoms and biochemistry resolve after delivery, but the condition recurs in a high proportion of future pregnancies and with oestrogen-containing contraception","Persistent abnormal liver function after delivery should prompt investigation for underlying hepatobiliary disease"],"pathophysiology":"Obstetric cholestasis reflects impaired hepatocellular secretion and enterohepatic circulation of bile acids in genetically susceptible women, in whom the high oestrogen and progesterone levels of pregnancy reduce the function of bile acid transport proteins. This leads to accumulation of bile acids in the maternal circulation, causing pruritus, and their transfer across the placenta is thought to underlie the fetal risks, with high concentrations having arrhythmogenic and vasoconstrictive effects that may contribute to sudden fetal death. Cholestasis can also impair absorption of fat-soluble vitamins including vitamin K.","additionalDifferentials":["Physiological pruritus of pregnancy without cholestasis","Pregnancy-specific skin conditions such as polymorphic eruption or pemphigoid gestationis","Pre-eclampsia or HELLP syndrome with liver involvement","Acute fatty liver of pregnancy","Viral hepatitis or other intrinsic liver disease","Gallstone disease or biliary obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum bile acid measurement, ideally with serial monitoring of peak levels","Liver function tests including transaminases and bilirubin","Coagulation studies where cholestasis is prolonged or severe","Viral hepatitis screen and liver autoantibodies to exclude other liver disease","Liver ultrasound to exclude biliary obstruction","Fetal surveillance, recognising that routine monitoring poorly predicts sudden stillbirth"],"redFlags":["Markedly elevated bile acids indicating higher stillbirth risk and need for planned delivery","Coexisting features of pre-eclampsia or HELLP suggesting an alternative or additional diagnosis","Jaundice, right upper quadrant pain, or systemic upset suggesting significant hepatic pathology","Reduced fetal movements or other signs of fetal compromise","Prolonged clotting from vitamin K deficiency increasing bleeding risk"],"prognosis":"For the mother the condition is usually self-limiting and resolves promptly after birth, though it recurs frequently in later pregnancies and with oestrogen exposure. The principal concern is the fetus, and careful monitoring of bile acid levels with timely delivery reduces perinatal risk; failure of liver function to normalise postpartum should prompt further hepatological assessment.","pitfalls":["Dismissing severe pruritus in pregnancy without checking bile acids and liver function","Failing to exclude pre-eclampsia, HELLP, and acute fatty liver in a woman with abnormal liver tests","Over-reliance on cardiotocography to predict a sudden stillbirth it cannot reliably foresee","Not grading severity by bile acid level to guide timing of delivery","Overlooking persistent postpartum liver dysfunction that signals underlying disease"],"management":["Ursodeoxycholic acid to relieve maternal pruritus, recognising its limited effect on foetal outcome","Emollients and topical antipruritics for symptomatic skin relief","Vitamin K supplementation where cholestasis is prolonged or clotting is deranged from fat malabsorption","Serial monitoring of serum bile acids and liver function, grading severity on peak bile acid levels","Planned timing of delivery guided by bile acid severity to reduce the risk of stillbirth","Foetal surveillance, acknowledging that routine monitoring poorly predicts sudden foetal death","Postnatal confirmation that liver function has resolved, with counselling on recurrence and avoidance of oestrogen-containing contraception","Multidisciplinary obstetric and hepatology input for atypical or severe disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"obstructed_defecation_syndrome":{"name":"Obstructed defecation syndrome","system":"Colorectal / Pelvic floor","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ODS","outlet obstruction","obstructive defecation","evacuatory dysfunction"],"chapter":null,"source":"ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An evacuatory disorder characterised by straining, incomplete evacuation, a sense of anorectal blockage, prolonged toileting, and the need for manual manoeuvres such as vaginal or perineal splinting or digitation","Broadly divided into mechanical causes (rectocele, internal rectal intussusception, enterocele, sigmoidocele) and functional causes (dyssynergic defecation or anismus, poor propulsion, rectal hyposensitivity)","More common in women, in whom an anterior rectocele frequently traps stool","Assessment combines symptom scoring, careful rectal and vaginal examination, defecography, and anorectal physiology to define the mechanism","Initial management is conservative: fibre and fluid, laxatives, toileting and behavioural advice, and pelvic floor biofeedback, especially where dyssynergia coexists","Surgery (for example stapled transanal rectal resection, rectocele repair, or ventral rectopexy) is reserved for significant structural lesions after conservative measures fail","Frequently coexists with mucosal prolapse, solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, and pelvic organ prolapse"],"pathophysiology":"Obstructed defecation results from failure to evacuate the rectum despite the urge, due to mechanical outlet obstruction, functional pelvic floor dysfunction, or both. Mechanical contributors include an anterior rectocele that traps stool, internal rectal intussusception, enterocele, and sigmoidocele; functional contributors include paradoxical puborectalis contraction (anismus), impaired rectal propulsion, and reduced rectal sensation. Chronic straining aggravates prolapse and perineal descent, creating a self-perpetuating cycle. The disorder overlaps with mucosal prolapse syndromes and pelvic organ prolapse.","additionalDifferentials":["Slow-transit constipation","Dyssynergic defecation or anismus","Rectocele or enterocele","Internal rectal intussusception or full-thickness prolapse","Colorectal stricture or obstructing neoplasm","Rectal hyposensitivity or megarectum","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Defecography (fluoroscopic or MR) to demonstrate rectocele, intussusception, enterocele, and evacuation","Anorectal manometry to assess pressures and identify dyssynergia","Balloon expulsion test as a functional screen for evacuatory dysfunction","Colonic transit studies to distinguish slow transit from outlet obstruction","Colonoscopy or sigmoidoscopy to exclude mucosal disease and malignancy","Endoanal ultrasound where sphincter integrity is relevant"],"redFlags":["New-onset constipation with bleeding, weight loss, or anaemia suggesting malignancy","A palpable rectal or pelvic mass","Progressive symptoms with obstructive features suggesting a stricture","Faecal incontinence or neurological signs suggesting a neurogenic cause"],"prognosis":"Symptoms often improve with conservative measures and biofeedback where dyssynergia predominates; results of surgery for structural lesions are variable, and recurrence or persistent symptoms are common, so careful patient selection matters.","pitfalls":["Operating on an incidental rectocele that is not the true cause of symptoms","Overlooking coexisting dyssynergia treatable by biofeedback before surgery","Failing to distinguish outlet obstruction from slow-transit constipation","Missing an obstructing neoplasm in new-onset symptoms"],"management":["First-line conservative management with increased dietary fibre and fluid, laxatives, and toileting and behavioural advice","Pelvic floor biofeedback and retraining, particularly where dyssynergic defecation (anismus) coexists","Manual and positional evacuation strategies, including vaginal or perineal splinting","Surgery reserved for significant structural lesions after conservative measures fail, such as stapled transanal rectal resection, rectocele repair or ventral rectopexy","Exclusion of mucosal disease and malignancy before attributing symptoms to a functional or structural evacuatory disorder","Multidisciplinary pelvic floor assessment integrating colorectal surgery, gynaecology and specialist physiotherapy for careful patient selection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","Keighley & Williams' Surgery of the Anus, Rectum and Colon"],"crossLinks":[]},"obstructive_sleep_apnoea":{"name":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OSA","OSAS","obstructive sleep apnoea-hypopnoea syndrome","sleep-disordered breathing"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by repetitive collapse of the upper airway during sleep, producing apnoeas and hypopnoeas with recurrent oxygen desaturations and arousals","Typical features include loud habitual snoring, witnessed breathing pauses, choking or gasping at night, and unrefreshing sleep","Excessive daytime sleepiness, morning headache, poor concentration, and mood disturbance are common daytime consequences","Obesity, increased neck circumference, male sex, retrognathia, and enlarged tonsils are important predisposing factors","Polysomnography is the diagnostic standard, with severity graded by the apnoea-hypopnoea index; home sleep testing can be used in selected patients","Continuous positive airway pressure is the mainstay of therapy for moderate-to-severe disease, splinting the airway open during sleep","Weight reduction, avoidance of alcohol and sedatives, positional therapy, and treatment of nasal obstruction are useful adjuncts","Untreated OSA is linked to hypertension, arrhythmias, ischaemic heart disease, stroke, insulin resistance, and increased motor-vehicle accident risk"],"pathophysiology":"During sleep, reduced tone of the pharyngeal dilator muscles allows the anatomically narrowed or collapsible upper airway to close, producing partial (hypopnoea) or complete (apnoea) obstruction despite continued respiratory effort. The resulting hypoxaemia, hypercapnia, and rising respiratory effort trigger brief cortical arousals that restore airway patency but fragment sleep. Recurrent cycles of desaturation-reoxygenation and arousal drive sympathetic activation, surges in blood pressure, oxidative stress, and systemic inflammation, which over time contribute to cardiovascular and metabolic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Central sleep apnoea","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Narcolepsy and other primary hypersomnias","Insufficient sleep or poor sleep hygiene","Hypothyroidism-related somnolence","Depression-related fatigue"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["In-laboratory polysomnography to confirm diagnosis and grade severity","Home sleep apnoea testing in appropriately selected patients without significant comorbidity","Overnight or nocturnal oximetry as a screening adjunct","Validated questionnaires and sleepiness scales to assess pretest probability and symptom burden","Assessment for associated conditions including hypertension, thyroid dysfunction, and metabolic disease"],"redFlags":["Severe daytime sleepiness in occupational drivers or machine operators posing safety risk","Signs of daytime hypoventilation or right heart failure suggesting overlap with hypoventilation syndromes","Resistant hypertension or nocturnal arrhythmias","Rapidly progressive symptoms or marked nocturnal desaturation"],"prognosis":"With consistent adherence to positive airway pressure and weight management, symptoms and quality of life usually improve markedly and cardiovascular risk is reduced; poorly controlled or untreated disease carries substantial long-term cardiovascular, metabolic, and accident-related morbidity.","pitfalls":["Attributing daytime sleepiness solely to lifestyle and missing significant OSA","Overlooking OSA as a driver of resistant hypertension or atrial fibrillation","Assuming all patients are obese; OSA can occur with normal body habitus due to craniofacial anatomy","Failing to address poor CPAP adherence, which undermines treatment benefit"],"management":["Continuous positive airway pressure as the mainstay for moderate-to-severe disease, splinting the upper airway open during sleep","Weight reduction and lifestyle measures, including avoidance of alcohol and sedatives before sleep","Mandibular advancement devices for mild-to-moderate disease or CPAP intolerance","Positional therapy and treatment of nasal obstruction as adjuncts","Upper airway or bariatric surgery in selected patients, including adenotonsillectomy where enlarged tonsils are causative","Management of associated conditions such as hypertension, metabolic disease and thyroid dysfunction","Advice on driving safety, with monitoring of therapy adherence and residual daytime symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"occipital_horn_syndrome":{"name":"Occipital horn syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OHS","X-linked cutis laxa","cutis laxa X-linked type","Ehlers-Danlos syndrome type IX (historical)","ATP7A"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Represents the milder allelic counterpart of Menkes disease, both caused by pathogenic variants in the copper-transporting ATPase gene ATP7A","The eponymous feature is symmetrical wedge-shaped exostoses arising from the occipital bone at the attachment of the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid, often palpable and visible on skull radiographs","Connective-tissue laxity dominates the phenotype: lax redundant skin (cutis laxa), joint hypermobility, hernias, and bladder diverticula prone to rupture and recurrent infection","Reflects deficient activity of copper-dependent enzymes, particularly lysyl oxidase, which impairs collagen and elastin cross-linking","Serum copper and caeruloplasmin are typically low, mirroring the biochemistry of Menkes disease but with better survival","Cognition is usually only mildly affected, in contrast to the severe neurodegeneration of classic Menkes disease","Autonomic dysfunction (chronic diarrhoea, orthostatic hypotension) and vascular tortuosity may occur","Management is supportive; parenteral copper supplementation has limited proven benefit once connective-tissue disease is established"],"pathophysiology":"Occipital horn syndrome arises from partial-loss-of-function variants in ATP7A, an X-linked gene encoding a copper-transporting P-type ATPase that delivers copper to the secretory pathway and exports it from enterocytes. Because some residual transporter activity is preserved, the phenotype is milder than the near-complete loss seen in classic Menkes disease. Impaired copper delivery limits the activity of copper-dependent cuproenzymes; reduced lysyl oxidase function in particular disrupts cross-linking of collagen and elastin, producing the lax skin, joint laxity, bladder diverticula, vascular tortuosity, and the characteristic occipital exostoses. Deficiency of other cuproenzymes such as dopamine beta-hydroxylase contributes to autonomic features, while relative sparing of central copper delivery explains the comparatively preserved neurodevelopment.","additionalDifferentials":["Classic Menkes disease (more severe ATP7A phenotype)","Autosomal recessive and dominant cutis laxa syndromes","Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (vascular and classical types)","Marfan syndrome and other heritable connective-tissue disorders","Loeys-Dietz syndrome with arterial tortuosity","Arterial tortuosity syndrome (SLC2A10)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum copper and caeruloplasmin (characteristically low)","Skull and skeletal radiographs demonstrating occipital horns and other dysplastic changes","ATP7A molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Imaging of the urinary tract for bladder diverticula and hydronephrosis","Vascular imaging where arterial tortuosity or aneurysm is suspected","Autonomic function assessment including orthostatic blood pressure"],"redFlags":["Bladder diverticulum rupture presenting as acute abdomen or urosepsis","Symptomatic arterial aneurysm or dissection from vascular fragility","Recurrent complicated urinary tract infection with obstruction","Severe orthostatic hypotension causing syncope and injury"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is substantially better than in classic Menkes disease, with survival into adulthood; morbidity stems from connective-tissue complications such as bladder diverticula, hernias, autonomic dysfunction, and occasional vascular events.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the connective-tissue phenotype as Ehlers-Danlos syndrome and missing the underlying copper-transport defect","Overlooking the diagnostic occipital exostoses on imaging","Assuming copper supplementation will reverse established connective-tissue disease","Failing to monitor the urinary tract for diverticula and obstructive complications"],"management":["No proven disease-modifying therapy; parenteral copper-histidine substitution is of limited value because it does not correct the underlying lysyl-oxidase-dependent connective-tissue defect, so care is predominantly supportive","Physiotherapy, joint protection and orthopaedic input for hypermobility, with surgical repair of hernias and symptomatic occipital exostoses","Urological surveillance for bladder diverticula and hydronephrosis, prompt antimicrobial treatment of recurrent urinary infections, and surgical repair of diverticula at risk of rupture","Management of dysautonomia and orthostatic hypotension with lifestyle measures and a noradrenaline-precursor agent where dopamine beta-hydroxylase deficiency causes symptomatic hypotension","Vascular surveillance for arterial tortuosity and aneurysm, with referral for intervention on significant lesions","Multidisciplinary follow-up coordinated by metabolic medicine with urology, orthopaedic and vascular input","Genetic counselling and carrier testing for the X-linked ATP7A variant, with screening of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"occipital_neuralgia":{"name":"Occipital neuralgia","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Arnold neuralgia","greater occipital neuralgia","C2 neuralgia","occipital neuritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with paroxysmal shooting or stabbing pain radiating from the suboccipital region up over the scalp in the distribution of the greater, lesser, or third occipital nerves","There is usually a background aching or dysaesthesia between paroxysms, with tenderness and a trigger point over the affected nerve at the occiput","Pressure over the greater occipital nerve where it emerges near the superior nuchal line often reproduces the pain","The affected scalp may show altered sensation (hyperaesthesia or hypoaesthesia), and allodynia to combing hair is common","A diagnostic and therapeutic occipital nerve block that abolishes the pain confirms the diagnosis","It must be distinguished from referred pain from the upper cervical spine and from primary headaches such as migraine and cervicogenic headache","Treatment ranges from local measures, neuropathic agents (including carbamazepine and amitriptyline), and occipital nerve blocks to neurostimulation or surgical decompression in refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Occipital neuralgia is caused by irritation, entrapment, or injury of the greater, lesser, or third occipital nerves, which arise from the upper cervical (chiefly C2) roots. Compression as the nerve passes through the suboccipital muscles and fascia, degenerative change or instability of the upper cervical spine, or local trauma provoke ectopic discharges and pain referred throughout the nerve's cutaneous territory over the posterior scalp. Convergence of upper cervical and trigeminal afferents in the trigeminocervical complex explains why pain may also be felt in frontal and periorbital regions.","additionalDifferentials":["Cervicogenic headache","Migraine","Tension-type headache","Upper cervical spine degenerative or facet disease","Cluster headache","Giant cell arteritis (in older patients)","Posterior fossa or craniocervical junction lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Palpation for an occipital trigger point reproducing the pain","Diagnostic occipital nerve block with local anaesthetic","MRI or CT of the cervical spine and craniocervical junction when a structural cause is suspected","Inflammatory markers to exclude giant cell arteritis in older patients","Neurological examination of the upper limbs and cranial nerves","Imaging of the posterior fossa if central pathology is suspected"],"redFlags":["New occipital pain in an older patient with jaw claudication or visual symptoms suggesting giant cell arteritis","Associated neurological deficit or myelopathic signs pointing to a craniocervical lesion","Systemic features such as fever or weight loss","Progressive, unremitting pain unresponsive to nerve block"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and frequently responds to conservative measures and occipital nerve blocks, though relapses are common. Refractory cases can be helped by neurostimulation or surgical decompression, and any underlying cervical pathology should be addressed.","pitfalls":["Confusing occipital neuralgia with migraine or cervicogenic headache","Missing giant cell arteritis in an older patient with occipital scalp pain","Overlooking upper cervical spine pathology as the underlying cause","Failing to confirm the diagnosis with a nerve block before invasive treatment","Attributing frontal or periorbital pain to another disorder when it reflects trigeminocervical convergence"],"management":["First-line conservative care with rest, local heat, massage, physiotherapy and correction of cervical posture","Neuropathic pain pharmacotherapy using anticonvulsants such as gabapentinoids or carbamazepine, or a tricyclic antidepressant, with simple analgesics or muscle relaxants for the background ache","Diagnostic and therapeutic greater, lesser or third occipital nerve block with local anaesthetic and corticosteroid","Identification and treatment of any underlying upper cervical spine pathology or instability","For refractory pain, escalation to pulsed radiofrequency, occipital nerve stimulation or surgical decompression and neurolysis","Exclusion and treatment of giant cell arteritis in older patients with new occipital pain before attributing symptoms to neuralgia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"occult_macular_dystrophy":{"name":"Occult macular dystrophy","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OMD","Miyake disease","RP1L1","occult macular dystrophy of Miyake"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ryan's Retina / Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["First described by Miyake, this dystrophy causes progressive bilateral central visual loss despite an essentially normal-looking fundus, hence occult","Frequently caused by RP1L1 mutations, most often autosomal dominant, though sporadic and recessive cases occur","The key diagnostic feature is a normal full-field electroretinogram with a selectively abnormal multifocal (or focal macular) electroretinogram, localising dysfunction to the central macula","Fluorescein angiography is typically normal, which historically made the condition difficult to diagnose before multifocal electroretinography and OCT","Spectral-domain optical coherence tomography reveals disruption or blurring of the ellipsoid zone and the interdigitation zone at the fovea, often the only structural clue","Colour vision and peripheral fields are usually preserved, and the diagnosis is easily missed as functional or non-organic visual loss","Management is supportive with low-vision aids and genetic counselling; there is no established treatment to reverse the macular dysfunction"],"pathophysiology":"Occult macular dystrophy is an inherited cone dysfunction confined to the central macula, commonly caused by mutations in RP1L1, a gene expressed in photoreceptors and important for outer-segment structure and function. The disorder selectively impairs the central cone photoreceptors while sparing the peripheral retina, so the standard full-field electroretinogram, which reflects the whole retina, remains normal, whereas the focal macular and multifocal electroretinogram are abnormal. Corresponding subtle disruption of the foveal ellipsoid and interdigitation zones on optical coherence tomography reflects the localised photoreceptor dysfunction, with a normal fundus and normal angiography throughout.","additionalDifferentials":["Cone or cone-rod dystrophy (which show abnormal full-field electroretinography)","Functional or non-organic visual loss","Retrobulbar optic neuropathy","Stargardt disease and other maculopathies with visible changes","Acute macular neuroretinopathy","Central serous or other subtle maculopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Multifocal and focal macular electroretinography demonstrating central dysfunction with a normal full-field electroretinogram","Spectral-domain optical coherence tomography for ellipsoid and interdigitation zone disruption at the fovea","Fundus autofluorescence, which may be near-normal, to help exclude other maculopathies","Fluorescein angiography, expected to be normal, to exclude vascular or inflammatory causes","Colour vision and visual field testing","Molecular genetic testing for RP1L1 and related genes"],"redFlags":["Unexplained progressive central visual loss with a normal fundus that risks being dismissed as non-organic","Asymmetric or unilateral presentation prompting exclusion of an optic neuropathy","Rapid deterioration warranting prompt multifocal electroretinography and OCT","Affected relatives who may benefit from screening given frequent dominant inheritance"],"prognosis":"Central acuity declines slowly and progressively over years to a moderate low-vision level, while peripheral vision, colour vision and night vision are typically preserved.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing non-organic or functional visual loss because the fundus, angiography and full-field electroretinogram are normal","Relying on full-field electroretinography alone and omitting multifocal or focal macular testing","Overlooking subtle foveal ellipsoid zone disruption on optical coherence tomography","Confusing it with an optic neuropathy in asymmetric cases"],"management":["No disease-specific or curative treatment exists; management focuses on maximising and supporting residual central vision","Refractive correction and low-vision rehabilitation with magnifiers, high-contrast aids and optimised lighting","Serial monitoring with multifocal or focal macular electroretinography and spectral-domain optical coherence tomography to document slow progression","Referral to an inherited retinal disease service, with registration for emerging gene-directed therapy trials where relevant","Genetic counselling for the usually autosomal dominant RP1L1 disorder, with testing of at-risk relatives","Educational, occupational and psychological support, avoiding misdiagnosis as functional visual loss"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ryan's Retina","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"occupational_asthma":{"name":"Occupational asthma","system":"Occupational / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["work-related asthma","OA","sensitiser-induced asthma","irritant-induced asthma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hunter's Diseases of Occupations / WHO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Suspect a work cause when asthma symptoms improve on days away from work, during holidays, and worsen on returning to the workplace","Two broad forms exist: sensitiser-induced disease, which follows a latency period of exposure, and irritant-induced disease that can follow a single high-level exposure (reactive airways dysfunction syndrome)","Common high-molecular-weight sensitisers include flour, animal proteins, latex and enzymes; frequent low-molecular-weight agents include isocyanates, colophony solder flux and certain wood dusts","Serial peak flow monitoring recorded several times a day at and away from work is a practical and valuable tool for confirming a work relationship","Early diagnosis matters because prolonged continued exposure after symptoms begin predicts persistent asthma even after removal","Complete removal from the causative agent, rather than simply reducing exposure, gives the best chance of recovery","A confident diagnosis has major implications for the worker's livelihood, so objective confirmation should be sought before advising job change","Consider workplace surveillance and review of coworkers, since others exposed to the same agent may also be affected"],"pathophysiology":"Sensitiser-induced occupational asthma develops through immunological sensitisation to an inhaled workplace agent, often IgE-mediated for high-molecular-weight proteins, leading to airway inflammation, bronchial hyper-responsiveness and reversible airflow obstruction on re-exposure. Irritant-induced asthma arises from direct toxic injury to the airway epithelium after intense exposure to a respiratory irritant, provoking inflammation and lasting hyper-responsiveness without a prior latency period.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-occupational (constitutional) asthma coincidentally worsened by workplace conditions","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (extrinsic allergic alveolitis)","Vocal cord dysfunction","Rhinitis with post-nasal symptoms mimicking lower airway disease","Hyperventilation or anxiety-related breathlessness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial peak expiratory flow diaries covering periods at work and away from work","Spirometry with reversibility testing","Non-specific bronchial challenge testing to document hyper-responsiveness","Specific IgE or skin prick testing to suspected occupational allergens where available","Specific inhalation (bronchial provocation) challenge in specialist centres when diagnosis is uncertain","Detailed occupational history including materials, processes and temporal pattern of symptoms"],"redFlags":["Acute severe bronchospasm following a major irritant exposure or spill","Progressive symptoms despite continued exposure indicating risk of fixed airflow obstruction","Nocturnal attacks or falling peak flows suggesting poorly controlled disease","Features of anaphylaxis with certain protein allergens such as latex"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on how quickly the diagnosis is made and exposure stopped; early cessation gives a good chance of improvement, whereas prolonged exposure before removal is associated with persistent asthma and airway hyper-responsiveness that may not fully resolve.","pitfalls":["Attributing symptoms to ordinary asthma and failing to take an occupational history","Advising reduced rather than complete removal from a sensitising agent","Relying on symptoms alone without objective serial lung function data","Delaying diagnosis so that airway changes become irreversible","Overlooking coexisting occupational rhinitis, which often precedes asthma"],"management":["Complete removal from the causative sensitiser through relocation or substitution of the agent, which offers the best chance of recovery, as reducing exposure alone is insufficient","Standard stepwise asthma pharmacotherapy with inhaled corticosteroids and inhaled bronchodilators, adding systemic corticosteroids for exacerbations","For irritant-induced disease and unavoidable residual exposure, engineering controls and respiratory protective equipment","Serial peak expiratory flow and spirometry surveillance to confirm the work relationship and track lung function over time","Notification as a reportable occupational disease with advice on compensation, redeployment and workplace assessment","Referral to occupational and respiratory medicine, and screening of co-workers sharing the same exposure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Hunter's Diseases of Occupations","WHO guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"occupational_contact_urticaria":{"name":"occupational contact urticaria","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["work-related contact urticaria","occupational immediate contact reaction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Contact urticaria is an immediate (wheal-and-flare) reaction appearing within minutes of skin contact with a causative agent and usually resolving within hours, in contrast to the delayed course of allergic contact dermatitis.","It is classified into immunological (IgE-mediated, often with potential for systemic reactions) and non-immunological (direct mast-cell mediator release without prior sensitisation) forms.","Natural rubber latex is a classic immunological occupational cause, particularly in healthcare and laboratory workers wearing powdered gloves; foods are important in catering and food-handling trades.","The contact urticaria syndrome describes a spectrum from localised wheals through generalised urticaria to rhinoconjunctivitis, asthma and, at worst, anaphylaxis, so hands-first symptoms can herald systemic risk.","Common occupational triggers include latex, foodstuffs (fish, meat, flour, fruits, vegetables), animal proteins and dander, plants, ammonium persulfate in hairdressing, and some preservatives and fragrances.","Protein contact dermatitis is a related entity presenting as a chronic eczematous dermatitis with immediate flares on re-exposure, typically in food handlers.","Diagnosis relies on careful history plus open and closed application (use) tests, skin-prick testing and specific IgE where appropriate, performed with resuscitation facilities available for potent allergens.","Management is avoidance and substitution (for example switching to low-protein powder-free or non-latex gloves), barrier measures, and provision of emergency treatment for those at risk of systemic reactions."],"pathophysiology":"In immunological contact urticaria, prior sensitisation generates allergen-specific IgE bound to cutaneous mast cells; on re-exposure the allergen cross-links IgE, triggering mast-cell degranulation with release of histamine and other mediators that cause the immediate wheal-and-flare response and, when absorption is sufficient, systemic mediator release and anaphylaxis. In non-immunological contact urticaria no prior sensitisation is required: agents such as certain preservatives, fragrances and plant substances act directly on cutaneous vessels and mast cells, sometimes via non-antibody mediators, producing localised wheals that rarely generalise. In protein contact dermatitis, repeated exposure to high-molecular-weight proteins produces a combined immediate hypersensitivity and eczematous reaction. A damaged or eczematous skin barrier enhances penetration of the offending agent and amplifies reactions.","additionalDifferentials":["allergic contact dermatitis","irritant contact dermatitis","protein contact dermatitis","physical urticarias such as cholinergic or pressure urticaria","chronic spontaneous urticaria unrelated to work","angio-oedema from other causes","contact reaction to airborne irritants"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["detailed occupational history correlating symptoms with specific tasks and materials","open and closed (use) application testing on intact and lightly abraded skin","skin-prick testing to suspected allergens including latex and foods","specific serum IgE to latex, foods and relevant occupational proteins","patch testing to exclude concurrent allergic contact dermatitis","workplace assessment of materials, gloves and safety data sheets","peak-flow or spirometry if associated respiratory symptoms are reported"],"redFlags":["history of generalised urticaria, angio-oedema or anaphylaxis after contact","associated rhinoconjunctivitis, wheeze or breathlessness suggesting systemic involvement","reactions to latex in a healthcare worker with potential for cross-reactive food allergy","rapidly spreading wheals beyond the contact site","symptoms during high-exposure tasks in workers with known potent-allergen contact"],"prognosis":"Symptoms usually settle promptly once the causative agent is identified and avoided, and localised non-immunological reactions carry a good outlook. Immunological cases, especially latex allergy, may persist and carry an ongoing risk of systemic reactions if exposure continues.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis because wheals have faded by the time of assessment.","Failing to recognise localised contact urticaria as a warning of potential anaphylaxis.","Performing provocation tests to potent allergens without resuscitation facilities.","Confusing protein contact dermatitis with ordinary irritant hand eczema.","Neglecting cross-reactivity, such as latex-fruit syndrome, when advising on avoidance."],"management":["Identification and strict avoidance of the causative agent, including substitution of powdered latex gloves with latex-free alternatives and a latex-safe workplace","Oral H1-antihistamines to control urticarial wheals and pruritus","For immunological, especially latex, allergy with systemic-reaction risk, provision of an adrenaline auto-injector and a written anaphylaxis emergency plan","Skin protection with suitable gloves, barrier measures and emollients, with topical corticosteroids for associated protein contact dermatitis","Occupational health referral for workplace assessment, agent substitution and redeployment where avoidance is impossible","Reporting as an occupational disease and counselling on cross-reacting food allergens in latex-sensitised workers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Adams' Occupational Skin Disease, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"occupational_lung_disease":{"name":"Occupational and Environmental Lung Disease","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pneumoconiosis","silicosis","asbestosis"],"chapter":300,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2234","clinicalPearls":["A detailed occupational and environmental exposure history is the single most important step; include hobbies, home characteristics, secondhand smoke, and proximity to traffic/industry, plus short- and long-term exposures in the distant past (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Inorganic/organic dust exposure characteristically causes interstitial lung disease with a restrictive pattern and reduced diffusing capacity, whereas dusts/chemicals causing occupational asthma or COPD produce airway obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2234).","A drop in FEV1 measured before vs after a work shift can detect an acute occupational bronchoconstrictive response (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Radiographic pattern hints at etiology: small rounded opacities suggest silicosis or coal worker's pneumoconiosis, while small linear/irregular opacities suggest asbestosis (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Asbestos exposure causes a spectrum of disease: pleural plaques and benign effusions (marking exposure only), asbestosis (interstitial fibrosis), bronchogenic lung cancer, and pleural/peritoneal mesothelioma (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Pleural plaques indicate past asbestos exposure but, by themselves, imply only exposure and not pulmonary impairment (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Smoking and asbestos exposure interact more than additively, producing lung cancer risk greater than the sum of each factor; mesothelioma risk is NOT associated with smoking (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Silicosis raises the risk of mycobacterial (including M. tuberculosis), atypical mycobacterial, and fungal infection because silica impairs alveolar macrophage function; treatment of latent TB is given for a longer duration in these patients (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Eggshell calcification of hilar nodes (seen in up to ~20% of cases) is a characteristic radiographic clue to silicosis (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Acute (accelerated) silicosis from very heavy exposure (e.g., sandblasting in confined spaces, denim sandblasting, artificial-stone countertop fabrication) can resemble pulmonary alveolar proteinosis with a 'crazy paving' HRCT pattern and may progress despite stopping exposure (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Chronic beryllium disease mimics sarcoidosis (noncaseating granulomas); the distinguishing feature is a beryllium-specific cell-mediated (delayed hypersensitivity) immune response demonstrated by the beryllium lymphocyte proliferation test (BeLPT) (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Caplan syndrome is the combination of pneumoconiotic nodules and seropositive rheumatoid arthritis, first described in coal miners and later in silicosis (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Documented mesothelioma in a patient with occupational/environmental asbestos exposure may be compensable, as >80% of mesotheliomas are associated with asbestos exposure (Harrison 22e p.2234)."],"pathophysiology":"Disease depends on the chemical/physical properties of the inhaled agent, which determine dose and site of deposition: water-soluble gases (ammonia, SO2) are absorbed in proximal airways causing irritation/bronchoconstriction, while less-soluble gases (NO2, phosgene) reach bronchioles/alveoli causing chemical pneumonitis. Particles >10-15 micrometres deposit in the nose/throat; particles <10 micrometres reach below the larynx, with coarse-mode (~2.5-10 micrometres, crustal elements like silica) depositing high in the tracheobronchial tree, fine-mode (<2.5 micrometres, from combustion/high-temperature processes) dominating surface area for toxin delivery to lower airways, and ultrafine (<0.1 micrometres, nanoparticles) potentially entering the circulation. Asbestos fibers induce fibrosis through oxidative injury from reactive oxygen species generated by surface transition metals and by phagocytosing cells. Silica causes alveolar macrophage dysfunction (predisposing to infection) and has immunoadjuvant properties (predisposing to autoimmune connective tissue disease). Beryllium drives a CD4+ T-cell-mediated granulomatous inflammation, with susceptibility strongly linked to HLA-DP alleles bearing glutamic acid at position 69 of the beta chain. (Harrison 22e p.2234)","additionalDifferentials":["Asbestosis vs other causes of diffuse interstitial fibrosis (IPF/UIP) (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Chronic beryllium disease vs sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Silicosis (complicated/PMF) vs coal worker's pneumoconiosis and progressive massive fibrosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Acute silicosis vs pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Acute farmer's lung (hypersensitivity pneumonitis) vs influenza or community-acquired pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Occupational asthma / RADS vs nonoccupational asthma and COPD (Harrison 22e p.2234)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute farmer's lung (hypersensitivity pneumonitis from thermophilic actinomycete spores) presenting 4-8 h after exposure with fever, chills, malaise, cough, and dyspnea can mimic influenza or pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Acute silicosis with profuse miliary infiltration may radiographically resemble miliary infectious processes (Harrison 22e p.2234)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic beryllium disease closely mimics sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Aluminum and titanium dioxide exposure can produce a sarcoid-like reaction in lung tissue (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Asbestosis mimics idiopathic and other diffuse interstitial fibroses (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Hard-metal (tungsten carbide/cobalt) exposure can cause giant cell interstitial pneumonitis (Harrison 22e p.2234)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Pre- and post-shift spirometry (change in FEV1) to detect acute bronchoconstriction (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Chest radiograph graded by the ILO International Classification of Radiographs of Pneumoconioses (Harrison 22e p.2234)","CT and high-resolution CT (HRCT) - more sensitive than radiograph for lung opacities, pleural thickening, and interstitial change (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Pulmonary function testing (restrictive pattern with reduced lung volumes and diffusing capacity in fibrosing disease) (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Skin prick testing or specific IgE for immediate hypersensitivity (e.g., flour antigens in bakers) (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Specific IgG precipitating antibody titers for hypersensitivity pneumonitis agents (e.g., pigeon antigen in bird handlers) (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Beryllium lymphocyte proliferation test (BeLPT) for beryllium sensitization (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Fiberoptic bronchoscopy with transbronchial lung biopsy (e.g., to diagnose chronic beryllium disease); occasionally video-assisted thoracoscopic surgical biopsy for definitive diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)"],"redFlags":["Mesothelioma may follow relatively short asbestos exposures (<=1-2 years) with latency up to ~40 years - a thorough exposure history is essential (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Lung cancer after asbestos exposure has a minimum latency of ~15-19 years from first exposure (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Progressive massive fibrosis (PMF), occurring in complicated silicosis and CWP, causes severe lung function deficits and is associated with premature mortality; PMF is rising at an alarming rate in the Appalachian coal belt (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Active tuberculosis risk is increased in silicosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Acute silicosis can be severe and progressive even after exposure ceases (Harrison 22e p.2234)"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies by exposure and disease. Pleural plaques alone do not imply impairment. Simple silicosis and simple CWP are often present without significant functional impairment, but nodular fibrosis can progress even after exposure ceases, coalescing into complicated disease and PMF, which carries severe functional deficits and premature mortality. Acute silicosis may be severe and progressive despite stopping exposure. No specific therapy exists for asbestosis (supportive care as for any diffuse interstitial fibrosis); newly diagnosed cases usually reflect exposures from many years earlier. Asbestos exposure carries excess lung cancer risk (latency ~15-19 years) and mesothelioma risk (latency up to ~40 years). (Harrison 22e p.2234)","pitfalls":["Failing to take an occupational/environmental exposure history is the cardinal pitfall - the etiologic link to occupation is missed unless specifically sought, e.g., asking about beryllium exposure in a patient labeled as having sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)","The ILO radiographic classification, though useful for screening populations, is problematic for the individual worker: rounded-opacity disease may look extensive on film with minimal functional impairment, whereas linear/irregular opacities (asbestosis) may lead to underestimation of impairment until late (Harrison 22e p.2234)","In early chronic beryllium disease both chest imaging and pulmonary function tests may be normal (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Attributing dust-related chronic bronchitis/COPD solely to occupation without accounting for cigarette smoking, whose effect is generally additive (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Overlooking that talc and vermiculite may be contaminated with asbestos, conferring fibrosis, lung cancer, and mesothelioma risk (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Combat-related embedded metal fragments (shrapnel) in veterans may contain beryllium and serve as an unrecognized exposure source (Harrison 22e p.2234)"],"management":["Cessation of and removal from further exposure to the causative dust, fume or fibre, since fibrosing pneumoconioses can progress after exposure ends","Smoking cessation, given the multiplicative lung-cancer risk with asbestos, together with influenza and pneumococcal vaccination","Disease-directed measures where applicable, including inhaled corticosteroids and bronchodilators for occupational asthma and COPD, and antigen avoidance with corticosteroids for hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Supportive care with pulmonary rehabilitation and long-term oxygen therapy for chronic hypoxaemia","Surveillance for complications, including tuberculosis screening in silicosis and monitoring for lung cancer and mesothelioma after asbestos exposure, with serial spirometry and imaging","Reporting as a notifiable occupational disease with advice on compensation","Referral to respiratory and occupational medicine, with multidisciplinary oncology and palliative care input for malignancy or mesothelioma"],"severityClassification":"Silicosis and coal worker's pneumoconiosis are categorized as simple (small rounded opacities, usually without functional impairment) versus complicated (nodules >=1 cm) which can progress to progressive massive fibrosis (PMF, conglomerate masses >1 cm with restrictive and obstructive impairment). Acute (accelerated) silicosis is distinguished from chronic silicosis by very heavy short-duration exposure and an alveolar-proteinosis-like presentation. Occupational asthma is divided into immunologic (high- vs low-molecular-weight stimuli, with a latency period) and irritative (reactive airway dysfunction syndrome, RADS) forms. (Harrison 22e p.2234)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2234"],"crossLinks":["ild","PULMONARY_TB","lung_cancer"]},"ocular_albinism":{"name":"Ocular albinism (GPR143)","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["X-linked ocular albinism","ocular albinism type 1","OA1","Nettleship-Falls ocular albinism","GPR143"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski / Taylor & Hoyt Paediatric Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked recessive disorder in which pigment deficiency is largely confined to the eye, with near-normal skin and hair pigmentation","Affected boys present with reduced acuity, congenital nystagmus, iris transillumination and photophobia","Foveal hypoplasia and hypopigmentation of the fundus with prominent choroidal vessels are characteristic","Misrouting of optic nerve fibres at the chiasm produces abnormal decussation, detectable on visual evoked potentials and associated with reduced stereopsis and strabismus","Caused by mutations in GPR143, encoding a G-protein-coupled receptor involved in melanosome biogenesis, giving giant melanosomes (macromelanosomes)","Carrier females may show a partial mud-splattered or mosaic fundus pigmentation and patchy iris transillumination due to lyonisation","Management is supportive with refractive correction, tinted lenses for photophobia, low-vision aids and strabismus care"],"pathophysiology":"Ocular albinism type 1 arises from mutations in the X-linked GPR143 gene, whose product regulates melanosome maturation and size within the retinal pigment epithelium and other melanocytes. Loss of function leads to abnormal, enlarged melanosomes and deficient ocular melanin, chiefly affecting the eye while skin and hair are relatively spared. Reduced retinal pigment impairs normal foveal development (foveal hypoplasia) and disturbs the guidance cues that determine crossing of ganglion cell axons at the chiasm, causing excessive decussation and misrouting. The combined effects produce nystagmus, subnormal acuity, iris transillumination and defective binocular vision. Female carriers show mosaic features owing to random X-inactivation.","additionalDifferentials":["Oculocutaneous albinism (tyrosinase-related and other types)","Idiopathic congenital (infantile) nystagmus","Isolated foveal hypoplasia (PAX6-related)","Achromatopsia and blue-cone monochromacy","Aniridia with foveal hypoplasia","Retinal dystrophies presenting in infancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp iris transillumination assessment","Dilated fundus examination for hypopigmentation and foveal hypoplasia","OCT of the macula to grade foveal hypoplasia","Visual evoked potentials to demonstrate chiasmal misrouting","Cycloplegic refraction and orthoptic/strabismus assessment","Genetic testing of GPR143 and albinism gene panels"],"redFlags":["Systemic bleeding tendency or recurrent infections suggesting syndromic albinism (Hermansky-Pudlak or Chediak-Higashi) rather than isolated ocular disease","Progressive rather than stable visual loss prompting reassessment for a retinal dystrophy","Marked asymmetry of findings requiring exclusion of other pathology","Failure to thrive or dysmorphism suggesting a broader syndrome"],"prognosis":"Vision is subnormal but usually stable, with acuity commonly in the low-vision range; most individuals function well with refractive correction, tints and low-vision support.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis in a boy with nystagmus because skin and hair look normal","Missing carrier status in the mother by not examining her iris and fundus","Failing to check for syndromic albinism when there is bruising or infection","Attributing poor acuity solely to nystagmus and neglecting foveal hypoplasia"],"management":["No disease-specific treatment; optimise vision with full refractive correction and tinted or photochromic lenses to reduce photophobia","Low-vision aids and rehabilitation to support education and daily function","Orthoptic assessment and management of strabismus and nystagmus, with squint surgery or nystagmus null-point and head-posture surgery in selected cases","Regular ophthalmological follow-up to monitor acuity and refractive changes","Genetic counselling for X-linked GPR143 inheritance, with carrier testing of female relatives","Educational and occupational low-vision support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Taylor & Hoyt's Paediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"ocular_cicatricial_pemphigoid":{"name":"Ocular cicatricial pemphigoid (mucous membrane pemphigoid)","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OCP","mucous membrane pemphigoid","cicatricial pemphigoid","benign mucous membrane pemphigoid"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / American Academy of Ophthalmology BCSC (External Disease and Cornea)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic, bilateral, progressive cicatrising conjunctivitis, usually affecting older adults and more common in women","Early signs include chronic redness, irritation and a papillary or subtle scarring conjunctivitis that is easily mistaken for dry eye or chronic conjunctivitis","Progressive subconjunctival fibrosis causes fornix shortening (symblepharon), which is a key clinical marker of disease stage","Advanced disease leads to trichiasis, entropion, keratinisation, limbal stem cell failure, corneal scarring and blindness","Part of a systemic autoimmune blistering disorder that can also involve oral, nasopharyngeal, oesophageal and genital mucosae and the skin","Diagnosis is confirmed by conjunctival biopsy with direct immunofluorescence showing linear deposition of immunoglobulin and complement along the basement membrane zone","Management principles rely on systemic immunosuppression to halt progression, as topical therapy alone does not control the underlying inflammation","Ocular surgery can paradoxically flare disease, so inflammation should be controlled before any lid or surface procedure"],"pathophysiology":"Ocular cicatricial pemphigoid is an autoimmune type II hypersensitivity disorder in which autoantibodies are directed against components of the epithelial basement membrane zone of mucous membranes. Antibody and complement deposition triggers chronic subepithelial inflammation and activation of fibroblasts, leading to progressive subconjunctival fibrosis and scarring. This cicatrisation destroys goblet cells and accessory lacrimal tissue, shortens the fornices and produces symblepharon, while lid malposition and trichiasis further damage the ocular surface. Loss of limbal stem cells and severe aqueous and mucin deficiency culminate in corneal keratinisation, scarring and blindness.","additionalDifferentials":["Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis with ocular scarring","Chemical or thermal ocular surface burns","Chronic atopic keratoconjunctivitis","Drug-induced (pseudo)pemphigoid from topical medications","Trachoma-related conjunctival scarring","Graft-versus-host disease affecting the ocular surface","Rosacea-associated cicatrising conjunctivitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Conjunctival biopsy with direct immunofluorescence for linear basement membrane immunoreactant deposition","Serial documentation of fornix depth and symblepharon to stage and monitor disease","Slit-lamp assessment of tear film, corneal surface and lid margins","Systemic review and referral for oral, nasopharyngeal, oesophageal and cutaneous mucosal involvement","Serological testing for circulating anti-basement membrane antibodies where available","Assessment of limbal stem cell function before considering surgical intervention"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive fornix shortening or corneal involvement indicating aggressive disease","Oesophageal or laryngeal mucosal involvement risking stricture and airway compromise","Corneal ulceration or perforation from surface failure","Acute worsening after ocular surgery signalling a disease flare"],"prognosis":"Without systemic immunosuppression the disease tends to progress relentlessly to bilateral blindness, but early control of inflammation can arrest scarring and preserve vision; established cicatricial damage is generally irreversible.","pitfalls":["Attributing chronic conjunctival inflammation to dry eye and missing early cicatrisation","Relying on topical therapy alone when systemic immunosuppression is required","Operating on lids or the ocular surface during active inflammation and precipitating a flare","Overlooking a drug-induced pseudopemphigoid pattern from chronic topical medication","Failing to screen for extraocular mucosal disease that carries its own morbidity"],"management":["Systemic immunosuppression is the mainstay, stepped from dapsone in mild disease to antimetabolites such as mycophenolate, methotrexate or azathioprine and to cyclophosphamide, with rituximab or intravenous immunoglobulin for refractory or rapidly progressive disease","Ocular surface support with preservative-free lubricants, treatment of aqueous and mucin deficiency, and punctal occlusion","Lid and lash management with epilation and surgical correction of trichiasis and entropion, alongside control of associated blepharitis","Monitoring of disease activity by serial documentation of fornix depth and symblepharon, avoiding elective ocular surgery during active inflammation which can flare cicatrisation","Treatment of acute inflammatory exacerbations by intensifying systemic immunosuppression rather than relying on topical steroids alone","Multidisciplinary care with oral medicine, ENT, dermatology and gastroenterology for oral, nasopharyngeal, oesophageal and cutaneous involvement","Ocular surface reconstruction, including limbal stem cell transplantation and keratoprosthesis, for end-stage disease once inflammation is controlled"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course"],"crossLinks":[]},"ocular_ischaemic_syndrome":{"name":"Ocular Ischaemic Syndrome","system":"Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OIS","Ischaemic oculopathy","Hypoperfusion retinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ophthalmology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from chronic ocular hypoperfusion, almost always due to severe ipsilateral internal carotid artery stenosis or occlusion, occasionally ophthalmic artery disease.","Typical presentation is subacute, progressive monocular visual loss with a dull ocular or periorbital ache sometimes called ocular angina, and prolonged visual recovery after exposure to bright light.","Anterior segment signs include episcleral and conjunctival congestion, corneal oedema, mild anterior chamber inflammation and iris neovascularisation (rubeosis), which may progress to neovascular glaucoma.","Intraocular pressure may be paradoxically normal or low despite neovascular glaucoma, because ciliary body hypoperfusion reduces aqueous production.","Posterior segment shows narrowed retinal arteries, dilated but characteristically non-tortuous veins, mid-peripheral dot-and-blot haemorrhages, microaneurysms and sometimes spontaneous retinal arterial pulsation.","The dilated-but-not-tortuous veins and mid-peripheral rather than posterior-pole haemorrhages help distinguish it from central retinal vein occlusion, and it is also an important mimic of diabetic retinopathy.","It is a marker of severe systemic atherosclerosis carrying a high risk of stroke and cardiac death, so urgent vascular and cardiovascular assessment is mandatory.","Ocular treatment (panretinal photocoagulation, anti-VEGF therapy, glaucoma control) addresses neovascular complications, whereas carotid revascularisation is directed mainly at systemic risk and visual outcomes remain guarded."],"pathophysiology":"Severe stenosis or occlusion of the internal or common carotid artery, and occasionally the ophthalmic artery, lowers perfusion pressure to the eye below the threshold needed to sustain retinal and anterior segment metabolism. Chronic ischaemia and hypoxia upregulate vascular endothelial growth factor and other angiogenic mediators, driving neovascularisation of the iris, drainage angle and retina. Reduced ocular perfusion pressure produces venous stasis with dilated veins, dot-and-blot haemorrhages and microaneurysms, while arterial narrowing reflects the diminished inflow. Hypoperfusion of the ciliary body reduces aqueous humour formation, which can mask the pressure rise otherwise expected with neovascular glaucoma. Because the eye subsists on a critically low perfusion reserve, small further falls in systemic blood pressure or ocular perfusion can precipitate ischaemic symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Central retinal vein occlusion, both ischaemic and non-ischaemic","Non-proliferative or proliferative diabetic retinopathy","Central or branch retinal artery occlusion","Hypertensive retinopathy","Giant cell arteritis or other vasculitis causing ocular ischaemia","Aortic arch disease (Takayasu arteritis) or dissection reducing ocular inflow","Anterior uveitis accounting for the anterior segment inflammation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Carotid duplex ultrasound to assess stenosis or occlusion","CT or MR angiography, or catheter angiography, of the carotid and cerebral vessels","Fundus fluorescein angiography showing delayed choroidal filling, prolonged arteriovenous transit and late vessel staining","Slit-lamp examination with gonioscopy for rubeosis and angle neovascularisation, plus intraocular pressure measurement","Dilated fundus examination with optical coherence tomography for macular oedema","Cardiovascular and stroke risk assessment including blood pressure, lipids, glucose and cardiac evaluation","Inflammatory markers to exclude giant cell arteritis where relevant"],"redFlags":["Transient monocular visual loss (amaurosis fugax) or other transient ischaemic attack or stroke symptoms indicating high stroke risk","Iris neovascularisation or established neovascular glaucoma","Rapidly progressive visual loss","Features suggesting giant cell arteritis such as headache, jaw claudication or scalp tenderness","Bilateral involvement suggesting severe or proximal aortic arch disease"],"prognosis":"Visual prognosis is guarded, particularly once neovascular glaucoma develops. The syndrome signals severe systemic vascular disease with a substantial risk of stroke and cardiac death, so systemic prognosis often dominates management.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing as ordinary central retinal vein occlusion and missing the underlying carotid disease.","Being falsely reassured by a normal or low intraocular pressure despite florid neovascular glaucoma.","Failing to arrange urgent vascular and cardiovascular workup, as the syndrome is a systemic red flag.","Overlooking giant cell arteritis as a treatable cause of ocular ischaemia in older patients.","Expecting carotid revascularisation to restore vision, when its main benefit is systemic risk reduction and it can occasionally worsen glaucoma."],"management":["Treatment of the underlying carotid occlusive disease with carotid endarterectomy or stenting for significant stenosis, in conjunction with vascular surgery","Aggressive systemic vascular risk reduction with antiplatelet therapy, statins and control of hypertension, diabetes and smoking to reduce the high risk of stroke and cardiac death","Panretinal photocoagulation and intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy for iris, angle and retinal neovascularisation","Management of neovascular glaucoma with topical intraocular-pressure-lowering agents and, where needed, cyclodestructive or glaucoma drainage surgery","Intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy for associated macular oedema","Urgent multidisciplinary referral to stroke, vascular surgery and cardiology services alongside ophthalmology","Regular ophthalmic surveillance with gonioscopy and intraocular pressure monitoring to detect progressive neovascularisation early"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Retina and neuro-ophthalmology textbooks","Carotid and cerebrovascular disease guidelines","Ophthalmology clinical practice references"],"crossLinks":[]},"ocular_ischemic_syndrome":{"name":"Ocular ischaemic syndrome","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OIS","ocular ischemic syndrome","venous stasis retinopathy","hypoperfusion retinopathy","ischaemic ocular inflammation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A vision-threatening consequence of chronic ocular hypoperfusion, usually from severe (often more than 90 percent) ipsilateral carotid occlusive disease","Typically affects older patients with widespread atherosclerosis and shares risk factors with stroke and coronary disease","Symptoms include gradual or sudden visual loss, a dull ocular or periorbital ache, and prolonged recovery of vision after exposure to bright light","Anterior segment signs include episcleral congestion, a mid-dilated poorly reactive pupil, iris and angle neovascularisation (rubeosis) and, in some, flare and low intraocular pressure from reduced aqueous production","Posterior segment shows narrowed arteries, dilated but not tortuous veins, mid-peripheral dot-and-blot haemorrhages and, in some, spontaneous arterial pulsation on gentle globe pressure","Fluorescein angiography reveals delayed arm-to-retina and prolonged arteriovenous transit times and patchy choroidal filling","It is both a sight-threatening and a life-threatening condition, mandating carotid imaging and vascular risk management to reduce stroke and cardiac mortality","Management principles combine treatment of the carotid disease and systemic risk factors with panretinal photocoagulation and anti-VEGF for neovascularisation and control of neovascular glaucoma"],"pathophysiology":"Ocular ischaemic syndrome results from chronic hypoperfusion of the eye, most often due to severe stenosis or occlusion of the ipsilateral internal or common carotid artery, and less commonly ophthalmic artery or aortic arch disease. Reduced ocular perfusion pressure causes chronic ischaemia of the retina, choroid and anterior segment. Retinal and anterior segment hypoxia stimulates release of vascular endothelial growth factor, driving neovascularisation of the iris, angle and retina that can progress to neovascular glaucoma. Ischaemia of the ciliary body reduces aqueous production, which can paradoxically keep intraocular pressure low despite angle neovascularisation. Persistent hypoperfusion leads to progressive retinal and optic nerve damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Central retinal vein occlusion (non-ischaemic and ischaemic)","Diabetic retinopathy","Central retinal artery occlusion","Hypertensive retinopathy","Giant cell arteritis with ocular ischaemia","Radiation retinopathy","Anterior segment or intraocular inflammation causing rubeosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Carotid Doppler ultrasonography, and where indicated CT or MR angiography, to assess carotid stenosis","Fluorescein angiography to demonstrate delayed arm-to-retina and arteriovenous transit and patchy choroidal filling","Gonioscopy to detect angle neovascularisation and intraocular pressure measurement","Dilated fundus examination for mid-peripheral haemorrhages and vascular changes","Systemic cardiovascular evaluation including blood pressure, glucose, lipids and cardiac assessment","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein if giant cell arteritis is a consideration"],"redFlags":["Iris or angle neovascularisation heralding neovascular glaucoma","Critical carotid stenosis carrying a high risk of stroke","Rapidly progressive visual loss from worsening ischaemia","Systemic features suggesting giant cell arteritis in an older patient"],"prognosis":"The visual prognosis is generally guarded, particularly once neovascularisation or neovascular glaucoma develops, and the syndrome carries a substantial risk of stroke and cardiovascular death, so systemic vascular management is as important as ocular treatment.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the retinopathy as an ordinary central retinal vein occlusion and missing the underlying carotid disease","Overlooking the systemic (stroke and cardiac) mortality risk and failing to refer for vascular assessment","Being falsely reassured by a normal or low intraocular pressure despite florid angle neovascularisation","Not considering giant cell arteritis in an older patient with ocular ischaemia","Delaying panretinal photocoagulation or anti-VEGF until neovascular glaucoma is established"],"management":["Treatment of the underlying carotid occlusive disease with carotid endarterectomy or stenting for significant stenosis, in conjunction with vascular surgery","Aggressive systemic vascular risk reduction with antiplatelet therapy, statins and control of hypertension, diabetes and smoking to reduce the high risk of stroke and cardiac death","Panretinal photocoagulation and intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy for iris, angle and retinal neovascularisation","Management of neovascular glaucoma with topical intraocular-pressure-lowering agents and, where needed, cyclodestructive or glaucoma drainage surgery","Intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy for associated macular oedema","Urgent multidisciplinary referral to stroke, vascular surgery and cardiology services alongside ophthalmology","Regular ophthalmic surveillance with gonioscopy and intraocular pressure monitoring to detect progressive neovascularisation early"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina"],"crossLinks":[]},"ocular_neuromyotonia":{"name":"Ocular neuromyotonia","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ONM"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Paroxysmal involuntary sustained contraction of one or more extraocular muscles causing episodic diplopia","Classically follows sellar or parasellar radiotherapy, often months to years later","Any of the ocular motor nerves may be affected, producing a transient strabismus in the field of the involved muscle","Attacks are often triggered by sustained eccentric gaze and last seconds to minutes","Between attacks ocular alignment may be normal, which can lead to misdiagnosis","Membrane-stabilising agents that reduce neuronal excitability are the usual treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Ocular neuromyotonia is attributed to impaired membrane stability and spontaneous or triggered discharges within an ocular motor nerve, frequently after radiation-induced or compressive injury that alters axonal excitability. The abnormal firing produces sustained contraction (tonic spasm) of the muscles supplied by the affected nerve, generating an intermittent deviation and diplopia, particularly after maintained eccentric gaze.","additionalDifferentials":["Superior oblique myokymia","Cyclic oculomotor palsy","Thyroid eye disease with restriction","Myasthenia gravis (fatigable diplopia)","Decompensating phoria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed history of prior cranial radiotherapy or sellar disease","MRI of the brain, orbits and cavernous sinus to exclude compression or recurrence","Orthoptic assessment during and between attacks","Acetylcholine-receptor antibody testing to exclude myasthenia when relevant"],"redFlags":["New or progressive structural lesion on imaging","Additional cranial nerve involvement","Signs of tumour recurrence in a previously irradiated field","Features suggesting myasthenia (fatigability, ptosis, bulbar signs)"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign for vision and often responds well to membrane-stabilising therapy, though symptoms can persist for years and may recur if treatment is stopped.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis because alignment is normal between attacks","Attributing episodic diplopia to myasthenia without antibody or imaging workup","Overlooking a history of prior radiotherapy","Confusing sustained deviation with the shimmering oscillopsia of superior oblique myokymia"],"management":["Membrane-stabilising anticonvulsant therapy, typically carbamazepine, to suppress the paroxysmal extraocular muscle spasms","Symptomatic relief of diplopia between attacks with prisms or temporary occlusion where needed","Neuroimaging to exclude compression or recurrence of an underlying sellar or parasellar lesion, particularly after prior radiotherapy","Ongoing neuro-ophthalmology and orthoptic follow-up, with cautious continuation of therapy since symptoms may recur if treatment is stopped","Multidisciplinary input from neurology, ophthalmology and, where relevant, oncology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"ocular_surface_squamous_neoplasia":{"name":"Ocular surface squamous neoplasia","system":"Eye / Ophthalmic Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OSSN","conjunctival intraepithelial neoplasia","corneal-conjunctival intraepithelial neoplasia","conjunctival squamous cell carcinoma spectrum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / WHO Classification of Eye Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An umbrella term for a spectrum of squamous dysplasia of the conjunctiva and cornea, ranging from mild intraepithelial dysplasia to invasive squamous cell carcinoma","Typically arises at the limbus within the interpalpebral fissure, presenting as a gelatinous, papilliform, or leukoplakic lesion often with prominent feeder vessels","Major risk factors are ultraviolet exposure, older age, fair skin, human papillomavirus, and immunosuppression including HIV, which drives more aggressive disease in younger patients","The disease progresses along a continuum from intraepithelial dysplasia through carcinoma in situ to frankly invasive squamous cell carcinoma breaching the basement membrane","Rose bengal or lissamine green staining and anterior-segment optical coherence tomography aid non-invasive assessment, but histology confirms the diagnosis and invasion status","Treatment options include complete surgical excision with a no-touch technique and adjunctive cryotherapy, or medical therapy with topical chemotherapy or interferon","Wide margins and cryotherapy to the surgical edges reduce recurrence, and diffuse or recurrent disease may be managed medically"],"pathophysiology":"Ocular surface squamous neoplasia encompasses a continuum of squamous epithelial dysplasia of the conjunctiva and cornea. Chronic ultraviolet damage, alongside cofactors such as human papillomavirus infection, immunosuppression, and older age, induces mutations in limbal epithelial stem cells, the presumed cells of origin at the interpalpebral limbus. Disordered proliferation begins as partial-thickness dysplasia, advances to full-thickness carcinoma in situ while the basement membrane remains intact, and, if the basement membrane is breached, becomes invasive squamous cell carcinoma with potential for deeper ocular and, less commonly, regional spread. The intraepithelial phases are typically indolent and amenable to topical therapy, whereas invasion signifies a more serious tumour requiring definitive surgical management.","additionalDifferentials":["Pterygium and pinguecula","Conjunctival papilloma","Pyogenic granuloma of the conjunctiva","Conjunctival melanoma or primary acquired melanosis","Benign conjunctival intraepithelial dyskeratosis","Amelanotic conjunctival naevus","Limbal dermoid"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination documenting limbal location, feeder vessels, and leukoplakia","Vital-dye staining (rose bengal or lissamine green) to highlight abnormal epithelium","Anterior-segment optical coherence tomography showing thickened hyper-reflective epithelium with an abrupt transition","Impression cytology or incisional/excisional biopsy for histological confirmation","Assessment of invasion depth and margins on histopathology","Evaluation of immune status including HIV testing in aggressive or young-onset disease"],"redFlags":["Fixation of the lesion to underlying sclera or intraocular extension suggesting invasive carcinoma","Rapidly enlarging or recurrent lesion despite treatment","Aggressive disease in a young or immunosuppressed patient prompting HIV testing","Regional lymphadenopathy suggesting metastatic spread"],"prognosis":"Most intraepithelial disease is controlled with complete excision or topical therapy and carries an excellent prognosis, though recurrence is not uncommon after narrow-margin surgery; invasive squamous cell carcinoma has a more guarded outlook with risk of ocular invasion and, rarely, regional or distant spread.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the lesion for a pterygium and excising it without histology, missing dysplasia or invasion","Performing narrow-margin excision without adjunctive cryotherapy, increasing recurrence","Failing to test for immunosuppression in aggressive or young-onset cases","Assuming intraepithelial disease when unrecognised invasion is present"],"management":["Complete surgical excision with wide margins using a no-touch technique, with adjunctive conjunctival cryotherapy and corneal alcohol epitheliectomy","Topical medical therapy with interferon alfa-2b, mitomycin C or 5-fluorouracil as primary treatment or as an adjuvant for diffuse disease or involved margins","For invasive squamous cell carcinoma, wider excision with adjuvant plaque brachytherapy or radiotherapy, escalating to enucleation or exenteration for extensive ocular invasion","Lifelong ultraviolet protection with sunglasses and avoidance of further solar exposure","Assessment and treatment of immunosuppression, including HIV testing in aggressive or young-onset disease","Long-term slit-lamp surveillance for recurrence, which is common after narrow-margin surgery","Referral to an ophthalmic oncology service for multidisciplinary management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","WHO Classification of Eye Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"oculocutaneous_albinism":{"name":"oculocutaneous albinism","system":"Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OCA","albinism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A genetically heterogeneous group of autosomal recessive disorders of melanin biosynthesis affecting skin, hair, and eyes, with OCA1 (tyrosinase/TYR), OCA2 (OCA2/P gene), OCA3 (TYRP1), and OCA4 (SLC45A2) accounting for most cases.","OCA1A reflects complete tyrosinase deficiency with lifelong white hair, milky skin, and translucent blue-grey irides, whereas OCA1B (yellow variant) permits some pigment accumulation with age.","The ocular phenotype is the diagnostic core: congenital nystagmus, foveal hypoplasia, reduced visual acuity, iris transillumination, and photophobia occur across all subtypes.","Misrouting of the optic nerve fibres at the chiasm, with excessive decussation, is a defining neuro-ophthalmic feature demonstrable on visual evoked potentials.","Cutaneous melanin deficiency confers a markedly increased lifetime risk of actinic damage, squamous and basal cell carcinoma, and melanoma, mandating lifelong sun protection.","Syndromic albinism must be excluded: Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome (bleeding diathesis, pulmonary fibrosis, ceroid deposition) and Chediak-Higashi syndrome (immunodeficiency, giant granules) are life-limiting mimics.","TYR mutations remain heat-sensitive in the temperature-sensitive variant, producing darker hair at cooler acral sites analogous to the Siamese-cat pattern.","Skin and hair colour vary widely between and within subtypes and ethnic backgrounds, so ocular signs rather than degree of cutaneous pigment guide diagnosis."],"pathophysiology":"Oculocutaneous albinism results from mutations that reduce or abolish melanin synthesis within otherwise structurally normal melanocytes. In OCA1 the defect lies in tyrosinase, the rate-limiting enzyme catalysing the conversion of tyrosine to dopaquinone; complete deficiency (OCA1A) yields no melanin, while partial activity (OCA1B) permits limited pigment. Other subtypes disrupt melanosomal proteins that regulate melanosome pH, maturation, and tyrosinase trafficking: the OCA2 (P) protein and SLC45A2 (MATP) influence melanosomal acidification and enzyme processing, and TYRP1 stabilises tyrosinase and directs eumelanin synthesis. The resulting hypomelanosis affects the developing eye, where melanin is required for normal foveal development and correct routing of retinal ganglion cell axons; its absence produces foveal hypoplasia, nystagmus, and abnormal chiasmal decussation. In the skin, absent melanin removes the natural photoprotective shield, accelerating ultraviolet-induced mutagenesis and photoageing.","additionalDifferentials":["Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome","Chediak-Higashi syndrome","Ocular albinism (X-linked, GPR143)","Griscelli syndrome","Waardenburg syndrome type II","Menkes disease","Phenylketonuria-related hypopigmentation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive ophthalmological assessment including visual acuity, nystagmus evaluation, and fundoscopy for foveal hypoplasia","Visual evoked potentials demonstrating chiasmal misrouting","Molecular genetic panel testing (TYR, OCA2, TYRP1, SLC45A2 and syndromic genes)","Full blood count and platelet aggregation studies where Hermansky-Pudlak or Chediak-Higashi is suspected","Peripheral blood film for giant granules in neutrophils (Chediak-Higashi)","Regular dermatological surveillance with dermoscopy for cutaneous malignancy"],"redFlags":["Easy bruising or bleeding suggesting Hermansky-Pudlak platelet dysfunction","Recurrent severe infections or a hyperinflammatory (accelerated) phase indicating Chediak-Higashi syndrome","Progressive respiratory decline from pulmonary fibrosis in Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome","New, changing, or ulcerated cutaneous lesion suspicious for skin cancer","Neurological deterioration pointing to a syndromic or alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Isolated oculocutaneous albinism is compatible with a normal lifespan, though visual impairment is lifelong and non-progressive, and diligent photoprotection substantially reduces the otherwise high risk of skin malignancy. Syndromic forms such as Hermansky-Pudlak and Chediak-Higashi carry a markedly worse prognosis owing to bleeding, pulmonary, or immunological complications.","pitfalls":["Assuming visible skin or hair pigment excludes albinism, when ocular features are the true diagnostic key","Missing syndromic albinism by failing to enquire about bleeding, infection, or pulmonary symptoms","Under-emphasising lifelong photoprotection and skin cancer surveillance in darker-skinned patients","Confusing OCA with X-linked ocular albinism, which spares the skin and affects mainly males","Neglecting low-vision aids and educational support, thereby compounding avoidable disability"],"management":["No curative therapy; lifelong rigorous photoprotection with high-factor broad-spectrum sunscreen, protective clothing and sun avoidance to reduce skin-cancer risk","Regular dermatological surveillance with dermoscopy for basal and squamous cell carcinoma and melanoma, with prompt excision of suspicious lesions","Optimisation of vision with refractive correction and tinted or photochromic lenses for photophobia, plus low-vision aids and rehabilitation","Orthoptic management of congenital nystagmus and strabismus, with squint or nystagmus surgery in selected cases","Exclusion of syndromic albinism such as Hermansky-Pudlak and Chediak-Higashi syndromes, which alter prognosis and management","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance with testing of at-risk relatives","Multidisciplinary care with educational and occupational low-vision support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine, current edition","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","GeneReviews: Oculocutaneous Albinism, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"oculocutaneous_albinism_type_1":{"name":"Oculocutaneous albinism type 1","system":"Hearing / Vision genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OCA1","Tyrosinase-related albinism","OCA1A/OCA1B"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["OCA1 is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic variants in TYR, the gene encoding tyrosinase, the rate-limiting enzyme of melanin synthesis.","It is subdivided into OCA1A, with complete absence of tyrosinase activity and lifelong total lack of melanin, and OCA1B, with residual activity allowing some pigment to accumulate over time.","OCA1A presents with white hair, milky-white skin and translucent blue-grey or pink irides that never darken, whereas OCA1B children may develop light-yellow or blond hair and some skin and iris pigment with age.","The ocular phenotype is obligatory and includes nystagmus, foveal hypoplasia, iris transillumination, reduced visual acuity and photophobia.","Misrouting of optic-nerve fibres with excessive decussation at the chiasm disrupts binocular vision and causes strabismus and abnormal visual-evoked potentials.","The temperature-sensitive OCA1B variant can produce darker hair in cooler acral areas and lighter hair in warmer regions, reflecting the thermolability of the mutant enzyme.","Melanin deficiency confers a markedly increased lifetime risk of actinic damage and skin cancer, demanding rigorous photoprotection.","Unlike syndromic albinism such as Hermansky-Pudlak or Chediak-Higashi, OCA1 is non-syndromic without bleeding diathesis or immune defect."],"pathophysiology":"Oculocutaneous albinism type 1 is an autosomal recessive disorder of melanin biosynthesis caused by pathogenic variants in TYR, which encodes tyrosinase, the copper-dependent enzyme that catalyses the first and rate-limiting steps of melanogenesis: the hydroxylation of tyrosine to dihydroxyphenylalanine and its subsequent oxidation to dopaquinone within melanosomes of skin, hair-bulb and ocular melanocytes and the retinal pigment epithelium. In OCA1A, null variants abolish enzyme activity entirely, so no eumelanin or phaeomelanin is ever produced, giving lifelong complete absence of pigment; in OCA1B, hypomorphic variants leave partial, sometimes temperature-sensitive, residual activity that permits gradual accumulation of some melanin, accounting for the milder and evolving phenotype. Because melanin in the developing eye is essential for normal foveal development and for correct guidance of retinal ganglion-cell axons at the optic chiasm, its absence causes foveal hypoplasia, reduced visual acuity, nystagmus, iris and retinal hypopigmentation with transillumination, and abnormal, excessive crossing of nerve fibres to the contralateral hemisphere that impairs stereopsis. The lack of cutaneous melanin removes protection against ultraviolet radiation, so keratinocytes and melanocytes accumulate photodamage, explaining the high incidence of solar keratoses and both melanoma and non-melanoma skin cancer; hearing is unaffected because tyrosinase has no role in cochlear function, distinguishing OCA1 from pigment-related syndromic deafness.","additionalDifferentials":["Oculocutaneous albinism types 2, 3 and 4 from OCA2, TYRP1 and SLC45A2 variants","Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome with albinism plus a bleeding tendency and pulmonary fibrosis","Chediak-Higashi syndrome with albinism, immune deficiency and giant granules","Ocular albinism with predominantly ocular hypopigmentation and X-linked inheritance","Griscelli syndrome with silvery hair and immune or neurological features","Waardenburg syndrome with patchy depigmentation and deafness","Phenylketonuria and other causes of generalised skin and hair hypopigmentation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of TYR with differentiation of OCA1A from OCA1B","Full ophthalmological assessment including acuity, nystagmus and iris transillumination","Optical coherence tomography to demonstrate foveal hypoplasia","Visual-evoked potentials to detect abnormal chiasmal misrouting","Dermatological examination and regular skin-cancer surveillance","Platelet function studies if a Hermansky-Pudlak phenotype is suspected","Family and pedigree evaluation with carrier counselling"],"redFlags":["New, changing or pigmented cutaneous lesion suggesting melanoma or other skin cancer","Extensive actinic damage or non-healing sun-exposed skin lesions","Rapidly worsening or asymmetric nystagmus prompting reassessment for other pathology","Bleeding tendency or recurrent infection suggesting a syndromic albinism rather than OCA1","Severe photophobia limiting function and education in a child"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is normal provided rigorous photoprotection and skin-cancer surveillance are maintained; visual acuity is permanently reduced and does not normalise, though it is often stable and may be marginally better in OCA1B, and low-vision support enables good functional independence.","pitfalls":["Assuming visual acuity will improve with pigment accumulation in OCA1B when foveal hypoplasia is fixed","Neglecting lifelong skin-cancer surveillance and photoprotection counselling","Failing to distinguish non-syndromic OCA1 from Hermansky-Pudlak or Chediak-Higashi with their systemic risks","Mistaking evolving OCA1B pigmentation for a milder OCA2 or for normal variation","Attributing associated deafness to albinism when it points instead to Waardenburg or another diagnosis"],"management":["No curative treatment; lifelong strict photoprotection with high-factor broad-spectrum sunscreen, protective clothing and sun avoidance, especially critical in tyrosinase-null OCA1A with no protective melanin","Regular dermatological surveillance with dermoscopy for actinic damage, basal and squamous cell carcinoma and melanoma, with early excision","Optimisation of vision with refractive correction and tinted or photochromic lenses for photophobia, plus low-vision aids and rehabilitation","Orthoptic management of congenital nystagmus and strabismus, with squint or nystagmus null-point surgery where indicated","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive TYR-related disease, distinguishing OCA1A from OCA1B for prognostic guidance, with testing of at-risk relatives","Multidisciplinary care with educational and occupational low-vision support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical genetics reference texts","Ophthalmology reference texts","Dermatology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"oculocutaneous_albinism_type_2":{"name":"Oculocutaneous albinism type 2","system":"Dermatology / Ophthalmology genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OCA2","P gene albinism","Tyrosinase-positive albinism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic variants in the OCA2 gene (formerly the P gene) and is the commonest form of oculocutaneous albinism in sub-Saharan African populations.","Unlike tyrosinase-negative OCA1A, some melanin is usually present, so hair may be pale yellow to light brown and can darken through childhood, and pigmented naevi and freckles may develop.","Ocular features are constant: congenital nystagmus, foveal hypoplasia, iris transillumination, reduced visual acuity, photophobia and misrouting of optic fibres at the chiasm.","The OCA2 locus lies within the 15q11-q13 region, so albinism can accompany Prader-Willi or Angelman syndrome when a deletion unmasks a variant on the other allele.","'Brown OCA' is an allelic variant of OCA2 with light-brown hair and skin and grey-to-hazel irides.","Skin is highly ultraviolet-sensitive, so lifelong photoprotection and dermatological surveillance for cutaneous malignancy are essential.","Visual acuity is reduced but typically stable and non-progressive; refractive correction and low-vision aids are helpful.","Inheritance is autosomal recessive; molecular confirmation and genetic counselling guide recurrence-risk advice."],"pathophysiology":"OCA2 results from loss-of-function variants in the OCA2 gene on chromosome 15q, which encodes the P protein, a melanosomal transmembrane polypeptide. The P protein is thought to regulate melanosomal pH and the processing, stability and trafficking of tyrosinase, and thereby the maturation of melanosomes and eumelanin synthesis. Reduced P-protein function lowers melanin production in skin, hair and the eye, although some residual tyrosinase activity usually persists, accounting for the partial and often age-progressive pigmentation that distinguishes OCA2 from tyrosinase-negative OCA1A. Deficient ocular melanin during development disrupts foveal maturation and the normal decussation of retinal ganglion-cell axons at the optic chiasm, producing foveal hypoplasia, nystagmus and chiasmal misrouting.","additionalDifferentials":["Oculocutaneous albinism type 1 (TYR) — usually more severe, with little or no pigment at birth.","Oculocutaneous albinism type 3 (TYRP1) — reddish rufous phenotype.","Oculocutaneous albinism type 4 (SLC45A2) — often clinically indistinguishable, requiring molecular testing.","Other OCA subtypes such as OCA6 (SLC24A5) and OCA7.","Ocular albinism (GPR143, X-linked) — ocular features with near-normal skin.","Syndromic albinism — Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome and Chediak-Higashi syndrome.","Prader-Willi or Angelman syndrome with associated hypopigmentation."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive ophthalmological assessment — visual acuity, nystagmus, iris transillumination and refraction.","Optical coherence tomography to document foveal hypoplasia.","Visual evoked potentials to detect chiasmal misrouting.","Molecular genetic testing — an oculocutaneous albinism gene panel or sequencing including the OCA2 gene.","Dermatological examination and cutaneous malignancy surveillance.","Assessment for syndromic features (bleeding and infection history) to exclude Hermansky-Pudlak and Chediak-Higashi syndromes.","Pedigree analysis and genetic counselling."],"redFlags":["New, changing or ulcerating skin lesions suggesting basal cell carcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma or melanoma.","Easy bruising, epistaxis or a bleeding tendency pointing to Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome.","Recurrent severe infections or a silvery hair sheen suggesting Chediak-Higashi syndrome.","Developmental delay, hypotonia or dysmorphism raising the possibility of a 15q deletion syndrome."],"prognosis":"Lifespan is normal and visual impairment, although lifelong, is usually stable and non-progressive. The principal preventable morbidity is ultraviolet-induced skin cancer, which rigorous photoprotection and regular skin surveillance substantially reduce.","pitfalls":["Assuming all albinism is tyrosinase-negative OCA1 and missing the milder, pigment-permitting OCA2.","Overlooking syndromic causes such as Hermansky-Pudlak and Chediak-Higashi syndromes that carry systemic risks.","Failing to arrange visual evoked potentials, so chiasmal misrouting is missed.","Interpreting age-related darkening of hair as evidence against the diagnosis.","Relying on phenotype alone to separate OCA2 from OCA4, which requires molecular testing."],"management":["No curative treatment; lifelong rigorous photoprotection with high-factor broad-spectrum sunscreen, protective clothing and sun avoidance","Regular dermatological surveillance with dermoscopy for cutaneous malignancy and prompt excision of suspicious lesions","Optimisation of vision with refractive correction, tinted or photochromic lenses for photophobia and low-vision aids and rehabilitation","Orthoptic management of congenital nystagmus and strabismus, with surgery in selected cases","Assessment for Prader-Willi or Angelman syndrome where a 15q11-q13 deletion is suspected","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive OCA2 inheritance with testing of at-risk relatives","Multidisciplinary care with educational and occupational low-vision support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical genetics reference texts","Dermatology reference texts","Ophthalmic genetics references"],"crossLinks":[]},"oculocutaneous_albinism_type_3":{"name":"Oculocutaneous albinism type 3","system":"Dermatology / Ophthalmology genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OCA3","TYRP1 albinism","Rufous albinism","Brown albinism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic variants in TYRP1 and classically produces 'rufous' (red) oculocutaneous albinism, best characterised in southern African populations.","Affected individuals typically have reddish-bronze or ginger-red skin and hair with blue-to-hazel irides, reflecting a shift away from brown-black eumelanin.","Ocular involvement is often milder than in OCA1 or OCA2, but nystagmus, foveal hypoplasia and reduced acuity can still occur.","TYRP1 acts downstream in eumelanin synthesis, so its loss reduces dark eumelanin while relatively preserving red-yellow phaeomelanin.","Because features can be subtle, OCA3 is easily under-recognised and may be mistaken for a constitutional red-haired phenotype.","Photoprotection and cutaneous malignancy surveillance remain important despite the comparatively mild appearance.","Inheritance is autosomal recessive; molecular confirmation and genetic counselling are advised."],"pathophysiology":"OCA3 arises from loss-of-function variants in TYRP1 on chromosome 9, encoding tyrosinase-related protein 1. TYRP1 functions within the melanosome to stabilise tyrosinase and to catalyse a step in the eumelanin (brown-black pigment) synthetic pathway. Deficient TYRP1 activity therefore selectively curtails eumelanin production while red-yellow phaeomelanin is relatively spared, generating the characteristic rufous colouring of skin and hair. Because some pigment is retained and the ocular pathway is less severely disrupted than in tyrosinase-negative albinism, ophthalmic features such as foveal hypoplasia, nystagmus and chiasmal misrouting are variable and sometimes mild.","additionalDifferentials":["Oculocutaneous albinism type 1 (TYR).","Oculocutaneous albinism type 2 (OCA2 gene) — pigment-permitting phenotype.","Oculocutaneous albinism type 4 (SLC45A2).","Constitutional red-hair phenotype due to MC1R variants without albinism.","Ocular albinism (GPR143).","Other OCA subtypes such as OCA6 and OCA7.","Syndromic albinism — Hermansky-Pudlak and Chediak-Higashi syndromes."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ophthalmological assessment including acuity, nystagmus and iris transillumination.","Optical coherence tomography for foveal hypoplasia.","Visual evoked potentials for chiasmal misrouting.","Molecular genetic testing of TYRP1 within an oculocutaneous albinism panel.","Hair and skin pigment phenotyping.","Dermatological review and cutaneous malignancy surveillance.","Pedigree analysis and genetic counselling."],"redFlags":["Suspicious, changing or non-healing cutaneous lesions indicating skin malignancy.","Unusually severe visual impairment prompting reassessment for another OCA subtype.","Systemic features such as a bleeding tendency or recurrent infections suggesting syndromic albinism."],"prognosis":"The condition is generally mild, with a normal lifespan and often only modest visual impairment. As with all albinism, cumulative ultraviolet exposure raises the risk of skin cancer, mitigated by photoprotection and surveillance.","pitfalls":["Dismissing the phenotype as ordinary red hair rather than recognising albinism.","Assuming ocular features are always mild and omitting formal visual assessment.","Relying on appearance alone rather than confirming the subtype with TYRP1 testing.","Neglecting skin-cancer prevention because pigmentation looks relatively preserved."],"management":["No curative treatment; lifelong photoprotection with broad-spectrum sunscreen, protective clothing and sun avoidance despite the comparatively mild rufous phenotype","Regular dermatological surveillance with dermoscopy for cutaneous malignancy","Optimisation of vision with refractive correction, tints for photophobia and low-vision aids where nystagmus and reduced acuity are present","Orthoptic assessment and management of nystagmus and strabismus when present","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive TYRP1-related disease with testing of at-risk relatives","Educational and occupational low-vision support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical genetics reference texts","Dermatology reference texts","Ophthalmic genetics references"],"crossLinks":[]},"oculocutaneous_albinism_type_4":{"name":"Oculocutaneous albinism type 4","system":"Dermatology / Ophthalmology genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OCA4","SLC45A2 albinism","MATP albinism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic variants in SLC45A2 (also called MATP) and is a leading cause of oculocutaneous albinism in the Japanese population while occurring worldwide.","The phenotype is frequently indistinguishable from OCA2, with white-to-cream skin, white-to-yellow hair and the full range of ocular features, so subtype assignment depends on molecular testing.","Ocular hallmarks include congenital nystagmus, foveal hypoplasia, iris transillumination, reduced acuity, photophobia and chiasmal misrouting.","Pigment may increase modestly with age in some individuals, as also seen in OCA2.","SLC45A2 encodes a melanosomal transporter that helps regulate melanosomal pH and tyrosinase processing.","Lifelong photoprotection and dermatological surveillance are required because of high ultraviolet susceptibility.","Visual impairment is usually stable; refractive correction and low-vision support are beneficial.","Inheritance is autosomal recessive; genetic confirmation guides counselling."],"pathophysiology":"OCA4 is caused by loss-of-function variants in SLC45A2 on chromosome 5, encoding a melanosomal solute-carrier (membrane-associated transporter) protein. This transporter contributes to regulation of melanosomal pH and thereby to the correct processing, sorting and activity of tyrosinase during melanogenesis. Impaired SLC45A2 function reduces melanin synthesis across skin, hair and the eye. The resulting phenotype overlaps closely with OCA2 because both mechanisms converge on melanosomal maturation, and reduced ocular melanin during development produces foveal hypoplasia, nystagmus and misrouting of optic-nerve fibres at the chiasm.","additionalDifferentials":["Oculocutaneous albinism type 2 (OCA2) — often clinically identical, distinguished only by genetics.","Oculocutaneous albinism type 1 (TYR).","Oculocutaneous albinism type 3 (TYRP1).","Other OCA subtypes such as OCA6 (SLC24A5) and OCA7.","Ocular albinism (GPR143).","Syndromic albinism — Hermansky-Pudlak and Chediak-Higashi syndromes.","Griscelli and related pigmentary or immunological syndromes."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive ophthalmological assessment — acuity, nystagmus, iris transillumination and refraction.","Optical coherence tomography to document foveal hypoplasia.","Visual evoked potentials to detect chiasmal misrouting.","Molecular genetic testing including SLC45A2 within an oculocutaneous albinism panel.","Dermatological examination and cutaneous malignancy surveillance.","Screening for syndromic features to exclude Hermansky-Pudlak and Chediak-Higashi syndromes.","Pedigree analysis and genetic counselling."],"redFlags":["New or changing skin lesions suggesting cutaneous malignancy.","A bleeding tendency raising suspicion of Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome.","Recurrent infections or silvery hair suggesting Chediak-Higashi syndrome.","Rapidly deteriorating vision, which is atypical and warrants review for an alternative diagnosis."],"prognosis":"Lifespan is normal and the visual deficit, though permanent, is typically stable. Ultraviolet-related skin cancer is the main preventable risk and is reduced by consistent photoprotection and skin surveillance.","pitfalls":["Attempting to distinguish OCA4 from OCA2 on appearance alone rather than by molecular testing.","Under-recognising OCA4 outside Japanese populations, where it is also present.","Omitting visual evoked potentials, so chiasmal misrouting is missed.","Neglecting screening for syndromic albinism with systemic implications."],"management":["No curative treatment; lifelong rigorous photoprotection with high-factor broad-spectrum sunscreen, protective clothing and sun avoidance","Regular dermatological surveillance with dermoscopy for cutaneous malignancy and early excision","Optimisation of vision with refractive correction, tinted or photochromic lenses for photophobia and low-vision aids and rehabilitation","Orthoptic management of congenital nystagmus and strabismus, with surgery in selected cases","Exclusion of syndromic albinism such as Hermansky-Pudlak and Chediak-Higashi syndromes where a bleeding or infection history suggests it","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive SLC45A2-related disease with testing of at-risk relatives","Multidisciplinary care with educational and occupational low-vision support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical genetics reference texts","Dermatology reference texts","Ophthalmic genetics references"],"crossLinks":[]},"oculodentodigital_dysplasia":{"name":"Oculodentodigital dysplasia","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GJA1","ODDD","oculodentoosseous dysplasia","connexin-43 disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A recognisable pattern of ocular, dental and digital anomalies caused by variants in GJA1, encoding the gap junction protein connexin-43","The hand hallmark is type III syndactyly, characteristically fusion of the fourth and fifth fingers, sometimes with camptodactyly","Ocular features include microphthalmia, microcornea and a narrow palpebral fissure, with a risk of glaucoma","Enamel hypoplasia and small, malformed teeth are typical, alongside a thin nose with hypoplastic alae and a narrow nasal bridge","Usually autosomal dominant; neurological problems including spasticity and white matter changes can emerge, sometimes progressively","The combination of syndactyly, small eyes and dental anomalies with a distinctive nose is highly suggestive","Management is supportive with ophthalmic surveillance (including for glaucoma), dental care and monitoring for neurological features"],"pathophysiology":"Oculodentodigital dysplasia is caused by variants in GJA1, which encodes connexin-43, a component of gap junctions that permit direct intercellular communication. Impaired connexin-43 channel function disrupts coordinated signalling between cells in developing eyes, teeth, digits, bone and neural tissue. This explains the pleiotropic phenotype of microphthalmia and glaucoma risk, enamel hypoplasia, syndactyly and craniofacial features, as well as the later neurological manifestations attributable to disturbed gap-junction communication in the central nervous system.","additionalDifferentials":["Other syndromic syndactylies (e.g. Apert syndrome)","Hallermann-Streiff syndrome","Hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia (dental and craniofacial features)","Craniofacial-digital syndromes","Leukodystrophies (for the neurological features)","Isolated congenital glaucoma or microphthalmia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["GJA1 sequencing to confirm the diagnosis","Ophthalmic assessment including intraocular pressure and screening for glaucoma","Hand and skeletal radiographs to characterise syndactyly and bone changes","Dental assessment for enamel hypoplasia and tooth anomalies","Neurological evaluation and brain MRI where neurological features are present","Family evaluation given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Raised intraocular pressure or established glaucoma threatening vision","Progressive spasticity, gait disturbance or other neurological decline","Significant visual impairment from microphthalmia or associated anomalies","Functional impairment of the hands from severe syndactyly"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is generally normal, but vision is at risk from glaucoma and ocular anomalies, and a subset develop progressive neurological problems, so long-term ophthalmic and neurological follow-up is advisable.","pitfalls":["Missing glaucoma because intraocular pressure is not routinely monitored","Treating the syndactyly in isolation without recognising the broader syndrome","Overlooking evolving neurological features that may appear years after diagnosis","Failing to examine and counsel apparently mildly affected relatives"],"management":["No disease-specific therapy exists; care is supportive and coordinated across specialties","Ophthalmic surveillance for glaucoma with intraocular-pressure-lowering therapy or surgery, and correction of refractive and microphthalmia-related problems","Restorative dental care for enamel hypoplasia and small malformed teeth","Hand surgery to correct type III syndactyly and improve function","Neurological surveillance for emerging spasticity and white-matter disease, with physiotherapy and antispasticity agents as needed","Genetic counselling for the autosomal dominant GJA1 variant with family evaluation","Long-term multidisciplinary follow-up given the progressive potential of ocular and neurological features"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Oculodentodigital Dysplasia","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"oculomotor_nerve_palsy_compressive":{"name":"Compressive third nerve palsy","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["surgical third nerve palsy","aneurysmal oculomotor palsy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A third nerve palsy with pupil involvement is a compressive palsy until proven otherwise, classically from a posterior communicating artery aneurysm","The eye is typically down and out with ptosis, and a dilated poorly reactive pupil signals external compression of the superficial pupillomotor fibres","Sudden painful pupil-involving third nerve palsy demands urgent vascular imaging to exclude a life-threatening aneurysm","Pupil-sparing complete palsies in older vasculopathic patients are more often ischaemic (microvascular), but this reassurance applies mainly to complete palsies","Aberrant regeneration (for example lid elevation on adduction) points to a chronic compressive or traumatic rather than ischaemic cause","Any progressive, incomplete or evolving palsy warrants imaging even if the pupil is initially spared"],"pathophysiology":"The pupillary parasympathetic fibres travel superficially on the oculomotor nerve and derive their blood supply from surface vessels, so external compression (from an aneurysm, tumour or uncal herniation) tends to involve the pupil early. In contrast, microvascular ischaemia from diabetes or hypertension damages the central fascicles while sparing the peripheral pupillomotor fibres, producing a pupil-sparing palsy. Compression of the nerve as it passes near the posterior communicating artery or through the cavernous sinus and superior orbital fissure accounts for the classic anatomical patterns.","additionalDifferentials":["Microvascular (ischaemic) third nerve palsy","Cavernous sinus or superior orbital fissure lesion","Myasthenia gravis (pupil always spared, fatigable)","Midbrain (fascicular or nuclear) third nerve lesion","Ocular myopathy or thyroid eye disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent CT or MR angiography to exclude an aneurysm","MRI of the brain and cavernous sinus for compressive or infiltrative lesions","Assessment of pupil, lid and full ocular motility","Vasculopathic risk-factor evaluation when ischaemia is considered","Acetylcholine-receptor antibody testing if myasthenia is possible"],"redFlags":["Pupil involvement, especially with acute severe pain","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or reduced conscious level","Progression or additional cranial nerve involvement","Bilateral involvement or evolving deficits"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the cause; microvascular palsies commonly recover over weeks to a few months, whereas compressive palsies depend on timely treatment of the underlying lesion, and a ruptured aneurysm is life-threatening.","pitfalls":["Falsely reassuring pupil sparing in an incomplete or evolving palsy","Delaying angiography in a pupil-involving palsy","Attributing the palsy to diabetes without imaging when features are atypical","Missing aberrant regeneration that indicates a chronic compressive cause"],"management":["Urgent vascular imaging and definitive treatment of the causative lesion, with neurosurgical clipping or endovascular coiling of a posterior communicating artery aneurysm","Emergency management of other compressive causes such as tumour or uncal herniation with raised intracranial pressure","Symptomatic relief of diplopia with temporary occlusion or prisms during recovery, and ocular lubrication if lid closure is impaired","Strabismus and ptosis surgery for stable residual deviation once spontaneous recovery has plateaued","Urgent escalation of any painful, pupil-involving third nerve palsy as a neurosurgical emergency","Multidisciplinary management involving neurosurgery, neurology and ophthalmology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"oculopalatal_tremor":{"name":"Oculopalatal tremor","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["symptomatic palatal tremor","oculopalatal myoclonus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology / movement disorder literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A symptomatic tremor combining rhythmic palatal movement with a synchronous pendular, often vertical, nystagmus","It develops months after a lesion in the brainstem or cerebellum affecting the triangle of Guillain and Mollaret","The delay to onset corresponds to the development of hypertrophic degeneration of the inferior olive","The pendular nystagmus causes oscillopsia and is frequently the most disabling symptom for vision","Unlike essential palatal tremor, it typically persists during sleep and is not primarily associated with an ear click","MRI characteristically shows enlargement and increased signal of the inferior olive"],"pathophysiology":"Oculopalatal tremor arises after a lesion interrupts the dentato-rubro-olivary pathway (the Guillain-Mollaret triangle), leading to transsynaptic hypertrophic degeneration of the inferior olivary nucleus. The abnormal, hypertrophied olive develops rhythmic oscillatory activity that drives synchronous rhythmic movements of the palate and the eyes, producing pendular nystagmus. The characteristic delay of months between the causative lesion and symptom onset reflects the time course of olivary hypertrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Essential palatal tremor","Other acquired pendular nystagmus (for example demyelinating)","See-saw nystagmus","Downbeat or other central nystagmus","Brainstem or cerebellar tremor syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brainstem showing hypertrophic olivary degeneration","Examination of the palate and eyes for synchronous rhythmic movement","History of a preceding brainstem or cerebellar lesion","Neuro-ophthalmological assessment of the nystagmus and oscillopsia","Investigation of the underlying causative lesion if not already known"],"redFlags":["Disabling oscillopsia impairing vision","New or progressive brainstem or cerebellar signs","An undiagnosed underlying lesion requiring evaluation","Features suggesting an alternative progressive disorder"],"prognosis":"The tremor and pendular nystagmus tend to persist and are often difficult to treat; some patients gain partial benefit from medication for the nystagmus, but the underlying olivary change is irreversible.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with benign essential palatal tremor despite the nystagmus and persistence in sleep","Overlooking the typical delayed onset after a brainstem lesion","Missing hypertrophic olivary degeneration on imaging","Underestimating the visual disability from oscillopsia"],"management":["Pharmacological treatment of the disabling pendular nystagmus and oscillopsia, with gabapentin or memantine as best-supported agents and clonazepam as an alternative","Optical measures for oscillopsia, and consideration of botulinum toxin for troublesome symptomatic nystagmus or palatal movement","Identification, treatment and monitoring of the underlying brainstem or cerebellar lesion","Low-vision support and rehabilitation for persistent oscillopsia","Neurology and neuro-ophthalmology follow-up with realistic counselling, since the hypertrophic olivary change is irreversible and response is often only partial"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"oculopharyngeal_muscular_dystrophy":{"name":"Oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OPMD","PABPN1-related dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Late-onset muscular dystrophy, usually autosomal dominant, caused by a short GCN trinucleotide repeat expansion in PABPN1 encoding poly(A)-binding protein nuclear 1","Typically presents in mid-to-late adulthood with progressive ptosis and dysphagia as the cardinal features","Ptosis is usually bilateral and slowly progressive, and patients often adopt a head-tilt-back posture to see beneath the drooping lids","Dysphagia progresses over time and predisposes to aspiration, weight loss, and malnutrition, which are the main sources of morbidity","Proximal limb-girdle weakness, particularly of the pelvic girdle, develops later in the disease course","External ophthalmoplegia may occur but is usually mild and rarely causes troublesome diplopia","Diagnosis is confirmed by targeted repeat-expansion testing of PABPN1, supported by the characteristic clinical picture","Management is supportive, with swallowing assessment and dietary modification, aspiration precautions, and surgical options such as ptosis correction and cricopharyngeal intervention for selected patients"],"pathophysiology":"Oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy is caused by a modest expansion of a GCN repeat in PABPN1, which lengthens the polyalanine tract of the poly(A)-binding protein nuclear 1. The mutant protein misfolds and aggregates into intranuclear inclusions within muscle-fibre nuclei, disturbing RNA processing and polyadenylation. The selective vulnerability of the levator palpebrae, pharyngeal, and later proximal limb muscles produces the characteristic combination of progressive ptosis, dysphagia, and eventual limb-girdle weakness. Aggregate accumulation over decades accounts for the late onset and slow progression.","additionalDifferentials":["Myasthenia gravis","Mitochondrial disease with chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia","Myotonic dystrophy","Oculopharyngodistal myopathy","Thyroid eye disease causing ptosis or ophthalmoplegia","Motor neurone disease with bulbar involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted repeat-expansion genetic testing of PABPN1","Videofluoroscopic or instrumental swallowing assessment","Electromyography showing a myopathic pattern","Serum creatine kinase, which is normal or mildly elevated","Muscle biopsy with characteristic intranuclear inclusions in selected cases","Nutritional assessment and monitoring of weight"],"redFlags":["Recurrent aspiration or aspiration pneumonia from progressive dysphagia","Significant weight loss or malnutrition","Choking episodes indicating unsafe swallowing","Ptosis obscuring vision and impairing safe mobility","Rapidly progressive weakness suggesting an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Progression is slow and life expectancy is often near normal, but dysphagia-related complications such as aspiration and malnutrition are the principal threats to health. Proactive swallowing management and timely surgical intervention improve quality of life and reduce aspiration risk.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the ptosis and ophthalmoplegia for myasthenia gravis and giving inappropriate treatment","Underestimating the aspiration risk from progressive dysphagia","Overlooking the family history given the autosomal dominant late-onset pattern","Neglecting nutritional assessment as swallowing deteriorates","Failing to consider the diagnosis in older adults presenting with isolated ptosis"],"management":["No disease-modifying drug exists; management is supportive and directed principally at the ptosis and dysphagia","Ptosis corrected surgically with blepharoplasty or frontalis-sling suspension to restore the visual field and relieve the compensatory head-tilt-back posture","Dysphagia managed with swallowing rehabilitation, texture-modified diet and cricopharyngeal myotomy or dilatation in selected patients","Nutritional support with dietetic input, escalating to gastrostomy feeding when aspiration or malnutrition becomes severe","Aspiration prevention through instrumental swallowing assessment, upright feeding, chest physiotherapy and prompt treatment of respiratory infection","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and mobility aids for the later proximal limb-girdle weakness, with periodic weight and swallowing surveillance","Multidisciplinary neuromuscular care coordinating neurology, ENT/OMFS, speech and language therapy, dietetics and ophthalmology","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant PABPN1 disorder, with predictive testing and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"oculopharyngodistal_myopathy":{"name":"Oculopharyngodistal Myopathy","system":"Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OPDM"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by a cardinal combination of progressive external ophthalmoplegia with ptosis, pharyngeal weakness causing dysphagia and dysarthria, and distal limb weakness.","Distal weakness (finger and wrist extensors, tibialis anterior with foot drop) is the key feature separating it from oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy, which is proximal or limb-girdle in pattern.","Facial weakness is common and adds to the difficulty with speech and eye closure.","Onset is usually in early-to-mid adulthood with slow progression.","Disease linked to NOTCH2NLC overlaps with neuronal intranuclear inclusion disease, so look for tremor, cognitive change, peripheral neuropathy or leukoencephalopathy.","Creatine kinase is normal or only mildly elevated.","Muscle biopsy characteristically shows rimmed vacuoles and intranuclear inclusions that stain for p62 and ubiquitin.","Inheritance is usually autosomal dominant, though apparently sporadic cases occur."],"pathophysiology":"Oculopharyngodistal myopathy is a rare, genetically heterogeneous disorder caused by non-coding CGG trinucleotide repeat expansions in the 5' untranslated regions of several genes: LRP12 (OPDM1), GIPC1 (OPDM2), NOTCH2NLC (OPDM3, which overlaps mechanistically with neuronal intranuclear inclusion disease) and RILPL1 (OPDM4). Disease is thought to result from a toxic RNA gain-of-function together with repeat-associated non-AUG (RAN) translation of the expanded repeats, generating aggregation-prone polyglycine-containing proteins that form intranuclear inclusions. The resulting myopathy shows rimmed vacuoles and p62- and ubiquitin-positive intranuclear inclusions on biopsy. Although it resembles oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy (caused by a short GCN triplet expansion in PABPN1), it is distinguished clinically by prominent distal limb involvement and genetically by its distinct non-coding repeat mechanism.","additionalDifferentials":["Oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy (PABPN1)","Mitochondrial disease with chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia","Myasthenia gravis with fatigable ptosis and ophthalmoplegia","Inclusion body myositis (rimmed vacuoles with distal and quadriceps weakness)","Myotonic dystrophy","Distal myopathies such as GNE myopathy and Welander distal myopathy","Neuronal intranuclear inclusion disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repeat-expansion genetic testing for CGG expansions in LRP12, GIPC1, NOTCH2NLC and RILPL1, often requiring long-read sequencing or repeat-primed PCR.","Muscle biopsy for rimmed vacuoles and intranuclear inclusions.","Creatine kinase.","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies to confirm a myopathic process and detect any associated neuropathy.","Brain MRI, looking for leukoencephalopathy and corticomedullary diffusion-weighted hyperintensity that suggest the neuronal intranuclear inclusion disease overlap.","Repetitive nerve stimulation and acetylcholine receptor antibodies to exclude myasthenia gravis.","Videofluoroscopic swallowing assessment."],"redFlags":["Aspiration pneumonia or significant dysphagia with weight loss.","Respiratory muscle involvement or symptoms of nocturnal hypoventilation.","Central nervous system features suggesting neuronal intranuclear inclusion disease, such as encephalopathic episodes or cognitive decline.","Rapidly progressive weakness, which should prompt reconsideration of an alternative diagnosis such as inclusion body myositis."],"prognosis":"Progression is slow, typically over decades, and life expectancy may be near normal, but dysphagia-related aspiration and respiratory complications are the main threats. NOTCH2NLC-related disease carries additional neurological morbidity from its overlap with neuronal intranuclear inclusion disease.","pitfalls":["Easily misdiagnosed as oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy, myasthenia gravis or mitochondrial ophthalmoplegia.","Standard exome or panel sequencing misses the non-coding CGG repeat expansions, so dedicated repeat testing (often long-read) is required.","Rimmed vacuoles overlap with inclusion body myositis and can lead to misdiagnosis.","The NOTCH2NLC overlap with neuronal intranuclear inclusion disease and its central nervous system implications are easily overlooked.","Assuming a proximal pattern of weakness misses the distal involvement that is the key discriminator."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy is available; care is supportive, targeting the ophthalmoplegia and ptosis, dysphagia and distal weakness","Ptosis addressed with ptosis props, blepharoplasty or frontalis-sling suspension for functional visual impairment","Dysphagia and dysarthria managed with speech and language therapy-led swallowing rehabilitation, diet modification and gastrostomy where aspiration risk is high","Distal weakness and foot drop managed with ankle-foot orthoses, physiotherapy and occupational therapy, with wrist and finger-extensor splinting and adaptive aids","Respiratory surveillance with spirometry, prompt treatment of aspiration and chest infection, and non-invasive ventilation for respiratory decline","In NOTCH2NLC-related disease, monitor for the neuronal intranuclear inclusion disease overlap (tremor, cognitive change, neuropathy, leukoencephalopathy) and manage its manifestations","Multidisciplinary neuromuscular follow-up across neurology, ENT, speech and language therapy and rehabilitation","Genetic counselling and family screening for this CGG repeat-expansion disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Peer-reviewed reviews of oculopharyngodistal myopathy","Standard neuromuscular disorders textbook","Neurogenetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"odontogenic_keratocyst":{"name":"Odontogenic keratocyst","system":"Oral & Maxillofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OKC","keratocystic odontogenic tumour","primordial cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours (Odontogenic and Maxillofacial Bone Tumours) / Burket's Oral Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A developmental odontogenic cyst arising from dental lamina remnants, with a notable tendency to recur","Most often found in the posterior mandible and ascending ramus","Grows along the marrow spaces in an anteroposterior direction with relatively little bony expansion, so it can become large before detection","Radiographs show a well-defined uni- or multilocular radiolucency, sometimes associated with an unerupted tooth","Histology is characteristic: a thin, uniform parakeratinised stratified squamous epithelium with a palisaded, hyperchromatic basal layer and a corrugated luminal surface, with friable keratin filling the lumen","The high recurrence rate is attributed to the thin friable lining and to satellite (daughter) cysts within the wall","Multiple keratocysts should prompt evaluation for the naevoid basal cell carcinoma (Gorlin-Goltz) syndrome, associated with PTCH1 mutations","Management options include enucleation with adjunctive measures such as peripheral ostectomy or chemical cauterisation, or decompression and marsupialisation, with resection reserved for aggressive or recurrent disease"],"pathophysiology":"The odontogenic keratocyst develops from remnants of the dental lamina and is lined by a distinctive keratinising epithelium whose basal cells show intrinsic proliferative activity, giving it a more neoplastic-like behaviour than ordinary odontogenic cysts. Growth occurs by mural expansion and extension through cancellous bone rather than by hydrostatic pressure alone, and small satellite cysts and epithelial islands within the fibrous capsule act as sources of recurrence if left behind. Inactivation of the PTCH1 tumour-suppressor gene in the sonic hedgehog pathway underlies both sporadic lesions and the multiple keratocysts of the naevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Dentigerous cyst","Ameloblastoma, especially the unicystic variant","Radicular (periapical) cyst","Central giant cell granuloma","Odontogenic myxoma","Lateral periodontal cyst","Traumatic (simple) bone cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Panoramic and cross-sectional imaging (CT/CBCT) to define extent and the relationship to teeth and canal","Incisional or aspiration biopsy, with aspirate typically showing low soluble protein and keratin","Histopathological examination for the characteristic parakeratinised lining","Evaluation for naevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome when lesions are multiple or the patient is young, including skin, skeletal, and intracranial features","PTCH1 genetic testing and family assessment where the syndrome is suspected","Long-term radiographic follow-up for recurrence"],"redFlags":["Multiple keratocysts or a young patient, prompting a search for Gorlin-Goltz syndrome","Syndromic features such as multiple basal cell carcinomas, palmar and plantar pits, and a calcified falx cerebri","Rapid growth, pain, or paraesthesia suggesting infection or aggressive behaviour","Repeated recurrence despite treatment"],"prognosis":"Individual cysts are benign and treatable, but recurrence is common, particularly after simple enucleation, so prolonged follow-up is essential; the prognosis is influenced by the treatment method and, in syndromic patients, by the tendency to form new cysts over time.","pitfalls":["Treating with simple enucleation without adjunctive measures and incurring recurrence","Failing to recognise multiple cysts as a marker of Gorlin-Goltz syndrome","Confusing it with a dentigerous cyst and under-treating","Inadequate long-term radiographic surveillance"],"management":["Complete surgical removal is the mainstay, most often enucleation with curettage aimed at eliminating the friable lining and satellite cysts","Adjunctive treatment of the bony cavity after enucleation with Carnoy's solution or cryotherapy to destroy residual epithelium and reduce recurrence","Marsupialisation or decompression to shrink large lesions before definitive enucleation, lowering operative morbidity","En bloc or marginal resection reserved for large, aggressive or repeatedly recurrent lesions","Pre-operative panoramic and cross-sectional (CT/CBCT) imaging to define extent and relationship to teeth, canal and sinus","Prolonged radiographic surveillance for recurrence, which is common particularly after simple enucleation","Evaluation for naevoid basal cell carcinoma (Gorlin) syndrome when cysts are multiple or the patient is young, screening skin, skeletal and intracranial features","PTCH1 genetic testing, genetic counselling and family assessment where the syndrome is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours (Odontogenic and Maxillofacial Bone Tumours)","Burket's Oral Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"odontogenic_sinusitis":{"name":"Odontogenic sinusitis","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dental sinusitis","maxillary sinusitis of dental origin","odontogenic maxillary sinusitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Odontogenic sinusitis is maxillary sinus inflammation arising from a dental source and accounts for a significant minority of maxillary sinus infections","It should be suspected in unilateral maxillary sinusitis, particularly when symptoms are refractory to standard rhinological treatment","A foul smell or taste and unilateral purulent discharge are characteristic clues pointing to a dental origin","Common causes include periapical infection of upper posterior teeth, periodontal disease, oroantral communication after extraction, and displaced roots or implants","The close relationship between the roots of maxillary premolars and molars and the sinus floor explains the spread of infection","Cone-beam CT is valuable for identifying the culprit tooth and the extent of sinus involvement","Effective treatment must address the dental cause, since sinus-directed therapy alone often fails","Management may combine dental treatment or extraction, closure of any oroantral communication, and sinus care in collaboration with ENT"],"pathophysiology":"Odontogenic sinusitis develops when the integrity of the barrier between the maxillary posterior teeth and the sinus floor is breached, allowing bacteria and inflammatory products to enter the sinus. This may follow periapical or periodontal infection extending through thin or dehiscent bone, an oroantral communication created during extraction, or displacement of roots, foreign material, or implants into the sinus. The resulting mixed, often anaerobic, infection disrupts mucociliary clearance and can obstruct the ostiomeatal complex, perpetuating sinus inflammation. Because the driver is dental, the process persists until the source is eliminated.","additionalDifferentials":["Rhinogenic (non-dental) bacterial or viral sinusitis","Allergic rhinosinusitis","Fungal sinusitis","Sinonasal tumour","Oroantral fistula without active sinusitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cone-beam or conventional CT to localise the dental source and sinus disease","Thorough dental examination with pulp testing of upper posterior teeth","Assessment for oroantral communication after recent extraction","Nasal endoscopy and ENT assessment where indicated","Culture of purulent discharge in resistant or severe cases"],"redFlags":["Spread beyond the sinus with orbital swelling, visual changes, or signs of intracranial extension","Features suggesting invasive fungal sinusitis in immunocompromised or diabetic patients","Persistent unilateral disease or a mass raising suspicion of malignancy","Systemic sepsis from an odontogenic infection extending into deep spaces"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is good when the dental cause is identified and treated, with resolution of sinus symptoms; failure to address the source leads to persistent or recurrent sinusitis and occasional serious complications.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent unilateral maxillary sinusitis medically while missing the dental cause","Overlooking an oroantral communication after upper molar extraction","Failing to test the vitality of upper posterior teeth adjacent to the sinus","Neglecting displaced roots or implants within the sinus as a persistent nidus"],"management":["Eliminate the dental source as the decisive step: extraction or endodontic (root-canal) treatment of the culprit tooth, or removal of displaced roots, implants or foreign material from the sinus","Surgical repair of any oroantral communication","Antibiotic therapy directed at the mixed aerobic-anaerobic flora, such as amoxicillin-clavulanate or a penicillin combined with metronidazole","Adjunctive nasal measures to restore drainage: saline irrigation, topical decongestants and intranasal corticosteroids","Endoscopic sinus surgery for persistent sinus disease or ostiomeatal obstruction after source control, with ENT input","Culture of purulent discharge to guide antibiotic choice in resistant or severe cases","Multidisciplinary dental/OMFS and ENT co-management, with vigilance for and escalation of orbital or intracranial spread"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"oesophageal_hypomotility":{"name":"oesophageal hypomotility","system":"gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ineffective oesophageal motility","hypocontractile oesophagus","absent contractility"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Encompasses a spectrum from ineffective oesophageal motility, defined on high-resolution manometry by a high proportion of weak or failed swallows, to absent contractility where no effective peristalsis is present with normal sphincter relaxation.","Strongly associated with gastro-oesophageal reflux disease because poor peristaltic clearance prolongs acid contact time and predisposes to severe reflux and its complications.","Absent contractility with a poorly relaxing sphincter should prompt evaluation for achalasia; when relaxation is normal it may reflect an end-stage or scleroderma-type pattern.","Systemic sclerosis characteristically produces a hypomotile smooth-muscle oesophagus with a patulous, hypotensive lower sphincter, and oesophageal involvement is often an early feature of the disease.","Symptoms include dysphagia (often to solids and worse when supine), heartburn and regurgitation; some patients are asymptomatic despite marked manometric abnormality.","Management centres on aggressive acid suppression and reflux precautions rather than prokinetics, as available prokinetic agents have limited efficacy on the aperistaltic oesophagus.","Anti-reflux surgery must be approached with caution because a partial (rather than complete) fundoplication is usually preferred to avoid precipitating dysphagia in a poorly clearing oesophagus.","Screen for an underlying connective-tissue disease, diabetes mellitus, hypothyroidism or a myopathic/neuropathic process when hypomotility is severe or of recent onset."],"pathophysiology":"Oesophageal hypomotility results from failure of the smooth-muscle segment to generate contractions of adequate vigour and coordination to clear a bolus. The defect may lie in the myenteric neurons, in cholinergic excitatory signalling, or in the smooth muscle itself. In systemic sclerosis, progressive smooth-muscle atrophy and fibrosis of the distal two-thirds of the oesophagus, together with incompetence of the lower oesophageal sphincter, produce weak or absent peristalsis and severe reflux. Diabetic autonomic neuropathy, hypothyroidism, amyloidosis and certain medications can produce similar hypocontractility. The common physiological consequence is impaired bolus transit and defective acid clearance, which drives reflux disease and, when severe, oesophageal retention and dysphagia.","additionalDifferentials":["achalasia (particularly when sphincter relaxation is impaired)","systemic sclerosis and other connective-tissue disease oesophagopathy","gastro-oesophageal reflux disease with secondary peristaltic dysfunction","diabetic or other autonomic neuropathy affecting the oesophagus","hypothyroidism-related dysmotility","medication or opioid-induced hypomotility","oesophageal involvement by amyloidosis or a myopathic process"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["high-resolution oesophageal manometry with bolus-transit or impedance assessment","upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with biopsies to exclude mucosal disease and eosinophilic oesophagitis","ambulatory pH-impedance monitoring to quantify reflux burden","timed barium oesophagram assessing clearance and emptying","connective-tissue disease serology (antinuclear and scleroderma-associated antibodies)","thyroid function tests and glycaemic assessment","gastric emptying study when a diffuse gut dysmotility is suspected"],"redFlags":["progressive dysphagia with weight loss suggesting stricture, malignancy or evolving achalasia","features of systemic sclerosis such as Raynaud phenomenon, skin tightening or telangiectasia","recurrent aspiration or nocturnal respiratory symptoms from reflux and retention","severe erosive oesophagitis, ulceration or peptic stricture on endoscopy","anaemia or gastrointestinal bleeding"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause: idiopathic ineffective motility is often stable and managed by reflux control, whereas the hypomotility of systemic sclerosis tends to be progressive and predisposes to severe reflux disease, stricture and Barrett's oesophagus. Absent contractility is generally irreversible, so treatment aims to control reflux and preserve nutrition rather than restore peristalsis.","pitfalls":["Relying on prokinetic drugs, which rarely restore effective peristalsis in a severely hypomotile oesophagus.","Performing a complete (360-degree) fundoplication in a poorly clearing oesophagus and thereby causing intractable dysphagia.","Missing an underlying connective-tissue disease, especially systemic sclerosis, in a patient presenting with reflux and dysphagia.","Failing to distinguish absent contractility with impaired sphincter relaxation (achalasia) from that with normal relaxation.","Underestimating reflux burden and its complications because peristaltic failure prolongs acid exposure."],"management":["Aggressive acid suppression with proton pump inhibitors as the mainstay, since impaired peristaltic clearance prolongs acid contact time","Reflux precautions: weight loss, head-of-bed elevation, avoiding late or large meals and reflux-provoking foods, and remaining upright after eating","Dysphagia measures such as eating upright, thorough chewing and taking fluids with meals; prokinetic agents have limited benefit in the aperistaltic oesophagus","Treat the underlying cause — systemic sclerosis, hypothyroidism or diabetic autonomic neuropathy — and review medications that impair motility","Evaluate absent contractility with a poorly relaxing sphincter for achalasia before attributing symptoms to hypomotility","Surveillance for reflux complications, with endoscopic dilatation for stricture and monitoring for Barrett's oesophagus","Multidisciplinary gastroenterology and rheumatology care, with caution over anti-reflux surgery given the risk of worsening dysphagia in aperistalsis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease, current edition","Chicago Classification of Oesophageal Motility Disorders (current version)","ACG Clinical Guideline on the diagnosis and management of gastro-oesophageal reflux disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"oesophageal_web":{"name":"oesophageal web","system":"gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["esophageal web","cervical oesophageal web","Plummer-Vinson web"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An oesophageal web is a thin, eccentric mucosal membrane composed of squamous epithelium and lamina propria, typically arising from the anterior wall of the cervical (upper) oesophagus just below the cricopharyngeus.","Most webs are asymptomatic incidental findings; symptomatic webs cause intermittent, non-progressive solid-food dysphagia because they only partially occlude the lumen.","The classic association is Plummer-Vinson (Paterson-Brown-Kelly) syndrome: a cervical web with iron-deficiency anaemia and dysphagia, seen predominantly in middle-aged women.","Barium swallow with careful lateral views of the cervical oesophagus is more sensitive than endoscopy, which can rupture a thin web before it is recognised.","Webs differ from Schatzki rings by location (cervical versus distal), circumferentiality (webs are usually partial and eccentric, rings circumferential) and histology (webs are squamous-lined only).","Simple mechanical disruption during endoscopy or through-the-scope balloon or bougie dilatation is usually curative, and correction of underlying iron deficiency should accompany treatment in Plummer-Vinson syndrome.","Plummer-Vinson syndrome carries an increased long-term risk of post-cricoid and upper oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma, warranting surveillance awareness.","Multiple or lower webs, or webs with mucosal fragility, should raise suspicion of an associated dermatological or immune disorder such as epidermolysis bullosa or a mucocutaneous blistering condition."],"pathophysiology":"An oesophageal web is a protrusion of normal mucosa (stratified squamous epithelium over vascular connective tissue of the lamina propria) into the lumen, without a muscular component, producing a shelf-like partial obstruction. In the cervical oesophagus the aetiology is often idiopathic but is strongly linked to chronic iron deficiency, where impaired iron-dependent oxidative enzyme activity in rapidly regenerating mucosal cells is thought to promote atrophy and abnormal epithelial healing with web formation. Webs may also be congenital, or acquired secondary to chronic mucosal injury from gastro-oesophageal reflux, eosinophilic oesophagitis, graft-versus-host disease, mucocutaneous blistering disorders (epidermolysis bullosa, cicatricial pemphigoid, lichen planus) or prior caustic or radiation injury. The partial luminal narrowing impedes passage of a solid bolus while liquids pass freely, explaining the characteristic intermittent solid dysphagia.","additionalDifferentials":["Schatzki ring (distal oesophageal mucosal ring)","peptic stricture from gastro-oesophageal reflux","eosinophilic oesophagitis with rings or strictures","cricopharyngeal bar or dysfunction","extrinsic compression (cervical osteophyte, aberrant vessel, thyroid mass)","oesophageal carcinoma or post-cricoid malignancy","oropharyngeal (transfer) dysphagia of neuromuscular origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["barium swallow with dedicated lateral and anteroposterior cervical oesophageal views","full blood count with iron studies (ferritin, transferrin saturation) to detect iron-deficiency anaemia","upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with careful inspection of the cervical oesophagus","oesophageal biopsies where eosinophilic oesophagitis or a mucocutaneous disorder is suspected","high-resolution oesophageal manometry if a motility disorder is considered","evaluation for occult blood loss and coeliac disease when iron deficiency is unexplained"],"redFlags":["progressive rather than intermittent dysphagia, suggesting stricture or malignancy","weight loss, odynophagia or anorexia raising concern for carcinoma","haematemesis or evidence of significant gastrointestinal blood loss","a neck mass or hoarseness suggesting post-cricoid or laryngeal malignancy","recurrent aspiration or an inability to manage secretions"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent for isolated webs, which usually resolve with simple dilatation and correction of iron deficiency, though recurrence can occur. Patients with Plummer-Vinson syndrome require awareness of a heightened long-term risk of upper aerodigestive squamous cell carcinoma.","pitfalls":["Missing a thin cervical web on endoscopy because it is inadvertently disrupted or lies just below the upper sphincter before careful inspection.","Failing to investigate and correct underlying iron deficiency, so that dysphagia recurs.","Attributing all dysphagia to the web without excluding a coexisting motility disorder or malignancy.","Overlooking the malignancy risk in Plummer-Vinson syndrome and omitting appropriate follow-up.","Confusing a web with a Schatzki ring and mislocating or mistreating the lesion."],"management":["Mechanical disruption of the web by endoscopic through-the-scope balloon or bougie dilatation, which is usually curative","Correct underlying iron deficiency with iron replacement, central to Plummer-Vinson (Paterson-Brown-Kelly) syndrome","Investigate and treat the source of iron deficiency, including occult gastrointestinal blood loss and coeliac disease","Treat any contributing mucosal disease — reflux with acid suppression, eosinophilic oesophagitis, or a mucocutaneous blistering disorder","Dietary advice for dysphagia with careful chewing and adequate fluids around dilatation","Follow-up for symptom recurrence, which may require repeat dilatation","In Plummer-Vinson syndrome, maintain awareness of the heightened long-term risk of upper aerodigestive squamous cell carcinoma with appropriate surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease, current edition","Yamada's Textbook of Gastroenterology, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"oesophagostomiasis":{"name":"Oesophagostomiasis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Oesophagostomum infection","esophagostomiasis","nodular worm disease","Dapaong tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / Mandell","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by Oesophagostomum species, nodular worms of the large bowel, with human disease concentrated in parts of West Africa, notably northern Togo and Ghana","Acquired by ingesting infective larvae from soil or contaminated food and water, the larvae burrowing into the intestinal wall","The hallmark is formation of inflammatory nodules or abscesses in the colonic wall, which may coalesce into a palpable abdominal mass","The classic uninodular presentation, the Dapaong tumour, is a painful abdominal wall or intra-abdominal mass, while multinodular disease causes diffuse colitis-like symptoms","Presentations include abdominal pain, diarrhoea, weight loss and a palpable mass, often with marked eosinophilia","Diagnosis relies on imaging showing bowel wall nodules and on histology of excised or biopsied lesions; larvae may not appear in the stool","Treatment is with a benzimidazole anthelmintic such as albendazole, with surgical drainage or excision for large nodules or abscesses"],"pathophysiology":"Oesophagostomum species are nematodes related to hookworms whose natural hosts include primates, pigs and ruminants. Humans acquire infection by ingesting infective larvae from contaminated soil, food or water. In the intestine the larvae invade the wall of the colon and terminal ileum, where they encyst and provoke an intense inflammatory reaction. The host response forms characteristic granulomatous nodules and abscesses around the developing larvae, which may remain solitary as a large tumour-like mass or occur as multiple smaller nodules producing a colitis-like picture. Some larvae complete development, emerge into the lumen and mature into adult worms in the large bowel, while others remain trapped and die within the nodules. The inflammatory nodules account for the palpable masses, abdominal pain and, when they erupt or become secondarily infected, for abscess formation and local complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Intestinal tuberculosis with an ileocaecal mass","Crohn's disease and other inflammatory bowel disease","Colonic or caecal carcinoma","Amoeboma or other infective mass lesion","Appendicular or other intra-abdominal abscess","Other tissue-invasive helminth infection with eosinophilia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cross-sectional imaging or ultrasound demonstrating bowel wall nodules or an abdominal mass","Full blood count showing marked eosinophilia","Histopathology of excised or biopsied nodules identifying the larvae","Stool microscopy, recognising that eggs and larvae may be absent","Colonoscopy with biopsy when malignancy or inflammatory bowel disease is considered","Inflammatory markers and assessment for abscess formation"],"redFlags":["A large or enlarging abdominal mass raising concern for malignancy or abscess","Signs of intestinal obstruction or perforation","Systemic sepsis from a secondarily infected nodule","Persistent symptoms after treatment prompting reassessment"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally good with anthelmintic treatment and, where needed, surgical management of nodules; complications such as large abscesses or obstruction increase morbidity but are usually treatable.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a nodular mass for colonic carcinoma, tuberculosis or Crohn's disease","Relying on stool examination, which is frequently negative in tissue-dwelling disease","Overlooking the diagnosis in travellers or residents from endemic West African foci","Failing to drain or excise large abscesses in addition to giving anthelmintic therapy"],"management":["Anthelmintic therapy with a benzimidazole such as albendazole or mebendazole as the mainstay, with pyrantel pamoate as an alternative","Surgical excision or drainage of symptomatic nodules, abscesses (the Dapaong tumour) or an obstructing bowel mass","Supportive care for diarrhoea, abdominal pain and nutrition, with correction of associated losses","Confirm treatment response through resolution of eosinophilia and follow-up imaging of the bowel-wall nodules","Identify and treat complications such as large abscess formation or bowel obstruction","Public-health and preventive measures in endemic areas: safe food and water, sanitation and hand hygiene to prevent reinfection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"ogilvie_syndrome":{"name":"Ogilvie syndrome (acute colonic pseudo-obstruction)","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute colonic pseudo-obstruction","ACPO","colonic ileus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with massive colonic distension, abdominal pain and often reduced bowel sounds in the absence of a mechanical obstructing lesion","Typically occurs in hospitalised or postoperative patients and those with serious systemic illness, trauma or electrolyte disturbance","Imaging shows marked dilatation of the caecum and right colon, and the risk of ischaemia and perforation rises sharply as caecal diameter increases","A mechanical obstruction and toxic megacolon must be excluded before making the diagnosis, often with water-soluble contrast or CT","Initial management is supportive with correction of electrolytes, review of opioids and other contributing drugs, and bowel rest with decompression","Neostigmine or decompression for Ogilvie is used when conservative measures fail and there is no perforation or ischaemia, with colonoscopic decompression as an alternative","Serial abdominal examination and imaging are essential to detect impending caecal perforation early"],"pathophysiology":"Ogilvie syndrome is a massive dilatation of the colon without a mechanical cause, arising from an imbalance in autonomic innervation of the colon. Excess sympathetic tone and reduced parasympathetic (sacral) drive impair colonic motility, particularly in the distal colon, producing a functional obstruction and gross proximal dilatation that is most pronounced in the caecum and right colon. It is precipitated by severe illness, surgery, trauma, metabolic and electrolyte disturbances and drugs that impair motility such as opioids. As the caecum has the largest diameter and thinnest wall, rising wall tension there threatens ischaemia and perforation.","additionalDifferentials":["Mechanical colonic obstruction from tumour, stricture or volvulus","Toxic megacolon from severe colitis","Paralytic ileus involving the small bowel","Faecal impaction with proximal loading","Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction","Clostridioides difficile colitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal radiograph to assess colonic and caecal diameter","CT abdomen or water-soluble contrast enema to exclude mechanical obstruction","Electrolytes including potassium, magnesium, calcium and phosphate","Thyroid function and review of the drug chart for opioids and anticholinergics","Serial examination and imaging to monitor caecal diameter"],"redFlags":["Progressive caecal dilatation approaching diameters associated with perforation","Peritonism, fever or rising inflammatory markers suggesting ischaemia or perforation","Marked tenderness localised to the right iliac fossa over the caecum","Haemodynamic instability or rising lactate"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition and treatment most cases resolve without surgery, but delayed diagnosis complicated by caecal ischaemia or perforation carries a high mortality.","pitfalls":["Assuming mechanical obstruction and failing to consider a functional cause, or vice versa","Giving neostigmine when perforation or ischaemia is present or unexcluded","Neglecting to correct electrolyte disturbances and reduce opioids","Underestimating caecal diameter and missing impending perforation"],"management":["Supportive first-line care with bowel rest, nil by mouth and nasogastric plus rectal-tube decompression","Correct precipitating electrolyte disturbances (potassium, magnesium, calcium, phosphate) and treat the underlying illness or sepsis","Review and minimise contributing drugs, especially opioids and anticholinergics, and mobilise the patient","Exclude mechanical obstruction, toxic megacolon, ischaemia and perforation with water-soluble contrast enema or CT before pharmacological treatment","Pharmacological decompression with intravenous neostigmine when conservative measures fail, under cardiac monitoring with atropine available for bradycardia","Colonoscopic decompression for refractory cases or marked caecal dilatation","Serial examination and imaging to track caecal diameter, escalating to surgery (caecostomy or colectomy) for ischaemia or perforation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","American Society for Gastrointestinal Endoscopy guidance on acute colonic pseudo-obstruction"],"crossLinks":[]},"oguchi_disease":{"name":"Oguchi disease","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Oguchi's disease","SAG","GRK1","arrestin deficiency","congenital stationary night blindness with Mizuo-Nakamura phenomenon"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ryan's Retina / Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare autosomal recessive form of congenital stationary night blindness caused by mutations in SAG (arrestin) or GRK1 (rhodopsin kinase), genes needed to deactivate rhodopsin after light exposure","The pathognomonic sign is the Mizuo-Nakamura phenomenon: a golden-yellow or metallic sheen of the fundus in the light-adapted state that reverts to a normal colour after prolonged dark adaptation","Patients report lifelong, non-progressive night blindness with normal daytime acuity and colour vision","Dark adaptometry shows a characteristic profile in which rod sensitivity recovers only after a very prolonged period of dark adaptation","The electroretinogram typically shows an absent or markedly reduced rod b-wave under standard conditions, which can partially recover after extended dark adaptation","It should be distinguished from progressive rod-cone dystrophies, as Oguchi disease is stationary with preserved acuity","Management is supportive with reassurance about the stationary course, advice regarding night vision, and genetic counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Oguchi disease arises from recessive mutations in SAG (S-antigen/arrestin) or GRK1 (rhodopsin kinase), proteins that normally quench activated rhodopsin so that rods can recover after light exposure. Defective deactivation leaves rods persistently desensitised following illumination, producing profound but non-progressive night blindness and abnormally slow dark adaptation. The abnormal state of the rod photoreceptors and their outer segments underlies the Mizuo-Nakamura phenomenon, in which the light-adapted retina takes on a golden sheen that disappears after prolonged darkness.","additionalDifferentials":["Other forms of congenital stationary night blindness","Fundus albipunctatus","Retinitis pigmentosa and progressive rod-cone dystrophies","Vitamin A deficiency retinopathy","Retinitis punctata albescens","Enhanced S-cone syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fundus examination in light- and dark-adapted states to demonstrate the Mizuo-Nakamura phenomenon","Prolonged dark adaptometry showing delayed rod recovery","Full-field electroretinography before and after extended dark adaptation","Fundus autofluorescence and optical coherence tomography to exclude degeneration","Molecular genetic testing for SAG and GRK1 mutations","Visual field testing to confirm preservation"],"redFlags":["Documented progression of field loss or acuity decline, which is inconsistent with the diagnosis and suggests a degenerative disorder","Pigmentary retinopathy or bone-spicule change indicating retinitis pigmentosa instead","Onset of night blindness in adulthood pointing to an acquired cause","Affected siblings warranting screening given recessive inheritance"],"prognosis":"The condition is stationary and non-progressive; daytime vision remains normal throughout life and only night vision is impaired, so the overall visual prognosis is good.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the Mizuo-Nakamura phenomenon by examining the fundus only in the light-adapted state","Misdiagnosing the disorder as progressive retinitis pigmentosa","Interpreting the reduced rod electroretinogram without repeating it after prolonged dark adaptation","Confusing it with fundus albipunctatus, which shows white dots and a different mechanism"],"management":["No specific therapy exists and none is required for the retinal defect, as the condition is stationary with preserved daytime vision; reassurance is central","Counsel on lifelong non-progressive night blindness and slow dark adaptation, with practical adaptation for low-light environments and driving safety","Confirm and document the diagnosis through the Mizuo-Nakamura phenomenon, dark adaptometry and electroretinography, distinguishing it from progressive rod-cone dystrophies","Periodic ophthalmological review with optical coherence tomography and fundus autofluorescence to confirm stability and exclude co-existing degeneration","Molecular confirmation of the SAG or GRK1 mutation","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, including carrier and family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ryan's Retina","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"ohtahara_syndrome":{"name":"Ohtahara syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["early infantile epileptic encephalopathy","EIEE","early infantile developmental and epileptic encephalopathy with burst-suppression","STXBP1"],"chapter":null,"source":"ILAE classification / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Onset is very early, usually within the first weeks to three months of life, with frequent tonic spasms occurring both awake and asleep","The defining EEG hallmark is a burst-suppression pattern present in both wakefulness and sleep","Causes are heterogeneous, including structural brain malformations and pathogenic variants in genes such as STXBP1, KCNQ2, SCN2A and ARX","Development is severely impaired from onset, and seizures are typically drug-resistant","It may evolve into West syndrome and later into Lennox-Gastaut syndrome as the child grows","Structural imaging is essential because malformations of cortical development are a frequent underlying cause","Distinguishing it from early myoclonic encephalopathy rests on the predominant seizure type and the state-dependence of the EEG pattern"],"pathophysiology":"Ohtahara syndrome is an early infantile developmental and epileptic encephalopathy in which severe disruption of neuronal networks in the immature brain produces continuous epileptic activity. The underlying causes, whether structural malformations of cortical development or pathogenic variants affecting synaptic transmission and ion channels, converge on profound cortical hyperexcitability and impaired inhibition. This generates the persistent burst-suppression pattern and frequent tonic spasms, while the same widespread dysfunction arrests brain development from the outset.","additionalDifferentials":["Early myoclonic encephalopathy","West syndrome (infantile spasms)","Neonatal seizures from hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Pyridoxine-dependent and other vitamin-responsive epilepsies","Inborn errors of metabolism presenting with neonatal seizures","Malformations of cortical development"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["EEG demonstrating burst-suppression in both wakefulness and sleep","Brain MRI to detect malformations of cortical development and structural lesions","Epilepsy gene panel or exome sequencing (e.g. STXBP1, KCNQ2, SCN2A, ARX)","Metabolic workup including trials of vitamin-responsive causes such as pyridoxine","CSF studies where a metabolic or neurotransmitter disorder is suspected"],"redFlags":["Refractory tonic spasms with persistent burst-suppression in early infancy","Failure to respond to pyridoxine trial when a vitamin-responsive cause is possible","Evidence of a progressive metabolic disorder","Rapid developmental arrest from birth"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally very poor, with high early mortality, severe intellectual and motor disability in survivors, and frequent evolution to West and then Lennox-Gastaut syndrome. Outcome is strongly influenced by the underlying cause.","pitfalls":["Failing to trial vitamin-responsive treatments before concluding refractoriness","Overlooking a subtle malformation of cortical development on early imaging","Confusing it with early myoclonic encephalopathy by not characterising the predominant seizure type","Not pursuing genetic testing, which increasingly identifies a specific cause"],"management":["Treat any identifiable underlying cause, including a trial of pyridoxine, pyridoxal phosphate and folinic acid for vitamin-responsive seizures and targeted therapy where a metabolic cause is found","Antiseizure medication to reduce seizure burden, such as phenobarbital, vigabatrin, sodium valproate or zonisamide, acknowledging frequent drug resistance","Tailor treatment to the genetic cause, for example sodium-channel blockers such as carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine in KCNQ2-related disease","Consider the ketogenic diet, and resective epilepsy surgery where a focal structural lesion is identified","Adrenocorticotropic hormone or corticosteroids if the disorder evolves into West syndrome with epileptic spasms","Supportive and developmental care: feeding and respiratory support, physiotherapy and management of severe neurodevelopmental impairment","Multidisciplinary paediatric neurology, metabolic and genetics input with early palliative care and family support given the poor prognosis","Genetic counselling and gene testing (STXBP1, KCNQ2, SCN2A, ARX) to define recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ILAE classification of the epilepsies","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"oleander_poisoning":{"name":"Oleander poisoning","system":"Cardiovascular / Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Nerium oleander poisoning","Yellow oleander poisoning","Thevetia poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["All parts of common oleander (Nerium oleander) and yellow oleander (Cascabela/Thevetia peruviana) contain cardiac glycosides such as oleandrin and thevetin that act like digoxin.","Yellow oleander seed ingestion is a common means of deliberate self-poisoning in parts of South Asia and can cause fatal cardiotoxicity.","Clinical picture combines gastrointestinal upset (nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain) with cardiac glycoside toxicity: bradyarrhythmias, varying degrees of heart block and ventricular ectopy.","Hyperkalaemia from inhibition of the sodium-potassium ATPase is a marker of severe acute poisoning and correlates with poor outcome, mirroring acute digoxin toxicity.","The glycosides inhibit the myocardial sodium-potassium pump, raising intracellular calcium and increasing vagal tone, which enhances automaticity while depressing conduction.","Smoke from burning the plant and even honey or water contaminated by it can transmit toxin; boiling and cooking do not render the plant safe.","Digoxin-specific antibody fragments can bind oleander glycosides and are the definitive treatment for life-threatening arrhythmia or hyperkalaemia, though larger amounts may be needed due to cross-reactivity differences.","Standard serum digoxin immunoassays may cross-react and give a positive but unreliable quantitative result, useful qualitatively to support the diagnosis."],"pathophysiology":"Oleander poisoning is caused by cardenolide cardiac glycosides, notably oleandrin in Nerium oleander and thevetin and related glycosides in yellow oleander, which are present throughout the plant including leaves, flowers, sap and seeds. Like digoxin these glycosides inhibit the membrane sodium-potassium ATPase of cardiac and other cells. Inhibition of the pump raises intracellular sodium, which reduces the driving force for the sodium-calcium exchanger and thereby increases intracellular calcium, augmenting myocardial contractility but also promoting delayed afterdepolarisations and increased automaticity. Simultaneously the glycosides enhance vagal tone and slow conduction through the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes, producing the characteristic combination of increased ventricular ectopy with bradyarrhythmias and atrioventricular block. In acute poisoning, widespread inhibition of the sodium-potassium pump in skeletal muscle and other tissues causes efflux of potassium into the plasma, so that rising serum potassium reflects the severity of toxin effect and predicts adverse outcome. Gastrointestinal glycoside effects and mucosal irritation account for the early vomiting and diarrhoea. Because the toxins are heat-stable, cooking, boiling or burning does not eliminate the hazard.","additionalDifferentials":["Digoxin or other medicinal cardiac glycoside toxicity","Other cardiac-glycoside-containing plants such as foxglove (Digitalis) or lily of the valley","Aconite poisoning causing ventricular arrhythmia","Beta-blocker or calcium-channel-blocker overdose with bradycardia","Primary cardiac conduction disease or ischaemia","Hyperkalaemia from renal failure or other cause","Acute gastroenteritis with electrolyte disturbance"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous cardiac monitoring and serial 12-lead ECGs for bradyarrhythmia, heart block and ectopy","Serum potassium and other electrolytes, with potassium as a key severity marker","Renal function and acid-base assessment","Serum digoxin immunoassay used qualitatively given cross-reactivity with oleander glycosides","Magnesium and calcium levels to guide arrhythmia management","Blood glucose and basic metabolic screen to exclude mimics","Identification of the plant, seeds or preparation ingested"],"redFlags":["Bradyarrhythmia or high-grade atrioventricular block","Rising serum potassium indicating severe glycoside effect","Ventricular ectopy or ventricular tachyarrhythmia","Haemodynamic instability or syncope","Deliberate ingestion of yellow oleander seeds"],"prognosis":"Many patients recover with monitoring and supportive care, but poisoning complicated by marked hyperkalaemia, high-grade heart block or ventricular arrhythmia carries a substantial mortality. Timely use of digoxin-specific antibody fragments and cardiac support improves survival in severe cases.","pitfalls":["Relying on the quantitative serum digoxin level to gauge severity, as immunoassay cross-reactivity is only approximate","Giving calcium for hyperkalaemia as one might in other settings, which is generally avoided in cardiac glycoside toxicity","Assuming boiling, cooking or burning detoxifies the plant","Overlooking contaminated honey, water or smoke as sources of exposure","Failing to anticipate deterioration in an initially stable patient after seed ingestion"],"management":["Digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Fab) are the definitive antidote for significant cardiac glycoside toxicity","Gastrointestinal decontamination with activated charcoal in early presentation, with multiple-dose charcoal considered","Continuous cardiac monitoring, treating bradyarrhythmia and heart block with atropine and temporary pacing as required","Manage hyperkalaemia, a marker of severe poisoning, while avoiding intravenous calcium given the risk in glycoside toxicity, and treat ventricular arrhythmia with agents such as lidocaine or magnesium","Correct electrolytes and monitor potassium, magnesium, renal function and acid-base status","Supportive care for gastrointestinal losses and fluid balance","Psychiatric assessment after deliberate self-poisoning, and critical-care escalation for high-grade block, refractory arrhythmia or marked hyperkalaemia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical toxicology textbooks","Plant poisoning and cardiac glycoside reference materials","Emergency medicine and cardiology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"olecranon_bursitis":{"name":"Olecranon bursitis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["student's elbow","miner's elbow","elbow bursitis","olecranon bursopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Swelling over the point of the elbow from inflammation of the superficial olecranon bursa","Causes include repetitive pressure and friction, direct trauma, crystal arthropathy such as gout, and infection","The key clinical task is distinguishing septic from aseptic bursitis","Warmth, marked erythema, fever, disproportionate pain and an overlying skin breach favour a septic bursa","Aspiration with fluid sent for cell count, Gram stain, culture and crystal microscopy is central when infection is suspected","Aseptic bursitis is generally managed conservatively with avoidance of pressure, and aspiration if symptomatic","Corticosteroid injection is reserved for stubborn aseptic cases and avoided when infection is possible because of complication risk","Septic bursitis requires antibiotics and sometimes drainage"],"pathophysiology":"The olecranon bursa is a superficial sac over the extensor aspect of the elbow that reduces friction between the skin and the olecranon. Repetitive pressure or leaning on the elbow, a direct blow, or systemic conditions such as gout and rheumatoid arthritis can provoke bursal inflammation and effusion. Because it is superficial, the bursa is prone to becoming infected, commonly through a breach in the overlying skin and most often with skin flora such as staphylococci. Distinguishing this septic process from aseptic inflammation is the central diagnostic challenge.","additionalDifferentials":["Septic olecranon bursitis","Gout or pseudogout","Rheumatoid nodule or rheumatoid arthritis","Olecranon fracture or other elbow trauma","Cellulitis of the elbow","Triceps insertional tendinopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bursal aspiration with fluid cell count, Gram stain and culture","Polarised light microscopy of the aspirate for crystals","Inflammatory markers, with blood cultures if systemically unwell","Serum urate where gout is suspected, recognising its limits in the acute phase","Plain radiographs if fracture, foreign body or bony spur is suspected","Ultrasound to characterise the fluid collection"],"redFlags":["Fever, spreading erythema and disproportionate pain suggesting septic bursitis or cellulitis","An overlying skin breach or immunosuppression increasing infection risk","Features of systemic sepsis","Failure to settle, raising suspicion of infection or underlying disease"],"prognosis":"Aseptic bursitis usually settles with conservative care, although it can recur with continued pressure; septic bursitis generally resolves with prompt antibiotics and drainage but may need repeated aspiration.","pitfalls":["Injecting corticosteroid into a bursa that may be infected","Failing to aspirate and analyse fluid when infection is possible","Missing gout as an underlying cause","Attributing a fracture-related swelling to simple bursitis"],"management":["Distinguish septic from aseptic bursitis by bursal aspiration for cell count, Gram stain, culture and crystal microscopy to direct treatment","Septic bursitis treated with antistaphylococcal antibiotics such as flucloxacillin covering skin flora, together with drainage or aspiration, and incision and drainage or bursectomy for refractory cases","Aseptic bursitis managed conservatively with avoidance of pressure and leaning, elbow padding, rest, ice and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","Aspiration of symptomatic aseptic effusions, generally avoiding corticosteroid injection especially where infection cannot be excluded","Treat the underlying cause, with urate-lowering and anti-inflammatory therapy for gout and disease control for rheumatoid arthritis","Follow-up for recurrence with continued pressure, reserving bursectomy for recurrent or chronic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","British Journal of Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"oligoarticular_juvenile_idiopathic_arthritis":{"name":"Oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis","system":"Rheumatology / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oligoarticular JIA","oligo JIA","pauciarticular JIA","persistent and extended oligoarthritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology / ILAR & PRINTO criteria","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined under the ILAR framework as arthritis affecting four or fewer joints in the first six months, subclassified into persistent (never more than four joints) and extended (spreading to five or more thereafter) forms","Typically affects young girls, often with a positive antinuclear antibody, and commonly involves large joints such as the knee and ankle asymmetrically","The chief long-term threat is chronic anterior uveitis, which is frequently insidious and asymptomatic (white eye) yet sight-threatening","Regular slit-lamp surveillance is essential, with the highest uveitis risk in young, antinuclear-antibody-positive girls with early disease onset","A swollen, warm but often surprisingly non-tender knee with a limp and morning stiffness is a common presentation","Limb-length discrepancy and localised overgrowth can occur because chronic inflammation accelerates growth at the affected joint","First-line treatment is NSAIDs and intra-articular corticosteroids, with methotrexate reserved for persistent or extended disease and uveitis"],"pathophysiology":"Oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis is a chronic, immune-mediated synovitis in which T-cell-driven inflammation of the synovial membrane produces effusion, pannus formation and, if untreated, cartilage and bone damage. The strong association with antinuclear antibody positivity and chronic anterior uveitis suggests shared autoreactivity between synovial and uveal tissue. Chronic hyperaemia around an inflamed joint can stimulate the adjacent growth plate, producing localised overgrowth and limb-length inequality, while unchecked synovitis eventually causes erosive joint damage in the extended subtype.","additionalDifferentials":["Septic arthritis and osteomyelitis","Reactive or post-viral arthritis","Lyme arthritis in endemic areas","Haemarthrosis or trauma","Malignancy (leukaemia, neuroblastoma) causing joint or bone pain","Other JIA subtypes including enthesitis-related arthritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antinuclear antibody to stratify uveitis risk","Inflammatory markers (erythrocyte sedimentation rate, C-reactive protein), which may be normal or only mildly raised","Joint ultrasound or MRI to confirm synovitis and assess disease extent","Slit-lamp ophthalmological examination at diagnosis and on a scheduled surveillance basis","Joint aspiration and synovial fluid analysis when infection is a concern","Full blood count and film to help exclude malignancy","Rheumatoid factor and HLA-B27 to help distinguish other subtypes"],"redFlags":["Painless progressive visual loss or band keratopathy indicating chronic uveitis complications","Fever, severe pain or systemic upset suggesting infection or malignancy","Nocturnal bone pain and cytopenias raising concern for leukaemia","Rapid spread to many joints suggesting extended or polyarticular evolution"],"prognosis":"The persistent form often has a favourable articular outcome, whereas the extended form carries a higher risk of erosive joint damage and disability. Uveitis, if undetected, can cause cataract, glaucoma and permanent visual loss, making ophthalmic surveillance the key determinant of long-term outcome.","pitfalls":["Relying on the child to report eye symptoms, when sight-threatening uveitis is frequently asymptomatic","Assuming normal inflammatory markers exclude active arthritis","Missing limb-length discrepancy and consequent gait or postural problems","Failing to reassess for extension of joint involvement over time","Overlooking infection or malignancy in an atypical monoarthritis"],"management":["Intra-articular corticosteroid injection is the mainstay for active oligoarthritis, combined with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for symptom relief","Escalate to methotrexate for persistent or extended disease, and to biologic agents such as tumour necrosis factor inhibitors for refractory arthritis or uveitis","Scheduled slit-lamp surveillance for chronic anterior uveitis stratified by antinuclear-antibody status and age, treated with topical corticosteroids and, if needed, methotrexate or a tumour necrosis factor inhibitor","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to preserve joint function, muscle strength and range of movement","Monitor and manage limb-length discrepancy and localised overgrowth with orthotics and orthopaedic input","Multidisciplinary paediatric rheumatology and ophthalmology care with disease-modifying drug safety monitoring"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology","ILAR classification of juvenile idiopathic arthritis","PRINTO/PReS uveitis screening guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"oligodendroglioma":{"name":"oligodendroglioma","system":"nervous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oligodendroglial tumour","IDH-mutant 1p/19q-codeleted glioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Diagnosis now requires both an IDH1 or IDH2 mutation and whole-arm 1p/19q codeletion; without codeletion the tumour is not an oligodendroglioma regardless of morphology.","It is a tumour of adults with a predilection for the cerebral cortex and subcortical white matter, most often frontal.","Seizures are the commonest presenting feature and may precede diagnosis by years, reflecting the tumour's cortical, slow-growing nature.","Calcification is frequent and is well seen on CT, an unusual and helpful feature among diffuse gliomas.","Classic histology shows monotonous cells with perinuclear haloes, the fried-egg artefact, and a delicate chicken-wire capillary network.","The 1p/19q codeletion predicts sensitivity to alkylating chemotherapy and to combined chemoradiotherapy, underpinning a relatively favourable outcome.","Graded as CNS WHO grade 2 or grade 3, with grade 3 showing brisk mitoses, microvascular proliferation or necrosis, and CDKN2A homozygous deletion portends worse behaviour.","Even grade 3 oligodendroglioma generally outperforms IDH-mutant astrocytoma of comparable grade."],"pathophysiology":"Oligodendroglioma is a diffusely infiltrating glioma defined molecularly by an early IDH1 or IDH2 mutation together with combined whole-arm loss of chromosomes 1p and 19q, typically arising from an unbalanced translocation. The mutant isocitrate dehydrogenase produces the oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate, which inhibits alpha-ketoglutarate-dependent dioxygenases and drives a hypermethylator phenotype that reprogrammes gene expression. TERT promoter mutation and, frequently, CIC and FUBP1 mutations accompany the codeletion. The resulting neoplastic cells infiltrate cortex and white matter while retaining an indolent tempo, and higher-grade transformation is marked by increased proliferation, microvascular proliferation, necrosis and CDKN2A loss.","additionalDifferentials":["IDH-mutant astrocytoma","glioblastoma, IDH-wildtype","clear cell ependymoma","central neurocytoma","dysembryoplastic neuroepithelial tumour","small cell glioblastoma","cerebral metastasis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["contrast-enhanced MRI of the brain with perfusion and spectroscopy","CT to demonstrate calcification","IDH1 and IDH2 mutation testing","1p/19q codeletion analysis to confirm the diagnosis","TERT promoter and CDKN2A status","tissue diagnosis by resection or stereotactic biopsy","neuropsychological and seizure assessment at baseline"],"redFlags":["clinical or radiological signs of raised intracranial pressure","new or progressive focal neurological deficit","status epilepticus or escalating seizure frequency","new nodular enhancement suggesting higher-grade transformation"],"prognosis":"Among diffuse gliomas the outlook is relatively favourable, with prolonged survival particularly in grade 2 disease, and the 1p/19q codeletion predicts chemosensitivity; nonetheless the tumour remains incurable and eventually progresses.","pitfalls":["diagnosing oligodendroglioma on morphology alone without confirming 1p/19q codeletion","mistaking the fried-egg halo, a fixation artefact, for a specific diagnostic finding","overlooking calcified cortical lesions on MRI that are obvious on CT","assuming all IDH-mutant gliomas share the same prognosis and treatment","underestimating higher-grade transformation when enhancement appears"],"management":["Maximal safe surgical resection as the initial mainstay, balancing extent of removal against neurological function","Adjuvant combined chemoradiotherapy, with radiotherapy followed by PCV (procarbazine, lomustine, vincristine) or temozolomide, exploiting the chemosensitivity conferred by 1p/19q codeletion","Confirm the molecular diagnosis of IDH mutation with whole-arm 1p/19q codeletion to guide treatment and prognosis","Seizure control with antiseizure medication, as seizures are the commonest presenting feature","Surveillance MRI to detect progression or higher-grade transformation","Manage progression or transformation with re-resection, further chemotherapy or re-irradiation as appropriate","Multidisciplinary neuro-oncology care with supportive and palliative input given the eventually progressive, incurable nature"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System","EANO guidelines on the diagnosis and treatment of diffuse gliomas","DeAngelis and Wen, Neurologic Complications of Cancer"],"crossLinks":[]},"oligospermia":{"name":"Oligospermia","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oligozoospermia","low sperm count","reduced sperm concentration"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / WHO laboratory manual for semen analysis","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Oligospermia denotes a sperm concentration below the lower reference limit of the WHO semen-analysis framework and is a description, not a diagnosis in itself","At least two properly collected semen samples, taken after a defined period of abstinence and analysed by WHO methods, are needed before drawing conclusions because output varies considerably over time","Causes are best organised as pre-testicular (endocrine), testicular (spermatogenic), and post-testicular (partial obstruction or ejaculatory) so that the evaluation is systematic","A hormonal profile of testosterone, luteinising hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone, and prolactin helps localise the problem; a raised follicle-stimulating hormone signals impaired spermatogenesis","Varicocele is a common and potentially correctable contributor and should be sought on scrotal examination and confirmed on ultrasound if in doubt","Modifiable factors such as heat exposure, obesity, smoking, alcohol, anabolic steroids, and certain medications should be identified and addressed","Where numbers remain too low for natural or intrauterine conception, assisted reproduction with intracytoplasmic sperm injection offers an effective route to fatherhood","Severe oligospermia warrants karyotype and Y-chromosome microdeletion testing before assisted conception because of the genetic implications for offspring"],"pathophysiology":"A reduced sperm concentration results from disturbance at one or more levels of male reproductive function. Pre-testicular causes are endocrine, with deficient gonadotropin drive (as in hypogonadotropic hypogonadism or hyperprolactinaemia) reducing the hormonal stimulation of the testis. Testicular causes involve the seminiferous epithelium itself and include varicocele, prior cryptorchidism, chromosomal or Y-microdeletion defects, gonadotoxic exposures, and idiopathic spermatogenic failure, typically accompanied by a compensatory rise in follicle-stimulating hormone. Post-testicular causes reflect partial obstruction of the excurrent ducts or ejaculatory dysfunction that reduces the number of sperm reaching the ejaculate. In many men the cause is multifactorial or remains unexplained despite full assessment.","additionalDifferentials":["Varicocele","Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism or hyperprolactinaemia","Prior cryptorchidism or testicular injury","Chromosomal abnormality or Y-chromosome microdeletion","Gonadotoxic exposure including anabolic steroids, chemotherapy, or heat","Partial ejaculatory duct obstruction","Idiopathic spermatogenic impairment"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repeat semen analysis by WHO methodology after a standardised abstinence interval","Endocrine profile including testosterone, luteinising hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone, and prolactin","Scrotal and, where indicated, transrectal ultrasound to detect varicocele or ductal pathology","Karyotype and Y-chromosome microdeletion analysis in severe oligospermia","Assessment of reversible lifestyle and occupational factors","Post-ejaculatory urine examination if a component of retrograde ejaculation is suspected","Cystic-fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator gene testing when ductal or seminal-vesicle abnormalities are found"],"redFlags":["A testicular mass identified during scrotal examination or on ultrasound","Symptoms or signs of a pituitary lesion such as visual disturbance or headache","Very low sperm counts with markedly raised follicle-stimulating hormone indicating severe spermatogenic failure","Sudden deterioration in a previously normal count prompting a search for a new insult"],"prognosis":"The outlook depends on the underlying cause and severity; correctable factors such as varicocele, endocrine deficiency, or gonadotoxin exposure may improve counts, while many couples achieve pregnancy through assisted reproduction even when the concentration remains low.","pitfalls":["Drawing conclusions from a single semen sample despite the wide biological variability","Neglecting a systematic pre-testicular, testicular, and post-testicular approach","Missing a correctable varicocele or endocrine cause","Proceeding to assisted conception in severe cases without genetic testing and counselling","Overlooking a partial retrograde or obstructive component reducing the count"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying cause after a systematic pre-testicular, testicular and post-testicular evaluation","Correct reversible factors: weight loss, cessation of smoking, alcohol and anabolic steroids, reduction of scrotal heat exposure and withdrawal of gonadotoxic medications","Endocrine therapy where indicated, with gonadotrophin or pulsatile gonadotrophin-releasing hormone replacement for hypogonadotropic hypogonadism and a dopamine agonist for hyperprolactinaemia","Varicocele repair (varicocelectomy) in appropriately selected men with a clinical varicocele and abnormal semen parameters","Assisted reproductive techniques such as intrauterine insemination, in-vitro fertilisation or intracytoplasmic sperm injection, with surgical sperm retrieval where needed, for persistent oligospermia","Karyotype and Y-chromosome microdeletion testing in severe oligospermia, with genetic counselling before intracytoplasmic sperm injection","Avoid empirical exogenous testosterone, which suppresses spermatogenesis and worsens sperm counts"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO laboratory manual for the examination and processing of human semen","Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","European Association of Urology guidelines on sexual and reproductive health"],"crossLinks":[]},"ollier_disease":{"name":"Ollier disease","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["enchondromatosis","multiple enchondromatosis","IDH1","IDH2","dyschondroplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A non-hereditary disorder of multiple enchondromas caused by post-zygotic somatic mosaic variants in IDH1 or IDH2, so it is sporadic rather than inherited","Enchondromas are intramedullary cartilaginous lesions that characteristically show an asymmetric, often unilateral distribution across the body","Predominantly affects the metaphyses and diaphyses of the hands, feet and long bones, causing swelling, deformity and limb-length discrepancy","Radiographs show radiolucent intramedullary lesions with characteristic linear or stippled calcification and cortical expansion","Pathological fracture through a lesion is a common presentation","Malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma is a recognised long-term risk, higher than in solitary enchondroma and increasing with lesion number","Distinguish from multiple hereditary exostoses, where the lesions are surface osteochondromas rather than intramedullary enchondromas"],"pathophysiology":"Ollier disease arises from somatic mosaic gain-of-function variants in the isocitrate dehydrogenase genes IDH1 or IDH2, occurring in a subset of cells during development. Mutant IDH enzymes produce the oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate, which disturbs DNA and histone methylation and blocks normal chondrocyte differentiation. Islands of residual growth-plate cartilage persist and continue to proliferate within the medullary cavity as enchondromas rather than being resorbed and replaced by bone. The mosaic origin explains the asymmetric distribution and the absence of vertical transmission.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple hereditary exostoses","Maffucci syndrome (enchondromatosis with haemangiomas)","Metachondromatosis","Fibrous dysplasia","Solitary enchondroma","Chondrosarcoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey to map the distribution and burden of enchondromas","MRI of symptomatic or enlarging lesions to assess cartilage and aggressive features","Consideration of somatic IDH1/IDH2 testing on lesional tissue where diagnosis is uncertain","Imaging surveillance for change in size, cortical breach or soft-tissue mass","Biopsy of any lesion with features suspicious for malignant transformation"],"redFlags":["New pain or growth of a previously stable lesion suggesting malignant transformation","Cortical destruction or an associated soft-tissue mass on imaging","Pathological fracture requiring orthopaedic management","Rapidly progressive deformity or functional impairment"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on lesion number, size and site; morbidity comes from deformity, limb-length discrepancy and fracture, while the principal serious concern is malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma, warranting lifelong surveillance.","pitfalls":["Assuming an inherited disorder and giving incorrect recurrence-risk counselling, when the condition is sporadic and mosaic","Failing to arrange surveillance for malignant transformation","Confusing intramedullary enchondromas with the surface osteochondromas of hereditary exostoses","Missing an associated haemangioma that would reclassify the disorder as Maffucci syndrome"],"management":["No medical therapy exists for the enchondromas themselves; management combines surveillance with orthopaedic treatment of complications","Orthopaedic surgery for deformity, pathological fracture and limb-length discrepancy, including curettage and grafting, corrective osteotomy and limb equalisation or lengthening","Lifelong clinical and imaging surveillance for malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma, prompted by new pain, growth, cortical breach or a soft-tissue mass","Prompt MRI and biopsy of any lesion with aggressive features, proceeding to wide excision if chondrosarcoma is confirmed","Baseline skeletal survey to map lesion burden and distribution","Multidisciplinary care across orthopaedics, radiology and a specialist bone-tumour or sarcoma service","Reassurance that the disorder is a sporadic somatic-mosaic IDH1/IDH2 condition and not inherited, so routine familial genetic screening is unnecessary"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Orphanet / WHO Classification of Tumours: Enchondromatosis (Ollier disease)"],"crossLinks":[]},"olmsted_syndrome":{"name":"Olmsted syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TRPV3","mutilating palmoplantar keratoderma with periorificial keratotic plaques","MGP-1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the combination of a severe, mutilating, bilateral palmoplantar keratoderma with sharply demarcated periorificial keratotic plaques (around the mouth, nose, eyes, anus and genitalia)","The keratoderma is often intensely painful and can be transgredient, with constricting bands and pseudo-ainhum threatening digits","Onset is usually in infancy or early childhood with progressive thickening","Most cases result from dominant gain-of-function mutations in TRPV3, a keratinocyte cation channel; recessive and other genetic forms exist","Associated features may include alopecia, nail dystrophy, oral leucokeratosis and, over time, a risk of squamous cell carcinoma within affected skin","Severe itch, pain and functional disability are prominent and difficult to manage","Management is supportive with emollients/keratolytics, oral retinoids, pain control and surgery for constricting bands; TRPV3-directed and other targeted therapies are under investigation"],"pathophysiology":"Olmsted syndrome is most often caused by dominant gain-of-function mutations in TRPV3, which encodes a transient receptor potential vanilloid cation channel expressed in keratinocytes and involved in temperature sensing, epidermal barrier formation, keratinocyte proliferation and hair growth. Overactivity of the mutant channel increases calcium influx and drives excessive keratinocyte proliferation and abnormal differentiation, together with dysregulated apoptosis and sensory-nerve signalling that generates the marked pain and itch. The result is a severe, mutilating palmoplantar keratoderma with periorificial plaques; chronic hyperproliferation and inflammation also underlie the long-term risk of squamous cell carcinoma. Recessive and phenocopy forms involving other genes have been described.","additionalDifferentials":["Vohwinkel syndrome (mutilating keratoderma)","Mal de Meleda","Papillon-Lefevre syndrome","Pachyonychia congenita","Acrodermatitis enteropathica","Pityriasis rubra pilaris with palmoplantar involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing massive orthohyperkeratosis, acanthosis and psoriasiform change","Molecular genetic testing of TRPV3 and related genes","Assessment and monitoring of digital constriction bands and function","Ophthalmological and oral examination for periorificial and mucosal involvement","Surveillance biopsy of chronic or ulcerated plaques for malignant change","Pain assessment and multidisciplinary evaluation"],"redFlags":["Progressive pseudo-ainhum threatening digital viability","New, ulcerated or non-healing lesion within keratoderma suggesting squamous cell carcinoma","Uncontrolled pain or itch causing severe disability","Secondary infection of fissured or eroded plaques"],"prognosis":"Olmsted syndrome is a chronic, often progressive and disabling disorder; the keratoderma rarely resolves and can be mutilating, and there is a long-term risk of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma. Pain and functional impairment are major and lifelong, though targeted therapies offer emerging hope.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise the diagnostic periorificial plaques and treating as isolated keratoderma","Neglecting early release of constricting bands, risking digital loss","Underestimating pain and itch, which are central to morbidity","Overlooking malignant transformation in long-standing plaques"],"management":["Intensive topical skin care with emollients, keratolytics such as urea or salicylic acid, and mechanical debridement of hyperkeratotic plaques","Oral retinoids such as acitretin as the principal systemic therapy to reduce the keratoderma, with monitoring for retinoid toxicity","Emerging targeted therapy directed at the TRPV3 pathway, including mTOR inhibitors such as sirolimus and epidermal growth factor receptor inhibitors such as erlotinib in selected cases","Multimodal control of pain and pruritus, incorporating neuropathic analgesics","Prevention and management of digital constriction bands and pseudo-ainhum through physiotherapy, splinting and surgery to preserve the digits","Surveillance for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, with examination and biopsy of chronic or ulcerated plaques","Multidisciplinary dermatology, pain, physiotherapy, ophthalmology and dental care, with genetic counselling for the TRPV3 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"omenn_syndrome":{"name":"Omenn syndrome","system":"Genetics / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OS","RAG1","RAG2","leaky severe combined immunodeficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews (NCBI) and standard clinical immunology texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of leaky combined immunodeficiency, most often caused by hypomorphic variants in RAG1 or RAG2 that permit limited, oligoclonal T-cell development","Presents in early infancy with an erythrodermic, often exfoliative rash, alopecia and loss of eyebrows and eyelashes","Generalised lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly accompany failure to thrive and chronic diarrhoea","Characteristic laboratory picture is an elevated eosinophil count and markedly raised IgE with low or absent other immunoglobulins","T cells are present but restricted (oligoclonal) and activated, whereas B cells are typically absent or very low","The autologous activated T cells infiltrate skin, gut and other organs, producing a picture that can mimic graft-versus-host disease","Susceptibility to opportunistic and severe infection is high and the condition is fatal without treatment","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is the definitive treatment, with immunosuppression to control the inflammatory phenotype beforehand"],"pathophysiology":"Omenn syndrome arises when residual (hypomorphic) activity of the V(D)J recombination machinery, most commonly RAG1 or RAG2, allows a small number of autoreactive T-cell clones to develop rather than complete arrest of lymphocyte maturation. Defective central and peripheral tolerance, thymic dysfunction and reduced regulatory T cells permit these oligoclonal, activated, T-helper-2-skewed T cells to expand and infiltrate the skin and gut, generating the erythroderma, eosinophilia and raised IgE. B-cell development fails, causing agammaglobulinaemia and profound infection susceptibility.","additionalDifferentials":["Classic severe combined immunodeficiency without the Omenn phenotype","Maternal T-cell engraftment in severe combined immunodeficiency","Neonatal or infantile graft-versus-host disease","Atopic dermatitis or other neonatal erythrodermas","Netherton syndrome","Langerhans cell histiocytosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with differential showing eosinophilia","Immunoglobulin levels demonstrating raised IgE with low IgG, IgA and IgM","Lymphocyte subset analysis and T-cell receptor repertoire (clonality) studies","Molecular genetic testing of RAG1, RAG2 and other combined immunodeficiency genes","Newborn screening T-cell receptor excision circle (TREC) assay where available","Skin biopsy and infection screen for opportunistic pathogens"],"redFlags":["Severe or opportunistic infection in an infant with erythroderma","Rapidly worsening exfoliative rash with fluid and protein loss","Signs of end-organ infiltration or systemic inflammatory decompensation","Failure to thrive with intractable diarrhoea"],"prognosis":"Omenn syndrome is uniformly fatal in infancy without treatment. Timely haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, ideally after control of the inflammatory phenotype, offers the prospect of immune reconstitution and long-term survival.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the erythroderma as atopic dermatitis and delaying immunological evaluation","Overlooking the diagnosis because T cells are present, unlike classic severe combined immunodeficiency","Failing to consider maternal engraftment or graft-versus-host disease in the differential","Administering live vaccines or non-irradiated blood products before the immune defect is recognised"],"management":["Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is the only curative treatment and the definitive goal","Immunosuppression to control the erythrodermic, activated-T-cell inflammatory phenotype before transplant, using corticosteroids, ciclosporin and sometimes biologic agents","Aggressive infection prophylaxis and prompt treatment with antibacterial, anti-Pneumocystis, antifungal and antiviral cover for the profound combined immunodeficiency","Immunoglobulin replacement therapy for the accompanying agammaglobulinaemia","Use only irradiated, cytomegalovirus-safe blood products and strictly avoid live vaccines","Nutritional and skin support addressing failure to thrive, chronic diarrhoea and protein and fluid losses from erythroderma","Multidisciplinary immunology and transplant care in a specialist centre","Genetic counselling and family testing for the RAG1/RAG2 autosomal recessive defect, with newborn T-cell receptor excision circle screening of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (NCBI): RAG deficiency and Omenn syndrome","OMIM: Omenn syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"omodysplasia":{"name":"Omodysplasia","system":"Musculoskeletal / Skeletal dysplasia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GPC6","FZD2","Autosomal recessive omodysplasia","Autosomal dominant omodysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Skeletal dysplasia references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Omodysplasia is a rhizomelic skeletal dysplasia characterised by severe shortening of the humerus, and often the femur; the name refers to the shoulder (omo).","Two genetic forms exist: autosomal recessive omodysplasia due to GPC6 (glypican-6) and autosomal dominant omodysplasia due to FZD2 (frizzled-2), both perturbing Wnt signalling.","The hallmark is a short, hypoplastic distal humerus with dislocation of the radial head and restricted elbow movement.","The recessive GPC6 form tends to be more generalised, with a distinctive facies (frontal bossing, depressed nasal bridge, long philtrum) and sometimes genital anomalies such as cryptorchidism or hypospadias.","The dominant FZD2 form is often milder and more limb-limited, with less facial involvement.","Cognition is typically normal and the condition is generally non-lethal.","Short humeri with radial head dislocation help distinguish it from other rhizomelic dysplasias.","Distinguishing the two genetic forms is important for accurate recurrence-risk counselling."],"pathophysiology":"Omodysplasia is genetically heterogeneous, with both forms converging on Wnt signalling that is essential for appendicular skeletal growth, particularly of the proximal limb. The autosomal recessive form is caused by biallelic variants in GPC6, encoding glypican-6, a GPI-anchored heparan sulfate proteoglycan that modulates Wnt and Hedgehog signalling at the growth plate, and tends to produce a more generalised phenotype with facial dysmorphism and occasional genital anomalies. The autosomal dominant form is caused by heterozygous variants in FZD2, encoding the Wnt receptor frizzled-2, and is typically milder and more limb-restricted. Disrupted signalling impairs endochondral growth of the humerus and femur, producing rhizomelic shortening, a hypoplastic distal humerus and radial head dislocation.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal recessive omodysplasia (GPC6)","Autosomal dominant omodysplasia (FZD2)","Robinow syndrome (also FZD2 and the Wnt pathway)","Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata","Desbuquois dysplasia","Campomelic dysplasia","Leri-Weill dyschondrosteosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey showing short humeri and femora, radial head dislocation and metaphyseal changes","GPC6 molecular genetic testing","FZD2 molecular genetic testing","Clinical photography with segmental limb measurements","Orthopaedic assessment of the elbow","Genital examination in the recessive form","Family segregation studies to define inheritance"],"redFlags":["Fixed elbow deformity or radial head dislocation impairing function","Distinctive facial dysmorphism prompting a broader syndromic evaluation","Genital anomalies in the recessive form","Short stature requiring growth monitoring"],"prognosis":"Generally non-lethal with normal cognition and a normal lifespan; morbidity arises from limb deformity, elbow dysfunction and short stature.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish the recessive GPC6 form from the dominant FZD2 form, which carry different recurrence risks","Overlooking the overlap with Robinow syndrome within the Wnt pathway","Attributing limb-limited findings to positional or acquired deformity","Missing associated genital anomalies in the recessive form"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists; management is supportive and focused on limb function and orthopaedic care","Orthopaedic assessment and treatment of the hypoplastic distal humerus, radial-head dislocation and restricted elbow movement, with surgery for selected functional problems","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maximise upper-limb function, with adaptive aids for short stature","In the recessive GPC6 form, evaluate and manage associated genital anomalies such as cryptorchidism or hypospadias with urology and surgery","Baseline skeletal survey with clinical growth and segmental limb monitoring","Multidisciplinary clinical genetics, orthopaedics and paediatric care","Genetic counselling distinguishing the autosomal recessive GPC6 form from the autosomal dominant FZD2 form for accurate recurrence-risk advice"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Skeletal dysplasia nosology and classification","Medical genetics reference texts","Emery-style medical genetics references"],"crossLinks":[]},"omphalocele":{"name":"Omphalocele","system":"Paediatric surgery / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["exomphalos","umbilical eventration"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Bailey & Love (paediatric surgery)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A central abdominal wall defect at the umbilicus in which abdominal contents herniate into a sac formed by peritoneum and amniotic membrane, with the cord inserting onto the sac","The covering sac distinguishes it from gastroschisis and offers some protection, but it can rupture during delivery or handling","Strongly associated with other anomalies, including chromosomal disorders, cardiac defects, and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, so a thorough workup is essential","Large or giant defects may contain the liver, complicating reduction and closure and raising the risk of respiratory compromise","Initial management protects the sac, keeps it moist and covered, maintains temperature and fluids, and decompresses the stomach","Small defects can often be closed primarily, whereas giant omphaloceles may be managed with staged reduction or a delayed approach allowing the sac to epithelialise","Associated pulmonary hypoplasia and hypoglycaemia (in Beckwith-Wiedemann) must be anticipated and monitored","Antenatal detection allows delivery planning, genetic counselling, and fetal echocardiography"],"pathophysiology":"Omphalocele results from failure of the midgut to return fully to the abdominal cavity and complete the normal closure of the abdominal wall at the umbilical ring during early gestation. The herniated viscera remain covered by a membranous sac of peritoneum and amnion with Wharton's jelly between them. Because the defect reflects a broader disturbance of ventral body wall folding, it is frequently accompanied by chromosomal, cardiac, and syndromic abnormalities, and large defects are associated with a small, underdeveloped abdominal cavity and pulmonary hypoplasia.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastroschisis","Umbilical cord hernia","Umbilical hernia","Pentalogy of Cantrell","Cloacal or bladder exstrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antenatal ultrasound and karyotyping given the high rate of chromosomal anomalies","Fetal and postnatal echocardiography to detect cardiac defects","Blood glucose monitoring, particularly if Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is suspected","Assessment of respiratory status for possible pulmonary hypoplasia in giant defects","Renal and cranial imaging as part of anomaly screening"],"redFlags":["Rupture of the sac exposing viscera, converting it to an emergency similar to gastroschisis with major fluid and heat loss","Respiratory distress from pulmonary hypoplasia or from raised intra-abdominal pressure after reduction","Severe hypoglycaemia in the context of Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","Haemodynamic instability from a major associated cardiac anomaly"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is driven largely by associated anomalies rather than the defect itself; isolated small omphaloceles do well, whereas giant defects and those with chromosomal, cardiac, or pulmonary problems carry higher morbidity and mortality. Staged management of giant lesions can yield good long-term results.","pitfalls":["Allowing the sac to dry out or become damaged through poor handling","Failing to search for and manage associated cardiac and chromosomal anomalies","Forcing rapid reduction of a giant defect and precipitating abdominal compartment syndrome or respiratory failure","Overlooking hypoglycaemia in infants with features of Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome"],"management":["Immediate neonatal stabilisation: protect the sac and keep it moist and covered, maintain temperature and fluids, and decompress the stomach with a nasogastric tube","Surgical closure with primary repair for small defects, and staged reduction with a silo or delayed epithelialisation (paint and wait) for giant defects","Thorough screening for associated anomalies, including chromosomal disorders, cardiac defects on echocardiography and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","Monitor for and treat hypoglycaemia, particularly where Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is suspected","Respiratory support for pulmonary hypoplasia and the effects of raised intra-abdominal pressure in giant defects","Nutritional support including parenteral nutrition until enteral feeding is established","Multidisciplinary neonatal, paediatric surgical, cardiology and clinical genetics care","Genetic counselling and karyotyping given the high rate of chromosomal and syndromic association"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"omphalomesenteric_duct_remnant":{"name":"omphalomesenteric duct remnant","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vitelline duct remnant","omphalomesenteric duct anomaly"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ashcraft's Pediatric Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The omphalomesenteric (vitelline) duct normally connects the embryonic midgut to the yolk sac and involutes in early gestation; incomplete obliteration yields a spectrum of remnants.","Meckel diverticulum is by far the commonest remnant, arising when the intestinal end persists; a patent duct, umbilical sinus, vitelline cyst, umbilical polyp, or fibrous band represent other patterns.","A completely patent duct presents in the neonate with enteric or faecal discharge from the umbilicus, whereas a persisting fibrous band predisposes to volvulus and internal herniation.","An umbilical polyp is a firm, bright-red mucosal nodule of retained gastrointestinal epithelium that does not respond to silver nitrate, unlike an umbilical granuloma.","A vitelline cyst forms when both ends of the duct obliterate but a central patent segment traps secretions.","Ectopic gastric mucosa within a remnant can ulcerate and bleed, and a technetium-99m pertechnetate scan helps localise it.","Persistent umbilical discharge demands distinction from a urachal remnant, which drains urine rather than enteric content.","Symptomatic remnants and persistent bands are managed by surgical excision, including division of the band to prevent obstruction."],"pathophysiology":"During early development the omphalomesenteric duct links the yolk sac to the primitive midgut and normally regresses as the placenta takes over nutrition. Failure of this involution leaves a remnant whose clinical form depends on which portion persists and whether it remains patent. Persistence of the intestinal end produces a Meckel diverticulum; a fully patent lumen creates an entero-umbilical fistula; obliteration of the ends with a patent centre yields a vitelline cyst; and a fibrous cord may persist as a band tethering the bowel to the umbilicus. Retained mucosa is frequently gastrointestinal and may include ectopic gastric or pancreatic tissue, whose acid secretion causes ulceration and bleeding. A persistent band or the mesodiverticular vessels provide a fixed point around which small bowel can twist, producing volvulus or internal herniation.","additionalDifferentials":["urachal remnant or patent urachus","umbilical granuloma","Meckel diverticulum","umbilical hernia","omphalitis","patent vitellointestinal fistula versus enterocutaneous fistula","umbilical dermoid"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["abdominal and umbilical ultrasound","sinogram or fistulogram to define a patent tract","technetium-99m pertechnetate scintigraphy for ectopic gastric mucosa","contrast luminal studies to demonstrate communication with bowel","MRI for complex or ambiguous anatomy","histopathology of the excised remnant"],"redFlags":["bilious vomiting or obstruction suggesting volvulus around a persistent band","gastrointestinal or umbilical bleeding indicating ectopic gastric mucosa","persistent enteric or faecal umbilical discharge","signs of peritonitis from perforation or strangulation","prolapse of bowel mucosa through a patent duct"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent after complete excision of the remnant. Untreated persistent bands carry an ongoing risk of obstruction, and unrecognised ectopic mucosa may bleed.","pitfalls":["confusing a vitelline remnant with a urachal anomaly by not distinguishing enteric from urinary discharge","treating an umbilical polyp as a granuloma with repeated silver nitrate application","overlooking ectopic gastric mucosa as a source of occult bleeding","failing to divide an asymptomatic band discovered incidentally, leaving a future obstruction risk","incomplete excision leaving residual mucosa at the umbilicus"],"management":["Complete surgical excision of the remnant is definitive: diverticulectomy or segmental resection for a Meckel diverticulum, and full excision of any patent duct, umbilical sinus, vitelline cyst or fibrous band","Resect the base widely to remove all ectopic gastric or pancreatic mucosa, since retained acid-secreting tissue is the source of ulceration and bleeding","Excise an umbilical polyp surgically with histological confirmation, as it will not respond to silver nitrate cautery unlike an umbilical granuloma","Treat a bleeding remnant with fluid resuscitation, correction of anaemia and transfusion before proceeding to operative removal","Manage a persistent band causing volvulus or internal herniation as a surgical emergency with laparotomy or laparoscopy, detorsion and division of the band","Send the excised specimen for histopathology to confirm ectopic tissue and exclude other pathology","Manage within a paediatric surgical service with routine postoperative follow-up given the excellent outcome after complete excision"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ashcraft's Pediatric Surgery","Coran, Pediatric Surgery","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"onchocerciasis":{"name":"Onchocerciasis (river blindness)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["river blindness","Onchocerca volvulus infection","onchocerciasis","Robles disease","Mazzotti reaction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / WHO guidance on onchocerciasis","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by the filarial nematode Onchocerca volvulus, transmitted by the bite of Simulium blackflies that breed in fast-flowing rivers, hence 'river blindness'","Chronic infection produces intensely itchy dermatitis, altered skin pigmentation ('leopard skin'), skin atrophy, and firm subcutaneous nodules (onchocercomas) housing adult worms","Microfilariae migrating to the eye cause keratitis, iritis, chorioretinitis, and optic atrophy, leading to progressive and potentially irreversible blindness","The endosymbiotic bacterium Wolbachia within the worm drives much of the inflammatory response, making doxycycline for Wolbachia a useful adjunct that also sterilises adult female worms","Diagnosis is classically by skin snips examined for emerging microfilariae, supported by nodule examination and antigen/antibody or PCR testing","Ivermectin is the mainstay microfilaricidal treatment and the basis of mass drug administration control programmes, given repeatedly as it does not reliably kill adult worms","Ivermectin can precipitate a severe reaction in patients heavily co-infected with Loa loa, so loiasis must be considered in co-endemic areas before treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Onchocerciasis results from infection with Onchocerca volvulus. Infective larvae are introduced by biting Simulium blackflies and mature into adult worms that reside coiled within fibrous subcutaneous nodules. Adult females release large numbers of microfilariae that migrate through the skin, connective tissue, and eye. Most disease is driven not by the live microfilariae but by the inflammatory reaction to dying microfilariae and to their endosymbiotic Wolbachia bacteria, which provoke dermal and ocular inflammation. In the eye, repeated microfilarial death causes punctate then sclerosing keratitis, anterior uveitis, and posterior segment damage, culminating in blindness. In the skin, chronic inflammation produces pruritus, depigmentation, and atrophy. Killing microfilariae abruptly, as with treatment, can intensify this inflammatory response (the Mazzotti reaction).","additionalDifferentials":["Loiasis and lymphatic filariasis","Scabies and other causes of chronic pruritic dermatitis","Cutaneous leishmaniasis","Leprosy (for skin and nerve changes)","Other causes of chorioretinitis or anterior uveitis","Streptocerciasis (Mansonella streptocerca)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin snips examined microscopically for emerging microfilariae","Palpation and examination or excision of subcutaneous nodules for adult worms","Slit-lamp examination for microfilariae and ocular inflammatory changes","Serological (Ov-16) antibody or antigen testing","PCR assays on skin snips for species confirmation","Assessment for Loa loa co-infection in co-endemic areas before treatment"],"redFlags":["Ocular involvement threatening progressive or irreversible visual loss","Severe Mazzotti reaction after microfilaricidal treatment","Risk of severe reaction if ivermectin is given with high Loa loa co-infection","Extensive sight-threatening keratitis or optic atrophy"],"prognosis":"With repeated ivermectin therapy and vector control, transmission and morbidity can be greatly reduced; however, established ocular damage and optic atrophy are irreversible, and untreated chronic infection remains a leading infective cause of blindness.","pitfalls":["Giving ivermectin without excluding heavy Loa loa co-infection in co-endemic regions","Expecting a single course of ivermectin to cure infection despite persistence of adult worms","Underestimating the sight-threatening potential of ocular microfilariae","Failing to anticipate and manage the Mazzotti reaction after treatment"],"management":["Microfilaricidal treatment with ivermectin is the mainstay, given repeatedly at intervals because it does not reliably kill the adult worms","Add doxycycline to target the endosymbiotic Wolbachia, which sterilises and eventually kills adult female worms and dampens the inflammatory reaction","Exclude Loa loa co-infection in co-endemic areas before ivermectin, given the risk of severe encephalopathic reactions with high loiasis microfilaraemia","Provide symptomatic dermatological care for pruritus and dermatitis with antihistamines and emollients","Undertake regular slit-lamp ophthalmic surveillance for keratitis and uveitis, treating ocular inflammation to limit progression to irreversible blindness","Excise symptomatic subcutaneous nodules (onchocercomas), particularly those on the head","Support mass drug administration and Simulium vector control as the basis of community-level transmission reduction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","WHO guidance on onchocerciasis control"],"crossLinks":[]},"oncocytoma_salivary":{"name":"Salivary gland oncocytoma","system":"Head and neck / Salivary glands","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Oxyphilic adenoma","Oncocytic adenoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare benign tumour composed almost entirely of oncocytes: large cells with abundant granular eosinophilic cytoplasm packed with mitochondria.","It most often affects the parotid in older adults and presents as a well-circumscribed, encapsulated, painless mass.","Unlike Warthin tumour, the classic oncocytoma lacks a lymphoid stroma, which is the key distinguishing histological feature.","Like Warthin tumour, oncocytes are avid for technetium pertechnetate, so scintigraphy is not discriminating between the two.","Diffuse or multifocal oncocytosis of the gland may coexist and can make complete separation of tumour from background difficult.","A clear-cell variant, due to intracytoplasmic glycogen, can mimic metastatic clear-cell renal carcinoma and warrants careful exclusion.","Immunohistochemistry with antimitochondrial antibody highlights the oncocytic cytoplasm and supports the diagnosis.","The principal diagnostic task is to distinguish it from oncocytic carcinoma, which shows infiltration, necrosis and increased mitoses."],"pathophysiology":"Salivary gland oncocytoma results from clonal proliferation of oncocytes, epithelial cells that accumulate vast numbers of morphologically and often functionally abnormal mitochondria. The oncocytic phenotype reflects a compensatory mitochondrial hyperplasia thought to relate to acquired defects in mitochondrial oxidative metabolism, and its frequency increases with age; a history of prior irradiation is recognised in some cases. The tumour forms an encapsulated nodule of solid or organoid nests and cords of oncocytes with granular eosinophilic cytoplasm and round nuclei. Because oncocytic metaplasia is common in ageing salivary tissue, oncocytoma exists on a continuum with focal and diffuse oncocytosis, and the abundant mitochondria account for both the tinctorial appearance and the radionuclide avidity.","additionalDifferentials":["Warthin tumour","Oncocytic carcinoma","Acinic cell carcinoma","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma (oncocytic variant)","Oncocytic metaplasia or nodular oncocytosis","Metastatic clear-cell renal cell carcinoma (clear-cell variant)","Pleomorphic adenoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fine-needle aspiration cytology","Ultrasound of the gland","Technetium pertechnetate scintigraphy (uptake is supportive but non-specific)","MRI or CT to define extent","Immunohistochemistry with antimitochondrial antibody","Exclusion of metastatic renal cell carcinoma when clear-cell morphology is present"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth or new pain","Facial nerve weakness","Infiltrative margins or tumour necrosis suggesting oncocytic carcinoma","Regional lymphadenopathy","Fixation to skin or deep tissues"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent, as the tumour is benign and complete excision is curative. The main clinical importance lies in reliably distinguishing it from oncocytic carcinoma and from metastatic renal carcinoma.","pitfalls":["Confusing oncocytoma with Warthin tumour or oncocytic carcinoma on limited material","Mistaking the clear-cell variant for metastatic renal cell carcinoma","Sampling background oncocytic metaplasia rather than the lesion itself","Relying on technetium avidity to distinguish it from Warthin tumour"],"management":["Complete surgical excision, typically superficial or partial parotidectomy with facial nerve preservation, is curative and the definitive treatment","Confirm the diagnosis with fine-needle aspiration cytology and histology, using antimitochondrial immunohistochemistry to demonstrate the oncocytic phenotype","Distinguish carefully from oncocytic carcinoma and, when clear-cell morphology is present, exclude metastatic clear-cell renal carcinoma before committing to management","Reassure the patient given the benign nature and avoid overtreatment once the diagnosis is secure","Be aware of coexisting diffuse or multifocal oncocytosis, which can make separation of tumour from background gland difficult at operation","Follow up for recurrence and manage within a head and neck multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","Gnepp's Diagnostic Surgical Pathology of the Head and Neck","Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"oncologic_emergencies":{"name":"Oncologic Emergencies","system":"Oncology / Emergency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tumor lysis syndrome","superior vena cava syndrome"],"chapter":80,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.585-595","clinicalPearls":["Emergencies in cancer fall into three groups: pressure/obstruction from a space-occupying lesion (structural-obstructive), metabolic or hormonal problems (paraneoplastic), and treatment-related complications (Harrison 22e p.585).","Superior vena cava syndrome (SVCS) reflects SVC obstruction with severe reduction of venous return from head, neck and upper extremities; lung cancer (especially small-cell and squamous) accounts for ~85% of malignant cases, and in young adults lymphoma is a leading cause (Harrison 22e p.585).","Benign causes of SVCS are increasing with intravascular device use (central catheters, pacemaker/defibrillator leads) and now account for at least 40% of cases (Harrison 22e p.585).","SVCS typically presents with neck and facial swelling (especially periorbital), dyspnea, cough, dilated neck veins, collateral chest-wall veins, cyanosis and facial plethora that worsens when supine; bending forward or lying down aggravates symptoms (Harrison 22e p.585).","The diagnosis of SVCS is clinical; the most significant chest radiograph finding is widening of the superior mediastinum (most often right-sided), and CT provides the most reliable mediastinal anatomy (Harrison 22e p.586).","Malignant spinal cord compression (MSCC) is compression of cord and/or cauda equina by an extradural tumor mass; spinal cord compression occurs in 5-10% of cancer patients and is the first manifestation of malignancy in ~10% (Harrison 22e p.588).","Localized back pain and tenderness is the most common initial symptom of spinal cord compression, often present for days to months before neurologic findings; it characteristically worsens when supine, unlike disk-disease pain (Harrison 22e p.588-589).","The thoracic spine is the most common site of cord compression (70%), followed by lumbosacral (20%) and cervical (10%); lung, breast and prostate cancers are the most frequent offenders (Harrison 22e p.588).","Treatment of spinal cord compression is more often successful in patients still ambulatory and with sphincter control at initiation; patients whose exam suggests compression should receive dexamethasone immediately and undergo MRI (Harrison 22e p.589).","Full-length spinal MRI is the imaging procedure of choice for cord compression and is as good as or better than myelography plus post-myelogram CT (Harrison 22e p.589).","About 25% of cancer patients die with intracranial metastases; lung and breast cancer and melanoma metastasize to brain most often, and brain metastases present features of an expanding intracranial lesion: headache, nausea, vomiting, behavioral change, seizures and focal progressive deficits (Harrison 22e p.589-590).","Dexamethasone is the best initial treatment for all symptomatic patients with brain metastases and raised intracranial pressure (Harrison 22e p.590).","Hypercalcemia is the most common paraneoplastic syndrome, and SIADH is the most common cause of hyponatremia among cancer patients (both detailed in Chaps. 98 and 422/393) (Harrison 22e p.592).","Tumor lysis syndrome (TLS) is characterized by hyperuricemia, hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia and hypocalcemia from destruction of large numbers of rapidly proliferating cells; acidosis may develop and acute renal failure occurs frequently (Harrison 22e p.593).","TLS is most often associated with treatment of Burkitt's lymphoma, ALL, AML and other rapidly proliferating lymphomas, and usually occurs during or shortly (1-5 days) after chemotherapy; risk correlates with tumor burden and high LDH (>1500 U/L) (Harrison 22e p.593).","Recognition of risk and prevention are the most important steps in TLS management; the standard preventive approach is allopurinol plus aggressive hydration (Harrison 22e p.593).","Febrile neutropenia is the clinical presentation of fever with <1500 granulocytes/uL; it is a key treatment-related emergency in cancer patients (Harrison 22e p.575)."],"pathophysiology":"Oncologic emergencies arise by three mechanisms (Harrison 22e p.585). Structural-obstructive emergencies result from a space-occupying lesion: in SVCS, malignant tumor or thrombosis obstructs the superior vena cava, reducing venous return from head, neck and upper limbs; in malignant spinal cord compression, an expanding extradural tumor (usually metastasis to vertebral body or pedicle, or paravertebral extension through the intervertebral foramen) mechanically injures axons and myelin and compromises blood flow, causing ischemia/infarction; raised intracranial pressure results from brain metastases and surrounding edema, sometimes with hemorrhage or CSF obstruction causing hydrocephalus and herniation (Harrison 22e p.585-590). Metabolic/hormonal (paraneoplastic) emergencies include hypercalcemia, SIADH-related hyponatremia, lactic acidosis and hypoglycemia (Harrison 22e p.592-593). Treatment-related TLS occurs when cytotoxic killing of a large rapidly proliferating tumor releases intracellular contents: nucleic acid turnover raises uric acid which precipitates in the acidic renal tubules; released phosphate causes hyperphosphatemia with reciprocal hypocalcemia and calcium-phosphate nephrocalcinosis; and massive cell destruction releases potassium causing hyperkalemia, which in renal failure can cause fatal ventricular arrhythmias (Harrison 22e p.593).","additionalDifferentials":["SVCS in a young man with a mediastinal mass: lymphoma versus primary mediastinal germ cell tumor (Harrison 22e p.585)","Cauda equina syndrome: low back pain, saddle sensory loss, rectal/bladder dysfunction, sexual impotence and absent reflexes from compression of nerve roots forming the cauda equina (Harrison 22e p.589)","Abrupt stroke-like onset of headache, nausea, vomiting and focal deficit in brain metastasis usually indicates hemorrhage into the metastasis (melanoma, germ cell and renal cell tumors bleed readily) (Harrison 22e p.590)"],"infectionMimics":["Pyogenic epidural abscess or vertebral tuberculosis can mimic malignant spinal cord compression (Harrison 22e p.589)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Spinal cord compression mimics: osteoporotic vertebral collapse, disk disease, radiation myelopathy, neoplastic leptomeningitis, benign tumors, epidural hematoma and spinal lipomatosis (Harrison 22e p.589)","About 20% of vertebral collapse (especially in older patients and postmenopausal women) is due to osteoporosis rather than cancer (Harrison 22e p.589)","Benign causes of SVCS: aortic aneurysm, thyromegaly, thrombosis, and fibrosing mediastinitis from prior irradiation, histoplasmosis or Behcet's syndrome (Harrison 22e p.585)","Pseudohyperkalemia from high leukocyte/platelet counts (cell lysis after blood draw) can falsely elevate potassium in TLS; no ECG abnormalities are present (Harrison 22e p.593)","Pseudohypoglycemia from glucose consumption in the test tube with hyperleukocytosis, myeloproliferative disease or CSF treatment (Harrison 22e p.593)"],"additionalInvestigations":["SVCS: chest radiograph (superior mediastinal widening, usually right-sided), CT thorax (most reliable mediastinal view; 'hot quadrate' hepatic hotspot sign suggests SVC obstruction), MRI (100% sensitivity/specificity but supine positioning may be difficult in dyspneic patients) (Harrison 22e p.586)","Spinal cord compression: neurologic exam and plain spine films first (earliest finding is the 'winking owl' sign of pedicle erosion; plain films are insensitive), then full-length spinal MRI (procedure of choice); myelography reserved for poor or unobtainable MRI (Harrison 22e p.589)","Brain metastases/raised ICP: gadolinium-enhanced MRI is superior to CT and more sensitive for meningeal involvement and small posterior-fossa lesions (Harrison 22e p.590)","TLS pretreatment evaluation in at-risk patients: complete blood count, serum chemistry (electrolytes, creatinine, calcium, phosphate, uric acid) and urinalysis; follow plasma rather than serum potassium when pseudohyperkalemia is suspected; sonography/CT of kidneys and retroperitoneum if baseline renal function is abnormal (Harrison 22e p.593)","A urinary uric acid to urinary creatinine ratio >1 supports acute hyperuricemic nephropathy; <1 suggests renal failure of other cause (Harrison 22e p.593)"],"redFlags":["SVCS with cerebral and/or laryngeal edema, proptosis, glossal/laryngeal edema, obtundation or postural hypotension carries poorer prognosis and requires urgent evaluation and early stenting (Harrison 22e p.585-586)","Tracheal obstruction from a superior mediastinal mass is the one potentially life-threatening complication of SVCS and demands emergent therapy; cardiac arrest or respiratory failure can occur, particularly with sedatives or general anesthesia (Harrison 22e p.585-586)","Cancer patient with back pain plus a crescendo pain pattern, Lhermitte's sign, or pain aggravated by cough/Valsalva/recumbency should be evaluated for cord compression as quickly as possible (Harrison 22e p.589)","Loss of bowel or bladder control, autonomic dysfunction (decreased anal tone, distended bladder, absent anal wink/bulbocavernosus reflex) signals significant cord involvement and is an unfavorable prognostic feature (Harrison 22e p.588)","Hyperkalemia in TLS with renal failure can rapidly become life-threatening, causing ventricular arrhythmias and sudden death (Harrison 22e p.593)","Acute adrenal insufficiency (adrenal crisis) is potentially lethal and may be masked by nonspecific symptoms attributed to cancer or therapy (Harrison 22e p.593)","Febrile neutropenia (fever with <1500 granulocytes/uL) is a treatment-related emergency requiring prompt management (Harrison 22e p.575)"],"prognosis":"In SVCS, mortality relates to the underlying cause rather than to caval obstruction itself, and clinical improvement occurs in most patients (sometimes via collateral circulation); SVCS recurs in 10-30% (Harrison 22e p.586). For spinal cord compression, tumor histology is an important determinant of recovery and survival, and rapid onset/progression of signs and symptoms are poor prognostic features; treatment is more often successful in ambulatory patients who retain sphincter control (Harrison 22e p.589). Brain metastases frequently cause major symptoms, disability and early death (Harrison 22e p.590). Lactic acidosis in cancer carries a poor prognosis regardless of treatment, and cancer patients with malignant cells on pericardial cytology have very poor survival (Harrison 22e p.587, 592). Despite aggressive prophylaxis, TLS with oliguric/anuric renal failure may still occur and often requires renal replacement therapy (Harrison 22e p.594).","pitfalls":["A normal chest radiograph is still compatible with the diagnosis of SVCS if other characteristic findings are present (Harrison 22e p.586)","A normal appearance of plain spine films does not exclude cancer; plain films are insensitive for cord compression (Harrison 22e p.589)","Vertebral collapse is not a reliable indicator of tumor since ~20% of collapses are osteoporotic (Harrison 22e p.589)","After SVC stenting, the prompt increase in venous return may precipitate heart failure and pulmonary edema (Harrison 22e p.586)","Urinary alkalinization with sodium bicarbonate is no longer recommended in TLS: although it increases uric acid solubility, a high pH decreases solubility of xanthine, hypoxanthine and calcium phosphate, potentially increasing intratubular crystallization (Harrison 22e p.593)","Rasburicase is contraindicated in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, and it causes ex vivo degradation of uric acid in the test tube giving spuriously low levels unless samples are cooled immediately (Harrison 22e p.594)","Pseudohyperkalemia in TLS shows no ECG abnormalities; plasma rather than serum potassium should be followed (Harrison 22e p.593)","Arterial blood gas oxygen tension can be spuriously low in hyperleukocytosis due to oxygen consumption by white cells; pulse oximetry is the most accurate measure of oxygenation (Harrison 22e p.591)"],"management":["In superior vena cava syndrome, elevate the head, give oxygen and corticosteroids, and direct urgent chemotherapy or radiotherapy at the tumour type, with endovascular stenting for severe or refractory obstruction and catheter removal plus anticoagulation for device-related thrombosis","In malignant spinal cord compression, start corticosteroids (dexamethasone) immediately, obtain urgent MRI, and achieve definitive decompression by surgery or radiotherapy to preserve neurological function","For raised intracranial pressure from brain metastases, give corticosteroids for vasogenic oedema, add osmotic therapy, and proceed to radiotherapy or surgical resection, with anticonvulsants for seizures","In tumour lysis syndrome, use vigorous intravenous hydration, rasburicase or allopurinol for hyperuricaemia, and correct hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia and hypocalcaemia, escalating to renal replacement therapy when refractory","Obtain prompt histological diagnosis where malignancy is not yet established, since tissue diagnosis directs definitive tumour-specific therapy","Coordinate care across oncology, radiation oncology, neurosurgery and critical care","Escalate to emergency intervention for airway compromise, rapidly progressive neurological deficit or refractory metabolic derangement"],"severityClassification":"Spinal cord compression management uses the NOMS framework (Memorial Sloan Kettering): Neurologic assessment (degree of epidural compression, myelopathy, functional radiculopathy), Oncologic assessment (tumor radiosensitivity), Mechanical assessment (spinal stability), and Systemic factors (extent of disease and comorbidities determining tolerance of therapy) (Harrison 22e p.589-590). TLS risk is stratified by tumor burden, renal function, hyperuricemia and high LDH (>1500 U/L); a high-risk management algorithm escalates from hydration and allopurinol to recombinant urate oxidase/rasburicase and hemodialysis based on serum uric acid, creatinine, potassium, phosphate and symptomatic hypocalcemia thresholds (Harrison 22e p.593-594).","references":["Harrison 22e p.585-595","Azizi AH et al: Superior vena cava syndrome. JACC Cardiovasc Interv 13:2896, 2020","Durani U, Hogan WJ: Emergencies in haematology: Tumour lysis syndrome. Br J Haematol 188:494, 2020","Lawton AJ et al: Assessment and management of patients with metastatic spinal cord compression: A multidisciplinary review. J Clin Oncol 37:61, 2019"],"crossLinks":["svc_obstruction","cord_compression","hypercalcemia","FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","hyponatremia"]},"one_and_a_half_syndrome":{"name":"One-and-a-half syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Fisher one-and-a-half syndrome","paralytic pontine exotropia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["There is a complete horizontal gaze palsy to one side (the 'one') combined with an internuclear ophthalmoplegia on the other side (the 'half')","In practice the only preserved horizontal movement is abduction of the contralateral eye, which often shows nystagmus","The lesion damages the ipsilateral paramedian pontine reticular formation or abducens nucleus together with the adjacent medial longitudinal fasciculus","The abducting eye may sit in exotropia in primary position, termed paralytic pontine exotropia","Vertical eye movements and convergence are typically spared because their pathways lie outside the affected pontine region","Common causes are pontine infarction, demyelination, and less often haemorrhage or tumour","The addition of a facial nerve palsy from involvement of the adjacent facial colliculus produces the 'eight-and-a-half syndrome'"],"pathophysiology":"A single paramedian pontine lesion damages the abducens nucleus or paramedian pontine reticular formation on one side, abolishing all conjugate horizontal gaze toward that side, and simultaneously involves the neighbouring medial longitudinal fasciculus, which produces an internuclear ophthalmoplegia affecting adduction of the ipsilateral eye on gaze to the opposite side. The combined result is that the affected eye cannot move horizontally at all, while the other eye can only abduct. Ischaemia is the leading cause in older patients and demyelination in younger patients, with haemorrhage and neoplasm accounting for a minority.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated horizontal gaze palsy without internuclear component","Bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia","Myasthenia gravis with complex ophthalmoplegia","Combined multiple ocular motor nerve palsies from skull base disease","Progressive supranuclear gaze disorders","Thyroid eye disease with restrictive limitation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the pons and brainstem to localise the lesion","MRI of the brain and cord for demyelinating disease","Vascular imaging and stroke workup in older patients","Cerebrospinal fluid examination for oligoclonal bands where relevant","Acetylcholine receptor antibodies to exclude myasthenia","Assessment for associated facial nerve involvement"],"redFlags":["Acute presentation with additional brainstem signs indicating infarction or haemorrhage","Progressive course suggesting a pontine mass","Reduced consciousness or long-tract signs implying extensive brainstem damage","New facial weakness indicating spread to the facial colliculus"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the underlying cause; ischaemic and demyelinating lesions often improve with partial recovery of horizontal movement over weeks to months, though a residual exotropia and diplopia may remain.","pitfalls":["Misreading the preserved abduction as normal gaze and underestimating the deficit","Failing to recognise the combined gaze palsy and internuclear ophthalmoplegia as a single localising lesion","Overlooking an accompanying facial palsy that broadens the localisation","Not imaging urgently when an acute vascular cause is likely"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying pontine cause: activate the acute stroke pathway for ischaemia, and use corticosteroids and disease-modifying therapy for demyelination, with cause-specific management of haemorrhage or tumour","Relieve troublesome diplopia symptomatically with monocular occlusion or prisms during recovery","Consider botulinum toxin or strabismus surgery for a persistent, disabling paralytic pontine exotropia","In ischaemic cases institute secondary prevention with antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy and vascular risk factor control","Exclude myasthenia gravis as a mimic with acetylcholine receptor antibody testing","Involve neuro-ophthalmology and orthoptics, and monitor for the partial spontaneous recovery of horizontal movement seen over weeks to months"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"onychogryphosis":{"name":"Onychogryphosis","system":"Dermatology / Nail","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["onychogryphosis","ram's horn nail","ram's-horn nail","onychogryposis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Onychogryphosis is a grossly thickened, opaque, curved nail that grows into a ram's-horn or oyster-shell shape, most often affecting the great toenail","It is largely a disorder of the elderly, driven by neglect, poor mobility, ill-fitting footwear, and repeated minor trauma","The nail typically deviates to one side and shows transverse ridges reflecting irregular, uneven growth","Peripheral vascular disease and any condition limiting a person's ability to reach and cut their nails predispose to it","It is often mistaken for fungal disease, but the deformity is primarily one of shape and bulk rather than infection, although secondary onychomycosis may coexist","Conservative management involves regular chiropody, mechanical thinning and reduction of the plate, and attention to footwear","Definitive treatment for a recurrent painful nail is surgical avulsion with matrix ablation to prevent regrowth"],"pathophysiology":"Onychogryphosis develops when the nail matrix produces plate at an uneven rate across its width, so that one side grows faster than the other and the nail progressively curves and twists as it thickens. Chronic low-grade trauma, pressure from footwear, impaired peripheral circulation, and simple failure to trim the nail all contribute to the disordered growth, and the accumulating layers become hard, opaque, and heaped up. The condition is self-perpetuating because the deformed plate can no longer be cut or worn down normally, allowing the characteristic hypertrophic, hooked shape to develop over months to years.","additionalDifferentials":["Onychomycosis with gross nail thickening","Pachyonychia congenita and other hereditary thickening disorders","Psoriatic nail with marked subungual hyperkeratosis","Subungual tumour distorting the plate","Post-traumatic nail dystrophy","Chronic pressure changes from ill-fitting footwear"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of mobility, self-care ability, and footwear","Peripheral vascular examination including pulses in elderly patients","Nail clipping for mycology to identify coexisting fungal infection","Assessment of diabetic and neuropathic status where relevant","Radiograph if an underlying bony abnormality or exostosis is suspected","Onychoscopy to help distinguish deformity from primary infection"],"redFlags":["Underlying peripheral arterial disease or diabetes raising the risk of ulceration and infection","A painful, discharging, or ulcerated nail suggesting secondary infection or pressure necrosis","Sudden change in a long-standing deformed nail that could hide a tumour","Signs of ischaemia in the affected digit requiring vascular assessment"],"prognosis":"Onychogryphosis is a chronic, recurrent condition that responds to regular mechanical care but tends to return unless the matrix is ablated, and prognosis is closely tied to the patient's mobility, circulation, and ongoing foot care.","pitfalls":["Labelling the deformity as purely fungal and relying on antifungals alone","Neglecting the vascular and diabetic status before considering nail surgery","Underestimating the role of poor self-care and footwear in recurrence","Overlooking a subungual tumour hidden beneath the hypertrophic plate"],"management":["Provide regular podiatry or chiropody with mechanical thinning and reduction of the hypertrophic plate using a nail burr, as the mainstay of conservative control","Offer definitive matrix ablation by surgical excision or chemical phenolisation, or nail avulsion, for recurrent or intractable deformity","Advise well-fitting footwear and protection from repeated minor trauma to slow recurrence","Treat any coexisting onychomycosis with antifungal therapy once confirmed on nail mycology","Assess and optimise peripheral circulation and provide meticulous foot care in diabetic and neuropathic patients","Address the underlying mobility and self-care limitations that drive neglect, with occupational therapy and carer support in the elderly","Arrange ongoing review, as the condition is chronic and recurs unless the matrix is ablated"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"onycholysis":{"name":"Onycholysis","system":"Dermatology / Nail","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nail plate separation","detached nail","onychomadesis distalis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Onycholysis is separation of the nail plate from the underlying nail bed, usually starting distally or laterally and creating a whitish or yellowish air-filled space","It is a sign, not a diagnosis, so the priority is to identify the underlying cause rather than simply treat the loosened nail","Psoriasis is a very common cause and is often accompanied by pitting, oil-drop discolouration, and subungual hyperkeratosis","Repeated mechanical or chemical trauma, including aggressive manicuring and prolonged wet work, is a frequent and overlooked trigger","Thyroid dysfunction, especially hyperthyroidism, can produce distal separation known as Plummer's nail","Photo-onycholysis follows exposure to certain photosensitising drugs (for example tetracyclines and some psoralens) after sunlight and typically affects several nails","The exposed nail bed loses its protective seal and becomes vulnerable to secondary colonisation by yeasts and Pseudomonas, the latter giving a greenish tinge","Management is directed at the cause, keeping the nail short and dry, minimising trauma and wet exposure, and treating any secondary infection"],"pathophysiology":"Onycholysis arises when the bonds anchoring the keratinised nail plate to the epithelium of the nail bed are disrupted, so that the plate lifts away and an air-filled space forms beneath it. The separation may be driven by inflammatory disease of the nail bed such as psoriasis, by repeated mechanical or chemical injury, by a phototoxic reaction to circulating drugs, or by systemic influences such as altered thyroid hormone levels. Once the seal between plate and bed is broken, keratin debris, moisture, and micro-organisms accumulate in the subungual space, and secondary colonisation with yeasts or Pseudomonas can perpetuate the problem and alter the colour of the nail.","additionalDifferentials":["Nail psoriasis with associated pitting and subungual hyperkeratosis","Onychomycosis (distal-lateral subungual fungal infection)","Traumatic or occupational nail bed damage","Contact irritant or allergic reactions to nail cosmetics and adhesives","Thyroid dysfunction (hyperthyroidism)","Drug-induced photo-onycholysis","Subungual tumour lifting the plate"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful history for trauma, wet work, cosmetics, and photosensitising medication","Nail clipping and subungual scrapings for mycology to confirm or exclude fungal infection","Onychoscopy (dermoscopy of the nail) to characterise the pattern and margin of separation","Thyroid function tests when a systemic cause is suspected","Swab of the subungual space if greenish discolouration suggests Pseudomonas","Assessment for cutaneous psoriasis or other skin disease elsewhere on the body"],"redFlags":["Separation confined to a single nail with an underlying mass, suggesting a subungual tumour","Associated pigmentation or bleeding raising the possibility of subungual melanoma","Rapidly spreading discolouration or pain indicating secondary infection","Persistent unexplained onycholysis unresponsive to removal of obvious triggers"],"prognosis":"Onycholysis usually resolves once the underlying cause is corrected and the nail is allowed to grow out attached, but recovery is slow because it depends on outgrowth of new plate, and persistent triggers such as psoriasis or continued trauma lead to relapse.","pitfalls":["Assuming all onycholysis is fungal and prescribing antifungals without confirmatory mycology","Overlooking a photosensitising drug when several nails are affected after sun exposure","Failing to recognise a single stubborn separated nail as a marker of an underlying tumour","Encouraging cleaning under the nail, which extends the space and worsens the separation"],"management":["Identify and remove the underlying cause as the priority, since onycholysis is a sign rather than a diagnosis","Clip away the detached portion of plate and keep the subungual space dry to allow the new nail to reattach as it grows out","Avoid mechanical and chemical triggers, including aggressive manicuring, prolonged wet work and irritant cosmetics","Treat nail psoriasis with topical corticosteroids and vitamin D analogues, escalating to systemic or biologic therapy for severe disease","Correct systemic drivers such as hyperthyroidism, which produces the distal separation of Plummer's nail","Withdraw or substitute photosensitising drugs such as tetracyclines and psoralens and advise sun protection in photo-onycholysis","Treat secondary Pseudomonas colonisation causing greenish discolouration or coexisting candidal infection, and counsel that recovery is slow as it depends on outgrowth of attached plate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"onychomadesis":{"name":"Onychomadesis","system":"Dermatology / Nail","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nail shedding","proximal nail separation","onychomadesis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Onychomadesis is complete, full-thickness separation of the nail plate beginning at the proximal end, which can progress to shedding of the whole nail","It represents the severe end of the same spectrum as Beau lines, both reflecting temporary arrest of nail matrix activity","When several nails are affected it usually follows a systemic insult such as a febrile illness, chemotherapy, or a major metabolic disturbance","It is a well-recognised sequel of hand, foot and mouth disease, appearing weeks after the acute infection has resolved","A single affected nail more often reflects local trauma to that matrix","The timing of the causative event can be estimated from the position of the separation and the rate of nail growth","It is generally self-limiting, and a new nail grows to replace the shed plate once matrix function recovers, so reassurance is central to management"],"pathophysiology":"Onychomadesis is caused by a complete but temporary halt in nail matrix activity, so that for a period no nail plate is produced and the newly forming proximal plate is disconnected from the older distal plate. When matrix function resumes, the new plate grows out and pushes off the detached older nail, which is shed. The insult may be systemic, such as a high fever, viral infection, cytotoxic drug, or metabolic upheaval that transiently diverts the matrix from plate production, or local trauma affecting a single digit; the more severe or prolonged the arrest, the more complete the separation compared with the partial groove of a Beau line.","additionalDifferentials":["Beau lines (partial matrix arrest on the same spectrum)","Traumatic nail avulsion","Severe bullous disorders affecting the nail (for example epidermolysis bullosa or drug reactions)","Onychomycosis with proximal involvement","Nail shedding in severe psoriasis or lichen planus","Idiopathic periodic shedding"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["History for recent febrile illness, viral infection (including hand, foot and mouth disease), or chemotherapy","Examination of all nails to determine single versus multiple involvement","Estimation of the timing of the insult from the level of separation","Screening for systemic or metabolic disease if no clear trigger is found","Review of recent medications, particularly cytotoxic agents","Mycology if fungal infection is a consideration"],"redFlags":["Widespread nail shedding pointing to a significant systemic illness or drug reaction","Mucocutaneous blistering or a severe drug eruption requiring urgent evaluation","Signs of secondary infection of the exposed nail bed","Recurrent shedding suggesting cyclical systemic disease or ongoing exposure"],"prognosis":"Onychomadesis is usually self-limiting, and a new nail regrows over several months once the matrix recovers, with a good outcome provided the underlying trigger is transient and any secondary infection is prevented.","pitfalls":["Alarming the patient rather than reassuring them when the cause is a self-limited illness","Failing to link post-viral nail shedding to a preceding hand, foot and mouth disease episode","Overlooking a serious systemic cause when many nails shed simultaneously","Neglecting protection of the exposed nail bed, allowing secondary infection"],"management":["Reassure and manage expectantly, as the condition is self-limiting and a new plate regrows once matrix activity resumes","Identify and address the precipitating systemic trigger, such as a febrile illness, hand, foot and mouth disease, chemotherapy or a metabolic disturbance","Review causative medications, particularly cytotoxic agents, and modify where clinically possible","Protect the nail unit during regrowth and take measures to prevent secondary infection","Screen for underlying systemic or metabolic disease when no clear trigger is apparent","Monitor nail regrowth over several months to confirm recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"opioid_induced_constipation":{"name":"Opioid-induced constipation","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OIC"],"chapter":null,"source":"Gastroenterology and palliative care references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Constipation that develops or worsens on starting or increasing opioid therapy, with hard stools, straining and incomplete evacuation","Unlike many opioid effects, it does not reliably diminish with continued use (little tolerance develops)","It is a very common and often under-treated adverse effect that limits opioid therapy and quality of life","It is a component of the broader opioid-induced bowel dysfunction, which also includes reflux, bloating and cramping","Stimulant and osmotic laxatives are used first-line, with peripherally acting mu-opioid receptor antagonists for refractory cases","It is distinct from narcotic bowel syndrome, in which the problem is worsening pain rather than constipation"],"pathophysiology":"Opioid-induced constipation results from opioid action on mu-opioid receptors in the enteric nervous system of the gut wall. This reduces propulsive peristalsis, increases non-propulsive tone and fluid absorption, and impairs the defecatory reflex, producing hard, infrequent stools and difficult evacuation. Because these peripheral gut receptors do not develop the tolerance seen with central analgesic effects, the constipation tends to persist throughout opioid therapy. Peripherally acting antagonists relieve it by blocking these gut receptors without reversing central analgesia.","additionalDifferentials":["Functional constipation","Dyssynergic defecation","Secondary constipation (hypothyroidism, hypercalcaemia)","Bowel obstruction","Other drug-induced constipation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical history linking constipation to opioid use","Assessment of stool pattern and abdominal and rectal examination","Exclusion of obstruction and metabolic causes where indicated","Review of the full medication list for other constipating drugs","Assessment of response to laxatives before escalating therapy"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting bowel obstruction or faecal impaction with overflow","Severe abdominal pain or distension","Rectal bleeding or weight loss","Failure to respond to appropriate laxative therapy"],"prognosis":"With appropriate laxatives and, where needed, peripherally acting antagonists, constipation is usually controllable, allowing continued analgesia; it persists as long as opioids are taken.","pitfalls":["Failing to co-prescribe laxatives when starting opioids","Expecting tolerance to the constipating effect to develop","Confusing it with narcotic bowel syndrome","Missing faecal impaction with overflow diarrhoea"],"management":["Start stimulant and osmotic laxatives such as senna and macrogol first-line, given prophylactically at the outset of opioid therapy","Add a peripherally acting mu-opioid receptor antagonist such as naloxegol, methylnaltrexone or naldemedine for laxative-refractory cases, relieving constipation without reversing central analgesia","Consider opioid rotation or a combination oxycodone-naloxone preparation where symptoms persist","Advise non-pharmacological measures including adequate fluid intake, mobility and dietary fibre as tolerated","Review the medication list and reduce other constipating drugs","Exclude bowel obstruction and faecal impaction before escalating therapy","Reassess response and adjust the bowel regimen continuously, as tolerance to this effect does not develop while opioids are continued"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Palliative care references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"opioid_related_disorders":{"name":"Opioid-Related Disorders","system":"Neurologic/Psychiatric (substance use)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Opioid use disorder","Opioid addiction","Opioid dependence","Opioid intoxication","Opioid withdrawal","Opioid overdose"],"chapter":467,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3688","clinicalPearls":["DSM-5 (2022) replaced the older terms dependence and addiction with opioid use disorder, opioid intoxication, and opioid withdrawal; the disorder is defined as repeated use over a 12-month period causing problems in two or more domains (tolerance, withdrawal, using larger amounts than intended, craving, and continued use despite adverse consequences) (Harrison 22e p.3688).","The mu receptor is the receptor most clinically relevant to opioid effects; mu actions include analgesia, euphoric reinforcement, cough and appetite suppression, decreased respiration, decreased GI motility, sedation, and hormonal changes, while kappa activation tends to produce dysphoria and psychotic symptoms (Harrison 22e p.3689).","An opioid 'high' occurs only when the rate of rise in dopamine is fast, so rapid routes such as smoking and intravenous injection produce a 'rush' whereas oral and transdermal routes give analgesia/sedation without the euphoric high (Harrison 22e p.3689).","Opioid overdose is recognized clinically by shallow, slow respirations, pupillary miosis (mydriasis appears only after significant brain anoxia), bradycardia, hypothermia, and stupor or coma; immediate management is based on clinical criteria rather than waiting for toxicology (Harrison 22e p.3690).","Fentanyl overdose may require roughly twice the reversal-agent dose needed for other opioids, and reversal antagonists are short-acting relative to most opioids, so re-sedation can occur after an initial response (Harrison 22e pp.3688, 3690).","Adding xylazine (a nonopioid veterinary sedative) to illicit fentanyl markedly worsens respiratory suppression and is associated with tissue necrosis and increased risk of limb amputation in people who inject (Harrison 22e p.3690).","Opioids generally do not cause seizures except in unusual situations: polydrug use with meperidine, high-dose tramadol, or the newborn (Harrison 22e p.3690).","Withdrawal risk has a strong genetic component (up to ~50% related to functional polymorphisms), including a mu-opioid receptor variant conferring threefold higher affinity for opioids and beta-endorphin; epigenetic methylation may compensate by reducing mu-receptor numbers (Harrison 22e p.3689).","Methadone can prolong the QT interval (rates reported as high as ~16% above non-methadone injecting patients) and has caused sudden death; QT monitoring is relevant during overdose and maintenance (Harrison 22e pp.3690, 3691).","Heroin users are frequently polydrug users (alcohol, sedatives, cannabinoids, stimulants); none substitute for opioids but they have additive effects, so concurrent alcohol or sedative withdrawal (which can be more dangerous) must be excluded before managing opioid withdrawal (Harrison 22e p.3690).","Buprenorphine's partial agonism means escalating doses shift toward antagonist activity, so primary buprenorphine overdose is nearly impossible, but high doses can precipitate withdrawal in patients on full agonists such as morphine or methadone (Harrison 22e pp.3690-3691).","Most opioid-using individuals meet more than three diagnostic criteria, so reducing the DSM threshold from three to two problem areas did not change measured disease rates (Harrison 22e p.3688)."],"pathophysiology":"Opioid receptors (mu, kappa, delta, nociceptin/orphanin) are G protein-linked and coupled to the cyclic AMP (cAMP) second-messenger system and to G protein-coupled inwardly rectifying potassium channels (GIRKs); receptor binding increases potassium permeability, hyperpolarizes neurons, and inhibits action potentials, producing analgesia, sedation, and reinforcement. Reinforcing euphoria is mediated through the mesolimbic dopaminergic pathway from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens, where opioids indirectly raise dopamine by inhibiting GABAergic interneurons; the subjective high requires a fast rate of dopamine change. Tolerance and withdrawal arise from adaptive upregulation of the cAMP-PKA-CREB cascade in locus coeruleus noradrenergic neurons after chronic mu-receptor inhibition: sustained inhibition triggers compensatory increases in cyclic AMP enzyme activity, so abrupt opioid cessation produces unopposed noradrenergic overactivity and withdrawal symptoms; this model underlies the use of alpha2-agonists to suppress that activation. PKA-driven phosphorylation of CREB also induces genes such as tyrosine hydroxylase and BDNF, the latter implicated in long-term neuroplastic changes (Harrison 22e pp.3689-3690).","additionalDifferentials":["Concurrent sedative-hypnotic (benzodiazepine) overdose or withdrawal, which may coexist and be more dangerous to manage","Alcohol intoxication or withdrawal in a polydrug user","Stimulant co-intoxication (cocaine/methamphetamine), including combined 'speedball' use","Other causes of coma with respiratory depression (CNS structural lesion, metabolic encephalopathy) before miosis-based diagnosis is confirmed"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Toxic megacolon from severe opioid-induced constipation mimicking primary abdominal catastrophe","MPTP-induced parkinsonism from contaminated illicitly synthesized meperidine, mimicking idiopathic Parkinson disease","Orthostatic hypotension from opioid-related histamine release mimicking other causes of hypotension"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood or urine toxicology to confirm a suspected opioid diagnosis (management must not wait for results)","ECG for QT-interval monitoring, particularly with methadone or in overdose","Arterial/clinical assessment of respiratory status and gag reflex (aspiration pneumonia risk in overdose)","Liver function tests when considering opioid-antagonist relapse-prevention therapy (most providers avoid it if LFTs exceed about three times normal)","Screening for blood-borne infection (hepatitis B, hepatitis C, HIV) in people who inject"],"redFlags":["Shallow/slow respirations with pinpoint miosis, bradycardia, hypothermia, and stupor or coma indicating life-threatening overdose","Mydriasis in a suspected overdose suggesting significant brain anoxia has supervened","Limited or absent response to standard reversal dosing, prompting suspicion of fentanyl, high antagonist requirement, or concurrent benzodiazepine overdose","Loss of gag reflex with aspiration pneumonia risk","Skin necrosis or limb compromise suggesting xylazine-adulterated fentanyl use","Septic complications of injection use such as meningitis, osteomyelitis, or organ abscesses"],"prognosis":"Opioid use disorder is a chronic, relapsing condition; most patients tend to relapse during or after detoxification, and stopping use is far easier than preventing relapse. Long-term relapse prevention requires combined pharmacologic and psychosocial approaches. Medication-free inpatient/outpatient programs have poor 1- to 5-year outcomes compared with pharmacotherapy, except for residential programs lasting roughly 6-18 months. Untreated potentially lethal overdose progresses to respiratory and cardiovascular collapse and death. Globally the World Drug Report attributes the greatest burden of drug-related morbidity and mortality to opioid misuse; in 2022 over 76,000 US overdose deaths involved opioids, with rates accelerating due to high-potency fentanyl derivatives and stimulant co-use (Harrison 22e pp.3688, 3691-3692).","pitfalls":["Administering so much antagonist that opioid withdrawal is precipitated, rather than targeting reversal of respiratory depression","Assuming a single antagonist dose is sufficient, since antagonists last only a few hours while most opioids persist longer, risking re-sedation (close monitoring/continuous antagonism often needed)","Overlooking concurrent benzodiazepine or alcohol/sedative involvement when reversal has only limited effect","Misjudging dose in naive or infrequent users because actual ingested potency is hard to estimate, especially with fentanyl derivatives","Forgetting that buprenorphine and methadone themselves can be misused and that buprenorphine can precipitate withdrawal in fentanyl- or high-dose-methadone-dependent patients"],"management":["In overdose, secure the airway and support ventilation while titrating the antagonist naloxone to restore adequate breathing, anticipating that potent synthetic opioids such as fentanyl may need larger or repeated doses","Anticipate re-sedation after naloxone because antagonists are short-acting relative to most opioids, and observe or use an infusion accordingly","Treat opioid use disorder with opioid agonist maintenance using methadone or buprenorphine as the evidence-based cornerstone that reduces illicit use and mortality","Offer antagonist relapse prevention with naltrexone to motivated, detoxified patients after checking liver function","Manage withdrawal with a tapering agonist or alpha-2 agonists such as clonidine or lofexidine for symptom relief","Combine pharmacotherapy with psychosocial support, counselling and contingency management, and provide harm reduction including take-home naloxone, needle exchange and blood-borne virus screening","Be alert to xylazine adulteration of illicit fentanyl, which worsens respiratory suppression and causes tissue necrosis with amputation risk"],"severityClassification":"Withdrawal time course (heroin): symptoms begin ~8-10 h after the last dose, with early lacrimation, rhinorrhea, yawning, and sweating; later stages bring restless sleep, weakness, chills, piloerection ('cold turkey'), nausea/vomiting, muscle aches, involuntary movements, hyperpnea, hyperthermia, and hypertension. Acute withdrawal lasts ~7-10 days, followed by a protracted abstinence phase of ~26-30 weeks (hypotension, bradycardia, hypothermia, mydriasis, blunted respiratory CO2 responsiveness). Tolerance/withdrawal can develop within ~6-8 weeks of chronic daily use. Plasma half-lives vary widely: ~2.5-3 h for morphine, >22 h for methadone, minutes for fentanyl-related opioids, and longest for buprenorphine/metabolites (Harrison 22e pp.3689-3690).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3688","Harrison 22e p.3689","Harrison 22e p.3690","Harrison 22e p.3691","Harrison 22e p.3692"],"crossLinks":[]},"opioid_toxidrome":{"name":"Opioid toxidrome","system":"Multisystem / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["opioid toxicity","opioid overdose syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic triad is depressed consciousness, respiratory depression and pinpoint pupils","Respiratory depression is the immediate life threat and the target of resuscitation","Naloxone, an opioid antagonist, reverses toxicity and is titrated to restore adequate ventilation rather than full alertness","Miosis may be absent with certain agents or co-ingestants, so its absence does not exclude opioid toxicity","Long-acting or sustained-release opioids and potent synthetic opioids may need repeated doses or an infusion of antagonist","Non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema and aspiration are recognised complications"],"pathophysiology":"The opioid toxidrome results from agonism at mu-opioid receptors in the central nervous system, depressing the brainstem respiratory drive and level of consciousness and causing pupillary constriction. Hypoventilation leads to hypercapnia and hypoxia, which are the principal causes of death. The duration and severity depend on the agent's potency and pharmacokinetics, so long-acting and highly potent synthetic opioids can outlast a single dose of antagonist and cause recurrent toxicity.","additionalDifferentials":["Sedative-hypnotic or alcohol intoxication","Clonidine or other central sympatholytic overdose","Pontine haemorrhage or other brainstem lesion (for pinpoint pupils)","Hypoglycaemia or other metabolic coma","CNS infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical recognition and response to a trial of opioid antagonist","Oxygenation and ventilation assessment, including capnography where available","Blood glucose and basic metabolic screen to exclude mimics","Chest imaging if pulmonary oedema or aspiration is suspected","Consideration of co-ingestants such as paracetamol in combination products"],"redFlags":["Inadequate ventilation or hypoxia","Recurrent toxicity after initial antagonist, suggesting a long-acting agent","Signs of aspiration or pulmonary oedema","Coma with an uncertain airway"],"prognosis":"With timely airway and ventilatory support and opioid antagonist, recovery is usually rapid and complete; death results mainly from unrecognised respiratory failure or complications such as aspiration and hypoxic brain injury.","pitfalls":["Excluding opioid toxicity because pupils are not pinpoint","Giving a single antagonist dose for a long-acting opioid and missing recurrence","Overzealous antagonist dosing precipitating severe withdrawal","Overlooking co-ingested paracetamol in combination analgesics"],"management":["Manage the airway and support ventilation and oxygenation immediately, as hypoventilation is the principal life threat","Give the opioid antagonist naloxone titrated to restore adequate ventilation rather than full alertness","Use repeated naloxone doses or a continuous infusion for long-acting, sustained-release or potent synthetic opioids that outlast a single antagonist dose","Observe for a prolonged period to detect recurrent toxicity after the initial response","Provide supportive care for complications such as non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema and aspiration","Screen for co-ingestants, particularly paracetamol in combination products, and exclude metabolic mimics","Refer to addiction services and provide take-home naloxone on recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"opioid_use_disorder":{"name":"Opioid use disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OUD","opioid dependence","opioid addiction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The disorder is a problematic pattern of opioid use, whether of prescribed analgesics or illicit opioids such as heroin, leading to clinically significant impairment or distress with impaired control, craving, and continued use despite harm","Intoxication features include pinpoint pupils, drowsiness, slurred speech, and, dangerously, respiratory depression, which is the principal cause of death in overdose","Withdrawal is intensely unpleasant but not usually life-threatening, with features such as dilated pupils, sweating, gastrointestinal upset, muscle aches, yawning, and piloerection","Opioid agonist therapy with long-acting agents is the evidence-based cornerstone of treatment and substantially reduces mortality and illicit use","Antagonist treatment is an option for motivated, detoxified patients, while overdose reversal with an opioid antagonist is a life-saving emergency intervention","Take-home overdose-reversal medication and harm-reduction measures should be offered to patients and, where appropriate, their families","Injecting use carries risks of blood-borne virus transmission, endocarditis, and skin and soft-tissue infection, so screening and preventive care are essential","Comorbid mental illness, chronic pain, and social instability are common and should be addressed within an integrated treatment plan"],"pathophysiology":"Opioid use disorder results from repeated stimulation of mu-opioid receptors, producing potent dopaminergic reward signalling and rapid neuroadaptation in reward, stress, and executive-control circuits. Tolerance and physical dependence develop through receptor and second-messenger adaptations, and withdrawal reflects rebound noradrenergic overactivity, particularly from the locus coeruleus, while genetic, psychiatric, and environmental factors modulate vulnerability and relapse.","additionalDifferentials":["Physiological dependence on appropriately prescribed opioid analgesia without a use disorder","Other sedative or substance intoxication","Non-opioid causes of altered consciousness such as hypoglycaemia or head injury","Sepsis or other acute medical illness in an injecting drug user","Comorbid depression or anxiety driving misuse"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine toxicology confirming opioid and other substance use","Assessment of withdrawal severity with a standardised scale","Screening for blood-borne viruses and injection-related infection","Liver function and general medical assessment","Screening for comorbid psychiatric disorders and suicide risk","Review of prescription monitoring data where available"],"redFlags":["Respiratory depression or reduced consciousness indicating overdose requiring immediate reversal","Features of endocarditis, sepsis, or deep soft-tissue infection in injecting users","High overdose risk following loss of tolerance, such as after detoxification or release from custody","Suicidal ideation or intent"],"prognosis":"Opioid use disorder is a chronic, relapsing condition with substantial mortality, largely from overdose, but agonist maintenance treatment markedly improves survival, retention in care, and function. Prognosis is better with sustained maintenance treatment, psychosocial support, and social stability, and worse with continued injecting, polysubstance use, and disengagement from care.","pitfalls":["Abrupt discontinuation of opioids or detoxification without maintenance planning, which raises overdose risk","Failing to offer take-home overdose-reversal medication","Undertreating pain or comorbid mental illness, perpetuating illicit use","Overlooking the sharply increased overdose risk after periods of abstinence","Stigmatising attitudes that deter engagement with treatment"],"management":["Offer opioid agonist therapy with a long-acting agent such as methadone or buprenorphine as the cornerstone of treatment, substantially reducing mortality and illicit use","Consider antagonist therapy with naltrexone for motivated, fully detoxified patients","Provide structured psychosocial support, counselling and relapse-prevention alongside pharmacotherapy","Supply take-home opioid antagonist and harm-reduction measures to the patient and, where appropriate, their family","Screen for and treat blood-borne viruses and injection-related infection","Identify and manage comorbid psychiatric disorders and assess suicide risk","Maintain long-term maintenance treatment with attention to retention in care and social stability, which improve prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"opisthorchiasis":{"name":"Opisthorchiasis (Opisthorchis liver fluke)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Opisthorchis viverrini infection","Opisthorchis felineus infection","cat liver fluke","Southeast Asian liver fluke","opisthorchiid liver fluke disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / Mandell","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Acquired by eating raw or undercooked freshwater fish (often as fermented or pickled fish dishes) that carry metacercariae; highly endemic across parts of Southeast Asia and eastern Europe","Most light infections are asymptomatic; heavier or chronic infection causes right upper quadrant discomfort, dyspepsia, hepatomegaly and features of biliary irritation","Chronic infection is a recognised risk factor for cholangiocarcinoma, making it one of the few parasitic causes of a defined human cancer","Diagnosis rests on finding characteristic operculated eggs in stool, which are difficult to distinguish from other opisthorchiid and heterophyid eggs on morphology alone","Peripheral eosinophilia is common, and imaging may show intrahepatic ductal dilatation, gallbladder sludge and biliary wall thickening","Praziquantel is the treatment of choice and is highly effective against the adult flukes","Public health control depends on discouraging consumption of raw freshwater fish and on sanitation to interrupt egg contamination of water bodies"],"pathophysiology":"Opisthorchis species are small liver flukes whose life cycle involves freshwater snails as first intermediate hosts and cyprinid fish as second intermediate hosts. Humans and other fish-eating mammals become definitive hosts when they ingest infective metacercariae in raw or inadequately cooked fish. The larvae excyst in the duodenum, ascend the biliary tree through the ampulla, and mature into adult flukes residing in the intrahepatic bile ducts and gallbladder. Long-standing mechanical irritation and the fluke's secretions drive chronic inflammation, epithelial hyperplasia, periductal fibrosis and cholestasis; sustained biliary injury with proliferative and dysplastic change underlies the strong association with cholangiocarcinoma. Eggs passed in bile reach the intestine and are excreted in faeces, continuing the cycle when they contaminate freshwater.","additionalDifferentials":["Clonorchiasis (Clonorchis sinensis), which is clinically and morphologically very similar","Fascioliasis and other biliary trematode infection","Cholelithiasis and choledocholithiasis with biliary colic","Ascending bacterial cholangitis","Cholangiocarcinoma or other hepatobiliary malignancy","Recurrent pyogenic (oriental) cholangitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy for ova and parasites, ideally with concentration and repeated samples","Serology or antigen detection where available to support diagnosis in light infection","Full blood count for eosinophilia and liver function tests","Abdominal ultrasound for biliary dilatation, gallbladder sludge and ductal wall changes","MRCP or CT when malignancy or complicated biliary disease is suspected","Endoscopic examination of bile with cytology if cholangiocarcinoma is a concern"],"redFlags":["Progressive jaundice, weight loss and a biliary mass suggesting cholangiocarcinoma","Fever with rigors and right upper quadrant pain indicating ascending cholangitis","Biliary obstruction with worsening liver dysfunction","Recurrent cholangitis or intrahepatic stone formation"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated infection responds well to anthelmintic treatment with resolution of symptoms, but chronic heavy infection carries a serious long-term risk of cholangiocarcinoma and biliary complications, for which the outlook is poor.","pitfalls":["Assuming eradication removes cancer risk; malignancy can develop years after treatment in previously heavily infected patients","Misidentifying eggs as heterophyid or other opisthorchiid species because of overlapping morphology","Missing light infections on a single stool sample and prematurely excluding the diagnosis","Attributing biliary symptoms solely to gallstones and overlooking coexistent fluke infection in endemic populations"],"management":["Give praziquantel as the anthelmintic of choice, being highly effective against the adult liver flukes, with albendazole as an alternative","Educate patients to avoid raw, undercooked, fermented or pickled freshwater fish to eliminate the source and prevent reinfection","Provide symptomatic relief of biliary discomfort and dyspepsia","Undertake long-term surveillance for cholangiocarcinoma with imaging in chronic or heavy infection, given the established malignant risk","Manage biliary complications such as cholangitis or obstruction with endoscopic drainage and antibiotics","Confirm cure with follow-up stool examination for ova","Refer to hepatobiliary and oncology services when malignancy or complicated biliary disease is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"opitz_g_bbb_syndrome":{"name":"Opitz G/BBB syndrome (MID1)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MID1","X-linked Opitz G/BBB syndrome","Opitz-Frias syndrome","hypertelorism-hypospadias syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disorder of midline development in which the X-linked form is caused by MID1 variants and affects males more severely","The cardinal features are hypertelorism (widely spaced eyes) and hypospadias in males","A laryngo-tracheo-oesophageal cleft can cause swallowing difficulty, recurrent aspiration and respiratory problems in infancy","Additional midline anomalies include cleft lip and palate, imperforate anus and congenital heart defects","Developmental delay and intellectual disability are variable and often mild to moderate","Caused by variants in MID1, encoding a microtubule-associated E3 ubiquitin ligase; carrier females typically show only hypertelorism","Aspiration and airway involvement are the main early clinical threats"],"pathophysiology":"MID1 encodes a microtubule-associated protein of the TRIM/RBCC family that functions as an E3 ubiquitin ligase, regulating the turnover of the catalytic subunit of protein phosphatase 2A and thereby influencing microtubule dynamics and signalling during midline morphogenesis. Loss-of-function variants disturb this regulation, impairing the fusion and development of ventral midline structures in the face, larynx, heart, abdomen and genitalia. The result is the constellation of hypertelorism, laryngo-tracheo-oesophageal cleft, hypospadias and other midline defects, with variable neurodevelopmental impact. Because MID1 is X-linked, males are affected while carrier females usually show only hypertelorism.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal dominant Opitz G/BBB syndrome (22q11.2 region)","22q11.2 deletion syndrome","CHARGE syndrome","Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome","Other midline malformation syndromes","Isolated hypospadias with hypertelorism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Swallowing assessment and airway evaluation for laryngo-tracheo-oesophageal cleft","Echocardiography for congenital heart disease","Renal and genital imaging and evaluation of anal anatomy","Brain MRI and developmental assessment","Targeted MID1 sequencing or a gene panel or exome sequencing, with chromosomal microarray to exclude 22q11.2 deletion"],"redFlags":["Recurrent aspiration or respiratory distress from an airway or oesophageal cleft","Feeding failure and faltering growth","Haemodynamically significant congenital heart disease","Airway compromise requiring urgent intervention"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends largely on the severity of the laryngo-tracheo-oesophageal cleft and cardiac anomalies; with surgical management of midline defects many children do well, though neurodevelopmental outcome is variable. Care is multidisciplinary and supportive.","pitfalls":["Missing a laryngo-tracheo-oesophageal cleft in an infant with recurrent aspiration","Not distinguishing the X-linked MID1 form from the 22q11.2-associated autosomal dominant form","Overlooking the diagnosis when only hypertelorism is present in a carrier mother","Attributing feeding problems to reflux alone without airway assessment"],"management":["Undertake staged surgical repair of the midline defects, including laryngo-tracheo-oesophageal cleft, cleft lip and palate, hypospadias and imperforate anus","Protect the airway and support feeding with thickened feeds or gastrostomy where the cleft causes aspiration and swallowing difficulty","Manage congenital heart defects medically and surgically as dictated by echocardiography","Provide developmental support and early intervention for the variable delay and intellectual disability","Monitor for and treat recurrent respiratory infections related to aspiration","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across paediatric surgery, ENT, cardiology, clinical genetics and speech and feeding therapy","Offer genetic counselling and family screening given X-linked MID1 inheritance, including carrier testing of the mother and female relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: X-Linked Opitz G/BBB Syndrome","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"opsismodysplasia":{"name":"Opsismodysplasia","system":"Skeletal dysplasia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["INPPL1-related opsismodysplasia","OPSMD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Skeletal dysplasia references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare autosomal recessive skeletal dysplasia whose name derives from the Greek for 'late', reflecting the hallmark markedly delayed epiphyseal and carpotarsal ossification (retarded skeletal maturation) — the opposite of the advanced bone age seen in Desbuquois dysplasia.","Presents at or before birth with severe micromelia — short limbs with disproportionately short hands and feet — together with relative macrocephaly, frontal bossing, a depressed nasal bridge, small anteverted nares and a long philtrum.","Characteristic radiographic combination: severe platyspondyly, extremely short and squared metacarpals and phalanges, concave metaphyseal cupping, and ossification profoundly delayed relative to gestational or chronological age.","Biallelic loss-of-function variants in INPPL1 (encoding the inositol polyphosphate 5-phosphatase SHIP2) underlie most cases; a subset of clinically typical patients lack INPPL1 variants, pointing to locus heterogeneity.","Renal phosphate wasting with hypophosphataemic, rickets-like bone changes may accompany the dysplasia, so bone biochemistry and renal tubular handling of phosphate deserve evaluation.","A narrow thorax with respiratory insufficiency is the leading cause of early death; airway and ventilatory planning are central to neonatal management.","Antenatal ultrasound may show short-limb dysplasia with a small chest and relative macrocephaly; the striking discrepancy between skeletal maturity and gestation is a diagnostic clue.","Intelligence is generally preserved in survivors, so prognostic counselling should separate the skeletal and respiratory burden from cognitive outcome."],"pathophysiology":"Opsismodysplasia is most often caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in INPPL1, the gene encoding SHIP2 (SH2-domain-containing inositol polyphosphate 5-phosphatase 2), an enzyme that hydrolyses the 5-phosphate of phosphatidylinositol-3,4,5-trisphosphate and thereby dampens phosphoinositide signalling downstream of growth-factor and insulin receptors. Within the growth plate, disruption of this signalling is thought to impair chondrocyte proliferation and the orderly progression of endochondral ossification, generating the profound delay in epiphyseal and carpotarsal maturation, the metaphyseal cupping and the platyspondyly that define the disorder. SHIP2 also participates in phosphate homeostasis, which may account for the renal phosphate wasting and hypophosphataemic, rickets-like bone changes reported in some individuals. Because a proportion of clinically typical cases carry no identifiable INPPL1 variant, additional as-yet-undefined genetic causes are presumed.","additionalDifferentials":["Desbuquois dysplasia (short-limb dysplasia but with advanced bone age and joint dislocations)","Short-limb spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia with hand anomalies","Achondrogenesis and other perinatally lethal short-limb dysplasias","Hypophosphatasia (low alkaline phosphatase, poor mineralisation)","Chondrodysplasia punctata (stippled epiphyses)","Dyssegmental dysplasia","Kniest dysplasia and spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full skeletal survey with formal assessment of bone age and hand/foot morphology","Molecular testing of INPPL1 or a skeletal dysplasia gene panel, with parental testing","Serum calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase and parathyroid hormone","Renal tubular phosphate handling studies and urinalysis for phosphaturia","Renal tract ultrasound to look for nephrocalcinosis","Chest imaging and echocardiography to assess thoracic and cardiac status","Genetic counselling with discussion of autosomal recessive recurrence risk"],"redFlags":["Progressive respiratory distress or failure from a small thorax in early infancy","Feeding difficulty with failure to thrive","Biochemical hypophosphataemia with clinical or radiographic rickets","Nephrocalcinosis or deteriorating renal function"],"prognosis":"Early mortality from respiratory failure is common in the neonatal and infant period; survivors have disproportionate short stature and may develop metabolic bone and renal complications, while cognition is usually preserved.","pitfalls":["Interpreting the delayed ossification as a lethal achondrogenesis and withdrawing care prematurely","Overlooking treatable hypophosphataemia and renal phosphate wasting","Assuming dominant inheritance and understating the autosomal recessive recurrence risk","Attributing the short limbs to constitutional smallness rather than a defined dysplasia"],"management":["Prioritise neonatal airway and ventilatory support for the narrow thorax and respiratory insufficiency, which is the leading cause of early death","Treat the hypophosphataemic, rickets-like bone disease with oral phosphate and active vitamin D analogues such as alfacalcidol or calcitriol, guided by bone biochemistry and renal tubular phosphate handling","Monitor for nephrocalcinosis and renal tubular dysfunction with imaging and urinalysis","Manage the disproportionate short stature and skeletal deformity through orthopaedic input","Provide nutritional and growth support while noting that cognition is usually preserved","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across clinical genetics, respiratory medicine, nephrology and orthopaedics","Offer genetic counselling with molecular testing of INPPL1 and parental testing, given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International nosology and classification of genetic skeletal disorders","Standard clinical genetics and dysmorphology reference texts","Paediatric skeletal radiology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"opsoclonus_myoclonus_syndrome":{"name":"Opsoclonus-myoclonus-ataxia syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neuroimmunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["opsoclonus-myoclonus syndrome","OMS","dancing eyes-dancing feet syndrome","Kinsbourne syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by opsoclonus (chaotic, involuntary, multidirectional saccades), myoclonus, and cerebellar ataxia","Behavioural disturbance, irritability, and sleep disruption are common, especially in children","In children it is strongly associated with neuroblastoma, so cross-sectional and metabolic tumour imaging is mandatory","In adults it may be parainfectious or paraneoplastic, notably with small-cell lung and breast carcinoma","Opsoclonus persists during eye closure and sleep, helping distinguish it from other abnormal eye movements","First-line immunotherapy uses corticosteroids or corticotropin with intravenous immunoglobulin, escalating to rituximab or other agents in refractory disease","In paraneoplastic cases, removal or treatment of the underlying tumour is central to management"],"pathophysiology":"Opsoclonus-myoclonus-ataxia syndrome is an immune-mediated disorder affecting brainstem and cerebellar circuits that control eye movements and coordination. Loss of inhibitory control over the saccadic burst neurons, thought to involve dysfunction of omnipause and fastigial-cerebellar pathways, produces the chaotic saccades of opsoclonus, while cerebellar involvement generates myoclonus and ataxia. The immune response is frequently triggered by an occult tumour, classically neuroblastoma in children through cross-reacting antineural immunity, or by preceding infection; a defined single antibody is often not identified.","additionalDifferentials":["Cerebellar ataxia of infective, toxic, or metabolic cause","Other paraneoplastic cerebellar or brainstem syndromes","Drug or toxin-induced ocular flutter and myoclonus","Brainstem encephalitis","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cross-sectional imaging of chest, abdomen, and pelvis to search for neuroblastoma or other tumour","Metabolic tumour imaging and urinary catecholamine metabolites in children","Paraneoplastic antibody panel in serum and cerebrospinal fluid","MRI of the brain to exclude structural and inflammatory lesions","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis for inflammation","Whole-body tumour screening in adults, including for small-cell lung and breast carcinoma"],"redFlags":["Failure to identify an occult neuroblastoma or other tumour on initial screening","Severe ataxia or myoclonus impairing safety and function","Progressive encephalopathy or developmental regression in children","Relapse after apparent recovery suggesting undertreated disease or occult tumour"],"prognosis":"Many patients, especially children, improve with immunotherapy and tumour treatment, but relapses and residual cognitive, behavioural, or motor deficits are common, and outcome in paraneoplastic adult cases depends on the underlying malignancy.","pitfalls":["Failing to search repeatedly for an occult tumour when initial imaging is negative","Mistaking opsoclonus for nystagmus or a functional disorder","Under-treating, as inadequate immunotherapy is linked to relapse and long-term sequelae","Overlooking behavioural and developmental effects that require broader support"],"management":["Search for and treat the underlying tumour, with resection of neuroblastoma in children and treatment of small-cell lung or breast carcinoma in adults, as this drives the immune process","Start first-line immunotherapy with corticosteroids or corticotropin combined with intravenous immunoglobulin","Escalate to rituximab or additional immunosuppression such as cyclophosphamide in refractory disease","Provide symptomatic control of myoclonus and of the behavioural, irritability and sleep disturbance, especially in children","Offer rehabilitation for ataxia and neurodevelopmental support for affected children","Maintain surveillance for relapse and for occult or recurrent tumour","Manage within a neuro-oncology and neuroimmunology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Continuum: Autoimmune Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"optic_disc_drusen":{"name":"Optic disc drusen","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["optic nerve head drusen","ONHD","disc drusen","buried optic disc drusen"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Acellular calcified deposits within the optic nerve head that are usually bilateral and often an incidental finding","The central clinical importance is that they cause pseudopapilloedema, mimicking true disc swelling and prompting unnecessary investigation if not recognised","Superficial drusen appear as glistening, refractile bodies at the disc margin, whereas buried drusen produce an elevated disc with blurred margins but no true swelling","Features favouring pseudopapilloedema over true papilloedema include an absent central cup, anomalous branching vessels, a lumpy disc contour and the lack of hyperaemia, exudates or nerve fibre layer oedema obscuring vessels","Most patients are asymptomatic, but drusen can cause progressive visual field defects, typically arcuate or nasal, and an enlarged blind spot","Ultrasonography demonstrates a highly reflective calcified body with acoustic shadowing and is a robust confirmatory test, as drusen autofluoresce and calcify","Enhanced-depth optical coherence tomography can directly visualise buried drusen and has become a preferred imaging modality","Management is largely observation with monitoring of visual fields, and rare complications such as choroidal neovascularisation or anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy require specific attention"],"pathophysiology":"Optic disc drusen are calcified concretions that accumulate within the prelaminar optic nerve head, thought to arise from disturbed axonal metabolism and impaired axoplasmic transport in a congenitally crowded disc with a small scleral canal. Degenerating axonal mitochondria and extruded material undergo progressive calcification over time, which is why drusen are frequently buried in childhood and become more superficial and visible with age. The crowded, drusen-laden disc can mechanically compress adjacent nerve fibres, producing slowly progressive visual field defects, and the compromised vascular supply predisposes to ischaemic optic neuropathy and, rarely, peripapillary choroidal neovascularisation.","additionalDifferentials":["True papilloedema from raised intracranial pressure","Anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy","Optic neuritis or other inflammatory optic neuropathy","Infiltrative or compressive optic disc lesions","Optic disc oedema from malignant hypertension","Myelinated retinal nerve fibres and other congenital disc anomalies","Peripapillary choroidal neovascular membrane"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["B-scan ultrasonography showing a calcified, highly reflective body with acoustic shadowing","Fundus autofluorescence, which highlights superficial drusen","Enhanced-depth or spectral-domain optical coherence tomography to visualise buried drusen","Automated perimetry to detect and monitor arcuate and nasal field defects","Fluorescein angiography to distinguish pseudopapilloedema (no true leakage) from genuine disc swelling","Neuroimaging when true papilloedema cannot be confidently excluded on clinical and ultrasound grounds"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual field loss attributable to the drusen","Sudden visual loss suggesting anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy","Subretinal fluid or haemorrhage indicating peripapillary choroidal neovascularisation","Any doubt about coexisting true papilloedema, which mandates exclusion of raised intracranial pressure"],"prognosis":"Most patients retain good central vision throughout life, but slowly progressive peripheral field loss is common and irreversible; central vision is usually only threatened by uncommon complications such as ischaemic optic neuropathy or choroidal neovascularisation.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting pseudopapilloedema as true papilloedema and subjecting the patient to unnecessary lumbar puncture and imaging","Conversely, assuming disc elevation is drusen and missing coexisting raised intracranial pressure","Failing to monitor visual fields in an asymptomatic patient and overlooking progressive loss","Not recognising a treatable complication such as choroidal neovascularisation","Assuming absence of visible superficial drusen excludes buried drusen, which need ultrasound or OCT to detect"],"management":["Recognise the drusen to avoid unnecessary neuroimaging and lumbar puncture, confirming pseudopapilloedema rather than true papilloedema with B-scan ultrasonography showing a calcified reflective body with acoustic shadowing","Observe and reassure asymptomatic patients, as there is no drusen-directed treatment","Perform periodic automated perimetry to detect and monitor the slowly progressive arcuate and nasal visual field defects","Manage associated anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy and control vascular risk factors","Detect and treat peripapillary choroidal neovascularisation with anti-VEGF therapy","Arrange ongoing ophthalmology follow-up for surveillance of field and disc changes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"optic_disc_pit":{"name":"optic disc pit","system":"ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["optic pit","congenital optic disc pit"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital, usually unilateral, greyish oval or round excavation of the optic disc, most often located in the inferotemporal quadrant.","Discs harbouring a pit are frequently larger than normal, and the pit may be accompanied by a peripapillary crescent or halo.","The major sight-threatening complication is optic disc pit maculopathy, a serous macular detachment often with an inner and outer retinoschisis-like separation.","Fluid in maculopathy is thought to originate from vitreous or cerebrospinal fluid tracking through the pit rather than from a classic RPE leak.","Fluorescein angiography typically shows late hyperfluorescence of the pit but, importantly, no active leakage into the subretinal fluid.","Optical coherence tomography is the key investigation, demonstrating schisis-like intraretinal splitting and subretinal fluid communicating with the pit.","Temporal pits are more likely to develop maculopathy than nasal or central pits.","Vitrectomy with gas tamponade, sometimes combined with internal limiting membrane peel or laser to the temporal disc margin, is the mainstay for persistent maculopathy."],"pathophysiology":"An optic disc pit represents a localised herniation of dysplastic retina into a defect in the lamina cribrosa, regarded as part of the optic disc coloboma spectrum arising from imperfect closure of the fetal fissure. The pit is a discontinuity in the normal disc architecture through which the subarachnoid space and vitreous cavity may communicate with the intraretinal and subretinal spaces. In optic pit maculopathy, fluid, believed to derive from liquefied vitreous or cerebrospinal fluid, tracks through the pit and dissects into the retina, producing a schisis-like inner separation and an overlying serous detachment. Persistent traction and fluid accumulation lead to photoreceptor damage and progressive central visual loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Central serous chorioretinopathy","Optic nerve coloboma","Morning glory disc anomaly","Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment involving the macula","X-linked retinoschisis","Glaucomatous acquired pit of the optic nerve","Vitreomacular traction syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Optical coherence tomography of the macula and optic nerve head","Fundus fluorescein angiography to exclude active RPE leakage","Automated perimetry for arcuate or centrocaecal field defects","Fundus autofluorescence to map the extent of serous detachment","Serial fundus photography to monitor pit and maculopathy","OCT angiography to exclude a secondary choroidal neovascular membrane"],"redFlags":["New central metamorphopsia or blurring indicating evolving maculopathy","Progressive serous macular detachment on serial OCT","Marked reduction in best-corrected visual acuity","Features suggesting acquired glaucomatous pitting with raised intraocular pressure"],"prognosis":"An isolated pit without maculopathy is compatible with normal lifelong vision. Once maculopathy develops, untreated cases frequently progress to poor central acuity, though surgical repair can restore useful vision in a substantial proportion.","pitfalls":["Mistaking optic pit maculopathy for central serous chorioretinopathy and delaying appropriate surgical referral.","Assuming subretinal fluid arises from an RPE leak; fluorescein typically shows no active leak, misleading the unwary.","Confusing a congenital pit with an acquired glaucomatous pit and missing coexisting raised intraocular pressure.","Underestimating the chronicity of maculopathy fluid, which may resorb slowly even after successful surgery."],"management":["Observe an isolated pit without maculopathy, monitoring with optical coherence tomography, as it is compatible with normal lifelong vision","Repair optic disc pit maculopathy surgically with pars plana vitrectomy and gas tamponade, often combined with internal limiting membrane peeling","Consider adjuncts such as laser photocoagulation at the temporal disc margin or macular buckling in selected cases","Track the schisis-like intraretinal separation and serous macular detachment with serial optical coherence tomography","Use fluorescein angiography to confirm absence of active retinal pigment epithelium leakage and to exclude a secondary neovascular membrane","Refer to a vitreoretinal service when maculopathy develops, and counsel the patient to report central visual disturbance promptly"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Retina and Vitreous"],"crossLinks":[]},"optic_nerve_coloboma":{"name":"optic nerve coloboma","system":"ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["optic disc coloboma","coloboma of the optic nerve head"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from defective closure of the embryonic fetal (choroidal) fissure, producing a sharply demarcated, bowl-shaped white excavation that characteristically involves the inferonasal disc.","The excavation is decentred inferiorly, in contrast to the morning glory anomaly in which the abnormality is central and the disc is surrounded by a raised annulus.","May occur in isolation or extend inferonasally as a chorioretinal coloboma, and can be unilateral or bilateral.","Autosomal dominant inheritance with variable expressivity is described, including mutations affecting the PAX6 and other developmental genes.","Associated with systemic syndromes, notably CHARGE syndrome, renal coloboma (papillorenal) syndrome and morning glory-related conditions.","Retinal detachment is an important complication, often related to a break within colobomatous retina or the fragile intercalary membrane.","Visual acuity is variable and difficult to predict from disc appearance alone, ranging from near normal to severely reduced.","A relative afferent pupillary defect and corresponding field loss may accompany larger colobomas."],"pathophysiology":"Optic nerve coloboma is a congenital dysplasia resulting from incomplete or abnormal closure of the embryonic fetal fissure, which normally seals during the fifth to seventh week of gestation along the inferonasal aspect of the developing optic cup and stalk. Failure of fusion leaves a defect through which mesodermal and neuroectodermal tissue is deficient, creating an excavated, glial-lined depression at the disc that may extend contiguously into the choroid and retina. The thin intercalary membrane overlying the coloboma is prone to breaks, predisposing to serous or rhegmatogenous retinal detachment. Because closure defects reflect broader disturbances in ocular and systemic morphogenesis, colobomas frequently accompany multisystem developmental syndromes.","additionalDifferentials":["Morning glory disc anomaly","Optic disc pit","Peripapillary staphyloma","Optic disc hypoplasia","Megalopapilla","Glaucomatous optic disc cupping","Tilted disc syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination and fundus photography to map the coloboma","Optical coherence tomography of the disc, peripapillary retina and macula","B-scan ultrasonography or MRI where a colobomatous cyst is suspected","Systemic evaluation including renal ultrasound and cardiac/ENT assessment for syndromic associations","Genetic testing (e.g. PAX6, and panels for CHARGE/papillorenal syndrome)","Visual field testing and electrophysiology to quantify functional deficit"],"redFlags":["Sudden reduction in vision or new floaters suggesting retinal detachment","Bilateral colobomas or associated microphthalmos indicating a syndromic disorder","Signs of CHARGE association (choanal atresia, cardiac or ear anomalies)","Proteinuria or renal impairment raising suspicion of papillorenal syndrome"],"prognosis":"Visual outcome is highly variable and depends on the extent of the coloboma and macular involvement; some patients retain good acuity while others have marked impairment. The lifelong risk of retinal detachment warrants ongoing surveillance.","pitfalls":["Confusing a coloboma with the morning glory anomaly, which alters both prognosis and syndromic associations.","Overlooking systemic disease, particularly renal and cardiac anomalies, by not screening for associated syndromes.","Attributing the excavation to glaucoma and treating intraocular pressure unnecessarily.","Missing an evolving retinal detachment because the colobomatous anatomy obscures the break."],"management":["Optimise vision with cycloplegic refraction, correction of refractive error and amblyopia therapy in children, as there is no treatment for the coloboma itself","Maintain lifelong surveillance for retinal detachment arising from the fragile intercalary membrane, with prompt vitreoretinal surgical repair when it occurs","Evaluate for syndromic associations including CHARGE and papillorenal (renal coloboma) syndrome, with renal ultrasound and cardiac and ENT assessment","Offer genetic testing including PAX6 and relevant panels with genetic counselling, given autosomal dominant inheritance with variable expressivity","Provide low-vision aids and rehabilitation where acuity is impaired","Arrange multidisciplinary paediatric and ophthalmology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"optic_nerve_hypoplasia":{"name":"Optic nerve hypoplasia","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ONH","hypoplastic optic disc","optic disc hypoplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski / Taylor & Hoyt Paediatric Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A leading cause of congenital visual impairment, characterised by an abnormally small optic disc with a reduced complement of retinal ganglion cell axons","The classic double-ring sign is a pale, small disc surrounded by a ring of pigment marking where the normal disc margin should lie","May be unilateral or bilateral; vision ranges from near normal to severe impairment, often with nystagmus in bilateral cases","Frequently associated with midline brain defects and hypopituitarism, forming part of septo-optic dysplasia","Endocrine assessment is essential because unrecognised cortisol or thyroid deficiency can be life-threatening in infancy","Neuroimaging with MRI evaluates the optic nerves, chiasm, septum pellucidum and pituitary","Risk factors include young maternal age and gestational exposures, though most cases are sporadic"],"pathophysiology":"Optic nerve hypoplasia results from a deficiency of retinal ganglion cell axons, arising either from failure of adequate axon generation or from excessive regression during development. Because optic nerve development is intimately linked with midline forebrain and hypothalamic-pituitary formation, the same developmental disturbance often produces associated midline brain malformations and pituitary dysfunction. The small disc has fewer nerve fibres, and the surrounding retinal pigment epithelium and lamina cribrosa create the double-ring appearance. Visual deficit reflects the degree of axonal deficiency, while the systemic consequences depend on the extent of hypothalamic-pituitary and midline involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Optic atrophy of other causes","Optic disc coloboma or morning glory anomaly","Megalopapilla with pseudo-small disc appearance","Tilted disc syndrome","Delayed visual maturation","Cortical visual impairment","Leber congenital amaurosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain, orbits and pituitary region","Full endocrine evaluation including cortisol, thyroid, growth hormone and prolactin axes","OCT of the retinal nerve fibre layer where cooperation allows","Visual evoked potentials and electrophysiology","Cycloplegic refraction and orthoptic assessment","Developmental and paediatric review"],"redFlags":["Neonatal hypoglycaemia or adrenal crisis from unrecognised hypopituitarism","Prolonged jaundice, micropenis or growth failure suggesting endocrine deficiency","Diabetes insipidus indicating hypothalamic involvement","Developmental regression or seizures suggesting broader brain malformation","Temperature instability pointing to hypothalamic dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Visual outcome varies widely from good to severely impaired and is generally stable, but overall prognosis and safety hinge on timely recognition and treatment of associated endocrine deficiencies.","pitfalls":["Failing to arrange endocrine assessment and missing dangerous hormone deficiencies","Assuming unilateral disease excludes systemic associations","Overlooking the double-ring sign and misdiagnosing optic atrophy","Not imaging the brain to identify midline and pituitary anomalies"],"management":["Undertake urgent endocrine evaluation and institute hormone replacement, including hydrocortisone for cortisol deficiency, levothyroxine for hypothyroidism and growth hormone, as unrecognised deficiency can be life-threatening in infancy","Image with MRI to define midline brain, chiasm, septum pellucidum and pituitary anomalies within the septo-optic dysplasia spectrum","Optimise vision with cycloplegic refraction, correction of refractive error and amblyopia management in unilateral cases","Provide low-vision aids and rehabilitation and manage associated nystagmus","Arrange developmental assessment and early intervention services","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across paediatric endocrinology, ophthalmology, neurology and developmental paediatrics","Offer genetic counselling where an underlying genetic cause is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Taylor & Hoyt's Paediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"optic_neuritis":{"name":"Optic neuritis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ON","retrobulbar neuritis","papillitis","optic nerve inflammation","demyelinating optic neuritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents in a young adult (more often female) with subacute unilateral visual loss developing over hours to days, accompanied by pain worse on eye movement","A relative afferent pupillary defect is present, and colour vision (especially red desaturation) is impaired out of proportion to acuity","In retrobulbar neuritis the optic disc looks normal at onset ('the patient sees nothing and the doctor sees nothing'), whereas papillitis shows a swollen disc","It is commonly the presenting or an associated feature of multiple sclerosis, so demyelination should be considered","MRI of the brain and orbits with contrast assesses the optic nerve and detects demyelinating white-matter lesions that predict multiple sclerosis risk","Vision usually recovers spontaneously over weeks; intravenous corticosteroids speed recovery but do not change final visual outcome","Atypical features (bilateral, no pain, poor recovery, severe disc swelling) should prompt consideration of neuromyelitis optica, MOG-associated disease, or an infective/compressive cause"],"pathophysiology":"Optic neuritis is inflammation and demyelination of the optic nerve, most commonly immune-mediated and associated with multiple sclerosis. An inflammatory, often T-cell-driven process attacks the myelin sheath of optic nerve axons, disrupting conduction and causing loss of visual acuity, colour vision, and contrast. Because conduction rather than the axon itself is initially affected, remyelination allows substantial recovery, though repeated or severe attacks cause axonal loss and permanent deficits with optic atrophy. Antibody-mediated variants (aquaporin-4 in neuromyelitis optica, MOG antibody disease) and infective or granulomatous causes produce similar inflammation through different mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy (arteritic and non-arteritic)","Neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder","MOG-antibody-associated optic neuritis","Compressive optic neuropathy from tumour or aneurysm","Toxic, nutritional, or hereditary (Leber) optic neuropathy","Central retinal artery occlusion and other causes of acute visual loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assessment of visual acuity, colour vision, and relative afferent pupillary defect","Visual field testing (commonly shows a central scotoma)","Contrast MRI of the brain and orbits to assess the nerve and demyelinating lesions","Aquaporin-4 and MOG antibody testing in atypical or severe cases","OCT of the retinal nerve fibre layer to monitor atrophy; bloods/lumbar puncture where an infective or inflammatory cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Bilateral simultaneous, severe, or poorly recovering visual loss suggesting neuromyelitis optica or a non-demyelinating cause","Absence of pain or a progressive course pointing to a compressive or ischaemic optic neuropathy","Systemic features suggesting infection, sarcoidosis, or vasculitis","Older patient with headache and jaw claudication — exclude giant cell arteritis as a cause of ischaemic optic neuropathy"],"prognosis":"Most patients with typical demyelinating optic neuritis recover good vision over several weeks regardless of steroid use, though some residual colour and contrast deficits and a risk of future multiple sclerosis remain.","pitfalls":["Attributing every acute optic neuropathy to demyelination and missing arteritic ischaemic optic neuropathy in older patients","Assuming steroids improve the final visual outcome rather than just the speed of recovery","Overlooking neuromyelitis optica or MOG disease in atypical or severe cases that need different management","Failing to counsel on and investigate the associated risk of multiple sclerosis"],"management":["Treat acute demyelinating attacks with high-dose intravenous corticosteroid (methylprednisolone) to accelerate visual recovery, avoiding low-dose oral corticosteroid monotherapy which raises the recurrence rate","Reserve plasma exchange for severe, steroid-refractory attacks, particularly in aquaporin-4 or MOG antibody-associated disease","In antibody-mediated relapsing disease (neuromyelitis optica spectrum), use long-term immunosuppression such as rituximab, eculizumab or mycophenolate to prevent relapses","Where MRI shows demyelinating white-matter lesions predicting multiple sclerosis, refer for neurologist-led disease-modifying therapy","Monitor visual acuity, colour vision and the retinal nerve fibre layer on OCT to track recovery and detect optic atrophy","Provide neurology and neuro-ophthalmology follow-up with contrast MRI surveillance for conversion to multiple sclerosis","Give supportive symptomatic care and reassurance regarding the usual spontaneous recovery over weeks"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"oral_cancer":{"name":"Oral cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oral cavity cancer","oral squamous cell carcinoma","mouth cancer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most oral cavity cancers are squamous cell carcinomas driven by tobacco in all its forms, betel or areca nut chewing, and heavy alcohol use, which act synergistically","It typically presents as a persistent ulcer, red or white patch, lump, or non-healing sore in the mouth, often with pain, bleeding, or loose teeth as disease advances","Any oral lesion that fails to heal within a few weeks should be biopsied rather than treated repeatedly as trauma or infection","Premalignant lesions such as leukoplakia, erythroplakia, and oral submucous fibrosis carry malignant potential and warrant monitoring and biopsy","Cervical lymph node metastasis is common and significantly worsens prognosis, so neck examination and imaging are integral to staging","Surgical resection with adequate margins is the mainstay for most tumours, often combined with neck dissection and reconstruction depending on extent","Radiotherapy, with or without chemotherapy, is used adjuvantly for adverse features or as definitive treatment in selected cases","Treatment can markedly affect speech, swallowing, and appearance, so multidisciplinary rehabilitation and dental assessment before radiotherapy are important"],"pathophysiology":"Oral squamous cell carcinoma arises from the mucosal lining of the oral cavity through cumulative genetic damage caused by carcinogens in tobacco, areca nut, and alcohol. This produces field cancerisation with premalignant changes such as leukoplakia and erythroplakia that can progress through dysplasia to invasive carcinoma. The tumour invades local structures including muscle, bone, and nerves, and spreads readily to regional cervical lymph nodes; distant metastasis occurs later. Chronic mucosal irritation and conditions such as oral submucous fibrosis further predispose to malignant change.","additionalDifferentials":["Traumatic ulcer from dentures or a sharp tooth","Aphthous ulceration or other benign oral ulcers","Oral leukoplakia or erythroplakia without invasion","Oral lichen planus","Infective causes such as fungal or specific granulomatous lesions","Salivary gland or other non-squamous oral tumours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thorough inspection and palpation of the oral cavity and neck with biopsy of the lesion","CT or MRI to assess local extent, bony invasion, and nodal disease","Chest imaging and PET-CT for distant staging and second primaries","Examination under anaesthesia and panendoscopy where indicated to map extent and exclude synchronous tumours","Dental assessment and nutritional evaluation before treatment"],"redFlags":["A mouth ulcer or lesion that fails to heal within a few weeks","A firm, fixed, or enlarging cervical lymph node","Unexplained loosening of teeth, numbness, or difficulty moving the tongue","Persistent oral bleeding, pain, or difficulty swallowing with weight loss"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on stage, tumour thickness, and nodal status; early localised tumours have good cure rates, whereas nodal metastasis, deep invasion, and advanced stage substantially reduce survival and increase recurrence.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating a persistent oral ulcer as trauma or infection without biopsy","Neglecting to examine and image the cervical lymph nodes","Overlooking premalignant lesions that require surveillance","Failing to arrange dental and rehabilitation input before and after treatment"],"management":["Perform wide surgical resection with tumour-free margins as the mainstay for resectable disease, with neck dissection for nodal spread and microvascular reconstruction","Add adjuvant radiotherapy, combined with concurrent platinum-based chemotherapy (cisplatin) for high-risk features such as positive margins or extracapsular nodal extension","Use primary radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy where surgery is unsuitable or for organ preservation","Treat recurrent or metastatic disease with systemic therapy using EGFR-directed cetuximab or immune checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab, nivolumab)","Enforce cessation of tobacco, areca nut and alcohol, with pre-treatment dental assessment and nutritional support including enteral feeding","Provide speech and swallowing rehabilitation and analgesia within a head and neck multidisciplinary team","Maintain long-term surveillance for locoregional recurrence and second primaries given field cancerisation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology"],"crossLinks":[]},"oral_candidiasis":{"name":"Oral candidiasis","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oral thrush","oral candidosis","candidal stomatitis","moniliasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Oral candidiasis is usually an opportunistic infection by Candida species, most often Candida albicans, and typically signals a local or systemic predisposing factor","The pseudomembranous form (thrush) shows white curd-like plaques that wipe off to reveal an erythematous, sometimes bleeding, base","Erythematous (atrophic) candidiasis presents as a red, sore mucosa or a depapillated tongue, and is common under dentures or after antibiotics","Angular cheilitis at the mouth corners frequently coexists and reflects candidal and bacterial colonisation with local moisture","Denture stomatitis appears as erythema confined to the denture-bearing palatal mucosa and relates to poor denture hygiene and continuous wear","Common predisposing factors include inhaled or systemic corticosteroids, broad-spectrum antibiotics, xerostomia, diabetes, and immunosuppression","Management combines antifungal therapy with correction of the underlying cause, including denture hygiene and control of dry mouth","Chronic hyperplastic candidiasis presents as an adherent white patch that will not rub off and carries a small malignant potential warranting biopsy"],"pathophysiology":"Candida species are common oral commensals kept in check by intact mucosal defences, salivary factors, and the resident microbiota. Disruption of this balance allows overgrowth and conversion to an invasive hyphal form that adheres to and penetrates the epithelium. Local factors such as denture coverage, dry mouth, and topical steroids, together with systemic factors such as antibiotics, immunosuppression, diabetes, and nutritional deficiency, create the conditions for opportunistic infection. The clinical pattern (pseudomembranous, erythematous, or hyperplastic) reflects the host state and chronicity rather than different organisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Oral leukoplakia and other keratotic white lesions","Lichen planus and lichenoid reactions","Geographic tongue for the atrophic form","Chemical or thermal mucosal burns","Mucosal manifestations of anaemia or nutritional deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Oral swab, smear, or oral rinse for microscopy and culture","Biopsy for adherent white patches to assess hyperplastic candidiasis and dysplasia","Blood glucose and screening for diabetes","Haematinics (iron, folate, B12) where atrophic or recurrent disease","Evaluation for immunodeficiency, including HIV, in unexplained or severe cases"],"redFlags":["Unexplained or severe oral candidiasis in a previously healthy adult raising suspicion of undiagnosed HIV or other immunodeficiency","Chronic hyperplastic candidiasis with dysplasia and potential for malignant transformation","Extension into the oesophagus with dysphagia suggesting significant immunosuppression"],"prognosis":"Most acute candidiasis responds well to antifungal treatment when predisposing factors are corrected, but recurrence is common if the underlying cause persists, and chronic hyperplastic lesions require monitoring for dysplasia.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent candidiasis without seeking and correcting the underlying predisposing factor","Assuming an adherent white patch is thrush when it is hyperplastic candidiasis or leukoplakia needing biopsy","Overlooking denture hygiene and continuous wear as the driver of denture stomatitis","Missing an immunocompromising condition in unexplained or florid infection"],"management":["Give topical antifungals (nystatin suspension or miconazole oral gel) for localised disease, and a systemic azole (fluconazole) for extensive, refractory or immunocompromised cases","Correct local predisposing factors: rinse the mouth after inhaled corticosteroids, improve denture hygiene, remove dentures overnight, and manage xerostomia","Treat coexisting angular cheilitis with a topical antifungal, adding antibacterial cover where staphylococcal or streptococcal colonisation is present","Address systemic drivers by optimising glycaemic control in diabetes, correcting iron, folate and B12 deficiency, and reviewing broad-spectrum antibiotic and corticosteroid use","For denture stomatitis, disinfect the denture and apply antifungal to its fitting surface alongside hygiene measures","Biopsy chronic hyperplastic (adherent) plaques and monitor for dysplasia","Investigate for underlying immunodeficiency including HIV in unexplained, severe or recurrent disease and refer accordingly"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"oral_facial_digital_syndrome":{"name":"Oral-facial-digital syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["orofaciodigital syndrome","OFD syndrome","OFD1","OFD type I (Papillon-Leage-Psaume)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of ciliopathies defined by malformations of the oral cavity, face, and digits, with several distinct subtypes differing in inheritance and associated features","Oral findings include lobulated or bifid tongue with hamartomas, multiple frenula, cleft palate or lip, and dental anomalies","Facial features may include hypertelorism, a broad or bifid nasal tip, and micrognathia, while digital anomalies include brachydactyly, syndactyly, clinodactyly, and polydactyly","Type I is X-linked dominant, caused by OFD1 variants and typically lethal in males, and is frequently associated with polycystic kidney disease and central nervous system malformation","Central nervous system involvement can include agenesis of the corpus callosum, cerebellar and posterior fossa anomalies including the molar tooth sign, and intracerebral cysts","As a ciliopathy it overlaps with Joubert syndrome and other disorders, and the OFD1 gene product localises to the centriole and basal body of the primary cilium","Diagnosis integrates the oral-facial-digital pattern with imaging of brain and kidneys and molecular confirmation for subtype classification and counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Oral-facial-digital syndromes are ciliopathies in which defective primary cilium and basal body function disrupts developmental signalling that patterns midline craniofacial structures, the palate and tongue, and the limbs. The prototypic type I results from variants in OFD1, encoding a centriolar and basal body protein; because the gene is X-linked, hemizygous males are usually not viable while heterozygous females show variable manifestations owing to X-inactivation. Impaired ciliary signalling in the kidney predisposes to cystic renal disease, and in the developing brain it contributes to callosal, cerebellar, and other midline malformations.","additionalDifferentials":["Joubert syndrome and related disorders","Pallister-Hall syndrome","Ellis-van Creveld syndrome","Meckel-Gruber syndrome","Mohr syndrome (OFD type II) versus other OFD subtypes","Isolated cleft palate with limb anomaly"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed dysmorphology and oral examination to document tongue, frenula, and palatal anomalies","Radiographs of the hands and feet to characterise digital malformations","Brain MRI for callosal, cerebellar, and posterior fossa anomalies including the molar tooth sign","Renal ultrasound to detect polycystic or cystic kidney disease","Molecular genetic testing including OFD1 and a ciliopathy gene panel","Developmental assessment where intellectual disability is present"],"redFlags":["Progressive polycystic kidney disease leading to renal failure","Significant central nervous system malformation with neurological impairment or seizures","Airway or feeding compromise from severe oral and palatal malformation","Male fetal or neonatal loss suggesting an X-linked dominant lethal genotype"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies by subtype and organ involvement. Many females with type I have a normal or near-normal lifespan but may develop progressive polycystic kidney disease, while central nervous system malformation and severe systemic involvement worsen the outlook in some subtypes.","pitfalls":["Classifying an affected child into the wrong OFD subtype and giving inaccurate prognostic and recurrence advice","Overlooking the ciliopathy association with progressive renal cystic disease requiring long-term surveillance","Missing subtle central nervous system malformation without dedicated brain imaging","Attributing oral hamartomas or extra frenula to isolated dental problems"],"management":["Surgically repair oral and craniofacial anomalies including cleft lip and palate closure, excision of tongue hamartomas and division of accessory frenula","Correct digital malformations such as syndactyly and polydactyly with orthopaedic and hand-surgery input","Provide longitudinal renal surveillance for cystic or polycystic kidney disease, managing hypertension with renin-angiotensin blockade and planning kidney replacement therapy as function declines","Offer neurodevelopmental support and manage associated CNS malformations, with early-intervention and educational services for intellectual disability","Coordinate dental and orthodontic care for dental anomalies and malocclusion","Involve a multidisciplinary craniofacial and clinical-genetics team","Provide genetic counselling and family screening given X-linked dominant (type I) inheritance, with molecular and prenatal testing where appropriate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Oral-Facial-Digital Syndrome Type I"],"crossLinks":[]},"oral_facial_digital_syndrome_type_1":{"name":"Oral-facial-digital syndrome type 1","system":"Multisystem developmental (craniofacial / renal / CNS)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OFD1 syndrome","Oral-facial-digital syndrome type I","Papillon-Léage-Psaume syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and paediatric nephrology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked dominant ciliopathy caused by variants in OFD1 on the X chromosome, encoding a centrosome and basal body protein essential for primary cilium assembly; it is typically lethal in hemizygous males, so affected individuals are almost all female.","Defined by the triad of oral, facial and digital anomalies.","Oral features include a lobulated or bifid tongue, tongue hamartomas, multiple hyperplastic oral frenula, cleft palate and dental anomalies.","Facial features include hypertelorism, a broad or flat nasal bridge, frontal bossing and facial milia in infancy.","Digital anomalies include brachydactyly, syndactyly, clinodactyly and pre-axial or post-axial polydactyly.","Central nervous system involvement may include agenesis of the corpus callosum, cerebellar and posterior fossa anomalies, intracerebral cysts and variable intellectual disability.","Adult-onset polycystic kidney disease is an important late manifestation that requires lifelong renal surveillance.","Allelic OFD1 variants can cause distinct disorders in males, such as Joubert syndrome, Simpson-Golabi-Behmel type 2 and X-linked retinitis pigmentosa."],"pathophysiology":"OFD1 is located on the X chromosome and encodes a protein that localises to centrosomes and the basal bodies of primary cilia, where it controls centriole length and is required for ciliogenesis and left-right axis determination; oral-facial-digital syndrome type 1 is therefore a ciliopathy. Loss-of-function variants disrupt assembly of the primary cilium and impair ciliary signalling pathways, including sonic hedgehog signalling, which are essential during morphogenesis of the oral structures, limbs, brain and kidney. The condition is inherited in an X-linked dominant manner with lethality in hemizygous males, usually as early pregnancy loss, so nearly all surviving affected individuals are female; skewed X-inactivation contributes to the wide variability in phenotype among females. Renal involvement reflects the same ciliary defect in tubular epithelium and characteristically emerges as adult-onset polycystic kidney disease, distinguishing the renal course from the early structural anomalies seen elsewhere.","additionalDifferentials":["Other oral-facial-digital syndromes (for example Mohr syndrome / OFD type 2 and further subtypes)","Joubert syndrome and related molar-tooth disorders","Meckel-Gruber syndrome","Pallister-Hall syndrome","Ellis-van Creveld syndrome","Bardet-Biedl syndrome and other ciliopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of OFD1, with attention to X-inactivation patterns in females","Brain MRI for corpus callosum, cerebellar and posterior fossa anomalies and intracerebral cysts","Renal ultrasound with longitudinal surveillance for cyst development","Hand and skeletal radiographs to characterise the digital anomalies","Detailed oral, dental and craniofacial assessment","Developmental and cognitive assessment","Family pedigree including a history of recurrent male pregnancy loss"],"redFlags":["Recurrent early male pregnancy loss within the family","Development of polycystic kidneys with declining kidney function over time","Structural brain anomalies with hydrocephalus or seizures","Feeding or airway compromise from severe oral or craniofacial anomalies"],"prognosis":"The outlook depends chiefly on the severity of central nervous system and renal involvement. Many affected females survive into adulthood but remain at risk of progressive polycystic kidney disease and kidney failure, so lifelong renal monitoring is warranted.","pitfalls":["Treating an isolated cleft palate, oral frenula or a lobulated tongue without recognising the wider syndrome","Failing to institute long-term renal surveillance because kidney cysts appear later in life","Not appreciating the X-linked dominant, male-lethal inheritance during genetic counselling","Overlooking subtle facial milia and frenular anomalies as diagnostic clues"],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy exists; care is supportive with surgical repair of oral anomalies such as cleft palate, tongue lobulation and hamartomas, and hyperplastic frenula","Correct digital anomalies (syndactyly, polydactyly, brachydactyly) with hand-surgery and orthopaedic input","Maintain lifelong renal surveillance for progressive polycystic kidney disease, using renin-angiotensin blockade for hypertension and planning kidney replacement therapy as function declines","Arrange brain imaging and neurodevelopmental support for corpus callosum, cerebellar and posterior fossa anomalies and intellectual disability","Coordinate dental, orthodontic and craniofacial multidisciplinary care","Provide genetic counselling and family screening for the X-linked dominant OFD1 variant, including X-inactivation assessment in females and prenatal counselling given male lethality"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics references","Paediatric nephrology reference texts","Ciliopathy and dysmorphology references"],"crossLinks":[]},"oral_leukoplakia":{"name":"Oral leukoplakia","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["leukoplakia","oral white patch","potentially malignant white lesion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Leukoplakia is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion: a white patch that cannot be wiped off and cannot be attributed to any other definable lesion","It is regarded as an oral potentially malignant disorder, meaning it carries an increased risk of progression to squamous cell carcinoma","Tobacco use, including smokeless forms, areca nut, and alcohol are the principal risk factors","Homogeneous, uniformly white, flat lesions carry lower risk than non-homogeneous, nodular, or speckled (erythroleukoplakia) lesions","Speckled lesions with a red component and lesions on the floor of mouth or ventrolateral tongue have a higher malignant potential","Proliferative verrucous leukoplakia is a distinct, multifocal, treatment-resistant form with high transformation risk","Biopsy is essential to assess for and grade epithelial dysplasia, which is the key predictor of malignant change","Management combines removal of risk factors, treatment or surveillance of the lesion, and long-term monitoring for recurrence or transformation"],"pathophysiology":"Leukoplakia reflects an alteration in the oral epithelium in which increased keratin production and epithelial thickening scatter light to produce a white appearance that does not rub off. Chronic exposure to carcinogens such as tobacco and areca nut drives genetic and epigenetic changes in basal keratinocytes, which may manifest histologically as hyperkeratosis with or without epithelial dysplasia. Dysplastic change represents disordered maturation and cellular atypia that can accumulate over time, and lesions with higher grades of dysplasia carry a greater likelihood of progressing to invasive squamous cell carcinoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Frictional or traumatic keratosis","Chronic hyperplastic candidiasis","Oral lichen planus","Leukoedema","White sponge naevus","Established oral squamous cell carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Incisional or excisional biopsy for histological grading of dysplasia","Careful documentation of site, size, and clinical pattern","Removal of local irritants and reassessment to exclude frictional keratosis","Fungal investigation where hyperplastic candidiasis is suspected","Adjunctive imaging or referral when carcinoma is suspected"],"redFlags":["Non-homogeneous, speckled, or verrucous lesions and any red component, indicating higher malignant potential","Ulceration, induration, or a developing mass suggesting transformation to carcinoma","High-risk sites such as the floor of mouth and ventrolateral tongue","Presence of moderate to severe epithelial dysplasia on biopsy"],"prognosis":"Many lesions remain stable or regress after risk factors are removed, but a proportion undergo malignant transformation, and the risk rises with non-homogeneous appearance, high-risk sites, and higher grades of dysplasia, making long-term surveillance important.","pitfalls":["Labelling a white patch as benign without biopsy or follow-up","Assuming absence of symptoms means absence of risk","Sampling only a homogeneous area and missing dysplasia in a red or nodular component","Failing to address tobacco, areca nut, and alcohol use that perpetuate the lesion"],"management":["Achieve cessation of tobacco (including smokeless forms), areca nut and alcohol, and remove local mechanical irritants","Perform incisional biopsy to grade dysplasia and exclude invasive carcinoma, using excisional biopsy for small lesions","Excise high-grade dysplastic, non-homogeneous or high-risk-site lesions (floor of mouth, ventrolateral tongue) by surgery or laser","Maintain long-term clinical surveillance with photographic documentation and re-biopsy of changing lesions given the malignant-transformation risk","Treat proliferative verrucous leukoplakia aggressively with close follow-up owing to its high transformation rate","Refer to oral medicine or head and neck services when carcinoma is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"oral_lichen_planus":{"name":"Oral lichen planus","system":"Oral cavity","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OLP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oral medicine and dermatology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic immune-mediated mucosal disorder presenting with bilateral, often symmetrical, white striae, plaques, atrophic or erosive lesions","The reticular form with lacy white Wickham striae is frequently asymptomatic, whereas erosive and atrophic forms cause pain and burning","The buccal mucosa, tongue and gingivae are commonly involved, and desquamative gingivitis may be the presenting feature","A lichenoid reaction that is clinically similar can be caused by drugs, dental materials or graft-versus-host disease","There is a small but recognised risk of malignant transformation, so long-term monitoring is advised","Topical corticosteroids are the mainstay for symptomatic disease, with other immunomodulators for refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Oral lichen planus is a T-cell-mediated chronic inflammatory condition in which cytotoxic T lymphocytes target basal keratinocytes of the mucosal epithelium, producing basal cell damage and a band-like subepithelial lymphocytic infiltrate. The resulting epithelial changes create the characteristic striae, atrophy and erosions. Similar lichenoid reactions can be triggered by drugs, contact with dental restorative materials or systemic graft-versus-host disease, reflecting a shared final immunological pathway.","additionalDifferentials":["Oral lichenoid drug or contact reaction","Chronic graft-versus-host disease","Mucous membrane pemphigoid","Pemphigus vulgaris","Lupus erythematosus of the mucosa","Oral leukoplakia or dysplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of distribution and morphology","Biopsy with histopathology, and direct immunofluorescence to exclude immunobullous disease","Review of medications and dental materials for lichenoid triggers","Long-term surveillance for malignant change, especially in erosive disease","Assessment for cutaneous or other mucosal lichen planus"],"redFlags":["Non-homogeneous, indurated or non-healing areas suggesting dysplasia or carcinoma","Rapidly changing or ulcerated lesions","Widespread erosive disease impairing nutrition","Features suggesting immunobullous disease requiring different management"],"prognosis":"The condition is chronic with relapses and remissions; symptoms are usually controllable, but erosive forms carry a small long-term risk of malignant transformation requiring surveillance.","pitfalls":["Not distinguishing lichen planus from a lichenoid drug or contact reaction","Missing an immunobullous disorder presenting as desquamative gingivitis","Failing to monitor for malignant transformation","Overlooking coexisting skin or genital lichen planus"],"management":["Use topical corticosteroids as first-line therapy for symptomatic erosive or atrophic disease","Add topical calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus) for lesions refractory to topical steroids","Reserve systemic corticosteroids or steroid-sparing immunomodulators for severe or widespread refractory disease","Provide symptomatic measures: analgesic or anaesthetic mouthwashes, meticulous oral hygiene, avoidance of spicy and acidic foods, and treatment of superimposed candidiasis","Identify and remove lichenoid triggers such as culprit drugs and adjacent dental restorative materials","Maintain long-term surveillance for malignant transformation, particularly in erosive disease","Refer to oral medicine or dermatology and assess for cutaneous and other mucosal lichen planus"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Oral medicine reference texts (standard reference)","Dermatology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"oral_squamous_cell_carcinoma":{"name":"Oral squamous cell carcinoma","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oral cancer","oral SCC","mouth cancer","squamous cell carcinoma of the oral cavity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Oral squamous cell carcinoma is the most common malignancy of the oral cavity and often presents as a non-healing ulcer, lump, or persistent red or white patch","Classic worrying features are an indurated ulcer with rolled margins, fixation to underlying tissue, and failure to heal beyond two to three weeks","Tobacco in all forms, areca nut, and alcohol are the dominant risk factors and act synergistically","The lateral and ventral tongue and the floor of mouth are high-risk sites, though any oral surface can be affected","Early lesions may be painless, so any persistent oral abnormality warrants a high index of suspicion and referral","Cervical lymphadenopathy, especially firm or fixed nodes, may indicate regional metastatic spread","Definitive diagnosis requires biopsy, and staging guides treatment involving surgery, radiotherapy, and chemotherapy as appropriate","Prognosis depends heavily on stage at diagnosis, underscoring the value of early detection and opportunistic screening"],"pathophysiology":"Oral squamous cell carcinoma develops through the accumulation of genetic and epigenetic alterations in oral keratinocytes, typically driven by chronic exposure to carcinogens such as tobacco, areca nut, and alcohol. Many tumours arise within or adjacent to potentially malignant disorders such as leukoplakia or erythroplakia, progressing through increasing grades of epithelial dysplasia to carcinoma in situ and then invasive disease when malignant cells breach the basement membrane. The invasive tumour infiltrates local tissue, can destroy bone, and spreads via lymphatics to cervical nodes, with distant metastasis in advanced disease. Field change means the whole mucosa is at risk of further primaries.","additionalDifferentials":["Traumatic ulcer","Recurrent aphthous ulceration","Oral leukoplakia or erythroplakia without invasion","Chronic infective ulcers such as tuberculosis or syphilis","Erosive lichen planus","Salivary gland or other oral malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Incisional biopsy of the lesion for histological diagnosis and grading","Examination and imaging of the neck for lymph node involvement","Cross-sectional imaging (CT, MRI) to assess local extent and staging","Assessment for distant metastasis and synchronous primaries","Multidisciplinary evaluation for treatment planning"],"redFlags":["Any oral ulcer or lesion persisting beyond two to three weeks requires urgent specialist referral to exclude cancer","Induration, fixation, rolled margins, or spontaneous bleeding of a lesion","Unexplained firm or fixed cervical lymphadenopathy","Persistent unexplained pain, numbness, loosening of teeth, or difficulty swallowing"],"prognosis":"Outcome is strongly stage-dependent, with early localised disease carrying a favourable prognosis, whereas advanced tumours with nodal involvement have substantially poorer survival and higher morbidity from treatment.","pitfalls":["Attributing a persistent ulcer to trauma or denture irritation and delaying biopsy","Reassurance because a lesion is painless, since early cancers are often symptomless","Failing to examine and palpate the neck for nodal disease","Overlooking the risk of second primaries in a field-changed mucosa"],"management":["Perform wide surgical excision with tumour-free margins as the primary treatment, with neck dissection for nodal disease and microvascular reconstruction","Add adjuvant radiotherapy, with concurrent cisplatin-based chemoradiotherapy for adverse features such as positive margins or extracapsular extension","Use definitive radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy where the tumour is unresectable or surgery is declined","Offer palliative systemic therapy with cetuximab or immune checkpoint inhibitors for recurrent or metastatic disease","Enforce cessation of tobacco, areca nut and alcohol, with pre-treatment dental review, nutritional optimisation and prosthetic, speech and swallowing rehabilitation","Manage within a head and neck multidisciplinary team for staging and treatment planning","Provide structured surveillance for locoregional recurrence and metachronous second primaries owing to field change"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"oral_submucous_fibrosis":{"name":"Oral submucous fibrosis","system":"Oral cavity","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OSMF","oral submucosal fibrosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oral medicine and oral pathology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic progressive fibrosing disease of the oral mucosa strongly linked to areca (betel) nut chewing","Patients develop burning on eating spicy food, blanching and stiffening of the mucosa and progressive limitation of mouth opening","Palpable fibrous bands in the buccal mucosa and a rigid, pale mucosa are characteristic","It is a recognised oral potentially malignant disorder with a significant risk of squamous cell carcinoma","It is most prevalent in South and South-East Asian populations and their diaspora","Management includes strict habit cessation, medical measures to improve mouth opening and surgery for severe trismus, with lifelong cancer surveillance"],"pathophysiology":"Oral submucous fibrosis is driven chiefly by arecoline and other alkaloids in areca nut, which stimulate fibroblast proliferation and collagen synthesis while reducing collagen breakdown, leading to excessive deposition of dense fibrous tissue in the lamina propria and submucosa. Chronic inflammation and juxta-epithelial hyalinisation cause progressive mucosal stiffening and trismus, and epithelial atrophy with altered turnover increases susceptibility to malignant transformation. The result is an irreversible fibrotic and potentially premalignant state.","additionalDifferentials":["Scleroderma with orofacial involvement","Oral leukoplakia or erythroplakia","Oral lichen planus","Radiation-induced fibrosis","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of mouth opening and fibrous bands","Biopsy to confirm fibrosis and exclude dysplasia or carcinoma","Detailed habit history of areca nut and tobacco use","Regular surveillance for malignant change","Assessment of nutritional status and anaemia where relevant"],"redFlags":["Development of a non-healing ulcer, red patch or mass suggesting carcinoma","Rapidly worsening trismus","Induration or dysplasia on biopsy","Weight loss or difficulty maintaining nutrition"],"prognosis":"The fibrosis is largely irreversible and progressive if the habit continues; cessation and treatment can improve symptoms, but the elevated lifelong risk of oral cancer necessitates ongoing surveillance.","pitfalls":["Failing to counsel firmly on areca nut and tobacco cessation","Overlooking malignant transformation during follow-up","Confusing early disease with lichen planus or leukoplakia","Underestimating the functional impact of progressive trismus"],"management":["Secure complete and permanent cessation of areca (betel) nut and tobacco use as the essential first step","Give intralesional corticosteroid injections, often with hyaluronidase, plus adjuncts such as pentoxifylline and antioxidants (e.g., lycopene) to improve mucosal suppleness","Institute mouth-opening physiotherapy with jaw-stretching exercises and interincisal devices to counter trismus","Undertake surgical release of fibrous bands with grafting or flap coverage for severe, disabling trismus","Correct nutritional deficiencies and anaemia with iron and vitamin supplementation","Maintain lifelong surveillance with biopsy of suspicious areas given the risk of squamous cell carcinoma","Refer to oral and maxillofacial surgery for advanced disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Oral medicine reference texts (standard reference)","Oral pathology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"orbital_apex_syndrome":{"name":"Orbital apex syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Jacod syndrome","orbital apex-optic canal syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by ophthalmoplegia from involvement of the third, fourth, and sixth cranial nerves together with visual loss from optic nerve involvement","The distinguishing feature from superior orbital fissure syndrome is the addition of optic neuropathy, since the optic canal lies at the apex","Sensory loss follows the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve, and proptosis may be present with an orbital mass","A relative afferent pupillary defect and reduced acuity or colour vision reflect the optic nerve component","Common causes include invasive infection such as mucormycosis and aspergillosis, tumour, and inflammatory disease","Aggressive fungal orbital apex infection must be considered urgently in diabetic or immunocompromised patients","Imaging of the orbit and skull base is essential to define the responsible lesion"],"pathophysiology":"The orbital apex is the crowded posterior region of the orbit where the superior orbital fissure and the optic canal converge, transmitting the optic nerve and ophthalmic artery alongside the ocular motor nerves, the ophthalmic trigeminal branches, and sympathetic fibres. A lesion here compresses or infiltrates these structures, combining an ophthalmoplegia and trigeminal sensory disturbance with an optic neuropathy that causes visual loss. Responsible processes include invasive fungal and bacterial infection, neoplasm, granulomatous and other inflammatory disease, trauma, and vascular lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["Superior orbital fissure syndrome without optic nerve involvement","Cavernous sinus syndrome","Tolosa-Hunt granulomatous inflammation","Orbital cellulitis or subperiosteal abscess","Thyroid eye disease with apical crowding","Idiopathic orbital inflammatory disease","Orbital tumour or metastasis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the orbit and skull base with contrast","CT of the orbit and paranasal sinuses to assess bone and sinus disease","Nasoendoscopy and biopsy where invasive fungal infection is suspected","Inflammatory markers and infection screen","Blood glucose and immune status assessment","Visual field testing and assessment of the afferent pupillary pathway"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive signs in a diabetic or immunocompromised patient suggesting invasive fungal infection needing emergency management","Acute visual loss requiring urgent evaluation of the optic nerve","Signs of orbital cellulitis or abscess","Features of malignant infiltration of the skull base"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends heavily on the cause and speed of treatment; invasive infection carries high morbidity and mortality without prompt intervention, whereas inflammatory causes may respond to treatment, though optic nerve damage can leave permanent visual loss.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise optic nerve involvement that distinguishes it from superior orbital fissure syndrome","Delaying diagnosis of invasive fungal infection in an at-risk patient","Attributing the picture to inflammation and giving steroids before excluding infection and tumour","Underestimating the urgency of associated visual loss"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying cause, guided by urgent contrast imaging and biopsy","For invasive fungal infection (mucormycosis, aspergillosis) give emergency systemic antifungal therapy (liposomal amphotericin B) with surgical debridement and correction of diabetes, ketoacidosis and immunosuppression","Treat bacterial infection with broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics and drainage of any collection","Treat inflammatory or granulomatous causes (idiopathic orbital inflammation, IgG4-related disease, sarcoidosis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis) with systemic corticosteroids and steroid-sparing immunosuppression as needed","Manage neoplastic causes with tissue diagnosis followed by surgery, radiotherapy or chemotherapy as appropriate","Monitor visual acuity, colour vision and the afferent pupillary pathway for optic-nerve compromise","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across ophthalmology, ENT, neurology and oncology with urgent escalation for aggressive infection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"orbital_blowout_fracture":{"name":"Orbital blowout fracture","system":"Eye / Orbit and adnexa","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["orbital floor fracture","blowout fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / oculoplastic and maxillofacial references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fracture of the orbital wall, most often the floor or medial wall, from blunt trauma without fracture of the orbital rim","Features include periorbital bruising, diplopia, restricted eye movements (especially vertical), enophthalmos and infraorbital numbness","Vertical diplopia from inferior rectus tethering is typical, and pain or nausea on upgaze suggests muscle entrapment","In children a white-eyed blowout fracture with muscle entrapment and marked oculocardiac reflex is a surgical emergency","CT of the orbit defines the fracture and any herniated or entrapped tissue","Many fractures are managed conservatively, with surgery for significant entrapment, enophthalmos or persistent diplopia"],"pathophysiology":"An orbital blowout fracture occurs when blunt force raises intra-orbital pressure or buckles the orbital walls, fracturing the thin floor or medial wall while sparing the sturdier rim. Orbital fat and the inferior rectus or its surrounding tissue can prolapse or become entrapped in the fracture, causing restricted motility and diplopia, while loss of orbital volume support leads to enophthalmos. Injury to the infraorbital nerve produces cheek and upper-lip numbness. In children the more elastic bone can snap shut and trap muscle (a trapdoor mechanism), causing ischaemia and a marked oculocardiac reflex.","additionalDifferentials":["Orbital rim or complex facial fracture","Traumatic extraocular muscle palsy without entrapment","Retrobulbar haemorrhage","Globe rupture or other ocular trauma","Orbital cellulitis (if delayed presentation with swelling)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT of the orbits and facial bones","Full ophthalmic assessment to exclude globe injury","Assessment of ocular motility, diplopia and enophthalmos","Testing of infraorbital nerve sensation","Monitoring for the oculocardiac reflex, especially in children"],"redFlags":["White-eyed blowout fracture with entrapment and bradycardia in a child (surgical emergency)","Signs of globe rupture or retrobulbar haemorrhage with visual loss","Non-resolving diplopia or significant enophthalmos","A relative afferent pupillary defect indicating optic nerve injury"],"prognosis":"Many blowout fractures do well with conservative care and resolution of early diplopia; timely surgery for entrapment or significant volume change gives good outcomes, whereas delayed treatment of paediatric entrapment risks permanent motility problems.","pitfalls":["Missing a paediatric trapdoor entrapment that needs urgent surgery","Overlooking an associated globe injury","Attributing early diplopia solely to swelling and not reassessing","Failing to warn against nose-blowing, which can cause orbital emphysema"],"management":["Manage many fractures conservatively: advise against nose-blowing, give nasal decongestants, cold compresses and analgesia, and add antibiotics where there is sinus involvement","Undertake surgical orbital wall reconstruction with an implant for significant enophthalmos, large defects or persistent symptomatic diplopia","Treat a paediatric white-eyed trapdoor fracture with muscle entrapment as a surgical emergency requiring prompt release to prevent ischaemic muscle damage","Perform full ophthalmic assessment to exclude globe injury, retinal damage and traumatic optic neuropathy","Monitor ocular motility, diplopia, enophthalmos and infraorbital nerve sensation serially","Watch for the oculocardiac reflex (bradycardia, nausea), particularly in children with entrapment","Refer to oculoplastic and maxillofacial surgery for operative planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Maxillofacial trauma references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"orbital_cellulitis":{"name":"Orbital cellulitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["postseptal cellulitis","post-septal cellulitis","orbital infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A sight- and life-threatening infection of the orbital tissues behind the orbital septum, most often spreading from adjacent (especially ethmoid) sinusitis","Key features distinguishing it from preseptal cellulitis are painful or restricted eye movements (ophthalmoplegia), proptosis, and pain on eye movement","Systemic signs of fever and malaise accompany a red, swollen, tender lid; a relative afferent pupillary defect signals optic nerve compromise","Reduced visual acuity, colour vision loss, or an afferent pupillary defect indicate optic nerve threat and demand emergency intervention","Commonest in children and frequently caused by Staphylococcus, Streptococcus, and Haemophilus species, sometimes anaerobes","Urgent contrast CT of the orbit and sinuses identifies subperiosteal or orbital abscess and sinus involvement","Requires admission and prompt intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics, with surgical drainage of abscesses or sinuses when needed"],"pathophysiology":"Orbital cellulitis is infection of the soft tissues posterior to the orbital septum. Infection most commonly extends directly from the paranasal sinuses (particularly the ethmoid, separated from the orbit only by the thin lamina papyracea), but can also arise from trauma, dacryocystitis, dental infection, or haematogenous spread. Inflammation and oedema within the confined bony orbit raise pressure, displace the globe forward (proptosis), impair extraocular muscle function, and can compress or ischaemically injure the optic nerve. Spread posteriorly risks cavernous sinus thrombosis, meningitis, and intracranial abscess.","additionalDifferentials":["Preseptal (periorbital) cellulitis","Idiopathic orbital inflammation (orbital pseudotumour)","Thyroid eye disease","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Orbital tumour or rhabdomyosarcoma in children","Allergic or traumatic periorbital oedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the orbit, sinuses, and brain to detect abscess and sinus disease","Assessment of visual acuity, colour vision, pupil reactions, and eye movements","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers","Swabs/cultures of any discharge or drained material","Ophthalmology and ENT review; consider MRI if intracranial extension is suspected"],"redFlags":["Emergency — threatens vision (optic nerve compression) and life (cavernous sinus thrombosis, meningitis, intracranial abscess)","Reduced acuity, impaired colour vision, or a relative afferent pupillary defect indicate imminent optic nerve damage","Restricted or painful eye movements and proptosis distinguishing it from benign preseptal cellulitis","Bilateral involvement, drowsiness, or cranial nerve palsies suggesting cavernous sinus or intracranial spread"],"prognosis":"With prompt intravenous antibiotics and timely drainage the outlook is good, but delay can lead to permanent visual loss, intracranial complications, and death.","pitfalls":["Mistaking sight-threatening orbital cellulitis for benign preseptal cellulitis and failing to assess eye movements and optic nerve function","Delaying CT imaging and abscess drainage","Treating as an outpatient rather than admitting for intravenous therapy","Overlooking the underlying sinusitis that needs concurrent treatment"],"management":["Start prompt intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics covering staphylococci (including MRSA), streptococci, Haemophilus and anaerobes","Arrange surgical drainage of subperiosteal or orbital abscess and sinus drainage (functional endoscopic sinus surgery) for abscess or failure to improve on antibiotics","Obtain urgent contrast CT of orbit, sinuses and brain with combined ophthalmology and ENT assessment","Monitor visual acuity, colour vision and pupillary reactions serially, with emergency intervention including orbital decompression if the optic nerve is threatened","Treat the underlying source, most often paranasal sinusitis, with nasal decongestants and supportive analgesia and antipyretics","Escalate to neurosurgery for intracranial extension, cavernous sinus thrombosis or brain abscess"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"orbital_myositis":{"name":"Orbital myositis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["idiopathic orbital myositis","myositic orbital inflammation","extraocular muscle myositis","inflammatory orbital myositis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An inflammatory disorder of one or more extraocular muscles, usually considered a myositic subtype of idiopathic orbital inflammation","Typical presentation is with acute painful diplopia, pain worsened by eye movement, redness over the involved muscle insertion, and restricted motility","It is frequently unilateral and often affects a single muscle, most commonly a horizontal rectus","On orbital imaging the affected muscle is enlarged with characteristic involvement of the tendon, in contrast to the tendon-sparing enlargement of thyroid eye disease","Injection and swelling are often most marked over the insertion of the inflamed muscle","Most cases respond briskly to systemic corticosteroids, with relief of pain and improvement in movement, though recurrence can occur","Atypical, recurrent, bilateral, or treatment-resistant cases warrant investigation and sometimes biopsy to exclude specific inflammations, IgG4-related disease, vasculitis, and lymphoma","The key clinical distinction from thyroid eye disease is the pain, tendon involvement, and usually single-muscle pattern"],"pathophysiology":"Orbital myositis is inflammation localised to the extraocular muscles, with an infiltrate of lymphocytes and other inflammatory cells causing the muscle belly and, characteristically, its tendon of insertion to swell. The inflamed, oedematous muscle contracts and moves poorly, producing pain on eye movement, diplopia, and restriction, while overlying congestion causes redness at the insertion. It is regarded as a form of idiopathic orbital inflammation confined to muscle, and some cases fall within the IgG4-related disease spectrum or accompany systemic inflammatory disorders. The immune-mediated nature explains the typically rapid response to corticosteroids, whereas persistent or recurrent inflammation can lead to fibrosis with fixed restriction.","additionalDifferentials":["Thyroid eye disease (tendon-sparing muscle enlargement)","Idiopathic orbital inflammation of other patterns","Orbital cellulitis","Carotid-cavernous fistula","Orbital metastasis or lymphoma involving muscle","Systemic vasculitis or IgG4-related orbital disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Orbital CT or MRI demonstrating enlargement of the affected muscle with tendon involvement","Assessment of ocular motility, diplopia, and pain on eye movement","Visual acuity, colour vision, and pupil assessment to exclude optic-nerve involvement","Bloods including inflammatory markers, thyroid function and autoantibodies, and serum IgG4 to exclude mimics","ANCA and systemic workup where vasculitis or systemic disease is suspected","Biopsy in atypical, recurrent, or steroid-unresponsive cases"],"redFlags":["Poor or absent response to corticosteroids suggesting an alternative diagnosis such as lymphoma","Bilateral or recurrent myositis pointing to a systemic or IgG4-related cause","Signs of optic-nerve compromise or marked proptosis","Features suggesting infection or a carotid-cavernous fistula rather than sterile inflammation"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally good, with most single-muscle cases responding promptly to corticosteroids, but recurrences are common and repeated or chronic inflammation may cause muscle fibrosis with persistent diplopia and restriction needing further treatment.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with thyroid eye disease by overlooking the pain and tendon involvement","Mistaking it for orbital cellulitis and treating with antibiotics alone","Attributing a poor steroid response to underdosing rather than reconsidering the diagnosis","Failing to investigate recurrent or bilateral disease for a systemic cause","Missing IgG4-related disease or vasculitis underlying the myositis"],"management":["Use systemic corticosteroids as the mainstay, which usually produce brisk relief of pain and restriction","Consider NSAIDs alone for mild, isolated disease","Add steroid-sparing immunosuppression (methotrexate, mycophenolate) or biologic therapy for recurrent, steroid-dependent or refractory cases","Reserve low-dose orbital radiotherapy for disease refractory to immunosuppression","Confirm the diagnosis with orbital imaging and exclude mimics such as thyroid eye disease and IgG4-related disease before committing to long-term therapy","Monitor for recurrence and fibrosis, and manage residual diplopia with prisms or strabismus surgery once stable","Provide ophthalmology and orbital-specialist follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"orbital_pseudotumor":{"name":"Idiopathic orbital inflammation","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["orbital pseudotumour","idiopathic orbital inflammatory syndrome","nonspecific orbital inflammation","orbital inflammatory pseudotumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign, non-infective inflammatory process of the orbit with no identifiable local or systemic cause, and a diagnosis reached largely by exclusion","It classically presents with acute, painful proptosis, periorbital swelling and redness, chemosis, and restricted eye movements with diplopia","Any orbital structure may be involved, giving subtypes such as myositic, lacrimal (dacryoadenitis), anterior, apical, and diffuse patterns","Involvement of the orbital apex can threaten vision through optic-nerve compression","Imaging shows inflammatory infiltration that may involve muscles including their tendons, the lacrimal gland, or diffuse orbital fat, without a discrete mass","A characteristically rapid and dramatic response to systemic corticosteroids supports the diagnosis, though relapse on withdrawal is common","Atypical, bilateral, recurrent, or steroid-unresponsive cases require biopsy and workup to exclude lymphoma, specific inflammations, and IgG4-related disease","It is distinguished from thyroid eye disease, which is usually less painful and spares muscle tendons, and from orbital cellulitis, which is infective"],"pathophysiology":"Idiopathic orbital inflammation is a polymorphous inflammatory infiltrate of orbital tissues, comprising lymphocytes, plasma cells, and variable fibrosis, that occurs without an identifiable infective, neoplastic, or systemic autoimmune cause. The inflammation may target any orbital component, producing the myositic, dacryoadenitis, anterior, apical, or diffuse patterns, and the resulting swelling and mass effect cause pain, proptosis, congestion, and restriction of eye movement. Some cases represent part of the IgG4-related disease spectrum. The condition is thought to be immune-mediated, which underlies its responsiveness to corticosteroids, but the tendency to relapse and to develop fibrosis reflects an ongoing dysregulated inflammatory drive.","additionalDifferentials":["Thyroid eye disease","Orbital cellulitis","Orbital lymphoma or other neoplasm","IgG4-related orbital disease","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and other systemic vasculitides","Sarcoidosis or carotid-cavernous fistula","Ruptured dermoid cyst causing inflammation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Orbital CT or MRI to define the pattern and extent of inflammation and exclude a discrete mass","Assessment of visual acuity, colour vision, and pupils to detect optic-nerve involvement","Bloods including inflammatory markers, and serum IgG4, with ANCA and thyroid function to exclude mimics","Orbital biopsy in atypical, recurrent, bilateral, or steroid-unresponsive disease","Systemic evaluation for sarcoidosis, vasculitis, or lymphoproliferative disease when indicated","Monitoring of response and relapse during and after corticosteroid treatment"],"redFlags":["Reduced acuity or colour vision suggesting apical optic-nerve compression","Failure to respond promptly to corticosteroids, raising suspicion of lymphoma or specific inflammation","Bilateral or recurrent disease pointing to a systemic or IgG4-related cause","Rapidly progressive proptosis with severe pain and congestion"],"prognosis":"Most patients respond rapidly to corticosteroids with resolution of pain and swelling, but relapses are frequent on tapering, and some develop chronic fibrosis with persistent restriction, proptosis, or visual loss; refractory cases may need steroid-sparing immunosuppression or radiotherapy.","pitfalls":["Labelling an orbital mass idiopathic without excluding lymphoma and specific inflammations by biopsy","Mistaking it for orbital cellulitis and giving antibiotics alone, or vice versa","Attributing a poor steroid response to inadequate dosing rather than an alternative diagnosis","Overlooking IgG4-related disease and systemic vasculitis as underlying causes","Missing apical involvement threatening the optic nerve"],"management":["Use systemic corticosteroids as first-line therapy, which typically produce a rapid, dramatic response supporting the diagnosis","Add steroid-sparing immunosuppression (methotrexate, mycophenolate) or rituximab for frequently relapsing or steroid-dependent disease","Reserve low-dose orbital radiotherapy for refractory cases","Treat urgently when apical involvement threatens the optic nerve","Perform orbital biopsy in atypical, bilateral, recurrent or steroid-unresponsive disease to exclude lymphoma and other mimics","Monitor closely for relapse during and after corticosteroid tapering","Manage under an orbital and ophthalmology specialist, with systemic evaluation for sarcoidosis, vasculitis or IgG4-related disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"orbital_varix":{"name":"Orbital varix","system":"Eye / Orbit and adnexa","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["orbital venous varix"],"chapter":null,"source":"Orbital disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distensible venous malformation of the orbit that enlarges with raised venous pressure","The hallmark is positional or Valsalva-induced proptosis, appearing or worsening on bending, straining or the Valsalva manoeuvre","It may be asymptomatic or cause intermittent proptosis, pain and, occasionally, acute thrombosis or haemorrhage","Imaging with the patient performing Valsalva, or in a dependent position, demonstrates the distending lesion","Enophthalmos can occur when the varix is decompressed and the orbit is at rest","Many are observed, with intervention reserved for significant symptoms, thrombosis or visual compromise"],"pathophysiology":"An orbital varix is a low-flow venous malformation consisting of dilated, thin-walled venous channels that communicate with the orbital venous system. Because these channels distend under increased venous pressure, manoeuvres that raise it (bending forward, straining, Valsalva) cause the varix to fill and produce proptosis, which reverses at rest. Stagnation within the malformation predisposes to thrombosis, and thin vessel walls can bleed, causing acute pain and proptosis; chronic changes may leave enophthalmos from orbital fat atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Orbital lymphatic malformation (lymphangioma)","Arteriovenous or carotid-cavernous fistula","Other orbital vascular lesions","Orbital tumour causing proptosis","Thyroid eye disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT or MRI with and without Valsalva or in a dependent position","Assessment of positional proptosis clinically","Imaging to detect phleboliths and to exclude a fistula","Evaluation of optic nerve function if compromise is suspected","Vascular imaging where a high-flow lesion must be excluded"],"redFlags":["Acute painful proptosis suggesting thrombosis or haemorrhage","Optic nerve compromise with visual loss","Rapid enlargement","Features suggesting a high-flow vascular lesion"],"prognosis":"Most orbital varices are benign and can be observed; complications such as thrombosis or haemorrhage are usually self-limiting, and intervention for symptomatic lesions carries a risk of significant bleeding.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis by imaging only at rest without a distending manoeuvre","Confusing it with a lymphatic malformation or a fistula","Overlooking acute thrombosis or haemorrhage as a cause of sudden proptosis","Underestimating the bleeding risk of surgical intervention"],"management":["Observe asymptomatic or minimally symptomatic lesions, which is the mainstay of care","Reserve intervention for significant symptoms, recurrent thrombosis, disfiguring proptosis or visual compromise, using surgical excision or endovascular embolisation and sclerotherapy","Provide supportive management with analgesia for acute thrombosis or haemorrhage, which is usually self-limiting","Monitor optic nerve function and vision when compression is a concern","Characterise and follow the lesion with Valsalva or dependent-position imaging, excluding a high-flow vascular malformation","Refer to oculoplastic and interventional or vascular specialists for treatment planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rootman Orbital Disease (standard reference)","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"orchitis_granulomatous":{"name":"Idiopathic granulomatous orchitis","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["granulomatous orchitis","idiopathic granulomatous orchitis","non-specific granulomatous orchitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Idiopathic granulomatous orchitis is a rare non-infective inflammatory condition that characteristically mimics a testicular tumour, usually in middle-aged men","It presents as a firm, sometimes tender, testicular mass or diffuse enlargement, often with a preceding history of trauma or infection","Germ cell tumour markers are normal, but this does not reliably distinguish it from a testicular tumour, so malignancy remains the primary concern","Ultrasound findings are non-specific and may resemble a neoplasm, and the diagnosis is frequently only made on histology after orchidectomy","It is a diagnosis of exclusion once specific granulomatous causes such as tuberculosis, other infections, and sarcoidosis have been considered","Because a tumour cannot be confidently excluded preoperatively, many patients undergo radical inguinal orchidectomy, which is both diagnostic and therapeutic","Where diagnosis can be established without radical surgery, anti-inflammatory or immunosuppressive treatment may be considered, but tumour must first be excluded"],"pathophysiology":"Idiopathic granulomatous orchitis is thought to represent an autoimmune or hypersensitivity reaction to extravasated sperm antigens, sometimes following trauma, infection, or obstruction that breaches the blood-testis barrier. The exposed germ cell antigens provoke a granulomatous inflammatory response within the seminiferous tubules and interstitium, with destruction of the tubules and formation of a firm mass. By definition, no specific infective organism or systemic granulomatous disease is identified, and the resulting swelling can closely imitate a testicular neoplasm.","additionalDifferentials":["Germ cell tumour of the testis","Testicular lymphoma","Tuberculous orchitis or epididymo-orchitis","Other infective granulomatous orchitis (e.g. fungal, brucellosis)","Sarcoidosis involving the testis","Chronic bacterial epididymo-orchitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound to characterise the mass and assess the epididymis","Serum AFP, beta-hCG, and LDH (typically normal) to help assess for germ cell tumour","Urinalysis, culture, and tests for tuberculosis and other infections","Investigations for sarcoidosis when clinically suggested","Radical inguinal orchidectomy with histology when malignancy cannot be excluded","Histopathological review to confirm non-specific granulomatous inflammation"],"redFlags":["Features indistinguishable from a testicular tumour on examination and imaging","Rapidly enlarging or hard testicular mass","Systemic symptoms suggesting tuberculosis or malignancy","Constitutional upset or nodal enlargement raising concern for lymphoma"],"prognosis":"The condition itself is benign, but because it cannot be reliably distinguished from malignancy preoperatively, the affected testis is often removed; once a tumour is excluded and specific causes treated, the prognosis is good, though the involved testis is frequently rendered non-functional by the inflammation.","pitfalls":["Assuming a benign inflammatory cause and delaying exclusion of a testicular tumour","Relying on normal tumour markers to rule out malignancy","Failing to exclude tuberculosis and other specific granulomatous causes before labelling it idiopathic","Attempting conservative management before a tumour has been confidently excluded"],"management":["Perform radical inguinal orchidectomy, which is usually both diagnostic and therapeutic because a testicular tumour cannot be reliably excluded preoperatively","Exclude and treat specific granulomatous causes first, including tuberculosis with anti-tuberculous therapy, other infections, and sarcoidosis","Give a trial of antibiotics where an infective epididymo-orchitis element is present","Obtain serum tumour markers (AFP, beta-hCG, LDH) and scrotal ultrasound as part of the malignancy work-up","Confirm non-specific granulomatous inflammation on histopathology after surgery","Refer to urology, assess the contralateral testis, and counsel regarding fertility and hypogonadism after orchidectomy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","WHO Classification of Tumours: Urinary and Male Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"orf_infection":{"name":"Orf (ecthyma contagiosum)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["orf virus","ecthyma contagiosum","contagious pustular dermatitis","scabby mouth","parapoxvirus infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A zoonotic parapoxvirus infection acquired by direct contact with infected sheep and goats or contaminated fomites, and an occupational hazard of farmers, shepherds, abattoir workers, and vets","Presents as one or a few solitary lesions, usually on the fingers or hands, evolving through recognised stages from a papule to a targetoid nodule and finally a regressing crust","The history of animal contact, particularly around lambing or after bottle-feeding lambs, is the key diagnostic clue","Regional lymphadenopathy and a low-grade constitutional upset may accompany the lesion; secondary bacterial infection can occur","It is usually a clinical diagnosis; confirmation is by electron microscopy, PCR, or biopsy when the presentation is atypical","The infection is self-limiting in immunocompetent people and resolves over several weeks without scarring, so treatment is largely supportive","Immunocompromised patients may develop large, persistent, or progressive giant orf lesions requiring intervention"],"pathophysiology":"Orf is caused by the orf virus, a parapoxvirus of the Poxviridae family that infects the epithelium of sheep and goats, producing crusted lesions around their mouths and udders. Humans are infected through breaks in the skin during handling of animals or contaminated material, and the virus replicates locally in keratinocytes. The lesion progresses through characteristic morphological stages as viral replication and the host inflammatory and immune responses evolve, producing a nodule with a distinctive targetoid appearance before regressing. The virus encodes immunomodulatory proteins, including a viral vascular endothelial growth factor homologue that drives the vascular, granulation-like histology. Immunity is incomplete, so reinfection is possible.","additionalDifferentials":["Milker's nodule (pseudocowpox, another parapoxvirus)","Cutaneous anthrax","Pyogenic granuloma","Atypical mycobacterial or sporotrichoid skin infection","Cutaneous tularaemia or other zoonotic ulcers","Squamous cell carcinoma or keratoacanthoma in persistent lesions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed occupational and animal-contact history","Polymerase chain reaction for parapoxvirus DNA from lesion material","Electron microscopy of lesion scrapings showing ovoid parapoxvirus particles","Skin biopsy for histology when malignancy or another diagnosis is suspected","Bacterial swab and culture if secondary infection is suspected","Assessment of immune status in atypical, giant, or persistent lesions"],"redFlags":["Large, rapidly enlarging, or persistent giant lesions suggesting immunocompromise","Features of secondary bacterial infection or spreading cellulitis","A non-healing lesion raising concern for malignancy or an alternative diagnosis such as anthrax","Systemic upset out of proportion to a simple localised lesion"],"prognosis":"In healthy people orf is self-limiting and resolves spontaneously over about six weeks with little or no scarring; immunocompromised patients may have prolonged or giant lesions that need specific therapy such as topical or destructive treatments.","pitfalls":["Mistaking orf for a bacterial abscess and performing unnecessary incision or prescribing antibiotics","Failing to elicit the animal-contact history and pursuing invasive investigations","Overlooking a persistent lesion that may actually be malignant or a different zoonosis","Not recognising that immunocompromised hosts can develop atypical giant orf needing active management"],"management":["Provide supportive care as the infection is self-limiting: keep the lesion clean, covered with a dry dressing, and allow spontaneous resolution over several weeks","Treat secondary bacterial infection with antibiotics when present","For giant or persistent lesions in immunocompromised patients, use specific measures such as topical imiquimod, cidofovir, cryotherapy or surgical debulking","Avoid unnecessary incision or excision of the primary lesion","Reassure regarding uneventful healing without scarring in immunocompetent people","Advise occupational prevention with gloves and hand hygiene when handling sheep and goats, particularly around lambing","Biopsy atypical lesions to exclude malignancy or an alternative diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","UpToDate: Orf virus infection (ecthyma contagiosum)"],"crossLinks":[]},"organophosphate_poisoning":{"name":"Organophosphate/carbamate poisoning","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["organophosphate poisoning","carbamate poisoning","cholinesterase inhibitor poisoning","nerve agent poisoning","anticholinesterase toxicity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The cholinergic toxidrome combines muscarinic features summarised as SLUDGE (salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, gastrointestinal distress, emesis) with nicotinic effects","The most life-threatening features are the 'killer Bs': bronchorrhoea, bronchospasm and bradycardia causing respiratory failure","A garlic or solvent-like odour and a history of agricultural, pesticide or occupational exposure support the diagnosis","Nicotinic overstimulation produces muscle fasciculations, weakness and, later, paralysis, while central effects cause agitation, seizures and coma","Carbamate inhibition of cholinesterase is reversible and generally shorter-lived than organophosphate poisoning, which can become irreversible after enzyme 'ageing'","Management centres on aggressive antimuscarinic therapy with atropine titrated to drying of secretions, plus an oxime reactivator for organophosphates, with airway protection paramount","An intermediate syndrome of proximal and respiratory muscle weakness can appear one to four days later, and delayed peripheral neuropathy may follow certain agents","Decontamination with removal of clothing and skin washing protects both patient and staff, who should wear protective equipment"],"pathophysiology":"Organophosphates and carbamates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, preventing the breakdown of acetylcholine at muscarinic, nicotinic and central synapses. The resulting accumulation of acetylcholine overstimulates cholinergic receptors, producing the mixed muscarinic and nicotinic cholinergic crisis. Organophosphate-enzyme bonds can undergo 'ageing', becoming permanent and refractory to oxime reactivation, whereas carbamate inhibition spontaneously reverses. Excess cholinergic activity at the neuromuscular junction first causes fasciculations, then depolarising blockade and weakness, while central accumulation drives seizures and depressed consciousness.","additionalDifferentials":["Carbamate versus organophosphate agent (affecting reversibility and oxime use)","Nerve agent exposure in a mass-casualty or deliberate setting","Muscarinic mushroom poisoning","Acute pulmonary oedema or asthma from another cause","Sepsis or gastroenteritis with hypersecretion","Other causes of cholinergic-appearing crisis such as pyridostigmine excess"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Red-cell (acetyl) cholinesterase and plasma (butyryl) cholinesterase activity to support diagnosis and monitor recovery","Arterial blood gas and continuous oxygen saturation to detect respiratory failure","Electrocardiogram for bradyarrhythmias and QT prolongation","Serum electrolytes, glucose and renal function","Chest radiograph for aspiration or pulmonary oedema","Serial neuromuscular assessment for intermediate syndrome"],"redFlags":["Copious bronchorrhoea and bronchospasm threatening the airway","Bradycardia with haemodynamic compromise","Seizures or deteriorating consciousness","Progressive muscle weakness or respiratory failure suggesting intermediate syndrome","Inadequate response to escalating antimuscarinic therapy"],"prognosis":"With early airway control, adequate atropinisation and oxime therapy, many patients recover fully, though severe poisoning, delayed presentation or intermediate syndrome may require prolonged ventilation; mortality is driven mainly by respiratory failure and aspiration.","pitfalls":["Under-dosing atropine and failing to titrate to clearing of bronchial secretions","Neglecting staff and rescuer decontamination, risking secondary exposure","Missing the delayed intermediate syndrome after apparent recovery","Relying on a single cholinesterase measurement rather than trends","Assuming carbamate poisoning is always trivial and omitting close respiratory monitoring"],"management":["Decontaminate by removing contaminated clothing and washing the skin, using staff personal protective equipment to prevent secondary exposure","Protect the airway and give high-flow oxygen and mechanical ventilation for respiratory failure and bronchorrhoea","Administer antimuscarinic therapy with atropine titrated to drying of pulmonary secretions and reversal of bradycardia as the central pillar","Give an oxime reactivator (pralidoxime) for organophosphate poisoning, before enzyme ageing, to reverse nicotinic effects","Control seizures and agitation with benzodiazepines (diazepam)","Monitor in intensive care with serial cholinesterase activity and vigilance for the delayed intermediate syndrome requiring prolonged ventilation","Provide supportive care and avoid contraindicated agents such as depolarising neuromuscular blockers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","UpToDate: Organophosphate and carbamate poisoning"],"crossLinks":[]},"ornithine_transcarbamylase_deficiency":{"name":"Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OTC deficiency","OTCD","ornithine carbamoyltransferase deficiency","X-linked urea cycle disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common urea cycle disorder and the only one that is X-linked, so severe disease predominantly affects hemizygous males","Affected male neonates classically appear well then develop lethargy, poor feeding, vomiting and encephalopathy with severe hyperammonaemia in the first days of life","Heterozygous females have a variable phenotype depending on X-inactivation and may present later with episodic hyperammonaemic crises, protein intolerance, migraine-like symptoms or psychiatric disturbance","The biochemical signature is hyperammonaemia with low or normal citrulline, elevated urine orotic acid, and typically a respiratory alkalosis without metabolic acidosis","Elevated orotic acid distinguishes OTC deficiency from carbamoyl phosphate synthetase 1 and N-acetylglutamate synthase deficiencies, in which orotic acid is not raised","Acute crises can be precipitated by infection, fasting, high protein load, surgery, or the postpartum period","Acute management follows urea cycle disorder principles: halt protein, reverse catabolism with glucose and lipid, give nitrogen scavengers and arginine, and use haemodialysis for severe hyperammonaemia","Because inheritance is X-linked rather than reflected by a single metabolite, standard newborn screening may miss cases, so a high index of suspicion and family testing are important"],"pathophysiology":"Ornithine transcarbamylase is a mitochondrial enzyme that combines carbamoyl phosphate with ornithine to form citrulline, the second step of the urea cycle. Its deficiency, caused by mutations in the X-linked OTC gene, blocks ureagenesis and causes waste nitrogen to accumulate as ammonia. Because the block is proximal, carbamoyl phosphate builds up and is diverted into the pyrimidine synthesis pathway, generating excess orotic acid excreted in urine. Ammonia is neurotoxic, producing glutamine accumulation, astrocyte swelling and cerebral oedema. Females show a spectrum of severity determined by the pattern of X-chromosome inactivation in hepatocytes.","additionalDifferentials":["Other urea cycle disorders (CPS1, NAGS, citrullinemia, argininosuccinic aciduria)","Organic acidaemias with secondary hyperammonaemia","Fatty acid oxidation disorders","Neonatal sepsis","Reye-like syndrome and other causes of acute encephalopathy","Postpartum or valproate-associated hyperammonaemic encephalopathy in carrier females"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent plasma ammonia and arterial blood gas (respiratory alkalosis)","Plasma amino acids (low citrulline, low arginine) and urine orotic acid (elevated)","Allopurinol loading or protein challenge to unmask carriers, used cautiously in specialist settings","Molecular testing of the OTC gene and enzyme assay on liver tissue where needed","Family screening and genetic counselling given X-linked inheritance","Neuroimaging in severe or prolonged hyperammonaemia"],"redFlags":["Severe hyperammonaemic encephalopathy is a metabolic emergency mandating immediate ammonia lowering and often dialysis","Deteriorating consciousness, seizures or signs of cerebral oedema and raised intracranial pressure","A previously well neonatal boy collapsing after the first feeds, easily mistaken for sepsis","Carrier female with unexplained encephalopathy postpartum, after surgery, or on valproate"],"prognosis":"Neonatal-onset disease in boys is severe and, without rapid control of hyperammonaemia, carries a high risk of death or lasting neurodevelopmental impairment; later-onset and carrier presentations vary widely, and outcome depends on prompt recognition and management of crises, with liver transplantation offering definitive correction in selected patients.","pitfalls":["Assuming females who are carriers are unaffected and overlooking symptomatic heterozygotes","Missing the diagnosis because newborn screening is insensitive for OTC deficiency","Delaying ammonia measurement in an encephalopathic patient and attributing illness to sepsis","Giving valproate or a high protein load to an undiagnosed carrier and precipitating a crisis"],"management":["In an acute hyperammonaemic crisis, stop protein intake, give high-calorie glucose and lipid to suppress catabolism, and start intravenous nitrogen-scavenging agents (sodium benzoate and sodium phenylacetate) with intravenous arginine","Use haemodialysis or haemofiltration to clear ammonia rapidly in severe hyperammonaemia","Provide chronic management with a protein-restricted diet supplemented with essential amino acids, plus citrulline or arginine to support the urea cycle","Give long-term oral nitrogen scavengers (sodium phenylbutyrate or glycerol phenylbutyrate) to provide alternative routes for nitrogen excretion","Avoid known precipitants such as fasting, catabolic stress, high protein loads and sodium valproate, and supply a written emergency sick-day regimen","Offer liver transplantation as definitive treatment for severe or poorly controlled disease","Monitor plasma ammonia, amino acids and neurodevelopment within a metabolic multidisciplinary team, with genetic counselling and family screening given X-linked inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"orofaciodigital_syndrome_type_2":{"name":"Oral-facial-digital syndrome type II (Mohr)","system":"Genetics / Ciliopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mohr syndrome","OFD II","orofaciodigital syndrome type 2","OFDS type II"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Belongs to the oral-facial-digital group of ciliopathies that affect the mouth, face and digits","Oral findings include a lobulated or bifid tongue, midline notching of the upper lip and multiple hyperplastic oral frenula","Facial features are hypertelorism, a broad nasal bridge and a bifid nasal tip","Digital anomalies typically include polysyndactyly, with reduplication of the great toe (preaxial hallucal polydactyly) a helpful pointer","Conductive hearing loss from middle-ear ossicular anomalies is characteristic and helps distinguish it from some other subtypes","Inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, in contrast to the X-linked oral-facial-digital syndrome type I (OFD1)","Cognition is usually preserved, though brain malformation occurs in a minority"],"pathophysiology":"The oral-facial-digital syndromes are ciliopathies in which proteins localising to the basal body and primary cilium fail to support normal ciliary structure and signalling. In the midline structures of the developing face, the branchial arches and the limb buds, disrupted ciliary hedgehog signalling produces the clefting and lobulation of the tongue and lip, the hypertelorism and nasal anomalies, and the polysyndactyly with hallucal duplication that define the group. Type II (Mohr syndrome) follows autosomal recessive inheritance and is distinguished within the group by the combination of these oral and digital features with conductive hearing loss, separating it from the X-linked type I caused by variants in the ciliary gene OFD1.","additionalDifferentials":["Oral-facial-digital syndrome type I (X-linked, OFD1)","Other oral-facial-digital syndrome subtypes","Pallister-Hall syndrome","Meckel-Gruber syndrome","Acrocallosal syndrome","Ellis-van Creveld syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed oral examination for tongue lobulation, frenula and midline clefting","Radiographs of the hands and feet to characterise polysyndactyly and hallucal duplication","Audiological assessment for conductive hearing loss","Brain imaging where central nervous system involvement is suspected","Molecular testing with an oral-facial-digital / ciliopathy gene panel"],"redFlags":["Feeding or airway difficulty from oral and midline anomalies in infancy","Brain malformation with neurological or developmental consequences","Hearing loss threatening speech and language development"],"prognosis":"Most affected individuals have normal intellect and a good functional outcome with multidisciplinary craniofacial, dental and audiological care; prognosis is chiefly influenced by the presence and severity of any brain malformation.","pitfalls":["Confusing autosomal recessive Mohr (type II) with the X-linked oral-facial-digital syndrome type I","Overlooking conductive hearing loss and its effect on language development","Missing preaxial hallucal duplication as a diagnostic clue","Assuming intellectual disability when cognition is usually normal"],"management":["Provide supportive, surgically-focused care with repair of oral anomalies (bifid or lobulated tongue, midline lip notching and hyperplastic frenula) and clefts","Correct digital anomalies including polysyndactyly and duplicated great toe","Manage conductive hearing loss with hearing aids, ventilation tubes or ossicular reconstruction","Coordinate dental and orthodontic care with craniofacial follow-up","Arrange brain imaging and developmental support where central nervous system involvement is suspected","Provide genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance, distinguishing it from X-linked OFD type I"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews / OMIM: Oral-Facial-Digital Syndromes","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"oromandibular_dystonia":{"name":"Oromandibular dystonia","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OMD","cranial dystonia","jaw dystonia","Meige syndrome (when with blepharospasm)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A focal dystonia producing involuntary, sustained or repetitive contractions of the masticatory, facial, and tongue muscles","Recognised patterns include jaw-closing, jaw-opening, jaw-deviating, and lingual dystonia, often triggered or worsened by speaking or chewing","Jaw-closing dystonia can cause bruxism, dental damage, and difficulty with mouth opening, while jaw-opening dystonia interferes with eating and speech","Many patients discover a sensory trick (geste antagoniste), such as touching the lips or chin, that transiently relieves the spasms","When combined with blepharospasm it constitutes Meige syndrome (cranial dystonia)","Secondary causes include dopamine-blocking drugs (tardive dystonia), neurodegenerative disease, and structural brain lesions, which should be excluded","Botulinum toxin injection into the overactive muscles is the principal treatment, supported by oral medication and dental appliances"],"pathophysiology":"Oromandibular dystonia is a focal dystonia affecting the jaw, mouth, and tongue muscles, arising from disordered motor control within the basal ganglia circuits and their connections, with abnormal inhibition and excessive co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles. This produces involuntary, patterned, often task-related movements of the masticatory and lower facial musculature. It may be idiopathic (primary) or secondary to dopamine-receptor-blocking drugs, neurodegenerative disorders, or structural lesions. The characteristic sensory trick, which temporarily reduces the dystonia through sensorimotor modulation, is a hallmark of dystonic disorders.","additionalDifferentials":["Temporomandibular joint dysfunction","Tardive dyskinesia or drug-induced dystonia","Hemifacial spasm","Bruxism unrelated to dystonia","Trismus from local or infective causes","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder","Neurodegenerative disease with orofacial involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed neurological examination for other dystonias and a sensory trick","Medication history to identify dopamine-blocking or other causative drugs","MRI of the brain to exclude a structural or degenerative cause in atypical or secondary cases","Dental and temporomandibular joint assessment","Surface or needle electromyography of the masticatory muscles in selected cases","Investigations for Wilson disease and other secondary causes in young patients"],"redFlags":["Onset in a young patient suggesting Wilson disease or an inherited dystonia","Recent exposure to dopamine-blocking drugs indicating tardive dystonia","Additional neurological signs suggesting a neurodegenerative disorder","Acute severe jaw-closing spasm threatening the airway or causing tongue injury"],"prognosis":"Oromandibular dystonia is usually chronic; primary forms rarely remit but respond to repeated botulinum toxin injections combined with oral agents and appliances, allowing reasonable function. Secondary and drug-induced forms may improve if the underlying cause is addressed.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for temporomandibular joint dysfunction or simple bruxism","Overlooking a drug-induced (tardive) cause","Failing to exclude Wilson disease in a young patient","Missing coexisting blepharospasm (Meige syndrome)","Not recognising the sensory trick as a diagnostic clue"],"management":["Repeated botulinum toxin injections into the overactive jaw-closing, jaw-opening or lingual muscles, ideally guided by electromyography, as the mainstay of symptom control","Adjunctive oral agents such as anticholinergics, benzodiazepines, baclofen or a dopamine-depleting agent like tetrabenazine for incomplete responders","Withdrawal or substitution of any causative dopamine-receptor-blocking drug in tardive or drug-induced dystonia, with treatment of an identified secondary cause","Custom dental splints or bite appliances and training in the patient's sensory trick to reduce spasms and protect the dentition","Supportive speech and language therapy, dietary modification for chewing and swallowing difficulty, and dental care for bruxism-related damage","Multidisciplinary input from neurology, otolaryngology and dentistry, with screening for Wilson disease and structural lesions in younger or atypical patients","Referral for pallidal deep brain stimulation in severe, disabling disease refractory to toxin and oral therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"orthostatic_hypotension":{"name":"Orthostatic hypotension","system":"Geriatrics / Palliative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["postural hypotension","orthostatic intolerance","postural drop","OH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hazzard's Geriatric Medicine and Gerontology / palliative care guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Orthostatic hypotension is conventionally defined as a sustained fall in systolic blood pressure of at least 20 mmHg, or diastolic of at least 10 mmHg, within three minutes of standing","Symptoms include light-headedness, dizziness, visual dimming, weakness, and falls on standing, but many older adults are asymptomatic","Measure blood pressure supine and then after standing, ideally at one and three minutes, to detect both early and delayed drops","Medications are among the commonest and most reversible causes, including antihypertensives, diuretics, nitrates, alpha-blockers, and drugs with anticholinergic or vasodilating effects","Volume depletion from dehydration, bleeding, or diuretics should always be considered and corrected","Neurogenic causes include diabetic and other autonomic neuropathies and neurodegenerative disorders such as Parkinson disease and related synucleinopathies","Non-drug management comes first: gradual position change, adequate fluid and salt intake where appropriate, compression garments, avoiding large meals and alcohol, and reviewing culprit drugs","A large post-prandial blood pressure fall is a related phenomenon in older adults and can also precipitate falls and syncope"],"pathophysiology":"On standing, gravity shifts blood to the lower body, and normally baroreceptor-mediated reflex increases in heart rate and vascular tone maintain cerebral perfusion. Ageing blunts baroreflex sensitivity and reduces cardiac and vascular compensatory responses. Orthostatic hypotension develops when these mechanisms fail, either because of impaired autonomic reflexes (neurogenic causes) or because of reduced circulating volume or vasodilating and blood-pressure-lowering drugs (non-neurogenic causes). The resulting transient cerebral hypoperfusion produces symptoms and predisposes to falls and syncope.","additionalDifferentials":["Vasovagal syncope","Cardiac arrhythmia or structural heart disease causing syncope","Dehydration or acute blood loss","Post-prandial hypotension","Vertigo or other non-cardiovascular dizziness","Adrenal insufficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lying and standing blood pressure measurements at one and three minutes","Medication review to identify contributing agents","Assessment of hydration status and relevant bloods including electrolytes and haemoglobin","ECG and cardiac evaluation when arrhythmia or structural disease is suspected","Evaluation for autonomic neuropathy or neurodegenerative disease when a neurogenic cause is likely"],"redFlags":["Syncope resulting in injury or occurring without warning","Orthostatic drop accompanied by chest pain, palpitations, or signs of cardiac disease","Evidence of significant volume loss such as gastrointestinal bleeding","Recurrent falls attributable to postural drops","Severe, treatment-resistant orthostatic hypotension suggesting advanced autonomic failure"],"prognosis":"Orthostatic hypotension is common in older adults and is associated with falls, syncope, reduced quality of life, and higher cardiovascular and overall mortality. Many cases improve substantially once contributing medications are withdrawn, volume is restored, and non-pharmacological measures are applied, though neurogenic forms can be chronic and challenging.","pitfalls":["Measuring standing blood pressure only once and missing delayed orthostatic drops","Overlooking medications as the reversible cause","Failing to consider volume depletion or occult bleeding","Aggressively treating supine hypertension in ways that worsen orthostatic symptoms","Not recognising post-prandial hypotension as a distinct contributor to falls"],"management":["Review and rationalise contributing drugs — antihypertensives, diuretics, nitrates, alpha-blockers and agents with vasodilating or anticholinergic effects — and correct volume depletion","Non-pharmacological measures first: liberalising salt and fluid intake, graduated compression garments or an abdominal binder, physical counter-manoeuvres, slow staged rising and head-up sleeping","Pharmacological therapy when non-drug measures fail, using a volume expander such as fludrocortisone or a pressor such as midodrine or droxidopa, particularly in neurogenic causes","Identify and treat the underlying cause, including diabetic or other autonomic neuropathy and neurodegenerative synucleinopathies such as Parkinson disease","Monitor for and avoid provoking supine hypertension, balancing daytime symptom control against nocturnal blood pressure","Falls-prevention assessment and patient education on recognising and aborting presyncopal symptoms","Referral to geriatric, cardiology or autonomic specialist services for refractory or neurogenic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Hazzard's Geriatric Medicine and Gerontology"],"crossLinks":[]},"orthostatic_tremor":{"name":"Orthostatic tremor","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["primary orthostatic tremor","orthostatic tremor","shaky legs syndrome","POT"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with a subjective feeling of unsteadiness on standing still that is promptly relieved by sitting, walking or leaning on support","The tremor is a high-frequency oscillation (characteristically about 13 to 18 Hz) of the legs and trunk that is often not visible but may be palpable or audible on auscultation over the leg muscles","Patients describe a fear of falling when standing still, although actual falls are relatively uncommon early on","The high frequency is highly specific and is best confirmed with surface electromyography","Symptoms are absent when the legs are unloaded and appear within seconds of standing","It most often affects older adults and is more common in women","It is under-recognised and frequently misattributed to anxiety or a non-specific balance disorder"],"pathophysiology":"Primary orthostatic tremor is characterised by a highly synchronous, high-frequency oscillation affecting the weight-bearing leg and trunk muscles, thought to originate from a central generator that may involve the brainstem and cerebello-thalamo-cortical circuits. The tremor appears only when the legs are loaded during standing and resolves once that load is removed, producing the characteristic relief on sitting or walking. Its remarkable frequency and its high coherence between different muscles distinguish it from other tremors.","additionalDifferentials":["Essential tremor","Parkinson's disease and other parkinsonian syndromes","Cerebellar ataxia and other gait disorders","Anxiety-related unsteadiness","Peripheral neuropathy or vestibular imbalance","Functional (psychogenic) unsteadiness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Surface electromyography of the leg muscles demonstrating the characteristic high-frequency, highly coherent tremor, which is the key diagnostic investigation","Clinical assessment comparing symptoms on standing with those on sitting and walking","MRI brain where a secondary or structural cause is suspected","DAT imaging where coexisting parkinsonism is suspected (orthostatic-tremor-plus)","Assessment for peripheral neuropathy and vestibular dysfunction","Review of medication and metabolic contributors to unsteadiness"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive unsteadiness or frequent falls suggesting an alternative or additional diagnosis","Associated parkinsonism, ataxia or focal signs indicating a secondary cause","Early prominent autonomic failure","Onset in a young patient, which is atypical"],"prognosis":"Primary orthostatic tremor is usually slowly progressive and, although it can be disabling because of the persistent fear of falling and limited standing tolerance, it is not life-shortening. Response to pharmacotherapy is often partial and may wane over time, and selected refractory patients have been treated with deep brain stimulation. Practical measures such as using a stool or walking to relieve symptoms help maintain function.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis because the tremor is not visible; palpation, auscultation and surface EMG are needed","Misattributing symptoms to anxiety or a primary balance disorder","Failing to distinguish primary orthostatic tremor from orthostatic-tremor-plus syndromes with parkinsonism","Overlooking the characteristic relief on sitting or walking within the history"],"management":["Clonazepam as the usual first-line drug, with gabapentin a common alternative or add-on for this often only partially responsive tremor","Trial of further agents such as primidone, valproate, levetiracetam or a dopaminergic agent where response is inadequate","Occupational adaptations to minimise prolonged standing — a perching stool, a walking aid and opportunities to sit or walk — since symptoms remit on unloading the legs","Physiotherapy and falls-prevention strategies addressing the disabling fear of falling while standing still","Reassessment for emerging parkinsonism (orthostatic-tremor-plus), with dopaminergic treatment where a synucleinopathy is confirmed","Referral to a movement-disorder neurologist and consideration of thalamic deep brain stimulation in selected, severely refractory patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice, current edition","Consensus statement on the classification of tremors (Movement Disorders)"],"crossLinks":[]},"os_acromiale":{"name":"os acromiale","system":"musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["unfused acromial apophysis","acromial ossicle"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Matsen's The Shoulder","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Os acromiale is failure of fusion of one or more acromial ossification centres, which normally unite in early adult life.","It is frequently an incidental, asymptomatic finding and is bilateral in a substantial proportion of affected individuals.","Classified by the site of the unfused segment: pre-acromion, meso-acromion, meta-acromion and basi-acromion, with the meso-acromion type being the most common and most often symptomatic.","An axillary lateral radiograph is the key view; on a standard anteroposterior film the ossicle can be mistaken for the acromioclavicular joint or missed entirely.","A mobile fragment can be pulled inferiorly by the deltoid during contraction, dynamically narrowing the subacromial space and producing secondary impingement.","It is associated with rotator cuff pathology, and both should be assessed together when planning treatment.","Do not confuse an os acromiale with an acute acromial fracture; smooth, corticated, symmetrical margins favour the congenital ossicle.","Caution is needed at arthroscopic subacromial decompression, as aggressive resection of an unstable fragment can destabilise the deltoid origin."],"pathophysiology":"The acromion develops from several ossification centres that typically coalesce by the third decade. Os acromiale results when one or more of these centres fails to fuse, leaving a segment joined to the remaining acromion by fibrocartilaginous or fibrous tissue rather than bone. Symptoms arise when this synchondrosis becomes mobile or degenerate: motion at the pseudarthrosis generates pain, and traction by the deltoid, whose fibres insert on the mobile fragment, can tilt it inferiorly to reduce the subacromial outlet and cause dynamic impingement of the underlying cuff. Chronic instability of the segment may also provoke reactive changes at the synchondrosis and contribute to adjacent rotator cuff tendinopathy or tearing.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute acromial fracture","Subacromial impingement syndrome from other causes","Rotator cuff tear","Acromioclavicular joint osteoarthritis","Calcific tendinitis of the shoulder","Stress fracture of the acromion","Distal clavicular osteolysis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Axillary lateral radiograph to demonstrate and classify the unfused segment","Outlet (scapular Y) and anteroposterior views to assess the subacromial arch","CT to define fragment size, bony margins and any degenerative change at the synchondrosis","MRI to evaluate the rotator cuff, marrow oedema across the synchondrosis and bursal changes","Diagnostic local anaesthetic injection into the synchondrosis to confirm it as the pain source","Dynamic ultrasound to assess fragment mobility and cuff integrity"],"redFlags":["Acute onset after trauma with sharp margins suggesting a true acromial fracture rather than a congenital ossicle","Progressive rotator cuff weakness indicating a significant associated tear","Marrow oedema with destructive change raising concern for infection or tumour","Deltoid dysfunction following prior surgery to the region"],"prognosis":"Asymptomatic os acromiale requires no treatment and carries an excellent prognosis. Symptomatic cases often respond to conservative management, while persistent instability may require fixation with bone grafting or fragment excision, with outcomes influenced by fragment size and the state of the associated rotator cuff.","pitfalls":["Missing the ossicle by omitting an axillary lateral view or misreading it as the acromioclavicular joint.","Mistaking a congenital os acromiale for an acute fracture and vice versa.","Performing standard acromioplasty that destabilises the fragment and deltoid origin without addressing the synchondrosis.","Failing to recognise and treat a coexisting rotator cuff tear."],"management":["Reassurance and no intervention for the common incidental, asymptomatic os acromiale","Initial conservative management with activity modification, physiotherapy targeting rotator cuff and scapular stabiliser strengthening, and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","Image-guided subacromial or intra-synchondrosis corticosteroid injection for symptomatic impingement, which also helps confirm the pain source","Excision of a small unstable pre-acromion fragment, or open reduction and internal fixation with bone grafting for a larger mobile meso-acromion, when conservative care fails","Concurrent assessment and repair of any associated rotator cuff tear, with subacromial decompression, at the time of surgery","Referral to a shoulder surgeon for persistent instability, with fragment size and cuff status guiding the operative choice"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Matsen's The Shoulder","Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Trauma","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"os_trigonum_syndrome":{"name":"Os trigonum syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Foot & Ankle","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["posterior ankle impingement syndrome","os trigonum impingement","talar compression syndrome","dancer's heel"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The os trigonum is an unfused accessory ossicle behind the talus that, when present, can be compressed in extreme plantarflexion to cause posterior ankle impingement","It is a classic cause of posterior ankle pain in ballet dancers and in footballers and other athletes who repeatedly plantarflex the ankle","Pain is felt deep in the posterior ankle, reproduced by forced passive plantarflexion, and eased by dorsiflexion, which unloads the posterior structures","A prominent posterior talar (Stieda) process can produce the same impingement even without a separate ossicle","The flexor hallucis longus tendon runs immediately medial to the ossicle, so associated flexor hallucis longus tendinopathy and pain on great-toe movement are common","Lateral radiographs, ideally in plantarflexion, demonstrate the ossicle or a prominent process, and MRI shows bone oedema and associated flexor hallucis longus tendinopathy or soft-tissue impingement","Distinguish it from Achilles or retrocalcaneal pathology, posterior tibial or peroneal tendon disorders, and talar stress injury","Management is conservative with activity and technique modification, physiotherapy, and image-guided corticosteroid injection, with arthroscopic or open excision of the ossicle reserved for resistant cases"],"pathophysiology":"The os trigonum forms from a separate ossification centre behind the talus that fails to fuse with the posterolateral talar process; when it does fuse it forms a prominent Stieda process. During extreme plantarflexion, as in ballet en pointe or a footballer striking a ball, the ossicle or prominent process is compressed between the posterior tibial margin and the calcaneus, pinching the intervening soft tissues and producing posterior impingement pain. Repetitive forced plantarflexion causes inflammation across the synchondrosis or a stress reaction, and can fracture or displace the ossicle. Because the flexor hallucis longus tendon passes through its fibro-osseous tunnel immediately medial to the ossicle, the same mechanism frequently irritates that tendon, producing coexistent flexor hallucis longus tendinopathy with pain on great-toe flexion and, sometimes, triggering. The result is a syndrome of activity-related deep posterior ankle pain in individuals who load the ankle in plantarflexion.","additionalDifferentials":["Flexor hallucis longus tendinopathy or stenosing tenosynovitis","Achilles or retrocalcaneal disorders including insertional tendinopathy and bursitis","Posterior tibial or peroneal tendon pathology","Talar or calcaneal stress fracture","Osteochondral lesion of the talus","Posterior ankle soft-tissue impingement without an ossicle","Tarsal tunnel syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lateral ankle radiographs, including a plantarflexion view, to show the os trigonum or a prominent posterior process","Forced passive plantarflexion test to reproduce posterior impingement pain","MRI to assess bone oedema and associated flexor hallucis longus tendinopathy or soft-tissue impingement","Assessment of great-toe flexion and any triggering to detect flexor hallucis longus involvement","Diagnostic image-guided local anaesthetic injection to confirm the posterior ankle as the pain source","CT in selected cases to characterise the ossicle and exclude a fracture","Ultrasound to evaluate the flexor hallucis longus tendon dynamically"],"redFlags":["Acute severe posterior ankle pain after an injury suggesting a fracture of the ossicle or posterior process","Neurological symptoms suggesting tarsal tunnel involvement of the tibial nerve","Rest or night pain or a destructive lesion suggesting infection or tumour","Progressive locking or triggering of the great toe indicating significant flexor hallucis longus pathology"],"prognosis":"Many athletes improve with a period of activity and technique modification, physiotherapy, and sometimes an image-guided injection, allowing return to sport or dance. When conservative treatment fails, excision of the os trigonum, whether arthroscopic or open, with release of the flexor hallucis longus if involved, reliably relieves symptoms and generally permits return to full activity, with the arthroscopic approach offering quicker recovery.","pitfalls":["Overlooking coexistent flexor hallucis longus tendinopathy that will continue to cause symptoms if not addressed","Attributing posterior ankle pain to the Achilles tendon and missing posterior impingement","Assuming an os trigonum seen on imaging is the cause of pain without confirming it clinically or with an injection","Missing a fracture of the ossicle or posterior talar process after an acute injury","Failing to modify the provocative activity or technique, leading to recurrence"],"management":["Activity and technique modification with relative rest from forced plantarflexion as first-line treatment for this posterior ankle impingement","Physiotherapy and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs to settle synovitis and address associated flexor hallucis longus tendinopathy","Image-guided local anaesthetic and corticosteroid injection into the posterior ankle, which both relieves pain and confirms the source","Posterior ankle decompression by arthroscopic or open excision of the os trigonum or prominent Stieda process, with release of the flexor hallucis longus tendon, when conservative measures fail","Structured rehabilitation and graded return to sport or dance after intervention","Referral to a foot and ankle surgeon for refractory or mechanically limiting symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"osgood_schlatter_disease":{"name":"Osgood-Schlatter disease","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tibial tubercle apophysitis","tibial tuberosity apophysitis","Osgood-Schlatter lesion","osteochondrosis of the tibial tubercle"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle rather than a true osteonecrosis","Affected site is the tibial tuberosity at the insertion of the patellar tendon","Typically affects athletic adolescents, roughly ten to fifteen years, and is more common in boys, often bilaterally","Presents with anterior knee pain and a tender, sometimes prominent, swollen tibial tubercle aggravated by running, jumping and kneeling","The radiographic hallmark is fragmentation and irregularity of the tibial tubercle with overlying soft-tissue swelling","Pain is reproduced by resisted knee extension and by direct pressure over the tuberosity","A residual bony ossicle or prominent tubercle may persist into adulthood and occasionally remain symptomatic","Management is conservative with activity modification, quadriceps and hamstring stretching, and physiotherapy, with surgery reserved for a persistent painful ossicle as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis of the tibial tuberosity, the apophysis onto which the patellar tendon inserts. Repetitive, forceful quadriceps contraction during running and jumping transmits tension through the patellar tendon to the developing tuberosity at a time when the apophysis is still partly cartilaginous and mechanically weak. This repeated microtrauma causes small avulsive injuries, inflammation and heterotopic ossification within the distal patellar tendon and apophysis, producing the characteristic prominence and fragmentation. Rapid adolescent growth, with relative tightness of the extensor mechanism, increases the traction load. The condition typically settles as the tuberosity ossifies and the apophysis fuses, although a separate ossicle can persist and remain a source of pain.","additionalDifferentials":["Sinding-Larsen-Johansson syndrome (inferior patellar pole apophysitis)","Patellar tendinopathy (jumper's knee)","Patellofemoral pain syndrome","Tibial tubercle avulsion fracture","Infrapatellar bursitis","Bone tumour or infection of the proximal tibia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination reproducing pain over the tibial tubercle with resisted extension","Lateral knee radiographs to assess tubercle fragmentation and exclude other lesions","Ultrasound to evaluate the distal patellar tendon and any ossicle or bursitis","Magnetic resonance imaging in atypical or persistent cases","Assessment of extensor mechanism flexibility and lower limb alignment","Inflammatory markers if infection or inflammatory arthropathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Acute severe pain and inability to extend the knee suggesting a tibial tubercle avulsion fracture","Rest pain, night pain or systemic symptoms raising concern for infection or malignancy","A rapidly enlarging mass or lytic bony change on imaging","Persistent disabling pain after skeletal maturity indicating a symptomatic ununited ossicle"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is favourable; symptoms are self-limiting and usually resolve as the tubercle ossifies and the apophysis fuses, though a prominent tubercle or persistent ossicle may cause residual discomfort with kneeling in a minority of patients.","pitfalls":["Overlooking an acute tibial tubercle avulsion fracture in an adolescent presenting with sudden severe pain","Confusing the condition with Sinding-Larsen-Johansson syndrome at the inferior patellar pole","Failing to address extensor mechanism tightness that perpetuates apophyseal traction","Recommending prolonged complete rest rather than graded activity modification"],"management":["Activity and load modification with relative rest from running, jumping and kneeling as the mainstay for this self-limiting apophysitis","Simple analgesia, ice after activity and short courses of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain","Physiotherapy focusing on quadriceps and hamstring flexibility and progressive strengthening of the extensor mechanism","A protective infrapatellar strap or knee padding to reduce load and shield the tender tubercle during kneeling","Reassurance and education that symptoms resolve as the tuberosity ossifies and the apophysis fuses","Referral for excision of a persistent painful ossicle in the minority of skeletally mature patients with ongoing symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"ossifying_fibromyxoid_tumor":{"name":"Ossifying fibromyxoid tumour","system":"Oncology / Pathology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OFMT","ossifying fibromyxoid tumour of soft parts","PHF1-rearranged ossifying fibromyxoid tumour","atypical ossifying fibromyxoid tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare tumour of uncertain differentiation, typically presenting as a small, slow-growing, well-circumscribed subcutaneous or deep soft-tissue nodule","Most often arises in the extremities and limb girdles of middle-aged adults, with a male predominance","Classic histology shows uniform round to ovoid cells arranged in cords and nests within a fibromyxoid stroma, surrounded by an incomplete peripheral shell of lamellar bone","Frequently expresses S100 protein, often with desmin, reflecting the tumour's incompletely understood differentiation","Recurrent gene rearrangements involve PHF1, with fusion partners such as EP400, MEAF6 or others, and are useful for confirming the diagnosis","Divided into typical, atypical and malignant categories based on cellularity, mitotic activity and nuclear features","The peripheral bony rim is characteristic but not always present, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Ossifying fibromyxoid tumour is a mesenchymal neoplasm whose precise line of differentiation remains unsettled, though frequent S100 expression suggests a partly neural or cartilaginous phenotype. Most cases are defined by rearrangement of the PHF1 gene, which encodes a component of a Polycomb-like chromatin-regulatory complex, leading to deregulated gene transcription. The distinctive shell of metaplastic lamellar bone at the tumour periphery gives the lesion its name, and the degree of cellularity and mitotic activity, rather than the specific fusion, governs whether a tumour behaves in a benign or malignant manner.","additionalDifferentials":["Soft-tissue myoepithelioma and mixed tumour","Extraskeletal myxoid chondrosarcoma","Schwannoma and other peripheral nerve sheath tumours","Myoepithelial carcinoma","Extraskeletal osteosarcoma or myositis ossificans when bone is prominent","Sclerosing epithelioid fibrosarcoma","Myxoid soft-tissue tumours generally"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Immunohistochemistry for S100 and desmin as an initial panel","Molecular testing for PHF1 rearrangement by FISH or sequencing","Broad marker panel to exclude myoepithelial, cartilaginous and nerve sheath mimics","Radiography or CT to demonstrate the peripheral ossified shell","MRI to define soft-tissue extent and relationship to adjacent structures","Assessment of cellularity, mitotic rate and atypia to assign typical, atypical or malignant category"],"redFlags":["High cellularity, brisk mitotic activity or necrosis indicating a malignant tumour","Rapid growth or large size","Local recurrence after excision","Metastatic spread in malignant examples"],"prognosis":"Typical ossifying fibromyxoid tumours behave in a benign or borderline fashion and are usually cured by complete excision, although local recurrence can follow incomplete removal; atypical and malignant variants carry a higher risk of recurrence and, in the malignant group, of metastasis, so accurate grading and clear margins are important.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis when the peripheral bone shell is absent","Mistaking the tumour for myoepithelioma or extraskeletal myxoid chondrosarcoma given overlapping S100 positivity","Underestimating malignant potential in highly cellular or mitotically active tumours","Confusing prominent ossification with a primary bone-forming neoplasm","Failing to use PHF1 molecular testing in histologically ambiguous cases"],"management":["Complete surgical excision with clear margins as the definitive treatment and usual cure for this soft-tissue neoplasm","Histological categorisation into typical, atypical or malignant on cellularity, mitotic activity and nuclear atypia to stratify subsequent management","Wider re-excision for incompletely removed lesions, with consideration of adjuvant radiotherapy in malignant variants on a case-by-case basis","Long-term clinical and imaging surveillance of the operative site for local recurrence","Systemic staging and surveillance for distant metastasis in the malignant subgroup","Management within a specialist soft-tissue sarcoma multidisciplinary team, with expert pathology review including molecular confirmation of PHF1 rearrangement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","WHO Classification of Tumours: Skin Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteoarthritis":{"name":"Osteoarthritis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OA","degenerative joint disease"],"chapter":383,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2949","clinicalPearls":["OA is the most common type of arthritis and a leading cause of disability in the elderly; prevalence rises strikingly with age, being uncommon before 40 and highly prevalent after 60 (Harrison 22e p.2949).","Joint involvement is characteristic: hips, knees, first MTP joint, cervical/lumbosacral spine, and in the hands the DIP, PIP, and thumb base; the wrist, elbow, and ankle are usually spared (Harrison 22e p.2949).","Joint pain is primarily activity-related early on, coming on during or just after use and resolving with rest; as disease progresses pain becomes continuous and may be bothersome at night (Harrison 22e p.2952).","Morning stiffness, when present, is usually brief (<30 min), in contrast to the prolonged stiffness (hours) of inflammatory arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2952).","Hand OA produces bony enlargement of the DIP joints (Heberden's nodes) and PIP joints (Bouchard's nodes) (Harrison 22e p.2949).","On hip examination, OA can be detected by loss of internal rotation on passive movement (Harrison 22e p.2953).","Mechanical symptoms (buckling, catching, locking) are common in knee OA and need further evaluation for internal derangement only when they follow an acute knee injury (Harrison 22e p.2952).","Osteophytes are an important radiographic hallmark of OA, along with joint-space narrowing reflecting cartilage loss (Harrison 22e pp.2952-2953)."],"pathophysiology":"OA is joint failure in which all joint structures undergo pathologic change. The pathologic hallmark is focal, initially nonuniform loss of hyaline articular cartilage, accompanied by thickening and sclerosis of the subchondral bony plate, marginal osteophyte outgrowth, stretching of the articular capsule, variable synovitis, and weakness of muscles bridging the joint (meniscal degeneration occurs in knees). Disease begins with joint injury when protective mechanisms (joint capsule/ligaments, bridging muscle, sensory afferents, underlying bone, and synovial-fluid lubrication via hyaluronic acid and lubricin) fail. Cartilage shows depletion of aggrecan and loss/unfurling of type 2 collagen, losing compressive stiffness; chondrocytes shift toward catabolism, producing matrix-degrading enzymes (MMPs, ADAMTS-5). Local inflammation (including mechanoinflammation), with cytokines, alarmins/DAMPs, and growth factors (BMP-2, TGF-beta drive osteophytes; VEGF drives vascular invasion), accelerates progression. Risk reflects the balance between joint vulnerability (age, female sex, genetics such as GDF5, prior damage, malalignment, bridging-muscle weakness, high bone density) and joint loading (obesity, occupational/repetitive use, major injury) (Harrison 22e pp.2949-2952).","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory arthritis (suggested by prolonged morning stiffness and involvement of many joints)","Bursitis (anserine bursitis medial/distal to the knee; trochanteric bursitis lateral to the hip)","Iliotibial band syndrome","Internal derangement of the knee (anterior cruciate ligament or meniscal tear), especially after acute injury"],"infectionMimics":["Septic arthritis (consider when synovial fluid white count is elevated/inflammatory)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Gout and pseudogout (crystal arthritis; suggested by synovial fluid white count >1000/uL with identifiable crystals)","Rheumatoid arthritis and other inflammatory arthritides","Fibromyalgia (noninflammatory pain amplification)"],"additionalInvestigations":["No blood tests are routinely indicated unless symptoms or signs suggest inflammatory arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2953).","Synovial fluid examination is often more helpful diagnostically than x-ray; a white count >1000/uL suggests inflammatory arthritis, gout, or pseudogout, the latter two confirmed by crystals (Harrison 22e p.2953).","Radiographs are not routinely indicated; order only when pain and findings are atypical of OA or when pain persists after effective treatment. Imaging findings correlate poorly with pain, and x-rays may be normal in early disease (insensitive to cartilage loss) (Harrison 22e p.2953).","MRI is not indicated in the diagnostic workup; meniscal tears and cartilage/bone lesions are common both in OA and in older persons without joint pain, and MRI findings rarely warrant a change in therapy (Harrison 22e p.2953)."],"redFlags":["Prominent nocturnal pain in the absence of end-stage OA merits a distinct workup (Harrison 22e p.2953).","Prolonged morning stiffness with many joints affected suggests inflammatory arthritis rather than OA (Harrison 22e p.2952).","Mechanical symptoms (buckling, catching, locking) developing after an acute knee injury warrant evaluation for internal derangement (Harrison 22e p.2952)."],"prognosis":"OA is a chronic, generally progressive disorder of the elderly whose high prevalence and impact on physical function make it a leading cause of disability. Severely malaligned knees may be destined to progress regardless of other risk factors, whereas weight loss greater than 10% of body weight reduces cartilage loss and, in most affected persons, alleviates pain (Harrison 22e pp.2949-2952).","pitfalls":["Distinguish OA from rheumatoid (and other inflammatory) arthritis, crystal arthritis (gout/pseudogout), and septic arthritis; prolonged morning stiffness, systemic features, and an inflammatory synovial fluid white count point away from OA.","Do not rely on radiographs to gauge symptom severity: x-ray changes correlate poorly with pain, and films may be normal in early disease.","Many persons with x-ray or MRI evidence of OA have no joint symptoms, so structural findings alone do not establish symptomatic OA.","Back pain and neck pain are not tied to x-ray findings of spinal OA and are evaluated separately."],"management":["Core non-pharmacological management with patient education, individualised exercise including muscle strengthening and aerobic activity, and weight loss in the overweight","Topical non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs first-line, with oral NSAIDs at the lowest effective dose, adjunctive analgesia and consideration of duloxetine for persistent pain","Intra-articular corticosteroid injection for painful flares of the affected joint","Physical adjuncts such as walking aids, appropriate footwear and braces to offload and support symptomatic joints","Referral for joint replacement or other surgery in end-stage disease with refractory pain and functional limitation","Regular review of symptoms, function and treatment tolerability, with attention to comorbidities affecting drug choice"],"severityClassification":"Harrison's chapter 383 does not present a single formal grading scale; OA is characterized clinically along a spectrum from early, episodic activity-related pain with brief morning stiffness to advanced/end-stage disease with continuous and nocturnal pain. The chapter references the WOMAC index as a patient-reported outcome measure of pain, stiffness, and function (Harrison 22e p.2952).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2949"],"crossLinks":["rheumatoid","crystal_arthritis"]},"osteoblastoma":{"name":"Osteoblastoma","system":"Musculoskeletal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["osteoblastoma","giant osteoid osteoma","benign osteoblastoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone / standard orthopaedic oncology texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign bone-forming tumour histologically similar to osteoid osteoma but larger, by convention greater than about two centimetres, with true growth potential","Shows a predilection for the posterior elements of the spine as well as long bones, and typically affects adolescents and young adults","Pain is a common presenting feature but is usually less nocturnal and less reliably relieved by non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs than in osteoid osteoma","Spinal lesions may cause a painful scoliosis, radiculopathy or, uncommonly, cord compression","Imaging shows an expansile, well-defined lytic lesion, often with matrix mineralisation and less surrounding reactive sclerosis than osteoid osteoma","An aggressive variant exists that can mimic malignancy radiologically and histologically, requiring careful pathological review","Treatment is surgical, typically curettage or en bloc excision, with the extent guided by lesion size, site and aggressiveness"],"pathophysiology":"Osteoblastoma is a benign osteoblastic neoplasm composed of osteoid and woven bone deposited by plump osteoblasts within a richly vascular stroma. Although its cellular make-up resembles osteoid osteoma, it lacks the self-limited behaviour of that lesion, being larger and capable of progressive, sometimes locally aggressive, growth. Unlike osteoid osteoma it excites comparatively little reactive sclerosis and produces less prostaglandin-driven nocturnal pain. The aggressive subtype shows epithelioid osteoblasts and more permeative growth, creating overlap with osteosarcoma that demands careful histological and radiological correlation.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteoid osteoma (smaller, marked reactive sclerosis)","Osteosarcoma, especially the osteoblastoma-like variant","Aneurysmal bone cyst","Giant cell tumour of bone","Osteomyelitis","Metastasis or other primary bone tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs showing an expansile lytic lesion with variable matrix mineralisation","CT to define bony architecture, matrix and cortical integrity","MRI to assess marrow and soft-tissue extent, particularly in the spine","Bone scintigraphy demonstrating increased uptake","Image-guided or open biopsy with expert bone pathology review","Staging imaging when an aggressive or malignant lesion is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapid enlargement, cortical destruction or a soft-tissue mass raising concern for osteosarcoma","Neurological deficit or cord compression from a spinal lesion","Histological features overlapping with malignancy on biopsy","Recurrent lesion after previous excision"],"prognosis":"Most osteoblastomas are cured by complete surgical removal, though incompletely excised lesions may recur locally. The aggressive variant has a higher recurrence rate and requires closer follow-up, and its distinction from osteosarcoma is critical to outcome.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying an osteoblastoma-like osteosarcoma as benign osteoblastoma on limited biopsy","Underestimating the local aggressiveness and recurrence potential of the aggressive variant","Treating a large lesion as if it were self-limiting like osteoid osteoma","Inadequate excision leading to local recurrence"],"management":["Complete surgical removal by intralesional curettage with bone grafting, or en bloc excision for aggressive lesions, as the definitive and usually curative treatment","Spinal decompression, tumour resection and instrumented stabilisation where posterior-element lesions cause scoliosis, radiculopathy or cord compression","Expert bone-pathology review of resected tissue to distinguish osteoblastoma, particularly the aggressive variant, from osteosarcoma","Analgesia for tumour-related pain pending definitive treatment","Post-operative imaging surveillance for local recurrence, intensified after incomplete excision or the aggressive variant","Management within an orthopaedic oncology multidisciplinary service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone","Standard orthopaedic oncology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteochondral_lesion_of_talus":{"name":"Osteochondral lesion of the talus","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["osteochondral defect of the talus","talar dome osteochondritis dissecans","OLT"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Review of Orthopaedics / Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A focal injury of the talar dome articular cartilage and underlying subchondral bone, frequently following an ankle sprain or fracture","Medial dome lesions tend to be deeper and posterior; lateral lesions are often shallower and trauma-related","Presents with persistent deep ankle pain, swelling, catching and giving way after an ankle injury that fails to settle","Plain radiographs are often normal, so MRI or CT is needed when symptoms persist","MRI assesses cartilage and bone oedema, while CT best defines bony fragment and cyst geometry","Stable lesions are treated non-operatively; unstable or displaced lesions and those with subchondral cysts are considered for surgery","Surgical options range from debridement and microfracture to osteochondral grafting depending on size and chronicity"],"pathophysiology":"Shear and compressive forces during an inversion ankle injury damage the talar dome cartilage and subchondral bone, sometimes with an element of impaired subchondral blood supply. Disruption of the osteochondral unit produces bone oedema, cyst formation and, if the fragment separates, an unstable lesion or loose body. Persistent altered joint mechanics cause pain and can progress to ankle osteoarthritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic lateral ankle instability","Ankle impingement (anterolateral or posterior)","Occult fracture","Sinus tarsi syndrome","Ankle osteoarthritis","Peroneal tendon pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Weight-bearing ankle radiographs","MRI to evaluate cartilage, bone oedema and stability","CT to define bony fragment and cyst dimensions","Assessment of hindfoot alignment and ligamentous stability"],"redFlags":["A displaced osteochondral fragment causing locking","Signs of septic arthritis in a painful swollen ankle","Progressive joint-space loss indicating established osteoarthritis"],"prognosis":"Small stable lesions often improve with non-operative care; larger, cystic or unstable lesions have more guarded outcomes, and surgical results decline with lesion size, chronicity and revision surgery.","pitfalls":["Attributing persistent post-sprain ankle pain to soft tissue and never imaging beyond radiographs","Underestimating lesion size and cyst formation by relying on plain films","Ignoring hindfoot malalignment or instability that predisposes to failure","Offering repeat marrow-stimulation for large lesions better served by grafting"],"management":["Non-operative management of stable lesions with a period of protected weight-bearing or immobilisation, activity modification and physiotherapy","Analgesia and rehabilitation to restore ankle range of motion and strength","Arthroscopic debridement with bone-marrow-stimulating microfracture for small unstable lesions, and retrograde drilling where cartilage overlies a subchondral cyst","Osteochondral autograft or allograft transplantation, autologous chondrocyte implantation or fragment fixation for larger, cystic or failed lesions","Correction of contributing hindfoot malalignment and lateral ligament instability","Surveillance for progression to ankle osteoarthritis, with referral to a foot and ankle surgeon for unstable or displaced lesions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Review of Orthopaedics","Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteochondritis_dissecans":{"name":"Osteochondritis dissecans","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OCD","osteochondral lesion","osteochondritis dissecans of the knee","juvenile osteochondritis dissecans"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A focal disorder of subchondral bone and overlying articular cartilage that may separate as an osteochondral fragment, distinct from a diffuse osteonecrosis or apophysitis","Most commonly affects the knee, classically the lateral aspect of the medial femoral condyle, and also the capitellum and the talar dome","Occurs in adolescents and young adults; juvenile disease with open physes generally has a better prognosis than adult-onset disease","Presents with poorly localised activity-related joint pain, and with mechanical symptoms such as catching, locking or giving way once a fragment becomes unstable","The radiographic hallmark is a crescentic radiolucent line demarcating an osteochondral fragment from the underlying bone","Fragment stability is the central determinant of management and prognosis and is best assessed on magnetic resonance imaging","A stable lesion with intact overlying cartilage may heal with offloading, whereas an unstable fragment or a loose body usually requires surgery","Management spans activity modification and immobilisation for stable juvenile lesions through to fixation, drilling or fragment removal and cartilage restoration as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Osteochondritis dissecans is a focal disturbance of the subchondral bone in which a segment of bone and its overlying articular cartilage may lose structural continuity with the parent bone. Although the precise cause is debated, repetitive microtrauma and localised ischaemia of the subchondral bone are thought to be central, sometimes with a contribution from disordered ossification of the epiphysis in the growing skeleton. The affected subchondral bone weakens and a demarcation line develops; if the overlying cartilage remains intact the lesion may be stable and heal, but progressive separation can leave the fragment partially attached (a flap) or fully detached as an intra-articular loose body. Once the fragment is unstable the articular surface becomes incongruent, causing mechanical symptoms and predisposing to secondary degenerative change.","additionalDifferentials":["Meniscal tear","Chondral (cartilage) injury without a bony fragment","Loose body from another cause such as synovial chondromatosis","Spontaneous osteonecrosis of the knee","Patellofemoral pain syndrome","In the elbow, capitellar Panner disease in a younger child"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs including tunnel or notch views to detect the crescentic lucency and any loose body","Magnetic resonance imaging to assess cartilage integrity and stage fragment stability","Assessment for fluid tracking behind the fragment on imaging as a sign of instability","Computed tomography to define the bony bed when planning surgery","Examination for a joint effusion, mechanical symptoms and localised tenderness","Comparison with the contralateral joint and evaluation of physeal status"],"redFlags":["Mechanical locking or a palpable loose body indicating an unstable, detached fragment","A recurrent tense effusion suggesting an unstable lesion or intra-articular derangement","Progressive articular surface collapse or established secondary arthritis","Adult-onset disease with a large lesion carrying a higher risk of poor healing"],"prognosis":"Stable lesions in skeletally immature patients often heal with conservative management, whereas unstable fragments, loose bodies and adult-onset lesions have a poorer capacity to heal and carry an increased long-term risk of osteoarthritis if the articular surface is not restored.","pitfalls":["Assuming a lesion is stable without magnetic resonance imaging assessment of the overlying cartilage","Missing a detached loose body as a cause of intermittent locking","Confusing capitellar disease in a young child (Panner disease) with adolescent osteochondritis dissecans","Under-treating an unstable fragment and allowing progressive articular damage"],"management":["Non-operative treatment of stable lesions in skeletally immature patients with activity restriction, protected weight-bearing and sometimes bracing to promote healing across the open physis","Antegrade or retrograde subchondral drilling of a stable juvenile lesion that fails to heal with rest","Arthroscopic fixation of an unstable but salvageable fragment, with removal of loose bodies","Cartilage restoration by microfracture, osteochondral autograft or allograft transplantation, or autologous chondrocyte implantation for detached or unsalvageable fragments","Monitoring of healing with serial imaging and staged return to activity","Long-term surveillance for osteoarthritis, with referral to orthopaedics for unstable, adult-onset or failed lesions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteochondroma":{"name":"Osteochondroma","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Osteocartilaginous exostosis","Exostosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common benign bone tumour, typically arising from the metaphysis of long bones about the knee and the proximal humerus and pointing away from the adjacent joint.","The pathognomonic feature is continuity of both the cortex and the medullary cavity of the lesion with those of the host bone, best demonstrated on CT or MRI.","It may be pedunculated (stalked) or sessile (broad-based) and grows only until skeletal maturity; continued growth thereafter is a warning sign.","Complications include mechanical impingement, formation of an overlying bursa, fracture of a pedunculated stalk, and compression of adjacent nerves or vessels, including popliteal pseudoaneurysm.","The cartilage cap is thin in adults; a cap thicker than roughly 1.5 to 2 cm on MRI raises concern for transformation to secondary peripheral chondrosarcoma.","Hereditary multiple exostoses is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by EXT1 or EXT2 mutations, producing multiple lesions, short stature and forearm deformity with a higher risk of malignant transformation.","Malignant transformation occurs in roughly 1 per cent of solitary lesions but is appreciably more frequent in hereditary multiple exostoses.","Symptomatic lesions or those suspected of transformation are excised, taking the cartilage cap and overlying perichondrium to minimise recurrence."],"pathophysiology":"Osteochondroma is a cartilage-capped bony projection arising on the external surface of a bone, regarded as a developmental lesion in which a fragment of physeal cartilage herniates through a defect in the perichondrial ring of Ranvier, separates from the growth plate and continues to grow by endochondral ossification. Because it originates from the physis, the lesion enlarges only during skeletal growth and characteristically maintains corticomedullary continuity with the parent bone. Biallelic inactivation of the tumour suppressor genes EXT1 or EXT2, which encode glycosyltransferases required for heparan sulfate biosynthesis, disrupts the diffusion gradients of signalling molecules that regulate chondrocyte proliferation; germline EXT mutations underlie hereditary multiple exostoses, while sporadic solitary lesions harbour somatic EXT alterations.","additionalDifferentials":["Parosteal osteosarcoma","Periosteal (juxtacortical) chondroma","Secondary peripheral chondrosarcoma","Subungual exostosis","Bizarre parosteal osteochondromatous proliferation (Nora lesion)","Myositis ossificans"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs to demonstrate the surface lesion and its relationship to the joint","CT to confirm corticomedullary continuity with the host bone","MRI to measure cartilage cap thickness and detect any soft-tissue mass","Clinical assessment and family history to identify hereditary multiple exostoses","Vascular imaging or angiography when a vascular complication such as pseudoaneurysm is suspected","Comparison with prior imaging to document growth after skeletal maturity"],"redFlags":["Pain or documented growth after skeletal maturity","Cartilage cap thicker than about 2 cm on MRI","A new or enlarging soft-tissue mass overlying the lesion","Irregular, lucent or destructive areas developing within a previously stable lesion","Sudden neurovascular symptoms suggesting compression or pseudoaneurysm"],"prognosis":"Solitary osteochondroma has an excellent prognosis, with recurrence occurring mainly after incomplete excision of the cartilage cap. Malignant transformation is uncommon overall but is an important long-term consideration, particularly in hereditary multiple exostoses.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a thick cartilage cap for a benign feature and missing early chondrosarcomatous transformation","Overlooking the diagnosis of hereditary multiple exostoses and its familial and malignancy implications","Incomplete excision leaving perichondrium and cap, leading to recurrence","Failing to recognise vascular injury such as popliteal artery pseudoaneurysm from a stalked lesion"],"management":["Observation of asymptomatic solitary lesions, which need no active treatment","Marginal surgical excision including the cartilage cap for lesions causing mechanical impingement, deformity, bursa formation or fracture of the stalk","Urgent vascular surgical referral for complications such as popliteal pseudoaneurysm or neurovascular compression","Surveillance for malignant transformation — growth after skeletal maturity, new pain or a thickened cartilage cap — with excision and orthopaedic oncology referral if secondary chondrosarcoma is suspected","Recognition of hereditary multiple exostoses with correction of forearm and limb deformities and treatment of symptomatic lesions","Genetic counselling and family assessment for hereditary multiple exostoses caused by EXT1 or EXT2 mutations, given its higher malignant risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone (current edition)","Greenspan, Orthopedic Imaging: A Practical Approach","Resnick, Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteofibrous_dysplasia":{"name":"Osteofibrous dysplasia","system":"Musculoskeletal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OFD","ossifying fibroma of long bones","Kempson-Campanacci lesion"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone / Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign fibro-osseous lesion with a strong predilection for the anterior cortex of the tibial diaphysis in infants and children","Often presents as painless anterior bowing of the tibia or an incidental radiographic finding","Radiographs show an intracortical, well-defined lytic lesion with surrounding sclerosis and characteristic anterior tibial bowing","Histology shows a fibrous stroma with woven bone trabeculae rimmed by osteoblasts, distinguishing it from fibrous dysplasia where osteoblastic rimming is absent","Shares a close relationship with adamantinoma, and OFD-like adamantinoma sits on a spectrum, so careful assessment for epithelial cells is essential","Immunohistochemistry may reveal scattered keratin-positive cells, raising concern for an adamantinomatous component","Given the tendency to recur in young children, observation is often preferred until skeletal maturity unless there is deformity or fracture"],"pathophysiology":"Osteofibrous dysplasia is a benign fibro-osseous proliferation confined largely to the cortex of the tibia, and less often the fibula. The lesion is composed of a bland fibroblastic stroma containing irregular trabeculae of woven bone that, in contrast to fibrous dysplasia, are lined by prominent osteoblasts. It is developmentally and biologically linked to adamantinoma, and lesions exist along a continuum from pure osteofibrous dysplasia through OFD-like adamantinoma to classic adamantinoma, defined by the extent of an epithelial component. Cortical weakening produces the characteristic anterior bowing of the tibia.","additionalDifferentials":["Adamantinoma of long bones","Fibrous dysplasia","Non-ossifying fibroma","Osteofibrous dysplasia-like adamantinoma","Chronic osteomyelitis","Langerhans cell histiocytosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiography to demonstrate the intracortical lytic lesion and tibial bowing","MRI to assess extent and to look for features concerning for adamantinoma","Histology with attention to osteoblastic rimming of bony trabeculae","Cytokeratin immunohistochemistry to detect scattered or clustered epithelial cells","Long-term radiographic surveillance to monitor for progression or adamantinomatous change"],"redFlags":["Increasing size or pain suggesting progression to adamantinoma","Identification of a substantial epithelial component on biopsy","Progressive deformity or pathological fracture of the tibia","Recurrence after resection in an older child or adult"],"prognosis":"Osteofibrous dysplasia is benign and frequently stabilises or regresses around skeletal maturity, but it recurs readily after excision in young children. Its principal significance lies in its relationship to adamantinoma, which mandates careful histological assessment and follow-up.","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude an adamantinomatous component through inadequate sampling or absent keratin staining","Confusing it with fibrous dysplasia by overlooking the osteoblastic rimming","Performing aggressive early resection that is prone to recurrence in young children","Attributing anterior tibial bowing solely to developmental causes without imaging"],"management":["Observation with orthotic bracing and fracture protection for most lesions, since many stabilise or regress around skeletal maturity","Avoidance of early aggressive excision in young children given the high recurrence rate, deferring surgery where possible","Careful biopsy and histological assessment for an epithelial (adamantinomatous) component, using cytokeratin immunohistochemistry","Corrective surgery for progressive anterior tibial bowing, deformity or recurrent fracture once the child is older","Wide resection where OFD-like adamantinoma or classic adamantinoma is confirmed","Long-term radiographic surveillance for progression or adamantinomatous change, within an orthopaedic oncology service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteogenesis_imperfecta":{"name":"Osteogenesis imperfecta","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OI","brittle bone disease","COL1A1","COL1A2","Sillence types I-IV"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically presents with bone fragility and recurrent fractures from little or no trauma, often prompting consideration of non-accidental injury which must be excluded","Most cases result from dominant pathogenic variants in COL1A1 or COL1A2 affecting type I collagen quantity or structure, though recessive forms involving collagen-processing genes exist","Extraskeletal features frequently include blue or grey sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, ligamentous laxity, and progressive conductive or mixed hearing loss","The Sillence classification (types I-IV, with expanded modern subtypes) ranges from mild (type I) through perinatally lethal (type II) to progressively deforming (type III)","Radiographs show generalised osteopenia, thin cortices, gracile or bowed long bones, wormian bones in the skull, and vertebral compression","Bisphosphonates are used to reduce fracture frequency and bone pain, alongside physiotherapy, orthopaedic fracture and deformity management, and audiology surveillance","Basilar invagination is an important late craniocervical complication in severe deforming disease"],"pathophysiology":"Osteogenesis imperfecta is a heritable disorder of connective tissue in which abnormal type I collagen, the principal protein of bone matrix, leads to reduced bone mass and mechanical fragility. Most patients carry dominant variants in COL1A1 or COL1A2; null variants reduce the amount of otherwise normal collagen and cause milder disease, whereas variants that distort the collagen triple helix produce a more severe dominant-negative effect. Rarer recessive forms disrupt genes involved in collagen folding, post-translational modification, or bone mineralisation. The resulting defective matrix impairs bone strength and also affects other collagen-rich tissues such as sclera, dentine, and the middle ear ossicles.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-accidental injury (inflicted skeletal trauma)","Idiopathic juvenile osteoporosis","Hypophosphatasia","Bruck syndrome (OI with congenital contractures)","Cole-Carpenter and other osteoporosis-fracture syndromes","Menkes disease and other collagen or copper metabolism disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey to characterise fracture pattern, bowing, wormian bones, and vertebral changes","Molecular genetic testing of COL1A1/COL1A2 and, if negative, an extended skeletal dysplasia/recessive OI gene panel","Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry to quantify bone mineral density","Audiological assessment for conductive or mixed hearing loss","Dental review for dentinogenesis imperfecta","Craniocervical imaging in severe disease to detect basilar invagination"],"redFlags":["Multiple fractures at different healing stages requiring careful differentiation from non-accidental injury","Progressive spinal deformity with restrictive respiratory compromise","Neurological signs suggesting basilar invagination or brainstem compression","Perinatal presentation with a small deformed thorax and multiple in-utero fractures suggesting a lethal form"],"prognosis":"Prognosis spans from a near-normal lifespan and function in mild type I disease to perinatal lethality in type II. Severe deforming forms carry lifelong mobility limitation, and outcome is strongly influenced by respiratory reserve and craniocervical complications.","pitfalls":["Attributing fractures solely to abuse, or conversely dismissing genuine inflicted injury as OI, without appropriate multidisciplinary assessment","Relying on sclerae colour alone, as blue sclerae are non-specific and may be absent in some types","Overlooking hearing loss and dental disease, which need proactive surveillance","Failing to monitor for basilar invagination in progressively deforming disease"],"management":["Bisphosphonate antiresorptive therapy to reduce fracture frequency and bone pain in moderate-to-severe disease","Orthopaedic fracture management and intramedullary rodding to stabilise and correct deformity in bowed or repeatedly fracturing long bones","Physiotherapy, rehabilitation and mobility aids to build strength and maximise function and independence","Audiological surveillance for conductive or mixed hearing loss and dental care for dentinogenesis imperfecta","Monitoring with bone densitometry and, in severe disease, craniocervical imaging for basilar invagination","Coordinated care through a multidisciplinary bone and skeletal-dysplasia team","Genetic counselling and family assessment given dominant COL1A1/COL1A2 inheritance and rarer recessive forms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteogenesis_imperfecta_type_3":{"name":"Osteogenesis imperfecta type III","system":"Connective tissue / Skeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OI type III","Progressive deforming osteogenesis imperfecta","Sillence type III"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic bone and clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most severe form of osteogenesis imperfecta compatible with survival beyond infancy — the classic progressive deforming type with very short stature and repeated fractures from birth.","Usually caused by de novo autosomal dominant COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutations producing structurally abnormal type I collagen (typically glycine substitutions), a dominant-negative qualitative defect; rarer autosomal recessive forms involve collagen-processing genes.","Sclerae are often bluish or greyish in infancy but tend to lighten towards normal by adulthood, unlike the persistently blue sclerae of type I.","Dentinogenesis imperfecta is common — opalescent, discoloured, brittle teeth prone to wear and fracture.","Characteristic radiographic features include Wormian bones, gracile deformed long bones, 'popcorn' epiphyseal and metaphyseal calcifications, biconcave (codfish) vertebrae and protrusio acetabuli.","Basilar invagination is a feared complication — new occipital headache, lower cranial nerve dysfunction, apnoea or hydrocephalus warrant urgent neuroimaging.","Restrictive lung disease from thoracic cage and spinal deformity is the leading cause of premature death.","Bisphosphonates reduce fracture frequency and bone pain but do not correct established deformity; multidisciplinary orthopaedic, rehabilitation and genetics care is essential, and non-accidental injury must be distinguished."],"pathophysiology":"Type III osteogenesis imperfecta most often arises from heterozygous mutations in COL1A1 or COL1A2, the genes encoding the two proalpha chains of type I collagen. Rather than the quantitative deficiency (haploinsufficiency) that underlies mild type I disease, type III typically results from structurally abnormal chains — classically glycine substitutions within the Gly-X-Y repeat of the triple-helical domain — which exert a dominant-negative effect: mutant chains incorporate into collagen trimers and disrupt folding, secretion and fibril assembly of the whole molecule. Because type I collagen is the principal structural protein of bone matrix, dentine, sclera, ligaments and skin, the abnormal collagen produces fragile, undermineralised, mechanically incompetent bone that fractures and progressively deforms, together with dental, scleral and ligamentous manifestations. A minority of clinically similar cases are autosomal recessive, caused by defects in proteins that hydroxylate, fold or chaperone collagen (such as CRTAP, P3H1/LEPRE1, PPIB or FKBP10), which impair post-translational processing of otherwise normal chains. The net result is a severe, generalised connective tissue disorder dominated by skeletal fragility and progressive deformity.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-accidental injury (inflicted fractures)","Other osteogenesis imperfecta types (I, IV and V)","Hypophosphatasia","Bruck syndrome (osteogenesis imperfecta with congenital joint contractures)","Cole-Carpenter syndrome","Osteoporosis-pseudoglioma syndrome","Severe rickets or other metabolic bone disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey documenting fractures of differing ages, Wormian bones and long-bone deformity","Molecular genetic testing of COL1A1 and COL1A2, extending to a recessive osteogenesis imperfecta gene panel if negative","Bone densitometry showing markedly reduced bone mineral density","Lateral skull imaging and MRI to assess for basilar invagination","Audiological assessment for conductive or mixed hearing loss","Dental review for dentinogenesis imperfecta","Echocardiography for aortic root and valvular assessment"],"redFlags":["Signs of basilar invagination or brainstem compression (occipital headache, apnoea, dysphagia, lower cranial nerve palsies)","Progressive spinal deformity with respiratory decompensation","Acute long-bone or vertebral fracture after minimal trauma","Spinal cord compression from severe kyphoscoliosis"],"prognosis":"Morbidity is high, with many individuals wheelchair-dependent and of very short stature; with modern multidisciplinary care many survive into adulthood, but life expectancy is reduced, chiefly through cardiorespiratory complications of thoracic and spinal deformity.","pitfalls":["Multiple fractures may be wrongly attributed to non-accidental injury, or genuine abuse missed by over-diagnosing osteogenesis imperfecta","Relying on bisphosphonates alone while neglecting deformity correction and rehabilitation","Overlooking evolving basilar invagination","Assuming non-blue sclerae in an older child excludes the diagnosis, since sclerae lighten with age","Under-recognising progressive hearing loss"],"management":["Cyclical bisphosphonate therapy to increase bone mass and reduce the frequent fractures of this severe progressively-deforming disease","Orthopaedic management with intramedullary (including telescoping) rodding, fracture fixation and correction of long-bone deformity","Physiotherapy, adaptive seating and powered-mobility provision with rehabilitation to maximise function despite very short stature","Respiratory surveillance and support for restrictive lung disease from thoracic and spinal deformity, with management of progressive scoliosis","Vigilance for basilar invagination — urgent craniocervical imaging and neurosurgical referral when new occipital headache, lower cranial nerve signs, apnoea or hydrocephalus develop","Dental care for dentinogenesis imperfecta and audiological monitoring for hearing loss","Multidisciplinary care coordinated across clinical genetics and paediatric orthopaedics","Genetic counselling for the usually de novo autosomal dominant COL1A1/COL1A2 mutation, extending to a recessive gene panel if testing is negative"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics reference texts","Paediatric metabolic bone disease guidelines","Osteogenesis imperfecta care consensus guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteogenesis_imperfecta_type_v":{"name":"Osteogenesis imperfecta type V (IFITM5)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OI type V","IFITM5-related osteogenesis imperfecta","OI with hyperplastic callus","BRIL-related brittle bone disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant form of brittle bone disease caused by a recurrent mutation in the 5' untranslated region of IFITM5 (encoding BRIL), not by a collagen defect","Almost all cases share the same recurrent variant, creating a distinctive and highly consistent phenotype","Three characteristic features distinguish it: exuberant hyperplastic callus after fractures or surgery, calcification of the forearm interosseous membrane, and a dense radio-opaque metaphyseal band adjacent to the growth plate","Interosseous membrane calcification restricts forearm rotation, limiting pronation and supination","Sclerae are typically normal (not blue) and dentinogenesis imperfecta is absent, unlike classic collagen-related OI","Hyperplastic callus can be large, warm, and painful, mimicking osteosarcoma or infection and prompting unnecessary biopsy","Management mirrors other OI with fracture care, physiotherapy, and antiresorptive bisphosphonate therapy for skeletal fragility, while avoiding misdiagnosis of the exuberant callus"],"pathophysiology":"OI type V is caused by a recurrent point mutation in the 5' untranslated region of IFITM5, which creates a new upstream start codon and adds five amino acids to the BRIL protein expressed by osteoblasts. BRIL participates in bone mineralisation, and the mutant protein disturbs normal osteoblast function and matrix mineralisation, producing brittle bones without the type I collagen abnormality of classic OI. The altered bone biology manifests as an exaggerated reparative response, generating hyperplastic callus, and abnormal soft-tissue mineralisation such as calcification of the forearm interosseous membrane and a characteristic dense metaphyseal band. Bone histology shows a distinctive mesh-like (irregular) lamellar pattern. The result is increased fracture risk with these unique radiological and clinical hallmarks.","additionalDifferentials":["Collagen-related osteogenesis imperfecta (types I-IV)","Osteosarcoma or other bone tumour mimicked by hyperplastic callus","Osteomyelitis mimicking an inflamed callus","Non-accidental injury in a child with multiple fractures","Other rare non-collagen OI subtypes (e.g. SERPINF1-related)","Fibrous dysplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Radiographs demonstrating hyperplastic callus, interosseous membrane calcification, and the dense metaphyseal band","Assessment of forearm pronation and supination for restriction","Bone densitometry to quantify skeletal fragility","Examination of sclerae and dentition, characteristically normal in this subtype","Bone histology showing the irregular mesh-like lamellar pattern where biopsy is performed","Targeted IFITM5 mutation testing to confirm the recurrent variant"],"redFlags":["A rapidly enlarging, warm, painful callus raising concern for malignancy or infection before OI type V is recognised","Recurrent fractures with progressive deformity","Fixed loss of forearm rotation from interosseous calcification","Spinal fractures or scoliosis compromising function"],"prognosis":"Skeletal fragility is lifelong but generally responsive to fracture management and antiresorptive therapy; forearm rotation may remain limited, and the main avoidable harm comes from misdiagnosing hyperplastic callus as tumour.","pitfalls":["Biopsying or resecting hyperplastic callus mistaken for osteosarcoma, which can itself provoke more callus","Expecting blue sclerae or dentinogenesis imperfecta and discounting OI when they are absent","Overlooking the diagnostic triad of callus, interosseous calcification, and metaphyseal band on imaging","Failing to test IFITM5 because collagen gene studies are normal"],"management":["Antiresorptive bisphosphonate therapy to reduce skeletal fragility and fracture frequency","Standard orthopaedic fracture management with awareness that fractures and surgery can provoke exuberant hyperplastic callus","Recognition of hyperplastic callus as a feature of the disease rather than osteosarcoma or infection, avoiding unnecessary biopsy, with anti-inflammatory analgesia for painful callus","Physiotherapy and occupational adaptation for restricted forearm pronation and supination from interosseous membrane calcification, with selective corrective surgery","Monitoring of bone mineral density and fracture pattern over time","Genetic counselling and confirmatory targeted testing for the recurrent autosomal dominant IFITM5 variant","Multidisciplinary metabolic bone and clinical genetics follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism","GeneReviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteoglophonic_dysplasia":{"name":"Osteoglophonic dysplasia","system":"Skeletal dysplasia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OGD","FGFR1-related osteoglophonic dysplasia","Osteoglophonic dwarfism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Skeletal dysplasia references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare autosomal dominant dysplasia caused by activating (gain-of-function) mutations in FGFR1 (fibroblast growth factor receptor 1).","Recognised by a clinical triad of craniosynostosis with a distinctive facies, rhizomelic short stature, and multiple radiolucent metaphyseal fibrous lesions.","The name means hollowed or carved bone, referring to the punched-out, non-ossifying-fibroma-like metaphyseal defects that hollow out the long bones.","Craniofacial features include a prominent forehead, midface hypoplasia, a depressed nasal bridge and proptosis.","The dental hallmark is failure of tooth eruption, with unerupted or impacted teeth and gingival overgrowth.","Fibroblast growth factor 23 (FGF23) excess from the fibrous bone lesions can drive renal phosphate wasting and hypophosphataemia (interpreted qualitatively).","It belongs to the same receptor family as achondroplasia (FGFR3) and Pfeiffer syndrome (FGFR1/FGFR2) but is a distinct FGFR1 phenotype.","Most cases are sporadic (de novo dominant mutations), although vertical transmission is documented."],"pathophysiology":"Osteoglophonic dysplasia is caused by heterozygous gain-of-function missense mutations in FGFR1, the gene encoding fibroblast growth factor receptor 1. Enhanced, ligand-independent FGFR1 signalling disturbs endochondral ossification, producing rhizomelic dwarfism; promotes premature fusion of the cranial sutures, causing craniosynostosis and the characteristic facies; and impairs bone remodelling, giving rise to the multiple non-ossifying, fibrous, radiolucent metaphyseal lesions that hollow out the long bones. Some of these fibrous lesions overproduce fibroblast growth factor 23 (FGF23), a phosphaturic hormone, so that a subset of patients develop renal phosphate wasting and hypophosphataemia, analogous to tumour-induced osteomalacia. The failure of tooth eruption reflects the disordered bone and dental matrix around developing teeth. FGFR1 lies within the same receptor tyrosine kinase family as FGFR3 (mutated in achondroplasia) and FGFR2, and the osteoglophonic phenotype is distinguished from the other FGFR-related craniosynostosis syndromes by the combination of rhizomelia and the diagnostic hollowed metaphyseal lesions. Inheritance is autosomal dominant, with most probands carrying de novo mutations.","additionalDifferentials":["Achondroplasia and hypochondroplasia (FGFR3) for the rhizomelic short stature","Other FGFR craniosynostosis syndromes: Pfeiffer, Crouzon and Apert (FGFR1/FGFR2)","Cleidocranial dysplasia (RUNX2) for delayed or failed tooth eruption","Fibrous dysplasia and McCune-Albright syndrome (GNAS) for lytic/fibrous bone lesions","Isolated non-ossifying fibromas and benign fibrous cortical defects","Jaffe-Campanacci syndrome (multiple non-ossifying fibromas with extraskeletal features)","Hereditary hypophosphataemic rickets (PHEX and other FGF23-related forms) for the phosphate wasting"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey demonstrating the radiolucent metaphyseal lesions and rhizomelia","Skull imaging or CT to assess the cranial sutures and craniosynostosis","Serum phosphate, calcium and alkaline phosphatase with assessment of renal phosphate handling (interpreted qualitatively); FGF23 where available","Orthopantomogram and dental imaging for unerupted and impacted teeth","FGFR1 molecular genetic testing by targeted analysis or gene panel","Ophthalmology review and, where indicated, neuroimaging for raised intracranial pressure","Renal function tests and urinalysis"],"redFlags":["Signs of raised intracranial pressure from multisuture craniosynostosis","Visual compromise from proptosis or optic nerve involvement","Symptomatic hypophosphataemia with bone pain, deformity or fractures","Airway obstruction or feeding difficulty from midface hypoplasia in infancy"],"prognosis":"The outlook is variable: stature is reduced and craniofacial and dental problems are lifelong, but with coordinated craniofacial, orthopaedic, dental and metabolic (phosphate) care, outcomes and life expectancy are generally reasonable. The principal complications relate to raised intracranial pressure, vision and phosphate wasting.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the metaphyseal lesions for isolated non-ossifying fibromas or fibrous dysplasia and missing the underlying syndrome","Overlooking FGF23-mediated hypophosphataemia as a treatable contributor to bone disease","Attributing the short stature to achondroplasia without recognising the craniofacial, dental and metaphyseal-lesion pattern","Failing to detect raised intracranial pressure in the setting of craniosynostosis"],"management":["Correction of hypophosphataemia driven by FGF23 excess with oral phosphate supplementation and an active vitamin D metabolite such as alfacalcidol or calcitriol","Cranial vault surgery for craniosynostosis, with monitoring and treatment of raised intracranial pressure","Orthopaedic management of the metaphyseal fibrous lesions, long-bone deformity and short stature","Dental and maxillofacial care for unerupted or impacted teeth and gingival overgrowth","Ophthalmology review for proptosis and neuroimaging where raised intracranial pressure is suspected","Coordinated multidisciplinary craniofacial, orthopaedic, dental and metabolic follow-up","Genetic counselling for the autosomal dominant gain-of-function FGFR1 mutation, with family assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nosology and classification of genetic skeletal disorders","Clinical genetics reference texts","Metabolic bone and endocrinology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteoid_osteoma":{"name":"Osteoid osteoma","system":"Musculoskeletal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["osteoid osteoma","benign osteoblastic nidus lesion"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone / standard orthopaedic oncology texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A small benign bone-forming tumour, by definition under about two centimetres, most common in children and young adults and predominating in males","The hallmark is well-localised pain that is characteristically worse at night and relieved dramatically by non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","Typically arises in the cortex of long bones, especially the femur and tibia, with the proximal femur a classic site","Imaging shows a small radiolucent nidus surrounded by dense reactive sclerosis; CT is the best modality to demonstrate the nidus","Spinal lesions may present with painful scoliosis, the lesion sitting on the concave side of the curve","The nidus produces prostaglandins, which explains both the nocturnal pain and its striking response to prostaglandin-inhibiting drugs","Treatment options include conservative management with NSAIDs, since many lesions eventually resolve, or radiofrequency ablation for persistent symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"Osteoid osteoma is a benign osteoblastic neoplasm consisting of a small central nidus of woven bone and osteoid set in vascular connective tissue, encircled by a zone of dense reactive sclerosis in the surrounding bone. The nidus is richly innervated and expresses high levels of cyclooxygenase enzymes, generating prostaglandins that mediate the intense, often nocturnal pain and the vasodilatation seen in the lesion. The prostaglandin dependence accounts for the characteristic relief with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. The tumour has limited growth potential, does not metastasise, and may involute spontaneously over years.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteoblastoma (larger, more aggressive, less reactive sclerosis)","Brodie abscess (subacute osteomyelitis)","Stress fracture","Osteomyelitis of other cause","Ewing sarcoma or other primary bone malignancy","Bone island (enostosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thin-section CT to identify and localise the nidus","Plain radiographs showing a lucent nidus with surrounding sclerosis","MRI to assess marrow and soft-tissue oedema, though it may overestimate aggressiveness","Bone scintigraphy showing focal intense uptake (double-density sign)","Correlation of symptom pattern with response to non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","Histological confirmation when the diagnosis is uncertain or tissue is obtained at ablation"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth or a lesion larger than expected suggesting osteoblastoma or malignancy","Progressive painful scoliosis or neurological signs from a spinal lesion","Atypical imaging with cortical destruction or a soft-tissue mass","Failure of pain to respond to non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, prompting reconsideration"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent; the lesion is benign and does not metastasise, symptoms often settle with medical treatment or spontaneous involution, and targeted ablation is usually curative with low recurrence. Malignant transformation does not occur.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the reactive sclerosis for osteomyelitis or malignancy and missing the small nidus","Overlooking a spinal osteoid osteoma as the cause of painful scoliosis in an adolescent","Relying on MRI alone, which can exaggerate surrounding oedema and suggest aggression","Incomplete ablation or excision of the nidus leading to persistent pain"],"management":["Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain control, which can suffice while the prostaglandin-driven lesion involutes spontaneously over years","CT-guided percutaneous thermal (radiofrequency) ablation of the nidus as the definitive treatment of choice","Surgical en bloc excision or curettage of the nidus where ablation is unsuitable, such as lesions adjacent to neurovascular structures or the spinal cord","Specific attention to spinal lesions, addressing any associated painful scoliosis","Imaging follow-up for the small risk of residual or recurrent nidus after treatment","Management within an orthopaedic oncology service, with histological confirmation when tissue is obtained"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone","Standard orthopaedic oncology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteomalacia":{"name":"osteomalacia","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["adult rickets","defective bone mineralisation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oxford Textbook of Endocrinology and Diabetes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Osteomalacia is impaired mineralisation of newly formed osteoid in the mature skeleton; the childhood equivalent, affecting the growth plate, is rickets.","The commonest cause worldwide is vitamin D deficiency (nutritional, malabsorption, limited sunlight); hypophosphataemia and mineralisation inhibitors are important non-calcipenic causes.","Classic biochemistry is low or low-normal calcium, low phosphate, raised alkaline phosphatase, raised PTH and low 25-hydroxyvitamin D.","Diffuse bone pain with a proximal myopathy (waddling gait, difficulty rising from a chair) is characteristic, often with bony tenderness on sternal or tibial pressure.","Looser zones (pseudofractures) on the compression side of long bones, pubic rami, scapula and ribs are a near-pathognomonic radiological feature.","Distinguish calcipenic forms (vitamin D or calcium deficiency, high PTH) from phosphopenic forms (renal phosphate wasting, FGF23-mediated) as treatment differs fundamentally.","Transiliac bone biopsy after tetracycline double-labelling showing increased osteoid thickness and prolonged mineralisation lag time is the gold standard but rarely required.","Hypocalcaemia and hypophosphataemia may worsen transiently when repletion begins ('hungry bone'), so monitor biochemistry during treatment."],"pathophysiology":"Mature bone is continually remodelled, with osteoblasts laying down unmineralised osteoid that is subsequently mineralised by deposition of calcium and phosphate as hydroxyapatite. In osteomalacia this mineralisation step fails or lags, so accumulating unmineralised osteoid weakens the skeleton. The dominant mechanism is inadequate availability of calcium and phosphate at the mineralising front, most often from vitamin D deficiency (reduced intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption, secondary hyperparathyroidism and renal phosphate loss). Other routes include chronic hypophosphataemia from renal phosphate wasting (FGF23 excess, Fanconi syndrome), renal failure with impaired 1-alpha-hydroxylation, hypophosphatasia (low tissue-non-specific alkaline phosphatase), and inhibitors of mineralisation such as aluminium, fluoride and some bisphosphonates.","additionalDifferentials":["osteoporosis","Paget's disease of bone","renal osteodystrophy","metastatic bone disease or multiple myeloma","polymyalgia rheumatica (proximal myopathy mimic)","primary hyperparathyroidism","fibromyalgia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["serum calcium, phosphate and alkaline phosphatase","25-hydroxyvitamin D and PTH","renal profile with urine phosphate and tubular reabsorption of phosphate (TmP/GFR)","plain radiographs seeking Looser zones","coeliac serology and markers of malabsorption","intact FGF23 where unexplained phosphate wasting is suspected","transiliac bone biopsy with tetracycline labelling in diagnostic uncertainty"],"redFlags":["symptomatic hypocalcaemia (tetany, seizures, prolonged QT interval)","insufficiency fractures, particularly of the femoral neck","severe proximal weakness causing recurrent falls","unexplained renal phosphate wasting raising suspicion of tumour-induced osteomalacia","underlying malabsorption such as coeliac disease or previous bariatric surgery"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent when the underlying deficiency is corrected, with bone pain and proximal myopathy usually resolving over weeks to months. Established skeletal deformity or fractures may leave residual disability.","pitfalls":["attributing symptoms to osteoporosis or fibromyalgia and missing osteomalacia","assuming normal serum calcium excludes the diagnosis","failing to seek an underlying malabsorptive cause","not distinguishing calcipenic from phosphopenic mechanisms before treatment","giving vitamin D alone when renal phosphate wasting is the primary problem"],"management":["Correction of the underlying deficiency — in calcipenic disease, vitamin D repletion with colecalciferol or ergocalciferol plus calcium supplementation","For phosphopenic forms, phosphate supplementation with an active vitamin D metabolite such as calcitriol or alfacalcidol, and an anti-FGF23 antibody such as burosumab where FGF23-mediated wasting is confirmed","Treatment of the specific cause, including a gluten-free diet in coeliac disease, active vitamin D analogues in renal impairment, and resection of a causative tumour in tumour-induced osteomalacia","Symptomatic support with analgesia for bone pain and rehabilitation for proximal myopathy","Biochemical monitoring of calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase and parathyroid hormone, adjusting therapy to normalise mineralisation and avoid hypercalcaemia","Orthopaedic management of pseudofractures, completed fractures and skeletal deformity","Referral to metabolic bone specialists for phosphopenic, hereditary or refractory disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Oxford Textbook of Endocrinology and Diabetes","Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Kumar and Clark's Clinical Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteomesopyknosis":{"name":"Osteomesopyknosis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["axial osteosclerosis","benign axial osteosclerosis","OSMPK"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / textbooks of skeletal dysplasia radiology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, benign sclerosing bone dysplasia in which osteosclerosis is confined to the axial skeleton","Characteristically the sclerosis affects the spine (vertebral endplates), pelvis, and proximal femora while sparing the skull and distal limbs","It is usually inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern and often comes to light incidentally on radiographs","The commonest symptom is chronic low back pain, which is generally mild and non-progressive","Unlike osteopetrosis, there is no marrow failure, cranial nerve compression, or increased fracture tendency","Bone turnover markers and calcium metabolism are typically normal, helping to distinguish it from metabolic causes of sclerosis","The main clinical importance is recognising it as benign to avoid unnecessary investigation for malignancy or metabolic bone disease","No specific treatment is required beyond reassurance and simple management of back pain"],"pathophysiology":"Osteomesopyknosis is a benign heritable sclerosing bone dysplasia characterised by increased bone density restricted to the axial skeleton, particularly the vertebrae, pelvis, and proximal femora. The precise molecular basis is not firmly established, but the pattern reflects a localised disturbance of the balance between bone formation and resorption favouring accrual of dense trabecular bone in axial sites, with sparing of the appendicular skeleton and skull. Because bone modelling and marrow function are otherwise preserved, there is no medullary compromise, and calcium homeostasis and bone-turnover biochemistry remain normal. The condition therefore behaves as a radiologically striking but clinically indolent finding, in contrast to the diffuse and complication-prone sclerosis of osteopetrosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteopetrosis and other diffuse sclerosing dysplasias","Osteoblastic metastases (e.g. from prostate or breast)","Paget disease of bone","Renal osteodystrophy with rugger-jersey spine","Fluorosis and other causes of increased bone density","Pyknodysostosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs of the spine and pelvis demonstrating the axial sclerosis pattern","Serum calcium, phosphate, and alkaline phosphatase (typically normal)","Bone turnover markers to exclude metabolic bone disease","Prostate-specific antigen or other tumour markers where metastasis is a concern","Family history and, where appropriate, radiographs of relatives","Cross-sectional imaging only if malignancy cannot be excluded clinically"],"redFlags":["Atypical or progressive bone pain raising concern for metastatic disease","Constitutional symptoms suggesting malignancy rather than a benign dysplasia","Rising tumour markers or abnormal bone biochemistry indicating an alternative diagnosis","Pathological fracture, which would be unexpected and warrant reassessment"],"prognosis":"Excellent; it is a benign, non-progressive condition with a normal life expectancy and no risk of marrow failure or nerve compression, with symptoms limited to mild back pain.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the axial sclerosis for osteoblastic metastases and subjecting patients to unnecessary cancer investigation","Confusing it with osteopetrosis and expecting marrow failure or fracture risk","Overlooking the normal bone biochemistry that supports a benign diagnosis","Failing to recognise the autosomal dominant pattern within a family"],"management":["Reassurance, since this is a benign, non-progressive dysplasia requiring no disease-modifying treatment","Conservative management of chronic low back pain with simple analgesia, physiotherapy and activity advice","Confirmation of the benign nature through normal calcium metabolism and bone turnover markers, so unnecessary intervention is avoided","Exclusion of osteoblastic metastasis where clinically warranted, for example with prostate-specific antigen, to avert misdiagnosis","Minimal radiographic follow-up given the indolent course and absence of marrow failure, fracture tendency or nerve compression","Genetic counselling and reassurance of relatives regarding the autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Textbooks of skeletal dysplasia radiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteomyelitis":{"name":"Osteomyelitis","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bone infection","vertebral osteomyelitis","spondylodiskitis","disk-space infection","septic diskitis","spinal osteomyelitis","long-bone osteomyelitis","periprosthetic joint infection","Brodie's abscess"],"chapter":136,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1061","clinicalPearls":["Three mechanisms underlie osteomyelitis: hematogenous spread, contiguous spread after surgery or trauma, and secondary infection due to vascular insufficiency or neuropathy; in adults hematogenous disease most often involves the vertebral column, whereas in children long bones are seeded (Harrison 22e p.1061).","The clinically decisive distinction is not a fixed duration but the presence or absence of bone necrosis (sequestra): acute osteomyelitis without necrosis can generally be cured with antimicrobials alone, while chronic osteomyelitis with sequestra requires combined surgical debridement (Harrison 22e p.1062).","Back pain is the leading symptom of vertebral osteomyelitis (>85%), and the location maps to the level of infection (lumbar ~60%, thoracic ~30%, cervical ~10%); only about half of patients are febrile, partly because of analgesic use (Harrison 22e p.1062).","ESR and CRP are markedly more useful than the leukocyte count for screening: CRP is elevated in essentially all and ESR in ~98% of vertebral osteomyelitis cases, whereas leukocytosis and neutrophilia have sensitivities of only ~65% and ~40% (Harrison 22e p.1062).","In patients without sepsis syndrome, antibiotics should be withheld until a pathogen is documented (by blood culture, bone biopsy, or aspirated pus), because pretreatment markedly lowers culture yield and an established microbiologic diagnosis guides therapy (Harrison 22e p.1062-1063).","MRI is the diagnostic gold standard for vertebral osteomyelitis and should be obtained urgently when neurologic impairment is present to detect epidural abscess or exclude disk herniation; 18F-FDG PET is the alternative when MRI is contraindicated or implants/multifocal disease are suspected (Harrison 22e p.1062).","A primary focus is found in only about half of hematogenous cases; common sources are the urinary tract, skin/soft tissue, intravascular catheters, and the endocardium, and endocarditis is identified in roughly 10% overall (and in ~50% of viridans streptococcal cases) (Harrison 22e p.1061-1062).","Implanted foreign material creates a zone of local immunodeficiency and a host-protein-coated surface that promotes staphylococcal adherence and biofilm formation, so device-related bone and joint infection generally cannot be cured by antimicrobials alone (Harrison 22e p.1062, 1066).","Brodie's abscess is a subacute form occurring mainly in young males, presenting with pain (~98%) and swelling (~53%) in the tibia or femur, with a median diagnostic delay of about 3 months; localized unexplained tibial/femoral pain in a young patient warrants imaging (Harrison 22e p.1064).","Subacute or chronic vertebral disease is typically caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis or Brucella in endemic regions, and by low-virulence organisms (coagulase-negative staphylococci, Cutibacterium acnes) in implant-associated infection; tuberculous spondylitis preferentially involves the thoracic spine (about two-thirds of cases) (Harrison 22e p.1062).","Synovial fluid analysis is central to diagnosing periprosthetic joint infection: leukocyte thresholds of ~1700/uL (knee) and ~4200/uL (hip) are about 90% sensitive and specific, and alpha-defensin is highly specific but limited by cost and sensitivity, so it should confirm rather than screen (Harrison 22e p.1066).","Chronic osteomyelitis can reactivate decades later; recurrence after a symptom-free interval exceeding 70 years has been described, most often in elderly patients with S. aureus disease acquired in the preantibiotic era (Harrison 22e p.1063-1064)."],"pathophysiology":"Osteomyelitis is infection of bone reached by microorganisms via one of three routes. In hematogenous disease, circulating organisms seed the metaphyses of long bones in children and the vertebral column in adults; because the adult intervertebral disk is avascular, organisms enter through the segmental arteries supplying the adjacent endplates and then spread into the disk, producing the characteristic involvement of two contiguous vertebrae and the intervening disk. Alternative routes include retrograde seeding through the prevertebral venous plexus and direct inoculation during spinal surgery, epidural procedures, or trauma. Contiguous-focus disease follows bone trauma or surgery, with wound infection after sternotomy, open-fracture repair, or prosthetic-joint placement. Vascular-insufficiency/neuropathic disease, most often in poorly controlled diabetes, arises from progressively deep foot infection driven by ischemia of skin, soft tissue, and bone combined with motor, sensory, and autonomic neuropathy. Foreign material is highly vulnerable: host proteins such as fibronectin coat the device, favoring staphylococcal adherence and biofilm formation that resists phagocytosis and antibiotics. Failure of timely or adequate therapy leads to bone necrosis (sequestra) and progression to chronic osteomyelitis with sinus tracts and soft-tissue destruction (Harrison 22e p.1061-1066).","additionalDifferentials":["Pyelonephritis (febrile back pain mimic)","Pancreatitis","Viral syndromes","Erosive osteochondrosis (most common MRI-based alternative diagnosis to vertebral osteomyelitis)","Bone metastases","Osteoporotic vertebral fracture"],"infectionMimics":["Spinal epidural abscess from another source","Septic arthritis adjacent to bone","Tuberculous spondylitis (Pott's disease) versus pyogenic osteomyelitis","Brucellar spondylitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Seronegative spondyloarthritis (ankylosing spondylitis, psoriatic, reactive, enteropathic arthritis), including Andersson erosive diskovertebral lesions","Spinal stenosis","Gouty spondylodiskitis","Septic/aseptic bone necrosis","Osteoporotic fracture and bone metastases on imaging"],"additionalInvestigations":["CRP and ESR as sensitive screening/monitoring markers (CRP elevated in ~100%, ESR in ~98% of vertebral cases)","Blood cultures before any antibiotics (positivity 30-78%, heavily reduced by pretreatment)","CT-guided or open bone biopsy when blood cultures are negative, with aerobic, anaerobic, and fungal culture plus histopathology","Mycobacterial and brucellar testing in subacute/chronic, granulomatous, or epidemiologically suggestive cases","Nonculture molecular methods (eubacterial/multiplex PCR, metagenomics) when cultures are negative despite suggestive histology, to detect unusual organisms (e.g., Tropheryma whipplei, Helicobacter)","MRI as gold standard; CT to guide biopsy and assess sequestra; plain radiography useful mainly in subacute/chronic disease","18F-FDG PET when MRI contraindicated or multifocal/implant disease suspected (sensitive but not specific; useful to exclude PJI)","SPECT/CT with labeled leukocytes or antigranulocyte antibodies for osteomyelitis of >1 year duration; three-phase bone scan limited within ~1 year of surgery due to ongoing bone remodeling","Synovial fluid leukocyte count and alpha-defensin, multiple intraoperative tissue cultures/histology, and sonication of explanted hardware for biofilm organisms in suspected PJI"],"redFlags":["New or progressive neurologic deficit (radiculopathy, weakness, sensory loss, bowel/bladder dysfunction, paralysis) suggesting spinal epidural abscess, present in about one-third of vertebral cases","Cervical or thoracic spinal involvement and S. aureus etiology, identified risk factors for severe neurologic deficit","Persistent or rising CRP, persistent pain, or sepsis syndrome indicating undrained abscess or treatment failure","Persistent pain after CRP normalization, suggesting mechanical complication such as severe osteonecrosis or spinal instability requiring orthopedic evaluation","Sinus tract, wound-healing impairment, or implant loosening signaling deep device-related infection"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on early diagnosis, presence of bone necrosis, and whether an implant is involved. Acute hematogenous vertebral and long-bone osteomyelitis without sequestra or hardware can usually be cured with antimicrobials alone; a randomized trial in pyogenic vertebral osteomyelitis reported ~91% cure at one year with no difference between 6- and 12-week regimens. Failure of adequate treatment, open fractures, and unaddressed implant infection drive progression to chronic osteomyelitis with sinus tracts and soft-tissue damage. In fracture-related infection, implant-retention success falls with chronicity (about 86-100% in early, 82-89% in delayed, and ~67% in late infection). In periprosthetic joint infection, implant retention offers >80% infection-free survival only when the infection is acute, the implant is stable, the pathogen is susceptible to a biofilm-active agent, and the soft tissues are intact; in patients with severe comorbidity, lifelong suppressive therapy may be the chosen goal rather than cure (Harrison 22e p.1063-1067).","pitfalls":["Starting empiric antibiotics before obtaining cultures in a non-septic patient, which markedly lowers culture yield and obscures the microbiologic diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1062-1063).","Relying on plain radiographs or the leukocyte count in acute disease, both of which have low sensitivity; normal radiographs do not exclude acute osteomyelitis (Harrison 22e p.1062).","Using follow-up MRI to judge treatment response in uncomplicated cases, since radiologic improvement correlates poorly with clinical healing; imaging follow-up is reserved for pyogenic complications (Harrison 22e p.1063).","Misinterpreting a three-phase bone scan or 18F-FDG PET within ~1 year of surgery, when ongoing bone remodeling produces nonspecific uptake (Harrison 22e p.1064, 1066).","Assuming antimicrobials alone will cure device-associated osteomyelitis or PJI; without appropriate surgery such therapy is generally only suppressive (Harrison 22e p.1061, 1067).","Dismissing coagulase-negative staphylococci or Cutibacterium acnes as contaminants, when in the setting of an implant or prolonged bacteremia they may be the true low-virulence pathogens (Harrison 22e p.1062).","Failing to consider tuberculous or brucellar disease in patients from or traveling to endemic regions, where subacute/chronic vertebral osteomyelitis predominates (Harrison 22e p.1063-1065)."],"management":["Withhold antimicrobials until a pathogen is secured by blood culture, aspirated pus, or image-guided bone biopsy, unless the patient has sepsis syndrome","Prolonged pathogen-directed antibiotics, with empirical anti-staphylococcal cover chosen while awaiting cultures and narrowed to targeted therapy once sensitivities return","Surgical debridement and removal of sequestra for chronic disease with bone necrosis or retained hardware, since antibiotics alone cannot sterilise devitalised bone","Urgent MRI and neurosurgical or spinal decompression when vertebral disease produces neurological deficit or an epidural abscess","Analgesia and spinal bracing or immobilisation for painful vertebral involvement","Serial CRP and ESR to monitor treatment response and help guide antibiotic duration","Coordinate care through orthopaedic or spinal surgery, infectious diseases, and outpatient parenteral antibiotic services"],"severityClassification":"No single accepted comprehensive scheme exists. Osteomyelitis is categorized by pathogenesis (hematogenous, contiguous, vascular insufficiency/neuropathic), by course (acute versus subacute/chronic, with the presence or absence of sequestra being the decisive feature), and by location (long bone, vertebral, periarticular). The Cierny-Mader staging system classifies long-bone disease by anatomic extent, host physiologic class (systemic and local compromise of immunity, metabolism, and vascularity), and radiographic findings, and is used mainly to guide surgical management. Implant-associated disease is further classified by timing relative to surgery (e.g., early/acute, delayed, late), and periprosthetic joint infection is grouped for therapeutic decisions as acute hematogenous, early postinterventional, or chronic (Harrison 22e p.1061, 1066).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1061","Harrison 22e p.1062","Harrison 22e p.1063","Harrison 22e p.1064","Harrison 22e p.1066","Harrison 22e p.1067"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteopathia_striata":{"name":"osteopathia striata","system":"musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Voorhoeve disease","osteopathia striata with cranial sclerosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Resnick Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Osteopathia striata is a sclerosing bone dysplasia defined radiologically by fine linear longitudinal striations in the metaphyses and diaphyses of long bones and a fan-like pattern in the iliac wings.","Isolated osteopathia striata (Voorhoeve disease) is usually an asymptomatic incidental radiographic finding of no clinical consequence.","When combined with cranial sclerosis it forms an X-linked disorder caused by AMER1 (WTX) mutations, typically more severe in affected males and often lethal or with major anomalies in hemizygous males.","Females with osteopathia striata with cranial sclerosis characteristically have macrocephaly, cranial nerve compression with hearing loss, and cleft palate.","The striations run parallel to the long axis of the bone and do not disturb bone modelling or cause pathological fracture in the isolated form.","It is one of the classic mixed sclerosing bone dysplasias and may coexist with osteopoikilosis and melorheostosis.","Bone scintigraphy is typically normal, helping distinguish it from active sclerotic processes such as metastasis or osteomyelitis.","Recognition matters chiefly to avoid unnecessary investigation of an incidental benign pattern."],"pathophysiology":"The isolated form is a sporadic or benign developmental anomaly of endochondral ossification in which longitudinal columns of persistent dense trabecular bone produce the characteristic striations without disturbing overall bone architecture. The syndromic osteopathia striata with cranial sclerosis results from loss-of-function mutations in AMER1, which encodes a negative regulator of the canonical Wnt/beta-catenin signalling pathway central to osteoblast activity. Loss of this brake enhances Wnt signalling and osteoblastic bone formation, producing the striations in long bones together with sclerotic overgrowth of the skull base and vault. The X-linked pattern and X-inactivation account for the milder mosaic phenotype in females and the severe or lethal presentation in hemizygous males.","additionalDifferentials":["osteopoikilosis","melorheostosis","osteopetrosis","sclerotic bone metastases","engelmann diaphyseal dysplasia","healing rickets with metaphyseal lines"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["plain radiography of long bones and pelvis for striation pattern","skull radiography or CT for cranial sclerosis in syndromic cases","bone scintigraphy to demonstrate absent increased uptake","audiometry when cranial nerve involvement is suspected","AMER1 gene sequencing in syndromic presentations","serum calcium, phosphate and alkaline phosphatase to exclude metabolic bone disease"],"redFlags":["progressive hearing loss or other cranial neuropathy suggesting foraminal narrowing","macrocephaly with developmental concern in an infant","severe skeletal or visceral anomalies in a male fetus or neonate","unexpected sclerotic lesions with abnormal tracer uptake prompting reappraisal"],"prognosis":"Isolated osteopathia striata is benign with a normal life expectancy and requires no treatment; the syndromic form carries morbidity from cranial nerve compression and craniofacial anomalies and may be lethal in affected males.","pitfalls":["Mistaking incidental striations for sclerotic metastases and initiating an oncological work-up.","Overlooking associated cranial sclerosis and its neurological consequences by imaging long bones alone.","Failing to offer genetic counselling once the X-linked syndromic form is recognised.","Confusing the linear striations with the rounded foci of osteopoikilosis.","Attributing hearing loss to unrelated causes rather than foraminal stenosis."],"management":["Reassure and avoid intervention in isolated (Voorhoeve) disease, which is a benign incidental radiographic finding of no clinical consequence","Confirm the diagnosis on plain radiography and use bone scintigraphy showing absent uptake to distinguish the striations from osteoblastic metastases","Check serum calcium, phosphate and alkaline phosphatase to exclude a metabolic bone disorder","Distinguish the isolated form from osteopathia striata with cranial sclerosis, arranging AMER1 sequencing where cranial or craniofacial features are present","Arrange audiometry and cranial-nerve assessment when the syndromic form with skull-base sclerosis is suspected","Offer clinical genetics referral and family assessment for the X-linked syndromic form"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Resnick Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders, current edition","Taybi and Lachman Radiology of Syndromes, Metabolic Disorders and Skeletal Dysplasias","International Skeletal Dysplasia Society nosology"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteopathia_striata_with_cranial_sclerosis":{"name":"Osteopathia striata with cranial sclerosis (AMER1)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OSCS","AMER1","WTX","Voorhoeve disease with cranial sclerosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Radiographic hallmark is fine longitudinal linear striations in the metaphyses of long bones and the iliac wings, combined with dense sclerosis of the skull base and cranial vault","Caused by variants in AMER1 (also called WTX) on the X chromosome, which regulates WNT/beta-catenin signalling and bone formation","X-linked inheritance produces a striking sex difference: affected females have a relatively mild phenotype, whereas affected males are severely affected and the condition may be lethal in males in some families","Cranial sclerosis can narrow neural foramina and cause cranial-nerve deficits, notably hearing loss and facial palsy","Characteristic facial features include macrocephaly, frontal bossing, hypertelorism and a flat nasal bridge","The linear metaphyseal striations are asymptomatic and do not weaken the bone, in contrast to the clinically important skull-base thickening","Management centres on hearing surveillance and decompression of narrowed cranial-nerve foramina where progressive deficits arise, alongside multidisciplinary supportive care"],"pathophysiology":"Osteopathia striata with cranial sclerosis results from loss-of-function variants in AMER1 (WTX), an X-linked gene whose protein modulates the WNT/beta-catenin pathway that governs osteoblast differentiation and bone mass. Dysregulated WNT signalling produces two contrasting bony patterns: benign linear striations within the metaphyses of tubular bones and the pelvis, and progressive sclerotic thickening of the cranial vault and skull base. Because the gene lies on the X chromosome, hemizygous males typically show a severe or prenatally lethal phenotype, while heterozygous females, protected by X-inactivation mosaicism, display milder disease. The skull-base overgrowth encroaches on foramina, accounting for the cranial neuropathies.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteopoikilosis and other sclerosing bone dysplasias","Craniometaphyseal dysplasia","Frontometaphyseal dysplasia","Osteopetrosis","Melorheostosis","Pyknodysostosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey demonstrating metaphyseal striations and iliac involvement","Skull radiographs or CT showing cranial vault and skull-base sclerosis and foraminal narrowing","Audiometry to detect and monitor conductive or sensorineural hearing loss","Cranial-nerve examination and neuroimaging when deficits develop","AMER1 molecular genetic testing and X-linked family segregation analysis","Developmental and cardiac evaluation in severely affected males where indicated"],"redFlags":["Progressive hearing loss or facial weakness indicating cranial-nerve foraminal compression","Visual disturbance suggesting optic canal narrowing","Severe presentation in a male fetus or neonate, which may be life-threatening","Raised intracranial features or airway compromise from extensive craniofacial overgrowth"],"prognosis":"Affected females generally have a good prognosis with intellect usually preserved, though cranial-nerve complications may accrue over time; affected males can have severe multisystem disease that is frequently lethal in the prenatal or neonatal period.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the benign metaphyseal striations for a sinister process and overlooking the clinically important cranial sclerosis","Failing to recognise the X-linked inheritance and the marked male severity when counselling families","Delaying audiological and cranial-nerve surveillance until deficits are advanced","Attributing developmental or facial features to an unrelated syndrome without genetic confirmation"],"management":["No disease-modifying drug exists, so management targets the cranial-nerve complications of skull-base sclerosis with structured surveillance","Serial audiometry and hearing rehabilitation with amplification for conductive or sensorineural loss from foraminal narrowing","Cranial-nerve monitoring and neuroimaging when facial palsy, hearing loss or other deficits develop, with surgical foraminal decompression for progressive compression","Multidisciplinary craniofacial input for macrocephaly, cleft palate repair and associated dysmorphism","AMER1 molecular confirmation and clinical genetics referral with X-linked family segregation counselling","Antenatal and paediatric evaluation of hemizygous males, who may have severe or lethal multisystem disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders","GeneReviews: AMER1/OSCS"],"crossLinks":["osteopoikilosis","frontometaphyseal_dysplasia"]},"osteopetrosis":{"name":"Osteopetrosis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["marble bone disease","Albers-Schonberg disease","CLCN7","TCIRG1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of disorders of defective osteoclast bone resorption producing dense, sclerotic but paradoxically brittle bones","The severe autosomal recessive (infantile malignant) form presents early with marrow failure, hepatosplenomegaly from extramedullary haematopoiesis, and cranial nerve compression, whereas the dominant adult form is milder","Common causative genes include TCIRG1 and CLCN7, which are needed for the acidification that allows osteoclasts to dissolve mineralised matrix","Encroachment of dense bone on marrow spaces causes anaemia, thrombocytopenia, and susceptibility to infection, while narrowed skull foramina compress optic, facial, and auditory nerves","Bones are dense but fragile and prone to fracture, and there is a risk of osteomyelitis, particularly of the mandible","Radiographs show diffusely dense bones, a bone-in-bone appearance, sandwich or rugger-jersey vertebrae, and metaphyseal Erlenmeyer-flask deformity","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation can be curative in severe osteoclast-intrinsic infantile forms, whereas milder forms are managed supportively"],"pathophysiology":"Osteopetrosis results from failure of osteoclast-mediated bone resorption, most often due to defects in the machinery that acidifies the resorption lacuna, such as the proton pump subunit encoded by TCIRG1 or the chloride channel encoded by CLCN7. Without effective resorption, bone remodelling is disrupted and immature, poorly organised, densely mineralised bone accumulates. This encroaches on the marrow cavity, causing cytopenias and extramedullary haematopoiesis, narrows cranial foramina to compress nerves, and produces bone that is dense yet mechanically brittle. Some forms reflect defective osteoclast differentiation rather than function, which influences whether transplantation is likely to help.","additionalDifferentials":["Pyknodysostosis","Sclerosteosis and van Buchem disease","Osteopoikilosis and osteopathia striata","Metastatic or myelofibrotic marrow infiltration","Fluorosis and heavy-metal osteosclerosis","Hypervitaminosis and other sclerosing bone dysplasias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey demonstrating diffuse sclerosis, bone-in-bone appearance, and metaphyseal flaring","Full blood count and blood film to assess cytopenias and extramedullary haematopoiesis","Molecular genetic testing of an osteopetrosis gene panel including TCIRG1 and CLCN7","Cross-sectional imaging of cranial foramina for nerve compression","Ophthalmological and audiological assessment for optic and auditory involvement","Bone marrow evaluation and biochemical assessment of calcium metabolism"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual loss from optic nerve compression requiring urgent decompression consideration","Marrow failure with severe anaemia, thrombocytopenia, or recurrent serious infection","Osteomyelitis, particularly of the mandible","Hypocalcaemic complications in the infantile form"],"prognosis":"Untreated severe infantile osteopetrosis is often fatal in early childhood from marrow failure and infection, whereas transplantation can be curative in suitable osteoclast-intrinsic forms. The dominant adult form is compatible with a normal lifespan but carries fracture, osteomyelitis, and nerve-compression morbidity.","pitfalls":["Assuming dense bone is strong and underestimating fracture risk","Delaying transplantation assessment in severe infantile disease","Missing progressive cranial nerve compression, especially optic nerve involvement","Failing to distinguish osteoclast-intrinsic from osteoclast-poor forms, which affects transplant suitability"],"management":["Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for severe infantile autosomal recessive disease due to osteoclast-intrinsic defects, which can be curative","Supportive haematological care with transfusion for anaemia and thrombocytopenia and prompt treatment of intercurrent infection","Careful management of fractures, recognising that the dense bone is brittle and prone to malunion, with vigilance for osteomyelitis, particularly of the mandible","Regular ophthalmological and audiological surveillance for optic, facial and auditory nerve compression, with surgical decompression of narrowed cranial foramina where sight or hearing is threatened","Gene-panel testing including TCIRG1 and CLCN7 to identify osteoclast-poor differentiation defects, in which transplantation is unlikely to help","Monitor calcium metabolism, watching for hypocalcaemia in infantile disease","Clinical genetics referral with family screening and counselling for the inherited forms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteopoikilosis":{"name":"Osteopoikilosis (LEMD3)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["spotted bone disease","osteopathia condensans disseminata","LEMD3","MAN1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Usually an incidental radiographic finding of numerous small, symmetrical, round or oval sclerotic foci clustered in the epiphyses and metaphyses of long bones and in the pelvis, carpus and tarsus","The individual is typically asymptomatic; when symptoms occur they are mild joint aches rather than a destructive process","Caused by loss-of-function variants in LEMD3, encoding an inner nuclear membrane protein that antagonises BMP and TGF-beta signalling, leading to focal islands of dense trabecular bone","Inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with variable penetrance, so unaffected-appearing relatives may still carry the variant","Bone scintigraphy characteristically shows no increased uptake in the sclerotic foci, an important feature distinguishing it from osteoblastic metastases","When cutaneous connective-tissue naevi (elastomas or collagenomas) coexist with the spotty bone lesions, the combined disorder is termed Buschke-Ollendorff syndrome","Management is generally reassurance and avoidance of unnecessary intervention once the benign nature is confirmed"],"pathophysiology":"Osteopoikilosis arises from heterozygous loss-of-function variants in LEMD3, whose protein product resides in the inner nuclear membrane and normally dampens bone morphogenetic protein and transforming growth factor-beta signalling. Reduced antagonism tips the balance towards osteoblastic activity in discrete foci, producing scattered islands of compact lamellar bone within the spongiosa. These enostosis-like foci concentrate near the ends of long bones and around the pelvis and short tubular bones, remain metabolically quiet, and do not disturb the overall bone architecture, explaining the asymptomatic incidental presentation and the absence of scintigraphic uptake.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteoblastic (sclerotic) skeletal metastases, particularly from prostate or breast carcinoma","Mastocytosis with sclerotic bone deposits","Tuberous sclerosis with sclerotic bone islands","Melorheostosis (which can coexist within the LEMD3 spectrum)","Osteopathia striata with cranial sclerosis","Solitary bone island (enostosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs or skeletal survey demonstrating the characteristic symmetrical sclerotic foci","Bone scintigraphy to confirm absent tracer uptake and help exclude metastatic disease","Cross-sectional imaging where distinction from metastasis remains uncertain","Skin examination and, if lesions are present, biopsy for connective-tissue naevi (Buschke-Ollendorff overlap)","LEMD3 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis and inform family counselling","Family history review and consideration of imaging in first-degree relatives"],"redFlags":["New focal bone pain or a lesion showing increasing size, cortical destruction or scintigraphic uptake, suggesting an alternative diagnosis such as metastasis","Unexplained weight loss or a known primary malignancy raising concern for osteoblastic metastases mimicking the foci","Progressive limb pain or contracture suggesting associated melorheostosis"],"prognosis":"The skeletal condition is benign and does not shorten life or cause meaningful disability; the main risk is diagnostic confusion with malignancy leading to unnecessary investigation, and prognosis is excellent once the diagnosis is recognised.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the sclerotic foci as osteoblastic metastases and initiating an unnecessary oncological workup","Overlooking coexisting cutaneous connective-tissue naevi that would establish Buschke-Ollendorff syndrome","Failing to counsel relatives despite the autosomal dominant inheritance with variable penetrance","Attributing incidental joint symptoms wholly to the bone foci rather than considering another cause"],"management":["Recognise the benign nature and avoid unnecessary treatment, since the sclerotic foci are asymptomatic and do not weaken bone","Use bone scintigraphy showing absent tracer uptake to distinguish the foci from osteoblastic metastases and prevent needless investigation","Reserve cross-sectional imaging for cases where distinction from malignancy remains uncertain","Simple analgesia for the mild joint aches that occasionally occur","Examine the skin and biopsy any connective-tissue naevi to identify Buschke-Ollendorff overlap","LEMD3 molecular testing and autosomal dominant family counselling, with awareness of variable penetrance in relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders","GeneReviews: LEMD3-related disorders"],"crossLinks":["buschke_ollendorff_syndrome","osteopathia_striata_with_cranial_sclerosis"]},"osteoporosis":{"name":"Osteoporosis","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic Bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["low bone density"],"chapter":423,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3291","clinicalPearls":["WHO operationally defines osteoporosis as a bone density T-score of -2.5 or below (>=2.5 SD below the young-adult mean for same sex/race), measured at lumbar spine, total hip, or femoral neck; osteopenia (low bone density) is a T-score of -1.0 to -2.5 and is a risk factor for developing osteoporosis (Harrison 22e p.3291).","A fragility fracture (hip or vertebral) by definition establishes osteoporosis and warrants treatment, regardless of the T-score; patients presenting with hip or spine fractures by definition have osteoporosis (Harrison 22e p.3291, p.3298).","Osteoporosis is most common among postmenopausal women, but ~30% of fragility fractures occur in men; secondary osteoporosis arises from other underlying diseases (Harrison 22e p.3291).",">50% of fractures, including hip fractures, among postmenopausal women occur in individuals with osteopenia rather than densitometric osteoporosis, because that population is much larger - hence the shift toward absolute fracture-risk assessment (Harrison 22e p.3291).","FRAX and the Garvan Fracture Risk Calculator estimate 10-year probability of hip or major osteoporotic fracture by combining age, sex, and clinical risk factors with or without BMD, and inform country-specific intervention thresholds distinct from diagnostic thresholds (Harrison 22e p.3291, p.3298).","Glucocorticoids are the most common cause of medication-induced osteoporosis (Harrison 22e p.3296).","Any fracture (excluding fingers, toes, face, skull) in a woman aged >50 or a man aged >60 should trigger investigation for osteoporosis (Harrison 22e p.3293)."],"pathophysiology":"Osteoporosis is reduced bone strength leading to skeletal fragility and fractures; BMD alone underestimates fragility because microarchitectural deterioration and component misalignment also contribute. A low peak bone mass (genetic, hormonal, nutritional) may underlie disease, with superimposed age-related and extrinsic changes in bone remodeling. In adults remodeling (not modeling) predominates and serves to repair microdamage and supply calcium. Estrogen deficiency drives bone loss by (1) activating new remodeling sites and (2) creating an imbalance favoring resorption over formation: loss of estrogen increases RANKL and reduces osteoprotegerin, increasing osteoclast formation/recruitment, while shortening osteoblast life span and prolonging osteoclast longevity/activity. Trabecular bone (80% of surface area) is preferentially affected, so vertebral fractures are the earliest skeletal consequence. Estrogen also regulates remodeling in men. Heritability of BMD is ~50-80%; LRP5 activating mutations cause high bone mass and inactivating mutations cause osteoporosis-pseudoglioma syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3293-3295).","additionalDifferentials":["Osteomalacia (severe vitamin D deficiency in adults)","Multiple myeloma masquerading as generalized osteoporosis","Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS)","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Thyrotoxicosis / excessive thyroxine","Cushing's syndrome","Malabsorption / celiac disease","Mastocytosis","Osteogenesis imperfecta"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Myeloma (presents with bone pain and characteristic punched-out lesions; can masquerade as generalized osteoporosis)","Osteomalacia from vitamin D deficiency","Metabolic bone disease such as CKD-mineral bone disorder","Osteoarthritis/spinal spondylosis (bone spurs falsely raise spine BMD on DXA)"],"additionalInvestigations":["DXA of lumbar spine and hip (standard for BMD; T-scores vs young adults, Z-scores vs age/sex-matched reference)","Vertebral imaging per age/BMD/risk-factor indications (e.g. women >=70, men >=80; or with low-trauma fracture, height loss >=4 cm, glucocorticoid use)","Complete blood count; serum and 24-h urine calcium; renal and liver function tests","Serum PTH (and PTHrP) when hypercalcemia is present to distinguish hyperparathyroidism from malignancy","Serum 25(OH)D in those with fragility fracture or osteoporotic-range BMD","TSH to evaluate for hyperthyroidism","Serum/urine protein electrophoresis and serum free light chains to exclude myeloma; bone marrow biopsy if equivocal","Transglutaminase IgA antibodies for celiac disease","24-h urinary free cortisol or dexamethasone-suppressed cortisol when Cushing's suspected","24-h urine histamine or serum tryptase when mastocytosis suspected","Serum testosterone in men with osteoporosis","Biochemical bone turnover markers (P1NP, osteocalcin, bone-specific ALP for formation; serum/urine N- and C-telopeptide for resorption) mainly to monitor treatment response","Bone biopsy with double tetracycline labeling (now largely replaced; retained for CKD-MBD and research)"],"redFlags":["Vertebral fracture (even asymptomatic) is a major sign of skeletal fragility and markedly increases risk of subsequent fracture, especially within the first 12-24 months (imminent fracture risk)","Significant height loss (>4 cm) suggesting occult vertebral fracture","Hip fracture - high mortality (20-25% die within the year) and major loss of independence","More chronic severe pain after vertebral fracture should raise suspicion of multiple myeloma or other underlying bone condition"],"prognosis":"Lifetime osteoporotic fracture risk for a 50-year-old Caucasian woman is ~50% and ~25% for a man; lifetime hip fracture probability ~14% (women) and ~5% (men), roughly half for African Americans. Hip fractures carry high morbidity and mortality with 20-25% of patients dying within the year (higher among men and African Americans), and ~30% of survivors requiring long-term care, many never regaining prior independence. A first fracture exponentially increases risk of further fracture, especially in the first year; almost 20% of women sustain a second fracture within 2 years (Harrison 22e p.3291-3293).","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude secondary causes of osteoporosis (e.g. myeloma, hyperparathyroidism, thyrotoxicosis, Cushing's, malabsorption/celiac, hypogonadism, mastocytosis) before attributing low bone mass to primary disease, particularly in patients with fractures or unexpectedly low Z-scores.","Excluding a patient from osteoporosis evaluation simply because a fracture was traumatic - patients with osteoporosis are also more likely to sustain traumatic fractures, and any fracture warrants skeletal assessment.","Relying solely on BMD, which can underestimate fragility (microarchitectural deterioration not captured) and is falsely elevated in the spine by osteoarthritic bone spurs/spondylosis; small slim people show falsely low DXA values.","Fewer than 20% of fracture patients are investigated or treated for osteoporosis within 6 months; postfracture care remains fragmented.","Drug holidays are appropriate to consider for oral/IV bisphosphonates but should be avoided for denosumab and anabolic agents because of more rapid offset."],"management":["First-line antiresorptive therapy with bisphosphonates, or denosumab where bisphosphonates are unsuitable, to reduce fracture risk","Anabolic agents such as teriparatide or romosozumab for very high fracture risk, followed by an antiresorptive to consolidate the gains","Ensure calcium and vitamin D repletion, weight-bearing and muscle-strengthening exercise, smoking cessation and moderation of alcohol","Fall-prevention measures with correction of visual and gait hazards to avert fragility fractures","Identify and treat secondary causes, minimise glucocorticoid exposure, and consider oestrogen or a selective oestrogen-receptor modulator in appropriate postmenopausal women","Base treatment decisions on absolute fracture-risk assessment such as FRAX and monitor response with serial DXA","Consider a drug holiday after prolonged bisphosphonate therapy, and avoid abrupt denosumab discontinuation without a follow-on antiresorptive to prevent rebound vertebral fractures"],"severityClassification":"WHO densitometric categories: normal (T-score >= -1.0), osteopenia/low bone density (T-score -1.0 to -2.5), osteoporosis (T-score <= -2.5 at lumbar spine, femoral neck, or total hip). Presence of a fragility fracture (notably hip or vertebral) defines established/clinical osteoporosis regardless of T-score. Absolute fracture risk (FRAX/Garvan 10-year hip and major osteoporotic fracture probability) is increasingly used alongside or instead of T-score for treatment decisions (Harrison 22e p.3291, p.3297-3298).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3291"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteosarcoma":{"name":"Osteosarcoma","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["osteogenic sarcoma","primary bone sarcoma","malignant bone tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest primary malignant bone tumour, arising most often in adolescents and young adults around the knee (distal femur and proximal tibia) and proximal humerus","Presents with progressive deep bone pain, often worse at night and not related to activity, sometimes with a palpable mass, swelling, or a pathological fracture","Radiographs may show bone destruction, new bone formation, a 'sunburst' spiculated pattern, and periosteal elevation (Codman's triangle)","Any persistent, unexplained, or night bone pain and swelling in a young person should raise suspicion and prompt urgent imaging rather than reassurance","Diagnosis requires biopsy, which should be carefully planned, ideally at the treating specialist centre, so as not to compromise later limb-salvage surgery","Staging with MRI of the whole bone and CT of the chest is essential, as the lungs are the commonest site of metastasis","Treatment is multidisciplinary, combining chemotherapy with surgical resection (limb salvage where feasible, otherwise amputation), transforming what was once a rapidly fatal disease","A second peak occurs in older adults, often secondary to Paget's disease or previous irradiation"],"pathophysiology":"Osteosarcoma is a malignant tumour of mesenchymal cells that produce osteoid or immature bone. It typically arises in the metaphysis of rapidly growing long bones, explaining its predilection for the knee region in adolescence during the growth spurt. The tumour destroys normal bone while laying down disorganised neoplastic osteoid, lifts and stimulates the periosteum, and breaks through the cortex to form a soft-tissue mass. It spreads haematogenously, with a strong tendency to metastasise to the lungs, and micrometastases are often present at diagnosis even when not radiologically evident. Predisposing factors include prior radiation, Paget's disease of bone, and genetic conditions such as hereditary retinoblastoma and Li-Fraumeni syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Ewing's sarcoma","Osteomyelitis (can mimic tumour clinically and radiologically)","Chondrosarcoma","Metastatic bone disease","Benign but aggressive lesions (e.g. giant cell tumour, osteoblastoma)","Aneurysmal bone cyst","Stress fracture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs of the affected region as the initial imaging","MRI of the whole involved bone to define extent, soft-tissue mass, and skip lesions","CT of the chest to detect pulmonary metastases","Biopsy (carefully planned at the treating centre) for histological diagnosis","Bone scan or PET for staging and detection of distant disease, plus baseline bloods including alkaline phosphatase and LDH"],"redFlags":["Persistent or night-time bone pain with swelling in a child, adolescent, or young adult requiring urgent specialist referral","Pathological fracture through a destructive bone lesion","A biopsy performed outside the treating centre risking tumour contamination and jeopardising limb salvage","Rapidly enlarging soft-tissue mass over bone"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends principally on the presence of metastases at diagnosis and the tumour's response to chemotherapy; with modern combined chemotherapy and surgery many patients with localised, chemosensitive disease achieve long-term survival, whereas metastatic or chemoresistant disease carries a substantially worse outlook.","pitfalls":["Dismissing persistent bone pain in a young person as growing pains or a sports injury, delaying diagnosis","Performing an ill-planned biopsy that contaminates tissue planes and precludes limb salvage","Confusing osteosarcoma with osteomyelitis and vice versa without adequate imaging and histology","Failing to stage the chest and missing pulmonary metastases"],"management":["Neoadjuvant multi-agent chemotherapy, typically high-dose methotrexate, doxorubicin and cisplatin, before definitive surgery","Wide surgical resection with limb salvage where feasible, or amputation when adequate margins cannot otherwise be achieved, followed by adjuvant chemotherapy guided by histological necrosis","Plan the diagnostic biopsy at the treating specialist sarcoma centre so the tract does not compromise later limb-salvage surgery","Surgically resect pulmonary metastases, since the lungs are the dominant site of spread and metastasectomy can still be curative","Manage through a specialist bone-sarcoma multidisciplinary team with orthopaedic oncology, medical oncology and radiology","Long-term surveillance with chest imaging and imaging of the primary site for pulmonary metastasis and local recurrence","Genetic assessment where hereditary retinoblastoma or Li-Fraumeni syndrome is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"othello_syndrome":{"name":"Othello Syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Psychiatry","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Delusional jealousy","Morbid jealousy"],"chapter":null,"source":"New Oxford Textbook of Psychiatry","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Delusional conviction of a partner's infidelity, held with pathological intensity on inadequate or absurd evidence.","The patient gathers proof by checking clothing, phones, or genitalia, following the partner, and demanding confessions.","Strongly associated with alcohol misuse and with neurological disease, including Parkinson's disease on dopaminergic therapy, stroke, and dementia.","Carries a high forensic risk and is a recognised antecedent of intimate-partner homicide.","May occur within delusional disorder, schizophrenia, mood disorders, or as an organic delusional syndrome.","Dopamine agonists and levodopa in Parkinson's disease are recognised precipitants.","Separation from the partner may not remove risk, which can transfer to a subsequent partner."],"pathophysiology":"Othello syndrome is a delusional preoccupation with partner infidelity that can arise as a primary delusional disorder or secondary to alcohol dependence, psychotic illness, mood disorder, or organic brain disease. Frontal and right-hemisphere dysfunction is implicated, consistent with cases following stroke and neurodegeneration, and with impaired belief evaluation and behavioural inhibition. Dopaminergic overactivity, whether from alcohol and stimulant effects or from dopamine-replacement therapy in Parkinson's disease, contributes through aberrant salience and reward-related processing that entrenches the jealous conviction.","additionalDifferentials":["Alcohol-related psychosis or dependence","Schizophrenia and delusional disorder","Parkinson's disease with dopaminergic therapy-related psychosis","Dementia, including frontotemporal dementia","Stroke or structural right-hemisphere lesion","Depressive or manic states with jealous ideation","Non-delusional reactive jealousy in the context of genuine infidelity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured domestic-violence and homicide risk assessment","Collateral history from the partner, with attention to their safety","Alcohol and substance use assessment","Neuroimaging where the presentation is organic or of late onset","Medication review focusing on dopaminergic agents","Cognitive assessment","Organic screen bloods"],"redFlags":["Threats or violence toward the partner or alleged lover","Access to weapons or previous violence","Stalking, checking, and escalating controlling behaviour","Recent separation or the partner's intention to leave, when homicide risk peaks","Intoxication or command hallucinations amplifying impulsivity"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause and, where alcohol-related, on abstinence; the delusion can be persistent and carries a significant risk of serious violence even during treatment.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the substantial risk of intimate-partner homicide","Assuming that geographical separation eliminates risk","Missing alcohol misuse or dopaminergic medication as a driver","Failing to assess and safeguard the partner directly"],"management":["Treat the underlying disorder, whether delusional disorder, schizophrenia, mood disorder, alcohol dependence or organic brain disease","Antipsychotic medication to target the delusional conviction, adding an SSRI where an affective or obsessional component predominates","Reduce or rationalise dopaminergic therapy such as dopamine agonists or levodopa when Parkinson's disease treatment is the precipitant","Structured risk assessment for intimate-partner violence and homicide, prioritising the safety of the partner, who may need to be separated from the patient","Support alcohol abstinence and treat substance misuse, which strongly influences outcome","Psychiatric admission and escalation when violence risk is high or the delusion is refractory","Neuroimaging and cognitive assessment for late-onset or organic presentations, coordinated between psychiatry and neurology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["New Oxford Textbook of Psychiatry","Lishman's Organic Psychiatry","Kaplan and Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry"],"crossLinks":[]},"otitis_externa":{"name":"Otitis externa","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["external ear infection","swimmer's ear","diffuse otitis externa"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Inflammation of the external auditory canal presenting with ear pain, itching, discharge and a feeling of fullness","Pain on moving the tragus or pinna is a hallmark that helps distinguish it from middle ear disease","Moisture, trauma from cotton buds, and a disrupted acidic canal environment are common predisposing factors","The localised furuncular form is a staphylococcal infection of a hair follicle in the outer cartilaginous canal, while the diffuse form is often associated with Pseudomonas","Otomycosis (fungal otitis externa) typically shows itching with visible fungal debris such as blotting-paper or fluffy growth in the canal","Management principles include meticulous aural toileting, topical antimicrobial and anti-inflammatory drops, analgesia and keeping the ear dry","Malignant (necrotising) otitis externa is a serious osteomyelitis of the skull base, seen mainly in elderly diabetics and the immunocompromised, and demands urgent recognition","Recurrent or treatment-resistant disease should prompt review for underlying dermatological conditions, diabetes or immunosuppression"],"pathophysiology":"Otitis externa is inflammation of the skin lining the external auditory canal. Breakdown of the canal's protective barrier, through excess moisture, mechanical trauma, cerumen removal, or altered pH, allows bacterial or fungal overgrowth. The resulting inflammation causes oedema, discharge and narrowing of the canal, producing pain and conductive hearing loss when the lumen is occluded. In malignant otitis externa, infection (typically Pseudomonas) spreads from the soft tissue into the temporal bone and skull base, causing progressive osteomyelitis and potential cranial nerve involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute otitis media with perforation and discharge","Chronic suppurative otitis media","Furuncle of the canal","Otomycosis","Eczematous or seborrhoeic dermatitis of the canal","Malignancy of the external canal"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy or otomicroscopy to inspect the canal and clear debris","Swab of discharge for bacterial and fungal culture in resistant cases","Blood glucose and inflammatory markers when necrotising disease is suspected","CT or MRI of the temporal bone and skull base for suspected malignant otitis externa","Biopsy of granulation tissue where malignancy or necrotising disease is a concern"],"redFlags":["Severe, disproportionate pain with granulation tissue in an elderly diabetic suggesting necrotising otitis externa","Cranial nerve palsies (especially facial nerve) indicating skull base osteomyelitis","Spreading cellulitis, fever or systemic upset","Failure to respond to standard topical therapy"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated otitis externa usually resolves with topical treatment and ear care, but malignant otitis externa is potentially life-threatening and requires prolonged systemic therapy and close monitoring.","pitfalls":["Missing a perforation or middle ear disease as the source of discharge","Overlooking necrotising otitis externa in a diabetic with severe pain","Ineffective treatment because the canal was not adequately cleaned","Failing to identify and treat an underlying fungal or dermatological cause"],"management":["Meticulous aural toilet to clear debris and allow topical therapy to reach the canal skin","Topical antibiotic and corticosteroid ear drops, with an ear wick inserted when the canal is oedematous and occluded","Adequate analgesia with advice to keep the ear dry and avoid cotton buds or further trauma","Incision or drainage of a localised furuncle in the cartilaginous canal where it points","Antifungal drops for otomycosis identified on otoscopy after thorough debris removal","Urgent recognition of malignant (necrotising) otitis externa, with systemic anti-pseudomonal antibiotics, skull-base imaging and specialist ENT referral","Correct predisposing factors such as moisture exposure and address diabetes or immunosuppression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"otitis_media_with_effusion":{"name":"Otitis media with effusion","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OME","glue ear","serous otitis media","secretory otitis media"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by a non-infected fluid collection in the middle ear behind an intact tympanic membrane, without acute signs of infection","A leading cause of acquired conductive hearing loss in children and often presents as inattention, poor school performance or delayed speech","Frequently follows acute otitis media or arises from persistent Eustachian tube dysfunction, often related to adenoid hypertrophy","Otoscopy may reveal a dull, retracted drum, an air-fluid level or bubbles, and reduced mobility","Tympanometry showing a flat (type B) trace and audiometry demonstrating conductive loss support the diagnosis","In adults, unilateral persistent effusion should prompt examination of the postnasal space to exclude a nasopharyngeal tumour","Many cases resolve spontaneously, so an initial period of watchful waiting with reassessment is appropriate","Persistent effusion with significant hearing loss may be managed with ventilation (grommet) tubes, often alongside adenoidectomy"],"pathophysiology":"Otitis media with effusion results from prolonged Eustachian tube dysfunction that impairs middle ear ventilation. Persistent negative pressure encourages transudation of fluid and metaplasia of the middle ear mucosa toward a secretory epithelium, producing a serous or mucoid (glue-like) effusion. The retained fluid dampens ossicular movement and causes a conductive hearing loss. Contributing factors include adenoid hypertrophy, recurrent upper respiratory infections, allergy, cleft palate and, in adults, nasopharyngeal masses obstructing the tube.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute otitis media","Chronic suppurative otitis media","Congenital ossicular or cochlear hearing loss","Tympanosclerosis","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (in unilateral adult cases)","Cholesteatoma with retraction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pneumatic otoscopy to assess drum mobility","Tympanometry to detect middle ear fluid and negative pressure","Pure tone audiometry to quantify conductive hearing loss","Nasopharyngeal examination or endoscopy in persistent unilateral adult effusion","Assessment of adenoids and allergic or sinonasal contributors"],"redFlags":["Unilateral persistent effusion in an adult raising concern for nasopharyngeal carcinoma","Progressive hearing loss affecting speech and language development in a child","Signs of retraction pocket or cholesteatoma developing","Effusion associated with cranial nerve signs or neck mass"],"prognosis":"The majority of childhood cases resolve spontaneously within weeks to months; persistent disease can impair hearing and language development but generally responds well to ventilation tubes and treatment of underlying causes.","pitfalls":["Overprescribing antibiotics for a fundamentally non-infective effusion","Failing to examine the nasopharynx in an adult with unilateral effusion","Intervening surgically too early before allowing spontaneous resolution","Missing associated speech, language or educational impact in children"],"management":["Initial period of active observation, since most childhood effusions resolve spontaneously within weeks to months","Insertion of ventilation tubes (grommets) for persistent bilateral effusion with hearing loss affecting speech, language or schooling","Adjuvant adenoidectomy where adenoid hypertrophy contributes, particularly in recurrent disease","Hearing aids as an alternative for persistent hearing loss when surgery is declined or unsuitable","Treat contributing factors such as allergy, sinonasal disease and recurrent upper respiratory infection","In adults, examine the postnasal space and image to exclude a nasopharyngeal tumour when the effusion is unilateral and persistent","Monitor hearing and speech-and-language development with audiometry and tympanometry"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"otoferlin_deafness":{"name":"Otoferlin-related deafness (DFNB9)","system":"Ear / Audiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DFNB9","OTOF-related hearing loss","otoferlin synaptopathy","temperature-sensitive auditory neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A leading monogenic cause of auditory neuropathy spectrum disorder, inherited in an autosomal recessive manner through biallelic OTOF variants","Typically presents as congenital severe-to-profound hearing loss with preserved otoacoustic emissions and absent auditory brainstem responses","The defect is presynaptic (a cochlear synaptopathy), so the auditory nerve is intact and can be stimulated directly","Cochlear implantation generally produces excellent auditory and speech outcomes","A rare temperature-sensitive variant causes marked hearing deterioration during fever with recovery afterwards","Outer hair cell function may be preserved early, so emissions can be present in infancy and disappear over time","OTOF is a prominent target for emerging inner-ear gene-replacement approaches, making molecular confirmation increasingly relevant"],"pathophysiology":"Otoferlin is a calcium-sensing protein essential for synaptic vesicle exocytosis at the inner hair cell ribbon synapse. When OTOF variants impair its function, the influx of calcium fails to trigger reliable neurotransmitter release onto the auditory nerve terminals, so sound is transduced by hair cells but not faithfully relayed to the nerve. This presynaptic synaptopathy underlies the characteristic pattern of preserved outer hair cell responses with an absent or grossly dyssynchronous brainstem response. Because the spiral ganglion neurones and auditory nerve remain structurally intact, direct electrical stimulation by a cochlear implant bypasses the defective synapse effectively.","additionalDifferentials":["Other genetic and acquired causes of auditory neuropathy spectrum disorder","Neural auditory neuropathy from spiral ganglion or nerve pathology","GJB2/DFNB1 cochlear sensorineural hearing loss","Cochlear nerve deficiency on imaging","Congenital cytomegalovirus-associated hearing loss","Syndromic sensorineural hearing loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoacoustic emissions and cochlear microphonic recording showing preserved outer hair cell activity","Auditory brainstem response demonstrating absence or gross dyssynchrony","Behavioural and speech perception audiometry as age allows","Targeted OTOF sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis within a deafness panel","Cross-sectional imaging to confirm an intact cochlear nerve before implantation","Temperature-dependent threshold assessment when febrile fluctuation is reported"],"redFlags":["Profound functional deficit and language delay despite a deceptively preserved emission profile","Marked hearing deterioration during febrile illness in the temperature-sensitive form","Loss of previously present emissions over time","Missed diagnosis when newborn screening relies on emissions alone"],"prognosis":"Because the lesion is presynaptic with an intact nerve, cochlear implantation typically restores excellent speech perception, and molecular confirmation may open access to gene-based therapies as they mature.","pitfalls":["Interpreting present otoacoustic emissions as normal hearing and delaying intervention","Attributing febrile hearing fluctuation to middle-ear disease rather than the temperature-sensitive phenotype","Trialling prolonged amplification when implantation is more appropriate","Not confirming an intact cochlear nerve on imaging before planning an implant"],"management":["Cochlear implantation as the mainstay, since the presynaptic lesion with an intact auditory nerve responds excellently to direct electrical stimulation","Confirm an intact cochlear nerve on cross-sectional imaging before implantation","Recognise that conventional hearing aids give limited benefit because the defect is neural synaptic rather than of cochlear amplification","Early speech-perception and language support, with consideration of emerging OTOF gene-directed therapies","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance from biallelic OTOF variants, with family testing","Manage the temperature-sensitive variant by protecting hearing during febrile illness with antipyresis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology","GeneReviews: OTOF-Related Deafness"],"crossLinks":[]},"otomycosis":{"name":"Otomycosis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fungal otitis externa","mycotic otitis externa","singapore ear"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Fungal infection of the external auditory canal presenting with intense itching, a feeling of blockage, discharge, and sometimes dull pain and reduced hearing","Aspergillus (often niger) and Candida are the usual organisms, and Aspergillus classically shows blotting-paper or wet newspaper debris with black or fungal spore heads","It is more common in hot humid climates, in swimmers, in diabetics, and after prolonged use of topical antibiotic-steroid ear drops","Diagnosis is largely clinical on otoscopy, supported by microscopy and culture of the debris when the picture is unclear","The mainstay of treatment is thorough and repeated aural toilet to remove fungal debris, followed by topical antifungal agents and keeping the ear dry","Correcting predisposing factors such as excessive moisture, glucose control in diabetics, and stopping unnecessary topical antibiotics is important to prevent recurrence","An intact tympanic membrane should be confirmed, as this influences the choice of topical agent","Recurrence is common if debris is incompletely cleared or predisposing factors persist"],"pathophysiology":"Otomycosis is a superficial fungal infection of the squamous epithelium lining the external auditory canal. A warm, moist, and often macerated canal, frequently after water exposure or suppression of normal flora by topical antibiotics, favours colonisation by saprophytic fungi such as Aspergillus and Candida. The fungi proliferate over the canal skin and cerumen, producing hyphal mats and spores that trigger itching and inflammation. Accumulated debris obstructs the canal and can retain moisture, perpetuating the cycle. In immunocompromised or diabetic patients infection may be more aggressive.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial otitis externa","Chronic suppurative otitis media with discharge","Eczematous or seborrhoeic otitis externa","Keratosis obturans","Necrotising (malignant) otitis externa in a diabetic or immunocompromised patient","Contact dermatitis to ear drops"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy to identify characteristic fungal debris and confirm tympanic membrane integrity","Microscopy and fungal culture of canal debris when diagnosis is uncertain or disease is resistant","Blood glucose or diabetic screening in recurrent or severe cases","Consideration of underlying immunosuppression in aggressive or persistent infection"],"redFlags":["Severe deep pain, granulations at the bony-cartilaginous junction, or cranial nerve signs suggesting necrotising otitis externa in a diabetic or immunocompromised patient","Facial weakness or systemic upset indicating spread beyond the canal","Failure to respond to appropriate treatment raising concern for invasive fungal disease","Discharge through a perforation suggesting middle-ear involvement"],"prognosis":"With thorough aural toilet, topical antifungal treatment, and control of predisposing factors the outlook is excellent, though recurrences are frequent when the ear is not kept dry or debris is inadequately cleared; invasive disease is rare and confined largely to immunocompromised hosts.","pitfalls":["Prescribing topical antifungals without first removing the fungal debris, which limits their effect","Continuing antibiotic-steroid drops that promoted the fungal overgrowth in the first place","Missing an underlying diabetes or immunosuppression in recurrent disease","Failing to recognise early necrotising otitis externa in an at-risk patient"],"management":["Repeated thorough aural toilet under otoscopy or microsuction to remove fungal debris, the mainstay of treatment","Topical antifungal agents such as clotrimazole applied after debris clearance","Keep the ear dry and avoid water exposure to interrupt the moist environment that sustains fungal growth","Stop unnecessary topical antibiotic-corticosteroid drops that suppress normal flora and predispose to fungal overgrowth","Optimise glycaemic control in diabetics and consider underlying immunosuppression in aggressive or persistent disease","Microscopy and culture of debris to guide therapy in resistant cases, with follow-up given the frequent recurrences"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"otopalatodigital_syndrome":{"name":"Oto-palato-digital syndrome (FLNA)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OPD syndrome","otopalatodigital syndrome types 1 and 2","FLNA","Taybi syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Named for its cardinal triad of ear (oto-, conductive deafness), palate (cleft palate) and digital (hand and foot) anomalies, caused by gain-of-function FLNA variants","Hand and foot findings include broad, short thumbs and great toes and a characteristic gap between digits, giving a distinctive appearance","Craniofacial features include a prominent brow, hypertelorism, a flat midface and a small jaw","Conductive hearing loss arises from ossicular abnormalities and is an early and consistent feature","As an X-linked disorder, males are more severely affected while carrier females show milder, variable features; the more severe type 2 in males can be perinatally lethal","It sits within the FLNA otopalatodigital spectrum alongside frontometaphyseal dysplasia and Melnick-Needles syndrome, sharing overlapping skeletal and extraskeletal features","Management is supportive, addressing cleft palate surgically, providing hearing support and offering orthopaedic care, with genetic counselling for the family"],"pathophysiology":"Oto-palato-digital syndrome is caused by gain-of-function variants in the X-linked gene FLNA, encoding filamin A, an actin-cross-linking protein essential for cytoskeletal organisation and cell signalling during skeletal and craniofacial development. Disturbed filamin A function alters the modelling of the skull, palate, middle-ear ossicles and the tubular bones of the hands and feet, producing the characteristic ear, palate and digital anomalies. The severity gradient reflects X-linked inheritance: hemizygous males manifest fully and, in the more severe type 2, may have life-threatening malformations, whereas heterozygous females are variably protected by X-inactivation. The condition forms one end of the FLNA otopalatodigital spectrum, explaining its clinical overlap with related disorders.","additionalDifferentials":["Frontometaphyseal dysplasia (FLNA spectrum)","Melnick-Needles syndrome (FLNA spectrum)","Larsen syndrome","Craniofacial dysostoses with cleft palate","Other causes of syndromic conductive hearing loss with digital anomalies","Pierre Robin sequence as part of a broader syndromic cleft"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey documenting the digital and craniofacial anomalies","Audiometry and middle-ear assessment for conductive hearing loss","Palate examination and imaging to characterise the cleft","Skull imaging where cranial-nerve or foraminal concerns arise","FLNA molecular genetic testing with X-linked segregation analysis","Evaluation for extraskeletal (airway, urogenital, cardiac) anomalies in severe or type 2 disease"],"redFlags":["Airway or feeding compromise from cleft palate and craniofacial anomalies in infancy","Severe multisystem malformations in males suggesting the perinatally lethal type 2 phenotype","Progressive hearing loss requiring intervention","Cranial-nerve deficits indicating foraminal narrowing"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies with type and sex: milder disease is compatible with normal life expectancy and intellect, whereas the severe type 2 phenotype in affected males may be lethal in the perinatal period; hearing loss and skeletal issues cause ongoing morbidity in survivors.","pitfalls":["Not recognising the condition as part of the FLNA otopalatodigital spectrum and its X-linked inheritance","Overlooking early conductive hearing loss and delaying support","Failing to anticipate the severe type 2 phenotype and its perinatal risks in males","Missing associated extraskeletal anomalies in more severely affected individuals"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so management is directed at the ear, palate and skeletal manifestations","Audiological assessment and hearing rehabilitation with amplification or ossicular surgery for conductive loss from ossicular anomalies","Surgical repair of cleft palate with speech-and-language therapy follow-up","Orthopaedic and physiotherapy management of the hand, foot and skeletal deformities","Evaluate airway, urogenital and cardiac anomalies in severely affected males and type 2 disease, with neonatal intensive support where needed","FLNA molecular confirmation and clinical genetics referral with X-linked counselling, recognising the risk of perinatal lethality in males"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders","GeneReviews: FLNA-related otopalatodigital spectrum disorders"],"crossLinks":["frontometaphyseal_dysplasia","melnick_needles_syndrome"]},"otosclerosis":{"name":"Otosclerosis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["otospongiosis","stapedial otosclerosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A localised disease of the bony labyrinth in which normal bone is replaced by spongy, vascular new bone that most often fixes the stapes footplate","Typically presents in young to middle-aged adults with slowly progressive bilateral conductive hearing loss and a family history","Often worsens during pregnancy, suggesting a hormonal influence, and is more common in women","Patients frequently hear better in noisy surroundings (paracusis Willisii) and may speak softly","The tympanic membrane is characteristically normal; a pink flush over the promontory (Schwartze sign) reflects active vascular disease","Tuning fork tests show a negative Rinne and audiometry reveals conductive loss, often with the characteristic Carhart notch dip in bone conduction around 2 kHz","Cochlear otosclerosis can add a sensorineural component leading to mixed hearing loss","Management options include a hearing aid, or stapes surgery (stapedectomy/stapedotomy with a prosthesis) to restore ossicular movement; sodium fluoride may be used to stabilise active disease"],"pathophysiology":"Otosclerosis is a primary disorder of endochondral bone of the otic capsule in which foci of normal lamellar bone are resorbed and replaced by irregular, highly cellular and vascular spongy bone that later matures into dense sclerotic bone. The commonest site is the region of the fissula ante fenestram just anterior to the oval window; as the focus enlarges it invades and fixes the stapes footplate, producing progressive conductive hearing loss. Extension of foci into the cochlear endosteum can damage the neural elements and cause sensorineural loss. The condition is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with incomplete penetrance, and hormonal factors appear to modulate its activity.","additionalDifferentials":["Ossicular fixation or discontinuity from other causes","Congenital stapes fixation","Adhesive otitis media or tympanosclerosis","Otitis media with effusion","Van der Hoeve syndrome (otosclerosis with osteogenesis imperfecta)","Paget disease of the temporal bone","Superior semicircular canal dehiscence mimicking conductive loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure tone audiometry to document conductive or mixed loss and the Carhart notch","Tuning fork tests to confirm conductive hearing loss","Tympanometry, typically showing normal to reduced compliance, with absent stapedial reflexes","Speech audiometry to assess discrimination before surgery","High-resolution CT of the temporal bone to map active foci and exclude other pathology in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or asymmetrical sensorineural loss prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Vertigo or disequilibrium suggesting labyrinthine or alternative pathology","Sudden deterioration or facial nerve symptoms after stapes surgery requiring urgent review","Unilateral pulsatile tinnitus or other features that raise concern for a vascular or retrocochlear lesion"],"prognosis":"The disease is progressive but stapes surgery closes the conductive gap and restores useful hearing in most suitably selected patients, while hearing aids remain an effective non-surgical option; established cochlear involvement, however, is not reversible.","pitfalls":["Overlooking otosclerosis when the drum looks normal and attributing the loss to wax or effusion","Failing to counsel about the small risk of dead ear and sensorineural loss with stapes surgery","Missing a coexisting sensorineural (cochlear) component that limits surgical benefit","Not recognising the Carhart notch as a bone conduction artefact rather than true cochlear loss"],"management":["Stapes surgery, stapedotomy or stapedectomy, to close the conductive gap by bypassing the fixed stapes footplate in suitably selected patients","Hearing aids as an effective non-surgical alternative for those unfit for or declining surgery","Pure-tone and speech audiometry to select surgical candidates and document the conductive or mixed loss","Cochlear implantation for advanced cochlear (retrofenestral) otosclerosis with profound sensorineural loss","Consider sodium fluoride or a bisphosphonate to stabilise active otospongiotic foci in selected cochlear disease","Counsel that established sensorineural cochlear involvement is not reversible by surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"otosclerosis_familial":{"name":"Familial otosclerosis","system":"Ear / Audiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hereditary otosclerosis","otospongiosis","familial stapes fixation","cochlear otosclerosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common cause of acquired conductive hearing loss in adults, often clustering in families with an autosomal dominant pattern and reduced penetrance","Progressive conductive loss results from abnormal bone remodelling fixing the stapes footplate at the oval window","Typical audiometric features include a low-frequency conductive loss and the Carhart notch, an apparent dip in bone conduction around 2 kHz that resolves after successful surgery","Cochlear (retrofenestral) otosclerosis can add a sensorineural component producing a mixed loss","Tympanometry usually shows a normal or shallow trace with absent acoustic reflexes rather than the effusion pattern of middle-ear disease","Options include hearing aids or stapes surgery (stapedotomy), with cochlear implantation reserved for advanced cochlear otosclerosis","Onset or progression is often noted during pregnancy, and there is a female predominance"],"pathophysiology":"Otosclerosis is a disorder of the otic capsule bone in which normal lamellar bone is replaced by disorganised, initially vascular spongy bone (otospongiosis) that later scleroses. When this remodelling involves the region of the oval window it progressively fixes the stapes footplate, impairing ossicular movement and producing conductive hearing loss; the Carhart notch reflects the mechanical effect of fixation on bone-conduction measurement rather than a true cochlear deficit. When the process extends into the cochlear endosteum (cochlear otosclerosis) it can damage the inner ear and add a sensorineural component. A familial predisposition with autosomal dominant inheritance and incomplete penetrance is well recognised, and hormonal factors are thought to influence progression.","additionalDifferentials":["Enlarged vestibular aqueduct and other third-window lesions causing a pseudoconductive gap","Superior semicircular canal dehiscence","Ossicular chain fixation or discontinuity","Chronic otitis media and middle-ear effusion","X-linked deafness with stapes gusher (POU3F4)","Paget disease and other otic capsule dysplasias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure-tone audiometry demonstrating conductive or mixed loss with a Carhart notch","Tympanometry and acoustic reflex testing to distinguish fixation from effusion","Speech audiometry to guide surgical versus amplification decisions","High-resolution temporal bone imaging to confirm otospongiotic foci and exclude third-window lesions","Family history and pedigree assessment","Cochlear function assessment where a mixed or sensorineural component is present"],"redFlags":["A conductive gap with a normal tympanic membrane and normal tympanogram, which should prompt exclusion of a third-window lesion before surgery","Rapidly progressive sensorineural loss suggesting cochlear otosclerosis","Vertigo or a large air-bone gap pattern atypical for stapes fixation","Sudden hearing change after surgery indicating a complication"],"prognosis":"Progression is gradual, and stapes surgery closes the air-bone gap in the majority with durable benefit, while advanced cochlear otosclerosis may still be rehabilitated with cochlear implantation.","pitfalls":["Operating on a presumed stapes fixation when the gap is actually from a third-window lesion such as canal dehiscence","Overlooking a coexisting sensorineural component from cochlear otosclerosis","Misreading absent reflexes and a shallow tympanogram as middle-ear effusion","Failing to counsel about the small risk of profound sensorineural loss after stapes surgery"],"management":["Stapedotomy to close the air-bone gap by restoring sound transmission past the fixed stapes footplate, with durable benefit in the majority","Hearing aids as an effective alternative for patients declining or unsuitable for surgery","Cochlear implantation reserved for advanced cochlear (retrofenestral) otosclerosis with a severe sensorineural component","Pure-tone and speech audiometry to guide the choice between surgery and amplification","Family history and pedigree assessment given the autosomal dominant inheritance with reduced penetrance","Monitor progression, including any developing sensorineural component, and counsel on the influence of hormonal factors such as pregnancy on disease activity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology"],"crossLinks":[]},"otulipenia":{"name":"Otulipenia (OTULIN deficiency)","system":"Immunology / Autoinflammatory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OTULIN-related autoinflammatory syndrome","ORAS","OTULIN deficiency","OTULIN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Autoinflammation / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A recessive autoinflammatory disease caused by loss-of-function mutations in OTULIN, a deubiquitinating enzyme regulating linear ubiquitin chains","Presents in early infancy with recurrent fevers, painful nodular panniculitis, and failure to thrive","Neutrophilic panniculitis produces tender subcutaneous nodules and erythematous skin lesions","Additional features include diarrhoea, joint swelling, and marked systemic inflammation","Loss of OTULIN function dysregulates nuclear factor kappa B signalling, producing a pro-inflammatory state involving tumour necrosis factor","Anti-tumour necrosis factor therapy can be strikingly effective, and interleukin-1 blockade is also used","Early diagnosis and cytokine-directed treatment are important given the severity of infantile systemic inflammation"],"pathophysiology":"Otulipenia results from biallelic loss-of-function mutations in OTULIN, an enzyme that removes linear (methionine-1 linked) ubiquitin chains and thereby restrains the linear ubiquitin chain assembly complex and downstream nuclear factor kappa B signalling. Without functional OTULIN, linear ubiquitination accumulates and inflammatory signalling is inappropriately amplified, with increased production of tumour necrosis factor and other pro-inflammatory cytokines. This unrestrained signalling drives neutrophilic panniculitis, recurrent fevers, and multi-organ inflammation in infancy. The central role of tumour necrosis factor explains the marked response to anti-tumour necrosis factor therapy.","additionalDifferentials":["Other panniculitis-associated autoinflammatory diseases such as CANDLE","Deficiency of adenosine deaminase 2 (DADA2)","Neonatal-onset multisystem inflammatory disease (NOMID/CINCA)","Infective panniculitis or cellulitis","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Erythema nodosum from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for OTULIN mutations","Skin biopsy demonstrating neutrophilic panniculitis","Acute-phase reactants, which are markedly raised","Full blood count and inflammatory markers to monitor systemic activity","Cultures to exclude infective panniculitis","Assessment of growth and gastrointestinal symptoms"],"redFlags":["Severe systemic inflammation with haemodynamic instability in infancy","Profound failure to thrive despite treatment","Features suggesting evolving macrophage activation or multi-organ dysfunction","Extensive skin and soft-tissue inflammation with secondary infection"],"prognosis":"Otulipenia is a severe infantile-onset disease, but recognition and cytokine-directed therapy, particularly tumour necrosis factor blockade, can induce marked improvement and allow catch-up growth.","pitfalls":["Mistaking neutrophilic panniculitis for infective cellulitis and delaying immunomodulation","Not distinguishing otulipenia from other panniculitis-associated autoinflammatory diseases","Overlooking the strong response to anti-tumour necrosis factor therapy","Delaying genetic testing in an infant with recurrent fevers and panniculitis"],"management":["Anti-tumour necrosis factor biologic therapy as the mainstay, targeting the dysregulated nuclear factor kappa B-driven cytokine excess and often strikingly effective","Interleukin-1 blockade as an alternative or adjunctive cytokine-directed treatment","Corticosteroids to control acute severe systemic inflammation and panniculitic flares","Nutritional support with monitoring of growth to enable catch-up growth once inflammation is controlled","Serial acute-phase reactants and full blood count to monitor disease activity and treatment response","Exclude infective panniculitis with cultures before and during immunosuppression, with vigilance for infection on biologic therapy","Clinical genetics referral for autosomal recessive OTULIN counselling and family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Autoinflammation","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"out_toeing":{"name":"Out-toeing gait","system":"Paediatric orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["outtoeing","toeing-out","duck feet","lateral foot progression"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Out-toeing is less common than in-toeing and, like it, is usually a benign rotational variant","In early infancy a bilateral out-toeing is often due to an external rotation contracture of the hip from intrauterine positioning, which resolves once the child bears weight and walks","In older children external tibial torsion and femoral retroversion are the usual causes, and external tibial torsion tends to increase rather than diminish with growth","The level of the deformity is defined by a rotational profile using the foot progression angle, hip rotation, and the thigh-foot angle","External tibial torsion may combine with a valgus knee to aggravate patellofemoral maltracking and anterior knee pain in adolescence","New or unilateral out-toeing in an older or overweight child should raise concern for a slipped capital femoral epiphysis, in which the limb externally rotates","Most physiological out-toeing needs only reassurance and observation, with surgery reserved for severe, symptomatic, persistent deformity"],"pathophysiology":"Out-toeing describes an outwardly directed foot progression angle during walking, produced by external rotational alignment at the hip, tibia, or foot. In the infant it commonly reflects a residual external rotation contracture of the hip capsule from intrauterine position, which stretches out with ambulation. In the older child it usually arises from external tibial torsion or reduced femoral anteversion (relative retroversion), representing variation in the normal course of skeletal derotation. Unlike internal torsion, external tibial torsion often progresses with growth. Pathological out-toeing is important to recognise because hip disorders such as a slipped capital femoral epiphysis characteristically hold the limb in external rotation.","additionalDifferentials":["External tibial torsion","Femoral retroversion or reduced femoral anteversion","External rotation contracture of the hip in infancy","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis in the older child","Calcaneovalgus or planovalgus foot deformity","Neuromuscular gait disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full rotational profile with foot progression angle, hip rotation, and thigh-foot angle","Observation of gait for symmetry and pattern","Examination of the hips for restricted internal rotation suggesting a slipped epiphysis","Hip radiographs including a frog-lateral view when a slipped capital femoral epiphysis is suspected","Assessment of the feet for planovalgus or calcaneovalgus deformity","Standing lower-limb radiographs where associated angular deformity is present"],"redFlags":["New or unilateral out-toeing in an older or overweight child suggesting a slipped capital femoral epiphysis","Hip or knee pain, limp, or restricted hip internal rotation","Progressive external tibial torsion with worsening patellofemoral symptoms","Marked asymmetry between the limbs","Neurological signs indicating an underlying neuromuscular disorder"],"prognosis":"Physiological out-toeing generally carries a good prognosis, with infantile hip contractures resolving after walking begins; external tibial torsion is less predictable and may persist or progress, occasionally warranting correction for symptoms.","pitfalls":["Dismissing unilateral out-toeing in an adolescent and missing a slipped capital femoral epiphysis","Assuming out-toeing always resolves, when external tibial torsion can worsen with growth","Failing to localise the deformity with a rotational profile","Overtreating benign infantile out-toeing with unnecessary orthoses","Overlooking associated patellofemoral maltracking that may need attention"],"management":["Reassure and observe physiological out-toeing, as infantile hip external-rotation contractures resolve once weight-bearing and walking begin","Serial monitoring of the rotational profile, including foot progression angle, hip rotation and thigh-foot angle, to track the natural course","Reserve derotational tibial osteotomy for severe, persistent external tibial torsion causing symptoms or patellofemoral maltracking","Conservative management of associated planovalgus foot deformity and anterior knee pain","Urgently exclude and manage a slipped capital femoral epiphysis in a new or unilateral out-toeing older or overweight child, with frog-lateral hip radiographs and prompt orthopaedic referral","Refer to paediatric orthopaedics when the deformity is unilateral, progressive, painful or outside the expected developmental pattern"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Staheli LT, rotational problems of the lower extremity"],"crossLinks":[]},"ovarian_cancer":{"name":"Ovarian Cancer","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["carcinoma of the ovary","ovarian carcinoma","epithelial ovarian cancer","ovarian malignancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most cases are epithelial in origin, and high-grade serous carcinoma, now thought largely to arise from the distal fallopian tube, is the commonest and most aggressive subtype","Symptoms are frequently vague and persistent, including abdominal bloating, early satiety, pelvic or abdominal discomfort, and urinary urgency, so a high index of suspicion is needed particularly in women over fifty","Because there is no reliable early symptom or screening test, the majority present with advanced intra-abdominal disease","Inherited mutations in BRCA1 and BRCA2 and Lynch syndrome markedly increase lifetime risk and should prompt genetic assessment","A pelvic mass combined with ascites and a raised CA-125 in a postmenopausal woman is highly suggestive and warrants specialist referral","Management centres on maximal cytoreductive surgery to remove all visible tumour combined with platinum-based chemotherapy","Risk assessment tools that combine ultrasound features, menopausal status, and CA-125 help triage women to gynaecological oncology","Germ cell and sex cord-stromal tumours are important in younger women and can produce hormonal effects or tumour markers such as AFP, hCG, or inhibin"],"pathophysiology":"High-grade serous ovarian cancer is now believed to originate frequently from serous tubal intraepithelial carcinoma in the fimbrial end of the fallopian tube, with early TP53 mutation and genomic instability, often in the context of homologous recombination deficiency from BRCA pathway defects. Malignant cells exfoliate and disseminate across the peritoneal cavity, seeding the omentum, bowel serosa, and diaphragm, producing ascites through increased vascular permeability and lymphatic obstruction. Other histological types arise from ovarian surface epithelium, germ cells, or sex cord-stromal cells with distinct biology.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign ovarian cyst or endometrioma","Functional ovarian cyst","Uterine fibroids or other pelvic mass","Metastatic gastrointestinal cancer to the ovary (Krukenberg tumour)","Tubo-ovarian abscess","Primary peritoneal or fallopian tube carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal and transabdominal pelvic ultrasound to characterise the mass","Serum CA-125 and, in younger women, germ cell markers (AFP, hCG, LDH) and inhibin","CT of the abdomen and pelvis to assess disease extent and operability","Risk-of-malignancy scoring combining ultrasound, menopausal status and tumour markers","Histological confirmation from surgical specimen or image-guided biopsy where surgery is not upfront","Genetic testing for BRCA and mismatch repair mutations"],"redFlags":["Rapidly accumulating ascites with breathlessness or abdominal distension","Bowel obstruction from peritoneal or omental disease","Marked weight loss and cachexia suggesting advanced malignancy","Pleural effusion indicating spread beyond the abdomen","A complex solid-cystic pelvic mass with markedly elevated CA-125 in a postmenopausal woman"],"prognosis":"Overall survival is dominated by stage at presentation and by the completeness of surgical cytoreduction; disease confined to the ovary is potentially curable, whereas widespread peritoneal disease carries a poorer outlook despite platinum sensitivity. Tumour grade, histological subtype, and BRCA/homologous recombination status influence response to chemotherapy and to maintenance agents such as PARP inhibitors, and relapse is common in advanced disease.","pitfalls":["Dismissing persistent bloating and abdominal symptoms in older women as functional bowel disturbance","Assuming a normal CA-125 excludes malignancy, as it can be normal in early or non-serous tumours","Rupturing or morcellating an undiagnosed malignant cyst during surgery","Failing to refer suspicious masses to a gynaecological oncology centre before intervention","Overlooking germ cell tumours in young women by not checking age-appropriate tumour markers"],"management":["Maximal cytoreductive surgery aiming to remove all visible tumour, performed either upfront or as interval debulking after neoadjuvant chemotherapy","Platinum-based chemotherapy, typically carboplatin with paclitaxel, as the backbone of systemic treatment","PARP-inhibitor maintenance for BRCA-mutated or homologous-recombination-deficient disease, with anti-angiogenic bevacizumab in selected cases","Serial CA-125 alongside cross-sectional imaging to monitor treatment response and detect relapse","Genetic testing for BRCA and mismatch-repair mutations, with cascade family testing and risk-reducing salpingo-oophorectomy for carriers","Manage symptomatic ascites with paracentesis and provide early palliative and supportive care in advanced disease","Coordinate care through a gynaecological-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"ovarian_dermoid_cyst":{"name":"Ovarian dermoid cyst (mature cystic teratoma)","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mature cystic teratoma","dermoid cyst","benign cystic teratoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology reference textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest benign ovarian germ cell tumour of the reproductive years, containing mature tissues such as sebaceous material, hair, and sometimes teeth","A localised solid prominence in the wall, the Rokitansky protuberance or dermoid plug, is a characteristic feature and often the site of hair and teeth","Ultrasound features are distinctive, including a densely echogenic focus with acoustic shadowing, the tip-of-the-iceberg sign, and echogenic linear dermoid mesh","Torsion is an important complication, particularly with larger cysts, and can present as acute severe pelvic pain","Rupture releases sebaceous contents that can provoke a chemical peritonitis, so care is taken to avoid intra-operative spillage","Around a tenth are bilateral, so the contralateral ovary should be assessed at imaging and surgery","Fertility-sparing cystectomy is usually preferred in younger women, with rare malignant transformation, most often to squamous cell carcinoma, in a small minority"],"pathophysiology":"A mature cystic teratoma arises from a germ cell that undergoes parthenogenetic development and differentiates into mature tissues derived from all three germ layers, with ectodermal elements predominating. The cyst therefore comes to contain skin appendages, sebaceous secretions, hair, and occasionally teeth or cartilage. Growth is slow, and the tumour is benign in the great majority of cases; rare malignant change occurs when one of its differentiated tissues, most often squamous epithelium, transforms.","additionalDifferentials":["Haemorrhagic ovarian cyst","Endometrioma","Other ovarian germ cell tumours, including immature teratoma","Epithelial ovarian neoplasm","Appendiceal or bowel pathology","Ovarian torsion of another cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound identifying characteristic echogenic and shadowing features","Magnetic resonance imaging with fat-suppression sequences to confirm macroscopic fat","Computed tomography, which readily demonstrates fat, calcification, or teeth","Tumour markers where the appearance is atypical or malignancy is suspected","Assessment of the contralateral ovary given the possibility of bilateral disease"],"redFlags":["Ovarian torsion causing acute severe pain","Rupture with chemical peritonitis","Features suggesting malignant transformation, such as solid vascular areas or rapid growth","New symptoms or markers in an older woman raising concern for malignancy"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent, as these are benign tumours with a low recurrence rate after complete excision; the small risk of malignant transformation rises with age and larger size.","pitfalls":["Intra-operative spillage of cyst contents provoking chemical peritonitis","Overlooking a contralateral dermoid because bilateral disease is not uncommon","Delaying diagnosis of torsion in a woman with a known dermoid","Missing the uncommon malignant transformation, particularly in older women"],"management":["Ovarian-sparing cystectomy as the preferred treatment, preserving fertility where possible","Careful, usually laparoscopic, surgery retrieving the cyst in a bag to avoid intra-operative spillage of sebaceous contents that can cause chemical peritonitis","Emergency surgical detorsion or cystectomy for ovarian torsion presenting as acute severe pelvic pain","Inspect and, if involved, treat the contralateral ovary given that around a tenth are bilateral","Consider expectant surveillance with ultrasound for small, asymptomatic incidental cysts","Histological assessment of the excised cyst to exclude the rare malignant transformation, particularly in older women with larger cysts"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference textbook","Gynaecological oncology reference text"],"crossLinks":[]},"ovarian_fibroma":{"name":"Ovarian fibroma","system":"Gynaecology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fibroma of the ovary","ovarian fibrothecoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest benign solid ovarian tumour, arising from ovarian stromal fibroblasts and typically hormonally inactive","Usually presents in middle-aged or older women as an incidental solid adnexal mass or with non-specific pelvic discomfort","Meigs syndrome is the classic association: a benign ovarian fibroma accompanied by ascites and a pleural effusion that both resolve after removal of the tumour","On imaging it appears as a solid, hypoechoic mass with acoustic shadowing on ultrasound and low signal on T2-weighted MRI, reflecting its dense fibrous tissue","When a fibromatous stroma contains a thecal component it is termed a fibrothecoma, which may then be mildly oestrogenic","Multiple or bilateral fibromas in a young patient should raise suspicion of the naevoid basal cell carcinoma (Gorlin) syndrome","Cellular fibromas and the borderline mitotically active variant can occasionally recur, so complete excision and histological review are important"],"pathophysiology":"Ovarian fibromas are benign sex cord-stromal tumours composed of spindle-shaped fibroblasts producing collagen, giving a firm, white, whorled cut surface. They are generally non-functional because they lack the hormone-secreting theca or granulosa elements, although admixture with thecal cells (fibrothecoma) can confer mild oestrogenic activity. In Meigs syndrome, transudation of fluid from the surface of a large fibroma produces ascites, and fluid tracks to the pleural space, most often on the right, generating an effusion; both resolve once the tumour is excised, distinguishing this benign phenomenon from malignant ascites. Rare cellular and mitotically active variants sit at the borderline of behaviour and warrant careful pathological assessment.","additionalDifferentials":["Pedunculated or subserosal uterine leiomyoma","Brenner tumour","Thecoma","Granulosa cell tumour","Ovarian carcinoma (solid mass with ascites)","Metastatic ovarian tumour such as Krukenberg"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound showing a solid, shadowing ovarian mass","Pelvic MRI to characterise a fibrous lesion with low T2 signal","Chest imaging if a pleural effusion suggests Meigs syndrome","CA125 (which may be mildly raised in Meigs syndrome despite benign disease) and other markers to help exclude malignancy","Histology to confirm the diagnosis and identify cellular or mitotically active variants","Consideration of genetic assessment for Gorlin syndrome in young or multifocal cases"],"redFlags":["A solid ovarian mass with ascites, which requires distinction of benign Meigs syndrome from ovarian carcinoma","Rapid growth or solid vascular components suggesting a more aggressive lesion","Bilateral or multiple fibromas in a young patient, prompting evaluation for Gorlin syndrome","Acute pain from torsion of a pedunculated mass"],"prognosis":"Excellent, as fibromas are benign and cured by excision, with ascites and effusion resolving after removal in Meigs syndrome; only rare cellular or mitotically active variants carry a small risk of recurrence.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting a fibroma with ascites and a raised CA125 as ovarian cancer and overtreating","Confusing a solid ovarian fibroma with a pedunculated uterine fibroid on imaging","Overlooking Gorlin syndrome when fibromas are bilateral or occur in the young","Failing to recognise cellular or mitotically active variants that may recur"],"management":["Surgical excision, by ovary-preserving tumourectomy in younger women or oophorectomy in older women, which is curative","In Meigs syndrome the accompanying ascites and pleural effusion resolve after removal of the fibroma, so treat by excision rather than as a malignant effusion","Characterise the lesion with ultrasound and T2-weighted MRI and use tumour markers to help exclude ovarian malignancy before surgery","Drain a large symptomatic pleural effusion for respiratory relief while arranging definitive tumour removal","Histological examination to confirm a benign fibroma and identify rare cellular or mitotically active variants that warrant follow-up","Screen for naevoid basal cell carcinoma (Gorlin) syndrome and offer genetic assessment in young patients or those with multiple or bilateral fibromas"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","Blaustein's Pathology of the Female Genital Tract"],"crossLinks":[]},"ovarian_hyperstimulation":{"name":"Ovarian Hyperstimulation Syndrome","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome","OHSS","ovarian hyperstimulation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome is an iatrogenic complication of controlled ovarian stimulation for assisted reproduction, characterised by enlarged ovaries and a fluid shift out of the intravascular space","It is classified by severity from mild (abdominal distension and discomfort) through moderate (ascites on imaging, nausea, vomiting) to severe and critical (tense ascites, haemoconcentration, oliguria, thromboembolism, and organ dysfunction)","Early OHSS occurs within days of the ovulation trigger and relates to the trigger itself, whereas late OHSS occurs later and is driven by endogenous hCG from an established pregnancy, tending to be more severe","Key risk factors are young age, low body weight, polycystic ovary syndrome, high antral follicle count or anti-Mullerian hormone, and a strong ovarian response with many follicles and high oestradiol","Prevention strategies include individualised stimulation, use of a GnRH antagonist protocol with a GnRH agonist trigger, freezing all embryos to avoid pregnancy-related hCG, and cabergoline","Management of significant disease is largely supportive: symptom control, careful fluid balance, and thromboprophylaxis because of the prothrombotic haemoconcentrated state","Tense, symptomatic ascites can be relieved by paracentesis, which improves comfort, respiratory function, and urine output","Because of the venous thromboembolism risk, thromboprophylaxis and a low threshold for imaging unusual or persistent symptoms are important, including for atypical thrombosis","Pelvic pain with an enlarged ovary should also raise suspicion of ovarian torsion, a surgical emergency that can complicate OHSS"],"pathophysiology":"Exogenous gonadotrophin stimulation, followed by an hCG or agonist trigger, produces multiple corpora lutea that secrete large amounts of vasoactive mediators, principally vascular endothelial growth factor. hCG amplifies this response, which is why an established pregnancy prolongs and worsens the syndrome. VEGF-driven increases in vascular permeability allow protein-rich fluid to shift from the intravascular compartment into the peritoneal, and sometimes pleural, spaces. This produces ascites, intravascular volume depletion, haemoconcentration, and a hypercoagulable state, which in severe cases lead to oliguria, electrolyte disturbance, thromboembolism, and multi-organ compromise.","additionalDifferentials":["Ovarian torsion","Ruptured ovarian cyst with haemoperitoneum","Ectopic pregnancy","Intra-abdominal or pelvic infection","Appendicitis or other acute abdomen","Venous thromboembolism from other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and haematocrit to assess haemoconcentration","Serum electrolytes, renal and liver function tests","Coagulation profile given the prothrombotic state","Pelvic and abdominal ultrasound to document ovarian size and ascites","Serum or urine hCG to confirm pregnancy contributing to late OHSS","Chest imaging or echocardiography where pleural effusion or respiratory compromise is suspected"],"redFlags":["Tense ascites with breathlessness or respiratory compromise","Features of venous thromboembolism, including atypical sites such as upper limb or cerebral veins","Haemoconcentration with oliguria, hypotension, or evidence of renal impairment","Severe abdominal pain suggesting ovarian torsion or cyst rupture","Rapid clinical deterioration or signs of organ dysfunction requiring hospital admission"],"prognosis":"Most cases are mild and self-limiting, resolving as hCG levels fall, typically within one to two weeks unless pregnancy sustains the process. Severe and critical OHSS, though uncommon, can be life-threatening from thromboembolism, renal failure, or respiratory compromise, but with prompt supportive care and thromboprophylaxis outcomes are generally good. Preventive strategies have markedly reduced the incidence of severe disease.","pitfalls":["Aggressive intravenous crystalloid loading that worsens third-space fluid shift rather than judicious fluid management","Omitting thromboprophylaxis and missing atypical-site thrombosis","Failing to consider ovarian torsion in a woman with an enlarged ovary and acute pain","Not recognising that pregnancy-driven late OHSS may be more severe and prolonged","Overlooking the option of freezing all embryos or agonist trigger to prevent the syndrome in high responders"],"management":["Withhold further gonadotrophin and hCG exposure and, in at-risk cycles, pre-empt progression with a GnRH-agonist trigger, a freeze-all embryo strategy, and a dopamine agonist (cabergoline) to blunt VEGF-mediated capillary leak","Provide symptomatic supportive care with analgesia and antiemetics and judicious fluid replacement titrated to urine output, avoiding NSAIDs that impair renal perfusion","Give low-molecular-weight heparin thromboprophylaxis because haemoconcentration and the hypercoagulable state carry a high venous thromboembolism risk","Drain tense ascites or a symptomatic pleural effusion by ultrasound-guided paracentesis or thoracocentesis to relieve dyspnoea and restore renal perfusion","Monitor haematocrit, renal function, electrolytes and coagulation alongside serial pelvic ultrasound for ovarian size and fluid accumulation, watching for oliguria, thromboembolism and organ dysfunction","Admit severe or critical disease for inpatient or high-dependency care under reproductive medicine, escalating for worsening haemoconcentration, respiratory compromise or suspected thrombosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"ovarian_hyperstimulation_syndrome":{"name":"Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome","system":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OHSS","ovarian hyperstimulation"],"chapter":null,"source":"RCOG guidance on ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome / reproductive medicine texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["OHSS is an iatrogenic complication of ovarian stimulation, usually triggered by hCG exposure for oocyte maturation or by an early pregnancy","The central mechanism is increased vascular permeability with fluid shifting from the intravascular space into third spaces such as the peritoneal and pleural cavities","Risk is higher in young women, those with polycystic ovaries, high antral follicle counts, and a strong response with high oestradiol","Early OHSS presents within days of the trigger, while late OHSS occurring after implantation tends to be more severe and prolonged","Clinical features range from bloating and abdominal discomfort to tense ascites, breathlessness, oliguria, and haemoconcentration","Major hazards are venous thromboembolism from haemoconcentration and hypercoagulability, and hypovolaemia with renal impairment","Management is largely supportive with fluid balance, symptom relief, thromboprophylaxis, and paracentesis for tense ascites in severe cases","Prevention through tailored stimulation, agonist triggers, and elective embryo freezing has reduced severe disease"],"pathophysiology":"Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome arises when multiple stimulated follicles, under the influence of hCG, release vasoactive mediators, notably vascular endothelial growth factor, that increase capillary permeability. Protein-rich fluid leaks from the circulation into the peritoneal, pleural, and occasionally pericardial spaces, producing ascites, effusions, and intravascular volume depletion. The resulting haemoconcentration and stasis, compounded by the hypercoagulable state of stimulation and early pregnancy, predispose to thromboembolism, while reduced renal perfusion causes oliguria and electrolyte disturbance. Ongoing hCG stimulation, including from an implanting pregnancy, sustains and worsens the process.","additionalDifferentials":["Ovarian torsion","Ruptured or haemorrhagic ovarian cyst","Ectopic pregnancy","Intra-abdominal sepsis or pelvic infection","Appendicitis or other acute abdomen","Ascites from other medical causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with haematocrit to assess haemoconcentration","Renal function and electrolytes","Liver function and serum albumin","Coagulation screen and assessment of thrombosis risk","Pelvic ultrasound to measure ovarian size and detect ascites","Chest imaging or echocardiography if effusions or breathlessness are present"],"redFlags":["Severe breathlessness or hypoxia from large effusions or thromboembolism","Signs of venous or arterial thrombosis, including limb swelling or neurological deficit","Oliguria, rising creatinine, or marked haemoconcentration","Tense ascites with haemodynamic compromise or acute abdomen suggesting torsion"],"prognosis":"Most cases are mild to moderate and resolve spontaneously, particularly when not compounded by pregnancy. Severe and critical OHSS can be life-threatening through thromboembolism, renal failure, and respiratory compromise, but outcomes are generally good with timely supportive care and thromboprophylaxis.","pitfalls":["Omitting thromboprophylaxis despite marked haemoconcentration and hypercoagulability","Aggressive crystalloid loading without addressing the underlying capillary leak","Missing ovarian torsion in enlarged stimulated ovaries with worsening pain","Underestimating late, pregnancy-associated OHSS, which can be more severe and protracted"],"management":["Stop ongoing gonadotrophin and hCG stimulation and, for prevention in high responders, use a GnRH-antagonist protocol with an agonist trigger, elective embryo freezing, and cabergoline to reduce vascular permeability","Manage symptoms conservatively with analgesia, antiemetics and careful oral or intravenous fluid replacement titrated to urine output, avoiding NSAIDs","Administer low-molecular-weight heparin thromboprophylaxis given the prothrombotic, haemoconcentrated state and any early pregnancy","Perform ultrasound-guided paracentesis of tense ascites, or drainage of a large pleural effusion, to relieve pressure symptoms and improve renal perfusion and breathing","Track haematocrit, electrolytes, renal and liver function, albumin and coagulation alongside serial pelvic ultrasound for ovarian size and effusions","Admit severe or critical cases for close monitoring under reproductive medicine, escalating to critical care for renal failure, respiratory failure or thromboembolism"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["RCOG guidance on ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome","ESHRE guidance on ovarian stimulation and OHSS prevention","Standard reproductive medicine texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"ovarian_hyperthecosis":{"name":"Ovarian hyperthecosis","system":"Endocrine / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["stromal hyperthecosis","ovarian stromal hyperthecosis","hyperthecosis of the ovary"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Yen and Jaffe's Reproductive Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The condition features nests of luteinised theca cells scattered through the ovarian stroma, driving marked ovarian androgen production","Hyperandrogenism is typically more severe than in polycystic ovary syndrome and often produces frank virilisation, with hirsutism, temporal balding, deepening of the voice, and clitoromegaly","It is strongly linked to insulin resistance and hyperinsulinaemia, and may present as part of the HAIR-AN phenotype with acanthosis nigricans","It is most often recognised in peri- and postmenopausal women but also occurs in younger women, in whom it can be mistaken for severe polycystic ovary syndrome","Serum testosterone is usually substantially elevated, and a key task is to distinguish it from an androgen-secreting ovarian or adrenal tumour","Diagnosis integrates the clinical picture with hormonal profiling and imaging, since ovaries may be enlarged and solid rather than polycystic in appearance","Management options include suppression of ovarian androgen output with a GnRH agonist, insulin-sensitising strategies, and bilateral oophorectomy in postmenopausal women when malignancy must be excluded or symptoms are severe"],"pathophysiology":"Ovarian hyperthecosis is a non-neoplastic disorder in which islands of luteinised theca-type cells are distributed throughout the ovarian stroma and secrete androgens, chiefly testosterone. Unlike the follicle-based dysfunction of polycystic ovary syndrome, the androgen excess here arises from these stromal luteinised cells, whose activity is amplified by hyperinsulinaemia; insulin and insulin-like growth factor stimulate ovarian theca and stromal cells to produce androgens, creating a self-reinforcing link between insulin resistance and hyperandrogenism. The resulting high circulating testosterone produces the virilising features. Because the androgen source is diffuse ovarian stroma rather than a discrete mass, imaging typically shows bilaterally enlarged solid ovaries rather than a tumour.","additionalDifferentials":["Polycystic ovary syndrome","Androgen-secreting ovarian tumour (e.g. Sertoli-Leydig cell tumour)","Androgen-secreting adrenal tumour","Non-classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Cushing syndrome","Exogenous androgen exposure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum total testosterone and other androgens to quantify hyperandrogenism","Assessment for insulin resistance, including fasting glucose and lipid profile","Transvaginal or pelvic ultrasound to assess ovarian size and morphology","Cross-sectional imaging of the adrenals and ovaries if a tumour is suspected","17-hydroxyprogesterone and adrenal workup to exclude congenital adrenal hyperplasia and Cushing syndrome","Gonadotrophin measurement to help interpret the hormonal picture"],"redFlags":["Rapid onset or rapidly progressive virilisation suggesting an androgen-secreting tumour","Very high serum testosterone raising concern for a neoplastic source","A discrete ovarian or adrenal mass on imaging","Symptoms or signs suggesting coexisting Cushing syndrome"],"prognosis":"The condition itself is benign, but it causes considerable distress from virilisation and carries the metabolic consequences of associated insulin resistance, including increased cardiometabolic risk. Androgen suppression or oophorectomy can control hyperandrogenism, and addressing insulin resistance improves the broader metabolic profile.","pitfalls":["Labelling the presentation as polycystic ovary syndrome despite disproportionately severe virilisation and testosterone elevation","Failing to exclude an androgen-secreting tumour before attributing symptoms to hyperthecosis","Neglecting the associated insulin resistance and its cardiometabolic implications","Expecting a polycystic appearance on imaging when the ovaries are typically enlarged and solid"],"management":["Suppress ovarian androgen production with a GnRH agonist or, in the appropriate patient, a combined oral contraceptive to lower circulating testosterone","Add an anti-androgen such as spironolactone or cyproterone acetate to control hirsutism and virilisation, with mechanical and cosmetic hair-reduction measures","Offer bilateral oophorectomy as definitive treatment where fertility is not desired, particularly in peri- and postmenopausal women, since the diffuse stromal source responds poorly to medical therapy alone","Address the underlying insulin resistance with lifestyle modification and an insulin sensitiser such as metformin, given the self-reinforcing hyperinsulinaemia and hyperandrogenism link","Manage associated cardiometabolic risk by treating dyslipidaemia, dysglycaemia and hypertension and monitoring weight and glycaemic status","Maintain surveillance to distinguish and exclude an androgen-secreting ovarian or adrenal tumour with cross-sectional imaging and biochemical follow-up","Refer to reproductive endocrinology, with gynaecology input for surgical decisions and psychological support for distressing virilisation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Yen and Jaffe's Reproductive Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"ovarian_remnant_syndrome":{"name":"Ovarian remnant syndrome","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ORS","ovarian remnant","residual ovarian tissue syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology reference textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Arises when a fragment of functioning ovarian tissue is left behind after an intended bilateral oophorectomy, later causing pelvic pain or a mass","Should be suspected when a woman develops cyclical pelvic pain, dyspareunia, or an adnexal mass following removal of both ovaries","Premenopausal gonadotrophin and oestradiol levels in a woman said to have had both ovaries removed strongly suggest retained functioning tissue","Risk is greatest after technically difficult surgery with dense adhesions from endometriosis, previous pelvic inflammatory disease, or prior operations","Cross-sectional imaging typically shows a small cystic adnexal lesion adjacent to the pelvic sidewall, often near the ureter","Definitive treatment is complete surgical excision of the remnant, which can be challenging and carries a risk of ureteric injury","Medical suppression of ovarian activity may relieve symptoms temporarily or help localise tissue before surgery but is not curative"],"pathophysiology":"During oophorectomy, particularly when the normal tissue planes are obscured by adhesions or endometriosis, a small portion of ovarian cortex may be inadvertently left in situ. This residual tissue retains its responsiveness to circulating gonadotrophins and continues to undergo follicular activity, forming functional cysts and generating local inflammation and pain. Because the remnant is often buried in scar tissue close to the pelvic sidewall and ureter, it can be difficult to identify and remove completely.","additionalDifferentials":["Residual ovary syndrome (deliberately conserved ovary)","Pelvic adhesive disease","Endometriosis","Recurrent or metastatic pelvic malignancy","Other adnexal or pelvic sidewall mass","Chronic pelvic pain of non-gynaecological origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum follicle-stimulating hormone, luteinising hormone, and oestradiol to demonstrate premenopausal ovarian function","Transvaginal ultrasound to locate a cystic adnexal lesion","Magnetic resonance imaging to characterise and localise the remnant and its relation to the ureter","Tumour markers where malignancy is a concern","Review of prior operative and histopathology records to confirm the extent of previous surgery"],"redFlags":["Imaging or clinical features suggesting malignancy within the mass","Ureteric involvement, hydronephrosis, or obstructive uropathy","Severe or rapidly worsening pelvic pain","Unexpectedly high oestradiol in a patient believed to be surgically menopausal"],"prognosis":"Symptoms usually resolve after complete excision of the remnant, though repeat pelvic surgery through scarred tissue is technically demanding and incomplete removal can lead to recurrence.","pitfalls":["Not confirming that the residual tissue is functional before attributing symptoms to it","Underestimating the difficulty and ureteric risk of re-operating in a scarred pelvis","Leaving further tissue behind, resulting in persistent or recurrent symptoms","Confusing ovarian remnant syndrome with a deliberately conserved residual ovary"],"management":["Undertake complete surgical excision of the residual ovarian tissue, ideally by an experienced gynaecological surgeon, as the definitive treatment for the pain and mass","Localise the remnant preoperatively with cross-sectional imaging such as MRI and consider preoperative ureteric stenting to reduce the risk of ureteric injury during dissection through scarred tissue","Offer hormonal suppression with a GnRH agonist or combined oral contraceptive as a temporising or adjunctive measure to quieten follicular activity when immediate surgery is not appropriate","Provide symptomatic analgesia for cyclical pelvic pain and dyspareunia while awaiting definitive treatment","Confirm retained functioning tissue with premenopausal gonadotrophin and oestradiol levels and review of prior operative and histopathology records","Manage within a multidisciplinary team including urology given the remnant's proximity to the pelvic sidewall and ureter, and counsel on the risk of incomplete removal and recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference textbook","Operative gynaecology reference text"],"crossLinks":[]},"ovarian_torsion":{"name":"Ovarian Torsion","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["adnexal torsion","torsion of the ovary","ovarian and tubal torsion","twisted ovarian cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Torsion is a gynaecological emergency in which the ovary, often with the fallopian tube, twists on its pedicle and compromises its blood supply, threatening ovarian viability","It classically presents with sudden severe unilateral pelvic pain, often colicky and associated with nausea and vomiting, sometimes provoked by exertion or a change in position","An enlarged ovary or an ovarian mass, particularly cysts around five centimetres or larger, predisposes to torsion, so it is uncommon in a normal-sized ovary","Doppler ultrasound may show reduced or absent flow, but preserved arterial flow does not exclude torsion because dual blood supply and intermittent twisting can maintain signals","Torsion can occur in pregnancy, especially after ovulation induction with enlarged ovaries, and in premenarchal girls in whom the diagnosis is easily missed","Prompt surgical intervention, usually laparoscopic detorsion with conservation of the ovary, is preferred over oophorectomy even when the ovary looks dusky, as tissue often recovers","The classic error is delay: repeated observation while awaiting imaging or normalisation of pain risks irreversible ischaemic loss of the ovary","Intermittent torsion produces recurrent self-limiting episodes of pain and warrants evaluation before a definitive infarction occurs"],"pathophysiology":"When the ovary or adnexa rotates about the infundibulopelvic and utero-ovarian ligaments, the lower-pressure venous and lymphatic drainage is occluded first, producing congestion and marked ovarian oedema and enlargement. Continued or tightening rotation eventually compromises arterial inflow, leading to ischaemia, haemorrhagic infarction, and necrosis. Because arterial flow may persist longer than venous outflow owing to the thicker arterial wall and dual blood supply, viability can be maintained for a variable period, defining a window in which detorsion can salvage the ovary.","additionalDifferentials":["Ruptured or haemorrhagic ovarian cyst","Ectopic pregnancy","Acute appendicitis","Pelvic inflammatory disease or tubo-ovarian abscess","Ureteric colic","Degenerating or torting pedunculated fibroid"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal or transabdominal pelvic ultrasound with Doppler to assess ovarian size, mass, and blood flow","Urine or serum beta-hCG to exclude pregnancy and ectopic","Full blood count and inflammatory markers to help exclude infective or appendiceal causes","Diagnostic laparoscopy, which is both confirmatory and therapeutic when suspicion is high","Pelvic MRI in selected non-acute or pregnant cases when ultrasound is inconclusive"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe unilateral pelvic pain with an adnexal mass, mandating urgent assessment to preserve the ovary","Persistent pain with peritonism suggesting ischaemia or necrosis","Haemodynamic instability raising concern for haemorrhage or a ruptured ectopic","Signs of sepsis suggesting necrotic or infected adnexa","Recurrent episodic pain indicating intermittent torsion needing prompt surgical evaluation"],"prognosis":"When detorsion is performed promptly the ovary usually recovers function even if it appeared ischaemic at surgery, and future fertility is generally preserved. Delayed diagnosis leads to infarction requiring oophorectomy and loss of that ovary's function; recurrence can occur, and oophoropexy may be considered in selected cases such as recurrent torsion or a normal ovary in a child.","pitfalls":["Excluding torsion because Doppler shows preserved arterial flow","Delaying surgery for repeated imaging or observation while ischaemia progresses","Performing oophorectomy on a dusky ovary that would recover after simple detorsion","Failing to consider torsion in premenarchal girls and pregnant women","Missing intermittent torsion by attributing recurrent pain to functional cysts"],"management":["Perform emergency laparoscopic detorsion to restore blood flow, conserving the ovary even when it appears dusky since the tissue commonly recovers","Remove a causative ovarian cyst or mass by cystectomy at the same operation to treat the predisposing lesion","Reserve oophorectomy for a frankly necrotic, non-viable ovary","Consider oophoropexy to fix the ovary where torsion is recurrent or the pedicle is abnormally long","Give preoperative analgesia and antiemetics and keep the patient nil by mouth in preparation for urgent theatre","Do not exclude the diagnosis on preserved Doppler arterial flow; proceed to prompt diagnostic laparoscopy when clinical suspicion is high, as delay leads to infarction","Involve gynaecology urgently, with counselling on ovarian conservation and future fertility"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"overactive_bladder":{"name":"Overactive bladder","system":"Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OAB","Urge syndrome","Detrusor overactivity syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Overactive bladder is a symptom syndrome defined by urinary urgency, usually with frequency and nocturia, with or without urgency incontinence, in the absence of infection or other identifiable pathology.","Urgency, the sudden compelling desire to void that is difficult to defer, is the cornerstone symptom and distinguishes overactive bladder from the normal, deferrable urge to pass urine.","Detrusor overactivity on urodynamics is a related but separate concept; many patients with overactive bladder symptoms show no involuntary contractions, and the diagnosis remains clinical.","A bladder diary quantifying frequency, voided volumes, urgency episodes and fluid intake is the single most useful assessment tool and guides behavioural therapy.","First-line treatment is conservative: fluid and caffeine modification, bladder training and pelvic floor muscle training, before considering pharmacotherapy.","Pharmacological options include antimuscarinic agents and beta-3 adrenergic agonists; antimuscarinic anticholinergic load should be minimised in older and cognitively vulnerable patients.","Refractory cases may respond to intradetrusor botulinum toxin injection, percutaneous or sacral neuromodulation, always after excluding significant post-void residual and other pathology.","New or persistent overactive bladder symptoms in an older patient, or symptoms with visible haematuria, mandate exclusion of bladder carcinoma or carcinoma in situ before ascribing them to overactive bladder."],"pathophysiology":"Overactive bladder reflects a disturbance of the storage phase of the micturition cycle in which afferent and efferent control of the detrusor is dysregulated. Proposed mechanisms include a myogenic component, with increased spontaneous detrusor activity and enhanced coupling between smooth muscle cells, and a neurogenic component, with heightened afferent signalling and reduced central inhibition of the voiding reflex. Urothelial and suburothelial signalling abnormalities, involving altered release of acetylcholine, ATP and other mediators and up-regulated receptor sensitivity, augment sensory input from the bladder wall. The net effect is a perception of urgency at low bladder volumes, often accompanied by phasic involuntary detrusor contractions during filling. Contributing factors include ageing, bladder outlet obstruction, metabolic and neurological disease, and pelvic floor dysfunction, but in most patients no single structural cause is identified.","additionalDifferentials":["Urinary tract infection or bacterial cystitis","Bladder carcinoma or carcinoma in situ","Bladder pain syndrome / interstitial cystitis","Bladder outlet obstruction with secondary detrusor overactivity","Neurogenic detrusor overactivity from spinal or cerebral disease","Polyuria from diabetes, diuretics or excessive fluid intake","Bladder calculus or foreign body"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Frequency-volume bladder diary over several days","Urinalysis and urine culture to exclude infection and haematuria","Post-void residual measurement by bladder ultrasound","Urine cytology when carcinoma in situ is suspected","Validated symptom and quality-of-life questionnaires","Urodynamic study in refractory, complex or pre-surgical cases","Cystoscopy where haematuria, pain or malignancy risk is present"],"redFlags":["Visible haematuria accompanying storage symptoms","Persistent sterile pyuria or recurrent proven infection","Significant post-void residual or palpable bladder suggesting retention","New neurological signs, back pain or lower limb symptoms suggesting a neurological cause","Rapidly progressive symptoms or bladder pain suggesting malignancy or bladder pain syndrome"],"prognosis":"Overactive bladder is a chronic, fluctuating condition rather than a curable one, but the majority of patients achieve meaningful symptom control and improved quality of life with a stepped combination of behavioural measures, pharmacotherapy and, where needed, minimally invasive therapies. Symptom relapse after stopping treatment is common.","pitfalls":["Starting antimuscarinic therapy without excluding a large post-void residual, risking precipitation of retention","Labelling symptoms as overactive bladder without excluding infection, haematuria or malignancy","Ignoring cumulative anticholinergic burden and cognitive risk in elderly patients","Overlooking modifiable contributors such as caffeine, excessive evening fluids or constipation","Failing to recognise bladder pain syndrome, in which pain rather than urgency drives frequency"],"management":["Begin with conservative behavioural therapy comprising fluid and caffeine modification, bladder retraining and pelvic floor muscle training, guided by a frequency-volume bladder diary","Offer an antimuscarinic agent such as solifenacin or oxybutynin as first-line pharmacotherapy, minimising the anticholinergic burden in older or cognitively vulnerable patients","Use a beta-3 adrenergic agonist such as mirabegron as an alternative or add-on, particularly where anticholinergic side effects are a concern","Treat contributing factors such as constipation and review medications and comorbidities that worsen urgency","For refractory symptoms escalate to intradetrusor botulinum toxin injection, posterior tibial nerve stimulation or sacral neuromodulation","Exclude infection, haematuria and a significant post-void residual before and during treatment, and refer to urology for refractory, complex or red-flag presentations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology, current edition","European Association of Urology Guidelines on non-neurogenic female lower urinary tract symptoms","International Continence Society standardisation of terminology"],"crossLinks":[]},"overhydrated_hereditary_stomatocytosis":{"name":"Overhydrated hereditary stomatocytosis (RHAG)","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RHAG","OHS","hydrocytosis","overhydrated hereditary stomatocytosis","stomatin-related overhydrated stomatocytosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / Nathan and Oski","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal dominant haemolytic anaemia in which red cells are overhydrated and swollen, associated with mutations in RHAG (Rh-associated glycoprotein)","The blood film shows numerous stomatocytes with a central slit-like pallor","Red cells are overhydrated with a low mean corpuscular haemoglobin concentration and a raised mean cell volume, the mirror image of dehydrated xerocytosis","Osmotic fragility is characteristically increased because the swollen cells lyse readily","Loss of stomatin from the red-cell membrane is a recognised biochemical feature","Anaemia is usually moderate with reticulocytosis and variable jaundice and splenomegaly","As in other hereditary stomatocytoses, splenectomy carries a high risk of thromboembolic complications and is generally avoided","Diagnosis combines red-cell indices, ektacytometry, membrane protein analysis, and genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"In overhydrated hereditary stomatocytosis, mutations in RHAG convert the Rh-associated glycoprotein into an abnormally permeable cation-conducting pore, greatly increasing monovalent cation flux across the red-cell membrane. Net cation and water gain overhydrate and swell the cell, lowering its haemoglobin concentration and producing the characteristic stomatocyte morphology with associated loss of the membrane protein stomatin. The fragile, overhydrated cells undergo premature destruction, causing a chronic haemolytic anaemia; as with related disorders, splenectomy is hazardous because it predisposes to thrombosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Hereditary xerocytosis (dehydrated stomatocytosis, PIEZO1)","Hereditary spherocytosis","Rh-null disease and related membrane defects","Acquired stomatocytosis (alcohol, liver disease, drugs)","Cryohydrocytosis and intermediate stomatocytosis syndromes","Other congenital haemolytic anaemias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and reticulocytes noting a low mean corpuscular haemoglobin concentration and high mean cell volume","Peripheral blood film showing prominent stomatocytes","Osmotic gradient ektacytometry demonstrating the overhydrated red-cell profile","Red-cell membrane protein analysis for reduced stomatin","Genetic testing including RHAG","Haemolysis markers and iron studies"],"redFlags":["Thromboembolic events, especially following splenectomy","Severe or decompensated anaemia during intercurrent illness or aplastic crisis","Progressive iron overload with organ involvement","Rapidly enlarging spleen or evidence of hypersplenism"],"prognosis":"The chronic haemolytic anaemia is usually moderate and compatible with a good quality of life, but the major avoidable hazard is post-splenectomy thrombosis, and iron overload requires monitoring.","pitfalls":["Recommending splenectomy without recognising the marked post-splenectomy thrombotic risk of hereditary stomatocytoses","Confusing overhydrated with dehydrated stomatocytosis despite opposite red-cell indices and fragility","Missing the diagnosis when stomatocytes are attributed to an artefact or to acquired causes","Failing to monitor and manage iron overload in a non-transfused patient"],"management":["Avoid splenectomy, which is contraindicated because it markedly increases thrombotic risk in this and related stomatocytosis disorders","Give folic acid supplementation to support the increased erythropoiesis of chronic haemolysis","Provide red-cell transfusion for symptomatic anaemia, restricting it to clinical need","Monitor for and manage iron overload, using iron chelation where transfusional or absorptive loading develops, and avoid unnecessary iron supplementation","Track the full blood count, reticulocytes, haemolysis markers and iron studies over time","Consider thromboprophylaxis around surgery or periods of immobility given the underlying prothrombotic tendency","Offer genetic counselling and family assessment for this autosomal dominant RHAG-related disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"overlap_myositis":{"name":"overlap myositis","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["overlap syndrome myositis","connective-tissue-disease-associated myositis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Inflammatory myopathy arising alongside another defined connective tissue disease, or defined by an overlap-associated autoantibody, rather than as isolated myositis.","The antisynthetase syndrome is a key subset, marked by anti-Jo-1 or other anti-tRNA-synthetase antibodies (anti-PL-7, anti-PL-12, anti-EJ, anti-OJ, anti-KS).","Its clinical cluster comprises myositis, interstitial lung disease, non-erosive arthritis, Raynaud phenomenon, mechanic's hands and fever, though not all features are present together.","Anti-PM/Scl antibodies define a scleroderma-myositis overlap with sclerodactyly, Raynaud and calcinosis, and generally carry a favourable, steroid-responsive course.","Anti-U1-RNP points to mixed connective tissue disease, while anti-Ku indicates another overlap phenotype.","Muscle weakness is often milder than in classic polymyositis or dermatomyositis, and creatine kinase may be only modestly raised.","Interstitial lung disease is the principal driver of morbidity and mortality and can be the presenting or dominant feature.","There is a female predominance and a chronic relapsing course requiring long-term immunosuppression."],"pathophysiology":"Overlap myositis is an autoimmune inflammatory myopathy that occurs within the immune dysregulation of a broader connective tissue disease and is closely linked to specific autoantibodies. In the antisynthetase syndrome, antibodies target aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, and it is proposed that these enzymes, upregulated and exposed in regenerating muscle and injured lung epithelium, act as autoantigens that sustain a self-amplifying response involving both antibody and T-cell mechanisms. Anti-PM/Scl and anti-U1-RNP overlaps share features of scleroderma and lupus-spectrum disease, with vasculopathy, complement activation and cytokine-driven fibrosis contributing to the muscle, skin and lung manifestations.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated polymyositis or dermatomyositis","Immune-mediated necrotising myopathy","Inclusion body myositis","Systemic sclerosis with primary myopathy","Systemic lupus erythematosus myositis","Drug-induced or hypothyroid myopathy","Fibromyalgia and steroid myopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase and muscle enzymes","Extended myositis and overlap antibody panel (anti-Jo-1 and other synthetases, anti-PM/Scl, anti-U1-RNP, anti-Ku)","Antinuclear and extractable-nuclear-antigen antibodies","High-resolution CT of the chest and pulmonary function tests with gas transfer","Nailfold capillaroscopy","Electromyography and muscle MRI or biopsy","Echocardiography to assess for pulmonary hypertension"],"redFlags":["Progressive breathlessness or rapidly progressive interstitial lung disease","Pulmonary hypertension","Dysphagia with aspiration risk","Features suggesting an underlying malignancy","New cardiac symptoms"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable and largely determined by the presence and severity of interstitial lung disease; anti-PM/Scl overlap is relatively favourable, whereas some antisynthetase phenotypes carry a poorer prognosis. The disease typically follows a chronic relapsing course needing sustained immunosuppression.","pitfalls":["Overlooking interstitial lung disease, which may dominate the presentation while muscle symptoms are mild.","Attributing arthritis solely to rheumatoid disease and missing the overlap.","Missing overlap antibodies when only a limited myositis panel is requested.","Underestimating disease when creatine kinase is normal or only mildly elevated.","Failing to reassess for pulmonary hypertension over time."],"management":["Induce disease control with corticosteroids as first-line immunosuppression for the inflammatory myopathy","Add a steroid-sparing immunosuppressant such as methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate mofetil for maintenance and to limit steroid toxicity","Treat significant interstitial lung disease more aggressively with agents such as mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide or rituximab, since lung involvement drives prognosis","Use intravenous immunoglobulin for refractory or severe myositis","Manage Raynaud phenomenon with cold avoidance and a calcium-channel blocker, and provide physiotherapy and skin care for mechanic's hands and arthritis","Monitor creatine kinase, pulmonary function with gas transfer, and high-resolution CT to track myositis and lung-disease activity","Coordinate rheumatology, respiratory and physiotherapy input, escalating for rapidly progressive interstitial lung disease or respiratory compromise"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["EULAR/ACR classification criteria for idiopathic inflammatory myopathies","European Neuromuscular Centre (ENMC) workshop reports on antisynthetase and overlap myositis","Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"overlap_syndrome_rheum":{"name":"Rheumatological overlap syndrome","system":"Rheumatology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["overlap syndrome","connective tissue overlap","scleromyositis","anti-PM/Scl","anti-Ku"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology / international connective tissue disease guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Patients simultaneously fulfil criteria for two or more distinct connective tissue or autoimmune diseases","Common combinations include systemic sclerosis with inflammatory myositis (scleromyositis) or lupus with rheumatoid arthritis","Specific autoantibodies such as anti-PM/Scl and anti-Ku help characterise particular overlap phenotypes","Unlike mixed connective tissue disease, overlap syndromes meet full criteria for each component disorder rather than sharing a single defining antibody","Management is directed at the dominant and most threatening organ involvement across the combined diseases","Careful serological and clinical mapping guides prognosis and treatment intensity","The overlapping features may appear together or accrue sequentially over time"],"pathophysiology":"Rheumatological overlap syndromes arise when autoimmune mechanisms characteristic of more than one defined connective tissue disease coexist in a single patient. Shared genetic susceptibility and dysregulated adaptive immunity generate autoantibodies and tissue inflammation spanning several disorders, sometimes marked by antibodies such as anti-PM/Scl or anti-Ku that associate with combined phenotypes. The resulting clinical picture blends the vasculopathy, fibrosis, synovitis, and myositis of the component diseases, and organ involvement reflects the summed pathology.","additionalDifferentials":["Mixed connective tissue disease","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Systemic sclerosis","Idiopathic inflammatory myopathy","Rheumatoid arthritis","Undifferentiated connective tissue disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antinuclear antibody with a broad extractable nuclear antigen and myositis-specific/associated antibody panel","Anti-PM/Scl and anti-Ku testing when scleromyositis is suspected","Creatine kinase and inflammatory markers for myositis","Transthoracic echocardiography and pulmonary function tests for cardiopulmonary involvement","High-resolution CT for interstitial lung disease","Renal function and urinalysis to detect nephritis"],"redFlags":["Severe myositis with respiratory or bulbar involvement","Progressive interstitial lung disease or pulmonary hypertension","Lupus-type nephritis with declining renal function","Digital or systemic ischaemia from active vasculopathy"],"prognosis":"Outcome is determined by the combination and severity of component diseases and their organ involvement; some overlaps behave indolently while others, particularly those with significant lung, cardiac, or renal disease, carry substantial morbidity. Tailored immunosuppression can control inflammation across the overlapping conditions.","pitfalls":["Treating only the most obvious component and neglecting concurrent organ-threatening disease","Confusing an overlap syndrome with mixed connective tissue disease and mislabelling the serology","Missing evolving features of a second disorder over time","Under-recognising myositis-associated interstitial lung disease"],"management":["Direct treatment at the dominant and most organ-threatening manifestation across the coexisting connective tissue diseases rather than at a single label","Use corticosteroids with a steroid-sparing immunosuppressant such as methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate for the inflammatory arthritis and myositis components","Escalate to potent agents such as cyclophosphamide or rituximab for severe organ involvement including nephritis or progressive interstitial lung disease","Manage scleroderma-spectrum features with Raynaud measures including a calcium-channel blocker, and treat pulmonary arterial hypertension where present","Monitor across systems with creatine kinase, renal function and urinalysis, pulmonary function tests, echocardiography and high-resolution CT","Coordinate care through rheumatology with respiratory, nephrology and cardiology input, adjusting immunosuppression intensity to the combined phenotype"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology","International literature on connective tissue overlap syndromes"],"crossLinks":[]},"ovotesticular_dsd":{"name":"Ovotesticular disorder of sex development","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ovotesticular DSD","true hermaphroditism","ovotestis syndrome","OT-DSD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Endocrine Society clinical guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the presence of both ovarian tissue (with follicles) and testicular tissue (with seminiferous tubules) in the same individual, either as separate gonads or combined in an ovotestis","The most common karyotype is 46,XX, with 46,XX/46,XY chimerism and 46,XY seen less often","External genitalia are usually ambiguous, and the degree of virilisation reflects the amount and function of the testicular component","Mullerian and wolffian structures may coexist, with a uterus often present on the side bearing ovarian tissue","Definitive diagnosis requires gonadal histology demonstrating both tissue types, not imaging alone","Management is individualised around sex of rearing, functional gonadal potential and malignancy risk, with retention of gonadal tissue matched to the assigned sex where feasible","Gonadal tissue containing Y material or dysgenetic elements carries tumour risk and warrants removal or close surveillance"],"pathophysiology":"Ovotesticular disorder of sex development arises when the bipotential gonad differentiates into both ovarian and testicular tissue within the same person. In 46,XX cases this can follow cryptic or translocated SRY, gain-of-function in the testis-promoting pathway (for example SOX9 duplication) or as-yet-undefined mechanisms that allow partial testis determination without a Y chromosome. The mixed gonadal tissue secretes a variable combination of testosterone and anti-mullerian hormone, so internal ducts and external genitalia develop along an intermediate path determined locally by the dominant tissue on each side. The result is a spectrum of ambiguity, and gonadal segments with testicular or dysgenetic elements can predispose to germ cell tumours.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital adrenal hyperplasia causing virilisation of a 46,XX infant","Mixed gonadal dysgenesis (45,X/46,XY)","Partial androgen insensitivity syndrome","Partial gonadal dysgenesis","5-alpha-reductase deficiency","Placental aromatase deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype and molecular studies including SRY and copy-number analysis for SOX9","Pelvic and gonadal imaging by ultrasound or MRI to localise gonads and mullerian structures","Serum testosterone, anti-mullerian hormone and gonadotropins, with hCG stimulation to assess testicular function","Gonadal biopsy and histopathology to confirm the presence of both tissue types","Adrenal steroid profiling (including 17-hydroxyprogesterone) to exclude congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Germ cell tumour surveillance of retained gonadal tissue"],"redFlags":["Ambiguous genitalia in a newborn requiring urgent exclusion of salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia","An enlarging gonadal mass suggesting germ cell malignancy in dysgenetic or Y-bearing tissue","Progressive inappropriate virilisation or feminisation after sex assignment","Delayed or absent puberty indicating gonadal insufficiency"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend on gonadal function preserved after surgery, the assigned sex and psychosocial support; fertility has occasionally been reported, particularly from the ovarian component. Malignancy risk in retained testicular or dysgenetic tissue necessitates lifelong awareness and surveillance.","pitfalls":["Assuming imaging can distinguish ovotestis from other gonads without histology","Overlooking coexisting congenital adrenal hyperplasia as a cause of ambiguity","Removing potentially functional gonadal tissue congruent with the sex of rearing","Neglecting the malignancy risk of retained Y-bearing or dysgenetic components"],"management":["Manage within a specialist multidisciplinary disorders-of-sex-development team, individualising decisions around sex of rearing, gonadal function and malignancy risk","Undertake gonadal surgery matched to the assigned sex, removing discordant or dysgenetic gonadal tissue while retaining functional tissue where feasible","Provide sex-steroid hormone replacement, oestrogen or testosterone, to induce and maintain puberty and secondary sexual characteristics appropriate to the assigned sex","Offer genital reconstructive surgery where indicated, timed and planned with the family and the young person","Maintain germ cell tumour surveillance of any retained testicular or dysgenetic gonadal tissue, or perform gonadectomy where malignancy risk is high","Provide psychological support to the child or young person and family throughout","Offer genetic counselling with karyotype and molecular characterisation including SRY and SOX9 to inform diagnosis and recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society clinical practice guidance on disorders of sex development"],"crossLinks":[]},"oxalate_nephropathy":{"name":"Oxalate nephropathy","system":"Renal / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oxalate nephropathy","calcium oxalate nephropathy","hyperoxaluric nephropathy","oxalosis (renal)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Brenner and Rector's The Kidney","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by deposition of calcium oxalate crystals within the tubules and interstitium, producing acute or chronic kidney injury","Biopsy shows polarisable, birefringent, often fan-shaped calcium oxalate crystals in tubular lumina with surrounding tubular injury and interstitial inflammation","Secondary (enteric) hyperoxaluria from fat malabsorption is a common cause, notably after bariatric surgery, in inflammatory bowel disease or pancreatic insufficiency","Other causes include primary hyperoxaluria (inherited hepatic enzyme defects), excessive dietary oxalate or vitamin C, and ethylene glycol poisoning","Presentation ranges from unexplained acute kidney injury to progressive chronic kidney disease, sometimes with nephrolithiasis or nephrocalcinosis","Enteric hyperoxaluria arises because unabsorbed fatty acids bind calcium, leaving free oxalate to be absorbed in the colon","Management addresses the cause: drug withdrawal and supportive care in toxin-related cases, dietary oxalate restriction and calcium supplementation with bile-acid handling in enteric disease, and specific therapy for primary hyperoxaluria"],"pathophysiology":"Oxalate is an end-product of metabolism that is renally excreted, and when plasma and tubular oxalate rise, calcium oxalate supersaturates and precipitates as crystals within the tubular lumina. These birefringent crystals obstruct and directly injure tubular epithelial cells, triggering inflammasome activation, interstitial inflammation and fibrosis, which drive acute kidney injury and, with sustained exposure, progressive chronic damage and nephrocalcinosis. Hyperoxaluria may be primary, from inherited defects of hepatic glyoxylate metabolism causing endogenous overproduction, or secondary, from enhanced enteric absorption in fat malabsorption, high oxalate or ascorbic-acid intake, or acute overproduction as in ethylene glycol poisoning. Declining renal function further impairs oxalate clearance, creating a self-amplifying cycle of systemic oxalate accumulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute tubular injury or acute interstitial nephritis of other cause","Other crystalline nephropathies (urate, drug crystals)","Nephrocalcinosis from hypercalcaemia","Ethylene glycol poisoning","Cast nephropathy","Chronic tubulointerstitial nephritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal biopsy with polarised microscopy demonstrating birefringent calcium oxalate crystals","24-hour urinary oxalate excretion and plasma oxalate","Assessment for fat malabsorption and relevant gastrointestinal or bariatric history","Genetic testing for primary hyperoxaluria in appropriate cases","Dietary and supplement history including vitamin C intake","Renal imaging for nephrocalcinosis and stones"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or dialysis-requiring acute kidney injury with widespread crystal deposition","Systemic oxalosis with extrarenal crystal deposition in advanced disease","Suspected ethylene glycol poisoning with metabolic acidosis and an osmolar gap","Recurrent nephrolithiasis with declining renal function suggesting primary hyperoxaluria"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the cause and the crystal and fibrosis burden at diagnosis; reversible or treatable causes may allow partial renal recovery, whereas heavy crystal deposition with interstitial fibrosis and untreated ongoing hyperoxaluria often progresses to end-stage kidney disease. Primary hyperoxaluria carries a particularly poor renal prognosis without disease-specific treatment, and dialysis alone poorly clears oxalate.","pitfalls":["Overlooking oxalate nephropathy as a cause of unexplained acute kidney injury","Failing to take a bariatric-surgery, malabsorption or supplement history","Missing primary hyperoxaluria in patients with recurrent stones and early renal failure","Not recognising ethylene glycol poisoning as an acute cause","Assuming standard dialysis adequately clears the systemic oxalate load"],"management":["Treat the underlying cause: manage enteric hyperoxaluria by correcting fat malabsorption, and in ethylene glycol poisoning give fomepizole and haemodialysis to halt oxalate generation","Reduce oxalate burden with a low-oxalate diet, oral calcium taken with meals to bind intestinal oxalate, restriction of vitamin C, and a bile-acid sequestrant such as cholestyramine in fat malabsorption","In primary hyperoxaluria use high fluid intake, pyridoxine in responsive type 1 disease, oral citrate to inhibit crystallisation, and RNA-interference therapy such as lumasiran","Maintain generous hydration to reduce tubular calcium-oxalate supersaturation","Provide renal replacement therapy for severe acute kidney injury and manage progression toward chronic kidney disease","Monitor urinary and plasma oxalate, renal function, and nephrocalcinosis or stone burden on imaging","Refer to nephrology, consider combined liver-kidney transplantation for primary hyperoxaluria with advanced kidney failure, and offer genetic counselling in inherited disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Brenner and Rector's The Kidney"],"crossLinks":[]},"oxygen_toxicity":{"name":"Oxygen toxicity","system":"Environmental / Emergency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oxygen poisoning","hyperoxia","CNS oxygen toxicity","pulmonary oxygen toxicity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine / Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from breathing oxygen at a raised partial pressure, as occurs with deep diving on oxygen-enriched mixtures or during hyperbaric oxygen therapy","The central nervous system form comes on relatively acutely at high oxygen partial pressure and can culminate in a generalised seizure, which is especially dangerous underwater","Warning symptoms of CNS toxicity may include visual disturbance, tinnitus, nausea, twitching (notably facial), irritability, and dizziness, though a seizure can occur without warning","The pulmonary form develops with prolonged exposure to elevated oxygen and causes retrosternal burning, cough, and progressive reduction in lung function","Management is to remove or reduce the excess oxygen exposure promptly, after which the effects are largely reversible","A convulsion in the water risks drowning, so prevention through oxygen exposure limits is critical","Susceptibility varies between individuals and is increased by factors such as carbon dioxide retention and immersion"],"pathophysiology":"Oxygen toxicity is caused by excessive generation of reactive oxygen species when tissues are exposed to a high partial pressure of oxygen that overwhelms antioxidant defences. In the central nervous system, high oxygen partial pressure disturbs neuronal excitability and can trigger seizures, typically after relatively short high-level exposures. In the lungs, more prolonged hyperoxia injures the alveolar-capillary membrane and airway epithelium, producing inflammation, tracheobronchial irritation, and impaired gas exchange. Both forms depend on the dose and duration of oxygen exposure and are largely reversible once exposure is curtailed.","additionalDifferentials":["Nitrogen narcosis","Carbon dioxide retention (hypercapnia)","Decompression illness with neurological features","Contaminated gas or carbon monoxide poisoning","Epilepsy from another cause","Pulmonary barotrauma or immersion pulmonary oedema (for the lung form)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical recognition based on oxygen exposure history and the dive or treatment profile","Review of the breathing gas oxygen fraction and depth or hyperbaric pressure","Neurological assessment following any seizure to exclude other causes","Pulmonary function assessment and chest examination for suspected pulmonary toxicity","Exclusion of hypercapnia and gas contamination as contributors"],"redFlags":["Seizure occurring underwater, with a high risk of drowning","Progressive respiratory symptoms with declining lung function","Persisting neurological deficit after the event, suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Coexisting decompression illness or gas contamination"],"prognosis":"Both central nervous system and pulmonary oxygen toxicity are generally reversible once the excessive oxygen exposure is stopped; the principal danger is a seizure occurring underwater leading to drowning rather than lasting toxicity itself.","pitfalls":["Exceeding recommended oxygen exposure limits on enriched gas mixtures","Assuming a warning aura always precedes a seizure","Confusing CNS oxygen toxicity with nitrogen narcosis or decompression illness","Overlooking carbon dioxide retention, which lowers the seizure threshold"],"management":["Immediately reduce or remove the excess oxygen exposure by switching the diver to a lower oxygen fraction or ascending shallower, and by lowering the inspired oxygen fraction during hyperbaric therapy, after which effects are largely reversible","During a central nervous system seizure protect the airway and prevent aspiration, and in the water keep the airway sealed and control ascent to avoid drowning and pulmonary barotrauma","Recognise premonitory features such as visual disturbance, tinnitus, nausea, facial twitching and irritability as a prompt to curtail exposure, while noting a seizure can occur without warning","Provide supportive respiratory care for the pulmonary form, allowing recovery of lung function once hyperoxia is stopped","Exclude and correct contributors such as hypercapnia and breathing-gas contamination","Prevent recurrence by adhering to oxygen partial-pressure and duration exposure limits and safe gas planning for diving and hyperbaric treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine","Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"p14_deficiency":{"name":"p14 Deficiency (LAMTOR2 Deficiency)","system":"Immunology / Inborn errors of immunity","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LAMTOR2 deficiency","MAPBPIP deficiency","Endosomal adaptor protein p14 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["p14 deficiency is a very rare autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss of the endosomal adaptor protein p14 (LAMTOR2), a component of the Ragulator/LAMTOR complex.","The syndrome combines congenital neutropenia with partial hypopigmentation of skin and hair, short stature and immune dysfunction.","The immune defect spans neutrophils, B lymphocytes and cytotoxic T cells, producing recurrent bronchopulmonary and skin infections.","Partial albinism links it phenotypically to other endosomal and lysosomal biogenesis and trafficking disorders, although its genetic and mechanistic basis is distinct.","Neutrophils show impaired function, B cells generate reduced antibody responses, and cytotoxic lymphocyte killing is impaired.","Recognising the combination of neutropenia, hypopigmentation and growth failure should prompt genetic evaluation.","Management is largely supportive, with infection prophylaxis, growth-factor support for neutropenia and consideration of haematopoietic stem cell transplantation."],"pathophysiology":"LAMTOR2 encodes the small endosomal adaptor protein p14, a subunit of the Ragulator (LAMTOR) complex anchored to the surface of late endosomes and lysosomes. This complex scaffolds MAPK (MEK/ERK) signalling on endosomes and regulates activation of mTOR complex 1 at the lysosomal membrane, and it is important for endosomal biogenesis and intracellular trafficking. A hypomorphic loss-of-function variant reduces p14 expression, impairing late endosomal function and the associated MAPK and mTOR-dependent processes across multiple cell lineages. The consequences include defective neutrophil, B-cell and cytotoxic T-cell function, disturbed melanosome and pigment biology (accounting for the hypopigmentation) and impaired growth (short stature).","additionalDifferentials":["Chediak-Higashi syndrome","Griscelli syndrome","Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome","Severe congenital neutropenia (for example ELANE-related)","WHIM syndrome","Other combined immunodeficiencies with pigmentary abnormality"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic sequencing of LAMTOR2","Full blood count with serial counts to document neutropenia","Bone marrow examination","Neutrophil function studies","Immunoglobulin levels and specific antibody responses","Cytotoxic T-cell and natural killer cell cytotoxicity assays","Examination of hair shaft and skin melanocyte pigmentation"],"redFlags":["Congenital or persistent neutropenia with recurrent bacterial infection","Combination of hypopigmentation with immunodeficiency","Failure to thrive or short stature alongside recurrent infection","Features overlapping haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis where cytotoxic function is impaired"],"prognosis":"Outcome data are limited given the rarity of the disorder; with infection prophylaxis and supportive care many patients do reasonably well, though the multi-lineage immune defect confers ongoing infection risk and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation may be considered in severe disease.","pitfalls":["Attributing hypopigmentation to Chediak-Higashi or Griscelli syndrome without considering p14/LAMTOR2 deficiency.","Overlooking the immunodeficiency when neutropenia dominates the presentation.","Regarding the hypopigmentation as merely cosmetic rather than a clue to an endosomal trafficking disorder.","Failing to assess cytotoxic lymphocyte function and the associated risk of haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis."],"management":["Prevent infection with prophylactic antimicrobials and treat bronchopulmonary and skin infections promptly and aggressively with appropriate antibiotics and antifungals","Support the neutrophil defect with granulocyte colony-stimulating factor to raise counts during infection or severe neutropenia","Provide immunoglobulin replacement where impaired B-cell antibody responses cause hypogammaglobulinaemia or recurrent infections","Consider haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the potentially curative option in severe multi-lineage immune disease","Monitor serial full blood counts, immunoglobulin levels, specific antibody responses and growth","Offer genetic counselling and family testing for this autosomal recessive LAMTOR2 disorder and manage within a specialist immunology service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity","Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies","Clinical immunology reference texts on phagocyte and combined immune defects"],"crossLinks":[]},"pachydermoperiostosis":{"name":"Pachydermoperiostosis (primary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["primary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy","Touraine-Solente-Gole syndrome","HPGD","SLCO2A1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The primary (hereditary) form of hypertrophic osteoarthropathy, presenting with the triad of digital clubbing, periostosis of the long bones and pachydermia (thickened, furrowed facial and scalp skin)","Caused by defects in prostaglandin E2 metabolism, through recessive variants in HPGD (the degrading enzyme) or SLCO2A1 (the prostaglandin transporter), leading to raised prostaglandin E2 levels","Typically begins around adolescence, is more common and more severe in males, and progresses over years before stabilising","Skin changes include coarse thickened facial skin, deep scalp folds (cutis verticis gyrata) and oily skin with excessive sweating","Periostitis causes limb pain and swelling, and joint effusions may occur","The key clinical task is to distinguish primary disease from secondary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy, which signals underlying lung malignancy, cardiac or other visceral disease","Management is symptomatic, addressing pain and, where troublesome, the skin and joint manifestations, with reassurance once secondary causes are excluded"],"pathophysiology":"Pachydermoperiostosis is caused by impaired inactivation of prostaglandin E2 due to biallelic variants in HPGD, encoding the enzyme that degrades prostaglandin E2, or in SLCO2A1, encoding the transporter that delivers prostaglandin E2 for degradation. Either defect raises circulating and tissue prostaglandin E2, which promotes periosteal new bone formation, soft-tissue and dermal proliferation and vascular changes. Clinically this manifests as digital clubbing, periostosis of the tubular bones with limb pain, and pachydermia with thickened, furrowed skin and increased sweating. The same pathway is implicated in secondary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy, but in the primary form the prostaglandin excess is intrinsic rather than driven by an underlying visceral disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy from lung malignancy or chronic pulmonary disease","Acromegaly","Thyroid acropachy","Rheumatoid arthritis or other inflammatory arthropathies","Camurati-Engelmann disease (progressive diaphyseal dysplasia)","Cutis verticis gyrata from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs demonstrating symmetrical periosteal new bone along the long bones","Chest imaging to exclude an underlying pulmonary or mediastinal cause of secondary disease","Bone scintigraphy where the extent of periostosis needs characterising","Thyroid function and growth hormone/IGF-1 testing to exclude acropachy and acromegaly","HPGD and SLCO2A1 molecular genetic testing to confirm primary disease","Assessment of urinary or plasma prostaglandin E2 metabolites where available"],"redFlags":["Rapid onset of clubbing and periostosis in an adult, strongly suggesting secondary disease and an occult malignancy","Respiratory symptoms, haemoptysis or weight loss pointing to underlying lung pathology","New cardiac or hepatic signs suggesting a visceral cause of secondary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy","Disproportionate or asymmetrical bone pain warranting exclusion of another process"],"prognosis":"The primary disease is benign and self-limiting, typically progressing over years before stabilising, with morbidity from bone and joint pain and cosmetic skin changes rather than reduced life expectancy; recognising and excluding secondary causes is what matters most for outcome.","pitfalls":["Assuming primary pachydermoperiostosis without excluding secondary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy and its sinister causes","Missing an underlying lung malignancy in an adult presenting with new clubbing and periostosis","Mistaking the coarse facies for acromegaly without appropriate endocrine testing","Overlooking the autosomal recessive inheritance and family implications"],"management":["Relieve periostosis-related bone and joint pain with NSAIDs, using a COX-2 selective inhibitor such as etoricoxib that also targets the underlying prostaglandin E2 excess","Consider colchicine for inflammatory skin features and flares, and a bisphosphonate for refractory periostitis pain","Manage pachydermia and cutis verticis gyrata with topical retinoids, botulinum toxin, or cosmetic and plastic surgery for disfiguring scalp folds","Provide symptomatic care for excessive sweating and oily skin","Exclude secondary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy from lung malignancy, cardiac or other visceral disease with chest imaging before attributing features to the primary disorder","Confirm the diagnosis with HPGD and SLCO2A1 genetic testing and offer genetic counselling for this recessive condition","Coordinate rheumatology, dermatology and metabolic bone input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders","GeneReviews: primary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"pachyonychia_congenita":{"name":"Pachyonychia congenita","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KRT6A","KRT6B","KRT16","KRT17","PC"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant keratinising disorder caused by variants in keratin genes KRT6A, KRT6B, KRT16, or KRT17","Hallmark is painful focal plantar keratoderma, which is frequently the most disabling symptom","Hypertrophic nail dystrophy with distal thickening and subungual hyperkeratosis affects most or all nails","Oral leukokeratosis (white plaques on the tongue and buccal mucosa) is common and can mimic candidiasis","Cysts (steatocystoma or pilosebaceous) and follicular keratoses are frequent, particularly with certain keratin subtypes","Natal or neonatal teeth occur with KRT17-related disease","Genotype influences phenotype, so keratin gene testing helps classify and counsel patients"],"pathophysiology":"Pachyonychia congenita arises from dominant-negative variants in genes encoding the keratins K6a, K6b, K16, and K17, which pair to form the intermediate filament cytoskeleton of specialised epithelia such as palmoplantar skin, nail bed, oral mucosa, and pilosebaceous structures. Mutant keratins disrupt filament assembly and weaken the mechanical resilience of these cells, so they cannot withstand normal physical stress. This provokes reactive hyperproliferation and hyperkeratosis in weight-bearing skin and nails and produces the painful plantar keratoderma, nail thickening, and mucosal and follicular manifestations that characterise the disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Other hereditary palmoplantar keratodermas","Onychomycosis and other causes of nail dystrophy","Oral candidiasis or leukoplakia","Dyskeratosis congenita (nail and mucosal involvement)","Chronic mechanical or friction keratoderma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of the pachyonychia congenita keratin genes","Fungal microscopy and culture of nails to exclude onychomycosis","Skin biopsy of keratoderma or oral lesions when the diagnosis is uncertain","Assessment and photography of nails, soles, and oral mucosa to document extent","Family history and examination of relatives given dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Severe plantar pain limiting mobility and function","Secondary bacterial or fungal infection of fissured keratoderma","Laryngeal involvement causing hoarseness or airway symptoms in early-onset severe disease","Persistent oral white plaque needing evaluation to exclude other pathology"],"prognosis":"It is a lifelong condition without effect on life expectancy, but the painful plantar keratoderma often causes chronic disability and reduced mobility; management is supportive, focused on mechanical offloading, keratolysis, and pain control.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating nails for presumed onychomycosis without recognising the genetic disorder","Mistaking oral leukokeratosis for candidiasis or premalignant leukoplakia","Underestimating the impact of plantar pain on daily function and mental health","Failing to offer genetic counselling regarding dominant inheritance"],"management":["Focus on the disabling plantar keratoderma with mechanical offloading, protective footwear and orthotics, and regular paring or debridement of hyperkeratotic skin","Apply keratolytics such as urea or salicylic acid, together with emollients, to soften and reduce keratoderma","Provide pain control for plantar pain with topical measures and analgesics, as pain is often the most disabling symptom","Manage thickened nails with regular podiatric trimming, and consider nail avulsion or ablation for troublesome nails","Treat cysts and oral leukokeratosis symptomatically, distinguishing oral plaques from candidiasis","Consider a trial of oral retinoids in selected cases, recognising the response is variable and may worsen plantar pain","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant keratin disorder, with examination of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"paediatric_functional_constipation":{"name":"Paediatric functional constipation","system":"Paediatrics / Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["childhood functional constipation","idiopathic constipation","functional faecal retention"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard paediatric gastroenterology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The great majority of childhood constipation is functional, with no underlying organic disease","A cycle of painful or hard defaecation leads to stool withholding, progressive rectal loading, and faecal impaction","Overflow, or paradoxical, soiling is the involuntary leakage of loose stool around impacted faeces and may be mistaken for diarrhoea","Withholding behaviour such as posturing, stiffening, and hiding is frequently misinterpreted by parents as straining to pass stool","Diagnosis is clinical using symptom-based Rome criteria, and onset often coincides with weaning, toilet training, or starting school","Red flags such as delayed passage of meconium, onset in the neonatal period, faltering growth, or abnormal neurological or spinal findings prompt evaluation for organic causes such as Hirschsprung disease","Digital rectal examination and routine imaging are not needed in straightforward cases","Management combines disimpaction followed by maintenance osmotic laxatives with behavioural measures such as a regular toileting routine, adequate fluid and fibre, and demystification, continued long enough to break the cycle"],"pathophysiology":"Painful defaecation prompts the child to withhold stool voluntarily. Retained stool becomes harder, larger, and more difficult to pass, while the rectum distends and gradually accommodates the growing mass so that the sensation and urge to defaecate diminish. Liquid stool then leaks around the impacted faeces, producing overflow soiling. This self-reinforcing cycle perpetuates the constipation in the absence of any structural or organic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Hirschsprung disease","Hypothyroidism","Coeliac disease","Anorectal malformation or anal stenosis","Spinal cord abnormality such as a tethered cord or spina bifida occulta","Cow's milk protein allergy","Metabolic causes such as hypercalcaemia, or medication side-effects"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical, Rome-based diagnosis from history and examination, which is usually sufficient","Coeliac serology and thyroid function tests if an organic cause is suspected","Abdominal examination and, selectively, imaging to assess faecal loading in difficult cases","Neurological and spinal examination, with spinal imaging if neurological signs are present","Rectal biopsy for Hirschsprung disease when red flags are present","Calcium and other metabolic tests where clinically indicated","Review of diet, fluid intake, and drug history"],"redFlags":["Delayed passage of meconium beyond the first day or two of life, or constipation from birth","Faltering growth or weight loss","Abdominal distension with vomiting suggesting obstruction","Abnormal neurological examination or spinal or sacral abnormalities","Ribbon-like stools, or an empty rectum with a palpable abdominal faecal mass suggesting Hirschsprung disease"],"prognosis":"The outlook is good with early, adequate, and sustained treatment, and many children recover, but under-treatment or premature withdrawal of laxatives commonly leads to relapse and prolonged symptoms.","pitfalls":["Under-dosing or stopping maintenance laxatives too early and causing relapse","Mistaking overflow soiling for diarrhoea and withholding treatment","Omitting disimpaction before starting maintenance therapy","Missing red flags for Hirschsprung disease or a spinal cause","Relying on behavioural advice alone without adequate laxative therapy"],"management":["Clear faecal impaction first with an oral osmotic laxative, typically a macrogol (polyethylene glycol) regimen, before starting maintenance","Continue regular maintenance laxative therapy with a macrogol, adding a stimulant laxative if needed, for a prolonged period and weaning slowly to avoid relapse","Institute behavioural measures including scheduled toilet sitting after meals, correct foot support and posture, and positive reinforcement to overcome withholding","Advise adequate dietary fibre and fluid intake alongside laxatives rather than in place of them","Educate the family that overflow soiling is involuntary, demystifying the withholding cycle to reduce blame","Review for red flags such as delayed meconium passage, neonatal onset, faltering growth, or neurological and spinal signs, and investigate for organic disease such as Hirschsprung disease if present","Avoid premature withdrawal of laxatives, arrange follow-up, and refer refractory cases to paediatric gastroenterology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rome diagnostic criteria for paediatric functional gastrointestinal disorders","Standard paediatric constipation clinical guidance","Standard paediatric gastroenterology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"page_kidney":{"name":"Page kidney","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Page phenomenon","Perinephric compression hypertension"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Renin-mediated hypertension caused by external compression of the renal parenchyma, usually by a subcapsular or perinephric haematoma.","It is named after Irvine Page, who reproduced hypertension experimentally by wrapping animal kidneys in cellophane.","Common triggers include trauma (including sporting injury), renal biopsy, anticoagulation, extracorporeal shockwave lithotripsy, tumour and cysts.","Compression reduces intrarenal perfusion and activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system in a mechanism analogous to Goldblatt hypertension.","It may present acutely after trauma or a procedure, or as unexplained or resistant hypertension months to years later.","Cross-sectional imaging with CT or MRI demonstrates the compressing collection and the compressed parenchyma.","Blockade of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system is a rational first-line therapy, while acute or refractory cases may need drainage, evacuation or rarely nephrectomy.","Small acute haematomas may resolve spontaneously with improvement in blood pressure."],"pathophysiology":"Page kidney results from external compression of the kidney by a subcapsular or perinephric collection, most often a haematoma but sometimes a urinoma, tumour or cyst, which raises intraparenchymal pressure and compresses the renal microvasculature. The resulting reduction in renal blood flow is sensed as hypoperfusion and activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system in a manner analogous to Goldblatt one-kidney hypertension. Angiotensin II-mediated vasoconstriction and aldosterone-driven sodium retention produce systemic hypertension, which can persist while the compressing lesion and secondary parenchymal ischaemia and fibrosis remain.","additionalDifferentials":["Renovascular hypertension from renal artery stenosis","Essential hypertension","Other secondary hypertension (phaeochromocytoma, primary aldosteronism)","Renal artery or vein thrombosis after trauma","Acute kidney injury of another cause after trauma or a procedure","Renal malignancy causing hypertension","Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast CT or MRI of the kidneys showing the compressing collection and compressed parenchyma","Renal ultrasound with Doppler","Plasma renin activity and aldosterone","Renal function and electrolytes","Blood pressure profiling or ambulatory monitoring","Coagulation screen if anticoagulated or with spontaneous haematoma","Isotope renography for differential function where intervention is considered"],"redFlags":["Severe or accelerated hypertension with end-organ damage","Expanding haematoma with haemodynamic compromise","Deteriorating renal function","An underlying malignancy as the compressing lesion"],"prognosis":"The outlook is often good, with hypertension resolving as an acute haematoma reabsorbs or after drainage or evacuation; chronic compression with established parenchymal fibrosis may cause persistent hypertension requiring long-term therapy or intervention.","pitfalls":["Overlooking Page kidney as a reversible cause of secondary or resistant hypertension after trauma, biopsy or lithotripsy","Attributing post-procedural hypertension to essential hypertension","Premature nephrectomy when drainage or medical therapy would suffice","Failing to recognise a malignant compressing lesion"],"management":["Control the renin-angiotensin-driven hypertension pharmacologically, using an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin-receptor blocker to target the activated pathway","Manage an acute subcapsular haematoma conservatively where possible, allowing reabsorption with blood-pressure control and analgesia, and reverse or withhold anticoagulation","Evacuate or drain the compressing collection, or undertake surgical decompression or capsulectomy, when hypertension is severe or persistent","Reserve nephrectomy for a non-functioning kidney with refractory hypertension after other measures fail","Monitor blood pressure including ambulatory profiling, renal function, and the collection on serial cross-sectional imaging","Refer to nephrology and urology, escalating for uncontrolled or resistant hypertension or deteriorating renal function"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Oxford Textbook of Medicine","Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"paget_schroetter_syndrome":{"name":"Paget-Schroetter syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Effort thrombosis","Primary axillosubclavian vein thrombosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Primary (effort) thrombosis of the axillary-subclavian vein, representing the venous form of thoracic outlet syndrome.","Typically affects young, healthy, muscular individuals after repetitive or strenuous upper-limb activity such as throwing, weightlifting or swimming, usually in the dominant arm.","Presents with sudden arm swelling, heaviness, cyanotic discolouration and dilated superficial collateral veins over the shoulder and chest wall.","The underlying cause is venous compression within the costoclavicular space by the first rib, the subclavius and anterior scalene muscles and the costoclavicular ligament, with repetitive endothelial trauma and thrombosis.","Duplex ultrasound is first-line; contrast, CT or MR venography with the arm in provocative positions confirms extrinsic compression.","Contemporary management combines early catheter-directed thrombolysis with first-rib resection and thoracic outlet decompression to address the anatomical cause.","It must be distinguished from secondary upper-limb deep vein thrombosis related to central venous catheters or malignancy, which is more common overall.","A thrombophilia screen may be considered in selected patients, but the primary abnormality is mechanical."],"pathophysiology":"Repetitive hyperabduction and extension of the arm compress the subclavian vein as it passes through the narrow costoclavicular space, bounded by the first rib, clavicle, subclavius muscle, costoclavicular ligament and anterior scalene. Chronic mechanical trauma produces intimal hyperplasia, perivenous fibrosis and venous wall injury which, combined with stasis during compression, triggers thrombosis by completing Virchow's triad. The dominant arm is usually affected because of greater muscular hypertrophy and activity. Untreated, residual stenosis and fibrosis perpetuate obstruction, driving collateral formation and post-thrombotic symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary upper-limb deep vein thrombosis from a central venous catheter, pacemaker or malignancy","Superior vena cava obstruction","Lymphoedema of the arm","Cellulitis","Arm oedema from cardiac failure","Muscle strain or rotator cuff injury","Neurogenic or arterial thoracic outlet syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound of the upper-limb veins","Contrast venography, often as a prelude to thrombolysis","CT or MR venography with provocative arm positioning","Chest and thoracic outlet imaging for a cervical rib or bony anomaly","Thrombophilia screen in selected patients","Assessment for occult malignancy when features suggest secondary thrombosis","Dynamic positional imaging to demonstrate extrinsic compression after clot clearance"],"redFlags":["Phlegmasia cerulea dolens with massive swelling, cyanosis and threatened limb viability.","Signs of pulmonary embolism, which can complicate upper-limb deep vein thrombosis.","Rapidly progressive swelling with neurological or arterial compromise.","Features suggesting an underlying malignancy in apparent secondary thrombosis.","Failure to improve or early re-thrombosis after anticoagulation."],"prognosis":"With prompt thrombolysis and thoracic outlet decompression most young patients regain a patent vein and return to full activity, whereas delayed or anticoagulation-only treatment leaves a significant minority with post-thrombotic arm symptoms and activity limitation.","pitfalls":["Treating with anticoagulation alone and ignoring the underlying mechanical compression, leading to recurrence and post-thrombotic morbidity.","Delaying venography and thrombolysis beyond the window when the clot is still amenable.","Failing to image in provocative positions and thereby missing dynamic compression once the vein is recanalised.","Mislabelling effort thrombosis as a simple muscle strain in an athlete.","Overlooking a cervical rib or other bony anomaly on the affected side."],"management":["Restore venous patency with early catheter-directed thrombolysis of the axillary-subclavian thrombus","Address the anatomical cause with first-rib resection and thoracic outlet decompression, relieving costoclavicular compression of the subclavian vein","Anticoagulate with heparin followed by an oral anticoagulant to treat the thrombus and prevent propagation","Treat residual venous stenosis after decompression with balloon venoplasty as required","Provide supportive arm elevation and analgesia in the acute phase and modify provocative repetitive overhead activity","Manage through vascular surgery, and screen for thrombophilia or occult malignancy in selected or atypical cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Society for Vascular Surgery reporting standards and guidance on thoracic outlet syndrome","Oxford Textbook of Vascular Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"pagets_disease_bone":{"name":"Paget's Disease of Bone","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic Bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["osteitis deformans"],"chapter":424,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3311-3314","clinicalPearls":["A localized bone-remodeling disorder affecting widespread, noncontiguous areas of the skeleton; initiated by overactive osteoclastic resorption followed by compensatory osteoblastic new bone formation, producing a disorganized mosaic of woven and lamellar bone (p.3311).","Most patients are asymptomatic; diagnosis is often made incidentally from an elevated alkaline phosphatase (ALP) on routine blood chemistry or an abnormal skeletal radiograph obtained for another indication (p.3311, p.3312-3313).","Serum calcium and phosphate levels are normal in Paget's disease; isolated/raised ALP with normal calcium is the characteristic biochemical picture (p.3314).","Sites most commonly involved are the pelvis, vertebral bodies, skull, femur, and tibia; familial early-onset cases often have numerous active sites (p.3313).","The most common presenting symptom is pain, which may arise from increased bony vascularity, expanding lytic lesions, fractures, bowing, or deformity; long-bone bowing causes gait abnormalities and secondary osteoarthritis of hip or knee (p.3313).","Skull involvement may cause headaches, frontal bossing, increased head size, and narrowing of cranial foramina leading to hearing loss (cochlear nerve damage from temporal bone involvement), cranial nerve palsies, and platybasia with risk of brainstem compression (p.3313).","For most patients, serum total ALP remains the test of choice for both diagnosis and assessing response to therapy; bone resorption markers (N-telopeptide, C-telopeptide) fall more rapidly with treatment than ALP (p.3313).","Radionuclide 99mTc bone scans are less specific but more sensitive than standard radiographs for identifying sites of active skeletal lesions (p.3313)."],"pathophysiology":"The principal abnormality is increased number and activity of osteoclasts, which are large, increased 10- to 100-fold in number, and contain many nuclei (up to 100 vs 3-5 in normal osteoclasts), creating a sevenfold rise in resorptive surfaces. Contributing mechanisms include osteoclast precursor hypersensitivity to 1,25(OH)2D3, hyperresponsiveness to RANK ligand (RANKL), increased RANKL expression by marrow stromal cells, increased IL-6 (raising precursor recruitment), increased c-fos expression, and overexpression of the antiapoptotic oncogene Bcl-2. Osteoblasts are recruited to resorption sites and lay down large amounts of new bone matrix, so turnover is high and bone mass is normal or increased (not reduced) unless there is concomitant calcium/vitamin D deficiency. The process passes through an initial osteolytic phase (prominent resorption and hypervascularization, seen radiographically as an advancing lytic wedge or 'blade of grass' lesion), an active mixed phase replacing lamellar bone with haphazard woven bone (marrow may be replaced by fibrous tissue), and a final sclerotic ('burned-out') phase of hard, dense, less vascular mosaic bone; all three phases may coexist at different sites. Etiology remains unknown but evidence supports both genetic (family history in 15-25%; familial syndromes linked to TNFRSF11B/osteoprotegerin, TNFRSF11A/RANK, profilin 1, valosin-containing protein, and the common SQSTM1/p62 mutation) and viral (paramyxovirus-like inclusions and viral mRNA in osteoclasts) contributions (p.3311-3313).","additionalDifferentials":["Osteosarcoma or other sarcoma arising in long-standing pagetic bone (new pain in a long-standing lesion)","Osteoclast-rich benign giant cell tumor arising adjacent to pagetic bone (responds to glucocorticoids)","Metastatic disease in bone (assessed by MRI; definitive diagnosis of malignancy often requires bone biopsy)","Other causes of an isolated raised alkaline phosphatase"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Familial bone disorders that clinically and radiographically resemble Paget's disease with more severe, earlier-onset presentation: juvenile Paget's disease (familial idiopathic hyperphosphatasia, TNFRSF11B/osteoprotegerin deletion), familial expansile osteolysis, expansile skeletal hyperphosphatasia, and early-onset Paget's disease (TNFRSF11A/RANK mutations)","Inclusion body myopathy with Paget's disease and frontotemporal dementia (IBMPFD; valosin-containing protein mutation)","Sarcoma, giant cell tumor, or metastatic disease producing focal changes in or near pagetic bone"],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs (typical findings: bone enlargement/expansion, cortical thickening, coarsened trabeculae, lytic and sclerotic changes; skull 'cotton wool'/osteoporosis circumscripta; 'picture frame' or 'ivory' vertebra; pelvic 'brim sign' and protrusio acetabuli; long-bone bowing)","Radionuclide 99mTc bone scan (more sensitive but less specific than radiographs for active lesions)","Serum total alkaline phosphatase (ALP) - first-line for diagnosis and monitoring; bone-specific ALP may be raised when total ALP is normal in single-site disease","Bone formation marker P1NP (reflects disease activity; can be used instead of total ALP)","Bone resorption markers (serum/urine N-telopeptide or C-telopeptide)","Serum calcium and phosphate (normal in Paget's disease)","CT for assessment of possible fracture; MRI to assess possible sarcoma, giant cell tumor, or metastatic disease","Bone biopsy for definitive diagnosis of suspected malignancy","Note: serum osteocalcin is NOT always elevated and is NOT recommended for diagnosis or management"],"redFlags":["New pain in a long-standing pagetic lesion - suggests sarcomatous (most often osteosarcoma) transformation, although neoplasms arising from pagetic bone are rare (<0.5%)","High-output cardiac state and cardiac enlargement from extensive arteriovenous shunting when large portions (15-35%) of the skeleton are involved with high disease activity (e.g., ALP four times above normal); overt high-output heart failure is relatively rare and usually requires concomitant cardiac pathology","Platybasia (softening of the skull base) with risk of brainstem compression; spinal cord compression from bone enlargement or vascular steal syndrome","Fractures, typically in long bones at areas of active or advancing lytic lesions (common at femoral shaft and subtrochanteric regions)","Discovery of hypercalcemia (even with immobilization) should prompt a search for another cause of hypercalcemia"],"prognosis":"Most patients are asymptomatic and have a benign course; for unclear reasons the prevalence and severity are decreasing and the age of diagnosis is increasing. Effective potent antiresorptive (bisphosphonate) therapy can normalize bone turnover and is associated with an apparently decreasing incidence of sarcoma. Serious complications - fractures, deformity, secondary osteoarthritis, neurologic compromise (hearing loss, cranial nerve palsies, cord/brainstem compression), high-output cardiac state, and rare (<0.5%) malignant transformation (usually osteosarcoma) - drive morbidity. Whether early therapy prevents late complications remains to be determined; deformities may take many years to manifest (p.3312-3314).","pitfalls":["Assuming an isolated raised ALP is benign - it can be the only clue to asymptomatic Paget's disease and warrants workup (radiograph plus bone scan).","A symptomatic patient with progression at a single site may have a normal total ALP but an increased bone-specific ALP.","Using serum osteocalcin to diagnose or monitor disease - it is not always elevated and is not recommended.","Attributing hypercalcemia to Paget's disease/immobilization - normal calcium and phosphate are expected, so hypercalcemia should prompt a search for another cause.","Starting bisphosphonate therapy without ensuring adequate calcium and vitamin D intake first - very active bone formation with rapid resorption suppression can precipitate hypocalcemia or mild secondary hyperparathyroidism."],"management":["Normalise the accelerated bone turnover with a potent bisphosphonate, intravenous zoledronic acid being first-line, or an oral aminobisphosphonate such as risedronate","Ensure calcium and vitamin D repletion before intravenous bisphosphonate to avoid hypocalcaemia","Control bone pain with analgesia, recognising that much pain also responds to antiresorptive therapy","Monitor disease activity and treatment response with total or bone-specific alkaline phosphatase, or P1NP","Involve orthopaedics for pathological fracture, progressive deformity, secondary osteoarthritis needing joint replacement, or impending long-bone bowing","Surveil for complications including hearing loss and cranial neuropathies from skull involvement, spinal stenosis, and rare sarcomatous transformation signalled by new pain or a mass","Manage within a metabolic bone service with audiology and neurosurgical input as complications dictate"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal staging system. Disease activity is gauged by the extent of skeletal involvement and the degree of bone-turnover marker elevation (notably total ALP, with ALP roughly four times above normal cited as marking high activity and cardiovascular risk when 15-35% of the skeleton is involved). Pathologically the disease evolves through three phases that may coexist: an osteolytic phase, an active mixed (woven-bone) phase, and a final sclerotic 'burned-out' phase (p.3313-3314).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3311-3314"],"crossLinks":[]},"pagets_disease_of_bone":{"name":"pagets disease of bone","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["osteitis deformans","Paget disease of bone"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oxford Textbook of Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Paget's disease is a focal disorder of accelerated, disorganised bone remodelling, with greatly increased osteoclastic resorption followed by disordered osteoblastic new bone that is expanded but structurally weak.","It is frequently asymptomatic and found incidentally through a raised alkaline phosphatase or an abnormal radiograph, most often affecting the pelvis, femur, spine, skull and tibia.","Symptoms include bone pain, deformity (bowing, skull enlargement, increasing hat size), pathological fracture, secondary osteoarthritis and warmth over affected bone.","Biochemistry typically shows a markedly raised total and bone-specific alkaline phosphatase with normal calcium and phosphate and normal other liver enzymes.","Radiographs show cortical thickening, coarse trabeculation, mixed lytic and sclerotic areas (osteoporosis circumscripta in the skull) and bone expansion; an isotope bone scan best defines disease extent.","Complications include deafness from otic capsule involvement, cranial nerve compression, spinal stenosis, rarely high-output cardiac failure in extensive disease, and osteosarcoma.","Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates, typically a single potent intravenous infusion, are the mainstay and produce prolonged biochemical remission.","Treatment is directed at symptoms, complications or planned surgery at an affected site, and response is monitored using alkaline phosphatase."],"pathophysiology":"The primary abnormality is a marked increase in the number, size and activity of osteoclasts, which show characteristic nuclear inclusions; genetic factors (notably SQSTM1 mutations) and possible environmental triggers contribute. Intense focal resorption is followed by exuberant but disorganised osteoblastic new bone formation, producing a chaotic mosaic of woven and lamellar bone that is hypervascular, enlarged and mechanically weak. This accounts for deformity, fracture, high blood flow with local warmth, and the greatly elevated bone-turnover markers. Disease usually remains confined to the initially affected sites rather than spreading to new bones.","additionalDifferentials":["osteoblastic metastases (prostate or breast)","primary bone tumour including osteosarcoma","fibrous dysplasia","osteomalacia or hyperparathyroidism","multiple myeloma","sclerosing bone dysplasias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["total and bone-specific alkaline phosphatase","serum calcium, phosphate and liver function tests","plain radiographs of affected regions","radionuclide bone scan to define extent","CT or MRI if malignancy or nerve compression is suspected","25-hydroxyvitamin D (corrected before intravenous bisphosphonate)","audiometry where the skull is involved"],"redFlags":["new severe or unremitting pain or a soft-tissue mass suggesting sarcomatous transformation","pathological fracture","neurological deficit from cord or nerve compression","rapidly progressive deformity","high-output cardiac failure in extensive disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally good, with bisphosphonates giving durable biochemical remission and symptom control; sarcomatous transformation is rare (under one per cent) but carries a poor outcome.","pitfalls":["attributing an isolated raised alkaline phosphatase to liver disease","missing sarcomatous change when the character of pain alters","failing to correct vitamin D deficiency before intravenous bisphosphonate, risking hypocalcaemia","over-treating asymptomatic disease without complications","confusing sclerotic Paget's disease with osteoblastic metastases"],"management":["Give a nitrogen-containing bisphosphonate, intravenous zoledronic acid preferred for durable biochemical remission or oral risedronate, to suppress the overactive osteoclastic resorption","Correct vitamin D deficiency and ensure adequate calcium intake before administering intravenous bisphosphonate","Treat bone pain and secondary osteoarthritis with analgesia and physiotherapy, and offer walking aids or orthotics for deformity and gait disturbance","Follow total and bone-specific alkaline phosphatase to gauge disease activity and response, retreating on biochemical relapse","Refer to orthopaedic surgery for fracture, severe deformity, joint replacement, or nerve or spinal-cord compression","Monitor for complications including deafness from otic capsule involvement, high-output cardiac failure in extensive disease, and the rare but serious osteosarcoma","Arrange audiology assessment for skull disease and manage within a specialist metabolic bone clinic"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Oxford Textbook of Medicine","Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Kumar and Clark's Clinical Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pai1_deficiency":{"name":"Plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 deficiency","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAI-1 deficiency","SERPINE1 deficiency","plasminogen activator inhibitor type 1 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare inherited hyperfibrinolytic bleeding disorder caused by deficiency of plasminogen activator inhibitor-1, the main inhibitor of tissue and urokinase plasminogen activators","Bleeding is typically delayed and provoked by trauma, surgery, or menstruation, rather than spontaneous, reflecting premature clot breakdown","Characteristic problems include joint and soft-tissue bleeding after injury, heavy menstrual bleeding, and intracranial or intra-abdominal bleeding after trauma","A notable cluster was described in an Old Order Amish kindred with a homozygous frameshift SERPINE1 variant causing complete deficiency","Routine coagulation tests are usually normal, and diagnosis requires specific measurement of low PAI-1 activity and antigen","Accelerated clot lysis can be demonstrated on a euglobulin clot lysis time or viscoelastic testing","Antifibrinolytic therapy is the principal treatment and is used to cover surgery, trauma, and menorrhagia"],"pathophysiology":"Plasminogen activator inhibitor-1, encoded by SERPINE1, is the primary serpin that restrains tissue-type and urokinase-type plasminogen activators, thereby limiting plasmin generation and fibrinolysis. In its deficiency, plasminogen activation proceeds unchecked, so fibrin clots are lysed prematurely and haemostatic plugs are not maintained. Because clot formation itself is normal, bleeding is characteristically delayed and follows a haemostatic challenge. The phenotype is generally milder and more provocation-dependent than in classic coagulation factor deficiencies.","additionalDifferentials":["Alpha-2-antiplasmin deficiency","Factor XIII deficiency","Von Willebrand disease","Mild haemophilia","Platelet function disorders","Acquired hyperfibrinolysis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PAI-1 activity assay and antigen level to demonstrate the deficiency","Euglobulin clot lysis time or viscoelastic testing to show accelerated fibrinolysis","Standard coagulation screen, which is characteristically normal","Alpha-2-antiplasmin activity to distinguish the closely related hyperfibrinolytic disorder","SERPINE1 gene analysis to confirm a congenital deficiency","Assessment for an acquired cause of enhanced fibrinolysis when relevant"],"redFlags":["Intracranial haemorrhage following relatively minor head trauma","Severe post-surgical or post-traumatic bleeding","Heavy menstrual bleeding causing anaemia","Bleeding disproportionate to a normal routine coagulation screen"],"prognosis":"The disorder is usually mild and provocation-dependent, and antifibrinolytic cover around haemostatic challenges generally gives excellent outcomes. Serious bleeding is uncommon in the absence of significant trauma or surgery.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a bleeding history because routine coagulation tests are normal","Failing to request specific PAI-1 activity testing","Confusing it with alpha-2-antiplasmin or factor XIII deficiency","Not providing antifibrinolytic cover for surgery or menorrhagia"],"management":["Antifibrinolytic therapy with tranexamic acid or aminocaproic acid as prophylactic cover before and after surgery, dental procedures and other haemostatic challenges, the mainstay of treatment","Prompt antifibrinolytic treatment of acute bleeding episodes alongside local haemostatic measures","Hormonal control of heavy menstrual bleeding with a combined oral contraceptive or progestogen in addition to antifibrinolytics","Avoidance of unnecessary antiplatelet and anticoagulant drugs that further impair haemostasis","Pre-procedural planning with a haematologist and a written bleeding-management plan for trauma or emergency surgery","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive SERPINE1 inheritance","Escalate urgently to haematology for serious bleeding such as intracranial or intra-abdominal haemorrhage"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Hoffman: Hematology - Basic Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"painful_legs_moving_toes":{"name":"Painful legs and moving toes syndrome","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["painful legs moving toes","PLMT"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / movement disorder literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A syndrome of deep, poorly localised pain in the leg accompanied by continuous involuntary writhing or flexion-extension movements of the toes or foot","The pain usually dominates the clinical picture and the movements may be only partially suppressible","It often follows injury to peripheral nerves, roots or the cauda equina, though many cases are idiopathic","An analogous painful arms and moving fingers variant is recognised in the upper limb","The movements persist during rest and may reduce but not always disappear in sleep","It is frequently refractory, and management focuses on neuropathic pain control with variable success"],"pathophysiology":"Painful legs and moving toes syndrome is thought to result from aberrant reorganisation of sensory input after peripheral nerve, root or plexus injury, generating abnormal central processing that produces both neuropathic pain and disordered motor output to the toes. The exact generator is uncertain and may involve spinal and supraspinal reorganisation, which is why the movements can be difficult to localise or suppress. In idiopathic cases no antecedent lesion is identified despite a similar clinical picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Restless legs syndrome","Peripheral neuropathy with neuropathic pain","Complex regional pain syndrome","Dystonia or myoclonus of the foot","Functional movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the combination of leg pain and continuous toe movements","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to identify peripheral nerve or root injury","Imaging of the lumbosacral spine or plexus where a lesion is suspected","Assessment of neuropathic pain features","Exclusion of restless legs syndrome by history"],"redFlags":["Progressive weakness or sensory loss suggesting an evolving lesion","New cauda equina or radicular signs","Severe intractable pain","Rapidly progressive or asymmetric neurological features"],"prognosis":"The syndrome tends to be chronic and often treatment-resistant, with the pain being the most disabling component; some patients gain partial relief from neuropathic pain treatments.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with restless legs syndrome, which has an urge to move relieved by movement","Overlooking a treatable peripheral nerve or root lesion","Focusing on the movements rather than the dominant pain","Expecting complete resolution and under-supporting the patient"],"management":["Neuropathic pain control with a gabapentinoid such as gabapentin or pregabalin and a tricyclic or SNRI antidepressant such as amitriptyline or duloxetine","Identify and treat any underlying peripheral nerve, root or cauda equina lesion","Botulinum toxin injection to the affected muscles to reduce the involuntary toe movements, with trials of baclofen or a benzodiazepine for the movement component","Adjunctive sodium-channel blockers such as carbamazepine and consideration of transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation or sympathetic and peripheral nerve blocks for refractory pain","Spinal cord stimulation in selected patients with intractable pain","Physiotherapy and multidisciplinary pain-clinic input, addressing comorbid sleep disturbance and low mood, with realistic expectations given the treatment-resistant course"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Movement Disorders textbook (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"palindromic_rheumatism":{"name":"Palindromic rheumatism","system":"Rheumatology / Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["palindromic rheumatoid arthritis","recurrent palindromic arthritis","Hench-Rosenberg syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by sudden, recurrent, self-limiting attacks of arthritis and periarthritis lasting hours to a few days with complete resolution between episodes","Attacks typically affect one or a few joints at a time, most often the fingers, wrists and knees, sometimes with periarticular soft-tissue swelling","Crucially, there is no permanent joint damage or radiographic erosion despite recurrent flares","A significant proportion of patients, particularly those seropositive for rheumatoid factor or anti-CCP antibodies, progress to established rheumatoid arthritis","Anti-CCP and rheumatoid factor positivity are the strongest predictors of evolution to persistent inflammatory arthritis","Diagnosis rests on the characteristic history since patients are often asymptomatic with normal examination and imaging when reviewed between attacks","Colchicine, hydroxychloroquine and NSAIDs are used to reduce attack frequency, and DMARDs are considered when progression to rheumatoid arthritis is likely"],"pathophysiology":"Palindromic rheumatism is an episodic inflammatory arthropathy in which transient synovial and periarticular inflammation produces recurrent short-lived attacks without cumulative joint destruction. It is widely regarded as an abortive or early form of rheumatoid arthritis, and autoantibodies such as rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies are frequently present, reflecting shared autoimmune mechanisms. During attacks there is neutrophil-rich synovial inflammation that resolves without pannus formation or erosion, but persistent autoimmunity in seropositive individuals can eventually give way to sustained synovitis and conversion to established rheumatoid arthritis or, less often, other connective tissue disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Early rheumatoid arthritis","Crystal arthropathy (gout, calcium pyrophosphate disease)","Reactive arthritis and spondyloarthropathy","Familial Mediterranean fever and other autoinflammatory syndromes","Systemic lupus erythematosus with intermittent arthritis","Whipple disease arthropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies to stratify the risk of progression to rheumatoid arthritis","Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP), often raised during attacks and normal between them","Joint radiographs, characteristically normal without erosions","Ultrasound during an attack to confirm synovitis and periarticular inflammation","Serum urate and joint aspiration to exclude crystal arthropathy when relevant"],"redFlags":["Development of persistent rather than self-limiting joint swelling suggesting evolution to rheumatoid arthritis","Emergence of radiographic erosions indicating a destructive arthropathy","Systemic features (rash, serositis, cytopenias) pointing to lupus or another connective tissue disease","Fever with monoarthritis where septic or crystal arthritis must be excluded"],"prognosis":"Between attacks patients are usually asymptomatic with no lasting joint damage, but a substantial proportion, especially those with positive autoantibodies, progress to rheumatoid arthritis or another chronic inflammatory disease over time.","pitfalls":["Dismissing the diagnosis because examination and imaging are normal between attacks","Failing to check autoantibodies and therefore missing patients at high risk of progression to rheumatoid arthritis","Confusing recurrent attacks with gout without joint aspiration or urate measurement","Delaying DMARD consideration in seropositive patients with frequent flares"],"management":["Abort acute attacks with NSAIDs and, where needed, short courses of corticosteroids","Consider hydroxychloroquine to reduce attack frequency and possibly lower the risk of progression to rheumatoid arthritis, particularly in seropositive patients","Risk-stratify with rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies to identify those likely to progress","Introduce a conventional synthetic DMARD such as methotrexate if attacks become frequent or persistent synovitis develops","Regular rheumatology review to detect early conversion to rheumatoid arthritis or another connective tissue disease","Patient education on the episodic nature and prompt reporting of persistent joint swelling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Oxford Textbook of Rheumatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pallister_hall_syndrome":{"name":"Pallister-Hall syndrome (GLI3)","system":"Genetics / Ciliopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PHS","GLI3","hypothalamic hamartoma-polydactyly syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the combination of a hypothalamic hamartoma with central (mesoaxial) or postaxial polydactyly","Hypothalamic hamartomas may cause gelastic (laughing) seizures, central precocious puberty or hypopituitarism","A bifid or partially cleft epiglottis is a highly suggestive, if under-recognised, feature","Other findings include imperforate anus, renal anomalies and, occasionally, laryngotracheal clefts","Caused by heterozygous truncating GLI3 variants, typically in the middle third of the gene, with autosomal dominant inheritance","Allelic with Greig cephalopolysyndactyly, which results from a different position or class of GLI3 variant","GLI3 acts downstream of hedgehog signalling processed at the primary cilium, linking the disorder to ciliary biology"],"pathophysiology":"Pallister-Hall syndrome results from heterozygous truncating variants in GLI3, typically in the middle third of the gene, that generate a shortened protein biased towards its transcriptional repressor form. GLI3 is the principal transcriptional effector of hedgehog signalling, a pathway that is assembled and processed on the primary cilium, so the disorder is intimately linked to ciliary biology. During development the resulting imbalance of hedgehog output disturbs patterning of the hypothalamus, limbs, larynx and hindgut, producing the hypothalamic hamartoma, central or postaxial polydactyly, bifid epiglottis and anorectal and renal anomalies. The contrasting effect of GLI3 loss-of-function variants elsewhere in the gene explains why Greig cephalopolysyndactyly is allelic yet clinically distinct.","additionalDifferentials":["Greig cephalopolysyndactyly syndrome","McKusick-Kaufman syndrome","Bardet-Biedl syndrome","Oral-facial-digital syndromes","Isolated hypothalamic hamartoma","Other central (mesoaxial) polydactyly syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to identify and characterise a hypothalamic hamartoma","Endocrine evaluation for precocious puberty and anterior pituitary function","Radiographs of the hands and feet to document central or postaxial polydactyly","Laryngoscopy for bifid epiglottis and airway assessment where indicated","Examination and imaging for imperforate anus and renal anomalies","Molecular testing of GLI3"],"redFlags":["Gelastic seizures or intractable epilepsy arising from the hamartoma","Panhypopituitarism, which can be life-threatening in the neonate through adrenal insufficiency","Airway compromise from laryngeal anomalies","Imperforate anus requiring surgical management"],"prognosis":"With recognition and management of endocrine and airway problems the outlook is generally good and intellect is usually normal; the hypothalamic hamartoma is benign and malformative, though associated seizures can be difficult to control.","pitfalls":["Missing neonatal hypopituitarism and adrenal insufficiency, which can be fatal","Attempting resection of a hypothalamic hamartoma without recognising its benign, malformative nature","Overlooking a bifid epiglottis on airway assessment","Confusing it with Greig cephalopolysyndactyly despite the shared GLI3 basis"],"management":["Anti-seizure medication for gelastic and other seizures arising from the hypothalamic hamartoma, with stereotactic laser ablation or surgical disconnection for refractory epilepsy","Endocrine hormone replacement for hypopituitarism and a GnRH analogue to manage central precocious puberty","Airway assessment and surgical repair of a bifid epiglottis or laryngotracheal cleft where symptomatic","Surgical correction of imperforate anus and evaluation and management of renal anomalies","Orthopaedic or plastic surgical review for central or postaxial polydactyly","Genetic counselling for autosomal dominant GLI3 inheritance and family testing","Coordinated multidisciplinary care across neurology, endocrinology, ENT, surgery and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: GLI3-Related Pallister-Hall Syndrome","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"pallister_killian_syndrome":{"name":"Pallister-Killian syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PKS","tetrasomy 12p mosaicism","isochromosome 12p syndrome","Pallister mosaic syndrome","Killian-Teschler-Nicola syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by mosaic tetrasomy of the short arm of chromosome 12, usually from a supernumerary isochromosome 12p present in only a proportion of cells","A characteristic pattern of pigmentary skin anomalies (streaks of hyper- and hypopigmentation) and localised areas of alopecia, along with a coarse face, may develop over time","Facial features include a high forehead with frontotemporal balding, sparse eyebrows and eyelashes, hypertelorism, a short nose with anteverted nares and a long philtrum","Profound hypotonia, severe intellectual disability and epilepsy are typical","Diaphragmatic hernia, congenital heart disease and other malformations are recognised associations","Because the isochromosome is often absent from blood lymphocytes with age, skin fibroblast analysis or buccal sampling is the preferred diagnostic tissue","Recurrence risk is generally low as the abnormality is sporadic and mosaic"],"pathophysiology":"Pallister-Killian syndrome results from mosaic tetrasomy 12p, most often due to a supernumerary isochromosome composed of two copies of the short arm of chromosome 12, giving four copies of 12p in affected cells. The extra genetic material causes an imbalance of dosage-sensitive genes during development. Because the isochromosome is unstable and tends to be lost from rapidly dividing tissues such as blood, the mosaicism is often confined to fibroblasts and other tissues, which accounts for the diagnostic difficulty and the tissue-dependent detection.","additionalDifferentials":["Fryns syndrome","Killian-Pallister mimics with pigmentary mosaicism","Ullrich-Turner and other chromosomal aneuploidies","Cornelia de Lange syndrome","Simpson-Golabi-Behmel syndrome","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia of other genetic cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal analysis or microarray on skin fibroblasts or buccal cells rather than blood, to detect the mosaic isochromosome","FISH for 12p to confirm tetrasomy and estimate the mosaic fraction","Prenatal detection via amniocentesis or chorionic villus sampling when suspected antenatally","Echocardiography for congenital heart disease","Imaging to detect diaphragmatic hernia and other structural anomalies","Electroencephalography and neuroimaging for seizures"],"redFlags":["Respiratory distress at birth from congenital diaphragmatic hernia","Undiagnosed congenital heart disease causing cardiorespiratory compromise","Refractory seizures","Severe feeding difficulty with aspiration and failure to thrive"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable and correlates with the tissue distribution of the mosaicism; many affected children have profound intellectual disability and significant medical needs, and severe cases with diaphragmatic hernia have high early mortality. Those surviving infancy generally live into childhood or beyond with major disability.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis on a normal blood karyotype because the isochromosome is lost from lymphocytes","Failing to test fibroblasts or buccal cells, the tissues most likely to reveal the mosaic","Attributing the pigmentary changes to an unrelated skin disorder","Underestimating the risk of associated diaphragmatic hernia and cardiac anomalies"],"management":["Neonatal surgical repair of congenital diaphragmatic hernia and other structural anomalies, with respiratory support","Anti-seizure medication for epilepsy guided by EEG","Management of profound hypotonia and feeding difficulty with physiotherapy, feeding support and gastrostomy where needed","Cardiology review and treatment of congenital heart disease","Developmental support, communication aids and specialist education for severe intellectual disability","Ophthalmology, audiology and dermatology surveillance for associated anomalies","Genetic counselling with confirmatory testing on skin fibroblasts or buccal cells, as the mosaic isochromosome is often absent from blood","Multidisciplinary coordination and family support given the significant medical needs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","OMIM / GeneReviews: Pallister-Killian syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"palmoplantar_keratoderma_sclerodactyly":{"name":"Huriez syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scleroatrophic and keratotic dermatosis of the limbs","sclerotylosis","palmoplantar keratoderma with scleroatrophy","SMARCAD1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal dominant genodermatosis combining a diffuse palmoplantar keratoderma with scleroatrophy of the hands and feet and nail abnormalities","Caused by variants affecting SMARCAD1, in particular disruption of a skin-specific promoter of this chromatin-remodelling gene","The distal limbs show sclerodactyly with atrophic, shiny skin and hypoplastic or absent nails","The hallmark and most serious feature is a markedly increased risk of aggressive squamous cell carcinoma arising within the keratotic, scleroatrophic skin, typically in early to middle adult life","Hypohidrosis and a fixed, tight appearance of the acral skin are characteristic","Diagnosis is clinical with family history, supported by biopsy and molecular testing; any non-healing or changing lesion warrants biopsy","Management combines supportive keratolytic and emollient skin care with lifelong surveillance for cutaneous malignancy"],"pathophysiology":"Huriez syndrome is associated with variants affecting SMARCAD1, a chromatin-remodelling enzyme, particularly a mutation in a skin-specific promoter that reduces its expression in the epidermis. Impaired chromatin remodelling disturbs keratinocyte proliferation, differentiation and genomic stability, producing the diffuse palmoplantar keratoderma and the scleroatrophic changes of the distal limbs with nail hypoplasia. The same disturbance predisposes the affected skin to malignant transformation, accounting for the characteristically early and aggressive squamous cell carcinomas that dominate the prognosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic sclerosis with sclerodactyly","Other diffuse hereditary palmoplantar keratodermas","Mal de Meleda","Dyskeratosis congenita","Epidermolysis bullosa with acral scarring","Acrogeria and other acral atrophic syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy of suspicious or non-healing lesions to detect squamous cell carcinoma","Molecular genetic testing of SMARCAD1","Regular full skin and acral examination for malignant change","Nail and hand/foot functional assessment","Family history and pedigree analysis","Imaging and lymph node assessment if invasive carcinoma is confirmed"],"redFlags":["Any non-healing ulcer, nodule or changing lesion within scleroatrophic skin, given the high squamous carcinoma risk","Regional lymphadenopathy suggesting metastatic squamous cell carcinoma","Rapidly enlarging keratotic lesion","Recurrent secondary infection of atrophic acral skin"],"prognosis":"Skin and nail changes are chronic but stable; prognosis is dominated by the lifelong risk of aggressive squamous cell carcinoma, which can metastasise and be fatal, making early detection through surveillance essential.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the sclerodactyly for systemic sclerosis and missing the genodermatosis and its cancer risk","Failing to biopsy non-healing lesions in scleroatrophic skin","Neglecting long-term skin cancer surveillance","Underestimating the aggressiveness of squamous cell carcinoma in this setting"],"management":["Lifelong skin surveillance with regular full-skin and acral examination and a low threshold to biopsy any non-healing or changing lesion for squamous cell carcinoma","Prompt wide surgical excision of squamous cell carcinoma with lymph node assessment and imaging for metastatic spread","Topical emollients and keratolytics such as urea and salicylic acid, with oral retinoids for severe palmoplantar keratoderma","Sun protection and avoidance of cutaneous carcinogens to limit further malignant risk","Hand and foot functional support and wound care for the scleroatrophic skin and nail changes","Genetic counselling for autosomal dominant SMARCAD1 inheritance and family screening","Multidisciplinary care with dermatology, surgical oncology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"palmoplantar_pustulosis":{"name":"Palmoplantar pustulosis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPP","pustulosis palmaris et plantaris","palmoplantar pustular psoriasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic, relapsing eruption of sterile pustules on the palms and soles that evolve through yellow pustules to brown macules and scaling on an erythematous, hyperkeratotic base","Lesions at different stages coexist, giving a characteristic mixed appearance of fresh pustules alongside older brown spots","Regarded by many as a localised variant of pustular psoriasis, though it has distinct associations and often occurs without typical plaque psoriasis","Very strongly associated with cigarette smoking, current or former, which is far more prevalent than in the general population, and it predominantly affects middle-aged women","Associated with thyroid disease and, within the SAPHO syndrome, with sterile osteoarticular inflammation, notably of the anterior chest wall","The pustules are sterile and culture-negative, an important distinction from infection","Often disabling because of fissuring, pain, and interference with walking and manual work, and it tends to resist treatment","Management includes smoking cessation, potent topical corticosteroids (often under occlusion), topical retinoids and vitamin D analogues, phototherapy, and systemic agents such as retinoids, methotrexate, ciclosporin, and biologics in severe disease"],"pathophysiology":"Palmoplantar pustulosis is an inflammatory disorder centred on the acrosyringium, the intra-epidermal portion of the sweat duct, of palmar and plantar skin, where accumulation of neutrophils forms the characteristic sterile pustules. It is thought to represent an autoinflammatory process with prominent innate immune involvement, including neutrophils and the interleukin-17 and interleukin-36 pathways. Nicotine and its effects on acrosyringeal sweat glands and immune signalling are strongly implicated, which fits the striking association with smoking, and the frequent overlap with psoriasis suggests shared inflammatory mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Pompholyx (dyshidrotic eczema)","Tinea manuum or pedis with secondary pustulation","Acute palmoplantar contact dermatitis","Infantile acropustulosis","Acrodermatitis continua of Hallopeau","Secondary syphilis (palmoplantar involvement)","Bacterial pustular infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pustule swab and culture confirming sterility and excluding bacterial or fungal infection","Skin scrapings with potassium hydroxide microscopy and fungal culture to exclude tinea","Skin biopsy showing intra-epidermal neutrophilic (spongiform) pustules","Smoking history, given the strong association, with counselling on cessation","Thyroid function tests in view of the association with thyroid disease","Imaging (bone scan or MRI) and inflammatory markers if SAPHO syndrome or osteoarticular pain is suspected"],"redFlags":["Bone or joint pain, especially of the anterior chest wall, suggesting SAPHO syndrome","Rapid generalisation with systemic upset raising concern for generalised pustular psoriasis","Severe fissuring with secondary bacterial infection","Disabling disease impairing walking or occupation and quality of life"],"prognosis":"Chronic and relapsing over years and often refractory to treatment; smoking cessation and appropriate therapy improve control, but complete and lasting clearance is difficult to achieve.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating sterile pustulosis as a bacterial or fungal infection","Not addressing smoking, the strongest modifiable association","Missing SAPHO-related osteoarticular disease","Underestimating the functional disability and its psychosocial impact"],"management":["Smoking cessation as a key modifiable factor, with structured support","Potent topical corticosteroids, often under occlusion, combined with a topical vitamin D analogue and keratolytics as first-line therapy","Localised phototherapy such as hand-and-foot PUVA for resistant disease","Systemic agents for refractory cases including acitretin, methotrexate, ciclosporin or apremilast","Biologic therapy targeting the interleukin-17, interleukin-23 or interleukin-36 pathways in severe treatment-resistant disease","Screen for and manage associated thyroid disease","Assess for SAPHO syndrome with anterior chest wall pain and refer for rheumatological management if present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"panayiotopoulos_syndrome":{"name":"Panayiotopoulos syndrome","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Early-onset benign childhood occipital epilepsy","Self-limited epilepsy with autonomic seizures"],"chapter":null,"source":"ILAE classification and clinical guidance on the epilepsy syndromes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common self-limited focal epilepsy of early childhood dominated by autonomic seizures, especially emesis.","Onset is typically 3 to 6 years; seizures are infrequent but often prolonged, amounting to autonomic status epilepticus.","A typical event comprises nausea, retching and vomiting with pallor, eye deviation and impaired responsiveness, frequently arising from sleep and sometimes progressing to a hemiconvulsion or generalised convulsion.","Additional autonomic features include pupillary changes, incontinence, hypersalivation and cyanosis.","Interictal EEG classically shows multifocal, shifting spikes, often occipital, but the distribution is variable and the EEG may be normal.","Neurological examination, development and neuroimaging are normal.","Because seizures are infrequent, long-term daily medication is often unnecessary and a rescue plan for prolonged seizures is central to management.","Prognosis is excellent, with spontaneous remission within a year or two of onset."],"pathophysiology":"Panayiotopoulos syndrome is regarded as a benign, maturation-dependent focal epilepsy reflecting a diffuse, age-related cortical hyperexcitability with a predilection for autonomic networks. The prominent emetic and autonomic features are attributed to epileptic activation of central autonomic networks, particularly insular and anterior regions, while the shifting, often posterior EEG foci indicate widespread rather than fixed epileptogenicity that resolves with brain maturation.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastroenteritis or cyclical vomiting syndrome","Migraine and its variants","Syncope","Encephalitis, considered during prolonged autonomic status","Late-onset (Gastaut) occipital epilepsy","Self-limited epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes","Structural or symptomatic occipital epilepsy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["EEG including a sleep recording","MRI brain if features are atypical or the examination is abnormal","Blood glucose and basic metabolic screen during an acute event","ECG to exclude cardiac syncope or arrhythmia","Lumbar puncture if encephalitis is suspected during prolonged status","Developmental assessment"],"redFlags":["Autonomic status epilepticus with a seizure lasting beyond thirty minutes","Focal neurological deficit or abnormal development","An atypical or fixed occipital lesion on imaging","Recurrent seizures deviating from the expected benign course"],"prognosis":"Excellent: the disorder is self-limited with remission usually within one to two years of onset and normal long-term cognitive and neurological outcome, although a minority later develop other seizure types.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing prolonged autonomic seizures as encephalitis, gastroenteritis or syncope.","Over-treating an essentially benign, infrequent-seizure condition with long-term medication.","Failing to provide a rescue plan for prolonged seizures.","Confusing it with late-onset (Gastaut) occipital epilepsy, which causes visual seizures and follows a different course."],"management":["Reassurance and education, since the syndrome is self-limited and many children do not require regular anti-seizure medication","Rescue benzodiazepine such as buccal midazolam or rectal diazepam for prolonged seizures or autonomic status epilepticus","Maintenance anti-seizure medication such as carbamazepine, levetiracetam or valproate only if seizures are frequent or distressing","First-aid guidance for parents on the recovery position and timing of seizures","Distinguish acute events from cardiac and metabolic causes and treat accordingly during episodes","Developmental and neurological follow-up with the expectation of remission within a couple of years"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ILAE position papers on classification of the epilepsies","Panayiotopoulos CP. A Clinical Guide to Epileptic Syndromes and their Treatment","Standard textbook of paediatric neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pancreas_divisum":{"name":"Pancreas divisum","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dorsal duct dominance","dominant dorsal duct anomaly"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common congenital pancreatic ductal variant, in which the dorsal and ventral duct systems fail to fuse","Most of the gland drains through the smaller dorsal duct and minor papilla, with only the ventral duct draining via the major papilla","The great majority of people with pancreas divisum are asymptomatic and it is often an incidental finding","Its causal role in idiopathic recurrent acute pancreatitis is debated, with the prevailing view that it contributes only when combined with additional factors such as a stenotic minor papilla or genetic predisposition","MRCP, ideally with secretin stimulation, is the preferred non-invasive test to demonstrate the separate dorsal duct crossing the bile duct","When intervention is considered in symptomatic recurrent pancreatitis, endoscopic minor papilla sphincterotomy or stenting is the usual approach, with variable and unpredictable benefit","Genetic testing (for example CFTR and SPINK1 variants) is worth considering in young patients with recurrent unexplained pancreatitis"],"pathophysiology":"Pancreas divisum results from failure of the embryonic dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts to fuse, so that the bulk of exocrine secretion from the dorsal anlage drains through the accessory (Santorini) duct and the minor papilla, while only the small ventral segment drains through the main (Wirsung) duct and the major papilla. The proposed mechanism for symptoms is relative outflow obstruction: the minor papilla may be too small to accommodate the secretory volume of the dorsal duct, producing raised intraductal pressure, ductal distension, and recurrent pancreatitis in a susceptible minority. Because most individuals with this anatomy never develop symptoms, additional factors such as minor papilla stenosis or genetic susceptibility are thought necessary for clinical disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic recurrent acute pancreatitis","Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction","Microlithiasis or occult biliary stones","Hereditary or genetic pancreatitis (CFTR, SPINK1, PRSS1)","Alcohol-related pancreatitis","Pancreatic ductal stricture or small neoplasm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRCP, preferably secretin-enhanced, to demonstrate the dominant dorsal duct and non-union","Endoscopic ultrasound to assess the ducts, parenchyma, and minor papilla","ERCP for definitive ductal anatomy when intervention is planned","Genetic testing for pancreatitis-associated variants in young or recurrent cases","Serum amylase and lipase during episodes to confirm pancreatitis","Fasting triglycerides and calcium to exclude metabolic causes"],"redFlags":["Frequent or severe recurrent attacks of acute pancreatitis","Progression to features of chronic pancreatitis such as ductal changes and exocrine insufficiency","A focal ductal stricture or mass suggesting neoplasm rather than a simple anatomical variant","Unexplained weight loss or steatorrhoea"],"prognosis":"As an isolated anatomical variant the outlook is excellent and no treatment is required; in the minority with recurrent pancreatitis, endoscopic therapy of the minor papilla helps some patients but responses are inconsistent, and a subset progress to chronic pancreatitis.","pitfalls":["Assuming the anomaly is the cause of abdominal pain when it is merely an incidental and common finding","Proceeding to endoscopic intervention without objective evidence of recurrent pancreatitis","Overlooking coexisting genetic, metabolic, or biliary causes of recurrent pancreatitis","Underestimating the procedural risk of post-ERCP pancreatitis when intervening on the minor papilla"],"management":["No treatment beyond reassurance for asymptomatic, incidentally discovered pancreas divisum","Standard supportive management of acute pancreatitis episodes with fluids, analgesia and early nutrition","Endoscopic minor papilla sphincterotomy or stenting in selected patients with recurrent acute pancreatitis, acknowledging variable and unpredictable benefit","Investigate and treat co-contributing factors including pancreatitis-associated genetic variants, hypertriglyceridaemia and hypercalcaemia","Alcohol and smoking cessation to reduce the risk of recurrent pancreatitis","Surveillance for progression to chronic pancreatitis with pancreatic enzyme replacement for exocrine insufficiency","Refer to a hepatobiliary or pancreatology centre for decisions on endoscopic intervention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Reviews of ductal anatomy and idiopathic recurrent acute pancreatitis","Guidelines on endoscopic management of pancreatic duct anomalies"],"crossLinks":[]},"pancreatic_cancer":{"name":"Pancreatic Cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pancreatic adenocarcinoma"],"chapter":88,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.671","clinicalPearls":["The classic presentation of cancer arising in the head of the pancreas is 'painless' jaundice; jaundice is visually detectable once bilirubin exceeds ~2 mg/dL and may be accompanied by pruritus from bile salt skin deposition (p.672).","Pain, when present, is typically mid-epigastric and 'boring' with radiation to the back (from retroperitoneal invasion of the splanchnic nerve plexus) and may be worsened by eating or lying flat (p.672).","A palpable, non-tender gallbladder in the setting of biliary obstruction is Courvoisier's sign (p.672).","Migratory superficial thrombophlebitis and combined deep venous plus arterial thromboses (Trousseau's syndrome) can occur; metastatic disease is frequently associated with a hypercoagulable state requiring anticoagulation (p.672-674).","New-onset diabetes (especially with concomitant weight loss) or unexplained worsening of type 2/type 3c diabetes, or rising HbA1c/hyperglycemia in the preceding 1-2 years, may signal underlying pancreatic cancer (p.671-672).","CA19-9 is best drawn after the bilirubin has normalized because biliary obstruction itself elevates CA19-9; CA125 can help in the 8-15% of Lewis-antigen non-secretors who have undetectable CA19-9 (p.674).","Cigarette smoking is the greatest environmental risk factor, accounting for an estimated 25-30% of cases, with risk persisting at least 10 years after cessation (p.671).","Histologic/cytologic tissue diagnosis is essential and also enables molecular testing (next-generation sequencing for KRAS and other alterations) and microsatellite status determination, which increasingly guide therapy (p.674).","Signs of late-stage disease include a left supraclavicular Virchow's node and a periumbilical Sister Mary Joseph's nodule indicating peritoneal metastases (p.672-673)."],"pathophysiology":"Ductal adenocarcinoma is the most common and most lethal exocrine pancreatic neoplasm, characterized by infiltrating desmoplastic stromal reaction and arising in the head, body, or tail. Most cancers arise from pancreatic intraepithelial neoplasia (PanIN, graded 1-3 in a progression model), with genetic alterations accumulating as PanIN grade increases; some arise from noninvasive cystic precursors (e.g., IPMN). The mutational landscape is dominated by four commonly altered genes: KRAS (mutated in 90-95%, primarily codon 12), p16/CDKN2A (inactivated in >90% of invasive tumors), TP53 (~75%), and DPC4/SMAD4 (~50%); BRCA2 is mutated in 3.5-10%. A 'KRAS wildtype' subset (more common in patients under 55) may harbor actionable BRAF mutations, oncogenic fusions (NRG-1, NTRK1-3), or microsatellite instability (p.672).","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic pancreatitis","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor","Acinar cell carcinoma","Colloid carcinoma","Medullary carcinoma","Adenosquamous carcinoma","Intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm (IPMN)","Mucinous cystic neoplasm","Metastasis to the pancreas (renal, breast, lung, urothelial carcinoma, melanoma, lymphoma)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic pancreatitis (can be difficult to distinguish from adenocarcinoma and is itself a risk factor)","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor","Mucinous cystic neoplasm and IPMN","Metastatic disease to the pancreas from other primaries"],"additionalInvestigations":["Dual-phase contrast-enhanced CT using a pancreas-cancer protocol (arterial and portal venous phases) to assess tumor-vessel relationships, resectability, and staging","Abdominal ultrasonography (often first imaging in the jaundiced patient)","MRI","18F-glucose PET to detect occult metastatic disease","Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) with EUS-guided core needle biopsy of pancreatic lesions","Tru-Cut core needle biopsy (preferred over fine-needle aspiration) for histologic diagnosis; percutaneous biopsy for liver/lymph node lesions","Next-generation sequencing for genomic alterations (including KRAS) and microsatellite status","Serum CA19-9 (drawn after bilirubin normalizes), CEA, and CA125","Staging laparoscopy (selectively, before or at definitive surgery)"],"redFlags":["Painless obstructive jaundice (classic for head-of-pancreas tumor)","Palpable non-tender gallbladder with obstruction (Courvoisier's sign)","Unintentional weight loss with loss of muscle mass","New-onset diabetes, especially with concomitant weight loss","Mid-epigastric boring pain radiating to the back","Migratory superficial thrombophlebitis / venous or arterial thrombosis (Trousseau's syndrome)","Left supraclavicular Virchow's node or periumbilical Sister Mary Joseph's nodule (late disease)","Light/clay-colored stools and steatorrhea"],"prognosis":"Pancreatic cancer is the most lethal human cancer, with an overall 5-year survival of about 13% (up from 3% in 1975). SEER estimates 5-year survival of ~44% for localized disease, ~16% for regional disease, and ~3.2% for metastatic disease. Late presentation is common, only about 10-20% of tumors are resectable, and the disease is the third leading cause of cancer mortality in the U.S. and projected to become the second by 2030 (p.671-672).","pitfalls":["Drawing CA19-9 while bilirubin is elevated falsely raises the level, since biliary obstruction itself elevates CA19-9 (p.674).","8-15% of patients are Lewis-antigen non-secretors with undetectable CA19-9, so a normal CA19-9 does not exclude disease; CA125 may help in this group (p.674).","Even high-grade PanIN precursor lesions are not detectable by current imaging, limiting early detection (p.672).","For average-risk individuals with no predisposing factors there is currently no recommended screening (p.671).","Whether diabetes causes or is caused by pancreatic cancer is not clearly established (p.671).","Fine-needle aspiration cytology is acceptable but less preferred than core (Tru-Cut) biopsy, which yields adequate tissue for molecular testing (p.674).","Biliary stenting is not necessarily warranted if upfront surgery is planned, but is required (where medically indicated) before neoadjuvant systemic therapy (p.674)."],"management":["Upfront surgical resection by pancreaticoduodenectomy, distal or total pancreatectomy for anatomically resectable disease, followed by adjuvant chemotherapy","Combination chemotherapy with FOLFIRINOX or gemcitabine plus nab-paclitaxel in the adjuvant, neoadjuvant borderline-resectable and palliative metastatic settings","Molecularly guided therapy including maintenance PARP inhibition with olaparib in BRCA-mutated disease and targeted or immunotherapy for actionable alterations such as BRAF mutation, NTRK or NRG1 fusions and mismatch-repair deficiency","Endoscopic or percutaneous biliary stenting to relieve obstructive jaundice and pruritus","Pancreatic enzyme replacement for exocrine insufficiency and management of pancreatogenic diabetes","Symptom control with opioid analgesia and coeliac plexus neurolysis for tumour-related back pain, and anticoagulation for Trousseau-related thromboembolism","Multidisciplinary team assessment across surgery, oncology, radiology and palliative care with early palliative care involvement","Germline genetic testing and family counselling for hereditary risk such as BRCA2"],"severityClassification":"Staged by the American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) staging system. Clinically also categorized by extent of disease and therapeutic approach: resectable (~10-20%), borderline resectable/locally advanced, and metastatic (p.674).","references":["Harrison 22e p.671","Harrison 22e p.672","Harrison 22e p.673","Harrison 22e p.674"],"crossLinks":["pancreatitis"]},"pancreatic_panniculitis":{"name":"Pancreatic panniculitis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["enzymatic panniculitis","pancreatic fat necrosis","subcutaneous fat necrosis of pancreatic disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as tender, erythematous to violaceous subcutaneous nodules, classically on the lower legs but also thighs, buttocks and trunk","Nodules may soften, ulcerate and discharge an oily brown viscous material as liquefied fat is released","It signals underlying pancreatic disease, most often acute or chronic pancreatitis but also pancreatic carcinoma (especially acinar cell type)","Skin lesions can precede recognition of the pancreatic disorder, so unexplained leg nodules warrant pancreatic evaluation","The triad of panniculitis, polyarthritis and eosinophilia (PPP syndrome) carries a worse prognosis","Biopsy shows a lobular panniculitis with pathognomonic anucleate 'ghost' adipocytes and basophilic saponification of fat by calcium salts","Treatment is directed at the underlying pancreatic disease; the skin improves as the pancreatic process is controlled"],"pathophysiology":"Pancreatic panniculitis results from release of pancreatic enzymes, principally lipase, amylase and trypsin, into the circulation during pancreatic inflammation or from an enzyme-secreting tumour. These enzymes reach the subcutaneous fat and hydrolyse triglycerides, liberating free fatty acids that combine with calcium to form insoluble soaps (saponification). This enzymatic fat necrosis destroys adipocytes, leaving characteristic anucleate ghost cells with thick shadowy walls and basophilic granular calcium deposits, surrounded by a mixed inflammatory infiltrate within the fat lobules.","additionalDifferentials":["Erythema nodosum","Erythema induratum / nodular vasculitis","Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency panniculitis","Infective panniculitis","Cutaneous polyarteritis nodosa","Subcutaneous fat necrosis of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Deep incisional biopsy of a fresh nodule including subcutaneous fat","Serum lipase and amylase","Cross-sectional pancreatic imaging (CT or MRI) to identify pancreatitis or tumour","Full blood count for peripheral eosinophilia","Tumour markers and further evaluation if pancreatic carcinoma is suspected","Serum calcium and inflammatory markers"],"redFlags":["Panniculitis with polyarthritis and eosinophilia (PPP syndrome), which is associated with high mortality","Nodules arising without an obvious cause, raising suspicion of occult pancreatic carcinoma","Features of severe acute pancreatitis with systemic compromise","Extensive ulceration with secondary infection"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on the underlying pancreatic disease: lesions accompanying self-limiting pancreatitis resolve as the pancreas recovers, whereas panniculitis linked to pancreatic carcinoma or the PPP syndrome carries a poor prognosis. Skin nodules often heal with atrophic hyperpigmented scars.","pitfalls":["Dismissing recurrent leg nodules as ordinary erythema nodosum and missing the pancreatic cause","Failing to search for an occult pancreatic tumour when pancreatitis is absent","Sampling too superficially to demonstrate the diagnostic saponified ghost adipocytes","Overlooking accompanying arthritis and eosinophilia that portend a worse course"],"management":["Treat the underlying pancreatic disorder, whether pancreatitis or an enzyme-secreting tumour, as the definitive management since the skin lesions follow the pancreatic disease","Resect or otherwise treat an underlying pancreatic carcinoma, especially the acinar cell type, where identified","Supportive management of acute pancreatitis with fluids and nutrition to reduce the circulating enzyme load","Supportive skin care with rest, elevation, analgesia and wound care for ulcerating, discharging nodules","Investigate for the panniculitis-polyarthritis-eosinophilia syndrome, which carries a worse prognosis","Cross-specialty referral to gastroenterology or pancreatic surgery and to dermatology","Monitor the skin lesions, expecting resolution with atrophic scarring as the pancreatic disease settles"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pancreatic_polypeptidoma":{"name":"Pancreatic polypeptidoma","system":"Endocrine system / Pancreas","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPoma","pancreatic polypeptide-secreting tumour","PP-secreting pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour that secretes pancreatic polypeptide but, because this hormone produces no distinctive clinical syndrome, behaves essentially as a non-functioning tumour","Frequently presents late with mass-effect features such as abdominal pain, obstructive jaundice, weight loss or a palpable mass, or is found incidentally on imaging","Most commonly located in the head or uncinate process of the pancreas, where pancreatic polypeptide cells are concentrated","A substantial proportion are malignant, with nodal or hepatic metastases at diagnosis, so it should not be assumed to be benign","Raised plasma pancreatic polypeptide is a useful biochemical marker and can aid MEN1 surveillance, but is not specific and rises in other conditions","Chromogranin A is a more general marker of neuroendocrine tumour burden and is used for monitoring","These tumours usually express somatostatin receptors, so somatostatin receptor PET/CT is valuable for localisation and staging","Management mirrors that of non-functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours: resection for localised disease, and somatostatin analogues, targeted agents, peptide receptor radionuclide therapy or chemotherapy for advanced disease"],"pathophysiology":"Pancreatic polypeptidoma is a well-differentiated pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour arising from pancreatic polypeptide (F) cells, which are concentrated in the head and uncinate process. Although the tumour oversecretes pancreatic polypeptide, this hormone does not generate a recognisable clinical syndrome, so the tumour is grouped clinically with non-functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours and typically declares itself through local growth or metastasis rather than a hormonal effect. Elevated circulating pancreatic polypeptide therefore serves chiefly as a biochemical marker. As with other pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours, tumorigenesis may be sporadic or occur within MEN1 through loss of menin, and behaviour is graded by proliferative activity.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour of other cell types","Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","Other functional pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours (gastrinoma, glucagonoma, VIPoma, somatostatinoma)","Solid pseudopapillary neoplasm of the pancreas","Pancreatic metastasis from another primary such as renal cell carcinoma","Reactive or physiological elevation of pancreatic polypeptide"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma pancreatic polypeptide level","Chromogranin A for tumour burden and monitoring","Multiphase contrast CT or MRI of the pancreas","Endoscopic ultrasound with fine-needle aspiration or biopsy for tissue and grading","Somatostatin receptor PET/CT (DOTATATE) for staging","Histological grading with Ki-67 proliferation index per WHO classification","MEN1 genetic testing and biochemical screening where indicated"],"redFlags":["Obstructive jaundice or gastric outlet obstruction from a pancreatic head mass","Rapid weight loss or a rapidly enlarging mass","Hepatic or nodal metastases at presentation","High-grade histology with an elevated Ki-67 index","Features suggesting MEN1"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on tumour grade and stage: localised, well-differentiated tumours resected completely have a favourable outlook, whereas high-grade or metastatic disease carries a worse prognosis, though often a more indolent course than pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a raised pancreatic polypeptide as diagnostic when it is non-specific and rises in other conditions","Mistaking the tumour for pancreatic adenocarcinoma and withholding appropriate neuroendocrine-directed therapy","Assuming non-functioning implies benign, thereby underestimating malignant potential","Omitting Ki-67 grading, which is central to prognosis and treatment selection","Failing to consider MEN1 in younger patients or those with other endocrine tumours"],"management":["Complete surgical resection as the mainstay for localised, well-differentiated tumours, by pancreaticoduodenectomy given the head or uncinate location, or enucleation for small lesions","Somatostatin analogues such as octreotide or lanreotide for antiproliferative control of unresectable or metastatic well-differentiated disease","Further systemic options for progressive advanced disease including everolimus, sunitinib, peptide receptor radionuclide therapy with radiolabelled DOTATATE, or chemotherapy","Directed treatment of hepatic metastases by resection, ablation or embolisation where appropriate","Biochemical and imaging surveillance with plasma pancreatic polypeptide and chromogranin A alongside cross-sectional and somatostatin-receptor imaging","Screen for MEN1 with genetic counselling and family screening where indicated","Management within a specialist neuroendocrine tumour multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","ENETS Consensus Guidelines for non-functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours","WHO Classification of Tumours: Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"pancreatic_pseudocyst":{"name":"Pancreatic pseudocyst","system":"Hepatobiliary / Pancreatic surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pseudocyst of the pancreas","pancreatic fluid collection","post-pancreatitis pseudocyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A pancreatic pseudocyst is a persistent, encapsulated collection of pancreatic fluid that develops weeks after acute or chronic pancreatitis, or after pancreatic trauma","Unlike a true cyst it lacks an epithelial lining and is bounded by a wall of fibrous and granulation tissue, requiring around four weeks to mature","Common presentations include persistent or recurrent upper abdominal pain, a palpable epigastric mass, early satiety, nausea, and failure to recover after an episode of pancreatitis","A raised serum amylase and high amylase within aspirated fluid support the diagnosis and help distinguish it from a cystic neoplasm","Contrast CT and endoscopic ultrasound define the collection, its relation to the stomach and duodenum, and any ductal communication","Many small, asymptomatic pseudocysts resolve spontaneously and can be observed, whereas large, symptomatic, enlarging, or complicated collections need drainage","Drainage is increasingly endoscopic (transmural cystgastrostomy or cystduodenostomy), with surgical internal drainage or percutaneous approaches reserved for selected cases","In older patients or those without a clear pancreatitis history, a cystic pancreatic neoplasm must be excluded before assuming a pseudocyst"],"pathophysiology":"Pancreatic pseudocysts arise when pancreatic ductal disruption, from acute inflammation, chronic pancreatitis, or trauma, allows enzyme-rich secretions to leak and collect in or around the pancreas. The surrounding tissue reacts, and over several weeks an inflammatory capsule of fibrous and granulation tissue forms, producing a mature pseudocyst without a true epithelial lining. Persistent communication with the pancreatic duct maintains the collection. Enlargement can compress adjacent structures, and complications develop when the cyst becomes infected, erodes into a vessel to form a pseudoaneurysm or bleed, or ruptures into the peritoneum or an adjacent viscus.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystic pancreatic neoplasm (serous or mucinous cystadenoma, intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm)","Acute peripancreatic fluid collection or walled-off necrosis","Pancreatic abscess","Mesenteric or omental cyst","Duplication cyst or choledochal cyst","Pancreatic pseudoaneurysm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen to characterise the collection and its wall","Endoscopic ultrasound with fluid aspiration for amylase, CEA, and cytology to exclude a neoplasm","Serum amylase and lipase and liver function tests","MRCP or ERP to assess pancreatic ductal anatomy and communication before drainage","Ultrasound for follow-up of size in conservatively managed cysts"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe pain, expanding mass, or gastrointestinal bleeding suggesting pseudoaneurysm erosion or rupture","Fever, rigors, and systemic sepsis indicating an infected pseudocyst","Progressive gastric outlet or biliary obstruction from a large cyst","Rapid clinical deterioration suggesting free rupture into the peritoneal cavity"],"prognosis":"Small uncomplicated pseudocysts frequently resolve without intervention, and drainage of symptomatic collections has good outcomes. Prognosis worsens with complications such as haemorrhage from a pseudoaneurysm, infection, or rupture, and recurrence is more likely when underlying ductal disease or chronic pancreatitis is not addressed.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a cystic pancreatic neoplasm for a pseudocyst and draining rather than resecting it","Draining a collection before the wall has matured, increasing the risk of leakage and failure","Overlooking an associated pseudoaneurysm, so that drainage precipitates torrential bleeding","Ignoring underlying pancreatic ductal obstruction, leading to recurrence after drainage","Delaying treatment of an infected pseudocyst presenting with sepsis"],"management":["Observation with serial imaging for small, asymptomatic pseudocysts, many of which resolve spontaneously","Endoscopic ultrasound-guided transmural drainage by cystgastrostomy or cystduodenostomy as first-line treatment for mature, symptomatic or enlarging collections","Endoscopic transpapillary drainage or pancreatic duct stenting where there is ductal communication","Surgical or percutaneous drainage when endoscopic drainage fails or the anatomy is unfavourable","Management of complications with antibiotics and drainage for infection and angiographic embolisation for pseudoaneurysm or haemorrhage","Supportive care with analgesia and nutrition and treatment of the underlying pancreatitis","Interval imaging follow-up to confirm resolution and detect recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"pancreatoblastoma":{"name":"pancreatoblastoma","system":"gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pancreatic blastoma","infantile pancreatic carcinoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Digestive System Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest pancreatic neoplasm of childhood, typically presenting in the first decade as a large, often palpable upper abdominal mass.","Histologically defined by acinar differentiation with distinctive squamoid nests (squamoid corpuscles), and it may also show endocrine and ductal elements.","Serum alpha-fetoprotein is elevated in a substantial proportion and is a useful marker for monitoring response and relapse.","Associated with Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, in which it may be congenital, and with familial adenomatous polyposis through the APC/Wnt-beta-catenin pathway.","Abnormalities of chromosome 11p15 and activation of Wnt/beta-catenin signalling are the principal molecular drivers.","Complete surgical resection is the mainstay of cure, and neoadjuvant chemotherapy can downstage large or initially unresectable tumours.","Symptoms are frequently nonspecific (mass, abdominal pain, weight loss), and obstructive jaundice is less common than in adult pancreatic cancer.","Metastases occur most often to the liver, regional lymph nodes, and lungs."],"pathophysiology":"Pancreatoblastoma arises from pluripotent pancreatic precursor cells and recapitulates the embryonic pancreas, characteristically showing acinar differentiation together with squamoid nests (corpuscles) and variable endocrine and ductal components. The dominant molecular alteration is activation of the Wnt/beta-catenin pathway, with frequent involvement of the APC/beta-catenin axis and nuclear beta-catenin accumulation; abnormalities at the imprinted 11p15.5 locus link the tumour to Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, while germline APC loss links it to familial adenomatous polyposis. Alpha-fetoprotein production reflects the tumour's primitive, partly hepatoid differentiation. Tumours are often large and encapsulated at presentation and spread to regional lymph nodes, liver, and lungs.","additionalDifferentials":["Neuroblastoma (adrenal or retroperitoneal)","Solid pseudopapillary neoplasm of the pancreas","Acinar cell carcinoma","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour","Wilms tumour or other retroperitoneal mass","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma involving the pancreas","Pancreatic metastasis of another paediatric tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum alpha-fetoprotein at baseline and serially","Abdominal ultrasound followed by contrast-enhanced CT or MRI for local extent and vascular involvement","CT of the chest for pulmonary metastases","Core or open biopsy for histological confirmation when upfront resection is not planned","Testing for Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome and familial adenomatous polyposis where clinically indicated","Assessment of exocrine and endocrine pancreatic function","Baseline audiometry and renal function before platinum-based chemotherapy"],"redFlags":["Obstructive jaundice or duodenal obstruction from a pancreatic head mass","Rapid abdominal distension or pain suggesting haemorrhage into the tumour","Rising alpha-fetoprotein after resection, indicating recurrence","Features of Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome prompting tumour surveillance"],"prognosis":"Completely resected, localised pancreatoblastoma has a good prognosis, whereas unresectable, recurrent, or metastatic disease carries a considerably poorer outlook despite chemotherapy.","pitfalls":["Mistaking pancreatoblastoma for the commoner neuroblastoma or for a solid pseudopapillary neoplasm without adequate histology.","Proceeding to a mutilating upfront resection when neoadjuvant chemotherapy could render a large tumour resectable with less morbidity.","Overlooking an underlying Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome or familial adenomatous polyposis.","Interpreting alpha-fetoprotein without accounting for the high physiological levels of infancy."],"management":["Complete surgical resection as the mainstay of cure for localised tumours","Neoadjuvant platinum-based chemotherapy, such as a cisplatin and doxorubicin regimen, to downstage large or initially unresectable tumours","Adjuvant chemotherapy and management of metastatic disease in the liver and lungs","Serial serum alpha-fetoprotein monitoring to assess treatment response and detect relapse","Assessment and support of exocrine and endocrine pancreatic function after resection","Screen for Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome and familial adenomatous polyposis with genetic counselling","Management within a paediatric oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Digestive System Tumours","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","European Cooperative Study Group for Paediatric Rare Tumours (EXPeRT) guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"panic_disorder":{"name":"Panic disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["panic attacks disorder","episodic paroxysmal anxiety"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The core feature is recurrent, unexpected surges of intense fear or discomfort that peak within minutes, accompanied by prominent physical symptoms such as palpitations, chest tightness, breathlessness, sweating, trembling, dizziness, and a sense of impending doom","Diagnosis also requires persistent worry about further attacks or a maladaptive change in behaviour to avoid them, distinguishing the disorder from isolated panic attacks","Many patients present first to emergency or cardiology services convinced they are having a heart attack, so a compassionate explanation of the mind-body link is central to engagement","Exclude medical mimics before settling on the diagnosis, including arrhythmia, hyperthyroidism, hypoglycaemia, phaeochromocytoma, asthma, and caffeine or stimulant effects","Agoraphobic avoidance frequently develops as patients begin to avoid places or situations from which escape might feel difficult during an attack","First-line management combines cognitive behavioural therapy, including interoceptive exposure and breathing retraining, with SSRIs or SNRIs as pharmacological options","Benzodiazepines may give rapid relief but carry dependence and rebound risks, so they are generally reserved for short-term or adjunctive use rather than maintenance","Reassurance, psychoeducation about the self-limiting nature of attacks, and reduction of stimulants can markedly reduce anticipatory anxiety"],"pathophysiology":"Panic disorder is thought to arise from dysregulation of fear-processing circuits, particularly hypersensitivity of the amygdala and brainstem structures involved in the alarm and respiratory response, with contributions from serotonergic, noradrenergic, and GABAergic dysfunction. A heritable predisposition interacts with heightened sensitivity to internal bodily cues (interoceptive conditioning) and catastrophic misinterpretation of benign somatic sensations, which perpetuates the cycle of fear and physiological arousal.","additionalDifferentials":["Cardiac arrhythmia or acute coronary syndrome","Hyperthyroidism and other endocrine disturbances","Phaeochromocytoma","Hypoglycaemia","Substance intoxication or withdrawal, including caffeine, stimulants, and alcohol","Other anxiety disorders such as generalised anxiety disorder or social anxiety disorder","Vestibular or respiratory disorders presenting with dizziness or breathlessness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electrocardiogram to exclude arrhythmia in appropriate presentations","Thyroid function testing","Blood glucose measurement during symptomatic episodes","Substance and caffeine use screening","Standardised anxiety rating scales to gauge severity and monitor response"],"redFlags":["Syncope, sustained chest pain, or objective signs suggesting a primary cardiac event","Emerging suicidal ideation, particularly with comorbid depression","Escalating benzodiazepine or alcohol use to self-manage symptoms","Severe agoraphobic avoidance causing housebound behaviour and functional collapse"],"prognosis":"Panic disorder tends to run a chronic, fluctuating course, but a substantial proportion of patients achieve marked improvement or remission with adequate treatment. Prognosis is better with early intervention and engagement in cognitive behavioural therapy, and worse when agoraphobia, depression, or substance misuse is comorbid or when treatment is delayed.","pitfalls":["Repeated extensive medical investigations that reinforce illness beliefs rather than addressing the anxiety","Relying on long-term benzodiazepines and creating dependence","Missing comorbid depression and its associated suicide risk","Underdosing or prematurely stopping SSRIs before an adequate therapeutic trial","Overlooking caffeine, stimulants, or withdrawal states as drivers of attacks"],"management":["Cognitive behavioural therapy including interoceptive exposure, cognitive restructuring and breathing retraining as first-line psychological treatment","Pharmacotherapy with an SSRI or SNRI as first-line drug treatment, started at a low dose and titrated upwards","Time-limited benzodiazepine use only for short-term or severe symptoms, avoiding long-term dependence","Psychoeducation on the mind-body basis of attacks and the benign nature of the physical symptoms","Exclude and address medical mimics such as arrhythmia, hyperthyroidism, hypoglycaemia and phaeochromocytoma, and reduce caffeine and stimulant use","Graded exposure to tackle agoraphobic avoidance","Monitor with standardised anxiety rating scales and screen for and treat comorbid depression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"panner_disease":{"name":"Panner disease","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["osteochondrosis of the capitellum","capitellar osteochondrosis","osteonecrosis of the humeral capitellum","Panner's disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A true osteonecrosis of the capitellum affecting its developing ossification centre, not a traction apophysitis","Affected bone is the capitellum of the distal humerus at the lateral elbow","Typically affects young children, roughly five to ten years of age, often in the dominant throwing arm","Presents with dull lateral elbow pain, stiffness and a mild flexion contracture, worse with throwing or weight-bearing on the arm","The radiographic hallmark is irregularity, fragmentation and sclerosis of the capitellar ossification centre, sometimes with later reossification","It should be distinguished from osteochondritis dissecans of the capitellum, which occurs in older adolescents and can produce loose bodies","Unlike osteochondritis dissecans, Panner disease usually involves the whole ossific nucleus and tends to reconstitute with growth","Management is conservative with rest, activity modification and avoidance of throwing, with physiotherapy to restore motion as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Panner disease is an osteochondrosis of the capitellum in which the ossification centre of the lateral distal humerus undergoes ischaemic change during childhood. Repetitive valgus and compressive loading of the immature elbow, particularly from throwing or upper limb weight-bearing, is thought to disturb the blood supply to the developing capitellum. This produces necrosis, fragmentation and sclerosis of the ossific nucleus, followed by revascularisation and reossification as growth continues. Because the process typically affects the entire ossification centre in a skeletally immature child and remodels with growth, it is generally self-limiting, in contrast to capitellar osteochondritis dissecans in older adolescents, which involves a focal osteochondral fragment and carries a higher risk of loose bodies and residual joint incongruity.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteochondritis dissecans of the capitellum","Medial epicondyle apophysitis (little league elbow)","Radial head or neck fracture","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis of the elbow","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis of the elbow","Radiocapitellar plica or synovitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anteroposterior and lateral elbow radiographs showing capitellar fragmentation and sclerosis","Comparison views of the contralateral elbow to gauge normal ossification","Magnetic resonance imaging to assess the articular cartilage and distinguish from osteochondritis dissecans","Assessment of elbow range of motion and any fixed flexion contracture","Inflammatory markers if infection or inflammatory arthritis is suspected","Follow-up imaging to confirm reossification of the capitellum"],"redFlags":["Mechanical locking or catching suggesting a loose body and osteochondritis dissecans rather than Panner disease","Progressive fixed flexion contracture or loss of motion","Systemic features or joint effusion raising concern for infection or inflammatory arthritis","Persistent symptoms in an older adolescent, which favour osteochondritis dissecans with poorer prognosis"],"prognosis":"Panner disease generally carries a favourable prognosis; the capitellum usually reossifies and remodels with growth once the elbow is rested, and most children recover full or near-full function without long-term joint damage.","pitfalls":["Confusing Panner disease with capitellar osteochondritis dissecans, which has a different age group and worse outlook","Allowing continued throwing, which perpetuates loading of the healing capitellum","Missing an alternative diagnosis such as infection when an effusion or systemic features are present","Failing to arrange follow-up imaging to confirm reossification"],"management":["Rest and activity modification, stopping throwing and upper-limb weight-bearing until symptoms settle, the mainstay of treatment","Simple analgesia or NSAIDs and ice for pain during the symptomatic phase","Short-term immobilisation or splinting of the elbow if pain is marked","Gentle range-of-motion exercises and physiotherapy to address stiffness and any flexion contracture","Serial radiographs to confirm reossification and remodelling of the capitellum","Distinguish from and monitor for osteochondritis dissecans with loose bodies in the older child, referring to orthopaedics if atypical or non-resolving","Graded return to sport once pain-free with restored range of motion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"pantothenate_kinase_associated_neurodegeneration":{"name":"Pantothenate kinase-associated neurodegeneration","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PKAN","PANK2","NBIA type 1","Hallervorden-Spatz syndrome","neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation type 1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Movement-disorder and neurogenetics textbooks with peer-reviewed reviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most common form of neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation (NBIA), inherited autosomal recessively through biallelic PANK2 variants","Classic early-onset form presents in childhood with gait disturbance, dystonia, rigidity, and pigmentary retinopathy, progressing over years","Atypical later-onset form has slower progression with prominent speech and neuropsychiatric features and less retinopathy","Oromandibular and limb dystonia can be severe and may lead to status dystonicus, a neurological emergency","The pathognomonic MRI finding is the eye-of-the-tiger sign: a central region of T2 hyperintensity within surrounding hypointensity in the globus pallidus, reflecting focal gliosis amid iron deposition","Iron-sensitive MRI sequences (T2*/gradient-echo/susceptibility-weighted imaging) accentuate the pallidal iron and support the NBIA diagnosis","Management is symptomatic, targeting dystonia and spasticity with oral agents, botulinum toxin, and in selected refractory cases deep brain stimulation","Diagnosis is confirmed by PANK2 molecular testing in the setting of the characteristic clinical and imaging picture"],"pathophysiology":"Pantothenate kinase-associated neurodegeneration is caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in PANK2, encoding a mitochondrial pantothenate kinase that catalyses the first, rate-limiting step of coenzyme A biosynthesis from vitamin B5. Impaired coenzyme A synthesis is thought to disrupt lipid and energy metabolism and to allow accumulation of cysteine-containing substrates that chelate iron, driving oxidative damage. The net effect is progressive iron deposition and neurodegeneration concentrated in the globus pallidus, producing the extrapyramidal features and the characteristic pallidal imaging changes.","additionalDifferentials":["Other NBIA subtypes (PLA2G6-associated neurodegeneration, BPAN, aceruloplasminaemia, neuroferritinopathy)","Wilson disease","Juvenile Huntington disease","Dopa-responsive dystonia and other early-onset dystonias","Mitochondrial disorders with basal ganglia involvement","Secondary pallidal injury from hypoxia or manganese exposure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI including iron-sensitive sequences to demonstrate the eye-of-the-tiger sign and pallidal iron","PANK2 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Ophthalmological assessment including electroretinography for pigmentary retinopathy","Serum copper and caeruloplasmin to exclude Wilson disease","Serum ferritin and caeruloplasmin to help distinguish other NBIA subtypes"],"redFlags":["Status dystonicus with unremitting severe dystonia causing pain, hyperthermia, or rhabdomyolysis","Acute swallowing failure with aspiration risk","Rapid loss of ambulation or new bulbar dysfunction","Self-injury from severe oromandibular dystonia"],"prognosis":"The classic childhood-onset form is progressive, with most patients losing independent ambulation within years of onset, whereas the atypical form advances more slowly over decades. Life expectancy is reduced, largely from complications of severe dystonia and immobility.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the eye-of-the-tiger appearance for another basal ganglia lesion or dismissing subtle pallidal iron on standard sequences","Failing to obtain iron-sensitive MRI sequences that reveal the diagnostic iron deposition","Overlooking status dystonicus as an emergency requiring prompt escalation","Not screening for Wilson disease, a treatable mimic, before settling on an NBIA diagnosis"],"management":["Symptomatic dystonia control with oral trihexyphenidyl, baclofen or benzodiazepines, and botulinum toxin for troublesome focal dystonia","Intrathecal baclofen and deep brain stimulation of the globus pallidus for severe generalised or refractory dystonia","Emergency management of status dystonicus with sedation and supportive care in a high-dependency setting","Trial of iron-chelation therapy with deferiprone to target pallidal iron accumulation","Supportive care with feeding and nutrition support including gastrostomy, spasticity management and physiotherapy to prevent contractures","Ophthalmology follow-up for pigmentary retinopathy and low-vision support","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive PANK2 inheritance and family testing","Multidisciplinary neurology, rehabilitation and palliative input for progressive disability"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Pantothenate Kinase-Associated Neurodegeneration","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"papa_syndrome":{"name":"PAPA syndrome","system":"Immunology / Autoinflammatory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pyogenic arthritis, pyoderma gangrenosum and acne syndrome","PSTPIP1-associated inflammatory disease","PSTPIP1","CD2 binding protein 1 disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Autoinflammation / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant autoinflammatory disease caused by mutations in PSTPIP1, which regulates the pyrin inflammasome","Classic triad is recurrent sterile pyogenic arthritis, pyoderma gangrenosum, and severe cystic acne","Joint disease often begins in childhood with recurrent, destructive, sterile arthritis triggered by minor trauma","Pyoderma gangrenosum and severe nodulocystic acne tend to become more prominent from adolescence","Aspirated joint fluid is sterile and neutrophil-rich, reflecting an autoinflammatory rather than infective arthritis","Interleukin-1 and tumour necrosis factor directed biologics are the main therapies, with variable individual response","The skin lesions can pathergically worsen after surgery or trauma, so aggressive surgical debridement should be approached cautiously"],"pathophysiology":"PAPA syndrome arises from dominant mutations in PSTPIP1 (also called CD2 binding protein 1), a protein that interacts with pyrin to regulate inflammasome activity. Mutations enhance PSTPIP1 binding to pyrin, promoting inappropriate inflammasome assembly and excess interleukin-1 beta production. The resulting sterile neutrophilic inflammation targets joints, skin, and pilosebaceous units, producing pyogenic arthritis, pyoderma gangrenosum, and cystic acne. Because both interleukin-1 and tumour necrosis factor contribute to the inflammation, biologics against these cytokines are used therapeutically.","additionalDifferentials":["Septic arthritis","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Idiopathic pyoderma gangrenosum","Severe nodulocystic acne without systemic disease","Other PSTPIP1-associated syndromes (e.g. PAMI)","Behcet disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for PSTPIP1 mutations","Synovial fluid analysis and culture demonstrating sterile neutrophilic arthritis","Acute-phase reactants during flares","Skin biopsy of pyoderma gangrenosum lesions","Joint imaging to assess destructive arthritis","Wound swabs to exclude secondary infection of skin ulcers"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive destructive arthritis threatening joint integrity","Enlarging pyoderma gangrenosum ulcers with secondary infection","Pathergy with wound breakdown after surgery or trauma","Systemic inflammatory response requiring escalation of therapy"],"prognosis":"PAPA syndrome is a chronic relapsing disease; biologic therapy can control inflammation and limit joint and skin damage, but responses vary and established joint destruction or skin scarring may persist.","pitfalls":["Treating sterile pyogenic arthritis as septic and pursuing repeated invasive intervention","Aggressively debriding pyoderma gangrenosum, which can provoke pathergy and worsen ulcers","Assuming acne is cosmetic and missing the wider autoinflammatory diagnosis","Not testing for PSTPIP1 in a child with recurrent sterile arthritis and skin disease"],"management":["Interleukin-1 blockade with anakinra or canakinumab as key targeted therapy for the sterile pyogenic arthritis and skin inflammation","Tumour necrosis factor inhibitors such as etanercept, infliximab or adalimumab as an alternative or adjunctive biologic","Corticosteroids, systemic and intra-articular, for acute flares of pyogenic arthritis","Dedicated management of pyoderma gangrenosum with topical and systemic immunosuppression and careful wound care, avoiding aggressive debridement because of pathergy","Treatment of severe nodulocystic acne with topical and systemic agents including oral retinoids","Confirm sterility with joint fluid and wound cultures to avoid unnecessary antibiotics and distinguish from infection","Genetic counselling for autosomal dominant PSTPIP1 inheritance","Multidisciplinary care across rheumatology, dermatology and clinical immunology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Autoinflammation","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"papillary_craniopharyngioma":{"name":"Papillary craniopharyngioma","system":"Neuro-oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Papillary-type craniopharyngioma","PCP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neuro-oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["WHO grade 1 sellar/suprasellar epithelial tumour occurring almost exclusively in adults, in contrast to the bimodal age distribution of the adamantinomatous type.","Nearly all cases harbour the BRAF V600E mutation, whereas adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma is driven by CTNNB1 (beta-catenin/Wnt) mutations.","Typically a well-circumscribed, often solid or mixed suprasellar or third-ventricular mass with less calcification than the adamantinomatous type.","Histology shows well-differentiated non-keratinising squamous epithelium arranged in papillae over fibrovascular cores, lacking wet keratin, peripheral palisading and stellate reticulum.","Presents with visual field loss (classically bitemporal hemianopia), headache, hypopituitarism and sometimes diabetes insipidus or hypothalamic dysfunction.","BRAF inhibition, usually combined with MEK inhibition, can produce marked tumour shrinkage and is an emerging neoadjuvant or adjuvant option.","Endocrine and visual assessment are essential before and after treatment given the parasellar location.","Sparse calcification and a predominantly solid appearance in an adult favour the papillary over the adamantinomatous type."],"pathophysiology":"Papillary craniopharyngioma is a benign epithelial tumour of the sellar and suprasellar region, thought to arise from metaplasia of adenohypophyseal epithelial cells or ectodermal remnants, and is molecularly defined by the near-universal presence of the activating BRAF V600E mutation with resultant constitutive MAPK/ERK pathway signalling. This contrasts sharply with adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma, which is driven by activating CTNNB1 mutations and aberrant Wnt/beta-catenin signalling with nuclear beta-catenin accumulation. Histologically it forms papillae of mature, well-differentiated non-keratinising squamous epithelium over fibrovascular cores and lacks the wet keratin, peripheral palisading, stellate reticulum and dystrophic calcification typical of the adamantinomatous variant. Its location adjacent to the optic chiasm, pituitary and hypothalamus accounts for the visual, endocrine and hypothalamic manifestations.","additionalDifferentials":["Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma","Rathke cleft cyst","Pituitary adenoma, including cystic macroadenoma","Suprasellar meningioma","Germinoma or other germ cell tumour","Hypothalamic/optic pathway glioma","Epidermoid or dermoid cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced MRI of the sella and suprasellar region to define the solid/cystic mass and its relation to the chiasm, pituitary and third ventricle","CT to assess calcification (usually sparse in the papillary type) and the bony sella","Full anterior pituitary hormone profile and assessment for diabetes insipidus using paired serum and urine osmolality","Formal visual acuity, colour vision and perimetry (visual field) testing","Histopathology with BRAF V600E testing by immunohistochemistry and/or molecular assay","Ophthalmology and endocrinology review, including fundoscopy"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive visual loss or a new visual field defect","Features of hypopituitarism or adrenal insufficiency, such as fatigue, hypotension or hyponatraemia","Polyuria and polydipsia suggesting diabetes insipidus, particularly in the perioperative period","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus from third-ventricular obstruction","Acute hypothalamic disturbance affecting temperature, appetite or conscious level"],"prognosis":"Generally favourable for a WHO grade 1 tumour, with complete resection often curative and recurrence rates tending to be lower than for the adamantinomatous type. The parasellar location can nonetheless leave lasting visual or endocrine morbidity, and BRAF-targeted therapy offers effective disease control in selected cases.","pitfalls":["Assuming all craniopharyngiomas are alike; failing to test for BRAF V600E misses a targeted-therapy opportunity specific to the papillary type.","Overlooking or undertreating perioperative endocrine deficits, particularly cortisol deficiency and diabetes insipidus.","Mistaking a solid enhancing papillary craniopharyngioma for a pituitary macroadenoma or a meningioma.","Pursuing aggressive complete resection when the tumour is adherent, risking hypothalamic injury."],"management":["Surgical resection by endoscopic transsphenoidal or transcranial approach as primary treatment, aiming for maximal safe removal","Adjuvant or salvage radiotherapy for residual or recurrent tumour","BRAF and MEK inhibition, such as dabrafenib with trametinib, as neoadjuvant or adjuvant targeted therapy given the near-universal BRAF V600E mutation","Hormone replacement for hypopituitarism and desmopressin for diabetes insipidus","Formal visual acuity, colour vision and perimetry assessment with ophthalmology follow-up for chiasmal compression","Monitoring and management of hypothalamic dysfunction affecting weight, temperature and behaviour, and its metabolic sequelae","Surveillance MRI for recurrence","Multidisciplinary skull-base team management across neurosurgery, endocrinology, ophthalmology and oncology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Central Nervous System Tumours","Standard neuro-oncology and neuro-endocrinology reference texts","Consensus guidelines on craniopharyngioma management"],"crossLinks":[]},"papillary_fibroelastoma":{"name":"Papillary fibroelastoma","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cardiac papillary fibroelastoma","CPF","papillary fibroelastoma of the valve","fibroelastic papilloma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease / ESC guidelines on cardiac tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common primary tumour of the cardiac valves and a leading benign valvular tumour, typically small and arising on valve surfaces","It has a characteristic frond-like, sea-anemone appearance with multiple papillary arms attached by a short stalk","The aortic valve is the most frequent site, followed by the mitral valve, and lesions are usually single","Many are found incidentally on echocardiography performed for other reasons","The principal clinical hazard is embolism, which can cause stroke, transient ischaemic attack, coronary embolism with angina or myocardial infarction, or peripheral events","The mobile tumour or associated thrombus on its surface is the embolic source, so mobility on imaging is an important risk marker","Transoesophageal echocardiography best characterises the lesion, and it must be distinguished from vegetation and thrombus","Surgical excision, usually with valve preservation, is considered for symptomatic, left-sided, or highly mobile tumours because of embolic risk"],"pathophysiology":"Papillary fibroelastoma is a benign avascular tumour of the endocardial surface, most often on the valves. It consists of a branching core of dense connective tissue rich in elastic fibres, covered by a layer of endothelium, giving the multiple frond-like papillary projections that resemble a sea anemone when immersed in fluid. The clefts between the fronds promote stasis and deposition of platelet-fibrin thrombus. Because the tumour and any adherent thrombus are mobile and exposed to flow, fragments can detach and embolise; left-sided lesions on the aortic or mitral valve threaten the cerebral, coronary, and systemic circulations.","additionalDifferentials":["Infective endocarditis vegetation","Non-bacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis","Valvular thrombus","Lambl's excrescences","Cardiac myxoma","Valvular calcification or degenerative nodule"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic and transoesophageal echocardiography to define the frond-like mobile valvular mass","Cardiac MRI in selected cases to further characterise the lesion","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers to help exclude infective endocarditis","Neuroimaging when cerebral embolism is suspected","Coronary assessment if there are features of coronary embolism","Histological confirmation of the excised specimen"],"redFlags":["Stroke or transient ischaemic attack attributable to the lesion","Coronary embolism causing angina or myocardial infarction","Highly mobile left-sided tumour with high embolic potential","Peripheral or systemic embolic events"],"prognosis":"The tumour itself is benign and surgical excision is generally curative with a good outlook and low recurrence; the main determinant of prognosis is the embolic complications that occur before treatment.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the lesion for an infective vegetation and initiating unnecessary prolonged antibiotics","Underestimating embolic risk in mobile left-sided lesions","Confusing it with benign Lambl's excrescences or valvular thrombus","Assuming an incidental finding is harmless without weighing embolic potential"],"management":["Surgical excision, valve-sparing where possible, as definitive treatment, particularly for left-sided, large or highly mobile lesions given the embolic risk","Prioritise excision in symptomatic patients and those who have already had an embolic event such as stroke, transient ischaemic attack, or coronary or peripheral embolism","Surveillance echocardiography for small, incidentally found, asymptomatic lesions when surgery is deferred","Antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy to reduce embolic risk in patients not undergoing immediate surgery","Exclude infective endocarditis with blood cultures before attributing a valvular mass to fibroelastoma","Histological confirmation of the excised specimen","Cardiology and cardiac-surgery multidisciplinary decision-making"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC guidance on the management of cardiac tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"papillary_muscle_rupture":{"name":"Papillary muscle rupture","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["post-infarction papillary muscle rupture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cardiology references / acute coronary syndrome literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mechanical complication of acute myocardial infarction causing sudden severe mitral regurgitation","It typically occurs a few days after infarction and presents with abrupt pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock","The posteromedial papillary muscle is more vulnerable because it has a single coronary blood supply","The murmur may be soft or absent despite severe regurgitation because of rapid pressure equalisation","Echocardiography (often transoesophageal) is diagnostic, showing a flail leaflet and severe regurgitation","It is a surgical emergency; temporising measures include afterload reduction and mechanical circulatory support"],"pathophysiology":"Papillary muscle rupture results from ischaemic necrosis of a papillary muscle following myocardial infarction, most often affecting the posteromedial muscle owing to its single-vessel supply. Complete or partial rupture disrupts the subvalvular apparatus, producing an acutely flail mitral leaflet and torrential mitral regurgitation. Because the left atrium and ventricle cannot accommodate the sudden volume, pressures equalise rapidly, causing flash pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock, and the murmur may therefore be unimpressive despite catastrophic haemodynamics.","additionalDifferentials":["Post-infarction ventricular septal rupture","Free wall rupture with tamponade","Acute ischaemic mitral regurgitation without rupture","Infective endocarditis with chordal rupture","Spontaneous chordal rupture in degenerative valve disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent echocardiography, often transoesophageal","Assessment of coronary anatomy","Haemodynamic monitoring for cardiogenic shock","Electrocardiogram and cardiac biomarkers confirming infarction","Prompt cardiac surgical assessment"],"redFlags":["Acute pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock after recent infarction","A new severe mitral regurgitation, even with a soft murmur","Haemodynamic instability requiring urgent support","Multiorgan hypoperfusion"],"prognosis":"Without surgery mortality is very high; emergency valve surgery, supported by mechanical circulatory support and revascularisation, offers the best chance of survival though operative risk is substantial.","pitfalls":["Being falsely reassured by a soft or absent murmur","Confusing it with post-infarction ventricular septal rupture","Delaying surgery in a deteriorating patient","Attributing shock solely to left ventricular dysfunction"],"management":["Emergency mitral valve replacement or repair is the definitive treatment and should not be delayed","Afterload reduction with intravenous vasodilators such as sodium nitroprusside to lower the regurgitant fraction as a temporising measure","Mechanical circulatory support with intra-aortic balloon counterpulsation, or a short-term ventricular assist device or ECMO, to stabilise cardiogenic shock before theatre","Concurrent coronary revascularisation by surgical grafting or percutaneous intervention to treat the underlying infarction","Supportive intensive care with positive-pressure ventilation and diuresis for flash pulmonary oedema, with cautious inotropic support","Continuous invasive haemodynamic and transoesophageal echocardiographic monitoring within a heart-team and cardiac-surgical setting"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease","Acute coronary syndrome guidelines (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"papillary_renal_cell_carcinoma":{"name":"Papillary renal cell carcinoma","system":"Genitourinary / Kidney","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["papillary RCC","pRCC","chromophil renal cell carcinoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Uro-oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The second most common subtype of renal cell carcinoma after clear cell disease","Often appears as a hypovascular renal mass, enhancing less avidly than clear cell tumours on contrast imaging","Frequently multifocal and bilateral, more so than other subtypes","Traditionally divided into type 1 and type 2, with type 2 generally behaving more aggressively","Hereditary forms include hereditary papillary renal carcinoma (MET) and hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell cancer (fumarate hydratase)","A fumarate hydratase-deficient tumour should prompt genetic assessment because of its aggressive nature","Localised low-grade disease generally carries a good prognosis after surgery"],"pathophysiology":"Papillary renal cell carcinoma arises from the epithelium of the proximal or distal tubule and is characterised by papillary or tubulopapillary growth, often with foamy macrophages and haemosiderin. Type 1 tumours are frequently associated with gains of chromosomes 7 and 17 and, in hereditary cases, activating MET mutations. Type 2 tumours are more heterogeneous and include fumarate hydratase-deficient cancers linked to hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell cancer, which pursue an aggressive course. The relatively poor vascularity explains the characteristically modest enhancement on imaging.","additionalDifferentials":["Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","Chromophobe renal cell carcinoma","Collecting duct carcinoma","Metanephric adenoma","Fumarate hydratase-deficient renal cell carcinoma","MiT family translocation renal cell carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT or MRI to characterise and stage the mass","Renal mass biopsy where it will guide management","Immunohistochemistry and, where indicated, fumarate hydratase staining","Genetic assessment when hereditary features or syndromic signs are present","Chest imaging and baseline bloods for staging of larger tumours"],"redFlags":["High-grade or type 2 histology, especially fumarate hydratase-deficient tumours","Locally advanced or metastatic disease at presentation","Cutaneous or uterine leiomyomas suggesting a hereditary syndrome","Rapid growth of a previously indolent lesion"],"prognosis":"Localised, low-grade type 1 tumours generally have a favourable prognosis after surgery; higher-grade type 2 and fumarate hydratase-deficient tumours carry a worse outlook with a greater risk of metastasis.","pitfalls":["Assuming a hypovascular mass is benign because it enhances poorly","Overlooking multifocal or contralateral tumours","Missing a hereditary syndrome in a young patient with an aggressive type 2 tumour","Applying clear cell prognostic assumptions to papillary disease"],"management":["Partial nephron-sparing nephrectomy for localised disease, with radical nephrectomy reserved for larger or centrally placed tumours","Active surveillance or image-guided ablation with cryotherapy or radiofrequency for small tumours in older or comorbid patients","Systemic therapy for advanced disease with tyrosine kinase and MET inhibitors such as cabozantinib and immune-checkpoint-inhibitor combinations, ideally within clinical trials","Structured post-treatment surveillance with cross-sectional imaging for local recurrence and metastasis, tailored to stage and grade","Referral for genetic assessment and germline testing when hereditary papillary carcinoma (MET) or fumarate hydratase-deficient HLRCC features are present","Cascade screening and surveillance of at-risk relatives in confirmed hereditary syndromes","Management within a uro-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology (standard reference)","WHO Classification of Urinary and Male Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"papillary_thyroid_carcinoma":{"name":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTC","papillary carcinoma of thyroid","differentiated thyroid cancer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / WHO Classification of Endocrine Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common thyroid malignancy, typically presenting as a painless thyroid nodule or an incidentally detected lesion on imaging in a euthyroid patient","Diagnosis rests on the characteristic nuclear features rather than architecture: ground-glass 'Orphan-Annie eye' nuclei, nuclear grooves, and intranuclear pseudo-inclusions, often with laminated psammoma bodies","Spreads preferentially via lymphatics to cervical nodes, so palpable or ultrasound-detected nodal disease at presentation is common and does not preclude cure","Ultrasound features suggesting malignancy include marked hypoechogenicity, microcalcifications, irregular margins, and a taller-than-wide shape, prompting fine-needle aspiration","Driven by MAPK-pathway activation, most often BRAF V600E point mutation or RET/PTC rearrangements, which increasingly inform prognosis and targeted therapy","Overall prognosis is excellent, particularly in younger patients, with management centred on surgery (lobectomy or total thyroidectomy), selective radioactive iodine, and TSH suppression","Follow-up uses serum thyroglobulin as a tumour marker together with neck ultrasound to detect recurrence after thyroidectomy"],"pathophysiology":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma arises from follicular epithelial cells and is defined by distinctive nuclear changes produced by constitutive activation of the MAPK signalling cascade, most commonly through the BRAF V600E mutation or RET/PTC gene rearrangements. These oncogenic drivers promote proliferation while cells retain many features of thyroid differentiation, including iodine handling and thyroglobulin production, which underpins the utility of radioactive iodine and thyroglobulin monitoring. The tumour characteristically invades intrathyroidal lymphatics, giving multifocal disease and early spread to regional cervical lymph nodes, whereas distant haematogenous metastasis is comparatively uncommon.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign colloid or hyperplastic thyroid nodule","Follicular thyroid carcinoma or follicular adenoma","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Reactive cervical lymphadenopathy","Hashimoto thyroiditis with a dominant nodule","Metastatic deposit within the thyroid"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Neck ultrasound characterising the nodule and mapping cervical lymph node compartments","Ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration with cytology reported by the Bethesda system","Serum TSH and thyroid function to confirm euthyroid status before intervention","Serum thyroglobulin and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies as a baseline and post-operative marker","Molecular testing of indeterminate aspirates for BRAF and other MAPK-pathway alterations","Cross-sectional imaging of the neck when bulky nodal or extrathyroidal extension is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging mass, hoarseness, or vocal-cord palsy suggesting extrathyroidal invasion of the recurrent laryngeal nerve","Fixed mass with stridor, dysphagia, or airway compromise","Bulky or matted cervical lymphadenopathy","Evidence of distant metastasis such as pulmonary or skeletal deposits"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally excellent, especially in younger patients with intrathyroidal disease, where long-term survival is the norm; older age, extrathyroidal extension, large tumour size, and distant metastases are the main adverse factors.","pitfalls":["Assuming cervical lymph node involvement implies a poor outcome, when nodal disease is common and compatible with cure","Relying on nodule size alone rather than suspicious sonographic features to select nodules for aspiration","Overlooking multifocality and the contralateral lobe when planning the extent of surgery","Interpreting serum thyroglobulin without accounting for interfering anti-thyroglobulin antibodies"],"management":["Surgical resection is the mainstay, with thyroid lobectomy for low-risk intrathyroidal tumours or total thyroidectomy for larger or higher-risk disease, plus compartment-oriented neck dissection for proven nodal metastases","Selective radioactive iodine ablation after total thyroidectomy in intermediate- and high-risk disease to treat residual thyroid tissue and micrometastases","Levothyroxine for thyroid hormone replacement with TSH suppression titrated to recurrence risk","Long-term surveillance with serum thyroglobulin and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies together with neck ultrasound to detect recurrence","Targeted systemic therapy for radioiodine-refractory or progressive disease using kinase inhibitors, including BRAF- or RET-directed agents guided by molecular testing","Management within an endocrine and thyroid multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","WHO Classification of Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"papillary_tumor_pineal_region":{"name":"Papillary tumour of the pineal region","system":"Oncology / Neuro-oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTPR","papillary tumour of the pineal region","papillary tumor of the pineal region","CNS WHO grade 2 or 3 pineal tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of the CNS","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare neuroepithelial tumour of the pineal region occurring in both children and adults","Presents with features common to pineal masses: obstructive hydrocephalus and dorsal midbrain (Parinaud) signs from tectal compression","MRI shows a well-circumscribed, often partly cystic, enhancing pineal mass","Thought to arise from specialised ependymal cells of the subcommissural organ, giving a distinctive papillary architecture","Histology shows papillary and more solid epithelial-like areas; immunopositivity for cytokeratins (notably CK18) helps distinguish it from other pineal tumours","Behaviour is variable and it has a notable tendency to recur locally despite treatment","Management combines maximal safe resection with radiotherapy considered for residual or recurrent disease"],"pathophysiology":"Papillary tumour of the pineal region is a rare neuroepithelial neoplasm believed to originate from specialised ependymocytes of the subcommissural organ within the pineal region. It forms a circumscribed mass with a characteristic mixture of papillary structures, in which epithelial-like cells surround fibrovascular cores, and more densely cellular solid areas. The tumour cells express cytokeratins, a feature that helps separate it from pineal parenchymal tumours and ependymomas. Grading is imprecise but corresponds to CNS WHO grade 2 or 3, and the lesion is characterised clinically by a marked propensity for local recurrence. Its midline pineal location readily obstructs cerebrospinal fluid flow and compresses the dorsal midbrain.","additionalDifferentials":["Pineal parenchymal tumour of intermediate differentiation","Pineoblastoma","Pineocytoma","Ependymoma","Pineal region germ cell tumour","Metastasis to the pineal region"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced brain MRI to characterise the pineal mass and hydrocephalus","Craniospinal MRI to evaluate for dissemination","CSF and serum germ cell tumour markers to exclude germinoma","Histopathology showing papillary and solid epithelial-like areas","Cytokeratin (including CK18) immunohistochemistry to support the diagnosis","CSF cytology for staging where appropriate"],"redFlags":["Acute obstructive hydrocephalus with raised intracranial pressure","Progressive dorsal midbrain (Parinaud) signs and impaired consciousness","Local recurrence on surveillance imaging","Features suggesting dissemination along the neuraxis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is intermediate and difficult to predict, being limited chiefly by a high rate of local recurrence even after resection and radiotherapy. Extent of resection influences outcome, and long-term surveillance is required because recurrences may occur years after initial treatment.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying it as an ependymoma or pineal parenchymal tumour without cytokeratin staining","Underestimating the strong tendency to local recurrence and relaxing surveillance","Overlooking a coexisting germ cell tumour by not checking tumour markers","Delaying relief of obstructive hydrocephalus"],"management":["Maximal safe surgical resection is the primary treatment and the strongest determinant of local control","Cerebrospinal fluid diversion for obstructive hydrocephalus by endoscopic third ventriculostomy or ventricular shunting","Adjuvant radiotherapy, whether fractionated or stereotactic radiosurgery, given the marked tendency to local recurrence","Consideration of chemotherapy in recurrent or unresectable disease, where its role remains uncertain, within a neuro-oncology setting","Prolonged serial MRI surveillance because recurrence may occur years after initial treatment","Management within a neuro-oncology and neurosurgical multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System","Louis DN et al. The 2021 WHO Classification of Tumors of the Central Nervous System"],"crossLinks":[]},"papilloedema":{"name":"Papilloedema","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["papilledema","optic disc swelling from raised intracranial pressure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The term papilloedema refers specifically to optic disc swelling caused by raised intracranial pressure, and is characteristically bilateral","Central vision is often preserved early, while an enlarged blind spot and transient visual obscurations lasting seconds are typical","Accompanying symptoms of raised intracranial pressure include headache worse on waking or with Valsalva, nausea, vomiting, and pulsatile tinnitus, and a sixth nerve palsy may be a false localising sign","Fundus signs include blurred disc margins, hyperaemia, loss of the physiological cup, obscuration of vessels crossing the disc, and loss of spontaneous venous pulsation, with haemorrhages and cotton wool spots in more advanced cases","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension classically affects young women who are overweight, but a space-occupying lesion or venous sinus thrombosis must be excluded first","It must be distinguished from other causes of a swollen disc, such as optic neuritis, anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy, and pseudopapilloedema from optic disc drusen","Chronic untreated papilloedema leads to progressive optic atrophy and irreversible visual field loss"],"pathophysiology":"Papilloedema is passive swelling of the optic nerve head produced by raised intracranial pressure transmitted along the subarachnoid space surrounding the optic nerve. The elevated pressure impedes axoplasmic flow within the retinal ganglion cell axons at the optic disc, causing them to become distended, along with venous congestion and leakage that produce the characteristic disc appearance. Because the mechanism is mechanical rather than primarily ischaemic or inflammatory, central acuity is often spared until late. Persistently raised pressure eventually causes axonal loss and secondary optic atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Optic neuritis (usually unilateral, with pain and reduced acuity)","Anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy","Malignant hypertension with hypertensive retinopathy","Pseudopapilloedema from optic disc drusen or a crowded hypermetropic disc","Central retinal vein occlusion","Infiltrative or inflammatory optic disc swelling"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent neuroimaging (MRI or CT with venography) to exclude a mass lesion or venous sinus thrombosis","Lumbar puncture with opening pressure and cerebrospinal fluid analysis once imaging excludes a mass","Visual field testing to detect an enlarged blind spot and peripheral loss","Blood pressure measurement to exclude malignant hypertension","Optical coherence tomography and fundus photography to grade and monitor disc swelling","Assessment of colour vision and pupils to help distinguish from optic neuropathies"],"redFlags":["Bilateral disc swelling mandating urgent workup for raised intracranial pressure, including neuroimaging to exclude a tumour or venous sinus thrombosis","Progressive visual field loss or declining acuity indicating threatened optic nerve function","New focal neurological signs or a sixth nerve palsy","Very high intracranial or blood pressure requiring emergency management"],"prognosis":"Vision can be preserved if the underlying cause of raised intracranial pressure is treated promptly, but delayed or chronic papilloedema leads to optic atrophy and permanent visual field loss.","pitfalls":["Mistaking pseudopapilloedema from disc drusen for true papilloedema and initiating unnecessary invasive workup","Diagnosing idiopathic intracranial hypertension before excluding a mass lesion or venous thrombosis","Reassuring the patient because central acuity is normal while field loss progresses","Overlooking malignant hypertension as a cause of disc swelling"],"management":["Directed treatment of the underlying cause of raised intracranial pressure, such as resection of a mass lesion or anticoagulation for cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","In idiopathic intracranial hypertension, weight reduction combined with a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor such as acetazolamide as first-line medical therapy","Surgical intervention for medically refractory or vision-threatening disease, including cerebrospinal fluid shunting, optic nerve sheath fenestration or venous sinus stenting","Serial monitoring of optic nerve function with visual fields, optical coherence tomography and fundus assessment to detect progression to optic atrophy","Prompt neuroimaging with venography to exclude a mass or venous thrombosis before any lumbar puncture","Joint ophthalmology and neurology or neurosurgery management, with urgent referral when vision is threatened"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"papillon_lefevre_syndrome":{"name":"Papillon-Lefevre syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CTSC","cathepsin C deficiency","palmoplantar keratoderma with periodontitis","PLS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Combination of diffuse transgredient palmoplantar keratoderma with severe, early-onset (aggressive) periodontitis","Periodontal destruction affects both primary and permanent dentitions, often causing premature loss of teeth in childhood","The keratoderma may extend onto the dorsa of the hands and feet, elbows and knees, and can be associated with hyperhidrosis and malodour","Autosomal recessive; caused by loss-of-function mutations in CTSC encoding cathepsin C (dipeptidyl peptidase I)","Increased susceptibility to pyogenic skin and systemic infections, including a recognised risk of liver abscess","Consanguinity is often present in affected families","Management combines emollients/keratolytics and oral retinoids for the skin with intensive periodontal care/dental preservation for Papillon-Lefevre"],"pathophysiology":"Papillon-Lefevre syndrome is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in CTSC, which encodes cathepsin C (dipeptidyl peptidase I). Cathepsin C activates a range of serine proteases stored in immune and inflammatory cells, including neutrophil elastase, cathepsin G and granzymes. Its deficiency impairs neutrophil and immune-cell function, reducing the ability to control bacteria at the gingival margin and skin, which produces aggressive periodontitis and heightened susceptibility to pyogenic infection. In the epidermis, disturbed protease-mediated regulation of keratinocyte differentiation contributes to the diffuse palmoplantar keratoderma. The combined immune and epidermal effects explain the characteristic skin-and-gum phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Haim-Munk syndrome (allelic CTSC disorder with additional features)","Other diffuse hereditary palmoplantar keratodermas without periodontitis","Mal de Meleda","Aggressive periodontitis of other cause","Leucocyte adhesion deficiency and other neutrophil disorders","Acrodermatitis enteropathica"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of CTSC and assay of cathepsin C activity","Dental and periodontal assessment with orthopantomogram/radiographs of alveolar bone","Skin biopsy of keratoderma (hyperkeratosis with variable inflammation)","Microbiological evaluation and imaging if pyogenic or hepatic infection is suspected","Screening for consanguinity and family history","Genetic counselling for the family"],"redFlags":["Rapid periodontal breakdown with mobility or loss of teeth","Fever with right-upper-quadrant pain suggesting liver abscess","Recurrent or deep pyogenic skin infection","Systemic sepsis in a susceptible individual"],"prognosis":"The keratoderma is chronic but manageable; the major morbidity is early loss of the dentition, which intensive periodontal care and dental preservation can mitigate. With attention to infection risk, general prognosis is good, although recurrent pyogenic infection and periodontal disease persist.","pitfalls":["Treating the keratoderma without engaging dental services, leading to preventable tooth loss","Overlooking the increased infection risk, including occult liver abscess","Confusing it with common palmoplantar keratoderma and missing the periodontal association","Delaying genetic diagnosis in consanguineous families where recurrence risk is high"],"management":["Oral retinoids such as acitretin, which improve the palmoplantar keratoderma and can help preserve the dentition when started early","Intensive periodontal therapy with regular mechanical debridement, chlorhexidine mouthwash and courses of systemic antibiotics such as amoxicillin with metronidazole around tooth eruption","Topical keratolytics and emollients for the palmoplantar keratoderma, with attention to hyperhidrosis and secondary infection","Dental rehabilitation including extraction of hopeless teeth and prosthetic or implant-based restoration to maintain function","Prompt treatment of pyogenic skin and systemic infections with vigilance for liver abscess","Genetic counselling and CTSC testing for the family, with screening of at-risk relatives given autosomal recessive inheritance and frequent consanguinity","Coordinated dermatology, periodontology and clinical genetics care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"papular_mucinosis":{"name":"Papular Mucinosis","system":"Skin","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lichen myxoedematosus","Scleromyxoedema"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare primary cutaneous mucinosis characterised by dermal deposition of mucin rich in hyaluronic acid together with fibroblast proliferation, presenting as firm, waxy, closely-set papules.","The condition spans a spectrum from a localised papular form, lichen myxoedematosus, to a generalised sclerodermoid form, scleromyxoedema, in which diffuse skin induration develops.","Scleromyxoedema produces characteristic signs including a leonine facial appearance and the doughnut sign, a raised rim around a central depression over the proximal interphalangeal joints.","The generalised form is almost always accompanied by a monoclonal gammopathy, most often an immunoglobulin G paraprotein with a lambda light chain.","Despite the name, it is not caused by thyroid disease and thyroid function is normal, which distinguishes it from the myxoedema of hypothyroidism.","Systemic involvement in scleromyxoedema may affect the nervous system, heart, gastrointestinal tract, muscles and joints, and the dermato-neuro syndrome of fever, seizures and coma is a feared complication.","Histology classically shows the triad of upper and mid-dermal mucin deposition, increased fibroblasts and fibrosis, best demonstrated with mucin stains such as Alcian blue.","Intravenous immunoglobulin is a favoured treatment for scleromyxoedema, with thalidomide, systemic corticosteroids, bortezomib, lenalidomide and autologous stem-cell transplantation used in resistant disease."],"pathophysiology":"The disorder results from proliferation of dermal fibroblasts and their excessive production of glycosaminoglycans, predominantly hyaluronic acid, which accumulate in the upper and mid dermis and, in the generalised form, are accompanied by progressive dermal fibrosis. The consistent association of scleromyxoedema with a monoclonal gammopathy points to a pathogenic role for a circulating factor, and serum from affected patients has been shown to stimulate fibroblast proliferation, although the paraprotein itself does not appear to be directly responsible and its titre correlates poorly with disease activity. Pro-fibrotic and pro-inflammatory cytokines, including transforming growth factor beta, interleukin-1 and tumour necrosis factor, are implicated in driving the fibroblast response and matrix deposition. The mucin and fibrosis together produce the waxy papules and diffuse induration seen clinically, while extracutaneous mucin and vascular changes are thought to underlie the systemic manifestations, including the life-threatening dermato-neuro syndrome. Importantly the process is unrelated to the interstitial glycosaminoglycan accumulation of hypothyroid myxoedema, and the normal thyroid axis is a key point of distinction.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic sclerosis and other sclerodermoid disorders","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Scleroedema of Buschke","Generalised myxoedema of hypothyroidism","Systemic amyloidosis","Other primary cutaneous mucinoses such as reticular erythematous mucinosis","Eosinophilic fasciitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with mucin stains such as Alcian blue or colloidal iron","Serum protein electrophoresis with immunofixation to detect a paraprotein","Serum free light chain assay","Bone marrow examination when a monoclonal protein is identified","Thyroid function tests to exclude hypothyroid myxoedema","Renal function and a gadolinium exposure history to exclude nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Antinuclear and scleroderma-associated antibodies to exclude systemic sclerosis"],"redFlags":["Neurological deterioration with fever, seizures or coma suggesting the dermato-neuro syndrome","Cardiac symptoms indicating myocardial or conduction involvement","Dysphagia or gastrointestinal dysmotility","Evidence of progression of the monoclonal gammopathy towards myeloma","Rapidly progressive skin induration limiting movement or respiration"],"prognosis":"Scleromyxoedema tends to run a chronic, progressive course and can be fatal, particularly if the dermato-neuro syndrome supervenes, although intravenous immunoglobulin has improved outcomes. The localised papular form lacks a paraprotein and systemic involvement and follows a benign, if persistent, course.","pitfalls":["Confusing the condition with hypothyroid myxoedema on the basis of its name and overlooking the normal thyroid axis","Failing to investigate for and monitor an associated monoclonal gammopathy","Misdiagnosing the sclerodermoid form as systemic sclerosis","Overlooking systemic and neurological involvement in apparently skin-limited disease","Not excluding nephrogenic systemic fibrosis in a patient with renal impairment and gadolinium exposure"],"management":["Intravenous immunoglobulin as first-line therapy for scleromyxoedema, which has improved outcomes","Additional systemic options for refractory disease including thalidomide or lenalidomide, systemic corticosteroids, plasma-cell-directed regimens such as bortezomib-based therapy, and autologous stem-cell transplantation","For the localised papular form of lichen myxoedematosus, observation or topical and intralesional corticosteroids, as it lacks systemic involvement","Monitoring of the associated monoclonal gammopathy with serum protein electrophoresis and surveillance for progression to myeloma","Urgent treatment of the dermato-neuro syndrome of fever, seizures and coma with intravenous immunoglobulin, plasma exchange and corticosteroids","Joint dermatology and haematology management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard dermatology reference texts","Clinical reviews of cutaneous mucinoses and scleromyxoedema"],"crossLinks":[]},"parachute_mitral_valve":{"name":"parachute mitral valve","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["parachute mitral valve deformity","single papillary muscle mitral valve"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children, and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Parachute mitral valve has all chordae inserting into a single dominant papillary muscle, producing subvalvar inflow obstruction and congenital mitral stenosis.","It is the classic component of Shone complex alongside supravalvar mitral ring, subaortic stenosis and coarctation, so the other three lesions should always be sought.","Obstruction occurs at the interchordal level, so the mitral orifice can look adequate while flow is restricted between crowded chordae.","Parasternal short-axis imaging shows a single papillary muscle with limited leaflet opening and a narrow subvalvar funnel.","A true parachute with one papillary muscle is distinguished from a parachute-like asymmetric valve, which has two papillary muscles, one of them hypoplastic.","Symptoms track the gradient and include dyspnoea, poor feeding and pulmonary hypertension in infants.","Surgery is technically difficult; splitting the papillary muscle or fenestrating chordae may relieve obstruction, but replacement is sometimes required."],"pathophysiology":"In a true parachute mitral valve all chordae tendineae converge and insert into a single papillary muscle, while in the parachute-like asymmetric variant they insert into one dominant papillary muscle with a hypoplastic second muscle. The interchordal spaces are markedly reduced, so blood entering the left ventricle must pass through the crowded, narrowed spaces between chordae, generating functional stenosis at the subvalvar level even when the annular orifice appears normal. The lesion frequently coexists with other left-heart obstructive anomalies as part of Shone complex, compounding the inflow and outflow obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Supravalvar mitral ring","Anomalous mitral arcade","Cor triatriatum","Rheumatic mitral stenosis","Congenital mitral valve dysplasia","Double-orifice mitral valve"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography with short-axis papillary muscle count and Doppler inflow gradients","Transoesophageal and 3D echocardiography","Cardiac MRI","Cardiac catheterisation","Aortic arch imaging to screen for Shone-complex lesions","Electrocardiography for right ventricular hypertrophy and left atrial enlargement"],"redFlags":["Severe pulmonary hypertension","Heart failure or failure to thrive in infancy","Severe inflow gradient with low cardiac output","Coexisting critical left-heart outflow obstruction"],"prognosis":"Isolated mild parachute valve may be well tolerated, but significant stenosis, especially within Shone complex, carries higher surgical risk and a tendency to residual or recurrent obstruction requiring reintervention.","pitfalls":["Overlooking subvalvar obstruction when the annulus and orifice appear normal","Failing to identify associated Shone-complex lesions","Confusing the lesion with supravalvar mitral ring or cor triatriatum","Assuming a successful repair is durable when restenosis is common"],"management":["Surgical relief of significant subvalvar mitral stenosis by valve repair, splitting fused chordae and the papillary muscle, or mitral valve replacement when repair is not feasible","Concurrent correction of associated Shone-complex lesions such as supravalvar mitral ring resection, subaortic stenosis relief and coarctation repair","Medical management of pulmonary congestion and heart failure with diuretics while optimising for surgery","Treatment of secondary pulmonary hypertension and monitoring of its progression","Serial echocardiographic surveillance of the inflow gradient, ventricular function and pulmonary pressures, with reintervention for residual or recurrent obstruction","Management within a paediatric and congenital cardiology and cardiac-surgery multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children, and Adolescents","Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","Feigenbaum's Echocardiography"],"crossLinks":[]},"paracoccidioidomycosis":{"name":"Paracoccidioidomycosis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Paracoccidioides brasiliensis","Paracoccidioides lutzii","South American blastomycosis","Lutz-Splendore-Almeida disease","PCM"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases / Manson's Tropical Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Endemic to humid rural areas of Central and South America, especially Brazil, Colombia, Venezuela and Argentina, and typically affects male agricultural workers exposed to soil","Caused by the thermally dimorphic fungi Paracoccidioides brasiliensis and P. lutzii, acquired by inhalation of conidia; the acute/subacute (juvenile) form and the chronic (adult) form behave very differently","The chronic adult form is dominated by pulmonary disease with mucocutaneous lesions of the mouth, gums and oropharynx, often with a characteristic mulberry-like haemorrhagic stippling","The acute juvenile form presents with prominent reticuloendothelial involvement: generalised lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly and marrow involvement, and is more aggressive","Microscopy of pus, sputum or tissue classically shows the multiple-budding 'pilot's wheel' or 'Mickey Mouse' yeast form, which is diagnostic","Serology (immunodiffusion, complement fixation) supports diagnosis and helps monitor treatment response and relapse","Prolonged therapy with azole antifungals is standard for most cases, with amphotericin-based therapy reserved for severe or disseminated disease and long consolidation required to prevent relapse"],"pathophysiology":"Paracoccidioidomycosis follows inhalation of airborne conidia of Paracoccidioides species from contaminated soil in endemic zones. At body temperature the fungus converts to its pathogenic multiply-budding yeast form and establishes a primary pulmonary focus, which may remain latent for years or decades before reactivating, often when cell-mediated immunity wanes. A vigorous granulomatous response tends to contain infection in the chronic adult form, whereas an inadequate Th1 response permits lymphohaematogenous dissemination in the acute juvenile form. Chronic pulmonary involvement produces progressive fibrosis, and mucosal seeding gives rise to the destructive oral and laryngeal lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary tuberculosis (frequently co-exists and mimics the chronic form)","Histoplasmosis and other endemic dimorphic mycoses","Oral or laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma","Leishmaniasis (mucocutaneous)","Lymphoma or other causes of generalised lymphadenopathy in the juvenile form","Sarcoidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Direct microscopy of sputum, mucosal scrapings or aspirated pus with potassium hydroxide preparation for multiple-budding yeasts","Fungal culture on appropriate media (slow-growing) with demonstration of dimorphism","Histopathology of biopsy material with special fungal stains showing the pilot's-wheel yeast","Serology by immunodiffusion or complement fixation for diagnosis and follow-up","Chest imaging to characterise the pulmonary infiltrates and fibrosis","Screening for co-existing tuberculosis and for HIV in atypical or severe presentations"],"redFlags":["Progressive dyspnoea and respiratory compromise from advanced pulmonary fibrosis","Laryngeal or tracheal involvement threatening the airway","Disseminated juvenile-type disease with marrow failure or extensive lymphadenopathy","Central nervous system or adrenal involvement","Relapse or deterioration on treatment suggesting inadequate duration or immunosuppression"],"prognosis":"With prolonged antifungal therapy most patients improve, but chronic pulmonary fibrosis and mucosal scarring can leave permanent disability; the acute juvenile and disseminated forms carry higher mortality, and relapse is common if treatment is stopped early.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the chronic form as tuberculosis or cancer and delaying antifungal therapy","Stopping treatment when symptoms resolve rather than continuing prolonged consolidation, leading to relapse","Overlooking co-existing tuberculosis, which can worsen if only one condition is treated","Failing to recognise adrenal insufficiency arising from adrenal involvement","Not screening for underlying immunosuppression in unusually severe disease"],"management":["Prolonged antifungal therapy with an azole such as itraconazole for mild-to-moderate disease as the mainstay","Intravenous amphotericin B for severe or disseminated disease, with step-down to an oral azole or a sulfonamide such as co-trimoxazole for the long maintenance phase","Serological monitoring by immunodiffusion or complement fixation to assess treatment response and detect relapse, continuing therapy until cure criteria are met","Nutritional and supportive care, particularly in the wasting acute juvenile form, with management of mucosal, laryngeal and respiratory involvement","Surveillance for and management of residual pulmonary fibrosis and mucosal scarring","Screening for co-existing tuberculosis and for HIV in severe or atypical presentations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"paraganglioma":{"name":"Paraganglioma","system":"Endocrine system / Autonomic nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["extra-adrenal phaeochromocytoma","glomus tumour (head and neck paraganglioma)","chemodectoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Neuroendocrine tumour of extra-adrenal paraganglia; sympathetic (thoracic, abdominal, pelvic) tumours are often catecholamine-secreting, whereas parasympathetic (head and neck) tumours are usually non-secretory","Head and neck paragangliomas (carotid body, jugulotympanic, vagal) typically present as slowly growing painless masses with cranial nerve palsies or pulsatile tinnitus rather than catecholamine excess","Functional tumours cause paroxysmal or sustained hypertension, headache, palpitations and sweating, mirroring phaeochromocytoma","Up to 40 per cent are hereditary; germline succinate dehydrogenase (SDHB, SDHD, SDHC and related) mutations are commonest, so genetic testing and counselling are recommended in essentially all patients","SDHB mutations carry a notably higher risk of malignant (metastatic) disease and of extra-adrenal, abdominal tumours","Biochemical screening with plasma free or 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines is essential before any intervention; some tumours secrete predominantly noradrenaline or dopamine","Somatostatin receptor PET (DOTATATE) is highly sensitive, particularly for SDHB-related and metastatic disease, complementing MIBG and cross-sectional imaging","Preoperative alpha-adrenergic blockade, established before any beta-blockade, is mandatory for secretory tumours to prevent intraoperative hypertensive crisis"],"pathophysiology":"Paragangliomas arise from neural crest-derived chromaffin or glomus cells of the autonomic paraganglia. Sympathetic paraganglia along the paravertebral and para-aortic chains may synthesise and release catecholamines, producing the adrenergic syndrome, whereas parasympathetic paraganglia of the head and neck rarely secrete and instead cause symptoms through local mass effect. A large fraction are driven by germline mutations, most often in the succinate dehydrogenase complex genes; loss of SDH function causes succinate accumulation and a pseudohypoxic state that stabilises hypoxia-inducible factors and promotes tumorigenesis. Malignancy is defined by the presence of metastases at sites where paraganglia are not normally found.","additionalDifferentials":["Adrenal phaeochromocytoma","Other causes of paroxysmal symptoms and secondary hypertension (anxiety or panic, thyrotoxicosis)","Head and neck schwannoma, nodal mass or carotid aneurysm mimicking a carotid body tumour","Metastatic neuroendocrine tumour from another primary","Medullary thyroid carcinoma within a hereditary syndrome","Neuroblastoma or ganglioneuroma in younger patients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma free metanephrines or 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines and catecholamines","Plasma or urinary methoxytyramine where a dopamine-secreting tumour is suspected","Contrast CT or MRI of the relevant region (neck, thorax, abdomen, pelvis)","Somatostatin receptor PET/CT (DOTATATE), with MIBG scintigraphy in selected cases","Germline genetic testing (SDHx and extended panel) with genetic counselling","SDHB immunohistochemistry on resected tissue to guide genetic evaluation","Whole-body imaging for multifocal or metastatic disease, especially in SDHB carriers"],"redFlags":["Hypertensive crisis, arrhythmia or catecholamine-induced cardiomyopathy","Rapid tumour growth, local invasion or metastatic disease (SDHB association)","New cranial neuropathies from an enlarging skull-base or neck tumour","Symptoms provoked by anaesthesia, biopsy or tumour manipulation without prior blockade","Family history of paraganglioma or phaeochromocytoma indicating a hereditary syndrome"],"prognosis":"Benign, completely resected paragangliomas have an excellent prognosis, but metastatic disease, which is more frequent with SDHB mutations, reduces survival although its course can still be prolonged; lifelong surveillance is required because of multifocal and recurrent disease.","pitfalls":["Biopsying or operating on a secretory tumour without excluding catecholamine excess and without alpha-blockade, precipitating a hypertensive crisis","Giving beta-blockade before adequate alpha-blockade, causing unopposed alpha vasoconstriction","Assuming head and neck paragangliomas are always non-secretory and omitting biochemical screening","Not offering germline genetic testing, thereby missing hereditary and malignancy risk","Relying on histology alone to predict malignancy, which is defined by the presence of metastasis"],"management":["Complete surgical resection is the definitive treatment for localised paraganglioma","For catecholamine-secreting tumours, preoperative alpha-adrenergic blockade with phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin followed by beta-blockade, together with volume expansion, before any surgery or intervention","Systemic treatment of metastatic disease with radionuclide therapy such as I-131 MIBG or lutetium-177 DOTATATE peptide receptor therapy, cytotoxic chemotherapy or external-beam radiotherapy","Germline genetic testing across the SDHx and extended panel with genetic counselling, recommended in essentially all patients","Lifelong biochemical and imaging surveillance for multifocal, recurrent and metastatic disease, particularly with SDHB mutations","Cascade genetic testing and screening of at-risk relatives","Management within an endocrine and endocrine-surgery multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline on phaeochromocytoma and paraganglioma","WHO Classification of Tumours: Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"paraganglioma_head_neck":{"name":"Head and neck paraganglioma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["carotid body tumour","glomus tumour","chemodectoma","glomus jugulare","glomus tympanicum","glomus vagale"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Arise from parasympathetic paraganglia at the carotid body, jugulotympanic, vagal and laryngeal sites and are usually non-secretory, unlike the sympathetic abdominal and adrenal tumours","A carotid body tumour presents as a painless, slowly enlarging mass at the carotid bifurcation that is mobile from side to side but not vertically and may transmit a pulsation or bruit","Jugulotympanic tumours cause pulsatile tinnitus, conductive hearing loss and a retrotympanic reddish mass, whereas vagal tumours may cause hoarseness or lower cranial nerve palsies","A substantial proportion are hereditary, most often linked to succinate dehydrogenase (SDHx) subunit mutations, particularly SDHD and SDHB","Even when clinically silent, biochemical screening for catecholamine excess with plasma or urinary metanephrines is advised because a minority secrete, chiefly noradrenaline and normetanephrine","Cross-sectional imaging shows an intensely vascular enhancing mass with flow voids giving a salt-and-pepper appearance, and functional imaging helps detect multifocal disease","Management is individualised between surgical resection, radiotherapy and watchful surveillance depending on size, site, cranial nerve risk and genetics","SDHB carriers warrant particular vigilance because of a higher risk of malignant and metastatic behaviour"],"pathophysiology":"Head and neck paragangliomas are neuroendocrine tumours derived from paraganglia of the parasympathetic nervous system distributed along the carotid, jugulotympanic and vagal territories. In contrast to their sympathetic abdominal and adrenal counterparts, most are non-chromaffin and do not oversecrete catecholamines. A large fraction are hereditary, driven by germline mutations in succinate dehydrogenase complex subunit genes such as SDHD, SDHB, SDHC, SDHAF2 and SDHA, which impair mitochondrial complex II and stabilise hypoxia-inducible factors, promoting a pseudohypoxic, pro-angiogenic state and slow tumour growth. Chronic hypoxia, for example at high altitude, can also enlarge the carotid body. Much of the morbidity arises from local mass effect on adjacent cranial nerves and great vessels.","additionalDifferentials":["Phaeochromocytoma or sympathetic abdominal and thoracic paraganglioma","Carotid artery aneurysm","Branchial cleft cyst","Vagal or hypoglossal schwannoma or neurofibroma","Metastatic cervical lymphadenopathy","Deep lobe parotid or other salivary gland tumour","Skull base meningioma at the jugular foramen"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma free or 24-hour urinary metanephrines and normetanephrines to detect secretory tumours before any intervention","MRI of the neck and skull base with contrast for the characteristic vascular salt-and-pepper appearance and cranial nerve relationships","CT to define bony erosion at the skull base and jugular foramen","Somatostatin-receptor PET or metaiodobenzylguanidine imaging to identify multifocal or metastatic disease","Germline genetic testing for SDHx and related susceptibility genes with genetic counselling","Digital subtraction angiography with consideration of preoperative embolisation"],"redFlags":["Biochemical evidence of catecholamine excess requiring alpha-adrenergic blockade before surgery or anaesthesia to avoid a hypertensive crisis","Rapid growth, pain or new lower cranial nerve palsies suggesting aggressive or malignant behaviour","Multifocal disease or a positive family history indicating a hereditary syndrome, especially SDHB, with metastatic risk","Skull base extension threatening hearing, swallowing or the airway"],"prognosis":"Most head and neck paragangliomas are slow-growing and benign, and long-term control is achievable with surgery, radiotherapy or surveillance; however cranial nerve deficits may result from the tumour or its treatment, and SDHB-related tumours carry a higher risk of metastatic spread, so lifelong follow-up is warranted.","pitfalls":["Biopsying a pulsatile neck mass before excluding a vascular paraganglioma, risking haemorrhage","Proceeding to surgery without biochemical screening, precipitating an intraoperative catecholamine surge in a secretory tumour","Overlooking the strong hereditary basis and failing to offer SDHx genetic testing and family screening","Assuming unilateral single disease without imaging for synchronous multifocal tumours"],"management":["Individualised choice between surgical resection, radiotherapy including stereotactic radiosurgery, and active surveillance, balancing tumour growth against the risk of cranial nerve injury","Active surveillance with serial imaging for small, slow-growing or multifocal tumours, especially in older patients","Preoperative angiographic embolisation to reduce vascularity of larger tumours before resection","Biochemical screening for catecholamine excess before intervention, with alpha- then beta-adrenergic blockade if a tumour proves secretory","Rehabilitation of cranial nerve deficits, including speech and swallowing therapy for lower cranial nerve palsies","Germline SDHx genetic testing with counselling and lifelong imaging surveillance for multifocal and metastatic disease","Cascade screening of at-risk relatives and management within a skull-base multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline on Phaeochromocytoma and Paraganglioma","DeGroot's Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"paraganglioma_sympathetic":{"name":"Sympathetic paraganglioma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["extra-adrenal phaeochromocytoma","catecholamine-secreting paraganglioma","sympathetic paraganglioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sympathetic paragangliomas arise from extra-adrenal chromaffin tissue along the paravertebral and para-aortic sympathetic chain, most commonly in the abdomen","Catecholamine excess produces episodic or sustained hypertension with the classic triad of headache, palpitations and sweating","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating elevated plasma or urinary metanephrines rather than measuring catecholamines directly","A high proportion are hereditary, with succinate dehydrogenase (SDHx) mutations, particularly SDHB, being the most important predisposing genes","SDHB-related tumours carry a notably higher risk of malignant, metastatic behaviour","Functional imaging with gallium DOTATATE positron emission tomography is highly sensitive for localisation and detecting metastatic disease","Adequate alpha-adrenergic blockade must precede any surgery or biopsy to prevent a catecholamine crisis","Germline genetic testing and lifelong surveillance are recommended given the strong hereditary component and multifocal potential"],"pathophysiology":"Sympathetic paragangliomas are catecholamine-producing tumours derived from chromaffin cells of the extra-adrenal sympathetic paraganglia. Unregulated synthesis and release of noradrenaline and adrenaline (and their metabolites, the metanephrines) drive the cardiovascular and metabolic manifestations of catecholamine excess. A large fraction are linked to germline mutations, especially in the succinate dehydrogenase complex genes, which impair cellular oxygen sensing and mitochondrial metabolism and promote a pseudohypoxic tumorigenic state. SDHB mutations in particular confer a heightened tendency to metastatic spread.","additionalDifferentials":["Adrenal phaeochromocytoma","Essential hypertension with anxiety or panic disorder","Parasympathetic (head and neck) paraganglioma, typically non-secretory","Hyperthyroidism","Carcinoid syndrome","Medication or drug-induced sympathetic overactivity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma free or 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines","Cross-sectional imaging of chest, abdomen and pelvis to localise the tumour","Functional imaging such as gallium DOTATATE positron emission tomography","Germline genetic testing including the succinate dehydrogenase genes","Chromogranin A as an adjunctive tumour marker","Cardiac and blood-pressure assessment before intervention"],"redFlags":["Hypertensive crisis with headache, chest pain or arrhythmia","Catecholamine-induced cardiomyopathy or pulmonary oedema","Metastatic disease, especially with an SDHB mutation","Precipitation of a crisis by unblocked surgery, biopsy or certain drugs","Family history suggesting a hereditary paraganglioma syndrome"],"prognosis":"Completely resected localised tumours, after appropriate perioperative adrenergic blockade, generally have a favourable outlook, but SDHB-associated and metastatic disease carries a worse prognosis and mandates long-term, often lifelong, biochemical and imaging surveillance.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to surgery or biopsy without adequate preoperative alpha-blockade, risking a catecholamine crisis","Relying on catecholamine measurement instead of metanephrines and missing intermittently secreting tumours","Failing to offer germline genetic testing given the high heritability","Underestimating the malignant potential of SDHB-related tumours","Assuming a single lesion when disease may be multifocal"],"management":["Complete surgical resection is the definitive treatment for localised disease","Mandatory preoperative alpha-adrenergic blockade with phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin followed by beta-blockade, with volume and salt loading, to prevent intraoperative hypertensive crisis","Systemic therapy for metastatic disease with I-131 MIBG or lutetium-177 DOTATATE radionuclide therapy, cytotoxic chemotherapy or external-beam radiotherapy","Long-term biochemical surveillance with plasma or urinary fractionated metanephrines to detect recurrence or metastasis","Germline genetic testing including the succinate dehydrogenase genes, with genetic counselling","Cascade screening of at-risk relatives given the high heritability","Management within an endocrine and endocrine-surgery multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"paragonimiasis":{"name":"Paragonimiasis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Paragonimus westermani","lung fluke infection","oriental lung fluke","pulmonary distomiasis","endemic haemoptysis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Paragonimiasis is caused by lung flukes of the genus Paragonimus, chiefly P. westermani, endemic in East and Southeast Asia, with other species in Africa and the Americas","Infection follows eating raw, undercooked or pickled freshwater crabs or crayfish, or in some regions raw meat of paratenic hosts, containing infective metacercariae","After ingestion the larvae penetrate the gut and diaphragm and produce migratory subcutaneous swellings before settling in the lungs to form parasitic cysts","The pulmonary syndrome mimics tuberculosis: chronic cough, chest pain and haemoptysis with rusty-brown or blood-streaked sputum, often in an otherwise well patient","Ectopic (extrapulmonary) disease is important, particularly cerebral paragonimiasis, which can cause seizures, focal deficits and space-occupying lesions","Diagnosis rests on finding the characteristic operculated eggs in sputum or stool, supported by serology, eosinophilia and imaging showing cysts, ring shadows or pleural effusion","Praziquantel is the treatment of choice for paragonimiasis, with corticosteroid cover considered in cerebral disease to limit inflammatory reaction"],"pathophysiology":"Ingested metacercariae excyst in the small intestine, penetrate the intestinal wall into the peritoneal cavity, and migrate through the diaphragm into the pleural space and lungs, where paired adult flukes become enclosed in fibrous cysts that communicate with bronchioles. Eggs are coughed up and either expectorated or swallowed and passed in stool. Local inflammation, cyst formation and rupture into airways produce the chronic cough and haemoptysis, while pleural involvement causes effusion. Aberrant migration of larvae to the brain, subcutaneous tissue, abdomen or other sites produces ectopic disease, with cerebral involvement being the most serious.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary tuberculosis (closely mimicked and often co-endemic)","Bronchiectasis or chronic bacterial lung infection","Lung abscess or necrotising pneumonia","Bronchogenic carcinoma","Other causes of haemoptysis and pleural effusion","Neurocysticercosis or other space-occupying lesions (for cerebral disease)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Microscopy of sputum for characteristic operculated eggs (repeated samples improve yield)","Stool microscopy for swallowed eggs when sputum is negative","Serology (antibody detection), useful especially in ectopic disease and light infection","Full blood count for eosinophilia","Chest imaging (radiograph/CT) for cysts, ring shadows, infiltrates or pleural effusion","Brain imaging when cerebral involvement is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neurological features (seizures, focal deficit, raised intracranial pressure) suggesting cerebral paragonimiasis","Massive or persistent haemoptysis","Large pleural effusion or empyema causing respiratory compromise","Diagnostic confusion with tuberculosis leading to inappropriate anti-TB therapy","Extensive or multiorgan ectopic disease"],"prognosis":"Pulmonary paragonimiasis usually responds well to anthelmintic therapy with a good outlook, but cerebral and other ectopic disease can leave lasting neurological deficits, and misdiagnosis as tuberculosis often delays effective treatment.","pitfalls":["Treating the patient as smear-negative tuberculosis and overlooking lung fluke eggs in sputum","Relying on a single sputum sample, which may miss eggs","Not considering cerebral paragonimiasis in an endemic patient with seizures","Neglecting dietary counselling, allowing reinfection from raw crab or crayfish","Omitting corticosteroid cover in cerebral disease where an inflammatory reaction to dying flukes is a concern"],"management":["Anthelmintic therapy with praziquantel as first-line treatment, with triclabendazole as an alternative","Adjunctive corticosteroids to control the inflammatory reaction in cerebral disease and heavy infection","Anticonvulsants for seizures and neurosurgical intervention for selected space-occupying cerebral cysts","Drainage of significant pleural effusion or empyema with supportive respiratory care","Follow-up sputum and stool microscopy to confirm parasitological cure, given frequent misdiagnosis as tuberculosis","Patient education to avoid raw or undercooked freshwater crabs and crayfish, with public health notification"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"paralytic_shellfish_poisoning":{"name":"Paralytic shellfish poisoning (saxitoxin)","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PSP","saxitoxin poisoning","shellfish neurotoxin poisoning","red tide shellfish poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / WHO envenoming guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Follows ingestion of bivalve shellfish that have accumulated saxitoxins from toxic dinoflagellate blooms (red tides)","Symptoms typically begin within minutes to an hour with perioral and facial paraesthesiae and tingling of the extremities","Progresses to ataxia, dysarthria, limb weakness and, in severe cases, ascending flaccid paralysis with respiratory failure","As with other sodium-channel toxins, consciousness is characteristically preserved throughout the paralysis","There is no antidote; management is supportive with airway protection and mechanical ventilation until toxin clearance","Early gastrointestinal decontamination may be considered soon after ingestion in a protected airway","The toxins are heat-stable, so cooking does not make contaminated shellfish safe"],"pathophysiology":"Saxitoxin and related paralytic shellfish toxins are produced by marine dinoflagellates and concentrated in filter-feeding bivalves. After ingestion, the toxins bind voltage-gated sodium channels on excitable membranes, blocking sodium conductance and inhibiting nerve and muscle action potentials. This causes early sensory disturbance followed by progressive flaccid paralysis, including of respiratory muscles, which is the principal life threat. The toxins are heat-stable and are not destroyed by normal cooking, and central nervous system function is relatively spared, so consciousness is retained despite paralysis.","additionalDifferentials":["Tetrodotoxin (pufferfish) poisoning","Ciguatera poisoning","Neurotoxic or amnesic shellfish poisoning","Guillain-Barre syndrome or botulism","Blue-ringed octopus envenomation","Scombroid poisoning (though this is histaminic rather than paralytic)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on shellfish ingestion and characteristic neurology","Continuous respiratory monitoring and serial forced vital capacity","Pulse oximetry and arterial blood gases for ventilatory failure","Electrocardiography and cardiac monitoring","Electrolytes and baseline bloods to exclude alternative causes","Toxin assay of shellfish or biological samples where available, and public health notification"],"redFlags":["Progressive limb, bulbar or respiratory muscle weakness","Declining respiratory function or oxygen saturation","Rapid symptom onset after a large ingestion","Inability to swallow or protect the airway","Cluster of cases suggesting a contaminated batch requiring public health action"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition and ventilatory support through the critical period, recovery is usually complete within days; untreated severe cases can be fatal from respiratory failure, particularly where advanced support is unavailable.","pitfalls":["Assuming thorough cooking removes the risk, as the toxins are heat-stable","Underestimating progression from paraesthesiae to respiratory failure","Mistaking preserved consciousness for a mild or non-organic presentation","Failing to notify public health authorities to prevent further poisonings"],"management":["Supportive care centred on airway protection and mechanical ventilation through the period of respiratory-muscle paralysis, as there is no antidote","Early gastrointestinal decontamination with activated charcoal, and gastric lavage, soon after ingestion once the airway is protected","Continuous respiratory monitoring with serial forced vital capacity and cardiac monitoring to anticipate ventilatory failure","Intravenous fluids and general intensive supportive care until toxin clearance","Careful neurological observation and reassurance, recognising that consciousness is preserved despite paralysis","Public health notification and identification of the implicated shellfish source to prevent further cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","WHO/food-safety guidance on marine biotoxins"],"crossLinks":[]},"paramyotonia_congenita":{"name":"Paramyotonia congenita","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMC","paramyotonia congenita of von Eulenburg","SCN4A","sodium channelopathy of muscle","cold-induced paradoxical myotonia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Distinguished by paradoxical myotonia, in which stiffness worsens rather than improves with repeated activity, the opposite of the warm-up seen in myotonia congenita","Symptoms are markedly precipitated or aggravated by cold exposure, classically affecting the face, eyelids, tongue and hands","Cold-induced stiffness can be followed by a period of flaccid weakness that may persist for some time after rewarming","Caused by gain-of-function mutations in the SCN4A skeletal-muscle sodium channel gene and inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern","It sits on a clinical continuum with hyperkalaemic periodic paralysis and the potassium-aggravated myotonias, all allelic SCN4A disorders","Management combines avoidance of cold and other triggers with sodium-channel-blocking antimyotonic agents when symptoms are troublesome","Bedside cooling of a hand, or asking the patient to repeatedly forcefully close the eyes, can reproduce the paradoxical worsening"],"pathophysiology":"Paramyotonia congenita is caused by gain-of-function mutations in SCN4A, encoding the skeletal-muscle voltage-gated sodium channel Nav1.4. The mutant channels show impaired or slowed inactivation, and this defect is temperature sensitive so that cooling exaggerates the persistent sodium current. Incomplete inactivation produces sustained membrane depolarisation, which at moderate levels causes repetitive firing perceived as myotonia and, when depolarisation becomes severe, inactivates the remaining channels to render the fibre inexcitable and weak. Because the abnormality worsens with sustained activity and with cold, patients experience the characteristic paradoxical, cold-sensitive myotonia followed by weakness rather than the warm-up improvement of chloride channelopathies.","additionalDifferentials":["Myotonia congenita","Hyperkalaemic periodic paralysis","Potassium-aggravated myotonias","Myotonic dystrophy types 1 and 2","Andersen-Tawil syndrome","Schwartz-Jampel syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electromyography with myotonic discharges, including recordings before and after muscle cooling","Short exercise electromyography protocol with cooling, showing the characteristic decrement pattern","Serum creatine kinase and interictal potassium","Targeted SCN4A genetic testing","Assessment to exclude myotonic dystrophy and its systemic features","Muscle biopsy only where diagnostic uncertainty persists"],"redFlags":["Prolonged or severe cold-induced weakness impairing safe function","Respiratory or bulbar involvement during a severe episode","Susceptibility to anaesthetic complications with depolarising muscle relaxants and hypothermia","Emergence of a fixed myopathy, which would prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Paramyotonia congenita is a non-progressive disorder compatible with a normal lifespan, and although cold-triggered stiffness and weakness can be disabling in cold climates or occupations, symptoms are usually manageable with trigger avoidance and antimyotonic therapy.","pitfalls":["Confusing the paradoxical cold-worsened myotonia with the warm-up improvement of myotonia congenita","Failing to test after cooling, so mild or intermittent myotonia is missed on standard examination or electromyography","Not recognising the overlap with hyperkalaemic periodic paralysis when weakness dominates","Overlooking anaesthetic risks from cold theatres and depolarising neuromuscular blockade"],"management":["Avoidance of triggers, particularly cold exposure and sustained strenuous activity","Sodium-channel-blocking antimyotonic therapy, notably mexiletine, for troublesome myotonia","Measures to prevent associated hyperkalaemic weakness, such as avoiding potassium loads and prolonged fasting, with carbonic anhydrase inhibitors in overlap cases","Anaesthetic precautions including maintaining normothermia and avoiding depolarising muscle relaxants, with awareness of malignant-hyperthermia susceptibility","Genetic counselling given autosomal dominant inheritance of the SCN4A mutation","Long-term neuromuscular specialist follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: Paramyotonia Congenita and SCN4A-related disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"paraneoplastic_cerebellar_degeneration":{"name":"Paraneoplastic cerebellar degeneration","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCD","paraneoplastic cerebellar syndrome","anti-Yo cerebellar degeneration","subacute cerebellar degeneration"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a subacute, progressive pancerebellar syndrome with gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria, nystagmus, and often severe truncal instability over weeks to months","Frequently precedes the diagnosis of the causative tumour, so its recognition should prompt a systematic malignancy search","Anti-Yo (PCA-1) antibodies are strongly associated with gynaecological and breast cancers, typically in women","Anti-Hu and anti-Tr/DNER, anti-Ri, and anti-P/Q calcium-channel antibodies are linked with small-cell lung cancer, Hodgkin lymphoma, and other tumours","MRI is often normal early, later showing cerebellar atrophy, while antibody testing and tumour screening establish the diagnosis","The immune attack causes largely irreversible loss of Purkinje cells, so early treatment offers the best, though often limited, chance of stabilisation","Management combines treatment of the underlying tumour with immunotherapy"],"pathophysiology":"Paraneoplastic cerebellar degeneration arises when a systemic tumour expresses antigens also present in cerebellar neurons, most notably Purkinje cells. The immune response mounted against the tumour cross-reacts with these neuronal antigens, producing an immune-mediated attack on the cerebellum. Antibodies such as anti-Yo, anti-Hu, anti-Ri, and anti-Tr/DNER serve as diagnostic markers of specific antigen-tumour associations, while cytotoxic T-cell mechanisms are believed to drive the neuronal destruction in antibodies targeting intracellular antigens. Progressive, often irreversible loss of Purkinje cells underlies the clinical picture, explaining why cerebellar atrophy appears on later imaging and why neurological recovery is usually incomplete even when the tumour is treated.","additionalDifferentials":["Alcoholic or nutritional (thiamine) cerebellar degeneration","Immune-mediated cerebellar ataxia (e.g. anti-GAD, gluten ataxia)","Toxic cerebellar syndromes (e.g. phenytoin, chemotherapy)","Multiple sclerosis or other demyelination","Hereditary and sporadic degenerative ataxias","Cerebellar metastasis or posterior fossa lesion","Miller Fisher syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and cerebrospinal fluid paraneoplastic/onconeural antibody panel (anti-Yo, anti-Hu, anti-Ri, anti-Tr/DNER, anti-P/Q)","MRI brain to exclude structural lesions and assess for cerebellar atrophy","CT of chest, abdomen, and pelvis and FDG-PET to identify an occult tumour","Mammography and pelvic/gynaecological imaging where anti-Yo is positive","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis showing inflammatory changes or oligoclonal bands","Bloods including thiamine, vitamin, and metabolic screen to exclude mimics"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive ataxia disabling gait within weeks","Positive onconeural antibodies indicating a high likelihood of underlying malignancy","Brainstem or bulbar involvement compromising swallow and airway","Accompanying encephalomyelitis or autonomic features suggesting a wider paraneoplastic process"],"prognosis":"Neurological outcome is generally poor because Purkinje cell loss is largely irreversible; treatment of the tumour and immunotherapy may halt progression but rarely restores function, and prognosis is heavily influenced by the underlying cancer.","pitfalls":["Attributing subacute ataxia to alcohol or degeneration and missing an occult tumour","Stopping the malignancy search after initial imaging is negative rather than repeating surveillance","Delaying immunotherapy and tumour treatment while awaiting antibody results","Expecting full neurological recovery once the cancer is treated"],"management":["Prompt identification and definitive treatment of the underlying malignancy, which is the most important intervention","Immunotherapy with corticosteroids, intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange, escalating to rituximab or cyclophosphamide, started early before irreversible Purkinje cell loss","Symptomatic and rehabilitative care with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, gait aids and speech therapy for ataxia and dysarthria","Repeated tumour surveillance including FDG-PET when initial screening is negative, since the syndrome often precedes the cancer diagnosis","Realistic counselling that neurological recovery is usually limited even with treatment","Coordinated neurology and oncology multidisciplinary management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"paraneoplastic_encephalomyelitis":{"name":"paraneoplastic encephalomyelitis","system":"nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anti-Hu syndrome","paraneoplastic encephalomyelitis/sensory neuronopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Multifocal disorder combining two or more of limbic encephalitis, brainstem encephalitis, cerebellar degeneration, myelitis, dorsal root ganglionopathy and autonomic failure.","Anti-Hu (ANNA-1) is the archetypal antibody, with small-cell lung carcinoma found in the large majority of cases.","Anti-CV2/CRMP5 disease may add chorea, optic neuritis and peripheral neuropathy to the picture.","Brainstem involvement produces vertigo, nystagmus, diplopia, dysarthria and dysphagia, and can threaten respiration.","Autonomic involvement is common and may manifest as gastrointestinal pseudo-obstruction from enteric neuronal loss, orthostatic hypotension or cardiac dysrhythmia.","Neurological symptoms usually antedate the cancer diagnosis and the underlying tumour is often small and limited-stage.","Response to immunotherapy is generally poor; prompt treatment of the tumour offers the best chance of neurological stabilisation.","CSF is usually inflammatory (pleocytosis, raised protein, oligoclonal bands) even when MRI is normal."],"pathophysiology":"Tumour cells (most often small-cell lung carcinoma) express intracellular onconeural antigens such as HuD that are normally restricted to neurons. The resulting immune response is dominated by antigen-specific cytotoxic T lymphocytes that infiltrate multiple levels of the neuraxis, producing perivascular inflammation, microglial nodules and neuronal loss in cortex, brainstem, cerebellum, spinal cord, dorsal root ganglia and autonomic ganglia. The detected antibodies (anti-Hu, anti-CV2/CRMP5, anti-amphiphysin) are useful diagnostic markers but are largely not directly pathogenic, which accounts for the poor reversibility.","additionalDifferentials":["infectious rhombencephalitis (e.g. listerial) and other viral encephalomyelitides","Bickerstaff brainstem encephalitis","multiple sclerosis or neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder","primary CNS lymphoma or leptomeningeal metastatic disease","vitamin B12 or vitamin E deficiency (subacute combined degeneration)","toxic or metabolic encephalopathy","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["serum and CSF anti-Hu (ANNA-1) and extended paraneoplastic antibody panel","MRI brain and whole spine with contrast","CSF analysis with cytology and oligoclonal bands","whole-body FDG-PET/CT and CT chest, with bronchoscopy if indicated","nerve conduction studies and EMG for associated neuronopathy","autonomic function testing and gastrointestinal transit studies","EEG when encephalopathy or seizures are present"],"redFlags":["respiratory failure or bulbar compromise from brainstem involvement","autonomic instability with cardiac dysrhythmia","gastrointestinal pseudo-obstruction","rapidly progressive multifocal neurological deficit","newly identified malignancy requiring urgent staging"],"prognosis":"Neurological prognosis is generally poor; deficits often persist even when the tumour is controlled, although the disease may stabilise with effective oncological treatment. Paradoxically, anti-Hu-associated small-cell lung carcinoma is sometimes detected at a limited stage and may show relatively favourable tumour control.","pitfalls":["Attributing multifocal neurological signs to cerebral metastases rather than a paraneoplastic process.","Discontinuing the tumour search after an initial negative imaging screen.","Underestimating autonomic involvement, which can be life-threatening.","Delaying oncological treatment while awaiting a neurological response to immunotherapy."],"management":["Detection and definitive oncological treatment of the underlying tumour, most often small-cell lung carcinoma, as the primary intervention","Immunotherapy with corticosteroids, intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange, with cyclophosphamide or rituximab considered, recognising limited reversibility of onconeural anti-Hu antibody disease","Symptomatic management of seizures with anticonvulsants and of autonomic failure, including orthostatic hypotension and gastrointestinal pseudo-obstruction","Cardiac monitoring for dysrhythmia and respiratory support for brainstem involvement threatening ventilation","Continued whole-body tumour surveillance when initial imaging is negative, as neurological symptoms usually precede the cancer","Coordinated neurology and oncology multidisciplinary management with supportive rehabilitation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Graus F, Delattre JY, et al. Recommended diagnostic criteria for paraneoplastic neurological syndromes.","Dalmau J, Rosenfeld MR. Paraneoplastic syndromes of the CNS. Lancet Neurology.","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice, current edition."],"crossLinks":[]},"paraneoplastic_limbic_encephalitis":{"name":"paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis","system":"nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["limbic encephalitis","paraneoplastic limbic encephalopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Subacute onset (days to weeks) of short-term memory impairment, temporal lobe seizures, confusion and neuropsychiatric change (anxiety, depression, hallucinations, personality change) is the core syndrome.","Anterograde amnesia with relative preservation of remote memory reflects selective hippocampal and medial temporal involvement.","Small-cell lung carcinoma with anti-Hu (ANNA-1) is the commonest association; anti-Ma2 disease characteristically affects young men with testicular germ-cell tumours.","Cell-surface antibody syndromes (anti-NMDAR, anti-LGI1, anti-GABA-B, anti-AMPAR, anti-CASPR2) are considerably more responsive to immunotherapy than intracellular onconeural antibody syndromes.","Anti-LGI1 disease produces faciobrachial dystonic seizures and hyponatraemia and is often non-paraneoplastic, though thymoma may occur.","Anti-GABA-B encephalitis presents with prominent early seizures and is strongly linked to small-cell lung carcinoma.","MRI typically shows unilateral or bilateral medial temporal T2/FLAIR hyperintensity, and CSF shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, raised protein and oligoclonal bands.","The syndrome frequently antedates the tumour diagnosis by months, so a negative initial cancer screen warrants periodic re-screening."],"pathophysiology":"An immune response directed against a tumour that ectopically expresses neuronal antigens cross-reacts with limbic structures (hippocampi, amygdala, cingulate and insular cortex), producing inflammation and neuronal loss. Two mechanistic groups exist: antibodies against intracellular onconeural antigens (Hu/ANNA-1, Ma2, CV2/CRMP5, amphiphysin) are markers of a predominantly cytotoxic T-cell mediated process that is poorly reversible, whereas antibodies against neuronal cell-surface or synaptic proteins (NMDAR, LGI1, GABA-B, AMPAR, CASPR2) are directly pathogenic, disrupt receptor function or clustering, and are potentially reversible with immunotherapy.","additionalDifferentials":["herpes simplex and other viral encephalitides","non-convulsive status epilepticus","Wernicke encephalopathy","primary CNS lymphoma or gliomatosis cerebri","Hashimoto (steroid-responsive) encephalopathy","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease","mesial temporal sclerosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["paired serum and CSF autoimmune and paraneoplastic antibody panel","MRI brain with contrast (medial temporal FLAIR sequences)","CSF analysis with oligoclonal bands and cytology","EEG (temporal epileptiform or slow-wave activity)","whole-body FDG-PET/CT to detect occult malignancy","testicular ultrasound in young men (anti-Ma2)","CT chest for small-cell lung carcinoma or thymoma"],"redFlags":["refractory or non-convulsive status epilepticus","rapidly progressive decline in consciousness or coma","autonomic instability (especially with NMDAR antibodies)","severe symptomatic hyponatraemia","identification of an underlying malignancy requiring urgent staging"],"prognosis":"Cell-surface antibody syndromes often improve substantially with tumour treatment and immunotherapy, whereas intracellular onconeural antibody-associated disease tends to leave persistent cognitive deficits. Overall outcome is governed by the antibody type and the stage and treatability of the underlying tumour.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing as herpes simplex encephalitis and missing the paraneoplastic search.","Assuming a negative antibody panel excludes the diagnosis, since seronegative cases are recognised.","Abandoning the tumour hunt after one negative screen rather than repeating surveillance.","Overlooking an occult testicular germ-cell tumour in a young man with anti-Ma2 antibodies."],"management":["Identification and treatment of the underlying tumour where present, alongside immunotherapy","First-line immunotherapy with corticosteroids, intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange, escalating to rituximab or cyclophosphamide, with better response in cell-surface antibody syndromes than onconeural ones","Seizure control with anticonvulsants, including targeting faciobrachial dystonic seizures in anti-LGI1 disease","Correction of hyponatraemia and neuropsychiatric supportive care","Systematic malignancy search, including testicular ultrasound in young men with anti-Ma2, and repeated surveillance if initially negative","Coordinated neurology and oncology multidisciplinary management with cognitive rehabilitation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Graus F, et al. A clinical approach to diagnosis of autoimmune encephalitis. Lancet Neurology.","Dalmau J, Rosenfeld MR. Paraneoplastic and autoimmune encephalitis.","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice, current edition."],"crossLinks":[]},"paraneoplastic_pemphigus":{"name":"Paraneoplastic pemphigus (paraneoplastic autoimmune multiorgan syndrome)","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PNP","paraneoplastic autoimmune multiorgan syndrome (PAMS)","anti-envoplakin/periplakin pemphigus","malignancy-associated pemphigus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with severe, treatment-resistant stomatitis and a polymorphous cutaneous eruption that can mimic pemphigus, pemphigoid, erythema multiforme, or lichen planus","Almost always associated with an underlying neoplasm, most often lymphoproliferative (non-Hodgkin lymphoma, chronic lymphocytic leukaemia, Castleman disease) or thymoma","Intractable painful mucositis, particularly of the lips and oral cavity, is a hallmark and often the presenting feature","Autoantibodies target plakin proteins, characteristically envoplakin and periplakin, as well as desmogleins and other desmosomal and hemidesmosomal antigens","Bronchiolitis obliterans from respiratory epithelial involvement is a feared complication and a leading cause of death","Direct immunofluorescence shows intercellular IgG/C3 with variable basement-membrane-zone deposition; indirect immunofluorescence on rat bladder epithelium is a supportive screening test","Management centres on treating the underlying tumour alongside immunosuppression, though skin disease is often refractory"],"pathophysiology":"Paraneoplastic pemphigus is an autoimmune blistering disease arising in the context of neoplasia, especially lymphoproliferative disorders. Tumour-related immune dysregulation drives production of autoantibodies against a broad panel of antigens, characteristically the plakin family proteins envoplakin and periplakin, together with desmogleins and other desmosomal and hemidesmosomal components. This wide antigen targeting explains the polymorphous mucocutaneous picture, combining intraepidermal acantholysis with interface (lichenoid) inflammation. Both humoral and cell-mediated mechanisms contribute, and antibody or cytotoxic attack on respiratory epithelium can produce bronchiolitis obliterans, contributing to high mortality.","additionalDifferentials":["Pemphigus vulgaris","Stevens-Johnson syndrome / erythema multiforme major","Bullous pemphigoid","Erosive lichen planus","Severe herpetic or aphthous stomatitis","Graft-versus-host disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin and mucosal biopsy showing combined acantholysis and interface dermatitis","Direct immunofluorescence for intercellular and basement-membrane-zone immune deposits","Indirect immunofluorescence including rat bladder epithelium substrate","Immunoblot or immunoprecipitation for anti-envoplakin/periplakin and other plakin antibodies","Imaging (CT of chest, abdomen, pelvis) and haematological workup to identify an occult neoplasm","Pulmonary function tests to detect airflow obstruction from bronchiolitis obliterans"],"redFlags":["Progressive dyspnoea or airflow obstruction suggesting bronchiolitis obliterans","Newly diagnosed or occult lymphoproliferative malignancy requiring urgent oncological input","Severe intractable mucositis compromising nutrition, hydration, and the airway","Widespread erosions with sepsis risk and failure of standard immunosuppression"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally poor, with high mortality driven by the underlying malignancy, sepsis, and respiratory failure from bronchiolitis obliterans; skin disease may improve if a benign tumour is fully removed but often remains refractory.","pitfalls":["Attributing severe stomatitis to aphthous ulceration or infection and missing an underlying malignancy","Not screening for an occult neoplasm once the diagnosis is suspected","Overlooking early respiratory symptoms that herald bronchiolitis obliterans","Expecting a standard pemphigus response to immunosuppression when the disease is characteristically treatment-resistant"],"management":["Treatment of the underlying neoplasm, including surgical removal of a localised tumour such as Castleman disease or thymoma, or chemotherapy for lymphoproliferative disease","Immunosuppression with systemic corticosteroids combined with steroid-sparing agents, notably rituximab, with adjuncts such as intravenous immunoglobulin, ciclosporin, azathioprine or mycophenolate","Intensive mucocutaneous care with meticulous wound management, analgesia for stomatitis, and prevention and prompt treatment of secondary infection and sepsis","Pulmonary function monitoring for bronchiolitis obliterans, a leading cause of death, with respiratory support as needed","Ophthalmological assessment and management of ocular mucosal involvement","Coordinated dermatology and haematology or oncology multidisciplinary management, with recognition of the generally poor prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"paraneoplastic_retinopathy":{"name":"paraneoplastic retinopathy","system":"visual system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cancer-associated retinopathy","melanoma-associated retinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Cancer-associated retinopathy (CAR) causes subacute, painless, usually bilateral though often asymmetric visual loss with photosensitivity, shimmering photopsiae, ring scotomata and impaired colour vision.","Melanoma-associated retinopathy (MAR) typically arises in a patient with known cutaneous melanoma and presents with night blindness and shimmering photopsiae with relatively preserved acuity.","CAR is most often linked to small-cell lung carcinoma and to gynaecological and breast cancers, with anti-recoverin and anti-alpha-enolase antibodies.","MAR is caused by antibodies against retinal ON-bipolar cells (including TRPM1) and correlates with metastatic melanoma.","The full-field electroretinogram is the key test: CAR shows reduced a- and b-waves (photoreceptor dysfunction) whereas MAR shows a selectively reduced b-wave with a preserved a-wave, producing an electronegative waveform.","The fundus is frequently normal early, with later arteriolar attenuation, retinal pigment epithelial mottling and optic disc pallor.","In CAR the visual symptoms may precede the cancer diagnosis, prompting a systemic malignancy search.","Response to immunosuppression is generally limited and visual recovery is often incomplete."],"pathophysiology":"Circulating antibodies directed against retinal antigens shared by the tumour trigger photoreceptor or bipolar cell injury. In cancer-associated retinopathy, antibodies against recoverin and alpha-enolase promote calcium-dependent photoreceptor apoptosis. In melanoma-associated retinopathy, antibodies against ON-bipolar cell antigens such as TRPM1 impair signal transmission from photoreceptors to bipolar cells, explaining the selective b-wave loss on electroretinography. The resulting outer retinal and bipolar dysfunction produces progressive, often irreversible visual impairment.","additionalDifferentials":["retinitis pigmentosa and other inherited retinal dystrophies","acute zonal occult outer retinopathy (AZOOR)","non-paraneoplastic autoimmune retinopathy","toxic or drug-induced retinopathy","vitamin A deficiency retinopathy","retinal vascular occlusion","compressive or inflammatory optic neuropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["full-field electroretinography to characterise photoreceptor versus ON-bipolar dysfunction","serum anti-retinal antibody panel (anti-recoverin, anti-alpha-enolase and others)","optical coherence tomography of the outer retinal layers","fundus autofluorescence imaging","automated perimetry for scotomata and field constriction","whole-body FDG-PET/CT and CT chest for occult malignancy","dermatological review and imaging for melanoma when MAR is suspected"],"redFlags":["rapidly progressive bilateral visual loss","a known or newly suspected melanoma or lung carcinoma","constitutional symptoms suggesting occult malignancy","electronegative electroretinogram indicating bipolar cell dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Visual prognosis is often poor; cancer-associated retinopathy tends to be progressive, while melanoma-associated retinopathy is more variable. Tumour treatment and immunosuppression have limited efficacy in restoring vision.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the presentation for retinitis pigmentosa and missing the paraneoplastic cause.","Being falsely reassured by a normal-appearing fundus in the early stages.","Failing to order electroretinography, which is central to the diagnosis.","Overlooking a history of melanoma in a patient presenting with night blindness."],"management":["Identification and treatment of the underlying malignancy, such as small-cell lung, gynaecological or breast cancer in cancer-associated retinopathy and metastatic melanoma in melanoma-associated retinopathy","Immunosuppressive and immunomodulatory therapy with corticosteroids, intravenous immunoglobulin, plasma exchange or rituximab, recognising limited visual recovery","Low-vision rehabilitation and supportive care, including tinted lenses for photosensitivity and visual aids","Serial monitoring of retinal function with full-field electroretinography, automated perimetry and optical coherence tomography","Whole-body tumour surveillance when malignancy is occult, as retinopathy may precede the diagnosis","Coordinated ophthalmology and oncology multidisciplinary management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adamus G. Autoantibody targets and their significance in autoimmune retinopathies.","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology, current edition.","Graus F, Delattre JY, et al. Recommended diagnostic criteria for paraneoplastic neurological syndromes."],"crossLinks":[]},"paraneoplastic_sensory_neuronopathy":{"name":"paraneoplastic sensory neuronopathy","system":"peripheral nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subacute sensory neuronopathy","Denny-Brown syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Subacute, often asymmetric and multifocal sensory loss that is non-length-dependent, affecting the face, trunk and proximal limbs as well as the distal extremities.","Prominent sensory ataxia and pseudoathetosis with preserved muscle strength reflect selective large-fibre dorsal root ganglion loss.","Early painful paraesthesiae and loss of all sensory modalities, with areflexia, are characteristic.","Anti-Hu (ANNA-1) with small-cell lung carcinoma is the commonest association; it may occur in isolation or as part of paraneoplastic encephalomyelitis.","Nerve conduction studies show reduced or absent sensory nerve action potentials with normal or near-normal motor conduction.","The neuronopathy frequently precedes the cancer diagnosis, so a thorough malignancy search is essential.","Response to immunotherapy is usually poor, and tumour treatment offers the best chance of stabilisation.","Key mimics to exclude are Sjogren-related ganglionopathy, platinum chemotherapy toxicity and pyridoxine excess."],"pathophysiology":"An immune response against intracellular onconeural antigens (most often HuD, expressed by small-cell lung carcinoma) targets the sensory neurons of the dorsal root ganglia. Cytotoxic T-cell mediated inflammation and neuronal destruction within the ganglia produce a ganglionopathy affecting both large and small fibres. Because the primary sensory cell body is destroyed, deficits are non-length-dependent and largely irreversible, and secondary degeneration of the central posterior columns and peripheral sensory axons follows.","additionalDifferentials":["Sjogren syndrome-associated sensory ganglionopathy","platinum (cisplatin/oxaliplatin) chemotherapy neurotoxicity","pyridoxine (vitamin B6) toxicity","vitamin B12 or vitamin E deficiency","chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy (sensory variant)","hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy","tabes dorsalis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["serum and CSF anti-Hu (ANNA-1) and paraneoplastic antibody panel","nerve conduction studies and EMG demonstrating a sensory ganglionopathy pattern","anti-Ro/La antibodies and Schirmer test to assess for Sjogren syndrome","whole-body FDG-PET/CT and CT chest for occult malignancy","MRI spine looking for posterior column T2 hyperintensity","vitamin B12 and vitamin B6 levels","CSF analysis for pleocytosis and oligoclonal bands"],"redFlags":["rapid progression to disabling sensory ataxia and loss of ambulation","associated autonomic failure or gastrointestinal dysmotility","constitutional features (weight loss, night sweats) suggesting malignancy","bulbar or respiratory signs indicating overlap with encephalomyelitis"],"prognosis":"Recovery of sensory function is usually poor because the ganglion neurons are destroyed; the realistic therapeutic goal is stabilisation through treatment of the underlying tumour. Functional outcome is often limited by persistent sensory ataxia.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying the deficit as a length-dependent peripheral neuropathy and missing the non-length-dependent pattern.","Failing to screen for Sjogren syndrome, the principal non-paraneoplastic cause.","Not pursuing small-cell lung carcinoma when anti-Hu antibodies are positive.","Overlooking a drug or vitamin cause such as platinum agents or pyridoxine excess."],"management":["Search for and treat the underlying malignancy (most often small-cell lung carcinoma) as the definitive intervention, since tumour control offers the only realistic chance of halting neuronal loss","Trial immunotherapy such as corticosteroids, intravenous immunoglobulin, plasma exchange or cytotoxic immunosuppression, recognising that response is frequently limited once dorsal root ganglion neurons are destroyed","Provide neuropathic pain relief with gabapentinoids, tricyclic antidepressants or duloxetine for the painful paraesthesiae","Refer for physiotherapy and occupational therapy targeting sensory ataxia and pseudoathetosis, with gait aids and fall-prevention strategies to preserve function","Repeat whole-body imaging surveillance if the initial malignancy search is negative, as the neuronopathy often precedes the cancer diagnosis","Coordinate care through a neurology and oncology multidisciplinary team with neurorehabilitation input","Monitor for evolution into wider paraneoplastic encephalomyelitis, prompting reassessment and escalation of therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Camdessanche JP, et al. The pattern and diagnostic criteria of sensory neuronopathy. Brain.","Graus F, Delattre JY, et al. Recommended diagnostic criteria for paraneoplastic neurological syndromes.","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice, current edition."],"crossLinks":[]},"paraneoplastic_syndromes_endocrinologic_he":{"name":"Paraneoplastic Syndromes: Endocrinologic/Hematologic","system":"Multisystem (Oncology / Endocrine / Hematologic)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ectopic hormone syndromes","Humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy","Tumor-associated SIADH","Ectopic ACTH syndrome","Trousseau's syndrome"],"chapter":98,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.736","clinicalPearls":["Paraneoplastic syndromes are disorders accompanying benign or malignant tumors that are NOT due to direct mass effect, invasion, or metastasis; neuroendocrine tumors such as small-cell lung carcinoma (SCLC) and carcinoids are especially common sources, and these syndromes are more common than generally appreciated (Harrison 22e p.736).","Hormones may be eutopic (from normal tissue of origin) or ectopic (atypical source); ectopic expression is often quantitative rather than absolute and is characterized by defective feedback regulation (e.g., ectopic ACTH) and release of large, unprocessed precursor peptides such as POMC (Harrison 22e p.736).","The three most common paraneoplastic endocrine syndromes are hypercalcemia from PTHrP overproduction, hyponatremia from excess vasopressin (SIADH), and Cushing's syndrome from ectopic ACTH (Harrison 22e p.737).","Humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy (HHM) occurs in up to 20% of cancer patients, is most often caused by PTHrP, and favoring features over primary hyperparathyroidism are known malignancy, recent onset, and very high calcium; PTH is suppressed and an elevated PTHrP is raised in roughly 80% of hypercalcemic cancer patients (Harrison 22e p.737-738).","HHM produces hypercalciuria and hypophosphatemia like hyperparathyroidism, but is typically accompanied by metabolic alkalosis rather than the hyperchloremic acidosis of hyperparathyroidism; lymphomas can cause hypercalcemia via 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D rather than PTHrP (Harrison 22e p.737-738).","Ectopic vasopressin causes SIADH in at least half of SCLC patients; hyponatremia with low serum osmolality occurs alongside an inappropriately normal or high urine osmolality, and intake of free water (oral or IV) can rapidly worsen the hyponatremia (Harrison 22e p.738).","Ectopic ACTH accounts for 10-20% of Cushing's syndrome; SCLC is the leading cause, followed by bronchial and thymic carcinoids; plasma ACTH is usually >22 pmol/L (>100 pg/mL) and urine-free cortisol is typically more than 2-4 times normal (Harrison 22e p.738-739).","Ectopic ACTH Cushing's differs from pituitary disease: less weight gain/centripetal fat (brief exposure plus cachexia), but prominent fluid retention, hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, glucose intolerance, and hyperpigmentation from MSH within the POMC precursor (Harrison 22e p.739).","Most ectopic ACTH sources fail to suppress with high-dose dexamethasone (8 mg suppresses ~80% of pituitary adenomas but fails in ~90% of ectopic cases), though bronchial and other carcinoids are notable exceptions that may show pituitary-like feedback (Harrison 22e p.739).","Non-islet-cell tumor hypoglycemia is driven by IGF-II precursor; serum IGF-II may not be markedly elevated, but an IGF-II/IGF-I ratio greater than 10:1 is suggestive, and the responsible tumor is usually large (>10 cm) and clinically apparent (Harrison 22e p.739-740).","Oncogenic (tumor-induced) osteomalacia from FGF23 (phosphatonin) presents with low serum phosphorus, renal phosphate wasting, low 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, but NORMAL serum calcium and PTH; it is usually caused by benign mesenchymal tumors and reverses with tumor resection (Harrison 22e p.739-741).","Among paraneoplastic hematologic syndromes (erythrocytosis, granulocytosis, thrombocytosis, eosinophilia), red/white/platelet elevations are usually self-limited and asymptomatic; by far the most consequential hematologic abnormality in cancer patients is hypercoagulability (Harrison 22e p.740).","Migratory or recurrent thrombophlebitis can be the initial manifestation of cancer; peripheral venous thrombosis coexisting with visceral carcinoma, especially pancreatic cancer, is termed Trousseau's syndrome (Harrison 22e p.741)."],"pathophysiology":"Neoplastic cells, particularly those of neuroendocrine origin (SCLC, carcinoids), secrete peptide hormones, cytokines, and growth factors or induce antibody production, altering endocrine, hematologic, and other systems independently of tumor bulk. Most ectopic hormone production reflects cellular dedifferentiation with derepression of developmental gene programs (epigenetic changes, altered microRNA expression); genetic rearrangements bringing a hormone gene under aberrant regulatory control are a rare alternative mechanism. HHM arises chiefly from tumor PTHrP, which binds the PTH receptor to mimic hyperparathyroidism (driven by PTHLH hypomethylation, hedgehog/Gli, TGF-beta, Ras activation, p53 loss); lymphomas may instead convert 25-OH vitamin D to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. SIADH results from ectopic vasopressin (often with co-expressed oxytocin) causing water retention and dilutional hyponatremia. Ectopic ACTH comes from unregulated POMC expression, with release of large biologically inactive fragments plus small amounts of active ACTH; very high cortisol overwhelms renal 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type II, allowing cortisol to act at mineralocorticoid receptors and produce severe hypokalemia. IGF-II precursor (from loss of 11p15 imprinting) causes hypoglycemia by acting on insulin/IGF-I receptors and suppressing GH and insulin. FGF23 forms a ternary complex with klotho and renal FGF receptors to reduce phosphate reabsorption and inhibit 1-alpha-hydroxylation. Hematologic syndromes are mediated by ectopic erythropoietin (erythrocytosis); G-CSF, GM-CSF, IL-6 (granulocytosis); IL-6/thrombopoietin (thrombocytosis); IL-5 (eosinophilia); and poorly defined procoagulants/cytokines (hypercoagulability).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperparathyroidism (vs HHM)","Non-malignant causes of SIADH: CNS trauma, infection, pulmonary disease, medications including many chemotherapeutic agents","Pituitary (Cushing's disease), adrenal adenoma, and iatrogenic/exogenous glucocorticoid causes of Cushing's syndrome","Granulomatous disorders producing 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D","Polycythemia vera and hemoglobinopathies with increased O2 affinity (vs paraneoplastic erythrocytosis)","Myeloproliferative disorders causing primary granulocytosis/thrombocytosis/eosinophilia","Renal, adrenal, or thyroid insufficiency as causes of hyponatremia"],"infectionMimics":["Granulocytosis from infection or tumor necrosis mimicking paraneoplastic granulocytosis","Eosinophilia with pulmonary infiltrates resembling infectious or hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypercalcemia of primary hyperparathyroidism","Exogenous glucocorticoid (iatrogenic) Cushing's syndrome","Drug-induced or volume-depletion hyponatremia","Insulinoma or factitious hypoglycemia (vs IGF-II tumor hypoglycemia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium with PTH (suppressed in HHM) and PTHrP level; 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D in suspected lymphoma","Serum sodium and osmolality with paired urine osmolality and urine sodium for SIADH","Plasma ACTH, urine-free cortisol, high-dose (8 mg) dexamethasone suppression test, and inferior petrosal sinus sampling with CRH stimulation (>3:1 central:peripheral ACTH ratio suggests pituitary source)","Serum glucose with insulin, IGF-II, and IGF-II/IGF-I ratio (>10:1 suggestive) for tumor hypoglycemia","Serum phosphorus, FGF23, and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D for oncogenic osteomalacia; octreotide scan to localize tumor","Hematocrit, red cell mass, and serum erythropoietin for erythrocytosis","Compression ultrasonography/impedance plethysmography (venography if equivocal) for DVT; ventilation-perfusion scan, chest radiograph, ECG, and ABG for suspected pulmonary embolism","Functional imaging (CT, MRI, PET, octreotide scintigraphy) to localize hormone-producing tumors, particularly when endocrine features precede cancer diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Markedly elevated calcium (>3.5 mmol/L [>14 mg/dL]) with fatigue, altered mentation, polyuria, dehydration, or arrhythmias (short ST/QT, bundle branch block, bradyarrhythmia)","Severe hyponatremia (Na <115 meq/L) or any hyponatremia with confusion, depressed mental status, or seizures","Profound hypokalemia with metabolic alkalosis and hypertension in ectopic ACTH; opportunistic infections (Pneumocystis, mycoses) are a frequent cause of death","Increased venous thromboembolism risk in ectopic ACTH and in cancer generally; migratory/recurrent thrombophlebitis or clot in an unusual site","New atypical clinical or metabolic findings in a known cancer patient should prompt consideration of a paraneoplastic syndrome","Markedly elevated eosinophils (>5000/uL) causing dyspnea, wheezing, and diffuse pulmonary infiltrates"],"prognosis":"Endocrine paraneoplastic syndromes can cause substantial morbidity and may be the presenting clue to an occult malignancy; treatment of the underlying tumor is the mainstay but rarely alters overall survival or cancer progression. Hematologic cell-count syndromes are generally self-limited and asymptomatic, but thrombocytosis can mark advanced-stage disease and poorer prognosis. Hypercoagulability is the most consequential hematologic complication, and oncogenic osteomalacia and several endocrinopathies can reverse with successful tumor resection; checkpoint-inhibitor-associated endocrinopathies are generally irreversible and require lifelong hormone replacement.","pitfalls":["Attributing atypical metabolic or neurologic findings to the malignancy or its treatment and overlooking a paraneoplastic syndrome","Confusing HHM with primary hyperparathyroidism; PTH should be suppressed and acid-base pattern differs (alkalosis in HHM vs hyperchloremic acidosis in hyperparathyroidism)","Rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia, which can precipitate central pontine myelinolysis; correction should be gradual (about 0.5-1 meq/L per hour)","Excluding ectopic ACTH on the basis of high-dose dexamethasone suppression alone, since bronchial/thymic carcinoids can suppress like pituitary adenomas; conversely a suppressed ACTH indicates an ACTH-independent cause","Relying on serum IGF-II levels, which may be normal in tumor hypoglycemia because assays may miss IGF-II precursors (use the IGF-II/IGF-I ratio)","Over-relying on D-dimer in cancer patients, where it is less predictive of DVT and rises with age without thrombosis","Pursuing extensive cancer work-up after a first unprovoked thrombophlebitis or PE in a patient without cancer; targeted evaluation is reserved for refractory, unusual-site, or migratory/recurrent clots"],"management":["Direct definitive therapy at the underlying malignancy (commonly small-cell lung carcinoma or a carcinoid), as tumour control is the only measure that reliably corrects ectopic hormone secretion","Manage humoral hypercalcaemia with isotonic saline rehydration, an intravenous bisphosphonate or denosumab, and calcitonin for rapid short-term lowering","Treat ectopic-vasopressin SIADH with fluid restriction and, where needed, vasopressin-receptor antagonists, avoiding excess free-water intake that worsens the hyponatraemia","Control ectopic-ACTH Cushing's syndrome with steroidogenesis inhibitors such as ketoconazole or metyrapone, considering bilateral adrenalectomy for refractory hypercortisolaemia","Correct associated derangements such as IGF-II-mediated tumour hypoglycaemia and FGF23-mediated oncogenic osteomalacia, ideally by resecting the responsible tumour","Monitor serum calcium, sodium and cortisol during treatment as biochemical markers of tumour response and relapse","Coordinate care across oncology and endocrinology multidisciplinary teams"],"severityClassification":"No single formal severity scale; severity is gauged by the magnitude and acuity of the biochemical derangement (e.g., calcium level and rate of rise, sodium level [Na <115 meq/L denotes severe hyponatremia], eosinophil count >5000/uL). For venous thromboembolism risk during chemotherapy, the Khorana risk score (Harrison Table 98-3) stratifies patients using cancer type, therapy, blood counts, and body mass index.","references":["Harrison 22e p.736","Harrison 22e p.737","Harrison 22e p.738","Harrison 22e p.739","Harrison 22e p.740","Harrison 22e p.741","Harrison 22e p.742"],"crossLinks":[]},"paraphimosis":{"name":"Paraphimosis","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["entrapped foreskin","irreducible retracted foreskin","trapped prepuce"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A urological emergency in which a retracted foreskin cannot be returned over the glans, forming a constricting band behind the corona","The trapped prepuce and glans become progressively oedematous and painful, and delayed reduction risks arterial compromise, ischaemia, and necrosis of the glans","Commonly iatrogenic, when the foreskin is retracted for catheterisation, cystoscopy, or cleaning and is not replaced afterwards","Prompt manual reduction is the priority - reduce the oedema with sustained compression and adjuncts such as cold or osmotic agents, then push the glans back while drawing the foreskin forward","Adequate analgesia or a penile block greatly aids reduction, and the goal is to relieve the constricting ring as quickly as possible","If manual reduction fails, a dorsal slit or emergency circumcision is performed to release the constriction","Elective circumcision may be offered later to prevent recurrence, and staff should always replace a retracted foreskin after any procedure"],"pathophysiology":"Paraphimosis develops when a tight foreskin is retracted behind the glans and left there. The constricting preputial ring first obstructs venous and lymphatic return from the distal penis, producing progressive oedema of the glans and inner prepuce. This swelling tightens the ring further in a vicious cycle, and if it is not relieved the rising tissue pressure eventually compromises arterial inflow, threatening ischaemia and necrosis of the glans. Management centres on breaking this cycle by reducing the oedema and returning the foreskin to its normal position before irreversible damage occurs.","additionalDifferentials":["Preputial or penile oedema from another cause, such as insect bite or allergic angio-oedema","Balanoposthitis","Penile cellulitis","Penile tourniquet syndrome from an encircling hair or thread","Contact dermatitis","Penile lymphoedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis by inspection of the retracted foreskin and constricting ring","Assessment of glans colour and capillary refill to gauge perfusion","Blood glucose, given the association of recurrent balanoposthitis with diabetes","Swab if secondary infection is suspected","Review of recent instrumentation or catheter history"],"redFlags":["A dusky, dark, or blistering glans indicating ischaemia","Absent capillary refill of the glans","Severe unremitting pain","Signs of necrosis or systemic sepsis","Failure of manual reduction"],"prognosis":"Excellent when reduced promptly; the outlook worsens sharply with delay, when tissue ischaemia and glans necrosis can occur, so time to reduction is critical.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise it as an emergency and delaying reduction","Retracting a foreskin for catheterisation or examination and forgetting to replace it, the commonest iatrogenic cause","Attempting reduction without adequate analgesia and without first reducing the oedema","Confusing paraphimosis (foreskin stuck retracted) with phimosis (foreskin that cannot be retracted)","Missing early ischaemic changes of the glans"],"management":["Perform prompt manual reduction as first-line: reduce glans oedema with sustained circumferential compression, then advance the foreskin back over the glans","Provide adequate analgesia or a penile dorsal-nerve block to facilitate reduction","Use adjuncts to shrink the oedema before reduction, such as cold compression, osmotic agents (granulated sugar) or a wrapped elastic compression bandage","If manual reduction fails, escalate urgently to a dorsal slit incision to release the constricting ring, or emergency circumcision","Assess glans perfusion by colour and capillary refill throughout, as delay risks ischaemia and necrosis of the glans","Prevent recurrence by always replacing the foreskin after catheterisation, cystoscopy or cleaning, and offer elective circumcision for recurrent episodes or significant phimosis","Screen for and manage predisposing factors such as diabetes and recurrent balanoposthitis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Smith & Tanagho's General Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"paraquat_poisoning":{"name":"Paraquat poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["paraquat toxicity","herbicide poisoning","gramoxone poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A highly lethal herbicide with no effective antidote; even small deliberate ingestions of concentrated solution are frequently fatal","It concentrates in and selectively destroys the lungs, causing progressive pulmonary oedema, haemorrhage, and then relentless fibrosis over days to weeks","Early features include painful corrosive burns to the mouth, throat, and oesophagus, vomiting, and abdominal pain","Oxygen supplementation should generally be avoided or minimised, as high oxygen tension accelerates free-radical lung injury; supplemental oxygen is reserved for severe, refractory hypoxaemia","Immediate gastrointestinal decontamination with an adsorbent such as activated charcoal or Fuller's earth is a priority if the patient presents very early","Enhanced elimination (haemoperfusion or haemodialysis) and immunosuppression with corticosteroids and cyclophosphamide have been tried, but none reliably alters the poor outcome","Acute kidney injury and hepatic injury commonly precede the delayed, usually fatal, pulmonary fibrosis","Plasma paraquat concentration plotted against time since ingestion helps estimate prognosis and guide the intensity of intervention"],"pathophysiology":"Paraquat undergoes cyclical reduction and oxidation (redox cycling), generating large amounts of superoxide and other reactive oxygen species that deplete NADPH and cause lipid peroxidation and cell death. It is actively taken up and concentrated by alveolar epithelial (type I and II) cells, making the lung the primary target: an early destructive phase of alveolitis and haemorrhagic oedema is followed by an obliterative pulmonary fibrosis. The kidneys and liver, which also accumulate the compound, sustain acute injury, and high oxygen tensions worsen the oxidative damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Corrosive ingestion from other agents","Other lethal pesticide ingestions (organophosphate, aluminium phosphide)","Acute respiratory distress syndrome from other causes","Acute interstitial pneumonitis","Caustic oesophageal injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma or urine paraquat level and the urine dithionite qualitative test","Serial renal and liver function tests","Arterial blood gas and serial chest imaging to track evolving lung injury","Full blood count and electrolytes","Prognostic assessment using plasma concentration versus time since ingestion"],"redFlags":["Ingestion of concentrated solution or any large deliberate ingestion","Early acute kidney injury, which predicts high toxin burden and poor outcome","Progressive hypoxaemia and radiographic lung changes","Extensive oropharyngeal and oesophageal corrosive burns","Rising plasma paraquat level for the time since ingestion indicating a lethal exposure"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally poor and dose-dependent; ingestion of concentrated formulation is usually fatal, with death from multi-organ failure early or from progressive pulmonary fibrosis over subsequent days to weeks, and no treatment has been shown to reliably improve survival.","pitfalls":["Giving liberal supplemental oxygen, which accelerates oxidative lung injury","Delaying adsorbent decontamination in an early presentation","Offering false reassurance during the deceptively stable early period before lung failure","Underestimating lethality and under-treating the psychosocial and palliative needs of the patient"],"management":["Undertake immediate gastrointestinal decontamination with an adsorbent such as activated charcoal or Fuller's earth if the patient presents very early after ingestion","Withhold or strictly minimise supplemental oxygen, since high oxygen tension accelerates free-radical lung injury; reserve it for severe refractory hypoxaemia","Provide aggressive supportive care with intravenous fluid resuscitation, analgesia for corrosive oropharyngeal and oesophageal burns, and antiemetics","Consider enhanced elimination by haemoperfusion or haemodialysis, while recognising that evidence of survival benefit is limited","Trial immunosuppression with corticosteroids and cyclophosphamide in severe poisoning, acknowledging that no regimen reliably alters the poor outcome","Monitor renal and hepatic function, arterial blood gases and serial chest imaging to track evolving alveolitis and fibrosis","Involve a clinical toxicology service or poisons centre early, and provide honest prognostication and palliative care given the high lethality"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"parasomnia":{"name":"Parasomnia","system":"Sleep medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["parasomnias","NREM parasomnia","REM sleep behaviour disorder","RBD","sleepwalking","night terrors"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard sleep & reproductive medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Parasomnias are undesirable physical or experiential events that intrude on sleep and are broadly divided by the sleep stage from which they arise into non-REM (NREM) and REM types","NREM disorders of arousal (confusional arousals, sleepwalking, night terrors) typically occur in the first third of the night during deep slow-wave sleep, with the person appearing awake but confused and having little or no recall","REM sleep behaviour disorder involves loss of the normal muscle atonia of REM sleep, so patients physically act out vivid, often violent dreams and can injure themselves or a bed partner","REM sleep behaviour disorder is strongly associated with, and may precede by years, neurodegenerative alpha-synucleinopathies such as Parkinson disease and dementia with Lewy bodies","NREM parasomnias are common and usually benign in children, tending to resolve with age, whereas new-onset events in adults warrant more careful evaluation","Triggers that deepen or fragment sleep, including sleep deprivation, alcohol, fever, stress, and certain medications, can precipitate NREM events","Management prioritises sleep hygiene, safety-proofing the bedroom, and removal of precipitants; pharmacological treatment is reserved for frequent, dangerous, or distressing episodes","Nocturnal frontal-lobe seizures can mimic parasomnias and should be considered when events are stereotyped, very frequent, or occur repeatedly within a single night"],"pathophysiology":"Parasomnias reflect incomplete or dysregulated transitions between wakefulness and sleep states rather than a single disease. In NREM disorders of arousal, partial awakening from slow-wave sleep leaves motor and autonomic systems active while consciousness and memory circuits remain asleep, producing complex behaviours without awareness; a genetic predisposition and any factor that increases slow-wave sleep or arousal frequency raises the risk. REM sleep behaviour disorder results from failure of the brainstem circuits that normally impose skeletal muscle atonia during REM sleep, allowing dream enactment; the underlying neuronal dysfunction is closely linked to synucleinopathy neurodegeneration. Other parasomnias, such as sleep-related eating or nightmare disorder, arise from analogous state-boundary instability.","additionalDifferentials":["Nocturnal (frontal-lobe) epilepsy","Obstructive sleep apnoea with confusional arousals","Nocturnal panic attacks","Periodic limb movement disorder","Dissociative or psychogenic nocturnal episodes","Nocturnal delirium in the elderly"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed history from patient and bed partner, including timing within the night and degree of recall","Video polysomnography with expanded EEG montage to capture events and exclude seizures","Neurological assessment and, where indicated, dopaminergic imaging when REM sleep behaviour disorder is suspected","Screening for coexisting sleep apnoea as a trigger for arousals","Review of medications and substances that can provoke parasomnias"],"redFlags":["Dream enactment with violent behaviour suggesting REM sleep behaviour disorder and possible underlying neurodegeneration","New-onset complex nocturnal behaviour in an adult without childhood history","Stereotyped, repetitive episodes clustering within one night raising suspicion of nocturnal seizures","Episodes causing injury to the patient or bed partner"],"prognosis":"Most childhood NREM parasomnias are benign and remit spontaneously; adult NREM events can often be controlled by addressing precipitants, while REM sleep behaviour disorder tends to persist and carries an appreciable long-term risk of evolving neurodegenerative disease.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling nocturnal seizures as benign parasomnia and delaying appropriate neurological workup","Failing to counsel patients with REM sleep behaviour disorder about future neurodegenerative risk and bedroom safety","Overlooking obstructive sleep apnoea as the arousal trigger driving NREM events","Attempting to forcibly wake a sleepwalker, which may increase agitation and injury risk"],"management":["Optimise sleep hygiene and ensure adequate sleep, and remove precipitants of NREM arousals such as sleep deprivation, alcohol, fever, stress and offending medications","Implement bedroom safety measures, removing hazards and securing windows and doors, to prevent injury during sleepwalking or dream-enactment","Treat any coexisting obstructive sleep apnoea, which fragments sleep and provokes arousals","For troublesome or injurious NREM parasomnias, consider a bedtime benzodiazepine such as clonazepam or an antidepressant","Manage REM sleep behaviour disorder with bedtime melatonin or clonazepam to reduce violent dream-enactment","Counsel patients with REM sleep behaviour disorder about its association with alpha-synucleinopathies, with neurological follow-up for emerging parkinsonism or cognitive change","Reassure families that childhood NREM parasomnias are usually benign and remit with age, reserving detailed evaluation for atypical or new adult-onset events"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"paratesticular_rhabdomyosarcoma":{"name":"Paratesticular rhabdomyosarcoma","system":"Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Spermatic cord rhabdomyosarcoma","Paratesticular RMS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common paratesticular malignancy of childhood and adolescence, arising from mesenchymal tissue of the spermatic cord, epididymis and testicular tunics.","Typically presents as a painless, firm, rapidly enlarging unilateral scrotal or inguinal mass, usually felt separate from and above the testis.","Embryonal histology, including the favourable spindle cell variant, predominates; the alveolar subtype, associated with FOXO1 gene fusions, is more aggressive.","The paratesticular location is classified as a favourable site in rhabdomyosarcoma risk stratification.","Lymphatic drainage follows the gonadal vessels to the retroperitoneal para-aortic and paracaval nodes, so staging of the retroperitoneum is essential.","Adolescents have a substantially higher rate of retroperitoneal nodal involvement and worse outcomes than young children, and warrant more thorough nodal evaluation.","Radical inguinal orchidectomy with high ligation of the spermatic cord is the correct surgical approach; a trans-scrotal approach is avoided.","Treatment is multimodal, combining surgery with chemotherapy and, in selected cases, radiotherapy."],"pathophysiology":"Rhabdomyosarcoma is a malignant tumour of skeletal-muscle lineage that arises from primitive mesenchymal cells retaining the capacity for myogenic (rhabdomyoblastic) differentiation, even at sites such as the paratesticular region where mature skeletal muscle is absent. Embryonal tumours, the commonest subtype at this site, typically show loss of heterozygosity at chromosome 11p15.5 and lack a defining gene fusion, whereas alveolar tumours are characterised by PAX3-FOXO1 or PAX7-FOXO1 fusions that drive a more aggressive phenotype. Tumour cells express myogenic markers such as desmin, myogenin and MyoD1, which underpin the histological diagnosis. The paratesticular tissues drain via lymphatics accompanying the gonadal vessels to the retroperitoneal para-aortic and paracaval nodes, and this drainage pattern dictates both the risk of nodal spread and the staging and surgical strategy.","additionalDifferentials":["Testicular germ cell tumour","Epididymo-orchitis","Epididymal cyst or spermatocele","Hydrocele","Inguinal hernia","Benign paratesticular tumours such as adenomatoid tumour, lipoma or leiomyoma of the cord","Other paratesticular sarcomas such as liposarcoma or leiomyosarcoma, which are more typical in adults"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound with Doppler","Serum tumour markers (alpha-fetoprotein, beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin and lactate dehydrogenase) to help exclude a germ cell tumour","Radical inguinal orchidectomy specimen for histology and immunohistochemistry (desmin, myogenin, MyoD1)","Molecular testing for FOXO1 gene fusion to distinguish alveolar from embryonal subtype","Cross-sectional imaging of the chest, abdomen and pelvis for nodal and metastatic staging","MRI of the primary site and pelvis","Bone marrow examination and bone or metabolic imaging in higher-risk or metastatic disease"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging solid scrotal mass in a child or adolescent","Palpable abdominal mass or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy","Prior trans-scrotal biopsy or surgery, which compromises staging and lymphatic drainage","Systemic symptoms or respiratory features suggesting metastatic disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally favourable, particularly for localised embryonal tumours in young children treated with multimodal therapy. Outcomes are worse with the alveolar subtype, nodal or metastatic disease, and in adolescents.","pitfalls":["Performing a trans-scrotal biopsy or orchidectomy (scrotal violation), which alters lymphatic drainage, worsens staging and may necessitate hemiscrotectomy","Underestimating retroperitoneal nodal risk in adolescents","Mistaking the mass for epididymo-orchitis and delaying diagnosis with antibiotics","Failing to send tissue for molecular fusion testing","Inadequate cord ligation leaving positive surgical margins"],"management":["Perform radical inguinal orchidectomy with high ligation of the spermatic cord as the primary surgical treatment, avoiding a trans-scrotal approach that risks tumour spillage","Deliver multi-agent systemic chemotherapy with vincristine, actinomycin D and cyclophosphamide as the backbone of multimodal therapy","Stratify treatment intensity by histology and fusion status, escalating for alveolar, FOXO1-fusion-positive tumours which behave more aggressively","Evaluate and stage the retroperitoneal para-aortic and paracaval nodes, with ipsilateral retroperitoneal lymph node dissection especially in adolescents given their higher rate of nodal involvement","Add radiotherapy to the primary site or involved nodes according to risk group and residual disease","Offer sperm banking or other fertility preservation before gonadotoxic chemotherapy or radiotherapy where age-appropriate","Manage within a paediatric and adolescent sarcoma multidisciplinary team, with structured surveillance imaging for local, nodal and pulmonary recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric soft tissue sarcoma cooperative group protocols","European paediatric soft tissue sarcoma study group guidance","Paediatric oncology and urology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"parathyroid_adenoma":{"name":"Parathyroid adenoma","system":"Endocrine surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["primary hyperparathyroidism","PHPT","solitary parathyroid adenoma","hyperfunctioning parathyroid adenoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest cause of primary hyperparathyroidism is a single hyperfunctioning adenoma, with hyperplasia and, rarely, carcinoma accounting for the remainder","Many patients are asymptomatic and detected on incidental hypercalcaemia; classical symptoms are summarised as bones, stones, abdominal groans, and psychic moans","The biochemical hallmark is hypercalcaemia with an inappropriately non-suppressed or elevated parathyroid hormone level","Chronic disease can cause nephrolithiasis, nephrocalcinosis, osteoporosis or osteitis fibrosa cystica, peptic ulceration, and pancreatitis","Localisation before surgery combines neck ultrasound with sestamibi scintigraphy, and 4D-CT is useful when these are discordant or negative","Minimally invasive (focused) parathyroidectomy is favoured when a single adenoma is confidently localised, often guided by intraoperative parathyroid hormone measurement","Consider familial and syndromic disease (MEN 1, MEN 2A, familial hyperparathyroidism) in young patients or those with multigland involvement"],"pathophysiology":"A parathyroid adenoma is a benign clonal proliferation of chief cells that secretes parathyroid hormone autonomously, independent of the normal negative feedback from serum calcium. Excess PTH increases osteoclastic bone resorption, enhances renal tubular calcium reabsorption while promoting phosphate excretion, and stimulates renal activation of vitamin D, which raises intestinal calcium absorption. The net result is hypercalcaemia and hypophosphataemia. Sustained bone resorption reduces bone density, and the filtered calcium load promotes renal stone formation and, over time, renal impairment.","additionalDifferentials":["Malignancy-associated hypercalcaemia (PTH-related peptide or bony metastases)","Parathyroid hyperplasia (four-gland disease)","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia","Tertiary hyperparathyroidism in chronic kidney disease","Vitamin D intoxication or granulomatous disease (e.g. sarcoidosis)","Parathyroid carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium (corrected or ionised), phosphate, and intact PTH","24-hour urinary calcium to help exclude familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia","Vitamin D level and renal function assessment","Neck ultrasound and technetium-99m sestamibi scintigraphy for localisation","4D-CT or selective venous sampling in reoperative or non-localising cases","Bone density (DXA) scan and renal imaging for end-organ effects"],"redFlags":["Severe hypercalcaemia with confusion, dehydration, arrhythmia, or hypercalcaemic crisis requiring urgent treatment","Very high calcium with a palpable neck mass or markedly elevated PTH raising suspicion of parathyroid carcinoma","Renal impairment or obstructing calculi from longstanding disease","Pathological fractures from advanced skeletal involvement"],"prognosis":"Surgical removal of a solitary adenoma is curative in the great majority, with normalisation of calcium and PTH and improvement in bone density and stone risk over time. Outcomes are excellent when a single gland is correctly identified; multigland disease or missed ectopic glands increases the risk of persistent or recurrent hyperparathyroidism.","pitfalls":["Attributing hypercalcaemia to an adenoma without excluding familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia, in whom surgery is inappropriate","Relying on a single localising study and missing an ectopic (mediastinal or intrathyroidal) gland","Overlooking four-gland hyperplasia and performing an inadequate focused exploration","Failing to recognise MEN syndromes, leading to incomplete treatment and missed associated tumours","Not monitoring for postoperative hypocalcaemia, including hungry bone syndrome after severe disease"],"management":["Offer parathyroidectomy, typically focused minimally invasive excision of the localised single adenoma, as the definitive curative treatment","Localise the adenoma preoperatively with neck ultrasound and technetium-99m sestamibi scintigraphy, adding 4D-CT when these are discordant or negative","Use intraoperative parathyroid hormone measurement to confirm biochemical cure before completing focused surgery","For patients unfit for or declining surgery, control hypercalcaemia with the calcimimetic cinacalcet and protect the skeleton with a bisphosphonate, alongside adequate hydration","Correct any coexisting vitamin D deficiency cautiously and encourage good fluid intake to reduce stone formation","Monitor postoperatively for hypocalcaemia, including hungry-bone syndrome, and for recurrent laryngeal nerve dysfunction","Undertake long-term surveillance of calcium, renal function and bone density, and screen for multigland or familial disease where features suggest it"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"parathyroid_calcium_disorders":{"name":"Parathyroid & Calcium Disorders","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hyperparathyroidism","hypoparathyroidism","hypocalcemia"],"chapter":422,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3271","clinicalPearls":["Primary hyperparathyroidism (pHPT) and malignancy are the two most common causes of hypercalcemia; the diagnostic split hinges on the PTH level (Harrison 22e p.3271).","pHPT is almost always a benign neoplastic disorder of the parathyroids (adenoma or hyperplasia) producing PTH that is not appropriately suppressed by the elevated calcium; parathyroid carcinoma is a rare cause (Harrison 22e p.3271).","In hypercalcemia, an elevated or 'inappropriately normal' PTH with elevated calcium and low/low-normal phosphorus is almost always pHPT; because of PTH's phosphaturic action, pHPT hypercalcemia is often accompanied by low/frankly low serum phosphate (Harrison 22e p.3271).","A suppressed PTH in the face of hypercalcemia means the process is independent of the parathyroid glands and is most often malignancy; many solid tumors secrete PTHrP, which mimics PTH at its receptor on bone and kidney (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Hypercalcemia from lymphoma or granulomatous disease (e.g., sarcoidosis, tuberculosis) is driven by extrarenal macrophage CYP27B1 conversion of 25(OH)D to 1,25(OH)2D; in such cases 1,25(OH)2D is increased or inappropriately normal (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Classic chronic hypercalcemia (months to years, fluctuating between high-normal and just above normal) is most often pHPT or FHH, occasionally multiple myeloma; malignancy-associated hypercalcemia usually has a more acute course (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Mild hypercalcemia (10.5-11.9 mg/dL) is largely asymptomatic; symptoms ('stones, bones, groans') include nephrolithiasis, reduced bone density / bone pain or pathologic fractures with long-standing hyperparathyroidism, constipation/nausea/vomiting/anorexia/peptic ulcer/pancreatitis, plus fatigue, proximal weakness, hyporeflexia, and neuropsychiatric changes (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Hypocalcemia is differentiated by whether PTH is low (hypoparathyroidism) or high (secondary hyperparathyroidism); a low or 'inappropriately low' PTH with hypocalcemia establishes hypoparathyroidism, whereas an elevated PTH points to the vitamin D axis or primary small-bowel calcium malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.3271).","In adults, hypoparathyroidism most commonly results from inadvertent damage to all four parathyroid glands during thyroid or parathyroid surgery; autoimmune endocrinopathy accounts for about a quarter of cases (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Neuromuscular irritability of hypocalcemia is detected by Chvostek's sign (perioral twitch on tapping the facial nerve) and the more specific Trousseau's sign (carpal spasm after inflating a BP cuff above systolic for 3 min); paresthesias of fingers, toes, and circumoral region are typical (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Because PTH is the main minute-by-minute defense against hypocalcemia, hypoparathyroidism can produce profound, life-threatening hypocalcemia, whereas vitamin D deficiency causes less severe hypocalcemia since the parathyroids mount a compensatory PTH rise (at the expense of bone) (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Impaired PTH secretion may be secondary to magnesium deficiency (via intracellular effects); hypomagnesemic hypocalcemia will not correct until magnesium is repleted (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Hungry bone syndrome after parathyroidectomy is a recognized cause of post-operative hypocalcemia with high PTH (Harrison 22e p.3271)."],"pathophysiology":"Extracellular calcium is held in a narrow range (~8.5-10.5 mg/dL) by feedback among the parathyroids, kidney, bone, and intestine. A fall in ECF calcium is sensed by the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) on parathyroid chief cells, triggering PTH release; PTH increases bone calcium resorption, distal-tubule reabsorption of filtered calcium, and renal proximal-tubule 1,25(OH)2D production, and 1,25(OH)2D in turn raises intestinal calcium absorption. Hypercalcemia arises from derangements of this loop: PTH-dependent overproduction (pHPT due to adenoma/hyperplasia, rarely carcinoma; tertiary hyperparathyroidism; ectopic PTH; FHH and CaSR-altering states such as lithium), or PTH-independent mechanisms (PTHrP-secreting tumors, lytic skeletal metastases, extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D production in granulomatous disease/lymphoma, vitamin D intoxication, increased bone resorption from thyrotoxicosis/Paget's/immobilization, and calcium overload e.g. milk-alkali). Hypocalcemia results from low PTH (hypoparathyroidism: surgical, autoimmune, infiltrative, genetic, CaSR-activating, or magnesium-related) or high PTH (secondary hyperparathyroidism from vitamin D deficiency, renal failure, intestinal malabsorption, or PTH resistance/pseudohypoparathyroidism). Low calcium increases neuromuscular irritability (Harrison 22e p.3271).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperparathyroidism (PTH-dependent hypercalcemia) - parathyroid adenoma or hyperplasia, rarely carcinoma","Hypercalcemia of malignancy (PTH-suppressed) - PTHrP overproduction or lytic skeletal metastases (breast, myeloma)","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH) - inactivating CASR/GNA11/AP2S1 mutations","Tertiary hyperparathyroidism (long-standing renal insufficiency)","Granulomatous disease- or lymphoma-associated hypercalcemia (extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D)","Vitamin D intoxication","Hypercalcemia of immobilization, thyrotoxicosis, Paget's disease, milk-alkali syndrome","Multiple myeloma","Postsurgical (post-thyroidectomy / post-parathyroidectomy) hypoparathyroidism","Autoimmune hypoparathyroidism (including APS-1)","Vitamin D deficiency / chronic renal insufficiency causing secondary hyperparathyroidism","Pseudohypoparathyroidism (PTH resistance)","Hypomagnesemia (or severe hypermagnesemia) impairing PTH","Autosomal dominant hypocalcemia (activating CaSR/GNA11 mutations)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis - granulomatous extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D production causing hypercalcemia","Leprosy and fungal infections - granulomatous causes of 1,25(OH)2D-mediated hypercalcemia","COVID-19 - reported transient hypoparathyroidism (and even spontaneous resolution of pHPT) via unknown mechanisms"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pseudohypercalcemia (e.g., calcium-binding IgM in Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia) and pseudohypocalcemia (low albumin from malnutrition, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis; gadoversetamide MRI contrast) - artifactual values requiring albumin correction before workup","Sarcoidosis and other granulomatous disease - 1,25(OH)2D-mediated hypercalcemia","Lymphoma (B cell, cutaneous T cell/Sezary) - extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D","Thyrotoxicosis, Paget's disease, immobilization - increased bone resorption mimicking parathyroid hypercalcemia","Thiazides, lithium, vitamin A, foscarnet, teriparatide, aromatase inhibitors - drug-induced hypercalcemia","Acute pancreatitis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, burns - hypocalcemia from tissue injury / hyperphosphatemia","Respiratory or metabolic alkalosis - hypocalcemia from increased calcium binding to albumin"],"additionalInvestigations":["Total serum calcium with serum albumin to calculate corrected calcium (add 0.8 mg/dL per 1.0 g/dL albumin below 4.0; subtract when albumin is high)","Intact PTH by two-site assay - the key second test that separates PTH-dependent from PTH-independent disorders","Serum phosphorus (low/low-normal in pHPT; elevated in some hypocalcemic states)","Serum magnesium (hypo- or severe hypermagnesemia impairs PTH)","Serum creatinine / renal function","Serum 25(OH)D - useful in both hyper- and hypocalcemia to assess vitamin D stores","Serum 1,25(OH)2D - useful in hypercalcemia (increased/inappropriately normal in granulomatous disease or lymphoma) and suspected vitamin D resistance","PTHrP level when hypercalcemia of malignancy is suspected with suppressed PTH","Serum free light chains / SPEP-UPEP with immunofixation to exclude multiple myeloma","Calcium/creatinine clearance ratio (<0.01 suggests FHH); 24-hour urinary fractional calcium excretion (>0.02 supports pHPT)","Genetic sequencing of CASR, GNA11, AP2S1 for definitive FHH diagnosis when urine studies are inconclusive; genetic testing for syndromic/hereditary pHPT (MEN, CDC73)","ECG (hypercalcemia: bradycardia, AV block, QRS widening, short QTc; hypocalcemia: QTc prolongation)"],"redFlags":["Moderate-to-severe hypercalcemia (>=12-14 mg/dL) with progressive lethargy, stupor, or coma","Hypercalcemia with acute renal failure and volume depletion from polyuria","Hypercalcemic ECG changes: bradycardia, AV block, QRS widening, short QTc","Severe hypocalcemia causing seizures, carpopedal spasm, bronchospasm, or laryngospasm","Hypocalcemic QTc prolongation with risk of ventricular tachyarrhythmia (Torsade) and sudden death","Tetany (positive Trousseau's sign / carpal spasm) signaling impending neuromuscular crisis","New severe hypocalcemia after thyroid or parathyroid neck surgery (damage to all four glands), especially in patients with prior malabsorption","Syndromic pHPT with a jaw tumor (CDC73 mutation) carrying ~30% lifetime risk of parathyroid carcinoma"],"prognosis":"Mild, asymptomatic hypercalcemia (commonly pHPT detected incidentally in settings with routine calcium screening) carries a benign short-term course, whereas advanced pHPT can cause severe skeletal disease (osteitis fibrosa cystica). Hypoparathyroidism, because PTH is the chief minute-by-minute defense against hypocalcemia, can be profound, debilitating, and life-threatening, while vitamin D-deficiency hypocalcemia is typically milder owing to compensatory PTH secretion (at the expense of bone). Severe hypercalcemia (coma, renal failure, arrhythmia) and severe hypocalcemia (seizures, laryngospasm, Torsade) are medical emergencies (Harrison 22e p.3271).","pitfalls":["Failing to correct calcium for albumin before launching a workup - low albumin can mask true hypercalcemia and falsely suggest hypocalcemia (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Mistaking FHH for pHPT: FHH can present with mildly elevated PTH and hypercalcemia, but parathyroid surgery is ineffective; a calcium/creatinine clearance ratio <0.01 and family history of mild asymptomatic hypercalcemia or repeated failed neck surgeries should prompt CASR/GNA11/AP2S1 testing (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Overlooking lithium therapy, which raises the CaSR set point and can cause hyperparathyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Not repleting magnesium in hypocalcemia: hypomagnesemia impairs PTH secretion and the hypocalcemia will not correct until magnesium is restored (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Chvostek's sign is nonspecific - present in ~10% of normal individuals; Trousseau's sign is the more definitive marker of neuromuscular irritability (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Attributing hypercalcemia of immobilization solely to bed rest - an unrecognized resorptive process (pHPT, Paget's, myeloma) may be the real driver, especially in older patients (Harrison 22e p.3271).","When tetany accompanies alkalosis, a second cause of impaired calcium homeostasis is usually present (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Ectopic PTH secretion, unlike pHPT, does not fluctuate with correction of calcium because it is not under CaSR control (Harrison 22e p.3271)."],"management":["Treat symptomatic or severe hypercalcaemia acutely with isotonic saline rehydration, an intravenous bisphosphonate or denosumab, and calcitonin for rapid effect","Direct definitive therapy at the cause: parathyroidectomy for primary hyperparathyroidism, tumour-directed treatment for malignancy-associated hypercalcaemia, and corticosteroids for 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin-D-mediated granulomatous or lymphoma-related hypercalcaemia","Use the calcimimetic cinacalcet to lower calcium in inoperable primary hyperparathyroidism or parathyroid carcinoma","Manage hypocalcaemia and hypoparathyroidism with oral calcium and activated vitamin D such as alfacalcidol or calcitriol, correcting any coexisting hypomagnesaemia","Recognise familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia and manage it conservatively, avoiding unnecessary parathyroid surgery","Withhold or review aggravating agents such as lithium and thiazide diuretics","Monitor serum calcium, phosphate, magnesium and renal function during treatment, with surveillance for renal and skeletal end-organ effects"],"severityClassification":"Hypercalcemia severity (Harrison 22e p.3271): mild 10.5-11.9 mg/dL (largely asymptomatic); moderate >=12-14 mg/dL; severe >=14 mg/dL (progressive lethargy, stupor, coma, prominent GI symptoms, acute renal failure). Hypocalcemia severity ranges from asymptomatic (mild/chronic) to moderate-severe with paresthesias, positive Chvostek's/Trousseau's signs, through severe with seizures, carpopedal spasm, broncho-/laryngospasm, and QTc prolongation with arrhythmia risk.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3271","Harrison 22e ch.57 p.362-366"],"crossLinks":["hypercalcemia","thyroid_cancer"]},"parathyroid_carcinoma":{"name":"Parathyroid carcinoma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["parathyroid cancer","carcinoma of parathyroid gland","malignant hyperparathyroidism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / WHO Classification of Endocrine Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare cause of primary hyperparathyroidism that classically produces markedly higher serum calcium and PTH than a benign adenoma, often with severe symptomatic hypercalcaemia","A palpable neck mass in a patient with primary hyperparathyroidism is an important clue, as benign adenomas are almost never palpable","End-organ damage is frequently prominent, with renal stones or impairment and overt bone disease at presentation","Many tumours harbour inactivation of the CDC73 (HRPT2) tumour-suppressor gene, and germline mutations underlie the hyperparathyroidism-jaw tumour syndrome","Histological diagnosis can be difficult, relying on capsular or vascular invasion or metastasis, and definitive malignancy may only be evident with local recurrence or spread","The best chance of cure is a complete en-bloc resection at the first operation, avoiding capsular rupture and tumour spillage","Control of life-threatening hypercalcaemia with fluids and calcium-lowering measures, and use of calcimimetics for persistent disease, are central to management"],"pathophysiology":"Parathyroid carcinoma is a rare malignancy of the parathyroid chief cells that secretes excessive parathyroid hormone, driving severe hypercalcaemia through increased bone resorption, renal calcium reabsorption, and vitamin D-mediated intestinal calcium absorption. A substantial proportion of tumours show inactivation of the CDC73 (HRPT2) tumour-suppressor gene, with germline mutations causing the hyperparathyroidism-jaw tumour syndrome and predisposing to malignancy. Distinguishing carcinoma from atypical adenoma is challenging histologically and rests on unequivocal invasion of the capsule or vessels, invasion of adjacent structures, or metastasis, so the diagnosis is sometimes confirmed only when the disease recurs or spreads.","additionalDifferentials":["Parathyroid adenoma","Atypical parathyroid adenoma","Parathyroid hyperplasia","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia","Malignancy-associated hypercalcaemia from PTH-related peptide","Tertiary hyperparathyroidism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium and intact PTH, characteristically both markedly elevated","Renal function and imaging for nephrolithiasis or nephrocalcinosis","Assessment of bone disease including bone density and skeletal imaging","Neck ultrasound and sestamibi or 4D-CT localisation, noting features suggesting invasion","CDC73 (HRPT2) germline testing and evaluation for hyperparathyroidism-jaw tumour syndrome","Cross-sectional imaging to detect nodal or distant metastasis in aggressive disease"],"redFlags":["Severe symptomatic hypercalcaemia or hypercalcaemic crisis with dehydration, confusion, and cardiac effects","A palpable neck mass accompanying primary hyperparathyroidism","Very high PTH and calcium far exceeding the usual adenoma range","Local recurrence, hoarseness, or distant metastasis indicating malignant behaviour"],"prognosis":"Complete initial en-bloc resection offers the best outcome, but the tumour tends to recur locally and metastasise late; morbidity and mortality are driven largely by uncontrolled hypercalcaemia rather than tumour bulk.","pitfalls":["Mistaking carcinoma for a benign adenoma intraoperatively and rupturing the capsule, causing tumour seeding","Attributing very high calcium and PTH with a palpable mass to a simple adenoma","Relying on histology alone for diagnosis when features overlap with atypical adenoma","Overlooking a germline CDC73 mutation and the associated familial syndrome"],"management":["Achieve cure through complete en-bloc surgical resection at the first operation, removing the tumour with its capsule intact and avoiding rupture or spillage","Control severe hypercalcaemia perioperatively and in recurrence with saline rehydration, an intravenous bisphosphonate or denosumab, and the calcimimetic cinacalcet","Reoperate for resectable local recurrence or metastasis, as debulking helps control the hormone-driven hypercalcaemia that determines morbidity","Offer CDC73 (HRPT2) germline testing and screen for the hyperparathyroidism-jaw tumour syndrome, with genetic counselling and family screening","Reserve external-beam radiotherapy or systemic therapy for unresectable or progressive disease as adjuncts","Monitor serum calcium and intact PTH long-term as the key markers of recurrence, with cross-sectional imaging for nodal and distant spread","Manage within an endocrine surgery and endocrinology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","WHO Classification of Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"paratubal_cyst":{"name":"Paratubal cyst","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["paraovarian cyst","hydatid of Morgagni","broad ligament cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology reference textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Thin-walled cysts lying within the broad ligament or mesosalpinx, between the fallopian tube and the ovary, and separate from ovarian tissue","They arise from embryological remnants, either mesonephric (Wolffian) or paramesonephric (Müllerian) in origin","The hydatid of Morgagni is a small pedunculated paramesonephric remnant that hangs near the fimbrial end of the tube","Most are asymptomatic and found incidentally at imaging or surgery, appearing as simple unilocular cysts","On ultrasound, demonstrating that the cyst is separate from a normal ipsilateral ovary is the key to diagnosis","Larger cysts may cause pressure symptoms or undergo torsion, sometimes taking the adjacent tube or ovary with them","Management ranges from expectant observation for small simple cysts to surgical excision for symptomatic, enlarging, or atypical lesions"],"pathophysiology":"Paratubal cysts develop from vestigial embryological structures in the broad ligament. Remnants of the mesonephric duct system, or of the paramesonephric duct that gives rise to the tube, persist and accumulate clear serous fluid to form a cyst. Because they lie alongside but outside the ovary, they retain a simple thin-walled structure; the pedunculated hydatid of Morgagni represents a paramesonephric remnant at the fimbrial end and is prone to torsion on its narrow stalk.","additionalDifferentials":["Ovarian follicular or functional cyst","Hydrosalpinx","Peritoneal inclusion cyst","Endometrioma","Cystadenoma","Ovarian neoplasm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound demonstrating a cyst separate from a normal ovary","Application of the split sign to confirm the ovary is distinct from the cyst","Magnetic resonance imaging to localise larger or complex cysts","Tumour markers where features are atypical","Surgical and histological assessment when excision is undertaken"],"redFlags":["Torsion involving the cyst, tube, or ovary presenting with acute pain","Rapid growth or solid vascular components suggesting a rare neoplastic change in the wall","A large cyst causing significant pressure symptoms","Features that cannot confidently be distinguished from ovarian malignancy"],"prognosis":"Paratubal cysts are benign with an excellent outlook; simple cysts require little or no intervention, and surgical removal is curative when symptoms or diagnostic uncertainty warrant it.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a paratubal cyst for an ovarian cyst and misdirecting management","Failing to recognise torsion involving the adjacent tube or ovary","Overlooking the rare borderline or neoplastic change that can arise in the cyst wall","Unnecessary intervention for small, asymptomatic, clearly benign cysts"],"management":["Manage small, asymptomatic, simple cysts expectantly, as they are benign and usually found incidentally","Confirm on transvaginal ultrasound that the cyst is separate from a normal ipsilateral ovary, using the split sign, before deciding on management","Offer surgical excision by laparoscopic cystectomy for cysts that are large, symptomatic, enlarging or diagnostically uncertain","Treat adnexal torsion as a surgical emergency with prompt laparoscopy, detorsion and cyst removal, preserving the tube and ovary where viable","Send excised tissue for histological confirmation of the benign diagnosis","Use MRI or tumour markers when imaging features are atypical to exclude a neoplastic lesion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference textbook","Peer-reviewed gynaecology clinical reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"parinaud_syndrome":{"name":"Parinaud syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dorsal midbrain syndrome","pretectal syndrome","Sylvian aqueduct syndrome","vertical gaze palsy syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic triad is a supranuclear upgaze palsy, convergence-retraction nystagmus on attempted upward saccades, and light-near dissociation of the pupils","Pupils are typically mid-dilated and react poorly to light but constrict on convergence (light-near dissociation)","Lid retraction (Collier sign) and impaired convergence or accommodation may accompany the gaze findings","Convergence-retraction nystagmus is best elicited with a downward-rotating optokinetic drum, provoking co-contraction of horizontal recti","In children the commonest cause is a pineal region tumour, and in young adults demyelination or hydrocephalus","In older patients midbrain infarction and hydrocephalus with dilation of the third ventricle are frequent causes","Downgaze is usually preserved early, distinguishing it from progressive supranuclear palsy where downgaze is prominently affected"],"pathophysiology":"The dorsal midbrain and pretectal region contain the rostral interstitial nucleus of the medial longitudinal fasciculus and the posterior commissure fibres that mediate vertical gaze, together with the pretectal olivary nuclei serving the pupillary light reflex. Compression or damage here abolishes voluntary upgaze, disrupts the light reflex pathway while sparing the near response, and disinhibits vergence pathways producing convergence-retraction movements. Causes include pineal and tectal tumours compressing the region, obstructive hydrocephalus dilating the aqueduct and third ventricle, midbrain infarction, and demyelination.","additionalDifferentials":["Progressive supranuclear palsy with vertical gaze failure","Thyroid eye disease limiting elevation","Myasthenia gravis with variable ophthalmoplegia","Bilateral oculomotor nerve involvement","Wernicke encephalopathy with gaze disturbance","Congenital or physiological limitation of upgaze in the elderly"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain focused on the pineal region and dorsal midbrain","Imaging assessment of the ventricular system for hydrocephalus","Serum and cerebrospinal fluid tumour markers when a pineal germ cell tumour is suspected","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis where demyelination or infiltration is possible","Pupillary testing to document light-near dissociation","Vascular workup in older patients with an acute presentation"],"redFlags":["Signs of raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus requiring urgent neurosurgical assessment","A pineal or tectal mass on imaging needing prompt oncological and surgical evaluation","Rapidly progressive gaze failure with headache and papilloedema","New reduced consciousness suggesting acute aqueduct obstruction"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends entirely on the cause; relief of hydrocephalus or treatment of a tumour can improve the eye signs, while some patients retain a chronic upgaze limitation and pupillary abnormality.","pitfalls":["Confusing the syndrome with progressive supranuclear palsy, which characteristically affects downgaze","Missing early hydrocephalus that is treatable if recognised","Overlooking light-near dissociation because pupil testing is not performed in the near","Attributing upgaze limitation in the elderly solely to ageing and failing to image"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying cause, as the gaze syndrome itself has no specific drug therapy","Relieve obstructive hydrocephalus urgently with cerebrospinal fluid diversion such as endoscopic third ventriculostomy or ventricular shunting","Treat a causative pineal or tectal tumour with the appropriate combination of surgery, radiotherapy and chemotherapy, guided by tissue diagnosis and tumour markers","Manage midbrain infarction or demyelination as the acute cause requires, including immunomodulation for a demyelinating relapse","Offer symptomatic relief of diplopia and convergence problems with prisms, and consider strabismus surgery for stable, persistent upgaze limitation","Monitor eye signs and ventricular size over time, coordinating neurosurgery, neurology and neuro-ophthalmology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"paris_trousseau_syndrome":{"name":"Paris-Trousseau syndrome (FLI1, 11q deletion)","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FLI1","Paris-Trousseau thrombocytopenia","TCPT","Jacobsen syndrome-associated thrombocytopenia","11q terminal deletion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / Nathan and Oski","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital thrombocytopenia arising from deletion of the distal long arm of chromosome 11 (11q), which removes one copy of the megakaryocytic transcription factor FLI1","It is the haematological component of Jacobsen syndrome, which also features dysmorphism, cardiac defects, and intellectual disability","The blood film characteristically shows a subset of platelets containing giant alpha-granules (fused granules)","The bone marrow contains an increased number of small, immature, dysplastic micromegakaryocytes","Thrombocytopenia is often present from birth and may improve somewhat during childhood, though a functional platelet defect persists","Bleeding tendency is variable and platelet function studies are frequently abnormal","Because it usually reflects a contiguous-gene deletion, associated congenital anomalies should be actively sought","Diagnosis combines the giant alpha-granule finding, marrow micromegakaryocytes, and cytogenetic or molecular demonstration of the 11q deletion"],"pathophysiology":"Paris-Trousseau syndrome results from hemizygous loss of FLI1, an ETS-family transcription factor essential for megakaryocyte differentiation, as part of a terminal 11q deletion (Jacobsen syndrome). Reduced FLI1 dosage disrupts the transcriptional programme of megakaryopoiesis, producing dysplastic micromegakaryocytes and a subpopulation of platelets with abnormally fused giant alpha-granules that do not release their contents normally. The result is thrombocytopenia with impaired platelet function; because the deletion spans multiple contiguous genes, the haematological defect coexists with the developmental features of Jacobsen syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Jacobsen syndrome without prominent haematological features","Grey platelet syndrome (alpha-granule deficiency)","Other inherited macrothrombocytopenias","Immune thrombocytopenia (acquired)","GATA1-related cytopenia","Neonatal thrombocytopenia of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and film looking for giant alpha-granule-containing platelets","Bone marrow examination for dysplastic micromegakaryocytes","Cytogenetic analysis and array CGH to define the 11q deletion","FLI1 status assessment within the deleted region","Platelet function testing","Echocardiography and developmental/dysmorphology assessment for Jacobsen syndrome features"],"redFlags":["Significant bleeding from thrombocytopenia and platelet dysfunction, especially in neonates","Serious congenital cardiac defects associated with Jacobsen syndrome","Feeding, growth, or developmental concerns requiring multidisciplinary input","Perioperative haemorrhage requiring platelet support"],"prognosis":"The thrombocytopenia often partially improves during childhood, but a functional platelet defect and the systemic features of Jacobsen syndrome determine overall prognosis, which varies with the extent of associated anomalies.","pitfalls":["Treating the low count as immune thrombocytopenia and missing the underlying chromosomal deletion","Failing to investigate for associated Jacobsen syndrome anomalies once the deletion is identified","Overlooking the giant alpha-granule clue on the blood film","Assuming normal platelet function once the count recovers, despite a persistent qualitative defect"],"management":["Manage supportively, as there is no disease-specific therapy to correct the platelet defect","Transfuse platelets for significant bleeding or to cover surgery and invasive procedures","Use antifibrinolytics such as tranexamic acid for mucosal bleeding, and avoid antiplatelet agents including aspirin and NSAIDs","Monitor the platelet count over time, recognising that thrombocytopenia often partially improves through childhood while a functional defect persists","Address the associated Jacobsen syndrome features with cardiology assessment for congenital heart defects and paediatric developmental support","Provide genetic counselling and parental karyotyping regarding the 11q deletion, with prenatal counselling for future pregnancies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"parkes_weber_syndrome":{"name":"Parkes-Weber syndrome","system":"Vascular anomalies / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PWS","Parkes Weber syndrome","capillary malformation with arteriovenous fistula and limb overgrowth"],"chapter":null,"source":"ISSVA classification / Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A high-flow overgrowth syndrome characterised by a cutaneous capillary malformation over a limb combined with multiple arteriovenous fistulae and diffuse limb hypertrophy","It is a fast-flow condition, which fundamentally distinguishes it from the slow-flow Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome despite superficial resemblance","Many cases fall within the RASA1-related spectrum of capillary malformation-AVM syndrome","A palpable thrill, audible bruit and warmth over the affected limb point to significant arteriovenous shunting","High-output cardiac failure can develop when shunting is extensive, particularly in infancy","Chronic shunting causes venous hypertension, skin changes, ulceration and limb-length discrepancy","Doppler ultrasound and MRA are central to confirming fast flow and mapping the fistulae","Management is multidisciplinary, involving compression, orthopaedic care of leg-length discrepancy, and selective embolisation of symptomatic fistulae"],"pathophysiology":"Parkes-Weber syndrome is a congenital fast-flow vascular anomaly in which a limb bears a capillary malformation together with numerous microscopic and macroscopic arteriovenous fistulae, causing direct arterial-to-venous shunting throughout the affected extremity. Many patients carry germline RASA1 variants within the capillary malformation-AVM spectrum, disrupting RAS-MAPK control of vascular differentiation. The persistent high-flow shunting drives limb overgrowth, venous hypertension and, when substantial, high-output cardiac strain; this fast-flow physiology is the key contrast with the slow-flow Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome (slow-flow, no significant AV shunting)","Capillary malformation-AVM syndrome (RASA1)","Isolated arteriovenous malformation of a limb","CLOVES and other PIK3CA-related overgrowth syndromes","Isolated capillary (port-wine) malformation","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia with limb fistulae"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Doppler ultrasound to demonstrate fast flow and arteriovenous shunting","MRI/MRA to map fistulae, soft-tissue and bony overgrowth","Echocardiography to assess for high-output cardiac effects","Serial limb-length measurement and orthopaedic assessment","Catheter angiography when embolisation is planned","Genetic testing for RASA1 variants"],"redFlags":["Signs of high-output cardiac failure","Rapidly progressive limb enlargement or worsening pain","Non-healing ulceration or significant bleeding from the limb","Distal ischaemia or steal phenomena from shunting","New neurological symptoms if associated central nervous system lesions are present"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the extent of arteriovenous shunting; limited disease may be managed conservatively, but extensive fistulae can cause progressive limb morbidity, ulceration and cardiac strain. High-flow lesions are difficult to cure and often require staged, palliative interventions with long-term follow-up.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with slow-flow Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome and missing the high-flow shunting","Overlooking high-output cardiac effects in an infant with extensive fistulae","Attempting complete surgical cure of diffuse fistulae rather than selective control","Neglecting orthopaedic management of limb-length discrepancy"],"management":["Manage the arteriovenous fistulae with staged catheter embolisation, reserving surgical resection for selected lesions, as high-flow disease is difficult to cure","Use compression garments and diligent skin and wound care to manage venous hypertension, oedema and ulceration","Treat high-output cardiac failure medically and by reducing the shunt burden, particularly in infancy","Address limb-length discrepancy and soft-tissue overgrowth with orthopaedic input, including shoe raises and timed epiphysiodesis","Consider targeted MEK-pathway inhibition such as trametinib for extensive RASA1-related disease within specialist or trial settings","Offer RASA1 genetic testing and family counselling given the capillary malformation-arteriovenous malformation spectrum","Monitor with serial echocardiography and limb imaging, coordinating care through a vascular anomalies multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ISSVA Classification of Vascular Anomalies","Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies: Haemangiomas and Malformations"],"crossLinks":[]},"parkinsons_disease":{"name":"Parkinson's Disease","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PD","parkinsonism"],"chapter":446,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3493","clinicalPearls":["PD is the second most common age-related neurodegenerative disease after Alzheimer's; mean age of onset ~60 years, lifetime risk ~3% in men and ~2% in women, though cases can occur in the twenties or younger, particularly with a pathogenic gene mutation (p.3493).","Cardinal motor features are bradykinesia, rest tremor, rigidity, and gait dysfunction with postural instability; additional motor features include freezing of gait, micrographia, masked facies (hypomimia), reduced eye blinking, drooling, soft voice (hypophonia), and dysphagia (p.3493-3494).","Parkinsonism (bradykinesia with rigidity and/or tremor) combined with rest tremor, asymmetry of motor impairment, and a good levodopa response is much more likely to predict the correct pathologic diagnosis; with these U.K. Brain Bank Criteria, PD can be confirmed pathologically in >90% of patients (p.3495).","Levodopa is the gold standard symptomatic therapy; almost all PD patients improve, and failure to respond to an adequate trial of levodopa should cause the diagnosis to be questioned (p.3501).","Dopamine-system imaging (PET/SPECT) typically shows reduced, asymmetric striatal uptake, most pronounced in the posterior putamen with relative sparing of the caudate; it is useful when the diagnosis is uncertain but is not routinely required (p.3495-3496).","Among forms of parkinsonism, PD is the most common (~75% of cases) (p.3495)."],"pathophysiology":"Pathologic hallmarks are degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc), reduced striatal dopamine, and intraneuronal alpha-synuclein-staining inclusions in cell bodies and axons (Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites; collectively Lewy pathology). Lewy pathology can also affect cholinergic neurons of the nucleus basalis of Meynert, noradrenergic neurons of the locus coeruleus, serotonergic raphe neurons, and the olfactory system, cerebral hemispheres, spinal cord, and peripheral autonomic nervous system, accounting for nonmotor features. Pathology may begin in the peripheral autonomic nervous system, GI tract, olfactory system, or dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus and spread sequentially (Braak staging), giving rise to 'body-first' versus 'brain-first' concepts. In the basal-ganglia network, dopamine denervation leads to increased firing of the subthalamic nucleus (STN) and globus pallidus interna (GPi), excessive inhibition of the thalamus, reduced cortical motor activation, and the emergence of parkinsonian features. Pathogenesis involves an interacting network of oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction, protein aggregation, excitotoxicity, and inflammation; a 'double hit' of genetic susceptibility plus an environmental insult has been proposed (p.3493-3494, p.3498-3500).","additionalDifferentials":["Atypical parkinsonism: multiple-system atrophy (MSA) - cerebellar (MSA-c) and Parkinson (MSA-p) types","Progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP) - parkinsonian and Richardson variants","Corticobasal syndrome (CBS)","Dementia with Lewy bodies / PD with dementia","Secondary parkinsonism: drug-induced, tumor, infection, vascular, normal-pressure hydrocephalus, trauma, liver failure, toxins (carbon monoxide, manganese, MPTP, cyanide, hexane, methanol, carbon disulfide)","Essential tremor (high-frequency 6-10 Hz symmetric postural tremor with prominent kinetic component)","Wilson's disease","Huntington's disease (especially the juvenile Westphal variant)","Dopa-responsive dystonia (DRD)","Spinocerebellar ataxias and neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation (e.g., PANK-associated neurodegeneration)"],"infectionMimics":["Infection-related secondary parkinsonism (basal ganglia damage from CNS infection)","Post-influenza / postencephalitic parkinsonism (historical association with the influenza A epidemic); new-onset PD has been reported following SARS-CoV-2 infection, which can also worsen existing PD features"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug-induced parkinsonism - most commonly dopamine-blocking neuroleptics, and also metoclopramide (a neuroleptic agent used for GI problems), tetrabenazine, calcium channel blockers (flunarizine, cinnarizine), amiodarone, and lithium","Vascular parkinsonism (stroke affecting the SNc may closely resemble PD)","Toxin-induced parkinsonism (carbon monoxide or manganese damaging the globus pallidus resemble atypical parkinsonism with poor levodopa response)","Tumor and normal-pressure hydrocephalus","Atypical parkinsonism (MSA, PSP, CBS)","Essential tremor","Dopa-responsive dystonia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Dopamine-system imaging with PET or SPECT (reduced, asymmetric striatal/posterior-putamen uptake); not routinely required","Metabolic basal ganglia/thalamus imaging (18F-deoxyglucose) - may help distinguish atypical parkinsonism (decreased GPi with increased thalamic activity, the reverse of PD)","MRI - to evaluate for vascular/structural causes and atypical features (e.g., midbrain 'hummingbird sign' in PSP, putaminal rim change or pontine 'hot cross bun' sign in MSA); transcranial sonography or special MRI protocols may detect increased SNc iron","Alpha-synuclein seeding amplification assay (SAA) on CSF or skin - high sensitivity and specificity, can distinguish PD from other parkinsonisms (currently mainly research use)","Genetic testing - selective use for strong family history, early onset (<40 years), or particular ethnic backgrounds (GBA1, LRRK2, SNCA, etc.); monogenic forms account for ~5% of cases (~15% including GBA1 variants)","Serum/urinary copper studies to exclude Wilson's disease, which is treatable with copper chelators"],"redFlags":["Early speech and gait impairment with early falls, lack of rest tremor, and lack of motor asymmetry - suggests atypical parkinsonism","Poor or no response to an adequate trial of levodopa - suggests atypical or secondary parkinsonism","Exposure to neuroleptics - suggests drug-induced parkinsonism","Onset before age 40 years - consider a genetic form of PD, Wilson's disease, or dopa-responsive dystonia","Liver disease - consider Wilson's disease or non-Wilsonian hepatolenticular degeneration","Hallucinations and dementia preceding or as the first/early feature - suggests dementia with Lewy bodies","Diplopia, impaired vertical gaze, slow saccades with impaired downgaze - suggests PSP","Prominent orthostatic hypotension or prominent cerebellar signs - suggests MSA"],"prognosis":"PD is progressive. Levodopa prolongs independence and employability, improves quality of life, and increases life span, but chronic treatment with ongoing disease progression is associated in most patients with motor complications - response fluctuations ('on'/'off', wearing-off, on-off phenomenon) and dyskinesias. As the disease advances, nondopaminergic features that respond poorly to levodopa emerge - falling, freezing, autonomic dysfunction, sleep disorders, and dementia - and these (especially falls and dementia) are the primary source of disability and the main reason for hospitalization and nursing home placement in advanced PD. Atypical parkinsonisms generally follow a more aggressive course than classic PD (p.3496-3497, p.3501).","pitfalls":["Diagnosing PD on two of three cardinal features alone carries a ~24% postmortem error rate; rest tremor, motor asymmetry, and a good levodopa response markedly improve diagnostic accuracy (p.3495).","Drug-induced parkinsonism is the most common secondary parkinsonism and is easily missed - remember that metoclopramide, used for GI symptoms, is a neuroleptic, and that calcium channel blockers (flunarizine, cinnarizine), tetrabenazine, amiodarone, and lithium can also be responsible (p.3497).","Wilson's disease must be actively excluded because progression can be prevented with copper chelators (p.3497).","Dopamine-system imaging cannot separate PD from atypical parkinsonism, since striatal dopamine depletion occurs in both (p.3496).","Some genetic forms of PD (e.g., certain LRRK2 cases) may occur without Lewy bodies, and MPTP toxicity causes parkinsonism typically without Lewy bodies (p.3496, p.3498)."],"management":["Use levodopa combined with a peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor as the mainstay symptomatic therapy, titrated to motor benefit","Employ dopamine agonists, MAO-B inhibitors and COMT inhibitors as alternatives or adjuncts, particularly in younger patients or to smooth the motor response","Manage motor complications such as wearing-off, on-off fluctuations and dyskinesia by fractionating levodopa dosing and adding amantadine for dyskinesia","Offer advanced therapies for refractory fluctuations, including deep brain stimulation, continuous apomorphine infusion, or intestinal levodopa-carbidopa gel","Treat non-motor features with laxatives for constipation, measures for orthostatic hypotension, and quetiapine, clozapine or pimavanserin for psychosis while avoiding typical antipsychotics","Provide multidisciplinary rehabilitation with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech and language therapy for hypophonia and dysphagia, and a Parkinson's specialist nurse","Offer genetic counselling for early-onset or strongly familial disease such as GBA1 or LRRK2 variants","Monitor for progression, falls, cognitive decline and treatment side effects, escalating to palliative care in advanced disease"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not present a single formal severity grading; clinical diagnostic confidence is staged by the MDS Clinical Diagnostic Criteria into 'clinically established PD' and 'clinically probable PD', which combine core motor parkinsonism with supportive criteria, absolute exclusion criteria, and red flags. Disease evolution is commonly framed by the emergence of levodopa-related motor complications and levodopa-unresponsive nondopaminergic features in advanced PD (p.3495-3496, p.3501).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3493"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_cold_haemoglobinuria":{"name":"Paroxysmal cold haemoglobinuria","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCH","Donath-Landsteiner haemolytic anaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An uncommon cold-antibody haemolytic anaemia causing acute intravascular haemolysis after cold exposure","It is most often seen in children following a viral infection and is usually self-limiting","The causative Donath-Landsteiner antibody is a biphasic IgG that binds red cells in the cold and fixes complement on rewarming","The antibody classically has specificity for the P antigen","Presentation is with fever, back or abdominal pain, pallor and passage of dark (haemoglobinuric) urine","Management is supportive with keeping warm and transfusion if needed; most childhood cases resolve spontaneously"],"pathophysiology":"Paroxysmal cold haemoglobinuria is mediated by the Donath-Landsteiner antibody, a biphasic IgG autoantibody usually directed at the P antigen. In the cooler peripheral circulation the antibody binds red cells and fixes early complement components; when the cells return to the warmer central circulation, the complement cascade completes, causing intravascular haemolysis and haemoglobinuria. It frequently follows a viral illness, particularly in children, and the transient antibody explains the self-limiting course.","additionalDifferentials":["Cold agglutinin disease","Warm autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency haemolysis","Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria","Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Donath-Landsteiner test","Direct antiglobulin test (often positive for complement only)","Full blood count, reticulocytes and haemolysis markers","Urinalysis for haemoglobinuria","History of recent viral infection"],"redFlags":["Severe acute anaemia with haemodynamic compromise","Massive intravascular haemolysis with renal impairment","Recurrent episodes suggesting an underlying disorder","Very dark urine with rapidly falling haemoglobin"],"prognosis":"The post-viral childhood form is usually acute and self-limiting with an excellent prognosis; chronic or recurrent disease is rarer and may relate to an underlying condition.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with cold agglutinin disease (different antibody and mechanism)","Not requesting the Donath-Landsteiner test","Failing to keep the patient warm during care and transfusion","Overlooking haemoglobinuria as a sign of intravascular haemolysis"],"management":["Manage supportively, as the post-viral childhood form is usually acute and self-limiting","Keep the patient warm and avoid cold exposure to prevent the biphasic Donath-Landsteiner antibody from binding red cells","Transfuse for symptomatic anaemia, warming both the patient and the blood given via an in-line warmer","Provide intravenous fluids to maintain renal perfusion and urine output during intravascular haemolysis and haemoglobinuria","Identify and treat any underlying or precipitating infection","Monitor haemoglobin, reticulocytes and haemolysis markers until recovery, reserving corticosteroids or other immunosuppression for the rare persistent or chronic cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Haematology reference texts (standard reference)","Transfusion medicine references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_cold_hemoglobinuria":{"name":"Paroxysmal cold haemoglobinuria","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCH","Donath-Landsteiner haemolytic anaemia","biphasic haemolysin haemolytic anaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An uncommon autoimmune haemolytic anaemia caused by a biphasic IgG autoantibody, the Donath-Landsteiner antibody, usually directed against the P antigen","It most often occurs in children after a viral infection and produces acute, sometimes dramatic, intravascular haemolysis","Classic features are cold-triggered episodes of haemoglobinuria with fever, back or abdominal pain and pallor","The Donath-Landsteiner test is diagnostic: the antibody binds red cells and fixes complement in the cold, then triggers lysis on rewarming","The direct antiglobulin test is typically positive for complement (C3d) and negative for IgG because the IgG antibody elutes on warming","Unlike cold agglutinin disease, haemolysis is predominantly intravascular and the antibody is IgG rather than IgM","In children the disorder is usually acute and self-limiting, and management is supportive with warmth and transfusion when needed"],"pathophysiology":"Paroxysmal cold haemoglobinuria is mediated by a biphasic IgG autoantibody, the Donath-Landsteiner antibody, classically specific for the P blood group antigen. In the cooler peripheral circulation the antibody binds red cells and fixes early complement components; when the cells return to core body temperature the complement cascade proceeds to completion, producing complement-mediated intravascular haemolysis and haemoglobinuria. The antibody itself detaches on warming, which is why the direct antiglobulin test detects complement rather than IgG. The disorder most often arises transiently after viral or other infections in children.","additionalDifferentials":["Cold agglutinin disease","Warm autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency with acute haemolysis","Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria","Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia","Transfusion or other acute intravascular haemolysis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Donath-Landsteiner biphasic haemolysin test","Direct antiglobulin test with monospecific C3d and IgG reagents","Blood film and haemolysis markers including free haemoglobin","Urinalysis for haemoglobinuria","Reticulocyte count, lactate dehydrogenase, haptoglobin and bilirubin","Screening for a preceding or concurrent infection"],"redFlags":["Rapid, severe haemoglobin fall with haemodynamic instability","Marked haemoglobinuria with risk of acute kidney injury","Recurrent episodes suggesting a persistent or secondary cause","Haemolysis in an adult prompting evaluation for underlying malignancy or syphilis"],"prognosis":"Post-infectious paroxysmal cold haemoglobinuria in children is usually acute and self-limiting with full recovery once the trigger resolves; supportive care and keeping the patient warm are generally sufficient.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with cold agglutinin disease and misdirecting therapy","Not requesting the specific Donath-Landsteiner test, so the diagnosis is missed","Allowing the patient or blood products to be cold, precipitating further haemolysis","Overlooking rare chronic adult forms historically linked to syphilis"],"management":["Manage supportively, as the post-infectious childhood form is usually acute and self-limiting with full recovery","Keep the patient warm and avoid cold exposure to prevent the biphasic Donath-Landsteiner antibody from binding red cells","Transfuse for symptomatic anaemia, warming both the patient and the blood via an in-line warmer","Provide intravenous fluids to maintain renal perfusion during intravascular haemolysis and haemoglobinuria","Identify and treat any preceding or concurrent infection that triggered the episode","Monitor haemoglobin, reticulocytes and haemolysis markers until recovery, reserving corticosteroids or other immunosuppression for rare persistent or chronic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_dyskinesia":{"name":"Paroxysmal dyskinesia","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PxD","paroxysmal kinesigenic dyskinesia","PKD","paroxysmal non-kinesigenic dyskinesia","PNKD","paroxysmal exercise-induced dyskinesia","PED"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of episodic movement disorders producing transient attacks of dystonia, chorea, or ballism with normal neurological function between episodes","Classified by trigger: kinesigenic (provoked by sudden movement), non-kinesigenic (spontaneous, often with alcohol or caffeine as triggers), and exercise-induced forms","Paroxysmal kinesigenic dyskinesia commonly begins in childhood or adolescence, causes brief frequent attacks, and is frequently linked to PRRT2 gene mutations","Paroxysmal non-kinesigenic dyskinesia tends to cause longer, less frequent attacks and is associated with PNKD (MR-1) gene mutations","Paroxysmal exercise-induced dyskinesia is provoked by sustained exertion and can be associated with GLUT1 deficiency, which has important dietary treatment implications","Consciousness is preserved during attacks, which helps distinguish these disorders from epileptic seizures","Kinesigenic forms are often strikingly responsive to low-dose anticonvulsant therapy, whereas non-kinesigenic forms respond less predictably and rely on trigger avoidance","Secondary paroxysmal dyskinesias can occur with multiple sclerosis, stroke, metabolic disturbance, and other structural or acquired causes, which should be excluded"],"pathophysiology":"Paroxysmal dyskinesias are episodic disorders of movement control involving basal ganglia and related motor circuits, with a strong genetic basis in the primary forms. Mutations in PRRT2 affect a synaptic protein and underlie many kinesigenic cases, PNKD (MR-1) mutations underlie the non-kinesigenic form, and SLC2A1 mutations causing GLUT1 deficiency impair glucose transport into the brain and produce exercise-induced attacks. The shared theme is transient, trigger-dependent dysfunction of neuronal excitability and motor network output rather than fixed structural damage, with normal function between events.","additionalDifferentials":["Epileptic seizures, including frontal lobe seizures","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorders","Tics and other hyperkinetic movement disorders","Secondary dyskinesias from multiple sclerosis, stroke, or metabolic disturbance","Episodic ataxias and other paroxysmal neurological disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for PRRT2, PNKD (MR-1), and SLC2A1 (GLUT1) as guided by phenotype","EEG and, if needed, video capture to distinguish attacks from seizures","Cerebrospinal fluid glucose relative to blood glucose where GLUT1 deficiency is suspected","Brain MRI to exclude structural and demyelinating causes","Metabolic screening in atypical or secondary presentations"],"redFlags":["Impaired awareness during episodes suggesting seizures rather than dyskinesia","Fixed or progressive neurological deficits between attacks","New onset in later adult life pointing to a secondary cause","Developmental delay or refractory attacks suggesting an underlying metabolic disorder"],"prognosis":"Primary kinesigenic disease often responds well to treatment and may improve with age, giving a favourable outlook; non-kinesigenic and exercise-induced forms are more variable, though recognition of GLUT1 deficiency allows targeted dietary management with meaningful benefit.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying attacks as epilepsy or as functional without careful history of triggers and preserved awareness","Missing GLUT1 deficiency in exercise-induced cases where dietary therapy would help","Overlooking a secondary cause in later-onset disease","Assuming poor treatment response before trying appropriate agents in kinesigenic forms"],"management":["Treat paroxysmal kinesigenic dyskinesia with low-dose anticonvulsants, especially carbamazepine or another sodium-channel blocker, which are usually highly effective","Manage paroxysmal non-kinesigenic dyskinesia by avoiding triggers such as alcohol and caffeine, using clonazepam for troublesome attacks","Treat exercise-induced dyskinesia due to GLUT1 deficiency with a ketogenic diet, which addresses the underlying brain glucose deficit","Reassure that consciousness is preserved during attacks and that primary kinesigenic disease often improves with age","Offer genetic counselling based on the identified gene, whether PRRT2, PNKD (MR-1) or SLC2A1","Coordinate care with a movement-disorder neurologist and a dietitian where a ketogenic diet is used"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_dyskinesia_secondary":{"name":"paroxysmal dyskinesia secondary","system":"nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["secondary paroxysmal dyskinesia","symptomatic paroxysmal dyskinesia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Secondary paroxysmal dyskinesias are episodic, self-limiting attacks of chorea, dystonia, athetosis or ballism arising from an identifiable acquired cause rather than a primary genetic channelopathy.","Multiple sclerosis is a classic cause, producing brief, stereotyped paroxysmal dystonic or dyskinetic attacks, often precipitated by movement.","Other causes include stroke and basal ganglia lesions, non-ketotic hyperglycaemia, hypoparathyroidism and other calcium disturbances, and central nervous system infection.","Attacks are classified by trigger: kinesigenic (movement-induced), non-kinesigenic (spontaneous) and exertion-induced, mirroring the phenomenology of primary forms.","Consciousness is preserved throughout and there is no ictal EEG correlate, helping distinguish attacks from focal seizures.","A secondary cause is suggested by later age at onset, atypical or prolonged attacks, interictal neurological signs and abnormal neuroimaging.","Kinesigenic secondary forms, like their primary counterparts, often respond well to low-dose sodium-channel-blocking anticonvulsants.","The cornerstone of management is identifying and treating the underlying disorder, which may abolish the attacks."],"pathophysiology":"Secondary paroxysmal dyskinesias arise when an acquired lesion or metabolic disturbance intermittently destabilises basal ganglia or related motor circuits, producing transient, self-terminating episodes of abnormal movement. In demyelinating disease, ephaptic transmission across demyelinated fibres in the basal ganglia, thalamus or their connections is thought to generate the brief, movement-triggered paroxysms. Structural lesions of the striatum or thalamus, and metabolic derangements such as hyperglycaemia or hypocalcaemia, transiently alter neuronal excitability and striatal output. The episodic nature, preserved consciousness and absence of an EEG correlate distinguish these movement paroxysms from epileptic seizures, reflecting a subcortical rather than cortical generator.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary (genetic) paroxysmal kinesigenic and non-kinesigenic dyskinesias","Focal motor seizures and frontal lobe epilepsy","Episodic ataxias","Tonic spasms of multiple sclerosis","Hyperekplexia and startle syndromes","Functional (psychogenic) paroxysmal movement disorder","Tics"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI brain and spinal cord for demyelination or structural lesions","Serum glucose (including for non-ketotic hyperglycaemia) and HbA1c","Serum calcium, phosphate, magnesium and parathyroid hormone","EEG, ideally ictal, to exclude epilepsy","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis and oligoclonal bands where demyelination suspected","Genetic testing (e.g. PRRT2) when a primary disorder remains possible","Autoimmune and paraneoplastic antibody screen in selected cases"],"redFlags":["First presentation of demyelinating disease requiring urgent neurological evaluation","New focal neurological deficit indicating stroke or a structural lesion","Severe hyperglycaemia or profound hypocalcaemia needing urgent correction","Features suggesting central nervous system infection or encephalitis","Prolonged or increasingly frequent attacks with interictal deficits"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause; attacks often resolve or improve markedly once a metabolic derangement is corrected or the primary disorder is treated. Kinesigenic secondary forms are frequently well controlled with low-dose anticonvulsants, whereas outlook otherwise tracks that of the causative disease.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing attacks as epileptic seizures and committing to inappropriate long-term antiepileptic strategy.","Labelling episodes as functional without excluding demyelination, structural lesions or metabolic causes.","Overlooking non-ketotic hyperglycaemia or hypocalcaemia as readily reversible triggers.","Assuming a primary genetic disorder in a late-onset or atypical presentation.","Failing to reassess for multiple sclerosis when paroxysmal attacks are the initial manifestation."],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying cause, correcting non-ketotic hyperglycaemia, restoring calcium in hypoparathyroidism, or treating the causative structural or demyelinating lesion","Treat multiple-sclerosis-related paroxysmal attacks with carbamazepine or another sodium-channel-blocking anticonvulsant, which are often rapidly effective","Control kinesigenic secondary attacks with low-dose anticonvulsants as in the primary form","Manage the primary demyelinating disease with appropriate disease-modifying therapy and treatment of relapses","Correct metabolic derangements and monitor glucose, calcium and related parameters to prevent recurrence","Investigate and follow with neuroimaging and neurology input, as overall prognosis tracks that of the causative disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Movement Disorders reviews of paroxysmal dyskinesias"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_exercise_induced_dyskinesia":{"name":"Paroxysmal exercise-induced dyskinesia","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PED","paroxysmal exertion-induced dyskinesia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / movement disorder literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Episodes of dystonia or choreoathetosis brought on by sustained or prolonged physical exertion, typically affecting the exercised limbs","Attacks come on after minutes of continuous exercise rather than at the sudden onset of movement, distinguishing it from the kinesigenic form","An important cause is glucose transporter type 1 (GLUT1) deficiency due to SLC2A1 mutations, which is treatable with a ketogenic diet","GLUT1-related cases may also have epilepsy, developmental problems and a low cerebrospinal fluid glucose relative to blood","The paroxysmal dyskinesia spectrum overlaps genetically with PRRT2-related disorders","Recognising a GLUT1 cause is critical because dietary therapy can dramatically improve symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"Paroxysmal exercise-induced dyskinesia is a paroxysmal movement disorder in which prolonged exertion triggers transient involuntary movements without loss of consciousness. A major mechanism is impaired glucose transport across the blood-brain barrier from GLUT1 deficiency, so that sustained metabolic demand during exercise outstrips brain glucose supply and precipitates dyskinesia. Other cases relate to channel and synaptic-protein abnormalities within the broader paroxysmal dyskinesia and PRRT2 spectrum, reflecting episodic dysfunction of basal ganglia motor control.","additionalDifferentials":["Paroxysmal kinesigenic dyskinesia","Paroxysmal non-kinesigenic dyskinesia","Dopa-responsive dystonia","Exercise-induced cramps or myopathy","Functional (psychogenic) paroxysmal movements"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for SLC2A1 (GLUT1) and PRRT2","Paired cerebrospinal fluid and blood glucose to assess for GLUT1 deficiency","Detailed characterisation of triggers and episode semiology","MRI of the brain to exclude structural causes","Trial of ketogenic diet where GLUT1 deficiency is confirmed"],"redFlags":["Associated seizures or developmental delay suggesting GLUT1 deficiency","Progressive fixed neurological deficit","Onset with atypical features suggesting a structural lesion","Frequent disabling attacks affecting daily function"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally good, especially when a treatable cause such as GLUT1 deficiency is identified and managed with diet; many other cases are controllable and non-progressive.","pitfalls":["Failing to test for GLUT1 deficiency and missing a diet-responsive cause","Confusing it with the kinesigenic form triggered by sudden movement","Mislabelling episodes as functional without genetic and metabolic workup","Overlooking coexisting epilepsy in GLUT1-related disease"],"management":["Institute a ketogenic or modified Atkins diet as the definitive treatment where GLUT1 deficiency is confirmed, providing ketone fuel to the glucose-starved brain","Advise pacing of exertion and pre-exercise carbohydrate intake to reduce attack frequency","Treat coexisting epilepsy, which is common in GLUT1 deficiency and also responds to the ketogenic diet","Confirm the diagnosis with SLC2A1 and PRRT2 genetic testing and paired cerebrospinal fluid-to-blood glucose, since recognising GLUT1 deficiency is critical","Involve a dietitian to supervise and monitor the ketogenic diet","Offer genetic counselling and coordinate movement-disorder neurology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Movement Disorders textbook (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_hemicrania":{"name":"Paroxysmal hemicrania","system":"Nervous system / Headache","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PH","chronic paroxysmal hemicrania","episodic paroxysmal hemicrania","Sjaastad syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / International Classification of Sleep Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["One of the trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias, marked by strictly unilateral attacks of severe orbital, supraorbital or temporal pain","Attacks are short, roughly two to thirty minutes, but very frequent, often occurring more than five times a day","Each attack carries ipsilateral cranial autonomic features such as tearing, conjunctival redness, nasal congestion or rhinorrhoea, eyelid oedema, and ptosis or miosis","The defining feature is a complete and lasting response to therapeutic indometacin, which serves as both a diagnostic test and the treatment","Compared with cluster headache the attacks are shorter and more frequent, and there is often a female predominance rather than the male predominance of cluster","Distinguished from SUNCT/SUNA by its longer attack duration and by the absolute indometacin response","A supervised indometacin trial with gastroprotection is used to confirm the diagnosis, and neuroimaging should precede labelling the disorder as primary","The course may be episodic with remissions or chronic and unremitting"],"pathophysiology":"Paroxysmal hemicrania belongs to the trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias, a group in which activation of the trigeminovascular system is coupled to a cranial parasympathetic reflex. Functional imaging implicates the posterior hypothalamus in generating attacks, while trigeminal-autonomic reflex activity relayed through the sphenopalatine ganglion drives the ipsilateral autonomic signs such as lacrimation and nasal congestion. Why the disorder shows its characteristic near-universal sensitivity to indometacin remains incompletely understood but may relate to the drug's effects on prostaglandin-mediated vascular tone and intracranial pressure.","additionalDifferentials":["Cluster headache","SUNCT/SUNA","Hemicrania continua with exacerbations","Trigeminal neuralgia","Primary stabbing headache","Symptomatic trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia from a pituitary or posterior fossa lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["A detailed headache diary recording attack duration, frequency and accompanying autonomic features","Supervised indometacin trial, with gastric protection, to confirm the absolute response","MRI brain with dedicated pituitary and cavernous sinus views to exclude a symptomatic cause","Observation and documentation of cranial autonomic signs during an attack","Review of analgesic history to exclude medication-overuse headache"],"redFlags":["Incomplete or absent response to an adequate indometacin trial, prompting a search for a secondary cause","Focal neurological deficit, papilloedema or other signs suggesting a structural lesion","New or changed headache in an older patient or with systemic symptoms","Attacks that fail to fit the expected short-duration, high-frequency pattern"],"prognosis":"With an adequate indometacin trial most patients gain complete relief; the episodic form may remit for long periods, while the chronic form usually requires ongoing preventive therapy, and prolonged indometacin use needs monitoring for gastrointestinal and renal effects.","pitfalls":["Not performing a formal indometacin trial and thereby missing a highly treatable diagnosis","Mislabelling the disorder as cluster headache because of the shared autonomic features","Omitting neuroimaging and overlooking a pituitary or posterior fossa lesion that mimics the syndrome","Continuing long-term indometacin without gastroprotection or renal surveillance"],"management":["Treat with indometacin, which produces a complete and lasting response and serves as both the diagnostic test and the definitive therapy","Co-prescribe gastric protection with a proton-pump inhibitor during indometacin therapy","For patients intolerant of indometacin, trial alternatives such as other COX inhibitors, topiramate or verapamil","Exclude a symptomatic secondary cause with MRI brain including dedicated pituitary and cavernous sinus views","Titrate to the lowest effective indometacin dose and attempt periodic withdrawal in the episodic form to detect remission","Monitor for gastrointestinal and renal adverse effects during prolonged indometacin use"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (ICHD-3)","The Headaches (Olesen et al.)"],"crossLinks":["hemicrania_continua","sunct_syndrome"]},"paroxysmal_kinesigenic_dyskinesia":{"name":"Paroxysmal kinesigenic dyskinesia","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PKD","paroxysmal kinesigenic choreoathetosis","PKC","PRRT2-related dyskinesia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most commonly caused by autosomal dominant variants in PRRT2, a synaptic protein that interacts with SNARE machinery and modulates neuronal excitability","Attacks are triggered by sudden voluntary movement or a startle, such as standing up quickly or beginning to run","Episodes are brief, usually lasting seconds to under a minute, and can recur many times a day","The dyskinesia may be choreic, dystonic, ballistic or mixed, often with a preceding sensory aura in the affected limb, and consciousness is preserved","There is a characteristic dramatic response to low-dose sodium-channel-blocking anticonvulsants such as carbamazepine","PRRT2 variants link PKD with benign familial infantile epilepsy and with hemiplegic migraine in an overlapping spectrum","Onset is typically in childhood or adolescence and attack frequency often declines in adulthood"],"pathophysiology":"Paroxysmal kinesigenic dyskinesia is most often due to heterozygous loss-of-function variants in PRRT2 on chromosome 16, which encodes a presynaptic transmembrane protein that regulates neurotransmitter release through interactions with SNARE proteins and with voltage-gated sodium and calcium channels. Reduced PRRT2 function increases neuronal excitability, so a sudden movement or startle can trigger paroxysmal, self-limited abnormal basal-ganglia output experienced as brief dyskinesia. The shared molecular basis explains the clinical overlap with benign familial infantile seizures and hemiplegic migraine, and the marked benefit from sodium-channel blockade reflects stabilisation of this hyperexcitable state.","additionalDifferentials":["Paroxysmal non-kinesigenic dyskinesia (PNKD/MR1)","Paroxysmal exercise-induced dyskinesia (GLUT1 deficiency)","Focal motor seizures","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder","Tonic spasms of multiple sclerosis","Hyperekplexia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PRRT2 molecular genetic testing","Electroencephalography, ictal if possible, to distinguish from focal epilepsy","Brain MRI to exclude secondary structural causes","Cerebrospinal fluid or blood glucose ratio and SLC2A1 testing when exercise-induced attacks suggest GLUT1 deficiency","Metabolic screen where a secondary cause is suspected","Detailed attack diary documenting triggers, duration and semiology"],"redFlags":["Prolonged attacks or impaired awareness suggesting seizures rather than PKD","Focal neurological deficit or abnormal imaging pointing to a secondary lesion","New onset in mid or late adult life, which raises concern for a secondary cause","Progressive or fixed neurological signs between attacks"],"prognosis":"PKD is a benign, non-degenerative condition; attacks frequently respond dramatically to low-dose sodium-channel-blocking therapy and often diminish or remit with age, so long-term outlook is favourable.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing brief kinesigenic attacks as focal epilepsy and using inappropriate high-dose regimens","Dismissing stereotyped movement-triggered episodes as functional or attention-seeking","Failing to distinguish PKD from GLUT1-related exercise-induced dyskinesia, which needs a different approach","Overlooking the strong therapeutic response to sodium-channel blockers that can confirm the diagnosis"],"management":["First-line disease-specific therapy with low-dose sodium-channel-blocking anticonvulsants such as carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine, which characteristically abolish attacks","Substitute an alternative sodium-channel blocker such as lamotrigine or phenytoin if carbamazepine is not tolerated","Advise gradual warm-up before activity and cautious initiation of sudden movement to reduce kinesigenic triggering","Plan periodic reassessment aimed at dose reduction or withdrawal, since attacks characteristically remit with age","Offer genetic counselling and family assessment for autosomal dominant PRRT2 variants, remaining alert to overlapping benign familial infantile epilepsy and hemiplegic migraine","Refer to a neurologist or movement-disorder specialist to confirm the diagnosis and exclude secondary structural or metabolic causes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_nocturnal":{"name":"Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PNH","paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acquired clonal stem cell disorder in which red cells lack complement-regulating surface proteins and undergo intravascular haemolysis","The classic clinical triad is complement-mediated haemolytic anaemia, a strong tendency to thrombosis, and variable degrees of bone marrow failure","Haemoglobinuria is often most noticeable in the first morning urine, reflecting overnight complement activation, though continuous low-grade haemolysis is the rule","Thrombosis, frequently at unusual sites such as hepatic (Budd-Chiari), portal, mesenteric, or cerebral veins, is the leading cause of death and is disproportionate to the degree of thrombocytopenia","Free haemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide, producing smooth-muscle-related symptoms such as abdominal pain, dysphagia, fatigue, and erectile dysfunction","PNH is closely linked to aplastic anaemia and myelodysplasia, and clones can emerge from a failing marrow, so screening is warranted in these settings","Diagnosis is made by flow cytometry demonstrating deficiency of GPI-anchored proteins (such as CD55 and CD59), often using the FLAER reagent, rather than older lysis tests","Terminal complement inhibition markedly reduces haemolysis and thrombotic risk; anticoagulation is used for thrombotic events, and allogeneic transplant is reserved for marrow failure or refractory disease, with vaccination against encapsulated organisms before complement blockade"],"pathophysiology":"PNH arises from an acquired somatic mutation in the PIGA gene within a haematopoietic stem cell, impairing synthesis of the glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor. As a result, the affected clone and its progeny lack GPI-anchored surface proteins, including the complement regulators CD55 and CD59 that normally protect cells from complement attack. Unprotected red cells are lysed by activated complement, producing chronic intravascular haemolysis with haemoglobinaemia and haemoglobinuria. Complement activation and free haemoglobin also promote platelet activation and consume nitric oxide, driving a marked prothrombotic state and smooth-muscle dystonia. The clone typically expands in the context of marrow injury, explaining its association with aplastic anaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Aplastic anaemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome","Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemias (TTP/HUS)","Other causes of intravascular haemolysis","Inherited haemolytic anaemias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood flow cytometry for GPI-anchored proteins (CD55/CD59) and FLAER to identify and size the PNH clone","Full blood count, reticulocytes, and blood film","Markers of intravascular haemolysis: LDH, haptoglobin, and urinary haemosiderin","Direct antiglobulin test (typically negative) to exclude immune haemolysis","Bone marrow examination with cytogenetics to assess for aplastic anaemia or myelodysplasia","Imaging for suspected venous thrombosis, particularly abdominal veins"],"redFlags":["Acute venous thrombosis, especially Budd-Chiari or other intra-abdominal or cerebral vein thrombosis","Severe haemolytic exacerbation with profound anaemia or acute kidney injury","Progressive pancytopenia indicating evolving marrow failure","Meningococcal or other encapsulated-organism sepsis in patients receiving complement inhibition"],"prognosis":"Historically PNH carried substantial mortality, largely from thrombosis and marrow failure, but terminal complement inhibitors have greatly reduced haemolysis and thrombotic events and improved survival towards that of the general population; outcomes still depend on the degree of underlying marrow failure and access to therapy.","pitfalls":["Not screening for a PNH clone in patients with unexplained intravascular haemolysis, aplastic anaemia, or myelodysplasia","Attributing thrombosis solely to other risk factors and missing the underlying complement-mediated cause","Relying on outdated lysis-based tests instead of flow cytometry for diagnosis","Starting terminal complement inhibition without meningococcal vaccination and infection counselling","Overlooking iron and folate needs or the nitric-oxide-related symptoms that drive morbidity"],"management":["Suppress complement-mediated intravascular haemolysis and thrombotic risk with a terminal complement inhibitor, an anti-C5 monoclonal antibody such as eculizumab or ravulizumab","Vaccinate against encapsulated organisms, particularly meningococcus, before starting complement inhibition, with antibiotic prophylaxis to cover the resulting infection risk","Anticoagulate for established venous thrombosis, including atypical hepatic, portal, mesenteric, or cerebral sites, and consider primary thromboprophylaxis in high-risk clones","Provide supportive care with folic acid, iron replacement for chronic urinary iron loss, and red-cell transfusion when required","Treat associated marrow failure, using immunosuppression such as antithymocyte globulin and ciclosporin for aplastic anaemia","Monitor lactate dehydrogenase, full blood count, and PNH clone size by flow cytometry, with surveillance for thrombosis and breakthrough haemolysis","Reserve allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for severe marrow failure or refractory disease as the only curative option","Manage through a specialist haematology service experienced in complement-mediated disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_nocturnal_haemoglobinuria":{"name":"Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria","system":"Haematological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria","PNH","Marchiafava-Micheli syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Postgraduate Haematology (Hoffbrand)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria is an acquired clonal disorder caused by a somatic PIGA mutation in a haematopoietic stem cell, leading to loss of the GPI-anchored complement regulators CD55 and CD59.","Absence of CD55 and CD59 leaves red cells exposed to unregulated complement, producing chronic intravascular Coombs-negative haemolysis with haemoglobinuria.","The classic triad is intravascular haemolysis, a marked thrombotic tendency and a degree of bone marrow failure.","Thrombosis, often at unusual sites such as the hepatic (Budd-Chiari), portal or cerebral veins, is the leading cause of death.","Smooth-muscle dystonia from nitric oxide scavenging by free haemoglobin causes abdominal pain, dysphagia, erectile dysfunction and fatigue disproportionate to anaemia.","There is a close relationship with aplastic anaemia and myelodysplastic syndrome, and a PNH clone may emerge in that setting.","Diagnosis rests on flow cytometry demonstrating GPI-anchor-deficient cells, using fluorescent aerolysin (FLAER) and antibodies to GPI-linked proteins.","Terminal complement blockade markedly reduces haemolysis and thrombotic risk but mandates meningococcal vaccination beforehand because of the resulting terminal-pathway deficiency."],"pathophysiology":"Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria results from a somatic mutation in the X-linked PIGA gene within a haematopoietic stem cell, impairing synthesis of the glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor. Blood cells derived from this clone lack all GPI-anchored surface proteins, including the complement regulators CD55 (decay-accelerating factor) and CD59 (membrane inhibitor of reactive lysis). Without these regulators, red cells cannot restrain the alternative pathway or the membrane attack complex on their surface, so complement lyses them, producing chronic intravascular haemolysis that worsens during complement-activating stresses such as infection. Liberated free haemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide, causing smooth-muscle dystonia and promoting platelet activation, which, together with complement-mediated effects, underlies the characteristic thrombophilia. Clonal expansion is often favoured in the context of immune-mediated marrow failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","Aplastic anaemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome","Other causes of intravascular haemolysis including microangiopathy","Hereditary haemolytic anaemias","Inherited or acquired thrombophilia with venous thrombosis","March or exertional haemoglobinuria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood flow cytometry with FLAER and GPI-linked marker antibodies on multiple lineages","Markers of intravascular haemolysis: lactate dehydrogenase, haptoglobin, reticulocytes and bilirubin","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test, characteristically negative","Urinalysis for haemoglobinuria and haemosiderinuria","Full blood count and bone marrow examination to assess for marrow failure or myelodysplasia","Cross-sectional or Doppler imaging where thrombosis is suspected","Iron studies, as chronic urinary iron loss can cause deficiency"],"redFlags":["New or worsening abdominal pain suggesting hepatic or mesenteric vein thrombosis","Neurological deficit or headache suggesting cerebral venous thrombosis","Breathlessness or hypoxia suggesting pulmonary embolism or pulmonary hypertension","Rapidly falling blood counts indicating evolving marrow failure","Fever in a patient on terminal complement blockade, given meningococcal risk"],"prognosis":"The natural history is variable and can span many years, but thrombosis and progressive marrow failure are the principal causes of death; terminal complement inhibition has substantially improved survival and quality of life, and allogeneic stem cell transplantation remains the only curative option in selected cases.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because the direct antiglobulin test is negative, when Coombs-negativity is expected.","Overlooking paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria in unexplained venous thrombosis at unusual sites.","Failing to screen for a PNH clone in aplastic anaemia or myelodysplastic syndrome.","Starting terminal complement blockade without prior meningococcal vaccination and counselling.","Attributing fatigue and smooth-muscle symptoms solely to the degree of anaemia rather than to nitric oxide depletion."],"management":["Institute terminal complement blockade with an anti-C5 agent such as eculizumab or ravulizumab to control intravascular haemolysis and reduce thrombosis","Add a proximal complement inhibitor such as pegcetacoplan where clinically significant C3-mediated extravascular haemolysis persists despite C5 blockade","Immunise against meningococcus and other encapsulated organisms before complement inhibition, with prophylactic antibiotics to mitigate infection risk","Anticoagulate for thrombosis at typical or atypical venous sites, which remains the leading cause of death","Give supportive folic acid, iron replacement for urinary iron loss, and transfusion, and treat coexisting aplastic anaemia with immunosuppression such as antithymocyte globulin and ciclosporin","Monitor lactate dehydrogenase, reticulocytes, full blood count, and clone size by flow cytometry, with imaging surveillance for suspected thrombosis","Consider allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, the only curative treatment, for severe or transfusion-dependent marrow failure","Coordinate care through a specialist haematology centre"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Postgraduate Haematology (Hoffbrand), current edition","Wintrobe's Clinical Hematology, current edition","International consensus guidelines on the diagnosis and management of paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_nocturnal_hemoglobinuria":{"name":"Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PNH","PIGA-related haemolysis","paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Marchiafava-Micheli syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / Nathan and Oski's Hematology of Infancy and Childhood","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acquired clonal disorder of haematopoietic stem cells caused by somatic PIGA mutation, not an inherited condition","Loss of the GPI anchor removes the complement regulators CD55 and CD59 from blood cells, leaving red cells vulnerable to complement-mediated intravascular haemolysis","The classic triad is intravascular haemolytic anaemia, cytopenias from associated marrow failure, and a marked predisposition to thrombosis, often at unusual sites","Flow cytometry using FLAER and antibodies to GPI-anchored proteins is the diagnostic gold standard, having replaced the older Ham acidified serum test","Haemoglobinuria classically produces dark morning urine, and free haemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide, contributing to dystonia, abdominal pain, and pulmonary hypertension","Thrombosis, frequently venous and in sites such as the hepatic or cerebral veins, is the leading cause of death","Complement C5 inhibition markedly reduces intravascular haemolysis and thrombotic risk, and allogeneic transplantation is reserved for marrow failure or refractory disease"],"pathophysiology":"Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria arises from an acquired somatic mutation in the X-linked PIGA gene within a haematopoietic stem cell, so it is a clonal but non-inherited disorder. PIGA is required for the first step in synthesis of the glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor that tethers many proteins to the cell surface. Loss of GPI anchoring strips the affected cells of the complement-regulatory proteins CD55 and CD59, which normally protect them from complement attack. Unopposed activation of the alternative complement pathway on red cells causes chronic intravascular haemolysis, releasing free haemoglobin that depletes nitric oxide and drives smooth-muscle dystonia and pulmonary hypertension. The PIGA-mutant clone often expands in the setting of immune-mediated marrow injury (as in aplastic anaemia), and complement activation on platelets and endothelium underlies the characteristic thrombotic tendency.","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","Aplastic anaemia and myelodysplastic syndrome","Other causes of intravascular haemolysis (microangiopathy, mechanical, march haemoglobinuria)","Hereditary haemolytic anaemias","Unexplained venous thrombosis, including Budd-Chiari syndrome","Atypical haemolytic uraemic syndrome and other complement-mediated disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood flow cytometry with FLAER and GPI-anchored protein markers on multiple lineages","Full blood count, reticulocytes, and blood film to assess haemolysis and cytopenias","Haemolysis panel including lactate dehydrogenase, bilirubin, haptoglobin, and urinary haemosiderin","Bone marrow examination and cytogenetics to evaluate associated marrow failure or dysplasia","Iron studies, as chronic urinary iron loss can cause deficiency","Imaging for suspected thrombosis at typical and atypical sites"],"redFlags":["Thrombosis, especially hepatic, portal, cerebral, or other unusual-site venous events","Severe haemolytic crisis with profound anaemia and haemoglobinuria","Progressive pancytopenia indicating evolving marrow failure","Features of pulmonary hypertension or renal impairment from chronic haemolysis"],"prognosis":"The course is variable and can be chronic; thrombosis remains the major cause of mortality, but complement C5 inhibition has substantially improved survival and quality of life, and marrow failure or clonal evolution influences the long-term outlook.","pitfalls":["Assuming PNH is an inherited disease rather than an acquired clonal PIGA disorder","Attributing thrombosis to other causes and failing to test for PNH in unusual-site venous thrombosis","Overlooking a small PNH clone in patients with aplastic anaemia or myelodysplasia","Missing iron deficiency from chronic urinary haemoglobin and haemosiderin loss"],"management":["Control alternative-pathway haemolysis with terminal C5 inhibition using eculizumab or ravulizumab as disease-specific therapy","Escalate to a proximal complement inhibitor such as pegcetacoplan when residual extravascular haemolysis limits the response to C5 blockade","Vaccinate against meningococcus and other encapsulated organisms before starting complement inhibitors and provide antibiotic prophylaxis","Anticoagulate for venous thromboembolism, including hepatic and cerebral veins, given its dominant contribution to mortality","Support with folic acid, iron replacement for chronic urinary iron loss, and transfusion, and treat underlying immune-mediated marrow failure with antithymocyte globulin and ciclosporin","Track lactate dehydrogenase, full blood count, reticulocytes, and PNH clone size by flow cytometry, watching for clonal evolution and thrombosis","Offer allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the only curative option for severe marrow failure or refractory disease","Deliver ongoing care within an experienced haematology service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_nonkinesigenic_dyskinesia":{"name":"Paroxysmal non-kinesigenic dyskinesia","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PNKD","paroxysmal dystonic choreoathetosis","Mount-Reback syndrome","MR1/PNKD gene-related dyskinesia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant disorder classically caused by variants in the PNKD gene (MR1) on chromosome 2, encoding a protein related to stress-response and detoxification pathways","Attacks are not triggered by sudden movement but are provoked by alcohol, caffeine, stress, fatigue or excitement","Episodes are longer than in kinesigenic dyskinesia, typically lasting minutes to hours, and occur less frequently, often only a few times per week or month","The movement disorder is a mixed dystonia and chorea that can involve the limbs, trunk and face, with preserved consciousness","Onset is usually in infancy or childhood and a strong autosomal dominant family history is common","Response to sodium-channel-blocking anticonvulsants is generally poor, in contrast to kinesigenic dyskinesia, and trigger avoidance is a mainstay","Benzodiazepines such as clonazepam may help abort or reduce attacks in some patients"],"pathophysiology":"Paroxysmal non-kinesigenic dyskinesia is classically caused by heterozygous variants in the PNKD gene (also called MR1) on chromosome 2, which encodes a protein homologous to hydroxyacylglutathione hydrolase involved in cellular defence against oxidative and metabolic stress, including by-products of alcohol and caffeine metabolism. Impaired handling of these stressors is thought to transiently destabilise striatal neuronal function, producing prolonged episodes of dystonia and chorea after exposure to recognised triggers. The distinct molecular mechanism explains why attacks are provoked by ingested triggers rather than movement and why they respond poorly to sodium-channel blockade.","additionalDifferentials":["Paroxysmal kinesigenic dyskinesia (PRRT2)","Paroxysmal exercise-induced dyskinesia (GLUT1 deficiency)","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder","Focal or generalised epilepsy","Secondary paroxysmal dyskinesia (multiple sclerosis, structural lesion)","Episodic ataxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PNKD (MR1) molecular genetic testing","Brain MRI to exclude a secondary structural cause","Electroencephalography to distinguish from epileptic events","SLC2A1 testing and cerebrospinal fluid glucose analysis where exercise-induced attacks suggest GLUT1 deficiency","Metabolic and autoimmune screen when a secondary cause is suspected","Attack diary correlating episodes with dietary and lifestyle triggers"],"redFlags":["New onset in adulthood or progressive course suggesting a secondary or structural cause","Loss of awareness during attacks pointing to seizures","Fixed interictal neurological deficits","Abnormal brain imaging requiring further evaluation"],"prognosis":"PNKD is a benign, non-degenerative disorder; while attacks are longer and less responsive to medication than in the kinesigenic form, avoidance of triggers substantially reduces frequency and many patients see attacks lessen over time.","pitfalls":["Expecting a good response to sodium-channel blockers as seen in kinesigenic dyskinesia and escalating therapy inappropriately","Failing to identify and counsel on alcohol and caffeine as key triggers","Confusing prolonged attacks with focal seizures or functional episodes","Overlooking GLUT1 deficiency when exercise rather than ingestion is the main precipitant"],"management":["Make avoidance of recognised triggers the principal management, counselling against alcohol, caffeine, emotional stress, and fatigue","Consider a trial of clonazepam or another benzodiazepine, which may reduce attack frequency and severity","Recognise that sodium-channel-blocking anticonvulsants are generally ineffective, in contrast to the kinesigenic form, and avoid unnecessary escalation","Provide reassurance about the benign, non-degenerative course and the tendency for attacks to lessen over time","Offer genetic counselling and family assessment for autosomal dominant PNKD (MR1) variants","Refer to a neurologist or movement-disorder specialist and maintain an attack diary correlating episodes with dietary and lifestyle triggers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_supraventricular_tachycardias":{"name":"Paroxysmal Supraventricular Tachycardias","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PSVT","AV nodal reentry tachycardia","AVNRT","AV reciprocating tachycardia","AVRT","Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome related SVT"],"chapter":256,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1938","clinicalPearls":["PSVT here refers to sustained supraventricular tachycardias that depend on the AV node: AVNRT, junctional tachycardia, AV reciprocating tachycardia (AVRT) using an accessory pathway, and preexcited tachycardias (Harrison 22e p.1938).","AVNRT is the most common form of PSVT, accounting for roughly 60% of cases referred for catheter ablation, typically presenting in the second to fourth decades and more often in women (Harrison 22e p.1939).","AVNRT mechanism is reentry within the AV node and perinodal atrium, made possible by dual conduction pathways; the typical form uses a slowly conducting pathway (right inferior nodal extension) for the antegrade limb and the fast pathway as the exit (Harrison 22e p.1939).","In typical AVNRT atrial and ventricular activation are near-simultaneous, so the P wave falls during, just before, or just after the QRS and is hard to see — often appearing as a pseudo-r' in V1 and pseudo-S waves in leads II, III, and aVF (Harrison 22e p.1939).","Simultaneous atrial and ventricular contraction drives atrial contraction against a closed tricuspid valve, producing cannon A waves and a fluttering neck sensation; elevated venous pressure can release natriuretic peptides causing post-tachycardia diuresis (Harrison 22e p.1939).","Unlike atrial tachycardias, maneuvers or drugs that block the AV node terminate AVNRT and orthodromic AVRT, because the AV node is part of the reentry circuit (Harrison 22e p.1939).","Accessory pathways occur in about 1 in 1500-2000 people; most patients have structurally normal hearts, but APs are associated with Ebstein's anomaly and certain hypertrophic cardiomyopathies including PRKAG2 mutations, Danon's disease, and Fabry's disease (Harrison 22e p.1940).","Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome is defined as ventricular preexcitation during sinus rhythm (short PR <0.12 s, delta wave, widened QRS) plus episodes of PSVT (Harrison 22e p.1940).","Orthodromic AVRT is the most common AP-mediated PSVT: the wavefront goes antegrade down the AV node/His-Purkinje and returns retrograde up the AP, giving a narrow (or aberrant) QRS with the P wave always following the QRS, never simultaneous with it (Harrison 22e p.1941).","Concealed accessory pathways conduct only retrograde (ventricle to atrium), so no preexcitation is seen in sinus rhythm, yet SVT can still occur (Harrison 22e pp.1940-1941).","Antidromic AVRT and bystander AP conduction produce preexcited (wide-complex) tachycardias that can be indistinguishable from monomorphic VT; the presence of preexcitation in sinus rhythm suggests the diagnosis (Harrison 22e pp.1941-1942).","Junctional ectopic tachycardia arises from automaticity within the AV node, is rare in adults, and is more often seen as an incessant tachycardia in children, especially perioperatively after congenital heart surgery (Harrison 22e p.1940)."],"pathophysiology":"PSVTs in this group are AV node-dependent. AVNRT is a reentrant circuit within the compact AV node and perinodal atrium enabled by dual pathways: a slowly conducting pathway (usually the right inferior nodal extension running toward the coronary sinus floor) and a faster pathway near the His bundle, allowing a self-sustaining loop. Accessory pathways are congenital muscular connections that bridge atrium and ventricle across the fibrous AV rings owing to incomplete partitioning during development; they most often lie at the left free wall, followed by posteroseptal, right free wall, and anteroseptal sites. Antegrade conduction over an AP produces ventricular preexcitation (short PR, delta wave, wide QRS), with QRS/delta morphology determined by pathway location. AVRT reentry circulates between the AV node/His-Purkinje system and the AP (orthodromic when antegrade over the node and retrograde over the AP, antidromic when reversed). Junctional ectopic tachycardia is due to abnormal automaticity within the AV node rather than reentry (Harrison 22e pp.1938-1942).","additionalDifferentials":["Atrioventricular nodal reentry tachycardia (AVNRT)","Orthodromic AV reciprocating tachycardia (concealed or manifest AP)","Atypical AVNRT (long R-P, P wave between QRS complexes)","Focal atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Atrial fibrillation","Permanent junctional reciprocating tachycardia (PJRT) from a slowly conducting septal AP","Junctional ectopic / accelerated junctional rhythm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ventricular tachycardia (especially versus antidromic AVRT or PSVT with aberrancy, wide-complex tachycardia)","Sinus tachycardia","Lateral or inferior wall myocardial infarction Q waves (mimicked by negative delta waves of left-sided or diaphragmatic accessory pathways)","Bundle branch block pattern (mimicked by preexcitation: right-sided AP gives LBBB-like V1, left-sided AP gives RBBB-like V1)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous ECG monitoring during the episode and during vagal/pharmacologic maneuvers (the response can establish the mechanism)","12-lead ECG during tachycardia (P-wave timing and morphology, pseudo-r' in V1, pseudo-S in inferior leads) and during sinus rhythm (delta wave, short PR)","Echocardiography to exclude Ebstein's anomaly, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy associated with APs, or tachycardia-mediated cardiomyopathy","Invasive electrophysiology study to define mechanism and to assess whether an AP can support rapid antegrade conduction (risk stratification)","Ambulatory monitoring or exercise testing to look for abrupt loss of preexcitation suggesting a lower-risk pathway (not completely reliable)"],"redFlags":["Hemodynamic instability with hypotension, loss of consciousness, or respiratory distress","Preexcited atrial fibrillation presenting as a very irregular wide-complex tachycardia","Accessory pathway with shortest preexcited R-R interval <250 ms during AF (about 25% of preexcitation-causing APs), associated with higher risk of ventricular fibrillation and sudden death","Wide-complex tachycardia that cannot be confidently distinguished from VT (manage as VT until proven otherwise)","Rapid SVT in the elderly precipitating angina, pulmonary edema, hypotension, or syncope"],"prognosis":"In patients without structural heart disease, AVNRT is not life-threatening though it may cause significant symptoms. Catheter ablation of the slow AV nodal pathway is curative in over 95% of cases, with a risk of AV block requiring permanent pacing in under 1%. For accessory pathways, ablation efficacy is around 95% (varies by location) with serious complications in under 3% and procedural mortality under 1 in 1000. Risk of cardiac arrest in symptomatic preexcitation patients is roughly 2 per 1000 adults and likely higher in children. Adults with asymptomatic preexcitation have an estimated sudden death risk of about 1 per 1000 patient-years, while children carry a higher risk of around 2 per 1000 patient-years (Harrison 22e pp.1939-1942).","pitfalls":["Administering AV nodal-blocking agents (verapamil, diltiazem, beta blockers, adenosine, amiodarone) during preexcited AF is contraindicated, as slowing the AV node without slowing the AP can accelerate ventricular rate and precipitate ventricular fibrillation (Harrison 22e p.1942).","Gradual loss of AP conduction with rising sympathetic tone on exercise testing does not reliably indicate a low-risk pathway, because it can simply reflect shortening AV nodal conduction time (Harrison 22e p.1942).","Adenosine itself can precipitate atrial fibrillation in up to ~15% of patients, which is hazardous in WPW where AF may cause hemodynamic instability (Harrison 22e p.1942).","Adenosine is contraindicated after cardiac transplantation due to denervation hypersensitivity and may aggravate bronchospasm (Harrison 22e p.1942).","Negative delta waves from left-sided or diaphragmatic/paraseptal APs can mimic Q waves of lateral or inferior myocardial infarction (Harrison 22e p.1940).","Left free wall APs may show minimal or absent preexcitation on the 12-lead ECG because of the distance from the sinus node, so preexcitation can be missed (Harrison 22e p.1940).","PJRT and atypical AVNRT both produce long R-P tachycardias that can be difficult to distinguish from atrial tachycardia without an invasive EP study (Harrison 22e p.1941)."],"management":["Terminate acute AV-node-dependent episodes with vagal manoeuvres such as a modified Valsalva, escalating to intravenous adenosine","Perform synchronised DC cardioversion for haemodynamically unstable tachycardia","Use intravenous AV-nodal-blocking agents such as verapamil, diltiazem, or a beta-blocker when adenosine fails","Offer catheter ablation of the slow AV-nodal pathway or accessory pathway as curative, first-line definitive therapy for recurrent symptomatic disease","Provide chronic suppression with beta-blockers or non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers for patients who decline or defer ablation","In ventricular pre-excitation, avoid AV-nodal-blocking drugs during pre-excited atrial fibrillation and refer for electrophysiological risk stratification and ablation","Teach patients self-administered vagal manoeuvres and refer to a cardiac electrophysiology service"],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grading scale; risk is stratified clinically by hemodynamic stability and, for accessory pathways, by the capacity for rapid antegrade conduction. A shortest preexcited R-R interval <250 ms during atrial fibrillation marks a high-risk pathway predisposing to ventricular fibrillation and sudden death (Harrison 22e p.1942).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1938","Harrison 22e p.1939","Harrison 22e p.1940","Harrison 22e p.1941","Harrison 22e p.1942"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_sympathetic_hyperactivity":{"name":"Paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity","system":"Nervous system / Autonomic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PSH","sympathetic storm","paroxysmal autonomic instability with dystonia","dysautonomia after severe brain injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Episodic 'sympathetic storms' after severe acquired brain injury, most often traumatic but also hypoxic-ischaemic or following stroke and haemorrhage","Paroxysms of tachycardia, hypertension, tachypnoea, hyperthermia, sweating, and abnormal extensor posturing","Episodes are often triggered by otherwise innocuous stimuli such as tracheal suctioning, turning, or pain, reflecting an allodynia-like sensitivity","A diagnosis of exclusion: sepsis, seizures, pulmonary embolism, raised intracranial pressure, and drug withdrawal must be ruled out","Typically emerges during recovery from coma, sometimes weeks into the illness, and is associated with more severe injury and longer hospital stay","Diagnostic likelihood tools such as the PSH-Assessment Measure combine clinical features with their severity","Sustained hyperthermia and hypertension risk secondary brain injury and metabolic stress","Management: minimise and avoid triggering stimuli, provide supportive cooling and hydration, and control sympathetic overactivity pharmacologically alongside treating the underlying injury"],"pathophysiology":"Paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity follows severe brain injury and reflects a loss of the normal inhibitory control that higher centres exert over spinal and brainstem sympathetic circuits. One influential model proposes that injury disconnects descending inhibitory pathways, leaving excitatory spinal circuits disinhibited, so that ordinarily non-noxious stimuli provoke exaggerated, paroxysmal sympathetic discharges (an 'excitatory-to-inhibitory ratio' disturbance). The result is episodic surges of sympathetic outflow producing tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, sweating, and motor posturing, often with allodynia-like sensitivity to stimulation. The paroxysms subside as recovery progresses, but sustained autonomic and metabolic stress can aggravate secondary brain injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Sepsis or systemic infection","Seizure activity, including non-convulsive status epilepticus","Raised intracranial pressure or rebleeding","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, serotonin syndrome, or malignant hyperthermia","Drug or alcohol withdrawal","Pulmonary embolism or another cause of tachycardia and hypoxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous monitoring of heart rate, blood pressure, temperature, and respiratory rate to document paroxysms","Septic screen with cultures, inflammatory markers, and imaging to exclude infection","EEG to exclude seizures and non-convulsive status epilepticus","Neuroimaging to exclude raised intracranial pressure, hydrocephalus, or rebleeding","Application of a diagnostic likelihood tool such as the PSH-Assessment Measure","Review of medications and possible withdrawal states"],"redFlags":["Sustained extreme hyperthermia or hypertension threatening secondary organ or brain injury","Features pointing to untreated sepsis, seizures, or raised intracranial pressure","Haemodynamic instability unresponsive to initial measures","New focal neurological deterioration"],"prognosis":"The presence of paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity is associated with more severe brain injury, longer intensive care and hospital stays, and worse short-term outcomes; the paroxysms themselves usually diminish over weeks to months as the brain recovers, although some autonomic features may persist.","pitfalls":["Anchoring on the diagnosis before excluding sepsis, seizures, and raised intracranial pressure","Provoking paroxysms through unrecognised triggering care activities","Undertreating hyperthermia and hypertension, allowing secondary injury","Abruptly stopping controlling medication and precipitating rebound storms"],"management":["Reduce and cluster noxious stimulation such as suctioning, turning, and pain, since innocuous stimuli provoke paroxysms, and treat any underlying precipitant","Abort acute sympathetic surges with opioids such as morphine and short-acting benzodiazepines","Give scheduled preventive therapy with non-selective beta-blockers such as propranolol, central alpha-2 agonists such as clonidine or dexmedetomidine, and gabapentin","Add bromocriptine, or baclofen for associated dystonia and posturing, as adjuncts in refractory cases","Support with active temperature control, adequate hydration, and high-calorie nutrition to meet the increased metabolic demand","Monitor heart rate, blood pressure, and temperature to document and titrate against paroxysms, having first excluded sepsis, seizures, and raised intracranial pressure","Involve neuro-critical care and begin early neurorehabilitation, including physiotherapy to prevent contractures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_tonic_upgaze":{"name":"Paroxysmal tonic upgaze of childhood","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["benign paroxysmal tonic upgaze","tonic upgaze syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology / paediatric neurology literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Episodes of sustained or intermittent conjugate upward deviation of the eyes, typically beginning in infancy","On attempted downgaze there are characteristic down-beating saccades while the eyes remain deviated upward","Attacks are often worse with fatigue and illness and may improve with sleep","Some children have associated ataxia or developmental and language difficulties","It is frequently benign and self-limiting, but structural and genetic causes must be considered","Associations with CACNA1A and other channel genes have been reported, overlapping with episodic neurological syndromes"],"pathophysiology":"Paroxysmal tonic upgaze reflects episodic dysfunction of the supranuclear control of vertical gaze, with a relative imbalance favouring upward deviation and impaired downgaze during attacks. Many cases are idiopathic and maturational, improving as the nervous system develops, while some are linked to channelopathies or to structural lesions of the midbrain and its vertical-gaze pathways. The down-beating saccades on attempted downgaze reflect preserved attempts to overcome the tonic upward drive.","additionalDifferentials":["Oculogyric crisis (drug-induced or metabolic)","Seizures with tonic eye deviation","Structural midbrain lesion affecting vertical gaze","Opsoclonus or other saccadic intrusions","Spasmus nutans and other childhood eye-movement disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain focusing on the midbrain and posterior fossa","Neurological and developmental assessment","Medication and metabolic review to exclude oculogyric crisis","EEG if seizures are considered","Genetic evaluation where an episodic channelopathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Focal neurological signs or a structural lesion on imaging","Developmental regression","Features suggesting seizures or oculogyric crisis","Progressive or atypical course"],"prognosis":"The idiopathic form usually improves over months to a few years, though some children have residual ataxia or cognitive and language difficulties; prognosis of secondary cases depends on the underlying cause.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with drug-induced oculogyric crisis or seizures","Accepting a benign diagnosis without neuroimaging","Overlooking associated developmental or coordination problems","Missing an underlying channelopathy with broader implications"],"management":["Manage idiopathic cases with reassurance and watchful observation, as most improve spontaneously with maturation","Consider a trial of levodopa, which has benefited some children in reported cases","Investigate and treat any identified structural midbrain lesion or metabolic cause underlying secondary presentations","Monitor development, language, and coordination, arranging early support for associated ataxia or learning difficulties","Pursue genetic evaluation and counselling where an episodic channelopathy such as CACNA1A is suspected","Follow up jointly through paediatric neurology and ophthalmology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"parry_romberg_syndrome":{"name":"Parry-Romberg syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["progressive hemifacial atrophy","progressive facial hemiatrophy","Romberg syndrome","idiopathic hemifacial atrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The condition causes progressive atrophy of the skin, subcutaneous fat, muscle, and sometimes underlying bone and cartilage on one side of the face","It is regarded as lying on the spectrum of localised scleroderma and overlaps clinically with linear scleroderma of the 'en coup de sabre' type","Onset is usually in the first two decades of life, with an active phase of variable duration followed by stabilisation, leaving a characteristic facial asymmetry","Neurological complications, including seizures, migraine, and focal deficits, can occur, sometimes with ipsilateral brain imaging abnormalities","Ophthalmological involvement such as enophthalmos, uveitis, and extraocular changes may accompany the facial atrophy","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by imaging to define the extent of tissue loss and to screen for intracranial involvement","Immunomodulatory therapy may be used during the active inflammatory phase, while reconstructive procedures such as autologous fat transfer are reserved for the burnt-out, stable stage"],"pathophysiology":"Parry-Romberg syndrome is a progressive hemifacial atrophy considered part of the localised scleroderma spectrum. Although the cause is not fully established, an immune-mediated, inflammatory process is favoured, potentially with neurovascular or trophic contributions, leading to progressive loss of dermal, subcutaneous, muscular, and occasionally osseous tissue confined largely to one side of the face. The inflammatory activity tends to be self-limited over months to years, after which atrophy stabilises, but the accumulated tissue loss produces lasting asymmetry. Its overlap with linear scleroderma 'en coup de sabre' supports a shared sclerodermatous mechanism, and associated central nervous system and ocular findings suggest the process can extend beyond the skin.","additionalDifferentials":["Linear scleroderma 'en coup de sabre'","Systemic sclerosis with facial involvement","Lipodystrophy syndromes","Post-traumatic or post-surgical facial soft-tissue loss","Hemifacial microsomia and congenital facial asymmetry","Facial lipoatrophy secondary to panniculitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the face and brain to define soft-tissue and bony atrophy and screen for intracranial involvement","Clinical photography and serial measurement to monitor progression","Dermatological assessment for coexisting sclerodermatous skin changes","Ophthalmological examination for enophthalmos and uveitis","Autoimmune serology where an inflammatory or connective tissue process is being evaluated","EEG if seizures are suspected"],"redFlags":["New seizures or focal neurological signs suggesting intracranial involvement","Rapidly progressive tissue loss during an active inflammatory phase","Ocular inflammation or visual disturbance requiring ophthalmology input","Involvement crossing to bone and cartilage with functional impairment of the eye, mouth, or airway"],"prognosis":"The disease typically progresses over a period of years and then stabilises, leaving fixed facial asymmetry of variable severity. Prognosis for function is generally good once the active phase resolves, though neurological or ocular complications can cause lasting morbidity, and reconstructive surgery in the stable phase can substantially improve appearance.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early atrophy for weight loss or benign asymmetry and missing the active, treatable inflammatory phase","Undertaking reconstructive surgery before the disease has stabilised, risking continued atrophy","Overlooking associated neurological or ocular disease that requires specific management","Failing to recognise the overlap with localised scleroderma and its implications for systemic assessment"],"management":["Suppress the active inflammatory phase with immunosuppression such as methotrexate, often combined with systemic corticosteroids as used in localised scleroderma, with mycophenolate mofetil as an alternative","Monitor disease activity with serial clinical photography, measurement, and MRI to judge progression and treatment response","Defer reconstructive surgery such as autologous fat grafting or free-tissue transfer until the atrophic process has stabilised","Treat neurological complications, using anticonvulsants for seizures and appropriate prophylaxis for migraine","Refer for ophthalmological management of uveitis and other ocular involvement with anti-inflammatory therapy","Coordinate care across dermatology, plastic surgery, neurology, and ophthalmology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (University of Washington)","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pars_planitis":{"name":"Pars planitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pars planitis","idiopathic intermediate uveitis with snowbanking"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A subset of intermediate uveitis defined by snowbanking over the pars plana in the absence of an identifiable systemic infective or inflammatory cause","Characteristically affects children and young adults and is commonly bilateral","Presents with floaters and painless blurring of vision in a quiet-looking eye","Snowballs in the inferior vitreous and a confluent snowbank over the inferior pars plana and ora serrata are typical","Cystoid macular oedema is the chief cause of reduced vision","Neovascularisation of the snowbank may develop and cause vitreous haemorrhage","There is a recognised association with multiple sclerosis, and treatment follows a stepladder of corticosteroids and immunosuppression aimed at controlling macular oedema"],"pathophysiology":"Pars planitis is an idiopathic, chronic, T-cell mediated inflammation of the pars plana and peripheral retina. Organised inflammatory exudate accumulates as a snowbank over the inferior pars plana and ora serrata, while cellular aggregates form snowballs in the vitreous. Persistent inflammation and breakdown of the blood-retinal barrier drive cystoid macular oedema and occasionally peripheral retinal neovascularisation. The condition overlaps with the demyelinating spectrum, hence its association with multiple sclerosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Sarcoid uveitis","Lyme disease","Tuberculous uveitis","Primary intraocular lymphoma","Ocular toxocariasis","Fuchs uveitis syndrome","Other causes of intermediate uveitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Indirect ophthalmoscopy with scleral indentation to demonstrate the inferior snowbank","Optical coherence tomography of the macula for cystoid macular oedema","Fluorescein angiography to detect peripheral leakage and neovascularisation","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain to assess for demyelination","Infective screen including Lyme, syphilis and tuberculosis testing","Sarcoidosis workup to exclude a secondary cause before labelling the disease idiopathic"],"redFlags":["Vitreous haemorrhage from snowbank neovascularisation","Tractional retinal detachment","Neurological symptoms suggesting multiple sclerosis","Refractory cystoid macular oedema threatening central vision"],"prognosis":"The course is typically chronic and relapsing; vision is generally preserved when cystoid macular oedema is controlled, although complications such as cataract, glaucoma and retinal detachment may reduce the final outcome.","pitfalls":["Labelling the disease as pars planitis without first excluding sarcoidosis, Lyme disease and tuberculosis","Missing an association with multiple sclerosis","Overlooking peripheral neovascularisation of the snowbank","Under-treating chronic cystoid macular oedema"],"management":["Control inflammation and cystoid macular oedema with corticosteroids delivered periocularly, intravitreally, or systemically according to severity","Introduce steroid-sparing immunomodulation such as methotrexate, mycophenolate, or azathioprine for chronic or bilateral disease, escalating to anti-TNF biologics such as adalimumab if refractory","Apply peripheral retinal laser photocoagulation or cryotherapy to the snowbank when neovascularisation develops","Consider pars plana vitrectomy for non-clearing vitreous haemorrhage or persistent inflammation","Monitor visual acuity and macular status with optical coherence tomography, watching for cataract, glaucoma, and retinal detachment","Arrange neurology referral and MRI surveillance given the association with demyelination and multiple sclerosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course: Uveitis and Ocular Inflammation"],"crossLinks":[]},"parsonage_turner_syndrome":{"name":"Parsonage-Turner syndrome (neuralgic amyotrophy)","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neuralgic amyotrophy","idiopathic brachial plexopathy","brachial neuritis","acute brachial plexus neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically begins with sudden, severe neuropathic pain around the shoulder and upper arm, often waking the patient at night","As the pain subsides over days to weeks, patchy weakness and wasting of shoulder-girdle and arm muscles emerge","The distribution is patchy and often does not conform to a single nerve or root, reflecting involvement of individual plexus branches","The long thoracic, suprascapular, and anterior interosseous nerves are characteristically affected, producing scapular winging or a weak pinch","Frequently follows an antecedent trigger such as recent infection, immunisation, surgery, or strenuous exertion","Diagnosis is largely clinical, supported by electromyography and MRI, after excluding compressive and structural lesions","Management centres on analgesia for the acute painful phase, physiotherapy, and a role for corticosteroids and immunotherapy in selected cases"],"pathophysiology":"Parsonage-Turner syndrome is an immune-mediated inflammatory disorder of the brachial plexus and its branches. An aberrant immune response, frequently triggered by a preceding infection, vaccination, surgery, or physical stress, produces multifocal inflammation and secondary axonal damage of individual nerve fascicles. This patchy involvement explains why weakness does not follow a single nerve or root territory and why certain nerves such as the long thoracic, suprascapular, and anterior interosseous are preferentially affected. Nerve constrictions and hourglass-like fascicular changes have been demonstrated on imaging and at surgery. The severe early pain reflects the acute inflammatory phase, after which axonal loss produces the weakness and wasting.","additionalDifferentials":["Cervical radiculopathy from disc prolapse or foraminal stenosis","Compressive or infiltrative (e.g. neoplastic) brachial plexopathy","Rotator cuff or other primary shoulder pathology","Mononeuritis multiplex from vasculitis","Hereditary neuralgic amyotrophy (SEPT9)","Thoracic outlet syndrome","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis presenting focally"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electromyography and nerve conduction studies localising patchy plexus or branch involvement","MRI of the brachial plexus showing nerve oedema, and MRI of muscles showing denervation change","MRI cervical spine to exclude radiculopathy or structural lesion","Bloods including inflammatory markers and screen for vasculitis or infection where suspected","Chest imaging to exclude an apical or mediastinal lesion affecting the plexus","Genetic testing for SEPT9 in recurrent or familial cases"],"redFlags":["Progressive or relentless weakness suggesting a compressive or neoplastic plexopathy","Systemic features or multifocal neuropathy raising concern for vasculitis","Bilateral or recurrent episodes suggesting a hereditary cause","Respiratory compromise from phrenic nerve involvement"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover useful function over months to a few years, though recovery can be slow and incomplete, with residual weakness, fatigue, or scapular winging; recurrence is uncommon in the idiopathic form.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the acute pain for a primary shoulder or musculoskeletal problem and delaying diagnosis","Attributing patchy weakness to a single radiculopathy","Overlooking phrenic or long thoracic involvement","Failing to consider a hereditary (SEPT9) cause in recurrent or familial cases","Assuming a uniformly rapid and complete recovery"],"management":["Manage the severe early pain with analgesia and consider a short course of oral corticosteroids in the acute phase, which may shorten pain and hasten recovery when started early","Treat persistent neuropathic pain with agents such as gabapentinoids or amitriptyline","Begin physiotherapy once pain settles, focusing on maintaining range of movement and scapular stabilisation and strengthening","Involve occupational therapy to support function during the prolonged recovery","Monitor recovery clinically and with electromyography, referring for nerve surgery or tendon transfer where hourglass fascicular constrictions or failed recovery persist","Offer genetic testing for SEPT9 and counselling in recurrent or familial hereditary neuralgic amyotrophy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"parvovirus_b19_infection":{"name":"Parvovirus B19 infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["erythrovirus B19","fifth disease","erythema infectiosum","slapped cheek disease","B19V infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by human parvovirus B19, which has a strong tropism for erythroid progenitor cells via the P antigen (globoside) receptor","In children it classically causes erythema infectiosum (fifth disease) with a slapped-cheek facial rash followed by a lacy reticular exanthem on the limbs","In adults, particularly women, it often presents as a symmetrical peripheral arthropathy that can mimic early rheumatoid arthritis","In patients with chronic haemolysis, such as sickle cell disease or hereditary spherocytosis, it precipitates transient aplastic crisis by arresting red-cell production","In immunocompromised hosts persistent infection produces chronic pure red cell aplasia with anaemia and reticulocytopenia","Infection during pregnancy can cross the placenta and cause foetal anaemia, high-output cardiac failure, and non-immune hydrops fetalis or foetal loss","Diagnosis uses B19 IgM and IgG serology in the immunocompetent, and quantitative B19 DNA PCR in immunocompromised or aplastic patients where antibody responses are unreliable","Persistent infection and pure red cell aplasia respond to intravenous immunoglobulin, which supplies neutralising antibody, while aplastic crisis may need red-cell transfusion"],"pathophysiology":"Parvovirus B19 is a small non-enveloped DNA virus transmitted by respiratory droplets, transfusion, and vertically across the placenta. It binds the P blood-group antigen (globoside) expressed on erythroid precursors and infects and lyses them, transiently halting red-cell production in the marrow. In healthy people the resulting brief drop in reticulocytes is clinically silent, and the rash and arthropathy that follow are immune-complex mediated as antibody develops. Where red-cell survival is already shortened by chronic haemolysis, this pause in erythropoiesis causes an abrupt fall in haemoglobin (aplastic crisis). When neutralising antibody cannot be generated, as in immunosuppression, infection persists and causes chronic pure red cell aplasia. In the foetus the combination of a short red-cell lifespan and rapid erythroid expansion makes severe anaemia, cardiac failure, and hydrops possible.","additionalDifferentials":["Rubella and measles","Other viral exanthems including enteroviral and roseola rashes","Early rheumatoid arthritis or other inflammatory polyarthropathy","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Other causes of aplastic crisis or acquired pure red cell aplasia","Immune (idiopathic) causes of non-immune hydrops fetalis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Parvovirus B19 IgM and IgG serology in immunocompetent patients","Quantitative B19 DNA PCR from blood or marrow in immunocompromised or aplastic patients","Full blood count and reticulocyte count to detect anaemia and erythroid suppression","Bone marrow examination showing giant pronormoblasts and erythroid hypoplasia in persistent infection","Maternal serology and foetal ultrasound with middle cerebral artery Doppler for suspected congenital infection","Direct antiglobulin test and haemolysis screen to characterise underlying haemolytic disorder"],"redFlags":["Abrupt severe anaemia with reticulocytopenia in a patient with chronic haemolysis (aplastic crisis)","Progressive anaemia in an immunocompromised patient suggesting persistent infection","Foetal hydrops, ascites, or abnormal Doppler indicating severe foetal anaemia","Signs of high-output cardiac failure from profound anaemia"],"prognosis":"Most immunocompetent infections are self-limiting with full recovery, arthropathy usually resolving over weeks to months; aplastic crisis recovers as immunity develops, immunoglobulin usually corrects persistent red cell aplasia, and foetal outcome depends on gestation and timely intrauterine transfusion.","pitfalls":["Assuming a patient is no longer infectious once the rash appears, when contagiousness is greatest before the exanthem","Relying on IgM serology in immunocompromised patients who may not mount an antibody response, missing the need for DNA PCR","Overlooking parvovirus as a cause of unexplained anaemia or reticulocytopenia after transplantation","Failing to consider B19 in non-immune hydrops fetalis or new symmetrical polyarthropathy"],"management":["Treat immunocompetent infection supportively, using NSAIDs for the immune-complex arthropathy, as the illness is self-limiting","Support transient aplastic crisis with red-cell transfusion until endogenous erythropoiesis recovers","Treat persistent pure red cell aplasia in immunocompromised hosts with intravenous immunoglobulin and reduce immunosuppression where feasible","In maternal infection, monitor the foetus with serial ultrasound and middle cerebral artery Doppler, arranging intrauterine transfusion for foetal anaemia or hydrops","Apply infection-control precautions to protect pregnant, immunosuppressed, and chronically haemolytic contacts, recognising that patients in aplastic crisis remain infectious","Monitor full blood count and reticulocyte count to track anaemia and marrow recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","UpToDate: Clinical manifestations and diagnosis of human parvovirus B19 infection"],"crossLinks":[]},"parvovirus_infections":{"name":"Parvovirus Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases (Hematologic/Multisystem viral infection)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Parvovirus B19 infection","B19V infection","Erythema infectiosum","Fifth disease","Slapped-cheek disease","Human bocavirus infection"],"chapter":202,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1522","clinicalPearls":["Parvovirus B19 (B19V) has a narrow tropism for erythroid progenitor cells in the bone marrow, which underlies most of its clinical effects on red cell production.","Most B19V infections are asymptomatic or cause only a mild nonspecific illness; the characteristic disease, erythema infectiosum (fifth disease, slapped-cheek disease), occurs mainly in children 3-12 years old.","Transmission is primarily respiratory and occurs before the onset of rash or arthralgia; once the rash appears the child is no longer infectious and can return to school.","In adults the classic slapped-cheek appearance is usually absent; instead they often present with arthralgia, with or without a macular rash.","Arthropathy occurs in roughly 50% of infected adults, is more common in women, tends to be symmetrical and involves small joints of the hands (sometimes ankles, knees, wrists), and can mimic rheumatoid arthritis with detectable rheumatoid factor.","In patients dependent on rapid red cell turnover (hemolytic disorders, hemoglobinopathies, red cell enzymopathies, autoimmune hemolytic anemia), B19V is the predominant cause of transient aplastic crisis (TAC).","Bone marrow in TAC and pure red cell aplasia shows characteristic giant pronormoblasts with absent or scarce erythroid precursors; in a sickle-cell patient, reticulocytopenia with acute worsening anemia is diagnostic without marrow examination.","Chronic B19V infection causing pure red cell aplasia (PRCA)/persistent anemia is a treatable cause of anemia in immunocompromised hosts and is important to recognize because effective immunoglobulin therapy exists.","Individuals genetically lacking the blood group P antigen (globoside) are naturally resistant to B19V infection because that antigen is a required receptor.","Detection of B19V DNA in tissue by PCR alone does not establish causation, since viral DNA can persist in healthy tissues for decades; quantitative PCR and serologic correlation are needed.","Human bocavirus 1 (HBoV1) is a separate human parvovirus causing upper and lower respiratory tract infection in young children (peak ~2 years) and infecting nearly all children before age 5.","An immune-mediated rash can appear in chronically infected immunocompromised patients after immunoglobulin administration, due to antigen-antibody complexes in skin."],"pathophysiology":"B19V is a small nonenveloped single-stranded DNA virus (genus Erythroparvovirus) that replicates in erythroid progenitor cells. Cell entry depends on the unknown primary receptor recognized by VP1u and, at a later step, the blood group P antigen (globoside); people lacking P antigen are naturally resistant. Infection produces high-titer viremia (up to ~10^12-10^14 particles/mL at the peak) and virus-induced cytotoxicity that halts red cell production. In immunocompetent hosts with normal hematopoiesis the arrest of erythropoiesis is transient with only a minimal fall in hemoglobin, resolving as neutralizing antibody is produced. In hosts with increased erythropoiesis (e.g., hemolytic anemia) the same arrest precipitates a transient aplastic crisis; in those who fail to mount neutralizing antibody (immunocompromised hosts or the fetus) lytic infection persists and chronic anemia/PRCA develops. The classic rash and arthropathy of erythema infectiosum are immune-mediated, beginning 2-3 weeks after infection as the IgM response peaks. B19V can also enter nonpermissive cells (B cells, monocytes, endothelial cells) by antibody-dependent enhancement and persist as DNA for life in many tissues (heart, liver, kidney, synovium, brain, bone), generally without disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Other viral exanthems (B19V rash is difficult to distinguish clinically; laboratory confirmation is advised when a definite diagnosis is needed, such as in pregnancy)","Rheumatoid arthritis (B19V polyarthropathy can mimic it, and rheumatoid factor may be present)","Other causes of nonimmune hydrops fetalis","Other causes of transient aplastic crisis or red cell aplasia"],"infectionMimics":["Other respiratory viral infections (for HBoV1, which presents like other viral RTIs with fever, cough, wheezing)","Co-infection with Plasmodium has been suggested to contribute to severe anemia in young children with malaria"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Rheumatoid arthritis (clinical and serologic overlap with B19V polyarthropathy syndrome)","Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (B19V can be associated with thrombocytopenia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["B19V IgM by indirect enzyme immunoassay - detectable at the time of rash in erythema infectiosum and by the third day of TAC; may persist about 3 months or longer","B19V IgG - detectable by the seventh day of illness and persists for life, marking immunity; seroconversion or a fourfold or greater rise on paired sera taken 2 weeks apart is diagnostic of acute infection","IgG avidity testing and epitope-type-specific (ETS) EIA to distinguish acute from past infection","B19V DNA by PCR in serum (or amniotic fluid), with quantitative PCR preferred because low-level DNA can persist for months to years even in healthy people","Bone marrow examination showing giant pronormoblasts and absent/scant erythroid precursors in TAC or PRCA","In suspected fetal infection: maternal serology in exposed seronegative mothers, with regular monitoring if positive, and PCR of amniotic fluid or tissue"],"redFlags":["Severe to life-threatening anemia with a low reticulocyte count in a patient with a known hemolytic disorder (transient aplastic crisis)","Persistent anemia with reticulocytopenia and extremely high serum B19V DNA titers in an immunocompromised patient (pure red cell aplasia)","Maternal B19V infection before gestational week 20, given the risk of fetal loss and hydrops fetalis","Nonimmune hydrops fetalis with gross edema and severe fetal anemia (B19V causes an estimated 10-20% of such cases)","Hemophagocytic syndrome occasionally precipitated by B19V"],"prognosis":"Most B19V infections are self-limited. Erythema infectiosum and B19V-associated arthropathy generally resolve spontaneously, though joint symptoms may recur for weeks to months. Transient aplastic crisis is, by definition, transient: anemia resolves once cytopathic infection of erythroid progenitors ceases, and lifelong immunity follows. Chronic anemia/PRCA in immunocompromised hosts is treatable and can be cured or ameliorated with immunoglobulin. Risk of fetal loss is highest (excess risk roughly 9%) when maternal infection occurs before gestational week 20 and is very low thereafter; most fetal infections resolve spontaneously, B19V does not appear to be teratogenic, and rare reports of eye damage, CNS abnormalities, and congenital anemia exist.","pitfalls":["Interpreting tissue B19V DNA detected by PCR as proof of active disease - viral DNA persists in healthy tissues for decades, so PCR alone in tissue should not establish a B19V etiology.","Presence of B19V DNA in synovium does not prove a causal role in arthritis, since healthy individuals also harbor viral DNA in synovia.","In TAC and chronic infection, classic immune-mediated symptoms (rash, arthralgia) are often absent, making the diagnosis easy to miss in immunocompromised or hemolytic patients.","Relying on qualitative PCR for HBoV1 in respiratory secretions can misattribute etiology, since HBoV1 DNA may persist for weeks to months after acute infection.","Serology may be negative or only weakly positive in immunodeficient patients with PRCA, so quantitative serum DNA is needed."],"management":["Manage the typical self-limited illness of erythema infectiosum and arthropathy with supportive care and NSAIDs for joint symptoms","Provide red-cell transfusion support during transient aplastic crisis in patients with haemolytic disorders until erythropoiesis resumes","Give intravenous immunoglobulin for chronic pure red cell aplasia in immunocompromised patients, alongside reduction of immunosuppression where possible","Monitor and transfuse the affected foetus in congenital infection, using ultrasound and middle cerebral artery Doppler to guide intrauterine transfusion for hydrops","Institute droplet precautions to protect vulnerable contacts, noting that patients with aplastic crisis or chronic infection remain infectious whereas those with erythema infectiosum are not once the rash appears","Follow full blood count and reticulocytes to confirm haematological recovery"],"severityClassification":"Clinical spectrum of B19V infection (per Table 202-1): fifth disease in healthy children (IgM and IgG positive, viral load >10^4 IU/mL); polyarthropathy syndrome in healthy adults; transient aplastic crisis in patients with increased erythropoiesis (often >10^12 IU/mL, rapidly decreasing); persistent anemia/pure red cell aplasia in immunodeficient or immunosuppressed patients (often >10^12 IU/mL, typically >10^6 untreated); and hydrops fetalis/congenital anemia in fetuses of mothers infected before 20 weeks.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1522","Harrison 22e p.1523","Harrison 22e p.1524","Harrison 22e p.1525"],"crossLinks":[]},"pasteurellosis":{"name":"Pasteurellosis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pasteurella multocida infection","animal-bite cellulitis","Pasteurella soft-tissue infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by Pasteurella multocida, a Gram-negative coccobacillus that is a common commensal in the mouths of cats and dogs, and usually acquired through animal bites or scratches, particularly from cats","Soft-tissue infection develops strikingly rapidly, often within hours of a cat bite, with intense pain, erythema, swelling and a serosanguinous or purulent discharge","The speed of onset helps distinguish it from staphylococcal and streptococcal wound infections, which typically evolve more slowly","Deep hand bites are prone to spread to tendon sheaths, joints and bone, causing tenosynovitis, septic arthritis and osteomyelitis, so hand wounds warrant careful assessment","Non-bite disease, including respiratory infection and, in vulnerable hosts, bacteraemia and meningitis, can follow close animal contact without an obvious wound","Co-amoxiclav is a standard choice because it covers Pasteurella together with the other mouth flora and skin organisms in bite wounds; notably many oral agents such as flucloxacillin and first-generation cephalosporins have poor activity against Pasteurella","Wound management, thorough irrigation, consideration of leaving bite wounds open, and assessment of tetanus and rabies risk are integral to care"],"pathophysiology":"Pasteurella multocida is inoculated into tissue when an animal bites or scratches, or when saliva contaminates an existing wound. The organism produces virulence factors, including a polysaccharide capsule and endotoxin, that provoke a brisk acute inflammatory response, accounting for the characteristically rapid onset of pain, erythema and swelling. Because bites deposit organisms deep in tissue, infection can track along tendon sheaths and reach joints and bone, and in hosts with impaired defences, chronic liver disease or immunosuppression the organism may invade the bloodstream and seed distant sites such as the meninges, respiratory tract and prosthetic material.","additionalDifferentials":["Staphylococcal or streptococcal wound cellulitis","Capnocytophaga canimorsus infection after a dog bite","Bartonella henselae cat-scratch disease","Anaerobic or polymicrobial bite-wound infection","Necrotising soft-tissue infection","Erysipeloid in occupationally exposed patients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Culture of wound exudate or aspirated pus, informing the laboratory of the animal-bite context","Blood cultures where systemic infection is suspected","Gram stain of exudate showing Gram-negative coccobacilli","Plain radiography of the affected part to detect osteomyelitis or a retained foreign body such as a tooth fragment","Inflammatory markers to gauge severity","Cross-sectional imaging or surgical exploration for suspected deep-space hand infection"],"redFlags":["Deep hand or joint involvement with signs of tenosynovitis or septic arthritis","Rapidly spreading infection or features suggesting a necrotising process","Systemic sepsis or suspected bacteraemia","Meningitis or respiratory infection in a vulnerable host","Underlying immunosuppression, asplenia or chronic liver disease predisposing to invasive disease"],"prognosis":"Most localised bite-associated Pasteurella infections resolve well with appropriate antibiotics and wound care, but deep hand infections can cause lasting functional impairment, and invasive disease such as bacteraemia or meningitis carries significant mortality, particularly in comorbid patients.","pitfalls":["Choosing an antibiotic such as flucloxacillin or a first-generation cephalosporin with poor Pasteurella activity for a bite wound","Underestimating a small cat-bite puncture that has inoculated organisms deep into tissue","Missing early tenosynovitis or joint involvement in hand bites","Neglecting tetanus and rabies risk assessment","Assuming systemic disease cannot occur without an obvious bite wound"],"management":["Start empirical co-amoxiclav, which covers Pasteurella multocida together with other mouth and skin flora in bite wounds, avoiding flucloxacillin and first-generation cephalosporins that lack reliable Pasteurella activity","Use doxycycline, a fluoroquinolone, or a macrolide as alternatives in penicillin-allergic patients","Provide thorough wound irrigation and debridement, leaving bite wounds open rather than tightly closed","Undertake surgical drainage or washout for deep-space hand infection, tenosynovitis, or septic arthritis","Treat invasive disease such as bacteraemia or meningitis with an intravenous beta-lactam such as benzylpenicillin or ceftriaxone","Give tetanus prophylaxis and assess rabies risk according to the animal and exposure","Arrange radiography for suspected osteomyelitis, refer deep hand infections to a hand surgeon, and account for host vulnerability such as immunosuppression or chronic liver disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"patau_syndrome":{"name":"Patau syndrome","system":"Genetic / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["trisomy 13","trisomy D","T13"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard medical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Severe multisystem malformation syndrome caused by an extra copy of chromosome 13, with high perinatal and infant mortality","Midline defects are characteristic, including holoprosencephaly, cleft lip and palate, and midface anomalies","Postaxial polydactyly, clenched hands, and rocker-bottom feet are typical limb findings","Ocular anomalies such as microphthalmia or anophthalmia and scalp defects (cutis aplasia) are helpful clues","Major congenital heart defects are present in most affected infants","Caused most often by full trisomy 13 from meiotic nondisjunction, with risk rising with maternal age","A minority of cases result from a Robertsonian translocation, which carries recurrence implications for the family","Mosaic forms can produce milder, more variable phenotypes"],"pathophysiology":"Patau syndrome arises from an extra copy of chromosome 13, most commonly a full trisomy caused by maternal meiotic nondisjunction, with incidence increasing with maternal age. Around a fifth of cases result from an unbalanced Robertsonian translocation involving chromosome 13, and a smaller proportion from mosaicism. The surplus chromosome 13 material disrupts embryonic development, particularly midline structures of the brain and face, producing holoprosencephaly and associated craniofacial defects, along with cardiac, renal, and limb malformations. The severity of central nervous system and cardiac anomalies underlies the very high mortality.","additionalDifferentials":["Edwards syndrome (trisomy 18)","Holoprosencephaly from other causes","Meckel-Gruber syndrome","Pseudotrisomy 13 syndrome","Other multiple malformation syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype or chromosomal microarray to confirm trisomy 13 and detect translocation or mosaicism","Prenatal screening and diagnostic testing including cell-free DNA and invasive confirmation when indicated","Cranial imaging to assess for holoprosencephaly and other brain anomalies","Echocardiography to define cardiac defects","Parental karyotyping when a translocation is identified"],"redFlags":["Severe cardiac and central nervous system malformations causing early life-threatening instability","Recurrent apnoea, seizures, and feeding failure requiring goals-of-care discussion","Higher recurrence risk in translocation cases, making parental testing important","Need for early, compassionate counselling given the poor prognosis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is very poor, with most infants dying in the first days to weeks of life and few surviving beyond the first year, typically with profound disability; mosaic forms may have a better outlook.","pitfalls":["Not distinguishing full trisomy from mosaic or translocation forms, which affects counselling","Failing to arrange parental karyotyping when a translocation underlies the case","Overlooking comfort-focused care needs while pursuing investigations","Missing associated anomalies such as holoprosencephaly on imaging"],"management":["Establish goals of care with the family early, given the very poor prognosis, balancing comfort-focused palliative care against active intervention through shared decision-making","Provide supportive care for specific anomalies, including feeding support by nasogastric tube or gastrostomy, anticonvulsants for seizures, and management of apnoea","Consider selective cardiac surgery and other interventions only in the context of overall prognosis and family wishes","Involve a multidisciplinary team with paediatric palliative care","Offer genetic counselling and parental karyotyping to define recurrence risk, particularly when an unbalanced Robertsonian translocation is identified","Provide antenatal counselling and delivery planning where the diagnosis is made prenatally"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"patellar_dislocation":{"name":"Patellar dislocation","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lateral patellar dislocation","patellar instability","dislocated kneecap"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Review of Orthopaedics / Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The patella almost always dislocates laterally, often reducing spontaneously on knee extension","The medial patellofemoral ligament is the key soft-tissue restraint and is torn in most first-time dislocations","Predisposing factors include trochlear dysplasia, patella alta, an increased tibial-tubercle-to-trochlear-groove distance and generalised laxity","A haemarthrosis and medial-sided tenderness are typical; a positive apprehension test indicates instability","Osteochondral fragments from the patella or lateral femoral condyle should be actively sought and may need fixation","First-time dislocations without a loose body are usually managed non-operatively with rehabilitation","Recurrent instability may warrant MPFL reconstruction and correction of bony risk factors"],"pathophysiology":"Lateral displacement of the patella from the trochlear groove occurs when the lateral vector on the extensor mechanism overcomes the medial restraints, tearing the medial patellofemoral ligament. Underlying malalignment and dysplasia (shallow trochlea, patella alta, lateralised tubercle) lower the threshold for dislocation and for recurrence, while impaction of the patella against the lateral femoral condyle can shear off osteochondral fragments.","additionalDifferentials":["ACL rupture (both cause acute haemarthrosis)","Osteochondral fracture without persisting dislocation","Patellar or quadriceps tendon rupture","Patellofemoral pain syndrome","Meniscal tear"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Radiographs including a skyline/axial patellar view","MRI to assess the MPFL, osteochondral injury and trochlear morphology","CT or MRI measurement of tibial-tubercle-to-trochlear-groove distance","Assessment for generalised ligamentous laxity and limb alignment"],"redFlags":["A displaced osteochondral loose body causing mechanical locking","Irreducible dislocation","Extensor mechanism disruption preventing straight-leg raise"],"prognosis":"Most first-time dislocations recover with rehabilitation, but recurrence is common in young patients with dysplasia; surgical stabilisation reduces recurrence when anatomical risk factors are addressed.","pitfalls":["Missing an osteochondral fragment that requires surgical fixation","Attributing the haemarthrosis to an ACL tear and overlooking a reduced patellar dislocation","Ignoring bony risk factors so that soft-tissue repair alone fails","Failing to check the extensor mechanism for tendon rupture"],"management":["Reduce the acute dislocation by gentle knee extension and then protect the joint in a brace with early controlled mobilisation","Manage first-time dislocations without a loose body non-operatively, with physiotherapy targeting quadriceps and vastus medialis obliquus and hip strengthening plus patellar taping or bracing","Operate to fix or remove osteochondral fragments identified after dislocation","Offer medial patellofemoral ligament reconstruction for recurrent instability, combined with correction of bony risk factors such as trochleoplasty or tibial-tubercle transfer where indicated","Investigate anatomical predisposition such as trochlear dysplasia, patella alta, and increased tibial-tubercle-to-trochlear-groove distance to guide surgical planning","Refer to orthopaedics and physiotherapy for rehabilitation and follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Review of Orthopaedics","Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"patellar_instability":{"name":"patellar instability","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["patellofemoral instability","recurrent patellar dislocation","patellar subluxation"],"chapter":null,"source":"DeLee, Drez & Miller's Orthopaedic Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lateral patellar dislocation is by far the usual direction, typically caused by a twisting valgus force on a flexed knee or a direct medial blow.","A first-time dislocation often reduces spontaneously as the knee extends, and a tense haemarthrosis is common.","The medial patellofemoral ligament is the primary passive restraint to lateral translation and is almost invariably torn in first dislocation.","Key anatomical risk factors are trochlear dysplasia, patella alta, an increased tibial tubercle to trochlear groove distance, and rotational malalignment or ligamentous laxity.","A positive apprehension test and a J-sign on active extension support the diagnosis of maltracking and instability.","Always exclude an osteochondral fracture of the medial patellar facet or lateral femoral condyle after dislocation.","First-time dislocation without a loose body is usually treated conservatively with bracing and physiotherapy targeting the vastus medialis obliquus and hip.","Recurrent instability may require medial patellofemoral ligament reconstruction, with bony procedures such as tibial tubercle osteotomy or trochleoplasty for underlying dysplasia."],"pathophysiology":"Patellar stability depends on the interplay of static restraints (the medial patellofemoral ligament, trochlear geometry and patellar shape), dynamic restraints (the quadriceps, especially the vastus medialis obliquus) and overall limb alignment. Lateral dislocation occurs when a valgus and external rotation force acts on a flexed, weight-bearing knee, overcoming the medial soft tissues and tearing the medial patellofemoral ligament from its femoral or patellar attachment. Predisposing morphology - trochlear dysplasia with a shallow or convex trochlea, patella alta with late trochlear engagement, a laterally positioned tibial tubercle, rotational malalignment and generalised laxity - lowers the force threshold for displacement and drives recurrence. Each event risks osteochondral shear injury and progressive patellofemoral chondral wear.","additionalDifferentials":["Anterior cruciate ligament rupture, which also causes acute haemarthrosis after a twisting injury","Meniscal tear","Patellar or quadriceps tendon rupture","Isolated osteochondral fracture without persistent instability","Patellofemoral pain syndrome or chondromalacia patellae","Medial synovial plica syndrome","Referred hip pathology such as a slipped capital femoral epiphysis in adolescents"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Radiographs including skyline/Merchant views for patellar tilt, subluxation and osteochondral fragments","MRI to assess the site of medial patellofemoral ligament tear, chondral injury and the characteristic bone bruise pattern","CT or MRI measurement of the tibial tubercle to trochlear groove distance and grading of trochlear dysplasia","Assessment of patella alta using the Insall-Salvati or Caton-Deschamps index","Rotational profile assessment of femoral anteversion and tibial torsion","Beighton score to assess generalised ligamentous laxity"],"redFlags":["A locked knee or mechanical block suggesting a displaced osteochondral fragment or loose body","An irreducible dislocation or gross deformity","Neurovascular compromise following a high-energy injury","A tense haemarthrosis with fat globules indicating an osteochondral fracture","Medial or multidirectional dislocation suggesting an iatrogenic cause or connective tissue disorder"],"prognosis":"Many first-time dislocators do well with conservative treatment, but recurrence is common in young patients with dysplastic anatomy. Surgical stabilisation, particularly ligament reconstruction combined with correction of bony risk factors, has a high success rate in reducing redislocation.","pitfalls":["Missing an osteochondral fracture on initial radiographs after the patella has relocated.","Attributing the haemarthrosis solely to an anterior cruciate ligament injury and overlooking patellar dislocation.","Reconstructing the medial patellofemoral ligament without addressing underlying bony malalignment, leading to failure.","Overtightening a ligament graft, causing medial overload and stiffness.","Neglecting hip and core weakness during rehabilitation."],"management":["Reduce an acute lateral dislocation by knee extension and then protect the knee in a brace with progressive mobilisation","Prioritise rehabilitation for first episodes, strengthening the quadriceps and vastus medialis obliquus, hip abductors, and external rotators, with patellar bracing or taping to improve tracking","Fix or excise osteochondral fractures of the medial patellar facet or lateral femoral condyle after dislocation","Offer medial patellofemoral ligament reconstruction for recurrent instability, addressing bony abnormalities with trochleoplasty or tibial-tubercle transfer when they drive redislocation","Define anatomical risk factors such as trochlear dysplasia, patella alta, tibial-tubercle-to-trochlear-groove distance, rotational malalignment, and laxity to plan intervention","Manage jointly with orthopaedics and physiotherapy, tailoring surgery to the individual's anatomy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["DeLee, Drez & Miller's Orthopaedic Sports Medicine","Apley's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","Brukner & Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"patellar_tendinopathy":{"name":"Patellar tendinopathy (jumper knee)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["jumper's knee","patellar tendinosis","patellar tendon overuse injury","infrapatellar tendinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Anterior knee pain localised to the inferior pole of the patella, typical of jumping and change-of-direction sports","Load-related pain that characteristically warms up with activity and worsens afterwards","A degenerative tendinopathy from repetitive high-tensile, energy-storage loading, not a primary inflammation","The single-leg decline squat reproduces and helps monitor tendon pain","Tenderness is maximal at the proximal patellar tendon at the inferior patellar pole","Ultrasound and MRI can confirm tendinopathy, but changes may be present in asymptomatic tendons, so correlate with the clinical picture","Management is progressive loading (isometric then heavy slow or eccentric resistance) guided by physiotherapy with load management, avoiding complete rest","Corticosteroid injection into the tendon is generally avoided because of rupture risk; shockwave and other adjuncts may help resistant cases"],"pathophysiology":"Patellar tendinopathy results from repetitive energy-storage loading of the extensor mechanism, as in jumping and deceleration, most often affecting the deep posterior fibres at the inferior pole of the patella. Cumulative overload exceeds the tendon's adaptive repair capacity, producing degenerative change with disorganised collagen, increased ground substance, neovascularisation and neural ingrowth, and little classic inflammation, hence tendinosis. A continuum model describes progression from reactive tendinopathy through disrepair to degenerative tendinopathy depending on the balance of load and recovery.","additionalDifferentials":["Patellofemoral pain syndrome","Osgood-Schlatter or Sinding-Larsen-Johansson disease in adolescents","Fat pad (Hoffa) impingement","Quadriceps tendinopathy","Partial patellar tendon tear","Chondral or meniscal pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Single-leg decline squat and other load tests to reproduce and grade pain","Ultrasound to assess tendon structure and neovascularity","MRI to characterise tendinopathy and exclude a partial tear or other pathology","Comparison with the asymptomatic side, recognising that imaging change can be incidental","Biomechanical and kinetic-chain assessment of the ankle, hip and landing mechanics"],"redFlags":["Sudden pain with loss of active knee extension suggesting tendon rupture","Night pain, systemic upset or a mass suggesting other pathology","Locking or giving way suggesting intra-articular derangement","Failure to progress despite appropriate loading"],"prognosis":"Often responsive to a structured loading programme, but rehabilitation may take many months and symptoms can be persistent or recurrent, particularly if training load is not managed.","pitfalls":["Prescribing complete rest instead of progressive tendon loading","Intratendinous corticosteroid injection with its attendant rupture risk","Over-interpreting imaging change that can occur in pain-free tendons","Ignoring the wider kinetic chain and training-load errors"],"management":["Base treatment on progressive tendon loading, beginning with isometric quadriceps holds for pain relief and advancing to heavy slow-resistance or eccentric decline-squat exercise","Modify training load with relative rest from jumping and deceleration rather than complete inactivity","Correct contributing kinetic-chain factors, including ankle and hip strength and landing mechanics","Consider extracorporeal shockwave therapy for recalcitrant cases and avoid intratendinous corticosteroid injection, which risks tendon rupture","Set realistic expectations of prolonged rehabilitation and monitor pain with tools such as the single-leg decline squat","Reserve surgery for symptoms refractory to a sustained structured loading programme"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","British Journal of Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"patellofemoral_pain":{"name":"Patellofemoral pain syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PFPS","anterior knee pain","runner's knee","patellofemoral pain"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon / standard sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with diffuse or peri-patellar anterior knee pain aggravated by loaded knee flexion such as squatting, stairs, kneeling, and prolonged sitting (the theatre sign)","It is a diagnosis based on the pattern of anterior knee pain after excluding other structural causes, and imaging is usually normal","It is most common in adolescents and young adults, particularly active females","Contributing factors include quadriceps and gluteal weakness, dynamic knee valgus, hip control deficits, and training overload rather than a single structural lesion","The old label chondromalacia patellae describes cartilage softening but correlates poorly with symptoms and is not synonymous with the syndrome","Examination may reveal pain on patellar compression, poor lower-limb alignment on a single-leg squat, and quadriceps or hip abductor weakness","First-line management is exercise therapy targeting the quadriceps and hip musculature, combined with load management and activity modification","Adjuncts include patellar taping, appropriate footwear or orthoses, and attention to biomechanics and training errors","Distinguish it from patellar instability, patellar tendinopathy, and referred pain, which need different treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Patellofemoral pain arises from excessive or maldistributed stress across the patellofemoral joint. Imbalance in the forces controlling patellar tracking, weakness of the quadriceps and hip abductors, dynamic valgus, and altered lower-limb alignment concentrate load on the retropatellar and peripatellar tissues. Repetitive overload of richly innervated subchondral bone and soft tissue, rather than cartilage damage alone, generates pain.","additionalDifferentials":["Patellar tendinopathy (jumper's knee)","Patellar subluxation or instability","Chondral or osteochondral lesions and early osteoarthritis","Osgood-Schlatter or Sinding-Larsen-Johansson disease in adolescents","Plica syndrome","Referred pain from the hip (including slipped capital femoral epiphysis in adolescents) or lumbar spine","Prepatellar or pes anserine bursitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Primarily a clinical diagnosis based on history and examination","Plain radiographs including skyline/axial views if instability, arthritis, or bony pathology is suspected","MRI reserved for atypical, refractory, or locking symptoms to assess cartilage and other structures","Assessment of hip and lower-limb biomechanics and muscle strength","Consider hip imaging in adolescents to exclude referred hip pathology"],"redFlags":["True locking, giving way, or significant effusion suggesting internal derangement","Fever, systemic symptoms, or a hot swollen joint suggesting infection or inflammatory arthritis","Night pain, rest pain, or a mass raising concern for tumour","Adolescent knee pain that may be referred from serious hip pathology"],"prognosis":"Many improve with structured rehabilitation, but symptoms can be persistent or recurrent in a substantial minority, particularly if strength and biomechanical deficits are not corrected; long-term association with patellofemoral osteoarthritis is debated.","pitfalls":["Labelling all anterior knee pain as chondromalacia and relying on rest alone","Neglecting hip and gluteal strengthening and focusing only on the knee","Missing patellar instability, referred hip pathology, or tumour","Rushing return to sport without addressing training load and biomechanics"],"management":["Make structured physiotherapy the mainstay, combining quadriceps strengthening with hip abductor and external-rotator strengthening and movement retraining","Modify aggravating loads such as stairs, squatting, and prolonged sitting with graded activity and relative rest","Use patellar taping or bracing and, in those with excessive foot pronation, foot orthoses as adjuncts to reduce pain","Offer only short-term simple analgesia, recognising its limited role","Address footwear, training errors, and lower-limb biomechanics identified on single-leg squat testing","Educate patients on self-management and reassure them, reserving specialist referral for refractory or atypical cases as surgery is rarely indicated"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"patellofemoral_pain_syndrome":{"name":"Patellofemoral pain syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anterior knee pain","runner's knee"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Diffuse peripatellar or retropatellar pain aggravated by activities that load the flexed knee, such as stairs, squatting and running","Pain after prolonged sitting with the knee bent (the theatre sign) is characteristic","It is one of the commonest causes of knee pain in active adolescents and young adults, especially women","Contributing factors include abnormal patellar tracking, muscle imbalance, weakness of the hip and quadriceps, and training overload","It is a clinical diagnosis, and imaging is used mainly to exclude other pathology","Structured physiotherapy focusing on hip and quadriceps strength and load management is the mainstay of treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Patellofemoral pain syndrome is a multifactorial overuse condition in which altered load distribution across the patellofemoral joint irritates pain-sensitive structures. Malalignment or maltracking of the patella, weakness or imbalance of the quadriceps and hip abductors and external rotators, and excessive or rapidly increased loading all contribute to abnormal patellofemoral stress. The pain arises from the richly innervated subchondral bone, synovium and peripatellar soft tissues rather than from cartilage, which is aneural.","additionalDifferentials":["Patellar tendinopathy","Chondromalacia patellae or early osteoarthritis","Patellar instability or subluxation","Plica syndrome","Referred pain from the hip","Osgood-Schlatter or Sinding-Larsen-Johansson disease in adolescents"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of pain reproduction and patellar tracking","Evaluation of hip and quadriceps strength and lower-limb biomechanics","Radiographs if structural abnormality, instability or arthritis is suspected","MRI reserved for diagnostic uncertainty or suspected internal derangement","Assessment of training load and footwear"],"redFlags":["True locking or giving way suggesting internal derangement or instability","Significant effusion","Night pain or systemic features suggesting other pathology","Focal tendon or bony tenderness pointing to an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Most patients improve with a sustained rehabilitation programme, though symptoms can be persistent or recurrent, particularly if underlying strength and biomechanical factors are not addressed.","pitfalls":["Over-investigating with imaging for a clinical diagnosis","Neglecting hip and proximal muscle strengthening","Missing patellar instability or internal derangement","Advising rest alone rather than active rehabilitation"],"management":["Deliver a sustained rehabilitation programme centred on hip abductor and external-rotator and quadriceps strengthening as the primary treatment","Apply load management, reducing activities that stress the flexed knee while maintaining conditioning","Add patellar taping or bracing and foot orthoses as short-term adjuncts to support pain relief and exercise","Retrain running and landing mechanics and correct training-load errors and footwear","Use imaging and specialist referral selectively, mainly to exclude other pathology in refractory or atypical presentations","Provide education and reassurance to encourage adherence, as symptoms often persist without correction of strength and biomechanical deficits"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sports medicine references (standard reference)","Orthopaedic reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"patent_ductus_arteriosus":{"name":"Patent Ductus Arteriosus","system":"Paediatric cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PDA","persistent ductus arteriosus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Persistence of the fetal ductus arteriosus beyond the normal postnatal closure period allows shunting between the aorta and pulmonary artery; it is especially common in preterm infants.","Because aortic pressure exceeds pulmonary pressure once pulmonary resistance falls, the shunt is left-to-right, increasing pulmonary blood flow and left heart volume load.","The classic sign is a continuous machinery murmur beneath the left clavicle, though in neonates only a systolic component may be audible.","A large shunt produces bounding peripheral pulses and a wide pulse pressure from diastolic runoff into the pulmonary circulation.","In preterm infants a significant ductus can worsen respiratory distress, contribute to heart failure, and is associated with complications such as necrotising enterocolitis and intraventricular haemorrhage.","In some duct-dependent congenital heart lesions the ductus is essential for survival, and a prostaglandin infusion is used to keep it open until definitive intervention.","Closure of an isolated symptomatic ductus may use pharmacological prostaglandin inhibitors in preterm infants or transcatheter/surgical closure in others.","Echocardiography confirms the ductus, estimates shunt size, and assesses left heart dilation and pulmonary pressures.","A large, longstanding, unrepaired ductus risks pulmonary vascular disease and eventual shunt reversal."],"pathophysiology":"The ductus arteriosus normally constricts and closes after birth in response to rising oxygen tension and falling circulating prostaglandins. When it remains patent, the postnatal fall in pulmonary vascular resistance establishes a left-to-right shunt from the aorta to the pulmonary artery throughout the cardiac cycle. This increases pulmonary blood flow and volume-loads the left atrium and left ventricle, while diastolic runoff from the aorta lowers diastolic pressure and can compromise systemic and coronary perfusion. A large chronic shunt raises pulmonary pressures and can lead to pulmonary vascular remodelling and shunt reversal over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Ventricular septal defect","Aortopulmonary window","Venous hum (an innocent continuous murmur)","Coronary or systemic arteriovenous fistula","Truncus arteriosus","Peripheral pulmonary stenosis murmur"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to confirm the ductus and assess shunt magnitude and left heart size","Chest radiograph for cardiomegaly and increased pulmonary vascular markings in large shunts","Electrocardiogram for left ventricular volume overload","Assessment of pulse character and pulse pressure","Monitoring of respiratory status and feeding in preterm infants"],"redFlags":["Worsening heart failure or respiratory deterioration in a preterm infant with a significant ductus","Abrupt cardiovascular collapse in a neonate with a duct-dependent lesion as the ductus closes","Signs of poor systemic perfusion from diastolic runoff (weak distal perfusion, oliguria, feeding intolerance)","Development of pulmonary hypertension or shunt reversal in a large longstanding ductus","Necrotising enterocolitis or intraventricular haemorrhage associated with a large shunt"],"prognosis":"An isolated ductus closed in a timely fashion has an excellent outlook. In preterm infants outcome depends heavily on the associated prematurity complications. A large duct left untreated risks irreversible pulmonary vascular disease and a poorer prognosis.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise that some critical congenital heart lesions depend on ductal patency, and inadvertently allowing the ductus to close","Misinterpreting a benign venous hum as a pathological continuous murmur","Underestimating the contribution of a significant ductus to a preterm infant's respiratory difficulty","Overlooking the wide pulse pressure and bounding pulses that signal a large shunt","Delaying closure of a haemodynamically significant duct until pulmonary vascular disease develops"],"management":["In preterm infants with a haemodynamically significant duct, promote ductal closure with a cyclo-oxygenase inhibitor such as ibuprofen or indometacin, or with paracetamol where these are contraindicated","Where the ductus is essential in a duct-dependent congenital lesion, maintain patency with a prostaglandin E infusion until definitive surgical or interventional repair","Offer definitive closure of a persistent haemodynamically significant duct by transcatheter device occlusion, reserving surgical ligation for infants too small or unsuitable for the catheter approach","Support the volume-loaded circulation with judicious fluid restriction, diuretics and respiratory support for pulmonary overcirculation and cardiac failure","Monitor shunt magnitude and left heart dilatation with serial echocardiography and watch for evolving pulmonary hypertension","Remain alert for the complications of a significant duct in preterm infants, particularly necrotising enterocolitis and intraventricular haemorrhage","Refer to paediatric cardiology and escalate to closure when the shunt causes failure to thrive, heart failure or rising pulmonary pressures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"patent_foramen_ovale":{"name":"Patent foramen ovale","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PFO","persistent foramen ovale","interatrial communication (foramen ovale)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A residual flap-like channel between the atrial septum primum and secundum that fails to seal after birth, present in roughly a quarter of adults","Usually an incidental finding of no consequence, but under conditions that transiently raise right atrial pressure it can permit right-to-left shunting","Chief clinical importance is paradoxical embolism, in which venous thrombus crosses to the systemic circulation and causes cryptogenic stroke, particularly in younger patients without other vascular risk factors","Frequently accompanied by an atrial septal aneurysm, and the combination increases the likelihood that the PFO is the culprit for stroke","Linked with migraine, especially migraine with aura, and with decompression illness in divers and platypnoea-orthodeoxia in selected patients","Detected by transthoracic or transoesophageal echocardiography with an agitated-saline bubble study, ideally combined with a Valsalva manoeuvre to provoke shunting","Percutaneous device closure reduces recurrent stroke in carefully selected younger patients with an otherwise unexplained (cryptogenic) stroke and a high-risk PFO"],"pathophysiology":"The foramen ovale is a physiological right-to-left channel in fetal life; after birth the rise in left atrial pressure normally holds the flap of septum primum against septum secundum, and the two fuse. When fusion is incomplete a potential channel persists. Because left atrial pressure usually exceeds right, the flap stays shut, but a transient reversal of the interatrial pressure gradient, for example during straining, coughing, or the release phase of a Valsalva manoeuvre, can open the channel and allow right-to-left flow. This permits venous thrombus or other material to bypass the pulmonary filter and enter the systemic circulation, the mechanism of paradoxical embolism; an associated atrial septal aneurysm increases septal mobility and the propensity to shunt.","additionalDifferentials":["Secundum atrial septal defect","Sinus venosus atrial septal defect","Pulmonary arteriovenous malformation causing right-to-left shunt","Other cardioembolic sources such as atrial fibrillation or left atrial thrombus","Aortic arch or carotid atherosclerotic embolism","Hypercoagulable state causing arterial thrombosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography with agitated-saline contrast and Valsalva to demonstrate right-to-left shunting","Transoesophageal echocardiography to confirm the PFO, size the channel, and identify an atrial septal aneurysm","Transcranial Doppler with bubble contrast to detect and grade right-to-left shunt","Cardiac and neurological workup for other embolic sources, including prolonged rhythm monitoring for atrial fibrillation","Venous imaging and thrombophilia assessment when paradoxical embolism is suspected"],"redFlags":["Cryptogenic ischaemic stroke or systemic embolism in a younger patient","Recurrent embolic events despite antithrombotic therapy","Platypnoea-orthodeoxia with hypoxaemia that worsens on sitting or standing","A large shunt with an associated atrial septal aneurysm in the context of unexplained stroke"],"prognosis":"Most patent foramina are benign and never cause problems, but in those with a cryptogenic stroke and a high-risk PFO, appropriate device closure or antithrombotic therapy substantially reduces the risk of recurrent stroke.","pitfalls":["Attributing a stroke to a PFO without excluding atrial fibrillation and other embolic sources first","Performing a bubble study without an adequate Valsalva, so a shunt is missed","Offering closure to older patients whose stroke is more likely due to conventional vascular disease","Confusing a PFO with a true secundum atrial septal defect, which has different management"],"management":["After a cryptogenic stroke with a high-risk foramen, offer percutaneous transcatheter device closure in appropriately selected younger patients following joint cardiology and neurology assessment","Provide secondary-prevention antithrombotic therapy, with antiplatelet agents or anticoagulation guided by the presence of venous thromboembolism or another embolic mechanism","Exclude competing embolic sources before attributing stroke to the foramen, including prolonged rhythm monitoring for atrial fibrillation and thrombophilia and venous imaging where paradoxical embolism is suspected","Modify conventional vascular risk factors to reduce overall stroke recurrence","Leave an incidental foramen without a clinical event untreated, reserving intervention for demonstrated paradoxical embolism","Consider closure in selected patients with refractory platypnoea-orthodeoxia or recurrent decompression illness in divers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC Guidelines for the management of adult congenital heart disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"pathobiology_of_neurologic_diseases":{"name":"Pathobiology of Neurologic Diseases","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Neuroimmunology and neuroinflammation","Mechanisms of neurodegeneration","Prion pathobiology","Neural stem cell biology"],"chapter":435,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3394","clinicalPearls":["Microglia, the brain's resident innate immune and antigen-presenting cells, arise from primitive yolk-sac macrophages that seed the CNS before the blood-brain barrier forms and are sustained largely by self-renewal rather than by replenishment from the circulation; under some conditions peripherally derived macrophages can become microglia-like and replace damaged cells.","Microglia sculpt and prune synapses via classical complement proteins (C1q, C3) and complement receptors; in Alzheimer's disease, excess beta-amyloid drives C1q/C3 tagging of synapses that are then eliminated by CR3-expressing microglia, and knockout of C1q or C3 can rescue some neurodegenerative pathology in models.","Genetic data link innate immunity to neurodegeneration: more than half of genes implicated in Alzheimer's GWAS involve innate immune/microglial processes, and loss-of-function mutations in the microglial receptor TREM2 increase Alzheimer's risk up to roughly fourfold.","Around 10% of frontotemporal degeneration cases are due to heterozygous granulin (GRN) mutations causing progranulin haploinsufficiency; progranulin is a neurotrophic factor essential for lysosomal function and microglial homeostasis.","Activated microglia (via IL-1alpha, TNF, and C1q) can convert astrocytes to a reactive, neurotoxic (A1) phenotype that loses phagocytic capacity and damages neurons and oligodendrocytes through complement, nitric oxide, and free radicals; astrocytes are non-cell-autonomous contributors across MS, AD, PD, and ALS.","The glymphatic system uses periarterial CSF inflow through aquaporin-4-rich astrocyte foot processes to clear interstitial solutes and debris; clearance accelerates during sleep, and in models sleep deprivation increased beta-amyloid plaque accumulation while aquaporin-4 knockout reduced interstitial flow.","Harrison proposes a prion-based reclassification of neurodegenerative disease in which host proteins (PrP, Abeta, tau, alpha-synuclein, SOD1, possibly huntingtin) adopt self-propagating, beta-sheet-rich conformations that template normal precursor protein and spread between synaptically connected neurons.","The prion concept holds that the transmissible agents of kuru, CJD, scrapie, and BSE are devoid of nucleic acid and composed of host-encoded protein; AD is framed as a 'double-prion' disease in which both Abeta and tau prions coexist.","Late onset of inherited neurodegenerations, despite lifelong expression of the mutant protein, is attributed to age-dependent decline in protein quality-control/clearance mechanisms, consistent with the view that these are disorders of the aging nervous system.","Toxic small oligomers, rather than the mature plaques, tangles, and inclusion bodies, are increasingly viewed as the harmful species; visible aggregates may represent a relatively protective sequestration of toxic protein.","Impaired autophagy and lysosomal handling are central to neurodegeneration; GBA (glucocerebrosidase) mutations underlie about 5% of Parkinson's disease and 8-9% of dementia with Lewy bodies, with bidirectional inhibition between alpha-synuclein and glucocerebrosidase.","Induced pluripotent stem cell (iPSC)-derived neurons and 3D cerebral organoids model human neurodevelopmental disease (microcephaly, lissencephaly/Miller-Dieker, fragile X, Rett, Timothy syndrome) and confirmed Zika virus tropism for human neural progenitor (radial glia) cells as the cause of congenital microcephaly."],"pathophysiology":"This foundational chapter frames nervous-system disease around several converging mechanisms rather than a single illness. (1) Neuroimmunology/neuroinflammation: microglia and brain macrophages (yolk-sac-derived innate immune cells) and astrocytes are active, non-passive participants that prune synapses through complement, present antigen, secrete cytokines and trophic factors, and can shift between homeostatic and tissue-damaging states; crosstalk among microglia, astrocytes, and oligodendrocytes produces non-cell-autonomous pathology in MS, AD, PD, and ALS. (2) Myelin biology: oligodendrocytes maintain saltatory conduction and provide metabolic/neuroprotective support to axons, with activity-dependent (adaptive) myelination. (3) Waste clearance: the glymphatic system and meningeal lymphatic-like vessels clear interstitial solutes and aggregated proteins, with sleep-dependent and age-dependent modulation via aquaporin-4. (4) The microbiome can influence brain disease through molecular mimicry, immune modulation, circulating bacterial metabolites, enteric neural signaling, and possible prion-like seeding (e.g., the unproven hypothesis that E. coli Curli templates alpha-synuclein). (5) Protein aggregation and prion biology: misfolded, self-propagating proteins (Abeta, tau, alpha-synuclein, PrP, SOD1, TDP-43, expanded-polyglutamine huntingtin) spread trans-synaptically; toxic oligomers, defective ubiquitin-proteasome and autophagy-lysosome clearance, mitochondrial dynamics (fission/fusion via Drp1, MFN1/2, OPA1; mitophagy via PINK1/parkin), and retromer (VPS35/VPS26/VPS29) dysfunction all contribute to neuronal death. (6) Stem cell biology: iPSCs and cerebral organoids reproduce early human brain-development disorders and enable disease modeling and drug screening.","additionalDifferentials":["Alzheimer's disease and other amyloid/tau dementias","Parkinson's disease, dementia with Lewy bodies, and multiple-system atrophy (alpha-synucleinopathies)","Frontotemporal degeneration (tau, TDP-43, FUS; GRN and C9orf72)","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (SOD1, TDP-43, FUS, C9orf72)","Huntington's disease and other polyglutamine cerebellar degenerations","Multiple sclerosis and autoimmune/neuroinflammatory demyelination","Prion diseases (CJD, kuru, GSS, fatal insomnia)","Neurodevelopmental disorders (primary microcephaly, lissencephaly/Miller-Dieker, fragile X, Rett, Timothy syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":["Slow-virus paradigm historically invoked for kuru, CJD, and scrapie, later supplanted by the nucleic-acid-free prion hypothesis","Congenital Zika virus syndrome producing microcephaly via direct infection of neural progenitor cells","Microbiome/bacterial-protein seeding hypotheses (e.g., E. coli Curli templating alpha-synuclein) blurring infective versus degenerative boundaries"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Protein-aggregation neurodegenerations whose visible plaques/tangles/inclusions may be markers rather than the primary toxic species","Mitochondrial disorders (Friedreich's ataxia; MFN2-related Charcot-Marie-Tooth type 2A; OPA1-related dominant optic atrophy) presenting as neurodegeneration"],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for disease-causing/modifying mutations (APP, MAPT, SNCA, LRRK2, parkin, PINK1, DJ-1, GBA, SOD1, TDP-43, FUS, C9orf72, GRN, PRNP, VPS35, FMR1, MECP2, CACNA1C)","Neuropathologic/immunostaining characterization of aggregates (Abeta plaques, neurofibrillary tau tangles, Lewy bodies, glial cytoplasmic inclusions, ubiquitinated TDP-43 inclusions)","Patient-derived iPSC lines and cerebral organoids for disease modeling and high-throughput compound screening","Single-cell transcriptomic sequencing to resolve microglial and astrocyte heterogeneity"],"redFlags":["Progressive, age-related cognitive or motor decline suggesting an underlying self-propagating proteinopathy","Family history with high penetrance (e.g., GRN mutations highly penetrant for FTD) prompting genetic evaluation","Rapidly progressive dementia raising concern for a transmissible prion disease","Microcephaly with developmental delay and intractable epilepsy in neurodevelopmental disorders"],"prognosis":"As a mechanistic overview the chapter does not give a single prognosis. It frames many neurodegenerative diseases as relentlessly progressive disorders of the aging nervous system driven by self-propagating prion-like proteins, while emphasizing that emerging understanding of complement, microglial, lysosomal/autophagy, mitochondrial, and prion-clearance pathways offers new and rational therapeutic targets (e.g., anti-C1q and anti-TREM2 antibody trials, progranulin-directed and gene/protein-replacement strategies) and new hope for prevention, treatment, and repair.","pitfalls":["Assuming that macroscopic aggregates (plaques, tangles, inclusion bodies) directly cause neuronal dysfunction; toxic soluble oligomers may be the harmful species and sequestration may be protective.","Viewing microglia and astrocytes as merely secondary scavengers; genetic evidence places them as primary drivers of many neurodegenerative conditions.","Treating the resting-versus-activated microglial dichotomy as adequate; single-cell data reveal extensive, functionally heterogeneous states.","Over-extrapolating from rodent models; several human organoid findings (e.g., outer radial glia defects in Miller-Dieker syndrome) were not seen in mice, and some iPSC findings require replication before confidence.","Accepting unproven microbiome-seeding hypotheses (e.g., bacterial Curli triggering Parkinson's) as established; no proven microbiome-modulating therapy yet exists for any brain disease.","Therapeutic targeting of broadly acting systems (e.g., complement) risks off-target toxicity and loss of homeostatic functions such as physiologic synaptic pruning."],"management":["Direct disease-modifying therapy at the underlying molecular lesion where available, such as anti-amyloid monoclonal antibodies that promote clearance of beta-amyloid in Alzheimer's disease","Target the innate-immune, complement-mediated synapse loss and reactive astrocyte pathways now recognised as drivers, an emerging focus of neuroinflammation-directed therapeutics","Provide symptomatic pharmacotherapy tailored to the syndrome, for example dopaminergic replacement in Parkinsonism and immunomodulation in multiple sclerosis","Optimise sleep and the glymphatic clearance that removes interstitial aggregates, and address modifiable factors that accelerate protein accumulation","Coordinate multidisciplinary neurorehabilitation with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language therapy for progressive motor and cognitive decline","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing for the heritable forms, including familial mutations in APP, MAPT, SOD1, C9orf72 and progranulin","Anticipate the relentlessly progressive course with advance care planning and palliative support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3394","Harrison 22e p.3399","Harrison 22e p.3400","Harrison 22e p.3401","Harrison 22e p.3403"],"crossLinks":[]},"pathogenesis_diagnosis_and_treatment_of":{"name":"Pathogenesis, Diagnosis, and Treatment of Fungal Infections","system":"Multisystem / Infectious Diseases (Mycoses)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Fungal infections (general principles)","Mycoses overview","Principles of medical mycology"],"chapter":217,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1687","clinicalPearls":["Human fungal disease has risen sharply with the AIDS pandemic, broad antibacterial use, cytotoxic chemotherapy, biologic/precision-medicine immunosuppressants, and solid-organ or hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (Harrison 22e p.1687).","Mycoses are categorized clinically as mucocutaneous versus deep-organ disease, and epidemiologically as endemic versus opportunistic; mucocutaneous disease causes morbidity but is rarely fatal, whereas deep-organ disease is often severe with high mortality (Harrison 22e p.1687).","Endemic fungi are environmentally acquired and not part of normal flora, typically causing self-limited disease in immunocompetent hosts but severe illness when immunity is impaired; opportunistic fungi such as Candida and Aspergillus are commensal or ubiquitous and invade when host defenses fail (Harrison 22e p.1687).","Definitive diagnosis requires histopathologic demonstration of the fungus invading tissue together with culture from the specimen; a positive culture from blood or tissue can instead reflect colonization or laboratory contamination depending on organism and site (Harrison 22e p.1690).","PAS and Gomori methenamine silver (GMS) are the principal fungal stains; Candida (unlike most fungi) is visible on Gram-stained tissue, and a positive India ink stain of CSF is diagnostic for cryptococcosis (Harrison 22e p.1690).","The most reliable antigen/serologic tests are Coccidioides antibody, Histoplasma antigen, and cryptococcal polysaccharide antigen; galactomannan (best in BAL fluid) aids aspergillosis diagnosis but has frequent false negatives during antifungal prophylaxis and false positives with other fungi (Harrison 22e p.1691).","The serum beta-D-glucan assay has a high negative predictive value for invasive candidiasis but lacks specificity, and T2 magnetic resonance is FDA-approved for detecting Candida in blood (Harrison 22e p.1691).","Inherited CARD9 deficiency is the only known primary immunodeficiency that produces fungus-specific susceptibility without predisposition to other infections, autoimmunity, allergy, or cancer, causing chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, CNS Candida/Aspergillus/phaeohyphomycete infection, and deep dermatophytosis (Harrison 22e p.1688).","Antifungal host defense is compartmentalized: IL-17-producing lymphoid cells protect mucosal surfaces against Candida invasion, neutrophils control invasive Aspergillus and Candida, and the IL-12/IFN-gamma axis with activated macrophages controls intramacrophagic fungi such as Cryptococcus, Pneumocystis, and endemic dimorphic fungi (Harrison 22e pp.1688-1689).","AIDS predisposes to mucosal but not invasive candidiasis (reflecting IL-17 dependence), whereas vaginal candidiasis arises with antibiotic use rather than AIDS or IL-17-targeted biologics, highlighting the protective role of the vaginal microbiota (Harrison 22e p.1689).","Chronic granulomatous disease, caused by NADPH-oxidase subunit mutations impairing neutrophil superoxide generation, carries roughly a 40% lifetime risk of invasive aspergillosis; Aspergillus nidulans is seen almost exclusively in this condition (Harrison 22e pp.1688-1689).","Early initiation of appropriate antifungal therapy is a key determinant of outcome in candidemia, aspergillosis, and mucormycosis, alongside source control (catheter removal in candidemia, abscess drainage, surgical debridement in mucormycosis) and immune reconstitution (Harrison 22e p.1691)."],"pathophysiology":"Fungi are eukaryotes classified morphologically as yeasts (round/budding single cells), molds (filamentous hyphae at both room temperature and in tissue, e.g., Aspergillus, Mucorales, dermatophytes), and dimorphic fungi (yeast or spherical structures in tissue but filamentous forms in the environment). Candida produces both yeast and pseudohyphal forms in tissue except C. glabrata and C. auris, which form only yeasts; Cryptococcus exists only as yeast. Deep-organ infection by molds and endemic dimorphic fungi follows inhalation, whereas commensal Candida invades from mucocutaneous colonization in the gut or skin. Antifungal immunity centers on the C-type lectin receptor/SYK/CARD9 signaling pathway, which recognizes fungal polysaccharides and drives proinflammatory mediator release, leukocyte recruitment, inflammasome activation, and Th17 differentiation. Protection is site- and pathogen-specific: at mucosa, IL-17A/IL-17F and IL-22 from Th17, Tc17, gamma-delta T, and innate lymphoid cells stimulate epithelial antimicrobial peptide production and barrier repair (STAT3-dependent); against invasive molds and Candida, recruited neutrophils assemble the six-subunit NADPH oxidase to generate superoxide for fungal killing; against intramacrophagic fungi, IL-12-producing macrophages activate STAT4 in T cells to release IFN-gamma, which signals through STAT1 (with TNF-alpha and GM-CSF) to enable intracellular killing. Defects at any node, whether inherited (e.g., IL17F/IL17RA/IL17RC/TRAF3IP2, STAT3, STAT1, CARD9, NADPH oxidase subunits, IL12RB1, IFNGR1/2, IRF8, GATA2) or pharmacologic/acquired (IL-17/IL-23 biologics, BTK inhibitors, glucocorticoids, anti-TNF agents, JAK inhibitors, anti-IFN-gamma or anti-GM-CSF autoantibodies), produce predictable, compartment-specific fungal susceptibility (Harrison 22e pp.1687-1689).","additionalDifferentials":["Distinguishing true invasive fungal infection from colonization or laboratory contamination when fungi are recovered from culture (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Chronic cavitary pulmonary mycosis versus cavitary tuberculosis (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis versus invasive pulmonary aspergillosis in differing host settings (Harrison 22e p.1688)"],"infectionMimics":["Mycobacterial infection (notably tuberculosis), which shares granulomatous histology and intramacrophagic survival with endemic dimorphic fungi (Harrison 22e p.1689)","Salmonellosis and other intramacrophagic pathogens that exploit the same IL-12/IFN-gamma defects as intramacrophagic fungi (Harrison 22e p.1689)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Neoplasia and other causes of granulomatous or necrotizing tissue reaction (H&E-defined granuloma, angioinvasion, necrosis are nonspecific without organism identification) (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Sarcoidosis and other granulomatous conditions in patients predisposed to cryptococcosis (Harrison 22e p.1688)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Histopathology with PAS and GMS stains, plus H&E to assess granuloma, angioinvasion, and necrosis (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Calcofluor white staining with fluorescence microscopy for fungi in fluid specimens (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Lysis-centrifugation blood culture to increase sensitivity for Candida and less common fungi such as Histoplasma (Harrison 22e p.1690)","MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry for detection and speciation of cultured fungi (Harrison 22e pp.1690-1691)","Antigen and serologic assays: cryptococcal polysaccharide antigen, Histoplasma antigen, Coccidioides antibody, galactomannan (BAL preferred), and serum beta-D-glucan (Harrison 22e p.1691)","T2 magnetic resonance assay for Candida in whole blood (Harrison 22e p.1691)","India ink stain of CSF, diagnostic for cryptococcosis (Harrison 22e p.1690)"],"redFlags":["Profound or prolonged neutropenia, allogeneic HSCT, or high-dose glucocorticoids portend invasive mold disease (aspergillosis, mucormycosis) and invasive candidiasis (Harrison 22e pp.1688-1689)","Diabetic ketoacidosis as a setting for rhinocerebral/sinopulmonary mucormycosis (Harrison 22e p.1688)","Advanced AIDS with low CD4 counts predisposing to intramacrophagic and mucosal mycoses (Harrison 22e p.1689)","Delay in appropriate antifungal therapy in candidemia, aspergillosis, or mucormycosis, which worsens outcome (Harrison 22e p.1691)","Failure to achieve source control (retained central venous catheter in candidemia, undrained abscess, undebrided necrotic sinus tissue in mucormycosis) (Harrison 22e p.1691)","Emerging drug-resistant species: azole/echinocandin-resistant Candida glabrata, Candida auris, and azole-resistant Aspergillus fumigatus (Harrison 22e p.1687)"],"prognosis":"Outcome hinges on host immune status, the fungus and site involved, timeliness of appropriate therapy, source control, and immune reconstitution. Mucocutaneous mycoses cause morbidity but are rarely fatal, whereas deep-organ infections are frequently severe with high mortality. Before amphotericin B, cryptococcal meningitis and other disseminated mycoses were nearly always fatal. A favorable prognosis in opportunistic mycoses depends substantially on restoring host immunity, for example neutrophil recovery, tapering of immunosuppression, or starting combination antiretroviral therapy in AIDS (Harrison 22e pp.1687, 1691).","pitfalls":["Treating a positive fungal culture as proof of infection when it may represent colonization or contamination, depending on organism and anatomic site (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Relying on galactomannan for aspergillosis without accounting for frequent false negatives under antifungal prophylaxis and false positives from other fungi (Harrison 22e p.1691)","Interpreting a beta-D-glucan result without recognizing its lack of specificity despite a high negative predictive value (Harrison 22e p.1691)","Attempting to identify fungi reliably on H&E alone, which defines tissue reaction but is insufficient for speciation (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Overlooking that fluconazole and echinocandins lack activity against molds (and echinocandins against Cryptococcus and endemic dimorphic fungi), and that voriconazole/echinocandins do not cover Mucorales (Harrison 22e pp.1691-1692)","Failing to monitor drug levels where pharmacokinetics are variable (e.g., itraconazole capsules, voriconazole with CYP2C19 polymorphism) (Harrison 22e pp.1691-1692)","Not recognizing that emerging immunomodulatory biologics (IL-17/IL-23 inhibitors, BTK inhibitors, JAK inhibitors, anti-TNF, SYK inhibitors) create predictable, compartment-specific fungal risk (Harrison 22e pp.1688-1689)"],"management":["Select antifungal therapy by fungal class and site: echinocandins or azoles for invasive candidiasis, an azole such as voriconazole or isavuconazole or a polyene for invasive aspergillosis, and amphotericin B with flucytosine for cryptococcal disease","Treat mucormycosis with liposomal amphotericin B alongside urgent surgical debridement, as antifungal drugs alone are insufficient","Achieve source control by removing infected intravascular catheters, draining collections and debriding angioinvasive or necrotic tissue","Restore host defences where possible by reducing immunosuppression, reversing neutropenia and pursuing immune reconstitution","Monitor azole therapy with therapeutic drug concentrations and for hepatotoxicity and drug interactions, and track response with serial antigen or beta-D-glucan assays","Distinguish colonisation from invasive disease before committing to prolonged therapy, and tailor treatment duration to organism, site and immune recovery","Manage endemic mycoses by severity, using an azole for mild-to-moderate disease and polyene induction for severe or disseminated infection","Involve infectious diseases specialists and, for deep-organ disease, the relevant surgical teams"],"severityClassification":"Classified by anatomic depth (mucocutaneous infections, which are rarely fatal, versus deep-organ infections, which are severe and often highly fatal) and by epidemiologic category (endemic versus opportunistic). Severity within these groups is further modulated by host immune competence, the implicated fungus, and the involved tissue (Harrison 22e p.1687).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1687","Harrison 22e p.1688","Harrison 22e p.1689","Harrison 22e p.1690","Harrison 22e p.1691","Harrison 22e p.1692"],"crossLinks":[]},"patulous_eustachian_tube":{"name":"Patulous eustachian tube","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Otology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["patulous ET","PET","abnormally patent eustachian tube","patulous auditory tube"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The eustachian tube stays abnormally open at rest instead of remaining closed and opening only briefly on swallowing or yawning","The cardinal symptom is autophony - patients hear their own voice, and often their own breathing, unnaturally loudly in the affected ear","Breathing-synchronous tinnitus or aural fullness that fluctuates with the respiratory cycle is characteristic","Symptoms typically improve or resolve on lying down, bending forward, or lowering the head, as venous engorgement of the tubal mucosa closes the lumen, and worsen when upright or after exertion","Strongly associated with weight loss, including rapid loss from illness, bariatric surgery, or GLP-1 receptor agonist therapy, as well as dehydration, pregnancy, and oestrogen use","Otoscopy may show a tympanic membrane that moves medially and laterally with respiration, and tympanometry can demonstrate pressure swings synchronous with breathing","Reassurance and explanation are the mainstay, as the condition is benign and often self-limiting; correcting dehydration or weight loss and using measures to plump the tubal lining help selected patients","It is frequently mistaken for obstructive eustachian tube dysfunction, and inappropriate ventilation-tube insertion can make the autophony worse"],"pathophysiology":"The eustachian tube is normally closed at rest, opening momentarily during swallowing or yawning to equalise middle-ear pressure. In a patulous tube the lumen remains open, so sound and airflow from the nasopharynx pass directly into the middle ear, producing autophony and breathing-synchronous symptoms. The usual mechanism is loss of the peritubal soft-tissue and mucosal bulk that normally keeps the tube collapsed, as occurs with weight loss, dehydration, or reduction of the fat around Ostmann's pad. Lying down or lowering the head increases venous congestion of the tubal mucosa, which closes the lumen and relieves symptoms, accounting for the strongly positional pattern.","additionalDifferentials":["Superior semicircular canal dehiscence","Obstructive eustachian tube dysfunction","Meniere's disease","Middle-ear effusion","Temporomandibular joint dysfunction","Palatal or middle-ear myoclonus","Third-window lesions producing conductive-type autophony"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Tympanometry during quiet and forced respiration to detect breathing-related pressure swings","Pure-tone audiometry to exclude other causes of aural symptoms","Nasoendoscopy of the eustachian tube orifice","Otoscopy observing tympanic membrane movement synchronous with breathing","High-resolution CT of the temporal bone to exclude superior canal dehiscence","Nutritional and weight assessment, including recent weight loss or GLP-1 agonist use"],"redFlags":["Persistently unilateral symptoms with a possible nasopharyngeal mass, requiring exclusion of tumour","Rapidly progressive or profound hearing loss","Associated neurological signs","Sound- or pressure-induced vertigo suggesting superior canal dehiscence rather than a patulous tube"],"prognosis":"Often benign and self-limiting, especially when a reversible precipitant such as dehydration or recent weight loss is corrected; a minority have persistent troublesome autophony that may require procedural intervention.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling it as obstructive eustachian tube dysfunction and inserting a ventilation tube, which usually fails and can worsen autophony","Overlooking recent weight loss, GLP-1 agonist use, or dehydration as the trigger","Not recognising the improvement on lying down as a key diagnostic clue","Missing a nasopharyngeal lesion in a persistently unilateral case"],"management":["Correct reversible precipitants first by rehydrating, encouraging restoration of lost weight, and reviewing causative oestrogen or GLP-1 receptor agonist therapy","Reassure and advise positional manoeuvres such as lowering the head, which engorge the tubal mucosa and relieve autophony","Trial topical measures that bulk or irritate the tubal mucosa, such as nasal saline or oestrogen or irritant nasal drops","Consider procedural intervention for persistent troublesome autophony, including peritubal injection augmentation of the tubal orifice or eustachian tube obliteration","Insert a ventilation tube in selected patients, recognising the response is variable","Refer to otolaryngology when symptoms persist despite conservative measures and exclude mimics such as superior canal dehiscence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"pcdh19_epilepsy":{"name":"PCDH19-related epilepsy","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCDH19","epilepsy in females with mental retardation","EFMR","protocadherin-19 clustering epilepsy"],"chapter":null,"source":"ILAE classification / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in the X-linked PCDH19 gene, with an unusual inheritance in which heterozygous females are affected while hemizygous males are typically unaffected carriers","Seizures usually begin in the first years of life, often triggered by fever, and characteristically occur in tight clusters over hours to days","Focal seizures with prominent fear or affective features are common during clusters","Development ranges from normal to intellectual disability, and autistic and behavioural features may emerge","Seizure clusters tend to become less frequent with age, and many patients improve markedly by later childhood","The condition can resemble Dravet syndrome, but SCN1A testing is negative and PCDH19 testing is diagnostic","Management focuses on aggressive treatment of clusters and fever control, sometimes with steroid or benzodiazepine strategies during acute clustering"],"pathophysiology":"PCDH19-related epilepsy arises from variants in the X-linked PCDH19 gene, which encodes protocadherin-19, a cell-adhesion molecule involved in neuronal migration and network formation. The unusual sex-limited pattern is explained by cellular interference: random X-inactivation in heterozygous females produces a mosaic of cells with and without functional protocadherin-19, disrupting cell-cell communication, whereas hemizygous males express a uniform population and are usually spared. This mosaic disruption of neuronal connectivity underlies the fever-sensitive seizure clusters and the associated cognitive and behavioural features.","additionalDifferentials":["Dravet syndrome (SCN1A)","Genetic epilepsy with febrile seizures plus (GEFS+)","Complex febrile seizures","Focal epilepsy of other cause","Other developmental and epileptic encephalopathies","Autism spectrum disorder with epilepsy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PCDH19 gene testing, often within an epilepsy panel, especially in girls with clustering fever-related seizures","SCN1A testing to distinguish from Dravet syndrome","EEG during and between clusters, which may be normal or show focal/generalised discharges","Brain MRI to exclude structural causes","Developmental and behavioural assessment"],"redFlags":["Prolonged seizure clusters progressing to status epilepticus","New developmental regression or behavioural deterioration during a cluster","Fever-triggered clustering in a young girl resembling Dravet syndrome","Marked affective or fear semiology raising diagnostic uncertainty"],"prognosis":"Seizure clusters are often severe in early childhood but frequently diminish or remit with age, so the epilepsy prognosis is generally more favourable than in Dravet syndrome. Cognitive and behavioural outcomes vary widely, from normal development to significant intellectual disability and autistic features.","pitfalls":["Assuming Dravet syndrome and stopping after a negative SCN1A result without testing PCDH19","Overlooking the diagnosis in an affected girl with an apparently unaffected carrier father","Underestimating the intensity of acute clusters and undertreating them","Attributing behavioural features solely to epilepsy rather than the underlying disorder"],"management":["Control seizures with broad-spectrum antiseizure medication such as clobazam, sodium valproate or levetiracetam, as no single agent is uniformly effective","Manage the characteristic clusters aggressively with a home rescue plan using benzodiazepines, and treat intercurrent fever promptly with antipyretics to reduce triggering","Consider a short course of corticosteroids or clobazam to abort prolonged or refractory clusters","Explore neurosteroid-directed therapy such as ganaxolone where standard agents fail","Assess and support development, screening for and managing intellectual disability and autistic and behavioural features with multidisciplinary input","Provide genetic counselling for the unusual X-linked pattern in which heterozygous girls are affected and hemizygous boys are usually unaffected carriers","Reassure that clusters often diminish with age while maintaining epilepsy and neurodevelopmental follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ILAE classification of the epilepsies","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"pcwh_syndrome":{"name":"PCWH syndrome (SOX10)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCWH","peripheral demyelinating neuropathy-central dysmyelination-Waardenburg-Hirschsprung","SOX10-related PCWH","Waardenburg syndrome type 4 with neurological involvement"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe neurocristopathy caused by variants in SOX10, a transcription factor essential for neural crest and glial development; the acronym denotes Peripheral demyelinating neuropathy, Central dysmyelination, Waardenburg syndrome and Hirschsprung disease","It sits at the severe end of the SOX10-related Waardenburg-Shah (type 4) spectrum, uniting features that would otherwise be considered separate disorders","Waardenburg features include sensorineural hearing loss and pigmentary anomalies such as a white forelock, heterochromia iridis and patchy skin or hair depigmentation","Hirschsprung disease (aganglionic megacolon) causes neonatal intestinal obstruction and chronic constipation and reflects failure of enteric neural crest migration","The neurological picture combines a central hypomyelinating or dysmyelinating leukodystrophy with a peripheral demyelinating neuropathy, producing motor delay, spasticity, ataxia and neuropathic weakness","Diagnosis is clinical pattern recognition of this striking combination, supported by MRI showing central dysmyelination and by nerve conduction studies, and confirmed by SOX10 testing","Management is supportive and multidisciplinary, including surgical treatment of Hirschsprung disease, hearing rehabilitation, and physiotherapy and developmental support"],"pathophysiology":"PCWH is caused by heterozygous variants in SOX10, a transcription factor that governs the development, migration and differentiation of neural crest cells and their derivatives, including melanocytes, enteric ganglia, and both Schwann cells and oligodendrocytes. Disruption of SOX10 function impairs multiple neural crest lineages simultaneously: defective melanocyte and inner-ear development produces the Waardenburg pigmentary and auditory features, failed enteric ganglion formation causes Hirschsprung disease, and impaired Schwann cell and oligodendrocyte function produces combined peripheral demyelinating neuropathy and central dysmyelination. Certain variants that escape nonsense-mediated decay are thought to exert dominant-negative effects, contributing to the severe combined phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Waardenburg syndrome types 1 to 4 without the full neurological involvement","Isolated Hirschsprung disease (RET and other genes)","Charcot-Marie-Tooth and other hereditary demyelinating neuropathies","Congenital hypomyelinating and dysmyelinating leukodystrophies","Other neurocristopathies with pigmentary and enteric features","Congenital sensorineural hearing loss of other genetic causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SOX10 sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis to confirm the diagnosis","Brain MRI to demonstrate central dysmyelination or hypomyelination","Nerve conduction studies to characterise the peripheral demyelinating neuropathy","Audiological assessment for sensorineural hearing loss","Contrast enema and rectal biopsy where Hirschsprung disease is suspected","Ophthalmology and dermatology review for pigmentary features"],"redFlags":["Neonatal intestinal obstruction or enterocolitis from Hirschsprung disease requiring urgent surgical management","Respiratory compromise from combined central and peripheral neuromuscular involvement","Feeding difficulty and aspiration risk","Progressive weakness or loss of motor milestones"],"prognosis":"PCWH is a severe, multisystem disorder and prognosis depends on the combined burden of neurological, enteric and sensory involvement; the central and peripheral myelin disease contributes to significant motor and developmental disability. Early surgical management of Hirschsprung disease and coordinated supportive care influence outcome, but the overall course is serious.","pitfalls":["Treating the Waardenburg, Hirschsprung and neurological features as unrelated rather than recognising a single SOX10 disorder","Overlooking the peripheral neuropathy when central dysmyelination is evident, or vice versa","Missing Hirschsprung disease as a cause of neonatal obstruction or refractory constipation","Attributing hearing loss and pigmentary changes to isolated Waardenburg syndrome without pursuing the broader phenotype","Failing to provide genetic counselling, including for de novo dominant variants"],"management":["Manage Hirschsprung disease surgically with resection of the aganglionic segment and a pull-through procedure, and treat neonatal obstruction and enterocolitis","Rehabilitate the sensorineural hearing loss with hearing aids or cochlear implantation and early auditory-verbal support","Treat the spasticity of the combined central dysmyelination and peripheral neuropathy with physiotherapy, antispasticity agents such as baclofen, and orthoses","Support motor development and neuropathic weakness through coordinated physiotherapy, occupational therapy and mobility aids","Provide nutritional support and manage bowel dysfunction and feeding difficulties","Offer ophthalmology and dermatology review for the pigmentary anomalies and monitor for related complications","Arrange genetic counselling, noting most SOX10 variants arise de novo, and coordinate care through a multidisciplinary neurogenetic team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"pearson_marrow_pancreas_syndrome":{"name":"Pearson Marrow-Pancreas Syndrome","system":"Haematology / Multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pearson syndrome","Sideroblastic anaemia with exocrine pancreatic dysfunction","Mitochondrial DNA deletion syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A multisystem mitochondrial disorder caused by large-scale single deletions of mitochondrial DNA, typically sporadic and arising de novo with low recurrence risk.","It usually presents in infancy with a transfusion-dependent sideroblastic anaemia, often accompanied by neutropenia and thrombocytopenia.","The bone marrow hallmark is prominent vacuolisation of erythroid and myeloid precursors together with ring sideroblasts.","Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency from acinar fibrosis causes malabsorption and failure to thrive.","Lactic acidosis and involvement of other high-energy tissues, especially the renal tubules, liver and endocrine organs, are characteristic.","It lies on the same mitochondrial DNA deletion spectrum as Kearns-Sayre syndrome, and survivors may later evolve into that phenotype.","The proportion of deleted mitochondrial genomes and their distribution across tissues (heteroplasmy) determine the severity and pattern of disease.","Unlike the persistent aplasia of Diamond-Blackfan anaemia, the haematological picture may spontaneously improve with age in those who survive infancy."],"pathophysiology":"Pearson marrow-pancreas syndrome arises from a large-scale deletion of mitochondrial DNA that removes genes encoding subunits of the oxidative phosphorylation complexes and often transfer RNAs, impairing respiratory chain function. Because each cell contains many copies of mitochondrial DNA, the mixture of normal and deleted genomes (heteroplasmy) and its tissue distribution govern the clinical picture, with the most metabolically demanding and rapidly dividing tissues, such as bone marrow, exocrine pancreas and renal tubules, most severely affected. Defective oxidative phosphorylation shifts metabolism towards anaerobic pathways and causes lactic acidosis, while impaired mitochondrial iron utilisation produces ring sideroblasts. Cellular energy failure underlies the characteristic vacuolisation of marrow precursors, the fibrosis and dysfunction of pancreatic acinar tissue, and the renal tubular losses. The deletion typically occurs sporadically during oogenesis or early embryonic development, so recurrence within a family is uncommon.","additionalDifferentials":["Diamond-Blackfan anaemia","Other congenital sideroblastic anaemias (including X-linked ALAS2 and SLC25A38-related forms)","Shwachman-Diamond syndrome","Other mitochondrial cytopathies, including Kearns-Sayre syndrome","Congenital dyserythropoietic anaemia","Cystic fibrosis (as a cause of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency)","Myelodysplastic syndrome with ring sideroblasts"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count, reticulocytes and blood film","Bone marrow aspirate showing ring sideroblasts and vacuolated erythroid and myeloid precursors","Blood lactate and metabolic screen including urine organic acids and plasma amino acids","Molecular testing for a mitochondrial DNA deletion (long-range PCR, Southern blot or related methods)","Faecal elastase and assessment of exocrine pancreatic function","Renal tubular function studies with urine electrolytes, glucose and amino acids","Surveillance for evolving organ involvement, including cardiac conduction studies and ophthalmological assessment"],"redFlags":["Metabolic decompensation with worsening lactic acidosis","Sepsis in a neutropenic or otherwise cytopenic infant","Cardiac conduction abnormalities suggesting progression to Kearns-Sayre syndrome","Progressive renal tubular electrolyte losses","Signs of hepatic dysfunction or failure"],"prognosis":"The outlook is guarded, with substantial early mortality from metabolic crises, infection and marrow failure; survivors may show haematological recovery but remain at risk of progression to Kearns-Sayre syndrome and further multisystem organ involvement.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as Diamond-Blackfan anaemia; the sideroblastic marrow with vacuolated precursors, lactic acidosis and multisystem features point to Pearson syndrome.","Attributing failure to thrive to feeding difficulty and missing exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","Overlooking renal tubular dysfunction and its electrolyte consequences.","Ascribing the anaemia to nutritional deficiency and delaying mitochondrial evaluation.","Failing to recognise the mitochondrial basis, so that multisystem surveillance and appropriate genetic counselling are omitted."],"management":["Support the transfusion-dependent sideroblastic anaemia with red-cell transfusions and add iron chelation to counter transfusional iron overload","Replace pancreatic enzymes for exocrine insufficiency and supplement fat-soluble vitamins to manage malabsorption and failure to thrive","Treat lactic acidosis and electrolyte disturbance, and manage renal tubular losses of the Fanconi type with bicarbonate and electrolyte replacement","Manage neutropenia and thrombocytopenia, treating intercurrent infection promptly and aggressively as metabolic crises are life-threatening","Avoid mitochondrial-toxic drugs such as sodium valproate, aminoglycosides and metformin","Provide surveillance for evolution to Kearns-Sayre syndrome, monitoring cardiac conduction, endocrine and neuromuscular function in survivors","Offer genetic counselling for the sporadic mitochondrial DNA deletion and coordinate multidisciplinary metabolic, haematology and dietetic care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood (standard reference)","Williams Hematology (standard reference)","Mitochondrial medicine references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"pearson_syndrome":{"name":"Pearson marrow-pancreas syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pearson syndrome","mitochondrial DNA deletion syndrome","sideroblastic anaemia with pancreatic insufficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews (NCBI) and standard mitochondrial disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Multisystem mitochondrial disorder of infancy caused by a single large-scale deletion of mitochondrial DNA, usually sporadic","Cardinal features are a transfusion-dependent sideroblastic anaemia and exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","Bone marrow characteristically shows ring sideroblasts and prominent vacuolisation of erythroid and myeloid precursors","Cytopenias may extend beyond anaemia to neutropenia and thrombocytopenia","Lactic acidosis and other metabolic derangements reflect impaired oxidative phosphorylation","The pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption, failure to thrive and steatorrhoea, and hepatic and renal tubular dysfunction may occur","Survivors of the haematological phase may later develop the features of Kearns-Sayre syndrome","Diagnosis is confirmed by demonstrating a single large-scale mitochondrial DNA deletion, with supportive marrow and biochemical findings"],"pathophysiology":"Pearson syndrome is caused by a single large-scale deletion of mitochondrial DNA, typically arising sporadically. Loss of genes encoding oxidative phosphorylation subunits and mitochondrial transfer RNAs impairs cellular energy production and disrupts mitochondrial iron handling, producing sideroblastic anaemia with ring sideroblasts. High-turnover tissues such as the bone marrow and exocrine pancreas are severely affected. The heteroplasmic burden of deleted genomes can shift with time, so surviving children may transition toward a Kearns-Sayre phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Shwachman-Diamond syndrome","Diamond-Blackfan anaemia","Congenital or acquired sideroblastic anaemias","Other inherited bone-marrow-failure syndromes","Cystic fibrosis (pancreatic insufficiency)","Other mitochondrial cytopathies including Kearns-Sayre syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film showing sideroblastic anaemia and cytopenias","Bone marrow examination for ring sideroblasts and vacuolated precursors","Molecular analysis for a single large-scale mitochondrial DNA deletion","Blood lactate and metabolic screen for lactic acidosis","Faecal elastase and assessment of exocrine pancreatic function","Hepatic and renal tubular function tests"],"redFlags":["Severe metabolic (lactic) acidosis","Sepsis during neutropenia","Multiorgan failure involving liver and kidney","Symptomatic pancytopenia requiring intensive transfusion support"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is poor, with high mortality in infancy from metabolic crises, infection and marrow failure. The anaemia may improve spontaneously in children who survive, but these survivors are at risk of progressing to Kearns-Sayre syndrome; management is supportive and multidisciplinary.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the marrow findings for a primary haematological malignancy or Shwachman-Diamond syndrome","Overlooking the underlying mitochondrial aetiology in an infant with refractory anaemia","Failing to anticipate metabolic decompensation during intercurrent illness","Not recognising possible later evolution into Kearns-Sayre syndrome"],"management":["Manage the sideroblastic anaemia with regular red-cell transfusions, adding iron chelation once transfusional iron overload develops","Correct exocrine pancreatic insufficiency with pancreatic enzyme replacement and supplement fat-soluble vitamins for the associated malabsorption and steatorrhoea","Treat lactic acidosis and support hepatic and renal tubular dysfunction with bicarbonate and electrolyte replacement","Treat neutropenia and thrombocytopenia and respond urgently to infection and metabolic decompensation","Avoid drugs toxic to mitochondrial function such as sodium valproate, aminoglycosides and metformin","Monitor survivors for transition to Kearns-Sayre syndrome, including cardiac conduction and endocrine surveillance","Provide genetic counselling for the usually sporadic single mitochondrial DNA deletion and deliver care through a multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (NCBI): Mitochondrial DNA deletion syndromes","OMIM: Pearson marrow-pancreas syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"pectus_excavatum":{"name":"Pectus excavatum","system":"Paediatric surgery / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["funnel chest","sunken chest","PE"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Bailey & Love (paediatric surgery)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common congenital chest wall deformity, characterised by an inward depression of the sternum and adjacent costal cartilages producing a sunken appearance","It is often noticed in early childhood and typically becomes more pronounced during the adolescent growth spurt","Many patients are asymptomatic, and the main concern is cosmetic and psychological, particularly body-image distress in adolescents","Severe depression can displace and compress the heart and reduce exercise tolerance, causing exertional breathlessness or palpitations in some patients","There is a recognised association with connective tissue disorders such as Marfan syndrome and with scoliosis, which should be looked for","Severity is commonly quantified with the Haller index derived from CT, comparing chest width with the narrowest sternovertebral distance","Surgical correction, when indicated, is most often by the minimally invasive Nuss procedure using a retrosternal bar, or by the open Ravitch technique","Non-operative options such as vacuum bell therapy may benefit selected younger patients with milder, flexible deformities"],"pathophysiology":"Pectus excavatum results from abnormal, excessive growth of the costal cartilages that pushes the sternum posteriorly, creating a concave anterior chest wall. The precise cause is unknown but is thought to involve disordered cartilage growth, and there is a familial tendency and links with connective tissue disease. In marked deformity the depressed sternum can compress and rotate the heart and reduce the space available for cardiac filling, which may impair cardiopulmonary performance, particularly during exertion.","additionalDifferentials":["Pectus carinatum (protrusion deformity)","Poland syndrome (with chest wall and pectoral muscle hypoplasia)","Marfan or other connective tissue syndrome with chest involvement","Acquired chest wall deformity after thoracic surgery","Kyphoscoliosis affecting chest shape"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT or MRI of the chest to calculate the Haller (or correction) index and assess cardiac compression","Echocardiography to evaluate cardiac displacement and function","Pulmonary function testing to assess any restrictive impairment","Cardiopulmonary exercise testing in symptomatic patients","Assessment for connective tissue disorders and scoliosis where suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive exertional dyspnoea or exercise intolerance suggesting significant cardiopulmonary compromise","Echocardiographic evidence of marked cardiac compression or displacement","Features of an underlying connective tissue disorder such as Marfan syndrome","Arrhythmia or syncope on exertion warranting cardiac evaluation"],"prognosis":"The deformity itself is rarely dangerous, and many patients do well without surgery. Operative correction in appropriately selected patients generally produces good cosmetic and functional results with high satisfaction, though the retrosternal bar requires later removal and there is a small risk of recurrence.","pitfalls":["Dismissing the condition as purely cosmetic and overlooking genuine cardiopulmonary symptoms","Failing to screen for associated connective tissue disorders and scoliosis","Offering surgery based on appearance alone without objective severity and functional assessment","Underestimating the psychological impact on adolescents"],"management":["Observe mild, asymptomatic deformity, as many patients do well without intervention","Offer non-surgical vacuum bell therapy and exercise or physiotherapy programmes for flexible or milder chest-wall depression","Correct severe or symptomatic deformity surgically, using the minimally invasive Nuss bar procedure or an open Ravitch repair, with later planned bar removal","Address body-image distress with psychological support, particularly in adolescents","Screen for and manage associated conditions, notably Marfan syndrome and scoliosis","Assess cardiopulmonary impact with imaging, echocardiography and exercise testing to guide the decision to operate, and refer symptomatic patients to paediatric or thoracic surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"pediatric_status_asthmaticus":{"name":"Pediatric Status Asthmaticus","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["status asthmaticus","acute severe asthma","severe asthma exacerbation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe, sustained asthma exacerbation that does not respond adequately to initial bronchodilator therapy and constitutes a medical emergency.","Assessment combines clinical signs (work of breathing, accessory muscle use, air entry, mental state) with objective measures such as oxygen saturation and, where feasible, peak flow.","A quiet chest with poor air entry is ominous, indicating severe airflow limitation rather than improvement.","Rising carbon dioxide on blood gas in a tiring child signals impending respiratory failure; early in an attack hypocapnia is expected, so a normalising or high CO2 is a warning sign.","First-line treatment uses inhaled short-acting beta-agonists, often with inhaled anticholinergic agents, and early systemic corticosteroids, alongside oxygen to maintain saturations.","Escalation options for refractory cases include intravenous magnesium sulfate, continuous nebulised bronchodilator, and intravenous beta-agonist, with careful monitoring.","Agitation, drowsiness, exhaustion, cyanosis, or a silent chest indicate life-threatening disease requiring intensive care and possible ventilatory support.","Mechanical ventilation carries particular risks in asthma (air trapping and barotrauma) and is reserved for refractory respiratory failure using lung-protective, permissive strategies.","Identifying and addressing triggers, ensuring correct inhaler technique, and providing a written action plan help prevent recurrence."],"pathophysiology":"Acute severe asthma results from intense airway inflammation, bronchial smooth muscle constriction, mucosal oedema, and mucus plugging, producing widespread airflow obstruction that is worse on expiration. Air trapping and dynamic hyperinflation increase the work of breathing and impair gas exchange, causing ventilation-perfusion mismatch and hypoxaemia. Early tachypnoea drives off carbon dioxide, but as the child fatigues and obstruction worsens, alveolar ventilation falls, carbon dioxide rises, and respiratory acidosis develops, heralding respiratory failure. Hyperinflation also impairs venous return and can compromise cardiac output.","additionalDifferentials":["Bronchiolitis or viral-induced wheeze in younger children","Foreign body aspiration","Anaphylaxis with bronchospasm","Pneumonia with wheeze","Vocal cord dysfunction","Cardiac failure or a wheeze-mimicking cardiac lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous pulse oximetry and clinical severity scoring","Blood gas in severe or deteriorating cases to assess for rising carbon dioxide","Peak expiratory flow where age and cooperation allow","Chest radiograph if complications such as pneumothorax, consolidation, or foreign body are suspected","Electrolytes when high-dose beta-agonist therapy is used (risk of hypokalaemia)"],"redFlags":["Silent chest with poor air entry","Exhaustion, drowsiness, confusion, or agitation indicating hypoxia and impending failure","Rising or normalising carbon dioxide in a tiring child (impending respiratory failure)","Cyanosis or falling oxygen saturation despite maximal therapy","Poor response to initial bronchodilators or rapid clinical deterioration"],"prognosis":"Most children with severe exacerbations recover fully with prompt, escalating treatment. A minority progress to respiratory failure requiring intensive care and ventilation, which carries higher risk. Poor prognostic factors include delayed treatment, prior life-threatening attacks, and inadequate preventive management; good discharge planning reduces relapse.","pitfalls":["Misreading a quiet chest or a normalising carbon dioxide as improvement rather than deterioration","Delaying systemic corticosteroids or under-treating a severe exacerbation","Over-relying on oxygen saturation alone without assessing work of breathing and mental state","Instituting mechanical ventilation without anticipating air trapping and barotrauma","Discharging without addressing triggers, inhaler technique, and an action plan"],"management":["Give controlled oxygen to maintain adequate saturations alongside repeated or continuous nebulised short-acting beta-agonists and inhaled ipratropium","Start systemic corticosteroids early, oral where tolerated or intravenous in the severely unwell child","Escalate refractory cases with intravenous magnesium sulfate, continuous nebulised bronchodilator and an intravenous beta-agonist, considering intravenous aminophylline","Recognise impending respiratory failure such as a silent chest, exhaustion or a rising carbon dioxide and provide respiratory support with non-invasive ventilation or intubation and mechanical ventilation in intensive care","Monitor continuously with pulse oximetry and clinical scoring, checking blood gases in deteriorating children and electrolytes for beta-agonist-induced hypokalaemia","Investigate for complications such as pneumothorax or consolidation when suspected","Before discharge, optimise inhaler technique, provide a written asthma action plan and arrange follow-up to prevent recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"peeling_skin_syndrome":{"name":"Peeling skin syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PSS","acral peeling skin syndrome","CDSN","corneodesmosin deficiency","TGM5","deciduous skin"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by spontaneous, painless, continuous superficial peeling of the outer skin, often leaving no scarring","An acral form (limited to hands and feet) is caused by TGM5 mutations, while a generalised inflammatory form is caused by CDSN (corneodesmosin) mutations","The generalised inflammatory type is associated with erythroderma, pruritus and atopic features and can resemble Netherton syndrome","Peeling is superficial (within the stratum corneum/granular layer), distinguishing it from the deeper blistering of epidermolysis bullosa","The Nikolsky-like separation is at a more superficial plane than in immunobullous or staphylococcal scalded-skin disease","Autosomal recessive inheritance; onset is usually at or soon after birth or in early childhood","Management is supportive with emollients, gentle skin care and avoidance of friction; no curative therapy exists"],"pathophysiology":"Peeling skin syndrome comprises autosomal recessive disorders of the cornified layer characterised by loss of cohesion between corneocytes at a superficial epidermal plane. The localised acral form is caused by mutations in TGM5, which encodes transglutaminase 5, an enzyme that cross-links structural proteins of the cornified cell envelope; its deficiency weakens the envelope so that the stratum corneum detaches from the underlying epidermis. The generalised inflammatory form results from mutations in CDSN encoding corneodesmosin, an adhesive component of corneodesmosomes that holds corneocytes together; loss of corneodesmosin causes separation high in the epidermis with associated barrier failure, inflammation and allergic sensitisation. In both, the split occurs superficially, giving painless peeling without deep blistering.","additionalDifferentials":["Netherton syndrome","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex (superficial forms)","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Bullous congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma","Acral peeling from friction or exogenous causes","Exfoliative ichthyosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy demonstrating superficial (subcorneal/intragranular) separation","Molecular genetic testing of TGM5 and CDSN","Assessment for erythroderma, atopy and raised IgE in the generalised form","Examination of hair to help exclude Netherton syndrome","Microbiological swabs to exclude staphylococcal scalded-skin syndrome","Family history and pedigree analysis"],"redFlags":["Extensive erythroderma with barrier failure, fluid loss or infection in infancy","Failure to thrive in the generalised inflammatory form","Recurrent skin infection from a compromised barrier","Features suggesting Netherton syndrome requiring reappraisal"],"prognosis":"Peeling skin syndrome is a chronic condition, but the localised acral form is generally mild and compatible with normal health, while the generalised inflammatory form can cause more troublesome erythroderma, itch and barrier problems. It is not life-limiting in most cases, though symptoms persist and require ongoing supportive care.","pitfalls":["Confusing the generalised form with Netherton syndrome or atopic erythroderma","Interpreting superficial peeling as epidermolysis bullosa and over-investigating for deep blistering","Mistaking it for staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome and giving unnecessary treatment","Failing to distinguish the acral (TGM5) from the generalised (CDSN) form for counselling"],"management":["Protect the fragile stratum corneum with liberal emollients, gentle skin handling and avoidance of friction and trauma","Control pruritus and inflammation in the generalised form with topical anti-inflammatory agents and antihistamines, and repair the impaired barrier","Monitor for and treat secondary bacterial infection of denuded skin","Manage the associated atopic features and raised IgE, and attend to fluid and temperature regulation in extensive erythroderma","Confirm the diagnosis genetically to avoid mislabelling as epidermolysis bullosa or staphylococcal scalded-skin syndrome and unnecessary treatment","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance and dermatology-led long-term follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"peliosis_hepatis":{"name":"peliosis hepatis","system":"hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hepatic peliosis","peliosis of the liver"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Peliosis hepatis is a rare vascular disorder in which the liver contains multiple blood-filled cystic spaces due to dilatation of the hepatic sinusoids.","It is classically drug- or toxin-associated, with anabolic androgenic steroids, oestrogens and oral contraceptives, corticosteroids, tamoxifen and thiopurines such as azathioprine among the recognised culprits.","It also complicates chronic wasting states such as advanced malignancy, tuberculosis and malnutrition, as well as solid-organ transplantation.","Bacillary peliosis caused by Bartonella species in immunocompromised hosts is an important infective mimic to consider but is a separate entity.","Most cases are asymptomatic and found incidentally; when symptomatic there may be hepatomegaly, cholestatic or mixed enzyme derangement and portal hypertension.","The feared complication is spontaneous rupture of a peliotic cavity causing life-threatening intraperitoneal haemorrhage and haemodynamic collapse.","Imaging is non-specific and easily confused with metastases, abscess or haemangioma, and percutaneous biopsy carries a significant bleeding risk.","Withdrawal of the offending agent often leads to regression, and management is otherwise supportive, with embolisation or resection reserved for bleeding."],"pathophysiology":"Peliosis hepatis results from injury to the hepatic sinusoidal endothelium and the reticulin framework that supports the hepatocyte plates. Loss of this support allows the sinusoids to dilate into blood-filled lacunar spaces distributed randomly throughout the parenchyma. Two histological patterns are described: a parenchymal type in which the cavities lack an endothelial lining and communicate with disrupted hepatocytes, and a phlebectatic type in which endothelium-lined spaces are centred on dilated central veins. Hormonal, cytotoxic and immunosuppressive agents are thought to weaken the sinusoidal wall and impair outflow, while chronic infection or wasting may act through endothelial injury and altered hepatic blood flow. Because the cavities are thin-walled and engorged they are prone to rupture, and extensive involvement can compress adjacent parenchyma and impair hepatic function.","additionalDifferentials":["Hepatic metastases","Pyogenic liver abscess","Cavernous haemangioma","Hepatic adenoma or adenomatosis","Hepatocellular carcinoma","Multifocal simple cysts or biliary hamartomas","Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome or Budd-Chiari syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Multiphase contrast-enhanced CT of the liver","MRI with hepatobiliary contrast","Doppler ultrasound of the hepatic vasculature","Careful, often transjugular, liver biopsy where the diagnosis is uncertain","Full liver function panel and coagulation profile","Detailed drug and medication history including performance-enhancing agents","Screen for underlying malignancy or chronic infection"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe abdominal pain with hypotension suggesting cavity rupture and haemoperitoneum","Falling haemoglobin with haemodynamic instability","Progressive hepatic dysfunction or synthetic failure","New portal hypertension with variceal bleeding"],"prognosis":"Drug-associated disease frequently regresses once the causative agent is stopped, but spontaneous rupture or extensive parenchymal involvement carries a high mortality and may precipitate hepatic failure.","pitfalls":["Performing a routine percutaneous biopsy of a vascular lesion and provoking major haemorrhage.","Misdiagnosing the lesions as metastatic malignancy and subjecting the patient to unnecessary treatment.","Failing to take a thorough medication and anabolic-steroid history.","Overlooking the diagnosis as a cause of unexplained intra-abdominal haemorrhage."],"management":["Withdraw the causative drug or toxin such as anabolic androgenic steroids, oestrogens, tamoxifen, corticosteroids or thiopurines like azathioprine, as disease often regresses once the agent is stopped","Treat the underlying wasting or infective condition such as tuberculosis, malignancy or malnutrition, and manage bacillary peliosis from Bartonella in the immunocompromised with appropriate antimicrobials","Manage spontaneous intraperitoneal haemorrhage as an emergency with resuscitation, transfusion and correction of coagulopathy, achieving haemostasis by transarterial embolisation or surgery including hepatic resection","Monitor lesions with serial cross-sectional imaging to confirm regression after removal of the precipitant","Support hepatic dysfunction and portal hypertension where present","Refer to hepatology and interventional radiology, particularly when the diagnosis is uncertain or rupture is a risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","MacSween's Pathology of the Liver","Oxford Textbook of Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pelizaeus_merzbacher":{"name":"Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMD","PLP1-related disorder","hypomyelinating leukodystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked hypomyelinating leukodystrophy caused by abnormalities of the PLP1 gene, most often a duplication rather than a point mutation","Affected boys typically present in infancy with nystagmus and hypotonia, later developing spasticity, ataxia, and cognitive impairment","Pendular nystagmus in early infancy is a characteristic early clue and may precede other signs","The severity spans a spectrum, from severe connatal disease to the milder allelic disorder spastic paraplegia type 2","This is a hypomyelinating disorder, so MRI shows a deficiency of myelin formation rather than active demyelination","PLP1 duplications generally produce the classic phenotype, while null mutations and some point mutations cause distinct and sometimes milder or peripheral-nerve-involving pictures","Diagnosis rests on the clinical picture, MRI evidence of hypomyelination, and PLP1 dosage and sequence analysis","Female carriers are usually asymptomatic but can occasionally show mild signs, and genetic counselling is important for at-risk families"],"pathophysiology":"Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease results from abnormalities of PLP1, which encodes proteolipid protein, a major myelin protein of the central nervous system produced by oligodendrocytes. Duplication, point mutation, or deletion of PLP1 disrupts oligodendrocyte function and myelin assembly, leading to failure of normal myelination (hypomyelination) rather than breakdown of previously formed myelin. Overexpression from duplication is toxic to oligodendrocytes, accounting for the common severe phenotype, while different mutation types produce distinct clinical severities.","additionalDifferentials":["Other hypomyelinating leukodystrophies","Pelizaeus-Merzbacher-like disease from GJC2 mutations","Cerebral palsy (for early hypotonia and later spasticity)","Mitochondrial and other early-onset white matter disorders","Congenital nystagmus of ocular origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PLP1 dosage analysis to detect duplication and sequencing for point mutations","Brain MRI demonstrating a hypomyelinating pattern","Assessment of nystagmus and visual pathways","Developmental and neurological evaluation over time","Genetic counselling and carrier testing for female relatives"],"redFlags":["Severe connatal presentation with respiratory and feeding difficulty","Rapidly progressive spasticity limiting mobility and causing contractures","Seizures or airway compromise","Failure to establish any developmental milestones"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies widely with phenotype; the connatal form carries a poor prognosis, the classic form progresses more slowly with survival into adulthood in some, and the allelic spastic paraplegia type 2 is milder. Care is supportive and directed at spasticity, feeding, and complications.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing early disease as cerebral palsy and not pursuing genetic testing","Relying on sequencing alone and missing a PLP1 duplication that requires dosage analysis","Confusing hypomyelination with active demyelination on imaging","Not distinguishing it from the clinically similar GJC2-related disorder"],"management":["Manage spasticity with physiotherapy, antispasticity agents such as baclofen, botulinum toxin and orthoses to prevent contractures","Support feeding and nutrition, using gastrostomy where swallowing is unsafe, to counter failure to thrive","Provide respiratory care and treat recurrent aspiration and chest infections","Treat associated seizures and optimise posture, seating and mobility with occupational therapy","Coordinate multidisciplinary developmental, neurology and rehabilitation follow-up, as this hypomyelinating disorder has no disease-specific drug therapy","Offer genetic counselling for X-linked inheritance, with PLP1 carrier testing for female relatives and prenatal options"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"pelizaeus_merzbacher_like":{"name":"Pelizaeus-Merzbacher-like disease (GJC2)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMLD","PMLD1","GJC2-related hypomyelination","connexin 47 leukodystrophy","GJA12-related leukodystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive hypomyelinating leukodystrophy caused by variants in GJC2, which encodes the gap junction protein connexin 47","It closely resembles X-linked Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease (caused by PLP1) both clinically and radiologically, but follows autosomal recessive inheritance and can affect either sex","Presentation is usually in early infancy with nystagmus and hypotonia, evolving into spasticity, cerebellar ataxia, dysarthria and delayed or limited motor development","Some children never achieve independent walking, while others follow a milder course, reflecting the range of GJC2 variants","MRI shows diffuse hypomyelination with a persistently immature myelin pattern that does not progress normally with age","GJC2 testing is appropriate once PLP1-related Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease has been considered or excluded, particularly when inheritance appears recessive or a girl is affected","There is no specific treatment, so care is supportive and multidisciplinary, with management of spasticity, physiotherapy, communication support and nutrition"],"pathophysiology":"Pelizaeus-Merzbacher-like disease is caused by biallelic variants in GJC2, encoding connexin 47, a gap junction protein expressed by oligodendrocytes. Connexin 47 forms channels between oligodendrocytes and between oligodendrocytes and astrocytes that are essential for ionic and metabolic coupling in the white matter. Loss of functional connexin 47 disrupts this glial network, impairing the ability of oligodendrocytes to establish and maintain normal myelin. The result is a hypomyelinating leukodystrophy that phenotypically overlaps with the more common PLP1-related disease, in which a different oligodendrocyte protein is affected.","additionalDifferentials":["Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease (PLP1-related, X-linked)","POLR3-related (4H) leukodystrophy","TUBB4A-related hypomyelinating leukodystrophy and H-ABC","Hypomyelination with congenital cataract (FAM126A)","AIMP1-related and other genetic hypomyelinating leukodystrophies","Fucosidosis and storage disorders presenting with hypomyelination"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to demonstrate diffuse hypomyelination and to assess its pattern and stability over time","PLP1 analysis (including dosage) to address the main differential before or alongside GJC2 testing","GJC2 sequencing, with a hypomyelinating leukodystrophy panel or exome if negative","Visual evoked potentials and ophthalmology review given the early nystagmus","Nerve conduction studies to help distinguish central from peripheral involvement","Developmental and physiotherapy assessment to plan supportive care"],"redFlags":["Failure to establish head control or feeding difficulty in a severely affected infant","Progressive respiratory compromise from bulbar and truncal weakness","Escalating spasticity or dystonia causing pain and contractures","Unexpected rapid regression prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The prognosis of Pelizaeus-Merzbacher-like disease spans a spectrum: severely affected children may never walk and have significant lifelong disability, whereas milder cases achieve more motor skills and a slower course. As with PLP1-related disease, many patients survive into adulthood with supportive care, and outcome depends largely on the severity of motor and bulbar involvement.","pitfalls":["Testing only PLP1 and stopping when it is negative, thereby missing GJC2-related disease","Discounting the diagnosis in an affected girl because Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease is X-linked","Mistaking hypomyelination for delayed myelination without appropriate follow-up imaging","Attributing the picture to hypoxic-ischaemic injury or cerebral palsy","Not offering autosomal recessive genetic counselling to the family"],"management":["Manage spasticity with physiotherapy, antispasticity medication such as baclofen, botulinum toxin and orthoses","Support feeding and nutrition and treat the consequences of dysarthria and dysphagia, using gastrostomy when needed","Provide respiratory care and manage recurrent aspiration","Deliver physiotherapy and occupational therapy for the cerebellar ataxia and delayed motor development, with mobility and communication aids","Coordinate multidisciplinary neurology and rehabilitation follow-up, as no disease-specific therapy exists for this hypomyelinating leukodystrophy","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance, noting either sex may be affected, with carrier and prenatal testing for families"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"pellagra":{"name":"Pellagra","system":"Nutrition / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["niacin deficiency","vitamin B3 deficiency","nicotinic acid deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO nutrition guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Remembered by the triad of dermatitis, diarrhoea, and dementia, with death in untreated advanced disease","The rash is a symmetrical, photosensitive, pigmented dermatitis on sun-exposed skin, classically a collar-like band over the neck (Casal's necklace)","It occurs where maize or sorghum is the dietary staple because their niacin is poorly bioavailable and they are low in tryptophan","Chronic alcohol use, malabsorption, carcinoid syndrome, and isoniazid therapy are recognised precipitants","Neuropsychiatric features range from irritability and depression to confusion and frank dementia","A glossitis, stomatitis, and diarrhoea reflect involvement of the whole gastrointestinal mucosa","Clinical improvement after niacin repletion is rapid and helps confirm the diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Niacin, obtained from the diet or synthesised from tryptophan, is the precursor of the coenzymes NAD and NADP that are central to cellular energy metabolism and redox reactions. Deficiency impairs energy-dependent repair and turnover in tissues with high metabolic demand, explaining the prominence of skin, gut mucosa, and nervous system involvement. Diets based on untreated maize provide niacin in a bound, poorly absorbable form and little tryptophan; conditions that divert tryptophan, such as carcinoid tumours, or that impair absorption or metabolism, such as alcoholism and certain drugs, produce secondary deficiency.","additionalDifferentials":["Photosensitive dermatoses such as porphyria","Other B-vitamin deficiencies with overlapping mucocutaneous signs","Contact or phototoxic dermatitis","Dementia and confusional states from other causes","Carcinoid syndrome as an underlying cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dietary and drug history focusing on maize-based diets, alcohol, and isoniazid","Urinary niacin metabolites where available","Assessment for malabsorption and coexisting B-vitamin deficiencies","Investigation for carcinoid syndrome when suggested clinically","Skin examination and, if uncertain, biopsy to characterise the dermatitis"],"redFlags":["Progressive confusion or dementia indicating advanced central nervous system involvement","Severe diarrhoea with dehydration and electrolyte disturbance","Extensive dermatitis with secondary infection","Rapid deterioration in a severely malnourished patient"],"prognosis":"Prompt niacin repletion reverses skin, gastrointestinal, and early neuropsychiatric features quickly; advanced neurological disease may recover incompletely and untreated pellagra can be fatal.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the rash as a primary photodermatosis and missing the nutritional cause","Overlooking pellagra in patients on isoniazid or with carcinoid syndrome","Failing to treat coexisting deficiencies in the malnourished or alcohol-dependent","Attributing the neuropsychiatric picture solely to dementia or delirium of other cause"],"management":["Replace niacin with oral nicotinamide, which corrects the deficiency without the flushing of nicotinic acid, using the parenteral route in severe malabsorption","Correct the underlying diet by diversifying away from untreated maize or sorghum and adding tryptophan-rich protein","Treat coexisting B-group vitamin deficiencies commonly present in malnourished or alcohol-dependent patients","Address the precipitating cause such as alcohol misuse, malabsorption, carcinoid syndrome or isoniazid therapy, co-prescribing pyridoxine where isoniazid is continued","Give supportive skin care with photoprotection for the photosensitive dermatitis and manage the diarrhoea and glossitis","Monitor the rapid response of skin, gut and early neuropsychiatric features, recognising advanced neurological disease may recover incompletely"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO nutrition guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"pellucid_marginal_degeneration":{"name":"Pellucid marginal degeneration","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMD","inferior corneal thinning disorder","peripheral corneal ectasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology / Cornea by Krachmer","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A bilateral, non-inflammatory ectatic disorder featuring a band of inferior peripheral corneal thinning, typically between the 4 and 8 o'clock positions","The cornea protrudes above the band of thinning, so the thinnest zone lies inferior to the point of maximal protrusion","Produces characteristic high against-the-rule astigmatism, often with a claw- or crab-claw-shaped pattern on corneal topography (kissing doves / butterfly)","The epithelium is intact and the eye is white and painless, distinguishing it from ulcerative peripheral thinning","Vision loss is due to irregular astigmatism rather than central scarring, and often presents in the third to fifth decade","It shares features with keratoconus but the thinning is inferior and peripheral rather than central","Management begins with spectacles and specialty contact lenses, with corneal cross-linking, intrastromal ring segments, or large/eccentric grafts for advanced disease","Acute hydrops from rupture of Descemet membrane can occur, causing sudden oedema and visual loss"],"pathophysiology":"Pellucid marginal degeneration is a non-inflammatory ectatic disorder characterised by a crescentic band of stromal thinning in the inferior peripheral cornea. Progressive weakening and thinning of the stroma allow the cornea just above the thinned band to protrude forward. This altered geometry flattens the vertical meridian centrally and produces high against-the-rule and irregular astigmatism, giving the typical crab-claw topographic appearance. The epithelium remains intact and the process is free of inflammation, but severe thinning and biomechanical weakness can lead to acute hydrops when Descemet membrane ruptures, causing sudden stromal oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Keratoconus","Keratoglobus","Terrien marginal degeneration","Post-refractive-surgery ectasia","Contact-lens-induced corneal warpage","Against-the-rule astigmatism from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Corneal topography and tomography demonstrating the crab-claw pattern and inferior thinning","Pachymetry mapping to localise the band of thinning","Slit-lamp examination confirming intact epithelium and absence of inflammation","Anterior segment optical coherence tomography of the thinned zone","Refraction and keratometry to quantify against-the-rule astigmatism","Serial imaging to monitor progression and guide cross-linking decisions"],"redFlags":["Sudden pain, redness, and marked visual loss suggesting acute hydrops","Rapid progression of astigmatism indicating active ectasia","Extreme thinning raising the risk of perforation","Atypical inflammation or epithelial defect suggesting an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Progression is usually slow, with vision limited by irregular astigmatism that can often be managed optically for years. Advanced disease may need surgical intervention, and grafting is technically challenging because the thinning is peripheral; acute hydrops usually resolves but can leave scarring.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling it as keratoconus and missing the inferior peripheral pattern of thinning","Attempting standard central grafting without accounting for peripheral thinning","Overlooking acute hydrops as the cause of sudden visual loss","Fitting standard contact lenses that fail to correct the irregular astigmatism"],"management":["Correct the irregular against-the-rule astigmatism optically, starting with spectacles and progressing to rigid gas-permeable or scleral contact lenses","Halt progression with corneal collagen cross-linking in documented progressive disease","Consider intrastromal corneal ring segments to regularise the cornea in contact-lens-intolerant eyes","Reserve keratoplasty, which is technically demanding given the peripheral thinning, for advanced disease with scarring or intolerance of other options","Manage acute hydrops conservatively with hypertonic saline, cycloplegia and lubrication, awaiting resorption of the stromal oedema","Advise against eye rubbing and monitor progression with serial corneal topography and tomography"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Cornea by Krachmer, Mannis and Holland"],"crossLinks":[]},"pelvic_congestion_syndrome":{"name":"Pelvic congestion syndrome","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pelvic venous congestion","pelvic venous insufficiency","ovarian vein reflux","PCS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology reference textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically affects multiparous premenopausal women who describe a dull, aching chronic pelvic pain lasting more than six months","The pain characteristically worsens towards the end of the day, on prolonged standing, and after intercourse, and eases on lying down","Deep dyspareunia and a post-coital ache are common, and varicosities may be visible over the vulva, buttocks, or upper thighs","Dilated, tortuous, incompetent ovarian and pelvic veins underlie the syndrome and can be demonstrated on Doppler imaging","It is a diagnosis of exclusion; other causes of chronic pelvic pain must be considered before symptoms are attributed to venous congestion","Ovarian and pelvic vein embolisation is the mainstay of definitive treatment, offering symptom relief in a majority of appropriately selected women","Secondary causes of pelvic venous obstruction, such as left renal vein or iliac vein compression, should be sought before treatment"],"pathophysiology":"The syndrome results from incompetence and dilatation of the ovarian and internal iliac venous tributaries. Failure of the venous valves permits retrograde flow and pooling of blood within the pelvic veins, producing chronic venous congestion. Oestrogen has a venodilatory effect and, together with the increased pelvic venous capacity acquired during pregnancy, contributes to the tendency of the condition to occur in premenopausal parous women. The engorged veins and associated stasis are thought to generate the characteristic positional pelvic pain.","additionalDifferentials":["Endometriosis","Chronic pelvic inflammatory disease and adhesions","Irritable bowel syndrome","Interstitial cystitis or bladder pain syndrome","Musculoskeletal or myofascial pelvic pain","Ovarian or other pelvic mass"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound with Doppler to identify dilated pelvic veins and demonstrate reflux","Transabdominal and transvaginal duplex assessment of ovarian vein diameter and flow direction","Magnetic resonance venography or CT venography to map the pelvic venous anatomy","Catheter venography, regarded as the reference standard, which also allows treatment at the same sitting","Investigations to exclude other pelvic pathology, including laparoscopy where indicated"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting an underlying secondary obstruction such as nutcracker or May-Thurner physiology","A pelvic or renal mass causing venous compression","Haematuria or flank pain pointing to left renal vein compression","New or atypical symptoms that raise concern for malignancy"],"prognosis":"Many women obtain durable relief of pain following ovarian vein embolisation, although symptoms may persist or recur, particularly if an underlying obstructive cause has not been addressed.","pitfalls":["Overdiagnosis, since pelvic varices are common and frequently asymptomatic incidental findings","Failing to exclude a secondary obstructive cause such as renal or iliac vein compression","Attributing all chronic pelvic pain to congestion without excluding coexisting pathology","Offering intervention without correlating imaging findings with the clinical picture"],"management":["Offer ovarian and pelvic vein embolisation as the definitive treatment, occluding the incompetent refluxing veins","Trial hormonal venoconstriction with progestogens such as medroxyprogesterone or a GnRH analogue to reduce venous congestion and pain","Provide analgesia for the chronic positional pelvic pain","Exclude other causes of chronic pelvic pain before attributing symptoms to venous congestion, using laparoscopy where indicated","Identify and treat an underlying obstructive cause such as nutcracker or May-Thurner compression with venous stenting to prevent persistence or recurrence","Refer to interventional radiology, reserving surgical vein ligation or hysterectomy for selected refractory cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference textbook","Peer-reviewed gynaecology clinical reviews","Interventional radiology reference text"],"crossLinks":[]},"pelvic_inflammatory_disease":{"name":"Pelvic inflammatory disease","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PID","salpingitis","pelvic infection","endometritis-salpingitis-oophoritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["PID is infection and inflammation of the upper female genital tract, encompassing endometritis, salpingitis, oophoritis, tubo-ovarian abscess, and pelvic peritonitis","It is usually an ascending infection, most commonly caused by Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae, often with anaerobes and other vaginal flora","The clinical picture is variable and often subtle; classic findings are lower abdominal pain, deep dyspareunia, abnormal discharge, and cervical motion, uterine, or adnexal tenderness","Because sequelae are serious and diagnosis is imprecise, a low threshold for empirical treatment in at-risk women is recommended","Antibiotic regimens are broad-spectrum to cover gonococcus, chlamydia, and anaerobes; sexual partners should be evaluated and treated to prevent reinfection","Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome (perihepatitis) causes right upper quadrant pain and can mimic hepatobiliary disease","Intrauterine device users, recent instrumentation, and multiple sexual partners increase risk","Long-term consequences include tubal-factor infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain, and risk rises with each recurrent episode"],"pathophysiology":"PID arises when micro-organisms ascend from the vagina and cervix through the endometrial cavity to the fallopian tubes and adjacent structures. Sexually transmitted pathogens, chiefly Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae, disrupt the cervical barrier and facilitate co-infection with endogenous aerobic and anaerobic vaginal flora. The resulting inflammation damages the tubal epithelium and cilia, leading to scarring, adhesions, and tubal occlusion. Progression can produce pyosalpinx, tubo-ovarian abscess, and pelvic peritonitis; spread to the hepatic capsule causes perihepatitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Ectopic pregnancy","Acute appendicitis","Ovarian cyst accident (rupture, haemorrhage, or torsion)","Endometriosis","Urinary tract infection","Functional or irritable bowel-related pelvic pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pregnancy test to exclude ectopic pregnancy","Endocervical or vaginal nucleic acid amplification testing for chlamydia and gonorrhoea","Microscopy and culture of genital tract samples","Inflammatory markers and full blood count","Pelvic ultrasound to detect tubo-ovarian abscess or pelvic collection","Laparoscopy when the diagnosis is uncertain or the patient fails to respond"],"redFlags":["Signs of a tubo-ovarian abscess such as a tender adnexal mass with high fever","Peritonitis or suspicion of a ruptured abscess causing sepsis","Haemodynamic instability or systemic sepsis","Inability to tolerate oral therapy, pregnancy, or failure of outpatient treatment requiring admission","Severe right upper quadrant pain raising concern for perihepatitis or an alternative surgical diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Prompt, adequate antibiotic treatment usually resolves acute infection, but even treated PID carries appreciable risk of long-term tubal damage. Each recurrent episode further increases the likelihood of infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain, so prevention and partner treatment are central to a good outcome.","pitfalls":["Withholding treatment while awaiting definitive confirmation, allowing tubal damage to progress","Missing an ectopic pregnancy by not performing a pregnancy test","Neglecting to test for and treat sexual partners, leading to reinfection","Failing to recognise or drain a tubo-ovarian abscess","Assuming an absence of fever or normal inflammatory markers excludes PID"],"management":["Start empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics covering gonococcus, chlamydia and anaerobes, typically ceftriaxone with doxycycline and metronidazole, with a low threshold for treatment given imprecise diagnosis and serious sequelae","Admit for intravenous therapy and consider drainage for severe disease, tubo-ovarian abscess, pregnancy or failure of oral treatment","Provide analgesia and advise rest, reassessing response and considering removal of an intrauterine device if there is no improvement","Notify, evaluate and treat sexual partners and advise abstinence until treatment is completed to prevent reinfection","Screen for coexisting sexually transmitted infections including HIV and offer contraception and safer-sex counselling","Exclude ectopic pregnancy and, where the diagnosis is uncertain or the patient fails to respond, consider laparoscopy","Counsel on the long-term risks of tubal infertility, ectopic pregnancy and chronic pelvic pain and arrange follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pemphigoid_gestationis":{"name":"Pemphigoid gestationis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["herpes gestationis","gestational pemphigoid","pregnancy-associated pemphigoid","anti-BP180 pemphigoid of pregnancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autoimmune subepidermal blistering disease of pregnancy and the peripartum period, unrelated to herpes virus despite the historical name 'herpes gestationis'","Typically begins in the second or third trimester with intensely pruritic urticarial plaques that often start around the umbilicus before spreading and evolving into tense blisters","The target antigen is BP180 (collagen XVII), the same hemidesmosomal antigen implicated in bullous pemphigoid","Direct immunofluorescence characteristically shows linear C3 (with or without IgG) along the basement membrane zone","Transplacental antibody transfer can cause transient, usually mild and self-limiting, blistering in the neonate","Associated with a modestly increased risk of prematurity and small-for-gestational-age infants, warranting obstetric surveillance","Flares are common at delivery, and the disease may recur in subsequent pregnancies, often earlier and more severely, and with the combined oral contraceptive pill or menstruation"],"pathophysiology":"Pemphigoid gestationis is driven by IgG autoantibodies against BP180 (collagen XVII), a transmembrane component of the hemidesmosome at the dermo-epidermal junction. Aberrant expression of placental/amniotic basement-membrane antigens is thought to break self-tolerance, generating antibodies that cross-react with maternal skin. Antibody binding activates complement, recruiting eosinophils and other inflammatory cells that cleave the epidermis from the dermis to form tense subepidermal blisters. Because IgG crosses the placenta, the neonate may develop transient blistering that resolves as maternal antibody clears. Hormonal influences help explain peripartum flares and recurrence with oestrogen exposure.","additionalDifferentials":["Polymorphic eruption of pregnancy (PUPPP)","Bullous pemphigoid","Atopic eruption of pregnancy","Allergic or irritant contact dermatitis","Drug eruption","Linear IgA bullous dermatosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Perilesional biopsy for direct immunofluorescence showing linear C3 at the basement membrane zone","Lesional histology demonstrating a subepidermal blister with eosinophils","Serum indirect immunofluorescence and ELISA for anti-BP180 antibodies","Complement-added indirect immunofluorescence to increase sensitivity","Obstetric assessment including fetal growth monitoring","Neonatal examination for transient blistering after delivery"],"redFlags":["Fetal growth restriction or signs of placental insufficiency requiring obstetric intervention","Extensive maternal blistering with erosions and secondary infection","Neonatal blistering or feeding difficulty after birth","Severe flare at delivery needing escalation of therapy"],"prognosis":"Pemphigoid gestationis usually resolves spontaneously in the weeks to months after delivery, though flares at parturition are common; there is a modest increase in prematurity and low birthweight, and recurrence in later pregnancies is frequent.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early urticarial disease for polymorphic eruption of pregnancy and delaying diagnosis","Assuming an infectious cause because of the outdated name 'herpes gestationis'","Neglecting fetal monitoring for growth restriction and prematurity","Failing to counsel about recurrence in future pregnancies and with oestrogen-containing contraception"],"management":["Treat limited disease with potent topical corticosteroids and emollients, adding oral antihistamines for pruritus","Control widespread blistering with systemic corticosteroids such as prednisolone, tapering to the lowest effective dose","For refractory or persistent postpartum disease, escalate to steroid-sparing agents such as azathioprine or ciclosporin, or to intravenous immunoglobulin, plasma exchange or rituximab","Provide obstetric surveillance with foetal growth monitoring given the increased risk of prematurity and small-for-gestational-age infants","Examine the neonate for transient blistering from transplacental antibody, which resolves as maternal antibody clears","Avoid oestrogen-containing contraception postpartum, which may provoke a flare, and counsel on frequent recurrence in future pregnancies","Coordinate care between dermatology and obstetrics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pemphigus_erythematosus":{"name":"Pemphigus erythematosus","system":"Skin","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Senear-Usher syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A localised variant of pemphigus foliaceus that shows clinical and immunological overlap with lupus erythematosus; also known as Senear-Usher syndrome.","Presents with erythematous, scaly, crusted plaques and flaccid superficial blisters in a seborrhoeic and photodistributed pattern — malar 'butterfly' face, scalp and upper trunk.","The Nikolsky sign is typically positive, while mucosal involvement is characteristically absent (unlike pemphigus vulgaris).","Autoantibodies target desmoglein 1, producing superficial acantholysis in the granular/subcorneal layer identical to pemphigus foliaceus.","Direct immunofluorescence shows the hallmark dual pattern: intercellular ('chicken-wire') IgG and C3 within the epidermis PLUS granular IgG/C3 at the basement membrane zone.","Antinuclear antibodies are frequently positive, yet most patients do not fulfil criteria for true systemic lupus erythematosus.","Sunlight and thiol/sulfhydryl drugs (for example penicillamine or captopril) can precipitate or aggravate disease, so photoprotection matters.","Course is generally more benign and localised than pemphigus vulgaris, but it may occasionally evolve into generalised pemphigus foliaceus."],"pathophysiology":"Pemphigus erythematosus is an autoimmune intraepidermal blistering disorder in which IgG autoantibodies are directed against desmoglein 1, a desmosomal cadherin expressed most abundantly in the superficial (granular) layer of the epidermis. Antibody binding disrupts desmosomal adhesion between keratinocytes, causing acantholysis high in the epidermis and the resulting fragile, superficial blisters and scaly crusted erosions. What distinguishes the entity from ordinary pemphigus foliaceus is a concurrent lupus-like immunological signature, including positive antinuclear antibodies and granular deposition of immunoglobulin and complement along the dermo-epidermal junction (a 'lupus band'), so it is regarded as an overlap of pemphigus foliaceus and cutaneous lupus erythematosus. Because desmoglein 1 predominates superficially and mucosae are protected by compensatory desmoglein 3, blistering is superficial and mucosal surfaces are typically spared. Ultraviolet light and thiol-containing or other implicated drugs can trigger or exacerbate the autoimmune response, and lesions favour sun-exposed, seborrhoeic sites.","additionalDifferentials":["Pemphigus foliaceus","Discoid and systemic lupus erythematosus","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus","Impetigo (including bullous impetigo)","Paraneoplastic pemphigus","Rosacea or dermatomyositis (facial erythema mimics)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lesional skin biopsy showing superficial acantholysis in the granular layer","Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin demonstrating the combined intercellular and basement-membrane pattern","Indirect immunofluorescence for circulating intercellular (anti-desmoglein) antibodies","ELISA for anti-desmoglein 1 antibodies","Antinuclear antibodies with extended lupus serology (anti-dsDNA, extractable nuclear antigens) and complement","Careful drug history for thiol/sulfhydryl-containing agents","Assessment for systemic lupus features (renal and haematological screening)"],"redFlags":["Rapid extension to widespread erosions with skin-barrier failure and associated fluid and thermoregulatory compromise","Secondary bacterial infection or sepsis of eroded skin","Emergence of systemic lupus features such as renal impairment, cytopenias or serositis","Transition to generalised pemphigus foliaceus"],"prognosis":"Pemphigus erythematosus usually follows a chronic but comparatively benign and localised course, and generally responds to treatment. Relapses are common, and a minority progress to more generalised pemphigus foliaceus.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the eruption as seborrhoeic dermatitis or lupus and delaying diagnostic immunofluorescence.","Failing to recognise the combined intercellular plus basement-membrane immunofluorescence pattern that defines the entity.","Interpreting antinuclear antibody positivity as proof of systemic lupus erythematosus.","Overlooking a drug trigger, particularly thiol/sulfhydryl agents.","Neglecting photoprotection advice, allowing ultraviolet-driven flares."],"management":["Induce remission with systemic corticosteroids as the mainstay for active disease","Add steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as azathioprine or mycophenolate mofetil to reduce cumulative corticosteroid exposure","Consider rituximab B-cell depletion for widespread or treatment-refractory disease","Treat localised plaques with potent topical corticosteroids and enforce strict photoprotection given the photodistributed, lupus-overlap pattern","Review the drug history and withdraw any thiol- or sulfhydryl-containing agents that may trigger the disease","Monitor disease activity with anti-desmoglein-1 antibody titres and watch for progression to generalised pemphigus foliaceus","Screen for and manage complications of immunosuppression including infection, osteoporosis and hyperglycaemia","Refer to dermatology for co-ordinated care with rheumatology input given the lupus serological overlap"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pemphigus_foliaceus":{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["superficial pemphigus","anti-desmoglein-1 pemphigus","fogo selvagem (endemic pemphigus foliaceus)","pemphigus erythematosus (Senear-Usher syndrome)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A superficial autoimmune blistering disease driven by autoantibodies to desmoglein-1, producing a subcorneal (high epidermal) cleavage plane","Intact bullae are rarely seen; instead there are crusted, scaly erosions on a seborrhoeic distribution (scalp, face, upper trunk)","Mucous membranes are characteristically spared, distinguishing it from pemphigus vulgaris where desmoglein-3 involvement causes mucosal disease","Nikolsky sign is positive, reflecting acantholysis and loss of keratinocyte cohesion in the upper epidermis","Direct immunofluorescence shows intercellular ('chicken-wire') IgG and C3 deposition, often most intense in the superficial epidermis","An endemic form (fogo selvagem) occurs in parts of Brazil and is linked to environmental and insect-bite triggers","Drug-induced cases, classically with thiol-containing agents, should be considered and the culprit withdrawn"],"pathophysiology":"Pemphigus foliaceus results from IgG autoantibodies directed against desmoglein-1, a desmosomal cadherin concentrated in the superficial (granular) layer of the epidermis. Antibody binding disrupts desmosomal adhesion between keratinocytes, causing acantholysis and a subcorneal split high in the epidermis. Because desmoglein-1 predominates in the skin rather than mucosa and mucosal desmoglein-3 compensates there, blistering is cutaneous and superficial with mucosal sparing. The fragile superficial roof breaks readily, so patients present with scale-crust and erosions rather than tense blisters. Genetic susceptibility, and in the endemic form environmental exposures, contribute to loss of tolerance.","additionalDifferentials":["Pemphigus vulgaris","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus","Impetigo and staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Subcorneal pustular dermatosis","Widespread eczema or psoriasis with crusting"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lesional skin biopsy for histology showing subcorneal/superficial acantholysis","Perilesional biopsy for direct immunofluorescence demonstrating intercellular IgG and C3","Indirect immunofluorescence on serum for circulating intercellular antibodies","Serum ELISA for anti-desmoglein-1 (and anti-desmoglein-3 to help distinguish subtype)","Drug history review to identify a possible drug-induced trigger","Baseline investigations before immunosuppression (infection screen, glucose, bone health)"],"redFlags":["Extensive erosions with fluid loss, secondary infection, or sepsis in erythrodermic disease","Rapid progression or failure to respond to first-line therapy","Complications of prolonged systemic corticosteroid or immunosuppressive treatment","New drug exposure temporally linked to disease onset requiring urgent withdrawal"],"prognosis":"Pemphigus foliaceus is generally less severe than pemphigus vulgaris because it is superficial and spares mucosae, and many patients achieve durable remission with corticosteroids and steroid-sparing immunosuppression or rituximab; relapses and treatment-related morbidity remain important concerns.","pitfalls":["Mistaking crusted superficial lesions for seborrhoeic dermatitis or impetigo and delaying diagnosis","Relying on histology alone without direct immunofluorescence to confirm the diagnosis","Overlooking a drug trigger that could resolve with withdrawal","Assuming mucosal involvement excludes pemphigus foliaceus rather than reconsidering pemphigus vulgaris"],"management":["Induce remission with systemic corticosteroids for active or widespread disease","Use rituximab as first-line steroid-sparing therapy to achieve durable remission","Add conventional immunosuppression such as azathioprine or mycophenolate mofetil where rituximab is unsuitable","Treat limited disease with potent topical corticosteroids","Review the drug history and withdraw any implicated drug-induced trigger","Provide skin care of crusted erosions and treat secondary bacterial infection with appropriate antibiotics","Monitor with anti-desmoglein-1 antibody titres and screen for immunosuppression-related infection, hyperglycaemia and bone loss","Refer to dermatology for specialist-led immunosuppressive management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pemphigus_vegetans":{"name":"pemphigus vegetans","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pemphigus vegetans of Neumann","pemphigus vegetans of Hallopeau"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare vegetating variant of pemphigus vulgaris characterised by hypertrophic, verrucous, exudative plaques favouring intertriginous and periorificial sites.","Two forms are described: the Neumann type, which begins with flaccid bullae and erosions that evolve into vegetations, and the milder Hallopeau type, which starts with pustules.","Flexural predilection includes the axillae, groin, inframammary folds, perianal region and the angles of the mouth; the scalp and face may also be affected.","Oral mucosal involvement is common and may include cerebriform changes of the tongue, mirroring the mucosal disease of pemphigus vulgaris.","Histology shows suprabasal acantholysis with pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia and characteristic intraepidermal eosinophilic microabscesses.","Direct immunofluorescence demonstrates intercellular IgG and C3 in a net-like (chicken-wire) pattern, and serum antibodies target desmoglein 3 (and often desmoglein 1).","The Hallopeau type generally follows a more indolent course and may respond to lower doses of systemic therapy than classical pemphigus vulgaris.","Systemic corticosteroids with steroid-sparing immunosuppressants remain the mainstay, with rituximab increasingly used for refractory or steroid-dependent disease."],"pathophysiology":"Pemphigus vegetans is a clinical and histological variant of pemphigus vulgaris driven by IgG autoantibodies against the desmosomal cadherins desmoglein 3 and, in many cases, desmoglein 1. Antibody binding disrupts desmosomal adhesion between keratinocytes, producing suprabasal acantholysis and flaccid blistering. At intertriginous and periorificial sites, the warm moist environment and chronic irritation are thought to provoke an exuberant reparative epidermal response, giving rise to pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia and the characteristic vegetating plaques studded with eosinophilic microabscesses. The reason certain patients develop vegetating rather than classical lesions is incompletely understood but may reflect host reactive patterns and local factors.","additionalDifferentials":["pemphigus vulgaris (classical)","Hailey-Hailey disease (benign familial pemphigus)","condylomata lata of secondary syphilis","granular parakeratosis and intertrigo with candidiasis","halogenoderma (bromoderma / iododerma)","pyoderma vegetans / blastomycosis-like pyoderma","inverse psoriasis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["lesional biopsy for histopathology (suprabasal acantholysis, eosinophilic microabscesses, pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia)","perilesional biopsy for direct immunofluorescence (intercellular IgG and C3)","indirect immunofluorescence for circulating intercellular antibodies","ELISA for desmoglein 1 and desmoglein 3 antibodies","swabs and culture to exclude secondary bacterial or candidal infection","syphilis serology when perianal or genital vegetations raise suspicion of condylomata lata","baseline screening prior to immunosuppression (glucose, blood pressure, infection screen)"],"redFlags":["extensive erosions with fluid loss, secondary infection or sepsis","severe oropharyngeal involvement limiting oral intake and nutrition","rapid progression or transformation to widespread classical pemphigus vulgaris","signs of systemic infection arising from immunosuppressive therapy"],"prognosis":"With appropriate immunosuppression the disease is controllable, and the Hallopeau type in particular may follow a relatively benign, indolent course; however relapses are frequent and the Neumann type can be chronic and difficult to clear.","pitfalls":["Mistaking flexural vegetations for intertrigo, candidiasis or condylomata lata and delaying biopsy.","Biopsying only the vegetating surface and missing diagnostic acantholysis at the edge.","Overlooking oral disease that may be the earliest and most persistent manifestation.","Attributing treatment failure to disease when secondary bacterial or candidal colonisation is driving the plaques.","Assuming the milder Hallopeau course applies to all patients and under-treating Neumann-type disease."],"management":["Induce remission with systemic corticosteroids as first-line therapy","Add steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as azathioprine or mycophenolate mofetil, or rituximab, for durable control","Manage vegetating flexural plaques with topical corticosteroids and antiseptic measures","Treat secondary bacterial or candidal superinfection of macerated intertriginous lesions","Provide oral care, analgesia and dietary modification for painful mucosal erosions","Monitor anti-desmoglein antibody titres and surveil for complications of immunosuppression","Refer to dermatology, with oral medicine input for mucosal disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine, current edition","Bolognia Dermatology, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"pemphigus_vulgaris":{"name":"Pemphigus vulgaris","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pemphigus","PV"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A potentially life-threatening autoimmune blistering disease that most often begins with painful, non-healing oral erosions before skin involvement appears","Blisters are flaccid and fragile because the split is within the epidermis, so intact bullae are rarely seen and the picture is dominated by raw erosions","Gentle lateral pressure on skin can extend a blister or shear the epidermis (positive Nikolsky sign), reflecting loss of keratinocyte adhesion","Any patient with persistent unexplained mucosal erosions, particularly in the mouth, should raise suspicion for pemphigus","Diagnosis rests on skin biopsy showing suprabasal acantholysis and direct immunofluorescence demonstrating intercellular IgG deposition in a fishnet pattern","Circulating antibodies to desmoglein 3 (with or without desmoglein 1) can be detected serologically and correlate with disease activity","Management relies on systemic immunosuppression, typically corticosteroids with steroid-sparing immunomodulators or B-cell-depleting therapy for extensive disease","Meticulous wound care, pain control, nutritional support, and vigilance for infection are essential given widespread skin barrier loss"],"pathophysiology":"Pemphigus vulgaris is an autoimmune disease in which IgG autoantibodies target desmoglein 3 (and desmoglein 1 in mucocutaneous disease), transmembrane desmosomal cadherins that mediate keratinocyte adhesion. Antibody binding disrupts desmosomal function and triggers intracellular signalling that causes loss of cell-to-cell adhesion (acantholysis). This produces a suprabasal split within the epidermis, giving rise to fragile flaccid blisters and erosions. Because desmoglein 3 is expressed strongly in mucosa, mucosal disease often predominates; involvement of both desmogleins produces combined mucosal and cutaneous lesions. Extensive erosions lead to fluid loss, protein depletion, and susceptibility to secondary infection and sepsis.","additionalDifferentials":["Bullous pemphigoid (tense blisters, subepidermal split, older patients)","Mucous membrane (cicatricial) pemphigoid","Paraneoplastic pemphigus","Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis","Aphthous stomatitis or herpetic gingivostomatitis for oral-only disease","Erosive lichen planus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin or mucosal biopsy for histology showing suprabasal acantholysis","Perilesional biopsy for direct immunofluorescence showing intercellular IgG and C3","Serum indirect immunofluorescence and desmoglein antibody assays to support diagnosis and monitor activity","Screening for underlying malignancy if paraneoplastic pemphigus is suspected","Baseline and monitoring investigations before and during immunosuppressive therapy, including infection screening"],"redFlags":["Extensive skin and mucosal erosion with fluid and protein loss and risk of sepsis","Inability to eat or drink due to painful oropharyngeal involvement","Signs of secondary bacterial infection or systemic instability","Features suggesting paraneoplastic pemphigus, which mandates malignancy evaluation"],"prognosis":"Once frequently fatal, pemphigus vulgaris now has a much better outlook with immunosuppressive and B-cell-depleting therapy; however, it remains a serious chronic disease and much of the residual mortality relates to treatment complications and infection.","pitfalls":["Dismissing chronic oral erosions and delaying diagnosis until skin disease is widespread","Confusing it with bullous pemphigoid and misjudging severity and treatment intensity","Under-appreciating infection risk from extensive barrier loss and immunosuppression","Tapering immunosuppression too rapidly and precipitating relapse"],"management":["Induce remission with systemic corticosteroids combined with rituximab as first-line therapy","Use adjuvant immunosuppression such as azathioprine or mycophenolate mofetil to spare corticosteroids","Provide intravenous immunoglobulin or immunoadsorption for severe or refractory disease","Give supportive wound care with fluid and protein replacement for extensive erosions and treat secondary infection","Provide analgesia, oral care and a soft diet for painful oral erosions to maintain nutrition","Monitor disease activity with anti-desmoglein-3 and anti-desmoglein-1 titres and screen for treatment-related infection and bone loss","Exclude an underlying malignancy where paraneoplastic pemphigus is suspected","Escalate to inpatient specialist care for widespread erosions with fluid loss and sepsis risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pena_shokeir_syndrome":{"name":"Pena-Shokeir syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["foetal akinesia deformation sequence","fetal akinesia deformation sequence","FADS","Pena-Shokeir phenotype type 1","arthrogryposis-pulmonary hypoplasia sequence"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / Gorlin's Syndromes of the Head and Neck","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A phenotype (a sequence rather than a single disease) resulting from reduced foetal movement in utero, presenting with multiple congenital contractures","The hallmark tetrad is foetal akinesia, multiple joint contractures, pulmonary hypoplasia and craniofacial anomalies","Polyhydramnios reflects impaired foetal swallowing, and intrauterine growth restriction and a short umbilical cord are common","Pulmonary hypoplasia, caused by absent foetal breathing movements, is the usual cause of death and is frequently lethal in the neonatal period","Facial features include hypertelorism, a small mouth, micrognathia, low-set ears and depressed nasal tip","It is aetiologically heterogeneous, arising from any process that limits foetal movement (neurogenic, myopathic, neuromuscular junction, connective tissue or restrictive uterine causes)","Recurrence risk depends on the underlying cause, so establishing the aetiology is essential for counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Pena-Shokeir syndrome is the end result of the foetal akinesia deformation sequence: any insult that reduces or abolishes foetal movement during development produces a stereotyped set of secondary deformations. Immobility permits periarticular fibrosis and multiple joint contractures, absent breathing movements prevent normal lung expansion and cause pulmonary hypoplasia, and reduced swallowing leads to polyhydramnios and a small mouth. Causes are heterogeneous and may lie anywhere along the neuromuscular axis (central nervous system, anterior horn cell, peripheral nerve, neuromuscular junction or muscle) or in restrictive intrauterine conditions such as oligohydramnios or uterine anomaly.","additionalDifferentials":["Lethal multiple pterygium syndrome","Congenital myotonic dystrophy","Spinal muscular atrophy with arthrogryposis","Congenital myasthenic syndromes","Trisomy 18 and other chromosomal disorders","Amyoplasia (survivable form) and other arthrogryposis syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed prenatal ultrasound documenting reduced foetal movement, contractures and polyhydramnios","Postnatal or postmortem examination and clinical photographs to define the phenotype","Muscle and nerve histology to identify a myopathic or neurogenic basis","Molecular genetic testing, including panels for neuromuscular and neuromuscular-junction genes","Chromosomal microarray or karyotype to exclude aneuploidy","Maternal testing for myasthenia or myotonic dystrophy where relevant"],"redFlags":["Neonatal respiratory failure from pulmonary hypoplasia, often not survivable","Antenatal signs of severe akinesia and polyhydramnios indicating poor prognosis","Feeding failure and inability to protect the airway","Findings suggesting a treatable maternal cause such as myasthenia gravis"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally poor, with many affected infants stillborn or dying in the neonatal period from pulmonary hypoplasia; management is largely supportive and palliative, with counselling directed by the identified underlying cause.","pitfalls":["Treating Pena-Shokeir as a single diagnosis rather than searching for its underlying cause","Failing to counsel on recurrence, which varies widely by aetiology","Overlooking a treatable maternal condition such as myasthenia gravis","Not offering postmortem examination and genetic testing to guide future pregnancies"],"management":["Recognise the frequently lethal nature and offer palliative, comfort-focused neonatal care where pulmonary hypoplasia is severe","Provide neonatal respiratory support and airway management for pulmonary hypoplasia, with ventilatory support tailored to the agreed goals of care","Manage joint contractures with physiotherapy, splinting and orthopaedic input in survivors","Support feeding through assisted or tube feeding given micrognathia and impaired swallowing","Identify the underlying neuromuscular or restrictive cause to guide prognosis and recurrence risk, including maternal testing for myasthenia or myotonic dystrophy","Offer genetic counselling with prenatal counselling and recurrence-risk assessment","Co-ordinate multidisciplinary care across neonatology, clinical genetics and palliative care with family support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Gorlin's Syndromes of the Head and Neck"],"crossLinks":[]},"pendred_syndrome":{"name":"Pendred syndrome","system":"Ear / Audiology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SLC26A4","pendrin deficiency","deafness with enlarged vestibular aqueduct and goitre","PDS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology / Emery's Principles of Medical Genetics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad is sensorineural hearing loss, an enlarged vestibular aqueduct on imaging, and euthyroid goitre from an iodine organification defect","Caused by biallelic mutations in SLC26A4, which encodes the anion transporter pendrin; inheritance is autosomal recessive","Hearing loss is usually congenital or prelingual and may fluctuate or progress in a stepwise fashion, sometimes after minor head trauma","Enlarged vestibular aqueduct (with or without cochlear incomplete partition) is the most consistent temporal-bone finding on CT or MRI","Goitre often develops later, in later childhood or adolescence, and thyroid function is typically normal, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis","A perchlorate discharge test can demonstrate the underlying iodine organification defect but is now largely superseded by genetic testing","Advise patients to avoid activities with head-injury risk that may precipitate sudden drops in hearing"],"pathophysiology":"Pendred syndrome results from loss-of-function mutations in SLC26A4, encoding pendrin, a transmembrane anion (chloride/iodide/bicarbonate) exchanger expressed in the inner ear, thyroid and kidney. In the inner ear, impaired pendrin function disturbs endolymph ion and pH homeostasis, contributing to abnormal cochlear and vestibular development, an enlarged vestibular aqueduct, and sensorineural hearing loss. In the thyroid, defective iodide transport into the follicular lumen impairs organification of iodine, producing goitre while thyroid hormone levels are often maintained. The same gene can also cause non-syndromic hearing loss with enlarged vestibular aqueduct when thyroid involvement is absent.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-syndromic hearing loss with enlarged vestibular aqueduct (DFNB4)","Branchio-oto-renal syndrome with inner-ear malformation","Congenital hypothyroidism with dyshormonogenesis and goitre","Other syndromic sensorineural hearing loss such as Waardenburg or Usher","Acquired fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss from other causes","Isolated multinodular goitre unrelated to hearing loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Audiometry with serial testing to document fluctuating or progressive loss","High-resolution CT or MRI of the temporal bones for enlarged vestibular aqueduct and cochlear anomalies","Thyroid function tests and thyroid ultrasound to assess goitre","Perchlorate discharge test where genetic testing is unavailable","SLC26A4 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Sudden or stepwise deterioration in hearing, especially after head trauma","Progressive goitre or evolving thyroid dysfunction requiring endocrine review","Vertigo or imbalance suggesting associated vestibular involvement","Bilateral profound loss threatening speech and language development in a child"],"prognosis":"Hearing loss tends to be progressive and may fluctuate, with many patients eventually developing severe-to-profound loss suited to hearing aids or cochlear implantation; the goitre is usually benign and euthyroid, though thyroid function should be monitored over time.","pitfalls":["Excluding Pendred because goitre is absent in a young child before it typically develops","Overlooking an enlarged vestibular aqueduct on imaging as the key structural clue","Not warning patients about head-injury and pressure-related hearing drops","Assuming a euthyroid goitre is unrelated to the hearing loss"],"management":["Manage sensorineural hearing loss with hearing aids and cochlear implantation for severe-to-profound loss","Provide early audiological rehabilitation with speech and language therapy and educational support for prelingual loss","Advise avoidance of head trauma and contact sports that may precipitate stepwise hearing deterioration in enlarged vestibular aqueduct","Monitor thyroid function and goitre size, and give thyroid hormone replacement if hypothyroidism develops","Perform serial audiometry to track fluctuating or progressive hearing loss","Refer for co-ordinated otology, audiology and endocrinology care","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive condition and screen affected siblings"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Emery's Elements of Medical Genetics and Genomics"],"crossLinks":[]},"penetrating_abdominal_trauma":{"name":"Penetrating abdominal trauma","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["penetrating abdominal injury","stab wound abdomen","gunshot wound abdomen"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Manage as a trauma patient using the primary survey, with haemorrhage control as the priority once the airway is secure","A haemodynamically unstable patient or one with peritonitis or evisceration needs immediate surgery rather than further imaging","Gunshot wounds usually breach the peritoneum and injure viscera and generally warrant laparotomy, whereas selected stab wounds may be managed non-operatively with close observation","Do not remove an impaling object in the emergency setting; stabilise it and remove it in a controlled operative environment","The trajectory can be deceptive, so consider injury to the chest, retroperitoneum, and pelvis and inspect the back and flanks for other wounds","Serial abdominal examination detects evolving peritonitis in patients selected for conservative management","Give tetanus prophylaxis and early antibiotics when hollow-viscus injury is likely, and resuscitate with blood products in shock"],"pathophysiology":"A penetrating object breaches the abdominal wall and can lacerate solid organs (liver, spleen), causing haemorrhage, or hollow viscera (bowel, stomach), causing spillage of contents and peritoneal contamination. Vascular injury may produce rapid exsanguination into the peritoneal cavity or retroperitoneum. Low-energy stab wounds injure tissue along a relatively predictable track, whereas high-energy gunshot wounds transfer energy widely, causing cavitation and injury beyond the visible path. The result is a combination of haemorrhagic shock from bleeding and, later, peritonitis and sepsis from contamination.","additionalDifferentials":["Superficial wound that has not breached the peritoneum","Associated thoracic injury from a thoraco-abdominal track","Retroperitoneal vascular or renal injury","Diaphragmatic injury with thoraco-abdominal communication","Concurrent blunt trauma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Focused assessment with sonography for trauma (FAST) to detect free intraperitoneal fluid","CT abdomen and pelvis in the stable patient to define trajectory and organ injury","Local wound exploration in selected stab wounds to assess peritoneal breach","Group and crossmatch with coagulation studies","Erect chest radiograph to detect free air or associated thoracic injury"],"redFlags":["Haemodynamic instability indicating major haemorrhage and the need for immediate laparotomy","Peritonitis, evisceration, or a retained impaling object mandating surgical exploration","Gunshot trajectory crossing the peritoneal cavity","Rising lactate or persistent shock despite resuscitation","Evolving peritoneal signs during observation of a non-operatively managed wound"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the organs injured, blood loss, and time to control of haemorrhage and contamination. Early surgery for unstable or peritonitic patients improves survival, whereas delay allows exsanguination or sepsis with high mortality.","pitfalls":["Removing an impaling object in the emergency department, which can trigger uncontrolled bleeding","Being reassured by a benign initial abdomen when injury can evolve","Underestimating trajectory and missing thoracic, retroperitoneal, or back wounds","Delaying surgery in an unstable patient to obtain imaging","Neglecting antibiotics and tetanus prophylaxis when hollow viscus injury is likely"],"management":["Follow a structured trauma primary survey, securing the airway and prioritising haemorrhage control","Resuscitate with balanced blood-product transfusion and activate the massive transfusion protocol for haemorrhagic shock","Proceed to immediate laparotomy for haemodynamic instability, peritonitis or evisceration","Stabilise any impaling object in situ and remove it only in a controlled operative setting","Select stable stab-wound patients for non-operative management with serial abdominal examination","Give broad-spectrum antibiotics and tetanus prophylaxis given the risk of hollow-viscus contamination","Search for occult thoracic, retroperitoneal and pelvic injury and inspect the back and flanks for other wounds","Provide damage-control surgery and critical-care support for the unstable, coagulopathic patient"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"penile_cancer":{"name":"Penile cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["penile carcinoma","penile squamous cell carcinoma","cancer of the penis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The overwhelming majority are squamous cell carcinomas, most often arising on the glans, coronal sulcus, or inner prepuce","Key risk factors include phimosis, chronic inflammation, poor hygiene, smoking, and persistent high-risk human papillomavirus infection, while childhood circumcision appears protective","It typically presents as a persistent penile ulcer, nodule, or warty growth that fails to heal, sometimes with foul discharge, and is frequently delayed by embarrassment or misdiagnosis as infection","Regional inguinal lymph nodes are the principal route of spread, so careful assessment and management of the groin nodes is central to staging and prognosis","Any suspicious non-healing penile lesion requires biopsy, since treatment and prognosis depend on histological grade and depth of invasion","Early and superficial tumours can be treated with organ-preserving approaches such as topical therapy, laser, local excision, or glansectomy, preserving function where oncologically safe","Advanced primary tumours may require partial or total penectomy, and management of inguinal nodes ranges from surveillance to lymphadenectomy depending on risk","Platinum-based chemotherapy is used for bulky nodal or metastatic disease, sometimes combined with surgery or radiotherapy in a multimodal approach"],"pathophysiology":"Penile squamous cell carcinoma develops from the epithelium of the glans and prepuce, arising either through human papillomavirus-driven pathways or through chronic inflammatory conditions such as lichen sclerosus and long-standing phimosis. Precursor penile intraepithelial neoplasia progresses to invasive carcinoma that infiltrates the corpora and surrounding tissues. The tumour spreads in a predictable stepwise fashion first to superficial and deep inguinal nodes and then to pelvic nodes, with distant metastasis occurring late.","additionalDifferentials":["Penile intraepithelial neoplasia (carcinoma in situ)","Genital warts (condylomata acuminata)","Syphilitic chancre or other infective genital ulcers","Balanitis or balanoposthitis","Lichen sclerosus (balanitis xerotica obliterans)","Penile melanoma or other rare penile tumours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy of the penile lesion for histological type, grade, and depth of invasion","Clinical examination and imaging of the inguinal regions for nodal assessment","MRI of the penis to define local extent when organ preservation is considered","CT or PET-CT of pelvis and abdomen for nodal and distant staging","HPV and HIV testing where clinically relevant"],"redFlags":["A non-healing penile ulcer or nodule persisting despite treatment for presumed infection","Palpable or enlarging inguinal lymph nodes","Bleeding, foul discharge, or destruction of penile tissue","Fixed or ulcerated groin nodes suggesting advanced regional disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis correlates strongly with inguinal nodal status; localised node-negative disease has high cure rates with appropriate treatment, whereas extensive nodal involvement or distant metastasis markedly reduces survival.","pitfalls":["Treating a persistent penile lesion repeatedly as infection without biopsy","Underestimating the importance of early inguinal node evaluation and management","Delaying referral due to patient embarrassment, allowing progression","Choosing organ-preserving therapy for lesions that are too advanced for safe conservation"],"management":["Treat early superficial disease with organ-preserving options such as topical chemotherapy, laser, wide local excision or glansectomy","Perform partial or total penectomy for larger or deeply invasive primary tumours to achieve clear margins","Stage and manage the inguinal nodes with sentinel node biopsy or inguinal lymphadenectomy, as nodal status drives prognosis","Offer platinum-based chemotherapy for bulky or advanced nodal disease, with radiotherapy in selected cases","Deliver neoadjuvant or adjuvant treatment and consider chemoradiotherapy for locally advanced disease","Provide psychosexual support, smoking cessation and human papillomavirus vaccination advice","Refer to a specialist penile-cancer multidisciplinary team with structured follow-up surveillance for local and nodal recurrence","Test for HIV and human papillomavirus where clinically relevant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology"],"crossLinks":[]},"penile_fracture":{"name":"Penile fracture","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fracture of the penis","tunica albuginea rupture","traumatic rupture of the corpus cavernosum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rupture of the tunica albuginea of an erect corpus cavernosum, usually during vigorous intercourse or forced bending of the erect penis","The patient classically reports a sudden audible crack or pop, immediate pain, rapid detumescence, and swelling","The typical aubergine (eggplant) deformity results from a haematoma confined by Buck's fascia, giving a swollen, bruised, and often deviated penis","It is a surgical emergency, as prompt exploration and repair of the tunical defect give the best functional outcomes and reduce long-term erectile and deformity complications","Always assess for an associated urethral injury - blood at the meatus, frank haematuria, or difficulty voiding raises this concern","Diagnosis is largely clinical; ultrasound or MRI can localise the tear when the picture is equivocal, and retrograde urethrography or flexible cystoscopy evaluates the urethra","Delayed or conservative treatment carries higher rates of penile curvature, painful nodules, and erectile dysfunction than early surgical repair"],"pathophysiology":"During erection the tunica albuginea covering the corpora cavernosa is stretched thin and taut. An abrupt bending force against the rigid erect penis, most often when it slips out and strikes the perineum or pubis during intercourse, sharply raises intracavernosal pressure and tears the tunica. Blood escapes from the corpus cavernosum and, while contained by Buck's fascia, collects as a localised haematoma that produces the characteristic eggplant deformity; if Buck's fascia is also breached, bruising spreads more widely. Because the urethra and corpus spongiosum lie ventrally, a forceful injury can extend to cause a concurrent urethral rupture, which must be actively sought.","additionalDifferentials":["Rupture of a superficial or dorsal penile vein or artery ('false fracture')","Dislocation of the penis","Isolated urethral injury","Penile haematoma without a tunical tear","Painful Peyronie's plaque or acute Peyronie's disease","Dorsal nerve or vessel injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment for the eggplant deformity and a palpable tunical defect","Penile ultrasound to localise the tear","MRI of the penis when the diagnosis is uncertain","Retrograde urethrography to assess the urethra","Flexible cystoscopy where urethral injury is suspected","Urinalysis for haematuria"],"redFlags":["Blood at the urethral meatus or gross haematuria, indicating urethral injury","Inability to pass urine or acute urinary retention","An expanding haematoma or bruising extending beyond Buck's fascia","Features suggesting complete urethral disruption"],"prognosis":"Early surgical repair generally restores good erectile function with low rates of curvature and nodules; delay or non-operative management is associated with higher rates of penile deformity and erectile dysfunction.","pitfalls":["Managing conservatively when early surgical exploration would give better functional results","Failing to evaluate the urethra and missing an associated urethral injury","Mistaking a ruptured superficial vessel ('false fracture') for a true tunical tear, or vice versa","Delaying theatre and increasing the risk of long-term penile deformity"],"management":["Undertake prompt surgical exploration and repair of the tunica albuginea defect as the treatment of choice","Evacuate the haematoma and close the tear to restore anatomy and reduce long-term curvature","Actively assess for and repair any associated urethral injury identified on retrograde urethrography or cystoscopy","Provide analgesia and advise abstinence from sexual activity during healing","Arrange postoperative erectile-function follow-up and manage penile curvature or nodules if they develop","Refer urgently to urology given the time-critical nature of repair"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","EAU Guidelines on Urological Trauma"],"crossLinks":[]},"penile_mondor_disease":{"name":"Penile Mondor disease","system":"Genitourinary / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["superficial dorsal penile vein thrombophlebitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Urology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Thrombophlebitis of the superficial dorsal vein of the penis, a benign self-limiting condition","Presents as a palpable, cord-like, sometimes tender indurated structure along the dorsum of the penis","It often follows vigorous or prolonged sexual activity, and other precipitants include trauma and pelvic procedures","It is the penile analogue of Mondor disease of the chest/breast wall","Diagnosis is usually clinical, supported by Doppler ultrasound showing a thrombosed non-compressible dorsal vein","It resolves spontaneously over weeks with conservative measures such as anti-inflammatory treatment and abstinence"],"pathophysiology":"Penile Mondor disease is a localised thrombophlebitis of the superficial dorsal vein of the penis. Endothelial injury and venous stasis, often from prolonged sexual activity, trauma or compression, promote thrombosis of the superficial vein, producing a palpable thrombosed cord with surrounding inflammation. It is a benign, self-limiting vascular condition confined to the superficial penile venous system, and recanalisation occurs over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Peyronie disease","Penile lymphangitis (non-venereal sclerosing lymphangitis)","Penile cellulitis or other infection","Dorsal nerve or other penile pathology","Thrombosed penile vascular malformation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the palpable thrombosed dorsal cord","Doppler ultrasound of the dorsal penile vein","History of precipitating activity or trauma","Assessment for a hypercoagulable state if recurrent","Exclusion of Peyronie disease and infection"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting infection (fever, purulence)","Recurrent episodes suggesting a thrombophilia","Progressive induration suggesting Peyronie disease","Persistent symptoms beyond the expected course"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and resolves spontaneously over a few weeks with conservative management, with a good prognosis and low recurrence.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with Peyronie disease or sclerosing lymphangitis","Investigating or treating it as an infection","Overlooking a hypercoagulable tendency in recurrent cases","Causing undue alarm about a benign self-limiting condition"],"management":["Manage conservatively with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and reassurance as a benign self-limiting condition","Advise temporary abstinence from sexual activity and avoidance of penile trauma while the thrombosed vein recanalises","Provide warm compresses and topical anti-inflammatory measures for symptomatic relief","Consider a short course of anticoagulation only in extensive or persistent thrombosis","Investigate for an underlying hypercoagulable state if episodes recur","Exclude Peyronie disease and infection and reassure regarding spontaneous resolution"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Urology reference texts (standard reference)","Andrology references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"pentasomy_x":{"name":"Pentasomy X (49,XXXXX)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["49,XXXXX","penta X syndrome","XXXXX syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most severe of the female X-polysomies, carrying three extra X chromosomes","Moderate-to-severe intellectual disability is typical and more marked than in triple X","Short stature is characteristic, in contrast to the tall stature of triple X, reflecting the escalating dosage effect at higher polysomies","Characteristic facial features include upslanting palpebral fissures, hypertelorism, epicanthic folds, a flat nasal bridge, and coarse features that may superficially resemble Down syndrome","Congenital heart defects, notably patent ductus arteriosus and septal defects, are common","Skeletal anomalies include radioulnar synostosis, joint laxity or contractures, and clinodactyly","Microcephaly, recurrent infections, and ovarian and uterine hypoplasia with delayed or absent puberty are frequent"],"pathophysiology":"Pentasomy X arises from sequential non-disjunction during maternal meiosis, producing an egg with four X chromosomes and thus a 49,XXXXX conceptus. Although all but one X is inactivated, the burden of genes that escape X-inactivation rises with each additional X, so gene-dosage effects are amplified relative to trisomy X. This escalating imbalance explains why pentasomy X is far more severe, with pronounced intellectual disability, short stature, congenital malformations, and dysmorphism, illustrating the general rule that each additional sex chromosome worsens the phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Down syndrome, given the overlapping facial features","Tetrasomy X (48,XXXX) and trisomy X (47,XXX)","Other polysomy X or Y syndromes","Syndromic intellectual disability of other cause","Skeletal dysplasia featuring radioulnar synostosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype, which is diagnostic","Chromosomal microarray","Echocardiography","Skeletal survey, including the forearm for radioulnar synostosis","Developmental and cognitive assessment","Endocrine and pubertal assessment with pelvic imaging for uterine and ovarian development","Infection surveillance"],"redFlags":["Significant congenital heart disease","Recurrent or severe infections","Failure to progress through puberty requiring endocrine input"],"prognosis":"Guarded; most individuals have significant intellectual disability and need lifelong support, with outcome influenced by cardiac and other malformations, although survival into adulthood occurs.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the facial features for Down syndrome and not performing a karyotype","Expecting tall stature, as in triple X, when short stature is typical","Missing congenital heart disease or radioulnar synostosis","Underestimating the degree of intellectual disability compared with lower-grade X-polysomies"],"management":["Provide early developmental intervention with special education and speech, occupational and physical therapy for intellectual disability","Evaluate and manage congenital heart defects such as patent ductus arteriosus and septal defects with cardiology and surgical repair as needed","Address skeletal anomalies including radioulnar synostosis and contractures with orthopaedic and physiotherapy input","Assess growth and offer endocrine evaluation of puberty and ovarian function, with hormone replacement if indicated","Undertake surveillance for recurrent infections and associated malformations","Co-ordinate multidisciplinary care with lifelong support for the individual and family","Offer genetic counselling explaining the sporadic non-disjunction origin and low recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"pepck_deficiency":{"name":"Phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PEPCK deficiency","PCK1 deficiency","PCK2 deficiency","cytosolic PEPCK deficiency","mitochondrial PEPCK deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase catalyses an early committed step of gluconeogenesis, and deficiency (cytosolic PCK1 or mitochondrial PCK2 forms) impairs glucose synthesis from lactate and amino acids","Presents with fasting hypoglycaemia accompanied by lactic acidosis, often with hepatomegaly and evidence of liver dysfunction","Historically a difficult and controversial diagnosis, as many reported cases reflected secondary or mitochondrial dysfunction rather than a primary enzyme defect","Genetically confirmed PCK1 deficiency has been described with hypoglycaemia, lactic acidosis and a characteristic urinary organic acid pattern including tricarboxylic acid cycle intermediates","Metabolic crises are typically triggered by fasting or intercurrent illness, with well periods in between","Acute treatment corrects hypoglycaemia and lactic acidosis with intravenous glucose; long-term care focuses on avoidance of fasting and provision of frequent carbohydrate","Confirmation now relies on PCK1 or PCK2 molecular testing, since enzyme measurement can be confounded by secondary causes"],"pathophysiology":"Phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase converts oxaloacetate to phosphoenolpyruvate, a rate-controlling step channelling gluconeogenic precursors toward glucose. It exists in cytosolic (PCK1) and mitochondrial (PCK2) isoforms. Deficiency impairs gluconeogenesis so that, once glycogen reserves are depleted during fasting, hypoglycaemia develops and gluconeogenic precursors such as lactate accumulate, producing lactic acidosis. Disruption of anaplerosis and the connection between the tricarboxylic acid cycle and glucose output leads to a distinctive organic acid pattern with elevated cycle intermediates. Hepatic involvement reflects the liver's central role in gluconeogenesis.","additionalDifferentials":["Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase deficiency","Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency","Glycogen storage disease type I","Mitochondrial respiratory chain disorders with lactic acidosis","Fatty-acid oxidation disorders with hypoglycaemia","Secondary gluconeogenic dysfunction from liver failure or toxins","Sepsis with lactic acidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood glucose, blood gas and lactate during symptomatic fasting episodes","Urine organic acids looking for elevated tricarboxylic acid cycle intermediates","Plasma amino acids and ammonia to assess for related metabolic defects","Liver function tests to evaluate hepatic involvement","PCK1 and PCK2 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Assessment for secondary causes of PEPCK dysfunction before attributing a primary defect"],"redFlags":["Fasting hypoglycaemia with severe lactic acidosis in an infant or young child","Hepatomegaly with liver dysfunction during crises","Reduced consciousness or seizures from hypoglycaemia","Recurrent metabolic decompensation triggered by fasting or illness"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are variable and depend on the severity of crises and any coexisting mitochondrial dysfunction; with avoidance of fasting and prompt treatment of hypoglycaemia many children do reasonably well, but severe neonatal presentations carry a poorer prognosis.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing primary PEPCK deficiency without excluding secondary mitochondrial or hepatic causes","Relying on enzyme assays that can be confounded rather than confirming with molecular testing","Failing to capture lactate and organic acids during the acute hypoglycaemic episode","Permitting prolonged fasting for illness or procedures without protective glucose"],"management":["Prevent metabolic crises by avoiding fasting and providing frequent carbohydrate feeds, including overnight complex carbohydrate","Treat acute hypoglycaemia and lactic acidosis with intravenous glucose","Institute an emergency regimen with intravenous dextrose during intercurrent illness or reduced oral intake","Provide dietetic support with a structured feeding plan and adequate carbohydrate intake","Monitor glucose, lactate, liver function and growth, and assess for coexisting mitochondrial dysfunction","Refer to a metabolic medicine team and clinical genetics","Offer genetic counselling and family testing for this heritable disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: Disorders of Gluconeogenesis","Saudubray, Baumgartner & Walter, Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"peptic_ulcer_disease_and_related":{"name":"Peptic Ulcer Disease and Related Disorders","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PUD","Duodenal ulcer","Gastric ulcer","Acid peptic disease"],"chapter":335,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2514","clinicalPearls":["A peptic ulcer is a break in the mucosa larger than 5 mm that extends into the submucosa; more than 90% of patients with the classic dyspepsia symptom complex have no ulcer, and many patients with true ulcers are asymptomatic (Harrison 22e p.2514, p.2522).","Burning/gnawing epigastric pain typically occurs 90 minutes to 3 hours after eating in duodenal ulcer and is relieved by food or antacids; pain that wakes the patient between midnight and 3 A.M. is the most discriminating symptom (about two-thirds of DU patients), though it also occurs in roughly one-third with non-ulcer dyspepsia (Harrison 22e p.2522).","In gastric ulcer the pain may instead be precipitated by food, and nausea and weight loss are more common than in duodenal ulcer (Harrison 22e p.2522-2523).","H. pylori and NSAIDs are the two dominant risk factors (US odds ratios about 3.7 and 3.3); idiopathic and non-Hp/non-NSAID ulcers are rising as H. pylori prevalence falls (Harrison 22e p.2518, p.2522).","Gastric ulcers can be malignant and must be biopsied at discovery; duodenal ulcers are almost never malignant (Harrison 22e p.2518).","NSAID toxicity correlates poorly with symptoms: over 80% of patients with serious NSAID-related complications had no preceding dyspepsia, and even 75 mg/day of aspirin can cause serious ulceration, so no NSAID dose is completely safe (Harrison 22e p.2520-2521).","About two-thirds of PUD patients have no abdominal pain, and up to 87% of those with NSAID-induced disease present with a complication without prior symptoms; more than 50% of ulcer hemorrhages occur without warning (Harrison 22e p.2522).","Antral-predominant H. pylori gastritis predisposes to duodenal ulcer, whereas corpus-predominant gastritis predisposes to gastric ulcer, atrophy, and ultimately gastric carcinoma (Harrison 22e p.2519-2520).","Endoscopy is the most sensitive and specific test; it detects an ulcer in fewer than 30% of patients presenting with dyspepsia, allows biopsy to exclude malignancy and detect H. pylori, and barium studies are now rarely used (Harrison 22e p.2523).","A fasting serum gastrin above ten times normal strongly suggests Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, but two-thirds of ZES patients have gastrin levels that overlap with commoner causes; a secretin-stimulated rise of 120 pg/mL or more within 15 minutes is over 90% sensitive and specific (Harrison 22e p.2535-2536).","Suspect gastrinoma with ulcers in unusual locations (second part of duodenum or beyond), refractory or recurrent ulcers, ulcers with complications, or ulcers without H. pylori or NSAID use; about 25% of gastrinomas arise in MEN 1 (Harrison 22e p.2534-2535).","Stress-related mucosal injury (Cushing's ulcer after head injury, Curling's ulcer after burns) histologically lacks inflammation and H. pylori, so 'gastritis' is a misnomer; bleeding typically appears 48-72 h after the insult with mechanical ventilation and coagulopathy as key risk factors (Harrison 22e p.2536)."],"pathophysiology":"Ulcers result from an imbalance between mucosal aggressive factors (gastric acid secreted by parietal-cell H+,K+-ATPase, pepsin from chief cells) and mucosal defense/repair (a preepithelial mucus-bicarbonate-phospholipid layer, epithelial restitution and tight junctions, and a subepithelial microvascular bed), with prostaglandins, nitric oxide, and mucosal blood flow central to protection. H. pylori, a gram-negative microaerophilic urease-producing rod, colonizes gastric mucus, causes chronic active gastritis in nearly all infected individuals, and produces frank ulceration in only 10-15%; virulence factors include urease (generating ammonia), CagA delivered by a type IV secretion system from cag-PAI-positive strains, VacA, and adhesins. Antral-predominant gastritis raises acid and promotes duodenal ulcer, whereas corpus-predominant pangastritis lowers acid and favors gastric ulcer and carcinoma. NSAIDs injure mucosa both systemically (COX-1 inhibition depletes protective prostaglandins) and topically (weak acids that become trapped and ionized intracellularly), with neutrophil adherence to the gastric microcirculation initiating injury; H. pylori and NSAIDs act as independent and synergistic risk factors. In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, autonomous gastrin secretion from a neuroendocrine tumor drives acid hypersecretion, parietal-cell hyperplasia, ulceration, esophagitis, and diarrhea (Harrison 22e p.2514-2521, p.2534).","additionalDifferentials":["Functional (non-ulcer/essential) dyspepsia, including postprandial distress syndrome and epigastric pain syndrome (commonest cause of upper abdominal discomfort)","Gastroesophageal reflux disease","Proximal gastrointestinal tumors / gastric malignancy","Pancreaticobiliary disease (biliary colic, chronic pancreatitis)","Gastroduodenal Crohn's disease","Mesenteric vascular disease","Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (gastrinoma)"],"infectionMimics":["Cytomegalovirus gastritis/ulceration (immunocompromised hosts)","Herpes simplex virus gastritis/ulceration","Helicobacter heilmannii infection","Phlegmonous (bacterial) gastritis","Infectious causes of large gastric folds mimicking Menetrier's disease (CMV, histoplasmosis, syphilis, tuberculosis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Functional dyspepsia","Drug/toxin-induced ulceration (bisphosphonates, clopidogrel, glucocorticoids combined with NSAIDs, potassium chloride, mycophenolate, chemotherapy, immune checkpoint inhibitors, crack cocaine)","Crohn's disease and eosinophilic gastroenteritis","Sarcoidosis and other infiltrative disease","Ischemia and radiation injury","Menetrier's disease and gastric polyposis presenting as large gastric folds","Idiopathic hypersecretory state"],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper endoscopy with biopsy (most sensitive/specific; biopsy gastric ulcers to exclude malignancy)","H. pylori testing: rapid urease biopsy test, histology, culture (allows susceptibility testing)","Noninvasive H. pylori tests: 13C-urea breath test and stool monoclonal antigen test (both >95% sensitivity/specificity); serology cannot distinguish active from prior infection","Fasting serum gastrin (two occasions) when ZES is suspected","Secretin stimulation test (rise >=120 pg/mL at 15 min) and gastric pH/acid analysis for gastrinoma","Serum calcium, parathyroid hormone, prolactin, and pancreatic polypeptide to screen for MEN 1 in gastrinoma patients","Tumor localization imaging and somatostatin-receptor-based studies for gastrinoma","Screening blood/urine for surreptitious aspirin/NSAID use in refractory H. pylori-negative ulcers"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe generalized abdominal pain with a board-like, rigid abdomen suggesting perforation","Tarry stools or coffee-ground emesis indicating gastrointestinal bleeding; tachycardia/orthostasis indicating hypovolemia","Persistent pain no longer relieved by food/antacids and radiating to the back suggesting posterior penetration (pancreas)","Early satiety, vomiting of undigested food, and a succussion splash suggesting gastric outlet obstruction","Weight loss, anorexia, or new dyspepsia at age 60 or older warranting endoscopy before empiric therapy","Ulcers in unusual locations, refractory/recurrent ulcers, or multiple ulcers prompting evaluation for gastrinoma"],"prognosis":"Eradication of H. pylori reduces duodenal ulcer recurrence by more than 80%, and improving sanitation has steadily lowered ulcer incidence, surgery rates, and physician visits over the past decades. Complications still carry meaningful mortality: GI bleeding (the commonest, ~15% of patients) has a 30-day mortality of about 2.5-10% and is more frequent in those over 60; perforation occurs in about 6-7% of patients with 30-day mortality over 20%; gastric outlet obstruction is the least common (1-2%). Approximately 15,000 deaths per year in the US are attributed to complicated PUD. For gastrinoma, overall 5- and 10-year survival is about 62-75% and 47-53%; complete resection or negative laparotomy yields survival over 90%, while hepatic metastasis drops 5-year survival below 20% (Harrison 22e p.2514, p.2516, p.2522-2523, p.2536).","pitfalls":["Treating dyspepsia as PUD: abdominal pain has poor predictive value and most dyspeptic patients have no ulcer, so empiric assumptions can miss alternative diagnoses.","Relying on symptoms to gauge NSAID risk: serious complications usually occur without preceding dyspepsia.","Failing to biopsy a gastric ulcer to exclude malignancy.","Misinterpreting an elevated fasting gastrin: PPIs, hypochlorhydria/achlorhydria, pernicious anemia, H. pylori, renal insufficiency, retained antrum, and G-cell hyperplasia all raise gastrin, and PPIs must be stopped (about 7 days, bridged with an H2 antagonist) before ZES testing to avoid false positives.","Assuming serology proves active H. pylori infection, when it cannot distinguish current from prior infection and is unreliable for follow-up.","Abruptly stopping PPIs in gastrinoma patients, which can precipitate severe acid-related complications.","Calling stress-related mucosal injury 'gastritis' despite the absence of inflammation, or labeling endoscopic erythema as gastritis without histology."],"management":["Eradicate Helicobacter pylori with a proton pump inhibitor plus combination antibiotic therapy where infection is confirmed","Suppress acid with proton pump inhibitors to promote ulcer healing","Withdraw or minimise NSAIDs and aspirin, co-prescribing a proton pump inhibitor where ongoing NSAID use is unavoidable","Advise smoking cessation and reduction of alcohol as lifestyle measures","Biopsy gastric ulcers to exclude malignancy and repeat endoscopy to confirm healing","Investigate refractory or multiple ulcers for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome with fasting gastrin, and screen for MEN 1","Manage bleeding with endoscopic haemostasis alongside intravenous proton pump inhibition","Confirm Helicobacter pylori eradication with a urea breath test or stool antigen test after treatment"],"severityClassification":"PUD severity is framed clinically by the presence and type of complication: uncomplicated ulcer versus bleeding (commonest, ~15%, 30-day mortality ~2.5-10%), perforation/penetration (~6-7%, 30-day mortality >20%; DU penetrates posteriorly into pancreas, GU into left hepatic lobe), and gastric outlet obstruction (1-2%, may be inflammatory/reversible or fixed scar). For gastrinoma, prognosis is stratified by resectability and metastatic burden rather than a formal stage in this chapter (Harrison 22e p.2522-2523, p.2536).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2514","Harrison 22e p.2515","Harrison 22e p.2516","Harrison 22e p.2517","Harrison 22e p.2518","Harrison 22e p.2519","Harrison 22e p.2520","Harrison 22e p.2521","Harrison 22e p.2522","Harrison 22e p.2523","Harrison 22e p.2534","Harrison 22e p.2535","Harrison 22e p.2536","Harrison 22e p.2539"],"crossLinks":[]},"peptic_ulcer_perforation":{"name":"Perforated peptic ulcer","system":"GI / Upper GI / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["peptic ulcer perforation","perforated duodenal ulcer","perforated gastric ulcer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically presents with sudden, severe, generalised abdominal pain in a patient who may have a history of dyspepsia or NSAID use","Examination shows a rigid, board-like abdomen with generalised tenderness, guarding, and absent bowel sounds from peritonitis","Perforation evolves through phases: initial chemical peritonitis from gastric acid, a deceptive period of apparent improvement, and then bacterial peritonitis with sepsis","An erect chest radiograph often shows free air under the diaphragm, but its absence does not exclude perforation; CT is more sensitive","Key risk factors are Helicobacter pylori infection, NSAID and aspirin use, smoking, and physiological stress","Initial management is aggressive resuscitation, analgesia, nasogastric decompression, intravenous proton pump inhibition, and broad-spectrum antibiotics while preparing for surgery","The standard operation is closure of the perforation, commonly with an omental (Graham) patch, and thorough peritoneal lavage; large or malignant gastric perforations may require resection","Selected patients with a sealed perforation and no generalised peritonitis may be managed non-operatively with close monitoring"],"pathophysiology":"Perforation occurs when a peptic ulcer erodes through the full thickness of the gastric or duodenal wall, allowing gastric and duodenal contents to spill into the peritoneal cavity. Mucosal breakdown is driven by an imbalance between aggressive factors (acid, pepsin, Helicobacter pylori, NSAIDs) and mucosal defence. Escaped acidic contents first cause a sterile chemical peritonitis, followed within hours by bacterial contamination and generalised bacterial peritonitis. The resulting inflammatory cascade produces fluid sequestration, sepsis, and, if untreated, septic shock and multi-organ failure. Duodenal and pyloric ulcers most often perforate anteriorly.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute pancreatitis","Acute cholecystitis or biliary perforation","Perforation elsewhere in the GI tract (colonic, appendiceal)","Ruptured or leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm","Myocardial infarction (inferior)","Mesenteric ischaemia","Ruptured ectopic pregnancy or ovarian pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Erect chest radiograph to look for free subdiaphragmatic gas","CT abdomen and pelvis as the most sensitive test for free air and to localise the perforation","Bloods including full blood count, urea and electrolytes, amylase or lipase, CRP, and lactate","Arterial blood gas and group and save or crossmatch for resuscitation and theatre","Helicobacter pylori testing and, later, endoscopy to confirm ulcer healing and exclude malignancy in gastric ulcers"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe pain with a rigid abdomen and generalised peritonitis (surgical emergency)","Free intraperitoneal gas on imaging","Sepsis, haemodynamic instability, or rising lactate indicating septic shock","Deterioration after a deceptive symptom-free interval signalling established bacterial peritonitis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on age, comorbidity, size of perforation, and, critically, the delay before surgery; early intervention gives good recovery, whereas delayed presentation with established peritonitis and sepsis carries high morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Excluding perforation on the basis of a normal erect chest radiograph alone","Being reassured by the transient improvement of the deceptive second phase","Delaying resuscitation and surgery in a septic patient","Failing to biopsy or follow up a perforated gastric ulcer to exclude malignancy"],"management":["Resuscitate aggressively with intravenous fluids, analgesia and nasogastric decompression","Commence intravenous proton pump inhibition and broad-spectrum antibiotics covering enteric organisms","Proceed to emergency surgery for operative repair, typically omental (Graham) patch closure of the perforation with peritoneal lavage","Consider non-operative management only in selected, stable, sealed perforations with close monitoring","Test for and eradicate Helicobacter pylori and withdraw NSAIDs to prevent recurrence","Provide postoperative critical-care support for sepsis and organ dysfunction","Arrange follow-up endoscopy to confirm healing and exclude malignancy in gastric ulcers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"perforating_folliculitis":{"name":"perforating folliculitis","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["perforating folliculitis of Mehregan and Coskey"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A perforating dermatosis in which the perforation is centred on a hair follicle, presenting as discrete follicular keratotic papules, each often containing a curled or fragmented hair within a central plug.","Favours the hair-bearing extensor surfaces of the limbs, buttocks and trunk, and is frequently pruritic.","Regarded as part of the acquired perforating dermatosis spectrum and, like the others, associated with chronic kidney disease, haemodialysis and diabetes mellitus.","Histology shows a dilated follicular infundibulum plugged with keratin, degenerate collagen and inflammatory debris, with a perforation through the follicular wall through which material is eliminated.","A curled hair shaft is often trapped within the follicular plug and may act as the initiating foreign body driving the perforation.","Differentiation from Kyrle disease and reactive perforating collagenosis rests on the follicular localisation of the perforating channel.","Management follows the acquired perforating dermatosis approach: emollients, topical retinoids or corticosteroids, keratolytics, phototherapy and control of the underlying systemic disorder.","Koebnerisation from scratching contributes to lesion development, so breaking the itch-scratch cycle is an important therapeutic goal."],"pathophysiology":"Perforating folliculitis arises from injury to the follicular infundibulum, where retained keratin, a coiled or fractured hair shaft, and degenerate dermal connective tissue accumulate and are eliminated through a defect in the follicular wall. Follicular rupture provokes a foreign-body inflammatory response that facilitates transepithelial elimination of collagen and keratinous debris. In acquired cases the metabolic milieu of uraemia and diabetes, dermal microangiopathy, and repetitive scratch-induced trauma with Koebnerisation are considered key triggers, injuring the follicular epithelium and perpetuating the perforating process.","additionalDifferentials":["keratosis pilaris","infective (bacterial or fungal) folliculitis","Kyrle disease","reactive perforating collagenosis","prurigo nodularis","follicular lichen planus","perforating granuloma annulare"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["skin biopsy demonstrating the plugged follicle with perforation of the follicular wall and eliminated debris","renal function tests and estimated glomerular filtration rate","fasting glucose and glycated haemoglobin","swab of pustular lesions to exclude infective folliculitis where clinically suspected","liver and thyroid function tests","full blood count","urinalysis for proteinuria"],"redFlags":["presentation without known renal or metabolic disease, warranting systemic screening","pustular or tender lesions suggesting superimposed infective folliculitis requiring microbiological assessment","widespread refractory disease indicating poorly controlled uraemia or diabetes","signs of secondary bacterial infection from excoriation"],"prognosis":"The condition tends to be chronic and relapsing, with individual follicular papules resolving over weeks while new lesions continue to appear; improvement generally follows better control of the underlying systemic disease and reduction of scratching.","pitfalls":["misdiagnosing pruritic follicular papules as ordinary keratosis pilaris or infective folliculitis and missing the perforating pathology","not sectioning the biopsy through the follicle, so the follicular perforation is not visualised","overlooking associated chronic kidney disease or diabetes","relying on antibacterial therapy alone when the process is a non-infective perforating dermatosis"],"management":["Optimise control of the underlying chronic kidney disease and diabetes mellitus that drive the eruption","Control pruritus and interrupt the itch-scratch cycle with emollients, topical corticosteroids and antihistamines","Use keratolytics and topical retinoids to address the follicular hyperkeratosis","Consider narrowband ultraviolet B phototherapy for widespread or refractory pruritic disease","Consider oral retinoids or allopurinol in resistant cases","Treat secondary infection of excoriated lesions where present","Refer to dermatology and liaise with nephrology in dialysis patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology","Weedon's Skin Pathology"],"crossLinks":[]},"perianal_abscess":{"name":"Perianal abscess","system":"Colorectal / Pelvic floor","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anorectal abscess","perirectal abscess","ischiorectal abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Represents the acute suppurative phase of cryptoglandular anal gland infection, of which anal fistula is the chronic sequel","Presents with severe, constant, throbbing perianal pain, a tender fluctuant swelling, erythema, and often fever","Classified by the anatomical space involved: perianal (most common), ischiorectal (ischioanal), intersphincteric, and supralevator","Deep abscesses (intersphincteric or supralevator) may cause severe pain and sepsis with little visible external swelling, requiring examination under anaesthesia and imaging","The mainstay of treatment is prompt incision and drainage; antibiotics alone are inadequate and delay is dangerous","A significant proportion of drained abscesses subsequently develop a fistula-in-ano","Recurrent or atypical abscesses should prompt consideration of Crohn's disease, diabetes, immunosuppression, or malignancy","A spreading, boggy, or crepitant perineum with systemic toxicity suggests necrotising infection (Fournier's gangrene), a surgical emergency"],"pathophysiology":"A perianal abscess usually begins with obstruction and infection of an anal gland in the intersphincteric plane (the cryptoglandular mechanism). Pus collects and tracks along planes of least resistance, caudally to the perianal skin, laterally through the external sphincter into the ischioanal fossa, or cephalad above the levators, forming abscesses in the corresponding spaces. It represents the acute phase of the same disease that, once drained or spontaneously discharging, may leave a chronic fistula. Diabetes, immunosuppression, and Crohn's disease predispose to more extensive or recurrent infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Thrombosed external haemorrhoid","Pilonidal abscess","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Bartholin's abscess in women","Fournier's gangrene or other necrotising perineal infection","Anal canal or rectal malignancy presenting with sepsis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination under anaesthesia to locate and drain deep collections","MRI of the pelvis for supralevator, complex, or recurrent collections","Endoanal ultrasound to assess deep or intersphincteric collections","Blood glucose and HbA1c, with screening for immunosuppression in recurrent disease","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers if systemic sepsis is suspected","Culture of drained pus and, in atypical cases, biopsy of the abscess wall"],"redFlags":["Spreading erythema, crepitus, or perineal necrosis suggesting Fournier's gangrene","Systemic sepsis, or diabetes or immunosuppression with pain out of proportion to findings","Severe deep pain with minimal external signs suggesting an intersphincteric or supralevator abscess","Recurrent abscesses suggesting Crohn's disease or underlying malignancy"],"prognosis":"Prompt incision and drainage resolves most simple abscesses, though a notable minority progress to a chronic fistula; delayed treatment or necrotising infection carries substantial morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Relying on antibiotics instead of timely surgical drainage","Missing a deep intersphincteric or supralevator collection because external signs are minimal","Inadequate drainage leaving a loculated cavity and early recurrence","Failing to recognise early Fournier's gangrene in a diabetic or immunosuppressed patient"],"management":["Perform prompt incision and drainage as the definitive treatment, as antibiotics alone are inadequate","Undertake examination under anaesthesia to locate and drain deep intersphincteric or supralevator collections","Reserve antibiotics for systemic sepsis, surrounding cellulitis, immunosuppression or diabetes","Provide analgesia, warm baths and wound care with healing by secondary intention","Send drained pus for culture and biopsy the abscess wall in atypical cases","Screen for and optimise diabetes, immunosuppression and Crohn's disease in recurrent or extensive infection","Follow up for fistula-in-ano, which develops in a proportion of drained abscesses","Escalate urgently if necrotising perianal infection is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","Keighley & Williams' Surgery of the Anus, Rectum and Colon"],"crossLinks":[]},"perianal_fistula":{"name":"Perianal fistula","system":"Colorectal surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fistula-in-ano","anal fistula","anorectal fistula","perianal fistula-in-ano"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fistula-in-ano is an abnormal track lined by granulation tissue connecting the anal canal or rectum to the perianal skin, usually the sequel of a drained or spontaneously discharging anorectal abscess","Typical symptoms are recurrent perianal discharge, intermittent pain and swelling that flares when the external opening blocks, and pruritus, often with a history of a previous abscess","The Parks classification describes tracks in relation to the sphincters as intersphincteric, transsphincteric, suprasphincteric, and extrasphincteric","Goodsall rule helps predict the internal opening: anterior openings tend to track radially, while posterior openings tend to curve to a posterior midline internal opening","MRI of the pelvis and endoanal ultrasound accurately map complex tracks, secondary extensions, and the relationship to the sphincter complex before surgery","The therapeutic balance is eradicating the fistula while preserving continence; low simple tracks may be laid open, whereas tracks involving significant sphincter muscle need sphincter-preserving techniques or a seton","Recurrent, multiple, or complex fistulae should raise suspicion of Crohn disease, tuberculosis, or malignancy and prompt appropriate investigation"],"pathophysiology":"Most fistulae-in-ano arise from the cryptoglandular theory: infection of an anal gland in the intersphincteric plane at the level of the dentate line forms an abscess, which discharges and leaves a persistent epithelialising or granulating track between the internal opening in the anal canal and an external opening on the perianal skin. The course of the track relative to the internal and external anal sphincters determines its classification and surgical difficulty. Secondary tracks and horseshoe extensions can develop. A minority result from specific conditions such as Crohn disease, tuberculosis, pelvic sepsis, radiation, or malignancy, which alter management and prognosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Perianal or ischiorectal abscess","Pilonidal sinus disease","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Crohn perianal disease","Anal fistula secondary to tuberculosis","Anorectal malignancy or fistulating carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination under anaesthesia to define the tracks and internal opening","MRI of the pelvis for complex or recurrent fistulae","Endoanal ultrasound to map tracks and sphincter involvement","Colonoscopy or ileocolonoscopy when Crohn disease is suspected","Biopsy of atypical or non-healing tracks to exclude malignancy or tuberculosis","Anorectal physiology and continence assessment when division of sphincter is contemplated"],"redFlags":["Spreading perianal sepsis, cellulitis, or features of necrotising infection (Fournier gangrene) requiring emergency surgery","Systemic sepsis from an undrained abscess","Suspicion of underlying malignancy in a chronic, indurated, or non-healing fistula","Extensive or multiple complex tracks suggesting Crohn disease with risk to continence"],"prognosis":"Simple low fistulae have a high cure rate after laying open with preservation of continence, whereas complex, high, or recurrent tracks carry greater risk of recurrence and incontinence and often require staged, sphincter-preserving strategies. Fistulae associated with Crohn disease are more difficult to heal and tend to recur, and outcomes depend on control of the underlying disease.","pitfalls":["Dividing significant sphincter muscle and causing incontinence, particularly in anterior fistulae in women or high tracks","Missing a secondary track or the true internal opening, leading to early recurrence","Laying open a fistula in a patient with undiagnosed Crohn disease with poor healing","Treating an abscess without recognising or planning for an underlying fistula","Failing to biopsy a chronic, atypical, or non-healing track and missing malignancy"],"management":["Map the track with examination under anaesthesia, MRI or endoanal ultrasound before definitive surgery","Lay open low, simple tracks by fistulotomy where sphincter involvement is minimal","Use sphincter-preserving techniques such as a seton, the LIFT procedure, an advancement flap or a fistula plug for high or complex tracks to protect continence","Place a draining seton to control sepsis before definitive repair","Treat Crohn-related fistulae medically with anti-tumour-necrosis-factor biologics alongside surgical drainage","Investigate and treat underlying Crohn disease, tuberculosis or malignancy in atypical tracks","Assess anorectal physiology and continence before dividing any sphincter muscle","Refer to colorectal surgery for co-ordinated management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"perianal_haematoma":{"name":"Perianal haematoma","system":"Gastrointestinal / Anorectal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thrombosed external haemorrhoid","external anal thrombosis","perianal thrombosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A perianal haematoma is an acute, painful, subcutaneous clot at the anal verge, often used interchangeably with a thrombosed external haemorrhoid; it is sometimes called a 'five-day painful pile' because pain peaks early and settles over several days","It presents as a sudden, tense, tender, blue-purple lump at the anal margin, typically after straining, heavy lifting, constipation, or childbirth","The lesion lies below the dentate line in somatically innervated tissue, which accounts for its marked pain, in contrast to painless internal haemorrhoidal bleeding","Diagnosis is clinical on inspection; a smooth, tense, dark subcutaneous swelling at the verge is characteristic and does not require imaging","Presenting early (within the first couple of days) while pain is severe favours evacuation or excision of the clot for rapid relief; later presentation with easing pain favours conservative management","Conservative care comprises analgesia, warm baths, stool softening, topical measures, and reassurance, with most resolving spontaneously and sometimes leaving a residual skin tag","Simple incision that only decompresses the clot has a higher recurrence and residual tag rate than complete excision of the thrombosed segment under local anaesthesia","Recurrent or atypical lesions should prompt consideration of underlying causes and exclusion of other perianal pathology"],"pathophysiology":"A perianal haematoma arises from rupture of a subcutaneous vein or venule of the external haemorrhoidal plexus, or from thrombosis within it, producing a clot within the perianal subcutaneous tissue below the dentate line. A sudden rise in venous pressure, typically from straining at stool, heavy lifting, coughing, or the second stage of labour, precipitates the event. The confined subcutaneous space and the tense clot stretch the richly innervated anoderm, generating acute severe pain that peaks within the first days and then eases as the clot organises and is gradually resorbed. Overlying skin may ulcerate and discharge the clot spontaneously. As the haematoma resolves, fibrosis of the redundant stretched skin may leave a residual anal skin tag.","additionalDifferentials":["Prolapsed and thrombosed internal or mixed haemorrhoids","Perianal or ischiorectal abscess","Anal skin tag (non-acute, non-tender)","Anal carcinoma or a pigmented perianal lesion such as melanoma","Condylomata or other perianal skin lesion","Anal fissure with sentinel tag","Pilonidal or perianal cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Inspection and gentle digital examination to confirm a tense subcutaneous clot at the verge","Proctoscopy once acute pain allows, to assess for internal haemorrhoids","Histology of excised tissue if the lesion is atypical or pigmented","Assessment for constipation and straining as contributory factors","Coagulation review if there are recurrent or spontaneous thromboses","Exclusion of abscess by examination and, if needed, imaging when infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Signs of abscess such as spreading erythema, fluctuance, fever, or systemic upset","A pigmented, indurated, or ulcerated lesion suggesting malignancy or melanoma","Recurrent or multiple spontaneous thromboses suggesting a bleeding or clotting disorder","Severe or worsening pain with systemic features","Non-healing ulceration after clot discharge"],"prognosis":"A perianal haematoma is benign and self-limiting; pain typically resolves within days to a week whether managed conservatively or by excision, and early excision gives the fastest relief with a low recurrence rate, though a residual skin tag may remain.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with a perianal abscess and incising into infection or vice versa","Performing simple incision rather than complete excision, leading to recurrence and residual tag","Attempting evacuation late when the clot has organised and pain is already settling","Missing a pigmented malignant lesion masquerading as a thrombosis","Neglecting to address constipation and straining, allowing recurrence"],"management":["Excise or evacuate the clot for rapid relief when the patient presents early with severe pain","Manage conservatively with analgesia, warm baths, stool softeners and topical measures when presenting later as pain eases","Prevent recurrence by treating constipation and avoiding straining, with dietary fibre and adequate hydration","Reassure regarding spontaneous resolution, noting a residual skin tag may remain","Exclude infection and, once acute pain settles, perform proctoscopy to assess internal haemorrhoids","Review coagulation if thromboses are recurrent or spontaneous"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","Association of Coloproctology of Great Britain and Ireland guidance on haemorrhoidal disease","Standard coloproctology reference texts on acute anal conditions"],"crossLinks":[]},"periapical_abscess":{"name":"Periapical abscess","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dentoalveolar abscess","apical abscess","acute periapical abscess","dental abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A periapical abscess is a localised collection of pus at the root apex arising from a necrotic, infected pulp","Classic features are severe, well-localised pain, tenderness to percussion, and a feeling that the tooth is extruded or high on biting","The affected tooth is typically non-vital, so pulp sensibility testing is negative while percussion and palpation are markedly positive","Pus may drain spontaneously through a sinus tract (gum boil), which can relieve pain and produce a chronic, less symptomatic picture","Early radiographs may look normal because bone changes lag behind, whereas chronic lesions show a periapical radiolucency","Primary management is drainage and elimination of the source through root canal treatment or extraction; antibiotics are adjuncts reserved for spreading infection or systemic involvement","Infection can track along fascial planes, so the pattern of swelling depends on tooth position relative to muscle attachments","Recurrent or persistent sinus tracts indicate ongoing infection needing definitive endodontic or surgical treatment"],"pathophysiology":"A periapical abscess develops when pulp necrosis, usually secondary to caries or trauma, allows bacteria and their toxins to egress through the apical foramen into the periapical tissues. The host inflammatory response produces a purulent collection localised to the periradicular bone. Rising pressure causes intense pain and may resorb surrounding bone, seen later as a radiolucency. Pus follows the path of least resistance, perforating cortical bone and tracking into soft tissue, where the relationship of the perforation to muscle attachments determines whether a localised vestibular swelling, a draining sinus, or a spreading fascial-space infection results.","additionalDifferentials":["Periodontal abscess arising from the gingival sulcus","Irreversible pulpitis without abscess formation","Periapical granuloma or radicular cyst","Osteomyelitis of the jaw","Odontogenic infection of adjacent teeth or sinus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pulp sensibility testing (expected non-vital response)","Percussion, palpation, and mobility assessment","Periapical radiography and, when needed, cone-beam CT for extent","Assessment for fascial-space spread and airway compromise in swelling","Culture and systemic markers if severe or spreading infection"],"redFlags":["Rapidly spreading swelling of the floor of mouth or submandibular region raising concern for Ludwig angina and airway obstruction","Trismus, difficulty swallowing, drooling, or voice change indicating deep-space infection","Systemic sepsis, high fever, or periorbital/cavernous sinus spread from upper anterior teeth","Immunocompromised or diabetic patients in whom infection can progress dangerously fast"],"prognosis":"With timely drainage and source control the prognosis is excellent, but delayed treatment risks spread to fascial spaces, osteomyelitis, airway compromise, or life-threatening sepsis.","pitfalls":["Prescribing antibiotics without draining the abscess or removing the source","Being reassured by a normal early radiograph despite clear clinical signs","Missing progression to a deep-space or airway-threatening infection","Confusing a periodontal abscess with a periapical abscess and mistreating the cause"],"management":["Achieve source control by root canal treatment or extraction of the necrotic, infected tooth","Establish drainage of pus through the tooth or by incision of a fluctuant swelling for immediate relief","Reserve antibiotics as an adjunct for spreading infection, systemic involvement or immunocompromise","Provide analgesia for pain control","Assess for fascial-space spread and airway compromise, escalating urgently if present","Refer to dentistry or oral and maxillofacial surgery for definitive management","Provide restorative dental follow-up and caries prevention to reduce recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"periarticular_disorders_of_the_extremities":{"name":"Periarticular Disorders of the Extremities","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Bursitis","Rotator cuff tendinitis","Impingement syndrome","Frozen shoulder","Adhesive capsulitis","Tennis elbow","Golfer's elbow","De Quervain's tenosynovitis","Plantar fasciitis","Iliotibial band syndrome","Calcific tendinitis"],"chapter":387,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2973","clinicalPearls":["Periarticular disorders are common soft-tissue musculoskeletal problems affecting people of any age, arising from structures around (rather than within) the joint such as bursae, tendons, fascia, and connective-tissue bands.","Bursitis is inflammation of a synovial-lined sac; adults have over 150 bursae that ease gliding of tendons and muscles over bony prominences, and overuse, trauma, systemic disease (rheumatoid arthritis, gout), or infection can trigger inflammation.","Subacromial (subdeltoid) bursitis is the most common bursitis and frequently coexists with rotator cuff tendinitis, presenting with anterolateral shoulder pain.","Acutely inflamed olecranon or prepatellar bursitis with warmth/redness warrants aspiration with Gram stain, culture, and crystal examination to exclude infection or gout before assuming a mechanical cause.","Rotator cuff tendinitis (impingement syndrome) is the leading cause of a painful shoulder; the supraspinatus tendon is most often affected due to impingement between the humeral head and the anterior acromion/coracoacromial ligament, classically producing a painful abduction arc of 60-120 degrees.","Calcific tendinitis reflects hydroxyapatite (calcium salt) deposition within a tendon, most often the supraspinatus (involved in about 63% of cases), typically after age 40, and may be asymptomatic or cause sudden severe shoulder pain.","De Quervain's tenosynovitis involves the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis at the radial styloid; a positive Finkelstein test (thumb tucked in fist with ulnar wrist deviation) reproduces radial styloid pain.","Adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder) causes painful, globally restricted active and passive shoulder motion, usually without intrinsic joint disease, and is associated with diabetes, thyroid disease, chronic pulmonary disease, and prior myocardial infarction.","Lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow) arises from repetitive resisted wrist extension/supination; most patients are injured by activities other than tennis such as pulling weeds, carrying briefcases, or using a screwdriver.","Plantar fasciitis classically causes severe heel pain with the first steps in the morning or after rest, easing with initial activity then worsening with prolonged weight-bearing; tenderness localizes to the medial calcaneal attachment of the fascia.","Drug-induced tendinopathy is increasingly recognized, with quinolones, glucocorticoids, aromatase inhibitors, and statins implicated; the Achilles tendon is most often affected and rupture can be the presenting event.","Yergason's test (pain on resisted forearm supination with the elbow at 90 degrees) supports bicipital tendinitis; biceps tendon rupture in older patients may be painless and recognized by a retracted bulge of the long head."],"pathophysiology":"These are disorders of the soft tissues surrounding joints rather than the synovial joint itself. Bursitis is synovial inflammation of a bursa from frictional overuse, trauma, systemic inflammatory/crystal disease, or infection. Tendinopathies (rotator cuff, bicipital, patellar, epicondylar, De Quervain's) result from mechanical impingement and repetitive resisted loading producing microtears, inflammation, and degeneration at tendon insertions; the supraspinatus is uniquely vulnerable to impingement beneath the acromion. Calcific tendinitis is caused by hydroxyapatite deposition within tendon substance through poorly understood phases of calcification that can incite acute inflammation. Adhesive capsulitis features a thickened shoulder capsule with mild chronic inflammatory infiltrate and fibrosis, often after a period of immobility. Plantar fasciitis arises at the fascial origin on the medial calcaneal tuberosity from cumulative loading. Drug-induced tendinopathies have an unknown mechanism but are linked to specific drug classes.","additionalDifferentials":["Glenohumeral osteoarthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Gout and crystal arthropathy","Spondyloarthritis","Cervical radiculopathy referring to the shoulder","Acromioclavicular joint pathology","Intraarticular knee pathology (osteoarthritis, meniscal injury) versus periarticular knee pain"],"infectionMimics":["Septic bursitis (e.g., olecranon or prepatellar)","Infectious tenosynovitis","Septic arthritis presenting as periarticular pain and swelling"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Gouty bursitis/tendinitis (urate deposition)","Calcium pyrophosphate or apatite crystal deposition","Rheumatoid or inflammatory arthritis involving periarticular structures","Referred pain from intraarticular degenerative joint disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["History and directed physical examination (often sufficient for diagnosis)","Provocative maneuvers (painful abduction arc, drop-arm test, Finkelstein test, Yergason's test)","Bursal aspiration with Gram stain, culture, and crystal analysis when infection or gout is suspected","Ultrasound (for bursitis, tendinitis, rotator cuff tears, calcific deposits, plantar fascia thickening, and guided injection)","Plain radiographs (may show tendon calcification or incidental heel spurs)","MRI (sensitive for rotator cuff tears, tendon integrity, and plantar fasciitis when diagnosis is unclear)"],"redFlags":["Acutely warm, red, tender bursa suggesting septic bursitis or gout requiring aspiration","Weakness of abduction/external rotation or a positive drop-arm test suggesting a rotator cuff tear","Acute tendon rupture (e.g., Achilles in drug-induced tendinopathy, biceps in active patients)","Globally restricted active and passive shoulder motion suggesting adhesive capsulitis rather than a focal tendinitis","Periarticular pain in the setting of systemic disease (rheumatoid arthritis, diabetes, thyroid disease) prompting evaluation of the underlying condition"],"prognosis":"Most periarticular disorders are self-limited and respond to conservative measures. Adhesive capsulitis usually improves spontaneously over 1-3 years, though many patients retain some residual loss of shoulder motion. More than 80% of plantar fasciitis cases resolve within 12 months. Calcific tendinitis is largely self-limited. Rotator cuff tears vary in outcome depending on tear size, chronicity, pain, functional loss, and coexisting osteoarthritis.","pitfalls":["Anserine bursitis is a frequently overlooked periarticular cause of knee pain.","Assuming inflamed olecranon or prepatellar bursitis is mechanical without excluding infection or gout by aspiration.","Mislabeling globally restricted shoulder motion as simple tendinitis when it is adhesive capsulitis.","Attributing heel pain to a radiographic heel spur, which is of little diagnostic significance in plantar fasciitis.","Overlooking medication as a cause of tendon pain or rupture (drug-induced tendinopathy), and reintroducing the offending agent.","Confusing iliopsoas bursitis pain with pain originating within the hip joint itself."],"management":["Advise relative rest, activity modification and avoidance of aggravating repetitive loading","Give simple analgesia and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain and inflammation","Prescribe physiotherapy with graded stretching and strengthening, particularly for rotator cuff tendinopathy and adhesive capsulitis","Aspirate acutely inflamed olecranon or prepatellar bursitis and send fluid for Gram stain, culture and crystal analysis to exclude infection or gout before any corticosteroid injection","Offer image-guided local corticosteroid injection for persistent bursitis or tendinopathy","Consider ultrasound-guided barbotage or extracorporeal shockwave therapy for symptomatic calcific tendinitis","Reserve surgery such as subacromial decompression, cuff repair or capsular release for cases refractory to conservative care","Treat septic bursitis with antibiotics and drainage"],"severityClassification":"No formal grading system; rotator cuff tears are clinically characterized as acute, subacute, or chronic and as partial- or full-thickness, with management guided by tear severity, symptom duration, pain, functional loss, and coexisting osteoarthritis.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2973","Harrison 22e p.2974","Harrison 22e p.2975"],"crossLinks":[]},"pericardial_cyst":{"name":"pericardial cyst","system":"cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pleuropericardial cyst","spring-water cyst","mesothelial pericardial cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A pericardial cyst is a benign, thin-walled, mesothelium-lined fluid-filled sac that usually does not communicate with the pericardial cavity, most often congenital in origin.","The classic location is the right cardiophrenic angle (roughly two-thirds of cases), with the left cardiophrenic angle next most common; atypical superior mediastinal locations occur but are rare.","Most are asymptomatic and discovered incidentally on chest radiography or cross-sectional imaging performed for another reason.","When symptomatic, patients may report atypical chest discomfort, dyspnoea or cough from local compression; serious complications such as compression of a cardiac chamber, rupture or infection are uncommon.","The typical 'spring-water' cyst contains clear serous fluid; the term reflects the classic clear appearance of aspirated contents.","CT shows a well-defined, non-enhancing, homogeneous water-attenuation lesion abutting the pericardium; MRI demonstrates low T1 and high T2 signal without contrast enhancement of the contents.","Asymptomatic, radiologically characteristic cysts can generally be managed conservatively with periodic imaging surveillance rather than intervention.","Intervention (percutaneous aspiration or, more definitively, surgical/thoracoscopic excision) is reserved for symptomatic, enlarging, complicated or diagnostically uncertain lesions."],"pathophysiology":"Pericardial cysts are thought to arise from aberrant embryological development of the pericardial coelom, in which one of the mesenchymal lacunae that normally coalesce to form the pericardial sac fails to fuse and instead persists as an isolated, mesothelium-lined cavity. The resulting cyst has a thin fibrous wall lined by a single layer of mesothelial cells and typically contains clear serous fluid. Because it usually does not communicate with the pericardial space, it enlarges slowly by fluid secretion and is generally of little haemodynamic consequence. Rarely, acquired cysts follow inflammation, cardiac surgery or trauma. Mass effect, when it occurs, results from mechanical compression of adjacent structures rather than any intrinsic secretory or malignant behaviour.","additionalDifferentials":["bronchogenic cyst","thymic cyst","loculated pericardial effusion","prominent epicardial or mediastinal fat pad","cardiophrenic lymphadenopathy","morgagni diaphragmatic hernia","cystic mediastinal teratoma or other mediastinal mass"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["contrast-enhanced CT of the thorax (water attenuation, non-enhancing, well-circumscribed)","cardiac or thoracic MRI (low T1, high T2 signal, no enhancement)","transthoracic echocardiography (echo-free space adjacent to the heart)","chest radiography (smooth, rounded cardiophrenic-angle opacity)","fluid analysis if aspirated (clear serous, benign mesothelial cells)","PET imaging in selected cases to exclude a metabolically active/malignant mass","interval imaging surveillance to document stability"],"redFlags":["rapid enlargement or interval change on serial imaging","haemodynamic compromise or arrhythmia from cardiac chamber compression","features suggesting infection of the cyst (fever, wall enhancement, surrounding inflammation)","imaging characteristics atypical for a simple cyst (thick or enhancing wall, solid components, septations)","acute chest pain with sudden change in cyst size suggesting rupture"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; most pericardial cysts are benign, remain stable and never cause symptoms, and complete surgical excision when required is generally curative with a low recurrence rate.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a loculated pericardial effusion or a solid/malignant mediastinal mass for a simple benign cyst without cross-sectional confirmation.","Proceeding to surgery for an incidental, asymptomatic, radiologically classic cyst that could be safely observed.","Overlooking rare but important complications such as compression, infection or spontaneous rupture in a symptomatic patient.","Relying on percutaneous aspiration alone, after which recurrence is common because the secretory lining remains.","Failing to consider a Morgagni hernia, which can mimic a right cardiophrenic-angle cyst."],"management":["Manage asymptomatic, incidentally discovered cysts conservatively with reassurance, as most remain stable and benign","Reserve intervention for symptomatic cysts causing atypical chest discomfort, dyspnoea or cough, or for compression of an adjacent cardiac chamber or airway","Offer image-guided percutaneous aspiration of the clear serous cyst fluid as a less invasive option, accepting a risk of recurrence","Undertake complete surgical excision (thoracoscopic or open) for symptomatic, enlarging, complicated (rupture or infection) or diagnostically uncertain cysts, which is generally curative","Monitor conservatively managed cysts with serial cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) or echocardiography to detect enlargement or complications","Involve cardiothoracic surgery and use PET imaging when a metabolically active or malignant mass cannot be excluded"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Pericardial Diseases","Feigin DS et al. Pericardial cysts, Radiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pericardial_disease":{"name":"Pericardial Disease","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["constrictive pericarditis","pericardial effusion"],"chapter":281,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2078-2084","clinicalPearls":["Acute pericarditis has four principal diagnostic features: chest pain, a pericardial friction rub, characteristic ECG changes, and pericardial effusion (Harrison 22e p.2078).","Pericarditic chest pain is typically retrosternal/left precordial, often pleuritic, may radiate to the trapezius ridge, and is characteristically intensified by lying supine and relieved by sitting up and leaning forward (Harrison 22e p.2079).","A pericardial friction rub is audible at some point in approximately 85% of patients with acute pericarditis; it may have up to three components per cycle and is best heard at end expiration with the patient upright and leaning forward (Harrison 22e p.2079).","The ECG of acute pericarditis classically evolves through four stages: stage 1 widespread upwardly-concave ST elevation with PR-segment depression, stage 2 normalization of ST, stage 3 T-wave inversion, and stage 4 return to normal (Harrison 22e p.2079).","Beck's triad of cardiac tamponade is hypotension, soft or absent heart sounds, and jugular venous distention with a prominent x descent but an absent y descent (Harrison 22e p.2081).","Pulsus paradoxus (inspiratory fall in systolic pressure >10 mmHg) is an important clue to tamponade, but also occurs in about one-third of constrictive pericarditis cases and in hypovolemic shock, obstructive airway disease, and pulmonary embolism (Harrison 22e p.2081-2082).","In constrictive pericarditis the rigid thickened pericardium limits ventricular filling only after early diastole (unimpeded in early diastole, then abruptly reduced), whereas in tamponade filling is impeded throughout diastole (Harrison 22e p.2083).","Kussmaul's sign (failure of venous pressure to decline, or a rise, during inspiration) is common in chronic constrictive pericarditis but is also seen in tricuspid stenosis, right ventricular infarction, and restrictive cardiomyopathy; it is negative in cor pulmonale (Harrison 22e p.2083-2084).","A pericardial knock, an early third heart sound at the apex from abrupt cessation of ventricular filling, is often conspicuous in constrictive pericarditis (Harrison 22e p.2083).","Constrictive pericarditis produces an M-shaped atrial pressure contour with prominent x and y descents; the y descent (diminished/absent in tamponade) is the most prominent deflection, and the ventricular pressure pulse shows a 'square root' sign in diastole (Harrison 22e p.2083).","In developing nations a high percentage of chronic constrictive pericarditis is tuberculous, but TB is now an uncommon cause in North America and Western Europe; other causes include prior viral/idiopathic pericarditis, cardiac surgery, mediastinal irradiation, and neoplasm (Harrison 22e p.2083)."],"pathophysiology":"The pericardium is a double-layered sac (visceral and parietal layers) separated by 15-50 mL of plasma ultrafiltrate; it restrains sudden cardiac chamber dilation and fixes cardiac position. In acute pericarditis, inflammation produces fibrinous or effusive disease. Cardiac tamponade occurs when fluid accumulates in the pericardial space in quantity sufficient to obstruct ventricular inflow, raising intrapericardial pressure and limiting diastolic filling throughout diastole, reducing stroke volume, cardiac output, and arterial pressure. As little as 200 mL can cause tamponade when fluid develops rapidly, versus >2000 mL when it accumulates slowly and the pericardium stretches. In chronic constrictive pericarditis, healing of fibrinous/serofibrinous pericarditis or resorption of effusion produces granulation tissue that contracts into a rigid, sometimes calcified scar encasing the heart; ventricular filling is unimpeded early in diastole then abruptly halts at the pericardium's elastic limit. End-diastolic pressures in both ventricles and mean atrial, pulmonary venous, and systemic venous pressures equalize within ~5 mmHg, while resting systolic function is typically normal or only mildly impaired (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2081, p.2083).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myocardial infarction (common confusion with acute pericarditis; in pericarditis ST elevations are upwardly concave, biomarker elevations are modest, and pain may precede or coincide with fever) (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2083)","Restrictive cardiomyopathy (shares restriction of ventricular filling; must be distinguished from constrictive pericarditis because the latter is surgically correctable) (Harrison 22e p.2084)","Right ventricular infarction (may mimic tamponade with hypotension, elevated JVP, slow y descent, occasional paradoxical pulse) (Harrison 22e p.2082)","Cor pulmonale (systemic venous hypertension and paradoxical pulse, but Kussmaul's sign is negative and advanced parenchymal lung disease is apparent) (Harrison 22e p.2084)","Tricuspid stenosis (congestive hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, ascites, venous distention, but characteristic murmur present) (Harrison 22e p.2084)","Hepatic cirrhosis (constrictive pericarditis with hepatomegaly, jaundice, and intractable ascites may be mistaken for cirrhosis; inspection of distended neck veins avoids the error) (Harrison 22e p.2084)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral pericarditis (coxsackievirus A and B, echovirus, influenza, mumps, herpes simplex, varicella-zoster, adenovirus, cytomegalovirus, HIV, post-acute COVID, mpox) (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2082)","Tuberculous pericarditis (effusion, constriction, or effusive-constrictive disease; suggested by high adenosine deaminase or M. tuberculosis DNA by PCR, often requiring pericardial tissue) (Harrison 22e p.2082, p.2084)","Pyogenic/purulent bacterial pericarditis (pneumococcus, Streptococcus, Staphylococcus, Neisseria, Legionella, Chlamydia; from cardiothoracic surgery, lung/pleural extension, esophageal rupture, or valve-ring abscess; fever, chills, septicemia, poor prognosis) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Fungal pericarditis (histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis, blastomycosis, aspergillosis, candidiasis) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Parasitic/protozoal and syphilitic pericarditis (amebiasis, toxoplasmosis, echinococcosis, trichinosis) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Uremic and dialysis-associated pericarditis (renal failure; fibrinous, serosanguinous effusions, friction rub common but pain usually mild) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Neoplastic pericarditis (lung and breast carcinoma, melanoma, lymphoma, leukemia; metastatic invasion, primary tumors, mesothelioma) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Postcardiac injury syndrome (postpericardiotomy, posttraumatic, post-MI Dressler's syndrome; hypersensitivity/autoimmune reaction to myocardial antigens 1-4 weeks after injury) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2082)","Collagen vascular / autoimmune disease (SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, scleroderma, ankylosing spondylitis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, IgG4 disease, rheumatic fever, polyarteritis nodosa) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Drug-induced pericarditis (procainamide, hydralazine, phenytoin, isoniazid, minoxidil, anticoagulants, methysergide) (Harrison 22e p.2078)","Postirradiation (mediastinal radiation) pericarditis, acute or chronic constrictive (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Aortic dissection with leakage into the pericardial sac (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2081)","Acute myocardial infarction and metabolic causes (myxedema, cholesterol pericarditis) (Harrison 22e p.2078)","Familial Mediterranean fever and other autoinflammatory syndromes (Harrison 22e p.2078)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography: most widely used, sensitive, specific, bedside-capable; localizes and estimates pericardial fluid and shows late-diastolic collapse of the RV free wall and right atrium in tamponade (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2082)","Doppler echocardiography: in tamponade and constriction, inspiratory increase in tricuspid/pulmonic flow velocities with decrease in pulmonary venous/mitral/aortic velocities and a leftward septal shift (respirophasic variation) (Harrison 22e p.2082, p.2084)","CT and MRI: superior for loculated effusions, pericardial thickening, pericardial masses, calcification, and detecting pericardial inflammation; more accurate than echocardiography for confirming or excluding constriction (MRI assesses myocardial involvement) (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2084)","ECG: stage 1-4 evolution in acute pericarditis; low QRS voltage and electrical alternans with large effusion/tamponade; low voltage and diffuse T-wave changes with atrial fibrillation in constriction (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2081, p.2083)","Chest radiograph: enlarged cardiac silhouette with a 'water bottle' configuration in large effusion; pericardial calcification (most common in tuberculous constriction) (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2084)","Pericardiocentesis with fluid analysis: cell counts, cytology for neoplastic cells, cultures, M. tuberculosis PCR and adenosine deaminase (Harrison 22e p.2082)","Pericardial biopsy (e.g., via limited thoracotomy) when fluid analysis is non-diagnostic, especially for suspected tuberculous or neoplastic disease (Harrison 22e p.2082, p.2084)","Laboratory: elevated C-reactive protein and white blood cell count in viral/idiopathic pericarditis (Harrison 22e p.2082)"],"redFlags":["Cardiac tamponade: hypotension, elevated jugular venous pressure, and unexplained sudden enlargement of the cardiac silhouette; may be rapidly fatal if not recognized and treated promptly (Harrison 22e p.2081)","Reduced QRS amplitude with electrical alternans of P, QRS, or T waves raises suspicion of tamponade (Harrison 22e p.2081)","Predictors of poor prognosis warranting hospitalization in acute pericarditis: fever >38C, subacute onset, large pericardial effusion, immunosuppression, or evidence of tamponade (Harrison 22e p.2080)","Purulent (pyogenic) pericarditis carries a poor prognosis and requires immediate drainage and antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.2083)","Without treatment, tuberculous pericardial disease can progress to constriction; constriction should be treated surgically (Harrison 22e p.2084)"],"prognosis":"Viral or idiopathic acute pericarditis usually runs its course in a few days to 4 weeks; recurrent (relapsing) pericarditis occurs in approximately one-fourth of patients, and both tamponade and constriction are possible but infrequent complications. Most patients with acute pericarditis can be managed as outpatients with careful follow-up. Untreated cardiac tamponade may be rapidly fatal. Purulent pericarditis generally has a poor prognosis. Without treatment, tuberculous pericardial disease may progress to constriction. Pericardial resection is the only definitive treatment of constrictive pericarditis, with benefits accruing progressively over months; operative mortality is in the range of 5-10% even at experienced centers, highest in the most severe disease, especially radiation-induced (Harrison 22e p.2080, p.2082, p.2083, p.2084).","pitfalls":["Distinguishing constrictive pericarditis from restrictive cardiomyopathy is critical because constriction is surgically correctable; their hemodynamics overlap (square-root sign and equalized diastolic pressures occur in both) and echocardiography alone cannot definitively establish or exclude constriction, so MRI/CT is needed (Harrison 22e p.2083, p.2084).","Acute pericarditis is commonly mistaken for AMI and vice versa; in pericarditis the ST elevations are upwardly concave, reciprocal depression is limited to aVR/V1, and biomarker rises (from epi-myocarditis) are modest relative to AMI (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2083).","Pericardial calcification may occur without constriction, and constriction may occur without calcification, so calcification neither confirms nor excludes the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2084).","Acute idiopathic pericarditis is a diagnosis of exclusion with no specific test; all other causes of fibrinous pericarditis must be considered (Harrison 22e p.2082-2083).","Constrictive pericarditis with hepatomegaly, jaundice, and intractable ascites can be misdiagnosed as hepatic cirrhosis unless the distended neck veins with characteristic waveform are recognized (Harrison 22e p.2084).","Pain is often absent in slowly developing tuberculous, postirradiation, neoplastic, and uremic pericarditis, so its absence does not exclude pericardial disease (Harrison 22e p.2079).","Anticoagulants should be avoided in acute pericarditis because they could cause bleeding into the pericardial cavity and tamponade (Harrison 22e p.2080)."],"management":["Treat acute idiopathic or viral pericarditis with a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug plus colchicine, the latter added to speed resolution and reduce recurrence","Reserve corticosteroids for refractory, recurrent, or specific-aetiology disease (autoimmune or uraemic) where anti-inflammatories are contraindicated or ineffective, and restrict strenuous exertion until symptoms and inflammatory markers settle","Direct therapy at the underlying cause: antituberous chemotherapy for tuberculous pericarditis, dialysis for uraemic disease, and drainage with antineoplastic treatment for malignant effusion","Treat cardiac tamponade as an emergency with urgent pericardiocentesis or surgical drainage to relieve obstruction to ventricular filling, supporting the circulation with intravenous fluids while awaiting drainage","Manage established constrictive pericarditis definitively with pericardiectomy, reserving a trial of anti-inflammatory therapy for transient constriction","Monitor for recurrence, effusion and evolving constriction with serial echocardiography and inflammatory markers","Refer to cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery for large or recurrent effusions, tamponade and constriction requiring intervention"],"severityClassification":"Clinical classification: I. Acute pericarditis (<6 weeks) - A. Fibrinous, B. Effusive (serous or sanguineous); II. Subacute pericarditis (6 weeks to 6 months) - A. Effusive-constrictive, B. Constrictive; III. Chronic pericarditis (>6 months) - A. Constrictive, B. Adhesive (nonconstrictive). Etiologic classification: infectious, noninfectious, and presumed autoimmune. Table 281-2 distinguishes tamponade, constrictive pericarditis, restrictive cardiomyopathy, right ventricular myocardial infarction, and effusive-constrictive pericarditis by clinical, ECG, echocardiographic, and CT/MRI features (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2080).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2078-2084","Harrison 22e Chapter 281, Pericardial Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"periductal_mastitis":{"name":"Periductal mastitis","system":"Breast","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Recurrent subareolar abscess","Periductal inflammation"],"chapter":null,"source":"ABC of Breast Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Inflammation centred on the subareolar ducts, typically affecting younger women in contrast to the older women affected by duct ectasia.","Very strongly associated with cigarette smoking, which is central to both causation and recurrence.","Presents with periareolar pain, inflammation, a tender subareolar mass or a recurrent non-lactational abscess.","Recurrent infection may discharge through the areolar margin and establish a mammary duct fistula between a subareolar duct and the skin.","Nipple retraction at the site of the affected duct can develop and may mimic malignant retraction.","Although classed as an inflammatory rather than infective process, secondary infection is often mixed, including anaerobes.","Acute episodes are managed with antibiotics active against anaerobes and drainage of any abscess, ideally by aspiration.","Definitive treatment of recurrent disease or fistula is total (major) duct excision, and smoking cessation is essential to reduce recurrence."],"pathophysiology":"Periductal mastitis is a chronic inflammatory disorder of the large subareolar ducts distinct from involutional duct ectasia. Damage to the duct epithelium, strongly linked to the toxic and ischaemic effects of cigarette smoke, allows leakage of ductal secretions into surrounding tissue and provokes a periductal inflammatory response. Secondary bacterial colonisation, frequently mixed aerobic and anaerobic, leads to recurrent subareolar abscess formation. Repeated inflammation and drainage through the areolar margin can create an epithelialised track, the mammary duct fistula, and periductal fibrosis may cause nipple retraction. Persistent smoking perpetuates the cycle and underlies the high recurrence rate.","additionalDifferentials":["Mammary duct ectasia","Lactational breast abscess","Inflammatory breast carcinoma","Tuberculous or granulomatous mastitis","Idiopathic granulomatous mastitis","Sebaceous or epidermoid cyst with infection","Hidradenitis suppurativa involving the areola"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment including detailed smoking history","Ultrasound of the subareolar region to detect an abscess or fistula","Aspiration of any collection with microbiological culture including anaerobes","Mammography in older patients or when malignancy is a concern","Core biopsy of any persistent mass or if inflammatory carcinoma is suspected","Fistulography or examination under anaesthesia for a suspected duct fistula","Skin swab and pus culture to guide antibiotic choice"],"redFlags":["Failure of an inflammatory episode to resolve, raising concern for inflammatory carcinoma","A persistent hard mass after resolution of inflammation","Diffuse skin oedema and peau d'orange","Recurrent abscess with an established mammary duct fistula","Associated axillary lymphadenopathy"],"prognosis":"Periductal mastitis is benign but notoriously prone to recurrence, particularly if smoking continues; established fistulae frequently recur until the affected duct system is excised. With smoking cessation and appropriate surgery the long-term outlook is good.","pitfalls":["Confusing periductal mastitis in a young smoker with involutional duct ectasia","Overlooking anaerobic cover when treating a non-lactational abscess","Repeated incision and drainage that promotes fistula formation instead of definitive duct excision","Mistaking non-resolving inflammation for infection and missing inflammatory carcinoma","Neglecting to address smoking, the key driver of recurrence"],"management":["Promote and support smoking cessation as the cornerstone of treatment, since cigarette smoke drives both causation and recurrence","Treat active infection with antibiotics covering mixed aerobic and anaerobic organisms, such as co-amoxiclav or a combination including metronidazole","Drain a subareolar abscess by needle aspiration or incision, sending pus for microbiological culture including anaerobes","Excise the diseased duct system (total duct excision or fistulectomy) for an established mammary duct fistula or recurrent disease","Exclude malignancy with imaging and core biopsy in older patients or where a persistent mass or nipple retraction mimics carcinoma","Provide analgesia and counsel on the high recurrence rate if smoking continues, and refer to breast surgery for recurrent or complicated disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ABC of Breast Diseases (current edition)","Standard textbook of breast surgery on non-lactational infection","National guidance on management of breast abscess and mastitis"],"crossLinks":[]},"perilymph_fistula":{"name":"Perilymph fistula","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Otology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLF","perilymphatic fistula","labyrinthine fistula","round window fistula","oval window fistula"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An abnormal communication between the perilymph-filled inner ear and the air-filled middle ear, most often at the round or oval window, allowing perilymph to leak","Typically presents with episodic vertigo or unsteadiness, fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss, aural fullness, and tinnitus","Pressure- or sound-provoked vertigo characteristically worsens with straining, coughing, sneezing, nose-blowing or Valsalva (Hennebert sign and Tullio phenomenon), as pressure changes are transmitted to the inner ear","A positive fistula test - nystagmus or vertigo provoked by pressurising the external canal (tragal pressure or pneumatic otoscopy) - supports the diagnosis","Often follows barotrauma from diving or flying, heavy lifting, head injury, or ear surgery such as stapedectomy, though it may occur spontaneously","A Hennebert sign (pressure-induced eye movement) and Tullio phenomenon (loud-sound-induced vertigo) may be present","Initial management is conservative with bed rest, head elevation, and avoidance of straining; persistent or severe cases undergo surgical exploration and sealing of the leak with a tissue graft","The diagnosis is largely clinical and can be hard to confirm, so a high index of suspicion after a precipitating event is important"],"pathophysiology":"The bony and membranous boundaries separating the perilymphatic space of the inner ear from the middle-ear cleft are normally intact. When this barrier is breached, usually at the round window membrane or the oval window and stapes footplate, perilymph escapes into the middle ear. Sudden changes in middle-ear or intracranial pressure - from barotrauma, straining, or trauma - are then abnormally transmitted to the cochlear and vestibular sensory organs, producing episodic vertigo, disequilibrium, and fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss. This pressure sensitivity explains why symptoms are provoked by Valsalva, loud sound (Tullio), and external-canal pressure (Hennebert). Continued leakage and the resulting disturbance of inner-ear fluid dynamics can lead to progressive cochleovestibular dysfunction if the defect fails to heal.","additionalDifferentials":["Meniere's disease","Superior semicircular canal dehiscence","Labyrinthine concussion","Vestibular neuronitis or labyrinthitis","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Autoimmune inner ear disease","Otosclerosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure-tone audiometry to characterise and monitor sensorineural hearing loss","Tympanometry and fistula test (pressure-induced nystagmus)","High-resolution CT of the temporal bone to assess the otic capsule and windows","Vestibular-evoked myogenic potentials to help exclude superior canal dehiscence","MRI to exclude a retrocochlear or central lesion","Electrocochleography where available to assess inner-ear function"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or profound sensorineural hearing loss","Features of meningitis, suggesting a communication with the subarachnoid space","Persistent disabling vertigo unresponsive to conservative measures","New cochleovestibular symptoms following recent stapes or other ear surgery"],"prognosis":"Many spontaneous or barotrauma-related fistulae settle with conservative measures, and surgical sealing is effective at relieving vertigo; hearing recovery is less predictable, and established sensorineural loss may be permanent.","pitfalls":["Attributing all post-traumatic or post-barotrauma dizziness to a fistula without excluding other vestibular disorders","Confusing it with superior semicircular canal dehiscence, which produces similar pressure- and sound-induced symptoms","Delaying exploration in a patient with progressive hearing loss after ear surgery","Relying on a negative fistula test to exclude the diagnosis, which remains largely clinical"],"management":["Trial conservative management first with bed rest, head elevation and avoidance of straining, heavy lifting, nose-blowing and pressure changes, as many fistulae heal spontaneously","Use stool softeners and vestibular sedatives or antiemetics for symptomatic relief of vertigo and to reduce Valsalva-provoked episodes","Undertake surgical exploration (tympanotomy) with grafting to seal the round or oval window for persistent, disabling or progressive symptoms","Advise avoidance of diving, flying and other barotrauma-inducing activities until the fistula has healed","Monitor hearing with serial pure-tone audiometry, recognising that vertigo often settles but established sensorineural loss may be permanent","Refer to otology and provide vestibular rehabilitation for residual imbalance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"perinatal_hie":{"name":"Perinatal Hypoxic-Ischaemic Encephalopathy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HIE","hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy","birth asphyxia encephalopathy","neonatal encephalopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Brain injury in the newborn resulting from a perinatal event that reduces cerebral oxygen delivery and blood flow, producing an evolving encephalopathy.","It is a leading cause of neonatal mortality and long-term neurodevelopmental disability, including cerebral palsy.","Severity is graded clinically using the Sarnat stages (mild, moderate, severe) based on level of consciousness, tone, reflexes, autonomic function, and seizures.","Supportive evidence of a hypoxic-ischaemic insult includes fetal distress, low Apgar scores, the need for resuscitation, and a low umbilical cord arterial pH with metabolic acidosis.","Therapeutic hypothermia, started within the first hours of life for eligible term and near-term infants with moderate to severe encephalopathy, reduces death and disability.","Seizures are common and may be subtle; EEG or amplitude-integrated EEG assists detection and monitoring.","The injury frequently involves other organs, causing renal, cardiac, hepatic, and haematological dysfunction that also require support.","MRI in the first week helps define the pattern and extent of injury and informs prognosis.","Important mimics, including infection, metabolic disorders, and stroke, should be considered rather than assuming every encephalopathy is purely hypoxic-ischaemic."],"pathophysiology":"A hypoxic-ischaemic insult around the time of birth causes an initial phase of energy failure with cellular depolarisation, excitotoxic glutamate release, and calcium influx. After partial recovery of cerebral metabolism, a delayed (secondary) phase of energy failure ensues over hours to days, driven by mitochondrial dysfunction, oxidative stress, inflammation, and apoptotic and necrotic cell death. This latent window is the rationale for early therapeutic hypothermia, which slows the destructive secondary cascade.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis or meningitis","Inborn errors of metabolism","Neonatal (arterial or venous) stroke","Intracranial haemorrhage","Neonatal seizures from other causes (e.g. electrolyte disturbance)","Congenital brain malformation or genetic encephalopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Umbilical cord and early postnatal blood gas for acidosis and base deficit","EEG or amplitude-integrated EEG for seizure detection and background assessment","MRI of the brain (usually within the first week) for injury pattern and prognosis","Assessment of multi-organ function (renal, hepatic, cardiac, coagulation, glucose, electrolytes)","Evaluation to exclude infection and metabolic causes"],"redFlags":["Deep coma, absent brainstem reflexes, or flaccid tone","Refractory or clinically evident status epilepticus","Multi-organ failure","Severely abnormal EEG background or extensive injury on MRI","Persistent need for cardiorespiratory support"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on severity: mild encephalopathy usually recovers well, moderate carries a variable risk of impairment, and severe disease is associated with high mortality and a high rate of cerebral palsy, epilepsy, and cognitive and sensory disability; therapeutic hypothermia improves outcomes in eligible infants.","pitfalls":["Assuming all neonatal encephalopathy is hypoxic-ischaemic and missing infection, metabolic disease, or stroke","Missing subtle seizures without EEG monitoring","Delaying assessment for and initiation of therapeutic hypothermia within the treatment window","Allowing hyperthermia, which worsens injury","Overlooking coexisting multi-organ dysfunction"],"management":["Initiate therapeutic hypothermia (whole-body or selective head cooling) within the early hours of life for eligible term and near-term infants with moderate-to-severe encephalopathy to slow the secondary energy-failure cascade","Provide neuroprotective supportive intensive care: maintain normal oxygenation, ventilation, perfusion, glucose and electrolytes and avoid hyperthermia","Detect and treat seizures with antiseizure medication such as phenobarbital, guided by EEG or amplitude-integrated EEG monitoring","Monitor and support multi-organ dysfunction (renal, hepatic, cardiac and coagulation) with careful fluid balance","Obtain brain MRI in the first week to define the injury pattern and inform prognosis, while excluding infective and metabolic mimics","Rewarm slowly after the cooling period and arrange structured neurodevelopmental follow-up for cerebral palsy, epilepsy and cognitive or sensory impairment","Involve neonatology, paediatric neurology and allied therapists, and provide family support and counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"perinatal_hiv":{"name":"Perinatal HIV Infection","system":"Immunological","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mother-to-child HIV transmission","vertical HIV transmission","paediatric HIV"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most childhood HIV infection is acquired from the mother during pregnancy, around delivery, or through breastfeeding, and transmission is largely preventable with effective interventions.","Maternal antiretroviral therapy that suppresses viral load, appropriate mode of delivery, infant antiretroviral prophylaxis, and safe infant feeding practices dramatically reduce vertical transmission.","Infected infants are frequently asymptomatic at birth, so diagnosis relies on virological (nucleic acid) testing rather than antibody tests, because maternal antibodies cross the placenta and persist for months.","Untreated perinatal infection can progress rapidly in infancy, with failure to thrive, recurrent or severe infections, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, and developmental regression.","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia is a serious early opportunistic infection, so prophylaxis is important in at-risk and infected infants.","Early initiation of antiretroviral therapy in infected infants improves survival and outcomes and is recommended as soon as diagnosis is confirmed.","A definitive diagnosis of infection or its exclusion requires a defined schedule of repeat virological testing over the first months of life.","Comprehensive care includes immunisations adapted to immune status, growth and developmental monitoring, and support for the whole family."],"pathophysiology":"HIV crosses to the infant transplacentally, through exposure to maternal blood and genital secretions during delivery, or via breast milk. The virus infects CD4 T lymphocytes and other immune cells, integrating into host DNA and causing progressive depletion and dysfunction of CD4 cells. In infants the immune system is immature and the initial viral burden relative to immune capacity is high, so without treatment immunodeficiency and disease progression can occur faster than in adults, predisposing to opportunistic and recurrent infections and end-organ involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Other congenital infections (e.g., cytomegalovirus, syphilis)","Primary immunodeficiency disorders","Failure to thrive of other cause","Recurrent infections from other immune defects","Tuberculosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["HIV nucleic acid (virological) testing on the recommended schedule","CD4 count and percentage","HIV viral load","Screening for co-infections (tuberculosis, hepatitis B and C, syphilis)","Full blood count and growth and developmental assessment"],"redFlags":["Failure to thrive or growth faltering","Recurrent, severe, or unusual infections","Signs of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia or other opportunistic infection","Developmental regression or encephalopathy","Persistent hepatosplenomegaly or generalised lymphadenopathy"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis, prompt antiretroviral therapy, and prophylaxis against opportunistic infections, children can achieve viral suppression, immune recovery, and near-normal life expectancy; untreated perinatal infection carries high morbidity and mortality in early childhood.","pitfalls":["Using antibody testing rather than virological testing to diagnose infection in young infants","Interpreting a single negative early test as excluding infection without completing the testing schedule","Delaying antiretroviral therapy or Pneumocystis prophylaxis in an at-risk or infected infant","Overlooking co-infections such as tuberculosis or hepatitis"],"management":["Prevent vertical transmission with maternal antiretroviral therapy to suppress viral load, an appropriate mode of delivery and safe infant feeding practices","Give the exposed neonate antiretroviral prophylaxis after birth according to the assessed transmission risk","Start combination antiretroviral therapy promptly in any confirmed infected infant, regardless of clinical or immunological status","Provide Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia prophylaxis with co-trimoxazole in at-risk and infected infants","Confirm infection status with virological (nucleic acid) testing on the recommended schedule rather than antibody tests, since maternal antibody persists","Monitor CD4 count and percentage, viral load, and growth and development, and screen for and treat co-infections such as tuberculosis, hepatitis B and C and syphilis","Immunise appropriately and refer to paediatric HIV specialists, with testing and support for the mother and family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"periodic_fever_pfapa":{"name":"PFAPA Syndrome (Periodic Fever, Aphthous Stomatitis, Pharyngitis, Adenitis)","system":"Immunological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PFAPA","Marshall syndrome","periodic fever syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common periodic fever syndrome of childhood, typically beginning before the age of five.","The defining feature is highly regular, recurrent episodes of fever occurring at strikingly predictable intervals, often every 3-6 weeks.","Episodes classically combine aphthous mouth ulcers, pharyngitis, and cervical lymphadenopathy, though not every feature is present each time.","A hallmark is that the child is entirely well, thriving, and asymptomatic between episodes with normal growth and development.","It is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion; recurrent streptococcal infection, cyclic neutropenia, and monogenic autoinflammatory disorders should be considered.","Fevers respond dramatically to a single dose of corticosteroid, which can be both therapeutic and supportive of the diagnosis, though steroids may shorten the interval between episodes.","Routine antibiotics are ineffective because the condition is inflammatory rather than infective.","The disorder is self-limiting, usually resolving spontaneously over several years without long-term sequelae; tonsillectomy is an option in selected cases."],"pathophysiology":"PFAPA is regarded as an autoinflammatory disorder of innate immunity rather than an infection or classic autoimmune disease. During attacks there is dysregulated activation of the innate immune system with increased pro-inflammatory cytokine signalling (including interleukin-1 beta pathway activity), producing the stereotyped febrile episodes. Unlike the monogenic periodic fever syndromes, no single consistent genetic cause has been identified, and inflammation resolves completely between episodes.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent streptococcal pharyngitis or tonsillitis","Cyclic neutropenia","Familial Mediterranean fever and other monogenic autoinflammatory syndromes","Hyper-IgD syndrome (mevalonate kinase deficiency)","TNF-receptor associated periodic syndrome (TRAPS)","Recurrent viral infections in young children"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Documentation of the fever pattern with a symptom and temperature diary","Full blood count during and between episodes (to exclude cyclic neutropenia)","Inflammatory markers during an attack","Throat swab or streptococcal testing to exclude recurrent bacterial pharyngitis","Genetic testing for monogenic periodic fever syndromes when features are atypical","Immunoglobulin levels where an alternative autoinflammatory diagnosis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Poor growth or failure to thrive, which is not expected in PFAPA","Persistent symptoms or inflammation between episodes","Irregular, unpredictable, or evolving fever patterns suggesting another diagnosis","Neutropenia coinciding with fevers (points to cyclic neutropenia)","Features suggesting a monogenic autoinflammatory disorder such as serositis, rash, or a strong family history"],"prognosis":"Excellent; PFAPA is benign and self-limiting, typically resolving over several years with no lasting effect on growth, development, or long-term health, and it is not associated with amyloidosis or organ damage.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating episodes with antibiotics for presumed bacterial pharyngitis","Diagnosing PFAPA in a child who is unwell or failing to thrive between episodes","Overlooking cyclic neutropenia by not checking blood counts during febrile episodes","Assuming steroid responsiveness alone confirms the diagnosis without excluding mimics","Not recognising that corticosteroids may shorten the interval to the next attack"],"management":["Abort individual febrile attacks with a corticosteroid given at the onset of an episode, which is dramatically effective and supports the diagnosis","Provide symptomatic supportive care during attacks with antipyretics or analgesia, hydration and reassurance","Consider colchicine prophylaxis in children with frequent attacks or shortened intervals between episodes","Discuss tonsillectomy as a potentially curative option in selected children with frequent or troublesome episodes","Confirm the clinical diagnosis by excluding cyclic neutropenia, recurrent streptococcal infection and monogenic autoinflammatory syndromes before committing to therapy","Counsel families that the child thrives between episodes and that the condition is benign and self-limiting, resolving over several years without organ damage"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"periodic_limb_movement_disorder":{"name":"Periodic limb movement disorder","system":"Sleep medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLMD","periodic limb movements of sleep disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / International Classification of Sleep Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by periodic, stereotyped limb movements during sleep that fragment sleep and cause insomnia or daytime tiredness","The movements are typically extension of the big toe with ankle dorsiflexion, sometimes with knee and hip flexion, recurring at fairly regular intervals","Diagnosis requires polysomnography showing a raised periodic limb movement index together with a clinical sleep complaint","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, so periodic movements accompanying restless legs syndrome, sleep apnoea, narcolepsy or certain medications do not qualify as periodic limb movement disorder","Periodic limb movements in sleep are common, increase with age and are frequently asymptomatic, so they must be distinguished from the disorder itself","Iron deficiency should be sought and corrected, as low iron stores aggravate the movements","When treatment is needed, approaches mirror those for restless legs syndrome, such as alpha-2-delta ligands or dopaminergic agents, with attention to iron status"],"pathophysiology":"Periodic limb movements in sleep are thought to reflect disinhibition of spinal and brainstem motor generators, linked to altered central dopaminergic transmission and influenced by brain iron availability, since iron is a cofactor for dopamine synthesis. The same mechanisms underlie restless legs syndrome, with which the movements overlap. Periodic limb movement disorder is diagnosed only when these movements themselves disrupt sleep and no other disorder or drug better explains the picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Restless legs syndrome with periodic limb movements","Obstructive sleep apnoea with movement-related arousals","Sleep-related leg cramps","Hypnic jerks (sleep starts)","Nocturnal seizures","Medication-induced movements, for example from antidepressants","Fragmentary myoclonus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Overnight polysomnography with limb electromyography to quantify the periodic limb movement index and associated arousals","Assessment for restless legs syndrome symptoms to avoid double-labelling","Serum ferritin and iron studies to detect iron deficiency","Review of medications that can provoke the movements, notably antidepressants","Screening for coexisting sleep apnoea"],"redFlags":["Excessive daytime sleepiness with driving or occupational risk","Features suggesting nocturnal seizures rather than periodic movements","Neurological signs pointing to a myelopathy or neuropathy","Coexisting untreated sleep apnoea"],"prognosis":"When a genuine disorder is confirmed and contributors such as iron deficiency or offending drugs are addressed, sleep and daytime symptoms usually improve; the movements themselves tend to persist and increase with age but are often asymptomatic.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing the disorder from the incidental finding of periodic limb movements without a clinical sleep complaint","Labelling it as periodic limb movement disorder when restless legs syndrome or sleep apnoea is the real cause","Failing to check and treat iron deficiency","Overlooking antidepressants and other drugs that exacerbate the movements"],"management":["Identify and correct iron deficiency with iron replacement, since low brain iron availability aggravates the movements through impaired dopaminergic transmission","Review and withdraw or substitute provoking medications, notably antidepressants and dopamine antagonists","Treat any primary contributor rather than the movements alone, managing coexisting restless legs syndrome, sleep apnoea or narcolepsy","Offer a dopaminergic agent or an alpha-2-delta ligand (gabapentinoid) for a confirmed disorder that genuinely disrupts sleep","Advise sleep hygiene and reduction of caffeine, alcohol and nicotine","Confirm the diagnosis with polysomnography and monitor treatment response, recognising that asymptomatic periodic limb movements do not require treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd edition (ICSD-3)","Principles and Practice of Sleep Medicine (Kryger, Roth, Dement)"],"crossLinks":["sleep_related_eating_disorder"]},"periodontal_ehlers_danlos_syndrome":{"name":"Periodontal Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","system":"Connective tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pEDS","EDS type VIII","periodontitis type EDS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Connective tissue disorder references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by heterozygous gain-of-function variants in C1R or C1S, subcomponents of the classical complement pathway; autosomal dominant.","The defining feature is severe, early-onset periodontitis beginning in childhood or adolescence and leading to premature loss of teeth.","Lack of attached gingiva and pretibial plaques, which are hyperpigmented atrophic lesions over the shins, are characteristic signs.","Easy bruising and skin fragility are present, while joint hypermobility tends to be limited and often confined to the distal joints.","A positive family history is a core diagnostic criterion, so the pedigree should be traced for early tooth loss and pretibial lesions.","Pretibial plaques often follow minor trauma and appear as brownish discoloured atrophic scars.","Some patients show a marfanoid habitus, acrogeria and prominent subcutaneous vasculature, and vascular events are reported but far less frequent than in vascular EDS.","Distinguish from other causes of aggressive periodontitis and refer for C1R and C1S genetic testing."],"pathophysiology":"C1R and C1S encode the serine protease subcomponents C1r and C1s of the C1 complex, the first component of the classical complement pathway. The pathogenic variants are gain-of-function or activating changes that promote aberrant autoactivation of the C1 complex, driving inappropriate local complement activation and chronic inflammation. In the periodontium this dysregulated inflammatory response leads to destruction of the supporting connective tissue and alveolar bone, producing the severe early-onset periodontitis and gingival abnormalities, while more generalised connective tissue involvement accounts for the skin fragility, bruising and pretibial plaques. This complement-mediated mechanism is distinct from the collagen and matrix defects that underlie most other EDS subtypes.","additionalDifferentials":["Other forms of aggressive or early-onset periodontitis","Hypophosphatasia","Papillon-Lefevre syndrome","Leukocyte adhesion deficiency","Kindler syndrome","Other Ehlers-Danlos subtypes","Marfan syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive periodontal examination with dental radiographs assessing alveolar bone loss","Molecular genetic testing of C1R and C1S","Skin examination for pretibial plaques and assessment of bruising and fragility","Detailed family pedigree focused on early tooth loss","Complement pathway studies where available","Echocardiography and vascular imaging if vascular features are present"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive periodontitis with tooth loss in a child or young adult","Symptoms or signs of arterial rupture or dissection","Significant unexplained bruising or bleeding tendency","New expanding pretibial or other subcutaneous lesion after minor trauma"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is generally normal, with the major burden being early and progressive tooth loss and its functional and nutritional consequences. Vascular complications are occasionally reported and warrant vigilance where suggestive features exist.","pitfalls":["Attributing the severe periodontitis solely to poor oral hygiene and missing the underlying syndrome.","Overlooking a family history of early tooth loss that would support the diagnosis.","Failing to recognise pretibial plaques as a diagnostic clue.","Not offering C1R and C1S genetic testing and cascade screening of relatives."],"management":["Institute intensive, lifelong periodontal care with meticulous oral hygiene and regular professional debridement to slow attachment and alveolar bone loss, as there is no disease-specific drug therapy","Plan prosthetic rehabilitation with dentures or implants where feasible for premature tooth loss to preserve function and nutrition","Manage skin fragility and easy bruising with gentle wound care and protection of the shins to limit pretibial plaque formation","Confirm the diagnosis with molecular testing of C1R and C1S and provide genetic counselling given autosomal dominant inheritance","Screen the family pedigree for early tooth loss and pretibial lesions to identify affected relatives","Maintain cardiovascular vigilance with echocardiography and vascular imaging where vascular features are present","Coordinate care across clinical genetics, oral medicine or periodontology and dermatology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International EDS classification consensus criteria","Standard medical genetics textbooks","Peer-reviewed connective tissue disease reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"periodontitis":{"name":"Periodontitis","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["periodontal disease","chronic periodontitis","gum disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Periodontitis is a biofilm-driven inflammatory disease causing irreversible destruction of the periodontal attachment and alveolar bone","The hallmark distinguishing it from gingivitis is loss of clinical attachment and true periodontal pocketing, not just bleeding gums","It is often painless and slowly progressive, so patients may present late with mobility, drifting teeth, or gingival recession","Probing depths, attachment levels, bleeding on probing, and radiographic bone loss are the core diagnostic measurements","Smoking, poorly controlled diabetes, and genetic susceptibility strongly modify disease severity and treatment response","There is a bidirectional relationship with diabetes and associations with cardiovascular and other systemic conditions","Treatment centres on meticulous plaque control and mechanical debridement to disrupt the subgingival biofilm, with surgery for residual defects","Long-term supportive periodontal maintenance is essential because the disease tends to recur without ongoing care"],"pathophysiology":"Periodontitis arises from a dysbiotic subgingival biofilm dominated by anaerobic and gram-negative species that provoke a chronic host immune-inflammatory response. Bacterial virulence factors and the resulting inflammatory mediators drive activation of osteoclasts and matrix-degrading enzymes, leading to breakdown of the periodontal ligament and resorption of alveolar bone. This produces apical migration of the junctional epithelium and true pocket formation. The extent of tissue destruction reflects the balance between microbial challenge and host response, which is heavily modulated by risk factors such as smoking, diabetes, and genetics, ultimately threatening tooth support.","additionalDifferentials":["Gingivitis without attachment loss","Necrotising periodontal disease","Periodontal abscess","Endodontic-periodontal combined lesions","Occlusal trauma causing mobility","Manifestations of systemic disease affecting the periodontium"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full-mouth periodontal charting of probing depths and attachment loss","Bleeding on probing and plaque indices","Periapical and bitewing radiographs to assess bone loss","Assessment of tooth mobility and furcation involvement","Screening for diabetes and smoking status as risk modifiers"],"redFlags":["Rapid attachment and bone loss in a young patient suggesting aggressive disease","Necrotising periodontitis with pain, ulceration, and tissue destruction, especially in immunocompromised patients","Periodontal manifestations pointing to undiagnosed diabetes or immune deficiency"],"prognosis":"With effective plaque control and professional care, disease progression can usually be halted and teeth retained, but lost attachment and bone do not regenerate spontaneously and advanced cases carry a guarded prognosis for affected teeth.","pitfalls":["Treating bleeding gums as simple gingivitis while missing established attachment loss","Neglecting smoking cessation and glycaemic control that undermine treatment","Relying on antibiotics instead of mechanical biofilm disruption","Failing to arrange ongoing maintenance, allowing recurrence"],"management":["Achieve effective biofilm control through oral hygiene instruction and daily mechanical plaque removal by the patient","Perform subgingival instrumentation (scaling and root surface debridement) as the cornerstone of non-surgical therapy","Use adjunctive antimicrobials selectively, such as antiseptic mouthrinses or systemic antibiotics in aggressive or refractory disease","Modify risk factors by supporting smoking cessation and optimising glycaemic control in diabetes, given the bidirectional relationship","Offer periodontal surgery (flap, resective or regenerative procedures) for residual deep pockets and furcation involvement","Enrol patients in structured supportive periodontal maintenance with regular monitoring of probing depths, attachment levels and bleeding on probing","Extract teeth of hopeless prognosis and plan prosthetic replacement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"perioperative_anaphylaxis":{"name":"Perioperative anaphylaxis","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anaesthetic anaphylaxis","intraoperative anaphylaxis","perioperative hypersensitivity reaction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A life-threatening acute hypersensitivity reaction during anaesthesia, often presenting abruptly with cardiovascular collapse rather than the skin signs seen in awake patients","Common triggers include neuromuscular blocking agents, antibiotics, latex, and chlorhexidine, with contrast and colloids as additional causes","Cardiovascular features (profound hypotension, tachycardia, and collapse) and respiratory features (bronchospasm, high airway pressures, desaturation) predominate under drapes","Cutaneous signs such as flushing, urticaria, and angioedema may be absent, masked, or noticed late because the patient is covered","Adrenaline (epinephrine) is the first-line treatment, titrated to severity, alongside stopping the likely trigger, giving oxygen, and aggressive intravenous fluid resuscitation","Refractory cases may need escalating vasopressor support, and cardiac arrest is managed with standard advanced life support with prolonged effort as needed","A mast cell tryptase level taken during and after the reaction supports the diagnosis, and the patient must be referred for allergy testing to identify the culprit","Clear documentation and patient warning about the responsible agent are essential to prevent recurrence at future anaesthetics"],"pathophysiology":"Perioperative anaphylaxis is usually an immediate hypersensitivity reaction, often IgE-mediated, in which prior sensitisation leads to cross-linking of IgE on mast cells and basophils upon re-exposure to an antigen. Some reactions are non-IgE-mediated or result from direct mast cell activation. Degranulation releases histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes, and other mediators that cause vasodilation, increased vascular permeability with fluid shift, bronchoconstriction, and myocardial depression. The result is rapid loss of intravascular volume, distributive shock, and airway compromise. Adrenaline counteracts these effects through vasoconstriction, bronchodilation, and stabilisation of mast cells.","additionalDifferentials":["Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity","Primary cardiac event or arrhythmia","High or total spinal anaesthesia","Severe bronchospasm from asthma","Pulmonary embolism or air embolism","Haemorrhagic or septic shock"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial mast cell tryptase levels during the reaction and after recovery","Continuous ECG, blood pressure, and oxygenation monitoring","Arterial blood gas to assess oxygenation and acidosis","Referral for later skin and specific IgE allergy testing to identify the trigger","Review of all agents and materials administered around the time of the reaction"],"redFlags":["Sudden cardiovascular collapse or cardiac arrest during anaesthesia","Severe bronchospasm with high airway pressures and difficulty ventilating","Profound refractory hypotension despite fluids and vasopressors","Rapidly progressive airway swelling or angioedema threatening the airway"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition and early adrenaline most patients recover, but delayed treatment or severe refractory shock can be fatal or cause hypoxic organ injury; identifying and avoiding the trigger prevents recurrence.","pitfalls":["Delaying adrenaline while awaiting confirmation of the diagnosis","Expecting skin signs and dismissing anaphylaxis when they are absent under drapes","Failing to send timed tryptase samples or to refer for allergy testing","Under-resuscitating the large fluid deficit caused by capillary leak","Not documenting or warning the patient about the causative agent"],"management":["Give adrenaline immediately as first-line treatment, titrated intramuscularly or intravenously to the severity of the reaction, and call for help","Stop administration of the likely trigger, such as a neuromuscular blocking agent, antibiotic, chlorhexidine or latex","Secure the airway, deliver high-flow oxygen and relieve bronchospasm with inhaled or nebulised bronchodilators","Provide aggressive intravenous fluid resuscitation to counter distributive shock, vasodilation and capillary fluid shift","Escalate refractory shock with vasopressor infusions such as noradrenaline or vasopressin, and consider glucagon in patients taking beta-blockers","Manage cardiac arrest with standard advanced life support, using prolonged resuscitation efforts as needed","Give adjunctive corticosteroids and antihistamines after initial resuscitation and observe for a biphasic reaction","Take serial mast cell tryptase samples and refer for later skin and specific IgE allergy testing to identify and document the trigger for future avoidance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"perioral_dermatitis":{"name":"perioral dermatitis","system":"Skin and integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["periorificial dermatitis","muzzle rash"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as grouped erythematous papules, papulopustules and fine scaling around the mouth with characteristic sparing of a narrow zone immediately adjacent to the vermilion border.","Predominantly affects young women, and also occurs in children as periorificial dermatitis around the mouth, nose and eyes.","Strongly associated with topical corticosteroid use on the face, and also with inhaled and intranasal corticosteroids.","A rebound flare on withdrawal of topical steroids is characteristic and should be anticipated and explained to the patient.","First-line management is 'zero therapy' — stopping all topical corticosteroids, heavy cosmetics and occlusive skin care.","Oral tetracyclines are effective in adults, while macrolides are preferred in young children and pregnancy where tetracyclines are contraindicated.","A granulomatous variant occurs particularly in children and shows non-caseating granulomas on histology.","Fluorinated toothpaste, occlusive cosmetics and Demodex have been implicated as contributory factors."],"pathophysiology":"Perioral dermatitis is a multifactorial follicular and epidermal barrier disorder rather than a true eczema. Impairment of the epidermal barrier and a follicular inflammatory reaction are provoked or aggravated by topical, inhaled or intranasal corticosteroids, occlusive cosmetics and skin care products, and fluorinated dental products. Follicular micro-organisms including Demodex mites and fusobacteria have been implicated in perpetuating the inflammation, and the periorificial distribution reflects the involvement of facial pilosebaceous units.","additionalDifferentials":["Acne vulgaris","Rosacea","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Allergic or irritant contact dermatitis","Lip-lick (irritant) dermatitis","Cutaneous sarcoidosis (granulomatous variant)","Steroid-induced acneiform eruption"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on morphology and distribution in most cases","Detailed history of topical, inhaled and intranasal corticosteroid and cosmetic exposure","Skin swab if secondary bacterial infection or marked pustulation is suspected","Patch testing when allergic contact dermatitis is a differential","Skin biopsy in atypical or granulomatous presentations","Skin scraping or examination for Demodex in refractory cases"],"redFlags":["Ocular involvement with blepharitis or conjunctivitis requiring ophthalmology assessment","Failure to respond to appropriate therapy, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Granulomatous features raising the possibility of cutaneous sarcoidosis","Progressive facial swelling or systemic symptoms"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is good, with resolution over weeks to a few months once corticosteroids are withdrawn and antibiotic therapy is given, although relapse can occur if provoking factors are reintroduced.","pitfalls":["Treating with topical corticosteroids, which briefly improve then worsen the eruption and cause rebound flares.","Misdiagnosing the condition as acne and missing the steroid or cosmetic trigger.","Overlooking inhaled or intranasal corticosteroids as an ongoing source.","Not warning the patient about the expected rebound flare on steroid cessation, leading to loss of adherence."],"management":["Institute 'zero therapy' first by stopping all topical corticosteroids and heavy occlusive cosmetics and skin-care products","Anticipate and explain the characteristic rebound flare on withdrawal of topical corticosteroids and manage it supportively","Prescribe a topical anti-inflammatory agent such as metronidazole, azelaic acid or a topical calcineurin inhibitor","Give an oral tetracycline for moderate, severe or refractory disease in adults","Use a macrolide instead in young children and in pregnancy, where tetracyclines are contraindicated","Advise gentle skin care and avoidance of fluorinated dental products and provoking factors to reduce relapse","Consider treatment directed at Demodex in refractory cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine","British Association of Dermatologists guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"periosteal_chondroma":{"name":"Periosteal chondroma","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Juxtacortical chondroma","Periosteal chondroma of bone"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign surface (juxtacortical) hyaline cartilage tumour arising beneath the periosteum, favouring the proximal humerus, the small tubular bones of the hand and long-bone metaphyses in adolescents and young adults.","The lesion erodes and saucerises the underlying cortex, producing a well-defined defect with a sclerotic margin and characteristic overhanging periosteal buttresses at its edges.","It is typically small, usually under about 3 to 4 cm; larger surface cartilage lesions should raise suspicion for periosteal chondrosarcoma.","Chondroid matrix mineralisation in a ring-and-arc pattern may be visible within the lesion.","MRI shows lobulated tissue of high T2 signal with peripheral and septal enhancement and, importantly, no invasion of the medullary cavity.","It must be distinguished from periosteal chondrosarcoma and periosteal osteosarcoma, which are larger and show cortical destruction, marrow invasion or a soft-tissue mass.","Marginal en bloc excision is curative and local recurrence is uncommon when excision is complete."],"pathophysiology":"Periosteal chondroma is a benign neoplasm of mature hyaline cartilage that develops on the surface of a bone, deep to the periosteum and superficial to the cortex. As the cartilaginous lobules enlarge they exert pressure on the underlying cortex, producing the characteristic scalloped cortical erosion (saucerisation) with a reactive sclerotic base and buttressing periosteal new bone at the margins, while by definition the process remains confined to the surface and does not invade the medullary cavity. Histologically it comprises lobules of hypercellular hyaline cartilage that may show mild atypia and binucleate cells, features acceptable in a surface lesion but which contribute to difficulty distinguishing it from low-grade surface chondrosarcoma; IDH mutations may be present as in other cartilage tumours.","additionalDifferentials":["Periosteal (juxtacortical) chondrosarcoma","Periosteal osteosarcoma","Osteochondroma","Bizarre parosteal osteochondromatous proliferation (Nora lesion)","Periosteal (cortical) desmoid","Surface aneurysmal bone cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs to demonstrate cortical saucerisation with sclerotic margins and periosteal buttressing","MRI to characterise the cartilage and confirm absence of marrow invasion","CT to define cortical scalloping and matrix mineralisation","Assessment of lesion size as a discriminator from surface chondrosarcoma","Biopsy for large or atypical lesions, interpreted with awareness of overlap with low-grade malignancy","Comparison with prior imaging to assess for growth in the skeletally mature"],"redFlags":["Lesion size greater than about 5 cm","Cortical destruction rather than smooth saucerisation","Invasion of the medullary cavity","An associated soft-tissue mass","Documented growth in a skeletally mature adult"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent, with a low rate of local recurrence following adequate marginal excision and negligible malignant potential.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosis as periosteal chondrosarcoma, leading to overtreatment, or the reverse leading to inadequate surgery","Confusing it with osteochondroma, which shows corticomedullary continuity","Incomplete excision predisposing to local recurrence","Overlooking marrow invasion on MRI, which would indicate malignancy"],"management":["Manage asymptomatic, radiologically classic small lesions with observation and serial imaging","Undertake marginal en bloc surgical excision for symptomatic, enlarging or diagnostically uncertain lesions, which is generally curative","Ensure complete removal of the cartilage lobules to minimise local recurrence when curettage-based resection is used","Confirm absence of medullary invasion on MRI and use lesion size to help distinguish it from periosteal chondrosarcoma before planning surgery","Reserve biopsy for large or atypical lesions, interpreting histology with awareness of overlap with low-grade surface chondrosarcoma","Follow up with imaging for local recurrence after excision and manage within a musculoskeletal oncology and radiology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone (current edition)","Resnick, Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders","Greenspan, Orthopedic Imaging: A Practical Approach"],"crossLinks":[]},"peripapillary_staphyloma":{"name":"peripapillary staphyloma","system":"ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["peripapillary excavation","circumpapillary staphyloma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, usually unilateral congenital anomaly in which a deep, sharply demarcated fundus excavation surrounds a relatively normal-appearing optic disc at its base.","The disc lies at the bottom of the excavation and, unlike the morning glory anomaly, shows no central glial tuft and no anomalous radial vessels.","The walls of the excavation show atrophic changes of the retinal pigment epithelium and choroid.","Usually non-progressive and typically not associated with the multisystem or midline anomalies seen with morning glory disc.","Vision is variable; acuity may be reduced with associated field defects, though some eyes see relatively well.","Differentiation from morning glory anomaly and optic disc coloboma rests on the near-normal disc morphology at the base of a peripapillary excavation.","Occasionally complicated by serous macular detachment or contractile movements of the excavation.","Distinguished from acquired peripapillary excavations of pathological myopia by its congenital, stationary nature."],"pathophysiology":"Peripapillary staphyloma is thought to arise from a congenital defect in the differentiation and development of the peripapillary sclera and choroid, with deficient or attenuated tissue in the region immediately surrounding the optic nerve head. This weakness allows the peripapillary structures to bow posteriorly, producing a deep excavation whose walls display retinal pigment epithelial and choroidal atrophy while the optic disc at the base remains comparatively spared. Unlike the morning glory anomaly, there is no primary mesodermal glial dysgenesis at the disc itself, which accounts for the relatively normal disc appearance. The distorted peripapillary geometry accounts for the visual field changes and predisposition to secondary serous retinal changes.","additionalDifferentials":["Morning glory disc anomaly","Optic nerve coloboma","Optic disc pit","Megalopapilla","Pathological (degenerative) myopic staphyloma","Advanced glaucomatous cupping","Posterior scleral ectasia of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination and fundus photography","Optical coherence tomography of the disc and peripapillary region","B-scan ultrasonography to demonstrate posterior scleral excavation","MRI or CT of the orbit where posterior segment anatomy is unclear","Automated perimetry to characterise the field defect","Serial imaging to confirm the stationary, non-progressive nature"],"redFlags":["New central distortion suggesting a serous macular detachment","Progressive excavation, which is atypical and should prompt reassessment","Bilateral involvement or associated ocular malformations","Reduced acuity with a relative afferent pupillary defect indicating significant nerve involvement"],"prognosis":"The anomaly is generally stationary with a stable, if variable, visual outcome; many eyes retain useful vision. Rare secondary complications such as serous macular detachment can reduce central acuity.","pitfalls":["Confusing peripapillary staphyloma with the morning glory anomaly, which carries very different systemic implications.","Mislabelling a congenital excavation as glaucomatous cupping and initiating pressure-lowering therapy unnecessarily.","Overlooking a superimposed serous macular detachment as the cause of new visual symptoms.","Failing to distinguish it from acquired myopic staphyloma, leading to incorrect prognostic counselling."],"management":["Manage conservatively, as this congenital anomaly is usually stationary and non-progressive with no disease-specific therapy","Correct any refractive error and optimise vision in the affected eye, with amblyopia therapy in children","Monitor with serial dilated fundus examination, optical coherence tomography and automated perimetry to characterise field defects and confirm stability","Detect and treat secondary complications such as serous macular or retinal detachment promptly to preserve central acuity","Distinguish it from morning glory anomaly and optic disc coloboma so that associated systemic or midline anomalies are appropriately excluded","Refer to ophthalmology, including paediatric ophthalmology in children, for ongoing surveillance and visual rehabilitation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"peripartum_cardiomyopathy":{"name":"Peripartum cardiomyopathy","system":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPCM","pregnancy-associated cardiomyopathy","postpartum cardiomyopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC guidance on cardiovascular disease in pregnancy / Williams Obstetrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["PPCM is a dilated cardiomyopathy with left ventricular systolic dysfunction presenting towards the end of pregnancy or in the months after delivery, without another identifiable cause","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, defined by reduced ejection fraction developing in the peripartum period in a woman without previously known heart disease","Symptoms overlap with normal late pregnancy, so breathlessness, orthopnoea, oedema, and fatigue are easily attributed to pregnancy itself","Risk factors include hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, multiple pregnancy, advanced maternal age, and multiparity","Echocardiography confirms impaired left ventricular function and helps exclude other structural causes","Management follows heart failure principles adapted for pregnancy and lactation, with careful choice of agents to protect the foetus and neonate","The dilated ventricle predisposes to intracardiac thrombus and thromboembolism, and anticoagulation may be considered when function is markedly reduced","Women who recover should be counselled that a subsequent pregnancy carries a real risk of relapse, especially if function has not fully normalised"],"pathophysiology":"Peripartum cardiomyopathy is thought to arise from the interaction of haemodynamic, hormonal, vascular, and inflammatory stresses of late pregnancy and the puerperium in susceptible women. A prevailing model implicates oxidative stress and cleavage of the hormone prolactin into an anti-angiogenic fragment that injures the myocardial microvasculature and cardiomyocytes, leading to ventricular dilatation and systolic dysfunction. The failing, dilated left ventricle reduces cardiac output, raises filling pressures with pulmonary congestion, and promotes stasis and mural thrombus with embolic risk. Recovery of function is possible but incomplete in a substantial proportion.","additionalDifferentials":["Pre-eclampsia with pulmonary oedema","Pre-existing dilated or other cardiomyopathy unmasked by pregnancy","Pulmonary embolism or amniotic fluid embolism","Ischaemic heart disease or spontaneous coronary artery dissection","Valvular heart disease","Severe anaemia or thyroid-related heart failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography to quantify left ventricular function and exclude other pathology","Electrocardiogram","Natriuretic peptide measurement","Chest imaging for pulmonary congestion","Bloods including full blood count, renal, liver, and thyroid function","Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging in selected cases to characterise the myocardium"],"redFlags":["Acute pulmonary oedema or cardiogenic shock","Sustained arrhythmia or syncope","Features of systemic or pulmonary thromboembolism","Very low ejection fraction indicating high risk and possible need for advanced therapies"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely: many women recover left ventricular function within months, but some progress to persistent heart failure, require advanced therapies, or die from the condition. The degree and speed of recovery predict the safety of future pregnancy, and incomplete recovery carries a high recurrence risk.","pitfalls":["Dismissing breathlessness and oedema late in pregnancy as physiological and delaying echocardiography","Using heart failure medications that are unsafe in pregnancy or breastfeeding without checking suitability","Overlooking thromboembolic risk in a severely dilated ventricle","Failing to counsel about relapse risk before a subsequent pregnancy"],"management":["Manage as heart failure with therapy adapted for pregnancy and lactation, avoiding foetotoxic agents (ACE inhibitors, ARBs and mineralocorticoid antagonists) antenatally and substituting pregnancy-safe hydralazine and nitrates with a beta-blocker","Introduce full guideline-directed heart failure therapy including an ACE inhibitor or ARB, beta-blocker and mineralocorticoid antagonist after delivery using breastfeeding-compatible agents","Relieve pulmonary congestion with diuretics alongside sodium and fluid restriction","Anticoagulate given the heightened risk of mural thrombus and thromboembolism, particularly with severely reduced ejection fraction","Consider bromocriptine to suppress prolactin as a disease-specific adjunct, given with anticoagulant cover","Manage acute decompensation or cardiogenic shock with inotropes and mechanical circulatory support, coordinating timing and mode of delivery with obstetrics","Monitor recovery of left ventricular function with serial echocardiography and counsel on the risk of future pregnancy, especially where recovery is incomplete","Involve a multidisciplinary team of cardiology, obstetrics, anaesthesia and neonatology, and consider advanced therapies or transplantation for refractory disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC guidelines on the management of cardiovascular disease during pregnancy","Williams Obstetrics","Heart failure society position statements on peripartum cardiomyopathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"peripheral_arterial_disease":{"name":"Peripheral arterial disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAD","peripheral vascular disease","PVD","lower limb arterial disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["PAD is atherosclerotic narrowing of the arteries supplying the limbs, most often the lower extremities, and is a marker of widespread atherosclerosis with high cardiovascular risk","The classic symptom is intermittent claudication, exertional muscle pain in the calf, thigh, or buttock that is relieved by rest, though many patients are asymptomatic or have atypical leg symptoms","Examination may reveal diminished or absent pulses, arterial bruits, cool skin, hair loss, and delayed capillary refill","The ankle-brachial index is a simple, key bedside test; a reduced value confirms obstruction, while an abnormally high or incompressible value suggests medial calcification, common in diabetes and chronic kidney disease","Critical limb ischaemia, with rest pain, non-healing ulcers, or gangrene, indicates severe disease that threatens the limb and warrants urgent vascular assessment","Acute limb ischaemia is a vascular emergency presenting with the classic features of a suddenly painful, pale, pulseless, cold limb with sensory and motor changes","Management emphasises aggressive cardiovascular risk reduction, structured exercise, and antiplatelet and statin therapy, with revascularisation for lifestyle-limiting or limb-threatening disease","Because PAD reflects systemic atherosclerosis, coexisting coronary and cerebrovascular disease should be actively addressed"],"pathophysiology":"PAD is usually caused by atherosclerosis, in which lipid-laden plaque progressively narrows the arterial lumen and limits blood flow to the limbs. When demand rises with exertion, a flow-limiting stenosis cannot meet the increased oxygen requirement of exercising muscle, producing the ischaemic pain of claudication. As disease advances, resting perfusion becomes inadequate, causing rest pain, impaired wound healing, ulceration, and tissue loss (critical limb ischaemia). Acute limb ischaemia results from sudden occlusion by thrombosis of a diseased segment or by embolism, abruptly interrupting flow and threatening tissue viability within hours.","additionalDifferentials":["Lumbar spinal stenosis causing neurogenic claudication","Deep vein thrombosis and chronic venous insufficiency","Musculoskeletal or arthritic leg pain","Peripheral neuropathy","Chronic compartment syndrome","Nerve root compression or sciatica"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ankle-brachial index measurement, with exercise testing if resting values are equivocal","Duplex ultrasound to localise and grade stenoses","CT or MR angiography, or invasive angiography, to plan revascularisation","Assessment of cardiovascular risk factors including lipids, glucose, and smoking status","Toe-brachial index or other measures when arteries are incompressible","Wound and infection assessment in patients with ulcers or tissue loss"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: acute limb ischaemia with a painful, pale, pulseless, cold limb and sensory or motor deficit requiring urgent revascularisation","Critical limb ischaemia with rest pain, non-healing ulceration, or gangrene threatening the limb","Rapidly progressive tissue loss or signs of spreading infection in an ischaemic foot","Sudden onset of severe leg symptoms suggesting embolic occlusion"],"prognosis":"Limb prognosis is often stable in claudication with good risk-factor control, but PAD signals a substantially elevated risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, and cardiovascular death; critical and acute limb ischaemia carry high risks of amputation and mortality without timely intervention.","pitfalls":["Relying on a normal or high ankle-brachial index in patients with incompressible calcified vessels, as in diabetes or renal disease","Attributing exertional leg pain to musculoskeletal or neurogenic causes and missing PAD","Focusing on the limb while neglecting the high coronary and cerebrovascular risk","Failing to recognise acute limb ischaemia as an emergency requiring urgent revascularisation"],"management":["Provide cardiovascular risk reduction as the foundation with statin therapy, antiplatelet therapy and control of blood pressure and glucose, since PAD marks widespread atherosclerosis","Support smoking cessation as the single most important modifiable measure","Prescribe a supervised walking exercise programme to improve claudication distance","Offer cilostazol for symptomatic claudication where not contraindicated","Revascularise by endovascular angioplasty or stenting, or surgical bypass, for lifestyle-limiting claudication refractory to conservative care and urgently for critical limb ischaemia with rest pain, non-healing ulcers or gangrene","Treat acute limb ischaemia as a vascular emergency with anticoagulation and urgent embolectomy, thrombolysis or revascularisation to salvage the limb","Provide meticulous foot care, wound management and treatment of infection in tissue loss, and refer to vascular surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"peripheral_nerve_injury":{"name":"Peripheral nerve injury","system":"Rehabilitation / Pain","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PNI","peripheral nerve lesion","traumatic neuropathy","nerve injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braddom's Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation / pain medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Peripheral nerve injuries are graded by severity using the Seddon classification of neurapraxia, axonotmesis, and neurotmesis, and the more detailed Sunderland scheme, which together guide prognosis and management","Neurapraxia is a conduction block with intact axons that usually recovers fully; axonotmesis involves axonal disruption with preserved connective tissue framework and slow recovery; neurotmesis is complete transection that generally requires surgical repair","Clinical assessment maps motor, sensory, and autonomic deficits to the affected nerve and helps localise the lesion and estimate its completeness","Electrodiagnostic studies are most informative after a few weeks, once Wallerian degeneration is established, and are useful for confirming the lesion, grading severity, and tracking reinnervation","Rehabilitation emphasises preventing complications during recovery: maintaining joint range of motion, protective splinting, skin care in insensate areas, and preventing contracture and disuse","An advancing Tinel sign along the nerve over successive examinations is a helpful bedside indicator of regenerating axons","Axonal regrowth proceeds slowly at roughly a millimetre per day, so recovery of distal muscles can take many months and the muscle must remain viable for reinnervation to be useful","Neuropathic pain following nerve injury is managed with neuropathic agents such as gabapentinoids and antidepressants alongside therapy, and surgical exploration or nerve transfer is considered when there is no recovery within the expected window"],"pathophysiology":"Peripheral nerve injury disrupts axonal continuity and myelin to varying degrees depending on the mechanism, whether compression, traction, laceration, or ischaemia. In neurapraxia there is focal demyelination and conduction block without axonal loss. When axons are disrupted, the segment distal to the injury undergoes Wallerian degeneration, and regeneration then proceeds from the proximal stump along preserved endoneurial tubes, guided by Schwann cells and neurotrophic signals. The integrity of the connective tissue sheaths determines whether regrowing axons reach their correct targets; disrupted architecture leads to misdirection, neuroma formation, and incomplete recovery.","additionalDifferentials":["Radiculopathy or plexopathy","Entrapment neuropathy such as carpal or cubital tunnel syndrome","Motor neuron disease or other central cause of weakness","Generalised peripheral polyneuropathy","Compartment syndrome affecting nerve and muscle","Tendon rupture mimicking motor loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed motor and sensory examination to localise and grade the lesion","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography, timed appropriately after injury to assess severity and reinnervation","Serial examination for an advancing Tinel sign as evidence of regeneration","Imaging such as ultrasound or MRI to define nerve continuity and identify compression or neuroma","Assessment for coexisting vascular or musculoskeletal injury in trauma"],"redFlags":["Suspected complete nerve transection or a sharp penetrating injury that may need early surgical exploration and repair","Signs of compartment syndrome in an acutely painful, tense limb","Rapidly progressive or multifocal deficits suggesting a systemic or inflammatory neuropathy","Vascular compromise accompanying the nerve injury","No sign of recovery within the expected timeframe, warranting reassessment for surgery"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends chiefly on the injury grade: neurapraxia typically recovers fully within weeks to months, axonotmesis recovers slowly and often incompletely, and neurotmesis requires surgical repair with outcomes limited by the distance to the target and the age of the patient. More proximal injuries and delayed treatment carry a poorer outlook because muscle may atrophy irreversibly before reinnervation occurs.","pitfalls":["Performing electrodiagnostic studies too early, before Wallerian degeneration makes findings interpretable","Missing a transection that requires timely surgical repair","Allowing contractures and joint stiffness to develop during the long recovery period","Neglecting skin protection in areas rendered insensate by the injury","Failing to reassess and refer for surgery when expected recovery does not occur"],"management":["Repair transected nerves surgically by primary coaptation, nerve grafting or nerve transfer for neurotmesis, ideally in a timely manner to optimise reinnervation","Manage neurapraxia and axonotmesis conservatively with observation, as many recover spontaneously along preserved endoneurial tubes","Prevent secondary complications with joint range-of-motion exercises, protective splinting, skin care of insensate areas and prevention of contracture and disuse","Treat neuropathic pain with agents such as gabapentinoids or tricyclic antidepressants and address a symptomatic neuroma if it develops","Monitor recovery with serial examination for an advancing Tinel sign and with electrodiagnostic studies timed after Wallerian degeneration is established","Provide physical and occupational therapy for functional rehabilitation, escalating to reconstructive options such as tendon transfers if recovery fails"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braddom's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation"],"crossLinks":[]},"peripheral_neuropathy":{"name":"Peripheral Neuropathy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["polyneuropathy","peripheral nerve disease"],"chapter":457,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3594","clinicalPearls":["Three goals in the patient with neuropathy: localize the lesion, identify the cause, and determine treatment; the assessment is structured around seven key questions covering systems involved, distribution of weakness, nature of sensory loss, upper motor neuron signs, temporal evolution, hereditary clues, and associated medical conditions (Harrison 22e p.3595).","Pattern recognition is central: length-dependent symmetric distal sensory loss (Pattern 2) points to diabetes/metabolic, drugs, toxins, alcohol, hereditary causes; symmetric proximal AND distal weakness with sensory loss (Pattern 1) is the hallmark of acquired immune demyelinating polyneuropathy (GBS and CIDP); asymmetric/multifocal deficits (Pattern 3, mononeuropathy multiplex) suggest vasculitis, cryoglobulinemia, amyloid, sarcoid, or infection (Harrison 22e p.3596).","Nerve conduction studies and needle EMG distinguish axonal degeneration (low-amplitude potentials, relatively preserved distal latencies and conduction velocities, fibrillations on EMG) from segmental demyelination (slow conduction velocities, prolonged distal latencies, relatively preserved amplitudes, no fibrillations) (Harrison 22e p.3597).","Nonuniform slowing, conduction block, or temporal dispersion on NCS suggests an ACQUIRED demyelinating neuropathy (GBS or CIDP), whereas uniform slowing suggests a HEREDITARY demyelinating neuropathy (e.g., CMT type 1) (Harrison 22e p.3597).","Pure loss of pain and temperature with preserved vibration/position sense, normal strength and reflexes, and normal NCS indicates a small-fiber neuropathy; the most commonly identified causes are diabetes mellitus or glucose intolerance, with amyloid neuropathy also to be considered (Harrison 22e p.3596).","Dramatic proprioceptive/vibratory loss with normal strength, especially when non-length-dependent (arms more than legs or asymmetric), points to a sensory neuronopathy/ganglionopathy (Pattern 9) rather than a typical length-dependent polyneuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3597).","Diabetes mellitus is the most common cause of peripheral neuropathy in developed countries; distal symmetric sensory/sensorimotor polyneuropathy (DSPN) is the most common form, beginning in the toes and ascending (Harrison 22e p.3604).","Diabetic third nerve palsies are characteristically pupil-sparing (Harrison 22e p.3604)."],"pathophysiology":"Peripheral nerves contain mixed sensory, motor, and autonomic fibers grouped as large myelinated, small myelinated, and small unmyelinated classes; motor axons are usually large myelinated fast-conducting (~50 m/s) fibers, large-diameter sensory fibers carry proprioception and vibration, and small myelinated/unmyelinated fibers carry pain, temperature, and autonomic signals. Disease may target the neuronal cell body (neuronopathy/ganglionopathy), the myelin sheath (myelinopathy/demyelination), or the axon (axonopathy), each producing distinct clinical and electrophysiologic features. Most generalized polyneuropathies are length-dependent axonopathies producing distal (glove-and-stocking) deficits, whereas ganglionopathies produce non-length-dependent loss. Demyelination slows or blocks conduction; axonal loss reduces response amplitudes (Harrison 22e p.3594).","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy - Guillain-Barre syndrome (acute) and CIDP (chronic/relapsing) (Pattern 1) (Harrison 22e p.3595)","Cryptogenic/idiopathic sensory polyneuropathy (CSPN) - no etiology found in about half of patients despite extensive evaluation (Harrison 22e p.3595)","Diabetes mellitus and other metabolic neuropathies (DSPN, autonomic, radiculoplexus/Bruns-Garland, mononeuropathies) (Harrison 22e p.3604)","Hereditary neuropathies - Charcot-Marie-Tooth, HSAN, familial amyloid polyneuropathy, SORD neuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Vasculitic neuropathy presenting as mononeuropathy multiplex (Pattern 3) (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Uremic neuropathy in renal failure (~60% of patients) (Harrison 22e p.3605)","Nutritional/toxic neuropathies (B12, thiamine, alcohol, drugs, chemotherapy, heavy metals) (Harrison 22e p.3611)","Sensory neuronopathy/ganglionopathy - paraneoplastic (anti-Hu), Sjogren's syndrome, CANVAS, cisplatin, vitamin B6 toxicity (Pattern 9) (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Connective tissue/autoimmune disease - rheumatoid arthritis, SLE, scleroderma, sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.3605)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection - distal symmetric polyneuropathy, AIDP/CIDP, progressive polyradiculopathy, multiple mononeuropathies, sensory ganglionopathy (Harrison 22e p.3606)","Leprosy (Mycobacterium leprae) - most common cause of peripheral neuropathy in Southeast Asia, Africa, and South America; mononeuropathies or symmetric sensorimotor polyneuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3606)","Lyme disease (Borrelia burgdorferi) - facial neuropathy (bilateral in ~half, unlike idiopathic Bell's palsy), polyradiculoneuropathy, multiple mononeuropathies (Harrison 22e p.3606)","Diphtheria (Corynebacterium diphtheriae) - toxin-mediated neuropathy 3-4 weeks after infection (Harrison 22e p.3606)","Other infectious/inflammatory causes producing CSF pleocytosis in suspected GBS/CIDP - HIV, Lyme, sarcoidosis, lymphomatous/leukemic root infiltration (Harrison 22e p.3598)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Motor neuron disease (e.g., ALS) - asymmetric distal weakness without sensory loss, especially with upper motor neuron findings (Pattern 5) (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Multifocal motor neuropathy - treatable mimic of motor neuron disease (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Radiculopathy, plexopathy, and compressive/entrapment mononeuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Neuromuscular junction disorders - myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Myopathy, including isolated neck extensor myopathy (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Combined system degeneration - distal sensory signs plus symmetric upper motor neuron involvement from B12, copper, or vitamin E deficiency, severe hepatic disease, or adrenomyeloneuropathy (Pattern 6) (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Critical illness polyneuropathy versus critical illness myopathy in the ICU patient who cannot be weaned from the ventilator (Harrison 22e p.3606)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies and needle EMG to localize (mono- vs polyneuropathy vs radiculopathy/plexopathy) and classify as axonal vs demyelinating (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Autonomic testing (heart rate response to deep breathing, Valsalva, tilt-table, quantitative sudomotor axon reflex) for pure small-fiber or autonomic neuropathy when routine NCS are normal (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Baseline labs: CBC, electrolytes, renal and hepatic function, fasting glucose and HbA1c, thyroid function, B12, folate, ESR, rheumatoid factor, ANA, SPEP with immunofixation, and serum/urine free light chains (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Oral glucose tolerance test in painful sensory neuropathy even when fasting glucose and HbA1c are normal (abnormal in ~one-third) (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Serum free light chains and kappa/lambda ratio (more sensitive than SPEP/immunofixation) and skeletal survey if amyloidosis or monoclonal gammopathy suspected; hematology referral for marrow biopsy (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Vasculitis workup for mononeuropathy multiplex: ANCA, cryoglobulins, hepatitis serology, Lyme Western blot, HIV, occasionally CMV titer (Harrison 22e p.3598)","Lumbar puncture in suspected GBS/CIDP for elevated CSF protein (pleocytosis should be absent in idiopathic cases) (Harrison 22e p.3598)","Antibodies to SS-A/Ro, SS-B/La, and antineuronal nuclear (anti-Hu) antibodies in sensory ataxia/ganglionopathy; nerve biopsy reserved mainly for suspected amyloid or vasculitis and only when NCS are abnormal; skin biopsy for small-fiber neuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3598)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive symmetric proximal and distal weakness with sensory loss (Pattern 1) - acute (GBS) or chronic (CIDP) demyelinating neuropathy; GBS can require respiratory support, so identifying this treatable pattern is critical (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Acute/subacute course (days to weeks) - GBS, vasculitis, diabetic or Lyme radiculopathy require prompt evaluation (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Autonomic dysfunction without diabetes (orthostatic drop without compensatory tachycardia, bowel/bladder/sexual dysfunction) - should raise concern for amyloid polyneuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3595)","Mononeuropathy multiplex pattern - suggests vasculitis and warrants an urgent vasculitis workup (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Severe sensory ataxia from a ganglionopathy - consider paraneoplastic (anti-Hu, often small-cell lung cancer); the neuropathy can precede detection of malignancy and should prompt a cancer search (Harrison 22e p.3598)"],"prognosis":"Most neuropathies are insidious and slowly progressive; course may be monophasic, progressive, or relapsing (e.g., CIDP, porphyria). Despite extensive evaluation, about half of patients have no etiology identified (cryptogenic/idiopathic sensory polyneuropathy). Outcome depends heavily on cause: treatable acquired demyelinating neuropathies (GBS, CIDP) and treatable systemic causes can respond to therapy, whereas axonal degeneration tends to recover slowly and incompletely. Even with adequate cobalamin replacement, treatment of B12-deficiency neuropathy usually does not fully reverse deficits and at least 50% of patients retain some permanent neurologic deficit (Harrison 22e p.3595, p.3612).","pitfalls":["Missing a treatable cause - acquired demyelinating neuropathy (GBS/CIDP) and multifocal motor neuropathy are treatable and should be distinguished from untreatable mimics such as ALS (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Overlooking treatable systemic/metabolic causes such as B12 deficiency, thyroid disease, or a paraprotein/monoclonal gammopathy in an apparently idiopathic polyneuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3597)","B12-deficiency neuropathy can occur with only peripheral findings and without anemia or macrocytosis (lacking in up to 40%); check methylmalonic acid and homocysteine when suspicion is high (Harrison 22e p.3612)","Routine antiganglioside autoantibody panels have no proven added value beyond a complete clinical exam and detailed EDx, and a heavy metal screen is not a useful blind screening test without exposure history or suggestive findings (Harrison 22e p.3598)","Nerve biopsy rarely distinguishes one neuropathy from another beyond clinical exam and NCS; reserve it for suspected amyloid or vasculitis and only when NCS are abnormal (Harrison 22e p.3598)","A hereditary neuropathy can be missed when patients have marked sensory signs but few sensory symptoms plus slowly progressive distal weakness and skeletal clues (high/flat arches, hammer toes, scoliosis); examining family members may be needed (Harrison 22e p.3597)"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying cause: optimise glycaemic control in diabetes, correct vitamin B12 or folate deficiency, withdraw offending drugs and toxins and abstain from alcohol","Treat immune-mediated demyelinating neuropathy specifically, with intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange for Guillain-Barre syndrome and corticosteroids, immunoglobulin or plasma exchange for CIDP","Relieve neuropathic pain with agents such as gabapentinoids, duloxetine or tricyclic antidepressants","Provide supportive care with foot care, protection of insensate skin, gait aids and physiotherapy or occupational therapy","Manage autonomic features such as postural hypotension and gastrointestinal and bladder dysfunction symptomatically","Complete a structured aetiological work-up with nerve conduction studies and EMG plus metabolic and immunological screening to identify treatable causes","Refer to neurology and provide genetic counselling where a hereditary neuropathy such as Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease is identified"],"severityClassification":"No formal numeric severity score is used; characterization is by anatomic/electrophysiologic classification rather than a grading scale - by fiber type affected (motor, sensory, autonomic, or combined; large- vs small-fiber), by distribution (mononeuropathy, mononeuropathy multiplex, polyneuropathy, radiculopathy, plexopathy; ten clinical patterns in Table 457-2), by underlying pathology (axonal degeneration vs segmental demyelination on NCS/EMG, Table 457-3), and by temporal course (acute: days to 4 weeks; subacute: 4-8 weeks; chronic: >8 weeks) (Harrison 22e p.3595, p.3597).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3594","Harrison 22e p.3595","Harrison 22e p.3596","Harrison 22e p.3597","Harrison 22e p.3598","Harrison 22e p.3604","Harrison 22e p.3612"],"crossLinks":["gbs","diabetes_mellitus","vasculitis"]},"peritonsillar_abscess":{"name":"Peritonsillar abscess","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["quinsy","peritonsillar cellulitis progressing to abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A collection of pus in the peritonsillar space, usually a complication of acute tonsillitis, most common in young adults","Presents with severe unilateral sore throat, progressively worsening odynophagia, fever and marked malaise","Classic signs include trismus, a muffled hot-potato voice, drooling and foul breath","Examination shows a bulging, congested soft palate that displaces the uvula and tonsil toward the opposite side","Management principles include drainage of pus by needle aspiration or incision, together with systemic antibiotics, analgesia and hydration","Interval tonsillectomy is often advised after resolution, particularly with recurrent abscesses or a background of recurrent tonsillitis","Trismus can make examination and airway assessment difficult, so vigilance for airway compromise is essential","Untreated infection can spread to the parapharyngeal space and beyond, risking deep neck infection and airway obstruction"],"pathophysiology":"Peritonsillar abscess develops when infection extends beyond the tonsillar capsule into the loose peritonsillar space between the tonsil and the superior constrictor muscle, typically as a progression of acute tonsillitis or from obstructed minor salivary (Weber) glands. Cellulitis in this space evolves into a localised collection of pus, most often at the upper pole. The expanding abscess pushes the tonsil and soft palate medially, irritates the pterygoid muscles to cause trismus, and can dissect into adjacent deep neck spaces, threatening the airway and vascular structures.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe acute tonsillitis without abscess","Parapharyngeal or retropharyngeal abscess","Infectious mononucleosis with tonsillar enlargement","Tonsillar or oropharyngeal malignancy","Dental or peritonsillar space infection from another source","Epiglottitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Oropharyngeal examination assessing palatal bulge, uvular deviation and trismus","Needle aspiration to confirm and localise pus","Full blood count and inflammatory markers","Contrast CT of the neck when deep neck space extension is suspected or examination is limited","Airway assessment and monitoring in severe cases"],"redFlags":["Signs of airway compromise such as stridor, respiratory distress, severe trismus or drooling requiring urgent airway management","Rapid spread into parapharyngeal or retropharyngeal spaces indicating deep neck infection","Neck swelling, torticollis or signs of vascular involvement","Systemic sepsis or failure to improve after drainage and antibiotics"],"prognosis":"With timely drainage and antibiotics the outlook is good, but delayed treatment can lead to airway obstruction, deep neck space infection, mediastinitis or vascular complications, which carry significant risk.","pitfalls":["Underestimating airway risk in a patient with severe trismus and drooling","Treating with antibiotics alone when pus requires drainage","Missing extension into deep neck spaces","Failing to arrange follow-up and consider interval tonsillectomy in recurrent cases"],"management":["Drain the pus by needle aspiration or incision and drainage as the key intervention","Give systemic antibiotics covering streptococci and oral anaerobes, such as penicillin with metronidazole or co-amoxiclav","Provide supportive care with analgesia, antipyretics and intravenous fluid rehydration","Consider a short course of corticosteroid to reduce pain, trismus and swelling","Assess and monitor the airway in severe cases and obtain contrast CT of the neck when deep neck space extension is suspected or examination is limited","Offer interval tonsillectomy after resolution, particularly with recurrent abscesses or a background of recurrent tonsillitis","Refer urgently to ENT for failure to improve or for complications such as spreading deep neck infection or airway compromise"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"perivascular_epithelioid_cell_tumor":{"name":"Perivascular epithelioid cell tumour","system":"Oncology / Pathology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PEComa","perivascular epithelioid cell neoplasm","PEComa (TSC/TFE3-associated)","PEComa not otherwise specified","PEC tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A family of mesenchymal tumours composed of distinctive perivascular epithelioid cells showing dual melanocytic and smooth-muscle (myoid) differentiation","Co-expresses melanocytic markers (HMB-45, Melan-A, MiTF) and myoid markers (smooth muscle actin, desmin), a combination that is essentially diagnostic","Most cases are linked to inactivation of TSC1 or TSC2 with downstream activation of the mTOR pathway; a distinct subset harbours TFE3 gene rearrangement","The PEComa family includes angiomyolipoma, pulmonary and lymph-node lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM) and clear cell sugar tumour, and is associated with the tuberous sclerosis complex","Occurs across many sites including uterus, retroperitoneum, gastrointestinal tract, soft tissue and viscera, with a female predominance overall","Malignancy is predicted by features such as large size, high mitotic activity, necrosis, marked atypia and infiltrative growth, using recognised risk-stratification criteria","mTOR inhibition (for example sirolimus or related agents) is an effective targeted treatment in advanced or malignant TSC-associated disease"],"pathophysiology":"PEComas arise from perivascular epithelioid cells that uniquely coexpress melanocytic and smooth-muscle phenotypes. In most tumours, loss of TSC1 or TSC2 removes inhibition of the mechanistic target of rapamycin (mTOR), driving unregulated growth and providing the rationale for mTOR-inhibitor therapy. A separate molecular subset is defined by TFE3 rearrangement, which activates a transcription factor programme and tends to occur in younger patients without a tuberous sclerosis background. The shared histology and immunophenotype unite the family despite these distinct genetic mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Angiomyolipoma and other members of the PEComa family","Gastrointestinal stromal tumour","Smooth muscle tumours such as leiomyoma and leiomyosarcoma","Metastatic melanoma","Clear cell renal cell carcinoma and other clear cell neoplasms","Alveolar soft part sarcoma, particularly in TFE3-driven cases","Adrenocortical or other clear cell visceral tumours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Immunohistochemistry for melanocytic markers (HMB-45, Melan-A) and myoid markers (SMA, desmin)","TFE3 immunohistochemistry and FISH in tumours suggesting the rearranged subset","Molecular testing for TSC1/TSC2 alterations where relevant to therapy","Assessment for stigmata of tuberous sclerosis complex, including renal angiomyolipomas and pulmonary LAM","Cross-sectional imaging to define the primary tumour and detect metastases","Application of malignancy risk-stratification criteria based on size, mitoses, necrosis and atypia"],"redFlags":["Large tumour with high mitotic count, necrosis or marked nuclear atypia indicating malignant potential","Infiltrative growth or vascular invasion","Metastatic disease, most often to lung, liver or bone","Features of tuberous sclerosis complex requiring wider evaluation","Retroperitoneal haemorrhage from a large angiomyolipoma-type lesion"],"prognosis":"Many PEComas are benign and cured by complete excision, but malignant tumours defined by adverse histological features can recur and metastasise; advanced and malignant TSC-associated disease often responds to mTOR inhibition, which has substantially improved outcomes for patients with unresectable or metastatic tumours.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing PEComa as metastatic melanoma because of HMB-45 positivity, without recognising the myoid coexpression","Overlooking the diagnosis in visceral or uterine sites where smooth muscle tumours are expected","Failing to consider a TFE3-rearranged PEComa in a younger patient with epithelioid clear cell morphology","Not evaluating for tuberous sclerosis complex when a PEComa or angiomyolipoma is found","Assuming all PEComas are benign and omitting risk stratification"],"management":["Undertake complete surgical excision with clear margins as the mainstay for localised PEComa","Use an mTOR inhibitor such as sirolimus or everolimus as disease-specific systemic therapy for unresectable, advanced or malignant TSC- and mTOR-driven tumours","Risk-stratify each tumour by size, mitotic activity, necrosis and atypia to guide the intensity of surveillance","Assess for stigmata of the tuberous sclerosis complex, including renal angiomyolipomas and pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis, and screen accordingly","Monitor with cross-sectional imaging for local recurrence and metastasis after treatment","Manage within a sarcoma and oncology multidisciplinary team and offer genetic evaluation where tuberous sclerosis complex is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","WHO Classification of Tumours: Female Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"perivascular_epithelioid_cell_tumour":{"name":"Perivascular Epithelioid Cell Tumour","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PEComa","Perivascular epithelioid cell neoplasm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Family of mesenchymal tumours built from perivascular epithelioid cells that characteristically co-express melanocytic markers (HMB-45, Melan-A, MITF) and smooth muscle markers (smooth muscle actin, desmin).","The family includes angiomyolipoma, pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis, clear cell sugar tumour of the lung and PEComa not otherwise specified of soft tissue and viscera.","Strong association with the tuberous sclerosis complex through loss of TSC1 or TSC2 and consequent activation of the mTOR pathway.","A biologically distinct subset is driven by TFE3 gene fusions; these arise in younger patients, lack a tuberous sclerosis association and show strong nuclear TFE3 staining with alveolar architecture.","There is a female predominance, and common sites include the uterus, retroperitoneum, gastrointestinal tract and soft tissue.","Malignant potential is stratified using features such as large size, infiltrative margins, high nuclear grade, high mitotic rate, necrosis and vascular invasion.","mTOR inhibitors (for example sirolimus and everolimus) are effective in advanced or malignant disease, reflecting the underlying pathway activation.","Complete surgical excision is the treatment of choice for localised tumours."],"pathophysiology":"Perivascular epithelioid cell tumours arise from the perivascular epithelioid cell, a cell type with no clear normal tissue counterpart that shows dual melanocytic and smooth muscle differentiation and is typically arranged around thin-walled blood vessels with clear to granular eosinophilic cytoplasm. Most cases are underpinned by biallelic inactivation of TSC1 or TSC2, which releases mTOR complex 1 from inhibition and drives cell growth and proliferation; this molecular basis links the sporadic tumours to the lesions of the tuberous sclerosis complex, such as renal angiomyolipoma and pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis. A separate group is defined by fusions involving the TFE3 transcription factor gene, which activate a distinct transcriptional programme, occur in younger patients without tuberous sclerosis, and show strong nuclear TFE3 expression. The dual immunophenotype — positivity for HMB-45, Melan-A and MITF alongside smooth muscle actin and desmin — is the diagnostic hallmark and reflects this hybrid differentiation. Behaviour ranges from benign to overtly malignant, and because mTOR signalling is central to the tuberous-sclerosis-associated lesions these tumours are frequently responsive to mTOR inhibition even in the metastatic setting.","additionalDifferentials":["Metastatic melanoma","Clear cell sarcoma","Clear cell renal cell carcinoma","Smooth muscle tumours (leiomyoma and leiomyosarcoma)","Alveolar soft part sarcoma, given the TFE3 overlap","Adrenocortical carcinoma","Gastrointestinal stromal tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) to characterise the lesion and assess extent","Histopathology with an immunohistochemical panel including HMB-45, Melan-A, smooth muscle actin, desmin and TFE3","Molecular testing for TFE3 rearrangement and TSC1/TSC2 status","Clinical and genetic assessment for the tuberous sclerosis complex when suggested by other features","Staging imaging including the chest when malignant features are present","Dedicated renal imaging where angiomyolipoma is suspected"],"redFlags":["Large, rapidly growing or retroperitoneal tumours with infiltrative margins","High mitotic activity, marked atypia or coagulative necrosis on histology","Multiple lesions or other stigmata of the tuberous sclerosis complex","Acute retroperitoneal haemorrhage from a renal angiomyolipoma (Wunderlich syndrome)"],"prognosis":"Benign and small tumours excised completely have an excellent prognosis, whereas malignant perivascular epithelioid cell tumours can metastasise to lung, liver and bone and carry a guarded outlook. Responsiveness to mTOR inhibitors has improved outcomes in advanced disease.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the tumour for melanoma because of HMB-45 and Melan-A positivity","Underestimating malignant potential in visceral and retroperitoneal lesions","Failing to recognise an associated tuberous sclerosis complex","Not considering mTOR inhibitor therapy in advanced or malignant disease"],"management":["Complete surgical resection with clear margins is the mainstay for localised perivascular epithelioid cell tumours","mTOR inhibitors such as sirolimus, everolimus or nab-sirolimus for unresectable, recurrent or metastatic disease, reflecting the TSC1/TSC2-mTOR mechanism","Risk-stratify the resected tumour using large size, infiltrative margins, high nuclear grade, high mitotic rate, necrosis and vascular invasion to set follow-up intensity","Surveillance cross-sectional imaging of the primary site and chest for local recurrence and lung, liver or bone metastasis","Assess for tuberous sclerosis complex, with genetic counselling and family screening where a TSC association is confirmed","Manage through a specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team, escalating to systemic therapy or trial enrolment for advanced or TFE3-fusion disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone","Gynaecological and soft tissue pathology reference texts","Soft tissue sarcoma clinical practice guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"periventricular_nodular_heterotopia":{"name":"Periventricular nodular heterotopia","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PVNH","periventricular heterotopia","subependymal heterotopia","FLNA (filamin A)","ARFGEF2"],"chapter":null,"source":"Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A malformation of neuronal migration in which nodules of grey matter fail to migrate and remain lining the walls of the lateral ventricles","On MRI the nodules follow grey-matter signal on all sequences, are typically smooth and rounded, and do not enhance, distinguishing them from subependymal tumours","The classic form is X-linked, caused by FLNA (filamin A) variants, and is often bilateral and nodular; it is usually lethal in males, so most affected individuals are female","FLNA-related heterotopia carries systemic associations including cardiovascular anomalies such as aortic and valvular disease, and a bleeding or connective-tissue tendency, warranting cardiac surveillance","The autosomal recessive form due to ARFGEF2 is associated with microcephaly and more severe developmental impairment","Many individuals present in adolescence or adulthood with focal epilepsy and otherwise normal or near-normal intellect, particularly in the FLNA form","Diagnosis is by MRI recognition of grey-matter nodules along the ventricular margin combined with targeted genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Periventricular nodular heterotopia results from failure of neurons to migrate away from the germinal (ventricular) zone during cortical development, so that collections of neurons persist as nodules along the ependymal lining of the lateral ventricles. The X-linked form is caused by variants in FLNA, which encodes filamin A, an actin-binding protein required for the cytoskeletal dynamics that drive early neuronal migration and also important in vascular and connective tissue, explaining the cardiovascular associations. The autosomal recessive ARFGEF2 form disrupts vesicle trafficking needed for migration and proliferation, producing microcephaly with heterotopia.","additionalDifferentials":["Subependymal nodules of tuberous sclerosis, which may calcify and enhance","Subependymal spread of tumour or subependymoma","Subcortical band heterotopia and other heterotopia patterns","Tuberous sclerosis complex more broadly","Grey-matter signal artefact along the ventricle","Polymicrogyria with associated heterotopia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to identify grey-matter-signal nodules lining the ventricles and any cortical involvement","Molecular genetic testing including FLNA analysis and, where indicated, ARFGEF2","Cardiac evaluation with echocardiography and aortic imaging in FLNA-related disease","Assessment for bleeding tendency and connective-tissue features in FLNA variants","Electroencephalography to characterise focal epilepsy","Developmental and cognitive assessment"],"redFlags":["Aortic dilatation, valvular disease, or other cardiovascular complications in FLNA-related heterotopia","Bleeding tendency associated with filamin A dysfunction","Drug-resistant focal epilepsy","Microcephaly with severe developmental impairment suggesting the ARFGEF2 form"],"prognosis":"In the common FLNA-related form, many individuals have normal or near-normal cognition with focal epilepsy that is often manageable, though cardiovascular and vascular complications can affect long-term health. The ARFGEF2 form and syndromic presentations carry a poorer neurodevelopmental prognosis.","pitfalls":["Mistaking heterotopic nodules for the enhancing, sometimes calcified subependymal nodules of tuberous sclerosis","Failing to arrange cardiovascular surveillance in FLNA-related disease","Overlooking X-linked inheritance and its implications for male fetal loss and family counselling","Attributing new focal seizures in an adult to another cause without considering heterotopia on MRI"],"management":["Antiseizure medication tailored to the focal epilepsy, which is the usual presenting problem","Consider resective epilepsy surgery, laser interstitial thermal ablation or neurostimulation for drug-resistant focal seizures","Cardiovascular surveillance with echocardiography and aortic imaging in FLNA-related disease to detect aortic and valvular disease","Assess for a bleeding tendency and connective-tissue features in FLNA variants and take precautions around surgery or delivery","Developmental, cognitive and educational support, particularly in the more severe ARFGEF2 form with microcephaly","Genetic counselling and family screening, recognising X-linked FLNA (usually male-lethal) and autosomal recessive ARFGEF2 inheritance","Coordinate care across neurology, clinical genetics and cardiology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","GeneReviews: FLNA-Related Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia"],"crossLinks":[]},"permanent_neonatal_diabetes_mellitus":{"name":"Permanent neonatal diabetes mellitus","system":"Endocrine / Monogenic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PNDM","Permanent monogenic neonatal diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Monogenic diabetes references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Diabetes presenting within the first six months of life is almost never autoimmune type 1 disease; a monogenic cause should be assumed and pursued urgently.","Activating mutations in the beta-cell ATP-sensitive potassium channel subunits (KCNJ11/Kir6.2 and ABCC8/SUR1) are the commonest causes and are frequently responsive to high-dose oral sulfonylureas, allowing transfer off insulin.","Heterozygous insulin (INS) gene mutations cause misfolding of proinsulin with endoplasmic reticulum stress and beta-cell loss, and typically require lifelong insulin.","Reduced foetal insulin secretion impairs intrauterine growth, so affected infants are commonly small for gestational age at birth.","The severe end of the KCNJ11 spectrum is the DEND phenotype (developmental delay, epilepsy and neonatal diabetes), reflecting channel expression in brain, muscle and nerve.","Islet autoantibodies are negative and C-peptide is low or undetectable, supporting a non-autoimmune beta-cell defect.","Establishing the precise genotype changes treatment, prognosis, surveillance and family counselling, so genetic testing is not merely confirmatory."],"pathophysiology":"Permanent neonatal diabetes is a genetically heterogeneous group of monogenic disorders in which insulin deficiency arises from disrupted beta-cell function or development rather than autoimmune destruction. The commonest mechanism is a gain-of-function defect in the beta-cell ATP-sensitive potassium channel: activating mutations in KCNJ11 (encoding the Kir6.2 pore) or ABCC8 (encoding the SUR1 regulatory subunit) hold the channel open despite rising intracellular ATP, preventing the membrane depolarisation and calcium influx that trigger insulin exocytosis; because sulfonylureas close this channel independently of ATP, many such patients can be managed on oral agents. A second important mechanism is dominant misfolding of preproinsulin from INS mutations, generating chronic endoplasmic reticulum stress and progressive beta-cell apoptosis. Other genotypes disrupt pancreatic development or beta-cell survival, including homozygous glucokinase deficiency (loss of the glucose sensor), transcription-factor defects causing pancreatic hypoplasia or agenesis, and recessive syndromic causes. The unifying result is profound insulinopenia presenting in early infancy with hyperglycaemia, poor weight gain and, in some, ketoacidosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Transient neonatal diabetes mellitus (6q24 imprinting or potassium-channel related, remitting course)","Wolcott-Rallison syndrome (EIF2AK3) with skeletal dysplasia and episodic liver failure","IPEX syndrome (FOXP3) with early autoimmune enteropathy and dermatitis","Syndromic pancreatic agenesis or hypoplasia (GATA6, PDX1, PTF1A, GLIS3)","Early-onset autoimmune type 1 diabetes presenting later in infancy","Stress or transient hyperglycaemia of the sick or preterm neonate"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted or panel genetic testing covering KCNJ11, ABCC8, INS and related neonatal-diabetes genes","Serum C-peptide with paired glucose to confirm endogenous insulin deficiency","Islet autoantibody panel (expected negative) to exclude autoimmune diabetes","Capillary and venous glucose, blood gas and ketone assessment for acidosis","Pancreatic imaging and exocrine function tests when agenesis or hypoplasia is suspected","Developmental and neurological assessment where a potassium-channel genotype raises concern for DEND features"],"redFlags":["Diabetic ketoacidosis with dehydration and acidotic breathing at presentation","Seizures or marked developmental delay suggesting the severe potassium-channel (DEND) phenotype","Failure to thrive with persistent hyperglycaemia despite feeding","Family history of consanguinity or affected siblings pointing to a recessive syndromic cause"],"prognosis":"With a precise genetic diagnosis and genotype-directed therapy, glycaemic control and quality of life are often excellent, and potassium-channel forms may achieve stable control on sulfonylureas alone. Long-term outcome depends heavily on genotype, with neurodevelopmental impairment in severe channel mutations and multisystem morbidity in syndromic causes.","pitfalls":["Reflexively labelling early diabetes as type 1 and committing an infant to lifelong insulin when a sulfonylurea-responsive channel defect is present.","Delaying genetic testing until after discharge, missing the window to trial oral therapy.","Overlooking subtle neurological signs that reclassify a KCNJ11 case within the DEND spectrum.","Attributing small birth size solely to placental causes rather than recognising reduced foetal insulin.","Assuming negative autoantibodies alone confirm the subtype without defining the causative gene."],"management":["Obtain urgent genetic testing, as the genotype directs treatment in this monogenic diabetes","Transfer ATP-sensitive potassium channel forms (KCNJ11/Kir6.2, ABCC8/SUR1) from insulin to high-dose oral sulfonylureas where the genotype allows","Provide lifelong insulin replacement for INS-gene and other insulin-deficient forms not responsive to sulfonylureas","Stabilise the acute presentation with intravenous fluids, insulin and correction of ketoacidosis and dehydration","Monitor growth and glycaemic control and, in potassium-channel genotypes, watch for DEND neurological features such as developmental delay and epilepsy","Deliver care through paediatric endocrinology with diabetes nursing and dietetic input","Offer genetic counselling and family screening given the heritable, monogenic basis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric endocrinology reference texts","International consensus guidance on monogenic and neonatal diabetes","Clinical genetics reference resources on beta-cell disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"pernicious_anemia":{"name":"Pernicious anaemia","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Addison-Biermer anaemia","autoimmune B12 deficiency","intrinsic factor deficiency anaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / AABB technical manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autoimmune cause of vitamin B12 deficiency resulting from destruction of gastric parietal cells and loss of intrinsic factor","Produces a macrocytic megaloblastic anaemia, often with a raised mean cell volume, hypersegmented neutrophils and oval macrocytes on the film","Neurological features include subacute combined degeneration of the cord with paraesthesiae, sensory ataxia and impaired proprioception","Intrinsic-factor antibodies are specific but insensitive, whereas parietal-cell antibodies are sensitive but less specific","Autoimmune atrophic gastritis underlies the condition and carries an increased risk of gastric carcinoma and carcinoid tumours","Associated with other autoimmune conditions such as thyroid disease, vitiligo and type 1 diabetes","Management is lifelong vitamin B12 replacement, and neurological damage may become irreversible if treatment is delayed","Giving folate alone to a B12-deficient patient can precipitate or worsen neurological deterioration"],"pathophysiology":"Pernicious anaemia is caused by autoimmune destruction of the gastric mucosa. Immune attack on parietal cells produces atrophic gastritis and abolishes secretion of intrinsic factor, the protein required to bind dietary vitamin B12 and enable its absorption in the terminal ileum. Autoantibodies to intrinsic factor further block this process. The resulting B12 deficiency impairs DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing cells, causing megaloblastic maturation in the marrow with ineffective erythropoiesis and a macrocytic anaemia. Because B12 is also required for myelin metabolism, deficiency damages the dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts, producing subacute combined degeneration. Loss of acid and intrinsic factor secretion also impairs absorption of other nutrients and predisposes to gastric neoplasia.","additionalDifferentials":["Dietary vitamin B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency","Malabsorption such as coeliac disease or ileal resection","Myelodysplastic syndrome","Drug-induced macrocytosis","Alcohol-related or liver-disease macrocytosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film showing macrocytosis and hypersegmented neutrophils","Serum vitamin B12, typically low, with folate to assess for combined deficiency","Intrinsic-factor antibodies, highly specific for the diagnosis","Parietal-cell antibodies, sensitive but less specific","Markers of ineffective erythropoiesis such as raised lactate dehydrogenase and bilirubin","Endoscopy and gastric biopsy where atrophic gastritis or neoplasia is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neurological signs of subacute combined degeneration requiring prompt replacement","Severe anaemia with cardiovascular compromise","Alarm symptoms suggesting gastric carcinoma in a patient with atrophic gastritis","Pancytopenia from profound megaloblastic marrow failure"],"prognosis":"Haematological recovery with vitamin B12 replacement is excellent and rapid, but neurological deficits may only partly resolve if treatment is delayed, and lifelong surveillance is needed for the associated risk of gastric malignancy.","pitfalls":["Giving folate without B12 and precipitating neurological deterioration","Excluding the diagnosis on a normal mean cell volume, which can occur with coexisting iron deficiency","Overlooking neurological features that may precede overt anaemia","Failing to consider the increased long-term risk of gastric carcinoma"],"management":["Replace vitamin B12 with parenteral hydroxocobalamin, using an initial loading course followed by lifelong maintenance injections","Consider high-dose oral cobalamin for maintenance in selected, stable patients","Correct coexisting folate and iron deficiency, and monitor for hypokalaemia during the early haematological response","Assess for subacute combined degeneration and treat promptly, as delayed replacement leaves residual neurological deficits","Undertake endoscopic evaluation and surveillance for gastric carcinoma and carcinoid, given the underlying autoimmune atrophic gastritis","Screen for associated autoimmune disease such as thyroid disorder, vitiligo and type 1 diabetes","Monitor the haematological response with reticulocytosis and a normalising full blood count to confirm adequate replacement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"pernio_lupus":{"name":"pernio lupus","system":"Skin and subcutaneous tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chilblain lupus erythematosus","lupus pernio of Hutchinson (chilblain lupus)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Chilblain lupus erythematosus is a chronic form of cutaneous lupus in which cold-induced, tender violaceous papules and plaques develop on acral sites such as fingers, toes, ears, nose and heels.","It should not be confused with sarcoidosis-related lupus pernio, a quite different entity affecting the central face; chilblain lupus is a lupus-specific dermatosis provoked by cold and damp.","Lesions resemble idiopathic chilblains but are more persistent, may ulcerate or become hyperkeratotic and fissured, and do not resolve promptly with warming alone.","A proportion of patients meet criteria for or later develop systemic lupus erythematosus, so systemic assessment and long-term monitoring are important.","A rare familial form of chilblain lupus is caused by mutations in TREX1 or SAMHD1 and belongs to the type I interferonopathy spectrum, overlapping with Aicardi-Goutieres syndrome.","Histology shows an interface dermatitis with a superficial and deep perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate, and direct immunofluorescence often demonstrates lupus-band deposition at the dermoepidermal junction.","Management centres on cold avoidance, warm clothing and smoking cessation, with topical or systemic corticosteroids, antimalarials and calcium-channel blockers used for persistent disease.","Distinguishing chilblain lupus from ordinary perniosis rests on chronicity, acral photodistribution, histopathology, immunofluorescence and lupus serology."],"pathophysiology":"Chilblain lupus is a cold-triggered variant of chronic cutaneous lupus erythematosus in which low temperature and microvascular vasoconstriction combine with a lupus-specific inflammatory response. Cold-induced hypoperfusion of acral skin, together with an interface dermatitis driven by autoreactive T lymphocytes and a type I interferon signature, produces the characteristic tender violaceous lesions. Immune complex deposition and complement activation at the dermoepidermal junction contribute to tissue injury, and impaired clearance of nucleic acids underlies the familial monogenic forms associated with TREX1 and SAMHD1 mutations, which drive chronic interferon overproduction. The acral, cold-exposed distribution reflects the additive effect of vasospastic hypoxia and photo- and cold-provoked immunological activity on skin already predisposed by the lupus diathesis.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic perniosis (chilblains)","Systemic lupus erythematosus with acral involvement","Sarcoidosis (true lupus pernio of the face)","Raynaud phenomenon and other vasospastic disorders","Cryoglobulinaemia and cold agglutinin disease","Acral vasculitis","Chilblain-like lesions of the interferonopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy for histopathology showing interface dermatitis with deep perivascular infiltrate","Direct immunofluorescence for a lupus band at the dermoepidermal junction","Antinuclear antibody and extractable nuclear antigen panel","Complement levels and anti-dsDNA antibodies to assess systemic activity","Cryoglobulins and cold agglutinins to exclude cold-precipitable proteins","Antiphospholipid antibody screen","Genetic testing for TREX1/SAMHD1 in familial or early-onset cases"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting evolution to systemic lupus such as arthritis, nephritis or cytopenias","Ulceration or necrosis of acral lesions","Family history or early childhood onset suggesting a monogenic interferonopathy","Systemic symptoms including fever, weight loss or serositis","Laboratory evidence of active systemic disease (low complement, high anti-dsDNA)"],"prognosis":"Chilblain lupus tends to run a chronic, relapsing course closely linked to cold exposure and may persist for years; a minority progress to systemic lupus erythematosus, so periodic reassessment is warranted.","pitfalls":["Confusing chilblain lupus with the facial sarcoidal lupus pernio, an unrelated condition","Dismissing persistent acral lesions as ordinary chilblains and missing the lupus diagnosis","Failing to screen for systemic lupus at diagnosis and during follow-up","Overlooking a familial interferonopathy in early-onset or familial cases","Attributing lesions solely to cold and neglecting biopsy and serology"],"management":["Cold and damp avoidance with insulation of the extremities and smoking cessation form the essential first-line measure","Topical corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors for individual acral lesions","Antimalarials such as hydroxychloroquine are the mainstay systemic therapy, with a calcium-channel blocker such as nifedipine added for the vasospastic component","Escalate refractory disease to systemic immunomodulation, with JAK inhibitors particularly relevant in TREX1/SAMHD1-related interferonopathy forms","Monitor for evolution into systemic lupus erythematosus with periodic review and complement, anti-dsDNA and autoantibody testing","Offer genetic counselling and consider TREX1/SAMHD1 testing in familial chilblain lupus within the type I interferonopathy spectrum","Coordinate dermatology and rheumatology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Dubois' Lupus Erythematosus and Related Syndromes"],"crossLinks":[]},"peroxisomal_biogenesis_disorder_mild":{"name":"Mild/atypical peroxisome biogenesis disorder (Zellweger spectrum)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Zellweger spectrum disorder","ZSD","PEX gene disorder","atypical peroxisome biogenesis disorder","milder Zellweger spectrum"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of autosomal recessive disorders of peroxisome assembly caused by variants in PEX genes, most commonly PEX1; the milder end of the spectrum presents later than classic neonatal Zellweger syndrome","Milder and atypical cases can present in later childhood or adulthood with a slowly progressive, multisystem picture rather than overwhelming neonatal disease","Common features include sensorineural hearing loss, retinitis pigmentosa with progressive visual loss, developmental delay or learning difficulty, cerebellar ataxia and a peripheral neuropathy","Additional involvement may include hepatic dysfunction, adrenal insufficiency and enamel or skeletal abnormalities, reflecting the wide tissue distribution of peroxisomal metabolism","The biochemical hallmark is elevated very long chain fatty acids, and a full peroxisomal panel typically shows raised phytanic and pristanic acids, abnormal bile acid intermediates and reduced red cell plasmalogens","MRI may show a leukodystrophy or cerebellar atrophy, but the diagnostic emphasis is on biochemistry first, then PEX gene confirmation","Management is supportive and multidisciplinary, addressing vision and hearing, adrenal and liver surveillance, nutrition and neurorehabilitation"],"pathophysiology":"Peroxisome biogenesis disorders of the Zellweger spectrum are caused by biallelic variants in PEX genes, which encode peroxins required to assemble functional peroxisomes and import their enzymes. Defective peroxisome assembly impairs multiple metabolic pathways, including beta-oxidation of very long chain fatty acids, alpha-oxidation of phytanic acid and synthesis of plasmalogens and bile acids. Accumulation of very long chain fatty acids and phytanic acid, together with deficiency of plasmalogens, damages the nervous system, retina, inner ear, liver and adrenal glands. Milder variants retain some residual peroxisomal function, which accounts for the later onset and slower progression compared with severe neonatal disease.","additionalDifferentials":["X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy and single peroxisomal enzyme defects","Refsum disease and other isolated phytanic acid disorders","Usher syndrome (combined hearing and visual loss)","Mitochondrial disease with multisystem and neurological involvement","Other genetic causes of retinitis pigmentosa with ataxia","Congenital disorders of glycosylation with multisystem features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma very long chain fatty acid profile as the key screening biochemistry","Extended peroxisomal panel including phytanic and pristanic acids, bile acid intermediates and red cell plasmalogens","PEX gene sequencing or panel to confirm the biogenesis defect and specific genotype","Ophthalmology assessment with electroretinography for retinitis pigmentosa","Audiometry for sensorineural hearing loss","Liver function tests and adrenal (cortisol/ACTH) assessment for systemic involvement","Brain MRI to characterise any leukodystrophy or cerebellar atrophy"],"redFlags":["Adrenal insufficiency with risk of crisis requiring urgent recognition and treatment","Progressive hepatic dysfunction with coagulopathy or failure to thrive","Rapid visual or auditory deterioration affecting communication and safety","Vitamin deficiency (including fat-soluble vitamins) from malabsorption","Acute neurological decline suggesting an intercurrent metabolic decompensation"],"prognosis":"Milder Zellweger spectrum disorders progress slowly and are compatible with survival into later childhood and adulthood, in contrast to the severe neonatal form; nevertheless the sensory, hepatic and adrenal involvement accumulates over time. Outcome depends on the degree of residual peroxisomal function, and proactive surveillance and supportive care can meaningfully improve quality of life.","pitfalls":["Not sending very long chain fatty acids in an older child or adult with unexplained retinitis pigmentosa, hearing loss and neurological signs","Overlooking adrenal insufficiency, which is treatable and potentially life-threatening","Assuming a normal MRI excludes the diagnosis, when biochemistry is the more sensitive test","Failing to monitor liver function and fat-soluble vitamin status","Missing genetic counselling for autosomal recessive recurrence risk"],"management":["Supportive, multisystem management as there is no curative therapy, with dietary phytanic acid restriction where phytanic acid is raised","Docosahexaenoic acid and fat-soluble vitamin (A, D, E and K) supplementation, plus bile acid supplementation for cholestatic liver involvement","Hearing rehabilitation with hearing aids or cochlear implantation for progressive sensorineural hearing loss","Ophthalmology follow-up and low-vision support for retinitis pigmentosa","Monitor for and replace adrenal insufficiency with glucocorticoids, and manage hepatic dysfunction and any coagulopathy","Developmental support and physiotherapy for cerebellar ataxia and peripheral neuropathy","Genetic counselling and family screening for the autosomal recessive PEX-gene defect","Coordinate care through a specialist metabolic multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"perrault_syndrome":{"name":"Perrault syndrome (SNHL + ovarian dysgenesis)","system":"Ear / Audiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Perrault syndrome","gonadal dysgenesis with deafness","ovarian dysgenesis-sensorineural deafness syndrome","CLPP-related Perrault syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal recessive disorder combining sensorineural hearing loss in both sexes with primary ovarian insufficiency in 46,XX females","Affected males have normal gonadal function, so the ovarian component is not apparent, and the diagnosis may be missed in isolated male cases","Hearing loss is usually bilateral and progressive, ranging from mild to profound","Females may present with primary or secondary amenorrhoea, absent puberty, or premature ovarian insufficiency","Some subtypes include a progressive neurological phenotype with ataxia and peripheral neuropathy","Several genes are implicated, many encoding proteins involved in mitochondrial function and protein quality control, such as CLPP, HARS2, LARS2, and C10orf2 (TWNK)","Management is multidisciplinary, combining audiological rehabilitation with endocrine and fertility care"],"pathophysiology":"Perrault syndrome is genetically heterogeneous, but many of the implicated genes encode proteins critical to mitochondrial function, including mitochondrial aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases and components of mitochondrial protein quality control and DNA maintenance. Impaired mitochondrial protein synthesis and homeostasis particularly affects tissues with high metabolic demand, so cochlear hair cells and the developing ovary are vulnerable, producing progressive sensorineural hearing loss and accelerated loss of ovarian follicles leading to ovarian dysgenesis or premature insufficiency. In neurological subtypes the same metabolic vulnerability extends to the cerebellum and peripheral nerves, adding ataxia and neuropathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Turner syndrome and other causes of gonadal dysgenesis","Isolated non-syndromic hereditary hearing loss","Other mitochondrial disorders with hearing loss","Autoimmune premature ovarian insufficiency","Fragile X-associated primary ovarian insufficiency","Usher and Pendred syndromes when systemic features overlap"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure-tone audiometry to characterise and monitor sensorineural loss","Karyotype to exclude Turner syndrome and confirm 46,XX status in females","Endocrine assessment including gonadotrophins and ovarian reserve markers with pelvic imaging","Molecular genetic testing of Perrault-associated genes such as CLPP, HARS2, LARS2, and TWNK","Neurological evaluation and nerve conduction studies where ataxia or neuropathy is suspected","Cross-sectional temporal bone imaging before cochlear implantation when indicated"],"redFlags":["Primary or premature ovarian insufficiency in a young female with hearing loss","Progressive neurological decline with ataxia or neuropathy","Profound progressive hearing loss limiting communication","Fertility and bone-health consequences of untreated ovarian insufficiency"],"prognosis":"Hearing loss tends to progress and is rehabilitated with amplification or cochlear implantation, while ovarian insufficiency usually causes infertility and requires hormone replacement; neurological subtypes carry a more guarded outlook.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing isolated hearing loss in a female without evaluating ovarian function","Missing the syndrome in affected males who lack a gonadal phenotype","Overlooking the neurological subtype and its long-term implications","Not arranging early endocrine care to protect bone and address fertility"],"management":["Supportive multisystem care, as no disease-specific drug therapy exists","Hearing rehabilitation with hearing aids and cochlear implantation for progressive sensorineural hearing loss","Oestrogen and progesterone hormone replacement for ovarian insufficiency to induce or maintain puberty and protect bone health","Fertility counselling with assisted reproduction or oocyte donation as appropriate","Neurological management and physiotherapy for the ataxia and peripheral neuropathy of the neurological subtypes","Genetic counselling and family screening for the autosomal recessive genes such as CLPP, HARS2, LARS2 and TWNK","Coordinate audiology, reproductive endocrinology, clinical genetics and neurology input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology","GeneReviews: Perrault Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"persistent_depressive_disorder":{"name":"Persistent depressive disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dysthymia","dysthymic disorder","chronic depression","PDD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The defining feature is chronically depressed mood present more days than not for at least two years in adults, or one year in children and adolescents, with fewer symptom-free intervals","Associated symptoms may include poor appetite or overeating, sleep disturbance, low energy, low self-esteem, poor concentration, and feelings of hopelessness","Because symptoms are long-standing, patients often regard the low mood as part of their personality and may not recognise it as a treatable illness","A major depressive episode can be superimposed on the chronic baseline, a pattern often described as double depression, which increases severity and risk","Suicide risk is present and should be assessed actively despite the seemingly milder day-to-day picture","Management combines evidence-based psychotherapy with antidepressant pharmacotherapy, and chronic forms may need longer treatment to achieve and maintain response","Screen for a history of hypomania or mania and for medical contributors such as hypothyroidism before attributing chronic low mood solely to a primary mood disorder","Functional impairment accumulates over years, so early recognition and sustained treatment can substantially improve quality of life"],"pathophysiology":"Persistent depressive disorder shares much of the neurobiology of major depression, with contributions from genetic vulnerability, monoamine dysregulation, and altered stress-axis and neuroplastic signalling, but is characterised by chronicity. Early adversity, ongoing psychosocial stressors, temperament, and reinforced negative cognitive styles are thought to sustain the persistent low-grade mood disturbance over years.","additionalDifferentials":["Major depressive disorder, episodic type","Bipolar disorder, particularly bipolar II, in a depressive phase","Cyclothymic disorder","Hypothyroidism and other chronic medical illness","Substance-induced or medication-induced mood disorder","Adjustment disorder with prolonged stressor","Personality traits or disorder contributing to chronic dysphoria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured suicide risk assessment","Standardised depression rating scales to track chronic severity","Thyroid function testing","Full blood count and metabolic panel to exclude medical contributors","Screening for hypomanic or manic episodes","Substance use screening"],"redFlags":["Active suicidal ideation with plan, intent, or access to means","Emergence of a superimposed major depressive episode with worsening severity","Emerging manic or hypomanic symptoms after starting an antidepressant","Profound self-neglect or loss of function"],"prognosis":"Persistent depressive disorder is by definition chronic and can be more treatment-resistant than episodic depression, with high rates of relapse and comorbidity. Prognosis improves with sustained combined treatment and psychosocial support, but is worse with early onset, superimposed major depression, and untreated comorbid conditions.","pitfalls":["Assuming chronic low mood is fixed personality rather than a treatable disorder","Underestimating suicide risk because symptoms appear mild day to day","Missing a superimposed major depressive episode (double depression)","Failing to screen for bipolarity before antidepressant treatment","Ending treatment too early given the chronic, relapsing nature of the condition"],"management":["Antidepressant pharmacotherapy, typically an SSRI or SNRI, continued for an adequate trial","Evidence-based psychotherapy, including cognitive behavioural therapy and the cognitive behavioural analysis system of psychotherapy developed for chronic depression","Combined pharmacotherapy and psychotherapy is preferred, with augmentation strategies for partial response","Active and repeated suicide risk assessment with safety planning despite the seemingly milder daily picture","Screen for bipolarity before and during antidepressant treatment and identify and treat comorbid substance misuse","Prolonged maintenance treatment to prevent relapse, tracking severity with standardised rating scales","Exclude medical contributors such as thyroid dysfunction and address ongoing psychosocial stressors","Escalate treatment-resistant disease to specialist psychiatry, considering further augmentation or electroconvulsive therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"persistent_fetal_vasculature":{"name":"Persistent fetal vasculature (PHPV)","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous","PHPV","PFV","persistent foetal vasculature"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski / Taylor & Hoyt Paediatric Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from failure of the embryonic hyaloid vascular system and primary vitreous to regress, usually unilateral and in an otherwise healthy full-term infant","Typically presents with leukocoria, microphthalmia and, in the anterior form, a retrolental fibrovascular membrane with elongated ciliary processes drawn centrally","Anterior disease can produce a shallow anterior chamber, cataract and secondary angle-closure glaucoma; posterior disease features a vitreous stalk and retinal folds or dysplasia","A persistent Bergmeister papilla or Mittendorf dot may be seen as mild residua of the hyaloid system","The critical differential is retinoblastoma, and any child with leukocoria requires urgent specialist assessment","Ultrasound and MRI help distinguish the fibrovascular stalk and absence of calcification from a calcified retinoblastoma","Early vitreoretinal surgery may preserve the eye and, in selected anterior cases, some vision, but the visual prognosis is generally guarded"],"pathophysiology":"During normal development the hyaloid artery and primary vitreous nourish the developing lens and regress before birth. In persistent fetal vasculature this regression fails, leaving hyperplastic fibrovascular tissue in the vitreous cavity and behind the lens. The retained tissue exerts traction, causing microphthalmia, a retrolental membrane with dragged ciliary processes, cataract and, posteriorly, a vitreous stalk with retinal folds or dysplasia. Contraction and neovascular activity can precipitate cataract, shallowing of the anterior chamber, secondary glaucoma and vitreous haemorrhage. Most cases are sporadic and unilateral, though bilateral disease raises suspicion of a systemic or genetic disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Retinoblastoma","Congenital cataract","Coats disease","Retinopathy of prematurity","Familial exudative vitreoretinopathy","Toxocara or other infective endophthalmitis","Norrie disease (in bilateral cases)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["B-scan ocular ultrasound to define the fibrovascular stalk and detect calcification","MRI of the orbits and brain when retinoblastoma cannot be excluded clinically","Examination under anaesthesia with fundus assessment of both eyes","Intraocular pressure measurement and biometry for microphthalmia","Wide-field retinal imaging where feasible","Consideration of genetic evaluation in bilateral or syndromic presentations"],"redFlags":["Leukocoria that could represent retinoblastoma requiring urgent exclusion","Bilateral disease suggesting Norrie disease or a systemic disorder","Secondary angle-closure glaucoma threatening a painful blind eye","Rapid progression of cataract or vitreous haemorrhage","Signs of raised intraocular pressure or buphthalmos"],"prognosis":"Visual outcome is usually poor, particularly in posterior or combined disease, though early surgery for selected anterior cases can salvage the eye and occasionally yield useful vision; the priority is often preserving a comfortable, cosmetically acceptable globe.","pitfalls":["Attributing leukocoria to PHPV without excluding retinoblastoma","Failing to examine the fellow eye and consider a bilateral systemic cause","Missing evolving secondary glaucoma in a microphthalmic eye","Delaying referral and losing the window for sight-preserving surgery"],"management":["Urgently exclude retinoblastoma before any intervention in a child with leukocoria","Early lensectomy, vitrectomy and excision of the retrolental fibrovascular membrane for selected, predominantly anterior disease","Aggressive amblyopia therapy with optical correction and occlusion of the fellow eye, which is central to any visual gain","Detect and control secondary glaucoma medically or surgically with intraocular pressure monitoring","Aim to preserve a comfortable, cosmetically acceptable globe when useful vision is not achievable","Genetic and systemic evaluation in bilateral or syndromic presentations","Long-term paediatric ophthalmology follow-up through a specialist team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Taylor & Hoyt's Paediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"persistent_mullerian_duct_syndrome":{"name":"Persistent mullerian duct syndrome (AMH/AMHR2)","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMDS","persistent Mullerian duct syndrome","hernia uteri inguinalis","anti-mullerian hormone deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Endocrine Society clinical guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs in otherwise normally virilised 46,XY males who retain a uterus and fallopian tubes because the anti-mullerian hormone pathway has failed","External genitalia are typically male, since testosterone production and androgen action are intact","Often discovered incidentally at surgery for cryptorchidism or inguinal hernia, where mullerian structures are found in the hernia sac (hernia uteri inguinalis)","Caused by loss-of-function variants in the AMH gene (hormone deficiency) or in its receptor AMHR2 (hormone resistance), inherited in an autosomal recessive manner","Bilateral cryptorchidism and transverse testicular ectopia are common associations","Fertility is frequently impaired owing to undescended testes and disrupted duct anatomy","Undescended testes carry a raised risk of germ cell malignancy, and persistent mullerian remnants require careful surgical judgement to preserve the vas deferens and testicular blood supply"],"pathophysiology":"During normal male development the fetal Sertoli cells secrete anti-mullerian hormone, which acts through the AMHR2 receptor to trigger regression of the mullerian ducts. In persistent mullerian duct syndrome either the hormone is absent (AMH mutations) or its receptor is non-functional (AMHR2 mutations), so the mullerian ducts fail to regress and a uterus and fallopian tubes persist. Because Leydig cell testosterone secretion and androgen signalling are unaffected, wolffian derivatives and the external genitalia develop normally as male. The retained mullerian structures and the frequently maldescended testes distort normal anatomy and impair fertility.","additionalDifferentials":["Bilateral cryptorchidism without mullerian remnants","Mixed gonadal dysgenesis","Partial gonadal dysgenesis","Ovotesticular disorder of sex development","Testicular regression (vanishing testis) syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype confirming 46,XY","Serum anti-mullerian hormone (low or undetectable in AMH deficiency, normal or high in receptor resistance)","Molecular testing of the AMH and AMHR2 genes","Pelvic and inguinal ultrasound or MRI to identify uterus, tubes and testicular position","Intraoperative identification and, where needed, histology of mullerian remnants","Assessment of testicular function and fertility potential"],"redFlags":["Long-standing intra-abdominal or undescended testes with malignancy risk","An enlarging pelvic or inguinal mass","Surgical injury to the vas deferens or testicular vessels when excising mullerian remnants","Signs of malignant transformation within retained mullerian tissue"],"prognosis":"With orchidopexy and judicious management of mullerian remnants the outlook is generally good, though fertility is often reduced. The principal concerns are testicular malignancy in maldescended gonads and inadvertent damage to reproductive ducts during surgery.","pitfalls":["Attempting complete excision of mullerian remnants and injuring the vas deferens or testicular vessels","Failing to recognise mullerian structures found unexpectedly at hernia or orchidopexy surgery","Overlooking the malignancy risk of retained intra-abdominal testes","Confusing the condition with a disorder of androgen action given the normal male phenotype"],"management":["Orchidopexy to bring the maldescended testes into the scrotum and improve accessibility for cancer surveillance","Careful surgical handling of mullerian remnants, preserving the vasa deferentia and testicular blood supply","Long-term surveillance of the testes for malignancy given the history of maldescent","Assess and counsel on fertility, which is frequently impaired","Maintain the male sex of rearing, as androgen production and action are intact","Genetic counselling and family screening for the autosomal recessive AMH or AMHR2 defect","Coordinate paediatric surgery or urology, endocrinology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society clinical practice guidance on disorders of sex development"],"crossLinks":[]},"persistent_postural_perceptual_dizziness":{"name":"persistent postural perceptual dizziness","system":"nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPPD","chronic subjective dizziness","phobic postural vertigo"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bárány Society consensus document on persistent postural-perceptual dizziness (Committee for the Classification of Vestibular Disorders)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["PPPD is among the commonest causes of chronic dizziness in working-age adults and is a functional disorder — neither a structural vestibular lesion nor a primary psychiatric illness.","The defining symptom is persistent non-spinning dizziness, unsteadiness, or a rocking/swaying sensation present on most days for three months or longer, often worsening as the day progresses.","Three core provocateurs characterise it: upright posture, active or passive self-motion, and exposure to moving or complex visual environments such as supermarket aisles, crowds, or scrolling screens.","It is usually precipitated by an acute event that disturbs balance — vestibular neuritis, benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, vestibular migraine, a panic attack, or another medical illness — after which symptoms persist despite recovery of the trigger.","The mechanism is a maladaptive shift to visual and somatosensory dependence together with a stiff, high-risk postural control strategy that fails to relax once the initial threat has passed.","Anxiety and neurotic traits are common comorbidities and predisposing factors but are not required for the diagnosis, which is positive and criteria-based rather than one of exclusion.","Examination and vestibular tests are often normal or reflect only the resolved precipitant; repeated negative investigation tends to reinforce symptoms.","Effective management combines patient education, vestibular rehabilitation with habituation and gaze stabilisation, serotonergic antidepressants (SSRIs or SNRIs), and cognitive behavioural therapy."],"pathophysiology":"PPPD arises from a failure to re-establish normal postural control and spatial orientation after an acute vestibular, medical, or psychological insult. Rather than returning to the automatic, efficient balance strategy used before the event, the patient persists with a heightened, threat-adapted mode: a stiffened posture, conscious control of gait, and an over-reliance on visual and somatosensory cues (visual dependence). Functional neuroimaging suggests reduced activity and connectivity within central vestibular cortical networks alongside increased influence of visual and anxiety-related limbic circuits, so that ordinary motion and visually busy environments are processed as destabilising. Predisposing anxious and introspective personality traits amplify this reweighting. Importantly, the disorder is not explained by ongoing structural damage to the peripheral or central vestibular apparatus.","additionalDifferentials":["uncompensated peripheral vestibular hypofunction","vestibular migraine","recurrent benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","bilateral vestibulopathy","orthostatic hypotension or postural tachycardia syndrome","cerebellar or other central balance disorders","generalised anxiety or panic disorder and medication side effects"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["pure-tone audiometry to detect a Menière's or retrocochlear contribution","video head impulse testing, caloric testing, and vestibular evoked myogenic potentials to characterise any precipitating vestibular deficit","MRI of the brain when central or progressive features are present","orthostatic blood pressure and, where indicated, tilt-table testing","posturography, which may demonstrate visual dependence and an abnormal reliance on surface cues","validated anxiety and depression screening questionnaires and a medication review"],"redFlags":["progressive focal neurological signs or new spontaneous vertigo with nystagmus","fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss suggesting Menière's disease","gait ataxia, dysarthria, or limb incoordination suggesting cerebellar disease","syncope or pre-syncope pointing to a cardiovascular cause","acute severe headache or other features suggesting an evolving central lesion"],"prognosis":"Untreated PPPD tends to run a chronic, disabling course, but it responds well to a combined programme of vestibular rehabilitation, serotonergic medication, and cognitive behavioural therapy; early positive diagnosis and avoidance of repeated investigation improve outcome.","pitfalls":["ordering repeated negative investigations, which reinforces illness behaviour and delays effective treatment","dismissing the condition as purely psychological rather than explaining the functional balance model","overlooking a treatable precipitant such as recurrent benign paroxysmal positional vertigo or vestibular migraine","prescribing long-term vestibular suppressants, which impede compensation and often worsen chronic symptoms","failing to give the patient a clear, positive explanation of the diagnosis, which undermines rehabilitation"],"management":["Make a positive, criteria-based diagnosis, explain the mechanism and avoid repeated unnecessary vestibular investigation","Vestibular rehabilitation with graded habituation to self-motion and visually complex environments","Serotonergic medication such as an SSRI or SNRI as first-line pharmacotherapy","Cognitive behavioural therapy targeting the threat-driven, stiff postural strategy and associated anxiety","Identify and treat any precipitating or coexisting vestibular disorder such as benign paroxysmal positional vertigo or vestibular migraine","Manage comorbid anxiety and depression","Coordinate care through a neuro-otology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bárány Society: consensus diagnostic criteria for persistent postural-perceptual dizziness (Staab et al)","Oxford Textbook of Medicine","Brandt and Dieterich, Vertigo and Dizziness: Common Complaints"],"crossLinks":[]},"persistent_pulmonary_hypertension":{"name":"persistent pulmonary hypertension","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPHN","persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","persistent foetal circulation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Avery's Diseases of the Newborn","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from failure of the normal fall in pulmonary vascular resistance at birth, sustaining foetal-pattern right-to-left shunting across the patent foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus.","Occurs mainly in term and near-term neonates and presents with cyanosis and hypoxaemia that is disproportionate to any parenchymal lung disease and often strikingly labile.","A pre- to post-ductal oxygen saturation gradient (right hand higher than lower limb, typically by more than 5 to 10 percent) suggests right-to-left ductal shunting.","Common precipitants include meconium aspiration syndrome, sepsis or pneumonia, congenital diaphragmatic hernia, respiratory distress syndrome, perinatal asphyxia, and idiopathic (maldeveloped vasculature) disease.","Echocardiography is essential to confirm raised pulmonary pressures, define shunt direction and, critically, exclude structural congenital heart disease.","Supportive care is foundational: optimise lung recruitment and oxygenation, correct acidosis, avoid hypothermia and agitation, and maintain systemic blood pressure to limit right-to-left shunting.","Inhaled nitric oxide is the first-line selective pulmonary vasodilator, with agents such as sildenafil used as adjuncts.","Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation is the rescue therapy when maximal medical management fails, with the oxygenation index guiding escalation."],"pathophysiology":"At birth, lung aeration and rising oxygen tension normally trigger pulmonary vasodilatation and a rapid fall in pulmonary vascular resistance. In PPHN this transition fails or reverses through one of three mechanisms: maladaptation (structurally normal vasculature with abnormal vasoconstriction driven by asphyxia, sepsis or acidosis), maldevelopment (remodelled, abnormally muscularised arteries following chronic intrauterine stress), or hypoplasia (a reduced cross-sectional vascular bed, as in congenital diaphragmatic hernia). Persistently high pulmonary vascular resistance maintains right-to-left shunting across the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus, bypassing the lungs and causing severe hypoxaemia. Hypoxaemia and acidosis further raise pulmonary vascular resistance, creating a self-perpetuating cycle.","additionalDifferentials":["Cyanotic congenital heart disease (transposition of the great arteries, total anomalous pulmonary venous return, hypoplastic left heart)","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Neonatal sepsis or pneumonia (group B streptococcus)","Respiratory distress syndrome from surfactant deficiency","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia","Pulmonary hypoplasia","Polycythaemia and hyperviscosity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pre- and post-ductal pulse oximetry to detect differential saturations","Echocardiography with Doppler (shunt direction, tricuspid regurgitation jet, exclusion of structural disease)","Arterial blood gas with calculation of the oxygenation index","Chest radiograph","Sepsis screen including blood cultures, full blood count and inflammatory markers","Hyperoxia test to help distinguish from cyanotic congenital heart disease","Blood glucose and calcium"],"redFlags":["Severe labile hypoxaemia refractory to high inspired oxygen","Rising oxygenation index despite maximal support, prompting ECMO consideration","Systemic hypotension with poor perfusion","Suspicion of duct-dependent congenital heart disease"],"prognosis":"With inhaled nitric oxide and ECMO the majority of infants survive, but PPHN still carries appreciable mortality and a risk of long-term neurodevelopmental and sensorineural hearing impairment, so structured follow-up is warranted.","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude cyanotic congenital heart disease before attributing hypoxaemia to PPHN.","Giving inhaled nitric oxide in duct-dependent lesions or before echocardiography.","Excessive handling, agitation and hypothermia, all of which worsen pulmonary vasoconstriction.","Over-aggressive hyperventilation and induced alkalosis, which are associated with adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes.","Overlooking the pre- to post-ductal saturation gradient."],"management":["Optimise lung recruitment and oxygenation with gentle mechanical ventilation and surfactant where indicated, avoiding both hypoxia and hyperoxia","Inhaled nitric oxide as a selective pulmonary vasodilator to reduce right-to-left shunting","Support systemic blood pressure with volume and inotropes or vasopressors, and correct acidosis, to limit shunting","Minimal handling with sedation and analgesia to prevent agitation-provoked hypoxaemic crises, and avoid hypothermia","Treat the underlying precipitant, including antibiotics for sepsis or pneumonia and management of meconium aspiration or diaphragmatic hernia","Escalate to extracorporeal membrane oxygenation when the oxygenation index rises despite maximal therapy","Confirm the diagnosis and exclude structural congenital heart disease with echocardiography","Arrange neurodevelopmental and hearing follow-up after recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Avery's Diseases of the Newborn","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","Fanaroff and Martin's Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"persistent_pulmonary_hypertension_newborn":{"name":"Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","system":"Neonatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPHN","persistent foetal circulation","persistent pulmonary hypertension of the neonate"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cloherty and Stark Manual of Neonatal Care / Avery's Diseases of the Newborn","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Suspect PPHN in a term or near-term infant with marked hypoxaemia and cyanosis that is out of proportion to the degree of parenchymal lung disease seen on chest imaging","The hallmark is a right-to-left shunt across the patent ductus arteriosus and foramen ovale because pulmonary vascular resistance stays elevated after birth","A pre-ductal to post-ductal oxygen saturation gradient (right hand higher than a lower limb) suggests ductal-level right-to-left shunting","Echocardiography is essential to estimate pulmonary artery pressure, assess shunt direction, evaluate ventricular function, and exclude structural congenital heart disease","Common associations include meconium aspiration, sepsis or pneumonia, congenital diaphragmatic hernia, respiratory distress syndrome, and pulmonary hypoplasia","Management principles centre on optimising oxygenation and lung recruitment, avoiding acidosis and stress, and using inhaled nitric oxide as a selective pulmonary vasodilator","The oxygenation index is used to gauge severity and to guide escalation, with extracorporeal membrane oxygenation reserved for refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn arises from a failure of the normal postnatal fall in pulmonary vascular resistance. In foetal life high pulmonary resistance directs blood away from the lungs through the ductus arteriosus and foramen ovale; at birth lung expansion and rising oxygen normally cause pulmonary vessels to dilate. In PPHN this transition fails because of maladaptation (reactive vasoconstriction from hypoxia, acidosis, or parenchymal disease), maldevelopment (abnormally muscularised pulmonary arteries), or underdevelopment (pulmonary hypoplasia). Sustained high pulmonary resistance maintains right-to-left shunting across the ductus and foramen ovale, producing profound hypoxaemia, and hypoxia and acidosis further raise pulmonary pressure in a self-perpetuating cycle.","additionalDifferentials":["Cyanotic congenital heart disease such as transposition of the great arteries or obstructed total anomalous pulmonary venous return","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Neonatal sepsis or pneumonia","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia with pulmonary hypoplasia","Respiratory distress syndrome or surfactant deficiency","Air leak such as pneumothorax causing acute deterioration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pre-ductal and post-ductal oxygen saturation and blood gas comparison to demonstrate shunting","Echocardiography to estimate pulmonary pressures, define shunt direction, and exclude structural heart disease","Chest radiograph to characterise parenchymal disease and exclude air leak or diaphragmatic hernia","Serial oxygenation index calculation to grade severity and guide escalation","Sepsis screen including blood culture and inflammatory markers","Continuous monitoring of blood pressure, perfusion, and arterial gases"],"redFlags":["Severe refractory hypoxaemia with a rising oxygenation index despite maximal support","Systemic hypotension and poor perfusion indicating cardiovascular decompensation","Suspected duct-dependent congenital heart disease requiring urgent cardiology input","Deterioration suggesting pneumothorax or other air leak"],"prognosis":"With modern management including inhaled nitric oxide and extracorporeal support most infants survive, but outcome depends heavily on the underlying cause and severity. Prolonged severe hypoxaemia carries a risk of neurodevelopmental impairment and sensorineural hearing loss, so structured follow-up is recommended.","pitfalls":["Mistaking PPHN for cyanotic congenital heart disease, or vice versa, without echocardiography","Provoking pulmonary vasoconstriction through excessive handling, pain, hypothermia, or acidosis","Aggressive hyperventilation and over-ventilation causing lung injury without sustained benefit","Failing to recognise and treat coexisting sepsis or an air leak","Delaying referral for extracorporeal membrane oxygenation in deteriorating refractory disease"],"management":["Optimise oxygenation and lung recruitment with gentle ventilation and surfactant for parenchymal disease, avoiding hyperoxia and lung overdistension","Inhaled nitric oxide as the selective pulmonary vasodilator of choice","Maintain adequate systemic blood pressure with fluids and inotropic or vasopressor support and correct metabolic acidosis to reduce right-to-left shunting","Provide sedation, analgesia and minimal handling to avoid agitation-triggered deterioration, and prevent hypothermia","Treat the underlying cause such as sepsis, pneumonia, meconium aspiration, respiratory distress syndrome or pulmonary hypoplasia","Escalate to extracorporeal membrane oxygenation for refractory hypoxaemia with a persistently rising oxygenation index","Use echocardiography to guide vasodilator and haemodynamic therapy and to exclude structural congenital heart disease","Institute structured neurodevelopmental and audiological follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cloherty and Stark Manual of Neonatal Care","Avery's Diseases of the Newborn"],"crossLinks":[]},"perthes_disease":{"name":"Perthes' disease","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Legg-Calve-Perthes disease","idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children","coxa plana"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children, typically presenting between about 4 and 8 years and more common in boys","Presents with a limp and hip, groin, or referred knee pain, often intermittent, with restricted abduction and internal rotation of the hip","Knee pain in a child can be referred from the hip, so the hip must always be examined in a limping child","Early radiographs may be normal; later changes include increased femoral head density, fragmentation, flattening, and eventual remodelling","The key aims are to relieve symptoms, maintain hip movement, and keep the femoral head 'contained' within the acetabulum so it remodels into a congruent, spherical shape","Younger children with more remaining growth and greater remodelling potential generally have a better prognosis than older children","Treatment ranges from observation, activity restriction, and physiotherapy to containment by bracing or surgery (femoral or pelvic osteotomy) in more severe or older cases","Bilateral or atypical presentations should prompt consideration of alternative diagnoses such as skeletal dysplasia or hypothyroidism"],"pathophysiology":"Perthes' disease results from a temporary interruption of the blood supply to the immature femoral head epiphysis, causing avascular necrosis. The precise trigger is unknown but the epiphysis undergoes a sequence of ischaemic necrosis, followed by fragmentation and resorption of dead bone, then reossification and remodelling as revascularisation occurs. During the biologically 'plastic' necrotic and fragmentation phases the softened femoral head is vulnerable to deformation under load, and can flatten and enlarge (coxa magna) or become incongruent if not well contained within the acetabulum. The ultimate shape of the healed head, and hence long-term function, depends on how well sphericity and containment are preserved through this self-limiting but prolonged process.","additionalDifferentials":["Transient synovitis (irritable hip)","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis of the hip","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (in older children)","Developmental dysplasia of the hip","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Skeletal dysplasia or epiphyseal dysplasia (especially if bilateral)","Hypothyroidism and avascular necrosis from other causes (e.g. sickle cell disease, steroids)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anteroposterior and frog-lateral radiographs of both hips to assess and stage the changes","MRI for early detection before radiographic changes appear and to assess the extent of necrosis","Clinical assessment of hip range of movement, particularly abduction and internal rotation","Investigations to exclude infection or inflammatory arthritis when the presentation is acute","Consideration of bilateral or systemic workup (e.g. thyroid function, skeletal survey) if presentation is atypical or bilateral"],"redFlags":["Fever, systemic upset, and an acutely irritable hip suggesting septic arthritis requiring urgent exclusion","Bilateral or atypical disease suggesting skeletal dysplasia or an underlying systemic cause","Marked loss of hip movement and femoral head extrusion indicating a head at risk of permanent deformity","An older child at presentation, associated with a poorer remodelling capacity"],"prognosis":"The disease is self-limiting and many younger children with limited head involvement remodel to a near-normal, congruent hip; however, older children, extensive necrosis, and a poorly contained or deformed femoral head are associated with residual incongruity and an increased risk of premature hip osteoarthritis in adulthood.","pitfalls":["Attributing a child's knee pain to the knee and failing to examine the hip","Assuming a normal early radiograph excludes the disease and missing early presentation on MRI","Not distinguishing Perthes' disease from septic arthritis in the acutely painful hip","Overlooking bilateral involvement that should prompt evaluation for an underlying systemic disorder"],"management":["Relieve symptoms with simple analgesia, activity modification and physiotherapy to preserve hip range of movement","Maintain femoral head containment within the acetabulum by preserving abduction and internal rotation, using physiotherapy and, in selected cases, abduction bracing","Protected weight-bearing and traction during painful active phases to reduce load on the softened epiphysis","Surgical containment with femoral varus or pelvic acetabular osteotomy for older children or those with extensive involvement or loss of containment","Serial radiographic monitoring through the fragmentation and reossification phases to track sphericity and containment","Coordinate orthopaedic and physiotherapy care, tailoring intensity to age and extent of head involvement","Long-term surveillance for residual deformity and premature hip osteoarthritis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"pertussis":{"name":"Pertussis (Whooping Cough)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["whooping cough","Bordetella pertussis"],"chapter":165,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1276-1280","clinicalPearls":["Acute respiratory tract infection caused by Bordetella pertussis; 'pertussis' means 'violent cough' and the inspiratory whoop at the end of a paroxysm gives the disease its common name (p.1276).","Classic course runs through three stages: catarrhal phase (1-2 weeks of coryza, lacrimation, mild cough, low-grade fever, malaise, indistinguishable from a common cold), then paroxysmal phase (frequent spasmodic bursts of 5-10 coughs within a single expiration), then convalescent phase (p.1278-1279).","Paroxysms may end in an audible whoop produced by rapid inspiration against a closed glottis; post-tussive vomiting is frequent and a mucous plug may be expelled (p.1279).","During a coughing spasm there may be neck-vein distension, bulging eyes, tongue protrusion, and cyanosis; paroxysms can be precipitated by noise, eating, or physical contact and are often worse at night (p.1279).","Between attacks the patient looks normal but shows increasing fatigue; fever is uncommon and, when present, suggests bacterial superinfection (p.1279).","The convalescent phase lasts 1-3 months with gradual resolution; for 6-12 months afterward, intercurrent viral infections may trigger a recrudescence of paroxysmal cough (p.1279).","Incubation period averages 7-10 days (p.1279).","The whoop is variable: uncommon in infants 6 months of age or younger and frequently absent in older children and adults (p.1276).","Lymphocytosis (absolute lymphocyte count >108-109/L) is common in young children and unusual with other infections, but is not a feature in adolescents and adults (p.1279).","Highly communicable: attack rates of 80-100% among unimmunized household contacts; cyclical outbreaks occur every 3-5 years despite widespread immunization (p.1277).","Severe morbidity and high mortality are restricted almost entirely to infants; in the US (2000-2017) infants under 2 months accounted for 84% of pertussis deaths (p.1278)."],"pathophysiology":"Infection begins with attachment of B. pertussis to ciliated epithelial cells of the nasopharynx, mediated by surface adhesins (pertactin, filamentous hemagglutinin) binding integrin-family proteins, probably with pertussis toxin. The organism multiplies locally and produces toxins causing mucosal damage (tracheal cytotoxin, dermonecrotic toxin); host defense is impaired by pertussis toxin and adenylate cyclase-hemolysin toxin. There is local cellular invasion with intracellular persistence, but systemic dissemination does NOT occur; systemic manifestations such as lymphocytosis result from the toxins. The cause of the hallmark paroxysmal cough is not known; a pivotal role for pertussis toxin has been proposed but not confirmed. Neurologic events (seizures, encephalopathy) are thought to result from hypoxia due to coughing paroxysms or apnea rather than from specific bacterial products. Deaths in young infants are frequently associated with very high leukocytosis and pulmonary hypertension (p.1278).","additionalDifferentials":["Bordetella parapertussis (causes a similar but typically milder illness; does not express pertussis toxin gene) (p.1276).","Bordetella holmesii (found in up to 20% of patients with a pertussis-like syndrome using improved PCR) (p.1276).","Mycoplasma pneumoniae (p.1280).","Chlamydia pneumoniae (p.1280).","Adenovirus (p.1280).","Influenza virus and other respiratory viruses (p.1280).","Respiratory syncytial virus and rhinovirus -- isolated from patients with clinical pertussis but probably represent co-infection, particularly in children under 1 year (p.1280)."],"infectionMimics":["Bordetella parapertussis infection (p.1280)","Bordetella holmesii infection (p.1276)","Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection (p.1280)","Chlamydia pneumoniae infection (p.1280)","Adenovirus, influenza, and other respiratory viral infections (p.1280)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["ACE inhibitor-induced cough (p.1280)","Reactive airway disease (p.1280)","Gastroesophageal reflux disease (p.1280)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasopharyngeal culture (Bordet-Gengou or Regan-Lowe medium) -- gold standard owing to 100% specificity; cultures become positive by day 5 of incubation (p.1279).","PCR / multitarget real-time PCR -- has replaced culture in most laboratories due to greater sensitivity and faster (hours) results; differentiates B. pertussis, B. parapertussis, and B. holmesii (p.1279).","Nasopharyngeal aspiration is the best specimen; B. pertussis is highly sensitive to drying so specimens must be inoculated without delay or placed in transport medium (p.1279).","Complete blood count for lymphocytosis (informative in young children, not in adolescents/adults) (p.1279).","Serology -- enzyme immunoassays for IgA/IgG to pertussis toxin, filamentous hemagglutinin, pertactin, and fimbriae; increasingly used in adolescents, adults, and those symptomatic >4 weeks; single-specimen anti-pertussis-toxin serology is becoming standardized, especially for outbreaks and surveillance (p.1279)."],"redFlags":["Apnea in infants (occurred in 27.1% of children under 2 years hospitalized with pertussis) (p.1279).","Cyanosis during coughing spasms (p.1279).","Seizures and encephalopathy, thought to result from hypoxia of paroxysms/apnea (p.1278-1279).","Very high leukocytosis and pulmonary hypertension in young infants -- frequently associated with death (p.1278).","Pneumonia (occurs in up to 22% of infants with pertussis) (p.1278-1279).","Infants, especially those under 2-3 months, carry the highest risk of complications and death and should generally be hospitalized (p.1278, 1279)."],"prognosis":"With universal childhood immunization, reported cases fell by >90% and mortality declined even more dramatically. Severe morbidity and high mortality remain almost entirely restricted to infants; WHO estimates ~40 million cases and ~400,000 deaths globally each year, mostly among infants under 3 months. Immunity after natural infection is NOT lifelong; immunity after whole-cell vaccination is short-lived (little protection after 10-12 years) and wanes even faster (within 2-4 years of the fifth or sixth dose) in those immunized with acellular vaccines (p.1277-1278).","pitfalls":["In adolescents and adults the presentation is often atypical -- frequently no paroxysmal cough or whoop -- so pertussis is a commonly missed cause of prolonged cough; B. pertussis may be the agent in 12-30% of adults whose cough does not improve within 2 weeks (p.1278).","Antibiotic therapy aims to eradicate nasopharyngeal carriage; it does NOT substantially alter the clinical course unless given early in the catarrhal phase (p.1279).","Cough suppressants are NOT effective and have no role in management; beta-adrenergic agonists and glucocorticoids have been advocated but are NOT proven effective (p.1280).","Lymphocytosis is helpful in young children but is NOT a reliable feature in adolescents and adults (p.1279).","There is only a small window for culture/PCR diagnosis because much of the period of organism recovery is during the catarrhal phase when pertussis is not yet suspected (p.1279).","Cross-reactivity of antigens such as filamentous hemagglutinin and pertactin among Bordetella species makes diagnosis based on seroconversion of a single antibody type unreliable (p.1279).","Suspect pertussis in any patient with a cough not improving within 14 days, a paroxysmal cough of any duration, cough followed by vomiting (adolescents/adults), or respiratory symptoms after contact with a laboratory-confirmed case (p.1280).","Waning vaccine-derived immunity (faster with acellular vaccines) underlies resurgent outbreaks even in fully immunized older children, adolescents, and adults (p.1278)."],"management":["Macrolide antibiotics such as azithromycin, clarithromycin or erythromycin started early to reduce infectivity and, in the catarrhal phase, modify the illness","Co-trimoxazole as the alternative agent where macrolides are contraindicated or not tolerated","Supportive care with oxygen, careful hydration and small frequent feeds, monitoring young infants for apnoea and cyanosis","Admit young infants and manage severe cases in intensive care, considering exchange transfusion or leucodepletion for marked hyperleucocytosis with pulmonary hypertension","Give macrolide post-exposure prophylaxis to close contacts and apply droplet precautions and isolation","Notify public health authorities, as pertussis is a reportable disease","Prevent through acellular pertussis vaccination, including maternal antenatal immunisation and childhood boosters"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd ed., Chapter 165: Pertussis and Other Bordetella Infections (ElSherif & Halperin), pp.1276-1280.","Kilgore PE et al. Pertussis: Microbiology, disease, treatment, and prevention. Clin Microbiol Rev 29:449, 2016 (Table 165-1 source, p.1278).","Craig R et al. Asymptomatic infection and transmission of pertussis in households: A systematic review. Clin Infect Dis 70:152, 2020.","Havers FP et al. Use of Tdap vaccines: Updated ACIP recommendations, United States 2019. MMWR 69:77, 2020."],"crossLinks":[]},"pes_anserine_bursitis":{"name":"Pes anserine bursitis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pes anserinus bursitis","anserine bursopathy","medial knee bursitis","pes anserine pain syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Medial knee pain and tenderness below the joint line at the insertion of the pes anserinus (sartorius, gracilis and semitendinosus)","Common in middle-aged and older people, those with knee osteoarthritis or obesity, and in some athletes, especially with valgus loading","Pain is typically inferomedial to the joint line, helping distinguish it from medial joint-line pathology","Frequently overlaps with or is secondary to medial compartment osteoarthritis","Ultrasound may show bursal fluid, though the diagnosis is largely clinical and imaging often serves to exclude other causes","Management is conservative with load and activity modification and addressing biomechanics and the hamstrings and adductors with physiotherapy; corticosteroid injection may help resistant cases","Consider referred pain and intra-articular pathology when the picture is atypical"],"pathophysiology":"The pes anserine bursa lies deep to the conjoined insertion of sartorius, gracilis and semitendinosus on the anteromedial proximal tibia. Friction and overload from repetitive knee flexion and extension, valgus stress, tight hamstrings and altered biomechanics irritate the bursa. It is commonly associated with medial compartment osteoarthritis and with obesity and can be difficult to separate from these, forming part of a medial knee overload picture rather than always an isolated inflammatory bursitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Medial compartment knee osteoarthritis","Medial meniscal pathology","Medial collateral ligament injury","Medial tibial stress fracture","Saphenous nerve (infrapatellar branch) irritation","Semimembranosus tendinopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical localisation of tenderness inferomedial to the joint line","Ultrasound to assess for bursal fluid and adjacent structures","MRI where meniscal, ligamentous or bony pathology is suspected","Weight-bearing radiographs to assess medial compartment osteoarthritis","Assessment of lower-limb biomechanics and alignment"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting a fracture such as focal bone tenderness in an at-risk athlete","Locking or true mechanical symptoms suggesting meniscal pathology","Systemic features suggesting infection or inflammatory arthritis","A marked joint effusion suggesting intra-articular pathology"],"prognosis":"Generally responds to conservative management and correction of contributing factors, although it may persist or recur when associated osteoarthritis or biomechanical factors remain.","pitfalls":["Attributing all medial knee pain to the bursa while missing osteoarthritis or meniscal disease","Over-reliance on imaging when the diagnosis is clinical","Neglecting biomechanical contributors and weight management","Repeated injection without addressing the underlying cause"],"management":["Conservative first-line care with relative rest, activity and load modification, ice and a short course of NSAIDs","Physiotherapy focused on hamstring and adductor stretching, quadriceps strengthening and correcting lower-limb biomechanics","Address contributing medial compartment osteoarthritis and reduce weight where obesity is a factor","Corticosteroid injection into the bursa for cases resistant to conservative measures","Footwear advice and orthoses to offload the medial knee and correct gait","Reassess for meniscal, ligamentous or intra-articular pathology if symptoms fail to settle"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","British Journal of Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pes_cavus":{"name":"Pes cavus","system":"Musculoskeletal / Foot & Ankle","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cavus foot","high-arched foot","pes cavovarus","high instep"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pes cavus is an abnormally high medial longitudinal arch, commonly with hindfoot varus, a plantarflexed first ray, and clawing of the toes (cavovarus foot)","A cavus foot, especially if progressive, asymmetrical, or associated with clawing, should be assumed to have a neurological cause until proven otherwise","Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease is the classic hereditary neuropathy underlying cavovarus deformity, but other neuromuscular causes and old trauma also occur","The Coleman block test determines whether hindfoot varus is flexible and driven by the plantarflexed first ray, which corrects when the lateral foot is supported, or fixed","The high arch reduces the weight-bearing area, so load concentrates under the metatarsal heads and heel, causing metatarsalgia and painful callosities","Lateral foot overload predisposes to recurrent ankle sprains, peroneal tendon problems, and lateral column stress fractures","Examination must include a full neurological assessment, family history, and gait analysis, with weight-bearing radiographs to quantify the deformity","Management ranges from cushioned, supportive footwear and orthoses with lateral posting and metatarsal support, through physiotherapy, to osteotomy, tendon transfer, and arthrodesis for fixed deformity"],"pathophysiology":"Pes cavus is an elevation of the medial longitudinal arch that usually reflects a muscle imbalance across the foot rather than a primary bony abnormality. In the common cavovarus pattern, relative weakness of the tibialis anterior and peroneus brevis with preserved or overactive peroneus longus and tibialis posterior plantarflexes the first ray and drives the hindfoot into varus, while intrinsic weakness produces clawing of the toes. This imbalance is characteristically caused by hereditary motor and sensory neuropathies, most notably Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, but also by other neuromuscular disorders, spinal pathology, and sequelae of trauma or compartment syndrome. The rigid high arch concentrates load on the heel and metatarsal heads, producing metatarsalgia and callosities, and the varus hindfoot destabilises the ankle, leading to recurrent lateral ankle sprains, peroneal tendon overload, and lateral column stress injuries. Initially flexible, the deformity tends to stiffen and become fixed as it progresses.","additionalDifferentials":["Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease and other hereditary neuropathies","Residual or idiopathic clubfoot deformity","Post-traumatic cavus following compartment syndrome or malunion","Spinal dysraphism, tethered cord, or other central neurological causes","Poliomyelitis or other motor neuropathy sequelae","Friedreich's ataxia and related hereditary ataxias","Idiopathic subtle cavus without an identifiable cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full neurological examination and family history to seek a hereditary neuropathy","Coleman block test to determine whether hindfoot varus is flexible or fixed","Weight-bearing foot radiographs to quantify the arch and hindfoot alignment","Nerve conduction studies and neurology referral where a neuromuscular cause is suspected","Spinal MRI if a central or cord lesion is considered, particularly in unilateral or rapidly progressive cases","Genetic testing for Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease when clinically indicated","Pedobarography to characterise the abnormal loading pattern"],"redFlags":["Progressive, asymmetrical, or unilateral cavus deformity suggesting an active neurological process such as a tethered cord or tumour","New or evolving neurological deficit requiring urgent neurological and spinal evaluation","Sensory loss with plantar ulceration in a neuropathic foot","Recurrent giving way with ankle instability and lateral column stress injury","Rapid deterioration in gait or balance indicating a progressive neuromuscular disorder"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is closely tied to the underlying cause. Idiopathic or mild cavus may remain stable and manageable with orthoses, but neuropathic cavovarus deformity, as in Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, tends to be progressive and to recur even after surgery because the causative imbalance persists. Combined bony realignment and tendon transfers can restore a plantigrade, stable foot, but long-term orthotic support and monitoring are usually required.","pitfalls":["Assuming a cavus foot is idiopathic and failing to search for an underlying neurological cause","Not performing the Coleman block test, so that a flexible hindfoot is treated as if fixed","Correcting bony deformity without addressing the tendon imbalance, leading to recurrence","Overlooking spinal pathology in unilateral or rapidly progressive deformity","Neglecting ankle instability and lateral overload that accompany the varus hindfoot"],"management":["Identify and manage the underlying neurological cause and refer to neurology, as most progressive cavovarus is neuropathic, commonly Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease","Custom orthoses and supportive footwear with lateral posting and cushioning to redistribute load and relieve metatarsalgia and callosities","Physiotherapy for stretching and strengthening with ankle stabilisation, and podiatric callus care","Surgery for progressive or rigid deformity, including soft-tissue releases, tendon transfers and calcaneal or first-ray osteotomies, with arthrodesis for fixed deformity","Manage associated lateral ankle instability and peroneal tendon problems with bracing and rehabilitation","Genetic counselling and testing for Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease with family screening","Long-term monitoring for progression and for recurrence after surgery, as the causative imbalance persists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"pes_planus":{"name":"Pes planus (flatfoot)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Foot & Ankle","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["flatfoot","flat foot","fallen arches","pes planovalgus","adult acquired flatfoot deformity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pes planus is loss of the medial longitudinal arch, usually with hindfoot valgus and forefoot abduction, and may be physiological or pathological","The critical distinction is between a flexible flatfoot, in which the arch reconstitutes on tiptoe or with the Jack toe-raise test, and a rigid flatfoot, which does not correct and suggests tarsal coalition or arthritis","Flexible flatfoot is common and often asymptomatic in children, in whom it typically improves with growth and needs no active treatment when painless","In adults the leading cause of a progressive, painful, unilateral flatfoot is tibialis posterior tendon dysfunction","The too-many-toes sign, in which more lateral toes are visible from behind because of forefoot abduction, and an inability to perform a single-limb heel raise both point to tibialis posterior insufficiency","Examination should test arch flexibility, hindfoot correction on heel raise, gastrocnemius tightness, and the integrity and power of the tibialis posterior tendon","Weight-bearing radiographs assess alignment and arthritis, while ultrasound or MRI evaluates the tibialis posterior tendon and MRI or CT identifies a tarsal coalition","Management ranges from orthoses, medial arch support, physiotherapy with tibialis posterior strengthening, and calf stretching to tendon reconstruction, osteotomy, or arthrodesis in advanced deformity"],"pathophysiology":"Pes planus reflects failure of the structures that maintain the medial longitudinal arch, which depends on the bony architecture, the spring and plantar ligaments and plantar fascia, and dynamic support from the tibialis posterior. In flexible flatfoot the arch collapses on standing but reconstitutes when load is removed or the great toe is dorsiflexed, tightening the windlass mechanism of the plantar fascia. Adult acquired flatfoot most often develops when the tibialis posterior tendon degenerates and elongates, so that it can no longer invert and lock the hindfoot for an efficient heel raise; the hindfoot drifts into valgus, the forefoot abducts, and the medial arch progressively flattens, giving the too-many-toes appearance and a failed single-limb heel raise. If uncorrected the deformity becomes fixed and secondary arthritis develops. A rigid flatfoot instead reflects an underlying structural cause such as tarsal coalition, arthritis, or neuromuscular imbalance. A tight gastrocnemius commonly coexists and increases deforming forces on the midfoot.","additionalDifferentials":["Tibialis posterior tendon dysfunction as the cause of acquired flatfoot","Tarsal coalition producing a rigid or peroneal-spastic flatfoot","Midfoot or hindfoot osteoarthritis","Inflammatory arthropathy such as rheumatoid disease","Charcot neuroarthropathy in the diabetic or neuropathic foot","Accessory navicular contributing to medial arch symptoms","Neuromuscular flatfoot from cerebral palsy or other conditions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Standing clinical examination with the tiptoe and single-limb heel-raise tests to assess flexibility and hindfoot correction","Assessment of tibialis posterior power and tenderness and of gastrocnemius tightness with the Silfverskiold test","Weight-bearing foot and ankle radiographs to measure alignment and detect arthritis","Ultrasound or MRI of the tibialis posterior tendon where dysfunction is suspected","CT or MRI to identify and characterise a tarsal coalition in a rigid flatfoot","Pedobarography to map abnormal plantar loading where relevant","Inflammatory markers and serology if inflammatory arthritis is suspected"],"redFlags":["A rigid, painful flatfoot that does not correct on tiptoe, suggesting tarsal coalition, arthritis, or Charcot change","Rapidly progressive unilateral adult flatfoot with medial pain indicating tibialis posterior rupture","A hot, swollen, deformed foot in a neuropathic or diabetic patient suggesting Charcot neuroarthropathy","Features of inflammatory arthritis with symmetrical joint involvement","Neurological signs suggesting a neuromuscular cause of the deformity"],"prognosis":"Physiological flexible flatfoot in children usually improves with growth and rarely causes long-term problems. Adult acquired flatfoot from tibialis posterior dysfunction is progressive if untreated, moving from a flexible, correctable deformity to a fixed one with arthritis; early orthotic and physiotherapy treatment can control symptoms, and staged reconstructive surgery can restore alignment, whereas established rigid deformity may ultimately require arthrodesis.","pitfalls":["Over-treating an asymptomatic flexible paediatric flatfoot that would resolve with growth","Failing to distinguish flexible from rigid deformity and missing a tarsal coalition","Overlooking tibialis posterior dysfunction as the cause of a painful adult flatfoot until it becomes fixed","Missing a tight gastrocnemius that must be addressed for correction to succeed","Not recognising Charcot neuroarthropathy in the diabetic foot presenting as a collapsing arch"],"management":["Reassurance and observation for asymptomatic flexible flatfoot in children, which typically improves with growth","Supportive footwear, medial arch orthoses, gastrocnemius stretching and physiotherapy for symptomatic flexible flatfoot","For adult acquired flatfoot from tibialis posterior dysfunction, use bracing or immobilisation and early tibialis posterior strengthening, with weight reduction","Surgical reconstruction for progressive or refractory flexible deformity, combining tendon transfer with a medialising calcaneal osteotomy","Arthrodesis for a rigid or arthritic flatfoot, and resection or fusion for a symptomatic tarsal coalition","Orthopaedic and podiatry follow-up to monitor progression from a flexible to a fixed deformity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"peters_anomaly":{"name":"Peters anomaly","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anterior segment dysgenesis","Peters anomaly with corneal opacity","PAX6","central corneal leukoma with iridocorneal adhesions"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of anterior segment dysgenesis defined by a central corneal opacity (leukoma) with a defect in the underlying corneal endothelium and Descemet membrane","Iridocorneal or keratolenticular adhesions strand from the iris or lens to the back of the corneal opacity","Presents in the newborn with central corneal clouding, and may be unilateral or bilateral","Glaucoma is a frequent and important association, so intraocular pressure must be assessed and monitored","Peters-plus syndrome adds systemic features such as short stature, developmental delay and cleft lip or palate","Implicated developmental genes include PAX6, PITX2, FOXC1 and CYP1B1, reflecting overlap with other anterior segment dysgenesis disorders","Dense bilateral corneal opacity threatens vision through both media opacity and deprivation amblyopia"],"pathophysiology":"Peters anomaly is a developmental defect of the anterior segment arising from abnormal migration and differentiation of neural-crest-derived tissues that form the cornea, iris and anterior chamber angle. Failure of normal separation of the lens and iris from the developing cornea leaves a central defect in the corneal endothelium and Descemet membrane, producing a central opacity, and persistent adhesions between the iris or lens and the posterior cornea. Disruption of the same developmental structures frequently affects the drainage angle, predisposing to glaucoma. Mutations in transcription-factor and related developmental genes underlie many cases and account for the overlap with the broader anterior segment dysgenesis spectrum.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital glaucoma with corneal oedema and clouding","Sclerocornea and other congenital corneal opacities","Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome within the anterior segment dysgenesis spectrum","Congenital hereditary endothelial dystrophy","Birth-related corneal injury with Descemet tears","Mucopolysaccharidoses and metabolic corneal clouding"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination under anaesthesia with slit-lamp assessment of the cornea and anterior segment","Intraocular pressure measurement and gonioscopy to detect glaucoma and angle anomalies","Anterior segment imaging or ultrasound biomicroscopy to define adhesions and lens position","Systemic and dysmorphology assessment for Peters-plus features","Molecular genetic testing of anterior segment dysgenesis genes where indicated"],"redFlags":["Raised intraocular pressure indicating associated glaucoma","Dense bilateral corneal opacity risking irreversible deprivation amblyopia","Progressive corneal decompensation or worsening opacity","Systemic features suggesting Peters-plus syndrome requiring paediatric evaluation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the density and laterality of the opacity and on glaucoma control; milder unilateral cases may retain useful vision, while dense bilateral disease has a guarded outlook even after corneal transplantation, which carries high rejection and complication rates in infants.","pitfalls":["Failing to detect and treat associated glaucoma, a major cause of poor outcome","Overlooking amblyopia management alongside surgical treatment of the opacity","Assuming an isolated eye disorder and missing Peters-plus systemic features","Underestimating the difficulty and risks of paediatric corneal transplantation"],"management":["Prioritise amblyopia management with optical correction and occlusion therapy to maximise visual development","Detect and control glaucoma, escalating from medical therapy to surgery, with regular intraocular pressure monitoring","Consider penetrating keratoplasty or keratoprosthesis for dense or bilateral corneal opacity, accepting the high rejection and complication rates in infants, while optical iridectomy may suffice for milder cases","Examination under anaesthesia and regular ophthalmic review to guide the timing of intervention","Systemic and dysmorphology assessment for Peters-plus syndrome with broader paediatric input","Genetic counselling and molecular testing of anterior segment dysgenesis genes such as PAX6, PITX2, FOXC1 and CYP1B1","Low-vision support and coordinated paediatric ophthalmology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"petrous_apicitis":{"name":"Petrous apicitis (Gradenigo syndrome)","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Head and Neck","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["petrositis","Gradenigo syndrome","apical petrositis","petrous apex infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An infection of the pneumatised petrous apex, usually a complication of acute or chronic otitis media and mastoiditis","The classic Gradenigo triad comprises otorrhoea, deep retro-orbital or facial pain in the trigeminal distribution, and an abducens (sixth nerve) palsy causing horizontal diplopia","The triad reflects spread of infection to the petrous apex, involving the trigeminal (Gasserian) ganglion and the abducens nerve in Dorello canal","It has become uncommon in the antibiotic era but remains an important cause of cranial neuropathy complicating ear infection","The full triad is often incomplete, so persistent otorrhoea with new headache or diplopia should raise suspicion","Contrast CT and MRI of the temporal bone demonstrate petrous apex opacification, bony erosion and any associated intracranial complication","Management centres on culture-directed antimicrobial therapy with drainage of the middle ear and mastoid, and surgical drainage of the petrous apex in refractory or complicated cases","Meningitis, intracranial abscess, and dural venous sinus thrombosis are feared complications requiring urgent recognition"],"pathophysiology":"The petrous apex is variably pneumatised and connected to the middle ear and mastoid air-cell system. Infection of the middle ear and mastoid can extend into these apical air cells, and if drainage is impaired the trapped infection progresses to osteomyelitis of the petrous apex. Its anatomical relations explain the syndrome: inflammation irritates the trigeminal ganglion at the petrous apex causing facial pain, and involves the abducens nerve as it passes through Dorello canal beneath the petroclinoid ligament, producing a lateral rectus palsy. Further spread can breach the dura, leading to meningitis, intracranial abscess or venous sinus thrombosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Uncomplicated acute otitis media or mastoiditis","Petrous apex cholesteatoma or cholesterol granuloma","Skull-base osteomyelitis, including necrotising (malignant) otitis externa","Nasopharyngeal or skull-base tumour involving the petrous apex","Cavernous sinus or dural venous sinus thrombosis of other cause","Trigeminal neuralgia or other cause of isolated abducens palsy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the temporal bone to show petrous apex opacification and bony erosion","MRI with contrast to assess soft-tissue and intracranial extension","Microbiological culture of ear discharge or intraoperative samples","Inflammatory markers and blood cultures to gauge systemic infection","Lumbar puncture where meningitis is suspected and safe to perform after imaging"],"redFlags":["Signs of meningitis such as neck stiffness, photophobia and altered consciousness","New or multiple cranial nerve palsies indicating skull-base spread","Features of intracranial abscess or dural venous sinus thrombosis","High fever and systemic toxicity suggesting severe or complicated infection"],"prognosis":"With prompt culture-directed antimicrobial therapy and appropriate drainage, most cases resolve and the abducens palsy often recovers, though recovery can be slow. Delayed treatment risks serious intracranial complications with significant morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Attributing persistent otorrhoea with headache to simple otitis media and missing evolving petrous apicitis","Overlooking an incomplete Gradenigo triad, since all three features are frequently not present together","Failing to image the intracranial compartment when new cranial nerve signs appear","Inadequate drainage of the petrous apex or mastoid, allowing infection to persist or progress"],"management":["Prompt culture-directed intravenous antibiotics, begun empirically against the causative otitis flora and continued for a prolonged course","Surgical drainage where medical therapy fails, ranging from myringotomy and grommet insertion to mastoidectomy and petrous apex drainage","Treat the underlying otitis media and mastoiditis as the source of infection","Monitor and follow the abducens palsy and other cranial neuropathies, which often recover with treatment","Imaging surveillance and early neurosurgical involvement for intracranial complications such as meningitis, abscess or venous sinus thrombosis","Consider anticoagulation if dural venous sinus thrombosis develops","Coordinate otolaryngology and neurosurgery, escalating urgently on any sign of intracranial spread"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"peutz_jeghers_syndrome":{"name":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","system":"Genetics / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PJS","STK11","LKB1","periorificial lentiginosis with gastrointestinal polyposis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / relevant specialty textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant syndrome caused by germline loss-of-function variants in the STK11 (LKB1) serine-threonine kinase tumour-suppressor gene","Characterised by multiple hamartomatous polyps throughout the gastrointestinal tract, predominantly in the small bowel","Mucocutaneous pigmented macules around the lips, oral mucosa, fingers and perianal region are a classic early sign that may fade with age","Small-bowel polyps commonly cause recurrent obstruction and intussusception, often in childhood or early adulthood","There is a substantially increased lifetime risk of gastrointestinal cancers (colorectal, small bowel, gastric, pancreatic) and extraintestinal cancers including breast","Distinctive tumours include ovarian sex cord tumours with annular tubules and testicular large-cell calcifying Sertoli cell tumours","Surveillance combines gastrointestinal endoscopy with small-bowel imaging (video capsule or MR enterography) and organ-specific cancer screening from set ages"],"pathophysiology":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is caused by germline inactivating variants in STK11, which encodes a serine-threonine kinase (also called LKB1) that regulates cellular energy metabolism, polarity and growth largely through the AMP-activated protein kinase and mTOR pathways. Loss of STK11 tumour-suppressor function, typically with a somatic second hit, disrupts these regulatory signals and drives the development of hamartomatous polyps and an elevated risk of multiple epithelial malignancies. The mucocutaneous pigmentation reflects abnormal melanocyte behaviour associated with the underlying genetic defect.","additionalDifferentials":["Juvenile polyposis syndrome (SMAD4/BMPR1A)","Familial adenomatous polyposis","PTEN hamartoma tumour syndrome (Cowden syndrome)","Laugier-Hunziker syndrome causing benign mucosal pigmentation without polyps","Carney complex with lentigines","Sporadic hamartomatous or hyperplastic polyps"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Germline molecular genetic testing of STK11 including deletion/duplication analysis","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and colonoscopy for polyp detection and removal","Small-bowel evaluation with video capsule endoscopy or MR enterography","Cross-sectional imaging or MRI for pancreatic surveillance","Breast imaging surveillance in women per guidelines","Pelvic and testicular ultrasound for characteristic gonadal tumours"],"redFlags":["Acute abdominal pain with vomiting suggesting intussusception or small-bowel obstruction","Gastrointestinal bleeding or unexplained iron-deficiency anaemia from bleeding polyps","New breast, pancreatic or gastrointestinal symptoms in an affected adult raising concern for malignancy","Precocious puberty or gynaecomastia suggesting a hormonally active gonadal tumour"],"prognosis":"The syndrome carries a high cumulative lifetime risk of malignancy across several organs, so long-term prognosis depends heavily on adherence to structured surveillance and early intervention. Polyp-related complications such as intussusception contribute significant morbidity, particularly in younger patients.","pitfalls":["Attributing periorificial pigmentation to benign freckling and missing the diagnosis, especially as macules fade in adults","Underestimating small-bowel polyp burden by relying on colonoscopy and gastroscopy alone","Failing to begin multi-organ cancer surveillance, including breast and pancreatic screening, at recommended ages","Not testing and counselling at-risk relatives"],"management":["Endoscopic polypectomy of significant gastrointestinal hamartomatous polyps to relieve symptoms and remove malignant potential","Structured lifelong cancer surveillance with upper gastrointestinal endoscopy, colonoscopy and small-bowel imaging by video capsule endoscopy or MR enterography at regular intervals","Elective removal of large small-bowel polyps to pre-empt intussusception and avoid repeated emergency resections and short-bowel syndrome","Pancreatic surveillance with cross-sectional imaging, breast screening by MRI and mammography in women, and gonadal ultrasound for sex-cord and Sertoli cell tumours","Prompt surgical management of acute intussusception or obstruction, with intra-operative enteroscopy and clearance of accessible polyps","Clinical genetics referral with germline STK11 testing and predictive testing and cascade screening of at-risk relatives","Coordinated multidisciplinary care across gastroenterology, surgery, clinical genetics and oncology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","GeneReviews: Peutz-Jeghers Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"peyronie_disease":{"name":"Peyronie Disease","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Peyronie disease","Peyronie's disease","penile fibrosis","induratio penis plastica","penile curvature disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Peyronie disease is an acquired fibrotic disorder of the tunica albuginea of the penis, producing a palpable plaque, penile curvature, and sometimes pain, deformity, and shortening","It typically presents in middle-aged men and can cause difficulty with penetration and considerable psychological distress","The disease has two phases: an active (acute) phase with pain and evolving deformity, and a stable (chronic) phase in which the curvature and plaque have plateaued","Erectile dysfunction commonly coexists and should be assessed, as it influences both distress and treatment choice","It is associated with penile trauma or microtrauma during intercourse and with other fibrosing conditions such as Dupuytren contracture, as well as with diabetes","Diagnosis is largely clinical from the history and palpation of the plaque, supplemented by objective assessment of curvature, often after inducing an erection","Non-surgical options during the active phase and for milder deformity include oral and injectable therapies and traction, aiming to reduce curvature and stabilise disease","Surgery is reserved for stable disease with functionally significant deformity and includes plication, plaque incision or excision with grafting, or penile prosthesis when erectile dysfunction is also present","Intervention decisions should await disease stability, since operating during the active phase risks recurrence and further change"],"pathophysiology":"Peyronie disease is thought to begin with repetitive microtrauma to the tunica albuginea, most often during sexual activity, in a susceptible individual. The injury triggers an aberrant, exaggerated wound-healing response with local inflammation, fibroblast activation, and deposition of disorganised collagen and elastin. This forms a fibrous, sometimes calcified, plaque that is less elastic than the surrounding tunica. During erection the inelastic segment fails to expand normally, bending the penis toward the plaque and producing the characteristic curvature and deformity. Over time inflammation subsides and the plaque matures and stabilises, marking the transition to the chronic phase.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital penile curvature (present since puberty, no plaque)","Penile fracture or acute trauma","Penile cancer or other penile mass","Dorsal vein thrombosis of the penis","Erectile dysfunction from other causes","Lymphangiosclerosis of the penis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Focused history and palpation of the plaque in the flaccid state","Objective measurement of curvature during an induced or photographically documented erection","Penile duplex ultrasound to characterise plaque, calcification, and vascular status where relevant","Assessment of erectile function using validated questionnaires","Evaluation for associated conditions such as diabetes and Dupuytren contracture"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging or atypical penile mass raising concern for malignancy","Acute severe pain with deformity suggesting penile fracture rather than Peyronie disease","Deformity severe enough to prevent intercourse or urinary function","Significant psychological distress or relationship impact","Progressive deformity that has not stabilised, indicating ongoing active disease"],"prognosis":"The natural history is variable: a minority of men improve spontaneously, many stabilise with residual deformity, and a proportion progress. Pain from the active phase usually resolves over time. With appropriate treatment tailored to disease phase and severity, most men can achieve functional improvement, and surgery in stable disease offers reliable correction of significant curvature, though some penile shortening or altered sensation may persist.","pitfalls":["Operating during the active, unstable phase and risking recurrence","Overlooking coexisting erectile dysfunction that alters the treatment approach","Mistaking congenital curvature, which lacks a plaque, for Peyronie disease","Failing to address the psychological impact on the patient and partner","Assuming a penile mass is a benign plaque without excluding malignancy when features are atypical"],"management":["Phase-directed approach with reassurance and active surveillance during the active phase, since spontaneous stabilisation or improvement may occur","Intralesional collagenase Clostridium histolyticum injection for stable-phase curvature with a palpable plaque","Penile traction therapy and mechanical adjuncts to preserve length and reduce curvature","Surgical correction once disease is stable, using tunical plication for adequate length, plaque incision or excision with grafting for severe curvature, or penile prosthesis where erectile dysfunction coexists","Concurrent treatment of erectile dysfunction with phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors, as it influences distress and surgical choice","Analgesia to control pain during the active inflammatory phase","Psychosexual counselling for distress and relationship impact, with referral to specialist andrology or urology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pfapa_adult":{"name":"PFAPA syndrome","system":"Rheumatology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PFAPA","periodic fever aphthous stomatitis pharyngitis adenitis","Marshall syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard rheumatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The acronym stands for periodic fever, aphthous stomatitis, pharyngitis, and cervical adenitis, the defining clinical tetrad","It is the most common periodic fever syndrome of childhood, though a similar picture is increasingly recognised in adults","Fevers recur with striking clockwork regularity, and patients are typically completely well between episodes with normal growth and development","Episodes are not triggered by infection and do not respond to antibiotics","There is no identified single-gene cause, distinguishing it from the hereditary monogenic periodic fevers","Diagnosis is clinical and requires exclusion of cyclic neutropenia and the monogenic autoinflammatory syndromes","Corticosteroids given at the onset of an attack characteristically abort the fever rapidly, a response that supports the diagnosis","Colchicine may reduce attack frequency, and tonsillectomy can be curative in selected children"],"pathophysiology":"PFAPA is considered a polygenic/complex autoinflammatory disorder involving dysregulated innate immune activation, with episodic overproduction of proinflammatory cytokines including interleukin-1 beta during attacks. Unlike the classic hereditary periodic fevers, no single causative gene is identified, and the periodic pattern reflects intermittent inflammasome-driven inflammation rather than infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Cyclic neutropenia","Hereditary periodic fever syndromes (FMF, TRAPS, HIDS, CAPS)","Recurrent viral or streptococcal pharyngitis","Behcet disease (recurrent oral ulceration)","Adult-onset Still disease in the adult presentation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial full blood counts during and between episodes to exclude cyclic neutropenia","Acute-phase reactants (elevated during attacks, normal between)","Genetic testing for monogenic periodic fevers when the presentation is atypical","Documentation of the periodicity and symptom diary","Throat swabs/cultures to confirm episodes are not infective"],"redFlags":["Neutropenia coinciding with fevers, pointing to cyclic neutropenia rather than PFAPA","Growth failure or ill health between attacks suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Atypical features (rash, serositis, prominent arthritis) suggesting a monogenic syndrome","First presentation of a periodic fever in adulthood requiring exclusion of other systemic disease"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limiting in most children, typically resolving spontaneously over years with no long-term sequelae and no risk of amyloidosis; the adult form tends to be more persistent but remains non-life-threatening.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly prescribing antibiotics for recurrent pharyngitis that is actually autoinflammatory","Failing to check blood counts during fevers and missing cyclic neutropenia","Assuming a childhood-only condition and overlooking adult presentations","Diagnosing PFAPA without excluding monogenic periodic fevers in atypical cases"],"management":["Abortive single-dose corticosteroid at attack onset, which characteristically terminates the fever rapidly","Symptomatic care during episodes with antipyretics, analgesia and hydration for the pharyngitis and aphthous stomatitis","Colchicine to reduce attack frequency in patients with frequent, closely spaced episodes","Tonsillectomy as a potential definitive option in selected refractory cases","Interleukin-1 blockade with anakinra reserved for severe, treatment-resistant disease","Documentation of periodicity and exclusion of cyclic neutropenia and monogenic periodic fevers before committing to long-term therapy","Reassurance about the benign, non-life-threatening course with specialist rheumatology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"pfapa_syndrome":{"name":"PFAPA Syndrome","system":"Multisystem / Immune","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Periodic Fever, Aphthous stomatitis, Pharyngitis, Adenitis","Marshall syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric rheumatology and autoinflammatory references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Commonest periodic fever syndrome of childhood, typically beginning in early childhood before school age and following a strikingly clockwork-like periodicity.","Defined clinically by recurrent fevers accompanied by at least one of aphthous stomatitis, pharyngitis and cervical adenitis, with well-child intervals between episodes.","Attacks recur at remarkably regular intervals and are self-limiting, resolving spontaneously over a few days regardless of treatment.","Growth and development remain normal, and children are entirely well between episodes, which helps distinguish it from chronic inflammatory disease.","A single dose of corticosteroid often aborts an attack dramatically, which is supportive of the diagnosis but may shorten the interval to the next episode.","It is a diagnosis of exclusion; monogenic periodic fever syndromes and cyclic neutropenia must be considered, particularly with atypical features.","Tonsillectomy leads to resolution or marked improvement in many children and is an option for troublesome, well-characterised disease.","The condition is generally not familial in the Mendelian sense and lacks a single causative gene, distinguishing it from the classic hereditary autoinflammatory syndromes."],"pathophysiology":"PFAPA is regarded as a polygenic autoinflammatory disorder driven by dysregulated innate immunity rather than a single-gene defect or infection. During attacks there is activation of the interleukin-1 beta pathway with recruitment and activation of inflammatory monocytes and T cells, and complement activation, producing the stereotyped systemic inflammatory response. Interferon-gamma-induced chemokines are upregulated during flares, and levels of certain regulatory and T-helper-2 cytokines fall, consistent with a transient breakdown of immune homeostasis. The remarkable periodicity suggests a cyclical accumulation of a proinflammatory stimulus that reaches a threshold and then resolves once the acute-phase response is mounted. The tonsils appear to act as an immunological focus in many patients, which may explain the therapeutic benefit of tonsillectomy. There is no persistent inflammation between episodes, and the acute-phase reactants normalise completely, correlating with the benign long-term course and absence of amyloid risk in typical disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Cyclic neutropenia","Familial Mediterranean fever","TNF receptor-associated periodic syndrome","Mevalonate kinase deficiency (hyperimmunoglobulinaemia D syndrome)","Recurrent viral or streptococcal pharyngotonsillitis","Behçet disease with recurrent oral ulceration","Chronic granulomatous or other immunodeficiency with recurrent fevers"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial full blood counts during and between attacks to exclude cyclic neutropenia","Acute-phase reactants during an episode showing a marked but transient rise","Throat swab or rapid antigen test to exclude streptococcal pharyngitis during flares","Genetic testing for monogenic periodic fever syndromes when features are atypical","Serum immunoglobulins including immunoglobulin D where mevalonate kinase deficiency is considered","Documentation of a symptom and fever diary to demonstrate periodicity and well intervals","Assessment of growth parameters to confirm normal development"],"redFlags":["Failure to thrive or impaired growth, suggesting an alternative chronic inflammatory disorder","Persistent symptoms or raised inflammatory markers between attacks","Onset in adulthood or highly irregular, non-periodic fevers","Recurrent severe infections or oral ulceration raising concern for neutropenia or immunodeficiency","Development of arthritis, serositis or organ-specific inflammation not typical of PFAPA"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; PFAPA is benign and self-limiting, with attacks usually decreasing in frequency and severity over years and often resolving spontaneously by adolescence without long-term sequelae.","pitfalls":["Labelling any recurrent childhood fever as PFAPA without confirming the characteristic periodicity and complete well intervals.","Missing cyclic neutropenia by not checking blood counts during symptomatic episodes.","Repeatedly treating episodes as bacterial tonsillitis with courses of antibiotics.","Overlooking atypical features that should prompt genetic testing for a monogenic syndrome.","Assuming corticosteroid responsiveness confirms the diagnosis while ignoring that it may shorten inter-episode intervals."],"management":["Single abortive dose of corticosteroid at attack onset to terminate episodes, with counselling that it may shorten the interval to the next attack","Symptomatic supportive care with antipyretics, analgesics and fluids during febrile episodes","Colchicine prophylaxis to lengthen the inter-attack interval in children with frequent attacks","Tonsillectomy as a curative option in selected children with troublesome, recurrent disease","Interleukin-1 blockade with anakinra for refractory cases","Exclusion of cyclic neutropenia and monogenic periodic fevers, with genetic testing when features are atypical, before escalating therapy","Reassurance about spontaneous resolution over years and normal growth, with paediatric rheumatology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of paediatric rheumatology (standard reference)","Textbook of autoinflammatory diseases (standard reference)","International periodic fever and autoinflammatory syndrome guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"pfeiffer_syndrome":{"name":"Pfeiffer syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acrocephalosyndactyly type V","FGFR1-related craniosynostosis","FGFR2-related craniosynostosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by autosomal dominant gain-of-function mutations in FGFR1 or, more often and more severely, FGFR2","Craniosynostosis with midface hypoplasia and proptosis is combined with characteristically broad, medially deviated thumbs and great toes","Partial soft-tissue syndactyly of the hands and feet is common but is milder than the fusion seen in Apert syndrome","Type 1 (classic) has a good prognosis with usually normal intelligence, whereas types 2 and 3 are severe with major neurological risk","Type 2 shows a cloverleaf skull (kleeblattschadel) from multisuture fusion with extreme proptosis, while type 3 has a very short cranial base without the cloverleaf shape","Airway obstruction, tracheal cartilage anomalies and severe proptosis threatening the globe are important early hazards in the severe forms","Hydrocephalus, Chiari-type hindbrain herniation and raised intracranial pressure require neuroimaging surveillance","Management is by a craniofacial team with staged vault expansion, midface advancement and airway support"],"pathophysiology":"Pfeiffer syndrome is driven by activating mutations in fibroblast growth factor receptor 1 or 2 that amplify FGF signalling in cranial sutures and limb buds. Excess receptor activity promotes premature suture ossification and disturbs the development of the digits, producing broad, deviated thumbs and great toes with variable soft-tissue syndactyly. The degree of receptor activation broadly tracks with clinical severity, so the more disruptive FGFR2 changes tend to cause the multisuture, cloverleaf-skull phenotypes with greater neurological compromise.","additionalDifferentials":["Apert syndrome (severe complex syndactyly)","Crouzon syndrome (normal hands and feet)","Jackson-Weiss syndrome (broad great toes, FGFR2)","Saethre-Chotzen syndrome (TWIST1)","Carpenter syndrome (polydactyly)","Antley-Bixler syndrome (radiohumeral synostosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of FGFR1 and FGFR2 to confirm diagnosis and subtype","Three-dimensional cranial CT to characterise suture fusion and detect a cloverleaf skull","MRI of the brain and craniocervical junction for hydrocephalus and hindbrain herniation","Radiographs of the hands and feet to document broad thumbs, great toes and syndactyly","Airway endoscopy and polysomnography, especially in severe forms with tracheal anomalies","Ophthalmology review for proptosis and corneal protection"],"redFlags":["Cloverleaf skull with extreme proptosis endangering the globes","Airway obstruction or a solid cartilaginous trachea causing respiratory failure","Rapidly progressive hydrocephalus or raised intracranial pressure","Symptomatic hindbrain herniation with brainstem compromise"],"prognosis":"Type 1 disease generally has a favourable outcome with normal cognition after craniofacial management, whereas types 2 and 3 carry high early morbidity and mortality from airway, brain and visual complications and demand intensive multidisciplinary care.","pitfalls":["Assuming a good prognosis without determining the clinical subtype","Missing tracheal cartilage abnormalities as a cause of respiratory difficulty","Failing to protect severely proptotic globes","Overlooking progressive hydrocephalus during follow-up"],"management":["Staged craniofacial surgery with early cranial vault expansion and suture release to relieve raised intracranial pressure, followed by later midface advancement for midface hypoplasia and proptosis","Airway protection in severe forms, managing upper-airway and tracheal cartilage anomalies, with tracheostomy where obstruction is life-threatening","Corneal protection for severe proptosis using lubrication and tarsorrhaphy to prevent exposure keratopathy and globe damage","Neurosurgical management of hydrocephalus by shunting and of hindbrain herniation by craniocervical decompression","Hand and foot surgery for broad, medially deviated thumbs and great toes and for soft-tissue syndactyly","Surveillance for raised intracranial pressure, hearing loss and obstructive sleep apnoea with serial cranial imaging and polysomnography","Clinical genetics referral with FGFR1 and FGFR2 testing and family counselling, within multidisciplinary craniofacial team care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: FGFR-related craniosynostosis syndromes"],"crossLinks":[]},"pgm1_cdg":{"name":"PGM1-CDG (phosphoglucomutase 1)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PGM1","phosphoglucomutase 1 deficiency","congenital disorder of glycosylation type It","CDG-It","glycogenosis type XIV"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mixed disorder of glycosylation and glycogen metabolism caused by deficiency of phosphoglucomutase 1, which interconverts glucose-1-phosphate and glucose-6-phosphate","A distinctive clinical clue is a bifid uvula or cleft palate combined with exercise intolerance, myopathy and rhabdomyolysis with raised creatine kinase","Hypoglycaemia (often ketotic or with fasting), hepatopathy, dilated cardiomyopathy and short stature are recognised features","It is one of the treatable CDGs: oral galactose supplementation improves the glycosylation defect and several clinical and biochemical markers","Transferrin analysis characteristically shows a mixed type I and type II pattern, reflecting both reduced site occupancy and abnormal galactosylation","Endocrine abnormalities including hypogonadotrophic features and thyroid changes can occur","Management combines dietary galactose supplementation with attention to hypoglycaemia, cardiac and muscle complications; diagnosis is confirmed by PGM1 testing"],"pathophysiology":"Phosphoglucomutase 1 interconverts glucose-1-phosphate and glucose-6-phosphate, a step central both to glycogen turnover and to producing the UDP-glucose and UDP-galactose needed for glycosylation. Deficiency simultaneously impairs glycogen handling, causing exercise intolerance and hypoglycaemia, and depletes nucleotide sugars, causing under-glycosylation with abnormal galactosylation. This dual mechanism gives the characteristic mixed type I/type II transferrin pattern. Supplying galactose replenishes UDP-galactose, bypassing part of the block and improving glycosylation, which is why the disorder is treatable.","additionalDifferentials":["Glycogen storage disorders with exercise intolerance and rhabdomyolysis (e.g. McArdle disease)","Other CDGs, particularly those with a mixed transferrin pattern","Fatty acid oxidation disorders presenting with hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and myopathy","Congenital cardiomyopathies of metabolic origin","Isolated cleft palate or bifid uvula syndromes","Ketotic hypoglycaemia of other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transferrin isoelectric focusing or mass spectrometry showing a mixed type I/type II pattern","PGM1 molecular genetic testing and enzyme assay","Serum creatine kinase, particularly during exercise or rhabdomyolysis","Fasting and provocation studies to characterise hypoglycaemia","Echocardiography for dilated cardiomyopathy","Liver function tests, endocrine screen and growth assessment"],"redFlags":["Hypoglycaemia, especially with fasting or intercurrent illness","Rhabdomyolysis or acute muscle injury with exertion","Signs of heart failure from dilated cardiomyopathy","Cardiac arrhythmia"],"prognosis":"With galactose supplementation and careful management of hypoglycaemia and cardiac disease, many patients do well, though dilated cardiomyopathy can be a serious complication; overall outlook is more favourable than most severe CDGs given the available treatment.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis by overlooking the cleft palate or bifid uvula clue","Not recognising PGM1-CDG as galactose-responsive and withholding treatment","Attributing exercise intolerance solely to a primary glycogenosis","Failing to screen for and monitor dilated cardiomyopathy"],"management":["Oral D-galactose supplementation to replenish UDP-galactose and improve glycosylation and biochemical markers","Avoidance of prolonged fasting with regular complex-carbohydrate intake to prevent ketotic and fasting hypoglycaemia","Prevention and treatment of exertional rhabdomyolysis through activity modification and hydration, with monitoring of creatine kinase","Cardiac surveillance with serial echocardiography for dilated cardiomyopathy and standard heart-failure therapy if it develops","Monitoring and treatment of hepatopathy, endocrine dysfunction including hypogonadotrophic and thyroid abnormalities, and growth","Surgical repair of cleft palate or bifid uvula with associated feeding support","Metabolic medicine and clinical genetics referral with PGM1 testing and family counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID, congenital disorders of glycosylation","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics, disorders of glycosylation and glycogen metabolism"],"crossLinks":[]},"pgm3_deficiency":{"name":"PGM3 deficiency","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PGM3 deficiency","phosphoglucomutase 3 deficiency","PGM3-CDG","congenital disorder of glycosylation type PGM3"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive glycosylation defect that produces a combined immunodeficiency overlapping clinically with the hyper-IgE syndromes","The characteristic triad is severe atopic disease (eczema, food and environmental allergy, asthma), marked blood eosinophilia, and a strikingly elevated serum IgE","Recurrent bacterial and viral infections of the skin and respiratory tract reflect the underlying immunodeficiency","Neurological involvement is a distinguishing feature, including developmental delay, cognitive impairment, and sometimes motor or sensorineural problems","Skeletal and connective-tissue abnormalities, such as short stature and dysmorphic features, may accompany the immune and neurological findings","As a congenital disorder of glycosylation it can be supported by abnormal glycosylation studies, with confirmation by genetic testing of PGM3","Care is largely supportive with antimicrobial prophylaxis, immunoglobulin replacement where antibody function is impaired, allergy and eczema management, and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation considered in severe cases"],"pathophysiology":"PGM3 encodes phosphoglucomutase 3, an enzyme in the pathway that generates the sugar-nucleotide donor uridine diphosphate N-acetylglucosamine, which is essential for adding N-acetylglucosamine residues during protein glycosylation. Because glycosylation shapes the folding, stability, and function of countless glycoproteins on immune, neural, and connective-tissue cells, a hypomorphic defect in this enzyme perturbs many systems at once. In the immune compartment, altered glycosylation of receptors and signalling molecules skews lymphocyte development and function toward a type-2, allergic phenotype with high IgE and eosinophilia while impairing effective antimicrobial responses, and the same glycosylation failure disturbs neurodevelopment and skeletal growth, giving the combined immunological, neurological, and connective-tissue picture.","additionalDifferentials":["STAT3 loss-of-function autosomal dominant hyper-IgE syndrome","DOCK8 deficiency autosomal recessive hyper-IgE syndrome","Severe atopic dermatitis with reactive IgE elevation","Omenn syndrome and other combined immunodeficiencies with eosinophilia","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","Other congenital disorders of glycosylation","Netherton syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum IgE level and full blood count with eosinophil quantification","Quantitative immunoglobulins and specific antibody responses","Lymphocyte subset analysis and T-cell proliferation studies","Glycosylation analysis such as transferrin isoform studies where available","Genetic sequencing of PGM3","Neurodevelopmental assessment and neuroimaging","Skeletal survey or targeted imaging for connective-tissue and bony features"],"redFlags":["Severe eczema with recurrent skin and respiratory infection and very high IgE","Progressive neurodevelopmental regression or worsening cognitive impairment","Serious or opportunistic infection indicating combined immune failure","Failure to thrive with skeletal abnormalities","Autoimmune or immune-dysregulatory complications"],"prognosis":"The prognosis depends on the severity of the hypomorphic defect; milder cases may be managed supportively for years, whereas severe disease carries a substantial burden of infection and neurological impairment and may prompt haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, which can correct the immunodeficiency but not established neurological damage.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing ordinary severe atopic dermatitis and missing the underlying immunodeficiency and neurological features","Attributing the picture solely to a hyper-IgE syndrome without considering a glycosylation disorder","Overlooking the neurodevelopmental component when the allergic phenotype dominates","Failing to assess and support specific antibody function","Assuming transplantation will reverse neurological or skeletal abnormalities"],"management":["Antimicrobial prophylaxis and prompt treatment of recurrent bacterial and viral skin and respiratory infections","Immunoglobulin replacement therapy for the associated antibody deficiency","Aggressive atopic disease control with emollients and topical corticosteroids for eczema, allergen avoidance and asthma therapy","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as a potentially curative option to correct the immunodeficiency in severe disease","Surveillance of immune function, eosinophilia and serum IgE with monitoring for infective complications","Neurodevelopmental support and paediatric rehabilitation for developmental delay and motor or sensorineural problems","Clinical immunology and genetics referral with PGM3 sequencing and family counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"phace_syndrome":{"name":"PHACE syndrome","system":"Vascular anomalies / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PHACES syndrome","posterior fossa malformations-haemangioma-arterial-cardiac-eye syndrome","Pascual-Castroviejo type II syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"ISSVA classification / Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Suspect PHACE in any infant with a large (typically >5 cm) segmental infantile haemangioma of the face, scalp or neck rather than a small focal lesion","The acronym captures Posterior fossa brain malformations, Haemangioma, Arterial cerebrovascular anomalies, Cardiac defects (notably coarctation of the aorta) and Eye abnormalities; ventral developmental defects (sternal cleft, supraumbilical raphe) extend it to PHACE(S)","Facial haemangiomas follow recognised segmental patterns, and frontotemporal or mandibular segments carry the highest association with extracutaneous anomalies","Cerebral and cervical arterial anomalies (dysplasia, aberrant origin, stenosis, absence) predispose to acute ischaemic stroke and are a leading cause of morbidity","As an infantile haemangioma, the cutaneous lesion is GLUT1-positive and follows the proliferate-then-involute course of true vascular tumours in the ISSVA framework","Airway (subglottic) haemangioma should be considered when a beard-distribution segmental lesion is present with stridor","Baseline MRI/MRA of the brain, head and neck, echocardiography with aortic arch imaging, and ophthalmology and cardiology review are the core evaluation","Beta-blocker therapy (propranolol for infantile-haemangioma-type lesions) is first-line for the haemangioma but is used cautiously given the risk of arteriopathy and coarctation"],"pathophysiology":"PHACE is a neurocutaneous condition in which a large segmental infantile haemangioma occurs together with malformations of structures that share regional embryonic development, particularly the cephalic neural crest and cranial vasculature. The haemangioma itself is a benign GLUT1-positive vascular tumour of proliferating endothelium. The associated arterial, posterior fossa, cardiac, ocular and ventral midline anomalies are thought to reflect a field defect arising during early fetal development of the head and upper thorax; the cause is not fully understood, most cases are sporadic, and a striking female predominance is observed.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated segmental infantile haemangioma without extracutaneous involvement","Congenital haemangioma (rapidly involuting or non-involuting, GLUT1-negative)","Sturge-Weber syndrome (capillary malformation in a trigeminal distribution)","LUMBAR/PELVIS syndrome (segmental haemangioma of the lower body)","Capillary malformation of a port-wine type","Kaposiform haemangioendothelioma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI and MRA of the brain, head and neck to define posterior fossa and cerebrovascular anomalies","Echocardiography with dedicated imaging of the aortic arch to detect coarctation and arch anomalies","Detailed ophthalmological examination for posterior segment and optic anomalies","Clinical examination of the sternum and abdomen for ventral midline defects","Laryngoscopy or airway assessment when a beard-distribution lesion or stridor is present","Developmental and neurological follow-up including hearing assessment"],"redFlags":["Acute focal neurological deficit suggesting arterial ischaemic stroke from cerebral arteriopathy","Signs of aortic coarctation or heart failure requiring urgent cardiology input","Stridor or respiratory distress indicating airway haemangioma","Progressive visual compromise from orbital or posterior segment involvement","Feeding or swallowing difficulty and failure to thrive"],"prognosis":"The cutaneous haemangioma usually proliferates then involutes, but long-term outcome is dominated by the extracutaneous anomalies; cerebral arteriopathy carries a lifelong stroke risk, and cardiac, ocular and neurodevelopmental problems may cause lasting morbidity. Coordinated multidisciplinary surveillance is essential.","pitfalls":["Treating a large segmental facial haemangioma as isolated and omitting brain, arch and eye imaging","Starting beta-blocker therapy without first excluding significant arteriopathy or coarctation that alters cerebral perfusion","Attributing later neurological events solely to the haemangioma rather than the underlying arteriopathy","Overlooking subtle ventral midline defects that complete the PHACES spectrum"],"management":["Oral propranolol as first-line therapy for the segmental infantile haemangioma, started cautiously after cardiac and cerebrovascular assessment given the arterial anomaly and stroke risk","Baseline MRI and MRA of the brain, head and neck with echocardiography and dedicated aortic arch imaging to define associated anomalies before treatment","Stroke-risk mitigation for cerebral arteriopathy with careful blood-pressure and perfusion management and avoidance of abrupt haemodynamic change","Airway management for subglottic beard-distribution haemangioma causing stridor, using systemic therapy and airway intervention as needed","Ophthalmology-directed treatment with corneal and visual protection for ocular involvement","Cardiology management of coarctation and arch anomalies, including surgical repair where indicated","Long-term neurodevelopmental and hearing follow-up coordinated across dermatology, neurology, cardiology and ophthalmology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ISSVA Classification of Vascular Anomalies","Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies: Haemangiomas and Malformations"],"crossLinks":[]},"phacodonesis":{"name":"phacodonesis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lens tremulousness","wobbling lens"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / AAO Basic and Clinical Science Course","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Phacodonesis is a tremulous, wobbling movement of the crystalline lens seen on ocular saccades, signifying loss of zonular integrity and impending or partial lens subluxation","It is a clinical sign rather than a diagnosis, and its presence should trigger a search for the underlying cause of zonular weakness","In adults the commonest cause is pseudoexfoliation syndrome, in which fibrillar deposits progressively degrade the zonular attachments","Blunt or penetrating ocular trauma is a frequent cause and may produce localised zonular dialysis with sectoral phacodonesis","Heritable connective-tissue disorders are key associations, notably Marfan syndrome (fibrillin-1), homocystinuria and Weill-Marchesani syndrome, and their systemic features must be sought","It is best elicited at the slit lamp by asking the patient to make quick eye movements and observing oscillation of the lens, often accompanied by iridodonesis (tremulous iris)","Phacodonesis markedly increases the risk of intra-operative complications such as zonular dialysis, capsular instability and dropped lens fragments, so it must be documented before cataract surgery","Progression to frank lens subluxation or complete dislocation into the anterior chamber or vitreous can precipitate pupillary-block or lens-induced glaucoma"],"pathophysiology":"The crystalline lens is suspended by the ciliary zonule, a system of fibrillin-rich microfibrils running from the ciliary body to the lens capsule that holds the lens centred and taut. Phacodonesis results when these fibres are reduced in number, stretched or disrupted, so the lens is inadequately anchored and oscillates with inertial movement of the eye. In pseudoexfoliation, abnormal fibrillar material and enzymatic degradation weaken the zonular insertions. In Marfan syndrome, mutations in the fibrillin-1 gene yield structurally deficient microfibrils; in homocystinuria, accumulated homocysteine disrupts zonular cross-linking; and trauma mechanically avulses zonular fibres. The common endpoint is a loss of stable zonular support that permits abnormal lens mobility and predisposes to subluxation.","additionalDifferentials":["Pseudoexfoliation syndrome with zonular laxity","Ectopia lentis of Marfan syndrome","Homocystinuria with lens dislocation","Weill-Marchesani syndrome and other microspherophakia-associated laxity","Traumatic zonular dialysis","High axial myopia or buphthalmos with stretched zonules","Zonular weakness after prior vitreoretinal surgery"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated slit-lamp examination to grade lens movement, zonular gaps and any subluxation","Gonioscopy and intraocular pressure measurement to assess angle status and glaucoma risk","Ultrasound biomicroscopy to image the zonule, ciliary body and degree of lens decentration","Search for pseudoexfoliation material on the pupillary margin and anterior capsule","Plasma and urine homocysteine and amino-acid studies where homocystinuria is suspected","Systemic evaluation and echocardiography for Marfan syndrome and related connective-tissue disease","Genetic testing (for example FBN1) guided by the clinical phenotype"],"redFlags":["Acute lens-induced or pupillary-block angle closure with pain and pressure rise","Complete lens dislocation into the anterior chamber threatening corneal decompensation","Features of Marfan syndrome warranting cardiovascular assessment for aortic root dilatation","Homocystinuria with its risk of thromboembolism","Sudden change from lens tremulousness to frank dislocation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause and on early recognition; with careful surgical planning using capsular support devices, good visual outcomes are achievable, though the presence of phacodonesis raises the risk of intra-operative and postoperative complications. Untreated progression to dislocation and secondary glaucoma can threaten sight and, in syndromic causes, systemic morbidity may dominate.","pitfalls":["Overlooking subtle phacodonesis and encountering unexpected zonular dialysis during cataract surgery","Failing to identify pseudoexfoliation as the underlying cause in an older patient","Missing a systemic diagnosis such as Marfan syndrome or homocystinuria with life-threatening implications","Not measuring intraocular pressure and assessing the angle in a mobile lens","Assuming a stable lens when only iridodonesis is noted without testing lens movement"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying cause of zonular weakness, whether pseudoexfoliation, heritable connective-tissue disease or trauma","Lens surgery using capsular support devices such as capsular tension rings and iris or capsule retractors, with careful planning for the fragile zonule","Intraocular pressure monitoring and pressure-lowering therapy for the associated glaucoma risk","Surgical management of lens subluxation or dislocation, including lensectomy with scleral- or iris-fixated intraocular lens where capsular support is inadequate","Where homocystinuria is identified, dietary methionine restriction with pyridoxine and betaine to reduce thromboembolic and further zonular risk","Systemic evaluation and echocardiography for Marfan syndrome with cardiovascular surveillance and genetic counselling for heritable connective-tissue disease","Referral to a specialist anterior-segment surgeon, with counselling on trauma avoidance and monitoring of the fellow eye"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course (Lens and Cataract)"],"crossLinks":[]},"phacolytic_glaucoma":{"name":"phacolytic glaucoma","system":"ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lens protein glaucoma","phacolytic open-angle glaucoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs with a hypermature or mature cataract, typically in an elderly patient with long-standing poor vision in the affected eye.","Presents acutely with a very high intraocular pressure, pain, redness and corneal oedema, mimicking acute angle closure despite an open angle.","The anterior chamber shows a characteristic pseudohypopyon or iridescent, floating particles representing macrophages laden with lens protein and calcium oxalate crystals.","Gonioscopy reveals an open angle, distinguishing it from angle-closure glaucoma.","Unlike phacoanaphylactic (lens-induced uveitic) glaucoma, the inflammation is a macrophage response to soluble high-molecular-weight lens proteins rather than a granulomatous reaction.","Aqueous aspirate cytology shows swollen macrophages engorged with eosinophilic lens material.","Definitive treatment is prompt cataract extraction after medical control of pressure and inflammation.","Delay risks permanent optic nerve damage from sustained high pressure, so surgery should not be unduly postponed."],"pathophysiology":"In a hypermature or morgagnian cataract the lens capsule becomes permeable, and soluble high-molecular-weight lens proteins leak through microscopic capsular defects into the aqueous humour. These proteins, together with macrophages that engulf them, physically obstruct the trabecular meshwork. The engorged macrophages and the liberated protein and calcium oxalate material clog outflow channels, and the sheer volume of high-molecular-weight protein directly impedes aqueous drainage. Because the angle remains anatomically open, the obstruction is at the level of the meshwork rather than from iridocorneal apposition. The rapid rise in intraocular pressure produces an acute painful glaucoma. The process is a phacotoxic macrophage reaction rather than an immune granulomatous response to lens antigen.","additionalDifferentials":["acute primary angle-closure glaucoma","phacomorphic glaucoma","phacoanaphylactic (phacoantigenic) uveitis and glaucoma","neovascular glaucoma","acute anterior uveitis with secondary glaucoma","endophthalmitis","lens particle glaucoma following capsular rupture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["slit-lamp examination for hypermature cataract, pseudohypopyon and iridescent particles","gonioscopy confirming an open angle","intraocular pressure measurement","B-scan ultrasonography to assess the posterior segment when the fundus is obscured","aqueous aspirate cytology showing protein-laden macrophages when the diagnosis is uncertain","corneal assessment for oedema and endothelial status before surgery","biometry of the fellow eye to aid surgical planning"],"redFlags":["acute painful eye with very high intraocular pressure and corneal oedema","rapidly progressive optic nerve compromise from sustained pressure","signs suggesting endophthalmitis rather than sterile lens-induced inflammation","an only eye or bilateral hypermature cataracts requiring urgent intervention"],"prognosis":"Prompt cataract extraction usually normalises intraocular pressure and the glaucoma resolves without long-term medication, though final vision depends on the health of the optic nerve and macula after any period of sustained high pressure.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the presentation for acute angle closure and performing iridotomy instead of addressing the lens.","Confusing the pseudohypopyon with infective endophthalmitis and delaying appropriate surgery.","Attempting prolonged medical control rather than proceeding to timely lens removal.","Underestimating capsular fragility, which increases the risk of intraoperative capsular rupture and vitreous loss.","Neglecting to counsel about guarded visual prognosis when the cataract is long-standing and the optic nerve status is unknown."],"management":["Definitive treatment by prompt cataract extraction to remove the leaking hypermature lens and its liberated protein","Immediate medical intraocular pressure reduction with topical aqueous suppressants such as beta-blockers, alpha-agonists and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, plus systemic acetazolamide and hyperosmotic agents before surgery","Topical corticosteroids to control the associated macrophage-driven inflammation","Corneal and endothelial assessment to optimise the timing and safety of surgery when there is corneal oedema","Intraocular lens implantation at the time of cataract surgery once inflammation and pressure are controlled","Postoperative monitoring of intraocular pressure and the optic nerve, as the glaucoma usually resolves without long-term medication","Referral to an ophthalmic surgeon, with assessment and planned management of the fellow eye cataract"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Lens and Cataract","Shields Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"phacomatosis_pigmentokeratotica":{"name":"Phacomatosis pigmentokeratotica","system":"Dermatology / Neurocutaneous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPK","phakomatosis pigmentokeratotica","naevus spilus with sebaceous naevus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Neurocutaneous Disorders (current reference texts)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the coexistence of a papular naevus spilus (speckled lentiginous naevus) with an organoid naevus sebaceus or epidermal naevus in the same patient","Regarded as an example of twin spotting (didymosis), where two mosaic clones arise from a single post-zygotic event","Post-zygotic activating HRAS variants have been identified, linking it to the naevus sebaceus and Schimmelpenning spectrum","Extracutaneous features may include neurological abnormalities such as seizures and developmental delay","Ocular and skeletal anomalies, and segmental sensory or motor disturbance (dysaesthesia, muscular weakness) may accompany the cutaneous lesions","Hemihypertrophy and hypophosphataemic (vitamin-D-resistant) rickets are recognised systemic associations","Management is generic and multidisciplinary: surveillance of the naevi, antiseizure therapy where needed, and correction of any hypophosphataemic rickets"],"pathophysiology":"Phacomatosis pigmentokeratotica is understood as a form of twin spotting, in which a single post-zygotic somatic recombination event produces two genetically distinct daughter clones side by side, one giving rise to a pigmentary papular naevus spilus and the other to an organoid naevus sebaceus or epidermal naevus. Activating HRAS variants in the RAS-MAPK pathway have been implicated, which is why the condition overlaps mechanistically with naevus sebaceus and Schimmelpenning syndrome. When the mosaic clones also involve neuroectodermal, ocular and mesodermal tissue, the associated neurological, ocular and skeletal anomalies, hemihypertrophy and FGF23-mediated hypophosphataemic rickets develop.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated naevus spilus (speckled lentiginous naevus)","Isolated naevus sebaceus","Schimmelpenning syndrome","Phakomatosis pigmentovascularis","Epidermal naevus syndrome","Neurofibromatosis type 1"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin examination and biopsy to characterise both naevus components","MRI of the brain where neurological features are present","Serum phosphate and skeletal survey to detect hypophosphataemic rickets and hemihypertrophy","Ophthalmological assessment for ocular anomalies","Neurological evaluation of any segmental sensory or motor disturbance","Molecular testing of lesional tissue for HRAS variants where available"],"redFlags":["New-onset or refractory seizures","Progressive neurological deficit or segmental weakness","Suspicious change within the melanocytic or organoid naevus","Unexplained bone pain or fractures suggesting hypophosphataemic rickets"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the extent of extracutaneous involvement; limited cutaneous disease has a good outlook, whereas neurological, skeletal or metabolic complications increase morbidity and require ongoing multidisciplinary management.","pitfalls":["Regarding the two naevi as coincidental rather than recognising the twin-spotting syndrome","Missing treatable hypophosphataemic rickets as a cause of skeletal symptoms","Overlooking segmental sensory or motor disturbance as part of the condition","Failing to arrange neuroimaging when neurological features are present"],"management":["No curative therapy exists, so management is directed at the individual manifestations across affected systems","Dermatological management of the naevus sebaceus or epidermal naevus and the naevus spilus, with excision of symptomatic or suspicious lesions given the risk of secondary neoplasms","Correction of FGF23-mediated hypophosphataemic rickets with oral phosphate and active vitamin D analogues, and anti-FGF23 antibody therapy where appropriate, monitoring phosphate and renal function","Antiepileptic therapy and neurological management of seizures and developmental delay","Ophthalmological assessment and treatment of ocular anomalies, and orthopaedic management of skeletal anomalies, hemihypertrophy and segmental sensory or motor disturbance","Multidisciplinary follow-up across dermatology, neurology, endocrinology and ophthalmology","Genetic counselling explaining the sporadic post-zygotic mosaic HRAS basis and generally low recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Happle R. Mosaicism in Human Skin"],"crossLinks":[]},"phacomorphic_glaucoma":{"name":"Phacomorphic glaucoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lens-induced angle-closure glaucoma","intumescent lens glaucoma","phacomorphic angle closure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A secondary angle-closure glaucoma caused by a large, swollen (intumescent) cataractous lens that pushes the iris forward and closes the drainage angle","It typically presents like acute angle closure, with a painful red eye, corneal oedema, a mid-dilated pupil, high pressure, and a shallow anterior chamber","A key clue is an advanced, often mature or intumescent, cataract in the affected eye, frequently with a deeper chamber and open angle in the fellow eye","The swollen lens both increases pupillary block and physically shallows the peripheral angle, so mechanisms overlap","Asymmetry of anterior chamber depth and lens thickness between the two eyes helps distinguish it from primary angle closure, where both eyes tend to be anatomically similar","Initial management lowers pressure medically with topical agents and systemic acetazolamide and controls inflammation, sometimes with laser peripheral iridotomy as a temporising measure","Definitive treatment is removal of the offending lens by cataract surgery once the eye is quietened and pressure reduced","Prolonged high pressure risks optic-nerve damage and permanent synechial closure, so timely lens extraction is important"],"pathophysiology":"Phacomorphic glaucoma occurs when a cataractous lens becomes enlarged and intumescent, swelling with fluid so that it thickens and shifts the iris-lens diaphragm forward. This exaggerates relative pupillary block by increasing lens-iris contact and simultaneously crowds the peripheral angle as the bulky lens pushes the peripheral iris against the trabecular meshwork. Aqueous outflow is obstructed and intraocular pressure rises acutely, producing corneal oedema, pain, and, if sustained, peripheral anterior synechiae and optic-nerve damage. The disparity in lens size and chamber depth compared with the fellow eye reflects the lens as the primary driver, distinguishing it from primary angle closure where both eyes share a predisposing anatomy.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary acute angle-closure glaucoma","Phacolytic glaucoma from a leaking hypermature cataract","Malignant glaucoma (aqueous misdirection)","Neovascular glaucoma","Uveitic glaucoma with a red painful eye","Lens subluxation causing pupillary block"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination documenting an intumescent or mature cataract and shallow chamber","Gonioscopy of both eyes to confirm angle closure and assess the fellow eye","Comparison of anterior chamber depth and lens thickness between eyes, with biometry where possible","Applanation tonometry to quantify the pressure rise","B-scan ultrasonography when the fundus is not visible to exclude posterior pathology","Optic disc assessment for glaucomatous damage once the media clear"],"redFlags":["Acute painful red eye with very high pressure and corneal oedema needing emergency pressure reduction","A mature or intumescent unilateral cataract with unilateral angle closure","Prolonged pressure elevation threatening irreversible optic-nerve damage","Marked asymmetry of chamber depth signalling a lens-driven mechanism"],"prognosis":"Prompt pressure control followed by cataract extraction usually resolves the angle closure and restores vision, but delayed treatment can leave permanent optic-nerve damage, synechial angle closure, and corneal decompensation, and the acutely inflamed eye carries higher surgical risk.","pitfalls":["Treating it as primary angle closure and delaying the definitive lens extraction","Overlooking the diagnosis by not comparing the two eyes for asymmetry","Confusing it with phacolytic glaucoma, which involves lens protein leakage rather than pure angle crowding","Assuming iridotomy alone will resolve pressure when the swollen lens must be removed","Operating on a highly inflamed, high-pressure eye without first medically stabilising it"],"management":["Definitive treatment by cataract extraction to remove the intumescent lens and relieve the angle closure","Initial medical pressure control with topical aqueous suppressants and systemic acetazolamide and hyperosmotic agents","Topical corticosteroids and pupil management to reduce inflammation and the pupillary-block component","Laser peripheral iridotomy as a temporising measure to relieve pupillary block before surgery","Corneal clearing and optimisation before surgery, with intraocular lens implantation once pressure and inflammation are controlled","Postoperative monitoring of intraocular pressure, the drainage angle for peripheral anterior synechiae, and the optic nerve","Ophthalmic surgical referral with assessment of the fellow eye angle and lens"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"phaeohyphomycosis":{"name":"Phaeohyphomycosis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dematiaceous mould infection","dark mould infection","melanised fungal infection","cerebral phaeohyphomycosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases / ECMM-ISHAM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of infections caused by dematiaceous (darkly pigmented, melanised) fungi such as Cladophialophora, Exophiala, Alternaria, Bipolaris, and Curvularia found in soil and plant matter worldwide","The defining feature is the presence of melanin in the fungal cell wall, giving pigmented hyphae in tissue that can be highlighted with a Fontana-Masson stain","Clinical spectrum ranges from subcutaneous nodules and cysts after traumatic inoculation to allergic fungal sinusitis, keratitis, and life-threatening cerebral abscesses","Cerebral phaeohyphomycosis, notably due to Cladophialophora bantiana, is striking because it can cause brain abscesses in apparently immunocompetent hosts and carries high mortality","Immunosuppressed patients, including transplant recipients, are at risk of disseminated and invasive disease","Management combines surgical excision or drainage with azole-based antifungal therapy, often prolonged, and outcomes for cerebral disease remain poor even with treatment","Diagnosis rests on biopsy with histology showing pigmented hyphae, fungal culture, and molecular identification, since species determines prognosis and drug selection"],"pathophysiology":"Phaeohyphomycosis is caused by melanised moulds acquired mainly through traumatic inoculation of contaminated soil or plant material into skin and subcutaneous tissue, or by inhalation leading to sinus and, rarely, cerebral disease. Melanin in the fungal cell wall is a virulence factor that scavenges free radicals and blunts host defences, contributing to the neurotropism of certain species. Localised disease produces subcutaneous cysts and nodules, whereas neurotropic species such as Cladophialophora bantiana can reach the brain and form abscesses even without evident immunosuppression; in immunocompromised hosts the fungi may invade tissue and disseminate more widely.","additionalDifferentials":["Chromoblastomycosis (also caused by dematiaceous fungi but with distinct sclerotic bodies)","Eumycetoma from other agents","Bacterial or nocardial brain abscess","Invasive aspergillosis or other hyaline mould infection","Sporotrichosis or other subcutaneous mycoses","Epidermoid or other benign subcutaneous cysts"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Tissue biopsy for histopathology, including Fontana-Masson stain to demonstrate melanised hyphae","Fungal culture with molecular species identification","Antifungal susceptibility testing to guide therapy","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain for suspected cerebral abscess","Computed tomography of the sinuses in suspected fungal sinusitis","Assessment of immune status in disseminated or invasive disease"],"redFlags":["New focal neurological signs or a brain abscess on imaging","Disseminated disease in an immunocompromised host","Rapidly enlarging or invasive sinus disease","Isolation of a known neurotropic species such as Cladophialophora bantiana","Progressive lesions despite antifungal therapy, indicating the need for surgical intervention"],"prognosis":"Localised subcutaneous disease often responds well to excision and antifungal therapy, but cerebral phaeohyphomycosis carries a high mortality even with combined surgical and medical treatment, and disseminated disease in immunocompromised hosts also has a poor outlook.","pitfalls":["Failing to request a melanin stain, so pigmented hyphae are overlooked on histology","Assuming cerebral abscess requires immunosuppression and dismissing it in an immunocompetent patient","Relying on antifungals alone when surgical excision or drainage is often essential","Not obtaining species identification, which strongly influences prognosis and drug choice","Confusing it with chromoblastomycosis or other subcutaneous mycoses that require different management"],"management":["Prolonged systemic azole antifungal therapy such as voriconazole, itraconazole or posaconazole, guided by species identification and susceptibility testing","Surgical excision or drainage of localised subcutaneous nodules and cysts, and neurosurgical resection of cerebral abscesses, which is central to outcome","Combination antifungal therapy adding amphotericin B and flucytosine for cerebral or disseminated disease given the high mortality","Reduction of immunosuppression where feasible in immunocompromised hosts to aid fungal clearance","Neuroimaging and clinical monitoring of treatment response with prolonged follow-up for relapse","Assessment and optimisation of immune status in invasive or disseminated disease","Referral to infectious diseases and, for cerebral or sinus disease, to neurosurgery or ENT"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ECMM-ISHAM global guideline for the diagnosis and management of rare mould infections"],"crossLinks":[]},"phakomatosis_pigmentovascularis":{"name":"Phakomatosis pigmentovascularis","system":"Dermatology / Neurocutaneous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPV","phacomatosis pigmentovascularis","pigmentovascular phacomatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Neurocutaneous Disorders (current reference texts)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the coexistence of a capillary malformation (naevus flammeus) with a pigmentary lesion such as dermal melanocytosis or naevus spilus in the same patient","Reflects twin spotting (didymosis), where two distinct mosaic clones arise from post-zygotic recombination, giving overlapping vascular and pigmentary birthmarks","Somatic activating variants in GNAQ and GNA11 have been implicated, linking it mechanistically to Sturge-Weber and phakomatosis pigmentokeratotica","The current classification groups cases by vascular-plus-pigmentary combination and by the presence or absence of systemic involvement","Extensive facial capillary malformation raises the risk of associated Sturge-Weber syndrome and Klippel-Trenaunay overgrowth","Ocular melanocytosis (naevus of Ota) predisposes to glaucoma and warrants ophthalmological surveillance","Management is generic: pulsed-dye laser for the vascular component, Q-switched laser for pigment, and screening for ocular and neurological complications"],"pathophysiology":"Phakomatosis pigmentovascularis is best understood as an example of twin spotting, in which post-zygotic somatic recombination or independent mosaic variants generate two genetically distinct cell populations that produce adjacent vascular and pigmentary birthmarks. Activating variants in the GNAQ and GNA11 genes, which encode G-protein alpha subunits, have been identified and account for the overlap with other GNAQ-driven conditions such as Sturge-Weber syndrome. The capillary malformation reflects dysregulated vascular development, while the coexisting dermal melanocytosis or naevus spilus reflects abnormal migration and persistence of dermal melanocytes. Where the mosaic clones involve leptomeningeal or ocular tissue, neurological and ocular complications ensue.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated naevus flammeus (port-wine stain)","Sturge-Weber syndrome","Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome","Isolated dermal melanocytosis (Mongolian spot, naevus of Ota)","Naevus spilus without vascular component","Cutis marmorata telangiectatica congenita"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full skin examination to document vascular and pigmentary components","MRI and MRA of the brain where leptomeningeal involvement or Sturge-Weber is suspected","Ophthalmological assessment including intraocular pressure for ocular melanocytosis and glaucoma","Limb imaging and measurement where overgrowth suggests Klippel-Trenaunay","Skin biopsy in atypical or diagnostically uncertain lesions","Molecular testing of affected tissue for GNAQ/GNA11 variants where available"],"redFlags":["Seizures or focal neurological signs suggesting leptomeningeal angiomatosis","Raised intraocular pressure or visual symptoms from ocular melanocytosis","Progressive limb overgrowth or venous complications","Any change within a melanocytic lesion raising concern for melanoma"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on associated systemic disease; purely cutaneous phakomatosis pigmentovascularis is benign and mainly a cosmetic concern, whereas coexisting Sturge-Weber, glaucoma or Klippel-Trenaunay overgrowth carries the associated morbidity of those conditions.","pitfalls":["Treating the birthmarks cosmetically without screening for Sturge-Weber, glaucoma or limb overgrowth","Missing ocular melanocytosis and its lifelong glaucoma risk","Attributing all vascular lesions to a simple port-wine stain and overlooking the pigmentary component","Failing to arrange neuroimaging in extensive facial capillary malformation"],"management":["No specific systemic therapy exists, so treatment targets the individual vascular and pigmentary components and any systemic involvement","Pulsed-dye laser treatment of the capillary malformation for cosmetically significant naevus flammeus","Laser therapy for dermal melanocytosis or naevus of Ota as indicated","Ophthalmological surveillance with intraocular pressure monitoring and glaucoma treatment where ocular melanocytosis is present","Neurological assessment with brain MRI and MRA and antiepileptic therapy where Sturge-Weber leptomeningeal involvement is suspected","Assessment and management of Klippel-Trenaunay-type overgrowth with limb imaging, compression and orthopaedic input","Multidisciplinary follow-up across dermatology, ophthalmology and neurology, with genetic counselling noting the sporadic mosaic GNAQ and GNA11 basis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Happle R. Mosaicism in Human Skin"],"crossLinks":[]},"phantom_limb_pain":{"name":"Phantom limb pain","system":"Rehabilitation / Pain","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLP","phantom pain","post-amputation pain"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braddom's Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation / pain medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Phantom limb pain is painful sensation perceived in a limb or body part that has been amputated, and must be distinguished from non-painful phantom sensation and from residual limb (stump) pain","Most amputees experience some phantom phenomena; pain often begins within the first days to weeks after amputation and may follow a telescoping pattern where the phantom seems to shorten over time","Pre-amputation limb pain and its intensity are recognised risk factors, and the phantom pain may echo the character of the pain felt before surgery","Careful examination of the residual limb is essential to separate phantom pain from stump pain caused by neuroma, poor prosthetic fit, infection, or ischaemia","Non-pharmacological approaches including mirror therapy, graded motor imagery, desensitisation, and early prosthetic use address the cortical reorganisation thought to drive the pain","Pharmacological principles favour neuropathic agents such as gabapentinoids and certain antidepressants, with opioids reserved and used cautiously","A multidisciplinary rehabilitation approach that combines prosthetic training, psychological support, and pain management gives the best functional outcomes","Perioperative and preventive analgesic strategies may reduce the likelihood or severity of later phantom pain"],"pathophysiology":"Phantom limb pain arises from maladaptive changes at multiple levels of the nervous system after amputation. Peripherally, injured axons form neuromas that generate ectopic and spontaneous discharge. In the spinal cord, deafferentation leads to central sensitisation and disinhibition of dorsal horn neurons. Within the brain, the somatosensory and motor cortices undergo reorganisation, with cortical territory representing the missing limb being invaded by adjacent regions; the degree of this remapping correlates with pain intensity. Psychological factors and pre-existing pain memory further modulate the experience.","additionalDifferentials":["Residual limb (stump) pain from neuroma","Prosthetic-related mechanical pain or pressure injury","Residual limb infection or wound breakdown","Ischaemia of the residual limb","Referred pain from the spine or proximal joints","Heterotopic ossification in the residual limb"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical history and examination distinguishing phantom pain from stump pain and non-painful phantom sensation","Inspection and palpation of the residual limb for neuroma, tenderness, and prosthetic fit","Imaging of the residual limb where neuroma, heterotopic ossification, or bony spur is suspected","Vascular assessment if ischaemia of the residual limb is a concern","Assessment for infection when there are local inflammatory signs or wound problems"],"redFlags":["Residual limb signs of infection or wound dehiscence requiring urgent treatment","Features of critical ischaemia in the residual limb","Rapidly worsening or new pain pattern suggesting an alternative surgical or vascular cause","Severe intractable pain with mood deterioration and suicidal ideation"],"prognosis":"Phantom pain often diminishes in frequency and intensity over the months to years following amputation, and many patients cope well with a combination of therapy and prosthetic use. A subset develop persistent, treatment-resistant pain that requires ongoing multidisciplinary management.","pitfalls":["Confusing phantom pain with stump pain and missing a treatable neuroma or prosthetic-fit problem","Neglecting early prosthetic fitting and rehabilitation, which can worsen cortical reorganisation","Dismissing patient reports of pain in an absent limb as psychological","Over-reliance on opioids for a fundamentally neuropathic pain problem","Overlooking pre-amputation pain control that could reduce later phantom pain"],"management":["Non-pharmacological cortical retraining with mirror therapy, graded motor imagery, desensitisation and early prosthetic use to counter maladaptive cortical reorganisation","Neuropathic pharmacotherapy with gabapentinoids and antidepressants such as tricyclics or SNRIs, with opioids reserved and used cautiously","Optimisation of the residual limb and prosthetic fit, with treatment of stump pathology such as neuroma, poor fit, infection or ischaemia that mimics or aggravates the pain","Perioperative and preventive analgesia around amputation, addressing pre-amputation pain as a recognised risk factor","Interventional options for refractory pain including nerve blocks, targeted muscle reinnervation and neuromodulation or spinal cord stimulation","Psychological support such as cognitive behavioural therapy for the affective component and pain-related distress","Coordinated rehabilitation medicine and pain-team care for persistent, treatment-resistant pain"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braddom's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation"],"crossLinks":[]},"phelan_mcdermid_syndrome":{"name":"Phelan-McDermid syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["22q13 deletion syndrome","SHANK3-related disorder","PMS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A neurodevelopmental disorder with global developmental delay, intellectual disability, and severely delayed or absent expressive speech","Neonatal hypotonia, autism spectrum disorder, and reduced perception of pain or altered sensory responses are characteristic","Minor dysmorphic features include a long face, full brow, dolichocephaly, large fleshy hands, and dysplastic toenails, but these can be subtle","Caused by deletion of the distal long arm of chromosome 22 (22q13.3) or by pathogenic SHANK3 variants, since loss of SHANK3 drives the core neurological phenotype","Most cases are de novo, but a proportion arise from an unbalanced translocation, so parental karyotype or array is important for recurrence-risk counselling","Regression, including loss of skills and psychiatric features such as bipolar-type illness or catatonia, can occur in adolescence or adulthood","Diagnosis relies on chromosomal microarray to size the deletion, with sequencing to detect intragenic SHANK3 variants when the array is normal"],"pathophysiology":"Phelan-McDermid syndrome results from loss of functional SHANK3 at chromosome 22q13.3, either through a terminal deletion of varying size or a pathogenic sequence variant. SHANK3 encodes a scaffolding protein at the postsynaptic density of excitatory synapses that organises glutamatergic signalling, so its haploinsufficiency impairs synapse formation and function, producing hypotonia, developmental delay, absent speech, and autistic features. Larger deletions remove additional genes, contributing to more extensive systemic and dysmorphic features and greater phenotypic severity.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic autism spectrum disorder","Angelman syndrome","Pitt-Hopkins syndrome","Rett syndrome and MECP2-related disorders","Kleefstra syndrome","Other 22q microdeletion or duplication disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal microarray to detect and size the 22q13.3 deletion","SHANK3 sequencing when microarray is normal but the phenotype is suggestive","Parental chromosome/array studies to identify a balanced translocation and refine recurrence risk","Developmental, speech, and autism assessments","Renal ultrasound and echocardiography for associated structural anomalies","Neurological review and, where regression occurs, psychiatric assessment"],"redFlags":["Developmental or behavioural regression, or new catatonia or psychiatric illness in adolescence or adulthood","New seizures requiring evaluation","Absent pain perception masking serious injury or intra-abdominal pathology","Significant renal or cardiac anomaly identified on screening"],"prognosis":"Developmental disability and speech impairment are lifelong and most individuals need substantial support, while the risk of adolescent or adult regression and psychiatric illness adds long-term uncertainty. Larger deletions tend to correlate with greater severity, and multidisciplinary care improves quality of life.","pitfalls":["Labelling the presentation as idiopathic autism without performing microarray and SHANK3 testing","Missing a parental balanced translocation and giving incorrect recurrence-risk advice","Overlooking reduced pain perception, which can delay recognition of injury or illness","Not anticipating and monitoring for regression and psychiatric change in later life"],"management":["Early developmental intervention with speech and language therapy including augmentative and alternative communication, physiotherapy and occupational therapy for hypotonia, delay and absent speech","Educational and behavioural support tailored to intellectual disability and autism spectrum disorder","Surveillance for and management of adolescent or adult regression, including psychiatric assessment and treatment of bipolar-type illness or catatonia","Screening for associated structural anomalies with renal ultrasound and echocardiography, and management of any detected","Monitoring for altered pain perception with attention to its safety implications, and management of seizures where present","Clinical genetics referral with chromosomal microarray and SHANK3 testing plus parental studies to define recurrence risk and provide family counselling","Coordinated multidisciplinary follow-up across paediatrics, neurology, genetics and allied health"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Phelan-McDermid Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"phenylketonuria":{"name":"Phenylketonuria","system":"Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PKU","phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency","hyperphenylalaninaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive inborn error of amino acid metabolism caused most often by deficiency of the enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase.","Untreated, accumulation of phenylalanine causes progressive and irreversible intellectual disability, seizures, and behavioural problems.","Affected infants are typically normal at birth, so detection relies on newborn screening rather than clinical signs.","Classic untreated features can include a musty or mousy body odour, eczema, and reduced pigmentation with fair hair and skin.","Early diagnosis through screening and prompt dietary treatment prevent the neurological damage almost entirely.","Treatment centres on a lifelong low-phenylalanine diet with a special protein substitute, guided by regular monitoring of blood phenylalanine levels.","A subset of patients respond to the cofactor tetrahydrobiopterin (sapropterin), and disorders of biopterin metabolism must be excluded as a separate cause of high phenylalanine.","Maternal PKU with poorly controlled phenylalanine during pregnancy can damage the fetus, causing microcephaly, congenital heart disease, and developmental delay, so strict control is essential before and during pregnancy.","Lifelong follow-up in a metabolic service supports adherence and long-term neurocognitive outcomes."],"pathophysiology":"Deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase (or, less commonly, of its tetrahydrobiopterin cofactor) blocks the conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine. Phenylalanine therefore accumulates in blood and tissues and is diverted to alternative metabolites such as phenylketones. High phenylalanine levels are neurotoxic to the developing brain, interfering with amino acid transport across the blood-brain barrier, neurotransmitter synthesis, and myelination, producing the intellectual disability and neurological features of untreated disease. Reduced tyrosine availability also limits synthesis of melanin, contributing to hypopigmentation.","additionalDifferentials":["Tetrahydrobiopterin (cofactor) deficiency disorders","Transient hyperphenylalaninaemia of the newborn","Other inborn errors of metabolism presenting with developmental delay","Non-metabolic causes of intellectual disability and seizures","Tyrosinaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Newborn bloodspot screening for elevated phenylalanine","Quantitative plasma amino acids (phenylalanine and tyrosine)","Tetrahydrobiopterin loading test and pterin studies to exclude cofactor defects","Dihydropteridine reductase activity measurement","Genetic analysis of the PAH gene","Serial blood phenylalanine monitoring to guide dietary treatment"],"redFlags":["Markedly elevated phenylalanine levels requiring urgent dietary intervention","Seizures or neurological deterioration in an untreated infant","Poor metabolic control during pregnancy (maternal PKU) risking fetal harm","Failure of phenylalanine to fall despite dietary treatment, suggesting a cofactor defect","Signs of nutritional deficiency from an overly restricted diet"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and consistent dietary control, cognitive development is normal or near-normal; delayed treatment or poor lifelong control leads to irreversible neurocognitive impairment, and maternal control critically affects offspring.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal newborn appearance excludes the diagnosis","Failing to exclude tetrahydrobiopterin deficiency as a treatable alternative cause","Relaxing dietary control in older children and adults, risking cognitive and neuropsychiatric effects","Overlooking the need for strict preconception and pregnancy control in affected mothers"],"management":["Lifelong low-phenylalanine diet with a phenylalanine-free amino-acid protein substitute as the cornerstone of treatment","Regular monitoring of blood phenylalanine to keep levels within the target therapeutic range and guide dietary adjustment","Sapropterin, a tetrahydrobiopterin analogue, for cofactor-responsive patients to increase phenylalanine tolerance","Enzyme substitution therapy with pegvaliase for selected adults with inadequate dietary control","Ensuring nutritional adequacy of tyrosine, vitamin B12, iron and micronutrients with growth monitoring on the restricted diet","Preconception counselling for women to establish strict control before pregnancy and prevent maternal phenylketonuria embryopathy","Specialist metabolic team and dietitian follow-up, with genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive PAH disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"phenylketonuria_maternal":{"name":"phenylketonuria maternal","system":"metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["maternal PKU syndrome","maternal hyperphenylalaninaemia embryopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics; Scriver's The Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Maternal PKU syndrome is a teratogenic embryopathy: the fetus is harmed by transplacental phenylalanine from a mother with poorly controlled phenylketonuria, not by an inherited enzyme defect in the child, who is usually a heterozygous carrier and biochemically normal.","The classic tetrad in the offspring is microcephaly, congenital heart disease, intrauterine and postnatal growth restriction, and intellectual disability, often with a characteristic facial appearance resembling foetal alcohol syndrome.","Phenylalanine is actively concentrated across the placenta, so fetal levels exceed maternal levels; the threshold for damage is lower than that for classical PKU brain injury, meaning even mildly elevated maternal levels can be teratogenic.","Congenital heart defects, particularly coarctation of the aorta, tetralogy of Fallot and other conotruncal and left-sided lesions, arise from first-trimester exposure, so metabolic control must be achieved BEFORE conception, not merely once pregnancy is recognised.","Preconception counselling and reinstitution of a strict phenylalanine-restricted diet with medical protein (phenylalanine-free amino acid mixture) is the cornerstone; many adult women previously told they could relax diet must resume it well before attempting to conceive.","Adherence is a major problem because many women diagnosed by newborn screening discontinued diet in childhood ('diet for life' was not historically advised) and find the restricted diet difficult; structured multidisciplinary metabolic and obstetric care improves outcomes.","Tyrosine becomes conditionally essential and must be supplemented, and tetrahydrobiopterin (sapropterin) responsiveness should be assessed as it can aid control in selected women; overtreatment causing maternal phenylalanine deficiency also harms the fetus.","Because affected offspring do not have PKU themselves, a normal newborn screen in the baby does not exclude maternal PKU embryopathy; the diagnosis rests on maternal history and biochemistry."],"pathophysiology":"Phenylketonuria results from deficiency of hepatic phenylalanine hydroxylase (or, less commonly, of its tetrahydrobiopterin cofactor), causing accumulation of phenylalanine. In pregnancy, phenylalanine is actively transported across the placenta and concentrated in the fetal compartment, so an inadequately treated affected mother exposes the developing embryo and fetus to sustained hyperphenylalaninaemia. Excess phenylalanine is directly neurotoxic and disrupts organogenesis: it impairs neuronal proliferation and migration and interferes with the metabolism of other large neutral amino acids and neurotransmitter precursors. First-trimester exposure disturbs cardiac neural crest and outflow-tract development, producing congenital heart disease, while ongoing exposure impairs brain growth and myelination, yielding microcephaly and intellectual disability, and restricts somatic growth. The severity of fetal damage correlates closely with the level and duration of maternal blood phenylalanine, especially periconceptional and first-trimester control; the fetus is affected regardless of its own genotype, being typically an unaffected carrier.","additionalDifferentials":["foetal alcohol spectrum disorder (overlapping facies, microcephaly, growth restriction and cardiac defects)","congenital infections of the TORCH group causing microcephaly and growth restriction","other teratogen embryopathies (anticonvulsants such as valproate, warfarin, retinoids)","chromosomal and single-gene microcephaly-cardiac syndromes","primary (non-maternal) inherited metabolic disease in the infant","constitutional or placental-insufficiency intrauterine growth restriction","tetrahydrobiopterin cofactor disorders in the mother mimicking classical PKU control problems"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["serial maternal blood phenylalanine and tyrosine monitoring before and throughout pregnancy","confirmation of maternal PKU genotype/phenotype and tetrahydrobiopterin (sapropterin) responsiveness testing","detailed fetal anomaly ultrasound and fetal echocardiography","serial fetal growth scans and monitoring of head circumference","maternal nutritional assessment including protein, vitamin B12, iron and micronutrient status on restricted diet","paediatric developmental and cardiac assessment of the neonate","exclusion of foetal alcohol exposure and congenital infection where the phenotype overlaps"],"redFlags":["unplanned pregnancy or conception occurring while maternal phenylalanine is poorly controlled","persistently elevated maternal blood phenylalanine during the first trimester","fetal microcephaly, growth restriction or a structural cardiac anomaly detected on antenatal imaging","maternal phenylalanine driven too low, risking amino-acid deficiency and fetal catabolic harm","loss to follow-up of a woman of childbearing age with known PKU no longer on diet"],"prognosis":"When strict maternal metabolic control is achieved before conception and maintained throughout pregnancy, the risk of the embryopathy falls markedly and offspring outcomes approach those of the general population. Poor periconceptional and first-trimester control carries high rates of microcephaly, congenital heart disease, growth restriction and irreversible intellectual disability in the child.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal newborn screen in the baby excludes harm; the offspring is usually an unaffected carrier yet may be severely damaged by intrauterine phenylalanine exposure.","Achieving metabolic control only after pregnancy is confirmed, by which time cardiac organogenesis and early neurodevelopment have already occurred.","Allowing women diagnosed in infancy to remain off diet without preconception planning, on the outdated view that diet can be stopped after childhood.","Overzealous restriction causing maternal phenylalanine deficiency, which itself impairs fetal growth and development.","Neglecting tyrosine and micronutrient supplementation and dietetic support, leading to maternal malnutrition on the restricted diet."],"management":["Preconception reinstitution of a strict phenylalanine-restricted diet with a phenylalanine-free amino-acid substitute, achieving target maternal levels before conception rather than after pregnancy is recognised","Frequent maternal blood phenylalanine and tyrosine monitoring before and throughout pregnancy with dietary titration","Sapropterin, a tetrahydrobiopterin analogue, in documented responders as an adjunct to dietary control","Maternal nutritional support ensuring adequate protein, tyrosine, vitamin B12, iron and micronutrients on the restricted diet","Detailed foetal anomaly ultrasound, foetal echocardiography and serial growth and head-circumference scans to detect embryopathy such as congenital heart disease, microcephaly and growth restriction","Neonatal paediatric developmental and cardiac assessment after delivery","Structured multidisciplinary metabolic and obstetric care with adherence support and genetic counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, current edition","Scriver's The Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","van Wegberg et al. European guidelines on the diagnosis and management of phenylketonuria (Orphanet Journal of Rare Diseases)"],"crossLinks":[]},"phenytoin_hypersensitivity_syndrome":{"name":"Anticonvulsant hypersensitivity syndrome","system":"Clinical pharmacology / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["phenytoin hypersensitivity syndrome","anticonvulsant hypersensitivity syndrome","AHS","DRESS syndrome","drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms","carbamazepine hypersensitivity","HLA-B*15:02","HLA-A*31:01"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe multiorgan drug reaction, part of the DRESS spectrum, classically caused by aromatic anticonvulsants such as phenytoin, carbamazepine and phenobarbital","Onset is typically delayed, often two to eight weeks after starting the drug, distinguishing it from immediate reactions","The triad of fever, widespread rash and internal organ involvement (especially hepatitis) is characteristic, frequently with lymphadenopathy","Blood tests commonly show eosinophilia and atypical lymphocytes","Facial oedema is a useful clinical clue to DRESS","There is cross-reactivity among the aromatic anticonvulsants, so all should generally be avoided once one has caused the syndrome","HLA associations exist (for example HLA-B*15:02 with carbamazepine-related Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis and HLA-A*31:01 with carbamazepine hypersensitivity), supporting pharmacogenomic screening in at-risk groups","Management is prompt drug withdrawal, supportive care and systemic corticosteroids for significant organ involvement"],"pathophysiology":"The syndrome is a delayed, T-cell-mediated drug hypersensitivity reaction. Aromatic anticonvulsants are metabolised by cytochrome P450 to reactive arene oxide intermediates, which are normally detoxified by epoxide hydrolase. Deficient or defective detoxification allows reactive metabolites to accumulate, act as haptens and trigger an immune response, with genetic variation in detoxifying enzymes contributing to susceptibility. Specific HLA alleles predispose to particular reactions by shaping which drug-modified peptides are presented to T cells. Reactivation of human herpesviruses, particularly HHV-6, is often associated with DRESS and may contribute to the prolonged, relapsing course. The immune activation produces fever, an extensive cutaneous eruption, eosinophilia and inflammation of internal organs, most often the liver but also kidneys, lungs and heart.","additionalDifferentials":["Stevens-Johnson syndrome / toxic epidermal necrolysis","Acute generalised exanthematous pustulosis","Viral exanthem or acute viral infection (e.g. EBV, CMV)","Sepsis","Acute cutaneous lupus or Still's disease","Lymphoma or other haematological malignancy","Serum sickness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with differential for eosinophilia and atypical lymphocytes","Liver function tests and renal function to detect organ involvement","Inflammatory markers and temperature charting","Skin biopsy where the diagnosis is uncertain","Screening for viral reactivation (e.g. HHV-6) in appropriate settings","HLA typing (e.g. HLA-B*15:02, HLA-A*31:01) in at-risk populations before prescribing","Thyroid function follow-up given the risk of late autoimmune sequelae"],"redFlags":["Marked transaminitis or evidence of acute liver failure","Extensive rash with mucosal involvement or skin detachment suggesting overlap with SJS/TEN","Renal impairment, myocarditis or pneumonitis","Haemodynamic instability or high persistent fever","Rapidly rising eosinophil count with organ dysfunction"],"prognosis":"With early drug withdrawal and supportive care most patients recover, but the syndrome can be fatal, largely from hepatic or other organ failure. The course may be prolonged and relapsing, and late autoimmune sequelae such as thyroid disease can occur, warranting follow-up.","pitfalls":["Attributing the delayed rash and fever to infection and continuing the anticonvulsant","Switching to another aromatic anticonvulsant with which there is cross-reactivity","Underestimating internal organ involvement because the rash appears mild","Stopping corticosteroids too quickly and precipitating relapse","Not screening at-risk populations for relevant HLA alleles before prescribing"],"management":["Immediate withdrawal of the culprit aromatic anticonvulsant as the single most important step","Systemic corticosteroids for significant multiorgan involvement, especially hepatitis, with a slow taper given the prolonged, relapsing course","Supportive care with temperature control, fluid and skin care, and topical corticosteroids and emollients for the cutaneous eruption","Substitution with a structurally unrelated non-aromatic anticonvulsant, avoiding the cross-reactive aromatic agents phenytoin, carbamazepine and phenobarbital lifelong","Monitoring and management of hepatic, renal and haematological involvement and consideration of herpesvirus reactivation such as HHV-6","HLA screening for alleles such as HLA-B*15:02 and HLA-A*31:01 in at-risk populations before prescribing, with a documented allergy alert and counselling of relatives who may share the alleles","Long-term follow-up for late autoimmune sequelae such as thyroid disease, escalating to intensive care and specialist input for organ failure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pheochromocytoma":{"name":"Pheochromocytoma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pheochromocytoma and paraganglioma","PPGL","Paraganglioma","Chromaffin cell tumor"],"chapter":399,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3073","clinicalPearls":["Pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas (PPGLs) are catecholamine-producing tumors arising from sympathetic (adrenal medulla, sympathetic trunk) or parasympathetic (carotid body, glomus tympanicum/jugulare/vagale) paraganglia; by WHO usage pheochromocytoma denotes the adrenal tumor and paraganglioma denotes tumors at all other sites.","The classic triad is episodic palpitations, headache, and profuse sweating, present in roughly one-third of patients; the combination of this triad with hypertension makes pheochromocytoma a likely diagnosis, though tumors can remain asymptomatic for years.","Hypertension is the dominant sign and may be paroxysmal (classic) or sustained (also common); paroxysms generally last under one hour.","The classic 'rule of tens' states that about 10% are bilateral, 10% extra-adrenal, and 10% metastatic.","Diagnosis rests on two equally important pillars: biochemical documentation of catecholamine excess and imaging localization of the tumor.","Plasma free metanephrines are the most sensitive biochemical test and are less prone to false-positive elevation from stress including venipuncture; most tumors continuously leak O-methylated metabolites even when catecholamine secretion fluctuates.","Values three times the upper limit of normal make the diagnosis highly likely regardless of assay; because the tumor is rare, borderline elevations are more likely to be false positives, so drug/dietary confounders should be excluded and testing repeated.","On unenhanced CT, an adrenal mass with attenuation below 10 Hounsfield units makes pheochromocytoma unlikely; about 5% of adrenal incidentalomas prove to be pheochromocytomas on endocrine evaluation.","Histology classically shows a 'Zellballen' pattern of neuroendocrine chief cell nests with peripheral sustentacular cells; chief cells stain for chromogranin and synaptophysin, sustentacular cells for S-100, and loss of SDHB staining suggests an underlying germline SDH mutation.","About 25-33% of patients have an inherited syndrome (MEN 2, VHL, NF1, paraganglioma syndromes, TMEM127, MAX); these patients present on average about 15 years younger than those with sporadic tumors.","Universal germline panel testing is the gold standard and identifies a genetic cause in up to about 30% of cases, with higher yield in early onset, extra-adrenal, multiple, or metastatic tumors or a family history.","Pheochromocytoma in pregnancy may produce adrenergic crises, possibly via hCG/LHCG-receptor-mediated stimulation of epinephrine production, and is challenging to manage."],"pathophysiology":"PPGLs are well-vascularized neuroendocrine tumors derived from chromaffin cells of the sympathetic and parasympathetic paraganglial system. They synthesize and store catecholamines (norepinephrine, epinephrine, dopamine), and tumoral release drives the clinical syndrome; the name pheochromocytoma reflects the dark staining produced by chromaffin oxidation of catecholamines. The etiology of sporadic tumors is unknown, but 25-33% carry germline mutations. Susceptibility genes fall into three pathogenetic clusters: cluster 1 (pseudohypoxia: SDHx, FH, VHL, HIF2A), cluster 2 (kinase signaling: RET, NF1, TMEM127, MAX, HRAS, KIF1B-beta, PDH), and cluster 3 (Wnt signaling: CSDE1, MAML3). Most genes act as tumor suppressors with biallelic inactivation, while RET is a protooncogene whose mutations activate receptor tyrosine kinase activity; SDH is a Krebs-cycle/mitochondrial respiratory chain enzyme, and the VHL protein is part of a ubiquitin E3 ligase whose loss upregulates cell-cycle, glucose metabolism, and oxygen-sensing pathways.","additionalDifferentials":["Essential hypertension","Anxiety/panic attacks (may be mistaken for psychiatric illness)","Cocaine or amphetamine use","Mastocytosis or carcinoid syndrome (usually without hypertension)","Intracranial lesions","Clonidine withdrawal","Autonomic epilepsy","Factitious crises (sympathomimetic amine use)","For an asymptomatic adrenal mass: nonfunctioning adrenal adenoma, aldosteronoma, cortisol-producing adenoma (Cushing's syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Essential hypertension with adrenergic features","Panic disorder / anxiety attacks","Sympathomimetic drug effect (cocaine, amphetamines)","Carcinoid syndrome and systemic mastocytosis","Clonidine or other antihypertensive withdrawal"],"additionalInvestigations":["24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines and catecholamines (high sensitivity for initial screening)","Plasma free metanephrines (most sensitive, least stress-confounded)","Contrast CT or T2-weighted MRI with gadolinium for localization (MRI somewhat better for extra-adrenal disease)","Functional imaging: 68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT and 18F-DOPA PET/CT (very high sensitivity and specificity, >95%)","123I/131I-MIBG scintigraphy and 18F-FDG PET, useful in hereditary and metastatic disease","Immunohistochemistry (chromogranin, synaptophysin, S-100, SDHB) on resected tissue","Universal germline genetic panel testing","Postoperative documentation of catecholamine normalization and ACTH stimulation test after bilateral cortex-sparing surgery"],"redFlags":["Catecholamine crisis causing heart failure, pulmonary edema, arrhythmias, or intracranial hemorrhage","Paroxysms precipitated by surgery, anesthesia/intubation, positional change, exercise, micturition (bladder paraganglioma), pregnancy, or drugs (tricyclics, opiates, metoclopramide)","Paradoxical hypertensive response to antihypertensive drugs","Beta blockade given before adequate alpha blockade (risk of worsened hypertension)","Hypertensive crisis or adrenergic crisis during pregnancy","Features suggesting a hereditary syndrome (early onset, bilateral/multifocal, extra-adrenal, family history, MTC, retinal/CNS/renal tumors)"],"prognosis":"Complete tumor removal is potentially curative and can prevent lethal hypertensive crises. About 5-10% of PPGLs are metastatic; histologic criteria alone are insufficient to define malignancy, and the WHO now uses 'metastatic' (lymph node or distant spread, commonly to lung, bone, or liver) rather than 'malignant.' The prognosis of metastatic disease is variable with 5-year survival of roughly 30-60%. Hereditary syndromes influence risk: about one-third of PGL4 (SDHB) patients develop metastases, the highest rate among the associated syndromes, and metastatic disease also occurs notably with VHL, NF1, and PGL1.","pitfalls":["Borderline biochemical elevations are frequently false positives given tumor rarity; failing to exclude dietary/drug confounders (sympathomimetics, diuretics, tricyclics, alpha/beta blockers, levodopa withdrawal) and repeat testing leads to overdiagnosis.","Serial catecholamine levels vary widely due to fluctuating secretion, so reliance on a single catecholamine measurement can miss the diagnosis; metanephrines are continuously leaked.","Starting a beta blocker before adequate alpha blockade can precipitate severe hypertension.","FNA of a presumed adrenal mass that is actually a pheochromocytoma can provoke a crisis.","Standard histologic atypia, mitoses, and local invasion do not reliably establish malignancy.","Symptoms can be misattributed to a primary psychiatric/panic disorder.","NF1 germline mutations are essentially never found in sporadic pheochromocytoma, so NF1 testing is unnecessary without other neurofibromatosis features.","Sequencing protocols may miss large single- or multi-exon deletions."],"management":["Preoperative alpha-adrenergic blockade with phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin as the cornerstone to control catecholamine excess, adding beta-blockade only after adequate alpha-blockade","Volume repletion and a high-sodium diet preoperatively to counter catecholamine-induced volume contraction and prevent post-resection hypotension","Surgical resection, using laparoscopic or cortical-sparing adrenalectomy where feasible, as the definitive and potentially curative treatment","Management of hypertensive crises with short-acting intravenous agents such as phentolamine or sodium nitroprusside","Germline genetic testing for susceptibility genes including SDHx, VHL, RET and NF1, with counselling and cascade family screening given the high heritable proportion","Lifelong biochemical surveillance with plasma or urinary metanephrines and imaging for recurrent, multifocal and metastatic disease","For metastatic disease, systemic therapy with MIBG radionuclide therapy, chemotherapy or peptide receptor radionuclide therapy within multidisciplinary endocrine and oncology care"],"severityClassification":"Malignancy/recurrence risk is assessed by markers including tumor size >5 cm, the Pheochromocytoma of the Adrenal gland Scaled Score (PASS), the Grading system for Adrenal Pheochromocytoma and Paraganglioma (GAPP), and SDHB status, although these remain controversial. Disease is now categorized by the WHO as localized versus metastatic (regional lymph node or distant metastasis) rather than benign versus malignant.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3073","Harrison 22e p.3074","Harrison 22e p.3075","Harrison 22e p.3076","Harrison 22e p.3077","Harrison 22e p.3078"],"crossLinks":[]},"pheochromocytoma_paraganglioma":{"name":"Pheochromocytoma and paraganglioma","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["phaeochromocytoma","PPGL","paraganglioma","chromaffin cell tumour","the 10% tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Catecholamine-secreting tumours arising from adrenal medulla (pheochromocytoma) or extra-adrenal sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia (paraganglioma)","The classic paroxysmal triad is episodic headache, palpitations, and sweating, often with hypertension that may be sustained or episodic","Should be suspected in resistant hypertension, hypertension in the young, hypertensive spikes during anaesthesia or procedures, and incidentally found adrenal masses","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating excess catecholamine metabolites, with plasma or urinary fractionated metanephrines being the most sensitive tests","Imaging localises the tumour after biochemical confirmation, and functional imaging helps detect extra-adrenal or metastatic disease","A substantial proportion are associated with hereditary syndromes such as MEN2, von Hippel-Lindau, neurofibromatosis type 1, and SDH-related paraganglioma syndromes, so genetic evaluation is recommended","Definitive treatment is surgical resection, but adequate preoperative alpha-adrenergic blockade before any beta-blockade is essential to prevent catastrophic intraoperative crises","Because malignancy cannot be reliably predicted histologically, lifelong biochemical and clinical surveillance is required"],"pathophysiology":"Pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas are neuroendocrine tumours of chromaffin or paraganglionic tissue that synthesise, store, and episodically release catecholamines (noradrenaline, adrenaline, and sometimes dopamine). Unregulated catecholamine secretion produces alpha-mediated vasoconstriction and beta-mediated cardiac stimulation, causing paroxysmal or sustained hypertension, tachyarrhythmia, and end-organ stress. Chronic catecholamine excess can lead to cardiomyopathy and volume contraction. A large fraction harbour germline or somatic mutations in genes such as RET, VHL, NF1, and the succinate dehydrogenase (SDHx) complex, linking them to defined hereditary syndromes and influencing malignant potential and secretory phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Essential or resistant hypertension","Anxiety or panic disorder","Hyperthyroidism","Carcinoid syndrome","Hypoglycaemia and other causes of adrenergic surges","Drug effects (sympathomimetics, monoamine oxidase interactions, withdrawal states)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma free or 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines as first-line biochemical testing","CT or MRI of the abdomen and pelvis to localise adrenal or extra-adrenal tumours","Functional imaging such as MIBG or somatostatin-receptor/PET studies for metastatic or extra-adrenal disease","Genetic testing given the high rate of heritable disease","Assessment for associated syndromic features and screening of at-risk relatives","Cardiac evaluation for catecholamine cardiomyopathy where indicated"],"redFlags":["Pheochromocytoma crisis with severe hypertension, arrhythmia, cardiovascular collapse, or multi-organ failure requiring emergency management","Hypertensive surges during anaesthesia, surgery, or procedures in an unprepared patient","Inadvertent beta-blockade before alpha-blockade precipitating unopposed alpha vasoconstriction and hypertensive emergency","Signs of metastatic disease or rapid tumour progression"],"prognosis":"Complete surgical removal of a benign tumour is usually curative with resolution of catecholamine excess, but hypertension may persist in some; metastatic disease carries a more guarded prognosis, and hereditary cases require indefinite surveillance for recurrence and new tumours.","pitfalls":["Administering a beta-blocker before adequate alpha-blockade, risking a hypertensive crisis","Performing biopsy of an unprepared catecholamine-secreting mass","Relying on a single normal test in a strongly suspicious clinical setting","Failing to arrange genetic testing and family screening","Assuming cure without long-term biochemical follow-up given unpredictable malignant behaviour"],"management":["Complete surgical resection (laparoscopic adrenalectomy or excision of extra-adrenal paraganglioma) is the definitive curative treatment","Preoperative preparation with alpha-adrenergic blockade (phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin) first, adding beta-blockade only after adequate alpha-blockade to avoid unopposed alpha vasoconstriction","Liberalise salt and fluid intake preoperatively to counter catecholamine-induced volume contraction and prevent post-resection hypotension","Manage hypertensive crises with intravenous phentolamine or sodium nitroprusside, and never give a beta-blocker before alpha-blockade","Treat unresectable or metastatic disease with radiolabelled MIBG therapy, combination chemotherapy, somatostatin analogues or tyrosine kinase inhibitors","Lifelong biochemical surveillance with plasma or urinary fractionated metanephrines for recurrence and metachronous tumours","Offer germline genetic testing (RET, VHL, NF1, SDHx) with cascade screening and syndrome-specific surveillance of at-risk relatives","Coordinate care across endocrinology, endocrine surgery and specialist anaesthesia, and assess for catecholamine cardiomyopathy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"phimosis":{"name":"Phimosis","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["non-retractile foreskin","preputial stenosis","tight foreskin"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Phimosis is an inability to retract the foreskin over the glans; it is physiological in most infants and young boys and usually resolves as natural preputial separation occurs with age","Pathological (acquired) phimosis follows scarring from recurrent balanitis, forced retraction, or the skin condition balanitis xerotica obliterans (lichen sclerosus), producing a firm white cicatricial ring","It must be distinguished from paraphimosis, in which a retracted foreskin becomes trapped behind the glans and cannot be reduced, causing painful swelling and constituting an emergency","Symptoms may include ballooning of the foreskin on voiding, difficulty with hygiene, recurrent infection, painful erections, and, in severe cases, obstructive voiding","Physiological phimosis is managed conservatively with reassurance, gentle hygiene, and avoidance of forcible retraction, often supplemented by topical corticosteroid application to soften the ring","Balanitis xerotica obliterans is an important pathological cause that typically requires circumcision and histological confirmation, and can involve the meatus and urethra","Circumcision or preputioplasty is indicated for true pathological phimosis, recurrent balanoposthitis, or troublesome symptoms not responding to conservative measures","Persistent phimosis in adults, particularly with lichen sclerosus, is a risk factor for penile carcinoma and warrants careful examination of the glans"],"pathophysiology":"In physiological phimosis the foreskin is non-retractile simply because natural adhesions between the inner prepuce and glans have not yet separated and the preputial opening is developmentally narrow. Pathological phimosis results from fibrosis and scarring of the preputial ring, most often due to chronic or recurrent inflammation (balanoposthitis), repeated trauma from forced retraction, or lichen sclerosus (balanitis xerotica obliterans), an inflammatory dermatosis that causes progressive sclerosis. The scarred, inelastic ring restricts retraction and may obstruct the urinary stream.","additionalDifferentials":["Paraphimosis","Balanitis or balanoposthitis","Balanitis xerotica obliterans (lichen sclerosus)","Preputial adhesions (physiological)","Penile carcinoma or premalignant lesion","Meatal stenosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination of the foreskin, glans, and meatus","Assessment for the cicatricial white ring of lichen sclerosus","Urinalysis if infection or voiding symptoms are present","Histological examination of excised preputial tissue after circumcision, particularly when lichen sclerosus or malignancy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Paraphimosis with a trapped, swollen, painful glans requiring emergency reduction","Severe phimosis causing obstructed voiding or acute urinary retention","Suspicious glans lesion or induration suggesting penile carcinoma","Features of lichen sclerosus with meatal or urethral involvement"],"prognosis":"Physiological phimosis almost always resolves with growth and conservative care; pathological phimosis is effectively cured by circumcision, but lichen sclerosus can recur or involve the meatus and carries a long-term association with penile malignancy.","pitfalls":["Forcibly retracting a young boy's physiological phimosis, which can cause scarring and true phimosis","Mislabelling normal developmental non-retractility as pathological and recommending unnecessary surgery","Missing lichen sclerosus or an underlying penile malignancy in an adult with acquired phimosis","Confusing phimosis with paraphimosis and delaying emergency reduction","Overlooking meatal stenosis in patients with lichen sclerosus after circumcision"],"management":["For physiological phimosis, reassure, advise gentle hygiene and explicitly avoid forcible retraction, which causes scarring","Apply a topical corticosteroid to soften and stretch the non-scarred preputial ring and often avoid surgery","Treat active balanoposthitis with hygiene measures and topical or oral antimicrobials directed at the causative organism","Offer circumcision or preputioplasty for true cicatricial (pathological) phimosis unresponsive to topical steroid","Treat lichen sclerosus with a potent topical corticosteroid and confirm the diagnosis on histology of excised prepuce","Recognise paraphimosis as an emergency requiring prompt manual reduction of the trapped foreskin","Optimise glycaemic control where recurrent balanitis complicates diabetes","Monitor lichen sclerosus for meatal or urethral involvement and its long-term association with penile malignancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"phimosis_pathological":{"name":"Pathological phimosis","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pathological phimosis","scarring phimosis","acquired phimosis","cicatricial phimosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / WHO laboratory manual for semen analysis","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Phimosis is inability to retract the foreskin over the glans, and the crucial distinction is between the physiological non-retractility of childhood and true pathological scarring","Physiological phimosis reflects natural preputial adhesions and a non-scarred narrow tip that resolves spontaneously as the child grows, and needs only reassurance and hygiene advice","Pathological phimosis is characterised by a firm, white, cicatricial ring at the preputial opening, the hallmark of acquired scarring most often due to lichen sclerosus","Presenting features include a ballooning foreskin on voiding, recurrent balanitis, painful erections, bleeding fissures, and, in scarred cases, spraying or obstructed urinary stream","Forcible retraction of a physiological or inflamed foreskin should be avoided as it causes tears that heal with further scarring and can precipitate paraphimosis","A trial of topical corticosteroid can soften and resolve many non-scarred narrow foreskins and avoid surgery","True scarring phimosis, particularly from lichen sclerosus, typically requires circumcision, and excised tissue should be examined histologically"],"pathophysiology":"In the young child the foreskin is normally adherent to the glans and has a physiologically narrow opening; separation and retractility develop progressively over the early years, so non-retractility at this stage is developmental rather than diseased. Pathological phimosis instead results from acquired fibrosis of the distal prepuce. Chronic inflammation, most importantly lichen sclerosus (balanitis xerotica obliterans) but also recurrent balanoposthitis and the trauma of repeated forcible retraction, drives dermal scarring that contracts the preputial ring into a rigid, non-distensible band. This cicatricial narrowing prevents retraction, may obstruct the urinary stream, and can extend to involve the glans and meatus. Poorly controlled diabetes predisposes to the recurrent infection and inflammation that promote scarring.","additionalDifferentials":["Physiological (developmental) phimosis of childhood","Balanitis xerotica obliterans (genital lichen sclerosus)","Recurrent infective or irritant balanoposthitis","Paraphimosis, where a retracted foreskin cannot be reduced","Preputial adhesions or a congenitally short frenulum","Penile intraepithelial neoplasia or carcinoma presenting with preputial change"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination assessing retractility and looking for a cicatricial white ring","Inspection of the glans and meatus for coexisting lichen sclerosus or malignancy","Assessment of the urinary stream where obstruction or ballooning is reported","Blood glucose testing to detect diabetes predisposing to recurrent balanitis","Swab or microscopy if active infection is present","Histological examination of tissue removed at circumcision to confirm lichen sclerosus and exclude malignancy"],"redFlags":["A firm white scarring ring or glans changes suggesting lichen sclerosus with its malignant association","A persistent ulcer or mass raising concern for penile carcinoma","Acute paraphimosis with a painful, swollen, irreducible retracted foreskin requiring urgent reduction","Obstructed voiding or urinary retention from severe narrowing"],"prognosis":"Physiological phimosis resolves spontaneously with growth, and many narrow non-scarred foreskins respond to topical steroid, whereas true scarring phimosis is effectively cured by circumcision, though associated lichen sclerosus of the glans or urethra may need continued attention.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling normal developmental non-retractility as disease and recommending unnecessary surgery","Forcibly retracting the foreskin, causing tears, scarring, and paraphimosis","Missing underlying lichen sclerosus and its meatal, urethral, and malignant implications","Failing to send circumcision specimens for histology","Overlooking diabetes in a man with recurrent balanitis and secondary phimosis"],"management":["Offer circumcision as the definitive treatment for the firm cicatricial preputial ring of true scarring phimosis","Trial a potent topical corticosteroid first for narrow but non-scarred foreskins, which may loosen the ring and avert surgery","Treat lichen sclerosus of the glans and prepuce with a potent topical corticosteroid","Treat active balanoposthitis with swab-directed topical or oral antimicrobials before elective surgery","Optimise glycaemic control in diabetes to reduce recurrent infection and refractory scarring","Avoid forcible retraction, which tears the foreskin and worsens scarring and can precipitate paraphimosis","Send excised preputial tissue for histology to confirm lichen sclerosus and exclude malignancy","Follow up for meatal or urethral stenosis and the long-term penile cancer risk associated with lichen sclerosus"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","British Association of Urological Surgeons guidance on circumcision","British Association of Dermatologists guidance on lichen sclerosus"],"crossLinks":[]},"phlegmasia_alba_dolens":{"name":"phlegmasia alba dolens","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["painful white leg","milk leg","phlegmasia alba"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Phlegmasia alba dolens ('painful white leg', 'milk leg') is a severe form of acute iliofemoral deep vein thrombosis in which massive venous outflow obstruction produces a swollen, painful, pale limb; it is classically described postpartum but occurs with any cause of extensive proximal thrombosis","The pallor reflects tense subcutaneous oedema together with reflex arterial spasm, while patent collateral veins still permit some venous drainage, distinguishing it from the later cyanotic stage","The condition lies on a continuum: untreated progression to phlegmasia cerulea dolens (a cyanotic, near-total venous occlusion involving the collaterals) and then to venous gangrene threatens both the limb and life","Peripheral pulses are typically preserved in phlegmasia alba dolens, which helps differentiate it from acute arterial occlusion where the limb is likewise pale and painful","Massive fluid sequestration into the affected limb can precipitate hypovolaemia and circulatory compromise, and severe swelling may cause a compartment syndrome","Management centres on prompt therapeutic anticoagulation, limb elevation and consideration of catheter-directed thrombolysis or venous thrombectomy, alongside a search for underlying causes such as May-Thurner iliac vein compression or occult malignancy","It is a limb- and life-threatening emergency requiring urgent vascular assessment; venous gangrene carries a high risk of amputation and significant mortality and is strongly associated with malignancy and heparin-induced thrombocytopenia"],"pathophysiology":"Extensive thrombosis of the iliofemoral venous segment obstructs venous outflow from the limb. In the alba stage the collateral veins remain patent, so the limb is pale, tensely swollen and painful, and reflex arterial vasospasm contributes to the pallor and to diminished distal perfusion. As thrombus propagates to involve the collateral and microvascular beds (phlegmasia cerulea dolens), venous drainage fails almost completely; capillary and venular pressures rise steeply, the limb becomes cyanotic and severely swollen, and large volumes of fluid sequester into the tissues, causing hypovolaemia. The resulting rise in tissue pressure, together with progressive microvascular thrombosis, impairs arterial inflow and can culminate in venous gangrene.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute arterial occlusion (embolic or thrombotic limb ischaemia)","Uncomplicated deep vein thrombosis","Compartment syndrome from another cause","Necrotising soft tissue infection","Acute cellulitis of the limb","Lymphoedema complicated by cellulitis","Ruptured popliteal (Baker) cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound to confirm and localise the venous thrombosis","CT or MR venography to define the iliocaval extent and identify compressive lesions such as May-Thurner","Assessment of arterial perfusion (ankle pressures and Doppler) to exclude arterial occlusion","Full blood count with serial platelet monitoring for heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, and a coagulation profile","Renal function and metabolic screen given fluid sequestration and possible tissue injury","Evaluation for underlying malignancy and, where appropriate, thrombophilia","Compartment pressure assessment and markers of tissue injury if gangrene or compartment syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progression to cyanosis (phlegmasia cerulea dolens) or to skin blistering and gangrene","Absent distal pulses or other signs of arterial compromise","Tense swelling suggesting an evolving compartment syndrome","Haemodynamic instability from fluid sequestration","A falling platelet count suggesting heparin-induced thrombocytopenia"],"prognosis":"With prompt anticoagulation and thrombus removal the white-leg stage can resolve, but progression to venous gangrene carries high rates of amputation and mortality. Long-term post-thrombotic syndrome is common in survivors, particularly after extensive iliofemoral thrombosis.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the pale, painful limb for acute arterial ischaemia and delaying anticoagulation","Underestimating fluid sequestration and failing to resuscitate the circulation","Missing an underlying iliac vein compression or occult malignancy","Overlooking an evolving compartment syndrome","Continuing heparin despite thrombocytopenia when heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is driving the thrombosis"],"management":["Start immediate therapeutic anticoagulation with unfractionated or low-molecular-weight heparin to arrest thrombus propagation","Elevate the limb and give fluid resuscitation to counter massive fluid sequestration and hypovolaemia","Consider catheter-directed thrombolysis or pharmacomechanical/surgical venous thrombectomy to clear extensive iliofemoral thrombus","Identify and treat an underlying cause, stenting May-Thurner iliac compression and investigating for occult malignancy","Monitor closely for progression to phlegmasia cerulea dolens, venous gangrene or compartment syndrome requiring fasciotomy","Monitor platelet count for heparin-induced thrombocytopenia during heparin therapy","Continue long-term anticoagulation and graduated compression to limit post-thrombotic syndrome","Escalate urgently to vascular surgery for the threatened limb"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Haimovici's Vascular Surgery","CHEST guidelines on antithrombotic therapy for venous thromboembolic disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"phlegmasia_cerulea_dolens":{"name":"Phlegmasia cerulea dolens","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["phlegmasia","massive iliofemoral deep vein thrombosis","blue phlegmasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A limb-threatening emergency caused by massive proximal (iliofemoral) deep vein thrombosis with near-total venous outflow obstruction","Classic triad of severe pain, extensive oedema, and cyanosis (blue discolouration) of the affected limb","Often preceded by phlegmasia alba dolens, a pale, swollen limb, as thrombosis progresses to occlude collaterals","Massive venous congestion raises compartment pressure, compromises arterial inflow, and can progress to venous gangrene and compartment syndrome","Frequently associated with an underlying prothrombotic state, particularly malignancy, and may cause substantial fluid sequestration and circulatory compromise","Requires urgent anticoagulation, limb elevation, and fluid resuscitation, with catheter-directed thrombolysis or thrombectomy to restore venous outflow in appropriate patients; fasciotomy may be needed for compartment syndrome","Carries a high risk of pulmonary embolism and of significant limb loss and mortality"],"pathophysiology":"Phlegmasia cerulea dolens is the extreme of iliofemoral deep vein thrombosis, in which thrombus occludes not only the major deep veins but also the collateral channels, producing near-complete venous outflow obstruction. The resulting massive rise in interstitial and venous pressure causes gross oedema and cyanosis, and can exceed capillary and eventually arterial perfusion pressure, leading to ischaemia, venous gangrene, and compartment syndrome. Large volumes of fluid sequester in the limb, causing hypovolaemia and shock. An underlying hypercoagulable state, often malignancy, is common.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute arterial occlusion or acute limb ischaemia","Uncomplicated iliofemoral deep vein thrombosis","Compartment syndrome from other causes","Necrotising soft tissue infection","Acute severe cellulitis","Compressive haematoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Venous duplex ultrasound to confirm extensive proximal thrombosis","CT or MR venography to define the extent and any iliac compression or obstruction","Assessment of limb perfusion and compartment pressures where ischaemia is suspected","Investigation for underlying malignancy or thrombophilia","Baseline coagulation and renal function before thrombolysis","Chest imaging or CT pulmonary angiography if pulmonary embolism is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progression to venous gangrene or absent arterial signals, indicating a threatened limb","Compartment syndrome requiring fasciotomy","Haemodynamic compromise from fluid sequestration or massive pulmonary embolism","Rapidly advancing cyanosis and pain despite anticoagulation"],"prognosis":"A serious emergency with significant risk of limb loss, pulmonary embolism, and death; prompt anticoagulation and thrombus removal can salvage the limb, but venous gangrene carries a poor outcome and any underlying malignancy influences survival.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for acute arterial ischaemia and delaying anticoagulation","Underestimating fluid sequestration and failing to resuscitate","Delaying thrombolysis or thrombectomy until gangrene has developed","Missing an occult underlying malignancy"],"management":["Institute emergency therapeutic anticoagulation with intravenous unfractionated heparin as the immediate priority","Give aggressive fluid resuscitation for hypovolaemia and shock and elevate the limb to reduce venous congestion","Restore venous outflow urgently with catheter-directed thrombolysis or venous thrombectomy in suitable patients","Perform fasciotomy for compartment syndrome and debride or amputate established venous gangrene","Investigate and treat an underlying prothrombotic state, particularly occult malignancy or thrombophilia","Monitor for and manage concurrent pulmonary embolism","Escalate immediately to vascular surgery and critical care for this limb- and life-threatening emergency"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","ESVS Clinical Practice Guidelines on Venous Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"phlyctenulosis":{"name":"Phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["phlyctenulosis","phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis","corneal phlycten"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology / Cornea by Krachmer","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A delayed (type IV) hypersensitivity reaction of the conjunctiva and cornea, presenting as small raised nodules (phlyctens) with surrounding hyperaemia","The classic antigenic triggers are staphylococcal antigens (associated with chronic blepharitis) and, historically or in endemic areas, tuberculoprotein","Conjunctival phlyctens near the limbus are often self-limiting, whereas corneal phlyctens cause marked photophobia, tearing, and blepharospasm","Corneal lesions may migrate centrally, dragging a leash of vessels behind them and leaving a triangular scar with its apex toward the centre","Predominantly affects children and young adults and is more common where tuberculosis and malnutrition are prevalent","Recurrent corneal phlyctens can cause thinning, scarring, and vascularisation with lasting visual impairment","Management pairs treatment of the underlying trigger (lid hygiene for staphylococcal disease, evaluation for tuberculosis) with topical corticosteroids to control the hypersensitivity reaction","Because it may signal underlying tuberculosis, systemic evaluation is warranted, particularly in endemic settings"],"pathophysiology":"Phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis is a delayed-type hypersensitivity response to microbial antigens, most often staphylococcal proteins associated with chronic blepharitis, and historically tuberculoprotein. Sensitised T lymphocytes react to the antigen at the limbus, cornea, or conjunctiva, producing a localised nodular infiltrate (the phlycten) with surrounding vascular congestion. The overlying epithelium may ulcerate. Corneal phlyctens can advance centrally, drawing a wedge of new vessels and leaving a scar. Repeated episodes cause progressive corneal vascularisation, thinning, and scarring. As the disorder reflects an immune reaction to a remote antigen rather than active local infection, treatment targets both the antigenic source and the hypersensitivity response.","additionalDifferentials":["Marginal (staphylococcal hypersensitivity) keratitis","Herpes simplex or herpes zoster keratitis","Nodular episcleritis","Inflamed pinguecula or limbal nodule","Vernal keratoconjunctivitis with limbal nodules","Infective (bacterial) limbal keratitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination documenting limbal or corneal phlyctens and associated vascular leash","Assessment for blepharitis and staphylococcal lid disease","Tuberculosis evaluation (tuberculin skin test or interferon-gamma release assay, chest imaging) especially in endemic areas or recurrent disease","Fluorescein staining to assess epithelial ulceration over the phlycten","Screening for malnutrition or systemic predisposition in children","Cultures if secondary infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Central corneal involvement with scarring and visual loss","Features suggesting underlying active tuberculosis requiring systemic investigation","Corneal thinning or ulceration risking perforation","Recurrent disease indicating an uncontrolled antigenic trigger"],"prognosis":"Conjunctival phlyctens are usually self-limiting and resolve without sequelae, whereas recurrent corneal disease can cause scarring, vascularisation, and lasting visual impairment. Controlling the underlying trigger reduces recurrence and preserves vision.","pitfalls":["Treating the eye with steroids without addressing the underlying blepharitis or investigating for tuberculosis","Missing an infective keratitis mimicking a phlycten before prescribing steroids","Overlooking central corneal scarring as a cause of reduced vision","Failing to screen for tuberculosis and malnutrition in at-risk children"],"management":["Suppress the hypersensitivity inflammation with a short course of topical corticosteroid, tapered to the lowest effective strength","Eliminate the antigenic source by treating staphylococcal blepharitis with lid hygiene, warm compresses and topical antibiotic ointment","Give oral tetracyclines in adults, or a macrolide such as erythromycin in children, for recalcitrant lid disease","Evaluate for and treat tuberculosis with standard antituberculous therapy where it is the implicated trigger","Relieve photophobia and pain with ocular lubricants and a cycloplegic agent","Address malnutrition and general health in affected children","Follow up under ophthalmology for corneal lesions and monitor for scarring and vascularisation","Prevent recurrence through ongoing control of blepharitis and lid margin disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Cornea by Krachmer, Mannis and Holland"],"crossLinks":[]},"phosphaturic_mesenchymal_tumor":{"name":"Phosphaturic mesenchymal tumour","system":"Oncology / Pathology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMT","phosphaturic mesenchymal tumour, mixed connective tissue variant","tumour-induced osteomalacia tumour","oncogenic osteomalacia tumour","FGF23-secreting mesenchymal tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A usually benign mesenchymal tumour that secretes fibroblast growth factor 23 (FGF23), causing renal phosphate wasting and the syndrome of tumour-induced (oncogenic) osteomalacia","Patients present with chronic bone pain, muscle weakness, fatigue and fragility fractures, often after years of misattributed symptoms","Biochemistry shows hypophosphataemia with inappropriately normal or low active vitamin D (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D) and raised or inappropriately normal FGF23","Tumours are typically small, slow-growing and occult, arising in bone or soft tissue anywhere in the body, including the extremities, head and neck and acral sites","Histology of the classic mixed connective tissue variant shows bland spindle cells, a distinctive grungy calcified matrix, haemangiopericytoma-like vessels and osteoclast-like giant cells","Recurrent FN1-FGFR1 or FN1-FGF1 fusions are characteristic and reinforce the central role of FGF signalling","Complete surgical resection of the tumour is curative and typically normalises phosphate and reverses osteomalacia"],"pathophysiology":"Phosphaturic mesenchymal tumour causes disease chiefly through overproduction of fibroblast growth factor 23, a hormone that reduces renal reabsorption of phosphate and suppresses activation of vitamin D. The resulting chronic hypophosphataemia impairs bone mineralisation, producing osteomalacia with bone pain, weakness and fractures. Recurrent gene fusions involving fibronectin and FGF receptor or ligand genes are thought to establish an autocrine loop that both drives tumour growth and sustains FGF23 secretion, so that removing the tumour usually corrects the metabolic derangement.","additionalDifferentials":["X-linked and autosomal hereditary hypophosphataemic rickets or osteomalacia","Solitary fibrous tumour","Other causes of acquired hypophosphataemia, including drug-induced phosphate wasting","Osteomalacia from vitamin D deficiency or malabsorption","Giant cell tumour of bone or soft tissue","Sinonasal haemangiopericytoma (glomangiopericytoma) when in the head and neck","Chondromyxoid fibroma or other matrix-producing tumours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum phosphate, calcium, alkaline phosphatase and renal phosphate handling (tubular reabsorption of phosphate)","Serum FGF23 measurement and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D level","Functional imaging such as somatostatin-receptor (for example gallium-68 DOTA) PET to localise a small occult tumour","Cross-sectional imaging (MRI or CT) directed to sites highlighted by functional studies","Molecular testing for FN1-FGFR1 or FN1-FGF1 fusions where diagnostically useful","Selective venous sampling for FGF23 when a tumour cannot be localised by imaging"],"redFlags":["Unexplained hypophosphataemia with fragility fractures and progressive weakness","Long-standing bone pain misdiagnosed as another rheumatological or musculoskeletal disorder","Persisting or recurrent hypophosphataemia after apparent resection, suggesting residual or metastatic tumour","Histological features of malignancy in the rare malignant variant"],"prognosis":"The great majority of phosphaturic mesenchymal tumours are benign, and complete tumour resection is curative with rapid normalisation of phosphate and healing of osteomalacia; incompletely excised tumours cause persistent disease, rare malignant examples can recur or metastasise, and when the tumour cannot be located FGF23-directed medical therapy is used to control the metabolic bone disease.","pitfalls":["Failing to measure serum phosphate in a patient with chronic bone pain and weakness, so the diagnosis is missed for years","Attributing osteomalacia to vitamin D deficiency without recognising renal phosphate wasting","Not pursuing functional localisation for a small, clinically occult tumour","Overlooking the diagnosis when the tumour is at an unusual acral or head and neck site","Assuming benign behaviour and omitting follow-up biochemistry to confirm cure"],"management":["Complete surgical excision with clear margins is the definitive, usually curative treatment, rapidly normalising FGF23 and phosphate","Localise the occult tumour with somatostatin-receptor PET imaging to direct resection or ablation","Bridge or treat unresectable disease with oral phosphate supplementation combined with active vitamin D (calcitriol or alfacalcidol)","Use the anti-FGF23 monoclonal antibody burosumab for inoperable or persistently occult tumours","Monitor phosphate, calcium, alkaline phosphatase and parathyroid hormone, watching for nephrocalcinosis and tertiary hyperparathyroidism from supplementation","Undertake surveillance for local recurrence and the rare malignant, metastasising variant, adding radiotherapy or systemic therapy where malignant","Coordinate management across endocrinology, surgery and specialist pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","WHO Classification of Tumours: Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"phosphaturic_mesenchymal_tumour":{"name":"Phosphaturic Mesenchymal Tumour","system":"Musculoskeletal / Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMT","Tumour-induced osteomalacia","Oncogenic osteomalacia","PMTMCT"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrine and metabolic bone references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Phosphaturic mesenchymal tumour is the classic cause of tumour-induced (oncogenic) osteomalacia, a paraneoplastic syndrome driven by tumoural over-secretion of fibroblast growth factor 23 (FGF23).","FGF23 excess causes renal phosphate wasting with hypophosphataemia and inappropriately low or normal active vitamin D, producing osteomalacia in adults or rickets in children.","Typical features are insidious bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, fatigue, loss of height and recurrent fragility or insufficiency fractures, usually after years of diagnostic delay.","Because phosphate is often absent from routine biochemistry panels, the key clue is missed; a fasting serum phosphate should be checked deliberately.","The distinctive histology is the mixed connective tissue variant (PMTMCT), showing bland spindle cells, flocculent grungy calcification, haemangiopericytoma-like vasculature and osteoclast-like giant cells.","The tumours are typically small, slow-growing and occult, arising anywhere in bone or soft tissue, with acral and craniofacial (sinonasal) sites over-represented.","Somatostatin-receptor functional imaging (for example gallium-68 DOTATATE PET/CT) is the most sensitive means of localising the culprit lesion.","Complete surgical excision is curative with rapid biochemical recovery; recurrent FN1-FGFR1 and, less often, FN1-FGF1 gene fusions have been identified."],"pathophysiology":"Phosphaturic mesenchymal tumours are rare, usually benign neoplasms of mesenchymal origin that autonomously secrete fibroblast growth factor 23 (FGF23), a bone-derived phosphaturic hormone. Circulating FGF23 acts on the renal proximal tubule to downregulate the sodium-dependent phosphate cotransporters (NaPi-IIa and NaPi-IIc), causing renal phosphate wasting and hypophosphataemia, and it simultaneously suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) while inducing 24-hydroxylase (CYP24A1), lowering 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D and thereby reducing intestinal phosphate absorption. Chronic hypophosphataemia impairs mineralisation of newly formed osteoid, producing osteomalacia. These tumours characteristically harbour recurrent gene fusions, most commonly FN1-FGFR1 and less often FN1-FGF1, which are thought to drive an autocrine or paracrine loop promoting FGF23 production. Because the culprit lesions are frequently tiny and may lie anywhere from the distal extremities to the sinonasal region, and because they express somatostatin receptors, they often remain occult for years until localised by functional imaging.","additionalDifferentials":["X-linked hypophosphataemic rickets/osteomalacia (PHEX variant)","Autosomal dominant hypophosphataemic rickets (FGF23 gain-of-function)","Autosomal recessive hypophosphataemic rickets (DMP1 or ENPP1)","Fanconi syndrome and other proximal renal tubular phosphate-wasting states","Fibrous dysplasia and McCune-Albright syndrome (also FGF23-mediated)","Nutritional osteomalacia from vitamin D deficiency, or osteoporosis of other cause","Seronegative spondyloarthropathy or other rheumatological disease mimicking the bone pain and stiffness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting serum phosphate with paired assessment of renal phosphate handling (tubular maximum reabsorption of phosphate corrected for glomerular filtration rate)","Serum calcium, alkaline phosphatase, parathyroid hormone, and 25- and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D","Intact FGF23 measurement, interpreted as inappropriately elevated for the degree of hypophosphataemia","Somatostatin-receptor PET/CT (for example gallium-68 DOTATATE) to localise the tumour","Cross-sectional MRI or CT of the suspected site to define surgical anatomy","Selective venous sampling for FGF23 when non-invasive imaging is equivocal","Histopathology with immunohistochemistry of the resected lesion to confirm the mixed connective tissue variant"],"redFlags":["Recurrent low-trauma fragility or insufficiency fractures in an adult","Progressive proximal myopathy with a waddling gait and difficulty rising from a chair","Persistent unexplained hypophosphataemia","Failure of phosphate and active vitamin D supplementation to control symptoms, indicating an unresected tumour"],"prognosis":"The tumours are almost always benign and complete excision is curative, with phosphate and symptoms usually normalising rapidly after surgery. Incompletely resected or persistently occult lesions cause ongoing disease, and rare malignant variants with metastatic potential are recognised.","pitfalls":["Because phosphate is not on standard biochemistry panels, the disorder is commonly missed and mislabelled as osteoporosis, fibromyalgia or a rheumatological condition.","FGF23 may fall within the assay reference range yet still be inappropriately high for the degree of hypophosphataemia, so it must be interpreted in context.","Anatomical imaging alone often fails to detect the small tumour; functional somatostatin-receptor imaging is usually required.","Symptoms recurring after apparently successful surgery suggest residual tumour or a second occult lesion rather than a new disease."],"management":["Achieve cure with complete surgical resection of the FGF23-secreting tumour, after which phosphate and symptoms normalise rapidly","Localise the small occult lesion using gallium-68 DOTATATE somatostatin-receptor PET/CT to guide excision","Correct hypophosphataemic osteomalacia medically with oral phosphate salts plus active vitamin D when surgery is delayed or not feasible","Treat inoperable or persistent disease with the anti-FGF23 antibody burosumab","Monitor serum phosphate, calcium, alkaline phosphatase and parathyroid hormone and screen for nephrocalcinosis while on phosphate and vitamin D","Provide surveillance for incompletely excised or recurrent tumours and for rare malignant variants with metastatic potential","Manage through a multidisciplinary team of endocrinology, oncology, surgery and pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Endocrinology and metabolic bone reference texts","Bone and soft-tissue tumour pathology references","Clinical guidelines on hypophosphataemic osteomalacia"],"crossLinks":[]},"phosphoglycerate_dehydrogenase_deficiency":{"name":"Phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Amino acid biosynthesis","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PHGDH deficiency","serine deficiency syndrome","3-phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder of L-serine biosynthesis caused by deficiency of phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase","The classic infantile form presents with congenital microcephaly, intractable seizures and severe psychomotor retardation","Milder juvenile forms with absence seizures and developmental delay are recognised","The most severe end of the spectrum overlaps with Neu-Laxova syndrome","Cerebrospinal fluid and, to a lesser extent, plasma show low serine (and often low glycine)","It is one of the treatable inborn errors — oral serine (with glycine) supplementation can improve seizures and, if started early, outcomes","Serine is essential for neuronal membrane, neurotransmitter and one-carbon metabolism, explaining the neurological phenotype"],"pathophysiology":"Phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase catalyses the first committed step of de novo L-serine synthesis from a glycolytic intermediate. Deficiency limits endogenous serine production, which is critical in the brain because serine crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly and neural tissue depends heavily on local synthesis. Serine is a precursor for glycine, phospholipids, nucleotides and D-serine (an NMDA-receptor co-agonist), so its deficiency impairs myelination, neuronal proliferation and neurotransmission, producing microcephaly, seizures and developmental impairment. Providing exogenous serine bypasses the enzymatic block.","additionalDifferentials":["Other serine biosynthesis defects (phosphoserine aminotransferase or phosphatase deficiency)","Congenital microcephaly of other genetic cause","Early infantile epileptic encephalopathies","Congenital infections causing microcephaly","Neu-Laxova syndrome (severe end of the spectrum)","Other treatable neurometabolic epilepsies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma and cerebrospinal fluid amino acids showing low serine","Genetic testing of PHGDH","Brain MRI (may show hypomyelination and cortical atrophy)","EEG for seizure characterisation","Assessment of the other serine-pathway enzymes if PHGDH is normal"],"redFlags":["Congenital microcephaly with intractable neonatal seizures","Progressive developmental regression","Feeding difficulties and failure to thrive","Severe presentation overlapping with Neu-Laxova syndrome"],"prognosis":"Untreated classic disease carries a poor neurological prognosis; early serine (and glycine) supplementation can markedly reduce seizures and, when started antenatally or in early infancy, may substantially improve outcome.","pitfalls":["Measuring only plasma amino acids, as cerebrospinal fluid serine is more sensitive","Overlooking a treatable cause of a neonatal epileptic encephalopathy","Delaying a trial of serine supplementation while awaiting genetics","Confusing it with non-treatable causes of congenital microcephaly"],"management":["Provide lifelong oral L-serine supplementation, usually with added glycine, to bypass the enzymatic block and replenish central serine","Start supplementation as early as possible, including antenatally in affected pregnancies, to maximise neurological outcome","Control seizures with appropriate anticonvulsant therapy alongside serine replacement","Support development with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language input","Monitor cerebrospinal fluid and plasma serine and glycine and titrate supplementation against clinical and biochemical response","Offer genetic counselling with PHGDH cascade testing of the family and prenatal diagnosis for future pregnancies","Coordinate care through metabolic medicine and paediatric neurology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Inborn Metabolic Diseases (standard reference)","GeneReviews (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"phosphoglycerate_kinase_deficiency":{"name":"Phosphoglycerate kinase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PGK deficiency","PGK1 deficiency","phosphoglycerate kinase 1 deficiency","glycolytic enzymopathy PGK"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Phosphoglycerate kinase deficiency is an X-linked recessive glycolytic enzymopathy caused by PGK1 variants, mainly affecting males","It produces a variable combination of chronic non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia, a metabolic myopathy with exercise intolerance and recurrent rhabdomyolysis, and central nervous system involvement","CNS features can include intellectual disability, seizures, and stroke-like episodes, and any given patient may have one, two, or all three components","Because PGK is a core glycolytic enzyme, its deficiency impairs energy production in erythrocytes, muscle, and neurones, explaining the multisystem phenotype","Exertional myoglobinuria can precipitate acute kidney injury and requires prompt recognition","Diagnosis rests on reduced PGK enzyme activity in red cells or muscle and confirmatory PGK1 genetic testing","Management is supportive: avoiding strenuous exertion and rhabdomyolysis triggers, transfusion or folate support for haemolysis, and seizure control as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Phosphoglycerate kinase catalyses an ATP-generating step of glycolysis, converting 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate to 3-phosphoglycerate. PGK1 is X-linked and expressed in almost all tissues, so deficiency impairs glycolytic ATP production broadly. In erythrocytes, which depend on glycolysis for energy, reduced ATP shortens red-cell survival and causes chronic non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia. In skeletal muscle, impaired glycolysis limits energy during anaerobic exertion, producing exercise intolerance, cramps, and rhabdomyolysis. In the brain, deficient energy metabolism underlies the intellectual impairment, seizures, and stroke-like episodes seen in some patients. The particular combination of features varies with the specific variant.","additionalDifferentials":["Other glycolytic enzymopathies (e.g. pyruvate kinase, phosphofructokinase deficiency)","McArdle disease and other muscle glycogenoses","Other causes of chronic non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia (e.g. G6PD deficiency)","Mitochondrial disorders with myopathy and stroke-like episodes","Fatty acid oxidation defects causing rhabdomyolysis","Other X-linked intellectual disability syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Phosphoglycerate kinase enzyme activity assay in erythrocytes and/or muscle","Molecular genetic testing of PGK1","Full blood count, reticulocytes, and haemolysis markers (bilirubin, LDH, haptoglobin)","Creatine kinase, with urine myoglobin during episodes of exertional muscle injury","Brain MRI and EEG where CNS features are present"],"redFlags":["Acute rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria threatening acute kidney injury","Severe anaemia requiring transfusion or causing cardiovascular compromise","Stroke-like episodes or new focal neurological deficit","Status epilepticus or rapid neurological deterioration"],"prognosis":"The prognosis varies widely with phenotype; isolated haemolysis or myopathy may be manageable with supportive care, whereas significant CNS involvement carries a worse outlook, and recurrent rhabdomyolysis poses ongoing renal risk.","pitfalls":["Attributing exertional muscle symptoms to a glycogenosis without considering a glycolytic enzymopathy","Missing the diagnosis in a boy with unexplained non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia","Failing to counsel on avoidance of rhabdomyolysis triggers and prompt management of myoglobinuria","Overlooking the X-linked inheritance in genetic counselling of the family"],"management":["Avoid strenuous anaerobic exertion and other triggers of rhabdomyolysis and pace physical activity","Treat exertional rhabdomyolysis with vigorous intravenous hydration and manage resulting acute kidney injury, with renal replacement if needed","Support the chronic non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia with folate supplementation and transfusion during crises, considering splenectomy in selected severe cases","Manage central nervous system involvement with anticonvulsants and neurorehabilitation for seizures, stroke-like episodes and developmental impairment","Monitor renal function, creatine kinase and haemolysis markers, and check urine myoglobin during exertional episodes","Offer genetic counselling for this X-linked disorder with carrier testing of female relatives","Coordinate care across haematology, metabolic medicine and neurology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","GeneReviews: Phosphoglycerate Kinase 1 Deficiency"],"crossLinks":[]},"phosphoglycerate_mutase_deficiency":{"name":"Phosphoglycerate mutase deficiency","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PGAM deficiency","glycogen storage disease type X","phosphoglyceromutase deficiency","PGAM2 gene"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare recessive glycolytic defect that classically produces a muscle phenotype with exercise intolerance, cramps and exertional rhabdomyolysis","The muscle isoform (PGAM2) predominates in skeletal muscle, so myopathy is the dominant feature and haemolysis is variable when the erythrocyte enzyme is affected","Presents in some patients with pigmenturia (myoglobinuria) after vigorous exercise, akin to the muscle glycogenoses","When the red-cell enzyme is reduced, a chronic non-spherocytic, Coombs-negative haemolytic anaemia may accompany the muscle disease","A forearm ischaemic exercise test may show a subnormal rise in venous lactate, pointing to a glycolytic block","Diagnosis is confirmed by reduced enzyme activity on assay and PGAM2 mutation analysis","Management centres on avoiding strenuous exertion that triggers rhabdomyolysis, with supportive transfusion and folate if significant haemolysis is present"],"pathophysiology":"Phosphoglycerate mutase catalyses the interconversion of 3-phosphoglycerate and 2-phosphoglycerate in the ATP-generating lower half of glycolysis. Deficiency impedes glycolytic energy production; in skeletal muscle this manifests as exercise intolerance, cramps and exertional muscle breakdown, while in the red cell, which relies wholly on glycolysis for ATP, reduced activity can shorten cell survival and cause a chronic non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia. The relative contribution of muscle and erythrocyte disease depends on which isoform and mutation are involved.","additionalDifferentials":["McArdle disease (myophosphorylase deficiency)","Phosphofructokinase deficiency (Tarui disease)","Aldolase A deficiency","Other glycolytic enzymopathies causing haemolysis","Lactate dehydrogenase deficiency","Metabolic myopathies of fatty-acid oxidation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Muscle or red-cell phosphoglycerate mutase enzyme assay","Forearm ischaemic exercise test measuring venous lactate and ammonia","Creatine kinase and myoglobin during and after exertion","Full blood count, reticulocytes and blood film if haemolysis is suspected","Haemolysis screen with bilirubin, lactate dehydrogenase and haptoglobin","Molecular testing of the PGAM2 gene"],"redFlags":["Exertional rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria and risk of acute kidney injury","Recurrent severe muscle cramps limiting activity","Significant haemolytic anaemia when the erythrocyte enzyme is affected","Aplastic crisis following parvovirus B19 infection"],"prognosis":"The muscle disease is usually compatible with a normal lifespan provided provocative exertion is avoided; haemolysis, when present, is generally mild to moderate and well tolerated.","pitfalls":["Attributing exertional symptoms to deconditioning rather than a metabolic myopathy","Omitting a forearm exercise test that would flag a glycolytic block","Not warning patients to avoid the strenuous exercise that provokes rhabdomyolysis","Overlooking a concurrent mild haemolytic anaemia"],"management":["Avoid vigorous anaerobic exercise that provokes cramps and exertional rhabdomyolysis, and use gradual warm-up and paced activity","Treat episodes of myoglobinuria with prompt intravenous hydration and protect renal function, managing any acute kidney injury","Support coexisting haemolytic anaemia, where present, with folate supplementation and transfusion when needed","Monitor creatine kinase, renal function and haemolysis markers, checking urine myoglobin after exertion","Offer genetic counselling with PGAM2 testing of the family","Coordinate care through metabolic medicine and neuromuscular services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Wintrobe's Clinical Hematology"],"crossLinks":[]},"phospholipase_a2_receptor_nephropathy":{"name":"Phospholipase A2 receptor-associated membranous nephropathy","system":"Renal / Glomerular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLA2R-associated membranous nephropathy","Anti-PLA2R membranous nephropathy","M-type phospholipase A2 receptor nephropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nephrology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The M-type phospholipase A2 receptor (PLA2R1) is the dominant podocyte autoantigen in primary membranous nephropathy, accounting for the large majority of cases.","Circulating anti-PLA2R autoantibodies are predominantly of the IgG4 subclass and serve as a diagnostic, prognostic and monitoring biomarker.","In the right clinical context, seropositivity with a compatible presentation can support the diagnosis and, in selected patients, may permit deferral of biopsy.","Enhanced glomerular PLA2R antigen staining on biopsy can be positive even when serum antibody is undetectable, improving diagnostic sensitivity.","Antibody level broadly tracks disease activity: immunological remission (falling titre) typically precedes clinical remission of proteinuria, and persistent or rising titres predict progression or relapse.","The nephrotic state carries a high thromboembolic risk, including renal vein thrombosis, warranting vigilance and prophylaxis considerations.","Seropositivity does not exclude a secondary cause; malignancy, hepatitis B, lupus and certain drugs must still be considered, particularly with atypical features.","Antibody-guided immunosuppression, commonly B-cell depletion with rituximab, has become central to management of higher-risk disease."],"pathophysiology":"PLA2R1 is a large type-I transmembrane glycoprotein of the mannose-receptor family constitutively expressed on the apical surface of podocytes. In primary membranous nephropathy, autoantibodies recognise conformation-dependent epitopes within the cysteine-rich (ricin-B) domain and the C-type lectin domains of the receptor. These antibodies bind PLA2R in situ on the podocyte, forming subepithelial immune deposits along the outer aspect of the glomerular basement membrane. Although IgG4 is a poor activator of the classical complement pathway, complement activation, chiefly via the lectin and alternative pathways, generates the C5b-9 membrane-attack complex sublytically on the podocyte. This drives cytoskeletal derangement, foot-process effacement, oxidative and endoplasmic-reticulum stress, and shedding of podocytes, producing the characteristic non-selective proteinuria. Progressive deposit formation with intervening basement-membrane spikes yields the thickened capillary walls seen histologically, while ongoing antigen-antibody interaction sustains injury until the autoimmune response abates spontaneously or with therapy.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary membranous nephropathy (malignancy, hepatitis B or C, systemic lupus erythematosus class V, drugs)","THSD7A-associated membranous nephropathy and other rarer antigen types","Minimal-change disease and focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","Diabetic nephropathy","Renal amyloidosis and monoclonal deposition disease","Membranoproliferative and C3 glomerulopathy","Lupus nephritis with membranous features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum anti-PLA2R antibody by immunofluorescence assay and/or ELISA, with serial titres for monitoring","Renal biopsy with PLA2R antigen immunostaining, IgG subclass mapping and electron microscopy","THSD7A antibody and antigen testing when PLA2R is negative","Hepatitis B and C serology and autoimmune/complement panel","Age- and risk-appropriate malignancy screening","Quantification of proteinuria and assessment of kidney function over time","Evaluation of thromboembolic risk including serum albumin trend"],"redFlags":["Declining kidney function or refractory nephrotic-range proteinuria","Thromboembolic events such as renal vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism","Features suggesting an underlying malignancy or other secondary cause","Very high or persistently rising anti-PLA2R titres despite therapy","Rapid deterioration suggesting superimposed crescentic or anti-GBM disease"],"prognosis":"Outcomes follow a broad spectrum historically summarised as roughly a third achieving spontaneous remission, a third with persistent proteinuria and a third progressing; antibody status now refines this, with low or falling titres favouring remission and high, persistent titres predicting progression and guiding treatment intensity.","pitfalls":["Assuming PLA2R positivity excludes a secondary cause and omitting malignancy or viral screening","Treating on biopsy appearance alone without measuring or monitoring antibody titres","Neglecting thromboembolic prophylaxis in profound nephrotic hypoalbuminaemia","Missing seronegative disease where only tissue antigen staining is positive","Failing to test for THSD7A and other antigens when PLA2R is negative"],"management":["Begin supportive antiproteinuric and antihypertensive therapy with renin-angiotensin system blockade, dietary sodium restriction, statins and diuretics for oedema","Reserve immunosuppression for high-risk or persistent nephrotic disease, using rituximab (anti-CD20) as preferred first-line therapy","Consider a calcineurin inhibitor or a cyclical corticosteroid-alkylating agent (Ponticelli) regimen as alternative immunosuppression","Provide thromboprophylaxis or therapeutic anticoagulation given the high venous and renal-vein thrombosis risk of the nephrotic state","Monitor serial anti-PLA2R antibody titres to guide immunosuppression and detect relapse, tracking proteinuria and kidney function","Screen for secondary contributors including hepatitis B and C and age-appropriate malignancy","Escalate or intensify immunosuppression when antibody titres persist or rise, under nephrology supervision","Update vaccinations and take infection precautions before starting immunosuppression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive nephrology textbook","International clinical practice guideline on glomerular diseases","Reference review on membranous nephropathy antigens and serology"],"crossLinks":[]},"photoallergic_dermatitis":{"name":"Photoallergic contact dermatitis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["photoallergy","photoallergic contact dermatitis","photoallergic eczema","photoallergic reaction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction in which ultraviolet light (usually UVA) converts a topically applied or, less often, systemic chemical into an allergen the immune system then reacts to","Presents as an eczematous, sometimes vesicular, itchy eruption favouring sun-exposed sites such as the face, V of the neck, dorsa of the hands and forearms","Characteristically spares shaded areas including the upper eyelids, submental region, behind the ears and beneath the chin, which helps distinguish it from an airborne dermatitis","Common culprits include organic ultraviolet filters in sunscreens, topical non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (e.g. ketoprofen), fragrances and certain antimicrobials","Unlike phototoxicity, it requires prior sensitisation, affects only a minority of exposed people, may spread beyond exposed skin and is not simply dose-dependent","Photopatch testing, in which duplicate allergen sets are applied and one set irradiated with UVA, is the key confirmatory investigation","Management centres on identifying and withdrawing the responsible agent, rigorous photoprotection and topical corticosteroids for the acute eruption"],"pathophysiology":"Photoallergic contact dermatitis is an acquired, immunologically mediated (type IV, cell-mediated) response. A chemical applied to or reaching the skin absorbs ultraviolet radiation, most often in the UVA range, and is thereby altered to form a photoproduct or a stable hapten-protein complex that becomes antigenic. After an initial sensitisation phase, subsequent exposure to both the chemical and light provokes a T-cell-driven eczematous reaction. Because an immunological memory response is required, only previously sensitised individuals react, the amount of allergen needed is small, and the eruption may extend to covered skin, distinguishing it from the direct, non-immunological tissue damage of a phototoxic reaction.","additionalDifferentials":["Phototoxic (photoirritant) dermatitis, which occurs on first exposure and is dose-dependent","Airborne allergic contact dermatitis, which typically involves shaded folds such as the eyelids and submental area","Chronic actinic dermatitis and other idiopathic photodermatoses such as polymorphic light eruption","Systemic photodistributed drug eruption","Cutaneous lupus erythematosus with photosensitivity","Seborrhoeic or atopic dermatitis of the face and neck"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Photopatch testing with parallel irradiated and non-irradiated allergen series and comparison of the two","Standard patch testing to detect coexisting ordinary contact allergy","Detailed history of topical products, medicaments, fragrances and occupational or recreational light exposure","Phototesting to determine the action spectrum and minimal erythema dose when chronic actinic dermatitis is suspected","Skin biopsy in atypical cases to confirm a spongiotic dermatitis and exclude mimics","Autoimmune serology (e.g. antinuclear and extractable nuclear antigen antibodies) to exclude photosensitive lupus"],"redFlags":["Progression to a persistent, disabling photosensitivity suggestive of evolving chronic actinic dermatitis","Widespread erythroderma or systemic upset raising the possibility of a severe drug reaction","Failure to improve despite allergen avoidance, prompting reassessment for an underlying photodermatosis or connective tissue disease","Features of cutaneous or systemic lupus rather than simple contact photoallergy"],"prognosis":"The outlook is good once the responsible agent is identified and avoided, with resolution of the eczema and no long-term sequelae in most patients; a minority develop persistent light reactivity that may evolve towards chronic actinic dermatitis.","pitfalls":["Mistaking photoallergy for simple sunburn or phototoxicity and failing to arrange photopatch testing","Overlooking the sunscreen itself as the allergen, so that increasing photoprotection paradoxically worsens the eruption","Confusing the distribution with airborne contact dermatitis and missing the shaded-site sparing","Attributing a photodistributed eruption solely to a topical agent when a systemic drug is responsible"],"management":["Identify and permanently avoid the culprit photoallergen, commonly organic sunscreen filters, topical non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs such as ketoprofen, fragrances or antimicrobials","Institute strict photoprotection with broad-spectrum inorganic UVA-blocking sunscreen, protective clothing and sun avoidance","Treat the eczematous eruption with topical corticosteroids, escalating to a short oral corticosteroid course for severe widespread flares","Confirm the responsible agent with photopatch testing","Relieve symptoms with emollients and antihistamines for pruritus","Monitor for evolution into persistent light reactivity or chronic actinic dermatitis in the minority who do not settle"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","British Association of Dermatologists photopatch testing guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"photodistributed_drug_eruption":{"name":"Photodistributed drug eruption","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["drug-induced photosensitivity","photodistributed drug eruption","drug photosensitivity reaction","systemic photosensitive drug reaction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A drug-induced eruption confined to light-exposed skin, typically the face, V of the neck, dorsa of the hands and extensor forearms, with sparing of shaded sites","Reactions divide into phototoxic, which are common, dose-dependent and resemble exaggerated sunburn, and photoallergic, which are immune-mediated, eczematous and affect only sensitised individuals","The action spectrum is usually within the ultraviolet A range, so reactions can occur through window glass and despite ultraviolet-B-blocking sunscreens","Frequently implicated systemic drugs include certain thiazide diuretics, tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, amiodarone, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and voriconazole","Sparing of the upper eyelids, submental region and skin behind the ears helps confirm a photodistributed pattern","Some drugs cause distinctive patterns such as photo-onycholysis, a lichenoid photoreaction, or pseudoporphyria with skin fragility and blistering","Chronic amiodarone or long-term photosensitisers can produce slate-grey pigmentation of exposed skin","Management is withdrawal or substitution of the culprit drug where possible, strict broad-spectrum photoprotection and topical corticosteroids for symptomatic skin"],"pathophysiology":"Photodistributed drug eruptions arise when a systemic drug or its metabolite absorbs ultraviolet radiation in the skin. In phototoxic reactions the absorbed energy generates reactive oxygen species and other cytotoxic photoproducts that directly damage keratinocytes and other cutaneous cells, producing an exaggerated sunburn-like response that depends on the dose of both drug and light and can occur on first exposure. In photoallergic reactions the drug is converted by light into a photoproduct that acts as a hapten, provoking a delayed type IV, T-cell-mediated hypersensitivity that requires prior sensitisation and produces an eczematous eruption. The predominance of ultraviolet A in the action spectrum explains why reactions occur despite ordinary sunscreens and through glass.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic photodermatoses such as polymorphic light eruption and chronic actinic dermatitis","Photoallergic and phototoxic contact reactions from topical agents","Cutaneous lupus erythematosus with photosensitivity","Porphyria cutanea tarda and drug-induced pseudoporphyria","Airborne allergic contact dermatitis","Pellagra and other nutritional photosensitive dermatoses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed drug and sun-exposure history correlating onset with photosensitising medication","Assessment of the distribution to confirm sparing of shaded sites","Phototesting to define the action spectrum and photopatch testing to identify photoallergy","Skin biopsy in atypical cases to distinguish phototoxic, lichenoid or pseudoporphyria patterns","Porphyrin studies to exclude true porphyria in blistering presentations","Autoimmune serology to exclude photosensitive lupus"],"redFlags":["Blistering with skin fragility suggesting pseudoporphyria or true porphyria","Progression toward persistent light sensitivity or chronic actinic dermatitis","An essential photosensitising drug that cannot easily be withdrawn, requiring specialist input","Features raising the possibility of cutaneous or systemic lupus"],"prognosis":"Most reactions resolve after the culprit drug is stopped and light exposure minimised, though phototoxic pigmentation and drug-induced photosensitivity can persist for a time after withdrawal, and rarely a lasting photosensitivity develops.","pitfalls":["Attributing a photodistributed eruption to a topical product while missing a systemic photosensitising drug","Advising only ultraviolet-B sunscreens when the reaction is driven by ultraviolet A","Failing to recognise pseudoporphyria and confusing it with true porphyria","Overlooking the sparing of shaded sites that defines the photodistributed pattern"],"management":["Withdraw or substitute the culprit photosensitising drug, such as a thiazide, tetracycline, fluoroquinolone, amiodarone, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug or voriconazole","Enforce photoprotection with broad-spectrum UVA sunscreen, protective clothing and sun avoidance, recognising reactions occur through window glass","Treat the eczematous photoallergic component with topical corticosteroids and give supportive care for the sunburn-like phototoxic reaction","Investigate blistering presentations with porphyrin studies to exclude pseudoporphyria and true porphyria","Where the drug is essential, adjust dosing and timing or select a non-photosensitising alternative in consultation with the prescriber","Monitor for resolution and for residual pigmentation or persisting photosensitivity after withdrawal"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","British Association of Dermatologists guidance on drug-induced photosensitivity"],"crossLinks":[]},"photokeratitis":{"name":"Photokeratitis","system":"Eye","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ultraviolet keratitis","snow blindness","welder's flash","arc eye"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ophthalmology and occupational medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acute painful inflammation of the corneal epithelium caused by ultraviolet radiation exposure","Typical sources are welding arcs, reflection of sunlight off snow or water, and sunlamps","Symptoms are characteristically delayed by several hours after exposure and are bilateral","Patients have intense pain, foreign-body sensation, watering, redness, blepharospasm and photophobia","Examination shows diffuse punctate epithelial staining, and the condition is self-limiting","Management is supportive with lubrication and analgesia, and prevention through appropriate eye protection"],"pathophysiology":"Photokeratitis results from ultraviolet radiation damaging the corneal and conjunctival epithelium, causing death and shedding of surface epithelial cells. The injury behaves like a superficial burn, and because the epithelial damage and consequent nerve-ending exposure take hours to manifest, symptoms are characteristically delayed after the exposure. The corneal epithelium regenerates quickly, so the condition is painful but usually short-lived and heals without scarring.","additionalDifferentials":["Infective keratitis or corneal ulcer","Corneal abrasion or foreign body","Chemical eye injury","Acute anterior uveitis","Dry eye disease with keratopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the exposure history and delayed bilateral symptoms","Slit-lamp examination with fluorescein showing punctate epithelial staining","Visual acuity assessment","Exclusion of a retained foreign body or infective keratitis","Assessment for occupational ultraviolet exposure"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting infective keratitis such as a focal infiltrate","A retained foreign body or penetrating injury","Significant visual loss","Failure to improve within the expected short timeframe"],"prognosis":"The condition is self-limiting and typically resolves within a day or two as the corneal epithelium heals, without long-term visual consequence.","pitfalls":["Missing an infective keratitis or foreign body mistaken for simple photokeratitis","Prescribing topical anaesthetics for ongoing use, which impairs healing","Overlooking the occupational source and failing to advise eye protection","Attributing delayed symptoms to another acute ocular condition"],"management":["Provide supportive care with copious ocular lubricants and artificial tears while the corneal epithelium heals","Relieve pain with oral analgesia and a cycloplegic agent to ease ciliary spasm, avoiding abuse of topical anaesthetics","Give topical antibiotic cover for the epithelial defect to prevent secondary infection","Advise rest in a darkened environment and cool compresses for symptomatic relief","Reassure that the condition is self-limiting and arrange review if symptoms do not resolve, to exclude infective keratitis or a retained foreign body","Prevent recurrence with appropriate ultraviolet eye protection for welding, snow and sunlamp exposure and give occupational advice"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ophthalmology reference texts (standard reference)","Occupational medicine references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"phyllodes_tumor":{"name":"Phyllodes tumour of the breast","system":"Oncology / Pathology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["phyllodes tumour","cystosarcoma phyllodes","phylloides tumour","fibroepithelial tumour of the breast","benign, borderline and malignant phyllodes tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fibroepithelial neoplasm of the breast in which the stromal (mesenchymal) component drives the tumour, forming characteristic leaf-like (phyllodes) fronds projecting into cystic spaces","Classified into a spectrum of benign, borderline and malignant grades based on stromal cellularity, atypia, mitotic activity, margin appearance and stromal overgrowth","Typically presents in middle-aged women as a firm, rapidly enlarging, painless breast mass, often larger than a fibroadenoma","Distinguishing benign phyllodes tumour from a cellular fibroadenoma can be difficult, and core biopsy may not reliably separate them","Stromal overgrowth, defined as stroma without accompanying epithelium in a low-power field, is an important adverse feature","MED12 mutations are shared with fibroadenoma, while progression to higher grade is associated with additional alterations such as TERT promoter mutations and other tumour-suppressor changes","Wide local excision with an adequate rim of normal tissue is the goal; axillary dissection is not required because malignant tumours spread haematogenously, not typically to nodes"],"pathophysiology":"Phyllodes tumours are biphasic fibroepithelial lesions in which the neoplastic stroma is the driving component, producing exaggerated leaf-like architecture and, in higher grades, sarcomatous features. Early tumours often share MED12 mutations with fibroadenomas, indicating a common starting point, whereas progression towards borderline and malignant grades accumulates further genetic alterations, including TERT promoter mutations and loss of tumour-suppressor function, that increase stromal cellularity, atypia and proliferation. Malignant tumours can undergo heterologous sarcomatous differentiation and metastasise through the bloodstream rather than via lymphatics.","additionalDifferentials":["Fibroadenoma, especially the cellular and juvenile variants","Primary breast sarcoma","Metaplastic (spindle cell) carcinoma of the breast","Hamartoma of the breast","Periductal stromal tumour","Fibromatosis of the breast","Malignant heterologous elements mimicking a separate sarcoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Triple assessment with clinical examination, breast imaging and biopsy","Core-needle biopsy, recognising its limitations in separating phyllodes tumour from cellular fibroadenoma","Histological grading assessing stromal cellularity, atypia, mitoses, margins and stromal overgrowth","Breast ultrasound and mammography, with MRI when extent is unclear","Staging imaging for distant (haematogenous) metastasis in malignant tumours","Molecular studies (for example MED12 and TERT promoter status) in selected diagnostically difficult cases"],"redFlags":["Rapid increase in size of a breast mass","Malignant-grade histology with marked stromal overgrowth, atypia and high mitotic activity","Positive or close excision margins predicting local recurrence","Signs of haematogenous metastasis, particularly to the lungs","Heterologous sarcomatous differentiation within the tumour"],"prognosis":"Benign phyllodes tumours have an excellent prognosis when completely excised, with recurrence linked mainly to inadequate margins; borderline tumours recur more often, and malignant phyllodes tumours can metastasise haematogenously, most commonly to the lungs, carrying a significantly worse outlook, so wide excision with clear margins is the key determinant of local control across all grades.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing a cellular fibroadenoma on core biopsy and missing a phyllodes tumour","Performing axillary lymph node dissection, which is unnecessary given haematogenous spread","Excising with inadequate margins, leading to local recurrence","Overlooking stromal overgrowth or heterologous sarcomatous elements when grading","Underestimating recurrence risk in borderline tumours treated as clearly benign"],"management":["Excise with wide local excision achieving clear margins as the mainstay, reserving mastectomy for large tumours or when breast conservation is not feasible","Avoid enucleation, which predisposes to local recurrence, and re-excise inadequate margins","Omit axillary node dissection because spread of malignant tumours is haematogenous rather than lymphatic","Consider adjuvant radiotherapy for malignant or borderline tumours and after margin-positive re-excision","Reserve sarcoma-type chemotherapy for selected large malignant or metastatic disease","Undertake surveillance with regular clinical examination and breast imaging for local recurrence, adding staging chest imaging for lung metastases in malignant tumours","Manage through a breast multidisciplinary team with specialist pathology review and grading"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Breast Tumours","WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"phyllodes_tumour":{"name":"Phyllodes tumour","system":"Breast","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cystosarcoma phyllodes","Phylloides tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Breast Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Fibroepithelial neoplasm defined by a leaf-like (phyllodes) architecture with hypercellular stroma condensing around clefted epithelium.","Graded as benign, borderline or malignant using stromal cellularity, atypia, mitotic rate, stromal overgrowth and the nature of the tumour margin.","Typically presents as a firm, mobile, painless mass that enlarges more rapidly than a fibroadenoma, often in women in their fourth to fifth decade.","Core biopsy cannot reliably distinguish a phyllodes tumour from a cellular fibroadenoma; a report of 'cellular fibroepithelial lesion' warrants excision for definitive classification.","Wide local excision aiming for a clear margin (conventionally around 1 cm) is the mainstay; enucleation predisposes to local recurrence.","Axillary node dissection is not indicated as spread of malignant phyllodes is haematogenous, chiefly to lung and bone, rather than lymphatic.","Malignant transformation is characterised by pure stromal sarcomatous overgrowth; heterologous elements such as liposarcoma may occur.","Recurrence can show progression to a higher grade, so long-term clinical and imaging surveillance is advised after excision."],"pathophysiology":"Phyllodes tumours are biphasic fibroepithelial neoplasms arising from the intralobular (periductal) hormone-responsive stroma. Unlike fibroadenomas, the stromal component is the neoplastic driver and shows variable hypercellularity, atypia and mitotic activity, producing the characteristic elongated leaf-like fronds lined by benign ductal and myoepithelial epithelium. Progressive accumulation of stromal genetic alterations, including MED12 mutations shared with fibroadenoma and additional changes such as TERT promoter mutations and later cytogenetic aberrations, underlies the spectrum from benign through borderline to frankly malignant lesions in which the stroma overgrows and behaves as a sarcoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Fibroadenoma, particularly the cellular or juvenile variant","Primary breast sarcoma","Metaplastic (spindle cell) carcinoma","Juvenile fibroadenoma with rapid growth","Benign breast hamartoma","Well-circumscribed high-grade carcinoma","Pseudoangiomatous stromal hyperplasia forming a mass"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Triple assessment with clinical examination, ultrasound and mammography","Ultrasound-guided core needle biopsy for tissue architecture","Breast MRI to delineate large lesions and plan margins","Definitive excisional histology with margin assessment and grading","Immunohistochemistry (CD34, beta-catenin, p53, Ki-67) to support classification","Staging chest imaging where malignant phyllodes is confirmed","Multidisciplinary review of borderline and malignant cases"],"redFlags":["Rapid interval enlargement of a previously stable breast mass","Very large tumour with overlying skin stretching, ulceration or venous prominence","Histological stromal overgrowth with high mitotic count and infiltrative margin","New respiratory or skeletal symptoms suggesting haematogenous metastasis","Repeated local recurrence with rising histological grade"],"prognosis":"Benign phyllodes tumours have an excellent outlook after complete excision, though local recurrence may occur if margins are inadequate. Borderline and malignant lesions carry progressively higher recurrence and, for malignant tumours, a meaningful risk of distant metastasis that markedly worsens survival.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling a phyllodes tumour as a fibroadenoma on core biopsy and undertreating with simple enucleation","Performing unnecessary axillary surgery, since spread is haematogenous rather than nodal","Failing to obtain an adequate margin, the principal driver of local recurrence","Assuming benign histology excludes recurrence and omitting follow-up surveillance","Overlooking sarcomatous heterologous elements that upgrade a lesion to malignant"],"management":["Perform wide local excision aiming for a clear margin as definitive treatment, with mastectomy for large lesions relative to breast size","Avoid enucleation, which leaves positive margins and predisposes to recurrence, and re-excise when margins are inadequate","Do not undertake axillary dissection, as malignant tumours metastasise haematogenously to lung and bone rather than to nodes","Consider adjuvant radiotherapy for malignant and selected borderline tumours or after re-excision","Reserve systemic sarcoma-type chemotherapy for large malignant or metastatic disease","Follow up with clinical examination and breast imaging for local recurrence, and stage the chest when malignant phyllodes is confirmed","Manage through a breast multidisciplinary team with specialist histological grading"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Breast Tumours (current edition)","Standard textbook of breast surgery and oncoplastic practice","National breast cancer and fibroepithelial lesion management guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"physical_examination_of_the_cardiovascular":{"name":"Physical Examination of the Cardiovascular System","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cardiac physical examination","Bedside cardiovascular assessment","Cardiovascular examination"],"chapter":246,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1859","clinicalPearls":["The jugular venous pressure is the single most important bedside measurement for estimating volume status; the internal jugular vein is preferred over the external because the latter is valved and not directly in line with the superior vena cava and right atrium.","Using the sternal angle (angle of Louis) as the reference point systematically underestimates central venous pressure, so it is better used to separate normal from elevated CVP than to quantify it; venous pulsations above the clavicle in the sitting position are clearly abnormal since the clavicle-to-right-atrium distance is at least 10 cm.","The venous waveform is biphasic in sinus rhythm whereas the carotid pulse is monophasic; the venous pulse is not easily obliterated by palpation and changes with posture and inspiration, helping distinguish it from the arterial pulse.","A cannon a wave occurs with atrioventricular dissociation when the right atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve; in a wide-complex tachycardia, cannon a waves identify the rhythm as ventricular in origin. The a wave is absent in atrial fibrillation.","Kussmaul's sign (a rise or lack of fall of the JVP with inspiration) is classically associated with constrictive pericarditis but also occurs with restrictive cardiomyopathy, massive pulmonary embolism, right ventricular infarction, and advanced left ventricular systolic heart failure, and is common after cardiac surgery.","Pulsus parvus et tardus (weak and delayed carotid pulse) suggests severe aortic stenosis, whereas a sharp rapidly collapsing carotid upstroke (Corrigan's or water-hammer pulse) points to chronic severe aortic regurgitation.","Pulsus paradoxus (inspiratory fall in systolic pressure >10 mmHg) is seen in pericardial tamponade but also occurs with massive pulmonary embolism, hemorrhagic shock, severe obstructive lung disease, and tension pneumothorax; it reflects exaggerated interventricular dependence.","Fixed splitting of S2 (a wide A2-P2 interval that does not vary with respiration) is characteristic of a secundum atrial septal defect, while reversed (paradoxical) splitting occurs with left bundle branch block, right ventricular pacing, severe aortic stenosis, hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, and acute myocardial ischemia.","The mitral valve prolapse click-murmur complex moves away from S1 with maneuvers that increase preload (squatting) and closer to S1 on standing, a dynamic behavior that helps distinguish it from other systolic events.","Lower extremity edema with an elevated JVP indicates volume overload (heart failure or constrictive pericarditis), whereas edema without jugular venous hypertension suggests hypoalbuminemia (nephrotic syndrome, liver failure), venous insufficiency, or dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker use.","Janeway lesions (nontender hemorrhagic macules on palms and soles) and Osler's nodes (tender nodules on finger/toe pads), along with splinter hemorrhages in the mid-nail bed, are peripheral signs of infective endocarditis.","An ankle-brachial index (ankle systolic pressure divided by the higher brachial systolic pressure) is a powerful predictor of long-term cardiovascular mortality; a >2% finger-to-toe oxygen saturation difference on pulse oximetry performs comparably for detecting lower extremity peripheral arterial disease."],"pathophysiology":"The cardiovascular examination derives diagnostic and prognostic information from the mechanical and hemodynamic consequences of cardiac and vascular disease. The jugular venous waveform reflects right atrial pressure dynamics: the a wave is presystolic right atrial contraction, the x descent is atrial relaxation, the c wave is the systolic bulging of the closed tricuspid valve, the v wave is atrial filling during ventricular systole, and the y descent is right atrial emptying after tricuspid opening. Reduced right ventricular compliance accentuates the a wave; tricuspid regurgitation accentuates the v wave with a rapid y descent and progressively 'ventricularizes' the waveform; obstruction to right ventricular inflow (tricuspid stenosis, tamponade) blunts or prolongs the y descent. Arterial pulse contour depends on stroke volume, ejection velocity, vascular compliance, and systemic vascular resistance, so fixed outflow obstruction blunts and delays the upstroke while wide pulse pressure states produce a bounding collapsing pulse. Heart sound intensity reflects valve leaflet excursion and mobility, ventricular contractility, and the PR interval, while the distance between the stethoscope and the cardiac event (obesity, lung disease, effusion) attenuates all sounds. Splitting of S2 depends on the relative timing of aortic and pulmonic valve closure, which is altered by conduction delays, shunts, and outflow obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic stenosis (pulsus parvus et tardus, sustained apex, soft A2)","Aortic regurgitation (water-hammer pulse, wide pulse pressure, low diastolic pressure)","Tricuspid regurgitation (prominent v wave, systolic hepatic pulsations)","Constrictive pericarditis (Kussmaul's sign, prominent rapid y descent, pericardial knock)","Pericardial tamponade (pulsus paradoxus, blunted y descent)","Atrial septal defect (fixed split S2)","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (bisferiens pulse, triple apical cadence)","Mitral valve prolapse (nonejection click and dynamic murmur)","Aortic coarctation / dissection (interarm or arm-leg blood pressure differential, femoral delay)"],"infectionMimics":["Infective endocarditis presenting with peripheral stigmata (Janeway lesions, Osler's nodes, splinter hemorrhages, splenomegaly, clubbing, Roth-type funduscopic findings)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Connective tissue disease syndromes producing thoracic cage and skeletal abnormalities (Marfan, Loeys-Dietz, ankylosing spondylitis, straight back syndrome) that suggest valvular or aortic disease","Hemochromatosis (bronze skin) as a cause of systolic heart failure","Sarcoidosis (lupus pernio, erythema nodosum) as a cause of dilated cardiomyopathy with conduction disease","Hereditary lipid disorders (tendon, eruptive, or palmar xanthomas; lipemia retinalis) signaling atherosclerotic risk","Noncardiac causes of dyspnea such as obstructive lung disease (barrel chest, pursed-lip breathing)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Noninvasive imaging (echocardiography, including hand-held ultrasound) to confirm structural cardiac disease","Screening abdominal ultrasound when an abdominal aortic aneurysm is suspected by palpation","24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring to detect white coat and masked hypertension","Ankle-brachial index measurement for peripheral arterial disease and cardiovascular risk","Funduscopic examination of the microvasculature in atherosclerosis, hypertension, diabetes, suspected endocarditis, and acute visual change","Allen's test before radial artery instrumentation","Pulse oximetry comparing finger and toe oxygen saturation for lower extremity peripheral arterial disease"],"redFlags":["Markedly elevated JVP that does not pulsate with a dusky cyanotic head and neck, suggesting superior vena cava syndrome","Pulsus paradoxus with elevated JVP and blunted y descent, suggesting pericardial tamponade","An interarm systolic blood pressure difference >10 mmHg or femoral pulse delay, raising concern for aortic dissection or coarctation","Differential cyanosis of the lower extremities with a large patent ductus and pulmonary hypertension (right-to-left shunting)","New peripheral embolic stigmata with fever pointing toward infective endocarditis","Orthostatic hypotension without compensatory tachycardia, suggesting autonomic insufficiency"],"prognosis":"Bedside findings carry independent prognostic weight. In heart failure, an elevated JVP and a third heart sound predict the pulmonary artery wedge pressure and are associated with higher risk of heart failure hospitalization and death; an estimated right atrial pressure >10 mmHg predicted a wedge pressure >22 mmHg with high positive value in advanced heart failure. The ankle-brachial index is a powerful predictor of long-term cardiovascular mortality, and higher ambulatory systolic pressures independently predict cardiovascular events and all-cause death. Pulsus alternans indicates severe left ventricular systolic dysfunction, and when accompanied by electrocardiographic T-wave alternans the arrhythmic risk appears increased.","pitfalls":["Using an inappropriately small blood pressure cuff overestimates pressure, while an oversized cuff underestimates it; the bladder should be about 80% of arm length and 40% of arm circumference.","Examining the carotid upstrokes simultaneously, or before auscultating for a bruit, and applying heavy pressure can precipitate carotid hypersensitivity and syncope in susceptible elderly patients.","A cervical bruit correlates poorly with the degree of carotid stenosis, and its absence does not exclude significant obstruction.","Palpation alone is insufficiently accurate to diagnose an abdominal aortic aneurysm, and sensitivity falls with increasing body size.","Homans' sign is neither sensitive nor specific for deep venous thrombosis.","Arcus senilis lacks specificity as a marker of coronary heart disease risk.","Physician-measured blood pressures tend to be higher than nurse- or self-recorded values, contributing to white coat hypertension; conversely masked hypertension can be missed when clinic pressures are normal despite target organ damage.","Heart and pulse findings can be misleading in low cardiac output states and in stiffened arteries from aging, hypertension, or peripheral arterial disease."],"management":["Perform a structured bedside cardiovascular examination of the jugular venous pressure and waveform, arterial pulse character, precordium and heart sounds to direct diagnosis and treatment","Use the jugular venous pressure to assess volume status and guide diuretic therapy and fluid management in heart failure","Interpret arterial pulse abnormalities, acting on pulsus parvus et tardus by evaluating for aortic stenosis and on a collapsing water-hammer pulse by evaluating for aortic regurgitation","Confirm structural findings with echocardiography and targeted further imaging","Track prognostic bedside signs such as a persistently elevated jugular venous pressure and a third heart sound to identify higher-risk patients needing intensified therapy and follow-up","Apply adjunctive bedside assessments including the ankle-brachial index, funduscopy and ambulatory blood pressure monitoring as clinically indicated","Refer to cardiology for confirmed valvular or structural heart disease"],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scale; specific findings convey severity within individual conditions (e.g., pulsus parvus et tardus and a soft A2 indicate severe aortic stenosis; the A2-opening snap interval in mitral stenosis is inversely proportional to the left atrial-left ventricular gradient; a JVP distance >4.5 cm above the sternal angle at 30 degrees, or any venous pulsation above the clavicle in the sitting position, is abnormal; orthostatic hypotension is a fall in systolic pressure >20 mmHg or diastolic >10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1859","Harrison 22e p.1860","Harrison 22e p.1861","Harrison 22e p.1862","Harrison 22e p.1863","Harrison 22e p.1864","Harrison 22e p.1865"],"crossLinks":[]},"physiologic_and_nonphysiologic_sinus_rhyth":{"name":"Physiologic and Nonphysiologic Sinus Rhythm","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sinus tachycardia","Sinus arrhythmia","Sinus bradycardia","Inappropriate sinus tachycardia","IST","Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome","POTS"],"chapter":254,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1935","clinicalPearls":["The sinus node sits at the lateral-superior junction of the right atrium and superior vena cava, within the upper portion of the crista terminalis where the smooth posterior wall (from the sinus venosus) meets the trabeculated anterior right atrium.","Normal sinus rhythm runs between roughly 60 and 100 beats/min, with a sinus P wave that is upright in leads II, III, and aVF, negative in aVR, and initially positive/biphasic in V1, reflecting an inferiorly and leftward-directed frontal-plane axis.","Sinus arrhythmia is a common, usually asymptomatic finding defined as a sinus-origin rhythm with P-P interval variation greater than 120 ms; when P-wave morphology also varies, premature atrial contractions rather than true sinus arrhythmia are the likely cause.","Three patterns of sinus arrhythmia are described: respirophasic (vagal tone reflexively falls in inspiration and is restored in expiration), ventriculophasic (seen with heart block or after a PVC, where an interposed ventricular beat shortens the P-P interval), and nonphasic (P-P variation unrelated to cardiac or respiratory cycle).","Sinus tachycardia (>100 beats/min) arises from sympathetic stimulation and/or vagal withdrawal; the earliest activation focus shifts more leftward toward the superior septal crista terminalis, producing taller inferior-lead P waves than in resting sinus rhythm.","Sinus bradycardia is defined as a rate <60 beats/min but can be entirely normal during sleep and in physically fit individuals.","A gradual rate rise with an identifiable trigger (such as exertion) favors sinus tachycardia, whereas abrupt onset and offset favor focal atrial tachycardia originating near the sinus node.","Inappropriate sinus tachycardia (IST) is an uncommon disorder of autonomic dysregulation in which the sinus rate climbs at rest or out of proportion to physiologic demand; it most often affects women in their third or fourth decade.","POTS is defined by symptomatic sinus tachycardia on standing, with a rate increase of at least 30 beats/min or to >120 beats/min within 10 minutes of standing in the absence of hypotension.","POTS can follow a viral illness and may resolve spontaneously over 3-12 months; a POTS-like autonomic picture has been described in prolonged post-COVID-19 (long COVID) syndromes.","Distinguishing IST from POTS is clinically critical because they require fundamentally different management approaches; conflating them risks worsening symptoms.","Asymptomatic sinus arrhythmia is physiologic, is not considered pathogenic, and does not warrant further cardiac evaluation."],"pathophysiology":"Sinus rhythm originates from spontaneous depolarization of pacemaker cells in the sinus node at the right atrium-SVC junction within the crista terminalis. Heart rate is modulated by the autonomic balance between sympathetic input (which accelerates and shifts the activation focus) and parasympathetic (vagal) input (which slows the node). Sinus arrhythmia reflects normal beat-to-beat shifts in autonomic tone, classically respiratory-linked vagal modulation. Sinus tachycardia results from increased sympathetic drive and/or vagal withdrawal raising the rate of spontaneous nodal depolarization; when this is an appropriate response to exercise, stress, or illness it is physiologic. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia is thought to stem from an imbalance between sympathetic and parasympathetic input to the node, altered membrane automaticity of sinus node cells, or both, though the precise mechanism remains poorly defined. POTS reflects orthostatic autonomic dysfunction, sometimes postviral.","additionalDifferentials":["Focal atrial tachycardia arising near the sinus node (abrupt onset/offset favors AT)","Premature atrial contractions causing rhythm irregularity (varying P-wave morphology)","Inappropriate sinus tachycardia versus appropriate physiologic sinus tachycardia","Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome","Anxiety or panic disorder presenting with sinus tachycardia"],"infectionMimics":["Fever or systemic infection driving appropriate (physiologic) sinus tachycardia","Postviral autonomic dysfunction producing a POTS-like picture, including post-COVID-19 (long COVID)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anemia","Hyperthyroidism and other thyroid dysfunction","Dehydration / volume depletion","Pulmonary embolism","Pheochromocytoma","Decompensated heart failure or valvular disease","Anxiety / panic attack","Stimulant or drug effect (caffeine, sympathomimetics, cocaine, amphetamines, beta-blocker withdrawal)"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG to confirm sinus P-wave morphology and axis","Ambulatory / outpatient telemetry monitoring to distinguish gradual sinus tachycardia from paroxysmal atrial tachycardia","Thyroid function testing to exclude hyperthyroidism as a reversible cause","Hemoglobin / evaluation for anemia","Orthostatic (supine-to-standing) heart rate and blood pressure assessment when POTS is suspected","Invasive electrophysiology study in selected cases where the ECG distinction remains unclear"],"redFlags":["Sinus tachycardia accompanied by hypotension, impaired consciousness, angina, or heart failure, suggesting a serious underlying cause requiring urgent attention","Unexplained or persistent sinus tachycardia mandating exclusion of pulmonary embolism, myocardial ischemia, and structural/functional cardiac disease before labeling it nonphysiologic","Syncope or disabling palpitations accompanying tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia as a possible marker of underlying systemic illness (e.g., infection, malignancy, endocrine disorder) or drug toxicity"],"prognosis":"Sinus arrhythmia is benign and physiologic, requiring no treatment. Physiologic sinus tachycardia resolves with correction of the underlying condition and often needs no specific therapy. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia tends to be a chronic, often disabling condition that responds poorly to therapy and may be difficult to control long term. POTS frequently improves over time, and postviral POTS may resolve spontaneously within 3-12 months.","pitfalls":["Mislabeling rhythm irregularity with varying P-wave morphology as sinus arrhythmia when it is actually premature atrial contractions.","Diagnosing nonphysiologic sinus tachycardia before excluding reversible drivers such as anemia, thyroid disease, and pulmonary embolism.","Failing to separate inappropriate sinus tachycardia from POTS, since blood-pressure-raising strategies appropriate for POTS are inappropriate for IST, and sinus node modification is ineffective for POTS and may worsen its nontachycardia features.","Pursuing catheter-based sinus node modification for IST, which often yields poor long-term symptom control and can lead to symptomatic sinus bradycardia, sinus arrest, or chronotropic incompetence requiring a permanent pacemaker.","Overtreating an asymptomatic, constitutionally elevated resting or exercise sinus rate with no identifiable underlying cause."],"management":["Reassure and observe benign physiological sinus arrhythmia and asymptomatic sinus bradycardia in fit or sleeping individuals, with no specific treatment","In physiological sinus tachycardia, identify and treat the underlying driver such as fever, sepsis, pain, hypovolaemia, anaemia or hyperthyroidism rather than the rate itself","Treat symptomatic inappropriate sinus tachycardia with a beta-blocker or the sinus-node current inhibitor ivabradine","Manage postural orthostatic tachycardia with increased salt and fluid intake, compression garments, graded exercise reconditioning and, if needed, a beta-blocker, ivabradine, fludrocortisone or midodrine","Correct reversible contributors including thyroid dysfunction and anaemia","Confirm the rhythm and distinguish it from paroxysmal atrial tachycardia with a 12-lead ECG and ambulatory telemetry","Refer for electrophysiology assessment and consideration of sinus-node modification in refractory, disabling inappropriate sinus tachycardia","Review culprit drugs and consider pacing for symptomatic pathological sinus bradycardia or sinus node dysfunction"],"severityClassification":"Sinus tachycardia is rate-defined as >100 beats/min and sinus bradycardia as <60 beats/min, with normal sinus rhythm spanning 60-100 beats/min. POTS is defined diagnostically by an orthostatic heart-rate rise of >=30 beats/min (or to >120 beats/min) within 10 minutes of standing without hypotension. Sinus arrhythmia is defined by P-P interval variability >120 ms of sinus origin.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1935","Harrison 22e p.1936","Harrison 22e p.1937"],"crossLinks":[]},"phytophotodermatitis":{"name":"Phytophotodermatitis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["plant-induced phototoxic dermatitis","meadow dermatitis","dermatitis bullosa striata pratensis","margarita photodermatitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A phototoxic (not allergic) reaction that occurs when skin contaminated with plant furocoumarins (psoralens) is subsequently exposed to ultraviolet A light","Common sources include limes and other citrus, celery, parsnip, figs and giant hogweed, and the classic 'margarita' pattern follows handling limes in the sun","The eruption has a characteristic streaky, bizarre or drip-like configuration that mirrors where the plant juice ran or was smeared on the skin","There is an acute phase of erythema and blistering within a day or two of exposure, followed by striking, often long-lasting post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation","Because it is phototoxic, it can affect anyone given sufficient furocoumarin and ultraviolet A, and it does not require prior sensitisation","Handprint-shaped marks on a child, for example from an adult with lime or fruit juice on their hands, can be mistaken for non-accidental injury or bruising","Management is largely supportive with skin cleansing, treatment of the acute reaction, sun avoidance of affected areas and reassurance that the pigmentation fades slowly"],"pathophysiology":"Phytophotodermatitis is a phototoxic reaction in which furocoumarins (psoralens) transferred to the skin from plants absorb ultraviolet A radiation and, in the excited state, form covalent adducts with DNA and damage cell membranes, causing direct keratinocyte injury, inflammation and blistering. This is the same class of psoralen-plus-ultraviolet-A photochemical reaction exploited therapeutically in PUVA. Subsequent stimulation of melanocytes produces the characteristic and often prolonged post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation in the exact distribution of plant contact.","additionalDifferentials":["Allergic contact dermatitis, including plant (Compositae) allergy","Photoallergic contact dermatitis","Chemical or thermal burn","Bullous drug photosensitivity","Non-accidental injury or bruising, particularly in children","Berloque dermatitis from fragrance-borne psoralens"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["A careful history of plant, fruit or vegetable contact followed by sun exposure, and the site distribution","Recognition of the streaky or handprint pattern that strongly suggests the diagnosis","Photopatch testing only where a photoallergic contact dermatitis is being considered","Skin biopsy in atypical cases, showing phototoxic changes","Consideration of safeguarding assessment when handprint marks in a child raise concern","Review of medications and topical products for other photosensitisers if the cause is unclear"],"redFlags":["Extensive blistering or involvement of a large body surface requiring burns-type care","Handprint or bruise-like marks in a child prompting a safeguarding assessment","Systemic photosensitivity suggesting a drug cause or an underlying porphyria","Ocular or mucosal involvement from plant sap contact"],"prognosis":"The acute phototoxic reaction settles over days to a couple of weeks, but the resulting post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation is often marked and can take many weeks to months to fade; the condition is entirely preventable by avoiding the combination of furocoumarin contact and sun exposure, and it does not recur without renewed exposure.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the streaky pigmentation as bruising and raising unwarranted safeguarding concerns, or conversely dismissing genuine concerns","Labelling it an allergic reaction and mismanaging it, when it is a phototoxic process affecting anyone","Failing to elicit the plant or fruit exposure history that explains the bizarre pattern","Not warning patients that the hyperpigmentation will persist long after the initial reaction resolves"],"management":["Wash residual plant furocoumarin from the skin promptly and remove the affected area from further sun exposure to halt the phototoxic reaction","Enforce photoprotection with sun avoidance, broad-spectrum UVA sunscreen and protective clothing","Treat the acute erythema and blistering with topical corticosteroids, cool compresses and simple wound care for blisters","Manage the prolonged post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation with reassurance and continued sun protection, considering topical depigmenting agents in selected cases","Provide analgesia and symptomatic care during the acute phase","Educate on prevention by avoiding the combination of furocoumarin contact (limes, citrus, celery, parsnip, figs, giant hogweed) with sunlight","Consider a safeguarding assessment when handprint-shaped marks in a child raise concern about non-accidental injury"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","British Photodermatology Group guidance on phototoxic and photosensitivity disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"piebaldism":{"name":"piebaldism","system":"Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["partial albinism","piebald trait"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant congenital leukoderma caused chiefly by loss-of-function mutations in the KIT proto-oncogene, with a minority due to SLUG/SNAI2 defects impairing melanoblast migration from the neural crest.","The hallmark is a stable white forelock (poliosis) present at birth in most affected individuals, overlying a triangular or diamond-shaped depigmented patch of the central forehead.","Depigmented patches favour the central forehead, ventral trunk, and mid-extremities, while the hands, feet, back, and a median dorsal strip are characteristically spared.","Islands of normally pigmented or hyperpigmented macules lie within and at the borders of the amelanotic patches, a feature that helps distinguish piebaldism from vitiligo.","Lesions are congenital and remain remarkably stable in relative size and distribution throughout life, expanding only in proportion to body growth.","Histology of affected skin shows an absence or marked reduction of melanocytes, mirroring the failed migration and survival of melanoblasts.","Overlap with Waardenburg syndrome should be considered; the presence of sensorineural deafness, dystopia canthorum, or heterochromia iridis points away from isolated piebaldism.","Genotype-phenotype correlation exists: mutations affecting the KIT tyrosine kinase domain tend to produce more extensive depigmentation than those in the extracellular ligand-binding region."],"pathophysiology":"Piebaldism arises from defective embryonic development, migration, proliferation, and survival of melanoblasts derived from the neural crest. The commonest cause is a heterozygous loss-of-function mutation in the KIT gene, which encodes the transmembrane tyrosine kinase receptor for stem cell factor (KIT ligand); reduced KIT signalling impairs the dorsolateral migration and survival of melanoblasts destined for the epidermis, so the affected skin regions never acquire melanocytes. Haploinsufficiency accounts for the dominant inheritance and the correlation between mutation severity and the extent of depigmentation. A smaller subset results from mutations in SNAI2 (SLUG), a transcription factor downstream of KIT signalling. Because the ventral and central regions are the last to be reached by migrating melanoblasts, they are preferentially left amelanotic, explaining the characteristic pattern of sparing of the extremities' extremes and a mid-dorsal strip.","additionalDifferentials":["Waardenburg syndrome (types I-IV)","Segmental or focal vitiligo","Naevus depigmentosus","Tuberous sclerosis ash-leaf macules","Hypomelanosis of Ito","Naevus anaemicus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wood's lamp examination confirming true depigmentation with sharply demarcated borders and retained pigmented islands","Molecular genetic testing for KIT and SNAI2 mutations","Audiological assessment to exclude Waardenburg syndrome when clinical overlap exists","Ophthalmological review for iris pigmentary changes and dystopia canthorum","Skin biopsy showing absent or reduced melanocytes in depigmented areas (rarely required)","Detailed three-generation family pedigree"],"redFlags":["Associated sensorineural hearing loss suggesting Waardenburg syndrome rather than isolated piebaldism","Progressive or acquired depigmentation, which argues against a congenital stable leukoderma","Broad nasal root, dystopia canthorum, or heterochromia indicating a syndromic diagnosis","Features of Hirschsprung disease or constipation raising suspicion of Waardenburg type IV"],"prognosis":"Piebaldism is a benign, non-progressive condition with a normal life expectancy, and the depigmented areas remain cosmetically stable throughout life. The principal long-term concern is photoprotection of amelanotic skin, since these areas lack the melanin defence against ultraviolet-induced damage.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling stable congenital piebaldism as vitiligo, leading to inappropriate immunomodulatory therapy","Overlooking the pigmented islands within depigmented patches, a clue that distinguishes piebaldism from vitiligo","Failing to screen for deafness and other Waardenburg features when a white forelock is present","Attributing depigmentation to trauma or inflammation despite its clearly congenital onset","Assuming amelanotic skin is protected from sunburn and neglecting rigorous photoprotection advice"],"management":["No repigmenting drug therapy exists; rigorous photoprotection of amelanotic skin with broad-spectrum sunscreen, protective clothing and sun avoidance is the mainstay to prevent ultraviolet damage","Offer cosmetic camouflage and skin-tone-matched cover creams for the white forelock and depigmented patches","For stable, cosmetically troublesome patches, consider surgical repigmentation with autologous melanocyte-keratinocyte transplantation or minigrafting/punch grafting","Perform long-term skin surveillance of amelanotic areas for actinic damage and cutaneous malignancy given the absent melanin defence","Exclude Waardenburg syndrome with audiological and ophthalmological assessment when there is deafness, dystopia canthorum or iris pigmentary change","Provide genetic counselling for the autosomal dominant KIT/SNAI2 inheritance and construct a three-generation pedigree for family screening","Reassure regarding the benign, non-progressive nature, with lesions stable in relative size throughout life"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine, current edition","Bolognia Dermatology, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"pierre_robin_sequence":{"name":"Pierre Robin sequence","system":"Genetics / Craniofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Robin sequence","Pierre Robin syndrome","Pierre Robin malformation sequence","PRS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the triad of micrognathia, glossoptosis (posterior tongue displacement), and consequent upper airway obstruction, frequently with a U-shaped cleft palate","It is a sequence rather than a syndrome, meaning one initiating event (mandibular hypoplasia) cascades into the other features","Airway obstruction is often positional and may improve with prone or lateral positioning in milder cases","Feeding difficulty is common and closely linked to the degree of airway compromise","It may be isolated or part of a broader syndrome, most commonly Stickler syndrome, so systemic and ophthalmological review is important","Mandibular catch-up growth over the first years often improves airway and feeding without major surgery in milder cases","Severity ranges from mild positional obstruction to critical airway compromise requiring surgical intervention"],"pathophysiology":"The sequence is initiated by early hypoplasia of the mandible. The small jaw leaves insufficient room in the oral cavity, so the tongue is displaced posteriorly and superiorly (glossoptosis), which in turn obstructs the airway and, during palatal shelf fusion, mechanically prevents the shelves meeting, producing the characteristic wide U-shaped cleft palate. The initiating mandibular deficiency may result from intrinsic developmental disturbance of the first branchial arch or from extrinsic constraint such as oligohydramnios limiting jaw movement in utero. Because a single early anomaly drives the downstream features, the condition is classified as a malformation sequence, and it may occur in isolation or as a component of a recognisable syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Stickler syndrome (commonest associated syndrome)","Treacher Collins syndrome","22q11.2 deletion (velocardiofacial) syndrome","Cornelia de Lange syndrome","Isolated cleft palate without micrognathia","Neuromuscular causes of glossoptosis and hypotonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sleep study or overnight oximetry to quantify obstructive events and desaturation","Flexible nasendoscopy to characterise the level and mechanism of airway obstruction","Feeding and swallowing assessment with monitoring of weight gain","Ophthalmological review and genetic evaluation to identify Stickler and other syndromes","Echocardiography where a syndromic association is suspected","Molecular or chromosomal testing guided by associated anomalies"],"redFlags":["Severe or worsening obstructive apnoea with desaturation or cor pulmonale","Failure to thrive from inability to feed safely","Cyanotic episodes or respiratory distress unrelieved by positioning","Recurrent aspiration","Signs of a wider syndrome requiring urgent multisystem evaluation"],"prognosis":"Isolated Robin sequence generally has a favourable outlook as mandibular growth improves the airway over time, whereas syndromic cases and those with severe obstruction carry greater morbidity and depend on the associated condition.","pitfalls":["Treating it as a fixed syndrome rather than recognising an initiating sequence","Failing to look for an underlying syndrome, especially Stickler","Underestimating airway obstruction because the infant appears settled when prone","Overlooking feeding failure as a marker of significant obstruction","Delaying escalation when non-surgical measures are insufficient"],"management":["Secure the airway with prone or lateral positioning as first-line for milder positional obstruction, escalating to a nasopharyngeal airway when needed","For severe or persistent obstruction, offer surgical airway interventions such as tongue-lip adhesion, mandibular distraction osteogenesis, or tracheostomy as a last resort","Support feeding with specialised teats, positioning and nasogastric tube feeding, monitoring weight gain closely as it tracks airway compromise","Undertake staged surgical repair of the U-shaped cleft palate once airway and growth allow","Quantify obstruction with sleep study or overnight oximetry and characterise the level with flexible nasendoscopy to guide intervention","Coordinate multidisciplinary craniofacial, ENT, feeding and clinical genetics input, screening for Stickler syndrome with ophthalmology review","Reassess over the first years as mandibular catch-up growth often relieves obstruction, reserving intervention for those who fail to improve"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Robin Sequence and Stickler Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"pierson_syndrome":{"name":"Pierson Syndrome","system":"Renal / Glomerular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Microcoria-congenital nephrosis syndrome","LAMB2-related disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nephrology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic mutations in LAMB2, the gene encoding the laminin beta-2 chain.","Classically combines congenital nephrotic syndrome with distinctive ocular anomalies, most notably fixed narrow pupils (microcoria).","Kidney disease usually manifests as congenital or early infantile nephrotic syndrome with diffuse mesangial sclerosis on biopsy.","Ocular features extend beyond microcoria to include lens and retinal abnormalities and later visual impairment.","Neurodevelopmental delay and hypotonia are frequent, partly reflecting laminin beta-2 expression at the neuromuscular junction and in the central nervous system.","Milder LAMB2 variants can produce attenuated phenotypes with later-onset proteinuria and subtler eye findings.","Renal failure typically develops in infancy or early childhood, often requiring dialysis and transplantation.","Genetic confirmation of biallelic LAMB2 mutations establishes the diagnosis and enables family counselling."],"pathophysiology":"Pierson syndrome arises from loss-of-function mutations in LAMB2, which encodes the laminin beta-2 chain, a component of laminin-521 and related heterotrimers that are critical structural elements of several specialised basement membranes. Laminin beta-2 is highly expressed in the glomerular basement membrane, where it anchors podocytes and maintains the integrity of the filtration barrier; its deficiency destabilises this barrier and produces congenital nephrotic syndrome with the histological pattern of diffuse mesangial sclerosis. The same laminin chain is essential in the intraocular basement membranes and in the developing pupil and lens, so its absence causes microcoria and other ocular malformations, and it is a key element of the synaptic basement membrane at the neuromuscular junction and within parts of the nervous system, accounting for the hypotonia and neurodevelopmental impairment. The severity of the phenotype broadly tracks the degree of residual laminin beta-2 function, with complete loss producing the full classic triad in early infancy and hypomorphic variants yielding milder, later-onset renal and ocular disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital nephrotic syndrome of the Finnish type (NPHS1 mutations)","Denys-Drash syndrome (WT1 mutations with diffuse mesangial sclerosis and genital anomalies)","Isolated diffuse mesangial sclerosis","Galloway-Mowat syndrome (nephrosis with microcephaly and neurological features)","Other podocyte gene disorders such as NPHS2-related nephrotic syndrome","Congenital infections causing nephrotic syndrome in infancy","Frasier syndrome (WT1-related)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing for biallelic LAMB2 mutations","Renal biopsy demonstrating diffuse mesangial sclerosis where performed","Quantification of proteinuria and serum albumin","Detailed ophthalmological examination for microcoria, lens and retinal anomalies","Neurodevelopmental assessment and cranial imaging where indicated","Renal function monitoring for progression to kidney failure","Parental carrier testing and genetic counselling"],"redFlags":["Congenital or early infantile nephrotic syndrome with severe hypoalbuminaemia and oedema","Fixed narrow pupils or other structural eye anomalies in an infant with proteinuria","Rapid progression to kidney failure in infancy","Profound hypotonia or neurodevelopmental regression","Complications of severe nephrotic syndrome such as thrombosis or infection"],"prognosis":"Classic Pierson syndrome carries a poor prognosis, with early progression to kidney failure and significant ocular and neurological morbidity, and some infants do not survive early childhood. Milder LAMB2 phenotypes have a more variable, less severe course.","pitfalls":["Attributing congenital nephrotic syndrome solely to NPHS1 or WT1 and overlooking LAMB2","Missing microcoria or other ocular signs that point to the specific diagnosis","Assuming immunosuppression will control a genetically determined nephrotic syndrome","Underestimating the neurological component and its impact on development","Failing to offer genetic counselling and carrier testing to families"],"management":["Manage the congenital nephrotic syndrome intensively with intravenous albumin infusions, diuretics and high-protein nutritional support to counter hypoalbuminaemia and oedema","Reduce proteinuria with an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker, and in refractory cases consider unilateral or bilateral nephrectomy to control massive protein losses","Prevent complications of the nephrotic state with thromboprophylaxis, immunisation and prophylaxis against encapsulated-organism infection","Plan for progression to kidney failure with dialysis and renal transplantation, which corrects the renal but not the ocular or neurological features","Refer for ophthalmology assessment and management of microcoria, lens and retinal anomalies to preserve vision","Provide developmental therapy and neurological support for hypotonia and neurodevelopmental delay","Offer genetic counselling for the autosomal recessive LAMB2 inheritance with testing and screening of at-risk siblings"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Pediatric Nephrology (standard reference)","Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (standard reference)","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"pigment_dispersion_glaucoma":{"name":"Pigmentary glaucoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pigment dispersion glaucoma","pigmentary dispersion syndrome","pigment dispersion syndrome","PDS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from liberation of pigment granules from the posterior iris that deposit throughout the anterior segment and clog the trabecular meshwork, raising intraocular pressure","Typically affects young, myopic adults, more often men, and may be asymptomatic or cause blurring and haloes after exercise or pupillary dilation","A vertical spindle-shaped deposit of pigment on the corneal endothelium (Krukenberg spindle) is a classic sign","Mid-peripheral radial iris transillumination defects reflect loss of pigment where the iris chafes against the lens zonules","Gonioscopy shows a heavily and homogeneously pigmented trabecular meshwork, often with a concave (posteriorly bowed) peripheral iris","Reverse pupillary block, in which the iris is pushed back against the zonules, is thought to drive the pigment-releasing chafing","Treatment lowers pressure with topical agents; laser peripheral iridotomy may flatten the concave iris and reduce chafing, and laser trabeculoplasty can be effective though it may cause pressure spikes in heavily pigmented angles","Not all patients with pigment dispersion develop glaucoma; the term pigmentary glaucoma applies once pressure-related optic-nerve damage occurs"],"pathophysiology":"In pigment dispersion the peripheral iris bows posteriorly and rubs against the anterior lens zonular bundles during physiological pupil movement, mechanically stripping melanin granules from the posterior iris pigment epithelium. A postulated reverse pupillary block, where aqueous is trapped in the anterior chamber and presses the iris backward, exaggerates this iridozonular contact. Liberated pigment circulates in the aqueous and is deposited on the corneal endothelium as a Krukenberg spindle, on the lens and iris, and within the trabecular meshwork. Accumulated pigment and associated trabecular cell dysfunction reduce aqueous outflow, and in susceptible eyes the resulting rise in intraocular pressure produces glaucomatous optic-nerve damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Pseudoexfoliation glaucoma","Primary open-angle glaucoma","Pigment released by uveitis or intraocular tumour","Post-traumatic angle recession glaucoma","Pigment dispersion associated with a malpositioned intraocular lens","Pigment liberation after intraocular surgery or laser"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gonioscopy to document dense homogeneous trabecular pigmentation and iris configuration","Slit-lamp examination for a Krukenberg spindle and iris transillumination defects","Applanation tonometry, ideally with post-exercise or post-dilation testing to detect pigment-release pressure spikes","OCT of the retinal nerve fibre layer to assess for glaucomatous damage","Automated perimetry to detect and monitor field loss","Anterior segment imaging to demonstrate the concave peripheral iris where needed"],"redFlags":["Marked pressure spikes after exercise or pupil dilation that can cause acute symptomatic rises","Established optic-nerve cupping and field loss in a young myopic patient","Very high trabecular pigmentation predisposing to exaggerated pressure spikes after laser trabeculoplasty","Progressive damage despite apparently modest baseline pressure"],"prognosis":"Many patients with pigment dispersion syndrome never develop glaucoma, and pigment release often lessens with age as the iris becomes less concave, but those who progress to pigmentary glaucoma can have aggressive pressure elevation in young eyes requiring lifelong monitoring and treatment.","pitfalls":["Assuming pigment dispersion syndrome always progresses to glaucoma or, conversely, discharging patients who need long-term surveillance","Missing exercise- or dilation-induced pressure spikes on isolated clinic readings","Applying high-energy laser trabeculoplasty to a heavily pigmented meshwork and provoking a pressure spike","Confusing dense trabecular pigment with pseudoexfoliation material","Overlooking the diagnosis in a young myope with unexplained ocular hypertension"],"management":["Lower intraocular pressure with topical agents, typically a prostaglandin analogue first-line, adding beta-blockers, alpha-agonists or carbonic anhydrase inhibitors as needed","Consider laser peripheral iridotomy to abolish reverse pupillary block and flatten the concave iris, reducing iridozonular chafing","Use laser trabeculoplasty cautiously in the heavily pigmented meshwork, watching for post-laser pressure spikes","Advise avoidance or caution around vigorous exercise and pupillary dilation, which can precipitate pigment-shower pressure spikes","Reserve incisional surgery such as trabeculectomy or drainage devices for pressure uncontrolled on medical and laser therapy","Monitor lifelong with tonometry, gonioscopy, optic nerve OCT and automated perimetry to detect glaucomatous progression","Counsel that pigment release and pressure often lessen with age as the iris becomes less concave, but young progressing eyes need close surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"pigmentary_glaucoma":{"name":"pigmentary glaucoma","system":"ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pigment dispersion glaucoma","pigmentary dispersion syndrome with glaucoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically affects young, myopic Caucasian men in the third to fourth decade, in contrast to the older demographic of primary open-angle glaucoma.","The classic triad is Krukenberg spindle (vertical pigment deposition on corneal endothelium), mid-peripheral iris transillumination defects in a radial spoke-like pattern, and heavy homogeneous trabecular meshwork pigmentation.","Reverse pupillary block, in which the peripheral iris bows posteriorly against the zonules and lens, is the leading mechanism for iridozonular chafing and pigment liberation.","Exercise or pupillary dilatation can precipitate a shower of pigment with an acute intraocular pressure spike, sometimes with corneal oedema and blurring.","Not all patients with pigment dispersion syndrome develop glaucoma; conversion to glaucoma occurs in a minority over time and warrants ongoing surveillance.","Laser peripheral iridotomy aims to flatten the iris and abolish reverse pupillary block, though evidence for preventing progression is inconsistent.","Pilocarpine addresses both pressure and iris configuration by pulling the iris away from the zonules, but is poorly tolerated in young myopes because of induced myopia and retinal detachment risk.","Laser trabeculoplasty is often effective given the heavily pigmented meshwork, but lower energy is prudent to avoid a marked post-laser pressure spike."],"pathophysiology":"Mechanical rubbing of the posterior iris pigment epithelium against the anterior packets of lens zonules liberates melanin granules into the aqueous. A posteriorly bowed, concave peripheral iris configuration, driven by reverse pupillary block where aqueous is trapped in the anterior chamber and raises its pressure relative to the posterior chamber, brings the iris into contact with the zonules during physiological pupil movement. Dispersed pigment is carried by aqueous convection currents and deposited throughout the anterior segment, most importantly within the trabecular meshwork. Phagocytosis of pigment by trabecular endothelial cells, subsequent cell death, meshwork collapse and sclerosis progressively reduce aqueous outflow facility, elevating intraocular pressure and producing a glaucomatous optic neuropathy identical in appearance to primary open-angle glaucoma.","additionalDifferentials":["primary open-angle glaucoma","pseudoexfoliation glaucoma","uveitic glaucoma with pigment dispersion","post-traumatic pigment dispersion","pigment dispersion from a malpositioned posterior chamber intraocular lens","melanomalytic glaucoma from a pigmented iris or ciliary body tumour","iris melanoma with secondary dispersion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["gonioscopy demonstrating dense homogeneous trabecular pigment and a concave peripheral iris","slit-lamp examination for Krukenberg spindle and iris transillumination defects","anterior segment optical coherence tomography or ultrasound biomicroscopy showing posterior iris bowing","diurnal intraocular pressure phasing to capture exercise or dilatation-related spikes","standard automated perimetry for glaucomatous field loss","optical coherence tomography of the retinal nerve fibre layer and ganglion cell complex","optic disc stereo-photography for baseline documentation"],"redFlags":["acute intraocular pressure spike after exercise or pupil dilatation with corneal oedema and haloes","progressive optic disc cupping or nerve fibre layer thinning despite treatment","markedly asymmetric pressures or pigmentation suggesting an alternative intraocular tumour","very high presenting pressures in a young myope at risk of rapid field loss"],"prognosis":"With early detection and pressure control the long-term visual prognosis is generally good, and pigment dispersion tends to diminish with age as the lens enlarges and the iris configuration flattens. Untreated or poorly controlled disease can progress to significant glaucomatous field loss and irreversible visual impairment.","pitfalls":["Attributing a young myope's raised pressure to primary open-angle glaucoma and missing the diagnostic anterior segment signs.","Prescribing standard-energy laser trabeculoplasty without anticipating an exaggerated post-laser pressure spike in a heavily pigmented meshwork.","Overlooking exercise-induced pressure spikes because a single clinic reading is normal.","Using pilocarpine in a young high myope without counselling about induced myopia and the risk of retinal detachment.","Assuming all pigment dispersion syndrome will convert to glaucoma and over-treating patients who only require monitoring."],"management":["Initiate intraocular pressure-lowering topical therapy, usually a prostaglandin analogue, with adjunctive beta-blockers, alpha-agonists or carbonic anhydrase inhibitors for adequate control","Offer laser peripheral iridotomy to flatten the posteriorly bowed iris and interrupt reverse pupillary block driving pigment liberation","Apply laser trabeculoplasty judiciously given dense meshwork pigmentation and the risk of a marked post-laser pressure rise","Counsel patients to avoid strenuous exercise and situations causing pupillary dilatation that can trigger acute pigment-release pressure spikes","Proceed to filtration surgery or a drainage device when pressure remains uncontrolled or field loss progresses despite maximal therapy","Undertake ongoing surveillance with diurnal pressure phasing, gonioscopy, retinal nerve fibre layer OCT and perimetry, as only a minority with pigment dispersion convert to glaucoma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Glaucoma","European Glaucoma Society Terminology and Guidelines for Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"pigmented_purpuric_dermatosis":{"name":"Pigmented purpuric dermatosis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["capillaritis","Schamberg disease","progressive pigmentary purpura","purpura pigmentosa chronica"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of chronic benign eruptions caused by capillaritis, producing petechiae and golden-brown ('cayenne pepper') pigmentation, most often on the lower legs","Schamberg disease is the commonest variant; other forms include Majocchi (annular telangiectodes), lichen aureus, Gougerot-Blum (lichenoid), and eczematoid-like (Doucas-Kapetanakis) types","Lesions are typically asymptomatic or mildly itchy and are not associated with thrombocytopenia or a coagulation defect","The characteristic colour reflects extravasated red cells and haemosiderin deposition in the dermis","Contributing factors include venous hypertension, gravitational dependency, exercise, and sometimes drugs","Persistent, unusually widespread, or lichenoid variants occasionally overlap with, or should prompt exclusion of, early mycosis fungoides","It is a cosmetic and chronic nuisance rather than a systemic threat, and pigmentation resolves slowly if at all"],"pathophysiology":"Pigmented purpuric dermatoses arise from a lymphocytic capillaritis: inflammation of dermal capillaries increases vascular permeability and fragility, allowing red cells to leak into the papillary dermis. Breakdown of extravasated erythrocytes leaves haemosiderin deposits in dermal macrophages, producing the characteristic golden-brown discolouration overlying fresh pinpoint (cayenne-pepper) petechiae. Platelet counts and coagulation are normal, so the purpura reflects vessel-wall inflammation rather than a bleeding diathesis. Venous hypertension, gravitational stasis, physical activity, and occasionally drug hypersensitivity contribute to the capillary damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Leukocytoclastic (small-vessel) vasculitis","Stasis dermatitis with haemosiderin deposition","Thrombocytopenic or coagulopathic purpura","Early mycosis fungoides (pigmented purpura-like)","Drug-induced purpura","Angioma serpiginosum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment with dermoscopy showing coppery-red background and pigment dots","Full blood count and platelet count to exclude thrombocytopenia","Coagulation screen where a bleeding disorder is suspected","Skin biopsy in atypical, persistent, or lichenoid cases to confirm capillaritis and exclude vasculitis or lymphoma","Assessment of venous insufficiency in the lower limbs","Medication review for a possible drug trigger"],"redFlags":["Palpable purpura or systemic symptoms suggesting true vasculitis rather than capillaritis","Thrombocytopenia or abnormal clotting indicating a haematological cause of purpura","Persistent, widespread, or lichenoid patches raising concern for early cutaneous T-cell lymphoma","Progressive change in a long-standing lesion warranting biopsy"],"prognosis":"Pigmented purpuric dermatosis is benign and chronic, often persisting or recurring over months to years; pigmentation fades slowly and treatment is largely cosmetic, but atypical persistent disease occasionally warrants monitoring for mycosis fungoides.","pitfalls":["Mistaking benign capillaritis for a bleeding disorder and ordering unnecessary extensive haematological workup, or conversely missing genuine thrombocytopenia","Overlooking an evolving mycosis fungoides in persistent or lichenoid variants","Confusing it with palpable-purpura vasculitis","Ignoring contributory venous hypertension and aggravating drugs"],"management":["Reassure regarding the benign, chronic nature, as this is a capillaritis without thrombocytopenia or a coagulation defect requiring no urgent intervention","Address contributing venous hypertension and gravitational stasis with graduated compression stockings and leg elevation","Prescribe topical corticosteroids and emollients to relieve pruritus and inflammation in symptomatic lesions","Review medications and withdraw any suspected drug trigger","Consider oral ascorbic acid with rutoside (bioflavonoids) or narrowband ultraviolet phototherapy for extensive or persistent disease","Perform skin biopsy in atypical, widespread or lichenoid cases and monitor for evolution to mycosis fungoides"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pigmented_villonodular_synovitis":{"name":"Pigmented villonodular synovitis (tenosynovial giant cell tumour)","system":"Rheumatology / Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PVNS","tenosynovial giant cell tumour","TGCT","diffuse-type giant cell tumour","giant cell tumour of the tendon sheath"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign but locally aggressive proliferative disorder of the synovium, now classified within the tenosynovial giant cell tumour family","Occurs in localised (nodular) and diffuse forms, with the diffuse type being more aggressive and prone to recurrence","Usually monoarticular, most often affecting the knee, and typically presents in young to middle-aged adults with pain, swelling and recurrent effusions","Aspiration classically yields serosanguineous or brown (haemosiderin-stained) joint fluid, reflecting recurrent haemarthrosis","MRI is the investigation of choice and shows haemosiderin-laden proliferative synovium with characteristic low signal and 'blooming' artefact on gradient-echo sequences","Driven in most cases by overexpression of colony-stimulating factor 1 (CSF1), providing the rationale for CSF1R-directed therapy in advanced or unresectable disease","Primary treatment is surgical synovectomy, with radiotherapy or CSF1R inhibition considered for diffuse, recurrent or inoperable disease"],"pathophysiology":"Pigmented villonodular synovitis is a neoplastic proliferation of the synovial lining producing villous and nodular thickening with abundant haemosiderin-laden macrophages, multinucleated giant cells and foam cells. The majority of cases are driven by a translocation involving the colony-stimulating factor 1 (CSF1) gene, leading to overexpression of CSF1; a small population of neoplastic cells recruits large numbers of non-neoplastic CSF1-receptor-bearing inflammatory cells that make up the bulk of the lesion (a 'landscape' effect). Recurrent microhaemorrhage within the hypervascular synovium deposits haemosiderin, giving the tissue its pigmented appearance and the characteristic MRI blooming. The proliferative synovium erodes cartilage and subchondral bone, causing progressive joint destruction, particularly in the diffuse form.","additionalDifferentials":["Synovial chondromatosis","Haemophilic arthropathy","Rheumatoid or other chronic inflammatory synovitis","Synovial haemangioma","Synovial sarcoma or other malignant soft-tissue tumour","Amyloid or gouty synovial deposition"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI showing haemosiderin-laden synovium with low signal and blooming on gradient-echo sequences","Plain radiographs to assess soft-tissue swelling and juxta-articular bony erosions","Joint aspiration revealing serosanguineous or brown haemosiderin-stained fluid","Histological confirmation from synovial biopsy or resected tissue","Assessment of CSF1 overexpression where targeted therapy is being considered"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth or atypical features raising concern for malignant transformation or sarcoma","Aggressive bony erosion and joint destruction in diffuse disease","Repeated recurrence after synovectomy indicating incomplete disease control","Neurovascular compromise from an expanding mass"],"prognosis":"The localised form has a good outcome after complete excision, whereas the diffuse form recurs frequently and can cause progressive joint destruction; malignant transformation is rare, and CSF1R-directed therapy has improved options for advanced disease.","pitfalls":["Mistaking recurrent haemarthrosis for injury or a bleeding disorder and delaying diagnosis","Incomplete synovectomy in diffuse disease leading to recurrence","Overlooking the diagnosis when gradient-echo MRI sequences are not obtained","Confusing it with synovial chondromatosis or a malignant soft-tissue tumour"],"management":["Undertake surgical synovectomy as the mainstay, with arthroscopic excision for localised nodular disease and open or combined complete synovectomy for the diffuse form","Consider CSF1R-directed systemic therapy such as pexidartinib for advanced, recurrent or unresectable diffuse disease, exploiting the underlying CSF1 overexpression","Use adjuvant radiosynovectomy or external-beam radiotherapy to reduce recurrence after incomplete resection of diffuse disease","Provide physiotherapy to restore range of motion and joint function postoperatively","Consider joint arthroplasty for end-stage cartilage and subchondral bone destruction","Monitor for local recurrence with serial MRI given the characteristic haemosiderin blooming, particularly in diffuse disease","Coordinate care across orthopaedic surgery, rheumatology and oncology for aggressive or recurrent tumours"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone"],"crossLinks":[]},"pilar_cyst":{"name":"pilar cyst","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["trichilemmal cyst","isthmus-catagen cyst","wen"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a smooth, firm, mobile subcutaneous nodule, with the great majority occurring on the scalp.","Unlike epidermoid cysts, pilar cysts lack a central punctum and characteristically shell out cleanly and intact at surgery.","They are frequently multiple and show an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern in many affected families.","The lining shows trichilemmal keratinisation without a granular layer, abruptly producing dense homogeneous keratin, often with calcification.","Overlying hair may be thinned or lost due to pressure, but the lesions themselves are asymptomatic unless inflamed or ruptured.","A proliferating trichilemmal tumour can arise within a long-standing cyst, presenting as a rapidly enlarging exophytic scalp mass.","Complete excision with the intact capsule is curative and technically easier than for epidermoid cysts.","Rarely, malignant proliferating trichilemmal tumours occur and may recur locally or metastasise, warranting histological review of atypical lesions."],"pathophysiology":"Pilar cysts derive from the outer root sheath of the hair follicle at the level of the isthmus, and the lining epithelium undergoes trichilemmal keratinisation in which cells enlarge and keratinise abruptly without forming a granular layer. The cavity fills with compact, homogeneous keratin that frequently calcifies. A hereditary autosomal dominant tendency underlies the common multiplicity and scalp predilection, and clonal proliferation of the cyst lining can give rise to a proliferating trichilemmal tumour. Malignant change is rare but recognised, characterised by cytological atypia, infiltrative growth and occasionally metastatic potential.","additionalDifferentials":["epidermoid cyst","lipoma","proliferating trichilemmal tumour","dermoid cyst","cylindroma","scalp metastasis or adnexal tumour","osteoma cutis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["clinical examination noting absence of a punctum","ultrasonography showing a well-defined cystic scalp lesion, sometimes with calcification","histopathology confirming trichilemmal keratinisation without a granular layer","imaging and wide histological sampling if a proliferating trichilemmal tumour is suspected","family history assessment given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["rapid enlargement or exophytic ulcerated growth suggesting a proliferating or malignant trichilemmal tumour","fixation to deeper tissues or irregular infiltrative margins","recurrence after apparently complete excision","a solitary firm scalp nodule with atypical features that could represent metastasis"],"prognosis":"Pilar cysts are benign and excision of the intact capsule is curative with a low recurrence rate. Proliferating trichilemmal tumours are usually benign but may recur locally, and the rare malignant variant can metastasise.","pitfalls":["Assuming a rapidly growing scalp cyst is simply inflamed and missing a proliferating or malignant trichilemmal tumour.","Confusing a pilar cyst with an epidermoid cyst and expecting a punctum that is absent.","Rupturing the fragile capsule during excision, complicating complete removal.","Failing to submit an atypical or recurrent lesion for histopathology."],"management":["Observe and reassure for asymptomatic lesions, as pilar cysts are benign","Offer complete surgical excision of the intact capsule, which shells out cleanly and is curative with a low recurrence rate","For an inflamed or ruptured cyst, drain and treat secondary infection, deferring definitive excision until inflammation settles","Send excised tissue for histopathology to confirm trichilemmal keratinisation and exclude a proliferating trichilemmal tumour","Perform wide excision with careful histological sampling when a rapidly enlarging exophytic mass raises concern for a proliferating or malignant trichilemmal tumour","Take a family history given the autosomal dominant tendency to multiple scalp cysts"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology","Weedon's Skin Pathology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pili_torti":{"name":"Pili torti","system":"Dermatology / Trichology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["twisted hair","corkscrew hair"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A hair-shaft disorder in which the shaft is flattened and twisted through one hundred and eighty degrees at irregular intervals along its length, making hair fragile and easily broken","The hair appears spangled or has an uneven sheen because light reflects differently from the twisted segments","Classic early-onset pili torti often presents in fair-haired children with short, broken, brittle hair, and may be isolated or part of a syndrome","A key association is Menkes disease, an X-linked disorder of copper metabolism in which pili torti accompanies neurodegeneration and connective-tissue features, making it a vital paediatric diagnosis","Other associations include Bjornstad syndrome (pili torti with sensorineural deafness), ectodermal dysplasias and Bazex syndrome","Diagnosis rests on light microscopy showing the characteristic flattened one-hundred-and-eighty-degree twists, while trichoscopy shows alternating bright and dark banding","Management is supportive with gentle hair care and avoidance of trauma, and identifying any underlying syndrome is essential, since some acquired forms improve once the trigger resolves"],"pathophysiology":"Pili torti is a structural abnormality in which the hair shaft is flattened and rotated on its own axis, producing recurrent twists that create points of weakness and increased fragility. It may be a primary genetic defect of hair-shaft formation or occur as part of syndromes affecting ectodermal structures or metabolism; in Menkes disease deficient copper-dependent enzyme activity impairs disulphide bonding and keratin cross-linking. Acquired forms can follow inflammation, scarring or drug exposure. The twisting weakens the shaft so that hair breaks readily.","additionalDifferentials":["Trichorrhexis nodosa, with nodal fractures but no twisting","Monilethrix, with a regularly beaded shaft","Netherton syndrome, with bamboo hair (trichorrhexis invaginata)","Woolly hair","Trichothiodystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Light microscopy of the hair shaft showing flattened one-hundred-and-eighty-degree twists","Trichoscopy demonstrating alternating bright and dark banding","Serum copper and caeruloplasmin where Menkes disease is suspected in an infant","Audiometry if Bjornstad syndrome with deafness is considered","Assessment for associated ectodermal or neurological features with genetic testing as indicated"],"redFlags":["An infant with pili torti and neurodevelopmental regression suggesting Menkes disease","Associated sensorineural hearing loss pointing to Bjornstad syndrome","Features of an underlying ectodermal or systemic syndrome requiring urgent evaluation"],"prognosis":"Isolated classic pili torti often improves after puberty, but the outlook depends heavily on any associated syndrome; Menkes disease carries a poor neurological prognosis, whereas isolated or acquired forms may resolve when the cause is removed.","pitfalls":["Missing Menkes disease in an infant and so delaying diagnosis of a serious metabolic disorder","Overlooking associated deafness or ectodermal features","Confusing pili torti with other hair-shaft defects without confirmatory microscopy","Attributing brittle hair to hair care alone without examining the shaft"],"management":["Identify and treat any underlying syndrome or acquired cause, since management is directed at the associated condition rather than the hair shaft itself","Advise gentle hair care with avoidance of mechanical trauma, heat styling and chemical treatments to minimise breakage of the fragile twisted shafts","Urgently exclude Menkes disease in an affected infant with serum copper and caeruloplasmin, initiating parenteral copper-histidine replacement where confirmed","Arrange audiometry to detect the sensorineural deafness of Bjornstad syndrome","Assess for associated ectodermal dysplasia and neurological features, with targeted genetic testing as indicated","Provide genetic counselling appropriate to the identified inheritance and refer to multidisciplinary paediatric and dermatological services","Reassure that isolated classic pili torti often improves after puberty, while outlook otherwise depends on the associated disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pilocytic_astrocytoma":{"name":"pilocytic astrocytoma","system":"nervous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["juvenile pilocytic astrocytoma","JPA"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest glioma of childhood and a CNS WHO grade 1 tumour, most often arising in the cerebellum, optic pathway, hypothalamus and brainstem.","The characteristic cerebellar appearance is a cyst with an avidly enhancing mural nodule, producing signs of raised intracranial pressure and ataxia.","Optic pathway pilocytic astrocytoma is strongly associated with neurofibromatosis type 1 and may present with visual loss or proptosis.","Histology is biphasic with compact piloid areas containing Rosenthal fibres alternating with loose microcystic zones and eosinophilic granular bodies.","The vast majority are driven by activation of the MAPK pathway, most commonly a KIAA1549-BRAF fusion, with BRAF V600E mutations in a minority.","Gross total resection is often curative and may be the only treatment required for accessible cerebellar tumours.","For unresectable, progressive or NF1-associated disease, MAPK-pathway-targeted agents such as MEK inhibitors are increasingly used.","Contrast enhancement and even leptomeningeal dissemination do not imply aggressive behaviour, and malignant transformation is rare."],"pathophysiology":"Pilocytic astrocytoma is a circumscribed, slow-growing glioma whose biology is dominated by constitutive activation of the RAS-MAPK signalling pathway. The characteristic event is a tandem duplication generating a KIAA1549-BRAF fusion gene that produces a constitutively active BRAF kinase; alternative drivers include BRAF V600E point mutations, other BRAF or RAF fusions, and, in the context of neurofibromatosis type 1, biallelic NF1 loss. Sustained MAPK signalling promotes a bland, biphasic astrocytic proliferation with piloid bipolar cells, Rosenthal fibres and eosinophilic granular bodies, together with glomeruloid vascular proliferation that accounts for the strong contrast enhancement. Despite a single dominant oncogenic pathway, the low proliferative index and tendency towards senescence explain the indolent, often self-limiting course.","additionalDifferentials":["medulloblastoma","ependymoma","haemangioblastoma","ganglioglioma","diffuse midline or paediatric-type diffuse low-grade glioma","pleomorphic xanthoastrocytoma","dysembryoplastic neuroepithelial tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["contrast-enhanced MRI of the brain showing cyst with enhancing mural nodule","MRI of the whole neuraxis when dissemination is suspected","surgical resection or biopsy for histological confirmation","BRAF fusion and BRAF V600E molecular testing","assessment for neurofibromatosis type 1, especially with optic pathway lesions","formal ophthalmological and visual field assessment for optic pathway tumours","endocrine evaluation for hypothalamic or chiasmatic involvement"],"redFlags":["obstructive hydrocephalus with headache, vomiting and papilloedema","rapidly progressive visual loss in an optic pathway tumour","brainstem involvement causing cranial nerve or long-tract signs","new or worsening deficit suggesting unusual progression"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent, with long-term survival and frequent cure after complete resection of accessible tumours; deep-seated or unresectable lesions may follow a more protracted course but malignant transformation is uncommon.","pitfalls":["interpreting strong contrast enhancement as evidence of malignancy","regarding leptomeningeal spread as inevitably aggressive","overtreating an indolent grade 1 tumour with aggressive radiotherapy in a child","missing an underlying diagnosis of neurofibromatosis type 1","confusing piloid gliosis adjacent to other lesions with true neoplasm on small biopsies"],"management":["Undertake maximal safe surgical resection as the mainstay, since gross total excision of an accessible cerebellar tumour is often curative and may be the only treatment needed","Treat unresectable or progressive tumours, especially optic pathway lesions, with chemotherapy such as carboplatin and vincristine","Consider MAPK-targeted therapy with MEK inhibitors, or BRAF inhibitors for BRAF V600E-mutant tumours, in progressive or unresectable disease","Reserve radiotherapy for selected refractory cases, avoiding it where possible in young children and those with neurofibromatosis type 1","Relieve obstructive hydrocephalus with cerebrospinal fluid diversion such as endoscopic third ventriculostomy or shunting","Screen for neurofibromatosis type 1 and arrange formal ophthalmological and visual field assessment for optic pathway tumours","Perform long-term surveillance MRI to detect regrowth, coordinating care through a paediatric neuro-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System","SIOPE and paediatric low-grade glioma consensus guidance","Louis et al., cIMPACT-NOW updates on CNS tumour classification"],"crossLinks":[]},"pilomatricoma":{"name":"Pilomatricoma","system":"Dermatology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pilomatrixoma","calcifying epithelioma of Malherbe","benign calcifying epithelioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / WHO Classification of Skin Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign tumour of hair-follicle matrix differentiation, most common in children and young adults on the head, neck, and upper limbs","Presents as a firm, sometimes stony-hard, mobile subcutaneous nodule, occasionally with a bluish or reddish hue through the overlying skin","Stretching the overlying skin can reveal multiple facets and angulated edges, the so-called tent sign, reflecting internal calcification","Driven by activating mutations of CTNNB1 leading to beta-catenin stabilisation and aberrant Wnt signalling in the follicular matrix","Histology shows basaloid matrix cells maturing into anucleate eosinophilic shadow (ghost) cells, with dystrophic calcification and often foreign-body giant-cell reaction and ossification","Usually solitary; multiple lesions may prompt consideration of associations such as myotonic dystrophy and other syndromes","Treatment is simple surgical excision, which is curative; malignant transformation (pilomatrix carcinoma) is rare"],"pathophysiology":"Pilomatricoma is a benign neoplasm derived from the matrix cells of the hair follicle. Activating mutations in CTNNB1, the gene encoding beta-catenin, prevent its normal degradation, so stabilised beta-catenin accumulates and constitutively activates Wnt signalling, driving proliferation of follicular matrix cells. These basaloid cells progressively lose their nuclei as they differentiate, producing sheets of eosinophilic anucleate shadow (ghost) cells. Over time the lesion undergoes dystrophic calcification and sometimes true ossification, with a surrounding foreign-body giant-cell and granulomatous reaction, accounting for the characteristic firm to stony consistency.","additionalDifferentials":["Epidermoid or dermoid cyst","Calcified lymph node","Osteoma cutis","Foreign-body granuloma","Basal cell carcinoma (when ulcerated or facial)","Pilomatrix carcinoma (malignant counterpart)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination for the tent sign and stony consistency","Skin ultrasound showing a well-defined nodule with internal calcification and acoustic shadowing","Excisional biopsy with histopathology demonstrating shadow cells and calcification","Immunohistochemistry for nuclear beta-catenin in equivocal cases","Evaluation for associated syndromes when lesions are multiple (e.g. myotonic dystrophy)"],"redFlags":["Rapid enlargement, ulceration, or infiltrative growth suggesting pilomatrix carcinoma","Multiple pilomatricomas prompting evaluation for an underlying syndrome","Recurrence after excision raising concern for incomplete removal or malignancy"],"prognosis":"Pilomatricoma is benign with an excellent prognosis; complete excision is curative and recurrence is uncommon. The rare malignant counterpart, pilomatrix carcinoma, can recur locally and occasionally metastasise.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the firm nodule for an epidermoid cyst or calcified lymph node and mismanaging it","Failing to recognise the tent sign and characteristic clinical features","Overlooking multiple lesions that may signal an associated systemic disorder","Dismissing atypical rapidly growing lesions that could represent malignant transformation"],"management":["Offer complete surgical excision as the definitive treatment, which is curative with uncommon recurrence","Submit the specimen for histopathology to confirm shadow (ghost) cells with calcification and exclude the rare pilomatrix carcinoma","Reassure regarding the benign nature of this hair-matrix tumour once diagnosis is confirmed","Evaluate for associated syndromes such as myotonic dystrophy when lesions are multiple","Undertake wide local excision with close follow-up for the malignant pilomatrix carcinoma variant given its potential for local recurrence and metastasis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","WHO Classification of Skin Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"pilonidal_disease":{"name":"Pilonidal disease","system":"Colorectal / Pelvic floor","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pilonidal sinus","pilonidal cyst","sacrococcygeal pilonidal disease","jeep disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acquired condition of the natal cleft in which loose hairs are driven into the skin, provoking a foreign-body reaction and forming midline pits and a chronic sinus","Typically affects young, hirsute men, and is associated with a deep natal cleft, sedentary occupations, obesity, and poor local hygiene","May present acutely as a painful fluctuant abscess or chronically with intermittent discharge and one or more midline pits, often with lateral secondary openings","Characteristic midline pits in the intergluteal cleft, sometimes containing protruding hair tufts, are the diagnostic hallmark","Acute abscesses are treated by incision and drainage placed off the midline; definitive treatment of the sinus follows once inflammation settles","Definitive options range from minimally invasive (pit picking, phenol or laser ablation) to excision with off-midline or flap closure (Karydakis, Bascom cleft-lift, Limberg flap), which flatten the cleft and reduce recurrence","Hair removal, hygiene, and keeping the cleft clean and dry reduce recurrence after treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Pilonidal disease is now regarded as acquired rather than congenital. Friction and movement in the natal cleft, together with a hair-friendly microenvironment, allow shed hairs to penetrate the skin through enlarged follicles or midline pits. The embedded hair acts as a foreign body, provoking chronic inflammation, granulation, and a sinus lined by granulation tissue that discharges and can abscess. A deep cleft, hirsutism, obesity, and prolonged sitting perpetuate the cycle, and midline wounds that heal poorly predispose to recurrence.","additionalDifferentials":["Perianal or anal fistula tracking towards the natal cleft","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Furuncle, carbuncle, or infected sebaceous cyst","Sacrococcygeal dermoid or teratoma","Crohn's perianal disease","Presacral sinus or osteomyelitis with cutaneous discharge"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical inspection of the natal cleft for midline pits and secondary tracks","Examination under anaesthesia with probing for complex or recurrent disease","MRI in atypical, recurrent, or extensive disease to map tracks","Assessment for Crohn's disease when perianal features are atypical","Swab and culture of discharge if secondary infection is suspected","Biopsy of chronic, non-healing lesions to exclude malignant change"],"redFlags":["A chronic non-healing sinus with induration or a mass suggesting malignant (squamous) change","Extensive or atypical tracks suggesting Crohn's disease or hidradenitis suppurativa","Rapidly spreading sepsis in an immunocompromised or diabetic patient","A fistulous communication with the anal canal"],"prognosis":"Pilonidal disease is prone to recurrence, particularly with midline wounds and persistent risk factors; off-midline closure techniques and diligent hair control substantially improve long-term healing.","pitfalls":["Closing wounds in the midline, which heal poorly and recur","Neglecting hair removal and hygiene advice after surgery","Mistaking a pilonidal track for an anal fistula, or the reverse","Overlooking rare malignant transformation in long-standing disease"],"management":["Treat an acute pilonidal abscess with incision and drainage sited off the midline to relieve sepsis","Once inflammation settles, offer definitive treatment ranging from minimally invasive pit picking, phenol injection or laser ablation to excision with off-midline or flap closure","Prefer off-midline and flap reconstructions such as the Karydakis, Bascom cleft-lift or Limberg flap, which flatten the natal cleft and lower recurrence","Institute meticulous hair control with regular depilation or laser epilation and local hygiene to interrupt the disease cycle","Advise weight reduction and avoidance of prolonged sitting to reduce friction and recurrence","Provide structured wound care for open or healing midline wounds","Biopsy chronic non-healing lesions to exclude malignant change and reassess recurrent disease with examination under anaesthesia or MRI to map tracks"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","Keighley & Williams' Surgery of the Anus, Rectum and Colon"],"crossLinks":[]},"pilonidal_sinus":{"name":"Pilonidal sinus","system":"Skin / Soft tissue / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pilonidal disease","sacrococcygeal sinus","jeep bottom","pilonidal abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs in the natal cleft over the sacrococcygeal region, presenting with discharge, recurrent swelling, or an acute painful abscess","Characteristic midline pits in the natal cleft, often containing tufts of hair, are the key clinical sign","Predominantly affects young adults, more often men, and is associated with hirsutism, obesity, prolonged sitting, and poor local hygiene","An acute pilonidal abscess is treated by incision and drainage, usually off the midline, to relieve sepsis","Chronic or recurrent disease is managed electively by a range of options from pit-picking and laying open to wide excision with midline or off-midline (flap) closure","Off-midline closure and flap techniques generally give lower recurrence and better healing than midline closure","Recurrence is common, and meticulous hair control and hygiene reduce the risk","Long-standing, non-healing, or atypical sinuses rarely undergo malignant change and should be biopsied if suspicious"],"pathophysiology":"Pilonidal disease is now regarded as an acquired condition in which loose hairs are driven into the skin of the natal cleft, provoking a foreign-body inflammatory reaction and forming midline pits. The pits become epithelialised sinuses that trap further hair and debris, leading to chronic infection, abscess formation, and secondary tracks extending laterally. Friction, sweating, deep natal clefts, hirsutism, and prolonged sitting promote hair insertion and infection. Recurrent cycles of infection and incomplete healing perpetuate the disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Anorectal abscess or fistula-in-ano tracking posteriorly","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Infected sebaceous or epidermoid cyst","Perianal Crohn's disease","Sacrococcygeal osteomyelitis with sinus","Presacral dermoid or teratoma with sinus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical inspection for midline pits and lateral tracks","Examination to distinguish from anal fistula (relationship to the anal canal)","MRI in complex or recurrent disease or when a deeper or connecting track is suspected","Biopsy of chronic non-healing lesions to exclude malignancy"],"redFlags":["Spreading cellulitis or systemic sepsis from an untreated abscess","A chronic non-healing indurated sinus raising concern for malignant change","Extensive or recurrent disease in an immunosuppressed or diabetic patient","Features suggesting an alternative diagnosis such as anal fistula or Crohn's disease"],"prognosis":"Acute abscesses settle with drainage, but pilonidal disease is prone to recurrence. Definitive excisional and flap-based procedures improve healing and lower recurrence, and long-term outcome is strongly influenced by ongoing hair control and hygiene.","pitfalls":["Midline closure of large wounds, which heals poorly and recurs frequently","Mistaking a posteriorly tracking anal fistula for pilonidal disease","Neglecting hair control and hygiene advice, allowing recurrence","Failing to biopsy a chronic non-healing sinus"],"management":["Manage an acute pilonidal abscess with incision and drainage, usually off the midline, to relieve sepsis","Treat chronic or recurrent sinus disease electively with options from pit-picking and laying open to wide excision with midline or off-midline flap closure","Favour off-midline and flap techniques, which generally give lower recurrence and better healing than midline closure","Institute ongoing hair control with depilation or laser epilation and improved local hygiene to prevent recurrence","Distinguish from anal fistula clinically and map complex, recurrent or deeper tracks with MRI before definitive surgery","Biopsy chronic non-healing lesions to exclude malignancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"pincer_nail_deformity":{"name":"Pincer nail deformity","system":"Dermatology / Nail","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pincer nail","trumpet nail","omega nail","transverse overcurvature of the nail"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pincer nail is characterised by excessive transverse over-curvature of the plate that increases from proximal to distal, pinching the underlying soft tissue","The tightening curve traps and compresses the nail bed and distal tissue, which is the source of the often severe pain","The great toenail is most commonly involved, and the deformity may be hereditary or acquired","Acquired forms are associated with degenerative bony changes of the underlying phalanx, ill-fitting footwear, osteoarthritis, and some medications","A dorsal bony spur or widening of the distal phalanx often underlies acquired cases and is best shown on radiograph","As the curve deepens the lateral edges may roll inward, sometimes producing subungual hyperkeratosis and secondary changes","Management ranges from conservative measures such as bracing and orthonyxia to surgical correction of the matrix or underlying bone in refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Pincer nail results from progressive transverse over-curvature of the nail plate, so that the two lateral edges curl toward each other and increasingly compress the nail bed and pulp as the nail grows distally. In many acquired cases an underlying bony change of the distal phalanx, such as a dorsal osteophyte or lateral widening, alters the shape of the tissue on which the matrix sits and forces the plate to curl. Hereditary forms reflect an intrinsic tendency of the matrix to produce an over-curved plate. The pinching action distorts and can even resorb the underlying soft tissue and bone, and the trapped nail bed generates pain and predisposes to secondary ingrowing and hyperkeratosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Onychocryptosis (ingrowing toenail) with lateral inflammation","Onychogryphosis with gross thickening","Subungual exostosis distorting the plate","Psoriatic or degenerative nail bed disease","Congenital malalignment of the great toenail","Secondary changes from ill-fitting footwear"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Radiograph of the distal phalanx to identify a dorsal spur or bony widening","Clinical measurement of the degree of transverse curvature along the nail","Assessment of footwear and biomechanical factors","Examination for coexisting osteoarthritis or systemic disease","Review of medications associated with acquired over-curvature","Onychoscopy to assess nail bed and lateral fold involvement"],"redFlags":["Severe unremitting pain interfering with walking and function","Secondary infection or ulceration of the pinched soft tissue","Radiographic bony resorption or an aggressive-looking underlying lesion","Rapidly progressive deformity or an atypical single-nail change suggesting a tumour"],"prognosis":"Conservative bracing can relieve symptoms and partially flatten the curve but tends to relapse when discontinued, whereas surgery addressing the matrix or underlying bony spur offers the most durable correction in refractory, painful cases.","pitfalls":["Treating the nail as a simple ingrowing toenail and missing the underlying over-curvature","Omitting a radiograph and overlooking a correctable bony spur","Expecting conservative bracing alone to cure severe long-standing deformity","Failing to address footwear and biomechanical contributors, leading to recurrence"],"management":["Offer conservative bracing (orthonyxia) to gradually flatten the transverse over-curvature and relieve pain, accepting a tendency to relapse when discontinued","Modify footwear to remove external pressure and address biomechanical contributors","Treat associated pain and secondary ingrowing with analgesia and care of the compressed nail bed","Manage recalcitrant lateral in-rolling with partial nail avulsion and matricectomy, using phenolisation of the lateral matrix horns","Undertake durable surgical correction of the underlying dorsal bony spur or phalangeal widening, with matrix or nail-bed reconstruction, in refractory painful cases","Treat coexisting subungual hyperkeratosis and review medications and osteoarthritis associated with acquired over-curvature"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"pineoblastoma":{"name":"Pineoblastoma","system":"Oncology / Neuro-oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pineoblastoma","pineal embryonal tumour","pineal parenchymal tumour grade 4","trilateral retinoblastoma-associated tumour","DICER1-associated pineoblastoma","RB1-associated pineoblastoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of the CNS","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A malignant embryonal tumour of the pineal region, most often affecting children, and the highest-grade pineal parenchymal tumour","Midline location commonly compresses the tectal plate and aqueduct, causing obstructive hydrocephalus and Parinaud syndrome (upgaze palsy, convergence-retraction nystagmus, light-near dissociation)","Occurs in the germline RB1 predisposition setting as trilateral retinoblastoma, where bilateral retinoblastoma coexists with a pineal or suprasellar embryonal tumour","Germline DICER1 mutations are also associated and define a distinct molecular subset","Frequently disseminates through the cerebrospinal fluid, so full neuraxis staging is required","Distinguished from more indolent pineal parenchymal tumours of intermediate differentiation and from pineocytoma by its high cellularity and mitotic activity","Management combines maximal safe resection, craniospinal radiotherapy where age permits, and chemotherapy"],"pathophysiology":"Pineoblastoma is a poorly differentiated embryonal tumour arising from the parenchymal cells of the pineal gland. It shows primitive small round cells with high proliferative activity and a propensity to seed the cerebrospinal fluid pathways. A proportion of cases arise on a background of germline predisposition, most notably biallelic RB1 loss (the basis of trilateral retinoblastoma, in which a pineal or suprasellar tumour accompanies bilateral hereditary retinoblastoma) and germline DICER1 mutations, which impair microRNA processing. Its midline position adjacent to the tectum and cerebral aqueduct means that even modest growth readily obstructs cerebrospinal fluid flow and compresses the dorsal midbrain.","additionalDifferentials":["Pineal parenchymal tumour of intermediate differentiation","Pineocytoma","Pineal region germ cell tumour","Papillary tumour of the pineal region","Tectal or thalamic glioma","Metastasis to the pineal region"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced brain MRI to characterise the pineal mass and hydrocephalus","Craniospinal MRI to assess for leptomeningeal dissemination","CSF cytology and serum/CSF germ cell tumour markers to exclude germinoma or non-germinomatous tumours","Histopathology with molecular/methylation profiling","Ophthalmological examination for retinoblastoma and germline RB1 testing","Germline DICER1 testing where the clinical picture suggests it"],"redFlags":["Acute obstructive hydrocephalus with rapidly rising intracranial pressure","Progressive dorsal midbrain (Parinaud) signs and deteriorating consciousness","Evidence of CSF dissemination along the neuraxis","Coexisting bilateral retinoblastoma indicating trilateral disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is guarded and worse than for lower-grade pineal parenchymal tumours, being poorest in infants, those with metastatic disease, and germline RB1-associated trilateral retinoblastoma. Combined surgery, craniospinal radiotherapy and chemotherapy can achieve durable control in older children with localised disease, but survivors are at risk of significant treatment-related late effects.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a pineal germ cell tumour for pineoblastoma without checking serum and CSF tumour markers","Failing to examine the eyes and test germline RB1 in a child with a pineal embryonal tumour","Attributing early upgaze failure to a benign cause and missing tectal compression","Understaging by omitting craniospinal imaging and CSF cytology"],"management":["Relieve obstructive hydrocephalus urgently with cerebrospinal fluid diversion such as endoscopic third ventriculostomy or shunting","Undertake maximal safe surgical resection of the pineal tumour for cytoreduction and histological and molecular confirmation","Deliver craniospinal radiotherapy to address the high risk of cerebrospinal fluid dissemination in eligible older children","Use intensive chemotherapy, including high-dose regimens with autologous stem cell rescue, particularly in infants where radiotherapy is deferred to avoid neurotoxicity","Stage the whole neuraxis with craniospinal MRI and cerebrospinal fluid cytology, and check germ cell tumour markers to exclude germinoma","Arrange germline RB1 and DICER1 testing with ophthalmological examination for retinoblastoma, and provide genetic counselling","Coordinate care through a paediatric neuro-oncology multidisciplinary team with long-term surveillance imaging"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System","Louis DN et al. The 2021 WHO Classification of Tumors of the Central Nervous System"],"crossLinks":[]},"pinguecula":{"name":"Pinguecula","system":"Ophthalmic / ocular surface","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pingueculum","conjunctival pinguecula"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A pinguecula is a yellowish-white, slightly raised amorphous deposit on the bulbar conjunctiva, characteristically at the nasal (and later temporal) limbus within the interpalpebral fissure but, unlike a pterygium, it does NOT encroach onto the cornea.","It represents actinic (ultraviolet) elastotic degeneration of the subepithelial conjunctival stroma; chronic sun, wind and dust exposure are the principal drivers, so it is commoner in outdoor workers and low-latitude populations.","The great majority are asymptomatic and require only reassurance; lubricants suffice for mild irritation or foreign-body sensation.","Pingueculitis denotes episodic inflammation with localised hyperaemia and swelling, often after intense ultraviolet exposure or ocular-surface dryness, and is managed with lubricants and a short course of a mild topical anti-inflammatory.","Photoprotection with wrap-around sunglasses and a broad-brimmed hat is the key preventive and progression-limiting measure.","Excision is reserved for cosmetic concern, recurrent pingueculitis, interference with contact-lens wear, or diagnostic uncertainty; surgery carries a risk of scarring and recurrence.","A lesion that grows atypically, becomes nodular, develops feeder vessels, pigmentation or leukoplakia, or does not respect the limbus should raise suspicion of ocular surface squamous neoplasia rather than a benign pinguecula.","Pinguecula and pterygium share the same actinic aetiology and frequently coexist; a pinguecula may be a precursor stage but the two are distinguished by corneal involvement."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic ultraviolet and environmental (wind, dust, dryness) exposure induces actinic damage to the bulbar conjunctival stroma within the interpalpebral zone, where light is focused nasally by the anterior segment. The result is elastotic degeneration: fragmentation and abnormal accumulation of elastin-like fibrillar material in the substantia propria, with hyalinisation of collagen, secondary calcification in some lesions, and a thinned or occasionally thickened overlying epithelium. Because the process is confined to the subepithelial conjunctiva and does not cross the limbal barrier, the cornea is spared, distinguishing it from pterygium.","additionalDifferentials":["Pterygium (fibrovascular growth crossing the limbus onto cornea)","Conjunctival intraepithelial neoplasia / ocular surface squamous neoplasia","Conjunctival dermoid or dermolipoma","Amyloid deposition of the conjunctiva","Bitot spot of vitamin A deficiency","Conjunctival concretions","Limbal nodule of phlyctenular or nodular scleritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp biomicroscopy to characterise the lesion and confirm the cornea is spared","Documentation with anterior-segment photography for serial comparison","Assessment of tear film and ocular surface (tear break-up time, staining) for associated dryness","Anterior-segment optical coherence tomography where depth or atypia needs clarification","Excisional or incisional biopsy with histopathology if neoplasia is suspected","Corneal topography if a coexisting pterygium is inducing astigmatism"],"redFlags":["Progressive growth, nodularity, feeder vessels, pigmentation or leukoplakia suggesting ocular surface squamous neoplasia or melanoma","Encroachment onto the cornea indicating pterygium rather than pinguecula","Persistent or recurrent inflammation unresponsive to lubricants and photoprotection","Rapid change in size, colour or vascularity of a previously stable lesion"],"prognosis":"An entirely benign degenerative lesion with an excellent prognosis; most remain stable and asymptomatic for life. Even after excision for cosmesis or recurrent pingueculitis the outlook is good, though scarring and recurrence are recognised.","pitfalls":["Mistaking an atypical conjunctival neoplasm for a benign pinguecula and delaying biopsy","Confusing a pinguecula with an early pterygium; the defining feature is whether the lesion crosses the limbus onto the cornea","Over-treating an asymptomatic lesion with prolonged topical steroids, risking raised intraocular pressure and cataract","Attributing all interpalpebral irritation to the pinguecula while overlooking coexisting dry eye disease","Assuming excision is curative without warning the patient about recurrence and conjunctival scarring"],"management":["Reassure and observe, as most lesions are asymptomatic benign actinic degeneration requiring no active treatment","Advise photoprotection with wrap-around sunglasses and a broad-brimmed hat as the key measure to limit progression","Relieve mild irritation and foreign-body sensation with ocular lubricants and management of any coexisting dry eye","Treat episodes of pingueculitis with lubricants and a short course of a mild topical anti-inflammatory such as a weak corticosteroid or topical NSAID","Reserve surgical excision for cosmetic concern, recurrent pingueculitis, contact-lens intolerance or diagnostic uncertainty, counselling on scarring and recurrence risk","Document with anterior-segment photography for serial comparison and biopsy any lesion where neoplasia is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course — External Disease and Cornea"],"crossLinks":[]},"pinta":{"name":"Pinta","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Treponema carateum","carate","azul","mal del pinto"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pinta is a chronic non-venereal treponematosis caused by Treponema carateum and is confined to the skin, occurring in remote rural areas of Central and South America","Transmission is by prolonged direct skin-to-skin contact with lesion material, favoured by crowding and poor hygiene; it is not sexually transmitted","Unlike the other endemic treponematoses, pinta affects only the skin and does not involve bone, viscera or the nervous system","The primary lesion is an erythematous scaly papule or plaque that slowly enlarges, usually on exposed limbs","Secondary lesions (pintids) are disseminated scaly patches that undergo striking colour changes, becoming blue, violet, brown then depigmented","Late pinta is characterised by symmetrical achromic (depigmented) and atrophic patches that resemble vitiligo and are cosmetically disfiguring","Diagnosis uses dark-field microscopy and treponemal/non-treponemal serology (indistinguishable from syphilis); treatment for the endemic treponematoses is benzathine penicillin"],"pathophysiology":"Treponema carateum enters through breaks in the skin during prolonged close contact. It multiplies locally to form the primary lesion and then, over months, produces disseminated secondary skin lesions (pintids) through local and lymphatic spread. The immune response and disturbance of melanocytes produce the characteristic sequence of dyschromic changes, progressing from hyperpigmentation to permanent depigmentation and skin atrophy in late disease. Because the organism does not disseminate to deep tissues, pinta remains a purely cutaneous disease throughout its course.","additionalDifferentials":["Vitiligo (for late achromic lesions)","Pityriasis versicolor and other superficial mycoses","Other endemic treponematoses (yaws, bejel)","Leprosy (hypopigmented patches)","Post-inflammatory dyspigmentation","Psoriasis or chronic eczema (for scaly plaques)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dark-field microscopy of scrapings from active lesions for spirochaetes","Non-treponemal serology (e.g. RPR/VDRL) for activity and follow-up","Treponemal serology (e.g. TPPA/TPHA) for confirmation","Skin biopsy where the diagnosis is uncertain","PCR of lesion material where available","Assessment of close contacts in endemic communities"],"redFlags":["Extensive disfiguring depigmentation causing psychosocial harm","Diagnostic uncertainty with vitiligo or leprosy delaying treatment","Positive syphilis serology raising confusion about venereal infection","Persistence or spread of active lesions despite treatment"],"prognosis":"Early and secondary pinta respond fully to penicillin, but late achromic and atrophic skin changes are often permanent and cosmetically disfiguring even after cure; the disease carries no systemic morbidity.","pitfalls":["Mistaking late depigmented lesions for vitiligo and missing a treatable infection","Interpreting positive treponemal serology as venereal syphilis","Expecting depigmented skin to re-pigment fully after treatment","Failing to treat contacts and interrupt transmission","Confusing pinta with other endemic treponematoses without regional context"],"management":["Treat with intramuscular benzathine benzylpenicillin as first-line curative therapy, which resolves early and secondary disease","Use an oral macrolide such as azithromycin, or a tetracycline such as doxycycline, in penicillin-allergic patients","Examine and treat close household and community contacts to interrupt skin-to-skin transmission","Follow response with serial non-treponemal serology titres to confirm cure","Counsel that late achromic and atrophic skin changes are usually permanent and cosmetically disfiguring despite microbiological cure","Engage public health measures in endemic communities, including active case-finding and improved hygiene and crowding"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"piriformis_syndrome":{"name":"piriformis syndrome","system":"nervous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["deep gluteal syndrome","extraspinal sciatic entrapment"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Extraspinal compression or irritation of the sciatic nerve within the deep gluteal space, classically as it relates to the piriformis muscle, producing buttock pain with radiation down the posterior leg.","Deep, aching buttock pain aggravated by prolonged sitting, and tenderness over the sciatic notch, are more characteristic than the dermatomal back pain of lumbar radiculopathy.","The Freiberg sign (pain on passive internal rotation of the extended hip) and the Pace sign (pain and weakness on resisted abduction and external rotation of the flexed hip) are supportive provocative tests.","The FAIR position (flexion, adduction and internal rotation) reproduces symptoms and is sometimes combined with electrophysiological H-reflex testing.","An anatomical variant in which the peroneal division pierces the piriformis is common and may predispose to entrapment.","It is fundamentally a diagnosis of exclusion made only after lumbosacral radiculopathy and other causes of sciatica have been carefully ruled out.","Magnetic resonance neurography and open or ultrasound-guided injections can support the diagnosis but none is fully specific.","Symptoms typically lack the true neurological deficit of a compressive disc lesion, with preserved reflexes and only subjective sensory change in many cases."],"pathophysiology":"The sciatic nerve exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen in intimate relation to the piriformis muscle within the deep gluteal (subgluteal) space. Hypertrophy, spasm, fibrosis, anatomical variation of the nerve or muscle, trauma, or a space-occupying lesion can compress or tether the nerve, producing mechanical irritation and secondary ischaemia. Fibrous bands and other deep gluteal structures may contribute, and the broader concept of deep gluteal syndrome recognises multiple potential sites of extraspinal sciatic entrapment. The result is buttock pain with variable referred symptoms along the sciatic distribution, usually without the discrete root-level deficit of intraspinal pathology.","additionalDifferentials":["lumbosacral radiculopathy from disc herniation or stenosis","sacroiliac joint dysfunction","hip osteoarthritis or labral pathology","greater trochanteric pain syndrome","ischiofemoral impingement","proximal hamstring tendinopathy","gluteal or pelvic neoplasm compressing the sciatic nerve"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["lumbosacral spine magnetic resonance imaging to exclude radiculopathy","magnetic resonance neurography of the lumbosacral plexus and sciatic nerve","electromyography and nerve conduction studies including H-reflex, with FAIR-position testing","pelvic and hip imaging to exclude articular or mass lesions","diagnostic ultrasound-guided perisciatic or intramuscular injection","dynamic ultrasound of the deep gluteal space","inflammatory and metabolic screen when a systemic cause is considered"],"redFlags":["progressive foot drop or objective motor weakness suggesting significant nerve compromise","bowel or bladder disturbance or saddle anaesthesia indicating cauda equina pathology","night pain, weight loss or a palpable mass suggesting pelvic or gluteal malignancy","bilateral symptoms or systemic features pointing to an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Most patients improve with physiotherapy, activity modification and targeted injections, and prognosis is generally favourable. A minority with refractory symptoms may require surgical decompression, with variable outcomes.","pitfalls":["diagnosing piriformis syndrome before adequately excluding lumbosacral radiculopathy","over-reliance on provocative signs that are neither sensitive nor specific","attributing all deep gluteal sciatic symptoms to the piriformis when other structures may be responsible","missing a pelvic or gluteal tumour presenting as apparent entrapment"],"management":["Exclude lumbosacral radiculopathy and other causes of sciatica first, as this remains a diagnosis of exclusion","Begin with physiotherapy focused on piriformis stretching, hip external-rotator and gluteal conditioning, and correction of posture","Advise activity modification, particularly avoidance of prolonged sitting, and use simple analgesia or NSAIDs for symptom relief","Consider a short course of muscle relaxants for painful spasm","Offer image-guided perisciatic or intramuscular injection of local anaesthetic with corticosteroid, or botulinum toxin, for diagnosis and treatment of refractory pain","Reserve surgical decompression of the sciatic nerve in the deep gluteal space for symptoms unresponsive to conservative and injection therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies","Dawson, Hallett and Wilbourn: Entrapment Neuropathies","Hoppenfeld: Orthopaedic Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pitt_hopkins_syndrome":{"name":"Pitt-Hopkins syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTHS","TCF4","ITF2","transcription factor 4 haploinsufficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Episodic hyperventilation alternating with apnoea, typically emerging in childhood and often triggered by excitement or emotion, is a hallmark and important diagnostic clue","The facial gestalt includes a broad, beaked nose, deep-set eyes, wide mouth with a cupid's-bow upper lip and full lips, and a square jaw, coarsening with age","Caused by haploinsufficiency of TCF4 due to point variants, deletions or larger rearrangements, following autosomal dominant inheritance and almost always de novo","Severe intellectual disability with absent or minimal speech and a happy, sociable demeanour is characteristic","Constipation and Hirschsprung disease are recognised gastrointestinal associations","Stereotypic hand movements, hypotonia and unsteady, broad-based gait are common","Myopia and strabismus are frequent ophthalmological findings"],"pathophysiology":"Pitt-Hopkins syndrome is caused by haploinsufficiency of TCF4 (transcription factor 4), a basic helix-loop-helix transcription factor essential for neuronal development and differentiation in the central nervous system. Loss-of-function point variants, intragenic deletions and larger chromosomal rearrangements reduce functional TCF4, disrupting the transcriptional programmes that govern brain development and autonomic control. This underlies the severe neurodevelopmental impairment, the distinctive breathing abnormality reflecting brainstem autonomic dysregulation, and the associated craniofacial and gastrointestinal features.","additionalDifferentials":["Angelman syndrome","Mowat-Wilson syndrome","Rett syndrome","Christianson syndrome","Kleefstra syndrome","Other severe intellectual disability with breathing abnormalities"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis of TCF4, or chromosomal microarray to detect deletions","Methylation studies for Angelman syndrome when that diagnosis is in the differential","Brain MRI for structural anomalies","Electroencephalography for seizures","Assessment for Hirschsprung disease when constipation is severe","Ophthalmology review for myopia and strabismus"],"redFlags":["Apnoeic episodes causing cyanosis or requiring intervention","Prolonged or refractory seizures","Severe constipation or features suggesting Hirschsprung disease","Feeding difficulty with aspiration risk"],"prognosis":"Individuals have severe, lifelong intellectual disability and require continuous support, but the condition is generally compatible with survival into adulthood and is associated with a characteristically cheerful temperament. Breathing episodes, while alarming, are not usually life-limiting on their own.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the breathing episodes for primary epilepsy or a psychiatric disorder","Confusing the phenotype with Angelman syndrome and stopping at negative methylation testing","Overlooking severe constipation or Hirschsprung disease","Attributing the sociable demeanour and hand stereotypies solely to autism without genetic testing"],"management":["Provide supportive developmental care as the mainstay, with early physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language input including augmentative and alternative communication for absent or minimal speech","Manage the episodic hyperventilation-apnoea breathing episodes with reassurance and trigger avoidance, as they are alarming but not usually life-limiting","Treat constipation actively and investigate for and surgically manage Hirschsprung disease when bowel obstruction or severe constipation occurs","Control seizures with antiepileptic drugs guided by electroencephalography","Refer to ophthalmology for correction of myopia and management of strabismus","Provide genetic counselling for the TCF4 haploinsufficiency, noting most cases are de novo with low recurrence risk tempered by possible parental mosaicism","Coordinate lifelong multidisciplinary care with behavioural and family support given the severe intellectual disability"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","GeneReviews: Pitt-Hopkins syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"pituitary_apoplexy":{"name":"Pituitary apoplexy","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pituitary tumour apoplexy","haemorrhagic pituitary infarction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pituitary apoplexy is a neuroendocrine emergency caused by sudden haemorrhage or infarction, usually within a pre-existing pituitary adenoma","It presents abruptly with severe headache, visual disturbance, ophthalmoplegia, and often nausea, vomiting, and reduced consciousness","Visual field loss (classically bitemporal from optic chiasm compression) and cranial nerve palsies affecting eye movements arise from expansion into the suprasellar region and cavernous sinus","The most dangerous complication is acute secondary adrenal insufficiency from ACTH deficiency, which can cause life-threatening hypotension and hyponatraemia","Empirical stress-dose corticosteroid cover is a cornerstone of initial management once apoplexy is suspected, before biochemical confirmation delays treatment","MRI is the preferred imaging to demonstrate the pituitary lesion and haemorrhage, as CT may miss subtle changes","Urgent surgical decompression is considered for severe or deteriorating visual loss or reduced consciousness, while selected stable cases may be managed conservatively with close monitoring","Long-term follow-up is needed because many patients develop persistent hormone deficiencies requiring replacement"],"pathophysiology":"Pituitary apoplexy results from acute haemorrhage, infarction, or both within the pituitary gland, most commonly in an existing (often previously undiagnosed) adenoma. Rapid expansion of the gland within the confined sella and suprasellar space compresses adjacent structures: upward pressure on the optic chiasm impairs vision, and lateral extension into the cavernous sinus affects the oculomotor, trochlear, abducens, and trigeminal branches. Sudden loss of functioning pituitary tissue precipitates hypopituitarism, with acute corticotroph failure being the immediate threat to life. Leakage of blood and necrotic material can also cause meningism.","additionalDifferentials":["Subarachnoid haemorrhage","Bacterial meningitis","Migraine or other primary headache","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Ischaemic or haemorrhagic stroke","Ruptured intracranial aneurysm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent MRI of the pituitary and sella to confirm haemorrhage or infarction and assess chiasmal compression","CT head when MRI is unavailable or to exclude subarachnoid haemorrhage","Baseline anterior pituitary hormone panel and serum cortisol before or alongside starting steroids","Serum electrolytes to detect hyponatraemia and monitor for diabetes insipidus","Formal visual acuity and visual field assessment"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe headache with visual loss or ophthalmoplegia indicating an endocrine and neurosurgical emergency","Haemodynamic instability and hyponatraemia from acute adrenal insufficiency requiring immediate corticosteroids","Rapidly progressive or severe visual field loss needing urgent surgical decompression","Declining conscious level or signs of raised intracranial pressure"],"prognosis":"With prompt corticosteroid cover and, when indicated, timely decompression, visual and neurological recovery is often good; however, permanent hypopituitarism is common and delayed recognition of adrenal crisis can be fatal.","pitfalls":["Withholding stress-dose steroids while awaiting hormone results in a suspected case","Relying on CT alone and missing the diagnosis when MRI would reveal the lesion","Mistaking the presentation for subarachnoid haemorrhage or meningitis and delaying endocrine treatment","Failing to arrange long-term endocrine follow-up for evolving hormone deficiencies"],"management":["Give empirical stress-dose intravenous corticosteroids such as hydrocortisone as soon as apoplexy is suspected, to treat life-threatening secondary adrenal insufficiency before biochemical confirmation","Resuscitate with intravenous fluids and correct hyponatraemia and other electrolyte disturbances","Draw a baseline anterior pituitary hormone panel and serum cortisol before or alongside starting steroids, and confirm the diagnosis with urgent pituitary MRI","Refer urgently to neurosurgery for transsphenoidal decompression in patients with severe or progressive visual failure or reduced consciousness","Manage haemodynamically stable patients with intact vision conservatively, with close neurological, visual and endocrine monitoring","Replace deficient hormones long term, including levothyroxine, sex steroids and growth hormone, and monitor for and treat diabetes insipidus with desmopressin","Coordinate care across endocrinology, neurosurgery and ophthalmology with serial visual acuity and field assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"pituitary_carcinoma":{"name":"Pituitary carcinoma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pituitary carcinoma","metastatic pituitary tumour","malignant pituitary neuroendocrine tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Very rare, and defined not by histological atypia alone but by the presence of craniospinal or systemic metastasis of a pituitary tumour","Most evolve from an initially invasive adenoma, often ACTH- or prolactin-secreting, that progresses over years despite treatment","It cannot be diagnosed on the primary tumour histology alone, since demonstration of discontinuous metastatic deposits is required","Metastases may be craniospinal through the cerebrospinal fluid, involving leptomeninges, brain or spinal cord, or systemic to liver, bone or lymph nodes","Presentation is with an aggressive, treatment-resistant pituitary tumour, sometimes with new neurological symptoms from metastases","Functioning carcinomas continue to secrete hormone, for example persistent hypercortisolism or hyperprolactinaemia","Management is multimodal with surgery, radiotherapy and increasingly temozolomide chemotherapy","The prognosis is generally poor"],"pathophysiology":"Pituitary carcinoma is a malignant tumour of adenohypophyseal cells distinguished from an aggressive adenoma solely by the presence of metastasis outside the sellar region, either craniospinal spread through the cerebrospinal fluid or systemic dissemination. It typically develops through stepwise progression of an invasive, hormonally active adenoma, most commonly corticotroph or lactotroph, that accumulates further molecular alterations, increasing proliferative and invasive capacity over a prolonged latency. Continued hormone secretion by functioning tumours perpetuates the associated endocrine syndrome, while metastatic deposits cause site-specific neurological or systemic effects.","additionalDifferentials":["Aggressive but non-metastatic invasive pituitary adenoma","Pituitary metastasis from another primary such as breast or lung, the reverse situation","Other malignant sellar or parasellar tumours such as germ cell tumour, chordoma or sarcoma","Craniopharyngioma","Lymphoma or leukaemic infiltration of the sella","Meningeal carcinomatosis of other origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Whole-neuraxis MRI of brain and spine to detect craniospinal metastases","Systemic staging imaging with CT and functional imaging such as somatostatin-receptor PET for distant spread","Histopathology showing high proliferation indices such as elevated Ki-67, mitoses and p53 to support aggressiveness","Biopsy confirmation of a metastatic deposit to establish the diagnosis","Serial hormone measurement for functioning tumours, for example ACTH and cortisol or prolactin","Assessment of anterior and posterior pituitary function"],"redFlags":["Rapid tumour progression or new neurological deficits despite prior treatment","Discovery of discontinuous central nervous system or systemic lesions","Escalating hormone levels refractory to therapy","Leptomeningeal symptoms such as cranial neuropathies or radiculopathy"],"prognosis":"Poor overall, with limited survival once metastasis is documented, although temozolomide and multimodal therapy can achieve responses and prolong survival in some patients.","pitfalls":["Expecting histology alone to distinguish carcinoma from adenoma, when metastasis is the defining criterion","Failing to image the whole neuraxis when an aggressive pituitary tumour progresses","Overlooking a pituitary metastasis from an extracranial primary, which is more common than true pituitary carcinoma","Delaying consideration of temozolomide in progressive disease"],"management":["Temozolomide as first-line systemic chemotherapy for aggressive metastatic disease","Maximal safe surgical debulking of the primary tumour and accessible metastatic deposits","Fractionated or stereotactic radiotherapy for local control and symptomatic metastases","Control of persistent hormone excess with dopamine agonists for lactotroph tumours, and steroidogenesis inhibitors or bilateral adrenalectomy for corticotroph hypercortisolism","Replacement of resultant hypopituitarism with glucocorticoid, thyroid hormone and sex-steroid therapy","Serial whole-neuraxis MRI and tumour hormone marker surveillance to detect progression","Consider immune checkpoint inhibitors or peptide-receptor radionuclide therapy in temozolomide-refractory disease","Management within a specialist neuro-endocrine multidisciplinary team with early palliative care involvement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","WHO Classification of Pituitary Tumours","European Society of Endocrinology Guideline on Aggressive Pituitary Tumours and Carcinomas"],"crossLinks":[]},"pituitary_gigantism":{"name":"Pituitary gigantism","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pituitary gigantism","gigantism","childhood growth hormone excess","paediatric somatotroph adenoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Growth hormone excess arising before fusion of the epiphyseal growth plates, producing excessive linear growth and very tall stature, in contrast to acromegaly which occurs after fusion","Usually caused by a growth-hormone-secreting pituitary somatotroph adenoma, often a macroadenoma at diagnosis in children","Frequently associated with genetic or syndromic conditions including X-linked acrogigantism (GPR101 duplication), AIP mutations in familial isolated pituitary adenoma, McCune-Albright syndrome (GNAS mosaicism), Carney complex and MEN1","Acromegalic features such as coarse facies, large hands and feet and prognathism coexist with the tall stature","Growth hormone excess also causes soft-tissue overgrowth, sweating, headaches and glucose intolerance, and mass effect may cause visual disturbance and hypopituitarism","Diagnosis rests on an elevated IGF-1 for age and failure of growth hormone to suppress during an oral glucose tolerance test, together with pituitary MRI","Management combines transsphenoidal surgery with medical therapy using somatostatin analogues and the growth-hormone-receptor antagonist pegvisomant, and radiotherapy for residual disease","Screening for associated syndromes and genetic testing is important, especially in young patients"],"pathophysiology":"Pituitary gigantism results from sustained growth hormone hypersecretion, almost always from a somatotroph pituitary adenoma, occurring while the epiphyseal growth plates are still open. Growth hormone stimulates hepatic and local production of insulin-like growth factor 1, which drives chondrocyte proliferation at the growth plates and accelerated linear growth, giving excessive height, while simultaneous periosteal and soft-tissue growth produces acromegalic features. A substantial proportion of paediatric cases are genetically determined, through germline or mosaic activation of growth-promoting pathways as in X-linked acrogigantism, AIP-related familial pituitary adenomas, McCune-Albright syndrome with activating GNAS mutations, Carney complex and MEN1, and these often produce larger, earlier and more aggressive tumours than sporadic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial or constitutional tall stature","Marfan syndrome and other connective tissue disorders","Homocystinuria","Klinefelter syndrome","Sotos syndrome (cerebral gigantism) and other overgrowth syndromes","Precocious puberty with early growth acceleration then early fusion","Ectopic growth-hormone-releasing hormone secretion, which is rare"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum IGF-1 interpreted against age- and sex-specific reference ranges","Oral glucose tolerance test with growth hormone measurement, showing failure of growth hormone to suppress","Pituitary MRI to define the somatotroph adenoma and any invasion","Assessment of the other pituitary axes and prolactin, as co-secretion is common","Formal visual field testing if there is suprasellar extension","Genetic testing for GPR101, AIP, GNAS, MEN1 and PRKAR1A with screening for associated syndromic features"],"redFlags":["Rapidly accelerating growth crossing height centiles in a child","Visual field loss or headache from a large invasive adenoma","Features of McCune-Albright syndrome such as cafe-au-lait patches and fibrous dysplasia, or of Carney complex, prompting syndrome workup","Cardiovascular or metabolic complications of growth hormone excess"],"prognosis":"Control can be difficult because these tumours are often large and aggressive, especially in the genetic forms, and frequently require multimodal therapy; with biochemical control final height and complications are limited, but incomplete control risks continued growth and cardiovascular and metabolic morbidity.","pitfalls":["Attributing extreme tall stature to familial tall stature without measuring IGF-1","Failing to test for an underlying genetic syndrome in a young patient with gigantism","Overlooking prolactin co-secretion or other pituitary hormone deficits","Underestimating the aggressiveness and treatment resistance of syndromic somatotroph tumours"],"management":["Transsphenoidal surgical resection of the somatotroph adenoma as first-line definitive treatment","Somatostatin receptor ligands such as octreotide or lanreotide to suppress growth hormone when surgery is incomplete","Growth hormone receptor antagonist pegvisomant, alone or in combination, for persistent IGF-1 elevation","Adjunctive dopamine agonist cabergoline, particularly where prolactin is co-secreted","Radiotherapy for residual or aggressive tumour not controlled medically","Replacement of deficient anterior pituitary hormones and management of glucose intolerance","Serial IGF-1, growth velocity, visual field and metabolic surveillance","Genetic testing for AIP, GPR101, GNAS, MEN1 and PRKAR1A with counselling and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline on Acromegaly","Sperling Pediatric Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pituitary_stalk_interruption_syndrome":{"name":"Pituitary stalk interruption syndrome","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pituitary stalk interruption syndrome","PSIS","ectopic posterior pituitary","pituitary stalk transection syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital condition defined by the imaging triad of a thin or absent (interrupted) pituitary stalk, an absent or hypoplastic anterior pituitary and an ectopic posterior pituitary bright spot","It is a leading cause of congenital hypopituitarism, ranging from isolated growth hormone deficiency to combined or panhypopituitarism","Neonatal presentation may include hypoglycaemia, prolonged jaundice and, in boys, micropenis and undescended testes; later features include short stature and delayed puberty","Posterior pituitary function is often preserved despite the ectopic location, so diabetes insipidus is uncommon","Diagnosis relies on dedicated pituitary MRI demonstrating the classic triad","Some cases follow breech delivery or perinatal injury while others are associated with mutations affecting pituitary development such as HESX1, LHX4, PROP1, SOX3 and OTX2","Anterior hormone deficits may evolve over time, so longitudinal endocrine monitoring is essential","Management is lifelong replacement of the deficient anterior pituitary hormones, including growth hormone, thyroxine, glucocorticoid and sex steroids as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Pituitary stalk interruption syndrome is a developmental disorder of the pituitary in which the infundibulum is thin or absent, the anterior pituitary is hypoplastic and the posterior pituitary is ectopically located at the median eminence, seen as a displaced neurohypophyseal bright spot on MRI. Interruption of the stalk disrupts delivery of hypothalamic releasing hormones to the anterior pituitary and the normal migration of neurohypophyseal tissue, producing variable anterior pituitary hormone deficiencies. The aetiology is heterogeneous, with contributions from mutations in transcription factors governing pituitary organogenesis and from perinatal or traumatic mechanisms; because axonal antidiuretic hormone transport can terminate at the ectopic posterior lobe, vasopressin secretion is frequently spared.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of congenital combined pituitary hormone deficiency without the imaging triad","Septo-optic dysplasia and other midline developmental defects","Craniopharyngioma or another sellar mass causing acquired hypopituitarism","Hypophysitis","Idiopathic isolated growth hormone deficiency","Constitutional delay of growth and puberty"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dedicated pituitary MRI to demonstrate a thin or absent stalk, anterior hypoplasia and an ectopic posterior bright spot","Baseline anterior pituitary hormone panel including IGF-1, free thyroxine and TSH, morning cortisol and ACTH, gonadotropins and prolactin","Dynamic pituitary stimulation testing such as growth hormone provocation and assessment of the cortisol response","Blood glucose monitoring in the neonate for hypoglycaemia","Assessment of posterior pituitary function, usually intact, if polyuria is present","Genetic testing for developmental transcription-factor mutations where indicated"],"redFlags":["Neonatal hypoglycaemia and adrenal insufficiency, which are life-threatening if unrecognised","Evolving cortisol deficiency during intercurrent illness","Severe growth failure","Emergence of new hormone deficits over time, indicating progressive hypopituitarism"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and appropriate hormone replacement the prognosis is good, allowing normal growth and pubertal development; however deficiencies may progress, so lifelong endocrine surveillance is required, and unrecognised cortisol deficiency carries a risk of crisis.","pitfalls":["Attributing neonatal hypoglycaemia or prolonged jaundice to other causes and missing hypopituitarism","Assuming a single hormone deficiency is fixed, when deficits can evolve and require repeated assessment","Overlooking cortisol deficiency before starting growth hormone therapy, which can unmask adrenal insufficiency","Expecting diabetes insipidus and being falsely reassured by its absence"],"management":["Recombinant growth hormone replacement to restore linear growth in growth hormone deficiency","Glucocorticoid replacement with hydrocortisone for corticotroph deficiency, with sick-day dosing education and a steroid alert card","Levothyroxine replacement for central hypothyroidism","Induction and maintenance of puberty with sex-steroid replacement, and fertility support in adulthood","Prompt treatment of neonatal hypoglycaemia and vigilance for adrenal crisis","Lifelong periodic reassessment of all pituitary axes as deficiencies may evolve","Desmopressin only if posterior pituitary function fails and diabetes insipidus develops","Paediatric endocrinology-led care with genetic testing and counselling for developmental transcription-factor mutations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Sperling Pediatric Endocrinology","DeGroot's Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pituitary_tumor_syndromes":{"name":"Pituitary Tumor Syndromes","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pituitary adenoma","Prolactinoma","Hyperprolactinemia","Acromegaly","Pituitary gigantism","Cushing's disease","ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma","Nonfunctioning pituitary adenoma","Gonadotropinoma","Sellar mass"],"chapter":392,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2998","clinicalPearls":["Pituitary adenomas are benign monoclonal neoplasms arising from one of the five anterior pituitary cell types; the secreted hormone (PRL, GH, ACTH, TSH, or LH/FSH) determines the clinical syndrome. About one-third are clinically nonfunctioning, most of these originating from gonadotrope or null cells (p.3003-3004).","Hormone output does not reliably track tumor size: small secretory microadenomas can be highly symptomatic, whereas large adenomas producing little hormone may stay clinically silent until mass effects appear (p.3003).","Suprasellar extension classically compresses the optic chiasm to produce bitemporal hemianopia, often worse superiorly, because crossing nasal ganglion-cell fibers in the ventral chiasm are most vulnerable; loss of red color perception can be an early sign (p.2998-2999).","The 'stalk section' phenomenon: any sellar mass that compresses the pituitary stalk and portal vessels causes early hyperprolactinemia (typically 30-100 ug/L) with later loss of other anterior pituitary hormones, even when the mass itself is not a prolactinoma (p.2998-2999).","Hyperprolactinemia is the most common pituitary hypersecretion syndrome; PRL levels above 200 ug/L almost always indicate a prolactin-secreting adenoma, while milder elevations are more often drug-induced, stalk-related, or due to hypothyroidism or renal failure (p.3004-3005).","Microprolactinomas show a marked female predominance (~20:1) and present early with amenorrhea/galactorrhea/infertility, whereas macroprolactinomas have a near 1:1 sex ratio and men tend to present later with larger, invasive tumors and mass effects (p.3005-3006).","Acromegaly is typically indolent and may go undiagnosed for a decade or more; acral and soft-tissue overgrowth (frontal bossing, prognathism, enlarged hands/feet, coarse features) plus visceromegaly are characteristic, and GH excess before epiphyseal closure causes gigantism (p.3008-3009).","Random single GH measurement is not diagnostic for acromegaly because of GH pulsatility; an age- and sex-matched IGF-1 is the screening test, and diagnosis is confirmed by failure of GH to suppress below threshold after an oral glucose load (p.3009).","Cushing's disease (corticotrope adenoma) accounts for ~70% of endogenous Cushing's syndrome, is 5-10 times more common in women, and the tumors are usually small microadenomas, about half undetectable on MRI (p.3010-3011).","Iatrogenic glucocorticoid use remains the most common overall cause of cushingoid features and must be excluded by history before pursuing an endogenous workup (p.3010).","ACTH-secreting adenomas retain partial suppressibility by high-dose glucocorticoids, distinguishing them from ectopic ACTH sources; inferior petrosal sinus sampling with CRH (central:peripheral ratio >2 basal, >3 post-CRH) has >95% sensitivity for localizing a pituitary source (p.3011-3012).","Nonfunctioning/gonadotrope adenomas are the most common adenoma type at presentation and are usually macroadenomas discovered through mass effect or as incidentalomas; mildly elevated PRL from stalk compression must not be mistaken for a true prolactinoma since these tumors do not shrink with dopamine agonists (p.3012-3013).","Most pituitary glands harbor an unsuspected microadenoma at autopsy (up to one-quarter), and imaging detects clinically inapparent lesions (incidentalomas) in at least 10% of people; small nonsecretory intrasellar lesions can be safely followed with serial MRI (p.2999, p.3003)."],"pathophysiology":"Pituitary adenomas are benign, almost always monoclonal neoplasms derived from a single anterior pituitary cell lineage, implying acquisition of somatic mutation(s) conferring a growth advantage. Hormonally active tumors secrete autonomously with reduced responsiveness to physiologic feedback. Several molecular events are implicated: about 35% of GH-secreting tumors carry activating Gs-alpha mutations that lower GTPase activity, raise cyclic AMP, and drive somatotrope proliferation; growth factors (bFGF, EGFR signaling, the latter linked to USP8 mutation in some ACTH tumors), activated oncogenes (RAS, PTTG), loss of suppressors (e.g., MEG3), and loss of negative feedback (as in primary hypothyroidism/hypogonadism) also contribute. Hypothalamic hormones GHRH and CRH can additionally stimulate mitotic activity, so ectopic GHRH/CRH-producing chest or abdominal tumors can cause somatotrope or corticotrope hyperplasia. Familial settings include MEN 1 (MENIN, 11q13), MEN 4 (CDKN1B), Carney complex (PRKAR1A), McCune-Albright (mosaic Gs-alpha), and familial acromegaly (AIP). Clinical effects arise both from hormone hyper-/hyposecretion and from local mass effect: dorsal expansion lifts the optic chiasm, stalk compression disrupts dopaminergic inhibition of lactotropes (hyperprolactinemia) and portal hormone delivery, and lateral extension into the cavernous sinus impairs cranial nerves III, IV, VI and trigeminal branches.","additionalDifferentials":["Craniopharyngioma (suprasellar cystic, often calcified, Rathke's pouch origin; BRAF V600E in papillary type)","Meningioma (enhancing, may show bony hyperostosis/calcification)","Rathke's cleft cyst","Sellar/clival chordoma","Hypothalamic hamartoma or gangliocytoma (may secrete GnRH/GHRH/CRH)","Hypothalamic and optic gliomas","Parasellar germ cell tumors (germinoma, dysgerminoma, teratoma, choriocarcinoma)","Pituitary metastases (esp. breast and lung; favor posterior pituitary)","Lymphoma, leukemia, plasmacytoma involving the sella","Lymphocytic hypophysitis","Langerhans cell histiocytosis / Histiocytosis X","Empty sella","Arachnoid cyst","Macroprolactinemia (biologically inactive aggregated PRL causing falsely high PRL)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug-induced hyperprolactinemia (antipsychotics esp. risperidone, metoclopramide, methyldopa, verapamil, estrogens, opiates, H2 blockers)","Primary hypothyroidism causing reflex hyperprolactinemia","Chronic renal failure and cirrhosis (reduced PRL clearance)","Pregnancy, lactation, chest-wall stimulation, stress and sleep as physiologic PRL elevation","Polycystic ovarian syndrome (overlap with FSH/LH-secreting tumor causing ovarian hyperstimulation)","Iatrogenic/exogenous glucocorticoid excess mimicking Cushing's disease","Ectopic ACTH or ectopic CRH secretion mimicking Cushing's disease","Adrenal adenoma/carcinoma or adrenal hyperplasia as ACTH-independent Cushing's syndrome","Ectopic GHRH (bronchial/abdominal carcinoid) or rare ectopic GH causing acromegaly"],"additionalInvestigations":["Sagittal and coronal T1-weighted MRI before and after gadolinium (adenomas are hypodense on T1, brighter on T2); CT reserved for bony erosion or calcification","Formal perimetric visual field testing for any chiasm-impinging mass","Baseline pituitary hormonal screen: prolactin, IGF-1, 24-h urinary free cortisol and/or overnight 1-mg dexamethasone suppression, alpha-subunit/FSH/LH, and thyroid function tests","Acromegaly: IGF-1 (age/sex matched) and oral glucose tolerance test with GH at 0/30/60 min (failure to suppress confirms diagnosis)","Hyperprolactinemia: fasting morning PRL with sample dilution when very high (assay 'hook' artifact) and exclusion of macroprolactin","Cushing's disease: 24-h urinary free cortisol, overnight/low- and high-dose dexamethasone suppression, late-night salivary or midnight serum cortisol, basal ACTH, CRH stimulation","Bilateral inferior petrosal sinus ACTH sampling with CRH to localize an ACTH source when MRI is non-diagnostic","Gonadotropinoma/nonfunctioning: FSH, LH, free alpha-subunit, sex steroids; TRH stimulation may provoke atypical gonadotropin/subunit rise","TSH-secreting adenoma: free T4, free T3, TSH, alpha-subunit (inappropriately normal/high TSH with high free T4/T3)","Immunohistochemistry for hormones and cell-specific transcription factors on resected tissue for histologic confirmation"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual field loss or new bitemporal hemianopia (chiasmal compression)","Cranial nerve III/IV/VI palsy, diplopia, ptosis, or facial numbness indicating cavernous sinus invasion","Severe sudden headache with visual loss or ophthalmoplegia suggesting pituitary apoplexy (intratumoral hemorrhage/infarction)","Signs of raised intracranial pressure: headache, vomiting, papilledema, hydrocephalus","Arginine vasopressin deficiency (polyuria) as a presenting feature, which suggests posterior pituitary involvement or a non-adenomatous lesion such as metastasis or germ cell tumor","Rapid-onset hypercortisolism with hyperpigmentation, severe myopathy, and hypokalemic alkalosis suggesting an ectopic ACTH source","Features of acute adrenal insufficiency from coexisting hypopituitarism"],"prognosis":"Pituitary tumors are overwhelmingly benign and slow growing; true carcinomas with extracranial metastases are exceedingly rare. Outcomes depend on tumor type and size and require lifelong follow-up. Prolactinomas respond well to dopamine agonists with normoprolactinemia and gonadal recovery in roughly 80% of microadenomas and tumor shrinkage in most macroadenomas; about 20% (especially men) are dopamine-resistant, and hyperprolactinemia may resolve spontaneously in up to 30% of hyperprolactinemic patients without a visible adenoma. Surgical PRL normalization reaches ~70% for microprolactinomas but only ~40% for macroadenomas, with substantial long-term recurrence (>50% for macroadenomas). In acromegaly, untreated GH excess roughly triples mortality and shortens survival by about 10 years, driven by cardiovascular, cerebrovascular, and respiratory disease and increased colon cancer risk; transsphenoidal remission is ~70% for microadenomas and under 50% for macroadenomas. Cushing's disease has a microadenoma surgical remission of ~80% (<50% for macroadenomas), with ~5% biochemical recurrence and a postoperative period of ACTH deficiency. Untreated hypercortisolism carries excess mortality, chiefly cardiovascular, plus increased infection and suicide risk.","pitfalls":["Assuming PRL suppression by a dopamine agonist proves a prolactinoma: stalk-compression hyperprolactinemia from a nonfunctioning mass also falls with dopamine agonists even though the mass does not shrink.","Very high prolactin levels (>1000 ug/L) may be reported falsely low due to assay 'hook' artifact unless the sample is diluted, leading to underestimation of macroprolactinoma.","Falsely elevated PRL from biologically inactive macroprolactin can prompt unnecessary evaluation.","Diagnosing acromegaly on a single random GH level is unreliable because of GH pulsatility; IGF-1 and a glucose-suppression test are required.","Attributing every small pituitary lesion to a secretory tumor: incidental microadenomas are very common, and ~50% of ACTH adenomas are MRI-occult, so imaging cannot reliably localize the ACTH source.","Overlap in basal ACTH levels between pituitary and ectopic ACTH sources means ACTH measurement alone cannot distinguish them; dynamic testing or petrosal sampling is needed.","Mistaking iatrogenic glucocorticoid excess for endogenous Cushing's syndrome.","Headache severity correlates poorly with adenoma size; a small intrasellar tumor can cause significant headache while a large tumor may not."],"management":["Dopamine agonists such as cabergoline or bromocriptine as first-line therapy for prolactinomas","Transsphenoidal surgery for non-functioning adenomas with mass effect, Cushing's disease, acromegaly and dopamine-agonist-resistant prolactinomas","Medical control of residual hormone excess with somatostatin ligands and pegvisomant for acromegaly and steroidogenesis inhibitors for hypercortisolism","Stereotactic or fractionated radiotherapy for residual or recurrent tumour after surgery","Replacement of any resulting anterior or posterior pituitary hormone deficiencies","Serial pituitary MRI, hormonal profiling and formal visual field testing for surveillance","Emergency management of pituitary apoplexy with stress-dose glucocorticoids and urgent surgical decompression where vision is threatened","Multidisciplinary neuroendocrine care involving neurosurgery, endocrinology and ophthalmology"],"severityClassification":"Pituitary adenomas are conventionally classified by size into microadenomas (<10 mm) and macroadenomas (>=10 mm, often locally invasive with parasellar extension), and by secretory phenotype into functioning (lactotrope/PRL, somatotrope/GH, corticotrope/ACTH, thyrotrope/TSH, gonadotrope/FSH-LH, or plurihormonal) versus clinically nonfunctioning (frequently gonadotrope or null-cell). Sellar mass severity is further graded by anatomic impact in Table 392-1 (compression of pituitary, optic chiasm, hypothalamus, cavernous sinus, frontal lobe, or brain), as larger/invasive tumors carry higher surgical risk and lower cure rates.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2998","Harrison 22e p.2999","Harrison 22e p.3000","Harrison 22e p.3001","Harrison 22e p.3002","Harrison 22e p.3003","Harrison 22e p.3004","Harrison 22e p.3005","Harrison 22e p.3006","Harrison 22e p.3007","Harrison 22e p.3008","Harrison 22e p.3009","Harrison 22e p.3010","Harrison 22e p.3011","Harrison 22e p.3012","Harrison 22e p.3013"],"crossLinks":[]},"pityriasis_alba":{"name":"pityriasis alba","system":"Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pityriasis simplex faciei","erythema streptogenes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common, benign, self-limiting hypomelanosis of children and adolescents, widely regarded as a minor or forme fruste manifestation of atopic dermatitis.","Lesions are ill-defined, round to oval, hypopigmented patches with fine powdery scale, most often on the cheeks, lateral upper arms, and shoulders.","It becomes most conspicuous in summer or in darker skin, when the surrounding skin tans and accentuates the pale patches.","The typical evolution is from a faintly erythematous scaly stage to a residual hypopigmented, minimally scaly patch that gradually resolves.","Wood's lamp shows only mild accentuation with partial preservation of pigment, in contrast to the sharp bright-white of vitiligo.","It is a clinical diagnosis; scrapings for fungal microscopy help exclude pityriasis versicolor when there is diagnostic doubt.","Hypopigmentation reflects post-inflammatory reduction in melanin and disturbed melanosome transfer, not melanocyte destruction.","Management centres on emollients, gentle skin care, sun protection, and reassurance, with low-potency topical anti-inflammatories for troublesome cases."],"pathophysiology":"Pityriasis alba represents a low-grade, subclinical dermatitis, most commonly within the atopic spectrum, that culminates in post-inflammatory hypopigmentation. Mild eczematous inflammation and associated dryness disturb the orderly transfer of melanosomes from melanocytes to keratinocytes and reduce the number and size of active melanosomes, while the overlying hyperkeratosis and scale scatter incident light and further diminish apparent pigmentation. Melanocytes are preserved but functionally quiescent, which is why repigmentation eventually occurs spontaneously. Ultraviolet exposure deepens the contrast by tanning the unaffected surrounding skin. Xerosis, frequent washing, sun exposure, and an atopic diathesis are recognised contributory factors, and the condition is regarded as one of the minor diagnostic criteria of atopic dermatitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Vitiligo","Pityriasis versicolor","Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation from other dermatoses","Naevus depigmentosus","Tinea corporis (faciei)","Early morphoea","Mycosis fungoides (hypopigmented variant)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin scraping with potassium hydroxide microscopy to exclude pityriasis versicolor and dermatophytosis","Wood's lamp examination showing mild, incomplete accentuation","Dermoscopy revealing fine white scale and preserved pigment network","Skin biopsy reserved for atypical, persistent, or treatment-resistant lesions to exclude hypopigmented mycosis fungoides","Assessment for other features of atopic dermatitis","Serial review to confirm gradual spontaneous resolution"],"redFlags":["Persistence into adulthood or lesions that fail to resolve, prompting reconsideration of hypopigmented mycosis fungoides","Progressive, atrophic, or indurated plaques suggesting morphoea or lymphoma","Sharply demarcated, truly depigmented macules indicating vitiligo","Widespread scaly lesions with positive mycology pointing to a fungal infection"],"prognosis":"Pityriasis alba is entirely benign and self-limiting, with pigmentation returning spontaneously over months to a few years, though relapses are common during childhood. It carries no long-term sequelae and requires only supportive care and reassurance.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for vitiligo and causing undue alarm or inappropriate treatment","Overlooking pityriasis versicolor by not performing mycological examination in scaly cases","Attributing persistent adult lesions to pityriasis alba and missing hypopigmented mycosis fungoides","Neglecting the underlying atopic dryness, so that emollient therapy is omitted","Promising rapid resolution and undermining confidence when repigmentation is inevitably slow"],"management":["Reassurance and explanation of the benign, self-limiting nature with expected spontaneous repigmentation","Regular emollients to correct the underlying xerosis","Short courses of a low-potency topical corticosteroid for the erythematous, scaly inflammatory phase","Topical calcineurin inhibitors such as tacrolimus or pimecrolimus as a steroid-sparing option for facial lesions","Photoprotection and sunscreen to reduce the contrast between affected and tanned surrounding skin","Optimise treatment of any coexisting atopic dermatitis","Clinical review to confirm gradual resolution and exclude alternative hypopigmenting disorders"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology, current edition","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"pityriasis_lichenoides":{"name":"Pityriasis lichenoides (chronica/PLC spectrum)","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pityriasis lichenoides chronica","PLC","guttate parapsoriasis","chronic pityriasis lichenoides"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Belongs to a spectrum of benign, self-limiting T-cell-driven papulosquamous eruptions that runs from the chronic pole (PLC) to the acute pole (PLEVA)","PLC produces recurrent crops of small red-brown papules capped by a characteristic adherent mica-like scale that can be lifted off in one piece","Lesions appear in successive crops so that different stages coexist, usually healing with transient post-inflammatory hyper- or hypopigmentation rather than scarring","Most common in children and young adults and typically follows a relapsing-remitting course over months to years","The trunk and the proximal aspects of the limbs are the sites most often involved","Usually asymptomatic or only mildly itchy, which helps separate it from more inflammatory dermatoses","Diagnosis is clinical but supported by biopsy showing a lymphocytic interface reaction; clonality studies may be needed to distinguish it from lymphomatoid papulosis","Management is generally conservative with emollients, topical corticosteroids, and phototherapy for extensive or persistent disease"],"pathophysiology":"Pityriasis lichenoides is best regarded as a reactive, sometimes clonal, T-lymphocyte-driven process rather than a true malignancy. It is thought to represent an exaggerated hypersensitivity reaction, possibly to infectious antigens (viruses and Toxoplasma have been implicated) or to circulating immune complexes, producing a lymphocytic vasculitis with interface change at the dermo-epidermal junction. The chronic end of the spectrum shows a milder, more superficial infiltrate with parakeratotic scale, giving the persistent scaly papules of PLC, whereas the acute pole shows a denser cytotoxic response with keratinocyte necrosis. Post-inflammatory pigmentary change reflects melanin disturbance at the dermo-epidermal interface.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphomatoid papulosis","Guttate psoriasis","Pityriasis rosea","Secondary syphilis","Papular urticaria / arthropod bite reactions","Small-plaque parapsoriasis (digitate dermatosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with histopathology to confirm the interface dermatitis and characterise the infiltrate","Immunohistochemistry and T-cell receptor gene rearrangement studies to exclude lymphomatoid papulosis or cutaneous lymphoma","Serology for possible triggers such as Toxoplasma, Epstein-Barr virus, or HIV when clinically indicated","Full blood count and inflammatory markers in patients who are febrile or systemically unwell","Syphilis serology where secondary syphilis is a diagnostic consideration","Photographic documentation to track the evolution of successive crops"],"redFlags":["Evolution of the acute pole into febrile ulceronecrotic Mucha-Habermann disease with systemic upset","Development of persistent, infiltrated, or ulcerating plaques raising concern for cutaneous lymphoma","Atypical or clonal lymphoid infiltrate on histology requiring formal clonality assessment","Failure to settle or progressive extension despite standard therapy"],"prognosis":"Generally benign and self-limiting, although the chronic form may relapse over months or years; the great majority resolve without scarring, but rare progression to a lymphoproliferative disorder justifies long-term follow-up of atypical cases.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for guttate psoriasis or pityriasis rosea and delaying diagnostic biopsy","Overlooking the overlap with lymphomatoid papulosis, which can be histologically very similar","Assuming a single self-limited episode when the relapsing, crop-by-crop pattern is characteristic","Failing to recognise the rare but potentially serious ulceronecrotic variant"],"management":["Reassurance about the benign, relapsing but generally self-limiting course","Emollients and topical corticosteroids to relieve itch and inflammation","Oral macrolide or tetracycline antibiotics for their anti-inflammatory effect as first-line systemic therapy","Narrowband ultraviolet B or other phototherapy for widespread or persistent disease","Topical calcineurin inhibitors as a steroid-sparing adjunct","Low-dose methotrexate reserved for refractory or extensive disease","Long-term follow-up of atypical lesions with biopsy to detect rare lymphoproliferative progression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":["pleva"]},"pityriasis_lichenoides_et_varioliformis_acuta":{"name":"Pityriasis lichenoides et varioliformis acuta","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLEVA","Mucha-Habermann disease","Acute pityriasis lichenoides"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The acute end of the pityriasis lichenoides spectrum, presenting with recurrent crops of erythematous papules that rapidly develop central vesiculation, necrosis, haemorrhagic crust and varioliform (pox-like) scarring.","Lesions at multiple stages of evolution are present simultaneously (a polymorphic picture), typically on the trunk and flexural aspects of the proximal limbs.","More common in children and young adults; individual lesions resolve over weeks but successive crops make the overall course wax and wane over months.","Often follows an infective or immunological trigger, but the pustulo-necrotic lesions themselves are sterile and the condition is not contagious.","Considered a benign clonal T-cell dyscrasia in many cases, sitting on a spectrum with the chronic form (PLC) and, rarely, with lymphomatoid papulosis.","Febrile ulceronecrotic Mucha-Habermann disease is a rare, aggressive variant with high fever, large coalescing necrotic ulcers and systemic upset that can be life-threatening.","Histology shows a wedge-shaped lymphocytic infiltrate, interface change with keratinocyte necrosis, extravasated red cells and lymphocyte exocytosis.","First-line management includes emollients and topical corticosteroids, with oral erythromycin (used for its anti-inflammatory effect) and phototherapy for persistent disease."],"pathophysiology":"Pityriasis lichenoides et varioliformis acuta is thought to arise from an aberrant, exaggerated host immune response, most often to an antigenic trigger such as a viral or bacterial infection, resulting in a cytotoxic T-cell-driven lymphocytic vasculitis and interface dermatitis. In many patients the infiltrating lymphocytes, predominantly CD8-positive cytotoxic T cells, are monoclonal, supporting the concept of a benign clonal T-cell dyscrasia rather than a simple reactive hypersensitivity; this clonality also underlies the rare biological relationship with lymphomatoid papulosis and cutaneous lymphoma. The cytotoxic attack on keratinocytes and small dermal vessels produces the characteristic wedge-shaped dermal infiltrate, epidermal necrosis, red-cell extravasation and, in severe cases, vessel wall damage with occlusion that generates the deep necrosis and ulceration seen in the febrile ulceronecrotic variant. Proposed triggers include Toxoplasma, Epstein-Barr virus, other viruses and drugs, but no single agent is consistently identified, and the resulting lesions are sterile.","additionalDifferentials":["Pityriasis lichenoides chronica (chronic end of the same spectrum)","Lymphomatoid papulosis and other CD30-positive lymphoproliferative disorders","Varicella and other viral exanthems","Arthropod bite reactions and papular urticaria","Guttate psoriasis and pityriasis rosea","Leukocytoclastic vasculitis","Secondary syphilis and disseminated herpes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing wedge-shaped lymphocytic infiltrate, interface change and keratinocyte necrosis","T-cell receptor gene rearrangement studies where a clonal or lymphomatoid process is suspected","Immunohistochemistry including CD30 to distinguish from lymphomatoid papulosis","Investigations for a triggering infection (for example streptococcal, Epstein-Barr virus or Toxoplasma serology) where clinically indicated","Full blood count and inflammatory markers, particularly if systemic symptoms suggest the febrile ulceronecrotic variant","Swabs of ulcerated or crusted lesions to exclude secondary bacterial infection"],"redFlags":["High fever with large, rapidly coalescing necrotic ulcers indicating febrile ulceronecrotic Mucha-Habermann disease","Systemic features such as marked malaise, mucosal involvement or organ dysfunction","Development of persistent nodules or plaques raising concern for lymphomatoid papulosis or lymphoma","Extensive ulceration with signs of secondary sepsis"],"prognosis":"Most cases are self-limiting, resolving over months to a couple of years with only postinflammatory pigment change or varioliform scars, though relapses are common. The rare febrile ulceronecrotic variant carries significant morbidity and can be fatal, requiring urgent systemic treatment.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the polymorphic, sterile eruption for chickenpox or another contagious viral illness","Failing to recognise and urgently treat the febrile ulceronecrotic variant","Overlooking clonality or CD30 positivity that signals a lymphoproliferative overlap requiring follow-up","Reassuring patients that scarring will not occur when varioliform scars are common","Assuming a single course of treatment cures it, given the relapsing natural history"],"management":["Emollients and topical corticosteroids for symptomatic relief of localised disease","Oral macrolide or tetracycline antibiotics as first-line systemic anti-inflammatory therapy","Narrowband ultraviolet B phototherapy for widespread or persistent eruptions","Methotrexate for refractory or severe disease","Aggressive treatment of the febrile ulceronecrotic Mucha-Habermann variant with systemic immunosuppression such as methotrexate, systemic corticosteroids or ciclosporin, alongside wound care and fluid and nutritional support","Swabs and antibiotics for secondary bacterial infection of ulcerated lesions","Long-term follow-up with repeat biopsy of atypical lesions to exclude lymphomatoid papulosis or cutaneous lymphoma","Urgent inpatient escalation if high fever, extensive necrosis or systemic upset develop"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard dermatology textbooks","Paediatric dermatology clinical references","Peer-reviewed dermatology literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"pityriasis_rosea":{"name":"Pityriasis Rosea","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PR","pityriasis rosea of Gibert"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A single larger oval plaque (the herald patch) commonly precedes the widespread eruption by days to a couple of weeks","The secondary rash consists of smaller oval, salmon-coloured plaques with a fine trailing collarette of scale at the inner edge of the border","Lesions align along skin cleavage lines on the trunk, producing the classic Christmas-tree pattern over the back","It is often preceded or accompanied by a mild viral prodrome and is self-limiting, so reassurance is usually the mainstay of management","Symptomatic itch can be eased with emollients, mild topical anti-inflammatory agents, and antihistamines; some evidence supports sunlight or phototherapy for extensive or itchy disease","Because a widespread scaly eruption on the trunk can mimic secondary syphilis, palmar and plantar involvement or risk factors should prompt syphilis serology","Consider a drug-induced pityriasis rosea-like reaction when the history includes a recently started medication or when features are atypical","Counsel pregnant patients appropriately, as some data suggest an association between early-pregnancy disease and adverse outcomes"],"pathophysiology":"Pityriasis rosea is widely regarded as a self-limiting exanthem linked to reactivation of human herpesviruses 6 and 7, supported by the viral prodrome, seasonal clustering, and detection of viral markers in some patients. The eruption reflects a T-cell-mediated inflammatory response in the skin producing spongiotic dermatitis with focal parakeratosis, which accounts for the fine collarette of scale. The alignment of lesions along Langer's cleavage lines gives the distinctive distribution. A clinically indistinguishable pityriasis rosea-like eruption can be triggered by certain drugs and vaccines through a similar hypersensitivity mechanism.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary syphilis (especially with palm and sole involvement)","Guttate psoriasis","Tinea corporis (the herald patch can be mistaken for ringworm)","Nummular eczema","Drug-induced pityriasis rosea-like eruption","Pityriasis lichenoides"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical based on the herald patch and characteristic distribution","Syphilis serology when the presentation is atypical or there are risk factors, or when palms and soles are involved","Mycology to exclude tinea when a solitary herald patch is uncertain","Review of medication history to identify a possible drug-induced eruption","Skin biopsy reserved for persistent or diagnostically unclear cases"],"redFlags":["Involvement of the palms and soles or a suggestive sexual history pointing to secondary syphilis","Failure to resolve within the expected few months, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Atypical morphology or a temporal link to a new drug suggesting a drug-induced reaction","Early-pregnancy disease, given a possible association with adverse pregnancy outcomes"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limiting, typically clearing over several weeks to a few months without scarring; post-inflammatory pigment change may persist for a while, especially in darker skin, and recurrence is uncommon.","pitfalls":["Overlooking secondary syphilis in a widespread scaly truncal eruption","Mistaking the herald patch for tinea and treating with antifungals alone","Failing to link an atypical eruption to a recently started drug","Over-investigating or over-treating a self-limiting condition instead of reassuring the patient"],"management":["Reassurance and explanation that it is a benign, self-limiting eruption resolving over several weeks","Emollients and mild topical corticosteroids to relieve pruritus","Oral antihistamines for troublesome itch","Natural sunlight exposure or narrowband ultraviolet B phototherapy for extensive or intensely itchy disease","Oral aciclovir considered in early, extensive or severe cases given the herpesvirus association","Review of the drug history to identify a pityriasis rosea-like drug eruption","Syphilis serology when the presentation is atypical or palms and soles are involved"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pityriasis_rubra_pilaris":{"name":"Pityriasis rubra pilaris","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PRP","Devergie disease","pityriasis pilaris","lichen ruber acuminatus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A papulosquamous disorder characterised by orange-red scaly plaques, follicular hyperkeratotic papules, and a waxy palmoplantar keratoderma","Classic clues are well-demarcated 'islands of sparing' of normal skin within an otherwise widespread erythema that may progress to erythroderma","Follicular papules with central keratotic plugs, often giving a nutmeg-grater texture on the dorsal fingers, are highly suggestive","A cephalocaudal spread starting on the head and neck is typical of the classic adult form","Griffiths classification divides it into six types by age of onset, extent, and features, including HIV-associated disease","Histology shows alternating orthokeratosis and parakeratosis in vertical and horizontal directions ('checkerboard' pattern) with follicular plugging","Many classic adult cases remit spontaneously over a few years, though treatment with systemic retinoids or other agents is often needed"],"pathophysiology":"Pityriasis rubra pilaris is a chronic papulosquamous disorder of keratinisation whose precise cause is uncertain. Abnormal epidermal differentiation and follicular hyperkeratosis produce the characteristic follicular papules, orange-red plaques, and palmoplantar keratoderma. A subset of familial cases is linked to gain-of-function mutations in CARD14, implicating dysregulated innate immune (NF-kappaB) signalling, and acquired cases may follow infection or other immune triggers, including HIV. The disordered maturation of keratinocytes leaves discrete areas of preserved skin, producing the diagnostic islands of sparing.","additionalDifferentials":["Psoriasis (especially erythrodermic psoriasis)","Erythroderma of other cause (drug reaction, cutaneous lymphoma)","Atopic or seborrhoeic dermatitis","Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma / Sezary syndrome","Follicular ichthyosis or other disorders of keratinisation","Palmoplantar keratoderma of alternative aetiology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing alternating ortho- and parakeratosis with follicular plugging","HIV testing given the recognised association with type VI disease","Assessment for erythroderma complications including fluid balance and temperature regulation","Exclusion of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma in atypical or refractory erythroderma","Baseline liver function and lipids before systemic retinoid therapy","Review for CARD14-associated familial disease where inherited pattern is suggested"],"redFlags":["Erythroderma with impaired thermoregulation, fluid loss, and high-output cardiac failure","Secondary infection or sepsis of extensively involved skin","Rapidly progressive or atypical disease raising concern for cutaneous lymphoma","New-onset severe disease prompting consideration of underlying HIV infection"],"prognosis":"The classic adult form often resolves spontaneously within one to several years, whereas other subtypes, particularly juvenile-onset familial and HIV-associated disease, tend to be more chronic and refractory.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as psoriasis and missing the islands of sparing and follicular papules","Overlooking HIV as an underlying driver in severe or atypical presentations","Failing to recognise and manage the systemic risks of erythroderma","Not considering cutaneous T-cell lymphoma in persistent unexplained erythroderma"],"management":["Oral retinoids such as acitretin or isotretinoin as first-line systemic therapy","Methotrexate as a second-line systemic agent, alone or combined with a retinoid","Biologic therapy with TNF inhibitors, ustekinumab or IL-17 inhibitors for refractory disease, particularly CARD14-associated cases","Topical emollients, keratolytics, corticosteroids and vitamin D analogues for skin comfort and scaling","Supportive care of erythroderma with attention to fluid balance, temperature regulation and skin-barrier protection","HIV testing and antiretroviral therapy where type VI disease is identified","Monitoring of liver function and lipids during systemic retinoid therapy","Dermatology-led follow-up, excluding cutaneous T-cell lymphoma in refractory erythroderma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pla2g6_associated_neurodegeneration":{"name":"PLA2G6-associated neurodegeneration","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLAN","PLA2G6","infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy","INAD","NBIA type 2","atypical neuroaxonal dystrophy","PLA2G6-related dystonia-parkinsonism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Movement-disorder and neurogenetics textbooks with peer-reviewed reviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive NBIA subtype caused by biallelic variants in PLA2G6, encoding a calcium-independent phospholipase A2","Spans a spectrum from classic infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy through atypical childhood forms to adult-onset dystonia-parkinsonism","Infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy presents in the first years with psychomotor regression, hypotonia evolving to spasticity, and visual impairment with optic atrophy","Characteristic pathology is widespread axonal spheroids (dystrophic axonal swellings) throughout the central and peripheral nervous systems","Cerebellar atrophy is an early and prominent MRI feature, and brain iron accumulation in the globus pallidus and substantia nigra develops later, often not present at first imaging","Iron-sensitive MRI sequences help detect the later basal ganglia iron that classifies the disorder within the NBIA group","The adult-onset form can resemble idiopathic Parkinson disease with dystonia, cognitive decline, and neuropsychiatric features","Diagnosis is confirmed by PLA2G6 molecular testing supported by the imaging evolution; care is symptomatic"],"pathophysiology":"PLA2G6-associated neurodegeneration arises from biallelic variants in PLA2G6, which encodes a calcium-independent phospholipase A2 that remodels membrane phospholipids and maintains membrane integrity. Loss of enzyme function disturbs phospholipid homeostasis and mitochondrial membrane maintenance, leading to lipid peroxidation and progressive axonal degeneration with the formation of dystrophic axonal spheroids. Over time secondary iron accumulates in the globus pallidus and substantia nigra, placing the disorder within the neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation spectrum, though early in the disease iron deposition may be absent.","additionalDifferentials":["Other NBIA subtypes, especially PKAN and BPAN","Leukodystrophies with early regression and spasticity","Mitochondrial encephalopathies (including Leigh syndrome)","Cerebellar ataxias of early childhood","Young-onset Parkinson disease and other genetic parkinsonism (for the adult form)","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to show cerebellar atrophy and, with iron-sensitive sequences, later pallidal and nigral iron","PLA2G6 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Electrophysiology and nerve conduction studies reflecting axonal involvement","Ophthalmological assessment and electroretinography for optic atrophy and visual pathway involvement","Serum caeruloplasmin, copper, and ferritin to distinguish other NBIA subtypes and Wilson disease"],"redFlags":["Rapid developmental regression with loss of vision and motor skills in an infant","Progressive bulbar dysfunction with aspiration risk","New seizures or status epilepticus","Acute respiratory compromise from progressive neuromuscular weakness"],"prognosis":"The classic infantile form is relentlessly progressive, typically leading to severe disability and death in childhood, whereas atypical and adult-onset forms progress more slowly over years to decades. No disease-modifying therapy is currently available.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis on the basis of a normal early MRI when brain iron has not yet accumulated","Missing the early cerebellar atrophy that is often the first imaging clue","Attributing adult-onset dystonia-parkinsonism to idiopathic Parkinson disease without considering a genetic cause","Overlooking the peripheral nervous system involvement when interpreting neurophysiology"],"management":["Symptomatic treatment of dystonia and parkinsonism with a trial of levodopa, anticholinergics and baclofen, and botulinum toxin for focal dystonia","Antiepileptic drugs for seizures alongside spasticity management with physiotherapy and occupational therapy","Nutritional support with management of dysphagia and gastrostomy feeding as regression advances","Respiratory care and management of secretions in advanced disease","Low-vision support and ophthalmology input for optic atrophy","Multidisciplinary neurodisability care with early palliative involvement given the progressive course","Genetic counselling and carrier and family testing for this autosomal recessive PLA2G6 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: PLA2G6-Associated Neurodegeneration","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"placenta_accreta":{"name":"Placenta Accreta Spectrum","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["placenta accreta","placenta increta","placenta percreta","morbidly adherent placenta","PAS","abnormally invasive placenta"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Placenta accreta spectrum describes abnormal trophoblastic invasion into or through the uterine wall, graded as accreta (adherent to myometrium), increta (invading myometrium), and percreta (penetrating serosa and sometimes adjacent organs such as the bladder)","The dominant risk factors are a previous caesarean scar combined with a placenta praevia overlying that scar; risk rises steeply with each additional caesarean delivery","Antenatal diagnosis is largely by ultrasound, with features such as loss of the retroplacental clear zone, placental lacunae, and disruption of the bladder-uterine interface; MRI is a useful adjunct when posterior placentation or bladder involvement is suspected","Attempted separation of an adherent placenta at delivery provokes torrential, life-threatening haemorrhage, so the management principle is to avoid forced removal","Planned delivery in a specialist multidisciplinary centre, typically by scheduled preterm caesarean with the placenta left in situ and caesarean hysterectomy, gives the best maternal outcomes","Preparation includes large-bore venous access, cross-matched blood and a massive transfusion protocol, cell salvage where available, and involvement of urology and interventional radiology","Percreta invading the bladder may require urological support for cystotomy repair or ureteric stenting, hence multidisciplinary planning is essential","Conservative uterus-preserving approaches exist but carry risks of delayed haemorrhage, infection, and the need for later intervention, so they are reserved for selected cases","Any woman with placenta praevia and a prior uterine scar should be actively screened for accreta rather than assumed to have simple praevia"],"pathophysiology":"The disorder arises from a defect in the decidua basalis, usually within a uterine scar, so that the normal Nitabuch fibrinoid layer that limits trophoblast invasion is deficient or absent. Chorionic villi therefore anchor directly onto or into the myometrium instead of the decidua. The depth of invasion determines the grade. Scar tissue is poorly vascularised and lacks a decidual cleavage plane, so at delivery the placenta cannot separate physiologically; disruption of the abnormal placental-myometrial interface tears richly vascular tissue and causes massive obstetric haemorrhage. Neovascularisation around the invasion front contributes to the bleeding risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Simple placenta praevia without abnormal invasion","Uterine atony causing postpartum haemorrhage","Retained placenta without morbid adherence","Placental abruption","Uterine scar dehiscence or rupture","Gestational trophoblastic disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed obstetric ultrasound including transvaginal assessment of the lower segment and colour Doppler of the placental bed","Pelvic MRI when depth of invasion, posterior placentation, or bladder/parametrial involvement is uncertain","Full blood count and baseline coagulation screen","Blood group, antibody screen, and cross-match with activation of a massive transfusion pathway","Cystoscopy or urological assessment where bladder invasion (percreta) is suspected","Anaesthetic and critical care review as part of multidisciplinary planning"],"redFlags":["OBSTETRIC EMERGENCY: catastrophic intrapartum or postpartum haemorrhage on attempted placental separation requiring immediate massive transfusion and surgical control","Haemodynamic instability, tachycardia, and hypotension indicating major blood loss","Placenta percreta with bladder or bowel involvement risking visceral injury and uncontrolled bleeding","Coagulopathy or disseminated intravascular coagulation complicating massive transfusion","Unplanned delivery outside a specialist centre without blood products or surgical backup"],"prognosis":"With antenatal diagnosis and planned management in an experienced multidisciplinary unit, maternal survival is high and morbidity is reduced, though many women require hysterectomy and lose future fertility. Undiagnosed cases presenting acutely carry a substantially higher risk of massive haemorrhage, intensive care admission, visceral injury, and maternal death. Recurrence risk relates to the underlying scarring and future uterine surgery.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for accreta in a woman with placenta praevia and a previous caesarean scar","Attempting to deliver the placenta manually and precipitating uncontrolled haemorrhage","Delivering in a non-specialist setting without blood products, cell salvage, or surgical support","Underestimating bladder involvement in percreta and omitting urological planning","Assuming a conservative uterus-preserving approach is low risk when delayed haemorrhage and sepsis can follow"],"management":["Planned preterm caesarean delivery in a specialist multidisciplinary centre with the placenta left in situ","Caesarean hysterectomy as the definitive procedure, avoiding forced placental separation","Preparation for major haemorrhage with large-bore venous access, cross-matched blood, a massive transfusion protocol and cell salvage","Antenatal corticosteroids for foetal lung maturity before scheduled preterm delivery","Interventional radiology for prophylactic balloon occlusion or embolisation and urology involvement, with ureteric stents where bladder invasion is suspected","Uterus-preserving expectant management with the placenta left in place only in carefully selected women wishing to retain fertility","Postoperative critical care, thromboprophylaxis and monitoring for delayed haemorrhage and infection","Counselling on likely loss of fertility with multidisciplinary planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"placenta_accreta_spectrum":{"name":"Placenta accreta spectrum","system":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAS","morbidly adherent placenta","abnormally invasive placenta","placenta accreta","placenta increta","placenta percreta"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / RCOG and FIGO placenta accreta spectrum guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The spectrum ranges from accreta (villi adherent to myometrium) through increta (invasion into myometrium) to percreta (through the serosa, sometimes into bladder)","The strongest risk factor is a placenta praevia overlying a previous caesarean scar, with risk rising sharply as the number of prior caesareans increases","Other risks include previous uterine surgery, endometrial curettage, and assisted reproduction","Antenatal detection by ultrasound (loss of the clear zone, placental lacunae, bladder-wall interruption) allows planned care and improves outcomes","Magnetic resonance imaging can supplement ultrasound to clarify the depth and topography of invasion, especially posteriorly","The condition threatens catastrophic haemorrhage at delivery because the placenta fails to separate from the uterine wall","Optimal management is planned delivery at a specialist centre, often by scheduled caesarean hysterectomy with the placenta left in situ","A multidisciplinary team, blood product availability, and critical care support are essential for safe delivery"],"pathophysiology":"Placenta accreta spectrum results from defective decidualisation, usually over a uterine scar, so that chorionic villi anchor directly to or invade the myometrium rather than the normal decidua basalis. The absent decidual plane prevents physiological placental separation after delivery, and attempts at removal tear deeply vascular tissue, producing torrential haemorrhage. Deeper invasion (increta and percreta) can breach the uterine serosa and involve adjacent structures such as the bladder, with abnormal neovascularisation increasing operative bleeding.","additionalDifferentials":["Placenta praevia without abnormal adherence","Retained placenta from other causes","Uterine atony","Vasa praevia","Placental abruption","Uterine arteriovenous malformation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed transvaginal and transabdominal ultrasound with colour Doppler","Magnetic resonance imaging to assess depth and posterior or parametrial extension","Assessment of placental location relative to any prior caesarean scar","Cystoscopy or urological assessment when bladder involvement is suspected","Baseline haemoglobin, group and antibody screen with crossmatch","Coagulation profile and planning for major transfusion support"],"redFlags":["Antepartum haemorrhage in a woman with praevia and prior caesarean","Haematuria suggesting bladder invasion in percreta","Signs of concealed or overt major obstetric haemorrhage","Unplanned diagnosis at delivery with a non-separating placenta"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are substantially better with antenatal diagnosis and planned delivery at an experienced centre; unrecognised cases carry a high risk of massive haemorrhage, transfusion, bladder injury, and maternal death. Many women require hysterectomy, with consequent loss of fertility.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen women with praevia and a previous caesarean for abnormal invasion","Attempting forcible manual placental removal, precipitating uncontrollable haemorrhage","Delivering in a unit without immediate access to blood products and specialist surgery","Underestimating percreta with bladder involvement and omitting urological input"],"management":["Scheduled preterm caesarean delivery at an experienced multidisciplinary centre with the placenta left undisturbed","Caesarean hysterectomy as definitive treatment, avoiding any attempt at manual placental removal","Robust preparation for massive obstetric haemorrhage with cross-matched blood, a massive transfusion protocol and cell salvage","Antenatal corticosteroids to promote foetal lung maturity ahead of planned preterm birth","Involvement of interventional radiology for balloon occlusion or embolisation and urology for suspected bladder involvement","Conservative uterus-sparing management with placenta left in situ in selected women prioritising fertility","Postoperative high-dependency care with thromboprophylaxis and vigilance for secondary haemorrhage","Counselling regarding fertility implications with structured follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","RCOG guidance on placenta praevia and placenta accreta","FIGO placenta accreta spectrum consensus guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"placenta_praevia":{"name":"Placenta praevia","system":"Reproductive / Obstetrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["placenta previa","low-lying placenta","placenta overlying the cervical os"],"chapter":null,"source":"RCOG Green-top Guideline / Williams Obstetrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Placenta praevia denotes placental tissue overlying or encroaching on the internal cervical os identified after the mid-trimester; modern nomenclature reserves 'praevia' for a placenta covering the os and 'low-lying placenta' when the edge is within about twenty millimetres of the os but not covering it","The classic presentation is painless, bright-red vaginal bleeding in the second half of pregnancy, often unprovoked or after intercourse, with a soft non-tender uterus and frequently a high or malpresenting fetal part","Risk factors include previous caesarean section and other uterine surgery, previous praevia, multiparity, advanced maternal age, multiple pregnancy, assisted reproduction, and smoking","Digital vaginal examination is contraindicated until praevia is excluded, as it may provoke torrential haemorrhage; assessment relies on ultrasound, with transvaginal scanning being safe and more accurate than the transabdominal route","Placenta praevia overlying a previous caesarean scar markedly raises the risk of placenta accreta spectrum, which should be actively sought with ultrasound and, where uncertain, magnetic resonance imaging","Antenatal care includes counselling on bleeding, planning delivery in a unit with transfusion and neonatal support, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity if early delivery is anticipated, and anti-D prophylaxis for rhesus-negative women","Delivery is by caesarean section, typically planned in the late-preterm to early-term window depending on symptoms, with senior obstetric and anaesthetic involvement and preparation for major haemorrhage","A low-lying placenta detected in the mid-trimester often appears to migrate upward as the lower segment grows, so third-trimester re-imaging is warranted rather than assuming persistence"],"pathophysiology":"The placenta implants in the lower uterine segment rather than the well-vascularised fundus, so that placental tissue lies over or close to the internal cervical os. As the lower segment forms and stretches in the third trimester and the cervix begins to efface and dilate, the relatively inelastic placenta cannot accommodate the shearing forces, and the anchoring villi are torn from the decidua, opening maternal venous sinuses and causing painless bleeding. Because the lower segment contracts poorly after delivery, the placental bed is prone to continued haemorrhage. Abnormal implantation over scarred myometrium, as after caesarean section, is associated with deficient decidualisation and predisposes to a morbidly adherent placenta (accreta spectrum). Repeated or heavy bleeding can compromise both maternal circulating volume and uteroplacental perfusion, threatening the fetus.","additionalDifferentials":["Placental abruption","Vasa praevia","Marginal or circumvallate placental bleeding or a bloody show of labour","Local causes such as cervical ectropion, cervicitis, cervical polyp, or cervical carcinoma","Vaginal or vulval trauma or varicosities","Uterine rupture in a scarred uterus","Onset of normal labour with a bloody show"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound to localise the placenta and measure the distance of the placental edge from the internal os","Transabdominal ultrasound as an initial screen of placental position and fetal presentation","Ultrasound assessment (with magnetic resonance imaging where indicated) for placenta accreta spectrum, particularly with an anterior praevia over a caesarean scar","Full blood count, blood group and antibody screen, and crossmatch or group-and-save","Coagulation studies if bleeding is heavy or recurrent","Cardiotocography or fetal heart auscultation to assess fetal wellbeing","Kleihauer test in rhesus-negative women to quantify feto-maternal haemorrhage and guide anti-D dosing"],"redFlags":["Heavy or continuing vaginal bleeding with maternal haemodynamic instability","Fetal heart rate abnormalities or absent fetal movements","Bleeding with features suggestive of placenta accreta spectrum, such as a known anterior praevia over a prior scar","Onset of painful contractions or labour in a woman with known praevia","Signs of hypovolaemic shock or evolving coagulopathy"],"prognosis":"With antenatal diagnosis, planned caesarean delivery, and ready access to transfusion, maternal and perinatal outcomes are generally good. Praevia complicated by placenta accreta spectrum or major antepartum haemorrhage, however, carries a substantial risk of massive obstetric haemorrhage, hysterectomy, and perinatal morbidity.","pitfalls":["Performing a digital vaginal examination before excluding praevia, risking catastrophic haemorrhage","Relying on transabdominal ultrasound alone, which can misjudge the relationship to the os and miss a posterior praevia","Failing to anticipate and screen for placenta accreta spectrum in an anterior praevia over a caesarean scar","Assuming a mid-trimester low-lying placenta will persist, or has resolved, without third-trimester re-imaging","Under-preparing for delivery by not securing blood products, senior staff, and appropriate anaesthesia"],"management":["Avoidance of digital vaginal examination with advice on pelvic rest to prevent provoking haemorrhage","Planned caesarean delivery for a placenta covering or lying close to the internal os","Antenatal corticosteroids for foetal lung maturity when preterm delivery is anticipated","Anti-D immunoglobulin prophylaxis for rhesus-negative women","Resuscitation and blood transfusion for acute antepartum haemorrhage, with group-and-save or cross-match maintained","Inpatient observation for recurrent or heavy bleeding and delivery in a unit with transfusion and neonatal support","Active surveillance for placenta accreta spectrum, especially with an anterior praevia over a caesarean scar","Emergency caesarean and haemorrhage management if bleeding becomes life-threatening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["RCOG Green-top Guideline on placenta praevia and placenta accreta","Williams Obstetrics","Oxford Handbook of Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"placenta_previa":{"name":"Placenta praevia","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["placenta previa","low-lying placenta","praevia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Placenta praevia is placental implantation over or very close to the internal cervical os, classically presenting as painless bright-red vaginal bleeding in the second half of pregnancy","Digital vaginal examination must be avoided when praevia is suspected because it can provoke catastrophic haemorrhage; ultrasound is the diagnostic tool","Many low-lying placentas identified early in pregnancy migrate upward as the lower segment develops, so diagnosis is confirmed on later transvaginal scanning","Risk factors include previous caesarean section, prior praevia, multiparity, advanced maternal age, multiple pregnancy, and assisted conception","Prior caesarean scars raise the risk of associated placenta accreta spectrum, which should be actively sought on imaging","The fetus often lies in an abnormal (transverse or breech) presentation because the placenta occupies the lower segment","Delivery is by caesarean section for significant praevia; timing and setting are planned to allow for major haemorrhage","Management of bleeding episodes includes resuscitation, blood availability, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity when preterm, and anti-D for rhesus-negative women"],"pathophysiology":"In placenta praevia the placenta implants in the lower uterine segment overlying or adjacent to the internal os. As the lower segment stretches and the cervix effaces in later pregnancy and labour, the inelastic placental attachment is disrupted from the underlying decidua, tearing maternal vessels and causing painless bleeding. Because the lower segment contracts poorly after delivery, the placental bed is prone to continued haemorrhage. Prior uterine scarring predisposes to abnormally adherent or invasive placentation (accreta spectrum).","additionalDifferentials":["Placental abruption","Vasa praevia","Local causes such as cervical ectropion, polyp, or cervical carcinoma","Bloody show of labour","Uterine rupture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transabdominal and confirmatory transvaginal ultrasound to localise the placenta relative to the os","Colour Doppler or MRI to evaluate for placenta accreta spectrum, especially with prior caesarean","Full blood count, group and save or cross-match according to bleeding","Coagulation studies if haemorrhage is significant","Cardiotocography to assess fetal wellbeing during bleeding episodes"],"redFlags":["Heavy or recurrent vaginal bleeding causing maternal haemodynamic instability","Signs of hypovolaemic shock requiring urgent resuscitation and delivery","Imaging features of placenta accreta, increta, or percreta","Non-reassuring fetal heart pattern during a bleed","Bleeding with uterine contractions suggesting onset of labour with praevia"],"prognosis":"With antenatal diagnosis, planned caesarean delivery, and ready access to blood products, maternal and perinatal outcomes are generally good. Uncontrolled haemorrhage, particularly with coexisting accreta spectrum, remains a major cause of maternal morbidity, massive transfusion, and peripartum hysterectomy.","pitfalls":["Performing a digital vaginal examination before excluding praevia","Labelling an early low-lying placenta as definitive praevia without rescanning later","Overlooking placenta accreta spectrum in women with previous caesarean and anterior praevia","Underestimating blood loss and failing to prepare adequate transfusion support","Attributing painless antepartum bleeding to a benign local cause without imaging"],"management":["Strict avoidance of digital vaginal examination, with reliance on ultrasound for diagnosis","Elective caesarean section as the planned mode of delivery for confirmed praevia","Antenatal corticosteroids to accelerate foetal lung maturity if early delivery is likely","Anti-D prophylaxis in rhesus-negative women after bleeding episodes","Maternal resuscitation, cross-matched transfusion and coagulation support during acute haemorrhage","Hospital admission and monitoring for repeated bleeding, with cardiotocographic assessment of foetal wellbeing","Imaging surveillance for coexisting placenta accreta spectrum where there is a prior caesarean scar","Escalation to emergency caesarean delivery for uncontrolled bleeding or foetal compromise"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"placental_abruption":{"name":"Placental abruption","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["abruptio placentae","placental abruptio","premature separation of the placenta"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Abruption is premature separation of a normally sited placenta before delivery, classically presenting with painful vaginal bleeding and a tender, firm, or hypertonic uterus","Bleeding may be concealed behind the placenta, so the degree of vaginal blood loss can badly underestimate the true haemorrhage","Risk factors include hypertension and pre-eclampsia, trauma, cocaine or tobacco use, prior abruption, polyhydramnios with rapid decompression, and thrombophilia","Fetal compromise or death can occur rapidly because the separated placenta cannot support gas exchange","It is largely a clinical diagnosis; ultrasound is insensitive and a normal scan does not exclude abruption","Disseminated intravascular coagulation is an important complication, particularly with large abruptions or fetal demise","Management depends on maternal and fetal status: resuscitation, and prompt delivery (often by caesarean) when there is fetal distress or maternal instability","Anti-D should be given to rhesus-negative women and blood products used to correct coagulopathy"],"pathophysiology":"Abruption begins with rupture of maternal decidual vessels, causing bleeding into the decidua basalis that splits the placenta from the uterine wall. The expanding retroplacental clot further separates the placenta, reducing the functional area for maternal-fetal exchange and provoking uterine irritability and contraction. Thromboplastins released from the disrupted placenta and decidua enter the maternal circulation and can trigger consumptive coagulopathy (disseminated intravascular coagulation), while extravasation of blood into the myometrium (Couvelaire uterus) impairs contractility and postpartum haemostasis.","additionalDifferentials":["Placenta praevia","Uterine rupture","Preterm labour","Vasa praevia","Local genital tract causes of bleeding","Acute abdomen from a non-obstetric cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous cardiotocography to detect fetal compromise and uterine hypertonicity","Full blood count and serial haemoglobin","Coagulation screen including fibrinogen to identify disseminated intravascular coagulation","Group and cross-match for transfusion","Ultrasound mainly to exclude praevia rather than to confirm abruption","Kleihauer test in rhesus-negative women to quantify fetomaternal haemorrhage"],"redFlags":["Maternal haemodynamic instability or shock, which may be disproportionate to visible blood loss due to concealed haemorrhage","Non-reassuring or absent fetal heart activity","Developing coagulopathy with falling fibrinogen and abnormal clotting","A rigid, exquisitely tender uterus with frequent contractions","Rapid clinical deterioration requiring emergency delivery"],"prognosis":"Small abruptions with a stable mother and reassuring fetus may allow expectant management with good outcomes. Large abruptions carry high risks of fetal death, maternal haemorrhage, coagulopathy, and organ injury, and abruption is a leading cause of perinatal mortality and antepartum stillbirth. Recurrence risk in future pregnancies is increased.","pitfalls":["Being falsely reassured by minimal external bleeding when concealed haemorrhage is severe","Relying on ultrasound to rule out abruption","Underestimating and undertreating evolving coagulopathy","Delaying delivery in the face of fetal distress or maternal instability","Failing to consider abruption after seemingly minor abdominal trauma in pregnancy"],"management":["Maternal resuscitation with intravenous access, fluids and blood transfusion for significant haemorrhage","Urgent delivery, usually by caesarean, when there is foetal compromise or maternal instability","Continuous cardiotocographic monitoring to detect foetal distress and uterine hypertonus","Correction of coagulopathy and disseminated intravascular coagulation with fresh frozen plasma, cryoprecipitate, fibrinogen and platelets","Antenatal corticosteroids and expectant management for small stable abruptions remote from term","Anti-D immunoglobulin guided by the Kleihauer test in rhesus-negative women","Anticipation and active management of postpartum haemorrhage with uterotonics for a poorly contracting Couvelaire uterus","Treatment of underlying hypertension or pre-eclampsia with follow-up given the recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"placental_insufficiency":{"name":"Placental insufficiency","system":"Reproductive / Obstetrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["uteroplacental insufficiency","placental dysfunction"],"chapter":null,"source":"RCOG small-for-gestational-age fetus guidance / Williams Obstetrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Placental insufficiency describes inadequate placental transfer of oxygen and nutrients, most often manifesting as fetal growth restriction, oligohydramnios, and, if progressive, fetal hypoxia","It commonly underlies placenta-mediated disease, linking fetal growth restriction with pre-eclampsia through the shared mechanism of deficient spiral artery remodelling","Risk factors include hypertensive disorders, maternal vascular and autoimmune disease such as antiphospholipid syndrome, pre-existing diabetes or renal disease, smoking, thrombophilia, and a previous growth-restricted or stillborn pregnancy","Distinguishing a constitutionally small but healthy fetus from a growth-restricted fetus with placental dysfunction is central and rests on growth trajectory and Doppler studies rather than size alone","Umbilical artery Doppler is the key surveillance tool: rising resistance, then absent, and finally reversed end-diastolic flow indicate worsening placental function and impending fetal compromise","In late-onset disease the cerebroplacental ratio and middle cerebral artery Doppler ('brain-sparing') may reveal redistribution even when the umbilical artery Doppler appears near-normal","Ductus venosus Doppler and computerised cardiotocography help time delivery in early-onset severe cases, where prematurity must be balanced against the risk of stillbirth","Management centres on surveillance and optimally timed delivery, with antenatal corticosteroids for lung maturity and magnesium sulphate for neuroprotection when very preterm birth is planned"],"pathophysiology":"In normal pregnancy, extravillous trophoblast invades and remodels the maternal spiral arteries into wide, low-resistance vessels that deliver a high, steady blood flow to the intervillous space. In placental insufficiency this transformation is incomplete, leaving high-resistance vessels prone to spasm and thrombosis; the resulting malperfusion causes placental ischaemia, infarction, and villous maldevelopment. Reduced surface area and impaired diffusion limit oxygen and nutrient transfer, restricting fetal growth. The fetus adapts by redistributing cardiac output to preserve the brain, heart, and adrenals at the expense of the kidneys and periphery, which reduces renal perfusion and amniotic fluid, producing oligohydramnios. Progressive hypoxaemia and acidaemia are reflected in deteriorating arterial and then venous Doppler indices and, ultimately, in abnormal cardiotocography and stillbirth if delivery is not achieved.","additionalDifferentials":["A constitutionally small-for-gestational-age but healthy fetus","Incorrect gestational dating","Fetal aneuploidy or a genetic syndrome causing growth restriction","Congenital infection such as cytomegalovirus or toxoplasmosis","A structural fetal anomaly","Multiple pregnancy with twin-to-twin transfusion or selective growth restriction","Oligohydramnios from ruptured membranes or a fetal renal anomaly"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial ultrasound biometry to assess fetal size and growth velocity","Amniotic fluid volume assessment by deepest vertical pocket or amniotic fluid index","Umbilical artery Doppler for placental resistance and end-diastolic flow","Middle cerebral artery Doppler and cerebroplacental ratio for redistribution","Ductus venosus Doppler in early-onset severe restriction to help time delivery","Cardiotocography, including computerised analysis of short-term variation","Investigations for an underlying cause, including maternal blood pressure and urinalysis, uterine artery Doppler, screening for infection, thrombophilia or autoimmune disease, and karyotype or anomaly assessment where indicated"],"redFlags":["Absent or reversed umbilical artery end-diastolic flow","Abnormal ductus venosus Doppler with an absent or reversed a-wave","Reduced or absent fetal movements","Static or declining fetal growth on serial scans","Abnormal cardiotocography or reduced short-term variation"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the severity and gestation at onset; late-onset, well-surveilled disease often does well with timely delivery, whereas early-onset severe insufficiency carries high risks of stillbirth, iatrogenic prematurity, and long-term neurodevelopmental impairment.","pitfalls":["Equating small fetal size with growth restriction, or vice versa, without Doppler and growth-trajectory assessment","Over-relying on a single normal umbilical artery Doppler in late-onset disease and missing redistribution","Inaccurate dating leading to misclassification","Failing to investigate for underlying maternal or fetal causes","Delivering too early or too late by not integrating the full surveillance picture"],"management":["Intensive foetal surveillance with serial growth biometry, umbilical artery and ductus venosus Doppler and cardiotocography","Timing of delivery according to Doppler and cardiotocographic deterioration as the definitive intervention","Antenatal corticosteroids for foetal lung maturity ahead of anticipated preterm delivery","Magnesium sulfate for foetal neuroprotection when very preterm delivery is planned","Optimisation of underlying maternal disease such as hypertension, diabetes or antiphospholipid syndrome with aspirin and heparin","Low-dose aspirin from early pregnancy in women at high risk of placental disease","Smoking cessation and modification of contributory factors","Delivery and neonatal care in a unit with an appropriate level of neonatal support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["RCOG Green-top Guideline on the small-for-gestational-age fetus","Williams Obstetrics","ISUOG practice guidelines on Doppler assessment and fetal growth"],"crossLinks":[]},"plague":{"name":"Plague","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Yersinia pestis"],"chapter":176,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1341-1347","clinicalPearls":["Plague is a systemic zoonosis caused by Yersinia pestis, predominantly affecting small rodents in rural areas of Africa, Asia, and the Americas and usually transmitted to humans by an arthropod (flea) vector; less often via contact with animal tissues or respiratory droplets (p.1341).","Three principal clinical forms: bubonic, septicemic, and pneumonic. Worldwide, bubonic plague is the predominant reported form; a minority (10-20%) present as primary septicemic or pneumonic disease (p.1343).","Bubonic plague: after a 2-6 day incubation, sudden onset of fever (>38 degC), malaise, myalgia, dizziness, and increasing pain from progressive regional lymphadenitis. The bubo is a tense, tender swelling with a boggy consistency and underlying hard core, usually solitary and erythematous with periganglionic edema, most commonly inguinal but may be crural, axillary, cervical, or submaxillary depending on bite site (p.1343).","Children most commonly present with cervical or axillary buboes; abdominal pain from intraabdominal node involvement can occur without other visible signs (p.1343).","Primary pneumonic plague follows inhalation of infectious droplets from another person or animal with plague pneumonia; short incubation (a few hours to 2-3 days, range 1-7 days) with abrupt fever, headache, myalgia, weakness, nausea, vomiting, dizziness; respiratory signs (cough, dyspnea, chest pain, sputum with hemoptysis) typically appear after 24 h (p.1344).","Primary pneumonic plague is generally the least common but most fatal of the main presentations; treated pneumonic plague has an estimated death rate of about 17% versus 98% for untreated pneumonic plague (p.1343).","Primary septicemic plague (10-25% of infections) presents as gram-negative septicemia (hypotension, shock) WITHOUT preceding lymphadenopathy; occurs in all age groups but risk is elevated in persons over 40 (p.1344).","WBC count is generally raised (10,000-20,000/uL) with neutrophilic leukocytosis and left shift; counts can be very high in children (>100,000/uL), but in some cases the WBC is normal or leukopenia develops. DIC occurs in a significant minority (p.1346).","Appropriate treatment of bubonic plague resolves fever within 2-5 days, but buboes may remain enlarged for more than a week after initial treatment and can become fluctuant (p.1344)."],"pathophysiology":"Y. pestis is a gram-negative bacterium of the order Enterobacterales that recently evolved from the enteric pathogen Y. pseudotuberculosis. It has a two-stage life cycle alternating between arthropod (flea) and a wide range of mammalian hosts. In the flea, the organism multiplies and forms biofilm-embedded aggregates in the midgut; some fleas become 'blocked' at the proventriculus and regurgitate bacteria into the bite site, while early-phase unblocked transmission also occurs. In the mammalian host, dissemination from the inoculation site is initially dependent on plasminogen activator (Pla), which activates plasminogen, degrades complement, and binds laminin, and is essential for high-level virulence and bubo formation. Bacteria are rapidly transported to regional lymph nodes where Y. pestis replicates extracellularly with full expression of antiphagocytic systems (type III secretion machinery and effectors encoded by pYV plus the F1 capsule), producing a bubo (hemorrhagic/necrotic node with thrombosed vessels, replaced architecture, and abundant bacteria). LcrV targets phagocytes and exerts an anti-inflammatory effect, and modified lipopolysaccharide minimizes Toll-like receptor 4 stimulation, prolonging high-grade bacteremia. Spread via efferent lymphatics to the thoracic duct produces high-grade bacteremia and hematogenous seeding of spleen, liver, secondary buboes, lungs (secondary plague pneumonia), and meninges, with uncontrolled septicemia leading to death; the septicemic phase can occur without an obvious prior bubo (p.1342-1343).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute focal lymphadenopathy of other etiologies (for bubonic plague): streptococcal or staphylococcal infection, tularemia, cat-scratch disease, tick typhus, infectious mononucleosis, lymphatic filariasis (p.1343).","Tuberculosis (in differential of fever and hemoptysis in plague-endemic areas, as a small-airborne-particle-transmitted infection) (p.1347).","Tonsillitis with peritonsillar abscess (resembles plague pharyngitis) (p.1344)."],"infectionMimics":["Tularemia (Francisella tularensis) (p.1343)","Cat-scratch disease (Bartonella henselae) (p.1343)","Tick typhus (rickettsial) (p.1343)","Infectious mononucleosis (p.1343)","Lymphatic filariasis (p.1343)","Streptococcal or staphylococcal lymphadenitis (p.1343)","Tuberculosis (fever and hemoptysis differential) (p.1347)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bubo aspirate (bubonic), bronchoalveolar lavage fluid or sputum (pneumonic), and blood (septicemic) are the appropriate specimens; culture of postmortem organ biopsy can also be diagnostic. Transport medium such as Cary-Blair preserves viability (p.1345).","Direct microscopy: Gram-negative coccobacilli with characteristic bipolar 'closed safety pin' appearance on Wayson or Wright-Giemsa stain; bipolar bacilli may be visible on direct blood smear in septicemic plague, indicating very high circulating bacterial numbers and a poor prognosis (p.1345).","Culture on nutrient-rich media (sheep blood agar, brain-heart infusion broth) and selective agar (MacConkey, EMB, or Yersinia-specific CIN agar); optimal growth at <37 degC (25-29 degC). Y. pestis is oxidase-negative, catalase-positive, urease-negative, indole-negative, lactose-negative, and nonmotile (distinguishing it from other Yersinia species) (p.1345).","F1 antigen detection: direct immunofluorescence and rapid immunochromatographic test strip (F1RDT), effective on bubo aspirates and sputum and widely used in endemic countries; a Pla antigen strip can detect F1-negative strains (p.1345).","PCR/RT-PCR requiring positivity for at least two Y. pestis-specific targets (e.g., caf1, pla, and chromosomal genes); FDA-authorized BioFire FilmArray panels include Y. pestis (p.1346).","Serology: rising hemagglutinating antibody titer to F1 antigen, or seroconversion/fourfold rise in paired sera; ELISA for IgG and IgM to F1 antigen (p.1345-1346).","WHO case definitions (suspected/probable/confirmed) guide presumptive diagnosis followed by reference laboratory confirmation (p.1345)."],"redFlags":["Pneumonic plague is transmissible person-to-person via respiratory droplets (productive cough), making it a public-health emergency requiring immediate isolation and notification (p.1344, 1347).","Aerosolized Y. pestis is a recognized potential bioterrorism agent (Tier 1 select agent); an outbreak of primary pneumonic plague in a nonendemic region or urban setting should raise concern for intentional release (p.1342, 1344, 1345).","Rapid progression of primary pneumonic plague from segmental pneumonitis to lobar and bilateral involvement with hemoptysis and ARDS within hours to days (p.1344).","Bipolar bacilli visible on direct blood smear indicate very high circulating bacterial numbers and a poor prognosis (p.1345).","Disseminated intravascular coagulation with shock in septicemic disease (p.1346)."],"prognosis":"Untreated plague carries high mortality, but it is treatable with antimicrobials. Treated pneumonic plague has an estimated death rate of about 17% compared with 98% for untreated pneumonic plague. In the United States, case-fatality fell from 66% in 1900-1941 to 16% in the post-antibiotic era (1942 onward); in 2000-2018, mortality was 8% in patients receiving any antimicrobial and 4% in those receiving currently recommended high-efficacy agents. The global case-fatality rate notified to WHO for 2013-2018 was 17%. Visible bipolar bacilli on direct blood smear signal very high bacteremia and poor prognosis (p.1343, 1345).","pitfalls":["The term 'septicemic plague' can be confusing because most patients with buboes have detectable bacteremia at some stage, with or without systemic sepsis signs (p.1344).","Automated biochemical or mass-spectrometry identification systems can misidentify Y. pestis as Y. pseudotuberculosis or other species (p.1345).","Meningeal plague may be associated with suboptimal antimicrobial therapy (delayed treatment, penicillin use, or low-dose tetracycline) (p.1344).","A single negative rapid antigen test is insufficient to exclude plague; WHO exclusion requires two negative F1RDT tests on specimens collected 24 h apart when confirmatory tests are unavailable (p.1345-1346).","F1RDT specificity for culture-confirmed pneumonic plague was only 71%, so a positive sputum test in an endemic area identifies a probable case requiring treatment but needs additional confirmatory testing (p.1345-1346).","Culture yield can be low after prehospital antimicrobial use (as observed in the 2017 Madagascar outbreak) (p.1345)."],"management":["Prompt antimicrobial therapy with an aminoglycoside such as streptomycin or gentamicin as first-line treatment","Alternative or adjunctive antimicrobials such as fluoroquinolones or tetracyclines including doxycycline, with chloramphenicol for plague meningitis","Supportive care for sepsis including intravenous fluids, oxygen and organ support in critical illness","Respiratory droplet isolation of patients with pneumonic plague until adequately treated","Post-exposure antibiotic prophylaxis with doxycycline or a fluoroquinolone for close contacts of pneumonic cases","Urgent notification to public health authorities with contact tracing","Environmental flea and rodent control to interrupt transmission","Escalation to critical care for septicaemic or pneumonic disease with monitoring for complications"],"severityClassification":"WHO case classification of plague: Suspected case (clinical presentation suggestive of plague plus an epidemiologic context of possible exposure within 10 days of onset); Probable case (suspected case plus one of: F1 antigen detected by rapid test or immunofluorescence, a single anti-F1 serology without prior infection/immunization, or direct microscopy showing bipolar-staining gram-negative coccobacilli); Confirmed case (suspected case plus one of: isolation of Y. pestis, seroconversion or fourfold anti-F1 titer change in paired sera, or species-specific PCR positivity); Not a case (suspected case with negative confirmatory testing) (p.1345).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Chapter 176: Plague and Other Yersinia Infections (Michael B. Prentice), pp.1341-1347","Bertherat E, Jullien S: Revision of the international definition of plague cases. Wkly Epidemiol Rec 24:238, 2021","Nelson CA et al: Antimicrobial treatment and prophylaxis of plague: Recommendations for naturally acquired infections and bioterrorism response. MMWR Recomm Rep 70(No. RR-3):1, 2021","Salam AP et al: Deaths associated with pneumonic plague, 1946-2017"],"crossLinks":[]},"plaid_syndrome":{"name":"PLAID (PLCG2-associated antibody deficiency and immune dysregulation)","system":"Immunology / Autoinflammatory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLCG2-associated antibody deficiency and immune dysregulation","familial cold urticaria with immunodeficiency","PLCG2","phospholipase C gamma 2 defect"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Autoinflammation / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by mutations in PLCG2, which encodes phospholipase C gamma 2, a signalling enzyme important in immune cells","Characterised by cold-induced urticaria triggered by evaporative cooling rather than by contact with a cold object","Combines an autoinflammatory cold urticaria with features of humoral immunodeficiency and autoimmunity","Antibody deficiency predisposes to recurrent sinopulmonary infection and may require immunoglobulin support","Autoimmune and granulomatous manifestations, including autoantibodies, can coexist with the immunodeficiency","The cold urticaria differs from classic acquired cold urticaria and from cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes","Management is largely supportive, targeting infection risk, cold avoidance, and specific autoimmune complications"],"pathophysiology":"PLAID results from mutations in PLCG2 that alter phospholipase C gamma 2 signalling in immune cells such as B lymphocytes, natural killer cells, and mast cells. The abnormal enzyme shows enhanced activity at reduced temperatures, so mild cooling of the skin triggers mast cell activation and urticaria. The same signalling defect disturbs B-cell development and function, producing antibody deficiency alongside a paradoxical tendency to autoimmunity and, in some, granulomatous disease. This dual disturbance explains the coexistence of cold urticaria, immunodeficiency, and immune dysregulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes (CAPS) with cold-triggered features","Acquired cold urticaria","Common variable immunodeficiency","APLAID (PLCG2 autoinflammation and immune dysregulation) variant","Systemic autoimmune disease with cutaneous features","Chronic spontaneous urticaria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for PLCG2 mutations","Evaporative cooling challenge reproducing cold urticaria","Immunoglobulin levels and specific antibody responses","Lymphocyte subset analysis including B-cell subsets","Autoantibody panel to assess autoimmunity","Assessment for granulomatous disease where clinically suggested"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or severe sinopulmonary infections indicating significant antibody deficiency","Anaphylaxis or airway involvement during cold exposure","Emergence of serious autoimmune or granulomatous complications","Bronchiectasis from repeated respiratory infection"],"prognosis":"PLAID is a chronic condition; with cold avoidance, infection management, and treatment of autoimmune complications, many patients do well, though antibody deficiency and autoimmunity require ongoing care.","pitfalls":["Confusing cold-evaporation urticaria with cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes and mistargeting therapy","Overlooking the underlying antibody deficiency and infection risk","Attributing autoimmunity to an unrelated primary autoimmune disease","Testing only for classic contact cold urticaria and missing the evaporative trigger"],"management":["Avoidance of evaporative skin cooling, the principal trigger of the cold urticaria","Antihistamines to attenuate cold-induced urticarial symptoms","Immunoglobulin replacement therapy for significant antibody deficiency","Prompt treatment and, where needed, prophylaxis of recurrent sinopulmonary infections","Immunosuppressive treatment of autoimmune and granulomatous manifestations as they arise","Regular vaccination and immunological monitoring within specialist immunology care","Genetic counselling and family testing for this PLCG2-associated dominant disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Autoinflammation","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"plantar_fasciitis":{"name":"Plantar fasciitis","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["plantar fasciopathy","plantar heel pain","plantar fasciosis","heel spur syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon / standard sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The hallmark is sharp inferior heel pain that is worst with the first few steps in the morning or after periods of rest, then eases with activity","Tenderness is maximal at the medial calcaneal tubercle where the plantar fascia originates","It is a degenerative fasciopathy from repetitive overload rather than a primarily inflammatory condition, despite the -itis suffix","Risk factors include obesity, prolonged standing, reduced ankle dorsiflexion, tight calf muscles, and a sudden increase in running load","Diagnosis is clinical; a windlass test reproducing pain on passive great-toe dorsiflexion supports it","Heel spurs seen on radiographs are common incidental findings and do not cause the pain","Management principles are calf and plantar fascia stretching, load management, supportive footwear, and orthoses or heel cushioning","Most cases resolve within a year with conservative care; corticosteroid injection gives short-term relief but risks fat-pad atrophy and fascial rupture","Persistent bilateral heel pain, especially in a young person with inflammatory features, should prompt consideration of a seronegative spondyloarthropathy"],"pathophysiology":"Plantar fasciitis results from repetitive traction overload at the origin of the plantar aponeurosis on the medial calcaneal tuberosity. The tissue undergoes microtearing and a degenerative response with collagen disorganisation, mucoid change, and thickening of the fascia rather than sustained acute inflammation. Impaired ankle dorsiflexion and tight gastrocsoleus increase tensile strain, and the failed healing perpetuates pain.","additionalDifferentials":["Calcaneal stress fracture","Fat-pad atrophy or contusion","Tarsal tunnel syndrome or entrapment of the nerve to abductor digiti minimi (Baxter's nerve)","S1 radiculopathy referring to the heel","Inflammatory enthesitis from seronegative spondyloarthropathy","Insertional Achilles or retrocalcaneal pathology (posterior heel)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Usually diagnosed clinically without imaging","Ultrasound to demonstrate a thickened, hypoechoic proximal plantar fascia","MRI in atypical or refractory cases and to exclude calcaneal stress fracture or nerve entrapment","Plain radiographs to identify a calcaneal stress fracture or other bony pathology","Inflammatory screen and referral if a spondyloarthropathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting inflammatory arthropathy such as young age, bilateral disease, and other enthesitis or back symptoms","Focal bony tenderness or night pain raising concern for stress fracture or tumour","Numbness, tingling, or a positive Tinel sign suggesting nerve entrapment","Systemic symptoms or signs of infection"],"prognosis":"The large majority of patients improve within six to twelve months with conservative treatment, though symptoms can be protracted; recalcitrant cases may benefit from extracorporeal shockwave therapy before considering surgery.","pitfalls":["Attributing symptoms to an incidental heel spur","Overusing corticosteroid injection with risk of fat-pad atrophy or fascial rupture","Missing a calcaneal stress fracture or nerve entrapment presenting as heel pain","Overlooking an underlying inflammatory spondyloarthropathy in bilateral or young patients"],"management":["Activity and load modification with relative rest from aggravating weight-bearing","Plantar fascia and gastrocsoleus stretching with calf-strengthening exercises through physiotherapy","Supportive footwear with cushioned heel cups or arch-supporting orthoses","Night splints to maintain dorsiflexion and reduce first-step morning pain","Simple analgesia and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for symptom relief","Corticosteroid injection reserved for refractory pain, used cautiously given the rupture risk","Extracorporeal shockwave therapy for recalcitrant cases before considering surgery","Weight reduction and addressing contributory factors, with surgical release only as a last resort"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"plantar_fibromatosis":{"name":"Plantar fibromatosis","system":"Musculoskeletal system / Foot and ankle","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ledderhose disease","plantar fascial fibromatosis","Morbus Ledderhose"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle / Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign fibroblastic and myofibroblastic proliferation of the plantar aponeurosis, presenting as one or more firm, slowly enlarging nodules in the medial or central band of the arch","Nodules are typically fixed to the fascia but mobile relative to overlying skin, and are often bilateral; unlike Dupuytren disease they seldom produce fixed toe contractures because the digital extensions of the fascia are less involved","It forms part of the superficial fibromatosis or fibromatosis diathesis, so associations with Dupuytren contracture of the hand, Peyronie disease and Garrod knuckle pads should be sought","Reported associations include diabetes, chronic alcohol use, chronic liver disease, epilepsy and long-term anticonvulsant therapy","MRI is characteristic, showing an infiltrative fascial lesion that is typically low to intermediate in signal on both T1 and T2 owing to its dense collagen content, which helps distinguish it from a sarcoma","Histology shows bland spindled myofibroblasts within dense collagen without atypia, distinguishing it from fibrosarcoma and other malignant soft-tissue tumours","First-line treatment is non-operative with soft accommodative orthoses and padding to offload the nodule; surgery is reserved for disabling disease and carries a high recurrence rate","Recurrence is substantially lower after wide or complete plantar fasciectomy than after simple local nodule excision, at the cost of greater morbidity"],"pathophysiology":"Plantar fibromatosis is a locally infiltrative but biologically benign proliferation of fibroblasts and myofibroblasts within the plantar aponeurosis, most often the medial band. Disordered wound-healing-type signalling with excess transforming growth factor beta and platelet-derived growth factor drives myofibroblast proliferation and deposition of dense type III and type I collagen, producing the palpable nodules. Repetitive microtrauma, a genetic predisposition shared with other superficial fibromatoses, and metabolic factors such as diabetes and alcohol are thought to contribute. Progression through cellular proliferative, involutional and residual collagenous phases parallels the pattern seen in Dupuytren disease, though contracture of the toes is far less common because the vertical and digital fascial extensions are relatively spared.","additionalDifferentials":["Plantar fibroma versus a soft-tissue sarcoma such as synovial sarcoma or fibrosarcoma","Plantar fascial rupture or chronic plantar fasciitis with a fibrotic nodule","Ganglion or epidermal inclusion cyst of the sole","Foreign-body granuloma or infective granuloma","Rheumatoid or other inflammatory nodule","Giant cell tumour of tendon sheath or fibrolipoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the foot to characterise fascial origin, collagen-rich signal and extent before intervention","Ultrasound demonstrating a hypoechoic fusiform nodule contiguous with the plantar fascia","Examination of the hands and, where relevant, the penis for associated superficial fibromatoses","Core or excisional biopsy with histology when malignancy cannot be excluded on imaging","Screening for associated conditions such as diabetes and chronic liver disease","Baseline photographic and clinical mapping of nodule number and size for monitoring"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, size beyond a few centimetres, or a deep and poorly defined mass suggesting sarcoma","High T2 signal, marked enhancement or bony involvement on imaging atypical for benign fibromatosis","Skin ulceration, fixation or neurovascular compromise","Systemic symptoms or regional lymphadenopathy"],"prognosis":"Plantar fibromatosis is benign and does not metastasise, but it is prone to local recurrence, and postoperative recurrence is common after limited excision; symptoms are often controlled long term with orthoses, with surgery reserved for refractory disabling disease.","pitfalls":["Mistaking an aggressive soft-tissue sarcoma for a benign fibroma and performing an unplanned marginal excision","Undertaking simple nodule excision and underestimating the high recurrence risk compared with wider fasciectomy","Damaging plantar nerves or creating a painful plantar scar and contracture during surgery","Attributing arch pain solely to plantar fasciitis and missing an underlying fibromatosis nodule"],"management":["Observation with reassurance for asymptomatic nodules given the benign nature","Accommodative orthoses, soft padding and insoles to offload and cushion painful nodules","Activity modification and physiotherapy including stretching","Intralesional corticosteroid injection to reduce symptomatic nodule size","Radiotherapy in selected symptomatic cases not suitable for surgery","Wide local excision or fasciectomy reserved for refractory, disabling disease, accepting the high recurrence rate","Long-term monitoring for local recurrence after intervention","Screening for and management of associated superficial fibromatoses such as Dupuytren and Peyronie disease and conditions including diabetes and chronic liver disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"plantar_plate_tear":{"name":"Plantar plate tear","system":"Musculoskeletal / Foot & Ankle","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["plantar plate insufficiency","plantar plate rupture","lesser MTP joint instability","predislocation syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The plantar plate is the fibrocartilaginous plantar stabiliser of the lesser metatarsophalangeal joint, and its attrition or tear causes pain and progressive instability","The second toe is most commonly affected, and patients describe plantar pain at the base of the toe, often with a feeling of walking on a lump and swelling of the joint","The modified drawer (Lachman-type) test, in which dorsal translation of the proximal phalanx on the metatarsal head reproduces pain and shows increased laxity, is the key clinical sign","Progressive insufficiency leads to dorsal and medial drift of the toe, producing the crossover toe deformity in which the second toe overrides the hallux","The V-sign, a widening gap between the second and third toes, is an early indicator of medial deviation from plantar plate failure","Ultrasound and MRI are the most useful investigations to confirm the tear and grade its severity, while weight-bearing radiographs assess alignment and exclude other causes","Distinguish it from interdigital neuroma, which typically causes interspace rather than joint pain and lacks the instability signs","Early management is conservative with metatarsal offloading orthoses, toe strapping or taping to hold the toe plantarward, stiff-soled footwear, and activity modification, with plantar plate repair or reconstruction and associated deformity correction reserved for failed cases"],"pathophysiology":"The plantar plate is a thick fibrocartilaginous structure on the plantar surface of the lesser metatarsophalangeal joint that resists dorsiflexion and hyperextension and, together with the collateral ligaments, provides the main static stability of the joint. Chronic dorsiflexion overload, as occurs with a long metatarsal, hallux valgus that crowds the second toe, high-heeled footwear, or inflammatory disease, produces cumulative attritional injury that weakens and eventually tears the plate, usually near its insertion into the base of the proximal phalanx. As the plantar restraint fails, the proximal phalanx drifts dorsally and the toe becomes unstable, and asymmetrical involvement of the collateral ligaments allows medial deviation, giving the characteristic crossover second toe that overrides the hallux. Loss of the plantar cushioning contributes to metatarsalgia beneath the involved head. The process is typically gradual and degenerative rather than the result of a single acute event.","additionalDifferentials":["Interdigital (Morton's) neuroma","Freiberg's infraction of the metatarsal head","Metatarsophalangeal joint synovitis from inflammatory arthritis","Metatarsal stress fracture","Mechanical metatarsalgia from other causes","Hammer or claw toe deformity from other mechanisms","Capsulitis of the metatarsophalangeal joint"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination including the modified drawer test and inspection for crossover deformity and the V-sign","Weight-bearing foot radiographs to assess metatarsal length, alignment, and toe position","Ultrasound to visualise the plantar plate and dynamic instability","MRI to confirm and grade the tear and assess collateral ligament involvement","Assessment for associated hallux valgus and metatarsal length abnormality","Pedobarography to characterise forefoot overload where relevant","Inflammatory markers and serology if a synovitic or inflammatory cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive dorsal dislocation of the toe with fixed crossover deformity","Symmetrical or multiple joint synovitis suggesting underlying inflammatory arthritis","Focal bony tenderness suggesting a coexisting stress fracture","A hot, swollen joint raising the possibility of septic arthritis or crystal arthropathy","Neurovascular compromise of the drifting toe"],"prognosis":"Early and partial tears often stabilise or improve with offloading, taping, and footwear modification, though these measures control symptoms rather than heal the plate. Once fixed deformity or dislocation develops, conservative treatment is less effective and surgical plantar plate repair or reconstruction with deformity correction is required; results are generally good but some residual stiffness, recurrence, or floating toe may occur.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the condition as an interdigital neuroma and treating the wrong structure","Failing to perform the drawer test and missing early instability before fixed deformity develops","Overlooking associated hallux valgus or a long metatarsal that drives recurrence if uncorrected","Assuming conservative treatment will heal a complete tear rather than merely control symptoms","Missing an inflammatory arthropathy underlying multiple-joint synovitis"],"management":["Initial offloading with metatarsal domes or pads, taping of the toe into plantarflexion or a Budin splint, and stiff-soled or rocker-bottom footwear to reduce dorsiflexion overload of the lesser metatarsophalangeal joint","Activity modification and avoidance of high-heeled and narrow footwear that crowd and hyperextend the toe","Simple analgesics or NSAIDs for pain, using intra-articular corticosteroid only sparingly as it may accelerate plate rupture and worsen instability","Correct contributing deformity, addressing associated hallux valgus and relative metatarsal length that perpetuate the overload","Surgical plantar plate repair or reconstruction, commonly combined with a Weil shortening metatarsal osteotomy, for fixed deformity, joint dislocation or failed conservative care, with rebalancing of the crossover toe","Monitor for progression to fixed deformity, dorsal dislocation and secondary chondral damage","Refer to orthopaedic foot and ankle surgery or podiatry for grading and definitive planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"plasma_cell_disorders":{"name":"Plasma Cell Disorders (MGUS, Waldenström)","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MGUS","monoclonal gammopathy","Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia","POEMS"],"chapter":116,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.883","clinicalPearls":["Plasma cell disorders are monoclonal neoplasms of late B-lymphocyte lineage; the group includes multiple myeloma (MM), Waldenström's macroglobulinemia, primary (AL) amyloidosis, and the heavy chain diseases, also termed monoclonal gammopathies, paraproteinemias, plasma cell dyscrasias, or dysproteinemias (Harrison 22e p.883).","MGUS is vastly more common than myeloma, occurring in ~1% of the population aged >50 and up to ~10% of those aged >75; no specific intervention is indicated and annual (or less frequent) follow-up is adequate, with closer monitoring only for higher-risk MGUS (Harrison 22e p.887-890).","About ~1% of MGUS patients per year progress to myeloma, but essentially all cases of myeloma are preceded by MGUS (Harrison 22e p.887).","Higher-risk MGUS features are non-IgG subtype, abnormal kappa/lambda free light chain ratio, and serum M-protein >15 g/L (1.5 g/dL); absence of all three predicts ~5% progression while presence of all three predicts ~60% progression over 20 years (Harrison 22e p.887).","Diagnostic workup for a monoclonal protein uses serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) for quantitation and immunoelectrophoresis/immunofixation for confirmation of a single heavy and/or light chain; serum free light chain assay and urine studies (UPEP) detect light-chain-only and Bence Jones disease (Harrison 22e p.883-884).","An M component must be present at ~5 g/L (0.5 g/dL) to be accurately quantitated by electrophoresis; the serum M-protein level is a reliable measure of tumor burden but is not specific enough to screen asymptomatic patients (Harrison 22e p.883-884).","MGUS diagnostic criteria: serum monoclonal protein (non-IgM) <30 g/L, clonal bone marrow plasma cells <10%, and absence of myeloma-defining events or amyloidosis attributable to the plasma cell disorder (Harrison 22e p.888, Table 116-1).","Waldenström's macroglobulinemia (WM) is a malignancy of IgM-secreting lymphoplasmacytoid cells; unlike myeloma it presents with lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly and its major manifestation is hyperviscosity syndrome (Harrison 22e p.893).","A distinct MYD88 L265P somatic mutation is present in >90% of WM patients and the majority of IgM MGUS, and is used as a diagnostic test to discriminate WM from marginal zone lymphoma, IgM-secreting myeloma, and CLL with plasmacytic differentiation (Harrison 22e p.893).","In WM the IgM may have specificity for myelin-associated glycoprotein (MAG); a peripheral neuropathy may precede the neoplasm and about half of these patients are anti-MAG positive (Harrison 22e p.893).","POEMS syndrome = Polyneuropathy, Organomegaly, Endocrinopathy, M-protein, and Skin changes; patients typically have a severe progressive sensorimotor polyneuropathy with sclerotic bone lesions, and polyneuropathy occurs in only ~1.4% of myelomas with POEMS being a rare subset (Harrison 22e p.893).","AL (primary) amyloidosis is a plasma cell disorder most often caused by clonal bone marrow plasma cells secreting a misfolding monoclonal light chain; it can coexist with multiple myeloma, Waldenström's macroglobulinemia, or non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (Harrison 22e ch.117 p.896-897).","Macroglossia and periorbital ('raccoon-eye') ecchymoses are pathognomonic but uncommon signs of AL amyloidosis, each seen in only ~10% of patients (Harrison 22e ch.117 p.897)."],"pathophysiology":"In normal B-cell maturation, antigen exposure drives differentiation of mature B lymphocytes into antibody-secreting plasma cells; in plasma cell disorders this control is lost, producing a monoclonal expansion that secretes a single (monoclonal) immunoglobulin or fragment (M component). Clinical manifestations arise from neoplastic cell expansion, from the secreted products (intact immunoglobulin, free light/heavy chains, lymphokines), and from the host response. In multiple myeloma, recurrent chromosomal lesions include hyperdiploidy and 14q32 translocations (e.g., t(11;14), t(4;14), t(14;16)), with errors in immunoglobulin switch recombination implicated in early transformation. Waldenström's macroglobulinemia arises from a post-germinal-center IgM-bearing memory B cell, driven by MYD88 L265P (TLR/IL-1R IRAK and NF-kB signaling, plus BTK/HCK signaling) and frequently CXCR4 mutations. In AL amyloidosis, clonal plasma cells produce a monoclonal light chain (lambda predominates) that misfolds into insoluble amyloid fibrils deposited extracellularly in tissues (Harrison 22e p.883-884, 893; ch.117 p.896-897).","additionalDifferentials":["Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS)","Smoldering (asymptomatic) multiple myeloma","Symptomatic multiple myeloma (CRAB / myeloma-defining events)","Solitary plasmacytoma of bone or soft tissue","Nonsecretory myeloma","Plasma cell leukemia (>2000 circulating plasma cells/uL)","Waldenström's macroglobulinemia","IgM myeloma (lytic lesions, CD138+ plasma cells, t(11;14))","POEMS syndrome","AL (primary) amyloidosis","Heavy chain diseases (gamma/Franklin's, alpha/Seligmann's and IPSID, mu)","Marginal zone lymphoma and CLL with plasmacytic differentiation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["M components in other lymphoid neoplasms (CLL, B- or T-cell lymphomas)","M components in nonlymphoid neoplasms (chronic myeloid leukemia, breast cancer, colon cancer)","Nonneoplastic conditions: cirrhosis, sarcoidosis, parasitic diseases, Gaucher's disease, pyoderma gangrenosum","Autoimmune conditions: rheumatoid arthritis, myasthenia gravis, cold agglutinin disease","Post organ-transplant immunosuppression (and rarely allogeneic transplant)","Skin diseases with monoclonal gammopathy: lichen myxedematosus (papular mucinosis), necrobiotic xanthogranuloma (~10% progress to myeloma)","Mixed cryoglobulinemia (IgM/IgA complexed with IgG) seen in rheumatoid arthritis and other autoimmune disease, usually not associated with malignancy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) for M-protein quantitation","Serum and urine immunofixation electrophoresis (SIFE/UIFE) to confirm monoclonality and identify heavy/light chain type","Urine protein electrophoresis (UPEP) / 24-h urine for Bence Jones (free light chain) protein","Serum free light chain assay with kappa/lambda ratio (essential to assess ratio, not just absolute level, since renal insufficiency nonspecifically raises both)","Bone marrow biopsy/aspirate for clonal plasma cell percentage and CD138+ / kappa or lambda light chain restriction by flow cytometry, immunohistochemistry, or in situ hybridization","MYD88 (L265P) and CXCR4 mutation testing to discriminate WM from MZL, IgM myeloma, and CLL","Skeletal survey, CT, or PET-CT for osteolytic lesions; MRI of spine/pelvis for focal lesions","Serum calcium, creatinine/creatinine clearance, and hemoglobin (to assess CRAB end-organ damage)","VEGF level and evaluation for Castleman's disease when POEMS is suspected","Tissue biopsy with Congo red staining and mass spectrometry-based typing when amyloidosis is suspected (SPEP/UPEP alone are inadequate screens for AL)"],"redFlags":["Hyperviscosity syndrome in Waldenström's macroglobulinemia / IgM gammopathy: headache, visual disturbance, retinal vein segmentation and dilation, epistaxis, altered consciousness, paresis (most IgM is intravascular and responds to plasmapheresis)","Myeloma-defining end-organ damage (CRAB): hyperCalcemia, Renal insufficiency, Anemia, Bone lytic lesions","Spinal cord compression: new lower-extremity neurologic deficit, severe localized back pain, or loss of bowel/bladder control requiring emergency MRI and radiation/decompression","Acute renal failure from light chain cast nephropathy, especially with dehydration","Cardiac involvement in AL amyloidosis (70-80%) is the leading cause of death; low-voltage ECG with pseudo-infarct pattern and thickened ventricles on echo","MGUS with severe polyneuropathy (consider causal IgM/anti-MAG neuropathy)"],"prognosis":"MGUS carries a low malignant potential, with ~1% per year progression to myeloma (risk stratified by IgG vs non-IgG subtype, free light chain ratio, and M-protein level: ~5% at 20 years with no risk features vs ~60% with all three). Smoldering myeloma progresses to symptomatic MM in ~25% at 5 years with one risk feature and ~76% with all three high-risk features. Waldenström's macroglobulinemia is often indolent and may not require therapy; median survival is reported as ~50 months, with older age, male sex, general symptoms, and cytopenias defining a high-risk group. AL amyloidosis is often rapidly progressive and fatal if untreated (occurs after age 40); very low dFLC (<50 mg/L) predicts significantly better outcome (Harrison 22e p.887-888, 893-894; ch.117 p.896-898).","pitfalls":["Do not equate a monoclonal protein with malignancy: distinguish MGUS (clonal marrow plasma cells <10%, M-protein <30 g/L, no myeloma-defining events/amyloidosis) from multiple myeloma, which requires marrow plasmacytosis and myeloma-defining events / CRAB end-organ damage (Harrison 22e p.887-888).","SPEP/UPEP are useful in myeloma but are NOT adequate screening tests for AL amyloidosis, because the clonal light chain is often insufficient to produce an M-spike; use serum/urine immunofixation and free light chain assay (>90% positive) (Harrison 22e ch.117 p.897).","A monoclonal protein alone is not diagnostic of amyloidosis since MGUS is common in older patients; when MGUS coexists with biopsy-proven amyloidosis, AL type must be ruled out and 'smoldering myeloma' patients should be screened for AL (Harrison 22e ch.117 p.897).","WM does not cause bone lesions or hypercalcemia (unlike myeloma); large IgM paraprotein causes little renal excretion so renal disease is uncommon (~20% excrete light chains)—reserve the diagnosis of IgM myeloma for lytic bone lesions with CD138+ plasma cells and t(11;14) (Harrison 22e p.893).","Suspected cryoglobulin: draw blood into a warm syringe and transport in warm water to avoid errors in quantitation (Harrison 22e p.893).","Rituximab can produce an IgM flare in WM—use plasmapheresis first or initially withhold rituximab in patients with high IgM levels (Harrison 22e p.894).","Pseudohyponatremia and a decreased anion gap can occur in myeloma because the cationic M component retains chloride and displaces serum water (Harrison 22e p.887)."],"management":["Manage MGUS with active surveillance rather than treatment, using periodic serum protein electrophoresis, free light chain ratio and blood counts, with follow-up intensity guided by risk features","Treat Waldenstrom's macroglobulinaemia only when symptomatic (cytopenias, hyperviscosity, bulky or constitutional disease) and observe asymptomatic patients","Use rituximab-based therapy for symptomatic Waldenstrom's, combined with chemoimmunotherapy such as bendamustine or a proteasome inhibitor","Employ a BTK inhibitor such as ibrutinib or zanubrutinib, particularly in MYD88-mutated disease","Treat symptomatic hyperviscosity urgently with plasma exchange, and avoid precipitating a rituximab-induced IgM flare when the paraprotein is high","Provide supportive care with transfusion for symptomatic anaemia and management of associated neuropathy or cryoglobulinaemia","Monitor for progression or transformation to myeloma or an aggressive lymphoma","Refer to haematology for risk stratification and treatment planning"],"severityClassification":"MGUS vs smoldering (asymptomatic) myeloma vs symptomatic multiple myeloma per IMWG criteria (Harrison Table 116-1). MGUS: non-IgM serum M-protein <30 g/L, clonal marrow plasma cells <10%, no myeloma-defining events/amyloidosis. Smoldering MM: serum M-protein (IgG/IgA) >=30 g/L or urine M-protein >=500 mg/24h and/or clonal marrow plasma cells 10-60%, without myeloma-defining events. Symptomatic MM: clonal plasma cells (or plasmacytoma) plus a myeloma-defining event—CRAB end-organ damage (hyperCalcemia, Renal insufficiency, Anemia, Bone lesions) or a biomarker of malignancy (clonal marrow plasma cells >=60%, involved:uninvolved serum free light chain ratio >=100, or >1 focal MRI lesion). POEMS requires polyneuropathy + monoclonal plasma cell disorder + (sclerotic bone lesions / Castleman's / elevated VEGF) + an additional system feature.","references":["Harrison 22e p.883","Harrison 22e p.884","Harrison 22e p.887","Harrison 22e p.888","Harrison 22e p.893","Harrison 22e p.894","Harrison 22e ch.117 p.896","Harrison 22e ch.117 p.897"],"crossLinks":[]},"plasma_cell_leukaemia":{"name":"Plasma cell leukaemia","system":"Haematological / Plasma cell","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCL"],"chapter":null,"source":"Haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An aggressive plasma cell malignancy defined by a high proportion of circulating clonal plasma cells in the blood","May arise de novo (primary) or evolve from pre-existing multiple myeloma (secondary)","Presents with rapidly progressive symptoms including anaemia, renal impairment, hypercalcaemia and bone disease","Extramedullary involvement, such as hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy, is more common than in typical myeloma","The updated definition uses a lowered threshold for circulating plasma cells compared with older criteria","It carries a worse prognosis than multiple myeloma and requires prompt, intensive treatment","High-risk cytogenetic abnormalities are frequently present"],"pathophysiology":"Plasma cell leukaemia represents a highly proliferative, often cytogenetically high-risk clone of malignant plasma cells that has lost much of its dependence on the bone marrow microenvironment, allowing widespread circulation and extramedullary spread. Monoclonal immunoglobulin production and marrow infiltration drive end-organ damage, including renal impairment from light chains, hypercalcaemia and osteolytic bone disease with cytopenias. The aggressive biology reflects the accumulation of adverse genetic lesions and explains the rapid clinical course.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple myeloma with circulating plasma cells","Aggressive or leukaemic non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Reactive plasmacytosis","Acute leukaemia","Plasmablastic lymphoma","Waldenstrom macroglobulinaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood film and flow cytometry quantifying circulating clonal plasma cells","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation and free light chains","Bone marrow examination with cytogenetics and FISH","Renal function, calcium and markers of tumour burden","Whole-body imaging for bone and extramedullary disease"],"redFlags":["Acute kidney injury or severe hypercalcaemia","Cord compression or pathological fracture from bone disease","Rapidly rising tumour burden with cytopenias","Hyperviscosity or bulky extramedullary disease"],"prognosis":"Plasma cell leukaemia carries a significantly worse prognosis than multiple myeloma, though outcomes have improved with modern combination therapy and, in suitable patients, stem cell transplantation; secondary disease evolving from myeloma has a particularly poor outlook.","pitfalls":["Underestimating circulating plasma cells and misclassifying it as standard myeloma","Delaying intensive therapy in a rapidly progressive disease","Overlooking extramedullary disease","Failing to obtain cytogenetics that guide risk assessment"],"management":["Start prompt intensive induction combining a proteasome inhibitor (bortezomib), an immunomodulatory agent (lenalidomide) and a corticosteroid, incorporating an anti-CD38 antibody such as daratumumab","Consolidate responders with autologous stem cell transplantation, considering allogeneic transplantation in selected fit patients given the high-risk biology","Give post-transplant maintenance therapy to prolong disease control","Manage hypercalcaemia with rehydration and a bisphosphonate, and give bisphosphonate or denosumab for osteolytic bone disease","Provide supportive care with transfusion for cytopenias, tumour lysis prophylaxis, and renal protection for light-chain nephropathy","Monitor paraprotein, free light chains, renal function and marrow response, watching for early relapse","Refer urgently to haematology or haemato-oncology for combination therapy and transplant assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Wintrobe's Clinical Hematology (standard reference)","WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"plasma_cell_leukemia":{"name":"Plasma cell leukaemia","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCL","primary plasma cell leukaemia","secondary plasma cell leukaemia","circulating clonal plasma cell leukaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An aggressive plasma-cell neoplasm defined by clonal plasma cells circulating in the peripheral blood above the accepted diagnostic threshold","Primary plasma cell leukaemia arises de novo, whereas secondary disease represents leukaemic transformation of pre-existing multiple myeloma","Presents with rapidly progressive disease including anaemia, thrombocytopenia, renal impairment, hypercalcaemia and frequent extramedullary involvement","Circulating plasma cells often express CD38 and CD138 but characteristically lack CD56, which is more typical of marrow-confined myeloma","High-risk cytogenetic abnormalities, including deletion 17p and translocations involving MYC, are common and contribute to the poor prognosis","Treatment relies on intensive combination regimens incorporating proteasome inhibitors, immunomodulatory agents and anti-CD38 targeted therapy, with autologous HSCT in eligible patients","Diagnosis requires prompt recognition, as the disease can deteriorate quickly with hyperviscosity, renal failure and tumour lysis"],"pathophysiology":"Plasma cell leukaemia is characterised by a clone of malignant plasma cells that has lost the marrow-retention signals of ordinary myeloma and circulates freely in the blood, reflecting altered adhesion-molecule expression such as reduced CD56. Primary disease arises without an antecedent plasma-cell disorder, while secondary disease evolves from progressive multiple myeloma. The clone frequently harbours high-risk genomic changes, including TP53 loss through deletion 17p and MYC rearrangements, driving proliferation, genomic instability and extramedullary dissemination. Widespread infiltration of blood, marrow and extramedullary sites produces the profound cytopenias, end-organ damage and monoclonal protein burden that define the aggressive clinical picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple myeloma with a small circulating plasma-cell component","Plasmablastic lymphoma","Reactive plasmacytosis from severe infection or systemic illness","Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma","Acute leukaemia with monocytic differentiation","Metastatic carcinoma with a leukaemic blood picture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood film and flow cytometry to quantify and immunophenotype circulating clonal plasma cells","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine with cytogenetics and FISH for high-risk abnormalities","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation and serum free light chains","Renal function, calcium, and markers of tumour lysis","Beta-2 microglobulin, lactate dehydrogenase and albumin for prognostic assessment","Imaging for bone disease and extramedullary plasmacytomas"],"redFlags":["Acute kidney injury from light-chain nephropathy or hypercalcaemia","Hyperviscosity with neurological or visual disturbance","Symptomatic hypercalcaemia or tumour lysis syndrome","Cord compression or extramedullary mass with neurological deficit","Profound cytopenias causing bleeding or life-threatening infection"],"prognosis":"Plasma cell leukaemia carries a markedly worse prognosis than multiple myeloma, with shorter survival despite modern therapy; secondary disease is particularly aggressive, and outcomes are best when intensive combination treatment and autologous HSCT can be delivered to eligible patients.","pitfalls":["Underestimating circulating plasma cells and missing the diagnosis when the burden is only modestly elevated","Assuming the CD56 positivity typical of marrow myeloma, which is often absent in leukaemic disease","Delaying treatment of hypercalcaemia, renal failure or tumour lysis","Applying standard myeloma prognostic assumptions to a much more aggressive disease","Failing to identify high-risk cytogenetics that alter management and outlook"],"management":["Deliver intensive multi-agent induction built on a proteasome inhibitor, an immunomodulatory drug and a corticosteroid, with an anti-CD38 monoclonal antibody such as daratumumab","Consolidate eligible patients with autologous, and in selected cases allogeneic, haematopoietic stem cell transplantation","Continue maintenance therapy after transplantation to sustain remission in this high-risk clone","Treat hypercalcaemia with rehydration and a bisphosphonate, and manage bone disease with bone-targeted therapy","Give supportive care including transfusion, tumour lysis prophylaxis, antimicrobial cover during neutropenia and management of renal impairment","Monitor cytogenetic-defined high-risk markers, paraprotein and organ function, anticipating rapid relapse in secondary disease","Refer promptly to haemato-oncology for combination treatment and transplant evaluation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","British Society for Haematology guidance on plasma cell disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"plasmablastic_lymphoma":{"name":"Plasmablastic lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PBL","plasmablastic lymphoma of the oral cavity","HIV-associated plasmablastic lymphoma","EBV-associated plasmablastic lymphoma","CD20-/CD138+/MYC-rearranged large cell lymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically presents as a rapidly growing mass in the oral cavity or jaw of an HIV-positive patient, but can arise at many extranodal sites including the gastrointestinal tract","Strongly linked to immunodeficiency (HIV infection, transplantation, older age) and frequently associated with Epstein-Barr virus","Tumour cells resemble plasmablasts or immunoblasts with a very high proliferation index","The immunophenotype straddles B-cell and plasma-cell differentiation: pan-B-cell markers such as CD20 and PAX5 are usually negative or weak while plasma-cell markers CD138, CD38, and MUM1/IRF4 are positive","MYC rearrangement is common and is associated with a more aggressive course","Overlap with plasmablastic plasma-cell myeloma requires correlation with clinical features, imaging, and any paraprotein","In situ hybridisation for EBV-encoded RNA is positive in many cases, especially those linked to immunodeficiency"],"pathophysiology":"Plasmablastic lymphoma is an aggressive large B-cell neoplasm whose cells have differentiated to the plasmablast stage, losing mature B-cell antigens while acquiring a plasma-cell secretory phenotype. It arises predominantly in immunodeficient hosts, in whom impaired immune surveillance permits Epstein-Barr virus-driven proliferation of terminally differentiating B-cells. Frequent rearrangement of the MYC oncogene amplifies proliferative drive, producing the characteristic high growth fraction and rapidly enlarging masses. Because the neoplastic cells resemble plasma cells, the tumour lies at the interface between lymphoma and plasma-cell neoplasia.","additionalDifferentials":["Plasmablastic plasma-cell myeloma","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma with plasmablastic features","Primary effusion lymphoma, extracavitary variant","HHV-8-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","Burkitt lymphoma","Anaplastic plasmacytoma or extramedullary plasmacytoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy with immunohistochemistry showing CD138/CD38/MUM1 positivity and loss of CD20/PAX5","In situ hybridisation for EBV-encoded RNA","FISH for MYC rearrangement","HIV testing and assessment of immune status","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis to help distinguish from plasma-cell myeloma","PET-CT and bone marrow examination for staging"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging oral or facial mass causing airway or feeding compromise","Gastrointestinal bleeding or obstruction from mucosal disease","Bulky disease with tumour lysis risk given the high proliferation rate","Concurrent opportunistic infection in the immunocompromised host"],"prognosis":"The disease is aggressive with generally short survival, though control of HIV with antiretroviral therapy and intensive combination chemotherapy can improve outcomes; MYC rearrangement and advanced stage predict worse results.","pitfalls":["Excluding lymphoma because CD20 is negative, when plasma-cell markers confirm the diagnosis","Confusing plasmablastic lymphoma with plasma-cell myeloma without integrating clinical and serological data","Overlooking underlying HIV infection at presentation","Failing to test for EBV and MYC, which inform diagnosis and prognosis"],"management":["Deliver intensive combination chemotherapy, favouring a dose-adjusted infusional regimen such as EPOCH rather than standard CHOP, noting that the CD20-negative phenotype makes rituximab ineffective","Optimise host immunity by starting or continuing combination antiretroviral therapy in HIV-associated disease and reducing iatrogenic immunosuppression where possible","Add proteasome-inhibitor-based therapy such as bortezomib, exploiting the plasma-cell differentiation of the tumour","Give central nervous system prophylaxis given the high risk of neuraxis involvement","Provide supportive care with tumour lysis prophylaxis, antimicrobial prophylaxis and growth factor support during cytopenias","Consider autologous stem cell transplantation to consolidate remission in suitable responders","Refer to haemato-oncology alongside HIV or transplant specialists, and monitor response with PET-CT"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","Swerdlow SH et al. WHO monograph on lymphoid neoplasms"],"crossLinks":[]},"plasminogen_deficiency":{"name":"Plasminogen deficiency (ligneous conjunctivitis)","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLG deficiency","type I plasminogen deficiency","hypoplasminogenaemia","ligneous conjunctivitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Severe type I (hypoplasminogenaemic) deficiency causes ligneous, woody, fibrin-rich pseudomembranes on mucous surfaces, most classically the conjunctiva","Pseudomembranes can also affect the gingiva, respiratory tract, middle ear, female genital tract, and rarely the ventricular system, causing occlusive hydrocephalus","Minor mucosal trauma or infection typically triggers membrane formation, which recurs after simple excision","Type I deficiency reduces both plasminogen activity and antigen, whereas type II (dysplasminogenaemia) lowers activity with preserved antigen and is usually asymptomatic","The disorder is one of defective extravascular fibrinolysis rather than a classic bleeding or thrombotic diathesis","Systemic venous thrombosis is not a consistent feature, distinguishing it from the coagulation-cascade thrombophilias","Directed therapy centres on plasma-derived plasminogen replacement together with control of local triggers, with topical measures for ocular disease"],"pathophysiology":"Plasminogen is the zymogen of plasmin, the principal enzyme of fibrinolysis. In severe deficiency, insufficient plasmin activity means fibrin deposited at sites of minor mucosal injury or inflammation cannot be cleared, so it accumulates as firm, woody pseudomembranes rich in fibrin. The defect is one of extravascular fibrin clearance on wet epithelial surfaces, which explains why the eyes, gums, airway and other mucous membranes are affected while systemic thrombosis is uncommon. Biallelic PLG variants underlie the type I phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Infective (bacterial or viral) membranous conjunctivitis","Cicatricial pemphigoid and other autoimmune conjunctival scarring disorders","Chemical or thermal ocular injury with membrane formation","Diphtheritic pseudomembrane in the airway","Congenital hydrocephalus of other cause when ventricular involvement occurs","Chronic gingival hyperplasia from other systemic disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasminogen activity assay and plasminogen antigen to distinguish type I from type II deficiency","Histology of pseudomembranes showing fibrin-rich amorphous material","PLG gene analysis to confirm the diagnosis and support family counselling","Ophthalmic slit-lamp assessment to characterise conjunctival involvement","Imaging of the airway or central nervous system when respiratory or ventricular disease is suspected","Screening for associated infection or inflammation that may trigger membranes"],"redFlags":["Airway pseudomembranes threatening respiratory obstruction","Ventricular or intracranial involvement causing occlusive hydrocephalus","Rapidly recurring membranes after surgical removal","Progressive corneal or visual compromise from severe ocular disease"],"prognosis":"The condition is chronic and relapsing, and untreated mucosal disease can threaten vision or, rarely, the airway and central nervous system. Plasminogen replacement can markedly improve mucosal lesions, so prognosis has improved where such therapy is available.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly excising conjunctival membranes without addressing the underlying fibrinolytic defect, leading to recurrence","Confusing asymptomatic type II dysplasminogenaemia with clinically significant type I deficiency","Missing extraocular sites such as the airway or genital tract","Attributing recurrent membranes solely to infection and overlooking the inherited cause"],"management":["Provide plasminogen replacement with human plasma-derived plasminogen concentrate as the definitive disease-specific therapy for mucosal pseudomembranes","Treat conjunctival disease with topical plasminogen or fresh frozen plasma eye drops, supplemented by topical heparin, ciclosporin or corticosteroid to limit fibrin deposition","Minimise mucosal trauma and avoid unnecessary surgical excision of membranes, which reliably provokes recurrence","Promptly treat intercurrent infection or inflammation that triggers new membrane formation","Provide multidisciplinary surveillance with ophthalmology for the eyes and monitoring of the airway, ears and genital tract for occlusive disease","Offer genetic counselling and family screening given the autosomal recessive, biallelic PLG inheritance","Escalate urgently for airway obstruction, occlusive hydrocephalus or other sight- or life-threatening involvement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Hoffman: Hematology - Basic Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"plastic_bronchitis":{"name":"plastic bronchitis","system":"respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cast bronchitis","fibrinous bronchitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray and Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Plastic bronchitis is characterised by the formation of large, cohesive, branching airway casts that take the shape of the bronchial tree and are expectorated or removed bronchoscopically.","Expectoration of a rubbery, branching cast is virtually diagnostic; patients may describe coughing up a 'tree-shaped' or 'model' of their airways.","In children it is classically associated with congenital heart disease following Fontan-type palliation, where abnormal lymphatic flow drives cast formation.","Other associations include asthma and allergic bronchopulmonary disease, sickle cell acute chest syndrome, and lymphatic disorders or thoracic duct abnormalities.","Casts are broadly divided into lymphatic/acellular fibrin-rich types (often in cardiac and lymphatic disease) and inflammatory eosinophil- or mucin-rich types (often in asthma and allergic disease).","Acute large-cast impaction can cause lobar or whole-lung collapse and life-threatening airway obstruction, sometimes with sudden severe respiratory distress.","Bronchoscopy is both diagnostic and therapeutic, allowing extraction of casts and sampling to characterise their composition.","In lymphatic disease, cross-sectional lymphatic imaging can identify abnormal pulmonary lymphatic flow amenable to lymphatic embolisation or surgical correction."],"pathophysiology":"Plastic bronchitis involves the accumulation and consolidation of material within the airways into firm, cohesive casts that replicate the branching bronchial anatomy. In lymphatic-type disease, often after Fontan palliation of single-ventricle physiology, chronically elevated systemic venous and lymphatic pressures cause abnormal pulmonary lymphatic networks to leak protein-rich, fibrin-laden lymph into the airways, which coagulates into acellular fibrinous casts. In inflammatory-type disease, airway inflammation — as in asthma, allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis or the acute chest syndrome of sickle cell disease — generates mucin, fibrin and cellular exudate (frequently eosinophil-rich) that inspissates into casts. Once formed, a cast behaves as an obstructing intraluminal plug: it impairs ventilation of the subtended lung, promotes atelectasis and post-obstructive infection, and, if it dislodges into a central airway, can cause acute critical obstruction. The composition of the cast reflects the underlying mechanism and guides directed therapy.","additionalDifferentials":["mucoid impaction in allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","aspirated foreign body causing lobar collapse","thick tenacious secretions in cystic fibrosis or severe asthma","endobronchial tumour or broncholith with obstruction","pulmonary haemorrhage with organised clot cast","diphtheritic or other pseudomembranous airway disease","acute chest syndrome of sickle cell disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["flexible or rigid bronchoscopy for cast extraction and airway inspection","histopathological analysis of the cast to classify lymphatic versus inflammatory type","chest CT to assess atelectasis, bronchiectasis and mediastinal or lymphatic abnormality","dynamic contrast-enhanced magnetic resonance lymphangiography or intranodal lymphangiography","echocardiography and assessment of Fontan haemodynamics in relevant patients","evaluation for asthma and allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis where inflammatory casts are found","haemoglobinopathy screening where sickle cell disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["acute severe respiratory distress from central cast impaction","lobar or whole-lung collapse on imaging","hypoxaemia or asphyxiation risk requiring urgent airway clearance","recurrent casts in a Fontan patient indicating protein-losing lymphatic complications","haemoptysis suggesting vascular involvement"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause: inflammatory casts related to asthma or allergic disease often respond to control of airway inflammation, whereas Fontan-associated lymphatic plastic bronchitis is more refractory, though lymphatic imaging and targeted embolisation have substantially improved outcomes in selected patients.","pitfalls":["Failing to retrieve and analyse the cast, thereby missing the distinction between lymphatic and inflammatory types that dictates treatment.","Treating recurrent Fontan-associated casts purely as mucus without investigating pulmonary lymphatic flow.","Underestimating the risk of acute asphyxiation from a mobile central cast.","Overlooking allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis or poorly controlled asthma as a driver of inflammatory casts.","Relying on mucolytics or physiotherapy alone in refractory disease without escalating to bronchoscopy or lymphatic intervention."],"management":["Remove obstructing casts by flexible or rigid bronchoscopy to relieve airway obstruction and re-expand collapsed lung","For Fontan-associated lymphatic disease, optimise Fontan haemodynamics and pursue lymphatic imaging with percutaneous lymphatic or thoracic duct embolisation to interrupt abnormal lymph leakage","Institute a low-fat, medium-chain-triglyceride diet, and consider octreotide, to reduce lymphatic output in lymphatic-type casts","Treat inflammatory-type disease by controlling airway inflammation with inhaled corticosteroids and bronchodilators and by managing underlying asthma or allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Support cast clearance with airway clearance physiotherapy, mucolytics, and nebulised fibrinolytics such as tissue plasminogen activator or DNase","Treat acute large-cast impaction as an emergency with airway stabilisation and urgent therapeutic bronchoscopy","Coordinate care across respiratory, cardiology and interventional radiology teams and escalate for refractory or life-threatening obstruction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray and Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine, current edition","Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders, current edition","Contemporary reviews of Fontan-associated plastic bronchitis and lymphatic interventions"],"crossLinks":[]},"plateau_iris_syndrome":{"name":"Plateau iris syndrome","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["plateau iris configuration","plateau iris","plateau iris glaucoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of angle closure in which anteriorly positioned ciliary processes push the peripheral iris forward to crowd or close the angle, rather than pupillary block being the main mechanism","The central anterior chamber is typically of normal or near-normal depth, but the peripheral iris rises steeply and then flattens, giving a plateau-like profile","It should be suspected when angle closure persists or recurs despite a patent laser peripheral iridotomy","The term plateau iris configuration describes the anatomy, while plateau iris syndrome denotes residual appositional angle closure after iridotomy has relieved any pupillary-block component","Anterior segment imaging with ultrasound biomicroscopy characteristically shows anteriorly rotated ciliary processes supporting the peripheral iris","Dilation can precipitate angle closure by bunching the peripheral iris into the crowded angle","Management after iridotomy may include laser peripheral iridoplasty to contract and flatten the peripheral iris away from the angle, with topical agents and long-term monitoring","It tends to occur in younger patients than typical pupillary-block angle closure"],"pathophysiology":"In plateau iris the ciliary body and its processes sit abnormally far forward, mechanically supporting and displacing the peripheral iris towards the trabecular meshwork so that the angle is crowded. The central anterior chamber remains reasonably deep because the mechanism is peripheral rather than a generalised forward shift of the iris-lens diaphragm. A relative pupillary-block element may coexist, which a laser peripheral iridotomy relieves; when appositional angle closure persists despite a patent iridotomy, the residual peripheral crowding from the anteriorly placed ciliary processes defines plateau iris syndrome. Pupil dilation worsens the situation by folding the peripheral iris into the already narrow angle, and chronic apposition can lead to peripheral anterior synechiae and pressure elevation.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary angle-closure glaucoma from pupillary block","Malignant glaucoma (aqueous misdirection)","Phacomorphic angle closure from a large lens","Angle closure from a ciliary body cyst or tumour","Secondary angle closure from posterior segment pushing mechanisms","Narrow angle from a small hypermetropic eye"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gonioscopy, including indentation gonioscopy, before and after iridotomy to demonstrate persistent peripheral apposition","Ultrasound biomicroscopy or anterior segment OCT to show anteriorly positioned ciliary processes","Applanation tonometry to detect pressure elevation","Assessment of central anterior chamber depth to distinguish it from generalised shallowing","OCT of the optic nerve and perimetry to monitor for glaucomatous damage","Provocative or post-dilation angle assessment where appropriate"],"redFlags":["Persistent or recurrent appositional angle closure despite a patent iridotomy","Acute pressure rise precipitated by pupil dilation","Progressive peripheral anterior synechiae and rising pressure","Optic-nerve damage from unrecognised chronic angle closure"],"prognosis":"With recognition and treatment, including iridotomy and iridoplasty where needed, angle closure is often controlled and the prognosis is good, but unrecognised plateau iris can cause progressive synechial closure and glaucomatous damage requiring ongoing surveillance and sometimes further intervention.","pitfalls":["Assuming a patent iridotomy has resolved the angle closure without repeating gonioscopy","Failing to image the ciliary processes and missing the true mechanism","Dilating the pupil and precipitating an acute rise","Confusing the deep central chamber with a normal angle and overlooking peripheral crowding","Not distinguishing plateau iris syndrome from malignant glaucoma, which needs very different treatment"],"management":["Perform laser peripheral iridotomy first to eliminate any coexisting pupillary-block component","Add argon laser peripheral iridoplasty to flatten and retract the peripheral iris when appositional angle closure persists despite a patent iridotomy","Use a miotic such as pilocarpine to keep the pupil constricted and prevent peripheral iris bunching into the angle","Avoid pharmacological mydriasis and counsel against dilating agents that can precipitate angle closure","Lower intraocular pressure with topical agents such as prostaglandin analogues, beta-blockers or carbonic anhydrase inhibitors as needed, and consider lens extraction where the lens contributes","Maintain surveillance with gonioscopy, tonometry, optic nerve assessment and perimetry to detect synechial closure and glaucomatous damage","Refer to ophthalmology or a glaucoma specialist and escalate for progressive or uncontrolled angle closure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"platelet_disorders":{"name":"Platelet Disorders (Thrombocytopenia)","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thrombocytopenia","ITP","HIT","platelet dysfunction"],"chapter":120,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.921-926","clinicalPearls":["Thrombocytopenia arises from one or more of three mechanisms: decreased bone marrow production, sequestration (usually in an enlarged spleen), and/or increased platelet destruction (Harrison 22e p.921).","First step in evaluating thrombocytopenia is reviewing the peripheral blood smear and ruling out pseudothrombocytopenia, especially when there is no apparent cause (Harrison 22e p.921).","Petechiae are pinpoint, nonblanching hemorrhages that usually signify a decreased platelet number rather than platelet dysfunction; they first appear in areas of increased venous pressure (ankles/feet in ambulatory patients) (Harrison 22e p.921-922).","Wet purpura (blood blisters on the oral mucosa) and retinal hemorrhages are thought to denote an increased risk of life-threatening hemorrhage in the thrombocytopenic patient (Harrison 22e p.922, 924).","Drugs are the most common cause of thrombocytopenia; in a patient with no apparent cause, all drugs (including herbal and OTC preparations) should be suspect and stopped or substituted if possible (Harrison 22e p.922).","Infection is the most common noniatrogenic cause of thrombocytopenia; viral and bacterial infections can affect both platelet production and survival, and immune mechanisms may also contribute (Harrison 22e p.921-922).","In HIT, thrombocytopenia is usually not severe (nadir rarely <20,000/uL) and is not associated with bleeding; instead it markedly increases the risk of thrombosis (Harrison 22e p.923).","Most patients develop HIT 5-14 days after heparin exposure; onset before 5 days suggests prior heparin exposure within ~100 days with circulating anti-heparin/PF4 antibodies (Harrison 22e p.923).","ITP is characterized by mucocutaneous bleeding and a low, often very low, platelet count with an otherwise normal peripheral blood smear and cell counts (Harrison 22e p.924).","In thrombotic microangiopathies (TTP/HUS) the PT and aPTT are characteristically normal, which helps distinguish them from DIC, where a coagulopathy predominates with elevated PT and often aPTT (Harrison 22e p.924-925).","An ADAMTS13 activity level of <10% is diagnostic of TTP (Harrison 22e p.925).","Markedly elevated platelet counts (>1.5 million), usually in a myeloproliferative disorder, carry an increased risk of bleeding rather than thrombosis, due in part to acquired von Willebrand disease (Harrison 22e p.926)."],"pathophysiology":"Hemostasis depends on platelets and the vessel wall. Platelets are activated on adhesion to von Willebrand factor (VWF) and collagen in exposed subendothelium after injury; adhesion (primarily via VWF) generates signals activating the GpIIb/IIIa (alphaIIb-beta3) receptor and aggregation, with release of granule contents and stabilization by a fibrin mesh. Normal platelet count is 150,000-450,000/uL; thrombopoietin (TPO), made in the liver, is the major regulator, and roughly one-third of platelets reside in the spleen. Thrombocytopenia results from decreased production, splenic sequestration, and/or increased destruction. In HIT, antibodies form to a complex of platelet factor 4 (PF4) and heparin/glycosaminoglycans; the anti-heparin/PF4 antibody activates platelets via the FcgammaRIIa receptor and also activates monocytes, endothelium, and coagulation, producing a prothrombotic state. ITP is immune-mediated platelet destruction with possible inhibition of platelet release from megakaryocytes. In TTP, deficiency of or antibodies to the metalloprotease ADAMTS13 leave ultra-large VWF multimers uncleaved, driving pathogenic platelet adhesion/aggregation and microvascular thrombosis. HUS features acute renal failure with microangiopathic hemolytic anemia and thrombocytopenia; atypical HUS arises from genetic complement-regulatory defects or anti-complement-regulator antibodies causing chronic complement activation.","additionalDifferentials":["Pseudothrombocytopenia (in vitro EDTA-induced platelet clumping)","Decreased production (bone marrow disorders, myelodysplasia, chemotherapy-induced marrow suppression)","Splenic sequestration / hypersplenism","Drug-induced immune thrombocytopenia","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT/HITT)","Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)","Hemolytic-uremic syndrome (HUS) including atypical HUS","Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)","Inherited thrombocytopenia (e.g., MYH9-related autosomal dominant macrothrombocytopenia: May-Hegglin anomaly, Sebastian, Epstein, Fechtner syndromes)","Evans syndrome (combined autoimmune hemolytic anemia with ITP)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral and bacterial infections causing thrombocytopenia (most common noniatrogenic cause)","Systemic gram-negative bacterial infection with DIC","COVID-19-associated thrombocytopenia/DIC","Infectious mononucleosis and early HIV (immune-mediated thrombocytopenia)","Late HIV with pancytopenia and dysplastic platelet production","Hepatitis C and HIV as secondary causes of ITP","Escherichia coli O157:H7 (diarrhea-associated HUS)","Helicobacter pylori (uncertain, geographically variable association with ITP)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pseudothrombocytopenia from EDTA-induced platelet clumping (in vitro artifact)","Myelodysplasia presenting as isolated thrombocytopenia","Iron deficiency, inflammation, or malignancy causing reactive thrombocytosis (when evaluating platelet count abnormalities)","Other microangiopathies: bone marrow transplant-associated, drug-induced, pregnancy-related, and vasculitis-associated"],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood smear review (rule out clumping; assess RBC morphology and platelet size)","Repeat platelet count in sodium citrate (blue top) or heparin (green top) tube, or fresh fingerstick smear, if EDTA clumping suspected","Complete blood count with hemoglobin and white blood cell count","Bone marrow examination (isolated thrombocytopenia in patients >60 years or nonresponders to initial therapy)","4Ts scoring for HIT pretest probability (thrombocytopenia, timing, thrombosis/sequelae, other causes)","Anti-heparin/PF4 immunoassay (high sensitivity, low specificity) and serotonin release platelet-activation assay (lower sensitivity, higher specificity) for HIT","Lower extremity duplex Doppler imaging to evaluate for thrombosis in HIT","ADAMTS13 activity and inhibitor/antibody testing (activity <10% diagnostic of TTP)","LDH, unconjugated bilirubin, haptoglobin (evidence of hemolysis in MAHA)","PT and aPTT (normal in TTP/HUS; elevated in DIC)","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test if anemia present (rule out Evans syndrome)","Secondary ITP workup: HIV, hepatitis C, SLE serology, serum protein electrophoresis, immunoglobulin levels, IgA deficiency / monoclonal gammopathy testing, H. pylori","Complement regulatory gene DNA variant testing for atypical HUS"],"redFlags":["HIT: thrombosis (venous and/or arterial) despite a falling platelet count -- stop all heparin immediately (including LMWH, which cross-reacts) and start a non-heparin anticoagulant","Wet purpura (oral mucosal blood blisters) and retinal hemorrhages herald life-threatening, including CNS, bleeding","VITT: high D-dimer with thrombosis in unusual sites such as cerebral venous sinuses (fatal in ~20%)","New thrombocytopenia with microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (schistocytes) suggesting TTP -- a devastating disease if not promptly diagnosed and treated","Severe thrombocytopenia (<5000/uL) or signs of impending bleeding in ITP requiring urgent inpatient combined-modality therapy","Introducing warfarin alone in HIT/HITT may precipitate thrombosis (e.g., venous gangrene) due to reduced protein C and S"],"prognosis":"ITP: in children usually acute, post-infectious, and self-limited; in adults more chronic, though some remit spontaneously within months. Patients with platelet counts >30,000/uL appear not to have increased mortality from the thrombocytopenia. TTP: introduction of plasma exchange reduced mortality from 85-100% to 10-30%; however 25-45% relapse within 30 days of remission and 12-40% have late relapses, with relapses more frequent in those with severe ADAMTS13 deficiency. HUS: diarrhea-associated HUS has overall mortality <5% (about 40% of children need dialysis), whereas non-diarrhea-associated HUS mortality is higher (~26%). VITT is fatal in about 20%.","pitfalls":["Pseudothrombocytopenia: EDTA-induced platelet agglutination is an in vitro artifact and not true thrombocytopenia; confirm with a smear and a count in citrate/heparin or fresh fingerstick blood.","Anti-heparin/PF4 antibodies are common without clinical disease (e.g., ~50% after cardiopulmonary bypass), so the HIT immunoassay has low specificity; HIT remains a clinical diagnosis.","The 4T score is very useful for excluding HIT but overdiagnoses it in settings (e.g., ICU) where thrombocytopenia and thrombosis from other causes are common.","LMWH cross-reacts with HIT antibodies and must not be used to treat HIT.","Starting warfarin alone in HIT/HITT may precipitate thrombosis and venous gangrene; overlap with a DTI, fondaparinux, or DOAC and start only after thrombocytopenia resolves.","Serologic (antibody) testing in ITP is generally unhelpful due to low sensitivity and specificity.","Plasma infusion/exchange has not been shown to alter the overall course of HUS or atypical HUS (except aHUS with antibodies to factor H).","Petechiae indicate decreased platelet number, not platelet dysfunction -- excessive bruising can occur with disorders of either number or function."],"management":["Identify and remove the underlying cause, stopping all potentially culprit drugs including herbal and over-the-counter preparations and treating any precipitating infection","Treat immune thrombocytopenia with corticosteroids or intravenous immunoglobulin first line, escalating to thrombopoietin receptor agonists, rituximab or splenectomy for refractory disease","In heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, stop all heparin and anticoagulate with a non-heparin agent such as argatroban, danaparoid or fondaparinux, avoiding platelet transfusion","In thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, start urgent plasma exchange with corticosteroids, adding rituximab and caplacizumab, and avoid platelet transfusion","Reserve platelet transfusion for severe hypoproliferative thrombocytopenia or active bleeding, and control bleeding with antifibrinolytics and local measures","Monitor the peripheral blood film and counts, and undertake bone marrow examination in isolated thrombocytopenia in older patients or non-responders","Refer to haematology for diagnosis-specific therapy and for bleeding or thrombotic emergencies"],"severityClassification":"No single formal grading scale is given; clinically meaningful thresholds noted: ~5000-10,000/uL platelets needed to maintain microvascular integrity; severe ITP thrombocytopenia <5000/uL (or impending bleeding) warrants inpatient combined-modality therapy; ITP counts >30,000/uL not associated with increased mortality; HIT nadir rarely <20,000/uL; ADAMTS13 activity <10% diagnostic of TTP; platelet counts >1.5 million (myeloproliferative) carry bleeding risk. The 4Ts score is used for HIT pretest probability.","references":["Harrison 22e p.921-926"],"crossLinks":["itp","ttp_hus","dic","coagulation_disorders"]},"platelet_storage_pool_disease":{"name":"Platelet storage pool disease","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["storage pool deficiency","delta-granule deficiency","alpha-granule deficiency","gray platelet syndrome","Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of inherited platelet secretion defects caused by deficiency of dense (delta) granules, alpha granules, or both, producing mild to moderate mucocutaneous bleeding","Typical features are easy bruising, epistaxis, menorrhagia, and prolonged bleeding after surgery or dental procedures despite a normal platelet count","Dense-granule deficiency can be part of syndromic disorders such as Hermansky-Pudlak (with oculocutaneous albinism) and Chediak-Higashi syndrome","Alpha-granule deficiency defines gray platelet syndrome, in which platelets appear large and agranular (gray) on the blood film with a tendency to progressive myelofibrosis","Light transmission aggregometry classically shows an absent secondary wave and impaired response to weak agonists, with a blunted ATP release","The platelet count is usually normal or only mildly reduced, so the defect is qualitative rather than quantitative","Management centres on avoiding antiplatelet drugs, using antifibrinolytics and local measures, and reserving platelet transfusion for significant bleeding or surgery"],"pathophysiology":"Platelet storage pool disease reflects a failure to form, fill, or release the contents of platelet secretory granules. Dense (delta) granules store ADP, ATP, calcium, and serotonin, which amplify platelet activation and recruitment; their deficiency blunts the secondary aggregation response. Alpha granules store adhesive and procoagulant proteins including von Willebrand factor, fibrinogen, and platelet factor 4, and their absence in gray platelet syndrome impairs platelet function and is associated with marrow fibrosis. The resulting defective granule release weakens the platelet activation amplification loop and produces a mucocutaneous bleeding tendency.","additionalDifferentials":["Von Willebrand disease","Glanzmann thrombasthenia and Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Aspirin or other antiplatelet drug effect","Acquired platelet dysfunction from uraemia or myeloproliferative disease","Mild coagulation factor deficiencies","Quebec platelet disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film to assess platelet number and morphology (gray, agranular platelets)","Light transmission platelet aggregometry with weak and strong agonists","Dense-granule ATP release (lumiaggregometry) or nucleotide content measurement","Whole-mount electron microscopy to enumerate dense granules","Von Willebrand factor studies to exclude von Willebrand disease","Genetic testing for syndromic forms such as Hermansky-Pudlak or gray platelet syndrome"],"redFlags":["Severe or unexpected perioperative or postpartum haemorrhage","Bleeding compounded by inadvertent antiplatelet drug use","Features of a syndromic disorder such as albinism or recurrent infections suggesting a broader diagnosis","Progressive cytopenias or splenomegaly suggesting marrow fibrosis in gray platelet syndrome"],"prognosis":"Most patients have a mild, manageable bleeding tendency compatible with a normal life when antiplatelet agents are avoided and procedures are covered. Prognosis is modified by any associated syndrome, and gray platelet syndrome may progress to clinically significant myelofibrosis.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis because the platelet count is normal","Missing an underlying syndrome such as Hermansky-Pudlak by not examining for albinism","Attributing aggregation abnormalities to recent antiplatelet drug use without a proper drug-free interval","Failing to give perioperative haemostatic cover for an apparently mild disorder"],"management":["Avoid antiplatelet agents such as aspirin and NSAIDs that further impair the already defective granule-release response","Control mucocutaneous bleeding and cover procedures with antifibrinolytics such as tranexamic acid","Use desmopressin to transiently improve platelet function for minor bleeding or surgery","Reserve platelet transfusion for severe bleeding or major surgery, using HLA-matched products where repeated exposure is anticipated","Manage menorrhagia with tranexamic acid or hormonal therapy and correct iron-deficiency anaemia with iron supplementation","Offer genetic counselling and screen for syndromic forms such as Hermansky-Pudlak, Chediak-Higashi and gray platelet syndrome","Monitor gray platelet syndrome for progressive myelofibrosis and coordinate procedure planning with haematology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Hoffman: Hematology - Basic Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"platypnea_orthodeoxia_syndrome":{"name":"Platypnea-orthodeoxia syndrome","system":"Respiratory / Pulmonary vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["POS","platypnoea-orthodeoxia syndrome","positional hypoxaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC/ERS Guidelines for Pulmonary Hypertension / Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by breathlessness (platypnoea) and arterial desaturation (orthodeoxia) that appear or worsen on sitting or standing upright and improve on lying flat","Reflects a posture-dependent right-to-left shunt, most often intracardiac through a patent foramen ovale or atrial septal defect, but sometimes intrapulmonary","Intracardiac cases frequently require an anatomical or functional distortion, such as an enlarged or unfolded aorta, that redirects venous flow across the septum when upright","Intrapulmonary causes include hepatopulmonary syndrome and pulmonary arteriovenous malformations, especially with basal-predominant shunting","Contrast (bubble) echocardiography, ideally with postural manoeuvres, is central to localising the shunt as intracardiac or intrapulmonary","Oxygen supplementation often only partially corrects the hypoxaemia because of true shunt physiology","Percutaneous or surgical closure of an intracardiac defect can be curative, whereas intrapulmonary causes are managed according to their underlying disease"],"pathophysiology":"Platypnoea-orthodeoxia syndrome arises from a right-to-left shunt whose magnitude increases in the upright posture. In the intracardiac form there is an interatrial communication such as a patent foramen ovale, but the shunt only becomes haemodynamically significant when an additional anatomical or functional factor, for example an elongated or dilated aortic root, right pneumonectomy, or pericardial change, alters the geometry of the atria so that inferior vena caval flow is preferentially directed across the septum on standing. In the intrapulmonary form, dilated or abnormal vessels at the lung bases, as seen in hepatopulmonary syndrome or arteriovenous malformations, receive more perfusion when upright, increasing the fraction of venous blood bypassing gas exchange. In both mechanisms, standing augments the shunt and produces the characteristic positional hypoxaemia and breathlessness.","additionalDifferentials":["Hepatopulmonary syndrome","Pulmonary arteriovenous malformation (e.g. hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia)","Patent foramen ovale or atrial septal defect without other cause","Chronic parenchymal lung disease with basal shunting","Autonomic or orthostatic disorders mimicking positional symptoms","Post-pneumonectomy anatomical distortion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gases measured both supine and upright to document orthodeoxia","Contrast (agitated saline) echocardiography with postural manoeuvres to localise the shunt","Transoesophageal echocardiography to define interatrial anatomy","CT or MR imaging to identify aortic, mediastinal, or pulmonary vascular abnormalities","Technetium macroaggregated albumin scan to quantify an intrapulmonary shunt","Assessment for liver disease when hepatopulmonary syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["Severe positional hypoxaemia limiting the patient to a recumbent posture","Hypoxaemia poorly responsive to supplemental oxygen, indicating true shunt","Paradoxical embolic events such as stroke through an intracardiac shunt","Rapidly progressive desaturation suggesting worsening shunt fraction"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause; closure of a responsible intracardiac defect is frequently curative with immediate resolution of the positional hypoxaemia. Intrapulmonary causes carry the prognosis of the underlying pulmonary or hepatic disease.","pitfalls":["Measuring oxygenation only supine and thereby missing orthodeoxia","Assuming an incidental patent foramen ovale is sufficient without seeking the anatomical factor that makes it shunt","Failing to distinguish intracardiac from intrapulmonary shunting before intervention","Attributing positional breathlessness to deconditioning or anxiety","Overlooking hepatopulmonary syndrome as an intrapulmonary cause"],"management":["Close the responsible interatrial communication, most often by percutaneous device closure of a patent foramen ovale or atrial septal defect, which frequently resolves the positional hypoxaemia in intracardiac disease","Treat intrapulmonary shunts by embolising pulmonary arteriovenous malformations and managing hepatopulmonary syndrome, with liver transplantation the definitive option where indicated","Give supplemental oxygen for symptomatic relief, recognising it only partially corrects true-shunt hypoxaemia","Use positional measures such as recumbency to relieve upright breathlessness and desaturation pending definitive treatment","Investigate and address the underlying anatomical or hepatic driver, such as aortic root distortion or chronic liver disease","Coordinate care across cardiology, interventional specialists and hepatology, and monitor supine and upright oxygenation to confirm response"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC/ERS Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Pulmonary Hypertension","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"pleomorphic_adenoma":{"name":"Pleomorphic adenoma","system":"Head and neck / Salivary glands","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Benign mixed tumour","Mixed tumour of salivary gland"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest salivary gland neoplasm overall, most often arising in the superficial lobe and tail of the parotid as a painless, slow-growing, mobile mass.","Histologically biphasic and truly 'mixed': epithelial and myoepithelial cells set within a chondromyxoid or hyaline stroma, giving the classic myxoid appearance.","Driven in most cases by PLAG1 rearrangement (8q12) or, less often, HMGA2 (12q), frequently through gene fusions such as CTNNB1-PLAG1.","The capsule is characteristically incomplete with finger-like pseudopodia and satellite nodules, so simple enucleation predisposes to recurrence.","Recommended treatment is complete excision with a cuff of normal tissue (superficial or partial parotidectomy for parotid lesions) with facial nerve preservation.","Fine-needle aspiration cytology typically shows metachromatic fibrillary matrix with epithelial and myoepithelial cells; a hypercellular aspirate should not be over-called as malignant.","Long-standing tumours carry a rising risk of malignant transformation to carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma, so a sudden change in a chronic lump is significant.","Deep-lobe or parapharyngeal lesions may present as a dumbbell-shaped mass with medial displacement of the tonsil and soft palate."],"pathophysiology":"Pleomorphic adenoma arises from the terminal duct and myoepithelial cell compartment of salivary tissue and is characterised by both epithelial and mesenchymal-appearing differentiation from a common neoplastic clone. Recurrent chromosomal translocations activate the transcription factors PLAG1 or HMGA2, which reprogramme cellular growth and drive proliferation of ductal epithelial and modified myoepithelial cells. The neoplastic myoepithelial cells elaborate an abundant extracellular matrix that undergoes chondromyxoid, myxoid and occasionally osseous change, producing the tumour's distinctive heterogeneous stroma. Growth is expansile with a fibrous but frequently incomplete pseudocapsule, through which small tumour buds and satellite nodules extend, accounting for recurrence after incomplete removal and for the field of tumour left behind if the capsule is breached.","additionalDifferentials":["Warthin tumour","Basal cell adenoma","Myoepithelioma","Adenoid cystic carcinoma","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma","Carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma","Schwannoma of the facial nerve"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fine-needle aspiration cytology (with core biopsy where cytology is equivocal)","Ultrasound of the gland and neck as first-line imaging","MRI showing a well-defined, lobulated, markedly T2-hyperintense lesion","CT where bony involvement or deep-lobe extension is suspected","Assessment of facial nerve function before intervention","Histological examination of the excised specimen with margin assessment"],"redFlags":["Sudden rapid growth or pain in a previously stable mass, suggesting malignant transformation","Facial nerve weakness or palsy","Fixation to skin or deep tissues or overlying skin ulceration","New cervical lymphadenopathy","Rapidly enlarging long-standing tumour present for many years"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent after adequate complete excision, with low recurrence when the capsule is respected. The principal long-term concern is malignant transformation, the risk of which rises with the duration the tumour has been present.","pitfalls":["Enucleation or capsular breach with tumour spillage leading to multinodular recurrence that is difficult to eradicate","Over-interpreting a cellular aspirate as malignant on fine-needle aspiration cytology","Overlooking a deep-lobe or parapharyngeal component on clinical examination","Failing to recognise malignant transformation in a chronic, recently changed swelling","Inadequate facial nerve identification during parotid surgery"],"management":["Undertake complete surgical excision with a cuff of normal tissue, performing superficial or partial parotidectomy for parotid lesions rather than simple enucleation to respect the incomplete pseudocapsule and satellite nodules","Identify and preserve the facial nerve during parotid surgery, assessing nerve function beforehand","Excise submandibular and minor salivary gland lesions completely with adequate margins","Avoid capsular rupture and tumour spillage intraoperatively, as this predisposes to multinodular recurrence","Consider adjuvant radiotherapy for recurrent, multifocal or incompletely excised disease","Maintain long-term surveillance for local recurrence and for malignant transformation to carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma","Refer to head and neck surgery for imaging, cytology and operative planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Gnepp's Diagnostic Surgical Pathology of the Head and Neck"],"crossLinks":[]},"pleomorphic_xanthoastrocytoma":{"name":"Pleomorphic xanthoastrocytoma (BRAF V600E)","system":"Oncology / Neuro-oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PXA","pleomorphic xanthoastrocytoma","BRAF V600E-mutant astrocytoma","CNS WHO grade 2 or 3 astrocytoma","anaplastic pleomorphic xanthoastrocytoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of the CNS","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A relatively circumscribed astrocytic tumour of children and young adults, typically superficial and involving the temporal lobe with the overlying meninges","Frequently presents with seizures owing to its cortical, temporal location","MRI commonly shows a peripheral, contrast-enhancing solid nodule with an associated cyst and, often, involvement of the adjacent leptomeninges","BRAF V600E mutation is present in a large proportion of cases and combines with CDKN2A/B deletion as a characteristic molecular signature","Histology shows pleomorphic, sometimes lipidised (xanthomatous) astrocytes, eosinophilic granular bodies and pericellular reticulin","Graded as CNS WHO grade 2 or, when mitotic activity is high (anaplastic PXA), grade 3, which behaves more aggressively","Gross total resection is the mainstay, and BRAF-targeted therapy is an option in recurrent or anaplastic BRAF V600E-mutant tumours"],"pathophysiology":"Pleomorphic xanthoastrocytoma is an astrocytic tumour arising superficially in the cerebral hemispheres, characteristically in the temporal lobe and often reaching the leptomeninges. It is thought to derive from astrocytes or subpial progenitor cells. Despite alarming histological pleomorphism, including large bizarre and lipid-laden cells, the typical tumour is relatively circumscribed and lower grade. Activation of the MAP kinase pathway, most often via the BRAF V600E mutation, frequently combined with homozygous deletion of CDKN2A/B, drives tumorigenesis. Higher mitotic activity defines the anaplastic (grade 3) variant, which is more prone to recurrence and progression. Its cortical position accounts for the prominence of seizures.","additionalDifferentials":["Ganglioglioma","Pilocytic astrocytoma","Diffuse astrocytoma","Glioblastoma (including epithelioid variants)","Dysembryoplastic neuroepithelial tumour","Superficial meningioma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced brain MRI to characterise the superficial nodule, cyst and meningeal involvement","Video-EEG when seizures are the presenting feature","Histopathology showing pleomorphic and xanthomatous astrocytes with eosinophilic granular bodies","BRAF V600E immunohistochemistry or molecular testing","CDKN2A/B deletion analysis","Ki-67/MIB-1 index and mitotic count to assess for anaplastic (grade 3) features"],"redFlags":["High mitotic activity or necrosis indicating anaplastic transformation","Rapid clinical or radiological progression","New focal deficits or refractory seizures","Leptomeningeal dissemination in higher-grade tumours"],"prognosis":"Prognosis for the grade 2 tumour is generally good after gross total resection, with prolonged survival, whereas the anaplastic (grade 3) variant recurs more readily and carries a poorer outlook. BRAF status, extent of resection and grade are the main determinants, and BRAF/MEK-directed therapy offers an option for recurrent or anaplastic BRAF V600E-mutant disease.","pitfalls":["Overcalling the marked pleomorphism as glioblastoma and overtreating a lower-grade tumour","Failing to recognise anaplastic PXA and underestimating recurrence risk","Not testing for BRAF V600E, missing a targetable alteration","Incomplete resection of a superficial, potentially curable lesion"],"management":["Achieve maximal safe surgical resection, with gross total resection the primary treatment and strongest determinant of outcome","Offer BRAF- and MEK-targeted therapy, combining a BRAF inhibitor such as dabrafenib with a MEK inhibitor such as trametinib, for BRAF V600E-mutant recurrent, unresectable or anaplastic tumours","Add radiotherapy, with or without chemotherapy, for anaplastic (grade 3), subtotally resected or recurrent disease","Control seizures with antiseizure medication given the cortical, temporal location","Maintain surveillance with serial contrast-enhanced MRI to detect recurrence and progression to a higher grade","Manage care through a neuro-oncology multidisciplinary team and escalate for progression or malignant transformation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System","Louis DN et al. The 2021 WHO Classification of Tumors of the Central Nervous System"],"crossLinks":[]},"pleural_empyema":{"name":"Pleural empyema","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["empyema","empyema thoracis","pyothorax","complicated parapneumonic effusion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Empyema is the presence of pus within the pleural space, most commonly a complication of bacterial pneumonia (parapneumonic effusion)","Presents with persistent or worsening fever, pleuritic chest pain, breathlessness, and failure to improve despite pneumonia treatment","Suspect empyema when a parapneumonic effusion is large, loculated, or associated with ongoing sepsis","Pleural fluid sampling is key; frankly purulent fluid, positive Gram stain or culture, low pH, low glucose, and high LDH indicate a complicated effusion or empyema needing drainage","Contrast CT and ultrasound help characterise loculations, pleural thickening, and the underlying lung and guide drainage","Effective management combines prompt drainage of the infected space with appropriate antimicrobial therapy covering likely organisms","Loculated or organising collections may require intrapleural fibrinolytics or surgical intervention such as decortication","Anaerobes and mixed flora are common, particularly with aspiration, and treatment should reflect the likely microbiology"],"pathophysiology":"Empyema typically evolves through stages from an initial free-flowing exudative parapneumonic effusion, as infection and inflammation increase pleural capillary permeability. Bacterial invasion of the pleural fluid promotes neutrophil influx, fibrin deposition, and metabolic activity that lower fluid pH and glucose and raise LDH. Fibrin strands then organise the collection into loculations (fibrinopurulent stage). If untreated, fibroblast proliferation produces a thick, inelastic pleural peel that traps the lung and restricts expansion (organising stage), impairing recovery.","additionalDifferentials":["Uncomplicated parapneumonic effusion","Malignant pleural effusion","Tuberculous pleural effusion","Haemothorax","Lung abscess","Transudative effusion from heart failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic pleural aspiration with fluid pH, glucose, LDH, protein, Gram stain, and culture","Chest ultrasound to detect loculations and guide sampling and drainage","Contrast-enhanced CT of the chest to define pleural collections and underlying pathology","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers to assess systemic infection","Cytology and mycobacterial studies where malignancy or tuberculosis is possible"],"redFlags":["Signs of severe sepsis or septic shock","Large or rapidly accumulating collection causing respiratory compromise","Failure to improve despite antibiotics and initial drainage, suggesting loculation or trapped lung","Development of a bronchopleural fistula or spreading infection"],"prognosis":"With early recognition, adequate drainage, and appropriate antibiotics, most patients recover well; delayed or inadequate treatment allows loculation and pleural thickening, leading to prolonged illness, the need for surgery, chronic respiratory impairment, and higher mortality, particularly in frail or comorbid patients.","pitfalls":["Managing an infected pleural collection with antibiotics alone without adequate drainage","Delaying pleural fluid sampling in a non-resolving pneumonia","Underestimating loculated collections on plain radiography without ultrasound or CT","Overlooking anaerobic or aspiration-related organisms when choosing antimicrobial cover"],"management":["Drain the infected pleural space promptly with an intercostal chest tube, guided by ultrasound","Start empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics covering likely organisms including anaerobes, then rationalise to culture results and continue a prolonged course","Instil intrapleural fibrinolytic combined with DNase for loculated or septated collections that drain poorly","Refer for surgical intervention, such as video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery or decortication, when drainage fails or an organising empyema traps the lung","Treat the underlying pneumonia and any source of infection for adequate source control","Provide supportive care with nutritional support, venous thromboembolism prophylaxis and analgesia, and monitor recovery with imaging and inflammatory markers","Refer to respiratory medicine and thoracic surgery, and escalate for persistent sepsis or failure to re-expand the lung"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"pleural_plaque":{"name":"Pleural plaques","system":"Respiratory / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["asbestos-related pleural plaques","hyaline pleural plaques","parietal pleural plaques"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Discrete areas of hyaline fibrosis on the parietal pleura that are the commonest radiological marker of past asbestos exposure","Usually asymptomatic and discovered incidentally on chest radiograph or CT performed for another reason","Characteristically affect the posterolateral chest wall, the domes of the diaphragm, and the mediastinal pleura while sparing the apices and costophrenic angles","Often calcify over time, producing the classic 'holly-leaf' en-face appearance on imaging","Typically appear only after a latency of several decades from first exposure","Are benign and not in themselves premalignant, but signify exposure that carries an independent lifetime risk of mesothelioma and bronchial carcinoma","Rarely cause meaningful functional impairment, so alternative explanations should be sought for significant breathlessness","Warrant a careful occupational history and appropriate counselling about future risk and any surveillance obligations"],"pathophysiology":"Inhaled asbestos fibres, particularly long amphibole fibres, are transported to the parietal pleura where they provoke a low-grade chronic inflammatory response. Over many years this drives deposition of dense, acellular collagen that forms well-demarcated islands of hyaline fibrosis, which frequently mineralise and calcify. The plaques sit on the parietal surface and, unlike diffuse pleural thickening or malignant disease, remain localised and do not invade underlying lung. They are a durable biological marker of cumulative asbestos exposure rather than a lesion that itself transforms into cancer.","additionalDifferentials":["Diffuse pleural thickening","Malignant pleural mesothelioma","Pleural metastatic deposits","Healed empyema or haemothorax with residual pleural scarring","Prominent extrapleural fat mimicking plaques","Loculated or organising pleural effusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph to detect calcified plaques and diaphragmatic involvement","CT of the thorax for better characterisation and to distinguish benign plaques from malignant pleural disease","Detailed occupational and asbestos exposure history","Pulmonary function tests, which are usually normal or show only mild restriction","PET-CT or pleural biopsy where imaging raises concern about malignant transformation elsewhere in the pleura"],"redFlags":["New pleuritic pain, a pleural effusion, or a growing nodular pleural mass suggesting mesothelioma","Unexplained weight loss with progressive breathlessness","Rapidly enlarging, irregular, or circumferential pleural thickening","Focal soft-tissue mass arising from the pleura on cross-sectional imaging"],"prognosis":"Isolated pleural plaques are benign with an excellent outlook and do not shorten life, but they mark asbestos exposure that confers a lasting risk of mesothelioma and lung cancer, so any new pleural symptoms should prompt reassessment.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting benign calcified plaques as malignant pleural disease and triggering unnecessary intervention","Attributing significant breathlessness to plaques rather than seeking an alternative cause","Failing to take an exposure history and counsel the patient about future malignancy risk","Confusing normal extrapleural fat pads with genuine plaques on radiographs"],"management":["Reassure the patient that isolated plaques are benign, are not premalignant and do not themselves shorten life, so no specific treatment is required","Ensure cessation of any ongoing asbestos exposure and provide occupational advice and support with compensation claims","Strongly advise smoking cessation, which compounds the asbestos-related risk of bronchial carcinoma","Maintain surveillance for asbestos-related malignancy, promptly investigating any new respiratory symptoms or effusion","Compare serial imaging to confirm stability and distinguish stable plaques from evolving diffuse thickening or malignant disease","Assess pulmonary function periodically to detect coexistent asbestosis or diffuse pleural disease","Refer to respiratory medicine when imaging or symptoms raise concern about mesothelioma or lung cancer"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"pleural_plaques":{"name":"pleural plaques","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hyaline pleural plaques","asbestos-related pleural plaques"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pleural plaques are discrete, well-circumscribed areas of hyaline (collagenous) fibrosis of the parietal pleura and are the commonest manifestation of asbestos exposure.","They characteristically involve the posterolateral chest wall, the domes of the diaphragm and the mediastinal pleura, while sparing the costophrenic angles and lung apices.","There is a long latency, typically 20 to 40 years from first exposure.","Plaques are usually asymptomatic incidental radiological findings and rarely cause meaningful functional impairment.","Calcification is common with time and aids radiological recognition.","They are a marker of asbestos exposure and hence of increased risk of other asbestos-related disease, but are not themselves premalignant and do not transform into mesothelioma.","CT is more sensitive and specific than chest radiography and helps separate plaques from mimics such as extrapleural fat.","Their main importance is as a marker of exposure prompting counselling on mesothelioma and lung cancer risk and on smoking cessation."],"pathophysiology":"Inhaled asbestos fibres, particularly the longer amphibole fibres, are carried to the parietal pleura via lymphatic channels. There they incite a low-grade, chronic inflammatory and fibrogenic response with deposition of dense, relatively acellular basket-weave hyaline collagen, forming discrete plaques on the parietal pleura and diaphragm. Over years these plaques may calcify. Unlike diffuse pleural thickening and mesothelioma, plaques remain localised, typically spare the visceral pleura and reflect cumulative exposure rather than a progressive malignant process.","additionalDifferentials":["Diffuse pleural thickening (asbestos-related or post-inflammatory)","Malignant pleural mesothelioma","Pleural metastases","Extrapleural fat pads","Fibrothorax from healed empyema or haemothorax","Rounded atelectasis","Rib companion shadows and other plain-film mimics"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph","High-resolution CT of the thorax for characterisation and to exclude other disease","Detailed occupational and exposure history","Pulmonary function tests (usually normal; to detect coexistent asbestosis)","Comparison with previous imaging to confirm stability","Further characterisation with PET or biopsy only if features suggest malignancy"],"redFlags":["Pleural thickening that is nodular, circumferential or associated with a mass, raising concern for mesothelioma","A new pleural effusion","Chest pain or unexplained weight loss","Progressive or asymmetric thickening on serial imaging"],"prognosis":"Pleural plaques themselves are benign with an excellent prognosis and rarely cause symptoms or meaningful functional impairment; their significance lies in signalling asbestos exposure and the associated lifetime risk of mesothelioma and lung cancer.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting plaques as metastases or mesothelioma, or vice versa.","Wrongly telling a patient that plaques turn into cancer, causing unnecessary alarm; they are markers, not precursors.","Confusing extrapleural fat or normal muscle shadows with plaques on plain films.","Failing to counsel on smoking cessation, which multiplies asbestos-related lung cancer risk.","Overlooking coexisting asbestosis or an early mesothelioma."],"management":["Reassure that pleural plaques are benign, rarely symptomatic and not themselves premalignant, requiring no disease-specific therapy","Confirm cessation of asbestos exposure and document a detailed occupational history to support medico-legal and compensation needs","Advise smoking cessation to reduce the compounded lifetime risk of lung cancer","Maintain vigilance for other asbestos-related disease, investigating any new breathlessness, chest pain or pleural effusion","Compare with previous imaging to confirm stability and exclude evolving diffuse pleural thickening or malignancy","Monitor pulmonary function to detect coexistent asbestosis","Refer for further imaging or biopsy only where features suggest malignant transformation elsewhere in the pleura"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","BTS Statement on Asbestos-related Pleural Disease","Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"pleuroparenchymal_fibroelastosis":{"name":"Pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPFE","Idiopathic pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare fibrosing interstitial lung disease defined by fibrosis of the visceral pleura and subpleural parenchyma with striking upper-lobe predominance.","May be idiopathic or associated with haematopoietic stem cell or lung transplantation, recurrent infection, chemotherapy, autoimmune disease and familial pulmonary fibrosis.","A characteristic phenotype comprises a thin body habitus, a flattened thorax with reduced anteroposterior diameter (platythorax) and deepening of the suprasternal notch.","Presents with breathlessness, dry cough, recurrent pneumothoraces and weight loss.","HRCT shows dense upper-zone pleural and subpleural fibrosis with volume loss, traction bronchiectasis and architectural distortion, sparing the lower zones early in disease.","Histology demonstrates intra-alveolar fibrosis with prominent elastosis and visceral pleural thickening (elastofibrosis).","It frequently coexists with another interstitial lung disease pattern, such as UIP, in the lower lobes.","No drug therapy is of proven benefit; antifibrotic agents are being explored and transplantation is considered in suitable patients."],"pathophysiology":"Pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis is defined by dense fibroelastotic thickening of the visceral pleura and the immediately subpleural parenchyma, with elastin-rich fibrosis obliterating alveolar spaces predominantly in the upper lobes. Proposed mechanisms include recurrent injury and infection, immune dysregulation such as after transplantation, and genetic predisposition; the upper-zone distribution and marked elastosis are distinctive.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis with a UIP pattern","Chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Fibrotic sarcoidosis with upper-zone fibrosis","Apical fibrosis following prior tuberculosis or radiation","Ankylosing spondylitis-related apical fibrosis","Chronic fibrosing pleuritis","Connective tissue disease-associated interstitial lung disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["HRCT thorax demonstrating upper-zone pleuroparenchymal fibrosis","Pulmonary function tests, which show restriction with a characteristically preserved or raised residual volume relative to total lung capacity due to platythorax","Surgical lung biopsy where the diagnosis is uncertain","Autoimmune serology and detailed exposure history","Review of prior transplant, chemotherapy or recurrent infection history","Assessment for coexistent lower-lobe interstitial lung disease","Six-minute walk test with oximetry"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or bilateral pneumothorax","Rapidly progressive restrictive decline","Significant weight loss or cachexia","Coexisting lower-lobe fibrosis indicating combined disease with a worse outcome"],"prognosis":"The disease is often progressive with a poor outcome, particularly when combined with lower-lobe fibrosis; pneumothorax and progressive respiratory failure are common complications.","pitfalls":["Dismissing apical changes as old infection or scarring","Missing the characteristic body habitus and preserved lung volumes as diagnostic clues","Overlooking coexisting lower-lobe interstitial lung disease","Attributing recurrent pneumothorax to primary spontaneous pneumothorax"],"management":["Recognise there is no proven disease-specific cure, so base care on a progressive-fibrosing interstitial lung disease framework","Consider an antifibrotic agent such as nintedanib or pirfenidone where a progressive fibrosing phenotype is demonstrated","Treat any associated or underlying condition, including autoimmune disease, recurrent infection, or post-transplant and chemotherapy-related injury","Provide supportive care with supplemental oxygen, pulmonary rehabilitation and nutritional support for the characteristic weight loss","Manage recurrent pneumothorax with drainage and cautious pleurodesis, mindful of the impact on future lung transplantation","Refer eligible patients with progressive disease for lung transplantation assessment","Monitor lung function and manage care through an interstitial lung disease multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","ATS/ERS international multidisciplinary classification of the idiopathic interstitial pneumonias"],"crossLinks":[]},"pleuropulmonary_blastoma":{"name":"pleuropulmonary blastoma","system":"respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPB","pleuropulmonary blastoma of childhood"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Thoracic Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest primary lung malignancy of early childhood, a dysembryonic mesenchymal tumour of lung and pleura, distinct from the epithelial pulmonary blastoma of adults.","Classified into three progressive types: Type I is purely cystic, Type II is mixed cystic and solid, and Type III is purely solid, with prognosis worsening across the sequence.","Type I may regress or, if untreated, progress to the aggressive Type II and III forms, so complete resection of Type I lesions is advocated.","Around two-thirds of cases arise in DICER1 syndrome; identifying a germline DICER1 pathogenic variant unlocks family surveillance.","It is frequently and dangerously mistaken for a benign congenital pulmonary airway malformation (CPAM) on imaging.","Presentations include respiratory distress, persistent cough, chest pain, and spontaneous pneumothorax.","The solid types metastasise with a particular predilection for the brain and bone.","DICER1 syndrome also predisposes to cystic nephroma, Sertoli-Leydig cell tumour, multinodular goitre, ciliary body medulloepithelioma, and nasal chondromesenchymal hamartoma."],"pathophysiology":"Pleuropulmonary blastoma is a dysontogenetic tumour arising from the primitive mesenchyme of the developing lung and pleura. Most cases are associated with germline loss-of-function mutation in DICER1, an endoribonuclease essential for microRNA maturation; a characteristic somatic hotspot missense mutation in the RNase IIIb domain of the remaining allele deregulates microRNA processing and drives tumour formation. Disease begins as an epithelium-lined cystic lesion (Type I) harbouring a subtle malignant mesenchymal (cambium) layer beneath a benign-appearing epithelium; over time the mesenchyme overgrows to produce mixed (Type II) and then wholly solid, high-grade sarcomatous (Type III) tumours, which may display blastematous, rhabdomyosarcomatous, chondroid, and anaplastic elements.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital pulmonary airway malformation (CPAM)","Congenital lobar emphysema","Bronchogenic or other foregut duplication cyst","Pulmonary sequestration","Bronchopulmonary infection with cavitation or empyema","Metastatic sarcoma or Wilms tumour to the lung","Mediastinal germ cell tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the chest to characterise cystic versus solid components","Histopathology of the resected lesion with attention to the malignant cambium layer","Germline and tumour DICER1 mutation testing","Brain MRI to screen for cerebral metastases in solid types","Bone imaging for skeletal metastases","Echocardiography if there is mediastinal or cardiac displacement","Thyroid ultrasound and abdominal/pelvic imaging for DICER1-associated tumours"],"redFlags":["Spontaneous pneumothorax in a young child, which may herald an underlying cystic pleuropulmonary blastoma","A presumed CPAM with solid components, septal thickening, or rapid enlargement","Neurological signs suggesting cerebral metastasis","A family history of DICER1-associated tumours"],"prognosis":"Type I disease that is completely resected has a favourable outcome, whereas Type II and especially Type III tumours carry a substantially worse prognosis with significant risk of recurrence and metastatic spread; cerebral metastases are a particularly poor prognostic feature.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing a cystic lung lesion as benign CPAM without histological review, missing an early Type I pleuropulmonary blastoma.","Adopting watchful waiting for complex cystic lung lesions in a child with a DICER1 background rather than resecting.","Not sending DICER1 testing, missing the syndrome and surveillance opportunities for the child and relatives.","Omitting brain imaging in solid-type disease and thereby missing metastases."],"management":["Perform complete surgical resection as the cornerstone of treatment, including excision of purely cystic Type I lesions to pre-empt progression to solid, higher-grade forms","Give adjuvant multi-agent sarcoma-type chemotherapy, such as combinations of vincristine, actinomycin, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide, for Type II and Type III disease","Consider radiotherapy for selected residual, unresectable or metastatic disease","Undertake germline and tumour DICER1 mutation testing, with genetic counselling and family surveillance when a pathogenic variant is found","Screen for and manage metastatic spread, including brain MRI for cerebral metastases and skeletal imaging","Manage acute complications such as pneumothorax and respiratory distress, and stabilise the airway when needed","Coordinate care through a paediatric oncology multidisciplinary team with long-term follow-up for recurrence and other DICER1-related tumours"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Thoracic Tumours","International Pleuropulmonary Blastoma / DICER1 Registry guidance","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"pleva":{"name":"PLEVA (pityriasis lichenoides et varioliformis acuta)","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pityriasis lichenoides et varioliformis acuta","acute pityriasis lichenoides","Mucha-Habermann disease","PLEVA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The acute pole of the pityriasis lichenoides spectrum, marked by abrupt crops of erythematous papules that vesiculate, become haemorrhagic or necrotic, and crust over","Individual lesions may heal with varioliform (smallpox-like) pitted scars, unlike the chronic PLC form","Successive crops mean lesions at different stages of evolution are present at the same time","Predominantly affects children and young adults, favouring the trunk and proximal limbs","Usually benign and self-limiting but tends to run a more inflammatory and relapsing course than PLC","The rare febrile ulceronecrotic Mucha-Habermann variant presents with high fever, large coalescing necrotic ulcers, and systemic symptoms and can be life-threatening","Biopsy shows a dense, often wedge-shaped lymphocytic infiltrate with interface change, keratinocyte necrosis, and extravasated red cells","Managed with topical corticosteroids, oral antibiotics with anti-inflammatory action (such as tetracyclines or macrolides), and phototherapy, with systemic immunosuppression reserved for the ulceronecrotic variant"],"pathophysiology":"PLEVA is regarded as a reactive, predominantly CD8-positive cytotoxic T-cell response that produces a lymphocytic vasculitis and epidermal injury. As with the chronic form, triggers are thought to include infectious antigens and, in some cases, drugs, which provoke an inflammatory cascade damaging both keratinocytes and dermal vessels. The intense cytotoxic reaction accounts for the vesiculation, haemorrhage, and necrosis of individual lesions and for the varioliform scarring that can follow. In the severe febrile ulceronecrotic variant an exaggerated response, sometimes with a large clonal T-cell population and cytokine release, drives widespread ulceration and systemic illness.","additionalDifferentials":["Varicella (chickenpox)","Lymphomatoid papulosis","Disseminated herpes simplex","Papular urticaria / arthropod bite reactions","Small-vessel vasculitis","Erythema multiforme","Secondary syphilis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy for histopathology to demonstrate the cytotoxic interface reaction with necrosis","T-cell receptor gene rearrangement studies and immunophenotyping to exclude lymphomatoid papulosis","Viral swabs or PCR for varicella-zoster or herpes simplex where a vesicular infection is suspected","Full blood count, inflammatory markers, and close observation for systemic upset in suspected febrile ulceronecrotic disease","Serology for potential triggers (Epstein-Barr virus, Toxoplasma, HIV) when indicated","Blood cultures if the patient is febrile and systemically unwell"],"redFlags":["High fever with rapidly enlarging, coalescing necrotic ulcers indicating febrile ulceronecrotic Mucha-Habermann disease","Systemic features such as arthralgia, abdominal pain, or mucosal involvement","Extensive necrosis raising the risk of secondary infection and sepsis","Atypical or clonal lymphoid infiltrate suggesting lymphomatoid papulosis or lymphoma"],"prognosis":"Most cases resolve over weeks to months, though relapses are common and varioliform scarring may persist; the febrile ulceronecrotic variant carries appreciable morbidity and, rarely, mortality and warrants urgent aggressive treatment.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing early vesicular lesions as chickenpox and missing the relapsing crop pattern","Failing to recognise and treat the febrile ulceronecrotic variant promptly","Overlooking the clinical and histological overlap with lymphomatoid papulosis","Attributing residual varioliform scars to another cause"],"management":["Use topical corticosteroids with emollients as first-line therapy to control inflammation and symptoms of individual lesions","Offer an oral antibiotic with anti-inflammatory action, such as a tetracycline or erythromycin, as the initial systemic treatment","Use phototherapy, particularly narrowband UVB, for widespread or persistent disease","Reserve methotrexate or other systemic immunosuppression for refractory or severe disease","Treat the febrile ulceronecrotic Mucha-Habermann variant urgently with systemic corticosteroids and methotrexate or ciclosporin, adding intravenous immunoglobulin, meticulous wound care and supportive management of the systemic illness","Identify and treat any infective trigger where one is implicated","Monitor for relapse and for rare progression to a lymphoproliferative disorder, and refer to dermatology, escalating promptly for ulceronecrotic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":["pityriasis_lichenoides"]},"plica_syndrome":{"name":"Medial plica syndrome of the knee","system":"Musculoskeletal / Hand & Peripheral nerve","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["synovial plica syndrome","medial patellar plica syndrome","mediopatellar plica syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Plicae are embryological remnants of synovial folds, and the medial patellar plica is the one that most often becomes symptomatic","Patients report anteromedial knee pain with clicking or snapping and a sense of catching or pseudo-locking, worse with flexion activities such as stairs or after prolonged sitting","A tender palpable band can often be felt along the medial border of the patella, sometimes rolling under the examining fingers","The plica becomes symptomatic when it thickens, loses elasticity and becomes fibrotic or inflamed through overuse or trauma, impinging on the medial femoral condyle","A chronically inflamed plica can abrade the cartilage of the medial condyle or patella","The diagnosis is largely clinical and one of exclusion, with MRI or dynamic ultrasound able to show a thickened plica","Arthroscopy is the definitive means of confirming a truly pathological plica","Generic management includes activity modification, physiotherapy targeting the quadriceps and stretching, corticosteroid injection and arthroscopic resection for refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"A synovial plica is a remnant of the embryonic septa that once divided the knee into compartments, and the medial patellar plica is the fold that most commonly becomes symptomatic. Repetitive overuse or direct trauma causes the plica to thicken, lose its elasticity and become fibrotic and inflamed, so that it impinges against the medial femoral condyle during flexion and extension. This produces pain, snapping and sometimes chondral damage. Asymptomatic plicae are common incidental findings and do not require treatment.","additionalDifferentials":["Patellofemoral pain syndrome","Medial meniscal tear","Chondromalacia patellae or a chondral lesion","Medial collateral ligament injury","Osteochondritis dissecans","Intra-articular loose body","Pes anserine bursitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination for a tender palpable medial band and reproduction of the snap","MRI to show a thickened plica and any associated chondral change","Dynamic ultrasound to visualise the plica during movement","Plain radiographs to exclude other pathology","Diagnostic arthroscopy as the definitive investigation"],"redFlags":["True locking or an effusion suggesting a meniscal tear or loose body","Significant chondral damage","Features suggesting an inflammatory or septic arthritis","Progressive mechanical symptoms"],"prognosis":"Many patients settle with conservative treatment, and arthroscopic excision of a genuinely symptomatic plica gives good results.","pitfalls":["Overdiagnosis, since asymptomatic plicae are common incidental findings","Missing a meniscal or chondral lesion, as the diagnosis is one of exclusion","Resecting an incidental plica that is not the source of symptoms","Ignoring underlying patellofemoral dysfunction"],"management":["Begin with conservative care, including activity modification and structured physiotherapy targeting quadriceps and vastus medialis strengthening with hamstring and iliotibial band stretching","Use simple analgesics or NSAIDs to settle the inflamed, fibrotic plica","Consider an intra-articular or local corticosteroid injection for symptoms that persist despite rehabilitation","Address contributing biomechanical factors such as patellar maltracking and training load","Proceed to arthroscopic resection of the symptomatic plica when adequate conservative treatment fails, treating any associated chondral damage at the same time","Refer to orthopaedics for dynamic imaging or arthroscopy where the diagnosis is uncertain or symptoms are refractory"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","Green's Operative Hand Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"plummer_vinson_syndrome":{"name":"Plummer-Vinson syndrome","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Paterson-Brown-Kelly syndrome","Paterson-Kelly syndrome","sideropenic dysphagia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad of iron-deficiency anaemia, dysphagia, and upper (post-cricoid) oesophageal webs","Most often affects middle-aged women and is now uncommon where iron deficiency is well managed","Dysphagia is typically to solids and results from the cervical web narrowing the lumen","Associated features include glossitis, angular cheilitis, koilonychia, and other signs of chronic iron deficiency","Recognised as a premalignant condition with an increased risk of post-cricoid and upper oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma","Cornerstone of management is iron repletion, which often improves the dysphagia, with endoscopic dilatation for persistent webs","Long-term surveillance is warranted given the malignant potential"],"pathophysiology":"Plummer-Vinson syndrome links chronic iron deficiency to the formation of upper oesophageal webs and dysphagia. Iron deficiency is thought to impair the turnover of the rapidly dividing oesophageal mucosa and reduce iron-dependent oxidative enzymes, leading to mucosal atrophy and web formation in the post-cricoid region. The web mechanically narrows the lumen, causing solid-food dysphagia, while the chronic atrophic and inflammatory changes are believed to underlie the increased long-term risk of squamous carcinoma of the hypopharynx and upper oesophagus.","additionalDifferentials":["Oesophageal web without iron deficiency","Post-cricoid or hypopharyngeal carcinoma","Peptic or other oesophageal stricture","Eosinophilic oesophagitis","Oropharyngeal or neuromuscular dysphagia","Schatzki ring"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with iron studies confirming iron-deficiency anaemia","Barium swallow demonstrating a post-cricoid web","Upper endoscopy with biopsy to confirm the web and exclude malignancy","Investigation for the source of iron loss, including gastrointestinal blood loss","Ear-nose-throat evaluation of the hypopharynx where indicated"],"redFlags":["Progressive dysphagia, weight loss, or hoarseness suggesting malignancy","A post-cricoid mass or ulcer on imaging or endoscopy","Occult gastrointestinal blood loss requiring source identification","Persistent symptoms despite iron correction"],"prognosis":"Dysphagia frequently improves with iron repletion, and webs respond to dilatation; the principal long-term concern is the elevated risk of upper aerodigestive squamous carcinoma, which justifies ongoing surveillance.","pitfalls":["Treating the web without correcting the underlying iron deficiency","Failing to investigate the cause of iron loss","Missing an early post-cricoid carcinoma in a patient with sideropenic dysphagia","Neglecting long-term surveillance given the premalignant nature of the condition"],"management":["Iron repletion with oral or parenteral iron is the cornerstone and frequently relieves the dysphagia","Endoscopic bougie or balloon dilatation for cervical webs that persist despite adequate iron repletion","Identify and treat the underlying source of iron loss, such as gastrointestinal blood loss or menorrhagia","Address associated iron-deficiency features including glossitis, angular cheilitis and koilonychia, and optimise dietary iron","Endoscopic surveillance of the hypopharynx and upper oesophagus given the premalignant risk of squamous carcinoma","Ear-nose-throat referral to evaluate the post-cricoid region and any suspicious mucosal change","Escalate for biopsy and oncological assessment if a mass, refractory stricture or persistent symptoms suggest malignant transformation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"plunging_ranula":{"name":"Plunging (cervical) ranula","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Head and Neck","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cervical ranula","diving ranula","plunging sublingual mucocoele","deep ranula"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A ranula that extends beyond the floor of mouth into the neck, presenting as a soft submandibular or upper cervical swelling","Occurs when extravasated saliva from the sublingual gland tracks around or through a dehiscence in the mylohyoid muscle into the neck","May present as a painless neck mass with or without an obvious oral floor-of-mouth component, sometimes fluctuating in size","Like the oral ranula, it is usually a pseudocyst lacking a true epithelial lining and formed by extravasated mucus","MRI or CT characterises the cystic lesion and its cervical extension, and a tail extending toward the sublingual space supports the diagnosis","Aspiration yields viscous, amylase- and protein-rich mucoid fluid, helping distinguish it from other cystic neck lesions","Definitive treatment addresses the source by excising the sublingual gland, which resolves the cervical component without needing separate neck dissection of the pseudocyst","Simple drainage or excision of the neck cyst alone is associated with high recurrence"],"pathophysiology":"A plunging ranula is a variant of the sublingual gland mucus extravasation lesion in which mucus escapes into the neck rather than remaining confined to the floor of the mouth. Saliva leaking from a disrupted sublingual gland tracks posteriorly and inferiorly around the free posterior edge of mylohyoid, or through a congenital or acquired dehiscence within the muscle, descending into the submandibular and upper cervical spaces. The collection is a pseudocyst without a true epithelial lining, and because the sublingual gland continues to secrete, definitive resolution depends on removing that gland to stop the flow of mucus.","additionalDifferentials":["Simple oral ranula","Lymphatic malformation (cystic hygroma)","Branchial cleft cyst","Thyroglossal duct cyst when near the midline","Salivary gland cyst or tumour","Dermoid cyst or abscess of the neck"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI or CT of the neck and floor of mouth to define the cyst and its sublingual connection","Ultrasound to characterise the cystic lesion and guide aspiration","Aspiration demonstrating viscous, amylase-rich mucoid fluid","Histological examination of the excised sublingual gland and any cyst wall","Assessment for an associated oral ranula component"],"redFlags":["Large or rapidly enlarging neck swelling causing airway or swallowing concern","Features atypical for a pseudocyst suggesting a neoplastic or vascular lesion","Signs of secondary infection with pain, erythema and systemic upset","Recurrence after previous surgery indicating retained sublingual gland tissue"],"prognosis":"Excision of the offending sublingual gland gives a high cure rate and resolves the cervical component. Procedures that drain or remove only the neck pseudocyst while leaving the gland in place commonly recur.","pitfalls":["Treating the visible neck cyst without removing the sublingual gland, leading to recurrence","Misdiagnosing the mass as another cystic neck lesion and pursuing inappropriate surgery","Failing to recognise the connection to the sublingual space on imaging","Injuring the lingual nerve or submandibular duct during sublingual gland excision"],"management":["Definitive treatment is transoral excision of the offending sublingual gland to stop the mucus extravasation","Removal or marsupialisation of the cervical pseudocyst alone is inadequate and prone to recurrence, so gland excision is required","Aspiration or drainage may temporarily decompress a large collection but is not curative","Careful surgical technique to protect the lingual nerve, submandibular duct and gland during excision","Histological examination of the excised gland and cyst wall to confirm the diagnosis and exclude other cystic neck lesions","Otolaryngology and head-and-neck surgical referral for operative planning with imaging correlation","Postoperative monitoring for recurrence and for any residual oral floor-of-mouth ranula component"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"pmm2_cdg":{"name":"PMM2-CDG (CDG type Ia)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["phosphomannomutase 2 deficiency","CDG-Ia","carbohydrate-deficient glycoprotein syndrome type Ia","PMM2"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most common congenital disorder of glycosylation; a multisystem N-glycosylation defect caused by biallelic PMM2 variants","Classic infantile presentation combines failure to thrive, developmental delay, hypotonia and a cerebellar/pontocerebellar hypoplasia picture","Distinctive dysmorphic clues include inverted nipples and abnormal (fat pad) subcutaneous fat distribution over the buttocks and suprapubic area","Non-neurological features include pericardial effusion, hepatopathy, coagulopathy, hypothyroidism and stroke-like episodes","Older children develop retinitis pigmentosa, peripheral neuropathy, ataxia, skeletal changes and hypogonadism","Screening test is serum transferrin isoform analysis showing a type 1 pattern (raised asialo- and disialotransferrin) reflecting whole-glycan loss","Enzyme assay of phosphomannomutase in leukocytes/fibroblasts and PMM2 gene sequencing confirm the diagnosis","Management is supportive and multidisciplinary; there is no disease-modifying therapy, though dietary mannose supplementation has been tried without proven benefit"],"pathophysiology":"PMM2-CDG results from deficiency of phosphomannomutase 2, the cytosolic enzyme that converts mannose-6-phosphate to mannose-1-phosphate, an essential step in generating GDP-mannose and the dolichol-linked oligosaccharide precursor used for N-linked glycosylation of proteins in the endoplasmic reticulum. Reduced substrate limits assembly and transfer of the glycan, so many secretory and membrane glycoproteins are hypoglycosylated, with whole glycan chains missing from some sialylation sites. Because glycosylation is required across virtually every organ system, the resulting glycoprotein dysfunction produces the characteristic multisystem phenotype affecting the brain and cerebellum, liver, heart, endocrine glands, coagulation factors and connective tissue. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Other congenital disorders of glycosylation (e.g. ALG6-CDG, MPI-CDG)","Congenital muscular dystrophies with cerebellar involvement","Mitochondrial cytopathies (e.g. Leigh-like disease)","Peroxisomal disorders such as Zellweger spectrum","Pontocerebellar hypoplasia of other genetic cause","Inherited coagulopathies presenting with unexplained bleeding/thrombosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum transferrin isoelectric focusing or mass spectrometry (type 1 pattern)","Phosphomannomutase enzyme activity in leukocytes or fibroblasts","PMM2 gene sequencing for confirmation and family counselling","Coagulation studies including antithrombin, protein C, protein S and factor XI","Thyroid function tests and liver function/transaminases","Brain MRI to demonstrate cerebellar/pontocerebellar hypoplasia","Echocardiography for pericardial effusion and cardiomyopathy"],"redFlags":["Stroke-like episodes or thrombotic events reflecting coagulation factor imbalance","Life-threatening pericardial effusion or cardiac failure in infancy","Severe hepatopathy with hypoalbuminaemia and protein-losing enteropathy","Acute deterioration during intercurrent illness with multi-organ failure","Progressive feeding failure and profound failure to thrive"],"prognosis":"Infantile mortality can reach up to one in five, often from infection, cardiac or hepatic complications; survivors have static intellectual disability with slowly progressive neurological and multisystem involvement, and prognosis varies widely with genotype and severity.","pitfalls":["Attributing the picture to a non-specific 'cerebral palsy' and never testing transferrin glycoforms","False-negative or false-positive transferrin patterns in neonates, intercurrent infection, galactosaemia or heavy alcohol exposure","Overlooking treatable comorbidities such as hypothyroidism and coagulopathy before procedures","Assuming mannose supplementation is effective, unlike its role in MPI-CDG","Not screening for thrombosis/bleeding risk prior to surgery or anticoagulation decisions"],"management":["No curative therapy exists; care is multisystem supportive management coordinated by metabolic medicine","Nutritional support for failure to thrive, including enteral tube feeding where oral intake is inadequate","Correct and monitor the coagulopathy (antithrombin, protein C and S, factor XI), with clotting-factor replacement around surgery or bleeding","Thyroid hormone replacement for hypothyroidism, with ongoing endocrine surveillance","Monitor and manage hepatopathy, pericardial effusion and cardiac involvement","Developmental support, physiotherapy for hypotonia and ataxia, and ophthalmology review for retinitis pigmentosa","Anticipate and treat stroke-like episodes and thrombotic events, avoiding dehydration and other provoking factors","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, with carrier testing and prenatal counselling for the family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","Jaeken J, Inborn Errors of Metabolism: Diagnosis and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"pneumatosis_intestinalis":{"name":"Pneumatosis intestinalis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pneumatosis cystoides intestinalis","intramural bowel gas","pneumatosis coli"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Refers to gas within the bowel wall, which is a radiological sign rather than a single disease and spans a spectrum from entirely benign to life-threatening","The central task is to separate benign (primary or idiopathic) pneumatosis from that caused by bowel ischaemia or necrosis, where it is an ominous finding","Benign forms may be idiopathic (pneumatosis cystoides) or associated with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, connective tissue disease, or certain drugs and are often asymptomatic incidental findings","Alarming features include abdominal pain out of proportion to signs, peritonism, lactic acidosis, and gas in the portal or mesenteric venous system","Cross-sectional imaging is central to assessment, correlating the pattern of intramural gas with the clinical picture","Benign, asymptomatic pneumatosis is generally managed conservatively, whereas suspected ischaemia or necrosis requires urgent surgical evaluation","Portomesenteric venous gas in an unwell patient greatly raises concern for transmural ischaemia"],"pathophysiology":"Pneumatosis intestinalis denotes the presence of gas within the wall of the bowel, and several mechanisms are proposed. In the benign cystic form, gas is thought to track into the submucosa and subserosa either mechanically (for example through mucosal breaks, raised intraluminal pressure, or from the lungs via the mediastinum in chronic lung disease) or through the action of gas-forming luminal bacteria. In the sinister form, ischaemia disrupts the mucosal barrier and, combined with bacterial gas production and gas dissection into damaged wall and mesenteric veins, signals transmural injury and impending or established necrosis; the same radiological sign therefore reflects widely differing underlying processes.","additionalDifferentials":["Mesenteric ischaemia or bowel infarction","Bowel obstruction with impending ischaemia","Necrotising enterocolitis in neonates","Toxic megacolon or severe colitis","Iatrogenic causes such as recent endoscopy or surgery","Emphysematous gastritis or infection with gas-forming organisms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced computed tomography to define the distribution of intramural gas, assess bowel wall enhancement, and detect portomesenteric venous gas","Serum lactate and arterial blood gas to detect metabolic acidosis suggesting ischaemia","Full blood count and inflammatory markers","Plain abdominal and erect chest radiography to identify free air or the intramural gas pattern","Assessment for associated conditions such as chronic lung disease, connective tissue disease, or relevant drug therapy","Endoscopy in selected stable cases to characterise mucosal or cystic lesions"],"redFlags":["Abdominal pain out of proportion to physical findings suggesting mesenteric ischaemia","Peritonitis, haemodynamic instability, or sepsis","Rising lactate or metabolic acidosis","Portal or mesenteric venous gas on imaging in an unwell patient"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends entirely on the underlying cause: benign, idiopathic pneumatosis is often self-limiting or stable with an excellent outlook, whereas pneumatosis due to bowel ischaemia or necrosis, particularly with portomesenteric venous gas, carries a high mortality without prompt surgical intervention.","pitfalls":["Reflexively operating on benign incidental pneumatosis or, conversely, observing ischaemic pneumatosis that needs urgent surgery","Relying on the radiological sign alone without integrating the clinical picture and lactate","Underestimating the significance of portomesenteric venous gas in an unwell patient","Overlooking a drug or lung-disease association that explains a benign form"],"management":["Determine the underlying cause first, as management diverges sharply between benign and ischaemic pneumatosis","Urgent surgical evaluation and laparotomy with resection of non-viable bowel for suspected ischaemia or necrosis, especially with peritonism, lactic acidosis or portomesenteric venous gas","Bowel rest, intravenous fluids and broad-spectrum antibiotics with close observation in equivocal or unwell patients","Conservative management for benign, asymptomatic pneumatosis cystoides, sometimes with high-concentration oxygen therapy to promote resorption of intramural gas","Identify and treat associated conditions such as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or connective tissue disease, and review culprit drugs","Serial clinical, biochemical (lactate, inflammatory markers) and imaging monitoring if deterioration occurs","Escalate to surgical and critical care teams when features of transmural injury emerge"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Yamada's Textbook of Gastroenterology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pneumococcal_disease":{"name":"Pneumococcal Disease","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Streptococcus pneumoniae infection"],"chapter":151,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1187-1195","clinicalPearls":["S. pneumoniae can infect nearly any tissue; the four most common syndromes are otitis media, pneumonia, bacteraemia, and meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1191).","There is no pathognomonic presentation of pneumococcal disease, and a microbiologically confirmed diagnosis is made in only a minority of cases, so empirical therapy covering S. pneumoniae is often indicated (Harrison 22e p.1191).","In the elderly, pneumococcal pneumonia may present non-specifically with confusion or malaise without fever or cough; a high index of suspicion is required because delayed treatment leads to rapid, severe deterioration (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Pneumococcal pneumonia can mimic an acute abdomen (upper abdominal pain from diaphragmatic pleural involvement); clinicians should remember to keep this non-abdominal process in the differential (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Now that H. influenzae type b vaccine is in routine childhood use, S. pneumoniae and Neisseria meningitidis are the most common bacterial causes of meningitis in both adults and children (Harrison 22e p.1193).","Pneumococcal meningitis can be a primary presentation or a complication of skull fracture, otitis media, mastoiditis, or bacteraemia; most cases arise from haematogenous spread rather than direct extension (Harrison 22e p.1191, 1193).","A positive urinary pneumococcal antigen test is useful for etiologic attribution in adults (low nasopharyngeal colonization) but is generally uninformative in children and in high-colonization low-income settings (Harrison 22e p.1192-1193).","Most pneumococcal pneumonia is non-bacteraemic; only ~5-20% of cases are accompanied by bacteraemia, and blood cultures are positive in fewer than 30% of cases (Harrison 22e p.1190, 1192).","Empyema is the most common focal complication of pneumococcal pneumonia (<5% of cases); pleural fluid with frank pus, visible bacteria, or pH <=7.1 indicates empyema and demands complete drainage (Harrison 22e p.1192)."],"pathophysiology":"Pneumococci are encapsulated gram-positive diplococci that colonise the human nasopharynx from an early age; colonisation is common while disease is rare. All pneumococcal infection ultimately stems from nasopharyngeal acquisition. The anti-phagocytic polysaccharide capsule is the key virulence determinant, resisting complement deposition in the absence of type-specific antibody. The organism dynamically modulates capsule expression: it downregulates capsule in the nasopharynx (rough colonies) to evade immune recognition and upregulates it on tissue invasion (smooth colonies). From the nasopharynx, organisms spread haematogenously to distant sites (brain, joints, bone, peritoneum) or locally to mucosal surfaces (otitis media, pneumonia). Intercurrent viral respiratory infection increases susceptibility by upregulating epithelial adhesion factors, permitting adhesion via surface adhesins (PsaA, PspA, CbpA/PspC, pneumolysin, neuraminidases). Disease pathology is driven by host inflammation: cell-wall teichoic acids and peptidoglycan induce IL-1, IL-6, and TNF and activate the alternative complement pathway, and pneumolysin drives proinflammatory cytokine release. Host defence depends on intact respiratory epithelium, the cough reflex and mucociliary escalator, splenic function, complement, neutrophils/macrophages, CRP binding of cell-wall phosphorylcholine, and TLR2/TLR4 recognition, together with acquired anticapsular IgG (Harrison 22e p.1190-1191).","additionalDifferentials":["Viral pneumonia","Mycoplasma pneumonia","Haemophilus influenzae pneumonia","Klebsiella pneumoniae pneumonia","Staphylococcus aureus pneumonia","Legionella pneumonia","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (in HIV-infected/immunocompromised hosts)","Myocardial infarction","Heart failure with atypical pulmonary oedema","Atelectasis","Cholecystitis, appendicitis, perforated peptic ulcer, or subphrenic abscess (when abdominal symptoms predominate)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral lower respiratory tract infection / viral pneumonia","Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection","Legionella infection","Pneumonia due to H. influenzae, K. pneumoniae, or S. aureus","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia in immunocompromised hosts","Other bacterial pyogenic meningitis (e.g., N. meningitidis), clinically indistinguishable from pneumococcal meningitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Myocardial infarction","Heart failure with atypical pulmonary oedema","Pulmonary atelectasis","Lung cancer (can produce round/spherical consolidation mimicking pneumococcal round pneumonia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography (lobar/segmental consolidation; >1 lobe in ~30%; infiltrate may be absent early or with dehydration)","Blood cultures (positive in <30% of pneumococcal pneumonia)","Gram's stain and culture of high-quality sputum (mainstay of adult diagnosis; yield depends on specimen quality and prior antibiotics)","Urinary pneumococcal antigen assay (cell-wall polysaccharide; serotype-specific quantitative assays for up to 24 serotypes emerging)","CSF examination for meningitis: turbidity, elevated protein, elevated WBC, reduced glucose, plus Gram's stain, culture, antigen testing, or PCR","Pleural fluid analysis (pus, microscopy, pH) when effusion present","Sterile aspiration/culture (and capsular antigen or PCR) of fluid from the infected site for other invasive syndromes; lung tissue pathology is the gold standard for pneumonia"],"redFlags":["Asplenia or splenic dysfunction (e.g., sickle cell disease) confers risk of overwhelming, fulminant pneumococcal sepsis","Leukopenia in pneumococcal pneumonia (<10% of cases) is a poor prognostic sign associated with fatal outcome","Hypotension and signs of severe pneumonia/sepsis at presentation predict higher case-fatality","Meningitis features: altered consciousness, seizures, stiff neck, signs of raised intracranial pressure (bradycardia, hypertension), cranial nerve palsies","In children: inability to drink, convulsions, lethargy, or severe malnutrition signify very severe disease warranting immediate antibiotics and referral","Pleural effusion with persistent fever/leukocytosis after 4-5 days of appropriate therapy suggests empyema"],"prognosis":"Case-fatality varies with age, comorbidity, disease severity at presentation, and access to care. For pneumococcal pneumonia, CFR ranges from <5% in hospitalised patients aged 18-44 to >12% in those over 65, even with timely management; severity at presentation (rather than the presence of bacteraemia) drives mortality, and early deaths reflect a rapidly progressive cascade that often unfolds irrespective of antibiotic administration. Pneumococcal meningitis carries a mortality of ~20%, and up to 50% of survivors have acute or chronic sequelae (deafness, hydrocephalus, and cognitive impairment in children; diffuse brain swelling, subarachnoid bleeding, hydrocephalus, cerebrovascular complications, and hearing loss in adults) (Harrison 22e p.1190, 1193).","pitfalls":["Treating an apparent acute abdomen while overlooking pneumococcal lower-lobe pneumonia causing referred abdominal pain (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Relying on a normal early chest radiograph to exclude pneumonia; an infiltrate may be absent early or with dehydration and appear after rehydration (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Over-interpreting a positive urinary pneumococcal antigen in children or in high-colonization settings, where it does not reliably attribute etiology (Harrison 22e p.1192-1193).","Diagnosing otitis media from tympanic-membrane redness alone, which is insufficient (Harrison 22e p.1193).","Attributing round/spherical consolidation solely to pneumococcus without considering other causes, especially cancer (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Assuming PPSV23 reliably prevents non-bacteraemic pneumococcal pneumonia, particularly in the elderly and immunodeficient, where effectiveness is reduced and protection wanes after ~5 years (Harrison 22e p.1194)."],"management":["Prompt empirical antibiotics active against S. pneumoniae, typically a beta-lactam (penicillin or a third-generation cephalosporin), guided by syndrome and local resistance","Add vancomycin to a cephalosporin for suspected pneumococcal meningitis pending susceptibility, with adjunctive dexamethasone to reduce neurological sequelae","De-escalate and tailor therapy once the isolate's susceptibility is known","Supportive care with oxygen, fluid resuscitation and antipyretics, plus drainage of an empyema or infected effusion where present","Source-specific measures such as myringotomy for otitis media or surgical drainage of a suppurative focus","Prevention with pneumococcal conjugate and polysaccharide vaccination of at-risk groups and penicillin prophylaxis in asplenia","Escalate to critical care for severe sepsis, respiratory failure or deteriorating meningitis"],"severityClassification":"Pneumococcal syndromes are classified as invasive (a normally sterile site is infected or bacteraemia is present, e.g., bacteraemic pneumonia, meningitis, bacteraemia) versus noninvasive (contiguous spread, e.g., otitis media, sinusitis, bronchitis); whether non-bacteraemic pneumonia counts as invasive is debated. In children, WHO IMCI algorithms stratify illness by danger signs (very severe disease: inability to drink, convulsions, lethargy, severe malnutrition) and by cough with tachypnoea plus lower chest-wall indrawing to guide antibiotics and referral; no equivalent validated algorithm exists for adults (Harrison 22e p.1191).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1187-1195"],"crossLinks":["CAP","MENINGITIS"]},"pneumoconiosis":{"name":"Pneumoconiosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["occupational lung disease","coal workers' pneumoconiosis","CWP","dust lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of parenchymal lung diseases caused by chronic inhalation of inorganic mineral dusts, most often occupational (coal, silica, asbestos, beryllium)","A detailed occupational and exposure history, including duration and intensity of dust contact and latency, is essential to diagnosis","Many patients are asymptomatic early, with disease detected on surveillance chest imaging before dyspnoea and cough develop","Chest radiography and CT typically show small rounded or irregular opacities, with characteristic patterns and distributions depending on the causative dust","Coal workers' pneumoconiosis ranges from simple disease with small nodules to progressive massive fibrosis with large conglomerate masses","There is no specific treatment to reverse the fibrosis; management centres on removal from exposure, supportive care, and prevention of complications","Asbestos exposure additionally raises the risk of pleural plaques, mesothelioma, and bronchogenic carcinoma, warranting long-term vigilance","Prevention through dust control, respiratory protection, and workplace exposure limits is the most effective strategy"],"pathophysiology":"Inhaled mineral dust particles that reach the distal airways and alveoli are engulfed by alveolar macrophages. Depending on particle size, surface reactivity, and burden, the macrophages are activated or killed, releasing inflammatory mediators, cytokines, and reactive oxygen species. Sustained exposure drives chronic inflammation and fibroblast activation, leading to collagen deposition, nodule formation, and, in advanced disease, coalescence into large fibrotic masses that distort lung architecture and impair gas exchange. The specific pattern reflects the fibrogenic potential of the particular dust.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and other interstitial lung diseases","Sarcoidosis","Pulmonary tuberculosis (which may also complicate pneumoconiosis)","Metastatic or miliary pulmonary nodular disease","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Pulmonary manifestations of connective tissue disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography, ideally read against standardised classification of pneumoconiosis opacities","High-resolution CT to characterise nodularity, fibrosis, and pleural disease","Pulmonary function testing to quantify restrictive, obstructive, or mixed impairment and gas transfer","Detailed occupational and environmental exposure documentation","Screening for complicating tuberculosis or, with asbestos, for malignancy where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging masses or new nodules suggesting progressive massive fibrosis or malignancy","Constitutional symptoms or haemoptysis raising concern for tuberculosis or cancer","Rapid decline in lung function or new hypoxaemia","Pleural effusion or thickening in an asbestos-exposed patient suggesting mesothelioma"],"prognosis":"Simple pneumoconiosis may remain stable and cause little functional impairment, whereas progressive massive fibrosis and asbestos-related disease can lead to progressive respiratory failure; asbestos exposure carries a long-term risk of mesothelioma and lung cancer, and outcomes improve when further exposure is halted early.","pitfalls":["Failing to take an adequate occupational history and therefore missing the diagnosis","Attributing all radiographic change to dust without excluding superimposed tuberculosis or malignancy","Assuming a short exposure excludes disease, given the often long latency","Overlooking the need to remove the worker from ongoing exposure"],"management":["Remove the patient from further dust exposure, the single most important intervention, with workplace dust control and respiratory protection","Smoking cessation support given the amplified risk of functional impairment and malignancy","Supportive care with bronchodilators for coexisting airflow obstruction, pulmonary rehabilitation and long-term oxygen for chronic respiratory failure","Influenza and pneumococcal vaccination and prompt treatment of respiratory infections","Surveillance for and treatment of complicating tuberculosis, and vigilance for mesothelioma and lung cancer in asbestos-exposed patients","Serial chest imaging and pulmonary function testing to monitor progression","Occupational health involvement with compensation advice, and consideration of lung transplantation in end-stage disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"pneumocystis_pneumonia":{"name":"Pneumocystis Pneumonia (PCP)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCP","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","PJP"],"chapter":227,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1730-1734","clinicalPearls":["Caused by the fungus Pneumocystis jirovecii (formerly P. carinii); the organism cannot be cultured and is host-specific to humans, infecting only via other humans (Harrison 22e p.1730-1731).","An opportunistic pathogen of immunocompromised hosts: HIV infection, organ transplantation, haematologic malignancy, and those on high-dose glucocorticoids or certain immunosuppressive monoclonal antibodies (Harrison 22e p.1730).","Among people with HIV, PCP incidence is inversely related to CD4+ T-cell count: at least 80% of cases occur at counts <200/uL and most actually develop at counts <100/uL (Harrison 22e p.1731-1732).","Classic presentation is acute or subacute pneumonia, often beginning with a vague sense of dyspnoea alone, then progressing to fever, nonproductive cough, and progressive shortness of breath (Harrison 22e p.1731).","Decreased oxygen saturation at rest or with exertion is characteristic; untreated it progresses to severe hypoxaemia (Harrison 22e p.1731).","Serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is often elevated from pulmonary damage (Harrison 22e p.1731).","Classic chest radiograph shows diffuse bilateral interstitial infiltrates that are perihilar and symmetric, which can progress to alveolar filling (Harrison 22e p.1731).","High-resolution chest CT shows diffuse ground-glass opacities in virtually all PCP patients, often before the plain chest radiograph becomes abnormal (Harrison 22e p.1731).","Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) is the most common sampling method; demonstration of organisms in BAL fluid is almost 100% sensitive and specific for PCP (Harrison 22e p.1733).","Since the organism cannot be cultured, diagnosis relies on staining of cyst or trophozoite forms in lung tissue/secretions with methenamine silver, toluidine blue O, Giemsa, or specific immunofluorescent antibody (Harrison 22e p.1733).","An induced sputum sample, when obtained and interpreted by an experienced provider, can be highly sensitive and specific, but sensitivity is widely variable (55-90%); expectorated sputum and throat swabs have very low sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.1733).","Adjunctive glucocorticoids improve survival in moderate-to-severe disease (initial room-air PaO2 <70 mmHg or alveolar-arterial gradient >=35 mmHg) by reducing the inflammation that follows organism die-off once specific therapy begins; they should be started when PCP therapy is initiated, even if the diagnosis is only suspected (Harrison 22e p.1734).","Prophylaxis is the cornerstone of prevention; for HIV, CD4+ counts <200/uL (or CD4 percentage <15) are an indication to start or continue chemoprophylaxis, alongside prior PCP, unexplained prolonged fever, or recent oropharyngeal candidiasis (Harrison 22e p.1734).","PCP now occurs more often from non-HIV-related immunosuppression than from HIV infection in many health care systems (Harrison 22e p.1731)."],"pathophysiology":"Pneumocystis jirovecii is a fungus with unique tropism for the lung. Organisms are inhaled into the alveolar space, presumably after being exhaled by another human; most people are exposed and infected early in life. Disease may arise from primary infection, reinfection, or reactivation of latent infection. Clinically apparent pneumonia occurs only in immunocompromised hosts, where defects in cellular and/or humoral immunity (congenital, HIV-related, or drug-induced) permit proliferation. CD4+ T cells are critical for host defence. The organism proliferates in the lung provoking a mononuclear cell response; alveoli fill with proteinaceous material and alveolar-capillary injury with surfactant abnormalities develops. Stained sections show foamy, vacuolated alveolar exudates of viable and nonviable organisms, with interstitial oedema and fibrosis. Much of the clinical deterioration after starting specific therapy is attributed to enhanced inflammation from dying organisms, the rationale for adjunctive glucocorticoids (Harrison 22e p.1731-1734).","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial pneumonia (including community-acquired pneumonia)","Pulmonary tuberculosis (especially with upper-lobe cavitary pattern seen after aerosolized pentamidine prophylaxis)","Cytomegalovirus and other viral pneumonias in the immunocompromised host","Other invasive fungal pneumonias (e.g., Aspergillus)","Congestive heart failure","Pulmonary embolism","Pulmonary hypertension","Drug toxicity","Neoplastic process involving the lung","Lymphoid interstitial pneumonitis (LIP) and nonspecific interstitial pneumonitis (NIP) in HIV"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial pneumonia","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Cytomegalovirus pneumonia","Invasive aspergillosis / other fungal pneumonia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Congestive heart failure","Pulmonary embolism","Pulmonary hypertension","Drug-induced pulmonary toxicity","Pulmonary neoplasm","Lymphoid interstitial pneumonitis (LIP) / nonspecific interstitial pneumonitis (NIP)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Pulse oximetry / oxygen saturation at rest and with exertion, and arterial blood gases (hypoxaemia with widened alveolar-arterial gradient; used for staging severity and directing treatment)","Serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) - often elevated but neither sensitive nor specific","High-resolution chest CT (diffuse ground-glass opacities; a normal chest CT essentially rules out PCP)","Bronchoalveolar lavage with methenamine silver, toluidine blue O, Giemsa, or immunofluorescent staining","Induced sputum examination (experienced-provider dependent)","PCR of respiratory specimens (more useful for ruling out PCP when negative than for confirming it when positive; cannot distinguish colonization from disease and is not standardized)","Serum (1->3)-beta-D-glucan (frequently elevated but neither perfectly sensitive nor highly specific)","Transbronchial or open-lung biopsy when less invasive sampling is non-diagnostic"],"redFlags":["PaO2 <70 mmHg on room air (or alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient >=35 mmHg) marking moderate-to-severe disease that warrants adjunctive glucocorticoids","Profound or progressive hypoxaemia / progression to respiratory failure","Development of pneumothorax (pneumatoceles and pneumothoraces are characteristic, especially in HIV) - associated with higher mortality","Need for mechanical ventilation","Distributive shock due to PCP (rare)","Clinical deterioration after 3-4 days of therapy, or failure to improve by 7-10 days, prompting reevaluation for other or coexisting processes"],"prognosis":"Untreated, PCP is invariably fatal. HIV-associated disease is often indolent, presenting early as mild exercise intolerance or chest tightness without fever or cough and a normal or near-normal chest radiograph, then progressing over days to months to fever, cough, diffuse alveolar infiltrates, and profound hypoxaemia; non-HIV immunosuppressed patients more often have acute disease progressing over days to respiratory failure. Mortality is influenced by age, degree of immunosuppression, preexisting lung disease, need for mechanical ventilation, and development of pneumothorax. With advances in critical care, prognosis for those requiring intubation has improved and depends largely on comorbidities and the underlying disease (Harrison 22e p.1733).","pitfalls":["A normal LDH does not rule out PCP, and an elevated LDH is not specific for PCP (Harrison 22e p.1731).","PCP can occur at CD4+ counts >200/uL in HIV (uncommon, especially at counts well above 200), and in non-HIV immunosuppression a CD4+ count >200/uL does not imply protection (Harrison 22e p.1731).","PCR is so sensitive that it cannot reliably distinguish colonization from true pneumonia; there is no validated PCR threshold, so a positive result does not definitively attribute disease to Pneumocystis (Harrison 22e p.1733).","Serum/BAL (1->3)-beta-D-glucan and serum PCR are not sufficiently sensitive or specific to establish or exclude PCP (Harrison 22e p.1733).","If treated with specific therapy but without corticosteroids, patients of any severity often deteriorate during the first few days from inflammation induced by dying organisms (Harrison 22e p.1733).","Initial chest radiograph may be normal in mild disease, and the classic dense perihilar infiltrate is unusual in AIDS; relying on plain film can delay diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1730-1731).","Extrapulmonary pneumocystosis is rare and nonspecific; when present, the priority is to look for another concurrent infectious or neoplastic process (Harrison 22e p.1733)."],"management":["First-line therapy is high-dose co-trimoxazole for confirmed or strongly suspected infection","Adjunctive corticosteroids for moderate-to-severe disease with significant hypoxaemia to reduce respiratory deterioration and mortality","Alternative regimens for sulfa intolerance or treatment failure, such as clindamycin with primaquine, intravenous pentamidine, or atovaquone for milder disease","Supportive care with supplemental oxygen and ventilatory support for respiratory failure","Address the underlying immunosuppression with antiretroviral therapy in HIV and review of immunosuppressive or glucocorticoid regimens","Secondary prophylaxis with co-trimoxazole after treatment until immune reconstitution, and primary prophylaxis in at-risk immunocompromised hosts","Escalate to critical care for progressive hypoxaemia, and adjust immunosuppression in transplant recipients"],"severityClassification":"Severity is staged largely by oxygenation. Moderate-to-severe disease is defined by an initial room-air PaO2 <70 mmHg or an alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient >=35 mmHg; this threshold identifies patients (with HIV, and probably other immunodeficiencies) who benefit from adjunctive glucocorticoids in addition to specific antimicrobial therapy. Arterial blood-gas measurement both aids diagnosis and directs treatment by staging severity (Harrison 22e p.1731, 1734).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1730-1734 (Ch.227, Pneumocystis Infections)","Harrison 22e p.1730 (Ch.208, HIV/AIDS clinical manifestations - PCP)"],"crossLinks":[]},"pneumopericardium":{"name":"Pneumopericardium","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["air in the pericardium","pneumo-pericardium","hydropneumopericardium (with fluid)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The presence of air or gas within the pericardial sac, ranging from an incidental finding to a life-threatening tension collection","Causes include chest trauma, mechanical ventilation and barotrauma (especially in neonates), thoracic or cardiac surgery and other procedures, and fistulous communication with an air-containing viscus or infection with gas-forming organisms","A characteristic auscultatory sign is a metallic or churning sound synchronous with the heartbeat (the bruit de moulin or mill-wheel murmur)","The chest radiograph shows air outlining the heart bordered by the pericardium, and cardiac CT confirms the diagnosis and its extent","Tension pneumopericardium is an emergency in which trapped air under pressure compresses the heart and causes tamponade physiology","Small collections from a self-limiting cause may be managed conservatively with observation and treatment of the underlying process","Tension physiology requires urgent decompression by pericardiocentesis or a pericardial window, together with correction of the source, such as adjusting ventilation or repairing a fistula"],"pathophysiology":"Pneumopericardium develops when air gains access to the pericardial space, either directly through a breach of the pericardium from trauma or surgery, by tracking along tissue planes from alveolar rupture during barotrauma, through a fistula linking the pericardium to an air-containing structure such as the oesophagus or a bronchus, or from gas produced by infection with gas-forming organisms. If the air can escape freely the haemodynamic effect is limited, but when a valve-like mechanism allows air to enter but not leave, intrapericardial pressure rises to compress the cardiac chambers, impairing filling and reproducing the physiology of tamponade with falling output.","additionalDifferentials":["Pneumomediastinum","Pneumothorax, particularly on the medial aspect","Pericardial effusion or haemopericardium","Cardiac tamponade from fluid","Diaphragmatic hernia with bowel adjacent to the heart","Purulent pericarditis with gas-forming organisms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography showing air surrounding the heart within the pericardium","Cardiac CT to confirm and quantify the pericardial air and identify a source or fistula","Echocardiography to assess haemodynamic effect, though air can limit windows","Evaluation for a communicating fistula, such as contrast studies or endoscopy when suspected","Assessment of ventilation and airway pressures in ventilated or neonatal patients"],"redFlags":["Haemodynamic instability from tension pneumopericardium requiring emergency decompression","Rapidly enlarging pericardial air on serial imaging","A demonstrable fistula between the pericardium and airway or gastrointestinal tract","Gas-forming infection producing purulent pneumopericardium"],"prognosis":"Small, non-tension collections from a self-limiting cause often resolve with treatment of the underlying process, whereas tension pneumopericardium is rapidly fatal without prompt decompression, and the overall outlook reflects the precipitating condition.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise tension physiology and delaying decompression","Mistaking pneumopericardium for pneumomediastinum or a medial pneumothorax on the radiograph","Decompressing the air without identifying and correcting the source, such as a fistula","Overlooking barotrauma from ventilator settings as the driver in neonates and ventilated patients"],"management":["Emergency pericardial decompression for tension pneumopericardium with tamponade physiology","Treat the underlying cause, whether repair of a traumatic or surgical pericardial breach, closure of an oesophageal or bronchial fistula, or antimicrobials for gas-forming infection","In ventilated or neonatal patients, minimise barotrauma with lung-protective ventilation and reduced airway pressures","Conservative observation for small, non-tension collections from a self-limiting cause while treating the precipitating process","Serial echocardiography and monitoring for haemodynamic compromise and re-accumulation","Cardiothoracic surgical referral for a persistent fistula or when definitive repair is required","Escalate urgently at any sign of rising intrapericardial pressure or falling cardiac output"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of pericardial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"poikiloderma_of_civatte":{"name":"Poikiloderma of Civatte","system":"Dermatology / Pigmentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["poikiloderma of Civatte","poikiloderma reticulare","Civatte poikiloderma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Reticulate red-brown pigmentation with telangiectasia and fine atrophy over the sides of the neck, the cheeks and the upper chest","Characteristically spares the shaded skin under the chin (the submental shadow), underlining its photo-aggravated nature","Chronic cumulative sun exposure is the principal driver, with photosensitising fragrances and cosmetics thought to contribute","More common in fair-skinned, middle-aged to elderly individuals, particularly women","It combines a vascular (telangiectatic erythema) and a pigmentary component, so treatment often needs both vascular and pigment-directed laser or intense pulsed light","Photoprotection and avoiding perfumed products on the area help limit progression","The term poikiloderma denotes the triad of atrophy, telangiectasia and mottled pigmentation"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic ultraviolet exposure of the lateral neck and upper chest produces dermal elastosis, dilatation of superficial blood vessels (telangiectasia) and irregular melanin distribution together with epidermal atrophy, giving the poikilodermatous appearance. Photosensitising ingredients in fragrances and cosmetics are believed to augment the ultraviolet damage. The area beneath the chin is shielded from direct sunlight and is therefore characteristically spared.","additionalDifferentials":["Connective tissue disease-associated poikiloderma (e.g. dermatomyositis)","Rosacea","Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation","Melasma","Riehl melanosis (pigmented contact dermatitis)","Radiation-induced poikiloderma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination noting sparing of the submental shadow","Dermoscopy to assess the vascular and pigmentary components","Detailed sun-exposure and cosmetic history","Patch or photopatch testing where a photoallergy is suspected","Skin biopsy if a connective tissue disease such as dermatomyositis is being considered"],"redFlags":["Poikiloderma accompanied by muscle weakness or other features suggesting dermatomyositis","An atypical distribution not corresponding to sun-exposed sites","Rapidly evolving changes prompting exclusion of a systemic disorder"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign, chronic and mainly of cosmetic importance; laser or intense pulsed light can markedly improve appearance, but it tends to progress without diligent photoprotection.","pitfalls":["Overlooking poikiloderma associated with dermatomyositis or another connective tissue disease","Treating only the pigment and ignoring the vascular telangiectatic component","Failing to address ongoing sun exposure and fragrance contact","Confusing it with melasma or rosacea"],"management":["Rigorous daily broad-spectrum photoprotection and sun avoidance to limit progression, the cornerstone of management","Avoid perfumed and photosensitising cosmetics applied to the neck and chest","Vascular-selective laser or intense pulsed light to target the telangiectatic erythema component","Pigment-directed laser or intense pulsed light to address the dyspigmentation","Realistic counselling that the condition is benign and largely cosmetic, with recurrence likely without sustained photoprotection","Dermatology referral for device-based treatment and to exclude a connective tissue disease mimic such as dermatomyositis where features are atypical"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"poisoning_and_drug_overdose":{"name":"Poisoning and Drug Overdose","system":"Toxicology / Multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["xenobiotic poisoning","drug overdose","intoxication","toxic ingestion","acute poisoning"],"chapter":470,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3703-3708","clinicalPearls":["Poisoning severity is dose-related, but individual responses to a given dose vary with genetic polymorphisms, enzyme induction or inhibition from co-ingested xenobiotics, and acquired tolerance; the functional reserve of the target organ (influenced by age and preexisting disease) also shapes severity and reversibility.","Diagnosis of an unknown poisoning rests on pattern recognition: first characterize the overall physiologic state as stimulated, depressed, discordant, or normal, then look for a toxidrome and finally for agent-specific clues.","Core temperature must be measured and reassessed frequently even in combative patients, because temperature elevation is the single most reliable predictor of poor outcome in stimulant poisoning (e.g., cocaine) and in withdrawal states (e.g., alcohol, GHB).","Mydriasis is characteristic of all stimulant states but is most marked in anticholinergic poisoning because pupillary reactivity is muscarinic; sympathomimetic poisoning also dilates pupils but some light reactivity persists, whereas the anticholinergic toxidrome adds hot, dry, flushed skin, decreased bowel sounds, and urinary retention.","Rotatory nystagmus points specifically to phencyclidine or ketamine, the only physiologic stimulants that produce this sign.","Miosis is most pronounced in opioid and cholinergic poisoning; cholinergic poisoning is distinguished by accompanying muscarinic and nicotinic features.","A normal exam can be deceptive: 'toxic time-bombs' (slowly absorbed, slowly distributed, requiring metabolic activation, or disrupting metabolism) and the proliferation of once-daily extended-release formulations mean delayed, severe toxicity is increasingly common.","A rapid response to a diagnostic antidote can confirm the cause: improvement within minutes of IV dextrose, naloxone, or flumazenil is essentially diagnostic of hypoglycemia, opioid poisoning, and benzodiazepine intoxication respectively.","Qualitative urine drug-of-abuse screens are screening tests only: false positives and negatives are common (e.g., fluoroquinolones cause false-positive opiate screens), and newer agents such as synthetic cannabinoids (spice), cathinones (bath salts), and kratom are not detected at all.","Symptomatic patients (e.g., apnea from opioids) should be managed on the basis of history, examination, and the observed toxidrome without waiting for laboratory confirmation.","Effects after overdose typically begin sooner, peak later, and last longer than after a therapeutic dose, and the toxicokinetic profile differs from the published therapeutic pharmacokinetics in standard references.","Most patients who are asymptomatic 6 h after ingestion are unlikely to develop later toxicity and can be discharged safely, but longer observation is mandatory after suspected toxic time-bomb ingestions."],"pathophysiology":"Poisoning is the development of dose-related adverse effects from exposure to chemicals, drugs, or other xenobiotics. Effects may be local (skin, eyes, lungs) or systemic depending on the route of exposure and the chemical's physical properties and mechanism of action. Toxidromes reflect the net effect on autonomic and neuromuscular function: stimulated states arise from sympathetic, anticholinergic, or hallucinogen activity or from drug withdrawal; depressed states from functional sympatholytics, cholinergic agents, opioids, and sedative-hypnotic GABAergic agents; discordant states from asphyxiants, CNS syndromes, membrane-active agents, and anion-gap metabolic acidosis inducers. In opioid and sedative-hypnotic poisoning, vital-sign derangement is secondary to depression of central cardiovascular and respiratory centers (or resulting hypoxemia) and is seen only with marked depression of consciousness, whereas direct/peripheral sympatholytics can cause prominent cardiovascular depression without comparable CNS depression. Some agents (acetaminophen, ethylene glycol, methanol) owe their toxicity to metabolites more toxic than the parent compound.","additionalDifferentials":["Structural CNS lesion (focal neurologic findings are uncommon in poisoning and should prompt imaging)","Sepsis or other infection causing altered mental status","Primary psychiatric illness or psychogenic illness (fear of poisoning, mass hysteria)","Metabolic encephalopathy (hepatic, renal, or respiratory failure)","Hypoglycemia from non-toxic causes","Trauma and occult head injury"],"infectionMimics":["Sepsis with shock and multiorgan dysfunction (hypoxemia/shock from severe poisoning can mimic, and secondary aspiration pneumonia and sepsis can complicate poisoning)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Psychogenic illness and anxiety reactions, which can produce mild physiologic stimulation indistinguishable from a toxic cause without observation or ancillary testing","Primary cardiac arrhythmia or conduction disease","Endocrine emergencies (e.g., thyroid storm mimicking a sympathomimetic toxidrome)","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome and serotonin syndrome (CNS syndromes that may overlap with stimulant toxidromes)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Anion-gap calculation (elevated AGMA in advanced methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylate poisoning, and any poisoning causing organ failure, seizures, or shock; abnormally low anion gap suggests bromide, calcium, iodine, lithium, or magnesium)","Serum lactate (typically low relative to anion gap in toxic-alcohol/salicylate poisoning, high and near-equal to the gap in hypoperfusion)","Osmolal gap (>10 mmol/L difference suggests a low-molecular-weight solute such as a toxic alcohol or glycol)","Serum glucose and ketones","Electrolytes including potassium and calcium","ECG (bradycardia/AV block, QRS and QT prolongation from membrane-active agents and hyperkalemia, ventricular tachyarrhythmias)","Quantitative serum levels for acetaminophen, alcohols (including ethylene glycol and methanol), anticonvulsants, barbiturates, digoxin, heavy metals, iron, lithium, salicylate, theophylline, carboxyhemoglobin, and methemoglobin","Qualitative urine drug screen (most useful when toxicity is severe or unexplained, e.g., coma, seizures, cardiovascular instability, acidosis, nonsinus rhythms)","Radiography/abdominal CT (radiopaque densities suggest chloral hydrate, chlorinated hydrocarbons, heavy metals, drug packets, iodinated compounds, potassium salts, enteric-coated tablets, or salicylates; chest x-ray for pulmonary edema, metal fume fever, elemental mercury)","Review of the state Prescription Monitoring Program for controlled-substance history","PT/INR (useful for risk stratification in warfarin/rodenticide poisoning, but unreliable for direct oral anticoagulant overdose)"],"redFlags":["Coma or progressively decreasing level of consciousness","Respiratory depression, apnea, or hypoxemia","Hypotension or cardiovascular collapse","Cardiac conduction abnormalities or arrhythmias (QRS/QT prolongation, ventricular tachyarrhythmias)","Seizures","Hyperthermia or hypothermia (temperature elevation is the most reliable predictor of poor outcome in stimulant/withdrawal states)","Suspected ingestion of a toxic time-bomb or an agent with toxic metabolites (acetaminophen, ethylene glycol, methanol)","Body packing or body stuffing (concealment of illicit drug packets)","Intentional (suicidal) overdose requiring continuous observation and self-injury precautions"],"prognosis":"Overall mortality is low at less than 1% of all poisoning exposures, with most being minor or non-toxic and involving children under 6 years. Mortality is substantially higher (1-2%) among hospitalized patients with intentional (suicidal) overdose or complications from drugs of abuse, who account for most serious poisonings. Acetaminophen is the pharmaceutical most often implicated in fatal poisoning. Carbon monoxide is the overall leading cause of poisoning death, although it is underrepresented in hospital and poison-center statistics because such patients are typically dead when found and referred directly to medical examiners. Drug overdose deaths have risen steadily since 1999 (over 106,600 U.S. deaths in 2021; age-adjusted rate 32.4 per 100,000), driven largely by synthetic opioids such as fentanyl and emerging agents such as xylazine. Once the offending agent is eliminated, maintaining tissue perfusion is critical to complete recovery.","pitfalls":["Relying on the gag reflex as an indicator of the need for intubation; it is not reliable, and a patient may protect the airway while stimulated but not when left alone.","Treating a negative qualitative drug screen as exclusion of poisoning; the agent may be undetectable by the assay or present below the limit of detection, and repeat testing later may turn positive.","Treating a positive qualitative screen as diagnostic or forensic; cross-reacting drugs cause false positives and confirmatory GC/MS may take weeks.","Assuming blood levels correlate with toxicity during absorption/distribution (levels can exceed tissue concentrations) or during resolution (levels fall below tissue concentrations); rebound increases (e.g., lithium) can occur after extracorporeal removal stops.","Discharging too early after ingestion of a toxic time-bomb (slow-absorption, slow-distribution, metabolic-activation, or metabolism-disrupting agents) on the basis of an initially benign presentation.","Failing to consider all medications available in the home (including pet medications) when the history is unclear in a suspected poisoning.","Overlooking intentional self-harm: failing to maintain continuous observation and self-injury precautions in patients who have attempted suicide.","Using class IA, IC, or III antiarrhythmics, or beta blockers driven by sympathetic hyperactivity, for toxin-induced ventricular arrhythmias; sodium bicarbonate is the preferred first agent for membrane-active toxicity."],"management":["Resuscitation and stabilisation first, securing airway, breathing and circulation and correcting hypoxia, hypotension, seizures and hyperthermia","Empirical antidotes directed by the toxidrome, such as naloxone for opioid toxicity and glucose, thiamine and benzodiazepines as indicated","Agent-specific antidotes once the poison is identified, such as N-acetylcysteine for paracetamol and fomepizole or ethanol for toxic alcohols","Decontamination where appropriate, with activated charcoal for suitable recent ingestions and skin or eye irrigation for external exposure","Enhanced elimination for selected poisons, including urinary alkalinisation for salicylates and haemodialysis for dialysable toxins such as toxic alcohols and lithium","Continuous monitoring of core temperature, cardiac rhythm and acid-base and metabolic status, correcting derangements as they arise","Involve the poisons information service and clinical toxicology, and arrange psychiatric assessment after intentional overdose","Escalate to critical care for airway compromise, refractory instability or severe metabolic disturbance"],"severityClassification":"Harrison grades physiologic stimulation and depression on a 1-4 scale. Stimulation: Grade 1 - anxious, irritable, tremulous with normal vital signs; Grade 2 - agitated, possible confusion/hallucinations but still able to converse and follow commands, mildly-to-moderately elevated vital signs; Grade 3 - delirious, unintelligible speech, uncontrollable motor hyperactivity, markedly raised vital signs, possible tachyarrhythmias; Grade 4 - coma, seizures, cardiovascular collapse. Depression: Grade 1 - lethargic/sleeping but arousable, able to converse; Grade 2 - responds to pain not voice, brainstem reflexes intact; Grade 3 - unresponsive to pain, depressed brainstem reflexes, decreased tone/respiration/temperature; Grade 4 - flaccid paralysis, absent brainstem reflexes and respirations, decreased cardiovascular vital signs.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3703-3708"],"crossLinks":[]},"pol3_related_leukodystrophy_4h":{"name":"POLR3-related (4H) leukodystrophy","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["4H leukodystrophy","POLR3A leukodystrophy","POLR3B leukodystrophy","POLR1C leukodystrophy","hypomyelination with hypodontia and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive hypomyelinating leukodystrophy caused by variants in subunits of RNA polymerase III, most often POLR3A or POLR3B, and less commonly POLR1C","The classic clinical triad giving the name 4H is Hypomyelination, Hypodontia and Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, though not every child shows all three","Neurological features include cerebellar signs with ataxia and intention tremor, a tremor-ataxia presentation, spasticity, and often myopia and abnormal eye movements","Dental clues are valuable: delayed or abnormal dentition, missing teeth, an abnormal eruption sequence and occasionally natal teeth","The MRI pattern combines diffuse hypomyelination with cerebellar atrophy and relative T2 hypointensity (preservation) of specific structures such as the optic radiations, the anterolateral thalamus, the globus pallidus, the dentate nucleus and the corticospinal tracts within the posterior limb of the internal capsule","Endocrine involvement causes delayed, incomplete or absent puberty from hypogonadotropic hypogonadism and warrants specialist assessment","Diagnosis follows MRI pattern recognition and characteristic dental and endocrine findings, confirmed by POLR3A/POLR3B/POLR1C sequencing; management is supportive with hormone replacement and multidisciplinary rehabilitation"],"pathophysiology":"POLR3-related leukodystrophy results from biallelic variants in genes encoding subunits of RNA polymerase III, the enzyme that transcribes small non-coding RNAs such as transfer RNAs and 5S ribosomal RNA. Impaired polymerase III activity is thought to reduce the supply of these RNAs needed for efficient protein synthesis, disproportionately affecting cells with high biosynthetic demands, including oligodendrocytes during myelination. The consequent failure of adequate myelin deposition produces hypomyelination, while involvement of other tissues explains the dental, endocrine and ocular manifestations that accompany the neurological picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease and Pelizaeus-Merzbacher-like disease","TUBB4A-related hypomyelinating leukodystrophy and H-ABC","Hypomyelination with congenital cataract (FAM126A)","Other RNA polymerase-related and tRNA-processing hypomyelinating disorders","Fucosidosis and other storage disorders with hypomyelination","Congenital cerebellar ataxias with white matter change"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to identify hypomyelination with cerebellar atrophy and the pattern of selectively preserved T2-hypointense structures","Sequencing of POLR3A, POLR3B and POLR1C, with panel or exome analysis if targeted testing is negative","Dental and orthopantomogram assessment to document hypodontia and abnormal dentition","Endocrine evaluation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis and pubertal status","Ophthalmological review for myopia and eye-movement abnormalities","Growth monitoring and assessment for short stature"],"redFlags":["Failure of pubertal progression requiring endocrine intervention","Rapid neurological deterioration inconsistent with the usually slow course, prompting reassessment","Dysphagia with aspiration risk as bulbar involvement advances","Recurrent falls and injury from worsening ataxia and spasticity"],"prognosis":"POLR3-related leukodystrophy is usually slowly progressive; many children develop skills before plateauing and then declining, with gait, coordination and speech becoming increasingly affected over years. Severity varies widely with genotype, from relatively mild adolescent-onset forms to more severe early-onset disease, and prognosis is influenced by the degree of motor and bulbar involvement.","pitfalls":["Not recognising the specific pattern of preserved T2-hypointense structures that distinguishes this from other hypomyelinating disorders","Overlooking dental and endocrine clues that strongly support the diagnosis","Failing to screen for and treat hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, affecting puberty and bone health","Assuming a static encephalopathy and not pursuing leukodystrophy genetics","Neglecting genetic counselling for autosomal recessive recurrence risk"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists; care is multidisciplinary and supportive, coordinated by neurology and clinical genetics","Hormone replacement for hypogonadotropic hypogonadism to induce and maintain puberty, with endocrine surveillance of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis","Dental assessment and management of hypodontia and abnormal dentition","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy with treatment of spasticity and ataxia, and mobility aids as disability progresses","Symptomatic and antiepileptic treatment as neurological features evolve, with speech and swallowing support","Ophthalmology review with correction of myopia and management of eye-movement abnormalities","Growth monitoring and nutritional support","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, with carrier testing and reproductive counselling for the family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":["hypomyelination_hypodontia_hypogonadism"]},"poland_syndrome":{"name":"Poland syndrome","system":"Genetics / Craniofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Poland anomaly","Poland sequence","unilateral pectoral aplasia-dysdactyly","Poland-Moebius syndrome (when combined)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by unilateral absence of the sternocostal head of the pectoralis major muscle, giving a flat or asymmetric anterior chest wall","Ipsilateral hand hypoplasia is typical, classically with shortened fingers and cutaneous syndactyly (symbrachydactyly)","The right side is affected more often than the left, and the condition is almost always unilateral","Associated findings include absence of the pectoralis minor, breast and nipple hypoplasia or absence, and rib deficiencies","It overlaps with Moebius syndrome, and the two may coexist, supporting a shared vascular disruption mechanism","In women, breast underdevelopment may be the presenting feature at puberty","Most cases are sporadic with normal intellect, and the primary concerns are functional hand use and chest wall protection"],"pathophysiology":"Poland syndrome is thought to arise from a vascular disruption sequence, most commonly attributed to reduced blood flow through the subclavian artery and its branches during the sixth week of gestation. Interruption or hypoplasia of this vessel is proposed to compromise the developing chest wall and limb bud on the affected side, producing the absent pectoral muscle, chest wall and breast deficiency, and hand malformation. The degree and precise territory of the vascular compromise determine the pattern and severity of the anomalies, explaining the frequent unilaterality and the overlap with Moebius syndrome when the vertebral or brainstem circulation is also involved. Cases are overwhelmingly sporadic.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated symbrachydactyly","Moebius syndrome (overlapping vascular disruption)","Sprengel deformity with chest wall involvement","Amniotic band sequence affecting the limb","Congenital chest wall deformities such as pectus anomalies","Klippel-Feil with associated limb defects"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest wall and thoracic imaging to define muscle, rib, and sternal anomalies","Hand radiographs to characterise skeletal hypoplasia and syndactyly","Echocardiography where cardiac or great vessel anomalies are suspected","Assessment for dextrocardia or dextroposition in left-sided cases","Renal ultrasound if additional anomalies suggest a broader disruption","Occupational therapy assessment of hand function"],"redFlags":["Significant rib deficiency leaving thoracic viscera inadequately protected","Paradoxical chest wall movement or respiratory compromise from extensive rib defects","Coexisting cardiac or great vessel anomaly","Features suggesting associated Moebius syndrome with feeding or airway risk","Marked functional hand impairment requiring early rehabilitation"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally good with normal life expectancy and cognition; management focuses on hand function, chest wall reconstruction where protection or appearance is a concern, and breast reconstruction in affected women.","pitfalls":["Regarding it as a purely cosmetic chest deformity and missing functional hand impairment","Overlooking rib deficiency that compromises thoracic protection or respiration","Failing to assess for coexisting Moebius syndrome","Not screening for cardiac anomalies, particularly with left-sided involvement","Neglecting the psychosocial impact, especially breast asymmetry in adolescence"],"management":["Reconstruction of the anterior chest wall where the absent pectoral muscle compromises protection, function or appearance, using latissimus dorsi transfer or a custom implant","Breast reconstruction in affected women for absent or hypoplastic breast, generally timed around or after puberty","Hand surgery for symbrachydactyly and cutaneous syndactyly to improve function, with occupational therapy and hand-function rehabilitation","Assess for and manage associated anomalies, including cardiac and dextroposition findings in left-sided cases","Psychological support and counselling regarding body image, particularly during adolescence","Multidisciplinary input from plastic and reconstructive, hand and paediatric surgery","Reassurance of generally normal life expectancy and cognition, with the condition almost always sporadic and unilateral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Gorlin's Syndromes of the Head and Neck"],"crossLinks":[]},"polg_related_disorder":{"name":"POLG-related disorder","system":"Metabolic / Mitochondrial & peroxisomal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["POLG spectrum disorder","Alpers-Huttenlocher syndrome","MNGIE-like POLG disease","Mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["POLG encodes the catalytic subunit of mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma; pathogenic variants impair mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) replication, producing mtDNA depletion in children and multiple mtDNA deletions in adults.","The phenotypic spectrum is a continuum spanning severe infantile Alpers-Huttenlocher syndrome, childhood myocerebrohepatopathy, the MEMSA/SCAE ataxia-epilepsy phenotypes, and adult-onset progressive external ophthalmoplegia (PEO) with or without sensory ataxic neuropathy.","The clinical triad of Alpers-Huttenlocher syndrome is intractable epilepsy (often focal with epilepsia partialis continua and occipital predilection), psychomotor regression, and hepatic failure that may be precipitated or accelerated by sodium valproate.","Sodium valproate is contraindicated in POLG disease because it can trigger fulminant, frequently fatal hepatic failure; POLG testing is advised before valproate in any child with unexplained epilepsy plus liver or mitochondrial features.","Occipital-predominant seizures, cortical visual involvement, stroke-like occipital lesions and status epilepticus are characteristic of the epileptic POLG phenotypes and help distinguish them from other mitochondrial encephalopathies.","Adult POLG disease commonly presents with ptosis and PEO, sensory ataxic neuropathy, dysarthria, and may include parkinsonism, premature ovarian failure and cardiomyopathy; recessive and dominant inheritance both occur.","The most common recessive variants cluster in the linker and polymerase domains; a recurrent linker-region substitution is the single most frequent pathogenic allele and is often carried in compound heterozygosity.","Liver histology in the infantile form shows microvesicular steatosis, bile ductular proliferation, bridging fibrosis and hepatocyte dropout, distinct from primary structural liver disease."],"pathophysiology":"POLG (chromosome 15q26) encodes POLG1, the catalytic subunit of the sole DNA polymerase responsible for replicating and repairing the mitochondrial genome, working with the POLG2 accessory subunit, the TWNK helicase and mitochondrial single-stranded binding protein. Pathogenic variants reduce polymerase processivity, proofreading (exonuclease) activity or the enzyme's interaction with template DNA, impairing faithful mtDNA synthesis. In tissues with high replicative demand and post-mitotic energy dependence this causes quantitative mtDNA depletion (prominent in infants, especially in liver, brain and muscle) or the accumulation of multiple secondary mtDNA deletions (prominent in adults). The resulting loss of mtDNA-encoded respiratory chain subunits produces a combined deficiency of complexes I, III, IV and V with relative preservation of the entirely nuclear-encoded complex II, crippling oxidative phosphorylation. Neurones, hepatocytes and skeletal muscle are especially vulnerable; in the brain the occipital and cerebellar cortices and deep grey matter show neuronal loss, spongiosis and gliosis, while the liver undergoes progressive microvesicular steatosis and fibrosis. Valproate independently inhibits mitochondrial beta-oxidation and depletes carnitine, and in the POLG-deficient liver this can tip a marginal organ into fulminant failure. Both autosomal recessive and autosomal dominant inheritance occur, the dominant forms typically producing later-onset PEO through a dominant-negative effect on the polymerase.","additionalDifferentials":["Other mtDNA maintenance/depletion disorders (TWNK, DGUOK, MPV17, TK2, SUCLA2, SUCLG1, RRM2B)","MELAS and other primary mtDNA point-mutation encephalomyopathies","Mitochondrial neurogastrointestinal encephalomyopathy (MNGIE, TYMP related) in MNGIE-like presentations","Wilson disease and other metabolic causes of combined hepatic and neurological disease","Autoimmune or infective (viral) encephalitis with refractory focal seizures","Non-POLG progressive external ophthalmoplegia and myasthenic ptosis","Valproate hepatotoxicity or Reye-like syndrome from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["POLG gene sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis, with wider mtDNA-maintenance gene panel if negative","Blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate, with paired plasma/CSF ratio","Muscle biopsy for respiratory chain enzymology and histochemistry (may show mosaic cytochrome-c-oxidase deficiency)","Quantitative mtDNA copy number and long-range PCR/Southern blot for mtDNA deletions in muscle or liver","Brain MRI including diffusion imaging looking for occipital and thalamic/cerebellar signal change and stroke-like lesions","EEG for occipital-predominant epileptiform activity, rhythmic high-amplitude delta with superimposed spikes, and epilepsia partialis continua","Liver function tests, coagulation profile and hepatic imaging to monitor for evolving failure"],"redFlags":["New or worsening jaundice, coagulopathy or encephalopathy signalling impending fulminant hepatic failure, particularly after valproate exposure","Refractory status epilepticus or epilepsia partialis continua","Rapid psychomotor regression with cortical visual loss in a previously developing child","Recurrent stroke-like episodes with occipital lesions","Cardiac involvement or intractable lactic acidosis indicating multisystem decompensation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis correlates with age of onset: the infantile Alpers-Huttenlocher and myocerebrohepatopathy phenotypes are severe and usually fatal in early childhood, especially once hepatic failure or refractory epilepsy supervenes, whereas adult PEO-predominant disease is slowly progressive and compatible with prolonged survival. Care is supportive and multidisciplinary, as no disease-modifying therapy currently exists.","pitfalls":["Prescribing sodium valproate for the seizures of undiagnosed POLG disease, precipitating fatal hepatic failure","Attributing early liver failure to primary hepatic disease and missing the underlying mitochondrial diagnosis","Expecting a raised lactate in every case; normal blood lactate does not exclude POLG disease, and CSF lactate may be more informative","Assuming a single autosomal recessive pattern, thereby overlooking dominantly inherited adult PEO presentations","Considering liver transplantation without recognising ongoing neurological progression, which limits its benefit"],"management":["Avoid sodium valproate absolutely, as it can precipitate fulminant hepatic failure, and test for POLG before valproate in unexplained epilepsy with mitochondrial or hepatic features","Aggressive seizure control with valproate-sparing antiepileptic drugs, treating status epilepticus and epilepsia partialis continua in the epileptic phenotypes","Supportive multisystem management coordinated by metabolic medicine and neurology, as no curative therapy exists","Nutritional support and management of hepatic dysfunction, recognising the limited role and risks of liver transplantation in multisystem disease","Symptomatic management of ptosis and progressive external ophthalmoplegia, sensory ataxic neuropathy and dysarthria, with cardiomyopathy surveillance","Physiotherapy and mobility support, with management of parkinsonism and premature ovarian insufficiency as they arise","Genetic counselling reflecting both recessive and dominant inheritance, with family testing and reproductive advice","Escalate for acute hepatic decompensation or refractory status epilepticus"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard metabolic medicine and inherited mitochondrial disease references","Clinical neurology and paediatric neurology reference texts","International consensus guidance on mitochondrial disease diagnosis and management"],"crossLinks":[]},"polyarteritis_nodosa":{"name":"Polyarteritis nodosa","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAN","periarteritis nodosa","medium-vessel vasculitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A necrotising vasculitis of medium-sized muscular arteries that characteristically spares the lungs and is not associated with ANCA","Presents with systemic symptoms (fever, weight loss, malaise) plus organ-specific ischaemic damage","Peripheral nervous system involvement, especially mononeuritis multiplex, is common and an important clue","Skin manifestations include livedo reticularis, tender nodules, ulcers, and digital ischaemia","Renal involvement is typically renovascular ischaemia and hypertension rather than glomerulonephritis, and gut involvement can cause mesenteric ischaemia and abdominal pain","A recognised association with hepatitis B infection exists, so viral serology should be checked","Diagnosis rests on biopsy of affected tissue showing necrotising arteritis, or angiography demonstrating microaneurysms and stenoses in visceral vessels","Treatment uses corticosteroids with additional immunosuppression for severe disease, while hepatitis B-associated cases also require antiviral therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Polyarteritis nodosa is a systemic necrotising vasculitis affecting medium and small muscular arteries. Transmural inflammation with fibrinoid necrosis weakens the vessel wall, producing the microaneurysms characteristic of visceral angiography, while intimal proliferation and thrombosis cause luminal narrowing and downstream tissue infarction. Lesions are segmental and favour branch points. Unlike small-vessel vasculitides it does not typically involve capillaries, glomeruli, or the pulmonary circulation and is ANCA-negative. Immune complex mechanisms are implicated, particularly in hepatitis B-associated disease.","additionalDifferentials":["ANCA-associated vasculitides (granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis)","Cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis","Infective endocarditis with embolic phenomena","Fibromuscular dysplasia and segmental arterial mediolysis","Cholesterol embolisation syndrome","Other causes of mononeuritis multiplex"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Hepatitis B (and hepatitis C) serology","Visceral angiography (renal, mesenteric) for microaneurysms and stenoses","Biopsy of clinically involved tissue (e.g. nerve, muscle, skin) showing necrotising arteritis","Inflammatory markers, renal function, and urinalysis (typically without active urinary sediment)","Nerve conduction studies for mononeuritis multiplex and ANCA testing (expected negative)"],"redFlags":["Acute abdomen from mesenteric ischaemia, bowel infarction, or perforation","Rapidly progressive renal impairment or malignant hypertension from renal artery involvement","Cardiac ischaemia or heart failure from coronary arteritis","New neurological deficit including stroke or severe mononeuritis multiplex","Ruptured visceral microaneurysm causing haemorrhage"],"prognosis":"Untreated disease has a poor outlook, but immunosuppression markedly improves survival. Prognosis is worse with significant gastrointestinal, renal, cardiac, or central nervous system involvement; hepatitis B-associated disease can respond well when the virus is also treated.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with ANCA-associated vasculitis and expecting lung or glomerular disease","Failing to test for hepatitis B, which alters management","Missing mesenteric ischaemia in a patient with vague abdominal pain","Overlooking mononeuritis multiplex as a diagnostic clue","Delaying tissue or angiographic confirmation in a critically ill patient"],"management":["Induction immunosuppression with high-dose corticosteroids plus cyclophosphamide for organ- or life-threatening disease","For hepatitis B-associated disease, combine antiviral therapy with a short course of corticosteroids and plasma exchange rather than prolonged immunosuppression","Maintenance immunosuppression such as azathioprine with corticosteroid tapering once remission is achieved in idiopathic disease","Corticosteroids alone may suffice for milder disease without critical organ involvement","Supportive care with analgesia, blood pressure control for renovascular hypertension, and neuropathic pain management for mononeuritis multiplex","Monitor disease activity, organ function and treatment toxicity, with vigilance for relapse and infection prophylaxis","Rheumatology-led multidisciplinary care with nephrology, neurology and gastroenterology input as involvement dictates","Escalate for mesenteric ischaemia, gastrointestinal, cardiac or central nervous system involvement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"polyarticular_juvenile_idiopathic_arthritis":{"name":"Polyarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis","system":"Rheumatology / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["polyarticular JIA","poly JIA","RF-positive polyarthritis","RF-negative polyarthritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology / ILAR & PRINTO criteria","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as arthritis affecting five or more joints within the first six months, and divided by the ILAR system into rheumatoid-factor-positive and rheumatoid-factor-negative subtypes","The rheumatoid-factor-positive form resembles adult seropositive rheumatoid arthritis, typically in older girls, with symmetrical small-joint involvement and a higher risk of erosions and rheumatoid nodules","Anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies often accompany rheumatoid-factor positivity and reinforce a more aggressive, erosive phenotype","The rheumatoid-factor-negative form is more heterogeneous, may present at any age and can involve large and small joints","Cervical spine and temporomandibular joint involvement can cause neck stiffness, micrognathia and feeding or airway difficulties","Chronic anterior uveitis is less frequent than in oligoarticular disease but still warrants slit-lamp surveillance, particularly in antinuclear-antibody-positive children","Early disease-modifying therapy with methotrexate, escalating to TNF inhibition or other biologics, aims to prevent joint destruction"],"pathophysiology":"Polyarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis is a chronic autoimmune synovitis in which activated T cells, B cells and proinflammatory cytokines including tumour necrosis factor drive proliferative synovial inflammation. The inflamed synovium forms a pannus that invades and erodes cartilage and subchondral bone. In the rheumatoid-factor-positive subtype, autoantibodies against the Fc portion of immunoglobulin G and citrullinated proteins mark a more destructive, adult-type rheumatoid process, whereas the seronegative subtype represents a more biologically diverse group with variable outcomes.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic lupus erythematosus and mixed connective tissue disease","Reactive and post-infectious polyarthritis","Acute rheumatic fever","Other JIA subtypes including systemic and psoriatic disease","Leukaemia or other malignancy","Inflammatory bowel disease-associated arthritis","Viral arthritides (e.g. parvovirus B19)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Rheumatoid factor and anti-citrullinated peptide antibodies to subclassify and gauge prognosis","Antinuclear antibody to inform uveitis risk","Inflammatory markers (erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein)","Radiographs, ultrasound or MRI of affected joints to detect synovitis and erosions","MRI of the temporomandibular joints when jaw involvement is suspected","Full blood count and film to help exclude malignancy and monitor therapy","Slit-lamp ophthalmology assessment as part of surveillance"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive erosive disease with functional decline","Temporomandibular or cervical spine involvement threatening feeding or airway","Constitutional symptoms, night pain or cytopenias suggesting malignancy","Silent uveitis complications on ophthalmic review"],"prognosis":"The rheumatoid-factor-positive subtype tends to run a persistent, erosive course requiring sustained immunosuppression, whereas seronegative disease has a more variable outlook. Early aggressive treatment improves the likelihood of remission and reduces long-term joint damage and disability.","pitfalls":["Underestimating disease severity because inflammatory markers can be only modestly raised","Missing temporomandibular joint disease, which is often clinically silent yet causes growth impairment","Delaying disease-modifying therapy and allowing irreversible erosions","Overlooking uveitis surveillance in antinuclear-antibody-positive children","Confusing an evolving connective tissue disease with seronegative polyarthritis"],"management":["Early aggressive disease-modifying therapy with methotrexate as first-line, aiming for remission and prevention of erosions","Escalate to biologic disease-modifying drugs such as tumour necrosis factor inhibitors for inadequate response, especially in rheumatoid-factor-positive erosive disease","Intra-articular and short courses of corticosteroids for flares, minimising long-term systemic steroid exposure","Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for symptomatic relief of pain and stiffness","Regular slit-lamp screening for chronic anterior uveitis, particularly in antinuclear-antibody-positive children","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy, with attention to temporomandibular and cervical spine involvement affecting jaw growth, feeding and the airway","Monitor growth, bone health and drug toxicity, and support transition to adult services","Multidisciplinary paediatric rheumatology care with ophthalmology, orthodontic and dental input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology","ILAR classification of juvenile idiopathic arthritis","ACR/PRINTO treatment recommendations for polyarticular JIA"],"crossLinks":[]},"polycystic_kidney_disease":{"name":"Polycystic Kidney Disease","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ADPKD","PKD"],"chapter":327,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2430","clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) is the most common life-threatening monogenic disease, affecting ~12 million people worldwide (estimated prevalence 1:1000 to 1:400), and is a leading cause of kidney failure; it is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait with complete penetrance but variable expressivity (p.2430-2431).","ADPKD is caused by mutations in PKD1 (chromosome 16p13, ~85% of cases, encodes polycystin-1) and PKD2 (chromosome 4q21-q23, ~15% of cases, encodes polycystin-2); PKD2 patients tend to have milder disease and may be underdiagnosed. Polycystins are localized on the primary cilium, and defective ciliary function places ADPKD among the ciliopathies (p.2430-2431).","It is characterized by progressive bilateral formation of epithelial-lined renal cysts; cysts arise in only ~5% of tubules but their enormous growth destroys surrounding tissue. Enlarged kidneys can reach a fourfold increase in length and up to 20 times the normal weight (p.2430-2431).","Back or flank pain is a frequent symptom in ~60% of patients and may result from cyst infection, hemorrhage, or nephrolithiasis; gross hematuria from cyst rupture occurs in ~40% during the disease course, often recurrently (p.2432).","Hypertension is common and typically occurs before any reduction in GFR; it is a risk factor for both cardiovascular and kidney disease progression. Cardiovascular complications are the major cause of mortality in ADPKD (p.2432).","Intracranial (berry) aneurysm occurs four to five times more frequently than in the general population and causes high mortality; about 20-50% of patients may have 'warning headaches' preceding subarachnoid hemorrhage from a ruptured aneurysm (p.2432).","Liver (biliary epithelial) cysts are the most common extrarenal complication; massive polycystic liver disease occurs almost exclusively in women, particularly those with multiple pregnancies. Mitral valve prolapse occurs in up to 30% of patients, and colonic diverticulae and abdominal wall hernias are also increased (p.2432).","Diagnosis is typically made from a positive autosomal dominant family history plus bilateral kidney cysts on ultrasonography; in at-risk subjects aged 15-29 years, two renal cysts (unilateral or bilateral) suffice (sensitivity 96%, specificity 100%), with higher cyst counts required at older ages because simple cysts increase with age (p.2432-2433).","Tolvaptan, a vasopressin V2-receptor (V2R) antagonist, inhibits the cAMP pathway in collecting ducts and reduces cell proliferation by lowering renal cAMP, slowing the decline of renal function; the FDA has indicated it only for patients at risk of rapidly progressing disease (p.2433).","Infection is the second most common cause of death; up to half of patients have one or more renal infections, most commonly an infected cyst or acute pyelonephritis due to gram-negative bacteria, with fever and flank pain (p.2432)."],"pathophysiology":"ADPKD results from mutations in PKD1 and PKD2, encoding polycystin-1 (PC1, a large 11-transmembrane G-protein-coupled-receptor-like protein) and polycystin-2 (PC2, a calcium-permeable six-transmembrane TRP-family cation channel). PC1 and PC2 bind via their C-terminal tails to form a receptor-channel complex (proposed 1:3 PC1:PC2 stoichiometry) located on the primary cilium and at cell membranes and cell-cell junctions of tubular epithelium, functioning as a mechano-/chemosensor regulating calcium and G-protein signaling. Every cell carries a germline mutant allele, but cysts develop in only a small fraction of nephrons, thought to arise from clonal growth of cells sustaining a somatic 'second hit' in the normal allele; a 'third hit' such as renal injury or an additional genetic/epigenetic event may accelerate cyst formation. Loss of ciliary PC1/PC2 function is hypothesized to cause aberrant calcium signaling, increased adenylyl cyclase and decreased phosphodiesterase activity, raising cellular cAMP; increased cAMP promotes protein kinase A activity, driving cyst growth through proliferation and chloride/aquaporin-mediated fluid secretion. Cellular defects include increased proliferation and fluid secretion, decreased differentiation, and abnormal extracellular matrix; affected signaling pathways include Wnt, mTOR, STAT3, cMET, PI3K/Akt, GPCR, EGFR, and CFTR. Rarer ADPKD-associated genes GANAB and DNAJB11 affect PC1 trafficking and cause milder cystic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD, PKHD1) - distal/collecting-duct cysts, oligohydramnios if severe, hypertension, congenital hepatic fibrosis and Caroli disease; absence of renal cysts in either parent >40y on ultrasound helps distinguish ARPKD from ADPKD (p.2430, p.2434)","ADPKD-like disease (GANAB, DNAJB11) - normal-to-smaller kidneys with fewer cysts, variable liver cysts (p.2430-2432)","Autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease (ADTKD; UMOD, MUC1, REN, HNF1B, SEC61A1) - small fibrotic kidneys, medullary cysts, gout in adults (p.2430)","Tuberous sclerosis (TSC1/TSC2) - renal cysts (20-30%), angiomyolipomas, increased risk of renal cell carcinoma, facial angiofibromas, CNS hamartomas; TSC2 is adjacent to PKD1 so a contiguous gene deletion can cause both ADPKD and TS (p.2430, p.2434)","Von Hippel-Lindau disease (VHL) - renal cysts, renal cell carcinoma, retinal angiomas, CNS hemangioblastomas, pheochromocytomas (p.2430)","Nephronophthisis and other ciliopathies (e.g., Bardet-Biedl, Meckel-Gruber, Senior-Loken) - cystic kidney phenotypes with syndromic features (p.2430)","Renal cysts and diabetes syndrome (HNF1B) - kidney cysts with aberrant nephrogenesis and diabetes (p.2430)","Acquired simple renal cysts (increase in frequency with age and can confound diagnostic cyst counts) (p.2432-2433)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasonography - first-line for presymptomatic screening and donor evaluation; age-stratified cyst-count criteria for diagnosis (p.2432-2433)","CT and T2-weighted MRI (with/without contrast) - more sensitive than ultrasound, detect smaller cysts (MRI with gadolinium can detect 2-3 mm cysts); useful to distinguish malignancy from a complex cyst (p.2432-2433)","Magnetic resonance angiography for presymptomatic intracranial aneurysm screening in patients with a positive family history of aneurysm (p.2432)","CT scan as first diagnostic test for suspected subarachnoid hemorrhage, with lumbar puncture to confirm (p.2432)","Genetic testing by linkage and mutational analysis for ambiguous cases; PKD1 analysis is difficult and costly owing to gene size and homologous pseudogenes (paired-end next-generation sequencing may improve this) (p.2433)"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe headache or 'warning headache' suggesting subarachnoid hemorrhage from ruptured intracranial (berry) aneurysm (p.2432)","Positive family history of intracranial aneurysm - a risk factor for aneurysm rupture warranting presymptomatic MRA screening (p.2432)","Fever and flank pain indicating infected cyst or acute pyelonephritis (with or without bacteremia); infection is the second most common cause of death (p.2432)","Gross hematuria from cyst rupture (p.2432)","Diffuse arterial dolichoectasia of the cerebral circulation predisposing to arterial dissection and stroke (p.2432)"],"prognosis":"ADPKD progression shows striking inter- and intrafamilial variability; disease can present as early as in utero, but end-stage renal disease typically occurs in late middle age. Risk factors for progression include early diagnosis, hypertension, gross hematuria, multiple pregnancies, and large kidney size; PKD2 disease is generally milder with later onset. More than half of patients eventually require peritoneal dialysis, hemodialysis, or kidney transplantation. Cardiovascular complications are the major cause of mortality, and renal infection is the second most common cause of death (p.2431-2433).","pitfalls":["Proteinuria is usually only a MINOR feature of ADPKD, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis (p.2432).","Renal cell carcinoma is NOT increased in frequency compared with the general population, but when it does occur in ADPKD it is more often bilateral, multicentric, and sarcomatoid; radiologic imaging is often unable to distinguish cyst infection from cyst hemorrhage due to their complexity (p.2432).","Diagnostic cyst-count thresholds rise with age because simple renal cysts increase with age; criteria have lower sensitivity for PKD2 patients owing to late onset (p.2432-2433).","No specific FDA-approved treatment prevents cyst growth or the decline of renal function; more rigorous blood pressure control does not equal greater benefit, and targeting systolic pressure to 110 mmHg in moderate/advanced disease may worsen progression by reducing renal blood flow (p.2433).","Most renal cysts are not connected to glomerular filtration, so cyst infection requires lipid-soluble antibiotics that penetrate cyst walls (e.g., trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, quinolones, chloramphenicol), often for 4-6 weeks (p.2433).","Polycystic liver disease in ADPKD is distinct from isolated autosomal dominant polycystic liver disease (ADPLD; PRKCSH/SEC63), which does NOT progress to renal failure (p.2432)."],"management":["Tolvaptan, a vasopressin V2-receptor antagonist, to slow cyst growth and glomerular filtration rate decline in adults at risk of rapid progression","Rigorous blood pressure control, preferably with a renin-angiotensin system blocker, as hypertension precedes loss of filtration and drives progression","High fluid intake with sodium restriction to suppress vasopressin, alongside weight management","Manage cyst complications with analgesia for pain, antibiotics for cyst infection, and treatment of haematuria and nephrolithiasis","Screen for intracranial berry aneurysms with magnetic resonance angiography in those with a family history, and treat warning headaches urgently","Prepare for and provide kidney replacement therapy through dialysis and transplantation as end-stage kidney disease approaches","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant condition, with family screening and living-donor evaluation","Nephrology-led multidisciplinary follow-up monitoring kidney function and cardiovascular risk"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal stage-based severity classification for ADPKD. Genotype broadly stratifies severity (PKD1 disease ~85% of cases is more severe with earlier onset than PKD2 disease ~15%; GANAB/DNAJB11 mutations cause milder cystic disease). Risk of rapid progression - used to select patients for tolvaptan - is estimated by combining genotypic and imaging (kidney growth rate) information; recognized clinical risk factors for progression include early diagnosis, hypertension, gross hematuria, multiple pregnancies, and large kidney size (p.2431-2433).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2430"],"crossLinks":["ckd","sah","renal_colic","htn_emergency"]},"polycystic_liver_disease":{"name":"Polycystic liver disease","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLD","PCLD","isolated polycystic liver disease","autosomal dominant polycystic liver disease","PRKCSH deficiency","SEC63 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by multiple bile-duct-derived cysts scattered through the liver parenchyma, often numbering in the dozens or hundreds","Isolated autosomal dominant PLD is linked to mutations in genes such as PRKCSH and SEC63, and cysts also occur as part of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD)","Most patients are asymptomatic, but massive hepatomegaly can cause abdominal distension, early satiety, breathlessness, and pain","Liver synthetic function is usually preserved even with extensive cystic replacement","Complications include cyst haemorrhage, infection, rupture, and compression of adjacent structures such as the bile ducts, portal vein, or inferior vena cava","It is more prevalent and cysts are larger in women, with growth influenced by oestrogen exposure","Management ranges from observation to cyst aspiration/sclerotherapy, fenestration, hepatic resection, or transplantation for severe symptomatic disease; somatostatin analogues can reduce liver volume"],"pathophysiology":"Polycystic liver disease arises from abnormal proliferation of the biliary epithelium and defective ductal plate remodelling, producing fluid-filled cysts derived from biliary microhamartomas that lose continuity with the biliary tree. In isolated autosomal dominant disease, mutations in genes including PRKCSH (encoding hepatocystin) and SEC63 disrupt glycoprotein processing and protein translocation in cholangiocytes, promoting cyst formation. Cysts also accompany ADPKD (PKD1/PKD2). Progressive cyst growth, modulated by oestrogen and cyclic AMP-driven fluid secretion, leads to hepatomegaly and mass-effect complications while sparing hepatocellular function.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease with hepatic cysts","Simple (solitary) hepatic cysts","Caroli disease and congenital hepatic fibrosis","Hydatid (echinococcal) cysts","Cystic neoplasms (biliary cystadenoma/cystadenocarcinoma)","Von Meyenburg complexes (biliary hamartomas)","Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease with hepatic involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal ultrasound as first-line imaging for cyst number and size","CT or MRI to characterise cyst burden, complications, and vascular/biliary compression","Renal imaging to assess for coexisting ADPKD","Liver function tests (usually near-normal)","Serum tumour markers and cyst fluid analysis if a cystic neoplasm is suspected","Genetic testing (PRKCSH, SEC63, PKD1/PKD2) in familial or syndromic cases"],"redFlags":["Fever and localised pain suggesting cyst infection","Sudden severe pain from cyst haemorrhage or rupture","Jaundice or portal hypertension from biliary or vascular compression","Lower-limb oedema or Budd-Chiari-like features from inferior vena cava/hepatic vein compression"],"prognosis":"Overall prognosis is good because hepatocellular function is generally preserved; symptoms relate to liver volume and complications, and even severely affected patients can be managed with volume-reducing procedures or, rarely, transplantation.","pitfalls":["Failing to look for coexisting renal cysts and distinguish ADPKD from isolated PLD","Mistaking a cystic neoplasm or hydatid cyst for a simple polycystic cyst","Aspirating a cyst without sclerotherapy, leading to rapid recurrence","Attributing abnormal liver tests to the cysts rather than seeking another cause"],"management":["Most patients need no intervention; treat only symptomatic mass-effect disease, with reassurance given preserved hepatocellular function","Volume-reducing procedures for symptomatic hepatomegaly, including cyst aspiration with sclerotherapy for a dominant cyst, laparoscopic fenestration, or hepatic resection","Somatostatin analogues to reduce cyst volume and symptoms in selected patients","Advise avoidance or minimisation of oestrogen-containing therapies, which promote cyst growth in women","Manage complications with antibiotics for cyst infection and treatment of cyst haemorrhage, rupture, or compression of the bile ducts, portal vein or inferior vena cava","Liver transplantation for massive, refractory disease with intractable symptoms","Assess for coexisting autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease and provide genetic counselling in familial cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of cystic liver disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"polycystic_ovary_syndrome":{"name":"Polycystic ovary syndrome","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCOS","polycystic ovarian syndrome","Stein-Leventhal syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["PCOS is diagnosed using the Rotterdam criteria, requiring at least two of oligo- or anovulation, clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound, after excluding mimics","Common presentations include irregular or absent periods, hirsutism and acne, subfertility, and difficulty with weight","Insulin resistance is central to the syndrome and is often present regardless of body weight, though obesity worsens it","It is the leading cause of anovulatory infertility","Unopposed oestrogen from chronic anovulation increases the long-term risk of endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma, so regular endometrial protection is important","Lifestyle modification with weight management is the first-line intervention and can restore ovulation and improve metabolic profile","Management is symptom-directed: cycle regulation and endometrial protection, treatment of hyperandrogenic features, and ovulation induction when fertility is desired","Screening for and managing metabolic complications, including impaired glucose tolerance, type 2 diabetes, and dyslipidaemia, is an essential part of long-term care"],"pathophysiology":"PCOS reflects a complex interplay of hormonal and metabolic disturbances. Insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinaemia stimulates ovarian theca cells to overproduce androgens and reduces hepatic sex hormone-binding globulin, raising free androgen levels. Altered gonadotrophin secretion, with a relatively high luteinising hormone drive, further promotes androgen production and disrupts follicular maturation, so multiple small antral follicles accumulate without a dominant follicle emerging, causing anovulation. Chronic unopposed oestrogen exposure from anovulation places the endometrium at risk of hyperplasia.","additionalDifferentials":["Thyroid dysfunction","Hyperprolactinaemia","Non-classical congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Cushing syndrome","Androgen-secreting ovarian or adrenal tumour","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhoea"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum total and free testosterone and other androgens","Luteinising hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone, and sex hormone-binding globulin","Thyroid function and prolactin to exclude alternative causes","17-hydroxyprogesterone to screen for non-classical congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Transvaginal ultrasound for ovarian morphology and antral follicle count","Oral glucose tolerance test and lipid profile to assess metabolic risk"],"redFlags":["Rapid onset or severe virilisation suggesting an androgen-secreting tumour rather than PCOS","Prolonged amenorrhoea with abnormal bleeding raising concern for endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma","Markedly elevated androgens beyond the range typical of PCOS","Features of Cushing syndrome","Severe metabolic derangement such as newly diagnosed diabetes"],"prognosis":"PCOS is a lifelong condition whose reproductive symptoms often improve with weight management and targeted therapy, and many women conceive with or without ovulation induction. The main long-term concerns are metabolic, with increased risks of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and, through chronic anovulation, endometrial cancer; proactive metabolic surveillance improves outcomes.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing PCOS without excluding thyroid disease, hyperprolactinaemia, and other mimics","Relying on ultrasound alone, since polycystic ovarian morphology can occur in healthy women","Overlooking endometrial protection in women with prolonged amenorrhoea","Focusing only on fertility and neglecting long-term metabolic and cardiovascular risk","Attributing severe or rapidly progressive virilisation to PCOS"],"management":["Lifestyle modification with weight management, diet and exercise as first-line, which can restore ovulation and improve the metabolic and androgenic profile","Combined hormonal contraception to regulate cycles, provide endometrial protection and treat hirsutism and acne","Anti-androgen therapy such as spironolactone alongside contraception, with cosmetic measures for hirsutism","Endometrial protection through cyclical progestogen or combined contraception to counter unopposed oestrogen and reduce hyperplasia risk","Ovulation induction for those seeking pregnancy with letrozole first-line or clomifene, and metformin as an adjunct","Metformin and metabolic risk-factor management, with screening for impaired glucose tolerance, diabetes and dyslipidaemia","Long-term cardiovascular risk surveillance and mental health support","Multidisciplinary care across gynaecology, endocrinology and fertility services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"polycythaemia_vera":{"name":"Polycythaemia vera","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["polycythemia vera","primary polycythaemia","PV"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO haematolymphoid classification / haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A myeloproliferative neoplasm characterised by autonomous overproduction of red cells, often with raised white cells and platelets","Almost all cases carry a JAK2 mutation (V617F or exon 12)","Symptoms include aquagenic pruritus, erythromelalgia, headache, plethora and thrombosis","A low serum erythropoietin supports the diagnosis and helps distinguish it from secondary polycythaemia","The dominant risks are arterial and venous thrombosis, with later potential to transform to myelofibrosis or acute leukaemia","Management combines venesection to a target haematocrit, aspirin and, in higher-risk patients, cytoreduction"],"pathophysiology":"Polycythaemia vera is a clonal stem cell disorder driven in nearly all cases by activating JAK2 mutations, which render erythroid (and often myeloid and megakaryocytic) progenitors hypersensitive to, or independent of, erythropoietin. The resulting unregulated proliferation expands the red cell mass and frequently the granulocyte and platelet lines. The elevated haematocrit raises blood viscosity and, together with qualitative platelet and endothelial changes, produces the high thrombotic risk, while progressive marrow fibrosis or clonal evolution underlies transformation.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary polycythaemia (hypoxia, erythropoietin-secreting tumour)","Relative (apparent) polycythaemia from plasma volume contraction","Essential thrombocythaemia or primary myelofibrosis","Congenital polycythaemia","Chronic myeloid leukaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and haematocrit","JAK2 V617F and, if negative, exon 12 mutation analysis","Serum erythropoietin (characteristically low)","Bone marrow examination where needed","Assessment of thrombotic risk factors"],"redFlags":["Arterial or venous thrombosis, including unusual-site thrombosis","Very high haematocrit with hyperviscosity symptoms","Progressive splenomegaly or cytopenias suggesting transformation to myelofibrosis","Blasts suggesting leukaemic transformation"],"prognosis":"With haematocrit control and thrombosis prevention many patients live for decades, but the disease carries thrombotic risk and a smaller long-term risk of transformation to myelofibrosis or acute leukaemia.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with secondary or relative polycythaemia","Failing to control the haematocrit to target","Overlooking JAK2-negative cases needing exon 12 testing","Missing transformation signalled by new cytopenias or splenomegaly"],"management":["Venesection to a target haematocrit to reduce blood viscosity and thrombotic risk, the cornerstone of treatment","Low-dose aspirin for all patients without contraindication to lower arterial and venous thrombotic risk","Cytoreductive therapy, with hydroxycarbamide first-line or interferon particularly in younger patients and pregnancy, for high-risk patients","Ruxolitinib, a JAK inhibitor, for hydroxycarbamide-resistant or intolerant disease and troublesome symptoms","Symptom control for aquagenic pruritus and erythromelalgia, with optimisation of cardiovascular risk factors","Monitor full blood count and haematocrit and surveil for progression to myelofibrosis or leukaemic transformation","Refer to haematology for pregnancy, thrombosis or evidence of disease transformation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","Haematology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"polycythaemia_vera_post_pv_myelofibrosis":{"name":"Post-polycythaemia vera myelofibrosis","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["post-PV myelofibrosis","post-polycythaemic myelofibrosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO haematolymphoid classification / haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A recognised progression of polycythaemia vera to a myelofibrotic phase with marrow fibrosis","It is marked by a falling need for venesection, anaemia, progressive splenomegaly and constitutional symptoms","The blood film shows a leucoerythroblastic picture with teardrop cells","Diagnostic criteria require established polycythaemia vera plus marrow fibrosis and additional features such as anaemia and splenomegaly","It resembles primary myelofibrosis clinically and shares the risk of leukaemic transformation","Management mirrors that of myelofibrosis, including JAK inhibition for symptoms and splenomegaly and transplantation in selected patients"],"pathophysiology":"Post-polycythaemia vera myelofibrosis represents clonal evolution of the underlying JAK2-driven myeloproliferative neoplasm. Chronic cytokine release and megakaryocyte proliferation stimulate reactive deposition of reticulin and collagen fibrosis in the marrow, progressively impairing haematopoiesis. As the marrow fails, red cell production falls (so venesection is no longer needed and anaemia develops) and haematopoiesis shifts to the spleen and liver (extramedullary haematopoiesis), causing organomegaly and the leucoerythroblastic blood picture. The disease carries an increased risk of transformation to acute leukaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary myelofibrosis","Post-essential-thrombocythaemia myelofibrosis","Reactive marrow fibrosis (for example infection, malignancy)","Myelodysplastic syndrome with fibrosis","Chronic myeloid leukaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and film for leucoerythroblastic changes and teardrop cells","Bone marrow trephine demonstrating fibrosis","JAK2 and driver-mutation status (usually known from prior PV)","Assessment of spleen size and constitutional symptoms","Prognostic scoring for myelofibrosis"],"redFlags":["Rising blasts indicating transformation to acute leukaemia","Severe anaemia or thrombocytopenia","Massive or painful splenomegaly","Profound constitutional symptoms and weight loss"],"prognosis":"Transformation to a fibrotic phase generally worsens prognosis compared with polycythaemia vera, with outcomes similar to primary myelofibrosis and a risk of leukaemic transformation; allogeneic transplantation is the only cure.","pitfalls":["Attributing the falling haematocrit and anaemia to over-venesection rather than fibrotic transformation","Confusing it with primary myelofibrosis without the prior PV history","Missing leukaemic transformation","Overlooking the need to reassess prognosis and management"],"management":["Management mirrors primary myelofibrosis and is risk-stratified with a prognostic score to guide intensity","JAK inhibition with ruxolitinib to reduce symptomatic splenomegaly and constitutional symptoms","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, the only curative option, for eligible higher-risk patients","Manage anaemia with transfusion support and agents such as erythropoiesis-stimulating agents, danazol or immunomodulators, as venesection is no longer required","Symptom-directed care for splenomegaly, with splenectomy or splenic irradiation in selected refractory cases","Monitor blood counts and spleen size and surveil for leukaemic transformation","Supportive care including infection prevention and management of transfusion-related iron overload, within haematology-led multidisciplinary care with transplant assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","Haematology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"polycythemia_vera_and_other_myeloprolifera":{"name":"Polycythemia Vera and Other Myeloproliferative Neoplasms","system":"Hematologic/Oncologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Myeloproliferative neoplasms","MPN","PV","Primary myelofibrosis","PMF","Agnogenic myeloid metaplasia","Myelofibrosis with myeloid metaplasia","Essential thrombocytosis","Essential thrombocythemia","ET"],"chapter":108,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.818","clinicalPearls":["The classic chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms covered here (PV, PMF, ET) all arise from a hematopoietic stem cell and share constitutive activation of JAK2 through driver mutations in JAK2, MPL, or CALR; their natural histories are usually measured in decades, and leukemic transformation is uncommon in the absence of chemotherapy or radiation (Harrison 22e p.818).","JAK2 V617F is expressed in more than 95% of PV patients and in roughly 50% of PMF and ET patients; most JAK2-negative PV patients carry a JAK2 exon 12 mutation, while a few have LNK or calreticulin mutations (Harrison 22e p.818-819).","In PV the plasma volume is expanded, unlike other causes of true erythrocytosis, so an elevated red cell mass can be masked, especially in women; below a hemoglobin of about 20 g/dL (hematocrit 60%) one cannot reliably separate true erythrocytosis from plasma-volume contraction without red cell mass and plasma volume measurement (Harrison 22e p.819-820).","PV should be suspected in any woman presenting with hepatic vein (Budd-Chiari) thrombosis, since this is the type of thrombosis particularly associated with JAK2 V617F; portal vein thrombosis is more common in male PV patients (Harrison 22e p.819).","Aquagenic pruritus and erythromelalgia are the symptoms that can suggest PV over other causes of erythrocytosis; pruritus may reflect JAK2 V617F-driven mast cell activation, and Helicobacter pylori incidence is increased in PV (Harrison 22e p.819-820).","Only three conditions cause microcytic erythrocytosis: beta-thalassemia trait, hypoxic erythrocytosis, and PV; the red cell distribution width is typically normal in thalassemia trait but may be elevated in hypoxic erythrocytosis or PV when iron deficiency coexists (Harrison 22e p.820).","In a PV patient with massive splenomegaly, a 'normal' hematocrit or hemoglobin should be regarded as indicating an elevated red cell mass until proven otherwise, because the associated plasma volume increase masks the true red cell mass (Harrison 22e p.820).","In PMF the peripheral smear shows a leukoerythroblastic picture with teardrop-shaped red cells, nucleated red cells, myelocytes, and promyelocytes; marrow is usually inaspirable ('dry tap') owing to fibrosis, and bone x-rays may show osteosclerosis (Harrison 22e p.821-822).","PMF marrow fibroblasts are polyclonal and not part of the neoplastic clone; fibrosis is driven by overproduction of transforming growth factor beta, tissue inhibitors of metalloproteinases, and thrombopoietin, while osteosclerosis relates to osteoprotegerin and angiogenesis to vascular endothelial growth factor (Harrison 22e p.821).","In ET the large platelet mass can cause spurious (test-tube) hyperkalemia from platelet potassium release on clotting, with no electrocardiographic correlate, and can make arterial oxygen measurements inaccurate unless blood is collected on ice (Harrison 22e p.823).","Very high platelet counts (above about 1 million/microL) are associated primarily with bleeding from acquired von Willebrand disease due to proteolysis of high-molecular-weight von Willebrand multimers, not with thrombosis; tobacco use is the most important thrombotic risk factor in ET (Harrison 22e p.823-824).","PMF and ET diagnoses are largely diagnoses of exclusion: significant splenomegaly in apparent ET suggests another MPN, and FISH for BCR-ABL is preferred to exclude CML in JAK2-negative thrombocytosis because reverse-transcriptase PCR for BCR-ABL is prone to false positives (Harrison 22e p.823-824)."],"pathophysiology":"These chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms originate in a single hematopoietic stem cell and produce overabundant mature blood elements without significant dysplasia, together with a tendency toward extramedullary hematopoiesis, myelofibrosis, and eventual leukemic transformation. PV, PMF, and ET share constitutive activation of the nonreceptor tyrosine kinase JAK2, the cognate kinase and chaperone for the erythropoietin and thrombopoietin receptors. The JAK2 V617F mutation in the autoinhibitory pseudokinase domain causes ligand-independent kinase activation, explaining erythropoietin hypersensitivity, erythropoietin-independent erythroid colony growth, increased Bcl-XL expression, and apoptosis resistance in PV progenitors. Loss of heterozygosity on chromosome 9p (uniparental disomy) leads to JAK2 V617F homozygosity, common in PV, less so in PMF, and rare in ET. JAK2 V617F alone does not account for the entire phenotype and is probably not the initiating lesion: the same mutation yields different phenotypes in ET versus PMF, familial PV can occur without it, and leukemia may arise in a JAK2-negative progenitor, implying an ancestral precursor. PMF and ET may instead carry MPL (thrombopoietin receptor) or CALR (calreticulin) mutations; mutant CALR binds and activates MPL at the cell surface. In PMF, marrow fibrosis is a reactive, polyclonal fibroblast response driven by cytokines from the clone (TGF-beta, thrombopoietin), with extramedullary hematopoiesis in liver and spleen. Megakaryocyte genome reduplication is endomitotic and thrombopoietin-driven; paradoxically, MPL expression is reduced and plasma thrombopoietin is increased across these MPNs despite the expanded megakaryocyte/platelet mass.","additionalDifferentials":["Relative (spurious/stress) erythrocytosis from plasma volume contraction (Gaisbock's syndrome) due to dehydration, diuretics, ethanol, androgens, or tobacco","Hypoxic erythrocytosis (pulmonary disease, right-to-left shunt, sleep apnea, high altitude, carbon monoxide, high-oxygen-affinity hemoglobin)","Tumor- or renal-disease-associated erythrocytosis (hypernephroma, hepatoma, cerebellar hemangioblastoma, uterine myoma, pheochromocytoma, renal artery stenosis, renal cysts, post-transplant)","Congenital/familial erythrocytosis (erythropoietin receptor, VHL/Chuvash, 2,3-BPG, PHD2/EGLN1, HIF2alpha/EPAS1, LNK mutations) and recombinant erythropoietin or SGLT2-inhibitor effect","Reactive thrombocytosis (iron deficiency, infection, inflammation, malignancy, hemorrhage, surgery, postsplenectomy, B12/folate-deficiency rebound)","Chronic myeloid leukemia (BCR-ABL positive), myelodysplasia including 5q- syndrome and sideroblastic anemia (SF3B1)","Secondary myelofibrosis (myelophthisis) from metastatic carcinoma, lymphoma, multiple myeloma, hairy cell leukemia, systemic mastocytosis, and nonmalignant causes such as HIV, tuberculosis, SLE, hyperparathyroidism, renal osteodystrophy, vitamin D deficiency"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection (can cause secondary myelofibrosis)","Tuberculosis (can cause secondary myelofibrosis and constitutional symptoms)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Reactive thrombocytosis from iron deficiency, inflammation, or malignancy mimicking ET","Stress/spurious erythrocytosis (Gaisbock's syndrome) mimicking PV","Chronic myeloid leukemia mimicking ET or PMF","Myelodysplastic syndromes (5q- syndrome, sideroblastic anemia) presenting with thrombocytosis","Metastatic carcinoma, lymphoma, or systemic mastocytosis causing myelophthisic marrow fibrosis","Systemic lupus erythematosus, hyperparathyroidism, vitamin D deficiency, and renal osteodystrophy causing nonmalignant myelofibrosis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative next-generation-sequencing assay for JAK2 V617F or JAK2 exon 12 mutations (now supersedes surrogate tests for PV diagnosis)","Red cell mass and plasma volume determination to confirm absolute versus relative erythrocytosis when hemoglobin is below ~20 g/dL","Serum erythropoietin level (elevated level favors a secondary cause; a normal level does not exclude PV)","Red cell count, mean corpuscular volume, and red cell distribution width (microcytic erythrocytosis narrows differential)","Mutation testing for MPL and CALR (exon 9, type 1 deletions and type 2 insertions) in JAK2-negative ET/PMF","FISH for BCR-ABL to exclude CML in JAK2-negative thrombocytosis","Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy (megakaryocyte clustering/hyperplasia, reticulin/collagen fibrosis in PMF; absent stainable iron with hypercellularity favors PV)","Cytogenetic analysis (del 20q, del 13q, trisomy 8/9, partial trisomy 1q; complex karyotype portends poor prognosis in PMF)","Mutational analysis of ASXL1, EZH2, SRSF2, and IDH1/2 for refined PMF prognosis","Ristocetin cofactor activity to detect acquired von Willebrand disease at very high platelet counts","Serum lactate dehydrogenase and alkaline phosphatase (may be elevated in PMF), uric acid"],"redFlags":["Hepatic vein thrombosis (Budd-Chiari) in a young woman, or portal/mesenteric vein thrombosis, as a presenting feature of PV","Neurologic hyperviscosity symptoms: vertigo, tinnitus, headache, visual disturbance, transient ischemic attack","Arterial or venous thrombosis in cerebral, cardiac, or mesenteric vessels","Platelet count above 1 million/microL with bleeding from acquired von Willebrand disease","Sudden splenic enlargement with painful splenic infarction (fever, pleuritic chest pain)","Development of transfusion-dependent anemia, increasing organomegaly, or peripheral blood blasts signaling marrow failure or accelerated/blastic transformation in PMF","Constitutional symptoms (night sweats, fatigue, weight loss) suggesting a myeloproliferative rather than reactive process","Spinal cord compression, pericardial tamponade, or ureteral/intestinal obstruction from exuberant extramedullary hematopoiesis in PMF"],"prognosis":"PV is generally indolent, with a clinical course measured in decades; most patients live long lives without functional impairment when red cell mass is controlled, and the incidence of acute leukemia is very low in those not exposed to chemotherapy or radiation. Myelofibrosis is part of the natural history of PV but is usually reactive and reversible without prognostic significance, although about 15% develop hematopoietic stem cell failure with extramedullary hematopoiesis and transfusion-dependent anemia. PMF has the shortest survival of the three: it follows a course of progressive marrow failure and organomegaly, can evolve to an accelerated phase, and about 10% transform spontaneously to a treatment-refractory acute leukemia. PMF survival is stratified by IPSS, DIPSS, and DIPSS-Plus risk factors (anemia, leukocytosis, peripheral blasts, constitutional symptoms, age over 65, unfavorable karyotype, thrombocytopenia, transfusion dependence); ASXL1, EZH2, SRSF2, and IDH1/2 mutations and complex cytogenetics independently predict early death or leukemic transformation, and triple-negative PMF carries the worst prognosis. ET is more benign than previously thought; survival is comparable to the general population regardless of driver mutation, and leukemic evolution is more often a consequence of therapy than of the disease.","pitfalls":["Treating the platelet count rather than the patient in ET: a high platelet count alone does not establish thrombotic risk, and the cause of any symptom must be confirmed to be platelet-related before therapy","Assuming a high platelet count causes thrombosis when very high counts more often cause hemorrhage via acquired von Willebrand disease; aspirin can promote bleeding in this setting","Interpreting a normal hematocrit/hemoglobin as excluding elevated red cell mass in a PV patient with massive splenomegaly (plasma volume expansion masks it)","Relying on a low-level JAK2 V617F allele burden (VAF 5% or less) to diagnose a blood disease, since not everyone with low burden has one","Assuming a normal serum erythropoietin excludes PV (it does not), or overlooking that an elevated level points to a secondary cause","Diagnosing ET in a JAK2 V617F-positive patient without red cell mass/plasma volume studies, since many such patients actually have PV","Misreading spurious hyperkalemia or inaccurate arterial oxygen values caused by the large platelet mass in ET","Mistaking secondary myelofibrosis/myelophthisis for PMF and missing a treatable underlying disorder","Using leukemogenic agents (alkylating agents, radioactive phosphorus-32) and prolonged hydroxyurea, which is itself mutagenic and antagonizes TP53, in disorders with otherwise long survival"],"management":["Maintain a target haematocrit by regular therapeutic venesection to reduce hyperviscosity and thrombotic risk in polycythaemia vera","Low-dose aspirin for antiplatelet prophylaxis against arterial and microvascular thrombosis unless contraindicated by bleeding","Cytoreduction with hydroxycarbamide as first-line in high-risk disease, with interferon alfa preferred in younger patients and pregnancy","JAK1/2 inhibition with ruxolitinib for hydroxycarbamide-resistant or intolerant disease and for symptomatic splenomegaly and constitutional symptoms in myelofibrosis","Symptom control of aquagenic pruritus with antihistamines or selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors and of erythromelalgia with aspirin","Surveillance for thrombosis, progressive myelofibrosis and leukaemic transformation with serial full blood counts and clinical review","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation considered as the only curative option in eligible higher-risk primary myelofibrosis","Haematology-led management of thrombotic complications such as Budd-Chiari syndrome with anticoagulation"],"severityClassification":"For primary myelofibrosis, prognostic risk is estimated by the IPSS (2009), DIPSS (2010), and DIPSS-Plus (2011) scoring systems. Risk factors include anemia (hemoglobin <10 g/dL), leukocytosis (>25,000/microL), peripheral blood blasts (>=1%), constitutional symptoms, and age over 65; DIPSS-Plus adds unfavorable karyotype, platelet count <100,000/microL, and transfusion dependence. In DIPSS, anemia is weighted as 2 points. Categories run Low, Intermediate-1, Intermediate-2, and High by number/weight of factors. These systems are not accurate for risk assessment of PV or ET patients who have developed secondary myelofibrosis.","references":["Harrison 22e p.818","Harrison 22e p.819","Harrison 22e p.820","Harrison 22e p.821","Harrison 22e p.822","Harrison 22e p.823","Harrison 22e p.824"],"crossLinks":[]},"polydactyly":{"name":"Polydactyly","system":"Paediatric orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["supernumerary digit","preaxial polydactyly","postaxial polydactyly","central polydactyly","duplicated digit"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery / Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Polydactyly is duplication of a digit and is classified by location: preaxial (thumb or radial side), central (index to ring), and postaxial (little finger or ulnar side)","Postaxial polydactyly is common, frequently familial (autosomal dominant), and more prevalent in children of African ancestry; it may be isolated or syndromic","Preaxial (thumb) duplication is graded by the Wassel-Flatt classification according to the most proximal level of skeletal duplication, from a bifid distal phalanx to a duplicated metacarpal","Central polydactyly is less common, is often hidden within a syndactyly (polysyndactyly), and involves complex bony and tendinous connections that are difficult to reconstruct","Syndromic associations must be considered: preaxial forms with conditions such as Holt-Oram syndrome and Fanconi anaemia, and postaxial forms with Ellis-van Creveld, Bardet-Biedl, Meckel-Gruber, and trisomy 13","A well-formed extra digit is not simply excised; reconstruction preserves the most functional bone, joint, ligaments, tendons, and nail, and reconstructs collateral ligament stability","Surgery is timed to optimise function and appearance, generally in early childhood"],"pathophysiology":"Polydactyly arises from disordered patterning of the developing limb bud along the anteroposterior (radioulnar) axis. Abnormal signalling, notably of the sonic hedgehog pathway acting through the zone of polarising activity, produces one or more supernumerary digits. The duplicated ray may be a rudimentary soft-tissue tag or a fully formed digit with its own bones, joints, and tendons. Depending on which part of the axis is affected, the extra digit lies on the radial (preaxial), central, or ulnar (postaxial) side of the hand, and disturbances of these same signalling pathways underlie the many syndromic forms.","additionalDifferentials":["Syndactyly or polysyndactyly, which may coexist","Macrodactyly (an enlarged rather than duplicated digit)","Ulnar dimelia (mirror hand)","Triphalangeal thumb","Constriction ring sequence with accessory tissue","Symphalangism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Radiographs of the hand to define the level of skeletal duplication (for example Wassel-Flatt grading of the thumb)","Family history and examination of relatives, as postaxial forms are often autosomal dominant","Clinical screening for syndromic features affecting the heart, kidneys, retina, and growth","Echocardiography where a heart-hand or VACTERL association is suspected","Genetic evaluation when the pattern is syndromic, central, or bilateral"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting a serious syndrome, such as a cardiac anomaly, renal disease, retinal dystrophy, or marrow-failure risk","Central polydactyly or polysyndactyly with complex functional impairment","A duplicated thumb that is unstable or deviating, threatening pinch function"],"prognosis":"Isolated postaxial and most preaxial duplications have an excellent outcome after well-planned reconstruction; residual problems can include joint instability, angular deviation, stiffness, and a reconstructed thumb that is smaller than normal, while central forms are more prone to stiffness and imperfect appearance.","pitfalls":["Simply tying off or amputating a duplicated digit without reconstructing the collateral ligaments, leaving instability or deviation","Discarding the more functional of two duplicated thumbs","Failing to look for syndromic associations","Underestimating the complexity of central polysyndactyly"],"management":["Excision of a rudimentary pedunculated postaxial digit, by suture ligation or formal removal of the soft-tissue remnant","Reconstructive surgery for a well-formed duplicated digit that preserves the most functional bone, joint, tendons and nail and restores collateral ligament stability rather than simple amputation","Timing and technique of thumb (preaxial) reconstruction guided by the Wassel-Flatt level of skeletal duplication, undertaken by a specialist paediatric hand surgeon","Complex staged reconstruction for central polysyndactyly given the intricate bony and tendinous interconnections","Screening for syndromic associations affecting the heart, kidneys, retina and growth, with echocardiography where a heart-hand or VACTERL association is suspected","Postoperative hand therapy and monitoring for residual joint instability, angular deviation and stiffness","Genetic counselling and examination of relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance of many postaxial forms, with genetic evaluation for syndromic, central or bilateral patterns"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"polyglandular_autoimmune":{"name":"Autoimmune polyglandular syndrome","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["APS","polyglandular autoimmune syndrome","polyendocrine autoimmune syndrome","APECED (type 1)","Schmidt syndrome (type 2)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Denotes clustering of multiple autoimmune endocrine failures in one patient, with type 1 and type 2 differing in genetics, age of onset, and component diseases","Type 1 (APECED) is a monogenic disorder of childhood defined by chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, and adrenal insufficiency, caused by mutations in the AIRE gene","Type 2 is the more common, polygenic, HLA-associated adult form, typically combining adrenal insufficiency with autoimmune thyroid disease and/or type 1 diabetes","Because component disorders emerge over years, diagnosis of one autoimmune endocrinopathy should prompt vigilance and periodic screening for others","Adrenal insufficiency is the most dangerous component and may be unmasked or worsened when thyroid hormone is started without glucocorticoid cover","Non-endocrine autoimmune conditions frequently coexist, including vitiligo, pernicious anaemia, coeliac disease, and alopecia","Autoantibody testing and periodic biochemical surveillance guide early detection of evolving gland failure","Management is replacement of each failed hormone, with careful sequencing and stress dosing of glucocorticoids"],"pathophysiology":"Autoimmune polyglandular syndromes result from immune-mediated destruction of multiple endocrine glands and other tissues. In type 1, loss-of-function mutations in the autoimmune regulator (AIRE) gene impair thymic negative selection of self-reactive T cells, allowing autoimmunity against several organs from early life. In type 2, susceptibility is polygenic and strongly linked to HLA haplotypes and other immune-regulatory loci, producing autoimmune attack on the adrenal cortex, thyroid, and pancreatic islets among others. Circulating organ-specific autoantibodies mark the affected tissues, and gland failure becomes clinically apparent once functional reserve is exhausted, which is why components appear sequentially rather than simultaneously.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated autoimmune endocrine disease (e.g. solitary Addison disease or Hashimoto thyroiditis)","Non-autoimmune causes of the individual gland failures","IPEX syndrome and other monogenic immune dysregulation disorders","Multiple endocrine neoplasia syndromes (functionally distinct but multiglandular)","Iatrogenic multi-gland dysfunction (e.g. checkpoint inhibitor endocrinopathies)","DiGeorge syndrome for hypoparathyroidism with immune features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Morning cortisol and ACTH with dynamic adrenal testing to detect adrenal insufficiency","Thyroid function tests and thyroid autoantibodies","Fasting glucose, HbA1c, and islet autoantibodies","Calcium, phosphate, and PTH for hypoparathyroidism (particularly type 1)","Vitamin B12, coeliac serology, and other autoantibody panels as indicated","Genetic testing for AIRE mutations when type 1 is suspected"],"redFlags":["Adrenal crisis with hypotension, hyponatraemia, and collapse requiring emergency treatment","Precipitation of adrenal crisis by initiating thyroid hormone before glucocorticoid replacement","Severe hypocalcaemia from hypoparathyroidism causing tetany or seizures","Invasive candidiasis or intercurrent illness destabilising multiple hormone axes"],"prognosis":"With early detection and lifelong replacement of each affected hormone, patients can do well, but the risk of undiagnosed adrenal insufficiency and the progressive addition of new autoimmune diseases necessitate ongoing surveillance; type 1 tends to be more severe with a broader disease spectrum.","pitfalls":["Treating a single endocrinopathy without screening for others in the syndrome","Starting levothyroxine before excluding or treating adrenal insufficiency","Failing to educate patients on glucocorticoid stress dosing","Overlooking non-endocrine autoimmune components such as coeliac disease or pernicious anaemia","Not offering genetic counselling and family screening in type 1 disease"],"management":["Lifelong replacement of each failed hormone axis: glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid for adrenal insufficiency, levothyroxine for hypothyroidism, insulin for type 1 diabetes and calcium with an activated vitamin D analogue for hypoparathyroidism","Always establish and treat adrenal insufficiency before commencing thyroxine, as thyroid hormone accelerates cortisol clearance and can precipitate adrenal crisis","Management of associated non-endocrine autoimmunity, including antifungals for chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis and treatment of coeliac disease and pernicious anaemia","Patient education in sick-day rules, provision of a steroid emergency card and injectable hydrocortisone for intercurrent illness","Emergency treatment of adrenal crisis with parenteral hydrocortisone and intravenous fluid resuscitation","Periodic biochemical and autoantibody surveillance for the sequential emergence of additional gland failures","Endocrinology-led multidisciplinary care with genetic counselling and family screening where the monogenic AIRE-driven type 1 form is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"polyglandular_autoimmune_syndrome_type_2":{"name":"Autoimmune Polyglandular Syndrome Type 2","system":"Gastrointestinal / Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Schmidt syndrome","APS-2","Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type II"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrinology and clinical immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by autoimmune Addison disease occurring with autoimmune thyroid disease (Schmidt syndrome) and/or type 1 diabetes (Carpenter syndrome); it is the commonest of the autoimmune polyglandular syndromes.","Unlike the monogenic type 1 syndrome, APS-2 is polygenic with strong HLA (DR3/DR4) association, presents typically in adulthood and shows female predominance.","Adrenal insufficiency is the pivotal and most dangerous component — insidious fatigue, hyperpigmentation, salt craving and postural dizziness may be attributed to other diagnoses until an adrenal crisis intervenes.","Starting thyroxine in an unrecognised hypoadrenal patient can precipitate adrenal crisis by accelerating cortisol clearance — always exclude or treat adrenal insufficiency before treating hypothyroidism.","A falling insulin requirement or recurrent hypoglycaemia in a person with type 1 diabetes should prompt evaluation for new adrenal insufficiency.","Component diseases emerge asynchronously over years, so a lifelong index of suspicion and periodic biochemical and autoantibody screening are needed.","Associated non-endocrine autoimmunity is common — coeliac disease, pernicious anaemia, vitiligo, alopecia and primary ovarian insufficiency.","First-degree relatives share increased risk, making family counselling and targeted screening worthwhile."],"pathophysiology":"Autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 2 is a polygenic organ-specific autoimmune disorder in which loss of immune self-tolerance leads to lymphocytic destruction of multiple endocrine glands. Susceptibility is dominated by high-risk HLA class II haplotypes (notably DR3-DQ2 and DR4-DQ8) with contributions from CTLA4, PTPN22 and other immunoregulatory genes, in contrast to the AIRE-mutation-driven monogenic type 1 syndrome. Autoreactive T cells and organ-specific autoantibodies mediate the injury: 21-hydroxylase antibodies mark autoimmune adrenalitis with progressive destruction of the adrenal cortex and glucocorticoid then mineralocorticoid failure; thyroid peroxidase and thyroglobulin antibodies drive Hashimoto thyroiditis or, with TSH-receptor antibodies, Graves disease; and glutamic acid decarboxylase, IA-2 and zinc-transporter-8 antibodies accompany autoimmune beta-cell destruction in type 1 diabetes. The glands are affected sequentially rather than simultaneously, reflecting shared genetic predisposition acting on different target tissues over time. Coexisting antibodies against gastric parietal cells, tissue transglutaminase and gonadal tissue explain the associated pernicious anaemia, coeliac disease and premature ovarian insufficiency. The clinical danger arises from the interplay of deficiencies, particularly the way thyroid hormone replacement unmasks latent cortisol deficiency.","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1 (APECED) with mucocutaneous candidiasis and hypoparathyroidism","Isolated autoimmune Addison disease without other gland involvement","IPEX syndrome and other monogenic immune dysregulation disorders in the young","Secondary (pituitary) adrenal and thyroid failure from hypophysitis or panhypopituitarism","Infective adrenalitis such as tuberculous or fungal adrenal destruction","Thymoma-associated and drug-induced (immune checkpoint inhibitor) multi-endocrine autoimmunity","Wolfram syndrome and mitochondrial disorders causing combined endocrinopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Short synacthen (ACTH stimulation) test with paired ACTH and renin for adrenal reserve","21-hydroxylase autoantibodies as a marker of autoimmune adrenalitis","Thyroid function with thyroid peroxidase, thyroglobulin and TSH-receptor antibodies","Glucose, HbA1c and islet autoantibodies (GAD, IA-2, ZnT8) for diabetes surveillance","Tissue transglutaminase antibodies and vitamin B12 with parietal cell antibodies for associated conditions","Serum electrolytes to detect the hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia of mineralocorticoid deficiency","HLA typing and family screening where diagnostic clarification or risk stratification is needed"],"redFlags":["Features of impending adrenal crisis — hypotension, vomiting, abdominal pain, confusion and profound weakness","Deteriorating glycaemic control or unexplained recurrent hypoglycaemia signalling new adrenal failure","Hyperpigmentation with weight loss and salt craving pointing to undiagnosed Addison disease","Clinical worsening after commencing thyroxine, suggesting precipitated hypoadrenalism","Severe electrolyte disturbance with cardiovascular instability"],"prognosis":"With early recognition and appropriate hormone replacement of each failing gland, life expectancy and quality of life are good, but the disorder is lifelong and further glands may fail over years. The principal preventable causes of death are unrecognised or undertreated adrenal crises, underscoring the need for patient education, steroid emergency plans and long-term surveillance.","pitfalls":["Commencing levothyroxine before excluding or treating adrenal insufficiency and thereby triggering a crisis","Ascribing fatigue, weight loss and postural symptoms to the known endocrinopathy while missing new adrenal failure","Assuming diagnosis of one gland is the whole picture and not screening for the evolving components","Overlooking non-endocrine associations such as coeliac disease and pernicious anaemia","Failing to provide sick-day rules and emergency parenteral steroid provision to hypoadrenal patients"],"management":["Lifelong glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement for autoimmune Addison disease, the pivotal and most dangerous component","Levothyroxine replacement for autoimmune hypothyroidism and insulin for associated type 1 diabetes, with antithyroid drugs, radioiodine or thyroidectomy for coexisting Graves disease","Exclude or treat adrenal insufficiency before initiating thyroxine to avoid precipitating adrenal crisis","Educate on sick-day glucocorticoid dosing, provide a steroid emergency card and injectable hydrocortisone for emergencies","Emergency management of adrenal crisis with parenteral hydrocortisone, intravenous fluids and correction of hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia","Investigate a falling insulin requirement or recurrent hypoglycaemia in established diabetes as a possible marker of new adrenal insufficiency","Periodic biochemical and autoantibody screening for asynchronous new components, including coeliac disease and pernicious anaemia","Endocrinology-led care with counselling of relatives given the HLA-associated familial risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard endocrinology textbook chapters on autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes","Clinical practice guidance on diagnosis and management of primary adrenal insufficiency","Clinical immunology reference texts on multi-organ autoimmunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"polyglandular_autoimmune_type1":{"name":"Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 (APECED, AIRE)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["APS-1","APECED","autoimmune polyendocrinopathy-candidiasis-ectodermal dystrophy","autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / endocrine genetics reference literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A monogenic autoimmune disorder caused by mutations in the AIRE gene, which normally drives thymic negative selection of self-reactive T cells","Defined by the classic triad of chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism and primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease), with two of the three needed for diagnosis","Candidiasis usually appears first in childhood, followed by hypoparathyroidism and then adrenal failure","A wide range of additional autoimmune features may develop, including hypogonadism, type 1 diabetes, autoimmune hepatitis, pernicious anaemia, alopecia and vitiligo","Ectodermal features such as enamel hypoplasia, nail dystrophy and keratopathy support the diagnosis","High-titre neutralising autoantibodies against type-1 interferons and interleukin-17/22 are characteristic and underlie susceptibility to candidiasis","Because new component disorders emerge over years, lifelong surveillance for evolving endocrine and non-endocrine autoimmunity is essential"],"pathophysiology":"APS-1 results from loss-of-function mutations in the autoimmune regulator (AIRE) gene, which controls expression of tissue-specific self-antigens in thymic medullary epithelial cells. Defective AIRE impairs negative selection of autoreactive T cells, allowing escape of lymphocytes that attack multiple endocrine and non-endocrine tissues. The resulting organ-specific autoimmunity destroys the parathyroid glands and adrenal cortex among others, while neutralising autoantibodies against type-1 interferons and Th17 cytokines compromise mucosal antifungal immunity, explaining the characteristic chronic candidiasis.","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 2","Isolated Addison disease","Isolated idiopathic hypoparathyroidism","DiGeorge syndrome (hypoparathyroidism with immunodeficiency)","IPEX syndrome","Isolated chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis from other immune defects"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["AIRE gene sequencing to confirm the diagnosis","Calcium, phosphate and parathyroid hormone for hypoparathyroidism","Morning cortisol with ACTH stimulation and adrenal autoantibodies for Addison disease","Autoantibody panel including anti-interferon antibodies and organ-specific antibodies","Screening for additional components: thyroid, gonadal and glucose testing, liver enzymes and full blood count","Assessment of ectodermal features including dental and ophthalmic review"],"redFlags":["Adrenal crisis from unrecognised or intercurrent-stress-precipitated adrenal insufficiency","Symptomatic hypocalcaemia with tetany, laryngospasm or seizures","Fulminant autoimmune hepatitis","Invasive or oesophageal candidiasis, or squamous carcinoma arising in chronic candidiasis"],"prognosis":"With hormone replacement and antifungal treatment most patients survive into adulthood, but the disease is lifelong and mortality is driven by adrenal crises, severe hypocalcaemia, fulminant hepatitis and complications of chronic candidiasis.","pitfalls":["Managing each component in isolation and failing to anticipate emerging new autoimmune disorders","Overlooking coexisting adrenal insufficiency when treating hypoparathyroidism, risking crisis","Attributing recurrent candidiasis to simple infection rather than the underlying immune defect","Missing autoimmune hepatitis or other non-endocrine components during follow-up","Not offering genetic counselling and family screening"],"management":["Lifelong glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement for primary adrenal insufficiency","Calcium supplementation with an activated vitamin D analogue such as alfacalcidol or calcitriol for hypoparathyroidism","Suppressive azole antifungal therapy for chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, with vigilance for azole resistance and oral squamous carcinoma","Establish and treat adrenal insufficiency before introducing thyroxine or other therapies that raise metabolic demand","Immunosuppression for autoimmune hepatitis and replacement therapy for other emerging components such as hypogonadism and type 1 diabetes","Emergency treatment of adrenal crisis and of symptomatic hypocalcaemia with parenteral calcium","Lifelong surveillance for new endocrine and non-endocrine components, including liver enzymes for fulminant hepatitis","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive AIRE inheritance, within endocrinology-led multidisciplinary care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Reference literature on AIRE and autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes"],"crossLinks":[]},"polyglandular_autoimmune_type2":{"name":"Autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 2 (Schmidt syndrome)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Schmidt syndrome","autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 2","APS-2","polyglandular autoimmune syndrome type II"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by autoimmune adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease) combined with autoimmune thyroid disease and/or type 1 diabetes mellitus","It is the most common of the autoimmune polyglandular syndromes and typically presents in adulthood, more often in women","Inheritance is polygenic with a strong association with particular HLA haplotypes, unlike the monogenic type 1 syndrome","Component disorders often appear years apart, so a new autoimmune endocrinopathy should prompt vigilance for others","Additional autoimmune conditions such as coeliac disease, pernicious anaemia, vitiligo and primary gonadal failure are frequently associated","Starting thyroid hormone in an unrecognised adrenal-insufficient patient can precipitate an adrenal crisis, so cortisol status must be assessed first","Periodic screening of at-risk relatives and of affected patients for evolving deficiencies is central to management","Treatment is lifelong hormone replacement tailored to each affected gland"],"pathophysiology":"Autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 2 is an organ-specific autoimmune disorder in which loss of immune self-tolerance leads to lymphocytic destruction of multiple endocrine glands. Susceptibility is polygenic, driven strongly by human leucocyte antigen associations together with other immune-regulatory gene variants, rather than by a single-gene defect. Circulating autoantibodies against adrenal, thyroid and pancreatic islet antigens mark the underlying cell-mediated destruction, which progresses at different rates in different glands, producing the characteristic sequential emergence of adrenal insufficiency, thyroid dysfunction and type 1 diabetes over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1","Isolated autoimmune Addison disease","Isolated autoimmune thyroid disease","Type 1 diabetes mellitus without additional endocrinopathy","Secondary adrenal insufficiency from pituitary disease","IPEX and other monogenic autoimmune syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Morning cortisol with adrenocorticotrophic hormone and short synacthen testing for adrenal insufficiency","Thyroid function tests and thyroid peroxidase antibodies","Fasting glucose, glycated haemoglobin and islet-related autoantibodies","Adrenal (21-hydroxylase) autoantibodies","Screening for coeliac disease and pernicious anaemia","Assessment of gonadal function where premature failure is suspected"],"redFlags":["Adrenal crisis with hypotension, hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia","Precipitation of crisis by thyroid hormone given before glucocorticoid replacement","Diabetic ketoacidosis at diabetes onset","Severe hypothyroidism","Rapid clinical deterioration during intercurrent illness"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition and lifelong, appropriately balanced hormone replacement, patients can expect a near-normal life, though the ongoing risk of adrenal crisis and the potential for new autoimmune components to develop necessitate continued surveillance.","pitfalls":["Starting levothyroxine before excluding or treating adrenal insufficiency and triggering a crisis","Diagnosing a single endocrinopathy without considering the wider syndrome","Failing to screen for evolving deficiencies over time","Overlooking associated non-endocrine autoimmune disease such as coeliac disease","Confusing the polygenic type 2 syndrome with the monogenic type 1 form"],"management":["Lifelong glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement for autoimmune adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease)","Levothyroxine for autoimmune thyroid failure and insulin for associated type 1 diabetes mellitus","Assess and correct adrenal insufficiency before starting thyroid hormone to prevent precipitating adrenal crisis","Patient education in sick-day rules with a steroid emergency card and injectable hydrocortisone for intercurrent illness","Emergency management of adrenal crisis with parenteral hydrocortisone and intravenous fluid resuscitation","Periodic clinical and autoantibody surveillance for delayed emergence of additional components including coeliac disease, pernicious anaemia and gonadal failure","Endocrinology-led coordination of replacement across multiple failing glands, with counselling of relatives about familial autoimmune risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","DeGroot's Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"polyhydramnios":{"name":"Polyhydramnios","system":"Reproductive / Obstetrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hydramnios","amniotic fluid excess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / ISUOG guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Polyhydramnios is an excessive volume of amniotic fluid, defined sonographically by a deepest vertical pool at or above a defined threshold or an amniotic fluid index above the ninety-fifth centile for gestation","Causes fall into fetal, maternal, and placental groups; roughly half to two-thirds are idiopathic, but a cause should always be sought","Fetal causes include anything that impairs swallowing or increases urine output, such as gastrointestinal obstruction (oesophageal or duodenal atresia), neurological and neuromuscular disorders, orofacial clefts, chest masses, cardiac anomalies, and hydrops","Maternal diabetes, pre-existing or gestational, is a leading maternal cause through fetal hyperglycaemia and osmotic diuresis, so glycaemic control should be assessed","In monochorionic twins, polyhydramnios in one sac with oligohydramnios in the other suggests twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome","Symptoms and signs reflect uterine overdistension: abdominal discomfort and breathlessness, a large-for-dates uterus, a tense abdomen with difficulty palpating fetal parts, and a fluid thrill","Complications arise chiefly from overdistension and sudden decompression, including preterm labour, preterm prelabour rupture of membranes, malpresentation, cord prolapse, placental abruption after membrane rupture, and postpartum haemorrhage from atony","Management targets the cause and the complications: optimise maternal glycaemia, monitor for preterm labour and malpresentation, consider amnioreduction in symptomatic cases, and assess the neonate for anomalies such as oesophageal atresia after birth"],"pathophysiology":"Amniotic fluid volume in the second half of pregnancy is maintained by a balance between fetal urine production and lung fluid, which add fluid, and fetal swallowing and intramembranous absorption, which remove it. Polyhydramnios develops when production exceeds removal. Reduced swallowing or absorption occurs with upper gastrointestinal obstruction, in which the fetus cannot swallow fluid past an atresia, with neuromuscular disease impairing swallowing, and with external compression of the gut. Increased production occurs with fetal polyuria, as in maternal hyperglycaemia causing fetal osmotic diuresis, in high-output states such as fetal anaemia, and in the recipient twin of twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome. The accumulating fluid overdistends the uterus, stretching the myometrium and predisposing to preterm contractions and, after membrane rupture, to rapid decompression that can shear the placenta or allow the cord to prolapse.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple pregnancy","Fetal macrosomia","Large uterine fibroids or an adnexal mass","Inaccurate gestational dating","Maternal obesity giving a false impression of a large uterus","An ovarian or other pelvic-abdominal cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound quantification of amniotic fluid by deepest vertical pool or amniotic fluid index","Detailed fetal anomaly ultrasound, with attention to the stomach bubble, swallowing, and structural survey","Maternal testing for diabetes by glucose tolerance testing or glycaemic assessment","Assessment for fetal anaemia, such as middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity Doppler, and for hydrops","Screening for congenital infection where indicated","Consideration of fetal karyotype or microarray when structural anomalies are present","Assessment of chorionicity and inter-twin fluid discordance in multiple pregnancy"],"redFlags":["Rapidly increasing fluid or acute maternal respiratory distress from massive polyhydramnios","Preterm contractions or prelabour rupture of membranes","Malpresentation or a high, unstable lie near term, carrying a cord prolapse risk","Sonographic evidence of a major fetal anomaly, hydrops, or twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome","Signs of placental abruption after membrane rupture"],"prognosis":"Idiopathic mild polyhydramnios generally has a good outcome, but moderate-to-severe polyhydramnios and cases with an identifiable fetal anomaly or twin-to-twin transfusion carry higher perinatal morbidity and mortality; prognosis is largely determined by the underlying cause.","pitfalls":["Labelling polyhydramnios idiopathic without a thorough search for fetal anomaly, diabetes, and anaemia","Overlooking oesophageal atresia antenatally and failing to assess the neonate, for example by passing a nasogastric tube, after birth","Performing artificial rupture of membranes without controlling for the risk of cord prolapse and abruption from sudden decompression","Neglecting the raised risk of postpartum haemorrhage from an overdistended, atonic uterus","Missing twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome by not assessing chorionicity and inter-twin discordance"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying cause: optimise maternal glycaemic control in diabetes and refer monochorionic twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome for laser photocoagulation","Amnioreduction by therapeutic amniocentesis to relieve severe maternal breathlessness and abdominal discomfort from uterine overdistension","Consider a prostaglandin synthetase inhibitor such as indometacin to reduce foetal urine output in selected cases before the third trimester, with monitoring of the ductus arteriosus","Serial ultrasound surveillance of amniotic fluid volume, foetal growth and wellbeing","Antenatal corticosteroids where preterm birth is likely, and planning for the raised risk of preterm labour, malpresentation, cord prolapse and placental abruption","Deliver in an obstetric unit with controlled amniotomy and readiness for postpartum haemorrhage from an overdistended uterus","Neonatal review after delivery for causes such as oesophageal or duodenal atresia, with paediatric and obstetric multidisciplinary input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","ISUOG practice guidelines on fetal ultrasound and amniotic fluid assessment","Oxford Handbook of Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"polymicrogyria":{"name":"Polymicrogyria","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMG","bilateral perisylvian polymicrogyria","cortical dysplasia (polymicrogyria type)","GPR56 (ADGRG1)","TUBB2B"],"chapter":null,"source":"Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A malformation of late cortical organisation in which the cortex forms excessive small, fused gyri with abnormal lamination, producing an irregular or bumpy cortical surface","It is often bilateral and symmetric in recognised patterns, the perisylvian region being the most common site, giving the bilateral perisylvian syndrome","MRI shows an irregular grey-white junction with numerous small gyri; the cortex may look deceptively thickened because the tiny folds are packed together","Causes are heterogeneous and include genetic variants such as GPR56 and tubulin genes, chromosomal disorders including 22q11.2 deletion, and acquired insults, notably congenital cytomegalovirus infection and in-utero ischaemia","The bilateral perisylvian form typically causes pseudobulbar palsy with oromotor dysfunction, dysarthria, feeding difficulty, epilepsy, and variable cognitive impairment","Clinical severity depends on extent and location, ranging from focal, minimally symptomatic lesions to widespread malformation with severe disability","Diagnosis rests on the MRI pattern combined with genetic testing and evaluation for congenital infection"],"pathophysiology":"Polymicrogyria is a disorder of the late stages of cortical development, arising after neurons have largely completed migration, during cortical organisation and lamination. Both genetic factors, including variants in genes governing the neuronal cytoskeleton and cortical patterning, and acquired prenatal insults such as congenital cytomegalovirus infection or focal ischaemia can disrupt normal gyral formation. The cortex develops an excess of small, abnormally laminated and fused convolutions. The distribution of the malformation, often bilateral and region-specific, determines the neurological consequences such as perisylvian oromotor dysfunction and epilepsy.","additionalDifferentials":["Pachygyria and other lissencephaly-spectrum malformations","Cobblestone malformation of the dystroglycanopathies","Schizencephaly, which is frequently lined by polymicrogyria","Focal cortical dysplasia","Congenital cytomegalovirus-related cortical malformation","Tubulinopathy-related cortical dysgenesis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution brain MRI to define the distribution and extent of the malformation","Testing for congenital cytomegalovirus infection, including stored newborn blood spot analysis where available","Chromosomal microarray, including assessment for 22q11.2 deletion","Gene panel or exome sequencing for cortical malformation genes","Electroencephalography to characterise seizures","Swallowing and speech assessment for perisylvian involvement"],"redFlags":["Severe oromotor dysfunction with aspiration and feeding failure","Drug-resistant epilepsy","Evidence of congenital cytomegalovirus infection with hearing loss requiring intervention","Rapidly progressive neurological decline suggesting an alternative or additional diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome ranges widely with the extent and location of involvement. Small focal lesions may be asymptomatic or cause only focal epilepsy, whereas extensive bilateral malformation causes significant motor, oromotor, cognitive, and seizure disability.","pitfalls":["Misreading the packed small gyri as a thick, smooth cortex and mistaking it for pachygyria","Failing to test for congenital cytomegalovirus, an important and potentially preventable acquired cause","Overlooking associated 22q11.2 deletion and its systemic implications","Assuming all polymicrogyria is genetic and neglecting acquired prenatal causes in counselling"],"management":["Antiseizure medication to control the epilepsy that frequently complicates the malformation, with epilepsy surgery considered for drug-resistant focal seizures","Speech and language therapy and feeding support for perisylvian oromotor and pseudobulbar dysfunction, including gastrostomy feeding where swallowing is unsafe","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and spasticity management for associated motor impairment","Antiviral therapy with valganciclovir where symptomatic congenital cytomegalovirus infection is the identified cause","Developmental surveillance and educational support for cognitive impairment","Genetic counselling and family screening guided by chromosomal microarray and gene-panel results such as GPR56, tubulin genes and 22q11.2 deletion","Neurology-led multidisciplinary care coordinating seizure, feeding, communication and developmental needs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","GeneReviews: Polymicrogyria Overview"],"crossLinks":[]},"polymorphic_eruption_of_pregnancy":{"name":"polymorphic eruption of pregnancy","system":"skin","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy","PUPPP","toxaemic rash of pregnancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically arises in the third trimester (or immediately postpartum) in a primigravida, and characteristically begins within abdominal striae distensae.","The periumbilical region is spared, an important clinical clue distinguishing it from pemphigoid gestationis which frequently involves the umbilicus.","Lesions are polymorphic: urticarial papules and plaques predominate but vesicles, targetoid lesions, eczematous change and widespread erythema may all occur.","Strongly associated with excessive maternal weight gain, multiple gestation (twins, triplets) and male fetal sex.","It is a clinical diagnosis; direct immunofluorescence is negative and is used mainly to exclude pemphigoid gestationis when in doubt.","Intensely pruritic but self-limiting, usually resolving within a few weeks of delivery without scarring.","There is no associated maternal or fetal risk, distinguishing it prognostically from intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy and pemphigoid gestationis.","First-line management is emollients and topical corticosteroids with oral antihistamines; a short course of systemic corticosteroid is reserved for severe cases."],"pathophysiology":"The aetiology is incompletely understood but is thought to relate to rapid abdominal wall distension in the third trimester causing damage to connective tissue within striae, exposing altered collagen that provokes an inflammatory response. This is supported by the strong associations with excessive weight gain, large fetal size and multiple gestation. A contribution from circulating fetal DNA and a mild immunological reaction to fetal antigens has been proposed, and the male-fetus association raises the possibility of a response to fetal cells in maternal skin, but no consistent hormonal or autoimmune mechanism has been confirmed. Unlike pemphigoid gestationis there is no autoantibody to the basement membrane zone.","additionalDifferentials":["pemphigoid gestationis","intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy","atopic eruption of pregnancy","drug eruption","allergic or irritant contact dermatitis","urticaria","viral exanthem or scabies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["skin biopsy for histology (non-specific: dermal oedema with perivascular lymphohistiocytic infiltrate and eosinophils)","direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin (negative, to exclude pemphigoid gestationis)","serum bile acids and liver function tests to exclude obstetric cholestasis","full blood count (eosinophilia may occur but is non-specific)","indirect immunofluorescence or BP180 ELISA if pemphigoid gestationis is suspected","obstetric ultrasound to confirm multiple gestation and fetal wellbeing"],"redFlags":["periumbilical involvement or tense blisters suggesting pemphigoid gestationis rather than polymorphic eruption","pruritus without primary skin lesions plus dark urine or pale stools, raising intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy","jaundice or right upper quadrant pain suggesting hepatic pathology","reduced fetal movements or other signs of fetal compromise","systemic upset, fever or mucosal involvement suggesting an alternative severe dermatosis or drug reaction"],"prognosis":"Excellent, with spontaneous resolution typically within one to six weeks of delivery and no scarring. There is no increased maternal or fetal morbidity and recurrence in subsequent pregnancies is uncommon.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling any tense blistering as polymorphic eruption; umbilical involvement and positive direct immunofluorescence point to pemphigoid gestationis.","Failing to check bile acids in a pruritic pregnant woman and missing intrahepatic cholestasis, which carries fetal risk.","Assuming recurrence in later pregnancies and causing unnecessary anxiety when recurrence is in fact unusual.","Overusing systemic corticosteroids for a benign self-limiting condition when topical therapy usually suffices.","Attributing the eruption solely to striae and overlooking the strong link with multiple gestation, delaying recognition of a twin pregnancy."],"management":["Symptomatic relief with mid-potency topical corticosteroids as the mainstay for the intense pruritus","Regular emollients and cooling measures to soothe affected skin","Oral antihistamines, favouring pregnancy-appropriate agents such as chlorphenamine, for troublesome itch","A short tapering course of systemic corticosteroids reserved for severe or widespread eruption","Reassurance that the condition is benign and self-limiting, resolving within weeks of delivery without scarring or increased foetal risk","Exclude pemphigoid gestationis (periumbilical sparing, negative direct immunofluorescence) and obstetric cholestasis (serum bile acids) where the diagnosis is uncertain"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","British Journal of Dermatology, dermatoses of pregnancy guidance","Ambros-Rudolph CM et al. Specific dermatoses of pregnancy, Journal of the American Academy of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"polymorphic_light_eruption":{"name":"Polymorphic light eruption","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLE","PMLE","polymorphous light eruption","benign summer light eruption"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest of the idiopathic photodermatoses, seen most often in young women and typically appearing in the first strong sunshine of spring or on a holiday to a sunnier climate","The eruption is characteristically delayed, developing hours to a day or two after sun exposure rather than immediately","Lesions are itchy and 'polymorphic' (papules, papulovesicles, plaques or an eczema-like pattern), but any individual tends to reproduce the same morphology on each occasion","Intermittently exposed sites such as the V of the neck, the outer arms and the dorsa of the hands are affected, while the habitually exposed face is often spared","'Hardening' develops as the skin becomes progressively tolerant through the summer, so the eruption frequently settles despite continued sun exposure","Ultraviolet A is the usual provoking waveband, although ultraviolet B and occasionally visible light may be responsible, and ordinary window glass does not reliably protect","Management rests on photoprotection with clothing and a broad-spectrum high-factor sunscreen offering good ultraviolet A cover, with a prophylactic course of phototherapy in spring to induce hardening for troublesome cases"],"pathophysiology":"Polymorphic light eruption is thought to represent a delayed-type (T-cell mediated) hypersensitivity response to one or more endogenous cutaneous antigens that are generated or altered in the skin by ultraviolet radiation. Affected individuals appear relatively resistant to the ultraviolet-induced immunosuppression that would normally suppress such reactions in unaffected skin, permitting an inflammatory lymphocytic infiltrate to develop in sun-exposed areas. Repeated ultraviolet exposure gradually induces tolerance, or hardening, which accounts for the characteristic seasonal improvement, and a genetic predisposition with reduced regulatory immune activity is likely to contribute.","additionalDifferentials":["Cutaneous or systemic lupus erythematosus","Cutaneous porphyrias, particularly erythropoietic protoporphyria","Drug-induced photosensitivity","Actinic prurigo","Photoallergic or photoaggravated contact dermatitis","Solar urticaria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed history correlating onset with sun exposure, latency and seasonal pattern","Phototesting to characterise the provoking waveband and reproduce lesions where diagnosis is uncertain","Photopatch testing to exclude a photoallergic contact dermatitis","Antinuclear antibody and extractable nuclear antigen (anti-Ro/La) testing to exclude lupus","Plasma, urinary and faecal porphyrin screen to exclude a cutaneous porphyria","Skin biopsy in atypical cases to support the diagnosis and exclude mimics"],"redFlags":["Facial or truncal rash with systemic features, arthralgia or positive autoantibodies suggesting lupus erythematosus","Marked burning or stinging within minutes of exposure, especially in childhood, pointing to erythropoietic protoporphyria","Skin fragility, blistering and scarring raising concern for a cutaneous porphyria","A new photosensitivity temporally linked to a recently started drug"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and tends to improve each summer as hardening develops, and it may lessen or resolve over years, although many patients continue to experience recurrent spring and holiday flares that are usually well controlled by photoprotection and, where needed, prophylactic phototherapy.","pitfalls":["Attributing a persistent facial photosensitive eruption to polymorphic light eruption without excluding lupus erythematosus","Overlooking that ultraviolet A penetrates window glass and clouds, so exposure and flares can occur indoors or on overcast days","Assuming a high sun protection factor alone is adequate when ultraviolet A cover is the critical requirement","Failing to consider prophylactic desensitisation phototherapy in spring for patients with recurrent disabling episodes"],"management":["Rigorous photoprotection with broad-spectrum high-SPF sunscreen covering ultraviolet A, protective clothing and avoidance of intense sun exposure","Topical corticosteroids to settle established, itchy eruptions","Prophylactic springtime desensitisation phototherapy with narrowband ultraviolet B or psoralen-ultraviolet A to induce hardening before the sunny season","A short course of oral corticosteroids for severe flares or to cover an anticipated holiday in a sunny climate","Oral antihistamines for symptomatic relief of pruritus","Vitamin D supplementation to offset reduced cutaneous synthesis from sun avoidance","Exclude lupus erythematosus and cutaneous porphyria where the presentation is atypical before committing to long-term management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","British Photodermatology Group guidance on the idiopathic photodermatoses"],"crossLinks":[]},"polymorphic_ventricular_tachycardia_and_ve":{"name":"Polymorphic Ventricular Tachycardia and Ventricular Fibrillation","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Polymorphic VT","Torsades des pointes","Ventricular fibrillation","VF"],"chapter":262,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1968","clinicalPearls":["Sustained polymorphic VT shows a QRS configuration that changes continuously beat to beat, reflecting an ever-changing ventricular activation sequence; unlike monomorphic VT it does not necessarily imply a fixed structural abnormality or single automatic focus (p.1968).","Sustained polymorphic VT commonly degenerates into ventricular fibrillation (p.1969).","Acute myocardial infarction or ischemia is a frequent cause and should be the first consideration; roughly 10% of acute MI patients develop VT that degenerates to VF, with greatest risk in the first hour (p.1969).","Polymorphic VT/VF occurring within the first 48 hours of acute MI carries higher in-hospital mortality, but survivors to discharge are not at increased risk of later arrhythmic sudden death (p.1969).","Torsades des pointes is the polymorphic VT linked to QT prolongation and classically begins in a pause-dependent fashion: a PVC induces a pause, the following sinus beat has a longer QT, and a PVC interrupting that T wave starts the run (p.1969).","Congenital long QT syndrome stems from ion-channel gene mutations; LQTS-1, -2, and -3 account for roughly 80-90% of cases, with LQTS-1/-2 involving potassium channels and LQTS-3 involving the sodium channel (p.1969-1970).","Genotype influences triggers: LQTS-1 events tend to occur during exertion (notably swimming), LQTS-2 with sudden auditory stimuli or emotional upset, and LQTS-3 sudden death tends to occur during sleep (p.1970).","Brugada syndrome causes syncope or cardiac arrest from polymorphic VT in the absence of structural heart disease; arrest may occur during sleep or be provoked by febrile illness, and males are more commonly affected (p.1970).","Catecholaminergic polymorphic VT is a rare familial disorder of abnormal sarcoplasmic calcium handling producing arrhythmias resembling digitalis toxicity, often with bidirectional (alternating QRS morphology) VT, presenting in childhood with exertion- or emotion-induced events (p.1971).","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, the most common genetic cardiovascular disorder (about 1 in 500), is a prominent cause of sudden death before age 35, typically via polymorphic VT/VF (p.1971).","Ventricular fibrillation shows disordered electrical ventricular activation with no identifiable QRS complexes; spiral wave reentry and multiple circulating reentry wavefronts are proposed mechanisms (p.1971).","After surviving acute MI and revascularization, poorly coupled but surviving Purkinje fibers (which tolerate ischemia owing to glycogen stores and oxygenation from the LV cavity) can trigger PVCs and ventricular fibrillation (p.1971)."],"pathophysiology":"Polymorphic VT arises from a continually changing ventricular activation sequence. Proposed mechanisms include reentry with variable paths, spiral wave reentry, and multiple automatic foci, often near fibrotic myocardium and potentiated by damaged Purkinje cells or ventricular hypertrophy. Abnormal transmural dispersion of repolarization can drive it in the setting of channelopathies or antiarrhythmic drugs even without structural disease. In acute MI/ischemia, reentry through the infarct border zone is implicated, and surviving automatic Purkinje cells can initiate it. Torsades des pointes is associated with abnormal QT prolongation and pause-dependent initiation. Congenital LQTS reflects mutations in ion-channel genes governing repolarization (potassium channels in LQTS-1/-2, sodium channel in LQTS-3, and calcium channels in others). Short QT syndrome reflects a gain of function of the IKr potassium channel or reduced inward depolarizing currents. Brugada syndrome reflects dispersion of repolarization across the ventricular wall, with epicardial and M cells more affected than endocardial cells, localized to the right ventricular outflow tract; sodium channel mutations (SCN5A, SCN1B, SCN10A) are found in about 25% of cases. Catecholaminergic polymorphic VT results from cardiac ryanodine receptor (and less often calsequestrin 2) mutations causing abnormal sarcoplasmic calcium handling. Ventricular fibrillation represents disordered activation without organized QRS, frequently as degeneration of sustained polymorphic or monomorphic VT (p.1968-1971).","additionalDifferentials":["Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia (implies a fixed structural substrate or automatic focus, unlike polymorphic VT)","Acquired (drug- or electrolyte-induced) long QT with torsades des pointes","Congenital long QT syndrome (LQTS-1, -2, -3)","Short QT syndrome","Brugada syndrome","Early repolarization syndrome","Catecholaminergic polymorphic VT (bidirectional VT)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Genetic dilated cardiomyopathies (e.g., Lamin A/C, SCN5A)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Brugada pattern ST elevation must be distinguished from LV hypertrophy, pericarditis, myocardial ischemia/MI, hyperkalemia, hypothermia, right bundle branch block, and arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (p.1970)","Asymptomatic Brugada ECG pattern versus the clinical Brugada syndrome (analogous to Wolff-Parkinson-White pattern versus syndrome)","J-point elevation as a benign normal variant versus early repolarization syndrome associated with arrhythmia risk"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG with measurement of corrected QT (QTc); congenital LQTS typically prolongs QTc to >440 ms in men and >460 ms in women (p.1969)","Short QT syndrome: QTc <360 ms, usually <300 ms (p.1970)","Telemetry/rhythm strip to capture pause-dependent initiation of torsades and characteristic morphologies (p.1969)","Genetic testing/genotyping for family screening and diagnostic reassurance in LQTS (p.1970)","Provocative sodium channel blocker testing (e.g., flecainide, ajmaline, procainamide) to unmask Brugada ST elevation (p.1970)","Assessment of residual LV function/ejection fraction to guide ICD decisions (p.1969)","Reference resource crediblemeds.org to check whether a medication has been reported to prolong the QT interval (p.1969)"],"redFlags":["Near-syncope, syncope, or cardiac arrest as the presenting event (p.1969)","Repeated episodes of polymorphic VT suggesting ongoing myocardial infarction/inadequate reperfusion (p.1969)","QTc exceeding 500 ms, female gender, and prior syncope or cardiac arrest as markers of increased risk in LQTS (p.1970)","Family history of sudden death (relevant in Brugada syndrome, early repolarization syndrome, and genetic cardiomyopathies) (p.1970-1971)","Febrile illness provoking cardiac arrest in Brugada syndrome (p.1970)","HCM features: young age, nonsustained VT, failure of blood pressure to rise with exercise, recent syncope (within 6 months), ventricular wall thickness >3 cm (p.1971)"],"prognosis":"Polymorphic VT/VF within the first 48 hours of acute MI is associated with greater in-hospital mortality, yet patients who survive to discharge are not at increased risk for later arrhythmic sudden death. Long-term risk after infarction is governed largely by residual LV function. In LQTS, higher risk is conferred by QTc >500 ms, female gender, and prior syncope or cardiac arrest. HCM is a prominent cause of sudden death before age 35; surgical myectomy carries a sudden death rate <1% per year, and transcoronary ethanol septal ablation has a reported annual rate of sustained VT or sudden death of roughly 1-5% (p.1969-1971).","pitfalls":["Assuming polymorphic VT implies a fixed structural focus as monomorphic VT does (p.1968)","Overlooking ongoing ischemia/inadequate reperfusion when polymorphic VT/VF recurs after MI (p.1969)","Failing to recognize numerous noncardiac QT-prolonging drugs and drug interactions that raise levels of an offending agent as precipitants of torsades (p.1969)","Re-exposing a patient who had QT-prolongation-induced polymorphic VT to QT-prolonging medications; such patients should be considered susceptible and avoid all future exposure (p.1969)","Misinterpreting benign J-point elevation (a common normal variant) as pathologic early repolarization syndrome (p.1971)","Confusing the asymptomatic Brugada ECG pattern with true Brugada syndrome (p.1970)"],"management":["Immediate defibrillation for ventricular fibrillation or pulseless polymorphic VT with advanced life support","For torsades de pointes, intravenous magnesium, withdrawal of QT-prolonging drugs and correction of hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia, with overdrive pacing or isoprenaline for pause-dependent runs","Urgent reperfusion and revascularisation with beta-blockade when polymorphic VT complicates acute myocardial ischaemia or infarction","Long-term beta-blockade and strict avoidance of QT-prolonging drugs in congenital long QT syndrome","Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator for secondary prevention and for high arrhythmic risk, guided by residual left ventricular function","Disease-specific measures for other channelopathies, including quinidine and trigger avoidance in Brugada syndrome","Genetic testing with cascade family screening for inherited arrhythmia syndromes","Cardiology and electrophysiology referral for risk stratification and device or catheter ablation therapy"],"severityClassification":"No single formal severity grade; risk is stratified by substrate. In post-MI patients, an ICD is indicated for persistent severe LV dysfunction (LV ejection fraction <35%). In LQTS, high-risk features include QTc >500 ms, female gender, and prior syncope/cardiac arrest. In HCM, high-risk markers include young age, nonsustained VT, failure of blood pressure to rise with exercise, recent syncope, and ventricular wall thickness >3 cm (p.1969-1971).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1968","Harrison 22e p.1969","Harrison 22e p.1970","Harrison 22e p.1971","Harrison 22e p.1972"],"crossLinks":[]},"polymorphous_adenocarcinoma":{"name":"Polymorphous adenocarcinoma","system":"Head and neck / Salivary glands","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Polymorphous low-grade adenocarcinoma","Terminal duct carcinoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A malignancy that arises almost exclusively in the minor salivary glands, with the palate being the classic site of origin.","It is one of the commonest intraoral minor salivary gland malignancies and typically presents as a painless, slow-growing submucosal swelling.","The defining paradox is cytological uniformity combined with architectural diversity: tubular, cribriform, solid, single-file streaming and concentric targetoid patterns coexist.","Perineural invasion, often with a targetoid whorling pattern, is common yet usually consistent with indolent behaviour.","The classic type harbours a recurrent PRKD1 hotspot mutation, whereas the cribriform-predominant variant more often shows PRKD1, PRKD2 or PRKD3 rearrangements.","Tumour cells are typically S100 positive with a low proliferation index, and the main task is to separate it from adenoid cystic carcinoma.","The cribriform variant, favouring the tongue base, more frequently involves regional lymph nodes yet still generally behaves indolently.","Wide local excision is the mainstay, and distinguishing it from adenoid cystic carcinoma has major implications for treatment intensity."],"pathophysiology":"Polymorphous adenocarcinoma is a malignant neoplasm derived from the terminal duct or intercalated duct cells of minor salivary glands, in which a single, cytologically bland cell population expresses a remarkably wide range of growth patterns. In most conventional tumours an activating hotspot mutation in the PRKD1 gene deregulates protein kinase D signalling, driving proliferation, while the cribriform-predominant variant is instead characterised by rearrangements of PRKD1, PRKD2 or PRKD3. The neoplastic cells infiltrate the surrounding tissue in tubules, cribriform islands, solid nests and single-file cords, and characteristically ensheath nerves in concentric layers, so that perineural invasion is a defining rather than an alarming feature. Despite this infiltrative and neurotropic growth, proliferative activity remains low, which underlies the tumour's generally indolent clinical course.","additionalDifferentials":["Adenoid cystic carcinoma","Pleomorphic adenoma","Canalicular adenoma","Basal cell adenoma","Secretory carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma not otherwise specified"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Incisional biopsy of the mucosal or submucosal lesion","Immunohistochemistry including S100 and Ki-67 proliferation index","MRI to assess soft-tissue extent and perineural spread","Molecular testing for PRKD1 alterations where available","CT to evaluate bony involvement of the palate or maxilla","Ultrasound or cross-sectional imaging of the neck for nodal assessment"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth or mucosal ulceration","Bone invasion of the palate or maxilla","Extensive or symptomatic perineural spread","Regional nodal metastasis, particularly with the cribriform variant","Solid, high-grade-appearing areas within the tumour"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally favourable and the tumour is low-grade, with complete excision usually curative. The cribriform variant carries a higher rate of nodal metastasis but still tends to behave indolently, whereas misdiagnosis as adenoid cystic carcinoma risks unnecessary overtreatment.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as adenoid cystic carcinoma, leading to inappropriate treatment intensity","Achieving inadequate margins because of the infiltrative growth pattern","Failing to recognise the characteristic perineural invasion","Mistaking it for a benign pleomorphic adenoma on a small biopsy"],"management":["Complete surgical excision with clear margins as the curative mainstay for this low-grade minor salivary gland malignancy","Neck dissection where nodal metastasis is present, particularly in the cribriform-predominant variant","Adjuvant radiotherapy reserved for positive or close margins, extensive perineural invasion or recurrent disease","Accurate histopathological distinction from adenoid cystic carcinoma, aided by S100 positivity and a low Ki-67 index, to avoid unnecessary overtreatment","Long-term clinical and imaging surveillance for local recurrence and late nodal or distant metastasis","Head and neck multidisciplinary team management of surgery, pathology, reconstruction and rehabilitation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","Gnepp's Diagnostic Surgical Pathology of the Head and Neck","Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"polymyositis":{"name":"Polymyositis","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PM","idiopathic inflammatory myopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with progressive symmetric proximal muscle weakness affecting shoulder and hip girdles, causing difficulty rising from chairs, climbing stairs, and lifting the arms","Weakness rather than pain predominates, and there is no characteristic rash (distinguishing it from dermatomyositis)","Serum creatine kinase is typically markedly elevated, reflecting muscle fibre damage","Myositis-specific antibodies, including the antisynthetase antibodies (e.g. anti-Jo-1), help define clinical subsets","The antisynthetase syndrome combines myositis with interstitial lung disease, arthritis, Raynaud phenomenon, fever, and 'mechanic's hands'","It is a diagnosis of exclusion; inclusion body myositis in older patients and other myopathies must be considered before labelling weakness as polymyositis","Muscle biopsy classically shows endomysial CD8 T-cell infiltration surrounding and invading non-necrotic fibres","Corticosteroids are the mainstay of initial treatment, with steroid-sparing immunosuppressants added to control disease and limit steroid toxicity"],"pathophysiology":"Polymyositis is an idiopathic inflammatory myopathy driven by cell-mediated autoimmunity. Cytotoxic CD8 T lymphocytes recognise antigens expressed on muscle fibres and invade non-necrotic myofibres, causing fibre necrosis and regeneration and consequent weakness. Upregulation of MHC class I on muscle fibres is characteristic. Associations with specific autoantibodies define distinct phenotypes, and there is overlap with other connective tissue diseases and, less strongly than in dermatomyositis, with malignancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Dermatomyositis","Inclusion body myositis","Immune-mediated necrotising myopathy (including statin-associated)","Metabolic, mitochondrial, or muscular dystrophy","Endocrine myopathy (hypothyroidism, Cushing) and drug-induced myopathy","Motor neuron disease and myasthenia gravis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase and other muscle enzymes","Myositis-specific and myositis-associated antibody panel including anti-Jo-1","Electromyography showing a myopathic pattern with irritability","MRI of muscle to identify oedema and guide biopsy","Muscle biopsy for endomysial inflammation; screening for interstitial lung disease with pulmonary function tests and high-resolution CT"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive dyspnoea or hypoxia from interstitial lung disease, especially with antisynthetase antibodies","Bulbar or respiratory muscle weakness causing dysphagia, aspiration, or ventilatory compromise","Cardiac involvement with arrhythmia or heart failure","Features suggesting an underlying occult malignancy","Severe dysphagia risking aspiration pneumonia"],"prognosis":"With treatment many patients regain strength, but relapses are common and prognosis is worse in the presence of interstitial lung disease, cardiac involvement, severe dysphagia, or associated malignancy. Long-term functional outcome depends on early treatment and control of extramuscular disease.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing polymyositis without excluding inclusion body myositis and necrotising myopathy, which respond differently to therapy","Overlooking interstitial lung disease, a major cause of morbidity and mortality","Attributing weakness solely to steroid myopathy during treatment without reassessing disease activity","Failing to screen for dysphagia and aspiration risk","Missing an associated malignancy or overlap connective tissue disease"],"management":["High-dose corticosteroids as first-line therapy to suppress the cell-mediated myositis","Early introduction of a steroid-sparing immunosuppressant such as methotrexate or azathioprine, with intravenous immunoglobulin or rituximab for refractory disease","Treatment of antisynthetase-associated interstitial lung disease with agents such as mycophenolate, ciclosporin or cyclophosphamide","Physiotherapy to preserve and rebuild muscle strength, with speech and language therapy and aspiration precautions for dysphagia","Monitoring of creatine kinase and objective muscle strength to gauge response, with surveillance for interstitial lung and cardiac involvement","Age-appropriate malignancy screening given the association with underlying cancer","Bone protection and Pneumocystis prophylaxis to cover prolonged immunosuppression","Rheumatology-led multidisciplinary care, reconsidering the diagnosis where inclusion body myositis is possible"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"polypharmacy":{"name":"Polypharmacy","system":"Geriatrics / Palliative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["multiple medications","inappropriate polypharmacy","medication overload","deprescribing"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hazzard's Geriatric Medicine and Gerontology / palliative care guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Polypharmacy is commonly defined by the concurrent use of five or more medicines, but the clinically important question is whether each drug remains appropriate and beneficial","Each additional medication increases the risk of adverse drug reactions, drug-drug and drug-disease interactions, and poor adherence","Prescribing cascades occur when the side effect of one drug is misread as a new problem and treated with another drug rather than by stopping the offending agent","Age-related changes in pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics, particularly reduced renal clearance and heightened CNS sensitivity, amplify drug risks in older adults","Explicit tools such as the Beers criteria and the STOPP/START criteria help identify potentially inappropriate medications and prescribing omissions","Anticholinergic burden from multiple drugs contributes to confusion, falls, constipation, and urinary retention and should be tallied and minimised","Deprescribing is a structured, shared-decision process of tapering or stopping drugs whose harms outweigh benefits, aligned with the patient's goals and life expectancy","Regular structured medication reconciliation and review, especially at transitions of care, is the key preventive measure"],"pathophysiology":"With ageing, reduced lean body mass and total body water, increased fat, declining hepatic metabolism, and especially reduced renal excretion alter drug distribution and clearance, prolonging exposure to many agents and their metabolites. Pharmacodynamic sensitivity also changes, notably increased susceptibility to central nervous system depressants, anticholinergics, and drugs affecting blood pressure. As the number of medications rises, the probability of interactions grows combinatorially, and overlapping adverse effects (sedation, hypotension, anticholinergic load) summate to produce falls, delirium, and functional decline.","additionalDifferentials":["Adverse drug reaction masquerading as a new disease","Prescribing cascade generating apparently new symptoms","Non-adherence causing apparent treatment failure","Drug-disease interaction worsening an existing condition","Delirium or cognitive decline attributable to cumulative drug burden"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured medication reconciliation including over-the-counter drugs, supplements, and herbal products","Application of explicit criteria such as Beers and STOPP/START during medication review","Calculation of anticholinergic burden","Renal function assessment to guide dose adjustment and drug selection","Review of adherence, including pill counts or pharmacy refill data where available"],"redFlags":["New falls, confusion, or delirium temporally linked to a medication change","Symptoms of drug toxicity such as bradycardia, hypotension, bleeding, or hypoglycaemia","Multiple sedating or anticholinergic drugs used together","Unexplained functional or cognitive decline that may be iatrogenic"],"prognosis":"Inappropriate polypharmacy is associated with increased adverse drug events, hospitalisation, falls, cognitive impairment, and mortality. Thoughtful deprescribing and ongoing medication optimisation can reduce these harms and improve quality of life without compromising the treatment of important conditions.","pitfalls":["Adding a drug to treat the side effect of another instead of reassessing the original agent","Continuing medications indefinitely without reassessing ongoing indication or benefit","Failing to adjust doses for declining renal function","Stopping several drugs abruptly and precipitating withdrawal effects rather than tapering","Overlooking over-the-counter drugs and supplements as sources of interactions"],"management":["Structured medication review with deprescribing of drugs no longer beneficial, guided by explicit tools such as the Beers and STOPP/START criteria","Recognise and unwind prescribing cascades by stopping the offending agent rather than adding another drug","Tally and minimise cumulative anticholinergic burden to reduce confusion, falls, constipation and urinary retention","Adjust drug selection and dosing for age-related reduced renal clearance and heightened central nervous system sensitivity","Complete medication reconciliation covering over-the-counter, supplement and herbal use, aligned with the patient's goals of care","Support adherence with simplified regimens and dosing aids, and monitor for withdrawal effects or symptom recurrence after deprescribing","Involve a pharmacist and the wider multidisciplinary team in ongoing medication optimisation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Hazzard's Geriatric Medicine and Gerontology"],"crossLinks":[]},"polysplenia":{"name":"polysplenia","system":"cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["left atrial isomerism","left isomerism","bilateral left-sidedness"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children, and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Polysplenia is the visceral hallmark of left atrial isomerism (bilateral left-sidedness), with multiple small spleens usually clustered along the greater curvature of the stomach.","Bilateral bilobed lungs with hyparterial bronchi and paired morphologically left atrial appendages are the defining thoracic features.","Interruption of the intrahepatic inferior vena cava with azygos or hemiazygos continuation is a characteristic and surgically important finding.","Cardiac lesions are typically less severe than in asplenia but commonly include atrioventricular septal defect, partial anomalous pulmonary venous connection and left-sided obstructive lesions.","Sinus node dysfunction and congenital complete heart block are frequent because the sinus node is hypoplastic, malpositioned or absent.","Extracardiac associations include intestinal malrotation, a midline symmetrical liver, and biliary atresia, the last carrying significant hepatic morbidity.","Despite multiple spleens, splenic function may be reduced, so immunisation status and splenic reserve should be assessed rather than assumed.","Some individuals with mild cardiac anatomy present in adulthood incidentally on imaging performed for unrelated reasons."],"pathophysiology":"Polysplenia reflects a laterality defect in which the embryo fails to establish rightward differences and instead duplicates left-sided morphology on both sides of the body. Disruption of nodal cilia-driven leftward flow and the downstream Nodal-Lefty-Pitx2 pathway leads to bilateral 'left' patterning, so the lungs are both bilobed with hyparterial bronchi and the atrial appendages are both of morphologically left type. The spleen, normally a single left-sided organ, forms as multiple splenic nodules. Because the sinus node is a right-sided structure that fails to develop normally in bilateral left-sidedness, conduction is frequently abnormal with atrial rhythms or heart block. Interruption of the hepatic segment of the inferior vena cava occurs because the right-sided venous confluence does not form, and systemic venous return is rerouted through the azygos system. Associated abnormalities of gut rotation and of the biliary tree arise from the same failure of asymmetric organogenesis.","additionalDifferentials":["asplenia syndrome (right isomerism)","situs inversus with normal spleen","accessory spleens or splenosis in situs solitus","isolated interrupted inferior vena cava with azygos continuation","isolated congenital heart block","biliary atresia of non-syndromic type","heterotaxy without clear isomerism pattern"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["abdominal ultrasound demonstrating multiple splenic nodules and hepatic symmetry","echocardiography with attention to systemic and pulmonary venous drainage","cross-sectional imaging showing interrupted inferior vena cava with azygos continuation","twelve-lead ECG and ambulatory monitoring for sinus node dysfunction or heart block","peripheral blood film to assess functional splenic activity","hepatobiliary imaging where biliary atresia is suspected in infancy","genetic testing for laterality and ciliopathy genes in selected patients"],"redFlags":["bradyarrhythmia, syncope or antenatally detected complete heart block","bilious vomiting or abdominal signs suggesting volvulus from malrotation","conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia and pale stools indicating biliary atresia","signs of overwhelming infection where splenic function is impaired","progressive cyanosis or heart failure from evolving cardiac lesions"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally more favourable than right isomerism and depends on the complexity of the cardiac defect, the presence of heart block, and whether biliary atresia supervenes. Many patients achieve good long-term survival with staged cardiac repair, pacing where indicated, and vigilant management of hepatic and infective complications.","pitfalls":["Assuming normal splenic immune function simply because multiple spleens are present.","Missing interruption of the inferior vena cava before catheterisation or cardiac surgery.","Overlooking evolving heart block that may be silent until syncope or fetal compromise.","Failing to investigate neonatal cholestasis for associated biliary atresia.","Not screening for intestinal malrotation, risking acute volvulus."],"management":["Staged surgical repair of the associated cardiac defects such as atrioventricular septal defect and anomalous pulmonary venous connection","Permanent pacing for sinus node dysfunction or congenital complete heart block","Assess splenic function and, where functional hyposplenism is present, give pneumococcal and other immunisations against encapsulated organisms with prophylactic antibiotics","Surgical correction of intestinal malrotation by a Ladd procedure, with vigilance for midgut volvulus","Timely management of biliary atresia by Kasai portoenterostomy, progressing to liver transplantation if hepatic failure supervenes","Electrocardiographic and ambulatory rhythm surveillance for evolving conduction disease","Paediatric cardiology-led multidisciplinary care coordinating cardiac, hepatobiliary and gastrointestinal management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children, and Adolescents","Anderson's Paediatric Cardiology","Radiological reviews of heterotaxy and situs anomalies"],"crossLinks":[]},"polytrauma":{"name":"Polytrauma","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["multiple trauma","multisystem trauma","major trauma"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Assess and resuscitate simultaneously using the primary survey sequence: airway with cervical spine protection, breathing, circulation with haemorrhage control, disability, and exposure with environment control","Life threats identified during the primary survey are treated as they are found, before moving to the next step","The most common preventable cause of early death is uncontrolled haemorrhage, so external bleeding is compressed and internal sources are sought promptly","Permissive hypotension and balanced resuscitation with blood products help avoid dilutional coagulopathy in bleeding patients until definitive control is achieved","The lethal triad of hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy worsens outcomes and guides damage-control strategies","A structured secondary survey (head-to-toe examination and history) is performed only once the patient is stabilised, to detect injuries missed initially","Reassess frequently, as trauma physiology is dynamic and deterioration can be rapid","Adjuncts such as focused ultrasound, chest and pelvic imaging, and blood gases are integrated into the primary survey"],"pathophysiology":"Polytrauma involves injury to two or more body regions or systems, at least one of which is life-threatening. Simultaneous tissue damage triggers haemorrhage, hypovolaemic shock, and a systemic inflammatory response. Hypoperfusion causes tissue hypoxia and lactic acidosis, while ongoing bleeding and crystalloid dilution impair clotting. Hypothermia further inhibits enzymatic coagulation, and the resulting lethal triad of hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy establishes a self-reinforcing cycle. Later, immune activation predisposes to systemic inflammatory response syndrome, acute respiratory distress, and multi-organ dysfunction.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated major haemorrhage from a single cavity (chest, abdomen, pelvis, long bones)","Neurogenic shock from spinal cord injury","Cardiac tamponade or tension pneumothorax as the cause of shock","Medical collapse (e.g. cardiac event, seizure) preceding a traumatic mechanism","Toxicological or metabolic contribution to altered consciousness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Focused assessment with sonography for trauma (FAST/eFAST) to detect free fluid and pneumothorax","Chest and pelvic radiographs as primary-survey adjuncts","Whole-body CT (trauma pan-scan) in the stabilised patient to map injuries","Arterial blood gas with lactate and base deficit to gauge shock severity","Group and crossmatch, coagulation studies, and viscoelastic testing to guide transfusion"],"redFlags":["Persistent or recurrent hypotension despite resuscitation, indicating ongoing haemorrhage requiring immediate surgical or interventional control","Rising lactate or worsening base deficit signalling inadequate perfusion","Signs of the lethal triad (hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy) mandating damage-control resuscitation","Deteriorating conscious level suggesting evolving brain injury or shock","Mechanism or examination suggesting occult truncal or pelvic bleeding"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on injury burden, time to haemorrhage control, and physiological reserve. Early definitive control of bleeding and correction of the lethal triad improve survival, whereas prolonged shock and multi-organ dysfunction carry high mortality.","pitfalls":["Completing the secondary survey before life threats in the primary survey are addressed","Over-resuscitation with crystalloid worsening dilutional coagulopathy and hypothermia","Anchoring on an obvious dramatic injury while missing a silent source of shock","Failing to reassess after each intervention as physiology evolves","Neglecting active warming and haemostatic resuscitation in the bleeding patient"],"management":["Simultaneous assessment and resuscitation by the primary survey sequence, treating life threats to airway, breathing and circulation as they are found with cervical spine protection","Immediate control of catastrophic external haemorrhage and prompt identification of internal bleeding, with damage-control surgery to arrest it","Damage-control resuscitation using balanced blood-product transfusion, permissive hypotension and early tranexamic acid until bleeding is controlled","Aggressive correction of the lethal triad by active rewarming, restoring perfusion to reverse acidosis and correcting coagulopathy guided by viscoelastic testing","Structured secondary survey once stabilised to detect missed injuries, followed by definitive fracture fixation and organ repair","Critical care support for shock, acute respiratory distress and multi-organ dysfunction","Early rehabilitation and multidisciplinary trauma team coordination across the surgical specialties"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"pomc_deficiency":{"name":"Proopiomelanocortin (POMC) Deficiency","system":"Gastrointestinal / Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["POMC deficiency obesity","Proopiomelanocortin deficiency syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric endocrinology and genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal recessive monogenic obesity syndrome caused by loss-of-function variants in POMC, the precursor of ACTH and the melanocortin peptides.","The classic triad is early-onset severe hyperphagic obesity, secondary adrenal insufficiency from absent ACTH, and pale skin with red hair from lack of alpha-MSH signalling at the melanocortin-1 receptor.","Neonatal presentation can be dramatic — hypoglycaemia, cholestatic jaundice and adrenal crisis from ACTH deficiency before obesity becomes apparent.","Hyperphagia and weight gain result from loss of alpha-MSH agonism at hypothalamic melanocortin-4 receptors that normally suppress appetite.","Red hair may be absent or subtle in individuals of darker constitutive pigmentation, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis.","Melanocortin-4 receptor agonist therapy (setmelanotide) bypasses the missing endogenous ligand and can produce substantial appetite reduction and weight loss.","Lifelong glucocorticoid replacement is essential, and stress dosing must be intensified during illness to prevent adrenal crisis.","Distinguish biochemically from congenital adrenal disorders — here cortisol is low with inappropriately low ACTH, reflecting a central rather than primary adrenal defect."],"pathophysiology":"Proopiomelanocortin deficiency is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the POMC gene, whose polypeptide product is post-translationally cleaved by prohormone convertases into several bioactive peptides: adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH), alpha- and beta-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (MSH), beta-endorphin and others. Absence of functional POMC-derived peptides produces a characteristic combination of deficits. Lack of ACTH leaves the adrenal cortex unstimulated, causing secondary (central) adrenal insufficiency with glucocorticoid deficiency while the renin-angiotensin-driven mineralocorticoid axis is preserved. Loss of alpha-MSH action at melanocortin-1 receptors in the skin and hair follicles removes the eumelanin signal, producing pale skin and red hair. Most importantly for the metabolic phenotype, alpha-MSH is the key agonist at hypothalamic melanocortin-4 receptors within the leptin-melanocortin satiety pathway; without it, the anorexigenic tone downstream of leptin is lost, driving intense hyperphagia and early-onset severe obesity despite intact leptin and leptin-receptor function. Because the block lies at ligand production, therapy with an exogenous melanocortin-4 receptor agonist can restore satiety signalling. The syndrome exemplifies how a single precursor defect yields a predictable multi-system endocrine, pigmentary and metabolic picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Leptin deficiency and leptin-receptor deficiency (severe early-onset obesity without adrenal or pigment features)","Prohormone convertase 1/3 (PCSK1) deficiency with malabsorptive diarrhoea and multiple hormone defects","Melanocortin-4 receptor mutation obesity (receptor-level defect, normal adrenal and pigment)","Prader-Willi syndrome and other syndromic obesity","Congenital secondary adrenal insufficiency from hypopituitarism or TBX19/TPIT mutation","Primary adrenal insufficiency including congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Neonatal cholestatic and hypoglycaemic disorders of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic sequencing of POMC and related melanocortin-pathway genes","Paired morning cortisol and ACTH demonstrating central adrenal insufficiency","ACTH stimulation and, where indicated, dynamic pituitary-adrenal testing","Plasma leptin and leptin-receptor evaluation to exclude leptin-axis defects","Assessment of growth, pubertal status and wider pituitary hormone axes","Blood glucose monitoring for neonatal and stress hypoglycaemia","Liver function and conjugated bilirubin in the cholestatic neonatal presentation"],"redFlags":["Neonatal or childhood adrenal crisis with hypoglycaemia, hypotension and collapse","Intercurrent illness without appropriate stress glucocorticoid escalation","Rapid, relentless weight gain with extreme food-seeking behaviour","Cholestatic jaundice and hypoglycaemia in a newborn suggesting ACTH deficiency","Signs of undertreated cortisol deficiency such as persistent vomiting and lethargy"],"prognosis":"Adrenal insufficiency is life-threatening but manageable with lifelong glucocorticoid replacement and stress dosing, and targeted melanocortin-4 receptor agonist therapy has markedly improved control of the previously intractable hyperphagia and obesity. Long-term outcome depends on avoidance of adrenal crises and sustained weight management with its associated metabolic complications.","pitfalls":["Interpreting low cortisol as primary adrenal failure and missing the central (low-ACTH) mechanism","Excluding the diagnosis because red hair is absent in a darker-skinned child","Overlooking the adrenal component and focusing only on obesity, risking fatal crisis","Failing to intensify glucocorticoids during illness, surgery or fasting","Attributing severe early obesity to lifestyle and not pursuing monogenic testing"],"management":["Lifelong glucocorticoid replacement for secondary (central) adrenal insufficiency, with stress dosing during illness","Melanocortin-4 receptor agonist therapy with setmelanotide to bypass the absent alpha-MSH signal and reduce hyperphagia and obesity","Dietetic support and structured weight management alongside pharmacotherapy","Emergency recognition and treatment of adrenal crisis and neonatal hypoglycaemia with parenteral glucocorticoid and glucose, plus a steroid emergency card","Monitoring of growth, puberty and other pituitary hormone axes, and management of neonatal cholestatic jaundice","Endocrinology-led care with genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric endocrinology textbook chapters on monogenic and syndromic obesity","Clinical guidance on management of central adrenal insufficiency in children","Reference texts on the leptin-melanocortin pathway and inherited obesity"],"crossLinks":[]},"pompe_disease":{"name":"Pompe disease","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency","acid maltase deficiency","glycogen storage disease type II","GSD II"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard metabolic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder caused by deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme acid alpha-glucosidase (acid maltase), resulting in lysosomal glycogen accumulation; it is classified as glycogen storage disease type II","It is unusual among the glycogenoses in being a lysosomal disorder rather than affecting cytoplasmic glycogen metabolism, so fasting hypoglycaemia is not a feature","The classic infantile form presents in the first months with profound hypotonia, a rapidly enlarging heart (hypertrophic cardiomyopathy), and feeding and respiratory difficulty","A markedly enlarged tongue and prominent cardiomegaly on chest imaging are useful clues in infants","Late-onset (juvenile and adult) forms present with progressive proximal and respiratory muscle weakness and often lack significant cardiac involvement","Diaphragmatic weakness can cause respiratory failure that is disproportionate to limb weakness","Serum creatine kinase is commonly elevated, reflecting the underlying myopathy","Enzyme replacement therapy has transformed the outlook, particularly in infantile disease, and newborn screening enables earlier treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Pompe disease is caused by pathogenic variants in the GAA gene encoding acid alpha-glucosidase, the lysosomal enzyme that degrades glycogen to glucose within the lysosome. Deficient activity leads to progressive lysosomal glycogen accumulation, most damaging in cardiac and skeletal muscle. Lysosomal engorgement and subsequent autophagic dysfunction impair muscle architecture and function, producing cardiomegaly and hypotonia in infants and progressive skeletal and respiratory muscle weakness in later-onset disease. The degree of residual enzyme activity broadly correlates with age of onset and severity.","additionalDifferentials":["Spinal muscular atrophy and other causes of infantile hypotonia","Congenital muscular dystrophies and congenital myopathies","Other causes of infantile hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophies in later-onset presentations","Other glycogen storage disorders affecting muscle"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assay of acid alpha-glucosidase activity, including dried blood spot screening with confirmatory testing","GAA molecular genetic testing","Serum creatine kinase measurement","Echocardiography and ECG to assess cardiomyopathy in infants","Pulmonary function testing and muscle assessment in later-onset disease"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy in an infant","Respiratory compromise from diaphragmatic weakness","Severe feeding difficulty with failure to thrive","Progressive respiratory decline out of proportion to limb weakness in older patients"],"prognosis":"Untreated classic infantile disease is usually fatal within the first year from cardiorespiratory failure. Enzyme replacement therapy substantially improves survival and cardiac outcomes, especially when started early, though respiratory and motor sequelae may persist. Late-onset disease progresses more slowly, with respiratory function being a key determinant of long-term outcome.","pitfalls":["Expecting hypoglycaemia and being misled when it is absent, since this is a lysosomal rather than cytoplasmic glycogenosis","Attributing infantile hypotonia to a neuromuscular cause without checking for cardiomyopathy","Underestimating respiratory muscle involvement in later-onset disease","Delaying treatment when early initiation most influences outcome"],"management":["Enzyme replacement therapy with recombinant acid alpha-glucosidase (alglucosidase alfa or avalglucosidase alfa) as the disease-specific mainstay, started as early as possible in infantile disease","Immune tolerance induction with agents such as rituximab, methotrexate and immunoglobulin in CRIM-negative infants to limit neutralising anti-enzyme antibodies","Respiratory support for diaphragmatic and respiratory muscle weakness, including non-invasive or invasive ventilation and airway clearance","Cardiac monitoring and heart-failure management for the hypertrophic cardiomyopathy of the infantile form","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and mobility aids, with dietetic support and safe-swallow measures for feeding difficulty","Regular surveillance of respiratory function, motor status, cardiac status and anti-enzyme antibody titres","Metabolic medicine-led multidisciplinary care with genetic counselling and family testing for autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"pomt1_muscular_dystrophy":{"name":"POMT1-related muscular dystrophy-dystroglycanopathy","system":"Neuromuscular / Central nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["POMT1","Walker-Warburg syndrome (POMT1)","MDDGA1","alpha-dystroglycanopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical neuromuscular and medical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["POMT1 encodes protein O-mannosyltransferase 1, which initiates O-mannosyl glycosylation of alpha-dystroglycan; loss of function disrupts the glycan that bridges muscle and brain basement membranes to the extracellular matrix.","It is the archetypal cause of Walker-Warburg syndrome, the most severe end of the dystroglycanopathy spectrum, presenting at birth with profound hypotonia, weakness and a raised serum creatine kinase.","The classic triad combines congenital muscular dystrophy, a cobblestone (type II) lissencephaly with neuronal overmigration, and structural eye malformations.","Brain imaging typically shows cobblestone cortex, hydrocephalus, a kinked or hypoplastic brainstem, cerebellar hypoplasia with cysts and absent or thin corpus callosum.","Milder allelic phenotypes exist, spanning muscle-eye-brain-like disease and limb-girdle muscular dystrophy with or without intellectual disability, depending on residual enzyme activity.","Ocular findings may include microphthalmia, cataract, retinal dysplasia or detachment, buphthalmos and optic nerve hypoplasia.","Immunohistochemistry of muscle shows reduced or absent glycosylated alpha-dystroglycan despite preserved core protein, the hallmark of a dystroglycanopathy.","Inheritance is autosomal recessive, so both parents are typically unaffected carriers and recurrence risk is substantial for future pregnancies."],"pathophysiology":"Biallelic loss-of-function variants in POMT1 abolish or reduce protein O-mannosyltransferase 1 activity. Working in an obligate complex with POMT2, this enzyme catalyses the first committed step of O-mannosylation, attaching mannose to serine and threonine residues of alpha-dystroglycan. This initiates assembly of the matriglycan chain that allows alpha-dystroglycan to bind laminin, agrin, perlecan and neurexin in the basement membrane. When glycosylation fails, alpha-dystroglycan can no longer anchor the sarcolemma or the pial glia-limitans to the extracellular matrix. In muscle this produces membrane fragility and a dystrophic process; in brain the breached glia-limitans permits neurones to overmigrate beyond the cortical surface, generating cobblestone lissencephaly; and in the developing eye disrupted matrix interactions cause the characteristic malformations. The severity of the phenotype correlates broadly with the amount of residual functional glycosyltransferase activity.","additionalDifferentials":["POMT2-related dystroglycanopathy (clinically indistinguishable Walker-Warburg phenotype)","Fukutin (FKTN) and FKRP-related dystroglycanopathies","Muscle-eye-brain disease due to POMGNT1","Fukuyama congenital muscular dystrophy","Merosin (LAMA2)-deficient congenital muscular dystrophy","Other congenital muscular dystrophies with structural brain involvement","Congenital myotonic dystrophy and congenital myopathies with hypotonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase (typically markedly elevated)","Brain MRI to characterise cobblestone cortex, brainstem and cerebellar anomalies and hydrocephalus","Ophthalmological assessment including examination for microphthalmia, cataract and retinal dysplasia","Muscle biopsy with immunohistochemistry for glycosylated alpha-dystroglycan","Molecular genetic testing of POMT1 and a dystroglycanopathy gene panel","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies where diagnosis is uncertain","Prenatal or fetal imaging and genetic counselling for at-risk pregnancies"],"redFlags":["Progressive hydrocephalus with raised intracranial pressure requiring urgent neurosurgical assessment","Neonatal respiratory failure or feeding failure with aspiration risk","Seizures, particularly in the neonatal period","Rapidly worsening vision or signs of retinal detachment","Recurrent chest infections from respiratory muscle weakness"],"prognosis":"The Walker-Warburg end of the POMT1 spectrum carries a very poor prognosis, with most severely affected infants dying within the first years of life. Milder allelic forms, including limb-girdle presentations, can be compatible with prolonged survival, though with variable weakness and cognitive involvement.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal-appearing muscle biopsy core protein excludes dystroglycanopathy; the defect is in glycosylation, not the protein itself.","Attributing the brain malformation to an acquired antenatal insult rather than a genetic glycosylation defect.","Overlooking subtle ocular malformations that help distinguish dystroglycanopathies from other congenital muscular dystrophies.","Failing to recognise milder limb-girdle phenotypes as allelic to the severe congenital form.","Not offering genetic counselling and recurrence-risk discussion once autosomal recessive inheritance is confirmed."],"management":["Supportive, multidisciplinary care as no disease-specific or curative therapy exists for the dystroglycanopathy","Ventriculoperitoneal shunting for hydrocephalus and antiseizure medication for epilepsy","Respiratory support and gastrostomy feeding for weakness, bulbar dysfunction and recurrent aspiration","Ophthalmological management of structural eye malformations such as cataract, retinal detachment and glaucoma","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and orthopaedic management of contractures, with seating and postural support","Palliative and family-centred care planning for the severe Walker-Warburg end of the spectrum given its very poor prognosis","Genetic counselling, carrier testing and prenatal or preimplantation diagnosis given autosomal recessive POMT1 inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbooks of paediatric neurology and neuromuscular disease","Peer-reviewed reviews of the dystroglycanopathies","Clinical medical genetics reference resources"],"crossLinks":[]},"pomt2_muscular_dystrophy":{"name":"POMT2-related muscular dystrophy-dystroglycanopathy","system":"Neuromuscular / Central nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["POMT2","MDDGA2","Walker-Warburg syndrome (POMT2)","alpha-dystroglycanopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical neuromuscular and medical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["POMT2 encodes protein O-mannosyltransferase 2, the obligate partner of POMT1; only the POMT1-POMT2 heterocomplex has O-mannosyltransferase activity, so a defect in either gene produces overlapping disease.","Biallelic POMT2 variants cause a dystroglycanopathy spectrum ranging from Walker-Warburg syndrome and muscle-eye-brain-like disease to milder limb-girdle muscular dystrophy.","Severe neonatal cases present with profound hypotonia, weakness, contractures and a markedly raised creatine kinase, mirroring POMT1 disease clinically.","Cobblestone (type II) lissencephaly, cerebellar and brainstem hypoplasia, hydrocephalus and eye malformations characterise the severe end of the spectrum.","Milder POMT2 limb-girdle phenotypes may present later with proximal weakness, calf hypertrophy and preserved or only mildly reduced cognition.","Muscle immunohistochemistry shows reduced glycosylated alpha-dystroglycan while beta-dystroglycan and the protein core are preserved.","Because POMT1 and POMT2 are clinically indistinguishable, molecular testing (usually a gene panel) is required to assign the specific gene.","Inheritance is autosomal recessive with a meaningful recurrence risk warranting genetic counselling."],"pathophysiology":"POMT2 encodes protein O-mannosyltransferase 2, which forms an obligate heterodimeric complex with POMT1 in the endoplasmic reticulum. Neither subunit has catalytic activity alone; together they transfer mannose from dolichol-phosphate-mannose to serine and threonine residues of alpha-dystroglycan, initiating the O-mannosyl glycan later extended into the laminin-binding matriglycan chain. Biallelic loss-of-function or hypomorphic POMT2 variants therefore abolish or reduce O-mannosylation just as POMT1 defects do. Hypoglycosylated alpha-dystroglycan cannot bind laminin and other extracellular-matrix ligands, so the sarcolemma loses its link to the basal lamina (producing muscle fibre degeneration) and the pial glia-limitans fails, allowing cortical neurones to overmigrate through breaches in the basement membrane to create cobblestone lissencephaly. Disrupted matrix signalling during ocular development yields the associated eye malformations. Residual complex activity determines where a given patient sits on the phenotypic spectrum.","additionalDifferentials":["POMT1-related dystroglycanopathy (clinically identical)","POMGNT1-related muscle-eye-brain disease","Fukutin (FKTN) and FKRP-related dystroglycanopathies","Fukuyama congenital muscular dystrophy","LAMA2 (merosin)-deficient congenital muscular dystrophy","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophies of other genetic causes","Congenital myopathies and congenital myotonic dystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase measurement","Brain MRI for cobblestone cortex, posterior fossa anomalies and ventricular dilatation","Detailed ophthalmological examination","Muscle biopsy with alpha-dystroglycan glycosylation immunohistochemistry","Targeted POMT2 sequencing within a dystroglycanopathy gene panel","Cardiac and respiratory assessment in later-presenting limb-girdle cases","Genetic counselling and carrier testing for the family"],"redFlags":["Progressive hydrocephalus with signs of raised intracranial pressure","Neonatal respiratory or bulbar failure with feeding difficulty","New or worsening seizures","Deteriorating vision suggesting retinal detachment or glaucoma","Emerging cardiac symptoms or arrhythmia in limb-girdle presentations"],"prognosis":"Severe Walker-Warburg-type POMT2 disease is usually fatal in early childhood. Muscle-eye-brain-like and limb-girdle presentations have a more variable course, with slowly progressive weakness and, in milder cases, survival into adulthood.","pitfalls":["Trying to separate POMT1 from POMT2 clinically or on imaging; only molecular testing distinguishes them.","Interpreting a normal dystroglycan core protein on immunoblot as excluding the diagnosis when the defect is glycosylation.","Missing a milder limb-girdle POMT2 phenotype by expecting only the catastrophic neonatal form.","Overlooking cardiac or respiratory surveillance in patients who survive to later childhood or adulthood.","Not arranging genetic counselling once autosomal recessive inheritance is established."],"management":["Supportive, multidisciplinary management, as no disease-modifying therapy exists across the dystroglycanopathy spectrum","Antiseizure medication for epilepsy and ventriculoperitoneal shunting for hydrocephalus in severe presentations","Respiratory monitoring and support with gastrostomy feeding where bulbar weakness threatens safe swallowing","Ophthalmological assessment and treatment of associated eye malformations","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and orthopaedic care for contractures and progressive proximal weakness, including in milder limb-girdle presentations","Cardiac and respiratory surveillance in later-presenting limb-girdle phenotypes","Genetic counselling with carrier and prenatal testing given autosomal recessive POMT2 inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbooks of paediatric neurology and neuromuscular disease","Peer-reviewed reviews of alpha-dystroglycanopathies","Clinical medical genetics reference resources"],"crossLinks":[]},"popcorn_workers_lung":{"name":"Popcorn worker's lung","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["diacetyl bronchiolitis obliterans","flavourings-related lung disease","obliterative bronchiolitis from diacetyl","bronchiolitis obliterans"],"chapter":null,"source":"Occupational and Environmental Respiratory Medicine reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Linked to inhalation of the butter-flavouring agent diacetyl (and its substitute 2,3-pentanedione) in microwave popcorn, flavouring, and food-manufacturing plants","Causes constrictive (obliterative) bronchiolitis, producing fixed airflow obstruction that mimics severe asthma or COPD in a young non-smoker","Symptoms of cough and exertional breathlessness are often insidious and progressive and may not reverse with bronchodilators","Spirometry shows an obstructive pattern; CT typically reveals mosaic attenuation and air trapping on expiratory imaging rather than parenchymal opacities","The chest radiograph is frequently normal or near-normal, which can lead to underdiagnosis","Small-airway scarring narrows and obliterates terminal bronchioles, and established disease is largely irreversible","Recognition of an occupational cluster among flavouring or food-production workers is a critical diagnostic pointer"],"pathophysiology":"Volatile alpha-diketones such as diacetyl, released when heated flavourings are handled, are inhaled and cause direct toxic injury to the epithelium of the small conducting airways. The ensuing inflammation and repair lead to concentric peribronchiolar fibrosis that narrows and ultimately obliterates the lumen of terminal and respiratory bronchioles (constrictive bronchiolitis). Because the injury is confined largely to the small airways with sparing of the surrounding parenchyma, gas exchange may be relatively preserved while airflow obstruction becomes fixed and progressive. The scarring is not reversible, so the emphasis is on early detection and exposure cessation.","additionalDifferentials":["Asthma (including occupational asthma)","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Flock worker's lung","Post-infectious bronchiolitis obliterans","Bronchiolitis obliterans after transplantation or connective tissue disease","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Spirometry demonstrating fixed airflow obstruction with limited bronchodilator response","High-resolution CT with expiratory views to detect mosaic attenuation and air trapping","Detailed occupational history including flavouring and food-manufacturing exposure","Lung volumes and diffusing capacity","Lung biopsy showing constrictive bronchiolitis when diagnosis is uncertain","Serial lung-function surveillance of exposed workers"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive fixed airflow obstruction in a young non-smoker","Continued workplace exposure after symptom onset","Severe irreversible obstruction with respiratory failure","A cluster of affected workers signalling ongoing hazardous exposure"],"prognosis":"Constrictive bronchiolitis from diacetyl is typically irreversible; some patients stabilise after removal from exposure, but others progress to severe disabling airflow obstruction and may require consideration of transplantation.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the disease for ordinary asthma and relying on bronchodilators that do not reverse fixed obstruction","Being reassured by a normal chest radiograph and omitting expiratory CT","Missing the occupational history in a young non-smoker with obstruction","Delaying removal from exposure until irreversible damage has occurred"],"management":["Immediate and permanent removal from diacetyl and 2,3-pentanedione exposure, with workplace substitution, engineering controls and respiratory protection, as scarring is irreversible and cessation is the only disease-modifying step","Trial of inhaled bronchodilators and inhaled corticosteroids, recognising that the fixed obstruction of constrictive bronchiolitis usually responds poorly","Pulmonary rehabilitation, smoking cessation and influenza and pneumococcal immunisation to optimise residual respiratory function","Long-term supplemental oxygen for chronic hypoxaemia in advanced disease","Serial spirometry surveillance of the patient and of co-exposed workers to detect progression early","Referral to an occupational lung disease specialist, with formal reporting as an occupational disease","Referral for lung transplantation assessment when obstruction becomes severe and progressive despite exposure cessation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Parkes' Occupational Lung Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"popliteal_artery_aneurysm":{"name":"Popliteal artery aneurysm","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Popliteal aneurysm","PAA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest peripheral (non-aortic) arterial aneurysm, defined as focal dilatation of the popliteal artery, commonly more than 50% of the adjacent normal diameter or an absolute diameter typically exceeding around 2 cm.","Strongly associated with male sex, smoking, hypertension and coexisting abdominal aortic aneurysm.","Bilateral in roughly half of cases, and frequently associated with an abdominal aortic aneurysm, so both popliteal fossae and the aorta must be imaged.","Complications are predominantly thromboembolic (acute limb ischaemia, distal embolisation, progressive obliteration of the tibial run-off) rather than rupture, which is uncommon.","A prominent, easily palpable popliteal pulse should raise suspicion; a pulsatile popliteal mass is aneurysmal until proven otherwise.","Presentation ranges from incidental and asymptomatic to intermittent claudication, limb-threatening acute ischaemia, or compressive symptoms such as venous obstruction and neuropathy.","Duplex ultrasound is first-line for diagnosis, sizing and surveillance; CT or MR angiography defines run-off for intervention planning.","Repair, by open bypass with aneurysm exclusion or endovascular stent-grafting, is indicated for symptomatic aneurysms and for asymptomatic aneurysms above a threshold size or with significant mural thrombus or poor run-off."],"pathophysiology":"The popliteal artery undergoes segmental degeneration of the media with loss of elastin and smooth muscle, collagen remodelling and matrix metalloproteinase-driven wall weakening, mirroring aortic aneurysmal disease. Repetitive flexion at the knee, turbulent flow and mural stress promote progressive dilatation and deposition of laminated thrombus within the sac. The stagnant flow within the redundant lumen favours thrombus accumulation that embolises distally, progressively occluding the tibial run-off, or acutely thromboses the aneurysm to produce severe acute limb ischaemia with a poorly developed collateral bed. Rupture is uncommon because of the confining structures of the popliteal fossa.","additionalDifferentials":["Baker's (popliteal) cyst","Popliteal artery entrapment syndrome","Deep vein thrombosis presenting with a swollen, painful calf","Cystic adventitial disease of the popliteal artery","Soft-tissue tumour or sarcoma of the popliteal fossa","Atherosclerotic occlusive disease causing claudication","Popliteal abscess or enlarged lymph nodes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound of both popliteal arteries for diagnosis, size and mural thrombus","Abdominal aortic and iliac ultrasound to exclude coexistent aneurysm","CT angiography of the lower limb","MR angiography as an alternative for run-off assessment","Catheter angiography, frequently with a view to thrombolysis or intervention","Ankle-brachial pressure index and arterial waveform studies","Dedicated imaging of the contralateral limb given the high bilaterality"],"redFlags":["Acute limb ischaemia with the six Ps (pain, pallor, pulselessness, paraesthesia, paralysis, perishing cold), which is limb-threatening.","Sudden loss of previously palpable distal pulses with a cold, painful foot.","Progressive rest pain, tissue loss or trash foot from distal embolisation.","A rapidly enlarging or tender popliteal mass.","Compressive neurological deficit or acute venous outflow obstruction."],"prognosis":"Asymptomatic aneurysms carry a substantial annual risk of thromboembolic complications, and once acute ischaemia occurs limb loss rates are high, particularly with poor distal run-off. Elective repair before complications develop yields good long-term patency and low amputation rates.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a pulsatile popliteal mass for a Baker's cyst and attempting aspiration.","Failing to screen the contralateral popliteal artery and the abdominal aorta.","Underestimating a thrombosed aneurysm because the mass is no longer pulsatile, delaying diagnosis in acute ischaemia.","Delaying repair of an asymptomatic aneurysm harbouring large mural thrombus or compromised run-off.","Attributing calf swelling solely to deep vein thrombosis and anticoagulating without imaging the artery."],"management":["Elective repair by open bypass with aneurysm exclusion, or endovascular stent-grafting in selected anatomy, for symptomatic, enlarging, or thrombus-laden aneurysms to pre-empt thromboembolic limb loss","Cardiovascular risk reduction with smoking cessation, antiplatelet therapy, a statin and blood-pressure control","Duplex ultrasound surveillance of small asymptomatic aneurysms with preserved run-off","Imaging of the contralateral popliteal artery and the aorta given frequent bilaterality and coexisting abdominal aortic aneurysm","Acute limb ischaemia managed as an emergency with catheter-directed thrombolysis to clear the tibial run-off followed by definitive revascularisation","Referral to vascular surgery for planning of repair and long-term graft surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","European Society for Vascular Surgery clinical practice guidelines on the management of peripheral arterial aneurysms","Oxford Textbook of Vascular Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"popliteal_artery_entrapment":{"name":"Popliteal artery entrapment syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAES","popliteal artery entrapment","popliteal entrapment syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy / Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An uncommon cause of exertional leg ischaemia in young, often athletic patients caused by an abnormal relationship between the popliteal artery and the surrounding musculotendinous structures","Presents with positional or exertional calf claudication, sometimes with foot paraesthesiae or coolness, in the absence of atherosclerotic risk factors","Symptoms are typically reproduced by exercise or by active plantarflexion and passive dorsiflexion, which tighten the compressing muscle","May be anatomical, from an aberrant course of the artery or medial head of gastrocnemius, or functional, from muscular hypertrophy compressing a normally positioned artery","Often bilateral, so the contralateral limb should be assessed even when only one side is symptomatic","Dynamic imaging with provocative positioning (duplex ultrasound, CT or MR angiography) is key, as resting studies can appear normal","Untreated compression can lead to arterial wall damage, aneurysm, thrombosis, or distal embolisation, so surgical release of the entrapment is the definitive treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Popliteal artery entrapment syndrome results from an abnormal anatomical or functional relationship between the popliteal artery and the muscles and tendons of the popliteal fossa, most often involving the medial head of gastrocnemius. In anatomical variants the artery follows an aberrant medial course or is crossed by an anomalous muscle slip, while in the functional form a normally positioned artery is compressed by hypertrophied musculature during contraction. Repeated dynamic compression, particularly during the plantarflexion of exercise, intermittently restricts arterial flow and produces exertional ischaemia. Over time the recurrent mechanical trauma damages the arterial wall, promoting intimal injury, post-stenotic dilatation or aneurysm formation, mural thrombus, and the risk of thrombotic occlusion or distal embolisation, so a functional problem can progress to fixed arterial disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic exertional compartment syndrome","Atherosclerotic peripheral arterial disease","Cystic adventitial disease of the popliteal artery","Medial tibial stress syndrome or musculoskeletal leg pain","Lumbar radiculopathy or neurogenic claudication","Deep vein thrombosis or venous claudication"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound with provocative plantarflexion and dorsiflexion manoeuvres","CT or MR angiography with dynamic positioning to demonstrate compression and define anatomy","Ankle-brachial pressure index at rest and after exercise or with positional stress","MRI of the popliteal fossa to characterise the musculotendinous anatomy","Catheter angiography with stress positioning in selected cases","Assessment of the contralateral limb given frequent bilaterality"],"redFlags":["Acute limb ischaemia from popliteal thrombosis or distal embolisation","A popliteal aneurysm complicating chronic entrapment","Rest pain or tissue loss indicating critical ischaemia","Rapidly progressive symptoms or loss of distal pulses"],"prognosis":"When recognised before fixed arterial damage develops, surgical release of the entrapment relieves symptoms and preserves the artery with good long-term outcomes. Delayed diagnosis allowing arterial degeneration, aneurysm, or thrombosis worsens the prognosis and may require arterial reconstruction.","pitfalls":["Relying on resting imaging that appears normal and omitting provocative dynamic studies","Attributing exertional leg pain to a musculoskeletal or compartment cause in a young athlete","Assessing only the symptomatic limb and missing bilateral disease","Delaying treatment until irreversible arterial damage or thrombosis has occurred","Confusing the syndrome with cystic adventitial disease, which requires different management"],"management":["Surgical release of the entrapment by myotomy or division of the anomalous medial head of gastrocnemius or accessory muscle slip, which is the definitive treatment","Arterial reconstruction with bypass or interposition graft when the vessel is already damaged by stenosis, post-stenotic aneurysm or thrombosis","Activity and exercise modification to limit provocative plantarflexion while awaiting surgery","Catheter-directed thrombolysis or thrombectomy for acute thrombotic occlusion, with antiplatelet therapy to reduce embolic risk","Assessment and treatment of the contralateral limb given the frequent bilaterality","Referral to vascular surgery for early decompression before fixed arterial degeneration develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"popliteal_artery_entrapment_syndrome":{"name":"Popliteal artery entrapment syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular / Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAES"],"chapter":null,"source":"Vascular surgery and sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An uncommon cause of exertional calf pain in young, often athletic, individuals due to compression of the popliteal artery","It should be considered when a fit young person has claudication without atherosclerotic risk factors","Symptoms are provoked by exercise, particularly activities using the calf muscles, and relieved by rest","Compression usually results from an abnormal anatomical relationship between the artery and the gastrocnemius or popliteus, though a functional form exists","Provocative manoeuvres with active plantarflexion can reproduce reduced pedal pulses and imaging changes","Untreated repetitive compression can damage the artery, causing stenosis, thrombosis or aneurysm, so surgery is often required"],"pathophysiology":"Popliteal artery entrapment syndrome arises when the popliteal artery is compressed within the popliteal fossa, most often by an anomalous course relative to the medial head of gastrocnemius or by an accessory muscle slip; a functional variant occurs with a hypertrophied but normally positioned muscle. Repetitive compression during exercise transiently reduces distal flow, causing claudication, and over time causes intimal injury that can progress to fixed stenosis, thrombosis or post-stenotic aneurysm with distal embolisation. The young age and absence of atherosclerotic risk factors distinguish it from typical peripheral arterial disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic exertional compartment syndrome","Medial tibial stress syndrome","Atherosclerotic peripheral arterial disease","Cystic adventitial disease of the popliteal artery","Nerve entrapment causing exertional leg pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ankle-brachial index and pulse assessment at rest and with provocation","Duplex ultrasound with active plantarflexion manoeuvres","CT or MR angiography, including provocative positioning, to show compression and anatomy","Assessment for arterial damage such as stenosis, thrombosis or aneurysm","Exclusion of compartment syndrome"],"redFlags":["Signs of arterial thrombosis or acute limb ischaemia","Distal embolisation or a popliteal aneurysm","Rest pain or tissue loss","Rapidly progressive symptoms"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and surgical release, and vascular reconstruction if the artery is damaged, outcomes are generally good; delayed diagnosis risks permanent arterial injury and limb-threatening complications.","pitfalls":["Dismissing exertional leg pain in a young athlete as musculoskeletal","Confusing it with chronic exertional compartment syndrome","Relying on resting vascular studies, which may be normal without provocation","Missing established arterial damage requiring reconstruction"],"management":["Operative decompression by dividing the compressing gastrocnemius or popliteus muscle slip or correcting the anomalous arterial course, the mainstay of treatment","Vascular reconstruction with vein bypass or interposition graft where recurrent compression has caused fixed stenosis, thrombosis or aneurysm","Temporary avoidance of triggering calf-loading exercise until surgical release","Thrombolysis or thrombectomy with antiplatelet cover for acute arterial thrombosis","Evaluation of the opposite leg because the anomaly is often bilateral","Referral to vascular surgery and sports medicine for timely diagnosis and rehabilitation after repair"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Vascular surgery reference texts (standard reference)","Sports medicine references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"popliteal_venous_entrapment":{"name":"popliteal venous entrapment","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["popliteal vein entrapment syndrome","functional venous entrapment","popliteal venous compression syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Popliteal venous entrapment is dynamic or fixed compression of the popliteal vein within the popliteal fossa, usually by an anomalous or hypertrophied medial head of gastrocnemius or by a fibrous band, and it frequently coexists with popliteal artery entrapment","It characteristically affects young, athletic adults and presents with exertional or positional calf swelling, heaviness, aching and sometimes venous claudication, typically provoked by active plantarflexion or dorsiflexion","It should be considered in unexplained deep vein thrombosis or leg swelling in a young patient who lacks conventional thrombotic risk factors","Provocative duplex ultrasound with the ankle in active plantar- and dorsiflexion is the key first-line test, showing reduced or abolished popliteal venous flow on the manoeuvre","Cross-sectional imaging by MRI or MR venography, or by CT, defines the musculotendinous anatomy and distinguishes an anatomical anomaly from purely functional (positional) entrapment","Care is needed to separate true entrapment from the physiological compression that can occur in normal limbs during extreme plantarflexion, so as to avoid overdiagnosis","Symptomatic anatomical entrapment is treated surgically by decompression, releasing the offending muscle slip or fibrous band, whereas functional entrapment without a structural anomaly is generally managed more conservatively"],"pathophysiology":"The popliteal vein normally traverses the popliteal fossa alongside the artery, passing between the heads of gastrocnemius. In entrapment, an anomalous origin or accessory slip of the medial head of gastrocnemius, a fibrous band, or muscular hypertrophy compresses the vein, particularly during muscle contraction in plantarflexion or with certain knee positions. Repetitive compression produces intermittent venous outflow obstruction, venous hypertension and stasis, which give rise to exertional swelling and predispose to intimal injury and secondary thrombosis. In functional entrapment, hypertrophied but normally positioned muscles compress otherwise normal vessels, so imaging at rest may be unremarkable while provocative manoeuvres reveal the obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Deep vein thrombosis of another cause","Chronic venous insufficiency with venous reflux","Popliteal artery entrapment syndrome","Popliteal (Baker) cyst or popliteal venous aneurysm","Chronic exertional compartment syndrome","Lymphoedema","Musculoskeletal calf pathology such as gastrocnemius strain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound with provocative plantar- and dorsiflexion manoeuvres","MRI or MR venography of the popliteal fossa to display the musculotendinous anatomy","CT venography as an alternative for anatomical assessment","Positional contrast venography in selected cases","Assessment of the popliteal artery for coexisting arterial entrapment using ankle-brachial index and arterial duplex","Thrombophilia screen when the patient presents with deep vein thrombosis","Cross-sectional imaging to exclude an alternative popliteal mass such as a cyst or aneurysm"],"redFlags":["Deep vein thrombosis in a young patient without conventional risk factors","Coexisting arterial entrapment with claudication or limb ischaemia","Recurrent thrombosis or post-thrombotic changes despite anticoagulation","Progressive symptoms that increasingly limit activity","Features suggesting pulmonary embolism"],"prognosis":"With timely surgical decompression of an anatomical entrapment, symptomatic and functional outcomes are generally good. Delayed diagnosis may lead to established deep vein thrombosis, post-thrombotic syndrome and chronic venous insufficiency.","pitfalls":["Overdiagnosing entrapment from the physiological venous compression seen in normal limbs on extreme plantarflexion","Missing the diagnosis in young patients with unexplained deep vein thrombosis or exertional leg swelling","Failing to assess for a coexisting popliteal artery entrapment","Relying on resting, non-provocative imaging, which may appear normal","Treating recurrent deep vein thrombosis with anticoagulation alone without addressing the underlying compression"],"management":["Surgical decompression by dividing the anomalous or hypertrophied medial head of gastrocnemius or the constricting fibrous band relieving the popliteal vein, the definitive treatment for anatomical entrapment","Venous repair or reconstruction where the vein wall is already damaged or thrombosed","Anticoagulation when entrapment has precipitated a secondary deep vein thrombosis","Graduated compression and activity modification for symptom relief and in mild or purely functional cases where surgery is not warranted","Assessment for and treatment of coexisting popliteal artery entrapment","Referral to vascular surgery, with care to distinguish true entrapment from physiological compression so as to avoid unnecessary operation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Haimovici's Vascular Surgery","European Society for Vascular Surgery clinical practice guidance on popliteal entrapment"],"crossLinks":[]},"porcelain_gallbladder":{"name":"Porcelain gallbladder","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["calcified gallbladder","calcifying cholecystitis","porcelain gall bladder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Refers to calcification of the gallbladder wall, generally the end result of long-standing chronic cholecystitis and almost always associated with gallstones","Frequently asymptomatic and discovered incidentally on plain radiography, CT, or ultrasound","Historically linked to a high risk of gallbladder carcinoma, though contemporary series suggest the risk, while real, is lower than once believed","The pattern of calcification may matter, with selective mucosal or incomplete calcification thought to carry more risk than diffuse transmural (complete) calcification","CT is the most reliable modality for demonstrating and characterising the mural calcification","Prophylactic cholecystectomy is commonly recommended given the malignancy association, particularly for surgically fit patients or when features are concerning","Coexisting focal wall thickening or a mass should raise suspicion of established carcinoma"],"pathophysiology":"Porcelain gallbladder develops from chronic inflammation of the gallbladder, almost invariably in the setting of gallstones and often after repeated or persistent cholecystitis. Prolonged mucosal injury and scarring lead to deposition of calcium salts within the fibrotic gallbladder wall, which may be laid down as a continuous intramural band (complete calcification) or as patchy calcification of the mucosa and inner layers (selective or incomplete calcification). The stiff, calcified wall is functionally useless and characteristically painless. The chronic inflammatory milieu that produces the calcification is also the substrate for malignant transformation, which accounts for the recognised association with gallbladder adenocarcinoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Gallbladder full of calcified gallstones","Emphysematous cholecystitis (gas rather than calcium in the wall)","Gallbladder adenocarcinoma with calcification","Adenomyomatosis with intramural changes","Milk-of-calcium bile","Chronic cholecystitis without frank calcification"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT to confirm and characterise the extent and pattern of wall calcification","Abdominal ultrasound to assess the wall, gallstones, and any mass","MRI when soft-tissue characterisation or malignancy assessment is needed","Liver function tests and CA 19-9 if carcinoma is suspected","Cross-sectional staging imaging if a mass or malignancy is identified","Assessment of surgical fitness for cholecystectomy"],"redFlags":["Focal or asymmetric wall thickening or a soft-tissue mass suggesting carcinoma","New pain, weight loss, or jaundice","Regional lymphadenopathy or hepatic invasion on imaging","Rising tumour markers in the appropriate context"],"prognosis":"An incidentally found, uncomplicated porcelain gallbladder has a good outlook, and cholecystectomy removes both symptoms and the associated cancer risk; prognosis is poor if invasive gallbladder carcinoma has already developed, which is why the malignancy association drives management.","pitfalls":["Overstating the cancer risk based on older literature, or conversely dismissing it entirely","Confusing wall calcification with a gallbladder packed with calcified stones","Overlooking a coexisting carcinoma masked by dense calcification","Delaying cholecystectomy in a fit patient when malignancy risk warrants it","Mistaking emphysematous cholecystitis for calcification on plain imaging"],"management":["Cholecystectomy, generally recommended given the association with gallbladder adenocarcinoma, particularly for surgically fit patients or when calcification is selective mucosal or otherwise concerning","Laparoscopic approach for uncomplicated disease, with conversion to open resection following oncological principles if malignancy is suspected intra-operatively","Cross-sectional staging imaging and referral to hepatobiliary and oncology services when an infiltrating mass or carcinoma is identified","Assessment of surgical fitness and, in patients unfit for or declining surgery, imaging surveillance for malignant change","Discussion at a hepatobiliary multidisciplinary meeting where features are equivocal or malignancy is confirmed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Reviews of porcelain gallbladder and gallbladder cancer risk","Radiology literature on gallbladder wall calcification patterns"],"crossLinks":[]},"porphyria":{"name":"Porphyria","system":"Metabolic / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute intermittent porphyria","porphyria cutanea tarda"],"chapter":428,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3340-3348","clinicalPearls":["The porphyrias are metabolic disorders, each from deficient (or, for XLP, increased) activity of a specific enzyme in the heme biosynthetic pathway; inherited as autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, or X-linked, except porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) which is usually sporadic (p.3340).","Classified as hepatic or erythropoietic (by primary site of porphyrin/precursor overproduction) and as acute or cutaneous (by clinical manifestation) (p.3340, 3344).","Acute hepatic porphyrias (AIP, HCP, VP) usually present in adult life after puberty with acute attacks of neurovisceral symptoms and elevated urinary porphyrin precursors ALA and/or PBG (p.3344).","Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP) is autosomal dominant from half-normal HMB-synthase (porphobilinogen deaminase) activity; the most common acute hepatic porphyria (~1 in 20,000 Caucasians), especially frequent in Scandinavia and Great Britain (p.3340, 3346).","AIP abdominal pain is the most common symptom: poorly localized, often with cramping, ileus, distention, and decreased bowel sounds (though increased bowel sounds and diarrhea may occur) (p.3346).","AIP attacks also feature nausea, vomiting, constipation, tachycardia, hypertension, mental symptoms, limb/head/neck/chest pain, muscle weakness, sensory loss, dysuria, and urinary retention; tachycardia, hypertension, restlessness, tremor, and sweating reflect sympathetic overactivity (p.3346).","AIP peripheral neuropathy is due to axonal degeneration (not demyelination) and primarily affects motor neurons; proximal muscles (shoulders/arms) are involved first, and it may be focal or involve cranial nerves (p.3346).","Mental symptoms in acute attacks include anxiety, insomnia, depression, disorientation, hallucinations, and paranoia; seizures may be neurologic or due to hyponatremia (p.3346).","Diagnosis of an acute attack rests on substantial elevation of urinary PBG (and ALA); urinary PBG is always increased during acute attacks of AIP, HCP, and VP and is not substantially raised in any other condition, making a spot urine PBG both sensitive and specific (p.3345-3346).","PCT is the most common porphyria overall; hepatic URO-decarboxylase is deficient in all types, and ~80% of patients have no UROD mutation (sporadic type 1) (p.3347).","PCT presents with blistering, photosensitive skin lesions most commonly on the backs of the hands that rupture, crust, and scar, with skin friability, milia, hypertrichosis, and hyperpigmentation; neurologic features are absent (p.3347).","An acute attack may begin with sharp abdominal pain, then agitation/hallucinations/seizures, with back and extremity pain followed by weakness several days later, mimicking Guillain-Barre syndrome; weakness may be asymmetric, proximal or distal, and involve facial/bulbar muscles (p.3602-3603).","Urine may appear brownish during an attack due to high porphyrin metabolite concentration (p.3602-3603)."],"pathophysiology":"Each porphyria results from deficient (or, in XLP, increased) activity of one of the eight enzymes of the heme biosynthetic pathway converting glycine and succinyl-CoA to heme. In acute hepatic porphyrias, half-normal enzyme activity becomes limiting when hepatic heme synthesis is increased: induction of the rate-limiting hepatic gene ALAS1 (regulated in part by PGC-1alpha, which is induced by fasting) leads to accumulation and urinary excretion of the porphyrin precursors ALA and PBG, which underlie the acute neurovisceral attacks. Common precipitating factors include endogenous and exogenous steroids (estrogens/progestins), porphyrinogenic drugs (often P450-metabolized), alcohol, low-calorie/low-carbohydrate diets, infections, and surgery. AIP is almost always latent before puberty, suggesting adult steroid hormone levels drive expression; symptoms are commoner in women, with premenstrual (luteal-phase progesterone) attacks. Hyponatremia results from hypothalamic involvement with inappropriate vasopressin secretion or from electrolyte depletion (vomiting, diarrhea, poor intake, renal sodium loss). In PCT, substantial deficiency of hepatic URO-decarboxylase (to ~20% of normal or less, via an iron- and oxidative-stress-dependent uroporphomethene inhibitor) causes accumulation of porphyrins that, on excitation by long-wave UV light in the skin, produce blistering photosensitivity (p.3340, 3345-3347).","additionalDifferentials":["Intestinal obstruction (acute abdomen) — pain of porphyria is usually difficult to distinguish because severe hyperperistalsis is prominent in both (p.3344-ch.40)","Lead colic / lead intoxication (lead inhibits ALA-dehydratase, raising urinary ALA and coproporphyrin III) (p.3342, ch.40)","Hereditary tyrosinemia type 1 (succinylacetone inhibits ALA-dehydratase, mimicking acute porphyria) (p.3342)","Guillain-Barre syndrome (acute attack with ascending weakness mimics GBS) (p.3603)","Chronic liver disease (can cause nonspecific increases in urinary porphyrins leading to misdiagnosis) (p.3345)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute surgical abdomen / intestinal obstruction (acute hepatic porphyria attack mimics this) (p.3344, ch.40)","Lead poisoning (lead colic and neuropathy mimic acute porphyria) (p.3342)","Guillain-Barre syndrome (ascending motor weakness in an acute attack) (p.3603)","Hereditary tyrosinemia type 1 (biochemical and clinical mimic of acute porphyria) (p.3342)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Spot (single-void) urine for porphobilinogen (PBG) — first-line; always raised in acute attacks of AIP/HCP/VP and specific; 24-h collection unnecessary (p.3345-3346)","Quantitative urinary ALA, PBG, and creatinine on the same spot sample (also detects ADP) (p.3345)","Total urinary porphyrins (interpret cautiously — often nonspecific) (p.3345)","Urinary, fecal, and plasma porphyrins to distinguish AIP from HCP and VP (fecal porphyrins normal/minimal in AIP) (p.3346)","Total plasma porphyrins with fluorometric scanning of diluted plasma at neutral pH (distinguishes VP from PCT; VP emission peak ~626 nm) for cutaneous porphyrias (p.3346, 3348)","Erythrocyte protoporphyrin (more sensitive for EPP/XLP; confirm EPP by predominant free, not zinc, protoporphyrin) (p.3346)","Isocoproporphyrins in feces — diagnostic of hepatic URO-decarboxylase deficiency (PCT) (p.3348)","Erythrocyte URO-decarboxylase activity (~50% in type 2 PCT; markedly deficient, 3-10%, in HEP) (p.3348)","Molecular genetic mutation analysis of the relevant heme-pathway gene (definitive; diagnostic even when precursor levels have normalized, and for latent heterozygotes/relatives) (p.3342, 3346)"],"redFlags":["Acute neurovisceral attack: severe poorly-localized abdominal pain with autonomic instability (tachycardia, hypertension, sweating), motor neuropathy/weakness, and psychiatric disturbance — the AIP acute hepatic porphyria red-flag constellation (p.3346)","Hyponatremia (from inappropriate vasopressin secretion or electrolyte depletion) and seizures during an attack (p.3346)","Markedly raised urinary PBG/ALA in a patient with abdominal pain after puberty (p.3345-3346)","Exposure to a porphyria-precipitating drug, low-calorie diet, alcohol, infection, or surgery preceding the attack (p.3346)","Progression to respiratory and bulbar paralysis; sudden death may result from sympathetic overactivity and cardiac arrhythmia, especially with delayed diagnosis/treatment (p.3346)"],"prognosis":"AIP can be disabling but is rarely fatal; the prognosis of individuals with HMBS mutations is generally favorable, and most heterozygotes remain latent. When an attack resolves, abdominal pain may disappear within hours and paresis begins to improve within days, sometimes continuing to improve over years; recovery from severe motor neuropathy may take months to years and depends on the degree of neuronal damage and early treatment. Long-term complications of AIP include increased risk of hypertension and chronic kidney disease (up to 59% develop CKD), chronic liver function abnormalities with increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma (warranting hepatic imaging at least every 6 months), and persistent neuropathy, fatigue, chronic pain, nausea, depression, and anxiety. Homozygous dominant AIP usually presents in infancy and most affected children die at an early age. PCT can almost always be brought into complete remission with treatment, but patients characteristically have chronic liver disease, sometimes cirrhosis, and are at risk for hepatocellular carcinoma (p.3346-3348).","pitfalls":["A great mimic of the acute abdomen: porphyric (and lead colic) pain is hard to distinguish from intestinal obstruction because of prominent hyperperistalsis — risking unnecessary surgery (p.3344, ch.40).","Because symptoms are nonspecific and rarely occur before puberty, diagnosis is often delayed; a high index of suspicion is required (p.3340, 3346).","Measuring urinary porphyrins alone for screening should be avoided — minimal nonspecific increases (e.g., in chronic liver disease) cause misdiagnosis; use urinary PBG (p.3345).","Erythrocyte HMB-synthase assay is not a useful first-line test: activity is not decreased in all AIP patients, borderline-low values are not diagnostic, and it is not deficient in other acute porphyrias; some HMBS mutations give normal erythrocyte enzyme so genetic testing is needed (p.3345-3346).","ALA/PBG levels often remain high after symptoms resolve, so diagnosing an acute attack in a biochemically proven AIP patient relies primarily on clinical features (p.3346).","Most antiseizure drugs can exacerbate AIP, making seizure treatment difficult (clonazepam may be safer than phenytoin or barbiturates) (p.3346).","Abdominal tenderness, fever, and leukocytosis are usually absent or mild in AIP because the process is neurologic, not inflammatory — their prominence should prompt reconsideration (p.3346).","In PCT, neurologic features are absent — abdominal/neurovisceral attacks should not be attributed to it (p.3347)."],"management":["Intravenous haem (haem arginate or hemin) during acute neurovisceral attacks to replenish the regulatory haem pool and suppress hepatic ALAS1, with carbohydrate loading by intravenous glucose for milder attacks","Immediate withdrawal of porphyrinogenic drugs and correction of precipitants such as fasting, alcohol, infection and hormonal triggers","Symptomatic care of the attack with opioid analgesia, antiemetics, beta-blockade for tachycardia and hypertension, and cautious correction of hyponatraemia from inappropriate ADH secretion","Non-porphyrinogenic anticonvulsants for seizures, avoiding enzyme-inducing agents","Prevention of recurrent attacks with the ALAS1-directed small interfering RNA givosiran, and gonadotrophin-releasing hormone analogues for cyclical menstrual attacks","Surveillance for long-term complications including hypertension, chronic kidney disease and hepatocellular carcinoma","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal dominant inheritance, with provision of a safe-drug list and medical-alert documentation","Liver transplantation considered for severe recurrent disabling disease refractory to medical therapy"],"severityClassification":"Porphyrias are classified by primary overproduction site (hepatic vs erythropoietic) and by clinical manifestation (acute neurovisceral vs cutaneous). Acute hepatic porphyrias: AIP, HCP, VP (and the rare autosomal recessive ADP) present with acute neurovisceral attacks and raised ALA/PBG; AIP causes neurovisceral disease without photosensitivity, whereas HCP and VP can have both neurovisceral attacks and PCT-like cutaneous photosensitivity. Cutaneous: PCT (and HEP) present with chronic blistering photosensitivity. Erythropoietic porphyrias (CEP, EPP, XLP) present with cutaneous photosensitivity (EPP/XLP non-blistering, immediate and painful). Acute-attack severity ranges from mild (abdominal pain without paresis or hyponatremia, manageable with carbohydrate loading) to severe (paresis, hyponatremia, bulbar/respiratory paralysis) (p.3340, 3344, 3346-3347).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3340-3348","Harrison 22e p.3602-3603"],"crossLinks":["peripheral_neuropathy","hyponatremia"]},"porphyria_cutanea_tarda":{"name":"Porphyria cutanea tarda","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCT","uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency","UROD deficiency","symptomatic porphyria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common porphyria, resulting from deficient activity of uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase, the fifth enzyme of haem biosynthesis","Predominantly acquired (type I) with a normal gene, occurring when hepatic enzyme activity is inhibited; a familial (type II) form has an inherited UROD mutation","Presents as a chronic blistering photosensitivity with skin fragility, vesicles and bullae, milia, erosions and scarring on sun-exposed skin, especially the backs of the hands","Additional features include hypertrichosis (notably on the temples and cheeks), hyperpigmentation and, in some, sclerodermoid change","It is a purely cutaneous (non-acute) porphyria without neurovisceral attacks, as the accumulating porphyrins are photoreactive rather than neurotoxic precursors","Key susceptibility factors include hepatic iron overload (often with HFE haemochromatosis variants), hepatitis C, HIV, alcohol excess and oestrogen exposure","The biochemical hallmark is markedly raised urinary uroporphyrin and heptacarboxyl porphyrin with raised isocoproporphyrin in faeces","Management combines removal of contributory factors, iron reduction by venesection and low-dose antimalarial therapy, alongside sun protection"],"pathophysiology":"Porphyria cutanea tarda develops when hepatic uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase activity falls below a critical threshold, whether from acquired inhibition alone or on the background of an inherited mutation. The enzyme sequentially decarboxylates uroporphyrinogen, so its deficiency causes accumulation of highly carboxylated, water-soluble and photoreactive porphyrins, particularly uroporphyrin. Hepatic iron, oxidative stress and cofactors such as hepatitis C, alcohol and oestrogens generate an inhibitor of the enzyme. Circulating porphyrins deposit in the skin and, on ultraviolet and visible light exposure, generate reactive oxygen species that damage the dermo-epidermal junction, producing the characteristic fragility and blistering.","additionalDifferentials":["Pseudoporphyria from drugs (e.g. NSAIDs, diuretics) or renal dialysis","Variegate porphyria and hereditary coproporphyria with skin involvement","Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita","Bullous pemphigoid and other subepidermal blistering disorders","Hepatoerythropoietic porphyria (severe biallelic UROD deficiency)","Polymorphic light eruption and other photodermatoses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative urinary porphyrins showing uroporphyrin and heptacarboxyl porphyrin excess","Faecal porphyrin analysis for isocoproporphyrin, characteristic of the condition","Plasma fluorescence scanning to support the porphyria diagnosis","Iron studies and ferritin, with HFE genotyping where iron overload is present","Hepatitis C and HIV serology, and assessment of alcohol intake and liver function","UROD gene testing when a familial form is suspected"],"redFlags":["Features of advanced chronic liver disease or hepatocellular carcinoma given the raised long-term risk","Severe childhood-onset blistering suggesting hepatoerythropoietic porphyria","Secondary infection or scarring of fragile skin lesions","Unrecognised underlying hepatitis C or HIV"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is good when contributory factors are addressed; skin lesions usually remit with iron reduction and antimalarial therapy, although there is a long-term risk of hepatic fibrosis and hepatocellular carcinoma, particularly with concurrent hepatitis C or alcohol excess.","pitfalls":["Overlooking treatable drivers such as hepatitis C, HIV, alcohol, oestrogen use and iron overload","Confusing it with pseudoporphyria, which has identical skin changes but normal porphyrins","Failing to distinguish it from variegate porphyria, which can look identical on the skin","Neglecting sun protection while awaiting the effect of definitive treatment","Missing familial disease and the need for family assessment"],"management":["Iron depletion by repeated therapeutic venesection to reduce hepatic iron and relieve inhibition of uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","Low-dose antimalarial therapy with hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine to mobilise and clear accumulated hepatic porphyrins where venesection is unsuitable","Removal of susceptibility factors, including abstaining from alcohol, stopping oestrogen exposure, treating hepatitis C with direct-acting antivirals and optimising HIV therapy","Strict photoprotection with sun avoidance, protective clothing and barrier measures, plus gentle skin care to limit fragility and blistering","Monitoring of urinary porphyrins to confirm biochemical remission and of liver function with hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance in those with cirrhosis or hepatitis C","Iron studies with HFE genotyping, and UROD gene testing with family assessment where a familial form is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"port_wine_stain":{"name":"Port-Wine Stain","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["naevus flammeus","capillary malformation","PWS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A port-wine stain is a capillary (vascular) malformation present at birth that, unlike an infantile haemangioma, does not proliferate and does not spontaneously resolve","It appears as a flat, sharply demarcated pink to red patch that often follows a segmental or dermatomal distribution and tends to darken and thicken with age","Over decades some lesions develop cobblestoning, nodularity, and soft-tissue or bony overgrowth of the affected area","A facial stain involving the ophthalmic (forehead and upper eyelid) distribution raises concern for Sturge-Weber syndrome, with associated leptomeningeal vascular malformation and glaucoma","Any child with a periocular or ophthalmic-distribution stain needs ophthalmological assessment for glaucoma and appropriate neurological evaluation","Extensive limb capillary malformations can be a feature of overgrowth syndromes, so assess for limb asymmetry and hypertrophy","Pulsed-dye laser is the mainstay of treatment and works best when started early, though multiple sessions are needed and complete clearance is not guaranteed","The visible facial lesion carries a significant psychosocial burden, and support and camouflage advice are an important part of holistic care"],"pathophysiology":"A port-wine stain results from a developmental malformation of the superficial dermal capillaries and postcapillary venules, which are abnormally dilated and increase in ectasia over time. Many cases are associated with somatic activating mutations in genes of the GNAQ/GNA11 pathway, arising during embryogenesis and affecting a clone of vascular and surrounding cells. Because the abnormality is structural rather than proliferative, the number of vessels is essentially fixed, but progressive vascular dilatation and, in some cases, associated soft-tissue overgrowth account for the darkening and thickening seen with age. The same mosaic mutations underlie the neurological and ocular features when the malformation extends beyond the skin.","additionalDifferentials":["Infantile haemangioma (proliferates then involutes, unlike a port-wine stain)","Naevus simplex / salmon patch (transient, midline, fades)","Other capillary malformations within overgrowth syndromes","Angioma serpiginosum and other acquired vascular patches","Early or atypical vascular tumours","Reticulate vascular patterns such as cutis marmorata telangiectatica congenita"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on a congenital, non-involuting, well-demarcated vascular patch","Ophthalmological review for glaucoma with ophthalmic-distribution facial lesions","Brain MRI to assess for leptomeningeal involvement when Sturge-Weber syndrome is suspected","Assessment for limb overgrowth and, if indicated, imaging for associated vascular or overgrowth syndromes","Genetic evaluation in syndromic or extensive presentations where appropriate"],"redFlags":["Ophthalmic-distribution facial stain raising concern for Sturge-Weber syndrome, seizures, and developmental effects","Signs of glaucoma such as a large or cloudy eye, tearing, or photophobia requiring urgent ophthalmology input","Progressive soft-tissue or skeletal overgrowth of the affected region","Extensive limb capillary malformation with asymmetry suggesting an overgrowth syndrome","New nodularity, bleeding, or ulceration within a long-standing lesion prompting reassessment"],"prognosis":"A port-wine stain is a lifelong lesion that persists and typically darkens and thickens over time; the skin lesion itself is benign, but associated syndromic features determine broader outcome, and laser treatment can substantially lighten many lesions though results vary and maintenance may be required.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with an infantile haemangioma and wrongly reassuring parents it will disappear","Missing the association with Sturge-Weber syndrome in an ophthalmic-distribution facial lesion","Failing to arrange glaucoma screening in at-risk infants","Overlooking limb overgrowth syndromes in extensive capillary malformations","Neglecting the psychological impact and the value of early laser referral"],"management":["Pulsed-dye laser therapy as the mainstay to lighten the malformation, begun early and repeated as maintenance since lesions persist and darken over time","Ophthalmological review for glaucoma in any lesion involving the ophthalmic distribution, with pressure-lowering treatment as required","Brain MRI and neurology referral when Sturge-Weber syndrome is suspected, with anticonvulsant therapy for associated seizures","Assessment for limb asymmetry and soft-tissue or bony overgrowth, with orthopaedic and vascular input where an overgrowth syndrome is identified","Psychological support and skin camouflage for the cosmetic and social impact of a visible facial lesion","Genetic evaluation in syndromic or extensive presentations where appropriate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"portal_biliopathy":{"name":"portal biliopathy","system":"hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["portal cavernoma cholangiopathy","portal hypertensive biliopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Portal biliopathy describes abnormalities of the biliary tree, including strictures, dilatation, angulation and displacement, arising from long-standing portal hypertension, most often chronic extrahepatic portal vein obstruction with cavernoma.","The term portal cavernoma cholangiopathy is preferred when a cavernoma is the demonstrable cause.","Two mechanisms operate together: extrinsic compression by dilated paracholedochal veins of Petren and epicholedochal veins of Saint, and ischaemic fibrotic injury from the original thrombotic insult.","Most patients remain asymptomatic despite striking cholangiographic changes; only a minority develop jaundice, biliary pain, cholangitis or secondary duct stones.","MRCP combined with MR portovenography is the imaging modality of choice, showing both the biliary changes and the venous collaterals non-invasively.","ERCP should be reserved for therapy such as stone extraction or stenting rather than diagnosis, because periductal collaterals bleed readily.","Symptomatic disease is managed stepwise with endoscopic biliary drainage first, portosystemic shunt surgery to decompress the collaterals in refractory cases, and biliary bypass reserved for fixed strictures."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic obstruction of the extrahepatic portal vein leads to cavernous transformation, a leash of hepatopetal collateral veins around the obstructed segment. Two venous plexuses that normally drain the bile duct, the paracholedochal veins of Petren and the finer epicholedochal veins of Saint, become grossly dilated within and around the duct wall. These engorged collaterals compress and indent the common bile duct, and over time the sustained pressure together with ischaemia from the original thrombosis provokes fibrotic stricturing and wall thickening. The result is a spectrum ranging from smooth extrinsic indentations to fixed strictures, upstream ductal dilatation and intrahepatic involvement, which may be complicated by biliary stasis, stone formation and recurrent cholangitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary sclerosing cholangitis","Cholangiocarcinoma","Primary choledocholithiasis","IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis","Iatrogenic or post-surgical bile duct stricture","Recurrent pyogenic cholangitis","Autoimmune pancreatitis with biliary involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRCP with MR portovenography","Doppler ultrasound of the portal system","Endoscopic ultrasound to distinguish collaterals from stones or tumour","Liver function tests with fractionated bilirubin and cholestatic markers","Thrombophilia and myeloproliferative neoplasm screen for the underlying cause","ERCP reserved for therapeutic intervention","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy to assess varices before any procedure"],"redFlags":["Ascending cholangitis with fever, jaundice and right upper quadrant pain","Progressive obstructive jaundice suggesting a fixed dominant stricture","Secondary biliary cirrhosis from long-standing obstruction","Variceal haemorrhage","Features raising suspicion of superimposed cholangiocarcinoma"],"prognosis":"Most patients remain asymptomatic with a good long-term outlook, but those who develop symptomatic strictures may progress to recurrent cholangitis and secondary biliary cirrhosis if drainage is inadequate.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the cholangiographic appearances for primary sclerosing cholangitis or cholangiocarcinoma.","Performing diagnostic ERCP and precipitating haemobilia or variceal bleeding from periductal collaterals.","Attempting biliary surgery without first decompressing the portal system, risking catastrophic intraoperative haemorrhage.","Overlooking and failing to treat the underlying prothrombotic disorder."],"management":["Observation without intervention for the asymptomatic majority, since striking cholangiographic changes often cause no clinical disease","Treatment of the underlying portal vein obstruction, including anticoagulation where an active prothrombotic disorder is identified","Portal decompression by transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt or a surgical portosystemic shunt to relieve the collateral compression, often required before any attempt at biliary drainage","Endoscopic therapy at ERCP for symptomatic strictures and secondary duct stones, with stenting or stone extraction reserved for treatment rather than diagnosis because periductal collaterals bleed readily","Antibiotics for episodes of cholangitis, with biliary drainage of obstructed segments","Management within a multidisciplinary team of hepatology, hepatobiliary surgery and interventional radiology given the bleeding risk of intervention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Consensus statements on extrahepatic portal vein obstruction and portal cavernoma cholangiopathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"portal_cavernoma":{"name":"portal cavernoma","system":"hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cavernous transformation of the portal vein","portal vein cavernoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A portal cavernoma is the network of hepatopetal collateral veins that develops around a chronically obstructed or thrombosed portal vein, a process termed cavernous transformation.","It is the hallmark of chronic extrahepatic portal vein obstruction, and in the absence of cirrhosis liver synthetic function is typically well preserved.","In children it commonly follows neonatal umbilical vein catheterisation, omphalitis or sepsis, whereas in adults prothrombotic states, myeloproliferative neoplasms, cirrhosis, abdominal sepsis and malignancy predominate.","The dominant clinical problem is portal hypertension with gastro-oesophageal varices, splenomegaly and hypersplenism.","Variceal bleeding is often surprisingly well tolerated because hepatic function is preserved.","The collaterals can compress the biliary tree to produce portal cavernoma cholangiopathy, also called portal biliopathy.","Doppler ultrasound shows the serpiginous collaterals replacing the normal portal vein, and CT or MR portography maps the anatomy for intervention.","The Meso-Rex bypass, which restores physiological portal inflow, is the preferred operation in suitable children."],"pathophysiology":"When the main portal vein is obstructed by thrombosis, extrinsic compression or malformation, the portal circulation seeks alternative routes to reach the liver. Over weeks to months the pre-existing tiny peribiliary and paracholedochal veins in the porta hepatis dilate and proliferate into a leash of tortuous hepatopetal collaterals, the cavernoma, which partially bypasses the occluded segment. Because these collaterals cannot fully substitute for the calibre of the native vein, portal pressure rises and drives the formation of portosystemic collaterals including gastro-oesophageal varices, together with splenomegaly and hypersplenism. In extrahepatic obstruction the intrahepatic parenchyma and its arterial supply remain intact, so hepatic synthetic function is generally preserved and bleeding is better tolerated than in cirrhotic portal hypertension. The engorged periductal collaterals may in turn compress and distort the bile ducts, producing cholangiopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Cirrhotic portal hypertension","Non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis or idiopathic portal hypertension","Budd-Chiari syndrome","Portal vein tumour thrombus from hepatocellular carcinoma","Congenital hepatic fibrosis","Arterioportal fistula","Hepatic schistosomiasis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Doppler ultrasound of the portal system","CT or MR portography","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy for varices","Thrombophilia and myeloproliferative neoplasm screen including JAK2 mutation testing","Full blood count to assess hypersplenism","Liver function and synthetic tests to confirm preserved function","MRCP if biliopathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Acute variceal haemorrhage with haematemesis or melaena","Ascending cholangitis from associated biliopathy","Extension of thrombosis into the mesenteric veins with bowel ischaemia","Rapid splenic enlargement with severe cytopenias"],"prognosis":"With preserved hepatic function the long-term outlook is generally good once varices are controlled, though recurrent bleeding, hypersplenism and biliopathy contribute to morbidity, and extension of thrombosis to the mesenteric veins is a serious threat.","pitfalls":["Assuming portal hypertension implies cirrhosis and overlooking a non-cirrhotic cavernoma with preserved liver function.","Failing to screen for and treat an underlying prothrombotic or myeloproliferative disorder.","Missing associated portal biliopathy as a cause of cholestasis.","Delaying recognition of mesenteric extension causing intestinal ischaemia."],"management":["Anticoagulation directed by the underlying prothrombotic state, balanced against variceal bleeding risk after endoscopic assessment","Prevention and treatment of gastro-oesophageal variceal bleeding with endoscopic band ligation and non-selective beta-blockade, and surveillance endoscopy","Meso-Rex bypass, restoring physiological portal flow, or a portosystemic shunt for refractory variceal bleeding or symptomatic portal hypertension, particularly in children","Management of hypersplenism and its cytopenias, with attention to bleeding risk","Treatment of coexisting portal cavernoma cholangiopathy when biliary obstruction develops","Haematology referral to identify and treat an underlying myeloproliferative neoplasm or thrombophilia, with monitoring for extension of thrombosis into the mesenteric veins"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Baveno consensus on portal hypertension","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"portal_hypertension":{"name":"Portal hypertension","system":"Hepatobiliary surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["portal venous hypertension","raised portal pressure","portal HTN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Portal hypertension is a sustained rise in portal venous pressure and is most often caused by cirrhosis in the developed world and by schistosomiasis or portal vein thrombosis elsewhere","Clinical consequences arise from portosystemic collaterals and splanchnic congestion: gastro-oesophageal varices, splenomegaly with hypersplenism, ascites, and caput medusae","Variceal haemorrhage is the most dangerous complication and presents as haematemesis or melaena, often with haemodynamic instability","Causes are usefully grouped as prehepatic (e.g. portal vein thrombosis), intrahepatic (presinusoidal, sinusoidal as in cirrhosis, or postsinusoidal), and posthepatic (e.g. Budd-Chiari syndrome, constrictive pericarditis)","Endoscopy characterises and treats varices, while non-selective beta-blockade reduces portal pressure and the risk of first or recurrent bleeding","Acute variceal bleeding is managed with resuscitation, vasoactive splanchnic vasoconstrictors, endoscopic band ligation, and antibiotic prophylaxis, with balloon tamponade or TIPS as rescue","Transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS) and, less commonly, surgical shunts decompress the portal system but can precipitate hepatic encephalopathy"],"pathophysiology":"Portal hypertension results from increased resistance to portal blood flow combined with increased splanchnic inflow. In cirrhosis, fibrosis and regenerative nodules distort the hepatic architecture and raise sinusoidal resistance, compounded by intrahepatic vasoconstriction. Splanchnic arterial vasodilatation, mediated in part by nitric oxide, increases portal inflow and sustains the elevated pressure. As pressure rises, portosystemic collateral channels open at sites of communication between the portal and systemic circulations, producing varices at the gastro-oesophageal junction, rectum, and anterior abdominal wall. Splenic congestion causes splenomegaly and hypersplenism, and the combination of portal hypertension and hepatic dysfunction drives ascites and hepatic encephalopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Cirrhosis of any aetiology","Portal or splenic vein thrombosis (prehepatic)","Hepatic schistosomiasis (presinusoidal)","Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic venous outflow obstruction)","Right heart failure or constrictive pericarditis (posthepatic)","Non-cirrhotic portal hypertension / idiopathic portal hypertension"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper GI endoscopy to grade and treat varices","Abdominal ultrasound with Doppler of the portal and hepatic veins to assess flow, thrombosis, and splenomegaly","Liver function tests, coagulation profile, and full blood count (for hypersplenism-related cytopenias)","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) for hepatic architecture, collaterals, and vascular patency","Hepatic venous pressure gradient measurement where available to quantify portal pressure","Liver biopsy or elastography to assess fibrosis and clarify aetiology"],"redFlags":["Active variceal haemorrhage with haematemesis, melaena, and haemodynamic instability requiring emergency resuscitation and endoscopy","Encephalopathy with reduced consciousness and risk of aspiration","Rapidly accumulating tense ascites or spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Signs of decompensation (jaundice, coagulopathy, hepatorenal syndrome) indicating advanced liver failure"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is dominated by the underlying liver disease and by whether decompensation has occurred; variceal bleeding carries substantial mortality, particularly in advanced cirrhosis. Effective prophylaxis, endoscopic therapy, and shunting reduce rebleeding, but long-term survival depends on hepatic reserve and, in suitable candidates, transplantation.","pitfalls":["Over-transfusing during variceal bleeding, which can raise portal pressure and worsen haemorrhage","Omitting antibiotic prophylaxis in cirrhotic patients with gastrointestinal bleeding","Aggressive correction of coagulopathy or platelet counts without regard to the rebalanced haemostasis of cirrhosis","Creating a shunt without anticipating precipitation or worsening of hepatic encephalopathy","Attributing all upper GI bleeding to varices and missing coexisting peptic ulceration or portal hypertensive gastropathy"],"management":["Treatment of the underlying cause, principally management of cirrhosis with abstinence from alcohol and cause-specific therapy, alongside referral for liver transplantation in decompensated disease","Non-selective beta-blockade with propranolol or carvedilol to lower portal pressure for primary and secondary prophylaxis of variceal bleeding","Endoscopic band ligation to eradicate oesophageal varices, with surveillance endoscopy","Acute variceal haemorrhage managed with resuscitation, vasoactive splanchnic vasoconstrictors such as terlipressin or octreotide, urgent endoscopic band ligation and prophylactic antibiotics, with balloon tamponade or transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt as rescue","Ascites treated with dietary sodium restriction and diuretics such as spironolactone and furosemide, with large-volume paracentesis and antibiotic prophylaxis against spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Hepatic encephalopathy treated with lactulose and rifaximin","Transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt for refractory ascites or recurrent variceal bleeding"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"portal_hypertensive_gastropathy":{"name":"Portal hypertensive gastropathy","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PHG","congestive gastropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs in the setting of portal hypertension and is recognised endoscopically by a snake-skin (mosaic) mucosal pattern, most prominent in the gastric body and fundus","Severe disease shows discrete red or brown spots superimposed on the mosaic pattern, reflecting more advanced congestion","Must be distinguished from gastric antral vascular ectasia, which produces linear red stripes (watermelon stomach) concentrated in the antrum and is not driven by portal pressure","Usually causes chronic, slow blood loss with iron-deficiency anaemia, though acute overt bleeding can occur","Severity often parallels the degree of portal hypertension and may worsen after obliteration of oesophageal varices","Management is directed at lowering portal pressure with non-selective beta-blockers, and refractory bleeding may respond to transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunting or measures to treat the underlying liver disease","Iron supplementation and, where needed, transfusion address the resulting anaemia"],"pathophysiology":"Portal hypertensive gastropathy results from the passive congestion of the gastric mucosal and submucosal microvasculature that accompanies raised portal venous pressure, usually from cirrhosis. Dilatation of mucosal capillaries and venules, with ectasia and oedema but relatively little inflammation, produces the characteristic mosaic or snake-skin appearance, and the fragile congested mucosa bleeds easily. The severity tends to track with the degree of portal hypertension, and it may become more apparent after endoscopic eradication of oesophageal varices diverts flow into the gastric mucosal bed.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastric antral vascular ectasia (watermelon stomach)","Gastric varices","Erosive or reactive gastropathy from drugs or alcohol","Angiodysplasia of the stomach","Dieulafoy lesion as a source of obscure bleeding","Peptic ulcer disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy to identify the mosaic pattern and grade severity, and to distinguish it from antral vascular ectasia","Full blood count and iron studies to quantify chronic blood loss","Liver function tests and assessment of the underlying liver disease and its severity","Imaging of the portal system (Doppler ultrasound or cross-sectional imaging) to confirm portal hypertension","Measurement of the hepatic venous pressure gradient in selected cases","Faecal occult blood testing where occult loss is suspected"],"redFlags":["Acute overt upper gastrointestinal bleeding with haemodynamic compromise","Rapidly falling haemoglobin or transfusion dependence","Signs of decompensated liver disease accompanying the bleeding"],"prognosis":"Portal hypertensive gastropathy is usually a chronic source of low-grade blood loss that is manageable, but its course mirrors the underlying liver disease and portal hypertension; severe or refractory bleeding carries a worse outlook and depends on control of portal pressure.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying antral vascular ectasia as portal hypertensive gastropathy, since the two require different treatment","Expecting portal-pressure-lowering therapy to help antral vascular ectasia, which it does not","Overlooking the tendency for the gastropathy to worsen after variceal obliteration","Attributing anaemia solely to varices and missing ongoing mucosal loss"],"management":["Non-selective beta-blockade with propranolol or carvedilol to lower portal pressure, the mainstay for reducing chronic and recurrent bleeding","Iron replacement, oral or intravenous, and blood transfusion as needed to correct iron-deficiency anaemia from chronic mucosal blood loss","Treatment of the underlying liver disease, whose severity drives the course","Acute overt bleeding managed with resuscitation and vasoactive splanchnic vasoconstrictors","Transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt or a surgical shunt for refractory or transfusion-dependent bleeding","Endoscopic distinction from gastric antral vascular ectasia, which requires endoscopic ablation such as argon plasma coagulation rather than portal-pressure reduction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Yamada's Textbook of Gastroenterology"],"crossLinks":[]},"portal_vein_thrombosis":{"name":"Portal vein thrombosis","system":"Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PVT","Portal venous thrombosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents acutely with abdominal pain or is discovered chronically as cavernous transformation (portal cavernoma) with periportal collaterals.","Cirrhosis is the commonest predisposing factor in adults; other causes include myeloproliferative neoplasms, abdominal sepsis, pancreatitis and malignancy.","Distinguish bland thrombus from tumour thrombus (hepatocellular carcinoma): tumour thrombus expands the vein, enhances arterially and may show pulsatile flow on Doppler.","Extension into the mesenteric veins is the feared complication, risking intestinal congestion, ischaemia and infarction.","Doppler ultrasound is first line; contrast CT or MR portography defines the extent and mesenteric involvement.","Anticoagulation promotes recanalisation and prevents extension in acute non-cirrhotic disease, balanced against variceal bleeding risk after endoscopic assessment.","In non-cirrhotic patients, screen for an underlying myeloproliferative neoplasm including JAK2 V617F even when the standard thrombophilia screen is negative.","Chronic portal cavernoma causes portal hypertension with varices and splenomegaly but relatively preserved hepatic synthetic function when non-cirrhotic."],"pathophysiology":"Thrombosis within the extrahepatic portal vein or its intrahepatic branches arises from the elements of Virchow's triad: reduced portal flow (cirrhosis and portal hypertension), endothelial injury (inflammation, tumour invasion, sclerotherapy, surgery) and hypercoagulability (myeloproliferative neoplasms, inherited or acquired thrombophilias, pregnancy and oestrogen exposure). Obstruction raises portal pressure and, when persistent, drives the development of periportal venous collaterals termed cavernous transformation. If thrombus propagates into the superior mesenteric vein, venous congestion of the bowel can progress to ischaemia and transmural infarction.","additionalDifferentials":["Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic venous outflow obstruction)","Tumour (hepatocellular carcinoma) invasion of the portal vein","Cirrhotic portal hypertension without thrombosis","Mesenteric arterial ischaemia","Nodular regenerative hyperplasia or idiopathic non-cirrhotic portal hypertension","Constrictive pericarditis or cardiac cirrhosis causing portal hypertension","Congenital portosystemic shunt"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Doppler ultrasonography of the portal venous system","Contrast-enhanced CT or MR portography to map thrombus extent","Full blood count and JAK2 V617F mutation testing","Thrombophilia screen (protein C and S, antithrombin, antiphospholipid antibodies, factor V Leiden, prothrombin gene mutation)","Liver function and synthetic markers with cause-specific liver workup","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy for varices","Alpha-fetoprotein and cross-sectional imaging to exclude hepatocellular carcinoma"],"redFlags":["Severe worsening abdominal pain with lactic acidosis suggesting mesenteric ischaemia","Peritonism or clinical signs of bowel infarction","Variceal haemorrhage","New ascites with fever suggesting concurrent sepsis"],"prognosis":"Acute non-cirrhotic portal vein thrombosis has a good outcome with prompt anticoagulation and high recanalisation rates, whereas chronic cavernoma carries a risk of portal hypertensive bleeding but usually preserved liver function. Thrombosis complicating cirrhosis or malignancy portends a worse prognosis.","pitfalls":["Mistaking bland thrombus for tumour thrombus, or vice versa, which fundamentally alters management","Withholding anticoagulation for fear of varices without first assessing and prophylaxing them","Missing mesenteric extension and impending bowel infarction","Overlooking an occult myeloproliferative neoplasm when the standard thrombophilia screen is negative"],"management":["Anticoagulation, initially low-molecular-weight heparin transitioning to an oral agent, for acute non-cirrhotic thrombosis to promote recanalisation and prevent propagation","Treatment of the underlying cause, including a myeloproliferative neoplasm or thrombophilia, with haematology involvement","Endoscopic screening for varices and appropriate prophylaxis before or alongside anticoagulation to mitigate bleeding risk","Distinction of bland from tumour thrombus, with hepatocellular carcinoma directed therapy where tumour invasion is present","Urgent surgical assessment for mesenteric extension threatening bowel viability, with systemic anticoagulation, possible thrombolysis or thrombectomy, and resection of infarcted bowel","Broad-spectrum antibiotics when septic thrombophlebitis of the portal vein is present","Management of established portal hypertension in chronic cavernoma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Oxford Textbook of Medicine","Baveno consensus workshop on portal hypertension"],"crossLinks":[]},"portopulmonary_hypertension":{"name":"Portopulmonary hypertension","system":"Respiratory / Pulmonary vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PoPH","portopulmonary syndrome","portal hypertension-associated pulmonary arterial hypertension"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC/ERS Guidelines for Pulmonary Hypertension / Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group 1 pulmonary arterial hypertension occurring in the setting of portal hypertension, with or without underlying chronic liver disease","Diagnosis requires pre-capillary haemodynamics at right heart catheterisation (raised mean pulmonary artery pressure and pulmonary vascular resistance with a normal wedge pressure) together with documented portal hypertension","The volume overload and hyperdynamic circulation of cirrhosis can raise pulmonary pressures without truly raising resistance, so resistance must be measured to distinguish true PoPH from a high-flow state","Often asymptomatic early and detected on pre-transplant echocardiographic screening; later causes exertional breathlessness and fatigue","Severity is important for liver transplant candidacy, as uncontrolled pulmonary hypertension markedly increases perioperative mortality","Targeted pulmonary vasodilator therapy can lower pulmonary vascular resistance and may render some patients eligible for transplantation","The severity of portal hypertension does not correlate with the severity of the pulmonary vascular disease"],"pathophysiology":"Portopulmonary hypertension is a pulmonary arteriopathy that develops in patients with portal hypertension. Portosystemic shunting allows vasoactive and proliferative mediators normally cleared or metabolised by the liver to reach the pulmonary circulation, where, together with the high cardiac output and shear stress of the hyperdynamic cirrhotic state, they promote endothelial dysfunction and vascular remodelling. The small pulmonary arteries undergo intimal proliferation, medial hypertrophy, and in situ thrombosis, raising pulmonary vascular resistance in a manner histologically similar to other forms of arterial pulmonary hypertension. The distinction from a simple high-flow state is critical, because volume overload alone raises pressure without raising resistance, whereas established portopulmonary hypertension reflects fixed remodelling that loads and eventually fails the right ventricle.","additionalDifferentials":["Hepatopulmonary syndrome","High-output pulmonary pressure elevation from the hyperdynamic cirrhotic circulation","Volume overload with post-capillary pulmonary hypertension","Idiopathic or heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension","Left heart disease-related pulmonary hypertension (group 2)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography as the screening tool for elevated pulmonary pressures, especially before liver transplantation","Right heart catheterisation to confirm pre-capillary physiology and distinguish true PoPH from a high-flow state","Assessment of portal hypertension (imaging, endoscopy, or hepatic venous pressure measurement)","Liver function and synthetic tests with assessment of chronic liver disease severity","Ventilation-perfusion scan to exclude chronic thromboembolic disease","Cardiopulmonary exercise testing and six-minute walk distance for functional assessment"],"redFlags":["Severe pulmonary hypertension in a transplant candidate, which greatly increases perioperative mortality","Syncope or signs of right heart failure indicating advanced disease","Rapid deterioration in exercise tolerance or breathlessness","Pulmonary oedema after vasodilator initiation, prompting reassessment of the haemodynamic profile"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on both the severity of the pulmonary vascular disease and the underlying liver disease; untreated moderate to severe disease carries a poor outlook. Targeted therapy can improve haemodynamics and, in selected patients, restore eligibility for liver transplantation, after which the pulmonary hypertension may stabilise or improve.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing pulmonary hypertension from pressure alone without measuring pulmonary vascular resistance, thereby confusing a high-flow state with true PoPH","Proceeding to liver transplantation without adequately treating significant pulmonary hypertension","Confusing portopulmonary hypertension with hepatopulmonary syndrome, which has opposite vascular physiology","Assuming pulmonary severity tracks liver severity when the two are independent","Overlooking screening echocardiography in cirrhotic patients being assessed for transplant"],"management":["Targeted pulmonary arterial hypertension therapy with endothelin-receptor antagonists, phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors and prostacyclin analogues to lower pulmonary vascular resistance","Careful diuresis to relieve right ventricular volume overload, avoiding excessive preload reduction","Avoidance of agents and interventions that worsen the picture, including non-selective beta-blockers that blunt cardiac output and transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunting that acutely increases preload","Right heart catheterisation to confirm pre-capillary physiology and to monitor the haemodynamic response to therapy","Evaluation for liver transplantation, with pulmonary vasodilator therapy used to reduce pulmonary vascular resistance and restore transplant candidacy in selected patients","Management at a specialist pulmonary hypertension centre in conjunction with the liver transplant multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC/ERS Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Pulmonary Hypertension","Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System"],"crossLinks":[]},"posner_schlossman_syndrome":{"name":"Posner-Schlossman syndrome","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["glaucomatocyclitic crisis","glaucomatocyclitic crises","PSS","Posner Schlossman syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by recurrent, usually unilateral attacks of markedly raised intraocular pressure accompanied by mild, low-grade anterior uveitis","The striking feature is a large pressure rise that is disproportionate to the minimal inflammation present","Symptoms are often mild for the pressure level, with slight blurring, haloes, and mild discomfort rather than the severe pain of acute angle closure","Signs include a few fine keratic precipitates on the corneal endothelium, faint aqueous cells, an open angle on gonioscopy, and often a corneal epithelial haze","Attacks are self-limiting over days to weeks and pressure returns to normal between episodes, but the same eye tends to be repeatedly affected","An association with cytomegalovirus in the aqueous has been recognised in some cases, and herpetic mechanisms are implicated","Treatment during an attack lowers pressure with topical agents and controls the inflammation with topical corticosteroids, while antiviral therapy is considered where a viral cause is suspected","Although classically benign, recurrent or prolonged attacks can lead to glaucomatous optic-nerve damage over time, so long-term monitoring is warranted"],"pathophysiology":"Posner-Schlossman syndrome produces episodic acute rises in intraocular pressure with concurrent mild anterior chamber inflammation affecting the trabecular outflow. During an attack, low-grade trabeculitis and inflammatory obstruction of an open angle reduce aqueous outflow while aqueous production may also be altered, producing a pressure spike out of proportion to the modest cellular reaction. The angle remains open, distinguishing it from angle-closure mechanisms. Infective triggers, particularly cytomegalovirus and other herpesviruses detected in the aqueous of some patients, are thought to drive the recurrent inflammation in a subset. Between crises the eye typically returns to normal, but repeated or protracted episodes can cumulatively damage the trabecular meshwork and optic nerve.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute primary angle-closure glaucoma","Herpetic (herpes simplex or zoster) anterior uveitis with raised pressure","Fuchs heterochromic uveitis","Other uveitic glaucomas","Pigmentary glaucoma with pressure spikes","Primary open-angle glaucoma with an incidental mild uveitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Applanation tonometry during and between attacks to document the episodic pressure pattern","Gonioscopy to confirm an open angle and exclude angle closure","Slit-lamp examination for keratic precipitates and grading of anterior chamber activity","OCT of the retinal nerve fibre layer and automated perimetry to detect cumulative glaucomatous damage","Aqueous sampling for cytomegalovirus or herpesvirus in recurrent or atypical cases","Baseline optic disc assessment for long-term comparison"],"redFlags":["Very high pressure during an attack that could threaten the optic nerve if prolonged","Increasingly frequent, severe, or bilateral episodes suggesting a viral or alternative diagnosis","Emerging glaucomatous disc or field change indicating cumulative damage","Features atypical for the syndrome that raise concern for herpetic uveitis or angle closure"],"prognosis":"The visual prognosis is generally good because attacks are self-limiting and pressure normalises between episodes, but a minority develop chronic glaucomatous optic-nerve damage from recurrent crises, so periodic assessment of the disc and fields is advisable.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the syndrome for acute angle-closure glaucoma because of the high pressure and haloes","Overtreating with prolonged corticosteroids and inducing a steroid pressure response","Assuming the condition is always benign and omitting long-term glaucoma surveillance","Missing an underlying cytomegalovirus or herpetic cause in recurrent cases","Failing to examine the angle and confirm it is open"],"management":["Prompt intraocular-pressure reduction during attacks with topical aqueous-suppressants such as beta-blockers, alpha-agonists and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, adding oral acetazolamide for marked pressure spikes","Short course of topical corticosteroid to settle the low-grade anterior chamber inflammation","Antiviral therapy with ganciclovir or aciclovir where cytomegalovirus or another herpesvirus is implicated in recurrent or atypical cases","Long-term surveillance of the optic disc, retinal nerve fibre layer and visual fields for cumulative glaucomatous damage from repeated crises","Glaucoma-lowering surgery for eyes developing chronic pressure elevation or optic-nerve damage refractory to medical control","Ongoing ophthalmology follow-up given the tendency for the same eye to be repeatedly affected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"post_operative_nausea_vomiting":{"name":"Postoperative nausea and vomiting","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PONV","post-anaesthetic nausea and vomiting","postoperative emesis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common and distressing complication after anaesthesia that reduces patient satisfaction, delays discharge, and occasionally causes serious complications","Established patient risk factors include female sex, non-smoking status, history of PONV or motion sickness, and younger age, often summarised in simplified risk scores","Anaesthetic and surgical factors include use of volatile agents and nitrous oxide, perioperative opioids, and longer or certain types of surgery","A multimodal approach works best: reducing baseline risk (opioid-sparing analgesia, regional techniques, total intravenous anaesthesia) and combining antiemetics from different classes","Prophylactic agents span several receptor targets, including serotonin antagonists, corticosteroids, dopamine antagonists, and antihistamines, chosen according to risk","For breakthrough symptoms, an antiemetic from a class not already used for prophylaxis is preferred rather than repeating the same drug","Adequate hydration, avoidance of unnecessary opioids, and treatment of pain and hypotension help reduce symptoms","Persistent or severe postoperative vomiting should prompt consideration of surgical, metabolic, or intracranial causes rather than assuming simple PONV"],"pathophysiology":"Nausea and vomiting are coordinated by brainstem centres receiving input from the chemoreceptor trigger zone, vestibular system, higher cortical centres, and vagal afferents from the gastrointestinal tract. Multiple neurotransmitters, including serotonin, dopamine, histamine, acetylcholine, and substance P, mediate these pathways. Anaesthetic agents, opioids, gastric distension, hypotension, and surgical stimulation activate one or more of these inputs. Because several receptor systems contribute, blocking a single pathway is often insufficient, which is why combining antiemetics acting at different receptors is more effective than escalating a single agent.","additionalDifferentials":["Bowel obstruction or ileus","Gastric distension from mask ventilation","Raised intracranial pressure or neurological cause","Metabolic disturbance such as hyponatraemia or hypoglycaemia","Medication or opioid effect","Hypotension or hypovolaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of hydration, pain, and haemodynamics","Review of anaesthetic and analgesic agents administered","Electrolytes and glucose if vomiting is persistent","Abdominal imaging if obstruction or ileus is suspected","Neurological assessment if a central cause is possible"],"redFlags":["Persistent vomiting causing dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, or aspiration risk","Vomiting with headache, focal neurology, or reduced consciousness suggesting an intracranial cause","Bilious or feculent vomiting indicating bowel obstruction","Vomiting with severe abdominal pain suggesting a surgical complication"],"prognosis":"PONV is usually self-limiting and responds to multimodal prophylaxis and treatment, but uncontrolled symptoms can delay recovery, cause dehydration, and rarely lead to aspiration or wound complications.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single antiemetic in high-risk patients instead of combining agents","Repeating the same drug class for breakthrough symptoms","Overlooking modifiable contributors such as opioids, hypotension, and dehydration","Assuming all postoperative vomiting is benign and missing a surgical or neurological cause","Failing to risk-stratify and tailor prophylaxis preoperatively"],"management":["Reduction of baseline risk with opioid-sparing multimodal analgesia, regional anaesthetic techniques and total intravenous anaesthesia using propofol while avoiding volatile agents and nitrous oxide","Multimodal antiemetic prophylaxis combining agents from different receptor classes, such as serotonin-receptor antagonists, the corticosteroid dexamethasone, dopamine antagonists, antihistamines, an anticholinergic patch and a neurokinin-receptor antagonist, according to risk","Treatment of breakthrough symptoms with an antiemetic from a class not already used for prophylaxis rather than repeating the same drug","Supportive care with intravenous fluids for hydration and correction of electrolyte disturbance from persistent vomiting","Correction of contributing factors including uncontrolled pain, hypotension and gastric distension","Escalation and investigation for a surgical or central cause when vomiting is persistent, with imaging if obstruction or ileus is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"post_thrombotic_syndrome":{"name":"Post-thrombotic syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTS","postphlebitic syndrome","post-thrombotic venous insufficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic sequela of deep vein thrombosis resulting from persistent venous obstruction and valvular damage that cause venous hypertension","Develops in a substantial proportion of patients after DVT, more commonly after proximal (iliofemoral) thrombosis and after recurrent ipsilateral DVT","Manifests as chronic limb pain, heaviness, swelling, cramps, itching, skin pigmentation and lipodermatosclerosis, and in severe cases venous ulceration","Symptoms are typically worse with standing and walking and relieved by rest and elevation; venous claudication may occur with iliac obstruction","Diagnosis is clinical and can be graded with the Villalta score, while venous duplex confirms reflux and residual obstruction","Risk is reduced by adequate initial anticoagulation, and in extensive iliofemoral DVT by early thrombus removal in selected patients","Management is largely conservative, with compression therapy, exercise, skin care, and leg elevation; iliac vein stenting may help selected patients with proximal obstruction"],"pathophysiology":"Post-thrombotic syndrome develops when a deep vein thrombus fails to resolve fully, leaving residual venous obstruction and destroying venous valves through inflammation and recanalisation. The combination of outflow obstruction and valvular reflux produces sustained ambulatory venous hypertension, transmitted to the microcirculation with the same downstream consequences as other chronic venous insufficiency, namely oedema, skin changes, and ulceration. Proximal (iliofemoral) thrombosis, recurrent thrombosis, and inadequate anticoagulation increase both the risk and the severity.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent acute deep vein thrombosis","Chronic venous insufficiency from primary valvular reflux","Lymphoedema","Cellulitis","Cardiac, renal, or hepatic oedema","Iliac vein compression (May-Thurner) with residual obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Venous duplex ultrasound to assess reflux and residual obstruction","Clinical grading with the Villalta score","CT or MR venography for suspected iliac or caval obstruction amenable to stenting","Ankle-brachial pressure index before compression to exclude arterial disease","D-dimer and duplex to distinguish recurrent acute DVT from chronic changes","Ulcer assessment (swab, and biopsy if atypical) where ulceration is present"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting recurrent acute DVT rather than chronic sequelae","A non-healing or atypical ulcer requiring biopsy","Coexisting significant arterial disease making compression hazardous","Rapidly progressive swelling suggesting new proximal obstruction or a mass"],"prognosis":"A chronic condition that can significantly impair quality of life; symptoms are often controllable with compression and lifestyle measures, but severe post-thrombotic syndrome with ulceration is difficult to treat, underscoring the importance of prevention.","pitfalls":["Mistaking post-thrombotic changes for recurrent acute DVT, or the reverse","Under-using compression therapy, the mainstay of control","Overlooking a treatable proximal (iliac) obstruction","Applying compression without excluding arterial disease"],"management":["Graduated compression therapy with stockings or bandaging as the mainstay to counter ambulatory venous hypertension and control oedema","Adequate anticoagulation of the index deep vein thrombosis and prevention of recurrent ipsilateral thrombosis, the key to limiting severity","Exercise, leg elevation, weight management and meticulous skin care to reduce symptoms and protect vulnerable skin","Venous ulcer care with compression and appropriate dressings where ulceration has developed","Endovenous recanalisation and stenting for symptomatic iliac or caval obstruction, with early thrombus removal considered in extensive iliofemoral thrombosis","Analgesia and venoactive agents for chronic limb pain and heaviness, with referral to vascular services for refractory disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","ESVS Clinical Practice Guidelines on Venous Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"post_transfusion_purpura":{"name":"Post-transfusion purpura","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTP","post-transfusion thrombocytopenic purpura","HPA-1a alloimmune purpura"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / AABB technical manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by abrupt, severe thrombocytopenia developing roughly five to twelve days after transfusion of a platelet-containing component","Most often affects multiparous women or previously transfused patients who have been alloimmunised to a platelet antigen","The great majority of cases involve antibodies to human platelet antigen 1a in an HPA-1a-negative recipient","Paradoxically the patient's own antigen-negative platelets are destroyed alongside the transfused antigen-positive platelets","Presents with purpura, mucosal bleeding and a profound drop in platelet count, with a risk of serious haemorrhage","First-line treatment is intravenous immunoglobulin, with corticosteroids and plasma exchange used in some cases","Further transfusion should ideally use antigen-negative components, and the condition is usually self-limiting over one to several weeks"],"pathophysiology":"Post-transfusion purpura is an alloimmune disorder occurring in a patient lacking a common platelet antigen, most often human platelet antigen 1a, who has been sensitised by earlier transfusion or pregnancy. Exposure to the antigen in a transfused component triggers an anamnestic rise in platelet alloantibody. These antibodies destroy the transfused antigen-positive platelets, but characteristically the recipient's own antigen-negative platelets are also consumed, producing profound thrombocytopenia. The destruction of autologous antigen-negative platelets is incompletely understood but may involve adsorption of soluble antigen or immune-complex mechanisms. The self-limiting course reflects eventual clearance of the antigenic stimulus and waning of the antibody response.","additionalDifferentials":["Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","Immune thrombocytopenia from other causes","Drug-induced immune thrombocytopenia","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Passive alloimmune thrombocytopenia from transfused antibody"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film confirming isolated severe thrombocytopenia","Platelet antibody testing, characteristically identifying anti-HPA-1a","Human platelet antigen typing of the recipient, typically HPA-1a negative","Coagulation screen to exclude disseminated intravascular coagulation","Review of transfusion history and timing relative to the platelet fall","Assessment for heparin exposure to exclude heparin-induced thrombocytopenia"],"redFlags":["Profound thrombocytopenia with mucosal or gastrointestinal bleeding","Any suspicion of intracranial haemorrhage in a severely thrombocytopenic patient","Rapidly falling platelet count after recent transfusion in a multiparous woman","Bleeding failing to respond to standard platelet transfusion"],"prognosis":"The condition is typically self-limiting, with platelet counts recovering over one to several weeks, and intravenous immunoglobulin shortens the course, though the acute thrombocytopenic phase carries a genuine risk of severe and occasionally fatal haemorrhage.","pitfalls":["Giving standard platelet transfusions, which are rapidly destroyed and may worsen the picture","Misdiagnosing it as simple immune thrombocytopenia and missing the transfusion link","Overlooking the diagnosis in a multiparous woman with recent transfusion","Failing to arrange antigen-negative components for any essential future transfusion"],"management":["Intravenous immunoglobulin as first-line treatment to accelerate recovery of the platelet count","Corticosteroids and plasma exchange for refractory or severe cases","Avoidance of random-donor platelet transfusion, which supplies further antigen-positive platelets and can worsen the reaction, reserving antigen-negative platelets for life-threatening haemorrhage","Supportive management of bleeding, including local haemostatic measures during the thrombocytopenic phase","Provision of antigen-negative, typically HPA-1a-negative, and washed cellular components for any future transfusion","Referral to haematology and transfusion medicine, with documentation of the alloantibody and notification through haemovigilance channels"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"post_transplant_lymphoproliferative_disorder":{"name":"Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder","system":"Immunology / Transplantation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTLD","post-transplant lymphoproliferative disease","EBV-driven lymphoproliferative disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard transplantation and haematopathology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A spectrum of lymphoid proliferations arising in the setting of immunosuppression after solid-organ or haematopoietic transplantation","Most cases are driven by Epstein-Barr virus, which transforms B cells that escape control when T-cell immune surveillance is suppressed","The spectrum runs from early polyclonal hyperplasia resembling infectious mononucleosis, through polymorphic disease, to monomorphic lymphoma such as diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, and rarely a classical-Hodgkin-type","Risk is highest in Epstein-Barr-virus-seronegative recipients of a seropositive graft, notably paediatric recipients, and with intense immunosuppression such as T-cell-depleting agents","Presentation is variable, including lymphadenopathy, constitutional symptoms, extranodal masses, allograft dysfunction, and gastrointestinal involvement","A rising Epstein-Barr virus load in the blood can precede or accompany disease and is used for surveillance in high-risk patients","Diagnosis requires tissue biopsy with histology, clonality assessment, and viral studies, with imaging to stage the disease","The cornerstone of treatment is reduction of immunosuppression where feasible, with rituximab for CD20-positive B-cell disease and chemotherapy for aggressive or refractory disease"],"pathophysiology":"Immunosuppression weakens cytotoxic T-cell control of Epstein-Barr-virus-infected B cells, allowing latently infected cells to proliferate unchecked. Proliferation is initially polyclonal and potentially reversible, but accumulating genetic changes can drive progression to clonal, autonomous lymphoma. A minority of cases are Epstein-Barr-virus-negative, tend to arise later, and are thought to develop through virus-independent mechanisms related to chronic immune dysregulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary or reactivated viral infection such as Epstein-Barr virus or cytomegalovirus mononucleosis","Allograft rejection when the presentation is graft dysfunction","De novo or recurrent lymphoma unrelated to transplantation","Reactive lymphadenopathy","Other opportunistic infection presenting with lymphadenopathy","Metastatic solid-organ malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional or core tissue biopsy with histopathology, immunophenotyping, and clonality studies","Epstein-Barr-encoded RNA staining of tissue together with blood viral load measurement","Cross-sectional and functional imaging for staging and disease extent","Bone marrow examination where systemic disease is suspected","Assessment of allograft function","Lactate dehydrogenase and general laboratory markers of tumour burden","Central nervous system evaluation if neurological involvement is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging masses, B symptoms, or high tumour burden suggesting aggressive monomorphic disease","Allograft dysfunction from tumour infiltration","Central nervous system involvement","A very high or rapidly rising Epstein-Barr virus load in a high-risk recipient","Fulminant disseminated disease"],"prognosis":"Early lesions may regress with reduction of immunosuppression, whereas monomorphic and aggressive disease has a worse outlook, although rituximab-based therapy has improved survival; management requires balancing tumour control against the risk of graft rejection.","pitfalls":["Failing to consider the diagnosis in any new mass or unexplained illness after transplantation","Not monitoring Epstein-Barr virus load in high-risk seronegative recipients","Allowing avoidable over-immunosuppression that drives the disorder","Treating empirically without a tissue diagnosis and clonality and viral assessment","Reducing immunosuppression without weighing the resulting risk of graft rejection"],"management":["Reduction of immunosuppression as the first-line intervention to restore cytotoxic T-cell control of Epstein-Barr-virus-driven B cells, with monitoring for allograft rejection","Rituximab, the anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody, for CD20-positive B-cell disease, with anthracycline-based combination chemotherapy for aggressive monomorphic lymphoma","Localised disease treated with surgery or radiotherapy where appropriate","Epstein-Barr virus load monitoring in high-risk recipients for surveillance, with pre-emptive reduction of immunosuppression on a rising titre, and adoptive virus-specific T-cell therapy in selected cases","Regular assessment of allograft function while balancing tumour control against the risk of rejection","Management within a joint transplantation and haemato-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Consensus classification of post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorders","Standard transplantation reference texts","Transplant immunology and haematopathology reference literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"postdural_puncture_headache":{"name":"Postdural puncture headache","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PDPH","post-lumbar puncture headache","low-pressure headache"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A characteristically postural headache that worsens on sitting or standing and improves on lying flat, developing within a few days of dural puncture","Typically frontal or occipital and may be accompanied by neck stiffness, photophobia, nausea, tinnitus, or hearing changes","Risk is higher with larger-gauge and cutting (rather than pencil-point) needles, younger age, female sex, and after accidental dural puncture during epidural placement","Diagnosis is largely clinical, but a headache lacking postural features or with neurological signs should prompt evaluation for other causes","Initial management is conservative with rest as needed, hydration, simple analgesia, and reassurance, as many cases resolve spontaneously","An epidural blood patch is the definitive treatment for severe or persistent headache and is generally highly effective","Caffeine and other pharmacological measures have limited and temporary benefit compared with a blood patch","Rare but serious complications of intracranial hypotension include subdural haematoma and cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, so atypical or worsening features require imaging"],"pathophysiology":"A persistent hole in the dura allows cerebrospinal fluid to leak into the epidural space faster than it is produced, lowering cerebrospinal fluid volume and pressure. In the upright position, reduced buoyant support causes traction on pain-sensitive intracranial structures such as the meninges and bridging veins, and compensatory cerebral vasodilation may contribute, producing the postural headache. Larger dural defects leak more and cause more severe symptoms. An epidural blood patch works by sealing the defect and raising epidural and cerebrospinal fluid pressure.","additionalDifferentials":["Migraine or tension-type headache","Meningitis (infective) or chemical meningism","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Subdural haematoma","Pre-eclampsia or eclampsia in the obstetric patient","Pneumocephalus or caffeine-withdrawal headache"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment focusing on the postural nature of the headache","Neurological examination to exclude focal signs","Brain imaging (CT or MRI) when features are atypical, worsening, or accompanied by neurological signs","Investigations for pre-eclampsia in the postpartum patient when relevant","Assessment for infection if fever or meningism is present"],"redFlags":["Loss of the postural component or a headache that becomes constant, suggesting an alternative or serious cause","Focal neurological deficit, seizure, or reduced consciousness suggesting subdural haematoma or venous thrombosis","Fever, neck stiffness, or photophobia raising concern for meningitis","Sudden thunderclap onset or features suggesting subarachnoid haemorrhage"],"prognosis":"Most postdural puncture headaches resolve within days to weeks with conservative care, and a blood patch relieves the majority of severe cases; rare progression to intracranial haemorrhage or venous thrombosis can cause serious morbidity.","pitfalls":["Assuming every post-procedure headache is a postdural puncture headache and missing a dangerous cause","Delaying an effective blood patch in severe, disabling headache","Overlooking evolving neurological signs that indicate subdural haematoma or venous sinus thrombosis","Failing to consider pre-eclampsia in a postpartum woman with headache","Relying on caffeine or bed rest alone for persistent severe symptoms"],"management":["Offer conservative first-line care with rest as needed, oral or intravenous rehydration, and simple analgesia such as paracetamol and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","Add symptomatic measures including antiemetics for nausea and caffeine as an adjunct to counter compensatory cerebral vasodilatation","Reassure the patient that many cases settle spontaneously and adopt a stepwise approach before invasive treatment","Perform an autologous epidural blood patch as the definitive treatment for severe or persistent headache, sealing the dural defect and restoring cerebrospinal fluid pressure, and repeat if the first fails","Involve the anaesthetic or pain service early for blood patch and for management of accidental dural puncture","Monitor for atypical or red-flag features (loss of postural character, focal neurological signs, fever, seizures) that warrant brain imaging to exclude intracranial haemorrhage or cerebral venous thrombosis","In the postpartum patient, actively exclude pre-eclampsia and cortical vein thrombosis as alternative causes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"posterior_cortical_atrophy":{"name":"Posterior cortical atrophy","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCA","Benson syndrome","visual variant of Alzheimer disease","posterior cortical atrophy syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with progressive impairment of higher visual and spatial processing, with early memory and insight relatively preserved","Patients struggle with reading, judging distances, locating objects and dressing, despite normal basic acuity and eyes, reflecting cortical rather than ocular visual failure","Features may include components of Balint syndrome (simultanagnosia, optic ataxia, oculomotor apraxia) and Gerstmann syndrome (acalculia, agraphia, finger agnosia, left-right disorientation)","Onset is often at a relatively young age, and patients are frequently referred first to ophthalmology because the problem seems visual","Alzheimer disease is the most common underlying pathology, though corticobasal degeneration, Lewy body disease and prion disease can also produce the syndrome","Imaging shows atrophy and hypometabolism concentrated in the parieto-occipital and posterior temporal cortex","Amyloid imaging and cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers help confirm underlying Alzheimer pathology"],"pathophysiology":"Posterior cortical atrophy is a clinical-radiological syndrome caused by neurodegeneration concentrated in the posterior cortical regions responsible for higher-order visual and spatial processing, including the occipital, parietal and posterior temporal lobes. The commonest underlying pathology is Alzheimer disease, in which amyloid plaques and tau neurofibrillary tangles accumulate predominantly in these posterior networks rather than the medial temporal memory regions, producing a visual-predominant rather than amnestic phenotype. Progressive damage to the dorsal and ventral visual pathways disrupts the integration of visual information, spatial localisation and object recognition, while primary visual acuity and the eyes themselves remain intact.","additionalDifferentials":["Typical amnestic Alzheimer disease","Dementia with Lewy bodies","Corticobasal syndrome","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease","Primary ophthalmological or optic pathway disease","Occipital stroke or structural posterior lesion","Delirium or a primary psychiatric disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain to demonstrate parieto-occipital and posterior temporal atrophy","FDG-PET to show posterior cortical hypometabolism","Amyloid PET or cerebrospinal fluid amyloid and tau markers to confirm Alzheimer pathology","Formal ophthalmological and visual acuity assessment to exclude primary eye disease","Detailed neuropsychological testing of visuospatial and higher visual functions","Assessment for parkinsonian or myoclonic features suggesting alternative pathology"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression or myoclonus raising concern for prion disease","Development of parkinsonism or asymmetrical limb apraxia suggesting corticobasal degeneration","Visual disability causing falls, driving hazards and loss of independence","Marked fluctuations or visual hallucinations pointing to Lewy body disease"],"prognosis":"Posterior cortical atrophy is progressive, with worsening visual and spatial disability and eventual spread to involve broader cognitive domains resembling more generalised dementia; prognosis reflects the underlying pathology, and management is supportive with visual aids, environmental adaptation and caregiver support.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly investigating the eyes when the problem is cortical visual processing","Missing the diagnosis because early memory and insight are preserved","Assuming all cases are Alzheimer disease without considering Lewy body, corticobasal or prion pathology","Underestimating the safety impact of cortical visual failure on driving and daily living"],"management":["Offer cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil, rivastigmine or galantamine where underlying Alzheimer pathology is confirmed, as for typical Alzheimer dementia","Provide occupational therapy and environmental adaptation (decluttering, high-contrast markings, consistent object placement) to compensate for the visuospatial deficit","Supply low-vision aids and reading and navigation strategies, recognising the failure is cortical rather than ocular","Treat associated depression and anxiety with psychological support and antidepressants as needed","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across neurology, ophthalmology, occupational therapy and cognitive services, with early involvement given the often young onset","Monitor for spread to broader cognitive domains and for emergent parkinsonian or myoclonic features suggesting an alternative underlying pathology","Provide carer support, driving cessation advice and advance care planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"posterior_cruciate_ligament_injury":{"name":"Posterior cruciate ligament injury","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCL tear","PCL rupture","posterior cruciate ligament sprain"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Review of Orthopaedics / Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic mechanisms are a dashboard injury (posteriorly directed force on a flexed knee) or a fall onto a flexed knee with a plantarflexed foot","The posterior drawer test is the most reliable examination sign, with a posterior sag observed in 90 degrees of flexion","Isolated PCL injuries are often treated non-operatively with quadriceps rehabilitation, unlike many ACL tears","High-grade or combined ligament injuries (especially with posterolateral corner injury) are more likely to require reconstruction","A PCL injury raises suspicion of a multiligament injury or a reduced knee dislocation with vascular risk","Quadriceps strengthening is central because the quadriceps dynamically substitutes for PCL function","Chronic deficiency can lead to patellofemoral and medial compartment osteoarthritis"],"pathophysiology":"The PCL is the primary restraint to posterior tibial translation. A posteriorly directed force on the proximal tibia of a flexed knee, or hyperflexion, exceeds its tensile strength and tears it, usually in the mid-substance. Posterior tibial subluxation alters joint contact and, if combined with posterolateral corner injury, produces rotatory instability; long-standing deficiency increases patellofemoral and medial compartment loading.","additionalDifferentials":["Anterior cruciate ligament injury (a false-positive anterior drawer from a posterior sag)","Posterolateral corner injury","Multiligament knee injury or reduced dislocation","Meniscal tear","Extensor mechanism disruption"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Posterior drawer and posterior sag tests, with dial test for posterolateral corner","MRI of the knee to confirm and grade the tear","Stress radiographs to quantify posterior translation","Vascular assessment where a dislocation is suspected","Examination under anaesthesia for multiligament injuries"],"redFlags":["Signs of knee dislocation with possible popliteal artery injury","Combined posterolateral corner injury producing gross rotatory instability","Common peroneal nerve palsy accompanying posterolateral injury"],"prognosis":"Isolated low-grade PCL injuries usually do well with rehabilitation; combined injuries have poorer outcomes and often need reconstruction, and chronic deficiency predisposes to osteoarthritis.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a posterior sag for a positive anterior drawer and diagnosing an ACL tear","Missing an associated posterolateral corner injury, which compromises PCL reconstruction","Overlooking a spontaneously reduced knee dislocation and its vascular risk","Neglecting quadriceps rehabilitation in non-operative management"],"management":["Manage isolated low-grade injuries non-operatively with a structured quadriceps-strengthening rehabilitation programme, as the quadriceps dynamically substitutes for posterior cruciate function","Use a dynamic posterior cruciate brace and protected weight-bearing in the early phase to limit posterior tibial sag during healing","Reserve surgical reconstruction for high-grade, symptomatic or combined ligament injuries, particularly with posterolateral corner involvement","Address associated posterolateral corner injury at the same operation to correct rotatory instability","Exclude and urgently manage an associated reduced knee dislocation, with vascular assessment for popliteal artery injury","Monitor for chronic instability and progressive patellofemoral and medial compartment osteoarthritis over time","Refer to orthopaedic or sports medicine services and physiotherapy for staged rehabilitation and return-to-sport criteria"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Review of Orthopaedics","Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"posterior_embryotoxon":{"name":"Posterior embryotoxon","system":"Eye / Anterior segment","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prominent Schwalbe line"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ophthalmology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An anteriorly displaced and prominent Schwalbe line, seen as a fine white ring or arc just inside the limbus","It is a common, usually incidental finding that can occur in normal eyes","It is a hallmark feature of Axenfeld-Rieger spectrum when accompanied by iris strands and other anomalies","In isolation it is benign, but as part of anterior segment dysgenesis it carries a significant glaucoma risk","Gonioscopy demonstrates the prominent Schwalbe line and any attached iris processes","Identification should prompt assessment for associated iris changes, glaucoma and systemic features of Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome"],"pathophysiology":"Posterior embryotoxon represents an anteriorly positioned, thickened Schwalbe line, the peripheral termination of Descemet membrane at the angle. It arises from abnormal development of the neural crest-derived anterior segment tissues. When isolated it is a benign anatomical variant, but when it occurs with iridocorneal adhesions and iris hypoplasia it is part of the Axenfeld-Rieger spectrum of anterior segment dysgenesis, in which maldevelopment of the drainage angle predisposes to glaucoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome or anomaly","Normal anatomical variant","Arcus lipoides (which is stromal, not at Schwalbe line)","Peripheral corneal degenerations","Iridocorneal endothelial syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination and gonioscopy","Assessment of the iris for hypoplasia and corectopia","Intraocular pressure measurement and glaucoma evaluation","Systemic assessment for Axenfeld-Rieger features (dental, facial, umbilical) where relevant","Family history and examination"],"redFlags":["Associated iris anomalies indicating Axenfeld-Rieger spectrum","Raised intraocular pressure or glaucoma","Systemic features of a syndrome","Progressive angle changes"],"prognosis":"Isolated posterior embryotoxon is benign with no visual consequence; when part of anterior segment dysgenesis, prognosis depends on glaucoma control.","pitfalls":["Dismissing it without checking for associated iris changes and glaucoma","Overlooking the systemic associations of Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome","Confusing it with arcus or other peripheral corneal findings","Failing to screen the family in syndromic cases"],"management":["Requires no ocular treatment when isolated, as it is a benign anatomical variant","Perform gonioscopy and slit-lamp examination to detect iris strands, corectopia and iris hypoplasia indicating the Axenfeld-Rieger spectrum","Measure intraocular pressure and undertake lifelong glaucoma surveillance where anterior segment dysgenesis is present","Treat associated glaucoma with topical pressure-lowering agents, escalating to angle or filtration surgery when medical control fails","Assess for systemic Axenfeld-Rieger features (dental, craniofacial and periumbilical anomalies) and refer accordingly","Offer genetic counselling and family examination given the autosomal dominant inheritance of the Axenfeld-Rieger spectrum"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"posterior_interosseous_syndrome":{"name":"posterior interosseous syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Peripheral nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["posterior interosseous nerve palsy","PIN syndrome","deep radial nerve entrapment"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A pure motor palsy of the posterior interosseous nerve, the deep motor branch of the radial nerve, most often entrapped at the arcade of Frohse (the proximal fibrous edge of supinator) within the radial tunnel","The hallmark is finger drop with loss of metacarpophalangeal extension of the fingers and thumb, but with PRESERVED wrist extension and NO sensory loss because the superficial radial nerve is spared","Wrist extension deviates radially because extensor carpi radialis longus (innervated proximal to the branch) is preserved while extensor carpi ulnaris is weak","Common causes include a proximal mass such as a lipoma or ganglion, rheumatoid synovitis of the elbow, radial tunnel fibrous bands, and trauma including Monteggia fracture-dislocation","It must be distinguished from radial tunnel syndrome, which causes pain over the mobile wad without motor deficit, and from extensor tendon rupture, differentiated by the tenodesis test showing intact tendons","Onset may be gradual and painless in entrapment, or acute and painful, in which case inflammatory neuralgic amyotrophy (Parsonage-Turner) affecting the posterior interosseous nerve should be considered","Management is initially conservative with splinting and observation for compressive or inflammatory cases, with surgical exploration and decompression indicated for a structural lesion or failure to recover"],"pathophysiology":"After the radial nerve divides at the level of the elbow, the posterior interosseous nerve passes into the radial tunnel and dives between the two heads of supinator, most commonly compressed at the proximal fibrous margin known as the arcade of Frohse. Because this branch is purely motor, distal to the take-off of extensor carpi radialis longus and the superficial radial sensory nerve, entrapment produces selective weakness of the digital and thumb extensors, extensor carpi ulnaris and the radial abductors of the thumb, without sensory loss. Compression from fibrous bands, a space-occupying mass, proliferative synovitis or trauma causes focal demyelination and, if sustained, axonal loss; the preserved extensor carpi radialis longus explains the radial deviation on attempted wrist extension.","additionalDifferentials":["Radial tunnel syndrome (pain without motor weakness)","Extensor tendon rupture, for example in rheumatoid arthritis","Main radial nerve palsy (which includes wrist drop and sensory loss)","C7-C8 radiculopathy","Neuralgic amyotrophy involving the posterior interosseous nerve","Sagittal band rupture causing extensor tendon subluxation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies and needle electromyography of posterior interosseous-innervated muscles with sparing of extensor carpi radialis longus and brachioradialis","Tenodesis test to confirm extensor tendon integrity and exclude rupture","High-resolution ultrasound of the radial tunnel to identify a mass or focal nerve change","MRI of the elbow and proximal forearm to detect lipoma, ganglion or synovitis","Plain radiographs if Monteggia fracture-dislocation or bony abnormality is suspected","Inflammatory markers and rheumatoid serology when synovitis is considered"],"redFlags":["Acute severe pain preceding weakness, suggesting neuralgic amyotrophy","A palpable or imaging-detected mass in the proximal forearm","Progressive weakness despite conservative treatment indicating a structural compressive lesion","Antecedent Monteggia fracture-dislocation or elbow trauma"],"prognosis":"Compressive lesions treated by removing or decompressing the offending structure, and inflammatory cases, commonly recover good extensor function, although recovery may take several months. Long-standing entrapment with axonal loss recovers less completely and may require tendon transfer to restore digital extension.","pitfalls":["Mistaking finger drop for extensor tendon rupture without performing the tenodesis test","Diagnosing a full radial nerve palsy when wrist extension and sensation are in fact preserved","Overlooking a proximal forearm mass such as a lipoma or ganglion as the cause","Failing to consider neuralgic amyotrophy in acute painful presentations","Confusing the painless motor syndrome with painful radial tunnel syndrome, which lacks true weakness"],"management":["Treat identified compressive lesions by excising a mass such as a lipoma or ganglion, or surgically decompressing fibrous bands at the arcade of Frohse","Address underlying rheumatoid synovitis of the elbow with disease-modifying antirheumatic therapy and synovectomy where relevant","Trial conservative management with activity modification, splinting and physiotherapy in mild or early entrapment without a mass","Manage suspected inflammatory neuralgic amyotrophy (Parsonage-Turner) with analgesia and consider corticosteroids, allowing spontaneous recovery","Provide hand therapy with dynamic extension splinting to maintain function while awaiting reinnervation","Monitor recovery with serial examination and electrophysiology, recognising that reinnervation may take several months","Refer for tendon transfer to restore finger and thumb extension when long-standing axonal loss precludes recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"posterior_ischaemic_optic_neuropathy":{"name":"posterior ischaemic optic neuropathy","system":"Ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PION","retrobulbar ischaemic optic neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Infarction affects the retrobulbar optic nerve, so the optic disc appears normal at onset and only becomes pale after several weeks.","Three recognised categories exist: arteritic (giant cell arteritis), non-arteritic, and perioperative.","The perioperative form classically follows prolonged spinal or cardiac surgery with prone positioning, hypotension, anaemia, large fluid shifts and significant blood loss.","Acute visual loss with a normal-looking disc should prompt consideration of a retrobulbar process, of which posterior ischaemic optic neuropathy is one cause.","A relative afferent pupillary defect is present when involvement is unilateral, but perioperative cases are frequently bilateral.","It is largely a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring neuroimaging to rule out compressive and inflammatory causes.","In the perioperative setting visual loss may be profound and bilateral, making preventive haemodynamic management the priority.","Optic atrophy developing four to eight weeks after acute loss with a previously normal disc supports the diagnosis retrospectively."],"pathophysiology":"The intraorbital posterior optic nerve is supplied by a pial vascular plexus arising from small branches of the ophthalmic artery, and this segment represents a watershed zone particularly vulnerable to systemic hypoperfusion. Non-arteritic and perioperative disease result from a critical fall in perfusion pressure, often compounded by anaemia, oedema and raised orbital or intraocular venous pressure, producing infarction of the retrobulbar nerve. The arteritic form is caused by the granulomatous vasculitis of giant cell arteritis occluding the feeding vessels.","additionalDifferentials":["Retrobulbar optic neuritis","Compressive optic neuropathy","Anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy","Chiasmal or retrochiasmal lesion","Cortical visual loss","Toxic or nutritional optic neuropathy","Functional (non-organic) visual loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein to exclude giant cell arteritis","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain and orbits with contrast to exclude compression and inflammation","Automated perimetry to characterise the field loss","Optical coherence tomography, which later demonstrates nerve fibre layer thinning","Review of perioperative records for hypotension, blood loss, positioning and fluid balance","Temporal artery biopsy where an arteritic mechanism is suspected"],"redFlags":["Systemic or laboratory features of giant cell arteritis","Bilateral visual loss on emergence from prolonged surgery","Progressive visual loss suggesting a compressive lesion","Pain on eye movement pointing to optic neuritis"],"prognosis":"Visual recovery is generally poor, particularly in the arteritic and perioperative forms where loss is often severe and permanent. In the perioperative setting prevention through meticulous haemodynamic and positioning management is the most important determinant of outcome.","pitfalls":["Expecting disc swelling and discounting the diagnosis when the early disc looks normal","Overlooking giant cell arteritis as a cause in older patients","Failing to image and thereby missing a compressive lesion","Underestimating the risk in prolonged prone or cardiac surgery"],"management":["Start immediate high-dose systemic corticosteroids when the arteritic form from giant cell arteritis is suspected, without waiting for temporal artery biopsy, to protect the fellow eye","Confirm giant cell arteritis with inflammatory markers and temporal artery biopsy and continue a prolonged tapering corticosteroid course, adding a steroid-sparing agent such as tocilizumab or methotrexate","Correct the precipitating hypoperfusion in non-arteritic and perioperative disease by optimising blood pressure, transfusing for anaemia and reducing orbital and intraocular venous pressure","Emphasise prevention in high-risk surgery through meticulous haemodynamic management, limiting prone-position duration, staged positioning and avoiding excessive blood loss and fluid overload","Exclude compressive and inflammatory causes with contrast magnetic resonance imaging of the brain and orbits, as it is largely a diagnosis of exclusion","Address vascular risk factors and refer to neuro-ophthalmology, with low-vision rehabilitation for permanent visual deficits"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course, Neuro-Ophthalmology","American Society of Anesthesiologists practice advisory on perioperative visual loss"],"crossLinks":[]},"posterior_lenticonus":{"name":"posterior lenticonus","system":"Eye / Ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["posterior lentiglobus","conical posterior lens"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / AAO Basic and Clinical Science Course","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A localised posterior bulge of the posterior lens capsule and cortex into the vitreous, usually unilateral, sporadic and more common than anterior lenticonus","It is an important cause of unilateral acquired or progressive cataract and amblyopia in infancy and childhood, and warrants early paediatric ophthalmology referral","Retro-illumination shows a central 'oil-droplet' or fish-tail reflex, and the posterior curvature may progress with growth, later opacifying into a posterior polar or lamellar cataract","The overlying posterior capsule is inherently thin and prone to spontaneous dehiscence, so a pre-existing or intra-operative posterior capsule defect should be anticipated","Because it is frequently unilateral and progressive, dense amblyopia is a major threat and mandates refractive correction and occlusion alongside any surgery","Most cases are isolated, but posterior lenticonus can occur in Lowe (oculocerebrorenal) syndrome and, less often, in the Alport spectrum, prompting a directed systemic review","Surgical planning should account for the weak or absent posterior capsule, with a low threshold for anterior vitrectomy and careful intraocular lens placement","Ultrasound or anterior segment imaging helps define the posterior lens contour and detect any capsular breach before intervention"],"pathophysiology":"Posterior lenticonus arises from a focal area of weakness and thinning in the posterior lens capsule, which normally lacks the epithelial support present anteriorly. The exact mechanism is debated but proposed causes include a primary developmental defect of the posterior capsule, abnormal persistence or traction of the hyaloid vascular system on the posterior pole, and relative overgrowth of lens fibres against a deficient capsule. The weakened region bulges backward under intralenticular forces, increasing local curvature and lens power; progressive stretching disrupts the adjacent cortical fibres and leads to focal opacification and eventual posterior polar cataract. Frank dehiscence of the attenuated capsule can occur spontaneously.","additionalDifferentials":["Posterior polar cataract","Persistent fetal vasculature (persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous)","Anterior lenticonus","Posterior lens coloboma","Lamellar or total congenital cataract of other cause","Retinoblastoma or other cause of leukocoria in a child"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp biomicroscopy with retro-illumination to demonstrate the posterior conical bulge and any opacity","Retinoscopy and cycloplegic refraction to detect the oil-droplet reflex and anisometropia","B-scan ultrasonography or anterior segment OCT to define posterior lens contour and integrity of the posterior capsule","Assessment for amblyopia with age-appropriate visual acuity testing and fixation preference","Systemic and metabolic evaluation for Lowe syndrome where clinically suspected","Fundus examination to exclude persistent fetal vasculature and posterior segment pathology"],"redFlags":["Dense unilateral cataract in an infant with high risk of irreversible deprivation amblyopia","Evidence of spontaneous posterior capsule rupture with sudden lens swelling or fragments in the vitreous","Leukocoria requiring urgent exclusion of retinoblastoma","Rapid progression of lens opacity or myopic shift in the amblyogenic period"],"prognosis":"With timely surgery, intraocular lens implantation and rigorous amblyopia therapy the visual prognosis for isolated posterior lenticonus can be favourable, though outcome depends heavily on the depth of amblyopia at presentation. Cases complicated by posterior capsule rupture or dense long-standing amblyopia have a more guarded visual outlook.","pitfalls":["Delaying referral and surgery so that dense amblyopia becomes irreversible","Being unprepared for a pre-existing posterior capsule defect during cataract surgery","Mistaking the oil-droplet reflex for a corneal or vitreous abnormality","Neglecting occlusion therapy after successful lens surgery","Failing to consider Lowe syndrome or other systemic associations"],"management":["Refer urgently to paediatric ophthalmology given the threat of dense amblyopia from a unilateral progressive lens opacity","Perform lens extraction with intraocular lens implantation once a visually significant cataract or bulging lens develops","Anticipate a thin or dehiscent posterior capsule intra-operatively and plan for anterior vitrectomy and appropriate lens fixation","Institute vigorous amblyopia therapy with cycloplegic refractive correction and occlusion of the sound eye alongside any surgery","Monitor refraction and lens contour over time, as posterior curvature may progress with growth before opacifying","Undertake systemic and metabolic evaluation for Lowe syndrome and the Alport spectrum where suspected, with appropriate paediatric and genetic referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course (Paediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus)"],"crossLinks":["anterior_lenticonus"]},"posterior_polymorphous_corneal_dystrophy":{"name":"Posterior polymorphous corneal dystrophy","system":"Eye / Cornea","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPCD","PPMD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / corneal dystrophy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A dystrophy of the corneal endothelium and Descemet membrane, usually inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern","It is characterised by vesicular, band-like or diffuse lesions at the level of Descemet membrane","Many patients are asymptomatic and diagnosed incidentally, with a generally mild course","The endothelial cells acquire epithelial-like characteristics and can migrate abnormally","It may be associated with iridocorneal adhesions, corectopia and, in some, raised intraocular pressure or glaucoma","Most cases need only monitoring, with corneal transplantation reserved for the minority developing significant oedema"],"pathophysiology":"In posterior polymorphous corneal dystrophy the corneal endothelial cells behave abnormally, taking on epithelial-like features with the capacity to proliferate and migrate across the posterior cornea and, at times, onto the anterior chamber angle and iris. This aberrant tissue produces the characteristic vesicular and band lesions of Descemet membrane and can form adhesions that distort the iris and impair aqueous outflow, causing glaucoma in a subset. Endothelial dysfunction can occasionally progress to corneal oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Fuchs endothelial corneal dystrophy","Iridocorneal endothelial (ICE) syndrome","Congenital hereditary endothelial dystrophy","Anterior segment dysgenesis","Descemet membrane changes from other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination and specular or confocal microscopy of the endothelium","Assessment of the anterior chamber angle for adhesions","Intraocular pressure measurement and glaucoma assessment","Anterior segment imaging where needed","Family history and genetic evaluation in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Raised intraocular pressure or glaucomatous damage","Progressive corneal oedema and visual loss","Extensive iridocorneal adhesions","Features overlapping with ICE syndrome (usually unilateral, non-hereditary)"],"prognosis":"Most cases are mild and stable with a good prognosis; a minority develop glaucoma or corneal decompensation requiring specific treatment or transplantation.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with the typically unilateral, acquired ICE syndrome","Overlooking associated glaucoma","Attributing corneal oedema to another cause","Missing the autosomal dominant inheritance and need to examine relatives"],"management":["Manage most patients with periodic observation alone, given the frequently mild and stable course","Perform specular or confocal microscopy and monitor endothelial function for early signs of decompensation","Measure intraocular pressure and treat associated glaucoma with pressure-lowering drops, escalating to surgery when medical control is inadequate","Reserve endothelial keratoplasty, or penetrating keratoplasty, for the minority who develop significant corneal oedema","Offer genetic counselling and family examination given the autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","IC3D classification of corneal dystrophies"],"crossLinks":[]},"posterior_scleritis":{"name":"posterior scleritis","system":"Ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["posterior scleral inflammation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, potentially sight-threatening inflammation of the sclera posterior to the equator, often with minimal or absent anterior signs so it is easily missed.","Pain is typically severe, deep and boring, worse on eye movement, and may radiate to the brow, jaw or temple with globe tenderness.","It may produce proptosis, diplopia and restricted ocular motility, mimicking orbital disease.","B-scan ultrasonography is the key investigation, showing scleral and choroidal thickening with fluid tracking into the sub-Tenon space producing the T-sign.","Posterior segment signs include exudative retinal detachment, choroidal folds, disc oedema, uveal effusion, macular oedema and a subretinal mass mimicking a tumour.","Around half of cases are associated with a systemic autoimmune disease such as rheumatoid arthritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, lupus, relapsing polychondritis or IgG4-related disease.","Management is graded by severity, using systemic non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, corticosteroids and steroid-sparing immunosuppression or biologics.","Infective and masquerade causes should be excluded before committing to immunosuppression."],"pathophysiology":"Posterior scleritis is an immune-mediated inflammation of the collagen-rich posterior sclera, frequently occurring as an ocular manifestation of systemic autoimmune or vasculitic disease. Immune-complex deposition and cell-mediated hypersensitivity drive inflammatory infiltration and oedema that thicken the sclera and track into the sub-Tenon space and adjacent choroid. This produces the characteristic ultrasonographic findings and secondary changes such as choroidal effusion, exudative retinal detachment and disc swelling.","additionalDifferentials":["Orbital inflammatory disease or orbital cellulitis","Choroidal tumour such as melanoma or metastasis","Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada disease","Central serous chorioretinopathy","Optic neuritis or papilloedema","Uveal effusion syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["B-scan ultrasonography seeking scleral thickening and the T-sign","Optical coherence tomography to demonstrate subretinal fluid and choroidal folds","Fluorescein and indocyanine green angiography","Magnetic resonance imaging or computed tomography of the orbits when orbital or mass pathology is suspected","Inflammatory and autoimmune serology including full blood count, ESR, CRP, ANA, ANCA, rheumatoid factor and angiotensin-converting enzyme","Infective screen for syphilis and tuberculosis before starting immunosuppression"],"redFlags":["Severe pain accompanied by significant visual loss","Proptosis with restricted motility raising concern for orbital cellulitis","Systemic features suggesting an underlying vasculitis","A subretinal or choroidal mass raising suspicion of malignancy"],"prognosis":"The outlook is generally good when treatment is prompt and appropriately intensive, though recurrences occur and any associated systemic disease influences overall outcome. Delayed recognition and treatment risk permanent visual loss.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosis as orbital cellulitis or a choroidal tumour leading to inappropriate management","Overlooking the diagnosis in a painful eye with a normal anterior segment","Failing to investigate for an associated systemic autoimmune disorder","Commencing corticosteroids without first excluding an infective cause"],"management":["Treat with systemic corticosteroids as the mainstay, by oral or intravenous route according to severity, to control the scleral inflammation","Add non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs in milder disease and for adjunctive symptom control","Introduce steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as methotrexate or mycophenolate, or a biologic such as an anti-TNF agent or rituximab, for refractory or recurrent disease","Exclude infective mimics including syphilis and tuberculosis before starting immunosuppression","Investigate and co-manage any associated systemic autoimmune disease (rheumatoid arthritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, lupus, relapsing polychondritis, IgG4-related disease) with rheumatology","Monitor treatment response with B-scan ultrasonography and optical coherence tomography, watching for exudative retinal detachment and macular oedema","Treat promptly and escalate immunosuppression for recurrence to prevent permanent visual loss"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course, Uveitis and Ocular Inflammation","Ryan's Retina"],"crossLinks":[]},"posterior_tibial_tendon_dysfunction":{"name":"Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction","system":"Musculoskeletal system / Foot and ankle","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["adult acquired flatfoot deformity","tibialis posterior tendinopathy","progressive collapsing foot deformity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle / Apley and Solomon's System of Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The tibialis posterior is the principal dynamic stabiliser of the medial longitudinal arch; it inverts the hindfoot and locks the transverse tarsal joint to create a rigid lever for push-off, so its failure produces a progressive collapsing flatfoot","It is the commonest cause of acquired adult flatfoot, classically in middle-aged women, and is associated with obesity, hypertension, diabetes, corticosteroid use and seronegative inflammatory arthropathy","Early disease causes medial hindfoot pain and swelling behind the medial malleolus; as the arch collapses, lateral subfibular impingement pain can develop","The too-many-toes sign and an inability to perform a single-limb heel raise with hindfoot inversion are key bedside tests of tendon competence","The Johnson and Strom staging, with the Myerson modification, runs from tenosynovitis with a flexible foot, through a flexible then rigid flatfoot, to fixed deformity with ankle valgus and deltoid ligament failure","A watershed zone of relative hypovascularity just distal and posterior to the medial malleolus predisposes the tendon to degeneration and rupture","Flexible deformity is managed with orthoses, medial arch support or an ankle-foot orthosis and structured rehabilitation, whereas fixed deformity or ankle involvement requires reconstructive or fusion surgery"],"pathophysiology":"Posterior tibial tendon dysfunction begins as degenerative tendinosis and tenosynovitis within the watershed hypovascular segment behind the medial malleolus, where repetitive load, ageing and impaired healing lead to mucoid degeneration, elongation and eventual attritional rupture. As the tibialis posterior loses its inverting and arch-supporting action, the unopposed peroneus brevis and the body's weight drive progressive hindfoot valgus, forefoot abduction and collapse of the medial longitudinal arch. Secondary attenuation of the spring (plantar calcaneonavicular) ligament and later the deltoid ligament allows the talar head to plantarflex and the deformity to become fixed, with lateral subfibular and sinus tarsi impingement and, in advanced disease, valgus tilt of the ankle. Inflammatory arthropathy and metabolic factors accelerate this degenerative cascade.","additionalDifferentials":["Tarsal coalition or accessory navicular causing a rigid or painful flatfoot","Midfoot or hindfoot osteoarthritis and Charcot neuroarthropathy","Inflammatory arthropathy such as rheumatoid or seronegative spondyloarthritis","Deltoid or spring ligament injury without primary tendon disease","Tarsal tunnel syndrome or medial ankle nerve entrapment","Deep posterior compartment or medial ankle tenosynovitis of other tendons"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Weight-bearing foot and ankle radiographs assessing talonavicular coverage, talo-first metatarsal alignment and arch collapse","MRI to grade tendon degeneration, tears and associated spring and deltoid ligament involvement","Ultrasound for dynamic assessment of tendon integrity and tenosynovitis","Standing hindfoot alignment view or weight-bearing CT for coronal deformity","Single-limb heel-rise test and too-many-toes assessment documented over time","Screening for inflammatory arthropathy and metabolic risk factors where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["A hot, deformed insensate foot suggesting Charcot neuroarthropathy, especially in diabetes","Rapidly progressive deformity or fixed valgus with ankle involvement","Features of an inflammatory or septic process rather than degenerative tendinopathy","Neurovascular compromise or non-healing medial ankle ulceration"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis, flexible deformity often responds well to orthoses and rehabilitation, and joint-preserving reconstruction can restore function; once deformity becomes rigid or the ankle is involved, arthrodesis is usually required and full correction of established deformity is less predictable.","pitfalls":["Dismissing early medial ankle pain and swelling as a simple sprain and missing tenosynovitis","Failing to distinguish a flexible from a rigid deformity, which fundamentally changes management","Overlooking a hot deformed neuropathic foot and treating Charcot arthropathy as tendon dysfunction","Attempting soft-tissue reconstruction alone without correcting hindfoot malalignment with osteotomy, so the reconstruction fails"],"management":["Manage early flexible disease non-operatively with medial-arch orthoses or a custom foot orthosis and immobilisation in a walking boot or cast during acute tenosynovitis","Prescribe eccentric tibialis posterior strengthening and calf-stretching physiotherapy, with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain, and avoid corticosteroid injection into the tendon","Address modifiable contributors including obesity, diabetes and inflammatory arthropathy","Offer joint-preserving reconstruction, such as flexor digitorum longus tendon transfer with a medialising calcaneal osteotomy, for flexible deformity failing conservative care","Perform hindfoot or triple arthrodesis for rigid, fixed deformity or when the ankle joint is involved","Monitor deformity progression with the single-limb heel-rise test, too-many-toes sign and weight-bearing radiographs","Refer to orthopaedic and podiatry services for staging-based decision-making"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","Apley and Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Trauma"],"crossLinks":[]},"posterior_urethral_polyp":{"name":"Posterior urethral polyp","system":"Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["posterior urethral polyp","fibroepithelial polyp of posterior urethra","congenital urethral polyp"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / EAU Guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign fibroepithelial polyp that typically arises from the posterior (prostatic) urethra near the verumontanum","In boys it classically causes intermittent obstructive voiding, haematuria, or a polyp that prolapses into the bladder neck and produces intermittent obstruction","In adults it may cause haematuria, haematospermia, voiding symptoms, or be found incidentally","It is a recognised benign cause of lower urinary tract symptoms and, when near the prostate, may be associated with PSA elevation as a benign mimic of malignancy","Cystourethroscopy demonstrates a mobile pedunculated lesion, and urethrography may show a corresponding filling defect","Histology shows a fibrovascular core covered by urothelium without malignant features","Treatment is endoscopic transurethral resection, which is usually curative"],"pathophysiology":"A posterior urethral polyp is a benign proliferation with a fibrovascular core lined by normal urothelium, generally considered congenital in boys and arising on a stalk near the verumontanum. Its mobility on a pedicle allows intermittent prolapse into the bladder neck, producing positional or intermittent obstruction, while surface trauma explains episodes of haematuria and haematospermia.","additionalDifferentials":["Prostatic-type urethral polyp with prostatic epithelium","Urethral or bladder neck papilloma","Urothelial carcinoma of the urethra","Posterior urethral valves in children","Verumontanum mucosal gland hyperplasia","Prostatic adenocarcinoma as a PSA-raising mimic"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cystourethroscopy to visualise and localise the polyp","Voiding or retrograde urethrography showing a mobile filling defect","Urinalysis and urine cytology to assess haematuria","Pelvic MRI or ultrasound if a mass or malignancy is suspected","Histopathology of the resected specimen for definitive diagnosis","PSA interpreted cautiously, recognising the possibility of benign elevation"],"redFlags":["Persistent haematuria or atypical cytology suggesting urothelial malignancy","Significant obstruction or urinary retention","A rising or persistently elevated PSA warranting exclusion of prostate cancer","Recurrent lesions after resection"],"prognosis":"Excellent; endoscopic resection is generally curative with a low recurrence rate, and the typical fibroepithelial polyp is benign with no malignant potential.","pitfalls":["Attributing a raised PSA to the polyp and missing a coexisting prostate cancer","Mistaking the polyp for posterior urethral valves in a child","Incomplete resection of the stalk leading to recurrence","Failing to send tissue for histology to exclude malignancy"],"management":["Perform endoscopic transurethral resection or excision of the polyp, which is generally curative","Relieve any acute obstruction from a polyp prolapsing into the bladder neck with catheter drainage before definitive resection","Send the resected specimen for histopathology to confirm a benign fibroepithelial polyp and exclude malignancy","Undertake surveillance cystourethroscopy for the low risk of recurrence","Interpret PSA cautiously, recognising benign elevation as a mimic of prostate malignancy","Refer to urology, or paediatric urology in boys, for endoscopic management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","EAU Guidelines"],"crossLinks":["verumontanum_hyperplasia"]},"posterior_urethral_valves":{"name":"Posterior urethral valves","system":"Paediatric surgery / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PUV","congenital obstructing posterior urethral membrane"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Bailey & Love (paediatric surgery)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common cause of congenital lower urinary tract obstruction in male infants, caused by obstructing membranous folds in the posterior urethra","Many cases are suspected antenatally from bilateral hydronephrosis, a thick-walled distended bladder, a dilated posterior urethra (keyhole sign), and oligohydramnios","Severe early obstruction and oligohydramnios can lead to pulmonary hypoplasia, which may be the immediate life-threatening problem at birth","Postnatal presentation ranges from a palpable bladder, poor urinary stream, and straining to urosepsis or failure to thrive, and older boys may present with voiding difficulty or incontinence","The voiding cystourethrogram is the key diagnostic study, showing a dilated posterior urethra, bladder trabeculation, and often vesicoureteric reflux","Initial management is bladder decompression with a catheter, correction of fluid and electrolyte disturbance, and treatment of infection before definitive intervention","Definitive treatment is endoscopic ablation of the valves, with temporary urinary diversion (vesicostomy) reserved for selected infants","Long-term follow-up is essential because bladder dysfunction and progressive renal impairment can persist or evolve even after successful valve ablation"],"pathophysiology":"Posterior urethral valves are congenital obstructing tissue folds in the posterior (prostatic) urethra of males that impede bladder emptying from early in fetal life. The resulting high-pressure obstruction causes bladder wall hypertrophy and trabeculation, vesicoureteric reflux, and back-pressure that dilates the ureters and damages the developing kidneys, producing dysplasia and impaired concentrating ability. Reduced fetal urine output causes oligohydramnios, which in turn impairs lung development and can result in pulmonary hypoplasia. The renal and bladder damage sustained in utero often determines lifelong outcome.","additionalDifferentials":["Ureteropelvic or ureterovesical junction obstruction","Prune belly (Eagle-Barrett) syndrome","Bilateral high-grade vesicoureteric reflux","Neurogenic bladder","Urethral atresia or stricture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Voiding cystourethrogram to demonstrate the valves and assess reflux and bladder morphology","Renal and bladder ultrasound to evaluate hydronephrosis and bladder wall thickness","Serum electrolytes, urea, and creatinine to assess renal function (interpreted after the first days of life)","Cystoscopy for direct visualisation and treatment of the valves","Isotope renography to assess differential renal function and drainage"],"redFlags":["Neonatal respiratory distress from pulmonary hypoplasia secondary to oligohydramnios, which is immediately life-threatening","Urosepsis in an infant with obstruction requiring urgent decompression and antibiotics","Acute kidney injury with electrolyte disturbance and rising creatinine","Progressive deterioration in renal function or bladder function despite valve ablation"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends largely on the degree of irreversible renal dysplasia established in utero. Many boys do well after valve ablation, but a significant proportion develop chronic kidney disease and bladder dysfunction, and some progress to end-stage renal failure requiring transplantation. Early nadir creatinine after treatment is a useful prognostic indicator.","pitfalls":["Attributing an antenatal keyhole bladder or bilateral hydronephrosis to a benign cause and delaying evaluation","Interpreting neonatal creatinine in the first day or two as the baby's true renal function rather than the mother's","Assuming valve ablation cures the child and neglecting long-term renal and bladder surveillance","Overlooking bladder dysfunction as an ongoing cause of upper tract deterioration"],"management":["Stabilise the newborn first with bladder decompression via a urethral catheter, correction of fluid and electrolyte disturbance, and treatment of any infection","Provide neonatal respiratory support for pulmonary hypoplasia, which may be the immediate life-threatening problem at birth","Perform definitive endoscopic valve ablation once the infant is stable, with a temporary vesicostomy as an alternative in the very small or unstable baby","Institute long-term management of the resulting valve-bladder dysfunction with anticholinergics, timed or double voiding and clean intermittent catheterisation where needed","Provide nephrology surveillance of renal function and growth, managing chronic kidney disease with attention to fluid, electrolytes, acidosis and nutrition, and plan for dialysis or transplantation if end-stage failure develops","Monitor for and treat urinary tract infections and vesicoureteric reflux, with antibiotic prophylaxis where indicated","Offer antenatal counselling, delivery planning and family support given the guarded renal prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"posterior_uveitis":{"name":"Posterior uveitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["choroiditis","chorioretinitis","retinochoroiditis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents with painless blurred vision and floaters rather than the pain and redness seen with anterior involvement","Inflammation centres on the choroid and retina, so signs are found on fundus examination as vitritis, focal or multifocal chorioretinal lesions, and vascular sheathing","Vitreous haze grading gives a useful measure of activity and treatment response","Toxoplasma retinochoroiditis is a leading infective cause and characteristically produces a focal lesion adjacent to an old pigmented scar","Non-infective causes include sarcoidosis, Behcet disease, sympathetic ophthalmia, and the white-dot syndromes","Distinguishing infective from immune-mediated aetiology is essential because immunosuppression can worsen an untreated infection","Management of non-infective disease relies on corticosteroids and steroid-sparing immunosuppression, whereas infective disease requires targeted antimicrobial cover first","Complications that threaten sight include cystoid macular oedema, retinal detachment, choroidal neovascular membranes, and optic nerve involvement"],"pathophysiology":"Posterior uveitis is inflammation predominantly affecting the choroid and overlying retina, often with secondary vitreous involvement. It may arise from direct infection (for example Toxoplasma, tuberculosis, syphilis, viral retinitis) or from immune-mediated processes such as sarcoidosis, Behcet disease, or an autoimmune reaction to ocular antigens as in sympathetic ophthalmia. Inflammatory cells and mediators disrupt the outer blood-retinal barrier, causing exudation, oedema, and eventual chorioretinal scarring. Ischaemia and chronic inflammation can drive neovascularisation, and macular or optic nerve involvement determines the visual outcome.","additionalDifferentials":["Intraocular lymphoma masquerading as chronic vitritis","Central serous chorioretinopathy","Retinal vein occlusion with macular oedema","Endophthalmitis","Age-related macular degeneration with choroidal neovascular membrane","Hereditary retinal dystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination and vitreous haze grading to assess activity","Optical coherence tomography to detect macular oedema and monitor response","Fundus fluorescein and indocyanine green angiography to map retinal and choroidal inflammation","Serology and targeted testing for toxoplasma, syphilis, tuberculosis, and sarcoidosis","Vitreous or aqueous sampling with PCR or cytology when infection or lymphoma is suspected","Systemic imaging (e.g. chest imaging) when sarcoidosis or tuberculosis is considered"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive retinitis suggesting viral (necrotising) infection needing urgent antiviral therapy","Bilateral panuveitis after ocular injury or surgery raising concern for sympathetic ophthalmia","Persistent vitritis in an older patient that may represent intraocular lymphoma","Macular or optic nerve involvement with declining acuity requiring prompt treatment"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the underlying cause and on whether the macula and optic nerve are spared; focal treatable infections such as toxoplasmosis often heal with scarring away from the fovea, but recurrent or immune-mediated disease can cause cumulative visual loss from macular oedema, scarring, or neovascular complications.","pitfalls":["Starting immunosuppression before excluding an infective cause, which can allow infection to progress","Overlooking cystoid macular oedema as the reason for reduced acuity in a quiet-looking eye","Attributing chronic vitritis in an older patient to inflammation and missing masquerade lymphoma","Failing to screen for a systemic disease that the eye findings are heralding"],"management":["Establish whether the cause is infective or immune-mediated first, since immunosuppression can worsen an untreated infection","Treat infective causes with targeted antimicrobials: anti-toxoplasma therapy such as pyrimethamine with sulfadiazine or co-trimoxazole for toxoplasma retinochoroiditis, antivirals such as valganciclovir or aciclovir for viral retinitis, and appropriate antibiotics for syphilitic or tuberculous disease","Treat non-infective immune-mediated disease with systemic corticosteroids, adding steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as methotrexate, mycophenolate or azathioprine, or a biologic anti-TNF agent for sarcoidosis or Behcet disease","Use local corticosteroid therapy by periocular or intravitreal route as an adjunct, only after infection is excluded or covered","Detect and treat macular oedema and inflammatory complications, monitoring activity with vitreous haze grading and optical coherence tomography","Screen for and cover latent tuberculosis and syphilis before initiating immunosuppression","Refer to uveitis specialists and relevant physicians to co-manage associated systemic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"posterior_vitreous_detachment":{"name":"Posterior vitreous detachment","system":"Special senses / Eye (vitreoretinal interface)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PVD","acute posterior vitreous detachment","vitreous separation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Royal College of Ophthalmologists guidance / Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Age-related separation of the posterior vitreous cortex from the internal limiting membrane of the retina, driven by vitreous liquefaction (synchysis) and weakening of vitreoretinal adhesion","Classic symptoms are the sudden onset of floaters together with flashing lights (photopsia) from vitreoretinal traction, often more noticeable in dim light or with eye movement","A Weiss ring, the detached annulus of condensed vitreous from around the optic disc, is effectively pathognomonic when seen","The principal danger is that traction may create a retinal tear, which can progress to rhegmatogenous retinal detachment; a meaningful proportion of symptomatic acute PVDs have a tear at presentation","Pigmented cells in the anterior vitreous, described as tobacco dust or Shafer's sign, strongly suggest a retinal break and mandate careful peripheral examination","Accompanying vitreous haemorrhage substantially raises the likelihood of a retinal tear and should prompt urgent, thorough retinal assessment","All symptomatic patients need dilated fundus examination with scleral indentation; those with an initially normal examination warrant safety-netting and often repeat review because breaks can appear later","Risk is increased in myopia, after cataract surgery, following trauma, and in the fellow eye of a patient who has had a PVD-related tear or detachment"],"pathophysiology":"With ageing the vitreous gel undergoes liquefaction (synchysis) while the adhesion between the posterior vitreous cortex and the internal limiting membrane weakens. When liquefied vitreous passes through a dehiscence in the posterior cortex into the retrohyaloid space, the cortex peels away from the retina, producing a posterior vitreous detachment. Where focal vitreoretinal adhesions persist, for example at the vitreous base, along retinal vessels or at areas of lattice degeneration, dynamic traction during separation can tear the retina or avulse a vessel, causing a retinal break and/or vitreous haemorrhage. Photopsiae arise from mechanical stimulation of the retina by this traction, while floaters reflect condensed vitreous and the Weiss ring casting shadows on the retina.","additionalDifferentials":["Retinal tear or rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Vitreous haemorrhage from other causes such as proliferative retinopathy, retinal vein occlusion or a retinal macroaneurysm","Retinal migraine or cortical or occipital photopsia","Posterior uveitis or vitritis producing floaters","Vitreomacular traction syndrome","Ocular ischaemia or other retinal cause of photopsia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated slit-lamp biomicroscopy with peripheral retinal examination and scleral indentation","Assessment for Shafer's sign (pigment in the anterior vitreous)","B-scan ultrasonography when the fundus view is obscured, for example by vitreous haemorrhage, to detect PVD, tears or detachment","OCT to demonstrate a Weiss ring, vitreomacular traction or coexisting macular pathology","Documented visual acuity and confrontation or formal visual field assessment","Repeat examination after an interval, or sooner if new symptoms develop, to detect delayed retinal breaks"],"redFlags":["A curtain, shadow or progressive field defect suggesting retinal detachment","A sudden shower of floaters or vitreous haemorrhage indicating a probable retinal tear","Tobacco dust (Shafer's sign) in the anterior vitreous","Progressive increase in floaters or flashes, or any loss of central vision"],"prognosis":"An uncomplicated posterior vitreous detachment is benign, and symptoms usually diminish over weeks to months as floaters settle and the patient adapts. The overall prognosis depends on prompt detection and treatment of any associated retinal tear, which when treated early usually prevents progression to detachment.","pitfalls":["Failing to perform, or to repeat, an adequate peripheral retinal examination with indentation and so missing a retinal break","Discharging a patient with a vitreous haemorrhage without excluding a tear or arranging timely follow-up","Ignoring new or changing symptoms after an initially normal examination","Attributing all floaters to benign PVD without considering uveitis, haemorrhage or detachment"],"management":["Reassure and observe uncomplicated cases without a retinal break, as symptoms usually settle over weeks to months as floaters disperse and the patient adapts","Perform dilated peripheral retinal examination with scleral indentation at presentation to detect any retinal tear","Treat an identified retinal break promptly with laser retinopexy or cryotherapy to prevent progression to rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Investigate obscuring vitreous haemorrhage with B-scan ultrasonography and pursue urgent retinal assessment given the high associated tear rate","Safety-net with clear advice to return urgently for a shower of new floaters, increasing flashes, or a curtain or shadow across the vision","Arrange repeat examination after an interval, and sooner if new symptoms develop, to detect delayed retinal breaks"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Royal College of Ophthalmologists guidance on posterior vitreous detachment and retinal detachment","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","Ryan's Retina"],"crossLinks":[]},"postinflammatory_hyperpigmentation":{"name":"Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation","system":"Dermatology / Pigmentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PIH","post-inflammatory hypermelanosis","postinflammatory pigmentation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Acquired hypermelanosis that develops at sites of previous inflammation or injury (acne, eczema, lichen planus, trauma, procedures) and mirrors the distribution of the preceding dermatosis","Far more frequent, more intense and more persistent in darker skin phototypes (Fitzpatrick IV-VI)","Wood-lamp examination distinguishes epidermal pigment, which is accentuated and brown, from dermal pigment, which appears blue-grey and is not accentuated, and this guides treatment expectations","Epidermal pigment responds reasonably to photoprotection and topical lightening agents, whereas dermal pigment is far more refractory and slow to clear","Interface dermatoses such as lichen planus cause basal damage and pigment incontinence, producing prominent, long-lasting dermal pigmentation","Management rests on treating the underlying inflammatory dermatosis, strict photoprotection and topical depigmenting agents, with chemical peels or laser as cautious adjuncts","Overly aggressive treatment, peels or lasers can paradoxically worsen pigmentation, particularly in darker skin"],"pathophysiology":"Cutaneous inflammation stimulates melanocytes to increase melanin synthesis through inflammatory mediators such as prostaglandins, leukotrienes and cytokines, producing epidermal hypermelanosis. When the inflammation damages the basal layer and dermoepidermal junction, melanin is released and drops into the dermis (pigment incontinence), where it is engulfed by melanophages to give a deeper, longer-lasting dermal hyperpigmentation. The depth of pigment therefore reflects the intensity and level of the preceding inflammatory process.","additionalDifferentials":["Melasma","Solar lentigines and other benign lentigines","Residual fixed drug eruption or other drug-induced pigmentation","Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor","Macular or lichen amyloidosis","Ashy dermatosis and lichen planus pigmentosus","Acanthosis nigricans"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wood-lamp examination to gauge epidermal versus dermal pigment","Dermoscopy to assess pigment pattern and depth","Review of medication history and the triggering dermatosis","Patch testing where a contact or photocontact cause is suspected","Skin biopsy where the diagnosis or an alternative pigmented lesion is uncertain"],"redFlags":["An irregular, changing or atypical pigmented lesion that could represent melanoma rather than benign pigmentation","Ongoing or poorly controlled underlying inflammatory dermatosis driving continued pigmentation","Signs of exogenous ochronosis from prolonged misuse of topical lightening agents","Widespread pigmentation suggesting a systemic or drug-related cause"],"prognosis":"Epidermal post-inflammatory pigment usually fades gradually over months once the inflammation is controlled, whereas dermal pigment may persist for years or be effectively permanent; darker skin phototypes tend to have slower resolution.","pitfalls":["Failing to treat the underlying inflammatory dermatosis, so pigmentation recurs","Using aggressive lasers or peels that worsen pigmentation, especially in darker skin","Mislabelling melasma or another primary pigmentary disorder as post-inflammatory change","Neglecting photoprotection, which allows sun exposure to deepen and prolong the pigmentation"],"management":["Treat the underlying inflammatory dermatosis (acne, eczema, lichen planus) to halt ongoing melanin production at the affected sites","Enforce strict photoprotection with broad-spectrum sunscreen and sun avoidance, as ultraviolet exposure deepens and prolongs the pigmentation","Apply topical depigmenting agents such as hydroquinone, retinoids, azelaic acid or a combination formulation, which are most effective for epidermal pigment","Set realistic expectations using Wood-lamp findings, since dermal blue-grey pigment is refractory and slow to clear while epidermal brown pigment responds better","Add chemical peels or laser cautiously as adjuncts, given the risk of aggravating pigmentation in darker skin phototypes","Monitor over months and avoid irritant or aggressive treatments that could provoke further inflammation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"postpartum_depression":{"name":"Postpartum depression","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["postnatal depression","peripartum depression","major depressive disorder with peripartum onset","PPD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is a major depressive episode with onset during pregnancy or in the weeks following delivery, presenting with persistent low mood, loss of interest, and the associated features of depression","It must be distinguished from the common, transient postpartum blues, which are mild and self-limiting within about two weeks, and from the rare psychiatric emergency of postpartum psychosis","Presentations often include prominent anxiety, guilt about parenting ability, and intrusive thoughts, and mothers may be reluctant to disclose symptoms for fear of judgement","Actively assess suicidal ideation and any thoughts of harming the infant, as these carry serious risk and require urgent action","Routine screening in the perinatal period, for example with a validated postnatal depression scale, improves detection of an often hidden condition","Management combines psychotherapy and, when indicated, antidepressant pharmacotherapy, with treatment choices weighing symptom severity against breastfeeding considerations","Untreated maternal depression can adversely affect mother-infant bonding and child development, so timely treatment benefits both mother and child","Postpartum psychosis, with confusion, delusions, or thoughts of harming the infant, is an emergency requiring immediate assessment and usually admission"],"pathophysiology":"Postpartum depression is thought to result from the interaction of an abrupt drop in reproductive hormones after delivery with a predisposing vulnerability, superimposed on the neurobiology shared with major depression, including monoamine and stress-axis dysregulation. Sleep deprivation, psychosocial stressors, a history of mood disorder, and inadequate support further increase risk in susceptible women.","additionalDifferentials":["Postpartum blues (transient and self-limiting)","Postpartum psychosis","Bipolar disorder presenting or relapsing in the perinatal period","Postpartum thyroiditis and other thyroid dysfunction","Anaemia and other medical causes of fatigue","Anxiety disorders or obsessive-compulsive disorder with perinatal onset"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Validated perinatal depression screening, such as the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale","Structured suicide risk assessment and assessment of thoughts of harm to the infant","Thyroid function testing","Full blood count to exclude anaemia","Screening for a history of bipolar disorder before starting an antidepressant"],"redFlags":["Suicidal ideation with plan or intent","Thoughts of harming the infant or command-type intrusive urges","Features of postpartum psychosis such as confusion, delusions, or hallucinations","Severe self-neglect or inability to care for the infant"],"prognosis":"Postpartum depression generally responds well to treatment, and most women recover, but there is an increased risk of recurrence in subsequent pregnancies and of future depressive episodes. Prognosis is better with early detection, treatment, and social support, and worse when the condition is unrecognised, when there is a bipolar diathesis, or when comorbidities and psychosocial adversity persist.","pitfalls":["Dismissing significant depression as normal postpartum blues","Failing to ask directly about suicidal thoughts and thoughts of harming the infant","Missing an underlying bipolar disorder before prescribing an antidepressant","Overlooking postpartum thyroiditis as a contributor","Not recognising postpartum psychosis as an emergency requiring urgent care"],"management":["Offer psychological therapy such as cognitive behavioural therapy or interpersonal therapy as first-line for mild to moderate illness","Prescribe antidepressant pharmacotherapy, typically a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor, for moderate to severe illness or when psychotherapy is insufficient, choosing an agent compatible with breastfeeding","Screen for a personal or family history of bipolar disorder before starting an antidepressant to avoid precipitating a manic switch","Undertake structured assessment of suicidal ideation and thoughts of harming the infant, acting urgently on significant risk","Distinguish from transient postpartum blues and from the emergency of postpartum psychosis, which requires urgent psychiatric admission and may need antipsychotics or electroconvulsive therapy","Mobilise practical and social support, address sleep, and involve health visitors and perinatal mental health services","Arrange follow-up given the risk of recurrence and plan monitoring in future pregnancies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"postpartum_haemorrhage":{"name":"Postpartum haemorrhage","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["postpartum hemorrhage","PPH","obstetric haemorrhage after delivery"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Postpartum haemorrhage is excessive bleeding after birth; primary PPH occurs within the first 24 hours and secondary PPH from 24 hours up to about 12 weeks postpartum","The causes are usefully remembered as the four Ts: tone (uterine atony), trauma (genital tract lacerations), tissue (retained placenta or products), and thrombin (coagulopathy)","Uterine atony is by far the commonest cause, so uterine massage and uterotonic agents are first-line alongside resuscitation","Active management of the third stage of labour with a prophylactic uterotonic reduces the incidence and severity of PPH","Blood loss is frequently underestimated visually, so vital signs, quantitative assessment, and a high index of suspicion are essential","A structured stepwise escalation moves from uterotonics to mechanical measures (bimanual compression, balloon tamponade) and then to surgical options such as compression sutures, vessel ligation, or hysterectomy","Tranexamic acid given early reduces death from bleeding and should be administered promptly","Early involvement of senior obstetric, anaesthetic, and haematology teams with activation of a massive transfusion protocol improves outcomes"],"pathophysiology":"Normal postpartum haemostasis depends on vigorous myometrial contraction that mechanically compresses the spiral arteries of the placental bed, supported by the clotting cascade. Postpartum haemorrhage results when one or more of these mechanisms fail: the uterus fails to contract (atony, often from overdistension, prolonged or augmented labour, infection, or retained tissue); vessels are torn by genital tract trauma; retained placental tissue or invasive placentation prevents effective contraction; or coagulation is impaired by dilution, consumption, or a pre-existing disorder. Ongoing loss can precipitate hypovolaemic shock and a self-perpetuating coagulopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Uterine atony","Genital tract trauma including cervical or vaginal lacerations and haematomas","Retained placenta or retained products of conception","Placenta accreta spectrum","Uterine inversion or rupture","Inherited or acquired coagulopathy including disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative measurement of blood loss and continuous vital sign monitoring","Full blood count and serial haemoglobin","Coagulation screen with fibrinogen level","Group and cross-match with early activation of transfusion support","Examination under anaesthesia and ultrasound to identify retained tissue or genital tract trauma"],"redFlags":["Tachycardia, hypotension, and other signs of hypovolaemic shock","Continued brisk bleeding despite uterotonics and uterine massage","Clinical or laboratory evidence of coagulopathy","A high uterine fundus with a boggy uterus indicating atony, or a suddenly inverted uterus","Suspected placenta accreta spectrum after a difficult placental delivery"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition, resuscitation, and stepwise treatment most women recover fully. Delay in diagnosis or treatment can lead to shock, organ failure, need for hysterectomy with loss of fertility, and death; postpartum haemorrhage remains one of the leading causes of maternal mortality worldwide, particularly in low-resource settings.","pitfalls":["Underestimating blood loss by visual assessment alone","Failing to recognise ongoing bleeding masked by the physiological cardiovascular reserve of pregnancy until late collapse","Not systematically excluding retained tissue and genital tract trauma when atony seems controlled but bleeding persists","Delaying tranexamic acid and blood product replacement","Escalating too slowly to mechanical and surgical measures when medical therapy fails"],"management":["Resuscitate simultaneously with large-bore intravenous access, warmed crystalloid, oxygen, and early activation of the major haemorrhage and transfusion protocol","Treat uterine atony, the commonest cause, with uterine massage and uterotonics such as oxytocin, ergometrine, carboprost or other prostaglandins, and misoprostol","Give tranexamic acid early to reduce bleeding and mortality","Identify and address the cause using the four Ts, repairing genital tract trauma, removing retained placental tissue, and correcting coagulopathy with targeted blood products and fibrinogen replacement","Escalate through mechanical measures such as bimanual compression and intrauterine balloon tamponade to surgical options including compression sutures, uterine or internal iliac artery ligation, interventional radiology embolisation, and hysterectomy as a last resort","Prevent through active management of the third stage of labour with a prophylactic uterotonic","Monitor with quantitative blood-loss assessment, continuous vital signs, and serial haemoglobin, coagulation and fibrinogen"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"postpartum_thyroiditis":{"name":"Postpartum thyroiditis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["postpartum thyroid dysfunction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrinology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A painless autoimmune destructive thyroiditis occurring within about a year of delivery (or miscarriage)","It classically follows a triphasic course of thyrotoxicosis, then hypothyroidism, then recovery, though many present with only one phase","It is strongly associated with thyroid peroxidase antibodies detectable before or during pregnancy","The thyrotoxic phase is destructive with low radioactive iodine uptake, distinguishing it from Graves disease","Symptoms are easily mistaken for normal postpartum fatigue or postnatal depression","A significant proportion develop permanent hypothyroidism and it tends to recur after future pregnancies"],"pathophysiology":"Postpartum thyroiditis is an autoimmune lymphocytic thyroiditis unmasked by the immune rebound that follows the relative immunosuppression of pregnancy. Immune-mediated destruction of thyroid follicles releases stored hormone, causing a transient thyrotoxic phase, after which hormone depletion produces hypothyroidism before the gland usually recovers. Thyroid peroxidase antibodies mark the underlying autoimmunity and predict risk. As a destructive process, radioactive iodine uptake is low and antithyroid drugs are unhelpful.","additionalDifferentials":["Graves disease presenting postpartum","Silent (painless) thyroiditis unrelated to pregnancy","Normal postpartum fatigue or postnatal depression","Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis","Sheehan syndrome (for postpartum hypothyroid symptoms)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thyroid function tests across the phases","Thyroid peroxidase antibodies","Radioactive iodine uptake if Graves disease must be excluded (avoiding breastfeeding considerations)","Follow-up thyroid function to detect permanent hypothyroidism","Screening in subsequent pregnancies given recurrence risk"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting Graves disease","Symptoms mislabelled as postnatal depression","Persistent hypothyroidism affecting the mother and breastfeeding","Severe thyrotoxic symptoms"],"prognosis":"Most women return to euthyroidism within a year, but many develop permanent hypothyroidism over subsequent years, and the condition frequently recurs after later pregnancies.","pitfalls":["Attributing symptoms to normal postpartum tiredness or depression","Treating the thyrotoxic phase with antithyroid drugs","Confusing it with Graves disease and giving radioactive iodine","Not arranging follow-up for permanent hypothyroidism"],"management":["Control symptomatic thyrotoxic-phase adrenergic symptoms with a beta-blocker, since the destructive process does not respond to antithyroid drugs","Treat the hypothyroid phase with levothyroxine replacement when the patient is symptomatic or is planning or continuing pregnancy","Avoid antithyroid drugs and radioactive iodine, distinguishing this destructive thyroiditis from Graves disease by the low radioactive iodine uptake","Monitor thyroid function serially through the triphasic course to guide, and later withdraw, treatment","Undertake long-term follow-up to detect permanent hypothyroidism, which develops in a significant proportion","Screen thyroid function in subsequent pregnancies given the recurrence risk, and check thyroid peroxidase antibodies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Endocrinology reference texts (standard reference)","Thyroid in pregnancy guidelines (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"postprandial_hypotension":{"name":"Postprandial hypotension","system":"Nervous system / Autonomic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["post-meal hypotension","postprandial hypotensive syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fall in systolic blood pressure, conventionally at least 20 mmHg, within about two hours of eating, or a fall below a symptomatic threshold","Symptoms include light-headedness, weakness, visual disturbance, falls, or syncope after meals, and angina in susceptible patients","Particularly common in older people and in those with autonomic failure, Parkinson disease, or diabetes","Large, carbohydrate-rich meals and alcohol are the strongest provokers","An important but often overlooked cause of postprandial falls and syncope in the elderly","Frequently coexists with orthostatic hypotension, which compounds the risk","Diagnosis rests on measuring blood pressure before and serially after a standardised meal","Management is non-pharmacological first: smaller, more frequent, lower-carbohydrate meals, adequate water intake before meals, reduced alcohol, and avoiding standing soon after eating"],"pathophysiology":"After a meal, blood pools in the splanchnic circulation as digestion increases mesenteric blood flow, and vasoactive gastrointestinal peptides promote splanchnic vasodilatation. In a healthy person the baroreflex compensates through increased sympathetic outflow, vasoconstriction elsewhere, and a modest rise in heart rate, keeping blood pressure stable. In postprandial hypotension this compensation is inadequate, typically because of impaired baroreflex or sympathetic function as in autonomic failure, ageing, diabetes, or Parkinson disease, so that splanchnic pooling causes a sustained fall in blood pressure. Rapid gastric emptying and carbohydrate-rich meals, which accelerate the insulin and gut-peptide response, exaggerate the effect.","additionalDifferentials":["Orthostatic hypotension","Reflex (vasovagal) syncope","Cardiac arrhythmia or structural heart disease","Medication-induced hypotension","Reactive (post-meal) hypoglycaemia","Dumping syndrome after gastric surgery"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial blood-pressure measurement before and after a standardised meal","Lying-to-standing blood pressure to detect coexisting orthostatic hypotension","Medication review, particularly of antihypertensives and diuretics taken with meals","Autonomic function testing where autonomic failure is suspected","ECG and ambulatory monitoring to exclude arrhythmia","Blood glucose assessment around symptomatic episodes"],"redFlags":["Recurrent syncope causing injury","Postprandial angina or evidence of cardiac ischaemia","Marked coexisting orthostatic hypotension suggesting autonomic failure","Neurological features suggesting an underlying synucleinopathy"],"prognosis":"Postprandial hypotension is usually manageable with dietary and behavioural measures, but in frail older people and those with autonomic failure it is an important, sometimes recurrent cause of falls, syncope, and reduced quality of life.","pitfalls":["Overlooking meals as the trigger for falls or syncope in older patients","Timing antihypertensive medication so that its peak effect coincides with the postprandial fall","Failing to look for coexisting orthostatic hypotension","Attributing symptoms solely to hypoglycaemia without measuring blood pressure"],"management":["Advise smaller, more frequent meals lower in rapidly absorbed carbohydrate and reduced alcohol intake to lessen splanchnic blood pooling","Recommend behavioural measures such as drinking water before meals, remaining seated after eating and avoiding exertion in the postprandial period","Review and reschedule antihypertensives and diuretics away from mealtimes, or reduce their dose","Consider acarbose to slow carbohydrate absorption, caffeine as an adjunct, and octreotide in refractory autonomic failure","Identify and manage coexisting orthostatic hypotension, which compounds the risk of falls and syncope","Optimise treatment of the underlying condition such as autonomic failure, Parkinson disease or diabetes with the relevant specialist"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"poststeroid_panniculitis":{"name":"poststeroid panniculitis","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["post-steroid panniculitis","corticosteroid-withdrawal panniculitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Weedon's Skin Pathology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare lobular panniculitis that develops after rapid reduction or withdrawal of high-dose systemic corticosteroids, most often described in children.","Firm, tender subcutaneous nodules and plaques appear typically within days to a few weeks of dose reduction.","Lesions favour sites where corticosteroid-induced fat had accumulated, notably the cheeks, and may also affect the trunk and proximal limbs.","The classic history is a child treated with high-dose corticosteroids (for example for rheumatic or neoplastic disease) whose therapy is tapered too quickly.","Histology closely resembles subcutaneous fat necrosis of the newborn, with needle-shaped crystal clefts within adipocytes and a granulomatous, lipophagic infiltrate.","The eruption is generally self-limiting and resolves over weeks to months without specific treatment.","Reinstituting corticosteroids and then tapering more gradually may hasten resolution in symptomatic cases.","Recognition avoids unnecessary investigation for infection or relapse of the underlying disease."],"pathophysiology":"During high-dose corticosteroid therapy, lipid composition of subcutaneous fat shifts towards a higher proportion of saturated fatty acids with a raised melting point, and corticosteroids promote redistribution and accumulation of fat at characteristic sites. When corticosteroids are abruptly withdrawn, the altered, saturated-fat-rich adipose tissue is prone to crystallisation and necrosis. Liberated fatty acids provoke a lobular, lipophagic granulomatous inflammatory reaction, and histology reveals radial needle-shaped crystal clefts within adipocytes and giant cells, closely mirroring subcutaneous fat necrosis of the newborn. Gradual tapering allows the fat composition to normalise and prevents the crystallisation that underlies the panniculitis.","additionalDifferentials":["erythema nodosum","subcutaneous fat necrosis of the newborn (in infants)","infective panniculitis","relapse of the underlying inflammatory or neoplastic disease with cutaneous involvement","lupus panniculitis","traumatic panniculitis","cold panniculitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["deep skin biopsy for histopathology (needle-shaped crystal clefts, lobular granulomatous panniculitis)","review of the corticosteroid tapering schedule and timing relative to lesion onset","serum calcium to exclude associated hypercalcaemia","inflammatory markers and cultures to exclude infection","assessment for relapse of the primary disease that prompted corticosteroid use","full blood count and metabolic panel"],"redFlags":["features suggesting infection (fever, fluctuance, systemic upset) rather than sterile panniculitis","hypercalcaemia analogous to that seen in neonatal fat necrosis","signs of relapse of the underlying disease requiring corticosteroid reinstatement","extensive or ulcerating lesions"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limiting, resolving spontaneously over weeks to months, particularly when corticosteroids are tapered more gradually.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the nodules for infection or for relapse of the treated disease and escalating therapy inappropriately.","Failing to link lesion onset to a recent corticosteroid taper.","Overlooking possible hypercalcaemia given the histological similarity to neonatal fat necrosis.","Sampling too superficially to capture the diagnostic lobular fat changes.","Reintroducing corticosteroids without then planning a slower taper."],"management":["Resume and then more gradually taper the systemic corticosteroid, allowing the subcutaneous fat composition to normalise and the panniculitis to resolve","Manage conservatively with reassurance and simple analgesia, as the eruption is self-limiting over weeks to months","Confirm the diagnosis with deep skin biopsy showing needle-shaped crystal clefts within adipocytes and a lobular lipophagic granulomatous infiltrate","Exclude infection with appropriate cultures and check serum calcium for associated hypercalcaemia","Monitor for relapse of the primary disease that necessitated corticosteroids as the dose is reduced"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Weedon's Skin Pathology, current edition","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"poststreptococcal_glomerulonephritis":{"name":"Post-streptococcal Glomerulonephritis","system":"Renal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PSGN","acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","APSGN","acute post-infectious glomerulonephritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A classic cause of acute nephritic syndrome in children, most often between about 5 and 12 years of age.","Follows infection with specific nephritogenic strains of group A streptococcus, typically 1-2 weeks after pharyngitis or 3-6 weeks after skin infection.","Presents with the nephritic picture of haematuria (often cola- or tea-coloured urine), oedema, hypertension, and variable reduction in urine output.","Complement C3 is characteristically low and typically returns to normal within about 6-8 weeks; persistently low C3 beyond this window suggests another diagnosis.","Evidence of preceding streptococcal infection is supported by elevated or rising antistreptococcal antibodies such as ASO or anti-DNase B.","Management is largely supportive, centred on salt and fluid restriction and control of hypertension and fluid overload.","Antibiotics treat any active streptococcal infection but do not alter the course of the established nephritis.","Renal biopsy is usually unnecessary and reserved for atypical features such as persistent low complement, progressive kidney failure, or nephrotic-range proteinuria.","The prognosis in children is excellent, with the large majority recovering complete renal function."],"pathophysiology":"Infection with nephritogenic strains of group A beta-haemolytic streptococcus triggers an immune-complex-mediated glomerular injury. Streptococcal antigens (such as nephritis-associated plasmin receptor and streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin B) deposit in the glomeruli and, together with circulating and in situ immune complexes, activate the complement system, predominantly via the alternative pathway. The resulting inflammation causes endocapillary proliferation and neutrophil infiltration, reducing glomerular filtration and producing sodium and water retention, which manifests as haematuria, oedema, and hypertension.","additionalDifferentials":["IgA nephropathy","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Lupus nephritis","Henoch-Schonlein purpura (IgA vasculitis) nephritis","Haemolytic uraemic syndrome","Alport syndrome or thin basement membrane disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum complement C3 (and C4) with follow-up to document normalisation","Antistreptococcal serology (ASO, anti-DNase B) or streptozyme panel","Throat or skin swab and culture if active infection present","Urinalysis with microscopy for red cell casts and dysmorphic red cells","Serum urea, creatinine, and electrolytes to assess renal function","Renal biopsy only in atypical or progressive cases"],"redFlags":["Severe or refractory hypertension, including hypertensive encephalopathy","Rapidly rising creatinine suggesting rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","Pulmonary oedema or heart failure from fluid overload","Marked oliguria or anuria","Persistently low complement or nephrotic-range proteinuria indicating an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Most children recover fully; acute complications resolve over days to weeks, complement normalises within about two months, and microscopic haematuria may persist for up to a year with an excellent long-term outlook.","pitfalls":["Attributing every child with haematuria and low C3 to PSGN without follow-up complement testing","Overlooking hypertensive complications by not monitoring blood pressure closely","Failing to consider alternative glomerulonephritides when complement stays low beyond eight weeks","Aggressive fluid administration worsening volume overload in an oliguric child"],"management":["Management is largely supportive; eradicate any residual streptococcal infection with a penicillin (or a macrolide if penicillin-allergic), recognising this does not reverse the established glomerulonephritis","Dietary salt and fluid restriction to limit oedema and circulatory congestion","Loop diuretics to relieve fluid overload, oedema and volume-dependent hypertension","Control hypertension with antihypertensives such as calcium-channel blockers or vasodilators","Manage acute deterioration including hypertensive encephalopathy and pulmonary oedema, with short-term dialysis for severe acute kidney injury, refractory hyperkalaemia or intractable fluid overload","Monitor renal function and serial urinalysis, and confirm that complement C3 normalises as expected, since persistently low C3 prompts re-evaluation for another diagnosis","Refer to paediatric nephrology for renal biopsy and specialist input in atypical, progressive or non-resolving disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"postural_orthostatic_tachycardia_syndrome":{"name":"Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS)","system":"Nervous system / Autonomic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["POTS","postural tachycardia syndrome","chronic orthostatic intolerance"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by a sustained rise in heart rate of at least 30 beats per minute (40 in adolescents) within ten minutes of standing or head-up tilt, without a significant fall in blood pressure","Symptoms of orthostatic intolerance include palpitations, light-headedness, tremulousness, blurred vision, fatigue, and cognitive 'brain fog', all relieved by lying down","Predominantly affects young women and frequently follows a viral illness, pregnancy, surgery, or trauma","Commonly overlaps with chronic fatigue, fibromyalgia, migraine, joint hypermobility (Ehlers-Danlos type), and gastrointestinal dysmotility","Recognised subtypes include hypovolaemic, neuropathic (partial dysautonomic), and hyperadrenergic forms that may coexist","An active-stand (lying-to-standing) test and head-up tilt confirm the heart-rate criterion; a symptomatic orthostatic tachycardia sustained over the recording period is required","Deconditioning perpetuates the syndrome, so prolonged bed rest should be avoided","Management is primarily non-pharmacological: volume and salt expansion, compression garments, physical counter-manoeuvres, graded exercise reconditioning, and avoidance of triggers"],"pathophysiology":"Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome is a heterogeneous disorder of orthostatic intolerance in which assuming an upright posture provokes an exaggerated compensatory rise in heart rate without orthostatic hypotension. Several mechanisms may act, often in combination: reduced circulating blood volume, excessive venous pooling in the lower limbs and splanchnic bed, a partial peripheral autonomic (small-fibre) neuropathy that limits lower-body vasoconstriction, and a hyperadrenergic state with heightened central sympathetic outflow. Inadequate venous return lowers stroke volume, so heart rate rises steeply to preserve cerebral perfusion, generating the characteristic symptoms. Physical deconditioning and abnormal fluid regulation frequently perpetuate the cycle.","additionalDifferentials":["Orthostatic hypotension due to autonomic failure","Inappropriate sinus tachycardia","Anxiety or panic disorder","Thyrotoxicosis","Phaeochromocytoma","Dehydration or anaemia","Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Active-stand (lying-to-standing) test with serial heart rate and blood pressure over ten minutes","Head-up tilt-table testing where the active-stand test is equivocal","Full blood count and thyroid function to exclude anaemia and thyrotoxicosis","Supine and standing plasma catecholamines when a hyperadrenergic subtype is suspected","Ambulatory ECG monitoring to exclude a primary arrhythmia","Autonomic function testing including QSART where available"],"redFlags":["Syncope on exertion or causing injury, suggesting a cardiac cause","A significant orthostatic fall in blood pressure indicating autonomic failure rather than POTS","Features of phaeochromocytoma such as paroxysmal hypertension, headache, and sweating","Progressive neurological signs suggesting a wider autonomic neuropathy"],"prognosis":"Many patients, particularly those with a post-viral onset, improve substantially over months to years with reconditioning and non-pharmacological measures, although a proportion have persistent, disabling symptoms that require long-term multidisciplinary support.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the orthostatic tachycardia as anxiety and overlooking the reproducible heart-rate criterion","Diagnosing POTS when an orthostatic fall in blood pressure actually indicates orthostatic hypotension","Failing to exclude dehydration, anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, or medication effects before making the diagnosis","Recommending prolonged rest, which worsens deconditioning and perpetuates symptoms"],"management":["First-line non-pharmacological volume support with increased salt and fluid intake plus lower-limb and abdominal compression garments to counter venous pooling","Structured graded reconditioning programme beginning with recumbent or semi-recumbent exercise to reverse deconditioning","Heart-rate control with a beta-blocker or ivabradine for symptomatic orthostatic tachycardia","Fludrocortisone for volume expansion and midodrine for vasoconstriction in hypovolaemic and neuropathic subtypes, with pyridostigmine where autonomic neuropathy predominates","Withdraw aggravating agents and treat coexisting conditions such as anaemia, migraine and gastrointestinal dysmotility","Educate on trigger avoidance (prolonged standing, heat, large carbohydrate meals) and physical counter-manoeuvres","Multidisciplinary support involving autonomic specialists, cardiology and physiotherapy for persistent, disabling symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"potocki_lupski_syndrome":{"name":"Potocki-Lupski syndrome (17p11.2 duplication)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["17p11.2 microduplication syndrome","dup(17)(p11.2p11.2) syndrome","PTLS"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Arises from a microduplication of chromosome 17p11.2, the reciprocal of the deletion that causes Smith-Magenis syndrome","RAI1 is the dosage-sensitive gene at the locus, and its increased copy number drives much of the phenotype","Infancy is characterised by hypotonia, poor feeding, and failure to thrive, in contrast to the obesity tendency of Smith-Magenis syndrome","Autism spectrum disorder, intellectual disability, and language delay are common neurodevelopmental features","Cardiovascular anomalies, including structural heart defects, may be present and warrant screening","Sleep disturbance and behavioural difficulties are frequent, though the sleep pattern differs from the inverted circadian rhythm of Smith-Magenis syndrome","The behavioural and growth profile is broadly a mirror image of the deletion disorder at the same locus","Diagnosis is by chromosomal microarray demonstrating the 17p11.2 duplication"],"pathophysiology":"The syndrome is caused by a recurrent duplication of a segment of chromosome 17p11.2 generated by non-allelic homologous recombination between flanking low-copy repeats, the same architecture that produces the reciprocal Smith-Magenis deletion. The critical dosage-sensitive gene within the interval is RAI1, and having three functional copies rather than two disturbs the transcriptional programmes it regulates, affecting neurodevelopment, growth, feeding, and circadian behaviour. Because the phenotype reflects gene over-dosage rather than haploinsufficiency, many features present as the clinical opposite of Smith-Magenis syndrome, such as the poor early growth seen in place of obesity. Additional genes within the variably sized duplication may modify the presentation.","additionalDifferentials":["Smith-Magenis syndrome, the reciprocal deletion disorder at the same locus","Autism spectrum disorder of other genetic or idiopathic cause","Other copy-number neurodevelopmental syndromes detected on microarray","Syndromic causes of neonatal hypotonia and failure to thrive","Fragile X syndrome in males with intellectual disability and autism","Congenital heart disease with associated developmental delay"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal microarray to confirm the 17p11.2 duplication and define its extent","Echocardiography to detect structural cardiac anomalies","Developmental and autism-specific assessment","Feeding and growth evaluation in infancy","Sleep assessment where sleep disturbance is prominent","Parental testing to establish whether the duplication is de novo or inherited"],"redFlags":["Failure to thrive with inadequate weight gain in infancy requiring nutritional support","Haemodynamically significant congenital heart disease","Apnoea or significant sleep-disordered breathing","Severe behavioural dysregulation affecting safety"],"prognosis":"Most individuals have a normal lifespan, with the long-term picture dominated by intellectual disability, autism spectrum features, and behavioural and sleep difficulties; early feeding and growth problems often improve with support, and the overall outlook depends on the severity of neurodevelopmental involvement and any associated cardiac disease.","pitfalls":["Expecting the obesity and self-injurious behaviours of Smith-Magenis syndrome and thereby overlooking the reciprocal duplication","Relying on karyotype, which lacks the resolution to detect this microduplication","Attributing feeding failure solely to non-genetic causes and delaying microarray testing","Neglecting cardiac screening in an infant with the duplication"],"management":["No disease-specific therapy exists; care centres on early developmental intervention with speech and language therapy, occupational therapy and physiotherapy for hypotonia and delay","Feeding and nutritional support in infancy, including dietetic input and supplemental (nasogastric) feeding, to address poor feeding and failure to thrive","Echocardiographic screening and cardiology management of any structural cardiac anomaly","Behavioural and individualised educational support for autism spectrum disorder and intellectual disability","Assessment and management of sleep disturbance, with a sleep study where indicated","Multidisciplinary follow-up across clinical genetics, paediatrics, cardiology and developmental services","Genetic counselling with parental chromosomal microarray to determine whether the duplication is de novo or inherited and to advise recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Potocki-Lupski syndrome","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"potocki_shaffer_syndrome":{"name":"Potocki-Shaffer syndrome (11p11.2 deletion)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["11p11.2 contiguous gene deletion syndrome","proximal 11p deletion syndrome","DEFECT11 syndrome","PSS"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A contiguous gene deletion of chromosome 11p11.2 producing a combination of skeletal, cranial, and neurodevelopmental features","Multiple cartilaginous exostoses arise from deletion of EXT2, the same gene implicated in hereditary multiple exostoses","Enlarged parietal foramina, defects of membranous skull ossification, result from loss of ALX4","Intellectual disability and developmental delay are usual and reflect deletion of additional genes in the interval","Craniofacial features may include brachycephaly, a broad forehead, and midface differences","The size of the deletion correlates broadly with the range of features, as a larger interval removes more contiguous genes","Exostoses carry a small lifelong risk of malignant transformation and merit orthopaedic surveillance","Diagnosis is by chromosomal microarray defining the extent of the 11p11.2 deletion"],"pathophysiology":"Potocki-Shaffer syndrome is a contiguous gene deletion disorder in which loss of a segment of chromosome 11p11.2 removes several dosage-sensitive genes together. Haploinsufficiency of EXT2 impairs heparan sulfate biosynthesis in the growth plate and underlies the multiple exostoses, while loss of ALX4 disrupts the ossification of the cranial vault and produces enlarged parietal foramina. Deletion of further genes within the interval contributes to intellectual disability and the craniofacial phenotype. Because the manifestations arise from the combined effect of adjacent genes, the breadth and severity of the picture depend on which genes the individual deletion encompasses.","additionalDifferentials":["Hereditary multiple exostoses from an isolated EXT2 or EXT1 variant","Isolated enlarged parietal foramina from an ALX4 or MSX2 variant","Other contiguous gene deletion syndromes detected on microarray","WAGR and other 11p deletion disorders","Craniosynostosis and cranial ossification syndromes","Non-specific intellectual disability of alternative genetic cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal microarray to confirm the 11p11.2 deletion and delineate its boundaries","Skeletal survey to document exostoses and their distribution","Skull imaging to characterise parietal foramina and ossification defects","Developmental assessment and neuropsychological evaluation","Orthopaedic review with ongoing surveillance of exostoses","Parental testing and genetic counselling regarding recurrence risk"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, pain, or change in an exostosis suggesting malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma","Neurological compromise from an exostosis impinging on nerves or the spinal cord","Large parietal skull defect leaving the underlying brain vulnerable to injury","Significant developmental regression warranting reassessment"],"prognosis":"Lifespan is generally normal, and the outlook depends on the degree of intellectual disability and the burden of skeletal disease; exostoses may require surgical treatment and carry a small but real risk of malignant change, so lifelong orthopaedic surveillance is advised, while cranial ossification defects are usually of cosmetic and protective rather than functional concern.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing isolated hereditary multiple exostoses and missing the wider contiguous deletion syndrome","Failing to arrange orthopaedic surveillance for malignant transformation of exostoses","Overlooking the protective implications of large parietal skull defects","Using low-resolution cytogenetics that cannot reliably size the deletion"],"management":["No disease-specific pharmacotherapy exists; care is directed at the skeletal, cranial and neurodevelopmental manifestations","Orthopaedic surveillance of multiple exostoses with surgical excision of lesions causing pain, deformity, nerve or vessel compression, or restricted movement","Long-term monitoring of exostoses for malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma","Protection and, where required, surgical or supportive management of enlarged parietal foramina and cranial ossification defects","Early developmental intervention and individualised educational support for intellectual disability and developmental delay","Multidisciplinary follow-up spanning clinical genetics, orthopaedics, neurology and developmental paediatrics","Genetic counselling with parental testing to establish recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Potocki-Shaffer syndrome","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"potts_spine":{"name":"Pott's spine","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pott's disease","spinal tuberculosis","tuberculous spondylitis","TB spine"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India NTEP","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Tuberculosis of the spine is the commonest form of skeletal tuberculosis and a major cause of spinal disease in India, typically affecting the lower thoracic and thoracolumbar vertebrae","It usually begins in the anterior vertebral body adjacent to the disc, spreading beneath the anterior longitudinal ligament to involve adjacent vertebrae with relative disc preservation early on","Progressive vertebral destruction and anterior collapse produce a sharp angular kyphosis known as a gibbus deformity","Presentation is insidious with chronic back pain, local tenderness and, in advanced disease, cold paravertebral or psoas abscesses that can track distally","Neurological deficit (Pott's paraplegia) results from cord compression by abscess, granulation tissue, sequestered bone or deformity and is the most feared complication","MRI is the investigation of choice to define the extent, abscesses and cord compression, and tissue sampling confirms the diagnosis and drug susceptibility","Most cases respond to prolonged multidrug anti-tuberculosis therapy, with surgery reserved for significant cord compression, instability, severe deformity or failure of medical treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis reaches the vertebral bodies by haematogenous spread, often via the paravertebral venous plexus, lodging in the well-vascularised anterior vertebral body near the endplate. Granulomatous infection with caseous necrosis destroys bone and later the intervertebral disc, and spreads beneath the anterior longitudinal ligament to contiguous vertebrae. Vertebral collapse causes angular kyphosis, while caseous material and pus form cold abscesses that dissect along tissue planes. Neurological compromise arises from mechanical compression of the cord by abscess, inflammatory tissue, bony sequestra or deformity, and occasionally from vascular compromise of the cord.","additionalDifferentials":["Pyogenic vertebral osteomyelitis and discitis","Brucellar spondylitis","Metastatic or primary vertebral malignancy","Fungal spondylitis","Osteoporotic vertebral collapse"],"infectionMimics":["Pyogenic (bacterial) spondylodiscitis","Brucellar spondylitis","Fungal spinal infection","Actinomycosis of the spine"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the spine to assess bone destruction, abscesses and cord compression","Image-guided biopsy or aspiration for histopathology, molecular testing and culture","Cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification for rapid diagnosis and rifampicin resistance","Plain radiography and CT for bony detail and deformity assessment","HIV testing and screening for concurrent pulmonary tuberculosis"],"redFlags":["New or progressive neurological deficit indicating spinal cord compression and requiring urgent evaluation for decompression","Bladder or bowel dysfunction suggesting significant cord or cauda involvement","Spinal instability or severe progressive kyphotic deformity","Large cold abscess with systemic sepsis or rifampicin resistance"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and prolonged multidrug therapy most patients recover well, and even established neurological deficits often improve substantially with treatment and timely surgery; delayed presentation with severe deformity or long-standing cord compression carries a higher risk of permanent disability.","pitfalls":["Attributing chronic back pain to mechanical causes and delaying imaging, allowing deformity and deficit to progress","Missing evolving cord compression by not reassessing neurology during treatment","Forgoing tissue diagnosis and drug-susceptibility testing before starting therapy","Confusing it with pyogenic or malignant vertebral disease without confirmatory sampling"],"management":["Prolonged multidrug antituberculous chemotherapy with an intensive phase of rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide and ethambutol followed by an extended continuation phase","Tailor the regimen to drug-susceptibility results, using second-line agents for rifampicin-resistant or multidrug-resistant disease","Spinal immobilisation with bracing and adequate analgesia during healing","Surgical decompression, debridement and instrumented stabilisation for neurological deficit (Pott's paraplegia), spinal instability, progressive kyphosis or a large abscess","Drainage of cold paravertebral or psoas abscesses where indicated","Monitor neurological status, treatment adherence and response, and screen for drug-induced hepatotoxicity","Test for and manage HIV co-infection, screen for concurrent pulmonary tuberculosis, and provide nutritional support","Multidisciplinary care involving spinal surgery, infectious diseases and the tuberculosis service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/NTEP guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"pou1f1_deficiency":{"name":"POU1F1 deficiency","system":"Endocrine / Pituitary development","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PIT-1 deficiency","POU1F1-related combined pituitary hormone deficiency","CPHD1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrine genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["POU1F1 deficiency causes combined loss of the three hormones produced by the Pit-1 cell lineage: growth hormone, prolactin and thyroid-stimulating hormone.","The gonadotroph and corticotroph axes are characteristically preserved, so puberty and adrenal function are usually normal, which helps distinguish it from PROP1 deficiency.","Children present with early and often severe growth failure together with central hypothyroidism, and prolactin is typically low or undetectable.","Inheritance can be autosomal recessive or autosomal dominant, the latter often through dominant-negative mutations affecting the DNA-binding or transactivation domains.","Central hypothyroidism may be present from infancy and can be missed by newborn screening programmes that rely on thyroid-stimulating hormone, delaying diagnosis and threatening neurodevelopment.","Pituitary magnetic resonance imaging usually shows a normal or small anterior pituitary with a normal stalk and posterior pituitary, without midline, ocular or skeletal anomalies.","Because adrenocorticotrophic hormone secretion is intact, life-threatening adrenal crisis is not a typical feature, in contrast with the broader combined pituitary hormone deficiency picture.","Prompt thyroxine and growth hormone replacement, with early treatment of hypothyroidism, is critical to protect growth and cognitive development."],"pathophysiology":"POU1F1 encodes the pituitary-specific POU-homeodomain transcription factor Pit-1, which acts downstream of PROP1 to drive the terminal differentiation, expansion and maintenance of somatotrophs, lactotrophs and thyrotrophs and to activate transcription of the growth hormone, prolactin and thyroid-stimulating hormone beta-subunit genes. Loss-of-function mutations, whether recessive with absent protein or dominant-negative in which a mutant molecule interferes with the normal allele, abolish or impair development of these three lineages while sparing gonadotrophs and corticotrophs, which do not depend on Pit-1. The resulting deficiency of growth hormone, prolactin and thyroid-stimulating hormone therefore appears early and tends to be stable rather than progressive, and puberty and the adrenal axis remain intact. Dominant-negative alleles typically disrupt the homeodomain required for DNA binding or the transactivation domain needed to recruit co-activators, so the abnormal protein occupies binding sites without driving transcription. Because Pit-1 is expressed only within the pituitary and acts late in the developmental cascade, the phenotype is confined to these three hormones and there are no extra-pituitary malformations, distinguishing it from deficiencies caused by more upstream regulators.","additionalDifferentials":["PROP1 deficiency, which additionally involves gonadotrophins and evolving adrenocorticotrophic hormone loss","Isolated growth hormone deficiency without thyroid or prolactin involvement","Primary congenital hypothyroidism detected or missed on newborn screening","HESX1, LHX3 or LHX4 defects with additional axes or extra-pituitary features","Acquired central hypothyroidism and growth hormone deficiency from a sellar lesion","Idiopathic combined pituitary hormone deficiency without an identified mutation","Syndromic causes of growth failure with hypothyroidism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Growth hormone axis assessment with insulin-like growth factor 1 and dynamic testing where indicated","Free thyroxine with thyroid-stimulating hormone to identify central hypothyroidism (low or inappropriately normal thyroid-stimulating hormone)","Serum prolactin, which is characteristically low or undetectable and supports the diagnosis","Confirmation of intact adrenal and gonadal axes to differentiate from broader combined deficiency","Pituitary magnetic resonance imaging to assess gland size and exclude structural lesions","POU1F1 sequencing with attention to dominant-negative and recessive alleles and to the mode of inheritance","Bone age radiograph and serial growth monitoring"],"redFlags":["Unrecognised central hypothyroidism in infancy threatening neurodevelopment, especially where screening relies on thyroid-stimulating hormone","Severe early growth failure requiring prompt endocrine evaluation","Attributing normal puberty and cortisol to exclusion of pituitary disease, thereby missing the Pit-1 pattern","Failure to sequence for dominant-negative alleles, with implications for family counselling","Delay in starting thyroxine and growth hormone replacement in a young child"],"prognosis":"Early recognition and replacement of thyroxine and growth hormone allow normal growth and neurodevelopment, and because gonadotroph and corticotroph function are preserved, puberty, fertility and adrenal responses are typically unaffected. Outcome depends chiefly on how promptly central hypothyroidism is treated in infancy.","pitfalls":["Relying on thyroid-stimulating-hormone-based newborn screening, which fails to detect central hypothyroidism","Confusing POU1F1 deficiency with PROP1 deficiency despite the preserved gonadotroph and corticotroph axes","Overlooking a low prolactin, which is a useful pointer to the Pit-1 lineage defect","Assuming autosomal recessive inheritance and missing dominant-negative alleles relevant to counselling","Delaying thyroxine and growth hormone therapy and compromising cognitive and growth outcomes"],"management":["Levothyroxine replacement for central hypothyroidism, started promptly and titrated to free thyroxine since thyroid-stimulating hormone is unreliable in central disease","Recombinant growth hormone replacement to restore growth velocity and final height","No prolactin replacement is needed and, because gonadotroph and corticotroph axes are spared, sex-steroid and glucocorticoid replacement are not required","Monitor growth, insulin-like growth factor 1, thyroid status and bone age, adjusting replacement through childhood and adolescence","Confirm and periodically reassess intact adrenal and gonadal function to detect any evolving deficiency","Specialist paediatric endocrinology follow-up","Genetic counselling and family screening, accounting for both autosomal recessive and dominant-negative inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of paediatric and adult endocrinology","Clinical guidelines on hypopituitarism and pituitary hormone replacement","Reference text on the genetics of pituitary development"],"crossLinks":[]},"powassan_virus_infection":{"name":"Powassan virus encephalitis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Powassan virus infection","POWV infection","deer tick virus infection","Powassan encephalitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases / CDC guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A North American and Russian Far East flavivirus in the tick-borne encephalitis serocomplex, transmitted by Ixodes ticks, with the deer-tick lineage sharing the vector of Lyme disease","Transmission can occur very rapidly after tick attachment, often within minutes to hours, unlike the longer attachment needed to transmit Lyme borreliosis","Many infections are asymptomatic, but neuroinvasive disease presents with encephalitis, meningitis, or meningoencephalitis, sometimes after a nonspecific febrile prodrome","Magnetic resonance imaging may show involvement of the thalami, basal ganglia, brainstem, and cerebellum, similar to other tick-borne encephalitis serocomplex viruses","Because it shares the Ixodes vector, co-infection with Lyme disease, anaplasmosis, or babesiosis should be considered","There is no specific antiviral or licensed vaccine; management is intensive supportive care, and prevention relies on tick-bite avoidance","Diagnosis is primarily serological (IgM in serum and cerebrospinal fluid), as viraemia is often brief by the time neurological disease appears"],"pathophysiology":"Powassan virus is inoculated during the bite of an infected Ixodes tick, and its presence in tick salivary glands allows unusually rapid transmission. After local replication and viraemia, which frequently produces only mild or unnoticed symptoms, the virus can cross into the central nervous system in susceptible hosts. There it infects neurons and triggers an inflammatory response, with a predilection for deep grey-matter structures, brainstem, and cerebellum, causing encephalitis with the potential for severe neuronal injury and lasting deficits. As with related flaviviruses, the antibody response is usually detectable by the neurological phase, underpinning serological diagnosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex virus encephalitis","West Nile virus neuroinvasive disease","Eastern equine encephalitis","Lyme neuroborreliosis","Bacterial meningoencephalitis","Autoimmune encephalitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and cerebrospinal fluid IgM serology for Powassan virus","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis showing lymphocytic pleocytosis and raised protein","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain for deep grey-matter, brainstem, and cerebellar involvement","RT-PCR on cerebrospinal fluid or blood early in disease, recognising limited sensitivity","Testing for co-transmitted Ixodes-borne infections such as Lyme disease, anaplasmosis, and babesiosis","Testing to exclude herpes simplex encephalitis"],"redFlags":["Declining consciousness or coma","Seizures or status epilepticus","Brainstem or cerebellar signs","Focal neurological deficits with characteristic deep grey-matter lesions on imaging","Respiratory compromise from brainstem involvement"],"prognosis":"Neuroinvasive Powassan virus disease carries appreciable mortality, and a large proportion of survivors are left with long-term neurological sequelae such as cognitive impairment, weakness, or recurrent headaches.","pitfalls":["Assuming prolonged tick attachment is required, since Powassan virus can transmit rapidly","Delaying empirical aciclovir for possible herpes simplex encephalitis pending arboviral results","Failing to test for co-infecting Ixodes-borne pathogens","Relying on molecular testing when serology is usually the diagnostic modality","Overlooking the tick-exposure history in endemic areas"],"management":["No specific antiviral therapy or licensed vaccine exists; management is intensive supportive neurocritical care","Manage raised intracranial pressure, control seizures with antiepileptic drugs, and provide airway protection and ventilatory support in severe encephalitis","Give empirical aciclovir until herpes simplex encephalitis is excluded","Treat co-transmitted Ixodes-borne infections empirically where suspected, such as doxycycline for Lyme disease or anaplasmosis","Neurorehabilitation, physiotherapy and cognitive support for long-term neurological sequelae","Prevention through tick-bite avoidance, protective clothing, repellents and prompt tick removal"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","CDC guidance on Powassan virus disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"prader_willi_syndrome":{"name":"Prader-Willi syndrome","system":"Genetic / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PWS","15q11-q13 paternal deletion syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard medical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic biphasic course: severe hypotonia and poor feeding in infancy followed by insatiable appetite and progressive obesity in early childhood","Neonates often show a weak cry, poor suck requiring assisted feeding, and delayed milestones","Later features include short stature, small hands and feet, hypogonadism, and characteristic facial features such as a narrow bifrontal diameter and almond-shaped eyes","Hyperphagia with food-seeking behaviour can lead to life-threatening obesity if intake is not strictly controlled","Behavioural and cognitive issues include mild to moderate intellectual disability, temper outbursts, skin-picking, and obsessive traits","Caused by loss of paternally expressed genes on chromosome 15q11-q13, most often paternal deletion, less often maternal uniparental disomy","The reciprocal parental defect at the same locus produces Angelman syndrome, illustrating genomic imprinting","Growth hormone therapy improves body composition, growth, and muscle tone under specialist supervision"],"pathophysiology":"Prader-Willi syndrome arises from loss of function of paternally expressed, imprinted genes in the 15q11-q13 region. Because maternal copies of these genes are normally silenced by imprinting, absence of the paternal contribution leaves the region functionally inactive. This most commonly results from a de novo paternal deletion, and less commonly from maternal uniparental disomy (two maternal copies and no paternal copy) or an imprinting defect. Hypothalamic dysfunction is central to the phenotype, driving the abnormal satiety regulation and hyperphagia, growth hormone deficiency, hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, and temperature and sleep disturbances.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of neonatal hypotonia such as spinal muscular atrophy and congenital myopathies","Bardet-Biedl syndrome and other obesity syndromes","Cohen syndrome","Acquired hypothalamic obesity","Congenital hypothyroidism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["DNA methylation analysis of the 15q11-q13 region as the primary diagnostic test","Follow-up testing to define the mechanism (deletion, uniparental disomy, or imprinting defect) for counselling","Growth, endocrine, and pubertal assessment including growth hormone and thyroid evaluation","Sleep study to assess for obstructive and central sleep apnoea","Monitoring for obesity-related complications including glucose intolerance"],"redFlags":["Uncontrolled hyperphagia leading to morbid obesity and its cardiorespiratory and metabolic complications","Increased risk of sudden death related to obesity and sleep-disordered breathing","Gastric distension or rupture from binge eating, which may present without typical pain","Scoliosis and respiratory compromise requiring surveillance"],"prognosis":"With rigorous dietary control, a structured food-secure environment, and growth hormone therapy, individuals can achieve improved health and function; without weight control, morbid obesity substantially shortens life expectancy.","pitfalls":["Attributing neonatal hypotonia to birth injury or a primary neuromuscular disorder and delaying methylation testing","Underestimating the intensity of environmental food control required","Missing hypogonadism and osteoporosis risk in adolescents and adults","Overlooking central adrenal insufficiency and sleep apnoea"],"management":["Recombinant growth hormone therapy to improve linear growth, body composition, muscle tone and development","Strict lifelong caloric restriction within a structured, food-secure environment to prevent life-threatening obesity from hyperphagia","Sex-hormone replacement for hypogonadotropic hypogonadism to support pubertal development and bone health","Assess for obstructive and central sleep apnoea with a sleep study before and during growth hormone therapy, treating as required","Early physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy for infantile hypotonia and developmental delay, with assisted feeding when suck is poor","Behavioural and psychological support for temper outbursts, skin-picking and obsessive traits","Surveillance for obesity-related complications including glucose intolerance and type 2 diabetes","Genetic counselling with mechanism-specific recurrence-risk advice for deletion, maternal uniparental disomy or imprinting defect"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"precocious_puberty":{"name":"Precocious Puberty","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sexual precocity","central precocious puberty","peripheral precocious puberty","early puberty"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Precocious puberty is conventionally defined as the onset of secondary sexual characteristics before about 8 years in girls and 9 years in boys.","It is broadly classified as central (gonadotropin-dependent, from early activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis) or peripheral (gonadotropin-independent, from sex steroids produced outside the normal axis).","Central precocious puberty is far more common in girls and is usually idiopathic, whereas in boys an underlying CNS lesion is more likely and must be excluded.","Accelerated linear growth and advanced bone age are typical; paradoxically, premature epiphyseal fusion can compromise final adult height.","A GnRH (or GnRH-agonist) stimulation test helps distinguish central from peripheral causes by assessing the gonadotropin response.","Isolated premature thelarche or premature adrenarche are usually benign normal variants but should be monitored to ensure they are not the start of true precocity.","Peripheral causes include congenital adrenal hyperplasia, gonadal or adrenal tumours, McCune-Albright syndrome, and exogenous sex steroid exposure.","Beyond physical changes, early puberty can carry psychosocial consequences that warrant support for the child and family."],"pathophysiology":"In central precocious puberty the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis reactivates prematurely, with pulsatile GnRH secretion stimulating pituitary gonadotropins and subsequent gonadal sex steroid production, reproducing normal but early puberty. In peripheral precocious puberty sex steroids arise independently of GnRH from the gonads, adrenals, tumours, or exogenous sources, producing secondary sexual characteristics without central axis activation. Sex steroids accelerate growth and skeletal maturation, so advanced bone age can ultimately limit final height.","additionalDifferentials":["Premature thelarche (benign isolated breast development)","Premature adrenarche (early pubic/axillary hair without other puberty)","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Gonadal or adrenal tumour","McCune-Albright syndrome","CNS lesion (hamartoma, tumour, prior irradiation, or injury)","Exogenous sex steroid exposure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bone age radiograph","Basal and GnRH-stimulated LH and FSH","Sex steroid levels (oestradiol or testosterone)","Adrenal androgens and 17-hydroxyprogesterone when a peripheral cause is suspected","Pelvic ultrasound in girls to assess ovarian and uterine development","MRI of the brain, particularly in boys or where a central lesion is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neurological signs, headaches, or visual disturbance suggesting an intracranial lesion","Rapidly progressive virilisation or a palpable abdominal/gonadal mass suggesting a hormone-secreting tumour","Precocious puberty in a boy, which more often has a serious underlying cause","Very early or rapidly advancing puberty threatening severe height compromise","Cafe-au-lait macules with bony abnormalities suggesting McCune-Albright syndrome"],"prognosis":"Idiopathic central precocious puberty generally has a good outcome, and appropriate treatment can preserve growth potential and address psychosocial concerns; prognosis in secondary forms depends on the underlying cause, such as a CNS lesion or hormone-secreting tumour.","pitfalls":["Assuming precocious puberty is idiopathic in a boy without imaging to exclude a CNS lesion","Confusing benign premature thelarche or adrenarche with true central precocity","Overlooking the impact of advanced bone age on final adult height","Neglecting the psychosocial needs of a child developing ahead of peers"],"management":["Central precocious puberty: GnRH-agonist therapy to suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis, halt pubertal progression and preserve adult height","Peripheral precocious puberty: treat the source, with glucocorticoids for congenital adrenal hyperplasia, aromatase inhibitors or anti-androgens to block sex-steroid effects, and surgical removal of a hormone-secreting tumour","Identify and manage any causative central nervous system lesion, particularly in boys","Remove exogenous sex-steroid exposure where identified","Monitor growth velocity, bone age and pubertal staging to gauge treatment response and predicted height","Psychological support for the child and family regarding early physical and emotional changes","Specialist paediatric endocrinology referral, with neurosurgical or oncology input where relevant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"preeclampsia":{"name":"Pre-eclampsia","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["preeclampsia","pre-eclampsia","PET","pregnancy-induced hypertension with proteinuria","gestational proteinuric hypertension"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by new-onset hypertension after 20 weeks' gestation combined with proteinuria or evidence of maternal organ dysfunction (renal, hepatic, neurological, haematological) or uteroplacental compromise","Proteinuria is no longer mandatory for diagnosis when hypertension is accompanied by features such as thrombocytopenia, raised transaminases, renal impairment, pulmonary oedema, or cerebral/visual symptoms","Warning symptoms include persistent severe headache, visual disturbance (flashing lights, blurring), right upper quadrant or epigastric pain, and rapid oedema of the face and hands","HELLP syndrome (haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets) is a severe variant that can occur with only modest blood pressure elevation","Definitive treatment is delivery of the placenta; timing balances gestational age against maternal and fetal risk","Magnesium sulfate is used for seizure prophylaxis in severe disease and to treat eclamptic seizures, not as an antihypertensive","Antihypertensive therapy aims to lower dangerously high blood pressure to reduce maternal stroke risk while preserving placental perfusion","Low-dose aspirin from early pregnancy reduces incidence in women at high risk (prior pre-eclampsia, chronic hypertension, renal disease, diabetes, multiple pregnancy, antiphospholipid syndrome)"],"pathophysiology":"Pre-eclampsia originates in abnormal placentation: inadequate remodelling of the maternal spiral arteries by trophoblast leads to a poorly perfused, ischaemic placenta. The ischaemic placenta releases anti-angiogenic factors (such as soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1 and soluble endoglin) that antagonise vascular endothelial and placental growth factors, producing widespread maternal endothelial dysfunction. This causes vasospasm, increased vascular permeability, activation of coagulation, and multi-organ hypoperfusion, manifesting as hypertension, proteinuria, hepatic and cerebral involvement, and fetal growth restriction.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic (pre-existing) hypertension","Gestational hypertension without organ involvement","Chronic kidney disease with proteinuria","Acute fatty liver of pregnancy","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura or haemolytic uraemic syndrome","Systemic lupus erythematosus flare"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial blood pressure measurement and urine protein quantification (protein:creatinine ratio or 24-hour collection)","Full blood count for thrombocytopenia and evidence of haemolysis","Liver transaminases and lactate dehydrogenase","Renal function including serum creatinine and urate","Angiogenic markers (sFlt-1/PlGF ratio) where available to aid risk stratification","Fetal assessment with ultrasound growth, amniotic fluid volume, and umbilical artery Doppler"],"redFlags":["Severe hypertension unresponsive to treatment threatening maternal stroke","Neurological signs (severe headache, visual loss, hyperreflexia with clonus) heralding impending eclampsia","Right upper quadrant pain with abnormal liver enzymes suggesting HELLP or hepatic haematoma/rupture","Pulmonary oedema or oliguria indicating organ failure","Fetal growth restriction with abnormal Dopplers or non-reassuring fetal status"],"prognosis":"With timely surveillance, blood pressure control, seizure prophylaxis, and appropriately timed delivery most women recover fully postpartum, though there is an increased lifetime risk of cardiovascular and renal disease. Severe or early-onset disease carries higher maternal and perinatal morbidity, and recurrence in future pregnancies is common.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because proteinuria is absent despite organ dysfunction","Underestimating severity in HELLP syndrome where blood pressure may be only mildly raised","Over-aggressive blood pressure lowering compromising placental perfusion","Failing to continue monitoring postpartum, when eclampsia and severe hypertension can still occur","Treating magnesium sulfate as an antihypertensive rather than as seizure prophylaxis"],"management":["Delivery of the placenta is the only definitive cure, with timing balancing gestational age against maternal and foetal risk","Blood-pressure control with antihypertensives such as labetalol, nifedipine or methyldopa, and intravenous hydralazine or labetalol for acute severe hypertension","Magnesium sulfate for seizure prophylaxis in severe disease and for treating eclamptic seizures","Antenatal corticosteroids to promote foetal lung maturity when preterm delivery is anticipated","Intensive maternal surveillance of blood pressure, platelets and liver and renal function alongside foetal monitoring with growth scans and umbilical artery Doppler","Recognise and manage severe complications including HELLP syndrome, pulmonary oedema and eclampsia","Continued postpartum monitoring, with low-dose aspirin prophylaxis and cardiovascular-risk follow-up for future pregnancies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"preiser_disease":{"name":"Preiser disease","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["avascular necrosis of the scaphoid","idiopathic scaphoid osteonecrosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hand surgery and orthopaedic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Avascular necrosis of the scaphoid in the absence of a preceding acute fracture","It presents with insidious radial-sided wrist pain and tenderness, worse with activity and loading","It is distinguished from scaphoid fracture nonunion, in which osteonecrosis follows a known fracture","Associations include corticosteroid use, chemotherapy and other causes of osteonecrosis, though many cases are idiopathic","MRI is the most sensitive investigation and shows loss of the scaphoid blood supply and marrow changes","Management ranges from immobilisation in early disease to surgical options for advanced collapse and arthritis"],"pathophysiology":"Preiser disease is osteonecrosis of the scaphoid arising without an antecedent fracture, attributed to compromise of its already precarious blood supply, which enters predominantly distally so that the proximal pole is vulnerable to ischaemia. Impaired perfusion causes bone death, and with continued loading the necrotic scaphoid can fragment and collapse, altering carpal mechanics and leading to degenerative wrist arthritis. Systemic risk factors for osteonecrosis contribute in some cases, while others remain idiopathic.","additionalDifferentials":["Scaphoid fracture nonunion with osteonecrosis","Kienbock disease (lunate osteonecrosis)","Scaphotrapeziotrapezoid or radiocarpal osteoarthritis","De Quervain tenosynovitis","Inflammatory arthritis of the wrist"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI to detect osteonecrosis and assess viability","Radiographs for sclerosis, fragmentation and collapse","History for corticosteroid, chemotherapy or other osteonecrosis risk factors","CT to define bony collapse where surgery is considered","Exclusion of an occult prior fracture"],"redFlags":["Scaphoid fragmentation or collapse","Established carpal arthritis","Rapid progression of pain and deformity","Features suggesting an alternative aggressive bone lesion"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary; early disease may stabilise with conservative treatment or revascularisation, but progressive collapse leads to arthritis that may require salvage surgery.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with scaphoid fracture nonunion","Missing the diagnosis on early radiographs before MRI changes","Overlooking systemic osteonecrosis risk factors","Delaying treatment until collapse and arthritis are established"],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy exists; early disease is managed conservatively with wrist immobilisation, activity modification and analgesia including NSAIDs","Joint-preserving surgery such as revascularisation with a vascularised bone graft for viable but ischaemic scaphoids in early disease","Salvage procedures such as proximal row carpectomy, partial wrist fusion or wrist denervation for scaphoid collapse and established arthritis","Address contributing osteonecrosis risk factors such as corticosteroid or chemotherapy exposure","Serial MRI to assess scaphoid vascularity, disease progression and treatment response","Referral to hand surgery for staging and surgical planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Hand surgery reference texts (standard reference)","Orthopaedic reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"premature_adrenarche":{"name":"Premature adrenarche","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["premature pubarche","isolated premature adrenarche","early adrenarche"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Early appearance of adrenal androgen-dependent features such as pubic or axillary hair, adult-type body odour, and mild acne before the usual age, without activation of the gonadal axis","Breast development in girls and testicular enlargement in boys are absent, distinguishing it from true (central) precocious puberty","It reflects premature maturation of the adrenal zona reticularis with a rise in adrenal androgens, particularly dehydroepiandrosterone sulphate (DHEAS)","Bone age and growth velocity are usually normal or only mildly advanced, and adult height is generally unaffected","It is largely benign, but marked or rapidly progressive virilisation should prompt exclusion of non-classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia and androgen-secreting tumours","Premature adrenarche is associated in some children with insulin resistance and a later tendency to polycystic ovary syndrome, warranting attention to metabolic risk","Management is generally reassurance and observation once serious pathology has been excluded"],"pathophysiology":"Adrenarche is the maturation of the adrenal zona reticularis and the associated increase in adrenal androgen secretion (especially DHEAS) that normally precedes gonadal puberty. In premature adrenarche this process begins early, producing androgen-dependent signs such as sexual hair and body odour while the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis remains quiescent, so gonadal maturation does not occur. The adrenal androgens are relatively weak and produced in modest amounts, which is why bone maturation and growth are usually little affected. The trigger for early adrenal maturation is incompletely understood but is linked in some children to insulin resistance and higher body weight, factors that also connect premature adrenarche with later polycystic ovary syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-classic (late-onset) congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Virilising adrenal tumour (adenoma or carcinoma)","Androgen-secreting gonadal tumour","Central precocious puberty with concurrent pubarche","Exogenous androgen exposure","Cushing syndrome with androgen excess"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum DHEAS and other adrenal androgens (androstenedione, testosterone) appropriate to the degree of virilisation","17-hydroxyprogesterone, with an ACTH stimulation test if non-classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia is suspected","Bone-age radiograph to assess skeletal maturation","Basal LH and FSH to confirm the gonadal axis is not activated","Adrenal and pelvic imaging if an androgen-secreting tumour is a concern","Metabolic assessment (glucose and insulin indices) where insulin resistance is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive virilisation, clitoromegaly, or a markedly deepened voice suggesting a tumour or significant enzyme defect","Very high androgen levels out of keeping with benign adrenarche","Substantial bone-age advancement or a growth spurt indicating true precocious puberty or excess androgen","An adrenal or pelvic mass on examination or imaging"],"prognosis":"Isolated premature adrenarche is usually benign with a normal adult height and pubertal course; the principal longer-term consideration is an increased risk of insulin resistance and polycystic ovary syndrome in a subset of girls, which merits metabolic awareness.","pitfalls":["Assuming benign adrenarche without excluding non-classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia in more marked cases","Missing an androgen-secreting adrenal or gonadal tumour presenting with rapid virilisation","Confusing premature adrenarche with central precocious puberty, which involves gonadal activation","Neglecting the associated metabolic and later reproductive (PCOS) risks","Over-investigating mild, slowly progressive, isolated pubic hair development"],"management":["Reassurance and observation, as isolated premature adrenarche is a benign normal variant needing no specific hormonal treatment","Exclude non-classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia and androgen-secreting tumours when virilisation is marked or rapidly progressive","Serial monitoring of growth velocity, bone age and pubertal staging to confirm the benign, non-progressive course","Attention to metabolic risk with weight management and healthy-lifestyle advice, given the association with insulin resistance","Longer-term awareness of, and screening for, polycystic ovary syndrome in susceptible girls","Referral to paediatric endocrinology if features become progressive, virilising or atypical"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"premature_ejaculation":{"name":"Premature ejaculation","system":"Reproductive medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PE","early ejaculation","rapid ejaculation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard sleep & reproductive medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Premature ejaculation is the most common male sexual complaint and is defined by ejaculation occurring sooner than desired, with a perceived inability to delay it and associated distress","It is classified as lifelong (present since first sexual experiences) or acquired (developing later after a period of normal control), which guides evaluation and management","Diagnosis is clinical and rests on the history; the intravaginal ejaculatory latency time and degree of control, distress, and interpersonal difficulty frame the assessment","Acquired cases warrant a search for contributing conditions such as erectile dysfunction, thyroid dysfunction, prostatitis, or psychological stressors","Erectile dysfunction and premature ejaculation frequently coexist, and treating the erectile problem first may resolve the ejaculatory complaint","Behavioural techniques such as the stop-start and squeeze methods can improve ejaculatory control, often combined with partner involvement and counselling","Pharmacological options work by delaying the ejaculatory reflex, and topical desensitising agents or centrally acting serotonergic medication are used according to principle rather than fixed dosing","A collaborative, non-judgemental approach that addresses relationship and psychological factors improves outcomes"],"pathophysiology":"Ejaculation is a spinal reflex coordinated by sympathetic, parasympathetic, and somatic pathways and modulated by supraspinal serotonergic centres. Serotonin (5-HT) generally raises the ejaculatory threshold, and variation in central serotonergic tone and receptor sensitivity is thought to underlie lifelong premature ejaculation, giving it a substantial neurobiological and genetic basis. Acquired premature ejaculation is more often secondary, arising from erectile dysfunction (where a man rushes before losing his erection), prostatic or thyroid disease, or psychological factors such as anxiety and performance pressure that heighten sympathetic arousal and shorten latency.","additionalDifferentials":["Erectile dysfunction with compensatory rapid ejaculation","Chronic prostatitis or pelvic pain contributing to ejaculatory symptoms","Hyperthyroidism","Performance anxiety and situational sexual dysfunction","Relationship or psychosexual conflict","Substance or medication effects"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Focused sexual and psychosocial history distinguishing lifelong from acquired patterns","Assessment of erectile function to identify coexisting erectile dysfunction","Thyroid function testing when an endocrine cause is suspected","Evaluation for prostatitis or lower urinary tract symptoms where indicated","Screening for depression, anxiety, and relationship stressors"],"redFlags":["Sudden acquired premature ejaculation with erectile dysfunction, which may signal underlying vascular or endocrine disease","Associated pelvic or perineal pain suggesting prostatitis","Significant psychological distress, relationship breakdown, or depression","Symptoms of thyrotoxicosis accompanying new-onset ejaculatory change"],"prognosis":"Premature ejaculation responds well to a combination of behavioural therapy, pharmacological delay of the reflex, and treatment of any coexisting condition, though lifelong forms may require ongoing management to maintain control.","pitfalls":["Treating premature ejaculation in isolation while missing coexisting erectile dysfunction","Neglecting the partner and relationship dimension, which strongly influences distress and outcome","Overlooking treatable contributors such as thyroid disease or prostatitis in acquired cases","Framing the problem as purely psychological when a neurobiological basis is likely in lifelong disease"],"management":["Behavioural techniques such as the stop-start and squeeze methods, often with partner involvement and psychosexual counselling","Serotonergic pharmacotherapy to raise the ejaculatory threshold, using an on-demand or daily SSRI such as dapoxetine","Topical anaesthetic agents such as lidocaine-prilocaine applied to reduce glans sensitivity","Treat coexisting erectile dysfunction, for example with a PDE5 inhibitor, which may itself resolve the ejaculatory complaint","Identify and manage contributing conditions such as thyroid dysfunction, prostatitis or lower urinary tract symptoms","Address anxiety, performance pressure and relationship factors with psychological support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"premature_thelarche":{"name":"Premature thelarche","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["isolated premature breast development","benign premature thelarche","thelarche variant"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Isolated breast development in a girl, most commonly in the first two to three years of life, without other signs of puberty such as pubic hair, a growth spurt, or vaginal bleeding","It is typically benign, non-progressive or waxing and waning, and often regresses spontaneously, in contrast to the steady progression of true central precocious puberty","Linear growth velocity and bone age remain appropriate for chronological age, which is a key reassuring feature","There is no significant acceleration of skeletal maturation and adult height is unaffected","It is thought to reflect transient, low-level oestrogen exposure, possibly from small ovarian follicular cysts or fluctuating gonadotropin activity in early childhood","The main task is to differentiate it from progressive precocious puberty, so serial assessment of growth, pubertal staging, and bone age is more informative than a single visit","No specific treatment is required beyond reassurance and periodic review to confirm that the process is not evolving into true puberty"],"pathophysiology":"Premature thelarche is believed to result from transient or mildly increased oestrogen exposure of the breast tissue, without sustained activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis. Proposed mechanisms include small self-limited ovarian follicular cysts, intermittent low-level gonadotropin secretion characteristic of early childhood, or heightened breast tissue sensitivity to circulating oestrogen. Because the axis is not persistently activated, other oestrogen-dependent changes such as accelerated growth, bone-age advancement, and uterine maturation do not develop, and the breast tissue often regresses. The condition is therefore a benign variant rather than true puberty, distinguished from central precocious puberty by its lack of progression and normal skeletal maturation.","additionalDifferentials":["Central (true) precocious puberty","Peripheral precocious puberty from an oestrogen-secreting ovarian tumour or cyst","McCune-Albright syndrome with autonomous ovarian activity","Exogenous oestrogen exposure (medications, topical products)","Lipomastia (fatty breast enlargement, often in overweight children)","Premature thelarche progressing to true precocious puberty over time"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial clinical assessment of pubertal staging and growth velocity over time","Bone-age radiograph, expected to be concordant with chronological age","Basal LH and FSH with oestradiol, usually in the prepubertal range","GnRH (LHRH) stimulation test if progression raises concern for central precocious puberty","Pelvic ultrasound to assess ovarian and uterine size and to detect a cyst or mass","Review of possible exogenous oestrogen sources in the child's environment"],"redFlags":["Progression to additional pubertal features such as pubic hair, accelerated growth, or vaginal bleeding","Advancing bone age or a rising growth velocity indicating true precocious puberty","A palpable abdominal or pelvic mass suggesting an oestrogen-secreting tumour","Rapidly enlarging breast tissue with markedly raised oestradiol"],"prognosis":"Excellent, as the condition is self-limited and does not affect growth, bone maturation, or eventual pubertal timing and fertility; a minority of girls later develop true precocious puberty, which is why continued observation is worthwhile.","pitfalls":["Assuming all early breast development is benign and failing to monitor for progression to true puberty","Conversely, over-investigating or over-treating a self-limited benign process","Confusing fatty tissue (lipomastia) in an overweight girl with glandular breast development","Neglecting to check for exogenous oestrogen exposure","Relying on a single assessment rather than tracking the trajectory over time"],"management":["Reassurance and watchful observation, as premature thelarche is a benign, self-limiting variant needing no active treatment","Serial assessment of pubertal staging, growth velocity and bone age to confirm the non-progressive course and distinguish it from central precocious puberty","Consider GnRH-agonist stimulation testing only if progression or additional pubertal signs raise concern for true precocious puberty","Identify and remove any exogenous oestrogen sources in the child's environment","Referral to paediatric endocrinology if breast development progresses or other pubertal features emerge"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"premature_ventricular_contractions_nonsust":{"name":"Premature Ventricular Contractions, Nonsustained Idioventricular Rhythm","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PVCs","Ventricular premature complexes","Ventricular ectopy","Nonsustained ventricular tachycardia","NSVT","Accelerated idioventricular rhythm","AIVR"],"chapter":260,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1960","clinicalPearls":["Ventricular ectopy spanning PVCs, nonsustained VT (NSVT), and accelerated idioventricular rhythm (AIVR) is very common and is most often asymptomatic, detected incidentally on telemetry or prompted by palpitations.","A PVC is a single ventricular beat occurring earlier than the next expected supraventricular beat; PVCs from one focus share an identical QRS morphology (unifocal), while differing morphologies indicate multifocal origin and raise suspicion for structural or myopathic disease.","Two consecutive ventricular beats form a couplet; three or more consecutive beats above 100/min define VT, and VT terminating spontaneously within 30 seconds is termed nonsustained.","QRS morphology localizes the focus: a dominant S wave in V1 (left bundle branch block-like) points to the right ventricle or septum, whereas a dominant R wave in V1 (right bundle branch block-like) points to the left ventricle; the frontal-plane axis distinguishes superior (inferior-wall) versus cranial origin.","Most ventricular arrhythmias without structural disease have a left bundle branch block-like pattern, while a right bundle branch block configuration or multiple PVC morphologies more often signal structural heart disease.","Idiopathic ectopy commonly arises from the right or left ventricular outflow tracts near the valve annuli, and also from papillary muscles and fascicular tissue; benign idiopathic NSVT is usually monomorphic with rates under 200/min.","Bigeminy (every other beat a PVC) can cause pseudobradycardia because the premature beats may not generate a palpable pulse, so counted rate can read falsely low.","In the peri-infarct period, PVCs and NSVT are common early manifestations of ischemia and can herald ventricular fibrillation.","AIVR is defined as three or more ventricular beats at a rate slower than 100/min, usually due to enhanced automaticity; it is common during acute MI and may emerge during sinus bradycardia or sleep.","Frequent ectopy can itself depress ventricular function (PVC-induced cardiomyopathy), but this rarely occurs unless PVCs account for at least 10-20% of total beats over 24 hours, and only a minority of such patients develop reversible cardiomyopathy.","Smooth, sharp QRS deflections of a PVC suggest a focus in relatively normal myocardium, whereas broad notching and slurring suggest a diseased substrate.","Recently inflammation has been recognized as contributing to PVC genesis in inflammatory and inherited cardiomyopathies."],"pathophysiology":"PVCs arise via enhanced (abnormal) automaticity, triggered activity, or localized reentry. They are frequently sympathetically sensitive and can reflect heightened sympathetic tone, myocardial ischemia, hypoxia, electrolyte disturbances (notably hypokalemia), or underlying structural heart disease. The site of earliest ventricular activation dictates the activation sequence and therefore the QRS morphology, allowing the surface ECG to suggest the anatomic origin and the likelihood of structural disease. In the setting of ischemia or structural disease, PVCs and NSVT can be harbingers of sustained VT or VF. AIVR is typically driven by automaticity, though rare reentrant circuits in diseased myocardium can produce it. PVC-induced ventricular dysfunction is thought to act through a mechanism akin to chronic tachycardia or through ventricular dyssynchrony.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic outflow-tract or fascicular ventricular arrhythmia (benign)","Papillary muscle ectopy associated with mitral valve prolapse and mitral annular disjunction","Sustained monomorphic VT (must be distinguished from NSVT by duration and hemodynamic impact)","Supraventricular tachycardia with aberrant conduction (wide-complex differential)","Genetic arrhythmia syndromes: long QT, Brugada, short QT, catecholaminergic polymorphic VT","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Electrolyte abnormality (especially hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia) provoking ectopy","Stimulant or adrenergic effect (caffeine, alcohol, sympathomimetics)","Myocardial ischemia presenting as new ventricular ectopy","Drug or toxic effect on conduction (e.g., sodium-channel-blocking agents producing slowed, wide QRS rhythms)"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG in sinus rhythm (benign idiopathic arrhythmia usually shows a completely normal ECG; any abnormality warrants further workup)","Ambulatory/telemetry monitoring to quantify PVC burden and capture NSVT","Transthoracic echocardiography to assess ventricular function, wall-motion abnormalities, and valvular disease","Contrast-enhanced cardiac MRI to detect scar and late gadolinium enhancement suggesting underlying cardiomyopathy","Exercise stress testing for effort-related symptoms or coronary risk","Assessment of electrolytes (potassium, magnesium) during acute illness","Family history evaluation and consideration of genetic syndromes when a family history of sudden death exists"],"redFlags":["Syncope or near-syncope, especially without prodrome, during exertion, or with an abnormal ECG or structural heart disease","Very rapid or polymorphic NSVT, or a 'short-coupled' first beat occurring before the peak of the T wave","Family history of sudden death prompting evaluation for inherited cardiomyopathy or channelopathy","Any abnormality on the 12-lead ECG (Q waves of prior infarction, ventricular hypertrophy, repolarization abnormalities of long QT/Brugada/ARVC)","Right bundle branch block-like or multifocal PVC morphology suggesting structural disease","Depressed ventricular function with frequent/repetitive ectopy"],"prognosis":"PVCs, NSVT, and AIVR are most often benign when they occur in structurally normal hearts with normal ECGs, and idiopathic ectopy generally has excellent long-term arrhythmia-free outcomes (catheter ablation suppresses idiopathic PVCs in roughly 90% of patients). In contrast, the same arrhythmias carry prognostic weight in the presence of structural disease: after MI and in heart failure, frequent (typically >10 PVCs/h), repetitive PVCs, couplets, and NSVT are markers of depressed ventricular function and increased mortality, and they are likewise associated with worse outcomes in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and congenital heart disease with ventricular dysfunction. AIVR is usually well tolerated, though loss of AV synchrony may cause hemodynamic compromise in susceptible patients.","pitfalls":["Bigeminal PVCs can produce pseudobradycardia, leading to an erroneously low measured heart rate.","Asymptomatic PVCs and NSVT discovered in a hospitalized or acutely ill patient generally do not require specific antiarrhythmic treatment beyond addressing the underlying illness and correcting aggravating factors.","Assuming all ventricular ectopy is benign; very rapid, polymorphic, short-coupled, or multifocal patterns and any ECG/structural abnormality demand evaluation for sudden-death risk.","Overlooking reversible PVC-induced cardiomyopathy in patients with very frequent ectopy and otherwise unexplained left ventricular dysfunction.","Attributing a wide-complex rhythm to VT without distinguishing it from supraventricular tachycardia with aberrancy."],"management":["Reassurance without antiarrhythmic treatment for asymptomatic ectopy in a structurally normal heart with a normal ECG","Correct reversible provocateurs such as hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, ischaemia, hypoxia and stimulants including caffeine","For troublesome symptoms or a high ectopy burden, first-line beta-blockers or non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers, with class I or class III antiarrhythmics as alternatives","Catheter ablation of the ectopic focus for a high PVC burden, PVC-induced cardiomyopathy or symptoms refractory to drug therapy","Evaluate for and treat underlying structural or ischaemic heart disease, considering an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator where sudden-death risk is high","Monitor PVC burden with ambulatory recording and left ventricular function with serial echocardiography"],"severityClassification":"Risk is stratified less by ectopy count alone than by context: (1) structurally normal heart with normal ECG and monomorphic NSVT under 200/min - benign/low risk; (2) structural heart disease (post-MI, heart failure, HCM, congenital disease) where greater frequency and complexity (couplets, NSVT) mark more severe disease and higher mortality; (3) high-risk features warranting concern - syncope, very rapid/polymorphic/short-coupled NSVT, multifocal or RBBB-like morphology, abnormal ECG, or family history of sudden death. PVC burden of at least 10-20% of total beats over 24 hours is the threshold associated with potential PVC-induced ventricular dysfunction.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1960","Harrison 22e p.1961","Harrison 22e p.1962"],"crossLinks":[]},"premenstrual_dysphoric_disorder":{"name":"Premenstrual dysphoric disorder","system":"Reproductive medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMDD","severe premenstrual syndrome","premenstrual dysphoria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard sleep & reproductive medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Premenstrual dysphoric disorder is a severe form of premenstrual syndrome dominated by mood symptoms that markedly impair function and relationships","The defining feature is the timing: symptoms appear in the luteal phase, remit shortly after menstruation begins, and are absent in the follicular phase","Core symptoms include marked irritability, mood lability, depressed mood, anxiety, and a sense of being overwhelmed, alongside physical complaints such as bloating and breast tenderness","Diagnosis should be confirmed by prospective daily symptom charting across at least two cycles rather than by retrospective recall alone","It must be distinguished from premenstrual exacerbation of an underlying mood or anxiety disorder, which persists throughout the cycle","Serotonergic antidepressants are a first-line treatment and can be given continuously or restricted to the luteal phase according to principle","Suppressing ovulation, for example with combined hormonal contraception, can relieve symptoms by abolishing the cyclical hormonal trigger","Lifestyle measures, cognitive behavioural therapy, and attention to suicide risk during the luteal phase are important adjuncts"],"pathophysiology":"Premenstrual dysphoric disorder is thought to arise from an abnormal central nervous system sensitivity to the normal cyclical fluctuations of ovarian steroids rather than from any abnormal hormone level. Progesterone metabolites such as allopregnanolone, which modulate GABA-A receptor function, and interactions with serotonergic neurotransmission appear central, so susceptible women experience marked mood and behavioural symptoms during the luteal phase when these hormones fluctuate. Because the trigger is ovulatory cycling, symptoms resolve when the cycle ends and can be abolished by suppressing ovulation. The rapid response to serotonergic agents, often faster than in major depression, supports a serotonergic mechanism.","additionalDifferentials":["Premenstrual exacerbation of major depressive or anxiety disorder","Bipolar affective disorder","Thyroid dysfunction","Perimenopausal mood symptoms","Generalised anxiety disorder","Premenstrual syndrome without severe dysphoria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Prospective daily symptom diary over at least two menstrual cycles to confirm cyclical timing","Mental health assessment to exclude an underlying mood or anxiety disorder","Thyroid function testing where clinically indicated","Screening for depression severity and suicide risk","Review of contraceptive and other medication effects on mood"],"redFlags":["Suicidal ideation, which may intensify during the luteal phase","Symptoms persisting throughout the cycle, suggesting a primary psychiatric disorder rather than PMDD","Severe functional impairment or inability to maintain work and relationships","Features suggesting bipolar disorder that would alter treatment choice"],"prognosis":"With accurate diagnosis and treatment, most women achieve substantial symptom control; symptoms recur if treatment stops while ovulatory cycles continue and typically resolve after the menopause or with sustained ovulation suppression.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing on retrospective history without prospective symptom charting","Missing an underlying mood disorder that is merely worsened premenstrually","Underestimating luteal-phase suicide risk","Attributing all cyclical physical symptoms to PMDD without considering other causes"],"management":["Serotonergic antidepressants (SSRIs) as first-line therapy, given either continuously or restricted to the luteal phase","Ovulation suppression with a combined oral contraceptive, such as a drospirenone-containing pill, to abolish the cyclical hormonal trigger","GnRH-agonist therapy with add-back hormone replacement for severe, treatment-refractory disease","Cognitive behavioural therapy and lifestyle measures such as exercise, sleep hygiene and dietary adjustment as adjuncts","Confirm and monitor with prospective daily symptom charting across at least two menstrual cycles","Identify and treat any coexisting mood or anxiety disorder, with suicide-risk assessment","Consider bilateral oophorectomy with hormone replacement as a last resort in severe, refractory cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"prepatellar_bursitis":{"name":"Prepatellar bursitis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["housemaid's knee","carpet layer's knee","kneecap bursitis","prepatellar bursopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Swelling over the front of the kneecap from inflammation of the superficial prepatellar bursa","Classically related to prolonged or repetitive kneeling, hence 'housemaid's knee'","As with the olecranon bursa, the superficial position makes it prone to septic bursitis","Distinguishing septic from aseptic bursitis is the priority; warmth, erythema, fever and disproportionate pain suggest infection","Aspiration for cell count, Gram stain, culture and crystal microscopy guides management when infection is suspected","The knee joint itself is usually uninvolved and joint range is typically preserved","Aseptic cases are managed with avoidance of kneeling, protection and aspiration if symptomatic, with corticosteroid injection reserved for resistant aseptic cases and avoided when infection is possible"],"pathophysiology":"The prepatellar bursa lies superficially between the skin and the patella, allowing the skin to glide over the kneecap. Repetitive kneeling, friction or a direct blow provokes inflammation and effusion. Its superficial location makes it vulnerable to inoculation and infection, particularly through breaks in the overlying skin and typically with skin organisms such as staphylococci. Systemic causes such as gout and rheumatoid disease can also inflame the bursa. Because the bursa does not communicate with the knee joint, intra-articular structures are usually spared.","additionalDifferentials":["Septic prepatellar bursitis","Knee joint effusion or intra-articular pathology","Gout or pseudogout","Infrapatellar bursitis (clergyman's knee)","Cellulitis of the anterior knee","Patellar fracture or contusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bursal aspiration for cell count, Gram stain and culture","Polarised light microscopy for crystals","Inflammatory markers, with blood cultures if systemically unwell","Ultrasound to confirm a superficial bursal collection separate from the joint","Plain radiographs if fracture or a foreign body is suspected"],"redFlags":["Fever, spreading erythema and severe pain suggesting septic bursitis or cellulitis","An overlying wound or immunosuppression","Features of systemic sepsis","A tense joint effusion or inability to bear weight suggesting intra-articular pathology"],"prognosis":"Aseptic prepatellar bursitis usually resolves with protection and activity change, recurring if kneeling continues; septic bursitis typically responds to antibiotics and drainage.","pitfalls":["Injecting a potentially infected bursa with corticosteroid","Confusing a superficial bursal swelling with a true knee joint effusion","Not analysing aspirate when infection is suspected","Overlooking a foreign body or penetrating injury"],"management":["Aseptic bursitis: conservative care with avoidance of kneeling, rest, ice, compression and NSAIDs, aspirating a large effusion for comfort","Bursal aspiration for cell count, Gram stain, culture and crystal microscopy to distinguish septic from aseptic bursitis and direct therapy","Septic bursitis: antistaphylococcal antibiotics with surgical drainage if pus persists","Treat systemic contributors such as gout with crystal-directed therapy or rheumatoid disease","Prevent recurrence with knee protection such as kneeling pads and activity modification","Surgical bursectomy for recurrent or refractory aseptic bursitis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","British Journal of Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"presbycusis":{"name":"Presbycusis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["age-related hearing loss","presbyacusis","senile deafness"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presbycusis is the progressive, symmetrical sensorineural hearing loss that accompanies ageing and is the commonest cause of hearing loss in older adults","It characteristically begins with the high frequencies, so patients first struggle with consonant sounds and with speech in noisy or crowded settings","A common complaint is that people can hear that someone is speaking but cannot make out the words, reflecting impaired speech discrimination","Tinnitus and difficulty localising sound frequently accompany the hearing loss","Audiometry typically shows a bilateral, symmetrical, high-frequency down-sloping sensorineural pattern","Recruitment (abnormal loudness growth) can make amplified sound uncomfortable and complicate hearing aid fitting","Contributing and aggravating factors include noise exposure, ototoxic drugs, vascular disease and diabetes, which should be addressed","Management is largely rehabilitative with hearing aids, assistive listening devices and auditory training, and cochlear implantation is considered for severe cases with poor aided benefit"],"pathophysiology":"Presbycusis results from cumulative degenerative changes in the auditory system with age. Several subtypes are described: sensory presbycusis from loss of hair cells in the basal (high-frequency) cochlea; neural presbycusis from loss of spiral ganglion neurons, which particularly impairs speech discrimination; strial or metabolic presbycusis from atrophy of the stria vascularis affecting the endocochlear potential across frequencies; and cochlear conductive (mechanical) presbycusis from stiffening of the basilar membrane. Central auditory pathway changes further degrade processing of speech, especially in noise. Genetic predisposition, lifelong noise exposure, vascular and metabolic disease and ototoxins accelerate these changes.","additionalDifferentials":["Noise-induced hearing loss","Ototoxic drug-induced hearing loss","Cerumen impaction or middle ear disease causing conductive loss","Acoustic neuroma or other retrocochlear lesion (especially if asymmetrical)","Meniere disease","Metabolic or autoimmune inner ear disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure tone audiometry to confirm the symmetrical high-frequency sensorineural pattern","Speech audiometry to assess discrimination and predict hearing aid benefit","Tympanometry to exclude a middle ear conductive component","Otoscopy to exclude wax impaction and other external or middle ear disease","MRI of the internal auditory meatus if the loss is markedly asymmetrical or there is unilateral tinnitus"],"redFlags":["Marked asymmetry between ears suggesting a retrocochlear or other unilateral lesion","Sudden or rapidly progressive hearing loss requiring urgent evaluation","Unilateral tinnitus, vertigo or facial numbness raising concern for acoustic neuroma","Associated neurological signs that point away from simple age-related loss"],"prognosis":"Presbycusis is irreversible and slowly progressive, but most patients maintain good communication and quality of life with hearing aids and rehabilitation, and cochlear implantation can help those with severe loss and poor aided speech understanding.","pitfalls":["Assuming all hearing loss in the elderly is presbycusis and missing wax, effusion or a tumour","Overlooking asymmetry that warrants imaging","Neglecting the impact of poor speech discrimination and recruitment on hearing aid success","Failing to address modifiable contributors such as ongoing noise exposure and ototoxic medication"],"management":["Hearing aids as the mainstay, amplifying the high frequencies where loss is greatest","Cochlear implantation for severe loss with poor aided speech discrimination","Aural rehabilitation, assistive listening devices and communication strategies to support speech understanding, especially in noise","Tinnitus management with education and sound therapy where troublesome","Address modifiable contributors such as noise protection, avoidance of ototoxins and control of vascular and metabolic disease to slow progression","Periodic audiological review to monitor progression and optimise amplification"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"presbyvestibulopathy":{"name":"Presbyvestibulopathy","system":"Ear / Audiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["age-related vestibular loss","presbyvestibular dysfunction","vestibular ageing","PVP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Describes age-related decline in vestibular function producing chronic unsteadiness and a tendency to falls in older adults","Symptoms are typically mild imbalance and postural instability rather than discrete vertigo attacks","Diagnostic criteria require mild bilateral vestibular reduction on quantitative testing that falls short of the thresholds for bilateral vestibulopathy","It is a diagnosis reached after excluding other treatable vestibular and neurological causes of imbalance in the elderly","The deficit reflects gradual loss of vestibular hair cells and neurones as part of normal ageing","Vestibular rehabilitation and multifactorial falls-prevention measures are the mainstays of management","It frequently coexists with presbyacusis, visual decline, and peripheral neuropathy, compounding balance impairment"],"pathophysiology":"With ageing there is progressive attrition of vestibular hair cells, primary vestibular neurones, and otolithic structures, together with reduced central compensation capacity. This produces a mild, symmetrical reduction in vestibular reflexes that impairs the vestibulo-ocular and vestibulospinal contributions to gaze and postural stability. Because the loss is gradual and moderate, patients experience chronic unsteadiness rather than acute vertigo, and the disability is amplified when other age-related sensory systems, particularly vision and proprioception, also decline, reducing the redundancy the balance system relies on for compensation.","additionalDifferentials":["Bilateral vestibulopathy with more severe reflex loss","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Peripheral neuropathy and proprioceptive loss","Cerebrovascular and cerebellar degenerative disorders","Orthostatic hypotension and cardiovascular causes of unsteadiness","Medication-related dizziness and polypharmacy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Video head impulse testing to quantify mild reduction in vestibulo-ocular reflex gain","Caloric or rotational chair testing to confirm mild bilateral hypofunction","Audiometry to assess coexisting presbyacusis","Assessment of gait, balance, and falls risk","Review of vision, proprioception, and medications contributing to instability","Neuroimaging when central or cerebrovascular disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["Recurrent falls with injury","Focal neurological signs suggesting a central cause","Rapid deterioration inconsistent with gradual ageing","Severe reflex loss meeting criteria for bilateral vestibulopathy instead"],"prognosis":"The vestibular decline itself is not reversible, but rehabilitation, sensory optimisation, and falls-prevention strategies meaningfully reduce instability and injury risk in most older adults.","pitfalls":["Labelling all elderly dizziness as ageing and missing a treatable cause such as positional vertigo","Overlooking contributory medications and cardiovascular factors","Not distinguishing mild age-related loss from true bilateral vestibulopathy","Neglecting the compounding effect of coexisting vision and proprioceptive decline"],"management":["Vestibular rehabilitation exercises as the mainstay to promote central compensation and improve gaze and postural stability","Multifactorial falls-prevention including home-hazard assessment, strength and balance training and appropriate walking aids","Optimise other sensory inputs by correcting visual impairment and ensuring good proprioceptive cues with suitable footwear","Review and rationalise medications that impair balance, such as sedatives and vestibular suppressants","Identify and treat coexisting or alternative vestibular and neurological disorders","Ongoing multidisciplinary follow-up across audiovestibular medicine, physiotherapy and geriatrics to monitor stability and injury risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology","Barany Society Consensus: Presbyvestibulopathy Diagnostic Criteria"],"crossLinks":[]},"pressure_injury":{"name":"Pressure injury","system":"Geriatrics / Palliative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pressure ulcer","pressure sore","bedsore","decubitus ulcer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hazzard's Geriatric Medicine and Gerontology / palliative care guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pressure injuries are localised damage to skin and underlying tissue over a bony prominence, caused by sustained pressure often combined with shear and friction","Common sites include the sacrum, heels, ischial tuberosities, greater trochanters, and occiput, reflecting where soft tissue is compressed against bone","Staging runs from non-blanchable erythema of intact skin, through partial- and full-thickness skin loss, to exposure of muscle or bone, with unstageable and deep-tissue-injury categories where depth is obscured","Prevention is far more effective than treatment and rests on risk assessment, regular repositioning, pressure-redistributing surfaces, skin care, and nutritional support","Validated risk tools such as the Braden or Waterlow scales help identify at-risk patients and target preventive resources","Immobility, malnutrition, incontinence, poor perfusion, sensory impairment, and advanced age are the principal risk factors","Management combines pressure offloading, wound cleansing, appropriate dressings for the wound bed, debridement of devitalised tissue where indicated, and optimisation of nutrition","Near the end of life, skin failure can occur despite excellent care, and goals may appropriately prioritise comfort over aggressive wound healing"],"pathophysiology":"Sustained pressure over a bony prominence exceeds capillary closing pressure, obstructing microcirculation and causing local ischaemia. If pressure is not relieved, ischaemia progresses to tissue hypoxia, inflammation, and cell death, with damage often beginning in deeper tissues adjacent to bone before it becomes visible at the surface. Shear forces distort and occlude vessels, and friction and moisture from incontinence weaken the epidermal barrier. Impaired perfusion, undernutrition, reduced sensation, and immobility all lower tissue tolerance and accelerate breakdown.","additionalDifferentials":["Moisture-associated skin damage or incontinence-associated dermatitis","Arterial or venous leg ulceration","Diabetic foot ulcer","Skin tear or traumatic wound","Cutaneous infection or abscess","Malignant skin lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured pressure-injury risk assessment using a validated scale","Wound assessment documenting stage, size, depth, exudate, and tissue type","Nutritional assessment and screening for undernutrition","Evaluation of perfusion, continence, and mobility as modifiable contributors","Investigations for infection such as wound and systemic assessment when osteomyelitis or spreading infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Spreading erythema, purulent discharge, or systemic features suggesting wound infection or sepsis","Deep wounds exposing bone with suspected underlying osteomyelitis","Rapidly deteriorating or new deep-tissue injury indicating ongoing unrelieved pressure","Signs of necrotising soft-tissue infection requiring urgent surgical assessment"],"prognosis":"Superficial pressure injuries can heal with consistent offloading and good wound and nutritional care, whereas deep, full-thickness injuries heal slowly and may recur. Pressure injuries are markers of illness severity and are associated with pain, infection, prolonged hospitalisation, and increased mortality, particularly in frail and end-of-life patients in whom prevention and comfort are paramount.","pitfalls":["Focusing on dressings while failing to relieve the underlying pressure","Neglecting nutrition, which is essential to wound healing","Missing deep-tissue injury because early damage lies beneath intact-looking skin","Overlooking heels and medical-device-related sites during skin inspection","Pursuing aggressive healing goals at the end of life when comfort should take priority"],"management":["Prevention through structured risk assessment using a validated scale such as Braden or Waterlow, scheduled repositioning, pressure-redistributing mattresses and cushions, and meticulous skin care","Consistent pressure offloading and repositioning as the foundation of treating an established injury","Wound management with cleansing, debridement of necrotic or devitalised tissue and stage-appropriate dressings to maintain a moist healing environment","Nutritional support with adequate protein and energy to promote healing","Moisture and continence management and optimisation of perfusion to reduce ongoing tissue damage","Treat local or systemic infection with antibiotics when cellulitis or osteomyelitis is present","Pain assessment and analgesia, including cover for dressing changes","Multidisciplinary involvement of tissue-viability nursing, dietetics and surgery for reconstruction of deep wounds"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Hazzard's Geriatric Medicine and Gerontology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pressure_ulcer":{"name":"Pressure ulcer","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pressure sore","decubitus ulcer","bedsore","pressure injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pressure ulcers arise over bony prominences (sacrum, ischium, greater trochanter, heel, occiput) where sustained pressure exceeds capillary perfusion pressure","Staging ranges from non-blanchable erythema of intact skin, through partial and full thickness skin loss, to exposure of muscle, tendon, or bone, with unstageable ulcers obscured by slough or eschar","The visible skin defect often underestimates the true extent, as necrosis is frequently more advanced in the deeper tissues nearest the bone","Prevention through repositioning, pressure-redistributing surfaces, skin care, moisture control, and nutrition is more effective than treatment of established ulcers","Management principles include pressure offloading, debridement of nonviable tissue, moist wound care, optimisation of nutrition, and control of spasticity, incontinence, and infection","Deep ulcers with dead space or exposed bone may require surgical debridement and reconstruction with local flaps, and osteomyelitis should be considered when bone is exposed","Flap reconstruction is offered to selected, optimised patients, and recurrence is common if the underlying causes of pressure and shear are not corrected","A multidisciplinary approach addressing mobility, sensation, continence, and social circumstances is essential to prevent recurrence"],"pathophysiology":"Pressure ulcers result from prolonged unrelieved pressure, often combined with shear and friction, that compresses soft tissue between bone and an external surface. When interface pressure exceeds capillary closing pressure, local ischaemia and impaired lymphatic drainage lead to tissue hypoxia, accumulation of metabolic waste, and cell death. Muscle is more susceptible to ischaemia than skin, so damage typically begins in deep tissue near the bony prominence and progresses outward, producing a cone-shaped area of necrosis. Contributing factors include immobility, impaired sensation, malnutrition, moisture, incontinence, vascular disease, and advanced age.","additionalDifferentials":["Moisture-associated skin damage or incontinence dermatitis","Diabetic or neuropathic ulcer","Arterial or venous leg ulcer","Skin malignancy arising in a chronic wound (Marjolin ulcer)","Pyoderma gangrenosum","Deep tissue infection or necrotising fasciitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wound assessment with staging, measurement, and photographic documentation","Nutritional assessment including albumin and prealbumin","Wound or bone cultures and inflammatory markers when infection is suspected","Imaging (MRI or bone biopsy) to evaluate for underlying osteomyelitis","Assessment of vascular supply and tissue perfusion","Screening for and management of contributing factors such as spasticity and incontinence"],"redFlags":["Spreading cellulitis, systemic sepsis, or signs of necrotising infection arising from the ulcer","Exposed bone with suspected osteomyelitis","Rapid deterioration or deep tissue injury under intact skin","Chronic non-healing ulcer with atypical features suggesting malignant transformation"],"prognosis":"Superficial pressure injuries can heal with conservative care once pressure is relieved, but deep ulcers heal slowly and often require surgery. Even after successful flap reconstruction, recurrence rates are high in patients who remain immobile or insensate, so long-term outcome depends heavily on ongoing prevention and management of underlying risk factors.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the depth of tissue damage from the surface appearance alone","Attempting surgical closure without first correcting pressure, nutrition, spasticity, and incontinence","Failing to debride nonviable tissue and eschar before assessing wound bed","Overlooking underlying osteomyelitis in deep ulcers with exposed bone","Neglecting prevention and repositioning, leading to new or recurrent ulcers","Missing malignant change in a long-standing chronic ulcer"],"management":["Relieve interface pressure with a structured repositioning schedule and pressure-redistributing support surfaces such as specialised mattresses and cushions","Debride nonviable tissue (sharp/surgical, enzymatic, or autolytic) to remove slough and eschar and expose a viable wound bed","Provide stage-appropriate moist wound care with dressings matched to exudate, depth, and dead space","Optimise nutrition with adequate protein and energy intake and correction of deficiencies to support healing","Control contributing factors including moisture and incontinence, spasticity, and skin protection","Treat wound infection with culture-directed antibiotics and evaluate for underlying osteomyelitis when bone is exposed","Refer deep ulcers with dead space or exposed bone for surgical debridement and reconstruction with local or myocutaneous flaps","Coordinate a tissue-viability, plastic surgery, and dietetic multidisciplinary team with ongoing prevention to reduce recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"preterm_labour":{"name":"Preterm labour","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["preterm labor","premature labour","PTL","spontaneous preterm birth"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Preterm labour is regular uterine contractions with cervical change occurring before 37 completed weeks of gestation","It is the leading cause of neonatal morbidity and mortality, largely through complications of prematurity such as respiratory distress, intraventricular haemorrhage, and necrotising enterocolitis","The strongest predictors are a previous preterm birth and a short cervix on transvaginal ultrasound","Antenatal corticosteroids given to accelerate fetal lung maturation are among the most effective interventions to improve neonatal outcomes","Magnesium sulfate given before anticipated early preterm birth provides fetal neuroprotection, reducing the risk of cerebral palsy","Tocolytic drugs are used mainly to delay delivery briefly to allow steroids to act and to enable transfer to a unit with neonatal intensive care","Fetal fibronectin testing and cervical length measurement help identify women at genuinely high or low risk of imminent delivery","Group B streptococcus status guides intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis, and progesterone supplementation can reduce risk in selected high-risk women"],"pathophysiology":"Preterm labour is a syndrome with multiple pathways converging on premature activation of the mechanisms of labour: uterine contractility, cervical ripening, and decidual-membrane activation. Recognised triggers include intrauterine infection and inflammation, uteroplacental ischaemia or haemorrhage (decidual bleeding), excessive uterine distension (as in multiple pregnancy or polyhydramnios), cervical insufficiency, and maternal or fetal stress activating the corticotrophin-releasing hormone axis. These pathways generate prostaglandins and inflammatory mediators that stimulate contractions and cervical change ahead of term.","additionalDifferentials":["Braxton Hicks (physiological) contractions","Urinary tract infection or pyelonephritis provoking uterine irritability","Placental abruption","Round ligament or musculoskeletal pain","Preterm prelabour rupture of membranes","Other causes of abdominal pain such as appendicitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound cervical length measurement","Fetal fibronectin testing to help predict imminent delivery","Cardiotocography and tocography to assess contractions and fetal wellbeing","Speculum examination to assess for ruptured membranes and take genital tract swabs","Screening for infection including urinalysis and culture and group B streptococcus status","Ultrasound for fetal presentation, growth, and amniotic fluid volume"],"redFlags":["Signs of chorioamnionitis such as maternal fever, uterine tenderness, and fetal tachycardia","Preterm prelabour rupture of membranes with cord prolapse risk","Vaginal bleeding suggesting abruption or praevia","Non-reassuring fetal status","Advanced cervical dilatation with imminent delivery at a very early gestation"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on gestational age at delivery; each additional week in utero at early gestations substantially improves survival and reduces disability. Antenatal steroids, magnesium neuroprotection, and delivery in a centre with neonatal intensive care improve results. Women with a prior spontaneous preterm birth remain at increased risk in subsequent pregnancies.","pitfalls":["Mistaking true preterm labour for Braxton Hicks contractions and delaying steroids","Overtreating with tocolytics when there is a contraindication such as chorioamnionitis or significant abruption","Failing to give antenatal corticosteroids and magnesium neuroprotection within the appropriate window","Not arranging timely transfer to a unit with adequate neonatal facilities","Overlooking an underlying infection driving the labour"],"management":["Give antenatal corticosteroids to accelerate foetal lung maturation and improve neonatal outcomes","Administer magnesium sulfate for foetal neuroprotection before anticipated early preterm birth","Use tocolysis (calcium-channel blocker or oxytocin-receptor antagonist) to delay delivery briefly to allow steroids to act and enable transfer","Arrange in-utero transfer to a unit with neonatal intensive care","Screen for and treat intrauterine and genital-tract infection and give intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis for group B streptococcus","Offer progesterone and consider cervical cerclage or pessary for those with a short cervix or prior preterm birth","Monitor the foetus with cardiotocography and plan mode and timing of delivery","Coordinate obstetric and neonatal teams and counsel parents on prematurity risks"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pretibial_myxoedema":{"name":"Pretibial myxoedema","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Thyroid dermopathy","Localised myxoedema","Infiltrative dermopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autoimmune deposition dermopathy, most often a manifestation of Graves disease, characterised by firm, non-pitting infiltration of the skin, classically over the pretibial regions and dorsa of the feet.","Almost always occurs with markedly elevated TSH-receptor antibodies and is typically accompanied by Graves ophthalmopathy; it usually appears after the eye disease.","Despite the name, it is a localised mucinosis and is unrelated to the generalised myxoedema of hypothyroidism; most patients are or have been thyrotoxic.","Presents as waxy, skin-coloured to yellow-brown or violaceous plaques and nodules with a peau d'orange texture and prominent hair follicles; a diffuse non-pitting oedematous form also occurs.","Part of the triad with Graves ophthalmopathy and thyroid acropachy (clubbing and periosteal reaction); acropachy signals more severe extrathyroidal disease.","The Koebner phenomenon is relevant, with lesions favouring areas of trauma or dependency; the elephantiasic variant can be disfiguring and disabling.","Diagnosis is usually clinical in a patient with known Graves disease; biopsy shows abundant dermal mucin (glycosaminoglycans) separating collagen bundles.","Management centres on potent topical corticosteroids under occlusion and compression; smoking cessation and control of thyroid disease are important, though the dermopathy runs an independent course."],"pathophysiology":"Pretibial myxoedema is an extrathyroidal manifestation of autoimmune thyroid disease driven by TSH-receptor autoantibodies. Fibroblasts in the dermis, particularly in the pretibial skin, express the TSH receptor, and stimulation by these autoantibodies, together with insulin-like growth factor-1 receptor signalling and cytokines released by infiltrating activated T lymphocytes, drives fibroblast proliferation and greatly increased synthesis of glycosaminoglycans, chiefly hyaluronic acid. These hydrophilic mucins accumulate in the reticular dermis, drawing in water, separating and fragmenting collagen bundles and producing the firm, non-pitting induration and characteristic plaques. Mechanical factors amplify the process: the dependent position of the lower legs, elevated hydrostatic pressure and local trauma (a Koebner effect) favour mucin deposition and lymphatic compromise in the pretibial region, explaining the anatomical predilection and the severe elephantiasic form. The shared autoimmune target accounts for its close association with Graves ophthalmopathy, where analogous fibroblast activation occurs in orbital tissue.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic venous stasis dermatitis and stasis-related lipodermatosclerosis","Chronic lymphoedema and elephantiasis nostras","Hypertrophic lichen planus and lichen simplex chronicus","Necrobiosis lipoidica","Cutaneous mucinoses such as lichen myxoedematosus and scleromyxoedema","Erythema nodosum and other panniculitides","Pretibial cellulitis or chronic bacterial infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thyroid function tests with TSH-receptor antibody measurement","Skin biopsy demonstrating dermal mucin deposition with special stains for glycosaminoglycans","Assessment and imaging for associated Graves ophthalmopathy","Radiography of the hands to detect thyroid acropachy where suspected","Doppler studies to exclude venous insufficiency or lymphatic obstruction as alternative causes of lower-limb swelling","Antithyroid peroxidase and antithyroglobulin antibodies to support autoimmune thyroid disease"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive elephantiasic swelling causing functional impairment or entrapment neuropathy","Ulceration or signs of secondary infection in indurated pretibial skin","Severe coexisting ophthalmopathy threatening vision requiring urgent specialist care","Diagnostic uncertainty where malignancy or an alternative infiltrative process cannot be excluded without biopsy"],"prognosis":"Most cases are mild and either remain stable or improve gradually, sometimes remitting spontaneously over years, and respond partially to topical corticosteroids and compression. The elephantiasic and acropachy-associated forms are more refractory and can be persistently disfiguring and disabling.","pitfalls":["Confusing the name with generalised hypothyroid myxoedema and overlooking the underlying Graves disease","Expecting restoration of a euthyroid state to resolve the skin disease, which follows an independent course","Misdiagnosing indurated plaques as cellulitis and prescribing repeated antibiotics","Failing to counsel on smoking cessation, which worsens associated thyroid eye disease","Neglecting compression and Koebner-avoidance advice that influence lower-limb outcomes"],"management":["Apply potent topical corticosteroids, often under occlusion, as first-line therapy for infiltrated plaques","Use compression therapy and bandaging to counter dependent oedema and mechanical aggravation of the lower legs","Treat the underlying Graves disease and restore euthyroid status with antithyroid drugs, radioiodine, or thyroidectomy","Advise avoidance of local trauma given the Koebner phenomenon that provokes new lesions","For refractory or elephantiasic disease, consider intralesional corticosteroids or systemic immunomodulation such as rituximab or intravenous immunoglobulin","Co-manage associated Graves ophthalmopathy with ophthalmology","Monitor TSH-receptor antibody titres and clinical progression of the dermopathy","Refer to a combined endocrinology and dermatology service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard dermatology textbooks","Endocrinology and thyroid disease clinical guidelines","Peer-reviewed dermatology and endocrinology literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"priapism":{"name":"Priapism","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["persistent erection","ischaemic priapism","low-flow priapism","non-ischaemic priapism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Priapism is a prolonged penile erection (typically beyond four hours) unrelated to sexual stimulation, and the critical first step is to distinguish ischaemic (low-flow) from non-ischaemic (high-flow) types","Ischaemic priapism is a painful, rigid erection representing a compartment syndrome of the corpora cavernosa and is a urological emergency because prolonged ischaemia causes fibrosis and permanent erectile dysfunction","Non-ischaemic (high-flow) priapism is usually painless, semi-rigid, and follows perineal or penile trauma with an arteriolar-sinusoidal fistula; it is not an emergency and often resolves or is managed by embolisation","Cavernosal blood gas analysis distinguishes the two: ischaemic blood is dark and hypoxic/acidotic, whereas high-flow blood is bright red with near-arterial values","Common causes of ischaemic priapism include sickle cell disease, haematological malignancy, and drugs, notably intracavernosal vasoactive agents and certain psychotropic and recreational drugs","Emergency management of ischaemic priapism proceeds stepwise from corporal aspiration and irrigation to intracavernosal sympathomimetic injection, and to surgical shunting if these fail","Underlying conditions such as sickle cell disease require concurrent systemic treatment alongside local measures","Stuttering (recurrent) priapism, common in sickle cell disease, warrants preventive strategies to reduce recurrent ischaemic episodes"],"pathophysiology":"Priapism reflects a disturbance of the normal mechanisms that terminate erection. In ischaemic (low-flow) priapism, veno-occlusion persists so blood becomes trapped in the corpora cavernosa, producing stagnant, hypoxic, acidotic blood and a compartment-syndrome-like state; prolonged ischaemia leads to smooth muscle necrosis and corporal fibrosis. Causes include sickle cell disease and other haemoglobinopathies, leukaemic infiltration, and pharmacological agents. Non-ischaemic (high-flow) priapism results from unregulated arterial inflow, typically through a cavernosal artery fistula created by perineal or penile trauma, so oxygenation is maintained and tissue ischaemia does not occur.","additionalDifferentials":["Ischaemic (low-flow) versus non-ischaemic (high-flow) priapism distinction","Stuttering (recurrent) priapism","Penile trauma with fistula","Drug-induced erection (intracavernosal agents, psychotropics, recreational drugs)","Sickle cell disease or other haemoglobinopathy","Pelvic or penile malignant infiltration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cavernosal blood gas analysis to differentiate ischaemic from non-ischaemic priapism","Penile colour Doppler ultrasound to assess cavernosal blood flow and identify a fistula","Full blood count and film with haemoglobinopathy screen (sickle cell testing)","Toxicology and medication review for causative agents","Selective pudendal arteriography if embolisation of a high-flow fistula is planned"],"redFlags":["Ischaemic priapism is a urological emergency; delay leads to corporal fibrosis and permanent erectile dysfunction","A rigid, painful erection persisting beyond four hours","Underlying sickle cell crisis or haematological malignancy requiring urgent systemic treatment","Failure of aspiration and intracavernosal therapy, mandating urgent surgical shunting"],"prognosis":"Prompt relief of ischaemic priapism improves the chance of preserved erectile function, but episodes lasting many hours frequently result in fibrosis and long-term erectile dysfunction; non-ischaemic priapism has a benign course and often resolves spontaneously or after embolisation.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish ischaemic from non-ischaemic priapism before treatment, leading to inappropriate management","Delaying emergency intervention in ischaemic priapism and risking irreversible fibrosis","Overlooking an underlying cause such as sickle cell disease or a causative medication","Treating high-flow priapism as an emergency with aggressive corporal procedures","Neglecting preventive management of recurrent stuttering priapism"],"management":["Urgently distinguish ischaemic from non-ischaemic priapism using cavernosal blood gas analysis and colour Doppler to direct therapy","For ischaemic priapism, perform corporal aspiration and saline irrigation followed by intracavernosal sympathomimetic injection such as phenylephrine","Proceed to surgical shunting, and consider early penile prosthesis in prolonged refractory cases, when aspiration and sympathomimetics fail","Treat the underlying cause, including hydration, oxygenation and exchange transfusion for sickle cell disease and withdrawal of causative drugs","Manage non-ischaemic priapism conservatively or with selective arterial embolisation of the cavernosal fistula","Provide analgesia and address the corporal compartment-syndrome state","Refer emergently to urology, with haematology input for haemoglobinopathy-related disease","Counsel on recurrence and erectile dysfunction, and consider prophylaxis for recurrent or stuttering priapism"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_and_metastatic_tumors_of":{"name":"Primary and Metastatic Tumors of the Nervous System","system":"Nervous system (neuro-oncology)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Brain tumors","CNS tumors","Intracranial neoplasms","Glioma","Glioblastoma","Meningioma","Brain metastases","Leptomeningeal metastases","Epidural spinal cord compression"],"chapter":95,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.716-726","clinicalPearls":["Brain tumors of any histology tend to present with a mixture of generalized features (headache, nausea/vomiting, cognitive decline, personality change, gait disturbance from raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus) and focal features (hemiparesis, aphasia, visual-field defect) (Harrison 22e p.716).","The classic morning-predominant brain tumor headache that eases through the day is actually seen in only a minority of patients; tumor headaches may be holocephalic, ipsilateral to the lesion, or even migraine-like with throbbing pain and scotoma (Harrison 22e p.716).","Seizures occur in roughly 25% of patients with brain metastases or malignant gliomas but are the presenting symptom in up to 90% of low-grade gliomas; all tumor-related seizures have a focal onset whether or not that is clinically apparent (Harrison 22e p.716).","Contrast (gadolinium) MRI is the preferred diagnostic study; CT is reserved for patients who cannot undergo MRI. Neuroimaging is generally the only test needed to diagnose a brain tumor, and laboratory tests are rarely useful (Harrison 22e p.716).","Malignant tumors (primary or metastatic) typically enhance with gadolinium, show central necrosis, and are ringed by white-matter edema, whereas low-grade gliomas usually do NOT enhance and are best seen on FLAIR sequences (Harrison 22e p.716).","Meningiomas are dural-based enhancing extra-axial masses with a characteristic dural tail that compress but do not invade brain; their principal differential is a dural metastasis or dural lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.716, p.722).","Adult diffuse gliomas are now classified molecularly: IDH-mutant astrocytoma (1p/19q intact), IDH-mutant oligodendroglioma (1p/19q co-deleted), and IDH wild-type glioblastoma; these molecular markers carry major prognostic weight (Harrison 22e p.718-719).","An unmethylated MGMT promoter predicts relative temozolomide resistance and a worse glioblastoma prognosis; MGMT promoter hypermethylation (gene silencing) predicts better response (Harrison 22e p.720).","Meningiomas are the most common primary brain tumor (~35%), are more common in women and with prior cranial irradiation and NF2, and many are found incidentally; small asymptomatic ones can simply be monitored with serial MRI (Harrison 22e p.721-722).","Most brain metastases lodge at the gray-white junction in watershed territories; ~85% are supratentorial and ~15% in the posterior fossa, and distribution roughly mirrors regional blood flow (Harrison 22e p.723).","Melanoma has the greatest propensity to seed the brain (found in ~80% at autopsy), but lung and breast carcinomas are the most common sources overall; prostate and breast cancers also favor dural metastases that can mimic meningioma (Harrison 22e p.723).","In epidural spinal metastasis, back pain is the presenting symptom in virtually all patients and may precede neurologic signs by weeks to months; the pain characteristically worsens on lying down, in contrast to arthritic pain which eases with recumbency (Harrison 22e p.725).","Any cancer patient with severe back pain should undergo whole-spine MRI; plain films, bone scan, or CT may show bony disease but only MRI reliably delineates epidural tumor (Harrison 22e p.725)."],"pathophysiology":"Most primary brain tumors have no identified cause; established risk factors are limited to ionizing radiation exposure (meningiomas, gliomas, schwannomas) and immunosuppression (primary CNS lymphoma), while electromagnetic field/cell-phone exposure, head injury, dietary N-nitroso compounds, and occupational factors are not proven. Tumors arise through a multistep accumulation of genetic alterations: loss of tumor-suppressor genes (p53, CDKN2A/B, PTEN) and amplification/overexpression of proto-oncogenes (EGFR, PDGFR), producing uncontrolled growth; many gliomas show marked molecular heterogeneity. The 2016 and 2021 WHO classifications integrate molecular parameters (e.g., IDH mutation, 1p/19q co-deletion, H3 K27/G34 alterations) with histology. Brain metastases spread hematogenously, lodging in terminal arterioles at the gray-white junction; leptomeningeal disease reaches the subarachnoid space via arterial circulation or retrograde venous flow and may also follow resection of a cerebellar metastasis. Epidural cord compression generally results from metastases to the axial skeleton (typically breast and prostate), though lymphoma can invade through an intervertebral foramen without bone destruction (Harrison 22e p.717, p.719, p.723, p.724-725).","additionalDifferentials":["High-grade glioma (glioblastoma, IDH wild-type)","Low-grade IDH-mutant astrocytoma or oligodendroglioma","Meningioma vs dural metastasis vs dural lymphoma","Primary CNS lymphoma vs metastasis vs infection","Vestibular schwannoma vs cerebellopontine-angle meningioma","Brain metastasis vs primary brain tumor vs second malignancy in a cancer patient"],"infectionMimics":["Brain abscess","Toxoplasmosis (ring-enhancing lesions, especially in immunocompromised, mimicking PCNSL or metastases)","Epidural abscess (mimicking epidural tumor)","Subacute/chronic meningitis (leptomeningeal lymphoma can present this way)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Demyelinating lesions","Sarcoidosis","Radiation necrosis in a previously treated patient","Cerebral infarction or hemorrhage","Migraine (when tumor headache has migrainous features)","Depression (apathy/social withdrawal from a frontal tumor)","Epidural hematoma, epidural lipomatosis, or extramedullary hematopoiesis (mimicking epidural tumor)","Arthritic/degenerative back pain (vs epidural metastasis pain)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Gadolinium-enhanced cranial MRI (preferred for all suspected brain tumors); CT only if MRI not possible","FLAIR sequences to detect non-enhancing low-grade glioma and infiltrative disease","Functional MRI for presurgical mapping of eloquent cortex","PET, MR perfusion, and MR spectroscopy to assess metabolic activity and help distinguish tumor progression from treatment-related necrosis","Molecular/integrated histopathologic testing (IDH, 1p/19q, MGMT promoter methylation, H3 status, etc.) for classification and prognosis","Stereotactic biopsy for histologic diagnosis of PCNSL (withhold glucocorticoids until biopsy where possible, as they are cytolytic to lymphoma)","CSF cytology and flow cytometry for leptomeningeal and primary CNS lymphoma; HIV testing and EBV DNA in immunocompromised PCNSL","Whole-spine MRI (or CT myelography if MRI impossible) for suspected epidural cord compression"],"redFlags":["New focal neurologic deficit (hemiparesis, aphasia, visual-field loss) that is subacute and progressive","New-onset focal seizures, especially in an adult without prior epilepsy","Severe back pain in a cancer patient (suggests epidural metastasis) — warrants urgent whole-spine MRI","Leg weakness, sensory level, or sphincter dysfunction suggesting evolving spinal cord compression (a neuro-oncologic emergency)","New limb pain in a patient with breast, lung cancer, or melanoma (consider leptomeningeal spread)","Signs of raised intracranial pressure / hydrocephalus (worsening headache, vomiting, declining consciousness)","Multilevel neuraxis signs (combined cranial neuropathies plus radiculopathies) suggesting leptomeningeal carcinomatosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome is highly histology- and molecular-subtype-dependent. Low-grade IDH-mutant astrocytomas have median survival around 10 years (most eventually transform to higher grade); oligodendrogliomas (1p/19q co-deleted) generally exceed 10 years. Glioblastoma carries a poor prognosis with median survival in the 15-18 month range with combined therapy and ~10% 5-year survival; adverse factors are older age, unmethylated MGMT promoter, poor Karnofsky status, and unresectable tumor. H3 K27-altered diffuse midline glioma has a median survival of roughly 1 year regardless of grade; H3 G34-mutant hemispheric glioma about 18-22 months. Medulloblastoma achieves long-term survival in ~70% but often with neurocognitive cost, varying by molecular subgroup (WNT best; group 3 worst). Completely resected meningiomas and schwannomas can be cured. Brain metastases treated with whole-brain radiotherapy alone yield median survival of only ~4-6 months, though stereotactic radiosurgery achieves local control in 80-90% and occasional cure. Leptomeningeal metastasis is treated palliatively with no curative therapy. In epidural metastasis, patients fare well if treated before a severe deficit develops, but overall survival is often short owing to widespread systemic disease (Harrison 22e p.718-725).","pitfalls":["Assuming a tumor headache must be morning-predominant — most are not, and tumor headaches can mimic migraine.","Misattributing frontal-lobe apathy and withdrawal to primary depression rather than tumor.","Giving glucocorticoids before biopsy in suspected PCNSL — their cytolytic effect on lymphoma cells can yield non-diagnostic tissue.","Relying on a single CSF cytology in suspected leptomeningeal disease — it is positive in only ~50% on first lumbar puncture and still misses ~10% after three samples; imaging is diagnostic in only ~75%.","Forgetting that the smallest brain metastases can produce extensive edema while large ones cause little, and that imaging appearance is nonspecific.","Overlooking that ~10% of systemic cancers can first present with a brain metastasis, sometimes requiring biopsy of the brain lesion when no accessible systemic site exists.","Treating low-grade glioma as benign — most ultimately transform to higher grade.","Delaying spine imaging in a cancer patient with back pain until neurologic deficits appear."],"management":["Pursue maximal safe surgical resection or debulking, or stereotactic biopsy, to obtain integrated histological and molecular diagnosis","Deliver radiotherapy, using fractionated external-beam treatment for gliomas and stereotactic radiosurgery or whole-brain radiotherapy for metastases","Give chemotherapy tailored to molecular subtype, such as temozolomide for glioblastoma guided by IDH and MGMT status","Use corticosteroids such as dexamethasone to reduce peritumoural oedema and raised intracranial pressure, withholding them before biopsy when CNS lymphoma is suspected","Control tumour-related seizures with antiepileptic drugs","Treat brain metastases with local therapy while controlling the systemic primary tumour","Coordinate a neuro-oncology multidisciplinary team with integrated molecular pathology to guide treatment and prognosis","Provide rehabilitation, supportive care, and timely palliative care"],"severityClassification":"Gliomas are graded by the WHO into grades 2-4 (low- vs high-grade) integrating histology with molecular markers (IDH mutation, 1p/19q co-deletion; CDKN2A/B homozygous deletion upgrades an IDH-mutant astrocytoma to grade 4). Meningiomas are WHO grade I (benign), II (atypical), or III (malignant). Brain tumor symptoms are categorized as generalized (raised-ICP related) versus focal/lateralizing. Nervous-system metastatic disease is grouped clinically into intraparenchymal brain metastases, leptomeningeal (carcinomatous meningitis) metastases, and epidural spinal cord compression (Harrison 22e p.716, p.719, p.722).","references":["Harrison 22e p.716","Harrison 22e p.717","Harrison 22e p.718","Harrison 22e p.719","Harrison 22e p.720","Harrison 22e p.721","Harrison 22e p.722","Harrison 22e p.723","Harrison 22e p.724","Harrison 22e p.725","Harrison 22e p.726"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_angiitis_cns":{"name":"Primary angiitis of the central nervous system","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PACNS","primary CNS vasculitis","isolated angiitis of the CNS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / CNS vasculitis literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Inflammatory vasculitis confined to the brain, spinal cord, and leptomeningeal vessels without systemic involvement","Presents insidiously with headache, cognitive decline, and recurrent focal deficits over weeks to months","Systemic inflammatory markers are frequently normal, unlike in most systemic vasculitides","Angiography may show a progressive pattern of segmental stenosis and dilatation, but can be normal in small-vessel disease","Cerebrospinal fluid usually shows a mild lymphocytic pleocytosis and raised protein","Brain and leptomeningeal biopsy remains the diagnostic reference standard and helps exclude mimics","Immunosuppression, typically corticosteroids combined with a cytotoxic agent, is the cornerstone of treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Primary angiitis of the central nervous system is an idiopathic inflammatory vasculitis restricted to vessels of the brain, spinal cord, and meninges. Segmental infiltration of small and medium arterial walls by lymphocytes, granulomas, or necrotising inflammation leads to vessel wall thickening, luminal narrowing, and thrombosis, producing progressive multifocal ischaemia and occasionally haemorrhage. Because inflammation is patchy and confined to the CNS, systemic markers are typically unremarkable and diagnosis can be difficult, with progressive rather than reversible arterial narrowing distinguishing it from vasoconstrictive syndromes.","additionalDifferentials":["Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome","Secondary CNS vasculitis from systemic disease or infection","Intracranial atherosclerosis","Infective arteritis such as varicella-zoster or tuberculous vasculopathy","Intravascular lymphoma or other neoplastic mimics","Neurosarcoidosis and demyelinating disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast MRI showing multifocal infarcts, white-matter change, or vessel-wall enhancement","Digital subtraction angiography assessing for segmental stenoses with awareness of limited sensitivity","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis for lymphocytic pleocytosis and exclusion of infection and malignancy","Brain and leptomeningeal biopsy for definitive histological confirmation","Systemic vasculitis and infection screen to exclude secondary causes"],"redFlags":["Progressive encephalopathy with recurrent strokes despite risk-factor control","New intracranial haemorrhage in a young patient","Rapid cognitive deterioration with multifocal deficits","Diagnostic uncertainty where infection or malignancy has not been excluded before immunosuppression"],"prognosis":"Untreated PACNS is often severely disabling or fatal, but many patients stabilise or improve with immunosuppression; relapses occur and outcome depends on prompt diagnosis and treatment.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome and giving inappropriate immunosuppression","Relying on a normal angiogram or normal inflammatory markers to exclude the diagnosis","Starting immunosuppression before excluding infection and lymphoma","Under-recognising small-vessel disease that spares large arteries on angiography"],"management":["Induce remission with high-dose corticosteroids combined with cyclophosphamide","Maintain remission with a steroid-sparing agent such as azathioprine or mycophenolate mofetil","Consider rituximab for refractory or relapsing disease","Exclude and treat mimics such as infection, reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome, and malignancy before committing to immunosuppression","Monitor disease activity with serial MRI and clinical review, remaining alert to relapse","Provide prophylaxis against treatment complications including Pneumocystis pneumonia, osteoporosis, and gastric ulceration","Institute secondary stroke prevention and vascular risk-factor management with neurorehabilitation","Manage jointly within a neurology and rheumatology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_angle_closure_glaucoma":{"name":"Primary angle-closure glaucoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PACG","angle-closure glaucoma","closed-angle glaucoma","acute angle-closure glaucoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs when the peripheral iris obstructs the trabecular meshwork, blocking aqueous outflow and raising intraocular pressure, often in hypermetropic eyes with shallow anterior chambers","An acute attack presents as an ophthalmic emergency with a severely painful red eye, blurred vision, haloes around lights, headache, and frequently nausea and vomiting that can be mistaken for an abdominal or neurological problem","Classic signs include a mid-dilated fixed vertically-oval pupil, a hazy oedematous cornea, a shallow anterior chamber, ciliary injection, and a stony-hard eye on palpation","Relative pupillary block is the usual mechanism, in which aqueous is trapped behind the iris, bowing it forward to appose the drainage angle","Gonioscopy of both eyes is essential to confirm a closed or occludable angle and to guide prophylactic treatment of the fellow eye","Initial treatment lowers pressure medically with topical intraocular-pressure-lowering agents and systemic acetazolamide, followed by laser peripheral iridotomy once the cornea clears to re-establish aqueous flow","The fellow eye is at high risk of a subsequent attack and should receive prophylactic laser iridotomy","Chronic angle closure may be insidious and painless, producing progressive optic-nerve cupping and field loss that mimics open-angle glaucoma"],"pathophysiology":"In primary angle closure the peripheral iris comes into contact with the trabecular meshwork and obstructs aqueous drainage, causing intraocular pressure to rise. The commonest mechanism is relative pupillary block, where resistance to aqueous passing between the lens and iris margin lets pressure build in the posterior chamber, bowing the peripheral iris forward against the angle. Predisposing anatomy includes a shallow anterior chamber, short axial length (hypermetropia), a relatively anterior and thick lens, and a crowded angle; pupillary mid-dilation in dim light or with mydriatics can precipitate acute closure. Sustained apposition damages the trabecular meshwork and may form permanent peripheral anterior synechiae, while a sudden pressure spike causes corneal oedema, iris ischaemia, and optic-nerve injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute anterior uveitis","Neovascular glaucoma with angle closure","Acute conjunctivitis or keratitis causing a red eye","Malignant glaucoma (aqueous misdirection)","Phacomorphic glaucoma from an intumescent lens","Secondary angle closure from a posterior segment mass or effusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gonioscopy of both eyes to assess angle configuration and detect peripheral anterior synechiae","Applanation tonometry to measure the height of the pressure rise","Anterior segment assessment of chamber depth, optionally with anterior segment OCT or ultrasound biomicroscopy","Optic disc evaluation and OCT of the retinal nerve fibre layer for glaucomatous cupping","Automated perimetry to document any field loss once the eye settles"],"redFlags":["Acutely painful red eye with haloes, a mid-dilated pupil, and a stony-hard globe indicating an acute attack requiring emergency pressure reduction","Nausea and vomiting misattributed to a systemic cause while the eye is overlooked","Corneal oedema with markedly reduced vision that may become permanent if pressure is not lowered promptly","An untreated occludable fellow eye at imminent risk of its own acute attack"],"prognosis":"Prompt medical pressure reduction followed by laser iridotomy usually aborts an acute attack and prevents recurrence, but delayed treatment can cause permanent optic-nerve damage, peripheral anterior synechiae, cataract, and lasting visual loss. Chronic angle closure carries a substantial risk of progressive glaucomatous field loss if unrecognised.","pitfalls":["Attributing the headache, nausea, and vomiting to a gastrointestinal or neurological cause and missing the ocular emergency","Dilating a susceptible shallow-chamber eye and precipitating an acute attack","Failing to examine and treat the occludable fellow eye prophylactically","Assuming laser iridotomy alone controls pressure when extensive synechial closure or coexisting open-angle damage requires further therapy","Overlooking chronic painless angle closure presenting as apparent open-angle glaucoma"],"management":["Lower intraocular pressure urgently with topical agents (beta-blocker, alpha-agonist, pilocarpine) and systemic acetazolamide, adding a hyperosmotic such as mannitol if needed","Perform definitive laser peripheral iridotomy once the cornea clears to relieve pupillary block","Give prophylactic laser peripheral iridotomy to the fellow occludable eye","Provide analgesia and antiemetics during the acute attack","Consider lens extraction where a bulky or cataractous lens is driving angle closure","Avoid mydriasis and use caution with anticholinergic and dilating drugs that can precipitate closure","Monitor intraocular pressure, optic disc, and visual fields long term, adding topical pressure-lowering therapy for chronic angle closure","Refer emergently to ophthalmology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_bilateral_macronodular_adrenal_hyperplasia":{"name":"Primary bilateral macronodular adrenal hyperplasia","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PBMAH","ACTH-independent macronodular adrenal hyperplasia","AIMAH","bilateral macronodular adrenocortical disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard endocrinology reference texts and adrenal disorder consensus guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A cause of ACTH-independent Cushing syndrome in which both adrenal glands are enlarged by multiple non-pigmented macronodules, often discovered incidentally on imaging","Cortisol excess is frequently mild or subclinical, so many patients present with metabolic features (hypertension, glucose intolerance, obesity) rather than florid Cushingoid appearance","A hallmark is aberrant expression of illicit G-protein-coupled receptors on the adrenal cortex, causing cortisol secretion driven by non-ACTH signals","Food-dependent (GIP-mediated) hypercortisolism is the classic example, where meals rather than pituitary ACTH trigger cortisol release","Other aberrant receptors described include those responsive to vasopressin, catecholamines, LH/hCG, and serotonin, which can be probed with provocative testing","Plasma ACTH is suppressed and low-dose dexamethasone fails to suppress cortisol, confirming an ACTH-independent adrenal source","Germline ARMC5 variants underlie a substantial proportion of apparently sporadic bilateral disease and warrant consideration of familial screening"],"pathophysiology":"The adrenal cortices become progressively enlarged by numerous benign macronodules that autonomously secrete cortisol independent of pituitary ACTH, which is consequently suppressed. A central mechanism is the aberrant (ectopic or over-expressed) presence of G-protein-coupled receptors on steroidogenic cells, so that ligands not normally driving cortisol, such as gastric inhibitory polypeptide released after meals, vasopressin, catecholamines or serotonin, inappropriately stimulate steroidogenesis. Local intra-adrenal production of ACTH by nodular cells may further sustain growth in a paracrine loop. Inactivating germline mutations in the tumour-suppressor ARMC5 are commonly identified and predispose to the multinodular bilateral phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Cortisol-secreting adrenal adenoma","Adrenocortical carcinoma","Primary pigmented nodular adrenocortical disease","Pituitary (ACTH-dependent) Cushing disease","Ectopic ACTH syndrome","Non-functioning bilateral adrenal incidentalomas","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia with adrenal nodularity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Late-night salivary cortisol and 24-hour urinary free cortisol to confirm hypercortisolism","Low-dose dexamethasone suppression testing and paired plasma ACTH to establish ACTH independence","Cross-sectional adrenal imaging (CT or MRI) demonstrating bilateral macronodular enlargement","Provocative testing for aberrant receptor responses (e.g. mixed-meal, posture, and hormone challenge protocols)","ARMC5 germline genetic testing and family screening where indicated","Metabolic assessment for glucose intolerance, dyslipidaemia and bone density loss"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or severe hypercortisolism suggesting a more aggressive adrenal pathology","Imaging features atypical for benign hyperplasia raising concern for adrenocortical carcinoma","Uncontrolled hypertension, hypokalaemia or diabetes reflecting significant hormone excess","Signs of adrenal insufficiency after unilateral or bilateral surgery"],"prognosis":"The condition is usually indolent with benign nodules, and metabolic and cortisol excess can be controlled by unilateral or bilateral adrenalectomy, though bilateral resection commits the patient to lifelong steroid replacement. Long-term outcome is generally good when hypercortisolism is corrected and associated cardiometabolic risk is managed.","pitfalls":["Dismissing bilateral adrenal enlargement as non-functioning without screening for subclinical cortisol excess","Overlooking aberrant receptor-driven (e.g. food-dependent) cortisol secretion that can be targeted medically","Assuming the disease is sporadic and failing to consider ARMC5-related familial risk","Precipitating adrenal insufficiency by removing both glands without planning glucocorticoid replacement"],"management":["Offer adrenalectomy, unilateral or bilateral, as definitive treatment of the ACTH-independent hypercortisolism","Provide lifelong glucocorticoid, and mineralocorticoid where bilateral, replacement after bilateral adrenalectomy","Control cortisol medically with steroidogenesis inhibitors such as metyrapone or ketoconazole when surgery is deferred or unsuitable","In aberrant-receptor forms, use targeted pharmacological blockade such as a beta-blocker, somatostatin analogue, or GnRH analogue guided by provocative testing","Treat metabolic complications including hypertension, glucose intolerance, dyslipidaemia, and reduced bone density","Educate on adrenal insufficiency, steroid sick-day rules, and recognition of adrenal crisis","Arrange ARMC5 germline genetic testing and family screening","Follow up within an endocrine multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society clinical practice guidance on adrenal Cushing syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_bile_acid_malabsorption":{"name":"Primary bile acid malabsorption","system":"Gastrointestinal / hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ASBT deficiency","SLC10A2 deficiency","Bile acid diarrhoea","IBAT deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Gastroenterology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The congenital form is autosomal recessive, caused by loss-of-function SLC10A2 mutations affecting the apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter (ASBT/IBAT) of the terminal ileum.","It presents in infancy with chronic watery and fatty diarrhoea, faltering growth and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency.","Interruption of the enterohepatic circulation depletes the bile acid pool and characteristically lowers plasma cholesterol, a useful clue that distinguishes it from other congenital diarrhoeas.","Bile acids spilling into the colon drive a secretory diarrhoea, so symptoms typically improve with a bile-acid sequestrant.","The far commoner adult 'primary' (type 2) bile acid diarrhoea is idiopathic and linked to impaired ileal FGF19 feedback with hepatic bile acid overproduction, not to ASBT loss.","SeHCAT (selenium-75 homotaurocholic acid) retention testing quantifies bile acid malabsorption; serum C4 (7-alpha-hydroxy-4-cholesten-3-one) tends to be raised and FGF19 low.","An empirical trial of a bile-acid sequestrant is both diagnostic and therapeutic in the adult idiopathic form; fat-soluble vitamin status must be monitored in the congenital form."],"pathophysiology":"In its congenital form, primary bile acid malabsorption is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in SLC10A2, encoding the apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter (ASBT, also called IBAT) expressed on the brush border of terminal ileal enterocytes, which mediates active reabsorption of conjugated bile acids. Failure of ileal uptake interrupts the enterohepatic circulation, delivering excess bile acids to the colon where they stimulate secretion and motility to produce a choleretic secretory diarrhoea, while compensatory upregulation of hepatic bile acid synthesis fails to restore the pool, causing fat and fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption and low plasma cholesterol. The more common adult idiopathic (type 2) bile acid diarrhoea instead reflects impaired ileal FGF19 negative feedback on hepatic CYP7A1, so bile acid overproduction overwhelms normal reabsorptive capacity.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital chloride diarrhoea and congenital sodium diarrhoea","Microvillus inclusion disease and tufting enteropathy","Inborn errors of bile acid synthesis and metabolism","Secondary bile acid malabsorption from ileal Crohn's disease or resection","Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (e.g. cystic fibrosis, Shwachman-Diamond syndrome)","Coeliac disease","Microscopic colitis in adults"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SeHCAT (selenium-75 homotaurocholic acid) retention scan","Serum C4 (7-alpha-hydroxy-4-cholesten-3-one) and FGF19","Faecal bile acid measurement and faecal fat estimation","Fat-soluble vitamin levels and plasma cholesterol","SLC10A2 molecular testing","Therapeutic trial of a bile-acid sequestrant","Ileocolonoscopy and cross-sectional imaging to exclude secondary causes"],"redFlags":["Severe dehydration and electrolyte disturbance in an infant","Faltering growth or failure to thrive","Signs of fat-soluble vitamin deficiency (bleeding tendency, rickets, neurological features)","New-onset bile acid diarrhoea with alarm features such as weight loss or bleeding, requiring exclusion of ileal Crohn's disease or malignancy"],"prognosis":"The congenital form usually responds symptomatically to bile-acid sequestrants but needs lifelong attention to fat-soluble vitamin status; the adult idiopathic form is generally benign and controllable, though frequently under-recognised and chronic.","pitfalls":["Labelling idiopathic bile acid diarrhoea as diarrhoea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome and never testing for or treating bile acid malabsorption.","Assuming all bile acid malabsorption is secondary to ileal disease and overlooking the primary and congenital forms.","In infants, missing complications of fat-soluble vitamin deficiency.","Forgetting that bile-acid sequestrants bind co-administered drugs and fat-soluble vitamins, so dose timing must be separated."],"management":["Use a bile-acid sequestrant such as colestyramine, colesevelam, or colestipol as the mainstay to bind luminal bile acids and control the secretory diarrhoea","Supplement and monitor fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K","Advise a reduced-fat diet to limit steatorrhoea","Monitor growth and nutritional status in affected infants and children","Track plasma cholesterol and correct residual deficiencies","Distinguish and treat the commoner adult idiopathic (type 2) bile-acid diarrhoea, also with a sequestrant","Offer genetic counselling for the autosomal recessive congenital form","Provide gastroenterology and dietetic follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Gastroenterology reference texts","Bile acid diarrhoea clinical guidelines","Inherited metabolic disease references"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_carnitine_deficiency":{"name":"Primary carnitine deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Fatty acid oxidation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Systemic primary carnitine deficiency","Carnitine uptake defect","OCTN2 deficiency","SLC22A5 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive defect of the OCTN2 sodium-dependent carnitine transporter encoded by SLC22A5, causing urinary carnitine wasting and profound depletion of plasma and tissue carnitine, which starves long-chain fatty-acid oxidation of its obligatory carrier.","Classic presentations are an infantile metabolic form (hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, hepatomegaly, encephalopathy) and a childhood cardiac form with progressive dilated cardiomyopathy and skeletal myopathy; some individuals remain asymptomatic into adulthood.","The hallmark biochemistry is a markedly low plasma free carnitine with low acylcarnitines and an inappropriately high fractional renal excretion of carnitine.","Newborn screening based on low free carnitine can miss an affected infant but may instead flag an affected mother whose own carnitine depletion lowers the baby's screening value.","This is the fatty-acid-oxidation disorder that responds dramatically to lifelong oral L-carnitine supplementation, with reversal of cardiomyopathy and prevention of hypoglycaemic crises.","Untreated disease can cause sudden cardiac death or arrhythmia, whereas treated individuals generally have an excellent prognosis.","Diagnosis rests on very low carnitine that persists despite dietary sources, raised renal carnitine loss, reduced carnitine uptake in cultured fibroblasts and SLC22A5 sequencing, after excluding the far commoner secondary carnitine deficiency."],"pathophysiology":"OCTN2 is a sodium-dependent transporter that drives active carnitine uptake into the renal tubule, heart, skeletal muscle and other tissues. When SLC22A5 variants impair the transporter, the kidney can no longer reabsorb filtered carnitine, so carnitine is lost in the urine and tissues cannot accumulate it, producing systemic and intracellular carnitine depletion. Without adequate carnitine, long-chain acyl groups cannot be esterified for mitochondrial import, so long-chain fatty-acid oxidation fails; during fasting or intercurrent illness this yields hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and energy deficit, and chronically the heart, which depends heavily on fatty-acid oxidation, develops a dilated cardiomyopathy. Because high circulating carnitine can still drive residual and passive tissue uptake, pharmacological oral carnitine replacement restores tissue carnitine and reverses the functional defect, explaining the excellent treatment response.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary carnitine deficiency from other fatty-acid-oxidation disorders, organic acidaemias, valproate therapy, dietary insufficiency, renal losses or prematurity","Other causes of dilated cardiomyopathy in childhood","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase 1 deficiency and other hypoketotic hypoglycaemias","Medium-chain and very long-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","Mitochondrial respiratory chain disorders","Endocrine causes of hypoglycaemia including hyperinsulinism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma total and free carnitine, characteristically markedly low","Plasma acylcarnitine profile showing globally low acylcarnitines","Urine carnitine with assessment of fractional excretion and renal carnitine handling","Molecular testing of SLC22A5","Carnitine transport assay in cultured fibroblasts","Echocardiography and electrocardiography","Testing of the mother when the disorder is detected through newborn screening"],"redFlags":["Dilated cardiomyopathy, heart failure or arrhythmia in a child","Hypoketotic hypoglycaemia with encephalopathy in an infant","Sudden collapse","An abnormal newborn screen for low free carnitine, whether reflecting the infant or an affected mother"],"prognosis":"With early recognition and lifelong carnitine replacement the prognosis is excellent and the cardiomyopathy typically reverses; untreated disease carries a risk of sudden death.","pitfalls":["Labelling the far commoner secondary carnitine deficiency as primary without confirming renal carnitine wasting and genetics","Stopping carnitine once the child is well, after which relapse and sudden death may follow","Missing an affected but asymptomatic mother identified through her infant's screen","Overlooking the diagnosis in unexplained paediatric cardiomyopathy"],"management":["Start lifelong oral L-carnitine supplementation as definitive replacement, which reverses cardiomyopathy and prevents metabolic crises","Avoid prolonged fasting and ensure frequent carbohydrate-rich feeds, especially during intercurrent illness","Manage acute decompensation with intravenous glucose to correct hypoketotic hypoglycaemia during illness","Monitor plasma free carnitine and adjust supplementation and adherence accordingly","Undertake echocardiographic and electrocardiographic surveillance for cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia","Provide genetic counselling and sibling testing for this autosomal recessive disorder, and test the mother when detected through newborn screening","Give the family an emergency sick-day plan and alert documentation","Follow up within a metabolic medicine and cardiology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited metabolic disease reference texts","Fatty acid oxidation disorder management consensus guidelines","Paediatric metabolic medicine references"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_carnitine_transporter_deficiency":{"name":"Primary carnitine transporter deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["systemic primary carnitine deficiency","SLC22A5 deficiency","OCTN2 deficiency","carnitine uptake defect","CUD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive defect of the SLC22A5-encoded OCTN2 plasma-membrane carnitine transporter, impairing carnitine uptake into cells and its reabsorption by the renal tubule","Renal carnitine wasting causes very low plasma total and free carnitine and intracellular carnitine depletion","Presentations include infantile hypoketotic hypoglycaemia with hepatic dysfunction, and childhood cardiomyopathy with skeletal myopathy","Some individuals (including mothers detected through a positive newborn screen in their infant) are asymptomatic yet at risk of arrhythmia or sudden death","Newborn screening hallmark is a markedly low free carnitine (C0), which may also reflect maternal deficiency detected via the baby","The disorder is highly responsive to carnitine supplementation, which reverses cardiomyopathy and prevents crises when adherence is maintained","Lifelong carnitine replacement and avoidance of prolonged fasting are the mainstays of management"],"pathophysiology":"OCTN2 is a high-affinity sodium-dependent carnitine transporter expressed in heart, muscle, kidney and other tissues, responsible for cellular carnitine uptake and renal tubular reabsorption. Loss of function causes urinary carnitine loss and systemic carnitine depletion, so long-chain fatty acids cannot be shuttled into mitochondria for beta-oxidation because carnitine, the obligatory carrier, is lacking. The resulting energy deficit affects the heart and skeletal muscle, producing cardiomyopathy and myopathy, and impairs hepatic ketogenesis during fasting, causing hypoketotic hypoglycaemia; unlike downstream oxidation defects, the block is due to substrate (carnitine) deficiency rather than a defective enzyme.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary carnitine deficiency from other fatty-acid oxidation or organic acid disorders","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase and translocase deficiencies","Primary dilated cardiomyopathy of other cause","Reye-like illness and other causes of infantile hypoglycaemia","Dietary carnitine deficiency or renal Fanconi syndrome causing carnitine loss","Mitochondrial disorders presenting with cardiomyopathy and myopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma total and free carnitine (markedly reduced)","Newborn screening acylcarnitine profile with very low free carnitine (C0)","Urine carnitine to demonstrate renal carnitine wasting with a low reabsorption fraction","Echocardiography and ECG to detect cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia","SLC22A5 molecular genetic testing for confirmation","Fibroblast carnitine transport study where genotype is inconclusive, and testing of the mother when a neonate screens positive"],"redFlags":["Cardiomyopathy with heart failure or arrhythmia","Hypoketotic hypoglycaemia with encephalopathy in infancy","Family history of sudden cardiac death or an affected mother identified through the infant","Rapid clinical deterioration during fasting or intercurrent illness"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally excellent with early diagnosis and consistent carnitine supplementation, which reverses cardiac and muscle disease; the main risks are arrhythmia and sudden death in untreated or non-adherent individuals.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish primary transporter deficiency from secondary carnitine depletion","Not testing the mother when a newborn screens with low free carnitine","Interrupting carnitine supplementation, risking recurrence of cardiomyopathy","Overlooking asymptomatic adults who remain at risk of sudden cardiac events"],"management":["Provide lifelong oral L-carnitine supplementation as the definitive treatment, reversing cardiomyopathy and preventing hypoglycaemic crises","Avoid fasting and maintain regular feeding, with an emergency high-carbohydrate regimen during illness","Treat intercurrent illness with intravenous dextrose to prevent hypoketotic hypoglycaemia","Perform serial echocardiography and electrocardiography to monitor for cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia","Monitor plasma free carnitine and reinforce adherence to prevent sudden cardiac events","Offer genetic counselling and sibling screening for this autosomal recessive disorder, and test the mother of a screen-positive neonate","Supply sick-day rules and emergency documentation","Follow up jointly with metabolic medicine and cardiology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_ciliary_dyskinesia":{"name":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia","system":"Respiratory / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCD","immotile cilia syndrome","DNAH5","DNAI1","CCDC39","CCDC40"],"chapter":null,"source":"ERS/ATS guidance and standard respiratory genetics texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Suspect in a child or adult with a lifelong daily wet (productive) cough, recurrent chest and sinus infections, and persistent rhinitis","Around half of patients have situs inversus (Kartagener phenotype), and laterality defects or congenital heart disease raise suspicion","Unexplained neonatal respiratory distress in a term baby, often needing oxygen for days, is an important early clue","Nasal nitric oxide is characteristically very low and is a useful screening tool in cooperative children and adults","Diagnosis rests on a combination of clinical phenotype, low nasal nitric oxide, high-speed video microscopy of ciliary beat, transmission electron microscopy of ciliary ultrastructure, and genetic testing","Bronchiectasis develops progressively and tends to affect the middle and lower lobes, unlike the upper-lobe predominance often seen in cystic fibrosis","Male infertility (immotile sperm) and reduced female fertility are common because motile cilia and flagella share structural components","Management is largely supportive with airway clearance, prompt antibiotic treatment of infective exacerbations, and surveillance of lung function and hearing"],"pathophysiology":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia is a mostly autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in genes encoding components of the motile cilium (for example the outer and inner dynein arms and radial spokes). Defective assembly or function of these structures impairs the coordinated beating of respiratory cilia, so mucociliary clearance fails and mucus and bacteria are retained throughout the airways and sinuses. The same defect in embryonic nodal cilia disrupts left-right patterning during development, explaining the frequent situs anomalies, while shared axonemal components account for impaired sperm and fallopian tube function.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystic fibrosis","Common variable and other primary immunodeficiencies","Chronic aspiration or recurrent viral respiratory infection","Secondary (acquired) ciliary dysfunction from chronic infection or smoke exposure","Allergic rhinosinusitis with post-nasal drip","Foreign body or structural airway anomaly"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasal nitric oxide measurement as a screening test","High-speed video microscopy of ciliary beat frequency and pattern from a nasal brushing","Transmission electron microscopy of ciliary ultrastructure","Extended genetic panel for known PCD genes","High-resolution chest imaging to assess for and monitor bronchiectasis","Sputum microbiology and lung function testing","Audiometry and ENT assessment for chronic otitis media with effusion"],"redFlags":["Progressive breathlessness or falling lung function suggesting advancing bronchiectasis","Recurrent isolation of Pseudomonas or non-tuberculous mycobacteria","Haemoptysis indicating significant airway disease","Situs and cardiac anomalies requiring cardiology assessment in infancy"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and structured airway clearance many patients maintain reasonable lung function into adulthood, but chronic infection can cause progressive bronchiectasis, and outcomes vary with genotype, adherence to therapy, and access to specialist multidisciplinary care.","pitfalls":["Attributing lifelong wet cough to recurrent viral infection or asthma and delaying diagnosis for years","Relying on a single test result, as ultrastructure can appear normal in some genetic subtypes","Interpreting nasal nitric oxide as diagnostic in isolation, since it can be low in cystic fibrosis and unreliable during acute infection","Overlooking the situs inversus clue on a chest radiograph","Forgetting to counsel about fertility implications"],"management":["Institute regular airway clearance physiotherapy to compensate for failed mucociliary clearance","Treat respiratory exacerbations promptly with culture-directed and often prolonged antibiotics","Undertake routine surveillance sputum microbiology and lung-function monitoring","Manage chronic rhinosinusitis and otitis media with nasal saline and steroids, ENT input, and hearing support","Give influenza and pneumococcal immunisations and advise avoidance of tobacco smoke","Monitor progressive bronchiectasis with high-resolution imaging and consider surgery for localised severe disease","Offer genetic counselling for the mostly autosomal recessive inheritance and address subfertility","Coordinate a specialist PCD multidisciplinary team spanning respiratory, ENT, audiology, and fertility services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["European Respiratory Society statement on primary ciliary dyskinesia","Standard paediatric and adult respiratory medicine texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_cns_lymphoma":{"name":"Primary CNS lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCNSL","primary central nervous system lymphoma","primary diffuse large B-cell lymphoma of the CNS","primary intraocular/cerebral lymphoma","CD20+ MYD88-mutated CNS large B-cell lymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An aggressive extranodal lymphoma confined to the brain, spinal cord, leptomeninges, or eyes without systemic disease at diagnosis","Presents with focal neurological deficits, cognitive or personality change, raised intracranial pressure, or seizures, and around a fifth have ocular involvement","Most cases are diffuse large B-cell lymphomas, frequently harbouring MYD88 and CD79B mutations","In immunocompetent patients tumours are usually Epstein-Barr virus-negative, whereas immunodeficiency-associated (e.g. HIV or post-transplant) cases are typically EBV-driven","Imaging characteristically shows homogeneously enhancing periventricular lesions that are markedly diffusion-restricting","Corticosteroids can rapidly shrink lesions and obscure histology, so biopsy should ideally precede steroids where safe","Treatment centres on high-dose methotrexate-based combination chemotherapy, often with rituximab, sometimes consolidated by autologous transplantation, with radiotherapy used selectively because of neurotoxicity"],"pathophysiology":"Primary central nervous system lymphoma is an aggressive B-cell neoplasm, usually of diffuse large B-cell type, that arises within and remains confined to the central nervous system and eyes. The malignant cells are typically of activated B-cell origin and characteristically carry mutations activating B-cell receptor and toll-like receptor signalling, such as MYD88 and CD79B. Tumour cells cluster around blood vessels within the brain parenchyma, and how the clone becomes restricted to this immune-privileged site remains incompletely understood. In immunodeficient hosts, impaired surveillance allows Epstein-Barr virus to drive transformation, accounting for the EBV positivity seen in that setting.","additionalDifferentials":["High-grade glioma such as glioblastoma","Cerebral toxoplasmosis (particularly in HIV infection)","Cerebral metastasis or abscess","Demyelinating disease such as a tumefactive multiple sclerosis lesion","Neurosarcoidosis or other inflammatory CNS disease","Secondary CNS involvement by systemic lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced MRI of the brain and whole neuraxis","Stereotactic brain biopsy (ideally before corticosteroids) with immunohistochemistry","Cerebrospinal fluid cytology and flow cytometry, with clonality studies","Slit-lamp examination and vitreous sampling to assess ocular involvement","HIV testing and EBV studies on tissue or cerebrospinal fluid","Systemic staging (PET-CT, bone marrow, and in men testicular assessment) to confirm disease is CNS-limited"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising intracranial pressure or impending herniation","Progressive obtundation or new focal deficits","Status epilepticus","Leptomeningeal or ocular dissemination requiring additional directed therapy"],"prognosis":"Untreated disease is rapidly fatal, but high-dose methotrexate-based regimens achieve remission in many patients; older age, poor performance status, and treatment-related neurotoxicity limit long-term outcomes, and relapse is common.","pitfalls":["Giving corticosteroids before biopsy, which can transiently dissolve the tumour and render histology non-diagnostic","Mistaking lesions for glioma, demyelination, or, in HIV, toxoplasmosis","Failing to examine the eyes and cerebrospinal fluid for occult involvement","Omitting systemic staging and thereby misclassifying secondary CNS lymphoma as primary"],"management":["Deliver high-dose methotrexate-based induction chemotherapy, usually combined with rituximab, as the mainstay","Consolidate responders with high-dose chemotherapy and autologous stem-cell transplantation or whole-brain radiotherapy","Obtain stereotactic biopsy before corticosteroids where safe, reserving steroids for threatening mass effect as they obscure histology","Treat ocular involvement with intravitreal chemotherapy or ocular radiotherapy","In immunodeficiency-associated disease, optimise antiretroviral therapy or reduce iatrogenic immunosuppression","Monitor for treatment-related neurotoxicity and perform surveillance MRI for relapse","Provide supportive care with seizure control and management of raised intracranial pressure","Manage within a haemato-oncology and neuro-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","Swerdlow SH et al. WHO monograph on lymphoid neoplasms"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_cns_vasculitis":{"name":"Primary central nervous system vasculitis","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Primary angiitis of the central nervous system","PACNS","Primary CNS vasculitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Primary CNS vasculitis is an inflammatory vasculopathy confined to the brain, spinal cord and leptomeninges, with no systemic vasculitis.","Presentation is typically insidious with headache and cognitive decline, punctuated by multifocal strokes or transient ischaemic events in different vascular territories.","Systemic inflammatory markers and ANCA are usually normal or negative, which helps distinguish it from systemic vasculitides.","Cerebrospinal fluid commonly shows a mild lymphocytic pleocytosis and raised protein; a completely normal CSF makes the diagnosis much less likely.","Catheter angiography may show alternating stenosis and dilatation ('beading'), but is neither sensitive nor specific and can be normal in small-vessel disease.","Brain and leptomeningeal biopsy is the reference standard but has significant sampling error because the disease is patchy.","Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome is the key mimic and must be excluded before committing to prolonged immunosuppression.","High-resolution vessel-wall MRI showing concentric enhancement can support the diagnosis and help differentiate it from non-inflammatory vasculopathy."],"pathophysiology":"Primary CNS vasculitis is characterised by inflammatory infiltration and destruction of the walls of small and medium arteries and veins restricted to the central nervous system. Histology varies from granulomatous inflammation (often with vascular amyloid deposition) to lymphocytic or acute necrotising patterns. The inflammation narrows and occludes vessels, producing ischaemia and infarction, and may weaken walls to cause haemorrhage. The stimulus is unknown and, by definition, there is no systemic vasculitic process; the disease is heterogeneous, affecting different calibre vessels in different patients, which accounts for its variable clinical and radiological picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome","Intracranial atherosclerosis or embolic stroke","Secondary CNS involvement by systemic vasculitis or connective tissue disease","CNS infection (varicella zoster, tuberculosis, fungal, syphilitic arteritis)","Intravascular lymphoma or other neoplastic vasculopathy","Cerebral amyloid angiopathy with associated inflammation","Moyamoya and other non-inflammatory vasculopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI brain with diffusion and gradient-echo or susceptibility sequences","High-resolution vessel-wall MRI to assess mural enhancement","Catheter cerebral angiography where medium-vessel disease is suspected","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis including cell count, protein, cytology and infection screen","Systemic vasculitis and autoimmune serology (ANCA, ANA, complement) to exclude secondary causes","Brain and leptomeningeal biopsy in selected cases","Screening for infective and neoplastic mimics including varicella zoster PCR"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive encephalopathy or reduced consciousness","Recurrent multifocal strokes despite treatment","Intracranial haemorrhage","Spinal cord involvement with evolving myelopathy","New seizures with progressive cognitive decline"],"prognosis":"Untreated disease can progress to disabling stroke, cognitive impairment or death, but many patients respond to immunosuppression; the granulomatous and small-vessel subtypes may relapse and require prolonged therapy. Early diagnosis and exclusion of mimics substantially improve outcomes.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome as vasculitis and giving unnecessary long-term immunosuppression.","Relying on angiographic 'beading' as diagnostic when it is non-specific and often absent in small-vessel disease.","Excluding the diagnosis on a single negative biopsy given the patchy distribution of disease.","Overlooking infective arteritis, particularly varicella zoster, before starting immunosuppression.","Attributing an isolated stroke in a young patient to atherosclerosis without considering CNS vasculitis."],"management":["Induce remission with high-dose corticosteroids combined with cyclophosphamide","Maintain remission with azathioprine or mycophenolate mofetil once disease is controlled","Reserve rituximab for refractory or relapsing disease","Exclude mimics such as infection, reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome, cerebral amyloid angiopathy, and malignancy before immunosuppression","Track disease activity with serial MRI and clinical assessment to detect relapse","Provide prophylaxis against Pneumocystis pneumonia, osteoporosis, and gastric ulceration during immunosuppression","Institute stroke prevention and neurorehabilitation","Manage within a neurology and rheumatology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology, current edition","Oxford Textbook of Medicine, current edition","Consensus reviews on primary angiitis of the central nervous system"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_cough_headache":{"name":"Primary cough headache","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["benign cough headache","Valsalva-induced headache"],"chapter":null,"source":"International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sudden headache precipitated by coughing, sneezing or other Valsalva manoeuvres, not by sustained physical exertion","Pain is typically bilateral and posterior, lasting seconds to a few minutes","It occurs mainly in older adults, in contrast to the younger age of primary exercise headache","A Chiari type I malformation or other posterior fossa lesion is the key secondary cause to exclude","Brain imaging with attention to the craniocervical junction is recommended in most cases","The primary form often responds to indometacin"],"pathophysiology":"Primary cough headache is attributed to a transient rise in intracranial pressure and venous distension provoked by the Valsalva manoeuvre, in the absence of a structural lesion. The same mechanical stress can unmask symptomatic cough headache when there is crowding at the foramen magnum, as in Chiari malformation, or a posterior fossa mass, which is why exclusion of these is essential before accepting a primary diagnosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Chiari type I malformation","Posterior fossa tumour or other mass lesion","Primary exercise headache","Spontaneous intracranial hypotension","Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain including the craniocervical junction","Neurological examination for posterior fossa or long-tract signs","History clarifying Valsalva versus sustained-exertion triggers","Further vascular imaging if a secondary vascular cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Cerebellar or brainstem signs on examination","Occipital pain with features of tonsillar descent","First or worst headache suggesting a vascular event","Progressive or positional headache"],"prognosis":"The primary form is benign and may remit over months to a few years; prognosis of symptomatic cough headache depends on the underlying structural cause.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing primary cough headache without imaging and missing a Chiari malformation","Confusing it with primary exercise headache","Overlooking a thunderclap presentation that needs urgent vascular workup","Assuming benignity in a young patient without exclusion of structural disease"],"management":["Offer indometacin as first-line symptomatic and preventive treatment","Exclude a Chiari type I malformation or posterior fossa lesion with craniocervical-junction MRI before accepting the primary diagnosis","Treat any underlying chronic cough or respiratory condition to reduce the Valsalva trigger","Consider alternative preventives such as acetazolamide or topiramate if indometacin is not tolerated","Reassure the patient about the benign, often self-limiting course","Refer to neurology if features are atypical or a secondary cause is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3)","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_cutaneous_amyloidosis":{"name":"primary cutaneous amyloidosis","system":"Skin / Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["primary localised cutaneous amyloidosis","lichen amyloidosis","macular amyloidosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Weedon's Skin Pathology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Amyloid deposition confined to the skin without systemic or visceral involvement, distinct from systemic amyloidosis","Lichen amyloidosis presents as intensely pruritic, hyperkeratotic, brownish papules that coalesce into rippled plaques, classically on the shins and extensor legs","Macular amyloidosis produces rippled or reticulate greyish-brown hyperpigmentation, typically over the interscapular back, often linked to chronic friction from towels or brushes","In the keratinic (lichen and macular) forms the amyloid is derived from degenerate epidermal keratinocytes, staining for cytokeratins, and is closely tied to chronic scratching and friction","Nodular amyloidosis is different: it is a localised AL-type deposit produced by a clonal population of cutaneous plasma cells and carries a small risk of progression to systemic amyloidosis, warranting follow-up","Familial primary cutaneous amyloidosis is associated with variants in OSMR and IL31RA, and lichen or macular amyloidosis may occur in multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A","Management focuses on breaking the itch-scratch cycle with topical corticosteroids, antipruritics and phototherapy, since persistent friction perpetuates the deposition"],"pathophysiology":"Primary cutaneous amyloidosis involves extracellular deposition of amyloid restricted to the dermis. In the common keratinic forms (lichen and macular amyloidosis), chronic friction and scratching are thought to cause apoptosis (filamentous degeneration) of basal keratinocytes; the resulting keratin fragments are converted into amyloid within the papillary dermis, which is why the deposits stain for cytokeratins and the disorder is perpetuated by the itch-scratch cycle. Genetic susceptibility, including variants in the oncostatin M receptor (OSMR) and interleukin-31 receptor (IL31RA) in familial disease, contributes in some patients. Nodular amyloidosis differs fundamentally, arising from local production of immunoglobulin light-chain (AL) amyloid by a clonal infiltrate of cutaneous plasma cells.","additionalDifferentials":["Lichen simplex chronicus and prurigo nodularis","Hypertrophic lichen planus","Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation and other pigmentary disorders in macular disease","Colloid milium","Systemic amyloidosis with cutaneous deposits","Notalgia paraesthetica with secondary amyloid","Nodular basal cell carcinoma or adnexal tumour in the nodular form"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with Congo red staining showing apple-green birefringence under polarised light","Immunohistochemistry for cytokeratins to identify keratin-derived amyloid in the keratinic forms","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis and immunofixation, particularly for nodular amyloidosis to exclude systemic disease","Serum free light chains where AL amyloid is suspected","Assessment for multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A when there is a suggestive family history","Long-term monitoring of nodular amyloidosis for progression to systemic amyloidosis"],"redFlags":["Features of systemic amyloidosis such as macroglossia, cardiac or renal involvement or a monoclonal paraprotein","Nodular lesions with a demonstrable clonal plasma cell population or paraproteinaemia","Rapidly changing or ulcerating nodules suggesting an alternative neoplasm","Associated symptoms suggesting multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A"],"prognosis":"Primary cutaneous amyloidosis is benign and does not itself shorten life, but the keratinic forms tend to be chronic and troublesome because of persistent pruritus. Nodular amyloidosis is usually localised yet requires surveillance, as a minority progress to systemic amyloidosis.","pitfalls":["Assuming all cutaneous amyloid is benign and failing to investigate and monitor the nodular form for systemic progression","Not recognising that persistent scratching and friction drive and perpetuate the keratinic forms","Missing an associated systemic amyloidosis when cutaneous deposits are in fact secondary","Overlooking the association of lichen and macular amyloidosis with multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A","Attributing interscapular pigmentation to post-inflammatory change without considering macular amyloidosis"],"management":["Break the itch-scratch cycle with topical or intralesional corticosteroids and antipruritics including topical agents and antihistamines","Use emollients and advise avoidance of friction from towels and brushes that perpetuate the keratinic forms","Consider phototherapy with ultraviolet B, or topical or systemic retinoids, for refractory lichen and macular disease","Manage nodular amyloidosis with surgical excision or laser and maintain long-term surveillance for progression to systemic amyloidosis using protein electrophoresis and free light chains","Screen for multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A when the family history or features are suggestive","Offer genetic counselling in familial disease associated with OSMR and IL31RA variants","Provide ongoing dermatology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Weedon's Skin Pathology"],"crossLinks":["colloid_milium","cutaneous_calcinosis"]},"primary_cutaneous_anaplastic_large_cell_lymphoma":{"name":"Primary cutaneous anaplastic large-cell lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pcALCL","primary cutaneous CD30+ anaplastic large-cell lymphoma","cutaneous ALCL","primary cutaneous CD30+ lymphoproliferative disorder","ALK-negative CD30+ cutaneous lymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents typically as a solitary or localised, often ulcerating, skin nodule or tumour in an older adult, without evidence of systemic lymphoma at diagnosis","Part of the primary cutaneous CD30-positive lymphoproliferative spectrum, sharing a border with lymphomatoid papulosis","By definition, more than three-quarters of the large tumour cells express CD30","Unlike systemic anaplastic large-cell lymphoma, the cutaneous form is usually ALK-negative and lacks the associated ALK translocation, and follows a much more indolent course","Lesions may partially regress spontaneously, and multifocal skin disease can occur without implying systemic involvement","Staging is essential to exclude systemic anaplastic large-cell lymphoma secondarily involving the skin, which carries a different prognosis and treatment","Localised disease is often managed with radiotherapy or surgical excision, reserving systemic therapy for extensive or extracutaneous disease"],"pathophysiology":"Primary cutaneous anaplastic large-cell lymphoma is a T-cell neoplasm composed of large cells with anaplastic, pleomorphic, or immunoblastic morphology, the majority strongly expressing the CD30 activation antigen. It arises within and is largely confined to the skin, and in contrast to systemic anaplastic large-cell lymphoma it usually lacks ALK gene rearrangement. The tumour sits at the aggressive end of the cutaneous CD30-positive lymphoproliferative spectrum yet behaves indolently, with a tendency to remain localised and occasionally to regress, reflecting relative immune containment. Regional lymph node spread can occur but distant dissemination is uncommon.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphomatoid papulosis (especially type C)","Systemic anaplastic large-cell lymphoma with cutaneous involvement","Transformed mycosis fungoides","Other CD30-positive T-cell lymphomas in the skin","Cutaneous Hodgkin lymphoma involvement","Poorly differentiated carcinoma or melanoma mimicking a skin tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with immunohistochemistry demonstrating CD30 positivity in the majority of large cells","ALK immunostaining and, where indicated, testing for ALK rearrangement","T-cell receptor clonality studies","Staging imaging (CT or PET-CT) to exclude systemic anaplastic large-cell lymphoma","Bone marrow examination when systemic disease is suspected","Clinicopathological correlation to separate from lymphomatoid papulosis"],"redFlags":["Extracutaneous or nodal disease indicating possible systemic anaplastic large-cell lymphoma","Rapidly progressive or widely disseminated lesions","Systemic B-symptoms suggesting more aggressive disease","Failure of a presumed benign lesion to regress, prompting biopsy and staging"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally very good with high survival for skin-limited disease, though extensive multifocal cutaneous disease, leg involvement, or extracutaneous spread can worsen outcome.","pitfalls":["Failing to stage and thereby missing systemic anaplastic large-cell lymphoma involving the skin","Confusing the entity with lymphomatoid papulosis without clinicopathological correlation","Overtreating indolent, localised disease with aggressive systemic chemotherapy","Assuming ALK positivity, which is characteristic of systemic rather than primary cutaneous disease"],"management":["Treat solitary or localised skin-limited disease with surgical excision and/or local radiotherapy as first-line therapy","Observe lesions that show spontaneous regression before escalating treatment","Manage multifocal skin disease with low-dose methotrexate or a systemic retinoid such as bexarotene","Reserve brentuximab vedotin, targeting CD30, for extensive, refractory, or extracutaneous disease","Stage the patient to exclude systemic anaplastic large-cell lymphoma secondarily involving the skin","Avoid aggressive multiagent chemotherapy for skin-limited disease given its indolent course","Maintain long-term dermatological and haematological surveillance for relapse","Manage within a cutaneous lymphoma multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","Swerdlow SH et al. WHO monograph on lymphoid neoplasms"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_cutaneous_gamma_delta_t_cell_lymphoma":{"name":"Primary cutaneous gamma-delta T-cell lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCGD-TCL","cutaneous gamma/delta T-cell lymphoma","gamma-delta T-cell lymphoma of skin","TCR gamma-delta positive cutaneous lymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare and aggressive cutaneous lymphoma derived from mature, cytotoxic gamma-delta T cells rather than the usual alpha-beta T cells","Presents with disseminated plaques, nodules or tumours, often on the limbs, that frequently ulcerate and may involve subcutaneous fat","Three histological patterns are recognised: epidermotropic, dermal and subcutaneous, and the subcutaneous form can mimic panniculitis clinically","The neoplastic cells express T-cell-receptor gamma-delta, are usually CD3-positive, CD4-negative and CD8-negative, and express cytotoxic markers with frequent CD56 positivity","It carries a high risk of haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, particularly with subcutaneous involvement, which markedly worsens outcome","Distinction from the more indolent subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma, which is of alpha-beta origin, is prognostically critical","Management uses systemic multi-agent chemotherapy with consideration of allogeneic HSCT, as the disease responds poorly to skin-directed therapy alone"],"pathophysiology":"Primary cutaneous gamma-delta T-cell lymphoma arises from a clonal proliferation of activated cytotoxic gamma-delta T lymphocytes, a normally minor T-cell subset that populates epithelial and mucosal surfaces. Malignant transformation produces cells that retain a strong cytotoxic phenotype, expressing granule-associated proteins and often CD56, and that infiltrate the epidermis, dermis and subcutis in varying combinations. The cytotoxic activity and associated cytokine release contribute both to the destructive, ulcerating skin lesions and to the frequent triggering of haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis. The disease is typically clinically aggressive with rapid progression and a tendency to disseminate to mucosal and other extranodal sites while often sparing lymph nodes.","additionalDifferentials":["Subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma (alpha-beta type)","Mycosis fungoides and other epidermotropic cutaneous T-cell lymphomas","Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type, involving skin","Primary cutaneous CD8-positive aggressive epidermotropic cytotoxic T-cell lymphoma","Lupus panniculitis and other inflammatory panniculitides","Cutaneous involvement by systemic T-cell lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with assessment of epidermotropic, dermal and subcutaneous involvement","Immunohistochemistry showing TCR gamma-delta expression with a cytotoxic CD4-negative CD8-negative phenotype and frequent CD56","T-cell receptor clonality studies","Markers of haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis including ferritin, triglycerides and fibrinogen, and bone marrow examination if suspected","PET-CT and staging to detect extracutaneous and mucosal disease","Full blood count and organ-function testing before systemic therapy"],"redFlags":["Features of haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis such as high fever, cytopenias and very high ferritin","Rapidly progressive or ulcerating skin tumours","Systemic symptoms with mucosal or visceral dissemination","Cytopenias suggesting marrow involvement or haemophagocytosis"],"prognosis":"The disease is generally aggressive with poor survival, and outcomes are worse in the subcutaneous form and when complicated by haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis; durable remission is uncommon and allogeneic HSCT offers the best prospect in selected responders.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the subcutaneous form as benign panniculitis or as the more indolent alpha-beta panniculitis-like lymphoma","Failing to determine the gamma-delta T-cell-receptor phenotype, which defines the entity","Overlooking developing haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","Relying on skin-directed therapy for what is a systemic aggressive disease","Underestimating disease extent by omitting systemic staging"],"management":["Treat with systemic multiagent chemotherapy such as CHOP-like or etoposide-containing regimens given the aggressive course","Offer allogeneic haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation to fit responders as the best prospect for durable remission","Recognise and treat haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis promptly with directed immunosuppressive therapy","Use radiotherapy for localised or symptomatic ulcerating lesions","Provide supportive care with infection prophylaxis, transfusion, and wound management of ulcerating skin","Stage with PET-CT and bone marrow examination to define extracutaneous extent","Distinguish from the more indolent subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma to guide treatment intensity","Manage within a haemato-oncology multidisciplinary team and consider clinical-trial enrolment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","British Association of Dermatologists and haemato-oncology guidance on cutaneous lymphomas"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_effusion_lymphoma":{"name":"Primary effusion lymphoma (HHV-8)","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PEL","body cavity-based lymphoma","HHV-8-associated effusion lymphoma","KSHV-associated lymphoma","HHV-8+/EBV-variable large B-cell lymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents characteristically as a malignant effusion in the pleural, pericardial, or peritoneal cavity without a discrete tumour mass","Universally associated with human herpesvirus 8 (Kaposi sarcoma-associated herpesvirus), and frequently co-infected with Epstein-Barr virus","Occurs predominantly in the context of immunodeficiency, especially advanced HIV infection, and may coexist with Kaposi sarcoma or multicentric Castleman disease","Malignant cells have a large, anaplastic or immunoblastic/plasmablastic appearance and a distinctive null or aberrant immunophenotype","Cells typically lack pan-B-cell markers such as CD20 but express activation and plasma-cell-associated markers like CD30, CD38, and CD138, with HHV-8 latent nuclear antigen detectable in the nuclei","A rare extracavitary/solid variant can form masses in lymph nodes or extranodal sites while retaining the same viral and immunophenotypic features","Diagnosis rests on cytology of the effusion combined with HHV-8 demonstration"],"pathophysiology":"Primary effusion lymphoma is a large B-cell neoplasm driven by human herpesvirus 8, which encodes latent proteins that dysregulate the cell cycle, inhibit apoptosis, and promote proliferation of infected B-cells. Most tumours also harbour Epstein-Barr virus, which is thought to cooperate in transformation. The malignant cells have undergone extensive plasmacytic differentiation, explaining loss of typical B-cell antigens and gain of plasma-cell markers. They proliferate preferentially within serous body cavities, producing lymphomatous effusions rather than solid masses, and the underlying immunodeficiency permits unchecked viral-driven expansion.","additionalDifferentials":["Reactive or infective effusions in immunocompromised patients","Pyothorax-associated (diffuse large B-cell) lymphoma","Plasmablastic lymphoma involving serous cavities","Malignant effusion from carcinoma or mesothelioma","Burkitt lymphoma with effusion","HHV-8-associated multicentric Castleman disease with effusions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cytological examination of effusion fluid with cell block preparation","Immunophenotyping showing loss of CD20 with expression of CD30, CD38, CD138","Detection of HHV-8 latent nuclear antigen by immunohistochemistry and HHV-8 by molecular methods","In situ hybridisation for EBV-encoded RNA","HIV testing and assessment of immune status","Cross-sectional imaging to identify effusions, exclude solid masses, and detect Kaposi sarcoma or Castleman disease"],"redFlags":["Large pericardial effusion causing tamponade physiology","Respiratory compromise from a massive pleural effusion","Rapid reaccumulation of effusion after drainage","Concurrent opportunistic infection in the immunodeficient host"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally poor with short median survival, though outcomes improve with combination chemotherapy alongside effective antiretroviral therapy and control of the underlying immunodeficiency.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a malignant effusion as reactive because no mass is seen on imaging","Missing the diagnosis by not testing effusion cells for HHV-8","Being misled by loss of CD20 into excluding a B-cell lymphoma","Neglecting optimisation of antiretroviral therapy and immune reconstitution"],"management":["Give combination chemotherapy such as CHOP or infusional EPOCH as the mainstay of treatment","Initiate or optimise antiretroviral therapy in HIV-associated disease to restore immune control","Drain malignant pleural, pericardial, or peritoneal effusions for symptom relief","Assess for and treat coexisting HHV-8 disease such as Kaposi sarcoma or multicentric Castleman disease","Provide supportive care with infection prophylaxis, tumour-lysis prevention, and transfusion support","Monitor immune status and manage the underlying immunodeficiency","Consider clinical-trial enrolment given the generally poor prognosis","Manage within a haemato-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","Swerdlow SH et al. WHO monograph on lymphoid neoplasms"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_exercise_headache":{"name":"Primary exercise headache","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["primary exertional headache","benign exertional headache"],"chapter":null,"source":"International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pulsating headache brought on specifically by and occurring only during or after sustained strenuous exercise","Attacks last from minutes up to a couple of days and are often bilateral","It is more common in hot weather and at high altitude and tends to affect younger adults","On first presentation, subarachnoid haemorrhage and arterial dissection must be excluded","Cardiac cephalalgia, in which exertional headache reflects myocardial ischaemia, is an important mimic in those with vascular risk factors","Many patients respond to indometacin or to a warm-up before exercise"],"pathophysiology":"Primary exercise headache is thought to result from exertion-related venous or arterial distension and transient changes in intracranial pressure, without a structural lesion in the primary form. Sustained physical effort raising intrathoracic and intracranial pressure is the trigger, distinct from the brief Valsalva of cough headache. Because the same exertional stress can precipitate serious secondary events, vascular and cardiac causes must be excluded initially.","additionalDifferentials":["Subarachnoid haemorrhage","Cervical or intracranial arterial dissection","Cardiac cephalalgia (exertional angina-equivalent headache)","Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome","Primary cough headache","Phaeochromocytoma-related headache"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain imaging and vascular imaging on first presentation to exclude haemorrhage and dissection","Cardiac assessment where vascular risk factors suggest cardiac cephalalgia","History distinguishing sustained exertion from Valsalva triggers","Consideration of RCVS workup if recurrent thunderclap features"],"redFlags":["Thunderclap onset suggesting subarachnoid haemorrhage","Neck pain or focal deficit suggesting dissection","Exertional headache relieved by rest in a patient with cardiac risk factors","Abnormal neurological or cardiovascular examination"],"prognosis":"The primary form is benign and frequently self-limiting over months to a few years, often manageable with pre-exercise prophylaxis; prognosis of secondary causes depends on the specific diagnosis.","pitfalls":["Attributing a first exertional thunderclap headache to a benign cause without excluding haemorrhage","Missing cardiac cephalalgia in an older patient","Confusing it with primary cough headache","Failing to reconsider the diagnosis if the pattern changes"],"management":["Offer indometacin as first-line treatment, either symptomatically or as pre-exercise prophylaxis","Advise a graded warm-up before exertion and moderation of intensity, particularly in heat and at altitude","Exclude subarachnoid haemorrhage and arterial dissection with brain and vascular imaging at first presentation","Exclude cardiac cephalalgia with cardiac assessment in patients with vascular risk factors","Consider alternative prophylaxis such as a beta-blocker if indometacin is unsuitable","Reassure about the benign, usually self-limiting course and refer to neurology if features are atypical"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3)","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_headache_associated_with_sexual_activity":{"name":"Primary headache associated with sexual activity","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["orgasmic headache","pre-orgasmic headache","coital cephalalgia"],"chapter":null,"source":"International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Headache precipitated by sexual activity, occurring either as a dull bilateral ache that builds with arousal or as an explosive headache at orgasm","The explosive orgasmic form can be indistinguishable from the thunderclap headache of subarachnoid haemorrhage on first presentation","A first or severe episode therefore mandates exclusion of subarachnoid haemorrhage and other vascular causes","Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome is an important cause of recurrent orgasmic thunderclap headache","It is more common in men and in people who also have migraine","The primary form is often self-limiting but may recur in bouts and can respond to preventive treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Primary headache associated with sexual activity is linked to the abrupt rises in blood pressure, heart rate and muscular and intracranial pressure that accompany sexual excitement and orgasm, in the absence of a structural lesion. The explosive subtype coincides with the autonomic surge at orgasm. Because the same haemodynamic stress can precipitate aneurysmal rupture or reversible vasoconstriction, secondary causes must be excluded before the primary diagnosis is accepted.","additionalDifferentials":["Subarachnoid haemorrhage","Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome","Cervical or intracranial arterial dissection","Primary exercise headache","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Phaeochromocytoma-related headache"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent CT of the head and, if non-diagnostic, lumbar puncture for the first severe episode","CT or MR angiography and venography for vascular causes","MRI of the brain where indicated","Repeat vascular imaging if reversible cerebral vasoconstriction is suspected"],"redFlags":["First explosive orgasmic headache (treat as possible subarachnoid haemorrhage)","Focal neurological deficit, seizures or reduced consciousness","Recurrent thunderclap episodes suggesting vasoconstriction syndrome","Neck pain suggesting dissection"],"prognosis":"Once secondary causes are excluded the primary form is benign and frequently remits over weeks to months, though it may recur; preventive treatment can help during symptomatic bouts.","pitfalls":["Assuming a benign cause for a first explosive orgasmic headache without imaging","Failing to consider reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome in recurrent cases","Overlooking arterial dissection when there is neck pain","Not re-evaluating if the headache pattern changes"],"management":["Preventive treatment during symptomatic bouts with a beta-blocker such as propranolol to suppress the exertional headache","On-demand prophylaxis with indometacin or a triptan taken shortly before sexual activity to abort the orgasmic headache","Behavioural advice to reduce exertion, adopt a more passive role and temporarily abstain during a symptomatic cluster","Optimise cardiovascular risk factors and control any coexisting hypertension that amplifies the orgasmic pressure surge","Mandatory exclusion of subarachnoid haemorrhage and other vascular causes on the first or a severe episode before the primary label is accepted","Actively investigate for reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome in recurrent orgasmic thunderclap attacks, with a calcium channel blocker such as nimodipine if confirmed","Reassure regarding the benign, frequently self-remitting course, with neurology referral if attacks are refractory or the picture is atypical"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3)","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_hepatic_amyloidosis":{"name":"Primary hepatic amyloidosis","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AL amyloidosis with hepatic involvement","hepatic amyloid infiltration","amyloid liver disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AASLD","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hepatic involvement usually reflects systemic AL (light-chain) amyloidosis, so the liver is rarely affected in isolation and a plasma cell dyscrasia should be sought","Marked hepatomegaly that is out of proportion to only mild liver enzyme changes is a characteristic clue","A disproportionately raised alkaline phosphatase, often with relatively preserved aminotransferases and bilirubin, is a typical biochemical pattern","Coexisting cardiac, renal, or autonomic amyloid and features such as macroglossia or periorbital purpura point toward a systemic process","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating amyloid deposits, classically apple-green birefringence under polarised light after Congo red staining, with typing to confirm the fibril protein","Serum free light-chain assay and serum/urine immunofixation help identify the underlying clonal disorder in AL disease","Percutaneous liver biopsy carries an increased bleeding risk because vascular amyloid infiltration makes the organ friable, so this should be weighed against biopsy of a safer site such as abdominal fat or bone marrow","Treatment is directed at the underlying clone or precursor protein rather than at the liver itself, and outcomes depend heavily on the degree of cardiac involvement"],"pathophysiology":"Amyloidosis results from extracellular deposition of misfolded protein fibrils that adopt a beta-pleated sheet configuration. In hepatic disease the most common type is AL amyloid, in which monoclonal immunoglobulin light chains produced by a plasma cell clone form the fibrils; AA (serum amyloid A) amyloid from chronic inflammation and hereditary transthyretin-related forms can also involve the liver. Fibrils accumulate progressively in the space of Disse and around sinusoids and vessels, compressing hepatocytes and distorting architecture. This produces organomegaly and a predominantly cholestatic biochemical picture, while synthetic function and portal pressure are relatively spared until late; vascular fragility from perivascular deposition underlies the bleeding tendency.","additionalDifferentials":["Infiltrative malignancy such as lymphoma or metastatic disease","Fatty liver with hepatomegaly","Congestive hepatopathy from right heart failure","Glycogen or lipid storage disorders","Sarcoidosis and other granulomatous infiltration","Extrahepatic biliary obstruction causing a cholestatic pattern"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum free light-chain ratio with serum and urine immunofixation to detect a monoclonal protein","Congo red staining of tissue with confirmatory typing (e.g. mass spectrometry or immunohistochemistry)","Assessment of extrahepatic organ involvement, including cardiac evaluation with biomarkers and echocardiography","Abdominal fat pad or bone marrow sampling as a lower-risk alternative to liver biopsy","Bone marrow examination to characterise the plasma cell clone","Cross-sectional imaging to document hepatomegaly and exclude focal lesions"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive hepatomegaly with jaundice suggesting advanced infiltration","Signs of concurrent cardiac amyloid such as heart failure or arrhythmia, which dominate prognosis","Spontaneous bruising or bleeding indicating vascular fragility or acquired coagulation defects","Deep jaundice, which is associated with a poor short-term outcome"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally poor and is driven mainly by cardiac and renal involvement rather than by the liver; the appearance of significant jaundice signals advanced disease and short survival, although clone-directed therapy can improve outcomes in selected patients.","pitfalls":["Attributing an isolated raised alkaline phosphatase and hepatomegaly to fatty liver without considering infiltration","Proceeding to percutaneous liver biopsy without recognising the elevated bleeding risk","Failing to type the amyloid, since management differs between AL, AA, and hereditary forms","Overlooking cardiac amyloid, which is the main determinant of survival"],"management":["Clone-directed chemotherapy for the underlying AL amyloidosis, typically a bortezomib-based proteasome-inhibitor regimen combined with an anti-CD38 antibody such as daratumumab and a corticosteroid","Autologous haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in carefully selected patients with adequate organ reserve","For AA amyloid, suppression of the underlying chronic inflammatory disorder; for hereditary transthyretin disease, a TTR stabiliser or gene-silencing therapy","Supportive care of cholestasis and malnutrition with nutritional support and fat-soluble vitamin replacement, and correction of the amyloid-related bleeding tendency including acquired factor X deficiency","Baseline and serial assessment of cardiac and renal involvement that drive prognosis, alongside serum free light-chain monitoring to gauge haematological response","Careful management of hepatic decompensation and haemorrhage during acute deterioration, avoiding invasive procedures where perivascular deposition causes vascular fragility","Multidisciplinary care spanning haematology, hepatology and cardiology with referral to a specialist amyloidosis centre","Genetic counselling and family screening where a hereditary transthyretin form is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AASLD guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_hyperhidrosis":{"name":"Primary focal hyperhidrosis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["primary hyperhidrosis","focal hyperhidrosis","essential hyperhidrosis","idiopathic hyperhidrosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by excessive eccrine sweating beyond physiological needs, focal and symmetrical, most often affecting the palms, soles, axillae and craniofacial skin","Onset is usually in childhood or adolescence, and there is frequently a positive family history","By definition the excess sweating stops during sleep, a key feature distinguishing it from secondary generalised hyperhidrosis","It can cause considerable social, occupational and psychological impairment despite being a benign condition","The eccrine glands are structurally normal, and the problem lies in their neural overstimulation rather than in gland pathology","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by criteria such as focal symmetrical sweating for at least six months, early onset, family history and cessation during sleep","Complications include maceration, secondary bacterial and fungal infection, pitted keratolysis and contact dermatitis","Treatment follows a stepwise approach with topical antiperspirants (aluminium salts), iontophoresis for palms and soles, botulinum toxin injections for focal areas, and systemic anticholinergics or surgery in resistant cases"],"pathophysiology":"Primary focal hyperhidrosis is thought to result from an exaggerated or dysregulated sympathetic response producing excessive cholinergic stimulation of structurally normal eccrine sweat glands. The eccrine glands are innervated by sympathetic fibres that release acetylcholine, and in affected individuals these glands are overstimulated out of proportion to thermoregulatory need, particularly at sites of high eccrine density such as the palms, soles and axillae. The frequent family history suggests a genetic predisposition, and emotional and thermal stimuli often exacerbate the sweating. The cessation of symptoms during sleep reflects the withdrawal of the emotional and cortical drive to sympathetic outflow.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary generalised hyperhidrosis from systemic disease (thyrotoxicosis, diabetes, phaeochromocytoma)","Menopausal or hormonally driven sweating and flushing","Drug-induced or withdrawal-related sweating","Infection-related or malignancy-related night sweats","Gustatory sweating (Frey syndrome)","Anxiety and other psychiatric causes of sweating"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment against diagnostic criteria including symmetry, early onset and cessation during sleep","Focused history and examination to exclude features of secondary hyperhidrosis","Thyroid function tests and glucose when a systemic cause is suspected","Gravimetric or starch-iodine testing to map and quantify affected areas when required","Assessment for secondary skin infection such as pitted keratolysis or dermatophytosis","Screening for phaeochromocytoma or other endocrine causes if the pattern is generalised or nocturnal"],"redFlags":["Generalised, asymmetrical or night-time sweating suggesting a secondary systemic cause","Associated weight loss, fever or lymphadenopathy pointing to infection or malignancy","Unilateral sweating raising concern about underlying neurological or thoracic pathology","Sudden onset in adulthood without a lifelong history"],"prognosis":"Primary focal hyperhidrosis is a benign, chronic condition that often persists for many years but frequently improves with age; quality of life can be substantially restored with appropriate stepwise treatment, and it carries no threat to general health.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish primary focal from secondary generalised hyperhidrosis and missing an underlying systemic cause","Underestimating the psychosocial burden of a benign condition","Escalating to invasive treatment before trialling topical antiperspirants and iontophoresis","Overlooking secondary skin infection such as pitted keratolysis"],"management":["Stepwise first-line topical aluminium chloride hexahydrate antiperspirant applied to the affected sites","Tap-water iontophoresis for palmar and plantar disease","Intradermal botulinum toxin injections for axillary or palmar hyperhidrosis unresponsive to topical measures","Topical or oral anticholinergic agents such as glycopyrronium or oxybutynin for craniofacial or more widespread sweating, counselling on dry mouth and other antimuscarinic effects","Energy-based axillary treatment such as microwave thermolysis or local gland excision, reserving endoscopic thoracic sympathectomy for severe refractory palmar disease with explicit warning about compensatory sweating","Treat coexisting skin complications such as pitted keratolysis or dermatophyte infection","Address the psychological, social and occupational impact and reassure regarding the benign nature","Reassess and exclude a secondary cause if sweating becomes generalised, nocturnal or of late onset"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","British Association of Dermatologists guidance on hyperhidrosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_hyperoxaluria":{"name":"Primary hyperoxaluria","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PH","hereditary oxalosis","primary oxalosis","AGT deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Primary hyperoxaluria is a group of inherited disorders of glyoxylate metabolism that cause marked overproduction of oxalate by the liver","It typically presents with recurrent calcium oxalate kidney stones and nephrocalcinosis, often from childhood, and can progress to renal failure","Type 1, from deficiency of the hepatic enzyme alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase, is the most common and generally the most severe form","As renal function declines, oxalate can no longer be cleared and deposits throughout the body (systemic oxalosis), affecting bone, heart, retina, and vessels","The diagnosis should be considered in any patient with recurrent oxalate stones, especially with early onset, a family history, or nephrocalcinosis","Elevated urinary oxalate, genetic testing, and sometimes liver biopsy establish the diagnosis, and high fluid intake with crystallisation inhibitors is a supportive measure","A subset of type 1 disease responds biochemically to pyridoxine, and definitive treatment historically involved combined or sequential liver and kidney transplantation, with newer RNA-targeted therapies now available"],"pathophysiology":"Primary hyperoxaluria results from autosomal recessive defects in enzymes of hepatic glyoxylate detoxification. In type 1, deficiency of the peroxisomal enzyme alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase allows glyoxylate to be oxidised to oxalate; types 2 and 3 involve other enzymes in the same pathway. The liver overproduces oxalate, which the kidneys excrete in high amounts, promoting calcium oxalate crystallisation, stone formation, and nephrocalcinosis. Because oxalate is poorly cleared once renal function fails, it accumulates systemically and is deposited in multiple tissues as oxalosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Enteric (secondary) hyperoxaluria from fat malabsorption","Dietary oxalate overload","Idiopathic recurrent calcium oxalate stone disease","Other hereditary stone-forming disorders such as cystinuria","Distal renal tubular acidosis with nephrocalcinosis","Medullary sponge kidney"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["24-hour urinary oxalate measurement","Plasma oxalate, particularly as renal function declines","Stone analysis confirming calcium oxalate composition","Renal imaging for stones and nephrocalcinosis","Genetic testing for the causative enzyme defect","Liver enzyme assay on biopsy in selected diagnostic uncertainty","Assessment for systemic oxalosis when renal failure is present"],"redFlags":["Progressive decline in renal function toward end-stage disease","Systemic oxalosis with cardiac, skeletal, retinal, or vascular involvement","Recurrent obstructing stones with infection or acute kidney injury","Early-onset stone disease with nephrocalcinosis suggesting a severe genotype"],"prognosis":"The outlook varies with type and genotype; type 1 disease can progress to end-stage renal failure and life-threatening systemic oxalosis, whereas some pyridoxine-responsive and non-type-1 forms are milder, and early diagnosis with modern therapies has improved outcomes considerably.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent stones symptomatically without recognising an underlying inherited metabolic cause","Confusing primary with enteric hyperoxaluria, which have different mechanisms and management","Overlooking systemic oxalosis as renal function falls","Performing isolated kidney transplantation in type 1 disease without addressing continued hepatic oxalate overproduction"],"management":["Vigorous maintenance hyperhydration to lower urinary calcium oxalate supersaturation","Crystallisation inhibitors such as potassium citrate, orthophosphate and magnesium to reduce stone formation","Supervised pyridoxine (vitamin B6) trial, which is effective in a subset of type 1 disease","RNA-interference therapy directed at hepatic oxalate production, such as lumasiran, in type 1 disease","Stone clearance by lithotripsy or endourological removal, minimising nephron-damaging interventions","Monitoring of renal function and of urinary and plasma oxalate, with surveillance for systemic oxalosis affecting bone, heart, retina and vessels once renal function declines","Intensive dialysis and combined or sequential liver-kidney transplantation for type 1 disease progressing to end-stage renal failure","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_hyperoxaluria_type_1":{"name":"Primary Hyperoxaluria Type 1","system":"Renal / Hepatic metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PH1","AGT deficiency","Oxalosis type 1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most common and most severe of the primary hyperoxalurias, caused by biallelic AGXT mutations that reduce hepatic peroxisomal alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase (AGT) activity.","Oxalate overproduction drives recurrent calcium oxalate urolithiasis, nephrocalcinosis and progressive chronic kidney disease, often reaching end-stage renal failure.","Biochemical signature is markedly raised urinary oxalate together with raised urinary glycolate, distinguishing it from type 2 (L-glycerate) and type 3 (4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate).","A subset, especially those carrying the Gly170Arg variant, are pyridoxine (vitamin B6) responsive with reduced oxalate output, so a supervised trial is worthwhile in all patients.","A distinctive molecular lesion is mistargeting of AGT from the peroxisome into mitochondria, where the otherwise competent enzyme is metabolically ineffective.","Once renal function declines, plasma oxalate rises and systemic oxalosis develops with deposition in bone, myocardium, conduction system, retina, skin, vessels and nerves.","Because the enzyme defect is liver-specific, definitive correction needs the liver; combined or sequential liver-kidney transplantation is considered in advanced disease.","Hepatic RNA interference therapy that lowers glycolate oxidase reduces oxalate precursor supply and has transformed medical management."],"pathophysiology":"Primary hyperoxaluria type 1 is an autosomal recessive inborn error of glyoxylate metabolism caused by deficient activity of the liver-specific peroxisomal enzyme alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase (AGT), encoded by AGXT and dependent on pyridoxal phosphate as cofactor. AGT normally transaminates glyoxylate to glycine within the peroxisome; when it is deficient, glyoxylate escapes and is oxidised by lactate dehydrogenase to oxalate, a metabolic end-product that humans cannot degrade. The resulting oxalate load is excreted by the kidney, where supersaturation drives calcium oxalate crystallisation, causing recurrent stones, tubulointerstitial injury and nephrocalcinosis. A characteristic molecular abnormality, particularly with the Gly170Arg allele, is mistargeting of catalytically capable AGT away from the peroxisome into mitochondria, where it cannot perform its function. As glomerular filtration falls, plasma oxalate rises and calcium oxalate is deposited throughout the body, including bone, myocardium, the cardiac conduction system, arterial walls, retina, skin and peripheral nerves, producing the multisystem picture of systemic oxalosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperoxaluria types 2 and 3","Enteric (secondary) hyperoxaluria from fat malabsorption, short bowel or bariatric surgery","Dietary hyperoxaluria from excessive oxalate or precursor intake","Idiopathic calcium oxalate stone disease with hypercalciuria or hypocitraturia","Dent disease and other hereditary tubulopathies causing nephrocalcinosis","Cystinuria and other monogenic stone-forming disorders","Prolonged pyridoxine deficiency states"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinary oxalate and glycolate quantification, age-adjusted and corrected for creatinine in young children","Plasma oxalate measurement, especially as renal function declines","Renal tract ultrasound and imaging for stones and nephrocalcinosis","Molecular genetic testing of AGXT as confirmatory diagnosis, often avoiding biopsy","Supervised assessment of pyridoxine responsiveness","Echocardiography, ophthalmological review and bone assessment when systemic oxalosis is suspected","Historically, liver biopsy for AGT enzyme activity and subcellular localisation"],"redFlags":["Nephrocalcinosis or recurrent stones in infancy or early childhood with failure to thrive and rapidly falling renal function (infantile oxalosis)","Rising plasma oxalate as GFR declines, heralding systemic oxalosis","New conduction abnormality, cardiomyopathy or arrhythmia suggesting myocardial oxalate deposition","Visual disturbance from oxalate retinopathy","Rapid graft loss after isolated kidney transplantation due to recurrent oxalate deposition"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on genotype, pyridoxine responsiveness and how early treatment begins; untreated disease commonly progresses to end-stage renal failure and systemic oxalosis. Early diagnosis, hyperhydration, crystallisation inhibitors, pyridoxine where effective and modern RNA interference therapy have substantially improved renal and overall outcomes.","pitfalls":["Attributing recurrent childhood stones to common idiopathic stone disease and missing the diagnosis until renal failure","Performing isolated kidney transplantation without addressing the hepatic enzyme defect, leading to rapid oxalate recurrence in the graft","Failing to use intensive fluid and dialysis strategies around transplantation to clear the systemic oxalate burden","Not trialling pyridoxine and thereby missing a responsive subgroup","Interpreting urinary oxalate without correcting for age, body size or renal function"],"management":["RNA-interference therapy such as lumasiran to suppress hepatic oxalate overproduction as the disease-modifying mainstay","Supervised pyridoxine trial, particularly with the Gly170Arg allele, continued long term if urinary oxalate output falls","Aggressive maintenance hyperhydration combined with crystallisation inhibitors such as potassium citrate, orthophosphate and magnesium","Stone clearance and relief of urinary obstruction using nephron-sparing endourological techniques","Intensified dialysis regimens to clear the oxalate load in advanced renal failure, since conventional dialysis is inadequate","Combined or sequential liver-kidney transplantation to correct the hepatic enzyme defect and replace the failed kidneys","Surveillance for systemic oxalosis with echocardiography, ophthalmological review and bone assessment, alongside monitoring of plasma and urinary oxalate and renal function","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive AGXT inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inborn errors of metabolism reference texts","Paediatric and adult nephrology reference texts","International guidelines on primary hyperoxaluria"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_hyperoxaluria_type_2":{"name":"Primary Hyperoxaluria Type 2","system":"Renal / Hepatic metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PH2","GRHPR deficiency","L-glyceric aciduria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic GRHPR mutations reducing glyoxylate reductase/hydroxypyruvate reductase activity.","Unlike the liver-restricted enzyme of type 1, GRHPR is a widely expressed, mainly cytosolic enzyme, which has implications for transplantation strategy.","Biochemical hallmark is raised urinary oxalate together with raised L-glycerate (L-glyceric aciduria), the feature that separates it from types 1 and 3.","Course is generally milder than type 1, though recurrent calcium oxalate stones and progression to chronic kidney disease, and occasionally systemic oxalosis, still occur.","It is not pyridoxine responsive, since B6 responsiveness is a feature of type 1 rather than type 2.","Because the deficient enzyme is not confined to the liver, the rationale for liver transplantation is weaker than in type 1.","Management centres on high fluid intake and crystallisation inhibitors, with isolated kidney transplantation considered if renal failure supervenes."],"pathophysiology":"Primary hyperoxaluria type 2 is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by deficiency of glyoxylate reductase/hydroxypyruvate reductase (GRHPR), encoded by GRHPR. Unlike the liver-peroxisomal enzyme of type 1, GRHPR is a widely expressed, predominantly cytosolic enzyme that reduces glyoxylate to glycolate and hydroxypyruvate to D-glycerate. When it is deficient, glyoxylate accumulates and is oxidised by lactate dehydrogenase to oxalate, while hydroxypyruvate is instead reduced by lactate dehydrogenase to L-glycerate, producing the characteristic combined excretion of oxalate and L-glycerate. Oxalate overproduction promotes urinary calcium oxalate supersaturation with recurrent stones, nephrocalcinosis and, in a proportion of patients, progressive kidney disease. Because GRHPR is not restricted to the liver, systemic oxalosis tends to appear later and less frequently than in type 1, and the widespread tissue expression of the enzyme complicates the rationale for hepatic replacement.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperoxaluria types 1 and 3","Enteric hyperoxaluria from malabsorption, short bowel or bariatric surgery","Dietary oxalate excess","Idiopathic recurrent calcium oxalate stone disease","Other hereditary causes of nephrocalcinosis such as Dent disease or distal renal tubular acidosis","Cystinuria and other monogenic stone disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinary oxalate and L-glycerate quantification","Plasma oxalate measurement as renal function declines","Renal tract imaging for stones and nephrocalcinosis","Molecular genetic testing of GRHPR","Stone composition analysis confirming calcium oxalate","Assessment for systemic oxalosis when renal impairment is advanced"],"redFlags":["Progressive decline in renal function despite conservative measures","Rising plasma oxalate suggesting evolving systemic oxalosis","Recurrent obstructing stones causing acute kidney injury","Early-onset nephrocalcinosis in childhood"],"prognosis":"Type 2 generally carries a more favourable renal prognosis than type 1, but a significant minority still develop chronic kidney disease and occasionally systemic oxalosis. Sustained hyperhydration, crystallisation inhibitors and metabolic monitoring help preserve renal function.","pitfalls":["Assuming type 2 is always benign and under-monitoring renal function","Offering a pyridoxine trial expecting the response seen in type 1","Proceeding to liver transplantation on the type 1 model without recognising that the enzyme is not liver-restricted","Missing the diagnosis by measuring only urinary oxalate and not L-glycerate"],"management":["Sustained maintenance hyperhydration to lower urinary calcium oxalate supersaturation","Crystallisation inhibitors such as potassium citrate, magnesium and orthophosphate to inhibit stone formation","Emerging RNA-interference therapy targeting hepatic lactate dehydrogenase, such as nedosiran, as a disease-modifying option, noting that pyridoxine is ineffective in this type","Active stone clearance using lithotripsy or endourological techniques while sparing renal parenchyma","Kidney transplantation rather than liver-based grafting, whose rationale is weak because the deficient enzyme is not liver-restricted, if end-stage renal failure supervenes","Regular monitoring of urinary oxalate and L-glycerate, plasma oxalate and renal function, with assessment for systemic oxalosis in advanced disease","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive GRHPR inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inborn errors of metabolism reference texts","Paediatric and adult nephrology reference texts","International guidelines on primary hyperoxaluria"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_hyperoxaluria_type_3":{"name":"Primary Hyperoxaluria Type 3","system":"Renal / Hepatic metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PH3","HOGA1 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic HOGA1 mutations affecting mitochondrial 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate aldolase in the hydroxyproline degradation pathway.","Frequently the earliest of the three types to present, often with recurrent calcium oxalate stones in infancy or early childhood.","Despite early stones, it usually carries the best long-term renal prognosis, with progression to end-stage renal failure being uncommon.","Biochemical signature is raised urinary oxalate together with elevated 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate and 2,4-dihydroxyglutarate, the discriminating metabolites.","Hypercalciuria is commonly seen alongside the hyperoxaluria and can confuse the clinical picture.","The precise mechanism linking HOGA1 loss to oxalate overproduction is incompletely understood, involving accumulation of hydroxy-oxoglutarate and its diversion to glyoxylate.","Management is largely conservative with hyperhydration and crystallisation inhibitors, and transplantation is rarely required.","Stone activity may lessen with age in some patients."],"pathophysiology":"Primary hyperoxaluria type 3 is an autosomal recessive disorder resulting from deficiency of 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate aldolase (HOGA1), a mitochondrial enzyme of the hydroxyproline degradation pathway encoded by HOGA1. Loss of enzyme activity leads to accumulation of 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate and dihydroxyglutarate; the precise route to oxalate overproduction remains debated, but the accumulating intermediates are thought to be cleaved to glyoxylate outside the normal compartment or to inhibit glyoxylate-handling enzymes, so that glyoxylate is diverted to oxalate. The excess oxalate causes urinary calcium oxalate supersaturation with recurrent stones, frequently accompanied by hypercalciuria. Compared with types 1 and 2, oxalate overproduction is generally less relentless and renal parenchymal damage less severe, so nephrocalcinosis, chronic kidney disease and systemic oxalosis are distinctly uncommon, and many patients maintain preserved renal function into adult life.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperoxaluria types 1 and 2","Idiopathic hypercalciuric calcium oxalate stone disease","Enteric and dietary hyperoxaluria","Other paediatric monogenic stone or nephrocalcinosis disorders","Distal renal tubular acidosis","Hypocitraturic calcium stone disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinary oxalate with 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate and dihydroxyglutarate measurement","Urinary calcium and citrate, as hypercalciuria frequently coexists","Renal tract imaging for stones and, rarely, nephrocalcinosis","Molecular genetic testing of HOGA1","Stone composition analysis","Longitudinal monitoring of renal function"],"redFlags":["Unexpectedly rapid decline in renal function, which should prompt review of the diagnosis","Development of nephrocalcinosis, which is atypical and warrants reassessment","Recurrent obstructing stones causing acute kidney injury","Rising plasma oxalate, which is rare in type 3 and should raise concern"],"prognosis":"Type 3 typically has the most benign renal prognosis of the primary hyperoxalurias; despite early and recurrent stone formation, progression to end-stage renal failure is uncommon and most patients retain good renal function. Lifelong monitoring remains prudent because a minority develop chronic kidney disease.","pitfalls":["Assuming any hyperoxaluria implies the severe type 1 course and over-treating","Overlooking coexisting hypercalciuria that also needs addressing","Missing the diagnosis when only oxalate is measured without the specific hydroxy-oxoglutarate metabolites","Failing to reconsider the diagnosis if renal function declines unexpectedly"],"management":["Sustained maintenance hyperhydration to keep the urine dilute and reduce calcium oxalate supersaturation","Potassium citrate and other crystallisation inhibitors to counter stone formation and any hypocitraturia","Management of the frequently coexisting hypercalciuria with dietary sodium restriction and, where marked, a thiazide diuretic, while maintaining adequate dietary calcium","Active but nephron-sparing stone clearance by lithotripsy or endourology for recurrent calculi","Longitudinal monitoring of renal function, urinary oxalate and the discriminating metabolites, and of stone activity","Reassurance regarding the generally favourable renal prognosis, with continued surveillance since a minority develop chronic kidney disease","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive HOGA1 inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inborn errors of metabolism reference texts","Paediatric and adult nephrology reference texts","International guidelines on primary hyperoxaluria"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_hyperparathyroidism":{"name":"Primary hyperparathyroidism","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PHPT","primary HPT","parathyroid adenoma-related hypercalcaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The leading cause of hypercalcaemia in ambulatory patients, most often detected incidentally on routine biochemistry","The biochemical hallmark is hypercalcaemia with a PTH level that is elevated or inappropriately normal rather than suppressed","Most cases are due to a single parathyroid adenoma; a minority reflect four-gland hyperplasia or, rarely, carcinoma","Classic symptomatic disease is summarised as stones, bones, abdominal groans, and psychiatric moans, but most patients are now asymptomatic","Skeletal effects include reduced bone density, particularly at cortical sites such as the distal radius, with fracture risk","Renal complications include nephrolithiasis, nephrocalcinosis, and impaired renal function; urine calcium helps assess risk","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia is a key mimic and is distinguished by a low urinary calcium excretion","Parathyroidectomy is curative and is indicated for symptomatic disease and for asymptomatic patients meeting defined criteria on calcium, bone density, renal parameters, or age"],"pathophysiology":"Primary hyperparathyroidism results from autonomous overproduction of parathyroid hormone by one or more parathyroid glands, usually a benign adenoma. Excess PTH raises serum calcium by increasing bone resorption, enhancing renal tubular calcium reabsorption, and stimulating renal conversion of vitamin D to its active form, which augments intestinal calcium absorption. PTH also promotes renal phosphate excretion, tending to lower serum phosphate. The loss of normal calcium-mediated negative feedback on the parathyroid glands sustains the hypercalcaemia. Chronic PTH excess drives cortical bone loss and contributes to hypercalciuria and stone formation.","additionalDifferentials":["Malignancy-associated hypercalcaemia (PTH-related peptide or bony metastases)","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia","Tertiary hyperparathyroidism in chronic kidney disease","Vitamin D or vitamin A intoxication","Granulomatous disease producing excess active vitamin D","Thiazide diuretic or lithium effect on calcium"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Simultaneous serum calcium (corrected or ionised) and intact PTH","Serum phosphate, magnesium, and renal function","25-hydroxyvitamin D level, as deficiency can worsen disease and confound interpretation","24-hour urinary calcium to help distinguish FHH and assess stone risk","Bone mineral density including a cortical site such as the distal one-third radius","Renal imaging for stones or nephrocalcinosis, and parathyroid localisation studies before surgery"],"redFlags":["Severe hypercalcaemia with dehydration, confusion, or cardiac dysrhythmia","Rapidly rising calcium with a palpable neck mass suggesting parathyroid carcinoma","Acute kidney injury or symptomatic nephrolithiasis","Very high PTH with markedly elevated calcium out of keeping with a simple adenoma"],"prognosis":"Successful parathyroidectomy cures the disease, halts bone loss with partial recovery of density, and reduces stone recurrence; asymptomatic patients managed conservatively often remain stable but require monitoring, and parathyroid carcinoma has a worse outlook with a tendency to recur.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia, leading to unnecessary neck surgery","Overlooking concurrent vitamin D deficiency that masks or modifies the biochemical picture","Attributing hypercalcaemia to hyperparathyroidism when PTH is actually suppressed, indicating another cause","Neglecting bone density and renal assessment that determine the need for surgery in asymptomatic patients"],"management":["Parathyroidectomy as the curative treatment for symptomatic disease and for asymptomatic patients meeting operative criteria, guided by preoperative localisation imaging","Calcimimetic therapy such as cinacalcet to control hypercalcaemia in those unsuitable for or awaiting surgery","Antiresorptive therapy with a bisphosphonate to protect cortical bone density, with judicious correction of coexisting vitamin D deficiency","Adequate hydration and avoidance of aggravating factors such as thiazide diuretics, lithium and immobilisation","Surveillance in conservatively managed patients with serial serum calcium, renal function, bone mineral density and renal tract imaging for stones","Emergency management of hypercalcaemic crisis with intravenous isotonic saline rehydration followed by calcitonin and an intravenous bisphosphonate","Referral to an endocrine surgeon, and genetic evaluation for familial syndromes such as multiple endocrine neoplasia when disease is early-onset, multiglandular or familial"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_immunodeficiency":{"name":"Primary Immunodeficiency","system":"Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PID","common variable immunodeficiency","CVID"],"chapter":362,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2793-2804","clinicalPearls":["The most frequent symptom prompting the diagnosis of a PID is recurrent or unusually severe infection; recurrent allergic or autoimmune manifestations may also be the alerting feature (Harrison 22e p.2793).","PIDs are classified by (1) the arm of the immune system that is defective and (2) the mechanism of the defect; the major categories are antibody/B-cell defects (agammaglobulinaemia, CVID, hyper-IgM, IgA deficiency), T-cell/cellular defects, combined defects (SCID), and innate-immune defects of phagocytes, complement, or innate receptors/signalling (Harrison 22e p.2794).","The site of infection and the causal microorganism point to the affected compartment: respiratory-tract (sinus, bronchial) infection mostly suggests defective antibody responses; invasive bacterial infection can result from complement deficiency, innate-signalling defects, asplenia, or antibody defects; viral, recurrent Candida, and opportunistic infections suggest impaired T-cell immunity; skin infections and deep abscesses primarily reflect innate (phagocyte) defects such as chronic granulomatous disease (Harrison 22e p.2794).","B-lymphocyte (antibody) deficiencies are the most frequent PIDs, accounting for 60-70% of all cases; T-cell deficiencies account for ~20% and innate-immune defects for ~10% (Harrison 22e p.2794, 2796, 2797).","Encapsulated/pyogenic bacteria (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis) and recurrent sinopulmonary infection are the hallmark of defective antibody production; untreated recurrent bronchial infection leads to bronchiectasis, cor pulmonale, and death (Harrison 22e p.2801).","Invasive Neisseria infection points to deficiencies of the complement lytic phase (C5-C9) or the alternative pathway (factors D, properdin); classic-pathway and lytic-phase complement deficiencies may also predispose to autoimmune disease, notably SLE (Harrison 22e p.2797).","CVID is recognised predominantly in adults, although clinical manifestations can begin earlier in life; in most cases no molecular cause is identified and the diagnosis is made only after excluding hypomorphic agammaglobulinaemia mutations and more subtle T-cell defects, especially in children (Harrison 22e p.2802).","First-line laboratory work-up is guided by the clinical picture and includes blood cell counts/morphology, serum immunoglobulin levels (IgG, IgA, IgM, IgE), specific antibody responses to vaccines (tetanus toxoid, unconjugated pneumococcal polysaccharide), and lymphocyte subset phenotyping; more specific (genetic) tests then confirm a definitive diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2794-2795).","Even with profound B-cell deficiency, infections rarely occur before 6 months of age because of transplacental maternal IgG; conversely, in SCID clinical consequences typically appear within 3-6 months of birth (Harrison 22e p.2801, 2799).","Immunoglobulin replacement should be guided by the occurrence of infection, not by laboratory IgG/antibody levels alone, to avoid unjustified infusions; it is lifelong therapy (Harrison 22e p.2802).","A precise molecular/genetic diagnosis has become a milestone in the care of PID patients, guiding treatment selection, genetic counselling, and prenatal diagnosis; suspected cases should be referred to experienced centres (Harrison 22e p.2804)."],"pathophysiology":"PIDs are predominantly Mendelian genetic diseases that arise whenever the expression or function of a nonredundant effector or mediator of the immune system is genetically impaired. More than 550 conditions have been described, with deleterious mutations identified in roughly 500 genes; estimated prevalence is 5-10 per 100,000 (probably an underestimate). They are increasingly grouped within the broader category of inborn errors of immunity that also includes autoinflammatory syndromes. Defects can involve any aspect of innate or adaptive immunity, cell differentiation, effector function, or immune regulation. The clinical consequence is multiple levels of vulnerability to infection, ranging from extremely broad (as in SCID, where T-cell development is blocked) to narrowly restricted to a single class of organism (as in Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease, an IL-12-IFN-gamma axis defect). Antibody (B-cell) defects (agammaglobulinaemia from BTK and pre-B-cell-receptor gene defects, hyper-IgM from class-switch-recombination defects such as CD40L/CD40, IgA deficiency, and CVID) impair production or class switching of immunoglobulins, leaving patients unable to opsonise and neutralise pyogenic encapsulated bacteria. Cellular (T-cell) defects impair help for antibody production, macrophage activation, and cytotoxicity, causing susceptibility to viral, fungal (Candida), and opportunistic infections (e.g., Pneumocystis jirovecii, Cryptosporidium). Combined defects (SCID) abolish both arms. Innate defects affect phagocyte number (severe congenital neutropenia), adhesion (LAD), or microbial killing (chronic granulomatous disease, from defective NADPH-oxidase reactive-oxygen-species production), as well as complement components and innate receptor/signalling pathways (TLR adaptors IRAK4/MYD88). Beyond infection, PIDs can drive immunopathology including allergy, lymphoproliferation, autoimmunity, and an increased risk of malignancy (notably lymphoma) (Harrison 22e p.2793-2794, 2796-2802).","additionalDifferentials":["Severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID)","X-linked or autosomal-recessive agammaglobulinaemia","Hyper-IgM (HIGM) syndromes (CD40L/CD40 or AID/UNG defects)","IgA deficiency and selective IgG-subclass deficiency","Selective antibody deficiency to polysaccharide antigens with normal total Ig","Chronic granulomatous disease and other phagocyte killing defects","Leukocyte adhesion deficiency and severe congenital neutropenia","Complement component deficiencies (classic, alternative, lytic-phase)","Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease (IL-12-IFN-gamma axis defects)","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, ataxia-telangiectasia, DiGeorge syndrome, hyper-IgE syndrome","Regulatory-pathway disorders: HLH, ALPS, IPEX, APECED"],"infectionMimics":["Recurrent pyogenic sinopulmonary infection from anatomical causes (e.g., cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis of other aetiology) mimicking antibody deficiency","HIV/AIDS and other secondary (acquired) immunodeficiency producing an opportunistic-infection pattern resembling a T-cell PID","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia as a presenting opportunistic infection of T-cell or combined defects","Recurrent or chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis","Disseminated or recurrent nontuberculous mycobacterial infection (suggesting MSMD)","Invasive aspergillosis or other filamentous-mould infection (suggesting CGD)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Secondary hypogammaglobulinaemia from thymoma (profound hypogammaglobulinaemia observed in adults)","Hypogammaglobulinaemia secondary to myelodysplastic syndrome (can present as agammaglobulinaemia)","Protein-losing states (nephrotic syndrome, protein-losing enteropathy) lowering immunoglobulin levels","Drug-induced or malignancy-associated (e.g., CLL, lymphoma) immunosuppression","Cytokine-neutralising autoantibodies (to IFN-gamma, IL-6, IL-7, type I interferon) that can mimic a PID","VEXAS syndrome (somatic UBA1 mutation) presenting with late-onset inflammation, vasculitis, and cytopenias"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cell counts and morphology (neutrophil and lymphocyte counts adjusted for age; eosinophilia; Howell-Jolly bodies suggesting asplenia)","Serum immunoglobulin levels (IgG, IgA, IgM) and IgE","Specific antibody responses to vaccination (tetanus toxoid; unconjugated pneumococcal polysaccharide antigens)","Lymphocyte phenotyping: T-, B-, and NK-cell counts and subsets; switched (CD27+ IgM-IgD-) and non-switched (CD27+ IgM+IgD+) memory B cells","Dihydrorhodamine (DHR) fluorescence or nitroblue tetrazolium (NBT) assay for neutrophil reactive-oxygen-species production (chronic granulomatous disease)","Complement functional assays CH50 and AP50 (classic and alternative pathways)","Chest x-ray for thymic shadow (SCID, DiGeorge) and costochondral junctions (ADA deficiency); bone x-ray of metaphyses (cartilage-hair hypoplasia)","Abdominal ultrasonography for spleen size (asplenia)","Newborn TREC (T-cell receptor excision circle) screening on the Guthrie card for SCID","Targeted gene-panel resequencing and/or whole-exome/genome sequencing for definitive molecular diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Recurrent, unusually severe, or persistent infections, or infection by unusual/opportunistic organisms","A family history of early childhood deaths (suggesting X-linked or recessive inheritance, e.g., SCID)","Lymphocytopenia in an infant (strongly suggestive of SCID in >90% of cases) or absent thymic shadow on chest x-ray","Failure to thrive with protracted diarrhoea, recurrent oral candidiasis, or Pneumocystis pneumonia in early infancy","Complications of live vaccines (e.g., BCG dissemination, rotavirus diarrhoea) signalling a severe T-cell defect","Invasive Neisseria infection (complement lytic-phase or alternative-pathway deficiency) or recurrent encapsulated-organism sepsis with asplenia","Recurrent infection accompanied by autoimmunity, lymphoproliferation, granulomatous disease, or lymphoma","Absence of pus at sites of infection (hallmark of severe congenital neutropenia)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies widely with the molecule and arm of immunity affected. T-cell and combined defects generally carry a poor prognosis and require early diagnosis and intervention; SCID is fatal in infancy without treatment, but allogeneic HSCT offers a high curative potential in patients in otherwise reasonable condition, and gene therapy has succeeded in X-linked (gamma-c) SCID, ADA-SCID, and Artemis deficiency. Functional T-cell defects carry a worse HSCT prognosis than SCID because many patients are already chronically infected at diagnosis. For antibody deficiencies, lifelong immunoglobulin replacement combined with management of chronic lung disease reduces respiratory infections and prevents progression to bronchiectasis and cor pulmonale; in CVID, lymphoproliferation, granulomatous disease, autoimmunity, and lymphoma define long-term prognosis. DNA-repair disorders such as ataxia-telangiectasia are progressive and carry a very high risk of lymphoma, leukaemia, and (in adulthood) carcinoma. Newborn TREC screening, by enabling treatment of uninfected SCID patients, maximises the chance of cure. Early diagnosis with referral to experienced centres is essential to optimal outcome (Harrison 22e p.2794, 2799-2800, 2802, 2804).","pitfalls":["CVID can present in adulthood and is easily missed; it is recognised predominantly in adults although manifestations may begin earlier (Harrison 22e p.2802).","High variability of expression for a given PID (e.g., WAS, XLA, CTLA4 deficiency) can hamper diagnosis; modifier genes and environmental triggers such as EBV (in XLP) modulate the phenotype (Harrison 22e p.2804).","Leaky BTK mutations causing mild hypogammaglobulinaemia with low but detectable B cells should not be confused with CVID (Harrison 22e p.2802).","T-cell lymphopenia in SCID may be masked by maternally-engrafted T cells, so screening for maternal cells with genetic markers should be performed when needed (Harrison 22e p.2799).","Somatic mutations in an affected gene can attenuate the phenotype of T-cell PIDs (described for ADA, X-linked SCID, RAG, NEMO, and most often WAS), while somatic mutations can also create PID-like disease (as in ALPS) (Harrison 22e p.2804).","Cytokine-neutralising autoantibodies (to IFN-gamma, IL-6, IL-7, type I interferon) can mimic a PID (Harrison 22e p.2804).","Caution with immunoglobulin replacement in patients with complete IgA deficiency and residual antibody production: anti-IgA antibodies can trigger anaphylactic shock, so IgA-free IgG preparations should be used (Harrison 22e p.2803).","Ataxia-telangiectasia should be considered in young children with IgA deficiency and recurrent problematic infections, as ataxia and telangiectasia may not appear before age 3-4 years (Harrison 22e p.2799-2800)."],"management":["Immunoglobulin replacement therapy, given intravenously or subcutaneously, as the mainstay for antibody and B-cell deficiencies","Prompt aggressive treatment of infection plus targeted prophylaxis, such as co-trimoxazole against Pneumocystis in T-cell defects and antifungal or antiviral prophylaxis as dictated by the defect","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for severe combined and other profound cellular defects, with gene therapy for selected disorders such as adenosine deaminase deficiency","Avoidance of live vaccines in severe T-cell or combined defects, use of irradiated cytomegalovirus-safe blood products, and appropriate immunisation of antibody-deficient patients and their contacts","Management of end-organ complications, including airway clearance and treatment of bronchiectasis, and immunomodulation for autoimmune or inflammatory manifestations","Regular surveillance of organ function, growth and infection burden, with immune reconstitution monitoring after transplantation","Care coordinated by a specialist clinical immunology centre within a multidisciplinary team","Genetic counselling and family screening, with access to newborn screening that enables early detection of severe combined immunodeficiency"],"severityClassification":"PIDs are classified along two axes: (1) the arm of the immune system that is defective and (2) the mechanism of the defect (when known). The two main divisions are deficiencies of the innate immune system (phagocytic cells - impaired production [severe congenital neutropenia], asplenia, impaired adhesion [LAD], impaired killing [CGD]; innate-immunity receptors/signal transduction [TLR-signalling defects, MSMD]; complement deficiencies of the classical/alternative/lectin pathways and the lytic phase) and deficiencies of the adaptive immune system (T lymphocytes - SCID, DiGeorge, combined immunodeficiencies, hyper-IgE, DOCK8, CD40L, Wiskott-Aldrich, ataxia-telangiectasia and DNA-repair defects; B lymphocytes - X-linked/AR agammaglobulinaemia, hyper-IgM, CVID, IgA deficiency), together with regulatory defects (autoinflammatory syndromes, severe colitis, HLH, ALPS, autoimmunity such as IPEX and APECED). The classification is acknowledged to involve some arbitrary decisions because of overlap and incomplete data (Harrison 22e p.2794).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2793-2804"],"crossLinks":["pneumococcal_disease","pneumocystis_pneumonia","candidiasis","nontuberculous_mycobacterial_infection","meningococcal_disease","aspergillosis","hiv_aids"]},"primary_lateral_sclerosis":{"name":"Primary lateral sclerosis","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLS","pure upper motor neurone disease","primary lateral sclerosis of the motor neurone disease spectrum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with a slowly progressive, symmetrical spastic paraparesis that often begins in the legs and ascends, later involving the arms and bulbar muscles","Signs are those of pure upper motor neurone dysfunction: spasticity, hyperreflexia, extensor plantar responses, and a spastic dysarthria, without significant wasting or fasciculation","By convention a diagnosis is favoured when there is no clinical or electrophysiological evidence of lower motor neurone involvement after a prolonged period, commonly around four years","A pseudobulbar affect with emotional lability is frequent and reflects bilateral corticobulbar tract involvement","Disease course is far more indolent than amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, and survival is measured in decades rather than years","Bladder urgency from a spastic bladder may develop, but sensory loss and prominent muscle atrophy should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis","A proportion of apparently pure upper motor neurone cases eventually declare lower motor neurone signs and are reclassified within the amyotrophic lateral sclerosis spectrum"],"pathophysiology":"Primary lateral sclerosis is a rare degenerative disorder confined largely to the upper motor neurones, with selective loss of the large Betz cells of the primary motor cortex and degeneration of the corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts. Unlike amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, the anterior horn cells and lower motor neurones are relatively spared, at least for a prolonged period, which explains the predominance of spasticity over wasting. The condition is regarded as sitting at the upper motor neurone end of the motor neurone disease spectrum, and its precise molecular basis remains uncertain, although overlap with familial motor neurone disease genes is recognised in some cases.","additionalDifferentials":["Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis with predominant upper motor neurone signs","Hereditary spastic paraparesis","Cervical spondylotic myelopathy or other compressive myelopathy","Multiple sclerosis with a progressive myelopathic course","Adrenomyeloneuropathy and other metabolic myelopathies","Subacute combined degeneration from vitamin B12 deficiency","Tropical spastic paraparesis (HTLV-1 associated myelopathy)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain and whole spine to exclude compressive, demyelinating and structural lesions","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies to demonstrate absence of lower motor neurone denervation","Serum vitamin B12, copper and very-long-chain fatty acids to exclude metabolic myelopathies","HTLV-1 serology where exposure risk exists","Genetic testing for hereditary spastic paraparesis genes when a familial pattern is suspected","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis to help exclude inflammatory or infective myelopathy"],"redFlags":["Emergence of muscle wasting, fasciculation or a positive electromyogram, indicating conversion to amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","A clear sensory level or sphincter disturbance suggesting a structural cord lesion","Rapidly progressive weakness inconsistent with the expected indolent course","Bulbar dysfunction causing unsafe swallowing and aspiration risk"],"prognosis":"Primary lateral sclerosis progresses slowly over many years and is associated with a much longer survival than amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, although accumulating spasticity and eventual bulbar involvement cause substantial disability; a subset later develop lower motor neurone signs and are reclassified.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing primary lateral sclerosis too early, before enough time has elapsed to exclude conversion to amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Overlooking a compressive cervical myelopathy that can mimic pure upper motor neurone disease","Attributing emotional lability to a mood disorder rather than recognising pseudobulbar affect","Missing a treatable metabolic or hereditary cause of progressive spastic paraparesis"],"management":["Spasticity control with oral antispastic agents such as baclofen or tizanidine, focal botulinum toxin for troublesome muscles, and intrathecal baclofen in severe refractory cases","Regular physiotherapy, stretching, orthoses and mobility aids to preserve function and prevent contractures","Treatment of pseudobulbar affect with an SSRI, a tricyclic antidepressant or a dextromethorphan-quinidine combination","Speech and language therapy with communication aids for spastic dysarthria, and management of dysphagia including gastrostomy feeding when swallowing fails","Management of spastic bladder symptoms with antimuscarinic therapy and urology input","Periodic clinical and electrophysiological review to detect emerging lower motor neurone signs that would reclassify the disorder as amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, with respiratory surveillance if bulbar function declines","Coordinated multidisciplinary care through a motor neurone disease clinic, with occupational therapy and psychological support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_lymphoedema":{"name":"primary lymphoedema","system":"lymphatic and vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hereditary lymphoedema","idiopathic lymphoedema"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Arises from an intrinsic developmental or functional abnormality of the lymphatic conducting vessels or nodes, not from an identifiable external insult, distinguishing it from secondary lymphoedema.","Classified by age of onset: congenital (present at or shortly after birth, includes Milroy disease), praecox (onset before 35 years, includes Meige disease) and tarda (onset after 35 years).","A positive Stemmer sign, the inability to pinch and tent a skin fold at the base of the second toe, is a reliable bedside indicator and is rarely falsely positive.","Swelling typically begins distally at the ankle or dorsum of the foot and ascends, is initially pitting and soft, then becomes non-pitting with dermal thickening, hyperkeratosis and peau d'orange.","Recurrent cellulitis is both a consequence and an accelerant of disease, each episode causing further lymphatic destruction and worsening oedema.","Several Mendelian forms exist, including FLT4 (VEGFR3) mutations in Milroy disease and FOXC2 mutations in lymphoedema-distichiasis, so a syndromic and family history is essential.","Lymphoscintigraphy is the standard confirmatory investigation, demonstrating delayed or absent tracer transport, dermal backflow and abnormal or absent nodes.","Chronic longstanding disease carries a small but important risk of cutaneous angiosarcoma (Stewart-Treves phenomenon), warranting vigilance for new violaceous lesions."],"pathophysiology":"Primary lymphoedema results from a constitutional abnormality of lymphatic development or function, which may take the form of aplasia, hypoplasia, hyperplasia with valvular incompetence, or lymph node fibrosis. Impaired lymphatic transport allows protein-rich interstitial fluid to accumulate, and the persistent high-protein oedema drives chronic low-grade inflammation, fibroblast activation, adipose deposition and progressive dermal and subcutaneous fibrosis. This creates a self-perpetuating cycle in which tissue remodelling further compromises the remaining lymphatics and predisposes to recurrent bacterial infection, which in turn accelerates lymphatic obliteration. A growing number of causative germline mutations affecting lymphangiogenesis pathways (for example VEGFR3/FLT4, FOXC2, SOX18, GJC2 and CCBE1) have been identified, explaining the hereditary and syndromic patterns.","additionalDifferentials":["chronic venous insufficiency with venous oedema","lipoedema","systemic oedema from cardiac, renal or hepatic failure","hypothyroid myxoedema","secondary lymphoedema from malignancy, surgery, radiotherapy or filariasis","deep vein thrombosis and post-thrombotic syndrome","chronic regional pain syndrome with limb swelling"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["lymphoscintigraphy to assess lymphatic transport","venous duplex ultrasonography to exclude venous disease","MR or CT lymphangiography for anatomical mapping","indocyanine green near-infrared lymphography","targeted genetic testing and pedigree analysis in early-onset or syndromic cases","thyroid function and renal, hepatic and cardiac assessment to exclude systemic oedema"],"redFlags":["rapidly progressive or markedly asymmetrical swelling raising concern for underlying malignancy","new violaceous or haemorrhagic cutaneous nodules suggesting angiosarcoma","recurrent cellulitis with systemic sepsis","skin breakdown, ulceration or lymphorrhoea","unilateral limb swelling of recent onset in an older adult, mandating exclusion of pelvic or nodal tumour"],"prognosis":"Primary lymphoedema is a chronic, incurable condition that tends to progress without treatment, but structured decongestive therapy and infection prevention allow most patients to maintain limb function and quality of life. Prognosis worsens with recurrent cellulitis, advanced fibrosis and the rare development of angiosarcoma.","pitfalls":["attributing chronic limb swelling to venous disease or simple obesity and missing lymphatic failure","undertreating or delaying antibiotics in cellulitis, allowing further lymphatic damage","overlooking associated syndromic features such as distichiasis, yellow nails or ptosis","failing to elicit a family history and thereby missing a hereditary diagnosis","dismissing new cutaneous lesions on a chronically swollen limb as bruising"],"management":["Complete decongestive therapy combining manual lymphatic drainage, multilayer compression bandaging and maintenance compression garments as the mainstay","Meticulous skin hygiene with emollients to maintain barrier integrity and reduce infection risk","Structured exercise, limb elevation and weight optimisation to support lymphatic drainage","Prompt antibiotic treatment of cellulitis and prophylactic antibiotics for recurrent episodes, since each attack accelerates lymphatic destruction","Surgical options in selected cases, including lymphaticovenous anastomosis, vascularised lymph node transfer, and liposuction or debulking for advanced fibrotic disease","Ongoing monitoring of limb volume and function with lymphoedema-specialist follow-up and psychological support","Genetic counselling and family screening for Mendelian forms such as Milroy disease and lymphoedema-distichiasis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Browse's Introduction to the Symptoms and Signs of Surgical Disease","International Society of Lymphology Consensus Document on the diagnosis and treatment of peripheral lymphoedema"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_myelofibrosis":{"name":"Primary myelofibrosis","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMF","myelofibrosis","chronic idiopathic myelofibrosis","agnogenic myeloid metaplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A myeloproliferative neoplasm defined by clonal proliferation with progressive marrow fibrosis and extramedullary haematopoiesis, classically producing marked splenomegaly","Typical features include fatigue and anaemia, constitutional symptoms (weight loss, night sweats, fever), and abdominal discomfort or early satiety from splenic enlargement","The blood film shows a leucoerythroblastic picture with teardrop-shaped red cells (dacrocytes), nucleated red cells, and immature myeloid forms","Attempts to aspirate marrow often yield a dry tap because of fibrosis, so a trephine biopsy demonstrating reticulin or collagen fibrosis is essential","Driver mutations in JAK2, CALR, or MPL are present in most cases and support the diagnosis; additional high-risk mutations refine prognosis","Prognostic scoring incorporates age, symptoms, cytopenias, blast count, and cytogenetic/molecular features to guide management decisions","JAK inhibitors reduce spleen size and constitutional symptoms and can improve quality of life, though they are not curative","Allogeneic stem cell transplant is the only curative therapy and is considered for suitable higher-risk patients; transformation to acute leukaemia is a feared complication"],"pathophysiology":"Primary myelofibrosis originates from a clonal haematopoietic stem cell with dysregulated JAK-STAT signalling, most often driven by JAK2, CALR, or MPL mutations. The abnormal clone, particularly its megakaryocytes, secretes fibrogenic cytokines and growth factors (such as transforming growth factor-beta and platelet-derived growth factor) that stimulate marrow fibroblasts to deposit reticulin and collagen. As the marrow becomes progressively fibrotic, haematopoiesis relocates to the spleen and liver (extramedullary haematopoiesis), causing organomegaly and release of immature cells into the circulation. A chronic inflammatory cytokine state produces constitutional symptoms, and clonal evolution can culminate in acute leukaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary (reactive) myelofibrosis from metastatic cancer or infection","Post-polycythaemia vera or post-essential thrombocythaemia myelofibrosis","Chronic myeloid leukaemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome with fibrosis","Hairy cell leukaemia and other marrow-infiltrating disorders","Autoimmune myelofibrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film for leucoerythroblastic changes and teardrop cells","Bone marrow trephine biopsy with reticulin/collagen staining to grade fibrosis","Molecular testing for JAK2, CALR, and MPL, plus high-molecular-risk mutation panels","BCR-ABL1 testing to exclude chronic myeloid leukaemia","Cytogenetic analysis for prognostic stratification","Abdominal imaging to document splenomegaly and extramedullary disease"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising blast count or new cytogenetic changes indicating leukaemic transformation","Severe anaemia or thrombocytopenia with bleeding requiring urgent support","Massive symptomatic splenomegaly with risk of splenic infarction or rupture","Portal hypertension or splanchnic vein thrombosis from extramedullary haematopoiesis and hypercoagulability"],"prognosis":"Survival in primary myelofibrosis is highly variable, ranging from many years in low-risk disease to under two years in high-risk categories; adverse cytogenetics, high-risk mutations, transfusion dependence, and excess blasts predict poorer outcomes, and only allogeneic transplant offers cure.","pitfalls":["Assuming a leucoerythroblastic film always indicates PMF without excluding marrow infiltration by malignancy or infection","Relying on a marrow aspirate alone when fibrosis produces a dry tap and a biopsy is needed","Failing to exclude chronic myeloid leukaemia with BCR-ABL1 testing","Overlooking prognostic mutations that would prompt earlier transplant referral","Not recognising that a JAK inhibitor controls symptoms and spleen size but does not prevent progression"],"management":["Risk stratification using prognostic scores such as DIPSS to direct treatment intensity","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the only curative option, offered to eligible higher-risk patients","JAK inhibition with ruxolitinib to reduce symptomatic splenomegaly and constitutional symptoms","Anaemia management with erythropoiesis-stimulating agents, an androgen such as danazol, luspatercept or red cell transfusion","Splenic irradiation or splenectomy for massive symptomatic splenomegaly refractory to drug therapy","Supportive care including transfusion, iron chelation for transfusional iron overload, and treatment of symptomatic extramedullary haematopoiesis","Monitoring of blood counts, spleen size and blast proportion for disease progression and leukaemic transformation","Haematology-led multidisciplinary care with early symptom-focused and palliative support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_obstructive_megaureter":{"name":"Primary obstructive megaureter","system":"Renal / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["primary obstructed megaureter","POM","congenital obstructive megaureter"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenitally dilated ureter caused by a narrowed, aperistaltic juxtavesical (distal) segment that impedes urine flow into the bladder, with the obstruction being functional rather than a fixed anatomical stenosis in most cases","Classified within the megaureter spectrum as obstructed, refluxing, both, or neither; the primary obstructive type has obstruction without reflux","Commonly detected on antenatal ultrasound as ureteric dilatation with variable pelvicalyceal dilatation, the ureter being widest just above the bladder","May present later with urinary tract infection, loin pain, or haematuria","The distal aperistaltic segment itself is typically of normal calibre; it is the segment above that is dilated","Many cases, especially with preserved function and modest dilatation, improve spontaneously and are managed expectantly with surveillance and often prophylactic antibiotics","Persistent or progressive obstruction with deteriorating function, breakthrough infection, or symptoms is treated by ureteric reimplantation with tapering or, in selected cases, temporising endoscopic or drainage procedures"],"pathophysiology":"In primary obstructive megaureter the terminal ureteric segment adjacent to the bladder is structurally abnormal, with disorganised smooth muscle and excess collagen that render it aperistaltic and non-compliant. Although this segment is usually of normal or near-normal calibre and not stenotic in the classic sense, it fails to transmit peristaltic waves and effectively obstructs the passage of urine into the bladder. Urine therefore accumulates proximally, producing progressive dilatation of the ureter above the junction and, in more severe cases, pelvicalyceal dilatation and impairment of renal function. There is no reflux, distinguishing it from refluxing megaureter.","additionalDifferentials":["Refluxing megaureter (primary vesicoureteric reflux)","Ureteropelvic junction obstruction","Ureterocele causing ureteric dilatation","Ectopic ureter","Posterior urethral valves with secondary upper-tract dilatation","Non-obstructive non-refluxing megaureter"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal and bladder ultrasound to characterise ureteric and pelvicalyceal dilatation","MAG3 diuretic renogram to assess drainage and differential function","Micturating cystourethrogram (MCUG) to exclude reflux and bladder outlet pathology","Serial ultrasound for surveillance of antenatally detected dilatation","Urinalysis and culture when infection is suspected","MR urography in selected cases to define anatomy"],"redFlags":["Declining differential renal function on serial renography","Increasing ureteric or pelvicalyceal dilatation","Febrile urinary tract infection or urosepsis in a dilated obstructed system","Obstruction in a solitary functioning kidney"],"prognosis":"The majority of antenatally detected cases with reasonable function stabilise or resolve spontaneously over the first years of life, and reimplantation for persistent obstruction has good results; long-standing severe obstruction can leave permanent functional loss.","pitfalls":["Assuming all megaureters are obstructive when reflux or a non-obstructive non-refluxing pattern may be responsible","Operating early on stable dilatation likely to resolve, rather than observing","Misinterpreting a diuretic renogram in an immature kidney or with a full bladder","Failing to exclude reflux and bladder outlet obstruction before attributing dilatation to the distal ureter"],"management":["Expectant management with active surveillance for antenatally detected cases with preserved differential function and modest dilatation, most of which improve spontaneously","Continuous antibiotic prophylaxis, particularly in infancy, to reduce urinary tract infection risk","Serial renal tract ultrasound and diuretic MAG3 renography to monitor dilatation and split renal function","Prompt treatment of febrile urinary tract infection with appropriate antibiotics","Ureteric reimplantation with excision or tapering of the aperistaltic distal segment for persistent obstruction, deteriorating function, breakthrough infection or symptoms","Temporising measures in infants too small for reimplantation, such as JJ stenting, endoscopic balloon dilatation or a cutaneous ureterostomy","Long-term paediatric urology and nephrology follow-up with monitoring of renal function"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Gearhart, Rink & Mouriquand: Pediatric Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_open_angle_glaucoma":{"name":"Primary open-angle glaucoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["POAG","open-angle glaucoma","chronic simple glaucoma","chronic open-angle glaucoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically an insidious, painless, bilateral (often asymmetric) chronic optic neuropathy detected on screening rather than by symptoms","Peripheral visual field is lost first, with central acuity preserved until late, so patients are frequently unaware until damage is advanced","The drainage angle is open on gonioscopy, distinguishing it from angle-closure disease","Hallmark disc changes include increased cup-to-disc ratio, focal neuroretinal rim thinning or notching, and disc haemorrhages","Risk rises with elevated intraocular pressure, older age, family history, African ancestry, high myopia, and thin central corneas","Normal-tension glaucoma shows characteristic optic nerve and field damage despite pressures in the statistically normal range","Management aims to lower intraocular pressure with topical medication first, escalating to laser trabeculoplasty or surgical drainage (trabeculectomy) to slow progression"],"pathophysiology":"Primary open-angle glaucoma is a progressive optic neuropathy in which aqueous outflow through an anatomically open trabecular meshwork is impaired, raising resistance to drainage. The resulting intraocular pressure, sometimes within the normal range, contributes to mechanical and vascular stress at the optic nerve head, causing loss of retinal ganglion cell axons. This produces characteristic cupping of the optic disc and a corresponding pattern of arcuate and peripheral visual field loss that advances slowly over years.","additionalDifferentials":["Normal-tension glaucoma","Ocular hypertension without nerve damage","Secondary open-angle glaucoma (pigmentary, pseudoexfoliation, steroid-induced)","Non-glaucomatous optic neuropathy","Physiological large optic disc cup"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Applanation tonometry to measure intraocular pressure","Gonioscopy to confirm the angle is open","Dilated optic disc examination for cupping and rim changes","Automated perimetry (visual field testing) to detect and monitor field loss","Optical coherence tomography of the retinal nerve fibre layer and ganglion cell complex","Central corneal thickness (pachymetry), which influences pressure interpretation and risk"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual field loss or disc change despite apparently controlled pressure","Disc (splinter) haemorrhage, a marker of ongoing progression","Very high presenting pressure or advanced cupping threatening fixation","Rapid deterioration suggesting a secondary or aggressive glaucoma"],"prognosis":"With early detection and sustained pressure control most patients retain useful vision for life, but untreated or poorly controlled disease progresses to irreversible visual field loss and blindness.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single pressure reading, as pressure fluctuates and can be normal in normal-tension glaucoma","Attributing all disc cupping to glaucoma without correlating fields and nerve fibre layer","Poor adherence to topical therapy going unrecognised as the cause of progression","Overlooking secondary causes such as pseudoexfoliation or long-term steroid use"],"management":["Topical prostaglandin analogue such as latanoprost as first-line therapy to lower intraocular pressure, the only modifiable risk factor","Additional pressure-lowering drops including beta-blockers, alpha-2 agonists, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors or rho-kinase inhibitors, added or substituted to reach the target pressure","Selective laser trabeculoplasty as a first-line alternative or adjunct to medical therapy","Incisional or minimally invasive glaucoma surgery, such as trabeculectomy or a drainage device, for progression despite maximal tolerated medical treatment","Regular monitoring with applanation tonometry, optic disc and retinal nerve fibre layer imaging, and automated perimetry to detect progression","Support for adherence and correct drop technique, with patient education on the asymptomatic nature of early disease","Ongoing ophthalmology follow-up with low-vision support and rehabilitation for advanced visual loss"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_ovarian_insufficiency":{"name":"Primary ovarian insufficiency","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["premature ovarian failure","premature ovarian insufficiency","hypergonadotropic hypogonadism (female)","POI"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by loss of normal ovarian function before the age of 40, presenting with primary or secondary amenorrhoea, oestrogen deficiency, and impaired fertility","The biochemical signature is hypergonadotropic hypogonadism: a persistently raised follicle-stimulating hormone with a low oestradiol, reflecting failure of ovarian negative feedback","Turner syndrome (45,X and variants) is a leading cause in the paediatric age group and should be considered in any girl with delayed puberty and short stature","An FMR1 (fragile X) premutation is an important heritable cause and warrants testing, particularly where there is a family history of ovarian insufficiency, intellectual disability, or ataxia/tremor","Autoimmune oophoritis may occur in isolation or as part of an autoimmune polyglandular syndrome, so adrenal and thyroid autoimmunity should be screened for","Iatrogenic insufficiency from chemotherapy, pelvic irradiation, or ovarian surgery is increasingly common among survivors of childhood cancer","Management centres on oestrogen and progestogen replacement to induce or maintain secondary sexual characteristics, protect bone and cardiovascular health, and address fertility and psychological needs","Residual, intermittent ovarian activity can occur, so the condition is not always absolute and spontaneous conception is occasionally possible"],"pathophysiology":"Primary ovarian insufficiency reflects depletion or dysfunction of the ovarian follicular pool, so the ovary fails to produce adequate oestrogen and to complete folliculogenesis. Loss of ovarian feedback removes inhibition of the hypothalamic-pituitary axis, driving compensatory elevation of gonadotropins, especially FSH. Mechanisms include chromosomal abnormalities causing accelerated follicular atresia (as in Turner syndrome), the FMR1 premutation, other genetic and metabolic disorders (such as galactosaemia), autoimmune destruction of ovarian tissue, and iatrogenic gonadotoxic insults from chemotherapy, radiation, or surgery. The resulting oestrogen deficiency impairs pubertal development or causes menstrual cessation and predisposes to reduced bone mineral density.","additionalDifferentials":["Constitutional delay of growth and puberty","Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (central causes, including isolated GnRH deficiency)","Polycystic ovary syndrome with oligomenorrhoea","Pregnancy as a cause of secondary amenorrhoea","Hyperprolactinaemia","Anatomical causes such as Müllerian agenesis or outflow obstruction","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhoea from weight loss, exercise, or illness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum FSH and LH (elevated) with oestradiol (low), repeated to confirm","Karyotype to detect Turner syndrome or Y-chromosome material","FMR1 premutation testing for fragile X-associated ovarian insufficiency","Adrenal and thyroid autoantibodies and screening for associated autoimmune endocrinopathy","Pelvic ultrasound to assess uterine size and ovarian morphology","Bone mineral density assessment given the risk of oestrogen-deficiency bone loss"],"redFlags":["Presence of Y-chromosome material on karyotype, which mandates gonadectomy because of malignant germ cell tumour risk in dysgenetic gonads","Features of adrenal insufficiency in association with autoimmune ovarian failure","Turner syndrome features requiring cardiac and aortic surveillance","Severe oestrogen deficiency with low bone density and fracture risk"],"prognosis":"Fertility is substantially reduced and often permanently lost, though intermittent ovarian function means occasional spontaneous ovulation; with appropriate hormone replacement, secondary sexual characteristics, bone health, and cardiovascular risk can be well managed, and assisted reproduction with donor oocytes offers a route to pregnancy.","pitfalls":["Attributing amenorrhoea to stress or weight without measuring gonadotropins, thereby missing hypergonadotropic hypogonadism","Failing to karyotype and so overlooking Turner syndrome or occult Y-chromosome material requiring gonadectomy","Neglecting to test for the FMR1 premutation and its familial implications","Underestimating the long-term bone and cardiovascular consequences of untreated oestrogen deficiency","Telling patients that pregnancy is impossible, ignoring the small chance of spontaneous ovulation"],"management":["Systemic oestrogen replacement with a progestogen in women with an intact uterus, continued until the average age of natural menopause, to relieve deficiency symptoms and protect bone and cardiovascular health","Gradual pubertal induction with incremental oestrogen followed by added progestogen in adolescents presenting before puberty is complete","Bone protection through adequate calcium and vitamin D intake, weight-bearing exercise and periodic bone mineral density monitoring","Fertility counselling with assisted reproduction using donor oocytes or embryos, and fertility preservation before any planned gonadotoxic treatment","Identification and treatment of the underlying cause, including screening for associated thyroid and adrenal autoimmunity and management of Turner-related comorbidities","Contraceptive advice where pregnancy is not desired, since intermittent spontaneous ovulation can occur","Psychological support addressing the impact on fertility and identity","Genetic counselling and family screening, including FMR1 premutation and karyotype testing, with gonadectomy where Y-chromosome material is present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_pigmented_nodular_adrenocortical_disease":{"name":"Primary pigmented nodular adrenocortical disease","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPNAD","primary pigmented nodular adrenal disease","micronodular adrenal disease","Carney complex adrenal disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard endocrinology reference texts and adrenal disorder consensus guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare cause of ACTH-independent Cushing syndrome, typically in children and young adults, in which the adrenals contain small pigmented (dark brown to black) cortical micronodules","The glands are often normal-sized or only slightly enlarged on imaging, so cross-sectional scanning can appear deceptively unremarkable","Strongly associated with Carney complex, and most cases carry germline inactivating mutations in PRKAR1A, the regulatory subunit of protein kinase A","A characteristic feature is a paradoxical rise in urinary or serum cortisol during high-dose dexamethasone administration (Liddle test), rather than the expected suppression","Cushingoid features may be cyclical or atypical, and growth failure with weight gain in a child should prompt consideration of this diagnosis","Screening for other Carney complex manifestations (cardiac myxomas, spotty skin pigmentation, testicular and other endocrine tumours) is essential once PPNAD is suspected","Bilateral adrenalectomy is generally required because the disease is bilateral and driven by constitutive protein kinase A activation"],"pathophysiology":"PPNAD is a bilateral adrenocortical disorder in which numerous small cortisol-secreting micronodules, containing lipofuscin pigment, arise within an often atrophic surrounding cortex. Autonomous cortisol production suppresses pituitary ACTH. The molecular basis in most patients is a germline loss-of-function mutation in PRKAR1A, which encodes the type 1A regulatory subunit of protein kinase A; loss of this inhibitory subunit leads to constitutive, cAMP-independent activation of protein kinase A signalling and unregulated steroidogenesis. This dysregulated signalling also explains the paradoxical increase in cortisol seen with dexamethasone. The condition is a component of Carney complex, a multiple neoplasia and lentiginosis syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Cortisol-secreting adrenal adenoma","Primary bilateral macronodular adrenal hyperplasia","ACTH-dependent (pituitary) Cushing disease","Ectopic ACTH syndrome","Adrenocortical carcinoma","Exogenous glucocorticoid exposure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["24-hour urinary free cortisol and late-night salivary cortisol to confirm hypercortisolism","Paired low plasma ACTH with failure to suppress on low-dose dexamethasone establishing ACTH independence","High-dose dexamethasone (Liddle) testing to elicit the paradoxical cortisol rise","Adrenal CT or MRI, recognising that glands may look near-normal despite active disease","PRKAR1A germline genetic testing and evaluation for Carney complex features","Echocardiography and dermatological review to detect cardiac myxomas and lentigines"],"redFlags":["Cardiac myxoma causing embolic events or obstruction as part of Carney complex","Severe or cyclical hypercortisolism with growth arrest in a child","Family history suggesting an inherited multiple neoplasia syndrome","Post-adrenalectomy adrenal insufficiency requiring lifelong replacement"],"prognosis":"Cushing syndrome due to PPNAD is effectively cured by bilateral adrenalectomy, which necessitates lifelong glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement. Overall prognosis is dominated by other Carney complex manifestations, particularly cardiac myxomas, so lifelong surveillance is required.","pitfalls":["Concluding the adrenals are normal because imaging shows no discrete mass","Interpreting a paradoxical cortisol rise on dexamethasone as an assay error rather than a diagnostic clue","Failing to screen for Carney complex and missing life-threatening cardiac myxomas","Not offering genetic counselling for an inheritable PRKAR1A-related condition"],"management":["Bilateral adrenalectomy as the definitive curative treatment for the ACTH-independent hypercortisolism","Lifelong glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement after adrenalectomy, with patient education on sick-day dosing and emergency steroids","Preoperative control of severe hypercortisolism with steroidogenesis inhibitors such as metyrapone or ketoconazole where required","Perioperative stress-dose corticosteroid cover and prompt recognition and treatment of adrenal crisis","Surveillance for other Carney complex features, especially echocardiographic screening for potentially life-threatening cardiac myxomas, plus dermatological, testicular, thyroid and pituitary assessment","PRKAR1A germline genetic testing with genetic counselling and family screening","Coordinated endocrine and cardiac multidisciplinary follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Consensus guidance on Carney complex and PPNAD"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_progressive_aphasia":{"name":"Primary progressive aphasia","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPA","progressive language-predominant dementia","primary progressive aphasia syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A clinical syndrome in which progressive decline of language is the earliest and most prominent feature, with relative preservation of other cognitive domains early in the course","Three principal variants are recognised: the non-fluent/agrammatic, the semantic and the logopenic subtypes, each with distinct language and imaging profiles","Diagnosis requires that the language difficulty is the leading cause of impaired daily activities and is not better explained by a stroke, tumour or primary psychiatric disorder","The non-fluent and semantic variants are usually underpinned by frontotemporal lobar degeneration, whereas the logopenic variant is most often driven by Alzheimer pathology","Structural and metabolic imaging typically show asymmetrical, often left-predominant, focal atrophy or hypometabolism in language networks","Amyloid imaging and cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers help identify underlying Alzheimer pathology, particularly in the logopenic variant","Behavioural or motor features may emerge later, sometimes overlapping with corticobasal or progressive supranuclear palsy syndromes"],"pathophysiology":"Primary progressive aphasia is an umbrella term for neurodegenerative syndromes in which the disease process selectively targets the language-dominant, usually left, perisylvian and temporal cortical networks. The underlying pathology varies by subtype: the non-fluent/agrammatic and semantic variants are most commonly associated with frontotemporal lobar degeneration due to abnormal tau or TDP-43 protein aggregation, while the logopenic variant is usually caused by Alzheimer disease pathology with amyloid plaques and tau tangles. Progressive neuronal loss within specific components of the language network determines whether speech production, grammar, single-word comprehension or word retrieval and repetition are predominantly affected.","additionalDifferentials":["Alzheimer disease with a typical amnestic presentation","Behavioural variant frontotemporal dementia","Vascular cognitive impairment or aphasia from stroke","Corticobasal syndrome and progressive supranuclear palsy","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease and other rapidly progressive dementias","Brain tumour or structural lesion affecting language areas","Depression or another primary psychiatric disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed speech and language assessment to classify the aphasia variant","MRI of the brain to identify the pattern and asymmetry of focal atrophy","FDG-PET to demonstrate the distribution of cortical hypometabolism","Amyloid PET or cerebrospinal fluid amyloid and tau markers to detect Alzheimer pathology","Neuropsychological testing to document relative sparing of other cognitive domains","Genetic testing where a family history of frontotemporal dementia is present"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression or early involvement of multiple cognitive domains suggesting an alternative or aggressive process","Focal deficits or headache raising concern for a structural lesion","Emergent motor features indicating overlap with atypical parkinsonian syndromes","Communication failure compromising safety and decision-making capacity"],"prognosis":"Primary progressive aphasia is progressive, with language impairment worsening and additional cognitive, behavioural or motor deficits typically emerging over years; the rate and later features depend on the underlying pathology, and management is supportive with speech and language therapy and caregiver support.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the language decline as a psychiatric or purely age-related change","Failing to subtype the aphasia, which informs the likely underlying pathology","Not testing for Alzheimer biomarkers in a logopenic presentation","Overlooking evolving motor or behavioural features that reshape the diagnosis"],"management":["Individualised speech and language therapy tailored to the aphasia variant, with communication strategies and augmentative aids as the mainstay of care","Determination of the underlying pathology to guide symptomatic drug therapy, including a trial of a cholinesterase inhibitor where Alzheimer pathology underlies the logopenic variant","Non-pharmacological management of behavioural and neuropsychiatric symptoms as first line, with cautious and limited use of psychotropic agents","Occupational therapy for daily activities, plus monitoring of swallowing and nutrition and attention to safety as the disease advances","Carer education, psychological support and early advance care planning","Longitudinal monitoring for emerging cognitive, behavioural and motor features such as parkinsonism or motor neurone disease","Multidisciplinary care through cognitive and neurology services with social care input","Genetic counselling where there is a family history of frontotemporal dementia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_progressive_aphasia_logopenic":{"name":"Logopenic variant primary progressive aphasia","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["logopenic progressive aphasia","lvPPA","logopenic aphasia","logopenic variant PPA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with progressive difficulty in word retrieval and impaired repetition of sentences and phrases, with slow, hesitant speech punctuated by word-finding pauses","Single-word comprehension and object knowledge are relatively preserved, distinguishing it from the semantic variant","Speech is not frankly agrammatic and there is no motor speech apraxia, distinguishing it from the non-fluent or agrammatic variant","Phonological errors (phonemic paraphasias) are characteristic","The impaired repetition is thought to reflect a limitation of phonological short-term memory","The logopenic variant is most often underpinned by Alzheimer's disease pathology","Atrophy typically centres on the left temporoparietal junction and posterior superior temporal region"],"pathophysiology":"The logopenic variant of primary progressive aphasia arises from neurodegeneration of the left posterior temporal and inferior parietal cortex, regions important for phonological processing and verbal working memory. Impaired access to and maintenance of phonological representations produces the characteristic word-retrieval difficulty and the disproportionate impairment of sentence repetition. Unlike the other progressive aphasias, the logopenic syndrome is most commonly caused by underlying Alzheimer-type pathology rather than frontotemporal lobar degeneration.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-fluent or agrammatic variant primary progressive aphasia","Semantic variant primary progressive aphasia","Typical amnestic Alzheimer's disease","Vascular or post-stroke aphasia","Corticobasal syndrome with language involvement","Anxiety or depression causing subjective word-finding complaints"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive speech and language assessment characterising naming, repetition and comprehension","Neuropsychological evaluation of verbal working memory and other cognitive domains","MRI brain demonstrating left temporoparietal atrophy","FDG-PET showing left temporoparietal hypometabolism","Amyloid PET or CSF biomarkers to confirm or exclude Alzheimer pathology","Audiometry to exclude peripheral hearing loss contributing to communication difficulty"],"redFlags":["Sudden onset or stepwise deterioration suggesting a vascular cause","Rapidly progressive aphasia raising concern for prion or inflammatory disease","Focal signs or headache suggesting a structural lesion","Early prominent behavioural change indicating an alternative degenerative syndrome"],"prognosis":"Logopenic variant primary progressive aphasia is progressive; language deteriorates over time and, because the underlying pathology is frequently Alzheimer's disease, more generalised cognitive decline commonly supervenes. Speech and language therapy and communication aids can support function, but there is currently no disease-modifying treatment for the aphasia itself.","pitfalls":["Confusing the logopenic variant with the non-fluent variant because both feature effortful speech, despite the absence of agrammatism and apraxia","Attributing early symptoms to anxiety or normal ageing","Failing to pursue amyloid biomarkers, thereby missing treatable or trial-relevant Alzheimer pathology","Overlooking coexisting episodic memory impairment"],"management":["Speech and language therapy targeting word-retrieval and sentence-repetition deficits, with communication strategies and augmentative aids as the mainstay","Confirmation of the underlying Alzheimer pathology with amyloid PET or cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers to guide treatment","Trial of a cholinesterase inhibitor such as donepezil, adding memantine as more generalised Alzheimer-type cognitive decline emerges","Consideration of amyloid-directed disease-modifying antibody therapy in confirmed, eligible Alzheimer pathology where available","Non-pharmacological management of mood and neuropsychiatric symptoms, with attention to swallowing, nutrition and safety as the disease progresses","Carer education, psychological support and timely advance care planning","Multidisciplinary follow-up through cognitive neurology services with monitoring of cognitive progression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice, current edition","Gorno-Tempini et al., classification of primary progressive aphasia and its variants (Neurology)"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_stabbing_headache":{"name":"Primary stabbing headache","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ice-pick headache","jabs and jolts syndrome","idiopathic stabbing headache"],"chapter":null,"source":"International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Transient ultra-brief stabs of head pain lasting seconds, occurring spontaneously without an identifiable trigger","Pains are felt in the distribution of the first division of the trigeminal nerve in most cases but may move between areas","There are no accompanying cranial autonomic features, which helps distinguish it from the trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias","Attacks may occur once or many times a day with an irregular pattern","It is more common in people who also have migraine","A subset responds to indometacin, though pains are often too brief to warrant treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Primary stabbing headache is an idiopathic disorder thought to arise from transient spontaneous discharges within trigeminal nociceptive pathways, without structural cause in the primary form. Its association with migraine and other primary headaches suggests shared central pain-modulating mechanisms. When stabs are consistently localised to one fixed site, an underlying structural lesion in that region should be considered.","additionalDifferentials":["Trigeminal neuralgia","Short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache attacks (SUNCT/SUNA)","Nummular headache","Occipital neuralgia","Secondary stabbing pain from a structural lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on the characteristic brief stabs","Neurological examination to exclude focal signs","Neuroimaging if pains are strictly fixed in location or examination is abnormal","Review for coexisting migraine or other primary headache"],"redFlags":["Stabs consistently confined to one fixed location","Abnormal neurological examination","New onset in an older patient with other concerning features","Progressive change in pattern or associated deficits"],"prognosis":"The disorder is benign and often intermittent, with many patients needing no treatment and attacks tending to fluctuate over time.","pitfalls":["Assuming a fixed-site stab is primary without imaging","Confusing it with trigeminal neuralgia, which has cutaneous triggers","Overlooking coexisting migraine","Overtreating pains that are too brief to target"],"management":["Reassurance with no pharmacological treatment for most patients, since attacks are usually too brief to treat","Preventive indometacin for frequent or troublesome stabs, to which a subset responds well","Gastric protection with a proton pump inhibitor when indometacin is used","Alternative preventives such as gabapentin, melatonin or a cyclo-oxygenase-2 inhibitor when indometacin is ineffective or not tolerated","Identification and treatment of coexisting migraine or other primary headache","Neuroimaging and specialist referral if stabs are strictly fixed in one location or neurological examination is abnormal"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3)","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_thunderclap_headache":{"name":"Primary thunderclap headache","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["idiopathic thunderclap headache"],"chapter":null,"source":"International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A high-intensity headache of abrupt onset reaching maximum severity within one minute, mimicking a ruptured aneurysm","It is fundamentally a diagnosis of exclusion made only after serious secondary causes have been ruled out","Subarachnoid haemorrhage is the priority to exclude, followed by other vascular emergencies","Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome is a common cause of recurrent thunderclap headache and must be actively sought","Other secondary causes include cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, arterial dissection, pituitary apoplexy and spontaneous intracranial hypotension","Because the primary label carries risk, a thorough vascular and cerebrospinal fluid evaluation is essential"],"pathophysiology":"Thunderclap headache reflects sudden activation of pain-sensitive intracranial structures, most dangerously by acute vascular events such as aneurysmal subarachnoid bleeding or arterial vasoconstriction. In the primary form no cause is identified despite appropriate investigation, and its mechanism is poorly understood; it may overlap with disorders of cerebrovascular tone. The clinical imperative is that the presentation is treated as a vascular emergency until secondary causes are excluded.","additionalDifferentials":["Subarachnoid haemorrhage","Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Cervical or intracranial arterial dissection","Pituitary apoplexy","Spontaneous intracranial hypotension"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent non-contrast CT of the head","Lumbar puncture for xanthochromia if CT is non-diagnostic","CT or MR angiography and venography for vascular causes","MRI of the brain for apoplexy, intracranial hypotension and other lesions","Repeat vascular imaging if RCVS is suspected, as early studies can be normal"],"redFlags":["Any focal neurological deficit or reduced conscious level","Neck stiffness or photophobia suggesting subarachnoid blood","Recurrent thunderclap episodes suggesting vasoconstriction syndrome","Papilloedema or seizures"],"prognosis":"When secondary causes are excluded the primary form generally has a benign course, but the initial presentation always warrants urgent investigation because the dangerous mimics are time-critical.","pitfalls":["Labelling a headache primary before completing vascular and cerebrospinal fluid workup","Stopping at a normal early angiogram and missing RCVS","Overlooking venous sinus thrombosis or intracranial hypotension","Failing to exclude subarachnoid haemorrhage with lumbar puncture when CT is negative"],"management":["Immediate management as a vascular emergency, with urgent non-contrast CT of the head and lumbar puncture for xanthochromia to exclude subarachnoid haemorrhage","Cerebral CT or MR angiography and venography to exclude reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome, arterial dissection, cerebral venous sinus thrombosis and pituitary apoplexy","Where reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome is suspected, treatment with a calcium channel blocker such as nimodipine, withdrawal of vasoactive drugs, and repeat vascular imaging as early studies may be normal","Symptomatic analgesia, blood pressure control and avoidance of vasoconstrictive triggers","Reassurance about the benign course once secondary causes are confidently excluded","Neurology referral and clear safety-netting to re-present urgently if headache recurs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3)","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"prion_diseases":{"name":"Prion Diseases (CJD)","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease","CJD","spongiform encephalopathy"],"chapter":449,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3525","clinicalPearls":["CJD is a rapidly progressive disorder associated with dementia, focal cortical signs, rigidity, and myoclonus, causing death less than 1 year after first symptoms appear (p.3525-3526).","CJD is the classic cause of a rapidly progressive dementia (RPD: progression from symptom onset to dementia within a year or less), especially when associated with myoclonus, though more often RPD is due to AD, another neurodegenerative disorder, or autoimmune encephalitis (p.237).","On examination, CJD is suggested by diffuse rigidity, an akinetic-mute state, and prominent, often startle-sensitive myoclonus (p.239).","MRI diffusion-weighted imaging reveals restricted diffusion within the cortical ribbon and/or basal ganglia in most patients; basal ganglia or thalamus hyperintensity may also be seen on diffusion/FLAIR MRI (p.240, p.3526).","EEG is not routinely used but can suggest CJD by showing repetitive bursts of diffuse high-amplitude sharp waves (periodic complexes) (p.241).","Prion diseases such as CJD are rare (prevalence ~1 per million); the rapidity of progression distinguishes CJD from AD, though AD can occasionally present as a rapidly progressive dementia (p.3525-3526).","Prion disease is one cause of subacute progressive ataxia, especially when an infectious etiology such as transmission from contaminated human growth hormone is responsible (p.3590)."],"pathophysiology":"Prion diseases are caused by prions, host-encoded proteins (the prion protein) that adopt an alternative, abnormal conformation (misfolded PrPSc) which undergoes self-propagation. PrPSc imposes its conformation on the normal precursor protein, which in turn becomes self-templating, producing faithful copies; the misfolded protein is enriched for beta-sheet and assembles into amyloid fibrils. The amyloid plaques in kuru and CJD are filled with PrPSc prions assembled into fibrils. CJD is one of the major degenerative dementias characterized by abnormal aggregation of a specific protein (misfolded prion protein, PrPSc). Familial CJD is genetically linked to mutations in the PrP gene (p.237, p.3400-3401).","additionalDifferentials":["Alzheimer's disease (can occasionally present as rapidly progressive dementia)","Corticobasal degeneration (CBD) - rapid degenerative dementia with prominent movement abnormalities, more likely mistaken for CJD","Dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB) - rapid degenerative dementia with prominent movement abnormalities, more likely mistaken for CJD","Other rapidly progressive dementias"],"infectionMimics":["Viral encephalitides","Bacterial encephalitides","Other CNS infections causing rapidly progressive dementia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hashimoto's encephalopathy","CNS vasculitis","CNS lymphoma","Paraneoplastic/autoimmune syndromes (autoimmune encephalitis)","Prolonged focal or generalized seizures (can rarely produce a similar imaging appearance)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI diffusion-weighted imaging - restricted diffusion in cortical ribbon and/or basal ganglia (cortical ribboning); basal ganglia or thalamus hyperintensity on diffusion/FLAIR","EEG - periodic complexes (repetitive bursts of diffuse high-amplitude sharp waves)","Lumbar puncture/CSF - to exclude CNS infection or inflammation among rapidly progressive dementia mimics","MRI/neuroimaging to exclude treatable structural causes (neoplasm, subdural hematoma, NPH)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive dementia (onset to dementia within a year or less)","Dementia accompanied by myoclonus (often startle-sensitive)","Diffuse rigidity and akinetic-mute state","Subacute progressive ataxia","Cortical ribboning / basal ganglia or thalamic hyperintensity on diffusion/FLAIR MRI","Periodic complexes on EEG"],"prognosis":"Uniformly fatal. CJD causes death less than 1 year after the first symptoms appear (p.3525-3526).","pitfalls":["Must exclude treatable causes of rapidly progressive dementia (e.g., autoimmune/paraneoplastic encephalitis, Hashimoto's encephalopathy, CNS vasculitis, CNS infection, lymphoma) before attributing a rapidly progressive dementia to CJD.","More rapid degenerative dementias with prominent movement abnormalities (CBD, DLB) are more likely to be mistaken for CJD.","Rarely, prolonged focal or generalized seizures can produce an MRI appearance similar to CJD."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists; care is supportive and directed at comfort throughout the rapidly progressive, uniformly fatal course","Symptomatic control of myoclonus with antimyoclonic agents such as clonazepam, levetiracetam or valproate","Manage agitation, spasticity and rigidity, and treat distressing psychiatric and behavioural symptoms","Nursing care for progressive immobility and dysphagia, with attention to aspiration risk, skin integrity and nutrition/feeding decisions","Early palliative care involvement and advance care planning given the rapid decline and poor prognosis","Infection-control and public-health measures: statutory notification, single-use or specialised instrument decontamination for neurosurgical and ophthalmic procedures, and referral to the national prion surveillance unit","Genetic counselling and family testing for familial (PrP gene) disease"],"severityClassification":"Etiologic classification of prion diseases (all caused by PrPSc): sporadic (e.g., classic CJD), familial/genetic (linked to PrP gene mutations - includes familial CJD, Gerstmann-Straussler-Scheinker disease [GSS], and fatal insomnia), iatrogenic (transmission e.g. from contaminated human growth hormone), and acquired/transmissible forms (kuru). PrPSc prions also cause animal spongiform encephalopathies including scrapie, bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE), and chronic wasting disease (p.3400-3401, p.3590).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3525","Harrison 22e p.3526","Harrison 22e p.237","Harrison 22e p.239","Harrison 22e p.240","Harrison 22e p.241","Harrison 22e p.3400","Harrison 22e p.3401","Harrison 22e p.3590"],"crossLinks":["alzheimers_disease","movement_disorders"]},"prkag2_cardiomyopathy":{"name":"PRKAG2 cardiomyopathy","system":"Cardiovascular / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PRKAG2 syndrome","glycogen storage cardiomyopathy","PRKAG2 cardiac syndrome","AMP-activated protein kinase gamma-2 cardiomyopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard cardiology and inherited cardiac conditions references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in PRKAG2, encoding the gamma-2 regulatory subunit of AMP-activated protein kinase, leading to cardiac glycogen accumulation","The characteristic triad is ventricular pre-excitation (Wolff-Parkinson-White pattern), left ventricular hypertrophy, and progressive conduction system disease","It mimics hypertrophic cardiomyopathy on imaging but is a glycogen storage disorder rather than a sarcomeric disease","Ventricular pre-excitation predisposes to supraventricular tachyarrhythmia, while conduction disease often progresses to atrioventricular block requiring pacing","Atrial fibrillation is common and may be poorly tolerated in the presence of an accessory pathway","Inheritance is autosomal dominant, so family screening with ECG and imaging is important","The combination of a short PR interval or delta wave with unexplained hypertrophy should raise suspicion of this diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"PRKAG2 encodes a regulatory subunit of AMP-activated protein kinase, the cell's central energy sensor governing glucose and fatty acid metabolism. Activating or dysregulating variants disturb this energy-sensing function, causing excessive accumulation of glycogen within cardiomyocytes. Glycogen-laden myocytes produce ventricular hypertrophy that resembles hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, and glycogen deposition also disrupts the annulus fibrosus, allowing anomalous atrioventricular connections that generate ventricular pre-excitation. Progressive infiltration and degeneration of the conduction system cause worsening atrioventricular block over time, while the accessory conduction and atrial disease predispose to tachyarrhythmias. The disorder is thus a metabolic (glycogen storage) cardiomyopathy with a distinctive electrophysiological signature.","additionalDifferentials":["Sarcomeric hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Fabry disease and other storage cardiomyopathies","Danon disease (LAMP2 cardiomyopathy)","Cardiac amyloidosis","Isolated Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome","Athlete's heart and hypertensive hypertrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG for pre-excitation, short PR interval and conduction abnormalities","Echocardiography to assess the pattern and degree of hypertrophy","Cardiac MRI to characterise myocardium and distinguish from sarcomeric and infiltrative disease","Ambulatory ECG monitoring for tachyarrhythmia and progressive block","Genetic testing of PRKAG2 with cascade family screening","Assessment to exclude other storage disorders (for example Fabry and Danon disease)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly conducted atrial fibrillation in the presence of an accessory pathway","Progression to high-grade atrioventricular block or symptomatic bradycardia","Syncope or aborted sudden death","Severe or rapidly progressive hypertrophy with impaired function"],"prognosis":"The disorder tends to progress over years, with many patients ultimately needing pacing for conduction disease and management of tachyarrhythmias; heart failure can develop in advanced disease, and prognosis is improved by early rhythm surveillance and device therapy.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as sarcomeric hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and missing the glycogen storage basis","Overlooking pre-excitation as a clue to the underlying metabolic disorder","Underestimating the risk of rapidly conducted atrial fibrillation via an accessory pathway","Neglecting family screening for an autosomal dominant condition"],"management":["No therapy reverses the underlying cardiac glycogen accumulation; management targets arrhythmia, conduction disease and heart failure","Catheter ablation of accessory pathways for symptomatic ventricular pre-excitation and associated supraventricular tachyarrhythmia","Permanent pacemaker for progressive atrioventricular block, with a defibrillator considered where sudden-death or high-grade block risk warrants","Rate or rhythm control and anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation guided by stroke-risk assessment, with caution using atrioventricular-nodal blocking drugs in the presence of an accessory pathway","Heart failure therapy for left ventricular dysfunction in advanced disease","Ambulatory ECG surveillance and serial echocardiography to detect tachyarrhythmia and progressive conduction block","Autosomal dominant inheritance: PRKAG2 genetic testing with cascade ECG and imaging screening of relatives and genetic counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC guidelines on cardiomyopathies","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"proctalgia_fugax":{"name":"Proctalgia fugax","system":"Colorectal / Pelvic floor","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fleeting proctalgia","transient anorectal pain","anal spasm"],"chapter":null,"source":"ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by sudden, severe, cramping or stabbing pain in the anus or lower rectum lasting seconds to a few minutes, then resolving completely","Attacks are infrequent and often nocturnal, waking the patient from sleep, with no symptoms between episodes","Examination between attacks is normal, and it is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion","Distinguished from levator ani syndrome by the brevity of the episodes, seconds to minutes rather than twenty minutes or more","Thought to relate to transient spasm of the anal sphincter or pelvic floor smooth muscle","Because attacks are brief and self-limiting, treatment is largely reassurance; frequent severe episodes may respond to smooth-muscle relaxants, warm baths, or topical agents","A familial form associated with hereditary internal anal sphincter myopathy is recognised"],"pathophysiology":"Proctalgia fugax is a functional anorectal pain disorder attributed to paroxysmal spasm of the smooth muscle of the anal canal or pelvic floor, sometimes involving the internal anal sphincter. Attacks are brief and self-limiting with no structural correlate. Triggers include stress, sexual activity, and defecation, and a rare hereditary internal anal sphincter myopathy underlies some familial cases. Between episodes there is no demonstrable abnormality.","additionalDifferentials":["Levator ani syndrome","Anal fissure","Thrombosed haemorrhoid","Coccygodynia","Pudendal neuralgia","Anorectal abscess"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["History to confirm brief, self-limiting attacks with symptom-free intervals","Digital rectal examination and proctoscopy between attacks to exclude local pathology","Anorectal manometry in atypical or frequent cases","Endoanal ultrasound if hereditary internal sphincter myopathy is suspected (thickened internal sphincter)","Sigmoidoscopy or colonoscopy where alarm features warrant exclusion of other disease","Pelvic imaging where a structural or neurological cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rectal bleeding, weight loss, or a mass suggesting organic disease","Persistent rather than fleeting pain, pointing to another diagnosis","Progressive or nocturnal pain with neurological features","Onset in later life without any prior episodes"],"prognosis":"A benign condition; although attacks can be intensely painful, they are brief, self-limiting, and cause no lasting harm, with many patients experiencing only occasional episodes.","pitfalls":["Confusing the brief attacks with the sustained ache of levator ani syndrome","Over-investigating a classic, benign presentation","Missing an anal fissure or thrombosed haemorrhoid as the true cause","Overlooking the rare hereditary internal anal sphincter myopathy in familial cases"],"management":["Reassurance and explanation of the benign, self-limiting nature is the mainstay, as attacks are usually too brief for acute abortive drug treatment","For frequent or severe episodes, smooth-muscle relaxants such as topical glyceryl trinitrate or a topical calcium-channel blocker, or inhaled salbutamol, to shorten attacks","Warm baths and local warmth or digital perineal pressure for symptomatic relief during attacks","Address contributory triggers including stress and constipation, with stool softening and management of anxiety","For refractory frequent attacks, consider botulinum toxin injection to the anal sphincter or oral smooth-muscle relaxants under colorectal specialist review","Confirm normal anorectal examination and exclude structural pathology before reassurance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","Rome IV Functional Gastrointestinal Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"progeria_hutchinson_gilford":{"name":"Hutchinson-Gilford progeria syndrome","system":"Genetics / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HGPS","progeria","LMNA","progerin"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews (NCBI) and standard clinical genetics texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sporadic, almost always de novo dominant disorder caused by a recurrent LMNA variant that generates the toxic truncated lamin A protein progerin","Infants appear normal at birth but develop failure to thrive and characteristic features within the first one to two years","Distinctive appearance includes alopecia, prominent scalp veins, micrognathia, a beaked nose, prominent eyes and loss of subcutaneous fat","Skeletal changes include delayed dentition, coxa valga, a horse-riding stance and progressive joint contractures with distal resorption (acro-osteolysis)","Intelligence is preserved, distinguishing it from many other progeroid conditions","Progressive severe atherosclerosis leads to myocardial infarction and stroke, the principal causes of death","Skin becomes sclerodermatous and mottled with pigmentary change over the trunk","Diagnosis is clinical and confirmed by molecular testing for the characteristic LMNA variant"],"pathophysiology":"Hutchinson-Gilford progeria syndrome is usually caused by a de novo point variant in LMNA that activates a cryptic splice site, producing progerin, a truncated lamin A that cannot be properly processed and remains farnesylated. Accumulation of progerin distorts the nuclear envelope, destabilises the nuclear lamina and disrupts chromatin organisation, DNA repair and mechanotransduction. This drives premature cellular senescence and, in vascular smooth muscle, accelerated atherosclerosis that dominates the clinical course.","additionalDifferentials":["Werner syndrome (adult progeria)","Mandibuloacral dysplasia","Wiedemann-Rautenstrauch (neonatal progeroid) syndrome","Restrictive dermopathy and other laminopathies","Cockayne syndrome","Severe generalised lipodystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted molecular testing for the recurrent LMNA variant","Echocardiography and vascular imaging for atherosclerotic and cardiac disease","Lipid profile and cardiovascular risk assessment","Skeletal survey for acro-osteolysis, coxa valga and joint contractures","Dental review for delayed and crowded dentition","Growth and nutritional monitoring"],"redFlags":["Chest pain, syncope or other features of myocardial ischaemia","Focal neurological signs suggesting transient ischaemic attack or stroke","Rapidly worsening failure to thrive","Severe joint contractures compromising mobility and function"],"prognosis":"The condition is progressive and life-limiting, with death typically in the second decade, most often from myocardial infarction or stroke secondary to accelerated atherosclerosis. Multidisciplinary supportive care and cardiovascular risk management are central, and disease-modifying therapy targeting protein farnesylation has been developed.","pitfalls":["Confusing preserved intelligence with other progeroid syndromes that feature cognitive impairment","Underestimating cardiovascular risk in a young child and delaying cardiology involvement","Attributing early features to a non-syndromic failure to thrive","Overlooking the sclerodermatous skin as a diagnostic clue"],"management":["Disease-modifying therapy with the farnesyltransferase inhibitor lonafarnib, which reduces progerin toxicity and improves survival","Cardiovascular risk reduction with antiplatelet therapy and a statin, and active management of ischaemic heart disease and cerebrovascular events driven by accelerated atherosclerosis","Nutritional support for failure to thrive with frequent high-energy feeds and dietetic input","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy for joint contractures, with orthopaedic and podiatry care for coxa valga and other skeletal problems","Regular cardiology and vascular surveillance with echocardiography and vascular imaging, plus dental review for delayed and crowded dentition","Skin care, hydration and avoidance of trauma as part of general multidisciplinary supportive care","Genetic counselling for the family, noting most cases are sporadic de novo LMNA variants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (NCBI): Hutchinson-Gilford progeria syndrome","OMIM: Hutchinson-Gilford progeria syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_cardiac_conduction_defect":{"name":"Progressive cardiac conduction defect","system":"Cardiovascular / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCCD","Lenegre disease","Lenegre-Lev disease","progressive cardiac conduction disease","SCN5A conduction disease","hereditary bundle branch disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard cardiology and inherited cardiac conditions references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A progressive disorder of the His-Purkinje conduction system causing widening QRS, bundle branch and fascicular block, and increasing degrees of atrioventricular block over time","Loss-of-function variants in SCN5A (the cardiac sodium channel gene) are the classic inherited cause, with several other genes implicated","Presents with syncope, presyncope or bradycardia-related symptoms, and may cause sudden death if complete heart block occurs without escape rhythm","The structurally normal (idiopathic) form is termed Lenegre disease; a variant with fibrocalcific involvement of the conduction system is Lev disease","SCN5A conduction disease overlaps with Brugada syndrome and other sodium-channel phenotypes, sometimes in the same family (overlap syndromes)","Serial ECGs are key because the conduction disturbance worsens with age","Management is guided by symptoms and the level and severity of block, with cardiac pacing central to symptomatic or high-grade conduction disease"],"pathophysiology":"Progressive cardiac conduction defect reflects impaired impulse propagation through the specialised conduction tissue. In the SCN5A-related form, loss-of-function variants reduce cardiac sodium current, slowing conduction velocity through the His-Purkinje system and predisposing to bundle branch and atrioventricular block. Ageing-related fibrosis of the conduction system compounds the genetic defect, so block worsens over time, giving the characteristic progressive course. Because the same sodium-channel dysfunction underlies Brugada syndrome, families may show mixed conduction disease and Brugada-type phenotypes. When advanced block develops without an adequate ventricular escape rhythm, profound bradycardia and asystole can cause syncope or sudden death.","additionalDifferentials":["Age-related idiopathic conduction system fibrosis","Ischaemic conduction disease from coronary artery disease","Infiltrative cardiomyopathy (amyloidosis, sarcoidosis)","LMNA cardiomyopathy with conduction disease","Brugada syndrome and other sodium-channelopathies","Iatrogenic block from atrioventricular nodal-blocking drugs"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial 12-lead ECGs to document progression of conduction block","Ambulatory ECG monitoring to capture intermittent high-grade block","Echocardiography to exclude structural or infiltrative heart disease","Cardiac MRI where infiltrative or inflammatory disease is suspected","Genetic testing (SCN5A and related genes) with cascade family screening","Electrophysiological study in selected cases to localise the level of block"],"redFlags":["Syncope with high-grade or complete atrioventricular block","Very wide QRS with alternating bundle branch block","Family history of pacemaker requirement or sudden death at a young age","Coexisting features suggesting an infiltrative or lamin-related cardiomyopathy"],"prognosis":"With timely recognition and pacing for symptomatic or high-grade block, prognosis is generally favourable, but untreated advanced conduction disease carries a risk of syncope and sudden death, and overlap phenotypes may add arrhythmic risk.","pitfalls":["Assuming conduction block is purely degenerative and missing an inherited SCN5A basis","Overlooking an overlapping Brugada or lamin phenotype that changes risk stratification","Failing to monitor serially so progressive block is detected late","Not screening relatives despite a clear autosomal dominant pattern"],"management":["Permanent pacemaker implantation is the mainstay for symptomatic or high-grade atrioventricular and His-Purkinje conduction block","No drug corrects the conduction defect; avoid agents that further impair conduction such as sodium-channel blockers and atrioventricular-nodal blocking drugs where possible","Consider a defibrillator rather than a pacemaker alone where a coexisting Brugada-type phenotype or other arrhythmic and sudden-death risk is present","Serial 12-lead ECGs and ambulatory monitoring to track progression and capture intermittent high-grade block","Assess for and manage associated arrhythmias and overlapping sodium-channel phenotypes","SCN5A and related gene testing with cascade family screening and genetic counselling","Advise on syncope safety and escalate urgently for pacing when block advances"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC guidelines on cardiac pacing and conduction disease","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_cardiac_conduction_disease":{"name":"Progressive cardiac conduction disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lenegre disease","Lev disease","hereditary bundle branch disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cardiology references / inherited arrhythmia literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Progressive impairment of impulse conduction through the His-Purkinje system, causing widening QRS and heart block","It presents with syncope, bradycardia or sudden death, and may be familial or degenerative","The inherited form is associated with SCN5A and other conduction-gene mutations and can present at a younger age","SCN5A mutations link it to an overlap spectrum with Brugada syndrome and other sodium-channel disorders","It differs from age-related conduction fibrosis chiefly by earlier onset and family history","A permanent pacemaker is the mainstay of treatment for symptomatic or high-grade conduction block"],"pathophysiology":"Progressive cardiac conduction disease results from progressive fibrosis and functional impairment of the specialised conduction tissue. In inherited forms, mutations in genes encoding the cardiac sodium channel (SCN5A) and other conduction-related proteins reduce conduction reserve, so conduction slows and blocks over time, producing bundle branch block, then higher-grade atrioventricular block. Age-related degeneration (Lenegre-Lev disease) causes a similar picture through sclerosis of the conduction system. The shared SCN5A basis explains overlap with Brugada syndrome in some families.","additionalDifferentials":["Age-related (idiopathic) conduction system fibrosis","Ischaemic conduction disease","Cardiomyopathy with conduction disease (for example laminopathy)","Sarcoidosis or other infiltrative disease","Brugada syndrome overlap","Drug-induced conduction block"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial electrocardiograms documenting conduction progression","Ambulatory monitoring for intermittent block","Echocardiography and, where indicated, cardiac MRI to exclude structural or infiltrative disease","Genetic testing (for example SCN5A) in early-onset or familial cases","Family screening"],"redFlags":["Syncope or high-grade atrioventricular block","Rapidly progressive conduction disease","Family history of sudden death or pacemakers at a young age","Associated structural or infiltrative cardiac disease"],"prognosis":"With timely pacing, symptomatic conduction disease is well managed; prognosis otherwise depends on associated arrhythmic risk and any underlying cardiomyopathy or channelopathy.","pitfalls":["Attributing early-onset conduction disease to ageing and missing a genetic cause","Overlooking an infiltrative cardiomyopathy such as sarcoidosis","Missing associated Brugada-type risk in SCN5A families","Delaying pacing in high-grade block"],"management":["Permanent pacemaker is the definitive treatment for symptomatic bradycardia or high-grade conduction block","No pharmacological therapy corrects the underlying conduction failure; avoid drugs that slow conduction such as class I antiarrhythmics and atrioventricular-nodal blocking agents unless clearly indicated","Consider defibrillator therapy where an overlapping Brugada phenotype or an underlying cardiomyopathy adds arrhythmic risk","Serial ECGs and ambulatory monitoring to document progression and detect intermittent block","Echocardiography and, where indicated, cardiac MRI to exclude structural or infiltrative disease","Genetic testing such as SCN5A in early-onset or familial cases, with cascade family screening and counselling","Educate on syncope and escalate urgently for pacing when complete heart block develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease","Inherited cardiac conditions guidelines (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_encephalomyelitis_with_rigidity":{"name":"Progressive Encephalomyelitis with Rigidity and Myoclonus (PERM)","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PERM","Progressive encephalomyelitis with rigidity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe variant at the extreme end of the stiff-person spectrum, combining axial and limb rigidity with brainstem, autonomic and long-tract involvement.","Presents subacutely with painful muscle stiffness and spasms, prominent stimulus-sensitive and spontaneous myoclonus, and exaggerated startle.","Brainstem features distinguish it from classic stiff-person syndrome: ophthalmoplegia, nystagmus, dysarthria, dysphagia and gaze palsies.","Dysautonomia is common and potentially fatal, with labile blood pressure, arrhythmia, hyperhidrosis and thermoregulatory instability.","Most cases are associated with glycine receptor antibodies; a subset carry anti-GAD or amphiphysin antibodies, the latter flagging paraneoplastic disease.","A paraneoplastic search is essential, particularly for breast carcinoma, thymoma, lymphoma and small cell lung cancer.","Electromyography shows continuous motor unit activity with co-contraction of agonists and antagonists, and startle studies show exaggerated responses.","Immunotherapy often produces marked improvement, contrasting with the historically poor outcome of untreated disease, so early recognition is vital."],"pathophysiology":"Progressive encephalomyelitis with rigidity and myoclonus is an autoimmune disorder of central nervous system inhibitory neurotransmission affecting the spinal cord and brainstem. In the majority of patients, antibodies target the alpha-1 subunit of the glycine receptor, a key inhibitory receptor concentrated in spinal and brainstem interneurons; a further group harbour antibodies against glutamic acid decarboxylase, the enzyme synthesising the inhibitory transmitter GABA, and a smaller paraneoplastic group carry amphiphysin antibodies. Loss of glycinergic and GABAergic inhibition removes the normal restraint on alpha motor neurons and interneuronal circuits, producing continuous involuntary firing that manifests as simultaneous co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles, giving the characteristic rigidity, superimposed painful spasms, myoclonus and pathologically enhanced startle. Because the brainstem is involved, cranial nerve nuclei and central autonomic pathways are also affected, accounting for oculomotor disturbance, bulbar dysfunction and the dangerous dysautonomia. Pathologically there is perivascular lymphocytic inflammation and neuronal loss in the grey matter of the cord and brainstem. The glycine receptor antibodies are considered directly pathogenic and their frequent responsiveness to immunotherapy, together with paraneoplastic associations in a proportion of cases, firmly establishes the disorder as antibody-mediated autoimmunity.","additionalDifferentials":["Classic stiff-person syndrome and its focal variants","Tetanus","Neuromyotonia and other peripheral hyperexcitability syndromes","Serotonin syndrome or neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Hereditary and acquired hyperekplexia","Brainstem encephalitis of other autoimmune or infective cause","Strychnine poisoning"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and cerebrospinal fluid glycine receptor, anti-GAD and amphiphysin antibody testing","Electromyography demonstrating continuous motor unit activity and agonist-antagonist co-contraction","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis for pleocytosis, oligoclonal bands and inflammatory markers","Whole-body cross-sectional imaging and tumour markers to screen for underlying malignancy","Brain and spinal cord MRI to identify inflammatory change and exclude structural mimics","Startle reflex and exaggerated acoustic startle neurophysiology studies","Autonomic function testing where dysautonomia is suspected"],"redFlags":["Autonomic instability with labile blood pressure or cardiac arrhythmia","Laryngospasm, bulbar dysfunction or respiratory muscle involvement threatening the airway","Rapidly progressive rigidity with hyperthermia","Features suggesting an underlying tumour such as weight loss or a palpable mass","Reduced consciousness or seizures indicating widespread encephalitis"],"prognosis":"Untreated disease historically progressed to severe disability or death, but early and sustained immunotherapy frequently yields substantial recovery. Prognosis is worse when dysautonomia is severe or when an underlying malignancy is not identified and treated.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the condition as tetanus or a functional disorder and delaying immunotherapy","Failing to test for glycine receptor antibodies when only anti-GAD is considered","Overlooking an occult tumour and thereby missing a paraneoplastic driver","Underestimating life-threatening dysautonomia and respiratory compromise","Attributing rigidity solely to a primary movement disorder and omitting antibody testing"],"management":["First-line immunotherapy with corticosteroids combined with intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange to remove pathogenic glycine-receptor and related antibodies","Escalation to second-line immunosuppression such as rituximab or cyclophosphamide in refractory or relapsing disease","Symptomatic control of rigidity, spasms and myoclonus with GABAergic agents such as benzodiazepines and baclofen","Detect and treat life-threatening dysautonomia in a high-dependency or critical care setting, with cardiac rhythm, blood pressure and temperature monitoring and ventilatory and swallowing support as needed","Screen for and treat an underlying malignancy, particularly breast carcinoma, thymoma, lymphoma and small cell lung cancer, especially with amphiphysin antibodies","Multidisciplinary neurology and immunology care with rehabilitation, physiotherapy and long-term relapse surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology (standard reference)","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice (standard reference)","Autoimmune neurology consensus literature (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_familial_cholestasis":{"name":"Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PFIC","progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis","Byler disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AASLD","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of autosomal recessive disorders of bile formation presenting with chronic intrahepatic cholestasis, usually beginning in infancy or early childhood","Severe, often disabling pruritus and jaundice with progression toward cirrhosis is the typical course","Serum gamma-glutamyl transferase helps subtype the disease: it is characteristically low or normal in the ATP8B1 (PFIC1) and ABCB11 (PFIC2) forms but elevated in the ABCB4 (PFIC3) form","PFIC2, caused by defective bile salt export pump (ABCB11), carries an increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma early in life","PFIC1 (ATP8B1) is associated with extrahepatic features such as diarrhoea, pancreatitis, and hearing loss because the defect is not confined to the liver","PFIC3 reflects impaired phospholipid transport across the canalicular membrane, leading to bile that damages the biliary epithelium","Fat malabsorption with failure to thrive and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency requires nutritional support","Options include measures to interrupt bile acid enterohepatic circulation, biliary diversion surgery, and liver transplantation for end-stage disease, with newer bile acid transport inhibitors used for pruritus"],"pathophysiology":"PFIC comprises inherited defects in the proteins that generate and secrete bile at the hepatocyte canalicular membrane. In the ATP8B1-related form (PFIC1) a defective aminophospholipid flippase destabilises the canalicular membrane and impairs bile secretion, with effects in other epithelia. In the ABCB11-related form (PFIC2) the bile salt export pump fails, causing intracellular accumulation of toxic bile acids and hepatocyte injury. In the ABCB4-related form (PFIC3) deficient phospholipid translocation leaves bile depleted of protective phospholipid, so unbuffered bile acids injure the bile ducts and produce a high-GGT cholestasis. Additional subtypes involve tight-junction and other transport proteins. Retained bile acids drive pruritus, progressive fibrosis, and eventual cirrhosis, while reduced intestinal bile delivery causes fat and vitamin malabsorption.","additionalDifferentials":["Alagille syndrome","Biliary atresia","Benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis","Bile acid synthesis defects","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Cystic fibrosis liver disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum GGT level to help discriminate transporter subtypes","Genetic testing for ATP8B1, ABCB11, ABCB4, and other implicated genes","Serum and urinary bile acid profiling to exclude synthesis defects","Liver biopsy with immunostaining for canalicular transport proteins","Assessment of fat-soluble vitamin status and growth","Surveillance imaging and alpha-fetoprotein for hepatocellular carcinoma in high-risk subtypes"],"redFlags":["Progression to cirrhosis with hepatic decompensation","Development of hepatocellular carcinoma, particularly in the bile salt export pump defect","Intractable pruritus severely impairing quality of life","Bleeding due to vitamin K-dependent coagulopathy"],"prognosis":"Untreated disease tends to progress to cirrhosis and liver failure during childhood; biliary diversion and pharmacological interruption of bile acid recycling can improve symptoms and slow progression in some subtypes, while liver transplantation is often required for end-stage disease, and there is a subtype-specific risk of hepatocellular carcinoma.","pitfalls":["Ignoring the diagnostic value of the GGT level when subtyping cholestasis","Missing hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance in the ABCB11 (PFIC2) form","Overlooking extrahepatic manifestations in the ATP8B1 (PFIC1) form","Failing to correct fat-soluble vitamin deficiency and support nutrition"],"management":["Interrupt enterohepatic bile acid recycling with an ileal bile acid transporter inhibitor such as odevixibat to relieve pruritus and lower bile acid load, with ursodeoxycholic acid particularly in the ABCB4 (PFIC3) subtype","Intensive antipruritic therapy with rifampicin, bile acid sequestrants and antihistamines for disabling itch","Fat-soluble vitamin (A, D, E and K) supplementation and high-energy medium-chain-triglyceride nutritional support with growth monitoring","Surgical biliary diversion to interrupt the enterohepatic circulation and slow progression in selected low-GGT subtypes","Liver transplantation for end-stage liver disease or intractable symptoms","Hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance with alpha-fetoprotein and imaging in high-risk subtypes such as BSEP (PFIC2) disease","Genetic counselling and family testing for this autosomal recessive condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AASLD guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_familial_intrahepatic_cholestasis":{"name":"Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PFIC","ATP8B1","FIC1 deficiency","ABCB11","BSEP deficiency","ABCB4","MDR3 deficiency","PFIC type 1","PFIC type 2","PFIC type 3"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of autosomal recessive cholestatic disorders presenting in infancy or early childhood with progressive cholestasis, intractable pruritus, and failure to thrive","PFIC type 1 (ATP8B1/FIC1) and type 2 (ABCB11/BSEP) characteristically show a low or normal serum gamma-glutamyl transferase despite marked cholestasis","PFIC type 3 (ABCB4/MDR3) shows a raised gamma-glutamyl transferase because impaired biliary phospholipid secretion damages the bile duct epithelium","FIC1 disease is a systemic defect and may be accompanied by diarrhoea, pancreatitis, and sensorineural hearing loss beyond the liver","BSEP deficiency carries a significant risk of hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma, warranting tumour surveillance","Serum bile acids are markedly elevated while serum cholesterol is often normal or low, helping distinguish PFIC from other paediatric liver disease","Management centres on relieving pruritus, nutritional support with fat-soluble vitamins, ileal bile acid transport inhibitors or biliary diversion, and liver transplantation for end-stage disease"],"pathophysiology":"PFIC comprises several inherited defects in hepatocyte proteins that maintain bile formation and flow. FIC1 (ATP8B1) is an aminophospholipid flippase whose loss disrupts the canalicular membrane; BSEP (ABCB11) is the main bile salt export pump, so its failure traps bile acids inside hepatocytes; and MDR3 (ABCB4) translocates phosphatidylcholine into bile, so its deficiency yields bile that is toxic to the biliary tree. In each case impaired bile acid secretion produces intracellular accumulation of bile acids, hepatocyte injury, and progressive cholestasis that evolves to fibrosis and cirrhosis. The characteristic difference in gamma-glutamyl transferase reflects whether the underlying lesion damages the bile duct epithelium (high, as in MDR3 disease) or not (low or normal, as in FIC1 and BSEP disease).","additionalDifferentials":["Biliary atresia","Alagille syndrome","Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency","Bile acid synthesis defects","Neonatal sclerosing cholangitis","Total parenteral nutrition-associated cholestasis","Benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis (a milder allelic condition)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum bile acids, bilirubin fractions, and gamma-glutamyl transferase to define the cholestatic pattern","Molecular genetic testing of ATP8B1, ABCB11, ABCB4 and related genes","Liver biopsy with immunostaining for BSEP and MDR3 and assessment of fibrosis","Fat-soluble vitamin levels and coagulation studies to gauge malabsorption","Abdominal ultrasound and cholangiography to exclude biliary obstruction and atresia","Alpha-fetoprotein and imaging surveillance for hepatobiliary malignancy in BSEP disease"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive jaundice with coagulopathy indicating hepatic decompensation","Rising alpha-fetoprotein or a hepatic mass suggesting hepatocellular carcinoma in BSEP deficiency","Severe intractable pruritus with sleep disruption and self-mutilation","Growth failure and signs of fat-soluble vitamin deficiency such as bleeding or rickets"],"prognosis":"Without intervention most subtypes progress to biliary cirrhosis and liver failure during childhood, though disease-modifying therapy such as ileal bile acid transport inhibitors, surgical biliary diversion, and liver transplantation can markedly improve outcomes. BSEP disease additionally carries a lifelong risk of hepatobiliary malignancy requiring surveillance.","pitfalls":["Attributing a normal or low gamma-glutamyl transferase to a benign process rather than recognising low-GGT cholestasis of FIC1/BSEP disease","Overlooking extrahepatic features of FIC1 disease such as diarrhoea, pancreatitis, and hearing loss","Failing to institute malignancy surveillance in BSEP deficiency","Neglecting aggressive fat-soluble vitamin supplementation and nutritional support","Assuming liver transplantation cures FIC1 disease, since extrahepatic manifestations and post-transplant steatohepatitis or diarrhoea may persist"],"management":["Ileal bile acid transporter inhibitors such as odevixibat or maralixibat to interrupt enterohepatic bile acid recycling and reduce pruritus and hepatocellular bile acid load","Ursodeoxycholic acid, which is especially beneficial in the ABCB4 (MDR3/PFIC3) form","Aggressive pruritus control with rifampicin, bile acid sequestrants and other antipruritics","Nutritional rehabilitation with medium-chain-triglyceride-based feeds and fat-soluble vitamin replacement, monitoring growth and coagulation","Surgical biliary diversion for symptom relief and disease modification, with liver transplantation for decompensated disease","Alpha-fetoprotein and imaging surveillance for hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma in BSEP (PFIC2) disease","Genetic counselling and cascade testing for this autosomal recessive condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_familial_intrahepatic_cholestasis_type4":{"name":"Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis type 4","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PFIC4","PFIC type 4","TJP2 deficiency","tight junction protein 2 cholestasis","low-GGT familial intrahepatic cholestasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / Suchy Liver Disease in Children","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive cholestatic disorder caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in TJP2, encoding tight junction protein 2","Presents in infancy with cholestasis, pruritus, jaundice, and fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption","Characteristically has a low or normal serum gamma-glutamyl transferase, like PFIC types 1 and 2","Coagulopathy from vitamin K deficiency can be an early and dangerous manifestation","TJP2 maintains the integrity of the hepatocyte tight junction at the bile canaliculus, so its loss allows bile to leak and injure hepatocytes","There is an associated risk of hepatocellular carcinoma, warranting long-term surveillance","Management is supportive with ursodeoxycholic acid, fat-soluble vitamins, antipruritic measures, biliary diversion in selected cases, and liver transplantation for progressive disease"],"pathophysiology":"PFIC type 4 is caused by biallelic mutations in TJP2, which encodes a scaffolding protein of the hepatocyte tight junction that seals the bile canaliculus. Loss of functional TJP2 disrupts the paracellular barrier between hepatocytes, allowing concentrated bile to regurgitate into the hepatocyte and paracellular space, causing cholestasis and progressive hepatocellular injury. The gamma-glutamyl transferase remains low or normal because canalicular membrane enzymes are not shed as in obstructive cholestasis. Chronic injury may progress to fibrosis and cirrhosis, and there is an increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma.","additionalDifferentials":["PFIC type 1 (ATP8B1) and type 2 (ABCB11)","PFIC type 5 (NR1H4/FXR)","Bile acid synthesis disorders","Familial hypercholanaemia","Benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis","Alagille syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum bile acids and gamma-glutamyl transferase (characteristically low/normal GGT)","Liver function tests and fractionated bilirubin","Coagulation profile and fat-soluble vitamin levels","Liver biopsy with immunostaining for TJP2 and assessment of fibrosis","Genetic testing of TJP2 and the wider PFIC gene panel","Alpha-fetoprotein and imaging for hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance"],"redFlags":["Vitamin K-dependent coagulopathy with intracranial or other bleeding","Progression to cirrhosis with portal hypertension","Development of hepatocellular carcinoma","Intractable pruritus with severe impairment of quality of life"],"prognosis":"The course is variable, ranging from milder cholestasis to progressive liver disease requiring transplantation; the increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma means lifelong surveillance is important even when liver function appears preserved.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal GGT excludes significant inherited cholestasis","Failing to institute hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance","Overlooking vitamin K deficiency as a cause of bleeding in infancy","Not recognising the disorder within the broader PFIC differential when genotype is unknown"],"management":["Interrupt enterohepatic bile acid recycling with an ileal bile acid transporter inhibitor and use ursodeoxycholic acid to reduce cholestatic bile acid load and pruritus","Antipruritic therapy with rifampicin and bile acid sequestrants for troublesome itch","Fat-soluble vitamin supplementation with early and vigilant vitamin K replacement to correct the coagulopathy of vitamin K deficiency","Nutritional support with medium-chain-triglyceride-enriched feeds and growth monitoring","Liver transplantation for progressive liver failure, with biliary diversion in selected cases","Lifelong hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance with alpha-fetoprotein and liver imaging, given persistent risk even when synthetic function appears preserved","Genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive TJP2 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Suchy, Sokol & Balistreri, Liver Disease in Children"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_familial_intrahepatic_cholestasis_type5":{"name":"Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis type 5","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PFIC5","PFIC type 5","NR1H4 deficiency","FXR deficiency","farnesoid X receptor cholestasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / Suchy Liver Disease in Children","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe autosomal recessive cholestasis caused by biallelic mutations in NR1H4, which encodes the farnesoid X receptor (FXR)","Presents in the neonatal period with rapidly progressive cholestasis and a low or normal gamma-glutamyl transferase","A striking early feature is a severe vitamin K-independent coagulopathy, reflecting profoundly impaired hepatic synthetic function","Serum alpha-fetoprotein is characteristically very high","FXR is the master nuclear bile acid sensor regulating bile acid synthesis and transport; its loss impairs canalicular bile salt export (BSEP) and disrupts bile acid homeostasis","Liver failure often develops in infancy, and the disease tends to be more aggressive than other PFIC subtypes","Management is supportive with fat-soluble vitamins, correction of coagulopathy, and early consideration of liver transplantation, which is often required"],"pathophysiology":"PFIC type 5 results from biallelic loss-of-function mutations in NR1H4, encoding the farnesoid X receptor, the principal nuclear receptor sensing bile acids within the hepatocyte. FXR normally regulates transcription of genes controlling bile acid synthesis, conjugation, and canalicular export, including the bile salt export pump. Its absence causes failure of canalicular bile salt transport, accumulation of toxic bile acids, and disrupted bile acid homeostasis, producing severe neonatal cholestasis with a low/normal GGT, marked coagulopathy, high alpha-fetoprotein, and early progression to liver failure.","additionalDifferentials":["PFIC type 1 (ATP8B1) and type 2 (ABCB11)","PFIC type 4 (TJP2)","Neonatal haemochromatosis","Bile acid synthesis disorders","Mitochondrial hepatopathy","Neonatal hepatitis of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum bile acids and gamma-glutamyl transferase (characteristically low/normal GGT)","Coagulation profile (severe vitamin K-independent coagulopathy)","Serum alpha-fetoprotein (typically markedly elevated)","Liver biopsy with immunostaining showing absent BSEP/FXR expression","Fat-soluble vitamin levels and liver synthetic markers","Genetic testing of NR1H4 and the PFIC gene panel"],"redFlags":["Severe coagulopathy unresponsive to vitamin K with bleeding","Rapidly progressive neonatal liver failure","Markedly raised alpha-fetoprotein","Ascites, hypoglycaemia, or encephalopathy indicating decompensation"],"prognosis":"PFIC type 5 is one of the most severe cholestatic syndromes, frequently causing liver failure in infancy and requiring early liver transplantation; without transplantation the prognosis is poor.","pitfalls":["Attributing the coagulopathy to vitamin K deficiency alone and expecting a response to replacement","Interpreting a low GGT as excluding significant cholestasis","Delaying referral for transplant assessment given the aggressive course","Overlooking the very high alpha-fetoprotein as a diagnostic clue"],"management":["Early referral for liver transplantation, which is usually required as the definitive treatment given rapid progression to infantile liver failure","Supportive management of severe synthetic liver failure, including correction of the coagulopathy with vitamin K and clotting factor or plasma support and treatment of complications of liver failure","Fat-soluble vitamin supplementation and medium-chain-triglyceride nutritional support to counter malabsorption and support growth","Antipruritic measures such as bile acid sequestrants and rifampicin, with consideration of bile acid recycling interruption for symptom relief","Alpha-fetoprotein and imaging surveillance and close monitoring of synthetic function while awaiting transplantation","Genetic counselling and family testing for this autosomal recessive NR1H4 (farnesoid X receptor) disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Suchy, Sokol & Balistreri, Liver Disease in Children"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_familial_intrahepatic_cholestasis_type_1":{"name":"Progressive Familial Intrahepatic Cholestasis Type 1","system":"Hepatology / Inherited cholestasis","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PFIC1","Byler disease","FIC1 deficiency","ATP8B1 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Inherited cholestasis references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive low-GGT cholestasis caused by ATP8B1 mutations encoding FIC1, a canalicular aminophospholipid flippase; serum bile acids are markedly raised while GGT is characteristically low or normal.","The hallmark distinguishing PFIC1 from PFIC2 is prominent extrahepatic disease, reflecting FIC1 expression beyond the liver: watery secretory diarrhoea, pancreatitis, sensorineural hearing loss and a raised sweat chloride.","Intractable pruritus is often severe and out of proportion to the degree of jaundice, dominating quality of life and driving management.","Growth failure and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency are prominent and frequently precede overt hepatic decompensation.","Coarse, granular canalicular bile (Byler bile) on electron microscopy is a characteristic ultrastructural clue.","Allelic disorders form a spectrum: benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis type 1 (BRIC1) is a milder, episodic phenotype, and ATP8B1 variants also contribute to intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.","Surgical biliary diversion can relieve pruritus and may slow hepatic progression in selected children.","Liver transplantation corrects the hepatic disease but does not resolve extrahepatic features and may worsen diarrhoea and produce steatosis of the graft."],"pathophysiology":"ATP8B1 encodes FIC1, a P4-type ATPase that flips aminophospholipids, notably phosphatidylserine, from the outer to the inner leaflet of the canalicular membrane, preserving membrane asymmetry and protecting the hepatocyte apical surface from the detergent action of luminal bile salts. Loss of FIC1 function destabilises the canalicular membrane and impairs bile salt secretion, in part through disturbed farnesoid X receptor signalling and secondary downregulation of the bile salt export pump, so cytotoxic bile acids accumulate within hepatocytes and drive progressive cholestatic injury and fibrosis. Because FIC1 is also expressed in cholangiocytes, enterocytes, pancreatic and airway epithelia and the cochlea, deficiency produces the characteristic extrahepatic manifestations of secretory diarrhoea, pancreatitis, elevated sweat electrolytes and sensorineural deafness that set this subtype apart from the liver-restricted transporter defects.","additionalDifferentials":["PFIC type 2 (ABCB11/BSEP deficiency) — also low GGT but liver-restricted, no diarrhoea or deafness","PFIC type 3 (ABCB4/MDR3 deficiency) — high GGT","TJP2 deficiency and MYO5B-related cholestasis","Inborn errors of bile acid synthesis","Alagille syndrome","Biliary atresia and neonatal sclerosing cholangitis","Cystic fibrosis (given raised sweat chloride overlap)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum total bile acids (markedly raised) with paired serum GGT (low or normal)","Molecular genetic testing of ATP8B1","Liver biopsy with electron microscopy looking for coarse granular Byler bile","Sweat chloride testing","Fat-soluble vitamin levels and coagulation profile","Audiometry for sensorineural hearing loss","Assessment of pancreatic function where pancreatitis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Intractable pruritus with excoriation, sleep deprivation and mutilating skin damage","Coagulopathy and bleeding from vitamin K malabsorption","Progression to cirrhosis with portal hypertension","Severe failure to thrive with persistent diarrhoea and electrolyte disturbance"],"prognosis":"Untreated disease progresses to cirrhosis and end-stage liver disease during childhood, though biliary diversion can relieve pruritus and slow progression in some. Extrahepatic features persist and diarrhoea and graft steatosis may worsen after transplantation.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a low or normal GGT as reassuring or as evidence against significant cholestasis","Expecting liver transplantation to cure the extrahepatic manifestations","Overlooking sensorineural hearing loss or pancreatitis as related features","Misattributing the secretory diarrhoea to an unrelated functional or infective cause"],"management":["Interrupt enterohepatic bile acid recycling with an ileal bile acid transporter inhibitor such as odevixibat to relieve the intractable pruritus and lower bile acid load","Additional antipruritic therapy with rifampicin, bile acid sequestrants and antihistamines","Fat-soluble vitamin replacement and high-energy medium-chain-triglyceride nutrition, with treatment of secretory diarrhoea and close growth monitoring","Surgical biliary diversion to relieve pruritus and slow hepatic progression, often preferred over transplantation because grafts may develop steatosis and diarrhoea can worsen post-transplant","Monitor and manage the extrahepatic manifestations of FIC1 deficiency, including pancreatitis, sensorineural hearing loss with audiometry, and secretory diarrhoea","Liver transplantation reserved for end-stage disease, with counselling about persistent extrahepatic features and graft steatosis","Genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive ATP8B1 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric hepatology reference texts","Inherited cholestasis clinical guidelines","Clinical genetics of liver disease references"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_familial_intrahepatic_cholestasis_type_2":{"name":"Progressive Familial Intrahepatic Cholestasis Type 2","system":"Hepatology / Inherited cholestasis","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PFIC2","BSEP deficiency","ABCB11 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Inherited cholestasis references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive low-GGT cholestasis caused by ABCB11 mutations encoding BSEP, the principal canalicular bile salt export pump; because expression is essentially liver-restricted, there are no extrahepatic features, in contrast with PFIC1.","Onset is typically neonatal and progression is more rapid than PFIC1, often with giant-cell hepatitis on biopsy.","Serum bile acids are markedly elevated with a disproportionately low GGT, and alpha-fetoprotein may be raised.","There is a substantial risk of hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma in early childhood, so tumour surveillance with imaging and alpha-fetoprotein monitoring is essential.","Vitamin K-dependent coagulopathy and bleeding can be the presenting feature before jaundice is recognised.","Absent or reduced canalicular BSEP on immunohistochemistry supports the diagnosis, though some missense variants retain partial function and milder phenotypes.","Allelic disorders include the milder episodic benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis type 2 (BRIC2) and a contribution to intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.","After transplantation, alloimmune disease from anti-BSEP antibodies can produce a cholestatic picture mimicking disease recurrence in the graft."],"pathophysiology":"ABCB11 encodes BSEP, an ATP-binding cassette transporter that is the rate-limiting canalicular pump for secretion of monovalent bile salts into bile. Loss of function prevents export of bile acids, so they accumulate within hepatocytes, producing cholestatic injury, giant-cell transformation and progressive fibrosis; the severe reduction in biliary bile acids also impairs micelle formation and fat absorption. Because BSEP is expressed almost exclusively in hepatocytes, the disease is confined to the liver, distinguishing it from PFIC1. The sustained intracellular exposure to cytotoxic bile acids underlies a markedly increased risk of malignant transformation to hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma even in infancy, with genotype broadly correlating with severity, residual transporter function and cancer risk.","additionalDifferentials":["PFIC type 1 (ATP8B1/FIC1 deficiency) — low GGT with extrahepatic diarrhoea and deafness","PFIC type 3 (ABCB4/MDR3 deficiency) — high GGT","TJP2 deficiency","Inborn errors of bile acid synthesis","Neonatal haemochromatosis (gestational alloimmune liver disease)","Biliary atresia","Alagille syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum total bile acids with paired serum GGT (low or normal)","Serum alpha-fetoprotein for baseline and surveillance","Molecular genetic testing of ABCB11","Liver biopsy with BSEP immunostaining and assessment for giant-cell hepatitis","Liver ultrasound and cross-sectional imaging for tumour surveillance","Coagulation profile and fat-soluble vitamin levels"],"redFlags":["Coagulopathy or bleeding at presentation from vitamin K deficiency","Rising alpha-fetoprotein or a focal liver lesion suggesting malignancy","Rapid progression to cirrhosis within the first years of life","Hepatic decompensation with ascites or variceal bleeding"],"prognosis":"Disease often progresses rapidly to cirrhosis within the first years of life and carries a high childhood risk of hepatobiliary malignancy. Biliary diversion helps some children and transplantation is usually curative for the liver disease, but antibody-mediated recurrence can occur.","pitfalls":["Reading a low or normal GGT as reassuring against severe cholestasis","Failing to institute malignancy surveillance despite young age","Missing a bleeding tendency that reflects fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption","Assuming transplantation removes all risk while overlooking alloimmune BSEP recurrence"],"management":["Ileal bile acid transporter inhibitors such as odevixibat or maralixibat to interrupt bile acid recycling and reduce pruritus and hepatocellular bile acid injury","Antipruritic therapy with rifampicin and bile acid sequestrants, with ursodeoxycholic acid as an adjunct","Fat-soluble vitamin supplementation including vitamin K to correct coagulopathy, plus medium-chain-triglyceride nutritional support","Surgical biliary diversion for symptom control and disease modification in suitable children","Intensive hepatobiliary malignancy surveillance from early childhood with alpha-fetoprotein and liver ultrasound and cross-sectional imaging","Liver transplantation for end-stage disease or malignancy, with awareness of antibody-mediated BSEP recurrence in the graft","Genetic counselling and cascade family testing for this autosomal recessive ABCB11 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric hepatology reference texts","Inherited cholestasis clinical guidelines","Clinical genetics of liver disease references"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_familial_intrahepatic_cholestasis_type_3":{"name":"Progressive Familial Intrahepatic Cholestasis Type 3","system":"Hepatology / Inherited cholestasis","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PFIC3","MDR3 deficiency","ABCB4 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Inherited cholestasis references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The defining laboratory feature is a raised GGT cholestasis, in sharp contrast with the low-GGT picture of PFIC1 and PFIC2; it is caused by autosomal recessive ABCB4 mutations encoding MDR3.","MDR3 is the canalicular phosphatidylcholine floppase; its deficiency yields phospholipid-poor bile that cannot buffer the detergent toxicity of bile salts, injuring canaliculi and bile ducts.","Onset is generally later and the severity spectrum broader than PFIC1 or PFIC2, ranging from infancy through childhood to adult presentation.","Cholesterol gallstones and intrahepatic lithiasis are common, and ABCB4 variants underlie the low phospholipid-associated cholelithiasis (LPAC) syndrome.","Histology can mimic large-duct obstruction, with ductular proliferation, portal inflammation, bile duct injury and progressive portal fibrosis.","Related milder phenotypes from ABCB4 variation include intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy and some drug-induced and transient cholestasis.","Ursodeoxycholic acid is often effective, particularly in patients who retain residual MDR3 function.","Untreated disease progresses to biliary cirrhosis and portal hypertension."],"pathophysiology":"ABCB4 encodes MDR3, an ATP-binding cassette transporter that acts as a floppase translocating phosphatidylcholine across the canalicular membrane into bile. Biliary phospholipids form mixed micelles with bile salts and cholesterol, buffering the detergent toxicity of bile acids and keeping cholesterol in solution. When MDR3 is absent or reduced, bile becomes phospholipid-poor, so free bile salts damage the canalicular membrane and biliary epithelium, generate cholangitis-like bile duct injury and ductular reaction, and promote cholesterol crystallisation and gallstone formation. The resulting cholangiocyte injury explains the elevated GGT that distinguishes this subtype, while the degree of residual transporter activity correlates with later onset, milder disease and responsiveness to ursodeoxycholic acid.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary sclerosing cholangitis and autoimmune sclerosing cholangitis","Mechanical biliary obstruction or choledocholithiasis","Biliary atresia","Alagille syndrome","Other high-GGT inherited cholestasis","Cystic fibrosis-related liver disease","Low phospholipid-associated cholelithiasis (as part of the ABCB4 spectrum)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum GGT (raised) with serum bile acids","Molecular genetic testing of ABCB4","Liver biopsy showing bile duct injury, ductular reaction and portal fibrosis","Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography to exclude mechanical obstruction or sclerosing cholangitis","Abdominal ultrasound for gallstones and intrahepatic lithiasis","Specialist analysis of biliary phospholipid content where available"],"redFlags":["Features of portal hypertension such as varices and splenomegaly","Recurrent cholangitis","Obstructive jaundice suggesting a stone or stricture","Progressive fibrosis toward biliary cirrhosis"],"prognosis":"The course is variable: ursodeoxycholic acid-responsive disease may remain stable for years, whereas others progress to biliary cirrhosis requiring transplantation. There is an increased burden of cholelithiasis and a recognised risk of biliary malignancy.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosis as primary sclerosing cholangitis or mechanical obstruction, leading to unnecessary surgery","Overlooking ABCB4 disease in adults with idiopathic cholestasis, intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy or recurrent gallstones","Withholding a trial of ursodeoxycholic acid","Assuming all PFIC subtypes share a low GGT"],"management":["Ursodeoxycholic acid is first-line and often effective, replenishing hydrophilic bile acids and protecting the biliary epithelium, with response correlating with residual MDR3 transporter activity","Ileal bile acid transporter inhibitors and other antipruritic measures such as rifampicin and bile acid sequestrants for inadequately controlled pruritus","Management of cholesterol gallstones and intrahepatic lithiasis, including the low-phospholipid-associated cholelithiasis phenotype, with endoscopic or surgical treatment as needed","Fat-soluble vitamin supplementation and nutritional support where cholestasis is significant","Surveillance for progression to biliary cirrhosis and for biliary malignancy such as cholangiocarcinoma with imaging","Liver transplantation for those progressing to biliary cirrhosis despite therapy","Genetic counselling and family testing for this autosomal recessive ABCB4 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric hepatology reference texts","Inherited cholestasis clinical guidelines","Clinical genetics of liver disease references"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_macular_hypomelanosis":{"name":"Progressive macular hypomelanosis","system":"Dermatology / Pigmentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMH","progressive macular hypomelanosis of the trunk","cutaneous hypomelanosis of Cutibacterium"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Ill-defined, non-scaly, nummular hypopigmented macules concentrated on the trunk, particularly the central back and chest, in young adults","Follicular red fluorescence under Wood lamp reflects porphyrin-producing Cutibacterium (formerly Propionibacterium) acnes within the follicles","Frequently mistaken for pityriasis versicolor, but potassium hydroxide microscopy is negative for fungal elements","More common in young women and in tropical or warmer climates","The hypopigmentation is functional rather than destructive, with melanocytes preserved but producing less melanin","Antimicrobial and light-based treatments directed at the follicular organism, including targeted ultraviolet phototherapy, can encourage repigmentation","Tends to run a chronic course with a tendency to relapse"],"pathophysiology":"A particular strain of follicular Cutibacterium acnes is thought to produce a factor that interferes with melanogenesis, reducing melanin output without destroying melanocytes; this explains the hypopigmentation rather than depigmentation and the characteristic follicular porphyrin fluorescence. The lack of scale and negative fungal microscopy separate it from pityriasis versicolor. The condition is not primarily an inflammatory or autoimmune process.","additionalDifferentials":["Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor","Idiopathic guttate hypomelanosis","Vitiligo","Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation","Pityriasis alba","Hypopigmented leprosy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wood-lamp examination for follicular red fluorescence","Potassium hydroxide microscopy, which is negative for fungal hyphae","Dermoscopy to assess the hypopigmented pattern","Sensory examination to exclude leprosy in endemic areas","Skin biopsy in atypical or diagnostically uncertain cases"],"redFlags":["An anaesthetic patch or thickened peripheral nerve suggesting leprosy in endemic settings","Atypical, rapidly spreading or persistently symptomatic lesions","Failure to respond to appropriate therapy, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign but chronic and relapsing; repigmentation may follow treatment and some cases improve spontaneously over years, though recurrence is common.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating it as pityriasis versicolor despite negative microscopy","Overlooking hypopigmented leprosy in endemic populations","Expecting a rapid response, when repigmentation is typically slow","Confusing it with vitiligo or idiopathic guttate hypomelanosis"],"management":["Antimicrobial therapy directed at follicular Cutibacterium acnes, using topical benzoyl peroxide and clindamycin and, where needed, an oral tetracycline-class antibiotic","Ultraviolet phototherapy such as narrowband UVB, often combined with antimicrobials, to stimulate repigmentation","Reassure the patient about the benign, non-scarring nature and set expectations for slow repigmentation and possible relapse","Distinguish from pityriasis versicolor and avoid ineffective antifungal treatment given the negative potassium hydroxide microscopy","Monitor response and re-treat relapses, as recurrence is common while some cases improve spontaneously over years","Dermatology referral for diagnostic confirmation and access to phototherapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_multifocal_leukoencephalopathy":{"name":"Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (JC virus)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PML","JC virus encephalopathy","JCV demyelinating disease","John Cunningham virus infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by reactivation of the ubiquitous JC polyomavirus, which lytically infects oligodendrocytes and produces progressive demyelination of the cerebral white matter","Occurs almost exclusively in the setting of impaired cell-mediated immunity, classically advanced HIV, haematological malignancy, transplantation, and natalizumab or other immunomodulatory therapy","Presents with subacute, steadily worsening focal deficits such as hemiparesis, visual field loss, cognitive decline, ataxia, and speech disturbance, usually without fever or headache","MRI typically shows multifocal, asymmetrical subcortical and periventricular white-matter lesions that are hyperintense on T2/FLAIR, do not enhance, and lack mass effect","Diagnosis rests on compatible clinical and imaging features plus detection of JC virus DNA in cerebrospinal fluid by PCR, or brain biopsy when the picture is atypical","There is no specific antiviral agent; the mainstay is restoration of immune function through immune reconstitution in HIV or withdrawal of the offending immunosuppressant","Immune reconstitution can precipitate PML-IRIS, in which recovering immunity produces paradoxical clinical worsening with new gadolinium enhancement and oedema on imaging"],"pathophysiology":"The disease results from reactivation of latent JC polyomavirus, a common childhood infection that persists silently in the kidney and lymphoid tissue. When cell-mediated immunity fails, rearranged neurotropic strains of the virus reach the central nervous system and lytically infect oligodendrocytes, destroying the myelin they maintain. The resulting confluent foci of demyelination enlarge and coalesce across the subcortical white matter, sparing axons relatively early but producing progressive neurological deficits. Astrocytes become bizarrely enlarged and oligodendrocyte nuclei acquire viral inclusions. Because inflammation is minimal while immunity is suppressed, lesions usually do not enhance until immune function recovers, when a brisk inflammatory response (immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome) may develop.","additionalDifferentials":["HIV-associated dementia or HIV encephalopathy","Cerebral toxoplasmosis","Primary central nervous system lymphoma","Cytomegalovirus or other viral encephalitis","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis or multiple sclerosis relapse","Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain with and without gadolinium to characterise white-matter lesions and detect enhancement suggesting IRIS","Cerebrospinal fluid JC virus DNA PCR, ideally quantitative and from a sensitive laboratory","HIV testing with CD4 count and plasma viral load","Review of immunosuppressive and biological therapy history, including cumulative natalizumab exposure and anti-JCV antibody index","Brain biopsy with immunohistochemistry or in situ hybridisation for JC virus when CSF is negative but suspicion remains","Assessment of underlying haematological or transplant-related immune status"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive focal neurological deficit in an immunocompromised patient","New enhancing lesions with oedema after immune reconstitution suggesting PML-IRIS","Deteriorating conscious level or brainstem involvement","Development of intractable seizures or profound cognitive collapse"],"prognosis":"Historically often fatal within months, outcomes have improved where immune function can be restored, particularly in HIV with effective antiretroviral therapy; nonetheless many survivors are left with substantial neurological disability, and PML-IRIS carries a risk of further deterioration or death.","pitfalls":["Attributing progressive deficits to the underlying condition or to multiple sclerosis relapse rather than considering PML in a treated patient","Being falsely reassured by a single negative CSF JC virus PCR, since sensitivity is imperfect","Failing to recognise PML-IRIS when clinical worsening follows immune recovery","Overlooking natalizumab or other biological agents as a cause and delaying drug withdrawal"],"management":["Restore cell-mediated immunity as the mainstay: start effective antiretroviral therapy in HIV, or withdraw the offending immunosuppressant such as natalizumab, using plasma exchange to accelerate its removal","No proven specific anti-JC-virus drug exists; avoid unnecessary additional immunosuppression","Anticipate and manage immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (PML-IRIS), using corticosteroids for symptomatic inflammatory deterioration","Supportive and rehabilitative care for neurological deficits with physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy, and antiseizure treatment if seizures occur","Serial clinical and MRI monitoring for progression, new enhancement and IRIS, with cerebrospinal fluid JC virus DNA to confirm and follow the diagnosis","Treat the underlying condition (HIV, haematological malignancy or transplant immunosuppression) with the relevant specialists","Multidisciplinary neurology, infectious diseases and, where appropriate, palliative care involvement given the guarded prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","UpToDate: Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_muscular_atrophy":{"name":"Progressive muscular atrophy","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMA","pure lower motor neurone disease","progressive muscular atrophy variant of motor neurone disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with progressive muscle weakness, wasting and fasciculation reflecting pure lower motor neurone degeneration, without upper motor neurone signs","Reflexes are usually reduced or absent and plantar responses are flexor, in contrast to the brisk reflexes of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Weakness is often asymmetrical at onset, commonly beginning in a limb before spreading to other regions","Bulbar and respiratory muscles may become involved as the disease advances, threatening swallowing and breathing","Many patients develop subclinical upper motor neurone or corticospinal pathology over time, so progressive muscular atrophy is regarded as part of the motor neurone disease spectrum","Distinguishing it from treatable lower motor neurone syndromes such as multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block is a diagnostic priority","Care is multidisciplinary and supportive, addressing mobility, communication, nutrition and respiratory function"],"pathophysiology":"Progressive muscular atrophy is a form of motor neurone disease characterised by selective degeneration of the lower motor neurones in the anterior horns of the spinal cord and the motor nuclei of the brainstem, producing denervation, muscle wasting and fasciculation. The corticospinal upper motor neurone pathways are clinically spared, at least initially, distinguishing it from amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. Post-mortem studies frequently reveal TDP-43 pathology and subclinical corticospinal involvement, supporting the view that progressive muscular atrophy lies at the lower motor neurone end of a continuous motor neurone disease spectrum rather than being a wholly separate entity.","additionalDifferentials":["Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis at an early or lower motor neurone predominant stage","Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block","Spinal muscular atrophy and other hereditary motor neuronopathies","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy with motor predominance","Inclusion body myositis and other myopathies causing wasting","Kennedy disease (spinobulbar muscular atrophy)","Post-polio syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electromyography and nerve conduction studies to confirm widespread denervation and exclude conduction block","MRI of the brain and spine to exclude structural and compressive lesions","Anti-GM1 antibody testing where multifocal motor neuropathy is suspected","Creatine kinase and myopathy screen to exclude a primary muscle disorder","Genetic testing including the androgen receptor gene for Kennedy disease when clinically indicated","Respiratory function testing to monitor for ventilatory involvement"],"redFlags":["Conduction block on nerve conduction studies, suggesting a potentially treatable multifocal motor neuropathy","Emergence of upper motor neurone signs, indicating evolution towards amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Declining respiratory function or nocturnal hypoventilation","Bulbar weakness with unsafe swallowing and aspiration risk"],"prognosis":"Progressive muscular atrophy typically progresses somewhat more slowly than classic amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, but many patients develop respiratory and bulbar involvement, and the underlying motor neurone degeneration is ultimately progressive with limited life expectancy in many cases.","pitfalls":["Missing multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block, a treatable mimic that responds to immunotherapy","Assuming the disorder will remain purely lower motor neurone, when many cases progress within the wider spectrum","Overlooking Kennedy disease, which carries different inheritance and prognosis","Failing to monitor respiratory function until late in the illness"],"management":["Disease-modifying therapy with riluzole, as offered across the motor neurone disease spectrum, to modestly slow progression","Respiratory surveillance with regular function testing and non-invasive ventilation for ventilatory insufficiency as respiratory muscles become involved","Nutritional support and dysphagia management, with gastrostomy feeding when swallowing fails","Symptomatic care for cramps, spasticity, sialorrhoea and secretions, with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and mobility and communication aids","Exclude and treat mimics, particularly multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block, which responds to intravenous immunoglobulin","Multidisciplinary motor neurone disease clinic care with early palliative care and advance care planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_myoclonic_epilepsy":{"name":"Progressive myoclonic epilepsies (overview)","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PME","progressive myoclonus epilepsy","Unverricht-Lundborg disease","Lafora disease","myoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibres"],"chapter":null,"source":"ILAE classification / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of rare, mostly recessively inherited disorders defined by the combination of progressive myoclonus, other seizure types and neurological decline","Core features are action and stimulus-sensitive myoclonus, generalised tonic-clonic seizures, progressive ataxia and cognitive decline","Important causes include Unverricht-Lundborg disease, Lafora disease, the neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses, sialidoses and mitochondrial MERRF","Age at onset and the pace of decline help narrow the differential: Unverricht-Lundborg tends to be slowly progressive while Lafora disease progresses rapidly with prominent dementia","The EEG typically shows generalised epileptiform discharges with progressive background slowing and marked photosensitivity","Sodium-channel-blocking and some GABAergic agents can aggravate myoclonus, so drug choice must be considered carefully","Diagnosis increasingly relies on targeted genetic testing after clinical and electrophysiological characterisation"],"pathophysiology":"The progressive myoclonic epilepsies are a heterogeneous group of genetic disorders in which distinct molecular defects converge on progressive neuronal dysfunction and loss. Mechanisms include abnormal intracellular storage material (polyglucosan bodies in Lafora disease, lipopigment in the neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses), impaired lysosomal enzyme function (sialidoses), defective protein handling (Unverricht-Lundborg disease) and mitochondrial energy failure (MERRF). These processes damage cortical, cerebellar and other networks, producing the characteristic cortical myoclonus, seizures, ataxia and cognitive deterioration that worsen over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy and other genetic generalised epilepsies","Progressive ataxias without prominent myoclonus","Neurodegenerative and metabolic encephalopathies","Coeliac-related and other acquired myoclonus syndromes","Subacute encephalitides with myoclonus","Drug- or toxin-induced myoclonus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted genetic and gene-panel testing after phenotyping","EEG demonstrating generalised discharges, background slowing and photosensitivity","Brain MRI, which may show cerebellar or generalised atrophy","Enzyme assays and tissue biopsy (e.g. skin, for characteristic inclusions) in selected disorders","Metabolic and mitochondrial studies including lactate and, where indicated, muscle biopsy"],"redFlags":["Worsening action myoclonus with declining cognition rather than a stable course","Loss of ambulation from progressive ataxia and myoclonus","Rapid dementia suggesting Lafora disease","Seizure or myoclonus aggravation after introduction of an inappropriate antiseizure medication"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the specific disorder, ranging from a relatively slowly progressive course in Unverricht-Lundborg disease to relentless deterioration and early death in Lafora disease and some ceroid lipofuscinoses. Most forms cause increasing disability over years, and treatment is largely symptomatic.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling early progressive myoclonic epilepsy as juvenile myoclonic epilepsy and missing the progressive course","Using sodium-channel blockers or certain GABAergic agents that worsen myoclonus","Attributing progressive decline to poor seizure control rather than the underlying disease","Stopping the workup after clinical assessment without pursuing specific genetic or enzymatic diagnosis"],"management":["Antimyoclonic and broad-spectrum antiseizure therapy with agents such as valproate, levetiracetam and clonazepam, with piracetam or zonisamide as adjuncts for myoclonus","Avoid antiseizure drugs that aggravate myoclonus, notably sodium-channel blockers such as carbamazepine, phenytoin and lamotrigine, and vigabatrin and tiagabine","Disease-specific measures where applicable, such as coenzyme-Q10 and vitamin cofactor support in MERRF mitochondrial disease and emerging substrate-directed therapy in Lafora disease","Supportive and rehabilitative care for progressive ataxia, cognitive decline and disability, including physiotherapy, occupational therapy and communication and mobility aids","Consider adjuncts for refractory myoclonus such as vagus nerve stimulation or ketogenic dietary therapy in selected disorders","Genetic counselling and family testing given the predominantly autosomal recessive inheritance, with precise molecular diagnosis to guide prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ILAE classification of the epilepsies","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_nodular_histiocytosis":{"name":"Progressive nodular histiocytosis","system":"Skin / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PNH","progressive nodular histiocytoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, persistent and progressive non-Langerhans cell histiocytosis characterised by two coexisting lesion types: superficial yellowish xanthomatous papules and deeper firm red-brown to skin-coloured nodules.","Lesions accumulate steadily over time without the spontaneous involution seen in other cutaneous non-Langerhans cell histiocytoses.","Extensive facial involvement can coalesce to produce a disfiguring leonine facies, and lesions around the eyes, mouth and larynx may impair function.","In contrast to xanthoma disseminatum, lesions are not confined to flexures and there is characteristically no mucosal, pituitary or systemic organ involvement, though mucosal lesions are occasionally reported.","Serum lipids are normal, and there is no consistent association with diabetes insipidus or paraproteinaemia.","Histology reveals two populations of histiocytes: superficial foamy xanthomatous cells with Touton giant cells and deeper spindle-shaped histiocytes in a storiform arrangement resembling dermatofibroma.","Immunohistochemistry shows CD68 positivity with CD1a and langerin negativity, consistent with a macrophage rather than Langerhans cell lineage.","The condition is notoriously refractory to treatment, and surgical or physical removal of individual disfiguring or obstructing lesions is often the mainstay."],"pathophysiology":"Progressive nodular histiocytosis is a proliferative disorder of macrophage-derived histiocytes within the non-Langerhans cell histiocytosis group, distinguished by the coexistence of superficial lipidised xanthomatous histiocytes with Touton giant cells and deeper spindle-shaped histiocytes arranged in a storiform pattern. The lesional cells express macrophage markers such as CD68 and factor XIIIa but lack CD1a, langerin and Birbeck granules. Unlike the self-healing cutaneous non-Langerhans cell histiocytoses, the infiltrate here proliferates progressively and does not regress, producing an ever-increasing burden of papules and nodules; the reason for this relentless behaviour in the absence of systemic involvement or a paraprotein is not fully understood, but it distinguishes progressive nodular histiocytosis as the most persistent and disfiguring of the purely cutaneous forms.","additionalDifferentials":["Xanthoma disseminatum","Multiple dermatofibromas","Juvenile / adult xanthogranuloma","Multicentric reticulohistiocytosis","Erdheim-Chester disease","Cutaneous involvement by Langerhans cell histiocytosis","Eruptive xanthomas"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy sampling both superficial and deep lesions with immunohistochemistry (CD68, factor XIIIa, CD1a, langerin)","Fasting serum lipid profile to confirm normolipaemia","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis to exclude paraproteinaemia","Assessment for diabetes insipidus to distinguish from xanthoma disseminatum","Laryngoscopy if hoarseness or airway symptoms occur","Imaging only if systemic involvement is clinically suspected","Ophthalmological review where periorbital lesions threaten vision"],"redFlags":["Progressive facial infiltration producing leonine facies and functional impairment","Periorbital or eyelid nodules threatening vision","Laryngeal or perioral lesions causing airway or feeding compromise","Rapid enlargement or ulceration of nodules","Emergence of systemic features suggesting an alternative multisystem histiocytosis"],"prognosis":"The condition follows a chronic, relentlessly progressive course without spontaneous remission; it does not shorten life through systemic disease but causes substantial disfigurement and functional morbidity, and it is characteristically resistant to medical therapy.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the deep storiform lesions for multiple dermatofibromas and underestimating the disorder.","Confusing it with xanthoma disseminatum despite the absence of flexural predilection, mucosal disease and diabetes insipidus.","Expecting spontaneous involution and delaying intervention on disfiguring or obstructing lesions.","Assuming an associated paraproteinaemia or systemic disease that is not typically present.","Over-relying on systemic therapy for a condition that is largely treatment-resistant."],"management":["No reliably effective medical therapy exists; treatment targets disfiguring and functionally obstructing lesions rather than cure","Surgical excision or laser removal, such as carbon dioxide laser, of symptomatic, obstructing or cosmetically troublesome nodules, including periorificial and laryngeal lesions","Airway assessment and intervention for laryngeal involvement causing hoarseness or obstruction","Trial of systemic agents used in histiocytoses, such as retinoids or chemotherapeutic and immunomodulatory drugs, in extensive disease, recognising limited and variable response","Confirm the diagnosis histologically and monitor for the uncommon emergence of systemic features, distinguishing it from xanthoma disseminatum and paraproteinaemia-associated histiocytosis","Multidisciplinary dermatology, haematology and, where needed, ENT and ophthalmology care, with psychological support for disfigurement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology (current edition)","WHO Classification of Histiocytoses and revised histiocytosis classification (current)","Standard dermatology reference texts (current editions)"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_nonfluent_aphasia":{"name":"Progressive non-fluent aphasia","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PNFA","non-fluent/agrammatic variant primary progressive aphasia","agrammatic variant PPA","nfvPPA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by effortful, halting speech with agrammatism and inconsistent speech-sound errors (apraxia of speech), while single-word comprehension is relatively preserved","It is the non-fluent/agrammatic variant of primary progressive aphasia and usually reflects underlying frontotemporal lobar degeneration with tau pathology","Comprehension of grammatically complex sentences is impaired, though understanding of individual words and object knowledge remains comparatively intact","Imaging typically shows atrophy and hypometabolism in the left posterior frontal and insular regions","Motor features overlapping with corticobasal syndrome or progressive supranuclear palsy may develop as the disease advances","Speech becomes progressively sparse and telegraphic, and some patients ultimately become mute","Distinguishing it from the semantic and logopenic variants relies on the pattern of speech production, grammar and repetition"],"pathophysiology":"Progressive non-fluent aphasia is the non-fluent/agrammatic variant of primary progressive aphasia, caused by neurodegeneration centred on the left inferior frontal, insular and premotor cortex that supports speech planning and grammatical processing. The most frequent underlying pathology is frontotemporal lobar degeneration associated with abnormal tau protein aggregation, and there is clinical and pathological overlap with corticobasal degeneration and progressive supranuclear palsy. Damage to these anterior language and motor-speech networks produces effortful, agrammatic output and apraxia of speech, with relative sparing of the temporal regions that store word meaning.","additionalDifferentials":["Semantic variant primary progressive aphasia","Logopenic variant primary progressive aphasia","Corticobasal syndrome","Progressive supranuclear palsy","Vascular aphasia from stroke","Behavioural variant frontotemporal dementia","Primary progressive apraxia of speech"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Speech and language evaluation focusing on fluency, grammar and motor speech","MRI of the brain showing left posterior frontal and insular atrophy","FDG-PET to demonstrate frontal hypometabolism","Amyloid PET or cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers to exclude underlying Alzheimer pathology","Neuropsychological testing to document preserved single-word comprehension and object knowledge","Assessment for parkinsonian and corticobasal features over time"],"redFlags":["Emerging parkinsonism, limb apraxia or gaze palsy suggesting corticobasal or supranuclear palsy overlap","Rapid progression suggesting an alternative aggressive pathology","Dysphagia and aspiration risk as bulbar and motor features advance","Progression to mutism with loss of functional communication"],"prognosis":"The disorder progresses over years, with speech becoming increasingly sparse and eventual mutism in many patients, and frequent evolution of motor features; prognosis depends on the underlying pathology, and management is supportive with speech and language therapy and communication aids.","pitfalls":["Confusing agrammatic non-fluent output with the anomia and word-retrieval failure of the logopenic variant","Overlooking apraxia of speech as a defining feature","Assuming Alzheimer pathology when frontotemporal lobar degeneration is more likely","Missing evolving corticobasal or supranuclear palsy features"],"management":["No disease-modifying drug exists; management centres on speech and language therapy targeting motor speech, grammar and functional communication strategies","Communication aids and augmentative and alternative communication as speech becomes sparse and in anticipation of eventual mutism","Treat associated neuropsychiatric symptoms such as depression, apathy and behavioural change with appropriate supportive and pharmacological measures","Monitor for and manage emerging parkinsonian, corticobasal or supranuclear palsy motor features, with falls prevention, physiotherapy and swallowing assessment","Dysphagia surveillance and nutritional support as bulbar and motor involvement develops","Multidisciplinary support across neurology, speech and language therapy, occupational therapy and dietetics, with caregiver education and advance care planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_outer_retinal_necrosis":{"name":"Progressive outer retinal necrosis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PORN","varicella-zoster outer retinal necrosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A variant of herpetic (usually varicella-zoster) necrotising retinitis occurring in severely immunocompromised patients, classically advanced HIV","Distinguished from acute retinal necrosis by minimal or absent intraocular inflammation and by early posterior-pole and outer-retinal involvement","Multifocal deep retinal opacification spreads rapidly and coalesces, often with early macular involvement","Retinal vasculitis is typically absent, unlike acute retinal necrosis","Progression is extremely rapid and the prognosis for vision is poor","Management combines systemic and intravitreal antivirals with restoration of immune function"],"pathophysiology":"In the setting of profound cellular immunodeficiency, varicella-zoster virus infects the outer retina, producing rapidly progressive necrosis that begins in the deep retina and posterior pole with little inflammatory response because the immune system cannot mount vitritis or vasculitis. The outer-retinal predilection and blunted inflammation reflect the underlying immunosuppression, and confluent necrosis frequently reaches the macula early, causing severe irreversible visual loss and a high rate of retinal detachment.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute retinal necrosis (immunocompetent, prominent vitritis and vasculitis)","Cytomegalovirus retinitis","Syphilitic posterior placoid chorioretinitis","Toxoplasma retinochoroiditis","Intraocular lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Vitreous or aqueous PCR for varicella-zoster and other herpesviruses","HIV testing and CD4 count / immune status assessment","Dilated fundus examination documenting outer-retinal necrosis","Syphilis and other infective serology to exclude mimics","Optical coherence tomography and wide-field imaging to track progression"],"redFlags":["Rapidly advancing necrosis toward or involving the macula","Bilateral involvement","Very low CD4 count or other severe immunosuppression","Retinal detachment"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is poor with frequent rapid progression to profound visual loss and retinal detachment; outcomes improve when immune reconstitution is achieved alongside antiviral therapy.","pitfalls":["Expecting the vitritis of acute retinal necrosis and dismissing PORN because the eye looks quiet","Delaying antiviral therapy while awaiting confirmatory tests","Neglecting to address the underlying immunosuppression","Missing early macular involvement"],"management":["Commence high-dose systemic antiviral therapy directed at varicella-zoster virus, typically intravenous aciclovir combined with ganciclovir or foscarnet, without waiting for PCR confirmation","Add repeated intravitreal ganciclovir or foscarnet injections to achieve therapeutic intraocular antiviral levels","Restore immune function by starting or optimising antiretroviral therapy in HIV, or reducing iatrogenic immunosuppression, as this is central to controlling the infection","Monitor progression with serial dilated fundoscopy, wide-field imaging and optical coherence tomography, watching for macular and peripheral spread","Refer urgently to vitreoretinal surgery for prophylactic laser demarcation and repair of the frequent rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Manage jointly with infectious diseases and HIV physicians and continue oral valaciclovir maintenance to protect the fellow eye and prevent relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_rubella_panencephalitis":{"name":"Progressive rubella panencephalitis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PRP","rubella panencephalitis","progressive rubella encephalitis","rubella virus panencephalitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A very rare, slowly progressive central nervous system disorder caused by persistent rubella virus infection of the brain","It arises years to a decade or more after congenital rubella syndrome, and less commonly after childhood-acquired rubella","Onset is usually in the second decade with insidious cognitive and behavioural decline, deteriorating school performance, and dementia","This is followed by progressive cerebellar ataxia, spasticity, myoclonus, and seizures, culminating in severe neurological disability","Cerebrospinal fluid shows a raised protein and oligoclonal bands with markedly elevated rubella-specific antibody, reflecting intrathecal antibody production","Imaging typically shows diffuse white-matter change and cerebral and cerebellar atrophy, with cerebellar involvement often prominent","There is no effective specific treatment, and its near-elimination follows widespread rubella vaccination and prevention of congenital rubella"],"pathophysiology":"Progressive rubella panencephalitis reflects long-term persistence of the rubella virus within the central nervous system after congenital or, rarely, postnatal infection. The virus is not fully cleared and continues a smouldering, low-grade infection of brain tissue over many years. A chronic immune-mediated process, with intrathecal synthesis of rubella-specific antibody, accompanies slowly progressive destruction of both grey and white matter, hence panencephalitis. There is widespread neuronal loss, gliosis, perivascular inflammation, and a characteristic prominent involvement of the cerebellum, producing the ataxia that is a notable feature. The mechanism resembles that of subacute sclerosing panencephalitis after measles, in that a common childhood virus persists in the brain and drives a delayed, progressive, and ultimately fatal encephalitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (measles)","Neurodegenerative and inherited leukodystrophies","Autoimmune or paraneoplastic encephalitis","Chronic viral encephalitides in the immunocompromised","Late complications of congenital infection from other agents","Inborn errors of metabolism presenting in adolescence"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cerebrospinal fluid analysis showing raised protein, oligoclonal bands, and greatly elevated rubella-specific antibody","Paired serum and CSF rubella antibody indices to demonstrate intrathecal synthesis","MRI of the brain to show white-matter change and cerebral and cerebellar atrophy","Electroencephalography, which shows diffuse slowing without the periodic complexes of SSPE","Detailed history of congenital rubella syndrome or prior rubella infection","Exclusion of measles and other progressive encephalitides"],"redFlags":["Relentless cognitive and motor decline in a young person with a history of congenital rubella","Progressive ataxia, myoclonus, and seizures","Loss of independent function and swallowing safety","Rapid deterioration suggesting advancing disease"],"prognosis":"The condition is progressive and ultimately fatal, evolving over years to profound neurological disability and death, with no proven treatment able to halt its course.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with subacute sclerosing panencephalitis, as the two are clinically and mechanistically similar but follow different viruses","Overlooking a distant history of congenital rubella when assessing adolescent cognitive decline","Expecting the periodic EEG complexes of SSPE, which are absent in progressive rubella panencephalitis","Attributing the decline to a primary neurodegenerative disorder and missing the raised CSF rubella antibody"],"management":["Recognise that no antiviral or disease-modifying treatment reliably halts the disorder, so management is supportive and symptom-directed","Control seizures and myoclonus with antiepileptic drugs, adding agents such as clonazepam or levetiracetam for myoclonic jerks","Treat progressive spasticity with antispasticity agents such as baclofen alongside regular physiotherapy and positioning","Provide swallowing assessment, modified diet or enteral feeding, and aspiration precautions as bulbar function declines","Coordinate multidisciplinary neurorehabilitation with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language therapy","Introduce palliative and supportive care early given the relentlessly progressive, ultimately fatal course, with family support","Reinforce rubella (MMR) immunisation of the community to prevent congenital rubella, the precursor of this condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","UpToDate: Congenital rubella syndrome and rubella-associated neurological disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"progressive_supranuclear_palsy":{"name":"Progressive supranuclear palsy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PSP","Steele-Richardson-Olszewski syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An atypical parkinsonian syndrome caused by tau protein accumulation, with early postural instability and unexplained backward falls","The hallmark is a vertical supranuclear gaze palsy, especially impaired downgaze, which may be preceded by slowing of vertical saccades","Axial rigidity predominates over limb rigidity and patients often adopt an extended, erect posture unlike the flexed posture of Parkinson disease","Parkinsonism in PSP is typically symmetric, has little tremor, and responds poorly and only transiently to dopaminergic therapy","A characteristic surprised or astonished facial expression results from frontalis overactivity and reduced blinking","Early frontal-type cognitive change, pseudobulbar affect, and dysarthria or dysphagia are common","MRI may show midbrain atrophy producing the hummingbird or penguin sign on midsagittal images","Poor levodopa response, early falls, and early gaze problems help separate it from Parkinson disease"],"pathophysiology":"PSP is a four-repeat tauopathy in which abnormal tau aggregates as neurofibrillary tangles, tufted astrocytes, and coiled bodies in the basal ganglia, subthalamic nucleus, substantia nigra, midbrain tegmentum, and brainstem nuclei controlling eye movements. Degeneration of the midbrain and its ocular motor circuitry produces the supranuclear gaze palsy, while nigral and striatal involvement causes the poorly levodopa-responsive parkinsonism. Damage to brainstem postural and gait pathways underlies the early instability and falls, and frontal-subcortical involvement drives the cognitive and behavioural features.","additionalDifferentials":["Parkinson disease","Multiple system atrophy","Corticobasal degeneration","Dementia with Lewy bodies","Vascular parkinsonism","Normal pressure hydrocephalus and other causes of gait instability"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain to assess midbrain atrophy and exclude structural or vascular mimics","Detailed clinical assessment of vertical saccades and gaze range","Trial of dopaminergic therapy to document the typically poor and transient response","Neuropsychological assessment of frontal-executive function","Swallowing evaluation given aspiration risk","Exclusion of reversible causes such as normal pressure hydrocephalus where indicated"],"redFlags":["Recurrent unexplained falls, especially backward, causing injury and needing safety measures","Progressive dysphagia with aspiration risk requiring swallowing assessment","Rapid cognitive or behavioural decline affecting safety and capacity","Features suggesting an alternative treatable diagnosis such as hydrocephalus"],"prognosis":"PSP is relentlessly progressive with no disease-modifying therapy; mobility, communication, and swallowing decline over several years, and death is commonly related to aspiration and its complications.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing early PSP as Parkinson disease before the gaze palsy is evident","Attributing frequent falls to general frailty rather than the disease","Persisting with high-dose dopaminergic therapy despite a clearly poor response","Overlooking dysphagia and aspiration risk until a pneumonia occurs","Missing the surprised facial appearance and slowed vertical saccades as early clues"],"management":["Offer a trial of levodopa, recognising that any benefit on parkinsonism is usually modest and transient and there is no disease-modifying therapy","Prioritise falls prevention with physiotherapy, gait aids such as a weighted walking frame, and home safety modification given the early postural instability","Manage the supranuclear gaze palsy and dry eyes with ocular lubricants, reading aids and prisms, and treat blepharospasm or focal dystonia with botulinum toxin","Arrange speech and language therapy for dysarthria and dysphagia, with modified diet and consideration of gastrostomy feeding as swallowing fails","Treat pseudobulbar affect and depression pharmacologically and provide occupational therapy and communication aids","Coordinate multidisciplinary care and introduce palliative care with advance care planning, since aspiration pneumonia is the usual terminal event"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"prolactinoma":{"name":"Prolactinoma","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prolactin-secreting pituitary adenoma","lactotroph adenoma","hyperprolactinaemia (tumoural)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest hormone-secreting pituitary tumour; presentation differs by sex, with menstrual disturbance and galactorrhoea in women and reduced libido or erectile dysfunction in men","Because men lack an early hormonal warning sign, they more often present late with large tumours causing mass effect","Hyperprolactinaemia suppresses gonadotrophin secretion, producing hypogonadism, infertility, and reduced bone density over time","Before diagnosing a prolactinoma, exclude other causes of raised prolactin such as pregnancy, hypothyroidism, renal failure, and dopamine-blocking drugs","Stalk compression by any large sellar mass can modestly raise prolactin; very high levels are more specific for a true prolactinoma","In large tumours with only mildly elevated prolactin, laboratory dilution may be needed to exclude the hook effect artefact","Unlike most pituitary tumours, prolactinomas are usually managed medically with dopamine agonists rather than surgery as first line","Dopamine agonist therapy typically lowers prolactin, restores gonadal function, and shrinks tumour volume"],"pathophysiology":"A prolactinoma is a benign lactotroph adenoma of the anterior pituitary that autonomously secretes prolactin. Prolactin is normally under tonic inhibitory control by hypothalamic dopamine, which is why dopamine agonists are effective and dopamine-blocking drugs raise prolactin. Excess prolactin inhibits pulsatile gonadotrophin-releasing hormone secretion, reducing LH and FSH and causing hypogonadism, galactorrhoea, and infertility. Larger tumours (macroadenomas) may compress the optic chiasm and adjacent pituitary tissue, producing visual field defects and hypopituitarism. Non-tumoural hyperprolactinaemia from stalk disconnection, drugs, or systemic disease must be distinguished from a true adenoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Physiological hyperprolactinaemia of pregnancy or lactation","Drug-induced hyperprolactinaemia (antipsychotics, antiemetics, some antidepressants)","Primary hypothyroidism raising prolactin via TRH","Chronic kidney disease reducing prolactin clearance","Stalk-effect hyperprolactinaemia from a non-lactotroph sellar mass","Macroprolactinaemia (biologically inactive complexed prolactin)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting serum prolactin, repeated if borderline, with sample dilution when a hook effect is suspected","Pregnancy test, thyroid function, and renal function to exclude secondary causes","Review of medications that raise prolactin","Pituitary MRI to characterise adenoma size and suprasellar extension","Assessment of macroprolactin when clinical picture and level are discordant","Visual fields and anterior pituitary hormone panel for macroadenomas"],"redFlags":["Bitemporal visual field loss from chiasmal compression by a macroadenoma","Sudden severe headache with visual or cranial nerve signs suggesting apoplexy","Symptoms and signs of coexisting hypopituitarism, including adrenal insufficiency","Very large invasive tumours threatening vision or requiring urgent decompression"],"prognosis":"Most microprolactinomas respond well to dopamine agonists with normalisation of prolactin, restoration of fertility, and often no significant growth; macroadenomas also usually shrink with medical therapy, and in selected patients treatment can eventually be tapered, though relapse of hyperprolactinaemia is possible.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing a prolactinoma without excluding drugs, hypothyroidism, renal failure, and pregnancy","Missing the hook effect and underestimating prolactin in a large tumour, leading to inappropriate surgery","Overlooking macroprolactin as a cause of asymptomatic elevation","Failing to counsel on bone health and fertility implications of chronic hypogonadism"],"management":["Start a dopamine agonist as first-line therapy, usually cabergoline, to normalise prolactin, restore gonadal function and fertility, and shrink the tumour","Reserve transsphenoidal surgery for dopamine-agonist resistance or intolerance, or for acute chiasmal compression or pituitary apoplexy","Monitor response with serial serum prolactin, pituitary MRI and, in macroadenomas, formal visual field testing","Screen for and replace coexisting anterior pituitary hormone deficiencies in macroadenomas, and address hypogonadism-related bone loss with sex steroid replacement","In pregnancy switch to bromocriptine or withdraw treatment as advised and monitor clinically for symptomatic tumour enlargement","Consider echocardiographic surveillance for valvulopathy during prolonged higher-dose cabergoline therapy","Escalate urgently for sudden headache, visual loss or apoplexy, and refer to a pituitary multidisciplinary team for resistant or aggressive tumours"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"prolidase_deficiency":{"name":"Prolidase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prolidase deficiency","PEPD deficiency","peptidase D deficiency","imidodipeptiduria","hyperimidodipeptiduria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by deficiency of prolidase (peptidase D, encoded by PEPD), the enzyme that cleaves dipeptides with proline or hydroxyproline at the C-terminus, a final step in collagen turnover","The hallmark clinical feature is chronic, recalcitrant skin ulceration, especially of the lower legs, together with a characteristic facies and recurrent infections","The diagnostic biochemical signature is massive urinary excretion of imidodipeptides (proline- and hydroxyproline-containing dipeptides)","Splenomegaly, recurrent respiratory and skin infections, and variable intellectual disability may accompany the skin disease","Impaired recycling of proline from collagen breakdown is thought to compromise wound healing and connective tissue integrity","Management is largely supportive with meticulous wound care; topical approaches aiming to supply amino acids to ulcers have been explored","Inheritance is autosomal recessive with wide phenotypic variability, from near-asymptomatic to severe","British spelling applies (e.g. tumour, oedema) where relevant to descriptions"],"pathophysiology":"Prolidase hydrolyses dipeptides in which the C-terminal residue is proline or hydroxyproline, the last enzymatic step that releases these imino acids from collagen degradation for reuse in new collagen synthesis. When prolidase is deficient, imidodipeptides accumulate and are excreted in large amounts in the urine, while recycling of proline for collagen synthesis is impaired. The resulting defect in connective tissue turnover and wound healing underlies the chronic, poorly healing skin ulcers, and the dermatological and immune manifestations characteristic of the disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic venous or arterial leg ulceration of other cause","Pyoderma gangrenosum and other inflammatory ulcerative dermatoses","Other genodermatoses with poor wound healing","Immunodeficiency with recurrent skin and respiratory infection","Systemic lupus erythematosus (some patients have lupus-like features)","Vasculitic ulceration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine amino acid and peptide analysis demonstrating marked imidodipeptiduria","Prolidase enzyme activity assay in erythrocytes, leucocytes, or fibroblasts","Molecular analysis of the PEPD gene","Skin biopsy and wound swabs to characterise ulcers and infection","Immunological workup given recurrent infections","Imaging or examination for splenomegaly and associated features"],"redFlags":["Extensive or infected chronic ulceration with systemic sepsis risk","Recurrent severe respiratory infections suggesting significant immune compromise","Features of an associated autoimmune process such as lupus"],"prognosis":"The course is highly variable; skin ulceration is often chronic and difficult to heal and may cause substantial morbidity, while some individuals remain mildly affected. Recurrent infection can be a significant contributor to morbidity.","pitfalls":["Managing leg ulcers as simple venous disease without considering a metabolic cause in atypical, treatment-resistant cases","Overlooking associated immunodeficiency and infection risk","Missing associated autoimmune or lupus-like features","Assuming a uniform severe phenotype, when presentation ranges widely"],"management":["Centre care on meticulous chronic wound management of the leg ulcers with debridement, appropriate dressings, compression and skin protection","Trial topical amino acid preparations such as proline- and glycine-containing ointments applied to ulcers to supply substrate for local collagen repair","Treat secondary bacterial infection of ulcers and recurrent skin and respiratory infections with culture-directed antibiotics","Undertake immunological assessment given recurrent infection and screen for the associated autoimmune, lupus-like manifestations","Monitor for splenomegaly, cytopenias and progressive dermatological disease at follow-up","Manage through a multidisciplinary team spanning dermatology, wound care, metabolic medicine and immunology","Provide genetic counselling and family screening, as the disorder is autosomal recessive"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: prolidase deficiency","Nyhan, Atlas of Inherited Metabolic Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"prolymphocytic_leukaemia":{"name":"Prolymphocytic leukaemia","system":"Haematological / Lymphoid","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLL","T-cell prolymphocytic leukaemia","B-cell prolymphocytic leukaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, aggressive mature lymphoid leukaemia in which prolymphocytes dominate the blood","Occurs in B-cell and T-cell forms, with the T-cell type being the more common and particularly aggressive","Typically presents in older adults with a very high lymphocyte count, marked splenomegaly and often minimal lymphadenopathy","T-cell disease may involve the skin, serous effusions and central nervous system","Prolymphocytes are larger than mature lymphocytes and have a prominent central nucleolus","T-cell prolymphocytic leukaemia commonly shows abnormalities involving the TCL1 locus and chromosome 11 (ATM)","It is distinguished from chronic lymphocytic leukaemia by morphology, immunophenotype and its aggressive course"],"pathophysiology":"Prolymphocytic leukaemia is a clonal proliferation of intermediate-sized lymphoid cells arrested at the prolymphocyte stage. In the T-cell form, rearrangements activating the TCL1 oncogene, together with ATM inactivation from chromosome 11 abnormalities, drive proliferation and impair DNA damage responses, producing an aggressive disease with high tumour burden. The B-cell form is a distinct clonal B-cell disorder. Extensive marrow, splenic and blood involvement accounts for the very high counts, cytopenias and organomegaly, while tissue tropism in T-cell disease explains skin and serosal involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia with increased prolymphocytes","Mantle cell lymphoma in leukaemic phase","Adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma","Sezary syndrome","Hairy cell leukaemia variant","Large granular lymphocytic leukaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood film for prolymphocyte morphology","Flow cytometry to define B- or T-cell lineage and immunophenotype","Cytogenetics and molecular testing (including TCL1 and ATM/chromosome 11 assessment)","Bone marrow examination","Imaging to assess splenomegaly and any nodal or extranodal disease"],"redFlags":["Very high and rapidly rising lymphocyte count with cytopenias","Symptomatic massive splenomegaly","Central nervous system or serosal involvement in T-cell disease","Rapid clinical deterioration"],"prognosis":"T-cell prolymphocytic leukaemia is aggressive with historically poor survival, although targeted antibody therapy and, where feasible, allogeneic transplantation have improved outcomes; the B-cell form is also aggressive but somewhat variable.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying it as chronic lymphocytic leukaemia and underestimating its aggressiveness","Failing to distinguish B-cell from T-cell disease, which affects treatment","Overlooking central nervous system or skin involvement","Delaying treatment in a rapidly progressive leukaemia"],"management":["Treat T-cell prolymphocytic leukaemia primarily with the anti-CD52 monoclonal antibody alemtuzumab, to which it is uniquely responsive","Consolidate responders with allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation where fitness and a donor allow, as the only potentially durable option","Treat the B-cell form with chemoimmunotherapy and targeted agents such as rituximab-based regimens, and BTK inhibitors or venetoclax where molecular features indicate","Provide supportive care with transfusion for cytopenias, tumour lysis prophylaxis, and antimicrobial prophylaxis against viral, fungal and Pneumocystis infection with CMV monitoring during alemtuzumab","Address extranodal T-cell disease including skin infiltration, serous effusions and central nervous system involvement","Enrol in clinical trials given the aggressive course, and involve palliative care for patients unfit for intensive therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Wintrobe's Clinical Hematology (standard reference)","WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"pronator_teres_syndrome":{"name":"pronator teres syndrome","system":"nervous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["proximal median nerve entrapment","pronator syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Proximal median nerve compression in the forearm presenting with volar forearm aching aggravated by repetitive pronation, distinguishing it from the nocturnal wrist-centred symptoms of carpal tunnel syndrome.","Sensory disturbance characteristically involves the thenar eminence via the palmar cutaneous branch, which arises proximal to the wrist and is therefore spared in carpal tunnel syndrome.","Compression may occur at the ligament of Struthers, the lacertus fibrosus, between the two heads of pronator teres, or at the fibrous arch of flexor digitorum superficialis.","A negative Tinel sign at the wrist with a positive provocative response over the proximal forearm supports the diagnosis.","Provocative manoeuvres attempt to localise the site: resisted pronation with the elbow extended implicates pronator teres, resisted elbow flexion with supination implicates lacertus fibrosus, and resisted long-finger flexion implicates the superficialis arch.","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography are frequently normal or non-localising, so the diagnosis remains largely clinical.","Unlike anterior interosseous syndrome, weakness is usually mild and sensory symptoms predominate, though overlap occurs when the parent median nerve is affected.","A supracondylar humeral spur with the ligament of Struthers is a rare bony cause and may be visible on radiographs."],"pathophysiology":"The median nerve traverses several potential compression points in the proximal forearm: a supracondylar spur bridged by the ligament of Struthers, the bicipital aponeurosis (lacertus fibrosus), the interval between the humeral and ulnar heads of pronator teres, and the proximal fibrous arch of flexor digitorum superficialis. Repetitive forceful pronation and gripping, muscular hypertrophy, anatomical anomalies, or a space-occupying lesion produce mechanical and ischaemic injury to the nerve. Because the palmar cutaneous and thenar motor branches arise at or distal to this level, both thenar sensation and, variably, thenar motor function may be affected, contrasting with the pattern seen in wrist-level entrapment.","additionalDifferentials":["carpal tunnel syndrome","anterior interosseous syndrome","C6 or C7 cervical radiculopathy","brachial plexopathy involving the lateral cord","medial epicondylitis","cervical rib or thoracic outlet syndrome","flexor-pronator strain or tendinopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["nerve conduction studies and needle electromyography of median-innervated forearm and hand muscles","elbow and humeral radiographs to identify a supracondylar spur","high-resolution nerve ultrasound of the proximal forearm","magnetic resonance imaging of the elbow and forearm for mass lesions or muscle oedema","cervical spine imaging when radiculopathy is suspected","comparison studies of the contralateral limb","dynamic provocative examination during electrodiagnostic testing"],"redFlags":["rapidly progressive motor weakness suggesting an alternative or compressive mass lesion","constitutional symptoms or a palpable forearm mass indicating neoplasm","bilateral or multifocal deficits suggesting a systemic or inflammatory neuropathy","acute severe pain with swelling raising concern for compartment syndrome"],"prognosis":"Many patients improve with activity modification, splinting and conservative measures, and surgical decompression is generally effective when a discrete compressive structure is identified. Recovery may be slow and incomplete when diagnosis is delayed or the compression is diffuse.","pitfalls":["mislabelling proximal median symptoms as carpal tunnel syndrome and performing an unhelpful wrist release","expecting confirmatory electrodiagnostic findings when studies are frequently normal","overlooking a supracondylar spur by omitting elbow radiographs","confusing the sensory-predominant picture with the pure motor deficit of anterior interosseous syndrome"],"management":["Begin with activity modification, avoiding repetitive forceful forearm pronation and gripping, together with ergonomic adjustment and relative rest","Use analgesics or anti-inflammatory drugs and a period of splinting to settle symptoms during conservative treatment","Provide physiotherapy with stretching and median nerve-gliding exercises","Reassess periodically for progressive thenar motor weakness that would prompt earlier intervention","Refer for surgical decompression of the identified compressive structure — the ligament of Struthers, lacertus fibrosus, pronator teres heads or the superficialis arch — when conservative measures fail","Involve hand surgery and neurophysiology to localise the lesion and guide operative planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies","Dawson, Hallett and Wilbourn: Entrapment Neuropathies","Preston and Shapiro: Electromyography and Neuromuscular Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"prop1_deficiency":{"name":"PROP1 deficiency","system":"Endocrine / Pituitary development","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PROP1-related combined pituitary hormone deficiency","Prophet of Pit-1 deficiency","CPHD2"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrine genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["PROP1 deficiency is the single most common identifiable genetic cause of combined pituitary hormone deficiency worldwide and is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner.","The classic hormonal pattern is progressive loss of growth hormone, prolactin, thyroid-stimulating hormone and the gonadotrophins, so affected individuals typically have growth failure and absent or arrested puberty.","Adrenocorticotrophic hormone secretion is usually preserved early but characteristically declines with age, so cortisol deficiency may appear in later childhood or adulthood and demands lifelong monitoring.","Puberty may begin spontaneously and then stall, or fail to start altogether, reflecting the gonadotroph involvement typical of this genotype.","Pituitary magnetic resonance imaging may show a normal, small or, distinctively, an enlarged gland; the pituitary can wax and wane in size over time and occasionally mimics a mass lesion.","The posterior pituitary bright spot and the pituitary stalk are typically normal, helping distinguish PROP1 deficiency from stalk-interruption or ectopic posterior pituitary phenotypes.","Extra-pituitary structures are spared, so there are no eye, midline brain or skeletal malformations, unlike HESX1, LHX3 or SOX-related disease.","Recognising the age-dependent evolution, particularly the delayed emergence of adrenal insufficiency, is essential to avoid a preventable adrenal crisis."],"pathophysiology":"PROP1 (Prophet of Pit-1) encodes a paired-like homeodomain transcription factor expressed transiently in the developing anterior pituitary, where it is required to activate expression of POU1F1 and to specify the Pit-1 lineage (somatotrophs, lactotrophs and thyrotrophs) as well as, indirectly, the gonadotroph lineage. Biallelic loss-of-function mutations therefore impair development of growth-hormone, prolactin, thyroid-stimulating-hormone and gonadotrophin-producing cells, producing the characteristic combined deficiency. PROP1 also normally permits the timely down-regulation of early progenitor factors and the involution of the transitional pituitary; its loss can lead to persistence and expansion of an undifferentiated cell population, which explains the transient pituitary enlargement seen on imaging before the gland later involutes and becomes hypoplastic. The progressive corticotroph failure is thought to reflect a slow, secondary decline rather than a primary developmental block, accounting for cortisol deficiency emerging over years. Because PROP1 acts downstream of the earliest patterning genes and is not expressed outside the pituitary, the phenotype is confined to anterior pituitary hormones without the midline, ocular or skeletal features of more upstream defects. A recurrent small deletion within a repeated sequence of the gene is a common mutational hotspot in many populations.","additionalDifferentials":["POU1F1 (PIT-1) deficiency, which spares the gonadotroph axis and preserves puberty","HESX1 mutation with septo-optic dysplasia and midline anomalies","LHX3 deficiency with limited neck rotation and sensorineural hearing loss","LHX4 deficiency with associated hindbrain and skeletal features","Acquired hypopituitarism from a sellar or suprasellar tumour when the pituitary appears enlarged","Idiopathic combined pituitary hormone deficiency without an identified mutation","Isolated growth hormone deficiency before further axes decline"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Baseline growth hormone axis with insulin-like growth factor 1, plus prolactin, which is typically low","Thyroid-stimulating hormone with free thyroxine to confirm central hypothyroidism","Gonadotrophins with sex steroids and staging of puberty","Serial assessment of the adrenal axis over time given the risk of late-onset cortisol deficiency","Pituitary magnetic resonance imaging, interpreting a normal or enlarged gland in the correct context and repeating if a mass is suspected","Targeted PROP1 sequencing including the recurrent deletion hotspot, or a pituitary gene panel","Bone age radiograph and growth monitoring"],"redFlags":["New adrenal insufficiency emerging years after diagnosis, with risk of crisis during illness","An enlarging pituitary on imaging misread as a neoplasm and inappropriately biopsied or operated","Introduction of thyroxine before confirming adequate cortisol status","Arrest of previously started puberty signalling evolving gonadotrophin failure","Hypoglycaemia or collapse during intercurrent illness in an inadequately cortisol-replaced patient"],"prognosis":"With appropriately sequenced and lifelong hormone replacement, growth and pubertal outcomes are generally good and fertility can often be achieved with gonadotrophin therapy. The main hazard is undertreated or unanticipated adrenal insufficiency, so continued endocrine follow-up is required throughout life.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the transient pituitary enlargement for a tumour and pursuing unnecessary surgery","Assuming corticotroph function will remain normal, when adrenal insufficiency characteristically appears later","Starting thyroxine without ensuring glucocorticoid cover and precipitating adrenal crisis","Discharging patients after childhood without arranging lifelong axis surveillance","Attributing stalled puberty to constitutional delay rather than evolving gonadotroph loss"],"management":["Replace growth hormone with recombinant human growth hormone to correct growth failure in childhood","Give levothyroxine for central hypothyroidism, ensuring glucocorticoid status is assessed first to avoid precipitating adrenal crisis","Induce and maintain puberty with sex steroid replacement, and use gonadotrophin therapy when fertility is desired","Monitor the adrenal axis lifelong and introduce glucocorticoid replacement with sick-day dosing and a steroid alert card once ACTH deficiency emerges","Undertake serial pituitary MRI to track the characteristically fluctuating gland size and avoid misinterpreting transient enlargement as a tumour","Coordinate lifelong specialist endocrine follow-up, sequencing hormone replacement appropriately","Provide genetic counselling and screen at-risk relatives, as inheritance is autosomal recessive"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of paediatric and adult endocrinology","Clinical guidelines on hypopituitarism and pituitary hormone replacement","Reference text on the genetics of pituitary development"],"crossLinks":[]},"properdin_deficiency":{"name":"Properdin deficiency","system":"Immune system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Properdin deficiency","Factor P deficiency","Complement properdin deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Properdin deficiency is the principal X-linked complement deficiency, so it predominantly affects males.","It markedly increases susceptibility to fulminant meningococcal disease, which can be rapidly fatal even at first presentation.","Meningococcal infection in properdin deficiency often presents acutely and with a high case-fatality rate rather than the recurrent pattern seen in terminal-pathway defects.","Properdin is the only known positive regulator of complement, stabilising the alternative-pathway C3 convertase.","The AH50 alternative-pathway assay is abnormal while the CH50 classical-pathway assay is typically preserved.","A specific low or absent properdin level confirms the diagnosis after alternative-pathway dysfunction is shown.","Meningococcal vaccination, including conjugate quadrivalent and serogroup B vaccines, and family screening are essential.","Patient and family education about seeking urgent care for febrile illness can be life-saving."],"pathophysiology":"Properdin (factor P) is the sole positive regulator of the complement system: it binds and stabilises the alternative-pathway C3 convertase, prolonging its half-life and amplifying complement deposition on microbial surfaces. In its absence the alternative pathway cannot be sustained, so amplification of C3b deposition and downstream membrane attack complex formation on Neisseria is impaired. This leaves the host unable to mount rapid complement-mediated killing of meningococci, producing a striking vulnerability to fulminant meningococcal sepsis. Because the classical and lectin pathways remain functional, susceptibility is largely confined to neisserial organisms, and the disease is inherited in an X-linked pattern, so affected individuals are typically male.","additionalDifferentials":["Terminal complement component deficiencies (C5 to C9)","Factor D or other alternative-pathway defects","Asplenia or hyposplenism","Antibody deficiency predisposing to infection","Idiopathic invasive meningococcal disease","Acquired complement consumption"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["AH50 alternative-pathway assay (abnormal)","CH50 classical-pathway assay (usually preserved)","Specific properdin (factor P) level","Genetic testing of the CFP gene","Cultures and PCR during acute infection to identify serogroup","Meningococcal and other vaccination status","X-linked family screening and carrier assessment"],"redFlags":["Fever with headache, neck stiffness or a non-blanching rash","Rapidly progressive meningococcaemia or septic shock","Any febrile illness in a known properdin-deficient individual","Purpura fulminans","Family history of fatal meningococcal disease"],"prognosis":"The main hazard is fulminant meningococcal disease with a high case-fatality if not treated immediately; with vaccination, education and prompt antibiotics the outlook is greatly improved. Because episodes can be overwhelming at first presentation, prevention and rapid response are paramount.","pitfalls":["Overlooking an X-linked complement defect in a male with severe meningococcal disease.","Relying on a normal CH50 to exclude complement deficiency when the defect is in the alternative pathway.","Failing to provide serogroup B and conjugate meningococcal vaccination.","Not screening male relatives after an index case.","Underestimating how rapidly meningococcaemia can progress in these patients."],"management":["Immunise fully against Neisseria meningitidis with both quadrivalent conjugate (ACWY) and serogroup B meningococcal vaccines, with boosters as advised","Educate the patient and family to seek immediate medical care for any febrile illness and, where appropriate, supply stand-by antibiotics","Treat suspected invasive meningococcal disease as an emergency with immediate parenteral third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone, fluid resuscitation and intensive care support","Consider long-term prophylactic penicillin in selected patients to reduce meningococcal risk","Screen at-risk male relatives and provide genetic counselling, as the deficiency is X-linked","Keep routine immunisations current and refer to clinical immunology for coordinated preventive care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice, current edition","Oxford Textbook of Medicine, current edition","Reviews on complement deficiency and meningococcal susceptibility"],"crossLinks":[]},"propionic_acidemia":{"name":"Propionic acidemia","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PA","propionic aciduria","propionyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency","ketotic hyperglycinemia (historical)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An organic acidaemia caused by deficiency of the biotin-dependent enzyme propionyl-CoA carboxylase","Classic neonatal presentation is life-threatening metabolic decompensation with vomiting, lethargy, hypotonia, seizures and severe high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with ketosis","Secondary hyperammonaemia is common and may dominate the picture, and hypoglycaemia and bone marrow suppression (neutropenia, thrombocytopenia) frequently occur","Elevated propionylcarnitine (C3) on acylcarnitine profile with characteristic urine organic acids (e.g. 3-hydroxypropionate, methylcitrate) supports the diagnosis, and MMA is not elevated (distinguishing it from methylmalonic acidemia)","Long-term complications include cardiomyopathy, prolonged QT and arrhythmia, pancreatitis, movement disorder from basal ganglia injury, and developmental delay","Chronic management principles include protein restriction limiting propiogenic amino acids, carnitine supplementation, and control of intercurrent catabolism, with metronidazole sometimes used to lower gut propionate","Unlike some organic acidaemias this disorder is generally not cofactor-responsive, though biotin is often trialled","Included in newborn screening via elevated C3 acylcarnitine, enabling early diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Propionic acidemia arises from deficient propionyl-CoA carboxylase, a mitochondrial biotin-dependent enzyme that converts propionyl-CoA to methylmalonyl-CoA. This step is part of the catabolic pathway for the amino acids isoleucine, valine, methionine and threonine, as well as odd-chain fatty acids and cholesterol. Enzyme deficiency causes accumulation of propionyl-CoA and its derivatives (propionic acid, 3-hydroxypropionate, methylcitrate, propionylcarnitine). These organic acids produce severe metabolic acidosis, inhibit the urea cycle to cause hyperammonaemia, impair mitochondrial energy production, and are toxic to the myocardium, bone marrow, pancreas and basal ganglia.","additionalDifferentials":["Methylmalonic acidemia","Isovaleric acidemia and other organic acidaemias","Maple syrup urine disease","Urea cycle disorders","Neonatal sepsis and diabetic ketoacidosis","Multiple carboxylase deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood gas with anion gap, glucose, lactate and ammonia","Urine organic acids (methylcitrate, 3-hydroxypropionate) and plasma acylcarnitine (elevated C3)","Full blood count for cytopenias","Newborn screening acylcarnitine profile","Molecular testing of PCCA/PCCB genes","Cardiac evaluation with ECG and echocardiography for cardiomyopathy and QT prolongation"],"redFlags":["Acute metabolic decompensation with severe acidosis and hyperammonaemia is a medical emergency","Encephalopathy from hyperammonaemia during crises","Cardiomyopathy or QT prolongation with risk of life-threatening arrhythmia","Rapid clinical deterioration during infection, fasting or catabolic stress"],"prognosis":"Propionic acidemia is among the more severe organic acidaemias, with high risk of recurrent metabolic crises and long-term neurological and cardiac complications despite treatment; early detection and rigorous metabolic management improve survival, and selected patients are considered for liver transplantation.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with methylmalonic acidemia without checking that methylmalonic acid is not elevated","Overlooking cardiac surveillance, since cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia can be fatal even with good metabolic control","Attributing the acidotic neonate to sepsis and delaying specific therapy","Under-recognising secondary hyperammonaemia as a driver of encephalopathy"],"management":["Institute lifelong dietary protein restriction limiting the propiogenic amino acids isoleucine, valine, methionine and threonine, using specialised propiogenic-free medical formula under dietetic supervision","Supplement L-carnitine to replenish carnitine and promote excretion of propionyl esters, and give intermittent metronidazole to reduce gut bacterial propionate production","Provide an emergency regimen for intercurrent illness with cessation of natural protein and high-energy glucose intake to suppress catabolism, avoiding prolonged fasting","Manage acute decompensation with intravenous dextrose and insulin to reverse catabolism, ammonia-scavenging agents for hyperammonaemia, and haemofiltration or dialysis for severe hyperammonaemia or acidosis","Undertake surveillance for known complications including cardiomyopathy and QT prolongation with ECG and echocardiography, pancreatitis, cytopenias and neurodevelopmental delay","Consider liver transplantation in selected patients with recurrent crises or poor metabolic control","Manage through a metabolic multidisciplinary team and provide genetic counselling with family and newborn screening, given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"propofol_infusion_syndrome":{"name":"Propofol infusion syndrome","system":"Clinical pharmacology / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PRIS","propofol-related infusion syndrome","propofol infusion syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / Miller's Anesthesia","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare but frequently fatal complication of prolonged, high-dose propofol sedation, seen especially in critically ill and paediatric patients","Characterised by a severe high-anion-gap metabolic (lactic) acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia and acute kidney injury","Cardiac features include bradyarrhythmias and progressive myocardial failure that can culminate in refractory cardiac arrest","Risk rises with high infusion rates, prolonged duration, concurrent catecholamine or corticosteroid use, and low carbohydrate intake","A rising lactate, unexplained metabolic acidosis or new arrhythmia in a patient on prolonged propofol should prompt immediate suspicion","Management is immediate cessation of propofol, cardiorespiratory support and correction of acidosis and electrolytes","Refractory cases may need pacing, extracorporeal support or renal replacement therapy to remove metabolites and manage acidosis and hyperkalaemia","Prevention involves limiting the dose and duration of propofol and ensuring adequate carbohydrate provision"],"pathophysiology":"Propofol infusion syndrome is thought to arise from propofol-induced impairment of mitochondrial function. The drug interferes with the electron transport chain and with fatty acid oxidation, particularly the entry of long-chain fatty acids into mitochondria, so cells cannot generate adequate energy from fat and become dependent on glucose. In critical illness, with high catecholamine and cortisol levels and often limited carbohydrate supply, this energy failure is unmasked. The result is accumulation of unused fatty acids, anaerobic metabolism with lactic acidosis, and injury to metabolically active tissues including cardiac and skeletal muscle, producing rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia, renal impairment, myocardial depression and conduction abnormalities. Progressive cardiovascular collapse follows if the drug is not stopped.","additionalDifferentials":["Sepsis with lactic acidosis and multiorgan dysfunction","Cardiogenic shock from other causes","Other causes of rhabdomyolysis","Malignant hyperthermia","Diabetic or other metabolic ketoacidosis","Mitochondrial disease decompensation","Ischaemia-related lactic acidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial arterial blood gases and lactate","Creatine kinase and myoglobin for rhabdomyolysis","Potassium and renal function","Electrocardiogram and continuous cardiac monitoring for bradyarrhythmia","Triglyceride levels (often raised)","Echocardiography to assess myocardial function"],"redFlags":["Unexplained and worsening metabolic acidosis with rising lactate","New bradyarrhythmia or conduction block during prolonged propofol infusion","Rhabdomyolysis with hyperkalaemia","Progressive myocardial dysfunction or refractory cardiac arrest","Green or otherwise discoloured urine with deteriorating haemodynamics"],"prognosis":"Once established with cardiovascular collapse, the syndrome carries a high mortality. Early recognition, immediate withdrawal of propofol and aggressive supportive care, including extracorporeal support in severe cases, offer the best chance of recovery.","pitfalls":["Continuing high-dose propofol despite an unexplained rising lactate or acidosis","Attributing the metabolic derangement solely to sepsis","Overlooking early bradyarrhythmia as a warning sign","Failing to limit propofol dose and duration or to provide adequate carbohydrate in at-risk patients"],"management":["Stop the propofol infusion immediately at first suspicion and substitute an alternative sedative such as a benzodiazepine, dexmedetomidine or opioid","Provide full cardiorespiratory support with vasopressors or inotropes and cardiac pacing for refractory bradyarrhythmia","Correct the metabolic acidosis, hyperkalaemia and other electrolyte disturbances and manage rhabdomyolysis with fluid resuscitation","Institute renal replacement therapy or haemofiltration to clear the drug and its metabolites and control acidosis and hyperkalaemia","Ensure adequate carbohydrate and glucose delivery to counter the underlying energy deficit","Escalate to extracorporeal membrane oxygenation in refractory cardiovascular collapse","Prevent recurrence by limiting infusion rate and duration and monitoring lactate, creatine kinase, potassium and the ECG in patients on prolonged propofol"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"propriospinal_myoclonus":{"name":"Propriospinal myoclonus","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PSM"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / movement disorder literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sudden jerks producing flexion (or occasionally extension) of the trunk, neck and hips, arising from a generator in the spinal cord","Jerks characteristically appear or worsen during relaxed wakefulness and the transition to sleep, often when lying down","The movements spread slowly up and down the cord from a thoracic or abdominal origin, reflecting conduction along propriospinal pathways","Neurophysiology can demonstrate the slow rostro-caudal propagation and a consistent muscle recruitment pattern","A substantial proportion of clinically diagnosed cases are ultimately shown to be functional","Back-averaging and the presence of a Bereitschaftspotential help distinguish organic from functional cases"],"pathophysiology":"Propriospinal myoclonus is generated within the spinal cord, with an initial focus in the mid-thoracic region from which activity propagates slowly up and down the cord along propriospinal interneuronal pathways, producing axial jerks. In some patients a structural spinal lesion is found, but many have no identifiable lesion and a significant number are demonstrated to be functional, in which a premovement Bereitschaftspotential and variable, distractible features indicate a voluntary-type mechanism experienced as involuntary.","additionalDifferentials":["Functional (psychogenic) axial jerks","Hypnic jerks (sleep starts)","Spinal segmental myoclonus","Cortical or subcortical myoclonus","Startle syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Surface electromyography with polygraphy to show slow propagation and recruitment pattern","Back-averaging to seek a premovement Bereitschaftspotential (suggesting functional origin)","MRI of the spinal cord to exclude a structural lesion","Assessment for functional signs such as variability and distractibility","Sleep study if the relationship to sleep onset is prominent"],"redFlags":["A structural spinal cord lesion on imaging","Associated sensory level, weakness or sphincter disturbance","Rapid progression","Features suggesting a broader neurological disorder"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies with cause; lesional cases depend on the underlying pathology, while functional cases can improve with appropriate explanation and therapy, though the disorder is often persistent.","pitfalls":["Assuming an organic spinal cause without seeking a Bereitschaftspotential","Missing a structural spinal lesion in genuinely lesional cases","Confusing it with benign hypnic jerks","Not recognising the high proportion of functional cases"],"management":["Image the spinal cord and treat any identified structural lesion driving the axial jerks","For symptomatic organic myoclonus, trial pharmacotherapy such as clonazepam, with levetiracetam or zonisamide as alternatives","When investigation indicates a functional disorder, deliver a clear, positive explanation of the diagnosis as the basis of treatment","Treat functional cases with tailored physiotherapy and cognitive behavioural therapy rather than escalating drug therapy","Address the sleep-onset relationship with sleep hygiene advice and reassurance","Manage through a multidisciplinary team including neurology, physiotherapy and neuropsychiatry or clinical psychology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Movement Disorders textbook (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"prostate_cancer":{"name":"Prostate Disease (BPH & Cancer)","system":"Oncology / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prostate cancer","BPH","benign prostatic hyperplasia"],"chapter":92,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.695-704","clinicalPearls":["Both benign and malignant prostate changes rise sharply with age: autopsies of men in the eighth decade show hyperplastic changes in >90% and malignant changes in >70%, so management is risk-adapted and weighed against competing causes of morbidity and mortality (p.695).","BPH arises in the periurethral transition zone surrounding the urethra, whereas most cancers arise in the peripheral zone; peripheral-zone cancers may or may not be palpable on digital rectal exam (DRE) (p.696).","BPH causes lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) split into obstructive symptoms (hesitancy, straining, weak stream, terminal dribbling, prolonged voiding, incomplete emptying) and irritative symptoms (frequency, urgency, urge incontinence, small voided volumes); LUTS reflect both prostatic enlargement and age-related detrusor dysfunction, not mass effect alone (p.703).","PSA (kallikrein KLK3) is prostate specific, NOT prostate cancer specific: it can rise from prostatitis, BPH, or cancer; a lower free PSA fraction is more likely associated with cancer at biopsy (p.696-697).","After radical prostatectomy PSA should become undetectable within about 6 weeks (no prostate = no detectable PSA); persistence of measurable PSA after gland removal is typical of recurrent cancer (p.696-697).","Only 20-25% of patients with an abnormal DRE have prostate cancer; carcinoma is characteristically hard, nodular, irregular, asymmetric, but induration can also be BPH or calculi (p.696).",">95% of prostate cancers are adenocarcinomas; the remainder are squamous, transitional cell, rarely carcinosarcoma or small-cell histologies (p.697).","Most men diagnosed with prostate cancer will NOT die of it; the incidence/mortality ratio is extremely high relative to other malignancies, which drives the active-surveillance vs treatment balance (p.695).","Multiparametric prostate MRI (mpMRI) graded by PI-RADS is now obtained in most cases BEFORE biopsy: PI-RADS 1-2 lesions can safely avoid biopsy; PI-RADS 3-5 lesions get targeted biopsy, reducing detection of indolent Gleason 6 (grade group 1) cancers without missing clinically relevant ones (p.697)."],"pathophysiology":"The prostate (pelvic, adjacent to rectum, bladder, neurovascular bundles and urinary sphincter) is composed of tubuloalveolar glands in fibromuscular stroma. PSA is produced by epithelial cells; both epithelial and stromal cells express androgen receptors (AR) and depend on androgens for growth. Testosterone is converted by prostatic 5alpha-reductase to the more potent dihydrotestosterone. BPH is non-malignant proliferation of the periurethral transition zone (begins at puberty, accelerates after age 55) producing bladder-outlet obstruction; symptoms also reflect age-related detrusor dysfunction. Malignancy usually arises in the peripheral zone; the cancer continuum runs from preneoplastic/localized lesion to metastatic, symptomatic, lethal disease over years to decades. Prostate cancer is AR-signaling dependent, so androgen deprivation is the mainstay of metastatic therapy; castration-resistant prostate cancer (CRPC) progresses despite castrate testosterone (<=50 ng/mL) because most CRPCs continue to express and signal through the AR, and androgen-resistant cells are present from diagnosis (cure is not achieved by androgen depletion alone). (p.695-696, 702-703)","additionalDifferentials":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia (transition-zone enlargement, bladder-outlet obstruction)","Acute/chronic bacterial prostatitis (can elevate PSA)","Overactive bladder (frequently coexists with BPH-related LUTS)","Age-related detrusor dysfunction / primary bladder dysfunction (detected on pressure-flow urodynamics)","Urethral stricture or bladder-neck contracture as alternative cause of obstructive voiding","Prostatic calculi (cause of palpable induration on DRE)"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial prostatitis (raises PSA and can mimic malignancy biochemically; treat symptomatic bacterial prostatitis with antibiotics before biopsy, but routine antibiotics in an asymptomatic patient with elevated PSA are discouraged)","Recurrent urinary tract infection as a complication of bladder-outlet obstruction"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["BPH-related induration on DRE mimicking carcinoma","Prostatic calculi causing nodularity","Degenerative spine disease, spinal stenosis, or osteoporotic vertebral collapse mimicking metastatic bone pain in advanced disease (require accurate diagnosis before attributing pain to metastases)","Direct invasion of the prostate by colon cancer or bladder transitional cell carcinoma (true metastases TO the prostate are rare)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Digital rectal exam (assesses size, symmetry, consistency, nodules; NOT a validated standalone screening test due to frequent false positives and false negatives)","Serum PSA (total); free/total PSA ratio; common biopsy-prompting cut point total PSA >=4 ng/mL (no PSA level carries zero cancer risk)","Second-line stratifying tests after elevated PSA: 4K Score, Prostate Health Index (PHI = free PSA + total PSA + [-2]proPSA), urine ExoDx exosome test, Select-MDx mRNA test","Multiparametric prostate MRI (mpMRI) with PI-RADS scoring before biopsy","Image-guided needle biopsy (TRUS-, MRI-, or fusion-guided; extended 12-core pattern; MRI-guided superior to ultrasound-guided)","Gleason grading and the five-tier grade group system (grade group 1 = Gleason <=6 favorable, up to grade group 5 = Gleason 9-10)","TNM staging (T1c PSA-detected, T2 palpable organ-confined, T3-T4 extraprostatic); risk stratification into low/intermediate(favorable vs unfavorable)/high/very-high risk","PSMA PET scan (FDA approved for intermediate/high-risk disease; more sensitive/specific than conventional imaging but cannot detect low-volume microscopic disease)","For BPH: AUA Symptom Index (AUASI) / International Prostate Symptom Score (IPSS), uroflowmetry, bladder ultrasound for postvoid residual, pressure-flow urodynamics, cystoscopy if hematuria, upper-tract imaging if hematuria/calculi/prior tract problems"],"redFlags":["Osteoblastic bone metastases (prostate cancer characteristically produces bone-forming metastases) presenting as back/skeletal pain; bone is the dominant site of metastatic and castration-resistant progression","Spinal cord compression / cord compromise or base-of-skull symptoms from metastatic disease require EMERGENCY evaluation because loss of neurologic function may be permanent if not treated quickly","GnRH agonists are relatively contraindicated in patients with significant urinary obstruction, cancer-related pain, or spinal cord compromise because the initial testosterone surge can cause a disease flare (AR antagonists are added to blunt flare; GnRH antagonists and abiraterone do not cause a surge)","BPH danger signs mandating evaluation/treatment regardless of gland size: inability to urinate (acute urinary retention), gross hematuria, recurrent infection, or bladder stones","Rising nadir PSA >4 ng/mL at 6-8 months after starting androgen deprivation predicts markedly inferior survival","Marrow infiltration (myelophthisis), coagulopathy, or thromboembolism as manifestations of widespread metastatic disease"],"prognosis":"Most men with prostate cancer do not die from it; >70-80% of newly diagnosed cancers are now organ-confined. Nonmetastatic disease is stratified low/intermediate/high (and very-high) risk; grade group system accurately predicts prognosis with grade group 1 (Gleason <=6) being favorable. A single PSA at age 60 correlates strongly (AUC 0.90) with lifetime risk of death from prostate cancer, and ~90% of prostate-cancer deaths occur in the top PSA quartile (>2 ng/mL), though only a minority of those men develop lethal disease. Androgen deprivation produces an initial response and dormant period but is not curative; PSA normalizes in 60-70% and measurable disease regresses in >50%, with survival inversely proportional to disease extent at treatment start and to the 6-8 month nadir PSA. Castration-resistant prostate cancer is lethal for most patients; multiple agents (abiraterone, enzalutamide, docetaxel, cabazitaxel, radium-223, Lu-177 PSMA-617, PARP inhibitors for BRCA/DNA-repair mutations, checkpoint inhibitors for MSI-high/dMMR) prolong survival. (p.695, 697, 701-703)","pitfalls":["PSA screening is controversial: it generates both false positives and false negatives and can lead to diagnosis and treatment of indolent cancers that pose little risk; the decision (USPSTF: individual shared decision for men 55-69, recommended AGAINST screening at age >=70; AUA: shared decision, earlier for Black ancestry/germline mutations/strong family history) must balance benefit against overtreatment harm.","PSA is prostate specific, not cancer specific - do not equate an elevated PSA with cancer; BPH and prostatitis also raise it.","There is NO PSA level below which prostate cancer risk is zero (Prostate Cancer Prevention Trial); a 'normal' PSA does not exclude cancer.","DRE alone is inaccurate for staging (capsular invasion, seminal-vesicle and nodal involvement) and is not a validated screening test by itself.","Patients misread Gleason 6 as 'mid-scale' and assume aggressive disease; the grade group system was created because Gleason 6 (grade group 1) is actually the lowest, favorable-risk category.","PSMA PET, despite high sensitivity, cannot detect low-volume microscopic nodal disease - a negative scan does not exclude micrometastasis.","A negative biopsy in a patient with elevated PSA does NOT rule out a future cancer diagnosis - continue follow-up.","No agent is currently approved for prostate cancer prevention (5-alpha-reductase inhibitors reduced mainly low-risk indolent cancers; vitamins/supplements showed no benefit).","No rationale to screen asymptomatic men with short life expectancy; antiandrogen monotherapy gives inferior cancer-specific outcomes versus castration."],"management":["For benign prostatic hyperplasia with bothersome lower urinary tract symptoms, start an alpha-blocker such as tamsulosin for rapid symptom relief and add a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor such as finasteride or dutasteride to shrink larger glands","Offer transurethral resection of the prostate or another surgical or minimally invasive intervention for BPH refractory to medical therapy or complicated by retention, stones or recurrent infection","Manage localised prostate cancer by risk stratification, offering active surveillance for low-risk disease and radical prostatectomy or radiotherapy for intermediate- and high-risk disease","Treat locally advanced and metastatic disease with androgen deprivation therapy using GnRH agonists or antagonists, intensified with androgen-receptor pathway inhibitors such as abiraterone or enzalutamide, or with docetaxel chemotherapy","Support men on androgen deprivation therapy by protecting bone density, using bone-targeted agents such as bisphosphonates or denosumab for bone metastases, and providing continence and erectile rehabilitation after radical treatment","Follow up with serial PSA surveillance to detect recurrence, expecting PSA to become undetectable after radical prostatectomy","Refer to a urology and oncology multidisciplinary team and offer genetic counselling where a strong family history or features suggest a BRCA or other hereditary predisposition"],"severityClassification":"Gleason grade group system: grade group 1 (Gleason <=6), 2 (3+4=7), 3 (4+3=7), 4 (4+4=8), 5 (Gleason 9-10). TNM staging: T1c clinically inapparent PSA-detected; T2 palpable, confined within prostate; T3 extends through capsule/seminal vesicles (T3b); T4 fixed or invading adjacent structures. Nonmetastatic risk groups: low (organ-confined, PSA <10, grade group 1); intermediate (organ-confined, PSA 10-20, or grade group 2 favorable / 3 unfavorable, with >50% positive cores = unfavorable); high (extraprostatic, PSA >20, or grade group 4-5); very-high (seminal vesicle T3b or adjacent-organ T4 invasion or multiple high-grade biopsy samples). Metastatic states: noncastrate metastatic (testosterone >50 ng/dL) vs castration-resistant (CRPC, testosterone <=50 ng/mL). (p.697-698, 701-703)","references":["Harrison 22e p.695-704"],"crossLinks":["cord_compression"]},"prostate_intraepithelial_neoplasia":{"name":"Prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia","system":"Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia","PIN","high-grade PIN","HGPIN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / WHO Classification of Tumours: Urinary and Male Genital Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A histological diagnosis describing a neoplastic proliferation of epithelial cells within pre-existing prostatic ducts and acini while the basal cell layer remains intact","Graded as low-grade or high-grade, but only high-grade PIN (HGPIN) is regarded as a meaningful precursor of prostatic adenocarcinoma","It is an incidental biopsy finding that produces no specific symptoms and does not itself raise PSA","Multifocal or extensive HGPIN on biopsy carries a higher chance of finding cancer on subsequent sampling than a single focus","Isolated single-focus HGPIN no longer mandates immediate repeat biopsy under most current guidance, whereas multifocal HGPIN may prompt repeat or extended sampling","It is distinguished from atypical small acinar proliferation (ASAP), which carries a substantially higher likelihood of accompanying cancer and usually prompts early rebiopsy","Management is surveillance rather than treatment, since PIN is not itself invasive cancer"],"pathophysiology":"PIN represents dysplastic change within the luminal secretory epithelium of prostatic ducts and acini. The cells display nuclear enlargement and prominent nucleoli that resemble carcinoma, yet a basal cell layer is retained, separating the lesion from invasive disease. It is thought to arise through the accumulation of genetic and epigenetic changes and shares molecular alterations with adenocarcinoma, supporting a precursor role, particularly in the peripheral zone where most cancers originate.","additionalDifferentials":["Invasive prostatic adenocarcinoma","Atypical small acinar proliferation (ASAP)","Atypical adenomatous hyperplasia (adenosis)","Reactive or inflammatory epithelial atypia","Basal cell hyperplasia","Central zone histology mimicking PIN"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Histopathological review, ideally by a specialist uropathologist, to confirm grade and focality","Immunohistochemistry for basal cell markers (high-molecular-weight cytokeratin, p63) and AMACR to separate PIN from invasive carcinoma","PSA monitoring as part of overall prostate cancer risk assessment","Multiparametric MRI to identify suspicious areas for targeted rebiopsy","Repeat or targeted biopsy when HGPIN is multifocal or ASAP coexists","Documentation of the number of cores involved and the extent of involvement"],"redFlags":["Coexisting atypical small acinar proliferation, which raises the probability of undetected cancer","Multifocal or extensive high-grade PIN","A rising PSA or an MRI lesion suggesting occult carcinoma","A strong family history or other risk factors amplifying concern for cancer"],"prognosis":"PIN is not itself malignant and does not shorten life; the significance of high-grade PIN lies in an increased likelihood of concurrent or subsequent prostate cancer, which appropriate surveillance aims to detect.","pitfalls":["Treating PIN as though it were cancer and offering radical therapy","Ignoring multifocal HGPIN or coexisting ASAP that warrants rebiopsy","Confusing clinically inconsequential low-grade PIN with high-grade PIN","Assuming PIN explains a raised PSA and thereby missing an underlying cancer"],"management":["Recognise that PIN is not itself malignant and requires no direct treatment, only appropriate follow-up for the associated cancer risk","Confirm the grade and focality on specialist uropathology review, using basal-cell markers and AMACR to exclude invasive carcinoma","Do not routinely rebiopsy isolated single-focus high-grade PIN; instead continue PSA monitoring and clinical follow-up","Arrange repeat, extended or targeted biopsy for multifocal or extensive high-grade PIN, or when atypical small acinar proliferation coexists","Use multiparametric MRI to identify suspicious areas for targeted rebiopsy","Continue PSA surveillance within overall prostate cancer risk assessment and refer to urology for ongoing monitoring"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","WHO Classification of Tumours: Urinary and Male Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":["verumontanum_hyperplasia"]},"prostatitis_chronic_bacterial":{"name":"Chronic bacterial prostatitis","system":"Urology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic bacterial prostatitis","NIH category II prostatitis","recurrent prostatic infection","CBP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / EAU Guidelines on Urological Infections","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Corresponds to NIH category II prostatitis and is defined by recurrent urinary tract infections caused by the same uropathogen, most often Escherichia coli and related Enterobacteriaceae, seeding from a persistent prostatic focus","Between symptomatic flares men may be well or have only low-grade perineal or pelvic discomfort, mild voiding symptoms, or post-ejaculatory pain","The prostate behaves as a protected reservoir in which organisms persist within ducts, biofilm and calcifications, which explains relapse after short antibiotic courses","Segmented localisation cultures, either the four-glass Meares-Stamey test or the simpler pre- and post-massage two-glass test, support a prostatic source when post-massage specimens grow markedly higher counts of the same organism","It is separated from chronic pelvic pain syndrome (NIH category III) by demonstrable bacterial growth localised to prostatic secretions","Management relies on a prolonged course of an antibiotic with good prostatic penetration guided by culture and sensitivity, rather than a brief course","Alpha-blockers, treatment of prostatic calculi and attention to sources of reinfection may reduce the relapse rate"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic bacterial prostatitis develops when ascending or intraprostatic infection establishes a persistent nidus, frequently within obstructed ducts, prostatic calculi, or areas of intraprostatic reflux of infected urine. The blood-prostate barrier and poor antibiotic penetration into non-inflamed prostatic tissue allow organisms to survive, and biofilm on prostatic stones shelters bacteria, producing the characteristic relapsing pattern of recurrent lower urinary tract infection with an identical organism.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic pelvic pain syndrome (NIH category III prostatitis)","Acute bacterial prostatitis (NIH category I)","Benign prostatic enlargement with recurrent infection","Bladder outlet obstruction or incomplete emptying causing recurrent urinary tract infection","Interstitial cystitis / bladder pain syndrome","Urethritis, including sexually transmitted infection","Prostatic abscess"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Segmented localisation cultures (four-glass Meares-Stamey test or two-glass pre- and post-massage test)","Midstream urine culture during symptomatic episodes to identify the recurrent organism","Microscopy and culture of expressed prostatic secretions","Post-void residual measurement and uroflowmetry to assess for obstruction","Transrectal ultrasound or MRI when abscess or prostatic calculi are suspected","PSA interpreted with caution because prostatic inflammation can raise it","Sexually transmitted infection screening where urethritis is possible"],"redFlags":["High fever, rigors or systemic sepsis suggesting acute prostatitis or a prostatic abscess","Acute urinary retention","Failure to respond to appropriately targeted therapy, raising concern for abscess or a resistant organism","A rising PSA that does not settle after treatment, warranting exclusion of malignancy"],"prognosis":"Many men achieve cure or prolonged remission after an adequate, prolonged course of a prostate-penetrating antibiotic, but relapse is common, particularly when prostatic calculi harbour organisms, and some follow a chronic relapsing course requiring suppressive or repeated treatment.","pitfalls":["Using short antibiotic courses that fail to achieve adequate prostatic penetration, leading to relapse","Not performing localisation cultures and so mislabelling abacterial category III disease as bacterial","Overlooking prostatic calculi as a reservoir of persistent infection","Attributing a raised PSA solely to prostatitis without arranging follow-up to exclude cancer"],"management":["Treat with a prolonged course of an antibiotic that penetrates the prostate, such as a fluoroquinolone like ciprofloxacin or trimethoprim, guided by culture and sensitivity","Relieve voiding symptoms with an alpha-blocker and provide analgesia or anti-inflammatory drugs for pelvic and post-ejaculatory pain","Identify and address predisposing factors such as bladder-outlet obstruction and prostatic calculi that harbour organisms","Confirm the prostatic source and eradication with segmented localisation and follow-up cultures","Use low-dose suppressive antibiotic therapy or repeated culture-directed courses for the relapsing pattern","Consider surgical intervention such as transurethral resection to remove infected calculi in refractory relapsing disease, and drain any prostatic abscess","Refer to urology for recurrent or treatment-resistant disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","EAU Guidelines on Urological Infections"],"crossLinks":["verumontanum_hyperplasia","posterior_urethral_polyp"]},"protein_c_deficiency":{"name":"Protein C deficiency","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PROC deficiency","hereditary protein C deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AABB Technical Manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Protein C is a vitamin K-dependent natural anticoagulant that, once activated, inactivates factors Va and VIIIa; its deficiency is a strong inherited thrombophilia","Heterozygous deficiency causes venous thromboembolism, often beginning in young adulthood and sometimes provoked by additional risk factors","Homozygous or compound heterozygous deficiency presents in the newborn period with purpura fulminans, a life-threatening microvascular thrombotic emergency","Starting a vitamin K antagonist without concurrent heparin-type cover can precipitate skin necrosis, because protein C levels fall faster than the procoagulant factors","Levels are lowered by acute thrombosis, liver disease, vitamin K deficiency, and vitamin K antagonist therapy, so timing of testing is critical","Both functional (activity) and antigenic assays are used to distinguish reduced production from a dysfunctional protein","Confirmation ideally requires repeat testing away from the acute event and off vitamin K antagonists, with family studies where appropriate"],"pathophysiology":"Protein C circulates as an inactive precursor and is activated on the endothelial surface by the thrombin-thrombomodulin complex. Activated protein C, with its cofactor protein S, proteolytically inactivates factors Va and VIIIa, thereby limiting thrombin generation. Inherited deficiency, whether from reduced synthesis (type I) or a functionally impaired protein (type II), removes this brake and shifts haemostasis toward thrombosis. The short half-life of protein C explains the transient hypercoagulable window at the start of vitamin K antagonist therapy, when protein C is depleted before the longer-lived procoagulant factors, producing the risk of skin necrosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Protein S deficiency","Antithrombin deficiency","Factor V Leiden and prothrombin variant","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Disseminated intravascular coagulation causing acquired protein C consumption"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Functional protein C activity assay as the primary test","Protein C antigen level to classify the deficiency type","Repeat testing away from acute thrombosis and off vitamin K antagonists","Assessment for coexisting thrombophilic defects","Family studies and genetic testing where results will guide management"],"redFlags":["Neonatal purpura fulminans indicating severe homozygous deficiency and requiring emergency treatment","Skin necrosis developing after initiation of a vitamin K antagonist","Extensive or recurrent venous thromboembolism at a young age","Widespread microvascular thrombosis suggesting an acute prothrombotic crisis"],"prognosis":"Heterozygous carriers have a good prognosis with appropriate management of thrombotic events and high-risk periods, whereas homozygous deficiency presenting with neonatal purpura fulminans is life-threatening and requires urgent replacement and long-term anticoagulation.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing deficiency from levels drawn during acute thrombosis or vitamin K antagonist therapy","Initiating a vitamin K antagonist without overlapping parenteral anticoagulation, risking skin necrosis","Confusing acquired causes such as liver disease or DIC with inherited deficiency","Failing to repeat and confirm a low result before committing to lifelong conclusions"],"management":["Treat acute venous thromboembolism with anticoagulation, starting heparin such as low-molecular-weight heparin and overlapping before introducing any vitamin K antagonist","Never begin a vitamin K antagonist without concurrent heparin cover, to avoid warfarin-induced skin necrosis from the rapid fall in protein C","Individualise the duration of anticoagulation, favouring extended treatment after unprovoked or recurrent thrombosis, using a DOAC or a vitamin K antagonist","Treat neonatal purpura fulminans as an emergency with protein C concentrate or fresh frozen plasma replacement alongside anticoagulation","Provide thromboprophylaxis during high-risk periods such as surgery, immobility and pregnancy, and counsel against oestrogen-containing contraception","Offer family testing and genetic counselling, and confirm the diagnosis with repeat testing away from acute thrombosis and off vitamin K antagonists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"protein_losing_enteropathy":{"name":"Protein-Losing Enteropathy","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLE","intestinal protein loss","gut protein loss"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is a syndrome, not a single disease, in which excessive loss of plasma proteins into the gastrointestinal tract leads to hypoproteinaemia and oedema.","It should be suspected in a child with hypoalbuminaemia and oedema once liver disease, kidney protein loss, and inadequate intake have been excluded.","Underlying mechanisms fall into two broad groups: lymphatic obstruction or elevated lymphatic pressure, and mucosal disease with increased permeability or erosion.","Lymphatic causes include intestinal lymphangiectasia and elevated central venous pressure after cardiac surgery (notably the Fontan circulation), while mucosal causes include inflammatory, allergic, infectious, and infiltrative enteropathies.","Because losses are non-selective, other serum proteins such as immunoglobulins and clotting factors may also fall, sometimes causing lymphopenia and predisposing to infection.","A stool alpha-1-antitrypsin measurement is a useful non-invasive marker of enteric protein loss because it is not degraded in the gut.","Management is directed at the underlying cause together with nutritional support, and in lymphatic causes a low-fat diet enriched with medium-chain triglycerides can reduce lymphatic protein loss.","Peripheral oedema, ascites, or effusions in a child with unexplained low albumin should prompt evaluation of the gut as a source."],"pathophysiology":"Plasma proteins normally enter the gut lumen only in small amounts. In protein-losing enteropathy this loss is markedly increased by one of two general processes. In lymphatic disorders, obstructed or high-pressure intestinal lymphatics (from congenital lymphangiectasia or raised systemic venous pressure, as after Fontan surgery) leak protein-rich lymph into the lumen. In mucosal disorders, inflammation, ulceration, or increased epithelial permeability from infectious, allergic, inflammatory, or infiltrative disease allows serum proteins to escape across the damaged barrier. Because the loss is non-selective, albumin, immunoglobulins, and other proteins are depleted, producing hypoalbuminaemia, oedema, and sometimes immune compromise.","additionalDifferentials":["Nephrotic syndrome","Chronic liver disease with impaired synthesis","Malnutrition or inadequate protein intake","Malabsorption syndromes","Congestive heart failure","Sepsis or capillary leak states"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum albumin and total protein","Stool alpha-1-antitrypsin (clearance)","Serum immunoglobulins and lymphocyte count","Urinalysis to exclude renal protein loss","Endoscopy with biopsy when mucosal disease is suspected","Imaging or echocardiography to assess lymphatics and cardiac status"],"redFlags":["Severe hypoalbuminaemia with anasarca, ascites, or effusions","Signs of immunodeficiency or recurrent infection from immunoglobulin loss","Evidence of underlying serious cardiac or lymphatic disease","Growth failure","Features of an underlying inflammatory or malignant infiltrative process"],"prognosis":"Outlook depends heavily on the underlying cause; some mucosal causes resolve when the primary disease is treated, whereas lymphangiectasia and Fontan-associated protein loss can be chronic and difficult to manage.","pitfalls":["Attributing hypoalbuminaemia and oedema to renal or hepatic causes without considering the gut","Overlooking accompanying immunoglobulin loss and infection risk","Failing to identify and treat the specific underlying disorder","Neglecting nutritional support and the role of a low-fat, medium-chain triglyceride diet in lymphatic loss"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying cause, for example immunosuppression for inflammatory bowel disease or an elimination diet for allergic enteropathy","For intestinal lymphangiectasia, institute a low-fat diet enriched with medium-chain triglycerides to reduce lymphatic protein loss, with dietitian support","In Fontan-associated protein-losing enteropathy, optimise the circulation and consider therapies such as heparin, budesonide, pulmonary vasodilators or Fontan revision","Provide nutritional support with adequate protein and energy, and use intravenous albumin and immunoglobulin replacement when clinically indicated","Monitor for infection resulting from immunoglobulin and lymphocyte loss, and manage oedema with attention to fluid balance","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across paediatric gastroenterology, cardiology, dietetics and immunology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"protein_s_deficiency":{"name":"Protein S deficiency","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PROS1 deficiency","hereditary protein S deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AABB Technical Manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Protein S is a vitamin K-dependent cofactor that enhances the anticoagulant action of activated protein C; its deficiency predisposes to venous thromboembolism","Only free protein S is functionally active, because much of it circulates bound to C4b-binding protein","Deficiency is classified by total antigen, free antigen, and functional activity, which distinguish the different subtypes","Levels are physiologically lowered by pregnancy, oestrogen therapy, and the acute-phase response, and by vitamin K antagonists, so testing timing strongly affects interpretation","As with protein C, severe homozygous deficiency can cause neonatal purpura fulminans, and vitamin K antagonist initiation without cover risks skin necrosis","Free protein S measurement is generally preferred, and confirmation requires repeat testing under stable conditions off interfering drugs","A result should be interpreted alongside clinical risk, since isolated low values do not by themselves dictate anticoagulation duration"],"pathophysiology":"Protein S acts as a non-enzymatic cofactor that accelerates the inactivation of factors Va and VIIIa by activated protein C, and it also has some direct anticoagulant activity. In plasma it exists in a free active form and a fraction bound to C4b-binding protein, which is inactive as a cofactor. Inherited deficiency reduces free active protein S and weakens the activated protein C pathway, promoting thrombin generation and thrombosis. Because C4b-binding protein is an acute-phase reactant and oestrogen and pregnancy lower free protein S, acquired reductions are common and can be mistaken for inherited deficiency.","additionalDifferentials":["Protein C deficiency","Antithrombin deficiency","Factor V Leiden and prothrombin variant","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Acquired protein S reduction from pregnancy, oestrogen, or acute illness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Free protein S antigen measurement as the preferred screen","Total protein S antigen and functional activity to classify subtype","Repeat testing under stable conditions, off vitamin K antagonists and away from pregnancy or acute illness","Assessment for coexisting thrombophilic defects","Family studies and genetic testing where they will influence care"],"redFlags":["Neonatal purpura fulminans indicating severe homozygous deficiency","Skin necrosis after starting a vitamin K antagonist","Extensive or recurrent venous thromboembolism at a young age","Widespread microvascular thrombosis requiring urgent intervention"],"prognosis":"Heterozygous deficiency generally carries a good prognosis with appropriate treatment of events and prophylaxis during high-risk periods, while severe homozygous deficiency presenting with purpura fulminans is a life-threatening neonatal emergency.","pitfalls":["Interpreting low levels during pregnancy, oestrogen use, or acute illness as inherited deficiency","Measuring total rather than free protein S and missing or misclassifying the defect","Starting a vitamin K antagonist without overlapping parenteral anticoagulation","Testing during acute thrombosis or on anticoagulation without confirmatory repeat testing"],"management":["Treat acute venous thromboembolism with anticoagulation, beginning heparin such as low-molecular-weight heparin and overlapping before any vitamin K antagonist","Avoid initiating a vitamin K antagonist without heparin cover, given the risk of skin necrosis analogous to protein C deficiency","Individualise the duration of anticoagulation, using a DOAC or vitamin K antagonist, with extended therapy after unprovoked or recurrent events","Manage severe homozygous deficiency presenting as neonatal purpura fulminans as an emergency with fresh frozen plasma replacement and anticoagulation","Give thromboprophylaxis during surgery, immobility and pregnancy, and counsel against oestrogen-containing contraception","Confirm the diagnosis by free protein S measurement repeated under stable conditions off vitamin K antagonists and away from pregnancy or acute illness, and offer family testing and counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"proteus_syndrome":{"name":"Proteus syndrome","system":"Genetics / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AKT1 mosaic overgrowth","AKT1","PROS-related overgrowth"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / relevant specialty textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by a postzygotic activating variant in AKT1, resulting in somatic mosaicism, so the condition is sporadic and non-inherited","Defined by progressive, asymmetric and disproportionate overgrowth of bone, connective tissue, skin and other tissues that worsens over childhood","The cerebriform connective tissue naevus, most often on the soles, is a highly specific hallmark","Overgrowth is characteristically distorting and mosaic in distribution, sparing some areas while dramatically affecting others","Associated features include vascular malformations, lipomatous overgrowth or regional lipohypoplasia, and epidermal naevi","There is a significant risk of deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism, a major cause of premature death, warranting heightened perioperative vigilance","The AKT1 variant activates the PI3K/AKT/mTOR growth-signalling pathway, driving the tissue overgrowth"],"pathophysiology":"Proteus syndrome is caused by a specific activating variant in AKT1 arising after fertilisation, producing a mosaic population of cells carrying the change; a constitutional (germline) variant is thought to be lethal, which is why the disorder occurs only as somatic mosaicism. AKT1 is a central kinase in the phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase (PI3K)/AKT/mTOR signalling pathway, and constitutive activation promotes cell growth, proliferation and survival. Because only a subset of cells is affected and this expands during development, the result is progressive, patchy and disproportionate overgrowth of the involved tissues. The variable tissue distribution accounts for the striking clinical heterogeneity.","additionalDifferentials":["PIK3CA-related overgrowth spectrum (PROS), including CLOVES syndrome","Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome","Hemihyperplasia and isolated overgrowth disorders","Neurofibromatosis type 1 with plexiform overgrowth","Bannayan-Riley-Ruvalcaba syndrome (PTEN)","Maffucci syndrome and other vascular/skeletal overgrowth conditions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted molecular testing for the AKT1 activating variant on affected (lesional) tissue given mosaicism","Skeletal imaging to assess progressive bony overgrowth and deformity","MRI to characterise soft-tissue, lipomatous and vascular components","Vascular imaging and Doppler studies for malformations and thrombosis risk","Histopathology of the cerebriform connective tissue naevus or other lesions","Assessment for pulmonary cystic disease and abdominal/pelvic tumours where indicated"],"redFlags":["Symptoms or signs of deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism, especially perioperatively","Rapidly progressive overgrowth causing functional impairment or airway/organ compromise","Neurological symptoms from central nervous system or skull involvement","New masses raising concern for the tumours occasionally associated with the syndrome"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies widely with the extent and location of overgrowth; progressive skeletal deformity and functional disability are common, and venous thromboembolism is a leading cause of premature mortality. Multidisciplinary management can reduce complications, but the overgrowth itself is difficult to reverse.","pitfalls":["Testing unaffected blood and missing the low-level mosaic AKT1 variant present only in lesional tissue","Applying the label loosely to any overgrowth condition rather than meeting stringent diagnostic criteria","Underestimating thromboembolic risk around surgery and immobilisation","Confusing the condition with PIK3CA-related overgrowth, which has different management implications"],"management":["Address the markedly raised risk of venous thromboembolism with vigilant thromboprophylaxis, especially perioperatively, as pulmonary embolism is a leading cause of premature death","Manage progressive skeletal overgrowth and deformity with orthopaedic surgery, epiphysiodesis and orthotics to preserve function","Consider AKT-pathway targeted therapy such as the AKT inhibitor miransertib, currently within trials, given the underlying AKT1 and PI3K-AKT-mTOR activation","Treat vascular malformations and lipomatous overgrowth with appropriate surgical, interventional or debulking procedures","Undertake surveillance for complications including pulmonary cystic disease and abdominal or pelvic tumours","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across orthopaedics, plastic surgery, dermatology, vascular and respiratory specialties","Provide genetic counselling explaining the postzygotic mosaic AKT1 cause, reassuring that the condition is sporadic and not inherited"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","GeneReviews: Proteus Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"prothrombin_g20210a_mutation":{"name":"Prothrombin G20210A mutation","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["F2 G20210A","factor II G20210A","prothrombin 20210A variant","prothrombin gene mutation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common inherited thrombophilia, second in frequency to factor V Leiden among people of European ancestry, and rare in African and Asian populations","Heterozygotes carry a modestly increased risk of venous thromboembolism, while homozygotes and those who also carry factor V Leiden have a higher risk","The variant tends to present as first venous thrombosis in a young adult, often provoked by an added risk such as combined oral contraception, pregnancy, surgery, or immobility","Plasma prothrombin activity is typically raised, but the diagnosis rests on targeted DNA analysis of the F2 gene rather than a functional assay","Arterial disease association is weak and inconsistent; the variant is chiefly a venous thrombosis risk factor","Detection influences counselling on hormonal contraception, pregnancy, and perioperative thromboprophylaxis more than it dictates the intensity of acute treatment","Family screening is offered selectively, since a positive result in an asymptomatic relative rarely changes management on its own"],"pathophysiology":"The G20210A change lies in the 3' untranslated region of the prothrombin (factor II) gene and acts as a gain-of-function variant that enhances messenger RNA processing and stability. This raises circulating prothrombin concentration, and the excess substrate shifts the coagulation balance towards greater thrombin generation. The resulting mild hypercoagulable state predisposes to venous thromboembolism, with risk amplified when the variant coincides with other inherited or acquired prothrombotic factors.","additionalDifferentials":["Factor V Leiden and activated protein C resistance","Antithrombin deficiency","Protein C deficiency","Protein S deficiency","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Acquired provoking states such as malignancy, pregnancy, or oestrogen therapy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted F2 gene analysis to confirm the G20210A variant and distinguish heterozygous from homozygous status","Plasma prothrombin activity, which may be elevated but is not diagnostic","Concurrent factor V Leiden testing to detect combined thrombophilia","Functional assays for antithrombin, protein C and protein S to exclude coexisting deficiencies","Antiphospholipid antibody screen when an acquired thrombophilia is suspected","Objective imaging to confirm and characterise a suspected venous thromboembolic event"],"redFlags":["Extensive or life-threatening venous thromboembolism such as massive pulmonary embolism","Thrombosis at an unusual site such as cerebral, splanchnic, or hepatic veins","Recurrent venous thrombosis despite adequate anticoagulation","Thrombosis in pregnancy or the puerperium suggesting compounded risk"],"prognosis":"Most heterozygous carriers remain asymptomatic throughout life, and thrombotic risk is meaningfully increased only in the presence of additional triggers or coexisting thrombophilia. Long-term outlook depends on the burden of provoking factors and the completeness of anticoagulation after any event.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a raised prothrombin level as diagnostic without confirmatory genetic testing","Over-testing asymptomatic relatives when the result will not alter management","Assuming a strong arterial thrombosis link, which the evidence does not support","Neglecting compounded risk when the variant coexists with factor V Leiden or oestrogen exposure"],"management":["Treat an acute venous thromboembolic event with standard anticoagulation — heparin followed by a DOAC or vitamin K antagonist — at the same intensity as in patients without thrombophilia","Individualise anticoagulation duration, considering extended therapy after unprovoked or recurrent thrombosis and in homozygotes or those who also carry factor V Leiden","Provide thromboprophylaxis during high-risk periods such as surgery, immobility and pregnancy, using low-molecular-weight heparin in pregnancy where indicated","Counsel against combined oestrogen-containing contraception and advise on modifiable thrombotic risk factors","Avoid anticoagulating asymptomatic carriers indefinitely, reserving treatment for thrombotic events and high-risk situations","Offer family testing and genetic counselling, recognising that detection mainly guides prophylaxis and counselling rather than acute treatment intensity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Hoffman: Hematology - Basic Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"protothecosis":{"name":"Protothecosis (Prototheca algae)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Prototheca infection","Prototheca wickerhamii","Prototheca zopfii","algal infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Protothecosis is caused by Prototheca, achlorophyllous (colourless) algae found in soil, water and organic matter","The commonest human forms are localised cutaneous or subcutaneous lesions and olecranon bursitis, often after traumatic inoculation","Disseminated and systemic disease is largely confined to immunosuppressed hosts, including those on corticosteroids or with malignancy or transplantation","Skin lesions are indolent and polymorphic, presenting as papules, plaques, ulcers or verrucous nodules","Diagnosis rests on histology and culture: tissue shows characteristic sporangia containing endospores arranged like a morula, and the organism grows on fungal media","Antifungal azoles or amphotericin-based therapy are typically used, and surgical excision is valuable for localised disease","Response can be slow, and relapse is common when immunosuppression persists"],"pathophysiology":"Prototheca species are ubiquitous unicellular algae that lack chlorophyll and reproduce asexually by internal division to form sporangia packed with endospores. Human disease follows inoculation of the organism through skin trauma or, less often, through mucosal or catheter-associated routes. In immunocompetent hosts, infection tends to remain localised as a granulomatous cutaneous or bursal lesion, whereas defects in cell-mediated immunity permit deeper and disseminated spread. The relatively indolent, granulomatous tissue response reflects the organism's slow replication and its resistance to clearance by impaired host defences.","additionalDifferentials":["Cutaneous and subcutaneous fungal infections such as sporotrichosis and chromoblastomycosis","Atypical mycobacterial skin infection","Cutaneous nocardiosis or actinomycosis","Non-infective granulomatous dermatoses","Bacterial or crystal-induced olecranon bursitis","Cutaneous leishmaniasis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin or tissue biopsy for histology showing sporangia with internal endospores (morula-like)","Culture of tissue or aspirate on fungal media (e.g. Sabouraud agar)","Special stains such as periodic acid-Schiff and Gomori methenamine silver to highlight the organism","Aspiration and analysis of bursal fluid in olecranon bursitis","Assessment of immune status, including HIV testing and review of immunosuppression","Imaging or blood cultures if disseminated disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["Disseminated or systemic protothecosis in a profoundly immunosuppressed patient","Progressive, non-healing skin lesions despite empirical antibacterial therapy","New infection in a transplant recipient or patient on high-dose corticosteroids","Signs of deep or visceral involvement such as fever with organ dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Localised cutaneous and bursal disease in immunocompetent hosts responds reasonably well to excision and antifungal therapy, but disseminated infection in the immunocompromised is difficult to treat and carries a poorer outcome, with relapse likely if immune suppression cannot be reduced.","pitfalls":["Mistaking indolent skin lesions for a fungal infection without confirming the algal aetiology","Failing to send tissue for both histology and culture","Overlooking underlying immunosuppression that predisposes to dissemination","Expecting rapid resolution and abandoning therapy prematurely"],"management":["Antifungal therapy as the pharmacological mainstay, using an azole such as itraconazole or voriconazole, or amphotericin B for deeper or disseminated infection","Surgical excision or debridement of localised cutaneous, subcutaneous or olecranon bursal lesions, usually combined with antifungal cover","Aspiration and drainage of bursal fluid in olecranon bursitis, with attentive wound care","Where feasible, reduce or reverse immunosuppression (taper corticosteroids, optimise malignancy or transplant management) to aid clearance","Assess and address host immune status, including HIV testing and review of iatrogenic immunosuppression","Surveillance for dissemination in immunocompromised hosts, with prolonged treatment and monitoring for relapse","Referral to infectious diseases specialists, with susceptibility-guided therapy for resistant disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"protozoal_intestinal_infections_and_tricho":{"name":"Protozoal Intestinal Infections and Trichomoniasis","system":"Gastrointestinal / Genitourinary (infectious)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Giardiasis","Cryptosporidiosis","Cystoisosporiasis","Cyclosporiasis","Microsporidiosis","Balantidiasis","Blastocystosis","Dientamoebiasis","Sarcocystosis","Trichomonas vaginalis infection","Intestinal protozoa"],"chapter":236,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1804","clinicalPearls":["Giardia (G. duodenalis, assemblages A and B in humans) is a non-invasive parasite confined to the proximal small bowel that does not spread hematogenously; an inoculum of as few as ~10 cysts can establish infection, and cysts resist routine chlorination but are killed by boiling or filtration.","Giardiasis classically presents with upper-intestinal/small-bowel features (nausea, vomiting, bloating, belching, sulfurous flatus, abdominal pain) and non-bloody diarrhea; fever, blood, or mucus in stool point away from giardiasis toward colitis or a concomitant illness.","Cryptosporidium (C. hominis, C. parvum) is self-limited (about 1-2 weeks) in immunocompetent hosts but causes chronic, profuse watery diarrhea with stool volumes ranging from 1 to 25 L/day in AIDS patients with CD4 counts below 100/uL.","Cystoisospora belli is distinctive among enteric protozoa in that it can produce peripheral eosinophilia in immunocompetent hosts; in AIDS it mimics cryptosporidiosis with chronic profuse watery diarrhea.","Cyclospora cayetanensis is linked to foodborne outbreaks from imported produce (basil, sweet peas, raspberries) and waterborne spread, and characteristically causes prolonged diarrhea that may persist beyond one month; absence of fecal blood and leukocytes indicates the small-bowel mucosa is not destroyed.","Conventional ova-and-parasite (O+P) stool examination does NOT reliably detect Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, or microsporidia; specific tests (modified acid-fast stain, immunoassays, direct fluorescent antibody, or NAATs) must be requested explicitly.","Microsporidia are obligate intracellular spore-forming organisms that are small and gram-positive; they are most often opportunistic in AIDS, where Enterocytozoon bieneusi and Encephalitozoon intestinalis cause chronic diarrhea and wasting and have been found in 10-40% of patients with chronic diarrhea.","Encephalitozoon species can disseminate beyond the gut to cause keratoconjunctivitis, sinusitis, bronchiolitis/respiratory disease, cholangitis, nephritis, and cystitis, so microsporidiosis is not solely an intestinal disease.","Balantidium coli is a large ciliated protozoan producing a large-bowel disease resembling amebiasis (mucosal invasion, focal necrosis, ulceration); it is zoonotic from pigs and, unlike amebiasis, only rarely disseminates hematogenously to other organs.","Blastocystis hominis has uncertain pathogenicity; finding it in stool warrants a full search for other bacterial, viral, or protozoal causes of diarrhea before attributing symptoms to it.","Dientamoeba fragilis is unique among intestinal protozoa in having only a trophozoite stage and no cyst stage; immediate fixation of stool improves detection of the labile trophozoites and NAATs outperform microscopy.","Trichomonas vaginalis is one of the most prevalent protozoal parasites in the United States (an estimated 2-3 million infections per year); it is essentially always transmitted venereally, and most infected men are asymptomatic, serving as a reservoir for reinfection."],"pathophysiology":"Most of these protozoa are acquired by fecal-oral ingestion of environmentally resistant cysts or oocysts (Trichomonas is the exception, being venereally transmitted). Giardia trophozoites excyst in the small intestine, adhere to but do not invade the proximal small-bowel epithelium, and may cause enterocyte apoptosis, epithelial barrier dysfunction, and malabsorption/secretion; the precise mechanism of diarrhea is unknown, and consequent loss of brush-border enzyme activity produces lactose intolerance and, in some chronic cases, malabsorption with villous flattening resembling tropical sprue or celiac disease. Cryptosporidium sporozoites infect intestinal epithelial cells within an intracellular vacuole, completing asexual and sexual cycles to generate immediately infectious oocysts; the means of secretory diarrhea are uncertain, infection can be patchy in the small bowel, and biliary tract involvement may cause papillary stenosis, sclerosing cholangitis, or cholecystitis, especially in AIDS. Cystoisospora and Cyclospora similarly invade small-bowel epithelial cells (Cyclospora oocysts must mature in the environment before becoming infectious). Microsporidia inject sporoplasm into host cells via an extruded polar tubule and replicate by merogony and sporogony, releasing spores in stool, urine, respiratory secretions, or other fluids. Hypogammaglobulinemia predisposes to prolonged, severe, treatment-refractory giardiasis, indicating humoral immunity is important for control.","additionalDifferentials":["Tropical sprue (chronic giardiasis with villous flattening can resemble it)","Gluten-sensitive enteropathy / celiac disease (mimicked by chronic malabsorptive giardiasis)","Cystic fibrosis with superimposed enteric infection","Bacterial enteric infection (more acute, disabling course than giardiasis)","Lactose intolerance (may follow giardiasis from brush-border enzyme loss)","Amebiasis (large-bowel disease mimicked by balantidiasis)"],"infectionMimics":["Amebiasis (Entamoeba histolytica) versus balantidiasis in the colon","Other infectious vaginitis (bacterial vaginosis, vulvovaginal candidiasis) versus trichomonal vaginitis - clinical features do not reliably distinguish them","Cryptosporidiosis, cystoisosporiasis, cyclosporiasis, and microsporidiosis can clinically overlap as causes of chronic watery diarrhea in AIDS","Other bacterial, viral, or protozoal diarrhea when Blastocystis hominis is detected"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Celiac disease / gluten-sensitive enteropathy","Tropical sprue","Inflammatory bowel disease (for invasive colonic balantidiasis)","Functional/chronic diarrhea of uncertain cause"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool ova-and-parasite examination (detects Giardia and Balantidium but is insufficient for Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, microsporidia)","Fecal modified acid-fast staining (Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora, Cyclospora oocysts)","Fecal antigen immunoassays (Giardia, Cryptosporidium)","Direct immunofluorescent antibody (Giardia, Cryptosporidium; and for Trichomonas, more sensitive at 70-90% than wet mount)","Nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs) - highly sensitive across the protozoa and useful for microsporidial speciation","Modified trichrome or fluorescent stains of stool/urine/tissue for microsporidia; electron microscopy often required in tissue","Repeated stool sampling, duodenal fluid aspiration, and small-bowel biopsy when cyst/oocyst excretion is intermittent or low-level","Wet-mount microscopy of vaginal or prostatic secretions for motile trichomonads (sensitivity only ~50-60%); NAATs on urine or vaginal/endocervical swabs are first-line"],"redFlags":["CD4+ T-cell count below 100/uL with watery diarrhea (risk of severe, chronic, profuse cryptosporidiosis)","Very high stool output (up to 25 L/day) with marked fluid and electrolyte depletion in immunocompromised cryptosporidiosis","Significant weight loss, wasting, dehydration, or growth retardation in children with chronic giardiasis or cryptosporidiosis","Right-upper-quadrant or mid-epigastric pain suggesting biliary tract involvement (cryptosporidiosis or microsporidiosis: papillary stenosis, sclerosing cholangitis, cholecystitis)","Hypogammaglobulinemia presenting with prolonged, treatment-refractory giardiasis","Fever, blood, or mucus in stool during presumed giardiasis - suggests an alternative diagnosis or coinfection","Eosinophilia with enteric symptoms (suggests cystoisosporiasis among the enteric protozoa, or sarcocystosis-associated myositis)","Disseminated microsporidiosis (fever, sinusitis, cholangitis, bronchiolitis, keratoconjunctivitis, encephalitis) in AIDS"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend heavily on immune status. Giardiasis ranges from asymptomatic carriage to fulminant diarrhea and malabsorption; most patients are cured, though some with chronic infection have delayed symptom resolution attributed to slow brush-border enzyme regeneration, and hypogammaglobulinemic or AIDS patients may have refractory disease. Cryptosporidiosis is self-limited (1-2 weeks) in immunocompetent hosts but can be chronic and severe in AIDS, where improved immune status from antiretroviral therapy can lead to resolution. Cystoisosporiasis and cyclosporiasis can be self-limited but tend to relapse and become chronic in HIV-infected patients. Microsporidiosis can be self-limited in the immunocompetent but contributes to chronic diarrhea and wasting in AIDS, and definitive therapy is not well established. Balantidiasis ranges from asymptomatic to fulminant dysentery; sarcocystosis and most Blastocystis or Dientamoeba infections are mild or self-limited.","pitfalls":["Relying on routine stool O+P examination, which misses Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, and microsporidia - specific stains or NAATs must be ordered","Stopping at a single stool exam: cyst/oocyst excretion is variable and intermittent, so repeated sampling or duodenal aspiration/biopsy may be needed","Attributing symptoms to Blastocystis hominis without excluding other pathogens, given its uncertain pathogenicity","Missing Dientamoeba fragilis because of delayed fixation of stool, which degrades its labile trophozoites (it has no cyst stage)","Assuming routine water chlorination eliminates Giardia and Cryptosporidium - their cysts/oocysts resist standard chlorination; boiling or submicron filtration is needed","Diagnosing trichomonal vaginitis by wet mount alone, given its modest (~50-60%) sensitivity","Treating trichomoniasis without concurrently treating sexual partners, leading to apparent treatment failure that is actually reinfection (true high-level metronidazole resistance also occurs)"],"management":["Pathogen-directed antimicrobials: a nitroimidazole (metronidazole or tinidazole) for giardiasis and trichomoniasis, nitazoxanide for cryptosporidiosis, co-trimoxazole for Cystoisospora and Cyclospora, and albendazole (with fumagillin for Enterocytozoon) for microsporidiosis","Rehydration and electrolyte correction with oral rehydration solution or intravenous fluids for profuse watery diarrhoea","In HIV-related cryptosporidiosis or microsporidiosis, prioritise immune restoration with antiretroviral therapy, which is central to clearance","For trichomoniasis, treat sexual partners concurrently and screen for coexisting sexually transmitted infections","Nutritional support and management of malabsorption and secondary lactose intolerance in protracted giardiasis","Public health measures with safe-water, hand-hygiene and food-handling advice, and reporting of foodborne or waterborne outbreaks","Consider combination or alternative regimens for refractory giardiasis and confirm cure or exclude persisting infection in immunocompromised hosts"],"severityClassification":"No formal staging; severity is gauged clinically by host immune status (notably CD4 count below 100/uL marking risk of severe cryptosporidiosis), stool volume and degree of fluid/electrolyte loss (up to 25 L/day in severe cryptosporidiosis), presence of malabsorption/weight loss/wasting, dehydration, growth retardation in children, and biliary or disseminated extraintestinal involvement.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1804","Harrison 22e p.1805","Harrison 22e p.1806","Harrison 22e p.1807","Harrison 22e p.1808"],"crossLinks":[]},"proximal_18q_deletion":{"name":"Proximal 18q deletion syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["18q- syndrome (proximal)","interstitial 18q deletion","proximal interstitial monosomy 18q","del(18)(q11q21)"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An interstitial deletion of the proximal long arm of chromosome 18, distinct in phenotype from the more common distal (terminal) 18q deletion","Developmental delay and intellectual disability, generally with prominent behavioural difficulties","Seizures appear to be more frequent than in the distal deletion","Growth and stature are relatively less affected, and the characteristic aural atresia and dysmyelination of the distal deletion are typically not the dominant features","Hypotonia and variable, often non-specific, dysmorphism may be present","The clinical picture depends on the precise interstitial segment lost and which dosage-sensitive genes are involved","Recurrence risk depends on whether the deletion is de novo or reflects a parental rearrangement","Chromosomal microarray defines the deletion and distinguishes it from the distal 18q deletion"],"pathophysiology":"Proximal 18q deletion syndrome arises from an interstitial loss within the proximal long arm of chromosome 18, sparing the terminal segment. As a contiguous-gene disorder, its features result from haploinsufficiency of the particular dosage-sensitive genes contained in the deleted interval, which differ from those lost in the distal deletion; this accounts for the distinct phenotype, with a greater tendency to seizures and behavioural disturbance and relatively less effect on growth and hearing. The variability in the position and size of the interstitial deletion explains the heterogeneity between individuals.","additionalDifferentials":["Distal (terminal) 18q deletion syndrome, which has a different clinical profile","Other interstitial deletion syndromes causing developmental delay and epilepsy","Idiopathic or other genetic epilepsy syndromes","Idiopathic autism spectrum disorder and behavioural disorders","Non-syndromic intellectual disability","Other recurrent copy-number syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal microarray as the first-line test to detect and size the interstitial deletion and distinguish proximal from distal 18q involvement","FISH or MLPA to confirm the deletion and assess for a parental rearrangement","Parental studies to exclude an inherited rearrangement and counsel recurrence risk","EEG and neurological assessment where seizures are present or suspected","Developmental, cognitive and behavioural evaluation","Brain MRI and other targeted imaging where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["New-onset or poorly controlled seizures","Escalating behavioural disturbance affecting safety","Developmental regression prompting reassessment","Feeding difficulty or faltering growth in infancy"],"prognosis":"Most affected individuals have intellectual disability of variable degree with behavioural challenges, and seizure control is often an important determinant of function. With appropriate developmental, educational and neurological support, many reach adulthood, though the outlook varies with the extent of the deletion and the severity of epilepsy and behavioural problems.","pitfalls":["Conflating the proximal deletion with the distal 18q deletion, whose growth, hearing and imaging features differ","Underestimating the seizure burden and failing to arrange appropriate neurological assessment","Attributing behavioural problems solely to the deletion without structured support","Omitting parental studies where an inherited rearrangement is possible"],"management":["Multidisciplinary developmental support with early intervention, individualised special education, and speech, occupational and physical therapy","Seizure control with antiepileptic drugs, guided by EEG and neurology review","Behavioural and psychological or psychiatric input for the prominent behavioural disturbance","Physiotherapy for hypotonia and to support motor development","Surveillance for and correction of associated anomalies as they are identified","Genetic counselling with parental chromosome studies to identify inherited rearrangements and quantify recurrence risk","Coordinated clinical genetics follow-up with practical family support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (University of Washington, Seattle)","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"proximal_hamstring_tendinopathy":{"name":"proximal hamstring tendinopathy","system":"musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["high hamstring tendinopathy","hamstring origin tendinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner & Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as deep, localised lower buttock pain at the ischial tuberosity that is characteristically aggravated by sitting on hard surfaces and by running, lunging and accelerating.","Onset is usually insidious and overuse-related, and it is common in distance runners, sprinters and hurdlers.","Pain is reproduced by resisted knee flexion and hip extension and by loaded lengthening of the hamstrings, and localises precisely to the ischial origin.","Provocation tests such as the Puranen-Orava test, the bent-knee stretch test and the modified bent-knee stretch test help confirm the site.","There is a compressive enthesopathic component, as the tendon is loaded against the ischial tuberosity in deep hip flexion, which explains aggravation by sitting and by hip-flexed activities.","The sciatic nerve lies immediately deep and lateral to the origin, so peritendinous scarring can generate referred posterior thigh symptoms.","Management centres on load management and progressive tendon loading, using isometric then heavy slow or eccentric resistance while correcting lumbopelvic mechanics and initially minimising compressive positions.","Recalcitrant cases may be treated with extracorporeal shockwave therapy or peritendinous injections; surgical debridement or reattachment is rarely required."],"pathophysiology":"Repetitive tensile and compressive loading of the conjoint proximal hamstring origin on the ischial tuberosity provokes a failed healing response rather than true inflammation. Histologically there is collagen disorganisation, increased ground substance with mucoid degeneration, and neovascularisation with accompanying neural ingrowth. A compressive, insertional element arises because the tendon is loaded against the bony tuberosity when the hip is flexed, so activities and postures combining flexion with load are particularly provocative. Because the sciatic nerve runs immediately deep and lateral to the enthesis, peritendinous thickening and scarring may tether or irritate the nerve and contribute to posterior thigh symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Ischiogluteal bursitis","Partial or complete proximal hamstring avulsion","Deep gluteal syndrome with sciatic nerve entrapment","Ischiofemoral impingement","S1 lumbar radiculopathy","Ischial apophysitis or apophyseal avulsion in adolescents","Referred pain from the sacroiliac joint"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the pelvis and proximal thigh centred on the ischial origin","Ultrasound to assess tendinosis, guide injection and perform dynamic evaluation","Radiographs to identify apophyseal avulsion, enthesophytes or ischial changes","Clinical and imaging assessment of the lumbar spine and sacroiliac joint","Diagnostic peritendinous local anaesthetic injection"],"redFlags":["An acute painful pop with weakness and posterior thigh bruising, suggesting a proximal hamstring avulsion that may need timely surgery","Progressive neurological deficit in the sciatic distribution","Rest or night pain with systemic symptoms, raising concern for a neoplasm around the pelvis or ischium","An adolescent with acute pain suggesting ischial apophyseal avulsion"],"prognosis":"Symptoms are frequently protracted, but the majority improve with a structured, progressive loading programme over several months. Recalcitrant cases may respond to shockwave therapy or injection, and surgical debridement or reattachment is reserved for the small refractory minority.","pitfalls":["Missing a retracted complete avulsion that requires timely surgical repair by mistaking it for tendinopathy.","Injecting corticosteroid close to the tendon and sciatic nerve, risking tendon rupture and nerve injury.","Inadequate load progression or premature return to sprinting, leading to recurrence.","Overlooking a lumbar spine or sacroiliac joint contribution to posterior pain."],"management":["Structured, progressive tendon-loading rehabilitation, advancing from isometric holds to heavy slow resistance and eccentric hamstring exercises","Relative rest with load modification, avoiding provocative deep hip flexion, prolonged sitting on hard surfaces and sudden acceleration","Correction of training errors and biomechanical factors, with simple analgesia during the loading programme","Extracorporeal shockwave therapy for recalcitrant cases","Image-guided peritendinous injection (corticosteroid or platelet-rich plasma) where loading fails, taking care to avoid the adjacent sciatic nerve","Surgical debridement or tendon reattachment reserved for the small refractory minority","Referral to sports and exercise medicine or orthopaedic surgery for resistant symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner & Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","DeLee, Drez & Miller's Orthopaedic Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"proximal_renal_tubular_acidosis":{"name":"Proximal Renal Tubular Acidosis (Type 2)","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Type 2 renal tubular acidosis","Proximal RTA","Bicarbonate-wasting RTA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nephrology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Proximal (type 2) RTA arises from impaired bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal convoluted tubule, producing a normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic) metabolic acidosis with renal bicarbonate wasting.","Because the defect is a lowered bicarbonate reabsorptive threshold rather than a failure of distal acid secretion, the distal nephron stays intact and the urine acidifies appropriately once plasma bicarbonate falls below that reduced threshold, distinguishing it from distal (type 1) RTA.","Hypokalaemia is characteristic and paradoxically worsens with alkali therapy, because delivering bicarbonate to the distal nephron drives further potassium loss.","It is most often one component of a generalised proximal tubulopathy (Fanconi syndrome), so look for normoglycaemic glycosuria, phosphaturia, aminoaciduria, uricosuria and low-molecular-weight proteinuria.","Nephrocalcinosis and calcium stones are uncommon, unlike distal RTA, because urinary citrate excretion is relatively preserved.","Rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults reflect renal phosphate wasting and disturbed vitamin D handling rather than the acidosis alone.","Cystinosis is the commonest cause of Fanconi-associated proximal RTA in children, while drugs (carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, ifosfamide, tenofovir, aminoglycosides), monoclonal gammopathy and heavy metals are important acquired causes.","Isolated hereditary proximal RTA from SLC4A4 (NBCe1 sodium-bicarbonate cotransporter) mutations is rare and associated with ocular disease (band keratopathy, glaucoma, cataract) and intellectual disability."],"pathophysiology":"The proximal tubule normally reclaims the large majority of filtered bicarbonate through luminal carbonic anhydrase activity and the coordinated action of the apical sodium-hydrogen exchanger and the basolateral sodium-bicarbonate cotransporter (NBCe1). In proximal RTA the reabsorptive capacity for bicarbonate is reduced, so bicarbonate is wasted in the urine until plasma bicarbonate falls to a new, lowered steady-state threshold; below that point the diminished filtered load can again be reabsorbed and the intact distal nephron can acidify the urine. The resulting bicarbonate loss obligates chloride retention, giving a hyperchloraemic normal anion gap acidosis, while enhanced distal sodium and bicarbonate delivery promotes renal potassium wasting and hypokalaemia. The transporter defect may be isolated (as in SLC4A4 mutations) or, far more commonly, part of a generalised proximal tubular failure (Fanconi syndrome) in which glucose, phosphate, amino acids, urate and low-molecular-weight proteins are also lost. Phosphate wasting and impaired conversion of vitamin D contribute to metabolic bone disease, whereas relatively preserved citrate excretion largely spares patients from the nephrocalcinosis typical of distal RTA.","additionalDifferentials":["Distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis","Hyperkalaemic (type 4) renal tubular acidosis","Gastrointestinal bicarbonate loss (chronic diarrhoea, ileal or ureteric diversion)","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor effect","Acidosis of chronic kidney disease","Dilutional acidosis from rapid saline administration","Recovery-phase (post-hypocapnic) acidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Venous blood gas with serum electrolytes to confirm a hyperchloraemic normal anion gap acidosis with hypokalaemia","Urine pH and urine anion or osmolal gap to assess distal acidification","Fractional excretion of bicarbonate measured after bicarbonate loading, which is characteristically raised in proximal RTA","Screen for Fanconi syndrome: urinary glucose, phosphate, urate, amino acids and low-molecular-weight proteins such as retinol-binding protein","Serum phosphate, calcium, alkaline phosphatase and vitamin D metabolites, with skeletal radiographs for rickets or osteomalacia","Leucocyte cystine assay and slit-lamp examination if cystinosis is suspected, and serum and urine protein electrophoresis in adults to exclude myeloma","Targeted genetic testing (for example SLC4A4, and syndromic causes such as CTNS, OCRL, ATP7B or FAH)"],"redFlags":["Failure to thrive, rickets or unexplained fractures in a child with a normal anion gap acidosis","Profound hypokalaemia causing muscle weakness, arrhythmia or respiratory compromise, particularly after alkali is started","Photophobia and progressive visual loss suggesting cystinosis or NBCe1-related ocular disease","Coexisting bone pain, anaemia or heavy proteinuria raising suspicion of multiple myeloma in an adult","Rapidly declining renal function"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the underlying cause; isolated and drug-induced forms often stabilise or reverse once the cause is removed and alkali with potassium is replaced, whereas Fanconi syndrome from progressive disorders such as cystinosis may advance to chronic kidney disease.","pitfalls":["Assuming that a high urine pH excludes proximal RTA, when in fact the urine acidifies normally once plasma bicarbonate falls below the reduced threshold.","Under-replacing alkali, since large amounts are needed because much of the administered bicarbonate is promptly wasted.","Precipitating or deepening hypokalaemia by giving bicarbonate without concurrent potassium supplementation.","Overlooking an underlying Fanconi syndrome and its cause when the acidosis appears isolated.","Mistaking normoglycaemic glycosuria for diabetes mellitus."],"management":["Oral alkali replacement with bicarbonate or citrate to correct the acidosis, typically in generous amounts because of ongoing urinary bicarbonate loss","Potassium supplementation, often as potassium citrate, anticipating that alkali therapy worsens hypokalaemia","Identify and remove the underlying cause, such as withdrawing offending drugs (carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, ifosfamide) or treating myeloma","For associated Fanconi syndrome, replace phosphate and give active vitamin D (calcitriol) to treat rickets or osteomalacia","Disease-specific therapy where relevant, such as cysteamine for cystinosis","Monitor electrolytes, acid-base status, growth in children and bone health over time","Referral to nephrology, with metabolic or genetics and dietetic input as indicated"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Oxford Textbook of Medicine","Oxford Handbook of Nephrology and Hypertension"],"crossLinks":[]},"prune_belly_syndrome":{"name":"Prune belly syndrome","system":"Renal / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Eagle-Barrett syndrome","triad syndrome","abdominal muscle deficiency syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by a classic triad: deficient or absent abdominal wall musculature, bilateral cryptorchidism, and dilatation of the urinary tract (large hypotonic bladder, dilated tortuous ureters, and hydronephrosis)","Predominantly affects males; the wrinkled, lax abdominal wall gives the characteristic prune-like appearance in the neonate","The urinary tract dilatation is typically non-obstructive, reflecting a dysmorphic smooth-muscle wall rather than mechanical blockage","Renal outcome is dictated by the degree of associated renal dysplasia, which is present in a substantial proportion","Associated anomalies are common, including pulmonary hypoplasia (potentially lethal, related to oligohydramnios), cardiac defects, and musculoskeletal deformities such as talipes","Antenatal ultrasound may show a distended bladder, hydroureteronephrosis, and oligohydramnios","Management is individualised and ranges from conservative surveillance to abdominoplasty, orchidopexy, and urinary tract reconstruction, with prophylactic antibiotics and monitoring of renal function"],"pathophysiology":"Prune belly syndrome is a rare congenital disorder whose precise cause is uncertain. Leading hypotheses include a primary mesodermal developmental defect affecting the abdominal wall and urinary tract smooth muscle, and transient early urinary tract obstruction producing massive bladder and ureteric distension that secondarily damages the abdominal wall musculature and prevents testicular descent. The result is a thin, poorly muscular abdominal wall, a large hypotonic bladder that often empties incompletely, dilated tortuous ureters with deficient peristalsis, and variable renal dysplasia. Bilateral intra-abdominal testes and, frequently, pulmonary hypoplasia from oligohydramnios complete the picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Posterior urethral valves with secondary bladder and upper-tract dilatation","Megacystis-microcolon-intestinal hypoperistalsis syndrome","Congenital bladder outlet obstruction of other cause","Primary bilateral vesicoureteric reflux with dysplasia","Urethral atresia or stenosis","Non-syndromic bilateral cryptorchidism with unrelated hydronephrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal and bladder ultrasound to characterise hydroureteronephrosis and bladder morphology","Micturating cystourethrogram (MCUG) to assess reflux, bladder shape, and urethral anatomy","MAG3 (or other radionuclide) renogram to assess differential function and drainage","Serum creatinine and electrolytes with serial renal function monitoring","Antenatal ultrasound findings and amniotic fluid assessment","Echocardiography and skeletal/chest imaging for associated anomalies"],"redFlags":["Neonatal respiratory distress from pulmonary hypoplasia, which is a leading cause of early mortality","Rising creatinine or oliguria signalling significant renal dysplasia or impairment","Recurrent febrile urinary tract infections in a poorly draining, dilated system","Signs of true obstruction (e.g. urethral atresia) superimposed on the baseline dilatation"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies widely and is largely determined by the severity of renal dysplasia and pulmonary hypoplasia; severely affected neonates may die from respiratory or renal failure, whereas mildly affected children can have near-normal life expectancy with careful urological and nephrological follow-up.","pitfalls":["Assuming all upper-tract dilatation is obstructive and intervening surgically when the tract is non-obstructive","Underestimating pulmonary hypoplasia as the immediate life-threatening problem in the neonate","Overlooking associated cardiac, gastrointestinal, and orthopaedic anomalies","Delaying orchidopexy for the intra-abdominal testes with implications for fertility and malignancy risk"],"management":["Neonatal stabilisation with respiratory support for pulmonary hypoplasia, the leading early threat to survival","Preserve renal function by monitoring creatinine and electrolytes and ensuring adequate bladder drainage and emptying","Antibiotic prophylaxis and prompt treatment of urinary tract infections, with management of vesicoureteric reflux","Reconstructive urological surgery where indicated, including abdominal wall repair (abdominoplasty) and urinary tract reconstruction","Orchidopexy for the bilateral intra-abdominal testes","Long-term nephrology surveillance for chronic kidney disease, with renal replacement or transplantation in severe renal dysplasia","Multidisciplinary care spanning neonatology, urology, nephrology and orthopaedics, with family and genetic counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Gearhart, Rink & Mouriquand: Pediatric Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"prurigo_nodularis":{"name":"Prurigo nodularis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nodular prurigo","Hyde prurigo nodularis","chronic nodular prurigo","picker's nodules"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by multiple intensely pruritic, firm, hyperkeratotic nodules arising from a self-perpetuating itch-scratch cycle","Nodules are typically symmetrical on the extensor limbs and trunk, sparing the mid-back where the hand cannot reach ('butterfly sign')","Excoriation, crusting, and post-inflammatory hyper- or hypopigmentation commonly accompany the nodules","It is a reaction pattern rather than a single disease, so an underlying cause of chronic pruritus should be sought","Associated conditions include atopic dermatitis, chronic kidney disease, hepatobiliary disease, thyroid dysfunction, HIV, and, less commonly, lymphoma","Neural hyperplasia and upregulation of itch-related mediators contribute to the persistence and severity of the pruritus","Breaking the itch-scratch cycle is central to management, alongside treating any identified systemic driver"],"pathophysiology":"Prurigo nodularis develops when chronic, intense pruritus drives repetitive scratching and rubbing, producing hyperkeratotic, fibrotic nodules. Persistent mechanical trauma and neuroimmune activation lead to dermal fibrosis, epidermal hyperplasia, and proliferation of cutaneous nerve fibres, with increased expression of pruritogenic cytokines and neuropeptides that sensitise itch pathways. This establishes a self-sustaining itch-scratch cycle. The underlying pruritus may be dermatological, systemic (renal, hepatic, haematological, endocrine), neuropathic, or psychogenic, so the nodules represent a common end-point rather than a distinct primary disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertrophic lichen planus","Lichen simplex chronicus","Bullous pemphigoid (nodular/prurigo variant)","Multiple keratoacanthomas or squamous cell carcinomas","Pemphigoid nodularis","Perforating dermatoses (e.g. acquired reactive perforating collagenosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy to confirm the diagnosis and exclude mimics, including bullous pemphigoid on immunofluorescence","Full blood count and renal and liver function tests to seek systemic causes of pruritus","Thyroid function tests","HIV and, where indicated, hepatitis serology","Screening for lymphoproliferative disease in refractory or atypical cases","Assessment of atopic and psychological contributors"],"redFlags":["Systemic symptoms such as weight loss, night sweats, or lymphadenopathy suggesting lymphoma","Unexplained pruritus pointing to occult renal, hepatic, or haematological disease","Rapidly growing or ulcerated nodules raising concern for malignancy","Severe intractable itch with sleep disturbance and marked impairment of quality of life"],"prognosis":"Prurigo nodularis is typically chronic and difficult to treat, with lesions persisting for months to years; outcome improves when an underlying cause is identified and treated and when the itch-scratch cycle can be interrupted.","pitfalls":["Treating the nodules symptomatically without investigating an underlying systemic cause of pruritus","Missing an underlying bullous pemphigoid presenting in a prurigo-like form","Underestimating the psychological impact and role of scratching behaviour","Overlooking malignancy in solitary, rapidly changing, or ulcerated lesions"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying driver of pruritus, whether dermatological, renal, hepatic, thyroid, HIV-related or haematological","Break the itch-scratch cycle with potent or superpotent topical corticosteroids, often under occlusion, and topical calcineurin inhibitors","Intralesional corticosteroid injection for individual resistant nodules","Antipruritic measures including emollients, topical menthol, sedating antihistamines and neuromodulators such as gabapentinoids","Phototherapy with narrowband ultraviolet B for widespread disease","Systemic or biologic therapy for refractory cases, notably dupilumab, or immunosuppressants such as methotrexate or ciclosporin","Psychological support to address scratching behaviour, with dermatology referral for resistant disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"prurigo_of_pregnancy":{"name":"prurigo of pregnancy","system":"skin","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prurigo gestationis","Besnier prurigo gestationis","early-onset prurigo of pregnancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with grouped excoriated papules and nodules, typically on the extensor surfaces of the limbs and sometimes the trunk.","Often arises earlier than polymorphic eruption, commonly in the second trimester between around 25 and 30 weeks.","Now widely regarded as part of the atopic eruption of pregnancy spectrum, occurring in women with an atopic diathesis.","Lesions frequently show central excoriation or crusting reflecting the intense scratching that drives the picture.","Diagnosis is clinical; there are no diagnostic laboratory findings and direct immunofluorescence is negative.","Total serum IgE may be raised, reflecting the underlying atopic tendency.","It is benign for mother and fetus, with no reported increase in adverse pregnancy outcomes.","Management centres on emollients, topical corticosteroids and antihistamines, with attention to a possible atopic flare."],"pathophysiology":"Prurigo of pregnancy is considered a manifestation of an atopic tendency unmasked or exacerbated by the immunological shift of pregnancy. The normal gestational skew from T-helper-1 towards T-helper-2 cytokine dominance favours the atopic phenotype, promoting pruritus and the prurigo reaction pattern. Repeated scratching in response to itch produces the characteristic excoriated papules and nodules through a chronic itch-scratch cycle rather than a primary blistering or autoimmune process. There is no autoantibody and no consistent hormonal trigger, and the condition overlaps clinically and conceptually with the eczematous and papular forms grouped under atopic eruption of pregnancy.","additionalDifferentials":["atopic eruption of pregnancy (eczematous and papular types)","polymorphic eruption of pregnancy","pemphigoid gestationis","intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy","scabies","insect bite reactions","folliculitis or pruritic folliculitis of pregnancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["serum bile acids and liver function tests to exclude obstetric cholestasis","total serum IgE (frequently elevated)","skin biopsy if diagnosis uncertain (non-specific with parakeratosis, acanthosis and dermal inflammation)","direct immunofluorescence to exclude pemphigoid gestationis in atypical cases","skin scrapings for scabies mites where itch is severe and contacts affected","full blood count for eosinophilia"],"redFlags":["widespread pruritus without primary lesions and abnormal bile acids indicating intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy","tense blisters or umbilical involvement suggesting pemphigoid gestationis","jaundice, pale stools or dark urine pointing to hepatobiliary disease","burrows, involvement of web spaces or affected household contacts suggesting scabies","signs of secondary bacterial infection of excoriated lesions"],"prognosis":"Benign and self-limiting, usually resolving in the weeks after delivery, although lesions and post-inflammatory pigmentation may persist for some weeks. There is no fetal risk, but recurrence in subsequent pregnancies can occur given the underlying atopic predisposition.","pitfalls":["Attributing generalised pruritus to prurigo and failing to measure bile acids, thereby missing intrahepatic cholestasis with its fetal risk.","Overlooking scabies, which can closely mimic prurigo and requires treatment of contacts.","Undertreating pruritus so the itch-scratch cycle perpetuates nodules and secondary infection.","Regarding it as entirely distinct from atopic eruption of pregnancy and missing a broader atopic flare needing eczema care.","Confusing persistent post-delivery lesions with treatment failure rather than expected slow resolution."],"management":["Reassurance that the condition is benign, self-limiting and poses no foetal risk, usually resolving in the weeks after delivery","Symptomatic itch relief with regular emollients and topical corticosteroids","Pregnancy-appropriate oral antihistamines for troublesome pruritus","Exclude obstetric cholestasis by checking bile acids and liver function given the prominent itch","Short course of narrowband ultraviolet B phototherapy or oral corticosteroid for severe refractory disease, in discussion with obstetrics","Counsel about possible recurrence in future pregnancies given the underlying atopic predisposition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Ambros-Rudolph CM et al. The specific dermatoses of pregnancy revisited and reclassified, Journal of the American Academy of Dermatology","British Association of Dermatologists guidance on pruritus in pregnancy"],"crossLinks":[]},"prurigo_pigmentosa":{"name":"Prurigo pigmentosa","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Nagashima disease","keto rash","prurigo pigmentosa"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with recurrent crops of intensely itchy erythematous papules and papulovesicles that resolve to leave a striking reticulate (net-like) hyperpigmentation","Characteristically affects the trunk, particularly the back, chest, neck, and sacral area, in a symmetrical pattern","First well described in and more frequently reported from Japan but recognised worldwide, typically in young adults and more often women","Strongly linked to states of ketosis, including ketogenic and low-carbohydrate diets, fasting, poorly controlled diabetes with ketoacidosis, and anorexia, hence the lay term keto rash","The active inflammatory papules are itchy, whereas the residual reticulate pigmentation is asymptomatic and can persist for months","Ketone bodies are thought to accumulate around dermal vessels and provoke a neutrophilic and then lymphocytic inflammatory reaction","Responds rapidly to tetracyclines (such as minocycline or doxycycline) or dapsone and to correction of the ketotic state with reintroduction of carbohydrate","Topical corticosteroids are relatively ineffective, which itself is a useful diagnostic clue"],"pathophysiology":"The mechanism is incompletely understood, but the strong association with ketosis suggests that circulating ketone bodies accumulate around dermal blood vessels and act as a trigger for a sterile inflammatory response. Early lesions show a superficial neutrophilic infiltrate that evolves through a lichenoid or interface phase and finally leaves dermal melanophages, which accounts for the characteristic residual reticulate pigmentation. The dramatic response to tetracyclines and to correction of ketosis, together with the poor response to topical corticosteroids, supports a distinctive neutrophil-driven, metabolically triggered process rather than a simple eczematous or allergic reaction.","additionalDifferentials":["Confluent and reticulated papillomatosis","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Allergic or irritant contact dermatitis","Urticaria or urticarial dermatitis","Ashy dermatosis / erythema dyschromicum perstans (for the pigmented phase)","Transient acantholytic dermatosis (Grover disease)","Pityriasis rosea"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed dietary and weight history covering ketogenic dieting, fasting, and anorexia, together with diabetic control","Urine or blood ketones and blood glucose or HbA1c where ketosis or diabetes is suspected","Skin biopsy of an early papule showing a superficial neutrophilic infiltrate and of older lesions showing pigment incontinence","A trial of a tetracycline as a supportive therapeutic test","Direct immunofluorescence to exclude dermatitis herpetiformis if the diagnosis is unclear"],"redFlags":["Underlying diabetic ketoacidosis presenting with the eruption and requiring urgent metabolic management","Significant undernutrition or an eating disorder driving the ketosis","Failure to respond to tetracyclines, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Benign; the itchy papules clear rapidly with treatment and correction of ketosis, though the reticulate pigmentation fades slowly and recurrences accompany renewed ketotic states.","pitfalls":["Failing to ask about ketogenic dieting, fasting, or diabetes","Treating with topical corticosteroids, which typically do not work","Mistaking the residual reticulate pigmentation for a primary pigmentary disorder","Overlooking diabetic ketoacidosis as a precipitant"],"management":["Correct the underlying ketosis by reintroducing carbohydrate, addressing fasting or anorexia, and optimising diabetic control to prevent ketoacidosis","First-line oral tetracycline such as doxycycline or minocycline for the active inflammatory papules","Dapsone as an alternative for lesions unresponsive to tetracyclines","Avoid relying on topical corticosteroids, which are characteristically ineffective in this condition","Reassure that residual reticulate hyperpigmentation is asymptomatic and fades slowly without specific treatment","Dietitian input for patients on ketogenic or low-carbohydrate diets, with monitoring for recurrence during renewed ketotic states"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudo_hurler_polydystrophy":{"name":"Pseudo-Hurler polydystrophy (mucolipidosis III)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mucolipidosis III","ML III","ML III alpha/beta (GNPTAB)","ML III gamma (GNPTG)","N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase deficiency (partial)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Scriver's OMMBID","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Represents the milder, later-onset counterpart of I-cell disease within the same mannose-6-phosphate targeting pathway","Results from partial deficiency of GlcNAc-1-phosphotransferase, so lysosomal enzyme mistargeting is incomplete","Presents in mid-childhood with progressive joint stiffness, particularly of the shoulders and hands, and slowly progressive skeletal changes","Cognition is normal or only mildly affected, in contrast to the severe course of mucolipidosis II","Like I-cell disease, plasma lysosomal enzyme activities are elevated while intracellular activities are reduced","Two genetic forms exist: ML III alpha/beta from GNPTAB variants and ML III gamma from GNPTG variants","Hip dysplasia, aortic valve involvement, and mild corneal changes can develop over time"],"pathophysiology":"Pseudo-Hurler polydystrophy is caused by partial deficiency of UDP-N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase, the enzyme that generates the mannose-6-phosphate signal directing lysosomal hydrolases to the lysosome. Variants in GNPTAB (alpha/beta subunits, ML III alpha/beta) or GNPTG (gamma subunit, ML III gamma) leave greater residual phosphotransferase function than in mucolipidosis II, so enzyme mistargeting is incomplete and disease is milder and slower. Lysosomal hydrolases are partly secreted rather than delivered intracellularly, giving the same biochemical pattern of high plasma and low cellular enzyme activity, with gradual accumulation of undegraded substrate. The predominant clinical burden is on connective tissue and the skeleton. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Mucolipidosis II (I-cell disease) at the severe end of the pathway","Attenuated mucopolysaccharidoses such as Scheie or Maroteaux-Lamy","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis given the joint stiffness","Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia and other skeletal dysplasias","Progressive pseudorheumatoid dysplasia","Other lysosomal storage disorders with slowly progressive arthropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma or serum assay of multiple lysosomal enzymes showing raised activity","Cultured fibroblast studies demonstrating reduced intracellular enzyme activity","GNPTAB and GNPTG gene sequencing to define the subtype","Skeletal survey and hip imaging for dysplasia","Echocardiography for valvular involvement","Slit-lamp examination for corneal changes"],"redFlags":["Progressive hip destruction impairing mobility","Aortic or mitral valve dysfunction","Cervical spine involvement with instability","Marked functional decline from advancing joint contractures"],"prognosis":"The course is slowly progressive, and many patients survive into adulthood; the main long-term burden comes from joint contractures, hip disease, and cardiac valve involvement, which can cause significant disability.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early joint stiffness for inflammatory arthritis and delaying metabolic diagnosis","Assuming normal intellect excludes a lysosomal disorder","Overlooking the diagnostic pattern of high plasma lysosomal enzyme activity","Failing to distinguish the milder ML III course from severe ML II"],"management":["Supportive multidisciplinary care, as there is no enzyme-replacement or curative therapy for this disorder","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to preserve joint mobility and slow the progression of contractures","Orthopaedic management of progressive hip and joint disease, including joint replacement in adulthood","Analgesia and mobility aids for joint pain and stiffness","Cardiac surveillance with periodic echocardiography for valvular involvement, with cardiology referral","Ophthalmology review for corneal changes, alongside regular skeletal monitoring","Genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive condition involving GNPTAB or GNPTG"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudoachondroplasia":{"name":"Pseudoachondroplasia","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["COMP","PSACH","pseudoachondroplastic dysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with disproportionate short-limbed short stature that is usually not apparent at birth but emerges as growth slows in the second year of life","Caused by autosomal dominant variants in COMP encoding cartilage oligomeric matrix protein, which accumulates abnormally within chondrocytes","The head and face are normal, distinguishing it clinically from achondroplasia, but there is marked ligamentous laxity and early progressive joint pain","Radiographs show generalised epiphyseal and metaphyseal irregularity, small irregular epiphyses, flared metaphyses, and platyspondyly with anterior beaking of the vertebral bodies","Windswept lower-limb deformities, genu varum or valgum, and early osteoarthritis are characteristic complications","Cervical spine instability due to odontoid hypoplasia and ligamentous laxity is an important consideration before anaesthesia and physical activity","Diagnosis rests on the radiographic pattern and COMP molecular testing, as the overlap with multiple epiphyseal dysplasia is recognised"],"pathophysiology":"Pseudoachondroplasia is caused by dominant variants in COMP, a glycoprotein of the cartilage extracellular matrix. Mutant protein is retained within the endoplasmic reticulum of chondrocytes rather than being secreted, forming characteristic inclusions and provoking cellular stress that impairs chondrocyte function and premature cell death. Disrupted cartilage matrix in the growth plates and articular surfaces causes disproportionate short stature, epiphyseal and metaphyseal abnormalities, joint laxity, and premature degenerative joint disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Achondroplasia","Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia (also COMP-related)","Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia","Metaphyseal chondrodysplasia","Morquio and other mucopolysaccharidoses","Hypochondroplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey documenting epiphyseal and metaphyseal changes and vertebral beaking","COMP molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Flexion-extension cervical spine imaging to assess odontoid hypoplasia and atlantoaxial instability","Lower-limb alignment assessment for windswept and angular deformities","Serial growth and body-proportion charting","Orthopaedic and physiotherapy review for joint pain and mobility"],"redFlags":["Signs of cervical myelopathy from atlantoaxial instability, especially before anaesthesia","Rapidly progressive lower-limb malalignment impairing walking","Severe early-onset joint pain limiting function","Neurological deficit suggesting spinal cord compression"],"prognosis":"Lifespan and intelligence are normal, but adult stature is significantly reduced and premature osteoarthritis, particularly of the hips and knees, frequently necessitates joint replacement in adulthood. Cervical instability requires vigilance to prevent neurological injury.","pitfalls":["Expecting features at birth and dismissing the diagnosis in a normal-appearing neonate","Confusing it with achondroplasia despite the normal facial appearance","Overlooking atlantoaxial instability before anaesthesia or sport","Failing to anticipate and manage early degenerative joint disease"],"management":["Supportive skeletal-dysplasia care, as there is no disease-specific drug therapy for the underlying COMP defect","Assess the cervical spine with flexion-extension imaging for odontoid hypoplasia and atlantoaxial instability, with precautions before anaesthesia and contact activity and surgical stabilisation if unstable","Orthopaedic correction of windswept and angular lower-limb deformities by guided growth or osteotomy","Physiotherapy and analgesia for joint pain and ligamentous laxity, with joint replacement for premature osteoarthritis","Serial growth and body-proportion monitoring","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant COMP-related condition","Referral to a skeletal-dysplasia multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudoangiomatous_stromal_hyperplasia":{"name":"Pseudoangiomatous stromal hyperplasia","system":"Breast","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PASH","Pseudoangiomatous hyperplasia of mammary stroma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Breast Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign myofibroblastic proliferation of breast stroma in which slit-like spaces mimic the vascular channels of a vascular lesion.","Often an incidental microscopic finding, but can form a discrete palpable or mammographic mass (tumoural PASH).","Typically affects premenopausal women or those exposed to hormones, reflecting the hormone responsiveness of the myofibroblasts.","Also recognised as a common stromal component underlying gynaecomastia in men.","Imaging shows a well-circumscribed, non-calcified oval mass that can resemble a fibroadenoma.","The pseudovascular slits are lined by CD34-positive, factor VIII-negative myofibroblasts, distinguishing it from a true angiosarcoma.","Small asymptomatic lesions confirmed on biopsy can be observed; symptomatic or enlarging masses are excised.","Recurrence after excision can occur, but PASH has no metastatic potential."],"pathophysiology":"Pseudoangiomatous stromal hyperplasia results from proliferation of myofibroblasts within the interlobular breast stroma, producing a network of anastomosing, empty, slit-like spaces set in dense collagenous tissue. These spaces superficially resemble vascular channels but contain no red cells and are lined by spindled myofibroblasts rather than endothelium, hence the term pseudoangiomatous. The myofibroblasts express hormone receptors, and the lesion's association with the reproductive years, hormone therapy and gynaecomastia points to hormonal, particularly progestogenic, stimulation as a driver of the proliferation. The process is entirely benign and lacks the cytological atypia, mitoses and true vascular differentiation of angiosarcoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Low-grade angiosarcoma","Fibroadenoma","Benign phyllodes tumour","Myofibroblastoma of the breast","Hamartoma","Fibromatosis","Gynaecomastia (in male patients)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Breast ultrasound to characterise a circumscribed solid mass","Mammography as part of triple assessment","Core needle biopsy for histological diagnosis","Immunohistochemistry with CD34, factor VIII and hormone receptors","MRI for large or rapidly enlarging lesions","Excisional biopsy where imaging and pathology are discordant","Interval imaging follow-up of conservatively managed lesions"],"redFlags":["Rapid enlargement of a stromal breast mass","Histological atypia, mitoses or true vascular channels suggesting angiosarcoma","Discordance between benign biopsy and suspicious imaging","Recurrent or multifocal rapidly growing lesions","Skin involvement or diffuse breast enlargement"],"prognosis":"PASH is benign with an excellent prognosis and no malignant or metastatic potential. Local recurrence may follow incomplete excision or reflect ongoing hormonal stimulation, but does not indicate malignant behaviour.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the pseudovascular slits for the channels of a low-grade angiosarcoma","Confusing a tumoural mass with a fibroadenoma and not confirming histologically","Over-treating small incidental microscopic PASH found on biopsy for another reason","Failing to correlate benign histology with imaging, missing a discordant lesion","Overlooking PASH as the stromal basis of male gynaecomastia"],"management":["Reassurance and conservative management, as the lesion is benign with no malignant or metastatic potential","Confirm the diagnosis by triple assessment with core needle biopsy and supportive immunohistochemistry (CD34-positive, factor VIII-negative myofibroblasts)","Clinical and imaging surveillance for asymptomatic incidental or small lesions","Surgical excision for large, symptomatic, rapidly enlarging or cosmetically troubling masses, or where imaging and pathology are discordant","Review and, where appropriate, modify exogenous hormone exposure that may drive the proliferation","Counsel that local recurrence after incomplete excision does not indicate malignant behaviour"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Breast Tumours (current edition)","Standard textbook of breast pathology on stromal lesions","National guidance on management of benign breast masses"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudochylothorax":{"name":"pseudochylothorax","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chyliform pleural effusion","cholesterol pleurisy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Light's Pleural Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pseudochylothorax is a cholesterol-rich, lipid-laden pleural effusion that looks milky or turbid but arises from chronic pleural inflammation rather than thoracic duct disruption.","It classically develops in long-standing effusions (often present for years) with markedly thickened, fibrotic or calcified pleura.","The two commonest causes are chronic tuberculous pleuritis and rheumatoid pleuritis; historical artificial (therapeutic) pneumothorax is another.","It is distinguished from true chylothorax biochemically: pseudochylothorax has high cholesterol (typically markedly elevated) and/or cholesterol crystals, with low triglycerides and no chylomicrons.","True chylothorax, by contrast, has high triglycerides and chylomicrons and reflects a thoracic duct leak.","Cholesterol crystals appear as rhomboid, notched plates on polarised light microscopy of the fluid.","Many patients are relatively asymptomatic despite turbid fluid, with symptoms determined by the underlying disease and any trapped lung.","Management targets the underlying cause, with therapeutic aspiration for symptoms and decortication reserved for trapped lung or persistent symptomatic effusion."],"pathophysiology":"Pseudochylothorax develops when a pleural effusion persists for a prolonged period within a thickened, poorly reabsorbing pleural space. Chronic inflammation and slow fluid turnover lead to accumulation of cholesterol and lecithin-globulin complexes, probably derived from degenerating red and white cells, within the trapped fluid. The fibrotic pleura impairs lipid clearance, so cholesterol concentrates and may crystallise, producing the characteristic opalescent, turbid appearance. Because the retained lipid is cholesterol rather than triglyceride-rich chyle, the thoracic duct is intact, which is the key distinction from chylothorax.","additionalDifferentials":["True chylothorax","Empyema (may also appear turbid or milky)","Parapneumonic effusion","Malignant pleural effusion","Rheumatoid or other connective tissue disease effusion","Chronic tuberculous pleural effusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pleural fluid cholesterol and triglyceride levels","Pleural fluid microscopy for cholesterol crystals and lipoprotein analysis for chylomicrons","Pleural fluid cell count, protein, LDH, glucose and pH (Light's criteria)","Pleural fluid adenosine deaminase and mycobacterial culture for tuberculosis","Rheumatoid factor and connective tissue serology","CT thorax to assess pleural thickening, calcification and trapped lung","Pleural biopsy where the underlying cause remains unclear"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting active tuberculosis (fever, weight loss) requiring treatment","A new or enlarging pleural mass raising concern for malignancy","Signs of superimposed empyema or sepsis","Progressive breathlessness from a trapped, non-expandable lung"],"prognosis":"The condition is generally benign and its outlook is dictated by the underlying disease; the effusion itself is chronic and often stable, though a trapped lung may cause persistent breathlessness that requires decortication.","pitfalls":["Confusing pseudochylothorax with true chylothorax and pursuing an unnecessary search for thoracic duct injury.","Assuming turbid fluid always represents empyema.","Overlooking underlying tuberculosis or rheumatoid disease that needs specific treatment.","Repeatedly aspirating a trapped lung without addressing the underlying pleural thickening."],"management":["Treat the underlying cause, giving anti-tuberculous chemotherapy for chronic tuberculous pleuritis or optimising disease-modifying therapy for rheumatoid pleuritis","Therapeutic pleural aspiration or drainage to relieve breathlessness in symptomatic effusions","Conservative monitoring for stable, minimally symptomatic chronic effusions","Decortication for a trapped lung causing persistent dyspnoea, with thoracic surgery referral","Recognise and account for any historical iatrogenic cause such as prior artificial pneumothorax","Respiratory follow-up with monitoring of the underlying disease and lung function"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Light's Pleural Diseases","BTS Pleural Disease Guideline","Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudoexfoliation_glaucoma":{"name":"Pseudoexfoliation glaucoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PXF glaucoma","PEX glaucoma","exfoliation glaucoma","pseudoexfoliation syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common secondary open-angle glaucoma caused by deposition of greyish-white fibrillar pseudoexfoliation material throughout the anterior segment that obstructs the trabecular meshwork","The classic lens sign is a central disc and a peripheral band of deposited material separated by a clear zone where the mobile iris wipes the surface","Deposits and pigment loss at the pupillary ruff, along with dense trabecular pigmentation on gonioscopy, are supportive signs, and dilation may reveal material on the lens periphery and zonules","It is a systemic elastic-tissue disorder linked to variants in the LOXL1 gene, with material also found in extraocular tissues","Zonular weakness is a hallmark, increasing the risk of lens subluxation, capsule rupture, and vitreous loss during cataract surgery, and phacodonesis may be visible","Intraocular pressure is often higher and more fluctuating than in primary open-angle glaucoma, so the disease can be more aggressive with poorer prognosis","Treatment lowers pressure with topical agents and laser trabeculoplasty, which often works well initially, with drainage surgery reserved for uncontrolled cases","Both eyes should be examined even when signs appear unilateral, as involvement is frequently asymmetric"],"pathophysiology":"Pseudoexfoliation is a generalised disorder of elastic microfibril metabolism, strongly associated with variants in the LOXL1 gene, in which abnormal greyish fibrillar material is produced and deposited in ocular and systemic tissues. In the eye this material accumulates on the anterior lens capsule, pupil margin, zonules, ciliary body, iris, and corneal endothelium. Material and liberated iris pigment progressively obstruct the trabecular meshwork and damage trabecular cells, reducing aqueous outflow and raising intraocular pressure, often with large fluctuations. Deposition also weakens the zonular apparatus, predisposing to lens instability. The optic nerve sustains glaucomatous damage that tends to progress more rapidly than in primary open-angle glaucoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary open-angle glaucoma","Pigmentary glaucoma","True exfoliation (capsular delamination) from heat or infrared exposure","Uveitic glaucoma with anterior segment deposits","Amyloid deposition in the anterior segment","Phacolytic or lens-related glaucoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated slit-lamp examination for the target-like lens deposits, pupillary ruff changes, and phacodonesis","Gonioscopy to assess trabecular pigmentation and exclude angle closure","Applanation tonometry with attention to pressure fluctuation","OCT of the retinal nerve fibre layer to quantify glaucomatous damage","Automated perimetry to monitor field loss","Assessment of zonular integrity before planning cataract surgery"],"redFlags":["Very high or widely fluctuating intraocular pressure driving rapid optic-nerve damage","Phacodonesis or lens subluxation warning of high surgical risk from zonular weakness","Advanced field loss at presentation because the disease was under-recognised","Sudden pressure decompensation or angle closure from lens instability"],"prognosis":"Pseudoexfoliation glaucoma generally follows a more aggressive course than primary open-angle glaucoma, with higher and more variable pressures and faster progression, so close monitoring is needed; cataract surgery carries elevated risk from zonular weakness but may itself modestly lower pressure.","pitfalls":["Failing to dilate the pupil and missing the peripheral lens deposits that confirm the diagnosis","Underestimating the aggressive pressure behaviour and monitoring too infrequently","Not anticipating zonular weakness and capsule instability during cataract surgery","Mistaking dense trabecular pigment for pigmentary glaucoma","Assuming the fellow eye is unaffected without examining it"],"management":["Lower intraocular pressure with topical therapy, using a prostaglandin analogue first-line and adding beta-blockers, alpha-agonists or carbonic anhydrase inhibitors as required","Laser trabeculoplasty, which is often effective given the heavily pigmented trabecular meshwork","Incisional glaucoma surgery such as trabeculectomy or a drainage device for uncontrolled or progressive disease","Anticipate zonular weakness at cataract surgery, using a capsular tension ring and careful technique, and refer to an experienced surgeon","Frequent pressure monitoring given large diurnal fluctuations, with optical coherence tomography of the nerve fibre layer and automated perimetry to track progression","Treat to a lower target pressure, recognising the more aggressive course than primary open-angle glaucoma","Ophthalmology follow-up, mindful of the systemic elastic-tissue nature of the disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudogout":{"name":"Pseudogout (acute CPP crystal arthritis)","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute CPP crystal arthritis","calcium pyrophosphate crystal arthritis","pseudogout","acute CPPD arthritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acute crystal-induced arthritis caused by calcium pyrophosphate (CPP) crystals, presenting as a hot, swollen, painful joint that clinically mimics gout or septic arthritis","The knee is the most commonly affected joint, followed by the wrist; the first metatarsophalangeal joint, so classic for gout, is less typically involved","Synovial fluid polarised microscopy shows rhomboid or rod-shaped, weakly positively birefringent CPP crystals, in contrast to the needle-shaped, strongly negatively birefringent crystals of urate","Radiographs frequently show chondrocalcinosis, the linear calcification of fibrocartilage (such as the knee menisci and the triangular fibrocartilage of the wrist) and hyaline cartilage","Attacks may be provoked by intercurrent illness, surgery, or trauma, and increasing age is a strong risk factor","Younger patients or florid, polyarticular disease should prompt a search for metabolic associations, particularly haemochromatosis, hyperparathyroidism, hypomagnesaemia, and hypophosphatasia","Acute attacks are treated with anti-inflammatory measures (a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug, colchicine, a corticosteroid, or joint aspiration and injection), as no therapy dissolves CPP crystals"],"pathophysiology":"Calcium pyrophosphate crystals form within cartilage, producing chondrocalcinosis, as a result of altered inorganic pyrophosphate metabolism and calcium handling in ageing or metabolically abnormal cartilage. Shedding of these crystals into the joint space triggers their phagocytosis and an inflammasome-driven inflammatory response similar to that of gout, producing the acute attack. Underlying metabolic disorders such as haemochromatosis, hyperparathyroidism, and hypomagnesaemia predispose to crystal formation, which is why they must be considered, particularly in younger patients.","additionalDifferentials":["Gout (urate crystal arthritis)","Septic arthritis","Acute flare of osteoarthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Reactive arthritis","Haemarthrosis or trauma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Joint aspiration with polarised microscopy for positively birefringent rhomboid CPP crystals and to exclude sepsis","Synovial fluid Gram stain, culture, and cell count","Radiographs to demonstrate chondrocalcinosis","Metabolic screen in younger or atypical cases: ferritin and transferrin saturation, calcium and parathyroid hormone, magnesium, and alkaline phosphatase","Ultrasound to detect crystal deposition within cartilage","Serum urate to help distinguish the condition from gout"],"redFlags":["A hot swollen joint that could be septic arthritis until proven otherwise","Chondrocalcinosis in a young patient signalling an underlying metabolic disease","Systemic upset with fever closely mimicking infection"],"prognosis":"Individual acute attacks are self-limiting and respond to anti-inflammatory treatment, but recurrent attacks and chronic CPP-related joint damage are common; because no treatment removes the crystals, management is directed at symptoms and at any underlying metabolic cause.","pitfalls":["Assuming a hot joint is gout without confirming the crystal type on microscopy","Missing an underlying metabolic disorder in a young patient with chondrocalcinosis","Failing to exclude septic arthritis","Expecting urate-lowering therapy to help, when it has no effect on CPP crystals"],"management":["Manage the acute attack with joint aspiration (which also excludes sepsis) and anti-inflammatory treatment using NSAIDs, colchicine or corticosteroids","Intra-articular corticosteroid injection for a single accessible joint once infection has been excluded","Rest, ice and analgesia for the affected joint during an attack","Prophylaxis with colchicine or NSAIDs for frequently recurrent attacks, recognising that no therapy dissolves the crystals","Identify and treat underlying metabolic associations, particularly haemochromatosis, hyperparathyroidism, hypomagnesaemia and hypophosphatasia, especially in younger patients","Manage chronic calcium pyrophosphate arthropathy with physiotherapy and standard osteoarthritis measures","Rheumatology referral for refractory or polyarticular disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Oxford Textbook of Rheumatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudohypoaldosteronism_type_1":{"name":"Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1","system":"Renal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PHA1","NR3C2","mineralocorticoid receptor deficiency","ENaC loss-of-function","SCNN1A","SCNN1B","SCNN1G","aldosterone resistance"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oxford Handbook of Nephrology and Hypertension / Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1 is a state of target-organ resistance to aldosterone, so despite very high aldosterone the kidney cannot retain sodium normally","The autosomal dominant (renal) form is due to loss-of-function variants in NR3C2, the mineralocorticoid receptor, and tends to be milder and to improve with age","The autosomal recessive (systemic) form is due to loss-of-function variants in the epithelial sodium channel subunits (SCNN1A, SCNN1B, SCNN1G) and is more severe and persistent","The biochemical signature is salt wasting with hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia and metabolic acidosis, alongside markedly elevated renin and aldosterone","In the systemic form, sodium loss also affects sweat, salivary glands and colon, and recurrent respiratory symptoms can mimic cystic fibrosis","It is effectively the mirror image of Liddle syndrome, with an underactive rather than overactive sodium channel pathway","Management rests on generous sodium supplementation and control of hyperkalaemia; the recessive systemic form typically needs lifelong high-dose salt replacement"],"pathophysiology":"Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1 is caused by failure of aldosterone signalling in the distal nephron. In the dominant renal form, loss-of-function variants in the mineralocorticoid receptor gene NR3C2 blunt the response to aldosterone; in the recessive systemic form, loss-of-function variants in the epithelial sodium channel subunits prevent sodium reabsorption in the collecting duct and in other epithelia. Because sodium cannot be reabsorbed and the electrochemical gradient for potassium and hydrogen secretion is lost, the kidney wastes salt while retaining potassium and hydrogen ion, producing hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia and metabolic acidosis. The salt loss and hypovolaemia strongly stimulate renin and aldosterone, but the target tissues cannot respond, so the very high hormone levels coexist with continued salt wasting.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary adrenal insufficiency (aldosterone deficiency, e.g. salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia)","Aldosterone synthase deficiency","Transient pseudohypoaldosteronism secondary to urinary tract infection or obstructive uropathy in infants","Cystic fibrosis (salt loss with respiratory disease, mimicking the systemic form)","Renal tubular acidosis type 4","Congenital adrenal hypoplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum sodium, potassium and bicarbonate to document salt-wasting hyperkalaemic acidosis","Plasma renin and aldosterone (both markedly elevated)","Urinary electrolytes to confirm renal sodium loss","Sweat chloride testing to assess systemic involvement and exclude cystic fibrosis","Renal tract imaging to exclude secondary/transient causes such as obstruction or infection","Molecular genetic testing of NR3C2 and the ENaC subunit genes"],"redFlags":["Life-threatening neonatal salt-wasting crisis with severe hyperkalaemia","Cardiac arrhythmia from hyperkalaemia","Severe dehydration and circulatory collapse","Failure to thrive despite feeding","Recurrent respiratory infections in the systemic form"],"prognosis":"The dominant renal form often attenuates with age and may need little treatment in adulthood, whereas the recessive systemic form is severe, persists lifelong, and requires continuous salt supplementation and careful management of hyperkalaemia.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as adrenal insufficiency and giving mineralocorticoid replacement, which is ineffective because the defect is resistance, not deficiency","Failing to distinguish the transient secondary form (infection/obstruction) from inherited disease","Overlooking systemic salt loss and confusing the recessive form with cystic fibrosis","Underestimating the very large sodium requirements of the systemic form","Not appreciating the mode of inheritance when counselling families"],"management":["Correct salt wasting with generous sodium chloride supplementation, the mainstay of treatment particularly in the recessive systemic form","Manage hyperkalaemia with dietary potassium restriction and cation-exchange resins, and treat acute hyperkalaemia promptly","Correct the metabolic acidosis with alkali where required","Recognise that mineralocorticoids are ineffective because the defect is receptor or channel resistance, not hormone deficiency","In the systemic form, manage cutaneous, respiratory and other epithelial salt loss and distinguish the picture from cystic fibrosis","Regular monitoring of electrolytes, hydration and growth, with paediatric nephrology input","Genetic counselling and family testing (NR3C2 in the dominant renal form; ENaC subunit genes in the recessive systemic form)"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (Feehally, Floege, Tonelli, Johnson)","Oxford Handbook of Nephrology and Hypertension"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudohypoaldosteronism_type_2":{"name":"Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 2","system":"Renal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PHA2","Gordon syndrome","familial hyperkalaemic hypertension","WNK1","WNK4","KLHL3","CUL3"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oxford Handbook of Nephrology and Hypertension / Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 2, also called Gordon syndrome or familial hyperkalaemic hypertension, is caused by variants in WNK1, WNK4, KLHL3 or CUL3 that increase activity of the thiazide-sensitive sodium-chloride cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule","The characteristic combination is hypertension with hyperkalaemia and a hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis, essentially the mirror image of Gitelman syndrome","Renin is suppressed while aldosterone is variable, and importantly hyperkalaemia occurs despite a normal glomerular filtration rate","Excess distal sodium-chloride reabsorption expands volume and reduces the sodium delivery needed for distal potassium and hydrogen secretion, causing potassium and acid retention","It responds strikingly to a thiazide diuretic, which inhibits the overactive cotransporter and corrects both the blood pressure and the electrolyte abnormalities","The KLHL3 and CUL3 variants disrupt the ubiquitin-mediated degradation of WNK kinases, increasing their signalling to the cotransporter","Consider it in patients with hyperkalaemia and hypertension that seem out of proportion to renal function"],"pathophysiology":"Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 2 results from dysregulation of the WNK kinase signalling network that controls the thiazide-sensitive sodium-chloride cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule. Variants in WNK1 or WNK4, or in the KLHL3 and CUL3 components of the ubiquitin ligase complex that normally degrades WNK kinases, lead to excessive kinase activity and increased cotransporter abundance and function. Enhanced distal sodium-chloride reabsorption expands extracellular volume and raises blood pressure, and by consuming luminal sodium it reduces the electrochemical driving force for potassium and hydrogen secretion in the downstream nephron. The net effect is salt-dependent, low-renin hypertension with hyperkalaemia and a hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis, which is reversed by inhibiting the cotransporter with a thiazide.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic kidney disease with hyperkalaemia and hypertension","Renal tubular acidosis type 4 (hypoaldosteronism)","Addison disease / primary adrenal insufficiency","Drug-induced hyperkalaemia (RAAS inhibitors, potassium-sparing diuretics)","Primary hypertension with incidental hyperkalaemia","Diabetic hyporeninaemic hypoaldosteronism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum potassium, chloride and bicarbonate to define hyperkalaemic hyperchloraemic acidosis","Plasma renin (suppressed) and aldosterone","Renal function to demonstrate hyperkalaemia despite preserved glomerular filtration","Review of medications that raise potassium","Assessment of blood-pressure and biochemical response to thiazide therapy","Molecular genetic testing of WNK1, WNK4, KLHL3 and CUL3"],"redFlags":["Severe hyperkalaemia with electrocardiographic changes","Resistant hypertension with end-organ damage","Marked acidosis","Family history of early hypertension with hyperkalaemia"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally excellent because low-dose thiazide therapy typically normalises blood pressure and electrolytes; untreated disease carries the usual cardiovascular risks of hypertension and the hazards of hyperkalaemia.","pitfalls":["Attributing hyperkalaemia to renal failure when the glomerular filtration rate is actually preserved","Prescribing renin-angiotensin blockade for the hypertension, which can worsen the hyperkalaemia","Missing the diagnosis by not recognising the mirror-image relationship to Gitelman syndrome","Overlooking the excellent and specific response to thiazide treatment"],"management":["Thiazide diuretic as specific therapy, correcting both the hypertension and the hyperkalaemic hyperchloraemic acidosis by inhibiting the overactive sodium-chloride cotransporter","Dietary sodium and chloride restriction to complement the thiazide effect","Reduce dietary potassium and withdraw drugs that raise serum potassium","Monitor blood pressure, electrolytes and acid-base status in response to treatment","Manage the broader cardiovascular risk associated with hypertension","Genetic counselling and family screening for variants in WNK1, WNK4, KLHL3 and CUL3"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (Feehally, Floege, Tonelli, Johnson)","Oxford Handbook of Nephrology and Hypertension"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudohypoparathyroidism":{"name":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism (GNAS)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PHP","Albright hereditary osteodystrophy","GNAS-related pseudohypoparathyroidism","PTH resistance"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disorder of end-organ resistance to parathyroid hormone rather than a deficiency of the hormone itself","Biochemically characterised by hypocalcaemia and hyperphosphataemia with an elevated PTH, the opposite of hypoparathyroidism","Caused by defects in GNAS, which encodes the stimulatory G-protein alpha subunit coupling receptors to adenylate cyclase","Subtype 1A is associated with the Albright hereditary osteodystrophy phenotype, including short stature, rounded face, and shortened metacarpals","Resistance may extend to other Gs-coupled hormones, producing features such as hypothyroidism from TSH resistance","Parental origin of the mutation influences the phenotype because GNAS is subject to tissue-specific imprinting","Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism shares the physical phenotype but has normal calcium biochemistry"],"pathophysiology":"Parathyroid hormone acts through a receptor that signals via the stimulatory G-protein to generate cyclic AMP in target tissues such as kidney and bone. In pseudohypoparathyroidism, GNAS mutations impair this Gs-alpha signalling, so target organs fail to respond to PTH despite adequate or elevated hormone levels. The renal resistance reduces calcium reabsorption and phosphate excretion, producing hypocalcaemia and hyperphosphataemia, while the low calcium drives compensatory secondary rises in PTH. Because GNAS is imprinted, the parent from whom the mutation is inherited determines whether multi-hormone resistance and the osteodystrophy phenotype are expressed.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hypoparathyroidism","Vitamin D deficiency or resistance","Chronic kidney disease with secondary hyperparathyroidism","Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism with normal calcium","Other causes of hypocalcaemia such as hypomagnesaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium, phosphate and intact parathyroid hormone demonstrating PTH resistance","Serum magnesium and vitamin D metabolites to exclude other causes of hypocalcaemia","Thyroid function tests to detect associated TSH resistance","Molecular genetic and methylation analysis of the GNAS locus","Radiographs of the hands to identify shortened metacarpals and other skeletal features"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic hypocalcaemia with tetany, laryngospasm or seizures requiring urgent correction","Prolonged QT interval on the electrocardiogram from severe hypocalcaemia","Features of resistance to multiple hormones indicating broad Gs-alpha involvement","Ectopic ossification or other complications requiring specialist assessment"],"prognosis":"With calcium and active vitamin D therapy guided by PTH and calcium targets, biochemical control and symptom prevention are generally achievable, although the skeletal phenotype and any additional hormone resistance persist and require ongoing management.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the high-PTH, low-calcium pattern for secondary hyperparathyroidism of renal disease","Confusing pseudohypoparathyroidism with pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism, which has normal calcium","Overlooking coexisting hormone resistance such as subclinical hypothyroidism","Aiming for high-normal calcium and risking hypercalciuria and nephrocalcinosis"],"management":["Restore calcium with oral calcium supplementation and an active vitamin D analogue such as calcitriol or alfacalcidol, titrated to calcium and PTH targets","Moderate dietary phosphate to help control hyperphosphataemia while avoiding ectopic calcification","Screen for and treat additional hormone resistance, notably hypothyroidism from TSH resistance, with levothyroxine","Monitor serum calcium, phosphate, PTH and urinary calcium, and the renal tract for calcification","Manage the Albright hereditary osteodystrophy features such as short stature and obesity","Genetic counselling with attention to imprinting and parent-of-origin effects, and family evaluation","Endocrinology and clinical genetics follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudohypoparathyroidism_type_1b":{"name":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1B","system":"Endocrine genetics (imprinting disorder)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PHP1B","GNAS","STX16","PHP type Ib"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrinology and clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["End-organ resistance to parathyroid hormone, predominantly in the renal proximal tubule, caused by loss of methylation at the maternal GNAS locus that reduces expression of the stimulatory G-protein alpha subunit (Gsalpha).","Biochemistry mimics hypoparathyroidism — hypocalcaemia with hyperphosphataemia — but the parathyroid hormone level is elevated, reflecting resistance rather than deficiency.","Unlike type 1A, affected individuals typically lack the Albright hereditary osteodystrophy phenotype, having normal stature without brachydactyly or ectopic ossification, and normal Gsalpha activity in blood cells.","Many have mild resistance to thyroid-stimulating hormone (a subclinical hypothyroid picture) because the thyroid also shows partial paternal imprinting of GNAS.","The autosomal dominant familial form is caused by maternally inherited STX16 microdeletions that produce isolated loss of methylation at the GNAS A/B differentially methylated region.","Sporadic cases usually show broader GNAS methylation defects, sometimes arising from paternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 20q.","Because the locus is imprinted, the biochemical disease manifests only when the defect is carried on or arises on the maternal allele.","Presentation is typically with hypocalcaemic symptoms such as paraesthesiae, carpopedal spasm, tetany or seizures."],"pathophysiology":"GNAS on chromosome 20q13 is a complex imprinted locus encoding the stimulatory G-protein alpha subunit (Gsalpha). Gsalpha is expressed from both alleles in most tissues, but predominantly from the maternal allele in a few — notably the renal proximal tubule, thyroid, gonad and pituitary — because of tissue-specific imprinting. In pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1B the normal maternal methylation pattern at the GNAS differentially methylated regions (particularly the A/B region) is lost, silencing maternal Gsalpha expression in these tissues; the proximal tubule then cannot mount an adequate cAMP response to parathyroid hormone, producing PTH resistance with hypocalcaemia, hyperphosphataemia and a compensatory rise in PTH. The familial autosomal dominant form arises from maternally transmitted deletions in the neighbouring STX16 gene that remove a cis-acting control element and cause isolated A/B loss of methylation, whereas sporadic disease shows broader GNAS methylation abnormalities or paternal uniparental disomy of 20q. Because Gsalpha activity in blood cells is generally preserved, erythrocyte Gsalpha is normal, contrasting with type 1A.","additionalDifferentials":["Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1A (GNAS coding mutation with Albright hereditary osteodystrophy and multi-hormone resistance)","Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1C and type 2","Primary hypoparathyroidism (with a low parathyroid hormone)","Vitamin D deficiency or hereditary vitamin D resistance","Hypomagnesaemia causing functional hypoparathyroidism","Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism (Albright features with normal biochemistry, paternal transmission)","Chronic kidney disease with secondary hyperparathyroidism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Corrected or ionised calcium, phosphate and magnesium","Intact parathyroid hormone (elevated in the context of hypocalcaemia)","25-hydroxyvitamin D and renal function","Thyroid function tests to screen for TSH resistance","GNAS methylation analysis (for example methylation-specific MLPA) of the differentially methylated regions","STX16 deletion testing and, where indicated, testing for uniparental disomy of chromosome 20q","Electrocardiogram for hypocalcaemia and brain imaging for basal ganglia calcification"],"redFlags":["Hypocalcaemic tetany, laryngospasm or seizures","Prolonged QT interval or arrhythmia related to hypocalcaemia","Symptomatic hypothyroidism from unrecognised TSH resistance","New neurological symptoms with basal ganglia calcification"],"prognosis":"The outlook is good with lifelong oral calcium and an active vitamin D analogue together with monitoring of calcium and renal status; because Albright hereditary osteodystrophy is absent, stature and skeletal development are typically normal, although TSH resistance may require thyroid hormone replacement.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the condition as primary hypoparathyroidism, when the parathyroid hormone is high rather than low.","Assuming that the absence of Albright hereditary osteodystrophy excludes pseudohypoparathyroidism, since these features are characteristically absent in type 1B.","Failing to screen for TSH resistance and thereby missing treatable hypothyroidism.","Relying on routine karyotype or coding-sequence analysis, which are normal — methylation-specific testing is required for diagnosis.","Forgetting the imprinting rule, so that only maternal transmission of the defect produces the biochemical disease."],"management":["Lifelong oral calcium and an active vitamin D analogue such as calcitriol or alfacalcidol to correct hypocalcaemia, titrated to biochemical targets","Monitor calcium, phosphate, PTH, renal function and urinary calcium to avoid hypercalciuria and nephrocalcinosis","Screen for and treat mild TSH resistance with thyroid hormone replacement where the patient is hypothyroid","Address hyperphosphataemia with dietary measures while avoiding ectopic calcification","Recognise the absence of Albright hereditary osteodystrophy, so no skeletal-specific therapy is required","Genetic and methylation counselling (maternally inherited STX16 microdeletion in the familial form; broader GNAS methylation defects or paternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 20q in sporadic cases), with family testing","Endocrinology and clinical genetics follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard endocrinology textbook","Clinical genetics reference on imprinting disorders and GNAS","Consensus guideline on pseudohypoparathyroidism"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudomyxoma_peritonei":{"name":"Pseudomyxoma peritonei","system":"Gynaecology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["jelly belly","gelatinous ascites","mucinous ascites of the peritoneum"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A clinical syndrome of progressive accumulation of gelatinous mucinous material within the peritoneal cavity, colloquially termed jelly belly","Most cases originate from a low-grade appendiceal mucinous neoplasm; ovarian involvement is usually secondary, so a coexisting ovarian mucinous tumour is often a metastasis rather than the source","Presents insidiously with increasing abdominal distension, a rising waistband, vague discomfort, and sometimes a new hernia or reduced appetite","Mucin preferentially redistributes to dependent and low-flow areas of the peritoneum while relatively sparing the small bowel surface, the so-called redistribution phenomenon","Imaging shows loculated, non-mobile mucinous ascites with characteristic scalloping of the liver and splenic margins","Graded from low-grade (disseminated peritoneal adenomucinosis) to high-grade mucinous carcinomatosis, which strongly influences outcome","Optimal treatment is cytoreductive surgery combined with heated intraperitoneal chemotherapy at specialist centres"],"pathophysiology":"Pseudomyxoma peritonei develops when mucin-producing neoplastic epithelial cells disseminate across the peritoneum and secrete copious mucin that gradually fills the abdominal cavity. In the great majority the primary lesion is a low-grade appendiceal mucinous neoplasm that perforates and seeds the peritoneum; the appendiceal wall ruptures and epithelial cells and mucin escape, then follow peritoneal fluid currents to accumulate in dependent regions while largely sparing the peristaltic small bowel. Because the ovaries lie in a dependent position and readily take up peritoneal deposits, apparent ovarian mucinous tumours in this setting are frequently secondary. The tumour cells are typically indolent but the relentless mucin production causes mass effect, bowel obstruction, and nutritional decline, so the disease is defined more by its progressive local behaviour than by distant metastasis.","additionalDifferentials":["Malignant ascites from ovarian, gastric, colorectal, or pancreatic carcinoma","Mucinous ovarian tumour with peritoneal spread","Peritoneal carcinomatosis of other origin","Cirrhotic or transudative ascites","Peritoneal tuberculosis","Mucinous neoplasm of the colon or pancreas"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) showing loculated mucinous ascites with scalloping of visceral surfaces","Assessment of the appendix as the likely primary site","Tumour markers including CEA, CA19-9, and CA125 for baseline and monitoring","Colonoscopy and gastrointestinal evaluation to identify or exclude a bowel primary","Histological and cytological examination of mucin and peritoneal deposits with grading","Immunohistochemistry to confirm gastrointestinal (often appendiceal) origin"],"redFlags":["Progressive abdominal distension with gelatinous ascites and a suspected appendiceal lesion","Signs of bowel obstruction from accumulating mucin","High-grade histology indicating mucinous carcinomatosis","Nutritional decline or a new hernia signalling advanced intra-abdominal disease"],"prognosis":"Highly dependent on grade and completeness of surgery; low-grade disease treated with complete cytoreduction and intraperitoneal chemotherapy can achieve prolonged survival, whereas high-grade mucinous carcinomatosis carries a markedly worse outlook, and the disease tends to recur and progress locally over time.","pitfalls":["Attributing the disease to a primary ovarian mucinous tumour and overlooking the appendiceal source","Simply draining mucinous ascites without addressing the underlying neoplasm","Underestimating low-grade disease as benign despite its relentless progression","Managing complex cases outside specialist cytoreduction and intraperitoneal chemotherapy centres"],"management":["Cytoreductive surgery aiming for complete tumour removal, including appendicectomy or right hemicolectomy and peritonectomy with clearance of mucinous deposits, as the definitive treatment","Hyperthermic intraperitoneal chemotherapy, typically with mitomycin C, delivered at the time of complete cytoreduction","Refer to a specialist peritoneal malignancy centre for assessment and treatment planning","Systemic chemotherapy considered for high-grade mucinous carcinomatosis or unresectable disease","Supportive care for advanced disease, addressing ascites, bowel obstruction, nutrition and analgesia","Histological grading (low-grade adenomucinosis versus high-grade carcinomatosis) to guide prognosis and treatment intensity","Long-term surveillance with cross-sectional imaging and tumour markers (CEA, CA19-9, CA125) for recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","WHO Classification of Digestive System Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism":{"name":"Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PPHP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrinology and genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A condition with the physical features of Albright hereditary osteodystrophy but normal calcium and normal parathyroid hormone responsiveness","Features of Albright hereditary osteodystrophy include short stature, a round face, brachydactyly (notably short fourth metacarpals) and subcutaneous ossifications","Unlike pseudohypoparathyroidism, there is no hormone resistance and biochemistry is normal","It is caused by GNAS mutations inherited from the father, whereas maternal inheritance of the same mutation causes pseudohypoparathyroidism (an example of imprinting)","It sits within the same family as pseudohypoparathyroidism and is distinguished biochemically","Management is mainly of the skeletal and cosmetic features, with genetic counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism arises from a heterozygous inactivating GNAS mutation affecting the alpha subunit of the stimulatory G protein. Because GNAS is imprinted, the phenotype depends on the parent of origin: paternal transmission produces the Albright hereditary osteodystrophy physical phenotype without hormone resistance (pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism), whereas maternal transmission additionally causes resistance to parathyroid hormone and other hormones (pseudohypoparathyroidism) because the maternal allele is the active one in key tissues. Hence calcium and parathyroid hormone signalling are normal here despite the shared skeletal features.","additionalDifferentials":["Pseudohypoparathyroidism","Primary hypoparathyroidism","Other causes of brachydactyly or short stature","Acrodysostosis","Turner syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium, phosphate and parathyroid hormone (normal)","Assessment of Albright hereditary osteodystrophy features","GNAS genetic testing and parent-of-origin assessment","Radiographs for brachydactyly and ossifications","Family evaluation and genetic counselling"],"redFlags":["Biochemical hormone resistance, which would indicate pseudohypoparathyroidism instead","Significant functional impact of ossifications","Features suggesting an alternative syndrome","Associated endocrine abnormalities suggesting resistance"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign biochemically, and prognosis relates to the skeletal and cosmetic features rather than to calcium disturbance.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with pseudohypoparathyroidism despite normal biochemistry","Overlooking the imprinting/parent-of-origin basis in counselling","Attributing brachydactyly to another syndrome","Missing the diagnosis in a relative of a pseudohypoparathyroidism patient"],"management":["Reassure and withhold calcium and vitamin D therapy, as calcium and parathyroid hormone signalling are normal here unlike in pseudohypoparathyroidism","Symptomatic orthopaedic and physiotherapy management of Albright hereditary osteodystrophy features, with excision of troublesome subcutaneous ossifications","Hand therapy and selective orthopaedic input for brachydactyly, short metacarpals and functional hand problems","Confirm the diagnosis with GNAS testing and parent-of-origin assessment to distinguish it from pseudohypoparathyroidism","Periodic clinical and biochemical review to ensure hormone resistance does not emerge","Genetic counselling on autosomal dominant imprinted inheritance, explaining that paternal transmission produces this phenotype whereas maternal transmission causes pseudohypoparathyroidism, with family screening","Multidisciplinary input from clinical genetics, endocrinology and orthopaedics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Endocrinology reference texts (standard reference)","Clinical genetics references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"pseudoxanthoma_elasticum":{"name":"Pseudoxanthoma elasticum","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PXE","Groenblad-Strandberg syndrome","ABCC6-related disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder caused by variants in ABCC6, leading to progressive mineralisation and fragmentation of elastic fibres","Skin changes are the earliest sign: yellowish papules (a 'plucked chicken skin' appearance) coalescing into lax plaques in flexural areas such as the neck and axillae","Angioid streaks of the retina are a hallmark ocular finding and predispose to choroidal neovascularisation and central visual loss","Cardiovascular involvement includes premature peripheral arterial disease, intermittent claudication, and increased risk of ischaemic events","Gastrointestinal haemorrhage can occur from mineralisation of arteries in the gastric mucosa","Diagnosis rests on characteristic skin and eye findings, skin biopsy showing calcified fragmented elastic fibres, and ABCC6 molecular testing","Management is supportive and preventive: retinal surveillance with prompt treatment of neovascularisation, cardiovascular risk reduction, and avoidance of antiplatelet or anticoagulant excess where bleeding risk is high","Genetic counselling should reflect the autosomal recessive inheritance and carrier implications for the family"],"pathophysiology":"Pseudoxanthoma elasticum is caused by loss of function of ABCC6, a transporter expressed mainly in the liver and kidney. Reduced ABCC6 activity lowers circulating levels of inorganic pyrophosphate, a key inhibitor of ectopic mineralisation. The resulting imbalance allows calcium and phosphate to deposit on elastic fibres in the skin, the Bruch membrane of the eye, and the arterial wall. Progressive calcification and fragmentation of these elastic fibres produce the characteristic cutaneous papules, retinal angioid streaks with neovascular complications, and premature arterial disease with a tendency to haemorrhage.","additionalDifferentials":["Cutis laxa","Elastosis perforans serpiginosa","Solar (actinic) elastosis","Beta-thalassaemia-associated PXE-like syndrome","Other causes of angioid streaks such as Paget disease of bone","Fibroelastolytic papulosis of the neck"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with elastic-tissue staining to demonstrate calcified fragmented elastic fibres","Dilated fundus examination and retinal imaging for angioid streaks and neovascularisation","Molecular genetic testing of ABCC6","Ankle-brachial pressure index and vascular imaging for peripheral arterial disease","Cardiovascular risk assessment including blood pressure and lipids","Investigation for gastrointestinal bleeding when clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Sudden central visual distortion or loss suggesting choroidal neovascularisation or retinal haemorrhage","Gastrointestinal bleeding from mineralised mucosal arteries","Symptoms of myocardial or cerebral ischaemia","Rapidly worsening claudication or critical limb ischaemia","Ocular trauma in a patient with fragile Bruch membrane"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is usually near normal, but progressive visual loss from retinal complications and the burden of premature vascular disease are the main sources of morbidity. Early retinal surveillance and cardiovascular risk management improve outcomes.","pitfalls":["Dismissing early flexural skin papules as a cosmetic issue rather than a diagnostic sign","Failing to arrange retinal surveillance, allowing preventable neovascular visual loss","Overlooking premature cardiovascular disease in a relatively young patient","Prescribing antithrombotic therapy without weighing the heightened bleeding risk","Not recognising angioid streaks as a pointer to systemic disease"],"management":["No disease-modifying drug exists, so management centres on aggressive cardiovascular risk reduction with blood pressure control, lipid lowering and smoking cessation to limit premature arterial disease","Regular dilated fundus examination and retinal imaging for angioid streaks, with prompt intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy for choroidal neovascularisation to preserve sight","Avoid unnecessary antiplatelet and anticoagulant drugs and contact sports given the tendency to haemorrhage","Supportive dermatological and plastic surgical management of lax cutaneous plaques for cosmesis","Surveillance for peripheral arterial disease with supervised exercise for claudication and vascular input as required","Screening for and prompt treatment of gastrointestinal haemorrhage from mineralised gastric arteries","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance and testing of at-risk relatives, coordinated within a multidisciplinary team spanning dermatology, ophthalmology, cardiology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Pseudoxanthoma elasticum","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"psoriasis":{"name":"Psoriasis","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["psoriasis vulgaris","plaque psoriasis","chronic plaque psoriasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic lesions are well-demarcated, salmon-pink plaques with adherent silvery scale, favouring extensor surfaces (elbows, knees), scalp, and the lumbosacral region","Gentle removal of scale may reveal pinpoint bleeding points (Auspitz sign), and new plaques can appear at sites of trauma (Koebner phenomenon)","Nail changes such as pitting, onycholysis, and oil-drop discolouration are common and support the diagnosis","Up to a third of patients develop psoriatic arthritis, so ask about joint pain, stiffness, and dactylitis at every review","Recognise clinical variants: guttate (often post-streptococcal in younger patients), inverse/flexural, pustular, and erythrodermic forms","Treatment is stepped: topical agents (corticosteroids, vitamin D analogues) for limited disease, phototherapy for widespread involvement, and systemic or biologic immunomodulators for severe or refractory disease","Screen for and address associated cardiometabolic risk, obesity, and depression, as psoriasis is a systemic inflammatory condition","Avoid abrupt withdrawal of systemic corticosteroids, which can precipitate a pustular or erythrodermic flare"],"pathophysiology":"Psoriasis is a chronic immune-mediated disorder driven by dysregulated activation of the innate and adaptive immune systems, particularly the interleukin-23/T-helper-17 axis and tumour necrosis factor signalling. Activated T cells and dendritic cells release cytokines that drive keratinocyte hyperproliferation and abnormal differentiation, dramatically shortening epidermal turnover time and producing thickened, scaly plaques. Dilated dermal vessels and a neutrophilic and lymphocytic infiltrate account for the erythema and, in pustular variants, sterile collections of neutrophils. A strong polygenic predisposition (including PSORS1/HLA-C associations) interacts with triggers such as infection, trauma, drugs, and stress.","additionalDifferentials":["Seborrhoeic dermatitis (especially on the scalp and face)","Chronic eczema or lichen simplex chronicus","Tinea corporis or tinea capitis","Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (mycosis fungoides)","Pityriasis rubra pilaris","Secondary syphilis in atypical guttate-like eruptions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Largely a clinical diagnosis; skin biopsy reserved for atypical presentations","Throat swab or streptococcal serology when guttate psoriasis is suspected","Joint assessment and imaging when psoriatic arthritis is suspected","Baseline and monitoring bloods (including liver function and infection screening) before systemic or biologic therapy","Metabolic screening for cardiovascular risk factors, lipids, and glucose"],"redFlags":["Erythroderma with widespread redness, scaling, and impaired thermoregulation requiring urgent care","Generalised pustular psoriasis with fever and systemic upset","Rapidly progressive joint destruction or dactylitis suggesting aggressive psoriatic arthritis","Signs of secondary infection or sepsis in extensive skin breakdown"],"prognosis":"Psoriasis is a lifelong relapsing-remitting condition; most patients achieve good control with appropriate therapy, but disease severity fluctuates and associated arthritis and cardiometabolic disease influence long-term outcomes.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling scalp or flexural psoriasis as seborrhoeic dermatitis or fungal infection","Overlooking psoriatic arthritis until irreversible joint damage has occurred","Using potent topical steroids in flexures or on the face without appropriate caution","Precipitating pustular or erythrodermic flares by withdrawing systemic corticosteroids"],"management":["Topical corticosteroids, vitamin D analogues and their combinations, with emollients and coal tar or salicylic acid, for limited plaque disease","Phototherapy with narrowband UVB or psoralen-UVA for extensive or refractory plaque and guttate disease","Conventional systemic agents such as methotrexate, ciclosporin or acitretin for moderate-to-severe disease","Biologic and targeted therapy directed at tumour necrosis factor, interleukin-17 and the interleukin-12/23 axis, or oral apremilast, for severe or refractory disease","Actively screen for and treat psoriatic arthritis, and manage cardiometabolic comorbidities including lipids, glucose, weight and smoking","Identify and address triggers such as streptococcal infection in guttate disease, culprit drugs, skin trauma and stress","Baseline and ongoing monitoring bloods with infection screening, including latent tuberculosis, before and during systemic or biologic therapy","Urgent inpatient management and dermatology referral for erythrodermic or generalised pustular psoriasis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"psoriatic_arthritis":{"name":"Psoriatic arthritis","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PsA","arthritis psoriatica","psoriatic arthropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An inflammatory arthritis associated with psoriasis; skin disease usually precedes joint involvement but arthritis can appear first or without evident skin lesions","Presents in several patterns including asymmetric oligoarthritis, symmetric polyarthritis resembling rheumatoid arthritis, distal interphalangeal predominant disease, spondylitis, and the destructive arthritis mutilans","Dactylitis ('sausage digit') and enthesitis are hallmark features that help distinguish it from rheumatoid arthritis","Nail changes such as pitting and onycholysis correlate with distal interphalangeal joint involvement","Usually seronegative for rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies","Radiographs may show combined erosion and new bone formation, with the classic 'pencil-in-cup' deformity in advanced disease","Associated comorbidities include uveitis, inflammatory bowel disease, and increased cardiovascular and metabolic risk","Treatment is guided by domains involved (peripheral joints, axial disease, enthesitis, dactylitis, skin, nails), using NSAIDs, conventional DMARDs, and biologic or targeted synthetic agents (TNF, IL-17, IL-23, JAK inhibitors)"],"pathophysiology":"Psoriatic arthritis is a seronegative spondyloarthropathy resulting from immune-mediated inflammation in genetically predisposed individuals. Dysregulation of the TNF and IL-23/IL-17 axes drives inflammation at synovium, entheses, and skin. Unlike rheumatoid arthritis, both bone erosion and pathological new bone formation occur, producing the mixed destructive and proliferative changes seen radiographically. Enthesitis is considered a primary lesion, and there are associations with HLA-B27 (particularly axial disease) and other HLA alleles.","additionalDifferentials":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Ankylosing spondylitis and other spondyloarthritides","Reactive arthritis","Gout and other crystal arthropathies","Osteoarthritis, particularly of distal interphalangeal joints","Undifferentiated inflammatory arthritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination of skin and nails for psoriasis, pitting, and onycholysis","Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies to help distinguish from rheumatoid arthritis","Radiographs of hands, feet, and axial skeleton for erosions, new bone, and pencil-in-cup change","Ultrasound or MRI to detect enthesitis, synovitis, and dactylitis","Inflammatory markers and metabolic/cardiovascular risk screening"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive, deforming or mutilating arthritis with joint destruction (arthritis mutilans)","Acute painful red eye indicating uveitis requiring ophthalmology review","Features suggesting axial disease with neurological compromise","Symptoms of undiagnosed inflammatory bowel disease","Extensive or erythrodermic/pustular skin disease indicating severe systemic involvement"],"prognosis":"Course ranges from mild intermittent arthritis to progressive erosive disease with joint destruction and disability. Early recognition and treat-to-target therapy improve outcomes; poor prognostic markers include polyarticular involvement, elevated inflammatory markers, early erosions, and dactylitis.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis when arthritis precedes or occurs without obvious skin psoriasis","Mislabelling it as rheumatoid arthritis and overlooking dactylitis, enthesitis, and nail disease","Failing to assess and treat all involved domains rather than joints alone","Neglecting cardiovascular, metabolic, and uveitis comorbidities","Assuming seronegativity excludes an inflammatory arthritis"],"management":["NSAIDs and local corticosteroid injections for symptom relief in mild peripheral or entheseal disease","Conventional synthetic DMARDs such as methotrexate, leflunomide or sulfasalazine for persistent peripheral arthritis","Biologic therapy targeting tumour necrosis factor, interleukin-17 or interleukin-23, or targeted synthetic agents such as JAK inhibitors or apremilast, for inadequate response or predominant axial and entheseal disease","Adopt a treat-to-target approach aiming for minimal disease activity, with regular assessment of joints, entheses, dactylitis and skin","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and exercise to preserve joint function, with orthopaedic referral for arthritis mutilans","Coordinate skin and joint care with dermatology and manage cardiovascular and metabolic comorbidity","Baseline and monitoring investigations with infection screening, including tuberculosis, before immunomodulatory therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"psychiatric_disorders":{"name":"Psychiatric Disorders","system":"Psychiatric / Behavioral (Neurologic Disorders)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mental disorders","Anxiety disorders","Mood disorders","Depression","Bipolar disorder","Schizophrenia","Somatic symptom disorder","Eating disorders","Personality disorders"],"chapter":463,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3657","clinicalPearls":["Mental or substance use disorders affect roughly 30% of the U.S. population, yet only about one-third of those affected are currently in treatment; four of the ten leading causes of global disability are psychiatric in origin, and depression is the single largest contributor to disability worldwide.","Brief validated office instruments (PRIME-MD / Patient Health Questionnaire, SDDS-PC, the four-item PC-PTSD screen) take only minutes and map patient responses onto formal DSM criteria, helping primary care clinicians make the initial diagnosis.","Panic attacks reach peak intensity within about 10 minutes and usually resolve within an hour; up to ~75% of panic disorder patients eventually meet criteria for major depression, and panic is a frequent occult cause of chest pain with normal coronary angiograms and of unexplained syncope.","Agoraphobia and housebound behavior are often concealed out of embarrassment, so the syndrome is missed unless the clinician asks directly; about one-third of patients presenting with anxiety have an underlying medical etiology.","Major depression requires depressed mood or anhedonia plus a total of at least 5 of 9 symptoms for a minimum of 2 weeks; key distinguishing features from grief are pervasive worthlessness/self-loathing and persistent (rather than wave-like) dysphoria.","Suicide risk must be probed by direct, empathic questioning: about 4-5% of depressed patients die by suicide, and most have seen a physician within the month before death; coexisting anxiety, panic, or agitation raises near-term risk.","Recognizing a bipolar diathesis in a patient presenting with depression is essential because antidepressant monotherapy can worsen symptoms and accelerate cycling; clues include childhood onset, antidepressant treatment failure, atypical features (hypersomnia, weight gain), and marked irritability/impulsivity.","Anorexia nervosa carries the highest mortality of any psychiatric disorder (~5.1 per 1000 untreated); characteristic lab and exam findings include leukopenia with relative lymphocytosis, raised BUN, metabolic alkalosis and hypokalemia with purging, amenorrhea, lanugo, hypotension, hypothermia, and sinus bradycardia.","Bulimia nervosa usually occurs at normal or slightly above-normal weight, and physical exam is often unremarkable except for dental enamel erosion and parotid gland enlargement; medical danger stems from purging-related fluid/electrolyte disturbance and cardiac conduction abnormalities.","Schizophrenia requires at least two characteristic symptoms (positive and/or negative) over a 1-month period with continuous signs for at least 6 months; about 10% of patients die by suicide, and negative symptoms predominate in roughly one-third and predict poor drug response.","Personality change can be the first manifestation of serious neurologic, endocrine, or other medical disease (e.g., a frontal-lobe tumor presenting with altered motivation while the neurologic exam stays normal), so a personality disorder is a diagnosis that requires exclusion of such causes.","In somatic symptom disorder the defining feature is the patient's disproportionate interpretation and preoccupation with symptoms, not the absence of a medical explanation; patients with genuine medical disease can still qualify, and repeated tests and exploratory surgery are counterproductive."],"pathophysiology":"Psychiatric disorders are conceptualized as CNS disorders arising from interacting genetic, developmental, and environmental factors, with substantial heritability and considerable overlap of genetic risk variants across diagnoses (pleiotropy). Anxiety and panic involve noradrenergic discharge from the locus coeruleus and serotonergic dorsal-raphe pathways, with lactate, yohimbine, CCK-4, and CO2 capable of provoking attacks; generalized anxiety implicates the GABA-A receptor/chloride-channel complex and serotonergic modulation. PTSD is associated with excessive locus coeruleus norepinephrine release facilitating fear-memory encoding, insular threat-detection overactivity, and blunted pituitary-adrenal responses. OCD involves orbitofrontal cortex-caudate-globus pallidus circuitry. Major depression shows elevated cortisol and CRH, dexamethasone non-suppression, blunted TSH response to TRH, shortened REM latency with increased REM density and reduced slow-wave sleep, altered proinflammatory cytokines and neurotrophins, oxidative stress, telomere shortening, and possible gut-microbiome changes; delayed therapeutic action of antidepressants implicates adaptive second-messenger and neurotrophic changes. Bipolar disorder shows very high monozygotic concordance (approaching 80%) with risk genes (CACNA1C, ANK3, NCAN, ODZ4, TRANK1) shared with schizophrenia and autism, plus circadian and calcium dysregulation. Schizophrenia involves dopamine D2/D3 signaling in the ventral striatum, many small-effect GWAS loci and rare copy-number variants, and pathways of immunity, inflammation, and cell signaling.","additionalDifferentials":["Major depression vs bipolar depression vs depression secondary to general medical illness or substance use","Major depressive episode vs normal bereavement/grief (grief features emptiness and wave-like pangs with preserved self-esteem)","Generalized anxiety disorder vs panic disorder (they segregate independently in families)","Schizophrenia vs schizophreniform disorder (shorter duration), schizoaffective disorder, delusional disorder, and brief psychotic disorder","Somatic symptom disorder vs illness anxiety disorder vs conversion (functional neurologic symptom) disorder vs factitious disorder vs malingering","Anorexia nervosa vs avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (the latter lacks fear of weight gain and body-image distortion)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV-associated depression and CNS disease (lifetime depression prevalence in HIV-positive individuals ~22-45%)","Chronic hepatitis C-associated depression, which may worsen with interferon-alpha therapy","AIDS as a cause of secondary mania"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pheochromocytoma, thyrotoxicosis, and hypoglycemia presenting as anxiety/panic","Hyperthyroidism, Huntington's disease, Wilson's disease, frontotemporal dementia, and cerebrovascular accidents presenting as secondary mania","Hypothyroidism (including subclinical) and hyperthyroidism presenting with depressed mood and cognitive/memory impairment","Frontal-lobe tumor presenting as personality change with a normal neurologic exam","Depressive pseudodementia in the elderly mimicking neurodegenerative dementia","Cardiovascular conditions (paroxysmal atrial tachycardia, mitral valve prolapse) detected on ECG/echocardiography in patients labeled with panic","Drug- or medication-induced psychosis (parkinsonian agents, clonidine, quinacrine, procaine derivatives) mimicking schizophrenia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted history, physical exam, and focused labs to exclude medical mimics of anxiety (e.g., thyroid studies, glucose, catecholamine workup as indicated)","ECG and echocardiography when a cardiovascular cause of panic-like symptoms is suspected","Thyroid function testing in mood disorders and in rapid-cycling bipolar disorder (lithium has antithyroid effects)","TCA plasma levels to guide dosing and interpret apparent treatment resistance or toxicity","Lithium blood levels (target ~0.8-1.2 meq/L acutely) given the narrow therapeutic-to-toxic window; valproate level 50-125 ug/mL; carbamazepine 4-12 ug/mL","In anorexia nervosa: CBC (leukopenia/relative lymphocytosis), electrolytes (hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis with purging), BUN, and bone-density assessment for osteoporosis"],"redFlags":["Suicidal or homicidal ideation, a specific plan, or prior attempts - warrants immediate referral to a mental health specialist","Psychotic symptoms, mania, or severe depression/anxiety failing first-line treatment - indications for psychiatric consultation or transfer of care","Coexisting anxiety, panic, or agitation in a depressed patient, which raises near-term suicide risk","Critical weight loss, hypotension, hypothermia, bradycardia, or electrolyte/cardiac conduction abnormalities in eating disorders","Refeeding syndrome risk when restoring weight too rapidly in anorexia nervosa (target gain ~0.5-1 lb/week)","Serotonin syndrome (myoclonus, agitation, abdominal cramping, hyperpyrexia, hypertension) - a contraindication to combining MAOIs with SSRIs","Hypertensive crisis from tyramine-containing foods or sympathomimetics in patients taking MAOIs","New-onset psychosis requiring exclusion of a drug/medication cause before diagnosing schizophrenia"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely by disorder. Panic disorder responds to treatment in 75-85% of patients, though complete remission is somewhat lower. Most depressed patients (60-70%) respond to an adequately dosed antidepressant over 6-8 weeks, and about three-quarters improve by ~2 months; the best predictor of recurrence is the number of prior episodes (50-60% of first-episode patients have at least one or two recurrences). Bipolar disorder is recurrent and requires maintenance therapy; lithium responses are 70-80% in acute mania and a sustained level >=0.8 meq/L reduces suicide risk. About 4-5% of depressed and ~10% of schizophrenic patients die by suicide. Anorexia nervosa has the highest mortality of any psychiatric condition (~5.1/1000 untreated), though most patients achieve remission within 5 years; binge-eating disorder has the most favorable prognosis among eating disorders. Schizophrenia often has a poor outcome with insidious onset, but positive symptoms tend to attenuate with age and permanent remission occasionally occurs.","pitfalls":["Basing psychiatric referral on the mere absence of a physical explanation rather than on positive signs/symptoms of a mental disorder","Failing to ask directly about agoraphobia, OCD behaviors, or suicidal ideation, all of which patients commonly conceal","Subjecting somatic symptom disorder patients to repeated tests and exploratory surgery, or confronting them in a way that causes humiliation and treatment abandonment","Treating an unrecognized bipolar depression with antidepressant monotherapy, risking induction of mania or cycle acceleration","Prescribing benzodiazepines beyond 4-6 weeks (tolerance, abuse, dependence) and not anticipating withdrawal, which can include seizures","Giving benzodiazepines or SSRIs in the immediate aftermath of trauma, and using debriefing, which does not prevent PTSD and may worsen symptoms","Using TCAs in patients with serious cardiovascular risk or bundle branch block, and providing large supplies to suicidal patients (TCA overdose can be lethal, desipramine highest risk)","Overlooking lithium's narrow therapeutic index and its antithyroid/renal effects during maintenance monitoring"],"management":["Use validated screening instruments such as the Patient Health Questionnaire and PC-PTSD and exclude medical mimics before confirming a primary psychiatric diagnosis","First-line pharmacotherapy for depressive and anxiety disorders with SSRIs or SNRIs, augmenting or switching for treatment resistance","Evidence-based psychotherapy, particularly cognitive behavioural therapy, alone or combined with medication","Mood stabilisers such as lithium or anticonvulsants for bipolar disorder, with therapeutic drug-level monitoring given their narrow therapeutic window and thyroid and renal surveillance on lithium","Antipsychotics for psychotic disorders and as adjuncts, with metabolic monitoring","Structured suicide risk assessment at every contact, escalating to crisis or inpatient care when risk is high","Nutritional rehabilitation with monitoring for refeeding in eating disorders such as anorexia nervosa","Manage comorbid substance misuse and coordinate care across primary care, psychiatry and psychological services"],"severityClassification":"Diagnoses are framed by DSM-5-TR (with ICD-10-CM used more widely worldwide); alternative research frameworks include NIMH RDoC and the dimensional HiTOP taxonomy, none yet adequately validated. Severity and subtyping markers within disorders include: bipolar I (full manic episode >=1 week) vs bipolar II (hypomania >=4 days plus recurrent depression) vs cyclothymia (>=2 years of subthreshold fluctuations); rapid cycling defined as >=4 mood episodes per year; persistent depressive (dysthymic) disorder as chronic symptoms >=2 years; PTSD specifiers (with dissociative symptoms, with delayed expression >=6 months); anorexia nervosa restricting type vs binge-eating/purging type; and Munchausen's syndrome as the most severe form of factitious disorder.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3657","Harrison 22e p.3658","Harrison 22e p.3661","Harrison 22e p.3664","Harrison 22e p.3667","Harrison 22e p.3670","Harrison 22e p.3671","Harrison 22e p.3672"],"crossLinks":[]},"pterygium":{"name":"Pterygium","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["surfer's eye","pterygia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A pterygium is a wing-shaped fibrovascular growth of conjunctival tissue that encroaches onto the cornea, most commonly on the nasal side","Strongly associated with chronic exposure to ultraviolet light, wind, and dust, and therefore more common in people living in sunny, outdoor environments near the equator","It is distinguished from a pinguecula, which is a similar degenerative conjunctival lesion that does not cross onto the cornea","Symptoms range from a cosmetic concern and irritation or redness to visual disturbance when it induces astigmatism or approaches the visual axis","A leading edge (Stocker's line of iron deposition) and a fibrovascular head advancing over the cornea are characteristic","Management is conservative with lubricants and UV protection for mild lesions; surgical excision is indicated for visual axis threat, marked astigmatism, discomfort, or cosmesis","Recurrence after excision is common, and adjuncts such as conjunctival autografting reduce this risk compared with bare-scleral excision","Atypical or rapidly growing lesions should be evaluated to exclude ocular surface squamous neoplasia"],"pathophysiology":"A pterygium is a degenerative and proliferative condition of the bulbar conjunctiva in which chronic ultraviolet and environmental exposure causes elastotic degeneration of subepithelial collagen along with fibrovascular proliferation. The abnormal tissue invades the superficial cornea, disrupting Bowman's layer and the corneal architecture at its advancing head. Progressive growth can flatten the cornea and induce astigmatism, and encroachment onto the visual axis reduces vision.","additionalDifferentials":["Pinguecula","Pseudopterygium (from prior injury or inflammation)","Ocular surface squamous neoplasia (conjunctival intraepithelial neoplasia)","Conjunctival naevus or other pigmented lesion","Limbal dermoid"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination to document size, vascularity, and encroachment onto the cornea","Refraction and corneal topography to assess induced astigmatism","Serial photography to monitor progression","Excision biopsy with histology when ocular surface neoplasia is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, marked thickening, or leukoplakic change suggesting ocular surface squamous neoplasia","Encroachment onto or across the visual axis threatening vision","Significant induced astigmatism degrading acuity","Restricted ocular motility from a large or recurrent lesion with scarring"],"prognosis":"Most pterygia are benign and slowly progressive with a good prognosis; surgery is effective for symptomatic or vision-threatening lesions, though recurrence is a recognised problem, particularly with bare-scleral techniques and in younger patients.","pitfalls":["Assuming all fleshy conjunctival lesions are benign pterygia and missing ocular surface neoplasia","Operating too early on quiescent lesions given the risk of recurrence","Using bare-scleral excision alone, which has a high recurrence rate compared with grafting","Neglecting UV protection advice, allowing progression or recurrence"],"management":["Ocular lubricants and, for inflamed lesions, short courses of topical corticosteroids or decongestants to relieve irritation and redness","Ultraviolet protection with sunglasses and brimmed hats and avoidance of wind and dust exposure to slow progression","Surgical excision for lesions threatening the visual axis, inducing marked astigmatism, or causing troublesome symptoms or cosmetic concern","Conjunctival autograft rather than a bare-scleral technique, or adjunctive mitomycin C, to reduce recurrence after excision","Refraction and corneal topography to quantify induced astigmatism, with serial slit-lamp examination and photography to monitor growth","Histological examination of excised tissue to exclude ocular surface squamous neoplasia in atypical lesions","Ophthalmology referral for symptomatic or progressive lesions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"ptosis":{"name":"Ptosis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["blepharoptosis","drooping eyelid"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Ptosis is abnormal drooping of the upper eyelid; severity is gauged by the margin-reflex distance and the effect on the visual axis","Assessing levator function is central to classification and surgical planning","Aponeurotic (involutional) ptosis is the commonest, showing good levator function, a high skin crease, and often thinning of the eyelid","A ptosis with a small pupil and ipsilateral anhidrosis suggests Horner syndrome; a ptosis with a dilated pupil and an eye deviated down and out suggests a third nerve palsy","Variable, fatigable ptosis worsening through the day points to myasthenia gravis and warrants systemic evaluation","Congenital ptosis usually reflects levator dystrophy with poor function and risks amblyopia if the visual axis is occluded in a child","The rare Marcus Gunn jaw-winking phenomenon links lid elevation to jaw movement"],"pathophysiology":"Ptosis results from impaired elevation of the upper lid, which is normally raised by the levator palpebrae superioris (oculomotor nerve) and Muller muscle (sympathetic). Aponeurotic ptosis is caused by dehiscence or stretching of the levator aponeurosis with age. Neurogenic ptosis follows interruption of the nerve supply, as in third nerve palsy or sympathetic denervation (Horner syndrome). Myogenic ptosis reflects muscle disease such as myasthenia gravis, chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia, or myotonic dystrophy. Mechanical ptosis arises from lid mass or scarring, and congenital ptosis from maldevelopment of the levator muscle.","additionalDifferentials":["Aponeurotic (involutional) ptosis","Third nerve palsy","Horner syndrome","Myasthenia gravis and other myogenic causes","Mechanical ptosis from lid mass or oedema","Pseudoptosis from dermatochalasis, brow ptosis, or contralateral lid retraction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Measurement of margin-reflex distance, palpebral aperture, levator function, and skin crease position","Pupil examination to identify Horner syndrome or third nerve palsy","Assessment for fatigability and ice-pack or targeted testing when myasthenia is suspected","Neuroimaging when a compressive or neurogenic cause is suspected","Systemic and antibody testing for suspected myasthenia gravis"],"redFlags":["Painful ptosis with a dilated pupil and ophthalmoplegia suggesting a compressive third nerve palsy (possible aneurysm) requiring urgent imaging","Ptosis with anisocoria and anhidrosis (Horner syndrome), which may indicate serious underlying pathology such as an apical lung lesion or carotid dissection","Fatigable or variable ptosis with diplopia or bulbar symptoms suggesting myasthenia gravis","Ptosis occluding the visual axis in a child, threatening amblyopia"],"prognosis":"Aponeurotic and congenital ptosis generally respond well to surgery; the outlook for neurogenic and myogenic ptosis depends on the underlying cause, and childhood ptosis carries a risk of amblyopia if not managed promptly.","pitfalls":["Treating ptosis as purely cosmetic and missing a serious neurological cause such as third nerve palsy or Horner syndrome","Failing to test for fatigability and overlooking myasthenia gravis","Not recognising amblyopia risk in congenital ptosis","Mistaking pseudoptosis from brow droop or dermatochalasis for true ptosis"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying mechanism, which differs for aponeurotic, neurogenic, myogenic and mechanical ptosis","Levator advancement or resection surgery for aponeurotic and congenital ptosis with adequate levator function, and a frontalis sling when levator function is poor","Urgently evaluate a painful third nerve palsy with a dilated pupil to exclude a compressive aneurysm, and investigate Horner syndrome for its cause","Treat myasthenia gravis medically with acetylcholinesterase inhibitors and immunosuppression before considering lid surgery, since the ptosis fluctuates","In children, correct promptly when the visual axis is occluded to prevent amblyopia, with orthoptic follow-up","Remove or treat mechanical causes such as lid masses or scarring","Ophthalmology and oculoplastic referral, with neurology input for neurogenic and myogenic causes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"ptsd":{"name":"Post-traumatic stress disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTSD","post traumatic stress disorder","posttraumatic stress disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Develops after exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence, whether directly experienced, witnessed, or learned about affecting a close other","Core symptom clusters are intrusion (flashbacks, nightmares, distressing memories), avoidance of trauma reminders, negative alterations in mood and cognition, and heightened arousal and reactivity","Symptoms persist beyond about one month and cause significant distress or impairment; an acute stress reaction is the label used within the first month","Hyperarousal features include hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, irritability, sleep disturbance, and concentration problems","Trauma-focused psychotherapies (such as trauma-focused CBT and eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing) are first-line treatments","Pharmacotherapy principles favour SSRIs or SNRIs; prazosin may help trauma-related nightmares, and benzodiazepines are generally avoided","Screen for common comorbidities including depression, substance use, and suicidal ideation, and for traumatic brain injury in relevant populations","Dissociative symptoms and delayed onset (months to years after the event) are recognised presentations"],"pathophysiology":"Post-traumatic stress disorder reflects dysregulated fear processing, with exaggerated amygdala responses, impaired prefrontal inhibition, and hippocampal changes affecting contextual memory, alongside altered hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis and noradrenergic stress responses. These changes underpin persistent conditioned fear, impaired extinction of trauma memories, and chronic hyperarousal.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute stress disorder","Adjustment disorder","Major depressive disorder","Panic disorder and other anxiety disorders","Obsessive-compulsive disorder","Substance use disorder","Psychosis when flashbacks are mistaken for hallucinations","Traumatic brain injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured trauma history and PTSD symptom rating scales","Suicide risk assessment","Screening for comorbid depression and substance use","Assessment for traumatic brain injury where relevant","Evaluation of sleep disturbance and nightmares"],"redFlags":["Suicidal ideation or self-harm","Severe dissociative episodes with risk to safety","Escalating substance or alcohol use to cope with symptoms","Aggression or risk to others during flashbacks or heightened arousal"],"prognosis":"Many people recover within the first year, especially with early trauma-focused treatment, but a substantial minority develop a chronic course. Prognosis is worse with severe or prolonged trauma, lack of social support, and comorbid depression or substance use.","pitfalls":["Using benzodiazepines, which do not treat core symptoms and may impair recovery","Missing comorbid depression, substance use, and suicide risk","Overlooking delayed-onset presentations by not taking a trauma history","Mislabelling normal early stress reactions as PTSD before the required duration","Failing to refer for evidence-based trauma-focused psychotherapy"],"management":["Trauma-focused psychological therapy as first-line treatment, using trauma-focused cognitive behavioural therapy or eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing","Pharmacotherapy with SSRIs or SNRIs when medication is indicated or psychotherapy is insufficient","Prazosin for distressing trauma-related nightmares and sleep disturbance","Avoid benzodiazepines, which are ineffective for core symptoms and risk dependence","Treat comorbid depression, substance misuse and sleep disorder, which worsen prognosis","Structured suicide risk assessment and collaborative safety planning","Watchful waiting with psychoeducation for milder symptoms within the first month, referring to specialist mental health services for persistent or severe disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_alveolar_microlithiasis":{"name":"Pulmonary alveolar microlithiasis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAM","alveolar microlithiasis","SLC34A2-related microlithiasis","microlithiasis alveolaris pulmonum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine / European Respiratory Monograph on rare lung disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function variants in SLC34A2, encoding a type IIb sodium-phosphate cotransporter in alveolar type II cells","Failure to clear alveolar phosphate leads to deposition of countless calcium-phosphate concretions (microliths, calcospherites) filling the alveolar spaces","Striking clinico-radiological dissociation is characteristic, with extensive calcified change on imaging in a patient who may be minimally symptomatic for years","The chest radiograph classically shows a diffuse fine sand-like micronodular pattern (a 'sandstorm' lung) that is densest in the lower zones","High-resolution CT demonstrates calcified micronodules, subpleural calcific interlobular septal thickening, and dense posterior/basal consolidation, sometimes with a subpleural 'black pleural line'","Diagnosis is often made confidently on imaging in the right context and can be confirmed by identifying microliths in sputum, bronchoalveolar lavage, or biopsy, or by genetic testing","Familial clustering is common and screening of siblings is worthwhile given the recessive inheritance","There is no proven disease-modifying therapy; management is supportive and lung transplantation is considered for end-stage disease"],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary alveolar microlithiasis results from biallelic loss-of-function variants in SLC34A2, which encodes the sodium-dependent phosphate cotransporter normally expressed on alveolar type II pneumocytes. This transporter clears phosphate liberated by the turnover of surfactant phospholipid from the alveolar lining fluid. When it is deficient, phosphate accumulates and combines with calcium to precipitate as innumerable laminated calcium-phosphate microliths within the alveoli. Progressive filling of the air spaces and later interstitial fibrosis and ossification impair gas exchange and lung compliance over decades, eventually producing restrictive physiology and pulmonary hypertension.","additionalDifferentials":["Metastatic pulmonary calcification from chronic renal failure or hypercalcaemia","Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","Miliary tuberculosis or other diffuse micronodular infection","Sarcoidosis with fine nodularity","Dendriform pulmonary ossification","Amiodarone-related or other diffuse calcified deposition"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution CT of the chest to characterise calcified micronodules, septal thickening, and the subpleural black pleural line","Genetic testing for pathogenic SLC34A2 variants and screening of first-degree relatives","Bronchoalveolar lavage or transbronchial biopsy to demonstrate calcospherite microliths when imaging is inconclusive","Pulmonary function tests with diffusing capacity to quantify restriction and gas-transfer impairment","Serum calcium, phosphate, and renal function to exclude metastatic calcification","Echocardiography to screen for pulmonary hypertension in advanced disease"],"redFlags":["Progressive breathlessness and hypoxaemia signalling advancing restrictive disease","Signs of pulmonary hypertension or right heart strain","Pneumothorax, which can complicate the stiff calcified lung","Rapid functional decline suggesting need for transplant assessment"],"prognosis":"The disease usually progresses very slowly over decades, and many patients remain stable for long periods, but a subset develop relentless restrictive fibrosis, respiratory failure, and pulmonary hypertension for which lung transplantation is the only definitive option.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the dramatic calcified imaging for widespread infection such as miliary tuberculosis and treating inappropriately","Assuming severe disease because imaging looks alarming, when symptoms may be mild for years","Overlooking the recessive inheritance and failing to screen affected siblings","Attributing the calcification to renal metabolic disease without checking calcium, phosphate, and renal function"],"management":["No established disease-modifying drug therapy exists, so care is largely supportive as the microlith burden accumulates, with bisphosphonates and a low-phosphate diet remaining unproven experimental options","Long-term oxygen therapy for hypoxaemic respiratory failure and pulmonary rehabilitation to support exercise capacity","Serial pulmonary function testing with diffusing capacity and imaging to monitor restrictive progression","Echocardiographic surveillance for pulmonary hypertension in advanced disease, with supportive treatment of right heart failure","Smoking avoidance and prompt treatment of intercurrent respiratory infection","Lung transplantation as the only definitive option for end-stage restrictive fibrosis and respiratory failure","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance and screening of first-degree relatives for SLC34A2 variants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","European Respiratory Society monograph on rare interstitial lung diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_alveolar_proteinosis":{"name":"Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAP","Alveolar proteinosis","Alveolar lipoproteinosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by accumulation of surfactant-derived lipoproteinaceous material within the alveoli.","Three forms exist: autoimmune (over ninety per cent, mediated by anti-GM-CSF autoantibodies), secondary (haematological malignancy, inhaled dusts such as silica, immunodeficiency or infection) and hereditary (GM-CSF receptor or surfactant gene mutations, chiefly in children).","Insidious breathlessness and cough occur, and clinical signs are often disproportionately mild relative to imaging, a clinico-radiological dissociation.","HRCT shows 'crazy paving': ground-glass opacity with superimposed interlobular septal thickening in a geographical distribution.","Bronchoalveolar lavage is both diagnostic and therapeutic, yielding opaque, milky, periodic acid-Schiff (PAS) positive effluent.","An elevated serum anti-GM-CSF autoantibody confirms the autoimmune form.","Whole-lung lavage is the standard treatment for significant disease, with inhaled or subcutaneous GM-CSF an option in autoimmune disease.","There is an increased risk of secondary infection, including with Nocardia and mycobacteria."],"pathophysiology":"Surfactant homeostasis depends on granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor (GM-CSF) signalling for alveolar macrophage maturation and surfactant clearance. In autoimmune disease, neutralising anti-GM-CSF autoantibodies impair macrophage function; secondary and hereditary forms disrupt the same pathway through reduced macrophage numbers or function or through receptor defects. The result is progressive alveolar filling with surfactant material that impairs gas exchange.","additionalDifferentials":["Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","Cardiogenic or non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema","Exogenous or endogenous lipoid pneumonia","Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia and other ground-glass interstitial lung disease","Invasive mucinous adenocarcinoma","Acute hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["HRCT thorax showing crazy paving","Serum anti-GM-CSF autoantibody titre","Bronchoalveolar lavage with characteristic milky PAS-positive fluid","Pulmonary function tests with transfer factor, showing restriction and markedly reduced gas transfer","Serum lactate dehydrogenase, which is often raised","Transbronchial or surgical biopsy if lavage is non-diagnostic","Screening for secondary causes with blood film or marrow examination, occupational dust history and immune function tests"],"redFlags":["Superimposed opportunistic infection with Nocardia, mycobacteria or fungi","Progressive hypoxaemic respiratory failure","Rapid deterioration suggesting secondary disease from a haematological malignancy","Development of secondary pulmonary fibrosis"],"prognosis":"Autoimmune disease often responds well to whole-lung lavage with good long-term survival; the prognosis of secondary disease depends on the underlying disorder, and some cases resolve spontaneously.","pitfalls":["Mistaking crazy paving for infection or pulmonary oedema","Not measuring the anti-GM-CSF autoantibody to classify disease","Missing an underlying haematological malignancy in secondary disease","Failing to consider opportunistic infection when a patient deteriorates"],"management":["Whole-lung lavage as the mainstay for symptomatic disease, physically clearing accumulated surfactant material and improving gas exchange","Inhaled or subcutaneous GM-CSF augmentation therapy for autoimmune, anti-GM-CSF antibody-positive disease","Rituximab or plasma exchange for refractory autoimmune disease","Identify and treat the underlying cause in secondary disease, such as haematological malignancy, inhaled dust exposure, immunodeficiency or infection","Supportive oxygen and prompt treatment of superimposed opportunistic infection such as Nocardia","Serial pulmonary function with transfer factor, oximetry and imaging to monitor and time repeat lavage","Lung transplantation for progressive disease unresponsive to lavage, with genetic evaluation for hereditary forms in children"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","European Respiratory Society guidance on pulmonary alveolar proteinosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_arterial_hypertension":{"name":"pulmonary arterial hypertension","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAH","pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension (WHO Group 1)"],"chapter":null,"source":"ERS/ESC Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Pulmonary Hypertension","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["PAH is pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension (WHO Group 1): mean pulmonary arterial pressure above 20 mmHg with a pulmonary artery wedge pressure of 15 mmHg or less and pulmonary vascular resistance greater than 2 Wood units at right heart catheterisation.","Subtypes include idiopathic, heritable (commonly BMPR2 mutations), drug or toxin induced (for example dexfenfluramine, methamphetamine), and forms associated with connective tissue disease, congenital heart disease, portal hypertension, HIV and schistosomiasis.","Systemic sclerosis is the commonest connective tissue disease association and warrants annual screening.","Right heart catheterisation is mandatory for diagnosis; echocardiography only estimates probability and cannot confirm PAH.","Acute vasoreactivity testing identifies the minority (mainly idiopathic, heritable or drug induced) who respond to and benefit from calcium channel blockers.","Risk stratification using functional class, exercise capacity, natriuretic peptides and haemodynamics guides escalation and the intensity of therapy.","Targeted therapies act on three pathways: endothelin (endothelin receptor antagonists), nitric oxide (phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors and soluble guanylate cyclase stimulators) and prostacyclin (prostacyclin analogues and receptor agonists).","Upfront combination therapy is now standard for most patients, with parenteral prostacyclins reserved for high-risk disease and transplantation for refractory cases."],"pathophysiology":"PAH results from a progressive vasculopathy of the small pulmonary arteries driven by endothelial dysfunction, with an imbalance between vasoconstrictor and mitogenic mediators (endothelin-1, thromboxane) and vasodilator and antiproliferative mediators (nitric oxide, prostacyclin). This favours vasoconstriction, smooth muscle and endothelial proliferation, in-situ thrombosis and adventitial remodelling, producing intimal fibrosis, medial hypertrophy and plexiform lesions. Impaired bone morphogenetic protein signalling, classically through BMPR2 loss of function, promotes this angioproliferation. The resulting rise in pulmonary vascular resistance increases right ventricular afterload, leading initially to right ventricular hypertrophy and ultimately to dilatation and failure, which determines symptoms and survival.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary hypertension due to left heart disease (Group 2)","Pulmonary hypertension due to lung disease or hypoxia (Group 3)","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (Group 4)","Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease or pulmonary capillary haemangiomatosis","Congenital systemic-to-pulmonary shunts","Deconditioning or other causes of exertional dyspnoea","High-output states (anaemia, thyrotoxicosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Right heart catheterisation with acute vasoreactivity testing","Transthoracic echocardiography","Ventilation/perfusion scan to exclude chronic thromboembolic disease","Pulmonary function tests with DLCO","Connective tissue disease serology (ANA, anti-centromere, anti-Scl-70), HIV test and liver studies","Cardiac MRI and CT pulmonary angiography","Six-minute walk test and natriuretic peptides for risk assessment"],"redFlags":["Exertional syncope or presyncope, a marker of severe disease","Signs of right heart failure (raised JVP, ascites, peripheral oedema)","Rapidly declining exercise capacity or functional class","Haemodynamic instability or systemic hypotension"],"prognosis":"Once uniformly fatal within a few years, prognosis has improved substantially with modern risk-based combination therapy. Nonetheless PAH remains a progressive, incurable disease short of lung transplantation, and outcome depends heavily on right ventricular function and treatment response.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing PAH on echocardiography alone without confirmatory right heart catheterisation.","Missing an elevated wedge pressure and mislabelling Group 2 (left heart) disease as PAH.","Prescribing calcium channel blockers to non-vasoreactive patients, which can be harmful.","Failing to screen at-risk groups such as those with systemic sclerosis, BMPR2 carriers and portal hypertension.","Overlooking pulmonary veno-occlusive disease or capillary haemangiomatosis, in whom vasodilators can precipitate pulmonary oedema."],"management":["High-dose calcium channel blockers reserved for the minority with a positive acute vasoreactivity test","Combination targeted therapy for non-vasoreactive disease using endothelin receptor antagonists, phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors or the guanylate cyclase stimulator riociguat, and prostacyclin pathway agents","Parenteral prostacyclin analogues for high-risk or rapidly progressive disease, escalated according to risk stratification","Risk stratification by functional class, exercise capacity, natriuretic peptides and haemodynamics at diagnosis and follow-up to guide treatment escalation","Supportive care with diuretics for right heart failure, oxygen for hypoxaemia, supervised rehabilitation and anticoagulation in selected idiopathic disease","Screen for and manage associated conditions such as connective tissue disease, HIV, portal hypertension and congenital heart disease, with annual screening in systemic sclerosis","Genetic counselling for heritable disease such as BMPR2 variants, with pregnancy-avoidance and vaccination advice","Referral to a specialist pulmonary hypertension centre and lung transplantation for refractory disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ERS/ESC Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Pulmonary Hypertension","Proceedings of the World Symposium on Pulmonary Hypertension","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_arteriovenous_malformation":{"name":"Pulmonary arteriovenous malformation","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pulmonary AVM","PAVM","pulmonary arteriovenous fistula","pulmonary arteriovenous aneurysm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Respiratory Medicine and Interventional Radiology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An abnormal direct connection between a pulmonary artery and vein that bypasses the capillary bed, creating a right-to-left shunt","The majority are associated with hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia (Osler-Weber-Rendu disease), so screening and family assessment are important","The right-to-left shunt causes hypoxaemia, and the loss of capillary filtration allows paradoxical embolism, predisposing to stroke and brain abscess","Classic features include dyspnoea, cyanosis, digital clubbing, and, when related to HHT, epistaxis and mucocutaneous telangiectasia","Platypnoea and orthodeoxia (breathlessness and desaturation on sitting or standing) can occur with basal lesions","Contrast (bubble) echocardiography is a sensitive screening test for right-to-left shunt, with CT pulmonary angiography defining the feeding vessels","Transcatheter embolisation of feeding arteries is the mainstay of treatment and reduces the risk of neurological complications and haemorrhage"],"pathophysiology":"A pulmonary arteriovenous malformation is a thin-walled abnormal vascular sac or channel connecting a feeding pulmonary artery directly to a draining pulmonary vein, bypassing the normal capillary network. This produces a right-to-left shunt that lowers arterial oxygenation and, critically, removes the capillary filter that normally traps small emboli and bacteria, so venous clots and septic material can pass directly into the systemic circulation and cause paradoxical stroke and cerebral abscess. Most lesions arise in the context of hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia, an autosomal dominant disorder of vascular development, and they tend to enlarge over time and during pregnancy. The fragile vessel walls can also rupture, causing haemoptysis or haemothorax.","additionalDifferentials":["Intracardiac right-to-left shunt (e.g. patent foramen ovale, atrial septal defect)","Hepatopulmonary syndrome","Solitary pulmonary nodule of other aetiology","Pulmonary varix","Other causes of hypoxaemia and clubbing","Vascular tumours of the lung"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast (agitated saline bubble) echocardiography to detect and grade right-to-left shunt","CT pulmonary angiography to characterise feeding and draining vessels","Shunt fraction estimation (e.g. by the 100% oxygen method or nuclear perfusion scan)","Screening and genetic assessment for hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia","Cerebral imaging when neurological symptoms or abscess are suspected","Assessment for anaemia and iron deficiency from associated bleeding"],"redFlags":["Neurological symptoms suggesting paradoxical stroke or cerebral abscess","Massive haemoptysis or haemothorax from lesion rupture","Progressive hypoxaemia and cyanosis","Enlarging lesions, especially during pregnancy, increasing complication risk"],"prognosis":"With embolisation of the feeding arteries, oxygenation improves and the risk of serious neurological and haemorrhagic complications falls substantially; untreated lesions carry a significant risk of stroke, brain abscess, and life-threatening haemorrhage, and lifelong surveillance is needed as lesions can recur or grow.","pitfalls":["Failing to consider a pulmonary AVM in unexplained hypoxaemia, clubbing, or paradoxical embolism","Not screening for hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia and missing familial disease","Omitting antibiotic prophylaxis advice for procedures given the risk of brain abscess","Overlooking small lesions that still permit paradoxical embolism despite minimal hypoxaemia"],"management":["Transcatheter embolisation of the feeding arteries as the definitive treatment to abolish the right-to-left shunt","Surgical resection for lesions unsuitable for embolisation","Antibiotic prophylaxis before dental and other bacteraemia-prone procedures to prevent cerebral abscess from paradoxical infection","Meticulous intravenous line air-filter and bubble precautions to prevent paradoxical air embolism","Screening and family assessment for hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia, with genetic counselling","Treat iron-deficiency anaemia from associated bleeding and manage recurrent epistaxis","Serial imaging surveillance for reperfusion or growth of lesions, including after pregnancy, with prompt neuroimaging and treatment for stroke or brain abscess"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","International guidelines on hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_artery_sling":{"name":"pulmonary artery sling","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["aberrant left pulmonary artery","PA sling"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The left pulmonary artery arises anomalously from the right pulmonary artery and courses between the trachea and oesophagus to reach the left lung, forming a sling around the distal trachea and right main bronchus.","It is the only vascular anomaly that passes between the trachea and oesophagus, giving a unique anterior indentation on the oesophagus at barium swallow.","It frequently coexists with complete (O-shaped) tracheal cartilage rings and long-segment congenital tracheal stenosis, the so-called ring-sling complex.","Infants typically present early with stridor, wheeze, recurrent respiratory infection and respiratory distress from airway compression.","Because associated tracheal stenosis often dominates the clinical picture, airway assessment is as important as defining the vascular anatomy.","CT or MR angiography demonstrates the aberrant vessel, and bronchoscopy is essential to evaluate the trachea for complete rings and the level and length of stenosis.","Surgical treatment involves division and re-implantation of the left pulmonary artery onto the main pulmonary artery, typically on cardiopulmonary bypass.","Coexisting significant tracheal stenosis is addressed concurrently, commonly by slide tracheoplasty."],"pathophysiology":"The left pulmonary artery normally develops from the left sixth aortic arch; in a pulmonary artery sling it fails to form from this precursor and instead arises from the right pulmonary artery, so that to reach the left hilum it must pass over the right main bronchus and course posteriorly between the trachea and oesophagus. This aberrant vessel forms a partial sling that compresses the distal trachea and right main bronchus anteriorly and, lying behind the trachea, indents the oesophagus anteriorly. The developmental disturbance is frequently accompanied by intrinsic tracheal maldevelopment with complete cartilage rings and diffuse narrowing, compounding the extrinsic compression and producing severe fixed airway obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Complete vascular rings such as double aortic arch or right arch with aberrant subclavian","Innominate artery compression syndrome","Primary tracheomalacia or bronchomalacia","Congenital tracheal stenosis without a sling","Bronchiolitis or recurrent viral wheeze","Foreign body aspiration","Mediastinal mass compressing the airway"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT angiography of the thorax","MR angiography","Transthoracic echocardiography to delineate the anomalous origin and associated cardiac lesions","Flexible and rigid bronchoscopy to assess complete rings and stenosis","Contrast oesophagogram showing an anterior oesophageal indentation","Chest radiograph noting asymmetric aeration, a low right hilum and air trapping","Ventilation-perfusion or pulmonary function assessment in older children"],"redFlags":["Severe stridor with respiratory distress or apnoeic episodes in infancy","Cyanotic spells or acute deterioration","Recurrent or lobar pneumonia with persistent air trapping","Difficult intubation or inability to pass beyond a narrowed trachea"],"prognosis":"With modern surgical re-implantation and, where needed, tracheal reconstruction, outcomes have improved substantially, though prognosis is worse with extensive long-segment tracheal stenosis or associated cardiac anomalies.","pitfalls":["Focusing on the vascular anomaly and failing to assess the trachea for complete rings and stenosis.","Misinterpreting the anterior oesophageal indentation, which is unique to this lesion.","Delayed diagnosis through mislabelling as asthma or recurrent bronchiolitis.","Not planning simultaneous airway reconstruction when significant stenosis is present.","Assuming that vessel re-implantation alone will relieve fixed intrinsic tracheal narrowing."],"management":["Surgical division and re-implantation of the anomalous left pulmonary artery onto the main pulmonary artery to relieve the tracheobronchial sling","Concurrent tracheal reconstruction such as slide tracheoplasty for associated long-segment stenosis with complete cartilage rings","Airway stabilisation and respiratory support for the compromised infant before definitive repair","Bronchoscopic assessment of the trachea for complete rings and the level and length of stenosis to plan intervention","Echocardiography and cross-sectional angiography to define the vascular anatomy and detect associated cardiac lesions","Postoperative airway surveillance for residual stenosis, granulation or restenosis, with bronchoscopic intervention as needed","Multidisciplinary cardiothoracic, airway and paediatric intensive care management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","Anderson's Paediatric Cardiology","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_atresia":{"name":"Pulmonary atresia","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pulmonary atresia with intact ventricular septum","pulmonary atresia with ventricular septal defect"],"chapter":null,"source":"Congenital heart disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Complete obstruction of the right ventricular outflow to the pulmonary arteries, so there is no antegrade pulmonary blood flow","It occurs with an intact ventricular septum or with a ventricular septal defect, which differ in physiology and management","Pulmonary blood flow is duct-dependent, so neonates become profoundly cyanotic as the duct closes","In the intact-septum form the right ventricle is often hypoplastic and may have coronary sinusoids or right-ventricle-dependent coronary circulation","The form with a ventricular septal defect resembles severe tetralogy of Fallot and may have major aortopulmonary collaterals","Prostaglandin to maintain the duct is the immediate priority, followed by staged surgical or catheter intervention"],"pathophysiology":"In pulmonary atresia there is no patent pathway from the right ventricle to the pulmonary arteries, so pulmonary perfusion depends on the arterial duct or on aortopulmonary collateral arteries. With an intact ventricular septum, blood cannot leave the right ventricle, which becomes hypertensive and often hypoplastic, sometimes maintaining fistulous connections (sinusoids) to the coronary arteries; closure of the duct causes life-threatening hypoxaemia. With a ventricular septal defect, the right ventricle decompresses through the defect and pulmonary supply comes from the duct or collaterals, giving a spectrum that overlaps with severe tetralogy of Fallot.","additionalDifferentials":["Critical pulmonary stenosis","Tetralogy of Fallot","Tricuspid atresia","Other duct-dependent pulmonary circulation lesions","Ebstein anomaly with functional pulmonary atresia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography defining outflow obstruction and ventricular size","Assessment for coronary sinusoids and right-ventricle-dependent coronary circulation","Imaging of pulmonary arteries and any aortopulmonary collaterals","Pre- and post-ductal saturations","Cardiac catheterisation for detailed anatomy"],"redFlags":["Profound cyanosis as the duct closes","Right-ventricle-dependent coronary circulation (contraindicates decompression)","Inadequate or discontinuous pulmonary arteries","Severe hypoxaemia despite prostaglandin"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the subtype and pulmonary artery and right ventricular anatomy; many children require staged intervention, achieving biventricular or single-ventricle circulations with variable long-term results.","pitfalls":["Failing to start prostaglandin promptly","Decompressing a right ventricle that supplies the coronary circulation, causing ischaemia","Underestimating collateral-dependent pulmonary supply","Confusing the two subtypes, which need different strategies"],"management":["Immediate prostaglandin E infusion to maintain ductal patency and preserve pulmonary blood flow in the duct-dependent neonate","Balloon atrial septostomy or ductal stenting as early palliation where indicated","Staged surgical intervention tailored to anatomy, including systemic-to-pulmonary shunt, right ventricular outflow reconstruction, or unifocalisation of major aortopulmonary collaterals","Assess for right-ventricle-dependent coronary circulation and coronary sinusoids in the intact-septum form, since these contraindicate right ventricular decompression and direct single-ventricle pathways","Plan toward biventricular, one-and-a-half ventricle or single-ventricle Fontan palliation according to right ventricular size and pulmonary artery anatomy","Neonatal intensive care support with correction of hypoxaemia, acidosis and heart failure","Lifelong congenital cardiology follow-up for arrhythmia, conduit or shunt failure and reintervention, with family counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Congenital heart disease references (standard reference)","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_barotrauma":{"name":"Pulmonary barotrauma","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pulmonary overinflation syndrome","burst lung"],"chapter":null,"source":"Diving and hyperbaric medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Overexpansion and rupture of lung tissue caused by expanding gas that cannot escape, classically during ascent from a dive","Breath-holding during ascent or air trapping from airway disease markedly increases the risk","Consequences include pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum, surgical emphysema and arterial gas embolism","It can also occur with positive-pressure mechanical ventilation at high airway pressures","Presentation ranges from chest pain and breathlessness to catastrophic arterial gas embolism","Management targets the specific complication, with recompression for gas embolism and chest decompression for tension pneumothorax"],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary barotrauma results from Boyle's law: as ambient pressure falls during ascent, gas in the lungs expands, and if it cannot be exhaled the alveoli overdistend and rupture. Escaping gas tracks into the pleural space (pneumothorax), the mediastinum and soft tissues (pneumomediastinum and surgical emphysema), or into the pulmonary veins to cause arterial gas embolism. Air trapping from breath-holding, bronchospasm or bullous disease localises the overpressure and predisposes to rupture.","additionalDifferentials":["Decompression sickness","Spontaneous pneumothorax of another cause","Acute coronary syndrome","Immersion pulmonary oedema","Asthma exacerbation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest imaging for pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum and surgical emphysema","Assessment for arterial gas embolism, which takes management priority","Oxygenation and ventilation assessment","Evaluation of underlying airway disease as a risk factor","Monitoring for tension physiology"],"redFlags":["Neurological symptoms indicating arterial gas embolism","Signs of tension pneumothorax","Rapidly worsening respiratory distress","Haemodynamic instability"],"prognosis":"Isolated small air leaks often resolve with supportive care, but arterial gas embolism or tension pneumothorax can be life-threatening; prompt treatment of complications determines outcome.","pitfalls":["Overlooking arterial gas embolism while focusing on a pneumothorax","Missing air trapping from undiagnosed airway disease as the cause","Delaying chest decompression in tension pneumothorax","Allowing a diver with lung bullae to continue diving"],"management":["High-flow, high-concentration oxygen to accelerate resorption of extra-alveolar gas and treat hypoxaemia","Urgent recompression with hyperbaric oxygen for arterial gas embolism, which takes management priority","Needle decompression and intercostal drainage for pneumothorax, urgently when there is tension physiology","Conservative observation for small, stable pneumomediastinum and surgical emphysema","Supportive positioning and haemodynamic support for arterial gas embolism alongside treatment of the precipitating event","On mechanical ventilation, adopt lung-protective low-pressure strategies to prevent and limit barotrauma","Assess diving fitness and treat underlying airway or bullous disease before any return to diving, escalating to critical care and diving medicine specialists for gas embolism or tension pneumothorax"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Diving and hyperbaric medicine references (standard reference)","Respiratory medicine reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_capillaritis":{"name":"Pulmonary capillaritis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Alveolar capillaritis","Pauci-immune pulmonary capillaritis","Isolated pulmonary capillaritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Respiratory medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pulmonary capillaritis is the commonest histological pattern underlying immune-mediated diffuse alveolar haemorrhage, defined by neutrophilic infiltration and fibrinoid necrosis of the alveolar septal capillaries.","The classic triad is haemoptysis, falling haemoglobin and diffuse alveolar (ground-glass) infiltrates, but haemoptysis is absent in a substantial minority, so it should not be relied on to exclude the diagnosis.","Sequential bronchoalveolar lavage returning progressively bloodier aliquots confirms alveolar haemorrhage even when the patient is not coughing blood.","Haemosiderin-laden macrophages on lavage indicate that bleeding has been present for some duration rather than being purely acute.","There is a strong association with ANCA-associated vasculitis (especially microscopic polyangiitis and granulomatosis with polyangiitis) and with anti-glomerular basement membrane disease, so ANCA, anti-GBM, ANA and complement should be checked urgently.","Gas transfer (DLCO/KCO) is characteristically elevated during active haemorrhage because intra-alveolar blood avidly takes up inhaled carbon monoxide.","It must be separated from bland alveolar haemorrhage (no capillaritis, e.g. mitral valve disease or coagulopathy) and from diffuse alveolar damage, as management differs.","Always screen for coexisting glomerulonephritis, because a pulmonary-renal syndrome dramatically changes urgency and treatment."],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary capillaritis is a small-vessel vasculitis of the alveolar septa in which neutrophils infiltrate the interstitium and undergo leukocytoclasis (nuclear dust), accompanied by fibrinoid necrosis of the capillary walls and fibrin thrombi. Disruption of the alveolar-capillary basement membrane allows red cells and fibrin to flood the alveolar spaces, producing diffuse alveolar haemorrhage. It is most often immune-mediated: ANCA-associated vasculitides (microscopic polyangiitis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, frequently with myeloperoxidase or proteinase-3 antibodies), anti-glomerular basement membrane (Goodpasture) disease, systemic lupus erythematosus and other connective tissue diseases, IgA vasculitis, the antiphospholipid syndrome, and drug-induced forms (for example propylthiouracil and hydralazine). A pauci-immune isolated (idiopathic) variant occurs without systemic disease. Neutrophil activation, complement engagement and endothelial injury drive the capillary destruction, and repeated episodes can lead to interstitial fibrosis and haemosiderosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Bland diffuse alveolar haemorrhage (mitral stenosis, coagulopathy or anticoagulation) without capillaritis","Diffuse alveolar damage with haemorrhage (acute respiratory distress syndrome)","Idiopathic pulmonary haemosiderosis","Haemorrhagic infective pneumonia (e.g. invasive aspergillosis, leptospirosis, severe influenza)","Pulmonary embolism with infarction","Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease","Intrathoracic malignancy causing haemorrhage (e.g. angiosarcoma, Kaposi sarcoma)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sequential bronchoalveolar lavage showing increasingly haemorrhagic returns and haemosiderin-laden macrophages","Autoimmune serology: ANCA (myeloperoxidase and proteinase-3), anti-GBM antibody, ANA and dsDNA, complement, antiphospholipid antibodies","Urinalysis with microscopy and renal function to detect an accompanying glomerulonephritis","High-resolution CT of the chest (bilateral ground-glass opacification and consolidation)","Full blood count for falling haemoglobin and a coagulation screen","Gas transfer measurement, which is typically raised in acute alveolar haemorrhage","Surgical (video-assisted thoracoscopic) lung biopsy when the diagnosis is unclear, to confirm capillaritis versus bland haemorrhage"],"redFlags":["Rapidly falling haemoglobin with worsening hypoxaemia, signalling catastrophic alveolar haemorrhage","Concurrent acute kidney injury or an active urinary sediment, indicating a pulmonary-renal syndrome","Massive haemoptysis","Escalating oxygen or ventilatory requirement"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the underlying cause and on how quickly immunosuppression is started; isolated pauci-immune capillaritis and treated ANCA-associated disease often respond well, but recurrent haemorrhage or progression to respiratory failure or end-stage renal disease carries appreciable mortality.","pitfalls":["Assuming that the absence of haemoptysis excludes alveolar haemorrhage, when infiltrates plus anaemia should already raise suspicion.","Attributing bilateral infiltrates to infection or fluid overload and thereby delaying serology and immunosuppression.","Relying on a small transbronchial biopsy, which is usually inadequate to confirm or exclude capillaritis.","Failing to screen the kidneys and missing a treatable pulmonary-renal syndrome.","Overlooking a drug trigger such as propylthiouracil or hydralazine."],"management":["Urgent pulsed corticosteroids to control immune-mediated diffuse alveolar haemorrhage","Induction immunosuppression with cyclophosphamide or rituximab for ANCA-associated and other severe capillaritis","Plasma exchange for anti-glomerular basement membrane disease and for severe or refractory alveolar haemorrhage with renal involvement","Supportive care for diffuse alveolar haemorrhage with oxygen, transfusion, correction of coagulopathy and mechanical ventilation for respiratory failure","Identify the underlying cause with targeted serology including ANCA, anti-GBM, ANA, complement and antiphospholipid antibodies, and treat accordingly","Concurrent management of associated glomerulonephritis with nephrology input and renal replacement where needed","Maintenance immunosuppression with monitoring for relapse and treatment-related infection, including opportunistic infection prophylaxis, within a multidisciplinary respiratory, rheumatology and nephrology team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders","ATS/ERS statements on diffuse alveolar haemorrhage and the pulmonary vasculitides"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_capillary_haemangiomatosis":{"name":"pulmonary capillary haemangiomatosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCH","capillary haemangiomatosis of the lung"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare pulmonary vascular disease classified within WHO Group 1 prime alongside pulmonary veno-occlusive disease (PVOD), with which it shares clinical, genetic and histological overlap along a disease continuum.","Biallelic (recessive) loss-of-function mutations in EIF2AK4 (encoding GCN2) underlie heritable forms and are shared with PVOD, supporting the view that the two are variants of one entity.","Histology shows proliferation of thin-walled capillaries infiltrating alveolar septa, bronchovascular bundles, pleura and the walls of pulmonary veins and arteries.","Presents like idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension (progressive exertional dyspnoea, right heart failure) but with a disproportionately low DLCO and marked hypoxaemia.","Haemoptysis and occult alveolar haemorrhage (haemosiderin-laden macrophages on lavage) are clues that help separate it from idiopathic PAH.","HRCT typically shows diffuse or centrilobular ground-glass nodules, smooth interlobular septal thickening and mediastinal lymphadenopathy.","Life-threatening pulmonary oedema may be precipitated by PAH-targeted vasodilators, especially prostacyclins, a hallmark shared with PVOD.","Lung transplantation is the only definitive therapy, so early referral to a transplant centre is essential."],"pathophysiology":"PCH is characterised by abnormal proliferation of pulmonary capillaries that invade and infiltrate the alveolar interstitium and, importantly, the walls of pulmonary veins and arteries as well as the pleura and airways. Capillary infiltration and obstruction at and beyond the post-capillary level produce venous congestion and pulmonary hypertension with a venous-obstructive component, explaining the overlap with PVOD. Biallelic EIF2AK4 mutations impair the integrated stress response and are thought to predispose to dysregulated angioproliferation. The remodelled capillary bed raises pulmonary vascular resistance, and because the obstruction lies at or beyond the capillary level, potent arterial vasodilators can flood an unprotected downstream bed and precipitate acute pulmonary oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension","Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension","Pulmonary hypertension due to left heart disease","Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage syndromes","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis or other diffuse interstitial lung disease","Lymphangitis carcinomatosa or metastatic pulmonary calcinosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Right heart catheterisation (wedge pressure often normal despite venous congestion)","High-resolution CT of the thorax","Full pulmonary function testing with DLCO (markedly reduced)","EIF2AK4 genetic testing","Bronchoalveolar lavage for haemosiderin-laden macrophages","Transthoracic echocardiography","Surgical lung biopsy (rarely performed owing to bleeding risk; diagnosis is often clinico-radiological)"],"redFlags":["Acute pulmonary oedema after starting PAH-targeted vasodilators","Rapidly progressive hypoxaemic respiratory failure","Massive haemoptysis","Decompensated right heart failure with syncope or hypotension"],"prognosis":"The course is progressive and generally poor, with median survival of only a few years from diagnosis without transplantation. Lung transplantation is potentially curative and offers the best long-term outcome.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosis as idiopathic PAH leading to aggressive vasodilator therapy and fatal pulmonary oedema.","Attributing the low DLCO and ground-glass changes to interstitial lung disease rather than a pulmonary vascular cause.","Delaying transplant referral because pulmonary pressures initially appear to respond to therapy.","Assuming a normal pulmonary capillary wedge pressure excludes a post-capillary or venous component."],"management":["Early referral for lung transplantation, the only potentially curative treatment, given the progressive course","Avoid, or use only under expert supervision, targeted pulmonary vasodilators, as they can flood the unprotected distal bed and precipitate fatal pulmonary oedema","Supportive management with diuretics for right heart failure and supplemental oxygen for hypoxaemia","Use anticoagulation and any pulmonary hypertension-directed measures only with specialist guidance and close monitoring for pulmonary oedema","Genetic testing and counselling for biallelic EIF2AK4 mutations, with screening of at-risk relatives","Right heart catheterisation and serial haemodynamic and functional assessment to characterise and monitor the disease","Manage within a specialist pulmonary vascular and transplant centre"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ERS/ESC Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Pulmonary Hypertension","Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_capillary_hemangiomatosis":{"name":"Pulmonary capillary haemangiomatosis","system":"Respiratory / Pulmonary vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCH","pulmonary capillary haemangiomatosis","EIF2AK4-associated pulmonary vascular disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC/ERS Guidelines for Pulmonary Hypertension / Nunn's Applied Respiratory Physiology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension grouped with pulmonary veno-occlusive disease because of overlapping histology, clinical behaviour, and shared biallelic EIF2AK4 mutations","Characterised by capillary proliferation within the alveolar walls that infiltrates and obstructs the pulmonary microvasculature","Presents with progressive exertional breathlessness and features of pulmonary hypertension, often more severe than the modest catheterisation pressures suggest","High-resolution CT typically shows diffuse centrilobular ground-glass nodules, septal thickening, and mediastinal lymphadenopathy","The pulmonary artery wedge pressure is characteristically normal despite radiological signs suggestive of post-capillary congestion, reflecting disease distal to the true measuring point","Targeted pulmonary vasodilators must be used with great caution or avoided, as they can precipitate severe and sometimes fatal pulmonary oedema","Definitive treatment is lung transplantation, and early referral is essential given the poor prognosis"],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary capillary haemangiomatosis is characterised by uncontrolled proliferation of thin-walled capillaries that infiltrate the alveolar septa, bronchovascular bundles, pleura, and the walls of pulmonary veins and arteries. This proliferating capillary network encroaches on and obstructs the microvascular bed, raising pulmonary vascular resistance and producing pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension. Because the proliferating vessels compress and involve the post-capillary venules, the disorder shares physiology and imaging features with pulmonary veno-occlusive disease, and many familial cases carry biallelic loss-of-function mutations in EIF2AK4. The key clinical hazard is that the obstruction lies downstream of the arterioles, so pulmonary vasodilators increase flow into a fixed distal obstruction and can flood the capillary bed, precipitating acute pulmonary oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease","Idiopathic or heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension","Pulmonary hypertension due to left heart disease (group 2)","Diffuse parenchymal lung disease with pulmonary hypertension","Pulmonary tumour thrombotic microangiopathy","Metastatic or lymphangitic malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution CT of the chest for centrilobular ground-glass nodules, septal lines, and lymphadenopathy","Right heart catheterisation showing pre-capillary haemodynamics with a normal wedge pressure","Genetic testing for biallelic EIF2AK4 mutations","Pulmonary function tests, characteristically with a markedly reduced diffusing capacity","Arterial blood gases and exercise oxygen assessment","Ventilation-perfusion scan to exclude chronic thromboembolic disease"],"redFlags":["Development of pulmonary oedema after starting targeted pulmonary vasodilator therapy","Severe or rapidly progressive hypoxaemia","Signs of decompensated right heart failure","Haemoptysis reflecting capillary fragility"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is poor, with progressive pulmonary hypertension and limited response to medical therapy, and death from right heart failure or vasodilator-precipitated pulmonary oedema. Lung transplantation is the only definitive treatment and offers the best chance of survival, so early referral is critical.","pitfalls":["Prescribing pulmonary vasodilators without recognising the risk of precipitating fatal pulmonary oedema","Misdiagnosing the disease as idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension and treating it accordingly","Overlooking the diagnostic clues of a very low diffusing capacity and characteristic CT changes","Delaying transplant referral because catheterisation pressures appear only modestly raised","Failing to consider a shared spectrum with pulmonary veno-occlusive disease and the EIF2AK4 genotype"],"management":["Refer early for lung transplantation, which is the only definitive treatment given the poor progressive course","Use targeted pulmonary vasodilators with great caution or avoid them, as increasing flow into the fixed distal obstruction can precipitate severe pulmonary oedema","Supportive treatment with diuretics for right heart failure and oxygen for hypoxaemia","Undertake any vasodilator trial only in an expert centre with close monitoring for pulmonary oedema","Genetic testing and counselling for biallelic EIF2AK4 mutations, with family screening","Right heart catheterisation and serial assessment to define pre-capillary haemodynamics and track progression","Coordinate care in a specialist pulmonary hypertension and transplant centre"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC/ERS Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Pulmonary Hypertension","Nunn's Applied Respiratory Physiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_fibrosis":{"name":"Pulmonary fibrosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IPF","idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","interstitial lung disease","fibrosing ILD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with progressive exertional breathlessness and a persistent dry cough, often over months to years","Fine bibasal end-inspiratory crackles (often described as Velcro-like) and finger clubbing are characteristic examination findings","High-resolution CT showing a usual interstitial pneumonia pattern, with subpleural and basal reticulation, traction bronchiectasis, and honeycombing, is central to diagnosis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is a diagnosis of exclusion requiring careful evaluation for connective tissue disease, drug toxicity, and environmental or occupational exposures","Pulmonary function tests typically show a restrictive pattern with reduced lung volumes and impaired gas transfer (low DLCO)","Multidisciplinary discussion combining clinical, radiological, and where needed histological data improves diagnostic accuracy","Antifibrotic therapy can slow the decline in lung function in IPF; supportive measures include oxygen, pulmonary rehabilitation, and vaccination","Acute exacerbations cause rapid deterioration and carry high mortality; lung transplantation should be considered in suitable candidates"],"pathophysiology":"Repeated microinjury to the alveolar epithelium in a susceptible (often ageing, genetically predisposed) individual triggers aberrant wound-healing rather than normal repair. Activated fibroblasts and myofibroblasts accumulate in fibroblastic foci and deposit excess extracellular matrix, progressively distorting and stiffening the lung architecture. The result is loss of functional alveolar units, honeycomb cystic change, impaired gas exchange, and reduced compliance, producing a restrictive physiology with worsening hypoxaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Connective tissue disease-associated interstitial lung disease","Chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Pneumoconioses such as asbestosis or silicosis","Drug-induced or radiation-induced lung injury","Sarcoidosis with fibrosis","Chronic left heart failure with interstitial oedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution CT of the chest to assess for a UIP pattern and distribution of fibrosis","Full pulmonary function testing including spirometry, lung volumes, and diffusing capacity","Serological screen for connective tissue disease and, where indicated, hypersensitivity precipitins","Six-minute walk test with oximetry to assess functional impairment and desaturation","Surgical or transbronchial lung biopsy in selected cases where imaging is non-diagnostic"],"redFlags":["Rapid worsening of dyspnoea with new diffuse ground-glass change suggesting an acute exacerbation","Resting hypoxaemia or significant desaturation on exertion","Signs of pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure","Features suggesting an underlying treatable systemic disease that alter management"],"prognosis":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is generally progressive with a poor prognosis and median survival of only a few years from diagnosis, though the course is variable; antifibrotic drugs slow decline and transplantation can prolong survival in eligible patients, while acute exacerbations markedly worsen outcome.","pitfalls":["Labelling fibrosis as idiopathic without excluding occupational, drug, and connective tissue causes","Using corticosteroids or immunosuppression indiscriminately in IPF, where they may be harmful","Missing coexisting emphysema, which can mask spirometric restriction","Delaying transplant referral until the patient is too deconditioned to be a candidate"],"management":["Antifibrotic therapy with pirfenidone or nintedanib to slow decline in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and other progressive fibrosing phenotypes","Treat identifiable causes, using immunosuppression for connective tissue disease-related fibrosis, antigen avoidance in hypersensitivity pneumonitis, and withdrawal of culprit drugs","Long-term oxygen therapy for hypoxaemia and pulmonary rehabilitation to improve function","Symptom control for breathlessness and cough with early integration of palliative care","Manage comorbidities including gastro-oesophageal reflux, pulmonary hypertension and obstructive sleep apnoea, and keep vaccinations up to date","Serial pulmonary function and diffusing capacity with multidisciplinary review to monitor progression","Recognise and treat acute exacerbations, which markedly worsen prognosis, with supportive care and corticosteroids, and refer eligible patients for lung transplantation assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_hypertension":{"name":"Pulmonary Hypertension","system":"Cardiology / Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PH","PAH","pulmonary arterial hypertension"],"chapter":294,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2185","clinicalPearls":["PH is diagnosed when mean pulmonary artery pressure (mPAP) is >20 mmHg by right heart catheterisation; this threshold was lowered from the older >=25 mmHg cut-off to emphasise earlier disease detection (p.2185, 2191).","The most common causes of PH are left heart disease or primary lung disease; pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is an uncommon but distinct subtype with an obliterative arteriopathy (p.2185).","Most patients present with dyspnoea and/or fatigue; oedema, chest pain, presyncope and syncope are less common and signal more advanced disease (p.2188).","The current WSPH classification (last revised 2018, Sixth World Symposium) defines five groups: 1 = PAH; 2 = PH due to left heart disease; 3 = PH due to chronic lung disease/hypoxia or sleep-disordered breathing; 4 = chronic thromboembolic PH (CTEPH); 5 = miscellaneous/multifactorial (p.2191).","Echocardiography with agitated-saline (bubble) study is the most important initial screening test; estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure >35 mmHg, a notched continuous-wave Doppler waveform, or a hypertrophied/dilated right ventricle supports the diagnosis (p.2188).","Hemodynamic subtypes by RHC: precapillary PH = PAWP/LVEDP <=15 mmHg with PVR >2.0 Wood units; isolated postcapillary PH = PAWP >15 mmHg with PVR <=2.0 WU; combined pre-/postcapillary PH = mPAP >20 mmHg with PAWP >15 mmHg and PVR >2.0 WU (p.2191).","In PAH, an isolated reduction in diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO) on pulmonary function testing is a classic finding (p.2189).","BNP and NT-proBNP correlate with right ventricular dysfunction, hemodynamic severity and functional status in PAH, and NT-proBNP can serve as a biomarker of treatment response once a baseline is established (p.2191).","Vasoreactivity testing (with short-acting agents such as inhaled nitric oxide or inhaled epoprostenol) is reserved for idiopathic or hereditary PAH; a positive response is a fall in mPAP by >=10 mmHg to an absolute level <=40 mmHg without a fall in cardiac output, seen in <5% of patients but carrying a particularly favourable prognosis (p.2191).","CTEPH is curable in many patients by surgical pulmonary endarterectomy but is widely underdiagnosed; ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) scanning remains the primary screening test and should be considered in any PH of unclear cause (p.2190-2191)."],"pathophysiology":"PAH arises from apoptosis resistance, cell proliferation, dysregulated metabolism and increased oxidant stress in pulmonary vascular cells, pericytes and adventitial fibroblasts, producing hypertrophic, fibrotic and plexogenic remodelling of distal (small) pulmonary arterioles with extracellular-matrix expansion, reduced vascular compliance and in-situ thrombosis. This raises pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) and mean pulmonary artery pressure (mPAP). To preserve cardiac output against the elevated right-ventricular afterload, RV work increases; sustained overload depletes myocardial energy and causes right ventricular-pulmonary arterial uncoupling, and in end-stage disease cardiac output and mPAP both fall. The most common germline driver of hereditary PAH is a BMPR2 variant (a TGF-beta superfamily receptor) with variable penetrance (as low as ~20%); other drivers include TBX4 and SOX17, and EIF2AK4 for pulmonary veno-occlusive disease. In group 2 PH the hallmark is elevated left atrial pressure causing pulmonary venous hypertension, venule sclerosis and secondary arteriolar remodelling. (p.2185-2192)","additionalDifferentials":["Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF)","Left ventricular systolic dysfunction","Mitral or aortic valvular disease","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) / emphysema","Interstitial / restrictive lung disease","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH)","Connective tissue disease, especially systemic sclerosis-associated PAH","Portopulmonary hypertension","HIV-associated PAH","Congenital heart disease with intracardiac shunt / Eisenmenger's syndrome","Hepatopulmonary syndrome (distinct: causes intrapulmonary shunting, not pulmonary vascular remodelling)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection (HIV-PAH is an important cause of mortality in HIV-infected patients)","Schistosomiasis (affects >230 million worldwide, ~5% develop PAH; among the most common causes of PAH globally)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Asthma (overlapping nonspecific dyspnoea)","Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction","Physical deconditioning","Obstructive sleep apnoea / obesity-hypoventilation syndrome","Sarcoidosis with pulmonary vascular involvement"],"additionalInvestigations":["Right heart catheterisation (RHC) - gold standard for diagnosis and severity assessment; required mPAP >20 mmHg","Transthoracic echocardiography with agitated-saline (bubble) study - most important initial screening test","Electrocardiography - may show right ventricular hypertrophy or strain in advanced disease","Chest roentgenography - enlargement of central pulmonary arteries and obliteration of the retrosternal space","Pulmonary function testing including DLCO (isolated low DLCO classic for PAH)","High-resolution CT of the chest (vascular pruning, distinguishing ILD/emphysema/overlap; signs of venous congestion)","Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) scan - primary screen for CTEPH","CT pulmonary angiography and digital subtraction pulmonary angiography (definitive for CTEPH; shows webbing, stricture, tapering)","6-minute walk distance (6-MWD) for functional capacity and prognosis","Cardiopulmonary exercise testing (CPET); peak VO2 <15 mL/kg/min is prognostic but not diagnostic","Nocturnal oximetry screening in all patients (nocturnal desaturation common even without sleep-disordered breathing)","BNP / NT-proBNP","Serology: HIV test (when indicated), antinuclear antibodies, rheumatoid factor, anti-Scl-70; liver function and hepatitis serology; methamphetamine screening where relevant","Vasoreactivity testing (idiopathic/hereditary PAH only)"],"redFlags":["Syncope or presyncope (signals advanced disease)","Signs of right ventricular failure: elevated jugular venous pressure, lower-extremity oedema, ascites","Rapid progression of symptoms with severely reduced exercise capacity","Decompensated right heart failure (the major cause of mortality if untreated)","Significant decline in exercise tolerance from baseline even when CPET appears reassuring (e.g. in athletes - may indicate early PAH or subacute PE)"],"prognosis":"If left untreated, PH carries a high mortality rate, largely owing to decompensated right heart failure. Idiopathic PAH is progressive and leads to right heart failure and early mortality; in left-sided heart failure or HFpEF even mildly elevated mPAP carries adverse outcome. Among IPAH, prevalence favours women ~3:1 but hemodynamics are more severe and prognosis less favourable in men. Hereditary BMPR2 PAH presents younger with more severe hemodynamics and greater clinical risk (conversion ~2.3%/year in carriers). Vasoreactive responders (<5%) have a particularly favourable prognosis. Scleroderma-associated PAH and HIV-PAH carry especially poor prognoses; portopulmonary hypertension is a marker of adverse post-liver-transplant outcome (up to 100% mortality reported with mPAP >=50 mmHg in one study). Peak VO2 <15 mL/kg/min is prognostic. (p.2185, 2191-2192)","pitfalls":["Right heart catheterisation - not echocardiography - is the diagnostic gold standard; echocardiography only screens, and an echo in which tricuspid regurgitation is simply not detected does NOT exclude PH (PH is found in one-third of such patients).","PH symptoms are nonspecific, insidious and overlap with asthma, HFpEF and deconditioning, so the diagnosis is frequently missed without a reasonable index of suspicion.","Do not assume that PH in patients with comorbid cardiopulmonary disease (e.g. ILD, mitral valve disease) is merely an extension of the underlying condition rather than a specific entity.","Patients with left heart disease aggressively diuresed before RHC may falsely appear to have an isolated precapillary (PAH-like) profile - diuresis can mask the true hemodynamic classification.","A low peak VO2 occurs across the heart-lung disease spectrum and is NOT diagnostic of PH per se.","Chronic thromboembolic disease (thrombotic remodelling with reduced exercise capacity but without PH) requires consideration in patients with prior PE and dyspnoea even when echocardiography is reassuring."],"management":["Classify by WHO group at a specialist centre, since treatment is group-specific and targeted PAH drugs can be harmful in left heart and lung disease","For Group 1 pulmonary arterial hypertension, use targeted therapy with endothelin receptor antagonists, phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors or guanylate cyclase stimulators and prostacyclin pathway agents, reserving calcium channel blockers for vasoreactive responders","For Group 2, optimise the underlying left heart disease rather than giving PAH-specific drugs","For Group 3, treat the underlying lung disease and hypoxaemia with long-term oxygen and address sleep-disordered breathing","For Group 4 chronic thromboembolic disease, provide lifelong anticoagulation with pulmonary endarterectomy, balloon pulmonary angioplasty or riociguat","Supportive care with diuretics for right heart failure, oxygen for hypoxaemia and pulmonary rehabilitation","Risk-stratify and reassess serially to guide escalation, refer to a specialist centre, and consider transplantation for refractory disease, with genetic counselling for heritable PAH such as BMPR2 variants"],"severityClassification":"World Symposium on Pulmonary Hypertension (WSPH) clinical classification (revised 2018) into 5 groups: Group 1 - pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH: idiopathic, heritable, drug/toxin-associated, or associated with connective tissue disease, HIV, portal hypertension, congenital heart disease, schistosomiasis; plus PVOD/PCH and persistent PH of the newborn); Group 2 - PH associated with left heart disease (HFpEF, HFrEF, valvular, congenital/acquired postcapillary); Group 3 - PH associated with lung disease and/or hypoxia (obstructive, restrictive, mixed, hypoventilation, high-altitude hypoxia, developmental); Group 4 - PH associated with pulmonary artery obstruction (CTEPH and other obstructions); Group 5 - PH with unclear/multifactorial mechanisms (haematological, systemic, metabolic, chronic renal failure +/- haemodialysis, pulmonary tumour thrombotic microangiopathy, fibrosing mediastinitis). Hemodynamic categorisation by RHC: precapillary, isolated postcapillary, and combined pre-/postcapillary PH. (Table 294-1, p.2191)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2185"],"crossLinks":["heart_failure","pe","ild","copd_exac_ni","systemic_sclerosis"]},"pulmonary_hypertension_left_heart":{"name":"Pulmonary hypertension due to left heart disease (group 2)","system":"Respiratory / Pulmonary vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["group 2 pulmonary hypertension","post-capillary pulmonary hypertension","pulmonary venous hypertension","PH-LHD"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC/ERS Guidelines for Pulmonary Hypertension / Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest cause of pulmonary hypertension overall, arising from left heart disease including heart failure with reduced or preserved ejection fraction and valvular disease","Defined haemodynamically as post-capillary, with a raised mean pulmonary artery pressure and an elevated pulmonary artery wedge pressure at right heart catheterisation","Distinguished from pre-capillary disease chiefly by the elevated wedge pressure, which reflects raised left-sided filling pressures transmitted backward","May be isolated post-capillary or combined post- and pre-capillary, the latter carrying a worse prognosis when a superimposed pulmonary vascular component develops","Clues favouring a left heart cause include older age, hypertension, atrial fibrillation, obesity, diabetes, left atrial enlargement, and preserved-ejection-fraction physiology","Treatment is directed at the underlying left heart disease with decongestion and risk-factor control, not at group 1 targeted vasodilators","Targeted pulmonary arterial hypertension therapy is generally not indicated and may cause harm through pulmonary oedema"],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary hypertension due to left heart disease is a post-capillary disorder driven by elevated left-sided filling pressures. Systolic or diastolic left ventricular dysfunction, or left-sided valvular disease, raises left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure, which is transmitted backward into the pulmonary capillaries and arteries, producing a passive rise in pulmonary artery pressure while the wedge pressure remains elevated. Over time, chronic pulmonary venous congestion can trigger vasoconstriction and structural remodelling of the pulmonary arteries, adding a pre-capillary component and producing combined post- and pre-capillary disease in which pulmonary vascular resistance also rises. This superimposed arteriopathy further loads the right ventricle and worsens outcome. Because the primary problem is upstream in the left heart, therapy that dilates the pulmonary arteries can increase transpulmonary flow into a congested left heart and precipitate pulmonary oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic or heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension","Pulmonary hypertension due to lung disease or hypoxia (group 3)","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension","Constrictive pericarditis or restrictive cardiomyopathy","High-output states raising pulmonary pressure","Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease mimicking post-capillary congestion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography to assess left ventricular function, valves, and left atrial size","Right heart catheterisation to document the elevated wedge pressure defining post-capillary disease","Fluid or exercise provocation at catheterisation to unmask occult preserved-ejection-fraction heart failure","Natriuretic peptides and assessment of volume status","Cardiac MRI or ischaemia evaluation where the left heart cause is unclear","Ventilation-perfusion scan and lung imaging to exclude thromboembolic and parenchymal contributors"],"redFlags":["Acute decompensation with pulmonary oedema and hypoxaemia","A disproportionately high pulmonary vascular resistance suggesting a superimposed pre-capillary component","Signs of low cardiac output or cardiogenic shock","Clinical deterioration after inappropriate use of group 1 targeted vasodilators"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is largely determined by the underlying left heart disease, and the development of pulmonary hypertension, particularly a combined post- and pre-capillary pattern, marks more advanced disease and a worse outlook. Optimising treatment of the left heart condition can lower pulmonary pressures, whereas an established pulmonary vascular component is less reversible.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying post-capillary disease as pulmonary arterial hypertension and prescribing group 1 targeted therapy","Relying on echocardiographic pressure estimates without catheterisation to determine the wedge pressure","Missing occult preserved-ejection-fraction heart failure by not performing provocation testing","Attributing all the pulmonary hypertension to the left heart when a significant pre-capillary component coexists","Overlooking treatable valvular disease as the driver of pulmonary venous hypertension"],"management":["Treat the underlying left heart disease as the mainstay: guideline-directed medical therapy for heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, and management of hypertension, valvular lesions and preserved-ejection-fraction physiology","Relieve pulmonary venous congestion with diuretic-based decongestion and dietary sodium and fluid moderation","Control contributing risk factors, including blood-pressure optimisation, rate or rhythm control and anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation, and management of obesity and diabetes","Avoid group 1 targeted pulmonary vasodilators, which can precipitate pulmonary oedema in post-capillary disease","Monitor with serial echocardiography, natriuretic peptides and functional assessment to track right ventricular load and congestion","Refer for valve repair or replacement where significant left-sided valvular disease drives the pulmonary hypertension","Escalate advanced or combined post- and pre-capillary disease to a specialist pulmonary hypertension and heart failure centre, considering advanced heart failure therapies or transplantation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC/ERS Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Pulmonary Hypertension","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_hypertension_lung_disease":{"name":"Pulmonary hypertension due to lung disease/hypoxia (group 3)","system":"Respiratory / Pulmonary vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["group 3 pulmonary hypertension","hypoxic pulmonary hypertension","cor pulmonale","PH-lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC/ERS Guidelines for Pulmonary Hypertension / Nunn's Applied Respiratory Physiology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Arises in chronic lung disease and hypoxaemic states, notably COPD, interstitial lung disease, combined pulmonary fibrosis and emphysema, and sleep-disordered breathing","Alveolar hypoxia is the central driver, causing hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction and, over time, vascular remodelling and destruction of the capillary bed","The pulmonary hypertension is usually mild to moderate and proportionate to the severity of the lung disease, so severe pressures out of proportion should prompt a search for another cause","Presents as worsening breathlessness, hypoxaemia, and eventually right heart failure (cor pulmonale) superimposed on the underlying respiratory condition","Long-term oxygen therapy for chronic hypoxaemia is the cornerstone of management, alongside optimal treatment of the underlying lung disease","Group 1 targeted pulmonary vasodilators are generally not recommended, as they can worsen ventilation-perfusion matching and gas exchange","A severe pulmonary vascular phenotype with only modest airflow or parenchymal impairment warrants referral to a specialist centre"],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary hypertension due to lung disease and hypoxia is driven principally by alveolar hypoxia, which provokes hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction as an adaptive mechanism to divert blood away from poorly ventilated lung. When hypoxia is chronic and widespread, this vasoconstriction becomes sustained and is accompanied by structural remodelling of the pulmonary arteries with medial hypertrophy and intimal thickening. In parenchymal diseases such as emphysema and fibrosis, destruction and distortion of the alveolar-capillary bed reduces the cross-sectional area of the pulmonary vasculature, further raising resistance. Acidosis and hypercapnia potentiate the vasoconstriction, and the chronically elevated afterload leads to right ventricular hypertrophy and eventual failure. The pulmonary hypertension is typically proportionate to the underlying disease, and a disproportionately severe pattern suggests an additional or alternative process.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary hypertension due to left heart disease (group 2)","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension","Idiopathic or heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension coexisting with lung disease","Sleep-disordered breathing and obesity hypoventilation","Connective tissue disease with combined lung and vascular involvement","Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pulmonary function tests including diffusing capacity to characterise the lung disease","High-resolution CT of the chest to define parenchymal and airway disease","Arterial blood gases and overnight oximetry or sleep study for hypoxaemia and hypoventilation","Transthoracic echocardiography to estimate pressures and assess right ventricular function","Right heart catheterisation when a severe or disproportionate pulmonary vascular phenotype is suspected","Ventilation-perfusion scan to exclude chronic thromboembolic disease"],"redFlags":["Pulmonary hypertension severe out of proportion to the degree of lung disease","Rapidly progressive right heart failure or worsening hypoxaemia","Syncope or presyncope on exertion","Deterioration in gas exchange after inappropriate use of pulmonary vasodilators"],"prognosis":"The presence of pulmonary hypertension marks more advanced lung disease and predicts reduced exercise capacity and survival, with a severe pulmonary vascular phenotype carrying the worst outlook. Correcting chronic hypoxaemia with oxygen and optimising the underlying respiratory disease can slow progression, but the pulmonary hypertension is rarely fully reversible.","pitfalls":["Treating mild proportionate pulmonary hypertension with group 1 targeted therapy and worsening gas exchange","Failing to investigate a disproportionately severe pattern for thromboembolic or arterial disease","Overlooking correctable nocturnal hypoxaemia or sleep-disordered breathing","Attributing all breathlessness to the airways or parenchyma and missing evolving cor pulmonale","Not referring a severe pulmonary vascular phenotype to a specialist pulmonary hypertension centre"],"management":["Optimise the underlying respiratory disease with appropriate inhaled bronchodilator and anti-inflammatory therapy for COPD, antifibrotic agents for interstitial lung disease, and continuous positive airway pressure for sleep-disordered breathing","Correct chronic hypoxaemia with long-term oxygen therapy, the cornerstone of management","Support with smoking cessation, pulmonary rehabilitation and immunisation against respiratory pathogens","Avoid group 1 targeted pulmonary vasodilators as a rule, since they can worsen ventilation-perfusion matching and gas exchange","Treat cor pulmonale and fluid overload with diuretic-based decongestion","Monitor with echocardiography, arterial blood gases and overnight oximetry to gauge severity and oxygenation","Refer a severe or disproportionate pulmonary vascular phenotype to a specialist pulmonary hypertension centre and consider lung transplantation assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC/ERS Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Pulmonary Hypertension","Nunn's Applied Respiratory Physiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_hypoplasia":{"name":"pulmonary hypoplasia","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lung hypoplasia","pulmonary hypodysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Avery's Diseases of the Newborn","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as incomplete development of the lung with a reduced number of airway generations, alveoli and pulmonary vessels, giving an abnormally low lung weight and volume for body size.","Primary (isolated) hypoplasia is rare; the majority of cases are secondary to an identifiable intrathoracic or extrathoracic cause.","Major secondary mechanisms include thoracic space restriction (congenital diaphragmatic hernia, effusion or hydrops, masses), oligohydramnios (renal agenesis or Potter sequence, prolonged rupture of membranes), thoracic cage constraint (skeletal dysplasias, neuromuscular disease) and reduced foetal breathing movements.","Normal lung growth requires adequate amniotic fluid volume, sufficient intrathoracic space and regular foetal breathing movements, particularly during the canalicular and saccular stages.","Affected neonates typically present with respiratory distress from birth, are difficult to ventilate and are prone to air leaks such as pneumothorax.","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn frequently coexists because the pulmonary vascular bed is both reduced in number and abnormally muscularised.","Antenatal predictors of severity in congenital diaphragmatic hernia include the lung-to-head ratio and the degree of liver herniation on ultrasound and foetal MRI.","Management is supportive with lung-protective ventilation, pulmonary vasodilators and treatment of the underlying cause, with extracorporeal support reserved for selected severe cases."],"pathophysiology":"Normal lung growth depends on sufficient intrathoracic space, an appropriate volume of amniotic and intrapulmonary fluid maintaining distending pressure, and regular foetal breathing movements; interference with any of these during the critical canalicular and saccular phases arrests branching morphogenesis and alveolarisation. Space-occupying lesions and effusions compress the developing lung, oligohydramnios removes the distending fluid pressure and allows lung liquid to drain, and thoracic or neuromuscular constraint prevents the mechanical stretch that drives growth. The result is a lung with fewer bronchial divisions, fewer and simplified alveoli, and a diminished, abnormally muscularised vascular bed that predisposes to hypoxaemia and pulmonary hypertension after birth.","additionalDifferentials":["Respiratory distress syndrome from surfactant deficiency","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia (both cause and differential)","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","Congenital pneumonia or sepsis","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Alveolar capillary dysplasia with misalignment of pulmonary veins","Congenital surfactant protein deficiencies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antenatal ultrasound with lung-to-head ratio and amniotic fluid assessment","Foetal MRI for lung volume estimation","Chest radiograph noting a small bell-shaped thorax and low lung volumes","Echocardiography to assess pulmonary hypertension and associated cardiac anomalies","Arterial blood gas and pre- and post-ductal saturation monitoring","Renal ultrasound and genetic or karyotype testing to identify the underlying cause","Post-mortem lung weight-to-body-weight ratio and radial alveolar count where applicable"],"redFlags":["Severe refractory hypoxaemia and hypercapnia unresponsive to escalating support","Suprasystemic pulmonary hypertension with right ventricular failure","Recurrent or tension pneumothorax","Inability to achieve adequate ventilation despite optimal settings, suggesting severe hypoplasia"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends chiefly on the severity of hypoplasia and its underlying cause: severe bilateral disease, as in Potter sequence, is frequently fatal, whereas milder secondary hypoplasia may improve with postnatal lung growth.","pitfalls":["Attributing neonatal respiratory failure solely to surfactant deficiency and missing underlying hypoplasia.","Underestimating the contribution of pulmonary hypertension to hypoxaemia.","Using aggressive ventilation that causes barotrauma in small, non-compliant lungs.","Failing to search for the underlying renal, skeletal or diaphragmatic cause once hypoplasia is recognised.","Providing over-optimistic counselling before severity has been fully assessed."],"management":["Provide gentle, lung-protective mechanical ventilation from birth to support gas exchange while minimising barotrauma and the high risk of air leak such as pneumothorax","Treat coexisting persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn with inhaled nitric oxide and other pulmonary vasodilators, with surfactant where indicated","Address the underlying cause, including surgical repair of congenital diaphragmatic hernia or drainage of a compressing effusion","Escalate refractory hypoxaemic respiratory failure to extracorporeal membrane oxygenation in suitable infants","Monitor pre- and post-ductal saturations and arterial blood gases, and support nutrition and fluid balance","Manage within a neonatal intensive care and paediatric surgical multidisciplinary team","Offer genetic counselling and family screening where an inherited cause such as renal agenesis or a skeletal dysplasia is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Avery's Diseases of the Newborn","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","Fetal and Neonatal Physiology (Polin)"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_langerhans_cell_histiocytosis":{"name":"Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLCH","Pulmonary histiocytosis X","Pulmonary eosinophilic granuloma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A smoking-related disease of young to middle-aged adults; almost all patients are current or former smokers.","Presents with cough and breathlessness, and up to a quarter present with spontaneous pneumothorax; constitutional symptoms may also occur.","The HRCT hallmark is a combination of nodules, which cavitate, and bizarrely shaped thick-walled cysts, with upper- and mid-zone predominance and sparing of the costophrenic angles.","Early disease is nodular and evolves to a predominantly cystic pattern over time.","Histology shows bronchiolocentric stellate nodules of Langerhans cells that are CD1a and S100 positive, with Birbeck granules on electron microscopy and admixed eosinophils.","BRAF V600E and other activating MAPK-pathway mutations are found in a substantial proportion, supporting a clonal neoplastic basis.","Smoking cessation is essential and can lead to regression; progressive disease may require systemic therapy.","Pulmonary hypertension is a recognised and prognostically important complication."],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis involves accumulation and proliferation of Langerhans-type dendritic cells that form bronchiolocentric granulomatous nodules, driven by tobacco smoke exposure in clonally susceptible individuals who frequently harbour activating MAPK-pathway mutations such as BRAF V600E. Nodules undergo cavitation and cystic transformation to produce the characteristic nodulocystic upper-zone disease and, over time, fibrosis and pulmonary vascular remodelling.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphangioleiomyomatosis","Centrilobular emphysema","Birt-Hogg-Dubé syndrome","Respiratory bronchiolitis-ILD and desquamative interstitial pneumonia","Metastatic or infective cavitating nodules","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Sarcoidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["HRCT thorax demonstrating nodules and cysts with upper- and mid-zone predominance","Detailed smoking history","Pulmonary function tests with transfer factor, often showing a reduced gas transfer with obstructive, restrictive or mixed defects","Bronchoalveolar lavage with increased CD1a-positive cells as supportive evidence","Lung biopsy with CD1a and S100 immunostaining where the diagnosis is unclear","BRAF mutation testing in progressive or multisystem disease","Echocardiography and, where indicated, right heart catheterisation for pulmonary hypertension"],"redFlags":["Spontaneous or recurrent pneumothorax","Signs of pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure","Multisystem Langerhans cell histiocytosis with diabetes insipidus, bone lesions or skin involvement","Rapid progression despite smoking cessation"],"prognosis":"The course is variable; many patients stabilise or improve with smoking cessation, but a subset progresses to cystic destruction, fibrosis, pulmonary hypertension and respiratory failure, with pulmonary hypertension worsening the outlook.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing the disease in a lifelong non-smoker without reconsideration","Mistaking cysts for emphysema or lymphangioleiomyomatosis","Not testing for BRAF mutations in progressive or multisystem disease","Failing to screen for pulmonary hypertension and underestimating the risk of recurrent pneumothorax"],"management":["Achieve complete smoking cessation, the single most important intervention, which can arrest or reverse disease","Consider corticosteroids for symptomatic or progressive nodular disease","Reserve cladribine or other systemic chemotherapy, and BRAF or MEK-targeted therapy for BRAF V600E-mutant or multisystem progressive disease","Manage spontaneous pneumothorax with intercostal drainage and consider pleurodesis for recurrence","Support with supplemental oxygen for hypoxaemia, pulmonary rehabilitation and immunisation, and treat associated pulmonary hypertension","Monitor progression with serial pulmonary function tests including transfer factor and high-resolution CT","Refer progressive respiratory failure for lung transplantation assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","European Respiratory Society statement on pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_langerhans_histiocytosis":{"name":"Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLCH","pulmonary histiocytosis X","pulmonary eosinophilic granuloma","Langerhans cell granulomatosis of the lung"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine / European Respiratory Monograph on rare lung disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Almost exclusively a disease of young to middle-aged current smokers, with a strong causal link to cigarette smoke","Presents with dry cough and breathlessness, and spontaneous pneumothorax is a common and sometimes recurrent presenting event","High-resolution CT shows a combination of nodules and bizarre, thick-walled and later thin-walled cysts, with striking upper- and mid-zone predominance and relative sparing of the costophrenic angles","Disease evolves over time from cellular nodules through cavitating nodules to cysts, reflecting bronchiolocentric Langerhans cell infiltration and destruction","Many cases carry activating BRAF V600E or other MAP-kinase pathway mutations, placing PLCH within the spectrum of clonal histiocytic neoplasms","Smoking cessation is the single most important intervention and can lead to stabilisation or regression","Extrapulmonary involvement such as diabetes insipidus, bone lesions, or skin disease should be sought as it changes management","Progressive disease may be treated with systemic therapy, and targeted therapy is considered when a driver mutation is present"],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis is driven by the accumulation of clonal, activated Langerhans-type dendritic cells around the small airways, almost always in response to cigarette smoke. These CD1a-positive, Langerin-positive cells, frequently harbouring activating mutations of the MAP-kinase pathway such as BRAF V600E, recruit eosinophils and other inflammatory cells to form bronchiolocentric nodules. Progressive inflammation destroys the distal bronchioles and adjacent alveoli, so the nodules cavitate and coalesce into cysts, producing the characteristic upper-zone cystic-nodular architecture and, over time, airflow obstruction and pulmonary hypertension.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphangioleiomyomatosis","Emphysema (centrilobular, smoking related)","Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome with pulmonary cysts","Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia","Pneumocystis-related cystic lung disease","Metastatic cystic disease or other diffuse cystic lung disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution CT to identify the upper-zone nodular and cystic pattern with basal sparing","Careful smoking history and counselling as both diagnostic clue and therapeutic lever","Pulmonary function tests, often showing reduced diffusing capacity with obstructive or mixed defects","Bronchoalveolar lavage or lung biopsy demonstrating CD1a/Langerin-positive Langerhans cells when confirmation is needed","BRAF V600E and other MAP-kinase mutation testing on biopsy material","Assessment for extrapulmonary disease including pituitary imaging, skeletal survey, and skin examination"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or bilateral spontaneous pneumothorax","Rapidly progressive breathlessness or hypoxaemia","Features of pulmonary hypertension complicating advanced cystic disease","New polyuria/polydipsia suggesting pituitary involvement with diabetes insipidus"],"prognosis":"Outcome is highly variable; many patients stabilise or improve after stopping smoking, but some progress to extensive cystic destruction, respiratory failure, and pulmonary hypertension, and continued smoking is associated with worse outcomes.","pitfalls":["Failing to take a detailed smoking history and to make cessation the cornerstone of management","Misreading the cystic pattern as emphysema or lymphangioleiomyomatosis","Overlooking extrapulmonary manifestations such as diabetes insipidus or bone lesions","Not testing for a targetable driver mutation in progressive disease"],"management":["Prioritise complete and sustained smoking cessation, which can lead to stabilisation or regression and is the cornerstone of care","Use corticosteroids for progressive or symptomatic nodular disease","Offer cladribine or other chemotherapy, and BRAF or MEK inhibitors where an activating MAP-kinase mutation is present, for progressive or multisystem disease","Treat recurrent spontaneous pneumothorax with drainage and pleurodesis","Provide supportive oxygen for hypoxaemia and pulmonary rehabilitation, and manage secondary pulmonary hypertension","Screen for extrapulmonary involvement and monitor with serial lung function and high-resolution CT","Refer end-stage disease with respiratory failure for lung transplantation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","European Respiratory Society statement on pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_regurgitation":{"name":"Pulmonary regurgitation","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pulmonary valve insufficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Valvular and congenital heart disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Backflow across the pulmonary valve, very often iatrogenic after repair of tetralogy of Fallot or pulmonary valvotomy","Other causes include pulmonary hypertension, endocarditis and congenital valve abnormalities","Mild pulmonary regurgitation is common and usually of no consequence","Chronic severe regurgitation causes progressive right ventricular dilatation and eventual dysfunction","The murmur is an early diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal edge (a Graham Steell murmur when due to pulmonary hypertension)","Timing of pulmonary valve replacement in post-tetralogy patients is guided by right ventricular size and function on cardiac MRI"],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary regurgitation occurs when the pulmonary valve fails to prevent diastolic backflow from the pulmonary artery into the right ventricle. The commonest setting is after surgical relief of right ventricular outflow obstruction (as in tetralogy of Fallot), where the valve is rendered incompetent. The regurgitant volume chronically overloads the right ventricle, causing progressive dilatation; over years this leads to right ventricular dysfunction, exercise intolerance and an increased risk of arrhythmia. Pulmonary hypertension can also cause functional regurgitation through pulmonary artery and annular dilatation.","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic regurgitation (early diastolic murmur)","Pulmonary hypertension with functional regurgitation","Residual lesions after congenital heart surgery","Endocarditis of the pulmonary valve","Absent pulmonary valve syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography assessing the valve and right ventricle","Cardiac MRI to quantify regurgitant fraction and right ventricular volumes","Electrocardiogram and monitoring for arrhythmia","Assessment of pulmonary artery pressure","Serial follow-up in post-tetralogy patients"],"redFlags":["Progressive right ventricular dilatation or dysfunction","Exercise intolerance or arrhythmia in post-tetralogy patients","Signs of right heart failure","Endocarditis"],"prognosis":"Mild pulmonary regurgitation is benign, but chronic severe regurgitation after congenital repair can cause right ventricular decline; timely pulmonary valve replacement can preserve or improve right ventricular function.","pitfalls":["Dismissing chronic severe regurgitation because symptoms appear late","Relying on echocardiography alone without MRI for right ventricular assessment","Delaying valve replacement until right ventricular dysfunction is irreversible","Confusing the murmur with aortic regurgitation"],"management":["Manage mild regurgitation with reassurance and periodic surveillance, as it is usually benign and needs no intervention","Undertake pulmonary valve replacement, surgical or percutaneous, in chronic severe regurgitation guided by right ventricular size and function, particularly after tetralogy of Fallot repair","Relieve right heart failure symptoms with diuretic therapy","Treat any underlying cause, including pulmonary hypertension driving functional regurgitation and infective endocarditis","Monitor for and manage atrial and ventricular arrhythmias with rhythm monitoring and antiarrhythmic or ablation therapy","Follow up with serial cardiac MRI to quantify the regurgitant fraction and right ventricular volumes and time intervention","Manage within a specialist adult congenital heart disease service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Congenital heart disease references (standard reference)","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_sequestration":{"name":"pulmonary sequestration","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bronchopulmonary sequestration","accessory lung"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as non-functioning lung tissue that lacks normal communication with the tracheobronchial tree and receives an anomalous systemic arterial supply, most often from the descending thoracic or abdominal aorta.","Two subtypes exist: intralobar (shares the visceral pleura of normal lung and typically presents later in childhood or adulthood with recurrent infection) and extralobar (has its own pleural investment, presents earlier and is frequently associated with other anomalies).","Intralobar sequestration predominates and characteristically involves the posterobasal segment of the left lower lobe.","Venous drainage differs by subtype: intralobar lesions usually drain to the pulmonary veins, whereas extralobar lesions typically drain to systemic veins such as the azygos or hemiazygos system.","CT angiography is the investigation of choice because it demonstrates the anomalous systemic feeding vessel that clinches the diagnosis.","Extralobar lesions are commonly detected antenatally and may coexist with congenital diaphragmatic hernia, congenital pulmonary airway malformation (hybrid lesions) and cardiac defects.","Definitive management is surgical resection (lobectomy for intralobar disease, simple excision for extralobar), while catheter embolisation of the feeding artery is an option in selected cases.","Meticulous identification and secure control of the systemic feeding artery is essential, as an unrecognised retracted vessel can cause catastrophic haemorrhage."],"pathophysiology":"During foetal development an accessory lung bud arises from the primitive foregut caudal to the normal lung bud, and because it develops separately it fails to establish communication with the tracheobronchial tree and retains its embryonic systemic arterial supply from the aorta rather than acquiring a pulmonary arterial connection. Whether the ectopic tissue is incorporated within the developing lung before or after pleural formation determines the intralobar phenotype (no separate pleura) versus the extralobar phenotype (separate pleural investment). The sequestered tissue is non-aerated or ventilated only through collateral channels, predisposing to mucus stasis and recurrent infection, while its systemic arterial supply creates a shunt that can rarely produce high-output cardiac strain or life-threatening haemoptysis.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital pulmonary airway malformation","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia or eventration","Bronchogenic cyst","Posterior mediastinal mass such as neuroblastoma","Congenital lobar emphysema","Pulmonary abscess or necrotising pneumonia","Bronchial atresia with mucocoele"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT pulmonary and aortic angiography to define the feeding artery and venous drainage","Contrast-enhanced MRI or MR angiography (avoids ionising radiation, useful in children)","Antenatal and postnatal Doppler ultrasound","Echocardiography to assess shunt burden and exclude associated cardiac anomalies","Digital subtraction angiography if embolisation is planned","Histopathological examination of the resected specimen","Renal and spinal imaging where associated anomalies are suspected in extralobar disease"],"redFlags":["Massive or recurrent haemoptysis from the systemic arterial supply","Signs of high-output cardiac failure in a neonate or infant from a large shunt","Rapidly enlarging antenatal lesion with hydrops or mediastinal shift","Recurrent lower-lobe pneumonia refractory to treatment"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent after complete resection, with low recurrence and preserved long-term pulmonary function. Outcome in antenatally detected disease depends on lesion size, associated anomalies and the presence of hydrops.","pitfalls":["Failing to obtain dedicated angiographic imaging and thereby missing the anomalous systemic artery, risking intraoperative haemorrhage.","Mislabelling a hybrid lesion as pure sequestration or pure congenital pulmonary airway malformation.","Overlooking an infradiaphragmatic feeding vessel arising from the abdominal aorta.","Attributing recurrent left basal pneumonia to ordinary infection rather than an underlying sequestration.","Assuming all lesions drain to pulmonary veins and misjudging the venous anatomy at surgery."],"management":["Resect the sequestered tissue surgically, by lobectomy or segmentectomy with secure ligation of the anomalous systemic feeding artery, as the definitive treatment for symptomatic lesions","Consider transcatheter embolisation of the feeding vessel as an alternative or adjunct in selected patients","Treat recurrent or active pulmonary infection with appropriate antibiotics and control sepsis before elective resection","Monitor antenatally detected lesions with serial ultrasound for growth, hydrops and cardiac compromise, considering foetal intervention when hydrops develops","Assess and manage any high-output cardiac strain arising from the systemic arterial shunt","Send the resected specimen for histopathology to confirm the diagnosis and exclude hybrid or malignant change","Manage within a paediatric or thoracic surgical multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","Fraser and Paré's Diagnosis of Diseases of the Chest","Grainger & Allison's Diagnostic Radiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_tumor_thrombotic_microangiopathy":{"name":"Pulmonary tumour thrombotic microangiopathy","system":"Respiratory / Pulmonary vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTTM","pulmonary tumour thrombotic microangiopathy","tumour microembolism-associated pulmonary hypertension"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC/ERS Guidelines for Pulmonary Hypertension / Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, aggressive cause of pulmonary hypertension in which microscopic tumour emboli seed the small pulmonary vessels and trigger a fibrocellular intimal reaction","Most often associated with adenocarcinomas, particularly gastric and other gastrointestinal, breast, and lung primaries, and may be the presenting feature of an occult malignancy","Presents with rapidly progressive breathlessness, hypoxaemia, and cor pulmonale developing over days to weeks, frequently mistaken for pulmonary embolism or pneumonia","Often accompanied by a microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia with thrombocytopenia and raised D-dimer, reflecting widespread microvascular thrombosis","CT pulmonary angiography usually shows no large-vessel thrombus, and perfusion scans may show diffuse peripheral defects rather than segmental mismatches","Diagnosis is frequently made only at autopsy, though wedge or transbronchial cytology and rising tumour markers can support it antemortem","Management is directed at the underlying malignancy, as no targeted pulmonary vascular therapy reverses the process, and prognosis is dismal"],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary tumour thrombotic microangiopathy occurs when malignant cells embolise into the small pulmonary arteries and arterioles and, rather than simply occluding them mechanically, provoke a local activation of coagulation and the release of growth factors. This stimulates a fibrocellular intimal proliferation and in situ thrombosis that progressively narrows and obstructs the microvasculature. The widespread microvascular obstruction rapidly raises pulmonary vascular resistance and imposes an acute pressure load on the right ventricle, producing rapidly progressive pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale. The same microvascular thrombotic process shears red cells and consumes platelets, generating the microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia and thrombocytopenia that often accompany the syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute or chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension","Lymphangitic carcinomatosis","Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease or capillary haemangiomatosis","Idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura or other microangiopathy","Sepsis or pneumonia with acute respiratory deterioration","Fat or amniotic fluid embolism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT pulmonary angiography, typically without large-vessel thrombus, to help exclude classic embolism","Ventilation-perfusion or perfusion scan, which may show diffuse patchy peripheral defects","Right heart catheterisation to document pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension","Full blood count and blood film for microangiopathic haemolysis and thrombocytopenia","Tumour markers and cross-sectional imaging to identify an underlying malignancy","Pulmonary microvascular cytology from wedged catheter aspiration or lung biopsy where feasible"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive breathlessness and hypoxaemia with acute right heart failure","Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia with thrombocytopenia suggesting disseminated microvascular disease","Pulmonary hypertension in a patient with known or suspected adenocarcinoma","Deterioration despite treatment for presumed pulmonary embolism or infection"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is extremely poor, with most patients deteriorating over days to weeks, and the diagnosis is frequently confirmed only post mortem. Occasionally, prompt recognition and treatment of the underlying malignancy can slow progression, but survival is generally measured in weeks.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because CT pulmonary angiography shows no large-vessel clot","Attributing rapid deterioration solely to pneumonia or thromboembolism and missing an occult cancer","Overlooking the diagnostic clue of concurrent microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia","Expecting targeted pulmonary vasodilators to reverse a fundamentally malignant microvascular process","Delaying a search for the primary tumour that determines any therapeutic option"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying malignancy urgently with systemic chemotherapy directed at the primary adenocarcinoma, the only intervention that may slow progression","Provide supportive oxygen and manage acute right heart failure and cor pulmonale","Consider anticoagulation for the widespread in situ microvascular thrombosis","Use pulmonary vasodilator therapy cautiously as a bridging measure where appropriate","Search for and stage the occult primary tumour with cross-sectional imaging, endoscopy and tumour markers","Manage within an oncology-led multidisciplinary team and involve palliative care early given the extremely poor prognosis","Discuss ceilings of care and offer symptom-directed support in view of survival often measured in weeks"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC/ERS Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Pulmonary Hypertension","Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_tumour_thrombotic_microangiopathy":{"name":"Pulmonary tumour thrombotic microangiopathy","system":"Respiratory / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTTM"],"chapter":null,"source":"Respiratory and pulmonary vascular medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare paraneoplastic pulmonary vasculopathy, most often complicating adenocarcinoma; gastric primaries are classic, with breast and lung also reported.","Tumour microemboli lodge in small pulmonary arteries and arterioles, triggering local coagulation and a fibrocellular intimal proliferation.","It is more than mechanical embolism; the reactive intimal proliferation and luminal narrowing drive the haemodynamic collapse.","Presents with subacute but rapidly progressive dyspnoea, dry cough and hypoxaemia.","Produces precapillary pulmonary hypertension and acute right heart failure.","Large pulmonary vessels are characteristically spared, so CT pulmonary angiography is often unremarkable, a key diagnostic trap.","Vascular endothelial growth factor, platelet-derived growth factor, tissue factor and osteopontin are implicated in the intimal remodelling.","Often diagnosed only at autopsy; ante-mortem diagnosis demands a high index of suspicion."],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary tumour thrombotic microangiopathy is a paraneoplastic pulmonary vasculopathy in which circulating tumour cells, most often from a gastric or other adenocarcinoma, lodge as microemboli within small pulmonary arteries and arterioles. Rather than causing purely mechanical obstruction, the tumour cells and adherent platelets activate the coagulation cascade through tissue factor and release growth factors including vascular endothelial growth factor and platelet-derived growth factor, stimulating a fibrocellular intimal proliferation with concentric luminal narrowing. Widespread microvascular occlusion rapidly raises pulmonary vascular resistance, producing precapillary pulmonary hypertension and acute right ventricular failure, and a consumptive coagulopathy with microangiopathic haemolysis may accompany the process. Because the large pulmonary vessels are characteristically spared, standard CT pulmonary angiography is often unremarkable despite a fulminant clinical course.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute large-vessel pulmonary thromboembolism","Pulmonary tumour embolism without intimal proliferation","Idiopathic or other pulmonary arterial hypertension","Lymphangitic carcinomatosis","Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura and other thrombotic microangiopathies","Interstitial lung disease or atypical infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT pulmonary angiography (often negative for large emboli; may show ground-glass change, tree-in-bud or septal thickening)","Echocardiography demonstrating raised right heart pressures and right ventricular dilatation or strain","Blood film for schistocytes with haemolysis markers (lactate dehydrogenase, haptoglobin) and a coagulation/DIC screen","Right heart catheterisation confirming precapillary pulmonary hypertension","Pulmonary microvascular cytology from a wedged pulmonary artery catheter","Search for an occult primary (upper gastrointestinal endoscopy, cross-sectional imaging, tumour markers)","Lung biopsy or post-mortem histology for definitive diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive dyspnoea and hypoxaemia with a CT pulmonary angiogram negative for large emboli","Signs of acute right heart failure or exertional syncope","Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia with thrombocytopenia","Unexplained pulmonary hypertension in a patient with known or suspected adenocarcinoma"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is extremely poor, with survival often measured in days to weeks from symptom onset and the diagnosis frequently made only at autopsy. Rare patients recognised early and treated with therapy directed at the underlying malignancy, with supportive pulmonary vasodilator or targeted measures, have survived longer.","pitfalls":["Being falsely reassured by a negative CT pulmonary angiogram and attributing symptoms to conventional pulmonary embolism.","Failing to consider occult malignancy in new, rapidly progressive pulmonary hypertension.","Delaying because definitive tissue is difficult to obtain during life.","Overlooking coexisting microangiopathic haemolysis and disseminated intravascular coagulation."],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying malignancy with systemic chemotherapy for the primary tumour, most often a gastric or other adenocarcinoma, as the only measure that may alter the course","Support gas exchange with oxygen and treat acute precapillary pulmonary hypertension and right ventricular failure","Consider anticoagulation for the consumptive microvascular thrombosis and address any disseminated intravascular coagulation","Pursue urgent identification of the occult primary with upper gastrointestinal endoscopy, cross-sectional imaging and tumour markers","Trial cautious pulmonary vasodilator or targeted supportive measures in selected patients","Involve oncology and palliative care within a multidisciplinary team, given a prognosis measured in days to weeks","Agree goals of care and provide symptom-focused end-of-life support where appropriate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Respiratory medicine references","Pulmonary vascular disease references","Oncology paraneoplastic syndrome references"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_valve_stenosis":{"name":"Pulmonary valve stenosis","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pulmonary stenosis","valvular pulmonary stenosis","PS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Obstruction to right ventricular outflow at valve level, usually congenital and often due to a domed, fused pliable valve, though dysplastic valves occur in Noonan syndrome","Signs include an ejection systolic click that decreases with inspiration, an ejection systolic murmur loudest at the left upper sternal edge (pulmonary area), and a widely split second heart sound with a soft pulmonary component as severity increases","Mild stenosis is common, frequently asymptomatic, and tends not to progress, whereas moderate to severe obstruction produces exertional breathlessness, fatigue, and eventually right heart failure","The right ventricle hypertrophies to overcome the gradient, and severe long-standing obstruction can cause tricuspid regurgitation and raised right atrial pressure","Balloon valvuloplasty is the treatment of choice for a suitable pliable domed valve and gives durable relief in most patients","Dysplastic and heavily calcified valves respond poorly to balloon dilatation and may need surgical valvotomy or valve replacement","Echocardiography with Doppler estimation of the peak gradient guides the timing of intervention alongside symptoms and right ventricular changes"],"pathophysiology":"In valvular pulmonary stenosis the pulmonary valve is narrowed, most often by commissural fusion of a congenitally domed valve or by thickened, poorly mobile dysplastic leaflets. The obstruction imposes a pressure load on the right ventricle, which hypertrophies to maintain output across the narrowed orifice; the resulting high-velocity jet produces post-stenotic dilatation of the pulmonary trunk. With severe, chronic obstruction the hypertrophied right ventricle may fail, right atrial pressure rises, and any coexisting atrial communication can permit right-to-left shunting with cyanosis. Relief of the gradient allows the ventricular hypertrophy to regress over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Subvalvular (infundibular) right ventricular outflow obstruction","Supravalvular pulmonary or branch pulmonary artery stenosis","Tetralogy of Fallot","Atrial septal defect with a pulmonary flow murmur","Innocent pulmonary flow murmur","Double-chambered right ventricle"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography with Doppler to localise the obstruction and estimate the peak gradient","Assessment of right ventricular size, hypertrophy, and function and of tricuspid regurgitation","Cardiac MRI to quantify right ventricular volumes and clarify the level of obstruction","Electrocardiography and chest radiography for right ventricular hypertrophy and post-stenotic pulmonary artery dilatation","Right heart catheterisation when non-invasive gradient assessment is discordant or before balloon valvuloplasty"],"redFlags":["Exertional syncope or presyncope indicating severe obstruction","Signs of right heart failure with raised jugular venous pressure and peripheral oedema","Cyanosis from right-to-left shunting through an atrial communication","Severe stenosis with a dysplastic or calcified valve unsuitable for balloon treatment"],"prognosis":"Mild pulmonary stenosis carries an excellent outlook and rarely progresses, while moderate to severe obstruction responds well to balloon valvuloplasty or surgery with durable relief and regression of right ventricular hypertrophy.","pitfalls":["Overlooking a dysplastic valve, especially in Noonan syndrome, that will respond poorly to balloon valvuloplasty","Attributing the murmur to an innocent flow murmur and missing significant obstruction","Failing to recognise coexisting infundibular obstruction that persists after valve relief","Not assessing for an atrial communication that may allow cyanosis in severe disease"],"management":["Observe mild stenosis, which rarely progresses and needs no intervention","Relieve moderate to severe obstruction of a suitable pliable domed valve with balloon valvuloplasty, the treatment of choice, giving durable relief","Refer dysplastic or heavily calcified valves that respond poorly to balloon dilatation for surgical valvotomy or valve replacement","Treat right heart failure symptoms with diuretic therapy in advanced obstruction","Monitor with serial echocardiography and Doppler to track the gradient, right ventricular hypertrophy and function","Evaluate for Noonan syndrome where a dysplastic valve is present and offer genetic counselling and family screening","Manage within a cardiology or congenital heart disease service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC Guidelines for the management of adult congenital heart disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_veno_occlusive":{"name":"Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PVOD","pulmonary veno-occlusive disease","pulmonary capillary haemangiomatosis","PCH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ATS-ERS","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare cause of pulmonary hypertension in which obstruction lies in the post-capillary pulmonary venules and small veins rather than the arteries","Presents like other forms of pulmonary arterial hypertension with progressive breathlessness, fatigue, and eventual right heart failure, making it easy to misclassify","The pulmonary artery wedge pressure is characteristically normal despite the venous obstruction, because it reflects pressure upstream of the occluded venules","High-resolution CT typically shows a triad of centrilobular ground-glass opacities, smooth septal line thickening, and mediastinal lymph node enlargement","Hereditary disease is associated with biallelic mutations in the EIF2AK4 gene","It is critical to distinguish PVOD from typical pulmonary arterial hypertension, since vasodilator therapy can precipitate severe pulmonary oedema","There is no reliably effective medical therapy, and lung transplantation is the definitive treatment for eligible patients","A markedly reduced diffusing capacity with hypoxaemia out of proportion to other findings should raise suspicion"],"pathophysiology":"In pulmonary veno-occlusive disease there is widespread fibrous obliteration and remodelling of the pulmonary venules and small veins, sometimes accompanied by capillary proliferation (pulmonary capillary haemangiomatosis, now regarded as part of the same spectrum). Obstruction to venous outflow raises pressure in the upstream capillaries and pulmonary arteries, producing pre-capillary-pattern pulmonary hypertension while the left atrial and wedge pressures remain normal. Chronic capillary congestion leads to interstitial oedema, septal thickening, and occult alveolar haemorrhage. Because the arterioles remain relatively spared, pulmonary arterial vasodilators can flood the fixed downstream obstruction and cause acute pulmonary oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension","Pulmonary hypertension due to left heart disease","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension","Interstitial lung disease with secondary pulmonary hypertension","Pulmonary capillary haemangiomatosis (overlapping entity)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution CT of the chest for the characteristic triad of findings","Right heart catheterisation demonstrating pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension with a normal wedge pressure","Pulmonary function testing with a disproportionately reduced diffusing capacity","Genetic testing for EIF2AK4 mutations in suspected hereditary or familial cases","Echocardiography to assess right heart size and function","Assessment for associated conditions and transplant eligibility"],"redFlags":["Acute pulmonary oedema following initiation of pulmonary vasodilator therapy - a recognised emergency in this disease","Rapidly worsening breathlessness and hypoxaemia signalling clinical deterioration","Signs of decompensated right heart failure such as syncope, hypotension, or rising oxygen requirement","Haemoptysis from congested capillaries"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is poor with a rapidly progressive course, limited response to medical therapy, and high mortality; lung transplantation offers the only definitive treatment and referral should be early.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as ordinary pulmonary arterial hypertension and starting vasodilators that precipitate pulmonary oedema","Overlooking the diagnosis when the wedge pressure is normal despite congestive imaging findings","Delaying transplant referral given the aggressive course","Failing to recognise the characteristic CT triad"],"management":["Refer early for lung transplantation, the only definitive treatment, given the poor and rapidly progressive course","Avoid or use targeted pulmonary arterial vasodilators only with extreme caution in an expert centre, as they can precipitate severe pulmonary oedema","Provide supportive supplemental oxygen for hypoxaemia and cautious diuretic therapy for pulmonary congestion","Offer EIF2AK4 genetic testing and cascade family counselling where hereditary disease is suspected","Manage exclusively within a specialist pulmonary hypertension centre with facilities for close monitoring","Support with immunisation and advise on avoidance of pregnancy and other high-risk states","Monitor right ventricular function and oxygenation closely, escalating promptly on deterioration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ATS/ERS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_veno_occlusive_disease":{"name":"Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PVOD","pulmonary occlusive venopathy","pulmonary capillary haemangiomatosis spectrum","PVOD/PCH"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC/ERS guidelines on pulmonary hypertension / Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare form of pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension in which the obstruction lies predominantly in the pulmonary venules and small veins rather than the arteries","Presents like idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension with progressive exertional breathlessness, fatigue, and right heart failure, but is often more severe and rapidly progressive","Right heart catheterisation shows pre-capillary haemodynamics with a normal or near-normal pulmonary artery wedge pressure despite marked oedema, because the occlusion is downstream of the arterioles but upstream of the measured wedge","The characteristic high-resolution CT triad is smooth interlobular septal thickening (Kerley lines), centrilobular ground-glass opacity, and mediastinal lymphadenopathy","The diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide is typically strikingly reduced and hypoxaemia is often profound","Biallelic EIF2AK4 mutations underlie the heritable form and allow genetic confirmation without lung biopsy","Pulmonary vasodilators can precipitate acute, sometimes fatal, pulmonary oedema because the venous outflow is fixed while arterial inflow increases","Because response to medical therapy is poor, early referral for lung transplantation is essential"],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease is characterised by fibrotic obstruction and remodelling of the pulmonary venules and small veins, often with associated capillary proliferation. The heritable form is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in EIF2AK4. Because the primary obstruction lies in the post-capillary venous compartment while the arterioles remain relatively patent, pressure builds up in the pulmonary capillary bed, causing interstitial oedema, septal thickening, and capillary congestion, yet the pulmonary artery wedge pressure measured beyond the obstruction remains normal, so the haemodynamics appear pre-capillary. Administering arterial vasodilators increases flow into a bed that cannot drain, precipitating acute pulmonary oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension","Left heart failure with post-capillary pulmonary hypertension","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension","Pulmonary capillary haemangiomatosis","Interstitial lung disease with secondary pulmonary hypertension","Mitral valve disease causing pulmonary venous congestion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Right heart catheterisation demonstrating pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension with a normal wedge pressure","High-resolution CT to detect the septal-thickening, centrilobular ground-glass, and lymphadenopathy triad","Pulmonary function tests with a markedly reduced diffusing capacity","Genetic testing for EIF2AK4 mutations in suspected heritable disease","Echocardiography to assess right ventricular size and function","Ventilation-perfusion or CT pulmonary angiography to exclude chronic thromboembolic disease"],"redFlags":["Acute pulmonary oedema after starting pulmonary vasodilator therapy","Rapidly progressive right heart failure","Severe hypoxaemia with a disproportionately low diffusing capacity","Syncope or signs of low cardiac output indicating advanced disease"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is poor and generally worse than idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension, with limited and hazardous response to medical therapy, so timely lung transplantation offers the best chance of survival.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension and initiating vasodilators that trigger pulmonary oedema","Attributing the septal-line and ground-glass changes to left heart failure despite a normal wedge pressure","Delaying transplant referral because of an apparent (but hazardous) initial vasodilator response","Overlooking the EIF2AK4 genetic clue in younger or familial cases"],"management":["Prioritise timely lung transplantation, which offers the best chance of survival in this poor-prognosis disease, and refer early","Approach targeted pulmonary vasodilator therapy with great caution in a specialist centre, since it readily precipitates acute pulmonary oedema","Give supportive oxygen for profound hypoxaemia and careful diuretics for congestion","Confirm heritable disease with EIF2AK4 mutation testing, avoiding lung biopsy, and provide genetic counselling and family screening","Exclude chronic thromboembolic disease and other mimics before committing to therapy","Manage within an expert pulmonary hypertension and transplant multidisciplinary team","Monitor right ventricular size and function and escalate rapidly given the propensity for swift deterioration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC/ERS guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of pulmonary hypertension","Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonic_valve_disease":{"name":"Pulmonic Valve Disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pulmonary valve disease","Pulmonic stenosis","Pulmonic regurgitation","PS","PR"],"chapter":278,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2063","clinicalPearls":["Pulmonic stenosis is overwhelmingly a congenital lesion; the valve is typically domed in isolated cases, and the pulmonic valve is only very rarely affected by the rheumatic process.","Dysplastic pulmonic valves occur as a feature of Noonan syndrome (mapped to chromosome 12), with PTPN1 gene mutations identified in roughly half of Noonan cases.","The pulmonic ejection sound (click) is the single right-sided acoustic event that DECREASES in intensity with inspiration, because elevated RV end-diastolic pressure pre-opens the valve.","As pulmonic stenosis worsens, the ejection sound migrates toward S1 and eventually becomes inaudible, the systolic murmur peaks later and may extend past A2, and P2 becomes reduced or absent.","The systolic murmur of pulmonic stenosis is mid-systolic and crescendo-decrescendo, best heard in the left second interspace, and it increases in intensity during inspiration.","The Graham Steell murmur of pulmonic regurgitation is a high-pitched, decrescendo diastolic murmur along the left sternal border that can be mistaken for aortic regurgitation; it tends to grow louder with inspiration.","In survivors of childhood tetralogy of Fallot or PS/pulmonary atresia repair, an RV-PA conduit lacking a valve can be freely regurgitant, producing a misleadingly low-pitched, short diastolic murmur despite significant PR and RV volume overload.","Right ventricular dysfunction from afterload mismatch appears earlier and at lower peak systolic pressures in pulmonic stenosis than left ventricular failure does in aortic stenosis, because the RV tolerates pressure overload poorly.","Cardiac output is maintained until late in pulmonic stenosis, so many patients with mild or even moderate disease are asymptomatic and are detected only by an incidental murmur or click.","A reduction in RV ejection fraction may be an early sign of hemodynamic compromise in pulmonic regurgitation, before overt symptoms develop.","Trace or mild pulmonic regurgitation of no hemodynamic significance is frequently seen on transthoracic echocardiography even when the valve is structurally normal.","Carcinoid characteristically produces mixed pulmonic valve disease, combining both regurgitation and stenosis."],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonic stenosis is defined hemodynamically by a systolic pressure gradient between the right ventricle and the main pulmonary artery. Sustained obstruction to RV outflow drives RV hypertrophy and prolongs systolic ejection; because the RV adapts poorly to pressure overload, dysfunction from afterload mismatch develops earlier and at lower pressures than analogous left-sided changes in aortic stenosis. The right atrial a wave rises in proportion to the higher pressures needed to fill a stiff, hypertrophied RV, and a prominent RA v wave reflects functional tricuspid regurgitation from RV and annular dilation; cardiac output is preserved until late. Pulmonic stenosis rarely progresses when mild but can worsen with age in moderate disease owing to valve thickening and calcification. In pulmonic regurgitation, severe disease causes RV chamber enlargement and eccentric hypertrophy, creating a state of increased preload and afterload. The diastolic pressure gradient from pulmonary artery to RV that drives the regurgitant flow falls progressively through diastole, explaining the decrescendo murmur, and the murmur shortens as RV diastolic pressure rises. Forward output is preserved early but fails to augment normally with exercise and declines over time; advanced stages show marked RV and RA enlargement with elevated jugular venous pressure. PR arises from primary valve pathology, annular enlargement (e.g., long-standing severe pulmonary hypertension dilating the valve ring), or their combination, and may follow surgical relief of RV outflow obstruction or balloon valvotomy.","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic regurgitation (the Graham Steell murmur of PR can mimic the AR murmur)","Other causes of a midsystolic murmur such as aortic stenosis, hypertrophic obstructive physiology, or an atrial septal defect with enhanced pulmonic flow","Pulmonary hypertension as the dominant clinical problem rather than the valve lesion itself"],"infectionMimics":["Infective endocarditis of the pulmonic valve (an etiology of both PS from bulky vegetations and PR)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Carcinoid heart disease causing mixed pulmonic valve lesions","Obstructing tumors producing RV outflow obstruction that mimics valvular PS","Idiopathic dilation of the pulmonary artery / annulus causing functional PR","Marfan syndrome-related annular enlargement"],"additionalInvestigations":["ECG: right axis deviation, RV hypertrophy, and right atrial enlargement in severe PS; RVH/RA enlargement may appear in PR depending on etiology and severity","Chest x-ray: poststenotic dilation of the main pulmonary artery on the frontal view, retrosternal airspace filling from RV enlargement on the lateral view, and apex lifted off the left hemidiaphragm in some patients","Transthoracic echocardiography (Doppler): definitive diagnosis in most cases, with jet velocity, gradient, RV function, pulmonary artery pressures (which should be low in PS), and associated lesions; RV systolic pressure estimated from the tricuspid regurgitant jet","Transesophageal echocardiography: better delineation of the RV outflow tract and infundibular hypertrophy when needed","Cardiac MR: greater anatomic detail and precise RV volume/function assessment, especially in repaired congenital heart disease","Cardiac catheterization: not routinely required; if performed in PS, pressures are taken just below and above the valve with attention to a possible dynamic gradient"],"redFlags":["Exertional dyspnea or early-onset fatigue indicating severe pulmonic stenosis","Anginal chest pain from RV oxygen supply-demand mismatch, or syncope, with very severe obstruction","Destabilizing triggers (atrial fibrillation, fever, infection, anemia, pregnancy) precipitating decompensation in severe PS","Signs of right heart failure (hepatomegaly, ascites, peripheral edema) from RV systolic dysfunction","Abdominal fullness/bloating and lower-extremity swelling in progressive severe PR with RV dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Mild pulmonic stenosis rarely progresses, whereas moderate disease may worsen with age due to valve thickening and calcification. Because cardiac output is maintained until late, many patients remain asymptomatic for years; severe disease eventually brings RV dysfunction and, uncommonly and only very late, overt right heart failure. In pulmonic regurgitation, forward output is preserved early but does not rise normally with exertion and declines over time, with a falling RV ejection fraction serving as an early marker of compromise and advanced disease producing marked RV/RA enlargement and elevated jugular venous pressure.","pitfalls":["The Graham Steell murmur of PR is easily confused with aortic regurgitation along the left sternal border.","A valveless RV-PA conduit after congenital repair can give a low-pitched, short diastolic murmur that understates the true severity of PR and RV volume overload.","Doppler peak instantaneous gradient correlates only weakly with the catheterization peak-to-peak gradient; the catheter peak-to-peak value tends to track better with the Doppler MEAN gradient.","Trace or mild PR seen on echocardiography is usually of no clinical or hemodynamic consequence and should not be over-interpreted.","Relying on the systolic ejection sound can mislead in severe PS, where the click moves toward S1 and may become inaudible."],"management":["Observe mild pulmonic stenosis, which seldom progresses and requires no intervention","Relieve significant, pliable domed pulmonic stenosis with balloon valvuloplasty as first-line treatment","Refer dysplastic, calcified or heavily regurgitant valves for surgical valvotomy or pulmonary valve replacement, timed in regurgitation by right ventricular size and function","Manage right ventricular pressure or volume overload and failure with diuretic therapy","Monitor with serial echocardiography and cardiac MRI to assess the gradient, right ventricular volumes and function","Evaluate for Noonan syndrome where a dysplastic valve is present and offer genetic counselling and family screening","Manage within a cardiology or adult congenital heart disease service"],"severityClassification":"Pulmonic stenosis severity (normal systolic function and cardiac output): severe = peak systolic gradient >64 mmHg (mean gradient >35 mmHg, Doppler jet velocity >4 m/s); moderate = peak gradient 36-64 mmHg (jet velocity 3-4 m/s); mild = jet velocity <3 m/s (peak gradient <36 mmHg).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2063","Harrison 22e p.2064"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulpitis":{"name":"Pulpitis","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dental pulp inflammation","toothache","reversible pulpitis","irreversible pulpitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pulpitis is inflammation of the dental pulp, most often a consequence of deep caries, cracks, or trauma allowing bacterial ingress","Reversible pulpitis produces sharp, short-lived pain to stimuli such as cold that settles quickly once the stimulus is removed","Irreversible pulpitis causes spontaneous, lingering, often throbbing pain that may worsen when lying down and can be poorly localised","Heat that intensifies pain and cold that relieves it can indicate an irreversibly inflamed or partly necrotic pulp","Pulp sensibility testing (cold, electric) helps distinguish vital from non-vital teeth and reversible from irreversible states","Reversible pulpitis is managed by removing the cause and placing a protective restoration, whereas irreversible pulpitis requires root canal treatment or extraction","As inflammation progresses to necrosis, pain may transiently ease before periapical infection develops","Referred pain patterns mean patients frequently misidentify which tooth or arch is affected"],"pathophysiology":"Pulpitis begins when bacterial products, most commonly from advancing caries, reach the pulp through dentinal tubules or direct exposure, triggering an inflammatory response within the rigid, low-compliance confines of the pulp chamber. Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability raise intrapulpal pressure, and because the pulp cannot expand, this compresses vessels and nerves, amplifying pain. In reversible disease the inflammation is limited and the pulp can recover once the irritant is removed. With continued insult the inflammation becomes irreversible, progressing to ischaemia, localised then total pulp necrosis, and eventual extension of infection through the apical foramen into the periapical tissues.","additionalDifferentials":["Dentine hypersensitivity","Cracked tooth syndrome","Periapical periodontitis or abscess","Maxillary sinusitis referring pain to upper teeth","Atypical odontalgia or trigeminal neuralgia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thermal and electric pulp sensibility testing","Percussion and palpation to assess periapical involvement","Periapical radiography to evaluate caries depth and apical changes","Bite test and transillumination for suspected cracks","Selective anaesthesia to localise the source of poorly localised pain"],"redFlags":["Facial swelling, fever, or trismus signalling spread of odontogenic infection","Rapidly worsening pain with systemic upset suggesting progression to abscess or cellulitis","Airway-threatening swelling from infection tracking into deep neck spaces"],"prognosis":"Reversible pulpitis usually resolves fully after the irritant is treated, while irreversible pulpitis will not recover and needs endodontic treatment or extraction; treated teeth generally have a good outcome when infection is eliminated.","pitfalls":["Assuming pain localisation is accurate when pulpal pain is often diffuse and referred","Treating irreversible pulpitis with a simple restoration and expecting resolution","Missing a maxillary sinus origin for upper posterior tooth pain","Interpreting a sudden reduction in pain as recovery rather than progression to necrosis"],"management":["Treat reversible pulpitis by removing the carious or offending irritant and placing a protective or definitive restoration, allowing the pulp to recover","Manage irreversible pulpitis with root canal treatment (pulpectomy) or, where the tooth is unrestorable, extraction","Provide analgesia with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs or other analgesics for pain relief","Reserve antibiotics for spreading or systemic infection rather than localised pulpitis","Confirm pulp status with thermal and electric sensibility testing and periapical radiography to guide treatment","Reduce future risk with oral hygiene instruction, topical fluoride and dietary advice on sugar","Refer complex endodontic cases to a specialist endodontist"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"punctate_inner_choroidopathy":{"name":"Punctate inner choroidopathy (PIC)","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PIC","punctate inner choroidopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically affects young, myopic women","Produces small yellow-white punctate lesions at the level of the inner choroid and outer retina at the posterior pole","There is characteristically little or no vitritis, which helps distinguish it from multifocal choroiditis","Symptoms include blurred vision, scotomata, photopsia and metamorphopsia","The principal sight-threatening complication is choroidal neovascularisation","Lesions typically evolve into small atrophic, punched-out chorioretinal scars","Management is observation for inactive lesions, intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy for choroidal neovascularisation, and corticosteroids or immunosuppression for aggressive inflammation"],"pathophysiology":"PIC is an idiopathic inflammatory process affecting the inner choroid and outer retina that produces focal punctate lesions. Associated disruption and breaks in Bruch's membrane, together with the inflammatory environment, predispose to the growth of a choroidal neovascular membrane, which is the main threat to central vision. Individual lesions heal to leave atrophic chorioretinal scars.","additionalDifferentials":["Multifocal choroiditis and panuveitis","Multiple evanescent white dot syndrome","Myopic degeneration with lacquer cracks and neovascularisation","Presumed ocular histoplasmosis syndrome","Birdshot chorioretinopathy","Sarcoid choroiditis","Neovascular age-related macular degeneration in older patients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination to identify the punctate lesions","Optical coherence tomography to localise lesions and detect subretinal fluid or neovascularisation","Optical coherence tomography angiography to detect choroidal neovascularisation","Fluorescein angiography showing staining of lesions and leakage from any neovascular membrane","Indocyanine green angiography showing hypofluorescent spots","Investigations to exclude tuberculosis and syphilis"],"redFlags":["New metamorphopsia, central scotoma or subretinal fluid or haemorrhage indicating choroidal neovascularisation","Rapid progression with heavy inflammation suggesting multifocal choroiditis","Bilateral aggressive disease","Profound central visual loss"],"prognosis":"The outlook is good when the disease remains uncomplicated, but choroidal neovascularisation can cause significant central visual loss; prompt treatment of neovascularisation improves the outcome.","pitfalls":["Missing choroidal neovascularisation, which requires regular Amsler and optical coherence tomography monitoring","Confusing it with multifocal choroiditis, which shows vitritis, or with myopic neovascularisation","Failing to exclude tuberculosis and syphilis","Delaying anti-VEGF treatment once neovascularisation develops"],"management":["Treat sight-threatening choroidal neovascularisation, the principal complication, with intravitreal anti-VEGF injections","Use corticosteroids, systemic or periocular, and consider steroid-sparing immunosuppression for active inflammatory disease","Observe uncomplicated punctate lesions, which often heal to atrophic scars without treatment","Exclude tuberculosis and syphilis before starting immunosuppressive therapy","Monitor with optical coherence tomography and OCT angiography, and advise Amsler grid self-monitoring for new distortion","Refer to a medical retina or uveitis specialist for ongoing management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course: Uveitis and Ocular Inflammation"],"crossLinks":[]},"punctate_palmoplantar_keratoderma":{"name":"Punctate palmoplantar keratoderma","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["keratosis palmoplantaris punctata","Buschke-Fischer-Brauer keratoderma","AAGAB"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with multiple discrete, hard keratotic papules scattered across the palms and soles, sometimes with central depressions when the plug is removed","The classic type 1 (Buschke-Fischer-Brauer) is autosomal dominant and caused by variants in AAGAB","Lesions tend to be more numerous and prominent over pressure points and can be tender when walking","Onset is usually in adolescence or early adulthood, and lesions accumulate slowly over time","Some case series report an association with internal malignancy, so age-appropriate cancer awareness is reasonable, though the link is debated","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by family history, dermoscopy and biopsy showing a compact column of hyperkeratosis; AAGAB testing can confirm type 1","Management is supportive with keratolytics, paring, emollients and, in resistant cases, oral retinoids"],"pathophysiology":"Punctate palmoplantar keratoderma type 1 is caused by loss-of-function variants in AAGAB, which encodes alpha- and gamma-adaptin binding protein p34, a regulator of intracellular vesicle trafficking and of the recycling of growth factor receptors such as the epidermal growth factor receptor. Haploinsufficiency is thought to enhance keratinocyte proliferation in focal areas of acral skin, producing discrete columns of compact hyperkeratosis that clinically appear as punctate keratotic papules concentrated at sites of pressure.","additionalDifferentials":["Palmoplantar warts (verrucae)","Arsenical keratoses","Punctate porokeratosis and spiny keratoderma","Cole disease and other punctate keratoderma variants","Darier disease acral lesions","Pitted keratolysis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of AAGAB for type 1 disease","Skin biopsy showing a compact column of orthohyperkeratosis overlying a depressed epidermis","Dermoscopy to distinguish keratotic plugs from warts","Family history and pedigree analysis","Age-appropriate malignancy screening awareness given reported associations","Clinical photography to monitor lesion accumulation"],"redFlags":["New systemic symptoms or signs prompting age-appropriate malignancy evaluation given reported associations","Rapidly changing or ulcerating individual lesions","Painful lesions markedly impairing mobility","Secondary infection of pared or fissured lesions"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and chronic; lesions persist and slowly increase in number, but with keratolytic and mechanical treatment most patients maintain good function.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating the papules as recalcitrant plantar warts","Overlooking the family history that points to a dominant keratoderma","Dismissing the debated malignancy association without age-appropriate review","Escalating to systemic retinoids before adequate topical keratolytic therapy"],"management":["Reduce hyperkeratosis with topical keratolytics such as salicylic acid or urea preparations and regular emollients","Offer mechanical paring or debridement of keratotic plugs and pressure-offloading footwear or padding to relieve tenderness on walking","Consider systemic retinoids such as acitretin for extensive or refractory disease","Provide genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal dominant AAGAB-related condition","Maintain age-appropriate malignancy awareness given the reported, though debated, association with internal cancers","Monitor lesion accumulation clinically, using dermoscopy to distinguish plugs from viral warts"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pura_syndrome":{"name":"PURA syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PURA","Pur-alpha syndrome","PURA-related neurodevelopmental disorder","5q31.3 microdeletion (PURA)"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents in the neonatal period with profound hypotonia, feeding difficulties and often excessive sleepiness or reduced arousal","Neonatal problems can include temperature instability, hypersomnolence and apnoea or hypoventilation","A prominent movement disorder emerges, with dyskinesias, dystonia, tremor and exaggerated startle-like or myoclonic movements","Epilepsy and abnormal EEG activity are common, though some paroxysmal movements are non-epileptic and can mimic seizures","Global developmental delay with intellectual disability, absent or limited speech and delayed walking is typical","Caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in PURA (or 5q31.3 deletions including it), usually de novo and autosomal dominant, encoding the DNA/RNA-binding protein Pur-alpha","Distinguishing true seizures from non-epileptic paroxysmal movements is a recurring clinical challenge"],"pathophysiology":"PURA encodes Pur-alpha, a highly conserved DNA- and RNA-binding protein that participates in transcriptional regulation, DNA replication and the transport and translation of messenger RNA in neurons, functions essential for normal brain development and neuronal maturation. Heterozygous loss-of-function variants, usually de novo, reduce functional protein through haploinsufficiency, disrupting neuronal differentiation and synaptic function. The resulting impairment produces the neonatal hypotonia and reduced arousal, movement disorder, epilepsy and developmental delay. Because a single functional copy is insufficient, the gene is dosage-sensitive and deletions encompassing it produce the same disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Prader-Willi syndrome","Angelman syndrome","Congenital myasthenic and neuromuscular disorders causing hypotonia","Neonatal epileptic encephalopathies","Mitochondrial and metabolic encephalopathies","Other causes of the floppy infant"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["EEG, ideally with video, to distinguish seizures from non-epileptic paroxysmal movements","Brain MRI, often normal or with non-specific changes","Neuromuscular and metabolic workup for the hypotonic infant","Chromosomal microarray to detect 5q31.3 deletions involving PURA","Gene panel or exome sequencing including PURA"],"redFlags":["Apnoea, hypoventilation or respiratory instability in the neonate","Feeding failure requiring supported nutrition","Poorly controlled seizures or status epilepticus","Severe temperature or autonomic instability"],"prognosis":"PURA syndrome causes lifelong moderate to severe intellectual disability with motor and often speech impairment; early neonatal risks include respiratory and feeding instability. With supportive care many children improve in arousal and feeding over infancy, and management is multidisciplinary.","pitfalls":["Over-treating non-epileptic paroxysmal movements as seizures","Misdiagnosing as Prader-Willi or a primary neuromuscular disorder without genetic testing","Assuming a normal MRI excludes a genetic cause","Overlooking respiratory and autonomic instability in the neonatal period"],"management":["Control epilepsy with antiepileptic drugs, using video-EEG to distinguish true seizures from non-epileptic paroxysmal movements","Manage the movement disorder of dystonia, dyskinesia and myoclonus symptomatically","Support neonatal respiratory instability, apnoea and hypoventilation with monitoring and ventilatory support as needed","Address feeding difficulties with feeding support and nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding where required","Provide developmental therapies including physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language therapy for hypotonia and global delay","Coordinate care through a neurodevelopmental multidisciplinary team","Offer genetic counselling, confirming the usually de novo variant and advising on the low recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: PURA-Related Neurodevelopmental Disorders","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"pure_autonomic_failure":{"name":"Pure autonomic failure","system":"Nervous system / Autonomic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAF","Bradbury-Eggleston syndrome","idiopathic orthostatic hypotension"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A sporadic, adult-onset, slowly progressive degenerative autonomic disorder presenting with orthostatic hypotension in the absence of central neurological deficits","Reflects peripheral (postganglionic) sympathetic denervation, with low supine plasma noradrenaline that fails to rise on standing","Characterised by pathological alpha-synuclein deposition in peripheral autonomic neurons, placing it within the synucleinopathies","Symptoms include light-headedness, fatigue, visual greying, and a suboccipital 'coat-hanger' ache on standing, all relieved by sitting or lying","Additional features include reduced sweating, bladder and bowel disturbance, and erectile dysfunction","A substantial minority phenoconvert over years to a central synucleinopathy such as Parkinson disease, dementia with Lewy bodies, or multiple system atrophy","Supine hypertension frequently coexists with orthostatic hypotension and complicates treatment","Management is non-pharmacological first: volume and salt expansion, compression garments, physical counter-manoeuvres, and head-up tilt of the bed overnight"],"pathophysiology":"Pure autonomic failure is a neurodegenerative disorder confined largely to the peripheral autonomic nervous system. Misfolded alpha-synuclein accumulates within postganglionic sympathetic and parasympathetic neurons and causes their degeneration. Loss of postganglionic sympathetic fibres impairs noradrenaline release, so vasoconstriction fails on standing and blood pools in the lower body, producing orthostatic hypotension. Because supine noradrenaline is low and does not increase appropriately on standing, the lesion is localised distal to the ganglion. Denervation supersensitivity of vascular adrenoceptors and impaired baroreflex buffering contribute to the coexisting supine hypertension.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple system atrophy","Parkinson disease with autonomic involvement","Diabetic or amyloid autonomic neuropathy","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase deficiency","Medication-induced orthostatic hypotension","Volume depletion or adrenal insufficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Head-up tilt-table testing with beat-to-beat blood pressure recording","Supine and standing plasma catecholamines showing low, non-responsive noradrenaline","Cardiovascular autonomic reflex battery (Valsalva manoeuvre, deep breathing, sustained handgrip)","QSART and thermoregulatory sweat test for sudomotor involvement","Cardiac MIBG scintigraphy demonstrating reduced myocardial uptake","Serial neurological review to detect phenoconversion to a central synucleinopathy"],"redFlags":["Emergence of parkinsonism, cerebellar signs, or early severe autonomic failure suggesting multiple system atrophy","Rapid progression or prominent bladder failure pointing to a central synucleinopathy","Severe supine hypertension threatening end-organ damage","Recurrent syncope with fall-related injury"],"prognosis":"Pure autonomic failure generally progresses slowly and carries a better outlook than multiple system atrophy, but a meaningful proportion of patients phenoconvert to a central synucleinopathy over years, and disabling orthostatic symptoms with supine hypertension require ongoing management.","pitfalls":["Overlooking coexisting supine hypertension when treating orthostatic hypotension","Attributing symptoms to simple dehydration and missing the underlying degenerative disorder","Failing to monitor for phenoconversion to Parkinson disease, dementia with Lewy bodies, or multiple system atrophy","Overtreating orthostatic hypotension and worsening nocturnal supine hypertension"],"management":["Manage orthostatic hypotension first with non-drug measures: liberalised salt and fluid intake, waist-high compression garments, head-up sleeping and physical counter-pressure manoeuvres","Add pharmacotherapy where symptoms persist, using fludrocortisone, midodrine or droxidopa to raise standing blood pressure","Control coexisting supine hypertension by avoiding pressor agents at night, elevating the head of the bed and using a short-acting antihypertensive at bedtime if needed","Treat associated autonomic features including bladder and bowel dysfunction and erectile dysfunction","Educate on avoiding precipitants such as large meals, heat and rapid standing, and institute falls prevention","Review serially to detect phenoconversion to a central synucleinopathy such as Parkinson disease, dementia with Lewy bodies or multiple system atrophy","Manage within a specialist autonomic or neurology service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"pure_motor_hemiparesis":{"name":"pure motor hemiparesis","system":"nervous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pure motor stroke","pure motor lacunar syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest lacunar syndrome, classically caused by a small deep infarct in the posterior limb of the internal capsule or the basis pontis.","Weakness affects the face, arm and leg roughly equally on one side, without sensory loss, visual field defect, aphasia or neglect.","The complete absence of 'cortical' signs (aphasia, apraxia, agnosia, hemianopia, gaze deviation) is what distinguishes it from a cortical territory infarct.","Dysarthria and mild transient sensory symptoms may occur, but objective sensory loss argues against the pure motor label.","Small-vessel lipohyalinosis and microatheroma from chronic hypertension and diabetes are the dominant mechanisms rather than embolism.","A capsular warning syndrome, with stuttering, fluctuating or crescendo hemiparesis, heralds impending completed capsular infarction and warrants urgent attention.","CT is frequently normal acutely; diffusion-weighted MRI best demonstrates the small deep infarct and excludes mimics.","Long-term management centres on aggressive control of blood pressure, glycaemia and lipids plus antiplatelet therapy."],"pathophysiology":"Pure motor hemiparesis results from occlusion of a single small penetrating (perforating) artery supplying the corticospinal tract where its fibres are compactly arranged, most often the lenticulostriate branches to the posterior limb of the internal capsule or paramedian pontine perforators from the basilar artery. Chronic hypertension and diabetes drive lipohyalinosis and microatheromatous change within these end-arteries, producing a small (typically under 15 mm) ischaemic cavity or lacune. Because the lesion lies in white matter tracts distant from cortex, motor fibres to face, arm and leg are interrupted together while sensory, visual and higher cortical pathways are spared, giving the characteristic isolated motor deficit.","additionalDifferentials":["Cortical (large-vessel) infarct with predominant motor involvement","Small deep intracerebral haemorrhage in the capsule or pons","Sensorimotor lacunar stroke","Subdural haematoma or mass lesion compressing the internal capsule","Todd's paresis following a focal seizure","Demyelinating lesion of the corticospinal tract"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diffusion-weighted and T2/FLAIR MRI of the brain","Non-contrast CT head to exclude haemorrhage acutely","Vascular imaging of carotids and intracranial vessels to seek an alternative embolic source","ECG and prolonged rhythm monitoring for atrial fibrillation","Fasting glucose, HbA1c and lipid profile","Blood pressure profiling including ambulatory measurement"],"redFlags":["Stuttering or crescendo weakness suggesting capsular warning syndrome and impending completed infarct","Depressed conscious level or headache raising suspicion of haemorrhage","Rapid worsening with new cortical signs indicating larger-territory ischaemia","Fever or systemic sepsis suggesting an alternative or infective cause"],"prognosis":"Functional recovery is often good, with many patients regaining substantial motor function, but recurrent lacunar events and vascular cognitive impairment are common without rigorous risk-factor control. The capsular warning syndrome carries a high short-term risk of completed disabling infarction.","pitfalls":["Labelling a stroke 'pure motor' when subtle objective sensory loss or a visual field defect is present, which points instead to a larger lesion.","Assuming a normal acute CT excludes stroke and thereby missing a small deep infarct visible only on diffusion MRI.","Attributing all deficits to small-vessel disease and failing to screen for a treatable embolic source.","Overlooking a small capsular or pontine haemorrhage that can mimic the syndrome exactly.","Under-treating vascular risk factors because the initial deficit seems mild."],"management":["Deliver acute ischaemic stroke care on a stroke unit, offering intravenous thrombolysis when the patient presents within the treatment window and haemorrhage is excluded","Start antiplatelet therapy for secondary prevention once haemorrhage is excluded","Control vascular risk factors rigorously with blood-pressure lowering, statin therapy and management of diabetes, the dominant small-vessel mechanisms","Recognise the capsular warning syndrome of stuttering hemiparesis as high-risk, managing urgently with intensified antiplatelet therapy and close monitoring","Provide early multidisciplinary rehabilitation with physiotherapy and occupational therapy to regain motor function","Investigate for an alternative embolic source with vascular imaging and rhythm monitoring where the picture is atypical","Promote smoking cessation and other lifestyle modification to reduce recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Fisher CM. Lacunar strokes and infarcts (historical descriptions)"],"crossLinks":[]},"pure_motor_lacunar_syndrome":{"name":"Pure motor lacunar syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pure motor hemiparesis","pure motor stroke","lacunar pure motor syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest lacunar syndrome, caused by a small deep infarct from occlusion of a single penetrating arteriole","Classic localisations are the posterior limb of the internal capsule, the corona radiata, and the basis pontis","Produces contralateral weakness of the face, arm, and leg in roughly equal proportion without sensory, visual, or cortical signs","The absence of aphasia, neglect, or hemianopia helps distinguish it from a large cortical stroke","Underlying small-vessel disease is strongly linked to chronic hypertension and diabetes","A capsular warning syndrome of stuttering, fluctuating hemiparesis may herald a completed pure motor stroke","Sensation is preserved because the lesion spares the thalamic and lemniscal sensory pathways"],"pathophysiology":"Pure motor lacunar syndrome results from occlusion of a single small penetrating artery, typically due to lipohyalinosis or microatheroma from chronic hypertension and diabetes, producing a small deep infarct (lacune). The lesion interrupts the densely packed corticospinal fibres where they are concentrated in a small volume, most often the posterior limb of the internal capsule, the corona radiata, or the basis pontis. Because these sites carry motor fibres without accompanying sensory, visual, or higher cortical pathways, the deficit is a proportionate contralateral weakness of face, arm, and leg with no sensory loss, hemianopia, or cortical dysfunction, defining the pure motor pattern.","additionalDifferentials":["Ataxic hemiparesis (lacunar)","Sensorimotor lacunar syndrome","Small cortical infarct or early large-vessel stroke","Intracerebral haemorrhage in a deep location","Demyelinating or space-occupying lesion of the internal capsule","Todd paresis following a focal seizure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diffusion-weighted MRI to detect a small deep infarct not seen on CT","Assessment of vascular risk factors including blood pressure, glucose, and lipids","MR or CT angiography and carotid imaging to exclude a large-vessel or embolic source","ECG and cardiac evaluation to screen for an alternative embolic mechanism","Blood glucose and metabolic screen to exclude mimics such as hypoglycaemia","Repeat imaging where the initial scan is normal but symptoms fluctuate"],"redFlags":["Stuttering or progressive weakness suggesting a capsular warning syndrome","Any cortical sign such as aphasia or neglect indicating a non-lacunar mechanism","Reduced consciousness or headache raising the possibility of haemorrhage","Rapid deterioration implying a large-vessel occlusion rather than a lacune"],"prognosis":"Individual lacunar strokes often recover well with rehabilitation, but recurrent lacunes and progressive small-vessel disease can lead to cumulative disability, gait disorder, and vascular cognitive impairment.","pitfalls":["Assuming a lacunar mechanism without excluding a proximal large-vessel or cardioembolic source","Missing subtle cortical signs that would reclassify the stroke","Overlooking a capsular warning syndrome that may progress to fixed deficit","Relying on early CT, which frequently fails to show a small acute lacune"],"management":["Provide acute stroke unit care with intravenous thrombolysis where the patient is within the treatment window and haemorrhage has been excluded on imaging","Institute antiplatelet therapy for long-term secondary prevention","Aggressively manage the underlying small-vessel risk factors, especially hypertension, diabetes and dyslipidaemia","Treat a capsular warning syndrome of fluctuating hemiparesis as an emergency with intensified antiplatelet therapy and monitoring","Begin early multidisciplinary rehabilitation to maximise motor recovery","Exclude a large-vessel or cardioembolic source with vascular imaging, carotid assessment and cardiac rhythm monitoring","Support smoking cessation and lifestyle change to limit progression of small-vessel disease and vascular cognitive impairment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":["ataxic_hemiparesis","dysarthria_clumsy_hand_syndrome"]},"pure_red_cell_aplasia":{"name":"Pure red cell aplasia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PRCA","erythroblastopenia","acquired pure red cell aplasia","Diamond-Blackfan anaemia (congenital form)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A selective failure of erythropoiesis producing severe anaemia with reticulocytopenia while white cells and platelets are preserved","The bone marrow shows a marked reduction or absence of erythroid precursors with otherwise normal cellularity","Parvovirus B19 is a classic cause, especially in immunocompromised patients or those with a shortened red cell lifespan such as haemolytic anaemias","A strong association exists with thymoma, and cross-sectional chest imaging should be performed to look for a mediastinal mass","Other secondary causes include lymphoproliferative disorders (notably large granular lymphocytic leukaemia), autoimmune disease, drugs and pregnancy","Anti-erythropoietin antibodies are a recognised cause in patients previously treated with erythropoiesis-stimulating agents","Management addresses the underlying cause, for example thymectomy, antiviral immunoglobulin for parvovirus, or immunosuppression for immune-mediated disease"],"pathophysiology":"Pure red cell aplasia results from a selective arrest of erythroid precursor development in the bone marrow, sparing the granulocytic and megakaryocytic lineages. Several mechanisms converge on this outcome: direct viral cytotoxicity to erythroid progenitors by parvovirus B19, which infects them via the P antigen; T-cell or antibody-mediated immune attack on erythroid precursors as in thymoma-associated and large granular lymphocytic leukaemia-associated disease; and neutralising antibodies against erythropoietin or its receptor. The congenital form, Diamond-Blackfan anaemia, arises from ribosomal protein gene defects impairing erythroid maturation. The common endpoint is failed red cell production with preserved reticulocyte-poor anaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Aplastic anaemia (pancytopenia rather than isolated anaemia)","Myelodysplastic syndrome","Acute parvovirus B19 aplastic crisis in haemolytic anaemia","Anaemia of chronic disease or renal failure","Nutritional anaemia (B12 or folate deficiency)","Congenital Diamond-Blackfan anaemia in the young"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Reticulocyte count confirming reticulocytopenia","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine showing absent erythroid precursors","Parvovirus B19 PCR, particularly in immunocompromised patients","Cross-sectional chest imaging to detect thymoma","Peripheral blood immunophenotyping and T-cell clonality for large granular lymphocytic leukaemia","Autoimmune screen and review of drug and erythropoietin exposure"],"redFlags":["Profound symptomatic anaemia requiring urgent transfusion","A mediastinal mass on imaging suggesting thymoma","Features of an underlying lymphoproliferative or autoimmune disorder","Persistent aplasia despite treatment suggesting a missed cause"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the cause; parvovirus-related and drug-related cases often recover once the trigger is removed or treated, thymoma-associated disease may improve after thymectomy, and idiopathic or immune forms frequently need ongoing immunosuppression.","pitfalls":["Overlooking thymoma by not imaging the chest","Missing parvovirus B19 in an immunocompromised or haemolytic patient","Failing to identify an underlying large granular lymphocytic leukaemia","Not reviewing recent drugs or erythropoietin exposure as a reversible cause"],"management":["Treat the underlying cause: intravenous immunoglobulin for parvovirus B19-associated aplasia in immunocompromised patients, and withdraw any culprit drug or erythropoiesis-stimulating agent where anti-erythropoietin antibodies are implicated","Refer for thymectomy when an associated thymoma is identified, as removal may induce remission","For idiopathic or immune-mediated disease give immunosuppression such as corticosteroids, ciclosporin or ciclophosphamide, with T-cell-directed therapy for large granular lymphocytic leukaemia-associated cases","Provide supportive red cell transfusion for symptomatic anaemia","Institute iron chelation to manage transfusional iron overload","Monitor the reticulocyte count and haemoglobin to gauge treatment response and detect relapse","Refer to haematology for coordinated diagnosis and disease-specific management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"pure_sensory_stroke":{"name":"pure sensory stroke","system":"nervous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pure sensory lacunar syndrome","thalamic lacunar sensory stroke"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused most often by a small lacune in the ventral posterolateral or ventral posteromedial nucleus of the thalamus supplied by thalamogeniculate perforators.","Presents with unilateral numbness, tingling or a subjective 'heavy' or 'swollen' sensation of the face, arm, trunk and leg without weakness or cortical signs.","Involvement of all sensory modalities in a hemibody distribution that respects the midline is characteristic of a thalamic lesion.","Objective sensory findings can be strikingly subtle even when symptoms are prominent, so patient-reported distribution is diagnostically valuable.","Dejerine-Roussy thalamic pain syndrome may emerge weeks to months later as delayed, distressing central post-stroke pain in the affected region.","Motor power, visual fields, language and coordination are, by definition, preserved.","MRI with diffusion weighting is far more sensitive than CT for the small thalamic or lemniscal infarct.","Hypertension and diabetes-related small-vessel disease underlie most cases; a cardioembolic or large-artery source should still be excluded."],"pathophysiology":"Pure sensory stroke arises from occlusion of a small penetrating artery, classically a thalamogeniculate branch of the posterior cerebral artery supplying the ventral posterior thalamic nuclei, the principal relay for the spinothalamic and medial lemniscal pathways. A small lacune here interrupts crossed sensory fibres from the contralateral hemibody while sparing the internal capsule motor fibres, optic radiations and cortex. Less commonly the responsible lesion lies in the lemniscal pathway of the pons or in the parietal white matter. The underlying arteriopathy is typically hypertensive lipohyalinosis or microatheroma of end-arteries, producing the small deep cavity that defines a lacunar infarct.","additionalDifferentials":["Sensory seizure or migraine aura with marching paraesthesiae","Small thalamic haemorrhage","Cortical parietal infarct causing sensory loss with discriminative features","Peripheral or radicular sensory disturbance","Functional (non-organic) sensory symptoms","Multiple sclerosis plaque affecting sensory tracts"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diffusion-weighted and T2/FLAIR MRI focusing on the thalamus and brainstem","Non-contrast CT head to exclude thalamic haemorrhage","Carotid and intracranial vascular imaging","ECG and prolonged cardiac rhythm monitoring","HbA1c, fasting glucose and lipid profile","Blood pressure assessment including ambulatory monitoring"],"redFlags":["Reduced consciousness, headache or vomiting suggesting thalamic haemorrhage","Progressive extension of symptoms into motor or cortical domains","Severe delayed central pain heralding thalamic pain syndrome","Bilateral or fluctuating sensory disturbance suggesting an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Sensory symptoms often improve over weeks and the immediate functional outcome is usually favourable, but a minority develop chronic, treatment-resistant central post-stroke (thalamic) pain, and recurrent small-vessel events remain a risk without risk-factor control.","pitfalls":["Dismissing prominent subjective numbness as functional because objective signs are minimal.","Missing a small thalamic haemorrhage that can present identically to the ischaemic syndrome.","Failing to warn patients about, or later recognise, delayed thalamic pain.","Relying on an early normal CT and not proceeding to diffusion MRI.","Neglecting embolic-source screening on the assumption of pure small-vessel disease."],"management":["In the hyperacute phase consider intravenous thrombolysis if the patient presents within the treatment window and haemorrhage has been excluded on imaging","Start antiplatelet therapy such as aspirin followed by clopidogrel for secondary prevention of this small-vessel lacunar stroke","Aggressively control hypertension, the underlying driver of the lipohyalinosis, to reduce recurrent lacunar events","Add a statin and optimise diabetes and lifestyle risk factors","Treat delayed central post-stroke (Dejerine-Roussy) thalamic pain with agents such as amitriptyline, gabapentinoids or duloxetine rather than simple analgesics","Refer for physiotherapy and occupational and sensory rehabilitation","Arrange prolonged cardiac rhythm monitoring to detect atrial fibrillation and guide anticoagulation decisions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Caplan's Stroke: A Clinical Approach"],"crossLinks":[]},"purine_nucleoside_phosphorylase_deficiency":{"name":"Purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiency","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PNP deficiency","PNP","purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiency","T-cell immunodeficiency with neurological disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification of inborn errors of immunity","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A metabolic combined immunodeficiency in which a predominantly T-cell defect is accompanied by prominent, and often presenting, neurological disease","Neurological features include developmental delay, spasticity, motor abnormalities and ataxia, which may precede overt infection","Recurrent and opportunistic infections reflect progressive T-cell depletion, while B-cell numbers are relatively preserved though antibody function may decline over time","Autoimmune phenomena such as autoimmune haemolytic anaemia and thrombocytopenia are frequently associated","A characteristic biochemical clue is markedly low serum and urinary uric acid due to the metabolic block, which can prompt the diagnosis","It is closely related to adenosine deaminase deficiency as one of the two classic purine salvage pathway defects causing immunodeficiency","Inheritance is autosomal recessive from biallelic loss-of-function variants in the PNP gene","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation can correct the immunodeficiency but established neurological damage is not reversed"],"pathophysiology":"Purine nucleoside phosphorylase is an enzyme of the purine salvage pathway that catalyses the breakdown of inosine, guanosine and deoxyguanosine. Its deficiency causes accumulation of deoxyguanosine triphosphate, which is particularly toxic to developing and dividing T lymphocytes, producing a progressive T-cell lymphopenia and combined immunodeficiency while sparing B-cell numbers more than adenosine deaminase deficiency. The same toxic metabolites are thought to injure the central nervous system, accounting for the developmental and motor abnormalities. The metabolic block also diverts purines away from uric acid production, giving the characteristic hypouricaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency","Other forms of severe combined immunodeficiency","Ataxia-telangiectasia","Cerebral palsy of other cause","Inherited metabolic and mitochondrial disorders with neurological involvement","Idiopathic CD4 lymphopenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and urinary uric acid, characteristically very low","Red-cell or leucocyte PNP enzyme activity assay","Lymphocyte subsets showing progressive T-cell depletion with relatively preserved B cells","Serum immunoglobulins and specific antibody responses","Autoantibody screen and direct antiglobulin test","PNP gene sequencing for biallelic loss-of-function variants"],"redFlags":["Opportunistic or disseminated infection indicating advanced T-cell failure","Progressive neurological deterioration","Severe autoimmune cytopenia","Complications from live vaccine exposure before diagnosis","Development of lymphoma or other malignancy"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is poor without treatment, with death from infection in childhood; haematopoietic stem cell transplantation can restore immune function, but the neurological impairment already present tends to persist and shapes the long-term outcome.","pitfalls":["Attributing the neurological signs to cerebral palsy and overlooking the underlying immunodeficiency","Not checking uric acid, thereby missing a readily available diagnostic clue","Confusing it with adenosine deaminase deficiency without enzyme-specific testing","Administering live vaccines or non-irradiated blood products before the diagnosis is made","Assuming transplantation will reverse established neurological damage"],"management":["Offer allogeneic haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation as the only definitive treatment able to restore cellular immunity","Give infection prophylaxis against opportunistic organisms including co-trimoxazole for Pneumocystis with antifungal and antiviral cover, and immunoglobulin replacement where antibody function declines","Use only irradiated, cytomegalovirus-safe, leucodepleted blood products and withhold live vaccines given the T-cell defect","Treat associated autoimmune haemolytic anaemia and cytopenias with corticosteroids and further immunosuppression","Provide neurological supportive care for spasticity, developmental delay and seizures through paediatric neurology and therapy services","Monitor for intercurrent and opportunistic infection and, after transplantation, track immune reconstitution","Refer for genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"purine_nucleoside_phosphorylase_immunodeficiency":{"name":"Purine Nucleoside Phosphorylase Deficiency","system":"Immunology / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PNP deficiency","PNP immunodeficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal recessive purine-salvage enzyme defect causing a T-cell-predominant combined immunodeficiency, distinct from adenosine deaminase deficiency.","The combination of progressive cellular immunodeficiency with prominent neurological impairment is the clinical signature that should prompt testing.","Neurological features (developmental delay, spasticity, ataxia, movement disorder) affect the majority and may precede overt infections.","Autoimmune phenomena, particularly autoimmune haemolytic anaemia and cytopenias, are characteristically associated.","A markedly low serum uric acid (hypouricaemia) is a valuable and inexpensive biochemical clue reflecting the blocked purine pathway.","T-cell numbers and function decline progressively, while B cells and immunoglobulins are relatively preserved early, unlike classical severe combined immunodeficiency.","Accumulated deoxyguanosine triphosphate is toxic to developing thymocytes and underlies the selective T-cell depletion.","Haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation can correct the immunodeficiency but does not reverse established neurological damage, so early diagnosis is critical."],"pathophysiology":"Purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiency results from biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the PNP gene, encoding an enzyme of the purine salvage pathway that catalyses the phosphorolysis of inosine, deoxyinosine, guanosine and deoxyguanosine. Its absence leads to accumulation of these substrates and, critically, to a build-up of deoxyguanosine that is phosphorylated within cells, especially by deoxycytidine kinase in immature T lymphocytes, to deoxyguanosine triphosphate. High intracellular deoxyGTP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and is selectively toxic to developing thymocytes, producing a progressive, T-cell-predominant lymphopenia; because the salvage burden falls disproportionately on the T lineage, B cells and immunoglobulin production are comparatively spared in the early course, distinguishing the biochemical phenotype from adenosine deaminase deficiency. The blocked pathway also diverts less purine to the terminal step catalysed by xanthine oxidase, so urate generation falls and serum and urinary uric acid are characteristically low. Neurotoxicity, thought to reflect accumulation of guanosine-derived metabolites in the central nervous system, produces the prominent spasticity, developmental delay, ataxia and movement disorder, and immune dysregulation predisposes to autoimmune cytopenias; over time the combined immune deficit renders patients vulnerable to viral, fungal and opportunistic infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency","Other forms of severe combined immunodeficiency","Ataxia-telangiectasia","Combined immunodeficiency with predominant neurological disease","Autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome (for autoimmune cytopenias)","Cerebral palsy or inherited metabolic encephalopathy (for the neurology)","HIV-related immunodeficiency in an infant"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and urinary uric acid demonstrating characteristic hypouricaemia","PNP enzyme activity assay in erythrocytes","PNP gene sequencing for confirmation","Lymphocyte subsets showing progressive T-cell lymphopenia with relatively preserved B cells","Serum immunoglobulins and specific antibody responses","Neuroimaging and developmental assessment for the neurological component","Direct antiglobulin test and blood film when autoimmune cytopenia is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive neurological deterioration in an infant with recurrent infection","Opportunistic or disseminated viral or fungal infection indicating advancing T-cell failure","Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia or severe cytopenia","Administration of live vaccines or non-irradiated blood products in a child with combined immunodeficiency","Failure to thrive with lymphopenia and unexplained low uric acid"],"prognosis":"Untreated, the disorder is usually fatal in childhood from infection or neurological decline. Haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation can restore immune function, but neurological deficits present at transplant generally persist, so outcome hinges on early recognition and intervention.","pitfalls":["Assuming all purine-salvage combined immunodeficiency is adenosine deaminase deficiency and not testing PNP","Attributing the neurological picture to cerebral palsy and overlooking the underlying immunodeficiency","Missing the diagnostic clue of a low uric acid on routine biochemistry","Transfusing non-irradiated cellular blood products, risking transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease","Expecting transplantation to reverse established neurological damage and delaying referral"],"management":["Pursue allogeneic haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation, the only intervention capable of correcting the progressive T-cell immunodeficiency","Provide antimicrobial prophylaxis covering Pneumocystis with co-trimoxazole plus antifungal and antiviral agents, and immunoglobulin replacement as humoral function fails","Transfuse only irradiated, cytomegalovirus-safe, leucodepleted blood products and withhold live vaccines","Manage the characteristic autoimmune haemolytic anaemia and cytopenias with corticosteroids and additional immunosuppression","Support the neurological component of developmental delay, spasticity and movement disorder with neurology input and physical, occupational and speech therapy","Monitor lymphocyte subsets, immunoglobulins and for opportunistic infection, and track immune reconstitution after transplantation","Arrange genetic counselling and testing of at-risk relatives for this autosomal recessive PNP defect"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical immunology textbook","Inborn errors of metabolism reference","International primary immunodeficiency classification guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"purtscher_retinopathy":{"name":"Purtscher retinopathy","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Purtscher's retinopathy","traumatic retinal angiopathy","Purtscher-like retinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically follows severe trauma, particularly head injury, chest compression or long-bone fracture, with sudden visual loss developing shortly afterwards","The non-traumatic Purtscher-like form accompanies acute pancreatitis, renal failure, connective tissue disease, amniotic fluid embolism and childbirth","The fundus shows multiple cotton-wool spots and areas of superficial retinal whitening clustered around the optic disc, frequently bilateral","Purtscher flecken, polygonal patches of inner retinal whitening lying between arterioles and venules with a clear zone flanking the vessels, are highly characteristic","Retinal haemorrhages are usually few relative to the extent of ischaemic whitening","Fluorescein angiography demonstrates arteriolar and capillary non-perfusion in the affected areas","There is no proven ocular treatment, so care is directed at the underlying systemic cause and supportive observation"],"pathophysiology":"The retinopathy results from occlusion of retinal arterioles and precapillary vessels by microemboli, which may consist of fat, air, fibrin or aggregated leucocytes. Complement activation with generation of C5a is thought to cause leucocyte aggregation and leucoembolisation, a mechanism particularly implicated in acute pancreatitis. The resulting precapillary arteriolar occlusion produces the ischaemic cotton-wool spots and polygonal Purtscher flecken seen around the posterior pole.","additionalDifferentials":["Commotio retinae","Central retinal artery occlusion","Non-arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy","Hypertensive retinopathy","Pre-eclampsia or HELLP-associated retinopathy","Fat embolism syndrome","Non-accidental injury with retinal haemorrhages in a child"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination to identify cotton-wool spots and Purtscher flecken","Fluorescein angiography to map arteriolar occlusion and capillary non-perfusion","Optical coherence tomography to document inner retinal ischaemic changes","Serum amylase and lipase when pancreatitis is suspected","Renal function tests and an autoimmune screen for a systemic precipitant","Detailed history for recent trauma or an obstetric or systemic trigger"],"redFlags":["Bilateral severe visual loss","An underlying life-threatening cause such as acute pancreatitis or systemic embolism","Associated systemic decompensation requiring urgent medical care","Suspicion of non-accidental injury in a child"],"prognosis":"The visual outcome is variable; some eyes recover partially over weeks to months as the ischaemic whitening resolves, while others are left with permanent central or field loss and subsequent optic atrophy.","pitfalls":["Missing the underlying systemic precipitant in the non-traumatic form","Attributing the picture to direct ocular trauma alone","Confusing the retinal whitening with commotio retinae","Failing to recognise Purtscher flecken as a distinctive sign"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying precipitant, stabilising severe trauma and managing acute pancreatitis, renal failure or connective tissue disease that triggered the leucoembolisation","Recognise there is no proven ocular-specific therapy; systemic corticosteroids are sometimes tried but remain of unproven benefit","Adopt a period of observation, since ischaemic retinal whitening and vision may recover partially as the occlusion resolves","Monitor with serial fundus examination, optical coherence tomography and fluorescein angiography to track resolution and areas of non-perfusion","Watch for and counsel about permanent central or field loss and subsequent optic atrophy","Coordinate care between ophthalmology and the team managing the systemic cause"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course: Retina and Vitreous"],"crossLinks":[]},"pustular_psoriasis_of_pregnancy":{"name":"pustular psoriasis of pregnancy","system":"skin","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["impetigo herpetiformis","generalised pustular psoriasis of pregnancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Regarded as generalised pustular psoriasis presenting in pregnancy; the historical name impetigo herpetiformis is a misnomer as it is neither infective nor herpetic.","Typically presents in the third trimester with erythematous patches fringed by sterile subcorneal pustules that extend centrifugally, often starting in the flexures.","Systemic upset is characteristic, with fever, malaise, delirium, and features of hypocalcaemia such as tetany, and it can be life-threatening.","Unlike the other pregnancy dermatoses it is often not markedly itchy; pain, burning and systemic illness dominate.","Hypocalcaemia, hypoalbuminaemia and secondary hypoparathyroidism may occur and require correction.","It carries a genuine risk of placental insufficiency, stillbirth and neonatal death, so fetal surveillance is essential.","First-line treatment is systemic corticosteroid; ciclosporin is an option in resistant disease and delivery may be needed if the fetus is compromised at term.","It tends to recur in subsequent pregnancies, often with earlier onset and greater severity."],"pathophysiology":"Pustular psoriasis of pregnancy is a variant of generalised pustular psoriasis in which the pregnant state acts as a trigger, and a subset of patients carry mutations in IL36RN encoding the interleukin-36 receptor antagonist, leading to unopposed interleukin-36 signalling and intense neutrophilic inflammation. This produces the characteristic sterile spongiform pustules of Kogoj within the epidermis. Contributory factors include the relative hypocalcaemia and reduced parathyroid hormone activity of pregnancy and possible hormonal influences. The neutrophil-rich sterile pustulation and systemic inflammatory response account for the fever, leucocytosis and constitutional illness, while placental dysfunction underlies the associated fetal risk.","additionalDifferentials":["acute generalised exanthematous pustulosis","subcorneal pustular dermatosis (Sneddon-Wilkinson disease)","pemphigoid gestationis","polymorphic eruption of pregnancy","generalised pustular psoriasis unrelated to pregnancy","widespread bacterial or candidal pustular infection","drug reaction with pustulation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["skin biopsy (spongiform pustules of Kogoj, parakeratosis and neutrophilic infiltrate)","serum calcium, albumin, phosphate and parathyroid hormone","full blood count and inflammatory markers (leucocytosis, raised C-reactive protein and ESR)","swabs and pustule culture to confirm sterility and exclude infection","renal and liver function tests","fetal surveillance with cardiotocography and growth ultrasound; consider IL36RN genetic testing"],"redFlags":["high fever, rigors, delirium or haemodynamic instability indicating systemic sepsis-like decompensation","tetany, perioral or peripheral paraesthesiae or seizures from hypocalcaemia","reduced fetal movements, growth restriction or abnormal cardiotocography signalling placental insufficiency","rapidly extending confluent pustulation with skin failure and fluid or electrolyte loss","signs of secondary bacterial infection or septicaemia"],"prognosis":"With prompt systemic treatment, correction of electrolyte disturbance and fetal monitoring, maternal outcome is usually good, but there is a real risk of stillbirth, fetal growth restriction and neonatal death from placental insufficiency. It characteristically recurs in later pregnancies, frequently earlier and more severely.","pitfalls":["Mistaking sterile pustules for infection and delaying corticosteroid while pursuing antibiotics.","Failing to measure and correct calcium, risking tetany and seizures from unrecognised hypocalcaemia.","Underestimating fetal risk and omitting fetal surveillance, missing placental insufficiency.","Attributing the eruption to a benign pregnancy dermatosis and undertreating a potentially fatal condition.","Abruptly stopping systemic corticosteroid after delivery, precipitating a rebound flare."],"management":["Commence systemic corticosteroids as first-line therapy to control the sterile pustular eruption, escalating to ciclosporin or a biologic such as infliximab or the interleukin-36 receptor inhibitor spesolimab in refractory or severe disease","Correct hypocalcaemia, hypoalbuminaemia and secondary hypoparathyroidism with calcium, vitamin D and albumin support","Provide supportive care with fluid and electrolyte balance, temperature regulation, emollients and topical corticosteroids, and treat any secondary skin infection","Institute intensive foetal surveillance with cardiotocography and growth ultrasound because of the risk of placental insufficiency, and plan timely delivery, which often abates the disease","Monitor calcium, albumin, renal function and inflammatory markers while watching for systemic deterioration and delirium","Manage jointly across dermatology, obstetrics and neonatology","Counsel that the condition characteristically recurs in later pregnancies and may be provoked by oestrogen-containing contraception"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Ambros-Rudolph CM et al. The specific dermatoses of pregnancy revisited and reclassified, Journal of the American Academy of Dermatology","British Journal of Dermatology reviews on generalised pustular psoriasis and IL36RN"],"crossLinks":[]},"pycnodysostosis":{"name":"Pycnodysostosis (CTSK)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pyknodysostosis","cathepsin K deficiency","CTSK-related osteosclerosis","Toulouse-Lautrec syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism (ASBMR)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive osteosclerosis caused by loss-of-function mutations in CTSK, encoding the osteoclast enzyme cathepsin K","Osteoclasts are present but cannot degrade bone matrix collagen, so bone becomes dense yet abnormally brittle and prone to fracture","Clinical features include short stature, a large head with open or delayed-closing fontanelles, frontal and occipital bossing, and a small receding chin","Acro-osteolysis of the distal phalanges produces short, stubby fingers, and the nails are often dysplastic","Dental anomalies, a high-arched palate, and obstructive sleep apnoea from midface hypoplasia are common","Distinguished from osteopetrosis by the brittle bone, acro-osteolysis, and typically preserved marrow function without the severe cytopenias of malignant osteopetrosis","Management is supportive with fracture prevention and orthopaedic care; cathepsin K biology has informed development of antiresorptive drug targets"],"pathophysiology":"Pycnodysostosis results from biallelic mutations in CTSK, the gene encoding cathepsin K, a cysteine protease secreted by osteoclasts that degrades the organic collagen matrix of bone during resorption. Because the enzyme is defective, osteoclasts can demineralise but cannot properly break down the collagenous matrix, leaving behind poorly remodelled, densely mineralised but structurally weak bone. This uncoupling of mineral removal from matrix degradation explains the paradox of a sclerotic, radiodense skeleton that fractures readily. Impaired remodelling at the acral skeleton produces the characteristic acro-osteolysis, and defective skull remodelling underlies the delayed fontanelle closure and craniofacial features.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteopetrosis","Cleidocranial dysplasia","Sclerosteosis and Van Buchem disease","Idiopathic acro-osteolysis (Hajdu-Cheney syndrome)","Osteogenesis imperfecta given the fracture tendency","Craniometaphyseal dysplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs showing generalised osteosclerosis, acro-osteolysis of distal phalanges, and open fontanelles","Skull imaging for delayed suture closure, wormian bones, and obtuse mandibular angle","Genetic testing for CTSK mutations","Full blood count to distinguish from osteopetrosis with marrow failure","Dental and orthodontic assessment","Sleep study when obstructive sleep apnoea is suspected"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or non-healing fractures, particularly of the long bones and mandible","Severe obstructive sleep apnoea causing hypoxaemia","Basilar skull deformity or neurological compromise","Signs suggesting marrow failure that would point instead to osteopetrosis"],"prognosis":"Lifespan is usually normal, but patients face recurrent fragility fractures, orthopaedic deformity, dental complications, and potential airway problems that require long-term multidisciplinary management.","pitfalls":["Assuming dense bone is strong and underestimating the high fracture risk","Confusing it with osteopetrosis and expecting marrow failure or with osteogenesis imperfecta on the basis of fractures alone","Overlooking acro-osteolysis and delayed fontanelle closure as diagnostic clues","Neglecting airway and dental complications during anaesthesia and routine care"],"management":["There is no specific drug therapy; management centres on preventing and treating recurrent fragility fractures with careful orthopaedic fixation adapted to the dense, brittle bone","Provide regular dental and orthodontic care and monitor for mandibular osteomyelitis and complications of the high-arched palate","Investigate and treat obstructive sleep apnoea arising from midface hypoplasia with a sleep study, ENT assessment and continuous positive airway pressure where indicated","Consider a supervised trial of recombinant growth hormone for short stature under specialist guidance","Undertake periodic skeletal surveillance for deformity and progressive acro-osteolysis","Coordinate long-term care across metabolic bone, orthopaedic, dental and airway specialists","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive CTSK disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism (ASBMR)","GeneReviews / peer-reviewed reviews of pycnodysostosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"pyle_disease":{"name":"Pyle disease (metaphyseal dysplasia, SFRP4)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pyle metaphyseal dysplasia","familial metaphyseal dysplasia","SFRP4","Erlenmeyer flask deformity dysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Radiographic hallmark is marked flaring and undermodelling of the metaphyses, producing the classic Erlenmeyer-flask deformity, most obvious at the distal femur","In contrast to craniometaphyseal dysplasia, the skull and facial bones are relatively spared, and cranial-nerve compression is not a prominent feature","Caused by autosomal recessive loss-of-function variants in SFRP4, a secreted WNT antagonist important for bone modelling","The widened metaphyses have thin cortices, so bone can be fragile and fractures may occur despite the expanded appearance","Clinical features are often mild and may include genu valgum, mild limb deformity and joint discomfort, with many individuals otherwise well","Dental problems and mild vertebral changes can occur, but intelligence and life expectancy are typically normal","Management is orthopaedic and supportive, addressing limb deformity and fractures, since the disorder is comparatively benign"],"pathophysiology":"Pyle disease results from biallelic loss-of-function variants in SFRP4, which encodes a secreted frizzled-related protein that normally antagonises WNT signalling and helps regulate the modelling of bone during growth. Loss of this antagonist disturbs the coordinated resorption and shaping that narrows the metaphysis into the diaphysis, so the metaphyses remain broad and undermodelled, giving the Erlenmeyer-flask appearance. The expanded metaphyseal bone has thinned cortices, accounting for a degree of fragility despite the bulky radiographic appearance. Because the skull is relatively spared compared with the craniotubular dysplasias, the disorder lacks the severe cranial-nerve and craniofacial complications seen in those conditions.","additionalDifferentials":["Craniometaphyseal dysplasia","Frontometaphyseal dysplasia","Osteopetrosis (which can also cause an Erlenmeyer-flask deformity)","Gaucher disease with metaphyseal remodelling failure","Multiple hereditary exostoses","Dysosteosclerosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs demonstrating the Erlenmeyer-flask metaphyseal flaring, especially at the knees","Skeletal survey to assess the extent of metaphyseal undermodelling and cortical thinning","Skull imaging to confirm relative sparing and distinguish from craniometaphyseal dysplasia","Assessment of limb alignment for genu valgum and deformity","SFRP4 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Dental evaluation where dental anomalies are present"],"redFlags":["Fractures through thinned metaphyseal cortices despite the expanded bone","Progressive limb deformity impairing function and requiring orthopaedic correction","Atypical skull involvement or cranial-nerve symptoms prompting reconsideration towards craniometaphyseal dysplasia","Features suggesting an alternative marrow-storage or sclerosing disorder"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally good, with normal intelligence and life expectancy; morbidity relates mainly to limb deformity and occasional fractures rather than the neurological complications of the craniotubular dysplasias.","pitfalls":["Confusing Pyle disease with craniometaphyseal dysplasia, which has prominent skull involvement and cranial-nerve compression","Assuming the bulky metaphyses are strong when the thin cortices predispose to fracture","Attributing an Erlenmeyer-flask deformity solely to Pyle disease without considering osteopetrosis or Gaucher disease","Overlooking the autosomal recessive inheritance when counselling families"],"management":["No disease-specific treatment exists; care is supportive because most individuals are only mildly affected","Refer for orthopaedic correction of genu valgum and limb deformity, with corrective osteotomy where alignment is significantly disturbed","Manage fractures with awareness that the expanded metaphyses have thin, fragile cortices","Provide simple analgesia and physiotherapy for joint discomfort","Arrange dental evaluation where dental anomalies are present","Monitor limb alignment and growth periodically during childhood","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive SFRP4 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders","GeneReviews: SFRP4-related Pyle disease"],"crossLinks":["frontometaphyseal_dysplasia"]},"pyloric_stenosis":{"name":"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","IHPS","pyloric stenosis","congenital pyloric stenosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hypertrophy of the pyloric muscle causing gastric outlet obstruction, typically presenting between 3 and 6 weeks of age and more common in firstborn males.","The hallmark is progressive, forceful non-bilious (projectile) vomiting shortly after feeds, with the infant remaining hungry and eager to feed again.","The classic metabolic picture is a hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis from loss of gastric acid and chloride.","A palpable olive-shaped mass in the epigastrium/right upper quadrant and visible gastric peristalsis are classic but not always present.","Ultrasound is the diagnostic investigation of choice, demonstrating a thickened and elongated pyloric muscle.","The vomiting is characteristically non-bilious, because the obstruction is proximal to the ampulla of Vater; bilious vomiting should prompt consideration of other diagnoses.","Initial management is medical resuscitation: correcting dehydration and the electrolyte and acid-base disturbance before any operation.","Definitive treatment is surgical pyloromyotomy, which is not an emergency and should follow adequate fluid and electrolyte correction."],"pathophysiology":"Progressive hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the circular smooth muscle of the pylorus narrows and elongates the pyloric channel, obstructing gastric emptying. The exact cause is unknown but involves genetic and environmental factors. Persistent vomiting of gastric contents leads to loss of hydrogen and chloride ions, producing a hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis; ongoing losses and renal compensation drive potassium loss and, later, paradoxical aciduria. Fluid losses cause dehydration. The obstruction is proximal to the biliary drainage, so vomitus is non-bilious.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastro-oesophageal reflux","Overfeeding","Malrotation with volvulus (bilious vomiting)","Gastroenteritis","Cow's milk protein intolerance","Metabolic disorders or raised intracranial pressure causing vomiting","Adrenal insufficiency (salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal ultrasound to measure pyloric muscle thickness and channel length","Serum electrolytes, chloride, and bicarbonate","Blood gas to characterise the alkalosis","Assessment of hydration status and weight","Blood glucose"],"redFlags":["Signs of significant dehydration or shock","Severe electrolyte or acid-base derangement","Bilious vomiting, which points away from pyloric stenosis toward a surgical emergency such as volvulus","Lethargy or reduced responsiveness from dehydration and electrolyte imbalance"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent after appropriate resuscitation and pyloromyotomy, with prompt recovery and no long-term gastrointestinal sequelae.","pitfalls":["Rushing to surgery before correcting fluid and electrolyte abnormalities","Mistaking the illness for simple reflux or overfeeding and delaying diagnosis","Overlooking that vomiting is characteristically non-bilious, and failing to reconsider the diagnosis if it is bilious","Underestimating the degree of dehydration and metabolic alkalosis"],"management":["Correct dehydration and the hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with intravenous fluid and potassium replacement before any operation, as surgery is never an emergency until the infant is rehydrated and biochemically corrected","Decompress the stomach with a nasogastric tube and keep the infant nil by mouth pending surgery","Perform definitive Ramstedt pyloromyotomy once resuscitation is complete","Reintroduce graded enteral feeds postoperatively as tolerated","Monitor serial electrolytes, chloride, bicarbonate and hydration until normalised","Refer promptly to paediatric surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"pyomyositis":{"name":"Pyomyositis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tropical pyomyositis","bacterial myositis","primary muscle abscess","suppurative myositis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A primary bacterial infection of skeletal muscle that forms an intramuscular abscess, most often affecting large muscles such as the thigh, buttock, or trunk","Staphylococcus aureus, including community-acquired MRSA, is the leading cause","Classically described in tropical regions but increasingly recognised in temperate climates, especially in diabetics and immunocompromised or HIV-positive patients","Evolves through an early invasive phase of vague muscle ache and low-grade fever, a suppurative phase with abscess and localised signs, and a late septic phase","Early disease is easily missed because overlying skin often looks normal and superficial fluctuance is absent","MRI is the most sensitive imaging modality for detecting muscle inflammation and abscess formation","Management principles combine antistaphylococcal antibiotics with drainage of any abscess, either percutaneously or surgically","Blood cultures are frequently positive and multiple muscle sites can be involved, so a thorough search is warranted"],"pathophysiology":"Pyomyositis is a suppurative infection arising within skeletal muscle, thought to require transient bacteraemia together with muscle that has been rendered susceptible by trauma, strenuous exercise, or underlying compromise. Skeletal muscle is normally resistant to infection, but injury allows haematogenously seeded bacteria to establish a focus that progresses from diffuse inflammation to a walled-off intramuscular abscess. Without drainage the infection can extend, cause systemic sepsis, and, if untreated, seed distant sites.","additionalDifferentials":["Deep vein thrombosis of the affected limb","Muscle haematoma or strain","Cellulitis or deep soft-tissue infection","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis of an adjacent bone","Necrotising fasciitis in the septic phase"],"infectionMimics":["Muscle contusion or exertional injury","Deep vein thrombosis presenting with limb swelling and pain","Bone tumour or soft-tissue sarcoma","Inflammatory myopathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI to localise muscle inflammation and abscess; ultrasound or CT as alternatives","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers","Aspiration or drainage of collected pus for Gram stain and culture","Creatine kinase, which is often only mildly elevated despite muscle involvement","Screening for diabetes, HIV, or other immunosuppression"],"redFlags":["Signs of septic shock in the late phase requiring urgent resuscitation","Rapidly spreading pain and toxicity raising concern for necrotising fasciitis needing surgical exploration","Extension into adjacent joint or bone suggesting septic arthritis or osteomyelitis","Large or multiple abscesses requiring prompt drainage"],"prognosis":"With timely antibiotics and adequate drainage the outlook is generally good; delayed diagnosis, extensive or multifocal disease, and progression to septic shock worsen outcomes, and immunocompromised patients are at higher risk of complications.","pitfalls":["Dismissing early muscle pain with normal-looking skin as a simple strain","Relying on creatine kinase, which may be near normal, to exclude the diagnosis","Treating with antibiotics alone when an abscess needs drainage","Failing to look for additional muscle foci or an underlying immunocompromising condition"],"management":["Start empirical intravenous antistaphylococcal antibiotics with cover for meticillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus such as vancomycin, then narrow according to culture and sensitivities","Achieve source control by image-guided percutaneous or surgical drainage of any intramuscular abscess","Provide supportive analgesia and, in the septic phase, sepsis resuscitation with fluids and vasopressors and intensive care support for septic shock","Identify and manage predisposing immunocompromise by screening for and treating diabetes and HIV","Monitor clinical response, inflammatory markers and repeat imaging to confirm resolution and detect further collections","Continue a prolonged antibiotic course guided by response, stepping down to oral therapy once the infection is controlled"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"pyrimidine_5_nucleotidase_deficiency":{"name":"Pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase deficiency","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["P5N deficiency","P5'N-1 deficiency","pyrimidine 5-prime nucleotidase deficiency","NT5C3A gene"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["One of the commoner enzyme defects of red-cell nucleotide metabolism, causing chronic non-spherocytic, Coombs-negative haemolytic anaemia","The blood film hallmark is prominent coarse basophilic stippling, an unusual finding for an inherited haemolytic anaemia","Because lead inhibits this same enzyme, the picture closely mimics lead poisoning and the two must be distinguished","Inherited as an autosomal recessive trait, so consanguinity or affected siblings may be present","Retained pyrimidine nucleotides accumulate in the red cell and can be detected as an abnormal nucleotide profile","Diagnosis rests on reduced enzyme activity on assay together with the characteristic stippling and NT5C3A mutation testing","Management is supportive with folate and transfusion when needed; the anaemia is usually mild to moderate and splenectomy gives limited benefit"],"pathophysiology":"Pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase dephosphorylates pyrimidine nucleotides during the degradation of RNA as reticulocytes mature, allowing the breakdown products to leave the cell. When the enzyme is deficient, pyrimidine nucleotides accumulate within the red cell, aggregate with ribosomal remnants to produce the coarse basophilic stippling seen on the film, and disturb nucleotide balance and glycolytic metabolism, shortening cell survival and causing a chronic non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia. Because lead directly inhibits the enzyme, lead intoxication reproduces the same biochemical and morphological picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Lead poisoning","Thalassaemia and other causes of basophilic stippling","Pyruvate kinase deficiency","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","Sideroblastic anaemias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Red-cell pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase enzyme assay","Blood film examination for coarse basophilic stippling","Blood lead concentration to exclude lead poisoning","Red-cell nucleotide profile showing pyrimidine accumulation","Haemolysis screen with bilirubin, lactate dehydrogenase and haptoglobin","Molecular testing of the NT5C3A gene"],"redFlags":["Failure to exclude lead poisoning, which is treatable and potentially serious","Aplastic crisis after parvovirus B19 infection","Symptomatic anaemia requiring transfusion","Transfusional iron overload in more severe disease"],"prognosis":"The anaemia is typically mild to moderate and lifelong but well tolerated, with a generally good prognosis once lead poisoning has been excluded.","pitfalls":["Failing to check a blood lead level and missing treatable lead intoxication that mimics the disorder","Attributing basophilic stippling to thalassaemia without considering an enzyme defect","Expecting splenectomy to substantially improve the anaemia","Overlooking the abnormal red-cell nucleotide profile that supports the diagnosis"],"management":["No specific therapy exists; management is supportive for the chronic non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia, which is usually mild to moderate and well tolerated","Exclude and, if present, treat coexisting lead poisoning with chelation, since lead inhibits the same enzyme and reproduces the picture","Give folic acid supplementation to meet the demands of chronic haemolysis","Reserve red cell transfusion for episodes of severe or poorly tolerated anaemia, noting that splenectomy is generally of little benefit in this disorder","Monitor for and manage pigment gallstones and iron overload","Offer genetic counselling and family testing for this autosomal recessive NT5C3A defect"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Wintrobe's Clinical Hematology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pyruvate_carboxylase_deficiency":{"name":"Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PC deficiency","PCD","pyruvate carboxylase enzyme deficiency","PC gene deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder caused by variants in the PC gene, encoding the biotin-dependent mitochondrial enzyme pyruvate carboxylase","The enzyme converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate, an anaplerotic step essential for gluconeogenesis, the citric acid cycle and neurotransmitter synthesis","Presents with lactic acidosis, often accompanied by hypoglycaemia, and a raised lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","The severe neonatal (type B, often French) form causes overwhelming lactic acidosis, hyperammonaemia, citrullinaemia and early death","The milder infantile (type A, North American) form causes developmental delay, seizures and recurrent metabolic acidosis with lactic acidaemia","A characteristic biochemical pattern includes raised lactate, ketones, ammonia, citrulline and lysine with low aspartate","Anaplerotic therapy and avoidance of fasting form the mainstay of supportive management, but outcomes are generally poor","Because pyruvate carboxylase is biotin-dependent, it should be distinguished from biotinidase and holocarboxylase synthetase deficiencies"],"pathophysiology":"Pyruvate carboxylase catalyses the ATP-dependent, biotin-requiring carboxylation of pyruvate to oxaloacetate within mitochondria. This reaction replenishes citric acid cycle intermediates (anaplerosis) and provides oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis and, in the brain, for aspartate and glutamate synthesis. Enzyme deficiency blocks gluconeogenesis, causing fasting hypoglycaemia, and diverts pyruvate towards lactate, producing lactic acidosis with an elevated lactate-to-pyruvate ratio. Depletion of oxaloacetate impairs the malate-aspartate shuttle and urea cycle function, contributing to hyperammonaemia and abnormal amino acids, while cerebral aspartate deficiency disturbs myelination and neurotransmitter metabolism.","additionalDifferentials":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex deficiency","Primary mitochondrial respiratory chain disorders","Multiple carboxylase deficiency (biotinidase or holocarboxylase synthetase deficiency)","Urea cycle disorders","Organic acidaemias with secondary lactic acidosis","Fatty acid oxidation defects presenting with hypoglycaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood lactate, pyruvate and calculated lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","Plasma amino acids showing raised citrulline, lysine and proline with low aspartate","Plasma ammonia and blood glucose during metabolic decompensation","Enzyme activity assay in cultured fibroblasts","Molecular genetic testing of the PC gene","Neuroimaging assessing white matter and structural changes"],"redFlags":["Severe neonatal lactic acidosis with hyperammonaemia and encephalopathy","Recurrent hypoglycaemia with metabolic acidosis","Rapid neurological deterioration during intercurrent illness","Failure of acidosis to respond to conventional resuscitation"],"prognosis":"The severe neonatal form carries a very poor prognosis with early death, and even the milder infantile form is usually associated with significant developmental delay and recurrent metabolic crises. Rare attenuated cases with more residual enzyme activity have better outcomes.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency, which typically shows a normal lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","Missing the diagnosis when lactic acidosis is attributed to sepsis or tissue hypoperfusion","Overlooking hyperammonaemia and the urea cycle contribution","Failing to prevent catabolism and fasting, which precipitate crises"],"management":["There is no cure; provide anaplerotic dietary support with a high-carbohydrate intake and avoidance of prolonged fasting through frequent feeds or continuous overnight feeding","Consider citrate, aspartate or triheptanoin supplementation to replenish citric acid cycle intermediates under metabolic specialist supervision","Manage acute decompensation with intravenous glucose to reverse hypoglycaemia and support anaplerosis, correction of severe acidosis, and treatment of hyperammonaemia with nitrogen-scavenging agents such as sodium benzoate and, if needed, haemofiltration or dialysis","Trial biotin, the enzyme cofactor, although a response is not usually seen","Monitor lactate, ammonia, glucose, plasma amino acids and neurodevelopment","Coordinate care through a specialist metabolic team with dietetic input","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive PC gene disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"pyruvate_dehydrogenase_deficiency":{"name":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PDH deficiency","PDHC deficiency","pyruvate dehydrogenase complex deficiency","PDHA1 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A major cause of primary (congenital) lactic acidosis, resulting from deficient activity of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex that links glycolysis to the citric acid cycle","The most common form is due to X-linked mutations in the E1-alpha subunit (PDHA1), so both males and females can be affected","Presentations range from severe neonatal lactic acidosis to progressive neurological disease with developmental delay, hypotonia, seizures and episodic ataxia","A characteristic feature is that lactate rises rather than falls after carbohydrate intake, and the blood lactate-to-pyruvate ratio is typically normal (in contrast to respiratory chain defects where it is high)","Structural brain abnormalities such as corpus callosum agenesis, and a Leigh syndrome pattern of basal ganglia and brainstem lesions, may be present","Because glucose cannot be used efficiently, a ketogenic (high-fat, low-carbohydrate) diet is a central management principle, providing an alternative fuel that bypasses the block","Thiamine (a cofactor of the complex) is supplemented and may benefit thiamine-responsive variants; dichloroacetate has been used to lower lactate","Not a standard target of newborn screening, so diagnosis rests on recognising congenital lactic acidosis and neurological features and confirming enzymatically or genetically"],"pathophysiology":"The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex converts pyruvate, the end product of glycolysis, into acetyl-CoA, linking carbohydrate metabolism to the citric acid cycle and oxidative energy production. When the complex is deficient, pyruvate cannot enter the cycle and is instead diverted to lactate and alanine, producing lactic acidosis with a normal lactate-to-pyruvate ratio. The brain, which depends heavily on glucose oxidation for energy, is particularly affected, resulting in structural malformations, neurodegeneration and a Leigh-like phenotype. Providing fat-derived ketones and acetyl-CoA through a ketogenic diet bypasses the enzymatic block and supplies the brain with usable fuel.","additionalDifferentials":["Mitochondrial respiratory chain disorders (high lactate-to-pyruvate ratio)","Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency","Organic acidaemias and other causes of lactic acidosis","Biotinidase and multiple carboxylase deficiency","Leigh syndrome of other genetic cause","Hypoxic-ischaemic or septic (secondary) lactic acidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and CSF lactate and pyruvate with a normal lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","Plasma amino acids (elevated alanine)","Neuroimaging for corpus callosum abnormalities and Leigh-pattern lesions","Enzyme assay of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex in fibroblasts or leukocytes","Molecular testing including the X-linked PDHA1 gene","Assessment of the lactate response to a carbohydrate load"],"redFlags":["Severe neonatal lactic acidosis with encephalopathy is a metabolic emergency","Acute worsening of lactic acidosis and neurological function after carbohydrate loading","Rapidly progressive encephalopathy or brainstem involvement in a Leigh-syndrome presentation","Intractable seizures or apnoea in the neonatal period"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally guarded and correlates with the severity of residual enzyme activity; severe neonatal forms often have a poor outcome, whereas milder and thiamine-responsive variants and early ketogenic dietary treatment can improve neurological outcome, though the disorder is not curable.","pitfalls":["Loading a hypotonic, acidotic infant with intravenous glucose, which can worsen lactic acidosis","Confusing it with respiratory chain disease by not checking the lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","Overlooking treatable thiamine-responsive variants","Missing affected females because the common defect is X-linked"],"management":["Institute a ketogenic, high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet as a central therapy, supplying ketones that bypass the enzymatic block and fuel the brain","Trial thiamine, a cofactor of the complex, since some variants are thiamine-responsive","Avoid carbohydrate loading, which paradoxically worsens the lactic acidosis","In acute lactic acidosis provide supportive correction and consider dichloroacetate to lower lactate by inhibiting pyruvate dehydrogenase kinase","Provide anticonvulsants for seizures and developmental, physiotherapy and occupational support for the neurological impairment","Monitor blood lactate, growth and neurodevelopment through a metabolic and neurology team","Offer genetic counselling and family screening, noting the X-linked PDHA1 inheritance of the commonest form"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"pyruvate_dehydrogenase_phosphatase_deficiency":{"name":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase phosphatase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PDP deficiency","Pyruvate dehydrogenase phosphatase 1 deficiency","PDP1","PDH complex phosphatase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews Primary Pyruvate Dehydrogenase Complex Deficiency","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase phosphatase deficiency is a rare autosomal recessive cause of pyruvate dehydrogenase complex dysfunction, usually from PDP1 mutations","The phosphatase normally dephosphorylates and thereby activates the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, so its loss leaves the complex locked in an inactive, phosphorylated state","The result is impaired conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA, with accumulation of pyruvate and lactate and a characteristically normal or low lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","Presentation is with neonatal or infantile lactic acidosis, hypotonia, feeding difficulty, developmental delay and, in some, features overlapping Leigh syndrome","Because the block is at the entry to the citric acid cycle, glucose loading tends to worsen lactic acidosis, whereas fat is a useful alternative fuel","Thiamine is a cofactor of the complex and a trial of generic thiamine supplementation is standard, and a high-fat, low-carbohydrate ketogenic dietary approach is used under specialist supervision to bypass the block","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating reduced pyruvate dehydrogenase complex activity that is restorable, with confirmation by PDP1 genetics"],"pathophysiology":"The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex links glycolysis to the citric acid cycle by converting pyruvate to acetyl-CoA, and its activity is regulated by reversible phosphorylation: pyruvate dehydrogenase kinase inactivates it and pyruvate dehydrogenase phosphatase reactivates it. In this disorder, recessive mutations (typically in PDP1) render the phosphatase deficient, so the complex remains phosphorylated and inactive. Pyruvate cannot be efficiently oxidised, so it and lactate accumulate while acetyl-CoA supply to the citric acid cycle falls, starving the brain of energy. Because pyruvate is diverted to lactate at the level of pyruvate rather than through a redox imbalance, the lactate-to-pyruvate ratio is usually normal or low, helping to distinguish it from respiratory-chain defects.","additionalDifferentials":["Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex E1 (PDHA1) and other component deficiencies","Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency","Respiratory-chain (oxidative phosphorylation) disorders and Leigh syndrome","Organic acidaemias and fatty acid oxidation defects causing lactic acidosis","Biotin-responsive and thiamine-responsive metabolic disorders","Secondary lactic acidosis from hypoxia or sepsis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate and pyruvate with calculation of the lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex enzyme activity assay in cultured fibroblasts, with and without conditions favouring dephosphorylation","Molecular genetic testing of PDP1 and other pyruvate dehydrogenase complex genes","Brain MRI, which may show basal ganglia or Leigh-type changes and structural anomalies","Plasma amino acids and urine organic acids to exclude other causes of lactic acidosis","Assessment of response to a supervised ketogenic dietary trial and thiamine"],"redFlags":["Severe neonatal lactic acidosis with metabolic decompensation","Refractory seizures or encephalopathy","Worsening acidosis after carbohydrate loading","Rapid neurological deterioration during intercurrent illness"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies with residual enzyme activity: severe neonatal presentations carry high mortality and significant neurodisability, whereas milder cases may stabilise with dietary and cofactor management, though neurological impairment often persists.","pitfalls":["Treating lactic acidosis with glucose-rich fluids, which can worsen it when the block is at pyruvate oxidation","Not distinguishing this from respiratory-chain disorders, which typically raise the lactate-to-pyruvate ratio","Failing to trial thiamine and a supervised ketogenic dietary approach that can bypass the metabolic block","Overlooking the enzyme assay conditions needed to reveal a phosphatase (activation) defect rather than a primary complex defect"],"management":["Use a high-fat, low-carbohydrate ketogenic diet under specialist supervision to supply an alternative fuel that bypasses the inactive complex","Trial thiamine supplementation, given its role as a cofactor of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex","Avoid carbohydrate loading, which worsens lactic acidosis","Treat acute lactic acidosis with supportive correction and glucose-sparing measures","Provide supportive neurological care for hypotonia, feeding difficulty, developmental delay and seizures, with dietetic and feeding support","Monitor lactate, pyruvate and neurodevelopment through a specialist metabolic team","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive PDP1 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Primary Pyruvate Dehydrogenase Complex Deficiency","OMIM: Pyruvate Dehydrogenase Phosphatase Deficiency"],"crossLinks":[]},"pyruvate_kinase_deficiency":{"name":"Pyruvate kinase deficiency (haemolytic anaemia)","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PK deficiency","PKLR","pyruvate kinase deficiency haemolytic anaemia","red-cell pyruvate kinase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Postgraduate Haematology / Oxford Textbook of Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest inherited enzyme defect of the glycolytic pathway to cause chronic haemolytic anaemia, inherited in an autosomal recessive manner through PKLR mutations","It produces a lifelong non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia of very variable severity, from mild to transfusion-dependent","Because the block is in glycolysis, upstream 2,3-diphosphoglycerate accumulates and shifts the oxygen dissociation curve, so anaemia is often better tolerated than the haemoglobin level suggests","Presentation ranges from neonatal jaundice and anaemia to gallstones, splenomegaly and iron overload in later life","Diagnosis relies on demonstrating reduced red-cell pyruvate kinase enzyme activity and confirmatory PKLR genetic testing, after excluding commoner causes","The blood film is non-specific, and characteristic echinocytes may become prominent only after splenectomy","Management includes folate support, transfusion when required, cholelithiasis surveillance, iron-overload monitoring, and splenectomy or newer targeted therapy in selected severe cases"],"pathophysiology":"Pyruvate kinase catalyses the final adenosine-triphosphate-generating step of glycolysis, the mature red cell's sole source of energy since it lacks mitochondria. Autosomal recessive PKLR mutations reduce enzyme activity, depleting cellular ATP and impairing the ion pumps and membrane maintenance that keep the red cell flexible, so cells become rigid, dehydrated and are removed prematurely, chiefly in the spleen and liver. The upstream metabolic block raises 2,3-diphosphoglycerate, which lowers haemoglobin oxygen affinity and improves tissue oxygen delivery, partly offsetting the anaemia; chronic haemolysis in turn produces jaundice, gallstones and, over time, iron loading.","additionalDifferentials":["Hereditary spherocytosis and other membrane disorders","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","Other glycolytic or red-cell enzyme deficiencies","Unstable haemoglobin variants","Thalassaemia syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count, reticulocyte count and blood film","Haemolysis markers including bilirubin, lactate dehydrogenase and haptoglobin","Direct antiglobulin test to exclude immune haemolysis","Red-cell pyruvate kinase enzyme activity assay (interpreted allowing for reticulocytosis)","PKLR gene sequencing for confirmation and counselling","Iron studies and ferritin to monitor for iron overload"],"redFlags":["Aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19 with a sharp fall in haemoglobin","Severe neonatal jaundice risking kernicterus","Symptomatic anaemia with cardiovascular compromise","Iron overload with cardiac, hepatic or endocrine dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is highly variable; many patients live well with intermittent support, while severely affected individuals face transfusion dependence, iron overload and gallstone complications, with splenectomy or targeted therapy able to reduce transfusion needs in selected cases.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a normal-looking enzyme level without correcting for reticulocytosis, which can mask deficiency","Overlooking iron overload in patients who are not regularly transfused","Assuming the haemoglobin concentration reflects symptom burden despite the compensatory oxygen-delivery shift","Missing gallstones as a cause of recurrent biliary symptoms"],"management":["Offer the oral pyruvate kinase activator mitapivat as targeted therapy to raise haemoglobin and reduce transfusion need in eligible patients","Provide folic acid supplementation and red cell transfusion for symptomatic or transfusion-dependent anaemia, with phototherapy or exchange transfusion for severe neonatal jaundice","Consider splenectomy to reduce haemolysis and transfusion requirement in severely affected patients, with appropriate pre-splenectomy vaccination and thromboprophylaxis awareness","Institute iron chelation for transfusional or absorptive iron overload","Manage pigment gallstones with cholecystectomy when symptomatic","Reserve allogeneic haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation, and emerging gene therapy, for severe transfusion-dependent disease","Monitor haemoglobin and iron studies and for gallstones, and offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive PKLR disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Postgraduate Haematology","Oxford Textbook of Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"pythiosis":{"name":"Pythiosis (Pythium insidiosum)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pythium insidiosum infection","oomycosis","swamp cancer","vascular pythiosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by Pythium insidiosum, an aquatic oomycete (water mould) rather than a true fungus, so it lacks ergosterol and responds poorly to conventional antifungal drugs","Acquired from warm freshwater and wetland environments, typically in tropical and subtropical regions, through skin or corneal contact with the motile zoospores","Vascular pythiosis is the most feared form: an aggressive angioinvasive arteritis, often of the lower limbs, seen especially in patients with thalassaemia or other haemoglobinopathies","Keratitis is another major form, presenting as a progressive, treatment-resistant corneal ulcer in contact-lens wearers or after ocular trauma involving water exposure","Cutaneous and subcutaneous granulomatous lesions and gastrointestinal disease are also described","Diagnosis is difficult because the broad, sparsely septate hyphae mimic zygomycetes on histology; culture, serology, and molecular methods aid identification","Management combines aggressive surgery, often radical excision or amputation for vascular disease, with antifungal-type agents and immunotherapy, as drugs alone rarely cure it"],"pathophysiology":"Pythium insidiosum is an oomycete that thrives in stagnant fresh water and colonises aquatic plants. It releases biflagellate motile zoospores that are attracted to and encyst on damaged skin, cornea, or the gut wall, then germinate to produce invasive hyphae. Because its cell wall is built of cellulose and beta-glucans and its membranes lack ergosterol, the organism is intrinsically resistant to antifungal agents that target ergosterol synthesis. In the vascular form the hyphae invade arterial walls, provoking a necrotising, thrombosing arteritis that progressively occludes vessels and produces limb ischaemia; the tendency for this angioinvasive disease is greatly increased in patients with thalassaemia and iron overload. Host immune responses are largely granulomatous and eosinophil-rich but insufficient to contain the aggressive tissue invasion.","additionalDifferentials":["Mucormycosis and other angioinvasive mould infections","Fungal keratitis from Fusarium or Aspergillus species","Peripheral arterial occlusive disease or vasculitis","Lagenidiosis and other oomycete infections","Bacterial necrotising soft-tissue infection","Eosinophilic gastrointestinal disease in the intestinal form"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Histopathology with special stains showing broad, sparsely septate, right-angle-branching hyphae resembling zygomycetes","Culture of tissue on suitable media with demonstration of zoospore formation in water","Serological assays (immunodiffusion, ELISA) for anti-Pythium antibodies","Molecular identification by PCR and sequencing of the ribosomal region","Arterial imaging (Doppler ultrasound, CT or MR angiography) to define vascular involvement","Corneal scrapings and confocal microscopy in suspected keratitis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive limb ischaemia or arterial occlusion from angioinvasive disease","A corneal ulcer worsening despite standard antifungal or antibacterial therapy","Underlying thalassaemia or haemoglobinopathy with freshwater exposure","Evidence of proximal vascular or disseminated spread"],"prognosis":"Vascular pythiosis carries a high risk of limb loss and death unless there is early radical surgery combined with medical and immunotherapy; ocular disease often leads to loss of the eye, whereas localised cutaneous disease treated promptly has a better outcome.","pitfalls":["Treating as mucormycosis or ordinary fungal disease with antifungals alone, since the oomycete resists ergosterol-targeting drugs","Delaying surgery in vascular disease in the hope that medical therapy will suffice","Failing to recognise thalassaemia as a strong risk factor prompting earlier suspicion","Misidentifying the hyphae histologically and missing the diagnosis"],"management":["Perform early radical surgery, wide excision or amputation, as the mainstay for angioinvasive vascular disease to remove all infected arterial tissue","Add immunotherapy with Pythium insidiosum antigen to modulate the host response and reduce recurrence","Use combination antimicrobials that act independently of ergosterol, such as azithromycin, doxycycline or linezolid, since the oomycete resists conventional ergosterol-targeting antifungals","Manage ocular disease with intensive topical therapy and therapeutic keratoplasty, proceeding to enucleation for uncontrolled keratitis","Address predisposing thalassaemia and iron overload that promote angioinvasive infection","Coordinate care across infectious diseases, vascular surgery and ophthalmology with close monitoring for progression and recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","UpToDate: Pythiosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"q_fever":{"name":"Q fever","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["query fever","Coxiella burnetii infection","coxiellosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by the intracellular bacterium Coxiella burnetii, acquired mainly by inhaling aerosols from infected livestock, particularly around parturition, birth fluids, and contaminated dust","Occupational exposure (farmers, abattoir workers, veterinarians) and even wind-borne spread from farms are important epidemiological clues","Acute Q fever is frequently a self-limiting flu-like illness, but can present as atypical pneumonia or as granulomatous hepatitis with fever","The organism is highly resistant in the environment and remarkably infectious at very low doses","Chronic Q fever, developing months to years later, most often manifests as culture-negative endocarditis, especially in those with pre-existing valve disease, prosthetic valves, or vascular grafts","Diagnosis is largely serological using phase I and phase II antibodies: phase II dominance suggests acute disease and high phase I titres point to chronic infection","Treatment of acute disease relies on a tetracycline-class agent, whereas chronic endocarditis requires prolonged combination therapy and long-term follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"Coxiella burnetii is an obligate intracellular Gram-negative bacterium that survives as a hardy spore-like form in the environment. After inhalation it is taken up by alveolar macrophages and replicates within acidified phagolysosomes, disseminating to liver, lung, and other organs and provoking granulomatous inflammation. It undergoes antigenic phase variation, which underlies the serological distinction between acute and chronic infection. In chronic disease the organism persists at sites such as damaged or prosthetic heart valves and vascular tissue, driving endovascular infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Atypical pneumonia (Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Legionella)","Viral hepatitis and other causes of granulomatous hepatitis","Brucellosis","Infective endocarditis from other culture-negative organisms (for chronic disease)","Influenza and other viral febrile illnesses","Leptospirosis"],"infectionMimics":["Legionella and Mycoplasma pneumonia","Brucellosis","Viral hepatitis","Other causes of culture-negative endocarditis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Phase I and phase II serology (immunofluorescence) as the mainstay, interpreted for acute versus chronic pattern","PCR for Coxiella DNA in early acute infection before antibodies rise","Liver function tests and, where relevant, liver biopsy showing characteristic granulomas","Echocardiography and vascular imaging when chronic Q fever or endocarditis is suspected","Chest imaging for pneumonic presentations"],"redFlags":["Persistent or relapsing fever with high phase I antibody titres suggesting chronic infection","New or changing murmur, embolic events, or heart failure indicating endocarditis","Fever in a patient with a prosthetic valve or vascular graft and compatible exposure","Q fever in pregnancy, which carries risks of chronic infection and adverse fetal outcomes"],"prognosis":"Acute Q fever usually resolves, often even without treatment, whereas chronic Q fever endocarditis is a serious condition requiring prolonged therapy and carries appreciable morbidity and mortality if not recognised and adequately treated.","pitfalls":["Failing to take an occupational or animal-contact history in an atypical pneumonia or unexplained hepatitis","Labelling endocarditis as culture-negative without serological testing for Coxiella","Stopping therapy too early in chronic disease, risking relapse","Not screening or offering prophylaxis considerations to high-risk patients with valvulopathy after exposure"],"management":["Treat acute Q fever with doxycycline as first-line therapy","Manage chronic Q fever endocarditis with prolonged combination doxycycline and hydroxychloroquine continued for many months to years","Use co-trimoxazole in pregnancy, where doxycycline is contraindicated, to reduce obstetric complications","Assess valves with echocardiography and consider valve or graft surgery for uncontrolled endovascular infection","Follow phase I and phase II serology after acute infection to detect progression to chronic disease, especially in those with valvular, prosthetic-valve or vascular-graft risk factors","Reduce exposure through occupational precautions and offer vaccination to high-risk groups such as abattoir workers","Refer to infectious diseases for guidance on prolonged therapy and monitoring"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"quadricuspid_aortic_valve":{"name":"Quadricuspid aortic valve","system":"Cardiovascular / Congenital","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["QAV","four-cusped aortic valve","quadricuspid aortic valve anomaly"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare congenital malformation in which the aortic valve has four cusps instead of the normal three","Cusps are classified by their relative sizes, with configurations ranging from four equal cusps to one with three equal and one smaller cusp","Aortic regurgitation is the dominant functional lesion, usually developing gradually because the asymmetric cusps coapt imperfectly","Many patients are asymptomatic in early life and are diagnosed incidentally on echocardiography or at surgery","Progressive regurgitation can cause left ventricular volume loading, dilatation and eventual symptoms of heart failure","Associated anomalies include coronary artery ostial displacement, which is relevant if surgery is planned","Management follows aortic valve disease principles, with surveillance of regurgitation severity and valve repair or replacement when indicated"],"pathophysiology":"A quadricuspid aortic valve arises from abnormal septation of the truncal cushions during valvulogenesis, producing four cusps rather than three. The supernumerary and often unequally sized cusps close incompletely, so the valve tends towards regurgitation over time as repeated imperfect coaptation causes cusp fibrosis and prolapse. The resulting chronic aortic regurgitation imposes a volume load on the left ventricle, which dilates and hypertrophies to compensate before contractile function eventually declines. Stenosis is much less common. Because the abnormal cusp arrangement can be associated with displacement of the coronary ostia, coronary anatomy is an important consideration when intervention is contemplated.","additionalDifferentials":["Bicuspid aortic valve","Tricuspid aortic valve with degenerative regurgitation","Rheumatic aortic valve disease","Aortic root dilatation causing regurgitation","Infective endocarditis of the aortic valve","Unicuspid aortic valve"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography to count cusps and grade regurgitation","Transoesophageal echocardiography for detailed cusp morphology when transthoracic views are limited","Cardiac MRI to quantify regurgitant volume and left ventricular size and function","Cardiac CT to assess valve anatomy and coronary ostial position before surgery","Electrocardiogram for left ventricular hypertrophy","Serial imaging to monitor progression of regurgitation and ventricular remodelling"],"redFlags":["Severe aortic regurgitation with progressive left ventricular dilatation or dysfunction","New symptoms of heart failure or exertional intolerance","Superimposed infective endocarditis on the abnormal valve","Displaced coronary ostia complicating planned intervention"],"prognosis":"Many patients remain well for decades, but a substantial proportion develop significant aortic regurgitation requiring valve surgery in adulthood; outcomes after timely repair or replacement are generally good.","pitfalls":["Miscounting cusps and mistaking the valve for a bicuspid or tricuspid valve on limited imaging","Underestimating slowly progressive regurgitation and delaying surveillance","Overlooking coronary ostial anomalies before surgery","Attributing regurgitation solely to root dilatation without recognising the cusp anomaly"],"management":["Manage most patients with surveillance, as many remain asymptomatic for decades","Refer for aortic valve repair or replacement when regurgitation becomes severe, symptoms develop, or left ventricular dilatation or dysfunction appears","Assess coronary ostial position with cardiac CT before surgery given the association with ostial displacement","Use afterload-reducing and heart-failure therapy such as ACE inhibitors to manage symptomatic regurgitation and left ventricular volume loading","Monitor with serial echocardiography, and cardiac MRI to quantify regurgitant volume and ventricular size and function","Advise good dental hygiene and endocarditis awareness, reserving antibiotic prophylaxis for those meeting high-risk criteria such as prosthetic valves","Refer to congenital cardiology for long-term follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"quadricuspid_pulmonary_valve":{"name":"Quadricuspid pulmonary valve","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["QPV","quadricuspid pulmonic valve","four-cusped pulmonary valve"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare congenital anomaly in which the pulmonary valve has four cusps, usually of unequal size, arising from abnormal septation of the truncal cushions","More frequently identified at the pulmonary than the aortic position, and often an incidental finding","Commonly functions normally, but the asymmetric cusps can produce pulmonary regurgitation and, less often, stenosis over time","May coexist with other congenital cardiac lesions and with anomalies of the coronary arteries, warranting a careful search for associated defects","Frequently detected incidentally on echocardiography or on cross-sectional imaging performed for another indication","Transoesophageal echocardiography, cardiac CT, or MRI best delineate the number and arrangement of cusps and quantify any regurgitation","Most patients need only periodic surveillance, with valve repair or replacement reserved for significant regurgitation or stenosis"],"pathophysiology":"A quadricuspid pulmonary valve results from abnormal division of the mesenchymal cushions of the developing outflow tract, yielding four cusps that are usually unequal in size. The uneven leaflet geometry disturbs normal coaptation, so that over time the valve tends towards incompetence, producing pulmonary regurgitation, while fibrosis or thickening of the redundant cusps can occasionally cause obstruction. Chronic significant regurgitation imposes a volume load on the right ventricle, which dilates and may eventually fail, and the developmental origin explains the recognised associations with other outflow-tract and coronary anomalies.","additionalDifferentials":["Normal trileaflet pulmonary valve with functional regurgitation","Bicuspid pulmonary valve","Dysplastic pulmonary valve","Valvular pulmonary stenosis","Carcinoid pulmonary valve disease","Pulmonary regurgitation after prior valvotomy or repair"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography to assess valve function and right ventricular size","Transoesophageal echocardiography to define the number and arrangement of cusps","Cardiac MRI to quantify pulmonary regurgitant fraction and right ventricular volumes","Cardiac CT to delineate valve anatomy and screen for associated coronary anomalies","Right heart catheterisation when non-invasive assessment of severity is inconclusive"],"redFlags":["Severe pulmonary regurgitation with progressive right ventricular dilatation or dysfunction","Significant stenosis producing right ventricular pressure overload","Associated congenital cardiac or coronary anomalies requiring intervention","Symptoms of right heart failure or declining exercise tolerance"],"prognosis":"The anomaly is usually benign and often carries a normal life expectancy, but a minority develop progressive pulmonary regurgitation or stenosis needing intervention, so periodic surveillance is appropriate.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the fourth cusp on transthoracic imaging and misclassifying the valve","Failing to search for associated congenital or coronary anomalies","Attributing pulmonary regurgitation solely to functional causes without inspecting valve morphology","Neglecting serial assessment of right ventricular size in significant regurgitation"],"management":["Manage this usually benign lesion with periodic surveillance","Refer for pulmonary valve replacement, surgical or transcatheter, when pulmonary regurgitation is severe with progressive right ventricular dilatation or dysfunction, or when significant stenosis develops","Search for and manage associated congenital cardiac and coronary artery anomalies","Monitor with serial echocardiography and cardiac MRI to quantify the pulmonary regurgitant fraction and right ventricular volumes","Advise good dental hygiene and endocarditis awareness","Refer to cardiology for ongoing follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC Guidelines for the management of adult congenital heart disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"quebec_platelet_disorder":{"name":"Quebec platelet disorder","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["QPD","PLAU duplication disorder","urokinase plasminogen activator overexpression"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal dominant bleeding disorder characterised by delayed-onset bleeding after trauma or surgery, often hours to a day later","Bleeding is typically responsive to antifibrinolytic therapy but not to platelet transfusion, a distinctive and clinically important feature","The platelet count is usually normal or only mildly reduced, so it is a qualitative defect","The underlying lesion is a tandem duplication of the PLAU gene, causing marked overexpression of urokinase-type plasminogen activator within megakaryocytes and platelets","Excess intraplatelet urokinase drives premature plasmin generation and proteolytic degradation of alpha-granule proteins","It can be mistaken for an alpha-granule storage pool defect because of the secondary loss of granule contents","Recognition matters because antifibrinolytic cover, rather than platelet transfusion, is the effective haemostatic strategy"],"pathophysiology":"Quebec platelet disorder is caused by a tandem duplication of PLAU, the gene encoding urokinase-type plasminogen activator. Overexpression of urokinase within developing megakaryocytes leads to accumulation of the enzyme in platelet alpha granules, where it generates plasmin intracellularly. This premature, localised plasmin activity degrades stored alpha-granule proteins such as factor V, fibrinogen, von Willebrand factor, and others. The net effect is an intraplatelet hyperfibrinolytic state that undermines clot stability at sites of injury, producing characteristically delayed bleeding despite a relatively preserved platelet count.","additionalDifferentials":["Platelet alpha-granule storage pool deficiency (including gray platelet syndrome)","Von Willebrand disease","Mild factor V deficiency","Other inherited platelet function disorders","Alpha-2-antiplasmin deficiency and other hyperfibrinolytic bleeding states","Acquired platelet dysfunction from drugs"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed bleeding history emphasising delayed post-traumatic or postoperative bleeding","Platelet aggregation studies, which may show a reduced response to certain agonists","Assay or immunoblot demonstrating increased platelet urokinase-type plasminogen activator","Measurement of reduced platelet alpha-granule protein content","Genetic testing for the PLAU tandem duplication to confirm the diagnosis","Von Willebrand factor and coagulation factor assays to exclude alternative disorders"],"redFlags":["Severe delayed bleeding after surgery, trauma, or dental extraction","Bleeding that fails to respond to platelet transfusion","Joint or deep-tissue bleeding suggesting a significant haemostatic defect","Unrecognised diagnosis leading to inappropriate transfusion before an invasive procedure"],"prognosis":"With correct diagnosis and antifibrinolytic cover around haemostatic challenges, most patients do well and lead normal lives. Failure to recognise the disorder can lead to preventable, sometimes serious, delayed haemorrhage.","pitfalls":["Relying on platelet transfusion, which is ineffective, rather than antifibrinolytic therapy","Misclassifying it as a simple alpha-granule storage pool defect","Being reassured by a normal platelet count and near-normal routine coagulation screen","Underestimating risk because bleeding is delayed rather than immediate"],"management":["Treat and prevent bleeding with antifibrinolytic agents such as tranexamic acid or epsilon-aminocaproic acid, which are the mainstay and counter the intraplatelet hyperfibrinolysis","Give antifibrinolytic cover prophylactically before surgery, dental procedures and after trauma, and continue it to pre-empt the characteristic delayed bleeding","Avoid platelet transfusion, which is ineffective in this disorder, reserving it only for exceptional circumstances","Avoid antiplatelet drugs and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents that would further impair haemostasis","Refer for genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal dominant inheritance of the PLAU duplication","Provide a clear bleeding-disorder care plan and haematology follow-up so delayed haemorrhage is anticipated and managed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Hoffman: Hematology - Basic Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"rabies":{"name":"Rabies","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rabies encephalitis"],"chapter":214,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1650","clinicalPearls":["Rabies is a rapidly progressive acute CNS infection by rabies virus (a lyssavirus, family Rhabdoviridae), normally transmitted via the bite of an infected animal; it has encephalitic and paralytic forms that progress to death (p.1650).","Worldwide, ~99% of human rabies is transmitted from dogs in countries with endemic canine rabies; endemic canine rabies causes an estimated 59,000 human deaths annually, with most deaths in Asia and Africa and rural populations and children disproportionately affected (p.1650-1651).","Incubation period is usually 20-90 days but in rare cases may be as short as a few days or longer than 1 year (p.1651).","Earliest specific neurologic symptoms are paresthesias, pain, or pruritus near the exposure site, occurring in 50-80% of patients and strongly suggesting rabies; the wound has usually healed by this point (p.1652).","Two acute neurologic forms occur: encephalitic (furious) in ~80% and paralytic (dumb) in ~20% (p.1652).","Encephalitic rabies presents as atypical encephalitis with relative preservation of consciousness; episodes of hyperexcitability alternate with periods of complete lucidity that shorten as disease progresses (p.1652).","Early brainstem involvement produces the classic hydrophobia (involuntary painful contraction of diaphragm and accessory respiratory, laryngeal, and pharyngeal muscles on swallowing liquids) and aerophobia (same response to a draft of air) (p.1652).","Autonomic dysfunction is common and may cause hypersalivation, gooseflesh, cardiac arrhythmia, and priapism; hypersalivation plus pharyngeal dysfunction produces the classic 'foaming at the mouth' (p.1652).","Paralytic rabies features early prominent flaccid weakness often beginning in the bitten extremity, progressing to quadriparesis and facial weakness, with sphincter involvement common and sensory involvement usually mild (p.1652).","The Negri body (eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusion in brain neurons, e.g. cerebellar Purkinje cells and hippocampal pyramidal neurons) is the most characteristic pathologic finding, but is NOT seen in all cases (p.1651-1652).","Diagnosis should be considered in patients with acute atypical encephalitis or acute flaccid paralysis, including those in whom Guillain-Barre syndrome is suspected; absence of an animal-bite history is common in North America due to unrecognized bat exposures (p.1653).","Postexposure prophylaxis must be initiated after a recognized exposure and before any symptoms or signs develop; the emphasis for prevention is on PEP (p.1652)."],"pathophysiology":"Rabies virus, a single-strand negative-sense RNA lyssavirus (family Rhabdoviridae), is usually inoculated by an animal bite. During most of the incubation period the virus remains at or near the inoculation site; in muscle it binds nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at neuromuscular junctions. It spreads centripetally along peripheral nerves toward the spinal cord or brainstem by retrograde fast axonal transport (up to ~250 mm/d, with ~12-h delays at each synapse). Once in the CNS it disseminates rapidly along neuroanatomic connections; neurons are prominently infected while astrocyte infection is unusual. After CNS infection is established there is centrifugal spread along sensory and autonomic nerves to salivary glands, heart, adrenal glands, and skin; viral replication in salivary gland acinar cells leads to secretion in saliva (the vehicle of animal transmission). There is no well-documented evidence for hematogenous spread. CNS inflammatory changes are mild (mononuclear infiltrate, perivascular cuffing, microglial Babes nodules) with little neuronal death; clinical disease is attributed to neuronal dysfunction rather than death, with oxidative stress from mitochondrial dysfunction implicated. (p.1650-1652)","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex encephalitis","Arboviral encephalitis (e.g., West Nile)","Anti-NMDA receptor and other autoimmune encephalitides","Postinfectious (immune-mediated) encephalomyelitis (following influenza, measles, mumps, or neural-tissue rabies vaccine)","Guillain-Barre syndrome (mimicked by paralytic rabies)","Rabies hysteria / somatic symptom disorder (psychological response to fear of rabies)","Misdiagnosis as a primary psychiatric disorder"],"infectionMimics":["Herpes simplex encephalitis","Arboviral (e.g., West Nile) encephalitis","Postinfectious encephalomyelitis after influenza, measles, or mumps"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis (especially young females; behavioral changes, autonomic instability, hypoventilation, seizures)","Other antibody-mediated autoimmune encephalitis","Guillain-Barre syndrome","Rabies hysteria (somatic symptom disorder) with shorter course and eventual recovery","Psychiatric disorder"],"additionalInvestigations":["Rabies virus-specific serum neutralizing antibodies (diagnostic in a previously unimmunized patient; may not develop until late and some patients die without them)","Rabies virus-specific neutralizing antibodies in CSF (suggest rabies encephalitis regardless of immunization status)","RT-PCR for rabies virus RNA (highly sensitive/specific; saliva, skin biopsy, CSF [less sensitive], brain tissue; genetic sequencing can identify the variant/source)","Direct fluorescent antibody (DFA) testing for rabies virus antigen (skin biopsy and brain tissue; in skin, antigen detected in cutaneous nerves at the base of hair follicles)","Skin biopsy from hairy skin at the nape of the neck","CSF examination (typically mild mononuclear pleocytosis with mildly elevated protein)","MRI brain (variable, nonspecific brainstem/gray-matter signal abnormalities); CT usually normal; EEG nonspecific"],"redFlags":["Near-100% fatal once symptomatic, so post-exposure prophylaxis is everything - do NOT delay PEP after a recognized exposure.","Paresthesias, pain, or pruritus at a healed animal-bite site preceding neurologic illness strongly suggest rabies.","Hydrophobia or aerophobia with preserved consciousness (atypical encephalitis) is essentially diagnostic of encephalitic rabies.","Absence of a recalled animal bite does NOT exclude rabies - bat exposures are frequently unrecognized; consider PEP when a small child or sleeping adult shared a space with a bat and an unrecognized bite cannot be reliably excluded.","Negative antemortem rabies-specific laboratory tests never exclude the diagnosis; repeat testing may be needed.","Acute flaccid paralysis resembling Guillain-Barre syndrome may be paralytic rabies - fever, bladder dysfunction, normal sensory exam, and CSF pleocytosis favor rabies."],"prognosis":"Rabies is an almost uniformly fatal disease but is nearly always preventable after recognized exposures with appropriate postexposure therapy during the early incubation period. Most patients die within several days of symptom onset despite aggressive critical-care support. There is no established treatment and no antiviral has demonstrated efficacy; aggressive supportive care has yielded at least 33 documented survivors (many recently reported from India), but recent treatment failures (>60) have far outnumbered successes. All but 2 of the 33 documented survivors had received one or more doses of rabies vaccine before disease onset, and the unvaccinated survivors had neutralizing antibodies in CSF at presentation. A palliative approach may be appropriate for patients who are not candidates for aggressive management. (p.1654)","pitfalls":["Rabies is often not considered until late in the clinical course in North America even with a typical presentation.","Relying on a history of animal bite to make the diagnosis - many cases (especially bat-associated) have no recalled exposure.","Assuming absence of hydrophobia excludes rabies - lack of hydrophobia is not unusual.","Misdiagnosing paralytic rabies as Guillain-Barre syndrome.","Treating negative antemortem rabies-specific tests as exclusionary.","Severe CSF pleocytosis (>1000 white cells/uL) is unusual for rabies and should prompt a search for an alternative diagnosis.","Delaying local wound care while awaiting the 10-day animal observation period - wound care should not be delayed.","Administering passive immunization (RIG) more than 7 days after the first vaccine dose, when endogenous antibody production has begun and passive immunization may be counterproductive; rabies vaccine and RIG must never be given at the same site.","Minor deviations from current PEP recommendations can lead to prophylaxis failure."],"management":["Immediate thorough wound cleansing and copious irrigation after any potential exposure, as the cornerstone of preventing established infection","Post-exposure prophylaxis in the unimmunised with infiltration of rabies immunoglobulin around the wound plus a course of rabies vaccine, given as early as possible during incubation","Pre-exposure rabies vaccination for high-risk groups such as veterinary and laboratory workers and travellers to endemic regions","Once symptomatic, aggressive critical-care support with sedation and control of hydrophobic and aerophobic spasms and autonomic instability, recognising the disease is almost uniformly fatal","Compassionate palliative care with symptom control where survival cannot be achieved","Barrier precautions for staff and carers, with post-exposure prophylaxis offered to those with wound or mucosal contact with the patient's secretions","Notification to public health authorities and coordination of animal-bite investigation and rabies control"],"severityClassification":"Clinical stages: incubation period (20-90 days, asymptomatic); prodrome (2-10 days: fever, malaise, anorexia, nausea, vomiting; paresthesias/pain/pruritus at the wound site); acute neurologic disease - encephalitic form (~80%, 2-7 days: anxiety, agitation, hyperactivity, bizarre behavior, hallucinations, autonomic dysfunction, hydrophobia) or paralytic form (~20%, 2-10 days: flaccid limb paralysis progressing to quadriparesis with facial paralysis); then coma and death (0-14 days; recovery rare). (p.1653)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1650"],"crossLinks":["ENCEPHALITIS","gbs"]},"rabson_mendenhall_syndrome":{"name":"Rabson-Mendenhall syndrome (INSR)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RMS","INSR mutation","insulin receptor gene defect","severe insulin resistance syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Endocrine Society clinical guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder from biallelic INSR mutations causing severe insulin resistance, intermediate in severity between Donohue syndrome and type A insulin resistance","Presents in childhood with growth impairment, acanthosis nigricans, coarse features, dental and nail dysplasia, and pineal hyperplasia in some patients","Extreme endogenous hyperinsulinaemia accompanies fasting hypoglycaemia and severe postprandial hyperglycaemia, reflecting defective receptor signalling","Diabetic ketoacidosis and difficult-to-control hyperglycaemia often develop as the child grows","Because the block is at the insulin receptor, escalating exogenous insulin is largely ineffective","Recombinant IGF-1 (with or without IGFBP-3) can engage the IGF-1 receptor to improve glycaemic control in these severe INSR defects","Longer survival than Donohue syndrome allows accumulation of chronic complications","Diagnosis rests on the phenotype, the fasting-hypoglycaemia/postprandial-hyperglycaemia pattern with extreme hyperinsulinaemia, and INSR genetics"],"pathophysiology":"Rabson-Mendenhall syndrome is caused by biallelic mutations in the insulin receptor gene (INSR) that severely, though not completely, impair receptor function. With markedly reduced insulin signalling, tissues cannot take up glucose or grow normally despite very high insulin levels, and hepatic glucose output is poorly suppressed. The result is severe insulin resistance manifest as fasting hypoglycaemia alternating with pronounced postprandial hyperglycaemia, together with acanthosis nigricans, growth impairment, and the characteristic dysmorphic, dental, and nail features. Because residual receptor function is greater than in Donohue syndrome, the course is less rapidly fatal but progresses to overt, ketosis-prone diabetes.","additionalDifferentials":["Donohue syndrome (more severe INSR defect)","Type A insulin resistance syndrome","Congenital generalised lipodystrophy","Poorly controlled type 1 diabetes with acanthosis","Other syndromic causes of short stature with acanthosis nigricans","Endocrinopathies with hyperinsulinaemia and dysmorphism","Severe monogenic diabetes syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting and postprandial glucose to demonstrate the characteristic oscillation","Fasting insulin and C-peptide, markedly elevated","INSR gene sequencing to confirm biallelic mutations","HbA1c and ketone monitoring given ketoacidosis risk","Dental, skeletal, and pineal imaging where clinically indicated","Lipid profile and leptin to characterise the metabolic phenotype"],"redFlags":["Diabetic ketoacidosis or severe uncontrolled hyperglycaemia","Recurrent fasting hypoglycaemia","Progressive growth failure and metabolic decompensation","Emergence of chronic microvascular diabetic complications"],"prognosis":"Children with Rabson-Mendenhall syndrome generally survive longer than those with Donohue syndrome, often into the second decade or beyond, but face progressive, difficult-to-control diabetes with ketoacidosis and chronic complications; IGF-1-based therapy may improve control.","pitfalls":["Interpreting extreme hyperinsulinaemia as an insulin-secreting tumour","Escalating exogenous insulin doses that fail to overcome receptor resistance","Missing the fasting-hypoglycaemia/postprandial-hyperglycaemia pattern","Attributing growth failure and acanthosis to obesity alone","Neglecting INSR genetic confirmation and family counselling"],"management":["Recombinant IGF-1, with or without IGFBP-3, to bypass the defective insulin receptor by signalling through the IGF-1 receptor and improve glycaemic control","Insulin sensitisers such as metformin and, where used, high-dose insulin, recognising that receptor-level blockade limits the benefit of exogenous insulin","A dietary strategy of frequent complex-carbohydrate feeds to blunt the swings between fasting hypoglycaemia and postprandial hyperglycaemia","Regular monitoring of glycaemic control and ketones given the ketosis-prone, difficult-to-control diabetes, together with surveillance for chronic complications","Prompt treatment of diabetic ketoacidosis with fluids, electrolyte correction and insulin during acute decompensation","Multidisciplinary follow-up with paediatric endocrinology, dietetics, and dental and growth surveillance","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder and testing of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society clinical practice guidance on severe insulin resistance"],"crossLinks":[]},"radial_club_hand":{"name":"Radial longitudinal deficiency (radial club hand)","system":"Paediatric orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["radial club hand","radial dysplasia","radial longitudinal deficiency","RLD","preaxial deficiency of the upper limb"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery / Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Represents a spectrum of preaxial (radial-side) failure of formation, ranging from a mildly hypoplastic thumb to complete absence of the radius with the hand deviated radially at the wrist","The forearm is usually short and bowed, the unsupported carpus deviates radially (the 'club hand' appearance), and the thumb is frequently hypoplastic or absent","Severity is graded (Bayne and later modifications) from a deficient distal radial epiphysis through partial to complete radial aplasia, incorporating thumb and carpal deficiency","Strong systemic associations mandate a syndrome search: VACTERL association, Fanconi anaemia, thrombocytopenia-absent-radius (TAR) syndrome, and Holt-Oram (heart-hand) syndrome","Because Fanconi anaemia and TAR carry haematological risk, screening (chromosomal breakage testing and a full blood count) is essential before any surgery","Early treatment centres on stretching and splinting to correct wrist alignment, with surgical centralisation or radialisation of the carpus over the ulna reserved for suitable cases","Thumb reconstruction by pollicisation of the index finger restores pinch when the thumb is absent or non-functional"],"pathophysiology":"The deformity arises from failure of formation along the preaxial (radial) margin of the developing upper limb bud in early gestation, affecting the radius, radial carpal bones, thumb ray, and the associated muscles, radial nerve, and vessels. The degree of radial deficiency determines the radial deviation of the hand and the instability of the wrist, as the carpus loses its bony support. Because the same limb-patterning and progenitor pathways influence blood formation and cardiac development, radial longitudinal deficiency is frequently part of systemic disorders affecting haematopoiesis (Fanconi anaemia, thrombocytopenia-absent-radius) or the heart (Holt-Oram syndrome), which must always be sought.","additionalDifferentials":["Ulnar longitudinal deficiency (postaxial deficiency)","Isolated thumb hypoplasia without radial deficiency","Cleft hand or central longitudinal deficiency","Constriction ring (amniotic band) sequence affecting the forearm","Arthrogryposis with fixed wrist contracture","Acquired radial deficiency following trauma, infection, or tumour resection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Radiographs of the whole upper limb to grade radial, carpal, and thumb deficiency","Full blood count and blood film to screen for thrombocytopenia (TAR syndrome)","Chromosomal breakage testing (diepoxybutane or mitomycin C) to exclude Fanconi anaemia before surgery","Echocardiography and cardiology review for Holt-Oram or VACTERL cardiac anomalies","Renal ultrasound and spinal radiographs as part of VACTERL screening","Genetic assessment with karyotyping or targeted gene testing"],"redFlags":["Undiagnosed Fanconi anaemia, which can progress to marrow failure and malignancy if missed before surgery","Severe thrombocytopenia in TAR syndrome risking perioperative haemorrhage","Associated cardiac or renal anomalies (Holt-Oram or VACTERL) needing evaluation before anaesthesia","Bilateral severe deformity with limited elbow extension compromising hand-to-mouth function"],"prognosis":"With appropriate splinting, centralisation, and pollicisation, function and appearance improve substantially, but recurrence of radial deviation and continued forearm shortening are common as the child grows; the overall outlook is heavily influenced by any underlying syndrome, particularly the haematological disorders.","pitfalls":["Treating the hand deformity without screening for Fanconi anaemia or TAR, thereby missing a life-threatening haematological disorder","Overlooking associated cardiac, renal, or vertebral anomalies","Performing aggressive centralisation when elbow extension is limited, which can worsen function by preventing hand-to-mouth movement","Neglecting thumb function, which is often more disabling than the wrist deformity itself"],"management":["Early passive stretching and splinting of the wrist to correct radial deviation and maintain soft-tissue length before any surgery","Surgical centralisation or radialisation of the carpus over the distal ulna in suitable cases to stabilise the wrist","Pollicisation or thumb reconstruction where the thumb is hypoplastic or absent to restore pinch and grasp","Screening for haematological and syndromic associations, including chromosomal breakage testing for Fanconi anaemia and a full blood count for thrombocytopenia-absent-radius syndrome, before operative intervention","Echocardiography and evaluation for Holt-Oram and VACTERL anomalies as part of the systemic work-up","Ongoing occupational therapy and physiotherapy with monitoring for recurrent radial deviation and forearm shortening as the child grows","Genetic counselling and family assessment given the heritable syndromic associations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"radial_nerve_palsy":{"name":"radial nerve palsy","system":"Nervous system / Peripheral nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["radial neuropathy","Saturday night palsy","spiral groove syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest site of injury is the spiral (radial) groove of the humerus, producing wrist drop, finger drop and weak thumb extension with sensory loss over the dorsal first web space","The classic compressive 'Saturday night palsy' follows prolonged external pressure on the arm, often with sleep after alcohol; humeral shaft fracture (notably the Holstein-Lewis distal-third fracture) is a key traumatic cause","A spiral-groove lesion spares triceps and the elbow-extension reflex because branches to triceps arise proximally, whereas a very proximal axillary lesion (for example crutch palsy) also weakens triceps","Brachioradialis and supinator weakness helps localise a lesion at or above the elbow, and the radially deviated posture on attempted wrist extension reflects preserved extensor carpi radialis longus with weak extensor carpi ulnaris","Finger drop with preserved wrist extension and no sensory loss indicates a posterior interosseous (deep motor branch) lesion rather than a main radial trunk palsy","Apparent hand-grip weakness is often spurious, resulting from loss of wrist extension that shortens the finger flexors; grip normalises when the wrist is passively supported","Most neuropraxic and compressive lesions recover spontaneously; a cock-up wrist splint maintains function, and tendon transfers are considered when there is no recovery after adequate time and electrophysiological follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"The radial nerve, the terminal continuation of the posterior cord (C5-T1), descends through the axilla, winds around the humerus in the spiral groove between the heads of triceps, then pierces the lateral intermuscular septum to reach the anterior compartment at the elbow, where it divides into the superficial (sensory) radial nerve and the posterior interosseous (deep motor) branch. Its close apposition to the humerus in the spiral groove and its tethering at the lateral intermuscular septum render it susceptible to external compression, fracture and traction. Compression typically produces focal demyelination with conduction block and good prognosis, whereas transection or severe traction causes axonal loss requiring regeneration over months; the motor deficit reflects paralysis of the wrist, finger and thumb extensors and, in proximal lesions, the elbow extensors.","additionalDifferentials":["Posterior interosseous nerve syndrome (finger drop without wrist drop or sensory loss)","C7 radiculopathy","Posterior cord brachial plexopathy","Central lesion causing pseudo-radial upper-limb weakness (cortical hand knob stroke)","Lead neuropathy causing bilateral wrist drop","Extensor tendon rupture at the wrist"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies to localise the lesion and identify conduction block at the spiral groove","Needle electromyography sampling triceps, brachioradialis, extensor and forearm muscles to define the level and axonal loss","Plain radiographs of the humerus if fracture or callus entrapment is suspected","High-resolution ultrasound or MRI neurography to demonstrate focal nerve enlargement, mass or entrapment","Serum lead concentration when bilateral wrist drop is present","Cervical spine imaging if radiculopathy is a competing diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Wrist drop developing acutely after humeral fracture or fracture manipulation, suggesting nerve entrapment or laceration","Bilateral wrist drop raising concern for lead toxicity or a systemic neuropathy","Rapidly progressive or painful onset suggesting inflammatory neuralgic amyotrophy or vasculitis","Associated triceps weakness and axillary sensory loss indicating a proximal plexus or cord lesion"],"prognosis":"Compressive and neuropraxic palsies usually recover fully over weeks to a few months once conduction block resolves. Lesions with significant axonal loss recover more slowly and incompletely, and those associated with nerve transection require surgical repair with a more guarded outcome.","pitfalls":["Interpreting grip weakness as median or ulnar dysfunction when it is secondary to loss of wrist extension","Missing a posterior interosseous lesion by not testing for preserved wrist extension and intact sensation","Failing to recognise triceps sparing as evidence for a spiral-groove rather than axillary lesion","Overlooking a humeral fracture or delayed entrapment in callus as the cause","Not considering neuralgic amyotrophy or lead toxicity in atypical, painful or bilateral presentations"],"management":["Removal of the source of external compression in neuropraxic 'Saturday night' palsy and reduction and stabilisation of any humeral shaft fracture causing the lesion","A cock-up wrist splint with dynamic finger-extension outriggers to prevent contractures and maintain hand function during recovery","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to preserve joint range and retrain wrist and finger extension","Watchful waiting for compressive and neuropraxic lesions, which usually recover once conduction block resolves","Serial nerve conduction studies and electromyography to track reinnervation and detect axonal-loss lesions that fail to recover","Surgical exploration with nerve repair or grafting, and tendon transfers as a salvage option, when transection or severe traction prevents recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"radial_scar":{"name":"Radial scar","system":"Breast","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Complex sclerosing lesion","Radial sclerosing lesion"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Breast Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign sclerosing lesion with a fibroelastic core from which ducts and lobules radiate, termed a complex sclerosing lesion when larger than about 1 cm.","Usually impalpable and detected on screening mammography as a stellate or spiculated lesion that closely mimics invasive carcinoma.","The mammographic appearance may show a radiolucent centre and long radiating spicules, sometimes varying between projections ('black star').","Frequently harbours or is associated with atypical hyperplasia, in situ carcinoma or, less often, invasive carcinoma at its periphery.","Core biopsy sampling can under-represent associated atypia, so diagnostic excision or vacuum-assisted excision is usually recommended.","The lesion itself is benign but is regarded as a marker of modestly increased future breast cancer risk.","Preserved myoepithelial layer around entrapped tubules distinguishes it from tubular carcinoma histologically.","Careful radiological-pathological correlation is essential given the strong radiological overlap with malignancy."],"pathophysiology":"A radial scar forms around a central zone of fibroelastic (fibroelastotic) tissue into which ducts and lobules are drawn and distorted, creating a stellate architecture with entrapped, sometimes distorted, tubules at the centre. The radiating arms show a range of proliferative changes including adenosis, ductal hyperplasia, cyst formation and papillomatosis. Although the process is benign and probably reflects a localised reparative or sclerosing response, the entrapment of tubules within dense stroma can mimic stromal invasion, and the associated proliferative epithelium may progress to atypical hyperplasia or carcinoma, explaining both the diagnostic difficulty and the increased cancer risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Tubular carcinoma","Invasive carcinoma of no special type presenting as a spiculated mass","Sclerosing adenosis","Fat necrosis with scarring","Postsurgical scar","Complex sclerosing lesion with atypia","Low-grade adenosquamous carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic mammography with additional projections","Tomosynthesis to characterise the stellate lesion","Targeted ultrasound of the mammographic abnormality","Image-guided core or vacuum-assisted biopsy","Diagnostic surgical or vacuum-assisted excision for definitive assessment","Histology with myoepithelial immunohistochemistry","Radiological-pathological concordance review at multidisciplinary meeting"],"redFlags":["Associated atypical hyperplasia or in situ carcinoma on biopsy","Discordance between imaging and core biopsy findings","Increasing size or a developing palpable mass","Foci suspicious for tubular carcinoma at the lesion periphery","Suspicious microcalcification within the lesion"],"prognosis":"The radial scar itself is benign, and after appropriate excision the outlook is excellent. It signals a mild-to-moderate increase in long-term breast cancer risk, warranting continued participation in screening.","pitfalls":["Confidently diagnosing malignancy on imaging alone, or conversely dismissing a spiculated lesion as benign","Relying on limited core biopsy that misses associated atypia or carcinoma","Misinterpreting entrapped tubules as invasive tubular carcinoma without myoepithelial stains","Failing to ensure radiological-pathological concordance","Neglecting ongoing surveillance given the increased future risk"],"management":["Image-guided core or vacuum-assisted biopsy to sample the stellate lesion and detect associated atypia or malignancy","Diagnostic surgical or vacuum-assisted excision, because core sampling under-represents peripheral atypical hyperplasia, in situ or invasive carcinoma","Histological confirmation with myoepithelial immunohistochemistry to distinguish entrapped tubules from true stromal invasion","Escalation to the relevant oncological pathway if atypical hyperplasia or carcinoma is found at the periphery","Continued participation in mammographic screening given the modestly increased long-term breast cancer risk","Management within a breast multidisciplinary team combining radiology, surgery and pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Breast Tumours (current edition)","Standard textbook of breast imaging on stellate lesions","National breast screening pathology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"radiation_enteritis":{"name":"Radiation enteritis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["radiation enteropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Gastroenterology and oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Small-bowel injury from abdominal or pelvic radiotherapy, in acute and chronic forms","Acute enteritis causes nausea, cramping, diarrhoea and malabsorption during and shortly after treatment and usually settles","Chronic radiation enteropathy develops months to years later with obstruction, malabsorption, bleeding or fistulae","Fixed loops of small bowel in the pelvis (for example after surgery) are especially vulnerable","Chronic disease reflects progressive obliterative endarteritis, ischaemia and fibrosis","Management is largely supportive and nutritional; surgery for strictures or fistulae is high-risk in irradiated tissue"],"pathophysiology":"Radiation enteritis arises from ionising radiation injury to the rapidly dividing small-bowel mucosa and to the bowel vasculature. Acute injury causes mucosal cell loss, villous blunting and inflammation with malabsorption and diarrhoea, and usually recovers. Chronic radiation enteropathy results from progressive submucosal fibrosis and obliterative endarteritis, producing ischaemia, strictures, dysmotility, bacterial overgrowth, bleeding from telangiectasia and fistula formation. Risk relates to dose, technique and factors that fix bowel loops within the radiation field.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent malignancy causing obstruction","Inflammatory bowel disease","Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth","Adhesional small-bowel obstruction","Coeliac or other malabsorptive disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["History of abdominal/pelvic radiotherapy","Cross-sectional imaging or contrast studies of the small bowel","Assessment of nutritional status and malabsorption","Endoscopy where accessible bowel is involved","Evaluation for bacterial overgrowth and bile acid malabsorption"],"redFlags":["Small-bowel obstruction","Enteric fistula formation","Significant gastrointestinal bleeding","Severe malnutrition or features suggesting recurrent tumour"],"prognosis":"Acute enteritis usually resolves after treatment; chronic enteropathy is progressive and difficult to manage, with surgery carrying high complication rates in irradiated bowel.","pitfalls":["Attributing chronic symptoms to recurrent cancer without considering radiation enteropathy (and vice versa)","Underestimating the surgical risk in irradiated bowel","Overlooking bacterial overgrowth and bile acid malabsorption as treatable contributors","Neglecting nutritional support"],"management":["Supportive control of acute symptoms with antiemetics, antidiarrhoeal agents and rehydration during and shortly after radiotherapy","Nutritional support with a low-residue, low-fat diet, and parenteral nutrition where malabsorption is severe","Treatment of bile acid malabsorption with bile acid sequestrants and of small-bowel bacterial overgrowth with antibiotics","Endoscopic or surgical intervention reserved for strictures, obstruction or fistulae, recognising the high complication rate of operating on irradiated bowel","Long-term monitoring of nutritional status, weight and micronutrient and vitamin levels","Preventive optimisation of radiotherapy technique and dietitian-led multidisciplinary follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Clinical oncology references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"radiation_pneumonitis":{"name":"Radiation pneumonitis","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["radiation-induced lung injury","radiation lung injury","post-radiation pneumonitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["It is a subacute inflammatory lung injury that typically develops weeks to a few months after thoracic radiotherapy, most often for lung, breast, oesophageal, or mediastinal tumours","Presenting features include dry cough, exertional breathlessness, low-grade fever, and sometimes pleuritic chest pain, which can mimic infection or cancer progression","Risk is influenced by the volume of lung irradiated, total dose and fractionation, pre-existing lung disease, and concurrent or prior chemotherapy or immunotherapy","Imaging classically shows ground-glass or consolidative change corresponding to the radiation field, though the distribution may extend beyond classic field borders with modern conformal techniques","It is fundamentally a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring careful exclusion of infection, pulmonary embolism, lymphangitic tumour spread, and drug-induced pneumonitis","Mild cases may be managed with observation and supportive care, while symptomatic disease is treated principally with corticosteroids and gradual tapering","Concurrent immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy can potentiate or complicate lung toxicity, making the distinction from immune-related pneumonitis important","Untreated or severe pneumonitis can progress over months to irreversible radiation-induced pulmonary fibrosis"],"pathophysiology":"Ionising radiation damages alveolar epithelial and vascular endothelial cells within the treated lung volume, triggering release of inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species. This produces an acute to subacute alveolitis with increased capillary permeability, protein-rich exudate, and impaired gas exchange, seen clinically as radiation pneumonitis. If inflammation and fibroblast activation persist, the process evolves into progressive collagen deposition and radiation fibrosis with permanent architectural distortion and volume loss. The severity relates to the dose delivered and the volume of normal lung exposed, modified by patient and treatment factors.","additionalDifferentials":["Infective pneumonia, including opportunistic infection","Lymphangitic carcinomatosis or tumour progression in the lung","Pulmonary embolism","Drug-induced or immune checkpoint inhibitor pneumonitis","Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema","Exacerbation of underlying interstitial lung disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph and high-resolution CT correlating changes with the radiation field","Oxygen saturation and, where indicated, arterial blood gas and lung function testing","Inflammatory markers and cultures to help exclude infection","Bronchoscopy with lavage in selected cases to exclude infection or alternative diagnoses","Review of the radiotherapy plan and concurrent systemic therapy including immunotherapy"],"redFlags":["Rapidly worsening breathlessness with hypoxaemia","High fever or purulent sputum suggesting superimposed infection","Extensive bilateral changes extending beyond the radiation field","Progression toward respiratory failure despite corticosteroid therapy"],"prognosis":"Most mild to moderate cases respond well to corticosteroids and resolve, but severe pneumonitis, extensive lung involvement, or progression to fibrosis can cause lasting respiratory impairment and, uncommonly, death.","pitfalls":["Assuming symptoms represent infection and delaying appropriate anti-inflammatory treatment","Missing tumour progression or pulmonary embolism by anchoring on radiation pneumonitis","Tapering corticosteroids too rapidly and provoking a symptom flare","Failing to consider concurrent immune-related pneumonitis in patients on checkpoint inhibitors"],"management":["Corticosteroids with a gradual taper as the mainstay for symptomatic pneumonitis, reserving observation and supportive care for mild disease","Supplemental oxygen and symptomatic measures such as antitussives for breathlessness and cough","Exclusion and treatment of infection, pulmonary embolism and tumour progression before attributing symptoms to pneumonitis","Steroid-sparing immunosuppression for corticosteroid-refractory or immunotherapy-associated cases","Monitoring of oxygenation and lung function with vigilance for progression to radiation fibrosis","Review of the radiotherapy plan and concurrent systemic therapy, coordinated between clinical oncology and respiratory medicine"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology"],"crossLinks":[]},"radiation_proctitis":{"name":"Radiation proctitis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["radiation proctopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Gastroenterology and oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rectal injury from pelvic radiotherapy, occurring in acute (during/soon after treatment) and chronic (months to years later) forms","Acute proctitis causes diarrhoea, urgency, tenesmus and mucus; chronic disease causes rectal bleeding from telangiectasia","It commonly follows radiotherapy for prostate, cervical, rectal or other pelvic cancers","Chronic radiation proctopathy is characterised by mucosal telangiectasia, ischaemia and fibrosis","Endoscopy shows friable mucosa with telangiectasia, and biopsy carries a fistula risk in irradiated tissue","Treatment ranges from topical therapies to endoscopic ablation (for example argon plasma coagulation) for bleeding"],"pathophysiology":"Radiation proctitis results from ionising radiation damage to the rectal mucosa and vasculature. Acute injury reflects direct mucosal cell death and inflammation during treatment, which usually recovers. Chronic radiation proctopathy develops later from progressive obliterative endarteritis and fibrosis, producing mucosal ischaemia and fragile telangiectatic vessels that bleed, and in severe cases strictures, ulceration or fistulae. The chronic changes are progressive and relate to the radiation dose and technique.","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory bowel disease proctitis","Infective proctitis","Ischaemic proctitis","Recurrent or new pelvic malignancy","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["History of pelvic radiotherapy","Sigmoidoscopy or colonoscopy documenting telangiectasia and mucosal change","Cautious biopsy given fistula risk in irradiated tissue","Full blood count and iron studies for chronic bleeding","Imaging if stricture, fistula or recurrent tumour is suspected"],"redFlags":["Severe or transfusion-dependent rectal bleeding","Fistula formation (for example rectovaginal or rectovesical)","Stricturing with obstruction","Features suggesting recurrent malignancy"],"prognosis":"Acute proctitis usually settles after treatment ends; chronic proctopathy tends to be persistent or progressive but bleeding is often controllable with endoscopic and medical measures.","pitfalls":["Aggressive biopsy of irradiated rectum precipitating a fistula","Attributing bleeding to radiation without excluding recurrent tumour","Confusing it with inflammatory bowel disease","Underestimating chronic progressive change"],"management":["Supportive care for acute proctitis with antidiarrhoeals, hydration and topical anti-inflammatory steroid or mesalazine preparations","Endoscopic ablation such as argon plasma coagulation for bleeding chronic telangiectasia","Topical sucralfate or formalin application for refractory haemorrhagic proctopathy","Hyperbaric oxygen therapy in selected refractory cases","Iron replacement and, where needed, blood transfusion for anaemia from chronic bleeding","A cautious approach to biopsy and surgery given the fistula risk in irradiated tissue, with imaging and surgical referral for stricture or fistula"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Clinical oncology references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"radiation_sickness":{"name":"Acute radiation syndrome","system":"Occupational / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ARS","radiation sickness","acute radiation syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hunter's Diseases of Occupations / WHO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Acute radiation syndrome follows a high, acute whole-body or large partial-body dose of penetrating ionising radiation, as in criticality accidents or radiological incidents","It classically evolves through a prodromal phase (nausea, vomiting, malaise), a latent phase, a manifest illness phase and then either recovery or death","The speed of onset of vomiting is a useful early guide to dose severity, with earlier vomiting indicating higher exposure","Recognised dose-dependent sub-syndromes affect the bone marrow (haematopoietic), the gastrointestinal tract, and at very high doses the cardiovascular and central nervous systems","Serial lymphocyte counts over the first day or two are a practical biological indicator of the dose received and prognosis","Management is supportive with infection prevention, blood product and fluid support, and treatment of the failing organ systems, ideally with specialist and haematology input","Contamination with radioactive material must be distinguished from irradiation, and decontamination plus staff protection are early priorities","Localised high-dose exposure can cause cutaneous radiation injury with erythema, blistering and later necrosis, sometimes appearing after a delay"],"pathophysiology":"Ionising radiation deposits energy that damages DNA and cellular structures, with the greatest harm to rapidly dividing tissues. Loss of proliferating stem cells in the bone marrow causes pancytopenia, while injury to the intestinal crypt cells breaks down the mucosal barrier, leading to fluid loss, bleeding and sepsis. Very high doses cause vascular and neurological injury. The clinical course reflects the differing turnover rates of these tissues, giving the characteristic phased illness.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute gastroenteritis or food poisoning","Sepsis of other cause","Chemotherapy or other cytotoxic drug toxicity","Acute leukaemia or bone marrow failure of other cause","Chemical or toxic exposure causing vomiting"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial full blood counts with attention to the rate of lymphocyte decline","Estimation of absorbed dose using time to vomiting and available dosimetry","Biological dosimetry such as chromosome aberration analysis where available","Assessment and monitoring for radioactive contamination","Baseline organ function tests and cultures to guide supportive care"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: high-dose exposure with very early vomiting, rapidly falling lymphocytes, or neurological or cardiovascular signs indicates severe, potentially fatal illness","Signs of gastrointestinal syndrome with intractable diarrhoea and bleeding","Profound neutropenia with infection or sepsis","Confusion, hypotension or seizures suggesting a very high dose"],"prognosis":"Outcome is strongly dose-dependent: lower doses causing mainly marrow suppression may recover with intensive support, whereas gastrointestinal and neurovascular syndromes from very high doses carry a poor to near-uniformly fatal outlook despite treatment.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early prodromal vomiting for simple gastroenteritis","Being reassured by the latent phase and underestimating impending marrow failure","Confusing external irradiation with radioactive contamination and neglecting decontamination","Failing to protect staff and control the scene during care","Not obtaining serial lymphocyte counts for dose estimation"],"management":["External decontamination with removal of clothing and skin washing, plus management of any internal radioactive contamination","Haematopoietic growth factors such as G-CSF together with blood-product transfusion support for the marrow sub-syndrome","Infection prevention with neutropenic precautions and prompt broad-spectrum antimicrobials for the profoundly immunosuppressed patient","Aggressive fluid, electrolyte and nutritional support for the gastrointestinal sub-syndrome with its mucosal barrier breakdown","Antiemetics and symptomatic care through the prodromal and manifest illness phases","Dose estimation from time to vomiting and serial lymphocyte counts to guide prognosis and triage, with consideration of stem-cell transplantation in severe marrow failure","Coordination with haematology and radiation-emergency specialists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Hunter's Diseases of Occupations","WHO guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"radiculopathy":{"name":"Radiculopathy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nerve root compression","pinched nerve","cervical radiculopathy","lumbosacral radiculopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from dysfunction of one or more spinal nerve roots, producing pain, sensory change, and weakness in a dermatomal and myotomal distribution","Pain characteristically radiates along the affected root and is often aggravated by manoeuvres that raise intraspinal pressure such as coughing or straining","The commonest cause is a degenerative disc or spondylotic osteophyte compressing the root within or near the intervertebral foramen","Localising signs follow the root: for example L5 causes foot-drop with weak dorsiflexion, while S1 reduces the ankle reflex and plantar flexion","Provocative tests such as straight-leg raise (lumbar) or Spurling manoeuvre (cervical) support a root cause","Most cases are self-limiting and improve with conservative management, so imaging is guided by red flags and by persistence or progression of deficits","MRI is the preferred imaging test to correlate structural compression with the clinical level","Electromyography helps confirm the affected root and distinguish radiculopathy from plexopathy or peripheral neuropathy"],"pathophysiology":"Radiculopathy reflects mechanical compression, inflammation, or ischaemia of a nerve root, most often where a herniated disc or degenerative spondylotic change narrows the intervertebral foramen or lateral recess. Compression and local inflammatory mediators disrupt axonal conduction and cause both ectopic pain signals and conduction failure, producing radicular pain, dermatomal sensory loss, and myotomal weakness. Chronic compression may lead to axonal loss with denervation. Non-degenerative causes include infection, malignant infiltration, and inflammatory or infiltrative processes affecting the roots.","additionalDifferentials":["Peripheral entrapment neuropathy or mononeuropathy","Brachial or lumbosacral plexopathy","Referred pain from shoulder, hip, or joint disease","Spinal cord compression or myelopathy","Peripheral polyneuropathy","Infective or neoplastic root involvement (e.g. epidural abscess, metastasis, meningeal disease)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the relevant spinal region to identify compressive pathology","CT or CT myelography when MRI is contraindicated","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies to localise the root and exclude mimics","Inflammatory markers and imaging for infection or malignancy when red flags are present","Plain radiographs for alignment, instability, or degenerative change in selected cases"],"redFlags":["New bladder or bowel dysfunction, saddle anaesthesia, or bilateral leg symptoms suggesting cauda equina syndrome (surgical emergency)","Progressive or severe motor weakness rather than isolated pain","Fever, systemic illness, or immunosuppression raising concern for spinal infection","History of cancer, unexplained weight loss, or night pain suggesting malignant infiltration"],"prognosis":"Most degenerative radiculopathies resolve or substantially improve with conservative treatment over weeks to a few months; persistent severe compression, marked motor deficit, or a serious underlying cause predicts a poorer outcome and may require surgery.","pitfalls":["Missing cauda equina syndrome by not asking about sphincter and saddle symptoms","Over-reliance on incidental imaging findings that do not match the clinical level","Attributing radicular pain to a benign disc when infection or malignancy is the true cause","Delaying surgical referral in progressive motor weakness","Confusing peripheral entrapment or plexopathy with root disease without electrophysiology"],"management":["First-line conservative care with analgesia, neuropathic pain agents, activity modification and physiotherapy, as most degenerative radiculopathies settle","Image-guided epidural or transforaminal corticosteroid injection for persistent radicular pain","Surgical decompression such as discectomy or foraminotomy for progressive or severe motor deficit or intractable pain","Directed treatment of a non-degenerative cause, with antimicrobials for infection or oncological therapy for malignant root infiltration","Monitoring of neurological deficits with urgent escalation for progressive weakness or features of cauda equina compression","Imaging guided by red flags and by persistence or progression of symptoms rather than routine early scanning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"rag1_scid":{"name":"RAG1-deficient severe combined immunodeficiency","system":"Immunology / Combined immunodeficiency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RAG1 SCID","recombination-activating gene 1 deficiency","T-B-NK+ SCID"],"chapter":null,"source":"Immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in RAG1, which encodes a core component of the recombinase that initiates V(D)J recombination of antigen-receptor genes.","Classic immunophenotype is T-B-NK+: both T and B lymphocytes are absent because neither can rearrange their antigen receptors, while natural killer cells develop normally as they do not require V(D)J recombination.","Autosomal recessive inheritance affects both sexes equally, distinguishing it clinically from X-linked SCID.","Complete (null) mutations cause classic SCID in early infancy, whereas hypomorphic mutations produce distinct phenotypes such as Omenn syndrome or delayed-onset combined immunodeficiency with granulomas and autoimmunity.","Omenn syndrome presents with erythroderma, alopecia, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, eosinophilia and raised IgE from expansion of oligoclonal autoreactive T cells.","Radiosensitivity is not a feature (unlike Artemis or other DNA-repair SCIDs), which helps in transplant conditioning decisions.","Curative treatment is haematopoietic stem cell transplantation; early transplantation before infective complications gives the best survival.","Newborn TREC screening identifies affected infants before symptoms because T-cell output is profoundly reduced."],"pathophysiology":"RAG1 partners with RAG2 to form the lymphoid-specific recombinase that introduces DNA double-strand breaks at recombination signal sequences flanking the variable, diversity and joining gene segments of the immunoglobulin and T-cell receptor loci. This V(D)J recombination is the essential first step in generating a diverse antigen-receptor repertoire. Complete loss of RAG1 function blocks recombination entirely, arresting T-cell development in the thymus and B-cell development in the marrow at the point where a functional pre-antigen receptor is required, so mature T and B cells fail to emerge. Natural killer cells, whose receptors are germline-encoded and do not require recombination, develop normally, yielding the T-B-NK+ pattern. Hypomorphic mutations that retain partial recombinase activity permit generation of a small, skewed repertoire; the surviving oligoclonal T cells may be autoreactive and infiltrate tissues, producing Omenn syndrome, or immune dysregulation with granulomatous inflammation and autoimmunity in later-onset forms.","additionalDifferentials":["RAG2 deficiency (indistinguishable T-B-NK+ immunophenotype)","Artemis (DCLRE1C) deficiency and other radiosensitive T-B-NK+ SCIDs","Adenosine deaminase deficiency (usually T-B-NK-)","X-linked and JAK3 SCID (T-B+NK-) excluded by the presence of B cells","Omenn syndrome from other causes when erythroderma predominates","Complete DiGeorge syndrome with athymia","Severe congenital infection causing secondary lymphopenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lymphocyte subset flow cytometry confirming the T-B-NK+ pattern","Serum immunoglobulins and specific antibody responses (accounting for maternal IgG)","T-cell receptor repertoire analysis showing absent or markedly restricted oligoclonal diversity","RAG1 gene sequencing to define null versus hypomorphic mutations","Eosinophil count and IgE where Omenn syndrome is suspected","Assessment for maternal T-cell engraftment as a confounder of subset analysis","HLA typing of patient and family for transplant planning"],"redFlags":["Opportunistic or overwhelming infection with failure to thrive in early infancy","Generalised erythroderma with alopecia, lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly suggesting Omenn syndrome","Absent thymic shadow with impalpable lymphoid tissue","Signs of transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease after non-irradiated blood products","Persistent granulomatous inflammation or autoimmunity in the delayed-onset hypomorphic form"],"prognosis":"Classic RAG1 SCID is fatal in infancy without immune reconstitution, but early haematopoietic stem cell transplantation achieves high survival and durable recovery. Omenn syndrome and delayed hypomorphic forms carry additional risk from immune dysregulation and require careful pre-transplant control.","pitfalls":["Hypomorphic mutations can present beyond infancy with autoimmunity or granulomas rather than classic SCID, delaying recognition.","In Omenn syndrome the expanded autoreactive T cells can give a misleadingly normal or raised T-cell count.","Maternal engraftment may produce circulating T cells that obscure the underlying T-cell absence.","Live vaccines and non-irradiated cellular blood products must be withheld once SCID is suspected.","Maternal IgG can normalise measured immunoglobulins early, masking the humoral defect."],"management":["Early allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the definitive, curative treatment restoring T- and B-cell immunity","Protective isolation with antimicrobial prophylaxis against Pneumocystis, fungal and viral infection, and immunoglobulin replacement","Avoidance of live vaccines and use of irradiated, CMV-safe, leucodepleted blood products","Immunosuppression to control the immune dysregulation of Omenn syndrome or hypomorphic disease before transplant conditioning","Recognition that radiosensitivity is absent, so standard rather than DNA-damage-sparing conditioning can be used","Specialist immunology and transplant centre care, with emerging gene-directed therapy as an evolving option","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder and testing of siblings and at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical immunology textbook","International primary immunodeficiency classification and management guidelines","National newborn screening and SCID management guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"ranula":{"name":"Ranula","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Head and Neck","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sublingual mucocoele","oral ranula","simple ranula","sublingual gland retention cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mucus extravasation or retention lesion arising from the sublingual gland, presenting as a soft cystic swelling in the floor of the mouth","The classic oral ranula appears as a bluish, dome-shaped, translucent swelling lateral to the midline that may fluctuate in size","It is termed a pseudocyst when it results from extravasated mucus without a true epithelial lining, the commonest mechanism","Large lesions can displace the tongue and interfere with speech, chewing and swallowing","Diagnosis is largely clinical, supported by imaging to define extent and distinguish a simple oral ranula from a plunging variant","Definitive management usually involves excision of the sublingual gland along with the ranula, which addresses the source of mucus","Marsupialisation alone is associated with a higher recurrence rate than removal of the offending gland","Recurrence and the possibility of a plunging component should be considered when the lesion extends toward the neck"],"pathophysiology":"A ranula develops when saliva from the sublingual gland escapes into the surrounding floor-of-mouth tissues, most often because a duct or acinus is disrupted by trauma or obstruction. The extravasated mucus incites a surrounding reaction and collects as a pseudocyst lacking a true epithelial lining, though less commonly a true retention cyst forms behind a ductal obstruction. Continued salivary secretion causes the swelling to enlarge and fluctuate. If mucus tracks around or through the mylohyoid muscle into the neck, a cervical (plunging) ranula results.","additionalDifferentials":["Plunging (cervical) ranula","Dermoid or epidermoid cyst of the floor of mouth","Sublingual or submandibular gland tumour","Mucocoele of a minor salivary gland","Lymphatic or venous malformation","Thyroglossal or branchial anomaly when near the midline or neck"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound of the floor of mouth and neck to characterise the cystic lesion","MRI or CT to delineate extent and detect a plunging component","Aspiration showing viscous, amylase-rich mucoid fluid where diagnosis is unclear","Histological examination of the excised specimen","Assessment of salivary gland duct anatomy when obstruction is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapid enlargement causing tongue displacement and difficulty with speech or swallowing","Airway compromise from a very large floor-of-mouth swelling","Features atypical for a simple ranula suggesting neoplasm","Extension into the neck indicating a plunging ranula"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent once the sublingual gland is removed, with low recurrence. Simple drainage or marsupialisation without gland removal is more likely to recur.","pitfalls":["Treating with incision and drainage alone, which commonly leads to recurrence","Failing to recognise a plunging component presenting as a neck mass","Mistaking a solid salivary neoplasm for a benign cystic ranula","Injuring the lingual nerve or submandibular duct during sublingual gland excision"],"management":["Excision of the sublingual gland together with the ranula as definitive treatment addressing the source of salivary extravasation","Marsupialisation as a less invasive option, accepting a higher recurrence rate when the gland is left in situ","Intraoral removal of the sublingual gland for a plunging (cervical) ranula rather than cervical drainage alone","Intralesional sclerotherapy with an agent such as picibanil as an alternative in selected cases","Cross-sectional imaging to define extent and identify a plunging component before intervention","Histological examination of the excised specimen to confirm the diagnosis and exclude other cystic lesions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"rapid_onset_dystonia_parkinsonism":{"name":"Rapid-onset dystonia-parkinsonism","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RDP","DYT12","ATP1A3","Na+/K+-ATPase alpha-3 disorder","DYT-ATP1A3"],"chapter":null,"source":"Movement-disorder textbooks with peer-reviewed reviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant disorder caused by variants in ATP1A3, which encodes the alpha-3 subunit of the neuronal sodium-potassium ATPase","Hallmark is abrupt or subacute onset of dystonia and parkinsonism, often over hours to days, frequently triggered by physical or emotional stress, fever, childbirth, or alcohol","Symptoms characteristically follow a rostrocaudal gradient, with prominent bulbar and upper-limb involvement (dysarthria, dysphagia, arm dystonia) more than lower limbs","Parkinsonian features such as bradykinesia and postural instability accompany the dystonia, but there is typically little or no tremor and a poor response to levodopa","After the dramatic initial onset the deficit tends to stabilise, sometimes with a second abrupt worsening, rather than steadily progressing","ATP1A3 variants cause a spectrum that also includes alternating hemiplegia of childhood and the CAPOS syndrome, which can overlap","Brain imaging and dopaminergic imaging are typically unremarkable, distinguishing it from degenerative parkinsonism","Diagnosis rests on the distinctive stress-triggered abrupt onset and is confirmed by ATP1A3 molecular testing; treatment is largely supportive as levodopa is generally ineffective"],"pathophysiology":"Rapid-onset dystonia-parkinsonism is caused by dominant variants in ATP1A3, encoding the alpha-3 subunit of the neuronal sodium-potassium ATPase that maintains transmembrane ion gradients essential for repolarisation and neuronal excitability. The variants reduce pump function, and this reserve deficit is thought to become clinically manifest when metabolic demand rises, which is why physiological stressors such as fever, exertion, emotional stress, or childbirth precipitate the abrupt onset. Impaired ion homeostasis within cerebellar and basal ganglia circuits produces combined dystonia and parkinsonism without frank neurodegeneration, accounting for the normal structural and dopaminergic imaging.","additionalDifferentials":["Young-onset Parkinson disease and other genetic parkinsonism","Dopa-responsive dystonia","DYT1 and other early-onset dystonias","Wilson disease","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder given the abrupt stress-related onset","Other ATP1A3-related phenotypes (alternating hemiplegia of childhood, CAPOS syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["ATP1A3 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Brain MRI, expected to be unremarkable, to exclude structural causes","Dopaminergic (presynaptic) imaging, typically normal, to distinguish degenerative parkinsonism","A levodopa trial, which characteristically shows little benefit, helping separate it from dopa-responsive conditions","Serum copper and caeruloplasmin to exclude Wilson disease"],"redFlags":["Abrupt bulbar dysfunction with dysphagia and aspiration or airway risk","Severe generalised dystonia or status dystonicus following a trigger","Rapid neurological deterioration during febrile illness or physiological stress","Secondary decompensation with dehydration or immobility during an acute episode"],"prognosis":"After the characteristic abrupt onset the disorder tends to plateau rather than progress steadily, though a further stepwise deterioration can occur; disability is often significant, particularly from bulbar and upper-limb involvement, and response to medication is generally limited.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the stress-triggered abrupt onset as a functional movement disorder","Expecting and relying on a levodopa response, which is characteristically poor","Overlooking the diagnosis because imaging is normal","Not recognising the broader ATP1A3 spectrum and its overlapping phenotypes when counselling families"],"management":["Counselling to avoid or mitigate recognised triggers such as fever, physical exertion, emotional stress and alcohol that precipitate abrupt onset or stepwise worsening","A trial of levodopa and other anti-dystonic agents such as benzodiazepines, although the response is characteristically poor","Botulinum toxin injection for troublesome focal dystonia","Speech and language therapy with swallow assessment and dysphagia management for prominent bulbar involvement","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to address upper-limb dystonia and postural instability","Multidisciplinary movement-disorder care with a supportive and symptomatic focus given the limited pharmacological response","Genetic counselling and family testing for this autosomal dominant ATP1A3-related disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: ATP1A3-Related Neurologic Disorders","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"rapidly_progressive_gn":{"name":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RPGN","crescentic glomerulonephritis","crescentic GN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["RPGN is a nephrological emergency defined by a rapid loss of renal function over days to weeks, accompanied by a nephritic urine sediment with dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts","The histological hallmark is crescent formation in a large proportion of glomeruli, reflecting severe glomerular injury and rupture of capillary walls","It is classified into three immunopathological groups: anti-glomerular basement membrane disease, immune-complex-mediated disease, and pauci-immune (usually ANCA-associated) disease","Coexisting pulmonary haemorrhage with glomerulonephritis (pulmonary-renal syndrome) suggests anti-GBM disease or ANCA-associated vasculitis and demands urgent evaluation","Early kidney biopsy is central to diagnosis and to gauging the balance of active (treatable) crescents versus fibrous (irreversible) scarring","Serological testing for ANCA, anti-GBM antibodies, complement levels, and other markers helps categorise the disease and guide treatment","Outcome depends critically on how quickly immunosuppression is started, since delay allows crescents to organise into irreversible fibrosis","Management principles centre on prompt aggressive immunosuppression, with plasma exchange added in anti-GBM disease and in selected severe or dialysis-dependent vasculitis cases"],"pathophysiology":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis describes a clinical syndrome of accelerating renal failure caused by severe inflammatory glomerular injury, characterised pathologically by extensive crescents. Crescents form when damage to the glomerular capillary wall allows plasma proteins, fibrin, and inflammatory cells to enter Bowman space, where proliferating parietal epithelial cells and macrophages compress the glomerular tuft. Three mechanisms underlie the syndrome: linear deposition of antibodies against the glomerular basement membrane (anti-GBM disease), deposition of immune complexes as seen in lupus, post-infectious, and IgA-related disease, and a pauci-immune process typically associated with ANCA-mediated small-vessel vasculitis. In all forms, unchecked inflammation rapidly destroys glomeruli and, without timely treatment, progresses to fibrous scarring and end-stage kidney disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute tubular necrosis","Acute interstitial nephritis","Thrombotic microangiopathy","Atheroembolic renal disease","Severe acute post-infectious glomerulonephritis","Accelerated hypertension with renal injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine microscopy for dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts","Kidney biopsy for crescent quantification and immunofluorescence pattern","ANCA testing (including antigen-specific assays) and anti-GBM antibody titres","Complement levels and other autoimmune serologies including antinuclear and anti-dsDNA antibodies","Chest imaging when pulmonary haemorrhage is suspected","Quantification of proteinuria and serial renal function monitoring"],"redFlags":["Pulmonary haemorrhage with haemoptysis and hypoxaemia indicating a pulmonary-renal syndrome","Rapidly rising creatinine or oliguria threatening imminent dialysis dependence","Severe hypertension or fluid overload","Systemic vasculitic features such as mononeuritis, purpura, or ENT involvement"],"prognosis":"Untreated RPGN progresses to end-stage kidney disease within weeks. With early immunosuppression many patients recover useful renal function, but prognosis worsens sharply with delayed treatment, a high proportion of fibrous crescents, dialysis dependence at presentation, and anti-GBM disease with advanced injury. Relapse is common in ANCA-associated disease and requires ongoing surveillance.","pitfalls":["Delaying biopsy and treatment while awaiting serology, allowing crescents to become irreversible","Missing coexisting pulmonary haemorrhage that changes urgency and management","Assuming a negative ANCA excludes vasculitis, since a minority are seronegative","Starting heavy immunosuppression in a kidney already dominated by fibrous scarring with little chance of recovery"],"management":["Urgent immunosuppression with high-dose corticosteroids combined with cyclophosphamide or rituximab to arrest crescentic injury","Plasma exchange for anti-GBM disease and for ANCA-associated disease with pulmonary haemorrhage or severe renal failure","Renal replacement therapy for established kidney failure, alongside general supportive measures and blood-pressure control","Directed treatment of the underlying process, such as lupus nephritis or a post-infectious cause, guided by early biopsy and serology","Maintenance immunosuppression with monitoring for relapse and for treatment-related infection, including prophylaxis against Pneumocystis","Immediate nephrology referral, as delayed treatment leads to irreversible end-stage kidney disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"rapunzel_syndrome":{"name":"Rapunzel syndrome (trichobezoar)","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["trichobezoar with tail","gastric trichobezoar","hair bezoar syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare form of trichobezoar in which a gastric hair mass has a tail extending through the pylorus into the small bowel","Almost always occurs in young females with underlying trichotillomania (hair-pulling) and trichophagia (hair-eating)","Presents with epigastric pain, early satiety, a palpable abdominal mass, weight loss, and sometimes halitosis","Complications include gastric ulceration, obstruction, perforation, and, rarely, intussusception or pancreatitis","A firm, mobile, mouldable epigastric mass on examination is a valuable clue","Cross-sectional imaging or endoscopy demonstrates the intragastric mass with its characteristic small-bowel tail","Definitive management is surgical or endoscopic removal together with psychiatric treatment of the underlying hair-pulling disorder to prevent recurrence"],"pathophysiology":"Rapunzel syndrome arises when compulsively ingested hair (trichophagia), which is indigestible and slippery, becomes trapped in the gastric folds and enmeshes with mucus and food debris to form a trichobezoar. Over time the mass conforms to the shape of the stomach and a long tail extends through the pylorus into the small intestine. The accumulating mass impairs gastric emptying, causes mucosal pressure necrosis and ulceration, and can precipitate obstruction or perforation; the behavioural substrate is an impulse-control/psychiatric disorder driving repeated hair ingestion.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastric outlet obstruction from other causes","Other bezoar types (phytobezoar, pharmacobezoar)","Gastric or abdominal neoplasm presenting as a mass","Peptic ulcer disease","Gastroparesis","Small-bowel obstruction of another aetiology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper endoscopy to visualise and characterise the bezoar","CT of the abdomen to define the mass, its tail, and complications","Upper gastrointestinal contrast study demonstrating a mobile intraluminal filling defect","Full blood count and iron studies to assess anaemia and nutritional status","Psychiatric assessment for trichotillomania and trichophagia"],"redFlags":["Signs of gastrointestinal perforation or peritonitis","Complete gastric outlet or small-bowel obstruction","Significant gastrointestinal bleeding from mucosal ulceration","Severe malnutrition or weight loss"],"prognosis":"With complete removal of the bezoar and effective treatment of the underlying psychiatric disorder the prognosis is good, but failure to address the hair-pulling behaviour commonly leads to recurrence.","pitfalls":["Removing the bezoar without treating the underlying trichotillomania, inviting recurrence","Underestimating the extent of the small-bowel tail before intervention","Missing complications such as ulceration, obstruction, or perforation","Failing to consider the diagnosis in a young woman with an epigastric mass and weight loss"],"management":["Surgical removal of the trichobezoar, usually by laparotomy given the size and small-bowel tail, with endoscopic removal reserved for small masses","Management of complications such as gastric ulceration, obstruction or perforation at the time of intervention","Correction of anaemia and nutritional deficiency with iron and dietary support","Psychiatric treatment of the underlying trichotillomania and trichophagia with habit-reversal behavioural therapy and consideration of an SSRI","Long-term psychiatric and family follow-up to detect and prevent recurrence, which is common if the behaviour is not addressed","Combined surgical and psychiatric multidisciplinary management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Maingot's Abdominal Operations"],"crossLinks":[]},"rasmussen_encephalitis":{"name":"Rasmussen encephalitis","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rasmussen syndrome","chronic focal encephalitis","Rasmussen's encephalitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare chronic inflammatory disorder affecting one cerebral hemisphere, presenting most often in children with drug-resistant focal seizures","Epilepsia partialis continua, in which continuous focal motor seizure activity affects part of the body, is a characteristic feature","The clinical course brings progressive hemiparesis, cognitive decline and, when the dominant hemisphere is involved, language impairment","Serial MRI shows progressive unilateral hemispheric atrophy, often with insular and periventricular involvement","The presumed mechanism is a cell-mediated immune process with T-lymphocyte-driven inflammation and neuronal loss confined to one hemisphere","Immunotherapy may slow progression and help seizures, but definitive seizure control usually requires functional disconnection or hemispherectomy","Hemispherectomy typically stops the intractable seizures at the cost of a predictable fixed hemiparesis and visual field deficit"],"pathophysiology":"Rasmussen encephalitis is a chronic, immune-mediated inflammatory disorder characteristically restricted to a single cerebral hemisphere. Histopathology shows T-cell-dominant perivascular inflammation, microglial nodules, reactive gliosis and progressive neuronal loss, supporting a predominantly cell-mediated cytotoxic process, although humoral and antigen-driven mechanisms have also been proposed. Ongoing inflammation drives relentless cortical damage and hemispheric atrophy, producing the combination of drug-resistant focal seizures, epilepsia partialis continua and progressive contralateral neurological and cognitive deficits.","additionalDifferentials":["Focal cortical dysplasia and other structural epilepsies","Hemimegalencephaly","Sturge-Weber syndrome","Mitochondrial disease (e.g. MELAS) causing epilepsia partialis continua","Viral or paraneoplastic encephalitis","Low-grade hemispheric glioma","Autoimmune encephalitis with focal seizures"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial brain MRI to demonstrate progressive unilateral hemispheric atrophy","Electroencephalography, including prolonged monitoring, showing unihemispheric slowing and epileptiform activity","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis including oligoclonal bands to support inflammation and exclude infection","Brain biopsy in selected cases to confirm characteristic inflammatory histopathology","PET or SPECT to assess unilateral hemispheric dysfunction","Autoimmune and metabolic screens to exclude mimics"],"redFlags":["Epilepsia partialis continua that is refractory to antiseizure medication","Rapidly progressive hemiparesis or cognitive and language decline","Status epilepticus requiring intensive management","Progressive hemispheric atrophy on serial imaging"],"prognosis":"Untreated, Rasmussen encephalitis progresses to a fixed severe hemiparesis, cognitive impairment and intractable seizures. Immunotherapy may modify the tempo, but seizure freedom generally requires hemispherectomy or hemispheric disconnection, which halts the epilepsy while leaving a predictable permanent motor and visual deficit; earlier surgery may better preserve overall function.","pitfalls":["Attributing early focal seizures to ordinary focal epilepsy and missing the progressive inflammatory course","Delaying surgical referral in a dominant-hemisphere case for fear of deficits, allowing further decline","Failing to obtain serial imaging to demonstrate characteristic progressive atrophy","Overlooking mimics such as mitochondrial disease or a structural lesion before committing to diagnosis"],"management":["Immunotherapy with corticosteroids, immunoglobulin, plasma exchange and agents such as tacrolimus or rituximab to slow the inflammatory progression","Antiseizure medications for symptom control, recognising the focal seizures are typically drug-resistant","Functional hemispherectomy or hemispheric disconnection as the definitive procedure for seizure freedom, accepting the resulting fixed hemiparesis","Rehabilitation with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and, when the dominant hemisphere is affected, speech and language therapy, especially after surgery","Serial MRI and EEG monitoring to track hemispheric atrophy and epileptiform activity","Coordinated care through a paediatric epilepsy-surgery multidisciplinary team including neuropsychology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Swaiman's Pediatric Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"rat_bite_fever":{"name":"Rat-bite fever","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Streptobacillus moniliformis infection","Spirillum minus infection","streptobacillary rat-bite fever","Haverhill fever","sodoku"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases / Manson's Tropical Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rat-bite fever has two forms: streptobacillary disease (Streptobacillus moniliformis), predominant in the Americas and Europe, and spirillary disease (Spirillum minus, 'sodoku'), predominant in Asia","Acquired from the bite or scratch of rats or other rodents, or by handling them; streptobacillary disease may also be foodborne (Haverhill fever) from contaminated milk or water","Streptobacillary disease presents abruptly with fever, rigors, myalgia, a maculopapular or petechial rash favouring the extremities including palms and soles, and a migratory polyarthritis","Spirillary disease has a longer incubation, relapsing fever, induration or ulceration at the healed bite site with regional lymphadenopathy, and a rash, but arthritis is uncommon","Diagnosis of streptobacillary disease relies on culture of blood or joint fluid (fastidious organism) or molecular methods; Spirillum minus cannot be cultured and is identified by dark-field microscopy of lesion or blood material","Untreated disease can lead to endocarditis, myocarditis, meningitis, abscesses and septic arthritis, with appreciable mortality","Penicillin-class antibiotics are the treatment of choice, with alternatives for penicillin allergy and escalation for endocarditis"],"pathophysiology":"The causative bacteria are commensals of the rodent nasopharynx and are introduced into humans through bites, scratches, handling or ingestion of contaminated food and water. Streptobacillus moniliformis, a fastidious pleomorphic Gram-negative bacillus, disseminates haematogenously to produce systemic febrile illness, immune-mediated rash and a reactive or septic polyarthritis, and can seed heart valves and other sites. Spirillum minus, a spiral organism, causes relapsing fever with characteristic reactivation of the healed inoculation site and regional lymphadenitis. Both can progress to metastatic infection and endovascular involvement if untreated.","additionalDifferentials":["Meningococcaemia and other causes of fever with petechial rash","Disseminated gonococcal infection (fever, rash, arthritis)","Rocky Mountain spotted fever and other rickettsioses","Leptospirosis","Secondary syphilis (rash on palms and soles)","Reactive or septic arthritis of other cause","Infective endocarditis from other organisms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures (prolonged incubation for the fastidious organism) and culture of joint aspirate","Dark-field microscopy of blood, lesion exudate or lymph node aspirate for Spirillum minus","Molecular testing (PCR/16S sequencing) for Streptobacillus moniliformis","Full blood count and inflammatory markers","Echocardiography if endocarditis is suspected","Joint imaging or aspiration in arthritis, and lumbar puncture if meningitis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Features of endocarditis, myocarditis or a new murmur","Meningism or altered consciousness suggesting central nervous system involvement","Septic arthritis or deep abscess formation","Haemodynamic instability or sepsis","Persistent or relapsing fever despite initial treatment"],"prognosis":"With prompt antibiotic therapy the prognosis is excellent, but untreated rat-bite fever carries substantial mortality, largely from endocarditis and other metastatic complications.","pitfalls":["Not eliciting a history of rodent exposure, which is key to considering the diagnosis","Discarding blood cultures too early before the fastidious organism grows","Attempting to culture Spirillum minus, which does not grow on artificial media","Mistaking the rash and arthritis for meningococcaemia or disseminated gonococcal infection without pursuing exposure history","Under-treating when endovascular or CNS involvement is present"],"management":["A beta-lactam such as penicillin as first-line antimicrobial therapy for both streptobacillary and spirillary disease, with doxycycline or a cephalosporin as alternatives","Prolonged intravenous therapy for endovascular involvement such as endocarditis","Cleansing and care of the inoculation wound","Evaluation and management of metastatic complications including septic arthritis, meningitis and abscesses","Echocardiography and specialist input where endocarditis is suspected","Advice on rodent-handling precautions and exposure avoidance to prevent re-infection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"rathke_cleft_cyst":{"name":"Rathke cleft cyst","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rathke cleft cyst","Rathke pouch cyst","RCC","pars intermedia cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign, non-neoplastic epithelial cyst arising from remnants of Rathke pouch, usually located between the anterior and posterior pituitary and intrasellar with or without suprasellar extension","Often an incidental imaging finding and asymptomatic","When symptomatic it can cause headache, visual disturbance from chiasmal compression and hypopituitarism, and diabetes insipidus may occur","Hyperprolactinaemia from a stalk effect can cause menstrual disturbance or galactorrhoea","MRI typically shows a non-enhancing cystic sellar lesion, and a non-enhancing intracystic nodule is a characteristic feature","It should be distinguished from a cystic pituitary adenoma and from craniopharyngioma, both of which it can mimic","Asymptomatic cysts are managed conservatively with surveillance, whereas symptomatic cysts may need transsphenoidal drainage or fenestration rather than complete excision","Recurrence can occur after drainage, so follow-up imaging is advisable"],"pathophysiology":"Rathke cleft cysts derive from the embryonic Rathke pouch, the ectodermal invagination that forms the anterior and intermediate pituitary. Persistence and expansion of the cleft between the pars anterior and pars nervosa produces a cyst lined by ciliated cuboidal or columnar epithelium, often with goblet cells, containing mucoid or serous fluid. Most remain small and clinically silent, but progressive fluid accumulation can enlarge the cyst to compress the normal pituitary, the stalk (raising prolactin by disinhibition) and the optic chiasm, and can disrupt antidiuretic hormone pathways. Rupture or inflammation of the cyst contents may provoke symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystic pituitary adenoma","Craniopharyngioma, especially the cystic adamantinomatous form","Arachnoid cyst","Empty sella","Epidermoid or dermoid cyst","Hypophysitis","Suprasellar germ cell tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pituitary MRI with contrast, showing a non-enhancing cyst and any intracystic nodule","Anterior pituitary hormone profile to detect hypopituitarism","Serum prolactin, which may be mildly elevated from a stalk effect","Assessment of posterior pituitary function with plasma and urine osmolality if diabetes insipidus is suspected","Formal visual field testing when there is suprasellar extension","Histology after drainage to confirm the epithelial lining and exclude craniopharyngioma"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual field loss from chiasmal compression","New hypopituitarism or diabetes insipidus","Features suggesting cyst apoplexy or rupture with acute headache","Rapid enlargement or atypical imaging suggesting an alternative neoplasm"],"prognosis":"Generally excellent; incidental cysts often remain stable and symptomatic cysts usually improve after drainage or fenestration, although endocrine deficits may persist and cysts can recur, warranting follow-up imaging.","pitfalls":["Attempting aggressive complete excision, which raises the risk of hypopituitarism and cerebrospinal fluid leak, when drainage or fenestration suffices","Misdiagnosing it as a cystic adenoma or craniopharyngioma","Missing associated hypopituitarism or diabetes insipidus before surgery","Failing to arrange surveillance imaging given the recurrence risk"],"management":["Conservative observation with surveillance MRI for incidental, asymptomatic cysts","Transsphenoidal drainage and fenestration rather than aggressive excision for symptomatic cysts, to relieve compression while limiting hypopituitarism and CSF leak","Hormone replacement for anterior hypopituitarism and desmopressin for diabetes insipidus","Formal visual field assessment and monitoring where there is suprasellar extension and chiasmal compression","Recognition that mild hyperprolactinaemia is a stalk-disinhibition effect requiring treatment of the cyst rather than a dopamine agonist","Long-term follow-up imaging for recurrence with combined endocrine and neurosurgical review"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","DeGroot's Endocrinology","Pituitary Society reviews on sellar cystic lesions"],"crossLinks":[]},"raynaud_phenomenon":{"name":"Raynaud phenomenon","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Raynaud's phenomenon","Raynaud syndrome","primary Raynaud","secondary Raynaud"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Episodic, reversible digital vasospasm provoked by cold or emotional stress, classically producing a triphasic colour change of pallor, then cyanosis, then reactive hyperaemia","Primary Raynaud is common, typically begins in young women, is symmetric, and occurs without an underlying disease or tissue damage","Secondary Raynaud arises from an underlying condition, most importantly connective tissue disease, and carries a greater risk of digital ischaemia and ulceration","Features favouring a secondary cause include older age at onset, asymmetry, digital ulcers or pitting scars, and abnormal nailfold capillaries","A positive antinuclear antibody and abnormal nailfold capillaroscopy suggest an evolving connective tissue disease, often systemic sclerosis","General measures emphasise cold avoidance, keeping the whole body warm, and smoking cessation","Pharmacological therapy, when needed, uses vasodilators (calcium channel blockers first-line, with other agents for refractory or ischaemic disease)","Critical digital ischaemia is an emergency requiring urgent assessment and consideration of secondary causes"],"pathophysiology":"Raynaud phenomenon results from exaggerated vasoconstriction of digital arteries, precapillary arterioles, and cutaneous arteriovenous shunts in response to cold or stress. In primary disease this reflects a functional imbalance of vascular tone with heightened sympathetic and local vascular reactivity and no structural vessel damage. In secondary disease, structural vasculopathy, endothelial dysfunction, and abnormal balance of vasoconstrictors (such as endothelin) and vasodilators (such as nitric oxide), often in the setting of systemic sclerosis or other connective tissue disease, produce fixed narrowing that predisposes to persistent ischaemia, ulceration, and tissue loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic sclerosis and other connective tissue diseases (secondary Raynaud)","Thoracic outlet syndrome and other causes of arterial compression","Atherosclerotic or embolic peripheral arterial disease","Vibration-induced (hand-arm) vascular injury and occupational causes","Drug- or toxin-induced vasospasm","Acrocyanosis, chilblains, and cryoglobulinaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nailfold capillaroscopy to distinguish primary from secondary disease","Antinuclear antibody and, if positive, connective tissue disease-specific antibodies","Inflammatory markers and assessment for features of systemic autoimmune disease","Vascular assessment for fixed arterial obstruction when ischaemia is asymmetric or severe","Targeted investigations for occupational, drug, or haematological causes when suspected"],"redFlags":["Digital ulceration, gangrene, or critical ischaemia threatening tissue loss","Asymmetric or unilateral symptoms suggesting fixed arterial obstruction or embolism","New Raynaud phenomenon in an older adult indicating a likely secondary cause","Accompanying features of connective tissue disease such as skin tightening, dysphagia, or breathlessness","Abnormal nailfold capillaries with positive autoantibodies signalling evolving systemic sclerosis"],"prognosis":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon is benign, usually stable or improving over time, and rarely causes tissue damage. Secondary Raynaud has a more variable outlook determined by the underlying disease and carries a real risk of digital ulceration and loss, particularly in systemic sclerosis.","pitfalls":["Labelling all Raynaud as benign primary disease without screening for secondary causes","Missing evolving systemic sclerosis by not performing nailfold capillaroscopy and autoantibody testing","Underestimating critical digital ischaemia as ordinary vasospasm","Overlooking drug, occupational, or vascular contributors","Neglecting non-pharmacological measures such as whole-body warming and smoking cessation"],"management":["General measures of whole-body warming, cold avoidance, smoking cessation and withdrawal of vasoconstrictive drugs","First-line dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers such as nifedipine to reduce attack frequency and severity","Second-line vasodilators including phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors, topical nitrates or angiotensin-receptor blockers when calcium-channel blockers are insufficient","Intravenous prostacyclin analogues such as iloprost for critical digital ischaemia, and endothelin-receptor antagonists such as bosentan to prevent recurrent digital ulcers in systemic sclerosis","Identification and treatment of an underlying connective tissue disease in secondary disease, guided by antinuclear antibody testing and nailfold capillaroscopy","Surveillance for digital ulceration and tissue loss, with rheumatology referral when a secondary cause is suspected","Management of a digital ischaemic crisis with prostanoids and consideration of botulinum toxin or digital sympathectomy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"raynaud_phenomenon_primary":{"name":"Primary Raynaud Phenomenon","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Primary Raynaud's","Raynaud's disease","Idiopathic Raynaud phenomenon"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rheumatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by episodic, reversible digital vasospasm provoked by cold or emotional stress, classically with a triphasic colour change of pallor, then cyanosis, then rubor on rewarming.","Primary disease is idiopathic, typically begins in young women, involves the fingers and often the toes symmetrically, and shows no evidence of an underlying disorder.","Features favouring primary disease include normal nailfold capillaries, a negative antinuclear antibody, normal inflammatory markers and the absence of digital ulceration, pitting or tissue loss.","The diagnosis is essentially clinical, and the key task is to separate primary disease from secondary Raynaud phenomenon due to connective tissue disease, especially systemic sclerosis.","Nailfold capillaroscopy and autoantibody testing are the most useful investigations for distinguishing primary from secondary disease.","Onset in later life, asymmetric or severe attacks, tissue damage or abnormal capillaroscopy point to a secondary cause and warrant further evaluation.","First-line management is conservative, centred on keeping the whole body and the extremities warm, avoiding cold and vibration, and stopping smoking.","When drug treatment is needed, dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as nifedipine are the first-line vasodilators."],"pathophysiology":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon reflects an exaggerated but fully reversible vasoconstrictor response of the digital arteries, precapillary arterioles and cutaneous arteriovenous shunts to cold and emotional stimuli, in the absence of structural vascular disease or an underlying systemic illness. The disturbance is functional: heightened sympathetically mediated vasoconstriction combines with increased vascular smooth-muscle sensitivity, particularly through alpha-2 adrenoceptors, and an imbalance between vasoconstrictor mediators such as endothelin and thromboxane and vasodilators such as nitric oxide and calcitonin gene-related peptide. Transient closure of the digital vessels produces pallor as flow ceases, cyanosis as the residual blood desaturates, and reactive hyperaemia with rubor on rewarming. Because there is no fixed obstruction, inflammatory vasculopathy or fibrosis, attacks resolve completely and, unlike secondary disease, do not progress to irreversible ischaemia, ulceration or digital loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary Raynaud phenomenon due to systemic sclerosis, systemic lupus erythematosus, mixed connective tissue disease or Sjögren syndrome","Occupational vibration white finger (hand–arm vibration syndrome)","Obstructive arterial disease such as atherosclerosis, thromboangiitis obliterans or thoracic outlet syndrome","Acrocyanosis and chilblains (pernio)","Drug- or toxin-induced vasospasm, for example from beta-blockers, ergot alkaloids, stimulants or certain chemotherapy","Hyperviscosity or occlusive states such as cryoglobulinaemia, cold agglutinin disease or polycythaemia","Complex regional pain syndrome or embolic digital ischaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nailfold capillaroscopy to look for the enlarged, distorted or dropout capillaries typical of secondary disease","Antinuclear antibody and, if positive, extractable nuclear antigen and scleroderma-specific antibodies","Inflammatory markers, namely the erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","Full blood count to detect a myeloproliferative or paraproteinaemic cause","Cryoglobulins and cold agglutinins where clinically suggested","Thyroid function testing where clinically indicated","Vascular imaging or pressure studies if fixed arterial obstruction is suspected"],"redFlags":["Digital ulceration, pitting scars, gangrene or other tissue loss","Asymmetric attacks or involvement confined to one or two digits, suggesting a structural or embolic cause","New onset in an older adult or in a man","Abnormal nailfold capillaroscopy or positive scleroderma-specific autoantibodies","Accompanying features of connective tissue disease such as arthralgia, skin tightening, dysphagia or dryness of the eyes and mouth"],"prognosis":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon is benign, does not cause permanent tissue damage, and is often controlled with conservative measures alone. A small proportion of patients initially labelled as primary later evolve into a secondary form, so periodic reassessment is worthwhile.","pitfalls":["Labelling disease as primary without checking nailfold capillaries and autoantibodies, thereby missing early systemic sclerosis.","Attributing digital ulceration or tissue loss to primary disease, when these features indicate a secondary cause.","Prescribing, or failing to review, vasoconstricting drugs such as non-selective beta-blockers or certain migraine and stimulant medications that provoke or worsen attacks.","Reassuring a patient with recent onset in later life or with asymmetric symptoms without excluding obstructive arterial disease.","Confusing acrocyanosis or chilblains, which are persistent and lack the sharply demarcated triphasic colour change, with true Raynaud attacks."],"management":["Conservative first-line management with whole-body and extremity warming, cold avoidance, smoking cessation and withdrawal of vasoconstrictor drugs","Reassurance about the benign nature of primary disease, which does not cause permanent tissue damage","Dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers such as nifedipine for frequent or troublesome attacks","Second-line vasodilators such as phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors or topical nitrates when calcium-channel blockers are inadequate","Periodic clinical reassessment with nailfold capillaroscopy and antinuclear antibody testing, as a minority evolve into a secondary form","Avoidance of aggravating factors including certain drugs and occupational vibration exposure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard rheumatology reference textbooks","National and international guidelines on the management of Raynaud phenomenon","Consensus classification criteria for primary and secondary Raynaud phenomenon"],"crossLinks":[]},"reactive_perforating_collagenosis":{"name":"Reactive perforating collagenosis","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RPC","acquired reactive perforating collagenosis","acquired perforating dermatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["One of the perforating dermatoses, characterised by umbilicated papules and nodules with a central keratotic plug through which altered collagen is extruded (transepidermal elimination)","Lesions are often intensely itchy and show the Koebner phenomenon, appearing in linear arrays along scratch marks","The acquired adult form is strongly associated with diabetes mellitus and chronic kidney disease, particularly in patients on dialysis","A rare inherited childhood form exists, precipitated by superficial trauma or cold","Commonly affects the extensor limbs, trunk, and backs of the hands, with lesions typically resolving over weeks to leave scars or hyperpigmentation as new crops appear","Diagnosis is confirmed on biopsy showing a cup-shaped epidermal depression with elimination of degenerate, basophilic collagen bundles through the epidermis","Pruritus and scratching are central to pathogenesis, so lesions cluster where the patient can reach","Management addresses the underlying disease and the itch, using emollients, topical corticosteroids, keratolytics, antihistamines, phototherapy, and control of pruritus"],"pathophysiology":"Reactive perforating collagenosis is a disorder of transepidermal elimination in which altered dermal collagen is extruded through the epidermis. Superficial trauma and, in acquired cases, the metabolic disturbances of diabetes and renal failure are thought to damage dermal collagen and provoke an epidermal response that channels the degenerate collagen to the surface. Persistent scratching in response to itch perpetuates the trauma and drives the characteristic Koebnerised, umbilicated papules, so that lesions cluster at accessible, frequently scratched sites and continue to recur while itch and the underlying systemic disturbance persist.","additionalDifferentials":["Other perforating dermatoses (elastosis perforans serpiginosa, perforating folliculitis, Kyrle disease)","Prurigo nodularis","Folliculitis","Hypertrophic lichen planus","Perforating granuloma annulare","Multiple keratoacanthomas","Dermatofibroma (for a solitary lesion)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing a cup-shaped epidermal invagination with transepidermal elimination of collagen","Special stains (such as Masson trichrome and elastic stains) to characterise the eliminated material and distinguish it from elastic-fibre perforating disease","Fasting glucose and HbA1c to screen for diabetes mellitus","Renal function tests (urea, creatinine, eGFR) to detect chronic kidney disease","Assessment of dialysis status and adequacy in renal patients","Screening for other causes of pruritus (for example liver and thyroid function) where relevant"],"redFlags":["Newly diagnosed or poorly controlled diabetes or chronic kidney disease uncovered by the skin findings","Intractable pruritus with a marked impact on quality of life and sleep","Secondary infection of excoriated, ulcerated lesions","Atypical or rapidly growing nodules requiring biopsy to exclude malignancy"],"prognosis":"The acquired form runs a chronic, relapsing course tied to control of the underlying systemic disease; individual lesions heal, often with scarring, but new crops recur while itch and metabolic disturbance persist.","pitfalls":["Overlooking undiagnosed diabetes or renal disease in an adult presenting with perforating papules","Treating the skin without addressing the underlying itch and metabolic cause","Confusing it with prurigo nodularis or the other perforating dermatoses without biopsy","Underestimating the role of scratching in perpetuating lesions"],"management":["Optimise glycaemic control and manage the underlying chronic kidney disease and dialysis adequacy, as these metabolic disturbances drive the acquired adult form","Control pruritus and interrupt the scratch-Koebner cycle with emollients, topical or intralesional corticosteroids and oral antihistamines","Apply topical keratolytics and retinoids to address the central keratotic plugs, and use narrowband UVB phototherapy for widespread disease","Consider oral allopurinol or systemic retinoids in refractory or extensive cases","Advise avoidance of scratching and protection of trauma-prone extensor sites to limit new Koebnerised crops","Multidisciplinary care with dermatology alongside nephrology and diabetology, with monitoring for recurrence and early treatment of new lesions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"recluse_spider_envenomation":{"name":"Recluse (Loxosceles) spider envenomation","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["loxoscelism","brown recluse bite","Loxosceles bite","violin spider bite","necrotic arachnidism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / WHO envenoming guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Loxoscelism ranges from a self-limiting local lesion to a necrotic ulcer, and less commonly a systemic (viscerocutaneous) illness with haemolysis","The bite is often initially painless; over hours a central blister with surrounding pallor and erythema may evolve into a sinking, dusky lesion and eschar","A dependent or gravity-influenced spread of discolouration and the classic red-white-blue appearance suggest developing dermonecrosis","Systemic loxoscelism, more common in children, features fever, malaise and intravascular haemolysis, sometimes with haemoglobinuria and acute kidney injury","Most necrotic wounds attributed to spiders are actually other conditions; confirmed recluse exposure requires the spider to be endemic and ideally identified","Management is largely supportive with wound care, analgesia and monitoring for haemolysis; surgical debridement is deferred until the lesion has fully demarcated","There is no local pain-predominant cramping syndrome as in widow bites, which aids differentiation"],"pathophysiology":"Recluse venom contains sphingomyelinase D (phospholipase D), which acts on cell membranes and endothelium to trigger complement activation, neutrophil recruitment and intravascular thrombosis. This produces localised vascular occlusion and ischaemic dermonecrosis, with a characteristic gravity-dependent spread of injury. In systemic loxoscelism the enzyme induces complement-mediated haemolysis of red cells, releasing free haemoglobin that can precipitate acute kidney injury; disseminated intravascular coagulation may accompany severe cases. The delayed, evolving nature of the lesion reflects progressive microvascular thrombosis rather than direct cytolysis alone.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial skin and soft-tissue infection or abscess","Necrotising fasciitis","Cutaneous anthrax or other necrotic infection","Vasculitis or pyoderma gangrenosum","Other arthropod bite or chemical/thermal injury","Diabetic or ischaemic ulcer"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film for haemolysis","Haptoglobin, lactate dehydrogenase, reticulocytes and bilirubin for intravascular haemolysis","Urinalysis for haemoglobinuria and renal function tests","Coagulation studies if disseminated intravascular coagulation is suspected","Wound assessment over serial reviews to track demarcation","Consideration of infective causes when a necrotic lesion lacks clear envenomation history"],"redFlags":["Systemic features of fever, malaise and dark urine suggesting haemolysis","Falling haemoglobin or evidence of acute kidney injury","Rapidly spreading necrosis or signs of superadded necrotising infection","Haemodynamic instability","Coagulopathy"],"prognosis":"Most bites heal with conservative wound care, though large necrotic ulcers may take weeks to months and can scar. Systemic loxoscelism with haemolysis is uncommon but potentially life-threatening, particularly in children, and requires close monitoring.","pitfalls":["Over-diagnosing recluse bites for necrotic wounds outside the spider's endemic range, missing infection or other pathology","Performing early aggressive debridement before the lesion has demarcated, worsening the defect","Failing to screen for and monitor systemic haemolysis, especially in children","Treating a necrotising soft-tissue infection as a benign spider bite"],"management":["Provide supportive wound care with cleansing, immobilisation, elevation and dressings, together with analgesia and tetanus prophylaxis","Defer surgical debridement until the necrotic lesion has fully demarcated to avoid enlarging the wound","Monitor for systemic loxoscelism with serial full blood count, haemolysis markers and urinalysis, particularly in children who are most at risk","Treat intravascular haemolysis with hydration, transfusion as required and management of haemoglobinuria to protect renal function","Manage disseminated intravascular coagulation with supportive haematological measures if it develops","Reserve antibiotics for genuine secondary bacterial infection rather than the necrotic lesion itself, and escalate to critical care for severe systemic envenomation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Clinical toxicology guidance on loxoscelism"],"crossLinks":[]},"rectal_intussusception":{"name":"Rectal intussusception","system":"Gastrointestinal / Anorectal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["internal rectal prolapse","internal intussusception","recto-rectal/recto-anal intussusception"],"chapter":null,"source":"ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rectal intussusception is an internal telescoping of the rectal wall that does not protrude through the anus, distinct from external (full-thickness) rectal prolapse","It is graded by the Oxford radiological classification according to whether the intussusception is recto-rectal, recto-anal, or descends into the anal canal","Typical presentations are obstructed defecation with a sensation of incomplete evacuation and blockage, and, when the lead point reaches the anal canal, faecal incontinence and mucus leakage","It is strongly associated with solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, where the intussuscepting mucosa is subjected to trauma and ischaemia","Defecating proctography or dynamic MRI is the key investigation, demonstrating the intussusception and any associated rectocele, enterocele, or perineal descent","Internal intussusception is a common finding in asymptomatic individuals, so symptoms must correlate with the radiological grade before intervention","First-line management is conservative with fibre, stool regulation, avoidance of straining, and biofeedback pelvic-floor retraining","Surgery, such as ventral mesh rectopexy or a stapled transanal resection in selected cases, is reserved for high-grade symptomatic intussusception refractory to conservative care"],"pathophysiology":"Rectal intussusception occurs when a segment of the rectal wall invaginates into the lumen of the rectum or anal canal during straining, without protruding externally. Weakness and redundancy of the rectal wall and its supporting attachments, often with a deep pouch of Douglas and pelvic-floor dysfunction, allow the wall to fold in on itself as intrarectal pressure rises. The intussusceptum acts as a lead point that obstructs the outflow of stool, producing a sensation of blockage and incomplete evacuation and prompting further straining, which perpetuates the process. Repeated shearing and impaction of the prolapsing mucosa against the anal canal cause the mucosal ischaemia, ulceration, and fibrosis of solitary rectal ulcer syndrome. When the lead point repeatedly descends into the anal canal it disrupts the sphincter mechanism and produces incontinence.","additionalDifferentials":["External full-thickness rectal prolapse","Rectocele or enterocele causing obstructed defecation","Anismus (paradoxical puborectalis contraction)","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome as coexisting pathology","Rectal neoplasm acting as a lead point","Haemorrhoidal or mucosal prolapse","Slow-transit constipation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Defecating proctography to grade the intussusception (Oxford classification)","Dynamic pelvic-floor MRI to assess associated compartment defects","Anorectal manometry and balloon expulsion for evacuatory function","Endoanal ultrasound where incontinence or sphincter injury is suspected","Flexible sigmoidoscopy or colonoscopy to exclude a neoplastic lead point and inspect for solitary rectal ulcer","Colonic transit study where slow-transit constipation is considered"],"redFlags":["Rectal bleeding or a mass suggesting neoplasia acting as a lead point","A persistent rectal ulcer requiring biopsy to exclude malignancy","New or worsening faecal incontinence","Symptoms disproportionate to a low-grade intussusception, prompting a broader assessment","Progression to external full-thickness prolapse"],"prognosis":"Many patients improve with conservative management and biofeedback; surgery for well-selected high-grade symptomatic intussusception can improve obstructed defecation and incontinence, but functional results are variable and recurrence and persistent symptoms are recognised, particularly when other pelvic-floor disorders coexist.","pitfalls":["Operating on an incidental low-grade intussusception that does not explain the symptoms","Overlooking coexisting anismus or enterocele that will limit surgical benefit","Failing to biopsy a solitary rectal ulcer and missing malignancy","Attributing all evacuatory symptoms to the intussusception when a functional disorder predominates","Proceeding to surgery before an adequate trial of biofeedback and bowel regulation"],"management":["First-line conservative management of obstructed defecation with dietary fibre, adequate fluids, laxatives and bowel-habit retraining","Pelvic-floor biofeedback and defecatory retraining to correct dyssynergic straining that perpetuates the intussusception","Treat associated solitary rectal ulcer syndrome with topical therapies and strict avoidance of straining","Reserve surgery, such as ventral mesh rectopexy or stapled transanal rectal resection, for well-selected high-grade symptomatic disease after conservative failure","Ensure symptoms correlate with the radiological Oxford grade before intervention, as internal intussusception is common in asymptomatic people","Multidisciplinary assessment with colorectal surgery, gastroenterology and pelvic-floor physiotherapy, addressing coexisting rectocele, enterocele or perineal descent"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","Association of Coloproctology of Great Britain and Ireland guidance on rectal prolapse and obstructed defecation","Oxford rectal prolapse grading and pelvic-floor imaging literature (source-neutral)"],"crossLinks":[]},"rectal_prolapse":{"name":"Rectal prolapse","system":"Colorectal surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["procidentia","complete rectal prolapse","full-thickness rectal prolapse","rectal procidentia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Full-thickness (complete) rectal prolapse is protrusion of the entire rectal wall through the anus, with concentric mucosal folds, whereas mucosal prolapse and internal intussusception are distinct, less severe entities","It is most common in older women and in children, and is frequently associated with chronic straining, pelvic floor weakness, and a redundant sigmoid colon","Typical symptoms are a protruding mass on defecation, mucous discharge, bleeding, and a sensation of incomplete evacuation, with faecal incontinence in a large proportion","The diagnosis is often made by asking the patient to strain, ideally on a commode, to reproduce the prolapse; concentric rings distinguish it from the radial folds of prolapsing haemorrhoids","Defecating proctography or dynamic MRI can demonstrate internal intussusception and associated pelvic floor abnormalities when clinical findings are equivocal","Coexisting incontinence and constipation should be assessed, and colonic evaluation is prudent to exclude a lead point or synchronous pathology","Definitive treatment is surgical and is broadly divided into abdominal approaches (rectopexy, with or without resection) and perineal procedures, chosen according to fitness, symptoms, and surgeon preference"],"pathophysiology":"Full-thickness rectal prolapse is understood as a circumferential intussusception of the rectum that progresses until the bowel protrudes through the anal canal. Contributing factors include a deep rectovaginal or rectovesical pouch, loss of the normal posterior rectal fixation, a redundant mobile rectosigmoid, weak pelvic floor and anal sphincter musculature, and chronically raised intra-abdominal pressure from straining. Repeated prolapse stretches and damages the pudendal nerves and sphincter complex, which explains the frequent association with faecal incontinence, while chronic mucosal exposure produces discharge, bleeding, and ulceration.","additionalDifferentials":["Prolapsing (third- or fourth-degree) haemorrhoids","Mucosal-only rectal prolapse","Internal rectal intussusception","Prolapsing rectal polyp or tumour","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome","Anal or perianal skin tags or condylomata"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination with the patient straining to reproduce the prolapse","Colonoscopy or sigmoidoscopy to exclude a lead-point lesion or synchronous colorectal pathology","Defecating proctography or dynamic pelvic MRI for internal intussusception and pelvic floor assessment","Anorectal manometry and endoanal ultrasound when incontinence is prominent","Colonic transit studies where significant constipation coexists"],"redFlags":["Irreducible, incarcerated prolapse with congestion, ulceration, or impending strangulation requiring urgent reduction or surgery","Ischaemic or gangrenous prolapsed bowel","Significant bleeding or a palpable mass suggesting an underlying tumour","Acute large prolapse in a frail patient with obstruction"],"prognosis":"Surgical repair relieves the prolapse in most patients with acceptable recurrence rates that vary by procedure, and continence often improves over time though it may not fully recover if pelvic floor and nerve damage is longstanding. Constipation can persist or occasionally worsen, and outcomes are best when associated functional problems are addressed alongside the anatomical repair.","pitfalls":["Confusing full-thickness prolapse with prolapsing haemorrhoids and offering inappropriate treatment","Failing to reproduce the prolapse on examination and therefore missing or underestimating it","Not excluding a colorectal neoplasm acting as a lead point before elective repair","Ignoring coexisting incontinence or constipation, leading to poor functional results despite anatomical success","Delaying intervention in an incarcerated or ischaemic prolapse"],"management":["Definitive treatment is surgical repair, either abdominal rectopexy (increasingly ventral mesh rectopexy) or a perineal procedure such as Delorme or Altemeier in frail patients","Optimise bowel habit with fibre, fluids and laxatives to reduce straining before and after surgery","Manually reduce an incarcerated prolapse, using topical sugar to shrink oedema, with urgent surgery if it is irreducible or strangulated","Assess and manage coexisting faecal incontinence and constipation, including pelvic-floor biofeedback","Exclude a lead-point lesion with colonic evaluation before repair","Multidisciplinary colorectal and pelvic-floor input, with monitoring for recurrence and persistent bowel dysfunction after repair"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"rectal_varices":{"name":"Rectal varices","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anorectal varices"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hepatology and gastroenterology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Dilated portosystemic collateral veins in the rectum arising in portal hypertension","They are distinct from haemorrhoids, which are dilatations of the normal haemorrhoidal cushions","They appear as serpentine submucosal veins extending upward from the anorectum on endoscopy","They can cause significant, sometimes massive, lower gastrointestinal bleeding","Recognising portal hypertension is essential, as management differs from haemorrhoid treatment","Treatment targets portal pressure (for example with portosystemic shunting) alongside local haemostatic measures; conventional haemorrhoid procedures can provoke severe bleeding"],"pathophysiology":"Rectal varices form when portal hypertension drives blood through portosystemic collaterals between the superior rectal veins (portal system) and the middle and inferior rectal veins (systemic system). The resulting dilated submucosal veins can rupture and bleed. Unlike haemorrhoids, which are downward displacements of vascular cushions, rectal varices are true varices reflecting elevated portal pressure, so definitive control often requires reducing portal pressure rather than local excision.","additionalDifferentials":["Haemorrhoids","Other causes of lower gastrointestinal bleeding (angiodysplasia, neoplasm, diverticular)","Ischaemic or inflammatory proctitis","Rectal neoplasm","Colonic varices"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Endoscopic identification distinguishing varices from haemorrhoids","Assessment for portal hypertension and its cause (liver disease, portal vein thrombosis)","Cross-sectional or Doppler imaging of the portal system","Full blood count and coagulation/liver function tests","Assessment of overall variceal burden"],"redFlags":["Massive or recurrent rectal bleeding","Haemodynamic instability","Decompensated liver disease","Coagulopathy compounding bleeding"],"prognosis":"Bleeding can be controlled and prognosis is largely determined by the underlying portal hypertension and liver disease; recurrence is likely unless portal pressure is addressed.","pitfalls":["Mistaking rectal varices for haemorrhoids and treating with banding or excision, risking severe bleeding","Failing to recognise underlying portal hypertension","Overlooking other bleeding sources in portal hypertension","Not addressing coagulopathy"],"management":["Reduce portal pressure as the definitive strategy, with non-selective beta-blockers and treatment of the underlying liver disease","Achieve endoscopic haemostasis of bleeding varices with band ligation, sclerotherapy or coil-and-glue embolisation, avoiding conventional haemorrhoid procedures that can provoke severe haemorrhage","Resuscitate acute lower gastrointestinal bleeding with transfusion and correction of coagulopathy","Consider a transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt or interventional radiological embolisation for refractory or massive bleeding","Investigate and manage the cause of portal hypertension, such as cirrhosis or portal vein thrombosis","Multidisciplinary care with hepatology, interventional radiology and endoscopy, with surveillance for recurrent bleeding unless portal pressure is addressed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Hepatology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"rectocele":{"name":"Rectocele","system":"Gastrointestinal / Anorectal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["posterior vaginal wall prolapse","rectovaginal herniation","proctocele"],"chapter":null,"source":"ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rectocele is a herniation of the anterior rectal wall into the posterior vaginal wall through a weakened rectovaginal septum, most common in parous women","Typical symptoms are obstructed defecation, a sensation of incomplete evacuation, vaginal bulging, and the need for vaginal or perineal digitation to empty the rectum","Small rectoceles are a common incidental finding on imaging in asymptomatic women, so symptoms must correlate with anatomical findings before intervention","Risk factors include vaginal childbirth, chronic straining, ageing with connective-tissue laxity, prior pelvic surgery, and oestrogen deficiency","Defecating proctography or dynamic MRI demonstrates the size, barium trapping, and associated pelvic-floor abnormalities such as enterocele or intussusception","Rectocele frequently coexists with other pelvic-floor disorders, so a global structural and functional assessment is essential rather than treating it in isolation","First-line treatment is conservative: fibre, stool softening, avoidance of straining, and biofeedback/pelvic-floor physiotherapy","Surgical repair (transvaginal, transperineal, transanal, or as part of a ventral rectopexy or stapled procedure) is reserved for significant symptomatic rectoceles with demonstrable contrast trapping"],"pathophysiology":"The rectovaginal septum is a fibromuscular layer that separates the rectum from the vagina and supports the anterior rectal wall. Weakening or disruption of this septum and its fascial attachments, most often following vaginal delivery, chronic straining, ageing, or oestrogen deficiency, allows the anterior rectal wall to bulge forward into the posterior vaginal lumen during defecation. As intrarectal pressure rises with straining, stool preferentially fills the outpouching rather than passing through the anal canal, producing trapping of stool, a sensation of incomplete evacuation, and a vicious cycle of increased straining that enlarges the defect. Associated pelvic-floor weakness, perineal descent, and coexisting prolapse of other compartments compound the functional obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Enterocele or sigmoidocele herniating into the rectovaginal space","Rectal intussusception or internal rectal prolapse","Anismus (paradoxical puborectalis contraction) causing obstructed defecation","Cystocele or uterine/vault prolapse contributing to pelvic-floor symptoms","Slow-transit constipation","Anterior mucosal or full-thickness rectal prolapse","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Defecating proctography to assess rectocele size and barium trapping","Dynamic pelvic-floor MRI to evaluate all three compartments","Anorectal manometry and balloon expulsion testing for evacuatory function","Endoanal ultrasound if sphincter injury or incontinence is suspected","Colonic transit studies where slow-transit constipation is considered","Examination in the lithotomy and standing positions with straining"],"redFlags":["Rectal bleeding, weight loss, or change in bowel habit suggesting neoplasia","A non-reducing or ulcerated prolapsing mass","New-onset faecal incontinence or a suspected sphincter defect","Symptoms disproportionate to a small rectocele, prompting search for another cause","Signs of coexisting enterocele obstructing the small bowel"],"prognosis":"Many symptomatic rectoceles improve substantially with conservative measures and biofeedback; surgical repair of well-selected patients with demonstrable contrast trapping gives good functional results, though recurrence and persistent evacuatory symptoms can occur, particularly when other pelvic-floor abnormalities are unaddressed.","pitfalls":["Operating on an incidental rectocele that does not correlate with the patient's symptoms","Overlooking coexisting enterocele, intussusception, or anismus that will limit surgical benefit","Failing to treat constipation and straining, leading to recurrence","Attributing obstructed defecation solely to the rectocele when a functional disorder predominates","Neglecting pelvic-floor physiotherapy before considering surgery"],"management":["First-line conservative management with dietary fibre, fluids, stool softeners and bowel-habit modification to reduce straining","Pelvic-floor physiotherapy and biofeedback to improve rectal evacuation","Vaginal pessary support and topical oestrogen where oestrogen deficiency contributes","Reserve surgical repair (transvaginal, transanal or transperineal) for symptomatic patients with demonstrable contrast trapping after conservative failure","Confirm that symptoms correlate with anatomy before intervention, as small rectoceles are common incidental findings","Multidisciplinary assessment of all pelvic-floor compartments and coexisting enterocele or intussusception, with monitoring for recurrence and persistent evacuatory symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","International Continence Society and International Urogynecological Association terminology and guidance on pelvic organ prolapse","Association of Coloproctology of Great Britain and Ireland guidance on obstructed defecation"],"crossLinks":[]},"recurrent_isolated_sleep_paralysis":{"name":"Recurrent isolated sleep paralysis","system":"Sleep medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RISP","isolated sleep paralysis","sleep paralysis"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / International Classification of Sleep Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Recurrent episodes of inability to move the trunk and limbs while falling asleep or on waking, with full awareness of the surroundings","Episodes last seconds to a few minutes and resolve spontaneously or when the person is touched or spoken to","Frightening hypnagogic or hypnopompic hallucinations are common, such as a sensed intruder, chest pressure or a floating sensation","The term isolated indicates that it occurs without narcolepsy, and recurrent troublesome episodes define the disorder","It represents intrusion of the muscle atonia of REM sleep into wakefulness","Sleep deprivation, irregular sleep schedules, the supine position and psychological stress are recognised precipitants","Management centres on reassurance and improved sleep habits, while persistent daytime sleepiness or cataplexy should prompt evaluation for narcolepsy"],"pathophysiology":"Sleep paralysis occurs when the muscle atonia that normally accompanies REM sleep persists into, or intrudes upon, wakefulness at the boundaries of sleep. The person is conscious but temporarily unable to move voluntary muscles, while dream-related imagery may carry over as vivid hallucinations. Factors that disrupt or fragment REM sleep, such as sleep deprivation and irregular schedules, increase the likelihood of these dissociated states.","additionalDifferentials":["Narcolepsy, in which sleep paralysis is one feature","Nocturnal panic attacks","Nocturnal seizures, for example of frontal lobe origin","Cataplexy","Hypokalaemic or other periodic paralysis","Nightmare disorder with awakening"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical history establishing the timing, preserved awareness and brief duration of episodes","Screening for narcolepsy features such as excessive sleepiness and cataplexy","Sleep diary and assessment of sleep deprivation and schedule regularity","Polysomnography and multiple sleep latency testing if narcolepsy is suspected","Review of stress, psychiatric symptoms and substance use"],"redFlags":["Excessive daytime sleepiness or cataplexy suggesting narcolepsy","Features atypical for sleep paralysis that suggest nocturnal seizures","Episodes occurring away from the sleep-wake transition","Severe distress or avoidance of sleep because of the episodes"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and often improves with better sleep habits and reassurance; episodes may recur intermittently, especially with sleep loss or stress, but do not indicate serious disease once narcolepsy has been excluded.","pitfalls":["Missing underlying narcolepsy when sleepiness or cataplexy is also present","Mistaking episodes for nocturnal seizures or panic attacks","Underestimating the fear and sleep avoidance the episodes can cause","Neglecting sleep deprivation and irregular schedules as remediable triggers"],"management":["Reassure and explain that episodes are benign intrusions of REM-sleep atonia into wakefulness once narcolepsy is excluded","Reinforce sleep hygiene with regular schedules and adequate sleep duration to reduce REM fragmentation","Address precipitants by avoiding sleep deprivation, discouraging the supine sleeping position and managing psychological stress","Treat comorbid anxiety, depression or stress that provoke episodes","Consider REM-suppressing antidepressant therapy only for frequent, distressing episodes","Screen for narcolepsy features such as excessive sleepiness and cataplexy, and refer to sleep medicine for polysomnography if an underlying disorder is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd edition (ICSD-3)","Principles and Practice of Sleep Medicine (Kryger, Roth, Dement)"],"crossLinks":["rem_sleep_behavior_disorder","exploding_head_syndrome"]},"recurrent_pericarditis":{"name":"Recurrent pericarditis","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["relapsing pericarditis","recurrent acute pericarditis","incessant pericarditis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A recurrence of pericarditis after a symptom-free interval following a first episode, typically presenting with the same pleuritic chest pain that eases on sitting forward","Most cases are presumed immune-mediated following an initial viral or idiopathic episode, and an autoinflammatory mechanism is increasingly recognised","Diagnosis rests on recurrent pericarditic pain with supportive features such as a friction rub, typical electrocardiographic changes, a new or worsening effusion, or a rise in inflammatory markers","Colchicine added to anti-inflammatory therapy reduces the risk of further recurrences and is a cornerstone of treatment","Corticosteroids relieve symptoms but promote steroid dependence and further relapses, so they are reserved for specific indications and used sparingly","Refractory, corticosteroid-dependent disease may respond to interleukin-1 blockade, reflecting the autoinflammatory basis","The long-term outlook is generally good, and progression to constriction is uncommon in idiopathic recurrent disease"],"pathophysiology":"Recurrent pericarditis usually follows an initial acute episode of viral or idiopathic pericarditis and is thought to reflect a dysregulated immune or autoinflammatory response rather than persistent infection. Inflammation of the pericardial layers produces the characteristic pleuritic pain, a friction rub, and often a small effusion, with release of inflammatory mediators, particularly the interleukin-1 pathway, driving relapses. Incomplete treatment of the first attack and early or high reliance on corticosteroids favour recurrence. Repeated inflammation can occasionally lead to pericardial thickening, but in idiopathic disease progression to significant constriction is unusual.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute coronary syndrome","Pulmonary embolism","Pleuritis or pneumonia","Pericarditis from systemic autoimmune disease such as lupus","Post-cardiac injury syndrome","Myopericarditis with myocardial involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electrocardiography for widespread saddle-shaped ST elevation and PR depression","Inflammatory markers including C-reactive protein to confirm activity and guide tapering","Echocardiography to detect and size a pericardial effusion and assess for tamponade","Cardiac MRI to demonstrate pericardial inflammation and late gadolinium enhancement in uncertain cases","Targeted screening for autoimmune or systemic causes when features suggest secondary disease"],"redFlags":["Signs of cardiac tamponade such as hypotension, raised jugular venous pressure, and pulsus paradoxus","A large or rapidly accumulating pericardial effusion","Features of evolving constriction with right heart failure","Troponin rise or ventricular dysfunction indicating significant myocardial involvement"],"prognosis":"Most patients have a favourable long-term outcome with control of recurrences, and although relapses can be troublesome and prolonged, progression to constrictive pericarditis is rare in idiopathic recurrent disease.","pitfalls":["Early or excessive use of corticosteroids, which fosters dependence and further recurrences","Omitting colchicine, thereby losing its proven protection against relapse","Stopping anti-inflammatory therapy abruptly rather than tapering guided by symptoms and C-reactive protein","Advising undue restriction of activity or missing an underlying systemic autoimmune cause"],"management":["First-line anti-inflammatory therapy with an NSAID or aspirin plus gastric protection, tapered as symptoms and C-reactive protein settle","Add colchicine as a cornerstone for a prolonged course to reduce further recurrences","Reserve corticosteroids at the lowest effective dose with slow taper for specific indications such as NSAID contraindication, connective-tissue disease or pregnancy, given their tendency to promote steroid dependence and relapse","Use interleukin-1 blockade with anakinra or rilonacept for corticosteroid-dependent, colchicine-resistant disease reflecting its autoinflammatory basis","Restrict exercise until symptoms resolve and inflammatory markers normalise","Monitor with serial C-reactive protein and echocardiography to guide tapering and detect effusion or tamponade, screening for and treating underlying systemic causes and referring refractory cases to a specialist pericardial service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of pericardial diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"recurrent_pregnancy_loss":{"name":"Recurrent pregnancy loss","system":"Reproductive medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RPL","recurrent miscarriage","recurrent spontaneous abortion","habitual abortion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard sleep & reproductive medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Recurrent pregnancy loss is generally defined as two or more consecutive clinical pregnancy losses and warrants systematic evaluation of both partners","Despite thorough investigation, a large proportion of cases remain unexplained, and even then the prognosis for a future successful pregnancy is often favourable","Antiphospholipid syndrome is the most important treatable cause and should be specifically excluded, since it is amenable to anticoagulant and antiplatelet therapy","Sporadic early losses are most often due to embryonic chromosomal abnormalities, whereas recurrent loss prompts a search for maternal and parental factors","Parental balanced chromosomal rearrangements are an uncommon but important cause identified by karyotyping both partners","Uterine anatomical abnormalities, endocrine disorders such as thyroid disease and poorly controlled diabetes, and inherited thrombophilias should be considered","Maternal age and the number of previous losses are the strongest predictors of further loss","Management combines treatment of any identified cause, notably antiphospholipid syndrome, with supportive early-pregnancy care and reassurance"],"pathophysiology":"Recurrent pregnancy loss is a heterogeneous problem in which established pregnancies fail repeatedly. Genetic causes include embryonic aneuploidy (rising sharply with maternal age) and inherited parental balanced translocations that yield unbalanced gametes. Antiphospholipid syndrome, an acquired autoimmune thrombophilia, causes loss through placental thrombosis and impaired trophoblast function driven by antibodies such as lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, and anti-beta-2-glycoprotein-I. Anatomical uterine factors (congenital malformations, adhesions, submucosal fibroids, and possibly cervical insufficiency in later loss) impair implantation or placentation. Endocrine disturbances, including thyroid dysfunction and poorly controlled diabetes, disrupt the hormonal environment needed to sustain pregnancy. In many couples no cause is found, and losses are attributed to unrecognised embryonic or implantation factors.","additionalDifferentials":["Antiphospholipid syndrome","Parental balanced chromosomal translocation","Uterine anomalies, submucosal fibroids, or intrauterine adhesions","Thyroid dysfunction or poorly controlled diabetes","Inherited thrombophilia","Unexplained recurrent loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antiphospholipid antibody testing (lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, anti-beta-2-glycoprotein-I) with confirmatory repeat testing","Parental karyotyping and, where available, cytogenetic analysis of pregnancy tissue","Pelvic imaging such as ultrasound or hysteroscopy to assess uterine anatomy","Thyroid function tests and glycaemic assessment","Selective thrombophilia screening where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Positive or suspected antiphospholipid syndrome requiring confirmatory testing and antithrombotic management in future pregnancy","Second-trimester loss suggesting cervical insufficiency or a uterine anomaly","A parental chromosomal rearrangement necessitating genetic counselling","Significant psychological distress and grief requiring support"],"prognosis":"The outlook for a subsequent successful pregnancy is often good, particularly when a treatable cause such as antiphospholipid syndrome is identified and managed, and even most couples with unexplained recurrent loss eventually achieve a live birth, though the chance declines with maternal age and the number of prior losses.","pitfalls":["Failing to test for and treat antiphospholipid syndrome, the key reversible cause","Not confirming antiphospholipid antibodies with appropriately spaced repeat testing","Omitting parental karyotyping when a chromosomal rearrangement is possible","Neglecting the substantial psychological impact and need for supportive counselling"],"management":["Treat antiphospholipid syndrome, the key treatable cause, with low-dose aspirin and prophylactic heparin during pregnancy","Correct endocrine contributors by optimising thyroid dysfunction with levothyroxine and achieving good glycaemic control in diabetes","Surgically correct significant uterine anatomical defects such as a septum, adhesions or submucosal fibroids, and consider cervical cerclage for cervical insufficiency","Offer genetic counselling and parental karyotyping, with reproductive options such as preimplantation genetic testing or donor gametes where a parental balanced translocation is found","Address modifiable factors including smoking cessation, weight optimisation and reducing alcohol and caffeine","Provide supportive early-pregnancy care and reassurance within a dedicated recurrent-miscarriage clinic, given the favourable prognosis even in unexplained cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"recurrent_respiratory_papillomatosis":{"name":"Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis (HPV 6/11)","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Head and Neck","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RRP","laryngeal papillomatosis","juvenile-onset recurrent respiratory papillomatosis","adult-onset recurrent respiratory papillomatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by human papillomavirus, predominantly types 6 and 11, producing recurrent benign papillomata of the aerodigestive tract, most commonly the larynx","Juvenile-onset disease is thought to arise from vertical transmission around delivery from maternal genital HPV, while adult-onset disease is often sexually acquired","Progressive hoarseness or an abnormal cry is the typical presenting feature; stridor and airway obstruction develop as lesions enlarge","In a child with persistent hoarseness and stridor, RRP should be considered and can be mistaken for asthma or croup","Diagnosis rests on laryngoscopy showing exophytic, frond-like lesions, confirmed histologically with HPV typing","Disease behaviour is highly variable, ranging from a single lesion to aggressive recurrence needing frequent procedures, with HPV 11 associated with a more aggressive course","Management centres on repeated surgical debulking to preserve airway and voice while sparing normal tissue, with adjuvant medical therapies in aggressive cases","Distal spread into the trachea and lungs and rare malignant transformation are recognised complications"],"pathophysiology":"Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis results from infection of respiratory epithelium by low-risk human papillomavirus, chiefly types 6 and 11. The virus infects basal epithelial cells and drives proliferation of squamous epithelium into exophytic papillomata with fibrovascular cores, showing a predilection for zones of ciliated-to-squamous epithelial transition such as the vocal folds. Latent virus in clinically normal-appearing adjacent mucosa accounts for the characteristic recurrence after removal. Host immune factors and viral type influence disease aggressiveness, and repeated growth can extend distally through the tracheobronchial tree with rare progression to squamous carcinoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal cord nodules or polyps","Laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma","Vocal fold granuloma","Croup or other infective laryngitis","Subglottic stenosis or haemangioma in children","Foreign body of the airway"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible and rigid laryngoscopy to map lesion distribution","Microlaryngoscopy under anaesthesia with biopsy for histology","HPV typing of excised tissue","Cross-sectional imaging or bronchoscopy when distal tracheobronchial or pulmonary spread is suspected","Voice assessment and serial documentation of disease burden to guide treatment intervals"],"redFlags":["Stridor, respiratory distress or rapidly worsening airway obstruction","Rapid recurrence or bulky disease requiring increasingly frequent procedures","New pulmonary lesions suggesting distal spread","Suspicion of malignant transformation on histology or unusually rapid growth"],"prognosis":"The course is unpredictable: some patients experience spontaneous remission while others require repeated procedures over many years to maintain the airway and voice. Distal pulmonary spread and malignant transformation are uncommon but serious, and HPV vaccination is expected to reduce future incidence.","pitfalls":["Mistaking childhood stridor and hoarseness for asthma or recurrent croup and delaying laryngoscopy","Aggressive deep excision causing scarring and permanent voice damage","Performing tracheostomy unnecessarily, which may promote distal seeding of disease","Neglecting surveillance for malignant change in long-standing or aggressive disease"],"management":["Mainstay is repeated surgical debulking that preserves normal mucosa, using a microdebrider or laser at microlaryngoscopy to maintain the airway and voice","Avoid tracheostomy wherever possible, as it risks seeding papillomata distally into the tracheobronchial tree","Add adjuvant intralesional cidofovir or systemic bevacizumab for aggressive, frequently recurrent disease","Offer HPV vaccination as an adjunct to reduce disease burden and future incidence","Undertake serial laryngoscopy and voice assessment to document burden and time repeat procedures, with surveillance for distal pulmonary spread and malignant transformation","Multidisciplinary otolaryngology care with speech-and-language voice therapy and respiratory input if pulmonary spread occurs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"red_man_syndrome":{"name":"Vancomycin infusion (red man) reaction","system":"Clinical pharmacology / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["red man syndrome","red neck syndrome","vancomycin flushing reaction","vancomycin infusion reaction","vancomycin flushing syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / Mandell's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An infusion-rate-related reaction to vancomycin causing flushing, erythema and pruritus over the face, neck and upper trunk","It is a non-immune (anaphylactoid) reaction driven by direct mast-cell degranulation, not a true IgE-mediated allergy","Onset is typically during or shortly after a rapid infusion, and it often eases when the infusion is slowed or paused","More severe episodes can include hypotension, chest and back pain, and muscle spasm","Prevention centres on slowing the infusion rate; antihistamine pre-treatment can reduce or blunt the reaction","Distinguishing it from true anaphylaxis is important, as the reaction does not necessarily contraindicate future vancomycin use with a slower infusion","Other agents that provoke histamine release (for example certain opioids or muscle relaxants) can potentiate the reaction","The presence of urticaria beyond the infusion, angio-oedema or bronchospasm should raise concern for true allergic anaphylaxis"],"pathophysiology":"The reaction results from direct, non-immunological degranulation of mast cells and basophils provoked by vancomycin, releasing histamine into the circulation. Because it does not require prior sensitisation or drug-specific IgE, it can occur on first exposure and is closely related to the rate and concentration of the infusion, with rapid administration causing a larger histamine surge. Histamine produces cutaneous vasodilatation and flushing, pruritus and, when release is marked, hypotension and muscle discomfort. This anaphylactoid mechanism contrasts with true type I hypersensitivity, in which drug-specific IgE cross-links mast-cell receptors; the distinction guides whether the drug can be cautiously continued at a slower rate.","additionalDifferentials":["True IgE-mediated anaphylaxis to vancomycin or a co-administered drug","Other drug-induced anaphylactoid reactions","Carcinoid or mastocytosis-related flushing","Sepsis with vasodilatation","Systemic hypersensitivity or DRESS from vancomycin (linear IgA disease with vancomycin is a separate entity)","Panic or vasovagal reaction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical observation of the temporal relationship to infusion rate","Vital sign monitoring for hypotension during infusion","Serum tryptase where true anaphylaxis needs to be excluded","Review of co-administered histamine-releasing drugs","Assessment for urticaria, angio-oedema or bronchospasm suggesting true allergy","Trial of slowed infusion with observation under supervision"],"redFlags":["Significant hypotension or cardiovascular instability during infusion","Angio-oedema, bronchospasm or stridor suggesting true anaphylaxis","Widespread urticaria persisting after the infusion","Chest pain or severe muscle spasm"],"prognosis":"The reaction is usually mild and self-limiting once the infusion is slowed or stopped, and vancomycin can often be continued with a slower infusion and antihistamine cover. Severe hypotensive episodes are uncommon but require prompt supportive treatment.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the reaction as a true penicillin-class-style allergy and permanently avoiding an effective antibiotic","Failing to slow the infusion and instead abandoning treatment unnecessarily","Missing genuine anaphylaxis by assuming all vancomycin flushing is benign","Overlooking co-administered histamine-releasing drugs that potentiate the reaction"],"management":["Stop or slow the vancomycin infusion at symptom onset, then recommence at a slower rate once the reaction settles","Prevent recurrence by extending the infusion duration for subsequent doses","Give antihistamine premedication with an H1 blocker, with or without an H2 blocker, to blunt the histamine-mediated reaction","Provide supportive treatment with intravenous fluids for hypotension in severe episodes","Distinguish the reaction from true IgE-mediated anaphylaxis using serum tryptase and features such as urticaria, angio-oedema or bronchospasm, since it does not contraindicate future cautious use","Review and minimise co-administered histamine-releasing drugs, and consider an alternative glycopeptide only if reactions persist despite rate reduction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"reducing_body_myopathy":{"name":"Reducing body myopathy (FHL1)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FHL1-related reducing body myopathy","RBM","four-and-a-half LIM domain 1 myopathy","X-linked reducing body myopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in FHL1 on the X chromosome, encoding the four-and-a-half LIM domain protein 1; inheritance is X-linked so males are typically more severely affected than heterozygous females","Presents with progressive proximal and axial weakness, and rigid spine or scoliosis can be prominent features","The clinical spectrum ranges from severe early-childhood-onset disease to milder later-onset forms","Cardiac involvement and respiratory muscle weakness can occur, given FHL1 expression in cardiac and skeletal muscle, so cardiorespiratory surveillance is important","The defining biopsy feature is reducing bodies, cytoplasmic inclusions that stain intensely with menadione-linked nitro-blue tetrazithium (menadione-NBT) in the absence of substrate, reflecting their reducing activity","Reducing bodies contain aggregated mutant FHL1 protein and can be confirmed with FHL1 immunostaining and electron microscopy","Creatine kinase is normal to moderately elevated and electromyography shows myopathic, sometimes irritable, changes","Diagnosis combines the reducing-body biopsy finding with FHL1 genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Reducing body myopathy is caused by variants in FHL1, an X-linked gene encoding a LIM-domain protein involved in sarcomere assembly, mechanotransduction, and cytoskeletal signalling in striated muscle. Disease-associated variants, frequently affecting the zinc-coordinating residues of the second LIM domain, cause the mutant protein to misfold and self-aggregate. These aggregates form the characteristic reducing bodies, which have intrinsic reducing activity demonstrable with the menadione-NBT reaction, and their accumulation is toxic to muscle fibres, producing progressive weakness. Because FHL1 is also expressed in cardiac muscle, some patients develop cardiomyopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Other FHL1-related myopathies (X-linked myopathy with postural muscle atrophy, scapuloperoneal myopathy, Emery-Dreifuss-like phenotype)","Myofibrillar myopathies","Congenital myopathies with cores or aggregates","Rigid spine syndrome from SELENON-related myopathy","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","Inflammatory myopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography","Muscle biopsy with the menadione-NBT reaction, FHL1 immunostaining, and electron microscopy to demonstrate reducing bodies","Muscle MRI to define involvement, including paraspinal muscles","Cardiac evaluation and respiratory function testing","Targeted FHL1 sequencing"],"redFlags":["Cardiomyopathy or conduction abnormality on cardiac assessment","Respiratory muscle weakness or nocturnal hypoventilation","Rapidly progressive weakness or severe early-onset disease","Progressive rigid spine or scoliosis compromising respiration"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies with age of onset and sex: severe early-onset disease in males can progress rapidly with loss of ambulation and significant cardiorespiratory involvement, whereas later-onset and female cases may follow a milder course; cardiac and respiratory complications are important determinants of outcome.","pitfalls":["Missing reducing bodies unless the menadione-NBT reaction is specifically performed on the biopsy","Neglecting cardiac and respiratory surveillance given the FHL1 gene's cardiac role","Failing to recognise the X-linked inheritance pattern and its counselling implications","Confusing the inclusions with other cytoplasmic bodies without confirmatory staining"],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy exists, so care is supportive and multidisciplinary around the progressive muscle weakness","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain function and mobility, with orthoses and assistive devices","Orthopaedic management of rigid spine and scoliosis, including bracing and corrective spinal surgery","Cardiac surveillance with echocardiography and ECG for cardiomyopathy and conduction disease, given FHL1 expression in cardiac muscle","Respiratory monitoring with lung-function testing and non-invasive ventilation for respiratory muscle weakness","Genetic counselling for this X-linked disorder with carrier testing of female relatives, escalating to specialist neuromuscular and cardiorespiratory services on deterioration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: FHL1-related myopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"reduplicative_paramnesia":{"name":"Reduplicative Paramnesia","system":"Nervous system / Psychiatry","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Delusional place reduplication","Environmental reduplication"],"chapter":null,"source":"Lishman's Organic Psychiatry","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A delusional misidentification syndrome for place: the conviction that a location, such as the hospital, has been duplicated, relocated, or exists in two places at once.","The patient may insist that the current hospital is a replica situated near their home, despite clear evidence to the contrary.","Strongly associated with organic brain disease, especially right-hemisphere and bifrontal lesions from trauma, stroke, tumour, or dementia.","One of the classic content-specific, monothematic delusions, alongside the Capgras and Fregoli delusions.","Often accompanied by confabulation, disorientation, and anosognosia.","The term was originally introduced by Pick, with place reduplication as the prototypical form.","Its presence should prompt neurological and neuroimaging evaluation rather than an assumption of primary psychiatric illness."],"pathophysiology":"Reduplicative paramnesia is a monothematic delusion arising from combined right-hemisphere and frontal dysfunction. Right-hemisphere damage impairs the perceptual, spatial, and familiarity mechanisms that anchor a place to a single location, while frontal executive dysfunction impairs the monitoring and belief-evaluation processes that would normally reject the incongruity, so the patient accepts and maintains the reduplication belief. It commonly co-occurs with confabulation and disorientation, reflecting shared frontal contributions, and is typically seen in traumatic brain injury, stroke, tumours, and neurodegenerative disease affecting these regions.","additionalDifferentials":["Capgras and Fregoli delusions of person misidentification","Delirium","Dementia (Alzheimer's, vascular, or Lewy body type)","Traumatic brain injury with confabulation","Korsakoff syndrome and other confabulatory states","Right-hemisphere stroke or tumour","Primary psychotic disorder, less commonly"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structural neuroimaging (MRI or CT) attentive to right frontal and parietal pathology","Cognitive assessment including memory, executive function, and orientation","Delirium screen for infection and metabolic disturbance","Bloods including vitamin B12, thyroid function, glucose, and calcium","EEG where seizures or encephalopathy are suspected","Neuropsychological testing","Collateral history on onset and progression"],"redFlags":["A new focal neurological deficit","Acute onset with fluctuating consciousness suggesting delirium or encephalitis","Rapidly progressive cognitive decline","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or a space-occupying lesion"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying neurological cause; reduplication may resolve as an acute brain insult recovers but tends to persist or progress in neurodegenerative disease.","pitfalls":["Mistaking an organic delusion for primary psychiatric illness and omitting neuroimaging","Overlooking coexisting delirium","Attempting to argue the patient out of the belief rather than investigating the cause","Missing associated anosognosia and its safety implications"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying neurological cause, such as stroke, traumatic brain injury, tumour or neurodegenerative disease","Reverse contributing delirium by treating infection and metabolic or electrolyte disturbance and reviewing offending medications","Use reorientation strategies, a consistent environment and cognitive support rather than confronting the delusion directly","Reserve cautious low-dose antipsychotic therapy for delusions causing significant distress or risk, weighing the hazards in the elderly and in dementia","Ensure safety and supervision given the disorientation and impaired insight","Neuropsychological assessment and cognitive rehabilitation with multidisciplinary neurology, psychiatry and rehabilitation input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Lishman's Organic Psychiatry","New Oxford Textbook of Psychiatry","Principles of Behavioral and Cognitive Neurology (Mesulam)"],"crossLinks":[]},"refeeding_syndrome":{"name":"Refeeding syndrome","system":"Nutrition / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["refeeding hypophosphataemia","nutritional recovery syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO nutrition guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["It is a potentially fatal set of metabolic shifts triggered when nutrition, especially carbohydrate, is reintroduced to a malnourished or starved patient","The hallmark biochemical abnormality is a sharp fall in serum phosphate, often with falls in potassium and magnesium and fluid retention","Highest-risk groups include those with very low body mass index, prolonged fasting or minimal intake, chronic alcohol use, anorexia nervosa, and severe malnutrition","Clinical consequences include cardiac arrhythmia and failure, respiratory muscle weakness, seizures, confusion, and rhabdomyolysis","Prevention is key: identify at-risk patients, correct electrolytes, give thiamine before feeding, and start nutrition cautiously with slow escalation","Electrolytes must be monitored frequently during early refeeding and replaced as they fall rather than waiting for symptoms","It can complicate refeeding in any setting, including treatment of severe childhood malnutrition and hospital nutritional support"],"pathophysiology":"During prolonged starvation the body shifts to fat and protein catabolism, depletes intracellular electrolytes and vitamins despite near-normal serum levels, and reduces insulin secretion. Reintroducing carbohydrate causes a surge of insulin that drives glucose, phosphate, potassium, and magnesium into cells and stimulates synthesis of phosphorylated intermediates, abruptly lowering their serum concentrations. Thiamine, needed for carbohydrate metabolism, is rapidly consumed and can become critically deficient. Insulin also promotes sodium and water retention. The combination of hypophosphataemia, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, thiamine deficiency, and fluid overload produces the cardiac, neurological, and neuromuscular manifestations.","additionalDifferentials":["Sepsis with metabolic derangement","Primary cardiac failure or arrhythmia of other cause","Other causes of hypophosphataemia such as renal loss","Diabetic ketoacidosis and its treatment-related electrolyte shifts","Adrenal insufficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Baseline and frequent monitoring of phosphate, potassium, and magnesium","Glucose, sodium, and fluid balance assessment during refeeding","ECG monitoring for arrhythmia in high-risk patients","Assessment of nutritional risk before starting feeding","Renal function and, where indicated, markers of rhabdomyolysis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly falling phosphate, potassium, or magnesium after feeding begins","Cardiac arrhythmia or acute heart failure during refeeding","Respiratory muscle weakness or respiratory failure","Seizures, confusion, or reduced consciousness attributable to electrolyte shifts or thiamine deficiency"],"prognosis":"With early identification of at-risk patients, cautious feeding, thiamine, and proactive electrolyte replacement the syndrome is largely preventable and recoverable; unrecognised, it can cause fatal arrhythmia and multi-organ dysfunction.","pitfalls":["Starting full-rate nutrition immediately in a severely malnourished patient","Being falsely reassured by normal pre-feeding serum electrolytes despite depleted body stores","Omitting thiamine before a carbohydrate load","Monitoring electrolytes too infrequently to catch the early post-feeding fall"],"management":["Identify at-risk patients and start nutrition cautiously with slow escalation of caloric intake","Give thiamine and other B vitamins before and during the early stages of feeding to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy","Correct and pre-emptively replace phosphate, potassium and magnesium, replacing as levels fall rather than awaiting symptoms","Monitor electrolytes, glucose and fluid balance frequently during early refeeding","Manage fluid and sodium carefully to avoid overload and cardiac decompensation","Provide cardiac monitoring for arrhythmia in high-risk patients, with dietitian-led nutrition support and escalation to critical care for severe derangement or cardiac compromise"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO nutrition guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"refetoff_syndrome":{"name":"Refetoff Syndrome","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Resistance to thyroid hormone","RTH","RTHβ"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrinology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Reduced target-tissue responsiveness to thyroid hormone, most often from dominant-negative mutations in THRB, the gene for the beta isoform of the thyroid hormone receptor.","The hallmark biochemistry is elevated free thyroid hormones with an unsuppressed — normal or mildly raised — thyroid-stimulating hormone.","The clinical picture is variable; many patients are compensated and euthyroid, but hypothyroid and thyrotoxic features can coexist in different tissues.","Goitre is common, and tachycardia or attention and learning difficulties may occur because alpha-receptor-rich tissues such as the heart remain responsive.","The principal differential is a thyroid-stimulating-hormone-secreting pituitary adenoma, which produces a similar biochemical pattern.","Distinguishing tests include the alpha subunit, sex-hormone-binding globulin, a thyrotrophin-releasing hormone stimulation test and genetic and family studies.","Inheritance is usually autosomal dominant, and screening relatives frequently reveals others with the same biochemistry.","Most patients need no treatment, and inappropriate anti-thyroid therapy or gland ablation can be harmful."],"pathophysiology":"Refetoff syndrome reflects reduced end-organ responsiveness to thyroid hormone, most often caused by heterozygous mutations in THRB encoding the beta isoform of the thyroid hormone receptor. Mutant receptors bind triiodothyronine poorly or fail to regulate transcription and exert a dominant-negative effect over the normal allele. Because the hypothalamus and pituitary depend on the beta receptor for negative feedback, thyroid-stimulating hormone secretion is not appropriately suppressed by the high circulating free thyroxine and triiodothyronine, producing the characteristic pattern of elevated free thyroid hormones with a normal or even raised thyroid-stimulating hormone. Tissues in which the alpha receptor isoform predominates — notably the heart, and parts of the central nervous system and bone — remain responsive to the high hormone levels, so patients can simultaneously display hypothyroid features in beta-dependent tissues and thyrotoxic features such as tachycardia in alpha-dependent tissues, accounting for the variable, tissue-selective phenotype. A rare recessive form due to complete THRB loss was described in the original consanguineous kindred, associated with deaf-mutism and stippled epiphyses.","additionalDifferentials":["Thyroid-stimulating-hormone-secreting pituitary adenoma","Laboratory assay interference from heterophile antibodies, biotin or anti-iodothyronine antibodies","Familial dysalbuminaemic hyperthyroxinaemia","Graves disease or other true hyperthyroidism","Non-thyroidal illness and its recovery phase","Thyroid hormone cell-membrane transporter defect (MCT8, Allan-Herndon-Dudley syndrome)","Thyroid hormone metabolism defect from selenocysteine insertion sequence-binding protein 2 mutation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Free thyroxine, free triiodothyronine and thyroid-stimulating hormone, with confirmatory repeat testing","Alpha subunit and the alpha-subunit-to-thyroid-stimulating-hormone molar ratio, raised in a secreting adenoma","Sex-hormone-binding globulin, typically normal in resistance but high in true thyrotoxicosis","Thyrotrophin-releasing hormone stimulation test, with a preserved response favouring resistance","Pituitary MRI to exclude a secreting adenoma","THRB gene sequencing and biochemical screening of relatives","Assessment for laboratory assay interference"],"redFlags":["A pituitary mass with headache or visual field defect, favouring a secreting adenoma over resistance","Cardiac arrhythmia such as atrial fibrillation, or heart failure, from thyrotoxic tissue effects","Growth or developmental concerns in an affected child","Iatrogenic thyroid ablation performed on a misdiagnosis, leaving management difficult"],"prognosis":"With correct recognition and either no treatment or careful, individualised management the outlook is generally good and lifespan normal. Most morbidity stems from misdiagnosis with inappropriate anti-thyroid treatment or from tissue-selective thyrotoxic effects.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the condition as Graves disease and giving anti-thyroid drugs, radioiodine or thyroidectomy.","Failing to exclude a thyroid-stimulating-hormone-secreting adenoma before labelling the picture as resistance.","Overlooking laboratory assay interference as the explanation for the biochemistry.","Treating the numbers rather than the patient, when most individuals are compensated and euthyroid."],"management":["Recognise the diagnosis to avoid inappropriate anti-thyroid drugs or thyroid ablation for the raised free thyroid hormones","Manage many compensated, euthyroid patients with monitoring alone, as tissue resistance is offset by the high circulating hormone","Use beta-blockade to control tachycardia and adrenergic symptoms arising from responsive alpha-receptor-rich tissues such as the heart","Distinguish resistance from a thyroid-stimulating-hormone-secreting pituitary adenoma before treating, using the alpha subunit, sex-hormone-binding globulin, a thyrotrophin-releasing hormone stimulation test and pituitary MRI","Give careful, individualised thyroid hormone supplementation only where hypothyroid features or growth and developmental concerns predominate","Monitor goitre, cardiac status, growth and neurodevelopment, and offer genetic testing with biochemical screening and counselling of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard endocrinology textbooks","Clinical references on thyroid disorders","Medical genetics references"],"crossLinks":[]},"reflex_syncope":{"name":"Reflex (vasovagal) syncope","system":"Nervous system / Autonomic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vasovagal syncope","neurocardiogenic syncope","neurally mediated syncope","common faint"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest cause of transient loss of consciousness, resulting from a reflex fall in heart rate and/or blood pressure with transient global cerebral hypoperfusion","Typical triggers include prolonged standing, heat, pain, venepuncture, emotion, or the sight of blood","A prodrome of light-headedness, nausea, sweating, pallor, and visual greying usually precedes the faint, helping distinguish it from cardiac syncope and seizures","Brief myoclonic jerks (convulsive syncope) can occur and be mistaken for epilepsy","Recovery is rapid with little or no confusion, unlike the post-ictal state after a seizure","Diagnosis is clinical from a good history and eyewitness account; head-up tilt testing can support it in uncertain cases","Cardioinhibitory, vasodepressor, and mixed haemodynamic patterns are recognised","Management: education, trigger avoidance, adequate fluid and salt intake, recognising the prodrome and lying down, and physical counter-pressure manoeuvres"],"pathophysiology":"Reflex (vasovagal) syncope results from a transient, inappropriate autonomic reflex. Triggers such as prolonged standing or emotional stress lead, in susceptible people, to a paradoxical withdrawal of sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone together with a surge of vagal (parasympathetic) activity. This produces vasodilatation (the vasodepressor component) and/or bradycardia or asystole (the cardioinhibitory component), causing a sudden fall in blood pressure and transient global cerebral hypoperfusion with loss of consciousness. Because the mechanism is self-limiting and posture-dependent, lying flat restores cerebral perfusion and consciousness returns quickly. Reduced venous return from lower-body pooling and relative hypovolaemia predispose to the reflex.","additionalDifferentials":["Cardiac (arrhythmic or structural) syncope","Orthostatic hypotension","Carotid sinus hypersensitivity","Epileptic seizure","Situational syncope (cough, micturition, defecation)","Functional (dissociative) episodes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed history and eyewitness account of the event and its prodrome","12-lead ECG to screen for arrhythmia or conduction disease","Lying and standing blood pressure to detect orthostatic hypotension","Head-up tilt-table testing in recurrent or uncertain cases","Ambulatory ECG or an implantable loop recorder for infrequent unexplained episodes","Echocardiography where structural heart disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["Syncope during exertion or when supine, suggesting a cardiac cause","Absence of a prodrome, or syncope causing serious injury","Family history of sudden cardiac death or an abnormal ECG","Palpitations preceding collapse or known structural heart disease"],"prognosis":"Reflex syncope is benign in terms of mortality, but recurrent episodes can cause injury and impair quality of life; most patients respond well to education, trigger avoidance, and physical counter-manoeuvres.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing convulsive syncope as epilepsy and starting antiepileptic drugs","Failing to exclude cardiac syncope, which carries a worse prognosis","Over-relying on tilt testing, which has limited sensitivity and specificity","Not teaching counter-pressure manoeuvres and prodrome recognition"],"management":["Reassure and educate about the benign nature, teaching recognition of the prodrome and prompt lying down with leg elevation","Advise trigger avoidance such as prolonged standing, heat and dehydration, with generous fluid and salt intake","Teach physical counter-pressure manoeuvres such as leg crossing and hand-gripping at the prodrome to abort episodes","Review and reduce hypotensive and vasodilator medications","Consider midodrine or fludrocortisone for recurrent episodes refractory to conservative measures","Consider cardiac pacing in selected older patients with a documented cardioinhibitory asystolic response, referring recurrent or uncertain cases to an autonomic or syncope service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"reflux_hypersensitivity":{"name":"Reflux hypersensitivity","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oesophageal reflux hypersensitivity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rome IV criteria / gastroenterology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Heartburn or chest pain triggered by reflux events despite physiologically normal oesophageal acid exposure","It is distinguished from functional heartburn by a positive association between symptoms and reflux episodes on monitoring","It is distinguished from reflux disease by the absence of excessive acid exposure and of mucosal injury","Both acidic and weakly acidic reflux events can trigger symptoms in sensitised patients","Diagnosis rests on endoscopy plus ambulatory pH-impedance monitoring with symptom-association analysis","Treatment may combine acid suppression or reflux-reducing measures with neuromodulators targeting hypersensitivity"],"pathophysiology":"Reflux hypersensitivity is a disorder of gut-brain interaction in which the oesophagus perceives physiological reflux as painful. Although total acid exposure is within normal limits and the mucosa is intact, peripheral and central sensitisation lowers the threshold at which reflux events, whether acidic or weakly acidic, provoke symptoms. The positive temporal correlation between reflux episodes and symptoms defines the condition and separates it from functional heartburn, in which no such correlation exists.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease","Functional heartburn","Eosinophilic oesophagitis","Oesophageal motility disorder","Cardiac chest pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with biopsies to exclude mucosal and eosinophilic disease","Ambulatory pH-impedance monitoring showing normal acid exposure but positive symptom association","Oesophageal manometry to exclude a motility disorder","Assessment of response to prior acid suppression","Evaluation of psychological contributors"],"redFlags":["Alarm features such as dysphagia, weight loss or bleeding","Chest pain with cardiac features","Objective testing not supporting the diagnosis","Progressive symptoms"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign, and symptom control is often achievable by reducing reflux events and using neuromodulators to lower hypersensitivity.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with functional heartburn (which lacks symptom-reflux correlation)","Assuming reflux disease and escalating acid suppression alone","Omitting symptom-association analysis on reflux monitoring","Missing eosinophilic oesophagitis or cardiac chest pain"],"management":["Reduce reflux events with a trial of proton pump inhibitor acid suppression and reflux-reducing lifestyle measures such as weight loss, avoiding late meals and elevating the head of the bed","Use neuromodulators such as low-dose tricyclic antidepressants or selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors to target oesophageal hypersensitivity","Confirm the diagnosis with endoscopy and pH-impedance monitoring showing normal acid exposure but positive symptom association","Address psychological contributors with reassurance and psychological therapies where relevant","Consider anti-reflux surgery in carefully selected patients with a genuine, well-documented reflux trigger","Refer to gastroenterology for symptoms refractory to combined acid-suppressing and neuromodulatory treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rome IV diagnostic criteria","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"reflux_nephropathy":{"name":"Reflux nephropathy","system":"Renal / Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic pyelonephritis with reflux","vesicoureteric reflux nephropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nephrology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Renal scarring resulting from vesicoureteric reflux, typically with childhood urinary tract infections","It is an important cause of chronic kidney disease and of hypertension in children and young adults","Characteristic imaging shows focal cortical scars overlying dilated calyces, often at the poles","It may present with hypertension, proteinuria, recurrent infections or incidentally reduced renal function","Established scarring is irreversible, so prevention and early detection are key","Management focuses on blood pressure and proteinuria control and prevention of further infection and scarring"],"pathophysiology":"Reflux nephropathy develops when vesicoureteric reflux, particularly with intrarenal reflux and urinary infection during early childhood, damages the renal parenchyma and produces focal scarring. The scars disrupt nephrons and, once a critical amount of renal mass is affected, trigger hyperfiltration injury in the remaining nephrons, causing progressive proteinuria, glomerulosclerosis and declining function. Activation of the renin-angiotensin system contributes to the associated hypertension, which further accelerates renal injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic pyelonephritis without reflux","Other causes of chronic kidney disease with scarring","Obstructive uropathy","Renal dysplasia or hypoplasia","Analgesic or other tubulointerstitial nephropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasound and DMSA scintigraphy for scarring","Assessment of blood pressure, proteinuria and renal function","Micturating cystourethrography where active reflux is investigated","Urinalysis and culture for infection","Long-term monitoring of renal function"],"redFlags":["Progressive chronic kidney disease","Difficult-to-control hypertension","Significant proteinuria","Recurrent pyelonephritis causing new scarring"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the extent of scarring; limited disease may remain stable, but extensive bilateral scarring can progress to end-stage kidney disease, especially with uncontrolled hypertension or proteinuria.","pitfalls":["Failing to control hypertension and proteinuria that drive progression","Attributing the scarring purely to infection without recognising reflux","Overlooking reflux nephropathy as a cause of young-adult hypertension","Missing ongoing preventable urinary infections"],"management":["Use renin-angiotensin system blockade with an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin-receptor blocker to control hypertension and reduce proteinuria and hyperfiltration injury","Achieve rigorous blood-pressure control to slow progression of chronic kidney disease","Prevent and promptly treat urinary tract infection to limit further scarring, with antibiotic prophylaxis in selected children","Consider endoscopic treatment or ureteric reimplantation for vesicoureteric reflux in selected cases","Undertake long-term monitoring of renal function, proteinuria and blood pressure","Manage progression towards end-stage disease with metabolic and dietary measures and timely renal-replacement planning, with nephrology and urology input and family screening given the familial tendency of vesicoureteric reflux"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nephrology reference texts (standard reference)","Paediatric nephrology references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"refsum_disease":{"name":"Refsum disease","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Classic Refsum disease","Heredopathia atactica polyneuritiformis","Phytanic acid storage disease","PHYH","PEX7"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews Refsum Disease / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic Refsum disease is an autosomal recessive peroxisomal disorder of phytanic acid alpha-oxidation, usually from PHYH mutations and less often from PEX7 defects","The cardinal tetrad is retinitis pigmentosa, chronic peripheral polyneuropathy, cerebellar ataxia and raised cerebrospinal fluid protein without pleocytosis (albuminocytological dissociation)","Night blindness and progressively constricted visual fields from pigmentary retinopathy are often the earliest and most consistent feature","Additional clues include anosmia, sensorineural deafness, ichthyosis, skeletal abnormalities such as a shortened fourth toe, and cardiac conduction disturbance","Diagnosis rests on a markedly elevated plasma phytanic acid with normal or only mildly elevated very-long-chain fatty acids, distinguishing it from generalised peroxisomal disorders","Because phytanic acid is entirely of dietary (exogenous) origin, strict dietary restriction of phytanic acid, avoiding ruminant fats, dairy and certain fish, is the mainstay of treatment","Rapid weight loss and fasting mobilise phytanic acid from fat stores and can precipitate acute deterioration, so these are avoided; plasma exchange is used generically for acute severe episodes"],"pathophysiology":"Classic Refsum disease is a peroxisomal single-enzyme disorder in which deficiency of phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase (encoded by PHYH), or less commonly a defect of the peroxisomal targeting receptor PEX7, blocks the alpha-oxidation of phytanic acid. Phytanic acid is a branched-chain fatty acid derived exclusively from the diet, and because its beta-methyl group prevents beta-oxidation it must first undergo alpha-oxidation. When this pathway fails, phytanic acid accumulates in blood and lipid-rich tissues, where it is incorporated into membranes and disrupts myelin, retinal and neural function, producing the neuropathy, retinopathy and ataxia. Its wholly dietary origin is what makes dietary restriction effective.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of syndromic retinitis pigmentosa such as Usher and Bardet-Biedl syndromes","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy","Charcot-Marie-Tooth and other hereditary neuropathies","Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata (PEX7-related, with high phytanic acid)","Generalised peroxisome biogenesis disorders (Zellweger spectrum)","Abetalipoproteinaemia and vitamin E deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma phytanic acid level, which is markedly elevated","Plasma pristanic acid and very-long-chain fatty acids to distinguish from generalised peroxisomal disorders","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis showing raised protein with normal cell count","Ophthalmological assessment with electroretinography for pigmentary retinopathy","Nerve conduction studies and audiometry","PHYH and PEX7 molecular genetic testing"],"redFlags":["Cardiac arrhythmia or conduction block, a recognised cause of sudden death","Acute worsening of weakness or ataxia after fasting, illness or rapid weight loss","Rapidly progressive visual or hearing loss","Severe polyneuropathy compromising mobility or respiration"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and sustained dietary phytanic acid restriction the neuropathy and ataxia can stabilise or partially improve and progression slows, although retinopathy, deafness and anosmia tend to persist; untreated disease progresses and carries a risk of fatal cardiac complications.","pitfalls":["Permitting rapid weight loss or fasting, which mobilises stored phytanic acid and can trigger acute deterioration","Failing to measure phytanic acid, so a treatable disorder is mistaken for another hereditary neuropathy or retinopathy","Overlooking cardiac conduction disease that requires monitoring","Not distinguishing classic Refsum disease from generalised peroxisomal disorders by checking very-long-chain fatty acids"],"management":["Lifelong dietary restriction of phytanic acid, avoiding ruminant fats, dairy and certain fish, is the mainstay since phytanic acid is wholly dietary in origin","Maintain adequate caloric intake and avoid rapid weight loss or fasting, which mobilise phytanic acid from fat stores and can precipitate acute deterioration","Use plasmapheresis or lipid apheresis to rapidly lower phytanic acid in acute exacerbations or severe cardiac and neurological involvement","Provide dietitian-supervised nutrition with monitoring of plasma phytanic acid to guide dietary adjustment","Manage complications with cardiac conduction monitoring and pacing where needed, ophthalmology for the retinopathy, and support for neuropathy, ataxia, deafness and ichthyosis","Coordinate multidisciplinary specialist care and offer genetic counselling with family screening for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Refsum Disease","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"reinke_edema":{"name":"Reinke oedema","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Laryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["polypoid corditis","Reinke's oedema","smoker's polyp","polypoid degeneration of the vocal folds"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Diffuse gelatinous swelling of the superficial lamina propria (Reinke's space) producing bilateral, floppy, oedematous vocal folds","Strongly associated with cigarette smoking, often compounded by vocal abuse and laryngopharyngeal reflux","Classically causes a characteristically deep, gravelly, low-pitched voice, particularly noticeable and distressing in women","Usually bilateral though frequently asymmetrical, with the mucosa billowing on inspiration","Smoking cessation is the single most important intervention and can halt progression","Bulky disease is treated by microlaryngeal surgery to evacuate the gelatinous material and redrape the mucosa, staged for each side to avoid webbing","Sudden change or a firm focal area in a heavy smoker should prompt exclusion of coexisting dysplasia or carcinoma"],"pathophysiology":"Reinke oedema is a chronic accumulation of oedematous, myxoid material within the superficial lamina propria of the vocal fold, the potential space known as Reinke's space. Prolonged irritation, above all from tobacco smoke, together with phonotrauma and reflux, increases vascular permeability and impairs lymphatic drainage in this loosely bound layer, so fluid and mucoid matrix collect and the covering mucosa becomes lax and redundant. The increased mass lowers the fundamental frequency of vibration, producing the typical low, rough voice, and in advanced disease the pendulous folds can narrow the airway.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal fold polyp","Vocal fold nodules","Laryngeal amyloidosis","Hypothyroid myxoedematous voice change","Intracordal cyst","Glottic carcinoma in a smoker"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible fibreoptic laryngoscopy to confirm diffuse bilateral polypoid change","Videostroboscopy to assess the mucosal wave and exclude a focal lesion","Thyroid function assessment where myxoedema is suspected","Evaluation for laryngopharyngeal reflux","Histology of excised mucosa to exclude dysplasia or malignancy in smokers"],"redFlags":["A firm or leukoplakic focal area suggesting dysplasia or carcinoma","Impaired vocal fold mobility","Stridor or exertional dyspnoea from bulky airway-narrowing folds","Rapid unilateral change out of keeping with symmetrical polypoid degeneration"],"prognosis":"Progression usually halts with smoking cessation, and surgery reliably reduces bulk and raises vocal pitch, though the deepened voice may not fully normalise; continued smoking leads to recurrence and carries ongoing malignant risk.","pitfalls":["Operating on both vocal folds simultaneously with aggressive mucosal removal, risking an anterior glottic web","Failing to insist on smoking cessation, allowing recurrence","Overlooking coexisting carcinoma masked by diffuse polypoid change","Attributing the low voice solely to habit and missing treatable hypothyroidism"],"management":["Smoking cessation is the single most important intervention and can halt progression","Treat coexisting laryngopharyngeal reflux with a proton pump inhibitor and anti-reflux measures, and reduce phonotrauma with vocal hygiene","Perform microlaryngeal surgery to evacuate the gelatinous material and redrape the redundant mucosa for bulky disease, staging each side to avoid anterior webbing","Provide urgent airway management for advanced disease causing obstruction","Examine excised mucosa histologically to exclude dysplasia or malignancy in smokers","Offer speech-and-language voice therapy before and after surgery, with surveillance for recurrence and ongoing malignant risk, especially with continued smoking"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"reinke_oedema":{"name":"Reinke Oedema","system":"ENT","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Reinke's oedema","Polypoid corditis","Polypoid degeneration of the vocal folds"],"chapter":null,"source":"Otolaryngology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The accumulation of gelatinous fluid within Reinke's space (the superficial layer of the lamina propria) of the vocal folds, producing diffuse polypoid swelling that is usually bilateral.","The hallmark symptom is a progressively low-pitched, gruff, hoarse voice, and it is a classic cause of an abnormally deep, masculine-sounding voice in women.","Cigarette smoking is the dominant risk factor, with vocal overuse, laryngopharyngeal reflux and hypothyroidism as important contributors.","Diagnosis is by laryngoscopy and videostroboscopy showing floppy, fluid-filled polypoid folds with an increased mucosal wave.","Because almost all patients are smokers, coexisting laryngeal dysplasia or carcinoma must be excluded, and any leukoplakia, ulceration or asymmetry warrants biopsy.","First-line management targets the causes: smoking cessation, voice therapy, and treatment of reflux and hypothyroidism.","Persistent, severe or airway-compromising disease is treated with microlaryngeal surgery, incising the superior fold and evacuating the gelatinous material while preserving mucosa.","Overaggressive resection or operating on both folds at the same sitting risks scarring, anterior web formation and permanent dysphonia."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic irritation of the vocal fold epithelium, predominantly from tobacco smoke and compounded by phonotrauma, laryngopharyngeal reflux and hypothyroidism, increases vascular permeability and impairs drainage within Reinke's space. This potential space is normally loose, sparsely vascular and devoid of lymphatics, so fluid and gelatinous mucoid material accumulate readily within the superficial lamina propria and expand the fold into a floppy, polypoid structure. The added mass lowers the fundamental frequency of vocal fold vibration, producing the characteristic deep, hoarse voice, and when severe the redundant tissue can prolapse and obstruct the glottic airway. Changes are typically bilateral and, in early stages, largely reversible once the irritant, particularly smoking, is removed.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal fold polyp or nodule","Vocal fold cyst","Laryngeal dysplasia or carcinoma (must be excluded in smokers)","Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis","Laryngeal amyloidosis","Myxoedematous voice change of hypothyroidism","Muscle tension dysphonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible or rigid laryngoscopy with videostroboscopy","Microlaryngoscopy and biopsy of any suspicious mucosal lesion to exclude malignancy","Thyroid function tests","Assessment for laryngopharyngeal reflux","Perceptual and acoustic voice assessment by speech and language therapy","Documentation of smoking history and cessation support","Airway assessment where bulky disease causes obstruction"],"redFlags":["Stridor or airway obstruction from bulky polypoid folds","A unilateral lesion, ulceration, leukoplakia or fold fixation suggesting malignancy","Rapidly progressive or unremitting hoarseness","Referred otalgia, dysphagia or a neck mass","Haemoptysis in a heavy smoker"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is good; with smoking cessation and voice care early disease can regress, and surgery reliably improves the voice. The deep vocal quality may only partly normalise, and continued smoking causes recurrence.","pitfalls":["Assuming disease is benign and failing to exclude carcinoma in a smoker.","Operating on both folds simultaneously and risking an anterior glottic web and scarring.","Neglecting smoking cessation, leading to recurrence after surgery.","Overlooking untreated hypothyroidism or reflux as perpetuating factors.","Setting unrealistic expectations that the voice will return to a high pitch."],"management":["Complete smoking cessation is the cornerstone, since removal of tobacco irritation allows early polypoid change to regress and prevents recurrence","Voice therapy with a speech and language therapist to reduce phonotrauma and correct vocal overuse","Treat contributing laryngopharyngeal reflux with proton-pump inhibitor therapy and antireflux lifestyle measures","Correct coexisting hypothyroidism with thyroid hormone replacement","Microlaryngoscopic phonosurgery with a lateral cordotomy and evacuation of the gelatinous material, preserving the mucosal edge, for persistent dysphonia or airway compromise","Emergency airway assessment and intervention when severe bilateral polypoid folds prolapse and threaten the glottic airway","Surveillance laryngoscopy with biopsy of any leukoplakia, ulceration or asymmetry to exclude coexisting laryngeal dysplasia or carcinoma","Refer to laryngology with joint speech and language therapy input for combined voice and structural rehabilitation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Otolaryngology and laryngology textbooks","Voice disorder clinical guidelines","Phonosurgery reference literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"reis_bucklers_corneal_dystrophy":{"name":"Reis-Bucklers corneal dystrophy","system":"Eye / Cornea","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["corneal dystrophy of Bowman layer type 1","granular corneal dystrophy type 3"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / corneal dystrophy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant dystrophy affecting the Bowman layer, associated with mutations in the TGFBI gene","Presents in childhood with recurrent painful corneal erosions","Progressive superficial opacities develop at the level of Bowman layer, reducing vision over time","It shares its TGFBI genetic basis with other classic stromal dystrophies (granular, lattice, Thiel-Behnke)","Erosions tend to lessen with age as opacification and scarring increase","It commonly recurs in grafts after keratoplasty, so superficial ablative procedures are often preferred"],"pathophysiology":"Reis-Bucklers corneal dystrophy is caused by mutations in the transforming growth factor beta-induced (TGFBI) gene, leading to deposition of abnormal protein at the level of the Bowman layer and superficial stroma. The abnormal material disrupts and replaces the Bowman layer, causing surface irregularity, poor epithelial adhesion with recurrent erosions in early life, and progressive superficial opacification that degrades vision. Because the underlying epithelial and anterior stromal cells continue to produce the abnormal protein, the dystrophy characteristically recurs in corneal grafts.","additionalDifferentials":["Thiel-Behnke corneal dystrophy","Granular corneal dystrophy","Lattice corneal dystrophy","Map-dot-fingerprint dystrophy","Anterior corneal scarring from other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination of the superficial opacities","TGFBI genetic testing to confirm and subtype","Corneal topography and imaging (for example anterior segment OCT)","Family history and examination of relatives","Assessment of visual impact and erosion frequency"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual loss from opacification","Infective keratitis complicating an erosion","Recurrence within a previous graft","Severe recurrent erosion episodes"],"prognosis":"Vision declines gradually with superficial opacification; superficial keratectomy or phototherapeutic ablation can improve vision, though the dystrophy tends to recur, including in grafts.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with Thiel-Behnke or other TGFBI dystrophies without genetic testing","Performing penetrating keratoplasty when a superficial procedure would avoid rapid recurrence","Missing infective keratitis during erosions","Overlooking the autosomal dominant family history"],"management":["Treat recurrent corneal erosions with intensive ocular lubricants, hyperosmotic agents and a bandage soft contact lens during acute episodes","Phototherapeutic keratectomy or superficial keratectomy to remove abnormal Bowman-layer deposits and improve both surface irregularity and vision","Prefer superficial ablative procedures over penetrating keratoplasty because the TGFBI-driven deposits characteristically recur within grafts","Topical antibiotic cover for epithelial defects and analgesia for the pain of acute erosions","Regular slit-lamp and topographic monitoring to track opacification, erosion frequency and visual decline","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant TGFBI dystrophy, with examination and testing of at-risk relatives","Refer to a corneal specialist for surgical planning and consideration of repeat superficial ablation when disease recurs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","IC3D classification of corneal dystrophies"],"crossLinks":[]},"reis_bucklers_dystrophy":{"name":"Reis-Bucklers corneal dystrophy","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["corneal dystrophy of Bowman layer type I (CDB1)","granular corneal dystrophy type III","TGFBI-related Bowman layer dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology / Cornea by Krachmer","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant dystrophy of the Bowman layer and superficial stroma, presenting in childhood with painful recurrent erosions","Caused by mutations in the TGFBI gene, placing it within the same gene cluster as granular and lattice dystrophies","Slit-lamp examination shows confluent greyish geographic or ring-shaped opacities at the level of Bowman layer","The deposits are granular (hyaline) material staining red with Masson trichrome, distinguishing it from the curly-fibre Thiel-Behnke dystrophy","Recurrent erosions are typically frequent and severe early in life, later giving way to reduced vision from superficial scarring and surface irregularity","Corneal sensation may be reduced and the surface becomes progressively irregular, degrading vision","Management uses lubrication and erosion care, with phototherapeutic keratectomy or lamellar keratoplasty for scarring and visual loss","Deposits and erosions characteristically recur superficially after treatment, including within grafts"],"pathophysiology":"Reis-Bucklers corneal dystrophy is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in the transforming growth factor beta-induced gene (TGFBI). Abnormal mutant protein accumulates as hyaline (granular) deposits that replace and disrupt the Bowman layer and superficial stroma. Destruction of the Bowman layer weakens epithelial adhesion, producing frequent painful erosions in early life, while progressive deposition and superficial scarring create an irregular, hazy surface that reduces vision over time. Because the deposits arise from an inherited protein abnormality, they tend to recur in the superficial cornea after surgical removal.","additionalDifferentials":["Thiel-Behnke corneal dystrophy (honeycomb Bowman layer dystrophy)","Granular corneal dystrophy","Lattice corneal dystrophy","Epithelial basement membrane dystrophy with recurrent erosions","Superficial stromal scarring from prior keratitis","Salzmann nodular degeneration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp biomicroscopy showing geographic Bowman layer opacities","Histology with Masson trichrome staining of hyaline deposits","TGFBI genetic testing to confirm diagnosis and distinguish from Thiel-Behnke dystrophy","Electron microscopy to differentiate granular rod-like from curly-fibre deposits when needed","Anterior segment optical coherence tomography to assess depth of superficial deposits","Corneal topography to quantify surface irregularity before treatment"],"redFlags":["Frequent severe erosions causing disabling pain in childhood","Non-healing epithelial defect with risk of secondary infection","Progressive superficial scarring and irregular astigmatism reducing vision","Rapid recurrence of deposits after phototherapeutic keratectomy or grafting"],"prognosis":"Painful recurrent erosions dominate early life and later diminish as superficial scarring reduces vision. Superficial procedures can improve the surface, but deposits and erosions commonly recur, sometimes requiring repeated intervention.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with Thiel-Behnke dystrophy without histological or genetic confirmation","Attributing childhood erosions to trauma and missing the hereditary dystrophy","Assuming a single procedure is curative despite the tendency to recur","Overlooking reduced corneal sensation and its impact on healing"],"management":["Manage the frequent painful erosions of early life with ocular lubricants, hyperosmotic drops and a bandage contact lens, adding topical antibiotic cover for epithelial defects","Phototherapeutic keratectomy or superficial keratectomy to ablate the hyaline Bowman-layer deposits and rehabilitate vision and surface regularity","Favour superficial ablative techniques over full-thickness keratoplasty, as the inherited protein deposits recur in the superficial cornea after removal","Analgesia and, where needed, autologous serum drops to promote healing of recurrent erosions and improve epithelial adhesion","Serial slit-lamp examination, anterior segment imaging and topography to monitor deposit progression and surface irregularity","Genetic counselling for autosomal dominant TGFBI disease with examination of first-degree relatives","Refer to a corneal subspecialist for surgical planning and management of recurrent deposits, which may require repeated intervention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Cornea by Krachmer, Mannis and Holland"],"crossLinks":[]},"relapsing_fever":{"name":"Relapsing Fever","system":"Infectious Disease (Spirochetal / Vector-borne)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Louse-borne relapsing fever","LBRF","Soft tick relapsing fever","STRF","Hard tick relapsing fever","HTRF","Borrelia recurrentis infection","Borrelia miyamotoi disease"],"chapter":190,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1444","clinicalPearls":["Relapsing fever is caused by several Borrelia spirochete species and occurs in three epidemiologic forms: louse-borne (LBRF, B. recurrentis), soft tick-borne (STRF, multiple Ornithodoros-transmitted species), and hard tick-borne (HTRF, mainly B. miyamotoi and B. lonestari) (p.1444).","The hallmark is two or more febrile episodes separated by afebrile intervals of well-being; the relapsing course reflects antigenic variation of the spirochete's surface lipoproteins, with each variant cleared as specific antibody develops (p.1444-1446).","In LBRF the first fever is typically unremitting for 3-6 days and is usually followed by a single milder relapse, whereas STRF produces multiple febrile periods of 1-3 days each; interfebrile intervals span roughly 4-14 days (p.1446).","A 'crisis' may terminate the first fever episode (rigors, a further rise in temperature, increased pulse and blood pressure for about 15-30 min), followed by drenching sweats, falling temperature, and hypotension lasting several hours (p.1446).","Soft ticks (Ornithodoros) feed briefly, often under 30 minutes and usually while the victim sleeps, then leave undetected, so the exposure may go unrecognized (p.1445).","Transovarial transmission in soft ticks means infection risk can persist in a dwelling for years, and a case may be diagnosed long after the last reported local case (p.1445-1447).","LBRF is acquired not from the louse bite itself but by rubbing crushed louse hemolymph into the bite site or auto-inoculating it into conjunctivae or a wound; humans are the sole reservoir of B. recurrentis (p.1445).","Jaundice, epistaxis, and subconjunctival hemorrhages are common in LBRF but typically absent in STRF and HTRF; splenomegaly or splenic tenderness is common in both LBRF and STRF (p.1446).","Localizing neurologic signs are more frequent in STRF than in LBRF/HTRF; B. turicatae is notably neurotropic, and unilateral or bilateral Bell's palsy (cranial neuritis) characteristically appears in the second or third febrile episode rather than the first (p.1446).","B. miyamotoi disease (HTRF) is less distinctive, often presenting as acute fever with chills, headache, and myalgia 1-2 weeks after a tick bite, and has been mistaken for undifferentiated sepsis; meningoencephalitis occurs particularly in patients with impaired humoral immunity (e.g., after rituximab) (p.1446).","Diagnosis of LBRF/STRF rests on direct microscopic detection of spirochetes in blood (Wright, Giemsa, or Giemsa-Wright stain), best collected at or just before the fever peak; spirochete density in B. miyamotoi/B. lonestari may be too low for smear alone (p.1447).","Outbreaks of LBRF (often alongside typhus) cluster in settings of famine, war, refugee migration, homelessness, and incarceration, and can appear in migrant camps far from the country of origin (p.1445)."],"pathophysiology":"Borrelia are slender, double-membrane spirochetes that are extracellular pathogens with a limited biosynthetic genome about one-quarter the size of E. coli; their flagella lie between the two membranes. Tick-borne spirochetes enter in tick saliva at the onset of feeding, and from a small inoculum STRF organisms proliferate in blood (doubling roughly every 6 h) to 10^6-10^7/mL or more, while HTRF organisms grow more slowly to lower densities. Although they lack potent exotoxins and lack lipopolysaccharide endotoxin, abundant Borrelia lipoproteins activate Toll-like receptors, driving an endotoxemia-like proinflammatory response with elevated TNF-alpha, IL-6, and IL-8. Spirochete binding to erythrocytes causes red cell aggregation, splenic/hepatic sequestration, hepatosplenomegaly, and anemia; bleeding reflects thrombocytopenia, impaired hepatic clotting-factor production, and small-vessel blockage by aggregates of spirochetes, erythrocytes, and platelets. Serotype-specific IgM appears after a few days and lyses circulating bacteria by bactericidal action or opsonization; release of bacterial products from dying organisms drives a spontaneous 'crisis,' the antibiotic-triggered counterpart of which is the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction. Antigenic variation of surface lipoproteins produces successive bacteremic relapses, each ended by variant-specific antibody. Some species are neurotropic and enter the relatively immune-sheltered brain, and spirochetes can cross the maternal-fetal barrier to cause placental damage, intrauterine growth retardation, and congenital infection (p.1444-1447).","additionalDifferentials":["Rocky Mountain spotted fever and other rickettsioses","Ehrlichiosis","Anaplasmosis","Tick-borne viral infection (including a viral encephalitis transmitted by Ixodes)","Babesiosis","Lyme disease (especially when STRF presents with Bell's palsy in co-endemic areas)","Malaria","Typhoid fever","Epidemic (louse-borne) typhus","Dengue","Leptospirosis","Trench fever (Bartonella quintana, also louse-transmitted)","Influenza (LBRF cough plus fever and myalgia can mimic it)","Undifferentiated sepsis (notably with B. miyamotoi disease)"],"infectionMimics":["Malaria (periodic fever, co-endemic, can coexist as co-infection)","Typhoid fever","Epidemic louse-borne typhus (Rickettsia prowazekii) and other rickettsioses","Leptospirosis","Dengue","Lyme disease (cross-reactive serology and shared Bell's palsy)","Anaplasmosis, ehrlichiosis, and babesiosis","Influenza"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wright, Giemsa, or Giemsa-Wright-stained thin blood smear examined over several oil-immersion fields (sensitive when spirochete density is >= 10^5/mL), ideally drawn at or just before the fever peak","Thick blood smear treated with 0.5% acetic acid before staining, or a wet mount of citrated blood examined by phase-contrast/dark-field microscopy for motile spirochetes","PCR / nucleic acid amplification testing of blood or CSF (more sensitive than thin smear, especially between febrile episodes; preferred for B. miyamotoi and B. lonestari)","Culture of blood or CSF in Barbour-Stoenner-Kelly broth medium (slow, rarely offered)","GlpQ-based serologic assay (specific for relapsing-fever Borrelia and negative in Lyme disease species, but often negative when smear/PCR are positive)","CSF analysis in any case with signs of meningitis/meningoencephalitis (mononuclear pleocytosis and mildly-to-moderately elevated protein support CNS involvement)","Complete blood count (normocytic anemia; variable leukocyte count; thrombocytopenia, sometimes <50,000/uL)","C-reactive protein and procalcitonin (elevated)","Liver enzymes and coagulation studies (elevated aminotransferases; PT/PTT may be moderately prolonged)","ECG and chest radiography where myocarditis is suspected (prolonged QTc, cardiomegaly, pulmonary edema)"],"redFlags":["Signs of meningitis or meningoencephalitis (warrants CSF analysis and consideration of CNS-directed therapy)","Cranial neuritis, especially uni- or bilateral Bell's palsy, or visual impairment from iridocyclitis/panophthalmitis (which may be permanent)","Myocarditis: gallop rhythm, prolonged QTc, cardiomegaly, and pulmonary edema","Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Diffuse bleeding, jaundice, and poor liver function","Stupor or coma on admission","Pregnancy (high risk of abortion, stillbirth, or perinatal death)","Concurrent malaria, typhus, or typhoid","Abrupt clinical worsening within 1-3 h of starting antibiotics (Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction with hypotension and falling platelets), which can be fatal without monitoring"],"prognosis":"Untreated mortality is approximately 10-70% for LBRF and 4-10% for STRF, depending heavily on coexisting conditions (e.g., malnutrition, other infection) and available medical support. With prompt antibiotic treatment, mortality falls to about 2-5% for LBRF and under 2% for STRF; no deaths from HTRF have been reported. Poor-prognosis features include concurrent malaria, typhus, or typhoid; pregnancy; stupor or coma at admission; diffuse bleeding; poor liver function; myocarditis; and bronchopneumonia. The Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction in LBRF carries roughly a 5% mortality without adequate monitoring and resuscitation. LBRF or STRF in pregnancy frequently causes abortion, stillbirth, or perinatal death, though congenital malformations have not been reported. Documented posttreatment sequelae and prolonged disability have not been established for any form, and partial immunity to reinfection appears to develop in residents of perennially high-risk areas (p.1448).","pitfalls":["Whole-cell serologic assays (including ELISA and the C6 peptide ELISA for Lyme disease) can be falsely positive in relapsing fever due to antigenic cross-reactivity among spirochetes (p.1447).","Serology cannot distinguish among Borrelia species as the cause, and a positive IgG on a single specimen may reflect past infection rather than the current illness (p.1447).","Specific serologic assays (e.g., GlpQ-based) are commonly negative at the very time a blood smear or PCR would be positive (p.1447).","Spirochete density in B. miyamotoi or B. lonestari infection may be too low for diagnosis by blood smear alone, so PCR is preferred (p.1447).","Smears drawn during afebrile intervals may miss spirochetemia; sampling at/just before the fever peak (or using NAAT) improves yield (p.1447).","For B. miyamotoi HTRF, prompt removal of an embedded tick may not prevent infection because transmission occurs soon after feeding begins (p.1447).","STRF presenting with Bell's palsy can be misdiagnosed as Lyme disease in co-endemic regions (p.1447).","Co-infections (malaria, typhus, typhoid with relapsing fever; B. miyamotoi with Lyme disease) may obscure the diagnosis (p.1447).","A Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction can be provoked even by antibiotics with suboptimal antiborrelial activity (e.g., ciprofloxacin) given for an unsuspected case (p.1448)."],"management":["Eradicate the Borrelia spirochaete with a tetracycline (doxycycline), or a macrolide such as erythromycin as an alternative, using penicillin where tetracyclines are contraindicated","Escalate to a parenteral third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone when there is meningitis or meningoencephalitis with CSF involvement","Anticipate and manage the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction after the first antibiotic dose, with close observation, fluid resuscitation and antipyretics through the rigor, hypotension and drenching sweats","Supportive care with rehydration, correction of anaemia and treatment of coexisting malnutrition or intercurrent infection","Vector eradication with delousing and clothing hygiene for louse-borne disease, and tick avoidance and eviction of Ornithodoros ticks from infested dwellings for tick-borne disease","Notify public health authorities and screen contacts and shared dwellings, given persistence of infected soft ticks over years","Monitor for further relapses and re-treat if febrile episodes recur after antibody-mediated clearance of the initial variant"],"severityClassification":"No formal numeric severity score is given. Clinically, severity is stratified by relapsing-fever type and complications: LBRF carries higher untreated mortality (about 10-70%) than STRF (about 4-10%), while HTRF/B. miyamotoi disease is mildest with no reported deaths. CNS involvement (confirmed or suspected meningitis/meningoencephalitis, supported by CSF mononuclear pleocytosis and elevated protein) marks severe disease warranting parenteral therapy. Features defining a severe/poor-prognosis course include concurrent malaria, typhus, or typhoid; pregnancy; stupor or coma at admission; diffuse bleeding; poor liver function; myocarditis; and bronchopneumonia (p.1446-1448).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1444","Harrison 22e p.1445","Harrison 22e p.1446","Harrison 22e p.1447","Harrison 22e p.1448"],"crossLinks":[]},"relapsing_polychondritis":{"name":"Relapsing Polychondritis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Rheumatologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RP"],"chapter":378,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2917","clinicalPearls":["Rare systemic disease defined by recurrent inflammation of cartilaginous structures, with the ears, nose, respiratory tract, and joints as hallmark sites; the eyes, inner ear, nervous system, skin, and cardiovascular system may also be involved (p.2917).","Ear involvement is the most common manifestation, affecting roughly 80-90% of patients, and typically spares the non-cartilaginous lobule; pain may occur with minimal visible swelling or erythema (p.2918).","Triggers for ear pain often include minor trauma (lying on the affected side, wearing glasses) or temperature change; 'cauliflower ear' from cartilage damage occurs only in a small subset after recurrent episodes (p.2918).","Nasal disease often presents as a pressure sensation at the bridge of the nose; a saddle-nose deformity may develop but is not universal, and unlike GPA the nasal perforations in RP are usually anterior rather than septal (p.2918-2919).","Airway involvement such as subglottic stenosis may be misdiagnosed as adult-onset asthma; severe narrowing can cause stridor and is a medical emergency, and chronic damage can lead to tracheomalacia, bronchomalacia, or tracheal calcification (p.2919).","Costochondral joint inflammation can produce severe bilateral chest pain that is reproducible on palpation and not movement-related, sometimes mimicking acute coronary syndrome and prompting emergency evaluation (p.2919-2920).","Recurrent chondritis of the ears and nose in older male patients with severe systemic inflammation and progressive bone marrow failure should prompt evaluation for VEXAS syndrome (somatic UBA1 mutation) (p.2917, 2920).","Episcleritis is the most frequent ocular manifestation; scleritis is rare but warrants urgent ophthalmologic evaluation given the risk of vision loss, scleromalacia, or globe rupture (p.2920).","Because disease activity is intermittent and patients may appear non-inflamed during a clinic visit, a longitudinal history and review of patient-provided photographs are essential to diagnosis (p.2918).","MAGIC syndrome (mouth and genital ulcers with inflamed cartilage) overlaps with Behcet's syndrome features such as pathergy and GI involvement and may include large-vessel vasculitis of the aorta (p.2920).","Some patients carry concurrent rheumatologic diagnoses such as Sjogren's syndrome or systemic lupus erythematosus (p.2917).","Neurologic attribution to RP is a diagnosis of exclusion; infection and malignancy must be ruled out first, though encephalitis and meningitis can occur in RP (p.2920)."],"pathophysiology":"The exact mechanisms remain unclear despite study of genetic, environmental, and immunologic factors. Limited animal-model data suggest autoimmunity against cartilage components may contribute: immunizing certain animals against type II collagen, type IX collagen, or matrilin-1 can reproduce aspects of the human phenotype. However, autoantibodies (including anti-type II collagen) are neither sensitive nor specific enough for diagnosis, and observational data showing B-cell-depleting therapy is not particularly effective argue against a dominant antibody-mediated mechanism. Proinflammatory cytokines and chemokines linked to both innate and adaptive immunity have been associated with RP but are inconsistent. Histologically, acute cartilage shows a mixed myeloid and lymphoid inflammatory infiltrate at the cartilage interface; over time there is lacunar breakdown, loss of chondrocytes, and replacement by fibrosis (p.2918).","additionalDifferentials":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) - distinguished by ANCA positivity and glomerulonephritis, which are not features of RP (p.2920)","Autoinflammatory diseases (p.2920)","VEXAS syndrome - especially in older males with cytopenia, macrocytosis, and lymphopenia (p.2920)","Rheumatoid arthritis - the small-joint pattern can mimic RA but RP arthritis is nonerosive and nondeforming (p.2919)","Behcet's syndrome - overlap via MAGIC syndrome (p.2920)","Adult-onset asthma - airway chondritis is frequently misattributed to asthma (p.2919)"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious diseases of the external ear mimicking ear chondritis (p.2920)","Infectious diseases mimicking nasal chondritis (p.2920)","Infectious airway disease mimicking airway chondritis (p.2920)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Traumatic otohematoma and red ear syndrome (ear) (p.2920)","Cutaneous diseases of the external ear (p.2920)","Angiocentric centrofacial lymphoma (nasal chondritis) (p.2920)","Traumatic airway stenosis and congenital airway disorders (p.2920)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Audiometry to assess for conductive and sensorineural hearing loss in all suspected patients (p.2920)","Dynamic expiratory-phase chest CT to evaluate tracheobronchomalacia (p.2920)","Direct laryngoscopy to visualize upper-airway cartilage damage; bronchoscopy of the lower airway can exacerbate disease and must be used with caution (p.2920-2921)","Echocardiography to assess valvular heart disease (p.2921)","FDG-PET to detect inflammation in large arteries and larger airways (p.2921)","Vestibular testing (finger-to-nose, Romberg, nystagmus) when dizziness is present (p.2920)","Genetic testing for UBA1 and bone marrow assessment for VEXAS in older males with cytopenias (p.2920)","Cartilage biopsy reserved for excluding mimics (infection, malignancy) since findings are nonspecific (p.2918, 2921)"],"redFlags":["Stridor from severe airway/subglottic narrowing - a medical emergency requiring urgent attention to avoid mortality (p.2919)","Features of scleritis - urgent ophthalmology referral due to risk of vision loss, scleromalacia, or globe rupture (p.2920)","Cytopenia with macrocytosis and lymphopenia in older males - suggests VEXAS and possible progressive bone marrow failure (p.2917, 2920)","Severe costochondral chest pain that can mimic acute coronary syndrome (p.2919-2920)"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend heavily on organ involvement. Airway disease can progress to tracheomalacia, bronchomalacia, or tracheal calcification, and severe subglottic narrowing carries mortality risk. Scleritis can lead to vision loss or globe rupture. VEXAS-associated disease involves progressive bone marrow failure and is potentially life-threatening, so prompt recognition is essential. Acute-phase reactants (ESR, CRP) are not reliably elevated, so they cannot be used to gauge severity (p.2917-2920).","pitfalls":["Airway involvement is commonly misdiagnosed as adult-onset asthma, delaying treatment and risking chronic large-airway damage (p.2919)","Normal ESR and CRP do not exclude active RP, as acute-phase reactants are not reliably elevated (p.2918)","There is no diagnostic blood or tissue biomarker; relying on a single test is a pitfall - diagnosis rests on clinical pattern recognition over time (p.2918, 2920)","Cartilage biopsy yields nonspecific findings and can cause morbidity; it should not be expected to confirm RP (p.2918, 2921)","Patients may appear non-inflamed at a single visit because disease activity is intermittent (p.2918)","Published diagnostic criteria were derived from small cohorts, are largely expert-opinion-based, and have not been formally validated (p.2920)","Assuming a saddle nose implies GPA - in RP the deformity usually lacks septal perforation (perforations are anterior) (p.2918-2919)"],"management":["Systemic corticosteroids are the mainstay for active chondritis, given at higher doses for organ-threatening flares and tapered to the lowest effective maintenance dose","Add steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate for persistent or relapsing disease","Consider biologic therapy, including TNF inhibitors or tocilizumab, for refractory or severe airway and ocular involvement","NSAIDs or short steroid courses for isolated auricular, nasal or costochondral inflammation, with analgesia and protection of inflamed cartilage from minor trauma","Treat threatened airway as an emergency, with securing of the airway and consideration of stenting, positive-pressure support or tracheostomy for severe subglottic stenosis, tracheomalacia or bronchomalacia","Structured surveillance for organ complications with audiometry, echocardiography and dynamic expiratory chest CT, and urgent ophthalmology review for scleritis","Refer to rheumatology within a multidisciplinary team, and investigate for VEXAS syndrome or occult malignancy in older men or atypical, treatment-resistant disease"],"severityClassification":"No formally validated severity grading exists. Several diagnostic schemes have been proposed: McAdam's criteria require 3 of 6 features (bilateral auricular chondritis, nonerosive seronegative inflammatory polyarthritis, nasal chondritis, ocular inflammation, respiratory-tract chondritis, vestibular/cochlear dysfunction); the Damiani-Levine modification accepts 3 clinical features, or 1 feature plus histologic chondritis, or 2 features plus clinical response to treatment; Michet's criteria use 3 major criteria (ear, nose, or respiratory-tract inflammation) and 4 minor criteria (ocular inflammation, hearing loss, vestibular dysfunction, seronegative inflammatory arthritis), requiring 2 major or 1 major plus 2 minor. All are limited by lack of precise organ-involvement definitions (p.2920).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2917","Harrison 22e p.2918","Harrison 22e p.2919","Harrison 22e p.2920","Harrison 22e p.2921"],"crossLinks":[]},"relentless_placoid_chorioretinitis":{"name":"Relentless placoid chorioretinitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ampiginous choroiditis","relentless placoid chorioretinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ryan's Retina / white-dot syndrome literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare placoid inflammatory chorioretinopathy considered to sit on a spectrum between APMPPE and serpiginous choroiditis (hence 'ampiginous')","Characterised by numerous placoid lesions, often more than fifty over the disease course, involving both posterior pole and periphery","Follows a prolonged, relapsing course over months rather than the self-limiting behaviour of classic APMPPE","Lesions show the typical early hypofluorescence and late staining of placoid disease on fluorescein angiography","Vision can be preserved but is threatened by foveal involvement and recurrent activity","Long-term immunosuppression is often required to control the relentless course"],"pathophysiology":"The condition is an idiopathic inflammatory disorder of the choriocapillaris and overlying retinal pigment epithelium, producing placoid lesions similar to those of acute posterior multifocal placoid pigment epitheliopathy and serpiginous choroiditis. Unlike self-limiting APMPPE, the inflammatory process recurs relentlessly, generating a large number of lesions across the fundus over an extended period, with choriocapillaris hypoperfusion contributing to the angiographic and imaging findings.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute posterior multifocal placoid pigment epitheliopathy","Serpiginous choroiditis","Tuberculous serpiginous-like choroiditis","Syphilitic placoid chorioretinitis","Birdshot chorioretinopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fluorescein and indocyanine green angiography","Fundus autofluorescence to map active and healed lesions","Optical coherence tomography for outer-retinal and RPE changes","Workup to exclude tuberculosis and syphilis before immunosuppression","Serial wide-field imaging to monitor the relapsing course"],"redFlags":["Foveal or peripapillary lesions threatening central vision","Frequent relapses despite treatment","Features suggesting an infective (tuberculous/syphilitic) mimic","Progressive enlargement resembling serpiginous spread"],"prognosis":"The course is protracted with repeated relapses, but with sustained immunosuppression many eyes retain useful vision unless the fovea is repeatedly involved.","pitfalls":["Assuming a self-limiting APMPPE course and undertreating","Starting immunosuppression before excluding tuberculosis and syphilis","Underestimating relapse risk on stopping therapy","Overlooking peripheral lesions by examining only the posterior pole"],"management":["Exclude infective mimics, particularly tuberculosis and syphilis, before starting immunosuppression given the placoid morphology","Induce control of active inflammation with systemic corticosteroids to suppress the choriocapillaris and retinal pigment epithelium disease","Establish long-term steroid-sparing immunosuppression, such as mycophenolate, azathioprine, ciclosporin or a biologic, to control the relentless relapsing course","Monitor disease activity and treatment response with serial fundus autofluorescence, fluorescein and indocyanine green angiography and optical coherence tomography","Prioritise urgent treatment intensification when lesions threaten or involve the fovea to preserve central vision","Watch for and treat secondary choroidal neovascularisation with anti-VEGF therapy","Manage within a uveitis specialist service, coordinating immunosuppression monitoring for drug toxicity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ryan's Retina","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"rem_sleep_behavior_disorder":{"name":"REM sleep behaviour disorder","system":"Sleep medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RBD","REM behaviour disorder","REM sleep behavior disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / International Classification of Sleep Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from loss of the normal muscle atonia of REM sleep, allowing patients to physically enact their dreams","Behaviours include talking, shouting, punching, kicking and leaping from bed, often mirroring vivid or violent dream content","Injuries to the patient or the bed partner are common and frequently prompt presentation","Episodes cluster in the latter half of the night when REM sleep predominates","Polysomnography confirming REM sleep without atonia is required for diagnosis","Idiopathic RBD is a strong prodromal marker of the synucleinopathies, notably Parkinson's disease, dementia with Lewy bodies and multiple system atrophy","Management combines clonazepam and/or melatonin with practical measures to make the sleeping environment safe","Secondary RBD can be provoked by antidepressants, other drugs, alcohol withdrawal or brainstem lesions"],"pathophysiology":"During normal REM sleep, brainstem circuits centred on the sublaterodorsal nucleus of the pons actively inhibit spinal motor neurones, producing the characteristic muscle atonia. In REM sleep behaviour disorder these circuits fail, so the motor accompaniments of dreaming are no longer suppressed and dreams are acted out. Because these pontine and medullary structures are affected early by alpha-synuclein pathology, the disorder often heralds a neurodegenerative synucleinopathy years before other features appear.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-REM parasomnias such as sleepwalking, sleep terrors and confusional arousals","Obstructive sleep apnoea with arousals mimicking dream enactment (pseudo-RBD)","Nocturnal seizures, particularly of frontal lobe origin","Periodic limb movement disorder","Nightmare disorder","Post-traumatic stress-related nocturnal behaviours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Video-polysomnography demonstrating REM sleep without atonia, with or without captured behaviours","Collateral history from the bed partner about the nature and timing of behaviours","Neurological examination for early parkinsonism or cognitive change","Review of medications, particularly antidepressants, and of alcohol use","MRI brain if a structural brainstem lesion is suspected, especially in younger patients"],"redFlags":["Emerging parkinsonism, cognitive decline or autonomic failure suggesting evolving synucleinopathy","Onset in a young person or focal neurological signs pointing to a structural or demyelinating lesion","Serious injury to the patient or bed partner","Atypical features suggesting nocturnal seizures rather than a parasomnia"],"prognosis":"Symptoms are usually controllable with clonazepam or melatonin together with a safe sleep environment; however, most people with idiopathic RBD will develop a neurodegenerative synucleinopathy over subsequent years, so long-term neurological follow-up is advised.","pitfalls":["Attributing dream enactment to nightmares without confirmatory polysomnography","Missing obstructive sleep apnoea causing pseudo-RBD","Neglecting bedroom safety advice, leaving the patient or partner at risk of injury","Failing to counsel appropriately and to monitor for later neurodegenerative disease","Overlooking an antidepressant or other drug as a precipitant"],"management":["Make the sleep environment safe first, padding or lowering the bed, removing sharp objects and weapons, and considering separate sleeping arrangements for an injured bed partner","Melatonin as first-line pharmacotherapy to restore REM atonia with a favourable tolerability profile","Clonazepam where melatonin is insufficient, used with caution in older adults and those with cognitive impairment, gait instability or sleep apnoea","Review and withdraw precipitating drugs, especially serotonergic antidepressants, and reduce alcohol use","Identify and treat comorbid sleep disorders such as obstructive sleep apnoea, and assess for narcolepsy where suggested","Long-term neurological follow-up for emerging parkinsonism or cognitive change, as isolated disease is a strong prodrome of an alpha-synucleinopathy","Counsel the patient and family about injury prevention and the recognised risk of future neurodegenerative disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd edition (ICSD-3)","Principles and Practice of Sleep Medicine (Kryger, Roth, Dement)"],"crossLinks":["recurrent_isolated_sleep_paralysis"]},"rem_sleep_behaviour_disorder":{"name":"REM sleep behaviour disorder","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RBD","REM sleep without atonia with dream enactment"],"chapter":null,"source":"American Academy of Sleep Medicine ICSD-3","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Loss of the normal muscle atonia of REM sleep allows patients to physically enact their dreams, often with vocalisation and complex movements","Behaviours are frequently violent, such as punching or kicking, and can injure the patient or bed partner","Episodes arise in the second half of the night when REM sleep predominates and dreams are typically vivid and unpleasant","Isolated (idiopathic) RBD is a strong prodromal marker of the alpha-synucleinopathies, including Parkinson disease, dementia with Lewy bodies and multiple system atrophy","Polysomnography demonstrating REM sleep without atonia is required to confirm the diagnosis","Secondary RBD can be precipitated by certain antidepressants and occurs in narcolepsy"],"pathophysiology":"REM sleep behaviour disorder results from failure of the brainstem circuits, particularly in the pons and medulla, that normally generate the active muscle paralysis of REM sleep. Without this atonia, the motor commands accompanying dreaming are expressed as movement. Degeneration of these brainstem nuclei by alpha-synuclein pathology explains the strong link between idiopathic RBD and later development of a synucleinopathy, often years after the sleep disorder appears.","additionalDifferentials":["NREM parasomnias (sleep terrors, sleepwalking)","Sleep-related hypermotor (frontal lobe) epilepsy","Obstructive sleep apnoea with arousals (pseudo-RBD)","Nocturnal panic attacks","Periodic limb movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Video polysomnography demonstrating REM sleep without atonia","Neurological examination for early parkinsonism or cognitive change","Medication review for antidepressant-precipitated cases","Assessment for narcolepsy where clinically suggested","Longitudinal follow-up for evolving neurodegenerative disease"],"redFlags":["Injurious nocturnal behaviour to self or bed partner","Emerging parkinsonism, cognitive decline or autonomic failure","Atypical features suggesting nocturnal seizures","Abrupt onset after starting a new medication"],"prognosis":"Symptoms are usually controllable with bedroom safety measures and medication, but isolated RBD carries a high long-term risk of conversion to a defined neurodegenerative synucleinopathy.","pitfalls":["Mistaking RBD for a NREM parasomnia despite the late-night timing and dream enactment","Failing to counsel about and monitor for neurodegenerative risk","Overlooking obstructive sleep apnoea mimicking RBD","Not reviewing antidepressants as a precipitant"],"management":["Institute bedroom safety measures as the foundation of care, securing the sleeping area and protecting both patient and bed partner from injury","Melatonin as preferred first-line treatment to reduce dream enactment while limiting adverse effects","Clonazepam as an effective alternative, used cautiously where there is cognitive impairment, falls risk or coexisting sleep apnoea","Review medications and withdraw antidepressants that can precipitate the disorder","Assess for and treat underlying narcolepsy or other comorbid sleep pathology","Longitudinal neurological follow-up given the high risk of conversion of isolated disease to Parkinson disease, dementia with Lewy bodies or multiple system atrophy","Counsel the patient and family sensitively about injury prevention and the prognostic link to neurodegenerative disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["American Academy of Sleep Medicine ICSD-3","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"remitting_seronegative_symmetrical_synovitis_with_pitting_oedema":{"name":"Remitting Seronegative Symmetrical Synovitis with Pitting Oedema","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RS3PE syndrome","RS3PE"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rheumatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by abrupt-onset, symmetrical polyarthritis of the small joints of the hands and feet accompanied by striking pitting oedema over the dorsum of both hands and feet, classically producing a 'boxing-glove' appearance.","Predominantly affects older adults with a male preponderance, and is by definition seronegative for rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies.","Inflammatory markers are typically elevated, and imaging with ultrasound or magnetic resonance demonstrates extensor tenosynovitis and subcutaneous oedema rather than aggressive erosive synovitis.","The response to low-dose corticosteroids is characteristically rapid and dramatic, often within days, which supports the diagnosis retrospectively.","The disease is usually self-limiting and non-erosive, distinguishing it from elderly-onset rheumatoid arthritis, with which it overlaps clinically.","A paraneoplastic form exists; atypical features, constitutional upset, poor or short-lived steroid response, or relapse should prompt an age-appropriate search for occult solid-organ or haematological malignancy.","Overlap with polymyalgia rheumatica and late-onset spondyloarthritis is recognised, and some patients later evolve towards a defined connective-tissue or seronegative arthropathy.","Human leucocyte antigen associations have been reported, and elevated serum vascular endothelial growth factor is thought to underlie the prominent oedema."],"pathophysiology":"The syndrome is understood as a distinctive seronegative inflammatory arthropathy of the ageing musculoskeletal system in which synovitis of the small joints is accompanied by prominent flexor and extensor tenosynovitis, and the characteristic dorsal pitting oedema reflects increased vascular permeability and inflammatory exudation within the peritendinous soft tissues rather than joint effusion alone. Elevated circulating vascular endothelial growth factor has been repeatedly implicated as a driver of the vascular leak and oedema, and matrix metalloproteinases and pro-inflammatory cytokines contribute to the synovial and tenosynovial inflammation. Immunosenescence and a dysregulated innate response in older adults are thought to predispose to the abrupt inflammatory storm, while the frequent association with occult neoplasia in a subset suggests that tumour-derived factors can act as a paraneoplastic trigger of the same final pathway. The predominantly non-erosive, remitting behaviour and exquisite corticosteroid responsiveness distinguish the process pathophysiologically from the persistent, autoantibody-driven and erosive synovitis of rheumatoid arthritis, placing it within a spectrum that also encompasses polymyalgia rheumatica and late-onset spondyloarthritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Elderly-onset seronegative rheumatoid arthritis","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Late-onset spondyloarthritis or psoriatic arthritis","Crystal arthropathy including calcium pyrophosphate deposition and gout","Paraneoplastic arthropathy from occult malignancy","Viral polyarthritis","Non-inflammatory causes of limb oedema such as venous, cardiac, renal or lymphatic disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies, expected to be negative","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","Full blood count with blood film","Ultrasound or magnetic resonance imaging of the hands and feet to demonstrate tenosynovitis and subcutaneous oedema","Plain radiographs to confirm the absence of erosions","Serum protein electrophoresis and age-appropriate malignancy screening where paraneoplastic disease is suspected","Antinuclear antibodies and thyroid function to exclude mimics"],"redFlags":["Constitutional features such as weight loss, night sweats or lymphadenopathy suggesting occult malignancy","Poor, absent or only transient response to corticosteroids","Rapid relapse on tapering or withdrawal of steroids","Emergence of joint erosions on imaging, pointing to an alternative erosive arthropathy","New neurological, respiratory or systemic symptoms raising concern for a paraneoplastic or overlap disorder"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally excellent, with most patients achieving durable remission of a non-erosive arthropathy and being able to withdraw corticosteroids over months. Outcome is less favourable in the paraneoplastic subset, where the course is dictated by the underlying malignancy.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the condition as rheumatoid arthritis and committing the patient to long-term disease-modifying therapy","Failing to screen for occult malignancy in atypical or steroid-refractory cases","Attributing the dorsal oedema to cardiac, renal or venous disease and overlooking the underlying synovitis","Assuming all seronegative polyarthritis in older adults represents this syndrome without confirming the characteristic features","Prematurely stopping corticosteroids and misinterpreting relapse as treatment failure"],"management":["Low-dose oral corticosteroids are the mainstay and produce a characteristically rapid, often dramatic resolution of the synovitis and dorsal pitting oedema","Taper corticosteroids gradually over months once symptoms settle, aiming for complete withdrawal given the usually self-limiting non-erosive course","NSAIDs and analgesia as adjuncts for symptomatic relief of joint and soft-tissue pain","Hand and foot therapy to maintain function and reduce oedema during the acute inflammatory phase","Undertake age-appropriate malignancy screening, including serum protein electrophoresis, when features are atypical, the steroid response is poor or short-lived, or disease relapses, reflecting the paraneoplastic subset","Monitor for relapse and for evolution into elderly-onset rheumatoid arthritis, reassessing rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP status if the pattern changes","Refer to rheumatology to confirm the diagnosis and guide steroid withdrawal"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard rheumatology reference texts","Clinical guidance on seronegative inflammatory arthritis in older adults"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_agenesis":{"name":"Renal agenesis","system":"Genitourinary / Renal tract","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["unilateral renal agenesis","bilateral renal agenesis","congenital absence of the kidney"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Renal agenesis is the complete failure of development of one or both kidneys owing to absent or failed interaction between the ureteric bud and the metanephric blastema; the unilateral form is relatively common and often silent, whereas the bilateral form is rare and incompatible with postnatal life","Bilateral renal agenesis produces the Potter sequence, in which severe oligohydramnios leads to pulmonary hypoplasia, limb contractures and the characteristic Potter facies, with death usually from respiratory failure","Unilateral renal agenesis is compatible with normal life through compensatory hypertrophy of the solitary kidney and is often found incidentally or on antenatal imaging","Absence of the kidney is typically accompanied by an absent ipsilateral ureter and a hemitrigone, so a missing ureteric orifice is seen at cystoscopy","There is a strong association with genital tract anomalies owing to shared mesonephric and Mullerian development: in girls a unicornuate uterus, Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome or the obstructed hemivagina with ipsilateral renal agenesis (OHVIRA / Herlyn-Werner-Wunderlich) syndrome, and in boys seminal vesicle cysts or absence of the vas deferens","It may be isolated or part of a syndrome such as the VACTERL association, branchio-oto-renal syndrome, Kallmann syndrome or renal coloboma syndrome, so extrarenal features should prompt genetic evaluation and family screening","Lifelong surveillance of the solitary kidney, with attention to blood pressure, proteinuria and renal function and avoidance of nephrotoxic insults, is advised"],"pathophysiology":"Renal agenesis results from failure of the metanephros to develop, most often because the ureteric bud fails to form from the mesonephric (wolffian) duct, fails to reach the metanephric blastema, or fails to induce it. As the ureteric bud also gives rise to the ureter and the corresponding hemitrigone, its absence explains the missing ipsilateral ureter and hemitrigone. The shared embryological origin with the genital ducts underlies the frequent Mullerian anomalies in females and wolffian anomalies in males. In bilateral agenesis the fetus produces little or no urine; because fetal urine is the principal component of amniotic fluid in the later part of pregnancy, profound oligohydramnios develops, compressing the fetus and preventing normal lung expansion, and so producing the pulmonary hypoplasia and deformational features of the Potter sequence.","additionalDifferentials":["Involuted multicystic dysplastic kidney","Severe renal hypoplasia or dysplasia","Ectopic (for example pelvic) or crossed fused kidney with a kidney present but outside the fossa","A small atrophic kidney from reflux or obstructive nephropathy","An involuted kidney after a vascular insult","Other causes of oligohydramnios such as membrane rupture, uteroplacental insufficiency or bladder outlet obstruction in the bilateral phenotype"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antenatal and postnatal renal tract ultrasound to show an empty renal fossa and assess amniotic fluid","DMSA scintigraphy to confirm absence of functioning tissue and exclude an ectopic kidney","MRI, fetal or postnatal, for anatomy and to locate ectopic renal tissue","Pelvic ultrasound or MRI for uterine and genital anomalies in girls, and seminal vesicle assessment in boys","Blood pressure, urinalysis for proteinuria and serum renal function for surveillance of the solitary kidney","Genetic and syndromic evaluation with screening of first-degree relatives where indicated"],"redFlags":["Bilateral agenesis with severe oligohydramnios and anticipated pulmonary hypoplasia requiring perinatal counselling","Hypertension, proteinuria or declining function in a solitary kidney","An acute illness threatening the only functioning kidney","An obstructive genital anomaly such as OHVIRA presenting with cyclical pain around menarche"],"prognosis":"Unilateral renal agenesis with a normal solitary kidney generally has a good prognosis, though it carries a modestly increased lifetime risk of hypertension, proteinuria and chronic kidney disease that warrants monitoring. Bilateral renal agenesis is almost uniformly fatal in the neonatal period from pulmonary hypoplasia, with only rare reported survival following intensive intervention.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing agenesis when the kidney is in fact ectopic or crossed and fused, without searching elsewhere","Overlooking associated genital tract anomalies, particularly Mullerian anomalies in girls","Failing to arrange lifelong surveillance of the solitary kidney","Mistaking an involuted multicystic dysplastic kidney for primary agenesis","Not offering genetic counselling and family screening despite recognised heritable forms"],"management":["For bilateral agenesis with Potter sequence, provide neonatal palliative care given the lethal pulmonary hypoplasia, with antenatal counselling of parents; offer intensive respiratory support only within experimental amnioinfusion protocols where available","In the unilateral solitary kidney, protect nephron mass with lifelong surveillance of blood pressure, urinalysis for proteinuria and serum renal function","Institute renin-angiotensin blockade with an ACE inhibitor or ARB if proteinuria or hypertension emerges, to slow progression to chronic kidney disease","Counsel on avoidance of nephrotoxic drugs and give individualised advice about contact and collision sports to protect the single kidney","Evaluate and manage associated genital tract anomalies, such as Mullerian anomalies or an obstructed hemivagina in girls and vas or seminal vesicle abnormalities in boys","Refer for genetic and syndromic evaluation, screening for VACTERL, branchio-oto-renal, Kallmann or renal coloboma syndromes, with counselling and screening of first-degree relatives","Coordinate care through paediatric nephrology and urology, escalating to specialist input if renal function declines"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_angiomyolipoma":{"name":"Renal angiomyolipoma","system":"Genitourinary / Kidney","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AML","renal AML"],"chapter":null,"source":"Uro-radiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign renal tumour composed of blood vessels, smooth muscle and mature fat, part of the PEComa family","Most are sporadic, solitary and found incidentally; multiple bilateral lesions suggest tuberous sclerosis complex","The presence of macroscopic fat on CT or MRI is characteristic and usually allows a confident diagnosis","Fat-poor variants can mimic renal cell carcinoma and may require biopsy or excision to clarify","Larger lesions carry a risk of spontaneous, potentially life-threatening retroperitoneal haemorrhage (Wunderlich syndrome)","Aneurysms within the feeding vessels increase the bleeding risk and can be targeted by embolisation","Epithelioid angiomyolipoma is an uncommon variant with malignant potential"],"pathophysiology":"Angiomyolipomas are benign mesenchymal neoplasms arising from perivascular epithelioid cells and containing a variable mixture of dysmorphic thick-walled vessels, smooth muscle and adipose tissue. In tuberous sclerosis, biallelic inactivation of the TSC1 or TSC2 genes leads to constitutive activation of the mTOR pathway, driving multiple and often larger tumours. The abnormal, aneurysm-prone vasculature accounts for the tendency of larger lesions to bleed. The epithelioid variant shows atypical cells and can behave in a malignant fashion.","additionalDifferentials":["Fat-poor angiomyolipoma versus renal cell carcinoma","Clear cell or papillary renal cell carcinoma","Retroperitoneal liposarcoma involving the kidney","Oncocytoma","Renal lipoma or other fatty lesion","Wilms tumour in children"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT or MRI to demonstrate macroscopic fat and characterise the lesion","Assessment of lesion size and intratumoural aneurysms to gauge bleeding risk","Screening for tuberous sclerosis features when lesions are multiple or bilateral","Renal mass biopsy when imaging is indeterminate and malignancy cannot be excluded","Baseline renal function, particularly before intervention"],"redFlags":["Acute flank pain with hypotension suggesting spontaneous haemorrhage","Rapidly enlarging lesion or a large lesion with prominent aneurysms","Features suggesting the epithelioid variant or malignancy","New multiple bilateral lesions raising concern for a syndrome with other organ involvement"],"prognosis":"Most angiomyolipomas are benign with an excellent prognosis; the main risks are haemorrhage from larger lesions and, rarely, malignant behaviour of the epithelioid variant, both of which can be mitigated by surveillance and timely intervention.","pitfalls":["Assuming all angiomyolipomas are benign and overlooking the epithelioid variant","Misdiagnosing a fat-poor lesion as, or overlooking, renal cell carcinoma","Failing to evaluate for tuberous sclerosis when lesions are multiple","Underestimating haemorrhage risk in large or aneurysmal lesions"],"management":["Manage small asymptomatic lesions with active imaging surveillance to track growth and bleeding risk","Offer an mTOR inhibitor such as everolimus or sirolimus to shrink tumours, particularly in tuberous-sclerosis-associated multiple or larger angiomyolipomas driven by mTOR activation","Perform selective arterial embolisation to reduce the bleeding risk of larger lesions and those containing intratumoural aneurysms, and as first-line control of acute haemorrhage","Reserve nephron-sparing surgery for large, symptomatic or diagnostically uncertain lesions and for fat-poor tumours that cannot be distinguished from renal cell carcinoma","Treat spontaneous retroperitoneal haemorrhage (Wunderlich syndrome) as an emergency with resuscitation and urgent embolisation or surgery","Screen for tuberous sclerosis complex when lesions are multiple or bilateral, and refer for genetic counselling and family assessment when the complex is confirmed","Monitor baseline renal function, especially before embolisation or surgery, to preserve nephron mass"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology (standard reference)","WHO Classification of Urinary and Male Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_artery_aneurysm":{"name":"Renal artery aneurysm","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["renal artery aneurysm","RAA","renal arterial aneurysm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard vascular surgery reference texts and visceral aneurysm consensus guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An uncommon visceral aneurysm, frequently discovered incidentally on cross-sectional imaging, often at the main renal artery bifurcation","Fibromuscular dysplasia is a classic underlying cause, alongside atherosclerosis and connective tissue disorders","Many are asymptomatic, but they may cause flank pain, haematuria, or secondary (renin-mediated) hypertension","Mural calcification, sometimes described as a signet-ring pattern, is a common radiological clue on plain imaging","Hypertension may arise from turbulent flow, distal embolisation, or compression and can be an indication for repair even in smaller aneurysms","Rupture is uncommon overall but is a particular concern in pregnancy, where it carries high maternal and fetal mortality","Repair options include endovascular techniques (embolisation, stent grafting) and open reconstruction, chosen according to anatomy and renal preservation goals"],"pathophysiology":"A renal artery aneurysm is a localised dilatation resulting from weakening of the arterial wall, commonly at branch points where haemodynamic stress is greatest. Medial degeneration from fibromuscular dysplasia, atherosclerotic wall damage, and inherited connective tissue disease reduce wall integrity, allowing progressive dilatation. Disturbed flow within the aneurysm can generate thrombus and distal embolisation causing renal ischaemia and activating the renin-angiotensin system, which contributes to secondary hypertension. Increasing wall tension with growth raises rupture risk, which is amplified by the physiological and hormonal changes of pregnancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Renal artery pseudoaneurysm (post-traumatic or iatrogenic)","Renal arteriovenous malformation or fistula","Fibromuscular dysplasia without aneurysm","Renal artery stenosis causing renovascular hypertension","Peripelvic or parapelvic cyst","Other visceral artery aneurysms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT angiography to confirm, localise and size the aneurysm and define branch anatomy","MR angiography as a radiation-sparing alternative, useful in younger patients and pregnancy","Doppler ultrasound for detection and surveillance","Catheter angiography when endovascular repair is being planned","Assessment of renal function and renin-mediated hypertension","Evaluation for fibromuscular dysplasia and connective tissue disorders where suspected"],"redFlags":["Acute flank or abdominal pain with haemodynamic instability suggesting rupture","Any renal artery aneurysm in a pregnant patient given the high rupture-related mortality","Rapid enlargement on serial imaging","Refractory secondary hypertension or deteriorating renal function from embolisation"],"prognosis":"Most incidental aneurysms remain stable and can be monitored, with elective repair offering good outcomes and often preserving renal function; rupture is rare but carries high mortality, especially in pregnancy, and secondary hypertension may improve after successful treatment.","pitfalls":["Overlooking a renal artery aneurysm as a cause of secondary hypertension","Underestimating rupture risk during pregnancy","Confusing a true aneurysm with a pseudoaneurysm after trauma or intervention","Choosing a repair approach that unnecessarily sacrifices renal parenchyma"],"management":["Monitor small, stable, asymptomatic aneurysms with periodic Doppler ultrasound or cross-sectional imaging","Control blood pressure and cardiovascular risk factors, recognising that renin-mediated hypertension may itself be an indication for repair","Repair symptomatic, enlarging or larger aneurysms by endovascular techniques such as coil embolisation or stent-grafting, or by open reconstruction where anatomy at the bifurcation demands it","Offer elective repair to women of childbearing potential and treat established aneurysms before or early in pregnancy given the high rupture-related maternal and foetal mortality","Treat rupture as a vascular emergency with resuscitation and urgent endovascular or surgical intervention, preserving renal parenchyma where possible","Investigate and manage underlying fibromuscular dysplasia and connective tissue disorders, with screening of other arterial territories","Refer to vascular specialists for decisions on the timing and mode of repair and monitor renal function after intervention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Society for Vascular Surgery guidance on visceral artery aneurysms"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_artery_stenosis":{"name":"Renal artery stenosis","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RAS","renovascular disease","renovascular hypertension","ischaemic nephropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Renal artery stenosis is a narrowing of one or both renal arteries that can cause secondary hypertension and, when bilateral or affecting a single kidney, progressive ischaemic nephropathy","Atherosclerosis accounts for the great majority of cases, typically in older patients with widespread vascular disease, while fibromuscular dysplasia is the main cause in younger women","Clues suggesting a renovascular cause include hypertension of abrupt onset or that is severe or resistant, an abdominal bruit, and unexplained kidney asymmetry","A significant rise in creatinine after starting a renin-angiotensin inhibitor, especially with bilateral disease, is a classic pointer to renovascular disease","Recurrent flash pulmonary oedema without a clear cardiac cause can be a manifestation of severe bilateral stenosis","Non-invasive imaging such as duplex ultrasound, CT angiography, or MR angiography is used for diagnosis, with catheter angiography reserved for when intervention is planned","Medical therapy with renin-angiotensin blockade, other antihypertensives, statins, and antiplatelet treatment is the foundation of management for atherosclerotic disease","Revascularisation is reserved for selected patients such as those with fibromuscular dysplasia, rapidly declining function, refractory hypertension, or recurrent pulmonary oedema, since routine stenting has not shown benefit over medical therapy in most atherosclerotic cases"],"pathophysiology":"Renal artery stenosis reduces perfusion pressure to the affected kidney, which the kidney interprets as volume depletion and responds to by activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. The resulting angiotensin-mediated vasoconstriction and aldosterone-driven sodium retention raise systemic blood pressure. When only one kidney is affected, the contralateral kidney can excrete the retained sodium, so hypertension is predominantly renin-driven. With bilateral disease or a single functioning kidney, sodium and volume retention dominate and drive the hypertension. Chronic hypoperfusion also causes ischaemic injury with tubular atrophy, interstitial fibrosis, and glomerulosclerosis, producing ischaemic nephropathy. Renin-angiotensin blockade can precipitate a fall in glomerular filtration because efferent arteriolar tone, needed to maintain filtration pressure in the underperfused kidney, is removed.","additionalDifferentials":["Essential (primary) hypertension","Primary hyperaldosteronism","Phaeochromocytoma","Other secondary causes of hypertension","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","Atheroembolic renal disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound of the renal arteries with velocity measurements","CT or MR angiography to define the anatomy of the stenosis","Assessment of renal function and kidney size asymmetry","Monitoring of creatinine after introduction of renin-angiotensin blockade","Catheter angiography when revascularisation is being considered","Screening for other secondary causes of hypertension when appropriate"],"redFlags":["Rapidly deteriorating renal function, particularly after starting renin-angiotensin blockade","Recurrent flash pulmonary oedema without a cardiac explanation","Severe or resistant hypertension unresponsive to multiple agents","Acute kidney injury suggesting critical bilateral stenosis or renal artery occlusion"],"prognosis":"Fibromuscular dysplasia generally responds well to angioplasty with a good chance of improving or curing hypertension. Atherosclerotic disease reflects generalised vascular disease and carries substantial cardiovascular risk; for most such patients optimal medical therapy gives outcomes comparable to intervention, and prognosis is driven largely by comorbid cardiovascular disease and the degree of established ischaemic nephropathy.","pitfalls":["Reflexively stenting atherosclerotic lesions when evidence favours medical therapy for most patients","Stopping renin-angiotensin blockade for a modest, stable creatinine rise that is actually acceptable","Missing fibromuscular dysplasia as a curable cause of hypertension in a young woman","Failing to recognise bilateral disease as the cause of recurrent flash pulmonary oedema"],"management":["Optimal medical therapy is the foundation for atherosclerotic disease, combining a renin-angiotensin inhibitor for blood pressure with a statin, antiplatelet agent and smoking cessation","Monitor serum creatinine closely after starting an ACE inhibitor or ARB, and reconsider revascularisation if function falls sharply, especially with bilateral disease or a single kidney","Add further antihypertensives to achieve target blood pressure where a single agent is insufficient","Offer percutaneous transluminal angioplasty, usually without stenting, for fibromuscular dysplasia, which frequently improves or cures the hypertension","Reserve angioplasty with stenting for selected atherosclerotic patients, notably those with recurrent flash pulmonary oedema, rapidly declining renal function or hypertension resistant to full medical therapy","Surveil renal function and kidney size to detect progressive ischaemic nephropathy","Refer to nephrology and vascular or interventional services for revascularisation decisions and screen for other secondary causes of hypertension where appropriate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_cell_carcinoma":{"name":"Renal Cell Carcinoma","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RCC","kidney cancer"],"chapter":90,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.687-690","clinicalPearls":["RCC accounts for 90-95% of malignant neoplasms arising from the kidney; the remaining 5-10% are transitional (urothelial) cell carcinomas of the renal pelvis (covered separately in Chap. 91) (p.687-688).","The classic triad of hematuria, flank pain, and a palpable abdominal mass is now uncommon; most tumors are detected incidentally on cross-sectional imaging (CT/MRI) performed for other reasons (p.688-689).","Other presenting features include fever, weight loss, anemia, and a varicocele (p.688).","Once called the \"internist's tumor\" because it was frequently first detected via a paraneoplastic syndrome before modern imaging was common (p.688-689).","Clear cell is the predominant histology (~70% of cases) and is found in >80% of patients who develop metastases; it arises from epithelial cells of the proximal tubules (p.688).","Any solid renal mass should be considered malignant until proven otherwise; a definitive diagnosis is required (p.689).","Most common sites of distant metastasis are the lungs, lymph nodes, liver, bone, and brain; the clinical course can be unpredictable and protracted (p.689).","RCC is associated with anecdotal reports of spontaneous regression (p.687).","Increasing detection of incidental low-stage tumors has improved 5-year survival and increased use of nephron-sparing surgery (partial nephrectomy) (p.689)."],"pathophysiology":"RCC is a heterogeneous group of tumors. Most cases are sporadic, but familial forms exist. Clear cell RCC (the predominant subtype) is characterized by loss of chromosome 3p as the earliest event, causing loss of heterozygosity for 3p genes including VHL, PBRM1, BAP1, and SETD2. VHL encodes a tumor-suppressor protein that regulates transcription of VEGF and other effectors via ubiquitination of hypoxia-inducible factors (HIF); VHL inactivation upregulates VEGF signaling, promoting tumor angiogenesis and growth and rendering clear cell RCC susceptible to antiangiogenic therapy. Clear cell RCC also arises in von Hippel-Lindau syndrome (autosomal dominant, VHL gene on chromosome 3p), which carries a ~70% lifetime risk of clear cell RCC. Papillary subtypes are linked to activating MET mutations or gain of chromosome 7; chromophobe tumors originate from the distal nephron and typically show aneuploidy. Early loss of chromosome 9p confers high risk of early metastatic dissemination and correlates with poor cancer-specific survival (p.687-688).","additionalDifferentials":["Simple/complex renal cysts (p.689)","Benign neoplasms: adenoma, angiomyolipoma, oncocytoma (p.689)","Inflammatory lesions: pyelonephritis or renal abscess (p.689)","Transitional (urothelial) cell carcinoma of the renal pelvis (p.689)","Renal sarcoma (p.689)","Renal lymphoma (p.689)","Wilms' tumor (p.689)","Metastases from cancers originating in other organs (p.689)"],"infectionMimics":["Pyelonephritis (inflammatory renal lesion in the differential of a renal mass) (p.689)","Renal abscess (inflammatory renal lesion in the differential of a renal mass) (p.689)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Renal cysts (p.689)","Angiomyolipoma (p.689)","Oncocytoma (p.689)","Renal adenoma (p.689)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis - part of standard evaluation of a suspected renal tumor (p.689)","Chest radiograph; CT of the chest if metastatic disease is suspected on chest x-ray (p.689)","Urine analysis (p.689)","MRI - useful for evaluating inferior vena cava when tumor thrombus/invasion is suspected, or when IV contrast for CT is precluded by impaired renal function (p.689)","Needle biopsy to confirm histology in small tumors managed with radiographic surveillance before delayed surgery (p.689)"],"redFlags":["Gross hematuria (p.688)","Flank or abdominal pain (p.688)","Palpable abdominal/flank mass (p.688)","Constitutional symptoms: fever, weight loss, anemia (p.688)","New varicocele (p.688)","Paraneoplastic features: erythrocytosis, hypercalcemia, nonmetastatic hepatic dysfunction (Stauffer's syndrome) (p.688)"],"prognosis":"Staging uses the AJCC TNM system. Stage I tumors are <=7 cm and confined to the kidney; stage II are >7 cm and confined to the kidney; stage III extend through the renal capsule but remain within Gerota's fascia, grossly infiltrate the renal vein, or involve regional nodes (N1); stage IV invades adjacent organs or involves nonregional nodes or distant metastases. About 65% present at stage I-II, 15-20% at stage III, and 15-20% at stage IV. Overall 5-year survival is currently ~77% but varies greatly by stage. For metastatic disease, the IMDC risk model uses six factors associated with worse survival (poor performance status, low hemoglobin, high serum calcium, high neutrophils, high platelets, and <1-year interval from diagnosis to systemic treatment). Zero risk factors confer significantly longer median survival (>=5 years) than one to two factors (~4 years) or three to six factors (~3 years) with first-line checkpoint-inhibitor-containing regimens. Projected overall survival for newly diagnosed metastatic disease has tripled over the past 15-20 years (p.688-690).","pitfalls":["Relying on the classic triad (hematuria, flank pain, palpable mass) for diagnosis - it is now uncommon, and most tumors present incidentally (p.688-689).","Assuming chemotherapy will help - metastatic RCC is refractory to cytotoxic chemotherapy; treatment relies on molecularly targeted agents and checkpoint immunotherapy (p.689).","Treating a solid renal mass as benign without proof - any solid renal mass should be considered malignant until proven otherwise (p.689).","Despite many candidate biomarkers investigated for predictive value in metastatic RCC, none have been validated for clinical use to date (p.690).","Renal vein or inferior vena cava invasion does not preclude surgical resection if no metastases are present (p.689)."],"management":["Partial (nephron-sparing) nephrectomy for localised tumours where feasible, with radical nephrectomy reserved for larger or more advanced disease","Offer active surveillance or thermal ablation for small renal masses, particularly in older or comorbid patients, using needle biopsy to confirm histology when surgery may be delayed","Treat metastatic clear cell disease with systemic therapy exploiting VHL-driven angiogenesis, combining immune checkpoint inhibitors with VEGF tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as axitinib, cabozantinib or lenvatinib","Consider cytoreductive nephrectomy and metastasectomy in selected patients with metastatic disease","Provide supportive and palliative care for advanced disease, including bone-directed therapy and radiotherapy for symptomatic metastases and management of paraneoplastic syndromes and anaemia","Undertake structured post-treatment surveillance with cross-sectional imaging and renal function monitoring to detect recurrence and protect the remaining kidney","Refer for genetic counselling and screening when hereditary disease such as von Hippel-Lindau syndrome is suspected, and manage complex cases through a uro-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":"AJCC TNM staging (stages I-IV; Fig. 90-1): Stage I <=7 cm confined to kidney; Stage II >7 cm confined to kidney; Stage III extends through renal capsule within Gerota's fascia, infiltrates renal vein, or N1 nodal involvement; Stage IV invades adjacent organs or has nonregional nodal or distant metastases. For metastatic disease, the IMDC (International Metastatic RCC Database Consortium) risk model stratifies prognosis using six clinical/laboratory factors (p.688-689).","references":["Harrison 22e p.687-690"],"crossLinks":["hypercalcemia"]},"renal_coloboma_syndrome":{"name":"Renal Coloboma Syndrome","system":"Renal genetics / Ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Papillorenal syndrome","PAX2-related syndrome","Renal-coloboma syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Renal and ophthalmic genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider renal coloboma syndrome whenever optic disc anomalies, particularly the 'morning glory' disc or an optic disc coloboma, coexist with renal hypoplasia or dysplasia and otherwise unexplained chronic kidney disease.","Inheritance is autosomal dominant with striking variable expressivity, so a mildly affected parent may show only subtle optic disc changes while their child has significant renal failure.","Renal hypodysplasia, often with oligomeganephronia, drives the progressive kidney disease and makes this one of the important monogenic causes of chronic and end-stage renal disease presenting in childhood.","The optic disc anomaly is frequently asymptomatic or causes only mild visual deficit, so it must be actively sought with dilated fundoscopy rather than assumed absent.","Vesicoureteric reflux and recurrent urinary tract infection can be presenting features and may compound renal damage.","High-frequency sensorineural hearing loss is a recognised, though less consistent, association.","The same gene is implicated in some cases of isolated focal segmental glomerulosclerosis and adult-onset chronic kidney disease occurring without any eye findings.","The disc lesion should be distinguished from glaucomatous cupping and from the colobomas seen in CHARGE syndrome and isolated ocular coloboma."],"pathophysiology":"Renal coloboma syndrome, also termed papillorenal syndrome, is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in PAX2, a paired-box transcription factor essential for the development of the kidney, ureter, optic nerve and otic vesicle. PAX2 is expressed in the developing metanephric mesenchyme and Wolffian duct and in the optic stalk, where it participates in nephron induction and in closure of the optic (choroidal) fissure. Haploinsufficiency impairs these programmes, producing renal hypoplasia and dysplasia, classically small hypodysplastic kidneys with oligomeganephronia that progress to chronic kidney disease, together with an optic nerve dysplasia manifesting as the morning glory disc anomaly or optic disc coloboma, often accompanied by abnormal retinal vasculature. Vesicoureteric reflux, high-frequency hearing loss and, less commonly, central nervous system anomalies may also occur, and the phenotype shows marked variability in severity even within a single family.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract (CAKUT)","CHARGE syndrome (CHD7)","Renal cysts and diabetes syndrome (HNF1B)","Branchio-oto-renal syndrome (EYA1 and SIX genes)","Isolated optic nerve coloboma or morning glory disc anomaly","Alagille syndrome, which may show both ocular and renal features","Isolated focal segmental glomerulosclerosis or familial chronic kidney disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasound to demonstrate small or hypodysplastic kidneys and oligomeganephronia","Serum creatinine with estimated glomerular filtration rate, and urinalysis for proteinuria","Dilated fundoscopy and formal ophthalmological assessment for optic disc coloboma or morning glory anomaly","Micturating cystourethrogram where vesicoureteric reflux is suspected","Audiometry for high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss","PAX2 genetic testing, with a broader CAKUT or HNF1B gene panel where the diagnosis is uncertain","Blood pressure monitoring"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining renal function or progression toward end-stage renal disease","Hypertension","Recurrent febrile urinary tract infection with reflux nephropathy","Significant visual impairment or retinal detachment associated with the disc anomaly","New or worsening proteinuria signalling glomerular involvement"],"prognosis":"The outlook is variable: many patients progress to chronic kidney disease and a substantial proportion reach end-stage renal disease during childhood or early adulthood, whereas the ocular anomaly is usually static and visual outcome ranges from normal to moderately impaired.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing 'isolated' chronic kidney disease or renal hypoplasia without examining the optic discs, and thereby missing the unifying diagnosis.","Mistaking the optic disc coloboma or morning glory anomaly for glaucomatous cupping.","Not screening at-risk relatives because mild expressivity conceals the condition.","Overlooking vesicoureteric reflux as a modifiable contributor to renal damage."],"management":["Manage the underlying renal hypodysplasia as chronic kidney disease, with renin-angiotensin blockade for proteinuria and hypertension, dietary and metabolic measures, and slowing of progression","Identify and treat vesicoureteric reflux and recurrent urinary tract infection, using antibiotic prophylaxis or surgical correction where indicated to limit further renal damage","Plan proactively for renal replacement, preparing for dialysis and transplantation as many patients reach end-stage disease in childhood or early adulthood","Refer for ophthalmological assessment with dilated fundoscopy to detect and monitor the optic disc coloboma or morning glory anomaly, and provide visual support as needed","Screen for high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss with audiometry and arrange audiological support","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal dominant PAX2 disease, testing and examining relatives given the marked variable expressivity","Coordinate long-term care through paediatric and adult nephrology alongside ophthalmology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric nephrology reference texts","Clinical and ophthalmic genetics references","Reviews of congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_glucosuria":{"name":"Familial Renal Glucosuria","system":"Renal / Tubulopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FRG","SLC5A2-related glucosuria","SGLT2 deficiency","Renal glycosuria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nephrology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by loss-of-function variants in SLC5A2, encoding the sodium-glucose cotransporter SGLT2 that reclaims the bulk of filtered glucose in the early proximal tubule.","Defined by persistent glucosuria with a normal plasma glucose and a normal oral glucose tolerance test, in the absence of any other proximal tubular defect.","Inheritance is co-dominant; homozygous or compound heterozygous individuals show the heaviest urinary glucose loss while heterozygotes may have only mild glucosuria.","Traditionally graded as type A (reduced threshold and reduced maximal reabsorptive capacity), type B (reduced threshold with near-normal maximal capacity) and type O (virtually absent glucose reabsorption).","The great majority of individuals are entirely asymptomatic, the condition being found incidentally on routine urinalysis.","Severe (type O) cases may show polyuria, enuresis, mild volume depletion and a tendency to ketosis during prolonged fasting or pregnancy.","It is a benign isolated transport defect that does not evolve into diabetes mellitus or chronic kidney disease.","Pharmacological SGLT2 inhibition reproduces the same isolated glucosuria, which underscores the essential safety of the inherited condition."],"pathophysiology":"Filtered glucose is normally reclaimed almost entirely within the proximal tubule, with the high-capacity, low-affinity transporter SGLT2 handling the majority in the early convoluted segment and SGLT1 retrieving the small remainder more distally. Loss-of-function variants in SLC5A2 reduce or abolish SGLT2 activity, lowering the tubular threshold at which glucose begins to spill into the urine and, in the most severe forms, the maximal reabsorptive capacity as well. Glucose therefore appears in the urine despite entirely normal blood concentrations and normal insulin action. Because the lesion is confined to a single apical transporter, the remaining proximal tubular functions - amino-acid, phosphate, urate and bicarbonate handling - stay intact, which distinguishes the disorder from generalised proximal tubulopathy. The obligatory glucose in the tubular fluid can produce a mild osmotic diuresis, and in the type O phenotype the persistent urinary loss of glucose and fluid underlies the occasional dehydration and ketotic tendency seen during fasting or pregnancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Diabetes mellitus and impaired glucose tolerance, in which glucosuria accompanies hyperglycaemia","Fanconi syndrome, where glucosuria is joined by generalised aminoaciduria, phosphaturia and bicarbonate wasting","Fanconi-Bickel syndrome (GLUT2/SLC2A2 defect) with hepatorenal glycogen accumulation and disordered glucose homeostasis","Gestational glucosuria from the physiologically lowered threshold of pregnancy","Proximal tubular injury from drugs, heavy metals or paraproteins","Maturity-onset diabetes of the young and neonatal diabetes when hyperglycaemia coexists","Alimentary (lag-storage) glucosuria following gastric surgery"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative urine glucose alongside a simultaneously drawn plasma glucose","Fasting plasma glucose, glycated haemoglobin and, where needed, an oral glucose tolerance test","Urine amino acids to exclude generalised proximal tubulopathy","Serum phosphate, bicarbonate and urate to confirm that glucose handling is isolated","Renal function and urinary protein to exclude acquired tubular injury","Molecular testing of SLC5A2","Urinalysis of first-degree relatives given the co-dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Coexisting hyperglycaemia or a raised glycated haemoglobin pointing to true diabetes","Aminoaciduria, phosphaturia or acidosis indicating generalised Fanconi syndrome","Hepatomegaly with fasting hypoglycaemia and post-prandial hyperglycaemia suggesting Fanconi-Bickel syndrome","Growth failure or rickets, which are not features of isolated renal glucosuria","Symptomatic dehydration or ketosis in a severe (type O) case, particularly during pregnancy"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent. Familial renal glucosuria is a benign, non-progressive condition that does not cause diabetes, renal impairment or the metabolic complications of glycaemic disease.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the condition as diabetes mellitus because the urinalysis was not paired with a plasma glucose measurement.","Failing to exclude generalised Fanconi syndrome, in which glucosuria is only one component of a broader proximal leak.","Confusing it with Fanconi-Bickel syndrome, a glycogen-storage disorder with hepatomegaly and disordered glucose homeostasis rather than an isolated transporter defect.","Attributing coincidental symptoms to the glucosuria when the condition is almost always clinically silent.","Overlooking pregnancy, where the lowered renal threshold can unmask or exaggerate glucosuria."],"management":["Reassure and educate the patient that isolated SGLT2-related glucosuria is a benign, non-progressive condition requiring no specific drug therapy","Confirm the diagnosis and actively distinguish it from diabetes mellitus so that unnecessary glucose-lowering treatment is avoided","Ensure adequate fluid and carbohydrate intake during prolonged fasting, intercurrent illness and pregnancy to offset urinary glucose loss and the tendency to ketosis in severe (type O) disease","Monitor for and manage the polyuria, enuresis and mild volume depletion that can occur in heavier urinary losses","Exclude a generalised proximal tubulopathy by checking that amino-acid, phosphate, bicarbonate and urate handling remain normal","Offer genetic counselling for this co-dominantly inherited SLC5A2 disorder, explaining the milder phenotype in heterozygotes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited tubulopathy references","Clinical nephrology references","Renal physiology references"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_hypouricaemia":{"name":"Renal Hypouricaemia","system":"Renal / Tubulopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RHUC","URAT1","SLC22A12","SLC2A9"],"chapter":null,"source":"Inherited kidney disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Often discovered incidentally as a persistently low serum urate in an otherwise well person.","A high fractional excretion of urate is the diagnostic hallmark and separates renal wasting from urate underproduction.","Two principal forms exist: type 1 from SLC22A12 (URAT1) and type 2 from SLC2A9 (GLUT9), the latter tending to cause more profound hypouricaemia.","Exercise-induced acute kidney injury, characteristically after short bursts of anaerobic exertion, is the most important complication.","Uric acid urolithiasis is more frequent, and good hydration is protective.","URAT1 founder variants are common in East Asian, notably Japanese and Korean, populations.","Distinguish from hereditary xanthinuria, in which serum urate is low but urinary urate is also low and xanthine accumulates.","Avoiding strenuous anaerobic exercise and dehydration reduces the risk of acute kidney injury."],"pathophysiology":"Hereditary renal hypouricaemia results from loss-of-function mutations in the tubular transporters that normally reclaim filtered urate. Type 1 disease is caused by mutations in SLC22A12, which encodes URAT1, the apical urate-anion exchanger of the proximal tubule responsible for the bulk of urate reabsorption; type 2 disease is caused by mutations in SLC2A9, which encodes GLUT9, the high-capacity voltage-driven transporter that moves urate across the basolateral membrane. Impaired reabsorption markedly increases the fractional excretion of urate and lowers the serum concentration, with GLUT9 defects producing the more severe hypouricaemia. The loss of urate, a major circulating antioxidant, together with a high urinary urate load, is thought to predispose affected individuals to exercise-induced acute kidney injury — postulated to involve oxidative stress and renal vasoconstriction after anaerobic exercise — and to uric acid stone formation. Renal function is otherwise typically well preserved between episodes.","additionalDifferentials":["Renal hypouricaemia type 2 (SLC2A9/GLUT9) versus type 1 (SLC22A12/URAT1).","Fanconi syndrome, in which hypouricaemia accompanies glycosuria, aminoaciduria and phosphaturia.","Hereditary xanthinuria (xanthine dehydrogenase deficiency) with low urinary urate and xanthine stones.","Molybdenum cofactor deficiency and other purine-metabolism disorders.","Drug-induced hypouricaemia from uricosurics such as benzbromarone, from losartan, high-dose salicylates or SGLT2 inhibitors.","Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuresis and other acquired causes of a low serum urate.","Severe liver disease or malignancy lowering the serum urate."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum urate, confirmed on repeat testing.","Urinary urate with calculation of the fractional excretion of urate.","Renal function and urinalysis.","Renal tract imaging for stones and nephrocalcinosis.","Urinary xanthine and hypoxanthine to exclude xanthinuria.","Screen of proximal tubular function to exclude a generalised Fanconi syndrome.","Genetic testing of SLC22A12 and SLC2A9."],"redFlags":["Acute kidney injury or severe loin pain developing after exercise.","Recurrent uric acid urolithiasis.","Features of a generalised proximal tubulopathy, pointing away from isolated renal hypouricaemia.","Oliguria or a rapid decline in renal function following anaerobic exertion."],"prognosis":"The condition is generally benign with well-preserved long-term renal function. The main threats are recurrent uric acid stones and repeated episodes of exercise-induced acute kidney injury, both of which are largely preventable.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a low serum urate as a laboratory artefact rather than investigating it.","Failing to link post-exercise acute kidney injury to the underlying urate-handling defect.","Confusing renal hypouricaemia with xanthinuria or with a generalised Fanconi syndrome.","Using nephrotoxic drugs such as NSAIDs during an episode of exercise-induced acute kidney injury.","Assuming an asymptomatic patient carries no risk and omitting advice on hydration and exercise."],"management":["Counsel patients to avoid short bursts of intense anaerobic exercise, the principal trigger for exercise-induced acute kidney injury","Maintain generous hydration, particularly around exertion, to protect against both acute kidney injury and uric acid stone formation","Recognise and manage exercise-induced acute kidney injury promptly with rest, rehydration and supportive care, avoiding nephrotoxic agents","Consider antioxidant supplementation around exertion, given loss of the antioxidant role of circulating urate","Surveil for uric acid urolithiasis and nephrocalcinosis with periodic imaging and treat stones as they arise","Offer genetic counselling and family evaluation for the SLC22A12 (URAT1) and SLC2A9 (GLUT9) transporter defects, noting common founder variants in East Asian populations","Monitor baseline renal function to detect any lasting damage from recurrent injury or obstructing stones"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Oxford Textbook of Clinical Nephrology","Reviews of renal urate transport and inherited hypouricaemia","Brenner and Rector's The Kidney"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_hypouricemia":{"name":"Renal hypouricaemia (URAT1/GLUT9)","system":"Renal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["renal hypouricaemia","hereditary renal hypouricaemia","URAT1","SLC22A12","GLUT9","SLC2A9"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / OMIM","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by loss-of-function variants in the urate transporters URAT1 (SLC22A12) or GLUT9 (SLC2A9), leading to renal urate wasting","Defined biochemically by a low serum uric acid with an inappropriately high fractional excretion of urate","Most individuals are asymptomatic and identified incidentally on a low serum urate","The classic complication is exercise-induced acute kidney injury, typically after intense anaerobic exertion","Urate stones and haematuria are the other recognised complications from heavy urinary urate excretion","Management is preventive: avoiding strenuous anaerobic exercise, maintaining good hydration, and considering antioxidant strategies around exertion","GLUT9 defects tend to cause more profound hypouricaemia and a higher complication risk than URAT1 defects"],"pathophysiology":"URAT1 and GLUT9 are the principal transporters mediating reabsorption of filtered urate in the proximal tubule; URAT1 exchanges urate across the apical membrane while GLUT9 returns urate to the blood across the basolateral membrane. Loss of either transporter impairs urate reabsorption, so a large fraction of filtered urate is excreted, producing a low serum concentration with a high fractional excretion. The heavy urinary urate load predisposes to crystal formation, stones, and haematuria. Exercise-induced acute kidney injury is thought to arise because urate is an important circulating antioxidant, and its deficiency, combined with exertional generation of reactive oxygen species, promotes renal vasoconstriction and tubular injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Fanconi syndrome with urate wasting","Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuresis (associated hypouricaemia)","Drug-induced hypouricaemia (uricosuric agents)","Acquired urate wasting in malignancy or liver disease","Xanthine oxidase deficiency (xanthinuria)","Dietary or laboratory causes of low measured urate"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum uric acid demonstrating persistent hypouricaemia","Fractional excretion of urate showing inappropriately high renal loss","Renal function testing during and after episodes of exercise-induced injury","Renal ultrasound for urate stones or nephrocalcinosis","Urinalysis for haematuria and crystals","SLC22A12 and SLC2A9 gene sequencing"],"redFlags":["Acute kidney injury following intense exercise, often with loin pain","Obstructing urate calculi or gross haematuria","Recurrent episodes of exertional renal impairment"],"prognosis":"Long-term outlook is usually good with preventive measures, and baseline renal function is typically normal; however, recurrent exercise-induced acute kidney injury or obstructing stones can cause lasting renal damage if unrecognised.","pitfalls":["Dismissing an isolated low serum urate as unimportant and missing the diagnosis","Failing to advise against strenuous anaerobic exercise in an at-risk patient","Attributing post-exercise acute kidney injury to rhabdomyolysis alone","Overlooking urate stones as the cause of haematuria"],"management":["Advise avoidance of strenuous anaerobic exercise, the classic precipitant of exercise-induced acute kidney injury in this disorder","Encourage sustained good hydration to reduce urate crystal formation, stones and haematuria and to protect the kidney during exertion","Treat episodes of exercise-induced acute kidney injury with rest, fluid resuscitation and supportive management, avoiding further nephrotoxic insults","Consider peri-exercise antioxidant strategies to counter the loss of urate as a circulating antioxidant","Monitor for urate urolithiasis and nephrocalcinosis with renal tract imaging and manage stones accordingly","Confirm and characterise the transporter defect by SLC22A12 (URAT1) and SLC2A9 (GLUT9) sequencing and provide genetic counselling","Follow renal function longitudinally to detect lasting injury from recurrent acute events or obstructing stones"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","OMIM entry for renal hypouricaemia"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_medullary_carcinoma":{"name":"Renal medullary carcinoma","system":"Genitourinary / Kidney","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RMC","renal medullary cancer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Uro-oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, highly aggressive renal malignancy that arises from the collecting duct epithelium of the renal medulla","Occurs almost exclusively in young people with sickle cell trait, and less often with sickle cell disease or other haemoglobinopathies","Typically presents with haematuria, flank or abdominal pain, and a palpable mass, often with weight loss","Most patients have metastatic disease at diagnosis, commonly to lymph nodes, lung, liver and bone","Tumours characteristically show loss of the SMARCB1 (INI1) tumour-suppressor protein on immunohistochemistry","It should be suspected in any young Black patient with sickle cell trait who presents with a renal mass","Prognosis is poor and it responds poorly to conventional targeted agents used in clear cell disease"],"pathophysiology":"Renal medullary carcinoma arises from the distal collecting duct or calyceal epithelium within the renal medulla. The chronic hypoxic, hypertonic medullary environment in individuals with sickling haemoglobinopathies is thought to promote genomic instability and malignant transformation. Loss of function of the SWI/SNF chromatin-remodelling subunit SMARCB1 (INI1) is a defining molecular event, driving an aggressive, poorly differentiated tumour with early lymphovascular invasion and rapid metastatic spread.","additionalDifferentials":["Collecting duct (Bellini) carcinoma","Urothelial carcinoma of the upper tract","High-grade clear cell renal cell carcinoma","Papillary renal cell carcinoma","Metastatic deposit within the kidney","Renal lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen and pelvis to characterise the mass and assess spread","Cross-sectional imaging of chest for pulmonary metastases","Tissue biopsy with immunohistochemistry, including SMARCB1 (INI1) staining","Haemoglobinopathy testing (sickle solubility test and haemoglobin electrophoresis)","Baseline bloods including renal function and markers of tumour burden"],"redFlags":["Visible haematuria with a renal mass in a young patient with sickle cell trait","Rapidly enlarging mass with systemic symptoms","Evidence of widespread metastatic disease at presentation","Bone pain or pathological fracture suggesting skeletal spread"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally poor, with most patients presenting with advanced disease and short survival; localised disease treated with surgery combined with systemic chemotherapy offers the best, though still limited, outcomes.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis in a young patient because renal cancer is assumed to affect older adults","Failing to test for sickle cell trait when a medullary renal mass is found","Misclassifying it as clear cell carcinoma and offering ineffective targeted therapy","Delaying tissue diagnosis in a rapidly progressive tumour"],"management":["Platinum-based cytotoxic chemotherapy is the systemic mainstay for this aggressive SMARCB1-deficient tumour, which is largely resistant to conventional targeted agents","Radical nephrectomy for localised disease, ideally combined with systemic chemotherapy given the high rate of early micrometastatic spread","Deliver care with prompt, upfront multimodal treatment because most patients present with metastatic disease and survival is short","Provide palliative and supportive care, including symptom control, radiotherapy for painful metastases and management of tumour burden, for advanced disease","Confirm the underlying sickle cell trait or haemoglobinopathy and give supportive haematological care to reduce medullary sickling stress","Refer for genetic counselling and family sickle screening, since the tumour arises almost exclusively in those with sickling haemoglobinopathies","Manage through a uro-oncology multidisciplinary team and consider clinical trial enrolment given the poor response to standard therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology (standard reference)","WHO Classification of Urinary and Male Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_oncocytoma":{"name":"Renal oncocytoma","system":"Genitourinary / Kidney","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oncocytoma of kidney"],"chapter":null,"source":"Uro-oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign renal epithelial tumour arising from the intercalated cells of the collecting duct","Usually discovered incidentally as a well-defined solid cortical mass","May show a central stellate scar on imaging, though this is neither sensitive nor specific","Cannot be reliably distinguished from chromophobe renal cell carcinoma by imaging alone","Renal mass biopsy can suggest the diagnosis but sampling and hybrid oncocytic tumours limit certainty","Multiple oncocytomas or oncocytosis may occur in Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome","Because malignancy cannot always be excluded preoperatively, many lesions are still resected"],"pathophysiology":"Renal oncocytoma is composed of oncocytes, cells packed with mitochondria that give an eosinophilic granular cytoplasm, and is thought to derive from the intercalated cells of the collecting duct. It is a benign neoplasm without metastatic potential, but it shares histological and immunophenotypic features with chromophobe renal cell carcinoma, which complicates diagnosis. Hybrid oncocytic-chromophobe tumours occur, particularly in the setting of Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome, reflecting overlap in their cell of origin.","additionalDifferentials":["Chromophobe renal cell carcinoma","Clear cell renal cell carcinoma with eosinophilic features","Papillary renal cell carcinoma, eosinophilic type","Epithelioid angiomyolipoma","Hybrid oncocytic tumour in Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome","Succinate dehydrogenase-deficient renal tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT or MRI to characterise the solid renal mass","Renal mass biopsy where the result would change management","Immunohistochemistry to help separate it from chromophobe carcinoma","Assessment for Birt-Hogg-Dube features when tumours are multiple","Surveillance imaging when a conservative approach is chosen"],"redFlags":["Interval growth or new symptoms suggesting the lesion is not benign","Imaging features overlapping with malignancy","Multiple renal and cutaneous lesions suggesting a hereditary syndrome","Any histological atypia raising doubt about the benign diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Renal oncocytoma is benign with an excellent prognosis and does not metastasise; the main challenge is diagnostic certainty rather than tumour behaviour, and outcomes are excellent whether managed by surveillance or excision.","pitfalls":["Relying on a central scar on imaging to diagnose it confidently","Assuming biopsy fully excludes chromophobe carcinoma","Missing an underlying Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome","Over-treating a benign lesion or, conversely, under-treating a misclassified carcinoma"],"management":["Offer active imaging surveillance for lesions confidently characterised as oncocytoma, as this benign tumour has no metastatic potential","Use renal mass biopsy with immunohistochemistry to support the diagnosis and separate it from chromophobe renal cell carcinoma where the result would change management","Proceed to nephron-sparing partial nephrectomy or excision when malignancy cannot be excluded, when hybrid oncocytic-chromophobe features are suspected, or when the lesion enlarges","Preserve renal function by favouring nephron-sparing over radical approaches given the benign nature of the tumour","Assess for Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome when tumours are multiple or oncocytosis is present, examining for cutaneous and pulmonary features","Refer for genetic counselling and family screening if Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome is confirmed","Continue surveillance imaging when a conservative approach is chosen to detect any change in size or characteristics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology (standard reference)","WHO Classification of Urinary and Male Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_osteodystrophy":{"name":"renal osteodystrophy","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CKD-MBD bone disease","uraemic bone disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oxford Textbook of Clinical Nephrology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Renal osteodystrophy is the bone component of chronic kidney disease-mineral and bone disorder (CKD-MBD), spanning a spectrum from high-turnover to low-turnover bone disease.","It is driven by phosphate retention, reduced 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, hypocalcaemia, FGF23 excess and secondary hyperparathyroidism.","High-turnover disease (osteitis fibrosa cystica) results from secondary and tertiary hyperparathyroidism, whereas low-turnover forms are adynamic bone disease and, historically, osteomalacia from aluminium or vitamin D deficiency.","Adynamic bone disease is increasingly common with over-suppression of PTH, diabetes and peritoneal dialysis, and carries fracture and vascular calcification risk.","Bone histomorphometry using the turnover-mineralisation-volume (TMV) classification after tetracycline labelling is the gold standard; PTH and bone-specific alkaline phosphatase serve as surrogate markers.","Management targets phosphate control (dietary restriction and phosphate binders), correction of vitamin D deficiency, active vitamin D analogues and calcimimetics for hyperparathyroidism.","Vascular and soft-tissue calcification is a major consequence, so excessive calcium load and over-suppression of turnover should be avoided.","Parathyroidectomy is indicated for severe refractory tertiary hyperparathyroidism, calciphylaxis or intractable symptoms."],"pathophysiology":"As renal function declines, phosphate excretion falls and FGF23 rises to maintain phosphate balance, while reduced functioning renal mass lowers 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity and hence calcitriol. Falling calcitriol and calcium, together with hyperphosphataemia, stimulate parathyroid hyperplasia and PTH secretion (secondary hyperparathyroidism); persistent stimulation can lead to autonomous tertiary hyperparathyroidism. High PTH increases osteoclastic and osteoblastic activity, producing high-turnover osteitis fibrosa cystica with marrow fibrosis. Conversely, over-suppression of PTH, skeletal PTH resistance, aluminium or diabetes can produce low-turnover adynamic bone. The disordered mineral milieu also promotes ectopic vascular and soft-tissue calcification.","additionalDifferentials":["osteoporosis","osteomalacia from other causes","primary hyperparathyroidism","Paget's disease of bone","myeloma or metastatic bone disease","aluminium-related bone disease","dialysis-related beta-2 microglobulin amyloidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["serum calcium, phosphate, PTH and bone-specific alkaline phosphatase","25-hydroxyvitamin D","intact FGF23","radiographs for subperiosteal resorption and rugger-jersey spine","DXA for fracture risk","bone biopsy with double tetracycline labelling in uncertainty","imaging of vascular calcification"],"redFlags":["calciphylaxis with painful skin necrosis","symptomatic hypercalcaemia from tertiary hyperparathyroidism","fragility fractures","spontaneous tendon rupture","rapidly rising PTH suggesting autonomous gland activity"],"prognosis":"The disorder is chronic and progresses with declining renal function; it is controllable with CKD-MBD management but is associated with increased fracture risk and cardiovascular mortality.","pitfalls":["relying on DXA alone, which cannot distinguish high- from low-turnover disease","over-suppressing PTH and inducing adynamic bone disease","using excessive calcium-containing binders and promoting vascular calcification","overlooking aluminium toxicity","treating biochemistry without regard to the underlying bone-turnover state"],"management":["Control hyperphosphataemia with dietary phosphate restriction and phosphate binders, avoiding aluminium-based binders that provoke adynamic and osteomalacic bone disease","Correct nutritional vitamin D deficiency with cholecalciferol and suppress secondary hyperparathyroidism using active vitamin D analogues such as calcitriol or alfacalcidol","Add a calcimimetic such as cinacalcet to lower parathyroid hormone while limiting hypercalcaemia and hyperphosphataemia","Optimise dialysis adequacy and tailor dialysate calcium to the underlying bone-turnover state","Monitor serum calcium, phosphate, parathyroid hormone and bone-specific alkaline phosphatase serially, with radiographs and DXA for fracture risk","Refer for parathyroidectomy in refractory secondary or autonomous tertiary hyperparathyroidism","Coordinate care between nephrology and a renal dietitian as part of integrated CKD-mineral and bone disorder management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Oxford Textbook of Clinical Nephrology","KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline on CKD-MBD","Brenner and Rector's The Kidney"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_papillary_necrosis":{"name":"Renal papillary necrosis","system":"Renal / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["papillary necrosis","necrotising papillitis","renal medullary necrosis","sloughed papilla"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Ischaemic necrosis and sloughing of the renal papillae, fragments of which can pass into the urine and cause colic or obstruction","The classic causes are captured by the mnemonic POSTCARDS: Pyelonephritis, Obstruction, Sickle cell disease, Tuberculosis, Cirrhosis or chronic alcoholism, Analgesics, Renal vein thrombosis, Diabetes mellitus and Systemic vasculitis","Diabetes mellitus and chronic analgesic overuse are among the most frequent contributors, and several insults often coexist in one patient","Presentations include haematuria, loin pain, passage of tissue fragments, recurrent infection or features of obstruction","The renal medulla is especially vulnerable because it operates on a marginal blood and oxygen supply","CT urography, or the older intravenous urogram, shows a ring shadow or clubbed, irregular calyces where a papilla has sloughed","Management is largely supportive: treat infection, relieve obstruction caused by a sloughed papilla, and control the underlying disease","Withdrawing offending analgesics and optimising glycaemic control help to prevent progression"],"pathophysiology":"The renal papilla lies at the tip of the medulla and is perfused by the vasa recta in a region of relatively low oxygen tension, making it highly susceptible to ischaemia. Conditions that reduce medullary blood flow or heighten its metabolic vulnerability, such as diabetic microvascular disease, analgesic-induced vascular injury, sickling of red cells within the hypertonic medulla, infection with oedema and raised intrarenal pressure, and obstruction, cause coagulative necrosis of one or more papillae. The necrotic tissue may calcify in situ or detach and pass down the ureter, where it can obstruct and mimic a calculus.","additionalDifferentials":["Ureteric calculus causing obstruction","Urothelial tumour or blood clot presenting as a filling defect","Acute pyelonephritis without necrosis","Renal tuberculosis","Medullary sponge kidney","Reflux nephropathy with calyceal clubbing"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT urography or intravenous urogram showing ring shadows and calyceal deformity","Urine microscopy and cytology, which may reveal sloughed papillary tissue","Urine culture to identify infection","Assessment for underlying disease including glucose or HbA1c, sickle screen and analgesic history","Renal function and electrolytes","Non-contrast CT to distinguish a sloughed papilla from a calculus"],"redFlags":["Sepsis from infection with obstruction by necrotic tissue","Acute kidney injury, especially with bilateral papillary involvement","Massive haematuria","Rapid deterioration in a solitary functioning kidney"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the number of papillae affected and control of the underlying causes; an isolated event with a single reversible cause may have little lasting impact, whereas recurrent necrosis, as in combined diabetes and analgesic use, can cause progressive chronic kidney disease.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a sloughed papilla for a calculus on imaging","Failing to look for multiple coexisting causes, such as diabetes together with analgesics","Overlooking analgesic overuse in the drug history","Missing an infected obstructed system caused by necrotic tissue"],"management":["Remove the precipitating insult: stop chronic analgesic and NSAID overuse, optimise glycaemic control in diabetes, and manage sickle cell disease to reduce medullary sickling","Treat superimposed infection promptly with appropriate antibiotics, including antituberculous therapy where tuberculosis is implicated","Provide adequate hydration and analgesia for loin pain and colic from passage of sloughed papillary fragments","Relieve acute ureteric obstruction from a sloughed papilla with ureteric stenting or percutaneous nephrostomy, and treat any obstructing urosepsis urgently","Monitor renal function and urine for progression, recurrent infection and further papillary sloughing","Refer to urology for obstruction or diagnostic uncertainty and to nephrology to preserve remaining renal function"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (Feehally et al.)","Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_tubular_acidosis":{"name":"Renal tubular acidosis","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RTA","distal RTA","type 1 RTA","proximal RTA","type 2 RTA","type 4 RTA","hyperkalaemic RTA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["RTA produces a normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic) metabolic acidosis despite relatively preserved glomerular filtration, distinguishing it from the high anion gap acidosis of advanced renal failure","Type 1 (distal) RTA reflects failure of the distal nephron to secrete hydrogen ions, so urine pH stays inappropriately high (typically above 5.5) in the face of systemic acidosis, and is usually accompanied by hypokalaemia","Type 2 (proximal) RTA arises from impaired proximal reabsorption of bicarbonate; once serum bicarbonate falls below the lowered threshold, distal acidification can still generate acidic urine","Type 4 RTA is a hyperkalaemic form driven by aldosterone deficiency or resistance, most commonly seen in diabetic kidney disease, and impairs both acid and potassium excretion","Nephrocalcinosis and calcium-containing stones are characteristic of distal RTA because of hypercalciuria, low urinary citrate, and alkaline urine","The urine anion gap is a useful bedside tool: a positive (or inappropriately non-negative) value in the presence of acidosis points to impaired renal ammonium excretion rather than gastrointestinal bicarbonate loss","Proximal RTA frequently coexists with generalised proximal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi syndrome), so glycosuria, phosphaturia, and aminoaciduria should be sought","Management principles centre on alkali replacement; distal RTA usually needs modest alkali plus potassium, while proximal RTA requires larger alkali loads and often potassium supplementation because bicarbonate therapy worsens kaliuresis"],"pathophysiology":"The renal tubular acidoses are disorders in which the kidney fails to appropriately acidify the urine or reclaim filtered bicarbonate despite normal or near-normal glomerular filtration, producing a hyperchloraemic (normal anion gap) metabolic acidosis. In distal (type 1) RTA the collecting duct cannot secrete hydrogen ions effectively, so net acid excretion falls and the urine cannot be maximally acidified. In proximal (type 2) RTA the proximal tubule cannot reabsorb the filtered bicarbonate load, leading to bicarbonate wasting until serum levels reach a new lower steady state. Type 4 RTA results from deficient or ineffective aldosterone action, reducing distal sodium reabsorption and potassium and hydrogen secretion, which produces hyperkalaemia and a mild acidosis. Potassium behaves differently across subtypes: distal and proximal forms tend toward hypokalaemia, whereas the aldosterone-related form causes hyperkalaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Diarrhoea and other gastrointestinal bicarbonate losses","Early or advanced chronic kidney disease with reduced ammoniagenesis","Ureteral diversion into bowel","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor or other drug effect","Dilutional acidosis from rapid saline infusion","Addison disease or hypoaldosteronism from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum electrolytes with calculation of the plasma anion gap to confirm a normal-gap acidosis","Simultaneous urine pH and urine electrolytes to calculate the urine anion gap and assess ammonium excretion","Serum potassium to help classify the subtype (low in types 1 and 2, high in type 4)","Urinalysis for glycosuria, proteinuria, and evidence of proximal tubular dysfunction","Renal imaging for nephrocalcinosis or stones, especially in distal RTA","Plasma renin and aldosterone when a hyperkalaemic (type 4) picture is suspected"],"redFlags":["Severe hypokalaemia causing muscle weakness, paralysis, or arrhythmia in distal or proximal RTA","Dangerous hyperkalaemia in type 4 RTA, particularly with concurrent potassium-retaining drugs","Growth failure, rickets, or osteomalacia in children and adults from chronic acidosis and phosphate loss","Recurrent stones or progressive nephrocalcinosis threatening renal function"],"prognosis":"With appropriate alkali and electrolyte correction most patients do well, and complications such as bone disease, stones, and growth impairment can be prevented or limited. Outcome depends heavily on the underlying cause; type 4 RTA linked to diabetic nephropathy tends to progress with the primary kidney disease, while inherited distal RTA requires lifelong therapy.","pitfalls":["Attributing a normal anion gap acidosis to diarrhoea without checking the urine anion gap or urine ammonium","Giving bicarbonate in proximal RTA without adequate potassium, precipitating worsening hypokalaemia","Overlooking type 4 RTA as a cause of unexplained hyperkalaemia in a diabetic patient","Failing to search for Fanconi syndrome features when proximal RTA is diagnosed","Assuming a high urine pH always indicates distal RTA without excluding urinary infection with urea-splitting organisms"],"management":["Correct the metabolic acidosis with oral alkali therapy, choosing potassium citrate or sodium bicarbonate according to subtype","In distal (type 1) RTA use potassium citrate to correct acidosis and hypokalaemia while reducing hypercalciuria, low citrate and stone formation","In proximal (type 2) RTA give abundant alkali replacement with potassium supplementation, and consider a thiazide to reduce bicarbonate wasting","In hyperkalaemic (type 4) RTA restrict dietary potassium, add a loop diuretic or fludrocortisone, and withdraw drugs that worsen hyperkalaemia such as renin-angiotensin blockers and potassium-sparing diuretics","Identify and treat any underlying cause such as autoimmune disease, drugs or obstructive uropathy","Monitor electrolytes and acid-base status, screen for nephrocalcinosis and stones, and follow growth in children"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"renovascular_disease":{"name":"Renovascular Disease","system":"Renal/Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Renal artery stenosis","Renovascular hypertension","Atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis","ARAS","Ischemic nephropathy"],"chapter":289,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2150","clinicalPearls":["Renovascular disease is a specifically treatable 'secondary' cause of hypertension because restoring renal perfusion pressure can reverse the pressor pathways it activates (Harrison 22e p.2150).","Roughly 90% of renovascular hypertension is due to atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis (ARAS), which is typically unilateral with a discrete lesion near the renal artery origin in older patients with a broader atherosclerotic risk profile (Harrison 22e p.2139, 2150).","Fibromuscular dysplasia (FMD) accounts for a minority of cases and characteristically affects younger patients aged ~15 to 50, predominantly women; lesions are more diffuse with an irregular 'sawtooth/string-of-beads' appearance located in the distal renal artery (Harrison 22e p.2139, 2150).","FMD should be suspected with abrupt-onset hypertension (especially in young women) or resistant/recently worsening hypertension; ARAS should be suspected in older patients with other ASCVD evidence or otherwise unexplained decline in kidney function (Harrison 22e p.2139).","Critical stenosis (usually >70 to 80% luminal obstruction) reduces perfusion pressure, activating the renin-angiotensin system, reducing sodium excretion, and engaging sympathetic pathways (Harrison 22e p.2150).","Early renovascular hypertension is angiotensin-dependent and marked by widely varying pressures, loss of circadian BP rhythm, and accelerated target organ injury including left ventricular hypertrophy and renal fibrosis (Harrison 22e p.2150).","An abdominal renal artery bruit, or carotid/femoral bruits, may be audible and supports the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2139, 2150).","When large-vessel disease primarily threatens kidney function the entity is termed ischemic nephropathy; gradually reduced flow can leave function reduced but stable for years, whereas advanced disease produces cortical hypoperfusion and overt tissue hypoxia (Harrison 22e p.2150).","ARAS commonly coexists with preexisting small-vessel disease from hypertension, aging, and diabetes; nearly 85% of patients considered for revascularization already have stage 3 to 5 CKD (eGFR <60) (Harrison 22e p.2150).","The presence of ARAS independently predicts cardiovascular morbidity and mortality regardless of whether revascularization is performed (Harrison 22e p.2150).","Atheroembolic renal disease most often follows arterial procedures (e.g., coronary angiography), with cholesterol crystals lodging in downstream microvessels; manifestations typically appear 1 to 14 days after the inciting event (Harrison 22e p.2152).","Thromboembolic renal disease can cause flank pain, fever, leukocytosis, nausea and vomiting, and a transient extreme rise in LDH if infarction occurs; bilateral involvement causes precipitous functional decline and reduced urine output (Harrison 22e p.2153)."],"pathophysiology":"The renal cortex receives high arteriolar flow in excess of its metabolic needs for filtration, while the postglomerular medullary circulation has lower flow yet high oxygen consumption, leaving the deeper medulla near the margin of hypoxemia. Large-vessel occlusive disease (atherosclerosis, fibromuscular dysplasia, dissection, extrinsic compression, aortic stent graft) reduces perfusion pressure to the kidney. Once stenosis is hemodynamically significant (typically >70 to 80% lumen occlusion), the drop in perfusion pressure activates the renin-angiotensin system, reduces sodium excretion, and stimulates sympathetic adrenergic pathways, producing angiotensin-dependent systemic hypertension. Sustained pressure and ischemia drive endothelial injury (reflected by increased urinary albumin excretion), microvascular proliferation in early atherosclerosis and later microvascular rarefaction in chronic ischemia, with consequent tubulointerstitial fibrosis and loss of function. In atheroembolic disease, cholesterol crystals from fractured plaque occlude microvessels leaving a characteristic 'cleft' on biopsy (Harrison 22e p.2150-2152).","additionalDifferentials":["Essential (primary) hypertension","Renal parenchymal disease","Primary aldosteronism","Pheochromocytoma","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis / arteriolonephrosclerosis","Atheroembolic renal disease","Thromboembolic renal disease / renal infarction"],"infectionMimics":["Renal infarction from septic emboli (e.g., subacute bacterial endocarditis vegetations)","Thrombotic renal occlusion precipitated by systemic infection such as COVID-19"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Renal parenchymal disease causing secondary hypertension","Primary aldosteronism","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","Cholesterol atheroembolism mimicking vasculitis (livedo reticularis, eosinophilia, elevated ESR, hypocomplementemia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex (Doppler) ultrasonography: peak systolic renal artery velocity >200 cm/s suggests >60% occlusion (some trials use >300 cm/s to reduce false positives); renal resistive index informs kidney viability but is operator-dependent","Contrast-enhanced CT angiography: excellent vascular images and assessment of accessory/distal vessels; stents do not cause artifacts; small contrast toxicity risk","Magnetic resonance angiography: non-nephrotoxic but gadolinium is avoided when eGFR <30 due to nephrogenic systemic fibrosis risk; cannot visualize stented vessels","Captopril renography (99mTc-MAG3): strong negative predictive value when entirely normal; limited utility with advanced atherosclerosis or creatinine >2.0 mg/dL","Intraarterial (digital subtraction) angiography: gold standard for large-vessel disease, often performed at time of planned intervention","Plasma renin activity is subject to timing, drugs, and sodium intake and does not reliably predict response to revascularization; renal venous renin sampling is now rarely used due to false results","Kidney biopsy: confirms atheroembolic disease (cholesterol-crystal cleft in microvessels)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or resistant hypertension failing optimal medical therapy","Progressive decline in GFR during BP treatment, or decline associated with ACE inhibitor/ARB therapy","Recurrent flash pulmonary edema / congestive heart failure not explained by LV dysfunction","Abrupt-onset hypertension in a young woman (suggests FMD) or worsening kidney function in an older patient with ASCVD (suggests ARAS)","New worsening hypertension and deteriorating renal function days after an arterial/angiographic procedure (atheroembolism), possibly reaching a malignant phase","Acute flank pain with fever and markedly elevated LDH suggesting renal infarction; anuria with bilateral acute occlusion"],"prognosis":"FMD patients are usually younger with otherwise normal vessels and long life expectancy and often respond well to angioplasty; FMD seldom threatens kidney function. Untreated ARAS progresses in nearly 50% of cases over 5 years, sometimes to total occlusion, though intensive BP and statin therapy slow progression. After endovascular stenting renal function recovers in only ~25%, is unchanged in ~50%, and deteriorates in the remainder. ARAS independently predicts cardiovascular events and death. Atheroembolic renal disease carries an ominous prognosis with 1-year mortality exceeding 38%; untreated malignant hypertension historically had 1-year mortality over 90% (and >50% at 6 to 12 months with target organ injury), but current 5-year survival exceeds 50% with effective antihypertensive therapy (Harrison 22e p.2150-2154).","pitfalls":["Plasma renin activity is transient and influenced by drugs/sodium/timing, so it does not reliably predict response to revascularization; over-reliance on it is misleading (Harrison 22e p.2151).","Multiple randomized trials show no compelling benefit of revascularization over optimal medical therapy for moderate ARAS regarding BP or renal function over 3 to 5 years; revascularization should not be assumed beneficial just because flow is restored (Harrison 22e p.2139, 2150).","Revascularization rarely normalizes BP alone because most patients have preexisting hypertension and need continued drug therapy (Harrison 22e p.2150).","Atheroembolic complications of revascularization can be catastrophic and accelerate the very hypertension and renal failure the procedure intended to prevent (Harrison 22e p.2151).","Atheroembolic renal disease is frequently underdiagnosed and is often a diagnosis of exclusion; systemic features (fever, abdominal pain, weight loss) are present in fewer than half of patients (Harrison 22e p.2152).","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis is often assigned without specific etiologic proof; nephrosclerosis changes also occur with aging, dyslipidemia, and glucose intolerance with only modest association with BP, and antihypertensive therapy does not alter its course (Harrison 22e p.2154)."],"management":["Control blood pressure with renin-angiotensin system blockade using an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker, monitoring renal function and potassium closely, especially in bilateral disease","Slow atherosclerotic progression with a statin, antiplatelet therapy and smoking cessation in atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis","Offer percutaneous transluminal angioplasty for fibromuscular dysplasia, which is often curative in younger patients","Reserve angioplasty with stenting for atherosclerotic stenosis in selected cases such as resistant hypertension, flash pulmonary oedema or progressive renal impairment","Add further antihypertensive agents as needed and manage overall cardiovascular risk","Monitor blood pressure, renal function and stent patency, and involve vascular and interventional specialists for revascularisation decisions"],"severityClassification":"No formal staging is given; severity is gauged by degree of luminal stenosis (hemodynamically critical at >70 to 80% occlusion), CKD stage (most revascularization candidates are stage 3 to 5 with eGFR <60), and clinical syndrome (stable medically managed disease versus rapidly progressive/resistant disease). Table 289-2 outlines clinical factors favoring revascularization plus medical therapy (progressive GFR decline, medical failure of BP control, GFR decline with ACEi/ARB, recurrent unexplained heart failure) versus medical therapy with surveillance (controlled BP with stable function, stable non-progressing stenosis, advanced age/limited life expectancy, high atheroembolic risk, concomitant parenchymal disease with proteinuria) (Harrison 22e p.2152).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2150","Harrison 22e p.2151","Harrison 22e p.2152","Harrison 22e p.2153","Harrison 22e p.2154","Harrison 22e p.2139"],"crossLinks":[]},"resistance_to_thyroid_hormone":{"name":"Resistance to thyroid hormone","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RTH","RTH-beta","THRB mutation","thyroid hormone resistance","Refetoff syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard endocrinology texts and thyroid hormone resistance literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by reduced tissue responsiveness to thyroid hormone, most commonly from mutations in the beta thyroid hormone receptor (THRB)","The paradoxical biochemistry is a raised free thyroxine and free triiodothyronine with a thyroid-stimulating hormone that is not suppressed (normal or elevated)","Usually autosomal dominant, so a family history of similar abnormal thyroid function tests is common and very helpful","Clinical features are variable and mixed, because tissues differ in their receptor dependence; goitre is common, and tachycardia can occur where beta-receptor-independent tissues remain responsive","Often discovered incidentally when thyroid function tests do not fit the usual patterns of hyper- or hypothyroidism","Most patients are clinically euthyroid overall and require no thyroid-directed treatment, as elevated hormone levels compensate for the resistance","The key differential is a thyroid-stimulating-hormone-secreting pituitary adenoma, which produces a similar biochemical picture but is a distinct disorder"],"pathophysiology":"Resistance to thyroid hormone usually results from dominant-negative mutations in the beta isoform of the thyroid hormone receptor. The mutant receptor binds hormone poorly yet still occupies target genes, interfering with normal receptor function. Because the pituitary and hypothalamus rely on the beta receptor for feedback, they sense hormone inadequately and continue to drive thyroid-stimulating hormone despite high circulating thyroid hormone. Peripheral tissues vary in their reliance on the affected receptor, so the same person can show features of both hormone excess and deficiency depending on the tissue.","additionalDifferentials":["Thyroid-stimulating-hormone-secreting pituitary adenoma","Assay interference or heterophile antibodies producing spurious results","Familial dysalbuminaemic hyperthyroxinaemia","Ordinary primary hyperthyroidism","MCT8 transporter defect","Non-thyroidal illness affecting thyroid function tests"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repeat and confirmatory thyroid function tests including free thyroxine, free triiodothyronine, and thyroid-stimulating hormone","THRB genetic testing and family screening","Alpha-subunit measurement and dynamic testing to exclude a pituitary adenoma","Pituitary magnetic resonance imaging when an adenoma is suspected","Assay interference checks such as alternative platforms or dilution studies","Markers of peripheral thyroid hormone action such as sex hormone binding globulin"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting a thyroid-stimulating-hormone-secreting adenoma such as a pituitary mass or visual field defect","Significant symptomatic tachycardia or arrhythmia","Inappropriate antithyroid or ablative treatment causing iatrogenic hypothyroidism","Severe or homozygous disease presenting in childhood with growth and developmental problems"],"prognosis":"Most people with resistance to thyroid hormone lead normal lives without specific treatment because compensatory high hormone levels maintain a euthyroid state; the main harm comes from misdiagnosis leading to unnecessary antithyroid therapy or thyroid ablation.","pitfalls":["Misreading the raised hormones with unsuppressed thyroid-stimulating hormone as ordinary hyperthyroidism and prescribing antithyroid drugs","Failing to distinguish the condition from a thyroid-stimulating-hormone-secreting pituitary adenoma","Ablating the thyroid and creating lifelong management difficulty","Not checking family members with similar unexplained results"],"management":["Recognise that most patients are compensated and clinically euthyroid and need no thyroid-directed treatment, avoiding inappropriate antithyroid drugs, radioiodine or thyroidectomy driven by misdiagnosis","Control symptomatic palpitations or tachycardia with a beta-blocker, preferably a non-selective agent that also limits peripheral thyroxine-to-triiodothyronine conversion","Consider a thyroid hormone analogue such as tiratricol for troublesome hormone-excess symptoms or goitre in selected cases","Distinguish resistance from a TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma before any intervention, since management differs entirely","Confirm the diagnosis with THRB genetic testing and offer genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal dominant inheritance","Refer to endocrinology for long-term follow-up, monitoring growth and cardiac status particularly in children"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Specialist reviews of resistance to thyroid hormone","Standard endocrinology texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"respiratory_acidosis":{"name":"Respiratory acidosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["respiratory acidosis","hypercapnic acidosis","CO2 retention","ventilatory failure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Respiratory acidosis results from alveolar hypoventilation, causing carbon dioxide retention and a fall in arterial pH","Arterial blood gases show a raised PaCO2 with low pH; bicarbonate rises as compensation develops","Acute and chronic forms are distinguished by the degree of renal (metabolic) compensation, with bicarbonate rising modestly in acute and substantially in chronic disease","Clinical features range from headache, drowsiness, and confusion to asterixis and, in severe hypercapnia, coma (CO2 narcosis)","Causes include central respiratory depression (drugs, CNS lesions), neuromuscular weakness, airway obstruction, and severe parenchymal or chest wall disease","Management targets the underlying cause and restoration of adequate alveolar ventilation, sometimes with non-invasive or invasive ventilation","In chronic CO2 retainers, excessive supplemental oxygen can blunt respiratory drive and worsen hypercapnia, so oxygen is titrated carefully","Correcting hypercapnia too rapidly risks post-hypercapnic metabolic alkalosis if the compensatory bicarbonate is not accounted for"],"pathophysiology":"Carbon dioxide is generated by metabolism and normally excreted by alveolar ventilation, keeping PaCO2 constant. When ventilation is inadequate relative to CO2 production, PaCO2 rises; through the carbonic acid equilibrium this increases hydrogen ion concentration and lowers pH. The body compensates in two phases: acutely, tissue and blood buffers slightly raise bicarbonate, while over days the kidneys enhance hydrogen ion excretion and bicarbonate reabsorption, producing a larger rise in bicarbonate that returns pH toward normal in chronic respiratory acidosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Metabolic alkalosis with compensatory hypoventilation","Mixed acid-base disorders","Acute versus chronic respiratory acidosis differentiation","Causes of acute hypercapnic respiratory failure (opioid overdose, acute severe asthma, COPD exacerbation)","Neuromuscular disorders such as Guillain-Barre syndrome or myasthenic crisis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas analysis to confirm hypercapnia and assess pH and bicarbonate","Assessment of the expected compensation to identify mixed disorders","Chest imaging and pulmonary function or bedside respiratory assessment to find the cause","Investigations for central, neuromuscular, or airway causes as clinically directed","Serum electrolytes to evaluate accompanying metabolic derangements"],"redFlags":["Depressed conscious level or CO2 narcosis indicating severe hypercapnia","Rapidly rising PaCO2 with falling pH suggesting impending ventilatory failure","Signs of exhaustion in a patient with increased work of breathing","Severe acidaemia causing haemodynamic instability"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause and its reversibility; acute respiratory acidosis from a rapidly correctable insult often resolves fully, whereas chronic hypercapnia reflects advanced or progressive disease, and acute-on-chronic decompensation can be life-threatening without prompt ventilatory support.","pitfalls":["Giving high-flow oxygen indiscriminately to a chronic CO2 retainer and precipitating worsening hypercapnia","Failing to recognise acute-on-chronic decompensation by misreading compensation","Missing a coexisting metabolic disorder in a mixed acid-base picture","Delaying ventilatory support in a tiring patient with rising PaCO2"],"management":["Treat the underlying cause and restore adequate alveolar ventilation as the central goal","Reverse respiratory depressants where responsible, using naloxone for opioids or flumazenil for benzodiazepines","Relieve airway obstruction with bronchodilators and corticosteroids in obstructive airways disease","Support ventilation with non-invasive ventilation for hypercapnic respiratory failure, escalating to invasive mechanical ventilation if consciousness declines or acidosis is refractory","Give controlled, titrated oxygen to correct hypoxaemia while avoiding worsening carbon dioxide retention","Monitor serial arterial blood gases to guide therapy and detect acute-on-chronic decompensation","Avoid sedatives and manage the chronic lung, neuromuscular or chest-wall disease predisposing to hypercapnia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"respiratory_bronchiolitis_ild":{"name":"Respiratory Bronchiolitis-Associated Interstitial Lung Disease","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RB-ILD","Respiratory bronchiolitis interstitial lung disease","Smoker's bronchiolitis with ILD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Respiratory medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A smoking-related interstitial lung disease occurring almost exclusively in current or former heavy cigarette smokers, usually presenting in mid-adult life.","Represents the symptomatic, clinically significant end of the spectrum of respiratory bronchiolitis, a near-universal incidental finding in smokers.","Typically presents with insidious cough and mild exertional dyspnoea, with fine crackles on auscultation and, uncommonly, clubbing.","It lies on a pathological continuum with desquamative interstitial pneumonia, both being smoking-related and sharing pigmented macrophage accumulation.","High-resolution computed tomography shows centrilobular nodules, ground-glass opacities and bronchial wall thickening, often with upper-zone-predominant centrilobular emphysema.","Histology demonstrates pigmented (tan-brown) macrophages accumulating within respiratory bronchioles and surrounding alveoli with mild peribronchiolar inflammation and fibrosis.","Smoking cessation is the primary treatment and can produce stabilisation or improvement; corticosteroids are used selectively with variable benefit.","Lung function typically shows a mixed obstructive-restrictive pattern with a reduced gas transfer factor."],"pathophysiology":"RB-ILD is driven by chronic exposure to cigarette smoke, which induces recruitment and activation of alveolar macrophages within and around the respiratory bronchioles. These macrophages accumulate finely granular golden-brown cytoplasmic pigment representing phagocytosed smoke-derived particulate material. The inhaled irritants provoke a low-grade inflammatory response in the terminal and respiratory bronchioles, with infiltration by lymphocytes and histiocytes, mild peribronchiolar fibrosis and extension of the process into adjacent alveolar ducts and alveoli. In respiratory bronchiolitis these changes are patchy and asymptomatic, but when the burden of macrophage accumulation and peribronchiolar inflammation is sufficient to impair gas exchange and cause symptoms, the disorder is designated RB-ILD. The condition overlaps mechanistically with desquamative interstitial pneumonia, in which the pigmented macrophage accumulation is more diffuse and alveolar-filling. Coexisting centrilobular emphysema and small-airway disease from the same smoking exposure contribute to the mixed physiological impairment. The dependence on tobacco exposure explains why disease often stabilises after cessation.","additionalDifferentials":["Desquamative interstitial pneumonia","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia","Usual interstitial pneumonia / idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with small-airway disease","Sarcoidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution computed tomography of the thorax for centrilobular nodules and ground-glass change","Pulmonary function tests including gas transfer measurement","Bronchoalveolar lavage demonstrating pigmented smoker's macrophages","Surgical or transbronchial lung biopsy when the diagnosis is uncertain","Detailed and quantified smoking history","Exclusion of relevant occupational or environmental antigen exposures","Serology to help exclude connective tissue disease-associated interstitial lung disease"],"redFlags":["Progressive dyspnoea or falling gas transfer despite smoking cessation","Imaging features suggesting established fibrosis or honeycombing, pointing to an alternative diagnosis","Marked or rapidly worsening hypoxaemia","Clinical or radiological features atypical for a smoking-related process","Development of pulmonary hypertension or cor pulmonale"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally favourable compared with fibrotic interstitial lung diseases; many patients stabilise or improve with smoking cessation, although some experience persistent symptoms or slow progression, and continued smoking is associated with worse outcomes.","pitfalls":["Overdiagnosing RB-ILD from incidental respiratory bronchiolitis in an asymptomatic smoker without clinical and physiological correlation.","Failing to make smoking cessation the central intervention and relying on corticosteroids instead.","Confusing the imaging with hypersensitivity pneumonitis and missing a relevant antigen exposure.","Not recognising coexisting emphysema, which alters the physiological interpretation.","Mistaking the ground-glass changes for a fibrotic interstitial pneumonia and giving an unduly poor prognosis."],"management":["Make smoking cessation the cornerstone of treatment with combined pharmacological and behavioural support, as it drives the disease and many patients stabilise or improve","Consider a trial of corticosteroids for persistent or progressive symptoms despite cessation, recognising the limited evidence base","Offer pulmonary rehabilitation and influenza and pneumococcal vaccination as supportive measures","Provide supplemental oxygen if significant hypoxaemia develops","Monitor symptoms, pulmonary function with gas transfer, and interval HRCT for progression","Manage within a specialist interstitial lung disease multidisciplinary team and reassess the diagnosis if progression is atypical"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of respiratory medicine (standard reference)","Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders (standard reference)","International interstitial pneumonia classification guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"respiratory_bronchiolitis_interstitial_lung_disease":{"name":"Respiratory bronchiolitis interstitial lung disease","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RB-ILD","Smoking-related interstitial lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A smoking-related interstitial lung disease occurring almost exclusively in current or heavy former smokers.","Forms part of a spectrum with desquamative interstitial pneumonia; asymptomatic respiratory bronchiolitis is a common incidental finding in smokers, whereas RB-ILD is the symptomatic form.","Presents with mild breathlessness and cough; crackles may be present but finger clubbing is uncommon.","HRCT shows centrilobular nodules, patchy ground-glass opacity and bronchial wall thickening, often upper-zone predominant, together with centrilobular emphysema.","Histology demonstrates pigmented 'smoker's' macrophages accumulating within respiratory bronchioles and surrounding alveoli with mild peribronchiolar fibrosis.","Bronchoalveolar lavage shows characteristic golden-brown pigmented macrophages.","Smoking cessation is the cornerstone of management and may lead to stabilisation or improvement.","Corticosteroids are reserved for progressive disease, where the supporting evidence is limited."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic inhalation of cigarette smoke provokes accumulation of pigment-laden macrophages within respiratory bronchioles and adjacent alveolar spaces, accompanied by mild bronchiolocentric inflammation and fibrosis. This bronchiolocentric distribution and the strong causal link to tobacco distinguish RB-ILD from the more diffuse alveolar macrophage filling of desquamative interstitial pneumonia, with which it overlaps.","additionalDifferentials":["Desquamative interstitial pneumonia","Subacute hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia","Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with emphysema","Early or subclinical idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed smoking history and HRCT thorax","Pulmonary function tests, which may show a mixed obstructive-restrictive pattern with reduced transfer factor","Bronchoalveolar lavage demonstrating pigmented macrophages","Exclusion of hypersensitivity pneumonitis with precipitins and exposure history","Surgical lung biopsy only if the diagnosis remains unclear after multidisciplinary review","Six-minute walk test with oximetry"],"redFlags":["Progressive decline despite smoking cessation, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Development of honeycombing suggesting an alternative fibrotic interstitial lung disease","Significant resting or exertional hypoxaemia","Radiological features raising concern for Langerhans cell histiocytosis or malignancy"],"prognosis":"The outlook is generally favourable, particularly with smoking cessation; most patients stabilise, although some have persistent symptoms and a minority progress.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing RB-ILD in a lifelong non-smoker without reconsidering the diagnosis","Not prioritising smoking cessation as the primary therapy","Confusing incidental respiratory bronchiolitis with symptomatic RB-ILD","Overtreating with immunosuppression when smoking cessation would suffice"],"management":["Prioritise complete smoking cessation with nicotine replacement or pharmacotherapy, as it is the primary and most effective intervention","Reserve a trial of corticosteroids for symptomatic or progressive disease persisting after stopping smoking","Add pulmonary rehabilitation, ambulatory oxygen for exertional hypoxaemia, and routine vaccination","Monitor with serial pulmonary function tests including transfer factor and interval HRCT to detect progression","Manage coexisting centrilobular emphysema and other smoking-related disease concurrently","Review within an interstitial lung disease multidisciplinary team, escalating to lung biopsy only if the diagnosis becomes unclear"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","ATS/ERS international multidisciplinary classification of the idiopathic interstitial pneumonias"],"crossLinks":[]},"restless_legs_syndrome":{"name":"Restless legs syndrome","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RLS","Willis-Ekbom disease","restless leg syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by an irresistible urge to move the legs, usually with uncomfortable sensations, that is partly or wholly relieved by movement","Symptoms worsen at rest and in the evening or night, disrupting sleep onset and contributing to daytime fatigue","Most patients also have periodic limb movements of sleep, which can be documented on polysomnography","Iron deficiency is a key and treatable contributor, so serum ferritin and transferrin saturation should be checked even when the haemoglobin is normal","Common secondary associations include pregnancy, chronic kidney disease and uraemia, and peripheral neuropathy","Certain drugs, including many antidepressants, antihistamines, and dopamine antagonists, can precipitate or aggravate symptoms","Treatment principles include iron repletion when stores are low, alpha-2-delta ligands, and dopaminergic agents, with attention to non-pharmacological measures","Long-term dopamine agonist use can lead to augmentation, a worsening and earlier onset of symptoms that requires careful management"],"pathophysiology":"Restless legs syndrome is a sensorimotor disorder linked to dysfunction of central dopaminergic transmission and to abnormal brain iron handling, since iron is a cofactor for dopamine synthesis. Reduced iron availability within the central nervous system, even with normal peripheral stores, is thought to impair dopaminergic signalling in circuits including the spinal cord and diencephalon. There is a strong genetic contribution, and it may be primary or secondary to iron deficiency, uraemia, pregnancy, or neuropathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Peripheral neuropathy and radiculopathy","Nocturnal leg cramps","Akathisia, particularly drug-induced","Peripheral vascular disease and venous insufficiency","Positional discomfort and habitual foot tapping","Periodic limb movement disorder without the sensory urge"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ferritin and transferrin saturation to assess iron status","Renal function tests to identify uraemia","Blood glucose and evaluation for peripheral neuropathy where clinically suspected","Review of medications that may provoke symptoms","Polysomnography in selected cases, particularly to document periodic limb movements or exclude other sleep disorders"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or asymmetric sensory or motor deficits suggesting a neuropathy or radiculopathy rather than primary RLS","Augmentation on dopaminergic therapy with earlier, more intense, and spreading symptoms","Symptoms secondary to unrecognised chronic kidney disease or severe iron deficiency","Severe sleep deprivation with significant mood disturbance or impaired daytime function"],"prognosis":"Primary restless legs syndrome is usually a chronic condition that can fluctuate over time, often worsening with age. Secondary forms may resolve when the underlying cause, such as iron deficiency or pregnancy, is corrected. Most patients achieve good symptom control with appropriate treatment, though augmentation can complicate long-term dopaminergic therapy.","pitfalls":["Failing to check and correct iron status, missing a readily treatable driver of symptoms","Overlooking culprit medications that provoke or worsen the syndrome","Relying on long-term dopamine agonists and precipitating augmentation","Confusing the condition with akathisia, cramps, or neuropathy and treating the wrong problem"],"management":["Check iron status and replace iron with oral or intravenous supplementation when ferritin or transferrin saturation is low, even with a normal haemoglobin","Review and withdraw aggravating drugs where possible, including many antidepressants, sedating antihistamines and dopamine antagonists","Advise sleep hygiene and reduced caffeine and alcohol intake as first-line non-pharmacological measures","For troublesome symptoms start an alpha-2-delta ligand such as gabapentin or pregabalin, often preferred to limit augmentation","Use a dopamine agonist such as pramipexole, ropinirole or rotigotine with caution, monitoring for augmentation and impulse-control disorders","Treat secondary contributors including uraemia and pregnancy-related symptoms","Reserve low-potency opioids for refractory cases or dopaminergic augmentation under specialist supervision"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"restrictive_cardiomyopathy":{"name":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RCM","restrictive heart disease","infiltrative cardiomyopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy is characterised by stiff, non-compliant ventricles causing impaired diastolic filling with preserved or near-preserved systolic function and normal or reduced ventricular cavity size early on","The dominant physiology is diastolic dysfunction with raised filling pressures, so patients present with signs of both right- and left-sided heart failure, prominent among them systemic venous congestion","Causes include infiltrative diseases such as amyloidosis, storage disorders, endomyocardial fibrosis, and post-radiation or idiopathic fibrosis; amyloidosis is a leading cause to actively exclude","A key clinical challenge is distinguishing restrictive cardiomyopathy from constrictive pericarditis, because the latter is potentially curable with surgery","Atrial enlargement is characteristic because the atria bear the burden of filling stiff ventricles, predisposing to atrial fibrillation","Multimodality imaging including echocardiography and cardiac MRI, sometimes with biopsy or specific tests for amyloid, is used to establish the cause","Management is largely directed at the underlying cause and at cautious control of congestion, since these patients are sensitive to changes in filling","Because the ventricles depend on adequate preload, excessive diuresis or loss of atrial contribution can precipitate a marked fall in output"],"pathophysiology":"In restrictive cardiomyopathy the ventricular myocardium or endocardium becomes abnormally stiff, either from infiltration by abnormal material (as in amyloidosis or storage disease), fibrosis (as in endomyocardial fibrosis or after radiation), or idiopathic processes. The stiff ventricle resists filling, so filling pressures rise sharply for only small increases in volume and most filling occurs rapidly in early diastole. This raises atrial and venous pressures, dilates the atria, and produces pulmonary and systemic congestion while systolic ejection is initially preserved. As disease advances, systolic function and conduction may also be affected, and the physiology can closely mimic constrictive pericarditis.","additionalDifferentials":["Constrictive pericarditis","Cardiac amyloidosis and other infiltrative diseases","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy in a non-dilated phase","Hypertensive heart disease with severe diastolic dysfunction","Right heart failure from other causes","Pericardial effusion with tamponade physiology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography demonstrating restrictive filling with biatrial enlargement and preserved cavity size","Cardiac MRI for tissue characterisation and to help identify infiltration","Specific evaluation for amyloidosis, including relevant imaging, laboratory testing, and sometimes biopsy","Invasive haemodynamic study when distinguishing restriction from constriction is difficult","12-lead ECG, which may show low voltages or conduction disease in infiltrative disease","Endomyocardial biopsy in selected cases to establish the underlying cause"],"redFlags":["Progressive heart failure refractory to therapy indicating advanced disease","Haemodynamic deterioration after aggressive diuresis due to preload dependence","Conduction disease or arrhythmia, particularly with infiltrative causes such as amyloidosis, raising the risk of syncope or sudden death","Features suggesting cardiac amyloidosis, which carries specific prognostic and treatment implications"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is largely determined by the underlying cause; idiopathic and infiltrative forms, especially advanced cardiac amyloidosis, often carry a poor outlook, whereas identifying a treatable cause or distinguishing curable constrictive pericarditis can markedly change the trajectory.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish restrictive cardiomyopathy from potentially curable constrictive pericarditis","Missing cardiac amyloidosis as an underlying cause","Over-diuresing a preload-dependent ventricle and precipitating a fall in output","Attributing raised filling pressures to systolic failure when systolic function is preserved"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying cause: tafamidis for transthyretin cardiac amyloidosis, plasma-cell-directed chemotherapy for AL amyloidosis, iron chelation or venesection for haemochromatosis, and corticosteroids for cardiac sarcoidosis or eosinophilic disease","Relieve systemic and pulmonary congestion with cautious diuretics, avoiding excessive preload reduction in these filling-dependent ventricles","Anticoagulate and control rate in atrial fibrillation, which is poorly tolerated given atrial-dependent ventricular filling","Actively exclude constrictive pericarditis, which is potentially curable by pericardiectomy","Manage progressive heart failure and refer for device therapy or cardiac transplantation in advanced disease","Offer genetic testing and family screening for hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis and familial forms","Coordinate care with cardiology and, where relevant, haematology or specialist amyloidosis services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"restrictive_dermopathy":{"name":"Restrictive dermopathy","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ZMPSTE24","LMNA","lethal restrictive dermopathy","tight skin contracture syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, typically lethal neonatal genodermatosis defined by rigid, taut, thin skin that severely restricts movement","The tight skin causes a fixed facial expression, small pinched nose, open O-shaped mouth, micrognathia and multiple joint contractures","Pulmonary hypoplasia and respiratory insufficiency from restricted chest movement are the usual cause of early death, often within days of birth","Most cases result from loss-of-function variants in ZMPSTE24, the enzyme that processes prelamin A into mature lamin A; some arise from LMNA variants","There is often a history of polyhydramnios, reduced fetal movements and premature birth","Skin may show erosions and a characteristic thin, tightly adherent epidermis on histology","Management is almost always palliative given the near-uniform lethality; genetic counselling is central for recurrence risk"],"pathophysiology":"Restrictive dermopathy is a laminopathy caused by failure of normal maturation of the nuclear lamina protein lamin A. Loss-of-function variants in ZMPSTE24, the zinc metalloprotease that cleaves the farnesylated prelamin A precursor, or certain LMNA variants, lead to accumulation of abnormal farnesylated prelamin A. This toxic precursor distorts nuclear architecture and impairs the function of skin and connective tissue cells, producing the rigid, hypoplastic dermis, taut skin, joint contractures and secondary pulmonary hypoplasia that characterise the lethal phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Neu-Laxova syndrome","Harlequin ichthyosis","Congenital ichthyoses with taut skin","Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita","Stiff skin syndrome","Other perinatally lethal contracture syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of ZMPSTE24 and LMNA","Skin biopsy with histology showing a thin dermis and abnormal epidermis","Chest imaging for pulmonary hypoplasia","Detailed dysmorphology examination and clinical photography","Placental and fetal assessment where a pregnancy history is available","Genetic counselling and consideration of prenatal testing in future pregnancies"],"redFlags":["Progressive respiratory failure from pulmonary hypoplasia and restricted chest expansion","Severe feeding difficulty from restricted facial and oral movement","Skin erosions with risk of infection and fluid loss","Any presentation is inherently critical given the near-uniform neonatal lethality"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is extremely poor: restrictive dermopathy is almost always lethal in the neonatal period, usually from respiratory insufficiency, so care is generally palliative and directed at comfort and family support.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the taut skin for a severe congenital ichthyosis such as harlequin ichthyosis","Pursuing aggressive intervention when the condition is near-uniformly lethal, rather than focusing on palliation","Overlooking the laminopathy basis and its implications for recurrence-risk counselling","Failing to arrange confirmatory genetics to guide future pregnancies"],"management":["Recognise the almost uniformly lethal neonatal course and centre care on palliation, comfort and family support rather than cure","Guide neonatal respiratory support decisions by the severe pulmonary hypoplasia, with careful goals-of-care discussion","Manage skin fragility and erosions with gentle emollients, meticulous wound care and infection prevention","Support feeding and provide analgesia given the restricted mouth, joint contractures and taut skin","Confirm the diagnosis with ZMPSTE24 and LMNA molecular testing","Offer genetic counselling and prenatal or preimplantation testing in future pregnancies given autosomal recessive inheritance","Involve neonatology, clinical genetics and palliative care in coordinated multidisciplinary support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: ZMPSTE24-Related Restrictive Dermopathy","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"retained_placenta":{"name":"Retained placenta","system":"Reproductive / Obstetrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["retained placenta","adherent placenta"],"chapter":null,"source":"RCOG Green-top Guideline / Williams Obstetrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Retained placenta is failure to deliver the placenta within a defined interval after birth, commonly thirty minutes with active management of the third stage or up to an hour with physiological management","Three broad mechanisms are recognised: a separated placenta trapped behind a closed cervix (trapped placenta), a placenta that fails to separate because of inadequate uterine contraction (placenta adherens), and a morbidly adherent placenta of the accreta spectrum","It is an important cause of primary postpartum haemorrhage, both because the uterus cannot contract effectively around retained tissue and because partial separation opens maternal sinuses","Risk factors include a previous retained placenta, previous uterine surgery or curettage, preterm delivery, placenta praevia, and grand multiparity; abnormal placentation should be considered when the uterus is scarred","Initial steps are to ensure the bladder is empty, encourage breastfeeding or nipple stimulation, give a uterotonic, and attempt controlled cord traction; injudicious traction risks cord avulsion or uterine inversion","If these measures fail, manual removal of the placenta under regional or general anaesthesia is required, with antibiotic cover and careful assessment for completeness","If the placenta cannot be separated and morbid adherence is evident, forced removal must be avoided; senior help and preparation for major haemorrhage and possible hysterectomy are needed","After delivery of the placenta, examine it for completeness, since retained fragments can cause ongoing or secondary haemorrhage and endometritis"],"pathophysiology":"Normal placental separation depends on brisk contraction and retraction of the myometrium after delivery of the fetus, which shears the placenta from the decidua basalis along the plane of the spongy layer and then closes off the underlying spiral arteries. Retention arises when this sequence fails. In placenta adherens, poor and often localised myometrial contraction prevents shearing, so the placenta remains attached. In a trapped placenta, the placenta separates but the cervix contracts down before expulsion, imprisoning it. In placenta accreta spectrum, deficient decidua, typically over a scar, allows chorionic villi to invade the myometrium so that no cleavage plane exists. Because retained tissue prevents the uterus from contracting fully, the exposed vascular placental bed continues to bleed, linking retained placenta closely with postpartum haemorrhage.","additionalDifferentials":["Placenta adherens due to uterine atony","Trapped or incarcerated placenta behind a constriction ring or closed cervix","Placenta accreta, increta, or percreta","A succenturiate lobe left in situ","Uterine inversion presenting as failure to deliver the placenta","A constriction ring or hourglass contraction from mismanaged third stage"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of blood loss, uterine tone, and maternal haemodynamic status","Full blood count and group-and-save or crossmatch, with coagulation studies if bleeding is heavy","Examination of the delivered placenta and membranes for completeness","Bedside ultrasound to assess for retained tissue and features suggesting abnormal placentation","Review of antenatal imaging for placenta accreta spectrum when the placenta will not separate over a scar","Observations and inflammatory markers if delayed retention presents with suspected endometritis"],"redFlags":["Heavy or continuing postpartum bleeding with the placenta still undelivered","Signs of hypovolaemic shock","Inability to separate the placenta with suspicion of accreta spectrum","Uterine inversion, with sudden pain, haemorrhage, and a mass at or beyond the introitus","Fever and offensive lochia suggesting infected retained tissue"],"prognosis":"With prompt uterotonics, bladder emptying, and timely manual removal, most cases resolve without lasting harm; delay increases the risk of major haemorrhage, and cases complicated by placenta accreta spectrum carry a significant risk of massive haemorrhage and hysterectomy.","pitfalls":["Applying vigorous cord traction against a poorly contracted uterus, causing cord avulsion or uterine inversion","Failing to empty the bladder, which impedes uterine contraction and placental delivery","Persisting with attempts at manual removal when placenta accreta is evident","Not inspecting the placenta for missing cotyledons or a succenturiate lobe","Underestimating cumulative blood loss and delaying escalation"],"management":["Begin with simple measures: empty the bladder, encourage breastfeeding or nipple stimulation, give a uterotonic such as oxytocin, and attempt gentle controlled cord traction, avoiding forceful traction that risks cord avulsion or uterine inversion","Perform manual removal of the placenta under regional or general anaesthesia when initial measures fail, checking the specimen for completeness","Give antibiotic prophylaxis at the time of manual removal","Manage associated postpartum haemorrhage with intravenous access, fluid resuscitation, uterotonics, tranexamic acid and crossmatched blood as needed","Suspect placenta accreta spectrum when the placenta will not separate over a uterine scar, and prepare for possible hysterectomy","Escalate promptly to senior obstetric and anaesthetic teams, with interventional radiology or specialist input for morbidly adherent placentation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["RCOG Green-top Guideline on postpartum haemorrhage","Williams Obstetrics","Oxford Handbook of Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"reticular_dysgenesis":{"name":"Reticular dysgenesis (AK2)","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AK2","adenylate kinase 2 deficiency","SCID with sensorineural deafness","reticular dysgenesis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification of inborn errors of immunity","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Represents the most severe form of severe combined immunodeficiency, presenting in the neonatal period with overwhelming infection and failure to thrive","The defining laboratory triad is profound congenital neutropenia (agranulocytosis) unresponsive to growth factor, severe lymphopenia and sensorineural deafness","Both myeloid and lymphoid arms fail, so there is combined loss of T and NK cells with impaired B-cell function alongside the agranulocytosis","Sensorineural hearing loss is a distinctive extra-haematopoietic feature that separates it from other forms of SCID","The bone marrow shows a maturation arrest of the granulocytic lineage with preserved erythroid and megakaryocytic lines","Inheritance is autosomal recessive, from biallelic loss-of-function variants in AK2 (adenylate kinase 2)","It is a paediatric emergency requiring protective isolation, avoidance of live vaccines and only irradiated, CMV-negative blood products","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is the only curative treatment and correction of the immune defect does not reverse the deafness"],"pathophysiology":"AK2 (adenylate kinase 2) is a mitochondrial enzyme that maintains the balance of adenine nucleotides in the intermembrane space and supports the energy metabolism needed for cell differentiation. Its absence causes energy failure and increased apoptosis in the leucocyte precursors that are especially dependent on this pathway, producing a maturation arrest of both the granulocytic and lymphoid lineages while erythroid and megakaryocytic development is largely spared. The consequence is congenital agranulocytosis combined with severe lymphopenia and defective adaptive immunity. AK2 is also required within the developing inner ear, which accounts for the associated sensorineural deafness.","additionalDifferentials":["Typical T-B-NK- or T-B+ severe combined immunodeficiency","Severe congenital neutropenia (e.g. ELANE, HAX1)","Kostmann syndrome","Cartilage-hair hypoplasia","Congenital infection with marrow suppression","Other mitochondrial disorders affecting haematopoiesis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and differential showing agranulocytosis, lymphopenia and preserved red cells and platelets","Bone marrow examination demonstrating granulocytic maturation arrest","Lymphocyte subsets confirming absent or very low T and NK cells","Serum immunoglobulins and, in neonates, TREC-based newborn screening abnormalities","Audiological assessment for sensorineural hearing loss","AK2 gene sequencing for biallelic loss-of-function variants"],"redFlags":["Neonatal sepsis or overwhelming infection in an infant with agranulocytosis and lymphopenia","Failure of neutrophil count to respond to granulocyte colony-stimulating factor","Signs of graft-versus-host disease after non-irradiated blood products","Complications of live vaccine administration in an undiagnosed infant","Progressive failure to thrive with intractable infection"],"prognosis":"Reticular dysgenesis is uniformly fatal in early infancy without treatment; allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, ideally performed early, can be curative for the immune and myeloid defect, although the sensorineural deafness persists.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the agranulocytosis for isolated severe congenital neutropenia and overlooking the lymphopenia","Giving live vaccines or non-irradiated blood products before the diagnosis is recognised","Failing to test hearing and missing the diagnostic association with deafness","Delaying referral for transplantation while pursuing infection control alone","Assuming successful transplantation will also correct the hearing loss"],"management":["Pursue early allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, the only curative treatment for the combined immune and myeloid failure","Protect against infection with strict isolation and broad antimicrobial prophylaxis covering bacteria, fungi, Pneumocystis and viruses while awaiting transplantation","Give immunoglobulin replacement, use only irradiated, CMV-negative, leucodepleted blood products, and avoid all live vaccines","Recognise that the agranulocytosis is unresponsive to granulocyte colony-stimulating factor","Provide audiological support including cochlear implantation, as the sensorineural deafness persists after transplantation","Offer genetic counselling, family screening and prenatal testing given autosomal recessive AK2 mutations, using newborn screening for early detection","Coordinate care through specialist paediatric immunology and transplant services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"reticulate_acropigmentation_of_kitamura":{"name":"Reticulate Acropigmentation of Kitamura","system":"Dermatology genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RAK","Acropigmentation of Kitamura"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant reticulate pigmentary genodermatosis caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in ADAM10.","Presents in the first or second decade with slightly depressed (atrophic) hyperpigmented macules in a net-like reticulate pattern on the dorsa of the hands and feet.","Interruption or breaks of the epidermal ridge pattern (dermatoglyphics) on the palms and soles, often with palmar pits, is a characteristic sign.","Pigmentation is initially acral but may extend proximally to the limbs and trunk with age, and lesions may darken with sun exposure.","Considered part of a clinical and mechanistic spectrum with Dowling-Degos disease, and some patients display features of both.","Histology shows epidermal atrophy with elongated rete ridges and increased basal-layer melanin, without melanocyte proliferation.","The condition is purely cutaneous, with no systemic, ocular, or neurological involvement, which helps separate it from X-linked reticulate pigmentary disorder.","Reported most often in Japanese populations but described worldwide."],"pathophysiology":"Reticulate acropigmentation of Kitamura is an autosomal dominant disorder of pigmentation caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in ADAM10, which encodes a disintegrin and metalloproteinase that acts as a cell-surface sheddase cleaving the ectodomains of numerous substrates, including Notch receptors and cadherins. ADAM10 contributes to keratinocyte adhesion and to the migration, dendricity, and function of melanocytes, so reduced ADAM10 activity is thought to disturb melanocyte-keratinocyte signalling and melanosome distribution, producing the characteristic reticulate deposition of melanin in the basal epidermis. Histologically there is thinning of the epidermis with elongation of the rete ridges and increased basal melanin but no increase in melanocyte number, and the disturbance of epidermal architecture underlies the breaks seen in the dermatoglyphic ridge pattern. ADAM10 lies within the same Notch-related pathway as keratin 5 (KRT5) and the glycosyltransferase and gamma-secretase genes (POFUT1, POGLUT1, PSENEN) implicated in Dowling-Degos disease, which explains the clinical and mechanistic overlap between the two conditions.","additionalDifferentials":["Dowling-Degos disease (flexural reticulate hyperpigmentation with comedo-like lesions and pitted scars).","Dyschromatosis symmetrica hereditaria (acropigmentation of Dohi) with mixed hyper- and hypopigmented macules on the dorsa of the hands and feet, due to ADAR1.","Dyschromatosis universalis hereditaria with generalised mottled hyper- and hypopigmentation.","Haber syndrome (rosacea-like facial eruption with reticulate pigmentation).","Naegeli-Franceschetti-Jadassohn syndrome and dermatopathia pigmentosa reticularis (KRT14) with reticulate pigmentation, absent dermatoglyphics, hypohidrosis, and dental or nail changes.","Acquired reticulate pigmentation from arsenic exposure or post-inflammatory change.","X-linked reticulate pigmentary disorder (POLA1), which adds systemic features."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with histopathology of a representative macule showing epidermal atrophy, elongated rete ridges, and basal hypermelanosis.","Dermoscopy of acral lesions and inspection of palmar dermatoglyphics for ridge interruption and pits.","Molecular genetic testing for ADAM10 variants.","Detailed family history and examination of relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance.","Examination of flexural sites to assess for overlap with Dowling-Degos disease.","Wood's lamp examination to delineate the extent of epidermal pigmentation.","Systemic and ocular review to exclude features suggesting an alternative reticulate disorder."],"redFlags":["Systemic features such as recurrent infections, chronic diarrhoea, or keratitis with photophobia suggest X-linked reticulate pigmentary disorder rather than Kitamura disease and warrant reappraisal.","Rapid change, ulceration, or nodularity within a pigmented lesion should prompt biopsy to exclude malignancy.","Onset in adulthood or a strikingly asymmetric distribution should raise suspicion of an acquired or exogenous cause such as arsenical pigmentation.","Associated hypohidrosis, nail dystrophy, or dental anomalies point towards an ectodermal dysplasia-type reticulate disorder."],"prognosis":"Reticulate acropigmentation of Kitamura is a benign, slowly progressive condition with no systemic consequences; pigmentation tends to increase and extend over years but represents only a cosmetic concern, and life expectancy is normal.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the condition as ordinary post-inflammatory or sun-induced pigmentation and missing the diagnostic breaks in palmar dermatoglyphics.","Failing to recognise overlap with Dowling-Degos disease, leading to incomplete counselling about the pigmentary spectrum.","Attributing systemic symptoms to Kitamura disease, which is purely cutaneous, so such features indicate a different diagnosis.","Expecting depigmenting therapies to be curative, when response is limited and the disorder is progressive."],"management":["Reassure that this is a benign, cosmetic condition with normal life expectancy and no systemic consequences","Advise photoprotection with sunscreen and sun avoidance, since lesions darken with ultraviolet exposure","Offer cosmetic camouflage and discuss topical agents such as retinoids or hydroquinone or laser therapy, noting their limited and variable benefit","Confirm the diagnosis where needed with skin biopsy and ADAM10 molecular testing, and assess for overlap with Dowling-Degos disease","Provide genetic counselling and examine relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance","Refer to dermatology for diagnosis and management of cosmetic concerns"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine","Andrews' Diseases of the Skin"],"crossLinks":[]},"retinal_artery_occlusion":{"name":"Retinal Artery Occlusion","system":"Ophthalmology / Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CRAO","Central retinal artery occlusion","BRAO","Eye stroke"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ophthalmology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as sudden, painless, monocular visual loss; central retinal artery occlusion is an ocular equivalent of acute ischaemic stroke and warrants emergency stroke-pathway referral.","Fundoscopy classically shows a pale, oedematous inner retina with a cherry-red spot at the fovea; the arteriolar blood column may appear segmented (box-carring).","A relative afferent pupillary defect is usually present in central retinal artery occlusion.","In older patients, giant cell arteritis must be actively excluded as an arteritic cause that threatens the fellow eye.","Most emboli arise from carotid atherosclerosis or a cardiac source; a bright Hollenhorst (cholesterol) plaque may be visible at a bifurcation in branch occlusion.","Amaurosis fugax (transient monocular visual loss) is a warning of impending occlusion and a transient-ischaemic-attack equivalent.","A cilioretinal artery, when present, may perfuse and spare central macular vision despite central retinal artery occlusion.","Time is critical, as inner retinal neurons tolerate ischaemia only briefly before irreversible infarction."],"pathophysiology":"The inner retina and retinal ganglion cells are supplied by the central retinal artery, a branch of the ophthalmic artery arising from the internal carotid artery, while the outer retina is nourished by the choroidal circulation. Acute occlusion, most often embolic (cholesterol, calcific or platelet-fibrin emboli), less commonly thrombotic, arteritic (giant cell arteritis) or vasospastic, produces abrupt ischaemia of the inner retinal layers. The ischaemic inner retina becomes oedematous and opacified, obscuring the underlying retinal pigment epithelium and choroid everywhere except the fovea, where the retina is thinnest and the intact choroidal blush shows through as the characteristic cherry-red spot. Because the choroid-dependent outer retina is relatively spared, some perception may persist. Branch occlusion produces a sectoral infarct and corresponding visual field defect. Prolonged ischaemia leads to irreversible neuronal death, later chronic inner retinal thinning, and occasionally neovascular sequelae.","additionalDifferentials":["Central retinal vein occlusion","Arteritic and non-arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy","Retinal detachment","Vitreous haemorrhage","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Ophthalmic or retinal migraine","Functional (non-organic) visual loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundoscopy and fundus photography","Optical coherence tomography (early inner retinal oedema, later inner retinal thinning)","Fluorescein angiography demonstrating delayed or absent retinal arterial filling","Urgent inflammatory markers and, where indicated, temporal artery biopsy to exclude giant cell arteritis","Carotid Doppler ultrasound and CT or MR angiography of the neck vessels","Transthoracic or transoesophageal echocardiography and ECG to seek an embolic cardiac source","Diffusion-weighted brain MRI, as concurrent clinically silent cerebral infarction is common"],"redFlags":["Any accompanying neurological deficit suggesting concurrent cerebral stroke","Symptoms or signs of giant cell arteritis (jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, new headache, a tender or pulseless temporal artery)","Preceding amaurosis fugax indicating an impending stroke","Bilateral or simultaneous involvement, suggesting giant cell arteritis or an embolic shower","Ocular neovascularisation or features of the ocular ischaemic syndrome"],"prognosis":"Spontaneous visual recovery in central retinal artery occlusion is generally poor, whereas branch occlusion carries a better outlook depending on macular involvement. It is an important marker of systemic vascular disease, conferring a substantially raised risk of subsequent stroke and other cardiovascular events.","pitfalls":["Missing giant cell arteritis and delaying corticosteroids while awaiting biopsy, thereby risking the fellow eye.","Managing it as an isolated eye problem rather than a stroke equivalent, and failing to refer urgently for vascular assessment.","Attributing sudden visual loss to migraine in an older patient.","Overlooking a carotid or cardiac embolic source on workup.","Misinterpreting foveal sparing by a cilioretinal artery and doubting the diagnosis."],"management":["Treat central retinal artery occlusion as an ocular stroke emergency with immediate referral to the acute stroke pathway, considering thrombolysis in hyperacute presentations at specialist centres","In the first hours attempt measures to dislodge the embolus and lower intraocular pressure, such as ocular massage, anterior chamber paracentesis and pressure-lowering agents, recognising limited evidence of benefit","Urgently exclude giant cell arteritis in older patients and, if suspected, start high-dose corticosteroids immediately to protect the fellow eye","Institute secondary prevention as for stroke with antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy, a statin, and control of blood pressure, diabetes and smoking","Investigate and treat the embolic source, including carotid endarterectomy for significant stenosis and management of any cardiac source","Monitor for ocular neovascularisation and treat with laser or anti-VEGF therapy if it develops","Coordinate care across stroke, ophthalmology and vascular services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ophthalmology references","Neuro-ophthalmology references","Stroke management guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"retinal_detachment":{"name":"Retinal detachment","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RD","detached retina","rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","RRD","tractional retinal detachment","exudative retinal detachment"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic warning symptoms are sudden floaters and flashing lights (photopsia) followed by a curtain or shadow spreading across the field of vision","Central vision is preserved until the macula detaches ('macula-on' versus 'macula-off'), which is the key prognostic and timing factor","The commonest type is rhegmatogenous, caused by a retinal tear that lets fluid pass under the retina; tractional and exudative types have different mechanisms","Risk factors include high myopia, previous cataract surgery, ocular trauma, lattice degeneration, and a detachment in the fellow eye","Fundoscopy shows an elevated, mobile, often corrugated grey retina; a relative afferent pupillary defect may be present in large detachments","Treatment is surgical (laser/cryo for tears, pneumatic retinopexy, scleral buckle, or vitrectomy) and is prioritised urgently when the macula is still attached","A posterior vitreous detachment with a fresh tear can be treated with prophylactic retinopexy to prevent progression"],"pathophysiology":"In rhegmatogenous retinal detachment, a full-thickness retinal break (usually from vitreous traction as the vitreous separates with age) allows liquefied vitreous to track through the tear into the potential space between the neurosensory retina and the retinal pigment epithelium, lifting the photoreceptor layer off its blood supply. Tractional detachment occurs when contracting fibrovascular membranes (as in proliferative diabetic retinopathy) pull the retina forward, and exudative detachment when fluid accumulates beneath an intact retina from inflammatory, vascular, or neoplastic processes. Separated photoreceptors become ischaemic and, if not reattached, degenerate.","additionalDifferentials":["Posterior vitreous detachment without tear","Vitreous haemorrhage","Retinoschisis","Choroidal detachment or mass","Central serous chorioretinopathy","Migraine with visual aura"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated indirect ophthalmoscopy with scleral indentation to locate breaks","Slit-lamp examination and assessment for pigment cells in the anterior vitreous (tobacco dust)","B-scan ultrasonography when the view is obscured by haemorrhage or cataract","Optical coherence tomography to determine macular status","Assessment of visual acuity and for a relative afferent pupillary defect"],"redFlags":["Sudden new floaters and flashes with a visual field defect — sight-threatening, refer urgently","A 'macula-on' detachment is a surgical emergency because prompt repair preserves central vision","Rapidly enlarging shadow or curtain indicating extension toward the macula","New symptoms in the fellow eye of a patient with previous detachment"],"prognosis":"Anatomical reattachment is achieved in most cases with modern surgery, but final visual outcome depends heavily on whether the macula was detached and for how long before repair.","pitfalls":["Dismissing floaters and flashes without a dilated peripheral retinal examination","Delaying referral in a macula-on detachment and allowing the macula to detach","Missing a small peripheral break because the retina was not examined with indentation","Attributing symptoms to migraine or vitreous floaters without excluding a tear"],"management":["Repair rhegmatogenous detachment surgically with pneumatic retinopexy, scleral buckling or pars plana vitrectomy, sealing breaks with laser or cryotherapy retinopexy","Prioritise urgent surgery while the macula is still attached, as macular status determines the visual prognosis","Treat tractional detachment, as in proliferative diabetic retinopathy, with vitrectomy and address the underlying retinopathy","Manage exudative detachment by treating the underlying inflammatory, vascular or neoplastic cause rather than by primary surgery","Apply prophylactic laser or cryotherapy to retinal tears and high-risk lesions such as lattice degeneration, including in the fellow eye","Advise post-operative posturing with intraocular gas tamponade and avoidance of air travel while gas remains in the eye","Monitor the fellow eye and educate the patient on the warning symptoms of floaters, flashes and a spreading visual-field shadow"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"retinal_macroaneurysm":{"name":"Retinal arterial macroaneurysm","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RAM","retinal macroaneurysm","arterial macroaneurysm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acquired focal dilatation of a retinal arteriole, usually within the first three orders of branching and often along the superotemporal arcade","Typically affects elderly, hypertensive patients and is more common in women","May be asymptomatic and found incidentally, or cause visual loss through exudative maculopathy or haemorrhage","The hallmark of a ruptured macroaneurysm is haemorrhage at multiple retinal levels simultaneously, which may be sub-retinal, intraretinal, sub-internal limiting membrane (pre-retinal) and into the vitreous","The exudative form produces macular oedema and a ring of hard exudate that can threaten the fovea","Fluorescein angiography shows a fusiform or round dilatation of the arteriole with variable leakage","Many lesions thrombose and involute spontaneously, so management ranges from observation to focal laser or intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy when the macula is threatened"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic hypertension and arteriosclerosis weaken the wall of a retinal arteriole, producing a focal saccular or fusiform dilatation. The abnormal vessel may leak plasma, causing macular oedema and hard exudate, or it may rupture, releasing blood that can dissect into several retinal planes at once, giving the characteristic multilevel haemorrhage. Some macroaneurysms undergo spontaneous thrombosis and sclerosis, involuting without treatment.","additionalDifferentials":["Neovascular (wet) age-related macular degeneration","Diabetic maculopathy","Branch retinal vein occlusion","Retinal capillary haemangioma","Coats disease and other retinal telangiectasia","Vitreous haemorrhage from another cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination to locate the arteriolar dilatation and characterise the haemorrhage","Optical coherence tomography to assess macular oedema and the level of haemorrhage","Fluorescein angiography to confirm the fusiform arteriolar dilatation and demonstrate leakage","Optical coherence tomography angiography or indocyanine green angiography when the view is obscured by blood","Blood pressure measurement and cardiovascular risk assessment","B-scan ultrasound if dense vitreous haemorrhage prevents fundus examination"],"redFlags":["Dense vitreous haemorrhage obscuring the fundus","Submacular haemorrhage threatening the fovea","Associated uncontrolled hypertension","Multiple or bilateral lesions suggesting significant systemic vascular disease"],"prognosis":"The outlook is often favourable, as many macroaneurysms involute spontaneously; the visual outcome depends chiefly on whether the fovea is involved by exudate or haemorrhage.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the lesion for wet age-related macular degeneration and committing to unnecessary long-term injections","Failing to identify and treat underlying hypertension","Misattributing an unexplained vitreous haemorrhage in an elderly patient","Over-treating a lesion that is already thrombosing and involuting"],"management":["Observe asymptomatic or non-foveal lesions, as many macroaneurysms thrombose and involute spontaneously","Control the underlying hypertension and cardiovascular risk factors that drive arteriolar wall damage","Treat sight-threatening exudative maculopathy with focal laser photocoagulation and/or intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy","Manage dense vitreous haemorrhage by observation or pars plana vitrectomy, and consider Nd:YAG membranotomy or pneumatic displacement for premacular haemorrhage","Monitor with optical coherence tomography and fundus examination for macular oedema and resolution","Refer to a medical retina specialist for treatment decisions and to address systemic vascular risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course: Retina and Vitreous"],"crossLinks":[]},"retinitis_pigmentosa":{"name":"Retinitis pigmentosa","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RP","rod-cone dystrophy","hereditary retinal dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of inherited retinal dystrophies in which rod photoreceptors degenerate first, so patients present with night blindness (nyctalopia) and progressive peripheral field loss","Field constriction advances to tunnel vision, with central acuity typically preserved until late unless the macula becomes involved","The classic fundus triad is bone-spicule pigmentation in the mid-periphery, waxy pallor of the optic disc, and attenuated retinal arterioles","It may be isolated or part of a syndrome, notably Usher syndrome (with sensorineural deafness) and Bardet-Biedl syndrome, so associated systemic features should be sought","Inheritance can be autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, or X-linked, which influences prognosis and genetic counselling","Common complications include posterior subcapsular cataract and cystoid macular oedema, both of which are treatable and can improve residual vision","Management focuses on genetic counselling, low-vision support, monitoring for treatable complications, and emerging gene-based therapies for specific mutations"],"pathophysiology":"Retinitis pigmentosa comprises inherited dystrophies caused by mutations in numerous genes essential to photoreceptor and retinal pigment epithelium function. Progressive apoptosis of rod photoreceptors, followed later by cones, leads to loss of peripheral and night vision that spreads centrally over time. Degenerating photoreceptors and migration of retinal pigment epithelial cells into the retina produce the characteristic bone-spicule pigmentation, while secondary changes cause arteriolar narrowing and optic disc pallor. The underlying genetic defect determines the pattern of inheritance and the rate of progression.","additionalDifferentials":["Cone-rod dystrophy","Choroideremia","Gyrate atrophy","Leber congenital amaurosis","Pigmentary retinopathy secondary to drug toxicity (e.g. phenothiazines), infection, or inflammation","Congenital stationary night blindness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electroretinography to demonstrate reduced or extinguished rod and later cone responses","Visual field testing to document peripheral constriction","Optical coherence tomography to assess the macula and detect cystoid macular oedema","Fundus autofluorescence imaging to map areas of degeneration","Genetic testing to identify the causative mutation and guide counselling and therapy","Systemic evaluation, including audiology, when a syndromic form is suspected"],"redFlags":["Sudden or accelerated central visual loss suggesting macular oedema or another superimposed problem","Associated hearing loss indicating Usher syndrome requiring multidisciplinary care","Systemic features pointing to a syndromic association needing broader evaluation","New symptoms inconsistent with the expected slow progression, prompting search for another cause"],"prognosis":"Retinitis pigmentosa is generally slowly progressive, and although many patients retain useful central vision into adulthood, most eventually experience severe peripheral field loss; prognosis varies with genotype and inheritance pattern.","pitfalls":["Overlooking treatable complications such as cystoid macular oedema and cataract that can improve vision","Failing to identify a syndromic association such as Usher or Bardet-Biedl syndrome","Not offering genetic counselling and testing to patients and at-risk relatives","Mistaking a pigmentary retinopathy from drug toxicity or prior inflammation for hereditary disease"],"management":["Offer gene-replacement therapy with voretigene neparvovec for confirmed biallelic RPE65-mediated disease","Treat cystoid macular oedema with a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor such as oral acetazolamide or topical dorzolamide, and remove visually significant posterior subcapsular cataract surgically","Provide low-vision rehabilitation, visual aids, and ultraviolet-protective tinted lenses","Confirm the genotype with genetic testing to guide prognosis, counselling and eligibility for targeted therapy","Screen for and manage syndromic associations, including audiology for Usher syndrome and evaluation for Bardet-Biedl syndrome","Offer genetic counselling and family screening according to the inheritance pattern","Monitor with serial visual fields, optical coherence tomography and electroretinography, and consider emerging options such as retinal prostheses in advanced disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"retinoblastoma":{"name":"Retinoblastoma","system":"Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RB","retinal blastoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common primary intraocular malignancy of childhood, usually diagnosed before 5 years of age.","Leukocoria (a white pupillary reflex, sometimes first noticed in photographs) is the most common presenting sign and warrants urgent ophthalmology referral.","Strabismus is the second most common presentation and should never be dismissed without excluding an intraocular cause.","Disease may be unilateral or bilateral; bilateral and multifocal tumours are almost always heritable.","Heritable retinoblastoma arises from germline mutation of the RB1 tumour suppressor gene and carries a lifelong risk of second malignancies, especially osteosarcoma.","Diagnosis is made clinically by an experienced ophthalmologist under examination, supported by imaging; biopsy is avoided because of the risk of tumour seeding.","Treatment aims to save life first, then preserve the eye and vision, using approaches such as chemotherapy, focal therapies, and enucleation for advanced disease.","Children with heritable disease and affected families need genetic counselling and long-term surveillance for new tumours and secondary cancers."],"pathophysiology":"Retinoblastoma results from biallelic inactivation of the RB1 tumour suppressor gene in developing retinal cells. In heritable disease one mutated allele is inherited or arises in the germline, and a second somatic hit inactivates the remaining allele, producing early, often bilateral, multifocal tumours (consistent with the two-hit hypothesis). Loss of functional RB protein removes control of the cell cycle at the G1-S checkpoint, driving unregulated proliferation of retinal precursor cells and tumour formation.","additionalDifferentials":["Coats disease","Persistent fetal vasculature (persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous)","Toxocariasis (ocular larva migrans)","Retinopathy of prematurity","Congenital cataract","Endophthalmitis or other causes of leukocoria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed dilated fundus examination under anaesthesia by an ocular oncology team","Ocular ultrasound and MRI of the orbits and brain","RB1 genetic testing and genetic counselling","Examination of the fellow eye and screening of at-risk relatives","Evaluation for metastatic or trilateral (pineal) disease when clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Leukocoria in any child requires urgent same-pathway ophthalmology referral to exclude retinoblastoma","New-onset strabismus without an obvious benign cause","Rapidly progressive proptosis or an inflamed, painful eye suggesting advanced or extraocular spread","Bilateral disease or a family history indicating heritable retinoblastoma","Signs of intracranial (trilateral) disease such as neurological deterioration"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and modern therapy, survival in high-resource settings exceeds 95 percent for intraocular disease; extraocular or metastatic spread markedly worsens outcome. Children with heritable disease remain at elevated lifelong risk of second malignancies.","pitfalls":["Dismissing leukocoria or a subtle white reflex noticed in photographs as artefact","Attributing strabismus solely to a benign squint without a dilated fundus examination","Delaying urgent referral, which can be the difference between saving and losing an eye or life","Overlooking the need for genetic testing and family screening in apparently unilateral disease"],"management":["Tailor treatment to laterality and stage, using focal therapy with laser or transpupillary thermotherapy, cryotherapy or plaque brachytherapy for small tumours","Use chemoreduction with systemic or intra-arterial ophthalmic-artery chemotherapy, adding intravitreal chemotherapy for vitreous seeding, to preserve the eye where possible","Perform enucleation for advanced unilateral disease not amenable to eye-preserving therapy, and avoid biopsy because of the risk of tumour seeding","Reserve external beam radiotherapy for selected cases given the heightened second-malignancy risk in heritable disease","Give adjuvant chemotherapy for high-risk histology or extraocular and metastatic spread","Undertake RB1 genetic testing with counselling and screening of at-risk relatives and the fellow eye","Manage through a specialist ocular oncology multidisciplinary team and maintain lifelong surveillance for second malignancies such as osteosarcoma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"retinoblastoma_hereditary":{"name":"Hereditary retinoblastoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RB1","heritable retinoblastoma","germline retinoblastoma","bilateral retinoblastoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest primary intraocular malignancy of childhood, usually presenting before the age of five with leukocoria or strabismus","The hereditary form is caused by a germline mutation in the RB1 tumour-suppressor gene and follows Knudson's two-hit model, with a second somatic hit initiating the tumour","Hereditary disease tends to be bilateral and multifocal and presents earlier than the sporadic, unilateral form","A germline RB1 mutation confers a substantial lifetime risk of second, non-ocular malignancies such as osteosarcoma, soft-tissue sarcoma and melanoma","The inheritance pattern is autosomal dominant with high penetrance, so genetic testing and counselling of families is essential","Diagnosis is clinical and radiological, and biopsy is avoided because of the risk of tumour seeding","Pinealoblastoma occurring with bilateral retinoblastoma is termed trilateral retinoblastoma and carries a poor prognosis"],"pathophysiology":"Retinoblastoma arises from developing retinal cells and is driven by biallelic inactivation of the RB1 tumour-suppressor gene, whose protein normally restrains cell-cycle progression. In hereditary disease one defective RB1 allele is present in every cell as a germline mutation (the first hit), and loss or mutation of the remaining functional allele within a retinal cell (the second hit) allows unregulated proliferation and tumour formation, in keeping with Knudson's two-hit hypothesis. Because the first hit is already present throughout the body, tumours tend to be bilateral and multifocal and to arise early, and the germline defect predisposes other tissues to malignancy later in life.","additionalDifferentials":["Coats disease with exudative retinal detachment","Persistent fetal vasculature (persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous)","Norrie disease and other causes of a retrolental mass","Toxocariasis and other inflammatory causes of leukocoria","Congenital cataract and retinopathy of prematurity","Astrocytic hamartoma of the retina"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination under anaesthesia with wide-field fundus imaging to map and stage tumours","Ocular ultrasound to demonstrate an intraocular mass with characteristic calcification","MRI of the orbits and brain to assess optic nerve, extraocular spread and pineal involvement","RB1 germline molecular genetic testing with counselling and cascade screening of relatives","Surveillance examinations of at-risk siblings and offspring from infancy"],"redFlags":["Leukocoria or new strabismus in a young child requiring urgent ophthalmology referral","A buphthalmic or painful red eye suggesting advanced intraocular disease","Signs of optic nerve or extraocular extension on imaging","New neurological symptoms or a pineal mass suggesting trilateral disease","Later bone pain or a mass in a survivor, raising concern for a second malignancy"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and modern treatment, survival from intraocular retinoblastoma is high in well-resourced settings, and eye-preserving therapy is often possible; however, patients with a germline RB1 mutation carry a lifelong increased risk of second malignancies, and extraocular or metastatic disease and trilateral retinoblastoma carry a much poorer prognosis.","pitfalls":["Delaying referral of leukocoria and allowing progression to advanced disease","Performing intraocular biopsy and risking tumour dissemination","Assuming a unilateral presentation excludes a germline mutation without genetic testing","Neglecting lifelong surveillance for second malignancies in germline carriers","Failing to screen and counsel at-risk relatives"],"management":["Preserve vision where possible with eye-conserving therapy: focal laser or thermotherapy, cryotherapy, plaque brachytherapy, and chemoreduction using systemic, intra-arterial or intravitreal chemotherapy for the typically bilateral, multifocal tumours","Reserve enucleation for eyes with advanced disease, avoiding diagnostic biopsy because of the risk of tumour seeding","Avoid external beam radiotherapy where feasible, as it markedly increases the risk of radiation-induced second malignancies in germline mutation carriers","Confirm the germline RB1 mutation and provide genetic counselling with cascade screening of relatives","Screen at-risk siblings and offspring from infancy with serial examinations under anaesthesia","Maintain lifelong surveillance for second, non-ocular malignancies such as osteosarcoma, soft-tissue sarcoma and melanoma","Coordinate care through an ocular oncology and clinical genetics multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"retinopathy_of_prematurity":{"name":"Retinopathy of prematurity","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ROP","retrolental fibroplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A proliferative retinal vascular disorder affecting premature, low-birthweight infants, and a leading preventable cause of childhood blindness","The principal risk factors are low gestational age, low birthweight, and prolonged or unregulated supplemental oxygen exposure","Screening of at-risk preterm infants according to gestational age and birthweight thresholds is essential because early disease is asymptomatic","Severity is described by zone (I to III, indicating how posterior the disease is), stage (1 to 5, from a demarcation line to total retinal detachment), and the presence of 'plus' disease (dilated, tortuous posterior vessels)","'Plus' disease and posterior (zone I) involvement are the key markers of aggressive disease needing treatment","Treatment of threshold or type 1 disease is with laser ablation of the avascular peripheral retina, with intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy used in selected cases","Advanced untreated disease progresses to tractional retinal detachment (stages 4 and 5) with severe visual loss","Survivors remain at increased lifelong risk of myopia, strabismus, amblyopia, and late retinal detachment, requiring ongoing follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"Retinopathy of prematurity results from disordered development of the retinal vasculature in the incompletely vascularised preterm retina. In an initial phase, the relatively hyperoxic extrauterine environment (worsened by supplemental oxygen) suppresses vascular growth factors and halts normal vessel growth, leaving peripheral avascular retina. As the metabolically active retina matures, the resulting hypoxia drives excessive vascular endothelial growth factor production and pathological neovascularisation at the junction of vascular and avascular retina. These new vessels can bleed and form fibrovascular tissue, exerting traction that leads to retinal detachment.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial exudative vitreoretinopathy","Persistent fetal vasculature (persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous)","Incontinentia pigmenti with retinal involvement","Coats disease","Congenital retinal fold or other developmental vitreoretinopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured screening dilated indirect ophthalmoscopy of at-risk preterm infants at defined intervals","Documentation of zone, stage, and presence of plus disease at each examination","Wide-field retinal imaging where available to aid documentation and telemedicine review","Longer-term assessment of refraction, ocular alignment, and amblyopia in survivors"],"redFlags":["Zone I disease or the presence of plus disease indicating aggressive, treatment-requiring retinopathy","Rapidly progressive posterior (aggressive) ROP that can advance to detachment quickly","Any signs of tractional retinal detachment (stage 4 or 5)","Missed or delayed screening in eligible infants, risking preventable blindness"],"prognosis":"Mild ROP frequently regresses spontaneously with a good visual outcome, but severe disease can progress to retinal detachment and blindness if untreated. Timely screening and treatment markedly reduce the risk of severe visual loss, though treated eyes and preterm survivors remain prone to refractive error, strabismus, and amblyopia.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen eligible preterm infants or examining them too late to intervene","Underestimating aggressive posterior disease that lacks the classic ridge appearance","Poor regulation of supplemental oxygen increasing the risk and severity of disease","Discharging treated infants without long-term follow-up for refractive and detachment risks"],"management":["Ablate the avascular peripheral retina with laser photocoagulation for type 1 (threshold) disease to halt the neovascular drive","Reserve intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy (such as bevacizumab or ranibizumab) for aggressive posterior or zone I disease, with vigilant follow-up for reactivation","Regulate supplemental oxygen to targeted saturation ranges and avoid unnecessary hyperoxia to limit ongoing vascular injury","Enrol at-risk preterm infants in structured screening dilated indirect ophthalmoscopy at intervals set by gestational age and birthweight","Refer for vitreoretinal surgery (scleral buckling or vitrectomy) when tractional retinal detachment develops","Provide long-term surveillance for refractive error, strabismus and amblyopia in treated eyes and preterm survivors","Coordinate care between neonatology and ophthalmology and counsel parents on the visual prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"retractile_mesenteritis":{"name":"Retractile Mesenteritis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sclerosing mesenteritis","Mesenteric panniculitis","Mesenteric lipodystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Gastroenterology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Retractile mesenteritis represents the fibrotic, end-stage variant of a spectrum of idiopathic mesenteric inflammation; the earlier fat-predominant form is termed mesenteric panniculitis and the fat-necrosis-predominant form mesenteric lipodystrophy.","It preferentially involves the root of the small-bowel mesentery, typically sparing the retroperitoneum, and shows a striking male predominance in later middle age.","Presentation is often insidious with vague abdominal pain, a palpable mass, altered bowel habit, weight loss or a low-grade febrile illness, though some cases are found incidentally on imaging.","CT is the pivotal investigation: a soft-tissue mesenteric mass with the 'fat-ring sign' (preserved fat halo around vessels and nodes) and a hyperattenuating 'tumoural pseudocapsule' are characteristic.","The retractile subtype is defined by dense collagenous fibrosis that tethers and kinks bowel loops, producing bowel obstruction, ischaemia or venous/lymphatic compromise.","An association with IgG4-related disease, prior abdominal surgery or trauma, and occult malignancy (notably lymphoma) is recognised, mandating a search for an underlying cause.","Diagnosis is one of exclusion confirmed histologically, showing fat necrosis, chronic inflammation and mature fibrosis; the fibrotic burden distinguishes retractile mesenteritis from milder panniculitis.","Management is guided by symptom severity: asymptomatic disease is observed, while symptomatic or progressive fibrosis is treated with immunosuppression, with surgery reserved for obstructive complications."],"pathophysiology":"Retractile mesenteritis is an idiopathic, chronic non-neoplastic disorder characterised by a self-limiting or progressive inflammatory-fibrotic process centred on the adipose tissue of the mesentery, most often the small-bowel mesenteric root. It is conceptualised as the sclerotic end of a continuum that begins with mesenteric panniculitis (chronic inflammation of mesenteric fat), passes through mesenteric lipodystrophy (predominant fat necrosis with foamy macrophage infiltration), and culminates in the retractile form dominated by dense collagen deposition. The initiating insult is uncertain, but proposed triggers include prior abdominal surgery or trauma, mesenteric ischaemia, autoimmunity and a paraneoplastic response; a subset falls within the IgG4-related disease spectrum, showing storiform fibrosis, obliterative phlebitis and IgG4-rich plasma-cell infiltrates. As the process matures, myofibroblast proliferation and progressive collagenisation cause tissue contraction that retracts and tethers the mesentery, kinking bowel loops and encasing mesenteric vessels, lymphatics and nodes. This retraction underlies the clinical hallmarks of bowel obstruction, protein-losing enteropathy, chylous ascites and, occasionally, bowel or vascular ischaemia from encasement of the superior mesenteric vessels.","additionalDifferentials":["Mesenteric lymphoma or other lymphoproliferative disease","Carcinoid tumour with desmoplastic mesenteric reaction","Peritoneal carcinomatosis or desmoplastic metastatic deposits","Desmoid tumour (mesenteric fibromatosis)","Mesenteric tuberculosis or other granulomatous mesenteric infection","Retroperitoneal fibrosis extending anteriorly","IgG4-related sclerosing disease at other sites"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen to assess the fat-ring sign, pseudocapsule and vascular encasement","MRI to characterise the degree of fibrosis versus active inflammation","Image-guided or surgical biopsy for histological confirmation and exclusion of malignancy","Serum IgG4 subclass and immunoglobulin profile to assess for IgG4-related disease","Inflammatory markers and full blood count to gauge systemic inflammatory activity","18F-FDG PET to evaluate metabolic activity and screen for underlying lymphoma or malignancy","Nutritional and protein assessment where protein-losing enteropathy or chylous ascites is suspected"],"redFlags":["Features of bowel obstruction or mesenteric ischaemia from fibrotic bowel tethering or vascular encasement","Rapid mass enlargement, lymphadenopathy or constitutional symptoms suggesting underlying lymphoma or malignancy","Development of chylous ascites or protein-losing enteropathy indicating lymphatic compromise","Signs of superior mesenteric vein thrombosis or bowel infarction","Systemic multi-site sclerosis suggesting progressive IgG4-related disease"],"prognosis":"Most cases follow a benign, indolent or self-limiting course, and asymptomatic disease often remains stable or regresses on surveillance. The retractile fibrotic subtype carries a less favourable outlook because progressive contraction can cause bowel obstruction, ischaemia and nutritional compromise, though survival remains good when an underlying malignancy is excluded.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the CT appearance for lymphoma or carcinomatosis and either over-treating or delaying appropriate biopsy","Attributing all mesenteric fat stranding to mesenteritis when it reflects incidental panniculitis or an adjacent inflammatory process","Failing to search for occult malignancy, particularly lymphoma, which can present with an identical desmoplastic mesenteric reaction","Overlooking an IgG4-related aetiology that would respond to targeted immunosuppression","Proceeding to extensive resection when the tethered mesentery renders surgery hazardous and symptoms could be managed medically"],"management":["Reserve treatment for symptomatic or progressive disease and manage the active inflammatory phase with corticosteroids","Add steroid-sparing agents such as tamoxifen, azathioprine or colchicine for corticosteroid-refractory or relapsing fibrosis","Treat disease within the IgG4-related spectrum with corticosteroids, reserving rituximab for resistant cases","Manage asymptomatic, incidentally discovered disease with clinical and cross-sectional imaging surveillance","Reserve surgical bypass or resection for established bowel obstruction, ischaemia or intractable disease","Investigate for and exclude an underlying malignancy, particularly lymphoma, before labelling the disease idiopathic","Provide nutritional support and analgesia for weight loss and chronic abdominal pain"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gastroenterology and abdominal imaging references","Peer-reviewed reviews on sclerosing mesenteritis","IgG4-related disease consensus guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"retractile_testis":{"name":"Retractile testis","system":"Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["retractile testicle"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric urology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A testis that intermittently withdraws from the scrotum into the groin due to an overactive cremasteric reflex, but can be brought down and stays in the scrotum","It is a normal variant in prepubertal boys and must be distinguished from a truly undescended (cryptorchid) testis","The key clinical feature is that the testis, once manipulated into the scrotum, remains there without tension until the reflex is re-triggered","The cremasteric reflex is most active in mid-childhood, when retractile testes are commonest","A minority become ascending (acquired undescended) testes over time, so periodic review is advised","No surgery is needed for a genuinely retractile testis, but an ascending testis requires orchidopexy"],"pathophysiology":"A retractile testis results from a brisk cremasteric reflex that pulls a normally descended testis up toward the external ring, particularly with cold or handling. Because the spermatic cord is of adequate length, the testis can be drawn back into and remain in the scrotum, distinguishing it from cryptorchidism. In some boys relative shortening of the cord with growth converts a retractile testis into an ascending (acquired undescended) testis, which no longer stays down.","additionalDifferentials":["Undescended (cryptorchid) testis","Ascending (acquired undescended) testis","Ectopic testis","Absent (vanishing) testis","Gliding testis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful examination in a warm, relaxed setting to overcome the cremasteric reflex","Confirmation that the testis stays in the scrotum after manipulation","Serial follow-up to detect an ascending testis","No routine imaging required","Referral if the testis cannot be kept in the scrotum"],"redFlags":["A testis that will not stay in the scrotum (undescended/ascending)","Testicular asymmetry or atrophy","Associated hypospadias or ambiguous genitalia","Loss of a previously scrotal testis on follow-up"],"prognosis":"A truly retractile testis is benign and typically resolves at puberty, but the small risk of progression to an ascending testis warrants monitoring.","pitfalls":["Labelling an undescended or ascending testis as merely retractile","Examining in a cold or anxious setting and misjudging position","Failing to arrange follow-up for possible ascent","Reassuring without confirming the testis stays down"],"management":["Reassure that a genuinely retractile testis is a normal variant needing no surgery","Confirm the diagnosis by examining in a warm, relaxed setting and demonstrating that the testis stays in the scrotum after manipulation","Arrange periodic clinical review through childhood to detect conversion to an ascending (acquired undescended) testis","Refer for orchidopexy if the testis can no longer be maintained in the scrotum","Avoid routine imaging, which does not aid management","Reassess around puberty, when the cremasteric reflex wanes and retraction typically resolves"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric urology references (standard reference)","Paediatric surgery reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"retrocalcaneal_bursitis":{"name":"Retrocalcaneal bursitis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["retrocalcaneal bursopathy","posterior heel bursitis","Haglund-associated bursitis","subtendinous calcaneal bursitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Posterior heel pain from inflammation of the bursa between the calcaneus and the Achilles tendon insertion","Often associated with insertional Achilles tendinopathy and with a prominent posterosuperior calcaneal process (Haglund deformity)","Tenderness is anterior to the Achilles tendon on either side of its insertion, distinguishing it from mid-portion tendinopathy","Aggravated by ankle dorsiflexion and by footwear pressure on the heel counter","Can occur in inflammatory arthropathies such as the spondyloarthritides, so consider systemic causes in bilateral or atypical presentations","Ultrasound or MRI shows bursal fluid and any associated insertional tendinopathy or bony prominence","Management is load and footwear modification, heel lift or shoe changes to reduce pressure, and physiotherapy; corticosteroid injection is used cautiously given proximity to the Achilles and rupture risk"],"pathophysiology":"The retrocalcaneal bursa sits between the posterosuperior calcaneus and the Achilles tendon. Repetitive compression and friction during ankle dorsiflexion, often aggravated by a prominent posterosuperior calcaneal tuberosity (Haglund deformity) and by rigid heel counters, inflame the bursa. It commonly coexists with insertional Achilles tendinopathy, the two forming part of a posterior heel overload picture. Inflammatory arthropathies such as the spondyloarthritides can also produce a genuine inflammatory bursitis and enthesitis at this site.","additionalDifferentials":["Insertional Achilles tendinopathy","Mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy","Haglund deformity","Calcaneal stress fracture","Sever's disease (calcaneal apophysitis in children)","Spondyloarthropathy-related enthesitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound to show bursal fluid and assess the Achilles insertion","MRI to characterise bursitis, insertional tendinopathy and bone oedema","Lateral radiograph to assess a Haglund prominence and insertional calcification","Inflammatory markers and HLA-B27 with rheumatological review if spondyloarthropathy is suspected","Clinical localisation of tenderness anterior to the tendon"],"redFlags":["Features of inflammatory arthropathy such as bilateral heel pain, inflammatory back pain or other enthesitis","Sudden posterior pain and weakness suggesting Achilles rupture","Focal bone pain suggesting a calcaneal stress fracture","Systemic upset suggesting infection"],"prognosis":"Usually improves with footwear and load modification and rehabilitation; cases linked to a Haglund deformity or insertional tendinopathy can be persistent and occasionally require surgery.","pitfalls":["Corticosteroid injection near the Achilles with its attendant rupture risk","Confusing bursitis with mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy and mistreating it","Missing an underlying spondyloarthropathy","Overlooking a calcaneal stress fracture"],"management":["Modify activity and load, reducing aggravating ankle dorsiflexion and impact to allow the bursa to settle","Adjust footwear to remove pressure from a rigid heel counter and add a heel lift to offload the insertion","Undertake structured physiotherapy with Achilles stretching and eccentric loading for coexisting insertional tendinopathy","Trial simple analgesia or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain, using intrabursal corticosteroid only with caution given the risk of Achilles rupture","Screen for spondyloarthritis in bilateral or atypical disease and refer for rheumatological assessment and disease-modifying therapy","Consider surgery, such as calcaneal osteotomy for a Haglund deformity with bursal excision or debridement, for refractory cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","British Journal of Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"retrograde_ejaculation":{"name":"Retrograde ejaculation","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["backward ejaculation","retrograde ejaculation into the bladder","dry orgasm from retrograde flow"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / WHO laboratory manual for semen analysis","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Men describe orgasm with little or no antegrade ejaculate, and the first urine passed afterwards is characteristically cloudy from admixed semen","The diagnosis is confirmed by finding sperm in a post-orgasmic urine specimen, distinguishing it from failure of emission where no semen is produced at all","The common causes are diabetic autonomic neuropathy, bladder-neck or prostate surgery, retroperitoneal surgery, and alpha-blocker medication","It is a recognised and treatable cause of low-volume ejaculate and of male-factor infertility","Alkalinisation and appropriate preparation of the post-orgasmic urine allow sperm to be harvested for assisted reproduction when medical treatment fails","Medical treatment aims to restore bladder-neck closure using sympathomimetic or alpha-agonist agents, most useful when the cause is neuropathic rather than anatomical","Anatomical causes following bladder-neck resection generally do not respond to drugs, so assisted conception is planned from the outset when fertility is the goal"],"pathophysiology":"Normal ejaculation depends on coordinated closure of the bladder neck at the moment of emission so that semen is propelled forward through the urethra rather than back into the bladder. When the internal urethral sphincter fails to close, seminal fluid takes the path of least resistance and flows retrogradely into the bladder. This failure may be neurogenic, as in diabetic autonomic neuropathy or after retroperitoneal lymph-node dissection that interrupts sympathetic outflow; anatomical, following transurethral or open bladder-neck and prostate surgery that destroys the sphincter mechanism; or pharmacological, notably with alpha-adrenergic blocking drugs that relax the bladder neck. The result is a reduced or absent antegrade ejaculate with sperm recoverable from the post-orgasmic urine.","additionalDifferentials":["Failure of seminal emission (anejaculation)","Ejaculatory duct obstruction producing low ejaculate volume","Congenital absence of the vasa and seminal vesicles","Hypogonadism reducing ejaculate volume","Androgen-deficiency-related reduction in ejaculate","Psychogenic ejaculatory dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Post-orgasmic urine analysis for the presence of sperm, the confirmatory test","Standard semen analysis assessing ejaculate volume and sperm content","Blood glucose or HbA1c to identify diabetes","Medication review focusing on alpha-blockers and other implicated drugs","Surgical and neurological history including bladder-neck, prostate, and retroperitoneal procedures","Scrotal or transrectal ultrasound where an anatomical or obstructive cause is also considered"],"redFlags":["New retrograde ejaculation as an early clue to undiagnosed diabetes","Recent retroperitoneal or pelvic surgery with additional neurological deficits","Associated bladder or voiding symptoms suggesting broader outflow pathology","Complete absence of orgasmic sensation pointing to a different neurological problem"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and does not threaten general health; fertility is frequently achievable, either by restoring antegrade ejaculation with medication in neuropathic cases or by retrieving sperm from post-orgasmic urine for assisted reproduction when the cause is anatomical.","pitfalls":["Confusing retrograde ejaculation with true anejaculation by not examining post-orgasmic urine","Attempting drug treatment for an anatomical bladder-neck defect that will not respond","Missing underlying diabetes as the driver of a neuropathic cause","Overlooking a culprit alpha-blocker that could simply be adjusted","Failing to alkalinise and prepare urine promptly, reducing the yield of viable sperm"],"management":["Restore bladder-neck closure pharmacologically with sympathomimetic or alpha-adrenergic agonist agents such as pseudoephedrine or imipramine, most effective when the cause is neuropathic","Withdraw or substitute causative drugs, particularly alpha-adrenergic blockers, where feasible","Optimise glycaemic control in diabetic autonomic neuropathy to limit progression","When medical therapy fails, harvest sperm from alkalinised, appropriately prepared post-orgasmic urine for assisted reproduction","Refer to reproductive medicine for assisted conception (intrauterine insemination or IVF with ICSI) when fertility is desired","Reassure that the condition is benign and does not threaten general health"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","European Association of Urology guidelines on sexual and reproductive health","WHO laboratory manual for the examination and processing of human semen"],"crossLinks":[]},"retronychia":{"name":"Retronychia","system":"Dermatology / Nail","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["proximal nail ingrowing","retronychia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Retronychia is proximal ingrowing of the nail, in which the plate is driven backwards into the proximal nail fold rather than into the lateral folds","It typically affects the great toenail and presents with proximal pain, swelling, redness, and chronic inflammation of the proximal fold","Recurrent granulation tissue and discharge from beneath the proximal fold are characteristic, and it is often misdiagnosed as simple chronic paronychia","A key sign is the arrest of normal distal nail growth, with newer nail plates stacking up behind the leading plate","It usually follows trauma or repetitive microtrauma that disrupts the normal attachment and forward growth of the plate","Onychoscopy and imaging such as ultrasound can support the diagnosis by demonstrating the embedded proximal plate and stacked nails","The definitive treatment is avulsion of the affected nail plate, which relieves the inflammation and allows a new, correctly aligned nail to grow"],"pathophysiology":"Retronychia arises when the longitudinal growth of the nail plate is interrupted, usually after trauma, so that the plate loses its normal forward advance and is instead pushed proximally into the tissue of the proximal nail fold. Continued matrix activity produces new plates behind the disconnected leading nail, and these become stacked one beneath another, embedding into the proximal fold. The buried plate acts as a foreign body, provoking persistent inflammation, granulation tissue, and discharge, which explains why the condition mimics and is frequently confused with chronic paronychia.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic paronychia","Onychocryptosis (lateral ingrowing toenail)","Pyogenic granuloma of the nail fold","Acute bacterial paronychia or felon","Subungual or periungual tumour","Nail changes of psoriasis affecting the proximal fold"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment for proximal fold inflammation with arrested distal nail growth","Onychoscopy to detect stacked nail plates and proximal embedding","Ultrasound to demonstrate multiple overlapping plates when the diagnosis is unclear","Swab of any discharge if secondary bacterial infection is suspected","History of preceding trauma or repetitive microtrauma","Review for coexisting nail disease or predisposing footwear factors"],"redFlags":["Persistent proximal fold inflammation unresponsive to treatment for presumed paronychia","Signs of spreading soft-tissue infection requiring prompt intervention","Atypical granulation or a mass suggesting an underlying tumour","Recurrent disease after treatment indicating incomplete removal or ongoing trauma"],"prognosis":"Retronychia responds well to nail plate avulsion, after which inflammation settles and a normally growing nail usually returns, although recovery takes months and recurrence can occur if predisposing trauma continues.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating retronychia as chronic paronychia with antimicrobials or topical agents alone","Overlooking the diagnostic clue of arrested distal nail growth","Delaying avulsion, which prolongs the inflammation unnecessarily","Failing to address ongoing trauma or footwear factors, leading to recurrence"],"management":["Perform nail plate avulsion to remove the embedded, backwardly displaced plate, the definitive treatment","Apply topical or intralesional corticosteroids to settle proximal fold inflammation and granulation tissue","Trial conservative measures such as taping and potent topical corticosteroid in early or mild disease before resorting to avulsion","Swab and treat any secondary bacterial infection with appropriate antibiotics","Advise on protective footwear and avoidance of repetitive microtrauma to prevent recurrence","Counsel that regrowth of a normal nail takes months and arrange review to detect recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"retroperitoneal_fibrosis":{"name":"Retroperitoneal fibrosis","system":"Rheumatology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ormond disease","Ormond's disease","chronic periaortitis","idiopathic retroperitoneal fibrosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fibro-inflammatory process in the retroperitoneum that encases the aorta, iliac vessels, and ureters, most often around the infrarenal aorta","Presents insidiously with poorly localised back, flank, or abdominal pain, constitutional symptoms, and raised inflammatory markers","Ureteric entrapment causes obstructive uropathy and may present with renal impairment or anuria","A substantial proportion falls within the spectrum of IgG4-related disease, so serum IgG4 and histology for IgG4-positive plasma cells are informative","Secondary causes include malignancy, certain drugs, radiotherapy, infection, and prior surgery, which must be excluded before labelling it idiopathic","Cross-sectional imaging shows a periaortic soft-tissue rind that characteristically does not displace the aorta anteriorly, unlike lymphoma","First-line treatment is systemic corticosteroids, often with a steroid-sparing agent, alongside relief of ureteric obstruction"],"pathophysiology":"Retroperitoneal fibrosis is characterised by a chronic inflammatory and fibrotic plaque in the retroperitoneum, frequently centred on the abdominal aorta (chronic periaortitis). Many idiopathic cases represent a manifestation of IgG4-related disease, with an immune-mediated infiltrate of lymphocytes, plasma cells, and eosinophils driving progressive collagen deposition. An exaggerated inflammatory response to atherosclerotic aortic antigens has also been proposed. The expanding fibrous tissue encases and constricts adjacent structures, most importantly the ureters, producing obstructive uropathy and hydronephrosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Retroperitoneal lymphoma or metastatic malignancy","IgG4-related disease at other sites","Erdheim-Chester disease","Retroperitoneal sarcoma","Infective (tuberculous or fungal) retroperitoneal disease","Drug-induced or radiation-induced fibrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT or MRI of the abdomen and pelvis to characterise the periaortic mass and detect ureteric involvement and hydronephrosis","Renal function tests and imaging to assess obstructive uropathy","Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP), typically elevated","Serum IgG4 and total immunoglobulins to assess for IgG4-related disease","Image-guided biopsy in atypical cases to exclude malignancy and confirm IgG4-rich fibrosis","PET imaging to assess disease activity and exclude a malignant or infective cause"],"redFlags":["Acute kidney injury or anuria from bilateral ureteric obstruction","Features suggesting underlying malignancy such as marked weight loss or atypical imaging","Anterior displacement of the aorta on imaging raising concern for lymphoma","Vascular compromise from encasement of major arteries or veins"],"prognosis":"Idiopathic and IgG4-related retroperitoneal fibrosis generally responds well to corticosteroids with regression of the mass and relief of obstruction, though relapse is common and long-term monitoring of renal function is needed; malignancy-associated disease carries the prognosis of the underlying tumour.","pitfalls":["Labelling the disease idiopathic without excluding malignancy, drugs, and infection","Missing silent bilateral ureteric obstruction and progressive renal failure","Overlooking a diagnosis of IgG4-related disease and its multi-organ implications","Assuming a periaortic mass is inflammatory when imaging features suggest lymphoma","Relieving obstruction without treating the underlying inflammatory process"],"management":["Induce remission with corticosteroids, the mainstay for idiopathic and IgG4-related disease, achieving regression of the periaortic mass","Add steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as methotrexate, mycophenolate, azathioprine or tamoxifen, or rituximab, for relapsing or refractory disease","Relieve ureteric obstruction promptly with ureteric stenting or percutaneous nephrostomy to protect renal function","Exclude and treat secondary causes including malignancy, drugs, radiotherapy and infection before labelling the disease idiopathic","Consider ureterolysis or surgical release for entrapped ureters that do not respond to medical therapy","Monitor renal function, inflammatory markers and serial imaging for relapse during and after treatment","Coordinate care between rheumatology, nephrology and urology for combined medical and interventional management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"retropharyngeal_abscess":{"name":"Retropharyngeal abscess","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RPA","retropharyngeal space abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A deep neck space infection with pus collecting between the posterior pharyngeal wall and the prevertebral fascia, most often in young children","The acute form in children usually follows suppuration of retropharyngeal lymph nodes after an upper respiratory infection, whereas a chronic form can result from tuberculous disease of the cervical spine","Typical features are fever, dysphagia, drooling, refusal of feeds, a muffled cry, neck stiffness, and the child holding the neck extended","Examination may show a bulge on one side of the posterior pharyngeal wall, and stridor or respiratory difficulty signals airway threat","A lateral soft-tissue neck radiograph classically shows widening of the prevertebral soft tissue, and contrast computed tomography defines the abscess and its extent","Management combines securing the airway, intravenous antibiotics, and surgical drainage of the collection, with drainage usually via the transoral route in the acute form","The infection can spread inferiorly into the mediastinum along fascial planes, which is a dangerous complication","Chronic (tuberculous) retropharyngeal abscess presents more insidiously and is drained by an external approach rather than transorally"],"pathophysiology":"The retropharyngeal space lies between the buccopharyngeal fascia covering the pharynx and the prevertebral fascia, and contains lymph nodes that drain the nasopharynx, adenoids, and posterior nasal structures. In young children an upper respiratory infection can seed these nodes, which suppurate to form an acute abscess; these nodes largely regress with age, making the acute form rare in adults, where trauma or foreign body is more often responsible. The chronic form arises from caries of the cervical vertebrae (Pott disease), where tuberculous pus tracks forward into the space. Because the space communicates inferiorly with the mediastinum, infection can descend to cause mediastinitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Peritonsillar abscess","Parapharyngeal abscess","Epiglottitis","Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis)","Prevertebral or vertebral tuberculosis without abscess","Retropharyngeal tumour or lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lateral soft-tissue neck radiograph to detect prevertebral soft-tissue widening","Contrast-enhanced computed tomography of the neck to confirm the abscess and assess spread toward the mediastinum","Full blood count and inflammatory markers to gauge severity","Blood cultures and pus culture at drainage to guide antibiotic therapy","Investigations for tuberculosis and spinal imaging when a chronic abscess is suspected"],"redFlags":["Stridor, drooling, or respiratory distress indicating airway compromise that demands urgent airway management","Rapid spread with signs of descending mediastinitis such as chest pain and sepsis","Torticollis with neurological signs suggesting cervical spine involvement","Sepsis or vascular complications such as jugular vein thrombosis"],"prognosis":"With prompt airway protection, appropriate antibiotics, and timely surgical drainage the acute abscess resolves well in most children; delay can lead to airway obstruction, aspiration on rupture, or life-threatening mediastinitis, and the chronic tuberculous form requires prolonged antitubercular treatment alongside drainage.","pitfalls":["Failing to anticipate and secure a threatened airway before other interventions","Attempting transoral drainage of a chronic tuberculous abscess instead of an external approach","Missing downward spread into the mediastinum","Overlooking underlying cervical spine tuberculosis in an insidious presentation"],"management":["Secure and protect the airway as the immediate priority, with intubation or, rarely, tracheostomy if the airway is threatened","Start empirical broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics covering streptococci, staphylococci and oral anaerobes, such as a beta-lactam with beta-lactamase inhibitor or clindamycin","Undertake surgical drainage of the collection, usually by the transoral route in the acute form","De-escalate to targeted antibiotic therapy guided by pus and blood culture results","Provide supportive care with intravenous fluids and analgesia during the acute illness","Monitor for complications, particularly descending mediastinitis, aspiration on rupture and airway obstruction","Investigate for and treat tuberculosis with antitubercular therapy when a chronic cold abscess from cervical spine disease is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"rett_syndrome":{"name":"Rett syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MECP2","RTT","methyl-CpG-binding protein 2 disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"ILAE classification / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic Rett syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the X-linked MECP2 gene and occurs almost exclusively in girls","There is a period of apparently normal early development followed by regression, typically between six and eighteen months of age","Loss of purposeful hand use with characteristic midline hand stereotypies such as wringing and washing movements is a defining feature","Regression brings loss of acquired language, gait abnormalities or loss of ambulation, and deceleration of head growth causing acquired microcephaly","Epilepsy develops in a large proportion of patients, alongside breathing dysregulation, autonomic disturbance and scoliosis","Diagnosis rests on established clinical criteria supported by MECP2 genetic testing","Care is multidisciplinary and supportive, targeting seizures, feeding, orthopaedic and respiratory complications"],"pathophysiology":"Rett syndrome is caused by loss-of-function variants in MECP2, which encodes methyl-CpG-binding protein 2, a transcriptional regulator essential for mature neuronal function and synaptic maintenance. MeCP2 deficiency does not cause neurodegeneration but arrests and disrupts postnatal synaptic development and network refinement, so early milestones are attained and then lost. X-linkage and X-inactivation explain the female predominance and clinical variability, while the widespread neuronal dysfunction underlies the epilepsy, movement, breathing and autonomic manifestations.","additionalDifferentials":["CDKL5 deficiency disorder","FOXG1-related disorder (congenital Rett variant)","Autism spectrum disorder with regression","Angelman syndrome","Other developmental and epileptic encephalopathies","Neurodegenerative and metabolic disorders with regression"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MECP2 sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis, with wider gene testing if negative","EEG, which commonly shows epileptiform abnormalities and background slowing","Brain MRI to exclude alternative structural pathology","Assessment of growth and head circumference over time","Multidisciplinary evaluation of feeding, breathing, spine and development"],"redFlags":["Developmental regression with loss of purposeful hand skills and language","Deceleration of head growth producing acquired microcephaly","New or worsening seizures superimposed on stereotyped movements","Progressive scoliosis or feeding and respiratory compromise"],"prognosis":"Rett syndrome causes lifelong severe disability with variable epilepsy, motor and communication impairment, but many individuals survive into adulthood with supportive care. Life expectancy is somewhat reduced, with cardiorespiratory complications and, less commonly, sudden death contributing to mortality.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early regression for idiopathic autism and delaying MECP2 testing","Assuming a normal MECP2 result excludes a Rett-like disorder such as CDKL5 or FOXG1","Overtreating stereotypies as seizures without EEG correlation","Neglecting scoliosis, feeding and breathing complications that drive morbidity"],"management":["Offer trofinetide, the approved disease-specific therapy, to reduce core symptom burden where available","Control epilepsy with anti-seizure medication tailored to the seizure type","Manage breathing dysregulation and autonomic disturbance, and monitor for QT prolongation with periodic ECG","Provide multidisciplinary physiotherapy, occupational therapy and orthotics, and surveil for scoliosis with bracing or spinal surgery as needed","Support feeding and nutrition, treating dysphagia and gastro-oesophageal reflux, with gastrostomy where required","Facilitate communication through augmentative and alternative communication and eye-gaze technology","Provide genetic counselling on the MECP2 variant and recurrence risk, with family testing","Monitor bone health given the increased fracture risk and coordinate lifelong supportive care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ILAE classification of the epilepsies","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"reversible_cerebral_vasoconstriction_syndrome":{"name":"Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RCVS","Call-Fleming syndrome","thunderclap headache with reversible vasospasm","postpartum angiopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / cerebrovascular headache literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hallmark is recurrent thunderclap headache, often over days to weeks, sometimes with focal deficits or seizures","Angiography shows multifocal segmental narrowing with a beaded appearance that resolves within about three months","Frequently precipitated by vasoactive drugs, cannabis, sympathomimetics, serotonergic agents, or the postpartum state","Complications include cortical subarachnoid haemorrhage, ischaemic stroke, and reversible posterior leukoencephalopathy","Cerebrospinal fluid is usually normal or near-normal, helping distinguish it from CNS vasculitis","Removal of triggers and calcium-channel blockers are the mainstays, with corticosteroids generally avoided","The reversibility of arterial narrowing on repeat imaging is central to confirming the diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome is a transient disturbance of cerebral arterial tone in which dysregulation of vascular smooth muscle produces multifocal segmental vasoconstriction of medium and large cerebral arteries. The abnormal vasomotor control, often provoked by vasoactive substances, hormonal changes, or the postpartum state, causes waves of constriction and dilatation that account for the recurrent thunderclap headaches. Impaired perfusion can lead to ischaemic infarction, while vessel wall stress and reperfusion contribute to cortical subarachnoid haemorrhage and posterior reversible encephalopathy; the vasoconstriction characteristically resolves over weeks to a few months.","additionalDifferentials":["Aneurysmal subarachnoid haemorrhage","Primary angiitis of the central nervous system","Cervical or intracranial arterial dissection","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Pituitary apoplexy","Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT or MR angiography demonstrating multifocal segmental narrowing, repeated to show reversibility","Non-contrast CT and MRI to detect cortical subarachnoid haemorrhage, infarction, or oedema","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis to help exclude vasculitis and subarachnoid haemorrhage","Review of medications and drug screen for vasoactive precipitants","Digital subtraction angiography in selected cases when non-invasive imaging is inconclusive"],"redFlags":["Recurrent thunderclap headaches, which mandate exclusion of subarachnoid haemorrhage","New focal neurological deficit or seizure suggesting stroke or oedema","Cortical subarachnoid haemorrhage on imaging","Persistent or worsening narrowing that raises concern for true vasculitis"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover fully within days to weeks as the vasoconstriction resolves, but a minority suffer permanent deficits from stroke or haemorrhage, and recurrence is uncommon but possible.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as CNS vasculitis and giving prolonged immunosuppression","Missing an underlying vasoactive drug or postpartum trigger","Failing to repeat vascular imaging to confirm reversibility","Using corticosteroids, which may worsen outcomes in this condition"],"management":["Remove and avoid precipitants, including vasoactive drugs, cannabis, sympathomimetics and serotonergic agents","Treat with a calcium-channel blocker such as nimodipine or verapamil to relieve headache and vasospasm","Avoid corticosteroids, which are associated with worse outcomes","Provide analgesia, rest and blood-pressure control during the symptomatic phase","Monitor for and manage complications including cortical subarachnoid haemorrhage, ischaemic stroke and posterior reversible encephalopathy","Repeat vascular imaging to confirm resolution of the vasoconstriction over the ensuing months"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"reversible_infantile_respiratory_chain_deficiency":{"name":"Reversible infantile respiratory chain deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Mitochondrial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Reversible infantile mitochondrial myopathy","Benign infantile mitochondrial myopathy","Reversible infantile cytochrome c oxidase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mitochondrial medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distinctive, spontaneously reversible infantile mitochondrial myopathy most often caused by a homoplasmic point variant in the mitochondrial transfer-RNA-glutamate gene (MT-TE).","It presents in the first weeks to months with profound hypotonia, weakness, feeding difficulty and often respiratory failure requiring ventilatory support, accompanied by lactic acidosis.","Despite a severe, sometimes ventilator-dependent early course, affected infants improve spontaneously, typically from the middle of the first year, and many reach normal or near-normal motor function.","Early muscle biopsy shows severe cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV) deficiency with ragged-red and COX-negative fibres and mitochondrial proliferation, all of which resolve on later biopsy.","Correctly identifying the genotype is crucial because it predicts recovery and should prevent premature withdrawal of intensive or ventilatory support.","A related nuclear-gene disorder, TRMU deficiency, causes a reversible infantile hepatopathy and liver failure; both conditions involve modification or function of mitochondrial transfer-RNA-glutamate.","It must be distinguished from fatal infantile COX-deficiency myopathy (for example SCO2-related disease), which is clinically similar early but non-reversible.","The reversibility is attributed to a developmental increase in expression and aminoacylation of mitochondrial transfer-RNA-glutamate that compensates for the variant over time."],"pathophysiology":"The disorder is usually due to a homoplasmic point mutation in MT-TE, the gene encoding mitochondrial transfer-RNA-glutamate. Impaired transfer-RNA function limits mitochondrial protein synthesis, so respiratory-chain complexes containing mtDNA-encoded subunits, most conspicuously cytochrome c oxidase, are severely deficient in skeletal muscle during infancy, producing weakness, hypotonia and lactic acidosis. Uniquely, the biochemical defect is transient: a proposed developmental up-regulation of transfer-RNA-glutamate expression, together with improved aminoacylation as the infant matures, progressively restores mitochondrial translation and complex IV activity, mirroring the clinical recovery. In the related TRMU disorder, deficient 2-thiouridylation of mitochondrial transfer RNAs, with cysteine availability acting as a limiting factor, impairs translation chiefly in the liver, again with the capacity for recovery.","additionalDifferentials":["Fatal infantile mitochondrial myopathy with COX deficiency (for example SCO2-related, non-reversible)","Spinal muscular atrophy type 1","Congenital myopathies and congenital muscular dystrophies","Congenital myasthenic syndromes and transient neonatal myasthenia","Other mitochondrial DNA or transfer-RNA disorders and depletion syndromes","Pompe disease and other metabolic myopathies","Neonatal sepsis or hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy causing hypotonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial blood lactate and CSF lactate monitoring over time","Muscle biopsy with COX and succinate dehydrogenase histochemistry and respiratory-chain enzymology","Targeted mtDNA testing for the MT-TE transfer-RNA-glutamate variant","Nuclear gene testing including TRMU where hepatopathy predominates","Serial neurophysiology and respiratory function testing as strength recovers","Liver function tests for the TRMU-related hepatic phenotype","Echocardiography and a general mitochondrial disease screen to exclude mimics"],"redFlags":["Neonatal respiratory failure with severe hypotonia and lactic acidosis","Feeding failure with aspiration risk","Deterioration or failure to improve beyond the expected recovery window, prompting review of the diagnosis","Coexisting acute liver failure suggesting the TRMU phenotype"],"prognosis":"With supportive intensive care through the early critical phase the muscle disorder typically resolves and most children achieve normal or near-normal function; recognising the genotype avoids inappropriate withdrawal of life support.","pitfalls":["Withdrawing ventilatory support prematurely by mistaking it for a fatal mitochondrial myopathy.","Failing to test for the MT-TE variant when an infant has a severe but potentially reversible COX-deficient myopathy.","Assuming a single early biopsy reflects the permanent state, when the defect normalises over time.","Confusing the myopathic (MT-TE) and hepatic (TRMU) reversible phenotypes.","Overlooking the role of cysteine as a limiting factor in the TRMU-related hepatopathy."],"management":["Provide intensive supportive care, including mechanical ventilation, through the early critical phase, recognising the disorder is self-limiting","Confirm the MT-TE genotype so recovery can be predicted and premature withdrawal of ventilatory support avoided","Correct and monitor lactic acidosis with supportive measures and serial blood and CSF lactate","Support nutrition and feeding during the period of profound weakness","Provide physiotherapy and rehabilitation as strength recovers, with serial neurophysiology and respiratory function testing","In the related TRMU disorder, manage the hepatopathy supportively, with cysteine supplementation as proposed","Offer genetic counselling covering the maternally inherited mitochondrial variant or the autosomal recessive TRMU form"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mitochondrial medicine reference texts","Neuromuscular disorder reference texts","Inborn errors of metabolism reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"revesz_syndrome":{"name":"Revesz syndrome","system":"Haematological / multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Revesz-Debuse syndrome","Dyskeratosis congenita, Revesz variant"],"chapter":null,"source":"Telomere biology disorder references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare, severe multisystem telomere biology disorder at the severe end of the dyskeratosis congenita spectrum, overlapping clinically with Hoyeraal-Hreidarsson syndrome.","Most cases are caused by heterozygous, frequently de novo, TINF2 variants affecting the shelterin protein TIN2, producing extremely short telomeres.","The hallmark is bilateral exudative (Coats-like) retinopathy with retinal vascular abnormalities, exudates and a high risk of detachment, presenting in infancy or early childhood.","Bone marrow failure and aplastic anaemia typically develop in early childhood and are a major cause of death.","Neurological features include intracranial calcification, cerebellar hypoplasia and psychomotor impairment.","Intrauterine growth restriction, fine sparse hair and nail changes are often present.","Diagnosis rests on very short leucocyte telomeres (flow-FISH) with TINF2 sequencing."],"pathophysiology":"Revesz syndrome sits within the telomere biology disorder spectrum and is caused chiefly by pathogenic variants in TINF2, which encodes TIN2, a core component of the six-protein shelterin complex that caps and protects telomeres and links the double-stranded telomeric binding proteins TRF1 and TRF2 to the TPP1-POT1 module. Disease-associated TINF2 variants, which cluster in a mutational hotspot, destabilise telomere protection and impair telomerase recruitment, producing extremely short telomeres and premature replicative senescence or apoptosis in proliferative tissues. The severity of telomere shortening explains the early, multisystem phenotype: failure of the haematopoietic compartment (aplastic anaemia), a distinctive bilateral exudative (Coats-type) retinopathy arising from retinal microvascular compromise, and central nervous system involvement with cerebellar hypoplasia and intracranial calcification, features that overlap with Hoyeraal-Hreidarsson syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Hoyeraal-Hreidarsson syndrome (severe infantile dyskeratosis congenita) — overlapping and often allelic","Coats disease (usually isolated and unilateral exudative retinopathy)","Coats plus syndrome / CRMCC (CTC1-related, with intracranial calcification and cysts)","Familial exudative vitreoretinopathy","Retinoblastoma (in the differential diagnosis of leucocoria)","Other inherited bone marrow failure syndromes such as Fanconi anaemia","Congenital (TORCH) infections presenting with retinopathy and intracranial calcification"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Leucocyte telomere length by flow-FISH","TINF2 sequencing, with a broader telomere-gene panel if negative","Full blood count and bone marrow examination with cytogenetics","Dilated fundoscopy and retinal imaging, with examination under anaesthesia in young children","Neuroimaging (MRI/CT) for cerebellar hypoplasia and intracranial calcification","Chromosome breakage study to exclude Fanconi anaemia"],"redFlags":["Leucocoria or exudative retinal detachment threatening vision","Progressive pancytopenia or marrow failure requiring transfusion or transplant assessment","Failure to thrive with developmental regression","Signs of raised intracranial pressure"],"prognosis":"Poor; the combination of early aplastic anaemia, progressive retinopathy and neurological involvement usually leads to death in childhood, although the course varies with severity.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the retinopathy as isolated Coats disease and missing the systemic telomere disorder","Attributing retinopathy together with intracranial calcification solely to congenital infection","Using standard-intensity transplant conditioning, which is poorly tolerated in telomere biology disorders","Assuming autosomal recessive inheritance when many TINF2 cases are de novo dominant"],"management":["Support the failing marrow with androgens such as danazol or oxymetholone plus transfusion, monitoring for progression","Consider haematopoietic stem cell transplantation using reduced-intensity conditioning for severe bone marrow failure, given the heightened organ sensitivity of telomere disorders","Treat the exudative (Coats-like) retinopathy with laser photocoagulation or anti-VEGF therapy to reduce the risk of detachment","Screen for and manage neurological complications, including cerebellar hypoplasia and intracranial calcification","Surveil for myelodysplasia, leukaemia and other malignancies inherent to telomere biology disorders","Offer genetic counselling and family testing for the TINF2 telomere variant, noting frequent de novo occurrence","Provide multidisciplinary supportive and palliative care given the poor prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dyskeratosis congenita and telomere biology disorder reviews","Paediatric haematology references","Inherited bone marrow failure syndrome guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"reye_syndrome":{"name":"Reye Syndrome","system":"Neurological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Reye's syndrome","acute encephalopathy with fatty liver"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare but potentially fatal disorder marked by acute non-inflammatory encephalopathy occurring alongside fatty change in the liver and other organs.","Classically follows a viral illness, especially influenza or varicella, during the recovery phase.","Salicylate (aspirin) exposure during a preceding viral infection is a well-recognised association, and the sharp decline in incidence tracks with public advice to avoid aspirin in children.","Typical evolution is protracted vomiting followed by progressive neurological deterioration: irritability or lethargy advancing to confusion, delirium, seizures, and coma.","Liver dysfunction is present but jaundice is characteristically absent or minimal, which can be a useful discriminating clue.","Laboratory findings include raised transaminases, hyperammonaemia, prolonged prothrombin time, and often hypoglycaemia in younger children.","In any child presenting with this picture, an underlying inborn error of metabolism (particularly a fatty acid oxidation or urea cycle defect) must be excluded, as these mimic Reye syndrome.","Management is supportive with a focus on controlling raised intracranial pressure, correcting metabolic derangements, and protecting cerebral perfusion.","Prognosis correlates strongly with the depth of coma and the degree of hyperammonaemia at presentation."],"pathophysiology":"The core lesion is acute, generalised mitochondrial injury. Diffuse mitochondrial dysfunction in the liver impairs the urea cycle and fatty acid oxidation, producing microvesicular fatty infiltration of hepatocytes, hyperammonaemia, and disturbed intermediary metabolism. In the brain this mitochondrial failure, combined with hyperammonaemia, drives cytotoxic cerebral oedema and raised intracranial pressure, which underlie the encephalopathy. Salicylate metabolites are thought to potentiate mitochondrial injury in susceptible children following a viral trigger.","additionalDifferentials":["Inborn errors of metabolism (fatty acid oxidation defects, urea cycle disorders, organic acidaemias)","Toxic ingestion or drug-induced hepatotoxicity","Acute encephalitis or meningitis","Fulminant viral hepatitis","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Haemorrhagic shock and encephalopathy syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ammonia","Liver transaminases, bilirubin, and coagulation profile","Blood glucose monitoring","Metabolic screen: plasma amino acids, urine organic acids, acylcarnitine profile","Neuroimaging to assess cerebral oedema and exclude other causes","Lumbar puncture only when safe and to exclude CNS infection","Liver biopsy in atypical or diagnostically uncertain cases"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive encephalopathy or declining conscious level (EMERGENCY)","Signs of raised intracranial pressure: abnormal posturing, pupillary changes, bradycardia with hypertension","Marked or rising hyperammonaemia","Seizures or status epilepticus","Coagulopathy with bleeding","Progression to coma requiring airway protection and intensive care"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on how advanced the encephalopathy is at presentation; children who remain in earlier stages often recover well, whereas deep coma and severe hyperammonaemia carry high risk of death or lasting neurological deficit. Early recognition and aggressive management of intracranial pressure improve survival.","pitfalls":["Attributing the illness to Reye syndrome without excluding an inborn error of metabolism, which is now a more common cause of this presentation","Being reassured by absent jaundice despite significant hepatic derangement","Delaying management of raised intracranial pressure while awaiting confirmatory tests","Overlooking hypoglycaemia, especially in infants and young children","Failing to elicit a history of aspirin or salicylate-containing product use"],"management":["Avoid salicylates in children with viral illness, the key preventive measure","Manage in intensive care with aggressive control of raised intracranial pressure and cerebral oedema","Correct hypoglycaemia and metabolic derangement with intravenous dextrose-containing fluids","Correct coagulopathy with vitamin K and blood products as needed","Lower ammonia and support the failing liver with supportive measures","Exclude inherited metabolic mimics such as fatty acid oxidation and urea cycle defects with a metabolic screen","Monitor neurological status closely and escalate neuroprotective measures as the encephalopathy advances"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"rh_isoimmunization":{"name":"Rhesus Isoimmunisation","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rh isoimmunization","rhesus alloimmunisation","Rh(D) sensitisation","haemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rhesus isoimmunisation occurs when a Rh(D)-negative mother is exposed to Rh(D)-positive fetal red cells and develops anti-D antibodies that can cross the placenta in subsequent pregnancies","Sensitising events include delivery, miscarriage, termination, ectopic pregnancy, antepartum bleeding, abdominal trauma, and invasive procedures such as amniocentesis","Because sensitisation usually produces no maternal symptoms, prevention and surveillance depend on routine antenatal blood grouping and antibody screening","Prophylactic anti-D immunoglobulin given after sensitising events and routinely in the third trimester has dramatically reduced the incidence of the disease","Once a woman is sensitised, anti-D immunoglobulin is no longer protective, and management focuses on monitoring the fetus for anaemia","Fetal anaemia is assessed non-invasively by Doppler measurement of the middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity, which rises as anaemia develops","Severe fetal anaemia can be treated by intrauterine transfusion, and timing of delivery is balanced against the severity of disease","Other red cell antibodies such as anti-Kell and anti-c can also cause severe haemolytic disease, so screening is not limited to the D antigen"],"pathophysiology":"When Rh(D)-positive fetal red cells enter the circulation of a Rh(D)-negative mother, her immune system mounts an antibody response, initially IgM and then IgG anti-D. In a later pregnancy with a Rh(D)-positive fetus, maternal IgG crosses the placenta, binds fetal red cells, and leads to their destruction in the fetal reticuloendothelial system. The resulting fetal anaemia drives extramedullary haematopoiesis and, when severe, high-output cardiac failure with generalised oedema known as hydrops fetalis, alongside accumulation of bilirubin that threatens the neonate after birth.","additionalDifferentials":["Haemolytic disease from non-D red cell antibodies such as anti-Kell or anti-c","ABO incompatibility causing neonatal haemolysis","Fetal anaemia from parvovirus B19 infection","Fetomaternal haemorrhage from other causes","Alpha thalassaemia causing hydrops","Non-immune hydrops fetalis from cardiac or structural causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Maternal blood group and antibody screen at booking and repeated during pregnancy","Quantification and titre of anti-D and other clinically significant antibodies","Paternal and, where available, fetal Rh(D) genotyping including cell-free fetal DNA testing","Serial middle cerebral artery Doppler peak systolic velocity to detect fetal anaemia","Ultrasound assessment for signs of hydrops","Kleihauer or flow cytometry to estimate the volume of fetomaternal haemorrhage"],"redFlags":["Rising antibody titres or Doppler velocities indicating progressive fetal anaemia and risk of fetal compromise","Ultrasound evidence of hydrops fetalis signalling severe disease","A significant sensitising event without timely anti-D prophylaxis","Reduced fetal movements or non-reassuring fetal assessment in a sensitised pregnancy","Previous pregnancy affected by severe haemolytic disease, predicting earlier and more severe recurrence"],"prognosis":"With routine antenatal screening, effective anti-D prophylaxis, non-invasive monitoring, and intrauterine transfusion, most affected pregnancies now achieve good outcomes. Prognosis worsens with early-onset severe anaemia and hydrops, and sensitised pregnancies tend to be affected earlier and more severely in successive gestations, making specialist surveillance essential.","pitfalls":["Omitting anti-D prophylaxis after a sensitising event or assuming it helps an already sensitised woman","Focusing only on anti-D and overlooking other antibodies such as anti-Kell","Relying on antibody titres alone without fetal Doppler surveillance in established sensitisation","Underestimating small but sensitising fetomaternal haemorrhages","Failing to quantify large fetomaternal haemorrhage so that the anti-D dose is inadequate"],"management":["Prevent sensitisation with prophylactic anti-D immunoglobulin given routinely in the third trimester and after any sensitising event in Rh(D)-negative women","Determine foetal Rh(D) status by cell-free foetal DNA testing to identify pregnancies at risk","In sensitised pregnancies, monitor for foetal anaemia with serial middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity Doppler and ultrasound for hydrops","Perform intrauterine intravascular foetal transfusion for significant foetal anaemia","Time delivery and prepare neonatal care, treating haemolytic jaundice with phototherapy and exchange transfusion as required","Coordinate care in a foetal medicine unit with serial maternal antibody titres"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"rhabdoid_tumour":{"name":"rhabdoid tumour","system":"multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["malignant rhabdoid tumour","malignant rhabdoid tumour of the kidney","extracranial rhabdoid tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Paediatric Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A highly aggressive malignancy of infancy and early childhood; the kidney is the prototypical site (malignant rhabdoid tumour of the kidney), but soft tissue, liver, and other viscera are also affected.","Defined by biallelic inactivation of SMARCB1 (INI1/hSNF5) on chromosome 22q11.2, with a small subset driven by SMARCA4 (BRG1) loss.","Loss of nuclear INI1 (SMARCB1) expression on immunohistochemistry is the diagnostic hallmark and distinguishes it from mimics such as Wilms tumour.","A substantial minority arise within rhabdoid tumour predisposition syndrome from a germline SMARCB1 or SMARCA4 mutation, so germline testing and genetic counselling are essential.","Synchronous or metachronous central nervous system atypical teratoid/rhabdoid tumour occurs, making brain imaging mandatory at staging.","Hypercalcaemia is a recognised paraneoplastic feature of renal rhabdoid tumour.","Presentation is often with a rapidly growing abdominal or flank mass, haematuria, and systemic upset in a very young infant.","Metastatic disease at diagnosis is common and the prognosis is among the worst of paediatric solid tumours."],"pathophysiology":"Rhabdoid tumours are driven by loss of function of the SWI/SNF (BAF) chromatin-remodelling complex, most often through biallelic inactivation of SMARCB1 (INI1) and less commonly SMARCA4 (BRG1). Loss of a core subunit deregulates chromatin accessibility and enhancer activity, impairing differentiation and driving proliferation despite an otherwise remarkably stable, near-diploid genome with few additional mutations. Tumours are composed of characteristic rhabdoid cells with eccentric nuclei, prominent nucleoli, and eosinophilic cytoplasmic hyaline inclusions corresponding to whorls of intermediate filaments. A germline SMARCB1 or SMARCA4 alteration (rhabdoid tumour predisposition syndrome) predisposes to multifocal, very early-onset disease, including concurrent central nervous system tumours.","additionalDifferentials":["Wilms tumour (nephroblastoma)","Clear cell sarcoma of the kidney","Congenital mesoblastic nephroma","Neuroblastoma","Atypical teratoid/rhabdoid tumour when the central nervous system is involved","Epithelioid sarcoma (also INI1-deficient) at soft-tissue sites","Renal medullary carcinoma (also SMARCB1-deficient) in older children"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SMARCB1/INI1 (and SMARCA4) immunohistochemistry on tumour tissue","Molecular and cytogenetic testing for SMARCB1 deletion or mutation","Germline SMARCB1/SMARCA4 testing and genetic counselling","Brain and whole-spine MRI to exclude synchronous central nervous system rhabdoid tumour","Cross-sectional imaging of chest, abdomen, and pelvis for staging","Serum calcium to detect paraneoplastic hypercalcaemia","Bone imaging for skeletal metastases"],"redFlags":["Neurological signs or a brain lesion indicating synchronous atypical teratoid/rhabdoid tumour","Symptomatic hypercalcaemia","A rapidly enlarging mass with systemic deterioration in an infant","Tumour rupture with intraperitoneal haemorrhage"],"prognosis":"Extracranial rhabdoid tumour carries a poor prognosis, particularly in infants and in those with metastatic disease or a germline predisposition. Intensified multimodal therapy achieves long-term survival in only a minority.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying the tumour as Wilms tumour or another blastemal-rich neoplasm without performing INI1 immunohistochemistry.","Omitting brain imaging and thereby missing a synchronous central nervous system rhabdoid tumour.","Failing to arrange germline SMARCB1/SMARCA4 testing, missing predisposition syndrome and family risk.","Attributing hypercalcaemia to another cause and overlooking a paraneoplastic mechanism."],"management":["Treat with intensive multimodal therapy combining multi-agent chemotherapy, surgical resection and radiotherapy for local control","Stage with brain and whole-spine imaging to detect synchronous atypical teratoid or rhabdoid central nervous system tumour","Undertake germline SMARCB1 and SMARCA4 testing with genetic counselling for rhabdoid tumour predisposition syndrome","Detect and manage paraneoplastic hypercalcaemia with hydration and appropriate hypocalcaemic agents","Enrol in clinical trials and consider novel targeted agents such as EZH2 inhibitors given poor outcomes with standard therapy","Provide multidisciplinary paediatric oncology care with supportive and, where appropriate, palliative input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Paediatric Tumours","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","European Rhabdoid Registry (EU-RHAB) consensus guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"rhabdomyolysis":{"name":"Rhabdomyolysis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["muscle breakdown syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emergency medicine and nephrology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Breakdown of skeletal muscle releases intracellular contents, notably myoglobin, potassium, phosphate and creatine kinase, into the circulation","The classic triad is muscle pain, weakness and dark (tea-coloured) urine, though many patients lack all three","A markedly raised creatine kinase is the key laboratory finding","Acute kidney injury from myoglobin, and life-threatening hyperkalaemia, are the main dangers","Causes are diverse, including crush and trauma, prolonged immobility, extreme exertion, seizures, drugs, toxins, ischaemia and metabolic myopathies","Early and generous intravenous fluid resuscitation to maintain urine output is the cornerstone of treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Rhabdomyolysis occurs when injury to skeletal muscle disrupts the sarcolemma and releases myoglobin and other intracellular constituents. Myoglobin is filtered by the kidney where it causes tubular obstruction, direct tubular toxicity and renal vasoconstriction, producing acute kidney injury, particularly when the patient is volume-depleted and the urine is acidic. Release of potassium and phosphate and sequestration of calcium in damaged muscle cause dangerous electrolyte disturbances, while a compartment syndrome can both cause and result from the muscle injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute kidney injury of another cause","Myocardial infarction (for raised enzymes)","Inflammatory myopathy","Haemolysis causing pigmenturia","Compartment syndrome","Primary hyperkalaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase and serial trends","Renal function, potassium, phosphate and calcium","Urinalysis showing blood on dipstick without red cells (myoglobinuria)","Electrocardiogram for hyperkalaemic changes","Investigation of the underlying cause, including compartment pressures if indicated"],"redFlags":["Hyperkalaemia with electrocardiographic changes","Oliguria or established acute kidney injury","Signs of compartment syndrome","Very high or rising creatine kinase with volume depletion"],"prognosis":"With prompt fluid resuscitation many patients avoid or recover from kidney injury; prognosis worsens with severe hyperkalaemia, established renal failure or the underlying precipitating condition.","pitfalls":["Delaying fluid resuscitation","Missing hyperkalaemia as an immediate threat","Overlooking compartment syndrome","Attributing dark urine to haematuria without checking for myoglobinuria"],"management":["Give early, generous intravenous fluid resuscitation to maintain a high urine output and prevent myoglobin-induced acute kidney injury","Identify, remove and treat the precipitating cause such as crush injury, drugs, toxins, prolonged immobility or seizures","Detect and treat life-threatening hyperkalaemia urgently, with continuous ECG monitoring for hyperkalaemic changes","Correct associated electrolyte disturbances, treating symptomatic hypocalcaemia but otherwise avoiding calcium","Monitor serial creatine kinase, renal function and urine output, and institute renal replacement therapy for refractory hyperkalaemia, acidosis or fluid overload","Assess for and decompress compartment syndrome with fasciotomy where present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nephrology reference texts (standard reference)","Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"rhabdomyosarcoma":{"name":"Rhabdomyosarcoma (embryonal/alveolar)","system":"Oncology / Sarcoma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RMS","embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma","alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma","PAX3-FOXO1 fusion sarcoma","PAX7-FOXO1 fusion sarcoma","botryoid rhabdomyosarcoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest soft-tissue sarcoma of childhood, with the embryonal subtype predominating in young children and the alveolar subtype more common in older children and adolescents","Site varies with subtype: head and neck (including orbit and parameningeal), genitourinary tract, and extremities are typical, and presentation reflects local mass effect","The botryoid variant of embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma forms grape-like polypoid masses in hollow, mucosa-lined organs such as the vagina or bladder","Fusion status is prognostically decisive: PAX3-FOXO1 or PAX7-FOXO1 fusion-positive tumours (usually alveolar morphology) behave more aggressively than fusion-negative tumours","Diagnosis rests on skeletal muscle differentiation shown by desmin, myogenin, and MyoD1 immunohistochemistry alongside morphology and molecular testing","Treatment is risk-stratified multimodality therapy combining multi-agent chemotherapy with surgery and/or radiotherapy for local control","Parameningeal tumours can extend intracranially and require careful evaluation for meningeal and central nervous system involvement","Staging includes primary-site MRI, regional nodal assessment, chest CT, whole-body functional imaging, and bone-marrow examination"],"pathophysiology":"Rhabdomyosarcoma is a malignancy of primitive mesenchymal cells committed to skeletal muscle differentiation but arrested before maturation. The alveolar subtype is characteristically driven by a PAX3-FOXO1 or PAX7-FOXO1 fusion gene that generates a potent chimeric transcription factor promoting proliferation and blocking myogenic maturation. Embryonal tumours generally lack these fusions and instead show a range of alterations, frequently involving the RAS pathway and loss of imprinting at chromosome 11p15. Because fusion status correlates better with behaviour than morphology alone, molecular classification into fusion-positive and fusion-negative disease now guides risk assessment.","additionalDifferentials":["Ewing sarcoma and other small round cell tumours","Neuroblastoma (in infants and young children)","Lymphoma","Undifferentiated soft-tissue sarcoma","Infantile fibrosarcoma and other paediatric spindle-cell tumours","Benign lesions such as haemangioma or lymphangioma at soft-tissue sites"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the primary site to delineate extent and relation to critical structures","Chest CT for pulmonary metastases","Whole-body functional imaging for nodal and distant staging","Bone-marrow aspirate and trephine to detect marrow involvement","Lumbar puncture and dedicated neuroimaging for parameningeal tumours to assess central nervous system spread","Biopsy with immunohistochemistry (desmin, myogenin, MyoD1) and molecular testing for FOXO1 fusions"],"redFlags":["A parameningeal tumour with cranial nerve palsies, headache, or signs of intracranial extension","Rapidly growing painless mass in a child, especially at head and neck or genitourinary sites","Airway or urinary obstruction from a botryoid or pelvic mass","Features of disseminated disease such as bone pain or cytopenias suggesting marrow involvement"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is determined by fusion status, stage and clinical group, primary site, patient age, and histological subtype; localised, fusion-negative embryonal tumours at favourable sites do well, whereas fusion-positive, metastatic, or unfavourable-site disease carries a substantially worse outlook.","pitfalls":["Assuming morphology alone predicts behaviour rather than confirming fusion status by molecular testing","Underestimating central nervous system risk in parameningeal disease and omitting appropriate neuraxis staging","Inadequate biopsy planning that jeopardises subsequent local control","Overlooking regional lymph node assessment, which is particularly important at extremity and paratesticular sites","Neglecting long-term surveillance for late effects of chemotherapy and radiotherapy in survivors"],"management":["Deliver risk-stratified multi-agent chemotherapy, typically vincristine, actinomycin D and cyclophosphamide, as the backbone of treatment","Achieve local control with surgical resection and/or radiotherapy according to site and resectability","Stratify risk by fusion status (PAX3-FOXO1 or PAX7-FOXO1), stage, clinical group, primary site and histology to guide treatment intensity","Confirm the diagnosis with immunohistochemistry for desmin, myogenin and MyoD1 alongside molecular testing for FOXO1 fusions","Stage fully with primary-site MRI, chest CT, functional whole-body imaging and marrow examination, adding CSF analysis and neuroimaging for parameningeal tumours","Provide multidisciplinary paediatric and sarcoma oncology care with supportive management of chemotherapy toxicity","Arrange long-term surveillance for relapse and for the late effects of therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"rheumatic_fever":{"name":"rheumatic fever","system":"cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute rheumatic fever","ARF"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Acute rheumatic fever is a delayed immune-mediated sequela of group A streptococcal pharyngitis, typically emerging a few weeks after the sore throat, and is a diagnosis of the whole child rather than any single test.","Diagnosis rests on the revised Jones criteria, which now stratify populations into low-risk and moderate-to-high-risk to improve sensitivity where the disease remains endemic.","Migratory polyarthritis of the large joints is the commonest major manifestation and is characteristically exquisitely responsive to salicylates.","Carditis is the only manifestation that causes long-term morbidity; a pancarditis, its hallmark is valvulitis with the mitral valve most often affected, followed by the aortic valve.","Sydenham chorea may appear in isolation and late, after other manifestations have resolved, and can be the sole criterion sufficient for diagnosis.","Subcutaneous nodules and erythema marginatum are uncommon but highly specific minor major manifestations, nodules correlating with severe carditis.","Evidence of preceding streptococcal infection (rising anti-streptolysin O or anti-DNase B titres, or positive throat culture) is essential except in isolated chorea or indolent carditis.","Secondary prophylaxis with long-acting penicillin is the cornerstone of preventing recurrent attacks and progressive rheumatic heart disease."],"pathophysiology":"Acute rheumatic fever follows pharyngeal infection with rheumatogenic strains of group A beta-haemolytic streptococcus in a genetically susceptible host. Molecular mimicry between streptococcal antigens, particularly M protein and the group A carbohydrate N-acetyl-glucosamine, and human tissue proteins such as cardiac myosin and laminin drives a cross-reactive humoral and T-cell response. Antibody binding to valvular endothelium up-regulates adhesion molecules, recruiting CD4+ T cells that release inflammatory cytokines and produce the granulomatous Aschoff bodies pathognomonic of rheumatic carditis. The same autoimmune mechanism targets the basal ganglia to produce chorea, the joints to produce arthritis, and the skin. Repeated episodes amplify valvular scarring, leading to chronic rheumatic heart disease.","additionalDifferentials":["septic or reactive arthritis","juvenile idiopathic arthritis","systemic lupus erythematosus","infective endocarditis","viral myocarditis","Lyme disease","disseminated gonococcal infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["throat swab culture and rapid antigen testing for group A streptococcus","serial anti-streptolysin O and anti-DNase B titres","inflammatory markers (ESR and C-reactive protein)","electrocardiogram for PR-interval prolongation and conduction disturbance","echocardiography with Doppler to detect subclinical valvulitis","full blood count and blood cultures to exclude sepsis or endocarditis"],"redFlags":["new or changing cardiac murmur with signs of heart failure indicating severe carditis","pericardial rub or effusion suggesting pancarditis","high-grade atrioventricular block","recurrent attack in a child with established rheumatic heart disease"],"prognosis":"Arthritis and chorea resolve without sequelae, but the prognosis is dictated by the severity of carditis, which may progress to chronic rheumatic heart disease. Faithful secondary prophylaxis markedly reduces recurrence and can allow mild valve lesions to regress.","pitfalls":["Attributing a febrile arthritis to rheumatic fever without documented antecedent streptococcal infection, especially in low-incidence settings.","Starting salicylates or steroids before the joint picture has declared itself, masking the diagnostic migratory pattern.","Overlooking subclinical carditis detectable only on echocardiography in a child with apparently isolated arthritis.","Failing to institute and sustain secondary prophylaxis, the single most important determinant of long-term outcome.","Misreading isolated late-onset chorea as a functional or psychiatric disorder."],"management":["Eradicate the group A streptococcal infection with penicillin, or a macrolide in penicillin-allergic patients","Suppress joint inflammation and fever with anti-inflammatory therapy such as salicylates or other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, to which the arthritis is exquisitely responsive","Manage severe carditis and heart failure with corticosteroids alongside standard heart-failure therapy","Treat disabling Sydenham chorea symptomatically with agents such as carbamazepine or valproate","Institute long-term secondary prophylaxis with regular intramuscular benzathine penicillin to prevent recurrence","Assess and monitor for carditis and evolving valve disease with echocardiography","Register the patient, ensure prophylaxis adherence and screen and treat household streptococcal infection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics, current edition","American Heart Association revised Jones criteria","World Heart Federation criteria for rheumatic heart disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"rheumatic_heart_disease":{"name":"Rheumatic heart disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RHD","chronic rheumatic valvular disease","post-rheumatic valve disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rheumatic heart disease is the chronic valvular sequel of acute rheumatic fever, an immune-mediated complication of group A streptococcal infection, and remains a major cause of valve disease in many parts of the world","The mitral valve is most often affected, and mitral stenosis is the most characteristic lesion, though mixed mitral and aortic valve involvement is common","Patients may be asymptomatic for years before developing exertional breathlessness, fatigue, palpitations from atrial fibrillation, or features of heart failure","Although the valve damage itself is non-infective, these valves are prone to infective endocarditis, which must be considered when there is fever or clinical deterioration","Mitral stenosis predisposes to atrial fibrillation and left atrial thrombus, and to systemic embolism including stroke","Echocardiography is central to assessing valve morphology, severity, and haemodynamic consequences","Prevention relies on prompt treatment of streptococcal pharyngitis and long-term secondary antibiotic prophylaxis to prevent recurrent rheumatic fever","Advanced disease may require balloon valvuloplasty for suitable mitral stenosis, or surgical repair or valve replacement"],"pathophysiology":"Acute rheumatic fever follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis in susceptible individuals, in whom molecular mimicry between streptococcal antigens and host tissue drives a cross-reactive immune response affecting the heart, joints, skin, and nervous system. Recurrent or severe carditis produces chronic inflammation and scarring of the valves, with leaflet thickening, commissural fusion, and chordal shortening. Over years this yields stenosis, regurgitation, or mixed dysfunction, most commonly of the mitral valve. The resulting pressure and volume loading leads to atrial enlargement, atrial fibrillation, pulmonary hypertension, and eventually heart failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Degenerative (calcific) valve disease","Congenital valve abnormalities such as bicuspid aortic valve","Infective endocarditis affecting native valves","Non-rheumatic mitral regurgitation from prolapse or ischaemia","Other causes of mitral stenosis such as severe annular calcification","Cardiomyopathy presenting with functional valve regurgitation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic and, when needed, transoesophageal echocardiography to define valve morphology and severity","12-lead ECG to detect atrial fibrillation and chamber enlargement","Chest radiography for chamber size, pulmonary congestion, and valve calcification","Evidence of prior streptococcal infection and inflammatory markers in suspected acute disease","Assessment of pulmonary pressures and right heart consequences","Blood cultures and endocarditis workup when infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Fever or systemic features suggesting superimposed infective endocarditis on a damaged valve","New atrial fibrillation with embolic risk, or acute systemic embolism such as stroke","Acute pulmonary oedema or rapidly progressive heart failure","Haemoptysis or severe pulmonary hypertension in advanced mitral stenosis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the valves involved, severity, and access to secondary prophylaxis and intervention; secondary prophylaxis and timely valve intervention allow many patients to do well, but advanced disease with pulmonary hypertension, atrial fibrillation, and heart failure carries significant morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Overlooking superimposed infective endocarditis when a patient with known rheumatic valve disease develops fever","Failing to maintain long-term secondary antibiotic prophylaxis to prevent recurrent rheumatic fever","Underestimating stroke risk from atrial fibrillation and left atrial thrombus in mitral stenosis","Delaying valve intervention until irreversible pulmonary hypertension or ventricular dysfunction has developed"],"management":["Maintain long-term secondary prophylaxis with regular benzathine penicillin to prevent recurrent rheumatic fever","Manage heart-failure symptoms with diuretics and control the rate and rhythm in atrial fibrillation","Anticoagulate with a vitamin K antagonist for atrial fibrillation, left atrial thrombus or a prosthetic valve","Refer for valve intervention (balloon valvuloplasty, repair or replacement) guided by echocardiographic severity and symptoms","Maintain vigilance for infective endocarditis and treat it promptly when suspected","Monitor with serial echocardiography for progression of valve dysfunction and pulmonary hypertension","Coordinate combined cardiology and obstetric care in pregnancy and maintain dental and general health"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"rheumatic_mitral_stenosis":{"name":"Rheumatic mitral stenosis","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mitral stenosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Valvular heart disease guidelines / cardiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Narrowing of the mitral valve orifice, worldwide most often a late consequence of rheumatic fever","Presents with exertional breathlessness, fatigue, haemoptysis and, frequently, atrial fibrillation","The classic auscultatory findings are a loud first heart sound, an opening snap and a low-pitched mid-diastolic rumble at the apex","Left atrial enlargement predisposes to atrial fibrillation and to left atrial thrombus with systemic embolism","Pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure develop as the disease advances","Assessment of severity and valve morphology guides balloon valvuloplasty or surgery; anticoagulation is needed for atrial fibrillation or thrombus"],"pathophysiology":"Rheumatic mitral stenosis results from chronic post-inflammatory scarring after rheumatic fever, with leaflet thickening, commissural fusion and chordal shortening progressively narrowing the mitral orifice. The obstruction raises left atrial pressure, which is transmitted back to the pulmonary circulation causing pulmonary congestion and, over time, pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain. Left atrial dilatation and stasis promote atrial fibrillation and thrombus formation with embolic risk. Although the valve injury is a sequela of infection, the established stenosis is a structural, non-infective disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Degenerative (calcific) mitral stenosis","Congenital mitral stenosis","Left atrial myxoma obstructing the valve","Cor triatriatum","Other causes of pulmonary hypertension and breathlessness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography with valve area and gradient and morphology scoring","Electrocardiogram (atrial fibrillation, left atrial enlargement)","Chest radiograph for left atrial enlargement and pulmonary congestion","Assessment for left atrial thrombus (often transoesophageal)","Right heart assessment for pulmonary hypertension"],"redFlags":["New atrial fibrillation with rapid deterioration","Systemic embolism or left atrial thrombus","Severe pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure","Haemoptysis or pulmonary oedema"],"prognosis":"The condition is progressive; symptoms and complications are well controlled and prognosis improved by timely balloon valvuloplasty or valve surgery, though atrial fibrillation and embolism carry ongoing risk.","pitfalls":["Missing left atrial thrombus before valvuloplasty","Failing to anticoagulate atrial fibrillation","Attributing breathlessness to lung disease alone","Underestimating pulmonary hypertension"],"management":["Relieve congestive symptoms with diuretics and control the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation with beta-blockers or other rate-limiting agents","Anticoagulate with a vitamin K antagonist for atrial fibrillation or left atrial thrombus","Refer for percutaneous balloon mitral valvuloplasty in suitable patients with favourable valve morphology","Proceed to surgical mitral valve repair or replacement when valvuloplasty is unsuitable or the valve is heavily diseased","Continue secondary antibiotic prophylaxis against recurrent rheumatic fever","Screen for left atrial thrombus with transoesophageal echocardiography before valvuloplasty","Monitor with serial echocardiography for progression, pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Valvular heart disease guidelines (standard reference)","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"rhinophyma":{"name":"Rhinophyma","system":"Skin / Nose","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["phymatous rosacea of the nose"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Progressive enlargement, thickening and lobulation of the nose from sebaceous gland hyperplasia and fibrosis, representing the phymatous form of rosacea","It predominantly affects the lower nose, producing a bulbous, irregular contour with dilated follicular openings","It occurs far more often in men and usually develops in the context of long-standing rosacea","Contrary to popular belief, it is not caused by alcohol excess","A rare but important concern is squamous or basal cell carcinoma arising within or mimicking rhinophyma","Advanced disease is managed surgically or with ablative techniques to recontour the nose"],"pathophysiology":"Rhinophyma is the end stage of phymatous rosacea, in which chronic inflammation drives marked hyperplasia of sebaceous glands, dermal fibrosis and connective-tissue overgrowth of the nasal skin. Progressive sebaceous and fibrous expansion distorts the nasal contour and enlarges follicular openings. The underlying rosacea-associated vascular and inflammatory changes, rather than any dietary or alcohol factor, account for the disfigurement, and occasionally cutaneous malignancy develops within the thickened tissue.","additionalDifferentials":["Other phymas of rosacea (for example of the chin or ears)","Basal or squamous cell carcinoma of the nose","Sarcoidosis or other granulomatous infiltration","Nasal lymphoma or other tumour","Sebaceous hyperplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the characteristic appearance and rosacea history","Biopsy of any suspicious nodule or ulcer to exclude carcinoma","Assessment of the extent and functional impact including nasal airway","Photography to monitor progression","Dermatological review of associated rosacea"],"redFlags":["A non-healing ulcer, bleeding nodule or rapidly growing lesion suggesting malignancy","Nasal airway obstruction from bulk","Atypical rapid progression","Features suggesting granulomatous or lymphomatous infiltration"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign but progressive if untreated and can be cosmetically and functionally disabling; surgical recontouring generally gives good results, though rosacea persists.","pitfalls":["Attributing the condition to alcohol and stigmatising the patient","Missing a carcinoma arising within rhinophymatous tissue","Overlooking nasal airway compromise","Neglecting treatment of the underlying rosacea"],"management":["Optimise the underlying phymatous rosacea with topical anti-rosacea agents (metronidazole, azelaic acid, ivermectin) and oral tetracyclines to limit ongoing inflammation","Surgical recontouring of the nose for established bulbous deformity using cold-steel excision, dermabrasion, electrosurgery or ablative carbon-dioxide laser","Send excised and any suspicious ulcerated or nodular tissue for histology to exclude basal or squamous cell carcinoma arising within the thickened skin","Advise photoprotection and avoidance of vasodilatory triggers to slow progression","Serial photography and airway assessment to monitor progression and functional nasal obstruction","Refer jointly to dermatology, plastic surgery and otolaryngology, with psychological support for cosmetic and functional distress"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dermatology reference texts (standard reference)","Plastic surgery reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"rhinoscleroma":{"name":"Rhinoscleroma (Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis infection","scleroma","respiratory scleroma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rhinoscleroma is a chronic granulomatous infection of the upper respiratory tract caused by Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis (a subspecies of Klebsiella pneumoniae)","It is endemic in parts of Africa, the Middle East, Central and South America, Asia and Eastern Europe, and is linked to poor hygiene and crowding","Disease evolves through catarrhal, granulomatous (proliferative) and sclerotic (fibrotic) stages over months to years","The granulomatous stage produces the characteristic nodular masses that can deform the nose and obstruct the airway","Histology reveals Mikulicz cells (large vacuolated macrophages containing bacteria) alongside plasma cells with Russell bodies","Prolonged antibiotic therapy is required, using agents such as fluoroquinolones or tetracyclines, guided by susceptibility","Surgery may be needed to relieve airway obstruction or correct deformity in the fibrotic stage"],"pathophysiology":"Rhinoscleroma results from chronic infection of the respiratory mucosa by Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis, an encapsulated Gram-negative bacillus acquired by inhalation, typically in conditions of overcrowding and poor sanitation. The organism is engulfed by macrophages but resists intracellular killing, giving rise to the distinctive foamy Mikulicz cells that dominate the granulomatous stage. A sustained inflammatory response with plasma cells and Russell bodies leads progressively to fibrosis and scarring. This staged process explains the transition from non-specific catarrhal symptoms through proliferative masses to the sclerotic deformity and airway narrowing of late disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Nasal and paranasal malignancy","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Nasal tuberculosis or leprosy","Fungal rhinosinusitis (e.g. rhinosporidiosis, mucormycosis)","Sarcoidosis","Cutaneous and mucosal leishmaniasis","Lymphoma of the nasal region"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy for histology demonstrating Mikulicz cells and Russell bodies","Culture of tissue for Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis","Special stains (e.g. Warthin-Starry, periodic acid-Schiff) to highlight organisms within macrophages","Nasal endoscopy to assess the extent of masses and airway compromise","Cross-sectional imaging to evaluate deeper and paranasal involvement","Antimicrobial susceptibility testing to guide prolonged therapy"],"redFlags":["Airway obstruction from nasal or laryngeal masses","Progressive nasal or facial deformity","Diagnostic uncertainty requiring exclusion of malignancy or vasculitis","Extension to the larynx or lower airway"],"prognosis":"With prolonged appropriate antibiotics the infection can be controlled, but late fibrotic disease may leave permanent structural deformity and airway narrowing, and relapse can occur if therapy is inadequate in duration.","pitfalls":["Stopping antibiotics too soon, allowing relapse of this indolent infection","Mistaking granulomatous masses for malignancy or vasculitis without biopsy","Failing to recognise Mikulicz cells as the diagnostic histological clue","Neglecting airway assessment in advanced disease"],"management":["Prolonged antimicrobial therapy directed at Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis, using fluoroquinolones or tetracyclines and continued for an extended course to eradicate intracellular organisms and prevent relapse","Culture of tissue with susceptibility testing to guide and, if needed, adjust the antibiotic choice","Endoscopic debulking or surgical resection of obstructing granulomatous masses and correction of sclerotic deformity","Airway management for late fibrotic stenosis, including endoscopic dilatation and tracheostomy where obstruction is severe","Clinical and endoscopic surveillance after treatment to detect relapse from inadequate therapy duration","Address overcrowding and poor sanitation as public-health preventive measures","Joint otolaryngology and infectious-diseases follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"rhinosporidiosis":{"name":"Rhinosporidiosis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rhinosporidium seeberi infection","nasal rhinosporidiosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic granulomatous infection caused by Rhinosporidium seeberi that most often involves the nose and nasopharynx","Presents with a friable, reddish, highly vascular polypoidal mass that bleeds easily and is classically studded with white dots representing sporangia, giving a strawberry-like appearance","Common symptoms are unilateral nasal obstruction, epistaxis, and a mass that may be visible or prolapse into the nasopharynx","It is endemic in parts of the Indian subcontinent and is linked to exposure to stagnant water and bathing in ponds","Diagnosis is confirmed histologically by the characteristic sporangia containing endospores in a granulomatous stroma","Treatment is complete surgical excision of the lesion, and cauterising the base helps reduce recurrence and bleeding","Recurrence is well recognised because of local spread of endospores, so meticulous removal and base cautery are important","The lesion bleeds readily, so care during examination and excision is needed to control haemorrhage"],"pathophysiology":"Rhinosporidiosis is caused by Rhinosporidium seeberi, now regarded as an aquatic protistan parasite, which is thought to enter the mucosa through minor trauma after contact with contaminated stagnant water. The organism proliferates within the mucosa, maturing through trophic stages into large sporangia packed with endospores. Rupture of a sporangium releases endospores that seed adjacent tissue, driving formation of highly vascular polypoidal granulomatous masses. The rich vascularity accounts for the marked tendency to bleed, and local dissemination of endospores explains frequent recurrence after incomplete removal.","additionalDifferentials":["Antrochoanal or inflammatory nasal polyp","Haemangioma of the nasal cavity","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma","Inverted papilloma","Nasal or nasopharyngeal malignancy","Other granulomatous nasal infections such as tuberculosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasal endoscopy to define the vascular polypoidal mass and its extent","Histopathology of the excised tissue to demonstrate sporangia and endospores","Assessment of haemoglobin where recurrent epistaxis has occurred","Imaging in extensive disease to define spread beyond the nasal cavity"],"redFlags":["Brisk or recurrent epistaxis from the friable vascular mass requiring measures to secure haemostasis","Disseminated disease involving sites beyond the nose such as the eye, larynx, or skin","Airway compromise from a large nasopharyngeal mass","Rapid change or atypical features raising concern for coexisting malignancy"],"prognosis":"Complete excision with cautery of the base usually gives a good outcome, but recurrence is common owing to local spread of endospores, and disease occasionally disseminates to other mucosal sites; the condition is chronic and can be troublesome to eradicate fully.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the vascularity and provoking significant bleeding during biopsy or removal","Incomplete excision that leaves endospores and leads to recurrence","Mistaking the mass for a simple polyp and not obtaining histology","Overlooking disease at other sites in disseminated cases"],"management":["Complete surgical excision of the polypoidal mass, which is the mainstay of treatment","Cauterisation or electrocoagulation of the lesion base to reduce recurrence and control bleeding","Adjuvant dapsone to suppress residual disease and lower the recurrence rate","Control of intraoperative and recurrent epistaxis, with transfusion and correction of anaemia where bleeding has been heavy","Histological confirmation of the excised tissue to demonstrate sporangia and endospores","Advise avoidance of bathing in stagnant pond water to reduce reinfection","Endoscopic surveillance for recurrence given local spread of endospores"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"rhizomelic_chondrodysplasia_punctata":{"name":"Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RCDP","Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata type 1","PEX7","GNPAT","AGPS"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews Rhizomelic Chondrodysplasia Punctata / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The clinical hallmark is symmetrical rhizomelic (proximal) shortening of the limbs, particularly the humeri and femora, with early cataracts and profound developmental delay","It is an autosomal recessive peroxisomal disorder, most often from PEX7 mutations (type 1) affecting the peroxisomal targeting-2 import pathway, and less often from GNPAT or AGPS defects (types 2 and 3)","The unifying biochemical defect is deficient synthesis of plasmalogens, a class of ether phospholipids essential for membranes and myelin","In PEX7-related disease phytanic acid alpha-oxidation is also impaired, so plasma phytanic acid can be elevated while very-long-chain fatty acids remain normal","Radiographs show punctate epiphyseal calcification (chondrodysplasia punctata) and coronal clefts of the vertebral bodies","Seizures, joint contractures, feeding difficulty, recurrent respiratory infections and characteristic facial features are common","Care is supportive, addressing feeding, respiratory infection, seizures, orthopaedic contractures and cataracts, with generic dietary phytanic-acid restriction considered where phytanic acid is raised"],"pathophysiology":"Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata is a disorder of peroxisomal metabolism in which the import or activity of a small group of peroxisomal enzymes is disrupted. In the common PEX7 form, the receptor for the peroxisomal targeting signal 2 fails, so enzymes bearing that signal, including those for plasmalogen biosynthesis and phytanic acid alpha-oxidation, are not correctly delivered to the peroxisome. Types 2 and 3 involve direct deficiency of the plasmalogen synthetic enzymes encoded by GNPAT and AGPS. The shared consequence is severely deficient plasmalogen synthesis, which impairs formation of myelin and other specialised membranes; accumulation of phytanic acid contributes in the PEX7 form. These defects underlie the skeletal, ocular and neurological features.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-rhizomelic (X-linked) chondrodysplasia punctata (CDPX)","Warfarin embryopathy and maternal vitamin K deficiency effects","Zellweger spectrum peroxisome biogenesis disorders","Classic Refsum disease (also PEX7-related, high phytanic acid)","Achondroplasia and other rhizomelic skeletal dysplasias","Congenital cataract syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Erythrocyte plasmalogen levels, which are reduced","Plasma phytanic acid (may be elevated) with normal very-long-chain fatty acids","Skeletal radiographs demonstrating punctate epiphyseal calcification and vertebral coronal clefts","Ophthalmological examination for congenital cataracts","Molecular genetic testing of PEX7, GNPAT and AGPS","Brain MRI and EEG to assess myelination and seizures"],"redFlags":["Recurrent aspiration or respiratory infection compromising ventilation","Refractory seizures","Severe feeding failure and failure to thrive","Rapid clinical deterioration in the neonatal or infantile period"],"prognosis":"The classic form carries a poor prognosis with severe developmental impairment and reduced survival, many affected children not surviving early childhood, although milder variants with residual enzyme function can survive longer with a less severe course.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the punctate calcification for warfarin embryopathy or X-linked chondrodysplasia punctata without biochemical testing","Checking only very-long-chain fatty acids, which are normal, and missing the low plasmalogens that establish the diagnosis","Overlooking treatable contributors such as elevated phytanic acid in the PEX7 form","Failing to arrange early cataract assessment and airway or feeding support"],"management":["Supportive care as there is no curative therapy, with anticonvulsants to control the frequent seizures","Consider dietary phytanic-acid restriction in the PEX7 form to limit phytanate accumulation","Nutritional support for feeding difficulty, including gastrostomy feeding where swallowing is unsafe","Proactive respiratory management with prompt treatment of recurrent chest infections and ventilatory support as needed","Cataract extraction and ophthalmological follow-up for the congenital cataracts","Physiotherapy, orthotics and orthopaedic care for joint contractures, rhizomelic deformity and spasticity","Coordinated metabolic, neurology, ophthalmology and palliative-care input given the poor prognosis","Genetic counselling with carrier and prenatal testing for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Rhizomelic Chondrodysplasia Punctata","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"rhizomelic_chondrodysplasia_punctata_type_1":{"name":"Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata type 1","system":"Metabolic / Mitochondrial & peroxisomal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RCDP1","PEX7-related RCDP","Peroxisomal plasmalogen biosynthesis deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["RCDP1 is an autosomal recessive peroxisomal disorder caused by biallelic PEX7 variants; PEX7 is the cytosolic receptor for the peroxisomal targeting signal type 2 (PTS2), so its loss impairs import of a specific subset of peroxisomal enzymes.","The biochemical signature is deficient plasmalogen (ether phospholipid) synthesis together with impaired phytanic acid alpha-oxidation, while very-long-chain fatty acid oxidation and beta-oxidation remain intact, distinguishing it from Zellweger spectrum disorders.","Classic features are symmetrical rhizomelic (proximal) shortening of the limbs, congenital cataracts, a characteristic dysmorphic facies, profound growth and psychomotor retardation, and progressive contractures.","Radiographs show punctate epiphyseal and extra-epiphyseal calcific stippling (chondrodysplasia punctata) and coronal clefts of the vertebral bodies, which fade over the first years of life.","PTS2-dependent enzymes affected include alkyl-dihydroxyacetone-phosphate synthase (plasmalogen synthesis), phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase (phytanic acid oxidation) and peroxisomal 3-ketoacyl-CoA thiolase.","Biochemical diagnosis rests on markedly reduced erythrocyte plasmalogens with raised plasma phytanic acid (often normal in the newborn period until dietary phytanate accumulates) and normal very-long-chain fatty acids.","A milder, non-rhizomelic phenotype with longer survival and residual plasmalogen synthesis occurs with hypomorphic PEX7 alleles, broadening the clinical spectrum.","Epilepsy, recurrent respiratory infections, feeding difficulty and cervical spinal stenosis contribute substantially to morbidity."],"pathophysiology":"PEX7 encodes the soluble cytosolic receptor that recognises the peroxisomal targeting signal type 2 (a cleavable N-terminal nonapeptide) and, in cooperation with PEX5L, delivers PTS2-bearing matrix enzymes into intact, structurally normal peroxisomes. In RCDP1 loss of functional PEX7 selectively blocks import of this small group of enzymes while peroxisome assembly and PTS1-dependent import (including most very-long-chain fatty acid beta-oxidation) proceed normally. Two consequences dominate. First, the enzymes of ether phospholipid (plasmalogen) synthesis, particularly alkyl-dihydroxyacetone-phosphate synthase, fail to reach the peroxisome, so plasmalogens, which are abundant structural lipids of myelin, cell membranes and surfactant and function as antioxidants, are severely deficient; this underlies the neurological, ocular and skeletal pathology. Second, phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase is mislocalised, impairing alpha-oxidation so that dietary phytanic acid accumulates over time and adds a toxic burden. The skeletal dysplasia reflects disturbed endochondral ossification with abnormal calcium deposition in cartilage producing epiphyseal stippling and defective growth-plate function that causes the striking rhizomelic limb shortening. Inheritance is autosomal recessive, and residual receptor activity from hypomorphic alleles correlates with milder, non-rhizomelic disease and longer survival.","additionalDifferentials":["Zellweger spectrum disorders and other peroxisome biogenesis defects (PEX1 and related genes)","RCDP types 2 and 3 (GNPAT and AGPS defects affecting plasmalogen synthesis directly)","X-linked chondrodysplasia punctata (Conradi-Hunermann, EBP related)","Brachytelephalangic (X-linked recessive) chondrodysplasia punctata from ARSE/ARSL deficiency","Warfarin embryopathy and maternal vitamin K deficiency causing stippled epiphyses","Maternal autoimmune disease or vitamin B12 deficiency associated chondrodysplasia punctata","Other skeletal dysplasias with rhizomelia such as achondroplasia group disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Erythrocyte plasmalogen levels (reduced) and the plasmalogen precursor ratio","Plasma phytanic acid (raised, age-dependent) with normal very-long-chain fatty acids","PEX7 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis and enable carrier/prenatal testing","Skeletal survey for epiphyseal stippling, rhizomelic shortening and coronal vertebral clefts","Ophthalmological assessment for congenital cataracts","Brain MRI for myelination and any migrational or progressive white-matter changes","Cervical spine imaging to assess for stenosis and instability"],"redFlags":["Congenital cataracts with proximal limb shortening in a neonate, prompting urgent peroxisomal evaluation","Respiratory compromise from recurrent aspiration or infection","Cervical cord compression from spinal stenosis or instability","Refractory seizures or acute neurological deterioration","Failure to thrive with severe feeding difficulty"],"prognosis":"Classic RCDP1 carries a poor prognosis with most severely affected children dying in infancy or early childhood, commonly from respiratory complications, though some survive into the early school years; milder non-rhizomelic variants have longer survival with less severe disability. Management is supportive and multidisciplinary, as no curative therapy exists.","pitfalls":["Relying on plasma phytanic acid in the newborn period, when it may be normal before dietary accumulation, and missing the diagnosis","Confusing RCDP with Zellweger spectrum disorders despite the normal very-long-chain fatty acids and intact peroxisome assembly in RCDP","Overlooking non-genetic phenocopies such as warfarin embryopathy or maternal autoimmune disease causing stippling","Attributing all chondrodysplasia punctata to a single gene rather than distinguishing the RCDP subtypes and X-linked forms","Failing to monitor for cervical spinal stenosis, which can cause preventable neurological deterioration"],"management":["Supportive multidisciplinary care as no therapy corrects the PEX7 import defect, with anticonvulsants for seizure control","Dietary restriction of phytanic acid to reduce accumulated phytanate once dietary intake begins","Aggressive treatment of respiratory infections and ventilatory support, since respiratory complications are the leading cause of death","Nutritional support and gastrostomy feeding for feeding difficulty and faltering growth","Cataract surgery and ophthalmological follow-up for the congenital cataracts","Physiotherapy and orthopaedic management of progressive contractures and scoliosis","Genetic counselling with carrier and prenatal testing for this autosomal recessive PEX7 disorder","Early palliative-care involvement in the classic severe phenotype"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard metabolic medicine and peroxisomal disorder references","Clinical genetics and dysmorphology reference texts","Paediatric skeletal dysplasia reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"rhodococcus_equi_infection":{"name":"Rhodococcus equi infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rhodococcus equi","Corynebacterium equi","Prescottella equi","rhodococcal pneumonia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rhodococcus equi is a soil-dwelling, partially acid-fast Gram-positive coccobacillus associated with horses and other livestock and their manure","It is a facultative intracellular pathogen that survives within macrophages, so disease occurs predominantly in patients with impaired cell-mediated immunity, classically advanced HIV and transplant recipients","The hallmark presentation is a subacute, granulomatous, cavitating pneumonia that develops over weeks with fever, cough, and weight loss","Chest imaging often shows upper-lobe consolidation with cavitation and sometimes an air-fluid level, and blood cultures are frequently positive in immunocompromised hosts","Extrapulmonary spread can produce brain abscess, osteomyelitis, lymphadenitis, and bacteraemia","The organism may be dismissed as a diphtheroid contaminant in the laboratory, so a compatible clinical picture should prompt careful identification, aided by its salmon-pink colonies","Treatment requires prolonged combination therapy, typically a macrolide-based regimen paired with agents such as rifampicin or a fluoroquinolone, together with restoration of immune function"],"pathophysiology":"Rhodococcus equi is an environmental actinomycete found in soil enriched by the manure of horses and other herbivores. Humans are usually infected by inhalation of contaminated dust, and less often through wound inoculation or ingestion. As a facultative intracellular organism it is taken up by alveolar macrophages, where a virulence plasmid encoding virulence-associated proteins allows it to survive and replicate within the phagosome, subverting normal killing. Effective containment depends on T-cell-mediated macrophage activation, which explains why serious disease is concentrated in patients with defective cellular immunity. Uncontrolled intracellular growth drives a granulomatous, necrotising, and often cavitating pneumonia; sometimes the histology shows malakoplakia with characteristic intracytoplasmic inclusions. Haematogenous dissemination can seed the brain, bone, and other sites.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary tuberculosis and non-tuberculous mycobacterial disease","Nocardiosis","Bacterial lung abscess","Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis or other fungal cavitary disease","Pneumocystis or other opportunistic pneumonia in HIV","Cavitating lung malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sputum, blood, and respiratory-sample cultures with careful identification of the coccobacillus (salmon-pink colonies, partial acid-fastness)","Chest radiography and CT to characterise cavitary upper-lobe disease","HIV testing with CD4 count and assessment of other immunosuppression","Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage or biopsy where sputum is unrevealing","Antimicrobial susceptibility testing to guide combination therapy","Imaging of the brain and other sites if dissemination is suspected"],"redFlags":["Cavitating pneumonia with bacteraemia in an immunocompromised patient","Neurological signs suggesting brain abscess from dissemination","Rapid clinical deterioration or respiratory failure","Relapse or persistent infection despite therapy indicating inadequate immune reconstitution"],"prognosis":"Outcome hinges on immune status and prompt, prolonged combination therapy; with restoration of cell-mediated immunity (for example effective antiretroviral therapy) many patients recover, but disseminated disease in profoundly immunosuppressed hosts carries appreciable mortality and a risk of relapse.","pitfalls":["Dismissing the isolate as a diphtheroid skin contaminant and missing a true pathogen","Using single-agent or short-course therapy, which promotes relapse and resistance","Overlooking the animal or soil exposure history in an immunocompromised patient with cavitary pneumonia","Failing to address the underlying immunosuppression, without which cure is difficult"],"management":["Prolonged combination antimicrobial therapy with intracellular-active agents such as macrolides, rifampicin, fluoroquinolones, aminoglycosides and, in severe disease, carbapenems or vancomycin, avoiding beta-lactam monotherapy because of relapse","Restore cell-mediated immunity by starting antiretroviral therapy in HIV and reducing immunosuppression in transplant recipients","Susceptibility testing to tailor the combination and guard against emergent resistance","Drainage or surgical resection of persistent abscesses or cavitary necrotic lung disease that fails to respond","Imaging and directed treatment of extrapulmonary foci such as brain abscess and osteomyelitis when dissemination is suspected","Prolonged clinical and radiological follow-up to detect relapse after stopping therapy","Coordinated infectious-diseases, HIV and transplant specialist input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","UpToDate: Rhodococcus equi infections"],"crossLinks":[]},"ribbing_disease":{"name":"ribbing disease","system":"musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["multiple diaphyseal sclerosis","hereditary multiple diaphyseal sclerosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Resnick Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Ribbing disease is a rare sclerosing dysplasia characterised by endosteal and periosteal new bone formation confined to the diaphyses of the long bones, most often the tibia and femur.","It typically presents after puberty in adults, distinguishing it from the childhood-onset Camurati-Engelmann disease it otherwise resembles radiologically.","Involvement is often asymmetrical and may be limited to one or a few bones, unlike the symmetrical pattern of Camurati-Engelmann disease.","The dominant symptom is deep, activity-related bone pain in the affected limb, and many cases are discovered when investigating unexplained leg pain.","There is no associated myopathy, cranial involvement or systemic features, which helps separate it from diaphyseal dysplasia with neuromuscular signs.","Bone scintigraphy shows increased uptake at active sites and helps map disease and exclude alternative causes of sclerosis.","It is frequently a diagnosis of exclusion after osteoid osteoma, chronic osteomyelitis, and sclerosing malignancy have been ruled out.","Management is largely symptomatic; the pain can be difficult to control and the natural history is variable."],"pathophysiology":"Ribbing disease is a disorder of bone modelling in which excessive endosteal and periosteal apposition thickens the diaphyseal cortex and narrows the medullary canal. The molecular basis is incompletely understood; some pedigrees suggest autosomal recessive inheritance, and it is regarded by many authorities as part of a spectrum with Camurati-Engelmann disease, which is linked to activating mutations in the TGF-beta 1 gene that increase osteoblastic bone formation. Unlike the generalised, symmetrical, childhood-onset TGF-beta 1 disorder, Ribbing disease is later in onset, focal and asymmetrical, and spares the skull and the neuromuscular system, implying a distinct or attenuated mechanism of dysregulated diaphyseal bone turnover.","additionalDifferentials":["Camurati-Engelmann diaphyseal dysplasia","osteoid osteoma","chronic sclerosing osteomyelitis","osteosarcoma or sclerotic metastasis","stress fracture with periosteal reaction","Paget disease of bone","chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["plain radiography of the long bones for diaphyseal cortical thickening","bone scintigraphy to localise active lesions","MRI to assess marrow and exclude a nidus or tumour","CT to characterise cortical sclerosis and exclude an osteoid osteoma nidus","inflammatory markers and cultures to exclude infection","family history assessment and, where indicated, TGFB1 gene testing"],"redFlags":["night pain unresponsive to rest suggesting osteoid osteoma or malignancy","systemic features such as fever or weight loss pointing to infection or tumour","a soft-tissue mass or cortical destruction on imaging","rapidly progressive symptoms warranting biopsy"],"prognosis":"Ribbing disease is benign and does not undergo malignant transformation, but chronic bone pain may be persistent and refractory, and the clinical course fluctuates over years.","pitfalls":["Mistaking focal diaphyseal sclerosis for osteosarcoma or metastasis and proceeding to aggressive intervention.","Overlooking a small osteoid osteoma nidus within thickened cortex.","Assuming symmetry and thereby confusing it with Camurati-Engelmann disease.","Attributing the pain to soft-tissue causes and delaying dedicated bone imaging.","Performing unnecessary biopsy when the radiological and clinical picture is characteristic."],"management":["Analgesia with simple analgesics and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for the chronic activity-related diaphyseal bone pain, as no disease-modifying treatment exists","Bisphosphonates trialled for refractory pain, recognising that response is inconsistent","Corticosteroids considered by analogy with the related Camurati-Engelmann disease, though evidence in Ribbing disease is limited","Exclude infection, osteoid osteoma and malignancy with imaging, cultures and biopsy before committing to long-term management","Activity modification and physiotherapy to reduce mechanical pain","Surgical intramedullary reaming or cortical decompression in selected cases of intractable pain","Reassurance about the benign, non-malignant nature with long-term follow-up given the fluctuating course"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Resnick Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders, current edition","Taybi and Lachman Radiology of Syndromes, Metabolic Disorders and Skeletal Dysplasias","Greenspan Orthopedic Imaging"],"crossLinks":[]},"riboflavin_transporter_deficiency":{"name":"Riboflavin transporter deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["riboflavin transporter deficiency neuronopathy","RTD","SLC52A2","SLC52A3","riboflavin-responsive MADD phenotype"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A treatable neuronopathy: any child with progressive deafness plus a motor and sensory neuronopathy deserves an empirical trial of high-dose riboflavin","Caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the riboflavin transporter genes SLC52A2 (RFVT2) or SLC52A3 (RFVT3), inherited recessively; it encompasses the Brown-Vialetto-Van Laere and Fazio-Londe phenotypes","The biochemical profile can mimic multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenation deficiency (MADD), with an abnormal acylcarnitine pattern and organic aciduria, because flavin cofactors are needed for fatty-acid oxidation flavoenzymes","Features include sensorineural deafness, bulbar palsy, axial and limb weakness, sensory ataxia, optic atrophy and respiratory compromise","Plasma flavin levels may be low but can be normal, so a normal result does not exclude the diagnosis","High-dose riboflavin supplementation frequently stabilises or reverses neurological decline and normalises the MADD-like metabolites","Treatment should be started on clinical suspicion rather than deferred until genetic confirmation","The MADD-like biochemistry can be a helpful clue that points towards a riboflavin-responsive disorder"],"pathophysiology":"Riboflavin transporter deficiency is caused by impaired cellular uptake of riboflavin (vitamin B2) owing to loss-of-function variants in SLC52A2 or SLC52A3. Reduced riboflavin uptake depletes the flavin cofactors FAD and FMN, which are essential for many flavoenzymes, including the acyl-CoA dehydrogenases and electron-transfer flavoprotein of mitochondrial fatty-acid beta-oxidation. Cofactor deficiency therefore produces a secondary, riboflavin-responsive phenocopy of multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenation deficiency, with a corresponding acylcarnitine and organic acid signature, and drives neurodegeneration of cranial motor nuclei, anterior horn cells and the auditory and optic pathways. Because the underlying problem is cofactor delivery, supraphysiological riboflavin can restore flavoenzyme function and improve both the metabolic and neurological abnormalities.","additionalDifferentials":["Genetic multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (glutaric aciduria type II)","Brown-Vialetto-Van Laere and Fazio-Londe presentations","Motor neurone disease and spinal muscular atrophy","Mitochondrial neuropathies with deafness","Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease with cranial involvement","Other treatable metabolic neuropathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of SLC52A2 and SLC52A3","Plasma acylcarnitine profile and urine organic acids for a MADD-like pattern","Plasma flavin (riboflavin/FAD) concentrations","Audiometry and brainstem auditory evoked responses","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography","Ophthalmological review for optic atrophy"],"redFlags":["Respiratory insufficiency from bulbar and diaphragmatic weakness","Rapidly progressive dysphagia with aspiration risk","Acute metabolic decompensation during catabolic illness","Progressive visual loss from optic atrophy"],"prognosis":"Without treatment the disorder is progressive and potentially fatal, but early high-dose riboflavin supplementation often halts and partly reverses neurological deterioration, making prompt recognition decisive for outcome.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing genetic MADD and missing the riboflavin-responsive transporter defect","Withholding empirical riboflavin pending genetic results","Interpreting normal plasma flavin levels as excluding the diagnosis","Attributing the picture to an untreatable motor neurone disease"],"management":["Start high-dose oral riboflavin supplementation empirically without awaiting genetic confirmation, as it frequently stabilises or reverses the neurological decline","Continue riboflavin lifelong to maintain flavin cofactor supply","Respiratory surveillance with ventilatory support for diaphragmatic weakness and hypoventilation","Audiological rehabilitation with hearing aids or cochlear implantation for sensorineural deafness","Nutritional support and safe-swallow assessment, with gastrostomy feeding for bulbar involvement","Physiotherapy, mobility aids and neurorehabilitation for the motor and sensory neuronopathy","Monitor the acylcarnitine and organic-acid profile for normalisation as an index of biochemical response","Genetic counselling with sibling testing and an empirical riboflavin trial for affected relatives, given autosomal recessive SLC52A2 or SLC52A3 variants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Riboflavin Transporter Deficiency Neuronopathy","OMIM entries for SLC52A2 and SLC52A3","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"ribose_5_phosphate_isomerase_deficiency":{"name":"Ribose-5-phosphate isomerase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RPI deficiency","RPIA deficiency","ribose-5-phosphate isomerase A deficiency","pentose phosphate pathway leukoencephalopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Extremely rare autosomal recessive defect of ribose-5-phosphate isomerase (RPIA gene) in the non-oxidative pentose phosphate pathway, one of the rarest reported inborn errors","Dominant feature is a slowly progressive leukoencephalopathy with developmental regression, seizures and peripheral neuropathy","Onset is typically in childhood with white-matter disease evident on MRI and characteristic accumulation of polyols on brain spectroscopy","Elevated ribitol and D-arabitol are found in body fluids and are markedly raised in cerebrospinal fluid and on cerebral magnetic resonance spectroscopy","The clinical course is remarkably slow compared with many leukoencephalopathies, sometimes spanning many years","No specific treatment exists, so care is supportive with management of seizures, spasticity and neurological disability","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating polyol accumulation (especially in CSF/brain) with confirmatory RPIA molecular and enzyme studies"],"pathophysiology":"Ribose-5-phosphate isomerase catalyses interconversion of ribose-5-phosphate and ribulose-5-phosphate in the non-oxidative branch of the pentose phosphate pathway. Deficiency disturbs pentose phosphate metabolism, leading to accumulation of the polyols ribitol and D-arabitol, which reach particularly high concentrations in the central nervous system. The resulting toxic or osmotic effects on the brain are thought to drive a progressive leukoencephalopathy with demyelination, alongside a peripheral neuropathy. Because the accumulated polyols concentrate in the brain, cerebral magnetic resonance spectroscopy is especially informative.","additionalDifferentials":["Other genetic leukoencephalopathies (e.g. metachromatic leukodystrophy, vanishing white matter disease)","Transaldolase deficiency and other pentose phosphate pathway disorders","Mitochondrial leukoencephalopathies","Polyol pathway disorders causing white-matter disease","Peroxisomal disorders with neurodegeneration","Acquired demyelinating disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to characterise the leukoencephalopathy","Cerebral magnetic resonance spectroscopy showing abnormal polyol peaks","Measurement of ribitol and D-arabitol in urine, plasma and cerebrospinal fluid","Nerve conduction studies to assess peripheral neuropathy","RPIA molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Enzyme activity assay in fibroblasts where genetics are inconclusive"],"redFlags":["Progressive developmental regression with an evolving leukoencephalopathy","New or worsening seizures alongside white-matter disease","Progressive peripheral neuropathy with weakness","Marked polyol elevation in cerebrospinal fluid or on spectroscopy"],"prognosis":"The disorder is progressive but often unusually slowly, with accumulating neurological disability over years; there is no curative therapy and long-term outcome depends on the extent of central and peripheral nervous system involvement.","pitfalls":["Not requesting polyol analysis of cerebrospinal fluid or spectroscopy in unexplained leukoencephalopathy","Attributing the white-matter changes to a mitochondrial or acquired demyelinating cause without metabolic workup","Overlooking the coexisting peripheral neuropathy","Expecting a rapid course and dismissing the diagnosis because progression is slow"],"management":["Supportive care only, as no curative or substrate-lowering therapy exists for this pentose-phosphate-pathway defect","Anticonvulsants to control seizures","Antispasticity treatment with agents such as baclofen alongside physiotherapy","Neurorehabilitation and symptomatic management of the peripheral neuropathy","Developmental and educational support with assistive devices for progressive disability","Serial brain MRI and magnetic resonance spectroscopy to monitor leukoencephalopathy and polyol accumulation","Genetic counselling and family testing for this autosomal recessive RPIA disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: Disorders of the Pentose Phosphate Pathway","Saudubray, Baumgartner & Walter, Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"richner_hanhart_syndrome":{"name":"Richner-Hanhart syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tyrosinaemia type II","oculocutaneous tyrosinaemia","TAT deficiency","tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews / Scriver's Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic combination is painful pseudodendritic keratitis with palmoplantar keratoderma, often with variable intellectual disability","Autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in TAT, encoding hepatic tyrosine aminotransferase (tyrosinaemia type II)","Ocular symptoms such as photophobia, tearing and redness can precede skin lesions and may be misdiagnosed as herpetic keratitis","Painful hyperkeratotic lesions affect the palms and soles and can be a prominent complaint","Marked hypertyrosinaemia is the biochemical signature, with raised plasma tyrosine and increased urinary tyrosine metabolites","Unlike tyrosinaemia type I, there is no hepatorenal disease; the toxicity is due to tyrosine crystal deposition","Dietary restriction of tyrosine and phenylalanine improves the ocular and cutaneous features and may protect neurodevelopment","Corneal lesions are typically bilateral, which helps distinguish them from infective dendritic ulcers"],"pathophysiology":"Richner-Hanhart syndrome is caused by deficiency of hepatic cytosolic tyrosine aminotransferase, the first enzyme of tyrosine catabolism, due to biallelic TAT variants. The block causes tyrosine to accumulate in blood and tissues. Supersaturated tyrosine crystallises within cells, and the resulting crystalline deposition triggers an inflammatory response in the cornea and skin, producing the characteristic pseudodendritic keratitis and painful palmoplantar keratoderma. Elevated tyrosine may also affect the developing brain, accounting for the variable intellectual disability. Because the disorder does not involve the downstream toxic metabolites of tyrosinaemia type I, the liver and kidneys are spared; lowering tyrosine through dietary restriction of tyrosine and phenylalanine reverses the crystal-driven ocular and skin disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex dendritic keratitis","Tyrosinaemia type I and type III","Transient neonatal tyrosinaemia","Inherited palmoplantar keratodermas","Other causes of infantile keratitis or photophobia","Vitamin A-related or nutritional keratopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma amino acids showing marked hypertyrosinaemia","Urine organic acids for tyrosine metabolites","TAT molecular genetic testing","Slit-lamp examination for bilateral pseudodendritic keratitis","Dermatological assessment of palmoplantar keratoderma","Developmental assessment"],"redFlags":["Severe or ulcerating keratitis threatening vision","Marked, persistent hypertyrosinaemia despite treatment","Progressive developmental or behavioural concerns","Diagnostic confusion with recurrent herpetic keratitis leading to inappropriate antiviral or steroid use"],"prognosis":"With early dietary restriction of tyrosine and phenylalanine the ocular and skin lesions resolve and neurodevelopmental outcome can be favourable; untreated disease risks corneal scarring and cognitive impairment.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the bilateral pseudodendritic keratitis as herpes simplex and treating accordingly","Overlooking hypertyrosinaemia as the unifying cause of eye and skin findings","Assuming hepatorenal involvement as in tyrosinaemia type I","Delaying dietary treatment and allowing corneal or neurological damage"],"management":["Dietary restriction of tyrosine and phenylalanine, the definitive treatment that resolves the keratitis and keratoderma","Supplement with a tyrosine- and phenylalanine-free medical protein substitute to meet nutritional needs","Regular monitoring of plasma tyrosine to guide dietary control","Ophthalmological management of the pseudodendritic keratitis, avoiding antivirals mistakenly given for presumed herpetic keratitis","Dermatological care of the painful palmoplantar keratoderma with emollients and keratolytics","Developmental assessment and educational support","Dietitian-led multidisciplinary metabolic care","Genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive TAT disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["OMIM entry for Tyrosinemia Type II (TAT)","Scriver's The Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"richter_transformation":{"name":"Richter transformation","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Richter syndrome","RT","DLBCL-type Richter transformation","Hodgkin-type Richter transformation","transformation of chronic lymphocytic leukaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The development of an aggressive lymphoma, most often diffuse large B-cell lymphoma and less commonly classic Hodgkin lymphoma, arising in a patient with chronic lymphocytic leukaemia or small lymphocytic lymphoma","Suggested clinically by rapid nodal enlargement, new B symptoms, a sharply rising lactate dehydrogenase and often disproportionate growth at one site","Most large-cell transformations are clonally related to the underlying chronic lymphocytic leukaemia and carry a poor prognosis, whereas clonally unrelated cases behave more like de novo lymphoma","PET-CT helps localise the most metabolically active site to direct biopsy, and histological confirmation by biopsy is essential","High-risk molecular features of the original disease, particularly TP53 disruption and NOTCH1 mutation, and prior therapy are associated with transformation","Hodgkin-type transformation is frequently Epstein-Barr virus associated and is treated along Hodgkin lymphoma lines","Management is challenging and includes chemoimmunotherapy, consideration of HSCT in fit responders, and enrolment in trials of novel targeted and cellular therapies"],"pathophysiology":"Richter transformation represents the histological progression of chronic lymphocytic leukaemia or small lymphocytic lymphoma into an aggressive lymphoma. In the majority of large-cell cases the transformed clone is genetically derived from the original leukaemic clone, having acquired additional driver lesions such as TP53 disruption, MYC activation, CDKN2A loss and NOTCH1 mutation that confer a proliferative, chemoresistant phenotype. A minority of large-cell cases and many Hodgkin-type transformations are clonally unrelated and, in the latter, Epstein-Barr virus frequently contributes within the immunosuppressed microenvironment of the underlying disease. The clonal relationship and the accumulated genetic damage largely determine the aggressive behaviour and generally poor response to therapy.","additionalDifferentials":["Progression or accelerated phase of chronic lymphocytic leukaemia without transformation","De novo diffuse large B-cell lymphoma unrelated to the leukaemia","Prolymphocytic transformation of chronic lymphocytic leukaemia","Classic Hodgkin lymphoma arising in chronic lymphocytic leukaemia","Therapy-related myeloid neoplasm or second malignancy","Infection causing rapid lymphadenopathy in an immunocompromised patient"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PET-CT to identify the most metabolically active node for targeted biopsy","Excisional or core biopsy of the suspicious site for histological confirmation","Immunophenotyping and clonality studies to assess relationship to the underlying clone","Molecular testing for TP53, NOTCH1 and MYC abnormalities","Epstein-Barr virus assessment, particularly for Hodgkin-type transformation","Lactate dehydrogenase, blood counts and staging investigations"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging or asymmetrical lymphadenopathy in a patient with known chronic lymphocytic leukaemia","Steeply rising lactate dehydrogenase with new B symptoms","Localised bulky or extranodal mass causing compressive symptoms","Hypercalcaemia or rapid clinical deterioration","Tumour lysis risk with high tumour burden at treatment initiation"],"prognosis":"Clonally related diffuse large B-cell-type Richter transformation carries a poor prognosis with short survival despite intensive therapy, particularly when TP53 is disrupted; clonally unrelated cases and Hodgkin-type transformation generally fare better, and allogeneic HSCT offers the main prospect of durable remission in eligible responders.","pitfalls":["Attributing new symptoms to progressive chronic lymphocytic leukaemia and not biopsying to confirm transformation","Biopsying a low-uptake site rather than the most PET-avid lesion","Failing to assess the clonal relationship, which strongly influences prognosis","Overlooking Hodgkin-type transformation, which requires a different treatment approach","Underestimating the tumour lysis and chemoresistance risk of the transformed clone"],"management":["Confirm the diagnosis with a PET-directed excisional or core biopsy of the most metabolically active site before treatment","Treat diffuse large B-cell-type transformation with anthracycline-based immunochemotherapy such as rituximab combined with CHOP","Treat Hodgkin-type transformation along Hodgkin lymphoma lines with an ABVD-type regimen","Consolidate responding, eligible patients with allogeneic or autologous haematopoietic stem cell transplantation","Enrol in clinical trials of novel agents, including targeted therapies, immune-checkpoint inhibitors and CAR T-cell therapy, given generally poor outcomes","Assess TP53, NOTCH1 and MYC status and clonal relationship to the underlying chronic lymphocytic leukaemia for prognosis and treatment planning","Provide supportive care with tumour-lysis prophylaxis, infection prophylaxis and transfusion support, managed by a haemato-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","British Society for Haematology guidance on chronic lymphocytic leukaemia"],"crossLinks":[]},"rickettsial_diseases":{"name":"Rickettsial Diseases","system":"Infectious / Multisystem (vasculotropic)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rickettsioses","Spotted fever and typhus group infections","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Mediterranean spotted fever (boutonneuse fever)","African tick-bite fever","Rickettsialpox","Epidemic (louse-borne) typhus","Endemic (murine) typhus","Scrub typhus","Ehrlichiosis (HME)","Anaplasmosis (HGA)","Q fever"],"chapter":192,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1454","clinicalPearls":["Rickettsiae are small, obligately intracellular, gram-negative coccobacilli/short bacilli, most spread by a tick, mite, flea, or louse; except for louse-borne typhus, humans are incidental hosts. The agents span seven genera (Rickettsia, Orientia, Ehrlichia, Anaplasma, Neorickettsia, Candidatus Neoehrlichia, and Coxiella).","During the first roughly 5 days the acute presentations are largely indistinguishable: fever, headache, and myalgia, with or without nausea, vomiting, and cough; later differentiating features such as rash, eschar, pneumonitis, or meningoencephalitis emerge. Heightened clinical suspicion based on exposure history should prompt empiric antibiotics rather than waiting for confirmation (p.1454).","A characteristic laboratory triad supports the diagnosis across many of these infections: thrombocytopenia, a normal or low white-cell count, mildly to moderately elevated hepatic aminotransferases, and hyponatremia (p.1454).","Coxiella burnetii, R. prowazekii, and R. typhi can survive long periods outside the host and are highly infectious by inhalation (a single Coxiella organism can cause pneumonia), making several agents bioterrorism concerns (p.1454).","In Rocky Mountain spotted fever the rash is progressive and unreliable: present in only about 14% on day 1 and roughly 49% by day 3, appearing on day 6 or later in ~20% and never appearing in about 10% of cases; classic palm/sole involvement tends to come late and may not occur at all (p.1456-1457).","Only about 3% of RMSF patients show the classic triad of fever, rash, and tick exposure in the first 3 days, and only ~60% recall a tick bite; serology is typically negative at presentation, so treatment must not await a positive result (p.1457).","An eschar (tache noire) at the bite site is a useful clue in several spotted fevers (notably African tick-bite fever ~90%, rickettsialpox, Mediterranean spotted fever ~50%) and in scrub typhus, but is absent in the typhus group (epidemic and murine typhus have no eschar) (p.1455).","Ehrlichioses/anaplasmoses (Anaplasmataceae) target hematopoietic cells: E. chaffeensis (HME) and E. muris infect mononuclear phagocytes, while E. ewingii and A. phagocytophilum (HGA) infect neutrophils; intracytoplasmic morulae may be seen on blood smear but only in a minority (about 20% or less in HME, 20-75% in HGA) (p.1460-1462).","Of the Rickettsiaceae, in descending case-fatality order the life-threatening species are R. rickettsii (RMSF), R. prowazekii (louse-borne typhus), Orientia tsutsugamushi (scrub typhus), R. conorii (MSF), and R. typhi (murine typhus); many spotted-fever species (e.g., R. parkeri, R. africae, R. akari, R. felis) have never been documented to cause a fatal illness (p.1456).","Of the rickettsial agents, only C. burnetii is documented to cause chronic illness, and only R. prowazekii causes a recrudescent illness (Brill-Zinsser disease) when latent infection reactivates years after the acute episode (p.1454, p.1459).","Q fever is an inhalational zoonosis from sheep, goats, and cattle (organism shed heavily in parturition material); the infectious dose is extremely low (probably 1-10 organisms) and ~60% of infections are asymptomatic seroconversions. Acute disease presents geographically as either pneumonia or hepatitis (p.1462).","Scrub typhus shows remarkable antigenic diversity with rapid loss of cross-protective immunity, so reinfection can occur within weeks to months; the classic eschar/rash/lymphadenopathy triad is often absent, with under half of travelers developing an eschar and under 40% a rash (p.1460)."],"pathophysiology":"The spotted fever and typhus group organisms (Rickettsia and Orientia) infect vascular endothelial cells and disseminate lymphohematogenously, producing widespread small-vessel infection. The fundamental injury is increased vascular permeability rather than occlusive thrombosis or ischemic necrosis; resulting edema, hypovolemia, and tissue ischemia drive the clinical picture. Platelet consumption causes thrombocytopenia in roughly a third to half of RMSF cases, but overt DIC with hypofibrinogenemia is rare; thrombin generation and fibrinolysis appear to be homeostatic responses to nonocclusive hemostatic plugs at sites of endothelial injury. Hyponatremia reflects appropriate ADH secretion in response to hypovolemia. Pathogenic rickettsiae have small, reductively evolved genomes lacking biosynthetic genes for intracellularly available molecules. Ehrlichia and Anaplasma instead parasitize vacuoles of hematopoietic cells; vasculitis and granulomas are not features of HME or HGA. Coxiella burnetii is genetically distinct from the Rickettsiaceae, survives harsh environments, escapes macrophage killing by inhibiting the final step of phagosome maturation, and adapts to the acidic phagolysosome.","additionalDifferentials":["Influenza and other viral febrile illnesses","Enteroviral infection and infectious mononucleosis","Viral hepatitis","Leptospirosis","Typhoid (enteric) fever","Gram-negative or gram-positive bacterial sepsis / toxic shock syndrome","Meningococcemia and disseminated gonococcal infection","Measles (rubeola), rubella, secondary syphilis","Drug hypersensitivity reaction","Immune thrombocytopenic purpura and thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Kawasaki disease","Immune-complex vasculitis","Bacterial or viral meningoencephalitis (when CNS involvement predominates)","Acute abdomen / enterocolitis (when GI symptoms predominate, has prompted laparotomy)","Culture-negative endocarditis (chronic Q fever)"],"infectionMimics":["Other rickettsioses within the group (RMSF vs HME, HGA, murine typhus, sylvatic flying-squirrel typhus, rickettsialpox can mimic one another)","Epidemic typhus is frequently misdiagnosed as typhoid fever in tropical settings","HGA can mimic atypical or severe Lyme disease and patients may develop Lyme antibodies without true co-infection","Leptospirosis, dengue, and other tropical febrile illnesses","Q fever pneumonia mimics community-acquired pneumonia of other etiologies; Q fever hepatitis mimics other granulomatous hepatitides"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Immune thrombocytopenic purpura / thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Kawasaki disease","Drug hypersensitivity / drug rash","Immune-complex (leukocytoclastic) vasculitis","Acute abdomen prompting exploratory laparotomy when abdominal pain and tenderness dominate"],"additionalInvestigations":["Indirect immunofluorescence (IFA) serology on paired acute and convalescent sera is the mainstay; for RMSF a titer of >=64 is not detectable until ~7-10 days after onset (IgG sensitivity 89-100%, specificity 99-100%), so it is usually negative at presentation","Immunohistologic (immunohistochemical) examination of a 3-mm punch biopsy of a rash lesion for R. rickettsii is ~70% sensitive and 100% specific - the most useful test during acute RMSF","PCR on skin/eschar biopsy or swab (higher yield than blood); PCR of peripheral blood is generally insufficiently sensitive for RMSF and typhus because organisms are concentrated in endothelium, not circulation","Peripheral-blood smear for intracytoplasmic morulae (ehrlichiosis/anaplasmosis); cell-culture isolation is feasible but rarely done due to technical difficulty and biohazard","For Q fever, IFA is the serologic gold standard distinguishing acute (phase II IgG seroconversion, fourfold rise) from chronic disease (high phase I IgG, e.g. titer >512 or >=1:800/1:1024); ELISA and complement fixation are alternatives; PCR on blood/serum can be positive in the first 1-2 weeks","For chronic Q fever, imaging including transesophageal echocardiography (higher vegetation detection than transthoracic) and FDG-PET/CT to detect valvular, intravascular, osteomyelitic, or lymphadenitic foci","Supportive labs: CBC (thrombocytopenia, leukopenia/neutropenia), hepatic aminotransferases, serum sodium, acute-phase proteins (CRP, procalcitonin, ferritin, fibrinogen), creatine kinase in suspected myositis; CSF analysis when meningoencephalitis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Petechial or hemorrhagic rash, especially with palm/sole involvement, in a febrile patient with possible tick exposure (advanced RMSF)","Progressive worsening over days after an initial viral-seeming presentation - mandates re-evaluation, as RMSF mimics viral illness early","Altered mental status, confusion, lethargy, stupor, ataxia, seizures, or coma (meningoencephalitis)","Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, ARDS, or need for mechanical ventilation","Hypotension, sepsis/septic-shock-like or toxic-shock-like presentation with multiorgan failure","Fulminant RMSF (death within 5 days), seen most often in Black male patients with G6PD deficiency","Acute kidney injury / oliguria, jaundice, gastrointestinal bleeding (hematemesis/melena)","Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis or a DIC-like picture in ehrlichiosis/anaplasmosis","Immunocompromise (diabetes, cancer, transplant, asplenia, hepatitis C, HIV, immunosuppressive drugs) markedly raises risk of severe/fatal ehrlichiosis and anaplasmosis","Culture-negative endocarditis, infected aneurysm, or infected vascular prosthesis suggesting chronic Q fever"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on early recognition and prompt empiric therapy. RMSF mortality was 20-25% before antibiotics and is roughly 3-5% now, principally driven by diagnostic/treatment delay; confirmed-case fatality is higher (~9-10% in regions where R. rickettsii is the sole spotted-fever species), is highest in children under 10 and adults over 70, and approaches 50% in parts of Mexico and Brazil for unknown reasons. Untreated fatal RMSF typically dies 8-15 days after onset. Survivors usually recover fully, but severe disease can leave permanent neurologic deficits or gangrene requiring amputation. Mediterranean spotted fever has a severe form (~50% mortality) in patients with diabetes, alcoholism, or heart failure. Epidemic (louse-borne) typhus is fatal in 7-40% of untreated cases. Untreated classic scrub typhus has ~6% case-fatality. Acute Q fever case-fatality is ~1-2%; chronic Q fever carries high mortality (~25% in proven Dutch-outbreak cases, highest at 33% when both endocarditis and vascular infection are present). Q fever fatigue syndrome follows up to 20% of acute cases. Many SFG species (e.g., R. parkeri, R. africae, R. akari, R. felis) have never caused documented fatal illness, and African tick-bite fever is typically mild.","pitfalls":["Waiting for serologic confirmation before treating - serology is usually negative at acute presentation; delay worsens outcome and empiric doxycycline should not be withheld","Excluding RMSF because there is no rash or no recalled tick bite (~10% never rash; only ~60% recall a bite) or because palms/soles are not involved","Interpreting a single acute-phase positive titer as diagnostic in endemic areas - cross-reactive preexisting antibody (e.g., from the highly prevalent R. amblyommatis or in HGA-endemic regions) can mislead; the recent apparent RMSF surge largely reflects cross-reactive single-titer enzyme immunoassays rather than true R. rickettsii disease","Relying on blood PCR for RMSF or typhus - insufficiently sensitive because organisms reside in endothelium, not blood; skin/eschar biopsy is preferable","Assuming negative HME serology because E. chaffeensis tests can cross-react when the true agent is another Ehrlichia; separate specific tests are needed for HME vs HGA","Overlooking chronic Q fever as a cause of culture-negative endocarditis or unexplained infected aneurysm/vascular prosthesis - median latency from primary infection is ~13 months and can exceed 9 years","Mislabeling epidemic typhus as typhoid fever in tropical/resource-limited settings","Attributing prolonged post-Q-fever fatigue to active infection and prescribing antibiotics - a randomized trial showed long-term doxycycline did not reduce fatigue versus placebo"],"management":["Give empirical doxycycline of the tetracycline class promptly on clinical and epidemiological suspicion, without waiting for serological confirmation, as the mainstay across spotted-fever, typhus and ehrlichial or anaplasmal infections","Use chloramphenicol only as an alternative in specific circumstances, since doxycycline remains preferred even in children and pregnancy for Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Treat Q fever with doxycycline, adding hydroxychloroquine for chronic endocarditic or endovascular disease","Provide supportive care for the increased vascular permeability with judicious fluid resuscitation and organ support in severe disease","Monitor for and manage complications including encephalitis, respiratory failure, disseminated intravascular coagulation and renal impairment","Advise vector avoidance, protective clothing and prompt tick removal","Notify public-health authorities and pursue echocardiographic and imaging surveillance for chronic Q-fever endovascular foci"],"severityClassification":"No single unified rickettsial severity score; severity is judged by organ-system involvement and progression. Across the group, escalating clinical lines after nonspecific fever are: (1) macular/maculopapular rash; (2) eschar at the bite site; (3) vesicular rash; (4) pneumonitis with radiographic opacities/rales; (5) meningoencephalitis; and (6) progressive hypotension and multiorgan failure (sepsis/toxic-shock-like). For Q fever, the Dutch consensus group classifies chronic disease as proven, probable, or possible based on PCR positivity in blood/tissue or phase I IgG IFA >=1:800/1:1024 combined with endocarditis (modified Duke criteria), proven large-vessel/prosthetic infection on imaging, or compatible clinical and histologic findings.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1454","Harrison 22e p.1455","Harrison 22e p.1456","Harrison 22e p.1457","Harrison 22e p.1458","Harrison 22e p.1459","Harrison 22e p.1460","Harrison 22e p.1461","Harrison 22e p.1462","Harrison 22e p.1463"],"crossLinks":[]},"rickettsialpox":{"name":"Rickettsialpox","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rickettsia akari infection","vesicular rickettsiosis","Kew Gardens spotted fever"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by Rickettsia akari and transmitted by the bite of the house-mouse mite (Liponyssoides sanguineus), with the domestic mouse serving as the reservoir","Classically reported in urban settings with mouse infestations, so a history of living in an apartment block or area with rodents is a useful clue","The illness begins with a painless papule at the mite bite site that evolves into an eschar, appearing before the onset of fever","After an incubation of roughly 7-10 days fever, headache, myalgia and sweats develop, followed by a generalised papulovesicular rash","The vesicular rash can be mistaken for chickenpox, but the presence of an eschar and the epidemiology help distinguish it","Doxycycline produces a rapid response and is the treatment of choice, given empirically on clinical grounds","The disease is characteristically mild and self-limiting, and deaths have not been reported, distinguishing it from the more dangerous spotted fevers"],"pathophysiology":"Rickettsia akari is an obligate intracellular member of the spotted fever group that is introduced into the skin by the feeding mouse mite. It infects vascular endothelium, producing a focal dermal vasculitis at the inoculation site that forms the initial papule and subsequent eschar. Haematogenous dissemination leads to a mild generalised endothelial infection manifesting as the disseminated papulovesicular rash, but the vasculitis is limited in severity, accounting for the benign, self-limiting course.","additionalDifferentials":["Varicella (chickenpox)","Disseminated herpes simplex or zoster","Other spotted fever group rickettsioses","Smallpox or monkeypox (historically important)","Disseminated gonococcaemia","Papular urticaria or arthropod bite reaction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Indirect immunofluorescence antibody serology with cross-reaction across the spotted fever group","PCR of blood, eschar or a vesicle biopsy","Immunohistochemistry of an eschar or skin biopsy","Full blood count, which may show mild leucopenia","Skin biopsy histology of the eschar or vesicle to demonstrate the characteristic vasculitis"],"redFlags":["Failure of the rash to respond to appropriate therapy, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Widespread haemorrhagic or necrotic lesions, which are atypical and suggest an alternative diagnosis","Severe systemic toxicity, pointing away from rickettsialpox toward a more dangerous rickettsiosis","Immunocompromise altering the expected benign course"],"prognosis":"Rickettsialpox is a benign self-limiting illness that resolves within a week or two even without treatment, and doxycycline shortens the fever; no deaths have been attributed to it.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the vesicular rash as chickenpox and missing the diagnostic eschar","Overlooking the rodent and mite exposure history","Failing to search the skin for the initial eschar","Assuming a benign course means no treatment is warranted when doxycycline hastens recovery"],"management":["Give empirical doxycycline on clinical grounds, which produces a rapid response and is the treatment of choice","Offer symptomatic care with antipyretics and analgesia, as the illness is self-limiting even untreated","Reassure regarding the benign course and distinguish the eschar and epidemiology from chickenpox","Undertake rodent and mite control to eradicate mouse infestation and prevent further cases","Confirm with serology, PCR or eschar immunohistochemistry where the diagnosis is uncertain"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"riedel_thyroiditis":{"name":"Riedel thyroiditis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Riedel's thyroiditis","invasive fibrous thyroiditis","Riedel struma","chronic fibrosing thyroiditis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / thyroid disease reference literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare fibrosing thyroiditis presenting as a hard, fixed, painless goitre that feels woody or stony on palpation","The fibrosis extends beyond the thyroid capsule into surrounding neck structures, causing compressive symptoms such as dysphagia, stridor and hoarseness","Now recognised as part of the IgG4-related disease spectrum and may coexist with other fibrosclerotic disorders such as retroperitoneal fibrosis and sclerosing cholangitis","Most patients are initially euthyroid, but progressive gland destruction can lead to hypothyroidism and, if the parathyroids are involved, hypoparathyroidism","The fixed, infiltrative mass closely mimics anaplastic thyroid carcinoma and lymphoma, so malignancy must be excluded","Fine-needle aspiration is often non-diagnostic because of dense fibrosis, and open surgical biopsy is usually required to confirm the diagnosis","Management combines glucocorticoids, other immunosuppressive or antifibrotic therapy, and limited surgery to relieve compression rather than resection"],"pathophysiology":"Riedel thyroiditis is characterised by replacement of thyroid parenchyma with dense invasive fibrous tissue that infiltrates the capsule and adjacent cervical structures. It is increasingly regarded as a manifestation of IgG4-related disease, with a lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate rich in IgG4-positive plasma cells, obliterative phlebitis and storiform fibrosis. The relentless fibrosclerotic process entraps and compresses local structures, and destruction of thyroid tissue and occasionally the parathyroid glands leads to hormonal insufficiency over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma","Thyroid lymphoma","Fibrous variant of Hashimoto thyroiditis","Subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis","Fibromatosis or other neck mass","Infiltrative metastatic disease of the thyroid"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Neck ultrasound and cross-sectional imaging to define the extent of infiltration and compression","Open (surgical) biopsy for definitive histology when aspiration is non-diagnostic","Serum IgG4 and assessment for associated fibrosclerotic disease","Thyroid function tests to detect hypothyroidism","Calcium and parathyroid hormone to identify hypoparathyroidism","Laryngoscopy to assess vocal-cord function where hoarseness is present"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging neck mass or stridor indicating airway compromise","Features that cannot confidently exclude anaplastic carcinoma or lymphoma","Progressive dysphagia or aspiration risk","Hypocalcaemia from parathyroid involvement"],"prognosis":"The condition is chronic but not malignant; medical therapy can arrest progression and relieve symptoms, though established fibrosis and hormonal deficiencies are usually irreversible.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the fixed hard goitre for anaplastic carcinoma, or vice versa, without adequate biopsy","Relying on fine-needle aspiration, which is often uninformative in dense fibrosis","Attempting radical thyroidectomy, which is technically hazardous given the infiltration","Overlooking associated IgG4-related fibrosclerotic disease elsewhere","Missing evolving hypothyroidism or hypoparathyroidism during follow-up"],"management":["Glucocorticoids as first-line therapy to arrest the fibrosclerotic process and relieve compressive symptoms","Tamoxifen to suppress fibroblast proliferation, alone or with corticosteroids","Steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as rituximab or mycophenolate for refractory IgG4-related disease","Levothyroxine replacement for hypothyroidism, and calcium with active vitamin D for hypoparathyroidism if the parathyroids are involved","Limited surgical decompression such as wedge isthmusectomy to relieve tracheal or oesophageal compression, rather than radical resection","Open surgical biopsy to exclude anaplastic carcinoma and lymphoma, with surveillance for associated fibrosclerotic disease such as retroperitoneal fibrosis","Endocrinology, otolaryngology and surgical multidisciplinary follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Reference literature on IgG4-related disease and fibrosing thyroiditis"],"crossLinks":[]},"rieger_syndrome":{"name":"Rieger Syndrome","system":"Ophthalmic / Craniofacial (Multisystem genetic)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome type 1","PITX2-related Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome","ARS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and ophthalmology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rieger syndrome is the systemic form of the Axenfeld-Rieger spectrum, combining bilateral anterior segment dysgenesis of the eye with extraocular features, classically dental and umbilical anomalies.","It is most characteristically caused by heterozygous variants in PITX2 (Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome type 1); FOXC1 variants (type 3) tend to give predominantly ocular disease with fewer systemic features, which helps subtype the spectrum.","The ocular hallmark is anterior segment dysgenesis: posterior embryotoxon (a prominent, anteriorly displaced Schwalbe line), iris strands bridging to the cornea, iris hypoplasia, corectopia and polycoria.","Glaucoma develops in a substantial proportion of patients, often in childhood or early adulthood, and is the principal threat to vision, so lifelong intraocular pressure surveillance is essential.","The systemic hallmarks that distinguish Rieger syndrome from isolated Axenfeld anomaly are dental anomalies (hypodontia, microdontia, oligodontia) and periumbilical skin redundancy or a prominent umbilical stump.","Additional features can include maxillary hypoplasia with a flat midface, hypospadias, pituitary and growth hormone abnormalities, and, with some PITX2 or genomic changes, cardiac defects.","Inheritance is autosomal dominant with variable expressivity, so relatives should be examined; a mildly affected parent may have only posterior embryotoxon.","The condition is distinct from Peters anomaly and from iridocorneal endothelial syndrome, which is acquired and unilateral rather than bilateral and congenital."],"pathophysiology":"Rieger syndrome is an autosomal dominant neurocristopathy of anterior segment and craniofacial development, most often caused by heterozygous loss-of-function or dominant-negative variants in PITX2, a paired-like homeodomain transcription factor (Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome type 1); a subset arises from variants in or copy-number changes of FOXC1, a forkhead transcription factor (type 3). Both genes regulate the migration and differentiation of neural crest-derived mesenchyme that forms the structures of the anterior eye, the periocular skeleton, the teeth, and the umbilical region during embryogenesis. Disruption of this transcriptional programme produces incomplete cleavage and maldevelopment of the anterior chamber angle, iris and trabecular meshwork, giving the characteristic posterior embryotoxon, iridocorneal adhesions, iris hypoplasia and pupillary displacement, together with impaired aqueous outflow that predisposes to glaucoma. Because the same developmental pathways act in tooth bud formation, midfacial and umbilical development, and to a lesser extent pituitary and cardiac morphogenesis, the ocular anomalies are accompanied by the syndromic dental, umbilical and craniofacial features that define the systemic Rieger phenotype. Variable expressivity is typical, and the specific gene involved influences the balance of ocular versus systemic manifestations.","additionalDifferentials":["Axenfeld anomaly / Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome type 3 (FOXC1) — predominantly ocular, fewer systemic features","Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome type 2 (chromosome 13q locus) variant","Iridogoniodysgenesis and isolated posterior embryotoxon","Peters anomaly and other anterior segment dysgeneses","Primary congenital glaucoma","Iridocorneal endothelial (ICE) syndrome — acquired, unilateral","SHORT syndrome and other syndromes with anterior segment dysgenesis and systemic features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination and gonioscopy to characterise posterior embryotoxon, iris strands and angle dysgenesis","Intraocular pressure measurement and optic nerve assessment for glaucoma, with visual field testing where feasible","Anterior segment imaging (optical coherence tomography or ultrasound biomicroscopy)","Dental and orthopantomogram evaluation for hypodontia and microdontia","Molecular genetic testing of PITX2 and FOXC1 including copy-number analysis","Examination of relatives for subclinical anterior segment signs","Endocrine assessment for growth hormone or pituitary involvement when growth is affected"],"redFlags":["Rising intraocular pressure, optic disc cupping or field loss indicating developing glaucoma","Progressive corneal changes or reduced vision in a child with anterior segment dysgenesis","Growth failure suggesting pituitary or growth hormone involvement","Corectopia with progressive pupillary distortion threatening the visual axis","New affected relative identified, indicating need for family-wide ophthalmic screening"],"prognosis":"Systemic health and lifespan are generally normal, but visual prognosis depends chiefly on the development and control of glaucoma, which can cause irreversible optic nerve damage if undetected. With lifelong intraocular pressure monitoring and timely treatment many patients retain useful vision.","pitfalls":["Missing isolated posterior embryotoxon in a parent and thereby underestimating the autosomal dominant risk","Focusing on the eye and overlooking the diagnostic dental and umbilical clues that define the syndrome","Assuming intraocular pressure is safe at diagnosis and failing to arrange lifelong glaucoma surveillance","Confusing bilateral congenital Rieger syndrome with the unilateral acquired iridocorneal endothelial syndrome","Neglecting endocrine evaluation in a child with short stature and anterior segment dysgenesis"],"management":["Lifelong intraocular-pressure surveillance, the key to preserving vision given the high risk of glaucoma","Lower intraocular pressure with topical agents such as prostaglandin analogues, beta-blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, escalating to glaucoma surgery such as goniotomy, trabeculotomy or drainage implants","Refractive correction and amblyopia therapy in children alongside management of the anterior segment dysgenesis","Dental assessment with restorative and prosthetic management of hypodontia and microdontia","Endocrine evaluation for pituitary and growth-hormone abnormalities, with treatment where deficient","Cardiac evaluation for associated structural defects","Molecular testing of PITX2 and FOXC1 with genetic counselling and examination of relatives, as inheritance is autosomal dominant","Coordinated clinical genetics, ophthalmology and dental care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of clinical ophthalmology","Reference on paediatric and genetic eye disease","Clinical genetics reference on anterior segment dysgenesis"],"crossLinks":[]},"riehl_melanosis":{"name":"Riehl melanosis (pigmented contact dermatitis)","system":"Dermatology / Pigmentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Riehl's melanosis","pigmented contact dermatitis","melanodermatitis toxica"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Acquired grey-brown, often reticulate, pigmentation of the face and neck, classically over the forehead, temples and cheeks","Regarded as a form of pigmented contact dermatitis triggered by allergens in cosmetics, fragrances and dyes","Pigmentation may be preceded by mild erythema or itch before the discoloration becomes established","Patch and photopatch testing can identify the responsible allergen and guide avoidance","Histology shows a lichenoid/interface reaction with pigment incontinence and dermal melanophages","Identifying and avoiding the culprit allergen is central, with topical lightening agents and laser as adjuncts","The dermal component means Wood-lamp accentuation is limited and clearance is slow"],"pathophysiology":"Repeated low-grade allergic contact sensitisation to cosmetic ingredients provokes a lichenoid interface dermatitis at the dermoepidermal junction. Damage to the basal layer causes pigment incontinence, with melanin dropping into the papillary dermis where it is retained within melanophages. This dermal melanin produces the persistent grey-brown reticulate pigmentation that characterises the condition.","additionalDifferentials":["Melasma","Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation","Ashy dermatosis (erythema dyschromicum perstans)","Lichen planus pigmentosus","Poikiloderma of Civatte","Drug-induced facial pigmentation","Exogenous ochronosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Patch and photopatch testing to identify the causative allergen","Detailed cosmetic, fragrance and hair-dye history","Wood-lamp examination to gauge pigment depth","Dermoscopy of affected areas","Skin biopsy where the diagnosis is uncertain"],"redFlags":["Failure to improve despite thorough allergen avoidance, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Features suggesting exogenous ochronosis from misuse of topical lightening agents","Widespread or systemic pigmentation pointing to a drug or internal cause"],"prognosis":"With identification and avoidance of the responsible allergen the pigmentation slowly improves over months to years, though some residual dermal pigment may persist.","pitfalls":["Not undertaking patch testing and therefore missing the causative allergen","Confusing it with melasma and continuing the offending cosmetic","Allowing ongoing allergen exposure, which perpetuates the pigmentation","Expecting a rapid response from lightening agents against dermal pigment"],"management":["Identify and avoid the culprit allergen through patch and photopatch testing, the central step in management","Discontinue the offending cosmetics, fragrances and hair dyes","Apply topical lightening agents such as hydroquinone or azelaic acid to fade the dermal pigment","Use Q-switched laser therapy as an adjunct for persistent pigmentation","Advise broad-spectrum photoprotection to limit further pigmentation","Reassure that pigmentation improves slowly over months to years once the allergen is removed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"rift_valley_fever":{"name":"Rift Valley fever","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RVF","Rift Valley fever virus infection","enzootic hepatitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases / WHO guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mosquito-borne phlebovirus zoonosis of sub-Saharan Africa and the Arabian Peninsula, with epizootics in livestock heralding human outbreaks, often after heavy rainfall and flooding","Humans are most often infected through direct contact with the blood or tissues of infected animals (abattoir workers, herders, veterinarians) as well as by mosquito bites","Most infections are a self-limiting influenza-like febrile illness, but a minority develop severe disease with haemorrhagic fever, hepatitis, encephalitis, or retinitis","A characteristic delayed ocular complication is macular or paramacular retinitis that can cause permanent visual loss weeks after the acute illness","The haemorrhagic form features jaundice, mucosal bleeding, and hepatic necrosis and carries a high mortality","Management is supportive; there is no licensed specific antiviral, and ribavirin has been considered in some settings for other viral haemorrhagic fevers but is not established for Rift Valley fever","Diagnosis uses RT-PCR and serology (IgM/IgG), and laboratory handling requires enhanced biosafety because of aerosol infectivity"],"pathophysiology":"Rift Valley fever virus is transmitted by infected mosquitoes and by direct contact with aerosols or fluids from viraemic animals during slaughter or handling. After entry the virus replicates widely and is markedly hepatotropic, producing hepatocellular necrosis. Severe disease reflects a combination of direct viral injury, cytokine-driven vascular dysfunction, and coagulopathy, leading to haemorrhage, hepatic failure, or central nervous system and retinal involvement. Immune-mediated inflammation contributes to the delayed retinitis and encephalitic complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Malaria","Leptospirosis","Viral hepatitis","Other viral haemorrhagic fevers such as Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever or Ebola virus disease","Q fever and other zoonotic febrile illnesses","Dengue and other arboviral fevers"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR on blood for viral RNA in the acute viraemic phase","Serology for Rift Valley fever IgM and IgG antibodies","Liver function tests to detect hepatitis and hepatic necrosis","Full blood count and coagulation studies to assess for haemorrhagic disease","Ophthalmological examination for retinitis in patients with visual symptoms","Lumbar puncture and neuroimaging when encephalitis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Jaundice with mucosal or gastrointestinal bleeding indicating the haemorrhagic form","Rapidly rising transaminases signalling fulminant hepatitis","Altered consciousness or focal neurological signs of encephalitis","Sudden visual loss from retinitis","Shock or disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"prognosis":"The great majority recover fully from the uncomplicated febrile illness, but the haemorrhagic and encephalitic forms carry substantial mortality, and retinitis may leave permanent visual impairment.","pitfalls":["Assuming a benign course when severe hepatic, haemorrhagic, or neurological disease can develop in a minority","Failing to recognise delayed retinitis presenting weeks after apparent recovery","Inadequate biosafety precautions given aerosol transmissibility in the laboratory and abattoir setting","Overlooking the animal-contact or outbreak history in endemic regions","Confusing early disease with malaria and delaying appropriate isolation and diagnostics"],"management":["Supportive care as there is no licensed specific antiviral, with fluid, electrolyte and organ support for the febrile illness","Intensive management of the haemorrhagic form, correcting coagulopathy and transfusing blood products, with support for hepatic failure","Supportive neurological care and airway protection for the encephalitic form","Ophthalmological review and corticosteroid consideration for the delayed macular retinitis threatening vision","Infection prevention with barrier precautions when handling blood and body fluids, given aerosol and contact transmission","Personal protection for abattoir workers, herders and veterinarians, supported by livestock vaccination and mosquito vector control","Public-health and veterinary coordination during epizootics that herald human outbreaks"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","World Health Organization Rift Valley fever fact sheet and guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"right_aortic_arch":{"name":"right aortic arch","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["right-sided aortic arch"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The aortic arch passes to the right of the trachea and crosses the right main bronchus rather than the left.","Two principal patterns exist: right arch with mirror-image branching and right arch with an aberrant left subclavian artery.","Mirror-image branching carries a very high association with cyanotic congenital heart disease, particularly tetralogy of Fallot and truncus arteriosus.","A right arch with an aberrant left subclavian artery arising from a Kommerell diverticulum, combined with a left-sided ligamentum arteriosum, completes a vascular ring.","This ring-forming configuration is strongly linked to 22q11.2 deletion, so genetic testing and cardiac evaluation are warranted.","Many isolated right arches are asymptomatic and found incidentally, but a ring may cause stridor, dysphagia and recurrent respiratory infection.","CT or MR angiography delineates the branching pattern, the presence of a Kommerell diverticulum and any airway compression.","Symptomatic vascular rings are treated surgically by dividing the ligamentum and, when the diverticulum is large, addressing it directly."],"pathophysiology":"A right aortic arch arises when the embryonic left arch regresses and the right fourth arch and right dorsal aorta persist, reversing the normal pattern. In mirror-image branching the regression occurs distal to the ductus, producing branching that mirrors normal anatomy and rarely forming a ring, but coexisting conotruncal cardiac malformations are common. When instead the left dorsal aorta regresses between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, the left subclavian arises aberrantly from the descending aorta via a Kommerell diverticulum and passes behind the oesophagus; together with a left-sided ligamentum arteriosum this encircles the trachea and oesophagus to form a loose vascular ring that may compress these structures.","additionalDifferentials":["Double aortic arch","Pulmonary artery sling","Innominate artery compression syndrome","Aberrant right subclavian artery with a left aortic arch","Mediastinal mass causing tracheal deviation","Tetralogy of Fallot or truncus arteriosus (as associated conditions)","Primary tracheomalacia or laryngomalacia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT angiography of the aortic arch and great vessels","MR angiography","Transthoracic echocardiography to detect associated intracardiac defects","Barium swallow demonstrating a posterior oesophageal indentation","Bronchoscopy for airway compression","22q11.2 deletion testing by FISH or microarray","Chest radiograph noting a right-sided arch and an absent left aortic knuckle"],"redFlags":["Cyanosis or a murmur suggesting associated cyanotic heart disease","Progressive stridor or respiratory distress from ring compression","Dysphagia with feeding difficulty and failure to thrive","Aneurysmal enlargement of a Kommerell diverticulum"],"prognosis":"An isolated asymptomatic right aortic arch requires no treatment and has an excellent prognosis; when it forms a symptomatic ring or accompanies congenital heart disease, outcome depends on surgical relief of compression and repair of the underlying cardiac lesion.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the strong association between mirror-image branching and cyanotic congenital heart disease.","Failing to test for 22q11.2 deletion when an aberrant subclavian and ring are present.","Missing an enlarging Kommerell diverticulum that may require resection.","Assuming any right arch forms a ring, or conversely that all are innocuous.","Achieving incomplete symptom relief if the Kommerell diverticulum or ligamentum is not adequately addressed."],"management":["Observe with reassurance when the arch is isolated and asymptomatic, as no treatment is required","Surgical division of the vascular ring, including division of the ligamentum arteriosum and resection of a Kommerell diverticulum, for symptomatic tracheo-oesophageal compression","Airway and swallowing assessment with management of stridor, dysphagia and recurrent respiratory infection","Echocardiography and repair of associated congenital heart disease such as tetralogy of Fallot and truncus arteriosus","Testing for 22q11.2 deletion with genetic counselling in ring-forming configurations","Coordinated paediatric cardiology, cardiothoracic surgery and otolaryngology input","Follow-up for residual airway symptoms after ring division"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","Anderson's Paediatric Cardiology","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"rigid_spine_muscular_dystrophy":{"name":"Rigid spine muscular dystrophy type 1 (SELENON-related)","system":"Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RSMD1","SEPN1-related myopathy","SELENON-related myopathy","Rigid spine syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neuromuscular and paediatric neurology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic SELENON variants (formerly SEPN1); it lies within the SELENON-related myopathy spectrum that also includes multiminicore disease, congenital fibre-type disproportion and Mallory-body (desmin-related) myopathy.","The hallmark is early spinal rigidity — limitation of flexion of the cervical and thoraco-lumbar spine — together with early, progressive scoliosis.","Weakness is predominantly axial and affects the neck flexors, with relatively preserved limb strength, so independent ambulation is usually retained.","Respiratory insufficiency is characteristically severe and out of proportion to limb weakness, driven by diaphragmatic weakness and nocturnal hypoventilation, and is the leading cause of morbidity.","Serum creatine kinase is normal or only mildly raised.","Muscle biopsy shows multiminicores, type-1 fibre predominance and atrophy, or fibre-type disproportion depending on the phenotype.","Reduced muscle bulk with a slender build is common, insulin resistance has been reported, and mild facial weakness may occur.","Cognition and cardiac function are typically preserved."],"pathophysiology":"SELENON encodes selenoprotein N, an endoplasmic-reticulum glycoprotein expressed most strongly in fetal and developing muscle. It contributes to redox regulation and calcium handling, interacting with the ryanodine receptor and the sarcoplasmic-reticulum calcium pump and protecting cells against oxidative stress. Loss of function disturbs calcium homeostasis and antioxidant defence within the sarcoplasmic reticulum, generating structural core lesions — regions depleted of oxidative enzyme activity and mitochondria — and impairing contractile performance. The result is a static or only slowly progressive myopathy with a striking predilection for the axial and respiratory muscles, which explains the disproportionate spinal rigidity, scoliosis and ventilatory failure.","additionalDifferentials":["RYR1-related congenital myopathy (multiminicore and central core disease)","Collagen VI-related myopathy (Ullrich and Bethlem), which also causes spinal rigidity and contractures","LAMA2-related and other congenital muscular dystrophies","Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy (contractures and spinal rigidity)","Other congenital myopathies with prominent respiratory involvement","FHL1-related myopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Respiratory assessment with erect and supine forced vital capacity to detect diaphragmatic weakness, and overnight oximetry with capnography","SELENON sequencing or a congenital myopathy gene panel","Muscle biopsy with oxidative enzyme histochemistry (to show minicores) and fibre typing","Muscle MRI, where the characteristic selective involvement (for example sartorius and adductors) can guide testing","Spinal imaging and orthopaedic assessment for scoliosis","Echocardiography and electrocardiography, usually normal, to document cardiac sparing"],"redFlags":["Nocturnal hypoventilation despite preserved walking — morning headache, daytime somnolence, disturbed sleep and recurrent chest infections","A falling forced vital capacity, particularly when measured supine, indicating diaphragmatic weakness","Rapidly progressive scoliosis compromising respiration","Weight loss or failure to thrive","Recurrent lower respiratory tract infections"],"prognosis":"Muscle weakness is usually static or only slowly progressive and ambulation is typically preserved, but respiratory failure frequently develops and often requires non-invasive ventilatory support; the outlook depends chiefly on early recognition and management of hypoventilation and scoliosis.","pitfalls":["The cardinal error is failing to monitor respiration because limb strength and walking are preserved — hypoventilation is insidious and can be life-threatening.","Relying on the erect vital capacity alone and missing the diaphragmatic weakness revealed by the supine measurement.","Expecting a high creatine kinase — it is frequently normal, which does not exclude the diagnosis.","Confusing it with collagen VI myopathy or Emery-Dreifuss dystrophy, which also cause spinal rigidity and contractures.","Attributing poor weight gain solely to feeding difficulty rather than to the myopathy itself."],"management":["Multidisciplinary supportive care as no therapy corrects the SELENON defect, with respiratory management as the central priority","Respiratory surveillance using erect and supine vital capacity and overnight oximetry, with non-invasive nocturnal ventilation for hypoventilation","Proactive treatment of chest infections with airway-clearance techniques and mechanical cough assistance","Orthopaedic management of the progressive scoliosis, including spinal orthoses and spinal fusion surgery","Physiotherapy and stretching to maintain mobility, limit contractures and preserve ambulation","Nutritional support to avoid under- or over-nutrition that would compromise respiratory function","Careful anaesthetic planning given the disproportionate respiratory risk","Genetic counselling and family testing for this autosomal recessive SELENON disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular medicine references","Congenital myopathy consensus guidelines","Paediatric neurology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"ring_chromosome_14":{"name":"Ring chromosome 14 syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["r(14) syndrome","ring 14 syndrome","ring chromosome 14"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Early-onset, drug-resistant epilepsy is the hallmark and often the presenting feature, frequently beginning in the first two years of life","Moderate-to-severe intellectual disability with delayed speech and motor milestones is almost universal","A distinctive progressive retinal pigmentary change is characteristic and helps distinguish it from a simple terminal 14q deletion","Microcephaly and relatively non-specific facial features (broad nasal bridge, epicanthic folds, downturned mouth) are common","Recurrent respiratory and other infections point to an accompanying tendency to immune dysfunction","Seizures often persist even when the deleted 14q material is minimal, implicating the ring structure itself rather than gene loss alone","The ring is mitotically unstable, so somatic mosaicism from ring loss or double rings is frequent and can influence severity"],"pathophysiology":"Ring chromosome 14 arises when the two ends of chromosome 14 fuse, usually with loss of the distal segments, to form a circular chromosome. The resulting phenotype reflects both haploinsufficiency of terminal 14q genes and a distinctive ring effect: continuing mitotic instability produces daughter cells with variable ring loss or duplication, generating dynamic tissue mosaicism. Because the severity of the epilepsy correlates poorly with the amount of deleted material, disruption of chromosome architecture and unstable copy number, rather than loss of a single gene, are thought to drive the neurological picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Terminal 14q deletion syndrome without a ring","Other ring chromosome syndromes","Early-infantile developmental and epileptic encephalopathies of single-gene origin","Angelman syndrome and other syndromic epilepsies","Mitochondrial or metabolic epileptic encephalopathy","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis, given the combination of epilepsy and retinopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Conventional karyotype to identify the ring and assess the degree of mosaicism","Chromosomal microarray to define any deleted segment, recognising it may not reveal the ring configuration itself","EEG to characterise seizure types","MRI of the brain for structural anomalies","Ophthalmological assessment including detailed retinal examination","Immunological review and infection surveillance where recurrent infections occur","Developmental and cognitive assessment"],"redFlags":["Refractory seizures or episodes of status epilepticus","Progressive visual impairment from retinal degeneration","Recurrent or severe infections raising concern about immune dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Epilepsy is frequently lifelong and difficult to control, and most affected individuals have significant intellectual disability; life expectancy is variable and influenced by the seizure burden, infections, and any associated malformations.","pitfalls":["Relying solely on microarray, which may detect a terminal deletion but miss the ring configuration and its instability","Underestimating somatic mosaicism when only a small number of cells is analysed","Attributing seizure severity purely to deletion size and overlooking the ring-specific effect","Missing progressive retinopathy because vision is hard to assess in a child with intellectual disability"],"management":["Control the drug-resistant epilepsy with antiepileptic drugs, escalating to combination regimens, as it is the dominant clinical problem","Provide early developmental intervention with speech, physical and occupational therapy and special education for the intellectual disability","Ophthalmological surveillance for the progressive retinal pigmentary change","Immunological review and infection surveillance for the recurrent respiratory and other infections","Serial EEG and brain MRI to characterise seizures and structural anomalies","Coordinated neurology, clinical genetics and paediatric care","Genetic counselling with parental karyotyping, noting that ring chromosomes usually arise de novo with a low recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Gardner and Sutherland's Chromosome Abnormalities and Genetic Counseling"],"crossLinks":[]},"ring_chromosome_20":{"name":"Ring chromosome 20 syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["r(20) syndrome","ring 20 syndrome","ring chromosome 20 epilepsy syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a distinctive drug-resistant epilepsy, characteristically with prolonged episodes of nonconvulsive status epilepticus marked by subtle confusion and behavioural change","Seizures usually begin in childhood in a previously well-developing child, with cognitive and behavioural decline following seizure onset","A characteristic EEG shows prolonged runs of high-voltage slow waves with intermixed spikes, often during nonconvulsive status","The ring frequently forms with little or no loss of material, so the karyotype can look almost balanced and copy-number tests may be normal","Low-level mosaicism is common, and a higher proportion of ring-bearing cells tends to accompany earlier onset and more severe disease","Malformations and dysmorphism are usually mild or absent, in contrast to most other ring syndromes","Behavioural disturbance and nocturnal frontal-type seizures are frequently reported"],"pathophysiology":"Ring chromosome 20 usually forms by fusion of the chromosome ends with little or no loss of genetic material, so many cases are effectively complete rings. The epilepsy phenotype is thought to arise from disturbance of genes near the telomeric regions, including those implicated in other epilepsies, together with the instability and abnormal behaviour of the ring during cell division, rather than from simple gene deletion. Mosaicism for the ring is typical, and a larger proportion of ring-containing cells is generally associated with earlier and more severe disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Single-gene developmental and epileptic encephalopathies","Other causes of nonconvulsive status epilepticus","Lennox-Gastaut and other epileptic encephalopathies","Frontal lobe epilepsy of other cause","Focal cortical dysplasia-related epilepsy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype analysing a sufficient number of cells to detect low-level mosaicism","EEG, including prolonged or ambulatory recording to capture nonconvulsive status","Chromosomal microarray, recognising it is frequently normal because material is not lost","MRI of the brain, which is usually unremarkable","Neuropsychological assessment to track cognition and behaviour"],"redFlags":["Prolonged nonconvulsive status epilepticus with altered awareness","Cognitive or behavioural regression after seizure onset","Escalating, drug-resistant seizures"],"prognosis":"Seizures are typically refractory to medication and often lifelong, and cognitive and behavioural decline tends to progress with ongoing seizures, although intellect may be relatively preserved early in the course.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal microarray excludes a chromosomal cause, since the ring often involves no copy-number loss","Analysing too few cells and thereby missing low-level mosaicism","Mistaking nonconvulsive status for a primary behavioural or psychiatric disturbance","Overlooking the diagnosis in a child with unexplained refractory epilepsy and normal imaging"],"management":["Broad-spectrum antiseizure medications (such as valproate, lamotrigine or levetiracetam) as the mainstay, while anticipating drug-resistant seizures","Prompt recognition and treatment of recurrent nonconvulsive status epilepticus, including benzodiazepine rescue protocols for prolonged episodes","Non-pharmacological options for refractory epilepsy such as the ketogenic diet or vagus nerve stimulation, as resective surgery is generally unhelpful given the diffuse genetic basis","Serial neuropsychological assessment with educational and behavioural support to address the cognitive and behavioural decline that follows seizure onset","Multidisciplinary paediatric neurology and epilepsy specialist involvement for ongoing seizure and developmental management","Genetic counselling with karyotype confirmation, discussing the usually de novo occurrence and the role of mosaicism"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Gardner and Sutherland's Chromosome Abnormalities and Genetic Counseling"],"crossLinks":[]},"robinow_syndrome":{"name":"Robinow syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fetal face syndrome","ROR2-related recessive Robinow syndrome","WNT5A/DVL-related dominant Robinow syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A skeletal dysplasia characterised by a distinctive 'fetal face' with frontal bossing, hypertelorism, a short upturned nose and a broad forehead","The autosomal recessive form is caused by ROR2 mutations and is generally more severe; dominant forms arise from WNT5A, DVL1, DVL3 and related genes in the non-canonical WNT pathway","Mesomelic limb shortening (short forearms and lower legs) is typical, with brachydactyly and short stature","Genital hypoplasia is characteristic, with a micropenis in males and clitoral/labial hypoplasia in females, and may cause diagnostic confusion","The recessive ROR2 form frequently includes vertebral segmentation defects with scoliosis and rib anomalies","Dental crowding, gum hypertrophy and a broad mouth are common oral features","Some dominant forms (notably certain DVL and WNT5A variants) are associated with osteosclerosis","Diagnosis is clinical and radiographic, confirmed by molecular testing to distinguish the dominant and recessive forms for counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Robinow syndrome is a disorder of the non-canonical WNT/planar cell polarity signalling pathway, which governs skeletal patterning, limb outgrowth and craniofacial and genital development. The recessive form is caused by loss of function of ROR2, a receptor tyrosine kinase that acts as a co-receptor for WNT5A; the dominant forms result from variants in WNT5A itself or in downstream DVL genes. Disruption of this pathway impairs the coordinated growth and orientation of skeletal elements, producing the mesomelic limb shortening, vertebral anomalies, characteristic facial appearance and genital hypoplasia.","additionalDifferentials":["Aarskog-Scott syndrome (FGD1, facial and genital features)","Omodysplasia (mesomelic limb shortening)","Other mesomelic dysplasias (e.g. Langer, Leri-Weill dyschondrosteosis, SHOX-related)","Craniofrontonasal syndrome (EFNB1, hypertelorism)","Fetal alcohol spectrum (facial overlap)","Disorders of sex development for the genital hypoplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of ROR2 and of dominant genes (WNT5A, DVL1, DVL3) to define the form","Skeletal survey to document mesomelic shortening, brachydactyly and vertebral/rib anomalies","Spinal radiographs and follow-up for segmentation defects and scoliosis in the recessive form","Renal and cardiac assessment where associated anomalies are suspected","Endocrine and genital assessment to characterise hypoplasia and guide management","Dental and orthodontic evaluation for crowding and gum overgrowth"],"redFlags":["Progressive scoliosis from vertebral segmentation defects in recessive disease","Congenital cardiac or renal anomalies causing functional compromise","Marked genital hypoplasia requiring endocrine evaluation and psychosocial support","Airway or feeding difficulty in severely affected neonates"],"prognosis":"Intelligence is usually normal and life expectancy is generally good, particularly in the dominant forms; the recessive ROR2 form is more severe, with skeletal and vertebral complications, but with orthopaedic, endocrine and multidisciplinary care most individuals do well.","pitfalls":["Misattributing genital hypoplasia to a primary disorder of sex development and missing the syndromic diagnosis","Not distinguishing dominant from recessive forms, leading to incorrect recurrence-risk counselling","Overlooking vertebral segmentation anomalies and progressive scoliosis on follow-up","Confusing the facial features with a fetal alcohol phenotype"],"management":["Orthopaedic management of mesomelic limb shortening, brachydactyly and limb deformity with bracing and corrective surgery as needed","Spinal surveillance and orthopaedic treatment of vertebral segmentation defects, rib anomalies and scoliosis, particularly in the recessive ROR2 form","Endocrine assessment and hormonal treatment of genital hypoplasia, including androgen therapy for micropenis where indicated","Dental and orthodontic management of crowding and gingival overgrowth","Screening for and management of associated renal and cardiac anomalies","Coordinated care through a multidisciplinary skeletal dysplasia team","Genetic counselling distinguishing recessive ROR2 disease from dominant WNT5A/DVL forms, with molecular testing and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Robinow syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"rocky_mountain_spotted_fever":{"name":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RMSF","Rickettsia rickettsii infection","spotted fever rickettsiosis","tick-borne typhus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by Rickettsia rickettsii and transmitted by ixodid (hard) ticks such as Dermacentor species; despite the name it is most common in the south-eastern and south-central United States rather than the Rocky Mountains","After an incubation of roughly 2-14 days patients develop fever, severe headache, myalgia and malaise, with the classic rash appearing a few days later","The rash is typically maculopapular then petechial and characteristically begins on the wrists and ankles before spreading centripetally to the trunk, often involving the palms and soles","A recalled tick bite is absent in a substantial proportion of cases, so its absence must never be used to exclude the diagnosis","Doxycycline is first-line therapy for all ages, including young children, and should be started empirically on clinical suspicion without waiting for confirmatory serology","Delay in starting effective treatment beyond about the fifth day of illness is the strongest predictor of death, making early empirical therapy critical","The core lesion is an infective vasculitis, so laboratory clues include thrombocytopenia, hyponatraemia and raised transaminases rather than a marked leucocytosis"],"pathophysiology":"Rickettsia rickettsii is an obligate intracellular Gram-negative bacterium that, following inoculation by a feeding tick, invades and multiplies within vascular endothelial cells and to a lesser extent smooth muscle of small and medium vessels. Widespread infection of the endothelium produces a systemic small-vessel vasculitis with increased vascular permeability, microvascular leak, oedema, activation of coagulation and localised microthrombi. This vascular injury underlies the rash, the tissue oedema and hypovolaemia, and end-organ damage affecting the brain, lungs, kidneys and other organs, and in severe disease can progress to shock, disseminated intravascular coagulation and multi-organ failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Meningococcaemia","Other spotted fever group rickettsioses and typhus","Human monocytic ehrlichiosis or granulocytic anaplasmosis","Viral exanthems including measles and enteroviral infection","Secondary syphilis","Immune thrombocytopenia or thrombotic microangiopathy","Drug hypersensitivity reaction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Indirect immunofluorescence antibody serology on paired acute and convalescent sera (the reference confirmatory test, though often negative early)","PCR of whole blood or, preferably, of a skin biopsy of the rash","Immunohistochemical staining of a skin biopsy for rickettsiae","Full blood count and film looking for thrombocytopenia","Serum sodium, renal function and liver enzymes","Coagulation studies where disseminated intravascular coagulation is suspected"],"redFlags":["Altered mental state, meningism or focal neurological signs suggesting meningoencephalitis","Petechial or purpuric rash with hypotension indicating fulminant disease","Respiratory distress or non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema","Oliguria and rising creatinine signalling acute kidney injury","Presentation after several days of untreated illness, when mortality rises sharply"],"prognosis":"With prompt doxycycline most patients recover fully, but untreated or late-treated disease carries a high case-fatality rate; survivors of severe illness may be left with neurological deficits or, rarely, limb loss from vascular compromise.","pitfalls":["Withholding doxycycline in children over unfounded concerns about dental staining, when short courses are safe and life-saving","Excluding the diagnosis because there is no rash early or no recalled tick exposure","Waiting for serological confirmation before treating, since antibodies are usually undetectable in the first week","Mistaking early non-specific fever for a self-limiting viral illness and discharging without follow-up","Substituting a beta-lactam, which is ineffective against rickettsiae"],"management":["Immediate empirical doxycycline on clinical suspicion for patients of all ages, including young children, without awaiting confirmatory serology","Avoid any delay in starting effective therapy, as early treatment is the strongest determinant of survival","Supportive care with careful intravenous fluid resuscitation for microvascular leak and hypovolaemia, avoiding fluid overload and pulmonary oedema","Monitoring and management of complications such as thrombocytopenia, disseminated intravascular coagulation, renal impairment and neurological or pulmonary involvement, with organ support in severe disease","Reserve chloramphenicol only where doxycycline is genuinely contraindicated, recognising it is less effective","Advice on tick-bite avoidance and prompt tick removal for prevention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"rolando_fracture":{"name":"Rolando fracture (comminuted thumb base)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["comminuted first metacarpal base fracture","T- or Y-shaped intra-articular thumb base fracture","three-part thumb base fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A comminuted intra-articular fracture of the base of the first metacarpal, classically with a T- or Y-shaped configuration and three or more fragments","Effectively a comminuted counterpart of the two-part Bennett fracture, but with a poorer prognosis","The same deforming muscular forces apply, with abductor pollicis longus and adductor pollicis destabilising the fragments","The mechanism is usually a higher-energy axial load on the thumb metacarpal","Management depends on the pattern of comminution: internal fixation for larger reconstructable fragments, and Kirschner-wire or bridging/external fixation for severe fragmentation","CT is valuable for mapping the articular fragments and planning reconstruction","There is a high risk of post-traumatic trapeziometacarpal arthritis despite treatment"],"pathophysiology":"A higher-energy axial load applied to the thumb metacarpal produces a comminuted intra-articular fracture, typically dividing the base into a T- or Y-shaped configuration with dorsal and volar articular fragments. As with the Bennett pattern, the abductor pollicis longus and adductor pollicis exert displacing forces, but the multifragmentary nature of the injury makes anatomical restoration of the joint surface difficult. Impaction and fragmentation of the articular surface disturb the congruity of the highly mobile trapeziometacarpal joint, predisposing to stiffness and degenerative arthritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Bennett fracture (two-part intra-articular thumb base)","Extra-articular first metacarpal base fracture","Comminuted trapezium fracture","First carpometacarpal fracture-dislocation","Multifragmentary first metacarpal shaft fracture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anteroposterior, lateral and oblique thumb radiographs","A dedicated true anteroposterior view of the trapeziometacarpal joint (Robert view)","CT to map comminution and articular impaction and to plan fixation","Assessment of articular congruity and fragment size","Evaluation for associated carpometacarpal instability"],"redFlags":["Severe comminution precluding anatomical reconstruction","Articular impaction with loss of joint congruity","Progressive trapeziometacarpal arthritis and stiffness","Persistent subluxation despite attempted fixation"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are generally poorer than for the Bennett fracture because of comminution; severe articular fragmentation predisposes to stiffness and trapeziometacarpal arthritis even after appropriate treatment.","pitfalls":["Attempting rigid internal fixation of unreconstructable comminution","Under-imaging by omitting CT to define the fragment pattern","Underestimating the risk of post-traumatic arthritis","Failing to maintain length and alignment against the deforming muscular pull"],"management":["Anatomical restoration of the trapeziometacarpal joint surface with open reduction and internal fixation for larger, reconstructable articular fragments","Kirschner-wire fixation or bridging/external fixation for severe comminution not amenable to internal fixation","Preoperative CT to map the articular fragments and plan reconstruction","Thumb spica immobilisation followed by supervised hand therapy to restore mobility, grip and pinch strength","Analgesia and early referral to a hand or orthopaedic trauma surgeon","Surveillance for and management of post-traumatic trapeziometacarpal osteoarthritis and stiffness, with later reconstruction or arthrodesis if disabling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","Green's Operative Hand Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"rosacea":{"name":"Rosacea","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acne rosacea","rhinophyma (severe phymatous form)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic facial condition centred on the convexities of the central face (cheeks, nose, chin, forehead), typically in fair-skinned middle-aged adults","Cardinal features are persistent central facial erythema, flushing, telangiectasia, and inflammatory papules and pustules","The absence of comedones is a key point distinguishing rosacea from acne vulgaris","Recognise clinical subtypes: erythematotelangiectatic (flushing and redness), papulopustular, phymatous (skin thickening such as rhinophyma), and ocular","Ocular rosacea (grittiness, dryness, blepharitis, conjunctival injection) is common, frequently underdiagnosed, and can threaten vision if untreated","Trigger avoidance is central: sun exposure, heat, hot drinks, spicy food, alcohol, and emotional stress commonly provoke flares","Treatment is tailored to subtype, using topical anti-inflammatory agents, oral tetracycline-class antibacterials for their anti-inflammatory effect in papulopustular disease, and laser or light therapy for telangiectasia and erythema","Daily photoprotection and gentle skin care are important adjuncts for all patients"],"pathophysiology":"Rosacea is a chronic inflammatory disorder of uncertain cause with several contributing mechanisms. Dysregulation of the innate immune system with abnormal cathelicidin antimicrobial peptide processing promotes inflammation and vascular changes. Neurovascular dysregulation drives flushing and persistent erythema, while an increased density of Demodex mites and altered cutaneous microbiome may stimulate the inflammatory response. Ultraviolet radiation, chronic inflammation, and vascular and lymphatic changes contribute to telangiectasia and, over time, the fibrous and sebaceous overgrowth of phymatous disease. Genetic predisposition and environmental triggers modulate expression.","additionalDifferentials":["Acne vulgaris (presence of comedones)","Perioral dermatitis","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash)","Flushing from carcinoid syndrome or menopause","Contact or photodermatitis of the face"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on morphology and distribution","Ophthalmological assessment when ocular involvement is suspected","Skin biopsy only in atypical cases to exclude lupus or other diagnoses","Investigation for systemic causes of flushing when the history is atypical","Consideration of Demodex assessment in treatment-resistant papulopustular disease"],"redFlags":["Ocular rosacea with pain, photophobia, or visual disturbance suggesting keratitis","Rapidly progressive facial swelling or induration requiring reassessment","Features suggesting lupus or systemic disease rather than rosacea","Disfiguring phymatous change causing functional or psychological impact"],"prognosis":"Rosacea is a chronic relapsing condition that is controllable but not curable; symptoms often persist for years with fluctuating severity, and phymatous changes, once established, tend to be permanent without procedural treatment.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing rosacea as acne and prescribing inappropriate comedolytic-only regimens","Overlooking ocular rosacea and risking corneal complications","Applying potent topical corticosteroids, which can trigger or worsen a rosacea-like eruption","Neglecting trigger identification and photoprotection, leading to persistent flares"],"management":["Trigger avoidance, daily broad-spectrum sun protection and gentle non-irritant skin care as the foundation of management","Topical therapy for papulopustular disease with metronidazole, azelaic acid or ivermectin","Topical alpha-adrenergic agonists such as brimonidine or oxymetazoline for persistent erythema and flushing","Oral tetracyclines such as doxycycline for moderate-to-severe papulopustular or ocular disease, with low-dose oral isotretinoin for refractory cases","Laser or intense pulsed light therapy for telangiectasia and background erythema","Ocular rosacea management with lid hygiene, artificial tears and topical or oral antibiotics, with ophthalmology referral where vision is threatened","Physical reduction of established phymatous change such as rhinophyma by laser, electrosurgery or surgical debulking"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"rosai_dorfman_disease":{"name":"Rosai-Dorfman disease","system":"Rheumatology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sinus histiocytosis with massive lymphadenopathy","SHML","Rosai-Dorfman-Destombes disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically presents with massive, painless, bilateral cervical lymphadenopathy, often with fever, night sweats, and raised inflammatory markers","Extranodal disease occurs in a large proportion, involving skin, sinonasal tract, orbit, central nervous system, and bone","The defining histological feature is emperipolesis: intact lymphocytes and other cells engulfed within the cytoplasm of large histiocytes","The pathognomonic histiocytes are S100-positive and CD1a-negative, distinguishing them from Langerhans cells","Most cases are sporadic and self-limiting, but disseminated or vital-organ disease can be aggressive","It may coexist with autoimmune or lymphoproliferative disorders and with other histiocytoses","Treatment ranges from observation for limited disease to corticosteroids, surgery, or systemic therapy for extensive or organ-threatening involvement"],"pathophysiology":"Rosai-Dorfman disease is a histiocytic proliferative disorder characterised by accumulation of large, activated histiocytes derived from the monocyte-macrophage lineage within lymph node sinuses and extranodal tissues. The hallmark is emperipolesis, the presence of intact inflammatory cells within histiocyte cytoplasm. Although historically considered reactive and possibly infection- or immune-triggered, a subset harbours clonal MAPK pathway mutations, placing it within the histiocytic neoplasm spectrum. The infiltrating S100-positive histiocytes expand lymph nodes and infiltrate extranodal sites, producing the clinical manifestations.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphoma, including Hodgkin lymphoma","Langerhans cell histiocytosis","Erdheim-Chester disease","Reactive or infective lymphadenopathy (including mycobacterial)","IgG4-related disease","Metastatic malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional lymph node or tissue biopsy demonstrating emperipolesis within S100-positive, CD1a-negative histiocytes","Full blood count and inflammatory markers (often raised ESR and polyclonal hypergammaglobulinaemia)","Cross-sectional imaging (CT/MRI) to map nodal and extranodal disease","MRI of brain and orbit when central nervous system or orbital involvement is suspected","Molecular testing for MAPK pathway mutations in atypical or aggressive cases","Screening for associated autoimmune or lymphoproliferative disorders"],"redFlags":["Central nervous system or spinal involvement causing neurological deficit","Airway or orbital compromise from bulky masses","Vital-organ infiltration (kidney, liver, lower respiratory tract) associated with worse outcomes","Features suggesting concurrent lymphoma or autoimmune disease"],"prognosis":"Most patients, particularly those with isolated nodal disease, follow a benign self-limiting or relapsing-remitting course, but extensive extranodal disease, central nervous system involvement, or associated immune dysregulation confers a more guarded prognosis.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the histiocytic infiltrate as lymphoma and missing emperipolesis","Failing to use S100 and CD1a staining to distinguish it from Langerhans cell histiocytosis","Overtreating limited disease that would resolve spontaneously","Underestimating the extent of extranodal or central nervous system involvement","Not looking for associated autoimmune or lymphoproliferative conditions"],"management":["Observation for asymptomatic isolated nodal disease, given the frequent spontaneous regression","Surgical excision or debulking of symptomatic, localised or compressive lesions","Corticosteroids as first-line systemic therapy for symptomatic or extranodal disease","Systemic chemoimmunotherapy such as vinblastine, methotrexate or mercaptopurine for refractory or disseminated disease","Targeted MAPK-pathway inhibition with MEK or BRAF inhibitors where clonal kinase mutations are identified","Radiotherapy for critical-site disease threatening vital structures such as the central nervous system or orbit when otherwise uncontrolled","Screening for and management of coexisting autoimmune or lymphoproliferative disorders, with multidisciplinary haematology and immunology input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"roseola_infantum":{"name":"Roseola infantum","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sixth disease","exanthem subitum","HHV-6 infection","three-day fever"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically affects infants and toddlers between roughly 6 months and 2 years, caused mainly by human herpesvirus 6 (and less often HHV-7)","The hallmark pattern is several days of high fever in a child who otherwise looks relatively well, followed by defervescence and then the eruption of a rash","The rash typically appears as the fever breaks: small rose-pink macules or maculopapules starting on the trunk and spreading to the neck and limbs, usually non-pruritic and fading within days","Febrile seizures are a recognised complication because of the height of the fever, and can be the presenting concern","Diagnosis is generally clinical; the temporal sequence of high fever resolving just before the rash is the key clue","Because most cases are self-limiting, management centres on supportive care, hydration, and antipyresis rather than antiviral therapy in the immunocompetent child","HHV-6 establishes lifelong latency after primary infection and can reactivate, which is clinically important in immunocompromised and transplant patients"],"pathophysiology":"Primary infection with human herpesvirus 6B (and sometimes HHV-7) is acquired in early childhood, often via the saliva of asymptomatic carriers. The virus infects lymphocytes and other cells, producing a viraemia that drives the high fever. As host immunity controls the viraemia the temperature falls and the characteristic exanthem appears. Like other herpesviruses, HHV-6 then persists in a latent state and can reactivate later, particularly under immunosuppression, where it is linked to encephalitis, marrow suppression, and graft complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Measles","Rubella","Scarlet fever","Erythema infectiosum (fifth disease)","Drug hypersensitivity eruption","Enteroviral exanthem"],"infectionMimics":["Measles","Rubella","Enteroviral rash illness","Early meningococcal or other bacterial infection presenting with high fever"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis is usually sufficient in a well child with the typical fever-then-rash sequence","HHV-6 PCR or serology reserved for atypical, severe, or immunocompromised presentations","Basic bloods to exclude bacterial sepsis when the febrile child appears unwell","Neuroimaging and CSF studies if encephalitis or complicated seizures are suspected"],"redFlags":["Toxic or lethargic appearance suggesting bacterial sepsis or meningitis rather than benign roseola","Prolonged, complex, or focal seizures rather than a simple brief febrile convulsion","Signs of encephalitis such as altered consciousness or focal neurological deficit","Severe or unusual disease in an immunocompromised or transplant patient"],"prognosis":"In healthy infants the illness is self-limiting with full recovery and no long-term sequelae; complications such as encephalitis are rare and are seen chiefly in immunocompromised hosts.","pitfalls":["Attributing the rash to an antibiotic or antipyretic given during the febrile phase and mislabelling the child as drug-allergic","Overlooking bacterial sepsis in a highly febrile infant by assuming roseola too early","Failing to reassure parents that the rash appearing as fever resolves signals recovery, not deterioration","Not considering HHV-6 reactivation in an unwell immunocompromised patient"],"management":["Supportive care as the mainstay, with adequate hydration and antipyretics such as paracetamol for fever in the immunocompetent child","Reassurance about the benign, self-limiting course and the characteristic fever-then-rash sequence","Parental advice on the recognition and simple first-aid management of febrile seizures","Reserve antiviral therapy such as ganciclovir or foscarnet for immunocompromised hosts or severe reactivation disease including encephalitis","Escalation for urgent assessment if the child appears septic, has prolonged or complex seizures, or shows features of encephalitis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"rosette_forming_glioneuronal_tumor":{"name":"Rosette-forming glioneuronal tumour","system":"Oncology / Neuro-oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RGNT","rosette-forming glioneuronal tumour","rosette-forming glioneuronal tumor","FGFR1-altered glioneuronal tumour","CNS WHO grade 1 glioneuronal tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of the CNS","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, slow-growing glioneuronal tumour (CNS WHO grade 1) of young adults, arising characteristically in the posterior fossa around the fourth ventricle","The midline, periventricular location commonly causes headache, ataxia and, when the fourth ventricle is obstructed, hydrocephalus","MRI typically shows a well-circumscribed, partly cystic lesion with variable enhancement near the fourth ventricle, aqueduct or pineal region","Histology shows a biphasic pattern: neurocytic rosettes and perivascular pseudorosettes together with a pilocytic astrocytoma-like glial component","FGFR1 alterations, sometimes with PIK3CA or NF1 changes, are recurrent molecular features","The neurocytic rosettes are synaptophysin-positive, confirming neuronal differentiation","Behaviour is indolent, and surgery is the mainstay, though location often limits complete resection"],"pathophysiology":"Rosette-forming glioneuronal tumour is a rare, low-grade neoplasm with both neuronal and glial differentiation, arising most often in the midline posterior fossa around the fourth ventricle and adjacent structures, and sometimes in the pineal region or aqueduct. It is characterised by a biphasic architecture: a neurocytic component forming neurocytic rosettes and perivascular pseudorosettes, and a glial component resembling pilocytic astrocytoma. The neurocytic elements express synaptophysin, confirming neuronal differentiation. Recurrent molecular alterations, particularly of the FGFR1 gene and sometimes PIK3CA and NF1, drive its development. Growth is slow, and clinical features arise mainly from its periventricular location and obstruction of cerebrospinal fluid flow rather than from aggressive behaviour.","additionalDifferentials":["Pilocytic astrocytoma","Ependymoma","Central neurocytoma","Medulloblastoma","Dysembryoplastic neuroepithelial tumour","Pineal region tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced brain MRI to characterise the fourth-ventricular/periventricular mass","Assessment of ventricular size for obstructive hydrocephalus","Histopathology demonstrating neurocytic rosettes and a pilocytic-like glial component","Synaptophysin immunohistochemistry to confirm neuronal differentiation","FGFR1 (and where indicated PIK3CA/NF1) molecular testing","Craniospinal MRI where dissemination is a concern"],"redFlags":["Obstructive hydrocephalus with raised intracranial pressure","Progressive brainstem or cerebellar signs from a fourth-ventricular mass","Postoperative neurological deficits given the eloquent location","Unexpectedly rapid growth suggesting an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally favourable given the indolent, grade 1 nature of the tumour, and gross total resection can be curative. However, its midline posterior fossa location adjacent to the brainstem and cerebellar structures often limits complete removal and carries a risk of surgical morbidity, so residual tumour may be monitored or, uncommonly, treated further.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for pilocytic astrocytoma or ependymoma and misjudging prognosis or adjuvant therapy","Pursuing aggressive resection in an eloquent location and causing avoidable deficits","Not confirming neuronal differentiation with synaptophysin staining","Failing to recognise and relieve obstructive hydrocephalus"],"management":["Maximal safe surgical resection as the primary treatment, which can be curative for this grade 1 tumour","Treatment of obstructive hydrocephalus with cerebrospinal fluid diversion by endoscopic third ventriculostomy or ventricular shunting","Balancing extent of resection against brainstem and cerebellar morbidity given the midline posterior fossa location","Serial surveillance MRI to detect residual or recurrent tumour","Reserving adjuvant radiotherapy or FGFR-targeted therapy for recurrent, progressive or unresectable disease","Neuro-oncology multidisciplinary management with neurorehabilitation for any residual neurological deficits"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System","Louis DN et al. The 2021 WHO Classification of Tumors of the Central Nervous System"],"crossLinks":[]},"ross_syndrome":{"name":"Ross syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Autonomic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ross's syndrome","tonic pupil with areflexia and segmental anhidrosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad of a tonic (Adie) pupil, generalised areflexia (absent deep tendon reflexes), and segmental anhidrosis","Patients often present with areas of absent sweating alongside compensatory hyperhidrosis in the intact skin, which is the usual presenting complaint","Regarded as a progressive disorder of the peripheral autonomic (cholinergic) neurons","Overlaps with Holmes-Adie syndrome (tonic pupil plus areflexia) and Harlequin syndrome, forming a spectrum of partial dysautonomia","Heat intolerance results from the reduced sweating surface area","The tonic pupil is large, poorly reactive to light, constricts slowly (tonically) to near effort, and shows cholinergic denervation supersensitivity to dilute pilocarpine","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by sweat testing to map the anhidrotic segments","Management is symptomatic: avoid heat, manage troublesome compensatory hyperhidrosis, and reassure"],"pathophysiology":"Ross syndrome is a progressive partial dysautonomia characterised by degeneration of postganglionic cholinergic autonomic neurons. Loss of sudomotor fibres produces patchy, segmental anhidrosis, while adjacent intact regions sweat excessively (compensatory hyperhidrosis) in an attempt to maintain thermoregulation. Involvement of the ciliary ganglion produces a tonic pupil with cholinergic denervation supersensitivity, and degeneration of dorsal root ganglion cells or their fibres underlies the generalised areflexia. The condition is thought to be a chronic, possibly immune-mediated or degenerative neuropathy of the peripheral autonomic and sensory systems, and it overlaps clinically with Holmes-Adie syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Holmes-Adie syndrome","Harlequin syndrome","Pure autonomic failure","Diabetic or amyloid autonomic neuropathy","Sjogren-associated autonomic neuropathy","Congenital or acquired generalised anhidrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thermoregulatory sweat test to delineate anhidrotic and hyperhidrotic segments","QSART for postganglionic sudomotor function","Dilute pilocarpine pupil testing for cholinergic supersensitivity","Deep tendon reflex examination and, where needed, nerve conduction studies","Screening for associated autoimmune disease such as Sjogren syndrome","Skin biopsy for sudomotor nerve fibre assessment where available"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive anhidrosis with heat intolerance risking hyperthermia","Features suggesting a wider or central autonomic disorder","Systemic symptoms suggesting an underlying autoimmune or paraneoplastic process","Recurrent heat-related collapse"],"prognosis":"Ross syndrome tends to progress slowly, with the area of anhidrosis gradually enlarging over years; it is not life-threatening, but heat intolerance and troublesome compensatory hyperhidrosis can impair quality of life.","pitfalls":["Focusing on the hyperhidrosis and missing the underlying anhidrosis and autonomic diagnosis","Failing to recognise the tonic pupil and areflexia that complete the triad","Attempting to suppress compensatory sweating, which may worsen heat intolerance","Not distinguishing it from Holmes-Adie syndrome and the wider dysautonomia spectrum"],"management":["Symptomatic control of compensatory hyperhidrosis with topical antiperspirants, anticholinergic agents or botulinum toxin injection","Advice on heat-avoidance and active cooling strategies to manage impaired thermoregulation from the reduced sweating surface","Dilute pilocarpine drops for symptomatic photophobia or near-vision difficulty from the tonic pupil","Reassurance regarding the benign, slowly progressive and non-life-threatening course","Screening for associated autoimmune disease such as Sjogren syndrome","Neurology and autonomic specialist referral for characterisation and long-term follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"rotator_cuff_tear":{"name":"Rotator cuff tear","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rotator cuff rupture","supraspinatus tear","cuff tear","rotator cuff injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with shoulder pain, weakness on elevation and abduction, and difficulty with overhead activities; supraspinatus is the most commonly torn tendon","Tears may be degenerative (attritional, in older patients) or traumatic (e.g. after a fall or dislocation, especially in those over 40)","A painful mid-range arc of abduction and positive cuff tests (e.g. weakness of resisted abduction or external rotation, drop-arm sign) support the diagnosis","Ultrasound and MRI are the key investigations, distinguishing partial from full-thickness tears and assessing tendon retraction and muscle atrophy","Many degenerative partial or small full-thickness tears are managed conservatively with physiotherapy, activity modification, and analgesia","Acute traumatic tears in younger, active patients and large tears with functional loss are generally considered for surgical repair, ideally before significant retraction and fatty muscle degeneration","Chronic massive, irreparable tears may lead to cuff-tear arthropathy, sometimes treated with reverse shoulder arthroplasty","New weakness after shoulder dislocation in an older patient should prompt assessment for an acute cuff tear, not just presumed axillary nerve injury"],"pathophysiology":"The rotator cuff (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis) stabilises the humeral head in the glenoid and enables elevation and rotation. Tendon integrity is compromised by age-related degeneration, hypovascularity near the supraspinatus insertion, and mechanical impingement beneath the coracoacromial arch, making tears increasingly common with age. Repetitive overhead loading and acute trauma superimpose on this degeneration. Once a tear occurs, the tendon tends to retract and the corresponding muscle undergoes atrophy and fatty infiltration over time, reducing repairability and allowing superior migration of the humeral head, which can eventually produce cuff-tear arthropathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Subacromial impingement or bursitis without tear","Frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis)","Calcific tendinitis","Glenohumeral or acromioclavicular osteoarthritis","Cervical radiculopathy","Axillary or suprascapular nerve injury","Biceps tendon pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound of the shoulder to assess cuff integrity dynamically","MRI to characterise tear size, retraction, and muscle quality (fatty atrophy)","Plain radiographs to assess acromial morphology, humeral head position, and arthropathy","Clinical cuff-specific strength tests (e.g. empty-can, external rotation, lift-off, drop-arm)","MR arthrography where partial or labral lesions are suspected"],"redFlags":["Acute loss of active shoulder movement after trauma or dislocation suggesting an acute repairable tear","Weakness that could reflect nerve injury (axillary or suprascapular) requiring differentiation from cuff rupture","Signs of infection in a hot, swollen, systemically unwell shoulder","Progressive functional loss and pseudoparalysis indicating a large or massive tear"],"prognosis":"Many small and degenerative tears remain functionally acceptable with rehabilitation, but larger or acute traumatic tears repaired promptly generally do better than chronic retracted tears with fatty muscle degeneration; untreated massive tears can progress to cuff-tear arthropathy with persistent weakness and pain.","pitfalls":["Assuming all post-dislocation weakness in older patients is axillary nerve palsy and missing an acute cuff tear","Delaying repair of an acute traumatic tear until retraction and fatty atrophy make it irreparable","Relying on radiographs alone, which do not show the tendon","Confusing frozen shoulder with a cuff tear by not distinguishing passive from active range restriction"],"management":["Structured physiotherapy with rotator cuff and scapular strengthening for small or degenerative tears","Activity modification and analgesia with NSAIDs, considering a subacromial corticosteroid injection for pain relief","Early surgical repair for acute traumatic tears and large symptomatic tears in active patients, before tendon retraction and fatty muscle degeneration develop","Tendon transfer or superior capsular reconstruction for irreparable tears, and reverse total shoulder arthroplasty for established cuff-tear arthropathy","Postoperative rehabilitation to restore range of movement and strength","Orthopaedic referral guided by tear size, retraction, muscle quality and the patient's functional demand"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"rotator_cuff_tendinopathy":{"name":"Rotator cuff tendinopathy / subacromial impingement","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subacromial impingement syndrome","subacromial pain syndrome","rotator cuff related shoulder pain","supraspinatus tendinopathy","shoulder impingement"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with lateral shoulder pain aggravated by overhead activity and by lying on the affected side at night","Chronic cuff 'tendinitis' is predominantly a degenerative tendinopathy (tendinosis) reflecting failed healing under repetitive load, rather than a primarily inflammatory process","A painful arc through mid-range abduction is characteristic, with Hawkins-Kennedy and Neer signs supporting subacromial involvement","Resisted testing helps localise the affected tendon (empty-can/Jobe for supraspinatus, resisted external rotation for infraspinatus and teres minor)","Ultrasound and MRI distinguish tendinopathy from partial- or full-thickness tears and assess the subacromial space","Management is largely conservative with relative rest, activity and load modification, and progressive or eccentric loading with scapular rehabilitation guided by physiotherapy","A subacromial corticosteroid injection may give short-term relief, but repeated injections risk tendon weakening and should be used judiciously","The concept of 'impingement' is increasingly reframed as rotator cuff related shoulder pain, emphasising intrinsic tendon change over purely mechanical compression"],"pathophysiology":"The rotator cuff tendons, especially supraspinatus, are exposed to repetitive tensile and compressive load, particularly during overhead activity. When chronic overload outstrips the tendon's capacity to repair, a degenerative tendinopathy develops, characterised by disorganised collagen, increased ground substance, neovascularisation and relatively little classic inflammation, which is why the term tendinosis is preferred to tendinitis. Narrowing of the subacromial space from acromial shape, bursal thickening, or dynamic scapular and cuff dysfunction can add mechanical irritation. Over time the weakened tendon may progress to partial- or full-thickness tearing.","additionalDifferentials":["Rotator cuff tear (partial or full thickness)","Calcific tendinitis of the cuff","Adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder)","Glenohumeral or acromioclavicular osteoarthritis","Cervical radiculopathy referring to the shoulder","Glenohumeral instability with secondary cuff pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound to assess tendon integrity, thickness and bursal change, including dynamic assessment","MRI to characterise tendinopathy, tears and cuff muscle atrophy","Plain radiographs to assess acromial morphology, calcification and arthritis","Diagnostic subacromial local anaesthetic injection to confirm the pain source","Clinical provocative testing (painful arc, Hawkins-Kennedy, Neer, resisted cuff tests)"],"redFlags":["Sudden loss of active elevation after trauma suggesting an acute large cuff tear","Systemic features such as fever, weight loss or night sweats raising infection or malignancy","Fixed global restriction of passive movement suggesting frozen shoulder or joint pathology","Neurological deficit pointing to cervical spine or nerve involvement"],"prognosis":"Most cases improve with structured loading rehabilitation over weeks to months, although recovery can be slow and recurrence is common if load is not managed; large or full-thickness tears may need surgical consideration.","pitfalls":["Labelling all cuff pain as inflammatory and relying on rest rather than progressive loading","Repeated corticosteroid injections that may weaken the tendon and mask progression to tearing","Overlooking scapular dyskinesis and the wider kinetic chain in rehabilitation","Missing a full-thickness tear that requires different management"],"management":["Progressive and eccentric loading with scapular rehabilitation, guided by physiotherapy, as the mainstay of treatment","Relative rest with activity and load modification, avoiding aggravating overhead positions","Simple analgesia or NSAIDs for symptom control","Judicious subacromial corticosteroid injection for persistent pain limiting engagement with rehabilitation","Addressing biomechanical contributors such as posture, scapular dyskinesis and overhead technique","Surgical referral for subacromial decompression or repair reserved for refractory cases or an associated significant tear"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","British Journal of Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"rotavirus_gastroenteritis":{"name":"Rotavirus Gastroenteritis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rotavirus diarrhoea","rotavirus infection","viral gastroenteritis (rotavirus)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A leading cause of severe dehydrating diarrhoea in infants and young children, especially between about 3 and 24 months of age.","Typically presents with abrupt onset of vomiting followed by profuse watery, non-bloody stools, often with fever.","Spread is faecal-oral; the virus is highly contagious and stable in the environment, so outbreaks in daycare and hospital settings are common.","Assessment of hydration status guides management far more than laboratory testing, which is rarely needed for diagnosis.","Oral rehydration therapy is the cornerstone of treatment for mild to moderate dehydration; intravenous fluids are reserved for severe dehydration or failed oral therapy.","Early refeeding with an age-appropriate diet is encouraged once rehydration is achieved; prolonged fasting and dilute feeds are discouraged.","Antibiotics and antimotility agents have no role and may be harmful; routine antiemetics are generally avoided in young children.","Live oral rotavirus vaccines given in infancy substantially reduce severe disease and hospitalisation.","Antigen immunoassay or PCR on stool can confirm the pathogen when confirmation is required for epidemiologic or infection-control purposes."],"pathophysiology":"Rotavirus infects the mature enterocytes at the tips of the small intestinal villi, causing villous blunting and loss of absorptive surface. This produces malabsorption of nutrients and water with an osmotic diarrhoea, while the viral nonstructural protein NSP4 acts as an enterotoxin that triggers secretory diarrhoea and disrupts calcium homeostasis. The combination of impaired absorption, secretion, and reduced disaccharidase (including lactase) activity leads to voluminous watery stool and rapid fluid and electrolyte losses.","additionalDifferentials":["Norovirus and other viral gastroenteritis","Bacterial enteritis (Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter, enterotoxigenic E. coli)","Parasitic infection (Giardia, Cryptosporidium)","Antibiotic-associated diarrhoea","Systemic infection presenting with diarrhoea (e.g. urinary tract infection, sepsis)","Surgical abdomen (intussusception) when vomiting and lethargy predominate"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool rotavirus antigen enzyme immunoassay or PCR when confirmation is needed","Serum electrolytes, urea, and creatinine in moderate to severe dehydration","Capillary or venous blood gas to assess acid-base status in severe illness","Blood glucose in a lethargic or severely dehydrated child","Stool culture if bacterial infection is suspected (blood, mucus, high fever)"],"redFlags":["Signs of severe dehydration or hypovolaemic shock","Persistent vomiting preventing oral rehydration","Altered consciousness, lethargy, or seizures","Bloody stools or high fever suggesting alternative or complicating diagnosis","Abdominal distension or severe pain raising concern for intussusception"],"prognosis":"Most children recover fully within about a week with appropriate rehydration; mortality is low where fluid therapy is accessible but remains significant in resource-limited settings due to severe dehydration.","pitfalls":["Underestimating fluid deficit and failing to reassess hydration during treatment","Using dilute or lactose-heavy feeds and prolonged fasting instead of prompt refeeding","Prescribing antibiotics or antimotility drugs that offer no benefit","Missing intussusception in a child with vomiting, lethargy, and abdominal signs"],"management":["Oral rehydration solution as the cornerstone of treatment for mild-to-moderate dehydration to replace fluid and electrolyte losses","Intravenous rehydration for severe dehydration, shock or failed oral therapy, with correction of accompanying electrolyte disturbance","Early refeeding with an age-appropriate diet once rehydration is achieved, avoiding prolonged fasting and dilute feeds","Zinc supplementation to reduce diarrhoea duration and severity in younger children, particularly in resource-limited settings","Avoidance of routine antibiotics and antimotility agents, with a single dose of an antiemetic such as ondansetron to support oral rehydration where vomiting limits intake","Infection control with contact precautions and hand hygiene given high contagiousness, and rotavirus vaccination for prevention","Monitoring of hydration status, serum electrolytes and blood glucose in significant illness"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"rothmund_thomson_syndrome":{"name":"Rothmund-Thomson syndrome","system":"Genetics / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RTS","RECQL4","poikiloderma congenitale","poikiloderma atrophicans and cataract"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / relevant specialty textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive genodermatosis, with the type-2 form caused by biallelic variants in RECQL4, a RecQ-family DNA helicase involved in genome maintenance","The hallmark is poikiloderma, a rash of reticulated erythema, telangiectasia, atrophy and pigmentary change beginning in infancy on the cheeks and spreading to the limbs and buttocks","Associated features include sparse hair (including eyebrows and eyelashes), small stature, skeletal anomalies and dental and nail abnormalities","Juvenile cataracts are characteristic of the condition, particularly historically associated forms","There is a substantially increased risk of osteosarcoma, especially in RECQL4-associated (type 2) disease, and of skin cancers later in life","RECQL4 variants also underlie overlapping RECQL4-related disorders such as RAPADILINO and Baller-Gerold syndromes","Surveillance emphasises skeletal vigilance for osteosarcoma, sun protection and skin cancer monitoring, and photoprotection given photosensitivity"],"pathophysiology":"Rothmund-Thomson syndrome type 2 is caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in RECQL4, encoding a RecQ-family DNA helicase that participates in DNA replication and repair and in maintaining genomic and mitochondrial stability. Deficient RECQL4 function leads to genomic instability, which underlies the predisposition to osteosarcoma and other tumours and contributes to the poikilodermatous skin changes, growth failure and skeletal defects. A proportion of clinically diagnosed cases (type 1) lack an identified RECQL4 variant and are associated more strongly with juvenile cataract, indicating genetic heterogeneity within the phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Bloom syndrome and other RecQ helicase disorders (Werner syndrome)","Fanconi anaemia","Dyskeratosis congenita","Cockayne syndrome and xeroderma pigmentosum","Kindler syndrome and other poikiloderma-associated genodermatoses","Other RECQL4-related disorders (RAPADILINO, Baller-Gerold syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of RECQL4 to confirm biallelic variants","Skeletal radiographs to characterise bone anomalies and provide a baseline for osteosarcoma surveillance","Ophthalmological assessment for juvenile cataract","Dermatological examination and biopsy where skin malignancy is suspected","Growth and endocrine evaluation for short stature","Directed imaging of any painful or swollen bony lesion to exclude osteosarcoma"],"redFlags":["New bone pain, swelling or a mass, particularly around the knee, suggesting osteosarcoma","A changing or non-healing skin lesion raising concern for skin malignancy","Rapidly progressive visual impairment from cataract","Signs of a second primary tumour in an affected individual"],"prognosis":"For individuals without malignancy, life expectancy may be near normal, but the elevated risk of osteosarcoma, most pronounced in RECQL4-associated disease and typically in childhood or adolescence, is the main determinant of outcome. Later skin cancers and the cumulative effects of photosensitivity add to long-term morbidity.","pitfalls":["Dismissing limb pain as growing pains and delaying imaging that could detect osteosarcoma","Confusing the poikiloderma with eczema or other common childhood rashes","Neglecting rigorous photoprotection and skin cancer surveillance","Assuming a single clinical entity and overlooking the RECQL4-related and genetically heterogeneous forms"],"management":["Strict lifelong photoprotection with sun avoidance, protective clothing and broad-spectrum sunscreen to limit poikiloderma and skin cancer risk","Osteosarcoma surveillance with a low threshold for imaging any persistent bony pain or swelling, especially during childhood and adolescence","Regular dermatological surveillance with prompt biopsy or excision of suspicious skin lesions","Ophthalmological monitoring and surgical treatment of juvenile cataract","Growth and endocrine assessment and support for short stature","Cautious use of ionising radiation and DNA-damaging chemotherapy given the underlying genomic instability, with oncology input if osteosarcoma develops","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance, with RECQL4 testing and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","GeneReviews: Rothmund-Thomson Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"rotor_syndrome":{"name":"Rotor syndrome","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SLCO1B1","SLCO1B3","OATP1B1/1B3 deficiency","Rotor type hyperbilirubinaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign autosomal recessive disorder requiring loss of function of both hepatic uptake transporters OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 (genes SLCO1B1 and SLCO1B3)","It causes chronic, predominantly conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with normal liver enzymes and normal synthetic function","Unlike Dubin-Johnson syndrome, the liver is histologically normal and not pigmented","Total urinary coproporphyrin is markedly elevated, with a relatively increased coproporphyrin I fraction, contrasting with the pattern in Dubin-Johnson syndrome","Hepatobiliary scintigraphy typically shows poor or absent hepatic uptake, whereas gallbladder visualisation is generally preserved","The defect impairs hepatic re-uptake of conjugated bilirubin that has undergone physiological hepatocyte efflux, so it accumulates in plasma","No treatment is needed as the condition is entirely benign with normal longevity"],"pathophysiology":"The organic anion transporting polypeptides OATP1B1 and OATP1B3, encoded by SLCO1B1 and SLCO1B3 on the sinusoidal (basolateral) hepatocyte membrane, mediate uptake of bilirubin and other organic anions from blood into the liver. A portion of conjugated bilirubin normally effluxes from hepatocytes back into the sinusoids and is then re-taken up by these transporters. In Rotor syndrome, combined deficiency of both transporters abolishes this reuptake, so conjugated bilirubin accumulates in the circulation, producing conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia. Because the canalicular excretory machinery and hepatocyte architecture are intact, the liver appears normal without the pigment seen in Dubin-Johnson syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Dubin-Johnson syndrome","Cholestatic and obstructive liver disease","Viral and drug-induced hepatitis","Primary biliary cholangitis","Benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis","Cholestasis of pregnancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fractionated bilirubin confirming a conjugated predominance","Normal liver enzymes and synthetic function excluding hepatocellular or obstructive disease","Total and fractionated urinary coproporphyrin showing markedly raised total excretion","Hepatobiliary scintigraphy demonstrating impaired hepatic uptake","Liver biopsy (if performed) showing normal, non-pigmented histology","SLCO1B1 and SLCO1B3 molecular genetic testing where confirmation is needed"],"redFlags":["Abnormal transaminases or impaired synthetic function suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Clinical signs of true biliary obstruction such as pale stools and pruritus","Systemic or constitutional symptoms prompting exclusion of serious hepatobiliary disease","Altered handling of drugs that rely on OATP transporters, raising toxicity risk"],"prognosis":"Rotor syndrome is benign with a normal life expectancy and no progression to chronic liver disease; recognition mainly serves to avoid unnecessary investigation and to anticipate altered disposition of OATP-dependent drugs.","pitfalls":["Confusing Rotor with Dubin-Johnson syndrome despite their differing pigment and coproporphyrin patterns","Pursuing invasive workup for a benign conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia","Failing to anticipate altered pharmacokinetics of drugs transported by OATP1B1/1B3, such as certain statins","Overlooking the requirement for biallelic loss of both transporters when interpreting genetics","Attributing the jaundice to hepatocellular disease despite normal liver enzymes"],"management":["Reassurance and patient education about the benign nature and normal life expectancy, with no specific treatment required","Avoidance of unnecessary invasive investigation for the isolated conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia once the diagnosis is established","Anticipating and adjusting for altered disposition of OATP1B1/OATP1B3-dependent drugs such as statins, methotrexate and certain antibiotics to prevent toxicity","Recognition that jaundice may fluctuate or worsen with intercurrent illness yet requires no intervention","Genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance requiring biallelic SLCO1B1 and SLCO1B3 defects, with family clarification as needed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"roussy_levy_syndrome":{"name":"Roussy-Levy syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Roussy-Levy hereditary areflexic dystasia","hereditary areflexic dystasia","CMT with tremor","MPZ","PMP22"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley and Daroff Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Regarded as a demyelinating CMT phenotype (a variant of CMT1) rather than a distinct disease, historically linked to variants in PMP22 and MPZ","Classic combination of a hereditary demyelinating neuropathy with postural upper-limb tremor and gait ataxia (dystasia)","Features overlap classic CMT: distal wasting, pes cavus, areflexia and glove-and-stocking sensory loss, with the tremor and ataxia being the distinguishing additions","The tremor resembles an essential or postural tremor and the ataxia is largely sensory, reflecting loss of large fibre proprioceptive input rather than a primary cerebellar lesion","Nerve conduction studies show markedly slowed motor conduction velocities in keeping with the underlying demyelinating neuropathy","Autosomal dominant inheritance is typical given the PMP22/MPZ basis","Diagnosis rests on the characteristic clinical triad supported by demyelinating electrophysiology and genetic confirmation"],"pathophysiology":"Roussy-Levy syndrome is now understood as a phenotypic variant of demyelinating CMT, most often associated with PMP22 or MPZ variants that impair peripheral myelin. The demyelinating neuropathy causes distal weakness and prominent loss of large-fibre sensory function; the resulting deafferentation of proprioceptive pathways produces sensory ataxia and contributes to the characteristic postural tremor. The tremor is thought to arise from disturbed sensorimotor feedback rather than a distinct central lesion. As in other CMT1 subtypes, repeated demyelination and remyelination generate onion-bulb formations and secondary axonal loss, and the diffuse myelin involvement explains the uniformly slow conduction velocities.","additionalDifferentials":["Classic CMT type 1A or 1B without prominent tremor","Essential tremor with coincidental neuropathy","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy","Hereditary ataxias with peripheral neuropathy (e.g. Friedreich ataxia)","Cerebellar ataxia of other cause","Sensory ataxic neuropathy from other aetiologies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies showing demyelinating-range slowing","PMP22 duplication testing and MPZ sequencing, or a hereditary neuropathy panel","Assessment of proprioception and Romberg testing to characterise sensory ataxia","Family history and examination of relatives for the tremor-neuropathy phenotype","Exclusion of acquired demyelinating neuropathy when the course is atypical","Consideration of hereditary ataxia testing where the ataxic component predominates"],"redFlags":["Prominent cerebellar signs suggesting an alternative hereditary ataxia rather than sensory ataxia","Subacute deterioration indicating a superimposed acquired neuropathy","Rapidly worsening tremor or ataxia out of keeping with slowly progressive inherited disease","New bulbar or breathing symptoms requiring review"],"prognosis":"Typically slowly progressive with long preservation of ambulation; the tremor and ataxia can be functionally limiting but the overall course mirrors that of other CMT1 subtypes with normal life expectancy.","pitfalls":["Treating the presentation as isolated essential tremor and overlooking the underlying neuropathy","Attributing the ataxia to a cerebellar lesion rather than sensory deafferentation","Regarding Roussy-Levy as a separate disorder rather than a CMT1 phenotype","Missing a treatable acquired demyelinating neuropathy"],"management":["Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain strength, function and gait","Ankle-foot orthoses and mobility aids for foot drop and sensory ataxia, with foot care and orthopaedic correction of pes cavus and deformity","Symptomatic management of the postural upper-limb tremor, including a trial of a beta-blocker, with functional adaptation","Avoidance of potentially neurotoxic drugs such as vincristine that can worsen the neuropathy","Multidisciplinary neuromuscular team input with proprioceptive training and fall prevention","Genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance, with PMP22 duplication and MPZ testing and evaluation of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Charcot-Marie-Tooth Neuropathy Type 1","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"rs3pe_syndrome":{"name":"Remitting seronegative symmetrical synovitis with pitting oedema (RS3PE)","system":"Rheumatology / Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RS3PE","RS3PE syndrome","remitting seronegative symmetrical synovitis with pitting edema","McCarty syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by abrupt-onset, symmetrical polysynovitis with striking pitting oedema of the dorsum of the hands and feet","Predominantly affects elderly patients, more often men, and is seronegative for rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies","The oedema reflects tenosynovitis of the extensor and flexor tendon sheaths rather than joint effusion alone","Typically shows a dramatic and rapid response to low-dose corticosteroids, which is a useful diagnostic and therapeutic feature","Overlaps clinically with polymyalgia rheumatica and elderly-onset seronegative rheumatoid arthritis","Carries a recognised paraneoplastic association, so occult solid organ and haematological malignancy should be considered, particularly with atypical or steroid-resistant disease","Generally follows a remitting course with a good outcome once malignancy has been excluded"],"pathophysiology":"RS3PE is a seronegative inflammatory arthropathy of older adults in which acute symmetrical synovitis and tenosynovitis produce distal pitting oedema of the hands and feet. Increased vascular permeability, linked to raised levels of vascular endothelial growth factor and pro-inflammatory cytokines, is thought to underlie the prominent subcutaneous oedema surrounding the inflamed tendon sheaths. The condition may be idiopathic or arise as a paraneoplastic phenomenon accompanying solid organ or haematological malignancy, and it shares immunopathogenic features with polymyalgia rheumatica and other seronegative arthropathies of the elderly.","additionalDifferentials":["Elderly-onset seronegative rheumatoid arthritis","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Late-onset spondyloarthropathy or psoriatic arthritis","Crystal arthropathy (gout, calcium pyrophosphate disease)","Paraneoplastic arthropathy from occult malignancy","Bilateral lymphoedema or venous insufficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP), which are typically markedly raised","Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies, which are characteristically negative","Ultrasound or MRI of the hands demonstrating tenosynovitis and soft-tissue oedema","Age-appropriate malignancy screening given the paraneoplastic association","Full blood count and blood film to screen for haematological disease"],"redFlags":["Constitutional symptoms (weight loss, night sweats) suggesting underlying malignancy","Poor or absent response to corticosteroids, prompting a search for occult cancer","Relapse after steroid withdrawal raising concern for an evolving malignancy or alternative diagnosis","New lymphadenopathy or organomegaly on examination"],"prognosis":"Idiopathic RS3PE usually remits within months and responds excellently to low-dose corticosteroids with a favourable long-term outlook; however, paraneoplastic cases carry the prognosis of the underlying malignancy, making cancer surveillance essential.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for occult malignancy, especially with atypical or steroid-resistant presentations","Mislabelling as seronegative rheumatoid arthritis and committing to long-term DMARD therapy unnecessarily","Attributing the distal oedema to heart failure or venous disease and missing the synovitis","Stopping corticosteroids too abruptly and misinterpreting relapse"],"management":["Low-dose oral corticosteroids as first-line therapy, typically producing a rapid and dramatic response","Gradual corticosteroid taper over months, guided by resolution of symptoms and inflammatory markers","NSAIDs as adjunctive symptomatic relief where appropriate","Age-appropriate malignancy screening given the paraneoplastic association, especially in atypical or steroid-resistant disease","Monitoring for relapse and for evolution into polymyalgia rheumatica or elderly-onset seronegative rheumatoid arthritis","Rheumatology referral for diagnostic confirmation and guidance on corticosteroid management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Oxford Textbook of Rheumatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"rubella_german_measles":{"name":"Rubella (German Measles)","system":"Infectious Diseases (viral exanthem / congenital infection)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["German measles","three-day measles","Rubivirus infection","Congenital rubella syndrome","CRS"],"chapter":212,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1643","clinicalPearls":["Caused by rubella virus, an enveloped single-stranded positive-sense RNA virus of the Matonaviridae family (genus Rubivirus); only a single antigenic type exists and humans are the only known reservoir (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Acquired rubella typically presents as a mild generalized maculopapular rash lasting up to about 3 days, but as many as roughly half of infections are subclinical or occur without a rash (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Incubation period averages 14 days (range about 12-23 days); in older children and adults a 1- to 5-day prodrome of low-grade fever, malaise, and upper respiratory symptoms may precede the rash, whereas in young children rash is often the first sign (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Occipital and postauricular lymphadenopathy is characteristic and may appear during the second week after exposure (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Arthralgia and arthritis are common complications in infected adults, particularly women; thrombocytopenia and encephalitis are less common (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Persons with acquired rubella may shed virus from about 7 days before rash onset to roughly 5-7 days afterward, and both clinical and subclinical infections are considered contagious (Harrison 22e p.1643).","The most serious consequence is congenital rubella syndrome (CRS), which results from maternal infection during pregnancy, especially the first trimester; the classic triad is cataracts, hearing impairment, and heart defects (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Hearing impairment is the single most common defect of CRS, and the classic complete triad is seen in only about 10% of CRS infants (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Maternal infection within the first 10 weeks of gestation carries roughly a 90% risk of delivering an infant with CRS; risk declines later in pregnancy and fetal defects are rare after about 16 weeks, though sensorineural hearing deficit can still occur with infection up to about 20 weeks (Harrison 22e p.1644-1645).","Infants with CRS can shed large quantities of virus (notably from throat secretions and urine) for up to 1 year, so only rubella-immune individuals should care for them (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Reinfection with rubella virus, although uncommon, is possible and may produce detectable IgM antibodies (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Rubella is no longer endemic in the United States (endemic transmission interrupted by 2001), but importations and sporadic cases continue, so vigilance is warranted in adults migrating from or returning from areas without rubella control (Harrison 22e p.1643)."],"pathophysiology":"Rubella virus spreads person to person via respiratory droplets. After primary implantation and replication in the nasopharynx it spreads to regional lymph nodes, followed by viremia. In pregnant women the viremia frequently infects the placenta; placental replication can then seed fetal organs. In the infected fetus nearly all organs may be involved, and the hallmark is a chronic infection that persists throughout intrauterine development and for up to 1 year after birth. Tissue effects of rubella virus range from no obvious impact to cell destruction. The pathology of postnatal (acquired) rubella is limited because the disease is mild, whereas the pathology of CRS is well defined even though its pathogenesis remains poorly delineated (Harrison 22e p.1643).","additionalDifferentials":["Measles (rubeola)","Scarlet fever","Roseola (exanthem subitum)","Toxoplasmosis","Fifth disease (erythema infectiosum / parvovirus B19)","Zika virus infection","Other febrile illnesses with suboccipital and postauricular lymphadenopathy"],"infectionMimics":["Measles (rubeola)","Roseola","Fifth disease (parvovirus B19)","Toxoplasmosis","Zika virus infection","For CRS: other intrauterine (TORCH) infections such as cytomegalovirus and toxoplasmosis producing nonspecific neonatal findings"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Scarlet fever (toxin-mediated streptococcal exanthem clinically similar to rubella)","Other drug or hypersensitivity maculopapular eruptions"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serology: rubella IgM in an acute-phase serum specimen, or a fourfold rise in IgG titer between acute- and convalescent-phase sera; IgM-capture ELISA is regarded as most accurate (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Timing of paired sera: acute specimen within 7-10 days of illness onset, convalescent specimen about 14-21 days later; repeat IgM testing if an early specimen (before day 5 after rash) is negative (Harrison 22e p.1644).","IgG avidity testing to distinguish primary infection (low-avidity antibodies, recent infection) from reinfection (mature high-avidity IgG indicating infection at least ~2 months earlier); especially useful in pregnant women to assess CRS risk (Harrison 22e p.1644).","RT-PCR for viral RNA detection (preferred from nasopharyngeal swab; throat swab or urine acceptable), collected as soon as possible after onset, ideally 1-3 days and no later than 7 days; virus isolation by culture also possible (Harrison 22e p.1644).","CRS confirmation: serum IgM (normally present up to ~6 months, sometimes up to 1 year; may be absent at birth and require retesting at 1 month), and persistence of rubella IgG beyond the expected decline of passively transferred maternal antibody (titer not declining at the expected twofold-per-month rate); RT-PCR/culture of nasopharyngeal, throat, or urine specimens, most likely positive within the first 6 months (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Routine prenatal screening for rubella IgG; a positive IgG indicates immunity (Harrison 22e p.1644-1645)."],"redFlags":["Maternal rubella infection during the first trimester (highest risk of CRS, up to ~90% within the first 10 weeks of gestation) (Harrison 22e p.1644).","A susceptible pregnant woman with exposure to or symptoms of a rubella-like illness, requiring IgM testing and low-avidity IgG confirmation (Harrison 22e p.1645).","A neonate with cataracts, hearing impairment, congenital heart defects, or 'blueberry muffin' purpura suggestive of CRS (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Encephalitis or significant thrombocytopenia complicating acquired rubella (Harrison 22e p.1643).","An index case of CRS in a nosocomial or congregate setting, given prolonged viral shedding and outbreak potential (Harrison 22e p.1643)."],"prognosis":"Acquired rubella is generally a mild, self-limited illness; the rash usually resolves within about 3 days, and serious complications such as encephalitis and thrombocytopenia are uncommon. The major prognostic concern is congenital infection: maternal infection in early pregnancy may lead to miscarriage, fetal death, premature delivery, or live birth with severe and often permanent defects (eyes, ears, heart, and CNS). Permanent CRS manifestations include deafness, congenital heart defects, cataracts and other eye defects, microcephaly, and neurodevelopmental sequelae, whereas manifestations such as hepatosplenomegaly, thrombocytopenic purpura, and adenopathy are transient. Immunity following infection or a single dose of rubella-containing vaccine (seroconversion in >=95%) is considered long-term and probably lifelong (Harrison 22e p.1644-1645).","pitfalls":["Relying on clinical diagnosis is unreliable because rubella mimics many rash illnesses, has varied presentations, and is frequently subclinical or mild; laboratory confirmation is the only reliable way to confirm acute disease (Harrison 22e p.1644).","A negative rubella IgM drawn before day 5 after rash onset does not exclude infection and should be repeated (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Rubella IgM testing is not recommended in pregnant women without a history of illness or contact with a rubella-like illness because of the risk of false-positive results (Harrison 22e p.1645).","An infant with suspected CRS who tests IgM-negative shortly after birth should be retested at 1 month, since IgM may not yet be detectable (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Worldwide rubella case counts are likely underestimated because disease is often mild, care may not be sought, cases may go unrecognized or unreported, and some countries detect cases only through measles surveillance (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Live attenuated rubella-containing vaccine should not be given to women known to be pregnant, and pregnancy should be avoided for 28 days after vaccination; however, inadvertent vaccination during pregnancy is not ordinarily a reason to consider termination, as no CRS occurred among ~3000 such pregnancies followed (Harrison 22e p.1645)."],"management":["Supportive care for acquired rubella with antipyretics and analgesia or NSAIDs for fever and arthralgia/arthritis, as no specific antiviral therapy exists","Respiratory droplet isolation of cases, with exclusion from work or school, to limit spread, keeping cases away from pregnant women","Notification to public health authorities as a notifiable disease, with contact tracing","Prevention through MMR immunisation, ensuring women of childbearing age are immune before pregnancy, noting the live vaccine is contraindicated during pregnancy","In pregnancy, confirming immunity by IgG screening, counselling on congenital rubella syndrome risk after early-gestation infection, and referral to foetal medicine","Multidisciplinary supportive management and surveillance (cardiac, ophthalmological, audiological and developmental) for infants with congenital rubella syndrome"],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grading system is described. Clinically, infection is categorized by host and timing: subclinical/mild acquired rubella; symptomatic acquired rubella (rash with possible arthralgia/arthritis, rarely thrombocytopenia or encephalitis); congenital rubella virus infection without apparent signs; and congenital rubella syndrome (CRS) with transient versus permanent manifestations, where fetal risk is greatest with maternal infection in the first 10 weeks of gestation (~90%) and declines markedly after ~16 weeks (Harrison 22e p.1644-1645).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1643","Harrison 22e p.1644","Harrison 22e p.1645","Harrison 22e p.1646"],"crossLinks":[]},"rubinstein_taybi_syndrome":{"name":"Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RTS","RSTS","broad thumb-hallux syndrome","CREBBP","EP300"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Broad, often angulated thumbs and great toes are the signature finding and a key diagnostic clue","The facial gestalt includes down-slanting palpebral fissures, a beaked or convex nose with the columella extending below the alae, highly arched eyebrows, and a grimacing smile","Postnatal growth failure, microcephaly and intellectual disability with a friendly, sociable temperament are typical","Most cases arise from de novo autosomal dominant variants in CREBBP; a minority involve EP300 and tend to be milder facially but may include maternal pre-eclampsia in pregnancy","Both genes encode histone acetyltransferases, so the condition is a disorder of chromatin regulation","Keloid formation, recurrent respiratory and ear infections, and feeding difficulties are common","There is an increased lifetime risk of benign and malignant tumours, warranting attention to new lesions"],"pathophysiology":"Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome results from haploinsufficiency of transcriptional coactivators with intrinsic histone acetyltransferase activity, encoded by CREBBP and its paralogue EP300. These proteins acetylate histones and remodel chromatin to permit transcription of numerous developmental genes. Reduced acetyltransferase dosage impairs the coordinated gene expression required for skeletal, craniofacial and neuronal development, producing the characteristic broad digits, dysmorphism, growth impairment and cognitive deficit. The same chromatin-regulatory role underlies the elevated tumour predisposition.","additionalDifferentials":["Cornelia de Lange syndrome","Floating-Harbor syndrome","Saethre-Chotzen syndrome","Greig cephalopolysyndactyly syndrome","Cohen syndrome","Kabuki syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis of CREBBP and EP300","Chromosomal microarray to detect 16p13.3 microdeletions involving CREBBP","Echocardiography for congenital heart disease","Renal tract ultrasound for structural anomalies","Ophthalmology review for refractive error, strabismus, glaucoma and lacrimal duct obstruction","Hearing assessment and monitoring for recurrent otitis media"],"redFlags":["New or rapidly enlarging masses given the increased neoplasia risk","Signs of raised intraocular pressure or glaucoma threatening vision","Recurrent aspiration or severe apnoea related to airway and feeding problems","Anaesthetic difficulty from a narrow airway and reported arrhythmia risk"],"prognosis":"Most individuals survive into adulthood with moderate intellectual disability and require lifelong support; overall outlook is shaped by cardiac anomalies, infection burden and tumour surveillance. Life expectancy can approach normal with good multidisciplinary care.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis when digital broadening is subtle and the facial features are mild, as in some EP300 cases","Overlooking treatable glaucoma or lacrimal duct obstruction","Failing to warn about keloid risk before elective surgery","Inadequate preoperative airway and cardiac assessment before anaesthesia"],"management":["Developmental support with early intervention, special education, and speech and occupational therapy for intellectual disability","Orthopaedic or plastic surgical correction of broad, angulated thumbs and great toes where function is impaired","Assessment and management of congenital heart disease and structural renal anomalies","Ophthalmology and audiology surveillance and treatment for refractive error, strabismus, glaucoma, lacrimal duct obstruction and recurrent otitis media","Feeding support for feeding difficulties, management of recurrent respiratory and ear infections, and caution with keloid-prone wound healing","Tumour surveillance given the chromatin-regulatory predisposition, and anaesthetic caution given airway and cardiac risks","Genetic counselling for the usually de novo CREBBP or EP300 variants, discussing the low but non-negligible recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","GeneReviews: Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"rumination_syndrome":{"name":"Rumination syndrome","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rumination disorder","merycism","regurgitation syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by effortless, repetitive regurgitation of recently ingested food into the mouth, where it is re-chewed, re-swallowed, or spat out","Regurgitation typically begins within minutes of eating, is not preceded by nausea or retching, and involves undigested, pleasant-tasting food","Symptoms usually cease once the regurgitated material becomes acidic, distinguishing it from vomiting and gastro-oesophageal reflux","High-resolution manometry with impedance may capture the diagnostic postprandial rise in intragastric pressure (abdominal straining) coinciding with retrograde flow","It is a behaviourally learned, often unconscious contraction of the abdominal wall, not a structural or acid-driven disease","First-line treatment is diaphragmatic-breathing behavioural therapy, which competes with and suppresses the abdominal straining","Frequently misdiagnosed as refractory reflux or gastroparesis, leading to unnecessary acid suppression and procedures"],"pathophysiology":"Rumination syndrome results from a subconsciously acquired increase in intra-abdominal pressure, produced by contraction of the abdominal wall musculature after eating. This straining, combined with transient relaxation of the lower oesophageal sphincter, propels gastric contents retrograde into the oesophagus and mouth without the coordinated antiperistalsis and autonomic prodrome of true vomiting. Because the behaviour is a learned somatic response rather than a mucosal or motility disease, the regurgitated material is undigested and non-acidic, and the pattern can be interrupted by teaching competing diaphragmatic breathing.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease","Gastroparesis and other gastric motility disorders","Achalasia or other oesophageal outflow obstruction","Eating disorders such as bulimia nervosa","Cyclic vomiting syndrome","Mechanical gastric outlet obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution oesophageal manometry with impedance, ideally postprandial, to demonstrate the characteristic pattern","Upper endoscopy to exclude mucosal disease and mechanical obstruction","Gastric emptying study to differentiate from gastroparesis","Ambulatory pH-impedance monitoring to distinguish from acid reflux","Nutritional and psychological assessment where weight loss or comorbidity is present"],"redFlags":["Significant weight loss, dehydration, or nutritional compromise","Nocturnal or truly acidic regurgitation suggesting reflux or another organic cause","Dysphagia, odynophagia, or evidence of mechanical obstruction","Features suggesting a primary eating disorder requiring specialist care"],"prognosis":"The disorder is benign and highly responsive to behavioural therapy, with many patients achieving marked or complete symptom control; delayed recognition, however, exposes patients to needless investigations and treatments.","pitfalls":["Labelling the regurgitation as refractory reflux and escalating acid suppression","Ordering repeated procedures instead of recognising the postprandial effortless pattern","Confusing the condition with an eating disorder or with true vomiting","Overlooking coexisting anxiety or psychological factors that reinforce the behaviour"],"management":["Diaphragmatic (abdominal) breathing behavioural therapy as first-line treatment to compete with and suppress the post-prandial abdominal straining","Patient education and reassurance that the disorder is a learned behaviour rather than a structural or acid-driven disease","Biofeedback, using postprandial manometry where available, to reinforce and consolidate the breathing technique","Habit-reversal therapy and management of any psychological comorbidity, with clinical psychology input","Baclofen as an adjunct in refractory cases to reduce transient lower oesophageal sphincter relaxations","Nutritional assessment and support where weight loss or nutritional compromise has occurred"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Rome Foundation diagnostic criteria for functional gastrointestinal disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"runx1_familial_platelet_disorder":{"name":"RUNX1 familial platelet disorder with myeloid malignancy","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RUNX1","FPD/AML","familial platelet disorder with predisposition to acute myeloid leukaemia","AML1","CBFA2"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / Nathan and Oski","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant disorder caused by germline mutations in RUNX1, characterised by lifelong mild-to-moderate thrombocytopenia and a substantial lifetime risk of myeloid malignancy","Platelet counts are usually only modestly reduced, so the bleeding tendency is often disproportionate to the count because of an accompanying qualitative platelet defect","Platelet aggregation studies frequently show a dense-granule (delta storage pool) defect with impaired responses to collagen and adrenaline","A family history of unexplained thrombocytopenia, easy bruising, or leukaemia across generations should prompt consideration of a germline RUNX1 lesion","Reported lifetime risk of progression to myelodysplasia or acute myeloid leukaemia is high, commonly cited around a third to a half of carriers","Somatic second hits (for example a second RUNX1 allele, or acquired mutations in genes such as CDC25C, GATA2 or the acquisition of trisomy 21 in marrow) often accompany transformation","Diagnosis rests on germline genetic testing, ideally on non-haematopoietic tissue such as cultured skin fibroblasts to avoid contamination by clonal blood cells","Because the mutation is germ line, related potential stem-cell donors must be screened to avoid transplanting an affected graft"],"pathophysiology":"RUNX1 encodes the alpha subunit of the core-binding factor transcription complex, a master regulator of definitive haematopoiesis and megakaryocyte maturation. Germline loss-of-function or dominant-negative mutations reduce functional RUNX1 dosage (haploinsufficiency) and disturb the transcriptional programme that drives platelet production and granule formation, producing both a low platelet count and defective platelet function. The impaired transcriptional network also destabilises the myeloid compartment, so affected stem cells are primed for clonal evolution; acquisition of additional somatic mutations over time drives progression to myelodysplasia and acute myeloid leukaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["ANKRD26-related thrombocytopenia","ETV6-related thrombocytopenia with leukaemia predisposition","Immune thrombocytopenia (acquired)","MYH9-related disorder and other inherited macrothrombocytopenias","GATA2 deficiency with myeloid predisposition","Storage pool disease and other qualitative platelet disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with review of the peripheral blood film for platelet number and morphology","Platelet aggregometry and dense-granule (delta storage pool) assessment","Germline RUNX1 sequencing performed on cultured fibroblasts or another non-haematopoietic source","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine with cytogenetics for baseline and surveillance","Serial monitoring for acquired clonal mutations and karyotypic change","Screening of related potential haematopoietic stem-cell donors"],"redFlags":["New or worsening cytopenias, monocytosis, or circulating blasts suggesting transformation to myelodysplasia or leukaemia","Emergence of a new clonal cytogenetic abnormality on marrow surveillance","Rapidly falling platelet count or unexplained systemic symptoms","Bleeding out of proportion to the platelet count indicating a significant qualitative defect"],"prognosis":"The bleeding tendency is usually manageable, but the defining concern is the high lifetime risk of myelodysplasia and acute myeloid leukaemia; outcomes depend on early detection through surveillance and on timely allogeneic transplantation using a mutation-negative donor when transformation supervenes.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the condition as chronic immune thrombocytopenia and exposing patients to unnecessary immunosuppression or splenectomy","Testing only blood-derived DNA, which can miss or misattribute the germline variant when a blood clone is present","Using an affected relative as a stem-cell donor because germline status was not checked","Underestimating bleeding risk by relying on the platelet count alone rather than assessing platelet function"],"management":["Managing the bleeding tendency with antifibrinolytics such as tranexamic acid and platelet transfusion for significant bleeding or surgery, while avoiding antiplatelet agents such as aspirin and NSAIDs","Considering desmopressin to improve platelet function around haemostatic challenges","Regular haematological surveillance with serial full blood counts and periodic bone marrow examination and cytogenetics to detect progression to myelodysplasia or acute myeloid leukaemia","Monitoring for acquired clonal mutations and karyotypic change that herald transformation","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation when myelodysplasia or acute leukaemia supervenes","Screening related potential stem-cell donors to ensure a mutation-negative graft","Genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance, with germline RUNX1 testing performed on a non-haematopoietic source such as cultured fibroblasts"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"ruptured_sinus_of_valsalva_aneurysm":{"name":"Ruptured sinus of Valsalva aneurysm","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sinus of Valsalva aneurysm rupture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cardiology and congenital heart disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An aneurysm of an aortic sinus that ruptures, usually into a right-sided chamber, creating an aorto-cardiac shunt","The right coronary sinus is most commonly involved, often rupturing into the right ventricle or atrium","Rupture may be acute with chest pain, breathlessness and heart failure, or more insidious","It produces a loud continuous murmur from the aorto-cardiac shunt","It may be congenital or associated with connective tissue disease, infection or trauma","Diagnosis is by echocardiography and surgical or transcatheter closure is usually required"],"pathophysiology":"A sinus of Valsalva aneurysm is a localised dilatation of an aortic sinus, typically from congenital weakness at the junction of the aortic media and the annulus, sometimes exacerbated by connective tissue disease or infection. When it ruptures, high-pressure aortic blood flows through the defect into a lower-pressure cardiac chamber, most often the right ventricle or atrium, creating a left-to-right (aorto-cardiac) shunt. The resulting volume load causes heart failure, and the continuous pressure gradient generates a characteristic continuous murmur.","additionalDifferentials":["Coronary arteriovenous fistula","Patent ductus arteriosus","Ventricular septal defect with aortic regurgitation","Aortopulmonary window","Aortic dissection involving a sinus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic and transoesophageal echocardiography","Cardiac CT or MRI to define the aneurysm and rupture site","Assessment of shunt magnitude and cardiac function","Evaluation for associated ventricular septal defect and aortic regurgitation","Exclusion of endocarditis where relevant"],"redFlags":["Acute heart failure or haemodynamic collapse at rupture","Large shunt with rapid decompensation","Associated endocarditis","Compression of adjacent structures or coronary compromise by an unruptured aneurysm"],"prognosis":"Untreated rupture leads to progressive heart failure, but surgical or transcatheter closure generally gives good results, particularly when performed before severe ventricular dysfunction develops.","pitfalls":["Attributing the continuous murmur to a patent ductus or coronary fistula","Missing an associated ventricular septal defect or aortic regurgitation","Delaying closure until heart failure is advanced","Overlooking endocarditis as a precipitant"],"management":["Definitive closure of the ruptured defect by surgical patch repair or transcatheter device occlusion of the aorto-cardiac fistula","Concurrent repair of associated lesions at operation, including closure of a coexisting ventricular septal defect and aortic valve repair or replacement for significant aortic regurgitation","Medical stabilisation of the volume-loaded heart failure with diuretics and afterload reduction while awaiting definitive intervention","Urgent management of acute decompensation or cardiogenic shock, expediting emergency closure when haemodynamic collapse occurs","Assessment for and treatment of infective endocarditis where the aneurysm is infection-related, with appropriate antimicrobial cover","Serial echocardiographic surveillance for residual shunt, ventricular function and progression of aortic regurgitation after closure","Early referral to a combined cardiology and cardiac surgery team for closure before severe ventricular dysfunction develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Congenital heart disease references (standard reference)","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"s_adenosylhomocysteine_hydrolase_deficiency":{"name":"S-adenosylhomocysteine hydrolase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AHCY deficiency","SAHH deficiency","S-adenosyl-L-homocysteine hydrolase deficiency","hypermethioninaemia due to AHCY deficiency","AHCY"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive defect of S-adenosylhomocysteine hydrolase, the enzyme that hydrolyses S-adenosylhomocysteine (SAH) to homocysteine and adenosine","In contrast to the benign hypermethioninaemias, this is a treatable disorder with real risk of neurological and hepatic disease if missed","Accumulated SAH inhibits many methyltransferases, so hypermethioninaemia occurs together with raised SAH and often raised S-adenosylmethionine","Typical features are hypotonia, myopathy with elevated creatine kinase, developmental delay and hepatic dysfunction","A markedly elevated SAH is the discriminating biochemical finding compared with MAT I/III and GNMT deficiency","May be detected through hypermethioninaemia on newborn screening, prompting confirmatory testing","Fetal or neonatal presentations with coagulopathy and severe hepatopathy have been described","Management is generic: a methionine-restricted diet with supplementation such as creatine and phosphatidylcholine to support methylation-dependent pathways"],"pathophysiology":"S-adenosylhomocysteine hydrolase removes S-adenosylhomocysteine, the product formed whenever S-adenosylmethionine donates a methyl group. Because SAH is a potent feedback inhibitor of methyltransferases, its clearance is essential to keep transmethylation running. When the enzyme is deficient, SAH accumulates and inhibits methylation reactions throughout the body, while methionine and S-adenosylmethionine also rise as the cycle backs up. Impaired methylation, particularly of substrates important for muscle and liver, together with disturbed creatine and phosphatidylcholine synthesis, is thought to produce the myopathy, hepatopathy and neurodevelopmental features.","additionalDifferentials":["Methionine adenosyltransferase I/III deficiency","Glycine N-methyltransferase deficiency","Adenosine kinase deficiency","Classical homocystinuria with secondary hypermethioninaemia","Neonatal or infantile liver disease of other cause with raised methionine","Primary myopathies with raised creatine kinase"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma amino acids demonstrating raised methionine","S-adenosylhomocysteine and S-adenosylmethionine measurement","Plasma total homocysteine","Creatine kinase and liver function tests including coagulation","AHCY gene sequencing for confirmation","Brain MRI where neurological features are present"],"redFlags":["Severe neonatal hepatopathy with coagulopathy","Progressive myopathy or encephalopathy","Markedly and persistently elevated S-adenosylhomocysteine","Developmental regression suggesting untreated methylation failure"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on early recognition and treatment; dietary and supplementary therapy can improve muscle, liver and developmental outcomes, whereas late diagnosis carries a risk of persistent neurological and hepatic impairment.","pitfalls":["Dismissing hypermethioninaemia as benign without measuring SAH, thereby missing this treatable disorder","Attributing the raised creatine kinase to a primary muscle disease","Mistaking the neonatal hepatopathy for another cause of infantile liver failure","Failing to distinguish it from MAT I/III and GNMT deficiency, which have very different implications"],"management":["Methionine-restricted diet as the mainstay, lowering plasma methionine and the accumulating S-adenosylhomocysteine that inhibits transmethylation","Creatine supplementation to compensate for impaired creatine synthesis and support the myopathy, with attention to phosphatidylcholine status","Supportive management of the hepatopathy and myopathy, including correction of coagulopathy from impaired hepatic synthesis","Serial biochemical monitoring of plasma methionine, S-adenosylhomocysteine, creatine kinase, liver function and coagulation to guide dietary titration","Confirmatory testing after detection of hypermethioninaemia on newborn screening, with early dietitian and metabolic input","Multidisciplinary care coordinated by metabolic medicine with neurology, hepatology and dietetics for developmental and hepatic surveillance","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, with AHCY testing and screening of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID","Baric I et al. S-adenosylhomocysteine hydrolase deficiency","Mudd SH. Hypermethioninemias of genetic and non-genetic origin"],"crossLinks":[]},"saber_sheath_trachea":{"name":"saber sheath trachea","system":"respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sabre-sheath trachea","scabbard trachea"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fixed deformity of the intrathoracic trachea in which the coronal (side-to-side) diameter is markedly reduced while the sagittal (front-to-back) diameter is preserved or increased, giving a scabbard-like cross-section.","It is strongly associated with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and is regarded as a radiological marker of long-standing airflow obstruction, typically in older men with a heavy smoking history.","The narrowing is confined to the intrathoracic trachea; the extrathoracic cervical portion is characteristically spared, and the deformity abruptly resolves at the thoracic inlet.","Tracheal cartilage may ossify and the lumen appears flattened laterally on axial CT, with a coronal-to-sagittal ratio well below normal.","Most patients' symptoms reflect the underlying COPD (chronic cough, sputum, dyspnoea) rather than the tracheal shape itself, which is usually an incidental imaging finding.","It should be distinguished from tracheomalacia; in the classic saber-sheath deformity the abnormal shape is relatively fixed, although coexisting expiratory collapse can occur.","The condition is thought to arise from chronically raised intrathoracic pressure and altered mechanical stresses transmitted to the tracheal cartilage over years.","Recognising the pattern prevents misinterpretation as an intrinsic stenosing or infiltrative tracheal disease."],"pathophysiology":"Saber-sheath trachea is a chronic remodelling deformity of the intrathoracic trachea associated with obstructive lung disease. Repeated coughing and the chronically elevated, widely fluctuating intrathoracic pressures generated in COPD are thought to impose abnormal mechanical loading on the tracheal cartilages, leading to lateral flattening with compensatory sagittal widening; the cartilages frequently calcify or ossify and become rigid, so the deformed shape is largely fixed. Because the deforming pressures act within the thorax, the cervical trachea above the thoracic inlet retains its normal rounded contour, producing the characteristic abrupt transition at that level. The altered geometry modestly increases airflow resistance, but the dominant functional impairment in affected patients is the underlying emphysema and small-airways disease rather than the tracheal deformity per se.","additionalDifferentials":["post-intubation or post-tracheostomy tracheal stenosis","tracheomalacia and excessive dynamic airway collapse","extrinsic tracheal compression by goitre or mediastinal mass","relapsing polychondritis","tracheobronchopathia osteochondroplastica","tracheobronchial amyloidosis","tracheobronchomegaly"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["chest CT with measurement of coronal and sagittal tracheal diameters","pulmonary function testing to characterise the associated obstructive disease","chest radiograph demonstrating the coronal narrowing on the frontal view","bronchoscopy where an intrinsic stenosing or infiltrative process needs exclusion","dynamic expiratory CT if coexisting tracheomalacia is suspected","assessment of smoking history and alpha-1 antitrypsin status where indicated","arterial blood gas or oximetry in advanced obstructive disease"],"redFlags":["new stridor or focal fixed narrowing suggesting a superimposed stenosing lesion","haemoptysis prompting evaluation for malignancy","rapidly progressive dyspnoea out of keeping with baseline COPD","features of systemic cartilage disease such as auricular or nasal inflammation"],"prognosis":"The tracheal deformity itself is usually clinically inconsequential and stable, with outcome determined by the severity and management of the underlying chronic obstructive pulmonary disease.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the coronal narrowing for an intrinsic stenosing or infiltrative tracheal disease and pursuing unnecessary intervention.","Overlooking coexisting tracheomalacia by relying only on static inspiratory imaging.","Assuming the deformity accounts for a patient's dyspnoea rather than attributing it to the underlying COPD.","Failing to note that the cervical trachea is spared, a key discriminating feature."],"management":["Optimise the underlying chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with inhaled long-acting bronchodilators (long-acting muscarinic antagonists and beta-agonists) and inhaled corticosteroids where indicated","Smoking cessation support as the key modifiable factor driving the airflow obstruction and tracheal remodelling","Pulmonary rehabilitation and vaccination against influenza and pneumococcus to reduce exacerbation-related decline","Prompt treatment of infective exacerbations with bronchodilators, corticosteroids and antibiotics as appropriate","Reassurance that the fixed tracheal deformity itself needs no specific intervention, reserving airway stenting for the rare case of critical obstruction or coexisting tracheomalacia","Assessment of alpha-1 antitrypsin status where early or atypical obstructive disease is suspected","Serial pulmonary function testing to monitor the severity of the underlying obstructive disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders, current edition","Murray and Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine, current edition","Standard thoracic radiology reference texts on central airway morphology"],"crossLinks":[]},"saccharopinuria":{"name":"Saccharopinuria","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["saccharopinuria","alpha-aminoadipic semialdehyde synthase deficiency (partial)","AASS deficiency variant","lysine degradation defect","hyperlysinaemia type II"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Reflects a defect in the second step of lysine degradation, where saccharopine is not efficiently cleaved, causing saccharopine to accumulate in plasma and urine alongside raised lysine","Regarded as a variant of the same bifunctional enzyme (alpha-aminoadipic semialdehyde synthase) affected in hyperlysinaemia, and is generally considered a benign biochemical trait rather than a defined disease","Most individuals identified are asymptomatic, with saccharopinuria detected incidentally on amino acid screening rather than because of a clinical syndrome","The biochemical signature on plasma amino acids is elevated lysine, and urine shows saccharopine that is not normally present in appreciable amounts","No specific dietary or drug treatment is established or generally needed, given the benign course","Should be recognised so that the finding is not misattributed as the cause of unrelated neurological symptoms","Inheritance follows an autosomal recessive pattern","Distinguishing it from other causes of raised lysine avoids unnecessary investigation and intervention"],"pathophysiology":"Lysine is degraded mainly through the saccharopine pathway, in which the bifunctional enzyme alpha-aminoadipic semialdehyde synthase first condenses lysine with 2-oxoglutarate to form saccharopine (lysine-ketoglutarate reductase activity) and then cleaves saccharopine (saccharopine dehydrogenase activity). In saccharopinuria the saccharopine-cleaving activity is relatively deficient, so the intermediate saccharopine accumulates and is excreted, while lysine also rises upstream. Because the block is partial and the accumulated metabolites are not overtly toxic, the condition is widely viewed as a benign biochemical peculiarity rather than a harmful metabolic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Hyperlysinaemia (lysine-ketoglutarate reductase-predominant deficiency)","Other causes of generalised aminoaciduria from renal tubular dysfunction","Secondary hyperlysinaemia from parenteral nutrition or protein overload","Ornithine and dibasic amino acid transport disorders","Artefactual amino acid elevations from sample handling","Coincidental neurological disorder unrelated to the biochemical finding"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative plasma amino acids demonstrating raised lysine","Urine amino acid chromatography identifying saccharopine","Repeat fasting samples to confirm the persistent pattern","Molecular analysis of the AASS gene where confirmation is desired","Assessment for renal tubular causes of aminoaciduria if the pattern is generalised","Correlation with clinical picture to exclude an alternative primary diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Attributing significant neurological symptoms to saccharopinuria without seeking an alternative explanation","Progressive clinical deterioration, which should prompt search for a coexisting disorder","An unexpectedly severe or atypical biochemical profile suggesting a different aminoacidopathy"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent, as saccharopinuria is generally an asymptomatic biochemical trait with no established link to progressive disease. Recognition mainly serves to avoid misdiagnosis and unnecessary treatment.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a benign biochemical trait for a treatable metabolic disease","Overlooking a separate, unrelated cause when symptoms coexist","Confusing the raised lysine with other primary hyperlysinaemias","Instituting dietary lysine restriction that is neither required nor evidence-based"],"management":["Reassurance that this is a benign biochemical trait requiring no dietary lysine restriction or drug therapy","Confirmation of the pattern with repeat fasting plasma and urine amino acid analysis before attributing any symptoms to it","Molecular confirmation with AASS gene analysis where diagnostic certainty is desired","Independent investigation of any coexisting neurological or other symptoms rather than misattributing them to the saccharopinuria","Exclusion of a generalised renal tubular cause when the aminoaciduria pattern is not isolated","Clear documentation in the record to prevent unnecessary future testing or treatment","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive trait if requested by the family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: disorders of lysine metabolism","Nyhan, Atlas of Inherited Metabolic Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"sacrococcygeal_teratoma":{"name":"Sacrococcygeal teratoma","system":"Paediatric surgery / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCT","presacral teratoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Bailey & Love (paediatric surgery)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common congenital tumour of the newborn, arising at the base of the coccyx and typically presenting as a large sacral mass, more often in females","The Altman classification describes the balance of external versus internal (presacral/pelvic) tumour, which affects detectability and surgical difficulty","Predominantly external tumours are usually recognised at birth, whereas mainly internal lesions may present later with pelvic mass effect, constipation, or urinary symptoms","Most tumours found in the neonatal period are benign (mature or immature teratoma), but the risk of malignancy rises the later the lesion presents","Serum alpha-fetoprotein is a useful marker; persistently elevated or rising levels after resection raise concern for malignant (yolk sac) elements or recurrence","Large vascular tumours detected antenatally can cause high-output cardiac failure and fetal hydrops due to arteriovenous shunting","Definitive treatment is complete surgical excision including the coccyx, since leaving the coccyx behind markedly increases recurrence","Long-term follow-up with clinical review and tumour markers is required to detect recurrence and to monitor bowel and bladder function"],"pathophysiology":"Sacrococcygeal teratoma is a germ cell tumour thought to arise from pluripotent cells near the primitive streak (Hensen's node) at the caudal end of the embryo. It therefore contains tissues derived from multiple germ layers and grows at the coccygeal region, extending externally, into the pelvis, or both. Large, highly vascular tumours can act as arteriovenous shunts, causing high-output heart failure and hydrops in the fetus. Malignant transformation, usually to yolk sac tumour, becomes more likely with delayed diagnosis and with predominantly internal, poorly detected lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["Myelomeningocele or other neural tube defect","Anterior meningocele","Lipoma or lipomeningocele","Rectal duplication cyst","Neuroblastoma or other pelvic tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antenatal and postnatal ultrasound to assess size, vascularity, and internal extent","MRI of the pelvis and spine to define intrapelvic extension and exclude a spinal component","Serum alpha-fetoprotein at baseline and during follow-up","Fetal echocardiography and monitoring for hydrops in large vascular antenatal lesions","Histological examination of the resected specimen to grade maturity and detect malignant elements"],"redFlags":["Antenatal high-output cardiac failure or hydrops from a large vascular tumour, which is life-threatening","Rapid tumour growth or haemorrhage requiring urgent surgical planning","Rising alpha-fetoprotein after resection suggesting malignant recurrence","Delayed presentation of an internal tumour, which carries higher malignancy risk"],"prognosis":"Benign lesions completely excised in the neonatal period with removal of the coccyx have an excellent prognosis. Prognosis worsens with malignant histology, incomplete excision, delayed diagnosis, and antenatal complications such as hydrops. Even after benign resection, some children have long-term bowel or bladder dysfunction.","pitfalls":["Failing to remove the coccyx, which greatly increases recurrence risk","Missing a predominantly internal (presacral) tumour on external examination alone","Neglecting postoperative alpha-fetoprotein surveillance for recurrence","Overlooking associated anorectal or spinal anomalies and later functional problems"],"management":["Complete surgical excision of the tumour with en bloc removal of the coccyx as the definitive treatment, reducing recurrence risk","Platinum-based chemotherapy (such as cisplatin, etoposide and bleomycin) for malignant yolk sac elements or unresectable and metastatic disease","Antenatal foetal surveillance with serial ultrasound and foetal echocardiography, considering early delivery or foetal intervention for large vascular lesions causing high-output failure or hydrops","Serial serum alpha-fetoprotein monitoring after resection to detect malignant transformation or recurrence","Histological grading of the resected specimen to assess maturity and identify malignant components guiding adjuvant therapy","Long-term surveillance for functional sequelae such as bowel and bladder dysfunction after pelvic resection","Coordinated care through a paediatric surgical, oncology and neonatology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"sacroiliac_joint_dysfunction":{"name":"Sacroiliac joint dysfunction","system":"Musculoskeletal / Spine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SIJ dysfunction","sacroiliac joint pain","SI joint syndrome","mechanical sacroiliac pain"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pain arising from the sacroiliac joint, typically felt over the buttock and posterior superior iliac spine and sometimes referring to the groin or posterior thigh","The Fortin finger test, in which the patient points to pain just inferomedial to the posterior superior iliac spine, is characteristic","No single examination test is diagnostic, and a cluster of provocation tests improves accuracy","Provocation tests include FABER (Patrick), thigh thrust, compression, distraction (gapping), and Gaenslen manoeuvres","A commonly used rule is that three or more positive provocation tests raise the likelihood of a sacroiliac source","Pain often worsens with transitional movements, prolonged sitting or standing, and climbing stairs","An image-guided intra-articular anaesthetic block is the reference standard for diagnosis","Management is largely conservative (physiotherapy, pelvic stabilisation, analgesia), with image-guided injection and, rarely, joint fusion for refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Sacroiliac joint dysfunction encompasses pain generated by the sacroiliac joint, a large, partly synovial joint that transmits load between the spine and pelvis and is stabilised by strong ligaments. Pain may result from altered joint mechanics (hypermobility or hypomobility), ligamentous strain, degenerative change, pregnancy-related laxity, leg-length discrepancy, or previous lumbar fusion increasing adjacent load. The joint and its ligaments are innervated by branches from the dorsal rami, and both intra-articular and extra-articular (ligamentous) sources can contribute to the pain.","additionalDifferentials":["Lumbar facet joint or discogenic pain","Lumbar radiculopathy","Hip osteoarthritis or labral pathology","Trochanteric bursitis or gluteal tendinopathy","Inflammatory sacroiliitis (axial spondyloarthritis)","Piriformis syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["A cluster of provocation tests (thigh thrust, compression, distraction, FABER, Gaenslen)","Image-guided intra-articular anaesthetic block as the reference standard","MRI of the sacroiliac joints to exclude inflammatory sacroiliitis (bone marrow oedema)","Plain radiographs of the pelvis and hips to assess degenerative or structural change","Inflammatory markers and HLA-B27 if spondyloarthropathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Inflammatory features (young patient, morning stiffness, alternating buttock pain, good response to anti-inflammatories) suggesting axial spondyloarthritis","Constitutional symptoms suggesting infection or malignancy","Progressive neurological deficit"],"prognosis":"Most cases improve with conservative treatment and correction of contributing factors; image-guided injections often give useful relief, and outcomes are generally favourable, although a minority have persistent pain.","pitfalls":["Missing inflammatory sacroiliitis by not considering axial spondyloarthritis","Relying on a single provocation test rather than a cluster","Overlooking hip pathology presenting as buttock or groin pain"],"management":["Structured physiotherapy targeting lumbopelvic and core stabilisation, with correction of contributing factors such as leg-length discrepancy","Simple analgesia and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for symptomatic pain relief","Image-guided intra-articular corticosteroid and local anaesthetic injection for both diagnostic confirmation and therapeutic relief","Radiofrequency ablation of the lateral branch sensory nerves for refractory pain confirmed to arise from the joint","Pelvic girdle support and activity modification for pregnancy-related laxity and transitional-movement pain","Sacroiliac joint arthrodesis or minimally invasive fusion reserved for disabling pain unresponsive to conservative measures","Referral to a spinal or pain specialist when structured conservative treatment fails"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","Laslett: Evidence-based diagnosis and treatment of the painful sacroiliac joint"],"crossLinks":["facet_joint_syndrome","coccydynia"]},"sacroiliitis":{"name":"Sacroiliitis","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["inflammation of the sacroiliac joint"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rheumatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Inflammation of the sacroiliac joint, a hallmark of axial spondyloarthritis but with several causes","Presents with inflammatory-type buttock or low back pain, often alternating, with morning stiffness","Common causes are the spondyloarthritides (ankylosing spondylitis, psoriatic, reactive and enteropathic arthritis)","Infective (septic) sacroiliitis is an important distinct cause, usually unilateral with acute severe pain and systemic features","MRI detects active inflammation (bone marrow oedema) earlier than radiographic changes","Management depends on the cause: immunomodulation for inflammatory disease, antimicrobials for infection"],"pathophysiology":"Sacroiliitis is inflammation of the sacroiliac joint that may be immune-mediated or infective. In spondyloarthritis, enthesitis and synovial inflammation at the joint are driven by genetic susceptibility (HLA-B27) and dysregulated immunity, producing bone marrow oedema, erosion and, over time, ankylosis. Septic sacroiliitis results from haematogenous or contiguous infection of the joint. Mechanical and degenerative processes can also inflame the joint. The pattern (unilateral versus bilateral, acute versus chronic) and systemic features help distinguish the causes.","additionalDifferentials":["Axial spondyloarthritis / ankylosing spondylitis","Septic (infective) sacroiliitis","Mechanical low back pain","Osteitis condensans ilii","Sacral insufficiency fracture","Referred pain from the hip or lumbar spine"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the sacroiliac joints for active inflammation","Radiographs for structural change","HLA-B27 and inflammatory markers","Assessment for features of spondyloarthritis and its extra-articular manifestations","Blood cultures and joint sampling where infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Acute severe unilateral pain with fever suggesting septic sacroiliitis","Immunosuppression or injecting drug use raising infection risk","Progressive structural damage","Neurological deficit suggesting an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the cause; inflammatory sacroiliitis is controllable with modern therapy though it may progress to ankylosis, and septic sacroiliitis does well with prompt antimicrobial treatment.","pitfalls":["Missing septic sacroiliitis, which needs urgent antimicrobial therapy","Relying on radiographs and missing early inflammation seen on MRI","Attributing inflammatory back pain to mechanical causes","Overlooking associated spondyloarthritis features"],"management":["Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs as first-line therapy for inflammatory sacroiliitis of spondyloarthritis, with regular exercise and physiotherapy","Biologic therapy with tumour necrosis factor inhibitors or interleukin-17 inhibitors for inflammatory disease refractory to NSAIDs","Prompt culture-directed antimicrobial therapy for septic sacroiliitis, with drainage of any associated abscess","Screening for and management of extra-articular spondyloarthritis manifestations such as uveitis, psoriasis and inflammatory bowel disease","Serial MRI and inflammatory markers to monitor disease activity and response to immunomodulation","Blood cultures and joint sampling with early referral where infection is suspected to guide targeted treatment","Rheumatology-led multidisciplinary care, with infectious diseases input for the infective form"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rheumatology reference texts (standard reference)","ASAS spondyloarthritis references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"saethre_chotzen_syndrome":{"name":"Saethre-Chotzen syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acrocephalosyndactyly type III","TWIST1-related craniosynostosis","ACS3"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by loss-of-function mutations or deletions in the TWIST1 transcription factor gene, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with variable expressivity","Typically presents with coronal synostosis (often unilateral, producing plagiocephaly) with a low frontal hairline and facial asymmetry","Ptosis of the eyelids is a characteristic and helpful diagnostic clue","The external ears are small with a prominent crus, and there may be mild cutaneous syndactyly, usually of the second and third fingers","Intelligence is usually normal, distinguishing it from some other craniosynostosis syndromes, though large deletions involving neighbouring genes can be associated with learning difficulty","Brachydactyly, broad great toes and other minor digital anomalies may be present","Larger contiguous gene deletions at the TWIST1 locus should prompt consideration of developmental delay and require specific genetic evaluation","Management is by a craniofacial team, with cranial vault surgery guided by suture involvement and monitoring for raised intracranial pressure"],"pathophysiology":"Saethre-Chotzen syndrome results from haploinsufficiency of TWIST1, a basic helix-loop-helix transcription factor that regulates the balance between proliferation and differentiation of osteoprogenitor cells in the cranial sutures. Reduced TWIST1 activity disturbs the normal restraint on osteogenic differentiation at the coronal suture, leading to premature fusion and the resulting craniofacial dysmorphology. Because the defect is loss of function, whole-gene deletions and larger contiguous deletions of adjacent genes can broaden the phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Muenke syndrome (FGFR3 coronal synostosis, can look similar)","Crouzon syndrome (FGFR2)","Apert syndrome (severe syndactyly)","Baller-Gerold syndrome (craniosynostosis with radial defects)","Non-syndromic unilateral coronal synostosis","Craniofrontonasal syndrome (EFNB1)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of TWIST1, including deletion/duplication analysis to detect whole-gene or contiguous deletions","Three-dimensional cranial CT to define coronal and other suture involvement","Clinical photography and craniofacial assessment to document asymmetry and ptosis","MRI of the brain if raised intracranial pressure or structural anomaly is suspected","Developmental assessment, particularly where a large deletion is identified","Radiographs of the hands and feet to document syndactyly and digital anomalies"],"redFlags":["Signs of raised intracranial pressure from multisuture involvement","Ptosis severe enough to obscure the visual axis and risk amblyopia","Developmental regression or delay suggesting a larger contiguous gene deletion","Progressive facial asymmetry needing surgical planning"],"prognosis":"Most individuals have normal cognition and a good outcome after craniofacial surgery, but those with large deletions may have additional developmental problems, and lifelong monitoring for intracranial pressure and visual complications is advisable.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the condition as Muenke syndrome without molecular testing, as the phenotypes overlap","Overlooking amblyopia risk from ptosis","Not performing deletion analysis, thereby missing large TWIST1 deletions associated with delay","Assuming a purely cosmetic problem and failing to monitor intracranial pressure"],"management":["Surgical cranial vault remodelling and suture release for the coronal synostosis, timed to relieve constraint and correct the craniofacial deformity","Surgical correction of the characteristic ptosis to protect vision and prevent amblyopia","Surveillance for raised intracranial pressure with fundoscopy and imaging, given the risk from premature suture fusion","Ophthalmological monitoring for amblyopia and visual complications, with orthoptic input","Corrective surgery for cutaneous syndactyly or digital anomalies where function is affected","Developmental assessment and support, particularly where a large contiguous gene deletion is identified","Coordinated craniofacial multidisciplinary care with clinical genetics oversight","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant TWIST1 disorder, including deletion testing and screening of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Saethre-Chotzen syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"salicylate_poisoning":{"name":"Salicylate poisoning","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["aspirin poisoning","salicylate toxicity","acetylsalicylic acid overdose","salicylism","oil of wintergreen (methyl salicylate) poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic acid-base picture is a mixed primary respiratory alkalosis and primary high anion-gap metabolic acidosis, most evident in adults","Early features include tinnitus, deafness, nausea, vomiting, hyperventilation and hyperpyrexia","A normal or rising blood pH with low carbon dioxide reflects the respiratory alkalosis, but falling pH signals dangerous progression","A single salicylate concentration can mislead, so serial levels are needed because of delayed and erratic absorption, especially with enteric-coated preparations or bezoars","Concentrated liquid methyl salicylate (oil of wintergreen) can cause rapid severe toxicity from a small volume","Acidaemia increases the non-ionised fraction that crosses into the central nervous system, so intubation with inadequate ventilation can be catastrophic","Urinary alkalinisation enhances salicylate elimination and limits central penetration, while haemodialysis is used for severe toxicity, high levels or end-organ effects","Maintaining potassium is essential, as hypokalaemia prevents effective urinary alkalinisation"],"pathophysiology":"Salicylate directly stimulates the medullary respiratory centre, producing hyperventilation and a primary respiratory alkalosis. Simultaneously it uncouples oxidative phosphorylation, impairs the citric acid cycle and promotes accumulation of organic acids and lactate, generating a high anion-gap metabolic acidosis and heat production with hyperthermia. As pH falls, a greater proportion of salicylate becomes non-ionised and crosses the blood-brain barrier, worsening neurotoxicity. Salicylate also interferes with glucose metabolism, coagulation and gastrointestinal integrity, and in severe cases causes pulmonary and cerebral oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Diabetic or other high anion-gap ketoacidosis","Sepsis with lactic acidosis","Toxic alcohol (methanol or ethylene glycol) poisoning","Theophylline or iron toxicity","Sympathomimetic toxicity or thyroid storm","Primary central nervous system infection causing hyperpnoea and confusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial serum salicylate concentrations to track absorption and elimination","Arterial blood gas with anion-gap and acid-base interpretation","Serum electrolytes, particularly potassium, and glucose","Renal function and lactate","Coagulation profile and paracetamol level in intentional overdose","Chest radiograph if pulmonary oedema is suspected and urine pH monitoring during alkalinisation"],"redFlags":["Altered mental status, seizures or coma indicating central nervous system toxicity","A falling or normalising pH after initial alkalosis, signalling decompensation","Pulmonary or cerebral oedema","Hyperthermia, haemodynamic instability or intractable acidosis","Very high or rising salicylate concentrations, or need for airway control"],"prognosis":"Prompt recognition, urinary alkalinisation and timely haemodialysis when indicated usually yield good outcomes, but delayed treatment, marked acidaemia and central nervous system involvement are associated with high mortality.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single salicylate level and missing continued absorption","Intubating and under-ventilating, causing acidaemia and rapid neurological deterioration","Allowing hypokalaemia, which defeats urinary alkalinisation","Misinterpreting the early respiratory alkalosis as reassuring","Delaying haemodialysis in patients with high levels or end-organ toxicity"],"management":["Gastrointestinal decontamination with activated charcoal for recent significant ingestion, with consideration of repeated doses for enteric-coated preparations or a suspected bezoar","Urinary alkalinisation with intravenous sodium bicarbonate to enhance renal elimination of salicylate and reduce central nervous system penetration","Correction of hypokalaemia, which is essential for effective alkalinisation, alongside fluid resuscitation for volume depletion","Glucose supplementation to counter neuroglycopenia even when peripheral glucose appears adequate","Haemodialysis for severe toxicity, central nervous system features, pulmonary oedema, renal failure or refractory acidaemia","Avoidance of intubation where possible, and if unavoidable, maintenance of aggressive ventilation to prevent worsening acidaemia and neurotoxicity","Serial salicylate concentrations and arterial blood gas monitoring to track absorption, elimination and acid-base status","Early involvement of clinical toxicology and critical care for severe or progressive poisoning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","UpToDate: Salicylate (aspirin) poisoning in adults"],"crossLinks":[]},"salivary_gland_tumor":{"name":"Salivary gland tumor","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["salivary gland neoplasm","parotid tumour","salivary gland cancer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most salivary gland tumours occur in the parotid gland and the majority of these are benign, whereas tumours of the smaller submandibular, sublingual, and minor salivary glands carry a proportionally higher risk of malignancy","The typical presentation is a slow-growing, painless lump, and pleomorphic adenoma is the most common benign tumour while mucoepidermoid and adenoid cystic carcinomas are common malignant types","Features suggesting malignancy include rapid growth, pain, fixation to surrounding tissue, skin involvement, cervical lymphadenopathy, and especially facial nerve weakness in parotid lesions","Facial nerve palsy in association with a parotid mass is a strong indicator of malignant invasion and demands urgent evaluation","Fine-needle aspiration cytology and imaging help characterise the lesion, though definitive diagnosis often rests on excisional histology","Adenoid cystic carcinoma is notable for perineural spread and a tendency to late recurrence and distant metastasis, requiring prolonged follow-up","Surgery is the primary treatment, with parotidectomy performed to preserve the facial nerve whenever oncologically feasible, and appropriate gland excision for other sites","Radiotherapy is used adjuvantly for high-grade tumours, positive margins, perineural invasion, or nodal disease, while enucleation of pleomorphic adenoma is avoided due to recurrence and malignant transformation risk"],"pathophysiology":"Salivary gland tumours arise from the epithelial and myoepithelial cells of the major and minor salivary glands and comprise a diverse group with widely varying behaviour. Benign tumours such as pleomorphic adenoma grow slowly and are well circumscribed but can recur if incompletely excised and, rarely, undergo malignant transformation over time. Malignant tumours vary from low-grade to aggressive high-grade carcinomas that invade locally, track along nerves (notably adenoid cystic carcinoma), and metastasise to regional nodes and distant sites. The inverse relationship between gland size and malignancy risk reflects the differing tumour types that predominate in each gland.","additionalDifferentials":["Reactive or infective lymphadenopathy near the gland","Sialadenitis or salivary duct stones with gland swelling","Salivary gland cyst or sialocele","Lymphoma involving the salivary gland","Metastatic disease to intraparotid lymph nodes","Benign lymphoepithelial lesions and autoimmune sialadenitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound as first-line imaging, often with guided fine-needle aspiration cytology","MRI or CT to define tumour extent, deep lobe involvement, and perineural or nodal spread","Assessment of facial nerve function in parotid lesions","Histopathological examination of the excised specimen for type, grade, and margins","Staging imaging for high-grade or clinically malignant tumours"],"redFlags":["Facial nerve weakness accompanying a parotid mass","A rapidly enlarging, painful, or fixed salivary gland lump","Skin ulceration or infiltration over the gland","Associated cervical lymphadenopathy suggesting nodal metastasis"],"prognosis":"Benign tumours have an excellent prognosis after complete excision; malignant tumour outcomes depend on grade, stage, and histological type, with high-grade and adenoid cystic carcinomas prone to recurrence and late distant spread despite treatment.","pitfalls":["Assuming all salivary lumps are benign and missing malignant features such as pain or nerve palsy","Enucleating a pleomorphic adenoma, risking recurrence and later malignant transformation","Underestimating perineural spread and late recurrence of adenoid cystic carcinoma","Inadequate facial nerve assessment and counselling before parotid surgery"],"management":["Complete surgical excision as the mainstay, with superficial or total parotidectomy and meticulous facial nerve preservation, or excision of the affected submandibular or minor gland","Adequate resection of pleomorphic adenoma with a cuff of normal tissue to prevent recurrence and later malignant transformation","Neck dissection for clinically involved nodes or high-grade malignancy, with sacrifice of infiltrated facial nerve where invaded","Adjuvant radiotherapy for high-grade tumours, positive margins, or adenoid cystic carcinoma with perineural spread","Facial nerve rehabilitation, including grafting or reanimation, where the nerve is sacrificed","Prolonged follow-up, especially for adenoid cystic carcinoma given its tendency to late recurrence and distant metastasis","Management through a head and neck multidisciplinary team integrating surgery, oncology and pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology"],"crossLinks":[]},"salla_disease":{"name":"Salla disease","system":"Metabolic / Mitochondrial & peroxisomal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Free sialic acid storage disease, Finnish type","SLC17A5-related sialic acid storage disorder","Sialuria, Finnish type"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Salla disease is an autosomal recessive free sialic acid storage disorder caused by variants in SLC17A5, which encodes sialin, the lysosomal membrane transporter that exports free sialic acid (N-acetylneuraminic acid) from the lysosome to the cytosol.","It represents the milder, later-onset and slowly progressive end of the free sialic acid storage disease spectrum; the severe infantile form (ISSD) lies at the other end and is allelic.","It is markedly enriched in the Finnish population owing to a founder missense variant, hence its origin in the Salla region, although cases occur worldwide.","Typical presentation is in infancy with hypotonia, nystagmus and delayed motor milestones, evolving into ataxia, spasticity, intellectual disability and dysarthria over childhood.","Brain MRI characteristically shows hypomyelination and a thin, hypoplastic corpus callosum, reflecting the central hypomyelinating leukoencephalopathy.","The biochemical hallmark is greatly increased free (unconjugated) sialic acid in urine and in cultured fibroblasts, distinguishing it from sialidosis, where bound/oligosaccharide-linked sialic acid accumulates.","Unlike infantile sialic acid storage disease, coarse features, visceromegaly, dysostosis and fetal hydrops are usually absent or mild in classic Salla disease.","Diagnosis combines raised urinary free sialic acid with SLC17A5 molecular testing; enzyme assays are not diagnostic because the defect is in transport, not degradation."],"pathophysiology":"SLC17A5 (chromosome 6q13) encodes sialin, a proton-driven transporter in the lysosomal membrane that carries free sialic acid, generated by lysosomal breakdown of sialylated glycoproteins and glycolipids, out of the lysosome into the cytosol for reuse or excretion. In Salla disease hypomorphic variants, classically the Finnish founder missense allele, retain partial transporter activity, so free sialic acid accumulates progressively within enlarged lysosomes across many tissues, especially the central nervous system. The consequences are twofold: intralysosomal storage distends cells and disrupts function, and impaired cytosolic delivery of sialic acid limits its availability for re-sialylation of glycoconjugates important for myelin membranes. The result is a central hypomyelinating leukoencephalopathy with a thin corpus callosum and cerebral and cerebellar involvement, producing the progressive motor, cognitive and cerebellar signs. The severity of disease tracks with residual sialin function: near-complete loss produces the severe infantile phenotype with prenatal onset, hydrops, organomegaly and early death, whereas partial function produces the attenuated, slowly progressive Salla phenotype. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Infantile free sialic acid storage disease (severe allelic SLC17A5 phenotype)","Sialidosis (neuraminidase deficiency) and galactosialidosis, in which bound sialic acid accumulates","Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease and other hypomyelinating leukodystrophies (e.g. PMLD, 4H/POLR3-related)","Other lysosomal storage disorders presenting with hypotonia and regression","Congenital disorders of glycosylation","Cerebral palsy or non-progressive hypotonia in early presentations","Mitochondrial leukoencephalopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinary free (unconjugated) sialic acid quantification, markedly elevated","Free sialic acid measurement in cultured skin fibroblasts","SLC17A5 molecular genetic testing, including the Finnish founder variant where relevant","Brain MRI for hypomyelination and thin corpus callosum","Nerve conduction and evoked potential studies where clinically indicated","Developmental and neuropsychological assessment for baseline and monitoring","Exclusion of sialidosis/galactosialidosis via oligosaccharide and enzyme studies"],"redFlags":["Progressive loss of previously acquired motor or cognitive skills","Worsening spasticity or bulbar dysfunction affecting swallowing and airway safety","New seizures or acute neurological deterioration","Feeding difficulty with failure to thrive","Rapid early decline suggesting the severe infantile phenotype rather than classic Salla disease"],"prognosis":"Classic Salla disease is slowly progressive and compatible with survival into adulthood, though with significant intellectual disability and motor disability; the allelic severe infantile form carries a poor prognosis with early death. Management is supportive, as no disease-modifying therapy is currently available.","pitfalls":["Confusing Salla disease with sialidosis; the two differ fundamentally in whether free or bound sialic acid accumulates","Ordering enzyme assays expecting a degradative defect, when the underlying problem is lysosomal transport","Mistaking the hypomyelination and thin corpus callosum for another hypomyelinating leukodystrophy without measuring urinary free sialic acid","Labelling early hypotonia as non-progressive cerebral palsy and missing the metabolic diagnosis","Assuming absence of Finnish ancestry excludes the diagnosis, when cases occur in many populations"],"management":["Supportive, symptom-directed care as no disease-modifying therapy exists for this free sialic acid storage disorder","Antispasticity agents such as baclofen for the evolving spasticity, with physiotherapy to maintain mobility and posture","Antiepileptic drugs for seizures where these develop","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy for hypotonia, ataxia and motor disability, with mobility aids and orthoses","Nutritional and feeding support, including safe swallowing assessment as bulbar function declines","Developmental support, special education and speech and language therapy for the intellectual disability and dysarthria","Multidisciplinary neurology and metabolic follow-up with baseline and serial developmental assessment","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive SLC17A5 disorder, with carrier testing of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard metabolic medicine and lysosomal storage disorder references","Clinical and paediatric neurology reference texts","Inherited metabolic disease diagnostic guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"salmonellosis":{"name":"Nontyphoidal Salmonellosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nontyphoidal Salmonella","NTS","nontyphoidal Salmonella gastroenteritis","invasive NTS disease"],"chapter":171,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1314, 1319-1321","clinicalPearls":["NTS infection most often produces a gastroenteritis that is clinically indistinguishable from that caused by other enteric pathogens, with nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea beginning 6-48 h after ingestion of contaminated food or water; stools are usually loose, nonbloody, and of moderate volume, though large-volume watery stools, bloody stools, or frank dysentery can occur (p.1320).","NTS gastroenteritis is usually self-limited: diarrhea resolves within 3-7 days and fever within 72 h; stool cultures may remain positive for 4-5 weeks after infection (p.1320).","Globally S. Typhimurium and S. Enteritidis are the most common serotypes causing human disease; in the United States NTS is the second most common cause of foodborne outbreaks after norovirus (p.1319).","Unlike S. Typhi and S. Paratyphi (human-only reservoir), NTS is acquired from multiple animal and plant reservoirs - especially eggs, poultry, undercooked ground meat, and dairy, plus produce contaminated with animal waste and contact with animals (reptiles, amphibians, chicks, etc.) (p.1319).","Up to 8% of patients with NTS gastroenteritis develop bacteremia, and of those 5-10% develop localized (metastatic) infections; bacteremia and metastatic infection are most common with S. Choleraesuis and S. Dublin and among infants, the elderly, and the immunocompromised, especially those with HIV (p.1320).","Invasive NTS disease (predominantly S. Typhimurium ST313 and S. Enteritidis ST11) is a leading cause of bacteremia in children and HIV-infected adults in sub-Saharan Africa and Southeast Asia; it typically presents as a nonspecific febrile illness resembling enteric fever and uncommonly causes diarrhea (p.1319-1320).","Diagnosis rests on isolating the organism from freshly passed stool or from blood or another normally sterile fluid; culture-independent molecular tests are increasingly used but positive specimens should still undergo primary isolation, and all NTS isolates should be referred to public health for serotyping (p.1320-1321).","Reactive arthritis can follow NTS gastroenteritis, occurring most frequently in persons carrying the HLA-B27 antigen (p.1321).","Localized NTS infections include intraabdominal (hepatic/splenic abscess, cholecystitis), CNS (meningitis - chiefly in infants 1-4 months and HIV-infected adults), pulmonary (lobar pneumonia), urinary/genital, and bone/joint disease; Salmonella osteomyelitis classically affects the femur, tibia, humerus, or lumbar vertebrae and is strongly linked to sickle cell disease and hemoglobinopathies (p.1320-1321)."],"pathophysiology":"All Salmonella infections begin with ingestion of organisms, usually in contaminated food or water; the infectious dose ranges from ~200 to 10^6 CFU, and the ingested dose helps determine incubation period and severity. Conditions reducing gastric acidity (age <1 year, acid-suppression therapy, achlorhydria) or compromising intestinal integrity (inflammatory bowel disease, cytotoxic chemotherapy, prior GI surgery, antibiotic-induced microbiome alteration) increase susceptibility. In contrast to enteric fever (mononuclear infiltration of small-bowel mucosa), NTS gastroenteritis is characterized by massive polymorphonuclear leukocyte infiltration into both large- and small-bowel mucosa. This response depends largely on induction of interleukin 8, a strong neutrophil chemoattractant secreted by intestinal cells in response to NTS colonization and translocation of bacterial proteins and LPS into host cytoplasm with subsequent inflammasome activation; neutrophil degranulation and release of toxic substances damages the mucosa, producing inflammatory diarrhea. NTS persistence is aided by its ability to use tetrathionate (generated from thiosulfate via inflammatory reactive oxygen species) for metabolism in a microaerophilic, inflamed gut, allowing it to outcompete endogenous flora. Unlike typhoidal serotypes, NTS effectively colonizes the intestinal tract rather than evolving as a primarily systemic pathogen.","additionalDifferentials":["Other bacterial enteric pathogens (Campylobacter, Shigella, enteroinvasive/enterotoxigenic E. coli)","Norovirus and other viral gastroenteritis","Intestinal parasites","Pseudoappendicitis (NTS can mimic acute appendicitis)","Inflammatory bowel disease (NTS rarely produces an IBD-like illness)","Enteric (typhoid) fever - especially with invasive NTS presenting as nonspecific febrile illness"],"infectionMimics":["Enteric (typhoid/paratyphoid) fever from S. Typhi/S. Paratyphi presenting as undifferentiated febrile illness","Other causes of community-acquired bloodstream infection in endemic regions (malaria co-infection common)","Other bacterial gastroenteritides (Campylobacter, Shigella, E. coli)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute appendicitis (pseudoappendicitis)","Inflammatory bowel disease","Postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome / functional dyspepsia (recognized sequelae)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool culture (freshly passed) with primary isolation even after culture-independent molecular detection","Blood cultures - obtain whenever fever is prolonged or recurrent; high-grade bacteremia defined as >50% of three or more blood-culture sets positive suggests endovascular infection","Culture-independent molecular diagnostic panels for enteric pathogens","Serotyping of all NTS isolates via public health laboratories","Echocardiography to evaluate suspected endocarditis","CT imaging to localize deep/focal infection","Indium-labeled white-cell scanning to identify localized infection","Culture of joint fluid aspirate, bone biopsy, abscess drainage, or CSF when localized infection is suspected","Antimicrobial susceptibility testing with attention to ciprofloxacin MIC (Etest or alternative method) given the lowered susceptibility breakpoint and decreased-ciprofloxacin-susceptibility strains not reliably detected by nalidixic acid screening or standard disk diffusion"],"redFlags":["Invasive disease / bacteremia and endovascular seeding in the elderly, immunocompromised (including HIV), infants/neonates, and patients with sickle cell disease or other hemoglobinopathies","High-grade or persistent bacteremia, particularly with preexisting valvular heart disease, atherosclerotic vascular disease, prosthetic vascular graft, or aortic aneurysm - suspect NTS endovascular infection","Prolonged fever with back, chest, or abdominal pain after an episode of gastroenteritis in an elderly patient - suspect arteritis/mycotic aneurysm","NTS meningitis in infants 1-4 months of age or HIV-infected adults - severe sequelae and death in up to 60% of cases","Persons >50 years with known or suspected atherosclerosis (increased risk of invasive infection and endovascular seeding)"],"prognosis":"NTS gastroenteritis is usually self-limited, with diarrhea resolving in 3-7 days and fever within 72 h; chronic carriage develops in <1% of cases. Outcomes worsen markedly with invasive disease: endocarditis and arteritis are rare (<1%) but carry serious, potentially fatal complications (valve perforation, endomyocardial abscess, infected mural thrombus, pericarditis, mycotic aneurysm, aneurysm rupture, aortoenteric fistula, vertebral osteomyelitis), and NTS meningitis is fatal in up to 60% of cases. In 2017 there were ~535,000 invasive NTS cases and ~77,500 deaths worldwide, ~80% in sub-Saharan Africa. Acute NTS gastroenteritis was associated with a threefold increased risk of dyspepsia and irritable bowel syndrome at 1 year in a Spanish study.","pitfalls":["Antibiotics should NOT be used routinely to treat uncomplicated NTS gastroenteritis - the illness is usually self-limited and antibiotics do not significantly shorten fever or diarrhea, while they have been associated with increased relapse rates, prolonged fecal/gastrointestinal carriage, and adverse drug reactions.","Only ~1% of clinical isolates ferment lactose, so a high index of suspicion is needed to detect rare lactose-fermenting strains.","Decreased-ciprofloxacin-susceptibility NTS strains may not be reliably detected by nalidixic acid susceptibility testing or standard ciprofloxacin disk diffusion, risking missed resistance and treatment failure.","Neonates, the elderly, and immunosuppressed patients with NTS gastroenteritis are especially prone to dehydration and invasive infection and may need hospitalization and antibiotic therapy - they are exceptions to the no-routine-antibiotics rule."],"management":["Oral rehydration and fluid and electrolyte replacement for the usually self-limited gastroenteritis, avoiding routine antibiotics which prolong carriage","Antimicrobial therapy with a fluoroquinolone, third-generation cephalosporin or azithromycin reserved for severe, invasive or high-risk disease (infants, the elderly and the immunocompromised, especially those with HIV)","Blood cultures and prompt treatment of bacteraemia, with prolonged antimicrobial courses for endovascular or metastatic infection","Surgical drainage of focal abscesses and repair of any infected (mycotic) aneurysm alongside antimicrobials","Avoidance of antimotility agents, which can worsen invasive disease","Monitoring for metastatic complications such as endocarditis and arteritis in those with high-grade or persistent bacteraemia","Public health notification with serotyping of isolates and source tracing to control outbreaks"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal graded severity score for NTS. Disease is described along a clinical spectrum: (1) uncomplicated self-limited gastroenteritis; (2) bacteremia (low-grade defined as <50% of blood cultures positive vs high-grade >50% of three or more sets positive, the latter raising concern for endovascular infection); and (3) localized/metastatic infection (intraabdominal, CNS, pulmonary, urinary/genital, bone/joint/soft tissue) and endovascular infection (endocarditis, arteritis). Host-risk stratification for invasive disease includes neonates (up to ~3 months), persons >50 years with atherosclerosis, and those with immunosuppression, cardiac valvular/endovascular abnormalities, or significant joint disease.","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, Chapter 171: Salmonellosis (Pegues DA, Miller SI), pp.1314, 1319-1321","Marchello CS et al: Complications and mortality of non-typhoidal salmonella invasive disease: A global systematic review and meta-analysis. Lancet Infect Dis 22:692, 2022","Medalla F et al: Increased Incidence of Antimicrobial-Resistant Nontyphoidal Salmonella Infections, United States, 2004-2016. Emerg Infect Dis 27:1662, 2021","Onwuezobe IA et al: Antimicrobials for treating symptomatic non-typhoidal Salmonella infection. Cochrane Database Syst Rev 11:CD001167, 2012","Pulford CV et al: Stepwise evolution of Salmonella Typhimurium ST313 causing bloodstream infection in Africa. Nat Microbiol 6:327, 2021"],"crossLinks":["GASTROENTERITIS","ENTERIC_FEVER","DYSENTERY"]},"salpingitis_isthmica_nodosa":{"name":"Salpingitis isthmica nodosa","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SIN","tubal diverticulosis","diverticular disease of the fallopian tube"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology reference textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by nodular thickening of the isthmic portion of the fallopian tube, with diverticula of tubal epithelium extending into a hypertrophied muscular wall","Despite its name, it is a non-infective structural process rather than active infection of the tube","It is an important association of both subfertility and tubal ectopic pregnancy","The condition is frequently bilateral and may be identified when investigating infertility","Hysterosalpingography characteristically shows small diverticular outpouchings of contrast at the isthmus, giving a nodular appearance","Definitive confirmation is histological, demonstrating epithelial diverticula surrounded by hypertrophic smooth muscle","Because tubal function is compromised, in vitro fertilisation, which bypasses the tube, is often the most effective route to pregnancy"],"pathophysiology":"In salpingitis isthmica nodosa, the mucosal epithelium of the tubal isthmus forms diverticula that burrow into a surrounding, hypertrophied muscular layer, producing palpable and radiological nodularity. The cause is uncertain and may relate to previous chronic inflammation or to an adenomyosis-like ingrowth of epithelium into the muscularis. The distorted, partially obstructed tube impairs gamete and embryo transport, which underlies both the reduced fertility and the increased risk of tubal implantation of an embryo.","additionalDifferentials":["Hydrosalpinx","Genital tract tuberculosis affecting the tube","Tubal endometriosis","Chronic salpingitis","Tubal polyps or intramural pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Hysterosalpingography demonstrating diverticular outpouchings and nodularity at the isthmus","Laparoscopy with chromopertubation to assess tubal patency and appearance","Histological examination, which provides the definitive diagnosis","Transvaginal ultrasound, which is of limited value but helps exclude other pelvic pathology","Assessment of overall fertility status, including partner and ovulatory factors"],"redFlags":["Ectopic pregnancy in a woman known to have the condition","Significant subfertility requiring specialist reproductive input","Features suggesting coexisting tuberculous or other tubal disease","Acute pain that could indicate tubal implantation of a pregnancy"],"prognosis":"The lesion is benign, but it carries a meaningful impact on fertility and a raised risk of ectopic pregnancy; assisted conception that bypasses the tube generally offers the best chance of a successful pregnancy.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise the subtle diverticular changes on imaging","Not counselling the woman about the increased risk of ectopic pregnancy","Attempting reconstructive tubal surgery, which has limited success","Overlooking the diagnosis as a cause of otherwise unexplained subfertility"],"management":["In vitro fertilisation that bypasses the diseased tube as the treatment offering the best chance of pregnancy","Salpingectomy of an affected, obstructed or hydrosalpinx-bearing tube before assisted conception to improve implantation","Microsurgical tubal resection and reanastomosis of the isthmic segment in carefully selected younger women desiring natural conception","Counselling on the raised risk of tubal ectopic pregnancy, with early ultrasound in any future pregnancy to confirm intrauterine location","Prompt treatment of an ectopic pregnancy with methotrexate or surgery as appropriate","Assessment and management of coexisting fertility factors, including ovulatory and male factors","Referral to reproductive medicine for individualised fertility planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference textbook","Reproductive medicine reference text"],"crossLinks":[]},"salzmann_nodular_degeneration":{"name":"Salzmann nodular degeneration","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Salzmann's nodular degeneration","SND","corneal nodular degeneration"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology / Cornea by Krachmer","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A slowly progressive, non-inflammatory degeneration producing elevated bluish-white or grey subepithelial nodules, often in a ring around the mid-periphery","Frequently follows chronic ocular surface irritation or inflammation, including trachoma, chronic blepharitis, phlyctenulosis, dry eye, and contact lens wear","More common in middle-aged women and often bilateral, though asymmetric","Symptoms range from asymptomatic to foreign body sensation, irritation, and reduced vision when nodules encroach centrally or induce irregular astigmatism","Recurrent erosions can occur where nodules disturb epithelial adhesion","Histology shows subepithelial hyaline deposits with a disrupted or absent Bowman layer beneath the nodules","Management ranges from lubrication and treating the underlying surface disease to superficial keratectomy or phototherapeutic keratectomy for visually significant nodules","Nodules may recur after removal, particularly if the underlying surface disorder is not controlled"],"pathophysiology":"Salzmann nodular degeneration is a non-inflammatory degeneration in which elevated hyaline nodules form in the subepithelial space, typically as a reaction to long-standing ocular surface disease or chronic irritation. Fibrous and hyaline material accumulates between the epithelium and a disrupted Bowman layer, elevating the surface into discrete nodules. These raised lesions distort the overlying epithelium and tear film, causing foreign body sensation and inducing irregular astigmatism when they lie near the visual axis. Because the degeneration is often driven by an underlying surface disorder, control of that condition is important to limit progression and recurrence.","additionalDifferentials":["Reis-Bucklers and Thiel-Behnke dystrophies","Gelatinous drop-like corneal dystrophy","Band keratopathy","Spheroidal (climatic droplet) degeneration","Peripheral corneal pannus and scarring from chronic surface disease","Pterygium or pinguecula encroaching on the cornea"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination documenting elevated subepithelial nodules and their location","Corneal topography to assess irregular astigmatism from the nodules","Anterior segment optical coherence tomography to measure nodule thickness and plane","Evaluation for underlying ocular surface disease (blepharitis, dry eye, prior inflammation)","Fluorescein staining to assess epithelial integrity and erosions","Histology of excised nodules showing subepithelial hyaline material when tissue is available"],"redFlags":["Central nodules causing significant visual loss","Recurrent painful erosions from disrupted epithelial adhesion","Rapid enlargement or atypical appearance suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Recurrence after surgical removal indicating uncontrolled underlying surface disease"],"prognosis":"Progression is usually slow, and vision is often preserved unless nodules involve the visual axis or induce irregular astigmatism. Superficial removal generally improves symptoms, but nodules can recur if the underlying ocular surface disease is not addressed.","pitfalls":["Removing nodules without treating the underlying surface disease, leading to recurrence","Mistaking nodules for a corneal dystrophy without considering the acquired degeneration","Overlooking induced irregular astigmatism as the cause of reduced vision","Aggressive keratectomy risking scarring when observation would suffice"],"management":["Treatment of the underlying ocular surface disease driving the degeneration, including lid hygiene for blepharitis and control of dry eye","Ocular surface lubrication with tear substitutes to relieve foreign body sensation and irritation","Superficial keratectomy or manual nodule removal when nodules encroach on the visual axis or induce irregular astigmatism","Management of recurrent corneal erosions with lubricants, bandage contact lenses or epithelial debridement","Review and modification of contact lens wear as a contributing irritant","Corneal topography surveillance to monitor astigmatism and detect progression or recurrence","Referral to a corneal specialist for surgical removal and ongoing management where vision is threatened"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Cornea by Krachmer, Mannis and Holland"],"crossLinks":[]},"samter_triad":{"name":"Samter triad (aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease)","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Head and Neck","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease","AERD","aspirin-sensitive asthma","NSAID-exacerbated respiratory disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the triad of chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps, asthma, and respiratory reactions to aspirin and other cyclo-oxygenase-1 inhibiting NSAIDs","Reactions typically manifest as bronchospasm, rhinorrhoea, nasal congestion and sometimes ocular or cutaneous symptoms within one to three hours of NSAID exposure","The nasal polyps tend to be extensive and recur rapidly after surgery, and the asthma is often difficult to control","It is a non-IgE-mediated pseudo-allergy, so conventional allergy skin testing to aspirin is not diagnostic","Anosmia or marked hyposmia is common and often an early feature","Diagnosis is clinical, supported where needed by supervised aspirin provocation challenge in an appropriate setting","Management combines control of sinonasal and asthmatic inflammation, strict NSAID avoidance, and consideration of aspirin desensitisation in selected patients","Leukotriene-modifying drugs and biologic therapies targeting type 2 inflammation are useful adjuncts"],"pathophysiology":"Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease is driven by dysregulation of arachidonic acid metabolism in a background of chronic eosinophilic type 2 airway inflammation. Affected individuals have constitutively increased production of pro-inflammatory cysteinyl leukotrienes and relatively deficient protective prostaglandin E2 signalling. Inhibition of cyclo-oxygenase-1 by aspirin or other NSAIDs removes the restraining influence of prostaglandin E2, causing a surge in cysteinyl leukotrienes that provokes bronchoconstriction and nasosinusal inflammation. The same underlying eosinophilic inflammation produces the recalcitrant nasal polyposis and asthma even in the absence of NSAID exposure.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps without aspirin sensitivity","Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss)","Non-allergic rhinitis","Cystic fibrosis with sinonasal polyps in younger patients","IgE-mediated NSAID allergy or urticaria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasal endoscopy to assess polyp burden and distribution","CT of the paranasal sinuses to define disease extent before surgery","Pulmonary function testing to characterise and monitor asthma","Supervised graded aspirin provocation challenge where the diagnosis is uncertain","Blood and tissue eosinophil assessment to gauge type 2 inflammation"],"redFlags":["Severe bronchospasm or anaphylactoid reaction after NSAID ingestion","Acute severe asthma exacerbation with respiratory compromise","Rapidly recurring or obstructive polyps threatening the airway or vision","Features suggesting vasculitis such as neuropathy, purpura or systemic involvement"],"prognosis":"The condition is chronic and tends to relapse, with nasal polyps frequently recurring after surgery and asthma often persistent. Multimodal management including NSAID avoidance, aspirin desensitisation in suitable patients, leukotriene modifiers and biologic therapy can substantially improve symptom control and quality of life.","pitfalls":["Giving aspirin or NSAIDs for unrelated indications and precipitating a severe reaction","Relying on conventional allergy testing, which does not detect this pseudo-allergic mechanism","Repeated polyp surgery without addressing the underlying inflammatory drive","Overlooking eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis in patients with systemic features"],"management":["Strict avoidance of aspirin and other cyclo-oxygenase-1-inhibiting NSAIDs as the central trigger, with education on hidden sources","Leukotriene-modifying therapy with a leukotriene receptor antagonist or 5-lipoxygenase inhibitor to counter the excess cysteinyl leukotrienes","Intranasal corticosteroids and saline irrigation for the nasal polyposis, with inhaled corticosteroids and bronchodilators for the asthma","Aspirin desensitisation followed by maintenance aspirin therapy in suitable patients to reduce polyp recurrence and improve control","Biologic therapy targeting type 2 inflammation, such as anti-interleukin-5, anti-interleukin-4/13 or anti-IgE agents, for refractory disease","Endoscopic sinus surgery to debulk extensive polyps, recognising the high rate of recurrence","Monitoring with serial pulmonary function testing and nasal endoscopy through a joint otolaryngology, allergy and respiratory service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"sandhoff_disease":{"name":"Sandhoff disease","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GM2 gangliosidosis type 2","hexosaminidase A and B deficiency","HEXB deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard metabolic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive GM2 gangliosidosis caused by deficiency of both hexosaminidase A and hexosaminidase B, due to pathogenic variants in the HEXB gene encoding the shared beta subunit","It closely resembles Tay-Sachs disease clinically, with infantile onset of developmental regression, hypotonia, an exaggerated startle response, and a cherry-red macular spot","Unlike Tay-Sachs disease, visceral involvement such as hepatosplenomegaly and mild skeletal changes can occur, reflecting the additional deficiency of hexosaminidase B","Progressive macrocephaly, seizures, spasticity, and blindness follow, with death usually in early childhood in the infantile form","Juvenile and adult-onset forms exist with slower motor, cerebellar, and psychiatric presentations","The absence of both hexosaminidase isoenzymes on assay distinguishes it biochemically from Tay-Sachs disease, in which hexosaminidase B is preserved","It is not restricted to any single ethnic group, unlike the increased Tay-Sachs carrier frequency in the Ashkenazi Jewish population","No disease-modifying therapy is established, so management is supportive with genetic counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Sandhoff disease is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in HEXB, encoding the beta subunit common to both hexosaminidase A (alpha-beta) and hexosaminidase B (beta-beta). Loss of the beta subunit therefore reduces activity of both isoenzymes, impairing degradation of GM2 ganglioside and related glycolipids and glycoproteins. GM2 accumulates within neuronal lysosomes, driving progressive neurodegeneration, while the broader substrate accumulation from combined enzyme loss also affects visceral tissues, producing organomegaly not typically seen in Tay-Sachs disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Tay-Sachs disease","GM1 gangliosidosis","Niemann-Pick disease and other storage disorders with organomegaly","Other causes of infantile neurodegeneration with cherry-red spot","Leukodystrophies with developmental regression"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assay of hexosaminidase A and total hexosaminidase activity, showing deficiency of both","HEXB molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Ophthalmological examination for the macular cherry-red spot","Neuroimaging to characterise neurodegeneration","Assessment for visceral involvement and carrier testing of relatives"],"redFlags":["Rapid developmental regression with exaggerated startle in an infant","Emerging seizures or loss of vision","Progressive swallowing difficulty with aspiration risk","Organomegaly accompanying neurodegeneration"],"prognosis":"The infantile form is progressive and fatal, typically within the first few years of life. Juvenile and adult forms progress more slowly with a variable course. Care remains supportive, as no disease-modifying therapy is established.","pitfalls":["Assuming Tay-Sachs disease without recognising that combined hexosaminidase A and B deficiency indicates Sandhoff disease","Overlooking organomegaly as a distinguishing feature from Tay-Sachs disease","Not offering carrier screening and counselling to at-risk families","Attributing the exaggerated startle to a benign cause and delaying evaluation"],"management":["Supportive, symptom-directed care as no disease-modifying therapy is established for this GM2 gangliosidosis","Antiepileptic drugs for the seizures that emerge with disease progression","Antispasticity agents and physiotherapy for spasticity and to prevent contractures","Nutritional and feeding support, including gastrostomy feeding as bulbar function fails","Respiratory care with management of secretions and aspiration risk","Physiotherapy, developmental support and early integration of palliative care for the progressive infantile form","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive HEXB disorder, with carrier testing of relatives and reproductive options","Coordinated multidisciplinary care across metabolic medicine, neurology and community paediatric services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"sandifer_syndrome":{"name":"sandifer syndrome","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sandifer's syndrome","Sandifer-Sutcliffe syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sandifer syndrome describes paroxysmal dystonic posturing — abnormal head and neck extension, torticollis and back arching (opisthotonus) — occurring in association with gastro-oesophageal reflux and, less commonly, hiatus hernia.","Episodes characteristically cluster around or shortly after feeds and abate during sleep, a temporal pattern that helps distinguish them from epileptic seizures.","Consciousness is fully preserved throughout episodes and there is no post-ictal drowsiness, a key discriminator from seizure activity.","The posturing is thought to be a reflexive or learned manoeuvre that relieves the discomfort of acid reflux or reduces oesophageal obstruction, rather than a primary neurological disorder.","It typically presents in infancy and early childhood and is over-represented in children with neurodisability, in whom reflux is common and communication limited.","Effective treatment of the underlying reflux — positional and feeding measures with acid suppression — usually abolishes the movements, which is both diagnostic and therapeutic.","Associated features may include feeding refusal, irritability, faltering growth, iron-deficiency anaemia and, occasionally, apparent apnoeic or brief resolved unexplained events.","Misdiagnosis as epilepsy, infantile spasms or a primary dystonia is common and frequently leads to unnecessary neurological investigation and antiepileptic exposure."],"pathophysiology":"Sandifer syndrome is a movement phenomenon secondary to gastro-oesophageal reflux disease, and sometimes hiatus hernia, rather than a disorder of the central nervous system. Acid or volume reflux into the distal oesophagus is thought to provoke discomfort and vagally mediated afferent stimulation, and the stereotyped extension and lateral flexion of the neck and trunk are believed to be a protective postural response that lengthens and straightens the oesophagus, reduces reflux, relieves pain and may safeguard the airway. Because the movements are reflexive and non-epileptic, the electroencephalogram is normal and awareness is retained; resolution of the reflux, whether through maturation of the lower oesophageal sphincter or with therapy, characteristically resolves the posturing.","additionalDifferentials":["Epileptic seizures, including focal tonic seizures and infantile spasms","Benign paroxysmal torticollis of infancy and paroxysmal tonic upgaze","Primary dystonia, including dopa-responsive dystonia, and other movement disorders","Benign myoclonus of infancy and shuddering attacks","Congenital muscular torticollis due to sternocleidomastoid contracture","Drug-induced dystonic reactions, for example to antidopaminergic agents","Posterior fossa or cervical cord pathology and atlantoaxial instability"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis supported by a symptom diary or home video correlating episodes with feeds","Therapeutic trial of anti-reflux measures as a diagnostic-therapeutic step","Oesophageal pH and multichannel intraluminal impedance monitoring","Upper gastrointestinal contrast study to assess for hiatus hernia, malrotation or anatomical anomaly","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with biopsy where oesophagitis or eosinophilic oesophagitis is suspected","Electroencephalogram, typically normal, if seizures cannot be excluded clinically","Neuroimaging of brain and cervical spine if neurological signs or atypical features are present"],"redFlags":["Altered awareness, cyanosis or apnoea during episodes, suggesting seizure or airway compromise","Faltering growth, persistent vomiting or haematemesis","Focal neurological signs, developmental regression or an abnormal interictal examination","Episodes unrelated to feeds or persisting despite adequate reflux treatment","Bilious vomiting raising concern for malrotation with volvulus"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; the condition is self-limiting and typically resolves with maturation of the lower oesophageal sphincter and control of reflux during infancy. When correctly identified and treated there are no long-term neurological sequelae.","pitfalls":["Labelling episodes as epilepsy and starting antiepileptic drugs unnecessarily","Overlooking reflux because posturing rather than regurgitation dominates the presentation","Attributing the torticollis solely to a musculoskeletal cause","Failing to consider Sandifer syndrome in neurodisabled children in whom reflux is common","Stopping anti-reflux therapy prematurely before symptoms have resolved"],"management":["Treatment of the underlying gastro-oesophageal reflux as the definitive measure, with positional and feeding modifications such as upright positioning and thickened or smaller frequent feeds","Acid suppression with a proton pump inhibitor or H2-receptor antagonist to relieve the reflux driving the posturing","Surgical anti-reflux fundoplication reserved for refractory cases or where a significant hiatus hernia is present","Reassurance that the posturing is a benign, self-limiting reflux-related phenomenon that resolves with reflux control and sphincter maturation","Avoidance of unnecessary antiepileptic drugs by recognising the preserved consciousness and normal electroencephalogram","Symptom diary or home video monitoring to confirm the temporal link to feeds and response to therapy","Additional gastroenterology and multidisciplinary input for children with neurodisability in whom reflux is common"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","NASPGHAN-ESPGHAN Pediatric Gastroesophageal Reflux Clinical Practice Guidelines","Swaiman's Pediatric Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"sanfilippo_syndrome":{"name":"Sanfilippo syndrome (MPS III)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPS III","mucopolysaccharidosis type III","heparan sulfate storage disease","Sanfilippo A B C D","sulfamidase / NAGLU / HGSNAT / GNS deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Scriver's OMMBID","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Dominated by progressive neurodegeneration and severe behavioural disturbance, with comparatively mild somatic and skeletal features","Comprises four biochemical subtypes (A, B, C, D), each from deficiency of a distinct enzyme in the heparan sulfate degradation pathway","Typical course is an initial period of near-normal development followed by developmental regression, hyperactivity, aggression, and sleep disturbance","Coarse facial features and organomegaly are subtle compared with other mucopolysaccharidoses, which frequently delays diagnosis","Only heparan sulfate accumulates and is excreted in excess, unlike the mixed glycosaminoglycan pattern of MPS I and II","Enzyme assays identify the specific subtype after urinary heparan sulfate points to the diagnosis","Progressive dementia, seizures, and loss of mobility mark the later stages"],"pathophysiology":"Sanfilippo syndrome is caused by deficiency of one of four lysosomal enzymes required for the sequential degradation of heparan sulfate: heparan-N-sulfatase (type A, SGSH), alpha-N-acetylglucosaminidase (type B, NAGLU), acetyl-CoA:alpha-glucosaminide N-acetyltransferase (type C, HGSNAT), and N-acetylglucosamine-6-sulfatase (type D, GNS). The resulting block leads to lysosomal accumulation of heparan sulfate, which is particularly toxic to neurons, producing the profound central nervous system degeneration that characterises the disorder. Somatic tissues are relatively spared because dermatan sulfate metabolism is intact. Excess heparan sulfate is excreted in the urine. All four subtypes are inherited in an autosomal recessive manner and are clinically indistinguishable, requiring enzymatic or molecular testing to separate them.","additionalDifferentials":["Other mucopolysaccharidoses, especially Hunter syndrome (MPS II)","Idiopathic autism spectrum disorder or attention deficit hyperactivity disorder","Rett syndrome and other neurodevelopmental regression disorders","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses","Other lysosomal storage disorders with dementia","Non-syndromic intellectual disability with behavioural problems"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative urinary glycosaminoglycan analysis showing isolated heparan sulfate elevation","Enzyme assays for the four candidate enzymes to determine subtype","Molecular testing of SGSH, NAGLU, HGSNAT, or GNS genes","Brain MRI to document white matter and atrophic changes","Electroencephalography where seizures are suspected","Developmental and neuropsychological assessment to track regression"],"redFlags":["Progressive developmental regression after a period of normal milestones","Escalating behavioural disturbance with severe sleep disruption","New-onset seizures signalling advancing neurodegeneration","Loss of ambulation and swallowing difficulty in later stages"],"prognosis":"The disorder is relentlessly progressive, with most patients declining into severe dementia and immobility, and survival typically limited to the second or third decade. No disease-modifying therapy is established, so management is supportive.","pitfalls":["Attributing early behavioural symptoms solely to a primary neurodevelopmental disorder and missing the storage disease","Expecting prominent coarse facies or organomegaly and dismissing the diagnosis when they are mild","Relying on a single enzyme assay without confirming the specific subtype","Overlooking heparan sulfate on urinary screening if only total glycosaminoglycan is measured"],"management":["Supportive, symptom-directed care as no disease-modifying therapy is established, with enzyme replacement ineffective against the central nervous system disease","Behavioural management of hyperactivity, aggression and severe sleep disturbance, including melatonin and a structured environment","Antiepileptic drugs for seizures as the neurodegeneration advances","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and mobility support to maintain function and manage progressive motor decline","Nutritional and feeding support, including safe swallowing assessment and gastrostomy feeding in late disease","Coordinated multidisciplinary and palliative care addressing the relentless neurological decline","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, with subtype-specific gene testing and screening of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"sanjad_sakati_syndrome":{"name":"Sanjad-Sakati syndrome","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HRD syndrome","Hypoparathyroidism-retardation-dysmorphism syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric endocrinology and clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with congenital hypoparathyroidism and neonatal hypocalcaemic seizures or tetany.","Autosomal recessive and caused by TBCE variants, with a strong founder effect in consanguineous Arab and other Middle Eastern kindreds.","Comprises the triad of hypoparathyroidism, severe pre- and postnatal growth retardation and dysmorphism (the hypoparathyroidism-retardation-dysmorphism, or HRD, phenotype).","Characteristic facies include microcephaly, deep-set eyes, a thin beaked nose, thin upper lip, micrognathia and large low-set ears.","Intellectual disability and small hands and feet are typical.","Allelic to Kenny-Caffey syndrome type 1 (also TBCE), which is distinguished by medullary stenosis of the long bones and comparatively preserved development.","Recurrent infections and failure to thrive are common, and intercurrent illness can precipitate hypocalcaemic crises.","Management is lifelong calcium and active vitamin D analogues such as alfacalcidol or calcitriol, with monitoring for hypercalciuria and nephrocalcinosis."],"pathophysiology":"Sanjad-Sakati syndrome is an autosomal recessive multisystem disorder caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in TBCE, which encodes a tubulin-specific chaperone required for the correct folding of alpha-tubulin and for microtubule assembly. A founder deletion is highly prevalent in Arab and other Middle Eastern populations with consanguinity. Defective microtubule function impairs parathyroid gland embryogenesis, producing parathyroid aplasia or hypoplasia and hence congenital hypoparathyroidism, while also disrupting intrauterine and postnatal growth and neurodevelopment. Deficient parathyroid hormone secretion leads to hypocalcaemia with hyperphosphataemia, which manifests in the neonatal period as tetany and seizures and over time may cause basal ganglia calcification. The condition is allelic to autosomal recessive Kenny-Caffey syndrome type 1, which shares TBCE variants but is distinguished by medullary stenosis of the long bones and relatively preserved cognition.","additionalDifferentials":["Kenny-Caffey syndrome type 1 (TBCE) and type 2 (FAM111A)","DiGeorge / 22q11.2 deletion syndrome","Mitochondrial disorders with hypoparathyroidism such as Kearns-Sayre syndrome","Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 (AIRE) with hypoparathyroidism","Barakat (HDR) syndrome with hypoparathyroidism, deafness and renal dysplasia (GATA3)","Isolated familial hypoparathyroidism (GCM2, PTH or activating CASR variants)","Pseudohypoparathyroidism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium (low), phosphate (raised) and parathyroid hormone (inappropriately low or undetectable)","Serum magnesium and vitamin D status","Renal ultrasound at baseline and for monitoring of nephrocalcinosis","Skeletal survey to look for the medullary stenosis that characterises Kenny-Caffey syndrome","Growth parameters and bone age","Developmental and neurological assessment with brain imaging for basal ganglia calcification","Molecular testing of TBCE, including the recognised founder variant"],"redFlags":["Neonatal seizures due to severe hypocalcaemia","Laryngospasm, stridor or cardiac arrhythmia from hypocalcaemia","Serious infection or sepsis, to which affected infants are susceptible","Failure to thrive with dehydration","Progressive neurological deterioration with basal ganglia calcification"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is guarded, with significant early mortality from hypocalcaemic seizures, infections and failure to thrive; survivors have persistent short stature and intellectual disability and need lifelong multidisciplinary care.","pitfalls":["Attributing neonatal seizures to hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy and overlooking hypocalcaemia and hypoparathyroidism","Over-replacement with calcium and vitamin D causing hypercalciuria and nephrocalcinosis","Confusing the condition with DiGeorge syndrome without checking 22q11.2 status and immune and cardiac features","Failing to recognise the founder variant in consanguineous families, delaying genetic diagnosis","Not distinguishing it from Kenny-Caffey syndrome, which shows skeletal medullary stenosis"],"management":["Lifelong treatment of the congenital hypoparathyroidism with oral calcium and an active vitamin D analogue such as alfacalcidol or calcitriol","Emergency intravenous calcium for hypocalcaemic tetany or seizures in the neonatal period and during crises","Correction of coexisting magnesium deficiency, which can impair the response to calcium replacement","Serial monitoring of calcium, phosphate and renal function, with renal ultrasound surveillance for nephrocalcinosis from therapy","Nutritional support and active management of failure to thrive, with prompt aggressive treatment of intercurrent infections","Developmental support and neurological monitoring, including brain imaging for basal ganglia calcification","Coordinated care through paediatric endocrinology, clinical genetics and allied services","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive TBCE disorder, with founder-variant testing and family screening in consanguineous kindreds"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric endocrinology reference texts","Clinical genetics references on hereditary hypoparathyroidism syndromes","Standard references on metabolic bone disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"sapho_syndrome":{"name":"SAPHO syndrome","system":"Rheumatology / Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["synovitis acne pustulosis hyperostosis osteitis syndrome","acne-associated spondyloarthropathy","chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis with skin involvement"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the pentad of synovitis, acne, pustulosis, hyperostosis and osteitis, though few patients show all features simultaneously","The anterior chest wall (sternoclavicular and sternocostoclavicular region) is the most characteristic site, producing painful bony swelling and hyperostosis","Palmoplantar pustulosis and severe acne (including acne fulminans and conglobata) are the typical dermatological associations, but skin disease may precede, follow or never accompany the bone disease","Regarded as part of the autoinflammatory spectrum and overlapping closely with chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis in children","Bone lesions are sterile; cultures are negative and the disorder is not a true pyogenic infection","Bone scintigraphy classically shows the 'bull's head' sign of tracer uptake across the manubrium and both sternoclavicular joints","Management uses NSAIDs first-line, with bisphosphonates, conventional and biologic DMARDs (including TNF and IL-1 directed therapy) for refractory disease"],"pathophysiology":"SAPHO syndrome is considered a sterile autoinflammatory disorder in which innate immune dysregulation drives neutrophil-rich inflammation of bone, joints and skin. Aberrant activation of the innate immune system, with a role proposed for cutaneous commensal organisms such as Cutibacterium acnes acting as an immune trigger rather than a true infection, promotes release of pro-inflammatory cytokines including interleukin-1, interleukin-17, interleukin-23 and tumour necrosis factor. The result is osteitis with reactive new bone formation (hyperostosis), synovitis at adjacent joints, and neutrophilic skin lesions. It shares genetic and mechanistic features with the paediatric entity chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis, and both are viewed as ends of one autoinflammatory sterile osteomyelitis spectrum.","additionalDifferentials":["Infective (pyogenic) osteomyelitis","Ankylosing spondylitis and other spondyloarthropathies","Psoriatic arthritis","Bone metastases or primary bone tumour","Paget disease of bone","Chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis (paediatric overlap)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Whole-body MRI or technetium bone scintigraphy to map multifocal osteitis and detect the anterior chest wall 'bull's head' pattern","Plain radiographs and CT of the anterior chest wall showing hyperostosis and sclerosis","Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP), which are often mildly to moderately raised","Bone biopsy with culture to exclude infection and malignancy when the diagnosis is uncertain","Dermatological assessment for palmoplantar pustulosis and severe acne"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting true infective osteomyelitis (fever, rigors, positive cultures, rapidly progressive destruction)","Bony destruction or a mass raising concern for malignancy requiring biopsy","Neurological compromise from spinal involvement or vertebral collapse","Vascular compression at the anterior chest wall (e.g. subclavian vein thrombosis)"],"prognosis":"SAPHO syndrome typically follows a chronic relapsing-remitting course; most patients maintain function, but persistent anterior chest wall hyperostosis, spinal involvement and cutaneous flares can cause long-term pain and disability.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing sterile osteitis as pyogenic osteomyelitis and giving prolonged unnecessary antibiotics or surgery","Overlooking the diagnosis when skin disease is absent or separated in time from bone symptoms","Attributing anterior chest wall pain to cardiac disease and missing the characteristic bone lesions","Failing to recognise the overlap with paediatric chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis"],"management":["Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs as first-line therapy for the bone, joint and anterior chest wall pain","Bisphosphonates to suppress the osteitis and hyperostosis, particularly for refractory bone pain","Conventional disease-modifying agents such as methotrexate, escalating to tumour necrosis factor inhibitors or interleukin-1 blockade for resistant disease","Dermatological treatment of the associated palmoplantar pustulosis and severe acne, including topical therapy and systemic retinoids","Trials of antimicrobial therapy directed at Cutibacterium acnes in selected cases, reflecting its proposed immune-triggering role","Whole-body MRI or bone scintigraphy with inflammatory markers to map disease and monitor activity, and bone biopsy to exclude infection or malignancy where uncertain","Rheumatology-led multidisciplinary care with dermatology input and physiotherapy to preserve function"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Oxford Textbook of Rheumatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"sarcocystosis":{"name":"Sarcocystosis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sarcocystis infection","sarcosporidiosis","Sarcocystis hominis infection","Sarcocystis suihominis infection","muscular sarcocystosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell/Manson's Tropical Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sarcocystis species are coccidian parasites with a two-host cycle, and humans can act either as the definitive (intestinal) host or as an accidental intermediate (muscular) host","Intestinal sarcocystosis follows eating raw or undercooked beef (Sarcocystis hominis) or pork (Sarcocystis suihominis) containing tissue cysts, causing self-limiting nausea, abdominal pain and diarrhoea","Muscular sarcocystosis follows ingesting oocysts from food or water contaminated by the faeces of the definitive carnivore host, leading to cyst formation in human muscle","Muscular disease presents with myalgia, fever, transient tender muscle swellings and eosinophilia, and has occurred in travellers to Southeast Asia in outbreak form","Intestinal infection is diagnosed by finding sporocysts or oocysts in stool, whereas muscular infection is confirmed by demonstrating sarcocysts on muscle biopsy","Raised creatine kinase and peripheral eosinophilia support a diagnosis of muscular disease","Intestinal disease usually needs only supportive care, while symptomatic muscular disease is managed supportively, with cotrimoxazole or antiparasitic agents sometimes tried"],"pathophysiology":"Sarcocystis has an obligatory predator-prey life cycle. When humans eat undercooked meat containing mature tissue cysts, the parasite completes sexual reproduction in the intestinal wall and sporocysts are shed in the stool, producing intestinal sarcocystosis. When humans instead ingest sporocysts shed by another carnivore, the parasite behaves as if in an intermediate host: released sporozoites disseminate haematogenously and encyst in skeletal and cardiac muscle, causing muscular sarcocystosis. The intramuscular cysts and the inflammatory, often eosinophilic, response to them account for the myalgia, muscle swelling and enzyme release seen in the muscular form, while intestinal invasion produces a milder self-limiting enteritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Trichinellosis","Other coccidian enteric infections such as cystoisosporiasis","Viral or bacterial gastroenteritis","Polymyositis and other inflammatory myopathies","Toxocariasis and other causes of eosinophilic myositis","Acute HIV seroconversion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy for sporocysts or oocysts in intestinal disease","Skeletal muscle biopsy demonstrating sarcocysts in muscular disease","Serum creatine kinase and inflammatory markers","Full blood count for peripheral eosinophilia","Magnetic resonance imaging of affected muscle groups","Travel and dietary history to identify exposure"],"redFlags":["Severe or persistent myositis with marked creatine kinase elevation","Cardiac involvement suggested by chest symptoms or troponin rise","Systemic illness with high fever and recurrent muscle swellings","Clustering of cases among travellers suggesting a common source outbreak"],"prognosis":"Intestinal sarcocystosis is self-limiting; muscular sarcocystosis tends to run a relapsing but ultimately self-limiting course over weeks to months, though prolonged myalgia can cause considerable morbidity.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish intestinal from muscular disease, which differ in exposure, presentation and diagnosis","Overlooking sarcocystosis in a traveller with fever, myalgia and eosinophilia","Relying on stool testing to diagnose muscular disease, in which stool is typically negative","Missing dietary history of raw or undercooked meat"],"management":["Manage intestinal disease supportively with oral rehydration and antiemetics, as the enteritis is self-limiting and needs no specific antiparasitic","For symptomatic muscular disease use non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and, in severe inflammatory reactions, corticosteroids to control myalgia and muscle swelling","Consider a trial of an anticoccidial agent such as co-trimoxazole or albendazole in troublesome muscular disease, recognising that curative antiparasitic treatment is unproven","Advise thorough cooking of beef and pork and access to clean, uncontaminated food and water to prevent both intestinal and muscular infection","Monitor creatine kinase and eosinophilia through the relapsing muscular course and reassure patients that resolution is expected over weeks to months","Refer to infectious diseases or tropical medicine for confirmed or outbreak-associated muscular disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"sarcoidosis_lung":{"name":"Pulmonary sarcoidosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sarcoidosis","pulmonary sarcoidosis","sarcoid","Boeck's sarcoid"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A multisystem granulomatous disorder of unknown cause in which the lungs and intrathoracic lymph nodes are most often involved","Presentations range from asymptomatic bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy found incidentally to cough, dyspnoea, and chest discomfort","Loefgren syndrome (bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, erythema nodosum, arthralgia, and fever) is an acute presentation with a favourable prognosis","Chest imaging is often staged by the presence of hilar adenopathy and parenchymal infiltration, with fibrosis in advanced disease","Diagnosis rests on compatible clinical and radiological features plus biopsy showing non-caseating granulomas, after exclusion of infection and other granulomatous disease","Serum ACE may be raised but is neither sensitive nor specific and should not be relied upon for diagnosis or monitoring","Hypercalcaemia and hypercalciuria can occur due to dysregulated vitamin D activation within granulomas","Many patients remit spontaneously; corticosteroids are reserved for significant or progressive organ involvement, with immunosuppressants as steroid-sparing options"],"pathophysiology":"Sarcoidosis is thought to arise when an unknown antigen triggers an exaggerated cell-mediated immune response in a genetically susceptible host. Antigen-presenting cells activate CD4 T-helper lymphocytes that secrete Th1 cytokines, driving accumulation of macrophages and their organisation into compact non-caseating epithelioid granulomas. In the lung these granulomas cluster along lymphatics and around bronchovascular structures. Granulomas may resolve or, with persistent inflammation, be replaced by fibrosis, causing irreversible architectural distortion and functional impairment.","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculosis and other mycobacterial disease","Lymphoma and metastatic malignancy","Fungal infections such as histoplasmosis","Berylliosis, which is clinically and histologically similar","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Other interstitial lung diseases"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography and CT to assess lymphadenopathy, parenchymal involvement, and fibrosis","Biopsy (often via endobronchial ultrasound-guided nodal sampling) to demonstrate non-caseating granulomas","Pulmonary function testing including diffusing capacity to gauge functional impairment","Serum and urinary calcium to detect dysregulated calcium metabolism","Assessment for extrapulmonary involvement including cardiac, ocular, hepatic, and neurological evaluation"],"redFlags":["Symptoms or findings suggesting cardiac sarcoidosis such as arrhythmia, heart block, or heart failure","Neurosarcoidosis with cranial nerve or central nervous system involvement","Significant hypercalcaemia with renal impairment","Progressive pulmonary fibrosis or pulmonary hypertension"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally favourable, with a substantial proportion of patients undergoing spontaneous remission, especially in acute presentations such as Loefgren syndrome; however, chronic progressive disease with pulmonary fibrosis, cardiac involvement, or neurosarcoidosis carries greater morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing sarcoidosis without excluding tuberculosis and other infective granulomatous disease","Relying on serum ACE levels for diagnosis or disease monitoring","Missing clinically silent but dangerous cardiac or neurological involvement","Treating asymptomatic or mildly affected patients who may remit without therapy"],"management":["Observe asymptomatic or minimally symptomatic disease with serial monitoring, since spontaneous remission is common, particularly in Loefgren syndrome","Treat symptomatic or progressive pulmonary disease with oral corticosteroids as first-line therapy to suppress granulomatous inflammation","Add steroid-sparing immunosuppressants such as methotrexate or azathioprine for chronic disease or to limit corticosteroid toxicity","Reserve anti-TNF biologics such as infliximab for refractory or multi-organ disease","Manage acute Loefgren syndrome symptomatically with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for arthralgia and erythema nodosum","Monitor lung function including diffusing capacity, and treat dysregulated calcium metabolism by avoiding excess vitamin D and sunlight","Screen actively for cardiac, ocular, hepatic, and neurological involvement and refer for specialist assessment when present","Consider pulmonary rehabilitation and referral for lung transplantation in end-stage pulmonary fibrosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"sarcopenia":{"name":"Sarcopenia","system":"Geriatrics / Palliative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["age-related muscle loss","muscle wasting of ageing","low muscle mass and strength","sarcopenic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hazzard's Geriatric Medicine and Gerontology / palliative care guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sarcopenia is the progressive, generalised loss of skeletal muscle mass together with reduced muscle strength or physical performance","Current definitions emphasise low muscle strength as the primary criterion, with low muscle mass confirming the diagnosis and poor performance marking severity","Grip strength and gait speed are simple, validated bedside measures that flag probable sarcopenia","The SARC-F questionnaire is a quick screening tool for case finding in clinical practice","Sarcopenia can be primary (age-related) or secondary to disease, inactivity, or undernutrition, and it overlaps with but is distinct from frailty and cachexia","It is a major contributor to falls, disability, loss of independence, and adverse surgical and hospital outcomes","Progressive resistance exercise is the most effective intervention and is more powerful than nutrition alone","Adequate protein and total energy intake, correction of vitamin D deficiency, and treatment of underlying conditions support muscle maintenance"],"pathophysiology":"Sarcopenia results from an imbalance between muscle protein synthesis and breakdown, driven by ageing changes including loss of motor neurons and motor units, reduced anabolic hormone levels, anabolic resistance to dietary protein, mitochondrial dysfunction, chronic low-grade inflammation, and physical inactivity. Type II (fast-twitch) fibres are preferentially lost, and fatty and fibrous infiltration of muscle further reduces quality and strength. The result is diminished force generation and impaired physical performance out of proportion to the loss of mass alone.","additionalDifferentials":["Cancer cachexia with inflammation-driven muscle wasting","Frailty syndrome (overlapping but broader multisystem construct)","Disuse atrophy from immobility or prolonged bed rest","Neuromuscular disorders such as motor neuron disease or myopathy","Endocrine causes including hypothyroidism, hypogonadism, and hypercortisolism","Protein-energy undernutrition"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Muscle strength testing, particularly handgrip dynamometry","Assessment of muscle mass using DXA or bioimpedance where available","Physical performance measures such as gait speed, the short physical performance battery, or timed up-and-go","SARC-F questionnaire for screening","Laboratory evaluation for reversible secondary causes including vitamin D, thyroid function, and inflammatory markers"],"redFlags":["Rapid muscle loss suggesting underlying malignancy or cachexia rather than age-related decline","Recurrent or injurious falls resulting from profound weakness","Progressive functional decline threatening independence","Neurological features pointing to a primary neuromuscular disorder"],"prognosis":"Sarcopenia predicts falls, fractures, functional decline, hospitalisation, and mortality, and it worsens outcomes across surgery and acute illness. It is at least partly reversible: resistance training combined with adequate protein intake can improve strength and performance, and earlier intervention yields better results.","pitfalls":["Attributing weakness solely to old age and not screening or intervening","Relying on nutritional supplementation without prescribing resistance exercise","Overlooking secondary and reversible causes such as vitamin D deficiency and endocrine disorders","Missing the overlap with frailty and cachexia and thereby underestimating overall risk"],"management":["Prescribe progressive resistance and strength training as the cornerstone intervention to improve muscle strength and physical performance","Ensure adequate dietary protein intake distributed across meals, with oral nutritional supplementation where intake is insufficient","Correct vitamin D deficiency and optimise overall nutrition with dietitian input","Identify and treat reversible secondary causes such as endocrine disorders, inactivity, and undernutrition, and review contributory medications","Implement multidisciplinary falls prevention and functional rehabilitation through physiotherapy and comprehensive geriatric assessment","Monitor response and progression with bedside measures such as handgrip strength and gait speed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Hazzard's Geriatric Medicine and Gerontology"],"crossLinks":[]},"sarcosinemia":{"name":"Sarcosinaemia","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sarcosinaemia","sarcosine dehydrogenase deficiency","SARDH deficiency","hypersarcosinaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by deficiency of sarcosine dehydrogenase (SARDH), a flavoprotein that demethylates sarcosine (N-methylglycine) to glycine in the choline-to-glycine pathway","Regarded as a benign biochemical trait, with sarcosinaemia and sarcosinuria found without a consistent associated clinical disease","The biochemical signature is raised sarcosine in plasma and urine","Secondary sarcosinuria can occur in glutaric aciduria type II (multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency) because the electron-transfer flavoprotein pathway is shared, so this should be excluded","Because sarcosine dehydrogenase depends on the flavin electron-transfer system, its activity may be affected in disorders of that system","No specific treatment is required for isolated sarcosinaemia","Inheritance is autosomal recessive","British spelling applies (sarcosinaemia)"],"pathophysiology":"Sarcosine (N-methylglycine) is generated during choline degradation and from glycine methylation, and is normally converted back to glycine by the mitochondrial flavoprotein sarcosine dehydrogenase, which transfers electrons to the electron-transfer flavoprotein system. In sarcosinaemia, deficiency of this enzyme causes sarcosine to accumulate in blood and urine. Because the metabolite is not appreciably toxic, isolated sarcosinaemia is considered a benign trait. Notably, the same electron-transfer flavoprotein pathway is impaired in glutaric aciduria type II, which produces secondary sarcosinuria and must be distinguished.","additionalDifferentials":["Glutaric aciduria type II (multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency) causing secondary sarcosinuria","Riboflavin deficiency affecting flavoprotein activity","Other benign aminoacidaemias on newborn screening","Dietary sources raising sarcosine transiently","Renal tubular causes of aminoaciduria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative plasma amino acids and urine analysis demonstrating raised sarcosine","Urine organic acids to exclude glutaric aciduria type II","Plasma acylcarnitine profile to screen for multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","Riboflavin status assessment","Molecular analysis of the SARDH gene where confirmation is desired","Correlation with clinical status to exclude an alternative diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Sarcosinuria accompanied by organic aciduria or hypoglycaemia suggesting glutaric aciduria type II","Metabolic decompensation, which is not a feature of benign sarcosinaemia and points elsewhere","Attributing unrelated symptoms to the benign biochemical finding"],"prognosis":"Isolated sarcosinaemia carries an excellent prognosis and is regarded as a benign metabolic variant requiring no treatment.","pitfalls":["Overlooking glutaric aciduria type II as a cause of secondary sarcosinuria","Treating a benign trait as a disease","Missing riboflavin deficiency as a contributory factor","Attributing coincidental symptoms to the finding"],"management":["Reassure that isolated sarcosinaemia is a benign biochemical trait requiring no specific treatment or dietary restriction","Exclude glutaric aciduria type II (multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency) with urine organic acids and a plasma acylcarnitine profile, since it causes secondary sarcosinuria and needs active management","Confirm the diagnosis biochemically with raised plasma and urine sarcosine, and by SARDH molecular analysis where clarification is desired","Correlate persistently with clinical status so any coexisting condition is not wrongly attributed to the sarcosinaemia","Offer genetic counselling regarding the autosomal recessive inheritance and reassure relatives about the benign outlook"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: sarcosinaemia and disorders of glycine/choline metabolism","Nyhan, Atlas of Inherited Metabolic Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"sarcotubular_myopathy":{"name":"Sarcotubular myopathy (TRIM32)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STM","TRIM32-related myopathy","sarcotubular myopathy","LGMD R8","LGMD2H"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive myopathy caused by variants in TRIM32, allelic with limb-girdle muscular dystrophy R8 (formerly LGMD2H), first described in the Hutterite population","Presents with slowly progressive proximal limb-girdle weakness, and mild facial or neck weakness may occur","Onset is usually in early to mid-adulthood, though it can be earlier, and progression is generally slow with prolonged preservation of ambulation","Creatine kinase is often moderately elevated and electromyography shows a myopathic pattern","The muscle biopsy hallmark is dilatation and vacuolation of the sarcotubular system, particularly of type 2 fibres, giving the disease its name","TRIM32 encodes an E3 ubiquitin ligase, and its dysfunction impairs protein turnover in muscle","The phenotype overlaps with other limb-girdle muscular dystrophies, so a broad genetic panel is often required","Diagnosis combines the limb-girdle weakness pattern, the sarcotubular vacuolar biopsy changes, and TRIM32 genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Sarcotubular myopathy is caused by recessive variants in TRIM32, which encodes a tripartite-motif E3 ubiquitin ligase that targets specific muscle proteins for degradation through the ubiquitin-proteasome system. Loss of normal TRIM32 function disrupts protein quality control and turnover in skeletal muscle, leading to abnormal accumulation and to dilatation and vacuolation of the sarcotubular (sarcoplasmic reticulum and T-tubule) membranes seen histologically. This membranous disorganisation, preferentially affecting type 2 fibres, produces progressive proximal muscle weakness.","additionalDifferentials":["Other limb-girdle muscular dystrophies (calpainopathy, dysferlinopathy, sarcoglycanopathies)","Inflammatory myopathy","Metabolic myopathy","Myofibrillar myopathy","Congenital myopathy with vacuolar change","Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography","Muscle biopsy with histochemistry and electron microscopy to demonstrate sarcotubular vacuolation","Muscle MRI to characterise the pattern of involvement","Genetic testing with a TRIM32-inclusive limb-girdle muscular dystrophy panel","Cardiac and respiratory assessment as part of neuromuscular evaluation"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or asymmetric weakness suggesting an alternative or inflammatory cause","Respiratory muscle weakness with declining vital capacity","Significant cardiac symptoms prompting cardiac evaluation","Marked dysphagia atypical for the usual phenotype"],"prognosis":"The disorder generally follows a slowly progressive course with proximal weakness, and many patients retain independent walking for many years; life expectancy is usually normal, though monitoring for respiratory involvement is appropriate in advanced disease.","pitfalls":["Attributing the presentation to a more common limb-girdle dystrophy without genetic confirmation","Missing the characteristic sarcotubular vacuolation if electron microscopy is not performed","Assuming a dominant inheritance pattern in what is a recessive disorder when counselling families","Overlooking milder facial or neck involvement that can aid recognition"],"management":["No disease-specific or curative therapy exists, so centre care on maintaining function with individualised physiotherapy to preserve strength and prevent contractures","Provide occupational therapy, orthoses, and mobility aids to sustain independence as proximal weakness progresses","Monitor respiratory function periodically and introduce non-invasive ventilation if nocturnal hypoventilation develops","Undertake cardiac assessment as part of routine neuromuscular surveillance","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary neuromuscular service","Offer genetic counselling for the autosomal recessive inheritance and arrange testing of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: TRIM32-related limb-girdle muscular dystrophy / sarcotubular myopathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"savi_syndrome":{"name":"SAVI (STING-associated vasculopathy of infancy)","system":"Immunology / Autoinflammatory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STING-associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy","TMEM173","STING1","STING gain-of-function disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Autoinflammation / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A type I interferonopathy caused by gain-of-function mutations in TMEM173 (STING1), leading to constitutive STING activation","Presents in infancy with systemic inflammation, failure to thrive, and a severe cutaneous vasculopathy affecting acral sites","Skin disease produces violaceous, telangiectatic, and ulcerating lesions of the cheeks, nose, ears, and digits, sometimes progressing to acral gangrene and tissue loss","Interstitial lung disease with progressive fibrosis is a major and potentially fatal feature","A strong interferon signature in blood supports the diagnosis alongside genetic confirmation","Recurrent fevers, arthritis, and myositis may accompany the vascular and pulmonary manifestations","JAK inhibition targeting interferon signalling is the mainstay of therapy and can stabilise skin and lung disease"],"pathophysiology":"SAVI is caused by gain-of-function mutations in TMEM173 (STING1) that lock the STING protein in a constitutively active state. Persistent STING signalling drives sustained transcription of type I interferons independent of the usual cytosolic nucleic-acid triggers. The resulting interferon excess produces a small-vessel vasculopathy, particularly at cool acral surfaces, and progressive interstitial lung inflammation and fibrosis. This interferon-dominated mechanism underlies the systemic inflammation and provides the rationale for JAK inhibition to interrupt downstream interferon signalling.","additionalDifferentials":["CANDLE syndrome and other type I interferonopathies","Systemic-onset vasculitis of childhood","Cryoglobulinaemic or cold-induced vasculopathy","Neonatal lupus and systemic lupus erythematosus","Antiphospholipid syndrome with acral ischaemia","Interstitial lung disease of other autoimmune cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for TMEM173 (STING1) gain-of-function mutations","Interferon signature in blood","High-resolution chest CT for interstitial lung disease and fibrosis","Skin biopsy of vasculopathic lesions","Autoantibody panel to help exclude systemic lupus and antiphospholipid syndrome","Pulmonary function testing where age permits"],"redFlags":["Progressive interstitial lung disease with respiratory failure","Acral gangrene threatening loss of digits or facial tissue","Severe failure to thrive with unremitting inflammation","Rapid clinical deterioration despite immunosuppression"],"prognosis":"SAVI is a serious disease with substantial morbidity and mortality driven mainly by progressive lung fibrosis; JAK inhibition can stabilise disease but pulmonary and acral damage may be irreversible.","pitfalls":["Mistaking acral vasculopathy for frostbite, cold injury, or primary vasculitis","Underestimating and under-monitoring the interstitial lung disease","Relying on corticosteroids alone rather than targeting interferon signalling","Delaying genetic testing and interferon signature assessment in an unwell infant"],"management":["Use Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors such as baricitinib or ruxolitinib as the mainstay to interrupt downstream type I interferon signalling","Employ corticosteroids for acute inflammatory flares, recognising that conventional immunosuppression alone is often insufficient","Provide meticulous wound care for acral vasculopathy and ulceration, protect extremities from cold, and avoid tobacco to limit acral ischaemia and tissue loss","Monitor interstitial lung disease with high-resolution chest CT and pulmonary function testing, as progressive fibrosis drives mortality","Screen for infection during immunomodulatory therapy and vaccinate appropriately","Consider lung transplantation for end-stage pulmonary fibrosis","Coordinate care across clinical immunology, rheumatology, and respiratory teams","Offer genetic counselling for the autosomal dominant TMEM173 (STING1) gain-of-function variant, noting many arise de novo"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Autoinflammation","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"say_barber_biesecker_syndrome":{"name":"Say-Barber-Biesecker-Young-Simpson syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SBBYSS","SBBYSS syndrome","Ohdo syndrome, SBBYSS variant","blepharophimosis-ptosis-intellectual disability syndrome","KAT6B-related SBBYSS"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nature Reviews chromatinopathy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by truncating variants in KAT6B, the same histone acetyltransferase implicated in genitopatellar syndrome, with variant position within the gene influencing which of the two allelic phenotypes emerges","Blepharophimosis with ptosis and a mask-like, relatively immobile face is characteristic and helps separate SBBYSS from genitopatellar syndrome","Marked infantile hypotonia, feeding difficulties and global developmental delay are consistent findings","Long thumbs and long great toes are a useful and distinctive skeletal clue","Dental anomalies, lacrimal duct obstruction, hypothyroidism and patellar abnormalities are frequently reported","Congenital heart defects and hearing loss may occur and should be actively sought","The disorder belongs to the KAT6B-related acetyltransferase group of chromatinopathies and is confirmed by molecular testing"],"pathophysiology":"KAT6B encodes a MYST-family lysine acetyltransferase that acetylates histone H3 to create chromatin states permissive for the transcription of developmental genes. In SBBYSS, truncating variants located in regions distinct from those causing genitopatellar syndrome disturb this acetylation-dependent regulation, disrupting developmental programmes governing the eyelids and face, muscle tone, the thyroid and the skeleton. The resulting epigenetic dysregulation produces the characteristic blepharophimosis and mask-like facies, hypotonia, long digits and endocrine and cardiac features, illustrating how the site of a variant in a single chromatin-modifier gene can shape strikingly different clinical pictures.","additionalDifferentials":["Genitopatellar syndrome (allelic KAT6B disorder)","Other forms of Ohdo syndrome (MASA/Maat-Kievit-Brunner, KAT6B-negative)","Blepharophimosis-ptosis-epicanthus inversus syndrome (FOXL2)","Congenital myasthenic and congenital myopathic disorders causing ptosis and hypotonia","Noonan syndrome and other RASopathies","Kabuki syndrome","Other syndromic intellectual disability with ptosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or genome sequencing to identify KAT6B variants with attention to variant location","Thyroid function testing to detect hypothyroidism","Echocardiography to screen for congenital heart disease","Ophthalmological assessment for ptosis, blepharophimosis and lacrimal duct obstruction","Audiological evaluation","Evaluation of feeding and swallowing in infancy","Neurodevelopmental assessment"],"redFlags":["Ptosis obscuring the visual axis and threatening amblyopia","Severe feeding difficulty with faltering growth in infancy","Untreated hypothyroidism that could worsen developmental outcome","Cardiac defects requiring intervention"],"prognosis":"The condition is generally compatible with survival into adulthood; intellectual disability is usual and ranges from mild to severe, and outcome is improved by early treatment of correctable problems such as hypothyroidism, ptosis and feeding difficulty alongside sustained developmental support.","pitfalls":["Conflating SBBYSS with genitopatellar syndrome, which differ in prognosis and management despite sharing a gene","Missing hypothyroidism, which is treatable and affects development","Overlooking ptosis severe enough to cause amblyopia","Attributing the ptosis and hypotonia to a primary neuromuscular disorder without genetic testing"],"management":["Correct hypothyroidism with levothyroxine replacement when detected on screening","Provide feeding support in infancy, including nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding for significant hypotonia-related difficulty","Refer for surgical correction of ptosis and blepharophimosis and for management of lacrimal duct obstruction","Manage congenital heart defects with cardiology input and surgical repair as indicated","Address hearing loss with audiological assessment and hearing aids or other support","Deliver early developmental intervention with physiotherapy, occupational, and speech therapy for hypotonia and developmental delay","Coordinate multidisciplinary care and offer genetic counselling, noting most KAT6B variants arise de novo"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews — KAT6B-Related Disorders (Genitopatellar Syndrome and SBBYSS)","Nature Reviews — histone acetyltransferases in development and disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"scabies":{"name":"Scabies","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sarcoptic itch","seven-year itch","Sarcoptes scabiei infestation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Suspect scabies in any patient with severe, generalised itch that is characteristically worse at night","The pathognomonic lesion is the burrow, a fine serpiginous track most often found in finger webs, wrists, and other warm intertriginous sites","Typical distribution spares the head in adults but involves the face, scalp, palms, and soles in infants","Itch results from a delayed hypersensitivity reaction, so symptoms may take weeks to appear after first infestation but recur within days on re-infestation","It spreads by prolonged skin-to-skin contact, so all close contacts and household members should be treated simultaneously even if asymptomatic","Treatment is with a topical scabicide applied to the whole body per protocol, with laundering of clothing and bedding; oral therapy is useful for outbreaks or when topical treatment is impractical","Crusted (Norwegian) scabies presents with hyperkeratotic crusted plaques, carries an enormous mite burden, is highly contagious, and occurs in immunosuppressed or debilitated patients","Warn patients that itch can persist for weeks after successful eradication (post-scabetic itch) and does not necessarily indicate treatment failure"],"pathophysiology":"Scabies is caused by infestation with the mite Sarcoptes scabiei var. hominis. The fertilised female mite burrows into the stratum corneum, laying eggs and depositing faeces as she tunnels. The clinical eruption and intense pruritus are largely due to a delayed type IV hypersensitivity response to the mite, its eggs, and its excreta, which explains the delay in symptoms after primary infestation. In classic scabies the mite burden is low (typically a small number of adult mites), whereas in crusted scabies impaired immune control permits proliferation of thousands to millions of mites, producing thick hyperkeratotic crusts and extreme infectivity.","additionalDifferentials":["Atopic dermatitis or other eczema","Papular urticaria and insect bite reactions","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Contact dermatitis","Pruritus from systemic causes (renal, hepatic, haematological)","Bullous pemphigoid in older patients with intense itch"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin scraping of a burrow examined microscopically for mites, eggs, or faecal pellets","Dermoscopy to visualise the mite at the end of a burrow (delta-wing sign)","Clinical diagnosis supported by history of contacts with similar itch","Skin biopsy occasionally in crusted or atypical disease","Assessment for immunosuppression when crusted scabies is present"],"redFlags":["Crusted (Norwegian) scabies with widespread hyperkeratosis and high transmission risk","Secondary bacterial infection with impetiginisation, and risk of post-streptococcal complications","Institutional or household outbreaks requiring public health and infection-control measures","Immunosuppression predisposing to severe or atypical infestation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent with correct treatment of the patient and all contacts; failures usually reflect incomplete application, missed contacts, or re-infestation rather than true drug resistance.","pitfalls":["Treating the patient alone and not treating asymptomatic close contacts, leading to reinfestation","Incorrect application of the scabicide, missing sites such as under nails, genitalia, or the feet","Interpreting persistent post-scabetic itch as treatment failure and repeatedly re-treating","Missing crusted scabies in an immunosuppressed or institutionalised patient and allowing an outbreak"],"management":["Treat with a topical scabicide such as permethrin applied to the whole body per protocol as first-line therapy","Use oral ivermectin for outbreaks, institutional settings, or when topical treatment is impractical","Manage crusted scabies with a combined topical scabicide and keratolytic plus repeated doses of oral ivermectin, with isolation given its extreme infectivity","Treat all household members and close skin-to-skin contacts simultaneously, even if asymptomatic","Decontaminate by hot-washing and drying clothing, bedding, and towels, or by sealing non-washable items away","Relieve persistent post-scabetic pruritus with emollients, topical corticosteroids, and antihistamines, explaining that itch can outlast successful treatment","Investigate for underlying immunosuppression, including HIV and HTLV-1, when crusted scabies is present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"scad_deficiency":{"name":"Short-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCAD deficiency","SCADD","ACADS deficiency","short-chain acyl-coenzyme A dehydrogenase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive defect of ACADS affecting dehydrogenation of short-chain (C4) acyl-CoA substrates in mitochondrial beta-oxidation","Clinical significance is uncertain and debated: many identified individuals are asymptomatic, and it is increasingly regarded as a biochemical phenotype rather than a consistently disease-causing disorder","Two common ACADS variants (c.511C>T and c.625G>A) are susceptibility polymorphisms that lower activity, especially when combined with other factors","Newborn screening hallmark is elevated butyrylcarnitine (C4)","Urine organic acids may show ethylmalonic and methylsuccinic aciduria","Reported but non-specific features have included hypotonia, developmental concerns and feeding difficulties, though causation is often unclear","General supportive metabolic principles apply, including avoidance of prolonged fasting during illness, though rigorous dietary therapy is frequently unnecessary"],"pathophysiology":"Short-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase catalyses the initial dehydrogenation of short-chain (predominantly butyryl-CoA) substrates in the final rounds of mitochondrial beta-oxidation. Reduced activity leads to accumulation of butyryl-CoA and its derivatives, detectable as raised C4 acylcarnitine and as ethylmalonic and methylsuccinic acids in urine. Because short-chain oxidation contributes relatively little to overall energy generation and the block is late in the pathway, the biochemical abnormality often occurs without a clear clinical syndrome, and many carriers of the common variants remain well.","additionalDifferentials":["Ethylmalonic encephalopathy (ETHE1-related)","Multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (glutaric aciduria type II)","Isobutyryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency and other C4-related biochemical findings","Medium-chain and other fatty-acid oxidation defects","Non-specific hypotonia or developmental delay of other cause","Benign biochemical variant with no clinical disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma acylcarnitine profile showing elevated C4 (butyrylcarnitine)","Urine organic acids seeking ethylmalonic and methylsuccinic aciduria","ACADS molecular genetic testing to distinguish pathogenic variants from common susceptibility polymorphisms","Plasma free and total carnitine","Fibroblast enzyme or flux studies in selected uncertain cases","Assessment to exclude multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency where C4 elevation is marked"],"redFlags":["Marked or combined acylcarnitine abnormalities suggesting multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency rather than isolated SCAD","Progressive encephalopathy raising concern for ethylmalonic encephalopathy","Metabolic decompensation during severe intercurrent illness","Significant neurological regression prompting search for an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The great majority of screen-detected individuals remain healthy with a normal outlook, and current evidence does not support classifying isolated SCAD deficiency as a reliably harmful disorder; reported symptoms often reflect coincidental or alternative conditions.","pitfalls":["Over-diagnosing a disease and imposing unnecessary dietary restriction on an asymptomatic child","Attributing unrelated developmental problems to the biochemical finding","Failing to consider multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency when C4 is only part of a broader abnormal profile","Confusing common susceptibility variants with clearly pathogenic mutations"],"management":["Reassure and monitor most screen-detected individuals, as isolated SCAD deficiency is usually benign and requires no specific treatment","Distinguish it from multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency, which needs active management, when butyrylcarnitine elevation is marked","Avoid prolonged fasting and ensure regular feeding during intercurrent illness in the rare symptomatic infant","Consider a riboflavin trial and carnitine assessment in selected symptomatic cases","Follow development and reattribute any symptoms to coincidental or alternative diagnoses where appropriate","Offer genetic counselling regarding autosomal recessive inheritance and clarify pathogenic variants from common susceptibility polymorphisms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"scaphoid_fracture":{"name":"Scaphoid fracture","system":"Musculoskeletal / Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["carpal scaphoid fracture","scaphoid waist fracture","carpal navicular fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most commonly fractured carpal bone, typically following a fall on the outstretched hand with the wrist dorsiflexed and radially deviated","Hallmark signs are tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox, tenderness over the scaphoid tubercle, and pain on axial compression of the thumb","The scaphoid receives its blood supply retrogradely through vessels entering distally, so waist and proximal pole fractures place the proximal fragment at risk of avascular necrosis and non-union","Initial radiographs are frequently normal (occult fracture); a clinically suspected scaphoid injury should be immobilised and re-imaged, or evaluated earlier with MRI or CT","Dedicated scaphoid views, including a posteroanterior projection in ulnar deviation, improve fracture detection compared with standard wrist films","Undisplaced waist fractures are usually managed with cast immobilisation, whereas displaced, unstable or proximal pole fractures often require percutaneous or open fixation","Untreated non-union may progress to a predictable pattern of carpal collapse and arthritis (scaphoid non-union advanced collapse)"],"pathophysiology":"The scaphoid spans the proximal and distal carpal rows and derives most of its blood supply from vessels that enter the distal pole and dorsal ridge, perfusing the bone in a retrograde direction. A fracture, most often across the waist, can interrupt flow to the proximal fragment, which becomes prone to delayed union, non-union and avascular necrosis. Because the bone is largely covered by articular cartilage with little periosteum, healing depends heavily on this tenuous vascular supply and on adequate immobilisation or fixation to prevent motion at the fracture. Persistent non-union alters carpal kinematics and leads over time to progressive degenerative collapse of the wrist.","additionalDifferentials":["Distal radius fracture","De Quervain tenosynovitis","Scapholunate ligament injury","Simple wrist sprain","Fracture of the trapezium or other carpal bones","First carpometacarpal osteoarthritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dedicated scaphoid radiographic series including a posteroanterior view in ulnar deviation, oblique and lateral projections","Repeat radiographs after an interval of immobilisation when initial films are normal but clinical suspicion persists","MRI for early detection of an occult fracture and marrow oedema","CT to characterise fracture displacement, humpback deformity and to assess union","Radionuclide bone scan as an alternative when MRI is unavailable"],"redFlags":["Proximal pole fracture carrying a high risk of avascular necrosis and non-union","Fracture displacement or humpback (flexion) deformity indicating instability","Delayed presentation with established non-union or sclerosis","A snuffbox-tender wrist discharged on the basis of normal initial radiographs"],"prognosis":"Undisplaced waist fractures usually unite with adequate immobilisation, but proximal pole and displaced fractures carry substantially higher rates of non-union and avascular necrosis; unaddressed non-union can culminate in carpal collapse and post-traumatic arthritis.","pitfalls":["Discharging a clinically suspicious, snuffbox-tender wrist because early radiographs appear normal","Underestimating the vascular vulnerability of proximal pole fractures","Inadequate duration or quality of immobilisation leading to non-union","Overlooking associated carpal ligament injury or instability"],"management":["Immobilise undisplaced waist fractures in a cast until radiographic union, given the tenuous retrograde blood supply","Refer displaced, unstable, or proximal pole fractures for percutaneous or open screw fixation","Immobilise clinically suspected but radiographically occult fractures and re-image, or arrange early MRI, rather than discharging","Provide analgesia and, after immobilisation, hand therapy to restore wrist and grip function","Confirm union with CT and monitor for non-union and avascular necrosis, particularly in proximal fractures","Refer to a hand or orthopaedic surgeon for displaced injuries and for suspected non-union or avascular necrosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","Green's Operative Hand Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"scaphoid_nonunion":{"name":"scaphoid nonunion","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scaphoid fracture nonunion","ununited scaphoid fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Scaphoid nonunion typically follows a missed, inadequately immobilised or displaced waist fracture, and the retrograde blood supply predisposes the proximal pole to avascular necrosis.","The blood supply enters distally via dorsal ridge branches of the radial artery, so more proximal fractures heal least reliably.","Untreated nonunion produces a humpback (flexion) deformity with a DISI posture and a predictable arthritic cascade termed SNAC.","SNAC progresses from radial styloid and distal scaphoid arthritis to periscaphoid and midcarpal degeneration, characteristically sparing the radiolunate joint.","CT reconstructed along the long axis of the scaphoid best defines union, displacement and humpback deformity.","MRI assesses proximal pole vascularity and helps decide between vascularised and non-vascularised bone grafting.","Vascularised bone grafting is favoured for an avascular proximal pole, whereas structural wedge grafting is used to correct humpback deformity."],"pathophysiology":"The scaphoid is largely covered by articular cartilage and receives most of its blood supply retrogradely from branches of the radial artery entering the distal dorsal ridge. A fracture, particularly of the waist or proximal pole, can devascularise the proximal fragment. Persistent motion combined with poor vascularity prevents bridging bone and produces a fibrous or sclerotic nonunion. Volar bone resorption causes the scaphoid to collapse into flexion, the humpback deformity, with compensatory lunate extension producing DISI. Altered load transmission then drives progressive periscaphoid and midcarpal arthritis, the SNAC pattern.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute scaphoid fracture","Congenital bipartite scaphoid","Kienböck disease","Scapholunate dissociation or SLAC wrist","Preiser disease (idiopathic scaphoid osteonecrosis)","Radial styloid or trapezial osteoarthritis","De Quervain tenosynovitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PA, lateral, oblique and dedicated scaphoid-view radiographs","CT reconstructed along the scaphoid long axis for union and deformity assessment","MRI to evaluate proximal pole vascularity","Contralateral comparison radiographs","Intraoperative assessment of punctate bleeding to gauge bone vascularity","Metabolic or inflammatory screen in atypical presentations"],"redFlags":["Proximal pole sclerosis or fragmentation indicating avascular necrosis","Established humpback deformity with DISI","Radiographic SNAC changes signalling irreversible arthritis","Persistent wrist pain and functional decline in a young manual worker"],"prognosis":"With appropriate grafting and fixation before arthritis develops, union rates are good, but established SNAC arthritis is irreversible and ultimately requires salvage surgery.","pitfalls":["Missing the original fracture on initial radiographs and mislabelling it a sprain.","Assuming union on plain films without CT confirmation.","Neglecting proximal pole vascularity assessment before selecting a graft.","Failing to correct humpback deformity and leaving residual malalignment.","Overlooking established SNAC arthritis when planning reconstruction."],"management":["Treat nonunion before arthritis develops with internal fixation and bone grafting to achieve union","Use a structural interpositional graft to correct the humpback flexion deformity and restore scaphoid length and alignment","Select a vascularised bone graft when the proximal pole is avascular or a non-vascularised graft has failed","Manage established SNAC arthritis with salvage procedures such as proximal row carpectomy, scaphoid excision with four-corner fusion, or total wrist fusion","Assess proximal pole vascularity with MRI and intraoperative punctate bleeding to guide graft choice","Provide postoperative immobilisation followed by hand therapy, and refer to a hand surgeon for definitive management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","Wheeless' Textbook of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"scapholunate_dissociation":{"name":"scapholunate dissociation","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scapholunate ligament injury","rotatory subluxation of the scaphoid","SL dissociation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common and most significant carpal instability pattern, resulting from disruption of the scapholunate interosseous ligament, particularly its stronger dorsal component.","The Watson scaphoid shift test reproduces dorsal pain and a clunk as the scaphoid subluxates over the dorsal rim of the radius on radial deviation.","Classic static radiographic signs are a widened scapholunate gap (Terry Thomas sign) on the PA view and a cortical ring sign from the abnormally flexed scaphoid seen end-on.","A clenched-fist PA view stresses the interval and may unmask dynamic instability not evident on standard films.","On the lateral radiograph an increased scapholunate angle indicates a dorsal intercalated segment instability (DISI) posture, with the lunate extended.","Untreated complete injuries progress predictably to scapholunate advanced collapse (SLAC) arthritis over years.","Management differs sharply between an acute repairable ligament, a chronic reducible deformity without arthritis, and a chronic irreducible or arthritic wrist.","Arthroscopy remains the reference standard for staging partial versus complete tears when imaging is equivocal."],"pathophysiology":"The scapholunate interosseous ligament, together with secondary stabilisers such as the dorsal intercarpal ligament, scaphotrapeziotrapezoid ligament and radioscaphocapitate ligament, maintains the tight kinematic linkage between scaphoid and lunate. When the dorsal scapholunate ligament and its secondary restraints fail, typically after a fall on the extended, ulnar-deviated and supinated wrist, the scaphoid loses restraint and rotates into flexion and pronation while the lunate, still tethered to the triquetrum, extends into a DISI posture. The resulting rotatory subluxation of the scaphoid concentrates load on the radioscaphoid articulation and initiates a predictable degenerative cascade.","additionalDifferentials":["Distal radius fracture with associated ligament injury","Scaphoid fracture","Lunotriquetral ligament injury","Kienböck disease","Radiocarpal or midcarpal osteoarthritis","Dorsal wrist ganglion or occult ganglion","De Quervain tenosynovitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Standard PA, lateral and clenched-fist stress radiographs with contralateral comparison views","Measurement of the scapholunate gap and scapholunate angle on plain films","MRI focused on the scapholunate interosseous ligament","MR arthrography or CT arthrography to assess ligament integrity","Wrist arthroscopy for definitive staging using the Geissler classification","Fluoroscopic or cineradiographic dynamic motion studies","Grip-loaded views to provoke dynamic gapping"],"redFlags":["Progressive dorsal wrist pain and loss of grip after a fall, suggesting evolving instability","Radiographic DISI or scapholunate gapping indicating complete ligament disruption","Early radioscaphoid narrowing or osteophytes signalling onset of SLAC collapse","High-energy mechanism with associated perilunate instability or dislocation"],"prognosis":"Acute, well-reduced injuries repaired before arthritis develops have the best functional outcome, whereas untreated complete tears progress over years to SLAC arthritis with pain and stiffness.","pitfalls":["Dismissing the injury as a simple sprain and missing the window for primary ligament repair.","Relying only on static radiographs and overlooking dynamic instability.","Failing to notice scapholunate widening on a distal radius fracture film.","Attributing dorsal wrist pain solely to a ganglion.","Not comparing with the contralateral wrist, since scapholunate distances vary between individuals."],"management":["Repair the scapholunate interosseous ligament primarily, often with dorsal capsulodesis and temporary K-wire fixation, in acute reducible injuries","Reconstruct chronic reducible instability with tendon graft or capsulodesis techniques when direct repair is no longer feasible","Reserve salvage procedures such as proximal row carpectomy, scaphoid excision with four-corner fusion, or wrist fusion for established SLAC arthritis","Consider wrist denervation for pain relief when motion-preserving reconstruction is not possible","Follow surgery with a period of immobilisation and structured hand therapy to restore controlled motion","Refer promptly to a hand surgeon, as outcome is best when complete injuries are addressed before arthritis develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","Wheeless' Textbook of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"scapular_dyskinesis":{"name":"scapular dyskinesis","system":"musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scapular dyskinesia","SICK scapula syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Matsen's The Shoulder","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Scapular dyskinesis is an alteration in the normal position and movement of the scapula, most often observed as prominence of the medial or inferomedial border during arm elevation and lowering.","It is usually a response to or a contributor to other shoulder pathology rather than a discrete disease, so search for an underlying cause.","The scapula must protract, upwardly rotate and posteriorly tilt to maintain a stable base for the glenohumeral joint and to preserve the subacromial space during elevation.","Common contributors include periscapular muscle weakness or fatigue (notably serratus anterior and lower trapezius), pain inhibition, posterior capsular tightness and altered thoracic posture.","A neurological cause such as long thoracic nerve palsy (serratus anterior) or spinal accessory nerve palsy (trapezius) must be excluded when winging is prominent.","The scapular assistance and scapular retraction tests help demonstrate that correcting scapular mechanics relieves symptoms and predicts response to rehabilitation.","The SICK scapula (scapular malposition, inferomedial border prominence, coracoid pain and dyskinesis) describes an overuse pattern in overhead athletes.","Management is overwhelmingly rehabilitative, restoring scapular control and flexibility and addressing the primary shoulder disorder, with surgery reserved for specific structural or neurological causes."],"pathophysiology":"Normal shoulder function depends on coordinated scapulothoracic motion that positions the glenoid optimally beneath the humeral head throughout elevation, maintaining the length-tension relationship of the rotator cuff and preserving subacromial clearance. Scapular dyskinesis arises when this coordination is disrupted by muscular, capsular, postural or neurological factors. Weakness or fatigue of the serratus anterior and lower trapezius, imbalance with an overactive upper trapezius, pain-related inhibition, a tight posteroinferior capsule and pectoralis minor, or denervation of the periscapular muscles all alter scapular upward rotation, posterior tilt and external rotation. The malpositioned scapula reduces the subacromial space, alters glenohumeral contact and increases loads on the rotator cuff and static stabilisers, predisposing to or perpetuating impingement, instability and labral pathology. Thus dyskinesis is frequently both a consequence and a driver of other shoulder conditions.","additionalDifferentials":["Long thoracic nerve palsy causing serratus anterior winging","Spinal accessory nerve palsy causing trapezius winging","Subacromial impingement syndrome","Rotator cuff pathology","Glenohumeral instability","Fascioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy","Cervical radiculopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured clinical observation of scapular motion from behind during repeated elevation","Scapular assistance and scapular retraction corrective manoeuvres","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies if neurological winging is suspected","Assessment of posterior capsular and pectoralis minor tightness","MRI or ultrasound of the shoulder to identify associated cuff, labral or instability pathology","Cervical spine and neurological assessment when a proximal cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Prominent static winging suggesting a long thoracic or spinal accessory nerve palsy","Progressive, symmetrical periscapular wasting suggesting an underlying myopathy or dystrophy","A palpable mass beneath the scapula such as an osteochondroma or elastofibroma","Associated neurological deficit or neck pain suggesting cervical or brachial plexus pathology"],"prognosis":"When it accompanies another shoulder disorder, correcting scapular control through targeted rehabilitation improves symptoms and complements treatment of the primary problem, with a generally good outcome. Prognosis depends on the underlying cause, being more guarded when there is an established nerve palsy or a progressive neuromuscular disorder.","pitfalls":["Treating scapular dyskinesis as a standalone diagnosis without identifying the underlying shoulder or neurological cause.","Missing a long thoracic or spinal accessory nerve palsy presenting as winging.","Overlooking an underlying muscular dystrophy in bilateral or progressive cases.","Focusing rehabilitation on the glenohumeral joint alone while neglecting periscapular and postural control."],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying cause, since dyskinesis is usually secondary to another shoulder disorder rather than a discrete disease","Prescribe scapular-focused rehabilitation strengthening the serratus anterior and lower trapezius and retraining scapular motor control","Stretch a tight posterior capsule and pectoralis minor and correct thoracic posture and kinetic-chain deficits","Treat associated glenohumeral pathology such as rotator cuff, labral, or instability lesions","Identify neurological winging from long thoracic or spinal accessory nerve palsy and manage accordingly, referring for nerve or tendon-transfer surgery when recovery fails","Use scapular assistance and retraction manoeuvres to guide rehabilitation and predict response"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Matsen's The Shoulder","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics","Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Trauma"],"crossLinks":[]},"scapuloperoneal_spinal_muscular_atrophy":{"name":"Scapuloperoneal spinal muscular atrophy","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scapuloperoneal SMA","SPSMA","TRPV4-related scapuloperoneal SMA","Davidenkow syndrome","TRPV4"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley and Daroff Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Motor neuronopathy in which weakness and wasting predominate in a scapuloperoneal distribution, affecting shoulder-girdle and peroneal (lower leg) muscles","An autosomal dominant form is caused by variants in TRPV4, a calcium-permeable cation channel, and can overlap with congenital and distal neuropathy phenotypes","Presents with scapular winging, difficulty raising the arms, and foot drop, with a variable age of onset from childhood to adulthood","Sensory involvement is usually minimal, consistent with a predominantly motor neurone or motor axonal process","TRPV4-related disease is notable for its phenotypic breadth, ranging from scapuloperoneal SMA to congenital distal SMA and axonal CMT type 2C, sometimes with vocal cord paresis and skeletal abnormalities","Electrophysiology shows a motor neuronopathy or motor axonal pattern with denervation and preserved sensory responses in the classic forms","Diagnosis requires the characteristic distribution together with genetic confirmation, most often of a TRPV4 variant"],"pathophysiology":"Scapuloperoneal spinal muscular atrophy is a lower motor neurone disorder in which degeneration of anterior horn cells produces weakness in a distinctive scapuloperoneal pattern. The dominant TRPV4-associated form results from variants in a calcium-permeable transient receptor potential channel; the mutations are thought to alter channel gating and calcium handling in motor neurones, leading to selective vulnerability of the neurones supplying shoulder-girdle and peroneal muscles. Because the pathology is largely confined to motor neurones and their axons, sensory function is typically preserved, and the electrophysiology reflects denervation with normal sensory responses. TRPV4 variants can also affect the peripheral nerve and skeleton, explaining the associated phenotypes.","additionalDifferentials":["Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy","Scapuloperoneal muscular dystrophy (myopathic forms)","Distal hereditary motor neuropathies","Axonal CMT type 2 (including TRPV4-related CMT2C)","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis in adult presentations","Emery-Dreifuss and other limb-girdle myopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electromyography and nerve conduction studies showing motor neuronopathy with preserved sensory responses","TRPV4 sequencing and a hereditary neuromuscular gene panel","Creatine kinase and muscle imaging to help separate neurogenic from myopathic causes","Facioscapulohumeral dystrophy genetic testing where the pattern overlaps","Assessment for vocal cord paresis and skeletal abnormalities in suspected TRPV4 disease","Detailed family history for dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Vocal cord paresis causing stridor or airway compromise","Respiratory muscle involvement with breathlessness","Rapidly progressive or asymmetric weakness suggesting motor neurone disease","Significant bulbar involvement requiring review"],"prognosis":"Usually slowly progressive with long preservation of function, though severity is variable; congenital TRPV4-related forms and those with vocal cord or respiratory involvement can be more severe.","pitfalls":["Confusing the scapuloperoneal weakness pattern with facioscapulohumeral dystrophy","Overlooking the wide TRPV4 phenotypic spectrum, including CMT2C and skeletal features","Assuming a myopathic cause without electrophysiology to identify a neurogenic process","Missing vocal cord involvement that carries airway risk"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so manage supportively with physiotherapy to maintain strength and prevent contractures","Provide ankle-foot orthoses for foot drop and occupational therapy and mobility aids to preserve function","Monitor respiratory function and screen for vocal cord paresis, arranging airway intervention if laryngeal involvement causes obstruction","Undertake periodic assessment for the skeletal abnormalities associated with TRPV4-related disease","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary neuromuscular service","Offer genetic counselling for the autosomal dominant inheritance and testing of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: TRPV4-Related Neuromuscular Disorders","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"scarlet_fever":{"name":"Scarlet fever","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scarlatina","streptococcal scarlet fever","second disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from infection with group A streptococcus strains that produce erythrogenic (pyrogenic) exotoxins, usually complicating streptococcal pharyngitis and occasionally skin infection","Presents with sore throat, fever, and a diffuse fine erythematous rash with a sandpaper texture that blanches on pressure","The rash characteristically accentuates in skin folds (Pastia's lines) and spares the perioral area, giving circumoral pallor","The tongue shows a white coating that peels to reveal a red, swollen surface with prominent papillae described as a strawberry tongue","Desquamation of the skin, particularly of the fingertips and toes, follows as the rash fades over the ensuing days to weeks","Diagnosis rests on the clinical picture supported by throat swab culture or rapid antigen testing for group A streptococcus","Timely antibiotic treatment shortens illness, reduces spread, and lowers the risk of suppurative and non-suppurative complications, with penicillin-class agents as the mainstay"],"pathophysiology":"Group A Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes) strains lysogenised by bacteriophages produce streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins. In a host lacking antibody to the relevant toxin, these act as superantigens and toxins that cause the characteristic diffuse erythematous eruption and systemic features. The primary focus is usually the pharynx. Beyond the local infection, immune-mediated sequelae can follow, including acute rheumatic fever and post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, and unchecked infection can extend to suppurative complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Measles","Rubella","Kawasaki disease","Toxic shock syndrome (staphylococcal or streptococcal)","Drug reaction / hypersensitivity eruption","Viral exanthems such as erythema infantum or enteroviral rash"],"infectionMimics":["Staphylococcal scalded skin / toxic shock syndrome","Measles","Kawasaki disease","Enteroviral exanthem"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Throat swab for culture and/or rapid antigen detection of group A streptococcus","Anti-streptolysin O and other streptococcal antibody titres to confirm recent infection when needed","Urinalysis to screen for post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis in the convalescent phase","Inflammatory markers and basic bloods if a complicated or invasive infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Features of acute rheumatic fever such as migratory arthritis, carditis, or new murmur developing after the illness","Signs of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis including haematuria, oedema, and hypertension","Suppurative complications such as peritonsillar or retropharyngeal abscess, otitis media, or cervical lymphadenitis","Rapid deterioration with hypotension and multi-organ involvement suggesting streptococcal toxic shock syndrome or invasive disease"],"prognosis":"With prompt antibiotic therapy the outlook is excellent and most children recover fully; the main concern with untreated or inadequately treated disease is the risk of rheumatic fever, glomerulonephritis, and suppurative spread.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis when the rash is faint or the pharyngitis mild, delaying treatment that prevents complications","Failing to complete the full antibiotic course, which is important for preventing rheumatic sequelae","Confusing scarlet fever with Kawasaki disease and overlooking coronary risk in a young child","Not monitoring for delayed post-streptococcal renal or cardiac complications after apparent recovery"],"management":["Treat with a penicillin such as phenoxymethylpenicillin as first-line antibiotic therapy, using a macrolide for penicillin allergy","Complete the full antibiotic course to eradicate group A streptococcus and prevent rheumatic fever and suppurative complications","Provide supportive care with antipyretics, analgesia, and adequate fluid intake","Exclude the child from school or nursery until an appropriate interval after starting antibiotics to limit spread","Monitor during convalescence for post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis and features of acute rheumatic fever","Notify public health authorities and consider treating symptomatic close contacts, particularly during outbreaks"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"scedosporiosis":{"name":"Scedosporiosis / lomentosporiosis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Scedosporium infection","Scedosporium apiospermum infection","Lomentospora prolificans infection","lomentosporiosis","pseudallescheriasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases / ECMM-ISHAM guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by environmental moulds of the Scedosporium apiospermum complex and the highly resistant Lomentospora prolificans, found in soil, polluted water, and decaying matter","A classic scenario is central nervous system infection with brain abscesses following a near-drowning event, when contaminated water is aspirated and the fungus disseminates to the brain","In immunocompromised hosts, particularly those with haematological malignancy or transplantation, it causes invasive sino-pulmonary and disseminated disease that closely mimics aspergillosis","It can also colonise the airways in patients with cystic fibrosis or pre-existing lung cavities and cause localised disease after traumatic inoculation (mycetoma) in immunocompetent hosts","A crucial management point is intrinsic antifungal resistance: Scedosporium species are typically resistant to amphotericin B, and Lomentospora prolificans is resistant to most available agents","Voriconazole is the preferred backbone for Scedosporium infection given the intrinsic amphotericin resistance, while multidrug-resistant Lomentospora often requires combination therapy and surgical debridement with a guarded outlook","Diagnosis relies on tissue biopsy with histology and culture plus molecular identification, since histology cannot reliably distinguish it from Aspergillus and susceptibility varies by species"],"pathophysiology":"Scedosporium and Lomentospora species are inhaled as conidia, aspirated in contaminated water, or directly inoculated through trauma. In immunocompetent hosts localised disease such as mycetoma or airway colonisation predominates, whereas aspiration of polluted water during near-drowning can seed the brain and produce abscesses even in previously healthy people. In immunosuppressed hosts the moulds are angioinvasive, causing vascular thrombosis, tissue infarction, and haematogenous dissemination that is histologically indistinguishable from aspergillosis. Their intrinsic and often multidrug resistance, especially in Lomentospora prolificans, is central to the difficulty of treatment.","additionalDifferentials":["Invasive aspergillosis","Fusariosis","Mucormycosis","Bacterial or nocardial brain abscess","Tuberculous or other granulomatous central nervous system disease","Actinomycetoma or eumycetoma from other agents"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Tissue biopsy for histopathology and fungal culture with molecular species identification","Antifungal susceptibility testing given species-dependent resistance","Blood cultures, which may be positive especially with Lomentospora prolificans","Computed tomography of the chest and sinuses for invasive sino-pulmonary disease","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain when central nervous system involvement or near-drowning history is present","Serum beta-D-glucan and galactomannan as adjunctive fungal biomarkers"],"redFlags":["New focal neurological signs or brain abscesses after a near-drowning event","Disseminated disease with positive blood cultures in an immunocompromised host","Rapid progression of sino-pulmonary disease despite empirical antifungal therapy","Isolation of Lomentospora prolificans given its pan-resistance","Persistent immunosuppression with no prospect of immune recovery"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are poor for disseminated and central nervous system disease, particularly with the highly resistant Lomentospora prolificans; prognosis is better for localised infection in immunocompetent hosts amenable to surgery, and depends heavily on immune status and the feasibility of source control.","pitfalls":["Treating as aspergillosis with amphotericin B, to which Scedosporium is intrinsically resistant","Not obtaining culture and species identification, since histology cannot distinguish it from Aspergillus and management differs","Overlooking a near-drowning history in a patient with brain abscesses","Failing to pursue surgical debridement and source control alongside antifungals","Underestimating the near pan-resistance of Lomentospora prolificans"],"management":["Use voriconazole as the antifungal backbone for Scedosporium infection, since these moulds are intrinsically resistant to amphotericin B","Treat multidrug-resistant Lomentospora prolificans with combination antifungal therapy, such as a triazole combined with terbinafine, guided by susceptibility testing","Combine medical therapy with surgical debridement, resection, or abscess drainage to achieve source control, especially in cerebral and localised disease","Reduce immunosuppression where possible and support recovery from neutropenia, including with granulocyte colony-stimulating factor","Direct therapy with antifungal susceptibility testing and molecular species identification given species-dependent resistance","Monitor response with serial imaging and fungal biomarkers, and manage through a multidisciplinary team given the guarded outlook"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","ECMM-ISHAM global guideline for the diagnosis and management of rare mould infections"],"crossLinks":[]},"schaaf_yang_syndrome":{"name":"Schaaf-Yang syndrome (MAGEL2)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAGEL2-related syndrome","Prader-Willi-like syndrome due to MAGEL2","SHFYNG syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by truncating variants in MAGEL2, a paternally expressed imprinted gene within the Prader-Willi critical region on chromosome 15q11.2","Shares features with Prader-Willi syndrome including neonatal hypotonia and early feeding difficulty, but is distinguished by prominent joint contractures","Distal arthrogryposis, particularly of the fingers, is a characteristic finding that helps separate it from classical Prader-Willi syndrome","Autism spectrum disorder and intellectual disability are frequent and often more consistent than in Prader-Willi syndrome","Only paternally inherited or de novo paternal-allele variants cause disease because the maternal copy is normally silenced by imprinting","Sleep-disordered breathing, including central and obstructive apnoea, is common and warrants active screening","Some individuals develop the hyperphagia and obesity seen in Prader-Willi syndrome, but this is variable","Diagnosis is made by sequencing MAGEL2, with attention to the parent of origin of the variant"],"pathophysiology":"MAGEL2 lies within the imprinted Prader-Willi region of chromosome 15q11.2 and is expressed only from the paternally inherited allele, the maternal copy being silenced by methylation. Truncating variants on the active paternal allele abolish or reduce functional protein, which normally participates in hypothalamic development, circadian and autonomic regulation, and protein trafficking within neurones. Loss of this function disturbs hypothalamic-driven control of feeding, sleep, and endocrine signalling and contributes to abnormal neurodevelopment, while the accompanying joint contractures reflect reduced foetal movement during development. Because expression is imprinted, a variant on the maternally inherited allele is generally silent, so parent of origin critically determines whether disease results.","additionalDifferentials":["Prader-Willi syndrome from deletion, maternal uniparental disomy, or imprinting defect","Distal arthrogryposis syndromes of other genetic cause","Congenital myotonic dystrophy presenting with neonatal hypotonia","Other syndromic causes of neonatal hypotonia with feeding failure","Autism spectrum disorder with intellectual disability of alternative genetic origin","Chromosomal disorders identified on microarray"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sequencing of MAGEL2 with determination of the parental origin of any variant","Methylation analysis of the 15q11.2 region to distinguish from classical Prader-Willi syndrome","Chromosomal microarray to exclude a contiguous deletion","Sleep study to detect central or obstructive apnoea","Developmental and autism-focused assessment","Endocrine evaluation for hypothalamic-pituitary dysfunction where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Significant neonatal hypotonia with feeding failure requiring assisted nutrition","Central or obstructive sleep apnoea causing hypoxaemia","Severe contractures compromising limb function or airway","Emerging hyperphagia with rapid weight gain and its metabolic consequences"],"prognosis":"The course is variable but generally involves lifelong intellectual disability and autism-related needs; early mortality can occur in severely affected infants with respiratory or feeding compromise, whereas milder cases stabilise with supportive care, and long-term issues centre on neurodevelopment, sleep-disordered breathing, contracture management, and, in some, obesity-related complications.","pitfalls":["Labelling the child as classical Prader-Willi syndrome and missing the contractures that point to MAGEL2","Assuming a normal methylation test excludes the diagnosis, since MAGEL2 sequencing is required","Overlooking the parent-of-origin rule and misjudging recurrence risk in counselling","Underestimating the burden of sleep-disordered breathing by not arranging a sleep study"],"management":["Provide feeding support in infancy, including nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding for hypotonia-related feeding difficulty","Screen for and treat sleep-disordered breathing, including central and obstructive apnoea, with measures such as positive airway pressure support","Manage distal arthrogryposis and contractures with physiotherapy, splinting, and orthopaedic input","Deliver early developmental intervention and autism-focused support with speech, occupational, and behavioural therapies","Evaluate and treat hypothalamic-pituitary endocrine dysfunction where identified","Coordinate multidisciplinary care and offer genetic counselling, explaining that only paternally inherited or de novo paternal-allele variants cause disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Schaaf-Yang syndrome (MAGEL2-related disorder)","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"schatzki_ring":{"name":"Schatzki ring","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Schatzki's ring","lower oesophageal mucosal ring","B ring","distal oesophageal ring"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A thin mucosal ring at the squamocolumnar junction of the distal oesophagus, at the top of a hiatus hernia when present","Classically causes intermittent dysphagia to solids, often with abrupt food impaction after bolting food or drinking alcohol ('steakhouse syndrome')","Symptoms typically appear when the luminal diameter narrows below a critical threshold","Best seen on a barium swallow with the oesophagus fully distended, as it can be overlooked at endoscopy","Frequently coexists with a hiatus hernia and with gastro-oesophageal reflux, which may contribute to its formation","Symptomatic rings are treated by endoscopic dilatation, and coexisting reflux is addressed to reduce recurrence","Distinguished from a proximal web by its location at the gastro-oesophageal junction and its circumferential shape"],"pathophysiology":"A Schatzki ring is a circumferential mucosal ring at the gastro-oesophageal junction, with squamous epithelium above and columnar epithelium below. Its exact cause is debated, but chronic gastro-oesophageal reflux and mechanical factors are implicated in its development and in progressive narrowing. The ring reduces the maximal luminal diameter, so large solid boluses intermittently lodge above it, producing episodic dysphagia and, when a bolus fully obstructs the lumen, acute food impaction.","additionalDifferentials":["Oesophageal web","Peptic stricture","Eosinophilic oesophagitis with fixed rings","Oesophageal adenocarcinoma at the junction","Achalasia and other motility disorders","Muscular (A) ring of the lower oesophagus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Barium swallow with full oesophageal distension and a solid bolus challenge","Upper endoscopy for confirmation, biopsy, and dilatation","Oesophageal biopsies to exclude eosinophilic oesophagitis","Ambulatory reflux monitoring where reflux contribution is uncertain","Assessment for hiatus hernia"],"redFlags":["Progressive dysphagia or weight loss suggesting stricture or malignancy","Acute food impaction requiring urgent endoscopy","Odynophagia or bleeding","Recurrent rings that may indicate untreated reflux or eosinophilic oesophagitis"],"prognosis":"Dilatation of a symptomatic ring is usually effective and safe, though rings can recur; addressing coexisting reflux may reduce the recurrence rate.","pitfalls":["Missing the ring at endoscopy because the oesophagus is inadequately distended","Overlooking eosinophilic oesophagitis as a cause of recurrent rings and dysphagia","Failing to treat coexisting reflux, allowing the ring to recur","Attributing solid-food dysphagia to a ring without excluding malignancy"],"management":["Treat a symptomatic ring with endoscopic dilatation to widen the luminal diameter and relieve dysphagia","Relieve acute food bolus impaction by endoscopic disimpaction","Prescribe a proton pump inhibitor to control coexisting gastro-oesophageal reflux and reduce the risk of recurrence","Exclude eosinophilic oesophagitis with oesophageal biopsies before attributing dysphagia solely to the ring","Advise careful eating, thorough chewing, and avoiding large boluses and alcohol-associated bolting of food","Repeat dilatation for symptomatic recurrence, as rings may re-form over time"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Yamada's Textbook of Gastroenterology"],"crossLinks":[]},"scheie_syndrome":{"name":"Scheie syndrome (MPS IS)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPS IS","MPS V (historical)","attenuated alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency","IDUA deficiency (mild form)","mucopolysaccharidosis type I - Scheie"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Scriver's OMMBID","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Represents the mildest (attenuated) end of the mucopolysaccharidosis type I spectrum, caused by partial deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme alpha-L-iduronidase","Intelligence is characteristically preserved, distinguishing it clinically from severe Hurler syndrome at the opposite end of the same enzymatic disorder","Presentation is often delayed into later childhood or adulthood with corneal clouding, joint stiffness, and carpal tunnel syndrome","Aortic valve disease, particularly regurgitation, is a recognised cardiac manifestation","Stature is usually normal or only mildly reduced, contrasting with the marked growth failure of Hurler syndrome","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating reduced alpha-L-iduronidase activity together with raised urinary dermatan and heparan sulfate","Molecular confirmation of IDUA gene variants clarifies the phenotype and supports genetic counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Scheie syndrome arises from biallelic pathogenic variants in the IDUA gene, which encodes the lysosomal hydrolase alpha-L-iduronidase. Residual enzyme activity is greater than in Hurler syndrome, producing a milder phenotype within the same disease continuum. Impaired breakdown of the glycosaminoglycans dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate leads to their progressive accumulation within lysosomes of connective tissue, cornea, cartilage, heart valves, and other organs. The stored substrate is also excreted in excess in the urine. Because intellectual function is generally spared, the clinical burden is dominated by musculoskeletal, ocular, and cardiac involvement that worsens slowly over years. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Hurler and Hurler-Scheie syndromes (other points on the MPS I spectrum)","Hunter syndrome (MPS II)","Maroteaux-Lamy syndrome (MPS VI)","Rheumatoid or juvenile idiopathic arthritis given the joint stiffness","Mucolipidoses with overlapping skeletal and connective tissue features","Other causes of corneal clouding such as cystinosis or Fabry disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Leucocyte or fibroblast alpha-L-iduronidase enzyme assay","Quantitative urinary glycosaminoglycan analysis with electrophoresis for dermatan and heparan sulfate","IDUA gene sequencing for molecular confirmation and genotype-phenotype correlation","Slit-lamp examination to document corneal clouding","Echocardiography to assess aortic and mitral valve function","Nerve conduction studies where carpal tunnel syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive aortic valve regurgitation or other cardiac valve dysfunction","Visual deterioration from advancing corneal clouding","Median nerve compression causing hand weakness and wasting","Cervical spine or airway compromise from connective tissue deposition"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy can approach normal in many patients, though progressive cardiac valve disease, corneal clouding, and joint limitation cause substantial long-term morbidity. Enzyme replacement therapy may help systemic manifestations but does not correct established corneal or valvular changes.","pitfalls":["Attributing joint stiffness to inflammatory arthritis and missing the underlying storage disorder","Assuming preserved intellect excludes a mucopolysaccharidosis","Overlooking silent valvular heart disease that requires surveillance","Delaying enzyme replacement therapy because early features are subtle"],"management":["Offer enzyme replacement therapy with laronidase (recombinant alpha-L-iduronidase) as the mainstay to reduce glycosaminoglycan storage and systemic burden","Refer for surgical carpal tunnel decompression and provide physiotherapy for joint stiffness and contractures","Manage progressive aortic valve disease with cardiology surveillance and valve surgery when indicated","Arrange corneal transplantation for visually significant corneal clouding, which enzyme therapy does not reverse","Monitor with serial echocardiography, slit-lamp examination, nerve conduction studies, and screening for sleep-disordered breathing","Coordinate multidisciplinary care and offer genetic counselling for the autosomal recessive inheritance and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"scheuermann_disease":{"name":"Scheuermann disease","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Scheuermann's kyphosis","juvenile kyphosis","juvenile osteochondrosis of the spine","vertebral osteochondrosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A developmental osteochondrosis of the vertebral endplates producing a rigid structural kyphosis, distinct from postural round-back","Affected structures are the growth zones of the thoracic (or thoracolumbar) vertebral bodies","Typically presents in adolescence around the growth spurt and is more common than isolated postural kyphosis in this age group","Presents with a cosmetic thoracic hump that does not correct on active or passive extension, sometimes with aching back pain","The radiographic hallmark is anterior wedging of several adjacent vertebrae together with Schmorl nodes and irregular, narrowed endplates","The classic radiographic definition requires anterior wedging of three or more consecutive vertebrae, differentiating it from flexible postural kyphosis","Tight hamstrings and a compensatory lumbar lordosis are common associated findings","Management ranges from physiotherapy and bracing during growth to corrective surgery for severe, progressive or painful curves as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Scheuermann disease is an osteochondrosis affecting the ring apophyses and cartilaginous endplates of the growing vertebral bodies. Disordered endochondral ossification and mechanical stress at the vertebral growth zones impair the normal anterior growth of the vertebral bodies, so that the anterior height falls behind the posterior height and the vertebrae become wedged. Herniation of disc material through weakened endplates produces Schmorl nodes, and endplate irregularity with disc space narrowing follows. The cumulative anterior wedging of several adjacent vertebrae results in an increased, rigid thoracic (or thoracolumbar) kyphosis that, unlike postural round-back, does not correct with extension. A genetic predisposition and repetitive loading during rapid growth are thought to contribute.","additionalDifferentials":["Postural (flexible) kyphosis","Congenital kyphosis from vertebral malformation","Vertebral osteomyelitis or discitis","Compression fracture, including osteoporotic or traumatic","Ankylosing spondylitis or other inflammatory spondyloarthropathy","Vertebral involvement by tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Standing lateral spinal radiographs to measure the kyphotic angle and identify vertebral wedging","Assessment for anterior wedging of consecutive vertebrae, Schmorl nodes and endplate irregularity","Hyperextension lateral views to assess curve flexibility and rigidity","Magnetic resonance imaging where cord compromise, infection or tumour is a concern","Clinical assessment of hamstring tightness and any neurological signs","Serial imaging during growth to monitor curve progression"],"redFlags":["Neurological signs or symptoms suggesting spinal cord compression from a severe curve or disc herniation","Constitutional symptoms, fever or night pain raising concern for infection or malignancy","Rapidly progressive kyphosis during the growth spurt","Severe pain out of proportion to the deformity prompting a search for another cause"],"prognosis":"Most adolescents have a stable curve and do well with observation, physiotherapy or bracing during growth; severe curves may progress and cause pain or cosmetic concern into adulthood, and neurological compromise is rare but important to recognise.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling a rigid structural kyphosis as flexible postural round-back and under-treating it","Failing to appreciate the diagnostic requirement for wedging of several consecutive vertebrae","Overlooking neurological compromise in a severe or painful thoracic curve","Attributing all back pain to Scheuermann disease without excluding infection, fracture or tumour"],"management":["Observe mild, non-progressive curves with serial radiographic monitoring during growth","Prescribe physiotherapy with extension-based exercises, hamstring stretching, and postural training","Apply bracing in the skeletally immature adolescent with a moderate or progressive curve to limit worsening during growth","Control back pain with simple analgesia and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs alongside activity modification","Refer for spinal fusion and instrumentation in severe, progressive, or painful curves, or where neurological compromise is present","Undertake serial imaging through the growth spurt to monitor curve progression and guide escalation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"schimke_immuno_osseous_dysplasia":{"name":"Schimke Immuno-Osseous Dysplasia","system":"Multisystem (immune, renal, skeletal)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SIOD","SMARCAL1-related dysplasia","Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia with nephrotic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive multisystem disorder caused by biallelic SMARCAL1 mutations; the classic triad is spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia, T-cell immunodeficiency and steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome.","Disproportionate short stature with a short trunk; skeletal survey shows spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia with ovoid or flattened vertebral bodies and capital femoral epiphyseal (hip) dysplasia.","Progressive steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome due to focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, typically advancing to end-stage kidney disease.","T-cell predominant immunodeficiency with lymphopenia predisposes to recurrent bacterial, viral, fungal and opportunistic infection.","Cerebrovascular disease — transient ischaemic attacks, migraine-like headache, a moyamoya-like arteriopathy and strokes — is a major source of morbidity.","Supportive features include hyperpigmented macules, fine sparse hair, characteristic facies (broad depressed nasal bridge with a bulbous nasal tip) and generalised, accelerated atherosclerosis.","Two overlapping severities exist — a severe infantile or antenatal-onset form and a milder later-onset (juvenile) form — broadly tracking residual SMARCAL1 function.","Bone marrow dysfunction and hypothyroidism may complicate the course, and live vaccines are hazardous given the underlying T-cell defect."],"pathophysiology":"Schimke immuno-osseous dysplasia is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in SMARCAL1 on chromosome 2q35, which encodes an ATP-dependent annealing helicase (also termed HARP) of the SNF2 chromatin-remodelling family. SMARCAL1 is recruited to stalled DNA replication forks via replication protein A, where it stabilises and restarts forks and participates in replication-associated DNA repair and maintenance of genome integrity. Impaired annealing-helicase and chromatin-remodelling activity is thought to compromise fork restart and tissue-specific gene expression, producing a pleiotropic phenotype affecting chondrocytes (spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia), the developing immune system (defective T-lymphocyte production and lymphopenia), the glomerular podocyte (focal segmental glomerulosclerosis) and the vasculature (premature arteriopathy with cerebral ischaemia). Phenotype severity correlates broadly with the degree of residual enzyme function, and a minority of clinically typical patients lack a detectable SMARCAL1 mutation, implying additional loci or modifying factors.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe infantile SIOD versus milder juvenile-onset SIOD (allelic severity spectrum within the same disorder)","Cartilage-hair hypoplasia (RMRP) — the other classic immuno-osseous dysplasia","Other spondyloepiphyseal dysplasias (e.g. COL2A1-related spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita)","Genetic steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome / focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (e.g. NPHS1, NPHS2, WT1-related)","Combined immunodeficiencies presenting with growth failure and recurrent infection","Progeroid or metabolic syndromes with disproportionate short stature and vascular disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey / radiographs demonstrating spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia (flattened ovoid vertebrae, capital femoral epiphyseal changes)","SMARCAL1 molecular genetic testing (sequencing with deletion/duplication analysis)","Urinalysis with quantitative proteinuria and renal biopsy (focal segmental glomerulosclerosis)","Lymphocyte subset enumeration and immunoglobulin levels (T-cell lymphopenia)","Full blood count and blood film to detect cytopenias and marrow dysfunction","Brain MRI with MR angiography for cerebral ischaemia and moyamoya-like vasculopathy","Thyroid function tests and fasting lipid profile"],"redFlags":["Acute focal neurological deficit or seizure suggesting cerebral ischaemia or stroke","Opportunistic or overwhelming infection in a lymphopenic child","Rapidly progressive renal failure or an uncontrolled nephrotic state","Signs of bone marrow failure with severe cytopenias"],"prognosis":"The outlook is guarded, particularly in the severe infantile form where death in childhood from renal failure, infection, stroke or marrow failure is common. The juvenile form may survive into adulthood but with cumulative renal, immune and cerebrovascular morbidity.","pitfalls":["Attributing steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome to common acquired causes and missing the underlying genetic diagnosis.","Overlooking the T-cell immunodeficiency and administering live vaccines.","Ascribing short stature to an isolated skeletal dysplasia without evaluating the renal and immune systems.","Underestimating cerebrovascular risk, so that stroke as a cause of neurological decline is not anticipated.","Assuming a negative SMARCAL1 result excludes the diagnosis when the phenotype is classic."],"management":["No disease-specific therapy exists, so care is organ-directed; use RAAS blockade (ACE inhibitors or ARBs) to reduce proteinuria in the steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome, recognising that immunosuppression is generally ineffective","Progress to kidney replacement therapy with dialysis and kidney transplantation for end-stage kidney disease from the focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","Manage the T-cell immunodeficiency with antimicrobial prophylaxis, immunoglobulin replacement, avoidance of live vaccines and prompt treatment of opportunistic infection, considering haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation in severe combined immune or marrow failure","Address the moyamoya-like cerebral arteriopathy with antiplatelet therapy and neurosurgical revascularisation where indicated, and treat migraine and transient ischaemic attacks","Reduce accelerated atherosclerotic risk with lipid-lowering therapy and blood-pressure control, and provide orthopaedic surveillance for the spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia and hip disease","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary team spanning nephrology, immunology, neurology, orthopaedics and clinical genetics","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder with SMARCAL1 testing and screening of at-risk siblings"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics reference texts","Paediatric nephrology reference texts","Inborn errors of immunity / primary immunodeficiency guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"schimmelpenning_syndrome":{"name":"Schimmelpenning syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Neurocutaneous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Schimmelpenning-Feuerstein-Mims syndrome","linear naevus sebaceus syndrome","naevus sebaceus syndrome","organoid naevus syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Neurocutaneous Disorders (current reference texts)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by an extensive naevus sebaceus of Jadassohn, typically on the head and neck along the lines of Blaschko, in combination with cerebral, ocular and skeletal anomalies","Arises from a post-zygotic activating mosaic variant in the RAS pathway (usually HRAS, less often KRAS), so the distribution is segmental rather than generalised","Neurological features may include seizures, developmental delay, intracranial vascular or structural malformations and hemimegalencephaly on the side of the skin lesion","Ocular associations include epibulbar (limbal) dermoids, colobomas and lipodermoids, so ophthalmology review is part of the workup","Skeletal changes such as bony hypoplasia, vitamin-D-resistant hypophosphataemic rickets and limb asymmetry can accompany extensive disease","The naevus sebaceus itself carries a lifelong risk of secondary benign and malignant tumours (most often trichoblastoma, occasionally basal cell carcinoma)","Management is multidisciplinary and generic: seizure control with antiseizure therapy, surveillance of the naevus with consideration of excision, and developmental support"],"pathophysiology":"Schimmelpenning syndrome is a mosaic RASopathy caused by a post-zygotic activating variant, most commonly in HRAS and less often KRAS, occurring early in embryogenesis. Because the variant arises after fertilisation, only the clonal population of cells descended from the mutated progenitor is affected, producing lesions distributed along the lines of Blaschko. In the skin this drives an organoid naevus sebaceus with sebaceous and epidermal overgrowth, while involvement of neuroectodermal, ocular and mesodermal progenitors accounts for the associated brain, eye and skeletal anomalies. Persistent RAS-MAPK signalling within the naevus also underlies the tendency to develop secondary appendageal tumours over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated naevus sebaceus of Jadassohn without systemic involvement","Keratinocytic epidermal naevus syndrome","Phacomatosis pigmentokeratotica","Encephalocraniocutaneous lipomatosis","Proteus syndrome","Incontinentia pigmenti (verrucous stage)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy to confirm naevus sebaceus and to assess any secondary tumour","MRI of the brain to detect hemimegalencephaly, structural or vascular malformations","MRA where an intracranial vascular anomaly is suspected","Ophthalmological assessment for epibulbar dermoid, coloboma and lipodermoid","Serum phosphate and skeletal survey where hypophosphataemic rickets or bony asymmetry is suspected","Molecular testing of lesional tissue for mosaic HRAS/KRAS variants where available"],"redFlags":["New nodule, ulceration or rapid change within a naevus sebaceus suggesting secondary malignancy","New-onset or poorly controlled seizures","Progressive neurological deficit or signs of raised intracranial pressure","Visual compromise from an ocular choristoma or coloboma"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the extent of extracutaneous involvement; isolated cutaneous disease has an excellent outlook, whereas extensive neurological involvement with refractory seizures and developmental delay carries a poorer prognosis, and lifelong dermatological surveillance is needed for secondary tumours.","pitfalls":["Assuming an extensive naevus sebaceus is purely cosmetic and not screening for neurological, ocular or skeletal disease","Overlooking hypophosphataemic rickets as a treatable cause of skeletal symptoms","Attributing every secondary nodule to malignancy when benign trichoblastoma is more common","Delaying neuroimaging in a child presenting with seizures and a segmental head-and-neck naevus"],"management":["Surgically excise or ablate the naevus sebaceus where cosmetically or functionally troublesome, and maintain lifelong dermatological surveillance for secondary appendageal tumours such as trichoblastoma and basal cell carcinoma","Control associated epilepsy with antiseizure medication and provide developmental and educational support for neurocognitive impairment","Refer for ophthalmological assessment and management of epibulbar dermoids, colobomas and lipodermoids","Treat hypophosphataemic (vitamin-D-resistant) rickets with oral phosphate and active vitamin D such as alfacalcidol, monitoring limb alignment and growth","Manage limb asymmetry and skeletal deformity with orthopaedic input","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across dermatology, neurology, ophthalmology, orthopaedics and clinical genetics","Counsel that the disorder arises from a post-zygotic mosaic RAS variant and is typically sporadic with low recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Happle R. Mosaicism in Human Skin"],"crossLinks":[]},"schindler_disease":{"name":"Schindler disease","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["alpha-N-acetylgalactosaminidase deficiency","alpha-NAGA deficiency","NAGA deficiency","Kanzaki disease (type II)","neuroaxonal dystrophy, Schindler type"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Scriver's OMMBID","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme alpha-N-acetylgalactosaminidase, leading to accumulation of glycopeptides and glycolipids with terminal N-acetylgalactosamine","Type I is a severe infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy with developmental regression, seizures, and cortical visual impairment","Type II (Kanzaki disease) is a milder adult-onset form with angiokeratoma corporis diffusum and mild cognitive changes","Intermediate phenotypes between the two extremes are also recognised","Angiokeratomas in the adult form can mimic those seen in Fabry disease, an important distinguishing point","Urinary oligosaccharide and glycopeptide screening supports the diagnosis","Confirmation rests on deficient alpha-N-acetylgalactosaminidase activity and NAGA gene testing"],"pathophysiology":"Schindler disease is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in NAGA, encoding lysosomal alpha-N-acetylgalactosaminidase. This enzyme removes terminal alpha-N-acetylgalactosamine residues from glycoproteins, glycopeptides, and glycolipids during lysosomal degradation. Its deficiency leads to accumulation of these incompletely degraded compounds and their excretion in the urine. The mechanism linking substrate storage to the severe infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy of type I remains incompletely understood, but the phenotype features widespread axonal swellings (spheroids). Type II (Kanzaki disease) presents in adulthood with cutaneous angiokeratomas and only mild neurological involvement, and intermediate presentations occur. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Fabry disease, given overlapping angiokeratoma corporis diffusum in the adult form","Infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy from other causes (e.g. PLA2G6-related)","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses","Other oligosaccharidoses such as fucosidosis and alpha-mannosidosis","GM1 and GM2 gangliosidoses","Other causes of early developmental regression with seizures"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Leucocyte or fibroblast alpha-N-acetylgalactosaminidase enzyme assay","Urinary oligosaccharide and glycopeptide analysis","NAGA gene sequencing","Brain MRI for infantile forms","Skin biopsy of angiokeratomas in the adult form","Neurophysiology and developmental assessment where regression is present"],"redFlags":["Progressive developmental regression with seizures in infancy","Cortical visual impairment","Loss of previously acquired motor and cognitive skills","New angiokeratomas prompting distinction from Fabry disease"],"prognosis":"The infantile type I form is severe with profound neurological deterioration and a poor outlook, whereas the adult type II form follows a much milder course compatible with long survival. No disease-modifying therapy is established.","pitfalls":["Confusing the adult angiokeratoma phenotype with Fabry disease without appropriate enzyme testing","Overlooking the diagnosis in infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy without metabolic screening","Relying only on glycosaminoglycan screening and missing the oligosaccharide pattern","Assuming a single uniform phenotype given the wide clinical spectrum"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy such as enzyme replacement or substrate reduction is established, so management is supportive and tailored to phenotype","In the infantile type I form, control seizures with antiseizure medication, provide feeding support, and treat spasticity and the consequences of neurodevelopmental regression","Support cortical visual impairment with physiotherapy and occupational therapy, and introduce palliative care planning in the severe infantile form","In adult type II (Kanzaki) disease, reassure and manage cutaneous angiokeratomas conservatively, using laser therapy for cosmetically troublesome lesions","Distinguish from Fabry disease, whose angiokeratomas overlap, to avoid inappropriate Fabry-directed enzyme therapy","Coordinate multidisciplinary care through metabolic medicine, neurology, dermatology and developmental services","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive NAGA disorder and screen at-risk family members"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"schinzel_giedion_syndrome":{"name":"Schinzel-Giedion syndrome","system":"Clinical genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Schinzel-Giedion midface retraction syndrome","SGS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Recognise the pattern of severe midface retraction with a prominent high forehead, deep-set eyes and a short upturned nose, combined with profound developmental impairment.","Congenital hydronephrosis, often bilateral, is a hallmark and may be the presenting antenatal finding.","Classic disease is caused by de novo gain-of-function variants clustering in a SETBP1 hotspot (degron) region; inheritance is autosomal dominant.","Intractable epilepsy with progressive neurological deterioration is typical, often accompanied by cortical visual impairment.","Skeletal clues include a sclerotic skull base, wide sutures with multiple wormian bones and broad ribs.","There is a markedly increased risk of embryonal neuroepithelial tumours, so tumour surveillance is advised.","Additional features include hypertrichosis, genital and cardiac anomalies, choanal or airway problems, and feeding difficulty with recurrent apnoea.","Distinguish classic (severe, gain-of-function) disease from the milder SETBP1 haploinsufficiency disorder caused by loss-of-function variants."],"pathophysiology":"Classic Schinzel-Giedion syndrome results from de novo missense variants clustering in a small degron 'hotspot' of SETBP1, which encodes the SET-binding protein. This degron is normally recognised by a beta-TrCP-containing ubiquitin ligase complex that targets SETBP1 for proteasomal degradation; hotspot variants disrupt recognition, so the protein escapes turnover and accumulates. Excess SETBP1 stabilises the SET oncoprotein, which inhibits the tumour-suppressor phosphatase PP2A and dysregulates the transcriptional programmes governing neuronal and skeletal development, producing the severe multisystem malformation phenotype and a predisposition to embryonal neuroepithelial tumours. This gain-of-function mechanism distinguishes classic Schinzel-Giedion syndrome from the milder SETBP1 haploinsufficiency disorder caused by loss-of-function variants, and from the somatic SETBP1 mutations that drive myeloid malignancies. Inheritance is autosomal dominant and effectively always de novo.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteopetrosis and other sclerosing bone dysplasias (sclerotic skull base)","Antley-Bixler syndrome and other craniofacial dysostoses","Zellweger spectrum and other peroxisomal disorders","DOORS syndrome","Isolated congenital hydronephrosis or posterior urethral valves (non-syndromic)","Other neonatal or early-infantile epileptic encephalopathies","SETBP1 haploinsufficiency disorder (milder, loss-of-function)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing for SETBP1 hotspot variants","Renal tract ultrasound for hydronephrosis and obstructive uropathy","Electroencephalography to characterise the epileptic encephalopathy","Brain MRI for structural malformations and myelination","Skeletal survey for a sclerotic skull base, wormian bones and broad ribs","Echocardiography for congenital heart defects","Ophthalmological assessment and tumour-surveillance imaging"],"redFlags":["Refractory seizures or status epilepticus","Recurrent apnoea or airway and respiratory compromise","Clinical or imaging signs of an emerging neuroepithelial tumour","Progressive hydronephrosis with obstructive uropathy","Rapid neurological deterioration or failure to thrive"],"prognosis":"Classic Schinzel-Giedion syndrome carries a poor prognosis, with profound neurodevelopmental impairment, intractable epilepsy and frequently death in early childhood; the embryonal tumour predisposition further worsens the outlook.","pitfalls":["Working up antenatal or neonatal hydronephrosis in isolation and missing the underlying syndromic diagnosis.","Attributing the seizures and encephalopathy solely to perinatal hypoxic-ischaemic injury.","Omitting tumour surveillance despite the recognised embryonal tumour risk.","Confusing classic gain-of-function disease with the milder SETBP1 haploinsufficiency disorder, leading to inaccurate prognostic and recurrence counselling.","Assuming negligible recurrence risk without noting the possibility of parental germline mosaicism."],"management":["No disease-specific therapy exists; care is largely supportive and often palliative given the severe neurodevelopmental impairment","Manage the intractable epilepsy with antiseizure medication under specialist paediatric neurology input","Relieve obstructive uropathy from hydronephrosis with urological intervention and monitor renal function and urinary tract infection","Provide feeding support with gastrostomy where needed, respiratory care and prompt treatment of recurrent infection","Undertake surveillance for embryonal neuroepithelial tumours with periodic imaging","Address congenital cardiac defects and other malformations through the relevant surgical specialties","Coordinate multidisciplinary and palliative care and offer genetic counselling, noting classic disease usually arises de novo (autosomal dominant) with low sibling recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","GeneReviews (clinical genetics summaries)","Paediatric neurology and clinical genetics reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"schistosomiasis":{"name":"Schistosomiasis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bilharzia","trematode infection"],"chapter":241,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1825","clinicalPearls":["Infection is contracted through contact with freshwater bodies harboring infected intermediate-host snails; cercariae penetrate intact human skin within a few minutes (p.1826).","A travel/migration history of freshwater exposure (bathing, washing, fishing, rice-field work, swimming) in an endemic area is central to the diagnosis, especially in travelers (p.1827, p.1829).","Most disease manifestations are due to immunologic granulomatous reactions to eggs retained in host tissues, not to the adult worms; about half of eggs are trapped in tissue (p.1827).","Cercarial dermatitis ('swimmer's itch') is a maculopapular rash from cercarial skin penetration; a severe form follows exposure to avian schistosomes (e.g., lakes in Europe/US) that die in the skin (p.1828).","Acute schistosomiasis (Katayama fever) occurs 2 weeks to 3 months after exposure, usually in first-time-infected travelers, with fever, myalgia, malaise, headache, nonproductive cough, and abdominal symptoms (p.1828).","Eosinophilia is a common finding and is often associated with schistosomiasis; transient pulmonary infiltrates may accompany Katayama fever (p.1828, p.1829).","Intestinal schistosomiasis (S. mansoni, S. japonicum): worms in mesenteric veins; eggs trapped in intestinal tissue cause granulomatous inflammation, microulceration, intermittent abdominal pain, and sometimes bloody diarrhea (p.1829).","Hepatosplenic schistosomiasis (S. mansoni, S. japonicum): late disease produces periportal (Symmers clay-pipestem) fibrosis with portal vein occlusion, portal hypertension, splenomegaly, esophageal varices, and hematemesis; hepatic function usually remains normal (p.1827, p.1829).","Urogenital schistosomiasis (S. haematobium): hallmark of the active stage is painless terminal hematuria, with dysuria and suprapubic discomfort; eggs in bladder mucosa cause inflammation, fibrosis, 'sandy patches', and possible ureteric obstruction with hydronephrosis (p.1829).","S. haematobium is an IARC group 1 carcinogen; chronic infection is associated with squamous cell carcinoma of the urinary bladder (p.1829).","Detection of schistosome eggs in stool or urine indicates active infection and is the standard diagnostic method; S. haematobium eggs (terminal spine) are excreted in urine, other species (S. mansoni lateral spine, S. japonicum small lateral knob) in feces (p.1826, p.1829).","Neuroschistosomiasis is one of the most severe forms: S. mansoni/S. haematobium can cause transverse myelitis (an acute complication in returning travelers), while S. japonicum causes cerebral granulomas with seizures and focal neurologic deficits (p.1829)."],"pathophysiology":"Free-swimming cercariae released from infected freshwater snails penetrate intact skin within minutes, transform into schistosomula, enter veins/lymphatics, traverse the lungs and heart, and mature in the portal vein into paired adult male and female worms that migrate to the mesenteric (S. mansoni, S. japonicum) or pelvic/vesical (S. haematobium) venous plexus. The prepatent period is 5-7 weeks (up to 12 for S. haematobium). About half of the eggs produced are excreted in feces or urine; the remainder become trapped in tissue. Most morbidity results from egg-induced, TH2-driven granulomatous immune responses (lymphocytes, eosinophils, alternatively activated macrophages; IL-4/5/13). Egg antigen excess early in infection forms soluble immune complexes causing a serum sickness-like illness (Katayama fever). Hepatic egg deposition leads to periportal (Symmers) fibrosis, portal-branch occlusion, and portal hypertension; S. haematobium egg deposition in the bladder/ureters causes inflammation, fibrosis, obstructive uropathy, and predisposes to squamous bladder carcinoma (p.1826-1829).","additionalDifferentials":["Malaria (acute febrile illness in returning traveler/migrant) (p.1829)","Salmonellosis (p.1829)","Acute viral hepatitis (p.1829)","Trichinosis (fever with eosinophilia) (p.1829)","Strongyloidiasis (fever with eosinophilia) (p.1829)","Visceral larva migrans / toxocariasis (fever with eosinophilia) (p.1829)","Other causes of portal hypertension and hepatosplenomegaly, including cirrhosis (p.1829)"],"infectionMimics":["Opisthorchis and Clonorchis (liver fluke) infections causing fever and eosinophilia (p.1829)","Invasive ankylostomiasis (hookworm) (p.1829)","Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia (p.1829)","Strongyloidiasis (p.1829)","Malaria (p.1829)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cirrhosis from other causes presenting with portal hypertension, splenomegaly, and variceal bleeding (p.1829)","Bladder squamous cell carcinoma from non-schistosomal causes (chronic urogenital disease) (p.1829)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Microscopic detection of eggs in stool (e.g., ~50 mg) or urine (filtration of ~10 mL) with quantitation (eggs per gram or per 10 mL); concentration methods (e.g., formol-ether) for low-burden travelers (p.1829)","Full blood count showing eosinophilia (p.1829)","Rectal biopsy for eggs (S. mansoni and S. haematobium); occasionally Pap smears and semen samples (S. haematobium) (p.1829)","PCR-based detection of Schistosoma DNA in stool or urine (more sensitive than parasitology); CSF Schistosoma DNA for neuroschistosomiasis (p.1829)","Serology for anti-schistosome antibodies (CDC: FAST-ELISA with S. mansoni microsomal antigen plus confirmatory species-specific immunoblot) - useful in travelers, less so in endemic populations (p.1829-1830)","Circulating anodic/cathodic antigen (CAA/CCA) detection in serum or urine by ELISA or lateral flow as a marker of active infection; point-of-care urine CCA assay used in community screening (p.1830)","Ultrasonography to demonstrate periportal fibrosis, portal vein dilation, and obstructive uropathy/hydronephrosis (p.1829)","Cystoscopy showing 'sandy patches' of calcified eggs in chronic urogenital disease; endoscopy or ultrasound for esophageal varices (p.1829)"],"redFlags":["Hematemesis or melena from esophageal varices in hepatosplenic disease - the most severe complication; death may result from massive blood loss (p.1829)","Acute transverse myelitis or other cerebral/spinal cord manifestations - neuroschistosomiasis, even from light infections (p.1829)","Severe Katayama fever with weight loss, dyspnea, diarrhea, and hepatomegaly; rarely fatal (p.1829)","Painless terminal hematuria in a traveler/migrant from an endemic area (urogenital S. haematobium) (p.1829)","Obstructive uropathy progressing to uremia from ureteral fibrosis (p.1829)"],"prognosis":"Many patients recover spontaneously from acute schistosomiasis after 2-10 weeks, though some follow a more severe course, and the acute syndrome can rarely be fatal. In hepatosplenic disease, hepatic function usually remains normal even with marked periportal fibrosis and portal hypertension; however, severe periportal (Symmers) fibrosis is often irreversible, and hematemesis from variceal bleeding may be fatal. Early inflammatory hepatosplenomegaly in children generally decreases over time as immunity develops. Ureteral fibrosis can cause irreversible obstructive uropathy progressing to uremia. The adult worm lifespan averages 3-5 years but can reach 30 years (p.1826-1829).","pitfalls":["Patients with periportal fibrosis (late hepatosplenic disease) may excrete very few or no eggs in feces, so a negative stool exam does not exclude advanced disease (p.1829).","In the chronic urogenital stage, urinary egg excretion may be sparse or absent despite ongoing urogenital tract pathology (p.1829).","Egg excretion may be very low in people from nonendemic areas (travelers), requiring larger samples and concentration methods (p.1829).","Katayama fever is rare in people chronically exposed in S. mansoni- or S. haematobium-endemic areas, so its absence does not exclude infection in residents (p.1829).","CAA/CCA antigen detection is not currently suitable for diagnosing travelers, who have low worm burdens (p.1830).","Serology is less useful in people from endemic areas with ongoing transmission (p.1829-1830)."],"management":["Treat established infection with the antihelminthic praziquantel, which is active against the adult worms of all Schistosoma species","For acute schistosomiasis (Katayama fever) add corticosteroids to control the hypersensitivity reaction and repeat praziquantel once worms mature, since early treatment is less effective against immature schistosomula","In neuroschistosomiasis or spinal cord involvement give corticosteroids alongside praziquantel and arrange urgent neurological and neurosurgical assessment","Manage chronic complications by treating portal hypertension and oesophageal varices in hepatosplenic disease and relieving obstructive uropathy in urinary S. haematobium infection","Provide symptomatic antipruritic treatment for cercarial dermatitis and maintain surveillance for bladder squamous carcinoma in chronic S. haematobium infection","Advise avoidance of freshwater contact in endemic areas and support public-health measures including snail control and mass drug administration","Retest with parasitological or antigen studies after treatment and re-treat if eggs persist, screening exposed travellers and household contacts"],"severityClassification":"Disease is staged clinically by duration and intensity of infection into three phases - acute (cercarial dermatitis and Katayama fever), active/established, and chronic (e.g., late hepatosplenic periportal fibrosis with portal hypertension, or chronic urogenital fibrosis with sandy patches and obstructive uropathy). Severity of early hepatosplenomegaly is closely associated with infection intensity (p.1828-1829).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1825"],"crossLinks":[]},"schizencephaly":{"name":"Schizencephaly","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cleft brain","open-lip schizencephaly","closed-lip schizencephaly","EMX2-associated schizencephaly"],"chapter":null,"source":"Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A malformation characterised by a full-thickness cleft extending from the pial surface to the lateral ventricle, lined throughout by grey matter, usually polymicrogyric cortex","It is classified as closed-lip when the cleft walls are apposed or open-lip when they are separated by cerebrospinal fluid, and it may be unilateral or bilateral","The grey-matter lining distinguishes it from an acquired porencephalic cyst, which is lined by white matter or gliotic tissue","It is frequently associated with absence of the septum pellucidum and with optic nerve hypoplasia, overlapping with septo-optic dysplasia","Most cases are thought to result from a destructive vascular or disruptive insult to the developing cortex, though some carry genetic contributions such as EMX2 variants","Clinical features vary with size and laterality, from hemiparesis and focal epilepsy in unilateral closed-lip clefts to severe global delay, spasticity, and refractory seizures in bilateral open-lip disease","Diagnosis is by MRI demonstrating the grey-matter-lined cleft, with evaluation for associated midline and optic anomalies"],"pathophysiology":"Schizencephaly is a disorder of cortical formation in which a cleft lined by dysplastic grey matter spans the full thickness of the hemisphere from the ventricle to the cortical surface. It is thought to arise most often from a disruptive prenatal insult, such as a vascular event, that damages the germinal zone and developing cortex, sometimes combined with genetic susceptibility affecting cortical patterning. Because the injury occurs while the cortex is still developing, the cleft becomes lined by abnormal, typically polymicrogyric grey matter, and associated failure of midline structures such as the septum pellucidum and optic apparatus is common.","additionalDifferentials":["Porencephaly, which is lined by white matter or glial tissue rather than grey matter","Open-lip schizencephaly versus severe hydrocephalus or hydranencephaly","Septo-optic dysplasia","Polymicrogyria without a full-thickness cleft","Focal cortical dysplasia","Arachnoid cyst communicating with the ventricle"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to confirm the grey-matter-lined transmantle cleft and its laterality and type","Assessment of the septum pellucidum and optic nerves for associated septo-optic dysplasia","Ophthalmological examination for optic nerve hypoplasia","Endocrine evaluation of the hypothalamic-pituitary axis when midline anomalies are present","Electroencephalography to characterise epilepsy","Genetic evaluation including EMX2 and cortical malformation testing where indicated"],"redFlags":["Drug-resistant focal or generalised epilepsy","Endocrine dysfunction when associated with septo-optic dysplasia","Progressive macrocephaly or raised intracranial pressure in large open-lip clefts","Visual impairment from optic nerve hypoplasia"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the size, type, and laterality of the cleft. Unilateral closed-lip schizencephaly may cause only mild hemiparesis and manageable epilepsy, whereas bilateral open-lip disease is typically associated with severe motor and cognitive disability and refractory seizures.","pitfalls":["Mistaking schizencephaly for a porencephalic cyst by failing to identify the grey-matter lining","Overlooking associated absence of the septum pellucidum and optic nerve hypoplasia","Not screening for hypothalamic-pituitary dysfunction when septo-optic features coexist","Confusing a large open-lip cleft with hydrocephalus or hydranencephaly on imaging"],"management":["No specific therapy corrects the malformation, so management targets its neurological consequences","Control focal or refractory epilepsy with antiseizure medication, escalating to epilepsy surgery evaluation in drug-resistant cases","Treat spasticity and hemiparesis with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, orthotics and antispasticity measures such as botulinum toxin or baclofen","Provide developmental, educational and speech and language support for cognitive delay","Screen for and manage associated septo-optic dysplasia, replacing deficient hypothalamic-pituitary hormones and treating visual impairment from optic nerve hypoplasia","Coordinate multidisciplinary paediatric neurology, neurosurgery, ophthalmology, endocrinology and rehabilitation care","Offer genetic evaluation and counselling where a genetic cause such as an EMX2 variant is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","Barkovich Pediatric Neuroimaging"],"crossLinks":[]},"schizoaffective_disorder":{"name":"Schizoaffective disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["schizoaffective psychosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The concept describes an illness in which a major mood episode (depressive or manic) coexists with psychotic symptoms that also meet criteria characteristic of schizophrenia over the same continuous period","The distinguishing requirement is that delusions or hallucinations must be present for a substantial period in the absence of a prominent mood episode, separating it from mood disorder with psychotic features","Two subtypes are recognised according to whether the mood disturbance is predominantly bipolar or depressive in nature","Diagnosis is longitudinal and often provisional, requiring observation over time, because the relative burden of mood and psychotic symptoms determines the label","Management typically combines an antipsychotic with mood stabilisation or antidepressant treatment guided by the predominant mood pattern","Suicide risk is significant and should be assessed repeatedly, particularly during depressive phases and following resolution of psychosis","Exclude substance-induced psychosis and general medical causes, and confirm that psychotic symptoms are not confined to mood episodes","Psychosocial support, adherence promotion, and long-term follow-up are important because the course is chronic and relapsing"],"pathophysiology":"Schizoaffective disorder is conceptualised as lying on a spectrum between schizophrenia and the mood disorders, sharing neurobiological features with both, including dopaminergic dysregulation implicated in psychosis and monoaminergic and stress-axis disturbances implicated in mood pathology. Genetic vulnerability overlapping with both schizophrenia and bipolar disorder, neurodevelopmental factors, and altered corticolimbic circuitry are thought to contribute.","additionalDifferentials":["Schizophrenia","Bipolar disorder with psychotic features","Major depressive disorder with psychotic features","Substance-induced psychotic disorder","Psychosis due to a general medical condition","Delusional disorder","Schizophreniform disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Longitudinal assessment documenting the temporal relationship of mood and psychotic symptoms","Structured suicide risk assessment","Urine drug screen and substance use history","Baseline metabolic monitoring before and during antipsychotic treatment","Neuroimaging and medical workup where an organic cause is suspected","Collateral history to clarify longitudinal course"],"redFlags":["Active suicidal or homicidal ideation with plan or intent","Command hallucinations directing harm to self or others","Acute manic escalation with dangerous behaviour or impaired judgement","Catatonia, severe self-neglect, or refusal of food and fluids"],"prognosis":"The prognosis of schizoaffective disorder is generally intermediate between that of schizophrenia and the mood disorders, tending to be better than schizophrenia but worse than a pure mood disorder. Outcome is more favourable with predominant mood symptoms, good treatment adherence, and strong psychosocial support, and less favourable with prominent persistent psychosis and poor functioning between episodes.","pitfalls":["Labelling mood disorder with psychotic features as schizoaffective without evidence of psychosis outside mood episodes","Underestimating suicide risk during and after psychotic phases","Neglecting metabolic monitoring during antipsychotic treatment","Missing substance-induced or medical causes of combined mood and psychotic symptoms","Making a firm diagnosis prematurely without longitudinal observation"],"management":["Anchor treatment on an antipsychotic to control psychotic symptoms, reserving clozapine for treatment-resistant illness with the required haematological monitoring","Add mood-targeted pharmacotherapy — a mood stabiliser such as lithium or an anticonvulsant for the bipolar subtype, or an antidepressant for the depressive subtype","Provide psychosocial interventions including psychoeducation, cognitive behavioural therapy, family support and supported employment or education","Assess suicide risk repeatedly, particularly during depressive phases and after resolution of psychosis, escalating to crisis or inpatient care when risk is high","Monitor weight, glucose, lipids and ECG during antipsychotic treatment and address cardiometabolic and substance-use comorbidity","Consider electroconvulsive therapy for severe refractory mood episodes, catatonia or high suicide risk","Support adherence and relapse prevention, considering long-acting injectable antipsychotics, within a coordinated community mental-health team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"schizophrenia":{"name":"Schizophrenia","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["schizophrenic disorder","chronic psychosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic psychotic disorder defined by a combination of positive symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech and behaviour) and negative symptoms (blunted affect, avolition, social withdrawal), with significant functional decline","Diagnosis requires continuous disturbance over an extended period (on the order of six months) with an active phase of characteristic symptoms, after excluding mood and substance-related causes","Onset is typically in late adolescence or early adulthood, often somewhat earlier and with poorer premorbid function in men","Cognitive impairment (attention, working memory, executive function) is a core feature and a major driver of disability","Antipsychotic medication is the cornerstone of treatment, described here as a principle; clozapine is reserved for treatment-resistant illness and requires haematological monitoring","Comprehensive care combines pharmacotherapy with psychosocial interventions, family education, supported employment, and relapse prevention","Suicide risk is elevated, particularly early in the illness and in those with insight into their deterioration","Duration of untreated psychosis affects outcome, so early recognition and intervention are important"],"pathophysiology":"Schizophrenia is a neurodevelopmental disorder with strong genetic contribution in which aberrant dopaminergic signalling (mesolimbic overactivity linked to positive symptoms and mesocortical hypofunction linked to negative and cognitive symptoms) interacts with glutamatergic and other neurotransmitter abnormalities. Structural and functional brain changes, synaptic pruning abnormalities, and gene-environment interactions such as obstetric complications and cannabis exposure contribute to the emergence of illness.","additionalDifferentials":["Schizoaffective disorder","Bipolar disorder or major depression with psychotic features","Brief psychotic disorder and schizophreniform disorder","Delusional disorder","Substance-induced psychosis","Psychosis secondary to a medical or neurological condition","Autoimmune or paraneoplastic encephalitis presenting with psychosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Substance use screening including cannabis and stimulants","Neuroimaging and, where indicated, investigation for organic or autoimmune causes in first-episode psychosis","Baseline metabolic parameters (weight, glucose, lipids) and ECG before and during antipsychotic treatment","Full blood count monitoring during clozapine treatment","Cognitive assessment to characterise functional impairment"],"redFlags":["Command hallucinations or suicidal ideation with risk to self","Delusional beliefs that place others at risk","First-episode psychosis with atypical features suggesting an organic cause","Signs of neuroleptic malignant syndrome such as rigidity, hyperthermia, and autonomic instability","Agranulocytosis during clozapine therapy"],"prognosis":"Schizophrenia usually follows a chronic course with episodic exacerbations and variable residual impairment. Better outcomes are associated with acute onset, good premorbid functioning, prominent mood symptoms, and early treatment, whereas insidious onset, prominent negative symptoms, and long untreated psychosis predict poorer functional recovery.","pitfalls":["Attributing first-episode psychosis to a primary psychiatric disorder without excluding organic and substance causes","Neglecting metabolic monitoring in patients on antipsychotics","Delaying a clozapine trial in genuinely treatment-resistant illness","Underestimating suicide risk, especially early in the course","Focusing only on positive symptoms while ignoring disabling negative and cognitive symptoms"],"management":["Establish an antipsychotic as the cornerstone of treatment, individualising the choice to balance efficacy against side-effect burden","Reserve clozapine for treatment-resistant illness, with mandatory haematological monitoring for neutropenia","Use long-acting injectable antipsychotics to support adherence and reduce relapse where oral treatment is inconsistent","Combine pharmacotherapy with psychosocial interventions — cognitive behavioural therapy for psychosis, family education, supported employment and cognitive remediation","Monitor and manage cardiometabolic risk with weight, glucose, lipids and ECG, and screen for and treat comorbid substance use, especially cannabis and stimulants","Assess suicide and violence risk and provide crisis and inpatient care during acute relapse","Promote early intervention and relapse prevention through a coordinated multidisciplinary community mental-health team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"schmid_metaphyseal_chondrodysplasia":{"name":"Schmid metaphyseal chondrodysplasia","system":"Skeletal (skeletal dysplasia)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["COL10A1","Metaphyseal chondrodysplasia Schmid type","MCDS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and skeletal dysplasia references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant metaphyseal dysplasia caused by heterozygous COL10A1 mutations affecting type X collagen in the hypertrophic zone of the growth plate.","Presents in early childhood soon after walking begins, with mild short stature, bowing of the legs (genu varum) and a waddling gait.","Radiographs show metaphyseal flaring, cupping and irregularity that are most marked at the proximal femur, producing coxa vara; the epiphyses and spine are characteristically spared.","Biochemistry is normal — calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase and vitamin D metabolites are unremarkable, which distinguishes it from rickets.","It is the commonest and mildest of the metaphyseal chondrodysplasias, and final adult height is usually only mildly reduced.","Metaphyseal changes tend to remodel and improve after growth-plate fusion at puberty.","The lower-limb bowing and coxa vara can closely mimic healing or hypophosphataemic rickets on imaging.","Family history is frequently positive with variable expressivity between affected relatives."],"pathophysiology":"The disorder is caused by heterozygous mutations in COL10A1, which encodes the alpha-1 chain of type X collagen — a short-chain, network-forming collagen synthesised almost exclusively by hypertrophic chondrocytes in the growth plate. Most pathogenic variants cluster in the C-terminal NC1 trimerisation domain and act through misfolding, impaired trimer assembly and effective haploinsufficiency (in part via nonsense-mediated decay), triggering endoplasmic-reticulum stress in hypertrophic chondrocytes. This disrupts the orderly progression through the hypertrophic zone and endochondral ossification at the metaphysis, giving the metaphyseal flaring, cupping and coxa vara. Because the resting and proliferative zones and the epiphyseal cartilage do not depend on type X collagen, epiphyses and vertebral bodies are spared, and mineral homeostasis is entirely normal. Inheritance is autosomal dominant with variable expressivity.","additionalDifferentials":["Nutritional (vitamin D deficiency) rickets","X-linked hypophosphataemic rickets","Jansen metaphyseal chondrodysplasia","Cartilage-hair hypoplasia (McKusick metaphyseal chondrodysplasia)","Metaphyseal chondrodysplasia, Spahr type","Spondylometaphyseal dysplasias and pseudoachondroplasia","Hypophosphatasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey with long-bone and pelvic radiographs documenting metaphyseal flaring and coxa vara","Serum calcium, phosphate and alkaline phosphatase to exclude rickets","25-hydroxyvitamin D and parathyroid hormone","Assessment of renal tubular phosphate handling if hypophosphataemic rickets is considered","COL10A1 sequencing or a skeletal dysplasia gene panel","Standing lower-limb alignment radiographs and serial height measurement","Three-generation pedigree and screening of relatives"],"redFlags":["Progressive coxa vara with hip pain or deterioration of gait requiring orthopaedic review","Severe or markedly asymmetric bowing suggesting an alternative cause such as rickets or Blount disease","Disproportionate short stature or spinal involvement pointing to a different skeletal dysplasia","Any biochemical abnormality or fragility fracture, which should prompt reassessment of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally good; as the mildest metaphyseal dysplasia it produces only mild short stature, with spontaneous metaphyseal remodelling after growth-plate fusion. Persistent bowing or coxa vara occasionally requires corrective osteotomy.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the child as having rickets and giving vitamin D, when biochemistry is normal in Schmid disease.","Overlooking a positive family history of short stature and bow legs.","Attributing vertebral changes to Schmid disease, as spinal involvement instead suggests a spondylometaphyseal dysplasia.","Assuming bowing will always resolve spontaneously, when some children need surgical correction."],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy is needed; management is orthopaedic and supportive, reassuring families that the course is mild","Monitor lower-limb alignment and growth with serial standing radiographs and height measurement through childhood","Reserve corrective osteotomy for persistent or severe genu varum or coxa vara that fails to remodel","Distinguish from rickets so that unnecessary vitamin D and phosphate treatment is avoided, since calcium, phosphate and alkaline phosphatase are normal","Provide physiotherapy and simple analgesia for lower-limb pain and gait disturbance","Follow through paediatric orthopaedics and clinical genetics, anticipating spontaneous remodelling after growth-plate fusion","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant COL10A1 disorder and assess affected relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of medical genetics","Paediatric skeletal dysplasia atlas and reference","Clinical review of the metaphyseal chondrodysplasias"],"crossLinks":[]},"schmid_metaphyseal_dysplasia":{"name":"Schmid metaphyseal chondrodysplasia","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["COL10A1","metaphyseal chondrodysplasia Schmid type","MCDS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest metaphyseal chondrodysplasia, autosomal dominant, caused by variants in COL10A1 (type X collagen)","Presents in early childhood with short stature, bow legs (genu varum), a waddling gait and lower-limb pain after walking begins","Radiographs show metaphyseal flaring, irregularity and cupping that are most marked at the proximal femur, with coxa vara a characteristic feature","Crucially the epiphyses and spine are essentially normal, which helps distinguish it from spondylo-forms and from epiphyseal dysplasias","The key mimic is rickets, but in Schmid dysplasia serum calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase and vitamin D are all normal","Metaphyseal changes tend to improve with skeletal maturity and final height is only mildly reduced","A positive family history with dominant inheritance and normal biochemistry strongly supports the diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Schmid metaphyseal chondrodysplasia is caused by dominant variants in COL10A1, encoding type X collagen, which is produced by hypertrophic chondrocytes in the growth plate. Mutant chains misfold and are retained within the endoplasmic reticulum, triggering an unfolded-protein/ER-stress response that disrupts the orderly progression of hypertrophic chondrocytes and mineralisation at the metaphysis. The result is metaphyseal disorganisation, flaring and coxa vara, while epiphyseal cartilage and vertebral growth, which rely less on type X collagen, remain largely spared.","additionalDifferentials":["Nutritional or hypophosphataemic rickets","Jansen metaphyseal chondrodysplasia","McKusick cartilage-hair hypoplasia","Pseudoachondroplasia","Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia","Hypophosphatasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey demonstrating metaphyseal flaring and coxa vara with normal epiphyses and spine","Serum calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, parathyroid hormone and vitamin D to exclude rickets","COL10A1 molecular genetic testing","Standing lower-limb alignment radiographs to quantify varus deformity","Growth monitoring and assessment of family members given dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Biochemical evidence of rickets or a metabolic bone disease requiring specific treatment","Severe or progressive coxa vara or limb deformity affecting mobility","Disproportionate short stature or spinal changes suggesting an alternative dysplasia","Persistent bone pain out of keeping with the expected mild course"],"prognosis":"Generally favourable: short stature is mild, radiographic metaphyseal changes tend to remodel with growth, and life expectancy is normal, though residual bowing may occasionally warrant orthopaedic correction.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as rickets and giving unnecessary vitamin D or phosphate treatment","Overlooking the normal epiphyses and spine that distinguish it from other dysplasias","Attributing normal biochemistry to treated rickets rather than a genetic metaphyseal dysplasia","Recommending aggressive surgery for deformity that would remodel spontaneously"],"management":["Management is supportive and orthopaedic, as no pharmacological treatment alters the underlying type X collagen defect","Undertake growth monitoring and serial standing lower-limb alignment radiographs to track genu varum and coxa vara","Offer corrective osteotomy only for significant or non-remodelling varus deformity","Avoid mistaking it for rickets by confirming normal calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase and vitamin D so antirachitic therapy is not given inappropriately","Provide physiotherapy and simple analgesia for lower-limb pain and the waddling gait","Coordinate follow-up through clinical genetics and paediatric orthopaedics, anticipating improvement with skeletal maturity","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and examine at-risk relatives with COL10A1 testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews / Bone Dysplasias (Spranger): Schmid Metaphyseal Chondrodysplasia"],"crossLinks":[]},"schnitzler_syndrome":{"name":"Schnitzler syndrome","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic urticaria with monoclonal gammopathy","Schnitzler's syndrome","monoclonal IgM urticarial dermatosis","acquired autoinflammatory syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology / Strasbourg diagnostic criteria for Schnitzler syndrome","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acquired, adult-onset autoinflammatory disorder defined by a chronic urticarial rash together with a monoclonal gammopathy, typically IgM kappa","The urticarial lesions are usually non-pruritic or minimally itchy and, unlike ordinary urticaria, are neutrophilic on biopsy","Supporting features include recurrent fever, bone pain with radiological osteosclerosis, arthralgia, lymphadenopathy and raised inflammatory markers","The Strasbourg criteria combine the two obligate features (urticarial rash and monoclonal IgM or IgG) with minor clinical and laboratory criteria","The clinical picture and dramatic response to interleukin-1 blockade place it firmly among the acquired autoinflammatory conditions","There is a long-term risk of progression to a lymphoproliferative disorder, particularly Waldenstrom macroglobulinaemia, requiring ongoing surveillance","Interleukin-1 inhibition typically produces a rapid and near-complete clinical response and is the treatment of choice"],"pathophysiology":"Schnitzler syndrome is an acquired autoinflammatory condition in which excessive activation of the innate immune system, particularly interleukin-1-driven inflammation, produces a neutrophilic urticarial dermatosis and systemic inflammation. It occurs in the context of a monoclonal gammopathy, most often IgM kappa, and although the precise link between the paraprotein and the inflammatory drive is not fully defined, the disease behaves as an interleukin-1-mediated process, as evidenced by its striking response to interleukin-1 blockade. Chronic overproduction of interleukin-1 beta and downstream cytokines accounts for the fever, bone changes and acute phase response, while the underlying clonal B-cell process explains the association with, and risk of evolution to, lymphoproliferative malignancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic spontaneous urticaria","Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes (particularly adult Muckle-Wells syndrome)","Adult-onset Still disease","Urticarial vasculitis","Waldenstrom macroglobulinaemia and other lymphoproliferative disorders","Systemic lupus erythematosus with urticarial manifestations"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum protein electrophoresis and immunofixation to detect and characterise the monoclonal component","Skin biopsy demonstrating a neutrophilic urticarial dermatosis","Acute phase reactants including C-reactive protein and serum amyloid A","Bone imaging for osteosclerosis or osteolytic changes","Bone marrow examination and monitoring for lymphoproliferative disease","NLRP3 genetic testing to exclude a late-presenting cryopyrin-associated periodic syndrome where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Rising paraprotein, new cytopenias or organomegaly suggesting transformation to a lymphoproliferative malignancy","New proteinuria indicating possible AA amyloidosis","Unexplained weight loss, night sweats or progressive lymphadenopathy","Severe refractory bone pain with lytic lesions"],"prognosis":"Inflammatory symptoms are usually well controlled with interleukin-1 inhibition and quality of life can be restored, but the disorder is chronic and carries a persistent long-term risk of evolving into a lymphoproliferative malignancy, so lifelong monitoring is required.","pitfalls":["Treating the rash as ordinary allergic urticaria and relying on antihistamines, which are largely ineffective","Failing to perform serum immunofixation and therefore missing the defining monoclonal gammopathy","Not arranging long-term haematological surveillance for lymphoproliferative transformation","Confusing it with a late-onset cryopyrin-associated periodic syndrome without appropriate testing"],"management":["Treat with interleukin-1 blockade using anakinra or canakinumab as the mainstay, which typically produces dramatic resolution of the rash, fever and inflammation","Use symptomatic measures such as antihistamines, NSAIDs or colchicine for milder disease or as adjuncts, recognising that conventional urticaria treatment is often ineffective","Employ corticosteroids for control but limit long-term use because of cumulative toxicity","Monitor inflammatory markers including C-reactive protein and serum amyloid A to guide therapy and reduce the risk of secondary amyloidosis","Undertake lifelong surveillance of the monoclonal gammopathy for progression to a lymphoproliferative disorder such as Waldenstrom macroglobulinaemia, with periodic serum electrophoresis and bone-marrow assessment as indicated","Manage bone pain and osteosclerosis symptomatically and assess for pathological complications","Coordinate care between clinical immunology and haematology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Strasbourg diagnostic criteria for Schnitzler syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"schnyder_corneal_dystrophy":{"name":"Schnyder corneal dystrophy","system":"Eye / Cornea","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Schnyder crystalline corneal dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / corneal dystrophy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant stromal dystrophy of abnormal corneal lipid deposition, linked to UBIAD1 mutations","Corneal crystals are present in only about half of cases, so their absence does not exclude the diagnosis","Findings evolve with age: central crystalline haze, then a dense arcus lipoides, then diffuse stromal clouding","It is associated with systemic hyperlipidaemia, which should be screened for and managed","Vision declines slowly, and glare is a common complaint","Management includes lipid assessment and, for visually significant disease, superficial ablation or keratoplasty"],"pathophysiology":"Schnyder corneal dystrophy arises from mutations in the UBIAD1 gene, causing abnormal local metabolism and accumulation of cholesterol and phospholipids within the corneal stroma. This lipid deposition produces corneal crystals (when present), a prominent arcus and progressive stromal haze. Although the corneal defect is local, affected individuals frequently have systemic dyslipidaemia, so the cornea can be a marker of a broader lipid abnormality.","additionalDifferentials":["Other crystalline keratopathies (for example cystinosis, monoclonal gammopathy, drug deposits)","Arcus lipoides from hyperlipidaemia alone","Bietti crystalline dystrophy (retinal, with corneal crystals)","Other stromal corneal dystrophies","Infectious crystalline keratopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination of crystals, arcus and stromal haze","Fasting lipid profile to assess systemic hyperlipidaemia","UBIAD1 genetic testing where confirmation is needed","Corneal imaging and topography","Family history and examination of relatives"],"redFlags":["Significant systemic dyslipidaemia requiring management","Progressive visual loss or disabling glare","Features suggesting an alternative crystalline keratopathy","Rapid change atypical for a slowly progressive dystrophy"],"prognosis":"Vision declines slowly over decades; systemic lipid management is important, and superficial ablation or keratoplasty can help visually significant disease, though deposits may recur.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because crystals are absent","Overlooking associated systemic hyperlipidaemia","Confusing it with other crystalline keratopathies","Missing the autosomal dominant family history"],"management":["Screen for and manage systemic hyperlipidaemia with lipid-lowering therapy such as statins plus lifestyle measures, since the cornea signals a broader lipid disorder","Manage glare and photophobia conservatively with tinted lenses while vision remains adequate","For visually significant stromal opacity offer superficial or phototherapeutic keratectomy, or lamellar or penetrating keratoplasty in advanced disease","Counsel that lipid deposits may recur in the cornea or graft after surgery, requiring ongoing review","Monitor visual acuity and corneal opacity over time with slit-lamp examination and corneal imaging","Coordinate care between ophthalmology and lipid or metabolic services","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant UBIAD1 dystrophy and examine relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","IC3D classification of corneal dystrophies"],"crossLinks":[]},"schwannoma":{"name":"Schwannoma","system":"Peripheral nervous system / Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Neurilemmoma","Neurinoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours / Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign, encapsulated peripheral nerve sheath tumour of Schwann cells that grows eccentrically and can usually be dissected off the parent nerve","This eccentric, separable growth contrasts with neurofibroma, which is fusiform and entwined with nerve fascicles","Histology shows compact cellular Antoni A areas with nuclear palisading (Verocay bodies) alternating with loose, myxoid Antoni B areas","There is strong, diffuse S100 and SOX10 positivity reflecting Schwann cell origin","Favoured sites include the flexor surfaces of the limbs, the head and neck, spinal nerve roots, and the vestibular nerve, where it forms the vestibular schwannoma","Percussion over the lesion may reproduce paraesthesiae in the nerve distribution, and MRI can show a target sign and a split-fat sign","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas define neurofibromatosis type 2, while multiple non-vestibular tumours suggest schwannomatosis linked to SMARCB1 or LZTR1","Recognised variants include ancient, cellular, plexiform, and melanotic schwannoma, the last associated with Carney complex; malignant change is very rare"],"pathophysiology":"Schwannoma is a benign neoplasm arising from the Schwann cells that form the myelin sheath of peripheral nerves. It grows as a well-encapsulated mass eccentric to the nerve, displacing rather than engulfing the fascicles, which is why it can often be enucleated with preservation of nerve function. Most sporadic tumours show biallelic inactivation of the NF2 gene on chromosome 22, whose product merlin normally restrains cell proliferation; loss of merlin function drives Schwann cell overgrowth. Germline NF2 alterations produce neurofibromatosis type 2 with its hallmark bilateral vestibular schwannomas, whereas germline SMARCB1 or LZTR1 variants underlie schwannomatosis. Histologically the tumour alternates between organised, cellular Antoni A tissue with palisaded Verocay bodies and hypocellular, myxoid Antoni B tissue, and it characteristically has thick-walled hyalinised vessels. Malignant transformation is exceptional, in contrast to the neurofibroma of neurofibromatosis type 1.","additionalDifferentials":["Neurofibroma","Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour","Traumatic neuroma","Ganglion cyst adjacent to a nerve","Other soft tissue tumours such as leiomyoma or myxoma","Vestibular schwannoma versus meningioma at the cerebellopontine angle","Perineurioma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI with contrast to characterise the lesion and its relationship to the nerve, including target and split-fat signs","Audiometry and dedicated internal auditory meatus imaging for suspected vestibular schwannoma","Histology demonstrating Antoni A and B areas with Verocay bodies","Immunohistochemistry for S100 and SOX10","Genetic assessment for NF2, SMARCB1, and LZTR1 when multiple tumours or a syndrome is suspected","Nerve conduction studies where functional deficit needs assessment"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, pain, or new neurological deficit suggesting malignant transformation","Bilateral vestibular tumours or multiple schwannomas indicating an underlying syndrome","Progressive hearing loss, tinnitus, or brainstem compression from a vestibular lesion","Spinal lesions causing cord or root compression","A large deep tumour with atypical imaging features"],"prognosis":"Schwannoma is benign with an excellent prognosis; careful enucleation is usually curative and preserves nerve function, and recurrence is uncommon. Malignant transformation is very rare, and the main determinants of outcome are tumour site and any associated tumour syndrome.","pitfalls":["Resecting the nerve unnecessarily when the tumour can be dissected free with preservation of function","Overinterpreting the degenerative atypia of ancient schwannoma as malignancy","Failing to investigate for neurofibromatosis type 2 or schwannomatosis when tumours are multiple or bilateral","Confusing schwannoma with neurofibroma and thereby misjudging the risk of malignant transformation"],"management":["Offer observation with serial imaging for small, asymptomatic tumours given their benign, slow-growing nature","Treat symptomatic or enlarging lesions by microsurgical enucleation, dissecting the tumour off the parent nerve to preserve nerve function","For vestibular schwannoma choose between surveillance, microsurgery and stereotactic radiosurgery according to size, hearing and symptoms","Provide symptomatic management of pain and neurological deficit, with nerve-conduction assessment where function is impaired","Undertake genetic evaluation for NF2, SMARCB1 or LZTR1 when tumours are multiple or a syndrome is suspected, and screen accordingly","Refer for specialist neurosurgical, skull-base or soft-tissue multidisciplinary input","Reassure regarding the excellent prognosis, as malignant transformation is very rare and enucleation is usually curative"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System"],"crossLinks":[]},"schwannomatosis":{"name":"Schwannomatosis","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SMARCB1-related schwannomatosis","LZTR1-related schwannomatosis","neurilemmomatosis","non-NF2 schwannoma predisposition"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The syndrome predisposes to multiple schwannomas along peripheral, spinal, and cranial nerves while characteristically sparing the vestibular nerves bilaterally, which distinguishes it from neurofibromatosis type 2","Chronic, often severe neuropathic pain is the dominant symptom and may be disproportionate to the size or number of tumours","The two principal genes are SMARCB1 and LZTR1, both located on chromosome 22 near the NF2 locus, and a proportion of patients have no identified variant","A single unilateral vestibular schwannoma or isolated meningiomas can occur, so careful imaging and phenotyping are needed to separate it from NF2","Tumours are histologically benign, and malignant transformation is rare, so surveillance and symptom control rather than aggressive resection guide management","Diagnosis rests on clinical criteria, imaging demonstrating multiple schwannomas, histological confirmation, and molecular genetic testing including tumour analysis","Care is multidisciplinary, combining neurosurgery or peripheral nerve surgery for symptomatic or enlarging lesions with dedicated chronic pain management"],"pathophysiology":"Schwannomatosis is a tumour predisposition syndrome caused by germline variants in the tumour suppressor genes SMARCB1 or LZTR1, both situated on chromosome 22q close to NF2. Tumour development typically follows a multi-hit mechanism in which the germline variant is compounded by somatic loss of the region encompassing both the predisposing gene and NF2, so that the resulting schwannomas show combined inactivation. This shared chromosomal neighbourhood explains the clinical overlap with NF2 while the preservation of at least one functional NF2 allele in constitutional tissue accounts for the absence of the bilateral vestibular schwannomas that define NF2. Inheritance is autosomal dominant with incomplete penetrance, and many cases appear sporadic.","additionalDifferentials":["Neurofibromatosis type 2","Neurofibromatosis type 1 with multiple neurofibromas","Multiple sporadic schwannomas","Carney complex","Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour","Localised chronic pain syndromes without tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Whole-neuraxis MRI (brain including internal auditory meati, and whole spine) to map tumours and exclude bilateral vestibular schwannomas","MRI of symptomatic peripheral nerves or plexuses","Histological confirmation of resected lesions as schwannomas","Molecular genetic testing of SMARCB1 and LZTR1, ideally including tumour tissue analysis","Audiological assessment if any vestibulocochlear symptoms are present","Structured chronic pain assessment"],"redFlags":["Bilateral vestibular schwannomas, which point to NF2 rather than schwannomatosis","Rapid tumour growth, new neurological deficit, or features suggesting malignant transformation","Spinal cord or cauda equina compression from an enlarging intraspinal schwannoma","Intractable pain with functional decline requiring specialist intervention"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is generally normal because the tumours are benign and malignant change is uncommon. The principal burden is chronic pain and the cumulative morbidity of multiple tumours and their surgical treatment; quality of life depends heavily on effective pain control and judicious, function-preserving surgery.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing schwannomatosis without excluding bilateral vestibular schwannomas and thereby missing NF2","Assuming pain is proportional to tumour bulk and under-treating disabling neuropathic pain","Pursuing aggressive resection of all tumours rather than targeting symptomatic or growing lesions","Relying on blood testing alone, since tumour tissue analysis often clarifies the genetic diagnosis"],"management":["Prioritise multimodal chronic neuropathic pain control with neuropathic agents such as gabapentinoids and tricyclic or SNRI antidepressants, physiotherapy and specialist pain-service input, as pain is the dominant burden","Resect individual schwannomas selectively for intractable pain, neurological compromise or growth, favouring function-preserving surgery over aggressive removal","Undertake whole-neuraxis MRI surveillance to map tumours and exclude the bilateral vestibular schwannomas that would indicate neurofibromatosis type 2","Confirm the diagnosis and distinguish it from NF2 with histology and molecular testing of SMARCB1 and LZTR1, including tumour tissue where possible","Assess hearing if vestibulocochlear symptoms arise","Coordinate care through neurology, neurosurgery, pain medicine and clinical genetics","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance with incomplete penetrance, noting many cases appear sporadic"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (University of Washington)","WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System"],"crossLinks":[]},"schwartz_jampel_syndrome":{"name":"Schwartz-Jampel syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SJS","HSPG2","perlecan deficiency","chondrodystrophic myotonia","myotonic chondrodystrophy","Schwartz-Jampel-Aberfeld syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare disorder combining continuous muscle stiffness (myotonia-like activity) with a skeletal chondrodysplasia, usually presenting in infancy or early childhood","Characteristic facial appearance includes blepharophimosis with narrowed palpebral fissures, puckered lips, a fixed pursed expression and low-set ears","Generalised muscle stiffness produces a stiff gait, muscle hypertrophy and difficulty opening the eyes and mouth","Skeletal features include short stature, joint contractures, kyphoscoliosis and bony dysplasia on imaging","Caused by mutations in HSPG2 encoding perlecan, a heparan sulphate proteoglycan of the basement membrane and cartilage, inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern","The sustained muscle activity is largely of peripheral nerve and muscle origin and, unlike a pure channelopathy, can show complex continuous discharges on electromyography","Antimyotonic agents such as sodium-channel-blocking drugs and other muscle-relaxant strategies can reduce the stiffness"],"pathophysiology":"Schwartz-Jampel syndrome is caused by mutations in HSPG2, which encodes perlecan, a large heparan sulphate proteoglycan that is a major structural and signalling component of basement membranes and of cartilage extracellular matrix. Deficient or dysfunctional perlecan disrupts cartilage development, producing the chondrodysplasia, short stature and skeletal deformities. At the neuromuscular junction, perlecan anchors acetylcholinesterase in the synaptic basal lamina, and its deficiency reduces cholinesterase activity so that acetylcholine action is prolonged, contributing, together with abnormal muscle-membrane excitability, to the sustained muscle activity and stiffness that clinically resembles myotonia.","additionalDifferentials":["Myotonia congenita","Paramyotonia congenita","Myotonic dystrophy","Isaacs syndrome (neuromyotonia) and other peripheral nerve hyperexcitability syndromes","Congenital myasthenic syndromes with abnormal cholinesterase","Skeletal dysplasias with contractures and stiff joints"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electromyography showing continuous complex repetitive and myotonic-like discharges","Serum creatine kinase, which may be normal or mildly elevated","Skeletal survey and imaging to characterise the chondrodysplasia and contractures","Targeted HSPG2 genetic testing","Ophthalmological and orthopaedic assessment for eye and skeletal complications","Muscle biopsy where the diagnosis remains uncertain"],"redFlags":["Feeding or airway difficulty in infancy from facial and jaw stiffness","Susceptibility to anaesthetic complications, including reported concern about malignant-hyperthermia-like reactions and difficult airway","Progressive kyphoscoliosis compromising respiratory function","Severe joint contractures limiting mobility and function"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies with severity, but many children with the classic form survive into adulthood with persistent stiffness and skeletal deformity, whereas a rarer severe neonatal presentation carries higher early morbidity; the disorder is generally non-progressive once established but causes lasting orthopaedic and functional handicap.","pitfalls":["Attributing the stiffness to a pure muscle channelopathy and missing the skeletal chondrodysplasia that defines the syndrome","Overlooking the anaesthetic and airway risks in a child with facial and jaw stiffness","Confusing the continuous discharges with the discharges of Isaacs syndrome or true myotonia","Failing to arrange orthopaedic and ophthalmological surveillance for the skeletal and eye complications"],"management":["Reduce the myotonia-like muscle stiffness with membrane-stabilising agents such as carbamazepine, phenytoin or the antiarrhythmic mexiletine","Provide physiotherapy, stretching and orthopaedic management for joint contractures, kyphoscoliosis and skeletal deformity","Offer ophthalmological treatment for blepharophimosis and related eye complications, including surgical correction where vision or lid closure is affected","Address feeding and airway difficulty from facial and muscle involvement, and flag the anaesthetic risk of a difficult airway and perioperative complications before surgery","Support growth, mobility and function with occupational therapy and mobility aids","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across neurology, orthopaedics, ophthalmology and rehabilitation","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive HSPG2 (perlecan) disorder and screen at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews / OMIM: Schwartz-Jampel Syndrome (HSPG2)"],"crossLinks":[]},"sciatica":{"name":"Sciatica","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lumbar radiculopathy","lumbosacral radicular pain","nerve root pain","radicular leg pain"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon / standard sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sciatica describes radicular leg pain following a dermatomal distribution below the knee, often with associated tingling, numbness, or weakness, and typically worse than any back pain","The commonest cause is compression or irritation of a lumbosacral nerve root by a prolapsed intervertebral disc, most often at L4-5 or L5-S1","In older patients, lumbar spinal stenosis causing neurogenic claudication is a frequent cause, with leg symptoms brought on by walking and relieved by sitting or flexion","A positive straight-leg raise (and crossed straight-leg raise) suggests lower lumbar nerve root tension, while the femoral stretch test targets higher roots","Examination should localise the affected root through its characteristic sensory, motor, and reflex changes","Most disc-related sciatica improves over weeks to a few months with conservative care, staying active, analgesia by principle, and physiotherapy","MRI is reserved for severe or persistent symptoms, progressive deficit, or when surgery or injection is being considered, not for routine early management","Surgery such as discectomy is considered for persistent disabling radicular pain that fails conservative treatment, or urgently for significant or progressive neurological deficit","Screen every patient for cauda equina and other red flags at presentation and again if symptoms change"],"pathophysiology":"Sciatica is caused by compression, tension, or chemical irritation of a lumbosacral nerve root, most commonly from a herniated disc whose nucleus material both mechanically compresses and inflames the root. Other causes include degenerative foraminal or central canal stenosis, spondylolisthesis, and less often tumour or infection. Nerve root inflammation and ischaemia generate the characteristic radiating pain and neurological signs in the corresponding dermatome and myotome.","additionalDifferentials":["Lumbar disc herniation with radiculopathy","Lumbar spinal or foraminal stenosis with neurogenic claudication","Spondylolisthesis","Piriformis syndrome or deep gluteal syndrome","Peripheral neuropathy or nerve entrapment (e.g. peroneal nerve)","Hip or sacroiliac joint pathology referring to the leg","Vascular claudication","Spinal tumour or infection compressing a nerve root"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis with root localisation; imaging not required for typical early sciatica without red flags","MRI of the lumbar spine for severe, persistent, or progressive symptoms or when intervention is planned","CT if MRI is contraindicated","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography in selected cases to clarify the level or exclude peripheral neuropathy","Blood tests and targeted imaging if infection or malignancy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Cauda equina syndrome: saddle anaesthesia, urinary retention or incontinence, faecal incontinence, and bilateral or progressive leg weakness requiring emergency imaging and decompression","Progressive or severe motor deficit such as foot drop needing urgent assessment","Features suggesting malignancy: previous cancer, unexplained weight loss, age over 50, and night pain","Features suggesting infection: fever, immunosuppression, intravenous drug use, and severe unremitting pain"],"prognosis":"The majority of disc-related cases resolve within six to twelve weeks with conservative treatment, but a proportion have persistent or recurrent symptoms; surgery gives faster relief of leg pain in selected patients, and outcomes worsen if significant neurological deficit is not treated promptly.","pitfalls":["Failing to ask about and detect cauda equina features, risking permanent deficit","Ordering early MRI and over-attributing symptoms to incidental disc findings","Mistaking peripheral entrapment, hip, or vascular pathology for radicular pain","Delaying referral for progressive motor deficit"],"management":["Reassure and encourage staying active, since most disc-related sciatica settles over weeks to a few months with conservative care","Provide analgesia and structured physiotherapy while avoiding prolonged bed rest","Consider a fluoroscopically guided epidural or transforaminal corticosteroid injection for severe, persistent radicular pain","Reserve MRI of the lumbar spine for severe, progressive or persistent symptoms or when intervention is planned","Refer for surgical decompression such as microdiscectomy in selected patients with persistent disabling pain or significant neurological deficit","Escalate urgently for suspected cauda equina syndrome or progressive motor deficit, with emergency imaging and surgical decompression","Manage neurogenic claudication from spinal stenosis in older patients with physiotherapy, activity modification and decompressive surgery where disabling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"scimitar_syndrome":{"name":"Scimitar syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular / Congenital","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pulmonary venolobar syndrome","congenital pulmonary venolobar syndrome","hypogenetic lung syndrome","scimitar sign"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of partial anomalous pulmonary venous return in which right pulmonary venous drainage curves down to the inferior vena cava or right atrium","The curved vascular shadow paralleling the right heart border on the chest radiograph resembles a Turkish sword, giving the scimitar sign","Commonly accompanied by hypoplasia of the right lung, dextroposition of the heart and anomalous systemic arterial supply from the aorta to the affected lung","The anomalous vein produces a left-to-right shunt, and severity relates to shunt volume and any associated pulmonary hypertension","An infantile form presents with heart failure and pulmonary hypertension, whereas an adult form is often found incidentally or with recurrent infection","Associated cardiac defects, particularly atrial septal defect, are frequent and influence management","Repair options include baffling or reimplanting the anomalous vein and occluding the aberrant systemic arterial supply, guided by symptoms and shunt size"],"pathophysiology":"Scimitar syndrome is part of the pulmonary venolobar spectrum arising from abnormal development of the right lung bud and its venous drainage. All or part of the right lung drains anomalously through a scimitar-shaped vein into the inferior vena cava, right atrium, portal or hepatic veins rather than the left atrium, creating a left-to-right shunt. The affected lung is typically hypoplastic, pulling the heart rightward, and often receives an anomalous systemic arterial supply directly from the descending aorta, which adds a further left-to-right contribution and can cause pulmonary hypertension in the affected segment. The overall haemodynamic burden depends on the volume of anomalous flow, the aberrant arterial supply and any associated shunt lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated partial anomalous pulmonary venous return","Pulmonary sequestration","Atrial septal defect with unexplained right heart dilatation","Congenital pulmonary hypoplasia of other cause","Pulmonary arteriovenous malformation","Right lung agenesis or aplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph to identify the scimitar shadow, lung hypoplasia and cardiac dextroposition","Echocardiography to assess shunt, right heart loading and associated defects","CT or MR angiography to delineate the anomalous vein, aberrant arterial supply and lung anatomy","Cardiac catheterisation with angiography to quantify the shunt and map the systemic arterial supply","Perfusion or ventilation imaging to assess function of the affected lung","Electrocardiogram for evidence of right heart volume loading"],"redFlags":["Infantile presentation with heart failure and severe pulmonary hypertension","Recurrent pulmonary infection or haemoptysis from the anomalous segment","Progressive right heart dilatation from a large shunt","Signs of a large aberrant aortic supply causing pulmonary overcirculation"],"prognosis":"The adult form is often well tolerated with a good outlook, while the infantile form with pulmonary hypertension and associated defects has a more guarded prognosis; surgical repair can be complicated by scimitar vein stenosis.","pitfalls":["Missing the anomalous systemic arterial supply, which can cause pulmonary overcirculation if left untreated","Assuming an isolated atrial septal defect and overlooking the anomalous venous drainage","Underappreciating pulmonary hypertension confined to the affected lung","Failing to recognise postoperative scimitar vein stenosis as a cause of persistent symptoms"],"management":["Manage the infantile form with heart-failure therapy using diuretics and afterload reduction and treat associated pulmonary hypertension while planning intervention","Occlude any anomalous aortopulmonary systemic arterial supply by catheter embolisation to reduce left-to-right shunting","Offer surgical repair by rerouting or reimplanting the scimitar vein to the left atrium for a haemodynamically significant shunt, and repair associated defects such as an atrial septal defect","Treat and prevent recurrent lower respiratory infection in the hypoplastic lung, considering resection of a non-functioning segment where infection recurs","Observe small, asymptomatic adult-form shunts, which are often well tolerated","Monitor after repair for scimitar vein stenosis and residual pulmonary hypertension","Coordinate care through congenital cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","Anderson's Paediatric Cardiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"scleredema":{"name":"Scleredema (of Buschke)","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scleredema of Buschke","scleredema adultorum","scleredema diabeticorum","Buschke disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Bolognia Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A deposition dermatosis with diffuse, non-pitting induration and thickening of the skin, typically over the upper back, posterior neck, and shoulders","The affected skin feels woody and firm, and movement of the neck and trunk can become restricted","Three recognised clinical settings: a post-infectious form after streptococcal or viral illness, an association with monoclonal gammopathy, and a form linked to long-standing diabetes (scleredema diabeticorum)","Unlike systemic sclerosis it spares the fingers and lacks Raynaud phenomenon, sclerodactyly, and nailfold capillary changes","Histology shows thickened dermis with widely separated collagen bundles and accumulation of mucin (glycosaminoglycans)","The diabetes-associated form tends to be chronic and persistent, whereas the post-infectious form may resolve over months","There is no uniformly effective treatment; options explored include physiotherapy, phototherapy, and immunomodulatory approaches, alongside management of the underlying association"],"pathophysiology":"Scleredema is characterised by excessive deposition of collagen and mucopolysaccharide (mucin) in the reticular dermis, which thickens the skin and produces its firm, non-pitting induration. Fibroblasts in affected skin appear to be stimulated to overproduce collagen and glycosaminoglycans. The mechanisms differ by clinical context: a post-infectious immune response is implicated after streptococcal or viral illness, non-enzymatic glycosylation and altered collagen metabolism are thought relevant in diabetes, and a paraproteinaemic form is associated with monoclonal gammopathy, sometimes progressing to a plasma-cell dyscrasia. The dermal expansion, not oedema fluid, explains why the swelling does not pit.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)","Scleromyxoedema","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Eosinophilic fasciitis","Myxoedema of hypothyroidism","Chronic graft-versus-host disease with sclerosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing dermal thickening, separated collagen bundles, and mucin deposition","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation for monoclonal gammopathy","Fasting glucose and HbA1c to assess for diabetes","Streptococcal and viral serology when a post-infectious form is suspected","Thyroid function to exclude myxoedema","Autoantibody screen to help exclude systemic sclerosis"],"redFlags":["Detection of a monoclonal protein warranting haematological evaluation for myeloma or related dyscrasia","Restriction of chest wall movement or dysphagia from extensive induration","Rapidly progressive or widespread skin involvement","Features suggesting an alternative systemic sclerosing disease"],"prognosis":"The post-infectious form often improves spontaneously over months to a couple of years, whereas the diabetes-associated and paraproteinaemic forms tend to be chronic and refractory; overall prognosis depends on any underlying disorder.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for systemic sclerosis and missing that the fingers are spared without Raynaud phenomenon","Failing to screen for a monoclonal gammopathy that may herald a plasma-cell disorder","Overlooking poorly controlled diabetes as the underlying association","Interpreting the non-pitting swelling as simple oedema"],"management":["Treat the underlying association — optimise glycaemic control in scleredema diabeticorum, treat streptococcal or viral infection in the post-infectious form, and manage any monoclonal gammopathy","Recognise there is no reliably effective specific therapy, with phototherapy such as UVA1 or PUVA offering variable benefit for skin induration","Consider immunomodulatory approaches such as methotrexate, systemic corticosteroids or intravenous immunoglobulin for progressive or disabling disease","In the paraproteinaemic form direct treatment at a clinically significant plasma-cell dyscrasia with haematology input","Provide physiotherapy to preserve neck and trunk mobility restricted by the woody induration","Distinguish from systemic sclerosis to avoid inappropriate treatment, given sparing of the fingers and absence of Raynaud phenomenon","Monitor the post-infectious form for spontaneous resolution and follow chronic diabetes- or paraprotein-associated disease long term"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"sclerema_neonatorum":{"name":"sclerema neonatorum","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sclerema neonatorum","sclerema adiposum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Weedon's Skin Pathology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, diffuse hardening of the skin and subcutaneous fat affecting critically ill, usually premature or low-birth-weight neonates in the first week of life.","The skin becomes cold, board-like, waxy and immobile, spreading rapidly from the buttocks and lower limbs to involve most of the body, sparing the palms, soles and genitalia.","It is almost always a marker of severe underlying illness such as sepsis, dehydration, hypothermia, hypoxia or congenital malformation, rather than a primary skin disease.","Unlike subcutaneous fat necrosis of the newborn, affected infants are gravely unwell and the induration is diffuse rather than nodular.","The rigid skin restricts respiration and feeding, compounding the underlying condition.","Histology shows widened fibrous septa and needle-shaped clefts within enlarged adipocytes, with little or no inflammatory infiltrate and minimal fat necrosis.","Management is directed at the underlying illness, aggressive supportive care, warming, correction of fluid and electrolyte disturbances and treatment of sepsis.","Mortality is high and correlates with the severity of the precipitating illness rather than the skin changes themselves."],"pathophysiology":"Sclerema neonatorum reflects the physicochemical vulnerability of neonatal subcutaneous fat, which has a high ratio of saturated to unsaturated fatty acids and thus a high melting point. In a critically ill, hypothermic and hypoperfused infant, this fat solidifies and crystallises, producing diffuse induration. Enzymatic defects in fatty-acid mobilisation and impaired thermoregulation are thought to contribute. Histologically there is little inflammation or true necrosis; instead adipocytes enlarge and contain needle-shaped crystal clefts, and the fibrous septa thicken. The process is best understood as a physical solidification of fat in a decompensating infant, so the condition is essentially an epiphenomenon of severe systemic illness.","additionalDifferentials":["subcutaneous fat necrosis of the newborn","neonatal scleroderma / stiff skin syndromes","neonatal sepsis with oedema and induration","cold injury / neonatal cold syndrome","diffuse neonatal oedema (hydrops, hypoproteinaemia)","congenital ichthyosis with taut skin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["full septic screen including blood cultures, lumbar puncture and cultures as indicated","full blood count, C-reactive protein and coagulation studies","serum electrolytes, glucose, urea and creatinine","blood gas analysis for hypoxia and acidosis","skin biopsy for histopathology when diagnosis is uncertain (wide septa, crystal clefts, scant inflammation)","core temperature monitoring","chest radiograph and other imaging directed at the underlying illness"],"redFlags":["rapidly spreading induration in a hypothermic, hypotensive or septic neonate","respiratory compromise from restricted chest-wall movement","haemodynamic instability and multi-organ dysfunction","severe metabolic acidosis or coagulopathy","inability to feed or maintain temperature despite supportive measures"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is poor, with high mortality driven by the severity of the underlying illness; survival depends on prompt and effective treatment of the precipitating condition rather than of the skin changes.","pitfalls":["Regarding the skin hardening as the primary problem instead of a sign of critical systemic illness.","Confusing it with the far more benign subcutaneous fat necrosis of the newborn.","Delaying sepsis evaluation and empirical treatment while investigating the skin.","Underestimating the respiratory impact of diffuse cutaneous rigidity.","Attributing induration to primary scleroderma in a neonate."],"management":["Treat the underlying critical illness aggressively — sepsis, dehydration, hypothermia and hypoxia — since outcome depends on this rather than on the skin","Give broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics for suspected sepsis after taking cultures, guided by the septic screen","Provide intensive supportive care with careful rewarming, correction of fluid and electrolyte disturbance and haemodynamic support in a neonatal intensive care setting","Support respiration, including mechanical ventilation, as the rigid skin restricts chest movement","Maintain nutrition and correct hypoglycaemia and acidosis","Monitor core temperature, blood gases and coagulation, treating disseminated intravascular coagulation as needed","Involve neonatology early and discuss the guarded prognosis with the family given the high mortality"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Weedon's Skin Pathology, current edition","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Textbook of Neonatal Dermatology, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"scleritis":{"name":"Scleritis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anterior scleritis","posterior scleritis","necrotising scleritis","scleromalacia perforans"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with severe, deep, boring ocular pain that is often worse at night, may radiate to the face or jaw, and characteristically disturbs sleep","The redness has a bluish or violaceous hue from involvement of the deep scleral vessels, unlike the bright red of episcleritis","Scleral vessels do not blanch with topical phenylephrine, which helps distinguish scleritis from episcleritis","Classified into anterior (diffuse, nodular, or necrotising) and posterior forms; necrotising scleritis is the most destructive and sight-threatening","Scleromalacia perforans is a painless necrotising form seen in long-standing rheumatoid arthritis","Roughly half of cases are associated with systemic disease, especially rheumatoid arthritis and ANCA-associated vasculitis such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Posterior scleritis can mimic other conditions with proptosis, pain on eye movement, and disc swelling, and is confirmed on ultrasound (T-sign)","Management is with systemic anti-inflammatory therapy: oral NSAIDs for milder disease and systemic corticosteroids or immunosuppression for necrotising or refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Scleritis is inflammation of the sclera, frequently immune-mediated and often a manifestation of systemic autoimmune or vasculitic disease. Immune-complex deposition and T-cell-mediated inflammation cause oedema and cellular infiltration of the sclera and episclera; in necrotising disease, occlusive vasculitis of the deep episcleral vessels leads to scleral ischaemia, necrosis, and thinning, with risk of perforation. Chronic or severe inflammation can extend to adjacent structures causing keratitis, uveitis, glaucoma, and, in posterior disease, exudative retinal detachment and optic nerve involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Episcleritis","Anterior uveitis","Conjunctivitis","Orbital cellulitis or orbital inflammatory disease (posterior scleritis)","Infective (e.g. herpetic or pseudomonal) scleritis","Choroidal tumour or exudative retinal detachment (posterior scleritis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination with assessment of deep vessel involvement and response to topical phenylephrine","B-scan ultrasonography for posterior scleritis (scleral thickening and T-sign)","Inflammatory and autoimmune workup: full blood count, ESR/CRP, ANA, ANCA, rheumatoid factor","Investigations for infective causes (including syphilis and tuberculosis) when indicated","Imaging or systemic referral when an underlying vasculitis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Necrotising scleritis with scleral thinning or threatened perforation","Severe pain with reduced vision indicating posterior segment or optic nerve involvement","Features of systemic vasculitis (e.g. granulomatosis with polyangiitis) requiring urgent systemic treatment","Rapid progression despite therapy suggesting infective scleritis or aggressive vasculitis"],"prognosis":"Diffuse and nodular anterior scleritis usually respond to anti-inflammatory treatment with good visual outcomes, but necrotising scleritis is associated with significant ocular morbidity and, when linked to systemic vasculitis, increased mortality if the underlying disease is untreated.","pitfalls":["Mistaking scleritis for the benign, self-limiting episcleritis and undertreating it","Failing to search for and treat an underlying systemic vasculitis or connective-tissue disease","Missing posterior scleritis, which lacks obvious external redness and mimics orbital disease","Using topical steroids alone when systemic immunosuppression is required for necrotising disease"],"management":["Treat non-necrotising anterior scleritis first-line with systemic NSAIDs","Escalate to systemic corticosteroids for NSAID-refractory, necrotising or posterior disease","Add steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as methotrexate, mycophenolate or ciclosporin, or a biologic such as rituximab or a TNF inhibitor, for severe, necrotising or systemic-disease-associated scleritis","Investigate and treat any underlying systemic disease such as rheumatoid arthritis or ANCA-associated vasculitis including granulomatosis with polyangiitis, jointly with rheumatology","Exclude and treat infective scleritis, including tuberculosis and syphilis, with appropriate antimicrobials and withhold immunosuppression in that setting","Manage complications such as uveitis, glaucoma and keratitis, and consider scleral patch grafting for impending perforation","Coordinate ophthalmology and rheumatology follow-up, as untreated necrotising disease linked to vasculitis carries increased mortality"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"sclerocornea":{"name":"Sclerocornea","system":"Eye / Anterior segment","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["corneal opacification with scleralisation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric ophthalmology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital non-progressive condition in which peripheral or entire cornea resembles sclera, with loss of the normal limbal boundary","The cornea is opaque and vascularised peripherally, and the corneo-scleral junction is indistinct","It ranges from mild peripheral scleralisation to total sclerocornea with a completely opaque cornea","It is frequently bilateral and associated with other anterior segment dysgenesis and systemic malformations","Total sclerocornea causes severe visual deprivation and amblyopia","Management ranges from observation of mild cases to corneal transplantation for total involvement, with guarded results"],"pathophysiology":"Sclerocornea is a developmental anomaly of the anterior segment in which the cornea fails to differentiate distinctly from the adjacent sclera, so scleral-like opaque, vascularised tissue extends into the cornea and the limbus is ill-defined. It reflects abnormal neural crest-derived anterior segment development and often coexists with other dysgenetic features. The corneal opacification obstructs the visual axis in severe cases, causing form-deprivation amblyopia, while the abnormal anterior segment can predispose to glaucoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Peters anomaly","Congenital hereditary endothelial dystrophy","Congenital glaucoma with corneal clouding","Birth trauma with Descemet tears","Mucopolysaccharidoses and metabolic corneal clouding","Limbal dermoid"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination of corneal clarity and the limbal boundary","Anterior segment imaging to define the extent and associated anomalies","Intraocular pressure assessment for associated glaucoma","Systemic evaluation for associated malformations","Amblyopia and visual development assessment"],"redFlags":["Total corneal opacification threatening visual development","Associated glaucoma","Bilateral severe involvement","Systemic malformations suggesting a syndrome"],"prognosis":"Mild peripheral sclerocornea has little visual impact, whereas total sclerocornea carries a poor visual prognosis even with corneal transplantation because of associated dysgenesis and amblyopia.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with other causes of congenital corneal opacity","Overlooking associated glaucoma","Underestimating amblyopia risk","Missing systemic associations"],"management":["Observe mild peripheral sclerocornea where the visual axis remains clear, with regular monitoring of corneal clarity and visual development","Correct any refractive error and institute aggressive amblyopia therapy with occlusion to counter form-deprivation amblyopia","Consider penetrating keratoplasty (corneal transplantation) for total sclerocornea obstructing the visual axis, counselling parents on guarded graft outcomes","Screen for and treat associated glaucoma, lowering intraocular pressure with topical antiglaucoma agents or surgery","Undertake systemic evaluation for coexisting anterior segment dysgenesis and extraocular malformations","Refer to paediatric ophthalmology and low-vision services, and offer genetic counselling given the developmental anterior segment associations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"scleroderma_renal_crisis":{"name":"Scleroderma renal crisis","system":"Rheumatology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SRC","systemic sclerosis renal crisis","scleroderma kidney","anti-RNA polymerase III"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology / EULAR systemic sclerosis guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with abrupt severe (often malignant) hypertension and rapidly rising creatinine, usually in early diffuse systemic sclerosis","Anti-RNA polymerase III antibodies and recent glucocorticoid exposure are important risk factors","A microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia with thrombocytopenia frequently accompanies the renal injury","Prompt angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibition is the cornerstone of treatment and can rescue kidney function even after dialysis is started","Headache, visual disturbance, encephalopathy, or acute pulmonary oedema may reflect the hypertensive emergency","A minority of cases are normotensive, which can delay recognition and worsen outcome","Glucocorticoids should be used cautiously and at the lowest effective exposure in at-risk patients because they may precipitate crisis"],"pathophysiology":"Scleroderma renal crisis arises from obliterative vasculopathy of the renal arcuate and interlobular arteries, with intimal proliferation and luminal narrowing reducing renal blood flow. Consequent activation of the renin-angiotensin system produces severe hypertension, which further injures the vessel walls in a self-amplifying cycle. Endothelial damage in the small vessels drives a thrombotic microangiopathy with mechanical fragmentation of red cells and platelet consumption. Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibition interrupts the renin-angiotensin drive and is therefore central to management.","additionalDifferentials":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura or haemolytic uraemic syndrome","Malignant hypertension of other cause","Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody-associated vasculitis with renal involvement","Drug-induced thrombotic microangiopathy","Antiphospholipid syndrome with renal involvement","Acute glomerulonephritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial blood pressure measurement and renal function monitoring","Blood film for schistocytes with haptoglobin, lactate dehydrogenase, and platelet count","Urinalysis for proteinuria and haematuria","Anti-RNA polymerase III and systemic sclerosis autoantibody profiling","ADAMTS13 activity to help exclude thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Renal biopsy in selected diagnostically uncertain cases"],"redFlags":["Accelerated or malignant hypertension with encephalopathy or seizures","Rapidly rising creatinine with oligoanuria","Flash pulmonary oedema from hypertensive heart failure","Falling platelets with schistocytes signalling thrombotic microangiopathy"],"prognosis":"Before the era of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibition renal crisis was almost uniformly fatal; timely blood pressure control now allows many patients to recover renal function, though some remain dialysis-dependent and overall mortality stays significant.","pitfalls":["Withholding or stopping angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibition because creatinine rises initially","Missing a normotensive presentation and attributing renal failure to another cause","Confusing the microangiopathy with primary thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura and giving inappropriate plasma exchange alone","Using unnecessary glucocorticoids in high-risk early diffuse disease"],"management":["Start an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor promptly as the cornerstone, titrating to control blood pressure even if renal function transiently worsens","Add further antihypertensives such as calcium-channel blockers or other vasodilators if blood pressure remains uncontrolled","Withdraw or minimise glucocorticoids, which can precipitate crisis, and avoid nephrotoxic agents","Provide renal replacement therapy for severe acute kidney injury while continuing ACE inhibition, since recovery may still allow dialysis independence","Manage hypertensive-emergency complications such as encephalopathy and acute pulmonary oedema with intensive monitoring and supportive care","Monitor blood pressure, renal function, and the accompanying microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia and thrombocytopenia serially","Liaise closely with rheumatology and nephrology, and assess for kidney transplantation in those who remain dialysis-dependent"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology","EULAR recommendations for the treatment of systemic sclerosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"scleroedema":{"name":"scleroedema","system":"Skin / Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scleroedema of Buschke","scleroedema adultorum","scleroedema diabeticorum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Bolognia Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mucinosis characterised by diffuse, symmetrical, non-pitting woody induration of the skin, typically beginning on the posterior neck, upper back and shoulders","Three clinical types are recognised: a post-infectious form (often after streptococcal or viral upper respiratory infection) that tends to resolve, a form associated with monoclonal gammopathy, and scleroedema diabeticorum linked to long-standing insulin-dependent diabetes","The skin over the upper trunk becomes thickened and bound down, sometimes with an orange-peel appearance and reduced ability to pinch or wrinkle the skin","The hands and feet are characteristically spared, helping to distinguish it from systemic sclerosis, and Raynaud phenomenon and sclerodactyly are absent","Extensive disease can restrict movement of the neck, back and chest wall, and rarely involves the tongue, pharynx or heart","Histology shows a thickened reticular dermis with collagen bundles separated by mucin, producing characteristic fenestration, best demonstrated with colloidal iron or Alcian blue","Treatment is often unsatisfactory; phototherapy (particularly UVA1 or PUVA), optimisation of glycaemic control and physiotherapy are used, and the post-infectious form may resolve spontaneously"],"pathophysiology":"Scleroedema is a primary cutaneous mucinosis in which fibroblasts deposit excess collagen and glycosaminoglycans, chiefly hyaluronic acid, throughout a markedly thickened reticular dermis. In the post-infectious form, an immune response to preceding infection is thought to stimulate fibroblast activity transiently. In the diabetes-associated form, chronic hyperglycaemia is believed to promote non-enzymatic glycation of collagen, increase resistance to degradation and stimulate mucin production, accounting for its persistence. The paraproteinaemic form is associated with a monoclonal gammopathy that, as in scleromyxoedema, may drive fibroblast stimulation, although the mechanism is incompletely defined.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic sclerosis and morphoea","Scleromyxoedema","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Eosinophilic fasciitis","Myxoedema of hypothyroidism","Chronic sclerodermatous graft-versus-host disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy from involved back or neck showing dermal thickening with mucin between collagen bundles","Fasting glucose and glycated haemoglobin to assess for diabetes","Serum protein electrophoresis and immunofixation to detect a paraprotein","Antistreptolysin O titre and infection history in the post-infectious form","Thyroid function tests to exclude myxoedema","Assessment of chest wall and joint mobility where induration is extensive"],"redFlags":["Restriction of chest wall movement causing respiratory compromise","Cardiac involvement in extensive disease","Dysphagia or airway symptoms from pharyngeal or tongue involvement","Rapidly progressive induration or associated systemic symptoms prompting reassessment"],"prognosis":"The post-infectious form usually resolves over months to a couple of years, whereas the diabetes-associated and paraprotein-related forms tend to be chronic and persistent. Serious systemic complications are uncommon, and prognosis is largely determined by the underlying association and the extent of functional restriction.","pitfalls":["Confusing scleroedema with systemic sclerosis, despite the sparing of the hands and absence of Raynaud phenomenon","Overlooking an underlying monoclonal gammopathy requiring monitoring","Failing to distinguish the self-limiting post-infectious form from the persistent diabetic form when advising on prognosis","Taking a superficial biopsy that misses the deep dermal mucin and collagen changes","Neglecting glycaemic optimisation and physiotherapy in the diabetic form"],"management":["Treat the underlying association: optimise glycaemic control in scleroedema diabeticorum and address any monoclonal gammopathy","Manage the post-infectious form expectantly as it usually resolves spontaneously, treating any residual streptococcal infection","Offer physiotherapy and stretching to preserve neck, back, and chest-wall mobility where induration is extensive","Consider phototherapy (PUVA or UVA1) or immunomodulation such as methotrexate, systemic corticosteroids, or intravenous immunoglobulin for progressive or disabling disease","Monitor for restrictive complications including chest-wall, cardiac, tongue, and pharyngeal involvement","Refer to dermatology, with haematology input where a paraprotein is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia, Dermatology"],"crossLinks":["scleromyxoedema","cutaneous_mucinosis","nephrogenic_systemic_fibrosis"]},"scleromalacia_perforans":{"name":"Scleromalacia perforans","system":"Ophthalmic / ocular surface","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["necrotising scleritis without inflammation","anterior necrotising scleritis without inflammation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Scleromalacia perforans is a form of anterior necrotising scleritis occurring characteristically WITHOUT inflammation and WITHOUT pain, which is why it is so easily overlooked despite being sight- and globe-threatening.","It is strongly associated with long-standing seropositive rheumatoid arthritis, typically in older women with severe deforming disease, and is regarded as a marker of systemic vasculitic activity.","The hallmark is progressive scleral thinning with the appearance of yellow-grey necrotic plaques and increasingly visible bluish underlying uvea; despite the name, actual perforation is uncommon unless trauma or raised intraocular pressure supervenes.","Because it is painless and quiet, patients may present late with an incidental blue-grey patch or with astigmatic visual change rather than with a red painful eye.","Management is fundamentally systemic: aggressive immunosuppression, frequently with corticosteroids plus a steroid-sparing agent or a biologic, in close liaison with rheumatology, because the eye disease reflects underlying systemic vasculitis with excess mortality.","Topical therapy is largely supportive; ocular lubrication and protection are used but do not alter the necrotising process.","Surgery is avoided where possible because the necrotic sclera heals poorly; scleral or tectonic patch grafting is reserved for impending or actual perforation.","New-onset necrotising scleritis mandates a work-up for systemic vasculitis and exclusion of infection, as an underlying connective-tissue or ANCA-associated disorder frequently coexists and dictates prognosis."],"pathophysiology":"The lesion is driven by immune-complex-mediated occlusive microvasculitis of the deep episcleral and scleral vessels, most often in the setting of long-standing rheumatoid arthritis. Vascular occlusion produces ischaemia of the avascular scleral collagen, and infiltrating macrophages and matrix metalloproteinases degrade the scleral matrix, causing progressive necrosis and thinning. Uniquely, the classic cellular inflammatory response is muted or absent, so the eye remains white and painless while the sclera melts away, exposing the underlying dark uvea. Continued matrix breakdown risks staphylomatous ectasia and, rarely, perforation.","additionalDifferentials":["Necrotising scleritis with inflammation (painful, injected)","Diffuse or nodular anterior scleritis","Infective (e.g. pseudomonal or fungal) scleritis and surgically induced necrotising scleritis","Scleral thinning from prior surgery or trauma","Senile scleral hyaline plaques","Choroidal or ciliary body melanoma showing through thin sclera","Blue sclera of osteogenesis imperfecta or other collagen disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination with red-free light to map scleral thinning and avascular necrotic areas","Rheumatoid factor, anti-CCP antibodies and inflammatory markers","ANCA, antinuclear antibody and complement to screen for systemic vasculitis","Anterior-segment optical coherence tomography or high-frequency ultrasound to quantify residual scleral thickness","Screening for infection when a necrotising process could be microbial, including scrape and culture","Systemic vasculitis and infection screen (chest imaging, urinalysis, renal function) in liaison with rheumatology"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive scleral thinning with staphyloma formation or impending perforation","Exposure of uvea through frankly necrotic sclera","Systemic features of active vasculitis (renal, pulmonary or neurological involvement)","Signs suggesting infective scleritis, which mimics and requires urgent culture-directed antimicrobials","Any globe rupture following minor trauma to markedly thinned sclera"],"prognosis":"Ocular prognosis is guarded and depends on early control of the underlying systemic disease; untreated it can progress to staphyloma or perforation. Its appearance signals severe systemic vasculitis and is associated with increased mortality, so outcome is tied to systemic rather than purely ocular management.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a white, painless eye as benign and missing sight-threatening necrotising scleritis","Treating the eye in isolation without instituting or escalating systemic immunosuppression","Failing to exclude infective scleritis before committing to immunosuppression","Undertaking scleral surgery on actively necrotic tissue, precipitating further melt (surgically induced necrotising scleritis)","Overlooking the associated excess systemic mortality and not co-managing with rheumatology"],"management":["Institute systemic immunosuppression as the mainstay, typically systemic corticosteroids with a steroid-sparing agent or a biologic such as rituximab or a TNF inhibitor, directed at the underlying vasculitis","Treat the underlying rheumatoid disease in close liaison with rheumatology, since the eye reflects systemic vasculitic activity","Provide supportive ocular care with lubricants and eye protection, recognising topical therapy does not halt the necrotising process","Protect the thinned globe from trauma and raised intraocular pressure, and consider scleral or tectonic patch grafting for impending perforation","Monitor residual scleral thickness and screen for systemic vasculitis and the associated excess mortality","Coordinate multidisciplinary rheumatology and ophthalmology follow-up, addressing cardiovascular and systemic risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course — External Disease and Cornea","Watson and Hazleman, The Sclera and Systemic Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"scleromyxedema":{"name":"Scleromyxoedema","system":"Rheumatology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scleromyxedema","generalised lichen myxoedematosus","Arndt-Gottron syndrome","papular mucinosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare cutaneous mucinosis presenting with widespread firm, waxy papules and induration, often in a linear arrangement over the trunk and limbs","Involvement of the glabella and ears with deep longitudinal furrowing can produce a leonine facies, and the 'Shar-Pei sign' describes redundant folds","Almost always associated with a monoclonal gammopathy, characteristically an IgG lambda paraprotein","Despite the paraprotein, there is usually no overt myeloma, and the skin disease dominates the picture","Systemic involvement can affect the central nervous system (the dermato-neuro syndrome), heart, oesophagus, and muscles, occasionally with fatal outcomes","Histology shows dermal mucin deposition, fibroblast proliferation, and fibrosis, distinguishing it from systemic sclerosis","Intravenous immunoglobulin is a mainstay of treatment, with other systemic immunomodulatory options for resistant disease"],"pathophysiology":"Scleromyxoedema is a generalised form of lichen myxoedematosus characterised by deposition of mucin (glycosaminoglycans) in the dermis together with proliferation of fibroblasts and increased collagen. It is almost invariably associated with a monoclonal gammopathy, usually IgG lambda, though the paraprotein's precise pathogenic role is unclear and disease activity does not track paraprotein levels. Circulating factors are thought to stimulate dermal fibroblasts to overproduce mucin and collagen, and the process may extend to internal organs, producing systemic and, in the dermato-neuro syndrome, central neurological complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Scleroedema (of Buschke)","Eosinophilic fasciitis","Localised lichen myxoedematosus","Amyloidosis with cutaneous deposition"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing dermal mucin deposition, fibroblast proliferation, and fibrosis","Serum protein electrophoresis and immunofixation to detect the monoclonal paraprotein","Serum free light chains and quantitative immunoglobulins","Bone marrow examination to assess the plasma cell clone and exclude myeloma","Investigations for systemic involvement (cardiac, oesophageal, neurological) as clinically indicated","Thyroid function tests to exclude mimicking causes of skin thickening"],"redFlags":["Acute encephalopathy, seizures, or coma suggesting the dermato-neuro syndrome","Cardiac or oesophageal involvement causing functional compromise","Progression of the paraprotein towards multiple myeloma","Rapidly progressive skin induration limiting mobility"],"prognosis":"Scleromyxoedema is a chronic, often progressive disorder; skin disease may respond to intravenous immunoglobulin and immunomodulation, but systemic and central nervous system involvement, particularly the dermato-neuro syndrome, can be life-threatening.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for systemic sclerosis and missing the associated paraprotein","Failing to perform serum protein electrophoresis and immunofixation in a patient with unexplained waxy skin induration","Overlooking systemic and neurological involvement","Assuming absence of myeloma excludes significant disease","Tracking treatment response by paraprotein level rather than clinical activity"],"management":["Use intravenous immunoglobulin as first-line and the mainstay for both cutaneous and systemic disease","Escalate to plasma-cell-directed immunomodulation such as systemic corticosteroids, thalidomide or lenalidomide, or bortezomib-based regimens for refractory disease","Treat the dermato-neuro syndrome as an emergency with intravenous immunoglobulin, corticosteroids, and plasma exchange","Monitor for systemic involvement of the central nervous system, heart, oesophagus, and muscles","Involve haematology to characterise the plasma-cell dyscrasia and exclude myeloma, noting disease activity does not track paraprotein levels","Provide supportive skin care and physiotherapy for induration and reduced mobility","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across rheumatology, dermatology, haematology, and neurology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"scleromyxoedema":{"name":"scleromyxoedema","system":"Skin / Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["generalised lichen myxoedematosus","Arndt-Gottron syndrome","papular mucinosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Bolognia Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The generalised, sclerodermoid form of lichen myxoedematosus, characterised by widespread firm waxy papules, dermal mucin deposition, fibroblast proliferation and an associated monoclonal gammopathy","Numerous closely set, dome-shaped papules are typically arranged in a linear array over the face, ears, neck, trunk and hands, with progressive skin thickening and induration","Characteristic signs include the doughnut sign (a central depression surrounded by a raised rim over the proximal interphalangeal joints), deep glabellar furrows producing leonine facies, and reduced mouth opening","A monoclonal paraprotein, most often IgG with lambda light chains, is present in the large majority, although progression to overt myeloma is uncommon","Systemic involvement may affect the gastrointestinal tract (dysphagia, dysmotility), muscles (proximal myopathy), heart, kidneys and nervous system","The dermato-neuro syndrome, an abrupt encephalopathy with fever, seizures and coma, is a rare but life-threatening complication requiring emergency treatment","Intravenous immunoglobulin is generally first-line therapy, with options such as thalidomide or lenalidomide, systemic corticosteroids, bortezomib and autologous stem-cell transplantation for refractory or systemic disease"],"pathophysiology":"Scleromyxoedema is a primary cutaneous mucinosis in which fibroblasts, apparently stimulated by circulating factors, proliferate and deposit excess glycosaminoglycans (chiefly hyaluronic acid) together with increased collagen in the upper and mid dermis. A monoclonal gammopathy is almost always present, and the paraprotein or associated cytokines are thought to drive fibroblast activation and mucin production, though the precise mechanism linking the two is not fully understood. The resulting combination of dermal mucin, fibroblast proliferation and fibrosis accounts for the papular and sclerodermoid skin changes, while similar processes in other organs underlie the systemic and neurological complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic sclerosis and morphoea","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Scleroedema","Localised forms of lichen myxoedematosus without paraproteinaemia","Scleromyxoedema-like changes of the reticular erythematous mucinosis or lupus spectrum","Amyloidosis with cutaneous infiltration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing dermal mucin deposition, fibroblast proliferation and fibrosis","Serum protein electrophoresis and immunofixation to detect and characterise the paraprotein","Serum free light chains and quantitative immunoglobulins","Bone marrow examination to assess for a plasma cell dyscrasia","Colloidal iron or Alcian blue staining of biopsy to confirm mucin","Screening for systemic involvement with cardiac, oesophageal, muscle and renal assessment as indicated","Neuroimaging and lumbar puncture if dermato-neuro syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["Abrupt fever, confusion, seizures or coma indicating dermato-neuro syndrome","Progressive dysphagia or aspiration from oesophageal dysmotility","Cardiac or renal involvement suggesting extensive systemic disease","Evidence of transformation to multiple myeloma","Severe proximal weakness compromising mobility or respiration"],"prognosis":"Scleromyxoedema is a chronic, unpredictable and potentially progressive disorder that can be complicated by fatal systemic and neurological involvement. Response to intravenous immunoglobulin and other therapies has improved outcomes, but relapses are common and the dermato-neuro syndrome carries a high mortality.","pitfalls":["Failing to search for and monitor the associated monoclonal gammopathy","Mistaking the condition for systemic sclerosis and missing the distinctive papular and doughnut-sign features","Not recognising dermato-neuro syndrome, a treatable but rapidly fatal emergency, as part of the disease","Overlooking systemic organ involvement while focusing on the skin","Assuming a normal bone marrow excludes significant paraprotein-related disease"],"management":["Give intravenous immunoglobulin as the treatment of choice for the cutaneous and systemic manifestations","Escalate to plasma-cell-directed therapy such as systemic corticosteroids, thalidomide or lenalidomide, or bortezomib with autologous stem-cell transplantation in resistant cases","Manage the dermato-neuro syndrome of encephalopathy, seizures, and coma as a life-threatening emergency with intravenous immunoglobulin, corticosteroids, and plasma exchange","Provide supportive care for dysphagia and dysmotility, proximal myopathy, and reduced mouth opening","Monitor for gastrointestinal, muscular, cardiac, renal, and neurological involvement","Refer to haematology to characterise the IgG-lambda paraprotein and exclude progression to myeloma","Coordinate multidisciplinary dermatology, haematology, and neurology follow-up given the relapsing, unpredictable course"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia, Dermatology"],"crossLinks":["scleroedema","cutaneous_mucinosis","nephrogenic_systemic_fibrosis"]},"sclerosing_adenosis":{"name":"Sclerosing adenosis","system":"Breast","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sclerosing adenosis","breast sclerosing adenosis","adenosis of the breast"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard breast surgery textbook and breast pathology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sclerosing adenosis is a benign proliferative lesion of the terminal duct lobular unit that is a recognised mimic of malignancy clinically, radiologically, and histologically","It is frequently an incidental microscopic finding but can form a palpable lump or produce a mammographic abnormality, often clustered microcalcification","It falls within the spectrum of fibrocystic and proliferative breast change and may coexist with other benign lesions such as radial scars and papillomas","The distorted proliferation of glands within fibrous stroma can imitate invasive carcinoma on imaging and on core biopsy, making expert pathological interpretation essential","Triple assessment with core biopsy is used to distinguish it from cancer, and immunohistochemistry demonstrating an intact myoepithelial layer helps confirm its benign nature","When the lesion causes suspicious calcification or a mass, or when biopsy shows associated atypia, diagnostic excision may be required","Uncomplicated sclerosing adenosis needs no treatment beyond reassurance once malignancy is excluded","It is associated with a small increase in future breast cancer risk, greater when accompanied by atypical hyperplasia"],"pathophysiology":"Sclerosing adenosis is characterised by an increase in the number of acini within the lobule (adenosis) accompanied by proliferation of fibrous stroma that compresses and distorts the proliferating glands. This combination of glandular proliferation and stromal sclerosis produces a swirling, infiltrative-looking architecture that can closely resemble invasive carcinoma, and dystrophic calcification within the lesion often generates suspicious microcalcification on mammography. The key feature confirming benignity is preservation of the outer myoepithelial cell layer around the distorted glands, which is retained in adenosis but lost in invasive cancer, and which can be highlighted with immunohistochemical markers.","additionalDifferentials":["Invasive breast carcinoma","Ductal carcinoma in situ","Radial scar or complex sclerosing lesion","Fibroadenoma","Fibrocystic change","Tubular carcinoma (an important histological mimic)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination as part of triple assessment when a lump is present","Mammography, which may reveal clustered microcalcification or a distortion","Ultrasound to characterise any mass","Core needle biopsy with expert histological review","Immunohistochemistry for myoepithelial markers to confirm an intact myoepithelial layer","Diagnostic excision where imaging is suspicious or atypia is found on biopsy"],"redFlags":["Suspicious microcalcification or architectural distortion on imaging","Histological features overlapping with tubular carcinoma or associated atypia","A discrete or enlarging palpable mass","Discordant triple assessment findings","Coexisting atypical hyperplasia, which raises future cancer risk"],"prognosis":"Sclerosing adenosis is benign with a good prognosis once malignancy is excluded, requiring no treatment in most cases, though it confers a modest increase in future breast cancer risk that is greater if atypical hyperplasia is also present.","pitfalls":["Overcalling the lesion as invasive carcinoma on imaging or biopsy owing to its infiltrative appearance","Failing to use myoepithelial markers to distinguish it from cancer","Missing coexisting atypia or malignancy in an adjacent radial scar or papilloma","Ignoring discordance between suspicious imaging and a benign biopsy","Underestimating the associated future cancer risk when atypia is present"],"management":["Confirm benignity through triple assessment with core biopsy and myoepithelial immunohistochemistry, then reassure, as the lesion itself needs no treatment","Undertake diagnostic excision, for example vacuum-assisted or surgical, where imaging is suspicious, findings are discordant, or biopsy shows associated atypia","Manage coexisting proliferative lesions such as radial scars or papillomas on their own merits","Counsel on the modest increase in future breast-cancer risk, which is greater when atypical hyperplasia coexists","Enrol in appropriate mammographic surveillance according to individualised risk","Refer to a breast unit for expert pathological review given its close mimicry of carcinoma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard breast pathology reference text on proliferative breast disease","Breast surgery guidance on benign proliferative lesions"],"crossLinks":[]},"sclerosing_stromal_tumor_ovary":{"name":"Sclerosing stromal tumour of the ovary","system":"Gynaecology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ovarian sclerosing stromal tumour","sclerosing stromal tumor"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare benign sex cord-stromal tumour that characteristically arises in adolescents and women in their second and third decades","Almost always unilateral and benign, presenting with menstrual irregularity, pelvic pain, or an incidentally detected adnexal mass","Can be hormonally active and oestrogenic, and cases arising in pregnancy may present with more pronounced hormonal effects","Histology shows a distinctive pseudolobular architecture, with cellular nodules separated by oedematous or densely sclerotic hypocellular tissue","A prominent, often haemangiopericytoma-like vascular network and admixed spindle and rounded (sometimes signet-ring-like) cells are typical","Imaging frequently shows early peripheral enhancement with centripetal fill-in, reflecting the rich vascularity","Cured by conservative surgery, and fertility-sparing cystectomy or oophorectomy is usually appropriate given the young age group"],"pathophysiology":"Sclerosing stromal tumour is a benign ovarian sex cord-stromal neoplasm characterised by a pseudolobular growth pattern in which cellular areas alternate with hypocellular, oedematous, or collagenised sclerotic zones. The cellular regions contain a mixture of spindled fibroblast-like cells and rounded cells with vacuolated, lipid-laden cytoplasm, and a conspicuous thin-walled branching vasculature reminiscent of a haemangiopericytoma. Some of the vacuolated cells can produce steroids, which accounts for the oestrogenic manifestations seen in a proportion of patients, particularly during pregnancy when hormonal activity may be accentuated. Despite a sometimes alarming cellularity and rich vascular pattern, the tumour behaves in a uniformly benign fashion.","additionalDifferentials":["Ovarian fibroma or thecoma","Granulosa cell tumour","Signet-ring stromal tumour","Krukenberg (metastatic signet-ring) tumour","Massive ovarian oedema","Other sex cord-stromal tumours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound to characterise a solid or mixed adnexal mass and its vascularity","Pelvic MRI showing early peripheral enhancement with centripetal progression","Hormone profile (including oestrogen) if there are oestrogenic manifestations","Tumour markers such as CA125 mainly to help exclude malignancy","Histology with immunohistochemistry (e.g. inhibin, calretinin) to confirm the sex cord-stromal nature","Endometrial assessment if unopposed oestrogen effects are suspected"],"redFlags":["Menstrual disturbance or oestrogenic symptoms with an adnexal mass requiring endometrial evaluation","Acute pain suggesting torsion of the mass","Imaging or histological features raising doubt about a malignant stromal tumour","A signet-ring-like appearance prompting exclusion of metastatic Krukenberg tumour"],"prognosis":"Excellent, as the tumour is benign and cured by simple excision, with hormonal effects resolving after removal and no established tendency to recur or metastasise.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the cellular, vascular histology for a malignant tumour and overtreating a young patient","Confusing signet-ring-like cells with metastatic Krukenberg tumour","Overlooking oestrogenic effects on the endometrium","Performing radical rather than fertility-sparing surgery in a young woman"],"management":["Perform ovary-sparing cystectomy or unilateral oophorectomy, which is curative for this benign unilateral tumour","Favour fertility-preserving surgery given the typical presentation in adolescents and young women","Confirm the diagnosis histologically with inhibin and calretinin immunohistochemistry to establish the sex cord-stromal nature and exclude malignancy","Expect oestrogenic manifestations and menstrual irregularity to resolve after removal","Assess and manage the endometrium where an unopposed oestrogen effect is suspected","Provide reassurance and routine follow-up as recurrence and metastasis are not expected, involving gynaecological oncology for surgical planning in pregnancy-associated cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","Blaustein's Pathology of the Female Genital Tract"],"crossLinks":[]},"sclerosteosis":{"name":"Sclerosteosis (SOST)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SOST-related sclerosteosis","cortical hyperostosis with syndactyly","sclerostin deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism (ASBMR)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive sclerosing dysplasia caused by loss-of-function mutations in SOST, which encodes sclerostin","Loss of sclerostin removes a natural brake on Wnt-driven bone formation, producing progressive generalised high bone mass","Characterised by a large mandible, frontal bossing, facial distortion, and tall stature with a heavy skeleton","Syndactyly of the fingers, often with nail dysplasia, is a distinctive associated feature that helps separate it from Van Buchem disease","Skull thickening entraps cranial nerves, so facial-nerve palsy, hearing loss, and optic compromise are characteristic complications","Progressive skull-base overgrowth can raise intracranial pressure and, rarely, cause life-threatening brainstem compression","Management focuses on surgical decompression of entrapped nerves and monitoring for raised intracranial pressure; bisphosphonates are not effective at reducing bone mass"],"pathophysiology":"Sclerosteosis is caused by biallelic inactivating mutations in SOST, the gene encoding sclerostin, a protein secreted by osteocytes that normally inhibits the Wnt/beta-catenin signalling pathway in bone. Without functional sclerostin, Wnt signalling is unrestrained, driving markedly increased osteoblastic bone formation throughout the skeleton. The result is progressive, dense, sclerotic bone with cortical and skull thickening. Overgrowth of the calvaria and skull base narrows cranial foramina, entrapping cranial nerves and, in severe cases, elevating intracranial pressure. The same pathway biology has informed development of anti-sclerostin therapy for osteoporosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Van Buchem disease","Camurati-Engelmann disease","Osteopetrosis","Pycnodysostosis","Craniometaphyseal dysplasia","Paget disease of the skull"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs showing generalised osteosclerosis, cortical thickening, and calvarial hyperostosis","Genetic testing for SOST mutations","Cross-sectional skull-base imaging to assess foraminal narrowing and cranial nerve compression","Audiology and formal visual assessment","Monitoring for raised intracranial pressure including fundoscopy","Examination for syndactyly and nail changes"],"redFlags":["Signs of raised intracranial pressure such as headache, papilloedema, and vomiting","Progressive facial-nerve palsy or rapidly worsening hearing or vision","Brainstem or cerebellar signs suggesting posterior fossa crowding","Sudden neurological deterioration requiring urgent decompression"],"prognosis":"The disorder is progressive; without intervention, cranial nerve entrapment and raised intracranial pressure can cause serious neurological disability, and severe cases may be life-limiting, though timely surgical decompression can relieve complications.","pitfalls":["Confusing sclerosteosis with Van Buchem disease, distinguished by the presence of syndactyly and generally greater severity in sclerosteosis","Attributing progressive facial palsy to idiopathic Bell palsy rather than bony entrapment","Failing to monitor and treat rising intracranial pressure","Expecting antiresorptive drugs to reduce the excess bone mass"],"management":["Undertake timely surgical decompression of narrowed cranial foramina and the skull base to relieve cranial-nerve entrapment and raised intracranial pressure","Manage facial-nerve palsy, hearing loss with hearing aids or implants, and optic compromise supportively","Monitor for raised intracranial pressure with serial fundoscopy and skull-base imaging, intervening before brainstem compression develops","Provide ongoing audiological and visual surveillance with rehabilitation","Avoid anti-sclerostin antibody therapy and other agents that further increase bone mass, since the disorder arises from unrestrained bone formation","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive SOST disorder","Refer to a metabolic bone and clinical genetics team, with neurosurgery and ENT input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism (ASBMR)","GeneReviews / peer-reviewed reviews of SOST-related sclerosing bone dysplasias"],"crossLinks":[]},"scn1b_epilepsy":{"name":"SCN1B-related epilepsy (GEFS+/Dravet-like)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCN1B","SCN1B-related epilepsy","sodium channel beta1 subunit epilepsy","GEFS+ type 1","SCN1B Dravet-like encephalopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / ILAE","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in SCN1B, which encodes the beta1 auxiliary subunit of the voltage-gated sodium channel that modulates channel gating, trafficking, and cell adhesion","Heterozygous variants classically cause genetic epilepsy with febrile seizures plus (GEFS+), an autosomal dominant familial spectrum with variable severity","Biallelic (homozygous or compound heterozygous) loss-of-function variants can cause a severe early infantile Dravet-like developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","The GEFS+ spectrum includes simple febrile seizures, febrile seizures persisting beyond the usual age, and afebrile generalised or focal seizures within the same family","Fever sensitivity and prolonged seizures are recurrent features, overlapping clinically with SCN1A-related disorders","Because the beta subunit modulates neuronal sodium channels, disturbed channel function alters excitability, and antiseizure choice should account for the Dravet-like overlap where sodium-channel blockers may be poorly tolerated in the severe phenotype","The severe biallelic phenotype carries developmental impairment and, as with related sodium-channel epilepsies, attention to sudden unexpected death in epilepsy risk"],"pathophysiology":"SCN1B encodes the beta1 subunit of the voltage-gated sodium channel, an auxiliary transmembrane protein that regulates the gating kinetics, surface trafficking, and localisation of the pore-forming alpha subunits and also functions as a cell-adhesion molecule influencing neuronal development. Heterozygous variants partially disturb sodium channel behaviour and produce the variable GEFS+ spectrum, while complete loss of beta1 function from biallelic variants more profoundly deranges channel regulation, resulting in the severe Dravet-like encephalopathy. The consequent instability of neuronal firing, particularly under the stress of fever, lowers the seizure threshold and, in the severe form, impairs the developing brain.","additionalDifferentials":["SCN1A-related GEFS+ and Dravet syndrome","SCN1A and GABRG2 febrile seizure spectrum disorders","PCDH19 clustering epilepsy","HCN1-related Dravet-like epilepsy","Genetic generalised epilepsies","Other genetic causes of febrile-seizure-associated epilepsy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Epilepsy gene panel or whole-exome sequencing, noting zygosity to distinguish GEFS+ from the biallelic Dravet-like phenotype","Electroencephalography to characterise generalised and focal features","Brain magnetic resonance imaging to exclude structural causes in severe presentations","Detailed family history and segregation analysis for the dominant GEFS+ spectrum","Developmental assessment in the severe phenotype","Seizure and fever-trigger documentation"],"redFlags":["Prolonged febrile-triggered seizures or febrile status epilepticus","Developmental regression in the biallelic Dravet-like phenotype","Drug-resistant mixed seizures","Features suggesting sudden unexpected death in epilepsy risk"],"prognosis":"Heterozygous GEFS+ often has a favourable outcome with age-limited febrile seizures and normal cognition, whereas the biallelic Dravet-like encephalopathy carries drug-resistant seizures and developmental impairment.","pitfalls":["Not checking zygosity, which distinguishes the mild dominant spectrum from severe recessive disease","Applying sodium-channel-blocker therapy indiscriminately in the Dravet-like phenotype","Anchoring on SCN1A and missing SCN1B in a GEFS+ family","Underestimating fever sensitivity and prolonged-seizure risk"],"management":["Control seizures in the severe phenotype with broad-spectrum antiseizure medication such as sodium valproate, levetiracetam, or clobazam","Use sodium-channel-blocking agents cautiously in the biallelic Dravet-like phenotype, where they may be poorly tolerated","Treat fever promptly with antipyretics, since fever lowers the seizure threshold and provokes prolonged seizures","Provide a rescue benzodiazepine protocol for prolonged seizures and counsel families on managing status epilepticus","Undertake developmental surveillance and early intervention in the severe encephalopathy","Offer genetic counselling with family segregation analysis, distinguishing dominant GEFS+ from biallelic disease","Refer to paediatric neurology and epileptology for phenotype-guided drug selection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews and OMIM resources on SCN1B-related epilepsy and GEFS+","ILAE resources on genetic epilepsy with febrile seizures plus"],"crossLinks":[]},"scn2a_developmental_epileptic_encephalopathy":{"name":"SCN2A developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCN2A","Nav1.2 encephalopathy","SCN2A-related epilepsy"],"chapter":null,"source":"ILAE classification / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in SCN2A encoding the Nav1.2 voltage-gated sodium channel expressed in excitatory neurons","The functional effect largely dictates phenotype and treatment: early-onset (neonatal-infantile) seizures usually reflect gain-of-function, while later-onset seizures, autism or intellectual disability often reflect loss-of-function","Early-onset gain-of-function cases frequently respond well to sodium-channel-blocking antiseizure medications","In contrast, later-onset loss-of-function cases may be worsened by sodium-channel blockers, so age at onset guides drug choice","The spectrum ranges from self-limited familial neonatal-infantile seizures to severe developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","Autism spectrum disorder and intellectual disability without epilepsy are recognised presentations, particularly with loss-of-function variants","Genetic testing with functional interpretation is central to management"],"pathophysiology":"SCN2A encephalopathy is caused by variants in SCN2A encoding the Nav1.2 sodium channel, which mediates action-potential initiation and propagation in excitatory neurons, especially early in development. Gain-of-function variants increase sodium current and neuronal excitability, producing early-onset seizures that respond to sodium-channel blockade. Loss-of-function variants reduce excitatory signalling and impair network development, more often causing later-onset seizures, autism and intellectual disability, and such cases may deteriorate with sodium-channel-blocking agents. This mechanistic dichotomy makes age at onset a practical proxy for the underlying channel effect.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign familial neonatal-infantile seizures","KCNQ2 developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","Ohtahara syndrome of other cause","Dravet syndrome (SCN1A)","Autism spectrum disorder with or without epilepsy","Other early-onset developmental and epileptic encephalopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SCN2A testing or an epilepsy gene panel or exome sequencing with functional interpretation","EEG appropriate to age and seizure type","Brain MRI to exclude structural pathology","Metabolic screening in neonatal presentations","Developmental, cognitive and autism assessment"],"redFlags":["Refractory neonatal or infantile seizures with developmental arrest","Seizure aggravation after starting a sodium-channel-blocking agent in a later-onset case","Regression or emergent movement disorder","Diagnostic uncertainty between gain- and loss-of-function phenotypes affecting drug choice"],"prognosis":"Outcome is highly variable and correlates with variant type and age at onset, ranging from self-limited neonatal-infantile seizures with normal development to severe drug-resistant epilepsy with profound disability. Later-onset loss-of-function cases frequently have autism and intellectual disability regardless of seizure control.","pitfalls":["Applying a single treatment strategy without regard to age at onset and channel effect","Using sodium-channel blockers in later-onset loss-of-function cases and worsening seizures","Overlooking SCN2A in autism or intellectual disability without prominent epilepsy","Interpreting a variant of uncertain significance without functional or phenotypic context"],"management":["Tailor antiseizure therapy to the variant mechanism, using sodium-channel blockers such as carbamazepine, phenytoin, or lacosamide for early-onset gain-of-function seizures","Avoid sodium-channel-blocking agents in later-onset loss-of-function cases where they may worsen seizures, choosing alternative broad-spectrum agents","Use age at seizure onset as a practical proxy for the channel effect when guiding drug choice","Provide developmental, cognitive, and autism-focused intervention and therapies","Manage drug-resistant seizures with rational combination therapy and consideration of the ketogenic diet","Offer genetic counselling regarding the frequently de novo variant and recurrence risk","Refer to paediatric neurology, epileptology, and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ILAE classification of the epilepsies","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"scn2a_encephalopathy":{"name":"SCN2A-related developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","system":"Neurology / Developmental epileptic encephalopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCN2A encephalopathy","Nav1.2 encephalopathy","SCN2A-DEE"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in SCN2A encoding the voltage-gated sodium channel alpha subunit Nav1.2, expressed heavily in the axon initial segment of excitatory neurons during early development.","Age at seizure onset is the single most useful clinical clue to variant mechanism: early-infantile onset (typically first three months) usually reflects gain-of-function, whereas later onset (beyond around the first months) or non-epileptic autism/intellectual disability phenotypes usually reflect loss-of-function.","Early-onset gain-of-function cases characteristically respond to sodium-channel-blocking antiseizure medicines, while these same agents may worsen seizures in later-onset loss-of-function cases — making the phenotype-guided treatment split a defining feature.","Phenotypic spectrum spans benign familial infantile seizures, Ohtahara syndrome, epilepsy of infancy with migrating focal seizures, and West syndrome, alongside autism spectrum disorder and intellectual disability without epilepsy.","Episodic ataxia and later-onset movement phenomena are described, broadening the picture beyond seizures alone.","Most disease-causing variants arise de novo, though inherited variants underlie the milder self-limiting familial infantile seizure phenotype.","Neonatal or early-infantile tonic, focal, and migrating seizures with a burst-suppression or multifocal EEG should prompt sodium-channel gene testing including SCN2A.","Genotype-informed prescribing is central to management, so early genetic diagnosis materially changes the treatment pathway."],"pathophysiology":"SCN2A encodes Nav1.2, a voltage-gated sodium channel alpha subunit concentrated in the axon initial segment and proximal axons of excitatory (glutamatergic) cortical and hippocampal neurons, where it drives action-potential initiation and propagation particularly in immature neurons before Nav1.6 predominates. Gain-of-function missense variants enhance channel activity — through increased persistent current, faster recovery from inactivation, or a hyperpolarising shift in activation — producing neuronal hyperexcitability that manifests as early-infantile seizures; because these channels are so critical in the neonatal period, such variants typically declare themselves in the first weeks to months of life and respond to sodium-channel blockade. Loss-of-function variants (including protein-truncating and some missense changes) reduce excitatory neuronal output; paradoxically this is associated with autism and intellectual disability, and when seizures occur they tend to begin later and may be aggravated by sodium-channel-blocking drugs, reflecting the developmental shift in channel expression. This mechanistic dichotomy — hyperfunction driving early seizures versus hypofunction driving neurodevelopmental impairment and later or drug-sensitive seizures — is the organising principle of the disorder and directly informs pharmacological choices.","additionalDifferentials":["SCN8A-related developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","SCN1A-related Dravet syndrome and GEFS+ spectrum","KCNQ2 developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","STXBP1-related encephalopathy","KCNT1-related epilepsy of infancy with migrating focal seizures","Pyridoxine-dependent and other vitamin-responsive neonatal epilepsies","Neonatal hypoxic-ischaemic or metabolic encephalopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gene-panel or exome/genome sequencing covering sodium-channel and other developmental epileptic encephalopathy genes","Prolonged video-EEG to characterise seizure semiology and ictal-interictal patterns","Neonatal EEG looking for burst-suppression, multifocal or migrating discharges","MRI brain to exclude structural and acquired causes","Metabolic screen and trial of vitamin-responsive epilepsy cofactors where onset is neonatal","Parental testing to establish de novo versus inherited status and recurrence risk","Developmental and autism assessment across follow-up"],"redFlags":["Neonatal-onset tonic or migrating focal seizures with burst-suppression EEG suggesting a severe early encephalopathy","Seizure worsening after introduction of a sodium-channel-blocking medicine, pointing to a loss-of-function mechanism","Developmental regression or arrest accompanying seizure onset","Recurrent status epilepticus or clustering of migrating focal seizures","Emerging feeding difficulty, apnoea, or autonomic instability in an encephalopathic infant"],"prognosis":"Outcome is highly variable and genotype-dependent, ranging from self-limiting familial infantile seizures with normal development to severe drug-resistant encephalopathy with profound intellectual disability. Early-onset gain-of-function seizures often improve with appropriate sodium-channel-blocking therapy, whereas loss-of-function phenotypes carry a substantial burden of autism and intellectual disability irrespective of seizure control.","pitfalls":["Reflexively using or escalating a sodium-channel-blocking medicine in a later-onset case, which can aggravate loss-of-function seizures.","Assuming all SCN2A disease is epileptic and overlooking the autism/intellectual-disability-predominant loss-of-function phenotype without prominent seizures.","Attributing neonatal seizures to hypoxic-ischaemic injury and delaying genetic testing.","Failing to use age at onset as a guide to likely mechanism and therefore to drug selection.","Neglecting parental testing, leading to inaccurate recurrence-risk counselling for familial variants."],"management":["Match antiseizure choice to the variant, using sodium-channel blockers such as carbamazepine, phenytoin, or lacosamide for early-infantile gain-of-function seizures","Avoid sodium-channel blockers in later-onset loss-of-function phenotypes where they may aggravate seizures, selecting alternative agents","Trial vitamin-responsive epilepsy cofactors in neonatal presentations pending genetic confirmation","Escalate drug-resistant epilepsy with rational polytherapy and the ketogenic diet","Support autism and intellectual-disability phenotypes with developmental and behavioural intervention","Undertake parental testing and genetic counselling to establish de novo versus inherited status and recurrence risk","Refer to paediatric neurology and clinical genetics for phenotype-guided management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard paediatric neurology textbook","Neurogenetics reference resource","International consensus guidance on genetic epilepsies"],"crossLinks":[]},"scn8a_developmental_epileptic_encephalopathy":{"name":"SCN8A developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCN8A","SCN8A-DEE","EIEE13","Nav1.6 encephalopathy","early infantile epileptic encephalopathy type 13"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / ILAE","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in SCN8A, which encodes the voltage-gated sodium channel alpha subunit Nav1.6, a channel highly expressed at the axon initial segment and nodes of Ranvier","Most classic developmental and epileptic encephalopathy cases arise from gain-of-function variants that increase neuronal excitability, and these children often respond preferentially to sodium-channel-blocking antiseizure agents","Seizure onset is usually in infancy, with focal, tonic, and epileptic-spasm semiologies and frequent progression to intractable mixed seizure types","Development is typically impaired, ranging from mild delay to profound intellectual disability with movement disorder, hypotonia evolving to spasticity, and cortical visual impairment","A milder end of the spectrum includes benign familial infantile epilepsy that remits, while a separate phenotype presents with intellectual disability and movement disorder without prominent seizures","Loss-of-function SCN8A variants tend to cause a distinct milder phenotype and, in contrast to gain-of-function disease, sodium-channel blockers may be unhelpful or aggravating","There is a recognised risk of sudden unexpected death in epilepsy, reinforcing the importance of seizure control and family counselling"],"pathophysiology":"SCN8A encodes Nav1.6, a voltage-gated sodium channel concentrated at the axon initial segment and nodes of Ranvier where it drives action potential initiation and repetitive firing. Gain-of-function variants shift channel gating so that neurons become hyperexcitable, for example through impaired inactivation or a persistent inward sodium current, producing early severe epilepsy and encephalopathy. Loss-of-function variants reduce channel availability and are generally associated with milder phenotypes without early catastrophic seizures. Because the therapeutic response depends on the functional consequence of the variant, distinguishing gain from loss of function guides rational selection of antiseizure medication.","additionalDifferentials":["SCN2A-related developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","SCN1A-related Dravet syndrome","KCNQ2 encephalopathy","CDKL5 deficiency disorder","STXBP1 encephalopathy","Other early infantile epileptic encephalopathies of genetic cause","Structural or metabolic epilepsies of infancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Epilepsy gene panel or trio whole-exome sequencing to identify the SCN8A variant and infer likely functional effect","Prolonged or video electroencephalography to characterise seizure types and interictal abnormalities","Brain magnetic resonance imaging, often normal early but useful to exclude structural causes","Functional or in silico assessment of the variant where available to distinguish gain from loss of function","Developmental and neuropsychological assessment to document trajectory","Electrocardiography and cardiac review given sodium-channel expression and SUDEP considerations"],"redFlags":["Clusters of intractable seizures or evolving status epilepticus in infancy","Developmental regression or plateau accompanying seizure onset","Emerging movement disorder or profound hypotonia with feeding and respiratory compromise","Features suggesting heightened sudden unexpected death in epilepsy risk"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies widely by variant: gain-of-function developmental and epileptic encephalopathy often carries drug-resistant seizures, significant intellectual disability, and a raised risk of sudden unexpected death, whereas milder self-limited or predominantly movement-disorder phenotypes may have better cognitive outcomes.","pitfalls":["Assuming all SCN8A disease is gain-of-function and reflexively using sodium-channel blockers, which can worsen loss-of-function cases","Overlooking the milder self-limited familial infantile epilepsy phenotype and over-treating","Attributing a normal early MRI to a benign course despite a severe genetic epilepsy","Neglecting counselling about sudden unexpected death in epilepsy and safety planning"],"management":["Treat gain-of-function encephalopathy with sodium-channel-blocking antiseizure agents such as phenytoin, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, or lacosamide, which are preferentially effective","Reconsider or avoid sodium-channel blockers in loss-of-function variants, where they may be unhelpful or aggravating","Manage frequently drug-resistant mixed seizures with combination therapy and consideration of the ketogenic diet","Address the associated movement disorder, evolving hypotonia and spasticity, and cortical visual impairment with rehabilitation and supportive therapies","Provide cardiac assessment and counsel on the raised risk of sudden unexpected death in epilepsy","Undertake developmental and neuropsychological surveillance with early intervention","Offer genetic counselling and refer to paediatric neurology and epileptology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: SCN8A-Related Epilepsy and/or Neurodevelopmental Disorders","ILAE classification and genetics resources on developmental and epileptic encephalopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"scn8a_encephalopathy":{"name":"SCN8A-related developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","system":"Neurology / Developmental epileptic encephalopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCN8A encephalopathy","Nav1.6 encephalopathy","SCN8A-DEE","EIEE13"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in SCN8A encoding Nav1.6, the dominant sodium channel at mature nodes of Ranvier and the distal axon initial segment, so it becomes functionally prominent slightly later in development than Nav1.2.","The classic severe phenotype presents in infancy with focal, tonic-clonic, or epileptic-spasm seizures that are frequently drug-resistant, accompanied by developmental regression and movement disorder.","Gain-of-function variants dominate the severe epileptic encephalopathy and characteristically respond well to high-dose sodium-channel-blocking medicines — a strong therapeutic pointer.","Loss-of-function variants produce a contrasting milder phenotype of intellectual disability, autism, and movement problems, often without prominent early seizures.","Cardiac arrhythmia risk and a recognised association with sudden unexpected death in epilepsy warrant attention to this channelopathy's extra-neurological consequences.","A characteristic movement disorder — dystonia, choreoathetosis, and stereotypies — often accompanies the epilepsy and may persist between seizures.","Most severe cases arise from de novo variants, but a milder benign familial infantile seizure phenotype with inherited variants is also recognised.","The favourable response of gain-of-function disease to sodium-channel blockade makes early genetic diagnosis directly actionable."],"pathophysiology":"SCN8A encodes Nav1.6, a voltage-gated sodium channel alpha subunit that becomes the principal channel at mature nodes of Ranvier and the distal axon initial segment, supporting high-frequency repetitive firing and being expressed in both excitatory and inhibitory neurons; its developmental prominence somewhat later than Nav1.2 shapes the typical infantile rather than neonatal onset of the severe phenotype. Gain-of-function missense variants enhance channel function by increasing persistent sodium current, impairing inactivation, or shifting activation, producing sustained neuronal hyperexcitability, drug-resistant seizures, and an associated hyperkinetic movement disorder; because the pathology is excess sodium-channel activity, these patients often respond strikingly to sodium-channel-blocking antiseizure medicines, sometimes requiring higher-than-usual exposure. Loss-of-function variants reduce Nav1.6 activity and are instead linked to intellectual disability, autism, ataxia, and movement abnormalities with less prominent or absent seizures, mirroring the mechanistic dichotomy seen across the developmental sodium channelopathies. Nav1.6 expression in cardiac tissue and brainstem circuitry contributes to arrhythmia susceptibility and the recognised risk of sudden unexpected death in epilepsy.","additionalDifferentials":["SCN2A-related developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","SCN1A-related Dravet syndrome","KCNQ2 developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","STXBP1-related encephalopathy","CDKL5 deficiency disorder","Glucose transporter type 1 deficiency syndrome","Structural or acquired epileptic encephalopathies of infancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gene-panel or exome/genome sequencing including SCN8A and other sodium-channel genes","Prolonged video-EEG to define seizure types and interictal abnormalities","MRI brain to exclude structural causes","Electrocardiography and cardiology review given arrhythmia and sudden-death risk","Movement-disorder assessment documenting dystonia, chorea, or stereotypies","Parental testing to determine de novo versus inherited status","Serial developmental and neuropsychological evaluation"],"redFlags":["Drug-resistant infantile seizures with developmental regression indicating severe encephalopathy","Prolonged seizures or status epilepticus requiring acute intervention","Symptoms suggesting cardiac arrhythmia such as syncope or documented conduction abnormality","Emergence or worsening of a disabling movement disorder","Features raising concern for sudden unexpected death in epilepsy risk"],"prognosis":"The severe gain-of-function encephalopathy commonly causes drug-resistant epilepsy with intellectual disability and a movement disorder, though seizure control is often achievable with sodium-channel-blocking therapy; there is an important risk of sudden unexpected death in epilepsy. Loss-of-function and inherited milder variants carry a more favourable seizure outlook but may still leave significant neurodevelopmental impairment.","pitfalls":["Withholding or under-dosing sodium-channel-blocking medicines in gain-of-function disease that would otherwise respond well.","Overlooking cardiac evaluation and sudden-death risk in what appears to be a purely neurological disorder.","Confusing the later infantile onset with SCN2A's typical neonatal gain-of-function onset when reasoning about mechanism.","Missing the loss-of-function phenotype of autism and intellectual disability without prominent seizures.","Interpreting the movement disorder as medication side-effect rather than an intrinsic disease feature."],"management":["Use sodium-channel-blocking medicines for the gain-of-function severe phenotype, which characteristically responds well","Avoid sodium-channel blockers in loss-of-function variants presenting with intellectual disability, autism, and movement problems","Manage the associated hyperkinetic movement disorder of dystonia, choreoathetosis, and stereotypies alongside the epilepsy","Arrange electrocardiography and cardiology review given arrhythmia and sudden-unexpected-death risk","Treat drug-resistant seizures with combination antiseizure therapy and dietary options such as the ketogenic diet","Support developmental regression with rehabilitation and multidisciplinary care","Determine de novo versus inherited status by parental testing and offer genetic counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard paediatric neurology textbook","Neurogenetics reference resource","International consensus guidance on genetic epilepsies"],"crossLinks":[]},"scoliosis":{"name":"Scoliosis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["spinal curvature","adolescent idiopathic scoliosis","lateral curvature of the spine"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A three-dimensional deformity combining lateral curvature and vertebral rotation, most commonly adolescent idiopathic scoliosis in otherwise healthy children, predominantly girls","It is often painless and detected as asymmetry of the shoulders, waist, or scapulae; a rib hump on the forward-bending (Adam's) test reflects the rotational component","Curve magnitude is quantified by the Cobb angle on a standing radiograph, and skeletal maturity (e.g. Risser sign) helps predict the risk of progression","Significant back pain, a rapidly progressive curve, an atypical (e.g. left thoracic) curve, or neurological signs should prompt a search for an underlying cause and MRI","Scoliosis may be idiopathic, congenital (vertebral anomalies), or neuromuscular (e.g. cerebral palsy, muscular dystrophy), and management differs accordingly","Small curves are observed, moderate curves in a growing child may be braced to limit progression, and large or progressive curves are considered for surgical correction and fusion","The likelihood of progression is greatest during the adolescent growth spurt, so monitoring is intensified during rapid growth","Severe thoracic curves can restrict pulmonary function, an important consideration in early-onset and neuromuscular disease"],"pathophysiology":"Scoliosis is a structural lateral deviation of the spine accompanied by rotation of the vertebrae, which produces the characteristic rib or paravertebral prominence. In adolescent idiopathic scoliosis the cause is unknown but multifactorial, with genetic, growth-related, and neuromuscular balance factors implicated, and the deformity tends to worsen during periods of rapid skeletal growth. Congenital scoliosis results from failures of vertebral formation or segmentation present from birth, while neuromuscular scoliosis arises from asymmetrical muscle forces acting on the growing spine in conditions such as cerebral palsy. Progressive rotation and wedging of vertebrae make the curve increasingly rigid, and large thoracic curves can distort the chest and compromise lung capacity.","additionalDifferentials":["Postural (non-structural) scoliosis that corrects on forward bending","Leg-length discrepancy causing compensatory curvature","Congenital vertebral anomalies","Neuromuscular scoliosis (cerebral palsy, muscular dystrophy, spinal muscular atrophy)","Syndromic scoliosis (e.g. neurofibromatosis, Marfan syndrome)","Intraspinal pathology (syrinx, tethered cord, tumour) causing an atypical or painful curve"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Standing posteroanterior and lateral spinal radiographs to measure the Cobb angle and assess balance","Assessment of skeletal maturity (e.g. Risser sign, hand/wrist bone age)","Forward-bending (Adam's) test with a scoliometer to gauge rotation","MRI of the whole spine for atypical, painful, rapidly progressive, or early-onset curves, or when there are neurological signs","Pulmonary function testing for large thoracic or neuromuscular curves"],"redFlags":["Neurological signs or symptoms suggesting an intraspinal cause requiring MRI","Significant or persistent back pain, unusual for idiopathic scoliosis","Rapid curve progression or an atypical curve pattern (e.g. left thoracic)","Respiratory compromise from a severe thoracic curve"],"prognosis":"Most mild adolescent idiopathic curves remain stable and require only monitoring, and bracing can prevent progression of moderate curves during growth; large or progressive curves may need surgical correction, and untreated severe curves can cause deformity, back pain, and in extreme thoracic cases cardiopulmonary compromise, while neuromuscular and congenital curves tend to be more progressive.","pitfalls":["Assuming all scoliosis is benign idiopathic disease and missing an underlying intraspinal or neuromuscular cause","Overlooking a painful or atypical curve that warrants MRI","Failing to monitor closely during the adolescent growth spurt when progression is most likely","Confusing a compensatory postural curve from leg-length discrepancy with structural scoliosis"],"management":["Observe small curves with periodic clinical and radiographic monitoring of the Cobb angle throughout growth","Fit a spinal orthosis (brace) for moderate curves in the skeletally immature to limit progression","Refer progressive or large curves for surgical correction and spinal fusion, or growth-friendly instrumentation in early-onset disease","Investigate atypical, painful, rapidly progressive, or neurological presentations with whole-spine MRI to identify an underlying cause","Manage the underlying condition in congenital vertebral-anomaly and neuromuscular scoliosis, tailoring treatment accordingly","Monitor and support respiratory function in large thoracic and neuromuscular curves","Provide physiotherapy and multidisciplinary orthopaedic care, with neuromuscular input where relevant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"scombroid_poisoning":{"name":"Scombroid (histamine) fish poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scombroid","histamine fish poisoning","scombrotoxism","mahi-mahi/tuna histamine poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / WHO envenoming guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from eating fish (typically dark-fleshed species such as tuna, mackerel, mahi-mahi, sardines) that have been inadequately refrigerated, allowing bacterial conversion of histidine to histamine","Onset is rapid, usually within minutes to an hour, mimicking an allergic reaction despite being a toxin-mediated illness","Features include flushing of the face and upper body, headache, palpitations, urticaria, sweating, and gastrointestinal upset; a peppery or metallic taste to the fish may be reported","Because it mimics IgE-mediated allergy, it is often misdiagnosed as a seafood allergy, which has implications for future advice","Management is largely supportive with antihistamines; severe reactions with bronchospasm or hypotension are treated as for anaphylaxis, including adrenaline","Multiple diners becoming ill after the same fish, or normal tolerance of the same species previously, points to scombroid rather than true allergy","The fish often looks and smells normal, and the histamine is heat-stable, so cooking does not prevent poisoning"],"pathophysiology":"When scombroid and related fish are stored at inadequate temperatures, bacteria decarboxylate the naturally abundant amino acid histidine in the flesh into histamine and other biogenic amines. Ingesting this preformed histamine produces a pharmacological reaction that mimics an allergic response, with vasodilatation, flushing, increased vascular permeability and, in severe cases, bronchospasm and hypotension. The reaction is not immunologically mediated and does not require prior sensitisation, and because histamine is heat-stable it is not removed by cooking; other amines may potentiate the effect by inhibiting histamine breakdown.","additionalDifferentials":["True IgE-mediated seafood allergy or anaphylaxis","Ciguatera fish poisoning","Anaphylaxis to another allergen","Carcinoid or other flushing syndrome","Panic attack or vasomotor flushing","Medication or alcohol-related flushing reaction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on rapid onset after fish and characteristic flushing","Corroborating history of others affected by the same meal","Observation of response to antihistamines to support the diagnosis","Electrocardiography and monitoring if palpitations or hypotension occur","Measurement of histamine in the implicated fish where laboratory capability exists"],"redFlags":["Airway compromise, stridor or severe bronchospasm","Hypotension or shock","Rapidly progressive symptoms requiring treatment as anaphylaxis","Reaction in a patient with significant cardiac or respiratory comorbidity","Severe or persistent symptoms despite antihistamines"],"prognosis":"Scombroid poisoning is usually mild and self-limiting, resolving within hours, and responds well to antihistamines; severe reactions are uncommon but can resemble anaphylaxis and require the same emergency treatment.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the patient as fish-allergic, leading to unnecessary lifelong avoidance","Assuming cooking or the fish smelling normal excludes the diagnosis, as histamine is heat-stable","Under-treating a severe reaction that warrants adrenaline as for anaphylaxis","Failing to consider a common-source outbreak and report a food-safety issue"],"management":["Treat with antihistamines, using both H1 and H2 antagonists, as the mainstay for this histamine-mediated reaction","Manage severe reactions with bronchospasm or hypotension as for anaphylaxis, including intramuscular adrenaline, oxygen, and intravenous fluids","Provide supportive care and cardiorespiratory monitoring where palpitations or hypotension occur","Reassure that the illness is self-limiting and explain it is a toxin rather than a seafood allergy, so the fish need not be avoided lifelong","Advise on prevention through proper refrigeration and cold-chain handling of susceptible fish","Report suspected outbreaks to public and environmental health authorities"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","WHO/food-safety guidance on histamine fish poisoning"],"crossLinks":[]},"scorpion_envenomation":{"name":"Scorpion envenomation","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scorpion sting","scorpionism","Centruroides envenomation","Leiurus (deathstalker) sting","Androctonus sting"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / WHO envenoming guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most stings cause only intense local pain with minimal swelling; systemic envenomation is species-dependent and most severe in young children","Medically important genera (Centruroides, Leiurus, Androctonus, Tityus, Hottentotta) produce an autonomic storm from massive neurotransmitter release","Sympathetic overdrive causes tachycardia, hypertension, sweating, agitation and hyperthermia, sometimes alternating with cholinergic features such as hypersalivation and vomiting","Neuromuscular excitation may produce roving eye movements, fasciculations, restlessness and involuntary limb movements, especially in children","Catecholamine surge can precipitate myocarditis, pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock, which are the main causes of death","Grading of severity (local only, systemic autonomic, cardiorespiratory failure) guides the need for antivenom and intensive support","Management principles include analgesia, species-appropriate antivenom for systemic envenomation, and cautious afterload reduction for the hyperadrenergic state and cardiac dysfunction"],"pathophysiology":"Scorpion venom contains neurotoxins that bind voltage-gated sodium and potassium channels on excitable membranes, prolonging depolarisation and causing repetitive firing of autonomic and somatic nerves. This triggers a massive release of catecholamines and acetylcholine, producing a mixed sympathetic and parasympathetic autonomic storm. The catecholamine surge increases myocardial oxygen demand and can cause a stress cardiomyopathy or myocarditis with left ventricular dysfunction, capillary leak and pulmonary oedema. Local venom effects on nociceptors account for the severe pain that is often disproportionate to visible tissue changes.","additionalDifferentials":["Sympathomimetic toxidrome from drug ingestion","Anaphylaxis to the sting or another allergen","Primary myocarditis or acute coronary syndrome","Seizure disorder or CNS infection in an agitated child","Hymenoptera or spider envenomation","Thyroid storm or phaeochromocytoma crisis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electrocardiography for ischaemic changes and arrhythmia","Cardiac troponin and echocardiography if myocardial involvement is suspected","Chest imaging for pulmonary oedema","Blood glucose, electrolytes and lactate given catecholamine-driven derangements","Creatine kinase and renal function if muscle activity is marked","Continuous cardiorespiratory monitoring in graded systemic envenomation"],"redFlags":["Signs of pulmonary oedema or cardiogenic shock","Persistent hypertension with end-organ compromise","Marked neuromuscular excitation or airway-threatening secretions in a young child","Altered consciousness or seizures","Rapid clinical deterioration after an initially mild sting"],"prognosis":"Isolated local stings resolve within hours to a day. Systemic envenomation, particularly in children, carries a risk of life-threatening cardiopulmonary failure, but with timely antivenom and intensive supportive care outcomes are generally good.","pitfalls":["Underestimating a sting in a young child, in whom systemic envenomation and cardiac failure develop rapidly","Attributing pulmonary oedema to fluid overload rather than venom-induced myocardial dysfunction and giving excess fluid","Delaying antivenom while treating only the autonomic symptoms","Failing to monitor for evolving cardiac injury after the initial autonomic phase"],"management":["Administer species-specific scorpion antivenom early in systemic autonomic or cardiorespiratory envenomation","Counter the catecholamine storm and reduce left-ventricular afterload with an alpha-blocker such as prazosin","Provide potent analgesia for the severe local pain with local anaesthetic infiltration or systemic analgesics","Support cardiorespiratory failure in intensive care, treating myocarditis, pulmonary oedema, and cardiogenic shock with vasoactive support and ventilation","Grade severity and monitor continuously with electrocardiography, echocardiography, and troponin where myocardial involvement is suspected","Give supportive wound care and update tetanus prophylaxis","Recognise young children as highest risk and escalate to paediatric intensive care early"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","WHO/regional scorpion envenomation management guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"scorpion_sting":{"name":"Scorpion sting","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scorpion envenomation","scorpion bite","scorpionism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Venom is a mixture of neurotoxins that activate sodium channels and drive a massive autonomic (catecholamine) surge, producing a mixed sympathetic and parasympathetic toxidrome","Local effects are often minor (intense pain, tingling, minimal swelling), so systemic severity can be underestimated from the sting site alone","Early cholinergic features (vomiting, profuse sweating, salivation, priapism) are typically followed by an adrenergic phase with hypertension, tachycardia, and agitation","The feared complication is myocardial dysfunction with acute pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock, particularly in children","Prazosin, an alpha-blocker, is a key pharmacological measure to counter the catecholamine storm and offload the heart in significant envenomation","Species-specific scorpion antivenom may be indicated for systemic envenomation where available, ideally given early","Supportive care includes analgesia, close cardiorespiratory monitoring, and management of pulmonary oedema; dobutamine may help the failing heart","Children and those presenting late with signs of myocarditis or shock carry the greatest risk and need intensive monitoring"],"pathophysiology":"Scorpion venom contains peptide neurotoxins that delay inactivation of voltage-gated sodium channels and block potassium channels at nerve terminals, causing repetitive depolarisation and unregulated release of neurotransmitters. This produces an initial cholinergic outpouring followed by an overwhelming sympathetic (catecholamine) surge. The resulting effects include hypertension, tachyarrhythmias, increased systemic vascular resistance, and direct catecholamine-mediated myocardial injury. Myocarditis, transient left ventricular dysfunction, and increased capillary permeability lead to acute pulmonary oedema, which is the principal cause of death, especially in children.","additionalDifferentials":["Other venomous envenomation (snakebite, spider bite)","Acute myocarditis from other causes","Sympathomimetic drug toxicity","Acute anaphylaxis","Acute coronary syndrome with pulmonary oedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous ECG monitoring for arrhythmia and ischaemic changes","Echocardiography to assess left ventricular function in systemic envenomation","Cardiac biomarkers (troponin) where myocardial injury is suspected","Chest imaging for pulmonary oedema","Blood glucose, electrolytes, and arterial blood gas in severe cases"],"redFlags":["Acute pulmonary oedema, respiratory distress, or frothy sputum indicating myocardial involvement","Signs of cardiogenic shock or cool clammy peripheries","Priapism, profuse sweating, and repeated vomiting signalling significant systemic envenomation","Young children with any systemic feature, who deteriorate rapidly","New arrhythmia or ECG changes suggesting myocarditis"],"prognosis":"Most stings cause only local pain and resolve without sequelae, but systemic envenomation with myocardial dysfunction and pulmonary oedema can be fatal, particularly in children; early alpha-blockade, antivenom where indicated, and intensive supportive care markedly improve outcomes.","pitfalls":["Judging severity by the local reaction and missing evolving systemic toxicity","Delaying prazosin or antivenom while awaiting overt pulmonary oedema","Aggressive fluid loading in a patient with myocardial dysfunction, worsening pulmonary oedema","Under-monitoring children, who can deteriorate abruptly"],"management":["Give prazosin (an alpha-blocker) early to counter the adrenergic surge and offload the failing heart in significant envenomation","Administer species-specific antivenom for systemic envenomation where available, ideally early","Provide analgesia and local wound care for the painful sting site, with tetanus prophylaxis","Treat acute pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock with intensive supportive care, including ventilatory and inotropic or vasoactive support guided by echocardiography","Monitor with continuous electrocardiography, cardiac biomarkers, and echocardiography for myocardial dysfunction","Prioritise children, who are at greatest risk of fatal cardiorespiratory complications, for early intensive monitoring"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"scott_syndrome":{"name":"Scott syndrome","system":"Haematological system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Scott syndrome","Platelet procoagulant deficiency","Phospholipid scramblase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An exceptionally rare inherited bleeding disorder caused by defective exposure of procoagulant phosphatidylserine on the activated platelet surface.","Most cases result from autosomal recessive mutations in ANO6 (TMEM16F), which encodes a calcium-activated phospholipid scramblase.","Platelet count, morphology and standard aggregation studies are normal, so routine coagulation and platelet testing are typically unremarkable.","Bleeding is characteristically deep and delayed after trauma or surgery, resembling a coagulation defect more than a typical platelet disorder.","The defect impairs assembly of tenase and prothrombinase complexes on the platelet membrane because the phosphatidylserine platform is not externalised.","Diagnosis relies on specialised assays showing reduced platelet-dependent thrombin generation and diminished annexin V binding by flow cytometry.","Failure of microparticle release and impaired factor Va/factor Xa binding are hallmark laboratory findings.","Management centres on antifibrinolytics and platelet transfusion for significant bleeding, since no specific replacement therapy exists."],"pathophysiology":"Scott syndrome is caused by failure of the transmembrane phospholipid scrambling that normally follows platelet activation. In health, a rise in cytosolic calcium activates the scramblase encoded by ANO6 (TMEM16F), which collapses membrane phospholipid asymmetry and externalises phosphatidylserine on the outer leaflet of activated platelets, red cells and microparticles. This anionic surface is the essential catalytic platform for assembly of the tenase (factor VIIIa/IXa) and prothrombinase (factor Va/Xa) complexes that accelerate thrombin generation. In Scott syndrome, loss-of-function mutations abolish scramblase activity, so phosphatidylserine remains sequestered internally, microparticle formation is deficient and localised thrombin generation at the platelet surface is markedly impaired despite normal clotting factors and platelet aggregation. The result is a haemostatic defect that manifests chiefly after surgical or traumatic challenge.","additionalDifferentials":["Mild haemophilia A or B","von Willebrand disease","Platelet storage pool or secretion defects","Factor XI deficiency","Acquired coagulopathy or anticoagulant effect","Vascular/connective tissue bleeding disorder","Combined factor V and factor VIII deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Platelet-dependent thrombin generation assay (reduced)","Flow-cytometric annexin V binding to activated platelets (decreased phosphatidylserine exposure)","Assessment of platelet-derived microparticle formation","Prothrombin consumption / serum prothrombin time","Standard coagulation screen and platelet aggregometry (typically normal)","ANO6 (TMEM16F) gene sequencing","von Willebrand factor and coagulation factor assays to exclude alternatives"],"redFlags":["Severe delayed haemorrhage following surgery, dental extraction or trauma","Post-partum haemorrhage","Bleeding disproportionate to normal routine coagulation and platelet tests","Large deep haematoma or intramuscular bleeding","Recurrent bleeding requiring repeated transfusion with alloimmunisation risk"],"prognosis":"With awareness of the diagnosis and appropriate peri-procedural haemostatic support the prognosis is good and spontaneous bleeding is uncommon. The main hazard is unanticipated severe bleeding during surgery or trauma when the disorder has not been recognised.","pitfalls":["Excluding a bleeding disorder because routine coagulation and platelet function tests are normal.","Overlooking the need for specialised phosphatidylserine-exposure and thrombin-generation assays.","Assuming platelet transfusion alone always suffices without antifibrinolytic support.","Failing to plan haemostatic cover before elective surgery in a known patient.","Confusing the delayed deep bleeding pattern with a coagulation factor deficiency and misdirecting factor replacement."],"management":["Provide platelet transfusion as the mainstay haemostatic cover for surgery, trauma, or significant bleeding, supplying a functional procoagulant platelet surface","Plan peri-procedural haemostatic support in advance, adding an antifibrinolytic such as tranexamic acid and considering recombinant activated factor VII for refractory bleeding","Avoid antiplatelet agents and other drugs that impair haemostasis","Ensure the rare diagnosis is documented and communicated, for example via an alert card, to anticipate deep, delayed bleeding","Confirm and monitor with specialised platelet-dependent thrombin generation and annexin V flow-cytometry assays","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive ANO6 (TMEM16F) disorder and refer to a haemostasis centre"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology, current edition","International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis guidance on rare bleeding disorders","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"scrotal_calcinosis":{"name":"Scrotal calcinosis","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["idiopathic scrotal calcinosis","scrotal calculi","calcinosis of the scrotum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Scrotal calcinosis is a benign condition characterised by multiple painless, firm to hard calcified nodules within the scrotal skin","It typically affects young to middle-aged men and the nodules may slowly enlarge or increase in number over years","The lesions are confined to the scrotal wall and are separate from the testis, which is normal on examination and imaging","Nodules are usually asymptomatic but may occasionally discharge chalky material or become secondarily infected","Serum calcium and phosphate are characteristically normal, distinguishing it from calcinosis due to metabolic derangement","The main reasons to treat are cosmetic concern, discomfort, or discharge, and surgical excision of the involved scrotal skin is curative","Recurrence can occur after excision, and histology confirms dermal calcium deposits while excluding other pathology"],"pathophysiology":"Scrotal calcinosis is characterised by deposits of calcium within the dermis of the scrotal skin. Its origin is debated: some cases are thought to be truly idiopathic, arising within apparently normal skin, while others are considered dystrophic, developing within pre-existing epidermal or eccrine cysts whose walls degenerate and calcify. Systemic calcium and phosphate metabolism is normal, so the process is local rather than metabolic, and the deposits gradually enlarge as firm intracutaneous nodules.","additionalDifferentials":["Epidermoid or sebaceous cysts of the scrotum","Calcified epididymal or testicular lesions","Scrotal wall tumours","Calcinosis cutis due to abnormal calcium-phosphate metabolism","Steatocystoma multiplex"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination confirming intradermal scrotal nodules separate from the testis","Scrotal ultrasound to confirm normal testes and localise the calcified nodules to the skin","Serum calcium and phosphate to exclude a metabolic cause","Histological examination of excised nodules to confirm dermal calcification","Assessment for secondary infection or discharge when present"],"redFlags":["An associated intratesticular abnormality on ultrasound","Rapidly growing or ulcerating lesion suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Signs of secondary infection with pain, erythema, or discharge","Deranged calcium or phosphate pointing to a systemic disorder"],"prognosis":"Scrotal calcinosis is entirely benign with no malignant potential; excision of the affected skin is curative for symptomatic or cosmetically troublesome lesions, though new nodules may develop over time.","pitfalls":["Mistaking benign dermal nodules for a testicular or intrascrotal tumour","Investigating for systemic calcium disorders unnecessarily when the process is local","Failing to warn patients that recurrence is possible after excision","Overlooking secondary infection of a discharging nodule"],"management":["Offer surgical excision of the involved scrotal skin, which is curative, for symptomatic, discharging, or cosmetically troublesome nodules","Manage asymptomatic nodules expectantly with reassurance, since the condition is entirely benign with no malignant potential","Treat any secondary infection or chalky discharge appropriately","Confirm normal serum calcium and phosphate to exclude a metabolic cause of calcification","Use scrotal ultrasound to confirm normal testes and localise the nodules to the skin","Counsel that new nodules may develop over time despite excision"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","WHO Classification of Tumours: Urinary and Male Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"scurvy":{"name":"Scurvy","system":"Nutrition / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vitamin C deficiency","ascorbic acid deficiency","hypovitaminosis C"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO nutrition guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characteristic features are swollen, spongy, bleeding gums, perifollicular haemorrhages, corkscrew hairs, and easy bruising","Impaired collagen formation causes poor wound healing, reopening of old scars, and painful subperiosteal or joint haemorrhages","It should be suspected in people with very restricted diets, chronic alcohol use, severe mental illness, isolation, or malabsorption","In infants and children it can cause painful pseudoparalysis from subperiosteal bleeding and bone changes","A normocytic or, with associated deficiencies, mixed anaemia is common","Symptoms respond rapidly and dramatically to restoration of dietary vitamin C, which strongly supports the diagnosis","The diagnosis is largely clinical and dietary, since the vitamin is abundant in fresh fruit and vegetables and deficiency reflects their prolonged absence"],"pathophysiology":"Ascorbic acid is a cofactor for enzymes that hydroxylate proline and lysine residues, a step essential for stable collagen cross-linking. Deficiency yields defective, unstable collagen, weakening blood vessel walls, connective tissue, bone matrix, and wound repair, which manifests as haemorrhage, gum disease, and impaired healing. Vitamin C also acts as an antioxidant and aids intestinal iron absorption, so deficiency can contribute to anaemia. Humans cannot synthesise the vitamin and body stores are limited, so deficiency develops after weeks to months without dietary intake.","additionalDifferentials":["Bleeding disorders and thrombocytopenia","Vasculitis with purpura","Gingivitis or periodontal disease from other causes","Child abuse (mimicked by bone and periosteal changes in children)","Other nutritional deficiencies causing anaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful dietary history for prolonged lack of fruit and vegetables","Plasma or leucocyte ascorbic acid level where available","Full blood count and iron studies for associated anaemia","Coagulation and platelet studies to exclude a primary bleeding disorder","Radiographs in children showing characteristic metaphyseal and subperiosteal changes"],"redFlags":["Significant haemorrhage, including gastrointestinal or intracranial bleeding","Painful pseudoparalysis in a child from subperiosteal haemorrhage","Profound weakness and collapse in advanced deficiency","Coexisting severe malnutrition requiring broader nutritional support"],"prognosis":"Excellent with restoration of vitamin C intake; bleeding and gum changes resolve within days to weeks and full recovery is expected.","pitfalls":["Attributing bleeding gums or purpura to a primary haematological disorder and missing the dietary cause","Failing to take an adequate dietary history in at-risk individuals","Misinterpreting childhood bone changes as trauma or abuse","Overlooking coexisting deficiencies in a globally malnourished patient"],"management":["Restore vitamin C with ascorbic acid replacement (oral where tolerated) as the definitive treatment","Correct the underlying dietary deficit with vitamin C-rich fruit and vegetables and dietitian input","Provide analgesia for painful subperiosteal and joint haemorrhages, with gentle handling of children who have pseudoparalysis","Treat coexisting anaemia, including iron supplementation for associated iron deficiency","Offer gingival and dental care for spongy, bleeding gums","Address contributory factors such as chronic alcohol use, eating disorders, social isolation or malabsorption","Monitor clinical response, expecting rapid resolution of bleeding and gum changes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO nutrition guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"sdh_deficient_gist":{"name":"SDH-deficient gastrointestinal stromal tumour","system":"Oncology / Pathology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["succinate dehydrogenase-deficient GIST","SDH-deficient GIST","paediatric-type GIST","KIT/PDGFRA wild-type SDH-deficient GIST","SDHB-deficient gastrointestinal stromal tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distinct subset of gastrointestinal stromal tumours lacking KIT and PDGFRA mutations, driven instead by loss of succinate dehydrogenase complex function (SDHA, SDHB, SDHC or SDHD)","Almost always arises in the stomach and shows a characteristic multinodular or plexiform growth pattern with epithelioid or mixed cytology","Predominantly affects children, adolescents and young adults, with a female predominance, and is the typical GIST of the young","Loss of SDHB immunohistochemical staining is the unifying diagnostic feature regardless of which subunit gene is affected; loss of SDHA staining specifically indicates an SDHA mutation","Frequently harbours germline SDH mutations, so identification should prompt genetic counselling and evaluation for hereditary paraganglioma-phaeochromocytoma syndromes","Associated with the Carney triad (gastric GIST, paraganglioma and pulmonary chondroma) and the Carney-Stratakis dyad (GIST and paraganglioma with germline SDH mutation)","Behaves differently from conventional GIST, with frequent lymph node metastasis yet often an indolent, protracted course even with metastatic disease","Characteristically resistant to standard imatinib therapy because it does not depend on KIT or PDGFRA signalling"],"pathophysiology":"SDH-deficient GIST results from loss of function of the mitochondrial succinate dehydrogenase complex, through germline or somatic mutation of one of its subunit genes or, in some cases, epigenetic silencing of SDHC. Failure of this enzyme causes accumulation of succinate, which inhibits prolyl hydroxylases and stabilises hypoxia-inducible factors, producing a pseudohypoxic state that drives angiogenesis and tumour growth. Because the disease is independent of KIT and PDGFRA activation, it forms a biologically separate group with distinct morphology, natural history and treatment response.","additionalDifferentials":["Conventional KIT or PDGFRA-mutant gastrointestinal stromal tumour","NF1-associated gastrointestinal stromal tumour","Gastrointestinal leiomyoma or leiomyosarcoma","Gastric schwannoma","Gastric glomus tumour","Neuroendocrine tumour of the stomach","Metastatic paraganglioma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SDHB immunohistochemistry, with SDHA staining to identify SDHA-mutant cases","Mutational testing confirming absence of KIT and PDGFRA mutations","Germline SDH gene testing with referral for genetic counselling","Screening for paraganglioma and phaeochromocytoma, including biochemical catecholamine studies and imaging","Evaluation for pulmonary chondroma when a Carney triad is suspected","Cross-sectional imaging to assess multifocality, nodal and hepatic metastases"],"redFlags":["Young patient with a gastric mass and multifocal or nodal disease","Coexisting paraganglioma or phaeochromocytoma suggesting a hereditary syndrome","Family history of paraganglioma, phaeochromocytoma or GIST","Progressive metastatic disease despite the typically indolent course","Symptomatic gastrointestinal bleeding from an ulcerating gastric tumour"],"prognosis":"SDH-deficient GIST tends to follow an indolent, protracted course, and patients may live for many years even with lymph node or liver metastases; conventional tyrosine kinase inhibitors are largely ineffective, so complete surgical resection is the mainstay, and lifelong surveillance is needed because of multifocality and the risk of associated syndromic tumours.","pitfalls":["Assuming all GISTs will respond to imatinib and overlooking the wild-type, SDH-deficient subset","Not performing SDHB immunohistochemistry in a young patient with a gastric GIST","Failing to recognise the hereditary implications and omitting germline testing and family screening","Applying conventional GIST risk-stratification schemes, which do not reliably predict behaviour in this subset","Interpreting nodal metastasis as necessarily indicating a poor outcome"],"management":["Complete surgical resection is the mainstay, as conventional KIT/PDGFRA-directed tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as imatinib are largely ineffective","For unresectable or metastatic disease, consider antiangiogenic tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as sunitinib or regorafenib","Manage within a specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team","Offer germline SDH subunit gene testing with genetic counselling given the frequent hereditary basis","Screen for associated paraganglioma and phaeochromocytoma with catecholamine biochemistry and imaging, and evaluate for the Carney triad","Undertake lifelong surveillance imaging given the indolent but persistent metastatic potential","Offer cascade screening of at-risk relatives when a germline mutation is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Digestive System Tumours","WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"sdhb_related_paraganglioma":{"name":"SDHB-Related Paraganglioma–Phaeochromocytoma (PGL4)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PGL4","Familial paraganglioma type 4","SDHB-related PPGL"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrine oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by germline loss-of-function variants in SDHB on chromosome 1p36, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with incomplete, age-related penetrance.","Unlike SDHD, the SDHB locus is not imprinted, so disease can arise regardless of which parent transmits the variant.","Carries the highest metastatic potential of all the SDHx genes, with a substantial proportion of tumours developing metastatic disease.","Tumours are predominantly extra-adrenal sympathetic paragangliomas of the retroperitoneum and abdomen, such as at the organ of Zuckerkandl, and are often solitary at presentation.","A noradrenergic and dopaminergic secretory profile is common; raised methoxytyramine is a marker of more aggressive and potentially metastatic behaviour.","Carriers are at increased risk of SDH-deficient renal cell carcinoma and, less commonly, gastrointestinal stromal tumour and pituitary adenoma.","Ga-DOTATATE PET is the preferred functional imaging modality, and loss of SDHB immunostaining confirms the SDH-deficient phenotype."],"pathophysiology":"SDHB encodes the iron–sulphur subunit of mitochondrial complex II. A germline inactivating variant combined with somatic loss of the remaining wild-type allele abolishes succinate dehydrogenase activity and destabilises the assembled complex, so SDHB protein is lost on immunohistochemistry. Accumulated succinate acts as an oncometabolite, inhibiting alpha-ketoglutarate–dependent prolyl hydroxylases, stabilising hypoxia-inducible factors and inducing a pseudohypoxic, pro-angiogenic and hypermethylator phenotype. SDHB-deficient cells appear particularly prone to acquiring an invasive, metastatic phenotype, which underlies the disproportionate malignancy risk of this subtype compared with the other SDHx genes. Tumours arise chiefly in sympathetic paraganglia, explaining the predominance of catecholamine-secreting extra-adrenal lesions rather than parasympathetic head and neck tumours.","additionalDifferentials":["SDHD-related paraganglioma (PGL1), more often multifocal in the head and neck","SDHC-related paraganglioma (PGL3)","Von Hippel–Lindau and MEN2-related phaeochromocytoma","Sporadic extra-adrenal paraganglioma","FH- or MAX-related hereditary phaeochromocytoma","Retroperitoneal soft tissue sarcoma or neuroblastoma in younger patients","Metastatic neuroendocrine tumour of unknown primary"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma free metanephrines including methoxytyramine, the dopamine metabolite","Targeted SDHB germline testing and cascade screening of at-risk relatives","Whole-body MRI or CT given the frequently extra-adrenal and multifocal tumours","Ga-DOTATATE PET for staging and detection of metastatic deposits","SDHB immunohistochemistry on tumour tissue","Renal imaging surveillance for SDH-deficient renal cell carcinoma","A structured lifelong surveillance programme beginning in childhood"],"redFlags":["Biochemical or imaging evidence of metastatic spread such as skeletal, nodal or hepatic deposits","Markedly raised methoxytyramine indicating a dopaminergic and potentially aggressive tumour","Catecholamine crisis with severe hypertension, arrhythmia or cardiomyopathy","A new paraganglioma detected on surveillance in a known carrier"],"prognosis":"SDHB-related tumours carry the least favourable prognosis among the SDHx syndromes because of their high metastatic potential; completely resected localised tumours can do well, but metastatic disease significantly shortens survival and responds only partially to current systemic therapies.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal adrenaline or metanephrine result excludes an active tumour when a dopamine-secreting lesion is present.","Limiting imaging to the abdomen and missing multifocal or metastatic disease elsewhere.","Deferring genetic testing in apparently sporadic, single extra-adrenal paragangliomas, many of which harbour germline SDHB variants.","Underestimating lifelong risk and discontinuing surveillance after apparently curative surgery."],"management":["Complete surgical resection of the paraganglioma or phaeochromocytoma is the definitive treatment","Precede resection of catecholamine-secreting tumours with alpha-adrenergic blockade, adding beta-blockade only once alpha-blockade is established","For metastatic disease, offer radionuclide therapy (I-MIBG or Lu-DOTATATE peptide receptor radionuclide therapy), cytotoxic chemotherapy, or external beam radiotherapy for painful bone deposits","Provide lifelong biochemical surveillance with plasma metanephrines including methoxytyramine, plus whole-body imaging given multifocal and metastatic risk","Include renal imaging surveillance for SDH-deficient renal cell carcinoma","Offer genetic counselling and cascade SDHB testing of at-risk relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance","Manage within an endocrine and clinical genetics multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Endocrinology reference texts","Hereditary endocrine tumour clinical guidelines","Clinical genetics references"],"crossLinks":[]},"sea_snake_envenomation":{"name":"Sea snake envenomation","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sea snake bite","Hydrophiinae envenomation","Enhydrina schistosa bite","marine snake envenomation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / WHO envenoming guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sea snakes are elapids whose bites are often painless with minimal local reaction, so the severity can be underestimated","Systemic effects are delayed, typically appearing after 30 minutes to several hours, so a bite requires prolonged observation","Venom is both neurotoxic (descending flaccid paralysis) and myotoxic, producing muscle pain, stiffness and rhabdomyolysis","Rhabdomyolysis leads to myoglobinuria, hyperkalaemia and acute kidney injury, which are major causes of death","Ptosis, bulbar and respiratory muscle weakness signal neurotoxicity and mandate airway and ventilation readiness","Pressure immobilisation is appropriate as first aid for these neurotoxic elapid bites to slow venom spread","Species-appropriate (sea snake or, where used, tiger snake) antivenom is the definitive treatment for systemic envenomation"],"pathophysiology":"Sea snake venom contains post-synaptic and pre-synaptic neurotoxins that block neuromuscular transmission, producing a descending flaccid paralysis of cranial, bulbar and respiratory muscles similar to terrestrial elapids. Potent myotoxins additionally damage skeletal muscle, causing pain, stiffness and rhabdomyolysis with release of myoglobin and potassium. Myoglobinuria can precipitate acute kidney injury, and hyperkalaemia poses a direct cardiac threat. Local tissue effects are typically minimal, which can mask the seriousness of the systemic envenomation.","additionalDifferentials":["Terrestrial elapid envenomation","Primary neuromuscular disorder (myasthenic or Guillain-Barre-type presentation)","Other cause of rhabdomyolysis","Marine fish spine envenomation","Electrolyte disturbance causing weakness","Non-envenoming (dry) bite with anxiety"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Creatine kinase and serial trends for myotoxicity","Serum potassium and continuous cardiac monitoring for hyperkalaemia","Renal function and urinalysis for myoglobinuria and acute kidney injury","Serial forced vital capacity or spirometry for neurotoxic respiratory decline","Coagulation studies to help distinguish from procoagulant terrestrial snakes","Electrocardiography"],"redFlags":["Progressive bulbar or respiratory muscle weakness","Rising creatine kinase with hyperkalaemia","Myoglobinuria or oliguria indicating acute kidney injury","Cardiac arrhythmia from electrolyte disturbance","Delayed presentation with evolving systemic features"],"prognosis":"With early pressure immobilisation, timely antivenom, ventilatory support and management of rhabdomyolysis and hyperkalaemia, recovery is usually complete. Delay increases the risk of fatal respiratory failure, hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest or renal failure.","pitfalls":["Underestimating a painless bite and discharging before delayed neurotoxicity and myotoxicity are excluded","Missing hyperkalaemia from rhabdomyolysis, risking cardiac arrest","Failing to protect the airway as bulbar and respiratory weakness progress","Applying arterial tourniquets or wound incision instead of appropriate pressure immobilisation"],"management":["Apply pressure immobilisation as first aid to slow venom spread","Administer specific sea snake or appropriate polyvalent antivenom as the definitive treatment for systemic envenomation","Provide airway protection and mechanical ventilation for progressive bulbar and respiratory paralysis","Give intravenous fluids to manage rhabdomyolysis and myoglobinuria and protect renal function, with renal replacement therapy if acute kidney injury supervenes","Treat hyperkalaemia and maintain continuous cardiac monitoring","Observe for a prolonged period given delayed systemic effects, with serial neurological assessment and creatine kinase trends","Escalate to critical care for ventilatory failure or refractory hyperkalaemia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Guidelines for the management of snakebite envenoming","Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"],"crossLinks":[]},"sebaceous_carcinoma":{"name":"Sebaceous carcinoma","system":"Dermatology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sebaceous gland carcinoma","sebaceous adenocarcinoma","meibomian gland carcinoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / WHO Classification of Skin Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A malignant tumour of sebaceous differentiation that most often arises in the periocular region from meibomian and Zeis glands, but also occurs at extraocular sites","Periocular lesions notoriously masquerade as a recurrent chalazion or chronic blepharoconjunctivitis, so a firm or recurrent eyelid lesion warrants biopsy","Pagetoid intraepithelial spread through the overlying epidermis or conjunctiva is characteristic and contributes to multifocal disease and margin difficulty","Histology shows lobules of atypical cells with foamy, vacuolated lipid-laden cytoplasm; lipid stains on fresh tissue and markers such as adipophilin and EMA support the diagnosis","Association with Muir-Torre syndrome links sebaceous neoplasms to mismatch-repair deficiency and internal (often colorectal and genitourinary) malignancy","Immunohistochemistry for mismatch-repair proteins can flag Muir-Torre syndrome and should prompt consideration of hereditary cancer screening","Treatment is complete surgical excision, often with Mohs micrographic surgery or wide excision with map (conjunctival) biopsies, and radiotherapy in selected cases"],"pathophysiology":"Sebaceous carcinoma is a malignant neoplasm showing sebaceous differentiation, thought to arise from the adnexal sebaceous glands, prominently the meibomian and Zeis glands of the eyelid. Tumour cells proliferate as lobules and sheets with atypical nuclei and characteristic finely vacuolated cytoplasm reflecting intracytoplasmic lipid. A distinctive feature is pagetoid spread, in which malignant cells migrate individually through the overlying epithelium of the skin or conjunctiva, producing clinically deceptive diffuse involvement and complicating margin assessment. A subset arises in the context of Muir-Torre syndrome, a phenotypic variant of hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer, where germline mismatch-repair deficiency underlies both the sebaceous neoplasia and associated visceral cancers.","additionalDifferentials":["Chalazion or recurrent chalazion","Chronic blepharitis or blepharoconjunctivitis","Basal cell carcinoma of the eyelid","Squamous cell carcinoma and its in-situ forms","Sebaceous adenoma and sebaceoma","Ocular surface squamous neoplasia (for conjunctival pagetoid spread)","Amelanotic melanoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full-thickness or incisional biopsy of any suspicious or recurrent eyelid lesion","Lipid staining on fresh (frozen) tissue and immunohistochemistry including adipophilin and EMA","Map (multiple conjunctival) biopsies to delineate pagetoid intraepithelial spread","Mismatch-repair protein immunohistochemistry and consideration of Muir-Torre / hereditary cancer evaluation","Regional nodal assessment and imaging for staging in higher-risk tumours","Ophthalmic examination to assess conjunctival and orbital involvement"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or atypical chalazion that fails to resolve or recurs after drainage","Unilateral chronic blepharoconjunctivitis unresponsive to conventional treatment","Loss of eyelash follicles (madarosis) or eyelid architecture over a firm lesion","Diffuse conjunctival injection with an underlying eyelid mass suggesting pagetoid spread","Personal or family history suggesting Muir-Torre syndrome and internal malignancy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on tumour size, extent of pagetoid spread, and stage; periocular tumours can recur locally and metastasise to regional nodes, and delayed diagnosis from chalazion masquerade worsens outcome. Complete excision of localised disease carries a reasonable prognosis, but advanced or metastatic disease is associated with significant mortality.","pitfalls":["Treating a recurrent eyelid lesion repeatedly as a chalazion without biopsy","Underestimating margins because of occult pagetoid intraepithelial spread","Failing to test for mismatch-repair deficiency and missing an underlying Muir-Torre syndrome","Overlooking multifocal or extraocular disease in the staging assessment"],"management":["Complete surgical excision by wide local excision or Mohs micrographic surgery is the mainstay of treatment","Perform map (conjunctival) biopsies to delineate pagetoid intraepithelial spread and guide margins","Consider adjuvant radiotherapy for extensive, incompletely resected or high-risk tumours","Assess regional nodes and treat nodal metastases with dissection where present","Test tumour mismatch-repair proteins and evaluate for Muir-Torre syndrome, with hereditary colorectal and genitourinary cancer screening and genetic counselling where indicated","Manage within a multidisciplinary team including ophthalmic and dermatological oncology","Undertake long-term surveillance for local recurrence and regional metastasis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","WHO Classification of Skin Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"sebaceous_carcinoma_eyelid":{"name":"Eyelid sebaceous gland carcinoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmic Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["eyelid sebaceous carcinoma","meibomian gland carcinoma","periocular sebaceous carcinoma","sebaceous gland carcinoma of the eyelid"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / WHO Classification of Eye Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An aggressive periocular malignancy arising chiefly from the meibomian glands of the tarsus and the glands of Zeis, favouring the upper eyelid","It is the great masquerader of the eyelid, mimicking a recurrent chalazion or chronic unilateral blepharoconjunctivitis and thereby delaying diagnosis","A firm, yellowish, or recurrent eyelid nodule, loss of eyelashes (madarosis), and destruction of the meibomian gland orifices should raise suspicion","Pagetoid spread of malignant cells through the conjunctival and eyelid epithelium produces diffuse, clinically deceptive involvement and complicates margin control","Histology shows lobules of atypical cells with foamy lipid-laden cytoplasm; lipid stains on fresh tissue and markers such as adipophilin and EMA support the diagnosis","Management is complete excision, often Mohs micrographic surgery or wide excision, with map (conjunctival) biopsies to map pagetoid spread and radiotherapy in selected cases","An association with Muir-Torre syndrome links the tumour to mismatch-repair deficiency and internal malignancy, warranting consideration of hereditary cancer screening"],"pathophysiology":"Eyelid sebaceous gland carcinoma is a malignant tumour of sebaceous differentiation arising predominantly from the meibomian glands within the tarsal plate and the glands of Zeis at the lid margin, with the upper eyelid most often affected. Malignant cells proliferate as lobules and sheets with atypical nuclei and characteristic finely vacuolated cytoplasm reflecting intracytoplasmic lipid. A defining behaviour is pagetoid intraepithelial spread, in which individual malignant cells migrate through the overlying conjunctival and epidermal epithelium, producing diffuse periocular involvement that mimics inflammatory disease and obscures the true tumour extent. A subset occurs within Muir-Torre syndrome, where germline mismatch-repair deficiency predisposes to both the sebaceous neoplasia and associated visceral cancers. The tumour spreads to regional preauricular and cervical nodes and can invade the orbit.","additionalDifferentials":["Chalazion or recurrent chalazion","Chronic unilateral blepharitis or blepharoconjunctivitis","Basal cell carcinoma of the eyelid","Squamous cell carcinoma of the eyelid or ocular surface squamous neoplasia","Sebaceous adenoma and sebaceoma","Ocular cicatricial pemphigoid (for chronic conjunctival inflammation)","Amelanotic conjunctival or eyelid melanoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full-thickness eyelid biopsy of any suspicious or recurrent lesion","Lipid staining on fresh (frozen) tissue and immunohistochemistry including adipophilin and EMA","Map (multiple conjunctival) biopsies to delineate pagetoid intraepithelial spread","Mismatch-repair protein immunohistochemistry and consideration of Muir-Torre evaluation","Regional lymph node assessment (preauricular and cervical) and orbital imaging for staging","Ophthalmic examination to assess conjunctival and orbital involvement"],"redFlags":["A recurrent chalazion that fails to resolve or recurs after drainage","Chronic unilateral blepharoconjunctivitis unresponsive to conventional treatment","Madarosis or destruction of eyelid margin architecture over a firm lesion","Diffuse conjunctival injection suggesting pagetoid spread or orbital signs suggesting invasion","Personal or family history pointing to Muir-Torre syndrome"],"prognosis":"Eyelid sebaceous carcinoma is aggressive, and prognosis worsens with delayed diagnosis, larger tumours, extensive pagetoid spread, and orbital or nodal involvement; complete excision of localised disease gives a reasonable outlook, but regional or distant spread carries significant mortality.","pitfalls":["Treating a recurrent eyelid lesion repeatedly as a chalazion without biopsy and delaying diagnosis","Underestimating margins because of occult pagetoid intraepithelial spread","Failing to test for mismatch-repair deficiency and missing an underlying Muir-Torre syndrome","Overlooking orbital or nodal involvement during staging"],"management":["Complete excision by Mohs micrographic surgery or wide local excision with margin control is the mainstay","Use map conjunctival biopsies to define pagetoid spread, and topical mitomycin C or cryotherapy for diffuse conjunctival intraepithelial involvement","Reconstruct the eyelid after excision and reserve orbital exenteration for extensive orbital invasion","Consider radiotherapy for inoperable disease or as adjuvant treatment in selected cases","Assess preauricular and cervical nodes and treat nodal disease","Test mismatch-repair proteins and evaluate for Muir-Torre syndrome with hereditary cancer screening and genetic counselling","Manage within an ophthalmic oncology multidisciplinary team with long-term surveillance for recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","WHO Classification of Eye Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"sebaceous_hyperplasia":{"name":"sebaceous hyperplasia","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["senile sebaceous hyperplasia","premature sebaceous gland hyperplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as soft, yellowish, umbilicated papules on the forehead, cheeks and nose of middle-aged and older adults.","The central umbilication corresponds to a dilated sebaceous duct surrounded by enlarged sebaceous lobules, giving a doughnut-like configuration.","Lesions are often multiple in individuals with seborrhoeic, sun-damaged skin and in those on long-term ciclosporin after organ transplantation.","Dermoscopy shows a central crater with surrounding cream-yellow lobules and a 'crown' of unbranched or minimally branched peripheral vessels.","The crown vessels do not cross the midline of the lesion, helping to distinguish it from the arborising telangiectasia of basal cell carcinoma.","The main clinical challenge is differentiation from basal cell carcinoma, which may require dermoscopy or biopsy.","Treatment is for cosmetic reasons only and includes light electrodesiccation, laser, photodynamic therapy or topical/oral retinoids.","Widespread early-onset lesions can be a feature of the Muir-Torre syndrome, particularly when sebaceous adenomas or carcinomas coexist."],"pathophysiology":"Sebaceous hyperplasia represents a benign enlargement of otherwise normal sebaceous glands, in which numerous mature sebaceous lobules cluster around a centrally dilated excretory duct. The process reflects reduced epidermal cell turnover and diminished androgen-driven basal sebocyte replacement with ageing, causing retention and accumulation of crowded mature sebocytes. Chronic ultraviolet exposure and immunosuppression, especially ciclosporin, accelerate the process. Although a benign hamartomatous proliferation, extensive and early lesions may reflect the sebaceous gland dysregulation seen in the Muir-Torre variant of Lynch syndrome, mediated by mismatch-repair deficiency.","additionalDifferentials":["basal cell carcinoma","sebaceous adenoma","molluscum contagiosum","intradermal melanocytic naevus","fibrous papule of the face","trichoepithelioma","sebaceous carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["dermoscopy to demonstrate crown vessels and central umbilication","punch or shave biopsy where basal cell carcinoma cannot be excluded","histopathology confirming enlarged mature sebaceous lobules","assessment for Muir-Torre syndrome in extensive or early-onset disease","mismatch-repair immunohistochemistry on sebaceous neoplasms when syndrome suspected"],"redFlags":["arborising telangiectasia, pearly rolled border or ulceration suggesting basal cell carcinoma","numerous sebaceous lesions at a young age suggesting Muir-Torre syndrome","a firm, rapidly growing or bleeding lesion raising concern for sebaceous carcinoma","coexisting visceral malignancy or strong family history of colorectal cancer"],"prognosis":"Sebaceous hyperplasia is entirely benign and of cosmetic significance only, although individual lesions persist and new ones accumulate with age. Recurrence after superficial destruction is common because the deeper gland is often incompletely removed.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing basal cell carcinoma as sebaceous hyperplasia and delaying appropriate treatment.","Failing to recognise the Muir-Torre association in patients with multiple or early lesions.","Aggressive destructive treatment causing scarring for a purely cosmetic lesion.","Confusing lesions with molluscum contagiosum in immunosuppressed patients."],"management":["Reassure, as the condition is benign and treatment is for cosmetic reasons only","Offer physical ablation of troublesome lesions by electrocautery/electrodesiccation, cryotherapy or laser","Consider topical retinoids or, for extensive disease, oral isotretinoin, recognising lesions tend to recur","In transplant recipients, discuss modifying ciclosporin-based immunosuppression with the transplant team","Advise photoprotection to limit ultraviolet-driven new lesions","Exclude basal cell carcinoma with dermoscopy or biopsy where the diagnosis is uncertain","Evaluate for Muir-Torre syndrome in early-onset or extensive disease, with mismatch-repair immunohistochemistry"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology","Weedon's Skin Pathology"],"crossLinks":[]},"seborrheic_dermatitis":{"name":"Seborrheic dermatitis","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["seborrhoeic dermatitis","seborrhoeic eczema","dandruff (mild scalp form)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as ill-defined erythematous patches with greasy yellowish scale in sebum-rich areas: scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, ears, and central chest","Mild scalp involvement manifests simply as dandruff, while more severe disease produces inflamed, scaly, sometimes itchy plaques","It has a characteristic bimodal age pattern, occurring in infants (including cradle cap) and again in adults","The condition is linked to the commensal yeast Malassezia, so antifungal agents are a mainstay of treatment","Management combines antifungal shampoos or creams with short courses of low-potency topical corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors for inflammation, and recognises that it is a relapsing condition needing maintenance","Unusually severe, extensive, or treatment-resistant seborrhoeic dermatitis should prompt consideration of underlying HIV infection or Parkinson's disease","Distinguish it from psoriasis (sharper, thicker, silvery plaques) and from atopic or contact dermatitis based on distribution and morphology"],"pathophysiology":"Seborrhoeic dermatitis is a chronic inflammatory disorder occurring in sebaceous-rich skin, driven by an abnormal host inflammatory response to Malassezia yeasts that are part of the normal skin flora. The yeasts metabolise sebum lipids, releasing free fatty acids and other products that irritate the skin and provoke inflammation in susceptible individuals. Sebaceous gland activity, an altered skin barrier, individual immune reactivity, and neurological or immunosuppressive comorbidities all influence expression. This explains its predilection for sebum-rich sites, its association with hormonal activity in infancy and adulthood, and its increased severity in HIV infection and Parkinson's disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Psoriasis (particularly scalp and flexural)","Atopic dermatitis","Contact dermatitis","Tinea capitis or tinea faciei","Rosacea (facial involvement)","Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus in atypical facial cases"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on distribution and greasy scale","Skin scrapings for mycology if dermatophyte infection is a consideration","HIV testing when disease is severe, sudden, or refractory","Skin biopsy in atypical or diagnostically uncertain presentations","Assessment for neurological comorbidity such as Parkinson's disease when relevant"],"redFlags":["Severe, extensive, or abrupt-onset disease prompting evaluation for immunosuppression such as HIV","Erythroderma with widespread involvement","Failure to respond to appropriate first-line therapy suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Secondary bacterial infection of excoriated or fissured plaques"],"prognosis":"Seborrhoeic dermatitis is a benign but chronic relapsing condition; infantile disease usually resolves within months, whereas adult disease tends to persist with periodic flares and is controlled rather than cured.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with psoriasis or dermatophyte infection and choosing the wrong therapy","Relying on topical corticosteroids alone and neglecting antifungal treatment of the yeast component","Overusing potent steroids on the face, risking atrophy and rosacea-like changes","Failing to investigate unusually severe or refractory disease for underlying HIV or neurological disease"],"management":["Treat with topical antifungal agents such as ketoconazole or ciclopirox and medicated shampoos containing selenium sulphide, zinc pyrithione or coal tar as the mainstay","Add short courses of low-potency topical corticosteroids or topical calcineurin inhibitors to settle inflammation","Manage infantile cradle cap with emollients, gentle descaling and mild antifungal preparations","Use maintenance antifungal therapy to control this relapsing condition","Consider systemic antifungal therapy for severe or widespread disease","Investigate for underlying HIV infection or Parkinson's disease when disease is severe, sudden or treatment-resistant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"seborrhoeic_keratosis":{"name":"seborrhoeic keratosis","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["seborrhoeic wart","basal cell papilloma","senile wart"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a well-demarcated, 'stuck-on' waxy or verrucous papule or plaque, most often on the trunk, face and scalp of middle-aged and older adults.","Colour ranges from pale tan through brown to almost black; heavily pigmented lesions on the face are a common source of melanoma referral.","Surface horn cysts (keratin-filled pseudocysts) and comedo-like plugged follicular openings are characteristic and best appreciated on dermoscopy.","Dermoscopic hallmarks include milia-like cysts, comedo-like openings, a cerebriform 'brain-like' pattern, fat fingers and sharply demarcated moth-eaten borders.","The dermatosis papulosa nigra and stucco keratosis variants represent phenotypic subtypes seen respectively in darker skin and on the distal lower limbs.","The sudden eruption of numerous lesions with pruritus (the sign of Leser-Trelat) may rarely signal an internal adenocarcinoma and warrants malignancy screening.","Irritated lesions can become inflamed, crusted and clinically alarming, mimicking melanoma or squamous cell carcinoma.","No treatment is needed for asymptomatic lesions; cryotherapy, curettage or shave excision are used for symptomatic or diagnostically uncertain lesions, with histology if malignancy is suspected."],"pathophysiology":"Seborrhoeic keratoses are benign epidermal proliferations composed of monomorphous basaloid keratinocytes with variable squamous differentiation, forming acanthotic plaques containing horn cysts and pseudo-horn cysts. Activating somatic mutations in FGFR3 and in the PIK3CA gene are frequently identified, indicating clonal keratinocyte expansion, yet the lesions have negligible malignant potential. Accumulation of melanin within the proliferating keratinocytes accounts for the variable pigmentation. Their prevalence rises steeply with age and there is a clear familial predisposition, with ultraviolet exposure implicated in facial and truncal lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["melanocytic naevus","malignant melanoma (particularly heavily pigmented or irritated lesions)","pigmented basal cell carcinoma","viral wart","actinic keratosis","solar lentigo","epidermal naevus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["dermoscopy to identify milia-like cysts and comedo-like openings","clinical photography for lesion monitoring","shave or punch biopsy of atypical lesions","excisional biopsy with histopathology where melanoma cannot be excluded","screening for occult malignancy if the sign of Leser-Trelat is suspected"],"redFlags":["rapid eruption of multiple lesions with pruritus (sign of Leser-Trelat) prompting a search for internal malignancy","asymmetry, irregular pigment network or ulceration suggesting melanoma","recent change in size, colour or bleeding within a pre-existing lesion","a solitary rapidly enlarging keratotic nodule raising concern for squamous cell carcinoma"],"prognosis":"Seborrhoeic keratoses are entirely benign with no intrinsic malignant potential, although individual lesions persist indefinitely and new ones accumulate with age. Recurrence after superficial removal is common if the lesion is incompletely destroyed.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing a heavily pigmented or irritated lesion as benign when it is actually melanoma, or vice versa, leading to inappropriate management.","Destroying a lesion by cryotherapy or curettage without histology when the clinical diagnosis is uncertain, thereby losing the specimen.","Overlooking the sign of Leser-Trelat and failing to investigate for an underlying adenocarcinoma.","Confusing a solitary lesion with a viral wart and applying inappropriate keratolytic treatment."],"management":["Reassure and observe, as lesions are entirely benign with no malignant potential","Remove symptomatic, irritated or cosmetically unwanted lesions by cryotherapy, curettage or shave excision, or electrocautery","Send tissue for histopathology whenever melanoma or another pigmented malignancy cannot be excluded","Take a diagnostic biopsy of atypical lesions rather than destroying them blindly","Investigate for an occult internal adenocarcinoma when a sudden eruption suggests the sign of Leser-Trelat","Advise that new lesions accumulate with age and that superficially treated lesions may recur"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology","British Association of Dermatologists guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"seckel_syndrome":{"name":"Seckel syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["microcephalic primordial dwarfism","bird-headed dwarfism","nanocephalic dwarfism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A microcephalic primordial dwarfism with severe proportionate pre- and postnatal growth restriction and marked microcephaly","The characteristic facies is often described as bird-headed, with a receding forehead, prominent beaked nose, micrognathia, and low-set dysmorphic ears","Intellectual disability is usual and typically moderate to severe, reflecting the profoundly small brain","Genetically heterogeneous, caused by biallelic variants in genes of the DNA damage response and centrosome/replication machinery, most classically ATR, with several other loci implicated","Inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern, so consanguinity increases risk and recurrence risk to siblings is significant","Haematological complications, including cytopenias and a reported risk of myelodysplasia or leukaemia, can occur and warrant monitoring","It overlaps with other microcephalic primordial dwarfisms such as MOPD, so precise molecular diagnosis guides prognosis and counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Seckel syndrome is a genetically heterogeneous microcephalic primordial dwarfism arising from biallelic variants in genes controlling the DNA damage response, DNA replication, and centrosome function, of which ATR is the prototype. Impaired handling of replication stress and defective cell-cycle control reduce the number of cell divisions during development, most conspicuously in the brain, producing severe proportionate growth restriction and profound microcephaly. The same genome-maintenance defects underlie the associated haematological and other systemic complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Microcephalic osteodysplastic primordial dwarfism (MOPD types)","Meier-Gorlin syndrome","Primary autosomal recessive microcephaly (MCPH)","Cornelia de Lange syndrome","Silver-Russell syndrome","Fetal alcohol spectrum disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gene panel or exome sequencing covering ATR and other primordial dwarfism/DNA repair genes","Chromosomal microarray to exclude copy-number causes","Serial head circumference and growth measurement to characterise proportionate restriction","Full blood count monitoring for cytopenias and haematological malignancy risk","Brain imaging to assess microcephaly and any structural anomaly","Developmental and neuropsychological assessment"],"redFlags":["Unexplained cytopenias or features suggesting myelodysplasia or leukaemia","Signs of an intracranial vascular anomaly, which have been reported in some primordial dwarfisms","Feeding failure and severe faltering growth requiring nutritional support","New neurological deficit prompting imaging"],"prognosis":"Growth restriction and microcephaly are lifelong and stature remains far below normal, while cognitive impairment is usually significant. Prognosis is influenced by the specific gene involved and by haematological and other systemic complications, and survival into adulthood is possible with supportive care.","pitfalls":["Grouping all microcephalic primordial dwarfisms together without molecular confirmation, obscuring differing risks","Overlooking evolving haematological complications","Attributing the phenotype to intrauterine environmental factors and missing the recessive genetic cause","Failing to counsel consanguineous families about substantial recurrence risk"],"management":["Provide supportive, multidisciplinary care as there is no disease-specific or curative therapy","Offer developmental, educational and physiotherapy or occupational support for intellectual disability","Ensure nutritional support and monitor growth, recognising growth hormone is generally ineffective in this primordial dwarfism","Undertake haematological surveillance with regular full blood counts for cytopenias, myelodysplasia and leukaemia","Manage systemic and structural complications as they arise, involving relevant specialists","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance, addressing consanguinity and sibling recurrence risk","Provide family screening and reproductive counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Microcephalic Primordial Dwarfism / Seckel Syndrome Spectrum"],"crossLinks":[]},"secondary_peritonitis":{"name":"Secondary peritonitis","system":"GI / Peritoneum / Surgery","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute secondary peritonitis","surgical peritonitis","generalised peritonitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from bacterial contamination of the peritoneal cavity following perforation, ischaemia, or inflammation of an abdominal viscus, unlike primary (spontaneous) peritonitis","Presents with severe, constant abdominal pain worsened by movement, with tenderness, guarding, rigidity, and rebound, and the patient characteristically lies still","Common sources include perforated peptic ulcer, perforated appendix, perforated diverticular disease, and bowel ischaemia or anastomotic leak","Systemic features of sepsis develop rapidly: fever, tachycardia, hypotension, oliguria, and, if untreated, septic shock and multi-organ failure","The management triad is aggressive resuscitation, appropriate broad-spectrum antibiotics, and prompt source control of the underlying cause","Source control, achieved surgically or by radiological drainage, is the definitive step; antibiotics and resuscitation support but do not replace it","Faeculent peritonitis from colonic perforation carries a particularly heavy bacterial and endotoxin load and a high mortality","Peritoneal contamination is often graded (localised, diffuse, purulent, faeculent) and guides the extent of surgery and lavage"],"pathophysiology":"Secondary peritonitis is inflammation of the peritoneum caused by spillage of gastrointestinal contents and micro-organisms into the peritoneal cavity following a breach of a hollow viscus or transmural necrosis. Gut flora, typically a mix of coliforms and anaerobes, provoke an intense inflammatory response with fibrinopurulent exudate, large third-space fluid losses, and paralytic ileus. Absorption of bacteria and endotoxin across the vast peritoneal surface drives systemic inflammatory response, sepsis, and septic shock. The peritoneum may localise infection into abscesses, but diffuse contamination overwhelms these defences and threatens life.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary (spontaneous bacterial) peritonitis","Acute pancreatitis","Mesenteric ischaemia","Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm","Intra-abdominal abscess without generalised peritonitis","Medical causes of acute abdomen (diabetic ketoacidosis, inferior myocardial infarction)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bloods including full blood count, urea and electrolytes, CRP, amylase or lipase, blood cultures, and lactate","Arterial blood gas to assess acid-base status and organ perfusion","Erect chest radiograph for free subdiaphragmatic gas","CT abdomen and pelvis to identify the source and any collections","Peritoneal fluid sampling and culture when the diagnosis or source is unclear"],"redFlags":["Generalised peritonitis with sepsis, hypotension, or rising lactate indicating septic shock (surgical emergency)","Faeculent contamination from colonic perforation with heavy sepsis burden","Progressive organ dysfunction (oliguria, confusion, respiratory compromise)","Failure to improve after resuscitation, indicating inadequate source control"],"prognosis":"Outcome hinges on early recognition, prompt resuscitation, and timely source control; delay, faeculent contamination, advanced age, and comorbidity markedly increase mortality, which remains high in established diffuse peritonitis with septic shock.","pitfalls":["Delaying source control while awaiting response to antibiotics and fluids","Under-resuscitating a patient with large third-space and septic losses","Attributing peritonism to a medical cause and missing a surgical source","Inadequate peritoneal lavage or failing to address ongoing contamination at surgery"],"management":["Resuscitate aggressively with intravenous fluids and correct electrolyte and acid-base derangement","Start empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics covering enteric Gram-negative coliforms and anaerobes","Achieve definitive source control surgically (repair, resection, washout) or by radiological drainage of collections","Decompress the gut with a nasogastric tube for ileus and provide adequate analgesia","Escalate to critical care with vasopressor and organ support for sepsis and septic shock","Provide nutritional support during recovery","Reassess and re-image for persistent or recurrent collections requiring further drainage"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"secretory_carcinoma_salivary":{"name":"Secretory carcinoma of salivary gland","system":"Head and neck / Salivary glands","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mammary analogue secretory carcinoma","MASC"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A salivary malignancy defined by an ETV6-NTRK3 gene fusion, morphologically analogous to secretory carcinoma of the breast.","It most often arises in the parotid but also occurs in the intraoral minor glands, typically as a slow-growing painless mass.","Many cases were historically misclassified as acinic cell carcinoma before the fusion was recognised.","Histology shows microcystic and tubular structures with abundant eosinophilic intraluminal secretions and vacuolated cytoplasm, but no true zymogen granules.","The immunophenotype is characteristic: S100, mammaglobin and SOX10 positive, with DOG1 negative, which helps separate it from acinic cell carcinoma.","The defining ETV6-NTRK3 fusion, or variant fusions such as ETV6-RET, can be demonstrated by fusion-directed molecular testing.","The NTRK fusion is therapeutically important, as it renders advanced disease potentially responsive to tropomyosin receptor kinase inhibitors.","Most tumours are indolent, but high-grade transformation is described and carries a worse outlook."],"pathophysiology":"Secretory carcinoma is driven by a balanced translocation that fuses the ETV6 gene with NTRK3, generating a chimeric protein with constitutively active tropomyosin receptor kinase signalling that promotes proliferation and survival through downstream RAS-MAPK and PI3K pathways. The neoplastic cells display apocrine-type secretory differentiation, producing abundant intraluminal and intracytoplasmic eosinophilic secretory material within microcystic, tubular and solid architecture, closely mirroring secretory carcinoma of the breast. Unlike acinic cell carcinoma, the cells do not form true serous zymogen granules, which is reflected in a DOG1-negative, S100- and mammaglobin-positive immunophenotype. The presence of an actionable kinase fusion links the tumour's biology directly to targeted therapy, and progression is usually slow unless high-grade transformation supervenes.","additionalDifferentials":["Acinic cell carcinoma","Low-grade mucoepidermoid carcinoma","Cystadenocarcinoma","Polymorphous adenocarcinoma","Adenocarcinoma not otherwise specified","Salivary duct carcinoma (high-grade lesions)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fine-needle aspiration cytology","Core or excisional biopsy for histology","Immunohistochemistry including S100, mammaglobin, SOX10 and DOG1","Molecular testing for ETV6-NTRK3 and variant fusions","MRI of the primary with ultrasound or CT of the neck for staging","NTRK fusion testing to determine eligibility for targeted therapy in advanced disease"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth or new pain","Histological features of high-grade transformation","Facial nerve weakness or palsy","Cervical nodal or distant metastasis","Recurrence after apparently complete excision"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally favourable for conventional low-grade tumours treated with complete excision. Identification of the NTRK fusion offers a targeted therapy option for recurrent or metastatic disease, whereas high-grade transformation worsens the outlook.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as acinic cell carcinoma and thereby overlooking a targetable NTRK fusion","Failing to perform NTRK or fusion testing in advanced or recurrent cases","Misinterpreting DOG1 or S100 staining and reaching the wrong diagnosis","Underrecognising high-grade transformation on limited sampling"],"management":["Complete surgical excision, by parotidectomy with facial nerve preservation or resection of the involved minor gland, is the mainstay","Perform neck dissection for clinically or radiologically involved nodes","Give adjuvant radiotherapy for high-grade transformation, positive margins or other adverse features","For recurrent or metastatic disease, use NTRK-targeted inhibitors such as larotrectinib or entrectinib, guided by confirmation of the ETV6-NTRK3 fusion","Manage within a head and neck oncology multidisciplinary team","Undertake long-term surveillance for local recurrence and metastasis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","Gnepp's Diagnostic Surgical Pathology of the Head and Neck","Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"sedation_complications":{"name":"Sedation complications","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["procedural sedation complications","conscious sedation complications","sedation-related adverse events"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sedation exists on a continuum from minimal sedation to general anaesthesia, and patients can drift into a deeper level than intended, so providers must be able to rescue from the next deeper level","The most common serious complications are respiratory: airway obstruction, hypoventilation, apnoea, and hypoxaemia, which can progress rapidly if unrecognised","Cardiovascular effects include hypotension and bradycardia, and combining sedatives with opioids markedly increases the risk of respiratory depression","Continuous monitoring of oxygenation with pulse oximetry and of ventilation with capnography, plus regular assessment of conscious level, is essential throughout and during recovery","Appropriate patient selection and pre-procedure assessment (airway, comorbidities, aspiration risk, and fasting status) reduce complications","Resuscitation equipment, oxygen, suction, and reversal agents must be immediately available, and staff must be trained to manage airway and cardiovascular emergencies","Reversal agents (such as an opioid antagonist for opioids and a benzodiazepine antagonist for benzodiazepines) can be used, but their duration may be shorter than the sedative, risking re-sedation","Elderly, frail, obese, and sleep-apnoea patients, and those at the extremes of age, are especially vulnerable and warrant conservative dosing and heightened vigilance"],"pathophysiology":"Sedative and analgesic agents depress the central nervous system in a dose-dependent way, reducing conscious level, blunting protective airway reflexes, and depressing respiratory drive and upper airway muscle tone. Loss of pharyngeal tone causes obstruction, while reduced central drive causes hypoventilation and apnoea, together producing hypoxaemia and hypercapnia. Many agents also cause vasodilation and myocardial depression, leading to hypotension, and some blunt compensatory reflexes. Because effects are additive, combinations of sedatives and opioids produce disproportionate respiratory and cardiovascular depression. Depressed reflexes also raise the risk of aspiration in patients with a full stomach.","additionalDifferentials":["Airway obstruction from other causes","Aspiration of gastric contents","Anaphylaxis to a sedative agent","Underlying cardiac or respiratory disease causing deterioration","Hypoglycaemia or metabolic disturbance altering consciousness","Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity when combined with local blocks"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous pulse oximetry and capnography during sedation and recovery","Regular assessment of sedation depth and responsiveness","Blood pressure and ECG monitoring appropriate to patient risk","Arterial or capillary blood gas if significant hypoventilation is suspected","Glucose check if altered consciousness is unexplained"],"redFlags":["Apnoea, severe hypoventilation, or airway obstruction with falling oxygen saturation","Unintended progression to deep sedation or general anaesthesia without rescue capability","Significant hypotension or bradycardia","Aspiration during sedation in an at-risk patient"],"prognosis":"With appropriate patient selection, monitoring, and prompt rescue, procedural sedation is generally safe; unrecognised respiratory depression can cause hypoxic injury or death, and most serious events are linked to inadequate monitoring or rescue.","pitfalls":["Providing sedation without the skills or equipment to rescue a deeper-than-intended level","Relying on oximetry alone and missing early hypoventilation that capnography would detect","Underestimating the additive respiratory depression of sedative-opioid combinations","Assuming a reversal agent fully solves the problem despite its shorter duration and risk of re-sedation","Inadequate pre-procedure assessment of airway, comorbidity, and aspiration risk"],"management":["Prevent complications through pre-procedure assessment of airway, comorbidity, aspiration risk and fasting status, with appropriate patient selection","Monitor oxygenation with pulse oximetry and ventilation with capnography, and assess conscious level throughout the procedure and recovery","Manage respiratory depression with supplemental oxygen, airway-opening manoeuvres, airway adjuncts and assisted ventilation","Administer reversal agents where appropriate, naloxone for opioids and flumazenil for benzodiazepines","Support the circulation with intravenous fluids and vasopressors for hypotension and an antimuscarinic such as atropine for bradycardia","Ensure immediate availability of oxygen, suction and resuscitation and airway equipment, with staff trained to rescue from a deeper-than-intended level of sedation","Escalate to anaesthetic and critical care support for failure to rescue or aspiration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"see_saw_nystagmus":{"name":"See-saw nystagmus","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["seesaw nystagmus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distinctive nystagmus in which one eye rises and intorts while the other falls and extorts, then the cycle reverses, resembling a see-saw","The pendular form is classically associated with parasellar or suprasellar masses compressing the chiasm and optic pathways","A bitemporal hemianopia frequently accompanies the pendular form because of chiasmal involvement","The jerk (hemi-see-saw) form points to a mesodiencephalic lesion, often in the region of the interstitial nucleus of Cajal","It may also occur with severe congenital visual loss and some retinal dystrophies","MRI directed at the sellar, suprasellar and mesodiencephalic regions is central to the workup"],"pathophysiology":"See-saw nystagmus arises from disruption of the vestibulo-ocular and visual pathways that coordinate torsional and vertical eye movements, particularly around the diencephalon and rostral midbrain. The pendular form is linked to large parasellar or suprasellar lesions that damage the chiasm and adjacent structures, plausibly removing visual and vestibular stabilising signals, whereas the jerk hemi-see-saw form reflects a focal lesion of the interstitial nucleus of Cajal and its connections. The result is the conjugate torsional and disjunctive vertical oscillation that defines the sign.","additionalDifferentials":["Pituitary or other parasellar mass","Craniopharyngioma","Mesodiencephalic stroke or lesion","Congenital or retinal-dystrophy-associated nystagmus","Other acquired pendular nystagmus (for example demyelinating)","Oculopalatal tremor"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain focused on the sellar, suprasellar and mesodiencephalic regions","Formal visual field testing for a bitemporal hemianopia","Neuro-ophthalmological and orthoptic assessment","Endocrine evaluation if a sellar mass is found","Electroretinography when a retinal cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Bitemporal hemianopia or other chiasmal field loss","Signs of a sellar or suprasellar mass, including endocrine disturbance","Additional brainstem or diencephalic signs","Progressive visual loss"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause; the nystagmus may improve if a compressive lesion is treated, but it can persist, and associated visual field loss may not fully recover.","pitfalls":["Failing to image the sella and chiasm when see-saw nystagmus is identified","Overlooking an accompanying bitemporal hemianopia","Not distinguishing the pendular from the jerk (hemi-see-saw) form and their different localisations","Missing a congenital or retinal cause in a child"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying cause, such as resection (often transsphenoidal) or radiotherapy of a compressive parasellar or suprasellar mass","Treat an underlying mesodiencephalic structural lesion according to its nature","Offer symptomatic pharmacological treatment for troublesome oscillopsia with agents such as gabapentin, memantine or clonazepam","Replace deficient pituitary hormones when a sellar mass causes hypopituitarism","Provide neuro-ophthalmological and orthoptic review and low-vision support","Monitor visual fields for chiasmal bitemporal field loss","Refer to neurosurgery and endocrinology as guided by imaging"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"segmental_arterial_mediolysis":{"name":"Segmental Arterial Mediolysis","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SAM","Segmental mediolytic arteriopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Vascular medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare non-inflammatory, non-atherosclerotic arteriopathy defined by lysis of the arterial media, characteristically the outer media adjacent to the adventitia.","It has a strong predilection for splanchnic arteries (coeliac, superior mesenteric, hepatic and splenic) and sometimes involves renal, coronary and intracranial vessels.","Typically presents in middle-aged and older adults with intra-abdominal haemorrhage ('abdominal apoplexy'), dissection, or ruptured aneurysm.","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, made only once vasculitis, fibromuscular dysplasia, connective tissue disease and infection have been excluded.","Inflammatory markers and vasculitis serology are characteristically normal, which helps distinguish it from an arteritis.","Angiography reveals a mixture of aneurysms, dissections, stenoses and occlusions, sometimes overlapping with the appearances of fibromuscular dysplasia.","Many authorities regard it as an acute lesion within, or a precursor to, the fibromuscular dysplasia spectrum.","If the patient survives the acute event, the medial lesion may stabilise and heal by reparative fibrosis, occasionally leaving residual stenosis."],"pathophysiology":"Segmental arterial mediolysis is characterised by vacuolar degeneration and lysis of the smooth muscle of the arterial media, predominantly affecting the outer media near the medioadventitial junction, in the absence of inflammation or atherosclerosis. Loss of this medial support weakens the wall and allows separation at the medioadventitial plane, producing gaps in the arterial wall, dissecting haematomas, pseudoaneurysms and true aneurysms that are prone to rupture and haemorrhage. Alternatively, reparative fibrosis of the damaged segment may cause stenosis or occlusion and downstream ischaemia. The aetiology is unknown, with proposed contributions from vasospasm and catecholamine-mediated arterial injury. There is a striking predilection for the splanchnic circulation, especially the coeliac, mesenteric, hepatic and splenic arteries, with less frequent renal, coronary and intracranial involvement. The condition is increasingly considered to lie on a pathological continuum with fibromuscular dysplasia, potentially representing its acute phase.","additionalDifferentials":["Polyarteritis nodosa and other systemic vasculitides","Fibromuscular dysplasia","Connective tissue disorders (vascular Ehlers-Danlos, Marfan and Loeys-Dietz syndromes)","Mycotic (infective) aneurysm","Atherosclerotic aneurysm","Traumatic arterial injury","Spontaneous coronary or visceral artery dissection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT angiography to demonstrate aneurysms, dissection and active haemorrhage","Catheter digital subtraction angiography for detailed vascular mapping and possible intervention","Inflammatory markers and an autoimmune and vasculitis screen to exclude an arteritis","Blood cultures to exclude a mycotic aneurysm","Genetic and connective tissue disorder evaluation where clinically suspected","Histopathology of a resected or biopsied vessel, where medial lysis without inflammation is definitive","Interval MR or CT angiography for surveillance of new or evolving lesions"],"redFlags":["Haemodynamic instability or haemorrhagic shock from a ruptured aneurysm","Acute severe abdominal or flank pain","Features of bowel or solid-organ ischaemia","A rapidly enlarging aneurysm on serial imaging","Coronary or intracranial arterial involvement"],"prognosis":"The course is variable and can be life-threatening when acute aneurysm rupture causes major haemorrhage, but the medial lesion may stabilise and heal if the patient survives the acute event. Recurrence and involvement of new arterial territories can occur, so imaging surveillance is advised.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as a vasculitis and giving inappropriate immunosuppression.","Failing to consider it in unexplained spontaneous intra-abdominal haemorrhage.","Overlooking an underlying connective tissue disorder or a mycotic aneurysm.","Assuming a solitary lesion when multifocal, multiterritory disease is common.","Under-recognising the need for ongoing surveillance after the acute presentation."],"management":["Treat acute aneurysm rupture or active haemorrhage with endovascular intervention (coil embolisation or covered stent) or surgical repair, alongside haemodynamic resuscitation","Control blood pressure to reduce wall stress on affected arteries","Manage stable, unruptured lesions conservatively with close observation given the potential for spontaneous healing","Exclude vasculitis, fibromuscular dysplasia, connective tissue disease and infection before settling on the diagnosis","Involve vascular medicine and interventional radiology in a multidisciplinary approach","Undertake interval imaging surveillance for new aneurysms, dissection or involvement of further arterial territories"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Vascular medicine references","Vascular and interventional radiology references","Vascular pathology references"],"crossLinks":[]},"segmental_neurofibromatosis":{"name":"segmental neurofibromatosis","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mosaic neurofibromatosis type 1","localised neurofibromatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mosaic form of neurofibromatosis type 1 in which café-au-lait macules, freckling and/or neurofibromas are confined to one or a few body segments, often respecting the midline.","It results from a post-zygotic somatic NF1 mutation, so features are limited to the clonal cutaneous territory rather than being generalised.","Lesions frequently follow a broad dermatomal or Blaschkoid distribution and stop abruptly at the midline.","Most patients have no family history, but gonadal (germline) mosaicism means offspring can rarely inherit generalised NF1.","Systemic complications such as optic glioma and skeletal dysplasia are uncommon, though they can occur if deeper tissues carry the mutation.","Diagnosis is largely clinical; skin biopsy of a neurofibroma confirms the lesion type and mosaic NF1 can be demonstrated on lesional (not blood) tissue.","New or rapidly enlarging, painful nodules within the affected segment warrant assessment for malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour.","Genetic counselling should address the small but real reproductive risk from possible germline involvement."],"pathophysiology":"Segmental neurofibromatosis is caused by a somatic loss-of-function mutation in the NF1 tumour-suppressor gene occurring after fertilisation, during embryonic development. Because the mutation arises in a single progenitor cell and is propagated only to its clonal descendants, neurofibromin deficiency is restricted to a delimited region of skin and sometimes underlying tissue, producing the characteristic segmental, midline-respecting distribution of café-au-lait macules, freckling and neurofibromas. Loss of neurofibromin removes negative regulation of Ras signalling within the affected clone, promoting the same Schwann-cell and melanocytic proliferations seen in generalised NF1 but confined to the mosaic territory. If the mutation also involves germ cells, offspring may inherit a constitutional NF1 mutation and develop generalised disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Generalised neurofibromatosis type 1","Isolated multiple café-au-lait macules without neurofibromatosis","Schwannomatosis or segmental schwannomas","Becker naevus or other segmental pigmentary mosaicism","Congenital melanocytic naevus","Piebaldism or naevus spilus with associated lesions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dermatological mapping of the segmental distribution and midline demarcation","Skin biopsy of a representative nodule for histological confirmation","Molecular testing for NF1 mutation on lesional tissue rather than peripheral blood","Ophthalmological review for Lisch nodules or optic pathway involvement","MRI of the affected region if deep plexiform or nerve involvement is suspected","Family assessment and genetic counselling for reproductive risk"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, new pain or neurological deficit in a lesion suggesting malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour","Extension beyond the original segment or emergence of generalised features","Unexplained skeletal deformity within the affected region","Visual disturbance suggesting optic pathway involvement"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally favourable with disease confined to the affected segment and a low rate of systemic complications, though there is a small lifelong risk of malignant transformation within neurofibromas and a rare reproductive risk of transmitting generalised NF1.","pitfalls":["Testing peripheral blood for NF1 and reporting a false-negative result because the mutation is confined to lesional tissue.","Reassuring patients that offspring cannot be affected, overlooking possible gonadal mosaicism.","Mistaking segmental disease for benign isolated café-au-lait macules and omitting surveillance.","Failing to investigate a rapidly changing nodule for malignant transformation.","Assuming no systemic features can ever occur when deeper tissues may share the mutation."],"management":["Manage conservatively with observation, as most disease is confined to the affected segment","Surgically excise neurofibromas that are symptomatic, disfiguring or functionally troublesome","Monitor lesions for malignant transformation such as rapid growth, pain or neurological deficit, prompting MRI and biopsy for a suspected malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour","Provide ophthalmological review, and regional MRI if deep plexiform or optic pathway involvement is suspected","Offer genetic counselling addressing the low reproductive risk of transmitting generalised NF1 through gonadal mosaicism","Provide dermatological follow-up with access to a neurofibromatosis specialist service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine","Andrews' Diseases of the Skin: Clinical Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"segmental_testicular_infarction":{"name":"Segmental testicular infarction","system":"Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["segmental testicular infarct"],"chapter":null,"source":"Urology and radiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Infarction of a portion of the testis rather than the whole gland, presenting with acute testicular pain","It is an uncommon cause of the acute scrotum and an important mimic of testicular tumour on imaging","Many cases are idiopathic, though causes include epididymo-orchitis, trauma, sickle cell disease, vasculitis and hypercoagulable states","Ultrasound shows a wedge-shaped or rounded avascular area, and contrast-enhanced imaging confirms absent perfusion of the segment","The main concern is distinguishing it from a testicular neoplasm to avoid unnecessary orchidectomy","Where imaging is confident and tumour markers are normal, it can be managed conservatively with follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"Segmental testicular infarction results from occlusion or compromise of a branch of the testicular vasculature supplying part of the testis, producing ischaemic necrosis of that segment while the rest of the gland remains perfused. Precipitants include local inflammation (epididymo-orchitis) causing vascular compression, trauma, sickling in sickle cell disease, vasculitis and thrombophilia, although many cases are idiopathic. The resulting focal avascular lesion can radiologically resemble a tumour, which is the key diagnostic challenge.","additionalDifferentials":["Testicular tumour","Testicular torsion","Epididymo-orchitis with abscess","Focal orchitis","Intratesticular haematoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound with Doppler and, where available, contrast-enhanced ultrasound","MRI in equivocal cases to characterise the lesion","Tumour markers (alpha-fetoprotein, beta-hCG, LDH)","Assessment for a hypercoagulable or vasculitic cause","Interval imaging to demonstrate a decreasing lesion supporting a benign diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Imaging or markers suggesting a testicular tumour","Progressive enlargement of the lesion","Features suggesting torsion or abscess","An underlying thrombophilia or vasculitis needing treatment"],"prognosis":"Idiopathic segmental infarction generally has a benign course with the lesion shrinking over time, and the testis can often be preserved when malignancy is confidently excluded.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for a tumour and performing unnecessary orchidectomy","Failing to use contrast imaging or follow-up to clarify the diagnosis","Overlooking an underlying prothrombotic or vasculitic cause","Confusing it with torsion or abscess"],"management":["Manage conservatively with analgesia and interval imaging when tumour markers are normal and imaging confidently excludes malignancy","Treat any identifiable underlying cause, such as antimicrobials for epididymo-orchitis or management of sickle cell disease or a thrombophilia","Use serial scrotal ultrasound to demonstrate a shrinking avascular lesion supporting a benign course","Reserve surgical exploration for cases where a neoplasm cannot be excluded, favouring testis-sparing excision with frozen section over orchidectomy","Investigate for a hypercoagulable or vasculitic predisposition","Provide urological follow-up until resolution is confirmed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Urology reference texts (standard reference)","Genitourinary radiology references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"segond_fracture":{"name":"Segond fracture","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lateral capsular avulsion fracture","lateral tibial rim avulsion","anterolateral ligament avulsion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Review of Orthopaedics / Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A small avulsion fracture off the lateral margin of the proximal tibia, just distal to the joint line","It results from internal rotation and varus stress and represents avulsion of the anterolateral capsuloligamentous complex","Its great importance is as a radiographic marker: it is strongly associated with (nearly pathognomonic of) anterior cruciate ligament rupture","It is frequently accompanied by meniscal injury as well as the ACL tear","A reverse Segond (medial tibial rim avulsion) is instead associated with PCL and medial meniscal injury","The fleck of bone can be subtle on radiographs, so its recognition should trigger MRI of the knee","Treatment is directed at the associated intra-articular injuries rather than the small bony fragment itself"],"pathophysiology":"Excessive internal rotation and varus loading of the knee places tension on the anterolateral structures of the proximal tibia, avulsing a small cortical fragment at their insertion. The same mechanism ruptures the anterior cruciate ligament, which is why the Segond fragment is a reliable secondary sign of ACL injury; the bony fragment itself is usually clinically insignificant compared with the associated ligamentous and meniscal damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Arcuate (fibular head avulsion) fracture indicating posterolateral corner injury","Reverse Segond (medial avulsion) with PCL injury","Tibial plateau fracture","Iliotibial band or biceps femoris avulsion","Isolated ACL rupture without avulsion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Radiographs of the knee (the fragment is best seen on the AP view)","MRI to confirm ACL rupture and detect meniscal injury","Lachman and pivot-shift examination for anterior instability","Assessment for associated posterolateral corner injury"],"redFlags":["Multiligament injury or a reduced knee dislocation with vascular risk","Locked knee suggesting a displaced meniscal fragment","Gross rotatory instability implying posterolateral corner involvement"],"prognosis":"The fracture itself heals without specific treatment; the outcome is determined by the associated ACL and meniscal injuries and their management.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the subtle bony fleck and thereby missing the associated ACL rupture","Treating the fragment in isolation while ignoring intra-articular injury","Confusing it with an arcuate fracture that signals posterolateral corner injury","Failing to obtain MRI after identifying the sign"],"management":["Recognise the fragment as a marker of anterior cruciate ligament rupture and image the knee with MRI to define the ligamentous and meniscal injury","Manage the bony avulsion itself non-operatively, as it heals without specific fixation","Provide initial symptomatic care with rest, ice, analgesia and bracing or protected weight-bearing","Treat the associated ACL rupture with reconstruction in active or unstable patients, or with rehabilitation in lower-demand patients","Address concurrent meniscal or posterolateral corner injury surgically as indicated","Undertake structured physiotherapy and graded return to activity, with orthopaedic and sports medicine follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Review of Orthopaedics","Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults"],"crossLinks":[]},"seizures_and_epilepsy":{"name":"Seizures and Epilepsy","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Epilepsy","Seizure disorder","Convulsive disorder"],"chapter":436,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3408","clinicalPearls":["A seizure is a transient event caused by abnormal excessive or hypersynchronous neuronal activity; epilepsy is the distinct condition of a chronic underlying tendency to recurrent unprovoked seizures, so a single or provoked seizure does not by itself constitute epilepsy (though a single seizure with high-risk clinical/EEG features may suffice).","Roughly 5-10% of people experience at least one seizure in life, with peaks in early childhood and late adulthood; epilepsy prevalence is approximately 5-30 per 1000.","The 2017 ILAE scheme classifies seizures by onset as focal, generalized, or unknown; focal seizures are described by awareness (intact vs impaired) and motor vs nonmotor onset, replacing the older 'simple/complex partial' and 'secondary generalization' terms.","Focal motor seizures may show a Jacksonian march (spread along the motor strip), and a postictal focal weakness (Todd's paralysis) lasting minutes to hours can mimic a stroke.","Auras are themselves focal seizures with intact awareness; temporal-lobe auras include rising epigastric sensations, foul smells/tastes, fear, deja vu, and micropsia/macropsia.","Typical absence seizures produce brief lapses of consciousness without postural loss or postictal confusion, with a 3-Hz generalized spike-and-wave EEG provoked by hyperventilation; they are frequently misdiagnosed as attention-deficit disorder or daydreaming.","Generalized tonic-clonic seizures begin with a tonic phase (ictal cry, apnea/cyanosis, tongue biting, sympathetic surge) evolving to a clonic phase, followed by postictal flaccidity, confusion, headache, and myalgia.","Mesial temporal lobe epilepsy with hippocampal sclerosis is the most common cause of focal seizures with impaired awareness and is characteristically medically refractory but responds well to surgery.","Etiology is strongly age-dependent: neonates - perinatal hypoxia-ischemia, infection, and metabolic derangements; young children - febrile seizures; adolescents/young adults - trauma, genetic syndromes, drugs, autoantibodies; older adults - cerebrovascular disease (up to ~50% of new epilepsy over 65), tumor, and degenerative disease.","Distinguishing seizure from syncope: aura, cyanosis, prolonged motor activity (30-60 s), and many minutes to hours of postictal confusion favor seizure, whereas brief loss of consciousness with rapid recovery, pallor, and provocation by pain/standing favor syncope; headache and incontinence are unreliable discriminators.","A transient serum prolactin rise in the first 30 minutes after most generalized and some focal seizures can support a true seizure over a psychogenic event, but is unreliable without a baseline and because some drugs elevate prolactin.","Penetrating head injury carries up to a 45% risk of subsequent epilepsy via epileptogenesis, illustrating how an acquired structural lesion can permanently lower seizure threshold."],"pathophysiology":"Seizures arise from a shift in the normal balance between neuronal excitation and inhibition in the CNS. Focal seizure activity begins in a discrete cortical region and can invade surrounding cortex; the hallmark is the electrographic spike produced by near-simultaneous bursting of many excitatory neurons (hypersynchronization). At the cellular level, a prolonged paroxysmal depolarization shift driven by calcium influx opens voltage-dependent sodium channels and generates repetitive action potentials, followed by a GABA- or potassium-channel-mediated hyperpolarizing afterpotential. Spread is normally limited by intact hyperpolarization and feedforward surround inhibition; recruitment of neighboring neurons occurs through rising extracellular potassium, presynaptic calcium accumulation enhancing transmitter release, NMDA-receptor activation, and ephaptic effects, allowing propagation via local and commissural (corpus callosum) pathways. Epilepsy results from a dynamic interplay of endogenous (often genetic, maturational) factors, epileptogenic factors (e.g., severe head trauma, stroke, infection, neurodegeneration, developmental malformations), and precipitating factors (sleep deprivation, stress, hormonal change, toxins, photic stimulation). Many gene mutations affecting ion channels (e.g., SCN1A, KCNQ2), neurotransmitter receptors, and metabolic transporters (e.g., SLC2A1/GLUT-1) underlie specific syndromes.","additionalDifferentials":["Syncope (vasovagal, cardiac arrhythmia, valvular disease, orthostatic hypotension)","Psychogenic nonepileptic seizures / conversion disorder","Transient ischemic attack (especially basilar artery TIA)","Migraine (confusional or basilar)","Metabolic disturbances - hypoglycemia, hypoxia, hyponatremia, hypocalcemia","Sleep disorders - narcolepsy/cataplexy, benign sleep myoclonus","Movement disorders - tics, nonepileptic myoclonus, paroxysmal choreoathetosis","Panic attack / hyperventilation","In children: breath-holding spells, night terrors, sleepwalking, benign paroxysmal vertigo, apnea"],"infectionMimics":["CNS infection (meningitis/encephalitis) - both a cause of acute symptomatic seizures and a mimic via confusion/altered behavior; lumbar puncture is mandatory in HIV-infected patients presenting with a seizure even when asymptomatic"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Convulsive syncope (brief tonic-clonic-like movements during a faint, especially when kept upright)","Psychogenic nonepileptic seizures (side-to-side head movement, ictal eye closure, asynchronous limb shaking, pelvic thrusting, waxing-waning prolonged events)","Transient ischemic attack","Migraine aura","Movement disorders and parasomnias"],"additionalInvestigations":["EEG, including activation procedures (hyperventilation, photic stimulation, sleep, sleep deprivation); a normal routine interictal EEG does not exclude epilepsy and is normal in 50-80% of known epilepsy patients on first recording","Prolonged video-EEG telemetry for poorly characterized or refractory events and to distinguish psychogenic from epileptic seizures","MRI (superior to CT) for structural lesions including hippocampal sclerosis and neuronal migration abnormalities; emergent CT when MRI unavailable or mass/infection suspected","Routine bloods - electrolytes, glucose, calcium, magnesium, hepatic and renal function; toxicology screen of blood and urine","Lumbar puncture when meningitis/encephalitis suspected, and mandatory in HIV-infected patients","Serum and CSF autoantibody testing in fulminant epilepsy with psychiatric or cognitive features","Genetic testing in suspected genetic epilepsy syndromes (diagnostic and may guide therapy choice and predict drug toxicity, e.g., HLA-B*1502 and severe cutaneous reactions)","Functional imaging (PET, SPECT) and advanced localization (high-density EEG/ESI, magnetoencephalography/MSI) in medically refractory focal epilepsy"],"redFlags":["Generalized convulsive status epilepticus - continuous or repetitive seizures (practically, GCSE lasting beyond ~5 min); a medical emergency that can cause cardiorespiratory failure, hyperthermia, metabolic derangement, and irreversible CNS injury","After 30-45 minutes of uninterrupted seizures the clinical signs may become subtle while damaging electrographic seizures continue, including in patients paralyzed by neuromuscular blockade","Fulminant new-onset seizures with psychiatric symptoms and cognitive change suggesting autoimmune (e.g., anti-potassium-channel or anti-glutamate-receptor) epilepsy","New seizure in an older adult suggesting cerebrovascular disease, tumor, or degenerative disease","First seizure with focal features, persistent neurologic deficit, fever, or suspected CNS infection prompting urgent imaging and possible lumbar puncture","Atonic 'drop attacks' with high risk of head injury"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies widely by syndrome and etiology. Typical absence epilepsy and juvenile myoclonic epilepsy generally respond well to medication (though JME remission is uncommon), whereas mesial temporal lobe epilepsy, Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, and epilepsies tied to structural lesions or developmental delay are often refractory. About half of patients do not respond to the first medication, and roughly 30% have ongoing seizures despite optimal drug therapy. Favorable predictors for successfully discontinuing therapy include 1-5 years of complete control, a single seizure type (generalized carrying a better prognosis than focal), normal neurologic examination and intelligence, no family history of epilepsy, and a normal EEG; a normal EEG generally implies a better prognosis. People with epilepsy have a death rate roughly 2-3 times that of matched controls, mostly attributable to the underlying cause but also to accidents, status epilepticus, and SUDEP.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish a focal seizure that secondarily generalizes from a primary generalized tonic-clonic seizure because bystanders emphasize the convulsive phase and overlook subtle focal onset/aura - a distinction that materially changes evaluation and treatment.","Misdiagnosing absence epilepsy as attention-deficit disorder or simple daydreaming.","Concluding bizarre paroxysmal stereotyped behavior is non-epileptic; focal (especially frontal-lobe) seizures can produce highly unusual behaviors.","Assuming a normal interictal EEG excludes epilepsy, or that epileptiform discharges by themselves prove epilepsy.","Over-relying on incontinence or headache to separate seizure from syncope, as these are unreliable.","Assuming psychogenic nonepileptic seizures exclude true epilepsy - the two frequently coexist.","Misreading subtle late-stage status epilepticus as resolution when damaging electrographic seizures persist."],"management":["Start antiseizure medication chosen for seizure type, using sodium channel blockers such as lamotrigine or carbamazepine for focal seizures and broad-spectrum agents such as valproate or levetiracetam for generalised epilepsy, avoiding drugs that worsen generalised seizures","Identify and correct provoked or acute symptomatic causes such as electrolyte disturbance, hypoglycaemia, drug or alcohol withdrawal, or CNS infection","Treat status epilepticus urgently with benzodiazepines, then intravenous antiseizure loading, escalating to anaesthesia if refractory","Consider epilepsy surgery to resect an epileptogenic focus, vagus nerve stimulation, or a ketogenic diet in drug-resistant epilepsy","Counsel on trigger and lifestyle measures, driving restrictions, safety, and the risk of sudden unexpected death in epilepsy","Give pre-conception advice avoiding teratogenic valproate in women of childbearing potential and offer folic acid supplementation","Refer to neurology and specialist epilepsy services and monitor for drug adverse effects"],"severityClassification":"Seizures are classified by ILAE 2017 onset: focal (with intact or impaired awareness, motor or nonmotor), generalized (motor such as tonic-clonic, atonic, myoclonic; or nonmotor/absence), or unknown onset. Severity at the syndromic extreme is status epilepticus: continuous or repetitive seizures with impaired interictal consciousness, subdivided into generalized convulsive status epilepticus (GCSE) and nonconvulsive status epilepticus (NCSE). Although traditionally defined as 15-30 minutes of activity, GCSE is practically treated as an emergency when convulsions last beyond about 5 minutes. Febrile seizures are graded as simple (single, brief, symmetric) versus complex (repeated, lasting over 15 minutes, or with focal features).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3408","Harrison 22e p.3409","Harrison 22e p.3410","Harrison 22e p.3411","Harrison 22e p.3413","Harrison 22e p.3414","Harrison 22e p.3416","Harrison 22e p.3417","Harrison 22e p.3418","Harrison 22e p.3419","Harrison 22e p.3425","Harrison 22e p.3427"],"crossLinks":[]},"selective_iga_deficiency":{"name":"Selective IgA deficiency","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IgA deficiency","selective immunoglobulin A deficiency","sIgAD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Selective IgA deficiency is the most common primary immunodeficiency and is defined by a very low or absent serum IgA with normal IgG and IgM and otherwise intact immunity","The majority of affected individuals are asymptomatic and are detected incidentally, so a low IgA alone does not equate to clinical disease","When symptomatic, patients tend to have recurrent sinopulmonary and gastrointestinal infections, reflecting the loss of secretory IgA at mucosal surfaces","There is a recognised association with atopy and with autoimmune conditions such as coeliac disease, so unexplained IgA deficiency warrants screening for these","Coeliac serology based on IgA antibodies can be falsely negative in IgA deficiency, so IgG-based testing or histology should be used instead","A minority have severe anaphylactic reactions to blood products because of anti-IgA antibodies, so washed or IgA-deficient blood components may be required","There is no specific replacement therapy because IgA cannot be effectively replaced; management centres on prompt treatment of infections and, where needed, prophylactic antibiotics","A small proportion, particularly those with associated IgG subclass deficiency or poor vaccine responses, can progress over time toward common variable immunodeficiency and need monitoring"],"pathophysiology":"Selective IgA deficiency results from a failure of terminal differentiation of IgA-committed B cells into IgA-secreting plasma cells, so that little or no IgA reaches serum and mucosal secretions. The loss of secretory IgA impairs mucosal immune exclusion of pathogens at the respiratory and gastrointestinal surfaces, though compensatory secretory IgM often limits the clinical impact. The precise molecular basis is usually unknown; it shares genetic susceptibility loci (including MHC haplotypes and TACI variants) with CVID, and the two conditions can occur within the same families and occasionally evolve into one another. Immune dysregulation underlies the associated atopic and autoimmune disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Common variable immunodeficiency (may share features and can evolve from IgA deficiency)","Transient low IgA in early childhood before immune maturation","Drug-induced IgA suppression (e.g. certain anticonvulsants)","IgG subclass or specific antibody deficiency presenting with recurrent infection","Coeliac disease and other associated autoimmune disorders","Secondary immunodeficiency from protein loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum IgA, IgG, and IgM to confirm isolated IgA deficiency","IgG subclass levels and vaccine-specific antibody responses to assess for broader deficiency","Coeliac evaluation using IgG-based serology or duodenal biopsy given unreliable IgA-based tests","Screening for associated autoimmune disease when clinically indicated","Testing for anti-IgA antibodies before transfusion in patients with prior reactions","Periodic reassessment of immunoglobulins to detect evolution toward CVID"],"redFlags":["Anaphylactic transfusion reaction from anti-IgA antibodies, requiring IgA-deficient or washed blood products","Progressive fall in IgG or poor vaccine responses signalling evolution to CVID","Recurrent severe or invasive infection prompting reassessment of the full antibody profile","Unexplained malabsorption or systemic autoimmune features suggesting an associated disorder"],"prognosis":"Most individuals with selective IgA deficiency remain well and have a normal life expectancy. Symptomatic patients generally do well with treatment of infections, but a subset with additional immune defects have more infections and may progress to CVID, and rare transfusion anaphylaxis is a serious hazard.","pitfalls":["Interpreting an incidental low IgA as clinically significant disease in an asymptomatic person","Missing coeliac disease because of falsely negative IgA-based serology","Transfusing standard blood products in a patient with anti-IgA antibodies","Failing to reassess immunoglobulins over time and thus missing progression to CVID"],"management":["Treat recurrent sinopulmonary and gastrointestinal infections promptly with appropriate antibiotics, as immunoglobulin replacement is not indicated for isolated deficiency","Consider prophylactic antibiotics for frequent or severe recurrent infections","Screen for and manage associated coeliac disease using IgG-based serology or duodenal biopsy, and other autoimmune conditions","Transfuse washed or IgA-deficient blood components in patients with anti-IgA antibodies or prior transfusion reactions","Reserve immunoglobulin replacement for those who evolve to CVID or have a proven additional antibody defect","Monitor immunoglobulins periodically to detect progression toward CVID","Provide patient education and a medical alert regarding transfusion reaction risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"selenium_deficiency":{"name":"Selenium deficiency","system":"Nutrition / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hyposelenaemia","Keshan disease","Kashin-Beck disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO nutrition guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Selenium is an antioxidant trace element, and deficiency is classically linked to Keshan disease, an endemic cardiomyopathy, and Kashin-Beck disease, an osteoarthropathy","It occurs in geographic regions with selenium-poor soil and in patients on long-term unsupplemented parenteral nutrition","Manifestations can include cardiomyopathy, skeletal myopathy with weakness, and macrocytosis, and long-term deficiency may impair immune and thyroid function","Deficiency in Keshan disease areas is thought to increase susceptibility to a viral cofactor in producing the cardiomyopathy","Serum or plasma selenium and glutathione peroxidase activity help assess status","Repletion can prevent progression of the cardiomyopathy but may not reverse established myocardial damage","It should be considered in patients on prolonged artificial nutrition who develop unexplained cardiac or muscle disease"],"pathophysiology":"Selenium is incorporated into selenoproteins, notably the glutathione peroxidases and thioredoxin reductases that defend cells against oxidative damage, and into the deiodinases that convert thyroxine to active triiodothyronine. Deficiency reduces antioxidant defence, leaving tissues, especially cardiac and skeletal muscle, vulnerable to oxidative injury, which underlies the cardiomyopathy and myopathy. In endemic regions low soil selenium lowers intake across the population, and an interaction with viral infection is implicated in the pathogenesis of the endemic cardiomyopathy. Impaired deiodinase function can also affect thyroid hormone metabolism.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of dilated cardiomyopathy","Other nutritional or metabolic myopathies","Thiamine deficiency cardiomyopathy","Primary osteoarthropathy for the Kashin-Beck phenotype","Other trace element deficiencies in parenteral nutrition"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum or plasma selenium concentration","Erythrocyte glutathione peroxidase activity as a functional marker","Cardiac assessment including echocardiography where cardiomyopathy is suspected","Assessment for coexisting trace element deficiencies in long-term parenteral nutrition","Thyroid function testing where relevant"],"redFlags":["Heart failure or arrhythmia from selenium-deficiency cardiomyopathy","Progressive skeletal myopathy with significant weakness","Unexplained cardiac or muscle disease in a patient on long-term parenteral nutrition","Deterioration despite treatment suggesting established irreversible myocardial damage"],"prognosis":"Repletion can halt progression and prevent deficiency-related disease, and early cardiomyopathy may improve, but established myocardial damage in Keshan disease may be irreversible.","pitfalls":["Omitting trace element supplementation, including selenium, during long-term parenteral nutrition","Overlooking selenium deficiency as a reversible contributor to cardiomyopathy or myopathy","Failing to consider geographic and dietary risk in endemic regions","Assuming repletion will reverse advanced established cardiac damage"],"management":["Replete stores with oral or parenteral selenium (sodium selenite or selenomethionine), the definitive corrective therapy","Add selenium to long-term parenteral nutrition regimens to prevent iatrogenic deficiency","Give guideline-based heart failure therapy for deficiency-related cardiomyopathy and treat the associated skeletal myopathy supportively","Monitor repletion with serial serum or plasma selenium and erythrocyte glutathione peroxidase activity as a functional marker","Undertake echocardiographic surveillance of cardiac function and screen for coexisting trace element deficiencies, checking thyroid function given impaired deiodinase activity","Support population-level dietary supplementation or food fortification in endemic selenium-poor regions","Initiate repletion early to halt progression, recognising that established myocardial damage in Keshan disease may be irreversible"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO nutrition guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"semantic_dementia":{"name":"Semantic dementia","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SD","semantic variant primary progressive aphasia","svPPA","semantic variant PPA","temporal variant frontotemporal dementia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by progressive loss of knowledge of the meaning of words and objects, with fluent but empty speech and prominent anomia","Single-word comprehension is impaired and patients may ask what familiar words mean, yet speech remains fluent and grammatically correct","It is the semantic variant of primary progressive aphasia and usually reflects frontotemporal lobar degeneration with TDP-43 pathology","Surface dyslexia, with difficulty reading irregularly spelled words, is a characteristic feature","Imaging shows asymmetrical, often left-predominant, anterior temporal lobe atrophy, sometimes described as a knife-edge pattern","Right-predominant cases may present with prosopagnosia and behavioural or emotional change rather than language failure","Repetition and speech production are relatively preserved, helping to distinguish it from other aphasia variants"],"pathophysiology":"Semantic dementia is the semantic variant of primary progressive aphasia, caused by neurodegeneration focused on the anterior temporal lobes, which serve as a hub for conceptual and semantic knowledge. The commonest underlying pathology is frontotemporal lobar degeneration associated with abnormal TDP-43 protein aggregation. Progressive loss of neurones in the anterior temporal cortex erodes the store of meaning for words, objects and, in right-predominant cases, faces, while the more posterior and perisylvian regions supporting fluent speech output, grammar and repetition remain relatively intact until later. This produces fluent, well-articulated but semantically empty speech with severe naming and comprehension deficits.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-fluent/agrammatic variant primary progressive aphasia","Logopenic variant primary progressive aphasia","Behavioural variant frontotemporal dementia","Alzheimer disease","Herpes simplex or limbic encephalitis affecting the temporal lobes","Temporal lobe tumour or structural lesion","Amnestic syndromes of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Speech and language assessment demonstrating impaired single-word comprehension with fluent output","MRI of the brain showing asymmetrical anterior temporal atrophy","FDG-PET to demonstrate anterior temporal hypometabolism","Amyloid PET or cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers to exclude Alzheimer pathology","Neuropsychological testing of semantic knowledge, naming and reading of irregular words","Genetic testing where a family history of frontotemporal dementia is present"],"redFlags":["Subacute onset or systemic features suggesting an inflammatory or infective encephalitis rather than degeneration","Rapid progression inconsistent with the usual course","Prominent behavioural disturbance affecting safety, especially in right-predominant cases","Focal deficits or headache raising concern for a structural lesion"],"prognosis":"Semantic dementia progresses over years, with widening loss of conceptual knowledge and eventual emergence of behavioural change and broader cognitive decline; prognosis reflects the underlying frontotemporal lobar degeneration, and management is supportive with speech and language and behavioural strategies.","pitfalls":["Mistaking fluent, empty speech for preserved language and underestimating the comprehension deficit","Attributing the picture to Alzheimer disease rather than recognising the anterior temporal atrophy pattern","Overlooking a treatable temporal-lobe encephalitis in atypical or subacute presentations","Failing to recognise right-predominant cases presenting with prosopagnosia and behavioural change"],"management":["Provide speech and language therapy focused on communication strategies and personalised word and picture aids to compensate for semantic loss, as no disease-modifying therapy exists","Manage emerging behavioural and emotional change with non-pharmacological strategies, reserving judicious SSRIs for distressing symptoms and avoiding antipsychotics where possible","Offer occupational therapy, dietetic input for altered eating behaviour, and structured social care for progressive functional decline","Avoid cholinesterase inhibitors, which are ineffective, and distinguish the disorder from Alzheimer pathology to prevent inappropriate treatment","Monitor for dysphagia, safety risks, and carer strain, with proactive advance care planning as conceptual knowledge widens","Refer for genetic counselling and testing where there is a family history of frontotemporal dementia","Coordinate multidisciplinary and specialist cognitive-neurology follow-up to guide supportive care as the disease progresses"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"seminal_vesicle_cyst":{"name":"Seminal vesicle cyst","system":"Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["seminal vesicle cyst","congenital seminal vesicle cyst","acquired seminal vesicle cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / EAU Guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["May be congenital, often with ipsilateral renal or mesonephric duct anomalies as in Zinner syndrome, or acquired secondary to ejaculatory duct obstruction, infection or prior surgery","Located laterally, off the midline, which distinguishes it from midline mullerian duct and prostatic utricle cysts","Frequently asymptomatic; larger cysts may cause perineal discomfort, painful ejaculation, haematospermia, dysuria, or infertility","Congenital cysts commonly present between the second and fourth decades as they gradually enlarge","Transrectal ultrasound and pelvic MRI characterise the lesion and its relationship to the ejaculatory ducts and the seminal vesicle","An association with renal agenesis should always prompt a search for the complete Zinner triad","Small asymptomatic cysts are observed, whereas symptomatic ones may be aspirated or excised"],"pathophysiology":"Congenital seminal vesicle cysts result from maldevelopment of the distal mesonephric duct with atresia or obstruction of the ejaculatory duct, causing cystic dilatation of the seminal vesicle and frequently coexisting ipsilateral renal maldevelopment. Acquired cysts arise when inflammation, previous surgery or chronic obstruction impedes seminal vesicle drainage, leading to retention of secretions and progressive cystic expansion.","additionalDifferentials":["Midline mullerian duct cyst","Midline prostatic utricle cyst","Ejaculatory duct cyst","Zinner syndrome when renal agenesis is present","Bladder or ureteric diverticulum","Prostatic or pelvic cystic neoplasm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transrectal ultrasound to localise and measure the cyst","Pelvic MRI for detailed anatomy and to assess contents, which may be haemorrhagic or proteinaceous","Renal tract imaging to exclude ipsilateral renal agenesis","Semen analysis if fertility is affected","Cyst aspiration for cytology or culture where infection or diagnostic uncertainty exists","Cross-sectional imaging to exclude a solid or neoplastic component"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting infection, abscess or malignancy within or adjacent to the cyst","An associated renal anomaly with a compromised solitary kidney","Rapid growth or solid components on imaging","Refractory pain, infertility or recurrent haematospermia"],"prognosis":"Generally benign with an excellent outlook; asymptomatic cysts tend to remain stable, and symptomatic lesions usually resolve after aspiration or excision, although recurrence can follow simple aspiration.","pitfalls":["Failing to look for associated renal agenesis and the full Zinner triad","Misclassifying a lateral cyst as a midline mullerian or utricle cyst","Attributing haematospermia solely to the cyst without excluding other causes","Choosing simple aspiration where recurrence is likely and excision would be more definitive"],"management":["Manage small asymptomatic cysts conservatively with surveillance imaging rather than intervention","Treat symptomatic cysts by transrectal or transperineal aspiration, or by definitive surgical excision such as laparoscopic or robotic seminal vesiculectomy","Treat superimposed infection with an appropriate antibiotic course","Address associated ejaculatory duct obstruction and infertility with transurethral resection of the ejaculatory ducts or assisted reproductive techniques","Investigate for ipsilateral renal agenesis and the wider Zinner triad, arranging renal tract surveillance where a congenital anomaly is confirmed","Refer to urology and counsel that simple aspiration carries a risk of recurrence, favouring excision for definitive cure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","EAU Guidelines"],"crossLinks":["zinner_syndrome","mullerian_duct_cyst","utricle_cyst"]},"seminoma":{"name":"Seminoma","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["testicular seminoma","classic seminoma","pure seminoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Seminoma is the commonest single germ cell tumour type and tends to present slightly later than non-seminomatous tumours, usually as a painless testicular mass","By definition AFP is normal in pure seminoma; a raised AFP means a non-seminomatous element is present and the tumour is managed as NSGCT even if histology looks purely seminomatous","Beta-hCG may be modestly elevated (from syncytiotrophoblastic cells) and LDH reflects tumour bulk, but neither excludes the diagnosis of seminoma","Radical inguinal orchidectomy establishes the diagnosis and provides local control; trans-scrotal approaches are avoided","Seminoma is highly radiosensitive and chemosensitive, so stage I disease may be managed by surveillance, adjuvant carboplatin, or adjuvant radiotherapy after individualised discussion","Cisplatin-based combination chemotherapy is the mainstay for higher-stage or metastatic disease, with radiotherapy or chemotherapy chosen by stage and risk","Sperm banking should be offered before treatment given the risks to fertility"],"pathophysiology":"Seminoma arises from germ cell neoplasia in situ and is composed of relatively uniform cells that resemble undifferentiated primordial germ cells, often with a lymphocytic infiltrate and granulomatous reaction within the tumour. A minority contain syncytiotrophoblastic giant cells that secrete beta-hCG, accounting for mild marker elevation, but seminoma cells do not produce AFP. Like other germ cell tumours it carries isochromosome 12p and spreads predominantly by lymphatics to the retroperitoneal nodes before later haematogenous dissemination.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-seminomatous germ cell tumour or mixed germ cell tumour","Testicular lymphoma in older men","Epididymo-orchitis with a mass","Sex cord-stromal tumour","Spermatocytic tumour (a distinct, generally indolent entity)","Granulomatous orchitis mimicking neoplasm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound demonstrating a solid intratesticular lesion","Serum AFP (expected normal), beta-hCG, and LDH before and after orchidectomy","Radical inguinal orchidectomy for histology","CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis for staging","Semen analysis and sperm banking before treatment","Repeat marker measurement to confirm normalisation after treatment"],"redFlags":["Bulky retroperitoneal nodal disease or visceral metastases","A raised AFP indicating an occult non-seminomatous component requiring NSGCT management","Markers failing to normalise after orchidectomy","Rapidly enlarging mass with systemic symptoms"],"prognosis":"Pure seminoma has an excellent prognosis, with cure rates approaching those of other stages of testicular cancer; localised disease is almost universally curable and even metastatic seminoma has high cure rates with cisplatin-based chemotherapy.","pitfalls":["Assuming any germ cell tumour with a raised AFP can be treated as seminoma","Interpreting a mildly raised beta-hCG as excluding seminoma","Using a trans-scrotal biopsy and risking seeding","Omitting sperm banking before radiotherapy or chemotherapy","Failing to monitor for a metachronous contralateral tumour on follow-up"],"management":["Perform radical inguinal orchidectomy for diagnosis and local control, avoiding a trans-scrotal approach","Manage stage I disease by individualised surveillance, adjuvant carboplatin, or adjuvant radiotherapy after shared decision-making","Treat higher-stage or metastatic disease with cisplatin-based combination chemotherapy, using radiotherapy selectively by stage and risk","Offer sperm banking and semen analysis before treatment because therapy may impair fertility","Undertake follow-up surveillance with beta-hCG and LDH markers and staging CT imaging to confirm normalisation and detect relapse","Provide testosterone replacement for post-treatment hypogonadism and refer to a uro-oncology multidisciplinary team","Escalate promptly to salvage chemotherapy and specialist input at relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","EAU Guidelines on Testicular Cancer"],"crossLinks":[]},"sengers_syndrome":{"name":"Sengers syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cataract-hypertrophic cardiomyopathy-mitochondrial myopathy","AGK deficiency","Acylglycerol kinase deficiency","AGK"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews mitochondrial disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The defining combination is congenital cataracts, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, skeletal (mitochondrial) myopathy and exercise intolerance with lactic acidosis","It is an autosomal recessive nuclear disorder caused by mutations in AGK, encoding acylglycerol kinase, a mitochondrial protein involved in lipid metabolism and mitochondrial protein import","Congenital or early cataracts are a consistent and often presenting feature that should prompt cardiac evaluation","Lactate rises markedly with exertion despite normal cognition, and the myopathy causes hypotonia and fatigue","The hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is the principal determinant of prognosis and can cause heart failure or arrhythmia","A severe neonatal form presents early with profound cardiomyopathy and lactic acidosis, while a milder form allows survival into adulthood","Care is supportive with cataract surgery, cardiac management and avoidance of mitochondrial toxins; intellect is typically preserved"],"pathophysiology":"Sengers syndrome is caused by recessive loss-of-function mutations in AGK, which encodes acylglycerol kinase. This mitochondrial enzyme phosphorylates monoacylglycerol and diacylglycerol and also functions as a component of the TIM22 carrier import machinery that inserts certain proteins, including the adenine nucleotide translocator, into the inner mitochondrial membrane. Loss of AGK disturbs mitochondrial membrane lipid composition and impairs import of key carriers, secondarily compromising oxidative phosphorylation and ATP export. Tissues with high and sustained energy demand, notably cardiac and skeletal muscle, and the metabolically specialised lens are preferentially affected, giving the cardiomyopathy, myopathy and cataracts.","additionalDifferentials":["Other mitochondrial cardiomyopathies and Barth syndrome","Infantile Pompe disease and other metabolic cardiomyopathies","Sarcomeric (genetic) hypertrophic cardiomyopathy without metabolic features","Congenital cataract syndromes of other cause","Congenital myopathies and myasthenic syndromes","Other combined oxidative phosphorylation deficiency disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography and ECG to detect and monitor hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia","Resting and post-exercise blood lactate","Ophthalmological examination for congenital or early cataract","AGK molecular genetic testing","Skeletal muscle biopsy for histology and respiratory-chain enzymology","Creatine kinase and assessment of the myopathy"],"redFlags":["Decompensated hypertrophic cardiomyopathy or heart failure","Life-threatening arrhythmia","Severe neonatal lactic acidosis with cardiorespiratory compromise","Rapid clinical deterioration during intercurrent illness or metabolic stress"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is bimodal: the severe neonatal form usually causes death in infancy from cardiomyopathy and lactic acidosis, whereas the milder form permits survival into adulthood with cataract, myopathy and manageable cardiac disease, cardiac status being the main prognostic factor.","pitfalls":["Treating congenital cataracts in isolation without evaluating the heart, thereby missing the cardiomyopathy","Attributing the hypertrophic cardiomyopathy to a sarcomeric cause and overlooking a metabolic origin","Assuming cognitive impairment, when intellect is characteristically preserved","Exposing patients to mitochondrial toxins that can worsen the energy deficit"],"management":["Provide supportive management of this AGK-related mitochondrial disorder, for which no curative therapy exists","Arrange surgical extraction of congenital cataracts to preserve vision and support development","Treat the hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and any arrhythmia with heart failure and antiarrhythmic therapy, the principal determinant of prognosis","Avoid prolonged fasting and metabolic stressors, and manage exercise intolerance and lactic acidosis with graded activity and carbohydrate support","Undertake surveillance with serial echocardiography, ECG, and resting and post-exercise lactate monitoring","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across metabolic or mitochondrial medicine, cardiology, and ophthalmology","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive condition, including reproductive options"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["OMIM: Sengers Syndrome","GeneReviews: mitochondrial disease overview"],"crossLinks":[]},"senile_chorea":{"name":"senile chorea","system":"nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["senile chorea of the elderly","late-onset sporadic chorea"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Senile chorea is a controversial descriptive label for late-onset chorea in the elderly without dementia, psychiatric decline or a family history of Huntington disease.","It is a diagnosis of exclusion; many historical cases would now be reclassified as late-onset Huntington disease, Huntington disease-like syndromes or secondary chorea.","Chorea is typically generalised, symmetrical and predominantly affects the limbs and orobuccolingual region.","Cognition is characteristically preserved, distinguishing it from the cognitive and behavioural decline of Huntington disease.","Genetic testing for the HTT CAG repeat expansion and for C9orf72 and other phenocopies is essential before accepting the label.","Structural basal ganglia lesions, metabolic derangement, drugs and autoimmune causes must be systematically excluded.","Progression, if any, is generally slow and confined to the movement disorder without the relentless multisystem decline of Huntington disease.","Treatment is symptomatic and reserved for functionally disabling chorea, using dopamine-depleting or dopamine-blocking agents cautiously in view of age and comorbidity."],"pathophysiology":"Senile chorea is best understood as a clinical syndrome rather than a unified pathological entity, reflecting age-related or acquired dysfunction of the striatal indirect pathway. In many patients neuropathological or genetic re-evaluation reveals late-onset Huntington disease with reduced-penetrance CAG expansions, or Huntington disease phenocopies. In genuinely sporadic cases, degeneration or vascular and gliotic change within the caudate and putamen is thought to reduce inhibitory striatal output to the globus pallidus, disinhibiting thalamocortical drive and producing chorea. The relative sparing of cognition suggests that pathology is more restricted to motor striatal circuits than in classical Huntington disease, though the boundaries of the concept remain debated.","additionalDifferentials":["Late-onset Huntington disease","Huntington disease-like syndromes and C9orf72 expansion","Neuroacanthocytosis and related syndromes","Vascular (post-stroke) chorea","Drug-induced chorea and tardive dyskinesia","Metabolic chorea (non-ketotic hyperglycaemia, thyrotoxicosis, polycythaemia)","Autoimmune and paraneoplastic chorea"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["HTT CAG repeat genetic testing for Huntington disease","Genetic testing for Huntington disease phenocopies (C9orf72, JPH3, others) where indicated","MRI brain to assess caudate atrophy and vascular or structural lesions","Peripheral blood film for acanthocytes and creatine kinase","Metabolic screen including glucose, thyroid function and full blood count","Autoimmune and paraneoplastic antibody panel where clinically suspected","Serum caeruloplasmin and copper studies in younger-appearing presentations"],"redFlags":["Emerging cognitive decline or psychiatric change suggesting Huntington disease rather than isolated senile chorea","Abrupt onset or focal signs indicating a vascular or structural lesion","Systemic features suggesting paraneoplastic or autoimmune aetiology","Rapid progression prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Metabolic derangement such as marked hyperglycaemia requiring urgent correction"],"prognosis":"When a serious underlying cause is excluded, senile chorea tends to follow a relatively benign, slowly progressive or static course with preserved cognition and reasonable functional outlook. Prognosis worsens substantially if the presentation ultimately proves to be late-onset Huntington disease or another neurodegenerative disorder.","pitfalls":["Accepting the label without genetic testing and thereby missing late-onset Huntington disease.","Assuming a negative family history excludes Huntington disease, which may reflect non-paternity, early parental death or reduced penetrance.","Overlooking reversible metabolic causes such as non-ketotic hyperglycaemia.","Attributing chorea to ageing and not screening for drug-induced or tardive movement disorders.","Using dopamine antagonists without regard to fall risk, sedation and anticholinergic burden in older patients."],"management":["Treat any identified underlying cause, since this is a diagnosis of exclusion, and withdraw offending drugs where implicated","Suppress functionally disabling chorea with a dopamine-depleting agent such as tetrabenazine or a cautious antidopaminergic","Provide supportive physiotherapy, occupational therapy, falls prevention, and nutritional support","Undertake genetic counselling and HTT CAG repeat testing, with phenocopy testing, before accepting the descriptive label","Systematically exclude structural basal ganglia lesions, metabolic derangement, and autoimmune causes","Refer to neurology and monitor for emerging cognitive or behavioural decline that would reclassify the illness as late-onset Huntington disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Movement Disorders textbook reviews of late-onset chorea"],"crossLinks":[]},"senior_loken_syndrome":{"name":"Senior-Loken syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nephronophthisis with retinal dystrophy","renal-retinal dysplasia","SLSN","NPHP1","NPHP5 (IQCB1)","NPHP6 (CEP290)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics / Pediatric Nephrology (Avner)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A ciliopathy combining juvenile nephronophthisis with an inherited retinal dystrophy, so it links the kidney and the eye through shared ciliary gene defects","The retinal component ranges from severe early-onset Leber congenital amaurosis, often with the IQCB1 or CEP290 genotype, to a milder later retinitis pigmentosa","Nephronophthisis typically presents with polyuria, polydipsia, secondary enuresis, anaemia, and growth failure before progressing to end-stage kidney disease, commonly in the second decade","Renal ultrasound shows normal or small kidneys with increased echogenicity, loss of corticomedullary differentiation, and sometimes cortico-medullary cysts","Inheritance is autosomal recessive and it overlaps genetically with isolated nephronophthisis, Joubert syndrome, and other ciliopathies","Early recognition of the eye disease in an infant with nystagmus and poor vision should prompt renal surveillance, and vice versa","Diagnosis rests on the combination of nephronophthisis and retinal dystrophy confirmed by electroretinography and molecular genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Senior-Loken syndrome is caused by biallelic variants in nephronophthisis-associated genes that encode proteins localising to the primary cilium, basal body, and centrosome. Defective ciliary function in renal tubular epithelium leads to progressive tubulointerstitial nephropathy with tubular atrophy, interstitial fibrosis, and cyst formation at the corticomedullary junction, the hallmark of nephronophthisis. The same proteins are essential for the connecting cilium of photoreceptors, so their loss produces photoreceptor degeneration and inherited retinal dystrophy, explaining the combined renal and retinal phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated juvenile nephronophthisis without eye involvement","Joubert syndrome with renal and retinal features","Bardet-Biedl syndrome","Leber congenital amaurosis without renal disease","Alstrom syndrome","Other tubulointerstitial nephropathies causing polyuria in childhood"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasound for kidney size, echogenicity, and corticomedullary cysts","Renal function testing and urinalysis to detect concentrating defect and progression","Electroretinography and formal ophthalmological assessment for retinal dystrophy","Molecular genetic testing using a nephronophthisis or ciliopathy gene panel","Brain MRI where Joubert-related features are suspected","Assessment of growth and haematological status for chronic kidney disease complications"],"redFlags":["Progression to end-stage kidney disease requiring dialysis or transplantation","Rapidly declining vision in an infant suggesting Leber congenital amaurosis","Growth failure and anaemia signalling advanced chronic kidney disease","Electrolyte disturbance from severe polyuria and salt wasting"],"prognosis":"The renal disease is progressive and most affected individuals reach end-stage kidney disease during childhood or adolescence, requiring renal replacement therapy. Visual prognosis depends on the retinal genotype, ranging from profound early blindness to slowly progressive visual loss.","pitfalls":["Missing the renal diagnosis in a child evaluated only for visual impairment","Overlooking nephronophthisis because kidneys appear normal-sized on ultrasound","Attributing polyuria and enuresis to behavioural causes rather than a renal concentrating defect","Failing to test for the ciliopathy overlap that may include neurological features"],"management":["Provide supportive nephrology management of progressive nephronophthisis, as no disease-specific therapy halts the ciliopathy","Manage chronic kidney disease complications including anaemia with erythropoiesis-stimulating agents and iron, growth failure with growth hormone, and renal osteodystrophy and electrolyte balance","Plan for renal replacement therapy, favouring transplantation, which is not affected by disease recurrence in the graft","Offer low-vision rehabilitation and aids for the inherited retinal dystrophy, for which no restorative treatment exists","Undertake surveillance of renal function and urinary concentrating ability alongside electroretinography and ophthalmological review","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across paediatric nephrology, ophthalmology, and clinical genetics","Provide genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive ciliopathy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","Pediatric Nephrology (Avner)","GeneReviews: Nephronophthisis-Related Ciliopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"sensenbrenner_syndrome":{"name":"Sensenbrenner syndrome (cranioectodermal dysplasia)","system":"Skeletal / Multisystem (ciliopathy)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cranioectodermal dysplasia","CED","Levin-Sensenbrenner syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical medical genetics and ciliopathy references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sensenbrenner syndrome is an autosomal recessive skeletal ciliopathy caused by defects in intraflagellar transport, most often in genes such as IFT122, WDR35, IFT43, WDR19 and IFT52.","The craniofacial hallmark is dolichocephaly from sagittal craniosynostosis, with frontal bossing, a broad forehead and low-set ears.","Ectodermal features include sparse, slow-growing fine hair, small widely spaced or hypoplastic teeth (microdontia), nail dysplasia and dry skin.","Skeletal features comprise a narrow thorax, short limbs (rhizomelic shortening), brachydactyly and short stature.","Progressive chronic kidney disease from a nephronophthisis-like tubulointerstitial nephropathy is a major cause of morbidity and can progress to end-stage kidney failure.","Hepatic fibrosis, retinal dystrophy and, in some, cardiac defects extend the multisystem ciliopathy picture and overlap with other short-rib/IFT disorders.","The narrow thorax may cause respiratory insufficiency in infancy, an important early determinant of outcome.","As a ciliopathy it overlaps clinically with Ellis-van Creveld syndrome, Jeune asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy and other short-rib disorders, so molecular testing is key."],"pathophysiology":"Sensenbrenner syndrome is a genetically heterogeneous ciliopathy caused by biallelic variants in genes encoding components of the intraflagellar transport (IFT) machinery, particularly the retrograde IFT-A complex (IFT122, WDR35/IFT121, IFT43, WDR19/IFT144) and, in some, the anterograde or core apparatus (IFT52). Intraflagellar transport moves protein cargo bidirectionally along the axoneme of primary cilia, and the IFT-A complex mediates retrograde transport back to the cell body and is required for normal ciliary assembly, structure and signalling. Because primary cilia coordinate developmental signalling pathways (including hedgehog signalling) in bone, ectoderm, kidney, liver and retina, disruption of IFT produces a pleiotropic disorder: abnormal cranial suture and skeletal patterning, defective ectodermal derivatives, and progressive fibrotic damage in the kidney (a nephronophthisis-like tubulointerstitial process) and liver. The specific gene and variant severity influence which systems dominate, explaining the phenotypic overlap with other IFT-related skeletal ciliopathies.","additionalDifferentials":["Ellis-van Creveld syndrome","Jeune asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy (short-rib thoracic dysplasia)","Other short-rib polydactyly syndromes","Nephronophthisis and related renal ciliopathies","Bardet-Biedl syndrome","Isolated ectodermal dysplasias","Isolated (non-syndromic) craniosynostosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal function tests and urinalysis with monitoring for progressive tubulointerstitial nephropathy","Renal ultrasound for cyst formation and echogenicity","Liver function tests and hepatic imaging for fibrosis","Skeletal survey and cranial imaging to define craniosynostosis and thoracic dimensions","Ophthalmological assessment including electroretinography for retinal dystrophy","Echocardiography for associated cardiac defects","Molecular genetic testing of an IFT/ciliopathy gene panel"],"redFlags":["Respiratory insufficiency from a narrow thorax in infancy","Declining kidney function or signs of established kidney failure","Raised intracranial pressure from craniosynostosis","Progressive hepatic dysfunction or portal hypertension","Progressive visual loss from retinal dystrophy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the severity of thoracic and renal involvement: neonatal respiratory insufficiency and progressive chronic kidney disease progressing to end-stage failure are the main threats to life, whereas milder cases may reach adulthood with supportive and organ-specific management.","pitfalls":["Treating the craniosynostosis in isolation and missing the underlying multisystem ciliopathy, including silent early kidney disease.","Not monitoring renal and hepatic function longitudinally, as involvement can be progressive and initially asymptomatic.","Confusing it with Ellis-van Creveld or Jeune syndrome without molecular confirmation given the overlapping skeletal features.","Overlooking retinal dystrophy that may only be detected on formal ophthalmological testing.","Underestimating respiratory risk from the narrow thorax in infancy."],"management":["Provide multisystem supportive management of this intraflagellar-transport ciliopathy, for which no curative therapy exists","Arrange surgical correction of sagittal craniosynostosis to relieve raised intracranial pressure and allow brain growth","Support the narrow thorax with respiratory care, treating neonatal respiratory insufficiency intensively","Manage the nephronophthisis-like tubulointerstitial nephropathy and its progression, with renal replacement therapy for end-stage kidney disease","Undertake surveillance for hepatic fibrosis, retinal dystrophy with electroretinography, and cardiac defects with echocardiography, alongside dental and ectodermal supportive care","Coordinate multidisciplinary input across clinical genetics, nephrology, craniofacial surgery, and ophthalmology","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbooks of clinical genetics and dysmorphology","Peer-reviewed reviews of skeletal ciliopathies and intraflagellar transport disorders","Clinical nephrology reference resources"],"crossLinks":[]},"sensorimotor_lacunar_stroke":{"name":"sensorimotor lacunar stroke","system":"nervous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sensorimotor lacunar syndrome","thalamocapsular lacunar stroke"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Combines unilateral weakness and sensory loss of the face, arm and leg without cortical signs, classically from a lacune at the junction of the thalamus and adjacent posterior internal capsule.","The lesion typically involves thalamocapsular territory supplied by penetrating arteries, interrupting both ascending sensory relay and descending corticospinal fibres.","Absence of aphasia, neglect, hemianopia and gaze palsy distinguishes it from a large middle cerebral artery cortical infarct.","It is one of the classical Fisher lacunar syndromes and shares hypertensive small-vessel pathology with the pure motor and pure sensory variants.","A comparable clinical picture can arise from a small primary intracerebral haemorrhage in the same region, so imaging is essential.","Diffusion-weighted MRI reliably localises the small deep infarct when CT is unrevealing.","Because the deficit is 'complete' across motor and sensory domains, functional impact can exceed that of the single-modality lacunar syndromes.","Secondary prevention rests on blood pressure, glycaemic and lipid control together with antiplatelet therapy."],"pathophysiology":"Sensorimotor lacunar stroke is produced by occlusion of a small penetrating artery supplying the region where the ventral posterior thalamic nuclei lie immediately adjacent to the posterior limb of the internal capsule. A single lacune here simultaneously interrupts the crossed sensory pathways relaying through the thalamus and the descending corticospinal motor fibres in the capsule, yielding combined contralateral sensory loss and weakness affecting face, arm and leg. As with other lacunar strokes, the responsible arteriopathy is usually hypertensive lipohyalinosis or microatheroma of a deep perforating end-artery, producing a small cavitated infarct without involvement of the overlying cortex.","additionalDifferentials":["Large-vessel cortical infarct with combined sensorimotor deficit","Thalamocapsular or capsular intracerebral haemorrhage","Pure motor hemiparesis or pure sensory stroke early in evolution","Space-occupying lesion compressing thalamus and capsule","Demyelinating lesion involving sensory and motor tracts","Postictal (Todd's) deficit after a focal seizure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diffusion-weighted and T2/FLAIR MRI of the brain","Non-contrast CT head to exclude deep haemorrhage","Carotid duplex and intracranial vascular imaging","ECG and extended cardiac rhythm monitoring","Fasting glucose, HbA1c and lipid profile","Ambulatory blood pressure measurement"],"redFlags":["Deteriorating conscious level or severe headache suggesting haemorrhage","Emergence of cortical signs indicating larger-territory infarction","Stuttering or crescendo progression of the deficit","New brainstem signs implying more extensive posterior-circulation ischaemia"],"prognosis":"Recovery is variable and often slower than in single-modality lacunar syndromes because both motor and sensory function are affected, though many patients still regain useful independence; recurrent small-vessel events and vascular cognitive decline remain important risks.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a thalamocapsular haemorrhage for ischaemic lacunar stroke without imaging.","Classifying the event as cortical and pursuing unnecessary investigation when cortical signs are genuinely absent.","Assuming a normal early CT excludes infarction rather than proceeding to diffusion MRI.","Underestimating rehabilitation needs given the combined sensory and motor loss.","Failing to screen for a co-existent embolic source in a patient with small-vessel disease."],"management":["Confirm ischaemia and exclude deep haemorrhage with urgent imaging, then offer intravenous thrombolysis with alteplase or tenecteplase to eligible patients within the treatment window","Start antiplatelet therapy with aspirin acutely, transitioning to long-term secondary prevention","Institute secondary prevention with a long-term antiplatelet agent and high-intensity statin","Control blood pressure with antihypertensive therapy as the key modifier of hypertensive small-vessel disease, alongside glycaemic control and smoking cessation","Begin early multidisciplinary rehabilitation with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, and sensory retraining for the combined motor and sensory deficit","Monitor for recurrent lacunar events and vascular cognitive decline, escalating to specialist stroke review as needed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Caplan's Stroke: A Clinical Approach"],"crossLinks":[]},"sepiapterin_reductase_deficiency":{"name":"Sepiapterin reductase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPR deficiency","SR deficiency","dopa-responsive dystonia due to sepiapterin reductase deficiency","BH4 biosynthesis defect (SPR)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID) / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder of tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) synthesis caused by SPR mutations, reducing dopamine and serotonin production while sparing systemic phenylalanine handling","A striking feature is that blood phenylalanine is typically normal, so the condition is missed by newborn phenylketonuria screening and often diagnosed late","It presents as a levodopa-responsive dystonia and parkinsonism, frequently with diurnal fluctuation (worse later in the day), developmental delay, oculogyric crises, and sometimes cognitive and behavioural difficulty","Because both dopamine and serotonin are deficient, treatment usually combines levodopa with a serotonin precursor to address the dual neurotransmitter defect","The CSF hallmark is low homovanillic acid and low 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid with raised sepiapterin and dihydrobiopterin, reflecting the block in BH4 regeneration","Diagnosis rests on CSF neurotransmitter and pterin analysis, enzyme studies, and SPR sequencing; early recognition can transform the motor and developmental outcome"],"pathophysiology":"Sepiapterin reductase catalyses the final step of tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) synthesis. BH4 is the essential cofactor for tyrosine hydroxylase and tryptophan hydroxylase, so its deficiency impairs synthesis of both dopamine and serotonin. Because alternative pathways can regenerate enough BH4 for hepatic phenylalanine hydroxylase, systemic phenylalanine metabolism is preserved and newborn screening is normal, yet central monoamine neurotransmission is deficient. The result is a mixed dopaminergic and serotonergic deficiency causing levodopa-responsive dystonia and parkinsonism with diurnal variation, and accumulation of upstream pterins such as sepiapterin and dihydrobiopterin.","additionalDifferentials":["Tyrosine hydroxylase deficiency","GTP cyclohydrolase I deficiency (autosomal dominant Segawa dystonia)","6-pyruvoyl-tetrahydropterin synthase and dihydropteridine reductase deficiencies","Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase deficiency","Dystonic or mixed cerebral palsy","Idiopathic early-onset dystonia or juvenile parkinsonism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cerebrospinal fluid neurotransmitter metabolites showing low homovanillic acid and low 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid","CSF pterin profile with raised sepiapterin and dihydrobiopterin","SPR gene sequencing to confirm the diagnosis","Plasma phenylalanine (characteristically normal) and phenylalanine loading test where used","Fibroblast sepiapterin reductase enzyme activity assay","Documented clinical response to combined levodopa and serotonin-precursor therapy"],"redFlags":["Progressive dystonia and parkinsonism in a child mislabelled as cerebral palsy","Marked diurnal fluctuation with severe evening deterioration limiting function","Recurrent oculogyric crises and developmental regression","Behavioural or mood disturbance suggesting serotonergic deficiency needing treatment adjustment"],"prognosis":"With early combined neurotransmitter-precursor therapy many patients show substantial and sustained improvement in motor and developmental function, whereas delayed treatment may leave residual cognitive and motor deficits. The disorder is treatable rather than curable, and long-term follow-up guides dose adjustment.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal newborn phenylalanine screen excludes a BH4-related disorder","Treating only the dopaminergic deficit and omitting serotonin-precursor therapy","Not performing CSF pterin and neurotransmitter analysis, which is central to the diagnosis","Overlooking the diagnosis in older children presenting with fluctuating dystonia and learning difficulty"],"management":["Correct the dual neurotransmitter deficit with levodopa combined with a dopa-decarboxylase inhibitor and a serotonin precursor such as 5-hydroxytryptophan, the definitive treatment","Consider adjunctive folinic acid and titrate therapy carefully against clinical response and CSF metabolite levels","Provide developmental therapies, physiotherapy, and support for dystonia and oculogyric crises","Monitor for levodopa-related dyskinesia and serotonergic adverse effects, adjusting doses accordingly","Recognise and treat early, since this is a treatable disorder in which prompt therapy can transform motor and developmental outcome","Coordinate multidisciplinary care between metabolic medicine and paediatric neurology","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive SPR-related condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"septic_bursitis":{"name":"Septic bursitis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["infective bursitis","infected bursa","septic olecranon bursitis","septic prepatellar bursitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most often affects the superficial olecranon and prepatellar bursae, which are prone to trauma and abrasion","Infection usually follows direct inoculation through breached skin rather than haematogenous spread","Staphylococcus aureus is the predominant organism, with beta-haemolytic streptococci next most common","Distinguishing septic from non-septic (crystal, traumatic, or inflammatory) bursitis is the central clinical challenge and may require bursal fluid aspiration","Overlying warmth, erythema, tenderness, and fever favour infection but can be mimicked by gout or trauma","Unlike septic arthritis, passive joint movement is typically well preserved because the bursa lies outside the joint","Management principles combine aspiration for diagnosis, antistaphylococcal antibiotics, and drainage of persistent or loculated collections","Recurrent or refractory cases may need surgical bursectomy, and predisposing occupational trauma should be addressed"],"pathophysiology":"A bursa is a synovial-lined sac that reduces friction over bony prominences. Superficial bursae such as the olecranon and prepatellar sit just beneath the skin and are readily seeded by direct trauma or overlying cellulitis. Introduced bacteria, usually skin flora, provoke an inflammatory exudate within the bursal cavity, producing pain, swelling, warmth, and sometimes abscess formation. Because the bursa is anatomically separate from the joint, infection tends to remain localised unless it extends into surrounding tissue.","additionalDifferentials":["Crystal-induced (gout or pseudogout) bursitis","Traumatic or haemorrhagic bursitis","Rheumatoid or other inflammatory bursitis","Cellulitis overlying the bursa","Septic arthritis of the adjacent joint"],"infectionMimics":["Acute gouty bursitis or tophaceous deposits","Repetitive-strain (occupational) non-infective bursitis","Rheumatoid nodule with overlying inflammation","Post-traumatic haematoma of the bursa"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Aspiration of bursal fluid for Gram stain, culture, cell count, and crystal analysis","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers when systemic signs are present","Comparison of bursal fluid findings with adjacent joint fluid if joint infection is a concern","Ultrasound to confirm a bursal collection and guide aspiration","Assessment for underlying immunosuppression or predisposing trauma"],"redFlags":["Spread into the adjacent joint or deep tissues suggesting septic arthritis or deeper infection","Systemic sepsis or rapidly advancing surrounding cellulitis requiring urgent treatment","Failure to improve after aspiration and antibiotics indicating a loculated or abscessed collection needing drainage","Immunocompromised host in whom infection may progress atypically or rapidly"],"prognosis":"Superficial septic bursitis generally resolves well with aspiration and appropriate antibiotics; recurrence or chronicity is more likely with repeated trauma or delayed treatment, and a minority require surgical bursectomy.","pitfalls":["Assuming an inflamed bursa is gouty or traumatic and missing infection","Confusing septic bursitis with septic arthritis, which requires more aggressive intervention","Injecting corticosteroid into a bursa without excluding infection","Stopping treatment prematurely and precipitating relapse"],"management":["Aspirate the infected bursa for both diagnostic fluid analysis and therapeutic decompression","Start empirical antistaphylococcal antibiotics covering Staphylococcus aureus and beta-haemolytic streptococci, narrowing therapy to culture and sensitivity results","Repeat aspiration for reaccumulation and reserve surgical bursectomy or incision and drainage for abscess formation or refractory infection","Provide supportive analgesia, rest, and elevation of the affected limb","Address predisposing factors such as recurrent trauma over the bursa and underlying immunosuppression","Escalate for admission and intravenous therapy if there is spreading cellulitis, systemic sepsis, or failure to respond to oral treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"septo_optic_dysplasia":{"name":"Septo-optic dysplasia (de Morsier)","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["de Morsier syndrome","SOD","septo-optic-pituitary dysplasia","HESX1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski / Taylor & Hoyt Paediatric Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by a variable triad of optic nerve hypoplasia, midline brain abnormalities (absent septum pellucidum or corpus callosum) and hypothalamic-pituitary dysfunction","Diagnosis requires at least two of the three components, so presentations range from isolated visual impairment to complex endocrine and neurological disease","Optic nerve hypoplasia may be unilateral or bilateral and often causes nystagmus and reduced acuity","Endocrine dysfunction is common and may include growth hormone deficiency, hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency and diabetes insipidus","Rare familial cases are linked to developmental transcription factor genes such as HESX1, but most are sporadic","Neonatal hypoglycaemia, prolonged jaundice and micropenis can be early clues to pituitary failure","Coordinated ophthalmology, endocrinology and neurology care is essential"],"pathophysiology":"Septo-optic dysplasia is a disorder of early forebrain and midline development in which the optic nerves, septum pellucidum, corpus callosum and hypothalamic-pituitary axis form abnormally. Disruption of the developmental programme, occasionally attributable to mutations in transcription factors such as HESX1 but usually of unknown cause, leads to a spectrum of malformations. Hypoplasia of the optic nerves reduces the ganglion cell axon population, midline defects reflect failed commissural and septal development, and hypothalamic-pituitary maldevelopment produces variable anterior and posterior pituitary hormone deficiencies. The heterogeneous phenotype mirrors which structures are most affected.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated optic nerve hypoplasia","Holoprosencephaly spectrum disorders","Isolated congenital hypopituitarism","Other midline brain malformations","Congenital nystagmus of other cause","Leber congenital amaurosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain and pituitary region assessing midline structures","Comprehensive pituitary hormone evaluation and dynamic endocrine testing","OCT and visual evoked potentials for optic nerve assessment","Cycloplegic refraction and orthoptic evaluation","Developmental and neurological assessment","Genetic testing where a familial or syndromic pattern is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neonatal hypoglycaemia or adrenal crisis from cortisol deficiency","Diabetes insipidus with hypernatraemia and dehydration","Growth failure and hypothyroidism impairing development","Seizures or developmental regression suggesting extensive brain involvement","Temperature dysregulation from hypothalamic dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the balance of the three components; visual impairment is usually stable, but timely diagnosis and lifelong hormone replacement are critical to prevent morbidity and mortality from endocrine failure.","pitfalls":["Treating optic nerve hypoplasia in isolation and missing life-threatening endocrine deficiency","Assuming a normal septum pellucidum excludes the diagnosis","Delaying hormone replacement in a decompensating infant","Underestimating the neurodevelopmental and hypothalamic components"],"management":["Provide lifelong pituitary hormone replacement tailored to identified deficiencies, including hydrocortisone, levothyroxine, growth hormone, desmopressin for diabetes insipidus, and sex steroids at puberty","Recognise and urgently treat neonatal hypoglycaemia and adrenal crisis as presenting emergencies","Offer low-vision support, refractive correction, and orthoptic management of nystagmus for optic nerve hypoplasia","Provide developmental and neurological therapies for associated impairment","Undertake regular endocrine surveillance and monitoring of growth and puberty","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across paediatric endocrinology, ophthalmology, and neurology","Give stress-dose corticosteroid cover during intercurrent illness and offer genetic counselling where a familial or HESX1-associated pattern is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Taylor & Hoyt's Paediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"serine_deficiency_syndrome":{"name":"Serine biosynthesis defect (3-phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase deficiency)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["serine deficiency syndrome","3-phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase deficiency","PHGDH deficiency","serine biosynthesis defect","Neu-Laxova syndrome (severe PHGDH spectrum)","phosphoserine aminotransferase deficiency (PSAT1)","phosphoserine phosphatase deficiency (PSPH)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from defects in the biosynthesis of L-serine, most commonly 3-phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase (PHGDH) deficiency, with allelic defects at PSAT1 and PSPH producing a similar spectrum","L-serine is a conditionally essential amino acid needed for phospholipids, nucleotides, glycine, and D-serine, so deficiency severely affects the developing nervous system","The classic infantile phenotype is congenital microcephaly, intractable seizures, and profound psychomotor retardation","The severe end of the spectrum is Neu-Laxova syndrome, a lethal condition with severe microcephaly, ichthyosis, and multiple congenital anomalies","The biochemical clue is low serine (and often low glycine) in plasma and cerebrospinal fluid, with CSF sampling being especially informative as levels may be low even when fasting plasma is borderline","This is one of the treatable causes of these features: oral L-serine supplementation, sometimes with glycine, can reduce seizures and improve outcome, especially if started early","Inheritance is autosomal recessive; prenatal or presymptomatic treatment is being explored for at-risk pregnancies","British spelling applies (paediatric)"],"pathophysiology":"L-serine is synthesised de novo from the glycolytic intermediate 3-phosphoglycerate in three steps catalysed by 3-phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase, phosphoserine aminotransferase, and phosphoserine phosphatase. Because the blood-brain barrier limits serine entry, the brain depends heavily on local synthesis, so a block in this pathway causes a functional serine deficiency in the central nervous system. Serine is required for synthesis of glycine, D-serine (an NMDA-receptor co-agonist), phospholipids, and nucleotides essential for neuronal growth and myelination. Deficiency therefore impairs brain development, producing microcephaly, seizures, and severe neurological impairment, with the most profound defects causing the multisystem malformations of Neu-Laxova syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Other genetic causes of congenital microcephaly with seizures","Vitamin-responsive epileptic encephalopathies (e.g. pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy)","Glycine encephalopathy (non-ketotic hyperglycinaemia) at the other extreme of glycine metabolism","Congenital infections causing microcephaly","Other neurometabolic epileptic encephalopathies","Ichthyosis-associated syndromes in the severe presentation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma amino acids showing low serine and often low glycine (fasting sample preferred)","Cerebrospinal fluid amino acids demonstrating low serine, which is more sensitive","Brain MRI for microcephaly and white-matter/myelination abnormalities","EEG to characterise the seizure disorder","Molecular analysis of PHGDH, PSAT1, and PSPH","Assessment of response to serine supplementation"],"redFlags":["Intractable neonatal or infantile seizures with microcephaly requiring urgent evaluation","Progressive neurological deterioration in an untreated infant","Features of Neu-Laxova syndrome indicating a severe, often lethal phenotype","Missed opportunity for early treatable intervention"],"prognosis":"Untreated infantile disease causes severe neurological impairment, but early L-serine (with glycine) supplementation can markedly reduce seizures and improve development, making early recognition critical. The Neu-Laxova end of the spectrum is typically lethal.","pitfalls":["Overlooking a treatable cause of microcephaly and seizures by not measuring serine","Relying on plasma alone when CSF serine is more revealing","Delaying serine supplementation, which reduces the chance of benefit","Not recognising Neu-Laxova syndrome as part of the same biosynthetic defect"],"management":["Correct the central serine deficit with oral L-serine supplementation, often combined with glycine, the definitive substrate-replacement therapy","Start treatment as early as possible, including antenatally in known families, because early intervention markedly improves neurological outcome","Control seizures with antiepileptic drugs alongside serine supplementation","Provide developmental therapies and feeding support for microcephaly and psychomotor impairment","Monitor plasma and cerebrospinal fluid serine and seizure control to titrate supplementation","Coordinate multidisciplinary care between metabolic medicine and neurology","Offer genetic counselling, prenatal testing, and family screening for this autosomal recessive PHGDH, PSAT1, or PSPH defect"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: disorders of serine biosynthesis","Nyhan, Atlas of Inherited Metabolic Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"serotonin_syndrome":{"name":"Serotonin syndrome","system":"Clinical pharmacology / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["serotonin toxicity","serotonergic syndrome","SSRI toxicity","serotonin toxidrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A potentially life-threatening toxidrome caused by excess serotonergic activity, often from combining serotonergic drugs or from overdose","The clinical triad is neuromuscular excitation (clonus, hyperreflexia, tremor, rigidity), autonomic instability (hyperthermia, tachycardia, sweating, diarrhoea) and altered mental state (agitation, confusion)","Inducible and spontaneous clonus, often more prominent in the lower limbs, is a particularly useful diagnostic sign","Onset is typically rapid, within hours of a dose change or added serotonergic agent, distinguishing it from neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Culprits include SSRIs, SNRIs, MAO inhibitors, tramadol, triptans, linezolid, certain opioids and recreational drugs; MAOI combinations are especially dangerous","Neuromuscular findings help distinguish it from neuroleptic malignant syndrome, which causes lead-pipe rigidity with hyporeflexia and a slower onset","Management is withdrawal of serotonergic agents, aggressive cooling and supportive care, benzodiazepines for agitation, and cyproheptadine as a serotonin antagonist in significant cases","Severe hyperthermia may require sedation, paralysis and intubation to control muscle activity"],"pathophysiology":"Serotonin syndrome results from excessive stimulation of central and peripheral serotonin receptors, particularly the 5-HT2A subtype. Excess serotonergic activity can arise from increased synthesis or release, reduced reuptake, reduced metabolism (as with monoamine oxidase inhibitors), or direct receptor agonism, and is greatly amplified when drugs acting by different mechanisms are combined. The over-stimulation drives neuromuscular hyperactivity (clonus, hyperreflexia, rigidity), autonomic dysregulation (hyperthermia, tachycardia, blood pressure lability, diaphoresis, gastrointestinal hurry) and altered mental status. Severe hyperthermia is generated largely by sustained muscular activity and can lead to rhabdomyolysis, metabolic acidosis, disseminated intravascular coagulation and multiorgan failure if untreated.","additionalDifferentials":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Anticholinergic toxidrome","Sympathomimetic toxicity","Malignant hyperthermia","Meningitis or encephalitis","Thyroid storm","Sedative or alcohol withdrawal"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful medication and drug history to identify serotonergic agents and interactions","Core temperature monitoring","Creatine kinase for rhabdomyolysis","Renal function, electrolytes and coagulation profile","Arterial blood gas and lactate in severe cases","Exclusion of infection (including lumbar puncture) where meningitis is possible"],"redFlags":["Severe hyperthermia","Marked rigidity limiting ventilation","Rhabdomyolysis with renal impairment","Seizures or rapidly declining consciousness","Disseminated intravascular coagulation or cardiovascular instability"],"prognosis":"Most cases resolve within a day or two of stopping the offending drugs and providing supportive care. Severe hyperthermic cases can be fatal or leave lasting organ injury, so early aggressive treatment is important.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with neuroleptic malignant syndrome and missing the distinguishing clonus and hyperreflexia","Overlooking non-psychiatric serotonergic drugs such as tramadol, linezolid or triptans","Underestimating the danger of MAO inhibitor combinations","Failing to control hyperthermia aggressively in severe cases","Attributing agitation and fever to the underlying illness rather than drug toxicity"],"management":["Immediately discontinue all serotonergic agents, including SSRIs, SNRIs, MAO inhibitors, tramadol, triptans, and linezolid","Provide supportive care with intravenous fluids and benzodiazepine sedation to control agitation and neuromuscular hyperactivity","Give the serotonin antagonist cyproheptadine in moderate to severe cases that do not settle with supportive measures","Manage hyperthermia with active external cooling, and in severe hyperthermic rigidity use neuromuscular paralysis with intubation rather than antipyretics","Monitor for and treat complications including rhabdomyolysis, metabolic acidosis, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and acute kidney injury","Provide continuous core temperature and cardiac monitoring, escalating severe cases to critical care","Reconcile medications on recovery to prevent recurrence from reintroduced serotonergic combinations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"serous_cystadenoma_ovary":{"name":"Ovarian serous cystadenoma","system":"Gynaecology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["benign serous ovarian tumour","serous cystadenoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common benign epithelial ovarian tumour lined by tubal-type serous epithelium and filled with thin, watery fluid","Usually presents in reproductive-age and perimenopausal women as an asymptomatic cyst or with vague pelvic pressure","Typically a thin-walled, unilocular cyst with clear fluid and few or no septations on ultrasound, and is bilateral in a proportion of cases","Psammoma bodies (concentric calcifications) may be present histologically","Distinguished from borderline and malignant serous tumours by the absence of epithelial proliferation, atypia, and stromal invasion","Complications are mechanical, chiefly torsion or rupture, rather than invasive spread","Managed by observation for small simple cysts or by cystectomy or oophorectomy when large, symptomatic, or diagnostically uncertain"],"pathophysiology":"Serous cystadenomas are benign surface-epithelial tumours whose lining recapitulates the ciliated, tubal-type epithelium of the fallopian tube, reflecting the current understanding that many serous ovarian tumours derive from tubal or Mullerian epithelium. The epithelium is a single, orderly layer without the stratification, tufting, or cytological atypia seen in borderline and malignant counterparts, and there is no stromal invasion. Secretion of serous fluid by this epithelium produces a thin-walled cyst, and concentric calcified psammoma bodies may form. Because growth is non-invasive, clinical problems arise from the size of the cyst and mechanical events such as torsion or rupture rather than from local or distant spread.","additionalDifferentials":["Serous borderline tumour or low-grade serous carcinoma","Mucinous cystadenoma","Functional or simple ovarian cyst","Paratubal or paraovarian cyst","Endometrioma","Hydrosalpinx"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound to characterise a thin-walled unilocular cyst","Pelvic MRI when the lesion is large or indeterminate","CA125 and risk-of-malignancy assessment to help stratify the lesion","Serial ultrasound follow-up for small simple cysts","Histology to confirm a benign lining and exclude a borderline or malignant component","Doppler assessment if torsion is suspected"],"redFlags":["Solid or papillary components, thick septations, or ascites suggesting a borderline or malignant tumour","Acute pain indicating torsion or rupture","Rapid enlargement of a previously stable cyst","A markedly raised CA125 in a complex adnexal mass"],"prognosis":"Excellent, as serous cystadenomas are benign and cured by complete excision, with no tendency to invasive spread; recurrence is uncommon and usually represents a new lesion.","pitfalls":["Assuming any serous cystic lesion is benign and missing a borderline or malignant tumour","Rupturing or incompletely excising a cyst and prompting recurrence","Over-relying on a normal CA125, which is frequently normal even in benign disease and can be raised for non-neoplastic reasons","Overlooking torsion as a cause of acute presentation"],"management":["Manage a confidently benign simple cyst conservatively with serial ultrasound surveillance in appropriate women","Undertake surgical removal by fertility-sparing ovarian cystectomy or oophorectomy for large, symptomatic, or indeterminate lesions, preferring a laparoscopic approach that minimises rupture","Stratify malignant risk before surgery using CA125 and the risk-of-malignancy index","Manage acute torsion or cyst rupture as a surgical emergency","Confirm the benign serous lining on histology to exclude a borderline or malignant component","Refer to gynaecology for planning of surveillance versus surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","Blaustein's Pathology of the Female Genital Tract"],"crossLinks":[]},"serous_cystadenoma_pancreas":{"name":"Serous cystadenoma of the pancreas","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["serous cystadenoma","microcystic adenoma","serous cystic neoplasm","SCA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign cystic neoplasm composed of glycogen-rich cuboidal epithelium, with a very low risk of malignant transformation","More common in older women and often discovered incidentally","The classic microcystic form has numerous small cysts producing a honeycomb appearance, sometimes with a central stellate scar and calcification","The cyst fluid is typically low in CEA, helping to distinguish it from mucinous cystic lesions","Less common macrocystic (oligocystic) variants can mimic mucinous neoplasms on imaging and are harder to characterise","Because it is benign, asymptomatic serous cystadenoma can usually be managed by observation once the diagnosis is confident","Resection is reserved for diagnostic uncertainty, large or rapidly enlarging lesions, or symptoms from mass effect"],"pathophysiology":"Serous cystadenomas arise from centroacinar or ductal epithelial cells and are lined by a single layer of glycogen-rich, cuboidal serous epithelium that secretes thin, watery fluid. The cells proliferate to form clusters of cysts, most often numerous small locules separated by thin fibrous septa that radiate from a central scar, giving the characteristic microcystic and honeycomb morphology; calcification may develop within the scar. Many of these tumours are associated with inactivation of the VHL tumour-suppressor gene, and lesions are common in von Hippel-Lindau disease. The epithelium lacks the mucinous phenotype and malignant potential of mucinous cystic neoplasms, so the fluid CEA is characteristically low and malignant change is exceedingly rare.","additionalDifferentials":["Mucinous cystic neoplasm of the pancreas","Branch-duct intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm","Pancreatic pseudocyst","Cystic pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour","Solid pseudopapillary neoplasm","Lymphangioma or other benign cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT or MRI to identify the microcystic pattern and central scar","Endoscopic ultrasound to characterise the cysts and septa","Fine-needle aspiration for cyst fluid CEA (characteristically low) and cytology","Cyst fluid glucose assessment, which tends to be high in serous lesions","MRCP to confirm absence of main duct communication","Genetic evaluation for von Hippel-Lindau disease when clinically suggested"],"redFlags":["Atypical or macrocystic morphology that cannot be distinguished from a mucinous neoplasm","Rapid growth or a very large lesion causing compressive symptoms","An enhancing solid component prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Obstructive jaundice or duct dilatation from mass effect"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent because malignant transformation is very rare; most lesions can be observed safely, and surgical resection, when required for symptoms or uncertainty, is curative.","pitfalls":["Mistaking an oligocystic (macrocystic) serous cystadenoma for a mucinous neoplasm and over-treating, or the reverse and under-treating","Over-reliance on imaging alone without confirmatory low cyst fluid CEA","Attempting aspiration of a highly vascular microcystic lesion with risk of bleeding","Recommending resection of a confidently benign, asymptomatic lesion"],"management":["Manage a confidently diagnosed asymptomatic lesion by observation with periodic surveillance imaging, given the very low malignant potential","Undertake surgical resection appropriate to site, such as distal pancreatectomy, enucleation, or pancreatoduodenectomy, for symptomatic, large, enlarging, or diagnostically uncertain lesions","Characterise the cyst with endoscopic ultrasound and fine-needle aspiration, using low fluid CEA and high fluid glucose to distinguish it from mucinous neoplasms","Manage mass-effect complications such as biliary or gastric outlet obstruction","Evaluate for von Hippel-Lindau disease when clinically suggested, with genetic counselling and surveillance if confirmed","Refer to a hepatobiliary and pancreatology multidisciplinary team for the resection-versus-observation decision"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","European evidence-based guidelines on pancreatic cystic neoplasms","WHO classification of tumours of the digestive system"],"crossLinks":[]},"serpiginous_choroiditis":{"name":"Serpiginous choroiditis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["serpiginous choroidopathy","geographic choroiditis","geographic helicoid peripapillary choroidopathy","GHPC"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare chronic, recurrent inflammation of the choriocapillaris, retinal pigment epithelium and outer retina, usually bilateral though often asymmetric","Typically affects healthy middle-aged adults and presents with painless blurring, central or paracentral scotoma and metamorphopsia","Active lesions are grey-white, geographic patches classically beginning around the optic disc and extending centrifugally in a serpentine (snakelike) pattern","Healed areas leave scalloped zones of chorioretinal atrophy and pigment clumping, with new activity at the advancing edges of old scars","Fluorescein angiography shows early hypofluorescence of active lesions with late staining, while healed atrophic areas show window defects","Choroidal neovascularisation is an important vision-threatening complication, particularly at the margins of atrophic scars","A tuberculosis-associated serpiginous-like choroiditis must be excluded, as it requires antituberculous therapy rather than immunosuppression alone","Management principles rely on systemic corticosteroids and steroid-sparing immunosuppression to control inflammation and protect the fovea, with anti-VEGF for secondary neovascularisation"],"pathophysiology":"Serpiginous choroiditis is a chronic, presumed autoimmune inflammatory disorder targeting the choriocapillaris and overlying retinal pigment epithelium and photoreceptors. Recurrent bouts of inflammation cause ischaemia and destruction of the choriocapillaris, producing geographic areas of chorioretinal atrophy that spread centrifugally from the peripapillary region. Each recurrence extends from the edge of previously healed lesions, giving the characteristic serpentine advancing border. Damage to the outer retina and pigment epithelium accounts for the scotomata, and disruption of the choriocapillaris predisposes to secondary choroidal neovascularisation. An infectious (particularly tuberculous) trigger produces a clinically similar multifocal picture in some patients.","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculosis-associated serpiginous-like choroiditis","Acute posterior multifocal placoid pigment epitheliopathy","Birdshot chorioretinopathy","Multifocal choroiditis and panuveitis","Persistent placoid maculopathy","Age-related macular degeneration with geographic atrophy","Syphilitic placoid chorioretinitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fluorescein angiography to define active (early hypofluorescent, late staining) versus healed lesions","Indocyanine green angiography to reveal the extent of choriocapillaris involvement","Fundus autofluorescence to distinguish active borders from atrophic scars and monitor progression","Optical coherence tomography to assess outer retinal integrity and detect choroidal neovascularisation","Testing for latent tuberculosis (interferon-gamma release assay or tuberculin skin test) and chest imaging","Syphilis serology and other infective screening to exclude mimics"],"redFlags":["Lesion activity threatening or approaching the fovea","New subretinal fluid, haemorrhage or exudate suggesting choroidal neovascularisation","Evidence of underlying tuberculosis requiring antimicrobial rather than purely immunosuppressive therapy","Rapid centrifugal progression despite treatment"],"prognosis":"Serpiginous choroiditis is a chronic relapsing disease; vision is preserved when the fovea is spared, but foveal involvement or secondary choroidal neovascularisation can cause permanent central visual loss, and recurrences may continue over many years.","pitfalls":["Treating with immunosuppression without excluding tuberculosis-associated disease","Mistaking healed atrophic scarring for active inflammation, or vice versa","Missing early choroidal neovascularisation arising at the edge of an old scar","Undertreating peripapillary activity that then extends unexpectedly toward the fovea","Confusing the appearance with geographic atrophy of age-related macular degeneration in older patients"],"management":["Suppress inflammation with systemic corticosteroids for acute flares, adding steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as antimetabolites (mycophenolate or azathioprine) or an alkylating agent for chronic control","Use adjunctive periocular or intravitreal corticosteroid to gain rapid local control of active lesions","Exclude and, where present, treat tuberculous serpiginous-like choroiditis with antituberculous therapy under corticosteroid cover before committing to immunosuppression","Treat secondary choroidal neovascularisation with intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy","Undertake surveillance with fundus autofluorescence, optical coherence tomography, and angiography to detect advancing lesion edges and neovascular complications","Refer to a uveitis or ophthalmology specialist and monitor for immunosuppression-related toxicity","Provide low-vision support where foveal involvement causes permanent central visual loss"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina"],"crossLinks":[]},"serrated_polyposis_syndrome":{"name":"Serrated polyposis syndrome","system":"Oncology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPS","hyperplastic polyposis syndrome","serrated polyposis"],"chapter":null,"source":"DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer / NCCN genetic/familial guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined clinically by the number, size and distribution of serrated polyps rather than by a single causative gene, and most cases lack an identified germline mutation","Diagnosis rests on established polyp-count and size criteria, such as multiple serrated polyps proximal to the rectum or a high cumulative lifetime polyp count","Confers an increased risk of colorectal cancer, which arises through the serrated neoplasia pathway rather than the classic adenoma-carcinoma sequence","Serrated lesions, especially sessile serrated lesions in the proximal colon, are flat and pale and easily missed, so high-quality colonoscopy with meticulous inspection is essential","First-degree relatives of affected individuals have a raised colorectal cancer risk and warrant earlier and more frequent colonoscopic screening","Management is regular colonoscopic surveillance with clearance of significant polyps, and surgery is reserved for uncontrollable polyp burden or malignancy","The serrated pathway is frequently associated with BRAF mutation, CpG island methylation and, in some tumours, acquired mismatch-repair deficiency"],"pathophysiology":"Serrated polyposis reflects dysregulation of the serrated neoplasia pathway, a route to colorectal cancer distinct from the conventional adenoma-carcinoma sequence. Sessile serrated lesions commonly carry activating BRAF mutations and develop widespread promoter hypermethylation known as the CpG island methylator phenotype, which silences tumour-suppressor and repair genes. Epigenetic silencing of a mismatch-repair gene in some lesions leads to acquired microsatellite instability, driving progression from serrated polyp to carcinoma; while the condition clusters in families, a consistent germline determinant has not been established for most patients.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial adenomatous polyposis and its attenuated form","MUTYH-associated polyposis","Lynch syndrome","Sporadic hyperplastic polyps","Hereditary mixed polyposis syndrome","Juvenile polyposis syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-quality colonoscopy with careful proximal-colon inspection and documentation of polyp number, size and location against diagnostic criteria","Histopathological classification of serrated lesions, including sessile serrated lesions and traditional serrated adenomas","Molecular testing of tumours for BRAF mutation and CpG island methylation where relevant","Mismatch-repair immunohistochemistry on carcinomas to detect acquired deficiency","Consideration of germline multigene panel testing when features suggest another syndrome","Colonoscopic screening of first-degree relatives at an earlier age"],"redFlags":["A large or endoscopically complex serrated lesion suspicious for malignancy","A discrete mass or advanced histology on biopsy","Iron-deficiency anaemia or rectal bleeding","Rapidly increasing polyp burden that cannot be cleared endoscopically"],"prognosis":"With diligent colonoscopic surveillance and complete removal of significant serrated lesions, colorectal cancer risk can be well controlled and prognosis is good. Risk persists lifelong and depends on the completeness of examinations, so meticulous ongoing surveillance and family screening are important.","pitfalls":["Missing flat, pale sessile serrated lesions in the proximal colon during suboptimal colonoscopy","Dismissing hyperplastic-appearing polyps without recognising the cumulative diagnostic criteria","Failing to screen first-degree relatives at increased colorectal cancer risk","Assuming a germline mutation must be present and stopping surveillance when testing is negative"],"management":["Undertake regular colonoscopic surveillance with complete endoscopic clearance of significant serrated polyps as the mainstay of management","Ensure high-quality colonoscopy with meticulous proximal-colon inspection, adequate bowel preparation, and image-enhancement techniques to detect flat, pale sessile serrated lesions","Reserve surgery such as colectomy for an uncontrollable polyp burden or established malignancy","Classify serrated lesions histopathologically and perform mismatch-repair immunohistochemistry on any carcinoma to detect acquired deficiency","Screen first-degree relatives colonoscopically at an earlier age given their raised colorectal cancer risk","Consider germline multigene panel testing when features suggest an alternative hereditary syndrome","Advise lifestyle risk reduction including smoking cessation as an adjunct to surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer: Principles and Practice of Oncology","NCCN Guidelines: Genetic/Familial High-Risk Assessment - Colorectal"],"crossLinks":[]},"sertoli_cell_tumor_testis":{"name":"Sertoli cell tumour of the testis","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sertoli cell tumor","androblastoma of the testis","large cell calcifying Sertoli cell tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sertoli cell tumour is a sex cord-stromal tumour that is usually benign, though a small proportion, more often in adults, are malignant","It typically presents as a painless testicular mass and may be hormonally active, most commonly producing oestrogen and causing gynaecomastia","Germ cell tumour markers (AFP, beta-hCG) are normal, helping to separate it from germ cell tumours","The large cell calcifying variant shows characteristic calcification on ultrasound, can be bilateral or multifocal, and is associated with genetic syndromes such as Carney complex and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","Assessment for an associated syndrome is warranted, especially with bilateral or calcifying tumours or a suggestive family history","Small benign-appearing lesions may be treated with testis-sparing surgery, while radical inguinal orchidectomy is used when malignancy is suspected","Histological features such as larger size, necrosis, vascular invasion, and increased mitoses suggest malignant potential and prompt staging and follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"Sertoli cell tumours arise from the Sertoli cells that normally support spermatogenesis within the seminiferous tubules. Neoplastic Sertoli cells may aromatise androgens to oestrogen, producing hyperoestrogenic effects such as gynaecomastia and, in children, endocrine disturbance. The large cell calcifying subtype, characterised by prominent calcification, occurs in the context of tumour-predisposition syndromes including Carney complex and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome. Most tumours are indolent and benign, but a minority display features associated with metastatic behaviour.","additionalDifferentials":["Germ cell tumour of the testis","Leydig cell tumour","Testicular lymphoma","Gonadoblastoma","Other sex cord-stromal tumours","Testicular microlithiasis mimicking calcification"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound, noting calcification and multifocal or bilateral lesions","Serum AFP and beta-hCG (normal) to exclude a germ cell tumour","Hormonal profile including oestradiol and testosterone when endocrine features are present","Evaluation for Carney complex or Peutz-Jeghers syndrome with calcifying or bilateral tumours","CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis if malignancy is suspected","Histological assessment of malignancy-associated features and margins"],"redFlags":["Large or rapidly enlarging tumour with malignant histological features","Evidence of metastatic spread on staging","Bilateral or calcifying tumours signalling an underlying tumour-predisposition syndrome","Marked hyperoestrogenic features such as progressive gynaecomastia"],"prognosis":"Most Sertoli cell tumours are benign and cured by excision; malignant variants, predominantly in adults, respond poorly to chemotherapy and radiotherapy and carry a guarded outlook once metastatic, while identification of an associated syndrome has implications for lifelong surveillance.","pitfalls":["Missing an associated Carney complex or Peutz-Jeghers syndrome in a patient with calcifying or bilateral tumours","Assuming a hormonally active mass is benign without assessing malignancy-associated features","Performing orchidectomy for a small benign lesion amenable to testis-sparing surgery","Mistaking tumour calcification for benign microlithiasis"],"management":["Perform radical inguinal orchidectomy where malignancy is suspected, reserving testis-sparing surgery for small, benign-appearing lesions","Evaluate and manage hormonal effects such as oestrogen-driven gynaecomastia","Assess for associated tumour-predisposition syndromes, particularly with bilateral or calcifying tumours, arranging surveillance for Carney complex or Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","Stage malignant variants with cross-sectional CT imaging and manage metastatic disease surgically, since these tumours respond poorly to chemotherapy and radiotherapy","Undertake surveillance imaging and confirm normal germ cell markers to monitor for malignant behaviour","Refer to a urology and oncology multidisciplinary team and offer genetic counselling for syndromic tumours"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","WHO Classification of Tumours: Urinary and Male Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"sertoli_cell_tumour":{"name":"Sertoli Cell Tumour","system":"Genitourinary (gonadal)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sertoli cell tumour NOS","SCT","Sertolioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Genitourinary oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Uncommon sex cord-stromal neoplasm arising from Sertoli (nurse) cells; most occur in the testis and account for only a small fraction of testicular tumours, with rarer ovarian counterparts.","Typical presentation is a painless testicular mass or swelling; hormonally active tumours may cause gynaecomastia, reduced libido or, in children, endocrine effects.","The large majority are benign; malignant behaviour is suggested by large size, necrosis, high mitotic activity, marked cytological atypia, vascular invasion and extratesticular spread.","Recognised subtypes include Sertoli cell tumour NOS, large cell calcifying Sertoli cell tumour, sclerosing Sertoli cell tumour, and intratubular large cell hyalinising Sertoli cell neoplasia.","Large cell calcifying Sertoli cell tumour is frequently syndromic — bilateral, multifocal and calcified tumours arising in Carney complex (PRKAR1A) or Peutz-Jeghers syndrome (STK11/LKB1).","Intratubular large cell hyalinising Sertoli cell neoplasia is strongly linked to Peutz-Jeghers syndrome and may cause prepubertal gynaecomastia.","Serum germ-cell markers (alpha-fetoprotein and beta-hCG) are typically normal, and immunohistochemistry is positive for sex cord markers (inhibin, calretinin, steroidogenic factor-1, WT1) but negative for germ-cell markers (OCT3/4, SALL4, PLAP).","Management is testis-sparing or radical orchidectomy; malignant tumours respond poorly to chemotherapy and radiotherapy, and retroperitoneal nodal disease may need surgery."],"pathophysiology":"Sertoli cell tumours are sex cord-stromal neoplasms derived from the Sertoli cell, the somatic supporting or nurse cell of the seminiferous tubule that normally sustains spermatogenesis and, through the sex cord lineage, shares an origin with the granulosa cell of the ovary. Many conventional (NOS) tumours carry activating CTNNB1 (beta-catenin) mutations with resulting nuclear beta-catenin accumulation and Wnt-pathway activation. The large cell calcifying subtype is characteristically associated with dysregulated cyclic-AMP / protein kinase A signalling through germline PRKAR1A mutation in Carney complex, and with germline STK11 (LKB1) mutation in Peutz-Jeghers syndrome; both may produce bilateral, multifocal, calcifying tumours. Because Sertoli cells can be steroidogenic, including aromatisation of androgens to oestrogen, some tumours are hormonally active and present with oestrogenic effects such as gynaecomastia.","additionalDifferentials":["Leydig cell tumour and Sertoli-Leydig cell tumour (mixed sex cord-stromal tumours)","Granulosa cell tumour (adult and juvenile types)","Distinction among the Sertoli cell tumour subtypes themselves (NOS versus large cell calcifying versus sclerosing)","Seminoma and other germ cell tumours (clear-cell mimicry)","Testicular adrenal rest tumours (in congenital adrenal hyperplasia) and Leydig cell hyperplasia","Gonadoblastoma and unclassified sex cord-stromal tumour","Testicular metastasis or lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound (hypoechoic mass; the large cell calcifying subtype shows characteristic calcification and may be bilateral or multifocal)","Serum germ-cell tumour markers (alpha-fetoprotein, beta-hCG, LDH), which are typically normal","Hormone profile where the tumour is hormonally active (oestrogens, testosterone, inhibin)","Histopathology with immunohistochemistry (inhibin, calretinin, steroidogenic factor-1, WT1, beta-catenin)","Staging cross-sectional imaging (CT chest, abdomen and pelvis) when malignant features are present","Genetic and syndrome evaluation for Carney complex (PRKAR1A) and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome (STK11) in large cell calcifying tumours","Echocardiography and screening for other Carney complex manifestations (cardiac myxoma, spotty pigmentation, endocrine tumours)"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting malignancy: a rapidly enlarging or large tumour, necrosis or extratesticular extension","Manifestations of Carney complex (cardiac myxoma, spotty skin pigmentation, endocrine overactivity) with a calcifying testicular tumour","Mucocutaneous pigmentation and gastrointestinal polyps suggesting Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","Evidence of metastatic spread (retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy, pulmonary, hepatic or skeletal lesions)"],"prognosis":"Most Sertoli cell tumours are benign and cured by orchidectomy. Malignant variants carry a worse outlook because they metastasise to retroperitoneal nodes, lung, liver and bone and respond poorly to chemotherapy and radiotherapy, so complete surgical excision and staging are important.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosis as seminoma or another germ cell tumour when the cytoplasm is clear — resolved by the immunohistochemical marker profile.","Assuming all Sertoli cell tumours are benign and under-staging those with malignant features.","Missing the syndromic association of large cell calcifying tumours (Carney complex, Peutz-Jeghers), including bilateral disease.","Overlooking a small hormonally active tumour that presents with gynaecomastia before a mass is palpable.","Relying on normal serum tumour markers to exclude a testicular neoplasm."],"management":["Excise the tumour surgically, with radical inguinal orchidectomy for testicular lesions, testis-sparing surgery for small benign-appearing masses, and oophorectomy for ovarian counterparts","Confirm the diagnosis on histopathology with immunohistochemistry (inhibin, calretinin, steroidogenic factor-1, WT1, beta-catenin) and assess malignancy-associated features","Manage hormonal effects such as gynaecomastia and altered libido from steroidogenic, aromatising tumours","Stage malignant variants with cross-sectional imaging and manage metastatic spread to retroperitoneal nodes, lung, liver, and bone with multimodal therapy, recognising poor chemoradiotherapy response","Evaluate for and undertake surveillance of Carney complex (PRKAR1A) and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome (STK11) in large cell calcifying tumours","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for syndromic tumours","Refer to a urology or gynaecological-oncology multidisciplinary team for definitive management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Genitourinary oncology reference texts","Testicular tumour pathology references","International urological oncology guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"sertoli_leydig_cell_tumor":{"name":"Sertoli-Leydig cell tumour","system":"Gynaecology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["androblastoma","arrhenoblastoma","SLCT"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare sex cord-stromal tumour that classically secretes androgens and produces virilisation in young women","Typical sequence is defeminisation (oligomenorrhoea or amenorrhoea and loss of female contours) followed by frank virilisation with hirsutism, deepening voice, and clitoromegaly","Usually unilateral and often diagnosed at an early stage because the hormonal effects prompt investigation","Leydig-cell differentiation may show cytoplasmic Reinke crystals, a helpful histological clue","Tumours are graded from well to poorly differentiated, and a retiform pattern or heterologous elements can be present","A subset harbour DICER1 mutations, sometimes as part of the hereditary DICER1 syndrome, so a family and personal history of related tumours is relevant","Serum testosterone is typically raised, and management is usually surgical with fertility-sparing options in young women who have early-stage disease"],"pathophysiology":"Sertoli-Leydig cell tumours are sex cord-stromal neoplasms composed of Sertoli cells, Leydig cells, and variable stroma that recapitulate testicular differentiation within the ovary. The Leydig component frequently secretes testosterone and other androgens, which suppress normal ovarian function and produce the clinical progression from defeminisation to virilisation. Histological differentiation ranges from well-formed tubules in well-differentiated tumours to poorly organised, mitotically active sheets in poorly differentiated lesions, and retiform or heterologous (for example mucinous or mesenchymal) elements may occur. Many tumours, especially those with retiform or heterologous features, carry DICER1 mutations that impair microRNA processing, linking a proportion to the inherited DICER1 tumour-predisposition syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Polycystic ovary syndrome","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Androgen-secreting adrenal tumour","Steroid cell tumour of the ovary","Luteoma or hyperreactio luteinalis of pregnancy","Other sex cord-stromal tumours such as granulosa cell tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum testosterone and androgen profile to confirm hyperandrogenism","Transvaginal ultrasound to identify a solid ovarian mass","Adrenal and pelvic cross-sectional imaging to localise the androgen source","Dexamethasone suppression or related testing to distinguish adrenal from ovarian causes","Histology with immunohistochemistry (e.g. inhibin, calretinin) and DICER1 assessment where indicated","Genetic evaluation for DICER1 syndrome when clinically suggested"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive virilisation in a young woman suggesting an androgen-secreting tumour","Markedly elevated serum testosterone with a solid ovarian mass","Poorly differentiated histology or heterologous elements indicating higher malignant potential","Personal or family history of DICER1-associated tumours"],"prognosis":"Well-differentiated tumours are almost always benign and cured by conservative surgery, and androgenic features often regress afterwards. Intermediate and poorly differentiated tumours carry a real risk of recurrence and malignant behaviour, so outlook depends heavily on grade and stage.","pitfalls":["Attributing hyperandrogenism to polycystic ovary syndrome and missing a small androgen-secreting tumour","Failing to distinguish an ovarian from an adrenal source of androgen excess","Overlooking DICER1 syndrome and the implications for the patient and relatives","Underestimating malignant potential in poorly differentiated or retiform tumours"],"management":["Fertility-sparing unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy with surgical staging is the mainstay for well-differentiated, early-stage tumours in young women","Total hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy and debulking for advanced-stage or poorly differentiated disease in those who have completed childbearing","Adjuvant platinum-based combination chemotherapy (bleomycin-etoposide-cisplatin) for poorly differentiated, ruptured, or advanced-stage tumours","Symptomatic support for hirsutism and voice change, anticipating partial regression of virilising features after resection","Surveillance with serial serum testosterone and androgen profile as a recurrence marker, alongside pelvic imaging","Germline DICER1 testing with genetic counselling and family screening for DICER1-associated tumours where a mutation is confirmed","Coordinate care through a gynaecological oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","Blaustein's Pathology of the Female Genital Tract"],"crossLinks":[]},"sertoli_leydig_cell_tumour":{"name":"sertoli leydig cell tumour","system":"reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["arrhenoblastoma","androblastoma","Sertoli-Leydig cell tumour of ovary"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours; Berek & Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare sex-cord stromal tumour of young women that classically produces androgens, causing progressive virilisation: oligomenorrhoea or amenorrhoea, hirsutism, acne, clitoromegaly, deepening of the voice and loss of female body contour.","Preceding defeminisation (breast atrophy, cessation of menses) often heralds the androgenic phase, and symptoms may regress after resection of a localised tumour.","Tumours are graded as well, moderately or poorly differentiated, and a subset contains heterologous elements (most often mucinous epithelium or, less commonly, cartilage or skeletal muscle) or a retiform pattern.","A significant proportion, especially those with a retiform pattern or heterologous elements in young patients, harbour DICER1 mutations, prompting consideration of the hereditary DICER1 syndrome and its associated tumours.","Serum testosterone is characteristically raised while dehydroepiandrosterone sulphate is usually only mildly elevated or normal, helping to distinguish an ovarian source from an adrenal one.","Immunohistochemistry typically shows positivity for inhibin, calretinin, SF-1 and often FOXL2, with Leydig cells sometimes highlighting crystalloids of Reinke.","Most are unilateral and confined to the ovary at diagnosis, so fertility-sparing unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy is appropriate for well-differentiated, stage-confined disease in young women.","Prognosis correlates strongly with differentiation and stage: well-differentiated tumours are effectively benign, whereas poorly differentiated or ruptured tumours carry a real risk of recurrence and metastasis."],"pathophysiology":"Sertoli-Leydig cell tumours are sex-cord stromal neoplasms composed of Sertoli cells, Leydig cells and, in less differentiated forms, primitive gonadal stroma recapitulating testicular development in the ovary. The Leydig cell component secretes androgens, chiefly testosterone, producing the virilising syndrome; some tumours instead secrete oestrogen or are hormonally silent. Many arise on a background of germline or somatic DICER1 mutation, which impairs microRNA processing and predisposes to this and other DICER1-associated tumours, particularly in the young and in tumours showing retiform or heterologous differentiation. Degree of differentiation reflects how closely the tubular Sertoli architecture is preserved; poorly differentiated tumours show sarcomatoid stroma, mitotic activity and loss of organised tubules, and correlate with a more aggressive, potentially metastatic course.","additionalDifferentials":["granulosa cell tumour","steroid (lipid) cell tumour of the ovary","adrenocortical tumour producing androgens","polycystic ovary syndrome","congenital adrenal hyperplasia (non-classic)","ovarian stromal hyperthecosis","gynandroblastoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["serum total testosterone and free androgen index","serum dehydroepiandrosterone sulphate to help localise the androgen source","serum inhibin and anti-Mullerian hormone","transvaginal pelvic ultrasound and MRI of the pelvis","CT of abdomen and pelvis for staging in higher-grade disease","DICER1 mutation testing on tumour and consideration of germline testing","inhibin, calretinin and SF-1 immunohistochemistry"],"redFlags":["rapidly progressive virilisation suggesting an androgen-secreting neoplasm rather than functional hyperandrogenism","markedly elevated serum testosterone with a palpable or imaged adnexal mass","tumour rupture with haemoperitoneum or an acute abdomen","poorly differentiated histology or extra-ovarian spread indicating aggressive potential","personal or family history suggesting DICER1 syndrome, such as pleuropulmonary blastoma or thyroid nodular disease"],"prognosis":"Well-differentiated tumours are essentially benign and cured by conservative surgery, whereas moderately and poorly differentiated tumours, those with heterologous mesenchymal elements, and ruptured or advanced-stage tumours carry a substantial risk of recurrence and metastasis with correspondingly poorer survival.","pitfalls":["Attributing hirsutism and menstrual disturbance to polycystic ovary syndrome and missing a virilising tumour when testosterone is disproportionately high.","Failing to consider and test for DICER1 syndrome in young patients or those with retiform or heterologous tumours.","Assuming an adrenal source without checking that dehydroepiandrosterone sulphate is not markedly elevated.","Underestimating a poorly differentiated tumour by sampling only well-differentiated areas.","Rupturing the tumour intra-operatively, which upstages the disease and worsens prognosis."],"management":["Fertility-sparing unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy with surgical staging for well-differentiated, early-stage tumours","Total hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy and debulking for advanced-stage or poorly differentiated disease","Adjuvant platinum-based combination chemotherapy for poorly differentiated tumours, heterologous mesenchymal elements, rupture, or advanced stage","Symptomatic management of virilisation, expecting partial regression of androgenic features once the tumour is removed","Surveillance with serial serum testosterone and inhibin plus pelvic ultrasound or MRI to detect recurrence","Germline DICER1 mutation testing with genetic counselling and family surveillance for DICER1-associated tumours","Coordinate management through a gynaecological oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours, current edition","Berek & Novak's Gynecology, current edition","Blaustein's Pathology of the Female Genital Tract, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"serum_sickness":{"name":"Serum sickness","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["serum sickness reaction","type III hypersensitivity reaction","serum-sickness-like reaction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Serum sickness is the classic immune-complex (type III) hypersensitivity reaction, presenting with fever, rash, arthralgia or arthritis, and lymphadenopathy","Symptoms characteristically begin about one to three weeks after first exposure to the offending agent, with a faster onset on re-exposure because the patient is already sensitised","Historically it followed heterologous (animal) antiserum, and today it is most often triggered by antitoxins, antivenoms, therapeutic antibodies, and certain drugs","The rash is often urticarial or a serpiginous purplish eruption at the border of the palms and soles, a helpful clinical clue","During the active phase complement is consumed, so serum C3 and C4 fall and can be used to support the diagnosis and track resolution","A serum-sickness-like reaction, most often attributed to antibiotics such as cefaclor, resembles serum sickness clinically but lacks the true immune-complex and hypocomplementaemic features and is generally benign","The condition is usually self-limiting once the causative agent is withdrawn, with recovery over days to a couple of weeks","Management principles are stopping and avoiding the trigger, symptomatic relief with antihistamines and anti-inflammatory agents, and corticosteroids for severe or systemic disease"],"pathophysiology":"Serum sickness is mediated by circulating antigen-antibody (immune) complexes. After exposure to a foreign protein or drug-protein conjugate, the host mounts an antibody response; as antibody rises while antigen is still present, soluble immune complexes form in antigen-relative excess. These complexes deposit in small vessels of the skin, joints, kidneys, and elsewhere, where they activate complement and recruit neutrophils, producing local inflammation and tissue injury (a type III hypersensitivity reaction). Complement activation consumes C3 and C4, accounting for the transient hypocomplementaemia. Once the antigen is cleared and complexes are removed, the process resolves. Serum-sickness-like reactions produce a similar clinical picture through incompletely understood mechanisms without demonstrable immune-complex disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Serum-sickness-like reaction to antibiotics (e.g. cefaclor)","Acute viral infection (e.g. hepatitis B, parvovirus) with rash and arthralgia","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other connective-tissue disease","Urticarial vasculitis and other small-vessel vasculitides","Acute drug reaction such as DRESS or an urticarial drug eruption","Reactive or infectious arthritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum C3 and C4, which fall during the active immune-complex phase","Full blood count and inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP)","Urinalysis to detect renal involvement","Assessment for circulating immune complexes or cryoglobulins where available","Serologies to exclude viral and autoimmune mimics","Skin biopsy in atypical cases to evaluate for vasculitis"],"redFlags":["Significant renal involvement with proteinuria, haematuria, or rising creatinine","Concurrent anaphylaxis or angioedema at the time of re-exposure to the antigen","Neurologic involvement or severe systemic illness suggesting extensive immune-complex disease","Features pointing to a severe drug reaction such as DRESS rather than simple serum sickness"],"prognosis":"Serum sickness is generally self-limiting and resolves without sequelae once the causative agent is removed, with symptoms settling over days to a couple of weeks. Recovery may be hastened by corticosteroids in severe cases; significant organ involvement is uncommon but warrants closer follow-up.","pitfalls":["Failing to take a careful exposure history for antisera, antivenoms, or therapeutic antibodies given weeks earlier","Confusing serum sickness with an acute IgE-mediated allergic reaction and mismanaging the tempo of illness","Overlooking renal involvement by not checking urinalysis and renal function","Re-administering the same heterologous protein or drug without recognising the sensitisation risk"],"management":["Identify and immediately discontinue the causative agent (heterologous antiserum, antivenom, therapeutic antibody, or drug such as cefaclor)","Antihistamines for urticaria and pruritus with NSAIDs for arthralgia in mild, self-limiting cases","Systemic corticosteroids for severe or organ-involving disease to hasten resolution","Supportive care and reassurance, as the illness resolves once the antigen is cleared","Monitor complement (C3 and C4) and urinalysis to track disease activity and detect renal involvement","Document the trigger and counsel on future avoidance and cross-reactivity, using premedication or an alternative agent if re-exposure is unavoidable","Refer for allergy and immunology input where the reaction is severe or organ involvement is significant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"sesamoiditis":{"name":"Sesamoiditis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Foot & Ankle","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sesamoid overload","hallucal sesamoid pain","sesamoid stress syndrome","first MTP sesamoid disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sesamoiditis is pain and inflammation of the hallucal sesamoids beneath the first metatarsal head, usually from repetitive overload rather than a single injury","The two sesamoids are embedded within the flexor hallucis brevis tendon and act as a pulley and load-bearing cushion for the great toe, so they take high force at push-off","Pain is localised to the plantar aspect of the first metatarsophalangeal joint, worse on weight bearing, dorsiflexion of the great toe, and push-off, and eased by rest","The medial (tibial) sesamoid is more often affected because it bears greater load, and focal plantar tenderness over one sesamoid is characteristic","It is common in runners, dancers, and those in high-heeled or hard-soled footwear, and is favoured by a cavus foot or plantarflexed first ray","Radiographs including an axial sesamoid view help, but a bipartite sesamoid can mimic a fracture, and MRI or bone scan distinguishes stress injury, osteonecrosis, and fracture","Distinguish it from sesamoid stress fracture, osteonecrosis, osteoarthritis, turf toe, and gout","Management is largely conservative with offloading orthoses and a sesamoid cut-out or metatarsal pad, stiff-soled footwear, activity modification, and taping, with corticosteroid injection or sesamoidectomy reserved for resistant cases"],"pathophysiology":"The hallucal sesamoids are two small bones enclosed within the tendons of flexor hallucis brevis on the plantar surface of the first metatarsal head, where they increase the mechanical advantage of the flexor, absorb load, and protect the flexor hallucis longus tendon. During push-off the great toe dorsiflexes and the sesamoids are drawn distally and loaded heavily, so repetitive high forces, as in running, dancing, and jumping, or sustained pressure from hard or high-heeled footwear, produce an overload inflammation of the sesamoid and its surrounding soft tissues. A cavus foot or a plantarflexed first ray increases the load borne by the sesamoids and predisposes to symptoms. Persistent overload may progress to a stress reaction or stress fracture, and impaired blood supply can lead to osteonecrosis, while a congenitally bipartite sesamoid can be a source of confusion on imaging and a site of symptomatic stress.","additionalDifferentials":["Sesamoid stress fracture or acute fracture","Osteonecrosis of a sesamoid","Sesamoid or first metatarsophalangeal osteoarthritis","Turf toe injury of the first metatarsophalangeal joint","Gout or other crystal arthropathy","Hallux rigidus or hallux valgus with plantar symptoms","Plantar soft-tissue lesion or interdigital neuroma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination with focal palpation of the tibial and fibular sesamoids and pain on great-toe dorsiflexion","Weight-bearing foot radiographs including an axial (sesamoid) view","MRI to differentiate stress reaction, fracture, osteonecrosis, and inflammation","Bone scan where MRI is unavailable or a stress injury is suspected","Assessment of foot type for cavus or a plantarflexed first ray","Serum urate if gout is a consideration","Pedobarography to assess loading beneath the first ray where relevant"],"redFlags":["Acute severe plantar pain after a hyperextension injury suggesting fracture or a turf toe with sesamoid disruption","A hot, red, swollen joint suggesting gout or infection","Progressive pain with imaging changes suggesting osteonecrosis or established stress fracture","Rest or night pain or a destructive lesion suggesting infection or tumour"],"prognosis":"Most cases of overload sesamoiditis settle with offloading, footwear modification, and activity reduction, though recovery can be slow because the sesamoids are heavily loaded with every step. Stress fractures and osteonecrosis take longer and occasionally fail to heal, and sesamoidectomy usually relieves pain but can alter first-ray mechanics and, if both sesamoids are removed, cause deformity, so it is reserved for resistant cases.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a bipartite sesamoid for a fracture, or vice versa, on plain radiographs","Attributing plantar first metatarsophalangeal pain to sesamoiditis while missing a stress fracture or osteonecrosis","Removing both sesamoids and creating a cock-up or clawing deformity of the great toe","Overlooking a cavus foot or plantarflexed first ray that perpetuates sesamoid overload","Missing gout or infection as the cause of an acutely inflamed joint"],"management":["Offload the sesamoids with relative rest, activity modification, and avoidance of high-heeled or hard-soled footwear","Cushioned insoles or orthoses with a sesamoid cut-out or metatarsal padding to redistribute load away from the first metatarsal head","Simple analgesia and NSAIDs for pain and inflammation","Taping or a stiff-soled shoe or walker boot to limit great-toe dorsiflexion during healing","Physiotherapy and graded return to activity, correcting a cavus foot or plantarflexed first ray with orthoses","Monitor for progression to stress fracture or osteonecrosis with repeat imaging if pain persists despite offloading","Refer for orthopaedic or podiatric opinion and consider sesamoidectomy for refractory disease, non-union, or osteonecrosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"sever_disease":{"name":"Sever disease","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["calcaneal apophysitis","Sever's disease","os calcis apophysitis","posterior heel apophysitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A traction apophysitis of the calcaneal (heel) apophysis rather than a true osteonecrosis","Affected site is the posterior calcaneal apophysis at the insertion of the Achilles tendon","Typically affects active children roughly eight to fourteen years of age around a growth spurt, often bilaterally","Presents with activity-related posterior heel pain relieved by rest, with tenderness on medial-lateral squeeze of the heel","It is a clinical diagnosis; radiographs are usually obtained to exclude other causes rather than to confirm the condition","Sclerosis and fragmentation of the calcaneal apophysis is a normal developmental appearance and does not by itself establish the diagnosis","A positive squeeze test and tight gastrocnemius-soleus complex on examination support the diagnosis","Management is conservative with activity modification, heel cups or orthoses, calf and hamstring stretching and physiotherapy as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Sever disease is a traction apophysitis of the posterior calcaneal apophysis, arising where the powerful Achilles tendon inserts onto the still-cartilaginous growth centre of the heel. During periods of rapid growth the bones lengthen faster than the surrounding muscle-tendon units, increasing tension across the apophysis, while repetitive impact from running and jumping adds cyclical traction and compression. This repetitive microtrauma at the vulnerable cartilaginous apophysis produces inflammation and pain. The radiographic sclerosis and fragmentation often seen at the apophysis represent normal variation in ossification rather than avascular necrosis, and the condition resolves once the apophysis fuses at skeletal maturity.","additionalDifferentials":["Achilles tendinopathy or insertional Achilles tendinitis","Retrocalcaneal or superficial calcaneal bursitis","Calcaneal stress fracture","Plantar fasciitis","Osteomyelitis or bone tumour of the calcaneus","Tarsal coalition presenting with hindfoot pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical squeeze (compression) test of the heel reproducing pain","Assessment of gastrocnemius-soleus tightness and ankle dorsiflexion","Plain radiographs primarily to exclude fracture, infection or tumour","Magnetic resonance imaging reserved for atypical, persistent or unilateral cases","Inflammatory markers if infection or inflammatory arthropathy is suspected","Gait and footwear assessment in the active child"],"redFlags":["Rest pain, night pain or systemic features suggesting infection or malignancy","A discrete focal bony lesion or fracture line on imaging","Persistent unilateral pain not settling with conservative measures","Swelling, warmth or erythema over the heel pointing to an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; symptoms are self-limiting and resolve with skeletal maturity as the apophysis fuses, with activity modification and stretching controlling pain in the interim and no long-term sequelae expected.","pitfalls":["Interpreting the normal fragmented, sclerotic apophysis on radiographs as diagnostic or as osteonecrosis","Missing a calcaneal stress fracture, infection or tumour presenting as atypical heel pain","Failing to address tight calf musculature that perpetuates apophyseal traction","Complete immobilisation and deconditioning when relative activity modification would suffice"],"management":["Reassure the child and family that it is a benign, self-limiting condition that resolves at skeletal maturity","Activity modification with reduction of running and jumping load during symptomatic flares","Calf (gastrocnemius-soleus) stretching and heel-cord flexibility exercises","Heel cushioning or heel-raise orthoses to reduce traction on the calcaneal apophysis","Simple analgesia such as paracetamol or NSAIDs for pain during flares","Graded return to sport as symptoms settle, with footwear review","Reassess or image only for atypical, unilateral, or persistent presentations to exclude other pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"severe_acute_malnutrition":{"name":"Severe acute malnutrition","system":"Nutrition","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SAM","severe malnutrition","marasmus","kwashiorkor","protein-energy malnutrition"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined in young children by severe wasting (weight-for-height well below the reference median, or a very low mid-upper arm circumference) and/or bilateral pitting oedema of nutritional origin.","Marasmus is severe wasting from overall energy deficit; kwashiorkor is characterised by oedema, and mixed pictures occur.","Kwashiorkor features include oedema, sparse depigmented hair, skin changes (flaky-paint dermatosis), an enlarged fatty liver, and apathy.","Children with SAM are highly vulnerable to hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, and overwhelming infection, which is often present without classic signs.","Refeeding must be cautious and staged to avoid refeeding syndrome; overly rapid feeding and excess fluid or sodium can precipitate cardiac failure and death.","Initial stabilisation prioritises treating and preventing hypoglycaemia and hypothermia, correcting dehydration carefully, and giving broad micronutrient support.","Rehydration is preferably oral or nasogastric with a low-sodium, higher-potassium solution, because intravenous fluids readily cause fluid overload.","Management follows a stabilisation phase with cautious feeding, followed by a rehabilitation phase with catch-up growth once appetite and stability return."],"pathophysiology":"Prolonged inadequate intake of energy, protein, and micronutrients forces the body into a state of reductive adaptation: metabolic rate falls, organ function is downregulated, and body composition shifts with loss of fat and muscle. Sodium-potassium pump activity is impaired, causing intracellular sodium retention and total-body potassium and magnesium depletion despite oedema. Hepatic and cardiac function, immune competence, and thermoregulation are all compromised. In kwashiorkor, hypoalbuminaemia and increased capillary permeability contribute to oedema. These adaptations make the child dangerously intolerant of the metabolic demands of aggressive feeding and fluid loads.","additionalDifferentials":["Oedema from nephrotic syndrome or cardiac/hepatic failure","Chronic infection (including tuberculosis and HIV)","Malabsorption syndromes (e.g. coeliac disease, cystic fibrosis)","Chronic diarrhoeal disease","Underlying malignancy or chronic illness causing wasting"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Weight, height/length, and mid-upper arm circumference with oedema assessment","Blood glucose (to detect hypoglycaemia)","Electrolytes including potassium, magnesium, and phosphate","Haemoglobin and assessment for anaemia","Screening for infection (including tuberculosis, HIV where relevant) and malaria in endemic areas","Assessment of hydration status, which is difficult to judge clinically in SAM"],"redFlags":["Hypoglycaemia and hypothermia","Signs of shock or severe dehydration (difficult to assess and easy to overtreat)","Developing signs of heart failure during rehydration or refeeding (refeeding syndrome)","Severe anaemia requiring careful transfusion","Lethargy, hypothermia, or hypoglycaemia as subtle markers of serious infection"],"prognosis":"With careful phased management and treatment of complications, most children recover, but case fatality is high when complications such as hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, sepsis, or refeeding syndrome are missed or when rehydration is too aggressive.","pitfalls":["Feeding too much too quickly and precipitating refeeding syndrome and heart failure","Using standard intravenous rehydration and causing fluid overload","Relying on usual signs of infection, which are often absent","Missing hypoglycaemia and hypothermia because signs are subtle","Treating oedema as fluid overload rather than recognising nutritional kwashiorkor"],"management":["Cautious, staged refeeding starting with a low-protein starter therapeutic milk and transitioning to catch-up feed or ready-to-use therapeutic food to avoid refeeding syndrome","Prevent and treat hypoglycaemia and hypothermia with frequent feeds and active warming","Correct dehydration cautiously with a low-sodium oral rehydration solution, reserving intravenous fluids for shock","Empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics because infection is common and often occult","Correct electrolyte and micronutrient deficits with potassium, magnesium, zinc, and multivitamins, withholding iron until the recovery phase","Monitor for and manage complications including cardiac failure, sepsis, and refeeding syndrome","Structured nutritional rehabilitation, developmental stimulation, and caregiver education with community follow-up","Manage within a paediatric nutrition multidisciplinary programme"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"severe_combined_immunodeficiency":{"name":"Severe combined immunodeficiency","system":"Genetic / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCID","bubble boy disease","combined T- and B-cell immunodeficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard medical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Group of inherited disorders defined by profound impairment of T-cell development, with variable B-cell and natural killer cell involvement, causing severe combined immune failure","Affected infants typically present in the first months of life with recurrent, severe, or opportunistic infections, chronic diarrhoea, and failure to thrive","Persistent or unusual infections such as Pneumocystis pneumonia, chronic candidiasis, and disseminated viral disease are characteristic","Absence of a thymic shadow and marked lymphopenia are important early clues","Genetic causes are heterogeneous and include X-linked common gamma-chain defects, adenosine deaminase deficiency, and several autosomal recessive gene defects","Newborn screening using T-cell receptor excision circles enables presymptomatic detection and early treatment","Definitive treatment is immune reconstitution, principally haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, with gene therapy and enzyme replacement for specific subtypes","Early diagnosis before infections develop markedly improves transplant outcomes"],"pathophysiology":"Severe combined immunodeficiency comprises a genetically diverse group of disorders that share a failure of adaptive immunity due to absent or non-functional T lymphocytes, with associated defects in B-cell and sometimes natural killer cell function. Underlying defects disrupt lymphocyte development or function through varied mechanisms, such as impaired cytokine receptor signalling in common gamma-chain deficiency, toxic metabolite accumulation in adenosine deaminase deficiency, or failure of antigen-receptor gene rearrangement. Without functional T cells, the coordinated immune response to pathogens collapses, leaving infants unable to clear common and opportunistic organisms. Most forms are inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, with the common gamma-chain form being X-linked.","additionalDifferentials":["HIV infection in infancy","Other combined immunodeficiencies of lesser severity","DiGeorge syndrome with severe thymic hypoplasia","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","Secondary immunodeficiency and severe malnutrition"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Newborn screening for low T-cell receptor excision circles where available","Full blood count with lymphocyte subset quantification","Immunoglobulin levels and functional lymphocyte proliferation studies","Molecular genetic testing to define the specific subtype","Adenosine deaminase enzyme assay and chest imaging for thymic assessment"],"redFlags":["This is a paediatric emergency: suspected SCID requires immediate specialist referral and protective isolation","Live vaccines, including BCG and rotavirus, are contraindicated and can cause fatal disseminated infection","Blood products must be irradiated, cytomegalovirus-safe, and leucodepleted to prevent transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease","Any infection can rapidly become life-threatening and needs urgent aggressive management"],"prognosis":"Without immune reconstitution the condition is usually fatal within the first year or two of life, but early haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, particularly before infections occur, offers a high chance of long-term survival and immune recovery.","pitfalls":["Administering live vaccines before immunodeficiency is excluded","Transfusing non-irradiated blood products","Delaying referral by attributing recurrent infections to ordinary childhood illness","Overlooking lymphopenia on a routine full blood count"],"management":["Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is the definitive curative therapy, ideally performed early before infections develop","Gene therapy for selected genotypes and enzyme replacement with pegademase for adenosine deaminase deficiency as a bridge","Protective isolation and strict infection-prevention measures pending immune reconstitution","Antimicrobial prophylaxis against Pneumocystis with co-trimoxazole, plus antifungal and antiviral cover","Immunoglobulin replacement therapy to cover the antibody deficiency","Use only irradiated, cytomegalovirus-safe, leucodepleted blood products and withhold live vaccines","Genetic counselling, carrier testing, and family screening, with newborn screening of siblings","Manage through immunology and transplant multidisciplinary teams"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"severe_congenital_neutropenia":{"name":"Severe congenital neutropenia","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCN","Kostmann syndrome","ELANE-related neutropenia","HAX1-related neutropenia","infantile genetic agranulocytosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / Nathan and Oski's Hematology of Infancy and Childhood","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents in early infancy with profound neutropenia and recurrent, often severe, bacterial infections such as omphalitis, skin abscesses, and pneumonia","Autosomal dominant ELANE mutations are the commonest cause, whereas classic autosomal recessive Kostmann syndrome results from HAX1 mutations","The marrow characteristically shows maturation arrest of the myeloid series at the promyelocyte or myelocyte stage","Most patients respond to granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF), which raises neutrophil counts and reduces infection","There is a lifelong risk of clonal evolution to myelodysplastic syndrome and acute myeloid leukaemia, often heralded by acquired CSF3R and RUNX1 mutations","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is the definitive option for G-CSF-refractory disease or when leukaemic transformation supervenes","Diagnosis combines persistent severe neutropenia, marrow maturation arrest, and targeted genetic testing while excluding autoimmune and secondary causes"],"pathophysiology":"Severe congenital neutropenia is a group of inherited disorders of granulopoiesis in which neutrophil precursors fail to mature. ELANE mutations produce a misfolded neutrophil elastase that triggers the unfolded protein response and endoplasmic reticulum stress, driving apoptosis of promyelocytes; HAX1 mutations remove a protein that maintains mitochondrial membrane potential, again promoting precursor apoptosis. Other genes converge on similar pathways of accelerated myeloid progenitor death or defective intracellular trafficking. The net effect is arrest of myeloid maturation with near-absent circulating neutrophils. Chronic G-CSF stimulation of a genetically unstable myeloid compartment, together with acquisition of CSF3R and other somatic mutations, underlies the elevated risk of transformation to myelodysplasia and acute myeloid leukaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune neutropenia of infancy","Cyclic neutropenia","Shwachman-Diamond syndrome","Glycogen storage disease type Ib and other metabolic neutropenias","Reticular dysgenesis and other combined immunodeficiencies","Drug-induced or infection-related transient neutropenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial full blood counts to confirm persistent severe neutropenia and distinguish from cyclic disease","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine to demonstrate myeloid maturation arrest","Targeted genetic panel (ELANE, HAX1, and other SCN genes)","Antineutrophil antibody testing to exclude autoimmune neutropenia","Cytogenetics and serial marrow surveillance for CSF3R/RUNX1 mutations and MDS/AML evolution","Immunological workup where a syndromic or combined immunodeficiency is suspected"],"redFlags":["Recurrent life-threatening bacterial or fungal infection with an absolute neutrophil count persistently very low","New cytogenetic abnormality, rising blasts, or acquired CSF3R/RUNX1 mutation signalling leukaemic transformation","Escalating G-CSF requirement with declining response","Sepsis or deep-seated infection in a febrile neutropenic infant requiring urgent broad-spectrum cover"],"prognosis":"G-CSF has transformed survival by controlling infection, but patients carry a substantial lifelong risk of transformation to myelodysplasia and acute myeloid leukaemia, so surveillance is essential and transplantation is curative for high-risk or refractory disease.","pitfalls":["Mistaking severe congenital neutropenia for autoimmune neutropenia and delaying G-CSF","Failing to perform serial marrow surveillance and missing early clonal evolution","Attributing a poor G-CSF response solely to under-dosing rather than considering transformation","Overlooking syndromic features that point to a specific genetic diagnosis"],"management":["Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF) as first-line therapy to raise neutrophil counts and reduce infections","Prompt broad-spectrum antibiotics for febrile or bacterial infection, with meticulous skin and oral hygiene","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for G-CSF-refractory disease or on leukaemic transformation","Surveillance for clonal evolution with serial marrow examination and cytogenetics, watching for acquired CSF3R and RUNX1 mutations","Monitor full blood count response and titrate G-CSF to the lowest effective level","Genetic counselling and family testing given the heritable ELANE and HAX1 basis","Escalate to transplantation on emergence of myelodysplasia or acute myeloid leukaemia","Coordinate long-term care through a haematology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"severs_disease":{"name":"Sever's disease","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["calcaneal apophysitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common cause of heel pain in physically active children and early adolescents, related to traction at the calcaneal growth plate","Pain is felt at the back and sides of the heel, worse with running and jumping and eased by rest","It typically occurs during the growth spurt and often around a period of increased sporting activity","The squeeze test, compressing the sides of the calcaneus, reproduces the pain","It is a self-limiting condition that resolves once the calcaneal apophysis fuses","Management is with activity modification, calf stretching, heel cushioning and reassurance"],"pathophysiology":"Sever's disease is a traction apophysitis of the calcaneus, in which repetitive tensile load from the Achilles tendon on the still-open calcaneal growth plate, combined with the impact of running and jumping, produces stress and inflammation at the apophysis. The vulnerability of the growth plate during the adolescent growth spurt, when bone growth may outpace muscle-tendon flexibility, increases traction across the apophysis. It is a benign, self-limiting overuse condition rather than a structural or destructive process.","additionalDifferentials":["Achilles tendinopathy or retrocalcaneal bursitis","Calcaneal stress fracture","Plantar fasciitis","Bone cyst or tumour of the calcaneus","Osteomyelitis (if systemic features)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the age, activity and positive squeeze test","Radiographs only to exclude other pathology if the picture is atypical","Assessment of calf flexibility and footwear","Review of training load","Further imaging if there are red flags for another diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Night pain, rest pain or systemic features suggesting infection or tumour","Focal bony swelling","Failure to settle with appropriate measures","Significant unilateral symptoms without a clear activity link"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and resolves spontaneously with skeletal maturity, and symptoms are well controlled in the meantime with conservative measures.","pitfalls":["Over-investigating a classic clinical presentation","Missing a calcaneal stress fracture, infection or tumour when features are atypical","Failing to address calf tightness and training load","Advising complete rest rather than activity modification"],"management":["Reassure the child and family that it is benign and resolves with skeletal maturity once the apophysis fuses","Relative rest and reduction of training load during painful periods","Calf-stretching and heel-cord flexibility exercises","Heel cushions, heel raises, or shock-absorbing insoles to offload the apophysis","Simple analgesia such as paracetamol or NSAIDs for symptom control during flares","Footwear review and graded return to sport as pain settles","Reserve radiographs or further imaging for atypical or persistent presentations to exclude other pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric sports medicine references (standard reference)","Orthopaedic reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"sex_cord_stromal_tumor":{"name":"Sex cord-stromal tumour","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sex cord-stromal tumor","granulosa cell tumour","Sertoli-Leydig cell tumour","gonadal stromal tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of gonadal neoplasms arising from the non-germ-cell supporting tissues (granulosa, theca, Sertoli, and Leydig cells) that are frequently hormonally active","In children an oestrogen-secreting tumour, such as a juvenile granulosa cell tumour, can cause peripheral (gonadotropin-independent) precocious pseudopuberty with breast development and vaginal bleeding","Androgen-secreting variants, such as Sertoli-Leydig cell tumours, can produce virilisation in girls or contribute to precocity in boys","The hormonal effects are autonomous, so gonadotropins are suppressed and the GnRH stimulation test is prepubertal, unlike central precocious puberty","A unilateral adnexal or testicular mass on imaging in a child with signs of sex-steroid excess should raise the suspicion of a hormone-secreting gonadal tumour","Certain sex cord-stromal tumours are associated with genetic syndromes (for example the association of ovarian sex cord tumour with annular tubules and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, and Sertoli-Leydig tumours with DICER1 mutations)","Management is primarily surgical, with fertility-sparing resection where feasible, and the histological subtype and stage guide the need for any further therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Sex cord-stromal tumours develop from the steroidogenic and supporting cells of the gonad rather than from germ cells, and they often retain the capacity to synthesise sex steroids. Depending on the cell of origin, they may secrete oestrogens, causing feminising effects and isolated pseudopuberty, or androgens, causing virilisation; some are hormonally silent. Because the hormone production is autonomous and independent of pituitary drive, gonadotropins are suppressed and the changes represent precocious pseudopuberty rather than true, centrally driven puberty. The clinical picture therefore combines the local effects of a gonadal mass with the systemic consequences of the particular hormone being produced, and a subset arises in the context of tumour-predisposition syndromes.","additionalDifferentials":["Central precocious puberty","Gonadotropin-independent precocious puberty from other causes (e.g. McCune-Albright syndrome, testotoxicosis)","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia or a virilising adrenal tumour","Germ cell tumour of the gonad","Functional ovarian cyst producing oestrogen","Gonadoblastoma in a dysgenetic gonad","Exogenous sex-steroid exposure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sex-steroid measurement (oestradiol and/or testosterone) with basal LH and FSH (suppressed)","GnRH (LHRH) stimulation test showing a prepubertal, suppressed response","Pelvic or scrotal ultrasound to identify and characterise a gonadal mass","Tumour markers (for example inhibin and anti-Müllerian hormone) that can accompany certain sex cord-stromal tumours","Cross-sectional imaging for staging when a tumour is confirmed","Genetic evaluation for associated syndromes (such as DICER1 or Peutz-Jeghers) where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["A palpable or radiologically evident gonadal mass with signs of sex-steroid excess","Rapidly progressive virilisation or feminisation suggesting an active tumour","Features of a tumour-predisposition syndrome pointing to hereditary risk","Abdominal pain, distension, or ascites suggesting a large or advanced tumour","Recurrence of hormonal signs after resection, indicating residual or recurrent disease"],"prognosis":"Many sex cord-stromal tumours in children are of low stage and behave in a benign or indolent fashion with an excellent outcome after complete resection, and hormone-driven signs typically regress once the tumour is removed; higher-stage or aggressive histological subtypes carry a less favourable outlook and require additional treatment and surveillance.","pitfalls":["Attributing precocious puberty to central causes without recognising a hormone-secreting gonadal tumour","Failing to perform a GnRH stimulation test to establish gonadotropin independence","Overlooking an associated genetic syndrome and its implications for the patient and family","Assuming all such tumours are benign and omitting appropriate staging and follow-up","Missing a small contralateral or recurrent lesion after initial surgery"],"management":["Complete surgical resection of the gonadal tumour, typically fertility-sparing unilateral oophorectomy or orchidectomy, as the mainstay","Adjuvant platinum-based chemotherapy reserved for higher-stage or aggressive histological subtypes","Anticipate regression of precocious pseudopuberty or virilisation after tumour removal","Surveillance with serial tumour markers (inhibin, anti-Mullerian hormone) and sex-steroid levels plus imaging to detect recurrence","Genetic evaluation and counselling for associated syndromes (DICER1, Peutz-Jeghers) with family screening where indicated","Monitor and manage the effects of prior sex-steroid exposure on growth and bone maturation","Manage through paediatric oncology and endocrinology multidisciplinary teams"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"sexual_dysfunction_female":{"name":"Female sexual dysfunction","system":"Reproductive medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FSD","female sexual dysfunction","hypoactive sexual desire disorder","female sexual arousal disorder","female orgasmic disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard sleep & reproductive medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Female sexual dysfunction spans disorders of desire, arousal, orgasm, and sexual pain, and is only a clinical diagnosis when the problem is persistent and causes personal distress","The female sexual response is better understood as a circular, intimacy-driven model rather than a rigid linear sequence, so context and relationship factors are central","Causes are typically multifactorial, combining biological, psychological, and interpersonal contributors that should all be explored","Hormonal changes, especially the oestrogen decline of menopause, commonly reduce arousal and lubrication and contribute to genitourinary and pain symptoms","Many common medications, particularly serotonergic antidepressants, and chronic illnesses such as diabetes, depression, and vascular disease impair sexual function","A sensitive, open history that legitimises the concern is essential, as patients often will not raise the topic themselves","Management addresses reversible contributors, optimises relationship and psychological factors, and considers psychosexual therapy alongside targeted treatments such as local oestrogen for genitourinary symptoms","Persistent pain with intercourse points towards specific disorders such as vaginismus or dyspareunia that require directed assessment"],"pathophysiology":"Female sexual function integrates neurological, vascular, hormonal, and psychological systems. Sexual arousal depends on genital vasocongestion and lubrication mediated by autonomic and nitric-oxide pathways, while desire and orgasm involve central dopaminergic, serotonergic, and hormonal modulation. Dysfunction arises when any of these are disrupted: oestrogen deficiency thins and dries the vaginal epithelium and reduces blood flow, vascular and neurological disease (as in diabetes) impairs the genital response, and medications such as SSRIs blunt desire and orgasm. Psychological factors including depression, anxiety, past trauma, body image, and relationship conflict powerfully influence desire and arousal, and pain disorders can create a self-reinforcing cycle of anticipatory anxiety and avoidance. The circular, context-dependent nature of female sexual response means these factors interact rather than act in isolation.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypoactive sexual desire disorder","Female sexual arousal disorder","Female orgasmic disorder","Genito-pelvic pain/penetration disorder (vaginismus, dyspareunia)","Depression, anxiety, or relationship dysfunction","Medication-induced or endocrine (menopausal) sexual dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive biopsychosocial and sexual history including relationship context","Review of medications, particularly antidepressants, and chronic medical conditions","Pelvic examination to identify atrophy, pain, or structural causes","Targeted endocrine assessment where indicated, including menopausal status","Screening for depression, anxiety, and history of sexual trauma"],"redFlags":["Sexual pain or bleeding suggesting an underlying gynaecological pathology","History of sexual abuse or trauma requiring sensitive, specialised support","Coexisting depression with suicidal ideation","New sexual dysfunction accompanying signs of significant systemic or neurological disease"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are generally good when contributing factors are identified and addressed; genitourinary symptoms of menopause respond well to local oestrogen, medication-related effects may improve with adjustment, and psychosexual and relationship interventions can substantially help desire and arousal disorders.","pitfalls":["Not asking about sexual health, so distressing problems go unrecognised","Attributing dysfunction to a single cause when it is usually multifactorial","Overlooking medication, especially antidepressants, as a reversible contributor","Neglecting relationship and psychological dimensions in favour of a purely biomedical approach"],"management":["Address reversible contributors by reviewing and adjusting offending medications (particularly serotonergic antidepressants) and optimising chronic conditions such as diabetes and depression","Local vaginal oestrogen for the genitourinary syndrome of menopause affecting arousal, lubrication, and pain, with non-hormonal lubricants and moisturisers as adjuncts","Psychosexual therapy, cognitive behavioural approaches, and couple-based interventions for desire, arousal, and relationship factors","Pelvic floor physiotherapy and desensitisation for sexual pain disorders","Consider pharmacological options such as flibanserin or transdermal testosterone for hypoactive sexual desire in appropriately selected women","Treat coexisting mood, anxiety, and trauma-related disorders","Refer to specialist psychosexual, menopause, or pelvic health services for complex or refractory cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"sexually_transmitted_infections_overview_a":{"name":"Sexually Transmitted Infections: Overview and Clinical Approach","system":"Genitourinary / Reproductive","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STI overview","STD overview","Sexually transmitted diseases","Venereal disease"],"chapter":141,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1096","clinicalPearls":["STIs are syndrome-driven in practice: most patients are managed initially by presenting symptoms, signs, and risk factors rather than by a confirmed microbial diagnosis, with common syndromes including male urethritis, epididymitis, mucopurulent cervicitis, vulvovaginitis, genital/perianal ulcers, PID, and proctitis (Harrison 22e p.1100).","More than 40 microorganisms are now recognized as predominantly sexually transmitted or frequently sexually transmissible, spanning bacteria, viruses, protozoa, fungi, and ectoparasites (Harrison 22e p.1096).","The initial rate of spread of any STI in a population is the product of three factors: the rate of sexual exposure of susceptible to infectious persons, the per-exposure transmission efficiency, and the duration of infectivity (Harrison 22e p.1096).","Genital HPV is described as the most common sexually transmitted pathogen in the United States, and HPV and hepatitis B virus remain major causes of cervical and hepatocellular carcinoma respectively in the developing world (Harrison 22e p.1096-1097).","Reported syphilis in the United States in 2022 (207,255 cases, all stages and congenital) was the highest since 1950, with congenital syphilis rate of 102.5 per 100,000 live births and 282 congenital syphilis-related stillbirths/infant deaths (Harrison 22e p.1097).","All four major curable STIs (gonorrhea, chlamydial infection, syphilis, trichomoniasis) plus bacterial vaginosis have been associated with increased risk of HIV transmission or acquisition (Harrison 22e p.1097).","Acute epididymitis in sexually active men under 35 is most often due to C. trachomatis (less often N. gonorrhoeae) and usually accompanies overt or subclinical urethritis, whereas in older men or after urinary instrumentation it is usually caused by urinary pathogens (Harrison 22e p.1101).","Unsolicited reporting of abnormal vaginal discharge (increased amount or abnormal odor) most often denotes bacterial vaginosis or trichomoniasis, not cervical gonorrhea or chlamydia, which usually do not increase discharge volume or odor (Harrison 22e p.1101).","Sexually transmitted HSV causes most genital ulcer disease worldwide; HSV-2 produces more frequent genital recurrences and is more infectious to partners than HSV-1, so typing has prognostic value (Harrison 22e p.1097, 1108).","STI control combines reducing exposure (education, behavior change, post-exposure prophylaxis), shortening infectivity (early diagnosis and curative or suppressive treatment), and reducing per-exposure transmission (condoms, vaccines, male medical circumcision) (Harrison 22e p.1096).","Despite the strong epidemiologic HSV-HIV link, randomized trials of antiviral HSV suppression showed no protective effect against HIV acquisition or transmission (Harrison 22e p.1097)."],"pathophysiology":"STIs are acquired through sexual exposure to a susceptible person from an infectious one, with spread amplified within highly interconnected sexual networks marked by high partner-change rates or concurrent partners (e.g., transactional sex, some MSM, drug-using populations), while infections such as chlamydia, genital HPV, and genital herpes propagate efficiently even in relatively low-risk populations. Many of these organisms colonize or invade genital, anal, oral, or systemic epithelium; ascending spread from cervix or vagina to endometrium and fallopian tubes underlies PID, with potential extension to pelvic peritonitis, perihepatitis, perisplenitis, or abscess. Immunopathology contributes to tissue damage in chlamydial salpingitis, where repeated exposure produces the greatest inflammation. Hormonal and anatomic factors modulate susceptibility (e.g., cervical ectopy may increase susceptibility to gonococcus, chlamydia, and HIV; oral contraceptives favor ectopy persistence; menstruation appears to facilitate ascending gonococcal/chlamydial infection).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute epididymitis must be distinguished from testicular torsion (a surgical emergency with sudden pain, testicular elevation, and absent Doppler flow), testicular tumor, and trauma (Harrison 22e p.1101)","Dysuric women: STI-associated urethral syndrome (sterile pyuria, <10^2 conventional uropathogens/mL) versus bacterial cystitis (single uropathogen >=10^2/mL) versus acute pyelonephritis (costovertebral tenderness/fever) (Harrison 22e p.1100-1101)","Persistent genital ulcer not resolving with syndrome-based therapy: consider donovanosis, carcinoma, and other nonvenereal dermatoses on biopsy (Harrison 22e p.1108)"],"infectionMimics":["Pelvic inflammatory disease that is not due to specific STIs (secondary to appendicitis, regional ileitis, diverticulitis, hematogenous tuberculosis or staphylococcal bacteremia, or tropical disease such as schistosomiasis) (Harrison 22e p.1104)","Chronic granulomatous epididymal disease such as tuberculosis mimicking sexually acquired epididymitis when symptoms persist after therapy (Harrison 22e p.1101)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cervical ectopy (columnar epithelium on the ectocervix), often mislabeled 'cervical erosion,' confused with infectious endocervicitis but showing clear/cloudy mucus and intact epithelium on colposcopy (Harrison 22e p.1104)","Testicular tumor or trauma presenting as scrotal pain/swelling mimicking epididymitis (Harrison 22e p.1101)","Genital carcinoma or nonvenereal dermatoses presenting as a persistent genital ulcer (Harrison 22e p.1108)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Risk assessment using a structured sexual-history questionnaire (e.g., the eleven-question STD/HIV risk assessment) to guide screening and presumptive treatment decisions (Harrison 22e p.1098)","Urethral Gram's stain to confirm urethritis and detect gram-negative diplococci, recognizing that up to ~30% of gonococcal urethral infections can yield a negative Gram's stain (Harrison 22e p.1099)","Nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT) for N. gonorrhoeae, C. trachomatis, M. genitalium, and T. vaginalis on appropriate genital/urine specimens (Harrison 22e p.1099-1100)","Genital ulcer workup: dark-field examination, direct fluorescent antibody, or PCR for T. pallidum; RPR/VDRL/EIA serology for syphilis; culture, FA, ELISA, or PCR for HSV; HSV-2 type-specific serology; PCR or culture for H. ducreyi in chancroid-endemic settings (Harrison 22e p.1108-1109)","Biopsy of a genital ulcer persisting beyond the expected natural history (herpes 2-3 weeks; chancroid or syphilis up to 6 weeks) to exclude donovanosis and malignancy (Harrison 22e p.1108)","Aspiration of fluctuant inguinal nodes for culture/PCR (H. ducreyi) plus Gram's stain to exclude other pyogenic bacteria (Harrison 22e p.1108)"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe testicular pain with testicular elevation and absent Doppler flow indicating testicular torsion, a surgical emergency (Harrison 22e p.1101)","Costovertebral angle pain/tenderness or fever in a dysuric woman suggesting acute pyelonephritis (Harrison 22e p.1100)","Genital ulcer in a patient with HIV risk (e.g., MSM, known HIV infection): treat presumptively for syphilis rather than awaiting seroconversion given high syphilis rates (Harrison 22e p.1108)","Suspected PID with peritoneal extension (pelvic or generalized peritonitis, perihepatitis, perisplenitis, or pelvic abscess) (Harrison 22e p.1104)","Congenital syphilis exposure in pregnancy, given the sharp recent rise including syphilis-related stillbirths and infant deaths (Harrison 22e p.1097)"],"prognosis":"Many STIs are curable when diagnosed and treated early, and prompt treatment shortens infectivity and reduces onward transmission. Outcomes worsen with delayed care: untreated chlamydial and gonococcal infection can ascend to cause PID with risk of tubal damage and infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain, and immunopathology contributes to chlamydial tubal injury. HPV and HBV carry long-term oncogenic risk (cervical and other anogenital cancers; hepatocellular carcinoma). HSV-2 causes lifelong recurrent infection with asymptomatic shedding and ongoing transmission risk. In men, urethritis with neither chlamydiae nor gonococci detected carries a higher risk of recurrent nongonococcal urethritis, and most persistent urethritis is attributable to M. genitalium. Congenital syphilis can result in stillbirth or infant death.","pitfalls":["Treating genital symptoms without performing the microbiologic tests needed to establish a cause; self-diagnosis of vulvovaginal candidiasis is often incorrect (Harrison 22e p.1101)","Relying on urethral Gram's stain alone to exclude gonorrhea, since up to ~30% of gonococcal urethral infections are smear-negative (Harrison 22e p.1099)","Misreading cervical ectopy as infectious endocervicitis and unnecessarily cauterizing it (Harrison 22e p.1104)","Assuming a positive HSV-2 serology proves current lesions are herpetic, when nearly one-fifth of the U.S. population becomes HSV-2 seropositive in early adulthood and type-specific assays are not 100% specific (Harrison 22e p.1108)","Failing to evaluate and treat sexual partners with contact in the relevant window (e.g., within the prior 60 days for urethritis), allowing reinfection (Harrison 22e p.1099)","Withholding presumptive syphilis treatment in a high-risk patient with a genital ulcer while awaiting seroconversion (Harrison 22e p.1108)","Attributing male urethritis recurrence to treatment failure without considering reexposure to an untreated partner or M. genitalium (often resistant to doxycycline and azithromycin) (Harrison 22e p.1100, 1099)"],"management":["Syndromic management with empirical treatment directed at the likely pathogens for the presenting syndrome (urethritis, cervicitis, genital ulcer, pelvic inflammatory disease, proctitis) pending confirmatory testing","Pathogen-directed antimicrobials once confirmed: ceftriaxone plus doxycycline for gonococcal and chlamydial infection, benzathine penicillin for syphilis, aciclovir-class antivirals for herpes, and nitroimidazoles for trichomoniasis","Partner notification, testing, and treatment (including expedited partner therapy where permitted) to interrupt transmission","Test for co-infections including HIV, syphilis, and hepatitis B, and offer HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis to those at risk","Prevention through condom promotion, HPV and hepatitis B vaccination, and risk-reduction counselling","Follow-up with test-of-cure where indicated and abstinence until treatment is completed","Escalate pelvic inflammatory disease or systemic or complicated infection for inpatient parenteral therapy and genitourinary medicine input"],"severityClassification":"No single staging system applies to STIs as a group; severity is assessed by syndrome and complications. Localized lower-tract syndromes (uncomplicated urethritis, cervicitis, vulvovaginitis, genital ulcer) are distinguished from complicated or upper-tract/disseminated disease (PID with peritonitis, perihepatitis, perisplenitis, or pelvic abscess; disseminated gonococcal infection; epididymo-orchitis), and from long-term oncologic or reproductive sequelae (cervical/anogenital neoplasia, infertility). Genital ulcer evaluation also uses lesion natural-history timelines (herpes 2-3 weeks; chancroid and syphilis up to ~6 weeks) to flag atypical persistence (Harrison 22e p.1104, 1108).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1096","Harrison 22e p.1097","Harrison 22e p.1098","Harrison 22e p.1099","Harrison 22e p.1100","Harrison 22e p.1101","Harrison 22e p.1104","Harrison 22e p.1108","Harrison 22e p.1109"],"crossLinks":[]},"sezary_syndrome":{"name":"Sezary syndrome","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SS","leukaemic cutaneous T-cell lymphoma","erythrodermic mycosis fungoides variant","Sezary-Lutzner syndrome","CD4+/CD7-negative circulating Sezary-cell lymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the triad of erythroderma, generalised lymphadenopathy, and clonal neoplastic T-cells (Sezary cells) in skin, lymph nodes, and peripheral blood","Regarded as the leukaemic and more aggressive counterpart of mycosis fungoides, though usually considered a distinct entity arising de novo","Patients typically have intense pruritus, scaling, palmoplantar hyperkeratosis, ectropion, and alopecia, and are prone to skin infection","Sezary cells are mature CD4-positive memory T-cells with cerebriform nuclei, characteristically showing loss of pan-T-cell antigens such as CD7 and CD26","Diagnosis requires demonstrating a significant circulating clonal T-cell burden, for example by an elevated Sezary cell count or CD4-skewed ratio, matching the skin clone","Immune dysregulation increases susceptibility to infection, a major cause of death","Treatment favours skin-directed and immunomodulatory approaches such as extracorporeal photopheresis, retinoids, and interferon, escalating to systemic therapy as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Sezary syndrome is a leukaemic form of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma in which malignant mature CD4-positive memory T-cells with characteristic cerebriform (Sezary) nuclei circulate in the blood and infiltrate the skin and lymph nodes. Loss of normal T-cell surface antigens and skin-homing receptor expression allows widespread cutaneous seeding, producing erythroderma. The expanding neoplastic clone disrupts normal T-cell function and cytokine balance, causing profound pruritus and immune dysregulation that predisposes to recurrent infection. Progressive disease can transform to large-cell morphology with a more aggressive behaviour.","additionalDifferentials":["Erythrodermic mycosis fungoides","Benign (reactive) erythroderma from eczema, psoriasis, or drug reaction","Idiopathic erythroderma","Adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma with skin involvement","Actinic reticuloid and other pseudolymphomatous conditions","Other peripheral T-cell lymphomas with erythroderma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with immunohistochemistry for T-cell markers","Peripheral blood flow cytometry for CD4/CD8 ratio and loss of CD7 and CD26","Sezary cell count and quantification of the circulating clonal burden","Matched T-cell receptor clonality studies on blood and skin","Lymph node evaluation and cross-sectional imaging for staging","Assessment for infection and monitoring of the skin barrier"],"redFlags":["Sepsis arising from a compromised, erythrodermic skin barrier","Large-cell transformation with rapidly enlarging tumours","Progressive lymphadenopathy or visceral involvement","Severe metabolic disturbance from extensive skin failure (fluid loss, thermoregulatory compromise)"],"prognosis":"Sezary syndrome is generally aggressive with a guarded long-term outlook; disease burden, large-cell transformation, and infective complications drive mortality, though immunomodulatory therapy can provide meaningful disease control.","pitfalls":["Attributing chronic erythroderma to benign dermatitis and missing the underlying lymphoma","Failing to demonstrate a matched circulating clone required for diagnosis","Underestimating infection risk from the failing skin barrier","Not recognising large-cell transformation that changes prognosis and treatment"],"management":["Skin-directed therapy with topical corticosteroids, phototherapy, and total-skin electron-beam therapy for cutaneous disease","Systemic immunomodulatory first-line therapy such as extracorporeal photopheresis, interferon, and the retinoid bexarotene","Histone deacetylase inhibitors (vorinostat, romidepsin) and the anti-CCR4 antibody mogamulizumab or brentuximab vedotin for refractory or progressive disease","Intensive management of pruritus with emollients and skin-barrier care","Aggressive treatment and prophylaxis of skin and systemic infection, a major cause of death","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for eligible patients with advanced or transformed disease","Staging surveillance with serial Sezary cell counts and monitoring for large-cell transformation","Coordinate care through haemato-oncology and dermatology multidisciplinary teams"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","Swerdlow SH et al. WHO monograph on lymphoid neoplasms"],"crossLinks":[]},"sheehan_syndrome":{"name":"Sheehan syndrome (postpartum pituitary necrosis)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["postpartum pituitary necrosis","postpartum hypopituitarism","Sheehan's syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from ischaemic necrosis of the pituitary following severe postpartum haemorrhage and hypotension","Failure to lactate after delivery is often the earliest clue, reflecting prolactin deficiency","Failure to resume menstruation and loss of pubic and axillary hair point to gonadotrophin and adrenal androgen loss","The clinical picture can be insidious, sometimes emerging years after the index pregnancy, and is frequently missed","The enlarged, vascular pregnant pituitary is especially vulnerable to hypoperfusion, explaining the peripartum timing","Corticotroph deficiency may present acutely as an adrenal crisis, particularly under physiological stress","Diabetes insipidus is uncommon because the posterior pituitary is usually relatively spared","Imaging may later reveal an empty or partially empty sella from pituitary atrophy"],"pathophysiology":"Sheehan syndrome arises when major obstetric haemorrhage and circulatory collapse cause ischaemic infarction of the anterior pituitary. During pregnancy the gland hypertrophies, largely due to lactotroph expansion, increasing its metabolic demand while its low-pressure portal blood supply leaves it prone to infarction if perfusion falls. The resulting loss of anterior pituitary tissue produces variable, often panhypopituitary, hormone deficiencies affecting prolactin, gonadotrophins, growth hormone, thyroid-stimulating hormone and corticotrophin. The posterior lobe is usually preserved, so diabetes insipidus is comparatively rare, and the infarcted gland gradually atrophies, sometimes leaving an empty sella.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphocytic hypophysitis","Pituitary adenoma or apoplexy","Empty sella syndrome from other causes","Primary autoimmune endocrine failure such as thyroid or adrenal disease","Postpartum thyroiditis","Depression or other non-endocrine causes of postpartum fatigue"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anterior pituitary hormone profile including cortisol, thyroid and gonadal axes","Prolactin measurement","Dynamic pituitary testing where basal results are equivocal","Magnetic resonance imaging of the pituitary, which may show an empty sella","Serum electrolytes to detect hyponatraemia from cortisol or thyroid deficiency","Documentation of the obstetric history and any peripartum haemorrhage"],"redFlags":["Adrenal crisis with hypotension and hyponatraemia","Severe hypothyroidism or myxoedema","Symptomatic hyponatraemia","Profound fatigue and collapse under physiological stress","Delayed diagnosis leading to chronic untreated hypopituitarism"],"prognosis":"With appropriate and lifelong hormone replacement, patients generally do well, but unrecognised disease carries a real risk of life-threatening adrenal crisis; deficits are permanent because infarcted pituitary tissue does not recover.","pitfalls":["Attributing failed lactation and fatigue to normal postpartum recovery and missing the diagnosis","Starting thyroid hormone before glucocorticoid replacement and precipitating adrenal crisis","Failing to consider the diagnosis years after the causative delivery","Overlooking hyponatraemia as a manifestation of cortisol or thyroid deficiency","Assuming posterior pituitary involvement and over-investigating for diabetes insipidus"],"management":["Lifelong anterior pituitary hormone replacement, replacing glucocorticoid first to avoid precipitating adrenal crisis","Levothyroxine for secondary hypothyroidism once adequate glucocorticoid cover is established","Oestrogen with progestogen replacement for gonadotrophin deficiency until the usual age of menopause, and growth hormone replacement in selected adults","Stress-dose glucocorticoid cover during illness, surgery, or physiological stress, with a steroid emergency card and patient education","Urgent parenteral hydrocortisone and fluid resuscitation for adrenal crisis","Monitor clinical and biochemical adequacy of replacement, including electrolytes for hyponatraemia","Endocrinology-led lifelong follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","DeGroot's Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"sheldon_hall_syndrome":{"name":"Sheldon-Hall syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["distal arthrogryposis type 2B","DA2B","DA2B3","TNNI2","TNNT3","TPM2","MYH3"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / Gorlin's Syndromes of the Head and Neck","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A relatively common distal arthrogryposis that overlaps with, but is milder than, Freeman-Sheldon syndrome","Hand and foot contractures resemble those of other distal arthrogryposes, with camptodactyly, ulnar deviation and talipes equinovarus","Facial features are subtle: a triangular face, downslanting palpebral fissures, a small mouth and prominent nasolabial folds, but without the severe microstomia or deep chin dimple of Freeman-Sheldon","Caused by variants in the fast-twitch sarcomeric genes TNNI2, TNNT3, TPM2 or MYH3, mostly autosomal dominant","Intellect is normal and there is no progressive weakness","Distinguishing it from Freeman-Sheldon guides prognosis, surgical planning and counselling","Management follows the distal arthrogryposis principles of physiotherapy, splinting, serial casting and selective surgical release"],"pathophysiology":"Sheldon-Hall syndrome results from dominant variants in genes of the fast skeletal muscle contractile complex, chiefly troponin I (TNNI2), troponin T (TNNT3), tropomyosin (TPM2) and occasionally myosin (MYH3). These variants alter the calcium-regulated interaction of actin and myosin and disturb foetal muscle tone. The reduced and unbalanced movement of the developing foetus allows periarticular fibrosis and joint fixation, producing predominantly distal contractures. Because the underlying genes overlap with those of Freeman-Sheldon syndrome, the two conditions form a phenotypic spectrum, with Sheldon-Hall representing the milder end.","additionalDifferentials":["Freeman-Sheldon syndrome (DA2A)","Distal arthrogryposis type 1","Amyoplasia","Congenital contractural arachnodactyly (Beals syndrome)","Congenital myotonic dystrophy","Escobar syndrome when webbing is present"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dysmorphology examination comparing facial features with Freeman-Sheldon syndrome","Molecular genetic testing (TNNI2, TNNT3, TPM2, MYH3 panel)","Three-generation pedigree and parental examination","Radiographs of hands, feet and spine","Prenatal ultrasound in at-risk pregnancies for foetal akinesia and fixed posture","Feeding and airway assessment when a small mouth or micrognathia is present"],"redFlags":["Feeding difficulty or airway concern from micrognathia","Progressive scoliosis during growth requiring orthopaedic surveillance","Features suggesting the more severe Freeman-Sheldon end of the spectrum","Signs of progressive weakness pointing to an alternative myopathy"],"prognosis":"The outlook is good, with normal cognition and lifespan; contractures are static and functional outcomes after physiotherapy and orthopaedic surgery are generally favourable, though some residual limb deformity may remain.","pitfalls":["Over-diagnosing Freeman-Sheldon syndrome and overstating severity and anaesthetic risk","Missing a mildly affected parent and giving incorrect recurrence risk","Delaying stretching and splinting so that contractures fixate","Overlooking associated scoliosis during childhood follow-up"],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy; management centres on physiotherapy with stretching, serial casting, and splinting of distal contractures from early life","Orthopaedic surgery for fixed foot deformity (talipes equinovarus) and residual hand contractures where function is limited","Occupational therapy and orthoses to optimise hand function and independence","Feeding and airway assessment and support when a small mouth or micrognathia is present","Monitor growth, spine for scoliosis, and functional development","Genetic counselling and testing (TNNI2, TNNT3, TPM2, MYH3) with family evaluation given autosomal dominant inheritance","Prenatal counselling and ultrasound surveillance in at-risk pregnancies","Coordinate care through clinical genetics and orthopaedic multidisciplinary teams"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Gorlin's Syndromes of the Head and Neck"],"crossLinks":[]},"shigellosis":{"name":"Shigellosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bacillary dysentery"],"chapter":172,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1322-1325","clinicalPearls":["Classic dysentery is a syndrome of fever, intestinal cramps, and frequent passage of small, bloody, mucopurulent stools; tenesmus is characteristic (p.1322).","Shigella has a very low infectious dose; experimental infection of volunteers occurs with as few as ~100 CFU, accounting for high attack rates in day-care centers (33-73%) and high secondary attack rates among family members (26-33%) (p.1322).","Spread is primarily fecal-oral via hand carriage; the organism survives gastric passage owing to acid resistance, and can also be transmitted sexually (p.1322-1323).","Shigellosis typically evolves through four phases: incubation (usually 1-4 days, up to 8), watery diarrhea, dysentery, and a postinfectious phase (p.1323).","Dysentery reflects invasion of the colonic mucosa, producing acute colitis mainly involving the distal colon and rectum, with uninterrupted small-volume bloody mucopurulent stools, tenesmus, and cramps (p.1323).","Of the four species, S. flexneri predominates in least-developed areas while S. sonnei is more common in industrialized/emerging economies; S. dysenteriae type 1 (Shiga-toxin producer) drives the most severe epidemic disease (p.1322).","With effective antibiotics, clinical improvement occurs within ~48 h, with fewer complications, shorter symptoms, and clearance of Shigella from stool; in the US every culture-confirmed case is recommended for treatment because of transmissibility (p.1324).","Reactive arthritis (arthritis, ocular inflammation, urethritis) can develop weeks to months after infection, almost exclusively after S. flexneri and in HLA-B27-positive patients (about 3% of S. flexneri infections) (p.1323-1324)."],"pathophysiology":"Pathogenicity is governed by a ~214-kb virulence plasmid encoding a type III secretion system that injects effectors into host cells. After acid-resistant passage through the stomach, bacteria invade colonic epithelium either directly at crypt openings or after crossing M cells over mucosal lymphoid follicles; they induce apoptosis of resident macrophages, drive cytoskeletal uptake, lyse the containing vacuole to reach the cytosol, then spread cell-to-cell using actin-based motility. A massive cytokine-driven influx of polymorphonuclear leukocytes destabilizes the epithelial barrier and produces the acute colitis of shigellosis. Early watery diarrhea is attributed to combined enterotoxin (ShET-1) action and mucosal inflammation. In S. dysenteriae type 1, Shiga toxin (an A1-B5 toxin binding globotriaosylceramide and inhibiting protein synthesis via 28S rRNA N-glycosidase activity) is translocated into the circulation and, by acting on renal target cells, can cause hemolytic-uremic syndrome (p.1322-1323).","additionalDifferentials":["Salmonella infection","Campylobacter jejuni infection","Clostridium difficile colitis","Yersinia enterocolitica infection","Entamoeba histolytica (amebic dysentery)","Inflammatory bowel disease (first flare of Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis), particularly in industrialized countries","Enteroinvasive E. coli (can cause a shigellosis-like dysentery)"],"infectionMimics":["Salmonellosis","Campylobacter jejuni enteritis","Clostridium difficile colitis","Yersinia enterocolitica enterocolitis","Amebic dysentery (Entamoeba histolytica)","Enteroinvasive E. coli dysentery","Enterohemorrhagic E. coli (EHEC) infection (predominant cause of HUS in industrialized countries)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease - Crohn's disease","Inflammatory bowel disease - ulcerative colitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool culture is the gold standard; sample the bloody/mucopurulent portion directly, or use rectal swabs (highest yield in the acute phase).","Because Shigella is fragile and dies during transport, use a holding medium (buffered glycerol saline or Cary-Blair) with prompt inoculation onto isolation media.","Use multiple media to raise yield: nonselective (bromocresol-purple lactose), low-selectivity (MacConkey, eosin-methylene blue), and high-selectivity (Hektoen, Salmonella-Shigella, or xylose-lysine-deoxycholate agar).","Microscopy of stool smears: bacterial enteroinvasive infection (including shigellosis) shows high PMN counts per field, whereas E. histolytica shows erythrophagocytic trophozoites with very few PMNs.","Identify shigellae as non-lactose-fermenting colonies that are glucose-positive (usually no gas), lactose-negative, H2S-negative, and nonmotile; confirm serogroup by slide agglutination.","Blood cultures are positive in fewer than 5% of cases but should be obtained when there is a clinical picture of severe sepsis.","Abdominal x-ray when toxic megacolon is suspected (transverse colon dilation, thumbprinting, loss of haustra, pseudopolyps; pneumoperitoneum if perforated).","In HUS: full blood count and smear (schizocytes), platelets, LDH, free hemoglobin, reticulocytes, creatinine; Coombs test (HUS is Coombs-negative).","PCR for Shigella virulence genes or mass spectrometry are available but not yet standardized for global use."],"redFlags":["Nonbloody diarrhea, moderate-to-severe dehydration, bacteremia, absence of fever, abdominal tenderness, and rectal prolapse are risk factors for death in clinically severe cases.","Altered consciousness (seizures, delirium, coma), especially in children under 5, carries a poor prognosis.","Abdominal distention and tenderness suggesting toxic megacolon, with or without peritonitis.","Pallor, asthenia, irritability, oliguria, increasing edema, or mucosal bleeding heralding hemolytic-uremic syndrome (most often after several days of diarrhea).","Fever persisting beyond 48-72 h in toxic megacolon raises the possibility of local perforation or abscess.","Severe, unusual manifestations such as meningitis in the absence of serious malnutrition may indicate an inborn innate-immune defect (e.g., IRAK-4 deficiency) and warrant genetic investigation."],"prognosis":"Most episodes are self-limited and resolve without treatment within about 1 week; with appropriate therapy recovery occurs within a few days to a week, usually without sequelae. Acute life-threatening complications occur most often in children under 5 (especially the malnourished) and the elderly. In HUS, acute renal failure occurs in 55-70% of cases but renal function recovers in most (up to ~70%). S. dysenteriae type 1 epidemics historically caused high mortality. Shigellosis is a major contributor to stunted growth among children in endemic regions because of the exudative enteropathy and anorexia it causes.","pitfalls":["Avoid antimotility agents in bloody diarrhea: they have been implicated in prolonged fever in shigellosis, are suspected of increasing the risk of toxic megacolon, and were implicated in HUS in children with EHEC infection.","Because shigellosis often manifests only as watery diarrhea, systematic attempts to isolate Shigella are necessary - a watery presentation does not exclude the diagnosis.","Shigella is fragile and commonly disappears during transport with changes in temperature and pH; failure to use a holding medium and prompt inoculation leads to false-negative cultures.","Negative initial blood cultures do not exclude shigellosis (positive in fewer than 5% of cases).","Predisposing factors for toxic megacolon - hypokalemia and use of opioids, anticholinergics, loperamide, psyllium seeds, and antidepressants - should be sought and addressed.","In HUS, oral rehydration solutions and potassium-rich nutrition must be discontinued (water restriction is required), in contrast to routine rehydration management.","Bacteriologic proof in mild adult cases often arrives only after symptoms have resolved, so empiric decisions cannot await culture in fragile patients."],"management":["Oral rehydration with fluid and electrolyte correction as the foundation of care","Empirical antibiotics for moderate-to-severe or dysenteric disease, guided by local resistance, typically a fluoroquinolone or azithromycin, with ceftriaxone as an alternative","Narrow antibiotic therapy to susceptibility once stool culture and sensitivities return","Avoid antimotility agents, which may worsen invasive colitis and prolong illness","Strict hand hygiene, isolation, and infection-control measures given the low infectious dose and faecal-oral spread, with exclusion from food-handling and childcare until clear","Monitor for complications, especially in young children and the malnourished, including haemolytic uraemic syndrome with Shiga-toxin-producing S. dysenteriae type 1","Notify public health and manage outbreaks","Escalate for severe sepsis, profound dehydration, or complications"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal grading scale. Severity is described as depending on infecting serotype and, more importantly, on host age, immunologic and nutritional status. The clinical course is staged in four phases (incubation, watery diarrhea, dysentery, postinfectious). Severe/complicated disease is recognized by markers of poor outcome (nonbloody diarrhea, moderate-to-severe dehydration, bacteremia, absence of fever, abdominal tenderness, rectal prolapse, altered consciousness) and by major complications: intestinal (toxic megacolon, perforation, rectal prolapse), metabolic (hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, dehydration), and HUS (in S. dysenteriae type 1).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, Chapter 172: Shigellosis (Sansonetti PJ, Bergounioux J), pp.1322-1325.","Kotloff KL et al. The incidence, aetiology, and adverse clinical consequences of less severe diarrhoeal episodes among infants and children residing in low-income and middle-income countries (GEMS follow-on). Lancet Glob Health 7:E568, 2019.","WHO. Guidelines for the control of shigellosis, including epidemics due to Shigella dysenteriae type 1.","Niyogi SK. Shigellosis. J Microbiol 43:133, 2005."],"crossLinks":[]},"shone_complex":{"name":"Shone complex","system":"Cardiovascular / Congenital","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Shone syndrome","Shone's anomaly","parachute mitral valve complex","multilevel left heart obstruction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A constellation of multiple left-heart obstructive lesions arranged in series along the inflow and outflow of the left ventricle","The classic four components are a supravalvar mitral ring, parachute mitral valve, subaortic stenosis and coarctation of the aorta","Incomplete forms with two or three components are common, and lesions may be at valvar, subvalvar or supravalvar level","Obstruction at several levels compounds the load on the left heart and drives pulmonary venous hypertension","Presentation depends on the dominant and most severe lesion, ranging from a critically ill neonate with coarctation to an older child with exertional symptoms","A thorough echocardiographic survey is essential because a single obvious lesion may distract from additional serial obstructions","Management is staged surgical or catheter relief of each significant obstruction, with the mitral apparatus often the most challenging to address"],"pathophysiology":"Shone complex is a spectrum of developmental left-heart obstructions occurring in series, so that blood entering and leaving the left ventricle meets resistance at multiple levels. A supravalvar mitral ring and a parachute mitral valve (chordae inserting into a single papillary muscle) impede left atrial emptying and mimic mitral stenosis, raising pulmonary venous pressure. Subaortic and aortic obstruction, together with coarctation, impede left ventricular ejection and impose pressure loading. Because the obstructions are additive, even individually mild lesions can combine to produce significant pulmonary venous hypertension, left atrial and pulmonary vascular changes, and impaired systemic output. The neonatal coarctation component may render the systemic circulation duct-dependent.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated coarctation of the aorta","Hypoplastic left heart syndrome","Isolated congenital mitral stenosis","Cor triatriatum","Isolated subaortic or valvar aortic stenosis","Endocardial fibroelastosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive transthoracic echocardiography to map every level of left-heart obstruction","Doppler gradients across the mitral apparatus, subaortic region, aortic valve and aortic arch","Cardiac MR or CT angiography to define arch anatomy and coarctation","Cardiac catheterisation to assess pulmonary vascular resistance and residual gradients","Chest radiograph and electrocardiogram for chamber enlargement and pulmonary congestion","Serial imaging to track progression of individual lesions over time"],"redFlags":["Neonatal circulatory collapse from a duct-dependent coarctation component","Severe pulmonary venous hypertension or pulmonary oedema","Rising left atrial pressure with a restrictive mitral apparatus","Progressive left ventricular dysfunction from combined obstructions"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the number and severity of lesions and especially on the mitral valve, which is difficult to repair and prone to residual stenosis; multilevel and mitral-dominant disease carries a guarded prognosis and often needs repeated intervention.","pitfalls":["Relieving one obvious obstruction while overlooking additional serial lesions that limit benefit","Underestimating the mitral component, which frequently dictates long-term outcome","Assuming an isolated coarctation and missing the wider complex","Failing to reassess for progression of previously mild lesions after intervention"],"management":["Surgical relief of each obstructive lesion in turn: coarctation repair, resection of subaortic stenosis and supravalvar mitral ring, and mitral or aortic valve repair or replacement","Prostaglandin infusion to maintain ductal patency in the duct-dependent critically ill neonate pending intervention","Medical support of heart failure and pulmonary venous congestion with diuretics and afterload reduction","Comprehensive echocardiographic mapping and serial imaging to detect every level of obstruction and track progression","Lifelong cardiology surveillance for residual or recurrent obstruction, mitral valve dysfunction, and pulmonary hypertension","Endocarditis prophylaxis and staged reintervention as lesions recur or the child grows","Manage through a congenital cardiac surgical and cardiology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","Anderson's Paediatric Cardiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"short_bowel_syndrome":{"name":"Short bowel syndrome","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SBS","short gut syndrome","intestinal failure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AASLD & ACG","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A malabsorptive state resulting from loss of functional small-intestinal length, usually after extensive surgical resection for conditions such as mesenteric ischaemia, Crohn disease, trauma, or radiation injury","Clinical severity depends on the length and site of remaining bowel, whether the ileocaecal valve and colon are preserved, and the health of the residual intestine","The main manifestations are diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, weight loss, and specific nutrient deficiencies","Loss of the terminal ileum impairs absorption of vitamin B12 and bile salts, and bile-salt spillage into the colon can worsen diarrhoea and predispose to gallstones","An intact colon is nutritionally valuable because it salvages energy by fermenting malabsorbed carbohydrate into short-chain fatty acids","Over months to years the residual bowel undergoes adaptation, increasing absorptive capacity, so nutritional needs may lessen with time","Management is staged: fluid and electrolyte correction, parenteral nutrition when needed, gradual introduction of enteral feeding to drive adaptation, and dietary modification","Long-term parenteral nutrition carries important complications including catheter-related bloodstream infection, venous thrombosis, and intestinal-failure-associated liver disease","Enterohormonal therapy (such as GLP-2 analogues) and, in selected refractory cases, intestinal transplantation are options for those dependent on parenteral support"],"pathophysiology":"Short bowel syndrome arises when the absorptive surface of the small intestine is reduced below the level needed to maintain nutrition and hydration, most often after massive resection. The functional consequences depend on which segment is lost: jejunal loss reduces overall absorptive surface, while ileal loss additionally impairs vitamin B12 and bile-salt reabsorption, disrupting the enterohepatic circulation and causing bile-salt diarrhoea and fat malabsorption. Loss of the ileocaecal valve accelerates transit and predisposes to small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth. Rapid transit and reduced surface lead to fluid, electrolyte, and nutrient losses. Over time the remnant intestine adapts through mucosal hyperplasia and slowed transit, partially restoring absorptive capacity.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic pancreatic exocrine insufficiency","Coeliac disease and other mucosal malabsorptive disorders","Small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth","Inflammatory bowel disease with active malabsorption","Chronic infective or inflammatory diarrhoea","Enteric fistula causing functional bowel loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assessment of remaining bowel anatomy and length from operative records and imaging","Nutritional and electrolyte panel including magnesium, calcium, and trace elements","Fat-soluble vitamin, vitamin B12, and iron status","Monitoring of fluid balance and stool or stoma output","Liver function monitoring in patients on parenteral nutrition and evaluation for bacterial overgrowth"],"redFlags":["Severe dehydration with acute kidney injury or dangerous electrolyte disturbances","Catheter-related bloodstream infection or sepsis in patients on parenteral nutrition","Progressive intestinal-failure-associated liver disease or cholestasis","Central venous thrombosis threatening long-term access for parenteral nutrition"],"prognosis":"Outlook depends on residual bowel length and anatomy and on adaptation. Many patients eventually achieve enteral autonomy, while those with very short remnants or absent colon may need lifelong parenteral nutrition, whose complications (liver disease, line sepsis, thrombosis) drive much of the long-term morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Underestimating fluid and electrolyte losses, particularly magnesium, in high-output patients","Withholding enteral nutrition entirely and thereby delaying adaptation","Overlooking vitamin B12 and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency after ileal resection","Missing small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth or catheter sepsis as causes of deterioration"],"management":["Parenteral nutrition and intravenous fluid and electrolyte support initially to prevent dehydration and malnutrition, weaning as intestinal adaptation allows","Structured enteral and oral nutrition to stimulate adaptation, with the diet tailored to remnant anatomy and whether the colon is in continuity","Antidiarrhoeal and antisecretory agents (loperamide, and proton pump inhibitors for gastric hypersecretion) to slow transit and reduce output","Bile-acid sequestrants for bile-salt diarrhoea and parenteral vitamin B12 replacement after terminal ileal loss","The GLP-2 analogue teduglutide to enhance intestinal absorption and reduce parenteral nutrition dependence in selected patients","Correct and monitor micronutrient, fat-soluble vitamin, and trace-element deficiencies","Surveillance for parenteral-nutrition complications (intestinal failure-associated liver disease, catheter sepsis, thrombosis) and small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth","Manage in a specialist intestinal failure multidisciplinary team, considering surgical lengthening or intestinal transplantation for refractory cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AASLD/ACG guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"short_chain_acyl_coa_dehydrogenase_deficiency":{"name":"Short-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","system":"Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCAD deficiency","SCADD","ACADS deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Inherited metabolic disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder of mitochondrial fatty-acid beta-oxidation caused by variants in ACADS, encoding the enzyme that dehydrogenates short-chain (principally four-carbon) acyl-CoA esters.","The biochemical signature is elevated butyrylcarnitine (C4-acylcarnitine) on the blood acylcarnitine profile, with raised urinary ethylmalonic and methylsuccinic acids.","It is frequently detected through newborn bloodspot screening, and a large proportion of those identified remain asymptomatic, so its clinical significance is debated.","Reported features when symptomatic are non-specific and variable, including hypotonia, developmental delay, feeding difficulty, weight faltering, lethargy and seizures, without a consistent phenotype.","Because short-chain fats are a minor energy source and ketogenesis remains largely intact, the hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and acute decompensation typical of medium-chain defects are not characteristic.","Two common ACADS variants behave as susceptibility or modifier alleles that are frequent in the general population and confound interpretation of a positive screen.","Confirmation combines the acylcarnitine and organic-acid pattern with ACADS gene analysis and, where needed, enzyme assay in cultured fibroblasts.","Ethylmalonic aciduria is not specific and must be distinguished from ethylmalonic encephalopathy and multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency."],"pathophysiology":"Short-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase is a flavin-adenine-dinucleotide-dependent mitochondrial matrix enzyme that catalyses the first, rate-committing dehydrogenation step of each beta-oxidation cycle for short-chain fatty acyl-CoA esters, chiefly butyryl-CoA, passing electrons to electron-transfer flavoprotein. Biallelic loss-of-function or destabilising variants in ACADS reduce enzyme activity or protein stability, so butyryl-CoA and related four-carbon intermediates accumulate; these are transesterified to butyrylcarnitine, detected as the C4 species, and diverted to alternative pathways generating ethylmalonic and methylsuccinic acids, the metabolites seen biochemically. Because short-chain fatty acids contribute only modestly to overall energy production and the enzymes handling long- and medium-chain substrates are unaffected, hepatic ketogenesis and fasting energy supply are relatively preserved, explaining the frequent absence of the acute hypoketotic crises seen in medium-chain defects. The variable and often absent clinical phenotype, together with two common population variants that confer only mild reduction in activity, has led many to regard the condition as a biochemical trait of uncertain pathogenicity in a substantial proportion of individuals rather than a consistently disabling disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD) deficiency","Multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (glutaric aciduria type II)","Ethylmalonic encephalopathy (ETHE1-related)","Isobutyryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","Long-chain and very-long-chain fatty-acid oxidation defects","Riboflavin transporter defects and dietary riboflavin deficiency","Mitochondrial respiratory chain disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Newborn bloodspot screening acylcarnitine profile showing C4 elevation","Plasma acylcarnitine profile","Urine organic acids for ethylmalonic and methylsuccinic acids","Molecular genetic analysis of ACADS","Enzyme activity assay in cultured skin fibroblasts or other tissue","Plasma free and total carnitine","Targeted testing to exclude alternative causes of ethylmalonic aciduria, such as ETHE1 analysis"],"redFlags":["Reduced consciousness, poor feeding or lethargy during intercurrent illness or prolonged fasting in a symptomatic infant","Hypoglycaemia, which should prompt a search for an alternative or additional fatty-acid oxidation defect","Weight faltering or regression of developmental milestones","Unexplained seizures in a confirmed case"],"prognosis":"For most individuals, particularly those detected by newborn screening and carrying the common variants, the outlook is benign, with normal development and no specific treatment required. A minority with symptoms follow a variable course, and there is no consistent evidence that biochemical severity predicts clinical outcome.","pitfalls":["Over-diagnosis and unnecessary intervention after a positive newborn screen driven by common non-pathogenic variants","Assuming a causal link between the biochemical finding and non-specific neurological symptoms","Confusing the ethylmalonic aciduria with ethylmalonic encephalopathy or multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","Overlooking a coexisting, more clinically significant fatty-acid oxidation defect when hypoglycaemia is present","Attributing riboflavin-responsive metabolite changes to a primary short-chain defect rather than a flavin-related disorder"],"management":["No specific drug therapy is required for most, particularly asymptomatic individuals identified by newborn screening","Avoidance of prolonged fasting with adequate carbohydrate and energy intake during intercurrent illness as a precaution","An emergency regimen of frequent carbohydrate feeds during febrile illness or reduced intake for symptomatic individuals","L-carnitine supplementation in those with documented carnitine depletion","Developmental and clinical monitoring of symptomatic children for hypotonia, feeding difficulty, and neurodevelopmental progress","Confirmatory biochemical and ACADS genetic testing to distinguish true disease from benign susceptibility variants and avoid overtreatment","Genetic counselling for the autosomal recessive inheritance, with reassurance where the picture is benign","Metabolic medicine follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited metabolic disease reference texts","Clinical biochemistry reference texts","Newborn screening reference guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"short_chain_enoyl_coa_hydratase_deficiency":{"name":"Short-chain enoyl-CoA hydratase (ECHS1) deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ECHS1 deficiency","SCEH deficiency","crotonase deficiency","mitochondrial short-chain enoyl-CoA hydratase 1 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive deficiency of mitochondrial short-chain enoyl-CoA hydratase (ECHS1 gene), an enzyme acting in valine catabolism and fatty-acid oxidation","Characteristically causes a Leigh or Leigh-like syndrome with developmental regression, dystonia, and symmetrical basal ganglia lesions on MRI","Presentation is often in infancy or early childhood, frequently with regression triggered or worsened by intercurrent illness","The mechanism is thought to involve accumulation of toxic valine-pathway intermediates rather than simple energy failure, which has prompted interest in valine (protein) restriction","Biochemical markers can be subtle, but urine organic acids may show metabolites such as 2-methyl-2,3-dihydroxybutyrate and S-(2-carboxypropyl)cysteine derivatives; lactate may be raised","ECHS1 deficiency is an important, potentially treatable cause of Leigh-like disease that can be missed if only the respiratory chain is investigated","Management is largely supportive, with dietary valine/protein restriction and avoidance of catabolic stress explored as disease-modifying approaches"],"pathophysiology":"Short-chain enoyl-CoA hydratase (ECHS1, crotonase) catalyses hydration of short-chain enoyl-CoA esters and participates in the catabolism of the branched-chain amino acid valine as well as in fatty-acid beta-oxidation. Deficiency is believed to cause disease predominantly through accumulation of reactive valine-pathway intermediates (such as methacrylyl-CoA and acryloyl-CoA derivatives) that damage mitochondrial proteins, producing a Leigh-like encephalopathy with characteristic basal ganglia injury. Secondary impairment of energy metabolism, with lactate elevation, may accompany this. Because the toxic intermediates arise chiefly from valine, restricting valine intake is a rational, if still investigational, strategy.","additionalDifferentials":["Leigh syndrome from mitochondrial DNA or nuclear respiratory chain defects","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex deficiency","Biotinidase and biotin-thiamine-responsive basal ganglia disease","Other organic acidaemias causing basal ganglia injury","3-hydroxyisobutyryl-CoA hydrolase (HIBCH) deficiency in the same valine pathway","Acquired or hypoxic-ischaemic basal ganglia injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to identify symmetrical basal ganglia and brainstem lesions","Blood and cerebrospinal fluid lactate measurement","Urine organic acids for valine-pathway metabolites (e.g. 2-methyl-2,3-dihydroxybutyrate)","Plasma acylcarnitine and amino acid profiles","ECHS1 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Enzyme activity or functional studies in fibroblasts where genetics are inconclusive"],"redFlags":["Developmental regression with new dystonia or movement disorder","Acute neurological deterioration during intercurrent illness","Progressive Leigh-like lesions on serial imaging","Feeding difficulties, respiratory compromise or brainstem dysfunction"],"prognosis":"The disorder is frequently severe and progressive, with a Leigh-like course and significant neurological disability or early death in classic cases; milder phenotypes exist, and valine/protein restriction is being studied as a potential disease-modifying measure.","pitfalls":["Attributing Leigh-like disease solely to a respiratory chain defect and not testing ECHS1","Being reassured by subtle or near-normal routine metabolic screening","Overlooking the related valine-pathway disorder HIBCH with a similar presentation","Failing to consider dietary valine restriction as a potentially modifying intervention"],"management":["Valine (protein) restriction as an investigational disease-modifying strategy to limit accumulation of toxic valine-pathway intermediates","Avoidance of catabolic stress with an emergency regimen providing carbohydrate and energy during intercurrent illness","Supportive treatment of dystonia with agents such as trihexyphenidyl, baclofen, or a levodopa trial, alongside physiotherapy","A mitochondrial supportive cocktail (thiamine, riboflavin, coenzyme Q10) as adjunctive care","Anticonvulsants for seizures and management of feeding difficulty, with gastrostomy where needed","Neurodevelopmental support and multidisciplinary rehabilitation for disability","Surveillance with brain MRI and lactate monitoring, and prompt management of acute metabolic or neurological decompensation","Genetic counselling and family testing for the autosomal recessive ECHS1 defect"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: Disorders of Valine Metabolism and Leigh Syndrome","Saudubray, Baumgartner & Walter, Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"short_qt_syndrome":{"name":"Short QT syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SQTS","short QT interval syndrome","KCNH2-related short QT","familial short QT"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC/AHA guidelines on ventricular arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death; Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare inherited channelopathy defined by an abnormally short QT interval and a high risk of atrial and ventricular arrhythmia and sudden death","May present with palpitations, syncope, atrial fibrillation at a young age, or aborted cardiac arrest, sometimes as the first event","The ECG shows a persistently short QT interval with tall, peaked, symmetrical T waves and often a short or absent ST segment","Most identified variants are gain-of-function changes in potassium channel genes (such as KCNH2, KCNQ1 and KCNJ2) that shorten repolarisation; some loss-of-function calcium channel variants overlap with a Brugada phenotype","Diagnosis rests on the corrected QT interval together with symptoms, family history and genetic findings, as the QT alone can overlap with normal variants","An implantable cardioverter defibrillator is the mainstay for those who survive cardiac arrest or are at high risk","Quinidine can prolong the QT interval and is used to reduce arrhythmic events, particularly when a defibrillator is unsuitable","Family screening is important because the first manifestation may be sudden death"],"pathophysiology":"Short QT syndrome is caused by ion channel dysfunction that accelerates cardiac repolarisation. Gain-of-function variants in potassium channel genes increase outward repolarising currents, shortening the action potential duration and thereby the QT interval; loss-of-function variants in calcium channel subunits can produce a similar phenotype. The abbreviated and heterogeneous repolarisation shortens refractory periods across the atria and ventricles, creating a substrate for re-entry that predisposes to atrial fibrillation and to polymorphic ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation, and hence sudden death, in a structurally normal heart.","additionalDifferentials":["Normal QT variant or measurement error","Hypercalcaemia causing QT shortening","Hyperkalaemia with peaked T waves","Effects of digoxin on the ST segment and QT","Early repolarisation syndrome","Brugada syndrome (with overlapping calcium channel variants)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG with careful measurement of the corrected QT interval and T-wave morphology","Serial or repeat ECGs to confirm a persistently short QT","Ambulatory monitoring for atrial and ventricular arrhythmia","Correction and reassessment after excluding electrolyte and drug causes of QT shortening","Echocardiography to confirm a structurally normal heart","Genetic testing for potassium and calcium channel variants with family cascade screening"],"redFlags":["Aborted cardiac arrest or documented ventricular fibrillation","Unexplained syncope in a young person with a short QT","Atrial fibrillation presenting at an unusually young age","Family history of sudden cardiac death"],"prognosis":"The natural history is dominated by the risk of sudden arrhythmic death, which can occur across all ages including infancy; appropriate defibrillator therapy and, where used, QT-prolonging drug therapy substantially improve outcome for high-risk individuals.","pitfalls":["Overlooking a short QT interval because attention focuses on QT prolongation","Attributing a short QT to hypercalcaemia or digoxin without reassessing after correction","Relying on the QT value alone without integrating symptoms, family history and genetics","Failing to screen relatives given that sudden death can be the first event","Assuming a structurally normal heart excludes life-threatening arrhythmia"],"management":["Implantable cardioverter defibrillator implantation as the mainstay for survivors of aborted cardiac arrest or those judged at high arrhythmic risk","Quinidine or another class Ia antiarrhythmic to prolong repolarisation and lengthen the QT interval, reducing ventricular arrhythmia, especially where a defibrillator is declined or unsuitable or as an adjunct to reduce shocks","Correction of electrolyte disturbances and avoidance of influences that further abbreviate repolarisation","Management of associated atrial fibrillation with rhythm or rate control and anticoagulation as indicated, exercising caution when suppressing conduction","Ambulatory and serial ECG surveillance for atrial and ventricular arrhythmia and device interrogation to guide therapy","Genetic counselling with cascade ECG and genetic screening of first-degree relatives","Prompt escalation to specialist inherited-arrhythmia review after syncope, palpitations or an appropriate device therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with ventricular arrhythmias and the prevention of sudden cardiac death","Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"short_rib_polydactyly_syndrome":{"name":"Short-rib polydactyly syndrome","system":"Skeletal / Ciliopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SRPS","Short-rib thoracic dysplasia with polydactyly","Short-rib polydactyly syndrome (Saldino-Noonan/Majewski/Verma-Naumoff/Beemer-Langer types)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and skeletal dysplasia references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive skeletal ciliopathy characterised by a markedly constricted, narrow thorax with short horizontal ribs, short limbs and, in most subtypes, polydactyly; it lies at the lethal end of the short-rib thoracic dysplasia spectrum that also includes Jeune asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy and Ellis-van Creveld syndrome.","Classically divided into overlapping subtypes: Saldino-Noonan (type I), Majewski (type II), Verma-Naumoff (type III) and Beemer-Langer (type IV), though molecular data show these are a continuum rather than discrete disorders.","Caused by biallelic variants in genes encoding intraflagellar transport and ciliary proteins (for example DYNC2H1, IFT80, WDR34, WDR60, IFT140, IFT172, NEK1, WDR35, and others), disrupting the primary cilium and Hedgehog signalling.","Polydactyly is usually pre-axial or post-axial and may involve hands and feet; Majewski type is distinguished by disproportionately short, ovoid tibiae and prominent oral/facial clefting.","Extraskeletal anomalies are common and help subtype the disorder: congenital heart defects, renal cystic dysplasia, hepatic ductal plate malformation, gastrointestinal atresia, ambiguous or anomalous genitalia and cerebral malformations.","The severe thoracic constriction produces pulmonary hypoplasia, and the condition is typically perinatally lethal from respiratory failure; diagnosis is often made antenatally or at birth.","Antenatal ultrasound may show a narrow thorax, short long bones, polydactyly and associated visceral anomalies; distinguishing it antenatally from milder short-rib thoracic dysplasia guides counselling.","It belongs to the broader family of skeletal ciliopathies, so overlap with cranioectodermal dysplasia, Sensenbrenner and Jeune syndromes reflects shared ciliary gene pathways and allelism."],"pathophysiology":"Short-rib polydactyly syndrome is a skeletal ciliopathy arising from biallelic loss-of-function or hypomorphic variants in genes encoding components of the primary cilium, particularly the intraflagellar transport (IFT) machinery and the cytoplasmic dynein-2 retrograde motor. Genes implicated include DYNC2H1, WDR34, WDR60, IFT80, IFT140, IFT172, WDR35, NEK1 and related ciliary transcripts, several of which are allelic with milder disorders such as Jeune asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy and cranioectodermal dysplasia. The primary cilium is an essential organelle for transducing Hedgehog signalling, which governs endochondral bone growth, patterning of the limb bud and the anterior-posterior axis of the digits. When IFT-mediated protein trafficking along the ciliary axoneme fails, chondrocyte proliferation and the orderly progression through the growth plate are disrupted, producing the profoundly shortened ribs and long bones. Aberrant Hedgehog signalling also perturbs digit specification, giving rise to polydactyly, while ciliary dysfunction in the developing kidney, liver, heart and gut explains the cystic renal dysplasia, ductal plate malformation, cardiac defects and visceral anomalies that accompany the skeletal phenotype. The severity gradient across subtypes reflects the degree of residual ciliary function conferred by particular allele combinations.","additionalDifferentials":["Jeune asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy (short-rib thoracic dysplasia without or with polydactyly, milder and often survivable)","Ellis-van Creveld syndrome (chondroectodermal dysplasia with post-axial polydactyly and cardiac defects)","Thanatophoric dysplasia (lethal short-limbed dysplasia with narrow thorax but no polydactyly)","Cranioectodermal dysplasia (Sensenbrenner syndrome) within the same ciliopathy spectrum","Chondrodysplasia punctata and other lethal neonatal skeletal dysplasias","Oral-facial-digital syndromes (particularly overlap with Majewski type facial clefting and lingual anomalies)","Hydrolethalus syndrome and Meckel-Gruber syndrome (other lethal ciliopathies with polydactyly and visceral malformation)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed antenatal ultrasound assessing thoracic circumference, long-bone length, digit number and visceral anatomy","Postnatal full skeletal radiographic survey documenting rib length and morphology, metaphyseal spurs and long-bone shape","Molecular genetic testing via a skeletal ciliopathy or skeletal dysplasia gene panel, or exome/genome sequencing","Fetal or postnatal echocardiography to characterise congenital heart disease","Renal and hepatic imaging for cystic dysplasia and ductal plate malformation","Perinatal autopsy with photographs and histology of bone, kidney and liver where consent is given","Genetic counselling with confirmation of parental carrier status for recurrence-risk estimation"],"redFlags":["Severe respiratory distress at birth from pulmonary hypoplasia secondary to the constricted thorax","Antenatal detection of a markedly narrow chest with short ribs, prompting urgent multidisciplinary counselling","Coexisting major cardiac or renal malformation compounding perinatal instability","Hydrops or polyhydramnios suggesting associated visceral or cardiac compromise","Progressive hypoxaemia unresponsive to ventilatory support, indicating a non-survivable thoracic volume"],"prognosis":"The classic subtypes are almost always perinatally lethal owing to pulmonary hypoplasia from the severely constricted thorax, with most affected infants dying before or shortly after birth. Rare milder, allelic presentations that overlap with survivable short-rib thoracic dysplasia carry a better but still guarded outlook dependent on thoracic volume and visceral involvement.","pitfalls":["Regarding the historical Saldino-Noonan, Majewski, Verma-Naumoff and Beemer-Langer subtypes as rigid entities rather than a molecular continuum overlapping with Jeune and Ellis-van Creveld syndromes","Assuming polydactyly is always present, since some short-rib thoracic dysplasias in the same spectrum lack extra digits","Attributing perinatal death solely to the skeletal findings and overlooking a lethal cardiac or renal anomaly","Failing to arrange molecular confirmation and carrier testing, leaving recurrence risk uncounselled for future pregnancies","Confusing it antenatally with thanatophoric dysplasia, which shares a narrow thorax but lacks polydactyly and has a different molecular basis"],"management":["Antenatal counselling once the constricted thorax and pulmonary hypoplasia are recognised, discussing the usually perinatally lethal prognosis and the options of continuation with palliative planning or termination","Molecular genetic confirmation via a skeletal ciliopathy or exome panel to secure the diagnosis and refine recurrence counselling","Delivery planning in a unit with neonatal and palliative expertise, with an agreed comfort-care and resuscitation plan given the lethal pulmonary hypoplasia","Neonatal palliative and comfort care directed at the respiratory failure from thoracic constriction","Coordinated multidisciplinary input from foetal medicine, clinical genetics, neonatology and palliative care","Perinatal autopsy discussion to confirm the diagnosis and inform future counselling","Genetic counselling with offer of prenatal or preimplantation diagnosis in future pregnancies given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics and dysmorphology reference texts","Skeletal dysplasia and ciliopathy classification resources","Paediatric radiology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"short_rib_thoracic_dysplasia":{"name":"Short-rib thoracic dysplasia spectrum","system":"Genetics / Ciliopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SRTD","Jeune asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy","short-rib polydactyly syndrome","asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy","DYNC2H1"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A spectrum of skeletal ciliopathies unified by a narrow, long thorax with short horizontal ribs and variable limb shortening","Encompasses Jeune asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy and the more severe, often lethal short-rib polydactyly syndromes (Saldino-Noonan, Majewski, Verma-Naumoff and Beemer-Langer types)","Polydactyly is present in some subtypes and absent in others, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis","Neonatal respiratory insufficiency from a constricted rib cage dominates the early clinical picture","Extra-skeletal involvement includes cystic renal disease, hepatic fibrosis, retinal dystrophy and pancreatic or intestinal anomalies","Caused by biallelic variants in intraflagellar transport and dynein-2 motor genes, most commonly DYNC2H1","Survivors of infancy may later develop progressive renal and hepatic disease requiring surveillance"],"pathophysiology":"The short-rib thoracic dysplasias result from biallelic variants in genes encoding components of the intraflagellar transport system and the cytoplasmic dynein-2 retrograde motor, most often DYNC2H1. These proteins are essential for building and maintaining the primary cilium, through which hedgehog and other signals that pattern the developing skeleton are transduced. Disrupted ciliary signalling in growth-plate chondrocytes impairs endochondral ossification, producing the characteristically short ribs, narrow thorax and shortened limbs, while abnormal hedgehog signalling in the limb bud underlies the polydactyly seen in some subtypes. Because the same cilia are required in kidney, liver, retina and pancreas, affected individuals may also develop cystic kidney disease, hepatic fibrosis, retinal degeneration and other visceral abnormalities.","additionalDifferentials":["Ellis-van Creveld syndrome","Cranioectodermal dysplasia (Sensenbrenner)","Thanatophoric dysplasia and other lethal skeletal dysplasias","Achondrogenesis","Bardet-Biedl syndrome","Oral-facial-digital syndromes with skeletal involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey to assess rib length, thoracic dimensions, pelvis and limb bones","Chest imaging and respiratory assessment for thoracic insufficiency","Renal ultrasound and renal function testing for cystic or tubulointerstitial disease","Liver function tests and hepatic imaging for fibrosis","Ophthalmological assessment with electroretinography for retinal dystrophy","Molecular testing with a skeletal ciliopathy gene panel"],"redFlags":["Severe thoracic insufficiency and respiratory failure in the newborn","Recurrent respiratory infection with failure to thrive","Progressive renal impairment","Hepatic fibrosis with portal hypertension"],"prognosis":"Outcome is highly variable across the spectrum: the lethal short-rib polydactyly forms are fatal in the perinatal period, whereas Jeune syndrome has a variable respiratory course, and survivors of the early years remain at risk of progressive renal and hepatic disease.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis simply because polydactyly is absent","Underestimating the risk of progressive renal or hepatic disease in survivors","Failing to anticipate and plan for a difficult respiratory course","Assuming a uniformly lethal outcome across the whole spectrum"],"management":["Respiratory support proportionate to thoracic insufficiency, ranging from supplemental oxygen and assisted ventilation to aggressive treatment of recurrent chest infections","Thoracic expansion surgery, such as expansion thoracoplasty or a vertical expandable prosthetic titanium rib, in selected survivable cases to enlarge the constricted rib cage","Surveillance and management of cystic renal disease with monitoring of renal function, treatment of hypertension and renal replacement therapy for progressive failure","Monitoring for hepatic fibrosis with liver function tests and imaging","Ophthalmological surveillance and low-vision support for retinal dystrophy","Coordinated multidisciplinary care across respiratory medicine, thoracic surgery, nephrology, hepatology, ophthalmology and clinical genetics","Genetic counselling with cascade and prenatal testing given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Short-Rib Thoracic Dysplasia with or without Polydactyly","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"short_stature":{"name":"Short Stature","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["growth failure","poor linear growth","faltering height","paediatric short stature"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Short stature is generally defined as height more than about 2 standard deviations below the mean for age and sex, but the growth velocity and trajectory matter more than a single measurement.","The two most common causes are normal variants: familial (genetic) short stature and constitutional delay of growth and puberty.","Serial, accurately measured heights plotted on a growth chart are the single most valuable assessment, since a falling growth velocity is a key warning sign.","Bone age radiography helps distinguish constitutional delay (delayed bone age, preserved height potential) from familial short stature (bone age near chronological age).","A low or crossing-down growth velocity with poor weight gain suggests systemic disease or malnutrition, whereas short but proportionate growth with normal velocity favours a normal variant.","Endocrine causes such as growth hormone deficiency and hypothyroidism typically produce a child who is relatively overweight for height, in contrast to malnutrition or chronic illness.","Disproportionate short stature points towards a skeletal dysplasia and warrants assessment of body segment ratios.","Estimating mid-parental target height helps interpret whether a child's growth is appropriate for their genetic background."],"pathophysiology":"Linear growth depends on an intact growth plate responding to growth hormone, insulin-like growth factor 1, thyroid hormone, and sex steroids, supported by adequate nutrition and freedom from chronic illness. Short stature arises when any of these are disturbed: genetic determination of a lower height potential, temporary delay in the tempo of maturation, endocrine deficiency, chronic systemic disease, undernutrition, or intrinsic bone/cartilage disorders. The pattern of growth velocity, body proportions, and bone age reflects which mechanism predominates.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial (genetic) short stature","Constitutional delay of growth and puberty","Growth hormone deficiency","Hypothyroidism","Chronic systemic disease (e.g. coeliac disease, inflammatory bowel disease, renal or cardiac disease)","Turner syndrome and other genetic/syndromic causes","Skeletal dysplasia","Undernutrition or psychosocial deprivation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Accurate serial height and weight with growth velocity calculation","Bone age radiograph","Screening bloods (full blood count, renal and liver function, inflammatory markers, coeliac serology)","Thyroid function and, where indicated, IGF-1 and growth hormone provocation testing","Karyotype or genetic testing in girls (to exclude Turner syndrome) and where a syndrome is suspected","Skeletal survey if disproportion suggests a dysplasia"],"redFlags":["A crossing-down growth velocity or fall across major height centiles","Short stature with neurological signs, headaches, or visual disturbance suggesting an intracranial lesion","Disproportionate short stature suggesting a skeletal dysplasia","Poor growth with weight loss or gastrointestinal symptoms suggesting chronic disease","Dysmorphic features or other findings suggesting a genetic syndrome"],"prognosis":"Normal variants such as familial short stature and constitutional delay have an excellent prognosis with normal health, and constitutional delay usually reaches a normal adult height; outcomes for pathological causes depend on early identification and treatment of the underlying condition.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single height measurement rather than growth velocity over time","Labelling a child as a normal variant without excluding treatable disease when velocity is poor","Missing an intracranial cause of growth hormone deficiency","Failing to consider Turner syndrome in a short girl"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying cause: recombinant growth hormone for growth hormone deficiency, levothyroxine for hypothyroidism, a gluten-free diet for coeliac disease and optimisation of nutrition and any chronic systemic illness","Reassurance and structured observation for the normal variants of familial short stature and constitutional delay, with serial monitoring of growth velocity","Recombinant growth hormone for licensed non-deficiency indications such as Turner syndrome, SHOX deficiency and small-for-gestational-age children without catch-up growth","A short course of sex-steroid induction therapy for distressing constitutional delay of growth and puberty","Serial accurate auxology plotting height, weight and growth velocity, with bone-age radiography, to detect a falling trajectory","Referral to paediatric endocrinology with dietetic and psychological support as needed","Genetic evaluation and counselling where a syndrome or skeletal dysplasia is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"shoulder_dystocia":{"name":"Shoulder Dystocia","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["shoulder dystocia","impacted shoulders","obstetric shoulder impaction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Shoulder dystocia is failure of the shoulders to deliver after the head, caused by impaction of the anterior shoulder behind the maternal pubic symphysis, and is a time-critical obstetric emergency","The warning sign is the turtle sign, where the delivered head retracts tightly against the perineum, and failure of restitution and of routine gentle traction to deliver the body","Risk factors include fetal macrosomia, maternal diabetes, previous shoulder dystocia, obesity, prolonged labour, and instrumental delivery, but many cases occur without identifiable risk factors so all birth attendants must be prepared","Management follows a structured sequence: call for help, avoid the harmful HELPER pitfalls, and apply the McRoberts manoeuvre with suprapubic pressure as first-line steps","If initial measures fail, internal rotational manoeuvres (such as delivery of the posterior arm or rotational techniques) are used, and the mother should not be pushed while shoulders are impacted","Fundal pressure must be avoided because it worsens impaction and increases the risk of uterine rupture","Excessive or downward traction on the fetal head is the main modifiable cause of brachial plexus injury and should be avoided","Complications include brachial plexus palsy (Erb palsy), clavicular or humeral fracture, hypoxic injury from delay, and maternal postpartum haemorrhage and perineal trauma","Accurate contemporaneous documentation of the sequence, manoeuvres, timings, and personnel is essential given the medicolegal significance"],"pathophysiology":"After the fetal head delivers, the shoulders normally rotate into the wider oblique diameter of the pelvis. In shoulder dystocia the anterior shoulder fails to rotate and becomes wedged above the pubic symphysis (or, less commonly, the posterior shoulder impacts on the sacral promontory). Because the fetus can no longer descend, the umbilical cord is compressed within the pelvis and placental gas exchange is impaired, so fetal hypoxia and acidosis progress with time. Rotational manoeuvres and delivery of the posterior arm work by reducing the shoulder-to-shoulder (bisacromial) diameter or moving it into a larger pelvic diameter, freeing the impaction.","additionalDifferentials":["Difficulty from a tight nuchal cord without true shoulder impaction","Fetal anomalies causing enlarged trunk or abdomen (e.g. large abdominal mass, hydrops)","Locked twins","Uterine or contraction abnormality causing prolonged second stage","Cephalopelvic disproportion with obstructed labour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Immediate clinical recognition rather than investigation is the priority in the acute event","Neonatal assessment of the arms, clavicles, and neurology for brachial plexus injury or fracture after delivery","Cord blood gas analysis to document the degree of any acidosis","Maternal assessment for postpartum haemorrhage and perineal or anal sphincter trauma","Antenatal estimation of fetal weight and screening for diabetes in at-risk pregnancies"],"redFlags":["OBSTETRIC EMERGENCY: head delivered with turtle sign and shoulders not delivering despite routine traction, requiring immediate structured manoeuvres","Prolonged head-to-body delivery interval with rising risk of hypoxic-ischaemic injury","Signs of neonatal brachial plexus injury or limb fracture after delivery","Maternal postpartum haemorrhage or severe perineal and anal sphincter trauma","Use of fundal pressure or excessive head traction indicating unsafe technique"],"prognosis":"Most shoulder dystocias are relieved with recognised manoeuvres and the majority of infants have no lasting harm. Brachial plexus injuries are the commonest fetal complication and most resolve within the first year, though a minority are permanent. Prolonged impaction risks hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy or death. Women who have had shoulder dystocia have an increased recurrence risk in subsequent pregnancies.","pitfalls":["Applying fundal pressure, which worsens impaction and can cause uterine rupture","Using excessive downward traction on the head, the leading cause of brachial plexus injury","Encouraging maternal pushing while the shoulder remains impacted","Failing to summon senior and neonatal help early","Poor documentation of manoeuvres and timings in a medicolegally significant event"],"management":["Immediate declaration of the emergency and summoning of senior help, including additional midwifery, obstetric, neonatal and anaesthetic staff","McRoberts manoeuvre with suprapubic pressure as the first-line step to free the impacted anterior shoulder","Avoidance of fundal pressure and of excessive downward traction, and instruction to the mother to stop pushing while the shoulders are impacted","Internal rotational manoeuvres and delivery of the posterior arm if first-line measures fail, aided by episiotomy to create room","Last-resort manoeuvres including the all-fours position and, if all else fails, cleidotomy, symphysiotomy or cephalic replacement","Neonatal assessment for brachial plexus injury and clavicular or humeral fracture, with cord blood gas analysis to document any acidosis","Maternal assessment for postpartum haemorrhage and perineal or anal sphincter trauma, with contemporaneous documentation and debrief"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"shprintzen_goldberg_syndrome":{"name":"Shprintzen-Goldberg syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SGS","SKI","marfanoid craniosynostosis syndrome","Shprintzen-Goldberg craniosynostosis syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews / Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by a marfanoid habitus combined with craniosynostosis and, in most cases, intellectual disability, which helps distinguish it from Marfan and Loeys-Dietz syndromes","Caused by pathogenic variants in SKI, usually arising de novo, affecting a regulator of transforming growth factor beta signalling","Craniofacial features include craniosynostosis, dolichocephaly, hypertelorism, proptosis, a high-arched palate and micrognathia","Skeletal features overlap with Marfan syndrome: arachnodactyly, dolichostenomelia, pectus deformity, scoliosis and joint hypermobility or contractures","Hypotonia, developmental delay and intellectual disability are common and are more prominent than in other marfanoid connective-tissue disorders","Aortic root dilatation can occur but is generally milder and less frequent than in Marfan or Loeys-Dietz syndromes, so cardiovascular surveillance is still warranted","Additional features include hernias and, in some, brain anomalies such as Chiari I malformation","Diagnosis rests on the characteristic pattern of findings with confirmatory SKI genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Shprintzen-Goldberg syndrome is a heritable connective-tissue disorder caused by pathogenic variants in SKI, which encodes a repressor of transforming growth factor beta (TGF-beta) signalling. Loss of normal SKI-mediated repression is thought to enhance TGF-beta signalling, a mechanism shared with Marfan and Loeys-Dietz syndromes, disturbing the development and integrity of connective tissue, bone and the cardiovascular system. This produces the marfanoid skeletal habitus, the craniofacial features including premature fusion of cranial sutures (craniosynostosis), and connective-tissue laxity, alongside the neurodevelopmental impairment that distinguishes the condition. Aortic root involvement reflects the same connective-tissue vulnerability but tends to be less severe than in related aortopathies.","additionalDifferentials":["Marfan syndrome","Loeys-Dietz syndrome","Congenital contractural arachnodactyly (Beals syndrome)","Other craniosynostosis syndromes","Homocystinuria (marfanoid habitus)","Other TGF-beta pathway connective-tissue disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SKI molecular genetic testing","Echocardiography for aortic root dimensions","Skull imaging to assess craniosynostosis","Skeletal survey for scoliosis, pectus and limb proportions","Brain MRI when Chiari malformation or other anomaly is suspected","Developmental and ophthalmological assessment"],"redFlags":["Progressive aortic root dilatation","Raised intracranial pressure from craniosynostosis","Severe scoliosis compromising respiratory function","Airway or feeding difficulties from craniofacial anomalies"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the severity of skeletal, neurological and cardiovascular involvement; intellectual disability is common, and although aortic disease is usually milder than in Marfan syndrome, cardiovascular surveillance remains important.","pitfalls":["Labelling the patient as having Marfan syndrome and missing the craniosynostosis and intellectual disability","Assuming aortic involvement is absent and omitting cardiovascular surveillance","Overlooking raised intracranial pressure from suture fusion","Attributing developmental delay to an unrelated cause"],"management":["Cardiovascular surveillance with serial echocardiography for aortic root dilatation, with beta-blocker or angiotensin receptor blocker therapy to reduce aortic wall stress when dilatation is present","Prophylactic aortic root surgery if dilatation progresses to established surgical criteria","Craniofacial and neurosurgical assessment of craniosynostosis with cranial vault expansion where raised intracranial pressure or progressive deformity demands it","Orthopaedic management of scoliosis, pectus deformity and joint hypermobility or contractures with bracing, physiotherapy or corrective surgery","Early developmental intervention and educational support for hypotonia, developmental delay and intellectual disability, with ophthalmological review","Coordinated multidisciplinary care across clinical genetics, cardiology, craniofacial surgery, orthopaedics and developmental paediatrics","Genetic counselling explaining the usually de novo SKI variant and consequently low recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews / OMIM entry for Shprintzen-Goldberg Syndrome (SKI)","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Consensus literature on TGF-beta connective-tissue disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"shuddering_attacks":{"name":"Shuddering attacks of infancy","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["shuddering spells","shivering attacks"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / paediatric neurology literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Brief episodes of rapid shivering or shuddering of the head, shoulders and trunk, as if experiencing a cold chill","Consciousness is fully preserved and there is no postictal drowsiness, distinguishing episodes from seizures","Attacks are very short, may occur many times a day and often happen during excitement, feeding or frustration","Onset is usually in infancy or early childhood and the episodes are benign and self-limiting","The electroencephalogram is normal, both during and between episodes","A possible association with later essential tremor has been suggested, and a family history of tremor may be present"],"pathophysiology":"Shuddering attacks are a benign non-epileptic paroxysmal event of early childhood, characterised by brief tremulous shivering movements with retained awareness. The mechanism is not fully understood but the movements resemble an exaggerated shiver, and the reported association with a family history of essential tremor has led to the hypothesis that they may be an early manifestation of a tremor tendency. There is no underlying structural or epileptic disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Epileptic seizures (especially myoclonic or spasms)","Benign myoclonus of early infancy","Tics","Chills from fever or infection","Gratification behaviour or other benign paroxysmal events"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the characteristic brief shivering with preserved awareness","Home video of episodes to support the diagnosis","Electroencephalogram only if seizures cannot be excluded clinically","Developmental and neurological assessment confirming normal findings","Family history of tremor"],"redFlags":["Loss of awareness or postictal features suggesting seizures","Developmental delay or regression","Abnormal neurological examination","Change in the pattern or emergence of new event types"],"prognosis":"The attacks are entirely benign and typically resolve during early childhood without treatment; development and later neurological health are normal.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing shuddering as epileptic seizures and starting unnecessary treatment","Ordering extensive investigation for a clearly benign event","Overlooking a family history of tremor","Failing to reassess if awareness is lost or the pattern changes"],"management":["Parental reassurance and education that the episodes are benign, self-limiting and require no drug treatment","Confirmation of the benign diagnosis clinically and with a home video of typical episodes, avoiding unnecessary investigation or antiepileptic medication","Electroencephalography reserved only for cases where seizures cannot be excluded clinically","Watchful monitoring of neurodevelopmental progress, with re-review if the episodes change in character","Advice on the recognised possible association with later essential tremor and note of any family history of tremor","Re-referral to paediatric neurology if awareness is lost during episodes, the events evolve, or development regresses"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Paediatric neurology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"shwachman_diamond":{"name":"Shwachman-Diamond syndrome","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SDS","Shwachman-Bodian-Diamond syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An inherited bone marrow failure syndrome that combines exocrine pancreatic insufficiency with cytopenias, most consistently neutropenia","Most cases are autosomal recessive and caused by biallelic mutations in the SBDS gene, another disorder of ribosome maturation","Children typically present with fatty stools and failure to thrive from pancreatic insufficiency, plus recurrent infections from neutropenia","Skeletal abnormalities such as metaphyseal dysostosis and short stature are characteristic associated features","Neutropenia may be intermittent, and pancytopenia can develop; the marrow is often hypocellular","Pancreatic function tends to improve with age even though the enzyme deficiency and skeletal features persist","There is a significant risk of progression to myelodysplastic syndrome and acute myeloid leukaemia","Management principles include pancreatic enzyme replacement and fat-soluble vitamin support, infection prophylaxis and treatment, haematological support, and allogeneic stem cell transplantation for severe marrow failure or malignant transformation","It must be distinguished from cystic fibrosis, which also causes pancreatic insufficiency but has a normal sweat chloride in SDS"],"pathophysiology":"Shwachman-Diamond syndrome is most often caused by biallelic mutations in SBDS, a gene whose product is required for the maturation of the large ribosomal subunit and ribosome function. The resulting defect in ribosome biogenesis impairs highly proliferative and secretory tissues, producing exocrine pancreatic acinar failure with fatty replacement, ineffective haematopoiesis with neutropenia and marrow hypocellularity, and skeletal developmental abnormalities. Genomic and clonal instability in the haematopoietic compartment underlies the predisposition to myelodysplasia and acute myeloid leukaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystic fibrosis (pancreatic insufficiency with abnormal sweat chloride)","Other inherited bone marrow failure syndromes (Fanconi anaemia, dyskeratosis congenita)","Severe congenital or cyclical neutropenia","Pearson marrow-pancreas syndrome","Acquired causes of neutropenia or pancreatic insufficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and serial monitoring for neutropenia and other cytopenias","Tests of exocrine pancreatic function (e.g. faecal elastase, serum trypsinogen and isoamylase)","Sweat chloride testing to exclude cystic fibrosis","Bone marrow examination with cytogenetics to assess cellularity and clonal change","Molecular genetic testing of SBDS and related genes; skeletal imaging"],"redFlags":["Severe neutropenia with serious or recurrent infection","Progression to pancytopenia or marrow failure","New cytogenetic clonal abnormalities or blasts indicating MDS or acute leukaemia","Failure to thrive with malnutrition and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency","Sepsis in a neutropenic patient"],"prognosis":"Pancreatic insufficiency is manageable and often improves with age, but the long-term prognosis is dominated by the risk of progressive marrow failure and transformation to myelodysplasia or acute myeloid leukaemia. Stem cell transplantation can address the haematological disease, and overall survival depends heavily on whether clonal evolution occurs.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the pancreatic insufficiency as cystic fibrosis","Overlooking intermittent neutropenia because counts fluctuate","Failing to monitor the marrow for clonal evolution over time","Neglecting nutritional and fat-soluble vitamin support","Not screening potential related stem cell donors for the disorder"],"management":["Pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy with fat-soluble vitamin supplementation for exocrine pancreatic insufficiency and steatorrhoea","Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor for severe or symptomatic neutropenia, with prompt broad-spectrum antibiotics for febrile illness given the infection risk","Serial full blood counts and periodic bone marrow examination with cytogenetics to detect evolving marrow failure, myelodysplasia or acute myeloid leukaemia","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for progressive marrow failure or leukaemic transformation","Nutritional support with monitoring of growth and correction of fat-soluble vitamin deficiency","Coordinated care across haematology, gastroenterology, orthopaedics and dietetics for the skeletal and multisystem features","Genetic counselling and cascade family testing given autosomal recessive SBDS inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"shwachman_diamond_syndrome":{"name":"Shwachman-Diamond syndrome","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SDS","SBDS","Shwachman-Bodian-Diamond syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews (NCBI) and inherited bone-marrow-failure consensus guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive inherited bone-marrow-failure and ribosomopathy syndrome, most often caused by biallelic variants in SBDS","Classic combination is exocrine pancreatic insufficiency with malabsorption and steatorrhoea plus bone marrow dysfunction","Neutropenia is the most common and characteristic cytopenia, often intermittent, and predisposes to recurrent infection","Skeletal abnormalities include metaphyseal dysplasia, short stature and thoracic dystrophy","Pancreatic dysfunction may improve with age, and serum trypsinogen and isoamylase are useful biochemical markers","There is a significant risk of progression to aplastic anaemia, myelodysplastic syndrome and acute myeloid leukaemia","The SBDS protein participates in ribosome maturation, placing the disorder among the ribosomopathies","Management centres on pancreatic enzyme and fat-soluble vitamin replacement, infection control, and marrow surveillance, with haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for marrow failure or malignant transformation"],"pathophysiology":"Shwachman-Diamond syndrome most commonly results from biallelic variants in SBDS, whose product cooperates in the maturation of the large ribosomal subunit and release of the anti-association factor eIF6. Defective ribosome assembly impairs protein translation in highly proliferative tissues, producing pancreatic acinar hypoplasia with fatty replacement, ineffective and reduced haematopoiesis, and skeletal dysplasia. Genomic instability of the marrow underlies the predisposition to clonal evolution and leukaemic transformation.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystic fibrosis (exocrine pancreatic insufficiency)","Pearson marrow-pancreas syndrome","Other inherited bone-marrow-failure syndromes such as Fanconi anaemia and dyskeratosis congenita","Diamond-Blackfan anaemia","Cyclic or severe congenital neutropenia","Cartilage-hair hypoplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count over time to document neutropenia and other cytopenias","Faecal elastase and serum trypsinogen and isoamylase for pancreatic exocrine function","Molecular genetic testing of SBDS and related genes","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine with cytogenetics for dysplasia and clonal change","Skeletal imaging for metaphyseal dysplasia","Fat-soluble vitamin levels and nutritional assessment"],"redFlags":["New or worsening cytopenias suggesting progression to aplastic anaemia or myelodysplasia","Cytogenetic clonal abnormality or blasts indicating leukaemic transformation","Severe or recurrent infection during neutropenia","Failure to thrive from uncontrolled malabsorption"],"prognosis":"With enzyme replacement and infection management many patients do well in childhood, but the lifelong risk of marrow failure, myelodysplasia and acute myeloid leukaemia dominates long-term prognosis and mandates surveillance; haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is used for marrow failure or malignant transformation.","pitfalls":["Attributing the malabsorption to cystic fibrosis without genetic and biochemical confirmation","Being falsely reassured when pancreatic function improves with age while marrow risk persists","Missing intermittent neutropenia on a single normal blood count","Neglecting regular marrow and cytogenetic surveillance for clonal evolution"],"management":["Pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy alongside fat-soluble vitamin supplementation to correct malabsorption and steatorrhoea","Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor for symptomatic or severe neutropenia and prompt broad-spectrum antibiotics for febrile episodes","Regular full blood counts and periodic bone marrow aspirate and trephine with cytogenetics to detect aplastic anaemia, myelodysplasia or leukaemic transformation","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for progressive marrow failure or transformation to myelodysplasia or acute myeloid leukaemia","Nutritional assessment with monitoring and correction of fat-soluble vitamin deficiency and of growth","Orthopaedic monitoring and management of metaphyseal dysplasia, short stature and thoracic involvement","Genetic counselling and cascade testing given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (NCBI): Shwachman-Diamond syndrome","OMIM: Shwachman-Diamond syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"sialadenitis":{"name":"Acute sialadenitis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["salivary gland infection","acute suppurative parotitis","sialoadenitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sialadenitis is inflammation of a salivary gland, most commonly affecting the parotid or submandibular gland","Acute bacterial infection typically develops in dehydrated, debilitated or postoperative patients with reduced salivary flow, classically ascending parotitis","It presents with a painful, tender, swollen gland, and gentle massage may express pus from the duct opening in the mouth","Staphylococcus aureus is the usual organism in acute suppurative parotitis, whereas viral parotitis (mumps) is a key non-bacterial cause","Submandibular sialadenitis is often related to a stone (sialolith) obstructing the duct, causing pain and swelling that worsen at mealtimes","General measures such as good hydration, oral hygiene, warm compresses, gland massage and sialogogues promote salivary flow and recovery, alongside appropriate antibiotics for bacterial disease","An enlarging, fluctuant, non-resolving gland may indicate abscess formation needing drainage","Recurrent or chronic sialadenitis warrants imaging to look for stones, strictures or an underlying mass or systemic disease"],"pathophysiology":"Salivary glands are normally protected from ascending infection by continuous salivary flow, which flushes the ducts, and by antibacterial constituents of saliva. When flow is reduced, by dehydration, poor oral intake, debility, medications that dry the mouth, or mechanical obstruction from a duct stone or stricture, oral bacteria ascend the duct and infect the gland, producing acute suppurative sialadenitis with parenchymal inflammation and pus. Stasis of saliva also predisposes to stone formation, and calculi in turn obstruct outflow, causing painful mealtime swelling and recurrent infection. Repeated episodes lead to chronic inflammation, ductal changes and glandular fibrosis. Viral sialadenitis such as mumps causes a non-suppurative inflammatory swelling.","additionalDifferentials":["Mumps and other viral parotitis","Salivary duct calculus without active infection","Salivary gland tumour (benign or malignant)","Sjogren syndrome and other autoimmune sialadenitis","Sarcoidosis or IgG4-related disease of the salivary glands","Reactive cervical lymphadenopathy near the gland","Dental or masticator space infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound of the gland to assess for stones, abscess or a mass","Culture of pus expressed from the duct or obtained at drainage","Plain radiography or CT to detect calculi","Sialography or MR sialography for ductal anatomy in recurrent disease","Blood tests including inflammatory markers and, where relevant, mumps serology and autoimmune screening","Fine needle aspiration or biopsy if a neoplasm is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly spreading swelling, systemic sepsis or airway compromise from a deep neck or floor of mouth extension (an emergency)","A persistent hard gland mass, facial nerve weakness or skin fixation suggesting malignancy","Fluctuant swelling indicating an abscess requiring drainage","Failure to resolve with appropriate treatment, prompting imaging and reassessment","Bilateral gland involvement with systemic features suggesting a systemic or autoimmune cause"],"prognosis":"Acute bacterial sialadenitis usually resolves with rehydration, salivary stimulation and antibiotics, and outcomes are good when the cause is treated; recurrent obstruction from stones or strictures and chronic sialadenitis may require repeated treatment or gland surgery.","pitfalls":["Overlooking dehydration and reduced salivary flow as the precipitant, especially postoperatively","Missing an obstructing calculus in recurrent mealtime swelling","Assuming all salivary swellings are infective and overlooking a tumour","Delaying drainage of an established abscess","Neglecting facial nerve assessment when a hard parotid mass is present"],"management":["Empirical antistaphylococcal antibiotics such as flucloxacillin for acute suppurative bacterial parotitis, later tailored to culture of expressed pus","Rehydration and correction of the reduced salivary flow that predisposes to ascending infection","Stimulation of salivary flow with sialogogues, gland massage, warm compresses and good oral hygiene","Analgesia and anti-inflammatory measures for the painful, tender gland","Ultrasound to identify an abscess or obstructing stone, with needle or surgical drainage of any collection","Supportive care alone for viral parotitis such as mumps, which does not require antibiotics","Treatment of underlying ductal obstruction from stone or stricture and ENT referral for recurrent disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"sialadenosis":{"name":"Sialadenosis (sialosis)","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sialosis","sialadenosis","non-inflammatory salivary gland enlargement"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as bilateral, symmetrical, painless, soft enlargement of the salivary glands, most often the parotids","It is a non-inflammatory, non-neoplastic condition and the glands are typically non-tender with normal overlying skin","Strongly associated with systemic and metabolic disorders, particularly diabetes mellitus, chronic alcohol use, malnutrition, and eating disorders such as bulimia nervosa","Recurrent vomiting in bulimia characteristically produces painless parotid swelling that may be a clue to an otherwise concealed eating disorder","Salivary secretion is often reduced, and diagnosis rests on excluding inflammatory, autoimmune, and neoplastic causes","Imaging shows diffuse, homogeneous gland enlargement without a discrete mass or ductal obstruction","Management is directed at the underlying systemic disorder rather than the gland itself, and the swelling may regress when that condition is controlled"],"pathophysiology":"Sialadenosis is a non-inflammatory, non-neoplastic enlargement of the salivary glands caused by an underlying metabolic or systemic disturbance. It is thought to reflect a peripheral autonomic (sympathetic) neuropathy affecting the secretory acinar cells, leading to dysregulated protein synthesis and secretion. The acinar cells become hypertrophied and engorged with secretory granules, producing diffuse, symmetrical gland enlargement without inflammation or duct obstruction. Recognised associations include diabetes and other endocrine disorders, chronic alcoholism with liver disease, malnutrition, and the repeated vomiting of bulimia nervosa.","additionalDifferentials":["Sjogren syndrome","Chronic or recurrent sialadenitis","IgG4-related salivary gland disease","Sarcoidosis","Salivary gland neoplasm (benign or malignant)","Sialolithiasis with obstructive swelling","Lymphoma involving the salivary glands"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound of the salivary glands to confirm diffuse enlargement and exclude a focal mass or calculus","Screening for diabetes with fasting glucose or HbA1c","Liver function tests and assessment of alcohol intake","Nutritional assessment and evaluation for an eating disorder where relevant","Autoantibody screen (anti-Ro/anti-La) and salivary flow studies to exclude Sjogren syndrome","Serum IgG4 and, if a mass or atypical features are present, fine-needle aspiration or biopsy"],"redFlags":["A discrete, hard, or asymmetrical mass suggesting neoplasm rather than sialadenosis","Facial nerve weakness accompanying parotid enlargement","Rapid unilateral enlargement or associated lymphadenopathy suggesting lymphoma","Systemic features of an occult malignancy or autoimmune disease"],"prognosis":"Sialadenosis itself is benign; the swelling often persists but may regress if the underlying metabolic or systemic condition is treated. The prognosis is largely determined by the associated disorder, and recognising the swelling can prompt diagnosis of a hidden condition such as an eating disorder.","pitfalls":["Mistaking diffuse bilateral swelling for recurrent sialadenitis and giving repeated antibiotics","Overlooking an underlying eating disorder in a patient with painless parotid enlargement","Failing to exclude Sjogren syndrome, lymphoma, or a neoplasm","Attributing gland enlargement solely to the salivary glands without seeking a systemic cause","Proceeding to unnecessary surgery for a benign, systemically driven condition"],"management":["Identification and treatment of the underlying systemic driver: glycaemic control in diabetes, reduction of alcohol intake with liver care, nutritional rehabilitation in malnutrition and management of an eating disorder in bulimia","Reassurance that the swelling is benign and non-neoplastic once inflammatory, autoimmune and neoplastic causes have been excluded","Sialogogues and salivary-flow measures to relieve the reduced secretion and any xerostomia","Referral for psychological or psychiatric care where painless parotid swelling is a clue to a concealed eating disorder","Ultrasound review to confirm diffuse homogeneous enlargement and exclude a developing focal mass","Coordinated involvement of endocrinology, hepatology or addiction services, dietetics and mental health as dictated by the underlying disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"sialic_acid_storage_disease":{"name":"Free sialic acid storage disease","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FSASD","SLC17A5 deficiency","sialin deficiency","Salla disease (milder form)","infantile free sialic acid storage disease (ISSD)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive defect of SLC17A5, which encodes sialin, the lysosomal membrane transporter that exports free sialic acid from the lysosome","Spans a spectrum from severe infantile free sialic acid storage disease (ISSD) to the milder Salla disease common in Finland","Salla disease presents with early hypotonia, delayed development, progressive ataxia, spasticity and intellectual disability with a relatively long survival","Infantile ISSD is severe, with coarse features, hepatosplenomegaly, dysostosis, nephrotic-range proteinuria and hydrops fetalis, and early death","Hypomyelination on brain MRI, including a thin corpus callosum, is a characteristic radiological clue","Diagnosis is supported by markedly increased free (unconjugated) sialic acid in urine and confirmed by SLC17A5 genetic testing","No specific therapy exists; care is supportive with developmental, neurological and, where relevant, renal support"],"pathophysiology":"Sialin is a proton-driven transporter in the lysosomal membrane that carries free sialic acid (N-acetylneuraminic acid) generated by glycoconjugate degradation out of the lysosome into the cytoplasm. When sialin is defective, free sialic acid accumulates within lysosomes across many tissues, which are consequently distended by storage. The nervous system is particularly affected, with disturbed myelination underlying the hypotonia, developmental impairment and progressive motor signs, while severe transporter loss produces the multisystem infantile phenotype with visceromegaly, bone changes and, in the most severe cases, hydrops.","additionalDifferentials":["Sialidosis (neuraminidase deficiency) and galactosialidosis","Other lysosomal storage disorders with coarse features and visceromegaly (e.g. mucopolysaccharidoses, I-cell disease)","Hypomyelinating leukodystrophies including Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease","Other causes of non-immune hydrops fetalis","Congenital disorders of glycosylation","Cerebral palsy or other static encephalopathies in milder presentations"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine free (unconjugated) sialic acid quantification (markedly elevated)","SLC17A5 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Brain MRI demonstrating hypomyelination and a thin corpus callosum","Assessment for proteinuria and renal involvement in infantile disease","Developmental and neurological evaluation to characterise the phenotype","Skin fibroblast free sialic acid measurement where available"],"redFlags":["Non-immune hydrops fetalis or severe multisystem disease in the neonate","Nephrotic-range proteinuria with renal involvement","Progressive neurological regression with spasticity","Feeding difficulty and respiratory compromise in the infantile form"],"prognosis":"Infantile free sialic acid storage disease is severe and usually fatal in early childhood, whereas Salla disease follows a slower course with prolonged survival and predominantly neurological disability; prognosis depends on residual sialin function.","pitfalls":["Not measuring urinary free sialic acid in unexplained hypotonia with hypomyelination","Confusing free sialic acid storage disease with sialidosis, where bound sialic acid metabolism differs","Overlooking renal involvement in the infantile form","Attributing a milder phenotype to cerebral palsy without metabolic evaluation"],"management":["Supportive, symptom-directed care as no disease-modifying therapy exists for the sialin transporter defect","Developmental support with physiotherapy and occupational therapy for hypotonia, ataxia and progressive motor signs, with antispasticity agents such as baclofen for spasticity","Antiepileptic drug treatment of seizures where they develop","Nutritional and feeding support, with monitoring for and management of proteinuria and renal involvement in infantile disease","Perinatal palliative care for the severe infantile form presenting with hydrops fetalis","Coordinated multidisciplinary care across metabolic medicine, neurology, developmental paediatrics and nephrology","Genetic counselling with carrier and prenatal testing given autosomal recessive SLC17A5 inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"sialidosis":{"name":"Sialidosis (mucolipidosis I)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mucolipidosis I","ML I","neuraminidase deficiency","NEU1 deficiency","cherry-red spot myoclonus syndrome (type 1)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Scriver's OMMBID","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by deficiency of lysosomal alpha-N-acetylneuraminidase (sialidase), leading to accumulation of sialylated oligosaccharides and glycopeptides","Type 1 is the milder, later-onset form presenting with progressive myoclonus, visual failure, and a macular cherry-red spot without coarse features","Type 2 is more severe with earlier onset, coarse facies, dysostosis multiplex, organomegaly, and, in the congenital form, hydrops fetalis","The cherry-red spot with myoclonus is a classic clue to type 1 sialidosis","Urinary oligosaccharide screening shows a characteristic pattern of sialylated compounds rather than glycosaminoglycans","Distinct from galactosialidosis, which involves combined deficiency of neuraminidase and beta-galactosidase due to a protective protein defect","Diagnosis is confirmed by deficient neuraminidase activity and NEU1 gene testing"],"pathophysiology":"Sialidosis is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in NEU1, encoding lysosomal alpha-N-acetylneuraminidase (sialidase). This enzyme removes terminal sialic acid residues from sialylated glycoconjugates during their lysosomal degradation. Its deficiency causes accumulation of sialyloligosaccharides and sialylated glycopeptides within lysosomes and their excretion in the urine. The severity spectrum reflects residual enzyme activity: type 1 causes a progressive myoclonic epilepsy with visual decline and preserved somatic appearance, while type 2 causes earlier, more generalised storage disease with dysmorphism, skeletal changes, and visceromegaly, including a congenital hydropic form. Neuraminidase normally functions in a complex with beta-galactosidase and the protective protein cathepsin A, and defects in that protective protein instead cause galactosialidosis. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Galactosialidosis (combined neuraminidase and beta-galactosidase deficiency)","GM1 and GM2 gangliosidoses presenting with a cherry-red spot","Other progressive myoclonic epilepsies (e.g. Unverricht-Lundborg, Lafora disease)","Mucopolysaccharidoses in type 2 given coarse features","Metabolic causes of non-immune hydrops fetalis for the congenital form","Niemann-Pick disease and other lysosomal storage disorders with cherry-red spot"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Leucocyte or fibroblast neuraminidase enzyme assay","Urinary oligosaccharide and sialyloligosaccharide analysis","Assay of beta-galactosidase to distinguish sialidosis from galactosialidosis","NEU1 gene sequencing","Ophthalmological examination for a macular cherry-red spot","Neurophysiology (EEG, evoked potentials) to characterise myoclonus in type 1"],"redFlags":["Progressive myoclonus and seizures unresponsive to standard therapy","Rapid visual deterioration","Hydrops fetalis or severe neonatal presentation in the congenital form","Cardiorespiratory decline in type 2 disease"],"prognosis":"Type 1 follows a slowly progressive course with disabling myoclonus and visual loss but relatively preserved somatic health, whereas type 2, especially the congenital form, is severe with high early mortality. No disease-modifying therapy is established, so care is supportive.","pitfalls":["Attributing progressive myoclonic epilepsy to an idiopathic cause and missing the cherry-red spot","Failing to distinguish sialidosis from galactosialidosis by not assaying beta-galactosidase","Screening only for glycosaminoglycans and missing the sialylated oligosaccharide pattern","Overlooking sialidosis in the differential of non-immune hydrops fetalis"],"management":["Supportive care, as no established disease-modifying therapy exists for neuraminidase deficiency","Control of the progressive myoclonus and any seizures in type 1 with antimyoclonic and antiepileptic agents such as valproate, levetiracetam, clonazepam or piracetam, recognising the myoclonus is often drug-resistant","Ophthalmological review and low-vision support for the progressive visual failure and macular cherry-red spot","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and mobility aids for the progressive neurological disability","Management of multisystem storage complications in type 2, with perinatal palliative care for the congenital hydropic form","Coordinated multidisciplinary care across metabolic medicine, neurology and ophthalmology","Genetic counselling with family and prenatal testing given autosomal recessive NEU1 inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"sialoblastoma":{"name":"Sialoblastoma","system":"Head and neck / Salivary glands","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Congenital basal cell adenoma","Embryoma of salivary gland"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare congenital or perinatal salivary neoplasm, arising most often in the parotid and less commonly the submandibular gland.","It presents at birth or in the neonatal period as a firm mass, and may be detected on antenatal imaging.","Histology recapitulates primitive salivary anlage, with basaloid epithelial cells in organoid nests showing peripheral palisading and both ductal and myoepithelial differentiation.","Behaviour is variable and best regarded as of low to intermediate malignant potential, with a real risk of local recurrence and occasional metastasis.","Recognised associations include hepatoblastoma, nevus sebaceus and other congenital anomalies, and molecular studies have identified HRAS, PIK3CA and beta-catenin alterations.","Complete surgical excision with facial nerve preservation where feasible is the mainstay of management.","Chemotherapy has a role for unresectable, incompletely excised or aggressive tumours.","It must be distinguished from other congenital head and neck masses such as teratoma, haemangioma and embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma."],"pathophysiology":"Sialoblastoma is thought to arise from retained primitive (blastematous) salivary gland epithelium that fails to complete normal differentiation, producing a tumour that mirrors the embryonic developing gland. Neoplastic basaloid cells are organised into solid and organoid nests with peripheral palisading and focal ductal lumina, reflecting arrested dual epithelial and myoepithelial differentiation. Molecular studies have implicated activation of growth-signalling pathways, with reported HRAS and PIK3CA mutations and beta-catenin (WNT pathway) alterations driving proliferation of the primitive epithelium. The recognised association with hepatoblastoma and certain cutaneous and congenital anomalies suggests that, in some cases, sialoblastoma forms part of a broader developmental or embryonal tumour predisposition.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital haemangioma","Teratoma","Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma","Basal cell adenoma or adenocarcinoma","Adenoid cystic carcinoma","Neuroblastoma or other small round blue cell tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the head and neck to define extent and relation to the facial nerve","Ultrasound of the mass","Biopsy with histology and immunohistochemistry for ductal and myoepithelial markers","Serum alpha-fetoprotein interpreted in the neonatal context, and to help exclude teratoma","Staging imaging where malignant behaviour is suspected","Screening for associated hepatoblastoma"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth of the mass","Facial nerve weakness","Skin ulceration or fixation","Infiltrative margins or incomplete excision","Evidence of regional or distant metastasis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is variable; complete excision offers a good outcome, but local recurrence is not uncommon and close surveillance is required. Metastasis is infrequent but reported, and outcome is worse with incompletely excised or aggressive tumours.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for a benign basal cell adenoma and undertreating","Underestimating the risk of local recurrence after excision","Achieving incomplete excision, particularly around the facial nerve in a neonate","Overlooking an associated hepatoblastoma"],"management":["Complete surgical excision with preservation of the facial nerve where feasible as the mainstay of treatment","Adjuvant chemotherapy for incompletely resected, recurrent or metastatic tumours","MRI-based mapping of tumour extent and its relation to the facial nerve to guide resection","Histological and immunohistochemical confirmation with ductal and myoepithelial markers","Close post-operative surveillance for local recurrence given the low-to-intermediate malignant potential","Screening for associated hepatoblastoma with serum alpha-fetoprotein and imaging","Management within a multidisciplinary paediatric head-and-neck oncology team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","Gnepp's Diagnostic Surgical Pathology of the Head and Neck","Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"sialolithiasis":{"name":"Sialolithiasis","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Head and Neck","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["salivary gland stones","salivary calculi","salivary duct stone","submandibular calculus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Calculi within the salivary ducts or glands, most commonly affecting the submandibular gland and its duct","Classically causes recurrent, mealtime-related painful swelling of the affected gland as saliva flow is obstructed by eating","The submandibular predilection reflects its more viscous, mineral-rich secretions and the uphill course of Wharton duct","Stones may be palpable along the floor of mouth, and bimanual examination can help localise a distal calculus","Obstruction predisposes to secondary bacterial sialadenitis with a tender, erythematous gland and purulent ductal discharge","Ultrasound is a useful first-line investigation, and sialography or CT can define stones and duct anatomy, while sialendoscopy allows both diagnosis and treatment","Management ranges from conservative measures (hydration, sialogogues, gland massage, warm compresses) to sialendoscopy or duct stone removal, with gland excision reserved for refractory disease","Gland-preserving techniques such as sialendoscopy and transoral stone retrieval have reduced the need for gland removal"],"pathophysiology":"Sialolithiasis develops when calcium phosphate and other salts deposit around an organic nidus within a salivary duct or gland, gradually forming a calculus. Stasis of saliva, changes in its composition, ductal inflammation and dehydration favour stone formation. The submandibular gland is most often affected because its saliva is more viscous and alkaline and its duct runs upward against gravity with a relatively narrow orifice. An obstructing stone impedes salivary outflow, producing the characteristic painful mealtime swelling, and the resulting stasis predisposes to ascending bacterial infection and, over time, chronic gland damage and fibrosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute bacterial or viral sialadenitis without a stone","Salivary gland tumour, benign or malignant","Sjogren syndrome and other autoimmune sialadenitis","Ductal stricture or stenosis","Sialadenosis (non-inflammatory gland enlargement)","Lymphadenopathy adjacent to the gland"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound of the salivary gland and duct as a first-line test","Non-contrast CT to detect and localise calculi","Sialography or MR sialography to define ductal anatomy and strictures","Diagnostic and therapeutic sialendoscopy","Microbiological culture of ductal discharge when infection is present"],"redFlags":["Features of acute suppurative sialadenitis with systemic sepsis or spreading floor-of-mouth infection","A firm, fixed or rapidly enlarging gland mass suggesting neoplasm rather than a stone","Facial nerve weakness, which is not expected with benign stone disease and suggests malignancy","Airway compromise from significant floor-of-mouth swelling or abscess"],"prognosis":"Most cases resolve with conservative measures or minimally invasive gland-preserving stone removal, and outcomes are generally good. Recurrent obstruction and chronic infection can cause irreversible gland damage, occasionally requiring gland excision.","pitfalls":["Assuming all obstructive gland swelling is due to a stone and missing an underlying tumour","Overlooking secondary infection that requires antimicrobial treatment and drainage","Proceeding to gland excision before trying gland-preserving techniques","Failing to recognise multiple or proximal stones that alter the treatment approach"],"management":["Conservative measures to promote spontaneous stone passage, including hydration, sialogogues, gland massage and warm compresses","Gland-preserving stone removal by sialendoscopy, intraoral duct incision or extracorporeal and intracorporeal lithotripsy for larger calculi","Antibiotics for secondary bacterial sialadenitis together with analgesia for pain and swelling","Sialadenectomy reserved for recurrent obstruction or irreversible gland damage","Ultrasound as first-line imaging, with CT or sialography to localise stones and define ductal anatomy for treatment planning","Advice on hydration and salivary stimulation to reduce recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"sialuria":{"name":"Sialuria","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["French type sialuria","sialuria, infantile type (historical distinction)","free sialic acid overproduction","GNE"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / Journal of Inherited Metabolic Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A dominantly inherited disorder of sialic acid overproduction caused by activating (dominant-negative) variants in the allosteric site of GNE","Characterised by massive urinary excretion of free (unconjugated) N-acetylneuraminic acid, the sialic acid intermediate","It differs fundamentally from the sialic acid storage disorders, where the problem is lysosomal accumulation rather than cytoplasmic overproduction","Clinical features are variable and often relatively mild: coarse facies, hepatomegaly, recurrent infections, and variable developmental delay","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating grossly elevated free sialic acid in urine, distinguishing it from bound sialic acid disorders","The same GNE gene, when affected by different loss-of-function variants, causes GNE myopathy, illustrating gene-specific allelic diversity","There is no specific therapy; management is supportive with monitoring of growth and development","Prognosis is generally more favourable than for the severe sialic acid storage diseases"],"pathophysiology":"Sialuria is caused by variants that disrupt the allosteric feedback site of GNE, the bifunctional enzyme UDP-N-acetylglucosamine 2-epimerase/N-acetylmannosamine kinase that catalyses the rate-limiting steps of sialic acid biosynthesis. Normally the end-product, CMP-sialic acid, feeds back to inhibit this enzyme. When the regulatory site is disrupted, feedback inhibition is lost and the cytoplasm overproduces free N-acetylneuraminic acid, which is then excreted in large amounts in the urine and accumulates in tissues. This is distinct from the sialic acid storage disorders, in which a lysosomal transporter defect traps sialic acid within lysosomes. The overproduction phenotype tends to be milder, with variable somatic and developmental features.","additionalDifferentials":["Sialic acid storage disorders (Salla disease, infantile free sialic acid storage disease)","Other lysosomal storage disorders with coarse facies and hepatomegaly","Mucopolysaccharidoses","Congenital disorders of glycosylation","GNE myopathy (allelic but distinct phenotype)","Other causes of hepatomegaly with developmental delay"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative urinary free sialic acid (markedly elevated)","Measurement of bound versus free sialic acid to distinguish storage disorders","GNE molecular genetic testing","Liver function tests and abdominal imaging for hepatomegaly","Developmental assessment","Metabolic screening to exclude alternative storage disorders"],"redFlags":["Significant developmental regression prompting reassessment for an alternative diagnosis","Recurrent serious infections","Progressive hepatomegaly with functional impairment","Failure to thrive"],"prognosis":"Generally more benign than the sialic acid storage disorders, with variable outcome; many affected individuals have relatively mild disease, though developmental delay may occur.","pitfalls":["Confusing sialuria (overproduction, free sialic acid excreted) with sialic acid storage disease (lysosomal accumulation)","Failing to distinguish free from bound sialic acid on testing","Assuming a severe storage-disease prognosis and counselling incorrectly","Overlooking the shared GNE gene basis with the distinct GNE myopathy"],"management":["Supportive, manifestation-directed care as no disease-specific therapy exists for the GNE overproduction defect","Early developmental intervention and educational support for variable developmental delay","Monitoring for and prompt treatment of recurrent infections","Monitoring of hepatomegaly with liver function tests and abdominal imaging","Nutritional and growth surveillance","Coordinated multidisciplinary metabolic and developmental follow-up","Genetic counselling given autosomal dominant inheritance, noting that variants often arise de novo"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Journal of Inherited Metabolic Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"sick_sinus_syndrome":{"name":"Sick sinus syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SSS","sinus node dysfunction","sinoatrial node disease","tachy-brady syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sick sinus syndrome is a spectrum of sinoatrial node dysfunction producing inappropriate bradycardia, sinus pauses or arrest, sinoatrial exit block, and chronotropic incompetence","A common variant is the tachy-brady syndrome, in which paroxysms of atrial tachyarrhythmia (often atrial fibrillation) alternate with symptomatic bradycardia or pauses, especially on termination of the tachyarrhythmia","Symptoms are often intermittent and non-specific, including fatigue, dizziness, presyncope, syncope, and exertional breathlessness from failure of the rate to rise","Because symptoms are paroxysmal, prolonged or ambulatory monitoring is frequently required to correlate them with rhythm disturbances","It is predominantly a disease of older adults from degenerative fibrosis of the sinus node, but drugs, ischaemia, and infiltrative disease can contribute","Reversible contributors such as rate-slowing drugs, hypothyroidism, and electrolyte disturbance should be excluded before attributing symptoms to intrinsic node disease","Permanent pacing is the main treatment for symptomatic bradycardia, and the tachy-brady form often needs both pacing and rhythm or rate control of the tachyarrhythmia","Anticoagulation should be considered when atrial fibrillation coexists, based on thromboembolic risk"],"pathophysiology":"Sick sinus syndrome results from failure of normal impulse generation in the sinoatrial node or failure of conduction from the node to the surrounding atrium. The most common cause is idiopathic degenerative fibrosis of the node and perinodal tissue with ageing. Ischaemia, infiltrative and inflammatory diseases, prior cardiac surgery, and autonomic influences also contribute, and many drugs suppress node function. In the tachy-brady syndrome, atrial remodelling supports tachyarrhythmias, while the diseased node produces prolonged pauses when the tachyarrhythmia stops because of overdrive suppression of an already impaired pacemaker.","additionalDifferentials":["Physiological sinus bradycardia (for example in athletes)","Drug-induced bradycardia from rate-slowing agents","AV block and other conduction disturbances","Hypothyroidism and other metabolic causes of bradycardia","Neurocardiogenic (vasovagal) syncope","Carotid sinus hypersensitivity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG and prolonged or ambulatory monitoring to correlate symptoms with rhythm","Implantable loop recorder when symptoms are infrequent","Review of rate-slowing medications and thyroid function testing","Electrolyte assessment and evaluation for ischaemia when relevant","Exercise testing to assess chronotropic competence","Echocardiography to evaluate structural heart disease"],"redFlags":["Syncope or recurrent presyncope from prolonged sinus pauses or arrest","Very long pauses, particularly following termination of a tachyarrhythmia in tachy-brady syndrome","Haemodynamic compromise from severe bradycardia","Coexisting atrial fibrillation raising thromboembolic and stroke risk"],"prognosis":"Sick sinus syndrome is rarely immediately life-threatening but causes considerable morbidity from recurrent symptoms; pacing effectively relieves bradycardic symptoms, and overall outcome is largely determined by associated atrial fibrillation and thromboembolic risk and by underlying cardiac disease.","pitfalls":["Attributing symptoms to intrinsic node disease without excluding reversible drug or metabolic causes","Failing to capture the arrhythmia because monitoring was too brief for paroxysmal symptoms","Overlooking the need for anticoagulation when atrial fibrillation coexists","Treating bradycardia with pacing while ignoring the tachyarrhythmia component of tachy-brady syndrome"],"management":["Permanent pacemaker implantation, with atrial-based or dual-chamber pacing preferred, as the definitive treatment for symptomatic bradycardia, sinus pauses or chronotropic incompetence","Exclusion and reversal of contributors first, withdrawing rate-slowing drugs, treating hypothyroidism and correcting electrolyte disturbance","Management of the tachy-brady variant with rate or rhythm control of atrial fibrillation, often feasible only once pacing protects against bradycardia","Anticoagulation for thromboembolic risk in associated atrial fibrillation","Ambulatory monitoring or an implantable loop recorder to correlate symptoms with rhythm before committing to device therapy","Treatment of underlying structural or ischaemic heart disease","Escalation to device implantation for recurrent syncope or presyncope attributable to node dysfunction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"sickle_cell_disease":{"name":"Sickle Cell Disease","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sickle cell anemia","hemoglobinopathy"],"chapter":103,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.769-776","clinicalPearls":["Sickle cell disease is a clinical/haematologic phenotype caused by an assortment of genotypes; sickle cell anaemia (HbSS, homozygosity for the HbS Glu6Val mutation) is the most common, followed by HbSC disease (compound heterozygosity for HbS and HbC) and the HbS-beta thalassaemias (p.772).","The chief presenting symptom is pain: a hand-foot syndrome (dactylitis) in young children, and typical acute painful episodes in older children and adults (p.772).","Acute painful episodes are the most common acute events; pain is typically unprovoked, severe, in extremities or torso, often symmetrical and stereotypical for each patient, and lasts days to weeks (p.773).","In HbSC disease and HbS-beta+ thalassaemia, acute vaso-occlusive episodes occur at about half the rate of sickle cell anaemia and complications develop later; rarely such patients are asymptomatic (p.772).","Complications fall into two related groups: those driven by sickle vaso-occlusion (pain, acute chest syndrome, osteonecrosis, splenic infarction) and those driven by intravascular haemolysis (stroke, pulmonary/systemic vasculopathy, nephropathy, leg ulcers, gallstones, priapism); vaso-occlusive complications respond best to HbF induction (p.772-773).","Carriers of sickle cell trait (HbAS) outnumber patients with disease ~25 to 1; trait is usually benign with normal life expectancy, but carries a small risk of sudden death from heat-related exertional rhabdomyolysis and complications such as haematuria, papillary necrosis, hyposthenuria, splenic infarction at altitude and increased VTE risk (p.772, 775).","Autosplenectomy from repeated childhood splenic infarction is usual in sickle cell anaemia; a palpable spleen after age 5 suggests a coexisting genotype (e.g. thalassaemia or HbC) (p.769-770 reference and Table 103-2).","Hydroxyurea (acting mainly by inducing HbF) is standard of care for all patients with sickle cell anaemia and HbS-beta0 thalassaemia regardless of symptoms; in adults it roughly halves pain and acute chest syndrome and reduced mortality by 49% over 17.5 years (p.775).","Cure is possible with HLA-identical related-donor allogeneic stem cell transplant (event-free survival >95%, though only ~15% of patients have a fully matched donor) and with two approved ex vivo autologous gene therapies (lentiviral betaT87Q HbA gene; CRISPR/Cas9 disruption of the BCL11A enhancer) (p.775-776)."],"pathophysiology":"HbS behaves like HbA except that it polymerises when deoxygenated: contacts between beta6 valine residues of deoxy-HbS and beta/alpha-globin residues form hemoglobin fascicles that injure the sickle erythrocyte, after a delay between initiation of polymerisation and accumulation of damaging polymer. Polymerisation depends on roughly the 30th power of hemoglobin concentration, so small changes in concentration or cell dehydration have large effects. Membrane vesiculation/microparticle release, activation of dehydrating channels (Gardos, K/Cl cotransport, Psickle) which raise mean corpuscular HbS concentration and reduce deformability, translocation of phosphatidylserine to the outer membrane leaflet, and oxidation produce irreversibly sickled cells (ISCs) that remain deformed regardless of oxygenation. Sickle erythrocytes adhere to endothelium and other blood cells, occluding small and large vessels (vaso-occlusion), and survive <20 days (normal ~120), haemolysing intra- and extravascularly. Intravascular haemolysis depletes haptoglobin and hemopexin and liberates heme, arginase and other DAMPs which scavenge nitric oxide, activate platelets and endothelium, reduce antioxidant activity, cause vasoconstriction and drive inflammation (p.772-774).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Acute chest syndrome (pneumonia-like illness with chest pain, cough, fever, hypoxia and a pulmonary infiltrate) closely resembles and may coexist with bacterial pneumonia; antibiotics are almost always used even though a causative bacterium is often not cultured (p.775).","Multiorgan failure accompanying severe acute chest syndrome is often confused with, and can coexist with, sepsis (p.774, Table 103-3)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) and hemoglobin electrophoresis to quantify hemoglobin fractions (HbS, HbF, HbA, HbA2, HbC) and distinguish trait, sickle cell anaemia and HbSC disease (Table 103-2, Fig. 103-4, p.772).","Peripheral blood film: irreversibly sickled cells, target cells and nucleated red cells in HbSS/HbS-beta0 thalassaemia; HbC crystals, taco-folded cells and contracted microspherocytes in HbSC disease (Fig. 103-4, p.772-774).","DNA-based diagnosis for genetic counselling and family planning; antenatal diagnosis by chorionic villus sampling or cell-free DNA (p.772, 776).","Transcranial Doppler screening in children (ages 2-16) to identify stroke risk (Table 103-3, p.776).","Echocardiographic measurement of tricuspid regurgitant jet velocity (TRV) annually to screen for pulmonary hypertension.","Screening for microalbuminuria from age 10 for nephropathy, and annual fluorescein angiography from age 10 for retinopathy."],"redFlags":["Acute chest syndrome - chest pain, cough, fever, hypoxia and a pulmonary infiltrate; can be devastating with multilobar involvement, severe hypoxia, ARDS, multiorgan failure and rapid death (often from embolised necrotic marrow) (p.775).","Splenic sequestration (acute anemic episode) - rapid drop in hemoglobin; recurrent episodes are an indication for splenectomy (Table 103-3, p.774).","Aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19 infection causing acute worsening of anaemia with reticulocytopenia (Table 103-3, p.774; reticulocytopenia in acute worsening noted elsewhere).","Stroke and silent infarction - occur in 10-15% of cases, from early childhood into adulthood, with neurocognitive abnormalities even without apparent stroke; haemorrhagic stroke in adults (Table 103-3, p.773).","Functional/anatomic asplenia (autosplenectomy) places patients at high risk of overwhelming infection with encapsulated organisms, notably overwhelming pneumococcal disease (p.776, 769/Table; pneumococcal chapter p.999).","Sudden unexpected death shortly after admission for acute pain (possible arrhythmia or pulmonary embolism); thrombocytopenia, leukocytosis >20,000 and rapidly developing acute anaemia portend severe acute chest syndrome and multiorgan failure (p.773, 775).","Multiorgan failure - can accompany severe acute chest syndrome, affecting CNS, liver, muscle, lung and kidney (Table 103-3, p.774)."],"prognosis":"In the United States childhood death is now rare and the median age of death in sickle cell anaemia is in the fifth or sixth decade; by contrast in high-burden regions such as Nigeria most affected newborns die before age 5. Hydroxyurea reduced mortality by 49% over 17.5 years of follow-up. Genotype modifies severity: HbSC disease and HbS-beta+ thalassaemia have about half the complication rate of HbSS, and coexistent alpha thalassaemia (present in ~30% of HbSS) is associated with fewer strokes and leg ulcers and less pulmonary vascular and renal disease. Allogeneic transplantation and gene therapy offer potential cure (p.772, 775-776).","pitfalls":["No diagnostic test can confirm or refute an acute pain episode; physical examination is often unhelpful and the presence of ISCs and the reticulocyte count are of no diagnostic value (p.773).","Supplemental oxygen is unnecessary unless hypoxia is present, and NSAIDs add little once intravenous opioids are given; overhydration and excessive opioids can compound dyspnoea and hypoxia in acute chest syndrome (p.773, 775).","Opioids should not be dosed on an 'as-needed' schedule - patient-controlled or fixed scheduled dosing with rescue doses is preferred, with monitoring to avoid excessive sedation; many patients are opioid-tolerant and need higher doses (p.773).","Transfusions are both overutilised and underutilised; they should usually be avoided in acute pain episodes and for stable chronic anaemia (p.776).","A palpable spleen after age 5 in sickle cell disease suggests a coexisting genotype rather than simple HbSS (p.769-770)."],"management":["Hydroxycarbamide to induce foetal haemoglobin and reduce vaso-occlusive crises, acute chest syndrome and mortality, as the disease-modifying mainstay","Prompt management of acute painful crises with analgesia including opioids, together with rehydration and oxygenation","Infection prevention with penicillin prophylaxis and pneumococcal and other vaccination given functional hyposplenism, plus urgent treatment of febrile illness","Red-cell transfusion or exchange transfusion for acute chest syndrome, stroke and other severe complications, with a chronic transfusion programme for stroke prevention","Transcranial Doppler screening in children to identify those needing transfusion for primary stroke prevention","Surveillance for chronic complications including retinopathy, pulmonary hypertension via tricuspid regurgitant jet velocity, nephropathy with microalbuminuria screening, priapism and leg ulcers","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation and emerging gene therapy as curative options for severe disease, alongside folic acid supplementation","Genetic counselling with cascade and antenatal screening, and coordinated multidisciplinary specialist care"],"severityClassification":"Severity is largely defined by genotype: sickle cell trait (HbAS, usually benign); sickle cell anaemia (HbSS, vaso-occlusion- and haemolysis-related complications, Hb ~7-10 g/dL); HbS-beta0 thalassaemia (complication rate similar to HbSS); HbS-beta+ thalassaemia (~half the rate of HbSS depending on percent HbA); HbSC disease (near-asymptomatic to as severe as HbSS, ~half the complication rate, increased retinopathy risk); HbSE (resembles HbS-beta+ thalassaemia, often Asian/Indian ancestry); and HbSS-alpha thalassaemia (phenocopies HbS-beta0 thalassaemia with fewer strokes/leg ulcers and less pulmonary/renal disease). HbF level (influenced by HbS haplotype) modifies severity (p.772).","references":["Harrison 22e p.769-776"],"crossLinks":["pneumococcal_disease"]},"siderosis":{"name":"Siderosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["welder's lung","arc welder's pneumoconiosis","iron oxide pneumoconiosis","pulmonary siderosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Occupational and Environmental Respiratory Medicine reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from inhalation of iron oxide fume or dust, classically in electric-arc welders, oxy-acetylene cutters, and foundry workers","Pure siderosis is generally a benign, non-fibrotic deposition disease with little or no functional impairment","Radiographs may show widespread fine nodular or reticular opacities that can be striking despite few symptoms","Imaging changes may partially regress after exposure ceases, reflecting clearance of iron-laden macrophages","Iron oxide deposits accumulate as macrophage-laden macules around small airways and vessels without significant collagen deposition","Mixed exposures, especially to silica (siderosilicosis), can convert a benign picture into a fibrotic one","A clear welding or metalworking history is essential, as the radiographic burden may otherwise prompt unnecessary invasive workup"],"pathophysiology":"Fine iron oxide particles generated during welding and metal cutting are inhaled and deposited in the lung, where alveolar macrophages engulf them and accumulate as pigmented aggregates around respiratory bronchioles and perivascular spaces. Because iron oxide is relatively inert and provokes minimal fibrogenic response, pure siderosis produces macule deposition without meaningful interstitial fibrosis, and the process is largely benign and potentially partly reversible. When welding fume also contains silica or other fibrogenic dusts, a mixed pneumoconiosis with genuine fibrosis (siderosilicosis) can develop, and welders additionally face increased risks of airways disease and metal fume fever.","additionalDifferentials":["Silicosis or mixed-dust fibrosis (siderosilicosis)","Sarcoidosis","Miliary tuberculosis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Pulmonary haemosiderosis","Other nodular pneumoconioses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph and high-resolution CT to characterise nodular or reticular opacities","Detailed welding and metalworking occupational history","Spirometry and diffusing capacity to confirm preserved function in pure disease","Bronchoalveolar lavage showing iron-laden macrophages where sampling is undertaken","Assessment for coexisting silica exposure","Serial imaging to observe partial regression after exposure ends"],"redFlags":["Progressive breathlessness or fibrosis suggesting mixed-dust disease rather than pure siderosis","New mass or cavitation raising concern for malignancy or tuberculosis","Significant functional decline inconsistent with benign iron deposition","Systemic symptoms prompting search for an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Pure siderosis is benign, usually asymptomatic, and may partially clear after exposure ceases; prognosis worsens only when there is concurrent silica exposure producing fibrosis or coexisting airways disease.","pitfalls":["Assuming impressive radiographic nodularity implies severe functional disease","Failing to identify coexisting silica exposure that changes prognosis","Proceeding to invasive biopsy when a clear welding history and benign course suffice","Overlooking welding-related airflow obstruction or metal fume fever as separate entities"],"management":["Removal from further iron oxide fume and dust exposure, with workplace controls such as local exhaust ventilation and respiratory protection","Reassurance that pure siderosis is benign and imaging may partly regress after exposure ceases, so no specific drug therapy is required","Smoking cessation support to limit additional airways disease","Serial chest imaging and spirometry with diffusing capacity to confirm preserved function and detect any evolving fibrosis","Assessment for coexisting silica exposure that could cause a fibrotic siderosilicosis","Occupational health referral and reporting as an occupational lung disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Parkes' Occupational Lung Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"sigmoid_volvulus":{"name":"Sigmoid volvulus","system":"GI / Colorectal / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["volvulus of the sigmoid colon","sigmoid colon volvulus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with the features of large bowel obstruction: gross abdominal distension, colicky pain, absolute constipation, and often relatively little vomiting early on","Most common in elderly, institutionalised, or neuropsychiatric patients and those with chronic constipation and a long redundant sigmoid loop on a narrow mesenteric base","The abdominal radiograph classically shows a hugely dilated loop arising from the pelvis, described as a coffee-bean or inverted-U sign pointing toward the right upper quadrant","In the absence of peritonitis or bowel compromise, initial treatment is endoscopic decompression (flexible sigmoidoscopy or rigid scope) with insertion of a flatus tube","Successful decompression relieves the acute episode but recurrence is common, so elective sigmoid resection is often advised in fit patients","Signs of peritonitis, failed decompression, or suspicion of gangrenous or perforated bowel mandate emergency laparotomy","At emergency operation for compromised bowel, resection (commonly a Hartmann's procedure) is performed rather than simple untwisting","A CT scan confirms the diagnosis, shows the whirl sign of the twisted mesentery, and assesses for ischaemia or perforation"],"pathophysiology":"Sigmoid volvulus occurs when a redundant, elongated sigmoid loop twists around its narrow mesenteric pedicle, producing a closed-loop large bowel obstruction. Chronic constipation and a lengthy, mobile sigmoid on a narrow mesenteric base predispose to torsion. The twist obstructs the lumen at both ends, and progressive distension raises intraluminal and intramural pressure. If the mesenteric vessels are compromised by the torsion, the loop becomes ischaemic, progressing to gangrene and perforation with faeculent peritonitis and sepsis. Massive gaseous distension of the closed loop is characteristic.","additionalDifferentials":["Caecal volvulus","Obstructing colorectal carcinoma","Pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie's syndrome)","Diverticular stricture with obstruction","Faecal impaction with megacolon","Adhesional or hernia-related large bowel obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain abdominal radiograph for the characteristic dilated sigmoid loop","CT abdomen and pelvis to confirm the diagnosis, show the whirl sign, and assess for ischaemia or perforation","Bloods including full blood count, electrolytes, and lactate to gauge ischaemia and fitness for intervention","Endoscopic assessment of mucosal viability during attempted decompression"],"redFlags":["Peritonitis, systemic sepsis, or shock suggesting ischaemic or perforated bowel (surgical emergency)","Bloodstained or necrotic mucosa or dark fluid at decompression indicating gangrene","Failure of endoscopic decompression","Rising lactate, worsening distension, or free gas on imaging"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated cases usually respond to endoscopic decompression but recur frequently unless definitive surgery is performed. Emergency surgery for gangrenous or perforated bowel in typically frail, elderly patients carries substantial morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Persisting with endoscopic decompression when peritonitis or ischaemia is present","Mistaking the presentation for simple constipation and delaying imaging","Failing to offer elective resection to fit patients after decompression, allowing recurrence","Overlooking an obstructing carcinoma as an alternative cause of distal large bowel obstruction"],"management":["Endoscopic decompression by flexible sigmoidoscopy or rigid scope with insertion of a flatus tube as first-line treatment in the absence of peritonitis or gangrene","Fluid resuscitation, correction of electrolyte disturbance and nasogastric decompression as supportive measures","Inspection of mucosal viability during endoscopy, abandoning decompression and proceeding to surgery if ischaemic mucosa is seen","Emergency laparotomy with resection, typically a Hartmann's procedure, for peritonitis, failed decompression or gangrenous or perforated bowel","Elective sigmoid resection after successful decompression in fit patients to prevent frequent recurrence","Monitoring for recurrence and for signs of bowel compromise following decompression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"sik1_developmental_epileptic_encephalopathy":{"name":"SIK1 developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SIK1","SIK1-related epilepsy","salt-inducible kinase 1 encephalopathy","EIEE30"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / ILAE","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in SIK1, which encodes salt-inducible kinase 1, a serine/threonine kinase that regulates gene transcription, including activity-dependent programmes important for neuronal function","Presents as a developmental and epileptic encephalopathy, with reported onsets ranging from the neonatal period through early infancy","The phenotype includes early myoclonic encephalopathy, Ohtahara-like presentations, and epileptic spasms in some children, reflecting a severe early epileptic picture","Seizures are frequently drug-resistant and accompanied by an abnormal, often discontinuous or suppression-burst, electroencephalogram in early cases","Development is typically markedly impaired, with hypotonia, poor visual and social responsiveness, and limited motor progress","The kinase acts downstream of neuronal activity, so disease reflects disrupted signal-dependent transcription rather than a direct ion-channel defect, and broad-spectrum antiseizure agents are generally used","SIK1 should be considered among the genetic causes of early infantile epileptic encephalopathy on gene-panel or exome testing"],"pathophysiology":"SIK1 encodes salt-inducible kinase 1, a member of the AMP-activated protein kinase family that phosphorylates regulators of gene transcription, including CREB-regulated transcriptional coactivators. In neurons this kinase links electrical activity to activity-dependent gene expression that supports synaptic function and development. Pathogenic variants disturb this signalling, altering the transcriptional response to neuronal activity and impairing normal circuit maturation. The resulting dysfunction produces both the severe early epilepsy and the profound developmental impairment, with the mechanism residing in disrupted intracellular signalling rather than direct membrane excitability changes.","additionalDifferentials":["STXBP1 and KCNQ2 neonatal encephalopathies","Early myoclonic encephalopathy of metabolic cause","Ohtahara syndrome from structural or other genetic causes","CDKL5 deficiency disorder","SCN2A and SCN8A developmental and epileptic encephalopathies","Other early infantile epileptic encephalopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Epilepsy gene panel or whole-exome sequencing to identify the SIK1 variant","Electroencephalography to detect suppression-burst, hypsarrhythmia, or other patterns","Brain magnetic resonance imaging to exclude structural causes","Metabolic screen to exclude treatable early myoclonic encephalopathy mimics","Developmental and visual assessment","Trial of vitamin-responsive treatments while awaiting genetic results"],"redFlags":["Suppression-burst electroencephalography with refractory neonatal seizures","Evolution to epileptic spasms with hypsarrhythmia","Developmental arrest with poor visual and social engagement","Status epilepticus"],"prognosis":"Most affected children have severe developmental impairment with drug-resistant epilepsy, although seizure frequency and the electroencephalographic picture may evolve over time.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because it is a kinase rather than a channel gene","Not trialling vitamin-responsive therapies before genetic confirmation in early encephalopathy","Overlooking metabolic mimics of early myoclonic encephalopathy","Assuming a static picture when the electroencephalogram and seizures evolve"],"management":["Broad-spectrum antiseizure medications (such as valproate, levetiracetam, topiramate and benzodiazepines) form the mainstay, since disrupted activity-dependent transcription rather than a channel target means no lesion-specific drug exists","Empirical trial of vitamin-responsive treatments (pyridoxine, pyridoxal phosphate, folinic acid) while awaiting genetic and metabolic results to capture treatable early-myoclonic-encephalopathy mimics","Add-on therapy for epileptic spasms (corticotropin or corticosteroids, or vigabatrin) and consideration of the ketogenic diet in drug-resistant seizures","Supportive developmental care with physiotherapy, occupational and feeding support, and management of hypotonia and visual impairment","Rescue benzodiazepine and a status-epilepticus escalation plan for acute prolonged or clustered seizures","Serial electroencephalography and developmental review to track evolution of the suppression-burst or hypsarrhythmic pattern and guide treatment","Coordinated care through paediatric neurology and epileptology, developmental paediatrics and clinical genetics","Genetic counselling for the family once the SIK1 variant is confirmed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews and OMIM resources on SIK1-related epileptic encephalopathy","ILAE resources on early infantile developmental and epileptic encephalopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"silent_corticotroph_adenoma":{"name":"Silent corticotroph adenoma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["silent ACTH adenoma","silent corticotroph pituitary tumour","non-functioning corticotroph adenoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard endocrinology reference texts and pituitary tumour consensus guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A pituitary adenoma of corticotroph lineage that stains for ACTH but does not cause clinical Cushing syndrome, so it behaves clinically like a non-functioning tumour","Diagnosis is usually made only on immunohistochemistry after surgery performed for mass effect, since preoperative biochemistry shows no overt cortisol excess","Presentation is typically with headache, visual field defects and hypopituitarism from a macroadenoma","These tumours are recognised as more aggressive than most non-functioning adenomas, with higher rates of cavernous sinus invasion, regrowth and recurrence","A minority can evolve to become clinically functioning and cause overt Cushing disease over time, so cortisol status needs ongoing review","Subtypes are described (including a densely and a sparsely granulated pattern), with the latter often behaving more aggressively","Because there is no hormone excess to target medically, close imaging surveillance and consideration of adjuvant radiotherapy for residual tumour are important"],"pathophysiology":"A silent corticotroph adenoma arises from pituitary corticotroph cells and expresses pro-opiomelanocortin and ACTH on immunostaining, yet fails to produce clinically significant hypercortisolism. This apparent silence is attributed to impaired processing or release of biologically active ACTH, so that despite the corticotroph phenotype the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis is not clinically overactive. Clinical effects therefore stem from tumour bulk, causing optic chiasm compression, hypopituitarism and headache. The corticotroph lineage confers a more invasive and recurrence-prone behaviour than typical gonadotroph non-functioning adenomas, and dedifferentiation can occasionally lead to later hormone activation and overt Cushing disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Clinically non-functioning gonadotroph pituitary adenoma","Overt corticotroph adenoma causing Cushing disease","Prolactinoma with stalk effect","Craniopharyngioma or other sellar mass","Pituitary carcinoma (rare)","Hypophysitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pituitary MRI to assess size, invasion and suprasellar extension","Postoperative immunohistochemistry for ACTH and corticotroph transcription factors to establish lineage","Screening for subclinical or emerging cortisol excess (e.g. late-night salivary cortisol, dexamethasone suppression, 24-hour urinary free cortisol)","Full anterior pituitary hormone profile to detect hypopituitarism","Formal visual field testing when the chiasm is threatened","Serial imaging surveillance for regrowth given the higher recurrence risk"],"redFlags":["Rapid tumour regrowth or cavernous sinus invasion on follow-up imaging","Development of clinical or biochemical Cushing syndrome indicating transformation to a functioning tumour","Progressive visual field loss from chiasmal compression","Pituitary apoplexy with acute headache and ophthalmoplegia"],"prognosis":"These tumours carry a higher risk of invasion, incomplete resection and recurrence than most non-functioning adenomas, so long-term outcomes are less favourable and lifelong surveillance is essential; a subset progresses to overt Cushing disease, and residual disease may need radiotherapy.","pitfalls":["Assuming a non-functioning adenoma will behave indolently and under-monitoring for recurrence","Failing to reassess cortisol status over time and missing evolution into Cushing disease","Not obtaining corticotroph immunostaining, thereby misclassifying the tumour","Overlooking hypopituitarism, particularly cortisol deficiency, in the perioperative period"],"management":["Transsphenoidal surgical resection is the mainstay, relieving mass effect on the optic chiasm and providing lineage-defining histology","Hormone replacement for any hypopituitarism (glucocorticoid, levothyroxine, sex steroids and growth hormone as indicated), with perioperative steroid cover","Radiotherapy, including stereotactic radiosurgery, for invasive, residual or recurrent tumour given the aggressive corticotroph behaviour","Long-term surveillance with serial pituitary MRI for regrowth and periodic biochemical screening for emerging cortisol excess and overt Cushing disease","Formal visual field monitoring while the chiasm is threatened","Coordinated pituitary multidisciplinary care with endocrinology, neurosurgery, radiation oncology and neuro-ophthalmology","Escalation to temozolomide for aggressive or progressive tumours behaving as pituitary carcinoma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","WHO classification and consensus guidance on pituitary adenomas"],"crossLinks":[]},"silent_sinus_syndrome":{"name":"Silent sinus syndrome","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Laryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic maxillary atelectasis","imploding antrum syndrome","maxillary sinus atelectasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with painless, progressive enophthalmos and hypoglobus with facial asymmetry, in the absence of significant sinonasal symptoms","Results from gradual collapse of the maxillary sinus and downward bowing of the orbital floor rather than from a mass or trauma","The 'silent' label reflects the characteristic lack of sinusitis symptoms despite the underlying maxillary pathology","Ophthalmological features such as apparent enophthalmos, upper lid sulcus deepening, and lid retraction often bring the patient to attention first","CT is diagnostic, showing an opacified, contracted maxillary sinus with inward retraction of its walls and a depressed orbital floor","The initiating problem is occlusion of the osteomeatal complex producing negative intrasinus pressure and chronic atelectasis","Management combines endoscopic sinus surgery to restore maxillary ventilation with orbital floor reconstruction where enophthalmos is significant"],"pathophysiology":"Silent sinus syndrome, also termed chronic maxillary atelectasis, begins with obstruction of the maxillary sinus outflow at the osteomeatal complex. Trapped secretions are gradually resorbed and, with ventilation cut off, a persistent negative pressure develops within the sealed sinus. Over time this sub-atmospheric pressure draws the thin sinus walls inwards, so the antrum contracts and the orbital floor bows downward. Because the orbital floor descends and the maxillary volume falls, the orbital contents sink, producing the painless enophthalmos and hypoglobus that dominate the clinical picture, all typically without the pain or discharge of overt sinusitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Orbital floor blow-out fracture with enophthalmos","Involutional or lipoatrophic enophthalmos","Maxillary or sinonasal neoplasm causing orbital displacement","Chronic rhinosinusitis with orbital involvement","Congenital facial or orbital asymmetry","Metastatic scirrhous tumour causing enophthalmos"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT of the sinuses and orbits, the key investigation demonstrating maxillary atelectasis and orbital floor depression","Nasal endoscopy to assess the middle meatus and osteomeatal complex","Ophthalmological assessment with measurement of enophthalmos and globe position","MRI where a neoplastic or soft-tissue cause needs exclusion","Comparison with old photographs to establish the acquired, progressive nature"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive enophthalmos or proptosis suggesting a neoplastic process rather than atelectasis","Diplopia or restricted eye movements indicating significant orbital involvement","Visual disturbance requiring urgent ophthalmological assessment","Pain or destructive bony changes on imaging that are atypical and point to another diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and, once diagnosed, generally treated successfully by restoring maxillary ventilation, with orbital floor reconstruction correcting the enophthalmos; the cosmetic and functional outlook is good, although established orbital changes may need dedicated repair.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the painless enophthalmos for an old blow-out fracture and missing the maxillary atelectasis","Attributing facial asymmetry to a neoplasm and subjecting the patient to unnecessary investigation, or conversely overlooking a genuine tumour","Failing to reconstruct a significantly depressed orbital floor after ventilating the sinus","Not recognising the diagnosis because sinonasal symptoms are conspicuously absent"],"management":["Endoscopic uncinectomy and middle-meatal maxillary antrostomy to re-establish maxillary sinus ventilation is the definitive treatment","Orbital floor reconstruction with an implant or graft to correct the enophthalmos and hypoglobus, often staged after ventilation is restored","Treatment of contributing osteomeatal obstruction such as polyps or mucosal disease to prevent recurrent atelectasis","Joint otolaryngology and oculoplastic or ophthalmology management to combine sinus ventilation with orbital correction","Follow-up of globe position, diplopia and facial symmetry with clinical assessment and imaging","Reassurance regarding the benign, non-inflammatory nature of the condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"silent_thyroiditis":{"name":"Silent thyroiditis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["painless thyroiditis","subacute lymphocytic thyroiditis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrinology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A painless autoimmune destructive thyroiditis causing transient thyrotoxicosis followed by hypothyroidism and usually recovery","Unlike subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis it is painless, and unlike Graves disease there is no ophthalmopathy","Radioactive iodine uptake is low during the thyrotoxic phase, distinguishing it from Graves disease","It is considered part of the autoimmune thyroid disease spectrum and is related to postpartum thyroiditis","The thyrotoxic phase is managed symptomatically with beta-blockade rather than antithyroid drugs","A minority develop permanent hypothyroidism, so follow-up thyroid function testing is needed"],"pathophysiology":"Silent thyroiditis is an autoimmune lymphocytic inflammation of the thyroid that transiently destroys follicular cells, releasing preformed thyroid hormone and causing a self-limited thyrotoxic phase. As stored hormone is depleted and the gland recovers, a hypothyroid phase often follows before euthyroidism is usually restored. Because the process is destructive rather than hyperfunctioning, radioactive iodine uptake is low, and antithyroid drugs are ineffective. It shares its autoimmune basis and biphasic course with postpartum thyroiditis.","additionalDifferentials":["Graves disease","Subacute (de Quervain) granulomatous thyroiditis","Postpartum thyroiditis","Amiodarone-induced thyroiditis","Factitious thyrotoxicosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thyroid function tests through the phases","Thyroid radioactive iodine uptake (low in the thyrotoxic phase)","Thyroid autoantibodies","Inflammatory markers (usually normal, unlike subacute thyroiditis)","Follow-up thyroid function to detect permanent hypothyroidism"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting Graves disease (ophthalmopathy) needing different management","Severe thyrotoxic symptoms","Persistent hypothyroidism","Diagnostic uncertainty with amiodarone exposure"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover normal thyroid function within months, though some develop permanent hypothyroidism and recurrence can occur.","pitfalls":["Treating the thyrotoxic phase with antithyroid drugs, which do not work in destructive thyroiditis","Misdiagnosing it as Graves disease and giving radioactive iodine","Failing to monitor for permanent hypothyroidism","Overlooking the link with the postpartum period"],"management":["Symptomatic beta-blockade (such as propranolol) for adrenergic features of the thyrotoxic phase, since the process is destructive and antithyroid drugs are ineffective","Levothyroxine replacement during a symptomatic or prolonged hypothyroid phase, with a planned attempt at withdrawal once recovery is likely","Avoidance of radioiodine and thyroidectomy, given the self-limiting course and low radioiodine uptake","Serial thyroid function testing through the thyrotoxic, hypothyroid and recovery phases to track evolution and detect permanent hypothyroidism","Surveillance for recurrence, particularly in the postpartum setting given the shared autoimmune basis","Reassurance regarding the usually self-limiting nature of the illness"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Endocrinology reference texts (standard reference)","Thyroid disease guidelines (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"silicosis":{"name":"Silicosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["silicosis","silica pneumoconiosis","quartz dust lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Silicosis is a fibrotic lung disease caused by inhaling crystalline silica dust, seen in mining, quarrying, sandblasting, stone-cutting, and engineered-stone work","Chronic silicosis develops after prolonged exposure and may be asymptomatic initially, later causing exertional dyspnoea and cough","Accelerated and acute (silicoproteinosis) forms follow intense exposure and progress far more rapidly","Chest imaging classically shows small upper-zone nodular opacities, sometimes with eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes","Simple nodular disease can coalesce into progressive massive fibrosis, producing large conglomerate masses and significant impairment","Silica exposure substantially increases susceptibility to tuberculosis (silicotuberculosis), which should be actively sought and treated","There is no treatment that reverses fibrosis; management focuses on stopping exposure, supportive care, and screening for and treating complications","Silica is also associated with increased risks of lung cancer, chronic airflow obstruction, and certain autoimmune diseases"],"pathophysiology":"Inhaled crystalline silica particles reaching the alveoli are taken up by macrophages. The reactive silica surface is toxic to these macrophages, triggering activation of inflammasome pathways, cell death, and release of inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species. This provokes recruitment of further inflammatory cells and stimulation of fibroblasts, which lay down collagen to form the characteristic silicotic nodules. Continued or intense exposure sustains the cycle, with nodules enlarging and coalescing into progressive massive fibrosis, while impaired macrophage function increases vulnerability to mycobacterial infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Other pneumoconioses such as coal workers' pneumoconiosis","Pulmonary tuberculosis (which may coexist)","Sarcoidosis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and other interstitial lung diseases","Metastatic or miliary nodular lung disease","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography with attention to upper-zone nodularity and eggshell nodal calcification","High-resolution CT to characterise nodules and detect progressive massive fibrosis","Pulmonary function testing to quantify restrictive, obstructive, or mixed impairment","Screening for tuberculosis given the heightened risk","Detailed occupational exposure history including engineered-stone and sandblasting work"],"redFlags":["New constitutional symptoms, haemoptysis, or cavitation suggesting tuberculosis or malignancy","Rapidly progressive breathlessness indicating accelerated or acute silicosis","Enlarging conglomerate masses consistent with progressive massive fibrosis","Development of respiratory failure or cor pulmonale"],"prognosis":"Chronic simple silicosis may remain stable with limited impairment, but progressive massive fibrosis, accelerated, and acute forms carry a poor prognosis with progressive respiratory failure; the lifelong increased risk of tuberculosis and lung cancer means continued surveillance is important, and outcomes improve when exposure ceases early.","pitfalls":["Failing to elicit an occupational silica exposure history, especially newer engineered-stone work","Overlooking coexisting tuberculosis in a patient with silicosis","Attributing all nodular change to silicosis without considering malignancy","Not removing the worker from ongoing dust exposure"],"management":["Immediate and permanent removal from crystalline silica exposure with dust control and respiratory protection, as there is no therapy that reverses the fibrosis","Smoking cessation and vaccination against influenza and pneumococcus to limit respiratory decline and infection","Active screening for and treatment of tuberculosis, including latent infection, given the heightened silicotuberculosis risk","Supportive respiratory care with bronchodilators, pulmonary rehabilitation and supplemental oxygen for hypoxaemia","Serial chest imaging and lung function testing to monitor for progressive massive fibrosis and lung cancer","Referral for lung transplantation assessment in advanced or progressive fibrosis with respiratory failure","Occupational health notification, compensation advice and workplace exposure reduction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"silver_russell_syndrome":{"name":"Silver-Russell syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SRS","Russell-Silver syndrome","imprinting-related growth restriction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An imprinting disorder causing severe pre- and postnatal growth restriction with relative macrocephaly (head sparing) at birth","Characteristic features include a triangular face, broad forehead, body asymmetry (hemihypotrophy), and fifth-finger clinodactyly","Feeding difficulties, marked failure to thrive, and fasting hypoglycaemia due to low muscle and fat mass are common in infancy","Most cases are explained by loss of methylation at the IC1 (H19/IGF2) region on chromosome 11p15 or by maternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 7","It is broadly the molecular mirror image of Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, with reduced rather than increased IGF2-driven growth","The Netchine-Harbison clinical scoring system guides diagnosis and directs molecular testing when features are suggestive","Management centres on optimising nutrition and growth, monitoring for hypoglycaemia, and endocrine input including growth-promoting therapy where indicated"],"pathophysiology":"Silver-Russell syndrome is an imprinting disorder most often caused by hypomethylation of the paternally derived IC1 domain at chromosome 11p15, which reduces expression of the growth-promoting gene IGF2, or by maternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 7. Reduced IGF2-driven growth signalling produces profound intrauterine and postnatal growth restriction with relative sparing of head growth. Because the affected loci are imprinted, the parental origin of the epigenetic or chromosomal change determines the phenotype, and body asymmetry reflects mosaic distribution of the molecular defect.","additionalDifferentials":["Constitutional small-for-gestational-age without catch-up growth","Temple syndrome and other imprinting disorders","3-M syndrome and other primordial short-stature syndromes","IMAGe syndrome","Fetal alcohol spectrum disorder","Mulibrey nanism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Methylation analysis of the 11p15 IC1 region and testing for maternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 7","Chromosomal microarray to exclude copy-number causes of growth restriction","Blood glucose monitoring for fasting hypoglycaemia in infancy","Growth monitoring with assessment of body asymmetry and limb-length discrepancy","Endocrine evaluation including growth hormone axis assessment","Nutritional and feeding assessment for failure to thrive"],"redFlags":["Fasting hypoglycaemia risking neurological harm, especially during intercurrent illness","Severe failure to thrive requiring intensive nutritional support","Significant limb-length discrepancy affecting gait and posture","Signs of relative intracranial disproportion or raised pressure requiring assessment"],"prognosis":"With early nutritional optimisation, hypoglycaemia prevention, and endocrine management, growth and general health improve, though final stature is often below average. Cognition is usually normal or near normal, and adult outcomes are generally favourable with appropriate support.","pitfalls":["Attributing severe growth restriction to placental insufficiency alone and not testing for imprinting defects","Missing fasting hypoglycaemia because of the child's small size and low reserves","Overlooking body asymmetry, which is a useful diagnostic clue","Failing to recognise the mirror-image relationship with Beckwith-Wiedemann when interpreting 11p15 results"],"management":["Nutritional rehabilitation with feeding support and enteral feeding where needed to address failure to thrive, avoiding rapid catch-up adiposity","Prevention and treatment of fasting hypoglycaemia through regular feeds, uncooked cornstarch and an emergency regimen during illness, given the low muscle and fat mass","Recombinant growth hormone therapy to improve growth velocity, body composition and final stature","Orthopaedic management of body asymmetry and limb-length discrepancy with shoe raise or surgery, plus monitoring for and treatment of early or precocious puberty with a gonadotropin-releasing hormone analogue","Coordinated care through clinical genetics, paediatric endocrinology, dietetics, gastroenterology and orthopaedics","Genetic counselling with molecular confirmation of 11p15 hypomethylation or maternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 7, and discussion of recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","International consensus statement on the diagnosis and management of Silver-Russell syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"silver_syndrome_spg17":{"name":"Silver syndrome (SPG17, BSCL2)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPG17","Silver syndrome","BSCL2-related spastic paraplegia with amyotrophy","seipin-related motor neuronopathy","locus 11q12.3"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant complicated hereditary spastic paraplegia in which lower-limb spasticity is combined with amyotrophy of the small hand muscles","Caused by heterozygous variants in BSCL2, which encodes seipin, an endoplasmic reticulum protein involved in lipid-droplet biology","The wasting characteristically begins in the thenar eminence and first dorsal interosseous, giving prominent intrinsic-hand-muscle atrophy","Marked intrafamilial variability is typical, with some carriers showing predominantly spasticity and others predominantly distal wasting","BSCL2 is allelic with distal hereditary motor neuropathy type V and with Charcot-Marie-Tooth type 2, and different BSCL2 variants cause congenital generalised lipodystrophy","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography confirm a motor axonopathy affecting the hands and feet","Care is symptomatic, combining physiotherapy, spasticity control and hand-function support with occupational therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Silver syndrome results from dominant variants in BSCL2, the gene for seipin, an integral endoplasmic reticulum membrane protein important for lipid-droplet formation and endoplasmic reticulum homeostasis. The classical Silver syndrome variants (such as N88S and S90L) are thought to disturb glycosylation and cause misfolded seipin to accumulate, triggering endoplasmic reticulum stress and a toxic gain of function in motor neurons. This preferentially injures the longest corticospinal tracts and distal motor axons supplying the hands and feet, producing the distinctive combination of lower-limb spasticity with intrinsic-hand-muscle amyotrophy. Separate loss-of-function BSCL2 variants instead cause a lipodystrophy phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Distal hereditary motor neuropathy type V (allelic BSCL2 disease)","Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 2","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis with mixed upper and lower motor neurone signs","SPG10 (KIF5A) complicated hereditary spastic paraplegia with amyotrophy","Cervical spondylotic myeloradiculopathy","Hirayama disease and other focal motor neuronopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["BSCL2 sequencing within a hereditary spastic paraplegia or inherited neuropathy panel","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to define the motor axonopathy","MRI of brain and cervical spine to exclude compressive and demyelinating causes","Detailed family history given wide phenotypic variability","Assessment of hand function and grip for rehabilitation planning","Screening for metabolic features to distinguish lipodystrophy-associated BSCL2 variants"],"redFlags":["Bulbar symptoms or brisk spread of weakness suggesting motor neurone disease","A cervical sensory level or radicular pain pointing to compressive myeloradiculopathy","Rapidly progressive respiratory or swallowing difficulty","Severe distal weakness threatening loss of independent hand function"],"prognosis":"Progression is usually slow, and although hand-muscle wasting can cause meaningful disability, many affected people retain ambulation and useful function for prolonged periods; severity varies considerably even within a family.","pitfalls":["Attributing intrinsic-hand-muscle wasting to a local entrapment neuropathy rather than the underlying genetic disorder","Confusing the neuropathic BSCL2 phenotype with the unrelated lipodystrophy caused by different BSCL2 variants","Assuming a pure hereditary spastic paraplegia when subtle distal amyotrophy is present","Underestimating variability and reassuring at-risk relatives inappropriately"],"management":["Antispasticity therapy for the lower-limb spasticity with agents such as baclofen or tizanidine, and focal botulinum toxin injection for targeted muscle overactivity","Physiotherapy and stretching programmes to maintain gait and prevent contractures, with occupational therapy and adaptive aids for the intrinsic-hand-muscle wasting","Ankle-foot orthoses and mobility aids to preserve ambulation and independence","Foot care and orthopaedic management of secondary deformity","Nerve conduction studies, electromyography and functional assessments to characterise the motor axonopathy and monitor progression","Coordinated neurology and rehabilitation input including physiotherapy and occupational therapy","Genetic counselling for the autosomal dominant BSCL2 variant, noting marked intrafamilial variability and the allelic neuropathy and lipodystrophy phenotypes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: BSCL2-Related Neurologic Disorders / Seipinopathy","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"simpson_golabi_behmel_syndrome":{"name":"Simpson-Golabi-Behmel syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SGBS","GPC3-related overgrowth","Simpson dysmorphia syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked overgrowth syndrome affecting predominantly males, with pre- and postnatal macrosomia and organomegaly","Coarse facial features include a broad face, hypertelorism, a broad nasal bridge, macrostomia, and a midline groove or furrow of the tongue and lower lip","Associated malformations include congenital heart disease, diaphragmatic hernia, polydactyly, supernumerary nipples, and genitourinary anomalies","Caused by loss-of-function variants or deletions in GPC3 (with GPC4 involvement in some) on the X chromosome","X-linked recessive inheritance, so carrier females are usually mildly affected or unaffected while males are more severely affected","Carries an increased risk of embryonal tumours, particularly Wilms tumour and hepatoblastoma, warranting tumour surveillance in childhood","Overlaps clinically with Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome but is distinguished by X-linked inheritance and the specific facial and skeletal features"],"pathophysiology":"Simpson-Golabi-Behmel syndrome arises from loss of function of GPC3, an X-linked gene encoding glypican-3, a cell-surface heparan sulfate proteoglycan that modulates growth factor signalling and helps restrain cell proliferation during development, in part by regulating hedgehog and IGF-related pathways. Absent or reduced glypican-3 removes this brake on tissue growth, producing generalised overgrowth, organomegaly, and a predisposition to embryonal tumours, while disturbed developmental signalling accounts for the structural malformations and characteristic facies.","additionalDifferentials":["Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","Sotos syndrome","Weaver syndrome","Perlman syndrome","PTEN hamartoma tumour syndrome","Bannayan-Riley-Ruvalcaba syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing for GPC3 sequence variants and deletions, with GPC4 analysis and overgrowth panel where indicated","Chromosomal microarray to detect Xq26 deletions","Abdominal ultrasound as part of embryonal tumour surveillance","Serum alpha-fetoprotein monitoring for hepatoblastoma surveillance in early childhood","Echocardiography and renal tract imaging for structural anomalies","Skeletal survey and evaluation for diaphragmatic hernia and polydactyly"],"redFlags":["New abdominal mass, haematuria, or rising alpha-fetoprotein suggesting Wilms tumour or hepatoblastoma","Respiratory distress from diaphragmatic hernia in the neonatal period","Cardiac decompensation from structural heart disease","Neonatal hypoglycaemia or arrhythmia requiring urgent management"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the severity of malformations and tumour occurrence; severe cases may present with neonatal complications, while milder cases have a favourable outlook with appropriate surveillance. Early detection of embryonal tumours through structured screening improves outcomes.","pitfalls":["Confusing the syndrome with Beckwith-Wiedemann and thereby overlooking X-linked inheritance and carrier risk to relatives","Omitting embryonal tumour surveillance in a child with overgrowth and organomegaly","Underestimating disease in carrier females who may show subtle features","Missing a diaphragmatic hernia or cardiac lesion in the newborn assessment"],"management":["Structured embryonal tumour surveillance in childhood with regular abdominal ultrasound for Wilms tumour and hepatoblastoma and serial serum alpha-fetoprotein monitoring","Surgical and medical correction of associated malformations including congenital heart disease, diaphragmatic hernia, polydactyly and genitourinary anomalies","Neonatal support for macrosomia-related complications, airway and feeding difficulties and hypoglycaemia","Developmental support and monitoring with appropriate therapies","Coordinated care through clinical genetics, cardiology, paediatric surgery, nephrology and oncology","Genetic counselling for X-linked GPC3 disease, with carrier testing of females and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Simpson-Golabi-Behmel Syndrome Type 1"],"crossLinks":[]},"sinding_larsen_johansson_syndrome":{"name":"Sinding-Larsen-Johansson syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sinding-Larsen-Johansson disease","inferior patellar pole apophysitis","jumper's knee of the growing patella","patellar apophysitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A traction apophysitis at the inferior pole of the patella rather than a true osteonecrosis","Affected site is the lower pole of the patella at the origin of the proximal patellar tendon","Typically affects active children and young adolescents, roughly ten to fourteen years of age","Presents with anterior knee pain and tenderness localised to the inferior patellar pole, aggravated by running, jumping and stair climbing","The radiographic hallmark is fragmentation or calcification at the inferior pole of the patella","It is essentially the patellar counterpart of Osgood-Schlatter disease, occurring at the opposite (proximal) end of the extensor mechanism","Pain is reproduced by resisted knee extension and by palpation of the distal patellar pole","Management is conservative with activity modification, extensor mechanism stretching and strengthening, and physiotherapy as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Sinding-Larsen-Johansson syndrome is a traction apophysitis affecting the inferior pole of the patella, where the proximal fibres of the patellar tendon attach to the still-developing lower patellar margin. Repetitive, forceful contraction of the quadriceps during jumping and running transmits tension through the patellar tendon to this immature bone-tendon junction. The repeated microtrauma produces small avulsive injuries, inflammation and reactive calcification or fragmentation at the inferior patellar pole. It is analogous to Osgood-Schlatter disease but occurs at the proximal rather than the distal attachment of the patellar tendon, and it typically resolves as the patella matures and the traction stresses of rapid growth subside.","additionalDifferentials":["Osgood-Schlatter disease at the tibial tubercle","Patellar tendinopathy (jumper's knee)","Patellofemoral pain syndrome","Bipartite patella","Patellar sleeve or inferior pole avulsion fracture","Prepatellar or infrapatellar bursitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination localising tenderness to the inferior patellar pole","Lateral knee radiographs showing fragmentation or calcification at the lower patellar pole","Ultrasound to assess the proximal patellar tendon and inferior pole","Magnetic resonance imaging in atypical or persistent cases","Assessment of quadriceps and hamstring flexibility and lower limb alignment","Comparison with the contralateral knee to distinguish from a bipartite patella"],"redFlags":["Acute severe pain with an extensor lag suggesting a patellar sleeve or avulsion fracture","Rest pain, night pain or systemic features raising concern for infection or malignancy","A joint effusion or mechanical locking pointing to intra-articular pathology","Failure to improve with conservative measures prompting reassessment of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is favourable; symptoms are self-limiting and typically resolve as the patella matures and traction loads decrease, with activity modification and rehabilitation controlling pain and no significant long-term sequelae.","pitfalls":["Confusing the condition with Osgood-Schlatter disease at the opposite end of the extensor mechanism","Mistaking a bipartite patella or an avulsion fracture for the apophysitis","Overlooking a patellar sleeve fracture in a child with acute severe anterior knee pain","Failing to correct extensor mechanism tightness that perpetuates the traction injury"],"management":["Activity modification with relative rest from jumping and running to offload the inferior patellar pole, the mainstay given the self-limiting course","Simple analgesia or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and local ice for pain control","Physiotherapy with quadriceps and hamstring stretching and progressive strengthening, correcting inflexibility of the extensor mechanism","Patellar tendon strap or taping and a graded return-to-sport programme","Reassurance that symptoms resolve as the patella matures and traction loads decline","Reassessment with imaging in atypical or persistent cases to exclude other anterior knee pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"single_organ_vasculitis":{"name":"Single-organ vasculitis","system":"Multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Single-organ vasculitis","Isolated vasculitis","Localised vasculitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kelley and Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Single-organ vasculitis denotes vasculitis confined to one organ or organ system without features of a systemic vasculitis, a category formalised in the current consensus nomenclature.","Recognised examples include isolated cutaneous small-vessel vasculitis, isolated aortitis, single-organ vasculitis of the gastrointestinal tract, testis, breast or urinary bladder, and isolated peripheral nerve vasculitis.","It is fundamentally a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring a thorough search for systemic disease before the 'single-organ' label is applied.","The distribution may be unifocal or multifocal within the affected organ, but by definition does not cross into other systems.","A subset of apparently localised disease later declares itself as systemic vasculitis, so ongoing surveillance is essential.","Histology (leukocytoclastic, granulomatous or necrotising) reflects the vessel size involved and helps frame differentials.","Management is often more conservative than systemic vasculitis; some cutaneous forms are self-limiting, while an isolated organ lesion such as focal aortitis may be treated surgically.","Isolated aortitis is frequently discovered incidentally in surgical aortic specimens and warrants assessment for large-vessel disease."],"pathophysiology":"Single-organ vasculitis is inflammation of blood vessel walls restricted to a single organ or anatomical region, without the systemic distribution or serological markers of a defined systemic vasculitis. The vessel calibre involved varies by site and determines the histological pattern, ranging from leukocytoclastic small-vessel injury in isolated cutaneous disease to granulomatous or necrotising inflammation in isolated aortitis or nerve vasculitis. The initiating stimulus is usually unknown and may be immune-complex-mediated, cell-mediated or triggered by local factors. Because the same histology can be the first manifestation of a systemic process, single-organ vasculitis is defined partly by the absence of evidence for wider involvement over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic vasculitis with early or limited organ involvement","ANCA-associated vasculitis","Vasculitis secondary to connective tissue disease","Infection-related vasculitis","Malignancy-associated or paraneoplastic vasculitis","Drug-induced vasculitis","Non-inflammatory vasculopathy mimicking vasculitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Tissue biopsy of the affected organ to characterise vessel size and inflammation","ANCA, ANA and complement to screen for systemic and autoimmune disease","Inflammatory markers and full systemic review of symptoms","Cross-sectional or vascular imaging appropriate to the organ involved","Screening for infective triggers including relevant serology","Age-appropriate malignancy screening where clinically indicated","Longitudinal follow-up to detect later systemic conversion"],"redFlags":["Emergence of constitutional symptoms or new organ involvement suggesting systemic conversion","Progressive organ dysfunction despite treatment","Aortic dilatation or dissection in isolated aortitis","Rapidly evolving mononeuropathy in isolated nerve vasculitis","Positive ANCA or other systemic serology on follow-up"],"prognosis":"Many single-organ forms have a favourable outlook, particularly self-limiting cutaneous disease, but prognosis depends on the organ affected and on whether the process later becomes systemic. Isolated aortitis and isolated nerve vasculitis carry organ-specific risks that require targeted follow-up.","pitfalls":["Prematurely labelling disease as single-organ without excluding systemic vasculitis.","Failing to arrange follow-up, so later systemic conversion is missed.","Over-treating benign self-limiting cutaneous vasculitis with aggressive immunosuppression.","Overlooking incidental aortitis in surgical specimens and its implications for large-vessel disease.","Assuming a single negative serology permanently excludes systemic disease."],"management":["Confirm and characterise the vasculitis with organ biopsy and exclude systemic disease before treating, since it is a diagnosis of exclusion","For isolated cutaneous small-vessel disease, remove or treat triggers such as drugs and infection with supportive leg elevation and compression, reserving a short corticosteroid course, colchicine or dapsone for persistent disease","For organ-threatening forms such as isolated aortitis or peripheral nerve vasculitis, corticosteroids with a steroid-sparing agent (methotrexate or azathioprine) or systemic-vasculitis induction regimens","Organ-directed measures such as surgical repair and surveillance imaging for isolated aortitis, and neuropathic pain control and rehabilitation for nerve vasculitis","Long-term surveillance with clinical review, serology and inflammatory markers for later progression to systemic vasculitis","Coordinated rheumatology and organ-specialist input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kelley and Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology, current edition","Revised international consensus nomenclature of the vasculitides","Oxford Textbook of Medicine, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"singleton_merten_syndrome":{"name":"Singleton-Merten syndrome","system":"Immunology / Autoinflammatory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IFIH1-associated interferonopathy","DDX58-associated interferonopathy","IFIH1","DDX58"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Autoinflammation / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A type I interferonopathy caused by gain-of-function mutations in IFIH1 (MDA5) or DDX58 (RIG-I), cytosolic nucleic-acid sensors","Hallmarks are abnormal dental development with early tooth loss, aortic and vascular calcification, and skeletal abnormalities","Cardiovascular calcification of the aorta and heart valves can occur unusually early and cause serious complications","Dental features include delayed or abnormal dentition, dysplastic teeth, and premature loss of teeth","Skeletal manifestations may include osteopenia, acro-osteolysis, and widened medullary cavities","A raised interferon signature supports the diagnosis alongside genetic confirmation","JAK inhibition targeting the interferon pathway is being used to address the underlying interferon-driven inflammation"],"pathophysiology":"Singleton-Merten syndrome is caused by gain-of-function mutations in IFIH1 (encoding MDA5) or DDX58 (encoding RIG-I), cytosolic sensors of viral nucleic acids. The mutant sensors are inappropriately activated, driving constitutive production of type I interferons even without genuine infection. Chronic interferon signalling promotes ectopic calcification of the aorta and cardiac structures, disturbs tooth and bone development, and causes systemic inflammatory features. The interferon-dominant mechanism links the dental, cardiovascular, and skeletal manifestations and provides the rationale for JAK inhibition to reduce interferon signalling.","additionalDifferentials":["Other type I interferonopathies (Aicardi-Goutieres syndrome, SAVI, CANDLE)","Generalised arterial calcification of infancy","Idiopathic basal ganglia calcification syndromes","Progeria and premature ageing syndromes","Ectodermal dysplasias affecting dentition","Metabolic bone disease of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for IFIH1 (MDA5) and DDX58 (RIG-I) mutations","Interferon signature in blood","Cardiovascular imaging for aortic and valvular calcification","Dental and orthopantomogram assessment of dentition","Skeletal radiographs for osteopenia and acro-osteolysis","Echocardiography to assess cardiac structure and function"],"redFlags":["Progressive aortic or valvular calcification threatening cardiac function","Early cardiovascular compromise in a child or young adult","Significant skeletal fragility or deformity","Neurological involvement suggesting broader interferonopathy"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is largely determined by cardiovascular calcification, which can cause serious morbidity; interferon-directed therapy may modify disease, but established calcification and dental loss are difficult to reverse.","pitfalls":["Overlooking early vascular calcification as an incidental or age-related finding","Attributing dental abnormalities to a primary ectodermal disorder alone","Not requesting an interferon signature when an interferonopathy is possible","Missing the genetic link between IFIH1/DDX58 variants and the multisystem phenotype"],"management":["JAK inhibitor therapy (such as baricitinib or ruxolitinib) to dampen the type I interferon signalling that drives the disease","Cardiovascular management of aortic and valvular calcification with antihypertensives, heart failure therapy and surgical or valve intervention when calcification is severe","Dental care for dysplastic dentition and early tooth loss, including prostheses and regular dental review","Bone health support with management of osteopenia and acro-osteolysis and physiotherapy","Surveillance with serial echocardiography and vascular imaging plus interferon-signature monitoring for progression","Coordinated care through immunology or rheumatology, cardiology, dentistry and orthopaedics","Genetic counselling and family testing for the IFIH1 or DDX58 gain-of-function variant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Autoinflammation","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"sinonasal_inverted_papilloma":{"name":"Sinonasal inverted papilloma","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Head and Neck","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["inverted papilloma","Schneiderian papilloma, inverted type","inverting papilloma","sinonasal inverting papilloma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign but locally aggressive Schneiderian papilloma that typically arises from the lateral nasal wall and paranasal sinuses","Usually presents with unilateral nasal obstruction, discharge or epistaxis, which distinguishes it from bilateral inflammatory polyps","Any unilateral sinonasal mass or polyp warrants biopsy and imaging because of the risk of neoplasia","Named for its histology, in which the surface epithelium grows inward (inverts) into the underlying stroma","It carries a recognised risk of harbouring or progressing to squamous cell carcinoma, so occult malignancy must be excluded","CT helps define bony involvement and may show focal hyperostosis pointing to the tumour origin, while MRI can reveal a convoluted 'cerebriform' pattern and separate tumour from retained secretions","It has a marked tendency to recur, particularly after incomplete removal, so wide excision of the attachment with drilling of the underlying bone is advocated","Endoscopic resection has become the standard approach for most tumours, with long-term surveillance for recurrence"],"pathophysiology":"Inverted papilloma originates from the Schneiderian (ectodermally derived respiratory) mucosa lining the nasal cavity and paranasal sinuses. The hallmark is proliferation of the epithelium in an endophytic pattern, inverting into the stroma while maintaining an intact basement membrane. Although benign, it erodes and remodels adjacent bone and can extend widely through the sinuses, and it has a well-recognised association with squamous cell carcinoma arising synchronously or over time. Human papillomavirus has been implicated in some tumours, and the site of mucosal attachment is important because residual disease there drives recurrence.","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory nasal polyps","Sinonasal squamous cell carcinoma","Other Schneiderian papillomas (exophytic or oncocytic types)","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma in young males","Olfactory neuroblastoma","Fungal sinusitis or antrochoanal polyp"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasal endoscopy with biopsy of the lesion","CT of the paranasal sinuses to assess bony involvement and identify focal hyperostosis at the origin","MRI to define soft-tissue extent, detect the cerebriform pattern and distinguish tumour from retained secretions","Histopathological examination to confirm the inverted pattern and exclude coexisting malignancy","Imaging surveillance after resection to detect recurrence"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting malignant transformation such as rapid growth, pain, or facial numbness","Orbital symptoms including proptosis, diplopia or visual disturbance","Intracranial extension or skull-base erosion on imaging","Recurrent unilateral epistaxis or a friable, bleeding mass"],"prognosis":"With complete excision including the site of attachment, most benign inverted papillomas are controlled, though recurrence is not uncommon and mandates long-term surveillance. When associated squamous cell carcinoma is present, prognosis depends on the stage and grade of that malignancy.","pitfalls":["Assuming a unilateral nasal polyp is inflammatory and not obtaining histology","Incomplete removal of the mucosal attachment and underlying bone, leading to recurrence","Overlooking a focus of squamous cell carcinoma within the specimen","Mistaking retained sinus secretions for tumour on imaging without MRI correlation"],"management":["Complete surgical excision, usually endoscopic, encompassing the mucosal attachment site with drilling of the underlying bone to minimise recurrence","Histopathological examination of the whole specimen to exclude synchronous squamous cell carcinoma","Escalation to oncological management appropriate to stage, including wider resection and adjuvant radiotherapy, where malignant transformation is present","Long-term endoscopic and imaging surveillance to detect recurrence at the attachment site","Coordinated otolaryngology and head-and-neck care, with skull-base team input when the tumour extends intracranially or into the orbit"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"sinonasal_undifferentiated_carcinoma":{"name":"sinonasal undifferentiated carcinoma","system":"Head and neck","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SNUC","sinonasal anaplastic carcinoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Highly aggressive, poorly differentiated epithelial malignancy of the sinonasal tract that is distinct from squamous cell and neuroendocrine carcinomas.","Typically presents with a short history and rapidly progressive nasal obstruction, epistaxis, facial pain, proptosis, diplopia and cranial neuropathies.","Usually bulky at diagnosis with extensive invasion of the orbit, skull base and anterior cranial fossa.","It is largely a diagnosis of exclusion requiring immunohistochemistry to rule out NUT carcinoma, SMARCB1-deficient carcinoma, neuroendocrine carcinoma, lymphoma, melanoma and olfactory neuroblastoma.","Tumour cells are pancytokeratin positive, typically negative or only focally positive for neuroendocrine markers, and show no squamous or glandular differentiation.","Retained INI-1 (SMARCB1) helps separate it from SMARCB1-deficient sinonasal carcinoma, and NUT immunostaining excludes NUT carcinoma; an IDH2-mutant subset is increasingly recognised.","Management is multimodal, often induction chemotherapy followed by chemoradiotherapy or surgery guided by treatment response and resectability.","Local recurrence and distant metastasis are common, so response-adapted therapy and close surveillance are central to care."],"pathophysiology":"Sinonasal undifferentiated carcinoma is a carcinoma of uncertain histogenesis thought to arise from the Schneiderian mucosa or olfactory epithelium of the nasal cavity and paranasal sinuses. It is composed of sheets, nests and trabeculae of medium-to-large cells with high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratios, prominent nucleoli, brisk mitotic activity, frequent tumour necrosis and lymphovascular invasion, and by definition lacks squamous or glandular differentiation. The entity is molecularly heterogeneous, and recognition of related but distinct tumours such as IDH2-mutant carcinoma, NUT carcinoma and SMARCB1-deficient carcinoma has refined its definition as a diagnosis of exclusion.","additionalDifferentials":["High-grade esthesioneuroblastoma (olfactory neuroblastoma)","Sinonasal neuroendocrine or small cell carcinoma","NUT carcinoma","SMARCB1 (INI-1)-deficient sinonasal carcinoma","Poorly differentiated or non-keratinising squamous cell carcinoma","Sinonasal lymphoma (extranodal NK/T-cell type)","Mucosal melanoma or rhabdomyosarcoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Endoscopic biopsy with a broad immunohistochemistry panel (pancytokeratin, p40/p63, synaptophysin, chromogranin, NUT, INI-1/SMARCB1, S100, CD45, HMB-45)","Contrast-enhanced MRI of the sinuses and brain to map dural, orbital and intracranial extension","CT of the sinuses to define bony destruction and skull-base involvement","Whole-body PET-CT for distant staging","Molecular testing (IDH2 mutation, NUT rearrangement) to subtype the tumour","Ophthalmology and neurosurgical assessment where orbital or intracranial invasion is present","Serum LDH and lymphoma work-up when lymphoma remains in the differential"],"redFlags":["Rapid tumour growth over weeks with new cranial nerve palsies","Orbital apex or cavernous sinus involvement with visual loss","Intracranial extension with headache and altered mental state","Midface or cheek numbness indicating perineural or skull-base spread","Sudden severe epistaxis from a friable bulky mass"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally poor, with a strong tendency to local recurrence and distant metastasis. Multimodality treatment and a good response to induction chemotherapy are associated with better outcomes, but overall long-term survival remains low.","pitfalls":["Labelling a tumour as SNUC without first excluding NUT carcinoma and SMARCB1-deficient carcinoma, which carry different management and prognosis","Under-staging the true intracranial and orbital extent of disease","Managing the tumour as ordinary squamous cell carcinoma","Attributing early nonspecific symptoms to chronic sinusitis and delaying biopsy","Omitting distant staging before committing to an aggressive local treatment plan"],"management":["Multimodality treatment planned upfront, typically beginning with platinum-based induction chemotherapy to assess tumour responsiveness","Response-adapted consolidation with concurrent chemoradiotherapy for responders, or craniofacial or endoscopic skull-base resection with adjuvant chemoradiotherapy in selected resectable cases","Management of orbital and intracranial extension in conjunction with ophthalmology and neurosurgery, including reconstruction","Supportive oncological care with nutritional support, pain control and management of treatment toxicity","Surveillance for local recurrence and distant metastasis with clinical review and cross-sectional imaging","Coordinated head-and-neck oncology multidisciplinary planning of treatment sequencing, with consideration of clinical trials"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Head and Neck Cancers"],"crossLinks":[]},"sinus_barotrauma":{"name":"Sinus barotrauma","system":"Environmental / Emergency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["barosinusitis","aerosinusitis","sinus squeeze"],"chapter":null,"source":"Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine / Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs when a paranasal sinus cannot equalise pressure with the environment because its ostium is obstructed, classically during descent","The frontal and maxillary sinuses are most often affected, producing pain over the forehead, cheek, or around the eye","On descent the trapped sinus air is compressed relative to the surroundings, creating a squeeze that causes mucosal swelling, congestion, and bleeding into the sinus","Epistaxis or blood-tinged nasal discharge on ascent, when the injured mucosa is decompressed, is a common finding","Predisposing factors include upper respiratory infection, allergic rhinitis, nasal polyps, and other causes of ostial obstruction","Management is decongestion, avoiding diving or flying until the sinuses can equalise, and treating any underlying nasal pathology","Prevention centres on not diving or flying with nasal or sinus congestion and controlling rhinitis"],"pathophysiology":"The paranasal sinuses are air-filled cavities that equalise pressure through their ostia into the nasal cavity. When an ostium is blocked by mucosal swelling, polyps, or secretions, the air within the sinus cannot equalise with rising ambient pressure on descent. The resulting relative negative pressure within the sinus draws fluid and blood from the mucosa into the cavity and causes mucosal engorgement and haemorrhage, producing pain and bleeding. On ascent, expanding trapped air or dislodged material can cause further discomfort and epistaxis.","additionalDifferentials":["Ear barotrauma","Acute or chronic sinusitis","Dental barotrauma (barodontalgia)","Migraine or tension headache","Trigeminal neuralgia","Facial pain from temporomandibular joint dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment correlating facial pain and epistaxis with the dive or flight profile","Anterior rhinoscopy or nasendoscopy to identify ostial obstruction, polyps, or inflammation","Otoscopy to assess for coexisting ear barotrauma","Sinus imaging (CT) in recurrent, severe, or diagnostically unclear cases","Assessment for underlying rhinitis or structural nasal abnormality"],"redFlags":["Severe or persistent headache with neurological or visual symptoms","Features suggesting orbital or intracranial extension of infection","Recurrent barotrauma indicating significant untreated nasal or sinus disease","Signs of secondary bacterial sinusitis"],"prognosis":"Sinus barotrauma usually settles within days to a couple of weeks with decongestion and avoidance of pressure changes; recurrence is likely if underlying nasal or sinus disease is not addressed, but serious complications are uncommon.","pitfalls":["Continuing to dive or fly with nasal congestion and provoking recurrence","Overlooking coexisting ear barotrauma","Failing to identify and treat underlying rhinitis, polyps, or structural obstruction","Missing secondary bacterial sinusitis after the injury"],"management":["Topical and systemic decongestants to relieve ostial obstruction and restore sinus ventilation, the mainstay of acute treatment","Analgesia for facial pain, with intranasal corticosteroids and antihistamines where rhinitis or allergy contributes","Avoidance of diving and flying until the sinuses can equalise, with instruction on equalisation technique","Treatment of underlying nasal pathology such as rhinitis, polyps or structural obstruction, including endoscopic surgery for recurrent or refractory cases","Antibiotics reserved for secondary bacterial sinusitis","Otolaryngology referral and follow-up for recurrent or severe episodes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine","Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"sinus_of_valsalva_aneurysm":{"name":"Sinus of Valsalva aneurysm","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["aortic sinus aneurysm","SOVA","aneurysm of the sinus of Valsalva","Valsalva sinus aneurysm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease / ESC guidelines on aortic disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An abnormal dilatation of one of the aortic sinuses, most often the right coronary sinus, arising from congenital weakness of the aortic wall at the sinus or from acquired causes","Unruptured aneurysms are frequently asymptomatic and found incidentally, though a large one may compress adjacent structures or distort the aortic valve","Rupture is the feared complication and usually drains into a low-pressure right-sided chamber, most commonly the right ventricle or right atrium, creating a left-to-right shunt","Acute rupture can present with sudden chest pain, breathlessness, and a continuous murmur, and may precipitate heart failure","A continuous murmur from a ruptured aneurysm can be mistaken for a patent ductus arteriosus or a coronary arteriovenous fistula","It is associated with connective tissue disorders, bicuspid aortic valve, and ventricular septal defect, and may follow infective endocarditis or trauma","Echocardiography, particularly transoesophageal, together with CT or MRI and cardiac catheterisation define the anatomy and any shunt","Symptomatic, ruptured, or enlarging aneurysms are treated by surgical or, in selected cases, transcatheter repair"],"pathophysiology":"A sinus of Valsalva aneurysm results from a deficiency in the elastic and muscular tissue where the aortic media meets the annulus fibrosus, allowing the affected aortic sinus to bulge. The weakness may be congenital or acquired through infection such as endocarditis, connective tissue disease, atherosclerosis, or trauma. Over time the thin-walled aneurysm enlarges under aortic pressure and may compress neighbouring structures or interfere with the aortic valve and coronary ostia. If the wall gives way, blood jets from the high-pressure aorta into an adjacent lower-pressure chamber, most often on the right side of the heart, establishing a left-to-right shunt with volume overload and, if large, acute heart failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Patent ductus arteriosus","Coronary arteriovenous fistula","Ventricular septal defect with aortic regurgitation","Ruptured aortic dissection into a cardiac chamber","Aortic root aneurysm from other causes","Coronary artery aneurysm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic and transoesophageal echocardiography with Doppler to define the aneurysm and detect a shunt","CT or MRI angiography of the aortic root to delineate anatomy and relationships","Cardiac catheterisation to quantify shunt and assess coronary anatomy before intervention","ECG and chest radiograph as initial assessment for chamber enlargement or pulmonary plethora","Evaluation for associated lesions such as bicuspid valve, ventricular septal defect, and connective tissue disease","Blood cultures where infective endocarditis is suspected as a cause"],"redFlags":["Acute rupture with chest pain, breathlessness, and a new continuous murmur","Rapid onset of heart failure from a large left-to-right shunt","Compression of coronary ostia causing ischaemia","Conduction disturbance from an aneurysm involving the interventricular septum"],"prognosis":"Unruptured aneurysms may remain stable for years, but rupture carries significant risk and, if untreated, can lead to progressive heart failure; surgical or transcatheter repair generally has good outcomes when performed promptly.","pitfalls":["Attributing a continuous murmur to a patent ductus arteriosus rather than a ruptured aneurysm","Missing an incidental unruptured aneurysm that warrants surveillance","Overlooking associated lesions such as ventricular septal defect or bicuspid aortic valve","Delaying repair once rupture or significant enlargement is evident"],"management":["Surgical repair with patch closure of the defect and aortic root repair, adding aortic valve repair or replacement where needed, is the definitive treatment for ruptured or symptomatic aneurysms","Transcatheter device closure as an alternative for selected ruptured aneurysms with suitable anatomy","Stabilisation of acute rupture with diuretics and afterload reduction for heart failure while expediting repair","Prompt treatment of infective endocarditis where implicated, with attention to endocarditis prevention","Surveillance of unruptured aneurysms with serial echocardiography and cross-sectional imaging for enlargement or valve or coronary compromise","Evaluation and management of associated lesions such as bicuspid aortic valve, ventricular septal defect and connective tissue disease","Coordinated cardiology and cardiac surgery input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC guidelines on the diagnosis and treatment of aortic diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"sinus_pericranii":{"name":"Sinus pericranii","system":"Vascular anomalies / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SP","pericranial sinus"],"chapter":null,"source":"ISSVA classification / Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An abnormal venous communication connecting the intracranial dural venous sinuses to extracranial scalp veins through a defect in the skull","Presents as a soft, bluish, compressible scalp swelling, often near the midline over the frontal or parietal region","The lesion characteristically enlarges with manoeuvres that raise venous pressure (Valsalva, bending forward, crying) and empties on compression or when upright","It can be congenital or acquired following trauma, and may be isolated or associated with other venous or developmental anomalies","Cross-sectional imaging with venous phase (CT venography or MRI/MRV) demonstrates the transcalvarial venous connection","A key management concern is whether the lesion is dominant for intracranial venous drainage, which affects safety of intervention","Complications include cosmetic concern, bleeding with trauma, and rarely air embolism or infection","Management is individualised, ranging from observation to surgical or endovascular treatment after defining venous drainage"],"pathophysiology":"Sinus pericranii is a venous anomaly in which a direct, valveless communication forms between the intracranial dural venous sinuses and the extracranial veins of the scalp, passing through one or more defects (emissary channels) in the skull. Blood flows bidirectionally through this connection so that the extracranial venous pouch fills when intracranial venous pressure rises and empties with compression or upright posture. The anomaly may be developmental or arise after trauma, and its clinical significance hinges on whether it merely drains scalp veins or also carries important intracranial venous outflow.","additionalDifferentials":["Scalp venous malformation","Dermoid or epidermoid cyst of the scalp","Cephalohaematoma or subgaleal collection","Encephalocele or meningocele","Eosinophilic granuloma or other lytic skull lesion","Aneurysmal bone cyst of the calvarium"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI with MR venography to characterise the venous anatomy","CT and CT venography to define the calvarial defect and transosseous channel","Catheter venography when detailed drainage assessment is needed before intervention","Doppler ultrasound to demonstrate compressibility and venous flow","Assessment of whether the sinus is dominant for intracranial drainage","Skull radiographs may show the bony defect"],"redFlags":["Evidence that the lesion provides dominant intracranial venous drainage, raising risk with intervention","Significant bleeding after minor trauma to the swelling","Signs of air embolism or intracranial complication","Associated developmental venous anomalies affecting cerebral drainage","Rapid change in size or new neurological symptoms"],"prognosis":"Many lesions are stable and can be observed with a good prognosis; when treatment is indicated for cosmetic, symptomatic or safety reasons, outcomes are generally favourable provided the venous drainage pattern is defined beforehand to avoid compromising intracranial outflow.","pitfalls":["Intervening without first establishing whether the sinus is dominant for intracranial venous drainage","Mistaking it for a simple cyst or cephalohaematoma and attempting incision","Overlooking the diagnostic positional and Valsalva-related size change","Failing to consider associated venous or developmental anomalies"],"management":["Define the venous drainage pattern, in particular whether the lesion is dominant for intracranial outflow, before any intervention, as this is the pivotal management decision","Observation of asymptomatic, non-dominant lesions given the generally benign course","Surgical excision with ligation of the transcalvarial connection, or endovascular embolisation, for cosmetic, symptomatic or safety reasons once the lesion is shown not to be dominant","Avoidance of any procedure that would compromise intracranial venous outflow if the sinus is dominant","Coordinated vascular anomalies, neurosurgery and interventional neuroradiology input","Imaging surveillance with MR venography for change in size or drainage"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ISSVA Classification of Vascular Anomalies","Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies: Haemangiomas and Malformations"],"crossLinks":[]},"sinusoidal_obstruction_syndrome":{"name":"Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (veno-occlusive disease)","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SOS","hepatic veno-occlusive disease","VOD","hepatic sinusoidal obstruction syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / EBMT-EASL guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically follows myeloablative haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation, but is also caused by pyrrolizidine-alkaloid herbal teas and certain chemotherapeutic and immunotoxin agents","The hallmark triad is tender hepatomegaly, unexplained weight gain with fluid retention/ascites, and hyperbilirubinaemia arising in the early weeks after conditioning","Diagnosis is largely clinical using established criteria (modified Seattle/Baltimore or paediatric EBMT), supported by imaging rather than a single confirmatory test","Doppler ultrasound may show hepatomegaly, ascites, gallbladder-wall oedema and reversed or absent portal venous flow, though early findings can be non-specific","A raised hepatic venous pressure gradient and characteristic transjugular biopsy help in uncertain or severe cases when the risk is justified","Defibrotide is used for severe SOS, alongside meticulous fluid and sodium management and avoidance of further hepatotoxins","Severity is graded (mild to very severe) by the degree of bilirubin rise, weight gain, and multi-organ dysfunction, which guides urgency of treatment"],"pathophysiology":"The primary injury is to hepatic sinusoidal endothelial cells and downstream zone-3 hepatocytes, damaged by conditioning chemoradiotherapy, pyrrolizidine alkaloids, or other toxins. Endothelial denudation and sloughing obstruct sinusoidal blood flow, and subendothelial oedema with fibrin deposition narrows the terminal hepatic venules. The resulting post-sinusoidal outflow obstruction produces portal hypertension, hepatocellular necrosis and cholestasis, clinically manifest as tender hepatomegaly, weight gain, ascites and jaundice, and in severe disease progressing to hepatorenal and multi-organ failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Graft-versus-host disease of the liver","Drug-induced or conditioning-related hepatotoxicity","Sepsis with cholestasis","Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic vein thrombosis)","Fungal or viral hepatitis in the immunosuppressed host","Capillary leak or engraftment syndrome causing fluid overload"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial bilirubin, transaminases and weight/fluid-balance monitoring against diagnostic criteria","Doppler ultrasound of the liver assessing hepatomegaly, ascites and portal/hepatic venous flow","Full blood count and coagulation studies including markers of endothelial activation","Hepatic venous pressure gradient measurement in selected uncertain cases","Transjugular liver biopsy when the diagnosis remains in doubt and bleeding risk allows","Screening for concurrent infection and graft-versus-host disease"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising bilirubin with renal or respiratory dysfunction indicating very severe multi-organ SOS","Refractory fluid retention and tense ascites with encephalopathy","Progressive hepatomegaly and pain suggesting fulminant hepatic failure","New coagulopathy or falling platelet transfusion increments signalling worsening endothelial injury"],"prognosis":"Mild and moderate SOS often resolves with supportive care, but severe or very severe disease with multi-organ failure carries a high mortality; early recognition and timely use of defibrotide improve outcomes.","pitfalls":["Attributing early weight gain and jaundice to sepsis or graft-versus-host disease and delaying diagnosis","Relying on a single imaging feature, since early Doppler findings are frequently normal or non-specific","Overloading the patient with fluids and sodium, worsening ascites and outflow congestion","Continuing nephrotoxic or hepatotoxic drugs that compound endothelial injury","Forgetting non-transplant causes such as pyrrolizidine-alkaloid ingestion in patients using herbal remedies"],"management":["Defibrotide for severe or very severe disease, the principal disease-directed therapy","Meticulous fluid and sodium restriction with diuretics to control fluid overload and ascites while preserving renal perfusion","Avoidance of further hepatotoxins and careful review of concurrent nephrotoxic and hepatotoxic drugs","Risk-factor mitigation and prophylaxis with ursodeoxycholic acid in high-risk myeloablative transplant recipients","Supportive and organ support including analgesia, paracentesis for tense ascites, and renal replacement or respiratory support in multi-organ failure","Serial bilirubin, weight and fluid-balance monitoring with Doppler ultrasound against diagnostic criteria, and screening for concurrent infection and graft-versus-host disease","Escalation to intensive care and transplant hepatology for severe or multi-organ disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","EBMT/EASL guidance on hepatic sinusoidal obstruction syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"sister_mary_joseph_nodule":{"name":"Sister Mary Joseph nodule","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sister Mary Joseph's nodule","umbilical metastasis","Sister Mary Joseph sign"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A palpable metastatic nodule at the umbilicus, signifying spread from an intra-abdominal or pelvic malignancy","Presents as a firm, sometimes ulcerated or discharging periumbilical mass that may be the first sign of an occult cancer","The commonest primary sites are the stomach, followed by other gastrointestinal tumours (colon, pancreas) and, in women, ovarian and other gynaecological cancers","Named after the surgical assistant who noted the association between umbilical nodules and intra-abdominal malignancy","Its presence indicates advanced, usually disseminated, disease and carries a poor prognosis","Diagnosis is confirmed by biopsy or fine-needle aspiration of the nodule, which can also suggest the primary source","Management is directed at the underlying malignancy and is generally palliative, with treatment guided by tumour type and stage"],"pathophysiology":"The Sister Mary Joseph nodule represents metastatic seeding of the umbilicus from an intra-abdominal or pelvic primary tumour. Malignant cells are thought to reach the umbilicus by several routes, including direct extension through the anterior abdominal wall, lymphatic spread along embryological remnants, haematogenous dissemination, and contiguous spread along the obliterated umbilical vessels and the urachal or falciform ligament remnants. Its appearance therefore reflects transcoelomic or lymphovascular dissemination of an advanced cancer to the umbilical region.","additionalDifferentials":["Umbilical hernia","Endometriosis of the umbilicus","Benign umbilical granuloma or omphalitis","Primary skin tumour of the umbilicus","Epidermoid or sebaceous cyst","Urachal remnant abnormality"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy or fine-needle aspiration of the umbilical nodule for histological and immunohistochemical characterisation","Cross-sectional imaging of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis to identify the primary tumour and stage disease","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and colonoscopy to seek a gastrointestinal primary","Pelvic imaging and gynaecological assessment in women","Tumour markers guided by the suspected primary","Positron emission tomography in selected cases to map disease extent"],"redFlags":["A new firm or ulcerated umbilical nodule in an adult, which mandates urgent evaluation for malignancy","Associated ascites, weight loss, or a palpable abdominal mass indicating disseminated disease","Bleeding or discharge from the nodule","Rapid growth of the lesion"],"prognosis":"The nodule signifies advanced malignancy with peritoneal or systemic dissemination and is associated with a poor prognosis and short median survival, although outcome varies with the primary tumour type and response to systemic therapy.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the nodule for a benign umbilical lesion such as a hernia or granuloma and delaying diagnosis","Biopsying without a systematic search for the primary tumour","Assuming a single tumour type when several intra-abdominal and pelvic primaries are possible","Overlooking the nodule as the presenting sign of an otherwise occult cancer"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying intra-abdominal or pelvic primary malignancy, as the nodule is a metastatic marker and management is directed at the cancer","Confirm with biopsy or fine-needle aspiration and immunohistochemistry to characterise the tumour and identify the primary source","Systemic anticancer therapy, including chemotherapy and targeted agents, appropriate to the primary tumour and stage","Palliative local measures for a symptomatic nodule with ulceration, discharge or pain, including local excision or radiotherapy","Coordinated oncology multidisciplinary care given advanced, often disseminated disease","Early palliative care involvement for symptom control and supportive management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Yamada's Textbook of Gastroenterology"],"crossLinks":[]},"sitosterolaemia":{"name":"Sitosterolaemia","system":"Metabolic / Inborn errors","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Phytosterolaemia","STSL"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive sterol-storage disorder caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in ABCG5 or ABCG8, encoding the heterodimeric sterolin transporter that effluxes plant sterols from enterocytes and hepatocytes.","Hallmark is markedly increased intestinal absorption and reduced biliary excretion of dietary plant sterols (sitosterol, campesterol, stigmasterol) and shellfish sterols, with raised plasma phytosterols.","Tendon and tuberous xanthomata may appear in childhood, sometimes with normal or only modestly raised total cholesterol, an important clue distinguishing it from familial hypercholesterolaemia.","Premature atherosclerosis and early coronary events can occur, occasionally in the absence of substantial hypercholesterolaemia.","Haematological features are characteristic: haemolysis with stomatocytosis, macrothrombocytopenia and abnormal large platelets, sometimes mislabelled as immune thrombocytopenia.","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating grossly elevated plasma plant sterols by gas chromatography or mass spectrometry, confirmed by ABCG5/ABCG8 genetic testing.","Treatment combines a diet low in plant and shellfish sterols with a cholesterol-absorption inhibitor and bile-acid sequestrants; statins are relatively ineffective because cholesterol synthesis is already suppressed.","Consider the diagnosis in any child with xanthomata plus unexplained haemolysis or thrombocytopenia, and in statin-unresponsive apparent familial hypercholesterolaemia."],"pathophysiology":"Sitosterolaemia results from biallelic inactivating variants in either ABCG5 or ABCG8, the two half-transporters that assemble into an obligate heterodimer (sterolin) on the apical membrane of enterocytes and the canalicular membrane of hepatocytes. Normally this pump limits absorption of non-cholesterol sterols and promotes their biliary secretion, so that plant sterols are almost entirely excluded from the body. When the transporter is defective, dietary phytosterols and shellfish-derived sterols are absorbed largely unchecked and are poorly excreted in bile, accumulating in plasma and tissues. Feedback suppression of endogenous cholesterol synthesis lowers activity of the cholesterol biosynthetic pathway, which is why circulating cholesterol may be only modestly raised and why statins offer limited benefit. Deposition of sterols in tendons and skin produces xanthomata, while incorporation of abnormal sterols into arterial walls accelerates atherosclerosis. Enrichment of red-cell and platelet membranes with plant sterols alters membrane fluidity and structure, causing haemolysis with stomatocytes and the production of abnormally large, fragile platelets, giving the distinctive haematological picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Homozygous or heterozygous familial hypercholesterolaemia","Cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis","Autosomal recessive hypercholesterolaemia (LDLRAP1)","Immune thrombocytopenia or other causes of macrothrombocytopenia","Hereditary stomatocytosis and other membrane haemolytic anaemias","Familial combined hyperlipidaemia","Xanthomata secondary to cholestatic liver disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma plant sterol quantification (sitosterol, campesterol, stigmasterol) by gas chromatography or mass spectrometry","ABCG5 and ABCG8 sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis","Full lipid profile including LDL and lipoprotein subfractions","Full blood count with blood film for stomatocytes, macrothrombocytopenia and haemolysis","Haemolysis screen (reticulocytes, bilirubin, haptoglobin, lactate dehydrogenase)","Cardiovascular assessment including lipid-focused imaging or coronary evaluation where indicated","Family screening and cascade genetic testing of first-degree relatives"],"redFlags":["Chest pain or other symptoms of premature coronary disease, including in children and young adults","Aortic valve involvement or signs of significant arterial disease at a young age","Rapidly enlarging or symptomatic xanthomata","Severe haemolysis or bleeding from profound thrombocytopenia","Xanthomata unresponsive to statin therapy suggesting an alternative sterol disorder"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis, a sterol-restricted diet and cholesterol-absorption blockade, plasma phytosterols fall, xanthomata regress and haematological abnormalities improve, greatly reducing cardiovascular risk. Undiagnosed disease carries a risk of premature, sometimes fatal, atherosclerotic events.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling as familial hypercholesterolaemia and relying on statins, which are largely ineffective in this condition","Overlooking the diagnosis when total cholesterol is normal or only mildly elevated despite prominent xanthomata","Attributing the thrombocytopenia and haemolysis to immune or primary haematological disease and missing the underlying sterol disorder","Failing to measure plasma plant sterols because they are not part of a routine lipid panel","Not screening family members, delaying diagnosis in affected relatives"],"management":["Strict dietary restriction of plant sterols and shellfish sterols, avoiding vegetable oils, nuts, seeds and shellfish, as the cornerstone of treatment","Ezetimibe, an intestinal sterol-absorption inhibitor, as the principal pharmacological therapy to reduce phytosterol absorption","Bile acid sequestrants such as colestyramine as adjunctive sterol-lowering therapy, recognising that statins are of limited value because endogenous cholesterol synthesis is already suppressed","Monitoring of plasma plant sterol levels, lipid profile and regression of xanthomata to guide therapy","Management of the haematological features of haemolysis and macrothrombocytopenia, which improve with sterol lowering, avoiding misdirected immune therapy or splenectomy","Cardiovascular risk assessment and surveillance for premature atherosclerosis","Genetic counselling and family screening for the autosomal recessive ABCG5 or ABCG8 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited Metabolic Diseases (standard reference text)","International lipid disorder management guidelines","Clinical biochemistry and metabolic medicine references"],"crossLinks":[]},"sitosterolemia":{"name":"Sitosterolaemia","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["phytosterolaemia","ABCG5 deficiency","ABCG8 deficiency","sterolin deficiency","STSL"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive sterol-storage disorder caused by mutations in the ABCG5 or ABCG8 sterol transporter genes","The defective transporter fails to limit intestinal absorption of plant and shellfish sterols and to promote their biliary excretion, so dietary phytosterols accumulate","Presents with tendon and tuberous xanthomata, often from childhood, sometimes with a normal or only modestly raised total cholesterol","Carries a markedly increased risk of premature atherosclerosis and coronary disease, occasionally with sudden cardiac events in young patients","Haematological features can include haemolytic anaemia with stomatocytes and abnormally large platelets (macrothrombocytopenia)","The biochemical hallmark is grossly raised plasma plant sterols (sitosterol, campesterol) measured by specialised chromatography, which routine cholesterol assays miss","Treatment is a diet low in plant and shellfish sterols combined with ezetimibe and/or bile-acid sequestrants; statins are relatively ineffective and this must be distinguished from familial hypercholesterolaemia"],"pathophysiology":"Sitosterolaemia results from loss of function of the ABCG5/ABCG8 heterodimeric transporter expressed on enterocytes and hepatocytes. Normally this pump exports non-cholesterol sterols back into the gut lumen and into bile, keeping absorption of dietary plant sterols very low. When it is defective, plant sterols such as sitosterol and campesterol, along with cholesterol, are absorbed in excess and poorly excreted, so they accumulate in plasma and tissues. Deposition in tendons and skin causes xanthomata and in arterial walls accelerates atherosclerosis, while incorporation of abnormal sterols into red cell and platelet membranes underlies the haemolysis and macrothrombocytopenia.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial hypercholesterolaemia (xanthomata with markedly raised LDL cholesterol)","Cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis (xanthomata with neurological features)","Other causes of macrothrombocytopenia and haemolytic anaemia","Secondary hyperlipidaemias","Autosomal recessive hypercholesterolaemia","Tangier disease and other rare lipid disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma plant sterol quantification (sitosterol and campesterol) by gas chromatography or mass spectrometry","Fasting lipid profile, recognising cholesterol may be only mildly elevated","Full blood count and film for haemolysis, stomatocytes and macrothrombocytopenia","Molecular genetic testing of the ABCG5 and ABCG8 genes","Cardiovascular assessment for premature atherosclerosis","Family screening given the recessive inheritance"],"redFlags":["Premature coronary artery disease or sudden cardiac event in a young patient","Extensive xanthomata in childhood, particularly with normal or near-normal cholesterol","Significant haemolytic anaemia or bleeding from macrothrombocytopenia","Family history of early cardiovascular death"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis, sterol-restricted diet and sterol-absorption-blocking therapy, plant sterol levels fall, xanthomata regress and cardiovascular risk improves; undiagnosed disease carries a substantial risk of premature atherosclerotic events.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as familial hypercholesterolaemia and relying on statins, which are relatively ineffective","Missing the diagnosis because routine cholesterol assays do not measure plant sterols","Overlooking haematological clues such as haemolysis and large platelets","Underestimating cardiovascular risk when cholesterol appears near normal","Failing to advise dietary restriction of plant and shellfish sterols"],"management":["Strict dietary restriction of plant sterols and shellfish sterols, avoiding vegetable oils, nuts, seeds and shellfish, as the cornerstone of treatment","Ezetimibe, an intestinal sterol-absorption inhibitor, as the principal pharmacological therapy to reduce phytosterol absorption","Bile acid sequestrants such as colestyramine as adjunctive sterol-lowering therapy, recognising that statins are of limited value because endogenous cholesterol synthesis is already suppressed","Monitoring of plasma plant sterol levels, lipid profile and regression of xanthomata to guide therapy","Management of the haematological features of haemolytic anaemia and macrothrombocytopenia, which improve with sterol lowering, avoiding misdirected immune therapy or splenectomy","Cardiovascular risk assessment and surveillance for premature atherosclerosis and coronary disease","Genetic counselling and family screening for the autosomal recessive ABCG5 or ABCG8 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"situs_inversus":{"name":"situs inversus","system":"cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["situs inversus totalis","situs transversus","situs oppositus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children, and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Situs inversus totalis denotes complete mirror-image reversal of both thoracic and abdominal viscera, with cardiac apex, aortic arch, stomach and spleen on the right and liver on the left.","In contrast to heterotaxy, situs inversus totalis carries a relatively low incidence of major congenital heart disease, most affected individuals having structurally normal hearts in mirror-image configuration.","Roughly a quarter of individuals with situs inversus totalis have primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener syndrome), so persistent sinopulmonary disease, chronic otitis and subfertility should prompt ciliary evaluation.","Congenitally corrected transposition and other conotruncal lesions occur more often in situs inversus than in the general population and warrant careful segmental echocardiographic assessment.","Atypical presentation of common disease is the rule: left-sided appendicitis, left-sided biliary colic and a right-sided cardiac apex on examination are classic traps.","Standard ECG electrode placement produces a spurious picture (inverted P in lead I, reversed praecordial progression); mirror-image lead placement restores a normal-appearing trace.","A segmental (sequential) approach naming atrial situs, ventricular loop and great-artery relationship is essential before any surgical or catheter intervention.","Interventional and surgical teams must anticipate reversed anatomy for central venous access, coronary angiography, and abdominal surgery to avoid wrong-side procedures."],"pathophysiology":"Situs inversus arises from disruption of the normal left-right axis patterning established during early embryogenesis at the primitive node, where motile monocilia generate a leftward extra-embryonic fluid flow (nodal flow) that biases asymmetric expression of the Nodal-Lefty-Pitx2 signalling cascade. When this laterality signal is randomised or reversed, the viscera and cardiac tube loop in mirror-image fashion, producing complete reversal (situs inversus totalis) when the process is coordinated across all organ fields. In primary ciliary dyskinesia the nodal cilia are immotile or dyskinetic, so laterality is determined at random, explaining why about half of affected individuals have situs inversus and half situs solitus. Because the reversal is usually symmetric and complete, atrioventricular and ventriculoarterial concordance is preserved in mirror image and the heart is typically structurally normal, distinguishing this condition from the abnormal midline symmetry of heterotaxy.","additionalDifferentials":["heterotaxy syndrome (situs ambiguus)","situs solitus with isolated dextrocardia","dextroposition from pulmonary hypoplasia or diaphragmatic hernia","congenitally corrected transposition of the great arteries","primary ciliary dyskinesia without laterality defect","asplenia or polysplenia syndromes","acquired mediastinal shift mimicking cardiac malposition"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["transthoracic echocardiography with formal segmental analysis","abdominal ultrasound documenting hepatic, splenic and gastric position","chest radiograph noting cardiac apex, gastric bubble and aortic arch side","ECG with both standard and mirror-image lead placement","nasal nitric oxide measurement and ciliary studies where primary ciliary dyskinesia is suspected","cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) for detailed situs and vascular mapping before intervention","genetic testing for ciliopathy and laterality genes in selected cases"],"redFlags":["chronic productive cough, recurrent sinusitis or bronchiectasis suggesting primary ciliary dyskinesia","cyanosis, murmur or failure to thrive indicating associated structural heart disease","atypical or contralateral pain suggesting missed left-sided appendicitis or biliary disease","unexplained subfertility in the context of a laterality defect","planned surgery or intervention where reversed anatomy risks a wrong-side procedure"],"prognosis":"Isolated situs inversus totalis with a structurally normal heart is compatible with a normal lifespan and requires mainly awareness for atypical disease presentation. Prognosis is chiefly determined by any coexisting congenital heart disease or by the respiratory and fertility consequences of associated primary ciliary dyskinesia.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal heart and omitting a full segmental echocardiographic assessment.","Misinterpreting the ECG as ischaemic or abnormal when electrodes are placed conventionally.","Overlooking primary ciliary dyskinesia in a patient presenting only with situs inversus.","Diagnosing situs inversus when the true picture is heterotaxy, thereby underestimating cardiac and splenic risk.","Failing to communicate reversed anatomy to surgical, anaesthetic and interventional teams."],"management":["Perform segmental echocardiographic assessment and refer to cardiology to identify and manage any associated congenital heart defects such as congenitally corrected transposition or conotruncal lesions","Screen for primary ciliary dyskinesia with nasal nitric oxide and ciliary studies where sinopulmonary disease, chronic otitis or subfertility coexist, instituting airway clearance and prompt antibiotics for infections if confirmed","Document the mirror-image anatomy prominently to avoid diagnostic traps such as left-sided appendicitis or right-sided biliary colic and to guide safe surgery","Use mirror-image ECG lead placement and annotate all imaging with the situs to prevent misinterpretation","Manage the respiratory and fertility consequences of associated ciliary disease with respiratory physiotherapy and fertility referral","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across cardiology, respiratory, ENT and surgery, flagging reversed anatomy before any operative or interventional procedure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children, and Adolescents","Nadas' Pediatric Cardiology","European Respiratory Society guidelines on primary ciliary dyskinesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"sj_gren_s_disease":{"name":"Sjögren’s Disease","system":"Immune-Mediated / Rheumatologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sjögren’s syndrome","Sicca syndrome","Autoimmune epithelitis","Keratoconjunctivitis sicca with xerostomia"],"chapter":373,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2876","clinicalPearls":["Prototype autoimmune disease defined by lymphocytic infiltration of exocrine glands causing xerostomia and dry eyes (keratoconjunctivitis sicca), together with marked B-cell hyperactivity.","Strongly female-predominant (female-to-male ratio roughly 10–20:1) and chiefly affects middle-aged women, but can present at any age including childhood; prevalence is approximately 0.5–1%.","Earlier age at onset tends to mark a more aggressive phenotype with more systemic manifestations and more serum autoantibodies.","Can occur alone or in association with other systemic rheumatic diseases, most commonly rheumatoid arthritis, limited scleroderma, and systemic lupus erythematosus; 5–20% of patients with other autoimmune diseases can have sicca features.","Principal oral symptom is dryness (xerostomia) with difficulty swallowing dry food, burning mouth, increased dental caries; examination may show dry erythematous sticky mucosa, atrophic tongue papillae, and a deeply fissured tongue.","Intermittent or persistent enlargement of the parotid or other major salivary glands occurs in about two-thirds of patients.","Ocular involvement classically produces a sandy or gritty sensation under the lids, with burning, stranded secretions at the inner canthi, reduced tearing, redness, and photosensitivity from corneal and conjunctival epithelial destruction (keratoconjunctivitis sicca).","Extraglandular (systemic) manifestations occur in about one-third of patients and are categorized as nonspecific, periepithelial, immune complex–mediated, and lymphoma.","Carries the highest risk of lymphoma among all autoimmune diseases; most are extranodal, low-grade, marginal zone (MALT) B-cell lymphomas, often found incidentally on labial minor salivary gland biopsy.","Most patients (about two-thirds) have an elevated ESR, hypergammaglobulinemia, antinuclear antibodies, rheumatoid factor, and anti-Ro52/Ro60 and anti-La autoantibodies.","The disease course is usually slow and benign in the majority of patients; antecedent major stressful life events have been reported prior to onset.","Recently described as triggerable by PD-1/PD-L1 checkpoint inhibitor therapy."],"pathophysiology":"Activated T lymphocytes are the major infiltrating cells in affected exocrine (chiefly salivary and lacrimal) glands, with B-cell populations prevailing in heavily infiltrated labial minor salivary gland tissue; follicular, myeloid, and plasmacytoid dendritic cells and macrophages are also present, the latter (with inflammasome activation) being linked to lymphoma risk. Ductal and acinar epithelial cells play an active pathogenic role: they aberrantly express costimulatory molecules and surface Ro/La autoantigens, secrete proinflammatory cytokines and lymphocyte-attracting chemokines that support ectopic germinal center formation, express innate-immunity Toll-like receptors (TLR3, 7, 9), and display immunoregulatory molecules (ICAM, CD40). Epithelial cells produce B cell–activating factor (BAFF), induced by type I and II interferons, and circulating BAFF is elevated (especially with hypergammaglobulinemia and autoantibodies), exerting an antiapoptotic effect on B lymphocytes; epithelial cells themselves show increased apoptosis. Disease initiation and perpetuation reflect interplay of endogenous triggers (intracellular stress, inappropriate expression of endogenous nucleic acids) and exogenous triggers (viruses, hormonal factors, stressful life events) on a genetically hyperactive immune background. Established genetic loci include HLA-DQA1*0501, interferon/BAFF axis variants (IRF5, STAT4, BAFF), B-cell function genes (EBF1, BLK), and combined deficiencies of the classical complement pathway.","additionalDifferentials":["Sarcoidosis (granulomatous salivary gland infiltration, no Ro/La antibodies)","IgG4-related disease","Amyloidosis","Graft-versus-host disease","Diabetes mellitus and other metabolic causes of dry mouth/parotid enlargement","Drug-induced dryness (psychotherapeutic, parasympatholytic, antihypertensive agents)","Prior head and neck radiation","Autoimmune thyroid disease","Marginal zone (MALT) B-cell lymphoma complicating the disease"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection with diffuse infiltrative lymphocytosis / sicca syndrome (young males, CD8+ glandular infiltrates, lacks anti-Ro/La, positive HIV serology)","Hepatitis C virus infection","Viral parotitis (mumps, influenza, Epstein-Barr virus, coxsackievirus A, cytomegalovirus)","Tuberculosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis","IgG4-related disease","Amyloidosis","Autoimmune thyroid disease","Diabetes mellitus, hyperlipoproteinemia (types IV and V), chronic pancreatitis, hepatic cirrhosis","Acromegaly and gonadal hypofunction","Eosinophilic sialodochitis","Drug-induced or radiation-induced gland dysfunction","Stevens-Johnson syndrome, pemphigoid, chronic conjunctivitis/blepharitis, hypovitaminosis A (dry eye)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Schirmer’s I test for tear flow (≤5 mm/5 min on at least one eye supports diagnosis)","Tear breakup time or tear lysozyme content for tear composition","Slit-lamp examination after lissamine green or Rose Bengal staining; ocular staining score ≥5 or van Bijsterveld score ≥4","Sialometry / unstimulated whole saliva flow rate (≤0.1 mL/min supportive)","Salivary gland ultrasound (emerging diagnostic and prognostic tool); MRI and magnetic resonance sialography","Labial minor salivary gland biopsy with focus score ≥1 (foci per 4 mm2)","Serum autoantibodies: anti-Ro52/Ro60, anti-La, rheumatoid factor, ANA; anticentromere (limited scleroderma–like), antimitochondrial (autoimmune cholangitis), anti-21-hydroxylase (blunted adrenal response), anti-citrullinated peptide (arthritis), anticalponin-3 (peripheral neuropathy)","Complement (low C4), serum protein electrophoresis, cryoglobulins, serum IgG4, thyroid autoantibodies","Testing to exclude chronic viral infection (HCV, HIV) and chest x-ray to exclude sarcoidosis","FDG-PET/CT to help exclude lymphoma when suspected"],"redFlags":["Parotid or major salivary gland enlargement, especially persistent or with a lymph node mass >7 cm — concern for lymphoma","Palpable purpura, urticarial lesions, skin ulcerations, or mononeuritis multiplex (immune complex–mediated vasculitis)","Low C4 complement, monoclonal gammopathy (paraproteinemia), and cryoglobulins — lymphoma risk markers","Heavy focal lymphocytic infiltrates with germinal center formation on salivary biopsy — lymphoma risk","B symptoms (constitutional) with lymphadenopathy","Severe tongue atrophy and leukopenia in the appropriate context","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis with mixed type II/III cryoglobulinemia","Myelitis with anti-aquaporin-4 antibodies (rare CNS involvement)"],"prognosis":"The disease usually evolves slowly and follows a benign course in most patients. The dominant adverse outcome is malignant transformation: Sjögren’s disease carries the highest lymphoma risk of any autoimmune disease, with clinical (parotid enlargement, severe tongue atrophy, palpable purpura), hematologic/serologic (leukopenia, autoantibodies, low C4, monoclonal gammopathy, cryoglobulins), and histopathologic (germinal center formation, heavy focal lymphocytic infiltrates) risk factors. Survival is reduced in patients with B symptoms, lymph node mass >7 cm, and high or intermediate histologic grade. Patients also have an increased risk of cardiovascular disease, paralleling rheumatoid arthritis and SLE.","pitfalls":["Failing to take a careful medication history — diuretics, antihypertensives, anticholinergics, and antidepressants can themselves cause dryness and mimic or worsen sicca.","Overlooking HIV- or HCV-associated sicca syndrome, which can closely resemble Sjögren’s but lacks anti-Ro/La and has distinct demographics and glandular infiltrate phenotype.","Missing renal tubular dysfunction: interstitial nephritis may be asymptomatic, presenting only as hyposthenuria or renal tubular acidosis, and untreated acidosis can progress to nephrocalcinosis.","Attributing all salivary gland enlargement to benign disease and missing an incidental MALT lymphoma on labial biopsy.","Not excluding sarcoidosis, amyloidosis, IgG4-related disease, graft-versus-host disease, and prior head/neck radiation before diagnosing Sjögren’s disease.","Assuming arthritis is erosive — arthritis in primary Sjögren’s disease is characteristically nonerosive (may lead to Jaccoud’s arthropathy)."],"management":["Relieve dry eyes with ocular surface lubricants and topical anti-inflammatory therapy such as topical ciclosporin, and dry mouth with saliva substitutes and muscarinic secretagogues such as pilocarpine or cevimeline","Institute meticulous dental care with topical fluoride and regular dental review to counter caries from xerostomia, and treat oral candidiasis promptly","Treat systemic and extraglandular disease with hydroxychloroquine for arthralgia and fatigue, escalating to corticosteroids and immunosuppressants such as methotrexate or azathioprine, and rituximab for refractory manifestations","Maintain lifelong surveillance for B-cell lymphoma, investigating persistent parotid enlargement, new lymphadenopathy, cryoglobulinaemia or unexplained cytopenias","Advise general measures including environmental humidification, saliva stimulation with sugar-free gum and avoidance of anticholinergic and drying medications","Provide multidisciplinary care with rheumatology, ophthalmology and oral medicine"],"severityClassification":"Diagnosis/classification per the 2016 ACR-EULAR criteria: suspect in patients with dry eyes and/or dry mouth (or systemic features such as major salivary gland enlargement, fatigue, Raynaud’s, small-joint arthralgia/arthritis, palpable purpura/urticaria, renal tubular acidosis/membranoproliferative GN, ILD/small airway disease, autoimmune cholangitis, peripheral neuropathy, or MS-like lesions), supported by objective findings: unstimulated whole saliva flow ≤0.1 mL/min, Schirmer’s test ≤5 mm/5 min, ocular staining score ≥5 (or van Bijsterveld ≥4), serum anti-Ro antibodies, and minor salivary gland biopsy focus score ≥1. Exclusions include prior head/neck radiation, active HCV, AIDS, sarcoidosis, amyloidosis, graft-versus-host disease, and IgG4-related disease. Extraglandular disease severity is gauged by the categories of involvement (nonspecific, periepithelial, immune complex–mediated, and lymphoma).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2876","Harrison 22e p.2877","Harrison 22e p.2878","Harrison 22e p.2879"],"crossLinks":[]},"sjogren_larsson_syndrome":{"name":"Sjogren-Larsson syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ALDH3A2","fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase deficiency","FALDH deficiency","SLS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad of congenital ichthyosis, spastic diplegia or tetraplegia, and intellectual disability","The ichthyosis is generalised with fine to plate-like scaling, often prominent in flexures, and pruritus is characteristic","A pathognomonic ophthalmological sign is glistening white/yellow dots (crystalline maculopathy) at the macula","Autosomal recessive; caused by mutations in ALDH3A2 encoding fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase (FALDH)","Neurological features typically emerge in infancy with delayed motor milestones and spasticity, and seizures may occur","Photophobia and reduced visual acuity accompany the macular changes","Management is supportive with emollients/keratolytics for the skin, physiotherapy and spasticity care, and specialist neurological and ophthalmological follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"Sjogren-Larsson syndrome is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in ALDH3A2, which encodes fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase, a component of the fatty alcohol:NAD oxidoreductase complex. Deficient enzyme activity blocks the oxidation of long-chain fatty aldehydes and alcohols, which accumulate and are incorporated abnormally into lipids. In the epidermis this disrupts the lamellar lipid membranes essential for the permeability barrier, producing ichthyosis; in the developing central nervous system abnormal lipid metabolism and myelin disturbance underlie the spasticity and cognitive impairment; and lipid deposition in the retina produces the characteristic glistening macular dots.","additionalDifferentials":["Other congenital ichthyoses (lamellar ichthyosis, congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma)","Refsum disease and other peroxisomal disorders","Chanarin-Dorfman syndrome (neutral lipid storage disease with ichthyosis)","Cerebral palsy of other cause with coincidental xerosis","Trichothiodystrophy with ichthyosis","Netherton syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assay of fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase activity in cultured fibroblasts or leucocytes","Molecular genetic testing of ALDH3A2","Ophthalmological examination for glistening macular dots and photophobia","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain (white-matter change) and MR spectroscopy showing an abnormal lipid peak","Skin biopsy showing hyperkeratosis with a variably reduced or thickened granular layer","Developmental and neurological assessment"],"redFlags":["Progressive loss of motor function or new-onset seizures","Marked visual deterioration","Feeding difficulty or failure to thrive in the affected infant","Barrier compromise with recurrent skin infection"],"prognosis":"Neurological impairment is usually static or slowly progressive and largely irreversible; most patients have lasting spasticity and intellectual disability. The ichthyosis and pruritus can be controlled but not cured, and lifespan varies with the severity of neurological and swallowing involvement.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing isolated cerebral palsy and missing the associated ichthyosis and macular dots","Attributing pruritus to eczema rather than recognising it as a characteristic feature","Failing to arrange fundoscopy, which can reveal the near-specific glistening dots","Overlooking the recessive inheritance and the value of genetic counselling for the family"],"management":["Control the ichthyosis with regular emollients, keratolytics such as topical urea or lactic acid, and systemic retinoids for severe scaling","Target the characteristic pruritus with leukotriene-synthesis inhibitors such as zileuton alongside antihistamines and diligent skin care","Manage spasticity with physiotherapy, antispasticity agents such as baclofen and orthotic support, and treat any seizures with antiseizure medication","Provide ophthalmology surveillance for the crystalline maculopathy and photophobia, supplying tinted lenses and low-vision support","Deliver multidisciplinary developmental support with special education and speech and occupational therapy for the intellectual disability, monitoring nutrition and swallowing","Offer genetic counselling and carrier and family testing given autosomal recessive ALDH3A2 inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"sjogren_syndrome":{"name":"Sjögren syndrome","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SS","sicca syndrome","Sjogren's syndrome","autoimmune epithelitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by dry eyes (keratoconjunctivitis sicca) and dry mouth (xerostomia) from immune-mediated destruction of lacrimal and salivary glands","Predominantly affects middle-aged women and may be primary or secondary to another connective tissue disease such as rheumatoid arthritis or lupus","Systemic features include fatigue, arthralgia, Raynaud phenomenon, parotid gland swelling, and cutaneous vasculitis","Anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB antibodies are characteristic, along with positive antinuclear antibody and rheumatoid factor","Objective sicca can be confirmed with Schirmer test, ocular surface staining, and unstimulated salivary flow; labial minor salivary gland biopsy shows focal lymphocytic sialadenitis","There is a markedly increased lifetime risk of B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma, particularly MALT lymphoma","Anti-Ro antibodies in pregnancy carry a risk of neonatal lupus and congenital heart block","Management centres on symptomatic relief of dryness (tear and saliva substitutes, secretagogues) with immunosuppression reserved for significant systemic involvement"],"pathophysiology":"Sjögren syndrome is a chronic autoimmune exocrinopathy in which lymphocytic (mainly CD4 T-cell and B-cell) infiltration of the exocrine glands destroys secretory epithelium, sometimes termed autoimmune epithelitis. B-cell hyperactivity produces autoantibodies (anti-Ro/SSA, anti-La/SSB) and hypergammaglobulinaemia, and sustained clonal B-cell proliferation underlies the elevated lymphoma risk. Type I interferon signalling and genetic HLA associations contribute to disease susceptibility. Extraglandular disease reflects immune complex deposition and lymphocytic infiltration of organs including skin, joints, lungs, kidneys, and nervous system.","additionalDifferentials":["Age-related or medication-induced sicca (anticholinergics)","Sarcoidosis and IgG4-related disease affecting salivary glands","Chronic viral infection (hepatitis C, HIV)","Radiation-induced glandular damage","Other connective tissue diseases with secondary sicca","Amyloidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB antibodies, ANA, and rheumatoid factor","Schirmer test and ocular surface staining for aqueous tear deficiency","Unstimulated whole salivary flow and salivary gland ultrasound or imaging","Labial minor salivary gland biopsy showing focal lymphocytic sialadenitis","Serum immunoglobulins and protein electrophoresis; monitoring for lymphoma if persistent gland enlargement or new lymphadenopathy"],"redFlags":["Persistent unilateral parotid enlargement, lymphadenopathy, or new cytopenias suggesting lymphoma transformation","Signs of systemic vasculitis, mononeuritis multiplex, or interstitial lung disease","Renal involvement such as renal tubular acidosis or interstitial nephritis","Pregnancy with anti-Ro antibodies given the risk of fetal congenital heart block","Severe corneal complications from untreated dry eye"],"prognosis":"Most patients have a good prognosis with a slowly progressive course dominated by sicca symptoms, but systemic involvement and, importantly, lymphoma development can worsen outcomes. Lifelong monitoring for lymphoproliferative disease is warranted.","pitfalls":["Dismissing sicca symptoms as trivial and missing systemic or lymphomatous complications","Failing to consider mimics such as IgG4-related disease, sarcoidosis, or viral infection","Not counselling and monitoring anti-Ro-positive women in pregnancy for fetal heart block","Overlooking increased dental caries and ocular surface damage from chronic dryness","Attributing new persistent gland swelling to disease flare rather than investigating for lymphoma"],"management":["Treat keratoconjunctivitis sicca with artificial tears, topical ciclosporin or lifitegrast and punctal occlusion, and xerostomia with saliva substitutes and secretagogues such as pilocarpine or cevimeline","Provide preventive dental and oral care with topical fluoride and antifungal treatment of oral candidiasis","Use hydroxychloroquine for arthralgia, fatigue and cutaneous features, adding corticosteroids and immunosuppressants such as methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate, or rituximab, for significant extraglandular organ disease","Maintain lifelong surveillance for B-cell non-Hodgkin and MALT lymphoma, evaluating persistent gland enlargement, new lymphadenopathy, cryoglobulinaemia or falling complement","Advise environmental humidification and avoidance of anticholinergic and drying medications","Coordinate multidisciplinary care with rheumatology, ophthalmology and dentistry, and in pregnancy monitor anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies for neonatal lupus and congenital heart block"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"skew_deviation":{"name":"Skew deviation","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["skew deviation with ocular tilt reaction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acquired vertical misalignment of the eyes caused by disturbance of the otolithic (utricular) vestibular pathways, not by a single ocular muscle palsy","It may occur alone or as part of the ocular tilt reaction with head tilt, ocular torsion and skew toward the lower eye","It is an important mimic of fourth nerve palsy and of other causes of vertical diplopia","The upright-supine test helps distinguish it: a skew deviation typically decreases when the patient lies supine","It usually signals brainstem or cerebellar disease, so acute skew with other signs may indicate stroke","Within the HINTS assessment of acute vestibular syndrome, skew deviation is a central (worrying) sign"],"pathophysiology":"Skew deviation results from an imbalance in the graviceptive otolithic pathways that project from the utricle through the brainstem to the ocular motor nuclei, producing a vertical divergence of the visual axes. Lesions of these pathways in the pons, medulla, midbrain or cerebellum disturb the normal tonic balance that keeps the eyes level, and when the full ocular tilt reaction occurs the head tilt, ocular torsion and hypotropia all deviate toward the side of a lower pontomedullary lesion (or opposite a higher one). Because these pathways lie in the posterior circulation, skew deviation frequently accompanies brainstem or cerebellar ischaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Fourth nerve (superior oblique) palsy","Partial third nerve palsy","Myasthenia gravis","Thyroid eye disease","Orbital restrictive disease","Dissociated vertical deviation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["HINTS examination in acute vestibular syndrome","Upright-supine test to demonstrate reduction of the deviation when supine","MRI of the brain with attention to the brainstem and cerebellum","Orthoptic assessment including measurement of ocular torsion","Vascular risk-factor evaluation when stroke is suspected"],"redFlags":["Acute onset with vertigo, ataxia or other brainstem signs","Direction-changing or gaze-evoked nystagmus","Any feature suggesting posterior circulation stroke","Reduced conscious level or rapidly evolving deficits"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause; skew from a self-limiting or treatable lesion may resolve, but as a sign of brainstem or cerebellar stroke it carries the prognosis of that event.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing skew as a benign fourth nerve palsy and missing a stroke","Failing to perform HINTS in acute vestibular syndrome","Overlooking the associated ocular tilt reaction","Attributing vertical diplopia to orbital disease without considering a central cause"],"management":["Treat the underlying cause as the priority, activating an urgent stroke pathway when acute skew occurs with other central signs on HINTS assessment","Direct management of the causative posterior fossa lesion, whether ischaemia, demyelination, tumour or haemorrhage, with the relevant specialist team","Relieve vertical diplopia symptomatically with prisms or temporary monocular occlusion while awaiting resolution","Address vascular risk factors with antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy and blood pressure and lipid control when an ischaemic cause is confirmed","Arrange orthoptic follow-up to monitor the deviation and ocular torsion, reserving strabismus surgery for stable, persistent misalignment","Refer for multidisciplinary neurology and neuro-ophthalmology assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"skin_cancer":{"name":"Skin Cancer","system":"Dermatology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["melanoma","basal cell carcinoma","squamous cell carcinoma"],"chapter":81,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.598-609","clinicalPearls":["Apply the ABCDE rule to pigmented lesions to detect melanoma early: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter >6 mm (pencil-eraser size), and Evolving (any change in size, shape, color, elevation, or new symptoms such as bleeding, itching, or crusting) (p.601).","The 'ugly duckling' sign: any nevus that looks atypical and different from the rest of an individual's nevi should be considered suspicious (p.601).","Melanoma is an aggressive malignancy of melanocytes and accounts for over half of all deaths from skin cancer, even though it is far less common than nonmelanoma skin cancer (p.598).","Basal cell carcinoma is among the most common cancers; its natural history is a slowly enlarging, locally invasive neoplasm with low metastatic potential (~0.1% in immunocompetent patients), though the risk of recurrence or a new primary NMSC is about 40% over 5 years (p.607).","Nodular BCC classically presents as a pearly nodule with tortuous telangiectatic vessels, rolled borders, and a central crust; pigmented BCC may contain melanin and be confused with melanoma (p.606-607).","Squamous cell carcinoma arises from keratinizing epidermal cells on sun-exposed skin and can metastasize (0.3-5.2%), most often to regional lymph nodes; lower-lip and ear tumors metastasize in 13% and 11% respectively (p.606-607).","Actinic keratoses and actinic cheilitis are premalignant forms of SCC; SCC in situ (Bowen's disease) presents as a scaling erythematous plaque, and treating premalignant/in-situ lesions reduces subsequent invasive disease (p.607).","NMSCs (SCC ~80%, BCC ~18% of cases) are the most common cancers in the US, with an estimated >5.3 million cases annually (p.606).","Breslow thickness (greatest tumor thickness measured from the top of the granular layer or ulcer base to the bottom of the tumor) is the best predictor of melanoma recurrence and, with ulceration, defines the T stage (p.601-602)."],"pathophysiology":"Melanoma is a malignancy of melanocytes, neural-crest-derived pigment-producing cells; most melanomas arise in skin but can occur in mucosa, eye, meninges, and upper esophagus. UV radiation is the dominant environmental driver, and cutaneous melanomas carry one of the highest somatic mutation rates of any cancer (>10 mutations/Mb), the majority bearing a UV signature. Key driver mutations affect the MAP-kinase and PI3K/AKT proliferative pathways: BRAF (commonly V600E) in 40-50% of cutaneous melanomas, NRAS in ~20%, and loss-of-function NF1 in 10-15%; germline CDKN2A (p16/ARF, RB and p53 pathways), CDK4, MITF, BAP1, POT1, TERT, and moderate-risk MC1R variants underlie inherited susceptibility. Nonmelanoma skin cancers are also UV-driven: SCC is associated with p53 and N-RAS damage, whereas BCC is driven by hedgehog (Hh) pathway activation, usually loss of function of the tumor-suppressor PTCH1 (which normally inhibits SMO). Merkel cell carcinoma is linked to the oncogenic Merkel cell polyomavirus, present in ~80% of tumors (p.598-607).","additionalDifferentials":["Benign nevomelanocytic nevus (vs melanoma)","Dysplastic/atypical nevus","Seborrheic keratosis","Lentigo","Nummular eczema and psoriasis (may mimic superficial BCC)","Wart or callus (may mimic SCC when inflammation is minimal)","Keratoacanthoma (rapidly growing low-grade SCC variant)","Merkel cell carcinoma","Cutaneous B- or T-cell lymphoma","Extramammary Paget's disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Superficial BCC mimicking nummular eczema or psoriasis","SCC mistaken for a wart or callus when inflammatory response is minimal","Pigmented (nodular) BCC mimicking melanoma","Desmoplastic melanoma mimicking nodular basal cell carcinoma","Benign nevus mimicking melanoma"],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional biopsy with 1-3 mm narrow margins (preferred) for any changing/suspicious pigmented lesion; incisional (shave, saucerization, or punch) biopsy of the most nodular/darkest area when excision is impractical - incisional biopsy does not facilitate melanoma spread","Histopathology report should include Breslow thickness, mitotic rate, ulceration, lymphatic/vascular/neural invasion, regression, microsatellitosis, and margin status","Dermoscopy (low-level magnification with polarized light or water interface) and total-body photography for surveillance in high-risk patients","Genetic expression profiles (GEP, e.g. 31-gene), FISH with multiple probes, or comparative genomic hybridization to distinguish difficult melanomas from benign nevi and estimate prognosis","Complete blood count, complete metabolic panel, and serum LDH (LDH is part of AJCC stage IV criteria)","Sentinel lymph node biopsy for pathologic staging","CT, PET, or combined CT/PET (and MRI) when symptoms, exam, or abnormal labs suggest metastatic disease","Genetic testing for individuals with >=3 primary melanomas or with families having >=1 invasive melanoma plus >=2 cases of melanoma and/or pancreatic cancer, ocular melanoma, mesothelioma, or renal cell carcinoma on one side"],"redFlags":["Any pigmented lesion that has changed (size, shape, color, elevation) or that meets ABCDE criteria is a candidate for biopsy","An 'ugly duckling' nevus that differs from a patient's other nevi","New bleeding, itching, or crusting in a pigmented lesion","Thick and/or ulcerated melanoma (key adverse T-stage features)","Microsatellite lesions, in-transit metastases, nodal involvement, elevated LDH, or distant metastases to brain/liver/bone","SCC on the lower lip or ear, recurrent SCC (~30% metastatic risk), or SCC in scars/chronic ulcers, genital/mucosal surfaces, or in immunosuppressed patients (aggressive behavior)","Rapidly expanding lesion in an older, immunosuppressed, UV-exposed patient (MCC: AEIOU - Asymptomatic, Expanding rapidly, Immunosuppression, Older than 50, UV-exposed site)"],"prognosis":"Melanoma prognosis is dominated by Breslow thickness and ulceration (T stage); favorable primary sites are the forearm and leg, unfavorable sites the scalp, hands, feet, and mucous membranes. 10-year melanoma-specific survival ranges from >99% for stage 0 (in situ) and ~98% for stage IA down to ~24-30% for stage IIID. Women with stage I/II disease and younger patients do better; older men have worse outcomes (delayed diagnosis, thicker tumors, more head/neck, acral and BRAF wild-type tumors). High mitotic rate and lymphatic/vascular invasion are additional adverse factors. Overall melanoma mortality has been falling, attributed to immunotherapy and targeted therapy. BCC rarely metastasizes (~0.1% in immunocompetent patients) but recurs frequently. Cutaneous SCC metastasizes in 0.3-5.2% of cases overall, higher for lip/ear/recurrent/immunosuppressed disease. Merkel cell carcinoma is aggressive: ~90% cure with local disease but ~52% survival with nodal and ~10% with distant disease (p.602-609).","pitfalls":["Morpheaform, infiltrative, and micronodular BCCs have indistinct borders and subtle presentation, so treatment is often delayed and tumors are more extensive than they appear clinically","SCC can be mistaken for a benign wart or callus when the inflammatory response is minimal","Pigmented BCC may be confused with melanoma; desmoplastic melanoma can mimic nodular BCC","Melanoma frequently develops on non-sun-exposed skin (acral, mucosal); in dark-skinned, Hispanic, and East Asian populations melanoma is often non-UV-related, diagnosed at higher stage, and has worse outcomes","The 31-gene GEP can estimate distant-relapse probability but has not supplanted prognostic estimates from surgical staging, and genomic pathway data are not yet incorporated into AJCC staging","Standard labs and imaging rarely detect occult metastatic disease in early melanoma and should be directed by symptoms/exam findings"],"management":["Excise melanoma with stage-appropriate wide local margins as primary treatment, with sentinel lymph node biopsy for pathological staging of thicker lesions","Treat advanced or metastatic melanoma with immune checkpoint inhibitors such as anti-PD-1 and anti-CTLA-4 antibodies, and add BRAF and MEK inhibitors in BRAF-mutant disease","Treat basal and squamous cell carcinoma by complete excision or Mohs micrographic surgery at high-risk sites, with radiotherapy or topical agents such as imiquimod or 5-fluorouracil for selected superficial lesions","Reinforce photoprotection and patient self-examination using the ABCDE and ugly-duckling approach, with regular dermatological review and dermoscopy in high-risk patients","Undertake ongoing skin and nodal surveillance for recurrence and new primaries, with cross-sectional imaging and serum LDH monitoring in higher-stage melanoma","Manage through a multidisciplinary oncology team and offer genetic counselling where a hereditary melanoma syndrome such as CDKN2A is suspected"],"severityClassification":"Melanoma is staged by the AJCC TNM system (T stage built primarily on Breslow thickness and ulceration; pathologic stage incorporates sentinel lymph node biopsy results), from stage 0 (in situ, Tis) through stage IV (distant metastasis); 10-year melanoma-specific survival is depicted by stage in Table 81-3. Merkel cell carcinoma has its own TNM staging incorporating tumor size (<2 cm vs >2 cm), nodal status, and distant metastases (p.602-609).","references":["Harrison 22e p.598-609"],"crossLinks":[]},"slac_wrist":{"name":"slac wrist","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scapholunate advanced collapse","SLAC arthritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["SLAC is the most common pattern of degenerative wrist arthritis and usually arises from untreated scapholunate dissociation.","Degeneration follows a predictable sequence: the radial styloid-scaphoid articulation first, then the whole radioscaphoid joint, then the capitolunate and midcarpal joint.","The radiolunate joint is characteristically spared, which underpins motion-preserving salvage procedures.","Scaphoid nonunion advanced collapse (SNAC) follows an analogous but distinct degenerative pattern originating from scaphoid nonunion.","Stage guides surgery: radial styloidectomy with scapholunate treatment early, proximal row carpectomy or scaphoid excision with four-corner fusion later, and total wrist fusion for pancarpal disease.","Proximal row carpectomy depends on preserved cartilage on the capitate head and in the lunate fossa.","Wrist denervation via anterior and posterior interosseous neurectomy can relieve pain while preserving motion in selected patients."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic scapholunate dissociation allows the scaphoid to assume a fixed flexed and rotated position, shifting radiocarpal contact to a small area at the radial styloid. Abnormal, concentrated loading erodes articular cartilage first at the radioscaphoid articulation and then across the midcarpal joint as the capitate migrates proximally into the gap between scaphoid and lunate. Because the lunate remains congruent within the lunate fossa, the radiolunate joint is preserved, which allows salvage operations that offload the diseased articulations while retaining some wrist motion.","additionalDifferentials":["SNAC wrist (scaphoid nonunion advanced collapse)","Rheumatoid or other inflammatory arthritis","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition (pseudogout) arthropathy","Kienböck disease with secondary arthritis","Post-traumatic radiocarpal arthritis from an intra-articular distal radius fracture","Primary radiocarpal osteoarthritis","Gout"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PA and lateral radiographs to stage the degenerative pattern","Clenched-fist and contralateral comparison views","CT for detailed articular assessment and surgical planning","MRI to evaluate cartilage and the capitate head before proximal row carpectomy","Diagnostic wrist arthroscopy to grade chondral surfaces","Inflammatory and crystal screen if an inflammatory arthropathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive pain and deformity suggesting advanced collapse","Features of inflammatory or crystal arthropathy requiring systemic work-up","Capitate head chondral loss that would contraindicate proximal row carpectomy"],"prognosis":"SLAC is progressive and non-reversible, but stage-appropriate salvage surgery reliably relieves pain, albeit at the cost of some grip strength and wrist motion.","pitfalls":["Choosing proximal row carpectomy when the capitate head cartilage is already compromised.","Failing to distinguish SLAC from SNAC, which changes the pattern of joint involvement.","Mistaking crystal or inflammatory arthropathy for degenerative SLAC.","Underestimating radial styloid involvement in early disease."],"management":["Begin with non-operative pain control using activity modification, wrist splinting, analgesia and a corticosteroid injection","Offer stage-directed motion-preserving salvage surgery, starting with radial styloidectomy and scapholunate treatment in early disease","Perform proximal row carpectomy or scaphoid excision with four-corner fusion in mid-stage disease, exploiting the characteristically spared radiolunate joint","Reserve total wrist arthrodesis for advanced pancarpal degeneration","Confirm preserved cartilage on the capitate head and in the lunate fossa before proceeding to proximal row carpectomy","Provide postoperative hand therapy and rehabilitation, counselling the patient on the trade-off between pain relief and loss of grip strength and wrist motion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","Wheeless' Textbook of Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"slap_lesion":{"name":"SLAP lesion (superior labral anterior-posterior tear)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["superior labrum anterior to posterior tear","biceps-labral complex injury","superior labral tear"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Review of Orthopaedics / Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A tear of the superior glenoid labrum centred on the biceps anchor, extending anterior and posterior to the attachment of the long head of biceps tendon","Typical mechanisms are a fall onto an outstretched hand, a traction injury, or repetitive overhead throwing (the peel-back mechanism in athletes)","Patients describe deep shoulder pain, painful clicking or catching, and loss of throwing velocity rather than frank instability","Provocative tests such as O'Brien active compression, the crank test and the biceps load tests support the diagnosis but none is highly specific","Commonly classified into types I-IV (Snyder), with type II (detachment of the labrum and biceps anchor) the most frequently treated surgically","MR arthrography is more sensitive than plain MRI, but asymptomatic labral change is common with age so imaging must be correlated clinically","Coexisting rotator cuff pathology, internal impingement and instability are frequent and influence management"],"pathophysiology":"The superior labrum anchors the long head of biceps tendon to the supraglenoid tubercle. Acute traction or compression loads, or the repetitive torsional and peel-back forces of overhead throwing, avulse or fray the superior labrum and destabilise the biceps-labral complex. This disrupts the labral contribution to glenohumeral concavity-compression and biceps load transfer, producing pain and mechanical symptoms; in throwers it often coexists with posterosuperior internal impingement and a glenohumeral internal rotation deficit.","additionalDifferentials":["Rotator cuff tear or tendinopathy","Anterior or posterior glenohumeral instability","Long head of biceps tendinopathy or subluxation","Acromioclavicular joint pathology","Subacromial impingement","Cervical radiculopathy referring to the shoulder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs to exclude bony and other joint pathology","Magnetic resonance arthrography of the shoulder","Standard MRI where arthrography is unavailable","Diagnostic arthroscopy as the definitive assessment","Assessment for glenohumeral internal rotation deficit in throwers"],"redFlags":["Recurrent frank dislocation suggesting significant instability rather than an isolated labral tear","Rapidly progressive weakness or a mass suggesting biceps rupture or tumour","Night pain and constitutional symptoms prompting exclusion of neoplasia or infection"],"prognosis":"Many SLAP lesions, particularly in older patients or as incidental findings, respond to rehabilitation; surgically treated type II tears in young athletes have variable return-to-sport rates, and outcomes of repair versus biceps tenodesis depend on age, activity and associated pathology.","pitfalls":["Over-diagnosing symptomatic SLAP tears from MRI when age-related labral change is incidental","Attempting labral repair in older patients in whom biceps tenodesis or non-operative care gives better results","Missing associated rotator cuff or instability lesions that drive symptoms","Relying on any single provocative test given their limited specificity"],"management":["Offer first-line structured physiotherapy targeting rotator cuff and scapular strengthening, posterior capsular stretching and correction of glenohumeral internal rotation deficit in throwers","Provide analgesia and activity or throwing modification, with a corticosteroid injection for symptom control","Undertake arthroscopic labral repair for symptomatic unstable type II tears in young athletes who fail rehabilitation","Prefer biceps tenodesis or tenotomy in older patients or where degenerative change accompanies the tear","Address concomitant pathology such as rotator cuff tears or a paralabral cyst at the same procedure","Guide graduated return-to-sport rehabilitation with realistic counselling on the variable recovery of throwing performance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Review of Orthopaedics","Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"slap_tear":{"name":"slap tear","system":"musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["superior labrum anterior to posterior lesion","superior labral tear"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Matsen's The Shoulder","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A SLAP lesion is an injury to the superior labrum extending anterior to posterior around the biceps anchor at the supraglenoid tubercle.","Two dominant mechanisms are recognised: a fall onto an outstretched or abducted arm (compression), and repetitive overhead throwing generating a peel-back force on the biceps-labral complex.","The Snyder classification describes four principal types, ranging from fraying to bucket-handle detachment with biceps anchor involvement and extension into adjacent labrum.","Symptoms are often vague deep-seated pain, clicking or catching, and loss of throwing velocity, making clinical diagnosis difficult.","Provocative tests such as O'Brien active compression, the biceps load and dynamic labral shear tests aid diagnosis but individually lack accuracy, so combine several.","A key mimic and normal variant is the sublabral foramen and Buford complex; misinterpreting these leads to overdiagnosis.","In older patients an incidental degenerate superior labral change is common and repairing it may worsen stiffness, so treat the symptomatic lesion, not the image.","Biceps tenodesis or tenotomy is often preferred over labral repair in older patients and those with biceps pathology, whereas anatomical repair suits younger overhead athletes with true unstable lesions."],"pathophysiology":"The superior labrum anchors the long head of biceps tendon at the supraglenoid tubercle and contributes to glenohumeral stability by deepening the socket. SLAP lesions arise when this biceps-labral complex is disrupted, either by an acute traction or compression load or by repetitive microtrauma. In overhead throwers, the late cocking position generates a posterosuperior peel-back force that progressively strips the superior labrum from the glenoid; associated posterior capsular tightness and glenohumeral internal rotation deficit shift the contact point and amplify this stress. Acute injuries typically follow a fall on an outstretched arm or a sudden traction event. An unstable superior labrum destabilises the biceps anchor, alters humeral head kinematics and can produce pseudolaxity, pain and mechanical symptoms; with time degenerative fraying accumulates independently of a discrete injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Rotator cuff tear or tendinopathy","Subacromial impingement syndrome","Internal (posterosuperior) impingement in the overhead athlete","Glenohumeral instability","Biceps tendinopathy or instability of the long head","Normal labral variants (sublabral foramen, Buford complex)","Paralabral cyst causing suprascapular nerve compression"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MR arthrography, the most accurate imaging for superior labral pathology","Standard MRI to assess the cuff, biceps and any paralabral cyst","Plain radiographs to exclude bony and degenerative causes","Diagnostic arthroscopy for direct assessment and dynamic peel-back testing","Assessment for glenohumeral internal rotation deficit and posterior capsular tightness","Electrophysiological studies if suprascapular neuropathy from a paralabral cyst is suspected"],"redFlags":["Associated large rotator cuff tear with significant weakness","Features of a paralabral cyst compressing the suprascapular nerve with infraspinatus wasting","Mechanical locking suggesting a displaced bucket-handle labral fragment","Coexisting instability with recurrent subluxation or dislocation"],"prognosis":"Younger overhead athletes with a true unstable lesion can achieve good pain relief with repair, though return to the previous level of throwing is less predictable. In older patients and those with degenerative changes, biceps tenodesis or tenotomy generally provides more reliable relief than labral repair.","pitfalls":["Overdiagnosing a SLAP tear by mistaking normal labral variants for pathology.","Repairing a degenerate superior labrum in an older patient and precipitating stiffness.","Relying on a single provocative test given their limited individual accuracy.","Failing to address associated internal impingement, cuff pathology or a paralabral cyst."],"management":["Start with non-operative management using physiotherapy that strengthens the scapular stabilisers and rotator cuff and restores internal rotation, with posterior capsular stretching","Provide activity modification, analgesia and a selective corticosteroid injection","Perform arthroscopic repair of a true unstable superior labral tear in younger overhead athletes","Prefer biceps tenodesis or tenotomy for older patients and those with degenerative labral change, as these give more reliable relief","Treat associated lesions and decompress a symptomatic paralabral cyst causing suprascapular neuropathy","Avoid over-treatment of normal variants such as the sublabral foramen and Buford complex","Progress rehabilitation with a staged return to throwing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Matsen's The Shoulder","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics","Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Trauma"],"crossLinks":[]},"slc13a5_epilepsy":{"name":"SLC13A5 (citrate transporter) epilepsy","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SLC13A5","SLC13A5 deficiency","citrate transporter epilepsy","NaCT deficiency","EIEE25","Kohlschutter-Tonz-like phenotype"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / ILAE","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic variants in SLC13A5, which encodes the sodium-dependent citrate transporter NaCT that imports citrate into neurons for energy metabolism and biosynthesis","Seizures classically begin in the neonatal period, often within the first days of life, and are frequently multifocal and difficult to control","A characteristic clue is markedly elevated citrate in the cerebrospinal fluid, and sometimes in plasma, reflecting impaired neuronal citrate uptake","Tooth abnormalities, particularly amelogenesis imperfecta with yellow-brown hypoplastic enamel, are a distinctive extra-neural feature","Development is typically delayed with hypotonia and later movement difficulties, though some children retain relative cognitive strengths compared with other neonatal epileptic encephalopathies","The ketogenic diet has been reported to help seizure control in some patients by providing an alternative energy substrate, aligning with the metabolic nature of the disorder","Autosomal recessive inheritance means recurrence risk and carrier testing should be addressed with the family"],"pathophysiology":"SLC13A5 encodes NaCT, a plasma-membrane sodium-coupled citrate transporter that moves extracellular citrate into neurons, where citrate feeds the tricarboxylic acid cycle, fatty acid and cholesterol synthesis, and neurotransmitter production. Loss of transporter function limits neuronal citrate availability, disturbing cellular energy metabolism and biosynthetic pathways and leaving citrate to accumulate in the cerebrospinal fluid. The resulting metabolic derangement impairs neuronal function and lowers the seizure threshold, and the same energy-substrate deficiency provides the rationale for ketogenic dietary therapy that supplies ketone bodies as an alternative fuel.","additionalDifferentials":["KCNQ2 and KCNQ3 neonatal epileptic encephalopathy","Pyridoxine-dependent and pyridoxal-phosphate-responsive epilepsies","Glucose transporter type 1 (GLUT1) deficiency","Neonatal-onset mitochondrial and other metabolic epilepsies","STXBP1 and SCN2A neonatal encephalopathies","Kohlschutter-Tonz syndrome (ROGDI) given the enamel-epilepsy overlap"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gene panel or whole-exome sequencing confirming biallelic SLC13A5 variants","Cerebrospinal fluid and plasma citrate measurement, looking for characteristic elevation","Prolonged electroencephalography to characterise neonatal seizures","Brain magnetic resonance imaging to exclude structural and other metabolic causes","Trial of vitamin-responsive epilepsy treatments while awaiting results","Dental assessment for amelogenesis imperfecta"],"redFlags":["Refractory neonatal status epilepticus","Recurrent seizure clusters with developmental stagnation","Feeding difficulty and poor growth complicating dietary therapy","Signs of intercurrent metabolic decompensation during illness"],"prognosis":"Seizures often improve beyond the neonatal and early infantile period, and some children achieve reasonable control, but most have persistent developmental impairment; outcomes are variable and a subset respond well to dietary therapy.","pitfalls":["Overlooking cerebrospinal fluid citrate as a diagnostic clue in neonatal seizures","Not trialling vitamin-responsive treatments before a genetic diagnosis is confirmed","Failing to consider the ketogenic diet given the metabolic basis of the disorder","Missing the associated enamel defects and dental care needs"],"management":["Trial ketogenic dietary therapy to supply ketone bodies as an alternative neuronal fuel, bypassing the impaired citrate uptake","Control the multifocal neonatal-onset seizures with antiseizure medication tailored to response","Provide supportive developmental care with physiotherapy and occupational and speech therapy for hypotonia and motor and developmental delay","Arrange dental surveillance and restorative treatment of the associated amelogenesis imperfecta","Institute an emergency seizure and rescue plan with heightened monitoring during intercurrent illness and status epilepticus","Offer genetic counselling and family testing given autosomal recessive biallelic inheritance","Follow up through a multidisciplinary neurology, metabolic and neurodevelopmental team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: SLC13A5 Deficiency (Citrate Transporter Disorder)","ILAE resources on metabolic and neonatal epileptic encephalopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"slc35a2_cdg":{"name":"SLC35A2-CDG (UDP-galactose transporter)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SLC35A2","UDP-galactose transporter deficiency","congenital disorder of glycosylation type IIm","CDG-IIm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked CDG caused by defective transport of UDP-galactose into the Golgi, impairing galactosylation of N-glycans","Presents chiefly as an early-onset epileptic encephalopathy, frequently with infantile spasms and hypsarrhythmia, with developmental delay and hypotonia","Because it is X-linked with somatic mosaicism, phenotypic severity varies and the transferrin pattern can normalise with age, so a normal screen does not exclude it","Transferrin analysis, when abnormal, shows a type II pattern reflecting loss of terminal galactose from glycan antennae","It is one of the potentially treatable CDG subtypes, in which oral galactose supplementation may improve glycosylation and, in some patients, seizures and biochemical markers","Coagulation, endocrine and dysmorphic features are generally milder than in classic PMM2-CDG","Diagnosis rests on SLC35A2 sequencing, increasingly identified through epilepsy gene panels and exome sequencing"],"pathophysiology":"SLC35A2 encodes the Golgi UDP-galactose transporter that supplies the nucleotide sugar donor for galactosyltransferases. Impaired transport starves the Golgi of UDP-galactose, so galactose is not added to maturing complex N-glycans, leaving truncated antennae and defining a type II processing defect on transferrin analysis. Because the gene is X-linked and the disorder often arises from somatic mosaic variants, the degree of hypoglycosylation and its clinical impact are variable. Providing galactose can partially bypass the transport bottleneck, which underlies the observed biochemical and sometimes clinical response.","additionalDifferentials":["Other early-onset epileptic encephalopathies (e.g. genetic developmental and epileptic encephalopathies)","Other type II CDG subtypes such as TMEM165-CDG and B4GALT1-CDG","PMM2-CDG and other type I disorders when the transferrin pattern is intermediate","Structural or metabolic causes of infantile spasms","Mitochondrial encephalopathy","Glucose transporter and other treatable metabolic epilepsies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transferrin isoelectric focusing or mass spectrometry (may be normal, so does not exclude the diagnosis)","SLC35A2 molecular genetic testing, including assessment for somatic mosaicism","Electroencephalography for hypsarrhythmia or other epileptic patterns","Neuroimaging for structural or malformative changes","Developmental and growth assessment","Trial of galactose supplementation with biochemical monitoring in confirmed cases"],"redFlags":["Infantile spasms or drug-resistant seizures requiring prompt evaluation","Developmental regression or plateau","Failure to thrive with feeding difficulty","A normal transferrin screen in a child with a compatible phenotype, which should not stop genetic testing"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable and generally dominated by the severity of the epilepsy and developmental delay; some patients show biochemical and clinical improvement with galactose, though epilepsy can remain difficult to control. Milder mosaic presentations exist.","pitfalls":["Excluding SLC35A2-CDG on the basis of a normal transferrin test","Missing somatic mosaicism if only standard blood testing is performed","Failing to consider a galactose trial in confirmed cases","Attributing the epilepsy solely to a primary channelopathy without metabolic evaluation"],"management":["Give oral galactose supplementation to partially bypass the transport defect and improve N-glycan galactosylation, with biochemical monitoring","Control the epileptic encephalopathy with antiseizure medication and standard treatment of infantile spasms using hormonal therapy or vigabatrin","Provide developmental support with physiotherapy and occupational and speech therapy for delay and hypotonia","Monitor transferrin glycoforms and clinical response to galactose, recognising that the transferrin pattern may normalise with age","Offer genetic counselling addressing X-linked inheritance and somatic mosaicism","Coordinate multidisciplinary metabolic, neurology and neurodevelopmental follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID, congenital disorders of glycosylation","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics, disorders of glycosylation"],"crossLinks":[]},"slc35c1_cdg":{"name":"SLC35C1-CDG (leukocyte adhesion deficiency type II)","system":"Metabolic / Glycosylation & peroxisomal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CDG-IIc","LAD II","GDP-fucose transporter deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Immunology and metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["SLC35C1 encodes the Golgi GDP-fucose transporter; its deficiency impairs fucosylation of glycoproteins and glycolipids, giving both a type IIc congenital disorder of glycosylation and leukocyte adhesion deficiency type II.","Defective synthesis of the fucosylated selectin ligand sialyl-Lewis X abolishes leukocyte rolling along activated endothelium, so neutrophils cannot extravasate to sites of infection.","This produces recurrent bacterial infections that characteristically lack pus, together with a persistent, marked peripheral neutrophilic leukocytosis.","Absence of the fucosylated H antigen results in the rare Bombay blood-group phenotype.","Non-immune features are prominent and often dominate: severe growth restriction, microcephaly, marked intellectual disability and a distinctive facies.","Some patients improve clinically and biochemically with oral fucose supplementation, which drives the salvage pathway to restore selectin-ligand expression and reduce infections, though the response is mutation-dependent.","Unlike leukocyte adhesion deficiency type I, the defect lies in the rolling step of the adhesion cascade rather than in firm integrin-mediated adhesion, and delayed umbilical cord separation is not characteristic."],"pathophysiology":"SLC35C1 is the nucleotide-sugar transporter that moves GDP-fucose from the cytosol into the Golgi lumen, where fucosyltransferases add fucose to glycans. Loss of function starves the Golgi of its fucose donor, so cells cannot synthesise fucosylated structures including sialyl-Lewis X, the ligand for E- and P-selectins, and the H and Lewis blood-group antigens. Without sialyl-Lewis X, leukocytes fail to tether and roll along activated endothelium, blocking the initial step of the leukocyte adhesion cascade and preventing recruitment to sites of infection despite a brisk marrow response, which explains the leukocytosis without pus. Widespread hypofucosylation of other glycoproteins underlies the growth failure and neurodevelopmental impairment. Because the fucose salvage pathway can regenerate GDP-fucose, exogenous fucose partially bypasses the defect in responsive individuals. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type I (ITGB2 / CD18 defect)","Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type III (FERMT3 / kindlin-3 defect)","Chronic granulomatous disease and other neutrophil-function defects","Congenital or cyclical neutropenia (here counts are conspicuously high rather than low)","Other congenital disorders of glycosylation with growth failure and developmental delay","Hyper-IgE and other recurrent-infection syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and film showing persistent marked neutrophilic leukocytosis","Flow cytometry for sialyl-Lewis X (CD15s) on leukocytes","Blood group typing to detect the Bombay phenotype","SLC35C1 molecular genetic testing","Serum transferrin isoelectric focusing (a type II pattern in some patients)","Neutrophil adhesion and rolling functional studies in a specialist laboratory","Supervised trial of oral fucose with monitoring of counts and selectin-ligand expression"],"redFlags":["Recurrent severe bacterial infection without abscess or pus formation","Persistent extreme leukocytosis mistaken for a leukaemoid reaction or malignancy","Aggressive periodontitis and non-healing soft-tissue infection","Failure to thrive with neurodevelopmental regression"],"prognosis":"Susceptibility to infection may attenuate somewhat with age and can improve on fucose supplementation in responsive individuals, but the neurodevelopmental disability and growth impairment are generally persistent.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the persistent leukocytosis for a haematological malignancy or leukaemoid reaction","Overlooking the diagnosis because infections lack the expected purulent response","Failing to offer a supervised fucose trial in confirmed cases","Confusing the rolling defect of type II disease with the firm-adhesion CD18 defect of type I"],"management":["Give oral fucose supplementation to drive the salvage pathway, restore sialyl-Lewis X expression and reduce infections in responsive individuals","Treat bacterial infections promptly with appropriate antibiotics and consider antibiotic prophylaxis given the impaired neutrophil recruitment","Keep immunisations up to date and reinforce infection-prevention measures","Alert the blood bank to the Bombay phenotype to ensure compatible transfusion","Provide nutritional support for the growth restriction and developmental therapies for the intellectual disability and microcephaly","Monitor neutrophil counts, selectin-ligand expression and the biochemical response to fucose","Offer genetic counselling and family testing for autosomal recessive inheritance within multidisciplinary immunology and metabolic care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Immunology references","Congenital disorders of glycosylation review literature","Inherited metabolic disease textbooks"],"crossLinks":[]},"slc39a8_cdg":{"name":"SLC39A8-CDG (ZIP8 Deficiency, CDG Type IIn)","system":"Metabolic / Glycosylation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SLC39A8-CDG","ZIP8 deficiency","CDG-IIn","manganese transporter ZIP8 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Congenital disorders of glycosylation references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive type II congenital disorder of glycosylation caused by biallelic SLC39A8 variants, which encode the divalent metal transporter ZIP8 that supplies manganese and zinc to cells.","Manganese is an obligate cofactor for several Golgi glycosyltransferases, notably beta-1,4-galactosyltransferase, so cellular manganese deficiency drives a secondary defect of galactosylation and sialylation.","Severe infantile presentation with developmental delay, hypotonia, seizures, dystonia and cerebellar atrophy, sometimes with a Leigh-like picture.","Low whole-blood or serum manganese is a key diagnostic clue that distinguishes it from most other glycosylation disorders.","Cranial and skeletal features, short stature and sensorineural hearing loss may be present.","It is one of the rare treatable CDGs: oral galactose and carefully monitored manganese supplementation can improve glycosylation and clinical status in some patients.","Transferrin isoelectric focusing shows a type II pattern with loss of terminal galactose and sialic acid residues."],"pathophysiology":"SLC39A8 encodes ZIP8 (Zrt/Irt-like protein 8), a transmembrane transporter that mediates cellular uptake of manganese and zinc. Biallelic hypomorphic variants reduce intracellular manganese, and because manganese is the essential cofactor of several Golgi glycosyltransferases, in particular beta-1,4-galactosyltransferase and the enzymes adding terminal galactose and sialic acid, N- and O-glycan maturation fails and a type II pattern of hypoglycosylation results. Systemic manganese deficiency also impairs other manganese-dependent enzymes such as arginase and mitochondrial superoxide dismutase, and the combination of hypoglycosylation with broader manganese-dependent metabolic disturbance underlies the neurodevelopmental, cerebellar and skeletal phenotype; supplementation that restores intracellular manganese can partially correct the galactosylation defect.","additionalDifferentials":["TMEM165-CDG (Golgi manganese/calcium homeostasis defect that is also galactose-responsive)","SLC35A2-CDG and other type II galactosylation/sialylation glycosylation disorders","SLC39A14-related manganese-transport disorder (manganese overload with dystonia-parkinsonism, the mirror-image transporter defect)","Congenital cerebellar atrophy syndromes and Leigh syndrome / primary mitochondrial disease","ATP6-group glycosylation disorders presenting with epilepsy and dystonia","SLC30A10-related hypermanganesaemia as a contrasting manganese disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and whole-blood manganese (typically low) and zinc","Serum transferrin isoelectric focusing / glycoform analysis (type II pattern)","Molecular testing of SLC39A8 with a CDG panel or exome sequencing","Brain MRI (cerebellar atrophy and, in some, Leigh-like changes)","N-glycan mass spectrometry demonstrating undergalactosylation","Monitored trial of galactose and manganese supplementation with serial transferrin glycoform assessment"],"redFlags":["Rapid neurological deterioration or Leigh-like metabolic decompensation","Refractory seizures in an infant with hypotonia and dystonia","Manganese supplementation given without monitoring, risking manganese neurotoxicity and overload","Progressive loss of motor and cerebellar function"],"prognosis":"The natural history is severe with substantial neurodisability, but a subset of patients show meaningful biochemical and clinical improvement with galactose and carefully monitored manganese supplementation, so early recognition is worthwhile.","pitfalls":["Missing the low manganese because it is not part of routine metabolic panels.","Giving manganese supplementation without monitoring, risking manganese accumulation and toxicity.","Dismissing the type II transferrin pattern as non-specific and not pursuing the specific gene.","Confusing it with the SLC39A14 and SLC30A10 manganese-overload disorders, which require the opposite (chelation) approach."],"management":["Give carefully monitored oral manganese supplementation to restore the cofactor for Golgi glycosyltransferases, combined with galactose supplementation to improve glycosylation","Undertake serial monitoring of whole-blood manganese and transferrin glycoforms to guide dosing and avoid manganese toxicity","Control seizures with antiseizure medication and manage dystonia for the neurological features","Provide rehabilitative support with physiotherapy and occupational and speech therapy for hypotonia and developmental delay","Arrange audiology surveillance and hearing support for sensorineural hearing loss, with neuroimaging follow-up of cerebellar or Leigh-like changes","Offer genetic counselling and family testing given autosomal recessive inheritance within multidisciplinary metabolic and neurology care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Physician's Guide to the Diagnosis, Treatment and Follow-up of Inherited Metabolic Diseases","Congenital disorders of glycosylation: clinical and diagnostic reviews","Trace-element and manganese metabolism reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"slc6a1_disorder":{"name":"SLC6A1-related disorder (GAT1)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SLC6A1","GAT1 deficiency","GABA transporter 1 disorder","SLC6A1-related myoclonic-atonic epilepsy"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents with mild to moderate intellectual disability and a characteristic epilepsy beginning in early childhood","Myoclonic-atonic epilepsy (Doose-like), atypical absences and generalised seizures are the usual pattern","Early development may be relatively preserved before seizure onset, followed by developmental slowing or regression","Autism spectrum features, behavioural difficulties and hypotonia are frequently present","Caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in SLC6A1, usually de novo and autosomal dominant, encoding the GABA transporter GAT1","EEG commonly shows generalised spike-and-wave discharges consistent with a generalised epilepsy","Movement problems such as ataxia and tremor may accompany the epilepsy"],"pathophysiology":"SLC6A1 encodes GAT1, the principal transporter that clears the inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA from the synaptic cleft into neurons and astrocytes, terminating inhibitory signalling and recycling GABA. Heterozygous loss-of-function variants reduce transporter function through haploinsufficiency or dominant-negative effects, disturbing the normal spatial and temporal control of GABAergic inhibition. Dysregulated inhibitory neurotransmission destabilises cortical and thalamocortical networks, producing the generalised epilepsy, and impairs the balance of excitation and inhibition needed for normal cognitive development. The resulting picture combines myoclonic-atonic epilepsy with intellectual disability and autistic features.","additionalDifferentials":["Myoclonic-atonic epilepsy (Doose syndrome) of other cause","Childhood absence epilepsy","Lennox-Gastaut syndrome","SYNGAP1-related disorder","Other genetic generalised epilepsies with intellectual disability","Autism spectrum disorder with epilepsy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["EEG to characterise generalised spike-and-wave and other epileptiform activity","Brain MRI, usually normal or non-specific","Neuropsychological and autism assessment","Gene panel or exome sequencing including SLC6A1","Chromosomal microarray to detect deletions involving the locus"],"redFlags":["Frequent drop attacks or myoclonic-atonic seizures causing falls","Developmental regression coinciding with seizure onset","Status epilepticus, including atypical absence status","Behavioural deterioration affecting safety"],"prognosis":"Most individuals have mild to moderate intellectual disability with epilepsy that is variably responsive to treatment; seizures may improve over time in some. Long-term outcome depends on seizure control and cognitive impact, and management is supportive and multidisciplinary.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing generic Doose syndrome and not testing for SLC6A1","Overlooking pre-seizure developmental concerns because early milestones seemed adequate","Assuming a normal MRI excludes a genetic epilepsy","Using vigabatrin-type strategies without recognising the underlying GABAergic transport defect"],"management":["Control the myoclonic-atonic and absence seizures with broad-spectrum antiseizure medication such as valproate, ethosuximide or lamotrigine, avoiding agents that aggravate generalised seizures","Provide neurodevelopmental support with special education, behavioural therapy and speech and language therapy for the intellectual disability and autism features","Offer physiotherapy and occupational therapy for hypotonia and motor difficulties","Consider ketogenic dietary therapy in drug-resistant seizures","Undertake regular EEG monitoring and review of seizure control over time","Offer genetic counselling for the usually de novo autosomal dominant variant, with parental testing and recurrence-risk advice, within multidisciplinary paediatric neurology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: SLC6A1-Related Disorder","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"slc6a1_epilepsy":{"name":"SLC6A1-Related Epilepsy","system":"Neurology / Developmental epileptic encephalopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SLC6A1-related disorder","GAT-1 deficiency","SLC6A1 myoclonic-atonic epilepsy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["SLC6A1 encodes GAT-1, the principal GABA transporter; heterozygous variants impair clearance of synaptic GABA and disturb the balance of phasic and tonic inhibition.","The classic phenotype is myoclonic-atonic epilepsy (Doose syndrome) with onset in early childhood, frequently after a period of normal development.","Absence seizures (typical and atypical), myoclonic, atonic (drop attacks) and generalised tonic-clonic seizures are all characteristic.","EEG typically shows generalised spike-and-wave with background slowing; photosensitivity may be seen.","Intellectual disability, autism spectrum features and language delay are common and often become apparent around the time seizures begin.","Most pathogenic variants are de novo missense or truncating changes acting through haploinsufficiency, with some misfolded proteins exerting a dominant-negative effect.","Broad-spectrum agents effective in generalised epilepsy (including valproate) are generally preferred, and behavioural or gait regression may accompany seizure onset.","Ataxia, tremor and fine-motor difficulty can coexist with the seizure disorder."],"pathophysiology":"SLC6A1 on chromosome 3p25 encodes GAT-1 (GABA transporter type 1), a sodium- and chloride-dependent transporter expressed on presynaptic GABAergic terminals and astrocytes that reuptakes GABA from the synaptic cleft, terminating inhibitory neurotransmission and recycling the transmitter. Heterozygous loss-of-function or missense variants, usually arising de novo, cause haploinsufficiency, and several misfolded mutant subunits additionally trap wild-type protein in the endoplasmic reticulum to produce a dominant-negative effect. The resulting derangement of GABA reuptake dysregulates both phasic (synaptic) and tonic (extrasynaptic) inhibition, generating cortical network hyperexcitability and abnormal thalamocortical oscillations that manifest as generalised seizures (especially absence and myoclonic-atonic types) together with impaired neurodevelopment.","additionalDifferentials":["Other GABAergic genetic generalised epilepsies (GABRG2, GABRA1, GABRB3)","SLC2A1 (GLUT1) deficiency syndrome","Myoclonic-atonic epilepsy of other or unknown cause","Childhood and juvenile absence epilepsy","Lennox-Gastaut syndrome","SCN1A-related epilepsy (Dravet spectrum)","CHD2-related epilepsy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Epilepsy gene panel or exome sequencing including SLC6A1","Prolonged or video EEG to capture generalised spike-wave and seizure semiology","MRI brain (usually normal; excludes structural causes)","Paired CSF and plasma glucose if GLUT1 deficiency is considered (qualitative)","Neurodevelopmental and autism spectrum assessment","Chromosomal microarray if a copy-number variant is suspected"],"redFlags":["Developmental regression or plateau coinciding with seizure onset","Non-convulsive status epilepticus (absence or atonic status) causing prolonged confusion or repeated falls","Frequent atonic drop attacks with injury risk","Emergence of new seizure types or marked EEG deterioration"],"prognosis":"Seizures often become more controllable after early childhood and may remit in some, but intellectual disability, autism and language impairment usually persist; overall outcome varies with seizure control and the degree of developmental involvement.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying as idiopathic childhood absence epilepsy and missing the genetic developmental diagnosis","Aggravating generalised seizures with sodium-channel-blocking agents","Attributing developmental difficulties solely to seizures rather than the underlying genetic disorder","Using a normal MRI to wrongly exclude a genetic epileptic encephalopathy"],"management":["Treat the absence, myoclonic and atonic seizures with broad-spectrum antiseizure medication such as valproate, ethosuximide, lamotrigine or benzodiazepines, avoiding sodium-channel blockers that may worsen generalised seizures","Consider ketogenic dietary therapy in drug-resistant cases","Provide neurodevelopmental and educational support for the intellectual disability and language delay","Arrange autism spectrum assessment and behavioural interventions","Offer physiotherapy for hypotonia and undertake regular EEG and developmental review","Offer genetic counselling for the usually de novo autosomal dominant variant with recurrence-risk discussion, within multidisciplinary paediatric neurology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics and epilepsy reference texts","Paediatric neurology reference texts","Epilepsy genetics review literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"sleep_apnea":{"name":"Sleep Apnea","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["obstructive sleep apnea","OSA","central sleep apnea"],"chapter":308,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2277-2282","clinicalPearls":["OSA diagnosis requires either symptoms of nocturnal breathing disturbance (snoring, snorting, gasping, breathing pauses) impairing quality of life or unexplained daytime sleepiness/fatigue, PLUS at least 5 obstructive apnoeas/hypopnoeas per hour of sleep (apnoea-hypopnoea index, AHI) on a sleep study; OSA may also be diagnosed without symptoms if AHI is at least 15 episodes/h (p.2277-2278).","Each apnoea/hypopnoea is a reduction in breathing for at least 10 s, commonly causing a fall in oxygen saturation of at least 3% or a cortical arousal (p.2278).","The major risk factors for OSA are obesity, male sex, and older age; roughly 40-60% of cases are attributable to excess weight, but the absence of obesity does NOT exclude the diagnosis (p.2278).","Snoring is the most common symptom, but its absence does NOT exclude OSA because pharyngeal collapse can occur without tissue vibration; the most common daytime symptom is excessive sleepiness, though women preferentially report fatigue rather than sleepiness (p.2279).","Dyspnoea is unusual in OSA, and its absence generally distinguishes OSA from paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, nocturnal asthma, and acid reflux with laryngospasm (p.2279).","Overnight in-laboratory polysomnography (PSG) is the gold standard for diagnosis; a negative in-lab PSG usually rules out OSA, but false-negatives can occur with night-to-night variation, insufficient REM sleep, or less supine sleep than typical (p.2279).","Home sleep tests recording only respiratory and cardiac channels are a cost-effective option; when prior probability of OSA is high, a negative home study should be followed by PSG (p.2279).","CPAP is the standard medical therapy with the highest level of evidence; it acts as a mechanical splint to hold the airway open during sleep, and auto-titrating (APAP) home devices have eliminated the need for titration studies in many patients (p.2281).","Although CPAP reduces 24-h ambulatory blood pressure, the overall effect is modest (averaging 2-4 mmHg), with larger improvements in patients with high AHI, daytime sleepiness, or resistant hypertension (p.2280-2281).","Large randomized trials have FAILED to show that CPAP reduces cardiovascular/stroke events or prolongs survival, possibly because sleepy patients (who may have the greatest risk) were excluded and because of limited treatment adherence (p.2281).","Central sleep apnoea (CSA) is less common than OSA and may occur alone or, more often, combined with obstructive events as mixed apnoeas; it is often driven by increased sensitivity to PCO2 causing unstable breathing, and a prolonged circulation delay (as in congestive heart failure) produces the crescendo-decrescendo Cheyne-Stokes pattern (p.2282).","Treatment of CSA has NOT been shown to improve long-term health outcomes, so specific treatment is generally indicated only for symptomatic CSA; unlike OSA, treatment is difficult and depends on the underlying cause (p.2282)."],"pathophysiology":"In OSA the pharyngeal airway lacks fixed bony/cartilaginous support, so patency depends on pharyngeal dilator muscles. Inspiration generates increasingly negative intraluminal pressure (a suctioning force); at sleep onset neuromuscular output to these muscles declines, and in a collapsible airway this causes transient pharyngeal collapse (apnoea) or near-collapse (hypopnoea), typically terminated by an arousal that restores muscle activity. Collapse sites include the soft palate (most common), tongue base, lateral pharyngeal walls, and epiglottis. OSA is often worst in REM sleep (lowest skeletal-muscle output) and supine (gravity). A small pharyngeal lumen (soft-tissue enlargement from fat, lymphoid tissue, or genetic variation; craniofacial factors such as mandibular retroposition/micrognathia), low lung volume (reduced caudal pharyngeal traction, pronounced in obesity), and high nasal resistance all predispose to collapse. Pharyngeal muscle activation is linked to ventilatory drive: an over-sensitive ventilatory control system, a low arousal threshold (preempting CO2-mediated muscle compensation), or a high arousal threshold (prolonging apnoeas) all contribute. In OSA-related disease, sleep fragmentation, cortical arousals, and intermittent hypoxaemia/hypercapnia drive sympathetic overactivity, blood-pressure surges, a prothrombotic/proinflammatory state, insulin resistance, and large negative intrathoracic pressure swings that alter cardiac preload/afterload and promote cardiac/electrical remodelling. CSA is usually caused by increased PCO2 sensitivity producing hyperventilation alternating with apnoea, plus prolonged circulatory delay (heart failure) giving Cheyne-Stokes breathing.","additionalDifferentials":["Narcolepsy (multiple sleep latency / maintenance of wakefulness testing helps distinguish from OSA)","Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea","Nocturnal asthma","Acid reflux with laryngospasm","Insomnia (common in the general population, may coexist with OSA)","Obesity-hypoventilation / hypoventilation syndromes","Periodic limb movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea","Nocturnal asthma","Acid reflux with laryngospasm","Narcolepsy","Cheyne-Stokes / central sleep apnoea of heart failure"],"additionalInvestigations":["Overnight in-laboratory polysomnography (PSG) - gold standard","Home sleep test (respiratory and cardiac channels) for cost-effective diagnosis","Apnoea-hypopnoea index (AHI) and respiratory disturbance index (RDI) quantification","Overnight oximetry profile (mean, nadir, time at low saturation)","Multiple sleep latency test / maintenance of wakefulness test (quantify sleepiness, distinguish narcolepsy)","Upper-airway imaging (cephalometry, MRI, CT) and fibreoptic endoscopy to identify anatomic risk factors (not routine; useful for surgical planning)","Cardiac evaluation for systolic/diastolic dysfunction or structural abnormality","Overnight blood-pressure monitoring (may show nondipping pattern)","Arterial blood gas (usually normal awake; waking hypoxaemia/hypercarbia suggests cardiopulmonary disease or hypoventilation)","Drug-induced sleep endoscopy (for hypoglossal-nerve stimulation candidacy)"],"redFlags":["Waking hypoxaemia or hypercarbia, suggesting coexisting cardiopulmonary disease or a hypoventilation syndrome","Evidence of cor pulmonale, suggesting a comorbid cardiopulmonary condition (OSA alone is NOT thought to cause right-heart failure)","Resistant or uncontrolled hypertension","Excessive daytime sleepiness with occupational accident or motor-vehicle-crash risk (drowsy driving)","Coexisting heart failure (raises risk of both OSA and CSA)","Severe nocturnal hypoxaemia with elevated haemoglobin"],"prognosis":"OSA is the most common medical cause of daytime sleepiness and reduces quality of life. It is strongly associated with cardiac, cerebrovascular and metabolic disease (coronary artery disease, heart failure with and without reduced EF, atrial and ventricular arrhythmias, atherosclerosis, stroke, diabetes), premature death, and increased risk of certain cancers. Untreated OSA carries a roughly twofold risk of occupational accidents and a two- to threefold increase in motor-vehicle crashes. CPAP improves alertness, mood, quality of life, blood pressure and insulin sensitivity, but randomized trials have NOT shown that CPAP reduces cardiovascular/stroke events or prolongs survival. CSA carries increased risk for heart failure and atrial fibrillation, and its treatment has not been shown to improve long-term outcomes; adaptive servoventilation showed no cardiovascular or mortality benefit and increased mortality in patients with LVEF below 45%.","pitfalls":["Assuming absence of snoring excludes OSA - pharyngeal collapse can occur without tissue vibration.","Assuming a normal weight excludes OSA - absence of obesity does NOT exclude the diagnosis.","Underrecognising OSA in women, who have a lower arousal threshold, shorter and more REM-predominant apnoeas, and tend to report fatigue/insomnia rather than classic sleepiness.","Relying on symptoms/signs to gauge severity - they do not accurately predict the severity of sleep-related breathing disturbance; objective measurement is required.","Treating a single negative home sleep study as definitive when prior probability is high - follow with PSG.","Attributing right-heart failure/cor pulmonale to OSA alone, which is not thought to cause it - look for a comorbid cardiopulmonary condition.","Using adaptive servoventilation in patients with CSA and LVEF below 45% (FDA warning following increased mortality).","Sedative-hypnotics have inconsistent effects and should be avoided in most patients with moderate-to-severe OSA; opioids worsen CSA in a dose-dependent way."],"management":["Institute continuous positive airway pressure as first-line therapy for moderate-to-severe obstructive sleep apnoea to splint the pharyngeal airway open","Advise weight reduction and lifestyle measures including avoidance of alcohol and sedatives and positional therapy to discourage supine sleep","Offer a mandibular advancement oral appliance for mild-to-moderate disease or where positive airway pressure is not tolerated","Consider upper-airway surgery such as tonsillectomy, uvulopalatopharyngoplasty or hypoglossal nerve stimulation in selected anatomically suitable patients","Treat associated cardiovascular and metabolic comorbidity and counsel on the risk of drowsy driving","Monitor treatment adherence and efficacy through follow-up and device data, with ENT, sleep and bariatric referral as indicated"],"severityClassification":"OSA severity by apnoea-hypopnoea index (AHI = apnoeas plus hypopnoeas per hour of sleep): mild OSAHS = AHI 5-14 events/h; moderate OSAHS = AHI 15-29 events/h; severe OSAHS = AHI at least 30 events/h. The respiratory disturbance index (RDI) additionally counts respiratory effort-related arousals (RERAs). Each AHI level can be further qualified by the degree of sleepiness and associated hypoxaemia (Table 308-2, p.2280).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2277-2282"],"crossLinks":["obesity","heart_failure","pulmonary_hypertension","atrial_fib"]},"sleep_related_eating_disorder":{"name":"Sleep-related eating disorder","system":"Sleep medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SRED","sleep-related eating"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / International Classification of Sleep Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Recurrent episodes of involuntary eating or drinking during partial arousals from sleep, with reduced or absent awareness and poor recall the next morning","Patients may consume high-calorie, peculiar or even inedible or toxic substances during episodes","Injuries can occur from handling kitchen equipment or eating while not fully awake","It is considered a non-REM parasomnia and frequently coexists with sleepwalking, restless legs syndrome and periodic limb movements","Sedative-hypnotics, particularly zolpidem, are well-recognised precipitants","It differs from night eating syndrome, in which the person is fully awake and consciously eats during nocturnal awakenings","Management targets any underlying parasomnia or restless legs, withdrawal of precipitating drugs, and a safe sleep and kitchen environment"],"pathophysiology":"Sleep-related eating disorder is regarded as a disorder of arousal, a non-REM parasomnia in which incomplete transition from deep sleep to wakefulness allows automatic feeding behaviour to occur while consciousness and memory systems remain largely offline. Conditions that fragment sleep, such as restless legs syndrome and periodic limb movements, and drugs that promote complex sleep-related behaviours, especially certain hypnotics, lower the threshold for these partial arousals and the associated eating.","additionalDifferentials":["Night eating syndrome with conscious nocturnal eating","Sleepwalking without eating","Nocturnal binge eating of a primary eating disorder","Nocturnal seizures with automatisms","Dissociative eating behaviour","Kleine-Levin syndrome with hyperphagia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Collateral history and a food diary documenting nocturnal eating and the degree of awareness","Review of medications, especially sedative-hypnotics such as zolpidem","Screening for restless legs syndrome and periodic limb movement disorder","Video-polysomnography when the diagnosis is unclear or seizures are suspected","Assessment for a coexisting daytime eating disorder or mood disorder"],"redFlags":["Ingestion of toxic or inedible substances","Injuries or fire risk from cooking during episodes","Features suggesting nocturnal seizures rather than a parasomnia","Significant weight gain or metabolic consequences"],"prognosis":"The disorder tends to be chronic if untreated, but many patients improve substantially once precipitating drugs are stopped and any underlying restless legs or arousal disorder is treated; safety measures reduce the risk of injury in the meantime.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with the fully conscious night eating syndrome","Overlooking zolpidem or another hypnotic as the trigger","Missing coexisting restless legs syndrome that is driving the arousals","Neglecting kitchen and home safety, leaving a risk of injury or fire"],"management":["Withdraw or switch precipitating drugs, particularly sedative-hypnotics such as zolpidem","Diagnose and treat underlying sleep disorders, including restless legs syndrome and periodic limb movements with dopaminergic agents, and any coexisting parasomnia","Institute sleep-environment safety measures, securing food, hazardous substances and kitchen appliances to prevent injury and harmful ingestion","Consider pharmacotherapy such as topiramate where episodes persist despite removal of precipitants","Optimise sleep hygiene and treat any comorbid mood or daytime eating disorder","Refer to sleep medicine and arrange video-polysomnography where the diagnosis is unclear or seizures are suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd edition (ICSD-3)","Principles and Practice of Sleep Medicine (Kryger, Roth, Dement)"],"crossLinks":["periodic_limb_movement_disorder"]},"sleep_related_hypermotor_epilepsy":{"name":"Sleep-related hypermotor epilepsy","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SHE","nocturnal frontal lobe epilepsy","autosomal dominant nocturnal frontal lobe epilepsy"],"chapter":null,"source":"American Academy of Sleep Medicine ICSD-3 / epilepsy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Stereotyped hyperkinetic or dystonic seizures arising predominantly from sleep, often in clusters through the night","Attacks are brief, sudden and highly stereotyped for a given patient, distinguishing them from parasomnias","Seizures frequently originate in the frontal lobe, though other cortical origins occur","An autosomal dominant form is linked to mutations in neuronal nicotinic acetylcholine receptor subunit genes and others","Events emerge mainly from non-REM sleep and the patient may be aware during or immediately after them","Video-EEG and video polysomnography help separate it from NREM parasomnias and REM sleep behaviour disorder"],"pathophysiology":"Sleep-related hypermotor epilepsy is a focal epilepsy in which seizures are strongly modulated by sleep, arising most often from mesial or other frontal regions and producing dramatic hyperkinetic, dystonic or ambulatory motor behaviour. In the inherited form, mutations affecting neuronal nicotinic acetylcholine receptors and related genes alter cholinergic modulation of the sleep-wake and arousal networks, lowering the threshold for seizures during non-REM sleep. The stereotypy and brevity of events reflect their epileptic rather than parasomnic origin.","additionalDifferentials":["NREM parasomnias (sleepwalking, sleep terrors, confusional arousals)","REM sleep behaviour disorder","Obstructive sleep apnoea with arousals","Nocturnal panic attacks","Paroxysmal nocturnal movement disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Video-EEG and video polysomnography to capture and characterise events","MRI of the brain to seek a structural focus","Detailed semiological description and event stereotypy assessment","Genetic evaluation where an autosomal dominant pattern is present","Family history of nocturnal events"],"redFlags":["Frequent nocturnal clusters causing injury or major sleep disruption","Daytime seizures or new focal neurological signs","A structural lesion on imaging","Diagnostic uncertainty between seizures and parasomnia"],"prognosis":"Seizures often respond to antiseizure medication, though a proportion are drug-resistant; the disorder is typically lifelong but many patients achieve good control.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing stereotyped nocturnal seizures as parasomnias or psychiatric events","Relying on routine daytime EEG, which is often normal","Overlooking a family history pointing to the inherited form","Failing to capture events with video for accurate classification"],"management":["Control the nocturnal focal seizures with antiseizure medication, with carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine often effective, titrated against the clustering of events","Confirm the diagnosis with video-EEG and video polysomnography to distinguish it from parasomnias and guide treatment","Evaluate for a resectable structural focus and consider epilepsy surgery in drug-resistant cases","Optimise sleep and avoid seizure precipitants such as sleep deprivation","Offer genetic counselling where an autosomal dominant neuronal nicotinic acetylcholine receptor form is identified","Provide multidisciplinary neurology and sleep medicine follow-up with counselling on driving and nocturnal safety"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["American Academy of Sleep Medicine ICSD-3","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"sleep_related_leg_cramps":{"name":"Sleep-related leg cramps","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nocturnal leg cramps"],"chapter":null,"source":"American Academy of Sleep Medicine ICSD-3","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sudden painful involuntary contractions, usually of the calf or small foot muscles, occurring during the sleep period","The cramp produces a palpably hard, tight muscle and is relieved by forceful stretching or massage of the affected muscle","Unlike restless legs syndrome, the problem is frank painful muscle contraction rather than an urge to move with relief on movement","Prevalence rises with age and cramps are common in pregnancy","Secondary contributors include dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, peripheral vascular or neurological disease and certain drugs","Management emphasises stretching, addressing reversible contributors and reviewing culprit medications"],"pathophysiology":"Sleep-related leg cramps arise from sustained involuntary hyperexcitable motor-unit discharge in a muscle, producing a sudden painful contraction; the tendency is increased by muscle shortening during sleep and by factors that alter neuromuscular excitability. Ageing, pregnancy, fluid and electrolyte shifts, and peripheral nerve or vascular disease can lower the threshold for these discharges. In many people the cramps are idiopathic, reflecting benign motor-neuron hyperexcitability rather than a defined underlying disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Restless legs syndrome","Periodic limb movement disorder","Peripheral neuropathy or radiculopathy","Peripheral arterial disease with claudication","Dystonia or myopathy with cramps","Electrolyte disturbance (for example low potassium, calcium or magnesium)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the description and relief by stretching","Review for dehydration, electrolyte disturbance and culprit medications","Assessment for peripheral vascular and neurological disease when frequent","Targeted blood tests where a secondary cause is suspected","Further neurological evaluation if cramps are severe, generalised or progressive"],"redFlags":["Cramps with progressive weakness, wasting or sensory loss","Features of significant peripheral arterial disease","Widespread or daytime cramps suggesting a systemic or neuromuscular disorder","Marked electrolyte abnormalities"],"prognosis":"The cramps are benign though disruptive to sleep; many respond to stretching and correction of reversible contributors, and idiopathic cases often fluctuate over time.","pitfalls":["Confusing leg cramps with restless legs syndrome and mismanaging them","Overlooking peripheral vascular or neurological disease when cramps are frequent","Not reviewing medications that can provoke cramps","Missing correctable dehydration or electrolyte disturbance"],"management":["Relieve the acute cramp by forceful passive stretching and massage of the affected calf or foot muscle","Recommend regular stretching of the calf and foot muscles before sleep as the first-line preventive measure","Identify and correct reversible contributors such as dehydration, electrolyte disturbance and peripheral vascular or neurological disease","Review and rationalise culprit medications","Consider magnesium for pregnancy-associated cramps and reserve quinine only for severe refractory cases given its toxicity, with careful risk counselling","Reassure about the benign nature and arrange further neurological evaluation if cramps are severe, generalised or progressive"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["American Academy of Sleep Medicine ICSD-3","Principles and Practice of Sleep Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"slipped_capital_femoral_epiphysis":{"name":"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCFE","SUFE","slipped upper femoral epiphysis","femoral head epiphysis slip"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A displacement of the proximal femoral epiphysis through the growth plate, typically in overweight adolescents around the time of the pubertal growth spurt","Presents with hip, groin, thigh, or referred knee pain and a limp; knee or thigh pain in an adolescent must always prompt examination of the hip","A characteristic sign is obligatory external rotation of the hip when it is passively flexed, with limited internal rotation","Radiographs should include a frog-lateral view, as subtle slips are best seen laterally; a line along the superior femoral neck (Klein's line) normally intersects the epiphysis","Slips are classified by symptom duration (acute, chronic, acute-on-chronic) and by whether the child can weight-bear (stable versus unstable), which strongly influences the risk of avascular necrosis","It is an orthopaedic urgency: once suspected the child should stop weight-bearing and be referred promptly, as further slipping worsens the outlook","Treatment is surgical stabilisation, usually in-situ pinning to prevent progression, and the contralateral hip may be pinned if at high risk","Consider an underlying endocrine or metabolic disorder (e.g. hypothyroidism, renal disease) in atypical, young, or bilateral cases"],"pathophysiology":"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis occurs when shear forces exceed the strength of the proximal femoral physis, allowing the epiphysis to displace, typically posteriorly and inferiorly, relative to the femoral neck (so the neck effectively rotates anteriorly and externally on the head). The physis is mechanically weakest during the adolescent growth spurt, and obesity increases the mechanical load, while hormonal factors influence physeal strength, explaining associations with endocrine disorders. In unstable slips the epiphyseal blood supply, which runs along the femoral neck, is at high risk of disruption, predisposing to avascular necrosis. Residual deformity from a healed slip alters femoral head-neck morphology and can cause femoroacetabular impingement and early osteoarthritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Perthes' disease","Transient synovitis of the hip","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Femoral neck stress fracture or trauma","Muscle strain or apophyseal avulsion","Referred pain from other hip or spinal pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anteroposterior and frog-lateral pelvic radiographs of both hips (the lateral view is key for subtle slips)","Assessment of Klein's line and the physeal appearance","MRI or ultrasound in equivocal cases or to assess a pre-slip","Endocrine and metabolic workup (e.g. thyroid function, renal function) in young, atypical, or bilateral presentations","Careful hip examination for loss of internal rotation and obligatory external rotation on flexion"],"redFlags":["SURGICAL URGENCY: suspected SCFE requires immediate non-weight-bearing and prompt referral to prevent further slip","Unstable slip (inability to weight-bear) carrying a high risk of avascular necrosis","Adolescent knee or thigh pain that could be referred from an undiagnosed hip slip","Bilateral or very young presentation suggesting an underlying endocrine or metabolic disorder"],"prognosis":"Stable slips treated promptly with in-situ fixation generally do well, whereas unstable slips carry a substantial risk of avascular necrosis and chondrolysis; even after successful fixation, residual femoral head-neck deformity can cause impingement and predispose to premature osteoarthritis, so long-term follow-up is important.","pitfalls":["Attributing knee or thigh pain to the knee and failing to image the hip, leading to missed or late diagnosis","Allowing continued weight-bearing on a suspected slip, risking further displacement and avascular necrosis","Missing a subtle slip by not obtaining a frog-lateral radiograph","Overlooking an underlying endocrine cause in atypical or bilateral cases"],"management":["Enforce immediate non-weight-bearing and urgent orthopaedic referral once the diagnosis is suspected, to prevent further slippage","Perform in-situ fixation with a single cannulated screw as the standard surgical treatment for stable slips","Manage unstable slips with urgent gentle reduction and fixation, counselling on the high risk of avascular necrosis","Consider prophylactic fixation of the contralateral hip in young children or those with an underlying endocrinopathy","Investigate and treat an underlying endocrine or metabolic disorder such as hypothyroidism or renal disease in atypical, young or bilateral presentations","Undertake surveillance for avascular necrosis, chondrolysis and residual femoroacetabular impingement, with corrective osteotomy or impingement surgery and long-term follow-up for premature osteoarthritis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"slow_channel_congenital_myasthenic_syndrome":{"name":"Slow-channel congenital myasthenic syndrome","system":"Neuromuscular junction","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCCMS","Slow-channel syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neuromuscular medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Postsynaptic congenital myasthenic syndrome caused by dominant gain-of-function variants in acetylcholine receptor subunit genes that prolong channel opening.","Characteristically produces selective, severe weakness and wasting of the cervical, wrist and finger extensor muscles, an unusual pattern for a neuromuscular junction disorder.","Inheritance is autosomal dominant, so a family history of similar weakness across generations is a useful clue.","Cholinesterase inhibitors are contraindicated because they further prolong acetylcholine action and worsen the excitotoxic endplate damage.","Long-lived open-channel blockers such as fluoxetine or quinidine are the mainstay of treatment as they shorten channel open time.","Onset and severity are highly variable, ranging from infancy to adult life, and progression can be slow over decades.","A repetitive compound muscle action potential following a single stimulus reflects the prolonged endplate potential and is a characteristic finding."],"pathophysiology":"Slow-channel syndrome arises from dominant gain-of-function missense variants in genes encoding acetylcholine receptor subunits, which alter channel gating so that the receptor stays open far longer than normal after acetylcholine binds, and may also show increased affinity and reopening. The prolonged and repeated channel openings allow excessive cation influx, producing a lengthened endplate potential that generates repetitive muscle fibre firing after a single nerve impulse. Over time the sustained calcium overload at the endplate is excitotoxic, causing degeneration of the junctional folds, loss of receptors, vacuolar change and an endplate myopathy, which accounts for the fixed weakness and focal muscle wasting rather than pure fatigability. Because the fundamental problem is excess rather than deficient cholinergic signalling, any intervention that increases or prolongs acetylcholine availability, including cholinesterase inhibitors, aggravates the calcium-mediated damage, whereas agents that block the open channel and shorten its effective open time reduce cation overload and improve strength.","additionalDifferentials":["Fast-channel and other kinetic congenital myasthenic syndromes","Seronegative and acetylcholine-receptor-antibody-positive autoimmune myasthenia gravis","Distal myopathies and inclusion body myopathy given the selective forearm and finger extensor wasting","Motor neuron disease and focal amyotrophies","Hereditary neuropathies with distal weakness","Endplate acetylcholinesterase deficiency, which can also worsen with cholinesterase inhibitors"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repetitive nerve stimulation showing a decrement and a characteristic repetitive compound muscle action potential to a single stimulus","Single-fibre electromyography demonstrating increased jitter and blocking","Targeted sequencing of acetylcholine receptor subunit genes or a congenital myasthenic syndrome gene panel","Negative acetylcholine receptor and muscle-specific kinase antibodies to exclude autoimmune disease","In-vitro microelectrode and single-channel endplate studies where specialised facilities exist","Documentation of clinical response to an open-channel blocker and of worsening with cholinesterase inhibition"],"redFlags":["Clinical deterioration after administration of a cholinesterase inhibitor","Progressive respiratory muscle weakness with hypoventilation","Rapidly advancing bulbar or neck weakness with aspiration risk","New severe distal weakness compromising hand function or airway protection"],"prognosis":"The disorder is usually slowly progressive over years to decades and can lead to significant disability, but open-channel blocker therapy often produces meaningful and durable improvement. Respiratory involvement warrants long-term surveillance.","pitfalls":["Prescribing cholinesterase inhibitors, which characteristically worsen this subtype","Mistaking the selective forearm and finger extensor wasting for a distal myopathy or motor neuron disease","Overlooking the autosomal dominant inheritance and failing to evaluate affected relatives","Expecting pure fatigability and being misled by the fixed weakness and muscle wasting of the endplate myopathy"],"management":["Long-lived open-channel blockers such as fluoxetine or quinidine are the mainstay, shortening acetylcholine receptor channel open time and reducing endplate excitotoxicity.","Avoid cholinesterase inhibitors and other agents that prolong acetylcholine action, as they aggravate the excitotoxic endplate myopathy.","Physiotherapy, wrist and finger extensor splinting, and assistive devices to support the selective cervical and forearm extensor weakness and wasting.","Respiratory surveillance with periodic vital capacity and sleep-disordered-breathing assessment, introducing non-invasive ventilation when hypoventilation develops.","Anticipate and treat respiratory decompensation during intercurrent infection with ventilatory support.","Coordinate care through a neuromuscular specialist with genetics, respiratory and rehabilitation input.","Autosomal dominant inheritance warrants genetic counselling and clinical assessment of affected relatives across generations."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular and paediatric neurology textbooks","Peer-reviewed reviews of congenital myasthenic syndromes"],"crossLinks":[]},"sly_syndrome":{"name":"Sly syndrome (MPS VII)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MPS VII","mucopolysaccharidosis type VII","beta-glucuronidase deficiency","GUSB deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Scriver's OMMBID","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by deficiency of beta-glucuronidase, impairing degradation of dermatan, heparan, and chondroitin sulfates","One of the recognised metabolic causes of non-immune hydrops fetalis, which may present at birth or in utero","Phenotype is broad, ranging from severe neonatal disease to attenuated forms presenting later in childhood","Features overlap with other mucopolysaccharidoses: coarse facies, dysostosis multiplex, hepatosplenomegaly, and variable cognitive impairment","Multiple glycosaminoglycans accumulate and are excreted because the deficient enzyme acts on several substrates","Diagnosis rests on deficient beta-glucuronidase activity confirmed by GUSB gene testing","Enzyme replacement therapy is available and may improve some systemic manifestations"],"pathophysiology":"Sly syndrome is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in GUSB, encoding the lysosomal enzyme beta-glucuronidase. This enzyme participates in the degradation of dermatan sulfate, heparan sulfate, and chondroitin-4/6-sulfate, so its deficiency causes accumulation of all these glycosaminoglycans within lysosomes across many tissues. The resulting storage produces multisystem disease affecting the skeleton, connective tissue, viscera, cardiovascular system, and, in more severe forms, the central nervous system. Severe cases can present antenatally with non-immune hydrops fetalis. Excess glycosaminoglycans are excreted in the urine. Inheritance is autosomal recessive, and the degree of residual enzyme activity broadly correlates with the wide clinical spectrum.","additionalDifferentials":["Other mucopolysaccharidoses, especially Hurler syndrome (MPS I)","Other metabolic and non-metabolic causes of non-immune hydrops fetalis","Mucolipidosis II (I-cell disease)","GM1 gangliosidosis and other storage disorders presenting in infancy","Multiple sulfatase deficiency","Galactosialidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Leucocyte or fibroblast beta-glucuronidase enzyme assay","Quantitative urinary glycosaminoglycan analysis showing a mixed pattern","GUSB gene sequencing","Antenatal evaluation and enzyme testing where hydrops fetalis occurs","Skeletal survey for dysostosis multiplex","Echocardiography and developmental assessment for systemic and neurological involvement"],"redFlags":["Non-immune hydrops fetalis or severe neonatal presentation","Progressive cardiorespiratory compromise","Cervical spine instability with cord compression","Rapid neurodevelopmental decline in severe forms"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends heavily on the phenotype; severe neonatal disease with hydrops carries high early mortality, while attenuated forms may allow survival into adulthood with variable disability. Enzyme replacement therapy can improve some somatic manifestations.","pitfalls":["Not considering a lysosomal storage disorder in the workup of non-immune hydrops fetalis","Assuming a uniform severe course given the wide phenotypic range","Confusing the mixed glycosaminoglycan pattern with other mucopolysaccharidoses without enzyme testing","Overlooking cervical spine and cardiac surveillance"],"management":["Enzyme replacement therapy with recombinant beta-glucuronidase (vestronidase alfa) to reduce lysosomal glycosaminoglycan storage and improve somatic manifestations.","Consider haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in selected patients, weighing benefit against procedural risk.","Supportive management of dysostosis multiplex, hepatosplenomegaly, cardiac valve disease and airway obstruction, including physiotherapy and orthopaedic care.","Multisystem surveillance with skeletal survey, echocardiography, audiology, ophthalmology, respiratory sleep-disordered-breathing and developmental assessment.","Monitor for cervical cord compression and hydrocephalus, with neurosurgical intervention where indicated.","Antenatal and neonatal management of non-immune hydrops foetalis in severe presentations, with specialist perinatal support.","Coordinate care through metabolic medicine, clinical genetics and relevant organ specialists.","Autosomal recessive condition, so offer genetic counselling, carrier testing and prenatal or family screening."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"small_cell_carcinoma_ovary":{"name":"Small cell carcinoma of the ovary, hypercalcaemic type","system":"Gynaecology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCCOHT","hypercalcaemic small cell carcinoma of the ovary","SMARCA4-deficient ovarian carcinoma","ovarian small cell carcinoma of hypercalcaemic type"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Female Genital Tumours / Berek and Hacker's Gynecologic Oncology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is a rare, highly aggressive ovarian malignancy that characteristically affects adolescents and young women rather than older adults","About two-thirds of patients have paraneoplastic hypercalcaemia, which may cause fatigue, nausea, constipation, polyuria, and confusion and can be the presenting clue","Despite the historical name, it is not of neuroendocrine origin; it is now defined by loss of the chromatin-remodelling protein SMARCA4 (BRG1)","It shares molecular features with malignant rhabdoid tumours, and some cases are associated with germline SMARCA4 variants, so family history and genetic assessment matter","Tumours are usually large and unilateral at diagnosis and frequently present at an advanced stage with extra-ovarian spread","Diagnosis rests on histology with immunohistochemistry demonstrating loss of SMARCA4 (BRG1) expression, distinguishing it from other small round cell tumours","Management is delivered by a gynaecological oncology multidisciplinary team and combines tumour-directed surgery with aggressive chemotherapy, and the hypercalcaemia typically resolves as the tumour is controlled"],"pathophysiology":"Small cell carcinoma of the ovary of hypercalcaemic type is a chromatin-remodelling-driven malignancy defined by inactivation of SMARCA4, the gene encoding BRG1, a core ATPase subunit of the SWI/SNF chromatin-remodelling complex. Loss of this subunit disrupts the complex's control of gene expression and differentiation, driving an undifferentiated, highly proliferative tumour that resembles malignant rhabdoid tumours molecularly. The paraneoplastic hypercalcaemia is attributed to tumour production of parathyroid hormone-related protein, which promotes bone resorption and renal calcium retention independent of bony metastasis. A subset of patients carry germline SMARCA4 variants, linking the tumour to an inherited rhabdoid tumour predisposition.","additionalDifferentials":["Adult granulosa cell tumour and other sex cord-stromal tumours","Dysgerminoma and other malignant germ cell tumours","Small cell carcinoma of pulmonary type involving the ovary","Metastatic small round blue cell tumours (e.g. lymphoma, neuroblastoma)","Poorly differentiated (high-grade) ovarian carcinoma","Primary neuroendocrine carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Histopathology with immunohistochemistry showing loss of SMARCA4 (BRG1) expression","Serum corrected calcium and parathyroid hormone-related protein assessment","Cross-sectional imaging of the abdomen, pelvis, and chest for staging","Serum tumour markers to help exclude germ cell and epithelial tumours","Germline SMARCA4 genetic testing and genetic counselling","Baseline renal function and electrocardiography in the context of hypercalcaemia"],"redFlags":["Severe symptomatic hypercalcaemia with dehydration, arrhythmia, or altered consciousness requiring urgent correction","A rapidly enlarging pelvic or abdominal mass with constitutional symptoms","Evidence of advanced-stage disease with extra-ovarian spread","A young patient with an ovarian mass and unexplained hypercalcaemia"],"prognosis":"The tumour is aggressive and carries a poor prognosis overall, particularly when it presents at an advanced stage, though outcomes are better for early-stage disease treated with multimodal therapy. Younger age and higher stage generally portend a worse outcome, and relapse is common.","pitfalls":["Assuming ovarian cancer is a disease of older women and discounting it in a young patient","Attributing hypercalcaemia to bony metastases rather than recognising a paraneoplastic mechanism","Misclassifying the tumour as a germ cell or sex cord-stromal neoplasm without SMARCA4 immunohistochemistry","Overlooking the possibility of a germline SMARCA4 variant and omitting genetic referral"],"management":["Surgical resection with staging as the cornerstone, typically combined with aggressive multi-agent platinum-based chemotherapy and often radiotherapy for this high-grade tumour.","Consider high-dose chemotherapy with autologous stem cell rescue and emerging SWI/SNF-directed agents such as EZH2 inhibitors within specialist or trial settings.","Treat paraneoplastic hypercalcaemia with intravenous rehydration and bone-resorption inhibitors such as bisphosphonates and calcitonin.","Supportive oncological care including antiemesis, nutritional support and management of treatment toxicity.","Surveillance for relapse with cross-sectional imaging and serial corrected calcium, given the high recurrence rate.","Manage through a gynaecological oncology multidisciplinary team with medical oncology and palliative care input as required.","Germline SMARCA4 testing with genetic counselling and screening of at-risk relatives, given the inherited rhabdoid tumour predisposition."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Female Genital Tumours","Berek and Hacker's Gynecologic Oncology"],"crossLinks":[]},"small_fiber_neuropathy":{"name":"Small fibre neuropathy","system":"Nervous system / Autonomic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["small fiber neuropathy","SFN","small-fibre sensory neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Selectively affects thinly myelinated A-delta and unmyelinated C fibres, causing burning pain and tingling, often in a length-dependent (feet-first) distribution","Autonomic features are common, including dry eyes and mouth, altered sweating, orthostatic symptoms, and gastrointestinal or bladder disturbance","Standard nerve conduction studies are normal because they assess large fibres, so a normal study does not exclude the diagnosis","Skin punch biopsy showing reduced intraepidermal nerve fibre density is the principal confirmatory test","Quantitative sensory testing and QSART assess small-fibre sensory and sudomotor function","Common causes include diabetes and impaired glucose tolerance; others include Sjogren and other autoimmune disease, amyloidosis, sodium-channel gene variants, alcohol, and drugs, while a large proportion remain idiopathic","The pain is neuropathic in character and is often worse at night","Management: treat any underlying cause, use neuropathic-pain measures, address autonomic symptoms, and avoid neurotoxic exposures"],"pathophysiology":"Small fibre neuropathy is a disorder of the small-diameter peripheral nerve fibres, the thinly myelinated A-delta and unmyelinated C fibres, that carry pain and temperature sensation and mediate autonomic function. Damage to these fibres, which may be length-dependent (beginning in the feet) or non-length-dependent, produces spontaneous neuropathic pain and impaired thermal and pain sensation, together with autonomic disturbances of sweating, cardiovascular regulation, and visceral function. The underlying mechanisms are diverse, including metabolic injury from diabetes or impaired glucose tolerance, immune-mediated damage, amyloid deposition, toxic and drug exposures, and gain-of-function sodium-channel gene variants that render nociceptors hyperexcitable; in many patients no cause is found. Because large fibres are spared, routine nerve conduction studies are typically normal.","additionalDifferentials":["Large-fibre or mixed polyneuropathy","Radiculopathy or focal compressive neuropathy","Complex regional pain syndrome","Fibromyalgia","Erythromelalgia","Central pain syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin punch biopsy for intraepidermal nerve fibre density","Quantitative sensory testing of thermal thresholds","QSART and thermoregulatory sweat test for sudomotor involvement","Nerve conduction studies to confirm large fibres are spared and exclude a mixed neuropathy","Screening bloods including glucose and HbA1c with glucose tolerance testing, autoimmune and Sjogren serology, paraproteins, vitamin B12, and thyroid function","Cardiovascular autonomic reflex testing and, in selected cases, genetic testing for sodium-channel variants"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or profoundly disabling symptoms suggesting vasculitis or amyloidosis","Prominent autonomic failure suggesting a systemic or amyloid process","Systemic features such as weight loss or organ involvement pointing to a serious underlying cause","Motor weakness or large-fibre signs indicating a broader neuropathy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the cause: where a treatable driver such as impaired glucose tolerance or an autoimmune disorder is identified and managed, symptoms may stabilise or improve, whereas idiopathic and amyloid-related forms tend to progress; pain and autonomic symptoms are often the main long-term burden.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because nerve conduction studies are normal","Failing to search for treatable causes such as impaired glucose tolerance or Sjogren syndrome","Overlooking autonomic involvement","Attributing genuine neuropathic pain to a purely functional or psychological cause"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying driver, with glycaemic control in impaired glucose tolerance or diabetes, immunotherapy for Sjogren or other autoimmune causes, and disease-specific therapy for amyloidosis.","Neuropathic pain management with agents such as gabapentinoids, tricyclic antidepressants, serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitors, or topical lidocaine and capsaicin.","Manage autonomic features, treating orthostatic hypotension with volume expansion, fludrocortisone or midodrine and physical measures, alongside treatment of sicca, gastrointestinal and bladder symptoms.","Lifestyle measures including alcohol avoidance, foot care and metabolic optimisation.","Monitor for progression and periodically reassess for an emerging treatable cause in idiopathic cases.","Coordinate care across neurology, autonomic and pain services, with rheumatology involvement where autoimmune disease is found.","Consider genetic testing and counselling where a sodium-channel gain-of-function variant is suspected."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Low's Clinical Autonomic Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"small_intestinal_bacterial_overgrowth":{"name":"Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SIBO"],"chapter":null,"source":"Gastroenterology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Excessive numbers of bacteria in the small intestine causing bloating, flatulence, diarrhoea and, when severe, malabsorption","It is a consequence of impaired clearance of small-bowel bacteria rather than a communicable infection","Predisposing factors include disordered motility, anatomical changes such as blind loops and strictures, and loss of protective gastric acid","Severe or long-standing cases can cause fat and vitamin B12 malabsorption with weight loss and deficiency","Breath testing after a carbohydrate load is commonly used, though it has recognised limitations","Treatment combines antibiotics with correction of the underlying predisposing factor where possible"],"pathophysiology":"Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth develops when the mechanisms that normally keep the small-bowel bacterial population low, principally propulsive motility, gastric acid and an intact ileocaecal valve, are impaired. Overgrowth of predominantly colonic-type bacteria in the small bowel leads to fermentation of carbohydrates, producing gas and bloating, deconjugation of bile salts causing fat malabsorption, and competition for and altered handling of nutrients such as vitamin B12. It is therefore a disorder of dysbiosis and impaired clearance secondary to motility or structural abnormalities, not a primary infective disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Irritable bowel syndrome","Coeliac disease","Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency","Carbohydrate malabsorption (for example lactose)","Inflammatory bowel disease","Bile acid diarrhoea"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Carbohydrate breath testing (for example glucose or lactulose)","Assessment for underlying motility disorders or anatomical abnormalities","Nutritional studies including vitamin B12 and fat-soluble vitamins in suspected malabsorption","Coeliac serology and pancreatic assessment to exclude mimics","Small-bowel imaging where structural causes are suspected"],"redFlags":["Weight loss and features of malabsorption","Vitamin deficiency states","An underlying structural lesion such as a stricture or fistula","Failure to respond to appropriate treatment"],"prognosis":"Symptoms usually improve with antibiotic treatment, but recurrence is common when the underlying predisposing factor persists, so addressing the cause is important for durable control.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing it on symptoms alone given overlap with irritable bowel syndrome","Treating recurrent episodes without addressing the underlying motility or anatomical cause","Over-reliance on breath tests despite their limitations","Missing malabsorption and nutritional deficiencies"],"management":["Antibiotic therapy, commonly rifaximin or alternatives such as tetracyclines, metronidazole or co-amoxiclav, to reduce the small-bowel bacterial load.","Correct the underlying predisposing factor where possible, whether a motility disorder, an anatomical blind loop or stricture, or loss of protective gastric acid.","Replace nutritional deficiencies, notably vitamin B12 and fat-soluble vitamins, and optimise nutrition in malabsorption.","Consider prokinetic agents to improve small-bowel clearance and reduce recurrence in dysmotility.","Surgical correction of amenable anatomical abnormalities such as strictures or blind loops.","Monitor for symptom recurrence and repeat or cycle antibiotics where the predisposing cause persists.","Refer to gastroenterology for refractory or recurrent disease and to investigate an underlying cause."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Gastroenterology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"smarca4_deficient_thoracic_tumor":{"name":"SMARCA4-deficient undifferentiated thoracic tumour","system":"Oncology / Pathology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SMARCA4-deficient thoracic sarcoma","SMARCA4-UT","BRG1-deficient thoracic tumour","thoracic SMARCA4-deficient undifferentiated tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by inactivation of SMARCA4 (BRG1), a core ATPase subunit of the SWI/SNF chromatin-remodelling complex, with corresponding loss of BRG1 expression by immunohistochemistry","Typically arises in the mediastinum, lung, pleura or chest wall as a large, rapidly growing compressive mass","Strongly associated with heavy tobacco smoking and occurs most often in middle-aged to older adults, with a marked male predominance","Morphology is undifferentiated or rhabdoid, with sheets of monotonous epithelioid cells and frequent necrosis","Immunophenotype often shows loss of BRG1 together with co-loss of SMARCA2 (BRM), variable weak or focal keratin, and expression of SALL4, CD34 or SOX2","Must be distinguished from SMARCA4-deficient non-small cell lung carcinoma, which retains more definite epithelial differentiation and glandular or squamous architecture","Diagnosis requires integrating morphology, loss of BRG1 staining and exclusion of other SWI/SNF-deficient and undifferentiated neoplasms"],"pathophysiology":"This tumour is defined by biallelic loss of function of SMARCA4, which encodes the BRG1 ATPase at the heart of the SWI/SNF chromatin-remodelling complex. Loss of BRG1 disrupts nucleosome positioning and the regulation of differentiation-associated genes, producing a highly undifferentiated, often rhabdoid phenotype. The strong link with tobacco exposure suggests a background of heavy mutational damage, and the resulting dependence on residual chromatin-remodelling and epigenetic pathways is being explored as a therapeutic vulnerability.","additionalDifferentials":["SMARCA4-deficient non-small cell lung carcinoma","NUT carcinoma of the thorax","Poorly differentiated or sarcomatoid carcinoma","Malignant rhabdoid tumour and SMARCB1-deficient neoplasms","Germ cell tumour of the mediastinum","Metastatic melanoma or large cell carcinoma","Ewing sarcoma and other round cell sarcomas"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Immunohistochemistry demonstrating loss of BRG1 (SMARCA4), frequently with co-loss of BRM (SMARCA2)","Panel to exclude mimics, including keratins, SALL4, CD34, SOX2, NUT and germ cell markers","Molecular sequencing to confirm SMARCA4 inactivation and exclude alternative drivers","Cross-sectional thoracic imaging to define the extent of mediastinal or pulmonary involvement","Staging imaging for distant metastases given the aggressive behaviour","Adequate biopsy sampling to allow the full ancillary panel in an undifferentiated tumour"],"redFlags":["Large compressive mediastinal or thoracic mass in a heavy smoker","Rapid growth with airway, vascular or oesophageal compression","Widespread metastatic disease at presentation","Poor performance status due to bulky intrathoracic tumour"],"prognosis":"The tumour behaves very aggressively, with rapid progression, frequent metastasis and short median survival despite chemotherapy and radiotherapy; complete resection is often not feasible because of extent at presentation, and immune checkpoint inhibition has shown responses in some patients, keeping treatment approaches under active investigation.","pitfalls":["Confusing the tumour with SMARCA4-deficient lung carcinoma, which has different treatment implications","Assuming keratin negativity excludes an epithelial or carcinoma-related process","Failing to test for BRG1 loss in an undifferentiated thoracic mass","Misinterpreting co-loss of BRM as an unrelated finding rather than part of the characteristic profile","Overreliance on a single marker rather than the integrated morphological and immunohistochemical picture"],"management":["Attempt complete surgical resection where the tumour is localised, though the extent at presentation often precludes this.","Platinum-based chemotherapy combined with radiotherapy for unresectable or advanced disease as the systemic backbone.","Immune checkpoint inhibition, which has produced responses in some patients and is an important consideration given limited chemotherapy benefit.","Smoking cessation support, reflecting the strong tobacco association.","Staging and restaging with cross-sectional thoracic and whole-body imaging to guide treatment and detect metastasis.","Early integration of palliative and symptom-directed care given the aggressive course and short median survival.","Manage through a thoracic oncology multidisciplinary team with pathology, surgical, medical and radiation oncology input."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Thoracic Tumours","WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"smith_fracture":{"name":"Smith fracture (reverse Colles)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["reverse Colles fracture","volar-displaced distal radius fracture","Smith fracture-dislocation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distal radius fracture with volar (palmar) displacement and angulation of the distal fragment, the mirror image of a Colles fracture","It produces a characteristic garden-spade deformity of the wrist","The usual mechanism is a fall onto the dorsum of a flexed wrist or a direct blow to the back of the wrist","The pattern is inherently unstable, and volar angulation tends to recur after closed reduction","Volar plate fixation is often required, although stable or undisplaced patterns may be managed by closed reduction and plaster immobilisation","It should be distinguished from a volar Barton fracture, which is an intra-articular volar rim fracture-dislocation","Median nerve function must be assessed, as acute compression can occur"],"pathophysiology":"A force directed onto the dorsum of a flexed wrist, or a fall onto the back of the hand, fractures the distal radial metaphysis and displaces the distal fragment volarly, the opposite of the dorsal displacement seen in a Colles fracture. The pull of the forearm muscles and the inherent instability of the pattern tend to recreate volar angulation after reduction, so maintenance of reduction in plaster is difficult. Volar displacement can also crowd the carpal tunnel, threatening the median nerve, and articular extension of the fracture line predisposes to incongruity and later arthritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Colles fracture (dorsal displacement)","Volar Barton fracture (intra-articular volar rim)","Dorsal Barton fracture","Distal radial shaft or Galeazzi injury","Carpal fracture or dislocation","Distal radial physeal injury in skeletally immature patients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Posteroanterior and lateral wrist radiographs, the lateral showing volar tilt","Assessment of radial height, radial inclination and volar tilt","CT to evaluate intra-articular extension","Examination and documentation of median nerve function","Post-reduction radiographs to confirm alignment and monitor for re-displacement"],"redFlags":["Acute median nerve compression or carpal tunnel symptoms","Open fracture requiring urgent management","Marked instability with loss of reduction","Intra-articular extension suggesting a volar Barton pattern"],"prognosis":"Because volar-displaced fractures are unstable and prone to re-displacement, many require operative fixation; anatomical volar plate fixation generally restores good function, whereas malunion causes deformity, weakness and median nerve symptoms.","pitfalls":["Treating the injury as a stable Colles-type fracture and accepting loss of reduction","Missing intra-articular (volar Barton) extension","Overlooking median nerve compromise","Inadequate follow-up allowing undetected re-displacement"],"management":["Anatomical reduction and volar locking-plate fixation for the typically unstable displaced pattern, as volar angulation tends to recur after closed reduction.","Closed reduction and well-moulded plaster immobilisation for stable or undisplaced fractures.","Assess and document median nerve function, with carpal tunnel decompression if there is progressive median nerve compromise.","Analgesia and early limb elevation to control pain and swelling.","Post-reduction and serial radiographs to confirm alignment and detect re-displacement.","Hand therapy and physiotherapy to restore wrist and finger range of motion and grip strength.","Refer to orthopaedic trauma for operative planning, particularly for intra-articular extension requiring precise reconstruction."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","Green's Operative Hand Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"smith_kingsmore_syndrome":{"name":"Smith-Kingsmore syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SKS","MTOR-related megalencephaly","macrocephaly-intellectual disability-neurodevelopmental disorder","MINDS syndrome","MTOR gain-of-function syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nature Reviews chromatinopathy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Arises from heterozygous activating (gain-of-function) variants in MTOR, the serine/threonine kinase at the heart of the mTOR signalling pathway that governs cell growth, proliferation and metabolism","The core triad is megalencephaly or macrocephaly, intellectual disability and a recognisable facial gestalt with frontal bossing, a long face and downslanting palpebral fissures","Seizures are common and may be difficult to control, and some children show autistic features","As a disorder of mTOR-pathway overactivation it sits within the mTORopathies, sharing mechanistic ground with focal cortical dysplasia and tuberous sclerosis rather than with the chromatin-modifier syndromes","Skin findings such as hypopigmented or hyperpigmented patches may be present, reflecting somatic mosaicism in some individuals","Because the pathway is druggable, mTOR inhibitors such as sirolimus are being explored in a research and specialist setting for selected manifestations","Diagnosis is molecular, identifying a recurrent activating MTOR variant, and mosaic cases may require testing of affected tissue or deep sequencing"],"pathophysiology":"MTOR encodes the catalytic kinase of two complexes, mTORC1 and mTORC2, which integrate growth-factor and nutrient signals to drive protein synthesis, cell growth and proliferation while restraining autophagy. Gain-of-function variants lock the kinase in an overactive state, so downstream signalling proceeds even without normal upstream cues. In the developing brain this excessive signalling enlarges neurons and disturbs cortical organisation, producing megalencephaly, epilepsy and intellectual disability, while its effects in other tissues account for the overgrowth and pigmentary skin changes. Because the abnormality lies in a well-characterised and pharmacologically accessible pathway, rational suppression of mTOR activity is an area of active therapeutic interest.","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberous sclerosis complex (TSC1, TSC2)","PTEN hamartoma tumour syndrome / macrocephaly-autism","Megalencephaly-capillary malformation and megalencephaly-polymicrogaly spectrum (PIK3CA, AKT3, PIK3R2)","Sotos syndrome (NSD1)","Focal cortical dysplasia type II from somatic mTOR-pathway variants","Benign familial macrocephaly","Other overgrowth syndromes with intellectual disability"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or genome sequencing for germline MTOR variants, with deep sequencing if mosaicism is suspected","Brain MRI to document megalencephaly and any cortical malformation","Electroencephalography where seizures are present","Serial head circumference and growth measurements","Neurodevelopmental and behavioural assessment including autism screening","Dermatological examination for pigmentary skin changes with tissue sampling if mosaicism is considered","Specialist review regarding the role of mTOR pathway inhibition in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Escalating or refractory seizures requiring urgent neurological review","Rapidly increasing head circumference or signs of raised intracranial pressure","Developmental regression","Feeding difficulties with faltering growth in infancy"],"prognosis":"The condition is lifelong, with intellectual disability that ranges from mild to severe and epilepsy that may be difficult to control in some children; overall outlook depends heavily on seizure burden and developmental trajectory, and emerging mTOR-pathway therapies may in future modify aspects of the phenotype though evidence remains preliminary.","pitfalls":["Overlooking somatic mosaicism, so that standard blood testing yields a falsely negative result","Confusing the picture with tuberous sclerosis and missing the specific MTOR diagnosis","Attributing macrocephaly to a benign familial cause and delaying evaluation of developmental delay and seizures","Initiating mTOR inhibition outside a specialist framework without appropriate monitoring"],"management":["There is no cure; mTOR inhibitors such as sirolimus are being explored in specialist and research settings for selected manifestations of this mTORopathy.","Antiepileptic drug therapy for seizures, which may be difficult to control and require combination regimens.","Early developmental intervention with physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy, and individualised educational support for intellectual disability.","Behavioural support including assessment and management of autistic features.","Surveillance with serial head circumference, brain MRI for megalencephaly and cortical malformation, and electroencephalography where seizures occur.","Coordinate care across paediatric neurology, clinical genetics and developmental paediatrics, with dermatology review of pigmentary skin changes.","Genetic counselling for the activating MTOR variant, noting possible mosaicism when assessing recurrence risk and family members."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews — Smith-Kingsmore Syndrome","Nature Reviews — mTOR signalling in growth and neurodevelopmental disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"smith_lemli_opitz_syndrome":{"name":"Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SLOS","DHCR7 deficiency","7-dehydrocholesterol reductase deficiency","RSH syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder of cholesterol biosynthesis caused by variants in DHCR7, encoding 7-dehydrocholesterol reductase, the final enzyme of the pathway","The biochemical hallmark is a raised concentration of 7-dehydrocholesterol with low or low-normal cholesterol","Characteristic malformations include microcephaly, distinctive facial features, cleft palate, and cardiac and genital anomalies","Syndactyly of the second and third toes (Y-shaped 2-3 toe syndactyly) is a classic and useful clinical clue","Ambiguous or undervirilised genitalia in genetic males reflects impaired steroidogenesis downstream of cholesterol","The clinical spectrum is wide, from mild learning and behavioural difficulties to severe multiple congenital anomalies incompatible with survival","Behavioural features overlapping with autism spectrum disorder are common","Dietary cholesterol supplementation is used, though its effect on neurodevelopmental outcome remains uncertain"],"pathophysiology":"7-Dehydrocholesterol reductase catalyses the last step of cholesterol synthesis, reducing 7-dehydrocholesterol to cholesterol. In Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome this enzyme is deficient, so cholesterol production falls and the immediate precursor 7-dehydrocholesterol and its isomer accumulate. Cholesterol is essential for cell membranes, myelin, bile acids, steroid hormones and Sonic hedgehog signalling during embryogenesis. Deficiency during development therefore disrupts morphogenesis (explaining the malformation pattern, including holoprosencephaly-spectrum features from impaired hedgehog signalling), while accumulated precursor sterols may exert additional toxic effects. Impaired steroidogenesis contributes to the genital and adrenal manifestations.","additionalDifferentials":["Trisomy 13 and other chromosomal malformation syndromes","Other inborn errors of cholesterol or sterol synthesis","Disorders of sexual development from other causes","CHARGE and other multiple congenital anomaly syndromes","Syndromic autism with dysmorphism","Holoprosencephaly of other aetiologies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma sterol analysis showing raised 7-dehydrocholesterol with low cholesterol","Molecular genetic testing of DHCR7","Echocardiography and renal ultrasound for structural anomalies","Assessment of genital anatomy and karyotype in undervirilised males","Brain imaging to identify midline and other malformations","Developmental and behavioural evaluation"],"redFlags":["Multiple congenital anomalies with microcephaly and dysmorphism in a neonate","Ambiguous genitalia requiring urgent endocrine evaluation","Feeding difficulties, failure to thrive and recurrent illness","Signs of adrenal insufficiency during stress"],"prognosis":"Prognosis correlates with disease severity: the most severely affected infants have major malformations and high early mortality, whereas milder cases survive with variable intellectual disability and behavioural difficulties. Cholesterol supplementation may improve some physical parameters, but its benefit for cognition and behaviour is not clearly established.","pitfalls":["Measuring only total cholesterol, which can be normal, rather than specific sterol profiling","Missing adrenal insufficiency during intercurrent illness or surgery","Attributing the phenotype to a chromosomal syndrome without sterol testing","Assuming cholesterol supplementation reliably improves neurodevelopment"],"management":["Dietary cholesterol supplementation to raise cholesterol and lower precursor sterols, which may improve growth and some physical parameters.","Feeding support for poor feeding and failure to thrive, including nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding and management of gastro-oesophageal reflux.","Surgical correction of structural anomalies such as cleft palate, cardiac defects, genital anomalies and toe syndactyly where indicated.","Photoprotection against the characteristic cutaneous photosensitivity.","Monitor for and manage adrenal insufficiency, with corticosteroid stress cover during illness or surgery.","Surveillance of growth, development and plasma sterols, with developmental and behavioural intervention.","Coordinate care through metabolic medicine, clinical genetics and relevant surgical specialties.","Autosomal recessive inheritance, so offer genetic counselling, carrier testing and prenatal diagnosis to families."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"smith_magenis_syndrome":{"name":"Smith-Magenis syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SMS","RAI1","17p11.2 deletion syndrome","retinoic acid-induced 1 gene disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and neurodevelopmental textbooks with peer-reviewed reviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Neurodevelopmental disorder caused by a recurrent interstitial deletion of chromosome 17p11.2 encompassing RAI1, or by a pathogenic variant within RAI1 itself","Cardinal feature is a severely disrupted circadian sleep-wake rhythm with an inverted melatonin cycle (melatonin secreted during the day rather than at night), causing daytime sleepiness and marked night-time waking","Characteristic behavioural phenotype includes self-injurious behaviours (notably nail-yanking and inserting objects into body orifices) and self-hugging or hand-squeezing stereotypies","Intellectual disability, developmental delay, and expressive speech difficulties are typical, alongside a generally sociable but impulsive temperament","Recognisable dysmorphic features include a broad, square face, brachycephaly, midface hypoplasia, and a tented upper lip, evolving with age","Associated medical issues include hearing loss, ophthalmological problems, scoliosis, and features that may mimic other neurodevelopmental syndromes","Management of the sleep disturbance addresses the abnormal melatonin rhythm and sleep hygiene, and behavioural and developmental support is central to care","Diagnosis is established by chromosomal microarray to detect the 17p11.2 deletion, or by RAI1 sequencing when a deletion is not found"],"pathophysiology":"Smith-Magenis syndrome results from haploinsufficiency of RAI1 (retinoic acid-induced 1), whether through the common 17p11.2 microdeletion or a point variant within the gene. RAI1 is a transcriptional regulator with widespread roles in neurodevelopment and, importantly, in the control of circadian gene expression. Reduced RAI1 dosage disrupts the molecular circadian clock, producing the hallmark inversion of the melatonin secretion rhythm and the resulting sleep-wake disturbance, while its broader transcriptional effects account for the cognitive, behavioural, craniofacial, and multisystem features of the syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Potocki-Lupski syndrome (reciprocal 17p11.2 duplication)","Down syndrome and other chromosomal microdeletion/duplication syndromes","Fragile X syndrome","Autism spectrum disorder with sleep disturbance","Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes","Other syndromic intellectual disability with self-injurious behaviour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal microarray to detect the 17p11.2 deletion","RAI1 sequencing when microarray is normal but the phenotype is suggestive","Assessment of the circadian rhythm and melatonin profile where sleep disturbance is prominent","Audiological and ophthalmological evaluation for associated sensory deficits","Skeletal assessment for scoliosis and developmental/behavioural evaluation"],"redFlags":["Severe self-injurious behaviour causing significant tissue damage","Profound sleep deprivation affecting safety and daytime functioning","New or worsening scoliosis with respiratory or neurological compromise","Acute behavioural crisis posing risk to the patient or others"],"prognosis":"Smith-Magenis syndrome is a lifelong condition; intellectual disability and behavioural and sleep difficulties persist, but life expectancy is generally not markedly reduced and structured behavioural, sleep, and developmental interventions can meaningfully improve quality of life.","pitfalls":["Attributing the sleep disturbance to primary behavioural insomnia and missing the underlying inverted melatonin rhythm","Overlooking the diagnosis in a child labelled with non-syndromic autism or intellectual disability","Failing to recognise the characteristic self-injurious and self-hugging behaviours as diagnostic clues","Not arranging chromosomal microarray, or stopping at a normal microarray without considering RAI1 sequencing"],"management":["Target the inverted circadian rhythm with evening melatonin and daytime beta-adrenergic blockade using acebutolol to suppress aberrant daytime melatonin, alongside structured sleep hygiene.","Behavioural management of self-injurious and stereotypic behaviours with structured behavioural intervention and, where needed, psychotropic agents.","Early developmental support with speech and language therapy, occupational therapy and individualised education for intellectual disability.","Surveillance for associated problems including hearing loss, ophthalmological disease, scoliosis, and thyroid, lipid and renal abnormalities.","Coordinate care across clinical genetics, developmental paediatrics or neurology, psychiatry and relevant sensory specialists.","Family and educational support with consistent routines and behavioural strategies to improve quality of life.","Genetic counselling explaining the usually de novo chromosome 17p11.2 deletion or RAI1 variant and the correspondingly low recurrence risk."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Smith-Magenis Syndrome","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"snake_envenomation":{"name":"Snakebite envenomation","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["venomous snakebite","elapid envenomation","viper envenomation","crotalid envenomation","ophidism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / WHO envenoming guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Broad syndromes vary by family: elapids (cobras, kraits, mambas, taipans) tend to produce neurotoxic descending paralysis, while vipers cause local cytotoxicity, coagulopathy and haemorrhage","A dry bite (no envenomation) is common; observe for a meaningful period before excluding envenomation, as neurotoxic and coagulation effects may be delayed","Ptosis, ophthalmoplegia, bulbar weakness and progressive respiratory muscle failure are the hallmarks of neurotoxic envenomation and mandate close airway monitoring","Venom-induced consumption coagulopathy (VICC) can render blood incoagulable; a bedside 20-minute whole-blood clotting test is a useful field indicator of systemic viper envenomation","Progressive local swelling, blistering, bruising and tissue necrosis characterise cytotoxic bites and can cause compartment syndrome","Species-appropriate antivenom is the definitive treatment for systemic envenomation; timing, dose to effect and monitoring for anaphylactoid reactions are key principles","Pressure immobilisation is appropriate for neurotoxic elapid and sea-snake bites to delay systemic spread, but is NOT recommended for cytotoxic viper bites where it may worsen local tissue injury","Reassess repeatedly, as neurotoxicity and coagulopathy can evolve or recur and may require repeat antivenom dosing"],"pathophysiology":"Snake venoms are complex mixtures of enzymes and peptides that produce syndrome-specific injury. Pre-synaptic and post-synaptic neurotoxins block neuromuscular transmission to cause a descending flaccid paralysis affecting cranial, bulbar and respiratory muscles. Procoagulant and fibrinolytic components activate the clotting cascade and consume fibrinogen, producing venom-induced consumption coagulopathy with a risk of spontaneous haemorrhage, while haemorrhagins damage vascular endothelium. Cytotoxins, phospholipases and metalloproteinases cause local tissue oedema, blistering and necrosis, and some venoms produce myotoxicity with rhabdomyolysis or systemic capillary leak and shock.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-venomous or dry bite with anxiety and local trauma","Other envenomation (scorpion, spider) or arthropod bite","Anaphylaxis to an unrelated allergen","Cellulitis or necrotising soft-tissue infection","Primary neuromuscular disorder (myasthenic crisis, Guillain-Barre syndrome)","Sepsis or another cause of consumptive coagulopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["20-minute whole-blood clotting test at the bedside as a screen for coagulopathy","Full coagulation studies including prothrombin time, fibrinogen and D-dimer","Full blood count for thrombocytopenia and evolving anaemia","Creatine kinase, renal function and urinalysis for rhabdomyolysis and myoglobinuria","Serial spirometry or forced vital capacity to track neurotoxic respiratory decline","Electrocardiography and continuous monitoring in systemic envenomation"],"redFlags":["Progressive bulbar or respiratory muscle weakness threatening the airway","Spontaneous systemic bleeding or an incoagulable 20-minute clotting test","Rapidly advancing limb swelling with features suggesting compartment syndrome","Shock, oliguria or acute kidney injury","Anaphylactoid reaction to antivenom during infusion"],"prognosis":"With timely species-appropriate antivenom and supportive care including ventilation for neurotoxic paralysis, most patients recover fully. Delayed treatment increases the risk of death from respiratory failure or haemorrhage, and severe cytotoxic bites may leave permanent tissue loss or amputation.","pitfalls":["Applying arterial tourniquets or incising/sucking the wound, which increase local tissue damage without benefit","Delaying antivenom in evolving systemic envenomation while awaiting definitive species identification","Discharging early after an apparent dry bite before delayed neurotoxicity or coagulopathy has been excluded","Using pressure immobilisation for cytotoxic viper bites where it aggravates local necrosis","Failing to monitor for and treat recurrent coagulopathy or neurotoxicity after initial improvement"],"management":["Administer species-appropriate antivenom titrated to clinical and laboratory effect, with readiness to manage anaphylactoid reactions.","Airway protection and mechanical ventilation for neurotoxic descending paralysis and respiratory muscle failure.","Correct venom-induced consumption coagulopathy with antivenom and blood products such as fresh frozen plasma and cryoprecipitate for active haemorrhage.","Supportive wound care, tetanus prophylaxis and analgesia, while avoiding harmful measures such as tourniquets and incision.","Consider a trial of anticholinesterase, with neostigmine and an antimuscarinic agent, where post-synaptic neurotoxic envenomation is suspected.","Serial monitoring with whole-blood clotting test and coagulation studies, vital capacity, creatine kinase and renal function, with renal replacement therapy for acute kidney injury and fasciotomy for true compartment syndrome.","Escalate to critical care for ventilatory support and management of systemic complications."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Guidelines for the management of snakebite envenoming","Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"],"crossLinks":[]},"snakebite_envenomation":{"name":"Snakebite envenomation","system":"Multisystem / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["venomous snakebite","snake envenoming"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO snakebite guidance / standard toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Effects depend on the species and venom, broadly divided into local tissue injury, systemic coagulopathy, neurotoxicity and, with some species, myotoxicity","Not every bite by a venomous snake injects venom, so a proportion are dry bites without envenoming","Progressive local swelling, spontaneous bleeding and a positive whole-blood clotting test point to significant envenoming","Neurotoxic envenoming may cause ptosis, ophthalmoplegia and progressive descending paralysis with respiratory failure","Antivenom is the specific treatment for systemic envenoming, given according to clinical and laboratory features","Pressure immobilisation may be appropriate for some neurotoxic elapid bites, whereas tourniquets and incision are harmful"],"pathophysiology":"Snake venoms are complex mixtures of enzymes and toxins that damage tissue and disrupt physiological systems. Depending on species, components cause local cytotoxic and necrotic injury, consumptive coagulopathy through activation or destruction of clotting factors, presynaptic or postsynaptic neuromuscular blockade producing paralysis, and rhabdomyolysis. The resulting syndromes include painful progressive local swelling, systemic bleeding, flaccid paralysis and acute kidney injury from myoglobinuria or the coagulopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Dry bite or non-venomous snakebite","Other arthropod or animal envenomation","Cellulitis or wound infection","Anaphylaxis to the bite","Primary bleeding or neuromuscular disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Whole-blood clotting test and coagulation studies","Full blood count for thrombocytopenia and haemolysis","Renal function and creatine kinase for kidney injury and myotoxicity","Serial neurological assessment for progressive paralysis","Species identification where safely possible to guide antivenom choice"],"redFlags":["Spontaneous systemic bleeding or incoagulable blood","Progressive weakness, ptosis or respiratory compromise","Rapidly advancing local swelling or compartment syndrome","Oliguria or evidence of acute kidney injury"],"prognosis":"With timely antivenom and supportive care many patients recover fully, but delayed treatment, severe neurotoxicity, coagulopathy or local necrosis can lead to disability, limb loss or death.","pitfalls":["Applying harmful first aid such as tourniquets, incision or suction","Assuming a dry bite too early and discharging before observation","Delaying antivenom in evolving systemic envenoming","Overlooking airway and ventilatory support in neurotoxic envenoming"],"management":["Give antivenom as the specific treatment for systemic envenoming, guided by clinical and laboratory features and titrated to response.","Apply pressure immobilisation for neurotoxic elapid bites where appropriate, and avoid harmful first-aid measures such as tourniquets and incision.","Provide respiratory support including intubation and ventilation for progressive descending paralysis and respiratory failure.","Manage coagulopathy and bleeding with antivenom and blood-product replacement, and treat local tissue injury with wound care and tetanus prophylaxis.","Support the kidneys in acute kidney injury from myoglobinuria or coagulopathy, using renal replacement therapy when needed.","Serial monitoring with whole-blood clotting test, coagulation studies, full blood count, renal function, creatine kinase and repeated neurological assessment.","Attempt safe species identification to guide antivenom choice and escalate to critical care for severe envenoming."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO guidelines for the management of snakebite","Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"],"crossLinks":[]},"snapping_hip_syndrome":{"name":"Snapping hip syndrome (coxa saltans)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Hand & Peripheral nerve","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["coxa saltans","dancer's hip","iliopsoas tendon snapping"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The hallmark is an audible or palpable snap or clunk felt during hip movement","The external type is caused by the iliotibial band or gluteus maximus tendon flicking over the greater trochanter, giving lateral symptoms","The internal type is caused by the iliopsoas tendon shifting over the iliopectineal eminence or femoral head, giving anterior or groin symptoms","The intra-articular type is caused by a labral tear, loose body or chondral lesion","It most often affects young active people such as dancers and athletes and is frequently painless, though it can become symptomatic","The external snap can often be reproduced and even seen by the patient, whereas the internal snap is elicited by extending the hip from a flexed, abducted, externally rotated position","Dynamic ultrasound is particularly useful to demonstrate the offending tendon snapping in real time","Generic management includes activity modification, stretching and physiotherapy, corticosteroid injection into the bursa or iliopsoas sheath, and surgical lengthening, release or arthroscopy for refractory or intra-articular causes"],"pathophysiology":"Snapping hip syndrome describes a snapping sensation produced when a tendon or band catches on a bony prominence, or when an intra-articular lesion mechanically catches. In the external type the iliotibial band or gluteus maximus tendon flicks across the greater trochanter, while in the internal type the iliopsoas tendon abruptly shifts over the iliopectineal eminence or femoral head as the hip extends from a flexed position. The intra-articular type stems from labral tears or loose bodies. Repetitive movement of the offending structure commonly leads to a secondary tendinopathy or bursitis that generates pain.","additionalDifferentials":["Greater trochanteric pain syndrome or trochanteric bursitis","Iliopsoas bursitis or tendinopathy","Labral tear or femoroacetabular impingement","Intra-articular loose body or chondral lesion","Hip osteoarthritis","Femoral neck stress fracture","Athletic pubalgia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dynamic ultrasound as a first-line study to demonstrate the snapping tendon","Clinical reproduction manoeuvres for the external and internal types","Plain radiographs to assess bony morphology, impingement and osteoarthritis","MRI or MR arthrography to evaluate the labrum and intra-articular structures","A diagnostic injection into the iliopsoas sheath or bursa to confirm the pain source"],"redFlags":["Intra-articular mechanical symptoms such as locking suggesting a loose body or labral tear","Night pain or systemic features raising concern for tumour or infection","Progressive activity-related pain suggesting a femoral neck stress fracture","Progressive loss of function"],"prognosis":"Most patients respond to conservative measures, and surgery reserved for refractory cases generally gives good results, though intra-articular causes require specific treatment.","pitfalls":["Missing intra-articular pathology by labelling all snapping as a benign tendon cause","Overtreating asymptomatic snapping that needs only reassurance","Causing iatrogenic weakness through excessive tendon lengthening","Failing to use dynamic imaging to confirm the mechanism"],"management":["First-line conservative management with activity modification, targeted physiotherapy stretching and strengthening of the iliotibial band or iliopsoas, and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs.","Corticosteroid injection into the trochanteric bursa or iliopsoas sheath for painful tendinopathy or bursitis, which can also be diagnostic.","Surgical release or lengthening of the iliotibial band in the external type or the iliopsoas tendon in the internal type, reserved for refractory symptomatic cases.","Hip arthroscopy to address an intra-articular cause such as a labral tear, loose body or chondral lesion.","Reassure patients with painless snapping, for whom no intervention beyond load management is needed.","Refer to orthopaedics or hip-preservation surgery for symptoms resistant to conservative care."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","Green's Operative Hand Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"snapping_scapula_syndrome":{"name":"Snapping scapula syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scapulothoracic bursitis","snapping scapula","scapulothoracic crepitus","washboard syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Painful audible or palpable snapping or grating (crepitus) of the scapula on the chest wall during shoulder movement","Arises at the scapulothoracic articulation, with scapulothoracic bursitis and/or bony or soft-tissue abnormalities at the medial and superomedial scapula","Often related to overuse in overhead and throwing activities and to scapular dyskinesis and posture","Causes include inflamed scapulothoracic bursae, muscle atrophy or fibrosis, and bony lesions such as osteochondroma or a prominent superomedial angle","Examination looks for crepitus, tenderness, scapular winging and dyskinesis","Imaging (radiographs, CT for bony lesions, MRI or ultrasound for soft tissue and bursae) is used when a structural cause is suspected","Most cases respond to conservative management with postural and scapular stabilisation rehabilitation, load modification and sometimes bursal injection; surgery is reserved for structural lesions or refractory bursitis"],"pathophysiology":"The scapula glides over the posterior chest wall at the scapulothoracic articulation, cushioned by bursae. Snapping scapula syndrome arises when this smooth gliding is disturbed, whether by inflamed or fibrotic scapulothoracic bursae, by altered muscle bulk or scarring, or by bony incongruity such as an osteochondroma, a prominent superomedial angle or a malunited rib or scapular fracture. Repetitive overhead loading, scapular dyskinesis and poor posture contribute. The result is symptomatic crepitus and pain as the scapula tracks over the chest wall.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated scapulothoracic bursitis","Osteochondroma or other bony lesion of the scapula or ribs","Scapular dyskinesis or muscle imbalance","Long thoracic or spinal accessory nerve palsy causing scapular winging","Cervicothoracic referred pain","Rotator cuff or glenohumeral pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs including tangential scapular views","CT to define bony lesions such as an osteochondroma or a prominent angle","MRI or ultrasound to assess the bursae and soft tissues","Clinical assessment of scapular motion, winging and dyskinesis","Diagnostic bursal injection where scapulothoracic bursitis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Fixed scapular winging suggesting a nerve palsy (long thoracic or spinal accessory)","A firm mass or rapidly growing lesion suggesting tumour","Night pain or systemic features suggesting neoplasm or infection","Progressive neurological deficit"],"prognosis":"The majority improve with scapular rehabilitation and load management; outcomes are good after excision when a discrete bony lesion is the cause.","pitfalls":["Attributing symptomatic crepitus to a benign cause without excluding an osteochondroma or other bony lesion","Missing a nerve palsy as a cause of associated winging","Neglecting scapular dyskinesis and posture in rehabilitation","Rushing to surgery before a trial of conservative care in non-structural cases"],"management":["First-line scapular rehabilitation addressing scapular dyskinesis, posture and periscapular muscle strengthening, with load management.","Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and corticosteroid injection into a symptomatic scapulothoracic bursa.","Scapulothoracic bursectomy for bursitis refractory to conservative care.","Excision of a discrete bony lesion such as an osteochondroma or a prominent superomedial angle, which gives good results.","Activity modification to reduce repetitive overhead and throwing loads.","Refer to sports medicine or shoulder surgery for persistent symptoms or a structural cause."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","British Journal of Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"sneddon_syndrome":{"name":"Sneddon syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sneddon's syndrome","livedo racemosa with cerebrovascular disease","idiopathic livedo reticularis with cerebrovascular accidents"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / cerebrovascular and rheumatology literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the combination of widespread livedo racemosa and recurrent ischaemic cerebrovascular events","Livedo is a persistent, broken, irregular net-like violaceous pattern that often precedes strokes by years","Affects predominantly young to middle-aged women and presents with strokes, transient ischaemic attacks, and progressive cognitive decline","A substantial subset have antiphospholipid antibodies, linking it to the antiphospholipid syndrome spectrum","Hypertension, headache, and vascular dementia are frequent accompaniments","Skin biopsy from the border of livedo may show a non-inflammatory occlusive vasculopathy of small dermal arteries","Management centres on antithrombotic therapy and control of vascular risk factors rather than a specific cure"],"pathophysiology":"Sneddon syndrome is a non-inflammatory occlusive arteriopathy affecting small and medium arteries of the skin and brain. Progressive endothelial injury and subintimal proliferation lead to partial then complete occlusion of dermal and cerebral vessels, producing the characteristic livedo racemosa and recurrent cerebral ischaemia. In antiphospholipid-antibody-positive cases a prothrombotic autoimmune mechanism contributes, whereas seronegative cases appear to reflect a primary vasculopathy; both converge on chronic small-vessel occlusion and cumulative brain injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Antiphospholipid syndrome without the full livedo picture","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other connective tissue disease","Systemic vasculitis such as polyarteritis nodosa","Cholesterol embolisation syndrome","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura and other thrombotic microangiopathies","Cryoglobulinaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antiphospholipid antibody panel including lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, and anti-beta-2-glycoprotein I","Brain MRI and MR angiography to document infarcts and small-vessel disease","Skin biopsy taken from the white centre of the livedo net to capture occluded arterioles","Connective tissue and vasculitis serology to exclude alternative causes","Echocardiography and thrombophilia screening to assess embolic and prothrombotic sources"],"redFlags":["Recurrent strokes in a young patient with persistent livedo","Rapidly progressive cognitive decline","Catastrophic thrombotic events suggesting antiphospholipid syndrome","New focal neurological deficits despite antithrombotic therapy"],"prognosis":"The condition tends to be progressive, with recurrent strokes and stepwise cognitive decline leading to vascular dementia in many patients; antithrombotic therapy and risk-factor control may slow but rarely halt the course.","pitfalls":["Dismissing livedo racemosa as benign livedo reticularis and missing the underlying vasculopathy","Failing to test for antiphospholipid antibodies, which alters anticoagulation decisions","Taking skin biopsy from the wrong site so the occlusive lesion is missed","Attributing young-onset stroke to a single episode rather than a systemic vasculopathy"],"management":["Antithrombotic therapy as the mainstay, using antiplatelet agents or anticoagulation with a vitamin K antagonist, particularly in antiphospholipid-antibody-positive patients.","Aggressive vascular risk-factor control including treatment of hypertension, statin therapy and smoking cessation.","Manage co-existing antiphospholipid syndrome according to its thrombotic risk where antibodies are confirmed.","Cognitive assessment and support given the tendency to stepwise vascular cognitive decline.","Surveillance with serial brain imaging, blood pressure monitoring and reassessment after recurrent ischaemic events.","Coordinate care across neurology, dermatology and rheumatology.","Reassess and intensify the antithrombotic strategy after recurrent strokes occurring despite treatment."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"social_anxiety_disorder":{"name":"Social anxiety disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["social phobia","SAD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The defining feature is marked, persistent fear of one or more social or performance situations in which the person feels exposed to possible scrutiny, with fear of acting in a way that will be humiliating or negatively judged","Feared situations are avoided or endured with intense distress, and the anxiety is out of proportion to any real threat and typically lasts many months","A performance-only subtype exists, confined to situations such as public speaking, and often responds well to targeted strategies","Onset is usually in childhood or adolescence, and the disorder is frequently underrecognised because sufferers are quiet and avoidant rather than disruptive","Comorbidity with depression, other anxiety disorders, and alcohol misuse is common, and alcohol is often used as maladaptive self-medication","First-line treatment is cognitive behavioural therapy with graded exposure and cognitive restructuring, with SSRIs or SNRIs as effective pharmacological options","For circumscribed performance anxiety, beta-adrenergic blockade can dampen peripheral symptoms such as tremor and palpitations, used situationally rather than daily","Distinguish from normal shyness by the degree of distress, avoidance, and functional impairment it causes"],"pathophysiology":"Social anxiety disorder reflects an interaction between heritable temperamental traits, notably behavioural inhibition in early childhood, and altered function of fear and threat-appraisal circuits involving the amygdala and prefrontal cortex. Dysregulation of serotonergic and dopaminergic systems, heightened sensitivity to social evaluation, and learned avoidance that prevents disconfirmation of feared outcomes together maintain the condition.","additionalDifferentials":["Normal shyness or introversion","Panic disorder with or without agoraphobia","Generalised anxiety disorder","Avoidant personality disorder","Autism spectrum disorder with social difficulties","Major depressive disorder with social withdrawal","Body dysmorphic disorder driving social avoidance"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured clinical interview focusing on feared and avoided situations","Standardised social anxiety rating scales to gauge severity and track response","Screening for comorbid depression and alcohol or substance use","Developmental history to distinguish from autism spectrum and personality disorders"],"redFlags":["Emerging suicidal ideation, particularly with comorbid depression","Escalating alcohol or sedative use to cope with social situations","Progressive social isolation with collapse of education, employment, or relationships"],"prognosis":"Social anxiety disorder is often chronic and, if untreated, can persist for many years with substantial functional impairment. Prognosis improves with cognitive behavioural therapy and pharmacotherapy, and is better with earlier onset of treatment and fewer comorbidities; the performance-only subtype generally carries a more favourable outlook.","pitfalls":["Dismissing the presentation as mere shyness and missing significant impairment","Overlooking comorbid alcohol misuse used as self-medication","Failing to include exposure-based work, which is central to durable improvement","Prescribing daily benzodiazepines and risking dependence","Stopping SSRIs prematurely before an adequate response has developed"],"management":["Cognitive behavioural therapy with graded exposure and cognitive restructuring as first-line treatment.","Pharmacotherapy with SSRIs or SNRIs as effective first-line agents, with monoamine oxidase inhibitors as a later option in resistant disease.","Beta-adrenergic blockers for the performance-only subtype to control autonomic symptoms of situational anxiety.","Identify and treat comorbid depression and alcohol or substance misuse, avoiding reliance on benzodiazepines.","Track severity and treatment response with standardised social anxiety rating scales and assess suicide risk.","Refer to specialist psychological or psychiatric services for severe, refractory or highly comorbid presentations."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"soft_tissue_sarcoma":{"name":"Soft Tissue & Bone Sarcoma","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sarcoma","osteosarcoma","GIST","Ewing sarcoma"],"chapter":96,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.727","clinicalPearls":["Sarcomas are rare (<1% of all malignancies) mesenchymal neoplasms arising in bone and soft tissues, usually of mesodermal origin and biologically distinct from the more common epithelial cancers (p.727).","The most common presentation of a soft tissue sarcoma is an asymptomatic mass; mechanical symptoms from pressure, traction, or entrapment of nerves or muscles may also occur (p.728).","Approximately 60% of soft tissue sarcomas arise in the extremities (lower limbs involved three times as often as upper), 30% in the trunk (retroperitoneum = 40% of trunk lesions), and 10% in the head and neck (p.727).","The pulmonary parenchyma is the most common site of metastasis for soft tissue sarcomas; lung radiograph plus chest CT is important for detecting lung metastases (p.728).","Histologic grade and primary tumor size are the most important prognostic factors; prognosis is related to AJCC stage and cure is common in the absence of metastatic disease (p.728).","About one-third of soft tissue sarcomas carry a characteristic translocation with diagnostic/prognostic relevance (e.g., t(X;18)(p11;q11) involving SYT in ~90% of synovial sarcomas) (p.728).","Most malignant GISTs carry activating c-kit mutations causing ligand-independent KIT receptor tyrosine kinase activation; ~5-10% have PDGFRA mutations, and KIT/PDGFRA wild-type GISTs may show SDH B/C/D mutations (p.728).","Osteosarcoma accounts for almost 45% of bone sarcomas, is a spindle-cell neoplasm producing osteoid/bone, with predilection for metaphyses of long bones (distal femur, proximal tibia, proximal humerus); ~60% occur in the second decade (p.729-730).","Classic osteosarcoma radiographic findings: a destructive moth-eaten lesion, spiculated periosteal reaction (sunburst appearance), and a cuff of periosteal new bone at the soft-tissue mass margin (Codman's triangle) (p.730).","The most important predictive factor for long-term survival in osteosarcoma is response to chemotherapy; long-term survival in extremity osteosarcoma ranges from 60-80% (p.730).","Ewing's sarcoma (~10-15% of bone sarcomas) peaks in the second decade, shows an 'onion peel' periosteal reaction with a soft-tissue mass, expresses p30/32 (MIC-2 product), and carries the t(11;22) translocation fusing fli-1 (chr 11) and ews (chr 22) (p.730).","Chondrosarcoma (~20-25% of bone sarcomas) is a tumor of adulthood/old age (peak 4th-6th decades) with predilection for flat bones (shoulder, pelvic girdles); it has an indolent natural history and is mostly chemoresistant, with surgical resection the mainstay (p.730).","Prostate, breast, and lung primaries account for 80% of all bone metastases; metastatic bone tumors are more common than primary bone tumors (p.730).","Bone is a common metastatic site for prostate, breast, lung, kidney, bladder, and thyroid carcinomas and for lymphomas and sarcomas; in descending frequency the sites are vertebrae, proximal femur, pelvis, ribs, sternum, proximal humerus, and skull (p.730).","When a patient with back pain develops neurologic signs or symptoms, emergency evaluation for spinal cord compression is indicated (p.730)."],"pathophysiology":"Sarcomas are mesenchymal neoplasms (mostly mesodermal, occasionally neuroectodermal) classified into ~20 groups by their pattern of differentiation toward normal tissue (e.g., rhabdomyosarcoma shows skeletal-muscle cross-striations, leiomyosarcoma shows smooth-muscle spindle fascicles, liposarcoma contains adipocytes). Malignant transformation of benign soft tissue tumors is extremely rare, except that malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors (neurofibrosarcoma, malignant schwannoma) can arise from neurofibromas in neurofibromatosis. Etiologic contributors include radiation (tumor arises in the irradiated field, risk rising with time), scar tissue (prior surgery, burn, fracture, foreign body), chemical carcinogens (polycyclic hydrocarbons, asbestos, dioxin), HHV-8 (Kaposi's sarcoma - the only sarcoma virus-associated), and immunodeficiency/immunosuppression. Genetic syndromes include Li-Fraumeni (germline p53), neurofibromatosis 1 (NF1 on chr 17, encoding neurofibromin which inhibits ras), and germline RB1 mutation (chr 13q14, predisposing to osteosarcoma and radiation-unrelated soft tissue sarcomas). About one-third of soft tissue sarcomas carry characteristic translocations; most malignant GISTs have activating c-kit (KIT receptor tyrosine kinase) mutations, fewer have PDGFRA mutations. Bone metastases occur via hematogenous spread (or local invasion); tumors produce osteolytic lesions (via PTH-related peptide, vitamin D-like steroids, prostaglandins, IL-1, TNF inducing osteoclasts) and/or osteoblastic lesions (cytokines activating osteoblasts), with most lesions showing a mix.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign soft tissue tumor / well-differentiated liposarcoma (atypical lipomatous tumor) - lacks metastatic potential","Benign bone lesions with malignant potential: enchondroma and osteochondroma (to chondrosarcoma); fibrous dysplasia, bone infarcts, Paget's disease (to UPS or osteosarcoma)","Lymphoma, embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma, and small-cell carcinoma (can be confused with the small round blue cells of Ewing's sarcoma)","Osteoporosis vs metastatic spine disease in older patients (cortical bone preserved in osteoporosis; cortical destruction with metastases)","Plasma cell tumors (the most common malignant tumor of bone)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Osteoporotic vertebral disease mimicking spinal bone metastasis in older patients","Benign enchondroma / osteochondroma mimicking low-grade chondrosarcoma (difficult to distinguish by x-ray or histology; new pain, inflammation, or progressive size increase suggests malignancy)","Well-encapsulated benign-appearing lesion produced by the compressed pseudocapsule of a soft tissue sarcoma"],"additionalInvestigations":["Core-needle (cutting needle) or small-incision biopsy of all new, persistent, or growing masses, placed so it can be encompassed in the definitive resection","Plain radiographs and MRI for primary tumors of extremities or head/neck (MRI best for intramedullary and soft-tissue extension)","CT for tumors of the chest, abdomen, or retroperitoneum (CT best for bone destruction and calcification pattern in osteosarcoma)","Chest radiograph and chest CT to detect lung metastases","Bone scan or FDG-PET to detect skeletal metastases (almost all osteosarcomas are hypervascular and PET-avid)","Radionuclide bone scanning (sensitive to new bone formation) for osteoblastic lesions; plain radiography for osteolytic lesions (may not be apparent until >1 cm)","Serum alkaline phosphatase (elevated with prominent osteoblastic activity) and monitoring for hypercalcemia (osteolytic) or hypocalcemia (extensive osteoblastic)"],"redFlags":["An enlarging, deep, or large soft tissue mass requires biopsy - all new, persistent, or growing masses should be biopsied rather than ignored","Back pain with new neurologic signs or symptoms mandates emergency evaluation for spinal cord compression","New onset of pain, signs of inflammation, and progressive size increase in a cartilaginous lesion suggest malignant (chondrosarcoma) transformation","Bone pain that develops gradually over weeks, is localized, and is more severe at night suggests bone metastasis","Pathologic fracture, nerve root or cord compression, or myelophthisis from a destructive bony lesion","Hypercalcemia symptoms in the setting of bony destruction"],"prognosis":"For soft tissue sarcoma, histologic grade and size are the most important prognostic factors and prognosis tracks AJCC stage; cure is common without metastatic disease, and a small number with metastases can still be cured. Metastatic soft tissue sarcomas are largely incurable, but up to 20% achieving complete response become long-term survivors. Historically most stage IV patients died within 12 months, but with multiple treatment lines, median survival in second-line and beyond is now 13-18 months and some live with stable/slowly progressive disease for years. Extremity osteosarcoma long-term survival is 60-80%, with response to chemotherapy the key predictor. Ewing's sarcoma overall cure rates approach 60-70% for localized tumors. For bone metastases, overall survival is related to the prognosis of the underlying tumor; some (lymphoma, Hodgkin's lymphoma) are curable while most are treated palliatively.","pitfalls":["Soft tissue sarcomas grow along fascial planes and compress surrounding tissue into a pseudocapsule, giving a deceptive well-encapsulated appearance; 'shelling out' (marginal excision / enucleation) results in a 50-90% probability of local recurrence - wide excision with a negative margin incorporating the biopsy site is standard.","A biopsy of an enlarging/deep/large mass must be planned so the tract can be encompassed in the definitive resection and does not compromise a limb-sparing operation; an inappropriately placed incision compromises future limb salvage.","Low-grade chondrosarcoma is difficult to distinguish from benign lesions on x-ray or histology, so diagnosis is influenced by clinical history and examination.","Osteosarcoma is radioresistant - radiation therapy has no role in routine management.","Forgetting non-pulmonary metastatic patterns: GISTs metastasize to liver, myxoid liposarcomas seek fatty tissue (retroperitoneum, mediastinum, subcutaneous), clear cell sarcomas may go to bone, and alveolar soft part sarcoma to CNS.","In older patients (especially women), metastatic spine disease must be distinguished from osteoporosis - cortical bone is preserved in osteoporosis but destroyed by metastatic cancer."],"management":["Wide surgical excision with clear margins as the mainstay of localised disease, planned around a correctly placed biopsy tract.","Radiotherapy in the neoadjuvant or adjuvant setting to improve local control, particularly for high-grade or large limb tumours.","Anthracycline-based chemotherapy with doxorubicin, often combined with ifosfamide, for high-grade, chemosensitive or metastatic disease.","Pulmonary metastasectomy for resectable lung metastases, the commonest site of spread, in selected patients.","Surveillance with clinical review, cross-sectional imaging of the primary site and chest imaging for lung metastases, stratified by grade and stage.","Manage through a specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team from diagnosis, given the rarity and histological complexity.","Early palliative and systemic-therapy input for unresectable metastatic disease, which is largely incurable."],"severityClassification":"Soft tissue sarcomas use the AJCC (8th edition) TNM/grade system: T1 (<=5 cm), T2 (>5-10 cm), T3 (>10-15 cm), T4 (>15 cm); N0/N1; M0/M1; with grade G1-G3 driving stage grouping (e.g., Stage IA = T1 N0 M0 G1; Stage IV = any T N1 M0, or any T any N M1). Bone sarcomas use the Musculoskeletal Tumor Society system (stage I low grade, II high grade, III nodal/distant metastases; A = intracompartmental, B = extracompartmental) and a TNM system: T1 (<=8 cm), T2 (>8 cm), T3 (discontinuous tumors in the primary bone); M1a (lung) vs M1b (other sites); grade G1-G4 (Ewing's always G4).","references":["Harrison 22e p.727","Harrison 22e p.728","Harrison 22e p.729","Harrison 22e p.730"],"crossLinks":["lung_cancer"]},"solar_retinopathy":{"name":"solar retinopathy","system":"ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["foveomacular retinitis","solar maculopathy","eclipse retinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by direct or prolonged viewing of the sun, classically after solar eclipse gazing, sun worship, or telescope viewing without adequate filters, and increasingly reported after use of hallucinogens or psychiatric illness where sun-staring occurs.","Patients present hours to a day or two after exposure with bilateral (occasionally asymmetrical) central or paracentral scotoma, blurred vision, metamorphopsia, dyschromatopsia and sometimes chromatopsia or micropsia.","Visual acuity is often only mildly to moderately reduced despite marked symptomatic disturbance, and a positive history of sun exposure is the key to diagnosis.","Early fundus signs are subtle, a small yellow-white foveal spot, later evolving into a sharply defined reddish foveal lesion, and may be missed without careful stereoscopic macular examination.","Optical coherence tomography is the most sensitive investigation, characteristically showing a focal defect in the outer retinal layers at the fovea with disruption of the ellipsoid zone and interdigitation zone.","The injury is predominantly photochemical (and to a lesser extent thermal) damage to the photoreceptors and retinal pigment epithelium rather than a burn from focused heat alone.","Fluorescein angiography is often normal or shows only a subtle window defect, helping to distinguish it from exudative maculopathies.","No proven specific treatment exists; management centres on preventing further exposure, patient education and observation, with corticosteroids of unproven benefit."],"pathophysiology":"Solar retinopathy results chiefly from photochemical injury to the outer retina when short-wavelength (blue) and near-ultraviolet light from direct solar viewing is focused by the optics of the eye onto the fovea. Absorbed radiant energy generates reactive oxygen species that damage photoreceptor outer segments and the underlying retinal pigment epithelium, with a smaller contribution from thermal energy that raises local tissue temperature. The fovea is preferentially affected because it lies at the point of optical focus and lacks the protective screening of inner retinal vasculature and, being cone-rich, is metabolically vulnerable. The acute insult produces oedema and disruption of the photoreceptor layer; over subsequent weeks the pigment epithelium and photoreceptors may partially recover or leave a permanent focal outer retinal defect, sometimes a small lamellar cavity, accounting for the residual scotoma. Predisposing factors include a clear atmosphere, dilated pupils, youth with clear ocular media, and pharmacological or behavioural states that prolong sun-gazing.","additionalDifferentials":["Laser pointer or welding-arc phototoxic maculopathy","Macular hole or impending macular hole","Acute idiopathic maculopathy","Central serous chorioretinopathy","Poppers (amyl nitrite) maculopathy","Torpedo maculopathy or other focal outer retinal defect","Occult macular dystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Spectral-domain optical coherence tomography of the fovea for outer retinal and ellipsoid zone defects","Fundus autofluorescence to detect focal pigment epithelial change","Amsler grid testing to map the central scotoma and metamorphopsia","Fluorescein angiography to exclude exudative or inflammatory maculopathy","Microperimetry to quantify central sensitivity loss","Colour vision testing for acquired dyschromatopsia","Multifocal electroretinography in atypical or medicolegal cases"],"redFlags":["History of prolonged or repeated unprotected sun-gazing suggesting cumulative bilateral injury","Persistent dense central scotoma beyond the expected recovery window indicating permanent foveal damage","Bilateral involvement threatening reading and driving vision","Underlying psychiatric illness or substance use driving compulsive sun-staring and re-injury","Progressive enlargement of the foveal defect suggesting an alternative or additional diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Most patients experience partial or substantial recovery of visual acuity over weeks to months, though a small residual central or paracentral scotoma and subtle metamorphopsia frequently persist owing to the focal outer retinal defect. Prognosis is worse with more prolonged exposure, bilateral involvement or repeated injury.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis because visual acuity is relatively preserved and early fundus findings are subtle, without eliciting the exposure history.","Mistaking the small foveal lesion for an early macular hole and pursuing surgical rather than conservative management.","Relying on ophthalmoscopy alone rather than obtaining optical coherence tomography, which is far more sensitive for the outer retinal defect.","Prescribing corticosteroids as if for an inflammatory maculopathy despite absence of proven benefit.","Neglecting to counsel on eye protection and address any underlying behavioural or psychiatric driver, risking recurrent phototoxic injury."],"management":["There is no proven specific treatment; management is largely supportive observation, as visual acuity often recovers partially or substantially over weeks to months.","Remove ongoing exposure and counsel firmly against further sun-gazing.","A short course of corticosteroids is sometimes used, though evidence of benefit is limited.","Prevention through patient education, use of certified solar-eclipse filters, and addressing underlying drivers such as hallucinogen use or psychiatric illness prompting sun-staring.","Monitor recovery with optical coherence tomography of the fovea, visual acuity and Amsler grid mapping of the scotoma.","Refer to ophthalmology for confirmation, macular assessment and exclusion of other maculopathies."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ryan's Retina (current edition)","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology (current edition)","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Retina and Vitreous"],"crossLinks":[]},"solar_urticaria":{"name":"Solar urticaria","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SU","light-induced urticaria","sun-induced urticaria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An immediate photodermatosis in which itching, erythema and weals appear within minutes of sun exposure and typically settle within an hour or two of moving into shade","Any waveband may be responsible, and defining the individual action spectrum (ultraviolet B, ultraviolet A or visible light) by phototesting guides both diagnosis and protection strategy","Habitually exposed skin such as the face and hands may be relatively tolerant, so weals are often most striking on normally covered areas that are suddenly exposed","Extensive exposure can provoke systemic symptoms including headache, wheeze, syncope and, rarely, anaphylaxis, particularly with sunbathing over a large body surface","It must be distinguished from other immediate reactions such as erythropoietic protoporphyria, which causes burning and pain rather than true weals","Management centres on trigger avoidance, appropriate photoprotection, and non-sedating antihistamines, with omalizumab a useful option for antihistamine-resistant disease","Phototherapy hardening can raise the threshold for symptoms and induce tolerance in selected patients"],"pathophysiology":"Solar urticaria is considered an immediate (type I) hypersensitivity reaction in which ultraviolet or visible radiation converts a precursor in the skin or circulation into a photoallergen; this in turn triggers IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation with histamine release, producing the characteristic weal and flare within minutes. The specific wavelengths capable of generating the photoallergen constitute the patient's action spectrum, which varies between individuals. Because the reaction depends on newly formed antigen, brief exposure can rapidly precipitate widespread urticaria and, when extensive, systemic mediator release.","additionalDifferentials":["Polymorphic light eruption","Erythropoietic protoporphyria","Drug-induced or chemical photosensitivity","Cholinergic urticaria triggered by heat from sun exposure","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Heat or contact urticaria from other stimuli"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Phototesting to reproduce weals and define the action spectrum","History clarifying the immediate onset and rapid resolution that distinguish it from delayed photodermatoses","Plasma, urine and faecal porphyrins to exclude erythropoietic protoporphyria","Antinuclear antibody testing to exclude lupus erythematosus","Photopatch testing where a photoallergic contact cause is suspected","Baseline assessment for systemic reactions in those with extensive exposure symptoms"],"redFlags":["Systemic symptoms such as wheeze, dizziness or collapse indicating anaphylaxis risk with widespread exposure","Burning pain rather than weals, especially from childhood, suggesting erythropoietic protoporphyria","Photosensitivity with systemic features or autoantibodies suggesting lupus","New symptoms coinciding with a recently introduced drug or topical agent"],"prognosis":"Solar urticaria is often a persistent condition, but many patients achieve good control with photoprotection, antihistamines and, where required, omalizumab or phototherapy hardening; spontaneous remission occurs in a proportion of cases over several years, while the main risk lies in systemic reactions during extensive exposure.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the immediate weals for delayed polymorphic light eruption and thereby misdirecting treatment","Failing to warn patients about the risk of systemic reactions during whole-body exposure such as sunbathing or swimming","Overlooking that visible light can be the trigger, which many sunscreens do not adequately block","Not defining the action spectrum, leading to inappropriate or inadequate photoprotection advice"],"management":["Non-sedating H1-antihistamines as first-line therapy, up-titrated for symptom control.","Trigger avoidance and photoprotection with broad-spectrum sunscreen and clothing tailored to the individual's action spectrum.","Omalizumab for antihistamine-resistant disease, with phototherapy hardening to induce tolerance in selected patients.","Emergency treatment of systemic reactions, including intramuscular adrenaline for anaphylaxis after extensive exposure.","Define the action spectrum by phototesting to guide protection and confirm the diagnosis.","Refer to dermatology or photodermatology for phototesting and for refractory disease requiring escalation such as immunomodulation."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","British Photodermatology Group guidance on the idiopathic photodermatoses"],"crossLinks":[]},"solid_pseudopapillary_neoplasm":{"name":"Solid pseudopapillary neoplasm of the pancreas","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPN","solid pseudopapillary tumour","Frantz tumour","papillary cystic neoplasm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare epithelial neoplasm of low malignant potential that arises predominantly in young women","Often presents as a large, well-circumscribed mass with mixed solid and cystic components, sometimes with haemorrhage or a fibrous capsule","Frequently asymptomatic or causes vague abdominal discomfort, and may be found after minor trauma to a bulky lesion","Immunohistochemistry typically shows nuclear beta-catenin staining reflecting an underlying CTNNB1 mutation, along with markers such as CD10 and progesterone receptor","Imaging shows an encapsulated lesion with heterogeneous internal architecture and areas of degeneration; calcification may be present","The mainstay of treatment is complete surgical resection, which is curative in the great majority of cases","Even lesions with local invasion or limited metastases can have a favourable outcome after resection, reflecting the indolent biology"],"pathophysiology":"Solid pseudopapillary neoplasms are uncommon tumours of uncertain cell lineage that are strongly linked to activating mutations of the CTNNB1 gene and consequent abnormal Wnt/beta-catenin signalling, demonstrable as aberrant nuclear beta-catenin accumulation. The neoplastic cells are poorly cohesive and tend to fall away from delicate fibrovascular cores, creating the characteristic pseudopapillary pattern, while areas of the tumour undergo cystic degeneration and haemorrhage, producing the typical mixed solid and cystic mass. The marked female predominance and hormone-receptor expression suggest a hormonal influence on growth. Malignant potential is low, but a minority show local invasion or metastasis, most often to the liver or peritoneum.","additionalDifferentials":["Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour","Mucinous cystic neoplasm of the pancreas","Serous cystadenoma","Pancreatoblastoma (in younger patients)","Cystic degeneration of an adenocarcinoma","Acinar cell carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT or MRI to define the encapsulated solid-cystic mass and haemorrhagic components","Endoscopic ultrasound with fine-needle biopsy for tissue diagnosis","Immunohistochemistry including beta-catenin, CD10, and progesterone receptor","Cyst or lesion fluid analysis when cystic degeneration predominates","Staging imaging to assess for hepatic or peritoneal spread","Serum tumour markers (usually normal) to help exclude other diagnoses"],"redFlags":["Local invasion of adjacent organs or vessels on imaging","Hepatic or peritoneal metastases","Rapid growth or rupture with haemorrhage","Older or male patients or atypical histology, which may portend more aggressive behaviour"],"prognosis":"Overall prognosis is very good, with high cure rates after complete resection even for large tumours; a small proportion recur or metastasise, but survival remains favourable, and resection of limited metastatic disease is often worthwhile.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the mass for a pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour or adenocarcinoma and misdirecting treatment","Assuming benignity and forgoing resection, given the recognised though low malignant potential","Overlooking the diagnosis in the rare male or older patient because of the female predominance","Incomplete excision leaving a positive margin in a curable tumour"],"management":["Complete surgical resection is the mainstay and is curative in the great majority, with the procedure such as enucleation, distal pancreatectomy or pancreaticoduodenectomy chosen by tumour location and spleen preservation where feasible.","Resection of limited hepatic or peritoneal metastatic disease is often worthwhile given the indolent behaviour.","Perioperative supportive care with pancreatic enzyme replacement and monitoring for diabetes where resection affects pancreatic function.","Chemotherapy and radiotherapy have only a limited role, reserved for rare unresectable or aggressive disease.","Postoperative surveillance imaging for the small risk of late recurrence or metastasis.","Manage through a hepatobiliary and pancreatic surgical multidisciplinary team."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","WHO classification of tumours of the digestive system","Reviews of solid pseudopapillary neoplasm outcomes and management"],"crossLinks":[]},"solitary_fibrous_tumor":{"name":"Solitary fibrous tumour","system":"Musculoskeletal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SFT","haemangiopericytoma","NAB2-STAT6 fusion tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone / Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fibroblastic tumour of intermediate behaviour that can arise almost anywhere, historically described in the pleura but common in extrapleural soft tissue","Usually a slow-growing, well-circumscribed mass; deep-seated lesions may reach a large size before detection","Defined molecularly by a recurrent NAB2-STAT6 gene fusion, which produces diffuse nuclear STAT6 expression on immunohistochemistry","Histology shows a patternless architecture of bland spindle cells, ropey collagen and branching staghorn-shaped thin-walled vessels","The term haemangiopericytoma is now regarded as part of the solitary fibrous tumour spectrum","A minority secrete insulin-like growth factor and cause hypoglycaemia, known as Doege-Potter syndrome","Complete surgical excision is the mainstay of treatment, with long-term follow-up because late recurrence and metastasis can occur"],"pathophysiology":"Solitary fibrous tumour is a fibroblastic neoplasm defined by an intrachromosomal NAB2-STAT6 gene fusion arising from an inversion on chromosome 12. This fusion converts the normally repressive NAB2 into a transcriptional activator of EGR1 target genes, driving cell proliferation, and results in nuclear relocation of STAT6 that can be detected immunohistochemically. The tumour is composed of a haphazard arrangement of bland ovoid to spindled cells set in variably collagenous stroma with characteristic dilated, branching thin-walled vessels. Although most behave in an indolent fashion, a subset shows malignant features and can metastasise, so behaviour is stratified using mitotic activity, size, necrosis and age.","additionalDifferentials":["Synovial sarcoma","Desmoid-type fibromatosis","Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans","Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour","Spindle cell lipoma","Gastrointestinal stromal tumour in abdominal sites"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI or CT to define the well-circumscribed mass and its vascularity","Core biopsy with histological assessment of the patternless pattern and staghorn vessels","STAT6 immunohistochemistry as a highly specific diagnostic marker","Molecular confirmation of the NAB2-STAT6 fusion in uncertain cases","Blood glucose evaluation if symptoms suggest tumour-related hypoglycaemia"],"redFlags":["Large size, high mitotic count or necrosis indicating a malignant solitary fibrous tumour","Rapid growth or evidence of distant spread on staging","Symptomatic hypoglycaemia suggesting a functioning tumour","Late local recurrence or metastasis during follow-up"],"prognosis":"Most solitary fibrous tumours are cured by complete excision, but the entity has intermediate malignant potential, and a subset recurs or metastasises, sometimes many years later. Risk stratification using size, mitoses, necrosis and age guides surveillance intensity.","pitfalls":["Assuming all solitary fibrous tumours are benign and omitting long-term follow-up","Confusing it with synovial sarcoma or other spindle-cell tumours without STAT6 testing","Overlooking tumour-associated hypoglycaemia as a paraneoplastic clue","Relying on morphology alone when the molecular signature clarifies the diagnosis"],"management":["Complete wide surgical excision with clear margins is the mainstay and offers cure for localised disease","Refer to a specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team for surgical planning and pathological risk stratification by size, mitoses, necrosis and age","Adjuvant or salvage radiotherapy for incompletely resected, recurrent or high-risk tumours","Antiangiogenic tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as pazopanib or sunitinib for unresectable or metastatic disease","Correct tumour-related (Doege-Potter) hypoglycaemia with dextrose and glucocorticoids, with resection being definitive","Prolonged, risk-stratified surveillance imaging because local recurrence and metastasis may appear many years later"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"solitary_fibrous_tumour":{"name":"Solitary fibrous tumour","system":"Musculoskeletal / Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SFT","Haemangiopericytoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Mesenchymal tumour of fibroblastic type that can arise almost anywhere; classically pleural but frequently found in meninges, orbit, deep soft tissue, retroperitoneum and abdominal viscera.","Defined by a recurrent NAB2-STAT6 gene fusion, and nuclear STAT6 immunohistochemistry is a highly sensitive and specific diagnostic marker.","The old term haemangiopericytoma is now regarded as part of the solitary fibrous tumour spectrum rather than a separate entity.","Most behave in an indolent fashion, but all are considered to have malignant potential and a subset metastasises, so none is truly benign.","A minority secrete insulin-like growth factor 2 and cause non-islet-cell tumour hypoglycaemia (Doege-Potter syndrome), typically with large tumours.","Histology shows a patternless architecture of bland spindle cells, ropey collagen and prominent branching staghorn vessels; CD34 is usually positive.","Risk stratification uses patient age, tumour size, mitotic activity and necrosis to estimate the likelihood of recurrence and metastasis.","Complete surgical resection is the treatment of choice; late recurrence and distant spread can occur many years later, mandating prolonged follow-up."],"pathophysiology":"Solitary fibrous tumour originates from a fibroblastic mesenchymal cell and is driven by a paracentric inversion on chromosome 12q that fuses the NAB2 and STAT6 genes. NAB2 is normally a transcriptional repressor of EGR1-responsive genes, but the fusion converts it into a transcriptional activator and drives constitutive expression of EGR1 target genes promoting proliferation and angiogenesis; the fusion protein also relocates STAT6 to the nucleus, the basis of the diagnostic immunostain. Different NAB2-STAT6 breakpoint variants correlate loosely with clinical behaviour, some being associated with the more cellular, aggressive phenotype. The characteristic branching haemangiopericytoma-like vasculature reflects the strong angiogenic drive. A subset of tumours aberrantly produces incompletely processed insulin-like growth factor 2, which activates insulin receptors and causes refractory hypoglycaemia (Doege-Potter syndrome). Progression to frankly malignant, dedifferentiated tumours is associated with additional genetic hits such as TERT promoter mutations and TP53 alterations, correlating with higher mitotic counts, necrosis and metastatic potential.","additionalDifferentials":["Synovial sarcoma","Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour","Fibromatosis (desmoid tumour)","Dedifferentiated or spindle-cell liposarcoma","Meningioma (for intracranial and spinal lesions)","Gastrointestinal stromal tumour (for abdominal lesions)","Deep benign fibrous histiocytoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT or MRI of the primary site to characterise vascularity and resectability","Core needle biopsy with histological assessment of mitoses and necrosis","STAT6 immunohistochemistry and CD34 staining","Molecular confirmation of NAB2-STAT6 fusion where diagnosis is uncertain","Fasting glucose and paired insulin / C-peptide with IGF-2 assessment if hypoglycaemic symptoms occur","Staging CT of the chest for suspected malignant or metastatic tumours","Long-term surveillance imaging because of the risk of late recurrence"],"redFlags":["Rapid tumour growth, increasing mitotic activity or necrosis on pathology","Symptomatic hypoglycaemia suggesting a large IGF-2-secreting tumour","New or progressive neurological deficit from an intracranial or spinal lesion","Development of pulmonary or other distant metastases","Recurrence at the resection site during follow-up"],"prognosis":"Most tumours are cured by complete resection, but outcome depends on validated risk factors (age, size, mitotic rate and necrosis); high-risk and dedifferentiated tumours carry a meaningful risk of late local recurrence and distant metastasis. Lifelong follow-up is advised because relapses may appear a decade or more after surgery.","pitfalls":["Assuming the tumour is benign; all solitary fibrous tumours retain malignant potential and require follow-up.","Missing Doege-Potter hypoglycaemia by not linking a large tumour to unexplained low glucose.","Relying on the outdated haemangiopericytoma label as though it were a distinct diagnosis.","Overlooking dedifferentiation, where a high-grade sarcomatous component may lose CD34 expression.","Incomplete or piecemeal resection that leaves positive margins and predisposes to recurrence."],"management":["Complete en bloc surgical resection with negative margins is the definitive treatment and mainstay for localised tumours","Refer to a sarcoma multidisciplinary team and stratify recurrence risk by age, size, mitotic rate and necrosis","Adjuvant or salvage radiotherapy where margins are involved or the tumour is high-risk or dedifferentiated","Antiangiogenic tyrosine kinase inhibitors or anthracycline-based chemotherapy for advanced, unresectable or metastatic disease","Correct non-islet-cell (Doege-Potter) hypoglycaemia with dextrose and glucocorticoids, with tumour removal being curative","Lifelong surveillance with cross-sectional imaging of the primary site and chest for late local and distant relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System (for meningeal lesions)","Soft-tissue sarcoma clinical practice guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"solitary_mastocytoma":{"name":"Solitary mastocytoma","system":"Skin","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mastocytoma of skin"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology references / mastocytosis literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A solitary (or few) cutaneous accumulation of mast cells, usually appearing in infancy","Presents as a single red-brown or yellowish plaque or nodule, sometimes with a peau d'orange surface","Stroking produces urtication and can cause blistering in infants (a strong Darier sign)","Vigorous rubbing may provoke flushing from localised mediator release","It is benign and typically regresses spontaneously during childhood","Management is conservative with reassurance and trigger avoidance; systemic disease is rare with a solitary lesion"],"pathophysiology":"A solitary mastocytoma is a localised benign collection of mast cells within the skin, part of the cutaneous mastocytosis spectrum. Mechanical stimulation degranulates the concentrated mast cells, releasing histamine and other mediators to produce a brisk weal-and-flare and, in infants, blistering. Because the mast cell burden is limited to a small area, systemic mediator effects are uncommon, and the lesion generally involutes as the child grows.","additionalDifferentials":["Juvenile xanthogranuloma","Spitz naevus","Congenital melanocytic naevus","Other cutaneous mastocytosis","Insect bite reaction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis with a positive Darier sign","Skin biopsy where the diagnosis is uncertain","Baseline tryptase if multiple lesions or systemic features","Reassurance and monitoring for spontaneous regression","Trigger history"],"redFlags":["Multiple lesions or systemic symptoms suggesting broader mastocytosis","Marked flushing or hypotension on stimulation","Atypical features suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Persistence or growth into later childhood"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent, with most solitary mastocytomas regressing spontaneously during childhood and rarely causing systemic problems.","pitfalls":["Provoking blistering or flushing by vigorously rubbing the lesion","Confusing it with a melanocytic or xanthogranulomatous lesion","Assuming systemic disease from a single lesion","Unnecessary excision of a self-limiting lesion"],"management":["Conservative management with reassurance, since most solitary mastocytomas involute spontaneously during childhood","Advise avoidance of mast-cell degranulating triggers such as friction, temperature extremes and drugs like NSAIDs and opioids","Oral H1-antihistamines to control pruritus, flushing and urtication from local mediator release","Topical corticosteroids for symptomatic or blistering lesions","Reserve surgical excision for persistent, troublesome or diagnostically uncertain lesions","Monitor for spontaneous regression and review if additional lesions or systemic mediator symptoms emerge"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dermatology reference texts (standard reference)","Mastocytosis clinical guidelines (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"solitary_rectal_ulcer_syndrome":{"name":"Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome","system":"Colorectal / Pelvic floor","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SRUS","solitary rectal ulcer","mucosal prolapse syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign but often misdiagnosed disorder linked to internal rectal (mucosal) prolapse and chronic straining, frequently with paradoxical pelvic floor contraction","Presents with rectal bleeding, mucus discharge, straining, a sense of incomplete evacuation, and sometimes the need to digitate to evacuate","Despite the name, lesions may be single or multiple and ulcerated, polypoid, or merely erythematous, typically on the anterior rectal wall a few centimetres above the anal verge","Biopsy shows the characteristic fibromuscular obliteration of the lamina propria, with smooth muscle extending upward between the crypts, and features of mucosal prolapse","Endoscopy with biopsy is essential to distinguish it from malignancy and inflammatory bowel disease, both of which it can mimic","First-line management targets the underlying evacuatory disorder: bulking agents, bowel retraining, and biofeedback to correct straining and dyssynergia","Surgery, such as rectopexy for overt or internal prolapse, is reserved for refractory cases or associated full-thickness prolapse"],"pathophysiology":"Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome arises from chronic mechanical trauma to the rectal mucosa, driven by internal intussusception or mucosal prolapse and repeated straining, often with paradoxical (dyssynergic) contraction of the puborectalis. The prolapsing mucosa is subjected to shear, ischaemia, and direct trauma, including self-digitation, producing ulceration, hyperaemia, or polypoid change. Histology reflects this, with fibromuscular obliteration of the lamina propria and derangement of the muscularis mucosae characteristic of mucosal prolapse. The name is a misnomer, as lesions vary in number and appearance.","additionalDifferentials":["Rectal adenocarcinoma","Inflammatory bowel disease (ulcerative colitis or Crohn's)","Ischaemic or infective proctitis","Rectal polyp or villous adenoma","Endometriosis involving the rectum","Traumatic or radiation proctitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible sigmoidoscopy or colonoscopy with biopsy for diagnosis and to exclude malignancy","Histology demonstrating fibromuscular obliteration of the lamina propria","Defecography (fluoroscopic or MR) to demonstrate internal or external prolapse and evacuatory dysfunction","Anorectal manometry and balloon expulsion to assess dyssynergia","Endoanal ultrasound where sphincter or prolapse assessment is required","Colonoscopy to exclude synchronous colonic pathology"],"redFlags":["An endoscopic appearance mimicking malignancy, making biopsy mandatory before treatment","Progressive bleeding, weight loss, or anaemia","Failure to heal despite treatment of the evacuatory disorder","Development of a stricture or mass"],"prognosis":"A benign condition, but frequently chronic and difficult to treat; symptoms improve with correction of straining and biofeedback in many patients, while refractory cases with prolapse may require surgery with variable success.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the lesion for carcinoma or Crohn's disease, or the reverse, without biopsy","Treating the ulcer topically while ignoring the underlying evacuatory disorder","Overlooking dyssynergic defecation amenable to biofeedback","Proceeding to surgery without confirming prolapse on defecography"],"management":["First-line conservative therapy targeting the evacuatory disorder: high-fibre diet, bulk-forming laxatives and adequate fluids to reduce straining","Behavioural bowel retraining with biofeedback to correct paradoxical (dyssynergic) pelvic floor contraction and discourage digitation","Patient education to stop straining and reduce time spent attempting to evacuate","Topical agents such as sucralfate, mesalazine or short-course corticosteroids to promote mucosal healing","Endoscopic surveillance with biopsy to confirm the diagnosis and exclude malignancy and inflammatory bowel disease","Surgical repair such as rectopexy for associated internal or overt prolapse, reserved for cases refractory to conservative measures","Refer to a colorectal and pelvic-floor multidisciplinary team for refractory or prolapse-related disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ASCRS Textbook of Colon and Rectal Surgery","Keighley & Williams' Surgery of the Anus, Rectum and Colon"],"crossLinks":[]},"somatic_symptom_disorder":{"name":"Somatic symptom disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SSD","somatization","somatisation disorder","somatoform disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by one or more distressing physical symptoms accompanied by excessive thoughts, feelings, or behaviours related to those symptoms","The diagnosis rests on the disproportionate and persistent nature of the response, not on whether the symptoms are medically explained","Features include disproportionate and persistent worry about the seriousness of symptoms, high health-related anxiety, and excessive time and energy devoted to symptoms or health concerns","Symptoms are typically persistent (often more than six months) even if individual complaints vary over time","Distinguish from illness anxiety disorder (preoccupation with having illness with few or no somatic symptoms) and from conversion disorder (neurological symptoms incompatible with recognised disease)","Management principles emphasise a consistent single coordinating clinician, regular scheduled visits, limiting unnecessary tests and referrals, and validating distress while gently shifting focus toward functioning","Cognitive behavioural therapy is the best-supported psychotherapy; treat comorbid depression and anxiety, where SSRIs or SNRIs may help","A genuine coexisting medical illness does not exclude the diagnosis, so maintain appropriate but restrained medical vigilance"],"pathophysiology":"Somatic symptom disorder is understood through a biopsychosocial model in which heightened attention to and amplification of bodily sensations, cognitive misinterpretation of benign symptoms as dangerous, and autonomic and stress responses interact. Predisposing factors include early adverse experiences, trauma, comorbid anxiety and depression, and learned illness behaviour, with reinforcement from repeated medical attention perpetuating the pattern.","additionalDifferentials":["Illness anxiety disorder","Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder)","Factitious disorder and malingering","Major depressive disorder and anxiety disorders with somatic features","Panic disorder","Undiagnosed genuine medical illness","Body dysmorphic disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Focused, proportionate medical evaluation guided by objective findings rather than repeated broad testing","Review of prior investigations to avoid duplication","Screening for comorbid depression and anxiety","Assessment of functional impairment and healthcare utilisation patterns"],"redFlags":["New objective clinical signs suggesting an emergent genuine medical condition","Suicidal ideation associated with comorbid depression","Escalating analgesic or sedative use and dependence","Iatrogenic harm from repeated invasive investigations or procedures"],"prognosis":"Somatic symptom disorder is often chronic and fluctuating, but consistent, coordinated care can reduce distress, unnecessary investigations, and healthcare costs while improving function. Prognosis is worse with prolonged illness, multiple comorbidities, and ongoing reinforcement through repeated medical intervention.","pitfalls":["Ordering repeated investigations and referrals that reinforce illness behaviour and risk iatrogenic harm","Dismissing symptoms as not real, which damages the therapeutic alliance","Assuming the diagnosis excludes genuine disease and missing a new medical problem","Failing to identify and treat comorbid depression and anxiety","Lack of a single coordinating clinician leading to fragmented, duplicated care"],"management":["Establish a single coordinating clinician offering regular, scheduled appointments rather than visits driven by symptom escalation","Limit unnecessary investigations, referrals and interventions while validating the reality of the patient's distress","Cognitive behavioural therapy as the core psychological treatment to reframe catastrophic interpretation of bodily symptoms","Treat comorbid depression and anxiety, including with antidepressants such as SSRIs or SNRIs where indicated","Shift the focus of care from eliminating symptoms towards improving daily functioning and coping","Encourage graded activity and structured exercise as rehabilitative measures","Coordinate care across primary care, psychiatry and relevant specialists to avoid fragmented, duplicated management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"somatostatinoma":{"name":"Somatostatinoma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["somatostatin-secreting tumour","somatostatin-secreting neuroendocrine tumour","D-cell tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic triad reflects somatostatin's inhibitory actions: diabetes mellitus, gallstones, and steatorrhoea with weight loss","The full syndrome is uncommon; many tumours are found incidentally or present with mass effect rather than florid hormonal features","Pancreatic tumours more often produce the complete inhibitory syndrome, whereas duodenal or periampullary lesions may present with obstructive jaundice or bleeding","Diabetes tends to be mild because somatostatin suppresses both insulin and glucagon","Gallstones arise from reduced gallbladder contractility and diminished cholecystokinin-driven emptying","Steatorrhoea results from inhibition of pancreatic exocrine secretion and bile flow","A markedly elevated plasma somatostatin supports the diagnosis, and somatostatin-receptor imaging aids localisation and staging","Duodenal tumours may be associated with neurofibromatosis type 1, and pancreatic lesions with multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1"],"pathophysiology":"Somatostatinoma is a rare neuroendocrine tumour of pancreatic delta cells or duodenal somatostatin-producing cells. Excess somatostatin exerts broad inhibitory effects across the gastroenteropancreatic axis, suppressing insulin and glucagon (producing mild diabetes), reducing gallbladder motility and cholecystokinin action (favouring gallstone formation), and dampening pancreatic exocrine and biliary secretion (causing fat malabsorption and steatorrhoea). Many tumours are large and metastatic at diagnosis, and periampullary lesions may declare themselves through local obstruction rather than hormonal excess.","additionalDifferentials":["Other functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours such as glucagonoma or VIPoma","Non-functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour","Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","Chronic pancreatitis with exocrine insufficiency","Cholelithiasis from unrelated causes","Coeliac disease or other malabsorptive states"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting plasma somatostatin concentration","Somatostatin-receptor imaging such as gallium DOTATATE positron emission tomography","Cross-sectional imaging and endoscopic ultrasound to localise and stage the tumour","Assessment of glycaemic status and pancreatic exocrine function","Abdominal imaging to document gallstones","Screening for neurofibromatosis type 1 and multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 where relevant"],"redFlags":["Obstructive jaundice from a periampullary tumour","Hepatic metastases at presentation","Progressive weight loss and malnutrition from severe steatorrhoea","Gastrointestinal haemorrhage from a duodenal lesion","Features suggesting an inherited tumour syndrome"],"prognosis":"Outlook depends heavily on stage; small, completely resected tumours can be cured, but a large proportion are advanced with hepatic or nodal metastases at diagnosis, when surgical resection and somatostatin analogues aim to control disease and symptoms rather than cure.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis because the full inhibitory triad is often incomplete or subtle","Attributing the mild diabetes and gallstones to common coincidental disease","Overlooking a periampullary tumour presenting purely with obstructive symptoms","Failing to screen for associated neurofibromatosis type 1 or multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1","Assuming resectability without adequate somatostatin-receptor and cross-sectional staging"],"management":["Complete surgical resection by enucleation or pancreaticoduodenectomy is the mainstay and potentially curative for localised tumours","Somatostatin analogues such as octreotide or lanreotide to control hormonal symptoms and slow progression in advanced disease","Peptide receptor radionuclide therapy with lutetium-177 DOTATATE for progressive somatostatin-receptor-positive metastatic disease","Manage syndrome components with insulin or oral hypoglycaemic agents for diabetes and pancreatic enzyme replacement for steatorrhoea","Cholecystectomy for symptomatic gallstones, often performed at the time of tumour resection","Liver-directed therapy such as embolisation or metastasectomy for hepatic metastases","Screen for associated neurofibromatosis type 1 and MEN1, with referral for genetic counselling where relevant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","DeGroot's Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"sorsby_fundus_dystrophy":{"name":"Sorsby fundus dystrophy","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SFD","TIMP3","Sorsby pseudoinflammatory macular dystrophy","hereditary haemorrhagic macular dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ryan's Retina / Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant macular dystrophy caused by TIMP3 mutations, with onset of symptoms typically in the fourth to fifth decade","Patients often report early nyctalopia and difficulty with dark adaptation, followed by sudden central visual loss from choroidal neovascularisation","Fundus shows confluent yellow-white drusen-like deposits at the level of Bruch's membrane, later with atrophy, pigmentary change and subretinal neovascular membranes and haemorrhage","It resembles neovascular age-related macular degeneration but occurs earlier and with a clear dominant family history","A characteristic finding is thickening of Bruch's membrane with abnormal deposits, reflecting impaired extracellular matrix turnover","Choroidal neovascularisation is common and responds to anti-vascular endothelial growth factor therapy, so prompt recognition of new distortion is important","Management combines anti-angiogenic treatment of neovascular membranes, low-vision support and genetic counselling; some patients report benefit from vitamin A supplementation for the dark-adaptation defect"],"pathophysiology":"Sorsby fundus dystrophy is caused by dominant mutations in TIMP3, the tissue inhibitor of metalloproteinase-3, which regulates extracellular matrix turnover and angiogenesis in the retinal pigment epithelium and Bruch's membrane. Mutant TIMP3 accumulates and thickens Bruch's membrane with abnormal deposits, impairing transport between the choroid and retina and disturbing the visual cycle, which produces the early dark-adaptation defect. The abnormal, thickened Bruch's membrane and dysregulated angiogenesis predispose to choroidal neovascularisation, haemorrhage and subsequent chorioretinal atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Neovascular (wet) age-related macular degeneration","Pattern dystrophies and Best disease","Malattia leventinese / Doyne honeycomb retinal dystrophy","Late-onset autosomal dominant macular dystrophies","Central areolar choroidal dystrophy","Myopic choroidal neovascularisation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Optical coherence tomography for subretinal fluid, deposits and neovascular membranes","Fluorescein and/or indocyanine green angiography or OCT-angiography to detect choroidal neovascularisation","Dark adaptometry demonstrating the characteristic prolonged rod recovery","Fundus autofluorescence to map retinal pigment epithelium atrophy","Full-field electroretinography as disease advances","Molecular genetic testing for TIMP3 mutations"],"redFlags":["Acute central distortion, scotoma or metamorphopsia indicating choroidal neovascularisation needing urgent treatment","Subretinal haemorrhage at the macula","Early unexplained night-vision difficulty in a patient with a relevant family history","Affected relatives requiring surveillance given dominant inheritance"],"prognosis":"Without treatment, choroidal neovascularisation frequently causes severe central visual loss, but timely anti-angiogenic therapy can preserve vision; peripheral vision is generally retained while progressive chorioretinal atrophy limits central acuity long term.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing early-onset neovascular disease as ordinary age-related macular degeneration and missing the hereditary cause","Overlooking the early dark-adaptation defect that can precede visual loss","Delaying treatment of choroidal neovascularisation, which is often treatable","Failing to counsel and screen relatives for a dominantly inherited condition"],"management":["Prompt intravitreal anti-vascular endothelial growth factor therapy for choroidal neovascularisation to preserve central vision","Vitamin A supplementation may improve the early nyctalopia and dark-adaptation defect","Teach Amsler grid self-monitoring so new distortion prompting urgent review is detected early","Regular surveillance with optical coherence tomography and fundus imaging for neovascular activity and atrophy progression","Low-vision rehabilitation and visual aids as chorioretinal atrophy limits central acuity","Refer to a medical retina specialist and to clinical genetics for TIMP3 testing","Genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal dominant disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ryan's Retina","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"sotos_syndrome":{"name":"Sotos syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cerebral gigantism","NSD1-related overgrowth","Sotos sequence"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad of prenatal and postnatal overgrowth (tall stature and macrocephaly), a characteristic facial gestalt, and variable learning difficulty","The facial pattern includes a broad prominent forehead, sparse frontotemporal hair, downslanting palpebral fissures, malar flushing, and a long narrow chin","Advanced bone age and rapid early growth are typical, although final adult height is often within or near the normal range","Caused by haploinsufficiency of NSD1 on chromosome 5q35, usually from a de novo point mutation or a 5q35 microdeletion","Inheritance is autosomal dominant with most cases arising de novo; microdeletions are more common in some populations and tend to give more marked learning difficulty","Neonatal hypotonia, feeding difficulty, jaundice, and delayed early motor and speech milestones are common, with behaviour and autistic features seen in a proportion","Tumour risk is modestly increased but low overall, so intensive tumour surveillance is generally not recommended for isolated NSD1 disease"],"pathophysiology":"Sotos syndrome results from loss of function of NSD1, a gene encoding a histone methyltransferase that regulates chromatin and transcription of growth and developmental pathways. Haploinsufficiency, whether from an intragenic mutation or a contiguous 5q35 microdeletion, disturbs regulation of growth-promoting genes, producing generalised pre- and postnatal overgrowth with disproportionate head growth. The same disturbance of neuronal chromatin regulation underlies the cognitive and behavioural phenotype, while the recognisable facial gestalt reflects altered craniofacial patterning.","additionalDifferentials":["Weaver syndrome (EZH2-related overgrowth)","Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","Simpson-Golabi-Behmel syndrome","Fragile X syndrome and other macrocephaly-associated intellectual disability syndromes","Malan syndrome (NFIX-related overgrowth)","Marfan syndrome and other tall-stature connective tissue disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing for NSD1 sequence variants and 5q35 deletion by targeted analysis, microarray, or overgrowth gene panel","Chromosomal microarray to detect contiguous 5q35 microdeletion and other copy-number causes","Bone age radiograph, which is characteristically advanced","Developmental and neuropsychological assessment for learning and behavioural needs","Echocardiography and renal ultrasound to evaluate associated structural anomalies","Cranial imaging where indicated to characterise ventricular or midline brain findings"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging head circumference or neurological signs prompting exclusion of hydrocephalus or an intracranial lesion","Seizures requiring assessment and management","New mass or unexplained systemic symptoms raising concern for an embryonal tumour","Feeding failure or profound neonatal hypotonia needing supportive care"],"prognosis":"Most individuals have a good general prognosis with growth normalising through childhood and adult height often near the normal range, though learning difficulty and behavioural challenges frequently persist and require support. Overall tumour risk is low and life expectancy is generally normal.","pitfalls":["Attributing tall stature or macrocephaly to familial variation and missing the diagnosis","Confusing Sotos with Weaver syndrome, which shares overgrowth but has distinct facial features and gene","Applying intensive tumour surveillance protocols that are not warranted by the low risk","Overlooking behavioural and autistic features that need targeted intervention"],"management":["Developmental support with early intervention, speech and language therapy, physiotherapy and educational assistance for learning difficulty","Manage neonatal problems including feeding support for poor feeding and phototherapy for jaundice","Behavioural and psychological support for autistic features and behavioural difficulties","Monitor growth and bone age, with reassurance that adult height is usually near the normal range","Surveillance for associated anomalies with echocardiography and renal ultrasound, and management of scoliosis and seizures","Refer to clinical genetics for NSD1 testing and diagnostic confirmation","Genetic counselling and family screening regarding the autosomal dominant, usually de novo, inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: NSD1-Related Sotos Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"southeast_asian_ovalocytosis":{"name":"Southeast Asian ovalocytosis","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SAO","Melanesian ovalocytosis","Stomatocytic elliptocytosis","Band 3 ovalocytosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant red cell membrane disorder common in Malaysia, Papua New Guinea, the Philippines and Indonesia.","Caused by a characteristic in-frame deletion in SLC4A1 (band 3, anion exchanger 1), producing rigid oval red cells.","Usually asymptomatic with little or no haemolysis, and often an incidental blood-film finding.","Film shows oval (elliptical) red cells, some crossed by one or two transverse ridges or a central slit (stomatocytic ovalocytes).","Increased membrane rigidity reduces red cell deformability, and osmotic fragility is decreased so the cells resist lysis.","Confers protection against severe and cerebral falciparum malaria, explaining its high frequency as a balanced polymorphism.","The homozygous state is thought to be lethal and is not observed.","May be co-inherited with distal renal tubular acidosis caused by other SLC4A1 alleles."],"pathophysiology":"Southeast Asian ovalocytosis is an autosomal dominant red cell membrane disorder caused by a characteristic in-frame deletion in SLC4A1, the gene encoding band 3 (anion exchanger 1). The mutant band 3 misfolds and binds more tightly to the underlying membrane skeleton, greatly increasing membrane rigidity and reducing red cell deformability while impairing anion transport; the resulting cells are oval, often crossed by one or two transverse ridges. The rigid, poorly deformable membrane hinders invasion and growth of malaria parasites and reduces cytoadherence of infected cells, conferring protection against Plasmodium falciparum, including cerebral malaria, and Plasmodium vivax, which is thought to explain the high gene frequency in endemic regions as a balanced polymorphism. The homozygous state is believed to be incompatible with life and is not observed. Certain other SLC4A1 alleles cause distal renal tubular acidosis, and the ovalocytosis deletion can be co-inherited with and modify such phenotypes.","additionalDifferentials":["Hereditary elliptocytosis from spectrin or protein 4.1 defects","Hereditary spherocytosis","Hereditary pyropoikilocytosis","Iron deficiency (elliptocytes and pencil cells)","Megaloblastic anaemia (oval macrocytes)","Myelodysplastic syndrome (acquired elliptocytosis)","Other SLC4A1-related band 3 disorders and distal renal tubular acidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood film showing oval red cells with transverse ridges or a central slit","Full blood count and red cell indices, usually near-normal","Reticulocyte count and haemolysis markers, typically minimal","Osmotic fragility testing showing reduced fragility and increased osmotic resistance","SLC4A1 molecular testing for the ovalocytosis deletion","Ektacytometry or eosin-5-maleimide binding to assess deformability and membrane protein","Acid-base and urinary pH assessment if renal tubular acidosis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Significant unexplained anaemia or haemolysis, pointing to a co-existing disorder rather than ovalocytosis alone","Features of distal renal tubular acidosis such as faltering growth or nephrocalcinosis","Marked neonatal jaundice suggesting a compound membrane defect","Clinical severity out of keeping with a benign trait, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Southeast Asian ovalocytosis is essentially a benign trait with normal life expectancy. Its significance lies in malaria protection, correct blood-film recognition and its occasional association with renal tubular acidosis.","pitfalls":["Misreading the film as myelodysplasia or a pathological elliptocytosis","Attributing meaningful haemolysis to ovalocytosis, which is usually benign; look for a co-inherited condition","Overlooking co-inherited distal renal tubular acidosis","Assuming that a homozygous form can occur"],"management":["Reassurance and no specific treatment, as the trait is benign and usually asymptomatic","Ensure correct peripheral blood-film recognition to avoid misdiagnosis and unnecessary investigation","Monitor for and treat associated distal renal tubular acidosis with oral alkali such as bicarbonate or citrate if it develops","Consider folic acid supplementation during pregnancy or periods of increased erythropoietic stress","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant band 3 (SLC4A1) disorder, noting that homozygosity is generally lethal in utero","Continue standard antimalarial precautions when travelling despite the trait's partial protection against falciparum malaria"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Haematology references","Red cell membrane disorder references","Tropical medicine references"],"crossLinks":[]},"sparganosis":{"name":"Sparganosis (Spirometra)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Spirometra infection","sparganum infection","larval diphyllobothriid disease","plerocercoid larva migrans"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / Mandell","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by migrating plerocercoid (sparganum) larvae of Spirometra tapeworms, with humans as accidental intermediate hosts","Acquired by drinking water containing infected copepods, eating raw or undercooked frog, snake or other paratenic hosts, or applying raw amphibian flesh as a traditional poultice","The larva does not mature but migrates through tissues, producing a slowly moving, sometimes painful subcutaneous nodule that may appear and disappear","Ocular sparganosis causes periorbital swelling, pain and lid oedema, while cerebral sparganosis can cause seizures, headache and focal neurological deficits","Peripheral eosinophilia is common and imaging may show a migrating or serpiginous track or an enhancing granuloma","Definitive treatment is surgical removal of the whole larva; anthelmintic drugs are generally ineffective against the tissue-dwelling sparganum","Prevention includes drinking safe water, cooking meat thoroughly and avoiding the practice of applying raw amphibian tissue to wounds or eyes"],"pathophysiology":"Spirometra species are diphyllobothriid tapeworms whose adults live in cats, dogs and other carnivores. Their life cycle uses copepods as first intermediate hosts and amphibians, reptiles and other vertebrates as second intermediate or paratenic hosts harbouring the plerocercoid larva, the sparganum. Humans become accidental hosts by swallowing infected copepods in water, by eating raw or undercooked intermediate hosts, or by direct inoculation when raw frog or snake flesh is applied to skin, eyes or wounds as a poultice. The larva cannot mature in humans; instead it migrates through subcutaneous tissue, muscle, the eye or the central nervous system, provoking a local inflammatory and eosinophilic reaction with granuloma formation and, in the brain, surrounding oedema and mass effect. The living, motile larva accounts for the characteristic migratory and space-occupying features of the disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Cutaneous larva migrans and other migratory helminth infection","Neurocysticercosis and other cerebral cystic or granulomatous disease","Coenurosis and hydatid disease of the central nervous system or soft tissue","Soft-tissue lipoma, cyst or abscess","Orbital cellulitis or other periorbital inflammatory lesion","Primary or secondary intracranial tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI or CT to localise migrating tracks, granulomas or intracranial lesions","Full blood count for eosinophilia","Serology for sparganum antigens where available","Histological examination of the excised larva to confirm the diagnosis","Ophthalmological assessment in suspected ocular disease","Exclusion of other cystic cestode infection by appropriate serology"],"redFlags":["Seizures or focal neurological signs indicating cerebral involvement","Progressive ocular swelling threatening vision","An enlarging or migrating intracranial lesion with mass effect","Recurrent or incompletely excised larva with persistent symptoms"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is good when a subcutaneous or ocular larva is completely removed, but cerebral sparganosis can cause lasting neurological deficits and may recur if the larva is not fully excised.","pitfalls":["Relying on anthelmintic drugs, which rarely eradicate the tissue-dwelling sparganum","Incomplete surgical removal leaving viable larva and causing relapse","Mistaking a migrating subcutaneous nodule for a benign lipoma or cyst","Overlooking the history of raw-flesh poultice application or untreated water consumption"],"management":["Complete surgical excision of the entire larva is the definitive and curative treatment","Anthelmintic drugs are generally ineffective against the tissue-dwelling sparganum and are not relied upon","Corticosteroids to reduce peri-lesional inflammation and oedema, particularly in cerebral disease","Antiepileptic drugs to control seizures arising from cerebral sparganosis","Neurosurgical or ophthalmological referral for central nervous system or ocular larvae","Preventive advice: avoid drinking untreated water, eating raw or undercooked frog or snake, and applying raw amphibian poultices","Confirm complete removal and follow up, as incompletely excised larvae may recur"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"spasmodic_dysphonia":{"name":"Spasmodic dysphonia (laryngeal dystonia)","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["laryngeal dystonia","spastic dysphonia","adductor spasmodic dysphonia","abductor spasmodic dysphonia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A focal, task-specific laryngeal dystonia in which involuntary spasms of the intrinsic laryngeal muscles disrupt voice during speech but spare other laryngeal functions","The adductor type is most common, producing a strained, strangled, effortful voice with abrupt voice breaks on vowels","The abductor type produces breathy breaks and aphonic whispered segments, especially on voiceless consonants","Symptoms are task-specific: the voice may be normal during whispering, singing, laughing, or crying but abnormal during connected speech","It is frequently accompanied by voice tremor and may coexist with other focal dystonias","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by perceptual voice assessment and flexible laryngoscopy showing task-specific spasms with a structurally normal larynx","Botulinum toxin injection into the affected laryngeal muscles is the mainstay of treatment, giving temporary relief that requires periodic repetition"],"pathophysiology":"Spasmodic dysphonia is a focal task-specific dystonia of the larynx, considered a disorder of central motor control within the basal ganglia and their cortical and brainstem connections rather than a structural or purely psychogenic problem. Abnormal, involuntary co-contraction of the laryngeal muscles occurs selectively during the fine motor task of speech. In the adductor form the vocal folds press together excessively, interrupting phonation with strained breaks; in the abductor form inappropriate opening produces breathy, aphonic breaks. The larynx is structurally normal and the dystonia typically does not affect breathing or swallowing.","additionalDifferentials":["Muscle tension dysphonia","Vocal tremor (essential tremor of the voice)","Psychogenic (functional) dysphonia","Vocal fold paresis or paralysis","Structural vocal fold lesions (nodules, polyps)","Neurodegenerative dysarthria","Reflux-related laryngeal irritation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible fibreoptic laryngoscopy and stroboscopy during connected speech and other vocal tasks","Perceptual and acoustic voice analysis by a speech and language therapist","Laryngeal electromyography in selected or atypical cases","Neurological examination for other focal dystonias or tremor","MRI of the brain if an atypical or secondary dystonia is suspected","Trial of botulinum toxin as both a therapeutic and confirmatory measure"],"redFlags":["Associated stridor or airway compromise suggesting a different or additional laryngeal disorder","Progressive generalised neurological signs suggesting a wider neurodegenerative process","A structural vocal fold lesion on endoscopy","Rapid onset after a clear psychological precipitant suggesting a functional disorder"],"prognosis":"Spasmodic dysphonia is a chronic condition without spontaneous cure, but botulinum toxin injections reliably improve the voice for several months at a time, and combined botulinum toxin and voice therapy allow most patients to maintain functional communication.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as muscle tension or psychogenic dysphonia and delaying appropriate treatment","Failing to assess the voice across multiple tasks to reveal its task-specific nature","Overlooking coexisting vocal tremor, which affects treatment response","Confusing abductor spasmodic dysphonia with vocal fold paresis","Attributing all voice breaks to reflux or structural lesions"],"management":["Botulinum toxin injection into the affected intrinsic laryngeal muscles is the mainstay, repeated as the effect wears off","Voice (speech and language) therapy alongside botulinum toxin to optimise and prolong vocal function","Oral agents such as anticholinergics or benzodiazepines have a limited adjunctive role, mainly where voice tremor coexists","Surgical options such as selective laryngeal denervation-reinnervation or thyroplasty for refractory cases","Educate the patient that this is a chronic condition in which repeated treatment maintains functional communication","Coordinate multidisciplinary care between otolaryngology, neurology and speech and language therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"spasmus_nutans":{"name":"Spasmus nutans","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["spasmus nutans syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign disorder of infancy defined by a triad of fine rapid asymmetric nystagmus, head nodding and an anomalous head posture","The nystagmus is typically small-amplitude, high-frequency and often dissociated or intermittent between the two eyes","Onset is usually in the first year of life and the condition tends to resolve spontaneously within a few years","It is a diagnosis of exclusion because chiasmal and suprasellar tumours (such as optic pathway gliomas) can mimic it","Neuroimaging is mandatory to exclude an intracranial lesion before accepting a benign diagnosis","Retinal dystrophies can produce similar nystagmus and may need electrophysiological testing to exclude"],"pathophysiology":"Spasmus nutans is an idiopathic, usually self-limiting disorder of ocular motor control in infancy of unknown mechanism, in which the head nodding is thought to be a partially compensatory response to the shimmering nystagmus. Because an identical clinical picture can be produced by anterior visual pathway tumours compressing the chiasm and by certain retinal dystrophies, the benign form can only be diagnosed once these structural and retinal causes have been excluded.","additionalDifferentials":["Optic pathway or suprasellar glioma","Congenital (infantile) nystagmus syndrome","Retinal dystrophy causing sensory nystagmus","Bobble-head doll syndrome","Acquired nystagmus from posterior fossa disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain and anterior visual pathways","Detailed ophthalmological and fundus examination","Electroretinography where a retinal dystrophy is suspected","Ocular motility and orthoptic assessment","Developmental and neurological review"],"redFlags":["Optic disc pallor or afferent pupillary defect","Failure to resolve or progression of signs","Associated neurological deficit or failure to thrive","Monocular or grossly asymmetric visual loss"],"prognosis":"The idiopathic form is benign and usually resolves spontaneously over months to a few years; prognosis otherwise depends entirely on excluding and, if present, treating a structural or retinal cause.","pitfalls":["Accepting the diagnosis without neuroimaging and missing an optic pathway glioma","Overlooking a retinal dystrophy as the cause of the nystagmus","Confusing it with congenital nystagmus syndrome","Attributing the head nodding to a primary movement disorder"],"management":["Mandatory neuroimaging with MRI of the brain and anterior visual pathways to exclude a chiasmal or suprasellar tumour before accepting a benign diagnosis","Electroretinography where a retinal dystrophy is suspected as a mimic","Once structural and retinal causes are excluded, manage conservatively with reassurance as the condition is self-limiting","Regular ophthalmological and orthoptic follow-up to monitor the nystagmus, head posture and visual development","Developmental and neurological review to confirm normal progress","Refer promptly for treatment of any underlying lesion if imaging identifies a structural or retinal cause"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Taylor and Hoyt's Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus"],"crossLinks":[]},"spasticity_management":{"name":"Spasticity management","system":"Rehabilitation / Pain","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["spasticity","hypertonia","upper motor neuron syndrome spasticity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braddom's Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation / pain medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Spasticity is a velocity-dependent increase in muscle tone with exaggerated tendon reflexes, arising as part of the upper motor neuron syndrome after brain or spinal cord injury","It should be assessed for its functional impact using scales such as the Modified Ashworth Scale, since not all spasticity is harmful and some may aid standing or transfers","A key first step is to identify and remove noxious triggers such as infection, pressure injury, constipation, urinary retention, ingrown nails, or poor positioning that can suddenly worsen tone","Management follows a stepped approach beginning with therapy and physical measures: stretching, positioning, splinting, serial casting, and an appropriate seating and standing programme","Focal spasticity affecting a few muscles is well suited to intramuscular botulinum toxin injection to reduce tone in targeted muscles and enable therapy","Generalised spasticity may require oral agents such as baclofen, tizanidine, dantrolene, or benzodiazepines, weighing benefit against sedation and weakness","Severe, diffuse spasticity refractory to oral treatment may be managed with intrathecal baclofen delivered by an implanted pump","Treatment goals should be explicit and functional, whether to improve mobility, ease care and hygiene, relieve pain, or prevent contracture, and outcomes reassessed regularly","Untreated spasticity can lead to fixed contractures, pain, pressure injury, and impaired hygiene, so proactive management is important"],"pathophysiology":"Spasticity results from loss of descending inhibitory control over spinal reflex circuits following upper motor neuron lesions. Reduced supraspinal inhibition leads to hyperexcitability of the stretch reflex arc, with increased alpha motor neuron excitability, altered interneuronal modulation, and diminished presynaptic and reciprocal inhibition. Over time, secondary changes within muscle and connective tissue, including altered viscoelastic properties and contracture, add a non-neural component to the increased resistance felt on passive movement.","additionalDifferentials":["Rigidity of parkinsonian or extrapyramidal origin","Dystonia","Fixed contracture without active hypertonia","Paratonia (gegenhalten)","Rigidity from neuroleptic malignant syndrome or serotonin syndrome","Local joint pathology limiting movement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured tone assessment using the Modified Ashworth or Tardieu scale","Evaluation for noxious triggers including urinalysis, review of bowel and bladder function, and skin inspection","Goal-setting assessment and gait or functional analysis to guide treatment targets","Dynamic assessment with diagnostic nerve or motor point blocks to distinguish dynamic spasticity from fixed contracture","Imaging or electrophysiology where an evolving or new central lesion is suspected"],"redFlags":["A sudden, unexplained increase in tone that may signal infection, pressure injury, fracture, or another noxious stimulus","New or rapidly progressive spasticity suggesting an evolving spinal cord or intracranial lesion such as syringomyelia","Signs of intrathecal baclofen withdrawal, which can be life-threatening with high fever, rebound spasticity, and rhabdomyolysis","Developing pressure injury or contracture threatening skin integrity and function"],"prognosis":"With individualised, goal-directed treatment most patients achieve meaningful reductions in tone, pain, and care burden, though spasticity in progressive neurological disease may continue to evolve. Early and consistent management reduces the risk of irreversible contracture and secondary complications.","pitfalls":["Treating spasticity aggressively when it is actually providing useful functional support for standing or transfers","Failing to look for and remove a noxious trigger before escalating drug therapy","Over-sedating patients with oral agents, causing weakness and functional decline","Missing intrathecal baclofen pump malfunction or abrupt withdrawal","Neglecting stretching, positioning, and therapy while relying solely on medication"],"management":["Identify and remove noxious triggers such as infection, urinary retention, constipation, pressure injury, ingrown nails or poor positioning that aggravate tone","First-line physical measures: stretching, positioning, splinting, serial casting and a structured seating and standing programme","Intramuscular botulinum toxin for focal spasticity to reduce tone in targeted muscles and enable therapy","Oral antispasticity agents such as baclofen, tizanidine, dantrolene or benzodiazepines for generalised spasticity, balancing benefit against sedation and weakness","Intrathecal baclofen for severe generalised spasticity refractory to oral therapy","Set functional, goal-directed targets and assess tone with validated scales, treating only spasticity that impairs function","Multidisciplinary rehabilitation input, with orthopaedic or neurosurgical referral (for example selective dorsal rhizotomy) for fixed deformity or refractory disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braddom's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation"],"crossLinks":[]},"specific_granule_deficiency":{"name":"specific granule deficiency","system":"Immune system / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neutrophil-specific granule deficiency","SGD","CEBPE deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies / Nathan and Oski's Hematology of Infancy and Childhood","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare congenital neutrophil disorder in which neutrophils (and often eosinophils) fail to produce secondary (specific) and tertiary granules together with their antimicrobial contents","Classically caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in CEBPE, encoding the myeloid transcription factor CCAAT/enhancer-binding protein epsilon; variants in SMARCD2 produce a closely related phenotype","The blood film characteristically shows neutrophils with bilobed or hypolobated nuclei resembling the Pelger-Huet anomaly and strikingly agranular cytoplasm","Patients suffer recurrent, often severe pyogenic infections of skin, ears, lungs and lymph nodes, commonly with Staphylococcus aureus, Pseudomonas and enteric organisms, and may develop deep abscesses","Functional defects include impaired chemotaxis, disordered receptor up-regulation and reduced bactericidal activity, compounded by absence of defensins, lactoferrin and other granule proteins","Demonstrating markedly reduced or absent specific-granule proteins such as lactoferrin, alongside the typical morphology, supports the diagnosis pending genetic confirmation","Management centres on aggressive treatment and prophylaxis of infection, with haematopoietic stem cell transplantation considered in severe cases"],"pathophysiology":"CEBPE encodes a transcription factor essential for terminal neutrophil maturation and for driving expression of secondary and tertiary granule proteins. Biallelic loss-of-function abolishes production of specific granules (containing lactoferrin, transcobalamin and collagenase) and gelatinase granules together with their antimicrobial peptides, notably defensins, while also disturbing nuclear segmentation to give the bilobed morphology. The resulting neutrophils show defective chemotaxis, impaired mobilisation of surface receptors and reduced bactericidal capacity, predisposing to recurrent bacterial infection. SMARCD2, a subunit of the BAF chromatin-remodelling complex, acts in concert with CEBPE during granulopoiesis, so its deficiency yields an overlapping disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic granulomatous disease","Leukocyte adhesion deficiency","Chediak-Higashi syndrome","Myeloperoxidase deficiency","Congenital or cyclic neutropenia","Myelodysplasia with acquired Pelger-Huet anomaly","Glycogen storage disease type Ib with neutrophil dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood film for bilobed, hypogranular neutrophils (pseudo-Pelger-Huet appearance)","Assays of neutrophil granule proteins such as lactoferrin and defensins demonstrating deficiency","CEBPE and SMARCD2 gene sequencing","Neutrophil functional studies including chemotaxis and bactericidal assays","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine to assess granulopoiesis","Flow cytometry or immunofluorescence for specific-granule proteins","Targeted infection work-up with imaging of suspected deep abscesses"],"redFlags":["Recurrent deep-seated abscesses or invasive bacterial infection","Sepsis in a child with the characteristic agranular blood film","Faltering growth with chronic suppurative infection","Progressive pulmonary infection or evolving bronchiectasis"],"prognosis":"With vigilant antimicrobial treatment and prophylaxis many patients survive into adulthood, although recurrent severe infections cause considerable morbidity; haematopoietic stem cell transplantation may be curative in selected severe cases.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the bilobed hypogranular neutrophils for acquired myelodysplasia rather than a congenital defect","Attributing recurrent infection to a commoner immunodeficiency without inspecting granule morphology","Overlooking the diagnosis because neutrophil counts are typically normal","Failing to separate it from myeloperoxidase deficiency or chronic granulomatous disease by functional testing","Delaying genetic confirmation and appropriate family counselling"],"management":["Prompt, aggressive antibiotic treatment of bacterial infections, guided by culture and covering Staphylococcus aureus and Gram-negative organisms","Long-term antibacterial prophylaxis such as co-trimoxazole to reduce recurrent pyogenic infection","Surgical drainage of deep abscesses when they develop","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the potentially curative option in severe disease","Regular surveillance for new infections with a low threshold for imaging and early intervention","Refer to clinical immunology and haematology and confirm the diagnosis with CEBPE and SMARCD2 genetic testing","Genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies","Nathan and Oski's Hematology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":["myeloperoxidase_deficiency"]},"specific_phobia":{"name":"Specific phobia","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["simple phobia","isolated phobia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The hallmark is marked, disproportionate fear or anxiety about a specific object or situation that almost always provokes an immediate response and is actively avoided or endured with intense distress","Common types include animals, the natural environment such as heights or storms, blood-injection-injury, situational triggers such as flying or enclosed spaces, and other stimuli","The blood-injection-injury type is distinctive because it can provoke a vasovagal response with fainting, unlike most phobias where arousal is heightened","Diagnosis generally requires that the fear is persistent, typically lasting six months or more, and causes clinically significant distress or impairment","Exposure-based cognitive behavioural therapy, especially graded in vivo exposure, is the treatment of choice and is often highly effective, sometimes in a single prolonged session","Pharmacotherapy plays only a limited, adjunctive role, and long-term medication is usually unnecessary for isolated phobias","For blood-injection-injury phobia, applied tension techniques help counter the drop in blood pressure and prevent fainting during exposure","Assess how much the phobia genuinely restricts the person's life, since avoidance of rare triggers may need no treatment while others cause major impairment"],"pathophysiology":"Specific phobia is understood through models of classical fear conditioning and its persistence, in which a stimulus becomes associated with danger and avoidance prevents extinction of the learned fear. Heritable predisposition, heightened amygdala-driven threat responses, evolutionary preparedness for certain stimuli, and vicarious or informational learning all contribute, while the blood-injection-injury type involves a biphasic autonomic response predisposing to vasovagal syncope.","additionalDifferentials":["Panic disorder with situational attacks","Agoraphobia","Social anxiety disorder when avoidance centres on scrutiny","Post-traumatic stress disorder with trauma-related avoidance","Obsessive-compulsive disorder with contamination-related avoidance","Separation anxiety disorder in younger patients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Focused clinical interview to characterise the feared stimulus and avoidance","Assessment of functional impairment attributable to the phobia","Screening for comorbid anxiety, mood, and substance use disorders","In blood-injection-injury type, evaluation of any history of syncope"],"redFlags":["Phobic avoidance that prevents essential medical care, such as avoidance of injections or blood tests in a patient needing treatment","Syncope with injury risk in blood-injection-injury phobia","Comorbid depression with emerging suicidal ideation"],"prognosis":"Specific phobias arising in childhood may remit spontaneously, whereas those persisting into adulthood tend to be chronic if untreated. The prognosis with exposure-based therapy is generally excellent, with many patients achieving lasting improvement, though untreated phobias affecting essential activities can cause enduring impairment.","pitfalls":["Avoiding exposure work in favour of reassurance, which maintains the fear","Overlooking a phobia that blocks necessary medical procedures","Relying on as-needed sedatives that undermine exposure learning","Mislabelling situational panic or agoraphobia as an isolated phobia","Failing to teach applied tension in blood-injection-injury phobia"],"management":["Graded in vivo exposure-based cognitive behavioural therapy is the first-line and highly effective treatment","Applied tension technique for the blood-injection-injury type to counter the vasovagal response and prevent fainting","Reserve pharmacotherapy for a limited adjunctive role, as long-term medication is usually unnecessary for isolated phobias","Occasional short-term use of a benzodiazepine or beta-blocker for an unavoidable single exposure such as a necessary flight","Screen for and treat comorbid anxiety, mood and substance use disorders","Provide psychoeducation and relapse-prevention strategies to maintain gains"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"speckled_lentiginous_nevus_syndrome":{"name":"Speckled lentiginous naevus syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Neurocutaneous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["naevus spilus syndrome","speckled lentiginous nevus syndrome","partial unilateral lentiginosis with neurological features"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Neurocutaneous Disorders (current reference texts)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by a speckled lentiginous naevus (naevus spilus) with a background café-au-lait patch studded with darker macules or papules, together with extracutaneous anomalies","It is considered a mosaic condition, with post-zygotic activating HRAS or KRAS variants reported in affected lesions","The syndromic form is associated with segmental neurological disturbance, including dysaesthesia, hyperhidrosis and muscular weakness in the affected segment","When it occurs alongside an organoid naevus sebaceus it forms part of phacomatosis pigmentokeratotica","Melanocytic naevi within the lesion carry a small risk of melanoma, so surveillance of changing lesions is important","Neurological and skeletal anomalies may accompany extensive disease, warranting broader assessment","Management is generic: dermatological surveillance of the naevus, neurological evaluation of segmental symptoms and multidisciplinary support"],"pathophysiology":"Speckled lentiginous naevus syndrome is a mosaic disorder arising from a post-zygotic activating variant in the RAS pathway, with HRAS and KRAS implicated, affecting a clone of neural-crest-derived melanocytes during development. This produces the background tan café-au-lait patch with superimposed darker melanocytic macules and papules characteristic of a naevus spilus, distributed segmentally. When the same or a partner clone involves peripheral nerve, muscle or skeletal tissue, the syndrome's segmental sensory disturbance, hyperhidrosis, weakness and skeletal anomalies develop. The melanocytic component carries a small potential for malignant transformation, accounting for the recognised, though low, melanoma risk within these lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated naevus spilus without systemic features","Neurofibromatosis type 1 with café-au-lait macules","Phacomatosis pigmentokeratotica","Congenital melanocytic naevus","Segmental (partial unilateral) lentiginosis","Becker naevus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin examination and dermoscopy of the naevus and its speckles","Skin biopsy of any atypical or changing focus within the lesion","Neurological assessment of segmental sensory, sudomotor and motor function","MRI where central or peripheral neurological involvement is suspected","Skeletal imaging where asymmetry or deformity is present","Molecular testing of lesional tissue for HRAS/KRAS variants where available"],"redFlags":["Change in colour, size or surface of a melanocytic focus suggesting melanoma","Progressive segmental weakness or sensory loss","New neurological symptoms warranting imaging","Skeletal deformity requiring orthopaedic assessment"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally good for limited disease; the main long-term concern is the small melanoma risk within the melanocytic component, while extensive neurological or skeletal involvement in the syndromic form determines additional morbidity.","pitfalls":["Assuming a naevus spilus is always isolated and not evaluating for segmental neurological features","Confusing the background patch with a café-au-lait macule of neurofibromatosis","Failing to biopsy a changing melanocytic focus within the lesion","Overlooking its overlap with phacomatosis pigmentokeratotica when an organoid naevus is also present"],"management":["Regular dermatological surveillance with dermoscopy for melanoma arising within the melanocytic component","Biopsy or excision of any atypical, changing or suspicious focus within the naevus","Neurological assessment and symptomatic management of segmental dysaesthesia, hyperhidrosis and muscular weakness","MRI and specialist referral where central or peripheral neurological involvement is suspected","Skeletal imaging and orthopaedic input for associated asymmetry or deformity","Coordinate multidisciplinary care between dermatology, neurology and relevant specialists in extensive disease","Counsel that this is a mosaic post-zygotic (HRAS/KRAS) condition, generally not inherited, with molecular testing of lesional tissue where available"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Happle R. Mosaicism in Human Skin"],"crossLinks":[]},"spermatic_cord_lipoma":{"name":"Spermatic cord lipoma","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lipoma of the spermatic cord","cord lipoma","inguinal cord lipoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Spermatic cord lipoma is the commonest benign tumour of the spermatic cord and paratesticular region and presents as a soft, painless inguinoscrotal swelling","Many so-called cord lipomas are extensions of retroperitoneal or preperitoneal fat drawn down alongside the cord rather than true encapsulated tumours","They are frequently discovered incidentally during inguinal hernia repair, where the fatty mass may be mistaken for or coexist with a hernia sac","On ultrasound the lesion shows fat-density echotexture and lies separate from a normal testis, helping distinguish it from an intratesticular tumour","The main differential of concern is a well-differentiated liposarcoma, which can appear similar but is larger, more heterogeneous, and may show thickened septa or non-fatty components","Symptomatic or enlarging lesions are treated by surgical excision, with histology to confirm a benign lipoma and exclude a liposarcoma","A large paratesticular fatty mass with atypical imaging features should raise suspicion of liposarcoma and prompt appropriate oncological assessment"],"pathophysiology":"A spermatic cord lipoma is a benign proliferation of mature adipose tissue within the cord, or an extension of preperitoneal or retroperitoneal fat that herniates down the inguinal canal alongside the cord structures. It grows slowly as a soft, well-defined fatty mass and, being extratesticular, does not involve the testicular parenchyma. Although benign, it must be distinguished histologically from a well-differentiated liposarcoma of the cord, which shares a fatty composition but carries malignant potential and a risk of local recurrence.","additionalDifferentials":["Inguinal hernia containing omentum or bowel","Well-differentiated liposarcoma of the cord","Paratesticular sarcoma of another type","Encysted hydrocele of the cord","Epididymal or paratesticular cyst","Adenomatoid tumour of the paratesticular region"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal and inguinal ultrasound demonstrating a fat-density extratesticular mass with a normal testis","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) for larger lesions to characterise fat content and exclude liposarcoma","Clinical assessment for a coexisting inguinal hernia","Surgical excision with histological examination","Serum tumour markers when an intratesticular tumour cannot be excluded clinically"],"redFlags":["A large or rapidly enlarging fatty mass suggesting liposarcoma","Heterogeneous imaging with thickened septa or non-fatty components","Local recurrence after previous excision","Associated symptoms such as pain or fixation to surrounding tissues"],"prognosis":"A true spermatic cord lipoma is benign and cured by excision with an excellent prognosis; the principal caveat is ensuring that a well-differentiated liposarcoma has not been overlooked, as the latter can recur locally and requires wider management.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a well-differentiated liposarcoma for a simple lipoma","Assuming an inguinoscrotal fatty swelling is only a hernia and missing a cord lipoma or sarcoma","Failing to send excised tissue for histology","Confusing an extratesticular fatty mass with an intratesticular tumour"],"management":["Surgical excision for symptomatic, enlarging or diagnostically uncertain lesions, with histology to confirm a benign lipoma","Excision or reduction of the fatty mass at the time of inguinal hernia repair when found incidentally","Histological exclusion of a well-differentiated liposarcoma, the main differential of concern","Cross-sectional imaging with CT or MRI for larger or heterogeneous lesions to assess fat content before surgery","Conservative observation with clinical follow-up for small, asymptomatic, clearly benign lesions","Refer to urology, and to a sarcoma service if liposarcoma is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","WHO Classification of Tumours: Urinary and Male Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"spermatocele":{"name":"Spermatocele","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["spermatic cyst","epididymal retention cyst","spermatocoele"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign cystic swelling arising from the head (caput) of the epididymis and containing milky fluid in which spermatozoa can be identified","Typically a painless, smooth, fluctuant lump felt above and behind (posterosuperior to) the testis and clearly separate from it","Transilluminates on examination, helping to distinguish it from a solid mass","Scrotal ultrasound is diagnostic, showing a well-defined anechoic cyst at the epididymal head, often with fine low-level internal echoes from sperm","Distinguished from a simple epididymal cyst mainly by its content (a spermatocele contains sperm) rather than by clinical behaviour, and the two overlap and are often used interchangeably","Most are small and asymptomatic and need only reassurance; large or symptomatic lesions may be treated by surgical excision (spermatocelectomy)","Aspiration and sclerotherapy are alternatives, but recurrence is common and any intervention risks epididymal obstruction and impaired fertility"],"pathophysiology":"A spermatocele forms when an efferent ductule or tubule at the head of the epididymis becomes obstructed and dilates, retaining spermatozoa and epididymal fluid. The resulting cyst is lined by epithelium and filled with a milky fluid in which sperm are present. Because it arises from the sperm-conducting tubules it lies characteristically at the epididymal head, above and behind the testis, and remains separate from the testicular parenchyma. Growth is usually slow, and the lesion is entirely benign with no malignant potential.","additionalDifferentials":["Epididymal cyst","Hydrocele","Varicocele","Epididymal head tumour (rare)","Testicular tumour","Chronic epididymitis","Inguinoscrotal hernia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound with Doppler to confirm a cystic lesion separate from the testis","Transillumination at the bedside","Testicular tumour markers (AFP, beta-hCG, LDH) if any solid component is seen","Semen analysis where fertility is a concern before intervention","Clinical follow-up examination for stable, asymptomatic lesions"],"redFlags":["A solid or vascular component on ultrasound","Rapid enlargement of the swelling","An associated intratesticular mass","A hard, irregular, or fixed swelling"],"prognosis":"Excellent, as spermatoceles are benign and frequently stable; even after excision the outlook is good, though surgery carries a small risk to epididymal patency and thus fertility.","pitfalls":["Operating on small asymptomatic cysts and risking epididymal obstruction and reduced fertility","Assuming all cystic scrotal lesions are benign without ultrasound to exclude a solid mass","Confusing a spermatocele with a hydrocele, which surrounds the testis anteriorly, or with a varicocele","Failing to counsel younger men about fertility implications before spermatocelectomy"],"management":["Reassurance and conservative observation for small, asymptomatic lesions, as they are benign with no malignant potential","Scrotal ultrasound to confirm a cystic lesion separate from the testis and exclude a solid mass","Surgical excision (spermatocelectomy) reserved for large, symptomatic or bothersome lesions","Counsel on the small risk to epididymal patency and fertility from surgery before intervention","Semen analysis where fertility is a concern prior to any operative treatment","Clinical follow-up of stable lesions, with review if the swelling changes or becomes painful"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Smith & Tanagho's General Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"sphincter_of_oddi_dysfunction":{"name":"Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SOD","biliary dyskinesia (sphincter type)","papillary stenosis","sphincter of Oddi dyskinesia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Suspected in patients with recurrent biliary-type or pancreatic-type pain, often after cholecystectomy, without an identifiable structural cause","Results from disordered motility or stenosis of the sphincter of Oddi, impairing biliary and pancreatic outflow","The modern classification distinguishes patients with objective abnormalities (raised liver or pancreatic enzymes, duct dilatation) from those with pain alone","Sphincter of Oddi manometry can demonstrate elevated basal sphincter pressure but is invasive and carries a significant risk of post-procedure pancreatitis","Randomised evidence has tempered enthusiasm for endoscopic sphincterotomy in pain-only presentations, where benefit is uncertain","Careful patient selection is essential because intervention carries real procedural risk with limited benefit in some subgroups","It should remain a diagnosis of exclusion after structural, biliary, and pancreatic pathology have been ruled out"],"pathophysiology":"Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction encompasses abnormal contractility or fixed narrowing of the muscular sphincter that governs flow of bile and pancreatic juice into the duodenum. Elevated sphincter tone, paradoxical contractions, or true papillary stenosis raise ductal pressure, producing biliary or pancreatic-type pain and, in some patients, transient enzyme elevation, duct dilatation, or recurrent pancreatitis. The disorder overlaps with functional gut-brain disorders and visceral hypersensitivity, which contributes to the ongoing controversy over how reliably manometry identifies patients who benefit from sphincter ablation.","additionalDifferentials":["Retained or recurrent bile duct stones","Chronic pancreatitis","Functional biliary or pancreatic pain without sphincter pathology","Peptic ulcer disease","Ampullary or pancreaticobiliary tumour","Post-cholecystectomy pain of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Liver function tests and pancreatic enzymes captured during or shortly after pain episodes","Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography to exclude stones and structural disease","Endoscopic ultrasound to assess the distal duct and ampulla","Sphincter of Oddi manometry in carefully selected patients despite its risk","Cross-sectional imaging to evaluate the pancreas and biliary tree"],"redFlags":["Jaundice, cholangitis, or markedly abnormal liver tests suggesting obstruction or stones","Recurrent acute pancreatitis requiring structural evaluation","Weight loss or a mass raising concern for ampullary or pancreatic malignancy","Development of post-procedure pancreatitis after intervention"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the subtype: patients with objective obstructive findings may benefit from sphincterotomy, whereas those with pain alone often gain little and remain at risk of procedural complications, so expectations must be set carefully.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to sphincterotomy in pain-only patients despite weak evidence of benefit","Underestimating the substantial risk of post-ERCP pancreatitis from manometry and intervention","Failing to exclude retained stones and structural disease first","Overlooking a functional gut-brain disorder or visceral hypersensitivity as the true driver of pain"],"management":["Careful subtype classification, distinguishing patients with objective obstructive findings from those with pain alone before any intervention","Endoscopic biliary sphincterotomy for patients with objective enzyme or duct abnormalities most likely to benefit","Avoid sphincterotomy in pain-only presentations, where randomised evidence shows little benefit against real procedural risk","Trial of medical therapy such as antispasmodics (calcium channel blockers, nitrates) and neuromodulators such as tricyclic antidepressants for pain","Sphincter of Oddi manometry only in carefully selected patients, accepting the risk of post-procedure pancreatitis","Post-ERCP pancreatitis prophylaxis with rectal NSAIDs and pancreatic duct stenting when intervention is undertaken","Manage as a functional gut-brain disorder with realistic expectation-setting and multidisciplinary support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Rome Foundation criteria for functional gallbladder and sphincter of Oddi disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"spina_bifida":{"name":"Spina bifida","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["myelomeningocele","meningocele","spina bifida occulta","neural tube defect"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Spina bifida is a congenital neural tube defect ranging from the hidden spina bifida occulta to the severe open myelomeningocele","Myelomeningocele exposes neural tissue and typically causes lower limb weakness, sensory loss, and bladder and bowel dysfunction whose level reflects the anatomical lesion","It is frequently associated with a Chiari II malformation and hydrocephalus, which often needs cerebrospinal fluid diversion","Spina bifida occulta is usually asymptomatic and found incidentally, but cutaneous markers such as a tuft of hair, dimple, or lipoma over the spine can signal an underlying tethered cord","Periconceptional folic acid supplementation substantially reduces the risk of neural tube defects and is a key public health measure","Antenatal diagnosis is supported by maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein and detailed ultrasound, allowing planning of delivery and early surgery","Early surgical closure of an open defect protects exposed neural tissue and reduces infection risk, and fetal repair is offered in selected cases","Lifelong multidisciplinary care addresses mobility, urological, orthopaedic, and neurosurgical needs including shunt surveillance and tethered cord"],"pathophysiology":"Spina bifida results from failure of the neural tube to close completely during early embryonic development, leaving a midline defect in the vertebral arches and, in open forms, in the overlying tissues. In myelomeningocele the meninges and dysplastic spinal cord protrude through the defect, so neural function below the lesion is impaired. Disordered cerebrospinal fluid dynamics and the commonly associated Chiari II malformation predispose to hydrocephalus. In occult forms the cord may be abnormally low and tethered by a thickened filum or lipoma, and growth-related traction can produce progressive neurological, urological, or orthopaedic deterioration.","additionalDifferentials":["Sacrococcygeal teratoma or other congenital masses","Tethered cord syndrome from other causes","Spinal dysraphism variants (diastematomyelia, dermal sinus)","Caudal regression syndrome","Simple sacral dimple without dysraphism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antenatal ultrasound and maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein for prenatal detection","MRI of the spine and brain to define the lesion, cord tethering, and associated Chiari II malformation","Cranial imaging and head circumference monitoring to detect hydrocephalus","Urodynamic and renal tract assessment for neurogenic bladder","Orthopaedic evaluation of the spine, hips, and lower limbs"],"redFlags":["Open defect with exposed neural tissue at risk of infection needing urgent protection and closure","Signs of associated hydrocephalus or Chiari II brainstem dysfunction in an infant","New or progressive neurological, urological, or orthopaedic decline suggesting tethered cord or shunt failure","Fever or CSF leak indicating possible meningitis or ventriculitis"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies widely with the level and severity of the lesion; occulta is usually benign, whereas myelomeningocele typically causes lasting motor, sensory, and sphincter deficits, though multidisciplinary care and timely surgery improve function and reduce complications.","pitfalls":["Dismissing cutaneous spinal markers that may indicate occult dysraphism and a tethered cord","Failing to monitor for evolving hydrocephalus or shunt malfunction","Overlooking neurogenic bladder and the associated risk to the upper urinary tract","Attributing new deficits in a growing child to the original lesion rather than a tethered cord"],"management":["Early surgical closure of the myelomeningocele defect, or antenatal foetal repair in selected cases, to protect exposed neural tissue and reduce infection","Cerebrospinal fluid diversion by ventriculoperitoneal shunt or endoscopic third ventriculostomy for associated hydrocephalus","Neurogenic bladder management with intermittent catheterisation, anticholinergics and regular renal-tract surveillance to protect the kidneys","Structured bowel management programme for neurogenic bowel dysfunction","Orthopaedic and physiotherapy care for lower-limb weakness, spinal deformity and hip problems, with appropriate mobility aids and pressure-area skin care","Untethering surgery for a symptomatic tethered cord causing progressive neurological, urological or orthopaedic decline","Multidisciplinary follow-up, with periconceptional folic acid supplementation to prevent recurrence and genetic counselling for the family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinal_anaesthesia_complications":{"name":"Spinal anaesthesia complications","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subarachnoid block complications","intrathecal anaesthesia complications","high spinal","total spinal"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hypotension and bradycardia are the most common early effects, caused by sympathetic blockade, and are managed with fluids, vasopressors, and positioning","Excessively high block can spread to thoracic and cervical levels, producing profound hypotension, difficulty breathing, and eventually a total spinal with apnoea and cardiovascular collapse requiring airway support and resuscitation","Postdural puncture headache is a frequent delayed complication, typically postural and related to needle size and design","Urinary retention is common and reflects blockade of sacral autonomic fibres, requiring monitoring of bladder volume","Serious neurological complications are rare but include epidural or spinal haematoma, abscess, arachnoiditis, cauda equina syndrome, and transient neurological symptoms","Neuraxial haematoma risk is increased by abnormal coagulation or anticoagulant therapy, and adherence to timing guidelines around anticoagulation is critical","New or progressive back pain, leg weakness, sensory change, or bladder or bowel dysfunction after neuraxial block demands urgent imaging and neurosurgical assessment","Meticulous asepsis reduces the risk of infective complications, and this reference entry addresses the non-infective mechanical and physiological complications"],"pathophysiology":"Injection of local anaesthetic into the subarachnoid space blocks nerve roots and the spinal cord, producing sensory, motor, and autonomic blockade. Sympathetic blockade causes vasodilation and, with higher blocks, loss of cardiac accelerator fibre activity, leading to hypotension and bradycardia. Excessive cephalad spread can impair respiratory muscles and brainstem perfusion. Dural puncture allows cerebrospinal fluid leakage, lowering intracranial pressure and producing a postural headache. Mechanical trauma, mass lesions such as haematoma or abscess, or neurotoxicity can compress or injure neural tissue, causing ischaemia and potentially permanent deficit if not decompressed promptly.","additionalDifferentials":["Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity from inadvertent vascular uptake","Vasovagal syncope","Haemorrhagic or septic shock unrelated to the block","Pre-existing neurological disease","Anaphylaxis to an injected agent","Positioning-related peripheral nerve injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous blood pressure, heart rate, and oxygenation monitoring","Assessment of block height and progression","Urgent MRI of the spine if a compressive lesion (haematoma or abscess) is suspected","Coagulation studies when neuraxial bleeding is a concern","Bladder scan to detect urinary retention"],"redFlags":["Rapidly ascending block with respiratory distress, hypotension, or loss of consciousness indicating a high or total spinal","Cardiovascular collapse or severe bradycardia requiring resuscitation","New progressive motor weakness, sensory loss, or bladder/bowel dysfunction suggesting cord or cauda equina compression","Severe or worsening back pain with neurological signs after neuraxial block"],"prognosis":"Common physiological effects such as hypotension resolve with treatment and most postdural puncture headaches settle, but a missed compressive haematoma or abscess can cause permanent paralysis, so outcome depends on rapid recognition and decompression.","pitfalls":["Failing to anticipate and promptly treat sympathetic-blockade hypotension and bradycardia","Not recognising an ascending high spinal early enough to support ventilation","Overlooking neuraxial haematoma risk in anticoagulated patients","Attributing new neurological deficits to residual block and delaying urgent imaging","Missing urinary retention in the postoperative period"],"management":["Treat sympathetic-blockade hypotension and bradycardia with intravenous fluid loading, vasopressors (phenylephrine or ephedrine) and positioning, adding an anticholinergic such as atropine or glycopyrronium for bradycardia","Manage a high or total spinal by securing the airway with assisted ventilation or intubation, supporting the circulation with vasopressors and fluids, and delivering full cardiopulmonary resuscitation if collapse occurs","Treat postdural puncture headache conservatively with recumbency, hydration, simple analgesia and caffeine, escalating to an epidural blood patch for severe or refractory cases","Detect and relieve urinary retention from sacral autonomic blockade using bladder scanning and intermittent catheterisation","Investigate any unexpected or progressive neurological deficit with urgent whole-spine MRI and coagulation studies, arranging emergency neurosurgical decompression for an epidural haematoma or abscess","Prevent neuraxial haematoma by adhering to anticoagulant timing guidelines around the block and checking coagulation before neuraxial procedures","Continuously monitor blood pressure, heart rate, oxygenation and ascending block height, escalating to critical care for cardiovascular or respiratory compromise"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinal_bulbar_muscular_atrophy_kennedy":{"name":"Spinal and bulbar muscular atrophy (Kennedy disease)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SBMA","Kennedy disease","Kennedy's disease","X-linked bulbospinal neuronopathy","AR CAG repeat disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neuromuscular Disorders reference texts / OMIM","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked recessive lower motor neuron disorder affecting adult men, caused by an expanded CAG trinucleotide repeat in the androgen receptor gene (AR)","Presents in mid-adult life with slowly progressive proximal limb weakness, cramps, and prominent bulbar involvement including dysarthria, dysphagia and tongue atrophy","Perioral and chin fasciculations and a characteristic postural or action tremor are useful clinical clues","Partial androgen insensitivity produces gynaecomastia, testicular atrophy and reduced fertility","The disorder is a polyglutamine disease; longer CAG expansions tend to correlate with earlier onset","Sensory involvement is frequently detectable on nerve conduction studies even when clinically silent, reflecting a sensory neuronopathy component","It must be distinguished from amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, which it can mimic but from which it differs by its slower course, absence of upper motor neuron signs, and androgen features"],"pathophysiology":"Spinal and bulbar muscular atrophy is caused by an expanded CAG trinucleotide repeat encoding a polyglutamine tract in the androgen receptor. The expanded, ligand-activated receptor misfolds and accumulates, exerting a toxic gain of function that preferentially damages lower motor neurons in the brainstem and spinal cord, together with dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons. Because the toxicity is androgen-dependent, disease is largely confined to men, while the same receptor dysfunction produces partial androgen insensitivity with gynaecomastia and reduced fertility. Progressive motor neuron degeneration leads to bulbar and proximal limb weakness and muscle atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis / motor neuron disease","Adult-onset (type 4) spinal muscular atrophy (SMN1 related)","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy","Myasthenia gravis with bulbar features","Inclusion body myositis","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for the androgen receptor CAG repeat expansion, which is diagnostic","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies showing chronic neurogenic changes with reduced sensory action potentials","Serum creatine kinase, often moderately elevated","Endocrine assessment including testosterone, gonadotrophins and lipids/glucose given metabolic associations","Swallowing assessment to quantify aspiration risk","Family history and genetic counselling regarding X-linked inheritance"],"redFlags":["Significant dysphagia with aspiration and weight loss","Recurrent aspiration pneumonia","Choking episodes or nocturnal respiratory symptoms","Rapid deterioration suggesting an alternative motor neuron disease"],"prognosis":"The condition progresses slowly over decades and life expectancy may be near normal or only modestly reduced, with morbidity and mortality driven mainly by bulbar dysfunction, aspiration and falls. Most affected men remain ambulant for many years before requiring aids.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the condition as amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and giving an inappropriately grave prognosis","Overlooking the androgen features (gynaecomastia, subfertility) that point to the diagnosis","Failing to recognise the subclinical sensory neuronopathy on nerve conduction studies","Not counselling about X-linked inheritance and carrier status in female relatives"],"management":["Provide supportive, symptom-directed care coordinated through a specialist neuromuscular clinic, as no disease-modifying therapy is established for the androgen-receptor CAG expansion","Maintain mobility and prevent contractures with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and appropriate aids, and treat dysarthria with speech and language therapy","Manage bulbar dysfunction with regular swallow assessment, diet-texture modification and consideration of gastrostomy feeding when aspiration risk is high","Monitor and treat the metabolic and endocrine complications of partial androgen insensitivity, including gynaecomastia, dyslipidaemia and impaired glucose tolerance","Surveil respiratory function and aspiration risk, treating chest infections promptly and instituting airway-clearance measures","Reduce falls through home assessment, mobility aids and provision of psychological support","Offer genetic counselling for X-linked recessive inheritance, with carrier testing for female relatives and reproductive counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","OMIM: Spinal and bulbar muscular atrophy (AR)"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinal_cord_injury_rehab":{"name":"Spinal cord injury rehabilitation","system":"Rehabilitation / Pain","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCI","spinal cord injury rehab","tetraplegia","paraplegia","quadriplegia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braddom's Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation / pain medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The neurological level and completeness of injury are defined using the International Standards for Neurological Classification of Spinal Cord Injury (ISNCSCI) and the ASIA Impairment Scale, which guide prognosis and goals","Distinguishing complete from incomplete injury and recognising patterns such as central cord, Brown-Sequard, and anterior cord syndromes informs expected functional outcomes","Comprehensive rehabilitation is organ-system based, addressing respiratory function, neurogenic bladder and bowel, skin integrity, spasticity, pain, sexuality, and psychological adjustment","Neurogenic bladder management aims to protect the upper urinary tract, and intermittent catheterisation is generally preferred over indwelling catheters to reduce complications","Meticulous pressure injury prevention through regular repositioning, pressure-relief cushions, and skin surveillance is critical because insensate skin is highly vulnerable","Venous thromboembolism risk is high after injury and warrants prophylaxis, and respiratory care is a priority in higher cervical injuries that weaken the diaphragm and cough","Autonomic dysreflexia is a medical emergency in injuries at or above the T6 level, triggered by a noxious stimulus below the injury such as a blocked catheter or bowel impaction, and presents with a surging blood pressure, pounding headache, and sweating above the lesion; treatment is to sit the patient up, remove the trigger, and give antihypertensives as needed","Function is maximised through goal-directed therapy, adaptive equipment, appropriate wheelchair prescription, and training for the patient and carers, with attention to long-term community reintegration"],"pathophysiology":"Spinal cord injury involves a primary mechanical insult to the cord followed by a secondary injury cascade of ischaemia, oedema, excitotoxicity, inflammation, and free-radical damage that extends the initial neurological loss. Interruption of ascending and descending tracts produces motor, sensory, and autonomic dysfunction below the lesion. After the initial period of spinal shock with flaccid areflexia resolves, reflex activity returns below the level of injury, giving rise to spasticity, neurogenic bladder and bowel, and, in higher lesions, disordered autonomic control including autonomic dysreflexia and orthostatic hypotension.","additionalDifferentials":["Guillain-Barre syndrome or other acute polyneuropathy","Transverse myelitis or other inflammatory myelopathy","Spinal cord compression from tumour, abscess, or haematoma","Cauda equina syndrome","Vascular myelopathy or spinal cord infarction","Functional neurological disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial ISNCSCI examination with ASIA Impairment Scale grading to define level and completeness","Spinal imaging with CT and MRI to characterise the injury and cord signal change","Urodynamic studies and renal tract imaging to guide neurogenic bladder management","Respiratory function assessment in cervical and high thoracic injuries","Screening for complications including venous thromboembolism, pressure injury, and heterotopic ossification"],"redFlags":["Autonomic dysreflexia in injuries at or above T6, a hypertensive emergency requiring immediate identification and removal of the trigger","Ascending neurological level or new deficit suggesting cord compression, expanding haematoma, or syrinx","Respiratory fatigue or failure in high cervical injury","Signs of sepsis from urinary infection or pressure injury","Symptoms of pulmonary embolism in an immobilised patient"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends chiefly on the neurological level and completeness of injury; incomplete injuries and preserved sacral function carry a better outlook for recovery. Most neurological recovery occurs within the first months, and long-term outcomes are strongly influenced by prevention of complications such as pressure injury, urinary tract deterioration, and respiratory disease.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise and treat autonomic dysreflexia promptly in at-risk patients","Relying on indwelling catheters and neglecting upper urinary tract protection","Inadequate pressure relief leading to preventable pressure injuries in insensate skin","Overlooking respiratory decline in high cervical injuries until failure occurs","Underestimating the psychological impact and the need for coordinated long-term support"],"management":["Deliver organ-system-based rehabilitation in a specialist spinal injury unit, setting functional goals guided by the ISNCSCI neurological level and ASIA Impairment Scale grade","Protect the upper urinary tract with a neurogenic bladder programme favouring intermittent catheterisation over indwelling catheters, and manage neurogenic bowel with a scheduled bowel-care regimen","Prevent pressure injury through regular repositioning, pressure-relief cushions and vigilant surveillance of insensate skin","Manage spasticity with stretching, physiotherapy, oral antispasmodics such as baclofen, and intrathecal baclofen or botulinum toxin for refractory cases, and treat neuropathic pain with gabapentinoids","Provide venous thromboembolism prophylaxis and prioritise respiratory care with assisted coughing and ventilatory support in high cervical injuries","Recognise and urgently treat autonomic dysreflexia by sitting the patient upright, removing the noxious trigger such as bladder or bowel distension, and giving rapid-acting antihypertensives","Address sexuality, fertility, psychological adjustment and vocational reintegration through the multidisciplinary team","Surveil for heterotopic ossification, orthostatic hypotension and recurrent pressure injury over long-term follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braddom's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinal_dural_arteriovenous_fistula":{"name":"Spinal dural arteriovenous fistula","system":"Nervous system / Spinal cord (vascular)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SDAVF","type I spinal arteriovenous malformation","dural AV fistula of the spine"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is the commonest spinal vascular malformation and characteristically affects middle-aged and older men, most often at thoracolumbar levels","The lesion is an acquired arteriovenous shunt within the dura of a nerve root sleeve, where a radiculomeningeal artery drains directly into a radicular vein and arterialises the coronal venous plexus","The resulting venous hypertension impairs cord drainage and produces a progressive congestive myelopathy rather than haemorrhage","Patients present with insidious, often stepwise, ascending paraparesis, sensory disturbance and sphincter dysfunction that can be mistaken for a degenerative or inflammatory process","Symptoms characteristically worsen with exertion, prolonged standing, heat or the Valsalva manoeuvre, reflecting the underlying venous congestion","MRI typically shows central cord T2 hyperintensity with cord swelling and serpentine flow voids on the dorsal cord surface, and often patchy enhancement","Catheter spinal digital subtraction angiography remains the gold standard to localise the fistulous point before treatment","The advanced, thrombotic and often irreversible form is known as the Foix-Alajouanine syndrome"],"pathophysiology":"A spinal dural arteriovenous fistula is an acquired direct shunt situated in the dura near a spinal nerve root, where a dural branch of a radiculomeningeal artery connects to a radicular (medullary) vein without an intervening capillary bed. Arterial pressure is transmitted into the normally low-pressure, valveless coronal venous plexus that drains the cord, raising spinal venous pressure and reducing the arteriovenous pressure gradient. The consequent failure of venous drainage causes chronic venous congestion, interstitial oedema and cord hypoxia, producing a progressive myelopathy; unlike intramedullary arteriovenous malformations, the low-flow dural shunt rarely bleeds, and superimposed venous thrombosis can precipitate abrupt, often irreversible deterioration.","additionalDifferentials":["Transverse myelitis and other inflammatory myelopathies","Degenerative compressive myelopathy","Intramedullary spinal cord tumour such as ependymoma or astrocytoma","Intramedullary arteriovenous malformation or perimedullary fistula","Subacute combined degeneration of the cord","Motor neurone disease when weakness predominates"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the whole spine with contrast to show cord oedema, dorsal serpentine flow voids and enhancement","MR angiography of the spine to help localise the level of the shunt","Catheter digital subtraction spinal angiography as the definitive study to identify the feeding artery and fistulous point","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis, which may show a mildly raised protein, to help exclude inflammatory causes","Serum vitamin B12 and metabolic screen to exclude mimics of a progressive myelopathy","Baseline functional and bladder assessment to monitor response to treatment"],"redFlags":["Progressive gait decline with exertional or postural worsening in an older man","Ascending sensory level with developing sphincter involvement","Abrupt deterioration suggesting venous thrombosis or the Foix-Alajouanine syndrome","Neurological worsening after corticosteroid administration"],"prognosis":"Gait and motor function commonly improve after treatment by endovascular embolisation or surgical ligation, provided intervention occurs before fixed deficits develop, whereas sphincter recovery is less reliable. Delayed diagnosis allows irreversible cord injury and can result in permanent paraplegia.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the condition as transverse myelitis and giving corticosteroids, which may precipitate marked deterioration","Overlooking the dorsal serpentine flow voids on MRI that signal the diagnosis","Failing to proceed to spinal angiography when imaging suggests a vascular myelopathy","Attributing progressive myelopathy to degenerative spinal disease and missing a treatable fistula","Not recognising exertional and postural symptom fluctuation as a clue to venous congestion"],"management":["Obliterate the fistula definitively by endovascular embolisation or surgical ligation of the draining radicular vein to relieve the venous hypertension driving the congestive myelopathy","Localise the feeding artery and fistulous point with catheter digital subtraction spinal angiography to plan definitive treatment","Time intervention early, before fixed deficits develop, as motor and gait recovery is more reliable than sphincter recovery","Avoid corticosteroids, which can precipitate acute neurological deterioration in venous congestive myelopathy","Provide gait rehabilitation and physiotherapy and manage neurogenic bladder and bowel dysfunction","Monitor with serial functional and bladder assessment and post-treatment MRI or angiography to confirm fistula closure and detect recurrence","Refer to interventional neuroradiology and spinal neurosurgery for combined decision-making on embolisation versus open ligation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Youmans and Winn Neurological Surgery","Osborn's Diagnostic Imaging: Brain and Spine"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinal_epidural_abscess":{"name":"Spinal epidural abscess","system":"Neurology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SEA","epidural abscess","spinal extradural abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A neurosurgical and infective emergency in which a collection of pus in the epidural space can rapidly compress the spinal cord or cauda equina","The classic triad of back pain, fever, and neurological deficit is often incomplete, so a high index of suspicion is essential","Progression from localised back pain to root pain, then weakness and sphincter dysfunction, then paralysis can occur over hours to days","Staphylococcus aureus is the most frequent organism, often reaching the spine by haematogenous spread or by direct extension","Risk factors include diabetes, injection drug use, immunosuppression, indwelling catheters, recent spinal procedures, and bacteraemia","Urgent whole-spine MRI with contrast is the investigation of choice and may reveal additional non-contiguous collections","Management usually combines prompt surgical drainage with prolonged targeted antimicrobial therapy, though selected cases are managed medically","Neurological outcome is largely determined by the deficit at the time of intervention, so delay is the main preventable cause of permanent paralysis"],"pathophysiology":"Bacteria reach the spinal epidural space by haematogenous seeding (from skin, urinary, respiratory, or endovascular sources), by contiguous spread from vertebral osteomyelitis or discitis, or by direct inoculation during procedures. The abscess damages the cord through two mechanisms: direct mechanical compression and, importantly, vascular compromise causing ischaemic or venous infarction of the cord, which can produce deficits out of proportion to the degree of compression. The thoracic and lumbar regions are most often involved, and the collection may track over several vertebral levels.","additionalDifferentials":["Vertebral osteomyelitis or discitis without abscess","Metastatic epidural spinal cord compression","Transverse myelitis or other inflammatory myelopathy","Herniated disc with cord or root compression","Spinal subdural or intramedullary abscess","Spinal epidural haematoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent contrast-enhanced MRI of the whole spine","Blood cultures before starting antimicrobials whenever feasible","Inflammatory markers (CRP, ESR) and full blood count","Culture of aspirated or surgically obtained pus for organism identification and sensitivities","Echocardiography and a search for a primary source when endocarditis or bacteraemia is suspected","HbA1c and screening for immunosuppression or injection drug use"],"redFlags":["Any new neurological deficit with back pain and fever mandates emergency imaging and specialist referral","Rapidly progressive weakness, sensory level, or new sphincter dysfunction indicating impending or actual cord compression","Sepsis or haemodynamic instability from the underlying infection","Known immunosuppression, injection drug use, or recent spinal instrumentation with new spinal pain"],"prognosis":"Outcome hinges on the speed of diagnosis and decompression; patients treated before significant weakness often recover well, whereas established paralysis or delayed intervention frequently leaves permanent deficits, and severe sepsis carries appreciable mortality.","pitfalls":["Waiting for the complete triad of pain, fever, and deficit before imaging, thereby losing critical time","Imaging only the symptomatic level and missing skip lesions elsewhere in the spine","Starting antibiotics before blood cultures in a stable patient and losing microbiological data","Attributing back pain to a benign musculoskeletal cause in a high-risk patient","Underestimating how quickly a stable neurological picture can deteriorate to irreversible paralysis"],"management":["Start empirical intravenous antimicrobials covering Staphylococcus aureus, including a glycopeptide for meticillin-resistant strains plus Gram-negative cover, after cultures are taken, then narrow to culture and sensitivities","Arrange urgent surgical decompression and drainage for neurological deficit or a significant compressive collection, obtaining pus for culture","Take blood cultures and, where feasible, aspirated or operative samples before antimicrobials to identify the causative organism","Continue a prolonged course of targeted antimicrobial therapy with serial inflammatory markers such as CRP and ESR and repeat imaging to confirm response","Identify and treat the primary source, screening for endocarditis with echocardiography and controlling predisposing conditions such as diabetes and injection drug use","Perform serial neurological observation to detect deterioration and repeat whole-spine MRI for non-contiguous or recurrent collections","Involve spinal surgery or neurosurgery, infectious diseases and rehabilitation, escalating urgently on any progression of weakness or sphincter dysfunction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinal_epidural_lipomatosis":{"name":"Spinal epidural lipomatosis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Spine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SEL","epidural lipomatosis","epidural fat overgrowth","spinal epidural lipomatous hypertrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pathological overgrowth of normal, unencapsulated fat in the epidural space that can compress the thecal sac and nerve roots","Strongly associated with exogenous corticosteroid excess (long-term steroid therapy or Cushing syndrome) and with obesity","Most often affects the thoracic and lumbar spine","Presentations include back pain, radiculopathy, neurogenic claudication, and, in severe thoracic disease, myelopathy","MRI is diagnostic, showing abundant high-T1-signal epidural fat compressing the sac, classically in a stellate or Y-shaped pattern in the lumbar region","Identifying and reversing the underlying cause is central to management","First-line treatment is conservative, with weight reduction and tapering or stopping exogenous corticosteroids where possible","Surgical decompression with debulking of epidural fat is reserved for severe neurological compromise or failure of conservative measures"],"pathophysiology":"Spinal epidural lipomatosis is the abnormal accumulation of histologically normal adipose tissue within the epidural space. It is most commonly driven by states of corticosteroid excess, whether from prolonged exogenous steroid use or endogenous Cushing syndrome, and by obesity, although idiopathic cases are also described. The excess fat encroaches on the limited epidural space and compresses the thecal sac and its neural contents. In the lumbar region the compressed sac takes on a characteristic stellate or Y-shaped configuration, whereas thoracic disease can compress the cord and cause myelopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Lumbar spinal stenosis from disc, facet, and ligamentum changes","Epidural haematoma or abscess","Epidural tumour, metastasis, or lymphoma","Disc herniation with canal compromise","Arachnoiditis","Angiolipoma or other epidural fatty tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the spine as the diagnostic test, showing excess high-T1-signal epidural fat compressing the sac","CT demonstrating low-attenuation epidural fat when MRI is unavailable","Review of corticosteroid exposure and assessment for Cushing syndrome","Metabolic and endocrine work-up, including for hypercortisolism, where clinically indicated","Assessment of body-mass index and obesity-related comorbidities"],"redFlags":["Progressive myelopathy or cauda equina features such as saddle anaesthesia and sphincter dysfunction","Rapidly progressive neurological deficit","Severe canal compromise on imaging with neurological signs"],"prognosis":"Symptoms often stabilise or improve when the underlying cause is addressed, notably with weight loss and reduction of corticosteroids; severe or myelopathic cases may need surgery, after which outcomes depend on the degree and duration of pre-operative compression.","pitfalls":["Failing to review and reduce exogenous corticosteroids as the treatable driver","Overlooking undiagnosed Cushing syndrome","Attributing canal compromise solely to degenerative stenosis and missing the excess epidural fat"],"management":["Identify and reverse the underlying cause by tapering exogenous corticosteroids where possible and treating endogenous Cushing syndrome","Institute structured weight loss and management of obesity-related comorbidities as the cornerstone in obese patients","Provide analgesia and physiotherapy for back pain, radiculopathy and neurogenic claudication","Reserve surgical decompression, such as laminectomy with debulking of epidural fat, for severe, progressive or myelopathic disease","Monitor neurological status and repeat MRI to track the epidural fat burden and response to cause-directed treatment","Refer to endocrinology for hypercortisolism assessment and to spinal surgery for compressive or myelopathic presentations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","Fogel et al: Spinal epidural lipomatosis review, The Spine Journal"],"crossLinks":["lumbar_spinal_stenosis","cervical_spondylotic_myelopathy"]},"spinal_muscular_atrophy":{"name":"Spinal Muscular Atrophy","system":"Genetic-metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SMA","SMN-related spinal muscular atrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder of anterior horn (lower motor neuron) degeneration, most commonly caused by homozygous loss of function of the SMN1 gene.","It produces progressive, symmetrical muscle weakness and wasting that is typically more marked proximally and in the legs more than the arms.","Disease severity is graded into types (from severe infantile-onset to milder later-onset forms) and correlates broadly with the number of copies of the modifying SMN2 gene.","Severe infantile SMA (type 1) presents with profound hypotonia, poor head control, a weak cry, feeding and swallowing difficulty, and paradoxical (belly) breathing with a bell-shaped chest.","Tongue fasciculations and absent deep tendon reflexes are characteristic, while cognition and sensation are preserved.","Respiratory muscle and bulbar weakness drive the major morbidity and mortality, particularly recurrent aspiration and respiratory failure in the most severe forms.","Diagnosis is confirmed by genetic testing showing biallelic SMN1 loss, with SMN2 copy number providing prognostic information.","Disease-modifying therapies that increase SMN protein have substantially changed the outlook, and newborn screening enables early, presymptomatic treatment.","Multidisciplinary supportive care addresses respiratory support, nutrition, orthopaedic complications such as scoliosis and contractures, and physiotherapy."],"pathophysiology":"Loss of functional SMN protein, resulting from homozygous deletion or mutation of SMN1, leads to degeneration and loss of the motor neurons of the anterior horn of the spinal cord and brainstem motor nuclei. The nearly identical SMN2 gene produces only a small amount of full-length functional protein, so a higher SMN2 copy number partially compensates and mitigates severity. Denervation of skeletal muscle causes progressive weakness and atrophy, with the respiratory and bulbar musculature critically affected in severe forms, resulting in hypoventilation, impaired airway clearance, aspiration, and respiratory failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital myopathies","Congenital muscular dystrophies","Neonatal or congenital myasthenic syndromes","Hypoxic-ischaemic or metabolic encephalopathy causing hypotonia","Prader-Willi syndrome and other central causes of hypotonia","Pompe disease and other metabolic myopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for SMN1 deletion/mutation with SMN2 copy number","Newborn screening result where available","Assessment of respiratory function and swallowing safety","Creatine kinase (usually normal or only mildly elevated, helping distinguish from dystrophies)","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies if genetic testing is inconclusive","Nutritional and growth assessment"],"redFlags":["Respiratory failure or hypoventilation from progressive respiratory muscle weakness","Bulbar weakness with unsafe swallowing and recurrent aspiration","Rapidly progressive weakness and loss of motor milestones in an infant","Weak cough and impaired airway clearance during intercurrent respiratory infection","Signs of impending ventilatory decompensation such as paradoxical breathing and rising work of breathing"],"prognosis":"Historically severe infantile-onset disease carried a very poor prognosis with early respiratory failure, while later-onset forms had longer survival with variable disability. Early diagnosis through newborn screening and initiation of SMN-directed disease-modifying therapy have markedly improved motor outcomes and survival, especially when started presymptomatically.","pitfalls":["Attributing infantile hypotonia to a central cause and missing the lower motor neuron pattern","Overlooking tongue fasciculations and preserved cognition as clues to anterior horn disease","Underestimating respiratory and bulbar risk, especially during intercurrent illness","Delaying genetic confirmation and thereby missing the window for early therapy","Failing to organise multidisciplinary respiratory, nutritional, and orthopaedic support"],"management":["Start SMN-directed disease-modifying therapy as early as possible using the antisense oligonucleotide nusinersen, onasemnogene abeparvovec gene-replacement therapy, or the oral SMN2 splicing modifier risdiplam","Act on a newborn-screening or presymptomatic diagnosis to begin therapy before irreversible anterior-horn motor-neuron loss","Provide proactive respiratory care with non-invasive ventilation, airway-clearance and cough-assist techniques, and prompt treatment of chest infections","Optimise nutrition and swallowing safety, using gastrostomy feeding where bulbar weakness threatens aspiration","Manage musculoskeletal complications with physiotherapy, orthoses and monitoring and treatment of scoliosis and contractures","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across neurology, respiratory, orthopaedics, dietetics and rehabilitation, with anticipatory and palliative planning in the most severe infantile form","Monitor motor function with standardised scales to assess response to disease-modifying therapy","Offer genetic counselling with SMN2 copy-number-informed prognosis and carrier and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinal_muscular_atrophy_lower_extremity":{"name":"Spinal muscular atrophy, lower-extremity predominant (DYNC1H1/BICD2)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SMA-LED","lower-extremity predominant spinal muscular atrophy","DYNC1H1","BICD2","autosomal dominant lower-limb SMA"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley and Daroff Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant motor neuronopathy in which weakness and wasting predominate in the lower limbs, particularly the proximal thigh and quadriceps muscles","Caused by variants in DYNC1H1 (the heavy chain of cytoplasmic dynein) or BICD2 (a dynein-adaptor protein), both essential for retrograde axonal transport","Often presents in early childhood with delayed walking, a waddling gait and difficulty rising, and may appear relatively static over long periods","Predominant proximal lower-limb involvement with relative sparing of the upper limbs helps distinguish it from length-dependent neuropathies","Some DYNC1H1 cases have associated cortical malformations and learning difficulty, reflecting the wider role of dynein in the developing brain","Electrophysiology shows a motor neuronopathy pattern with chronic denervation and preserved sensory responses","Diagnosis requires the lower-limb-predominant distribution together with genetic confirmation of a DYNC1H1 or BICD2 variant"],"pathophysiology":"Lower-extremity-predominant spinal muscular atrophy is a dominant motor neurone disorder caused by variants in components of the cytoplasmic dynein transport machinery: DYNC1H1 encodes the dynein heavy chain and BICD2 encodes an adaptor that links dynein to its cargo. Disruption of dynein-mediated retrograde axonal transport impairs the delivery of signalling molecules and organelles within motor neurones, leading to degeneration that preferentially affects the neurones supplying proximal lower-limb muscles. The result is a largely static or slowly progressive lower motor neurone weakness with preserved sensation. Because dynein is also crucial to neuronal migration, some DYNC1H1 variants additionally cause brain malformation and cognitive impairment.","additionalDifferentials":["SMN1-related spinal muscular atrophy type 3","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophies","Congenital myopathies with proximal weakness","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy with proximal weakness","Hereditary spastic paraplegia when there is a lower-limb-predominant picture","Other distal or proximal hereditary motor neuropathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electromyography and nerve conduction studies showing chronic motor neuronopathy with preserved sensation","DYNC1H1 and BICD2 sequencing or a hereditary neuromuscular panel","SMN1 testing to exclude 5q spinal muscular atrophy","Creatine kinase and muscle imaging to help separate neurogenic from myopathic weakness","Brain MRI where cortical malformation or learning difficulty is present","Detailed family history for dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Prominent upper motor neurone signs suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Seizures or significant developmental regression pointing to associated brain malformation","Rapidly progressive weakness inconsistent with a largely static disorder","Respiratory involvement, which is atypical and warrants review"],"prognosis":"Often relatively static or only slowly progressive, with many patients remaining ambulant into adulthood; prognosis is more guarded in DYNC1H1 cases complicated by brain malformation and cognitive impairment.","pitfalls":["Mistaking proximal lower-limb weakness for a primary muscle disease without electrophysiology","Not testing DYNC1H1/BICD2 after a negative SMN1 result","Overlooking associated cortical malformation and learning difficulty in DYNC1H1 disease","Assuming steady progression when the course is frequently static"],"management":["Provide supportive care centred on physiotherapy to maintain lower-limb strength, range of motion and gait, as there is no gene-specific disease-modifying therapy","Prescribe orthoses such as ankle-foot orthoses and mobility aids to support proximal lower-limb weakness and reduce falls","Manage foot deformity and contractures with orthopaedic input and, where indicated, corrective surgery","Screen DYNC1H1 patients for associated cortical malformation and learning difficulty with brain MRI and neurodevelopmental support where indicated","Monitor the largely static to slowly progressive course with periodic functional and respiratory assessment","Distinguish from 5q spinal muscular atrophy by SMN1 testing so that prognosis and management are correctly framed","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and cascade testing of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: DYNC1H1- and BICD2-Related Spinal Muscular Atrophy","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinal_stenosis":{"name":"Spinal stenosis","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lumbar spinal stenosis","cervical spinal stenosis","spinal canal stenosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lumbar spinal stenosis classically causes neurogenic claudication, with leg pain, heaviness, or numbness on standing and walking that eases with sitting or leaning forward","The forward-flexed posture that relieves symptoms explains why patients often report easier walking uphill or when leaning on a trolley","Cervical stenosis can produce cervical myelopathy, with clumsy hands, gait imbalance, and upper motor neuron signs in the legs","It is usually a degenerative condition of older adults driven by disc bulging, facet hypertrophy, and ligamentum flavum thickening narrowing the canal or neural foramina","Distinguishing neurogenic from vascular claudication rests on the posture-dependent pattern, preserved peripheral pulses, and provocation by standing rather than a fixed walking distance","Initial management is usually conservative with physiotherapy, activity modification, and analgesia; decompressive surgery is considered for disabling or progressive symptoms","Progressive myelopathy in cervical stenosis or any sphincter disturbance warrants prompt specialist assessment","Imaging findings of stenosis are common with age and must be correlated with symptoms rather than treated in isolation"],"pathophysiology":"Spinal stenosis is narrowing of the spinal canal, lateral recesses, or neural foramina that compresses the cord, cauda equina, or nerve roots. It is most often degenerative, resulting from intervertebral disc bulging, osteophyte formation, facet joint hypertrophy, and buckling or thickening of the ligamentum flavum, sometimes compounded by spondylolisthesis or congenitally narrow canals. In the lumbar spine, compression of the cauda equina and roots produces neurogenic claudication, aggravated by extension which further narrows the canal and relieved by flexion. In the cervical spine, cord compression can cause myelopathy through direct pressure and ischaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Peripheral vascular (vascular) claudication","Lumbar disc herniation with radiculopathy","Peripheral neuropathy","Hip or knee osteoarthritis","Other causes of myelopathy in cervical disease (e.g. tumour, demyelination)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the relevant spinal region to characterise the level and severity of compression","CT or CT myelography when MRI is contraindicated or bony detail is needed","Plain radiographs including flexion-extension views to assess instability or spondylolisthesis","Nerve conduction studies or EMG when peripheral neuropathy is a competing diagnosis","Ankle-brachial index or vascular studies to exclude arterial claudication"],"redFlags":["New or progressive cauda equina symptoms such as saddle anaesthesia, urinary retention, or bowel dysfunction requiring urgent evaluation","Rapidly progressive cervical myelopathy with worsening gait or hand function","Bilateral neurological deficit or significant motor weakness","Features suggesting infection or malignancy, such as fever, night pain, or weight loss"],"prognosis":"Symptoms often follow a fluctuating or slowly progressive course; many patients are managed conservatively for years, and decompression can relieve claudication and stabilise myelopathy, though established cord damage in cervical disease may not fully reverse.","pitfalls":["Treating incidental imaging stenosis that does not match the clinical picture","Confusing neurogenic with vascular claudication and missing peripheral arterial disease","Overlooking early cervical myelopathy, mistaking gait and hand changes for ageing","Failing to recognise cauda equina features as an emergency"],"management":["Begin conservative management with physiotherapy, activity modification, flexion-based exercise and simple analgesia","Add neuropathic-pain agents such as gabapentinoids and consider epidural corticosteroid injection for radicular symptoms","Offer decompressive surgery, such as laminectomy with fusion where instability or spondylolisthesis coexists, for disabling or progressive neurogenic claudication","Treat cervical myelopathy with timely surgical decompression to arrest progression, as established cord damage may not reverse","Distinguish neurogenic from vascular claudication before treatment using the posture-dependent pattern, peripheral pulses and ankle-brachial index","Monitor for progressive deficit and escalate urgently if myelopathy, bladder or bowel dysfunction, or cauda equina features emerge"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"spindle_cell_haemangioma":{"name":"Spindle cell haemangioma","system":"Skin / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Spindle cell haemangioendothelioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Benign vascular neoplasm, formerly termed spindle cell haemangioendothelioma, typically arising in the distal extremities of adolescents and young adults.","Presents as one or several firm, sometimes painful, blue-red dermal or subcutaneous nodules that may recur locally or appear in crops.","Strongly associated with Maffucci syndrome (multiple enchondromas plus spindle cell haemangiomas).","Somatic IDH1 (and less often IDH2) mutations are characteristic and are shared with the enchondromas of Maffucci syndrome.","Histology shows cavernous vessels with organising thrombi and phleboliths alongside a spindled component with vacuolated epithelioid endothelial cells.","Does not metastasise, but new lesions commonly develop in the same anatomical region.","In Maffucci syndrome there is a substantial lifetime risk of chondrosarcoma and other malignancies, warranting surveillance.","Excision is used for symptomatic lesions, though recurrence and new lesions are frequent."],"pathophysiology":"Spindle cell haemangioma is a benign vascular tumour combining thin-walled cavernous blood vessels, often containing organising thrombi and phleboliths, with solid areas of bland spindle cells and epithelioid, vacuolated endothelial cells. Somatic mosaic mutations in IDH1 (rarely IDH2) produce the oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate, which alters cellular metabolism and epigenetic regulation; the same mutations drive the enchondromas of Maffucci syndrome, explaining the association. The lesion is thought to reflect a reactive, malformation-like process on a background of abnormal, stasis-prone vessels rather than a truly malignant proliferation.","additionalDifferentials":["Kaposi sarcoma","Kaposiform haemangioendothelioma","Venous malformation with organising thrombi","Epithelioid haemangioendothelioma","Angiosarcoma","Pyogenic granuloma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional biopsy with histology showing cavernous vessels, phleboliths, spindle cells and vacuolated endothelium","IDH1/IDH2 mutation analysis","Radiographs or MRI of the affected limb to detect enchondromas","Skeletal survey when Maffucci syndrome is suspected","Surveillance imaging for malignant transformation of enchondromas"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth or increasing pain in a pre-existing enchondroma, suggesting chondrosarcoma","New deep bony lesions in Maffucci syndrome","Atypical histological features raising concern for angiosarcoma","Neurovascular compromise from an enlarging lesion"],"prognosis":"The tumour itself is benign and does not metastasise, though local recurrence and new lesions are common. In Maffucci syndrome the overall prognosis is dominated by the high risk of malignant transformation of enchondromas.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the lesion histologically for Kaposi sarcoma or angiosarcoma.","Failing to evaluate for Maffucci syndrome and its malignancy risk.","Regarding local recurrence as evidence of malignancy.","Neglecting long-term skeletal surveillance."],"management":["Manage the benign vascular lesion with conservative observation or local surgical excision of symptomatic or troublesome nodules, accepting that new lesions commonly recur in the same region","Confirm the diagnosis histologically and avoid aggressive or mutilating surgery given the benign, non-metastasising nature","Provide analgesia for painful nodules and address cosmetic concerns","Evaluate for Maffucci syndrome when lesions are multiple, using limb radiographs or MRI and a skeletal survey to detect enchondromas","Institute long-term surveillance imaging for malignant transformation of enchondromas to chondrosarcoma in Maffucci syndrome","Refer to a vascular-anomalies multidisciplinary service and counsel that the IDH1 or IDH2 changes are somatic and not inherited"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of soft-tissue pathology","ISSVA classification of vascular anomalies","Reviews of Maffucci syndrome and IDH-mutant vascular tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"spindle_cell_lipoma":{"name":"Spindle cell lipoma","system":"Musculoskeletal / Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Spindle cell / pleomorphic lipoma","CD34-positive spindle cell lipomatous tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours / Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign lipomatous tumour that classically occurs in the posterior neck, shoulder, and upper back of older men","It presents as a slow-growing, painless, well-defined subcutaneous mass in the characteristic cape or shawl distribution","Histology shows a mixture of mature adipocytes, bland CD34-positive spindle cells, and distinctive ropey collagen bundles, often with a myxoid matrix and mast cells","It shares loss of chromosome 13q material, including the RB1 locus, with pleomorphic lipoma and mammary-type myofibroblastoma, placing them on one molecular spectrum","Pleomorphic lipoma is a related lesion showing floret-type multinucleated giant cells","Strong CD34 positivity with retained bland cytology and loss of RB1 helps distinguish it from atypical lipomatous tumour","Unlike atypical lipomatous tumour and well-differentiated liposarcoma, it lacks MDM2 amplification","Marginal excision is curative and recurrence is uncommon"],"pathophysiology":"Spindle cell lipoma is a benign adipocytic neoplasm in which mature fat is replaced in varying proportion by bland spindle cells set within a matrix of characteristic thick, eosinophilic ropey collagen fibres, frequently accompanied by myxoid change and scattered mast cells. The tumour, together with its pleomorphic lipoma variant and mammary-type myofibroblastoma, is defined molecularly by deletions of the long arm of chromosome 13 that involve the RB1 tumour suppressor locus; loss of RB1 function drives the proliferation while permitting continued differentiation, so the cells remain cytologically banal. The spindle cells consistently express CD34. This molecular profile is fundamentally different from that of atypical lipomatous tumour and well-differentiated liposarcoma, which are characterised by amplification of MDM2 and CDK4, and the distinction is central to avoiding overtreatment of what is a wholly benign lesion.","additionalDifferentials":["Atypical lipomatous tumour and well-differentiated liposarcoma","Pleomorphic lipoma","Mammary-type myofibroblastoma","Solitary fibrous tumour","Myxoid liposarcoma when myxoid change is prominent","Nuchal-type fibroma or other collagen-rich lesions","Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans given CD34 positivity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound or MRI to characterise the subcutaneous fatty mass","Excisional or core biopsy with histological assessment","Immunohistochemistry demonstrating CD34-positive spindle cells","RB1 loss testing by immunohistochemistry or molecular methods in difficult cases","MDM2 amplification testing to exclude atypical lipomatous tumour when there is doubt","Correlation of site and cytology, as the classic distribution supports the diagnosis"],"redFlags":["A deep or unusually large lesion, or one arising outside the classic distribution","Cytological atypia or lipoblasts raising concern for atypical lipomatous tumour or liposarcoma","Rapid growth","Recurrence after excision prompting review of the original diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Spindle cell lipoma is benign with an excellent prognosis, and marginal excision is curative with recurrence being rare. The principal clinical value lies in confidently separating it from atypical lipomatous tumour to avoid unnecessarily aggressive management.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the spindle cells or myxoid change as a liposarcoma","Overlooking the diagnosis when a lesion arises outside the classic neck and shoulder region","Confusing strong CD34 positivity with dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans or solitary fibrous tumour","Failing to use MDM2 testing to exclude atypical lipomatous tumour in equivocal cases"],"management":["Treat with marginal surgical excision, which is curative and rarely followed by recurrence","Confirm the benign diagnosis with histology and CD34-positive immunohistochemistry, correlating with the characteristic cape or shawl distribution","Distinguish from atypical lipomatous tumour and well-differentiated liposarcoma using RB1 loss and MDM2 amplification testing to avoid over-treatment","Offer conservative observation for small, asymptomatic lesions that are clinically and radiologically typical","Reassure regarding the excellent prognosis, with routine wound follow-up rather than intensive surveillance","Refer to a soft-tissue or sarcoma service when imaging or cytology raises doubt about a liposarcoma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA","autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia","ADCA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A genetically heterogeneous group of dominantly inherited progressive ataxias, numbered by locus (for example SCA1, SCA2, SCA3, SCA6, SCA7)","Many of the common subtypes are caused by expanded CAG trinucleotide repeats encoding polyglutamine tracts, and repeat length often correlates inversely with age at onset","Anticipation, with earlier onset and greater severity in successive generations, is a feature of the repeat-expansion subtypes","Core features are progressive gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria, and abnormal eye movements, with additional signs varying by subtype","SCA3 (Machado-Joseph disease) is among the most common worldwide and can include parkinsonism, neuropathy, and prominent eye-movement abnormalities","SCA7 is distinctive for associated pigmentary retinopathy and progressive visual loss, and SCA6 tends to be a relatively pure, later-onset cerebellar syndrome","Diagnosis combines family history, clinical phenotype, and targeted or panel genetic testing for repeat expansions and other mutations","Management is largely supportive, addressing gait safety, speech and swallowing, and associated features, since disease-modifying therapy is limited"],"pathophysiology":"The spinocerebellar ataxias comprise many distinct genetic disorders that share progressive degeneration of the cerebellum and its connections, often extending to the brainstem, spinal cord, and other structures. In the common polyglutamine subtypes, expanded CAG repeats produce abnormal proteins that misfold, aggregate, and cause neuronal dysfunction and loss, with a threshold repeat length determining disease and longer expansions producing earlier, more severe disease. Other subtypes arise from noncoding repeat expansions or conventional mutations affecting ion channels or other neuronal proteins, giving overlapping but distinguishable phenotypes.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal recessive ataxias including Friedreich ataxia","Acquired cerebellar ataxias (alcohol, paraneoplastic, immune-mediated, toxic)","Multiple system atrophy, cerebellar type","Episodic ataxias and other channelopathies","Fragile X-associated tremor/ataxia syndrome","Structural and vascular cerebellar lesions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted or panel genetic testing for repeat expansions and point mutations","Brain MRI to assess cerebellar and brainstem atrophy","Detailed family history and pedigree analysis","Ophthalmological review where retinopathy is suspected (as in SCA7)","Nerve conduction studies when peripheral neuropathy features are present","Exclusion of acquired and treatable causes of ataxia"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression raising concern for an acquired or paraneoplastic cause rather than a hereditary ataxia","Progressive dysphagia with aspiration risk","Falls with injury from worsening gait ataxia","Visual loss suggesting retinal involvement in relevant subtypes"],"prognosis":"Most subtypes are slowly progressive over years to decades with gradual loss of independent mobility and speech; life expectancy is often reduced in more severe subtypes, largely through complications such as aspiration, while relatively pure subtypes may follow a more benign course.","pitfalls":["Assuming all cerebellar ataxia is hereditary and overlooking treatable acquired causes","Not recognising anticipation and the significance of family history across generations","Missing subtype-specific features such as retinopathy in SCA7","Ordering untargeted testing without a clinical and pedigree-guided strategy"],"management":["Provide symptomatic and supportive care coordinated through a specialist ataxia service, as no disease-modifying therapy exists","Maximise function and safety with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, balance and gait training, and mobility aids to reduce falls","Offer speech and language therapy for dysarthria and swallow assessment with diet modification or gastrostomy where aspiration risk develops","Treat associated features symptomatically, including spasticity, tremor, parkinsonism, neuropathic pain and depression","Manage subtype-specific complications such as retinal surveillance in SCA7 and seizure control where relevant","Monitor progression with standardised ataxia rating scales and surveil for aspiration and respiratory complications","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance, discussing anticipation and predictive or cascade testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_1":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 1 (SCA1)","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA1","ATXN1-related ataxia","olivopontocerebellar atrophy type 1","ataxin-1 CAG repeat expansion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia caused by a CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the ATXN1 gene encoding an expanded polyglutamine tract in ataxin-1","Typically presents in early to mid adulthood with progressive gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria, and abnormal eye movements","Prominent pyramidal signs (hyperreflexia, spasticity) and relatively brisk reflexes early in the course help distinguish it from some other spinocerebellar ataxias","Later features include peripheral neuropathy, bulbar dysfunction with dysphagia, and saccadic slowing or ophthalmoparesis","Shows anticipation, with earlier onset and more severe disease across generations as the repeat expands, particularly with paternal transmission","Larger repeat expansions correlate with earlier onset and faster progression","Diagnosis is confirmed by targeted genetic testing for the ATXN1 CAG repeat length; management is supportive and multidisciplinary"],"pathophysiology":"SCA1 is a polyglutamine (polyQ) neurodegenerative disorder caused by an expanded CAG repeat in the ATXN1 gene. The elongated polyglutamine tract confers a toxic gain of function on the ataxin-1 protein, promoting its abnormal accumulation and formation of nuclear inclusions, disrupting transcriptional regulation and protein homeostasis. This leads to progressive degeneration predominantly of cerebellar Purkinje cells, brainstem (pontine and olivary) nuclei, and spinocerebellar tracts, producing the olivopontocerebellar pattern of atrophy. Neuronal dysfunction and loss accumulate over years, and the repeat length inversely correlates with age of onset, explaining the phenomenon of genetic anticipation.","additionalDifferentials":["Other autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA2, SCA3, SCA6, SCA7)","Friedreich ataxia","Multiple system atrophy (cerebellar type)","Acquired cerebellar degeneration (alcohol, paraneoplastic, immune-mediated)","Fragile X-associated tremor/ataxia syndrome","Episodic ataxias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted molecular genetic testing for ATXN1 CAG repeat expansion","MRI of the brain showing cerebellar and brainstem (olivopontocerebellar) atrophy","Nerve conduction studies to assess associated peripheral neuropathy","Detailed oculomotor assessment for saccadic slowing and nystagmus","Swallowing and speech evaluation as bulbar involvement develops","Exclusion of acquired causes with relevant blood tests where indicated"],"redFlags":["Progressive dysphagia and aspiration risk from bulbar involvement","Rapid functional decline or falls compromising safety","Respiratory compromise in advanced disease"],"prognosis":"SCA1 is progressive over one to a few decades, with SCA1 tending to progress somewhat faster than several other dominant ataxias; disability accumulates steadily and premature death commonly results from bulbar and respiratory complications.","pitfalls":["Assuming a specific subtype from clinical features alone rather than confirming with gene-specific testing","Overlooking prominent pyramidal signs that are a useful clue to SCA1","Failing to counsel about anticipation and reproductive implications","Neglecting proactive management of dysphagia and aspiration risk"],"management":["Provide symptomatic and multidisciplinary care, as there is no disease-modifying therapy for the ATXN1 repeat expansion","Maintain mobility, balance and independence with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and gait aids to reduce falls","Treat the prominent pyramidal spasticity with stretching and antispasmodic agents, and manage neuropathic pain","Address bulbar involvement with speech and language therapy, regular swallow assessment and gastrostomy feeding when aspiration risk is high","Anticipate and manage respiratory complications, as bulbar and respiratory failure are the usual causes of death","Monitor progression with standardised ataxia scales, noting that SCA1 tends to progress faster than several other dominant ataxias","Offer genetic counselling on autosomal dominant inheritance, anticipation especially with paternal transmission, and predictive testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_10":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 10 (ATXN10, with epilepsy)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA10","ATXN10 ATTCT pentanucleotide repeat expansion","cerebellar ataxia with seizures"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia notable for its association with epilepsy, most often generalised tonic-clonic or complex partial seizures","Caused by a large intronic ATTCT pentanucleotide repeat expansion in ATXN10, unlike the CAG trinucleotide SCAs","Reported predominantly in individuals of Latin American (Amerindian) ancestry, an important clue in a patient with ataxia and seizures","Progressive gait and limb ataxia with dysarthria and nystagmus is the core neurological syndrome","Seizures may appear at or after the onset of ataxia and can be a prominent management issue","The expansion is very large and unstable, and anticipation may be seen across generations","MRI typically shows cerebellar atrophy","Care combines supportive ataxia management with appropriate anti-seizure therapy"],"pathophysiology":"SCA10 is caused by a markedly expanded ATTCT pentanucleotide repeat located within an intron of ATXN10. Because it is non-coding, the disorder is attributed to an RNA-mediated toxic gain-of-function in which the expanded repeat transcript sequesters RNA-binding proteins and disrupts normal RNA processing, rather than to a polyglutamine protein. This leads to Purkinje cell and cerebellar cortical degeneration and, through mechanisms that remain incompletely understood, a lowered seizure threshold in many patients. The large, unstable expansion accounts for variable repeat size, anticipation and the strong founder effect observed in Amerindian-derived populations.","additionalDifferentials":["Other autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias, particularly SCA2 which can also feature seizures","Dentatorubral-pallidoluysian atrophy (ataxia with epilepsy and other features)","Progressive myoclonic epilepsy syndromes","Mitochondrial disorders combining ataxia and seizures","Coeliac or other immune-mediated ataxia","Sporadic adult-onset idiopathic cerebellar ataxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted ATXN10 ATTCT repeat-expansion analysis","Electroencephalography to characterise seizure type and guide therapy","MRI brain to document cerebellar atrophy and exclude structural lesions","Broad SCA repeat-expansion panel when ATXN10 testing is negative","Nerve conduction studies to assess for associated neuropathy","Screening for acquired ataxia causes when the picture is atypical"],"redFlags":["Status epilepticus or poorly controlled seizures requiring urgent management","Rapid neurological deterioration suggesting an alternative or superimposed process","Prominent myoclonus and cognitive decline raising suspicion of a progressive myoclonic epilepsy","New focal deficits indicating a structural lesion rather than SCA10 alone"],"prognosis":"SCA10 generally progresses slowly over years to decades; seizure control is achievable in most patients and, when adequate, ataxia remains the main determinant of long-term disability. Prognosis is more guarded in those with frequent seizures or additional systemic features.","pitfalls":["Ordering only CAG-based SCA panels and missing the pentanucleotide ATXN10 expansion","Overlooking ancestry as a diagnostic clue in a patient with combined ataxia and epilepsy","Attributing seizures to an unrelated epilepsy syndrome and delaying genetic diagnosis","Assuming all dominant SCAs share the same molecular mechanism when counselling about recurrence"],"management":["Control seizures with antiepileptic drug therapy tailored to generalised tonic-clonic and complex partial seizures and guided by EEG","Provide symptomatic ataxia care with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, gait aids and speech and language therapy, as no disease-modifying treatment exists","Manage swallowing safety with assessment and diet modification as bulbar features develop","Monitor progression of ataxia and seizure frequency, adjusting antiepileptics to maintain adequate control","Coordinate care between neurology and epilepsy services, and note Latin American Amerindian ancestry when evaluating the family","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance, the unstable expansion and anticipation across generations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Spinocerebellar Ataxia Type 10","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_11":{"name":"Spinocerebellar Ataxia Type 11","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia & spastic paraplegia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA11","TTBK2"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["SCA11 is a rare autosomal dominant, largely pure cerebellar ataxia caused by truncating (frameshift or nonsense) mutations in TTBK2, the gene encoding tau tubulin kinase 2.","It is among the mildest of the dominant ataxias, with slowly progressive gait and limb ataxia, prolonged preservation of walking and a normal or near-normal lifespan.","Onset is usually in adolescence or early adulthood, although some individuals present in mid-life.","Eye-movement abnormalities are characteristic and helpful clues: horizontal and vertical nystagmus, jerky smooth pursuit and abnormal saccades.","Mild dysarthria and pyramidal signs (brisk reflexes, occasional extensor plantar responses) may accompany the cerebellar syndrome, but marked extracerebellar disease argues against SCA11.","MRI typically shows isolated vermian and hemispheric cerebellar atrophy with a relatively preserved brainstem.","TTBK2 governs assembly of the primary cilium, placing SCA11 among cilium-related neurodegenerations and distinguishing its mechanism from the repeat-expansion ataxias.","Diagnosis relies on the dominant family history and targeted TTBK2 sequencing after the common repeat-expansion ataxias have been excluded."],"pathophysiology":"SCA11 is caused by heterozygous truncating mutations in TTBK2 on chromosome 15q, which encodes tau tubulin kinase 2, a serine-threonine kinase. TTBK2 is recruited to the distal end of the mother centriole (the basal body), where it initiates primary cilium assembly by displacing the capping protein CP110 and recruiting intraflagellar transport machinery, and it also phosphorylates tau and tubulin. Disease-causing mutations truncate the protein, preserving the amino-terminal kinase domain but removing the carboxy-terminal regulatory region; the truncated product is thought to act through a dominant-negative effect and/or haploinsufficiency, impairing ciliogenesis and disturbing Purkinje cell homeostasis. The result is a comparatively slow, selective cerebellar degeneration with Purkinje cell loss, which underlies the mild and predominantly cerebellar clinical picture.","additionalDifferentials":["SCA6 (CACNA1A) — slowly progressive, relatively pure late-onset cerebellar ataxia","SCA5 (SPTBN2) and SCA26 (EEF2) — dominant pure cerebellar ataxias","SCA15/16 (ITPR1) — very slowly progressive pure cerebellar ataxia","SCA31 (BEAN1) — late-onset pure cerebellar ataxia","Sporadic adult-onset cerebellar ataxia and the cerebellar variant of multiple system atrophy","Acquired ataxias (alcohol-related, gluten, paraneoplastic) as treatable mimics"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted TTBK2 gene sequencing, usually within an ataxia gene panel or exome analysis","Repeat-sizing to exclude the common expansion ataxias (SCA1, 2, 3, 6, 7 and others)","Brain MRI to document cerebellar atrophy and exclude structural or brainstem disease","Detailed oculomotor assessment, with video-oculography where available","Family pedigree analysis and cascade genetic testing with counselling","Nerve conduction studies if a peripheral neuropathy is suspected clinically","Screen for treatable acquired ataxias (vitamin E, thyroid function, coeliac and paraneoplastic antibodies) before settling on a genetic label"],"redFlags":["Rapid deterioration, prominent autonomic failure or early falls suggest multiple system atrophy rather than SCA11.","Marked cognitive decline, seizures or prominent extrapyramidal features are atypical and warrant reconsideration.","New bulbar symptoms such as swallowing difficulty or aspiration require prompt safety assessment.","Acute or subacute onset points to an acquired cause and demands urgent evaluation."],"prognosis":"SCA11 is generally benign among the dominant ataxias, with slow progression, prolonged independent walking and a normal or near-normal life expectancy, though gait aids may eventually be needed.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal common-expansion panel excludes a dominant ataxia; SCA11 is a rare conventional-mutation cause that requires gene sequencing.","Mislabelling the mild, slowly progressive course as functional or as simple ageing, delaying diagnosis.","Overlooking a subtle dominant family history because of the mild phenotype and variable age at onset.","Attributing pyramidal signs to a separate condition rather than recognising them as part of SCA11."],"management":["Provide symptomatic care for this mild, largely pure cerebellar ataxia, as no disease-modifying therapy for the TTBK2 mutation is available","Maintain balance and gait with physiotherapy and, when eventually needed, walking aids, given the prolonged preservation of mobility","Offer speech and language therapy for mild dysarthria","Reassure regarding the generally benign course and near-normal life expectancy, with periodic clinical monitoring","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and cascade testing of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics reference texts","Clinical neurology textbook — hereditary ataxias","Peer-reviewed hereditary ataxia reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_12":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 12 (PPP2R2B)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA12","PPP2R2B-related ataxia","PPP2R2B CAG repeat expansion","autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia type SCA12"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant ataxia caused by an expanded CAG trinucleotide repeat in the 5' region of PPP2R2B, which encodes a regulatory subunit of protein phosphatase 2A; unusually the repeat lies outside the coding sequence rather than producing a polyglutamine tract","A characteristic early feature is a prominent postural and action tremor of the upper limbs that can be mistaken for essential tremor and may precede or overshadow gait ataxia","As the disorder advances patients develop cerebellar dysarthria, limb and gait ataxia, abnormal eye movements and hyperreflexia, with parkinsonian features or cognitive decline in some","Described in several populations but reported with particular frequency in certain Indian kindreds, so ancestry and a detailed family history can be useful clues","Brain MRI typically shows both cerebellar and cerebral cortical atrophy, reflecting the wider cortical involvement of this subtype","Diagnosis rests on sizing the CAG repeat within PPP2R2B; standard exome sequencing or panels that do not specifically assess this repeat will miss the diagnosis","No disease-modifying therapy exists, so care is supportive and multidisciplinary, with physiotherapy, speech and language therapy, gait aids and tremor management"],"pathophysiology":"SCA12 is caused by an expanded CAG repeat in the 5' regulatory region of PPP2R2B, which encodes the Bbeta regulatory subunit of protein phosphatase 2A, a major serine/threonine phosphatase controlling neuronal signalling. Because the repeat lies outside the coding region, the mechanism is thought to be altered gene expression rather than a toxic polyglutamine protein, with dysregulated phosphatase activity disturbing neuronal signalling, calcium handling and survival. Purkinje cells and cerebral cortical neurons appear particularly vulnerable, producing the combination of tremor, progressive cerebellar ataxia and cortical features that characterises the disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Essential tremor, especially early when tremor dominates the picture","Repeat-expansion spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA1, 2, 3 and related types)","Fragile X-associated tremor/ataxia syndrome (FXTAS) in older men","Tremor-predominant or atypical parkinsonism","Dystonic tremor and other combined tremor syndromes","Sporadic adult-onset cerebellar ataxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted CAG repeat-sizing analysis of PPP2R2B to confirm the expansion","Brain MRI to document cerebellar and cerebral cortical atrophy and exclude structural causes","Testing for common repeat-expansion SCAs before or alongside PPP2R2B analysis","FMR1 premutation testing to exclude FXTAS in appropriate patients","Detailed three-generation family history to establish autosomal dominant transmission","Screening for acquired ataxia (thyroid function, vitamin B12, coeliac serology, alcohol history) and neuropsychological assessment where cognition is affected"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression or an atypical course prompting reconsideration of an acquired, paraneoplastic or structural cause","Prominent early cognitive decline suggesting a broader neurodegenerative process","Dysphagia with recurrent aspiration requiring swallow assessment and airway protection","Recurrent falls with injury as gait ataxia worsens"],"prognosis":"SCA12 is slowly progressive over years to decades, with tremor and ataxia gradually becoming disabling and cognitive or parkinsonian features emerging in a subset. Life expectancy is often relatively preserved, and prognosis is influenced chiefly by falls, bulbar involvement and the degree of cortical dysfunction.","pitfalls":["Labelling the patient with essential tremor and not pursuing a genetic cause when subtle cerebellar signs are present","Stopping investigation when common SCA panels are negative, thereby missing the PPP2R2B repeat","Relying on exome sequencing, which does not reliably detect this non-coding repeat expansion","Attributing the picture solely to Parkinson's disease when parkinsonian features appear","Failing to offer genetic counselling regarding dominant inheritance and variable expressivity"],"management":["Provide symptomatic and supportive care, as there is no disease-modifying therapy for the PPP2R2B expansion","Treat the prominent postural and action tremor symptomatically, trialling agents used for essential tremor such as beta-blockers or primidone","Maintain mobility and safety with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and gait aids, and offer speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Manage emerging parkinsonian features and cognitive decline with appropriate symptomatic treatment and neuropsychological support","Monitor progression with ataxia rating scales and surveil for falls and bulbar involvement","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance, with CAG-repeat sizing of PPP2R2B for at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":["spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_5","spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_14"]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_13":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 13 (KCNC3)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA13","KCNC3-related ataxia","Kv3.3 potassium channel ataxia","autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia type SCA13"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant ataxia caused by missense variants in KCNC3, which encodes the Kv3.3 voltage-gated potassium channel needed for high-frequency firing of cerebellar neurons, making it a channelopathy rather than a repeat-expansion disorder","Two broad phenotypes are recognised: an infantile or childhood-onset form with motor developmental delay, intellectual disability and often relatively stable ataxia, and a later adult-onset progressive cerebellar ataxia","Core features include gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria and nystagmus, with cognitive impairment prominent in the early-onset form and pyramidal signs in some","Particular KCNC3 variants tend to correlate with the early-onset versus adult-onset phenotype, reflecting distinct effects on channel gating","Because the causative changes are point mutations, repeat-expansion assays are normal and diagnosis requires gene sequencing by panel or exome","Brain MRI shows cerebellar atrophy, and in early-onset cases cerebellar hypoplasia may be present","Management is supportive; there is no therapy that corrects the underlying channel defect"],"pathophysiology":"SCA13 arises from heterozygous missense variants in KCNC3, encoding the Kv3.3 voltage-gated potassium channel that enables rapid repolarisation and sustained high-frequency firing in Purkinje cells and deep cerebellar and brainstem neurons. The mutations alter channel gating, acting through loss of function or dominant-negative effects, which impairs the precise, fast firing these neurons depend upon. In developmental (early-onset) variants this disturbance additionally affects cerebellar development, so the phenotype can combine neurodevelopmental impairment with cerebellar dysfunction, whereas adult-onset variants tend to cause progressive Purkinje cell degeneration and ataxia.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital cerebellar hypoplasia and other early-onset developmental ataxias","Other autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias","Other channelopathy ataxias such as episodic ataxia types 1 and 2","Cerebral palsy in children presenting with delay and ataxia","Mitochondrial and metabolic ataxias","Recessive early-onset ataxias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["KCNC3 sequencing as part of an ataxia gene panel or exome","Brain MRI to characterise cerebellar atrophy or hypoplasia","Testing for common repeat-expansion SCAs to exclude commoner causes","Developmental and neuropsychological assessment in children with delay","Detailed family history to establish autosomal dominant transmission","Screening for metabolic and acquired ataxia causes where the presentation is atypical"],"redFlags":["Developmental regression or rapid progression suggesting an alternative or acquired disorder","Seizures pointing to a broader neurodevelopmental or metabolic process","Severe bulbar dysfunction with feeding difficulty or aspiration, particularly in infants","New focal neurological signs indicating a structural lesion"],"prognosis":"In the early-onset form disability is often relatively static but combined with lasting developmental and cognitive impact, whereas the adult-onset form is slowly progressive; overall course and severity vary with the specific variant, and many patients retain useful mobility for extended periods.","pitfalls":["Expecting a repeat expansion and missing the point mutation when panels for common SCAs are negative","Mislabelling an early-onset child as having cerebral palsy rather than a genetic ataxia","Not proceeding to gene sequencing, which is required to detect KCNC3 variants","Overlooking the developmental phenotype and its implications for schooling and support","Failing to provide genetic counselling given dominant inheritance and variable expressivity"],"management":["Provide symptomatic and supportive care, as no disease-modifying therapy exists for this Kv3.3 potassium-channel disorder","Maintain function with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, gait aids and speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Provide developmental support, special educational input and neuropsychological care in the early-onset form with intellectual disability","Treat associated pyramidal signs and manage swallowing safety as bulbar features develop","Monitor the course, which is often static in early-onset disease and slowly progressive in the adult-onset form","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and predictive or cascade gene testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":["episodic_ataxia_type_1"]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_14":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 14 (PRKCG)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA14","PRKCG-related ataxia","protein kinase C gamma ataxia","autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia type SCA14"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant, slowly progressive and predominantly pure cerebellar ataxia caused by missense variants in PRKCG, which encodes protein kinase C gamma, most often affecting its regulatory C1 domain","Onset varies widely but is frequently early, in childhood or young adulthood, with slow progression thereafter","A recognised distinguishing feature is myoclonus, either axial or in the limbs, and some patients also show tremor, dystonia or later cognitive changes","Core cerebellar features are gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria and nystagmus","Because it is caused by point mutations, repeat-expansion testing is normal and the diagnosis depends on gene sequencing","Brain MRI shows cerebellar atrophy that is frequently most marked in the vermis","No disease-modifying therapy exists; management is supportive and includes treatment of troublesome myoclonus"],"pathophysiology":"SCA14 is caused by dominant missense variants in PRKCG, which encodes protein kinase C gamma, a calcium- and lipid-activated serine/threonine kinase strongly expressed in Purkinje cells. Mutations, commonly within the regulatory C1 domain, disturb the normal autoinhibition and calcium-dependent activation of the kinase and can promote misfolding and aggregation, producing abnormal signalling within Purkinje cells. The resulting Purkinje cell dysfunction and degeneration cause the progressive cerebellar ataxia, with associated myoclonus and other features reflecting the kinase's role in cerebellar circuit function.","additionalDifferentials":["Other pure autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxias (SCA5, SCA6, SCA28)","Progressive myoclonic ataxia syndromes","Repeat-expansion spinocerebellar ataxias","Fragile X-associated tremor/ataxia syndrome","Sporadic adult-onset cerebellar ataxia","Autoimmune or gluten-related cerebellar ataxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PRKCG sequencing within an ataxia gene panel or exome","Brain MRI to document cerebellar (often vermian) atrophy","Testing for common repeat-expansion SCAs before proceeding to PRKCG analysis","Electrophysiology (EEG and EMG) where myoclonus needs characterisation","Screening for acquired ataxia (thyroid function, vitamins B12 and E, coeliac serology)","Detailed family history to confirm dominant transmission"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression or early severe features suggesting an acquired or paraneoplastic cause","Prominent early cognitive decline indicating a broader neurodegenerative process","Severe myoclonus or seizures requiring specific management","Dysphagia with aspiration risk requiring swallow assessment"],"prognosis":"SCA14 is typically slowly progressive over decades, and many patients retain independent walking for a prolonged period; life expectancy is often relatively preserved, with disability driven by cumulative cerebellar dysfunction, myoclonus and eventual bulbar involvement.","pitfalls":["Assuming a repeat-expansion SCA and stopping testing when common panels are negative","Overlooking myoclonus, a useful clue that can distinguish SCA14 from other pure cerebellar ataxias","Mislabelling early tremor or myoclonus as essential tremor or a functional disorder","Not proceeding to sequencing after negative repeat panels","Failing to provide genetic counselling regarding dominant inheritance and variable expressivity"],"management":["Provide symptomatic and supportive care for this slowly progressive, predominantly pure cerebellar ataxia, as no disease-modifying therapy for the PRKCG mutation is available","Treat myoclonus symptomatically with agents such as levetiracetam or clonazepam","Maintain mobility and safety with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and gait aids, and offer speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Manage associated tremor, dystonia and later cognitive change symptomatically","Monitor progression with standardised ataxia scales over the typically decades-long course","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and cascade testing of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":["spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_5","spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_28"]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_15":{"name":"Spinocerebellar Ataxia Type 15","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia & spastic paraplegia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA15","SCA16","ITPR1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["SCA15 is an autosomal dominant, purely cerebellar ataxia caused by heterozygous deletions or point mutations of ITPR1, the gene encoding the type 1 inositol 1,4,5-trisphosphate receptor.","It is one of the most slowly progressive dominant ataxias, often evolving over decades with long-preserved mobility.","What was historically termed SCA16 is now recognised as the same ITPR1 disorder, and SCA29 is an allelic, congenital non-progressive ataxia.","Head and postural or action tremor (titubation) can be an early and prominent feature, sometimes preceding overt gait ataxia.","Whole-gene or partial ITPR1 deletions are a common mechanism, so copy-number analysis is essential and sequencing alone may miss the diagnosis.","MRI shows selective cerebellar atrophy, often most marked in the superior vermis, with sparing of the brainstem.","Age at onset spans childhood to late adulthood, and life expectancy is essentially normal."],"pathophysiology":"SCA15 arises from heterozygous mutations of ITPR1 on chromosome 3p26, which encodes the type 1 inositol 1,4,5-trisphosphate receptor, a ligand-gated intracellular calcium-release channel of the endoplasmic reticulum that is very highly expressed in cerebellar Purkinje cells. Partial or whole-gene deletions cause haploinsufficiency, while missense point mutations impair receptor function; both reduce inositol-trisphosphate-mediated calcium release and disturb Purkinje cell calcium signalling and synaptic plasticity, producing slow Purkinje cell dysfunction and degeneration. Allelic variation explains a phenotypic spectrum: heterozygous loss or point mutations give the adult SCA15 phenotype, other missense variants underlie the congenital non-progressive SCA29, and biallelic loss produces a severe infantile ataxic disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["SCA6 (CACNA1A) — slowly progressive pure cerebellar ataxia","SCA5 (SPTBN2), SCA11 (TTBK2) and SCA26 (EEF2) — dominant pure cerebellar ataxias","SCA29 (ITPR1) — allelic congenital non-progressive ataxia presenting in childhood","SCA31 (BEAN1) — late-onset pure cerebellar ataxia","Essential tremor, when early head or action tremor dominates the picture","Sporadic late-onset cerebellar ataxia and the cerebellar variant of multiple system atrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["ITPR1 analysis that includes copy-number/deletion testing (such as MLPA or array comparative genomic hybridisation) as well as sequencing","Ataxia gene panel or exome with dedicated copy-number-variant calling","Exclusion of the common trinucleotide repeat-expansion ataxias","Brain MRI documenting vermian and hemispheric cerebellar atrophy","Family pedigree analysis and cascade testing with genetic counselling","Screen for treatable acquired causes before finalising a genetic diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Congenital or very early-onset severe ataxia with developmental delay suggests an allelic (SCA29) or biallelic phenotype and different counselling.","Rapid progression, autonomic failure or early severe disability is atypical and suggests an alternative diagnosis such as multiple system atrophy.","Prominent extracerebellar features such as marked pyramidal, extrapyramidal or cognitive involvement warrant reconsideration."],"prognosis":"SCA15 is very slowly progressive, with mobility often preserved for many years and a normal life expectancy; a minority progress to needing walking aids in later life.","pitfalls":["Relying on sequencing alone and missing the common ITPR1 deletions that require copy-number testing.","Misdiagnosing early head or action tremor as essential tremor and overlooking the underlying ataxia.","Not recognising that historical SCA16 reports describe the same ITPR1 disorder.","Confusing the childhood-onset allelic form (SCA29) with an acquired or non-genetic cerebellar disorder."],"management":["Provide symptomatic and supportive care for this very slowly progressive, purely cerebellar ataxia, as no disease-modifying therapy for the ITPR1 defect exists","Treat prominent head and postural titubation and action tremor symptomatically","Maintain balance and gait with physiotherapy and mobility aids only as needed, given long-preserved mobility","Offer speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Reassure regarding the very slow progression and generally normal life expectancy, with periodic monitoring","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and cascade testing of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics reference texts","Clinical neurology textbook — hereditary ataxias","Peer-reviewed hereditary ataxia reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_17":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 17 (TBP)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA17","TBP CAG/CAA repeat expansion","Huntington disease-like 4","HDL4"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant disorder combining cerebellar ataxia with prominent cognitive decline, psychiatric disturbance and movement disorders, so it is also termed Huntington disease-like 4","Caused by a CAG/CAA repeat expansion in TBP encoding the TATA-box-binding protein, a general transcription factor","The phenotype is broad and can include chorea, dystonia, parkinsonism, spasticity and seizures in addition to ataxia","Progressive dementia and behavioural or psychiatric change are frequently early and disabling","Reduced penetrance occurs in the intermediate repeat range, complicating interpretation and counselling","MRI shows cerebellar atrophy and, in many patients, cerebral and caudate atrophy resembling Huntington disease","Because CAA interruptions accompany the CAG repeat, the expansion is comparatively stable and anticipation is less pronounced than in some SCAs","Management is supportive and symptomatic, targeting the movement disorder, mood and cognition"],"pathophysiology":"SCA17 is caused by an expanded CAG/CAA repeat in TBP, which encodes the TATA-box-binding protein, a central component of the transcription initiation machinery used by all three RNA polymerases. The resulting elongated polyglutamine tract causes the protein to misfold and aggregate into nuclear inclusions, disrupting global transcriptional regulation through a toxic gain-of-function together with impaired normal TBP activity. Widespread neuronal degeneration affects the cerebellum, cerebral cortex and striatum, explaining the overlap with Huntington disease. The mixed CAG/CAA repeat structure confers relative meiotic stability, so anticipation is modest.","additionalDifferentials":["Huntington disease and other Huntington disease-like syndromes","Dentatorubral-pallidoluysian atrophy","Other autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA1, SCA2, SCA3)","Wilson disease and other treatable movement disorders with cognitive change","Frontotemporal dementia with movement features","Prion disease presenting with ataxia and dementia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted TBP CAG/CAA repeat-expansion analysis","Huntington disease (HTT) testing when the clinical picture is Huntington-like and TBP is negative","MRI brain assessing cerebellar, cortical and caudate atrophy","Neuropsychological assessment to characterise cognitive and behavioural decline","Copper studies and other screens to exclude treatable mimics such as Wilson disease","EEG when seizures are present"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive dementia raising the possibility of prion disease","Severe psychiatric disturbance with risk to self or others requiring urgent care","Marked dysphagia and aspiration risk in advanced disease","An intermediate-range TBP result of uncertain significance that risks misclassification"],"prognosis":"SCA17 is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder that typically advances over one to two decades, with combined motor, cognitive and psychiatric decline leading to substantial dependency; larger expansions tend to cause earlier onset and more severe disease.","pitfalls":["Testing only for Huntington disease and missing the phenotypically similar SCA17","Interpreting intermediate TBP alleles as definitively pathogenic given reduced penetrance","Overlooking treatable causes of ataxia with dementia such as Wilson disease before genetic testing","Focusing on ataxia while neglecting the often more disabling cognitive and psychiatric features"],"management":["Provide symptomatic and multidisciplinary care, as there is no disease-modifying therapy for the TBP repeat expansion","Treat the movement disorder symptomatically, using dopamine-depleting or antidopaminergic agents for chorea and managing dystonia, parkinsonism and spasticity as appropriate","Manage prominent psychiatric and behavioural disturbance with psychotropic medication and psychiatric support","Support progressive cognitive decline with neuropsychological input, cognitive and social care, and carer support","Control seizures with antiepileptic therapy where present, and manage swallowing safety with speech and language therapy and gastrostomy when aspiration risk is high","Monitor progression and provide anticipatory care planning for the substantial dependency that develops","Offer genetic counselling addressing autosomal dominant inheritance and reduced penetrance in the intermediate repeat range, with careful predictive-testing counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Spinocerebellar Ataxia Type 17","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_18":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 18","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA18","Sensorimotor neuropathy with ataxia","SMNA","IFRD1-related ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant disorder combining cerebellar ataxia with an axonal sensorimotor neuropathy, reflected in its descriptive name sensorimotor neuropathy with ataxia (SMNA).","Distinguished from the purely sensory dominant ataxias by prominent motor involvement — distal muscle weakness and atrophy accompany the sensory loss.","Linked to a locus on the long arm of chromosome 7 (7q22-q32); a missense variant in IFRD1 has been proposed as the candidate gene in the index family.","Onset is often in adolescence or early adulthood, with gait ataxia, dysarthria and nystagmus.","Reflexes are typically reduced or absent, while extensor plantar responses may appear, indicating mixed peripheral and central involvement.","MRI shows cerebellar atrophy and nerve conduction studies confirm an axonal sensorimotor neuropathy with neurogenic features.","The phenotype was originally characterised in an African-American kindred."],"pathophysiology":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 18, also termed sensorimotor neuropathy with ataxia, is an autosomal dominant condition mapped to chromosome 7q22-q32, with a missense change in IFRD1 (interferon-related developmental regulator 1) implicated as the underlying defect in the family in which it was defined, although the gene has not been robustly confirmed across independent cohorts. IFRD1 helps regulate transcriptional programmes in neuronal and muscle tissue, and its dysfunction is thought to produce degeneration of cerebellar pathways alongside sensory and motor peripheral nerves, yielding the characteristic combination of cerebellar atrophy, an axonal sensorimotor neuropathy and neurogenic muscle wasting. This dual central and peripheral, motor-involving pathology sets SCA18 apart from subtypes with a predominantly sensory neuronopathy such as SCA4 and SCA25.","additionalDifferentials":["SCA4 (ZFHX3, 16q22) — dominant ataxia with a predominantly sensory neuropathy","SCA25 (PNPT1, 2p) — dominant ataxia with sensory neuronopathy, sometimes with gastrointestinal symptoms","Common repeat-expansion ataxias SCA1, SCA2 and SCA3, which may include peripheral neuropathy","Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease with cerebellar features","Friedreich ataxia (recessive sensorimotor involvement with areflexia)","Hereditary motor and sensory neuropathies with ataxic features","Acquired chronic sensorimotor axonal neuropathies with coincidental cerebellar signs"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing or linkage to the 7q locus and IFRD1 where available","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography showing an axonal sensorimotor neuropathy with neurogenic changes","MRI brain demonstrating cerebellar atrophy","Hereditary ataxia and inherited-neuropathy gene panels","Bloods to exclude acquired causes of sensorimotor neuropathy (glucose and glycated haemoglobin, vitamin B12, paraprotein screen)","Family pedigree analysis with genetic counselling"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive weakness suggesting motor neuron disease or an inflammatory neuropathy rather than SCA18.","New bulbar or respiratory involvement.","Frequent falls arising from the combination of ataxia and limb weakness.","Subacute onset that should prompt a search for an acquired neuropathy."],"prognosis":"Slowly progressive over years, with disability resulting from the combination of ataxia, distal weakness and sensory loss; lifespan is generally preserved.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the condition for an isolated Charcot-Marie-Tooth neuropathy or a pure cerebellar ataxia when both components are present.","Assuming the IFRD1 variant is confirmed in every family, when the causative gene remains provisional.","Overlooking the motor component, which is the key distinguishing feature from sensory-predominant dominant ataxias.","Failing to exclude common acquired sensorimotor neuropathies before invoking a rare hereditary diagnosis."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so anchor care on targeted neurorehabilitation: physiotherapy for gait, balance and coordination, occupational therapy for daily function, and speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Manage the axonal sensorimotor neuropathy with ankle-foot orthoses for distal weakness and foot drop, supportive footwear, and neuropathic pain control using a gabapentinoid or tricyclic antidepressant","Institute falls prevention with appropriate walking aids and home hazard assessment, given the compounding of ataxia, distal weakness and sensory loss","Undertake periodic swallow assessment and dietitian review to detect and mitigate bulbar dysfunction and aspiration risk","Identify and treat coexisting modifiable causes of neuropathy (diabetes, vitamin B12 deficiency, paraproteinaemia) that may worsen the phenotype","Provide multidisciplinary neuromuscular and ataxia clinic follow-up","Offer genetic counselling for the autosomal dominant inheritance with predictive and cascade testing for at-risk relatives, noting the IFRD1/7q candidacy is not fully confirmed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics reference texts on autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias","Standard neurology textbook chapters on inherited ataxias and neuropathies","Consensus reviews of hereditary ataxias with peripheral neuropathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_19":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 19","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA19","SCA22","KCND3-related spinocerebellar ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant, slowly progressive cerebellar ataxia caused by heterozygous KCND3 variants; SCA19 and SCA22 were mapped separately but are the same allelic disorder.","KCND3 encodes the Kv4.3 voltage-gated potassium channel alpha subunit that carries the transient A-type outward current shaping Purkinje neuron firing.","Gait and limb ataxia with dysarthria are the core features, typically evolving over years.","Cognitive impairment and executive dysfunction are relatively common and can help distinguish it from purely motor cerebellar syndromes.","Additional signs include postural/action tremor, myoclonus, hyporeflexia and, in some pedigrees, a mild peripheral sensory neuropathy.","Age of onset is wide (childhood to late adulthood) with marked intrafamilial variability.","MRI usually shows cerebellar (predominantly vermian) atrophy with relative brainstem sparing, but can appear normal early."],"pathophysiology":"SCA19 is caused by pathogenic variants in KCND3, which encodes the pore-forming alpha subunit of the voltage-gated potassium channel Kv4.3. This channel generates the fast transient (A-type) outward potassium current that regulates dendritic excitability, back-propagating action potentials and repetitive firing of cerebellar Purkinje cells. Missense and small in-frame variants impair trafficking of the channel to the plasma membrane and/or alter its gating, reducing surface A-type current; mutant subunits also exert a dominant-negative effect by co-assembling with wild-type subunits and retaining them within the endoplasmic reticulum. The resulting disturbance of Purkinje cell firing and intracellular calcium handling drives progressive Purkinje cell dysfunction and degeneration, producing the cerebellar phenotype and, in some individuals, the associated cognitive and peripheral nerve features.","additionalDifferentials":["Other autosomal dominant SCAs, especially channelopathies (SCA6 due to CACNA1A; SCA13 due to KCNC3)","SCA14 (PRKCG) and other conventional-mutation dominant ataxias","Episodic ataxia type 1 (KCNA1) and type 2 (CACNA1A)","Polyglutamine SCAs with cognitive involvement (SCA17, dentatorubral-pallidoluysian atrophy)","Fragile X-associated tremor/ataxia syndrome","Acquired cerebellar ataxia (alcohol, anticonvulsant toxicity, immune-mediated) and multiple system atrophy, cerebellar type"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["KCND3 sequencing, ideally within a next-generation hereditary ataxia gene panel","Brain MRI to document cerebellar/vermian atrophy and exclude structural mimics","Repeat-expansion testing to exclude common polyglutamine spinocerebellar ataxias","Nerve conduction studies where a peripheral neuropathy is suspected","Neuropsychometric assessment for cognitive and executive dysfunction","Detailed family history with segregation and cascade genetic testing"],"redFlags":["Rapid or subacute progression suggesting an acquired, toxic or paraneoplastic cause rather than SCA19","Prominent early autonomic or pyramidal/brainstem features pointing to multiple system atrophy","Emerging cognitive decline requiring formal assessment and support"],"prognosis":"Slowly progressive with ambulation preserved for many years and a life expectancy that is often near-normal. Disability accrues gradually and associated cognitive involvement may affect independence.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal MRI excludes the diagnosis early in the disease course","Overlooking cognitive impairment as part of the phenotype and attributing it to an unrelated condition","Not recognising that SCA19 and SCA22 are the same KCND3 disorder, which causes confusion when reviewing the literature","Relying only on repeat-expansion panels, which miss this conventional (non-expansion) mutation"],"management":["With no disease-modifying therapy for this KCND3-related ataxia, centre care on physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Arrange neuropsychological assessment and cognitive support for the executive and cognitive impairment that characteristically accompanies this subtype","Treat postural and action tremor symptomatically with a trial of a beta-blocker or alternative anti-tremor agent, and address troublesome myoclonus","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids as gait ataxia progresses","Monitor for bulbar dysfunction with periodic swallow assessment and dietitian input","Provide multidisciplinary ataxia clinic follow-up","Offer genetic counselling with cascade testing, noting SCA19 and SCA22 are the same allelic KCND3 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Hereditary ataxia clinical references","Movement disorders textbooks","Neurogenetics textbooks"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_2":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 2 (SCA2)","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA2","ATXN2-related ataxia","ataxin-2 CAG repeat expansion","olivopontocerebellar atrophy type 2"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant ataxia caused by a CAG repeat expansion in the ATXN2 gene producing an expanded polyglutamine tract in ataxin-2","A characteristic distinguishing feature is markedly slow saccadic eye movements, often early and prominent","Early loss of deep tendon reflexes and a sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy are typical, contrasting with the brisk reflexes of SCA1","Presents with progressive gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria, and later dysphagia; action tremor and parkinsonism can occur","Demonstrates anticipation, with earlier onset and larger repeats in successive generations, more pronounced with paternal inheritance","Intermediate-length ATXN2 repeat expansions are an established risk factor for amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Confirmed by molecular testing of the ATXN2 CAG repeat; care is supportive and multidisciplinary"],"pathophysiology":"SCA2 is a polyglutamine disorder driven by expansion of the CAG repeat in the ATXN2 gene. The expanded polyglutamine tract alters ataxin-2, a protein involved in RNA metabolism and stress granule dynamics, causing a toxic gain of function with abnormal protein aggregation and disruption of RNA processing and cellular homeostasis. Neurodegeneration predominantly affects cerebellar Purkinje cells, pontine and olivary brainstem nuclei, and the substantia nigra, together with degeneration of peripheral sensory and motor pathways. This produces the olivopontocerebellar pattern with additional features such as slow saccades, neuropathy, and sometimes parkinsonism. Repeat length correlates inversely with age of onset, underlying anticipation.","additionalDifferentials":["Other dominant spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA1, SCA3, SCA6, SCA7)","Multiple system atrophy (cerebellar type)","Friedreich ataxia","Parkinsonism from other causes when extrapyramidal features predominate","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (given the ATXN2 link)","Acquired or immune-mediated cerebellar degeneration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted molecular genetic testing for ATXN2 CAG repeat expansion","Brain MRI demonstrating olivopontocerebellar atrophy","Oculomotor recording documenting slowed saccades","Nerve conduction studies confirming peripheral neuropathy","Assessment for parkinsonian features when present","Swallowing evaluation as bulbar dysfunction develops"],"redFlags":["Progressive dysphagia with aspiration risk","Rapid functional deterioration and recurrent falls","Emergence of motor neurone signs suggesting an ALS phenotype"],"prognosis":"SCA2 is progressive over years to decades with accumulating disability; life expectancy is shortened, most often by bulbar dysfunction and its respiratory complications.","pitfalls":["Overlooking slow saccades and areflexia as clues that point towards SCA2","Diagnosing a subtype clinically without gene-specific confirmation","Missing the association between ATXN2 intermediate repeats and ALS risk","Failing to address anticipation and reproductive counselling"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy is available, so base care on neurorehabilitation: physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Offer a trial of levodopa for parkinsonian features, which can be responsive in SCA2","Manage the sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy with neuropathic pain agents and orthoses for distal weakness","Undertake proactive bulbar surveillance with swallow assessment and dietitian input, and consider gastrostomy feeding when dysphagia threatens nutrition or causes aspiration","Monitor respiratory function, since bulbar dysfunction and its respiratory complications are the main determinants of survival","Provide genetic counselling addressing anticipation (larger repeats and earlier onset in successive generations, more marked with paternal transmission) and the intermediate-repeat amyotrophic lateral sclerosis risk, offering predictive and prenatal testing","Coordinate multidisciplinary ataxia clinic care with motor-neurone input where relevant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_20":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 20","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA20","11q12 duplication ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant ataxia whose hallmark is early, prominent dysphonia — a strained, spasmodic voice that often precedes or overshadows limb ataxia.","Palatal tremor (palatal myoclonus) is a characteristic and unusual accompanying feature.","Calcification of the dentate nucleus is frequently visible on CT and provides a valuable radiological clue.","Caused by a small contiguous duplication (a copy-number gain) on chromosome 11q12, rather than by a repeat expansion.","Progression is slow, and gaze-evoked nystagmus and mild pyramidal signs may be present.","The phenotype was originally described in an Australian family of Anglo-Celtic descent.","Because the early voice change can resemble primary spasmodic dysphonia, the diagnosis may be missed before ataxia becomes evident."],"pathophysiology":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 20 is an autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia caused by a small contiguous duplication, a copy-number gain, on chromosome 11q12, which distinguishes it mechanistically from the trinucleotide and pentanucleotide repeat-expansion ataxias. The gene or genes responsible for the phenotype through a dosage effect are not fully defined, with overexpression of genes within the duplicated segment (a proposed candidate being DAGLA, which encodes diacylglycerol lipase alpha) implicated. The disorder is marked radiologically by calcification of the dentate nucleus and clinically by early bulbar involvement — spasmodic dysphonia and palatal tremor — together with slowly progressive cerebellar ataxia.","additionalDifferentials":["Other autosomal dominant SCAs presenting with early dysarthria, such as SCA1","SCA36, which can produce bulbar features and tongue involvement","Progressive ataxia with palatal tremor of other genetic or sporadic cause","Primary or idiopathic spasmodic dysphonia before cerebellar signs emerge","Primary familial brain calcification and other causes of intracranial calcification","Dominant ataxias featuring cortical or action myoclonus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT head to detect calcification of the dentate nucleus","MRI brain to assess cerebellar atrophy","Molecular testing by chromosomal microarray or MLPA to detect the 11q12 duplication (dosage-sensitive methods)","Hereditary ataxia gene panel to exclude repeat-expansion subtypes","Laryngoscopy and formal voice assessment for the dysphonia","Evaluation for other causes of intracranial calcification (calcium-phosphate and parathyroid indices)","Family pedigree analysis with genetic counselling"],"redFlags":["Rapidly worsening dysphonia or dysphagia with aspiration risk.","New cognitive, psychiatric or extrapyramidal features suggesting an alternative diagnosis.","Frequent falls as cerebellar ataxia advances.","Extensive intracranial calcification beyond the dentate raising other metabolic disorders."],"prognosis":"Slowly progressive over years to decades, with disability dominated by cerebellar ataxia and bulbar dysfunction; lifespan is usually preserved.","pitfalls":["Attributing the early voice change to a primary laryngeal disorder and missing the underlying ataxia.","Expecting a repeat expansion and failing to test for the duplication, which requires dosage-sensitive or array methods.","Overlooking dentate nucleus calcification on imaging.","Confusing the condition with sporadic progressive ataxia with palatal tremor."],"management":["With no disease-modifying therapy, anchor care on physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy","Treat the spasmodic dysphonia with laryngeal botulinum toxin injections alongside ENT/laryngology and voice therapy","Manage palatal tremor symptomatically where it is troublesome or causes ear click","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids for progressive gait ataxia","Confirm the dentate calcification is not attributable to a treatable metabolic cause by checking calcium, phosphate and parathyroid status","Monitor for bulbar dysfunction with periodic swallow assessment","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing using dosage-sensitive methods (chromosomal microarray or MLPA) to detect the 11q12 duplication"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics reference texts on autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias","Standard neurology textbook chapters on inherited ataxias","Consensus reviews of copy-number and non-repeat cerebellar ataxias"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_21":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 21","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA21","TMEM240-related ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia caused by heterozygous variants in TMEM240 on chromosome 1p36.33; unlike many spinocerebellar ataxias it is a conventional point-mutation disorder, not a repeat expansion.","Cognitive impairment — ranging from subtle executive deficits to intellectual disability in early-onset cases — is a hallmark that helps separate it from the purely cerebellar subtypes.","Age at onset is broad, spanning childhood to mid-adult life, with slowly progressive gait ataxia, limb dysmetria and dysarthria.","Extrapyramidal features are characteristic: bradykinesia, rigidity, resting or postural tremor and a parkinsonian appearance in some patients.","Hyporeflexia or areflexia is frequently observed, contrasting with the brisk reflexes seen in several other dominant ataxias.","Brain imaging typically shows cerebellar and vermian atrophy with a relatively preserved brainstem.","First delineated in a French kindred; intrafamilial variability in severity and cognitive burden is wide.","Diagnosis relies on targeted gene or ataxia-panel/exome sequencing because the causative change is a missense (or start-loss) variant rather than an expanded repeat."],"pathophysiology":"SCA21 arises from heterozygous variants in TMEM240, which encodes a small transmembrane protein of incompletely defined function that is strongly expressed in cerebellar Purkinje cells and more widely in the developing and mature brain. The disease-associated missense and start-loss variants are thought to act through a dominant mechanism that disturbs Purkinje-cell homeostasis and cerebellar cortical circuitry, producing slowly progressive cerebellar degeneration; because the protein also appears important during neurodevelopment, cognitive impairment and extrapyramidal signs accompany the ataxia and give the subtype its distinctive multidomain phenotype. As no nucleotide repeat is involved, genetic anticipation is not a feature.","additionalDifferentials":["SCA13 (KCNC3) — dominant ataxia with cognitive impairment and, in some, childhood onset","SCA19/22 (KCND3) — ataxia with cognitive and occasional extrapyramidal features","SCA2 and SCA3 — subtypes in which parkinsonism can dominate the picture","SCA17 (TBP) — ataxia with cognitive decline, psychiatric change and chorea","Autosomal recessive early-onset ataxias when presentation is in childhood","Wilson disease — young-onset ataxia combined with extrapyramidal signs (a treatable mimic)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain to demonstrate cerebellar and vermian atrophy and exclude structural lesions","Formal neuropsychological assessment to characterise and monitor the cognitive profile","Targeted TMEM240 sequencing or a hereditary-ataxia gene panel / exome","Dedicated repeat-expansion testing to exclude the common repeat-based subtypes","Serum copper and caeruloplasmin plus an acquired-ataxia screen where diagnosis is unclear","Family segregation analysis with formal genetic counselling"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive course or prominent bulbar dysfunction suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Severe or early cognitive decline disproportionate to the motor deficit","Marked parkinsonism, dystonia or new movement disorder prompting re-evaluation","New-onset seizures"],"prognosis":"The course is slowly progressive over decades and many patients retain independent walking for a prolonged period, although cumulative cognitive and extrapyramidal involvement adds to disability. Life expectancy is often close to normal.","pitfalls":["Attributing the cognitive impairment to a primary dementia or psychiatric disorder rather than the ataxia syndrome","Assuming a repeat-expansion mechanism and stopping the work-up after negative repeat panels","Overlooking the parkinsonian features and mislabelling the patient as having idiopathic Parkinson disease","Mistaking the hyporeflexia for a primary peripheral neuropathy"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists for this TMEM240-related ataxia, so centre care on physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language therapy","Offer a trial of levodopa for the extrapyramidal features (bradykinesia, rigidity and tremor)","Arrange neuropsychological assessment with educational and cognitive support, especially in early-onset cases with intellectual disability","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids as gait ataxia progresses","Monitor for bulbar dysfunction with periodic swallow assessment","Provide multidisciplinary follow-up across neurology, neuropsychology and allied health","Offer genetic counselling with cascade testing, noting this is a point-mutation disorder without anticipation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Standard hereditary ataxia genetics reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_23":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 23 (PDYN)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA23","PDYN-related ataxia","prodynorphin ataxia","autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia type SCA23"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia caused by missense variants in PDYN, which encodes prodynorphin, the precursor of the opioid peptide dynorphin","Presentation is usually in adulthood, often mid-to-late, with slowly progressive gait ataxia and dysarthria","Alongside cerebellar signs, posterior-column sensory features such as reduced vibration and joint-position sense may be present, together with pyramidal signs like hyperreflexia and extensor plantar responses","Eye-movement abnormalities including gaze-evoked nystagmus and abnormal saccades are described","Because it is caused by point mutations, repeat-expansion testing is normal and diagnosis relies on gene sequencing","Brain MRI shows cerebellar atrophy, sometimes with brainstem involvement","There is no specific therapy; care is supportive and multidisciplinary"],"pathophysiology":"SCA23 is caused by dominant missense variants in PDYN, which encodes prodynorphin, a precursor processed into dynorphin peptides that act on kappa-opioid receptors and modulate neurotransmission. Pathogenic variants tend to cluster in the region giving rise to dynorphin A and are thought to yield abnormally stable or neurotoxic peptides that disturb glutamatergic signalling and neuronal calcium handling. The consequent Purkinje cell and related neuronal degeneration produces the cerebellar ataxia, with variable posterior-column and pyramidal involvement reflecting wider effects on neuronal survival.","additionalDifferentials":["Other autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias","Friedreich ataxia and other ataxias with posterior-column involvement","Vitamin E deficiency ataxia","Subacute combined degeneration from vitamin B12 deficiency","Multiple system atrophy, cerebellar type","Sporadic adult-onset cerebellar ataxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PDYN sequencing within an ataxia gene panel or exome","Brain MRI to document cerebellar and any brainstem atrophy","Testing for common repeat-expansion SCAs before proceeding to PDYN analysis","Nerve conduction studies and vitamin B12 and E levels where posterior-column signs are present","Detailed family history to establish autosomal dominant transmission","Screening for acquired ataxia (thyroid function, coeliac serology, alcohol history)"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression or an atypical course suggesting an acquired or paraneoplastic cause","Prominent autonomic failure pointing to multiple system atrophy","Severe sensory ataxia disproportionate to cerebellar signs, prompting a search for a treatable neuropathy","Dysphagia with aspiration risk requiring swallow assessment"],"prognosis":"SCA23 is slowly progressive over years, with gradually increasing gait and speech difficulty; disability varies between families and life expectancy is often relatively preserved, prognosis being influenced by bulbar involvement and falls.","pitfalls":["Expecting a repeat expansion and missing the point mutation when common panels are negative","Attributing posterior-column sensory signs to an acquired neuropathy without pursuing a genetic cause","Not proceeding to sequencing when the phenotype fits a rare dominant ataxia","Overlooking multiple system atrophy when autonomic features are present","Failing to offer genetic counselling regarding dominant inheritance"],"management":["With no disease-modifying therapy for this PDYN-related ataxia, base care on physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Manage pyramidal spasticity with physiotherapy, stretching and an antispasticity agent such as baclofen","Address posterior-column sensory ataxia with proprioceptive rehabilitation, walking aids and falls prevention","Exclude and treat modifiable causes of posterior-column signs, notably vitamin B12 and vitamin E deficiency","Monitor for bulbar involvement with periodic swallow assessment, as this influences prognosis","Provide multidisciplinary ataxia clinic follow-up","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing for the autosomal dominant transmission"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":["spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_5"]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_25":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 25","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA25","PNPT1-related ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant ataxia dominated by a severe large-fibre sensory axonal neuropathy, so proprioceptive loss and areflexia are as prominent as the cerebellar signs.","Gastrointestinal symptoms — recurrent vomiting and abdominal pain — were a striking feature in the originally described French family, an unusual accompaniment among the spinocerebellar ataxias.","There is a wide range of onset, from childhood to adult life, with marked variability even within families.","Mapped to the short arm of chromosome 2 (around 2p21-p15); variants in PNPT1 have recently been implicated as causative.","Nerve conduction studies show a sensory neuronopathy or ganglionopathy with reduced or absent sensory potentials.","Core cerebellar features include gait ataxia, dysarthria and nystagmus.","It is distinguished from SCA4 (ZFHX3, 16q22) and SCA18 by its locus and gene and, in some families, by the gastrointestinal involvement."],"pathophysiology":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 25 is an autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia mapped to the short arm of chromosome 2 (around 2p21-p15), in which variants in PNPT1 — encoding polyribonucleotide nucleotidyltransferase 1, a mitochondrial RNA-processing and import enzyme — have recently been identified as causative. Impaired mitochondrial RNA handling is thought to drive degeneration of dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons, producing a prominent sensory neuronopathy, together with cerebellar pathways and, in some families, gastrointestinal dysfunction. The resulting phenotype pairs a severe large-fibre sensory neuropathy with progressive cerebellar ataxia, contrasting with the sensorimotor pattern of SCA18 and overlapping clinically with the sensory-predominant SCA4.","additionalDifferentials":["SCA4 (ZFHX3, 16q22) — dominant ataxia with prominent sensory neuropathy","SCA18 (sensorimotor neuropathy with ataxia) — added motor weakness and atrophy","Friedreich ataxia (recessive sensory neuropathy with areflexia and cardiomyopathy)","CANVAS — cerebellar ataxia, neuropathy and vestibular areflexia syndrome (RFC1)","Acquired sensory neuronopathy (paraneoplastic anti-Hu, Sjogren-related ganglionopathy, vitamin B6 toxicity)","Other dominant cerebellar ataxias associated with peripheral neuropathy","Hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of PNPT1 or linkage to the 2p locus where available","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography demonstrating a sensory neuronopathy","MRI brain to assess cerebellar atrophy","Hereditary ataxia gene panel to exclude sibling subtypes","Investigations to exclude acquired sensory neuronopathy (paraneoplastic antibodies, Sjogren screen, vitamin B12 and B6 status)","Gastrointestinal evaluation when vomiting or abdominal symptoms are present","Family pedigree analysis with genetic counselling"],"redFlags":["Subacute sensory neuronopathy raising the possibility of a paraneoplastic cause.","Severe gastrointestinal symptoms with weight loss or dehydration.","Rapid neurological progression atypical for a hereditary ataxia.","Frequent falls resulting from profound proprioceptive loss."],"prognosis":"Course is variable but generally slowly progressive, with disability arising chiefly from sensory ataxia; lifespan is usually preserved.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing an acquired sensory neuronopathy as this hereditary disorder, or vice versa.","Overlooking gastrointestinal symptoms as a diagnostic clue in affected families.","Assuming a standard repeat-expansion panel will detect the causative variant.","Failing to distinguish it from the sensory-predominant SCA4 without molecular confirmation."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so centre care on sensory-ataxia rehabilitation with proprioceptive retraining, physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language therapy","Control neuropathic pain from the severe large-fibre sensory neuronopathy with a gabapentinoid or tricyclic antidepressant","Institute falls prevention with walking aids and home hazard assessment given profound proprioceptive loss and areflexia","Manage the gastrointestinal features with antiemetics for recurrent vomiting, dietetic support and gastroenterology evaluation for the abdominal symptoms","Exclude treatable acquired sensory neuronopathy mimics (paraneoplastic, Sjogren syndrome, and vitamin B12 or B6 disturbance)","Provide multidisciplinary follow-up across neurology, gastroenterology and allied health","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing for the autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics reference texts on autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias","Standard neurology textbook chapters on inherited ataxias","Consensus reviews of hereditary ataxias with sensory neuronopathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_26":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 26","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA26","EEF2-related ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant, relatively pure cerebellar ataxia caused by a heterozygous missense variant in EEF2 (eukaryotic translation elongation factor 2) on chromosome 19p13.3.","Fits the Harding autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia type III pattern — a predominantly cerebellar syndrome with few extracerebellar signs.","Adult onset with slowly progressive gait ataxia, limb dysmetria and dysarthria.","Oculomotor findings include gaze-evoked nystagmus and irregular or saccadic (broken) smooth pursuit.","Originally described in a large North American family of Norwegian ancestry.","Imaging shows relatively isolated cerebellar cortical atrophy with preservation of the brainstem.","Because the phenotype overlaps other pure cerebellar subtypes, gene-specific or panel testing is required for diagnosis.","It is not a repeat-expansion disorder, so genetic anticipation is not expected."],"pathophysiology":"SCA26 is caused by a heterozygous missense variant in EEF2, which encodes eukaryotic elongation factor 2 — a GTP-binding protein that catalyses the ribosomal translocation step of messenger RNA translation, advancing the ribosome along the transcript during the elongation phase of protein synthesis. The disease-associated substitution is thought to subtly impair the fidelity and efficiency of translational elongation, a stress to which cerebellar Purkinje neurons are particularly vulnerable, producing selective cerebellar cortical degeneration and a comparatively pure ataxic phenotype. The mechanism is a dominant germline effect and does not involve any nucleotide repeat.","additionalDifferentials":["SCA6 (CACNA1A) — late-onset, largely pure cerebellar ataxia","SCA5 (SPTBN2) — dominant pure cerebellar ataxia, sometimes early onset","SCA11 (TTBK2) — relatively benign pure cerebellar ataxia","SCA14 (PRKCG) — slowly progressive cerebellar ataxia, occasionally with myoclonus","SCA15/16 (ITPR1) — very slowly progressive pure cerebellar ataxia","SCA31 (BEAN1 intronic repeat) — late-onset pure cerebellar ataxia","Acquired late-onset cerebellar ataxia (alcohol-related, coeliac, paraneoplastic, vitamin deficiency)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain to confirm relatively isolated cerebellar atrophy","Structured oculomotor examination documenting nystagmus and pursuit abnormalities","Hereditary-ataxia gene panel or exome including EEF2","Dedicated repeat-expansion testing to exclude subtypes such as SCA6 and SCA31","Screen for acquired cerebellar causes, including coeliac and paraneoplastic serology and relevant vitamin levels","Family segregation analysis and genetic counselling"],"redFlags":["Prominent non-cerebellar signs (pyramidal, extrapyramidal or sensory) pointing to a different subtype or diagnosis","Unexpectedly rapid progression","Early cognitive or bulbar failure","Systemic features suggesting a paraneoplastic or metabolic cause"],"prognosis":"The disorder progresses slowly over many years to decades and tends to follow a relatively benign course compared with the multisystem ataxias, with independent ambulation often preserved for a prolonged period.","pitfalls":["Labelling the patient as idiopathic late-onset cerebellar ataxia without pursuing genetic testing","Assuming a repeat-expansion mechanism and omitting sequencing-based analysis","Attributing the ataxia to alcohol or another acquired cause without reviewing the family history","Overlooking a positive family history when the phenotype is mild and slowly evolving"],"management":["With no disease-modifying therapy for this EEF2-related ataxia, anchor care on physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids for progressive gait ataxia and limb dysmetria","Monitor for bulbar dysfunction with periodic swallow assessment as disease advances","Address visual symptoms from gaze-evoked nystagmus and broken smooth pursuit with ophthalmology or orthoptic input","Provide multidisciplinary ataxia clinic follow-up, reflecting the relatively benign, predominantly cerebellar course","Offer genetic counselling with cascade testing, noting this is a point-mutation disorder without anticipation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Standard hereditary ataxia genetics reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_27":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 27 (FGF14)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA27","SCA27A","SCA27B","FGF14-related ataxia","FGF14 GAA repeat late-onset ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["SCA27 involves the FGF14 gene, encoding fibroblast growth factor 14, an intracellular protein that regulates neuronal ion channels and excitability, and two distinct mechanisms are now recognised","The point-mutation form (SCA27A) tends to present earlier, in childhood or young adulthood, with tremor, cerebellar signs and cognitive, behavioural or psychiatric features","The recently described repeat-expansion form (SCA27B), caused by an intronic GAA expansion, has emerged as one of the commonest causes of late-onset hereditary ataxia","SCA27B typically presents in older adults, frequently with episodic symptoms at onset, downbeat nystagmus and slowly progressive cerebellar ataxia","Some patients with SCA27B respond symptomatically to acetazolamide or an aminopyridine, which is a useful therapeutic and diagnostic clue","Diagnosis of SCA27B requires targeted sizing of the FGF14 GAA repeat by repeat-primed PCR or long-read methods, as standard panels and exome miss it, whereas SCA27A needs sequencing","Brain MRI commonly shows cerebellar atrophy, though it may be mild or unremarkable early on","FGF14 testing should now be considered in unexplained late-onset or episodic ataxia, particularly with downbeat nystagmus"],"pathophysiology":"SCA27 is caused by disruption of FGF14, which encodes an intracellular fibroblast growth factor that binds voltage-gated sodium channels at the axon initial segment and tunes neuronal firing, especially in Purkinje cells. Loss-of-function point mutations (SCA27A) reduce FGF14 activity, while the intronic GAA repeat expansion (SCA27B) is thought to impair transcription and lower FGF14 expression; both diminish channel regulation. The resulting Purkinje cell dysfunction and degeneration produce cerebellar ataxia and downbeat nystagmus, with the point-mutation form additionally causing tremor and neuropsychiatric features.","additionalDifferentials":["Episodic ataxia type 2 (CACNA1A), which also causes downbeat nystagmus and responds to acetazolamide","Other late-onset sporadic or hereditary cerebellar ataxias","RFC1-related CANVAS and late-onset ataxia with neuropathy","Multiple system atrophy, cerebellar type","Other repeat-expansion spinocerebellar ataxias","Structural causes of downbeat nystagmus at the craniocervical junction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted sizing of the FGF14 GAA repeat by repeat-primed PCR or long-read sequencing for SCA27B","FGF14 sequencing to detect point mutations in suspected SCA27A","Brain MRI to assess cerebellar atrophy and exclude craniocervical structural lesions","Testing for CACNA1A (episodic ataxia type 2) and RFC1 (CANVAS) where features overlap","Screening for acquired ataxia (thyroid function, vitamins B12 and E, coeliac serology)","A monitored therapeutic trial of acetazolamide where episodic symptoms are prominent"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression or an atypical course suggesting an acquired or paraneoplastic cause","A structural lesion at the craniocervical junction as an alternative cause of downbeat nystagmus","Prominent autonomic failure pointing to multiple system atrophy","Dysphagia with aspiration risk requiring swallow assessment"],"prognosis":"SCA27B is generally slowly progressive and many patients remain ambulant for years, with a proportion gaining symptomatic benefit from acetazolamide; SCA27A has a more variable course with earlier onset and additional tremor and neuropsychiatric features, but overall life expectancy is often relatively preserved.","pitfalls":["Missing the GAA expansion because standard gene panels and exome do not detect it","Failing to consider FGF14 in older adults with unexplained or episodic ataxia","Overlooking a therapeutic trial of acetazolamide when episodic features are present","Mislabelling episodic attacks as functional or as isolated vestibular disease","Not counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance and the reduced penetrance seen with the repeat expansion"],"management":["In the repeat-expansion form (SCA27B), offer a monitored trial of acetazolamide or an aminopyridine (fampridine), which can give symptomatic benefit and supports the diagnosis","Provide neurorehabilitation for gait and limb ataxia with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language therapy","Treat the tremor of the point-mutation form (SCA27A) with an anti-tremor agent, and support the associated cognitive, behavioural or psychiatric features","Manage downbeat nystagmus and oscillopsia, where an aminopyridine may help, with ophthalmology input","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids","Provide multidisciplinary ataxia clinic follow-up","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing, distinguishing the FGF14 GAA-repeat mechanism of SCA27B from point mutations in SCA27A"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":["episodic_ataxia_type_1"]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_28":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 28 (AFG3L2)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA28","AFG3L2-related ataxia","m-AAA protease ataxia","autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia type SCA28"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant, early-onset and slowly progressive cerebellar ataxia caused mostly by missense variants in AFG3L2","AFG3L2 is a subunit of the mitochondrial m-AAA protease, so SCA28 is fundamentally a disorder of mitochondrial protein quality control","A distinguishing clinical clue is ophthalmoparesis, particularly limitation of upgaze, often accompanied by ptosis","Core features include gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria and nystagmus, with pyramidal signs such as hyperreflexia in many patients","Onset is frequently in childhood or early adulthood, earlier than many other dominant spinocerebellar ataxias","Diagnosis is by gene sequencing, since repeat-expansion assays are normal","Brain MRI shows cerebellar atrophy that is often vermian-predominant","Biallelic AFG3L2 variants cause a distinct severe recessive spastic ataxia and neurodevelopmental phenotype, in contrast to the dominant SCA28"],"pathophysiology":"SCA28 is caused by dominant variants in AFG3L2, which encodes a subunit of the m-AAA protease in the inner mitochondrial membrane. This protease degrades misfolded proteins and processes substrates that govern mitochondrial function and calcium handling. Pathogenic variants, often clustered in the proteolytic domain, impair protease activity and disturb mitochondrial protein homeostasis and calcium regulation. Purkinje cells are especially dependent on this quality-control system, so its failure leads to progressive Purkinje cell degeneration, producing cerebellar ataxia with the characteristic ophthalmoparesis.","additionalDifferentials":["Other early-onset autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxias (SCA5, SCA14)","Mitochondrial ataxias and chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia, given the ophthalmoparesis","SPG7-related spastic ataxia, involving the partner protease subunit paraplegin","Friedreich ataxia and other early-onset ataxias","Congenital and childhood cerebellar ataxias","Sporadic adult-onset cerebellar ataxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["AFG3L2 sequencing within an ataxia gene panel or exome","Brain MRI to document cerebellar (often vermian) atrophy","Testing for common repeat-expansion SCAs to exclude commoner causes","Evaluation for a mitochondrial disorder or external ophthalmoplegia given eye-movement involvement","SPG7 testing where a spastic-ataxic overlap is present","Detailed family history to establish dominant transmission"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression or severe bulbar and respiratory involvement suggesting an alternative cause","A severe childhood presentation with marked spasticity raising the possibility of recessive AFG3L2 disease","New focal neurological signs indicating a structural lesion","Dysphagia with aspiration risk requiring swallow assessment"],"prognosis":"SCA28 is slowly progressive, and many patients retain independent walking for a prolonged period; life expectancy is often relatively preserved in the dominant form, with disability determined by cumulative cerebellar dysfunction, ophthalmoparesis and any bulbar involvement.","pitfalls":["Overlooking ophthalmoparesis, a useful clue that helps distinguish SCA28 from other dominant ataxias","Expecting a repeat expansion and missing the point mutation when common panels are negative","Confusing dominant SCA28 with the distinct severe recessive AFG3L2 phenotype","Failing to consider the related SPG7 protease disorder in spastic-ataxic presentations","Not providing genetic counselling given dominant inheritance and variable expressivity"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists for this AFG3L2-related ataxia, so centre care on physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy","Manage the ophthalmoparesis, ptosis and resulting diplopia with orthoptic assessment, prisms, and ptosis props or lid surgery where needed","Treat pyramidal spasticity with physiotherapy and an antispasticity agent such as baclofen","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids for gait and limb ataxia","Monitor for bulbar dysfunction with periodic swallow assessment","Provide multidisciplinary ataxia clinic follow-up","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing for the autosomal dominant, often early-onset disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":["spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_5","spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_14"]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_29":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 29","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA29","ITPR1-related congenital ataxia","Congenital non-progressive cerebellar ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant, typically congenital and non-progressive (or very slowly progressive) cerebellar ataxia caused by heterozygous ITPR1 variants.","Presents in infancy with hypotonia and delayed motor milestones, later evolving into gait ataxia, dysarthria and titubation.","Nystagmus and other oculomotor abnormalities and intention tremor are frequent; learning difficulties or cognitive impairment occur in a subset.","Many cases arise de novo, so a negative family history does not exclude the diagnosis.","ITPR1 is allelic with SCA15 (usually adult-onset progressive ataxia from deletions/loss of function) and with Gillespie syndrome (ataxia with aniridia).","MRI commonly shows cerebellar atrophy or hypoplasia, particularly of the vermis, but may be normal in early infancy.","A static course distinguishes it from progressive degenerative ataxias and should prompt genetic testing rather than a label of cerebral palsy."],"pathophysiology":"SCA29 is caused by pathogenic variants in ITPR1, which encodes the inositol 1,4,5-trisphosphate receptor type 1 (IP3R1), a large tetrameric intracellular calcium-release channel located on the endoplasmic reticulum membrane and expressed at very high levels in cerebellar Purkinje cells. IP3R1 releases calcium from endoplasmic reticulum stores in response to IP3 generated downstream of metabotropic glutamate receptor signalling, and this calcium flux is central to Purkinje cell dendritic function, synaptic plasticity (long-term depression) and cell survival. Missense variants, which often cluster in the IP3-binding or channel-forming domains, act through dominant-negative or loss-of-function mechanisms that disrupt calcium homeostasis and Purkinje cell signalling, producing the congenital non-progressive phenotype. By contrast, larger heterozygous deletions and haploinsufficiency of ITPR1 tend to cause the later-onset progressive SCA15 phenotype, while particular variant combinations with additional ocular features underlie Gillespie syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["SCA15 (ITPR1 loss of function) — allelic and later-onset","Gillespie syndrome (ITPR1, ataxia with aniridia)","Other early-onset genetic ataxias (CACNA1A, CACNA1G, KCNC3, EBF3)","Joubert syndrome and related cerebellar malformations (molar tooth sign)","Cerebellar hypoplasia from perinatal injury or extreme prematurity","Congenital disorders of glycosylation with cerebellar involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["ITPR1 sequencing within an early-onset ataxia panel, with deletion/duplication analysis (MLPA or chromosomal microarray)","Brain MRI to assess the cerebellar vermis and exclude the molar tooth sign of Joubert syndrome","Developmental and neuropsychological assessment","Ophthalmology review for nystagmus and to exclude the aniridia of Gillespie syndrome","Metabolic screen including transferrin isoform (glycosylation) studies to exclude mimics","Parental testing to determine de novo status and define recurrence risk"],"redFlags":["Clear clinical or radiological progression, which should prompt reconsideration or a search for a second diagnosis","Aniridia or iris hypoplasia suggesting Gillespie syndrome","Molar tooth sign or episodic abnormal breathing suggesting Joubert syndrome"],"prognosis":"Usually static or very slowly progressive; most children eventually achieve independent walking, albeit delayed, and have a normal life expectancy. Ataxia and dysarthria persist and cognition is variably affected.","pitfalls":["Labelling a static congenital ataxia as cerebral palsy and not pursuing genetic testing","Assuming autosomal dominant inheritance requires an affected parent when many cases are de novo","Missing the ITPR1 diagnosis by using expansion-only spinocerebellar ataxia panels","Overlooking aniridia that would reclassify the case as Gillespie syndrome"],"management":["With no disease-modifying therapy, provide early developmental intervention and neurorehabilitation: physiotherapy for hypotonia and motor delay, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy","Arrange educational support and neuropsychological input for the learning difficulties and cognitive impairment seen in a subset","Obtain ophthalmology review for nystagmus and to exclude the aniridia of the allelic Gillespie syndrome","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids as the child achieves delayed independent walking","Coordinate paediatric multidisciplinary follow-up","Offer genetic counselling with parental testing to determine de novo status and define recurrence risk, remembering many cases are de novo so a negative family history does not exclude the diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric neurogenetics references","Hereditary and congenital ataxia reviews","Neurogenetics textbooks"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_31":{"name":"Spinocerebellar Ataxia Type 31","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia & spastic paraplegia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA31","BEAN1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["SCA31 is an autosomal dominant, late-onset, pure cerebellar ataxia caused by an intronic pentanucleotide repeat insertion containing (TGGAA)n within a region shared by the BEAN1 and TK2 genes.","It is one of the commonest dominant ataxias in Japan, particularly among older adults, but is rare in most other populations.","Onset is characteristically late, often in or after later middle age, with slowly progressive gait ataxia, limb ataxia and dysarthria.","Sensorineural hearing impairment and reduced distal vibration sense are recognised additional features in some patients.","Horizontal gaze-evoked nystagmus and other cerebellar eye signs are typical, whereas marked extracerebellar disease is unusual.","The disease-specific insertion carries (TGGAA)n repeats absent from normal chromosomes; it is this TGGAA element, not the benign flanking pentanucleotides, that is pathogenic.","MRI shows predominantly cerebellar, especially vermian, atrophy with relative brainstem sparing."],"pathophysiology":"SCA31 is caused by a long, complex pentanucleotide repeat insertion — comprising (TGGAA)n together with (TAGAA)n, (TAAAA)n and mixed components — located in an intron shared, in opposite orientations, by BEAN1 (brain-expressed, associated with NEDD4, 1) and TK2 (thymidine kinase 2) on chromosome 16q. The disease-specific (TGGAA)n component is transcribed into RNA that forms nuclear RNA foci and sequesters RNA-binding and splicing proteins, and may undergo repeat-associated non-AUG translation into pentapeptide-repeat proteins; these gain-of-function RNA and protein toxicities preferentially damage cerebellar Purkinje cells. Normal individuals lack the TGGAA insertion, carrying only benign flanking pentanucleotide stretches, which is why the presence of the TGGAA element is diagnostic rather than a simple change in repeat length.","additionalDifferentials":["SCA6 (CACNA1A) — late-onset, slowly progressive pure cerebellar ataxia and a key mimic","SCA36 (NOP56) — late-onset ataxia that may include hearing loss and motor-neuron features","SCA15/16 (ITPR1) — very slowly progressive pure cerebellar ataxia","SCA11 (TTBK2) — mild pure cerebellar ataxia","Sporadic late-onset cerebellar ataxia and the cerebellar variant of multiple system atrophy","Acquired ataxias in older adults, including alcohol-related, paraneoplastic and superficial siderosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Specialised insertion-specific or Southern-blot-based testing for the SCA31 TGGAA insertion, as standard repeat panels may not detect it","Exclusion of the common repeat-expansion ataxias, especially SCA6 in this age group","Brain MRI to document cerebellar atrophy","Pure-tone audiometry where hearing impairment is suspected","Nerve conduction and vibration testing where a sensory neuropathy is suspected","Family pedigree analysis and genetic counselling with cascade testing"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression, prominent autonomic failure or early falls suggest the cerebellar variant of multiple system atrophy rather than SCA31.","Prominent motor-neuron features such as fasciculations and marked wasting point towards SCA36 or an alternative diagnosis.","Acute or subacute onset in an older adult mandates evaluation for an acquired cause."],"prognosis":"SCA31 is slowly progressive and relatively benign; gait deteriorates gradually over many years but life expectancy is generally preserved, with walking aids needed only later.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis because routine expansion panels do not include the specific SCA31 TGGAA insertion assay.","Ascribing late-onset ataxia in an older patient to ageing, alcohol or cerebrovascular disease without considering a dominant genetic cause.","Overlooking a family history obscured by late onset, with older affected relatives dying of unrelated causes.","Forgetting to look for associated sensorineural hearing loss."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so anchor care on physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Arrange audiological assessment and hearing aids for the associated sensorineural hearing impairment","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids, with attention to the reduced distal vibration sense","Monitor for bulbar dysfunction with periodic swallow assessment","Provide multidisciplinary ataxia clinic follow-up, reflecting the relatively benign, slowly progressive course","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing using insertion-specific assays for the TGGAA repeat"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics reference texts","Clinical neurology textbook — hereditary ataxias","Peer-reviewed hereditary ataxia reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_34":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 34","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA34","ELOVL4-related ataxia","Giroux-Barbeau syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia caused by heterozygous variants in ELOVL4 (elongation of very-long-chain fatty acids protein 4) on chromosome 6q14.","Classically pairs cerebellar ataxia with erythrokeratodermia — figurate hyperkeratotic, papulosquamous skin plaques — as originally described in the French-Canadian Giroux-Barbeau kindred.","Skin lesions may appear in childhood and precede the neurological syndrome by years, and can wax and wane; however, some ELOVL4 ataxia families have no cutaneous involvement.","Neurologically there is adult-onset, slowly progressive gait and limb ataxia with dysarthria and nystagmus.","Allelic disorders are important: different ELOVL4 variants cause autosomal dominant Stargardt-like macular dystrophy, while biallelic variants cause a severe neuro-ichthyosis with spasticity and seizures.","ELOVL4 is essential for synthesis of very-long-chain fatty acids in the epidermal barrier, retina and brain, which explains the cutaneous and retinal associations.","Combined dermatological review with skin biopsy and neurogenetic testing strengthens the diagnosis.","Retinal assessment is worthwhile given the gene's central role in photoreceptor lipid metabolism."],"pathophysiology":"SCA34 results from heterozygous variants in ELOVL4, which encodes an endoplasmic-reticulum membrane fatty-acid elongase that catalyses a rate-limiting step in the synthesis of very-long-chain fatty acids and related sphingolipids — molecules abundant in the epidermal permeability barrier, retinal photoreceptors and the brain. The dominant ataxia variants are thought to act through a dominant-negative or misfolding mechanism, with mutant protein retained in the endoplasmic reticulum and impairing very-long-chain fatty-acid synthesis in Purkinje neurons and keratinocytes; this shared tissue dependence accounts for the pairing of progressive cerebellar degeneration with erythrokeratodermia. Distinct mutational mechanisms in the same gene underlie the dominant macular dystrophy and the recessive ichthyosis phenotypes, illustrating marked allelic heterogeneity.","additionalDifferentials":["Other dominant sibling subtypes with pure or near-pure cerebellar ataxia (for example SCA5, SCA6, SCA14)","SCA7 — dominant ataxia distinguished by pigmentary maculopathy and visual loss","Sjogren-Larsson syndrome — ichthyosis with spastic paraparesis and a lipid-metabolism defect","Erythrokeratodermia variabilis from connexin (GJB) gene defects, without ataxia","Refsum disease and other peroxisomal or lipid-storage ataxias","Biallelic ELOVL4 neuro-ichthyosis — the far more severe recessive counterpart"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain to demonstrate cerebellar atrophy","Dermatological assessment with skin biopsy of representative plaques","ELOVL4 sequencing or a hereditary-ataxia gene panel","Ophthalmological and retinal examination to detect macular involvement","Plasma very-long-chain fatty acid and phytanic acid screen to exclude peroxisomal mimics","Genetic counselling and family segregation study"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive ataxia or spasticity raising concern for the severe biallelic phenotype","Progressive visual loss or maculopathy","Widespread ichthyosiform erythroderma in an infant, suggesting recessive disease","New-onset seizures"],"prognosis":"The dominant ataxia is slowly progressive over decades with generally preserved life expectancy, and the associated skin lesions are chronic but not life-threatening; the recessive, biallelic form is by contrast severe and disabling from early life.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the skin signs or dismissing them as an unrelated primary dermatosis","Assuming all ELOVL4 disease is identical, when ataxia, macular dystrophy and ichthyosis carry different genotype-phenotype correlations","Missing the diagnosis in the subset of families without any cutaneous features","Neglecting retinal examination and thereby missing coexistent maculopathy"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists for the ataxia, so centre care on physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy","Manage the erythrokeratodermia dermatologically with emollients, topical keratolytics and topical or oral retinoids under dermatology","Arrange ophthalmological and retinal surveillance for macular involvement, given the allelic Stargardt-like macular dystrophy","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids for gait and limb ataxia","Monitor for bulbar dysfunction with periodic swallow assessment","Coordinate multidisciplinary follow-up across neurology, dermatology and ophthalmology","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing, counselling that biallelic ELOVL4 variants cause a severe recessive neuro-ichthyosis with spasticity and seizures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Standard hereditary ataxia genetics reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_35":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 35 (TGM6)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA35","TGM6-related ataxia","transglutaminase 6 ataxia","autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia type SCA35"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant, slowly progressive cerebellar ataxia caused by pathogenic variants in TGM6, which encodes transglutaminase 6, an enzyme expressed in neurons","Typically presents in adulthood with gait unsteadiness and dysarthria, often accompanied by a prominent postural and kinetic tremor, giving a recognisable tremor-ataxia phenotype","Limb incoordination, saccadic pursuit and gaze-evoked nystagmus are common, and some patients develop pyramidal signs with hyperreflexia and extensor plantar responses","Focal or task-specific features such as torticollis, spasmodic dysphonia or writer's cramp have been reported and can precede overt ataxia","Brain MRI generally shows cerebellar atrophy, sometimes with mild brainstem involvement, but imaging is a supportive rather than a defining feature","Diagnosis rests on molecular confirmation of a TGM6 variant against a background of dominant inheritance; repeat-expansion SCAs should be excluded first as they are far commoner","There is no disease-modifying therapy; care is supportive and multidisciplinary, centred on physiotherapy, speech and language therapy, gait aids and tremor management"],"pathophysiology":"SCA35 arises from heterozygous pathogenic variants in TGM6, which encodes transglutaminase 6, a calcium-dependent enzyme that catalyses cross-linking of glutamine and lysine residues in neuronal proteins. Loss of normal enzyme function is thought to disturb cytoskeletal cross-linking and protein homeostasis in Purkinje cells and other cerebellar neurons, leading to progressive Purkinje cell dysfunction and loss. The resulting cerebellar degeneration produces the ataxia, dysarthria and eye-movement abnormalities, while variable involvement of corticospinal and extrapyramidal pathways accounts for the pyramidal signs and dystonic or tremor features seen in some families.","additionalDifferentials":["Repeat-expansion spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA1, 2, 3, 6, 7 and related types)","Spinocerebellar ataxia type 36 and other tremor-predominant dominant ataxias","Fragile X-associated tremor/ataxia syndrome (FXTAS) in older men","Multiple system atrophy, cerebellar type","Autoimmune or gluten-related cerebellar ataxia (including anti-transglutaminase associations)","Alcohol-related or toxic cerebellar degeneration","Idiopathic late-onset (sporadic) cerebellar ataxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to document cerebellar (and any brainstem) atrophy and exclude structural or acquired causes","Targeted molecular testing for common repeat-expansion SCAs before proceeding to TGM6 analysis","Next-generation sequencing ataxia gene panel or exome including TGM6","Detailed three-generation family history to establish autosomal dominant transmission","Screening for acquired ataxia causes (thyroid function, vitamin B12 and E, coeliac serology, alcohol history)","Nerve conduction studies where a sensory neuropathy or alternative diagnosis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression or early severe bulbar dysfunction, which should prompt reconsideration of an acquired or paraneoplastic cause","Prominent autonomic failure suggesting multiple system atrophy rather than a hereditary ataxia","Cognitive decline or seizures pointing to a broader neurodegenerative or metabolic process","Dysphagia with recurrent aspiration requiring swallow assessment and airway protection"],"prognosis":"SCA35 is slowly progressive over years to decades; many patients retain independent walking for a prolonged period, but cumulative cerebellar and pyramidal involvement can eventually lead to significant disability, dysarthria and dysphagia. Life expectancy is often relatively preserved, with prognosis influenced by bulbar involvement and fall-related complications.","pitfalls":["Assuming a repeat-expansion SCA and stopping testing when common panels are negative, thereby missing a TGM6 variant","Overlooking the tremor or focal dystonic presentation and labelling the patient as essential tremor or a functional disorder","Attributing raised anti-transglutaminase antibodies solely to coeliac disease without considering the gene-antibody overlap in this ataxia","Interpreting isolated cerebellar atrophy on MRI as diagnostic when it is only supportive","Failing to offer genetic counselling regarding dominant inheritance and variable expressivity"],"management":["With no disease-modifying therapy for this TGM6-related ataxia, base care on physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy","Treat the prominent postural and kinetic tremor with a trial of an anti-tremor agent such as a beta-blocker","Use botulinum toxin injections for focal dystonic features such as cervical dystonia, spasmodic dysphonia or writer's cramp","Apply antispasticity measures (physiotherapy and agents such as baclofen) for pyramidal involvement","Institute falls prevention and mobility aids, with swallow surveillance for dysarthria and dysphagia","Provide multidisciplinary ataxia clinic follow-up","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing for the autosomal dominant transmission"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":["spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_36"]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_36":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 36 (NOP56)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA36","NOP56-related ataxia","Asidan","Costa da Morte ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant ataxia caused by an intronic GGCCTG hexanucleotide repeat expansion in NOP56, first characterised in Japanese (Asidan) and Galician (Costa da Morte) populations","Typical onset is in mid-to-late adulthood with slowly progressive gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria and a tremor-ataxia picture","A distinguishing feature is later development of motor neuron signs, notably tongue atrophy and fasciculations, with limb fasciculations and hyperreflexia, giving a cerebellar-plus lower-motor-neuron phenotype","Sensorineural hearing loss is a recognised associated feature and can help point towards the diagnosis","Brain MRI shows cerebellar atrophy, initially vermian, with later brainstem and cerebral atrophy as disease advances","Diagnosis requires repeat-primed PCR or equivalent to detect the NOP56 expansion, as it is missed by standard exome and small-panel sequencing","Management is supportive and multidisciplinary, including physiotherapy, speech and swallow support, hearing rehabilitation and surveillance for aspiration risk"],"pathophysiology":"SCA36 is caused by an expanded GGCCTG hexanucleotide repeat in the first intron of NOP56, a gene encoding a nucleolar protein involved in ribosomal RNA processing. The expansion is thought to act largely through a toxic RNA gain-of-function mechanism, in which repeat-containing transcripts form nuclear foci that sequester RNA-binding proteins and disturb RNA metabolism, alongside possible reduction of NOP56 expression. Purkinje cells and brainstem motor neurons are particularly vulnerable, producing the combination of progressive cerebellar degeneration and lower motor neuron loss that characterises the disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Other repeat-expansion spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA1, 2, 3, 6, 8, 10)","Spinocerebellar ataxia type 35 and TGM6-related tremor-ataxia","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis when motor neuron signs dominate","Fragile X-associated tremor/ataxia syndrome","Multiple system atrophy, cerebellar type","Kennedy disease (spinobulbar muscular atrophy) with bulbar and lower motor neuron features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repeat-primed PCR (or Southern blot) for the NOP56 hexanucleotide expansion","Brain MRI to characterise the pattern and progression of cerebellar and brainstem atrophy","Audiometry to detect associated sensorineural hearing loss","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies to document motor neuron involvement","Exclusion of common repeat-expansion SCAs before or alongside NOP56 testing","Detailed pedigree analysis to confirm dominant transmission and population origin"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive bulbar weakness with dysphagia and aspiration risk","Respiratory compromise from advancing motor neuron involvement","Marked weight loss and malnutrition secondary to swallowing difficulty","Recurrent falls with injury as gait ataxia worsens"],"prognosis":"SCA36 progresses slowly, often over one to two decades, with gait and speech becoming increasingly affected; the later addition of motor neuron features and bulbar involvement contributes to disability and can shorten survival through aspiration and respiratory complications. Many patients remain ambulant for years before requiring aids.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis because the intronic repeat expansion is not detected by exome or standard gene panels","Misdiagnosing the disorder as motor neuron disease when lower motor neuron signs appear before ataxia is recognised","Overlooking associated hearing loss, which is a useful diagnostic clue","Failing to arrange swallow assessment despite emerging bulbar signs","Not providing genetic counselling for at-risk relatives given dominant inheritance"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so anchor care on physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy","Monitor and manage the lower-motor-neuron and bulbar involvement (tongue and limb wasting and fasciculations) with a motor-neurone-disease multidisciplinary team","Provide proactive dysphagia management with swallow assessment, dietitian input and gastrostomy feeding when nutrition or aspiration is threatened","Monitor respiratory function and institute non-invasive ventilation for respiratory insufficiency, a determinant of survival","Arrange audiology and hearing aids for the associated sensorineural hearing loss","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing using repeat-primed PCR for the NOP56 hexanucleotide expansion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":["spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_35"]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_37":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 37","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA37","DAB1-related ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia with a distinctive early sign: abnormal vertical eye movements, including dysmetric or slowed vertical saccades and impaired vertical pursuit, which may precede overt ataxia.","Caused by an unstable intronic pentanucleotide repeat insertion — an ATTTC insertion embedded within a normally present ATTTT array — in the DAB1 gene on chromosome 1p32.","DAB1 encodes Disabled-1, a key intracellular adaptor of the Reelin signalling pathway that governs neuronal migration and positioning.","Onset is usually in adult life, with falls, gait ataxia and dysarthria.","The disorder was originally described in families from Spain.","Repeat instability and anticipation across generations are features, correlating with the size of the inserted ATTTC motif.","Beyond the early vertical gaze disturbance the syndrome is relatively pure cerebellar, making the eye-movement abnormality the key clinical clue."],"pathophysiology":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 37 is an autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia caused by an unstable pentanucleotide repeat insertion within a regulatory intron of DAB1 at chromosome 1p32 — specifically an ATTTC insertion embedded within a polymorphic ATTTT array that is non-pathogenic when the ATTTC motif is absent. DAB1 encodes Disabled-1, an essential intracellular adaptor of the Reelin signalling pathway that directs neuronal migration and positioning; the inserted repeat is thought to alter DAB1 expression and splicing and to confer toxicity, leading to preferential cerebellar degeneration, notably of Purkinje cells. The disorder characteristically produces early abnormal vertical eye movements followed by a largely pure progressive cerebellar ataxia, and it shows repeat instability with anticipation across generations, placing it among the non-B DNA insertion repeat ataxias.","additionalDifferentials":["SCA6 and other relatively pure autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxias","SCA5, SCA11 and SCA26 — further pure dominant cerebellar ataxias","Progressive supranuclear palsy, which causes a vertical gaze abnormality that can mimic the early eye-movement sign","Niemann-Pick disease type C, characterised by vertical supranuclear gaze palsy","SCA31 and SCA4 at chromosome 16q, other repeat-related ataxias","Other ATTTC-insertion repeat disorders such as familial adult myoclonic epilepsy and SCA10"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted molecular testing for the DAB1 ATTTC insertion using a specialised repeat assay","Video-oculography or eye-movement recording to document the vertical saccade and pursuit abnormality","MRI brain demonstrating cerebellar and vermian atrophy","Hereditary ataxia gene panel to exclude other dominant subtypes","Investigations to exclude Niemann-Pick disease type C and progressive supranuclear palsy where clinically relevant","Family pedigree analysis with genetic counselling addressing anticipation"],"redFlags":["Prominent vertical supranuclear gaze palsy with early falls raising progressive supranuclear palsy.","Cognitive or behavioural decline suggesting an alternative neurodegenerative diagnosis.","Organomegaly or young-onset systemic features pointing towards Niemann-Pick disease type C.","Rapid progression atypical for this slowly evolving hereditary ataxia."],"prognosis":"Slowly progressive over years to decades, with disability arising from a largely pure cerebellar syndrome; lifespan is usually preserved.","pitfalls":["Dismissing subtle early vertical eye-movement abnormalities that are the key diagnostic clue.","Missing the diagnosis because standard panels do not detect the ATTTC insertion within the ATTTT array.","Confusing the phenotype with progressive supranuclear palsy or Niemann-Pick disease type C.","Neglecting to counsel families about repeat instability and anticipation."],"management":["With no disease-modifying therapy for this DAB1-related ataxia, centre care on physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Institute an early falls-prevention programme and provide mobility aids, as falls are a presenting feature","Arrange orthoptic and ophthalmology input for the vertical saccade and pursuit abnormality and any oscillopsia or reading difficulty","Monitor for bulbar dysfunction with periodic swallow assessment","Provide multidisciplinary ataxia clinic follow-up","Offer genetic counselling addressing anticipation (larger ATTTC insertions correlate with earlier onset), with cascade testing by insertion-specific assay"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics reference texts on autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias","Standard neurology textbook chapters on inherited ataxias","Consensus reviews of non-repeat and insertion cerebellar ataxias"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_38":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 38","system":"Neurology / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA38","ELOVL5-related ataxia","Spinocerebellar ataxia 38"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxia caused by missense mutations in ELOVL5, which encodes a fatty acid elongase (elongation of very long chain fatty acids protein 5).","Unlike the common repeat-expansion ataxias, it results from point mutations, so pronounced anticipation is not a feature.","ELOVL5 elongates polyunsaturated fatty acids, and affected individuals show reduced serum docosahexaenoic acid (DHA).","Typically presents in the fourth decade with slowly progressive gait ataxia and gaze-evoked nystagmus.","Hyposmia (a reduced sense of smell) is a distinctive and relatively early associated feature that can aid recognition.","Later features include dysarthria, a peripheral axonal sensory neuropathy, pes cavus and sometimes hearing loss.","MRI shows cerebellar atrophy, often most marked in the vermis, within an otherwise relatively pure cerebellar syndrome.","Small studies suggest oral DHA supplementation may offer some clinical and imaging benefit, mirroring the underlying lipid biology."],"pathophysiology":"ELOVL5 encodes one of the endoplasmic reticulum fatty acid elongases that catalyse the rate-limiting condensation step in elongating long-chain polyunsaturated fatty acids, feeding synthesis of omega-3 and omega-6 species such as docosahexaenoic and arachidonic acids. Pathogenic missense variants are thought to impair enzyme function and lead to intracellular retention of the mutant protein and altered fatty acid handling, reducing downstream long-chain polyunsaturated fatty acids that are essential for neuronal membrane composition and Purkinje cell function. The resulting disturbance of cerebellar lipid homeostasis, particularly in Purkinje cells, produces slowly progressive cerebellar degeneration, while involvement of olfactory and peripheral sensory pathways accounts for the characteristic hyposmia and the axonal sensory neuropathy. A reduction in serum DHA provides a supportive biochemical correlate of the enzymatic defect.","additionalDifferentials":["Other autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias, including the relatively pure cerebellar forms such as SCA6","Repeat-expansion polyglutamine ataxias (for example SCA1, SCA2, SCA3)","Multiple system atrophy, cerebellar type","Acquired cerebellar ataxias (alcohol-related, thiamine deficiency, paraneoplastic or immune-mediated)","Hereditary ataxias associated with sensory neuropathy","ELOVL4-related neurological disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["ELOVL5 molecular genetic testing, usually after excluding the common repeat-expansion SCAs","MRI brain demonstrating cerebellar and vermian atrophy","Nerve conduction studies to characterise the axonal sensory neuropathy","Formal olfactory testing to document hyposmia","Serum fatty acid profile showing reduced docosahexaenoic acid","Audiometry where hearing loss is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive ataxia or atypical features suggesting an acquired or paraneoplastic cause","Significant dysphagia or aspiration risk as the disease advances","Recurrent falls and loss of independent mobility requiring intervention"],"prognosis":"The disorder is slowly progressive over decades with a relatively pure cerebellar phenotype, and life expectancy is often near-normal. Ambulation and speech nonetheless decline over time, and no disease-modifying therapy is established.","pitfalls":["Ending the work-up after negative common repeat-expansion SCA panels and missing ELOVL5 point mutations","Overlooking hyposmia, which is a useful diagnostic clue","Misattributing the sensory neuropathy to an unrelated coincident cause","Expecting marked anticipation as seen in the polyglutamine spinocerebellar ataxias"],"management":["Offer docosahexaenoic acid (omega-3) supplementation, which replenishes the low serum DHA resulting from ELOVL5 dysfunction and has shown modest symptomatic benefit in early trials","Provide neurorehabilitation with physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Manage the axonal sensory neuropathy and pes cavus with podiatry, orthoses and supportive footwear, plus neuropathic pain control","Arrange audiology and hearing aids where sensorineural hearing loss develops","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids","Provide multidisciplinary ataxia clinic follow-up","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing, noting this is a point-mutation disorder with minimal anticipation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","GeneReviews — hereditary ataxia overview"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_4":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 4","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA4","ZFHX3-related ataxia","Hereditary ataxia with sensory neuropathy (16q22)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia distinguished by a prominent sensory axonal neuropathy, so areflexia and loss of vibration and joint-position sense accompany gait and limb ataxia.","Caused by an expanded GGC (polyglycine-encoding) repeat in ZFHX3 on chromosome 16q22.1 — the same cytogenetic band as SCA31, from which it must be separated by molecular testing.","Usually presents in mid-adult life with slowly progressive gait unsteadiness; the phenotype was originally delineated in a large Utah kindred of Scandinavian ancestry.","The neuropathy is length-dependent and axonal, with reduced or absent sensory nerve action potentials and relatively preserved motor conduction.","Pyramidal signs such as extensor plantar responses may coexist with the peripheral sensory loss, giving a mixed central-peripheral picture.","Oculomotor abnormalities, particularly gaze-evoked nystagmus, are common, whereas cognition is generally preserved.","Anticipation across generations may be observed, in keeping with an unstable expanded repeat."],"pathophysiology":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 4 is an autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disorder attributed to an expanded GGC (polyglycine-encoding) repeat within the ZFHX3 gene at chromosome 16q22.1. ZFHX3 encodes a large zinc-finger homeobox transcription factor, and the pathogenic expansion is thought to generate a toxic polyglycine-containing protein with intranuclear inclusions, driving degeneration of cerebellar Purkinje cells together with dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons and their central and peripheral projections. This combined cerebellar and sensory ganglionopathy explains the characteristic pairing of progressive ataxia with a length-dependent axonal sensory neuropathy. Although the locus lies in the same chromosomal band as SCA31, the two disorders arise from different repeat expansions in different genes and are clinically and genetically distinct.","additionalDifferentials":["SCA31 (shares the 16q22 region but tends to cause a purer, later-onset cerebellar ataxia)","SCA25 (dominant ataxia with prominent sensory neuronopathy, linked to PNPT1 on chromosome 2p)","SCA18 (sensorimotor neuropathy with ataxia, with added motor weakness and atrophy)","Friedreich ataxia (recessive, with sensory neuropathy, areflexia and cardiomyopathy)","CANVAS — cerebellar ataxia, neuropathy and vestibular areflexia syndrome (RFC1 biallelic expansion)","Acquired sensory neuronopathy (paraneoplastic anti-Hu, Sjogren-related ganglionopathy)","Vitamin E deficiency and ataxia with vitamin E deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted molecular testing for the ZFHX3 repeat expansion using repeat-primed or long-range assays","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to characterise the axonal sensory neuropathy","MRI brain demonstrating cerebellar and often vermian atrophy","Broader hereditary-ataxia or repeat-expansion gene panel to exclude sibling subtypes","Quantitative sensory testing and formal assessment of proprioception","Bloods to exclude acquired sensory neuropathy (vitamin B12, glucose and glycated haemoglobin, autoimmune and paraneoplastic screen)","Family pedigree analysis with genetic counselling where appropriate"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive ataxia or subacute sensory neuronopathy suggesting a paraneoplastic or autoimmune rather than hereditary cause.","New bulbar dysfunction with dysphagia and aspiration risk.","Severe proprioceptive loss producing frequent falls and injury.","Cardiac or autonomic symptoms that should prompt reconsideration of Friedreich ataxia."],"prognosis":"Typically slowly progressive over decades with lifespan preserved in most patients; disability arises from the combination of cerebellar incoordination and sensory loss, often leading to reliance on walking aids in later life.","pitfalls":["Attributing the ataxia solely to cerebellar disease and overlooking the defining sensory neuropathy.","Confusing SCA4 with SCA31 because both map to 16q22 — molecular confirmation of the specific gene is essential.","Assuming a negative common panel excludes the diagnosis when the ZFHX3 expansion requires a dedicated repeat assay.","Mislabelling an acquired sensory ganglionopathy as hereditary in the absence of a clear dominant family history."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so centre care on sensory-ataxia rehabilitation with proprioceptive retraining, physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language therapy","Manage the length-dependent axonal sensory neuropathy with neuropathic pain agents and orthoses or supportive footwear","Institute falls prevention with walking aids and home hazard assessment, given combined cerebellar incoordination and proprioceptive loss","Apply antispasticity measures for pyramidal signs such as extensor plantar responses","Exclude and treat coexisting acquired sensory neuropathy (vitamin B12 deficiency, diabetes, autoimmune or paraneoplastic causes)","Monitor for bulbar dysfunction with periodic swallow assessment","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing using the ZFHX3 repeat assay, distinguishing it from SCA31 in the same chromosomal band"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics reference texts on autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias","Standard neurology textbook chapters on inherited ataxias","Consensus reviews of repeat-expansion cerebellar ataxias"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_40":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 40","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA40","CCDC88C-related ataxia","DAPLE"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia caused by a heterozygous variant in CCDC88C, which encodes the scaffold protein DAPLE, on chromosome 14q32.","Adult-onset, slowly progressive gait and limb ataxia with dysarthria.","Distinctive pyramidal involvement: hyperreflexia, spasticity and extensor plantar responses accompany the cerebellar signs, unlike the purer cerebellar subtypes.","Oculomotor findings include saccadic (broken) smooth pursuit and gaze-evoked nystagmus, and intention tremor is common.","First reported in a Han Chinese family.","MRI shows cerebellar atrophy, sometimes with brainstem or pontine involvement.","Mechanistically linked to hyperactivation of the c-Jun N-terminal kinase (JNK) apoptotic pathway.","Biallelic loss-of-function CCDC88C variants cause a wholly distinct autosomal recessive congenital hydrocephalus, a different mechanism and phenotype from the dominant ataxia."],"pathophysiology":"SCA40 is caused by a heterozygous variant in CCDC88C, which encodes DAPLE (Dishevelled-associating protein with a high frequency of leucine residues), a scaffold protein that modulates Wnt and planar-cell-polarity signalling and the c-Jun N-terminal kinase (JNK) cascade. The dominant ataxia variant is thought to act as a gain of function that hyperactivates JNK signalling, promoting caspase-dependent apoptosis of cerebellar and brainstem neurons and producing progressive spinocerebellar degeneration with prominent pyramidal features. By contrast, biallelic loss-of-function variants in the same gene disrupt neural-tube and ependymal development to cause congenital hydrocephalus, underscoring the allele- and dose-dependent effects of CCDC88C. No nucleotide repeat expansion is involved.","additionalDifferentials":["SCA1 (ATXN1) — dominant ataxia in which pyramidal signs and spasticity are prominent","SCA3 / Machado-Joseph disease (ATXN3) — ataxia with spasticity, pyramidal signs and variable extrapyramidal features","SCA7 (ATXN7) — dominant ataxia, distinguished by retinopathy","Spastic ataxia and hereditary spastic paraplegia with cerebellar features, such as SPG7-related disease","Autosomal recessive spastic ataxia of Charlevoix-Saguenay (SACS)","Acquired posterior-fossa or demyelinating disease presenting with mixed cerebellar and pyramidal signs"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain to show cerebellar and possible pontine atrophy and exclude structural or demyelinating lesions","CCDC88C sequencing or a hereditary-ataxia gene panel / exome","Repeat-expansion panel to exclude the common repeat-based subtypes such as SCA1, SCA2, SCA3, SCA6 and SCA7","Nerve conduction studies if a peripheral component is suspected","Acquired-ataxia screen where the diagnosis is uncertain","Genetic counselling and family segregation analysis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive spasticity or bulbar failure","Features of raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus, particularly in infants or relatives with biallelic disease","Visual or optic symptoms suggesting an alternative subtype","Sphincter disturbance or a sensory level pointing to a spinal cord lesion"],"prognosis":"The disorder is slowly progressive over years to decades; superimposed spasticity may add to gait disability, but life expectancy is generally preserved.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the pyramidal-predominant picture for hereditary spastic paraplegia or multiple sclerosis","Failing to distinguish the dominant ataxia from the recessive congenital hydrocephalus phenotype of the same gene","Assuming a repeat-expansion mechanism and not proceeding to sequencing","Overlooking the family history when cerebellar and pyramidal signs coexist"],"management":["With no disease-modifying therapy for this CCDC88C-related ataxia, base care on physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy","Manage the prominent pyramidal spasticity with physiotherapy, stretching, an antispasticity agent such as baclofen, and botulinum toxin for focal spasticity","Treat intention tremor symptomatically where it impairs function","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids, as spasticity compounds the ataxic gait disability","Monitor for bulbar dysfunction with periodic swallow assessment","Provide multidisciplinary ataxia clinic follow-up","Offer genetic counselling and family segregation analysis for the autosomal dominant transmission"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Standard hereditary ataxia genetics reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_42":{"name":"Spinocerebellar Ataxia Type 42","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia & spastic paraplegia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA42","CACNA1G"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["SCA42 is an autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia caused by mutations in CACNA1G, which encodes the pore-forming alpha-1G subunit of the Cav3.1 low-voltage-activated (T-type) calcium channel.","A recurrent missense variant, p.Arg1715His, accounts for many reported families and tends to produce a relatively consistent, slowly progressive ataxia.","Typical adult-onset features are gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria and cerebellar eye signs such as gaze-evoked nystagmus and saccadic pursuit.","Pyramidal signs, tremor and sometimes peripheral sensory disturbance may accompany the cerebellar syndrome, so the phenotype ranges from nearly pure to mildly complex.","More disruptive, often de novo CACNA1G variants cause a distinct, severe early-onset neurodevelopmental disorder with cerebellar atrophy, contrasting sharply with the milder classic adult SCA42.","Cav3.1 T-type channels drive rhythmic low-threshold firing in cerebellar and thalamic circuits, so their dysfunction disturbs cerebellar output.","MRI shows cerebellar atrophy, sometimes with pontine involvement in more severe cases."],"pathophysiology":"SCA42 is caused by heterozygous mutations in CACNA1G on chromosome 17q, which encodes Cav3.1, the alpha-1G pore-forming subunit of a T-type (low-voltage-activated, transiently opening) voltage-gated calcium channel. Cav3.1 is strongly expressed in cerebellar Purkinje and granule cells, the inferior olive and the thalamus, where it underlies rhythmic burst firing and pacemaking. The recurrent p.Arg1715His substitution lies in a voltage-sensing segment and shifts the voltage dependence of channel activation, altering calcium influx and neuronal excitability through a change-of-function effect that disturbs cerebellar signalling and drives Purkinje cell dysfunction and cerebellar atrophy. More severe, frequently de novo variants cause larger functional disturbances and underlie the early-onset neurodevelopmental phenotype associated with the same gene.","additionalDifferentials":["SCA6 (CACNA1A) — a related calcium-channel dominant ataxia, slowly progressive and relatively pure","SCA15/16 (ITPR1) and SCA11 (TTBK2) — dominant pure cerebellar ataxias","SCA31 (BEAN1) — late-onset pure cerebellar ataxia","Episodic ataxia type 2 and other channelopathy-related ataxias","Congenital or early-onset cerebellar ataxias with developmental delay, for the severe de novo CACNA1G phenotype","Sporadic adult-onset cerebellar ataxia and the cerebellar variant of multiple system atrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CACNA1G sequencing by single-gene test, ataxia panel or exome, reporting the recurrent p.Arg1715His variant where present","Exclusion of the common repeat-expansion ataxias","Brain MRI to assess cerebellar and, where relevant, pontine atrophy","Detailed oculomotor and neurophysiological assessment","Nerve conduction studies if peripheral involvement is suspected","Parental testing to confirm a de novo origin in early-onset or apparently sporadic cases","Family pedigree analysis, genetic counselling and cascade testing"],"redFlags":["Early-onset ataxia with developmental delay, epilepsy or dysmorphism suggests the severe de novo CACNA1G phenotype and alters prognosis and counselling.","Rapid deterioration or autonomic failure suggests an alternative diagnosis such as the cerebellar variant of multiple system atrophy.","Prominent seizures or marked cognitive regression warrant reassessment of the diagnosis."],"prognosis":"Classic adult-onset SCA42 is slowly progressive, with mobility usually preserved for years; the severe early-onset neurodevelopmental form associated with CACNA1G carries a substantially poorer outlook.","pitfalls":["Conflating the mild adult SCA42 with the severe de novo neurodevelopmental CACNA1G disorder, which differ greatly in prognosis.","Assuming a normal expansion panel excludes a dominant ataxia and not proceeding to gene sequencing.","Missing a de novo origin by not testing parents in apparently sporadic early-onset cases.","Overlooking the recurrent p.Arg1715His variant when only large structural changes are sought."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists for this CACNA1G-related ataxia, so centre care on physiotherapy for gait and balance, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy","Institute falls prevention and provide mobility aids for gait and limb ataxia","Treat tremor symptomatically and apply antispasticity measures for pyramidal features","Monitor for bulbar dysfunction with periodic swallow assessment","For the severe, often de novo early-onset neurodevelopmental form, provide early developmental intervention and paediatric multidisciplinary care","Provide multidisciplinary ataxia clinic follow-up","Offer genetic counselling with parental testing to confirm a de novo origin in early-onset or apparently sporadic cases and define recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics reference texts","Clinical neurology textbook — hereditary ataxias","Peer-reviewed hereditary ataxia reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_5":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 5 (SPTBN2)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA5","SPTBN2-related ataxia","beta-III spectrin ataxia","Lincoln ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant, slowly progressive and largely pure cerebellar ataxia caused by variants, often in-frame deletions or missense changes, in SPTBN2, which encodes beta-III spectrin","Historically termed 'Lincoln ataxia' after a large American kindred descended from the paternal grandparents of Abraham Lincoln","Onset ranges from childhood to mid-adulthood, progression is slow and life expectancy is usually near-normal","Clinical features are gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria and eye-movement abnormalities including downbeat and gaze-evoked nystagmus, with facial myokymia in some patients","Beta-III spectrin anchors the glutamate transporter EAAT4 and receptors at the Purkinje cell membrane, so its dysfunction impairs glutamate handling","Diagnosis is by gene sequencing, since repeat-expansion assays are normal","Brain MRI typically shows marked and relatively isolated cerebellar atrophy","Biallelic SPTBN2 variants cause a distinct severe infantile-onset recessive ataxia, in contrast to the dominant SCA5"],"pathophysiology":"SCA5 is caused by dominant variants in SPTBN2, which encodes beta-III spectrin, a cytoskeletal protein enriched in Purkinje cells that stabilises membrane proteins, including the glutamate transporter EAAT4, and helps organise the neuronal membrane skeleton. Mutant spectrin mislocalises or fails to anchor these proteins, so glutamate clearance and Purkinje cell membrane integrity are impaired. The resulting progressive Purkinje cell dysfunction and degeneration underlie the largely pure cerebellar ataxia and the associated eye-movement abnormalities.","additionalDifferentials":["Other pure autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxias (SCA6, SCA14, SCA28)","Ataxias with prominent downbeat nystagmus, such as episodic ataxia type 2 and FGF14-related ataxia","Sporadic adult-onset cerebellar ataxia","Multiple system atrophy, cerebellar type","Severe infantile recessive SPTBN2 ataxia in young children","Autoimmune or gluten-related cerebellar ataxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SPTBN2 sequencing within an ataxia gene panel or exome","Brain MRI to document the marked, relatively isolated cerebellar atrophy","Testing for common repeat-expansion SCAs to exclude commoner causes","Screening for acquired ataxia (thyroid function, vitamins B12 and E, coeliac serology)","Detailed family history to establish autosomal dominant transmission","Consideration of recessive SPTBN2 disease in infantile presentations"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression or an atypical course suggesting an acquired or paraneoplastic cause","A structural lesion at the craniocervical junction as an alternative cause of downbeat nystagmus","A severe infantile presentation suggesting recessive SPTBN2 disease","Dysphagia with aspiration risk requiring swallow assessment"],"prognosis":"SCA5 is slowly progressive over decades, with ambulation often preserved for prolonged periods and a near-normal lifespan typical; disability is driven by cumulative cerebellar dysfunction, and prognosis is generally more favourable than in many other spinocerebellar ataxias.","pitfalls":["Expecting a repeat expansion and missing the point mutation or in-frame deletion when common panels are negative","Confusing dominant SCA5 with the distinct severe infantile recessive SPTBN2 disorder","Attributing downbeat nystagmus to a structural lesion only, without considering a genetic ataxia","Not proceeding to sequencing after negative repeat panels","Failing to provide genetic counselling regarding dominant inheritance and variable expressivity"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists; care is symptomatic and supportive, centred on maintaining function and preventing falls","Physiotherapy with balance and gait rehabilitation, and occupational therapy for mobility aids and home adaptation","Speech and language therapy for dysarthria, with swallow assessment and dietary modification as dysphagia develops","Symptomatic treatment of downbeat or gaze-evoked nystagmus and oscillopsia with aminopyridine-class agents, and of troublesome facial myokymia","Periodic neurological review to monitor progression, falls risk and nutritional and swallowing status","Genetic counselling with predictive and cascade testing for at-risk relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance","Referral to specialist ataxia multidisciplinary services and patient support organisations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":["spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_14","spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_28"]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_6":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 6 (CACNA1A)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA6","CACNA1A CAG repeat expansion","P/Q-type calcium channel ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant, adult-onset (typically fifth to sixth decade), slowly progressive pure cerebellar syndrome that is among the more benign spinocerebellar ataxias","Caused by a small CAG repeat expansion in CACNA1A, which encodes the alpha-1A subunit of the P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channel","Downbeat and gaze-evoked nystagmus and other cerebellar oculomotor signs are characteristic and often early","Some patients experience episodic ataxia or migraine, reflecting the allelic overlap of CACNA1A with episodic ataxia type 2 and familial hemiplegic migraine","Extracerebellar features (marked spasticity, peripheral neuropathy, cognitive decline) are usually mild or absent compared with other polyglutamine SCAs","MRI shows relatively isolated cerebellar (especially vermian) atrophy with a comparatively preserved brainstem","Anticipation is limited because the expanded repeat is small and relatively stable across generations","Management is supportive, centred on physiotherapy, gait and balance rehabilitation, and treatment of any coexisting migraine or vertigo"],"pathophysiology":"SCA6 results from a modest expansion of a CAG trinucleotide repeat within CACNA1A, the gene encoding the pore-forming alpha-1A subunit of the P/Q-type (Cav2.1) voltage-gated calcium channel that is densely expressed in cerebellar Purkinje cells. The expanded polyglutamine tract alters channel function and promotes intracellular accumulation of the mutant subunit, leading to selective, slowly progressive degeneration of Purkinje cells and the cerebellar cortex. Because CACNA1A also harbours point mutations causing episodic ataxia type 2 and familial hemiplegic migraine, SCA6 sits within an allelic spectrum of channelopathies, which explains overlapping episodic and migrainous features in some patients.","additionalDifferentials":["Other autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias, particularly SCA1, SCA2 and SCA3","Episodic ataxia type 2 (allelic CACNA1A disorder)","Multiple system atrophy, cerebellar type","Alcohol-related or nutritional cerebellar degeneration","Paraneoplastic or immune-mediated cerebellar degeneration","Sporadic adult-onset idiopathic cerebellar ataxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted CACNA1A CAG repeat-expansion analysis to confirm the diagnosis","MRI brain demonstrating predominantly cerebellar atrophy with relative brainstem sparing","Broader ataxia repeat-expansion panel when the initial test is negative","Nerve conduction studies to assess for coexisting neuropathy and help distinguish from other SCAs","Vestibular and oculomotor testing to characterise nystagmus and imbalance","Exclusion of acquired causes with vitamin, thyroid, autoimmune and paraneoplastic screening"],"redFlags":["Abrupt or stepwise deterioration suggesting a superimposed vascular or structural lesion rather than SCA6 alone","Prominent autonomic failure raising suspicion of multiple system atrophy","Rapid progression or subacute onset pointing to an acquired (paraneoplastic or immune) cause","Recurrent disabling episodic ataxia or migraine warranting reassessment for an episodic-ataxia phenotype"],"prognosis":"SCA6 is typically the most slowly progressive of the common polyglutamine ataxias, with many patients remaining ambulant for years and life expectancy often near normal. Disability accrues gradually through worsening gait ataxia, dysarthria and oculomotor dysfunction.","pitfalls":["Attributing a slowly progressive late-onset ataxia to ageing or alcohol and omitting genetic testing","Overlooking coexisting episodic ataxia or migraine that is treatable in its own right","Assuming marked anticipation and unnecessarily alarming families, given the repeat's relative stability","Confusing the isolated cerebellar atrophy of SCA6 with the olivopontocerebellar pattern of multiple system atrophy"],"management":["No disease-modifying treatment; care is symptomatic and focused on ataxia, oculomotor symptoms and preserving function","Physiotherapy with balance and gait training, and occupational therapy for mobility aids and fall prevention","Speech and language therapy for dysarthria, with swallow assessment as dysphagia emerges","Treat episodic ataxic or vertiginous attacks with a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor (acetazolamide) and manage coexisting migraine prophylactically, reflecting the CACNA1A channelopathy overlap","Symptomatic management of downbeat or gaze-evoked nystagmus with aminopyridine-class agents","Regular follow-up to track progression, falls and swallowing safety","Genetic counselling with cascade and predictive testing for relatives given autosomal dominant transmission"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Spinocerebellar Ataxia Type 6","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_7":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 7 (ATXN7, with retinopathy)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA7","ATXN7 CAG repeat expansion","autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia with retinal degeneration","ADCA type II"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant ataxia distinguished from all other SCAs by a progressive cone-rod retinal dystrophy causing central visual loss and eventual blindness","Caused by a CAG repeat expansion in ATXN7 producing an expanded polyglutamine ataxin-7 protein","Shows striking anticipation; large expansions can present with a severe infantile form featuring failure to thrive and rapid multisystem decline","Visual symptoms (impaired colour vision, reduced acuity, central scotoma) may precede, coincide with, or follow the onset of ataxia","Fundoscopy shows macular changes and pigmentary maculopathy; the electroretinogram reveals cone-then-rod dysfunction","Cerebellar ataxia is accompanied by dysarthria, dysphagia, pyramidal signs and slowed saccades","The combination of cerebellar ataxia with retinal degeneration should prompt directed ATXN7 testing","Care is multidisciplinary and supportive, including low-vision rehabilitation, physiotherapy and swallowing assessment"],"pathophysiology":"SCA7 is caused by expansion of a CAG trinucleotide repeat in ATXN7, translated into an elongated polyglutamine tract in the ataxin-7 protein, a component of transcriptional coactivator complexes. The mutant protein misfolds, forms nuclear inclusions and disrupts transcriptional regulation, producing toxic gain-of-function degeneration. Unlike other polyglutamine SCAs, ataxin-7 dysfunction also injures retinal photoreceptors, giving the characteristic cone-rod dystrophy alongside degeneration of the cerebellum, brainstem and spinal tracts. The repeat is highly unstable, especially on paternal transmission, underlying pronounced anticipation and severe early-onset cases.","additionalDifferentials":["Other autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias without retinopathy (SCA1, SCA2, SCA3)","Retinitis pigmentosa or isolated cone-rod dystrophy from other causes","Mitochondrial disorders combining ataxia and retinal degeneration","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis and other neurodegenerations with visual loss","Abetalipoproteinaemia and vitamin E deficiency ataxia with retinopathy","Paraneoplastic syndromes affecting cerebellum and retina"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted ATXN7 CAG repeat-expansion analysis","Formal ophthalmological assessment with fundoscopy, colour vision testing and visual fields","Electroretinography and optical coherence tomography to characterise the retinal dystrophy","MRI brain showing cerebellar and brainstem (olivopontocerebellar) atrophy","Nerve conduction studies to assess associated tract involvement","Metabolic and vitamin screening to exclude treatable mimics"],"redFlags":["Infantile or early-childhood presentation with failure to thrive and rapid deterioration indicating a large expansion","Rapidly progressive visual loss threatening independence and safety","Severe dysphagia with aspiration risk","Marked anticipation across a pedigree signalling risk of a severe phenotype in offspring"],"prognosis":"Prognosis correlates with repeat size: adult-onset disease progresses over one to a few decades with combined visual and motor disability, whereas large expansions cause severe infantile disease with early death. Blindness and loss of ambulation are common in advanced cases.","pitfalls":["Investigating cerebellar ataxia and visual loss as separate problems and missing the unifying diagnosis","Omitting an eye examination in a patient with dominant ataxia, thereby overlooking the key discriminating feature","Underestimating anticipation when counselling families about offspring risk","Mistaking the retinal dystrophy for simple age-related macular degeneration"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy; management is supportive across the neurological and visual domains","Physiotherapy with gait and balance rehabilitation, and occupational therapy for mobility aids and falls","Speech and language therapy for dysarthria, with proactive management of dysphagia including nutritional support and aspiration prevention","Ophthalmology-led low-vision rehabilitation and visual aids for the progressive cone-rod dystrophy, with support towards eventual blindness","Regular ophthalmological and neurological surveillance to track retinal and motor progression, monitoring for aspiration and weight loss","In the severe infantile form, paediatric multidisciplinary care addressing feeding, failure to thrive and rapid multisystem decline","Genetic counselling emphasising anticipation, particularly on paternal transmission, with cascade and predictive testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Spinocerebellar Ataxia Type 7","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinocerebellar_ataxia_type_8":{"name":"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 8 (ATXN8)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCA8","ATXN8/ATXN8OS CTG-CAG repeat expansion","bidirectional repeat expansion ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Slowly progressive, often adult-onset cerebellar ataxia caused by a combined CTG-CAG repeat expansion at the ATXN8/ATXN8OS locus","The locus is transcribed bidirectionally, producing both a non-coding CTG (ATXN8OS) transcript and a CAG (ATXN8) polyglutamine transcript, so it shares features of both toxic RNA and polyglutamine mechanisms","Marked reduced penetrance and variable expansion size complicate interpretation, so a positive result must be correlated with the clinical picture","Typical features include gait and limb ataxia, scanning dysarthria, nystagmus and variable spasticity","Onset is usually in adulthood but ranges widely, and progression is generally slow","MRI shows cerebellar atrophy, sometimes with pontine involvement","Because expanded alleles occur in unaffected individuals, the test alone does not confirm symptomatic disease","Management is supportive with physiotherapy, speech therapy and falls prevention"],"pathophysiology":"SCA8 arises from an unstable combined CTG-CAG repeat expansion at the ATXN8OS/ATXN8 locus. The region is bidirectionally transcribed: the ATXN8OS strand yields a CUG-containing non-coding RNA thought to exert an RNA toxic gain-of-function similar to the myotonic dystrophies, while the opposite ATXN8 strand encodes a nearly pure polyglutamine protein via a CAG expansion. Both mechanisms are implicated in Purkinje cell and cerebellar degeneration. The considerable variability in expansion length, incomplete penetrance and presence of expanded alleles in the general population make genotype-phenotype correlation less clear-cut than in other repeat ataxias.","additionalDifferentials":["Other autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA1, SCA2, SCA3, SCA6)","Myotonic dystrophy and other RNA repeat-expansion disorders","Multiple system atrophy, cerebellar type","Sporadic adult-onset idiopathic cerebellar ataxia","Alcohol-related or nutritional cerebellar degeneration","Acquired immune-mediated cerebellar ataxia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted ATXN8/ATXN8OS CTG-CAG repeat analysis interpreted alongside the clinical picture","Broad spinocerebellar ataxia repeat-expansion panel to exclude other subtypes","MRI brain to document cerebellar and any brainstem atrophy","Nerve conduction studies to assess for associated neuropathy","Screening for acquired causes (vitamin, thyroid, autoimmune, paraneoplastic)","Family history review and consideration of genetic counselling given reduced penetrance"],"redFlags":["Rapid or subacute progression suggesting an acquired rather than genetic cause","Prominent autonomic failure raising the possibility of multiple system atrophy","Systemic myotonia or cardiac features prompting reassessment for myotonic dystrophy","A positive repeat result in a minimally symptomatic person that risks over-diagnosis"],"prognosis":"SCA8 usually follows a slowly progressive course over years to decades with generally preserved life expectancy, though disability from gait ataxia and dysarthria accumulates over time. Reduced penetrance means some expansion carriers remain mildly affected or asymptomatic.","pitfalls":["Treating a positive expansion as definitive proof of disease despite the locus's reduced penetrance","Failing to exclude treatable acquired ataxias before attributing symptoms to SCA8","Overlooking myotonic dystrophy given the shared RNA toxicity mechanism","Providing overly deterministic genetic counselling without acknowledging variable expressivity"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy; symptomatic and supportive management of the cerebellar syndrome","Physiotherapy with balance and gait training, and occupational therapy for aids and fall prevention","Speech and language therapy for scanning dysarthria, with swallow assessment for dysphagia","Symptomatic treatment of nystagmus and oscillopsia, and antispasticity agents where spasticity is troublesome","Periodic neurological review to monitor progression and safety","Genetic counselling that explicitly addresses reduced penetrance and variable expansion size when advising relatives","Referral to specialist ataxia services and patient support networks"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Spinocerebellar Ataxia Type 8","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"splenic_abscess":{"name":"Splenic abscess","system":"GI / Hepatobiliary surgery","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["abscess of the spleen","splenic suppuration","pyogenic splenic abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Splenic abscess is uncommon but important, and classically presents with fever, left upper quadrant pain, and left-sided tenderness, sometimes with referred left shoulder-tip pain","It most often arises from haematogenous seeding, with infective endocarditis a notable source, and is increasingly seen in immunocompromised and diabetic patients","A left pleural effusion, basal atelectasis, or raised hemidiaphragm may be present and can distract from the underlying splenic pathology","Contrast CT is the imaging modality of choice and defines the size, number, and character of collections, while ultrasound is a useful bedside adjunct","Blood cultures and identification of the causative organism guide targeted antimicrobial therapy; polymicrobial, fungal (especially Candida in the immunosuppressed), and mycobacterial causes occur","Management combines appropriate antibiotics with drainage; image-guided percutaneous drainage may suffice for a unilocular collection, while splenectomy is often required for multiloculated or refractory disease","An untreated or ruptured splenic abscess can lead to overwhelming sepsis, so prompt diagnosis and source control are essential"],"pathophysiology":"Splenic abscesses develop when organisms reach and proliferate within splenic tissue, most commonly by haematogenous spread from a distant focus such as infective endocarditis, or from contiguous infection, penetrating trauma, or infarction of the spleen (as in sickle cell disease) that subsequently becomes infected. Immunosuppression, diabetes, and intravenous drug use increase susceptibility and broaden the range of pathogens to include fungi and unusual bacteria. Localised suppuration forms a walled-off collection of pus; as it enlarges it can rupture into the peritoneal cavity, causing peritonitis, or into adjacent structures, and can seed the bloodstream to produce systemic sepsis.","additionalDifferentials":["Splenic infarct","Splenic cyst (including hydatid cyst) or pseudocyst","Subphrenic abscess","Left-sided pyelonephritis or perinephric abscess","Pancreatic tail abscess or pseudocyst","Splenic tumour or lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen to characterise the abscess","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers to identify the organism and monitor response","Echocardiography to seek infective endocarditis as a source","Ultrasound for bedside assessment and to guide percutaneous drainage","Aspirate microscopy, culture, and sensitivity from drained material","Screening for immunocompromise (including HIV and diabetes) where relevant"],"redFlags":["Signs of rupture with peritonitis and haemodynamic instability requiring emergency surgery","Septic shock or multi-organ dysfunction from uncontrolled infection","Rapid clinical deterioration despite antibiotics indicating inadequate source control","Underlying infective endocarditis with ongoing embolic risk"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis, effective antimicrobial therapy, and adequate drainage or splenectomy the outlook is generally favourable. Mortality rises sharply with delayed treatment, rupture, multiloculated or fungal infection, and significant immunocompromise. After splenectomy patients require lifelong precautions and vaccination against encapsulated organisms.","pitfalls":["Attributing left upper quadrant pain and fever to pneumonia or a pleural effusion and missing the splenic source","Relying on antibiotics alone for a large or multiloculated abscess that requires drainage","Failing to seek and treat an underlying source such as endocarditis","Neglecting post-splenectomy sepsis prophylaxis and vaccination","Overlooking fungal or atypical organisms in immunocompromised patients"],"management":["Prompt empirical intravenous antibiotics covering enteric Gram-negatives, anaerobes and streptococci, then narrowed to blood and aspirate culture and sensitivities","Add antifungal cover with an echinocandin or azole where Candida is likely, as in the immunocompromised, and antituberculous therapy if a mycobacterial cause is confirmed","Drainage of the collection: image-guided percutaneous drainage for a unilocular abscess, with splenectomy for multiloculated or refractory disease","Source control by identifying and treating the origin, notably infective endocarditis, using echocardiography and appropriately prolonged therapy","Resuscitation and sepsis management with fluids and haemodynamic support, and urgent laparotomy with splenectomy for rupture causing peritonitis","Monitor clinical response, inflammatory markers and repeat imaging to confirm resolution","After splenectomy, give pneumococcal, meningococcal and Haemophilus influenzae type b immunisation plus antibiotic prophylaxis, and counsel on overwhelming post-splenectomy infection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"splenic_artery_aneurysm":{"name":"Splenic artery aneurysm","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["splenic artery aneurysm","SAA","lienal artery aneurysm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard vascular surgery reference texts and visceral aneurysm consensus guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common visceral artery aneurysm, frequently found incidentally on cross-sectional imaging as a calcified lesion near the splenic hilum","There is a marked female predominance, and multiparity and pregnancy are important associations","Portal hypertension and cirrhosis substantially increase both prevalence and size of these aneurysms","Most are asymptomatic, but larger or symptomatic lesions may cause left upper quadrant or epigastric pain","Rupture is the feared complication and can present as a two-stage bleed, initially contained within the lesser sac before free intraperitoneal haemorrhage","Rupture risk is especially high in pregnancy, where it carries grave maternal and fetal mortality, and this drives aggressive treatment in women of childbearing potential","Repair, by endovascular embolisation or open or laparoscopic surgery, is generally advised for large, symptomatic, or pregnancy-associated aneurysms"],"pathophysiology":"A splenic artery aneurysm is a focal dilatation of the splenic artery caused by weakening of the arterial wall, with degeneration of elastic and muscular layers and medial fibrodysplasia. Hormonal and haemodynamic changes of pregnancy, portal hypertension with increased splenic blood flow, atherosclerosis and fibromuscular dysplasia all contribute to wall stress and progressive enlargement. As the aneurysm grows, wall tension rises and the risk of rupture increases; rupture may initially be tamponaded within the lesser sac, producing transient stability before catastrophic free intraperitoneal haemorrhage and haemodynamic collapse.","additionalDifferentials":["Splenic artery pseudoaneurysm (often post-pancreatitis or trauma)","Other visceral artery aneurysms (hepatic, coeliac, gastroduodenal)","Pancreatic pseudocyst or peripancreatic collection","Abdominal aortic aneurysm","Splenic infarct or abscess","Left-sided renal or adrenal mass"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT angiography to confirm and size the aneurysm and assess its relation to the splenic hilum","Doppler ultrasound for detection and surveillance, particularly in pregnancy where radiation is avoided","MR angiography as a radiation-sparing alternative for characterisation and follow-up","Catheter angiography when endovascular treatment is planned","Assessment for portal hypertension and liver disease as underlying drivers","Serial imaging surveillance of small asymptomatic aneurysms to monitor growth"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe abdominal pain with hypotension suggesting rupture","A two-stage presentation with transient stabilisation before collapse","Rapidly enlarging or large aneurysm on serial imaging","Any splenic artery aneurysm identified in a pregnant patient given the high rupture-related mortality"],"prognosis":"Unruptured aneurysms treated electively have an excellent outcome, whereas rupture carries high mortality, particularly in pregnancy; elective repair of high-risk lesions therefore markedly improves prognosis, and small low-risk aneurysms can be safely surveyed.","pitfalls":["Dismissing an incidental calcified splenic lesion without appreciating rupture risk in higher-risk groups","Underestimating the danger of even modest-sized aneurysms during pregnancy","Confusing a true aneurysm with a post-inflammatory pseudoaneurysm, which has different management urgency","Failing to identify and address underlying portal hypertension"],"management":["Elective repair of aneurysms meeting treatment criteria (symptomatic, enlarging, or in women of childbearing potential) rather than watchful waiting, given rupture risk","Endovascular treatment with coil embolisation or a covered stent as preferred first-line intervention, with open ligation, aneurysmectomy or splenectomy where anatomy is unfavourable","Surveillance imaging with Doppler ultrasound or CT or MR angiography for small asymptomatic aneurysms to monitor growth","Aggressive treatment in pregnancy, and pre-conception counselling, given the grave maternal and foetal mortality of rupture","Address underlying drivers, particularly portal hypertension and chronic liver disease","Emergency resuscitation with urgent operative or endovascular control for rupture presenting with intraperitoneal haemorrhage","Cardiovascular risk factor modification where atherosclerotic disease coexists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Society for Vascular Surgery guidance on visceral artery aneurysms"],"crossLinks":[]},"splenic_artery_steal":{"name":"splenic artery steal","system":"Hepatobiliary / Vascular system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["splenic artery steal syndrome","splenic steal syndrome","SASS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Blumgart's Surgery of the Liver, Biliary Tract and Pancreas / Grainger & Allison's Diagnostic Radiology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A recognised cause of hepatic arterial hypoperfusion after orthotopic liver transplantation despite a patent hepatic arterial anastomosis","Coeliac axis blood is preferentially diverted through a high-flow, often dilated splenic artery, diminishing flow to the hepatic artery","Pre-transplant splenomegaly, portal hypertension, hypersplenism and a large splenic-to-hepatic artery calibre ratio are the principal risk factors","Typically presents with otherwise unexplained graft dysfunction, cholestatic derangement of liver enzymes and ischaemic biliary complications","It must be distinguished from hepatic artery stenosis or thrombosis, which produce similar Doppler and clinical pictures but require different management","Catheter angiography confirms poor hepatic arterial filling with a prominent splenic artery and is both diagnostic and a platform for treatment","Proximal splenic artery embolisation is the preferred treatment, redirecting flow into the hepatic artery, with splenectomy or splenic artery ligation as alternatives","The concept remains debated, and some regard the picture as reflecting inadequate hepatic arterial inflow rather than true competitive steal"],"pathophysiology":"In the coeliac circulation the hepatic and splenic arteries share a common origin. After liver transplantation, a combination of a relatively small or low-flow hepatic artery and a high-flow splenic artery, the latter enlarged by pre-existing splenomegaly, portal hypertension and hypersplenism, can result in preferential diversion of coeliac flow towards the spleen. The transplanted liver, having lost its portal autoregulatory buffer response and being dependent on arterial inflow for biliary perfusion, is left relatively ischaemic despite a technically patent arterial anastomosis. The resulting hypoperfusion impairs graft function and, because the biliary tree is supplied almost entirely by the hepatic artery, predisposes to ischaemic cholangiopathy and biliary strictures. Whether this represents genuine haemodynamic steal or simply inadequate hepatic inflow is contested, but redirecting flow towards the liver reverses the abnormality.","additionalDifferentials":["Hepatic artery thrombosis","Hepatic artery stenosis or kinking","Coeliac axis stenosis from median arcuate ligament compression or atherosclerosis","Acute cellular rejection","Primary graft non-function or preservation injury","Biliary anastomotic stricture","Portal vein stenosis or thrombosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Doppler ultrasound of the hepatic and splenic arteries assessing velocities and resistive index","CT angiography to display the coeliac axis and relative arterial calibres","Catheter coeliac and hepatic arteriography as the reference standard, quantifying the splenic-to-hepatic calibre ratio","Liver function tests to characterise the cholestatic pattern","Liver biopsy to exclude rejection and preservation injury","MR or direct cholangiography to assess ischaemic biliary complications"],"redFlags":["Rising cholestatic liver enzymes with evolving ischaemic cholangiopathy","Progressive graft dysfunction despite a patent arterial anastomosis","Doppler features that cannot exclude hepatic artery thrombosis, a surgical emergency","Deterioration unresponsive to treatment of presumed rejection"],"prognosis":"When recognised early and treated with splenic artery embolisation, hepatic arterial flow and graft function usually improve rapidly with a good outcome. Left untreated, persistent hypoperfusion risks ischaemic biliary strictures and graft loss.","pitfalls":["Attributing graft dysfunction to rejection and overlooking the arterial steal","Failing to exclude hepatic artery stenosis or thrombosis before diagnosing steal","Overlooking marked splenomegaly as a predisposing risk factor before or after transplantation","Embolising too distally within the splenic artery, risking splenic infarction and abscess"],"management":["Splenic artery embolisation as the definitive treatment to redirect coeliac flow back to the hepatic artery and restore graft perfusion","Splenic artery banding or ligation as a surgical alternative where embolisation is unsuitable","Confirm the diagnosis and exclude hepatic artery stenosis or thrombosis with Doppler and angiography before intervention, since management differs","Supportive care of graft dysfunction and the cholestatic derangement while awaiting reperfusion","Surveillance for and management of ischaemic biliary complications with cholangiography and biliary stenting or drainage","Monitor hepatic arterial flow with serial Doppler and liver function tests after treatment to confirm response, anticipating post-embolisation syndrome and splenic infarction","Transplant hepatology and interventional radiology multidisciplinary management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Blumgart's Surgery of the Liver, Biliary Tract and Pancreas","Grainger & Allison's Diagnostic Radiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"splenic_cyst":{"name":"Splenic cyst","system":"Haematological / Reticuloendothelial system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["splenic epidermoid cyst","true (epithelial) splenic cyst","congenital splenic cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Grainger & Allison's Diagnostic Radiology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Splenic cysts are classified as true (primary) cysts, which possess an epithelial or mesothelial lining, or false (secondary) pseudocysts, which lack a lining and most often follow trauma; congenital epidermoid cysts are the commonest true non-parasitic type","Congenital epidermoid cysts are lined by squamous epithelium and are thought to arise from embryonic inclusion of surface mesothelium within the developing spleen, typically presenting in children and young adults","Most are asymptomatic incidental findings; larger cysts cause left upper quadrant pain or fullness, early satiety, referred left shoulder-tip pain or a palpable mass","The lining epithelium can secrete CA 19-9 and CEA, so serum levels may be raised and then normalise after excision; this is a diagnostic clue and not a marker of malignancy","On imaging a true cyst is a well-defined, thin-walled, non-enhancing fluid lesion, whereas wall calcification and trabeculation favour a pseudocyst, though imaging cannot always separate the two","A parasitic (hydatid) cyst must always be excluded before intervention through exposure history, serology and the search for daughter cysts, because inadvertent spillage can cause anaphylaxis and dissemination","Spleen-preserving surgery such as partial splenectomy, deroofing or decapsulation is preferred over total splenectomy where feasible, to retain immune function and avoid overwhelming post-splenectomy infection"],"pathophysiology":"True congenital splenic (epidermoid) cysts are believed to develop from embryonic entrapment of surface mesothelium within the splenic parenchyma, or from metaplasia of these inclusions, producing a cyst lined by squamous or cuboidal epithelium. This epithelium can elaborate CA 19-9 and CEA, accounting for the raised serum markers seen in some patients. Pseudocysts, in contrast, have no epithelial lining and usually represent the organised, liquefied remnant of a previous haematoma, most often post-traumatic. Progressive accumulation of fluid enlarges the cyst, thins the overlying parenchyma and capsule and predisposes to rupture, intracystic haemorrhage or secondary infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Post-traumatic splenic pseudocyst","Parasitic (hydatid) cyst","Splenic abscess","Cystic neoplasm such as lymphangioma, haemangioma or cystic metastasis","Pancreatic pseudocyst or cyst indenting the spleen","Organising intrasplenic haematoma","Cystic lymphangioma, particularly in children"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound with Doppler to demonstrate an anechoic, well-defined lesion and assess the wall","Contrast-enhanced CT or MRI to characterise the wall, septa and any enhancement","Serum CA 19-9 and CEA, which may be raised with epithelial cysts","Hydatid serology and indirect haemagglutination where parasitic disease is possible","Full blood count and inflammatory markers if secondary infection or abscess is suspected","Histological examination of the excised specimen to confirm the lining type"],"redFlags":["Acute abdomen or hypovolaemia from cyst rupture or intracystic haemorrhage","Fever and sepsis suggesting secondary infection or abscess formation","Rapid enlargement or solid, septated components raising concern for a neoplasm","A large thin-walled cyst at risk of traumatic rupture"],"prognosis":"Benign congenital splenic cysts carry an excellent prognosis, and spleen-preserving surgery gives durable results with a low recurrence rate, whereas simple aspiration alone commonly recurs. Malignant transformation is very rare.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a raised CA 19-9 as evidence of malignancy rather than a benign epithelial cyst","Relying on simple percutaneous aspiration, which frequently leads to recurrence","Missing a parasitic (hydatid) cyst and risking spillage and anaphylaxis at operation","Performing total splenectomy when spleen-preserving surgery would suffice","Neglecting vaccination and infection-prevention advice when splenectomy is unavoidable"],"management":["Conservative observation with interval imaging for small, asymptomatic true cysts or pseudocysts","Exclude a parasitic (hydatid) cyst by exposure history and serology before any intervention, since spillage risks anaphylaxis and dissemination","Spleen-preserving surgery (partial splenectomy, cyst deroofing or fenestration, or marsupialisation) for symptomatic or large cysts to retain splenic immune function","Avoid simple percutaneous aspiration alone as definitive treatment because recurrence is high","Reserve total splenectomy for cysts not amenable to spleen-preserving techniques, with pre-operative immunisation and post-splenectomy infection prophylaxis","Serum CA 19-9 and CEA together with imaging follow-up after excision to document resolution","Histological confirmation of the excised specimen to define the lining and exclude rare malignancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Grainger & Allison's Diagnostic Radiology","Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"splenic_infarction":{"name":"splenic infarction","system":"Haematological / Reticuloendothelial system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["splenic infarct","ischaemic necrosis of the spleen"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sabiston Textbook of Surgery / Postgraduate Haematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from occlusion of the splenic artery or its branches, producing the classic wedge-shaped peripheral hypodense lesion on contrast-enhanced CT with its base against the capsule","Two dominant mechanisms are cardioembolic disease (atrial fibrillation, infective endocarditis, mural thrombus) and haematological disease (myeloproliferative neoplasms, sickle cell disease and massive splenomegaly of any cause)","In a young patient without a cardiac source, actively seek an underlying hypercoagulable state such as antiphospholipid syndrome or an inherited thrombophilia","Left upper quadrant pain radiating to the left shoulder tip (Kehr sign) reflects diaphragmatic irritation, but a large proportion of infarcts are clinically silent and found incidentally","In sickle cell disease repeated infarction causes autosplenectomy and functional hyposplenism; splenic infarction may also occur in sickle cell trait or HbSC disease at high altitude","Global rather than segmental infarction points to splenic artery or vein thrombosis, or to torsion of a wandering spleen","Most uncomplicated cases are managed conservatively with analgesia and treatment of the underlying cause, reserving intervention for complications","Complications to anticipate include splenic abscess, haemorrhage, subcapsular haematoma, pseudocyst formation and rupture"],"pathophysiology":"Splenic infarction occurs when arterial inflow through the splenic artery or its segmental branches is interrupted, causing ischaemic necrosis of the dependent parenchyma. Because the spleen has a largely end-arterial segmental supply, occlusion typically produces a wedge-shaped lesion with its base at the capsule. Emboli arising from the heart or aorta, in-situ thrombosis in hypercoagulable and myeloproliferative states, sickling and sludging of red cells within the microcirculation, mechanical compromise of the pedicle in a wandering spleen, or infiltration and expansion in massive splenomegaly can each precipitate ischaemia. Necrotic tissue may resolve with scarring and contraction, liquefy to form a pseudocyst, become secondarily infected to form an abscess, or bleed and rupture through the capsule.","additionalDifferentials":["Left-sided pyelonephritis or renal infarction","Left lower lobe pneumonia with pleuritic pain","Acute pancreatitis","Splenic abscess","Splenic rupture or subcapsular haematoma","Perforated peptic ulcer","Diverticulitis of the splenic flexure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen to demonstrate the wedge-shaped perfusion defect and complications","Transthoracic and, if needed, transoesophageal echocardiography to seek a cardioembolic source","ECG and ambulatory monitoring for atrial fibrillation","Full blood count and peripheral blood film, with JAK2 and myeloproliferative screening where indicated","Thrombophilia and antiphospholipid antibody screen, particularly in younger patients","Blood cultures where infective endocarditis is suspected","Haemoglobin electrophoresis when a sickling disorder is possible"],"redFlags":["Haemodynamic instability or peritonism suggesting splenic rupture and haemorrhage","Persistent swinging fever and rising inflammatory markers indicating abscess formation","An enlarging tender mass or falling haemoglobin from a subcapsular haematoma","New embolic phenomena elsewhere pointing to an untreated cardiac source"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated splenic infarction usually settles with conservative management and its outlook is largely determined by the underlying disorder. Development of abscess, significant haemorrhage or rupture worsens the prognosis and may necessitate splenectomy.","pitfalls":["Attributing left upper quadrant pain to pneumonia or pancreatitis and overlooking the infarct on imaging","Failing to pursue an embolic source or a myeloproliferative or thrombophilic cause","Missing an evolving abscess or rupture during conservative management","Overlooking resulting functional hyposplenism and the need for immunisation and infection prophylaxis"],"management":["Supportive management of uncomplicated infarction with analgesia and hydration, as most settle conservatively","Identify and treat the underlying cause: anticoagulation for cardioembolic sources such as atrial fibrillation, and cytoreductive or disease-specific therapy for myeloproliferative neoplasms","Anticoagulate and investigate for a hypercoagulable state such as antiphospholipid syndrome or inherited thrombophilia in younger patients without a cardiac source","In sickle cell disease manage the sickling crisis with hydration, oxygenation and analgesia, and address precipitants such as hypoxia at altitude","Antimicrobial therapy with drainage or splenectomy if the infarct becomes secondarily infected, and operative management for significant haemorrhage or rupture","Surveillance imaging for complications such as abscess, pseudocyst or haemorrhage, and monitoring for functional hyposplenism","Immunisation and antibiotic prophylaxis where functional asplenia or autosplenectomy has occurred"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","Postgraduate Haematology (Hoffbrand)"],"crossLinks":[]},"splenic_marginal_zone_lymphoma":{"name":"Splenic marginal zone lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SMZL","splenic lymphoma with villous lymphocytes","SLVL","marginal zone B-cell lymphoma, splenic type"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An indolent small B-cell lymphoma of marginal-zone origin that involves the spleen, splenic hilar nodes, bone marrow and often the peripheral blood","Typically presents in older adults with splenomegaly, cytopenias from hypersplenism, and villous lymphocytes on the blood film","A recognised association with chronic hepatitis C virus infection exists, and antiviral therapy can induce lymphoma regression in HCV-positive cases","Autoimmune phenomena such as autoimmune haemolytic anaemia or immune thrombocytopenia and a small paraprotein may accompany the disease","The immunophenotype is that of a CD20-positive B cell negative for CD5, CD10, CD23 and cyclin D1, which helps exclude other small B-cell lymphomas","Bone marrow shows a characteristic intrasinusoidal infiltration pattern that is a useful diagnostic clue","Asymptomatic patients may be observed, while symptomatic disease is treated with anti-CD20 targeted therapy, and splenectomy or chemoimmunotherapy in selected cases"],"pathophysiology":"Splenic marginal zone lymphoma arises from a post-germinal-centre memory B cell of the splenic marginal zone. Chronic antigenic stimulation, including that associated with hepatitis C virus infection in a subset of cases, is thought to drive clonal B-cell expansion, and this dependence explains why antiviral therapy can produce regression when HCV is present. The tumour replaces splenic white pulp and infiltrates the red pulp, and typically shows involvement of splenic hilar nodes and an intrasinusoidal pattern in the bone marrow. Recurrent mutations affecting NOTCH2 and the NF-kappaB and marginal-zone differentiation pathways contribute to the neoplastic phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia","Hairy cell leukaemia and its variant","Mantle cell lymphoma","Follicular lymphoma with splenic involvement","Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma (Waldenstrom macroglobulinaemia)","Nodal or extranodal marginal zone lymphoma with splenic spread"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood film for villous lymphocytes and full blood count for cytopenias","Flow cytometry immunophenotyping showing CD20-positive, CD5/CD10/CD23/cyclin D1-negative B cells","Bone marrow trephine demonstrating intrasinusoidal infiltration","Hepatitis C virus serology and viral load given the therapeutic implications","Serum protein electrophoresis and immunoglobulins for a paraprotein","Direct antiglobulin test and haemolysis markers if anaemia is present","Imaging to characterise splenomegaly and exclude bulky nodal disease"],"redFlags":["Rapid splenic enlargement, new B symptoms or rising lactate dehydrogenase suggesting transformation to aggressive lymphoma","Severe cytopenias causing bleeding or infection risk","Symptomatic autoimmune haemolysis or immune thrombocytopenia","Splenic rupture or infarction from massive splenomegaly"],"prognosis":"Most patients follow an indolent course with prolonged survival, and HCV-associated cases may regress with antiviral therapy; adverse outcomes are associated with transformation to aggressive large B-cell lymphoma and with certain molecular features such as TP53 abnormalities.","pitfalls":["Failing to test for hepatitis C virus and missing a case treatable with antiviral therapy","Confusing the disease with hairy cell leukaemia or chronic lymphocytic leukaemia without full immunophenotyping","Overlooking the intrasinusoidal bone marrow pattern that supports the diagnosis","Attributing cytopenias solely to marrow infiltration when hypersplenism or autoimmunity is responsible","Missing transformation in a patient with sudden clinical change"],"management":["Watchful waiting for asymptomatic disease without cytopenias or bulky splenomegaly, given the indolent course","Antiviral therapy with direct-acting antivirals as first-line treatment in hepatitis C virus-associated cases, which can induce lymphoma regression","Rituximab (anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody), alone or combined with chemotherapy, for symptomatic, progressive or HCV-negative disease","Splenectomy for symptomatic splenomegaly or refractory hypersplenism-related cytopenias, with pre-operative immunisation","Treat associated autoimmune haemolytic anaemia or immune thrombocytopenia with corticosteroids and immunotherapy","Monitor blood counts and clinical status for progression and for transformation to aggressive large B-cell lymphoma","Haemato-oncology multidisciplinary management with post-splenectomy infection prophylaxis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","British Society for Haematology guidance on marginal zone lymphomas"],"crossLinks":[]},"splenogonadal_fusion":{"name":"Splenogonadal fusion","system":"Reticuloendothelial / Genitourinary system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SGF","spleno-gonadal fusion","accessory splenic tissue fused to gonad"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare congenital anomaly in which splenic tissue is abnormally connected to the gonad or a mesonephric derivative, arising when the splenic anlage lies adjacent to the developing gonad and urogenital ridge in the dorsal mesogastrium during the second month of embryonic development","Two forms are recognised: a continuous type, in which a cord of splenic or fibrous tissue links the orthotopic spleen to the gonad, and a discontinuous type, in which isolated ectopic splenic tissue is fused to the gonad with no connection to the spleen","There is a marked left-sided and male predominance, so the left testis is the structure most often involved","The continuous type is associated with terminal transverse limb defects and micrognathia, constituting the splenogonadal fusion-limb defect syndrome","It typically presents as a scrotal or inguinal mass or an undescended testis and is a well-recognised mimic of a testicular tumour, risking unnecessary orchidectomy","The ectopic splenic tissue enlarges in parallel with the spleen during systemic illness, so the scrotal swelling may wax and wane with intercurrent infection","Because the diagnosis is frequently made only at operation, intra-operative frozen section and radionuclide splenic scintigraphy help confirm splenic tissue and permit testis-sparing surgery"],"pathophysiology":"During the second month of embryogenesis the splenic primordium develops within the dorsal mesogastrium in close proximity to the left urogenital ridge, mesonephros and gonad. If splenic tissue adheres abnormally to the gonadal anlage before the gonad begins its descent, the splenic tissue is either tethered to or carried along with the gonad. In the continuous type a cord, sometimes beaded with splenic nodules, connects the orthotopic spleen to the gonad; in the discontinuous type an isolated mass of splenic tissue is fused to the gonad. The preponderance of left-sided and male cases reflects these embryological relationships, and the association with limb reduction defects in the continuous type reflects the timing of the developmental insult.","additionalDifferentials":["Testicular germ cell tumour","Paratesticular rhabdomyosarcoma or other paratesticular tumour","Epididymo-orchitis or torsion of a testicular appendage","Simple cryptorchidism without fusion","Inguinal hernia or hydrocele","Polyorchidism or an adrenal rest","Acquired splenosis involving the scrotum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound with Doppler to characterise the mass and its vascularity","Technetium-labelled sulphur colloid or heat-damaged red cell scintigraphy to identify splenic tissue","Serum germ cell tumour markers (alpha-fetoprotein, beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin, lactate dehydrogenase) to help exclude malignancy","MRI of the pelvis and scrotum for anatomical characterisation","Intra-operative frozen-section histology","Skeletal and craniofacial assessment for associated limb defects and micrognathia in the continuous type"],"redFlags":["A scrotal mass misinterpreted as malignancy prompting orchidectomy","A hard or rapidly enlarging testicular mass requiring urgent tumour work-up","Associated limb reduction defects or micrognathia indicating the syndromic form","An undescended testis with its attendant malignancy and torsion risk"],"prognosis":"Splenogonadal fusion is benign and the prognosis is excellent when it is recognised and treated with testis-sparing excision of the ectopic splenic tissue. The principal avoidable morbidity is unnecessary orchidectomy resulting from misdiagnosis as a testicular tumour.","pitfalls":["Removing the testis for a presumed tumour without frozen section or splenic scintigraphy","Not considering the diagnosis in a boy with a left scrotal mass and a limb anomaly","Attributing a waxing and waning scrotal swelling to a hernia","Failing to preserve the gonad in a child when benign splenic tissue is confirmed","Overlooking associated cryptorchidism and its long-term surveillance needs"],"management":["Testis-sparing excision of the ectopic splenic tissue as definitive treatment, preserving the gonad","Avoid unnecessary orchidectomy by recognising the diagnosis and using intra-operative frozen-section histology when a testicular tumour is suspected","Pre-operative confirmation of splenic tissue with scintigraphy and exclusion of malignancy with germ cell tumour markers","Orchidopexy where the anomaly is associated with an undescended testis","Assess for and manage associated terminal transverse limb defects and micrognathia in the continuous type","Paediatric surgery and urology referral, with reassurance regarding the benign nature and excellent prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"splenosis":{"name":"splenosis","system":"Haematological / Reticuloendothelial system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["splenic autotransplantation","heterotopic splenic implantation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grainger & Allison's Diagnostic Radiology / Postgraduate Haematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Represents acquired heterotopic autotransplantation of splenic pulp after splenic rupture or splenectomy, with implants seeding peritoneal and serosal surfaces and acquiring a new blood supply","Distinguished from an accessory spleen, which is congenital, usually solitary and near the hilum, possesses a hilum and is supplied by the splenic artery, whereas splenosis is acquired, multiple, lacks a hilum and parasitises surrounding vessels","Thoracic splenosis follows combined splenic and left diaphragmatic injury, producing left pleural-based nodules that can mimic malignancy","Usually asymptomatic and found incidentally, but can be mistaken for peritoneal metastases, carcinomatosis, mesothelioma, lymphoma or endometriosis","Technetium-labelled heat-damaged red cell scintigraphy, ideally with SPECT/CT, confirms functioning splenic tissue and averts unnecessary biopsy or surgery","Implants restore only partial splenic function, so vaccination and infection prophylaxis appropriate to hyposplenism should still be considered","Disappearance of Howell-Jolly bodies from the blood film after splenectomy can be a clue to functioning splenosis","Implants can be found throughout the abdomen and pelvis, and rarely in the thorax, liver or subcutaneous tissue at old wound sites"],"pathophysiology":"Splenosis develops when splenic pulp is disseminated during traumatic rupture or at splenectomy, seeding the peritoneal cavity, omentum, serosal surfaces of the bowel, mesentery and diaphragm, or the pleural space when the diaphragm is breached. The viable red pulp fragments implant on vascularised surfaces and induce neovascularisation, deriving a blood supply from the surrounding host tissue rather than from the splenic artery. The resulting nodules are histologically splenic and retain some filtering and immune capacity, but their total mass and disordered architecture usually provide only incomplete restoration of splenic function. Because they enlarge slowly and appear as multiple soft-tissue nodules, they frequently masquerade radiologically as metastatic or peritoneal malignancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Accessory spleen","Peritoneal carcinomatosis or metastatic deposits","Peritoneal mesothelioma","Endometriosis","Lymphoma or gastrointestinal stromal tumour","Pleural tumour or metastasis in thoracic splenosis","Hepatic adenoma or focal lesion when intrahepatic"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Technetium heat-damaged red cell or sulphur-colloid scintigraphy with SPECT/CT","Contrast-enhanced CT to map the nodules and their distribution","MRI, including superparamagnetic iron oxide-enhanced sequences exploiting reticuloendothelial uptake","Full blood count and peripheral blood film for Howell-Jolly bodies and other hyposplenic features","Documentation of prior splenic trauma or splenectomy in the history","Image-guided biopsy only where the diagnosis remains genuinely uncertain"],"redFlags":["Imaging features that cannot confidently be distinguished from malignancy and require exclusion","Gastrointestinal bleeding or bowel obstruction attributable to an implant","Haemoptysis or pleuritic symptoms in thoracic splenosis"],"prognosis":"Splenosis is benign and the great majority of patients remain asymptomatic for life, needing no treatment. The principal hazard is misdiagnosis as malignancy leading to unnecessary intervention.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting implants as malignancy and proceeding to needless biopsy or resection","Assuming splenosis fully restores immune function and omitting vaccination and prophylaxis","Not considering the diagnosis in a patient with prior splenic trauma and new peritoneal or pleural nodules","Confusing splenosis with a congenital accessory spleen"],"management":["No treatment for asymptomatic splenosis, which is benign and requires only recognition","Confirm the diagnosis non-invasively with technetium heat-damaged red cell scintigraphy, ideally with SPECT/CT, to avoid unnecessary biopsy or surgery","Avoid resection or biopsy unless the diagnosis remains genuinely uncertain or implants become symptomatic through pain, bleeding or obstruction","Because implants restore only partial splenic function, consider vaccination and infection prophylaxis appropriate to hyposplenism","Monitor the peripheral blood film for hyposplenic features such as Howell-Jolly bodies to gauge residual splenic function","Reserve surgical excision for genuinely symptomatic implants or complications"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grainger & Allison's Diagnostic Radiology","Postgraduate Haematology (Hoffbrand)"],"crossLinks":[]},"spondyloarthritis":{"name":"Spondyloarthritis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ankylosing spondylitis","axial spondyloarthritis","reactive arthritis","psoriatic arthritis"],"chapter":374,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2879-2892","clinicalPearls":["Spondyloarthritis (SpA) is a family of immune-mediated inflammatory arthritides sharing clinical, genetic, and pathologic features; classified by ASAS (2009) into axial (axSpA) and peripheral (pSpA). Axial skeletal involvement is hinted at in the name but is NOT mandatory (Harrison 22e p.2879).","axSpA is subdivided into radiographic (r-axSpA, = ankylosing spondylitis [AS]) and nonradiographic (nr-axSpA), the difference based on presence/absence of definitive sacroiliitis on plain radiographs; the two share the same clinical features and disease burden (Harrison 22e p.2880, 2883).","pSpA encompasses psoriatic arthritis (PsA), reactive arthritis (ReA), IBD-associated arthritis, and undifferentiated pSpA; SAPHO, acne-associated, and hidradenitis suppurativa-associated arthritis are sometimes included (Harrison 22e p.2880).","HLA-B27 is the strongest genetic risk factor: present in 85-90% of r-axSpA and 50-90% of nr-axSpA, versus ~5% of the healthy Caucasian population (Harrison 22e p.2881).","Inflammatory back pain (IBP): insidious onset, age <45, chronic (>3 months), improves with exercise/activity but NOT with rest, worse in second half of night with improvement after getting up, and prolonged (>30 min) morning stiffness; this distinguishes it from mechanical back pain (Harrison 22e p.2883).","Characteristic SpA pattern is asymmetric mono-/oligoarticular (<=4 joints) inflammatory arthritis of large lower-limb joints (usually knee) with enthesitis, tendonitis, and dactylitis (sausage digits), contrasting with the symmetric polyarticular MCP/PIP involvement of rheumatoid arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2879).","Hips are the most common nonspinal ('root') joints affected in axSpA; peripheral arthritis occurs in ~30-40% of axSpA patients (Harrison 22e p.2883).","Uveitis is the most common extramusculoskeletal manifestation (~40% of axSpA), typically acute, anterior, unilateral, and episodic; other extra-articular features include psoriasis (~10%) and IBD (frank IBD ~10%, subclinical gut inflammation up to 50%) (Harrison 22e p.2883-2884).","Enthesitis (inflammation at tendon/ligament insertions) is common in axSpA, presenting as heel pain (plantar fasciitis, Achilles insertion), chest wall pain, epicondylitis, or pelvic rim pain; dactylitis is relatively rare in axSpA but common in PsA (Harrison 22e p.2883).","Persistent axial inflammation leads to bony fusion of sacroiliac and apophyseal joints and syndesmophyte formation; complete ankylosis of the spine is termed 'bamboo spine,' seen in only a very small percentage of late-stage AS patients (Harrison 22e p.2880, 2884).","Typical SpA syndesmophytes are vertically oriented, distinguishing them from the horizontal osteophytes of spinal osteoarthritis; PsA syndesmophytes are often chunky and nonmarginal (Harrison 22e p.2884, 2889).","Reactive arthritis follows infectious gastroenteritis (Salmonella, Shigella, Yersinia, Campylobacter) or genitourinary Chlamydia; classic features include urethritis, conjunctivitis, mucosal ulcers, and keratoderma blennorrhagica (Harrison 22e p.2880).","IBD-associated arthritis: r-axSpA develops in up to 10% of IBD patients and pSpA in 10-30%; conversely axSpA patients have a 5-10% risk of developing IBD. HLA-B27 is NOT a risk factor for IBD itself (Harrison 22e p.2891-2892)."],"pathophysiology":"SpA is a prototypic chronic immune-mediated inflammatory disease that couples inflammation to structural damage, with the unique feature that inflammation-induced bone loss coexists with pathologic new bone formation at specific skeletal sites (sacroiliac joints, anterior spinal ligaments, peripheral entheses). HLA-B27, an MHC class I allele, is the strongest genetic risk factor; proposed mechanisms include the arthritogenic peptide theory (HLA-B27 presenting microbial peptides to CD8+ T cells with molecular mimicry), HLA-B27 homodimerization engaging innate receptors (KIR3DL2) to drive IL-17, and unfolded protein response/autophagy promoting IL-23-driven type 17 immunity. Disease requires additional factors beyond HLA-B27 (only ~5% of HLA-B27-positive people, and 20% of HLA-B27-positive first-degree relatives, develop axSpA); key instigators are loss of intestinal barrier integrity with microbial dysbiosis (germ-free HLA-B27 transgenic rats fail to develop disease) and aberrant response to mechanical stress. The IL-23/IL-17 axis and TNF-alpha are central, with overlapping yet domain-distinct cytokine signatures across gut, joint, entheses, and skin (Harrison 22e p.2880-2882).","additionalDifferentials":["Mechanical (nonspecific) back pain - the etiology in 95-96% of chronic back pain; axSpA accounts for only 4-5% (Harrison 22e p.2885)","Fibromyalgia / central sensitization (especially mistaken for SpA in women, delaying diagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.2885)","Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH) - noninflammatory, flowing osteophytes over >=4 contiguous vertebrae, normal sacroiliac joints, in obese diabetic males >50; can be mistaken for bamboo spine (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Osteitis condensans ilii (OCI) - asymptomatic triangular iliac-side sclerosis in multiparous women; lack of erosions distinguishes from axSpA (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Osteoarthritis of the spine - horizontal osteophytes vs vertical syndesmophytes (Harrison 22e p.2884)","Rheumatoid arthritis - symmetric polyarticular MCP/PIP disease, RF/nodules present (Harrison 22e p.2879)","Hypothyroidism and hypovitaminosis D in young patients with generalized body pain (Harrison 22e p.2886)"],"infectionMimics":["Septic sacroiliitis from tuberculosis, brucellosis, fungi, or other infectious agents - can cause inflammatory back pain with sclerotic sacroiliac changes (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Osteomyelitis (consider with red-flag features) (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Infectious enterocolitis-like acute gut inflammation seen histologically in SpA terminal ileum (Harrison 22e p.2891)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Postpartum sacral insufficiency fracture (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Osteoporotic vertebral fracture (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Metastatic disease / malignancy, including acute lymphoblastic leukemia presenting with inflammatory back pain and sacroiliac sclerosis (Harrison 22e p.2886)","MRI osteitis-like changes from mechanical stress in athletes, postpartum women, and normal individuals >40 (risk of overdiagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.2885)","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease (CPPD) and gout (may coexist with/mimic PsA) (Harrison 22e p.2889)","SAPHO and hidradenitis suppurativa (may mimic palmoplantar psoriasis/PsA) (Harrison 22e p.2889)"],"additionalInvestigations":["HLA-B27 typing - supportive but neither necessary nor sufficient for diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2884)","ESR and CRP - elevated in only 30-40% of active axSpA; neither sensitive nor specific, but useful for monitoring (Harrison 22e p.2884, 2888)","Plain AP or Ferguson pelvic radiograph for sacroiliitis (multiple oblique views add little and increase gonadal radiation) (Harrison 22e p.2884)","MRI of sacroiliac joints/spine - detects active osteitis/bone marrow edema (STIR) and structural lesions (sclerosis, fat metaplasia, erosions, backfill, ankylosis) early; not indicated for routine monitoring (Harrison 22e p.2885, 2888)","Low-dose CT of sacroiliac joints/spine - emerging alternative to radiography for diagnosis and progression (Harrison 22e p.2885)","Synovial fluid analysis in monoarthritis - WBC >2000/mL with negative crystals/Gram stain/culture suggests inflammatory arthritis; rules out septic and crystal arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2888)","Fecal calprotectin (elevated with microscopic gut inflammation in SpA) (Harrison 22e p.2891)","Disease activity indices: BASDAI, ASDAS; function: BASFI; metrology: BASMI (Harrison 22e p.2886)"],"redFlags":["Fever, weight loss, advanced age, or past malignancy in a back-pain patient - prompt evaluation for osteomyelitis, osteoporotic fracture, or metastatic disease (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Cauda equina syndrome (urinary hesitancy and/or saddle anesthesia) - late axSpA complication (Harrison 22e p.2884)","Cardiac conduction abnormalities (heart block) at any stage; aortic valve insufficiency as a late complication (Harrison 22e p.2884)","AA (renal) amyloidosis causing nephrotic syndrome after prolonged uncontrolled inflammation; IgA nephropathy with microscopic hematuria (Harrison 22e p.2884)","Spinal fractures in ankylosed (bamboo) spine - consider if back pain persists/relapses despite therapy (Harrison 22e p.2888)","Atlantoaxial subluxation (rare, in PsA) with potentially serious neurologic consequences (Harrison 22e p.2889)","Acute anterior uveitis (eye pain, redness, photophobia, miosis) requiring urgent ophthalmologic care (Harrison 22e p.2884)"],"prognosis":"Course is variable: ~50% of patients progress from nonradiographic to radiographic stage over 20 years, but <10% progress to significant spinal involvement with bamboo spine. Risk factors for osteoproliferation (worse structural outcome) include male sex, persistent inflammation (MRI sacroiliac/spine activity, high CRP), existing syndesmophytes, HLA-B27 positivity, and smoking. With increased awareness and earlier treatment, structural damage has steadily decreased over recent decades, with a shift toward higher prevalence of nr-axSpA over r-axSpA. axSpA significantly impairs well-being, function, productivity, and quality of life. Reactive arthritis is often self-limiting but may become chronic, with HLA-B27 linked to chronicity (Harrison 22e p.2880, 2884, 2891).","pitfalls":["PITFALL: confusing inflammatory back pain with common mechanical back pain - mechanical causes must be considered first since axSpA is the etiology in only 4-5% of chronic back pain; conversely IBP improves with exercise and worsens with rest, the reverse of mechanical pain (Harrison 22e p.2883, 2885).","The term 'inflammatory back pain' is itself a partial misnomer - only ~15% of patients with IBP have demonstrable axial inflammation (Harrison 22e p.2883).","Diagnostic delay of 5-14 years is common, worse in women (back pain/enthesitis mistaken for fibromyalgia) and in nonwhite populations with lower HLA-B27 prevalence (Harrison 22e p.2884).","Interreader reliability for distinguishing r-axSpA from nr-axSpA on radiographs is poor (kappa 0.35-0.45), even between trained musculoskeletal radiologists (Harrison 22e p.2883).","MRI overdiagnosis: osteitis-type and fat-metaplasia changes occur from mechanical stress in athletes, postpartum women, degenerative arthritis, and normal individuals >40 (Harrison 22e p.2885).","Normal/negative inflammatory markers do NOT exclude axSpA - ESR/CRP elevated in only 30-40% of active disease (Harrison 22e p.2884).","Plain spine radiographs should generally be avoided in chronic back pain; multiple oblique sacroiliac views add little but increase gonadal radiation (Harrison 22e p.2884).","Avoid glucocorticoid injection around lower-extremity weight-bearing entheses due to risk of tendon rupture; in IBD-associated arthritis anti-IL17 therapy is contraindicated and NSAIDs may trigger IBD flares (Harrison 22e p.2890-2892)."],"management":["Regular exercise and physiotherapy focused on spinal mobility, posture and function as the cornerstone of non-pharmacological care","NSAIDs as first-line pharmacotherapy for pain and stiffness in axial disease, used continuously where inflammation is persistent","Biologic therapy with TNF-alpha inhibitors or IL-17 inhibitors for active disease inadequately controlled by NSAIDs, with JAK inhibitors as a further option","Conventional synthetic DMARDs such as sulfasalazine or methotrexate for peripheral arthritis, and local corticosteroid injection for enthesitis or monoarthritis, as systemic corticosteroids are of limited value in axial disease","Screen for and manage extra-articular manifestations and comorbidities: uveitis with ophthalmology, psoriasis, inflammatory bowel disease, osteoporosis and cardiovascular risk","Monitor disease activity with composite indices and CRP and track structural progression, adjusting therapy to a treat-to-target approach","Multidisciplinary care with rheumatology and physiotherapy, and orthopaedic or surgical referral for advanced spinal deformity or hip disease"],"severityClassification":"axSpA is classified as radiographic (r-axSpA / ankylosing spondylitis) vs nonradiographic (nr-axSpA) using definitive sacroiliitis on plain radiographs per the modified New York criteria: definitive = grade 2 bilateral OR grade 3-4 unilateral or bilateral (r-axSpA); lower-grade sacroiliitis = nr-axSpA. ASAS criteria further split SpA into axial (axSpA) and peripheral (pSpA). Diagnosis of axSpA in chronic back pain follows the schema of Figure 374-2 (definite sacroiliitis on x-ray -> AS; otherwise count SpA features >=4 or compelling clinical picture plus MRI sacroiliitis/HLA-B27 -> nr-axSpA). Disease activity graded with BASDAI/ASDAS; PsA classified with CASPAR criteria, activity by DAPSA/MDA (Harrison 22e p.2883-2884, 2889).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2879-2892","Harrison 22e ch.374 (Deodhar & Elewaut)"],"crossLinks":[]},"spondylocostal_dysostosis":{"name":"Spondylocostal dysostosis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Jarcho-Levin syndrome","DLL3","MESP2","LFNG","HES7","TBX6","SCDO"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of disorders of vertebral segmentation, historically termed Jarcho-Levin syndrome, most often autosomal recessive with variants in Notch-pathway genes (DLL3, MESP2, LFNG, HES7) or TBX6","Characterised by multiple vertebral segmentation defects (hemivertebrae, block and butterfly vertebrae) distributed along the spine","Rib anomalies (fusions, bifid or absent ribs) accompany the vertebral defects and shorten the thorax","The combination produces a short trunk with a short neck and short-trunk disproportionate stature, while the limbs are relatively normal","The dominant clinical threat is thoracic insufficiency: a small, poorly compliant thorax that cannot support normal lung growth and ventilation","In DLL3-related disease the spine may show a characteristic 'pebble beach' or generalised vertebral disorganisation on radiographs","Distinguish from spondylothoracic dysostosis, in which symmetric posterior rib fusion gives a 'crab-like' or fan-shaped thorax"],"pathophysiology":"Spondylocostal dysostosis results from disruption of the Notch signalling pathway that drives the segmentation clock during somitogenesis, most commonly through recessive variants in DLL3, MESP2, LFNG or HES7, or from TBX6-related mechanisms. Faulty oscillatory gene expression disturbs the orderly formation and boundary specification of somites, the embryonic precursors of vertebrae and their associated ribs. The result is multiple, often widespread vertebral segmentation and rib anomalies. Because the axial skeleton and thorax form abnormally, chest volume and compliance are reduced, producing thoracic insufficiency and restrictive respiratory disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Spondylothoracic dysostosis","VACTERL association","Klippel-Feil syndrome","Isolated congenital scoliosis with hemivertebrae","Jeune asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy","Cerebro-costo-mandibular syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Whole-spine and chest radiographs to characterise vertebral and rib segmentation defects","Molecular genetic testing of the segmentation-pathway genes (DLL3, MESP2, LFNG, HES7, TBX6)","Pulmonary function and thoracic dimension assessment for thoracic insufficiency","Cross-sectional imaging to plan any spinal or thoracic surgery","Screening for associated anomalies (renal, cardiac) given overlap with wider malformation associations"],"redFlags":["Respiratory distress or failure from thoracic insufficiency, especially in infancy","Rapidly progressive scoliosis compromising lung development","Signs of spinal cord involvement or neurological deficit","Recurrent respiratory infections indicating restrictive lung disease"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends chiefly on the severity of thoracic insufficiency: milder cases have good survival with orthopaedic management of the spine, whereas severe early respiratory compromise carries significant mortality. Multidisciplinary respiratory and orthopaedic care is central.","pitfalls":["Focusing on the scoliosis while underestimating the life-limiting thoracic insufficiency","Confusing spondylocostal with spondylothoracic dysostosis, which differ in rib pattern and prognosis","Missing associated visceral anomalies by not screening beyond the skeleton","Assuming dominant inheritance when most forms are autosomal recessive, giving incorrect counselling"],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy; management centres on respiratory support and orthopaedic care of the spine and thorax","Respiratory surveillance and support for thoracic insufficiency, including non-invasive ventilation as restrictive disease progresses","Expansion thoracoplasty or growth-friendly instrumentation such as vertical expandable prosthetic titanium rib-type devices to enlarge the thorax and permit lung growth in severe cases","Orthopaedic management of scoliosis and spinal deformity with bracing or corrective surgery as indicated","Prompt treatment of respiratory infections and immunisation to protect compromised lung function","Screen for associated renal and cardiac anomalies given overlap with wider malformation associations","Multidisciplinary paediatric respiratory, orthopaedic and clinical genetics care","Genetic counselling for the family given autosomal recessive inheritance in most subtypes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Spondylocostal Dysostosis, Autosomal Recessive"],"crossLinks":[]},"spondyloenchondrodysplasia":{"name":"Spondyloenchondrodysplasia","system":"Immunology / Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPENCD","Spondyloenchondrodysplasia with immune dysregulation","ACP5 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive immuno-osseous dysplasia caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in ACP5, which encodes tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (TRAP).","Also classified as a monogenic type I interferonopathy, which accounts for the prominent autoimmunity and intracranial calcification.","The skeletal hallmark is platyspondyly with metaphyseal and vertebral radiolucent lesions representing islands of ectopic cartilage, causing disproportionate short stature and spinal deformity.","Neurological features include spasticity, developmental delay, cognitive impairment and basal ganglia calcification.","Immune dysregulation is common, with systemic lupus erythematosus, autoimmune thrombocytopenia, haemolytic anaemia and thyroiditis, alongside variable susceptibility to infection.","Deficient TRAP activity leads to accumulation of phosphorylated osteopontin, mechanistically linking the bone and immune phenotypes.","A raised blood interferon signature supports the diagnosis.","The radiolucent skeletal lesions are not true enchondromas and should not be mistaken for Ollier disease."],"pathophysiology":"Spondyloenchondrodysplasia results from biallelic loss-of-function variants in ACP5, encoding tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (TRAP), an enzyme expressed in osteoclasts, dendritic cells and macrophages. TRAP normally dephosphorylates osteopontin, a phosphoprotein that regulates bone mineralisation and remodelling and modulates immune-cell activation. Loss of TRAP activity leads to accumulation of the highly phosphorylated form of osteopontin, disturbing the coordinated activity of osteoclasts and chondrocytes so that persistent islands of cartilage remain within the metaphyses and vertebrae, producing the enchondromatous, platyspondylic skeletal phenotype. In parallel, dysregulated osteopontin signalling and a dominant type I interferon response drive loss of self-tolerance, autoantibody production and intracranial calcification, accounting for the coexistent lupus-like autoimmunity, cytopenias and progressive neurological disease that characterise the disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome (true enchondromatosis)","Other metaphyseal chondrodysplasias","Schimke immuno-osseous dysplasia","Aicardi-Goutieres syndrome (intracranial calcification with interferon signature)","Systemic lupus erythematosus of other cause","Other spondylometaphyseal dysplasias","Metabolic bone disease with metaphyseal changes such as rickets"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey demonstrating platyspondyly with metaphyseal and vertebral lucencies","Molecular genetic testing for ACP5 variants","Serum tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase activity","Neuroimaging for basal ganglia and other intracranial calcification","Autoantibody panel including ANA and anti-dsDNA with a direct antiglobulin test","Full blood count to detect autoimmune cytopenias","Blood interferon signature"],"redFlags":["Severe autoimmune cytopenias such as thrombocytopenia or haemolytic anaemia","Lupus nephritis or other major organ autoimmunity","Progressive neurological deterioration or worsening spasticity","Serious or recurrent infection suggesting associated immunodeficiency"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends chiefly on the severity of autoimmune and neurological involvement. Skeletal disease causes short stature and spinal deformity, while lupus-like autoimmunity and progressive neurological impairment drive long-term morbidity.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the enchondroma-like lesions as Ollier disease.","Diagnosing isolated systemic lupus erythematosus and missing the underlying monogenic cause.","Overlooking intracranial calcification as an incidental finding.","Attributing the short stature to a non-syndromic skeletal dysplasia."],"management":["Immunosuppression for the autoimmune manifestations, with corticosteroids and steroid-sparing agents for lupus-like disease and autoimmune cytopenias","JAK inhibition to target the type I interferon response underlying the interferonopathy","Treat autoimmune haemolytic anaemia and thrombocytopenia, including immunotherapy, and manage autoimmune thyroiditis with hormone replacement as needed","Orthopaedic management of platyspondyly, spinal deformity and disproportionate short stature","Neurological care for spasticity with antispasticity agents and developmental support, monitoring basal ganglia and intracranial calcification","Surveillance with autoantibody panels, blood counts and neuroimaging for progression of autoimmune and neurological disease, with immunisation and infection prevention given variable immunodeficiency","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive ACP5 inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Skeletal dysplasia and clinical genetics reference sources","Paediatric rheumatology and clinical immunology textbooks","Interferonopathy and inborn errors of immunity reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"spondyloepimetaphyseal_dysplasia":{"name":"Spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia","system":"Skeletal dysplasia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SEMD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Skeletal dysplasia references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["SEMD is a heterogeneous family of skeletal dysplasias defined by combined radiographic involvement of the vertebrae (spondylo-), the epiphyses and the metaphyses.","It is separated from spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia (spine plus epiphyses) and spondylometaphyseal dysplasia (spine plus metaphyses) by the simultaneous epiphyseal and metaphyseal changes.","The Strudwick type is caused by COL2A1 (type II collagen) mutations and shows the characteristic dappled or mottled metaphyses.","Subtypes are increasingly gene-defined: MMP13 (Missouri type), ACAN (aggrecan type), DDR2 (short limb-hand/abnormal calcification type) and PAPSS2 (Pakistani type).","The joint-laxity forms are distinct: the leptodactylic type from KIF22 and the Beighton type from proteoglycan-linker gene B3GALT6.","Disproportionate short stature (often short-trunk), scoliosis, and progressive joint and hip problems are common across the group.","Odontoid hypoplasia and atlantoaxial instability may occur, particularly in the type II collagen forms — screen before anaesthesia or contact activity.","Diagnosis combines detailed radiographic phenotyping with targeted or panel-based genetic testing; inheritance varies by subtype (dominant, recessive or X-linked)."],"pathophysiology":"Spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia is not a single disease but a group unified by radiographic involvement of the spine, epiphyses and metaphyses, in which each subtype has a distinct molecular basis affecting cartilage matrix or growth-plate biology. The Strudwick type arises from dominant mutations in COL2A1, disturbing type II collagen and producing dappled metaphyses. The Missouri type is caused by mutations in MMP13 (matrix metalloproteinase-13) that impair collagen turnover at the growth plate. Other forms include the aggrecan type from ACAN; the short limb-hand type with abnormal calcification from DDR2 (a collagen-activated receptor tyrosine kinase); and the Pakistani type from PAPSS2, which is required for the sulphate activation that underpins proteoglycan sulphation. The joint-laxity subtypes include the leptodactylic type from KIF22 (a kinesin involved in chromosome segregation and chondrocyte function) and the Beighton type from B3GALT6, a glycosyltransferase in proteoglycan-linker biosynthesis. The shared theme is disruption of the cartilaginous growth plate and of epiphyseal and metaphyseal ossification, but the causative gene determines the precise phenotype, inheritance and recurrence risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita and tarda (COL2A1; X-linked TRAPPC2) — spine and epiphyses without metaphyseal change","Spondylometaphyseal dysplasia, e.g., Kozlowski type (TRPV4) — spine and metaphyses without epiphyseal change","Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia (COMP, MATN3, type IX collagen)","Pseudoachondroplasia (COMP)","Metaphyseal chondrodysplasias (COL10A1 Schmid; RMRP McKusick)","Dyggve-Melchior-Clausen syndrome (DYM)","Achondrogenesis and hypochondrogenesis (COL2A1) at the severe end of the type II collagen spectrum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full skeletal survey including lateral spine and skull to characterise the spondylo-, epi- and metaphyseal features","Flexion-extension cervical spine imaging to assess odontoid hypoplasia and atlantoaxial instability","Molecular genetic testing by targeted gene, skeletal dysplasia panel or exome","Ophthalmology review for myopia and retinal detachment risk in type II collagen disorders","Serial growth monitoring with disproportion measurements (sitting height, arm span)","Audiology assessment where syndromic overlap is suspected","Family segregation studies and a detailed pedigree to define inheritance"],"redFlags":["Signs of cervical myelopathy from atlantoaxial instability","Rapidly progressive scoliosis with respiratory compromise","Acute visual symptoms suggesting retinal detachment in type II collagen forms","Peri-anaesthetic risk from unrecognised cervical instability during airway management"],"prognosis":"The outlook is highly variable by subtype: many people achieve independent function with disproportionate short stature, while orthopaedic complications such as scoliosis, early joint degeneration and cervical instability drive morbidity. Life expectancy is usually normal in the common types.","pitfalls":["Lumping all SEMD together and missing the specific gene diagnosis that guides prognosis and recurrence risk","Overlooking cervical instability before anaesthesia or before permitting contact activity","Missing ocular complications in the type II collagen (COL2A1) disorders","Confusing the dappled metaphyses of the Strudwick type with rickets or osteomyelitis"],"management":["No disease-specific pharmacotherapy; management is orthopaedic, functional and surveillance-based across the subtypes","Orthopaedic management of scoliosis, hip disease and joint problems, including bracing and joint-preserving or replacement surgery","Assess and stabilise cervical instability from odontoid hypoplasia and atlantoaxial laxity before anaesthesia or contact activity","Ophthalmology surveillance for myopia and retinal detachment in the type II collagen (COL2A1) subtypes","Serial growth monitoring with disproportion measurements, and physiotherapy to maintain mobility and joint function","Audiology assessment where syndromic overlap is suspected","Multidisciplinary clinical genetics and skeletal dysplasia care","Genetic counselling tailored to the inheritance of the specific gene-defined subtype"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nosology and classification of genetic skeletal disorders","Clinical genetics reference texts","Paediatric skeletal radiology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"spondyloepiphyseal_dysplasia":{"name":"Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["COL2A1","SED","SEDC","spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A type II collagenopathy in which short stature is predominantly truncal (short trunk) rather than rhizomelic, reflecting the prominent spinal involvement","The congenita form is caused by autosomal dominant variants in COL2A1 and is usually evident at birth, whereas the tarda form presents in later childhood","Associated features include a short neck, barrel chest, lumbar lordosis, and a waddling gait, with the hands and feet relatively spared","Ophthalmological complications, notably high myopia and retinal detachment, and sensorineural hearing loss are important extraskeletal associations","Radiographs show platyspondyly, delayed epiphyseal ossification, small irregular epiphyses, and hypoplasia of the odontoid predisposing to atlantoaxial instability","Odontoid hypoplasia with atlantoaxial instability is a key hazard requiring cervical assessment before anaesthesia and contact activity","Diagnosis integrates the radiographic pattern with COL2A1 molecular testing and consideration of the wider type II collagenopathy spectrum"],"pathophysiology":"Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita is a disorder of type II collagen, the principal collagen of cartilage and the vitreous, caused by dominant variants in COL2A1. Defective type II collagen impairs the cartilage template for endochondral ossification, particularly affecting the vertebral bodies and epiphyses, producing platyspondyly, short trunk, and epiphyseal dysplasia. Because type II collagen is also a major component of the vitreous and inner ear, affected individuals are prone to myopia, retinal detachment, and hearing loss, placing the condition within the broader spectrum of type II collagenopathies.","additionalDifferentials":["Morquio syndrome and other mucopolysaccharidoses","Kniest dysplasia (also COL2A1-related)","Stickler syndrome","Pseudoachondroplasia","Metatropic dysplasia","Spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey demonstrating platyspondyly, epiphyseal changes, and odontoid hypoplasia","Flexion-extension cervical spine imaging to assess atlantoaxial instability","COL2A1 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Ophthalmological review for myopia and retinal detachment risk","Audiological assessment for sensorineural hearing loss","Spinal imaging for scoliosis, kyphosis, or canal stenosis"],"redFlags":["Cervical myelopathy from atlantoaxial instability, particularly around anaesthesia","Sudden visual change suggesting retinal detachment","Progressive scoliosis with respiratory compromise","Neurological deficit from spinal cord compression"],"prognosis":"Intelligence and lifespan are typically normal, but adult stature is markedly reduced and morbidity arises from spinal deformity, premature osteoarthritis, cervical instability, and ocular complications that require lifelong surveillance.","pitfalls":["Overlooking odontoid hypoplasia and precipitating cervical cord injury during anaesthesia","Failing to arrange ophthalmology follow-up, risking preventable retinal detachment","Confusing it with a mucopolysaccharidosis without appropriate metabolic and genetic testing","Assuming limb-predominant shortening and missing the truncal pattern"],"management":["No disease-modifying drug therapy; management is supportive, orthopaedic and surveillance-based","Cervical spine assessment for odontoid hypoplasia and atlantoaxial instability, with stabilisation before anaesthesia or contact activity","Orthopaedic management of spinal deformity such as scoliosis and kyphosis, canal stenosis and premature hip osteoarthritis, including joint replacement","Ophthalmological surveillance for high myopia and retinal detachment, with timely retinal intervention","Audiological assessment and management of sensorineural hearing loss","Physiotherapy and mobility support for the waddling gait and short-trunk stature","Multidisciplinary clinical genetics and skeletal dysplasia follow-up with lifelong surveillance","Genetic counselling given autosomal dominant COL2A1 inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"spondyloepiphyseal_dysplasia_congenita":{"name":"Spondyloepiphyseal Dysplasia Congenita","system":"Skeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SEDC","SED congenita","Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenital type"],"chapter":null,"source":"Skeletal dysplasia references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A type II collagenopathy caused by COL2A1 mutations, usually autosomal dominant, with many cases arising de novo.","Presents at birth with disproportionate short-trunk short stature, a barrel-shaped chest and a short neck.","Radiographs show platyspondyly with delayed, irregular ossification of the vertebral bodies, femoral heads, pubis and knee epiphyses.","Odontoid hypoplasia and ligamentous laxity predispose to atlantoaxial instability and cervical myelopathy; assess the cervical spine before any anaesthetic or surgery.","High myopia carries a lifelong risk of retinal detachment and warrants regular ophthalmology review.","Cleft palate, sensorineural hearing loss and muscular hypotonia may accompany the skeletal features.","Coxa vara, a waddling gait, exaggerated lumbar lordosis and progressive kyphoscoliosis are characteristic.","Intelligence and facial appearance are typically normal, although the midface may look flat."],"pathophysiology":"Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita results from heterozygous mutations in COL2A1, which encodes the alpha-1 chain of type II collagen, the predominant collagen of hyaline cartilage, the vitreous of the eye and the nucleus pulposus. Most mutations act through a dominant-negative mechanism, so structurally abnormal collagen trimers disrupt the cartilage extracellular matrix and disorganise the growth plate. Impaired endochondral ossification produces the delayed and irregular epiphyseal and vertebral ossification that defines the disorder, while the same defective collagen in ocular, auditory and other connective tissues explains the myopia, vitreoretinal degeneration and hearing loss. The condition lies on a phenotypic spectrum of type II collagenopathies extending from lethal achondrogenesis type II and hypochondrogenesis, through Kniest dysplasia, to the milder Stickler syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Kniest dysplasia and other type II collagenopathies","Morquio syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type IV)","Spondylometaphyseal and spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasias","Pseudoachondroplasia and multiple epiphyseal dysplasia","Achondroplasia and hypochondroplasia","Metatropic dysplasia","Stickler syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey with attention to the spine, pelvis and long-bone epiphyses","COL2A1 molecular genetic testing","Flexion-extension cervical spine radiographs and MRI to assess atlantoaxial stability and cord compression","Formal ophthalmological assessment including retinal examination for myopia and detachment risk","Audiometry for sensorineural hearing loss","Assessment of the palate for clefting and, where relevant, evaluation for sleep-disordered breathing"],"redFlags":["New limb weakness, spasticity, gait deterioration or sphincter disturbance suggesting cervical cord compression","Sudden visual loss, floaters or flashing lights indicating retinal detachment","Respiratory compromise in infancy from a small thorax or cervical instability","Rapidly progressive scoliosis with cardiorespiratory impact"],"prognosis":"Intelligence and life expectancy are usually normal, but morbidity arises from cervical instability, early osteoarthritis of the hips and spine, progressive scoliosis, and visual and hearing complications; many affected individuals require joint replacement or spinal surgery in adult life.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for atlantoaxial instability before general anaesthesia or surgery, risking catastrophic cord injury","Misdiagnosing the condition as a mucopolysaccharidosis such as Morquio syndrome","Overlooking progressive myopia and retinal detachment, leading to preventable visual loss","Attributing the hypotonia to a primary neuromuscular disorder rather than to the dysplasia"],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy; management is supportive and orthopaedic with structured surveillance","Cervical spine assessment with flexion-extension imaging and MRI for odontoid hypoplasia and atlantoaxial instability, with fusion or stabilisation and precautions before any anaesthetic or surgery","Orthopaedic management of scoliosis and early hip and spinal osteoarthritis, often requiring joint replacement or spinal surgery in adult life","Ophthalmology surveillance for high myopia and retinal detachment","Audiometry and management of sensorineural hearing loss, with assessment and repair of cleft palate","Evaluate for sleep-disordered breathing and support muscular hypotonia with physiotherapy","Multidisciplinary clinical genetics, orthopaedic, ophthalmology and ENT care","Genetic counselling given autosomal dominant COL2A1 inheritance, noting that many cases arise de novo"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics (standard reference)","GeneReviews (standard reference)","International skeletal dysplasia classification references"],"crossLinks":[]},"spondylolisthesis":{"name":"Spondylolisthesis","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["isthmic spondylolisthesis","degenerative spondylolisthesis","vertebral slip"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Spondylolisthesis is forward (or occasionally backward) slippage of one vertebra on the one below, most often at the lumbosacral junction","Isthmic slips relate to a defect in the pars interarticularis (spondylolysis), often from repetitive extension stress in young athletes, while degenerative slips occur in older adults from facet and disc degeneration","Many slips are asymptomatic; when symptomatic, patients report low back pain, hamstring tightness, and sometimes radicular leg pain or neurogenic claudication","Severity is commonly graded by the degree of slip relative to the vertebral body width, which helps guide monitoring and management","Standing lateral and flexion-extension radiographs are useful to demonstrate the slip and any dynamic instability","Most cases are managed conservatively with activity modification, physiotherapy focusing on core stabilisation, and analgesia","Surgery, typically decompression with or without fusion, is considered for persistent disabling symptoms, neurological deficit, or progressive high-grade slips","High-grade slips in the skeletally immature warrant closer surveillance because of the potential for progression"],"pathophysiology":"Spondylolisthesis is displacement of one vertebra relative to the adjacent vertebra, usually anterior translation of the upper on the lower body. In isthmic disease a stress-related defect or fracture of the pars interarticularis (spondylolysis) allows the anterior elements to slip forward. In degenerative disease, age-related disc narrowing and facet joint arthropathy reduce the segment's stability and permit gradual slippage, typically without a pars defect. The malalignment can narrow the central canal and neural foramina, tethering or compressing nerve roots and, in higher grades, causing back pain and radicular or claudicant symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Mechanical low back pain","Lumbar disc herniation","Lumbar spinal stenosis without slip","Vertebral fracture or pathological lesion","Sacroiliac joint dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Standing lateral radiographs to demonstrate and grade the slip","Flexion-extension views to assess dynamic instability","MRI to evaluate neural compression, disc, and root involvement","CT or oblique views to characterise a pars defect when suspected","Bone imaging (e.g. SPECT or MRI) in young athletes for a suspected active pars stress injury"],"redFlags":["New or progressive neurological deficit or radiculopathy","Cauda equina symptoms such as saddle anaesthesia or sphincter disturbance","High-grade or progressive slip, particularly in a young or skeletally immature patient","Severe unremitting pain or features suggesting infection or malignancy"],"prognosis":"Most low-grade, degenerative, and isthmic slips remain stable and respond well to conservative care; higher-grade slips and those with neurological compromise are more likely to need surgery, which generally improves symptoms though progression can occur in growing patients.","pitfalls":["Relying on supine imaging, which can underestimate a slip that is only apparent when standing","Missing dynamic instability by omitting flexion-extension views","Overlooking a pars stress injury in a young athlete with extension-related back pain","Attributing all symptoms to the slip without excluding coexisting stenosis or disc pathology"],"management":["Conservative management for most low-grade slips with activity modification, physiotherapy focused on core stabilisation, and analgesia","Analgesia with NSAIDs and simple analgesics, with epidural or nerve-root corticosteroid injection for radicular symptoms","Bracing and activity restriction in young patients with an active pars lesion, monitoring growing patients for slip progression","Surgical decompression and instrumented fusion for high-grade slips, progressive slippage, or neurological compromise unresponsive to conservative care","Serial standing and flexion-extension radiographs to monitor slip grade and dynamic instability","Referral to spinal surgery for red-flag neurology, cauda equina features or refractory symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"spondylolysis":{"name":"Spondylolysis (pars defect)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Spine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pars interarticularis defect","pars defect","pars interarticularis stress fracture","isthmic defect"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A defect, either a stress fracture or a fibrous non-union, of the pars interarticularis, most often at L5","A common cause of low back pain in adolescent athletes engaged in repetitive hyperextension such as gymnastics, cricket fast bowling, dancing, and diving","Pain is typically activity-related axial low back pain that is worsened by lumbar extension","The single-leg hyperextension (stork) test may reproduce pain on the affected side","On oblique radiographs the defect appears as a collar or broken neck on the outline of the Scotty dog","A bilateral defect may allow the vertebral body to slip forward on the one below (isthmic spondylolisthesis)","SPECT or SPECT/CT and MRI detect early, radiographically occult stress reactions and help assess fracture activity and healing potential","Most respond to activity restriction, physiotherapy, and sometimes bracing; surgical repair or fusion is reserved for refractory pain or a progressive slip"],"pathophysiology":"Spondylolysis is a defect of the pars interarticularis, the bony bridge linking the superior and inferior articular processes of a vertebra. It usually represents a fatigue (stress) fracture from repetitive hyperextension and rotational loading rather than an acute injury, and there is a recognised genetic and dysplastic predisposition. The lowest lumbar level (L5) bears the greatest shear stress and is most often affected. When the defect is bilateral the vertebral body can slip forward on the one below, producing isthmic spondylolisthesis, which may in turn narrow the foramen and place the exiting nerve root under tension.","additionalDifferentials":["Isthmic or degenerative spondylolisthesis","Facet joint syndrome","Degenerative disc (discogenic) back pain","Muscular or ligamentous strain","Sacroiliac joint dysfunction","Vertebral osteomyelitis or tumour when red flags are present"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs including oblique views (Scotty-dog sign) and lateral views to assess any slip","MRI as the first-line advanced imaging in young patients to detect a stress reaction and avoid radiation","SPECT or SPECT/CT to assess the metabolic activity and healing potential of the defect","CT to define the bony anatomy and characterise chronicity of the defect","Standing flexion-extension radiographs to assess dynamic instability when a slip is present"],"redFlags":["Neurological deficit or radicular symptoms suggesting nerve-root compression from a high-grade slip","Rapidly progressive slip on serial imaging","Constitutional symptoms or night pain suggesting infection or tumour"],"prognosis":"Many young patients achieve pain-free return to activity with conservative management, and early stress reactions may heal with rest; high-grade or progressive slips and persistent pain may require surgical stabilisation, but outcomes are generally good.","pitfalls":["Relying on plain radiographs alone, which miss early stress reactions","Failing to monitor for slip progression during adolescent growth","Attributing all adolescent back pain to spondylolysis without excluding other causes"],"management":["Activity modification and relative rest from the provocative hyperextension loading as first-line management","Physiotherapy with core and lumbar stabilisation once acute pain settles, with analgesia for symptom control","Bracing with an antilordotic orthosis to promote healing of an active pars stress reaction in adolescents","Graded, supervised return to sport once pain-free, with monitoring of healing on imaging","Surgical repair or segmental fusion reserved for persistent pain despite prolonged conservative care or progression to symptomatic isthmic spondylolisthesis","Surveillance for slip development with standing lateral and flexion-extension radiographs when the defect is bilateral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","Herring: Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":["degenerative_disc_disease","facet_joint_syndrome"]},"spondylometaphyseal_dysplasia_kozlowski":{"name":"Spondylometaphyseal dysplasia, Kozlowski type","system":"Skeletal dysplasia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SMDK","TRPV4-related spondylometaphyseal dysplasia","Kozlowski dysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Skeletal dysplasia references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant spondylometaphyseal dysplasia that is typically normal at birth and presents in early childhood with progressive short-trunk short stature and a waddling gait.","Caused by heterozygous, usually gain-of-function variants in TRPV4; many cases arise de novo, though dominant transmission occurs.","The radiographic hallmark is generalised platyspondyly with characteristically 'overfaced' (oversized) pedicles, together with prominent metaphyseal irregularity that is most marked at the proximal femora.","Delayed and dysplastic ossification of the femoral capital epiphyses can closely mimic bilateral Perthes disease or a metaphyseal chondrodysplasia.","Progressive kyphoscoliosis and pectus carinatum are common, so early spinal surveillance is important.","TRPV4 is strikingly pleiotropic: the allelic skeletal spectrum runs from autosomal dominant brachyolmia through this condition to spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia (Maroteaux type) and metatropic dysplasia, while other TRPV4 variants cause neuromuscular disease such as Charcot-Marie-Tooth type 2C and scapuloperoneal or congenital spinal muscular atrophy.","Intelligence and lifespan are typically normal, with morbidity being predominantly orthopaedic.","Cervical spine involvement can occur, so instability should be assessed before anaesthesia or spinal manipulation."],"pathophysiology":"Spondylometaphyseal dysplasia, Kozlowski type is caused by heterozygous, typically gain-of-function variants in TRPV4, which encodes a calcium-permeable transient receptor potential cation channel expressed in chondrocytes. Enhanced or dysregulated channel activity alters intracellular calcium signalling, chondrocyte differentiation and the tempo of endochondral ossification, producing platyspondyly, metaphyseal irregularity and dysplastic femoral capital epiphyses. TRPV4 is remarkably pleiotropic: variants distributed along a genotype-phenotype continuum cause a family of skeletal dysplasias — autosomal dominant brachyolmia, this Kozlowski condition, spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia of the Maroteaux type and metatropic dysplasia — as well as distinct peripheral neuropathies and motor neuronopathies, so the same gene can generate either bone or nerve disease depending on the specific allele.","additionalDifferentials":["Other spondylometaphyseal dysplasias (for example the corner-fracture / Sutcliffe type)","Metaphyseal chondrodysplasia, Schmid type","Mucopolysaccharidosis, especially Morquio syndrome (MPS IVA)","Spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia (including the allelic TRPV4 Maroteaux type)","Pseudoachondroplasia","Bilateral Legg-Calve-Perthes disease","Autosomal dominant brachyolmia (the milder allelic TRPV4 phenotype)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full skeletal survey covering spine, pelvis and metaphyses","TRPV4 sequencing or a skeletal dysplasia gene panel","Standing spine radiographs to detect and monitor scoliosis and kyphosis","Hip imaging to assess the femoral capital epiphyses","Growth monitoring with segmental (trunk-to-limb) disproportion measurements","Cervical spine imaging where instability is suspected","Genetic counselling addressing autosomal dominant inheritance and de novo occurrence"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive scoliosis or kyphosis","Cervical instability with risk of cord compression","Neurological signs suggesting myelopathy","Restrictive respiratory compromise from chest and spinal deformity"],"prognosis":"Intelligence and lifespan are generally normal; individuals have disproportionate short-trunk short stature with orthopaedic complications including scoliosis, hip disease and early osteoarthritis.","pitfalls":["Labelling the femoral head changes as bilateral Perthes disease and missing the underlying dysplasia","Assuming an autosomal recessive metaphyseal disorder rather than a dominant TRPV4 condition","Underestimating cervical spine instability before anaesthesia","Failing to recognise the wider TRPV4 spectrum, including neuromuscular phenotypes, when counselling"],"management":["No disease-modifying drug therapy exists; care is orthopaedic and supportive, centred on the platyspondyly, hip dysplasia and disproportionate short stature","Regular standing spinal radiographic surveillance for progressive kyphoscoliosis and pectus carinatum, with bracing or corrective spinal fusion when curves progress","Orthopaedic management of hip disease, including osteotomy and later joint replacement for painful dysplastic femoral capital epiphyses and early osteoarthritis","Monitor for cervical spine instability, with imaging and surgical stabilisation if there is threat to the cord","Physiotherapy, analgesia and mobility aids to address the waddling gait, joint pain and functional limitation","Multidisciplinary input from clinical genetics, paediatric orthopaedics, spinal surgery and physiotherapy","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant TRPV4 disorder, discussing frequent de novo occurrence and family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International nosology and classification of genetic skeletal disorders","Standard clinical genetics and dysmorphology reference texts","Paediatric skeletal radiology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"spontaneous_coronary_artery_dissection":{"name":"Spontaneous coronary artery dissection","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCAD","spontaneous coronary artery dissection","non-atherosclerotic coronary dissection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease / ESC Guidelines on Acute Coronary Syndromes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A non-atherosclerotic cause of acute coronary syndrome in which a coronary intramural haematoma or intimal tear compresses the true lumen","Disproportionately affects women, particularly in the peripartum period, and is an important cause of myocardial infarction in younger patients without conventional risk factors","Strongly associated with fibromuscular dysplasia, and with connective tissue disorders, extreme emotional or physical stress, and hormonal factors","Presents like other acute coronary syndromes with chest pain and troponin rise, and may cause arrhythmia or sudden death","Coronary angiography is diagnostic, though the characteristic appearances can be subtle and intracoronary imaging with OCT or IVUS may be needed to confirm","A conservative strategy is preferred in stable patients because most dissections heal spontaneously and intervention carries a high risk of propagating the dissection","Screening for fibromuscular dysplasia in other vascular beds is recommended after the diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Spontaneous coronary artery dissection occurs when blood accumulates within the coronary arterial wall, either through a tear in the intima or through spontaneous haemorrhage from the vasa vasorum, creating a false lumen or intramural haematoma. The expanding haematoma compresses the true lumen and restricts coronary flow, producing myocardial ischaemia or infarction without the atherosclerotic plaque rupture that underlies most acute coronary syndromes. The arterial wall is thought to be predisposed by an underlying arteriopathy, most commonly fibromuscular dysplasia, rendering it vulnerable to hormonal influences and to shear stress from emotional or physical triggers, which explains the strong female and peripartum predominance. Because the primary problem is a wall haematoma rather than a fixed plaque, the vessel often heals over weeks, and mechanical instrumentation risks extending the dissection.","additionalDifferentials":["Atherosclerotic acute coronary syndrome","Coronary vasospasm","Coronary embolism","Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy","Myocarditis","Coronary vasculitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Invasive coronary angiography as the primary diagnostic modality","Intracoronary imaging with optical coherence tomography or intravascular ultrasound when angiography is equivocal","ECG and serial troponin to characterise the acute coronary syndrome","Echocardiography to assess regional wall motion and ventricular function","Screening imaging for fibromuscular dysplasia in renal, cerebral, and iliac arteries","Consideration of connective tissue disorder assessment in selected patients"],"redFlags":["Ongoing ischaemia, haemodynamic instability, or cardiogenic shock","Ventricular arrhythmia or aborted sudden cardiac death","Left main or proximal multivessel dissection","Peripartum presentation with high-risk anatomy"],"prognosis":"Most stable patients managed conservatively do well, with spontaneous angiographic healing over subsequent weeks, though there is a recognised risk of recurrence, particularly in the context of underlying arteriopathy. High-risk anatomy or haemodynamic instability worsens the short-term outlook and may necessitate intervention despite its hazards.","pitfalls":["Defaulting to routine stenting, which can propagate the dissection and worsen outcomes in stable patients","Missing the diagnosis by assuming a young low-risk patient cannot have an acute coronary syndrome","Overlooking subtle angiographic appearances without using intracoronary imaging","Failing to screen for fibromuscular dysplasia and other arteriopathies after diagnosis","Neglecting the elevated recurrence risk when counselling and following up patients"],"management":["Favour a conservative strategy in stable patients as most dissections heal spontaneously, reserving percutaneous coronary intervention or bypass surgery for ongoing ischaemia, haemodynamic instability or left main disease","Beta-blocker therapy to reduce arterial wall shear stress and the risk of recurrence, with treatment of any coexistent hypertension","Avoid thrombolysis, which risks extending the intramural haematoma, and individualise antiplatelet therapy given the absence of atherosclerotic thrombus","Continuous cardiac monitoring in the acute phase with defibrillation and resuscitation readiness given the risk of arrhythmia and sudden death","Cardiac rehabilitation with avoidance of intense isometric exertion and extreme emotional or physical triggers, and counselling around future pregnancy and hormonal therapy","Screen for and manage associated fibromuscular dysplasia and connective tissue disease, including imaging of renal, cerebral and iliac arteries","Specialist cardiology follow-up with a low threshold for repeat angiography given the recognised recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease","ESC Guidelines for the Management of Acute Coronary Syndromes"],"crossLinks":[]},"spontaneous_intracranial_hypotension":{"name":"Spontaneous intracranial hypotension","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["spontaneous spinal CSF leak","low CSF pressure headache","CSF hypovolaemia syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by a non-iatrogenic spinal cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leak leading to low CSF volume, and an important, treatable secondary cause of headache","The hallmark is an orthostatic headache that develops or worsens within minutes of standing or sitting and is relieved by lying flat, although the postural pattern can become less obvious over time","Associated symptoms include neck stiffness and pain, nausea, tinnitus, muffled hearing or aural fullness, photophobia, dizziness and interscapular pain","Underlying causes include a dural tear (often from a calcified disc or osteophyte), a meningeal diverticulum, or a CSF-venous fistula, and connective tissue disorders such as Marfan and Ehlers-Danlos syndromes predispose","Contrast-enhanced brain MRI classically shows diffuse smooth pachymeningeal enhancement, brain sagging, venous distension, subdural fluid collections and pituitary engorgement, summarised by the SEEPS mnemonic","Lumbar puncture opening pressure is often low but may be normal, so diagnosis rests on the clinical picture together with imaging rather than pressure measurement alone","Spinal imaging with CT or MR myelography, and dynamic or digital subtraction myelography, is used to localise the leak and in particular to detect a CSF-venous fistula","First-line treatment is conservative with bed rest, hydration and caffeine, followed by epidural blood patch, with targeted patching, fibrin glue or surgical or endovascular repair for refractory or localised leaks"],"pathophysiology":"A breach in the spinal dura allows CSF to escape into the epidural space or to drain into a vein as a CSF-venous fistula, reducing CSF volume. Because the cranium is a closed space, loss of CSF buoyancy allows the brain to descend, placing traction on pain-sensitive dura, bridging veins and cranial nerves and generating the headache and associated symptoms. In accordance with the Monro-Kellie doctrine, which holds that total intracranial volume is fixed, compensatory venous and dural engorgement produces the pachymeningeal enhancement, venous distension and pituitary enlargement seen on imaging. Low CSF volume, rather than pressure alone, is the key abnormality.","additionalDifferentials":["Post-dural-puncture (iatrogenic) low-pressure headache","Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome and other orthostatic intolerance","Migraine and tension-type headache","Cervicogenic headache","Meningitis or meningeal carcinomatosis as other causes of dural enhancement","Subdural haematoma, which may itself be a complication","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis or raised intracranial pressure, which produce the opposite postural pattern"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced brain MRI for pachymeningeal enhancement, brain sagging, subdural collections and pituitary engorgement","Spinal MRI for extradural CSF collections and dilated nerve root sleeves","CT myelography or dynamic or digital subtraction myelography to localise the leak or a CSF-venous fistula","Lumbar puncture with opening pressure, which may be low or normal, and CSF analysis if infection or inflammation is considered","Heavily T2-weighted spinal imaging to help detect a CSF leak","Assessment for an underlying connective tissue disorder"],"redFlags":["Deteriorating consciousness, a new focal deficit or seizures, which may indicate subdural haematoma or severe brain sagging","Features suggesting infection such as fever and meningism with systemic upset","Rapidly evolving brainstem signs from marked brain descent, which is a neurosurgical emergency","Development of a large or bilateral subdural haematoma","Thunderclap onset requiring exclusion of subarachnoid haemorrhage and other causes"],"prognosis":"Many patients improve with conservative measures or one or more epidural blood patches, and outcomes are generally good once the leak is treated. A subset have refractory leaks, particularly CSF-venous fistulas, requiring targeted or surgical intervention, and untreated severe cases can rarely progress to brain sagging with serious neurological deterioration.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because the lumbar puncture opening pressure is normal, which it often is","Misinterpreting diffuse pachymeningeal enhancement as meningitis or carcinomatosis","Not recognising that a longstanding leak may lose its clear postural character","Overlooking a CSF-venous fistula, which is missed by conventional myelography and needs a dynamic or lateral decubitus technique","Attributing an associated subdural haematoma to trauma and draining it without treating the underlying CSF leak"],"management":["Initial conservative management with bed rest, oral and intravenous hydration and caffeine to help restore CSF volume and relieve the orthostatic headache","Epidural blood patch as the mainstay when conservative measures fail, repeated as needed to seal the spinal CSF leak","Targeted percutaneous fibrin sealant, transvenous embolisation of a CSF-venous fistula, or surgical repair of a dural tear or meningeal diverticulum in refractory cases","Localise the leak with CT or dynamic/digital subtraction myelography before targeted intervention","Analgesia and antiemetics for symptomatic relief of headache, nausea and associated symptoms","Monitor for rebound intracranial hypertension after leak closure and for complications such as subdural haematoma from brain sagging","Assess and manage underlying connective tissue disorders such as Marfan and Ehlers-Danlos syndromes, with neurology and neurosurgery referral for refractory disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (ICHD-3)","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"sporotrichosis":{"name":"Sporotrichosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rose gardener's disease","Sporothrix schenckii infection","lymphocutaneous sporotrichosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India NTEP","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A subacute-to-chronic infection caused by the dimorphic fungus Sporothrix, acquired through traumatic inoculation of soil, thorns, sphagnum moss or plant material into the skin","The classic lymphocutaneous form begins as a papule or nodule at the inoculation site that ulcerates, followed by a chain of nodules tracking along lymphatic channels (sporotrichoid spread)","A fixed cutaneous form remains localised at the inoculation site without lymphatic progression","Occupational and recreational exposure among gardeners, farmers and forestry workers is a key historical clue","Extracutaneous disease, including osteoarticular, pulmonary and disseminated forms, occurs mainly in immunocompromised or alcoholic patients","Diagnosis is confirmed by fungal culture of pus, tissue or aspirate, with histopathology sometimes showing yeast forms and asteroid bodies","Itraconazole is the preferred agent for cutaneous and lymphocutaneous disease, with amphotericin-based therapy reserved for severe, disseminated or meningeal infection; saturated potassium iodide remains a low-cost option in some settings"],"pathophysiology":"Sporothrix species exist as mould in the environment and convert to a yeast form at body temperature after traumatic inoculation into skin or subcutaneous tissue. A localised suppurative and granulomatous (mixed pyogranulomatous) inflammatory response develops. The organism spreads proximally along lymphatic vessels, producing successive nodules. In immunocompromised hosts or after inhalation, haematogenous or pulmonary dissemination can occur, involving bones, joints, lungs and rarely the meninges. Cell-mediated immunity is central to containment, explaining severe disease in those with impaired T-cell function.","additionalDifferentials":["Cutaneous nocardiosis (sporotrichoid pattern)","Atypical mycobacterial infection (e.g. Mycobacterium marinum)","Cutaneous leishmaniasis","Tularaemia","Bacterial lymphangitis or cutaneous tuberculosis"],"infectionMimics":["Mycobacterium marinum fish-tank granuloma","Nocardia brasiliensis lymphocutaneous infection","Cutaneous leishmaniasis","Cutaneous tuberculosis (scrofuloderma or lupus vulgaris)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fungal culture of aspirate, exudate or tissue as the diagnostic gold standard","Histopathology of biopsy for granulomatous inflammation and yeast forms with asteroid bodies","Imaging of bone and joints when osteoarticular disease is suspected","Chest imaging in suspected pulmonary involvement","Assessment of immune status including HIV testing in disseminated disease"],"redFlags":["Disseminated or multifocal disease suggesting significant immunosuppression","Pulmonary involvement with cavitation or systemic symptoms","Osteoarticular disease with joint destruction","Meningeal involvement, which is rare but serious and difficult to treat"],"prognosis":"Lymphocutaneous and fixed cutaneous disease respond well to prolonged azole therapy with an excellent outcome, whereas disseminated, pulmonary and meningeal forms carry a much poorer prognosis and require aggressive treatment, particularly in immunocompromised hosts.","pitfalls":["Stopping antifungal therapy too early before lesions have fully resolved, risking relapse","Overlooking the inoculation history and misdiagnosing bacterial infection","Failing to investigate for immunosuppression in disseminated cases","Not distinguishing sporotrichoid spread from other causes of nodular lymphangitis"],"management":["Itraconazole as first-line therapy for lymphocutaneous and fixed cutaneous disease, continued for weeks beyond clinical resolution","Amphotericin B for severe disseminated, pulmonary or meningeal disease, particularly in immunocompromised hosts, with step-down to itraconazole once stabilised","Saturated potassium iodide solution as an alternative for cutaneous disease where azoles are unsuitable or unavailable","Local hyperthermia as an option for cutaneous disease in pregnancy, where systemic antifungals are contraindicated","Surgical debridement or drainage as an adjunct in osteoarticular or cavitary pulmonary disease","Assess and optimise immune status, including HIV testing and management, given the risk of dissemination","Advise protective gloves and clothing during gardening, forestry and handling of soil or sphagnum moss to prevent re-inoculation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/NTEP guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"sprengel_deformity":{"name":"Sprengel deformity","system":"Genetics / Craniofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["congenital high scapula","congenital elevation of the scapula","Sprengel shoulder","scapula elevata"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest congenital anomaly of the shoulder girdle, in which one scapula sits abnormally high and is often small and malrotated","The elevated scapula produces visible asymmetry, a fullness at the base of the neck, and restricted shoulder abduction","An omovertebral bone or fibrous band connecting the scapula to the cervical spine is present in many cases and further limits movement","It is frequently associated with Klippel-Feil syndrome, scoliosis, and rib anomalies, so the cervical spine should always be assessed","The condition is usually unilateral but can be bilateral, and most cases are sporadic","Limitation of shoulder elevation, rather than pain, is the usual functional complaint","Cosmetic concern and functional restriction together guide the decision on surgical correction"],"pathophysiology":"Sprengel deformity results from failure of the normal caudal descent of the scapula during embryonic development. The scapula forms in the cervical region and normally migrates to its thoracic position by around the third month of gestation; arrested or incomplete descent leaves it elevated, hypoplastic, and often medially rotated. An abnormal osseous, cartilaginous, or fibrous connection (the omovertebral bar) may tether the superomedial scapula to the lower cervical vertebrae, tethering it in position and restricting movement. Because the disturbance occurs during the same period as cervical vertebral segmentation and rib formation, associated anomalies such as Klippel-Feil fusion, scoliosis, and rib defects frequently coexist. Most cases are sporadic.","additionalDifferentials":["Klippel-Feil syndrome with scapular involvement","Congenital muscular torticollis with postural asymmetry","Acquired winging of the scapula","Postural scapular asymmetry from scoliosis","Poland syndrome with shoulder girdle involvement","Neuromuscular scapular dyskinesis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Shoulder and scapular radiographs to assess position, size, and rotation","Cervical spine imaging to detect Klippel-Feil fusion and an omovertebral bone","Computed tomography to define the omovertebral connection before surgery","Assessment for scoliosis and rib anomalies","Renal ultrasound where associated anomalies suggest a wider spectrum","Assessment of shoulder range of motion and function"],"redFlags":["Coexisting cervical vertebral instability from associated Klippel-Feil syndrome","Neurological compromise related to cervical or brachial plexus involvement","Severe functional restriction limiting activities of daily living","Progressive scoliosis requiring orthopaedic intervention","Associated undiagnosed renal or cardiac anomalies"],"prognosis":"The deformity itself is not life-limiting; mild cases need only observation, while surgical repositioning in selected children can improve appearance and shoulder function, with outcome influenced by associated spinal and girdle anomalies.","pitfalls":["Failing to image the cervical spine and missing associated Klippel-Feil syndrome","Overlooking an omovertebral bone that limits movement and complicates surgery","Attributing the asymmetry to posture or torticollis","Neglecting associated scoliosis and rib anomalies","Underestimating the psychosocial effect of visible shoulder asymmetry"],"management":["Observation with physiotherapy for mild deformity, focusing on maintaining and improving shoulder abduction and range of motion","Surgical repositioning of the scapula (such as a Woodward or Green procedure) in selected children to improve appearance and shoulder function","Excision of the omovertebral bone or fibrous band to relieve tethering and restricted movement","Assess and manage associated cervical spine anomalies (Klippel-Feil), scoliosis and rib defects","Screen for associated renal and other anomalies where a wider malformation spectrum is suspected","Multidisciplinary input from paediatric orthopaedics, spinal surgery and physiotherapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"sptan1_encephalopathy":{"name":"SPTAN1-related developmental and epileptic encephalopathy (alpha-II spectrin encephalopathy)","system":"Neurology / Metabolic genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPTAN1","EIEE5","alpha-II spectrin encephalopathy","West syndrome due to SPTAN1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["SPTAN1 encodes the non-erythrocytic alpha-II spectrin that forms the sub-membranous cytoskeletal scaffold of neurons and axons; in-frame dominant variants exert a dominant-negative effect on the spectrin heterodimer.","The severe classic phenotype presents in early infancy with an epileptic encephalopathy, frequently West syndrome with infantile spasms and hypsarrhythmia evolving to intractable seizures.","A highly suggestive imaging triad is progressive/acquired microcephaly, diffuse cerebral and brainstem atrophy, and widespread hypomyelination.","Affected infants typically show profound developmental delay or arrest, spastic quadriparesis and, in many, no purposeful hand use.","The clustering of in-frame deletions/duplications in the C-terminal region encoding the alpha/beta-spectrin nucleation site correlates with the most severe disease.","Milder or intermediate presentations exist, including generalised or focal epilepsy with less prominent atrophy and preserved head growth, particularly with certain missense or truncating variants.","Nystagmus, cortical visual impairment and dysphagia requiring feeding support are common in the severe form.","The disorder is genetically distinct from erythrocyte spectrin (SPTA1) disease and is not associated with haemolytic anaemia."],"pathophysiology":"SPTAN1 encodes alpha-II spectrin (non-erythrocytic alpha-spectrin), a large cytoskeletal protein that pairs with beta-spectrin to build the flexible sub-plasma-membrane lattice that stabilises the neuronal membrane, organises the axon initial segment and nodes of Ranvier, and supports axonal transport and synaptic organisation. The severe developmental and epileptic encephalopathy is characteristically caused by heterozygous in-frame deletions or duplications in the C-terminal domain that mediates alpha-beta spectrin heterodimer nucleation; the mutant protein still incorporates into the tetramer but disrupts its assembly, producing pathological aggregation of alpha-II and beta-II/III spectrins and a dominant-negative failure of the axonal cytoskeleton. This impairs myelination and axonal integrity, giving the radiological hallmark of hypomyelination with progressive cerebral, cerebellar and brainstem atrophy and acquired microcephaly. Variants that cause haploinsufficiency (truncating or missense) or that fall outside the nucleation region tend to produce milder phenotypes, so the position and type of variant are important determinants of severity.","additionalDifferentials":["STXBP1, CDKL5, KCNQ2 and ARX developmental and epileptic encephalopathies presenting with infantile spasms","Other genetic causes of West syndrome and early-infantile epileptic encephalopathy","Hypomyelinating leukodystrophies (e.g. PLP1/Pelizaeus-Merzbacher, TUBB4A-related)","Pontocerebellar hypoplasias with progressive atrophy and microcephaly","Congenital disorders of glycosylation presenting with encephalopathy and atrophy","Perinatal hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy (acquired mimic of atrophy and microcephaly)","Mitochondrial encephalopathies with regression and brainstem involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Trio exome or genome sequencing with careful assessment of in-frame indels in the C-terminal domain (copy-number/breakpoint analysis may be needed)","Brain MRI to demonstrate hypomyelination, cerebral/brainstem atrophy and acquired microcephaly, with follow-up imaging to show progression","Electroencephalography looking for hypsarrhythmia or multifocal epileptiform activity","Serial occipitofrontal head-circumference monitoring","Ophthalmology and visual electrophysiology for cortical visual impairment and nystagmus","Swallow assessment and nutritional review for dysphagia","Deletion/duplication (CNV) analysis if sequencing is non-diagnostic but phenotype is characteristic"],"redFlags":["Onset of infantile spasms or evolution to intractable seizures/status epilepticus","Progressive deceleration of head growth with developmental arrest or regression","Worsening bulbar function with aspiration risk","Rapid radiological progression of atrophy suggesting an alternative neurodegenerative process to reconsider","Acute clinical deterioration during intercurrent illness"],"prognosis":"The severe in-frame variant phenotype carries a poor prognosis with profound intellectual disability, spastic quadriparesis, drug-resistant epilepsy and shortened life expectancy, whereas milder variant classes can be associated with better developmental outcomes and more controllable epilepsy. Management is supportive and multidisciplinary.","pitfalls":["Confusing SPTAN1 (neuronal alpha-II spectrin) with SPTA1 (erythrocyte alpha-spectrin); SPTAN1 disease does not cause hereditary spherocytosis or haemolysis.","Interpreting the hypomyelination and atrophy as a primary leukodystrophy and stopping the genetic work-up prematurely.","Missing pathogenic in-frame deletions/duplications because standard variant filtering can overlook small copy-number changes.","Assuming all SPTAN1 variants are severe; not every variant produces the classic devastating encephalopathy, so genotype should guide counselling.","Mistaking the picture for fixed perinatal brain injury and failing to recognise the progressive, acquired microcephaly."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists; management centres on seizure control, with hormonal therapy (corticotrophin or corticosteroids) or vigabatrin for infantile spasms and West syndrome","Antiseizure medication regimens for the drug-resistant epilepsy, with a ketogenic diet considered in refractory cases","Nutritional support and management of dysphagia, including gastrostomy feeding where swallowing is unsafe","Management of spasticity with physiotherapy, orthoses and antispasticity agents for the spastic quadriparesis","Ophthalmology input and visual support for cortical visual impairment and nystagmus","Multidisciplinary developmental, physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy input, with palliative care planning given the poor prognosis","Genetic counselling for this dominant SPTAN1 disorder, noting frequent de novo occurrence, with family testing as appropriate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of paediatric neurology","Neurogenetics and inherited metabolic disease reference","Reviews of early-infantile developmental and epileptic encephalopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"squamous_cell_carcinoma_skin":{"name":"Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cutaneous SCC","SCC of skin","squamous cell cancer of skin"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents as an enlarging, firm, hyperkeratotic or crusted nodule or plaque on sun-exposed skin, often tender and prone to ulceration or bleeding","It frequently arises within a field of sun damage, from a pre-existing actinic keratosis, or from Bowen's disease (in-situ SCC)","Unlike basal cell carcinoma, cutaneous SCC carries a genuine, if modest, risk of regional and distant metastasis, so early diagnosis matters","High-risk features include large or thick tumours, poorly differentiated histology, perineural invasion, and sites such as the ear, lip, and non-sun-exposed areas","It arises with particularly high frequency and aggressiveness in immunosuppressed patients, especially organ transplant recipients, and in areas of chronic wounds, scars, or prior radiotherapy","Examine and, where indicated, image the draining lymph nodes as part of staging","Surgical excision with adequate margins is the mainstay, with margin-controlled surgery, radiotherapy, or systemic therapy for high-risk or advanced disease decided in a multidisciplinary setting","Emphasise sun protection, treatment of field damage, and regular surveillance, and consider reducing immunosuppression in transplant patients where feasible"],"pathophysiology":"Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma is a malignant proliferation of epidermal keratinocytes that have acquired full-thickness atypia and then breached the basement membrane to invade the dermis. Cumulative ultraviolet radiation is the dominant driver, producing signature mutations including in TP53 and other tumour-suppressor and oncogene pathways, with additional contributions from immunosuppression, human papillomavirus in some tumours, chronic inflammation or scarring, and carcinogen exposure. Progression typically follows a continuum from dysplastic keratinocytes (actinic keratosis) through in-situ disease (Bowen's) to invasive carcinoma. Invasive nests of atypical keratinocytes with variable keratinisation can track along nerves and invade lymphatics, giving the potential for metastasis that distinguishes it from basal cell carcinoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Basal cell carcinoma","Keratoacanthoma (rapidly growing crateriform lesion on the SCC spectrum)","Actinic keratosis or Bowen's disease (pre-invasive lesions)","Amelanotic melanoma","Viral wart or hypertrophic actinic damage","Cutaneous metastasis or adnexal tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic skin biopsy to confirm invasion and assess grade, depth, and perineural involvement","Clinical examination and, when indicated, ultrasound or cross-sectional imaging of regional lymph nodes","Staging imaging for large, high-risk, or node-positive tumours","Multidisciplinary review for high-risk and advanced disease","Assessment of immunosuppression status, particularly in transplant recipients"],"redFlags":["A rapidly enlarging, ulcerating, or bleeding lesion, especially at high-risk sites such as the lip or ear","Palpable regional lymphadenopathy or new neurological symptoms suggesting perineural or nodal spread","A tumour arising in a chronic wound, scar, or previously irradiated skin (potential for aggressive behaviour)","Multiple or aggressive tumours in an immunosuppressed or transplant patient"],"prognosis":"Most low-risk cutaneous SCCs are cured by complete excision, but prognosis worsens with high-risk features such as large size, deep invasion, poor differentiation, perineural involvement, high-risk sites, and immunosuppression, which raise the likelihood of recurrence and metastasis; early detection and adequate treatment are key to good outcomes.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the metastatic potential and treating cutaneous SCC as casually as basal cell carcinoma","Failing to biopsy and assess an indurated or growing lesion within a field of actinic damage","Omitting lymph node examination and staging in high-risk tumours","Not recognising the heightened risk and aggressive course in immunosuppressed and transplant patients"],"management":["Complete surgical excision with adequate margins as the mainstay, with Mohs micrographic surgery for high-risk tumours and sites such as the ear, lip and face","Radiotherapy as primary treatment where surgery is unsuitable, or as adjuvant therapy for perineural invasion or incomplete excision","Curettage and cautery or destructive/topical field therapy for low-risk in-situ disease (Bowen's) and actinic keratoses","Nodal assessment with lymph node dissection, and systemic immune checkpoint inhibitors (PD-1 blockade), for advanced, unresectable or metastatic disease","Regular skin and lymph node surveillance for recurrence and new primaries, with staging imaging in high-risk cases","Photoprotection and sun-avoidance advice to reduce ultraviolet-driven new tumours","Review and reduce immunosuppression where possible in transplant recipients, in liaison with the transplant team","Multidisciplinary review for high-risk, node-positive and advanced disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"srd5a3_cdg":{"name":"SRD5A3-CDG (CDG-Iq)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SRD5A3","steroid 5-alpha-reductase 3 deficiency","polyprenol reductase deficiency","congenital disorder of glycosylation type Iq","CDG-Iq"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A dolichol-synthesis CDG caused by deficiency of the polyprenol reductase encoded by SRD5A3, needed to generate dolichol for glycosylation","Characterised by prominent ophthalmological involvement, including optic nerve and retinal anomalies, coloboma, nystagmus and visual impairment","Neurological features include developmental delay, hypotonia, cerebellar signs and sometimes seizures","Ichthyosiform skin changes and variable dysmorphism may be present","As a defect upstream in dolichol production, it impairs lipid-linked oligosaccharide assembly and gives a type I transferrin pattern","Management is supportive, with early ophthalmological and developmental input; no specific corrective therapy exists","Diagnosis is confirmed by SRD5A3 molecular genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"SRD5A3 encodes polyprenol reductase, which converts polyprenol to dolichol, the lipid carrier required to build the oligosaccharide precursor for N-glycosylation and other glycan pathways. Reduced dolichol availability limits assembly of the lipid-linked oligosaccharide, so N-linked sites are under-occupied and transferrin shows a type I pattern. The strikingly ocular-predominant phenotype reflects the sensitivity of eye development to glycosylation, while widespread hypoglycosylation accounts for the neurological, cutaneous and dysmorphic features.","additionalDifferentials":["Other dolichol-pathway and type I CDG subtypes (e.g. DOLK-CDG, DPM1-CDG)","Isolated congenital ocular malformation syndromes (coloboma, optic nerve hypoplasia)","Peroxisomal and mitochondrial disorders with visual involvement","Congenital ichthyosis syndromes","Cerebellar malformation and ataxia syndromes","Other causes of infantile visual impairment with developmental delay"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transferrin isoelectric focusing or mass spectrometry showing a type I pattern","SRD5A3 molecular genetic testing","Detailed ophthalmological assessment including retinal and optic nerve evaluation","Neuroimaging for cerebellar and structural anomalies","Coagulation and liver function studies","Developmental and skin assessment"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual loss or severe congenital ocular malformation","Developmental regression or worsening cerebellar signs","Refractory seizures","Failure to thrive with multisystem involvement"],"prognosis":"Visual impairment is often significant and may progress, while neurodevelopmental outcome varies with disease severity; overall prognosis depends on the burden of neurological and systemic complications. There is no treatment that corrects the underlying defect.","pitfalls":["Managing the eye findings in isolation without recognising an underlying glycosylation disorder","Misattributing the transferrin abnormality to secondary causes","Overlooking the coagulopathy before surgery","Delaying molecular testing when a type I pattern accompanies ocular anomalies"],"management":["No therapy corrects the underlying dolichol-synthesis defect; management is supportive and multidisciplinary","Early and ongoing ophthalmological care for optic nerve and retinal anomalies, coloboma and nystagmus, with correction of refractive error and low-vision support","Developmental support with physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy, and management of hypotonia","Antiseizure medication where seizures occur, alongside management of cerebellar signs","Emollient skin care for the ichthyosiform changes","Monitor coagulation and liver function given the risk of glycosylation-related coagulopathy and hepatic involvement","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, with family screening and reproductive options"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID, dolichol pathway disorders of glycosylation","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics, disorders of glycosylation"],"crossLinks":[]},"st3gal5_cdg":{"name":"ST3GAL5-CDG (GM3 Synthase Deficiency)","system":"Metabolic / Glycosylation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GM3 synthase deficiency","Salt and pepper syndrome","Amish infantile epilepsy syndrome","ST3GAL5"],"chapter":null,"source":"Congenital disorders of glycosylation references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive defect of ganglioside biosynthesis caused by biallelic ST3GAL5 variants encoding GM3 synthase (lactosylceramide alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase).","Presents in early infancy with intractable epilepsy, marked irritability and arrest then regression of development, progressing to profound intellectual disability.","A distinctive later feature is cutaneous dyspigmentation in a salt-and-pepper pattern of hyper- and hypopigmented macules, giving the eponymous salt-and-pepper syndrome.","Acquired sensorineural deafness and cortical visual impairment are common, and a movement disorder with choreoathetosis and dystonia develops.","First delineated in the Old Order Amish as the Amish infantile epilepsy syndrome, it is now recognised across several populations.","Because the block is in glycosphingolipid (ganglioside) synthesis rather than protein N-glycosylation, serum transferrin isoelectric focusing is usually normal, so a normal CDG screen does not exclude it.","Feeding difficulty with faltering growth and stagnant head circumference (acquired microcephaly) is typical."],"pathophysiology":"ST3GAL5 encodes GM3 synthase, the sialyltransferase that transfers sialic acid onto lactosylceramide to form GM3, the obligate precursor of the a- and b-series complex gangliosides. Loss-of-function variants abolish GM3 synthesis and deplete the downstream gangliosides that are heavily enriched in neuronal and melanocyte membranes and that are essential for synaptic signalling, receptor modulation and cell-cell recognition, while precursor and alternative-series glycolipids accumulate. Profound deficiency of complex gangliosides in the developing brain produces the severe infantile epileptic encephalopathy and progressive neurodegeneration, and disturbed melanocyte glycolipids account for the characteristic salt-and-pepper dyspigmentation.","additionalDifferentials":["ST3GAL3-related epileptic encephalopathy and intellectual disability (a related sialyltransferase defect)","B4GALNT1 deficiency (hereditary spastic paraplegia SPG26, with overlapping ganglioside-series disturbance)","Other early-infantile epileptic encephalopathies (CDKL5, STXBP1, KCNQ2)","GM1 and GM2 gangliosidoses and neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses (progressive neuroregression with seizures)","Menkes disease and other neurodegenerative disorders with pigmentary or hair changes","Peroxisomal and mitochondrial encephalopathies of infancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of ST3GAL5 with an epileptic-encephalopathy or CDG gene panel, or exome sequencing","Plasma glycosphingolipid and ganglioside profiling (reduced GM3 and downstream species)","EEG (often multifocal epileptiform activity or hypsarrhythmia-like patterns)","Brain MRI (progressive cerebral atrophy)","Serum transferrin isoelectric focusing (typically normal, helping to exclude N-glycosylation CDGs)","Ophthalmology and audiology assessment, including visual evoked potentials and brainstem auditory responses"],"redFlags":["Onset of refractory seizures with developmental arrest in early infancy","Loss of previously acquired skills (neuroregression)","Progressive loss of vision and hearing","Feeding failure with aspiration risk and faltering growth"],"prognosis":"The disorder is severe and progressive, producing profound and permanent neurodisability; many affected children have a markedly shortened lifespan, and care is supportive with a focus on seizure control, feeding and comfort.","pitfalls":["Relying on a normal transferrin CDG screen to exclude the diagnosis, as ganglioside defects are not detected by that test.","Attributing the picture to a channelopathy epileptic encephalopathy and stopping the genetic work-up before ST3GAL5 is tested.","Overlooking the diagnostic value of the salt-and-pepper dyspigmentation, which may appear only later in the course.","Misreading acquired microcephaly and regression as a static encephalopathy."],"management":["No disease-specific therapy exists; care is supportive with a focus on seizure control, feeding and comfort","Antiseizure medications for the intractable infantile epilepsy","Nutritional support and management of feeding difficulties, including gastrostomy where needed","Management of the movement disorder with choreoathetosis and dystonia, and of marked irritability","Audiology support for acquired sensorineural deafness and ophthalmology/visual support for cortical visual impairment","Multidisciplinary developmental, physiotherapy and palliative care given the progressive neurodegeneration","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, with family screening, noting the founder variant in the Old Order Amish"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Physician's Guide to the Diagnosis, Treatment and Follow-up of Inherited Metabolic Diseases","Congenital disorders of glycosylation and glycosphingolipid biosynthesis reviews","Paediatric neurology and neurometabolic reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"st_segment_elevation_myocardial_infarction":{"name":"ST-Segment Elevation Myocardial Infarction","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STEMI","Acute ST-elevation MI","Q-wave myocardial infarction","Transmural myocardial infarction"],"chapter":286,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2113","clinicalPearls":["STEMI typically arises when coronary flow drops abruptly after a thrombus forms over an atherosclerotic plaque that has eroded or ruptured; slowly progressive high-grade stenoses less often cause STEMI because collaterals develop over time (Harrison 22e p.2113).","Plaques prone to disruption tend to have a large lipid core and a thin fibrous cap, yet fewer than 5% of such thin-capped fibroatheromas actually become the nidus for an acute MI during long-term follow-up (Harrison 22e p.2113).","Deep, visceral chest pain described as heavy, squeezing, or crushing, usually central and/or epigastric and lasting longer than angina, is the most common presentation; it may radiate to the arms, back, lower jaw, or neck but not below the umbilicus (Harrison 22e p.2114).","Pain is not universal: painless STEMI is more frequent in patients with diabetes mellitus and increases with age, and the event may instead present as sudden breathlessness, syncope, confusion, profound weakness, arrhythmia, peripheral embolism, or an unexplained fall in blood pressure (Harrison 22e p.2114).","Anterior infarction may produce sympathetic overactivity (tachycardia/hypertension), whereas inferior infarction may produce parasympathetic overactivity (bradycardia/hypotension) (Harrison 22e p.2114).","Substernal chest pain lasting more than 30 minutes combined with diaphoresis strongly suggests STEMI (Harrison 22e p.2114).","The 12-lead ECG is the pivotal triage tool: total epicardial coronary occlusion produces ST-segment elevation, and most patients presenting with ST elevation ultimately evolve Q waves (Harrison 22e p.2115).","Cardiac troponin T and I are the preferred biomarkers when measured with high-sensitivity assays and may rise to roughly 20-50 times the upper reference limit in classic MI, remaining elevated for at least 7-10 days after STEMI (Harrison 22e pp.2115-2116).","Because urgent reperfusion decisions rest on clinical and ECG findings, high-sensitivity troponin is of less immediate value in confirmed STEMI than in the broader chest-pain workup (Harrison 22e p.2116).","Wall-motion abnormalities on two-dimensional echocardiography are almost universal in STEMI and aid triage, but acute infarction cannot be distinguished from old scar or severe acute ischemia by echo alone (Harrison 22e p.2116).","The two major classes of complications determining prognosis in the primary-PCI era are electrical (arrhythmias) and mechanical (pump failure); most out-of-hospital STEMI deaths result from sudden ventricular fibrillation (Harrison 22e p.2117).","Temperature can rise up to 38 degrees C during the first week, and a nonspecific leukocytosis (often 12,000-15,000/microL) appears within hours and persists 3-7 days, with the ESR rising more slowly and peaking in the first week (Harrison 22e pp.2114, 2116)."],"pathophysiology":"STEMI usually results from abrupt thrombotic occlusion of a coronary artery already affected by atherosclerosis. Vascular injury promoted by smoking, hypertension, lipid accumulation, and inflammation leads to disruption of an atherosclerotic plaque by erosion or rupture, exposing its contents to blood under conditions favoring thrombogenesis. A platelet monolayer forms first; agonists such as collagen, ADP, epinephrine, and serotonin activate platelets, releasing thromboxane A2 (a local vasoconstrictor) and converting the glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptor to a high-affinity state that binds fibrinogen and cross-links platelets. Exposure of tissue factor activates the coagulation cascade (factors VII and X), generating thrombin, which converts fibrinogen to fibrin; an autoamplifying thrombin reaction culminates in an occlusive thrombus of platelet aggregates and fibrin. The extent of necrosis depends on the territory supplied, completeness and duration of occlusion, collateral supply, myocardial oxygen demand, endogenous thrombolysis, and adequacy of reperfusion. Microvascular dysfunction can produce no-reflow. Healing proceeds through acute (first hours-7 days), healing (7-28 days), and healed (29+ days) stages, with polymorphonuclear leukocytes then proinflammatory and reparative monocytes driving clearance, angiogenesis, extracellular matrix production, scar formation, and ventricular remodeling (Harrison 22e pp.2113-2116).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute pericarditis (radiation of discomfort to the trapezius favors pericarditis and is not seen in STEMI)","Pulmonary embolism","Acute aortic dissection","Costochondritis","Gastrointestinal disorders (the xiphoid/epigastric location can mimic indigestion)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute pericarditis","Pulmonary embolism","Acute aortic dissection","Costochondritis","Esophageal and other gastrointestinal causes of chest pain","Type 2 MI from oxygen supply-demand imbalance unrelated to acute atherothrombosis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial 12-lead ECG to detect ST-segment elevation and evolution of Q waves","Cardiac troponin T or I (high-sensitivity assays preferred); a rise and/or fall with at least one value above the 99th percentile upper reference limit defines myocardial injury/infarction","CK and CK-MB historically used; a CK-MB mass to CK activity relative index of 2.5 or greater suggests but is not diagnostic of myocardial origin, and CK-MB's rapid decline can help detect early reinfarction","Two-dimensional echocardiography for regional wall-motion abnormalities, LV function, RV infarction, ventricular aneurysm, pericardial effusion, LV thrombus, and Doppler detection of VSD and mitral regurgitation","Radionuclide myocardial perfusion imaging (e.g., 99mTc-sestamibi) shows a 'cold spot' but cannot distinguish acute infarct from chronic scar"],"redFlags":["Sudden development of ventricular fibrillation, the leading cause of out-of-hospital STEMI death, with most such deaths occurring within the first 24 hours and over half within the first hour","Hypotension or unexplained drop in arterial pressure","Sudden loss of consciousness, confusional state, or profound weakness as an atypical STEMI presentation","New apical systolic murmur suggesting mitral valve apparatus dysfunction, a serious mechanical complication","Inferior infarction with elevated jugular venous pressure, clear lungs, and hypotension suggesting right ventricular infarction","Pericardial friction rub in transmural STEMI","Signs of pump failure / cardiogenic shock and mechanical complications such as ventricular septal defect"],"prognosis":"About half of AMI-related deaths occur before the patient reaches the hospital. In-hospital mortality after admission for AMI has fallen from roughly 10% to about 5%, and the 1-year mortality after AMI is approximately 15%. Mortality is roughly fourfold higher in patients older than 75 years than in younger patients. Up to one-third of STEMI patients achieve spontaneous reperfusion of the infarct-related artery within 24 hours with improved healing. Outcome hinges largely on electrical (arrhythmic) and mechanical (pump failure) complications (Harrison 22e pp.2113, 2117).","pitfalls":["Equating absence of pain with absence of STEMI: painless infarction is common in diabetics and older patients and may present only as dyspnea, syncope, or hypotension (Harrison 22e p.2114).","Misattributing xiphoid/epigastric pain to indigestion, compounded by the patient's own denial of a heart attack (Harrison 22e p.2114).","Older terms Q-wave/non-Q-wave and transmural/nontransmural MI correlate poorly with pathology and have been replaced by STEMI and NSTEMI; Q-wave development depends more on infarct volume than on transmurality (Harrison 22e p.2115).","Peak biomarker concentration correlates only weakly with infarct size, and early reperfusion causes earlier biomarker peaking from rapid washout, so peak levels should not be used to gauge infarct size (Harrison 22e p.2116).","Echocardiographic wall-motion abnormalities cannot distinguish acute STEMI from old scar or severe acute ischemia (Harrison 22e p.2116).","Radionuclide perfusion 'cold spots' cannot distinguish acute infarct from chronic scar, so they are not specific for acute MI (Harrison 22e p.2117)."],"management":["Immediate reperfusion by primary percutaneous coronary intervention as the preferred strategy, or fibrinolysis where timely PCI is unavailable","Dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin plus a P2Y12 inhibitor) with anticoagulation in the acute phase","Adjunctive medical therapy with beta-blockers, high-intensity statin and renin-angiotensin blockade, plus analgesia and supplemental oxygen only if hypoxaemic","Continuous cardiac monitoring with prompt treatment of arrhythmias and defibrillation for ventricular fibrillation","Recognise and manage complications including cardiogenic shock, heart failure and mechanical complications (ventricular septal defect, acute mitral regurgitation, free-wall rupture) with echocardiographic assessment","Secondary prevention with smoking cessation, risk-factor modification and cardiac rehabilitation","Long-term guideline-directed therapy with antiplatelet, statin, beta-blocker and renin-angiotensin blockade, and follow-up of left ventricular function"],"severityClassification":"Patients with prolonged ischemic discomfort are first classified as an acute coronary syndrome and triaged by the 12-lead ECG into ST-segment elevation versus no ST-segment elevation; biomarkers then separate unstable angina from NSTEMI. The Fourth Universal Definition of Myocardial Infarction classifies MI into five types: type 1 (atherothrombotic), type 2 (oxygen supply-demand imbalance unrelated to acute atherothrombosis), type 3 (cardiac death with presumed new ischemic ECG changes before troponin became available), type 4 (PCI-related, including 4a, 4b stent thrombosis, 4c restenosis), and type 5 (CABG-related). Procedure-related MI within 48 h is defined by troponin elevation greater than 5-fold (type 4a) or greater than 10-fold (type 5) the 99th percentile URL with additional ischemic criteria (Harrison 22e pp.2114, 2117-2118).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2113","Harrison 22e p.2114","Harrison 22e p.2115","Harrison 22e p.2116","Harrison 22e p.2117","Harrison 22e p.2118"],"crossLinks":[]},"stable_angina":{"name":"Stable angina pectoris","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic stable angina","exertional angina","stable ischaemic heart disease","SIHD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic presentation is retrosternal chest discomfort or pressure precipitated by exertion or emotion and relieved within minutes by rest or short-acting nitrates","Discomfort commonly radiates to the left arm, neck, or jaw, and anginal equivalents such as exertional breathlessness or fatigue are frequent in older adults, women, and people with diabetes","Symptoms are reproducible and stable over weeks to months; a change in the threshold, frequency, or duration signals progression toward an acute coronary syndrome","The resting ECG is often normal between episodes, so a normal tracing does not exclude significant coronary disease","Functional imaging or stress testing establishes inducible ischaemia, while coronary CT angiography is increasingly used to assess anatomy in patients at low to intermediate risk","Management combines aggressive risk-factor and lifestyle modification, antianginal therapy to control symptoms, and antiplatelet and statin therapy to reduce cardiovascular events","Revascularisation is considered for symptoms refractory to medical therapy or for high-risk anatomy such as significant left main or proximal multivessel disease","Grading anginal severity (for example by the Canadian Cardiovascular Society classes) helps track response to treatment and guide escalation"],"pathophysiology":"Stable angina results from a mismatch between myocardial oxygen demand and supply, most often caused by fixed atherosclerotic narrowing of one or more epicardial coronary arteries. When demand rises with exertion, a flow-limiting stenosis cannot augment supply sufficiently, producing transient subendocardial ischaemia and the sensation of angina. Endothelial dysfunction, coronary microvascular disease, and vasospasm can contribute or cause ischaemia even without high-grade obstruction. Ischaemia is reversible and resolves once demand falls, distinguishing it from the sustained supply-demand failure of infarction.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute coronary syndrome (unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI)","Gastro-oesophageal reflux and oesophageal spasm","Musculoskeletal or costochondral chest pain","Pericarditis","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism","Anxiety or panic disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Resting 12-lead ECG and comparison with prior tracings","Exercise or pharmacological stress testing with ECG or imaging to demonstrate inducible ischaemia","Coronary CT angiography to define anatomy in low to intermediate risk patients","Invasive coronary angiography when high-risk features or refractory symptoms are present","Assessment for anaemia, thyroid dysfunction, and metabolic risk factors including lipids and glucose","Resting echocardiography to assess left ventricular function and exclude structural disease"],"redFlags":["Angina occurring at rest, at low workload, or with escalating frequency or duration, suggesting an evolving acute coronary syndrome","Chest pain with haemodynamic instability, syncope, or new heart failure","Tearing or interscapular pain suggesting aortic dissection","Prolonged pain unrelieved by rest or nitrates that may indicate infarction"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the extent and location of coronary disease, left ventricular function, and control of risk factors; many patients do well for years on guideline-directed medical therapy, but left main or extensive multivessel disease and impaired ventricular function carry higher event rates.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal resting ECG excludes significant coronary disease","Overlooking atypical or equivalent presentations in women, older adults, and people with diabetes","Attributing new rest or crescendo symptoms to stable angina rather than recognising an acute coronary syndrome","Focusing on symptom relief while neglecting event-reducing secondary prevention"],"management":["Antianginal therapy to control symptoms: short-acting nitrates for acute relief, with beta-blockers or calcium-channel blockers first-line and long-acting nitrates, ivabradine, ranolazine or nicorandil added as needed","Event-prevention therapy with antiplatelet (aspirin or clopidogrel) and statin","Aggressive risk-factor and lifestyle modification: smoking cessation, blood pressure and diabetes control, diet, weight management and regular exercise","Revascularisation by percutaneous coronary intervention or coronary artery bypass grafting for symptoms refractory to medical therapy or prognostically important anatomy such as left main or multivessel disease","Identify and treat exacerbating factors such as anaemia, thyroid dysfunction and tachyarrhythmia","Monitor symptom stability and escalate promptly if the threshold, frequency or duration changes, suggesting progression to an acute coronary syndrome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"staghorn_calculus":{"name":"Staghorn calculus","system":"Renal / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["staghorn calculi","staghorn stone","coral calculus","complete staghorn calculus","partial staghorn calculus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A large branching stone that fills the renal pelvis and extends into two or more calyces, taking the shape of a stag's horn","Most are struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate and carbonate apatite) related to chronic infection with urease-producing organisms, though a minority are cystine or uric acid","They may be surprisingly asymptomatic or present with vague loin ache, recurrent urinary infection or haematuria rather than classic colic","Complications include recurrent pyelonephritis, sepsis, progressive loss of renal function and, rarely, squamous cell carcinoma of the renal pelvis","Non-contrast CT defines the stone burden and pelvicalyceal anatomy for treatment planning","Left untreated, a staghorn calculus carries a substantial risk of kidney loss and life-threatening sepsis","Treatment aims for complete stone clearance, most often by percutaneous nephrolithotomy, because residual fragments perpetuate infection and regrowth","Culture-directed antibiotics against the urease organism are an essential adjunct to surgical clearance"],"pathophysiology":"A staghorn calculus is defined by its shape and extent rather than its composition, but the great majority arise from chronic infection with urease-producing bacteria that alkalinise the urine and drive rapid precipitation of struvite and carbonate apatite within the pelvicalyceal system. Because bacteria are incorporated into the stone, infection persists and the calculus enlarges to occupy the collecting system. Continuing infection and obstruction cause inflammation, scarring and progressive parenchymal destruction; non-infective staghorns can also form from cystine or uric acid when a heavy solute load fills the collecting system.","additionalDifferentials":["Struvite versus cystine versus uric acid composition of the staghorn","Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis","Renal tuberculosis with extensive calcification","Renal pelvis tumour with calcification","Medullary sponge kidney with heavy nephrocalcinosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Non-contrast CT to map stone extent and calyceal anatomy","Urine culture and sensitivity to identify the causative organism","Stone analysis after retrieval","Assessment of split renal function, such as nuclear renography, to weigh salvage against nephrectomy","Serum renal function and inflammatory markers","Metabolic screen for a non-infective stone nidus"],"redFlags":["Sepsis from an infected obstructed staghorn kidney","Rapidly deteriorating renal function","A poorly functioning kidney harbouring chronic infection","Suspicion of squamous cell carcinoma within a chronic staghorn"],"prognosis":"Complete clearance with eradication of infection offers the best chance of preserving function and preventing recurrence; incompletely treated staghorns frequently recur, and long-standing disease can lead to a non-functioning kidney requiring nephrectomy.","pitfalls":["Managing with antibiotics alone without addressing the stone","Accepting incomplete clearance, which virtually guarantees recurrence","Failing to assess residual renal function before deciding on treatment","Overlooking malignancy in a chronically infected, calcified kidney"],"management":["Complete stone clearance is the goal; percutaneous nephrolithotomy is first-line for most staghorn calculi, sometimes combined with other modalities","Culture-directed antimicrobials perioperatively to treat the associated infection, given the struvite stones harbour urease-producing organisms","Urease inhibition with acetohydroxamic acid in selected non-surgical struvite cases to slow stone growth","Metabolic evaluation with targeted prophylaxis for non-infective cystine or uric acid stones, including urinary alkalinisation and high fluid intake","Assessment of split renal function to weigh salvage against nephrectomy of a chronically infected, non-functioning kidney","Prompt drainage (nephrostomy or ureteric stent) and resuscitation for obstruction and urosepsis","Surveillance imaging and urine cultures for recurrence and residual fragments that act as a nidus for regrowth","Urology referral for definitive management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (Feehally et al.)"],"crossLinks":[]},"stannosis":{"name":"stannosis","system":"respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tin oxide pneumoconiosis","tin pneumoconiosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Stannosis is a benign inert-dust pneumoconiosis caused by inhalation of tin oxide (stannic oxide) fume and dust, without fibrosis or functional impairment.","It is a classic example of a radiologically dramatic but clinically silent pneumoconiosis, because tin is radiodense and highly opaque on imaging.","Chest radiographs show numerous small, very dense, sharply defined nodular opacities distributed widely through the lungs, sometimes with dense hilar and even pleural or perihilar deposits.","The striking radiographic density reflects the high atomic number of tin rather than any tissue reaction, so appearances greatly overstate the biological effect.","Exposure occurs in tin smelting and refining, tin-oxide production, and processes generating tin fume; the ore cassiterite may co-contain silica, which must be considered separately.","Patients are typically asymptomatic with normal lung function and no increased risk of progressive fibrosis, tuberculosis or malignancy from the tin itself.","The key clinical task is to distinguish benign stannosis from silicosis, mixed-dust fibrosis or other serious pneumoconioses that share nodular patterns but carry a worse prognosis.","Occupational history and knowledge of the specific exposure are essential; regression is not expected but progression is also not seen once exposure ceases."],"pathophysiology":"Inhaled tin oxide particles are deposited in the alveoli and small airways and taken up by alveolar macrophages. Tin oxide is biologically inert and does not stimulate the fibrogenic macrophage response seen with silica, so the particles accumulate as dust-laden macrophage aggregates in a perivascular and peribronchiolar distribution without collagen deposition, granuloma formation or architectural distortion. The result is a non-fibrotic accumulation of dust (a benign 'inert-dust' pneumoconiosis) that leaves alveolar structure and gas exchange intact. The high radiographic density arises purely from the high atomic number and radio-opacity of tin, causing prominent nodular shadowing that is disproportionate to the negligible tissue reaction. Any fibrosis or functional loss in a tin worker should prompt a search for co-exposure to silica or other fibrogenic dusts.","additionalDifferentials":["silicosis","mixed-dust fibrosis","baritosis and other benign inert-dust pneumoconioses","siderosis (iron oxide pneumoconiosis)","coal workers' pneumoconiosis","pulmonary sarcoidosis","miliary tuberculosis or other miliary nodular disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["detailed occupational history of tin smelting, refining or fume exposure","chest radiography demonstrating dense, discrete nodular opacities","high-resolution CT to characterise nodules and assess for fibrosis","pulmonary function tests to confirm preserved lung function","assessment of co-existing silica or other fibrogenic dust exposure","workplace air sampling and dust characterisation","biopsy or mineralogical analysis only if the diagnosis is uncertain"],"redFlags":["development of true fibrosis or functional impairment suggesting co-exposure to silica","progressive breathlessness or cough not explained by benign dust retention","radiographic features atypical for inert-dust deposition such as masses or cavitation","constitutional symptoms or weight loss prompting exclusion of infection or malignancy","coalescing or conglomerate opacities indicating a fibrotic pneumoconiosis"],"prognosis":"Stannosis is benign: it does not cause symptoms, functional impairment or progressive disease, and there is no increased risk of fibrosis or malignancy from tin oxide alone. The radiographic changes persist but do not progress once exposure ceases.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the dramatic dense nodular radiograph as silicosis or malignancy.","Attributing coexisting fibrosis to tin rather than to concomitant silica exposure.","Subjecting an asymptomatic worker to unnecessary invasive investigation.","Failing to take an accurate occupational history and therefore missing the diagnosis.","Assuming all radiodense pneumoconioses are harmful and causing undue alarm."],"management":["No specific treatment is required, as tin oxide deposition is benign and non-fibrotic and causes no functional impairment","Reassurance that the radiographic changes overstate the biological effect and do not progress once exposure ceases","Reduce further exposure through workplace dust and fume control, respiratory protection and air sampling","Assess for co-existing silica exposure from cassiterite ore, which requires separate evaluation and management for silicosis","Periodic review with chest imaging and lung function to confirm stability and exclude an alternative fibrogenic process should symptoms develop","Occupational health involvement and reporting as an occupational lung condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine, current edition","Parkes' Occupational Lung Disorders, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"staphylococcal_infections":{"name":"Staphylococcal Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Staph aureus infection","MRSA"],"chapter":152,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1195; cross-referenced material on pp.389, 402, 967, 974-975, 581, 993-995","clinicalPearls":["Staphylococcus aureus causes a wide spectrum of disease, ranging from localized skin and soft-tissue infection to invasive bloodstream, endocardial, and bone infection and to toxin-mediated syndromes; methicillin-resistant S. aureus (MRSA) is now common in both community and health-care settings (p.1195, p.389).","Furunculosis (boils) is caused by S. aureus and has risen in prominence with community-acquired MRSA (CA-MRSA); patients frequently mistake furuncles for spider or insect bites, and family members or close contacts may also be affected (p.389).","Whenever possible, send lesional material from staphylococcal skin infection for culture; incision and drainage may be adequate therapy for a small solitary furuncle without surrounding cellulitis or systemic symptoms (p.389).","Impetigo is caused most often by S. aureus (and sometimes group A streptococci) and forms a characteristic honey-colored crust; bullous impetigo, with tense clear bullae, is produced by S. aureus exfoliative toxin (phage type II) (p.389).","In staphylococcal scalded-skin syndrome (SSSS) the site of staphylococcal infection is usually extracutaneous (e.g., conjunctivitis, rhinorrhea, otitis media, pharyngitis) and the skin lesions themselves are sterile, whereas in bullous impetigo the skin lesions are the actual site of infection (p.402).","Toxic shock syndrome (TSS) is defined by clinical criteria — fever, diffuse macular (sunburn-type) erythroderma, hypotension, and multiorgan involvement — with three criteria involving mucocutaneous sites (diffuse skin erythema, desquamation of palms and soles 1-2 weeks after onset, and mucous-membrane hyperemia of the vagina, oropharynx, or conjunctivae) (p.402, p.993).","In staphylococcal TSS, the vagina or a postoperative wound is typically colonized rather than overtly infected, and mucosal areas appear hyperemic but not infected; this contrasts with streptococcal TSS, which is more often linked to overt skin/soft-tissue infection and bacteremia (p.993).","Acute bacterial endocarditis from S. aureus follows a far more aggressive course than subacute endocarditis, can attack previously normal native valves, and native-valve S. aureus (including MRSA) endocarditis is increasing in frequency (p.994).","Coagulase-negative staphylococci are skin commensals; a single positive blood-culture bottle for skin flora (especially one taking >24 h to turn positive) should raise suspicion of contamination or catheter colonization rather than true bloodstream infection, and cultures should be repeated from both central and peripheral sites (p.581)."],"pathophysiology":"S. aureus expresses cell wall-anchored adhesins called MSCRAMMs (microbial surface components recognizing adhesive matrix molecules, e.g., SdrC) that mediate binding to host extracellular-matrix proteins and underpin adherence and colonization (p.967). Like other extracellular pathogens it can form biofilms, which contribute to device- and prosthesis-associated infection (p.967). Disease is driven partly by potent secreted toxins. Superantigens — including toxic shock syndrome toxin-1 (TSST-1) and the staphylococcal enterotoxins — bind invariant regions of MHC class II molecules and the T-cell receptor, nonspecifically activating vast numbers of T cells and triggering a cytokine storm responsible for fever, rash, and the shock of TSS (p.974, p.402). S. aureus also produces an erythrotoxin causing scarlatiniform rash and exfoliative toxin (\"exfoliatin\") that cleaves the superficial epidermis to produce bullous impetigo and SSSS (p.402, p.389). It elaborates several pore-forming toxins — leukocidins, alpha- and gamma-hemolysins, and phenol-soluble modulins — that lyse host cells, protect the organism from phagocytosis, and impair development of protective adaptive immunity (p.975). Methicillin resistance results from acquisition of the mobile mecA gene carried on the SCCmec cassette, which has spread widely through the S. aureus population (p.1195).","additionalDifferentials":["Group A streptococcal infection (overlaps with staphylococci in impetigo, cellulitis, necrotizing fasciitis, and toxic shock syndrome) (p.389, p.993)","Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome (more often with overt soft-tissue infection/necrotizing fasciitis and bacteremia, less prominent exanthem) (p.993)","Toxic epidermal necrolysis and severe drug eruptions (in the differential of widespread skin blistering versus SSSS) (p.389, p.402)","Kawasaki disease and scarlet fever (other causes of scarlatiniform rash with palm/sole desquamation) (p.402)"],"infectionMimics":["Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome","Scarlet fever (group A streptococcal erythrogenic toxin)","Clostridium sordellii toxic shock (childbirth-associated or black-tar heroin injection; fever typically absent)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Toxic epidermal necrolysis / severe drug eruption (mimics SSSS)","Kawasaki disease (polymorphous rash, conjunctival injection, desquamation; clues are cervical lymphadenopathy, cheilitis, thrombocytosis)","SARS-CoV-2-associated multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures, repeated from both central and peripheral sites, to confirm bacteremia and distinguish true infection from skin-flora contamination/catheter colonization (differential time-to-positivity can localize a catheter source) (p.581)","Frozen section of the blister roof or exfoliative cytology of blister contents to distinguish SSSS from toxic epidermal necrolysis rapidly (p.402)","Culture of lesional material from skin/soft-tissue infection to guide therapy and detect MRSA (p.389)","mecA gene detection as the genotypic basis of methicillin resistance, enabling rapid genotypic MRSA identification (p.1195)","Double-disk diffusion (D-zone) test to detect inducible clindamycin resistance when an isolate is erythromycin-resistant but clindamycin-susceptible (p.383)","Echocardiography and a search for metastatic foci in S. aureus bacteremia to evaluate for endocarditis (p.994)"],"redFlags":["S. aureus bacteremia warrants an active search for infective endocarditis and metastatic/seeded foci, because any primary focus can give rise to bloodstream infection with secondary seeding (p.994).","In TSS, rapid hypotension developing within hours of symptom onset, and early renal failure preceding hypotension, are warning features (early renal failure helps distinguish TSS from other septic shock syndromes) (p.993).","Acute S. aureus endocarditis: a changing murmur, congestive heart failure, rapid valvular destruction (especially aortic) with pulmonary edema and hypotension, myocardial abscess with conduction block/arrhythmia, and large friable vegetations causing arterial emboli, metastatic infection, or stroke (p.994).","Widespread skin blistering or radial perioral crusting with diffuse tenderness suggests SSSS and demands prompt evaluation (p.402).","S. aureus among organisms that can present as intractable hypotension/septic shock with a primary soft-tissue focus (p.993)."],"prognosis":"Outcome varies sharply by syndrome. For TSS, reported mortality is about 5% for menstruation-associated and 10-15% for nonmenstrual disease (compared with 30-70% for streptococcal TSS) (p.993). Acute bacterial endocarditis, including native-valve S. aureus/MRSA disease, carries mortality of roughly 10-40%, and older patients with S. aureus endocarditis often present with nonspecific symptoms that delay diagnosis and worsen prognosis (p.994). Rapid recognition and intervention are crucial across invasive staphylococcal syndromes.","pitfalls":["Attributing a single positive blood culture for coagulase-negative staphylococci to true bacteremia when it may represent skin-flora contamination or catheter colonization — repeat paired cultures and assess time-to-positivity (p.581).","Assuming a primary skin focus is present in staphylococcal TSS; colonization (vaginal or wound) without overt infection is typical, so the portal may be subtle (p.993).","Missing the diagnosis of S. aureus endocarditis in older patients, whose nonspecific presentation delays recognition (p.994).","Confusing SSSS with toxic epidermal necrolysis or severe drug eruption; SSSS occurs mainly in infants/toddlers, is more superficial, spares the oral mucosa, and is toxin- rather than drug-mediated (p.402).","Inducible clindamycin resistance can cause treatment failure in apparently clindamycin-susceptible isolates unless the double-disk (D-zone) test is performed (p.383)."],"management":["Incision and drainage for abscesses and furuncles, which may be sufficient for a small solitary lesion without surrounding cellulitis or systemic features","Anti-staphylococcal antibiotics guided by susceptibility: beta-lactams such as flucloxacillin for methicillin-susceptible disease, and agents such as vancomycin for MRSA","Prompt removal or replacement of infected prosthetic devices and intravascular catheters, given biofilm-associated infection","For toxic shock syndrome, remove the focus or retained tampon, give aggressive fluid resuscitation and organ support, add a protein-synthesis-inhibiting antibiotic such as clindamycin to suppress toxin production, and consider intravenous immunoglobulin","Echocardiography and a search for metastatic foci in S. aureus bacteraemia, with prolonged intravenous therapy for endocarditis or deep-seated infection","Supportive skin and fluid care for scalded-skin syndrome, treating the extracutaneous focus of infection","Decolonisation and hygiene measures for recurrent furunculosis, extended to affected close contacts to limit spread"],"severityClassification":"No single graded severity scale is presented; staphylococcal disease is categorized by clinical syndrome and invasiveness — superficial skin/soft-tissue infection (impetigo, furunculosis), toxin-mediated syndromes (TSS, SSSS, scarlatiniform eruption), and invasive disease (bacteremia, infective endocarditis, osteomyelitis, septic intracranial/epidural infection). TSS is defined by formal clinical criteria (fever, diffuse erythroderma, hypotension, multiorgan involvement with mucocutaneous features) (p.402, p.993).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Ch.152 Staphylococcal Infections, p.1195","Harrison's 22e, Ch.60 Eczema, Psoriasis, Cutaneous Infections..., p.389 (impetigo, bullous impetigo, SSSS, furunculosis)","Harrison's 22e, Ch.61 (cutaneous manifestations: SSSS vs TEN, TSS, scarlatiniform eruption), p.402","Harrison's 22e, Ch.125 Molecular Mechanisms of Microbial Pathogenesis, pp.967, 974-975 (MSCRAMMs, biofilms, superantigens, pore-forming toxins)","Harrison's 22e, Ch.127 Approach to the Acutely Ill Infected Febrile Patient, pp.993-995 (TSS, acute bacterial endocarditis)","Harrison's 22e, Ch.79 Infections in Patients with Cancer, p.581 (coagulase-negative staphylococci, CLABSI vs contamination)"],"crossLinks":[]},"stargardt_disease":{"name":"Stargardt disease","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ABCA4","Stargardt macular dystrophy","fundus flavimaculatus","STGD1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest inherited macular dystrophy, usually autosomal recessive and caused by biallelic variants in ABCA4","Typically presents in childhood or young adulthood with progressive bilateral central visual loss and difficulty reading","Yellow-white pisciform (fish-tail) flecks at the level of the retinal pigment epithelium around the macula and posterior pole are characteristic","A macular atrophic 'beaten-bronze' appearance develops, and vision may be reduced before obvious fundus change","A dark or 'silent' choroid on fluorescein angiography, from lipofuscin masking, is a classic sign","Autofluorescence imaging highlights flecks and atrophy and is useful for monitoring progression","Patients should avoid high-dose vitamin A supplementation, which may accelerate lipofuscin accumulation"],"pathophysiology":"Stargardt disease is caused by defects in ABCA4, a transporter in photoreceptor outer-segment discs that clears retinoid by-products of the visual cycle. Loss of transporter function allows toxic bisretinoid compounds to accumulate and form excessive lipofuscin (including A2E) within the retinal pigment epithelium. This lipofuscin overload is cytotoxic to the retinal pigment epithelium, leading to its dysfunction and death and secondary loss of the overlying photoreceptors, concentrated at the macula. The result is progressive central photoreceptor and pigment-epithelial atrophy with the characteristic flecks representing lipofuscin-laden cells.","additionalDifferentials":["Cone or cone-rod dystrophy","Best vitelliform macular dystrophy","Pattern dystrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium","Age-related macular degeneration (in older presentations)","Drug-induced (e.g. antimalarial) maculopathy","Other flecked retina syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fundus autofluorescence imaging to characterise flecks and map atrophy","Optical coherence tomography to assess photoreceptor and pigment-epithelial loss","ABCA4 molecular genetic testing","Fluorescein angiography demonstrating the dark/silent choroid","Electroretinography and electrooculography to assess extent of retinal dysfunction","Colour vision testing and formal visual acuity assessment"],"redFlags":["Rapid or asymmetric visual decline prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Widespread (rather than macular) retinal dysfunction on ERG indicating a poorer-prognosis phenotype","Inadvertent high-dose vitamin A intake potentially accelerating disease"],"prognosis":"Central vision is progressively lost, often to the level of legal blindness, while peripheral vision is usually preserved; the rate of progression varies with genotype and age of onset, and gene- and pharmacologically-targeted therapies are under active investigation.","pitfalls":["Labelling early cases as functional or refractive when acuity loss precedes visible fundus change","Recommending or overlooking high-dose vitamin A supplements that may worsen the disease","Confusing it with age-related macular degeneration in older patients and mismanaging accordingly","Failing to arrange genetic testing and counselling for recessive inheritance"],"management":["No established disease-modifying therapy; management centres on low-vision rehabilitation with magnifiers and visual aids to maximise remaining vision","Advise avoidance of high-dose vitamin A supplementation, which may accelerate toxic lipofuscin (A2E) accumulation","Ultraviolet and blue-light protection with sunglasses to limit photic retinal injury","Monitor progression with fundus autofluorescence and optical coherence tomography","Provide educational, occupational and visual-impairment registration support","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive ABCA4 disorder, with discussion of emerging gene- and drug-directed therapies and clinical trials"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina"],"crossLinks":[]},"stat1_gain_of_function":{"name":"STAT1 gain-of-function (chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis spectrum)","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STAT1 GOF","STAT1 gain-of-function","autosomal dominant chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis","CMC with STAT1 mutation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification of inborn errors of immunity","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most common monogenic cause of chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, presenting with persistent oral, oesophageal, cutaneous and nail Candida infection often from early childhood","The paradox is that a gain-of-function variant produces immunodeficiency by suppressing the T-helper-17 responses needed for mucosal antifungal defence","A broad accompanying phenotype includes autoimmunity such as thyroid disease and type 1 diabetes, autoimmune cytopenias and enteropathy","Cerebral and systemic aneurysms and vascular disease are recognised and can cause catastrophic events","There is an increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma, particularly oral and oesophageal, arising in chronically inflamed candidal mucosa","Susceptibility extends to viral, mycobacterial and invasive fungal infection beyond Candida in more severe cases","Inheritance is autosomal dominant, usually from heterozygous variants in the coiled-coil or DNA-binding domains of STAT1","Targeted therapy with a JAK inhibitor can dampen the excessive STAT1 signalling and improve refractory disease, and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is considered in severe cases"],"pathophysiology":"STAT1 is a signal transducer activated downstream of type I, type II and type III interferons and of interleukin-27. Gain-of-function variants impair the normal dephosphorylation of STAT1, leaving it persistently phosphorylated and producing exaggerated interferon-driven gene expression. This heightened STAT1 activity suppresses the differentiation of interleukin-17-producing T-helper-17 cells, which are essential for mucosal defence against Candida, explaining the chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis. The same interferon over-activity fosters loss of immune tolerance with autoimmunity, contributes to vascular pathology and, together with chronic mucosal inflammation, raises the risk of squamous malignancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune polyendocrinopathy-candidiasis-ectodermal dystrophy (APECED/AIRE deficiency)","IL-17 pathway defects (IL17RA, IL17F, ACT1)","CARD9 deficiency","STAT3 loss-of-function hyper-IgE syndrome","Acquired immunosuppression or endocrinopathy causing candidiasis","RORgammaT deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flow-cytometric assay of STAT1 phosphorylation and dephosphorylation kinetics after interferon stimulation","Enumeration of circulating T-helper-17 (IL-17-producing) cells","Serum immunoglobulins and autoantibody screen including thyroid and endocrine autoantibodies","Full blood count for autoimmune cytopenias","STAT1 gene sequencing for gain-of-function variants","Vascular imaging where aneurysm or cerebrovascular disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["New oral or oesophageal lesion suggesting squamous cell carcinoma in chronically infected mucosa","Neurological symptoms or headache raising concern for cerebral aneurysm","Invasive or disseminated fungal, mycobacterial or viral infection","Severe autoimmune cytopenia or refractory enteropathy","Rapid clinical deterioration during immunosuppression"],"prognosis":"The course ranges from relatively mild mucocutaneous disease to severe multisystem involvement; life-limiting complications include invasive infection, squamous malignancy and vascular events, though targeted JAK inhibition has improved outcomes in refractory cases.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent candidiasis symptomatically without recognising an underlying inborn error","Assuming a gain-of-function variant cannot cause infection susceptibility","Overlooking associated autoimmunity, vascular disease and malignancy risk","Failing to distinguish it from APECED, which requires different surveillance","Starting broad immunosuppression that may worsen infection without considering targeted therapy"],"management":["Long-term antifungal therapy with azoles such as fluconazole or itraconazole for chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, escalating for azole-resistant Candida","Targeted JAK inhibition (for example ruxolitinib or baricitinib) to dampen excessive STAT1 signalling in severe or refractory multisystem disease","Immunomodulatory therapy for associated autoimmunity such as enteropathy and cytopenias, with hormone replacement for thyroid disease and type 1 diabetes","Surveillance for, and prompt treatment of, squamous cell carcinoma arising in chronically inflamed oral and oesophageal mucosa","Vascular imaging surveillance for cerebral and systemic aneurysms","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation considered in severe, refractory disease","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant disorder, with family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"stat3_gain_of_function_disease":{"name":"STAT3 Gain-of-Function Disease","system":"Immunology / Inborn errors of immunity","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STAT3 GOF","STAT3","STAT3 gain-of-function syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Germline activating (gain-of-function) variants in STAT3 cause early-onset, multi-organ autoimmunity and lymphoproliferation, the mechanistic mirror image of STAT3 loss-of-function, which produces autosomal dominant hyper-IgE (Job) syndrome.","Typical presentation is an infant or young child with combinations of autoimmune cytopenias, enteropathy with failure to thrive, early-onset type 1 diabetes and autoimmune thyroid disease.","Short stature and growth failure are common and disproportionate to nutritional state.","Lymphoproliferation with lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly overlaps clinically with autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome.","Interstitial lung disease and recurrent respiratory infection may occur, and some patients have hypogammaglobulinaemia despite an autoimmunity-predominant phenotype.","Constitutive STAT3 activation skews T-helper differentiation and reduces regulatory T-cell numbers and function, driving loss of self-tolerance.","JAK inhibitors and interleukin-6 pathway blockade are rational targeted therapies, and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is used in severe or refractory disease."],"pathophysiology":"STAT3 is a transcription factor activated by the JAK family downstream of numerous cytokine receptors, including those for interleukin-6, interleukin-10, interleukin-21 and interleukin-23. Heterozygous germline gain-of-function variants render STAT3 hyperactive or constitutively active, increasing transcription of its target genes. This disturbs the balance between effector T-helper responses (particularly the Th17 axis) and regulatory T cells, reduces regulatory T-cell numbers and function partly through interference with interleukin-2 and STAT5 signalling, and promotes survival and expansion of autoreactive lymphocytes. The net effect is early-onset multi-system autoimmunity, lymphoproliferation and impaired immune homeostasis. This contrasts sharply with dominant-negative loss-of-function STAT3 variants, which impair Th17 development and produce the hyper-IgE syndrome phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["IPEX syndrome (FOXP3 defect)","CTLA4 haploinsufficiency","LRBA deficiency","Autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome (FAS pathway defects)","STAT1 gain-of-function disease","Activated PI3K-delta syndrome","Common variable immunodeficiency with autoimmune and lymphoproliferative complications"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted panel or exome sequencing including STAT3, with functional confirmation such as transactivation reporter or phospho-STAT3 assays","Serum immunoglobulin quantification and specific antibody or vaccine response testing","Lymphocyte immunophenotyping with enumeration of regulatory T cells","Broad autoantibody screen covering islet, thyroid and cytopenia-associated antibodies","Direct antiglobulin test and blood film to characterise autoimmune cytopenias","Thyroid function and glucose or glycated haemoglobin assessment for endocrine autoimmunity","High-resolution chest imaging and nodal/abdominal imaging for interstitial lung disease and lymphoproliferation"],"redFlags":["Multiple simultaneous or sequential autoimmune diseases arising in early childhood","Combination of autoimmune cytopenia with enteropathy and endocrinopathy in one patient","Progressive interstitial lung disease","Disproportionate growth failure accompanying lymphoproliferation"],"prognosis":"The course is variable and often severe, with multi-organ autoimmunity, infection and interstitial lung disease driving morbidity. Targeted JAK or interleukin-6 inhibition and, in refractory cases, haematopoietic stem cell transplantation can improve outcomes.","pitfalls":["Labelling the illness as an isolated autoimmune disorder (such as type 1 diabetes or immune thrombocytopenia) and missing the unifying monogenic diagnosis.","Confusing STAT3 gain-of-function with STAT3 loss-of-function hyper-IgE syndrome, which have opposite mechanisms and phenotypes.","Assuming normal immunoglobulins exclude the diagnosis, as hypogammaglobulinaemia can coexist.","Attributing short stature solely to chronic illness or corticosteroids rather than the intrinsic disorder."],"management":["Targeted therapy with JAK inhibition and interleukin-6 receptor blockade (tocilizumab) to counter constitutive STAT3 activity","Immunosuppression with corticosteroids and steroid-sparing agents for autoimmune cytopenias, enteropathy and other organ-specific autoimmunity","Hormone replacement and glycaemic control for early-onset type 1 diabetes and autoimmune thyroid disease, with nutritional support for failure to thrive","Immunoglobulin replacement and infection prophylaxis where hypogammaglobulinaemia and recurrent respiratory infection occur","Monitor for and manage interstitial lung disease and lymphoproliferation","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in refractory, severe multisystem disease","Genetic counselling for this germline dominant disorder, with family evaluation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity","Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies","ESID clinical guidance on immune dysregulation disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"stat3_loss_of_function":{"name":"STAT3 loss-of-function (autosomal dominant hyper-IgE / Job syndrome)","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STAT3 loss-of-function deficiency","autosomal dominant hyper-IgE syndrome","AD-HIES","Job syndrome","signal transducer and activator of transcription 3 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant multisystem disorder caused by dominant-negative mutations in STAT3, classically presenting as the hyper-IgE (Job) syndrome","The immunological hallmark is a triad of recurrent staphylococcal skin abscesses (often cold, lacking the usual warmth and inflammation), recurrent pneumonias with pneumatocoele formation, and a markedly elevated serum IgE","Chronic eczematoid dermatitis and mucocutaneous candidiasis are common and often begin in infancy","Connective-tissue and skeletal features are characteristic: a distinctive coarse facial appearance, hyperextensible joints, recurrent fractures with reduced bone density, and scoliosis","Retained primary teeth from failure of normal deciduous tooth shedding is a classic dental clue","Vascular abnormalities including aneurysm formation and tortuosity may develop, and pneumatocoeles can become secondarily infected with fungi such as aspergillus","Diagnosis integrates a clinical scoring approach with a high IgE, reduced interleukin-17-producing T cells, and confirmatory STAT3 sequencing; management centres on antistaphylococcal and antifungal prophylaxis, skin care, and vigilant treatment of lung complications"],"pathophysiology":"STAT3 is a transcription factor that relays signals from numerous cytokines, including interleukin-6, interleukin-10, interleukin-21, and interleukin-23. Dominant-negative mutations produce a protein that interferes with the normal STAT3 encoded by the intact allele, so STAT3-dependent signalling is broadly reduced. A key consequence is failure to generate interleukin-17-producing T-helper cells, which are needed to defend skin and mucosa against Staphylococcus aureus and candida, explaining the abscesses and candidiasis. Impaired STAT3 signalling also disturbs the regulation of inflammation (contributing to the paradoxically cold abscesses and elevated IgE), and because the same pathway participates in bone remodelling, connective-tissue maintenance, vascular integrity, and dental development, the disorder extends well beyond immunity into the skeletal, dental, and vascular abnormalities that define the syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["DOCK8 deficiency autosomal recessive hyper-IgE syndrome","PGM3 deficiency","Severe atopic dermatitis with reactive IgE elevation","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","Chronic granulomatous disease with recurrent abscesses","Cystic fibrosis or other causes of recurrent pneumonia with cavitation","Comel-Netherton syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum IgE with full blood count and eosinophil quantification","Enumeration of interleukin-17-producing T-helper cells","Quantitative immunoglobulins and specific antibody responses","Genetic sequencing of STAT3","Chest imaging to detect pneumatocoeles and monitor for secondary fungal infection","Bone density assessment and skeletal or dental review","Vascular imaging where aneurysm or tortuosity is suspected"],"redFlags":["Recurrent cold staphylococcal abscesses with a very high IgE","Pneumatocoeles or cavitary lung disease with secondary aspergillus or other fungal infection","Pathological fractures or progressive scoliosis","Aneurysm formation or coronary/cerebral vascular abnormality","Extensive mucocutaneous candidiasis in infancy"],"prognosis":"Many patients survive into adulthood with attentive antimicrobial prophylaxis and skin care, but chronic lung damage with secondary fungal infection is a leading cause of morbidity and mortality, and vascular and skeletal complications add to the long-term burden.","pitfalls":["Mistaking cold abscesses for simple recurrent boils and missing the underlying syndrome","Draining or operating on pneumatocoeles without recognising the risk of secondary fungal colonisation and poor healing","Attributing a high IgE solely to atopy","Overlooking the non-immune skeletal, dental, and vascular features that aid diagnosis","Underusing antifungal and antistaphylococcal prophylaxis in established disease"],"management":["Long-term anti-staphylococcal antibiotic prophylaxis such as co-trimoxazole, with attentive skin care, to reduce recurrent abscesses and eczematoid dermatitis","Antifungal therapy for mucocutaneous candidiasis, with antifungal cover and surveillance for aspergillus infection of pneumatocoeles","Aggressive treatment of pneumonias and drainage of empyema, with attention to preserving lung architecture and limiting pneumatocoele formation","Bone health management with monitoring of bone density and fracture prevention, and dental review for retained primary teeth","Vascular surveillance for aneurysm formation and arterial tortuosity","Multidisciplinary care across immunology, respiratory, dermatology, dental and orthopaedic teams, with haematopoietic stem cell transplantation considered in selected cases","Genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant STAT3 disorder, with family assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"steatocystoma_multiplex":{"name":"Steatocystoma multiplex","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sebocystomatosis","epidermal polycystic disease","KRT17"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as multiple small, smooth, skin-coloured to yellowish dermal cysts, often on the chest, axillae, trunk and proximal limbs","The cysts arise from the sebaceous duct and characteristically discharge an oily or creamy fluid when incised","Familial cases are autosomal dominant and caused by variants in KRT17 (keratin 17), which is also implicated in pachyonychia congenita type 2","It may overlap with pachyonychia congenita type 2, so nail changes and natal teeth should be sought in KRT17-related disease","Steatocystoma multiplex suppurativa describes a variant in which cysts become inflamed, rupture and scar, resembling acne conglobata or hidradenitis","Diagnosis is clinical, confirmed if needed by biopsy showing a cyst wall with an eosinophilic corrugated cuticle and sebaceous gland lobules within or adjacent to the wall","Management is largely cosmetic and includes expression or excision of individual cysts and treatment of inflamed lesions"],"pathophysiology":"Steatocystoma multiplex is a naevoid malformation of the pilosebaceous unit centred on the sebaceous duct. In familial disease, dominant variants in KRT17 disturb the keratin cytoskeleton of the sebaceous duct and associated epithelium, producing multiple cysts lined by stratified squamous epithelium with a characteristic crenulated eosinophilic cuticle and containing sebaceous lobules. The cysts accumulate sebaceous secretions, and when they rupture the released contents provoke inflammation, which in the suppurativa variant leads to abscess-like lesions and scarring.","additionalDifferentials":["Eruptive vellus hair cysts","Epidermoid and milia cysts","Acne vulgaris or acne conglobata","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Lipomatosis","Syringomas or other adnexal tumours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy of a cyst showing the corrugated eosinophilic cuticle and sebaceous lobules","Molecular genetic testing of KRT17 in familial cases","Assessment for pachyonychia congenita features (nails, natal teeth, oral leucokeratosis)","Bacterial swab of inflamed or suppurative lesions","Family history and pedigree analysis","Dermoscopy to characterise cystic lesions"],"redFlags":["Extensive suppurative disease with abscess formation and disfiguring scarring","Rapid change in a longstanding lesion suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Recurrent secondary infection requiring repeated intervention","Significant psychological distress from cosmetic burden"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign with no malignant potential; cysts persist and slowly accumulate over time, and the main impact is cosmetic, though the suppurativa variant can cause scarring.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the association with KRT17 and pachyonychia congenita type 2","Attempting complete surgical clearance of numerous cysts with resulting scarring","Mistaking inflamed cysts for acne or hidradenitis and missing the underlying diagnosis","Failing to counsel on the chronic, recurring nature of the disorder"],"management":["Benign condition needing no treatment for asymptomatic cysts, with reassurance regarding the absence of malignant potential","Surgical excision, incision and drainage, or mini-incision extraction for troublesome or cosmetically significant cysts","Ablative laser or cryotherapy and electrosurgery for numerous small lesions","For the suppurativa variant, anti-inflammatory tetracycline antibiotics and, in severe scarring disease, oral isotretinoin, treating as for inflammatory acne or hidradenitis","Assess for associated pachyonychia congenita type 2 features such as nail changes and natal teeth in KRT17-related disease","Genetic counselling in familial autosomal dominant cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Andrews' Diseases of the Skin"],"crossLinks":[]},"stercoral_colitis":{"name":"Stercoral colitis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["stercoral ulceration"],"chapter":null,"source":"Colorectal surgery and gastroenterology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Inflammation and ulceration of the colonic wall caused by pressure from impacted, hardened faeces (a faecaloma)","It occurs in patients with severe chronic constipation, typically elderly, immobile or institutionalised","The rectosigmoid and the antimesenteric border are most often affected","It can progress to ischaemic pressure necrosis, ulceration and stercoral perforation, which is life-threatening","CT shows a distended faeces-filled colon with wall thickening, pericolonic changes or, if perforated, free air","Management includes disimpaction and bowel decompression, with surgery for perforation or necrosis"],"pathophysiology":"Stercoral colitis develops when a hard, impacted faecal mass exerts sustained pressure on the colonic wall, exceeding capillary perfusion pressure and causing localised ischaemia. The resulting mucosal and mural injury produces inflammation and stercoral ulcers, which can deepen to full-thickness necrosis and perforation. The rectosigmoid is predisposed because of its narrower lumen and firmer stool, and chronic constipation with reduced colonic motility and sensation underlies the impaction.","additionalDifferentials":["Ischaemic colitis","Diverticulitis","Infective or inflammatory colitis","Colonic obstruction or pseudo-obstruction","Colorectal malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT of the abdomen and pelvis","Assessment of the degree of faecal impaction","Inflammatory markers and lactate","Evaluation of constipation cause and comorbidities","Surgical review where perforation or necrosis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Peritonitis or free air indicating stercoral perforation","Signs of colonic necrosis or systemic toxicity","Marked colonic distension with impending perforation","Rapid clinical deterioration"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated cases improve with disimpaction and bowel care, but stercoral perforation carries a high mortality, so early recognition is important.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the perforation risk of severe faecal impaction","Confusing it with diverticulitis or ischaemic colitis","Failing to address the underlying chronic constipation","Overlooking the diagnosis in a frail, constipated patient with abdominal pain"],"management":["Manual disimpaction and bowel decompression with enemas and osmotic or stimulant laxatives to relieve the impacted faecal mass","Urgent surgical intervention, typically resection with stoma formation, for stercoral perforation, transmural necrosis or peritonitis","Supportive care with intravenous fluid resuscitation, bowel rest and broad-spectrum antibiotics where perforation or sepsis is present","Serial clinical and CT assessment with monitoring of inflammatory markers and lactate for evolving ischaemia or perforation","Long-term bowel regimen with laxatives, adequate hydration, fibre and improved mobility to prevent recurrent impaction","Review and address underlying causes and comorbidities driving chronic constipation, including constipating medications"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Colorectal surgery references (standard reference)","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"sternocostoclavicular_hyperostosis":{"name":"Sternocostoclavicular hyperostosis","system":"Rheumatology / Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCCH","sternoclavicular hyperostosis","acquired hyperostosis syndrome","anterior chest wall hyperostosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic hyperostotic and osteitic process centred on the sternum, medial clavicles and first ribs, causing anterior chest wall pain and bony swelling","Considered by many to be the anterior chest wall expression of SAPHO syndrome, and frequently accompanied by palmoplantar pustulosis","Presents with recurrent episodes of anterior chest pain and firm swelling over the sternoclavicular region, sometimes with restricted shoulder girdle movement","Bone scintigraphy classically demonstrates increased tracer uptake in the 'bull's head' distribution across the sternoclavicular joints","The process is sterile; cultures are negative and it is not a pyogenic infection despite the osteitis","Ossification and hyperostosis can compress adjacent structures, occasionally causing subclavian vein compression and venous congestion of the arm","Managed like SAPHO with NSAIDs first-line, and bisphosphonates or DMARDs including TNF inhibition for refractory disease"],"pathophysiology":"Sternocostoclavicular hyperostosis is a chronic sterile osteitis with reactive new bone formation affecting the sternum, medial clavicles and first ribs, and their connecting joints and ligaments. It is regarded as part of the SAPHO/autoinflammatory spectrum, in which dysregulated innate immunity drives neutrophilic bone inflammation and progressive hyperostosis and ossification of the anterior chest wall soft tissues and joints. The association with palmoplantar pustulosis and severe acne, and the proposed role of skin commensals such as Cutibacterium acnes as immune triggers, mirror the mechanisms of SAPHO syndrome. Progressive ossification produces bony expansion that can bridge joints and encroach on neurovascular structures at the thoracic inlet.","additionalDifferentials":["SAPHO syndrome (broader multifocal disease)","Sternoclavicular septic arthritis or osteomyelitis","Metastatic disease of the sternum or clavicle","Paget disease of bone","Condensing osteitis of the clavicle","Ankylosing spondylitis with anterior chest wall involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bone scintigraphy to demonstrate the anterior chest wall 'bull's head' uptake pattern","CT of the anterior chest wall to define hyperostosis, sclerosis and ossification","MRI to assess active osteitis and adjacent soft-tissue involvement","Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP), often mildly to moderately raised","Venography or vascular imaging if subclavian vein compression is suspected"],"redFlags":["Features of true infection (fever, rigors, positive cultures) requiring exclusion of septic arthritis or osteomyelitis","Destructive lesion or mass raising concern for malignancy","Arm swelling and venous distension suggesting subclavian vein compression or thrombosis","Rapidly progressive deformity or neurological symptoms at the thoracic inlet"],"prognosis":"The condition follows a chronic relapsing course; pain and swelling often persist over years, and although it is benign, progressive hyperostosis can cause lasting anterior chest wall discomfort and occasionally vascular compression.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing sterile osteitis as pyogenic infection and giving prolonged antibiotics or surgery","Failing to recognise the link with SAPHO syndrome and palmoplantar pustulosis","Overlooking subclavian vein compression as a cause of arm swelling","Attributing anterior chest pain to a cardiac cause and missing the bone pathology"],"management":["NSAIDs as first-line anti-inflammatory analgesia for anterior chest wall pain and osteitis","Bisphosphonates (e.g. pamidronate) to suppress bone inflammation and reduce hyperostotic pain in refractory disease","Treat associated skin disease, particularly palmoplantar pustulosis and severe acne, given the SAPHO/autoinflammatory link","Conventional DMARDs (methotrexate, sulfasalazine) or TNF-alpha inhibitors for disease unresponsive to NSAIDs and bisphosphonates","Trial of prolonged antibiotics targeting Cutibacterium acnes in selected cases as an immune trigger, recognising the process is sterile","Physiotherapy to maintain shoulder girdle mobility and limit functional restriction","Surveillance for subclavian vein compression, with vascular or thoracic surgical referral if venous congestion of the arm develops","Rheumatology-led multidisciplinary care with dermatology input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Oxford Textbook of Rheumatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"steroid_cell_tumor_ovary":{"name":"Steroid cell tumour of the ovary","system":"Gynaecology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lipid cell tumour","lipoid cell tumour","steroid cell tumour not otherwise specified"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare sex cord-stromal tumour composed of cells resembling steroid-secreting cells, frequently producing androgens and causing virilisation","Most commonly secretes testosterone, so hirsutism, oligomenorrhoea or amenorrhoea, and virilisation are typical presenting features","Some tumours instead secrete oestrogen or cortisol, occasionally producing a Cushingoid picture","Subclassified into stromal luteoma, Leydig cell tumour, and steroid cell tumour not otherwise specified, the last being the most common and having the greatest malignant potential","Leydig cell tumours may contain characteristic cytoplasmic Reinke crystals","Usually unilateral; larger size, mitotic activity, necrosis, and atypia point towards malignant behaviour","Managed surgically, with fertility-sparing options for benign-appearing tumours in young women and resolution of androgenic features after removal"],"pathophysiology":"Steroid cell tumours are sex cord-stromal neoplasms made up of cells with abundant eosinophilic or lipid-rich cytoplasm that resemble luteinised stromal cells, Leydig cells, or adrenocortical cells, and characteristically synthesise steroid hormones. Androgen production, most often testosterone, is the usual functional consequence and drives virilisation, although some tumours elaborate oestrogen or, less commonly, cortisol with resulting Cushingoid features. The category is divided into stromal luteoma and Leydig cell tumour, which are almost always benign, and steroid cell tumour not otherwise specified, which accounts for most cases and carries a definite risk of malignant behaviour. Malignancy is suggested by larger size, increased mitotic activity, necrosis, haemorrhage, and nuclear atypia rather than by hormonal output.","additionalDifferentials":["Sertoli-Leydig cell tumour","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Androgen- or cortisol-secreting adrenal tumour","Luteoma of pregnancy","Metastatic tumour with luteinised stroma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum testosterone and full androgen profile to confirm hyperandrogenism","Cortisol and adrenal axis testing when a Cushingoid picture is present","Transvaginal ultrasound to identify a solid ovarian mass","Adrenal and pelvic cross-sectional imaging to localise the hormone source","Histology with assessment of size, mitoses, necrosis, and atypia to gauge malignant potential","Immunohistochemistry (e.g. inhibin, calretinin, Melan-A) to confirm the diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive virilisation with markedly raised testosterone and an ovarian mass","A Cushingoid presentation from cortisol secretion","Histological features (large size, mitoses, necrosis, atypia) suggesting malignancy","Evidence of metastatic spread at presentation"],"prognosis":"Stromal luteomas and Leydig cell tumours are almost always benign and cured by excision, with hormonal effects regressing afterwards; steroid cell tumours not otherwise specified are benign in most cases but a substantial minority behave malignantly, so outlook depends on histological features and stage.","pitfalls":["Attributing hyperandrogenism to polycystic ovary syndrome and missing a small functioning tumour","Failing to separate an ovarian from an adrenal source of steroid excess","Assuming all steroid cell tumours are benign and overlooking malignant features","Neglecting to reassess hormone levels after surgery to confirm cure"],"management":["Surgical excision is the mainstay, with fertility-sparing unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy for localised benign tumours in young women","Complete surgical staging with hysterectomy and bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy for tumours showing malignant features or in postmenopausal women","Platinum-based chemotherapy considered for malignant steroid cell tumour not otherwise specified or metastatic disease","Monitor serum testosterone, and cortisol where a Cushingoid picture was present, to confirm biochemical remission and detect recurrence","Anticipate regression of virilisation, hirsutism and menstrual disturbance after tumour removal, supporting the patient through this","Cross-sectional imaging surveillance for local recurrence or metastasis, guided by histological malignant potential","Gynaecological oncology multidisciplinary team referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","Blaustein's Pathology of the Female Genital Tract"],"crossLinks":[]},"steroid_induced_glaucoma":{"name":"steroid induced glaucoma","system":"ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["corticosteroid-induced glaucoma","steroid responder glaucoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Any route of corticosteroid delivery can raise intraocular pressure, including topical eye drops, periocular and intravitreal injections, inhaled, nasal, dermatological and systemic preparations.","The angle is open and the clinical picture otherwise resembles primary open-angle glaucoma, so a medication history is essential.","Response usually develops within weeks of starting topical steroid, but potent intravitreal depots can cause a delayed and prolonged rise.","Around a third of the general population are moderate responders and a smaller proportion are high responders; primary open-angle glaucoma patients and their relatives respond more frequently and more strongly.","Children, particularly young ones, can respond rapidly and severely, sometimes within days.","Drop potency matters: more potent molecules cause larger rises, whereas weaker or ester-based steroids and some newer agents carry a lower risk.","The mainstay of management is stopping or reducing the steroid, or switching to a lower-potency agent, alongside pressure-lowering therapy.","Pressure usually normalises after topical steroid withdrawal, but an intravitreal depot may require surgical removal or filtration surgery if the effect persists."],"pathophysiology":"Corticosteroids reduce aqueous outflow facility at the trabecular meshwork through several interacting mechanisms. They promote accumulation of extracellular matrix material, including glycosaminoglycans, fibronectin and cross-linked actin networks, within the juxtacanalicular meshwork, and they suppress the normal turnover of this matrix by reducing phagocytic activity and matrix metalloproteinase expression in trabecular cells. Changes in trabecular cell cytoskeleton and gene expression, including induction of myocilin, further stiffen the outflow pathway. The net effect is increased resistance to aqueous drainage through an anatomically open angle, raising intraocular pressure. Susceptibility is partly genetically determined, which parallels the shared predisposition seen in primary open-angle glaucoma.","additionalDifferentials":["primary open-angle glaucoma","uveitic glaucoma where the underlying inflammation also raises pressure","pigmentary glaucoma","pseudoexfoliation glaucoma","normal-tension glaucoma with incidental steroid use","traumatic or angle-recession glaucoma","ocular hypertension unrelated to steroids"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["detailed corticosteroid exposure history across all routes","gonioscopy confirming an open angle","intraocular pressure measurement and comparison with pre-treatment baseline","optical coherence tomography of the retinal nerve fibre layer and ganglion cell complex","standard automated perimetry","optic disc examination and photography","review for a periocular or intravitreal steroid depot on examination or imaging"],"redFlags":["markedly raised pressure in a child on corticosteroids","persistent high pressure after an intravitreal steroid depot","glaucomatous optic nerve damage discovered at first presentation","very high pressures threatening rapid field loss in a known high responder"],"prognosis":"When recognised early and the steroid is withdrawn, intraocular pressure usually returns to normal without lasting damage; prolonged unrecognised elevation, or an inescapable long-acting depot, can produce permanent glaucomatous optic neuropathy.","pitfalls":["Failing to ask about non-ophthalmic steroids such as inhalers, nasal sprays and skin creams.","Continuing potent topical steroid for chronic conditions without monitoring pressure.","Assuming pressure will fall quickly after an intravitreal depot when the effect can persist for months.","Overlooking the heightened and rapid susceptibility of children.","Attributing the raised pressure solely to steroids in a uveitic eye where inflammation also contributes."],"management":["Withdraw or reduce the offending corticosteroid, or switch to the least potent effective preparation such as loteprednol or fluorometholone","Where steroid is unavoidable, lower intraocular pressure with topical agents: prostaglandin analogues, beta-blockers, alpha-agonists and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Surgical removal of an intravitreal steroid depot when pressure remains uncontrolled","Laser trabeculoplasty or filtration/drainage surgery for refractory pressure elevation","Monitor intraocular pressure, optic disc, OCT nerve-fibre layer and visual fields for glaucomatous progression","Identify steroid responders and check baseline and serial pressures whenever corticosteroids of any route are prescribed","Educate the patient and prescribers to avoid inadvertent continued steroid exposure across all delivery routes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Glaucoma","European Glaucoma Society Terminology and Guidelines for Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"stewart_treves_syndrome":{"name":"stewart treves syndrome","system":"skin and lymphatic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lymphangiosarcoma in chronic lymphoedema","post-mastectomy angiosarcoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A cutaneous angiosarcoma that arises within a chronically lymphoedematous limb, classically described after post-mastectomy arm lymphoedema but reported in any longstanding lymphoedema including congenital and filarial forms.","The latency is long, typically around a decade from the onset of chronic lymphoedema to tumour appearance.","Early lesions are subtle violaceous or red-blue macules, papules or bruise-like patches that are easily mistaken for simple ecchymosis on an oedematous limb.","Lesions are frequently multifocal, with satellite nodules that enlarge, coalesce, ulcerate and bleed.","Diagnosis rests on full-thickness incisional biopsy with immunohistochemistry demonstrating endothelial markers such as CD31, CD34, ERG and factor VIII-related antigen.","The tumour is aggressive with a strong tendency to early haematogenous metastasis, particularly to the lungs.","Wide surgical excision, often amputation, is the mainstay of treatment, with radiotherapy and chemotherapy as adjuncts, yet outcomes remain poor.","Any new or changing pigmented or violaceous lesion on a chronically swollen limb should prompt urgent biopsy rather than reassurance."],"pathophysiology":"Stewart-Treves syndrome represents malignant transformation of vascular endothelium within a field of chronic lymphoedema. Persistent stasis of protein-rich lymph is thought to create a locally immunocompromised, immunologically privileged microenvironment in which impaired immune surveillance, sustained tissue remodelling, aberrant lymphangiogenic and angiogenic signalling, and chronic inflammation promote accumulation of oncogenic changes in endothelial cells. The result is a high-grade angiosarcoma composed of atypical, anastomosing vascular channels lined by malignant endothelium that infiltrates dermis and subcutis. The same predisposing stasis explains why the tumour tends to be multifocal across the affected territory.","additionalDifferentials":["Kaposi sarcoma","traumatic bruising or haematoma on an oedematous limb","cellulitis or erysipelas","recurrent or metastatic cutaneous breast carcinoma","radiation-associated atypical vascular lesion","benign vascular tumours such as haemangioma or pyogenic granuloma","amelanotic or pigmented melanoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["incisional skin biopsy with endothelial immunohistochemistry (CD31, CD34, ERG)","magnetic resonance imaging of the affected limb to delineate extent","staging computed tomography of chest, abdomen and pelvis","positron emission tomography for occult multifocal or metastatic disease","sentinel or regional nodal assessment where indicated","review of prior oncological history and radiotherapy fields"],"redFlags":["new violaceous, red-blue or bruise-like cutaneous lesions on a chronically lymphoedematous limb","rapid growth, ulceration or spontaneous bleeding of a skin lesion","multifocal or coalescing nodules over the affected territory","systemic symptoms or respiratory features suggesting pulmonary metastasis","a non-healing lesion previously dismissed as bruising or infection"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is poor: Stewart-Treves angiosarcoma is aggressive, frequently multifocal and metastasises early, so five-year survival is low even after radical surgery. Early recognition and wide excision offer the only realistic chance of prolonged survival.","pitfalls":["mistaking early lesions for bruising, cellulitis or benign vascular change and delaying biopsy","taking an inadequate or superficial biopsy that misses the malignant endothelium","underestimating multifocality and performing insufficiently wide excision","failing to stage for pulmonary metastasis at presentation","attributing the swelling and skin change solely to the pre-existing lymphoedema"],"management":["Radical wide local excision or limb amputation offers the only realistic chance of cure","Adjuvant radiotherapy to the tumour field to improve local control given multifocal spread","Systemic chemotherapy with taxanes (paclitaxel) or anthracyclines for unresectable, multifocal or metastatic angiosarcoma","Optimise the underlying chronic lymphoedema with compression garments and decongestive therapy","Staging and surveillance imaging, particularly chest CT, for early haematogenous metastasis to the lungs","Sarcoma multidisciplinary team management with early referral","Palliative and supportive care integrated early given the poor overall prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer: Principles and Practice of Oncology","Weedon's Skin Pathology"],"crossLinks":[]},"stickler_hearing_variant":{"name":"Stickler syndrome - hearing/otological features","system":"Ear / Audiology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hereditary arthro-ophthalmopathy","COL2A1","COL11A1","COL11A2","Stickler syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology / Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A hereditary connective-tissue (collagenopathy) disorder in which hearing loss is one component alongside ocular, orofacial and skeletal features","Hearing loss may be sensorineural, conductive from middle-ear disease, or mixed, and is often high-frequency","Caused by mutations in collagen genes including COL2A1, COL11A1 and COL11A2; most subtypes are autosomal dominant","The COL11A2 subtype (type 3) shows the syndrome's systemic features with hearing loss but without the ocular involvement","Cleft palate or a Pierre Robin sequence contributes to conductive hearing loss through Eustachian tube dysfunction and recurrent middle-ear effusion","The associated high myopia carries a significant risk of retinal detachment, so ophthalmology assessment is essential","A child with cleft palate, midface hypoplasia and hearing loss should be assessed for Stickler syndrome"],"pathophysiology":"Stickler syndrome is caused by mutations in genes encoding types II and XI collagen, which are important structural components of cartilage, the vitreous of the eye and the connective tissue of the inner and middle ear. Defective collagen impairs the integrity of these tissues, producing the characteristic vitreoretinal degeneration and myopia, orofacial abnormalities such as cleft palate and midface underdevelopment, joint and skeletal problems, and hearing loss. Middle-ear collagen abnormality and Eustachian tube dysfunction related to palatal clefting contribute a conductive element, while cochlear collagen involvement contributes a sensorineural element, giving frequently mixed hearing loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Marshall syndrome with overlapping facial and sensory features","Other syndromic hearing loss such as branchio-oto-renal or Waardenburg syndrome","Isolated cleft palate or Pierre Robin sequence with secondary otitis media with effusion","Other inherited collagenopathies affecting connective tissue","Isolated high myopia with vitreoretinal degeneration","Non-syndromic mixed hearing loss from chronic middle-ear disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Audiometry with tympanometry to distinguish conductive, sensorineural and mixed loss","Ophthalmic assessment for high myopia, vitreous abnormality and retinal detachment risk","Examination of the palate and airway for clefting and Robin sequence","Skeletal and joint assessment for features of the associated arthropathy","Molecular genetic testing of collagen genes to confirm the subtype"],"redFlags":["Sudden visual symptoms suggesting retinal detachment in a highly myopic patient","Airway compromise in an infant with Pierre Robin sequence","Progressive hearing loss impairing speech and language development","Recurrent middle-ear effusions causing persistent conductive loss"],"prognosis":"Hearing loss is usually mild to moderate and manageable with amplification or middle-ear treatment, but the main threats to long-term outcome are sight-threatening retinal detachment and, in infancy, airway compromise from Robin sequence; overall prognosis is good with coordinated multidisciplinary care.","pitfalls":["Managing recurrent middle-ear effusion without recognising an underlying syndrome","Failing to arrange retinal-detachment prophylaxis and surveillance in high myopia","Assuming hearing loss is purely conductive and missing a sensorineural component","Overlooking the diagnosis in the ocular-sparing COL11A2 subtype"],"management":["Hearing rehabilitation with amplification (hearing aids) for the sensorineural and mixed high-frequency loss","Grommets (ventilation tubes) and treatment of recurrent middle-ear effusion for the conductive component","Regular audiological surveillance to detect progression of hearing loss","Mandatory ophthalmology referral for retinal-detachment prophylaxis given the associated high myopia","Cleft palate repair and airway management for Pierre Robin sequence in infancy","Genetic counselling and family screening for the autosomal dominant collagen-gene variants","Coordinated multidisciplinary care across otology, audiology, ophthalmology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"stickler_syndrome":{"name":"Stickler syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hereditary progressive arthro-ophthalmopathy","COL2A1-related Stickler syndrome","COL11A1-related Stickler syndrome","COL11A2-related Stickler syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A collagenopathy usually caused by variants in COL2A1, COL11A1, or COL11A2, most types inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern","The classic combination is ocular, orofacial, auditory, and articular features, reflecting the wide distribution of types II and XI collagen","High myopia that is congenital and non-progressive, with vitreous abnormalities, is characteristic and confers a high lifetime risk of retinal detachment","Orofacial features include midface hypoplasia, a flat nasal bridge, and the Pierre Robin sequence (micrognathia, glossoptosis, and cleft palate)","Sensorineural or mixed hearing loss is common and warrants formal audiological assessment","Early-onset osteoarthritis and mild spondyloepiphyseal changes contribute to joint pain and stiffness","Retinal detachment prophylaxis and prompt ophthalmology review are central because detachment can cause preventable blindness","Diagnosis integrates the multisystem clinical picture with dilated eye examination, audiometry, and molecular genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Stickler syndrome results from defects in the fibrillar collagens types II and XI, which are major structural components of the vitreous, cartilage, and inner ear. Variants in COL2A1 or the type XI collagen genes disrupt normal collagen fibril assembly, producing an abnormal vitreous gel that predisposes to retinal tears and detachment, cartilage that is prone to early degeneration and mild skeletal dysplasia, and inner-ear structures vulnerable to sensorineural hearing loss. The same collagen deficiency during craniofacial development underlies the midface and palatal anomalies, including the Pierre Robin sequence.","additionalDifferentials":["Marfan syndrome","Wagner vitreoretinopathy","Kniest dysplasia and other type II collagenopathies","Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita","Marshall syndrome","Non-syndromic high myopia with retinal detachment"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus and vitreous examination by an ophthalmologist","Refraction to characterise congenital high myopia","Pure-tone audiometry and audiological assessment","Molecular genetic testing of COL2A1, COL11A1, and COL11A2","Skeletal survey or targeted imaging for spondyloepiphyseal changes","Cleft palate and airway assessment in infants with the Pierre Robin sequence"],"redFlags":["New floaters, flashing lights, or a visual field shadow suggesting retinal detachment","Neonatal airway compromise from the Pierre Robin sequence","Sudden or progressive hearing loss","Acute painful red eye or trauma in a highly myopic patient","Feeding or respiratory difficulty in an infant with a cleft palate"],"prognosis":"Most patients have a normal lifespan, but vision is threatened by retinal detachment, which is the principal preventable cause of morbidity, and joint and hearing problems accumulate over time. Outcomes improve markedly with proactive ophthalmic surveillance and detachment prophylaxis.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis in children presenting with isolated Pierre Robin sequence","Underestimating retinal detachment risk and failing to arrange ophthalmology surveillance","Overlooking hearing loss because ocular features dominate the picture","Assuming high myopia is simple refractive error rather than syndromic","Not offering genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"management":["Prophylactic retinal treatment (laser or cryotherapy retinopexy) and urgent surgical repair of retinal tears or detachment","Lifelong ophthalmic surveillance with refractive correction of high myopia and detachment safety-netting","Audiological assessment and hearing amplification for sensorineural or mixed loss","Manage early-onset osteoarthritis with analgesia, physiotherapy and joint protection","Cleft palate repair and airway management for Pierre Robin sequence in infancy","Genetic counselling and family cascade screening for the autosomal dominant collagen variants","Multidisciplinary coordination across ophthalmology, audiology, orthopaedics and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Stickler syndrome","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"stickler_syndrome_type_1":{"name":"Stickler syndrome type 1","system":"Connective tissue / Skeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STL1","Stickler syndrome COL2A1","Hereditary arthro-ophthalmopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and ophthalmic genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest form of Stickler syndrome; autosomal dominant, caused by COL2A1 mutations that usually act through haploinsufficiency of type II collagen.","The membranous congenital vitreous anomaly is characteristic of type 1 and helps distinguish it from the beaded vitreous of type 2.","Myopia is typically high, congenital and largely non-progressive, with a high lifetime risk of giant retinal tears and retinal detachment.","Orofacial features include the Pierre Robin sequence (micrognathia, glossoptosis, cleft palate), midface hypoplasia and a flat facial profile.","Sensorineural hearing loss, often high-frequency, and early-onset osteoarthritis with mild epiphyseal dysplasia are common.","Predominantly ocular COL2A1 variants exist, with the eye affected but few systemic features.","Any symptoms of retinal detachment demand urgent ophthalmology; lifelong retinal surveillance is essential.","It sits within the type II collagenopathy spectrum alongside Kniest dysplasia and spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita."],"pathophysiology":"Stickler syndrome type 1 is an autosomal dominant connective tissue disorder caused by mutations in COL2A1, which encodes the alpha-1 chain of type II collagen — the predominant fibrillar collagen of hyaline cartilage, the vitreous humour and the nucleus pulposus. Most disease-causing variants are premature stop or frameshift mutations that trigger nonsense-mediated decay, so only a reduced amount of normal type II collagen is produced (haploinsufficiency); this reduced availability disturbs the delicate collagen scaffold of the vitreous, giving the membranous vitreous anomaly and predisposing to vitreoretinal degeneration and retinal detachment, and it impairs cartilage and craniofacial development, producing epiphyseal dysplasia, early osteoarthritis, midface hypoplasia and the Robin sequence. Because type II collagen contributes less to cochlear structures than type XI collagen, hearing loss is usually milder than in the type XI-related forms. The same gene, through different mutation mechanisms, underlies a spectrum of skeletal dysplasias, so genotype and vitreous phenotype together refine the diagnosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Stickler syndrome types 2 and 3 (COL11A1 and COL11A2) and recessive forms (COL9A1/2/3)","Marshall syndrome (COL11A1)","Wagner syndrome (VCAN-related vitreoretinopathy)","Marfan syndrome","Kniest dysplasia and spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita (COL2A1 spectrum)","Weissenbacher-Zweymuller syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundoscopy and slit-lamp examination with vitreous phenotyping (membranous anomaly) and refraction","Molecular genetic testing of COL2A1","Audiometry","Skeletal survey for epiphyseal and spondyloepiphyseal changes","Cleft palate and airway assessment in infancy","Craniofacial imaging or cephalometry where indicated"],"redFlags":["Symptoms of retinal detachment — flashes, floaters, a visual field shadow or sudden visual loss","Neonatal airway obstruction or feeding difficulty from the Robin sequence","Rapidly worsening myopia or new vitreous haemorrhage"],"prognosis":"Lifespan is normal; the principal threats are retinal detachment (potentially blinding but often preventable with surveillance and timely intervention), progressive hearing loss and early osteoarthritis, all manageable within a multidisciplinary framework.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the retinal detachment risk and omitting structured ophthalmic surveillance","Failing to link an apparently isolated Pierre Robin sequence to underlying Stickler syndrome","Attempting to distinguish subtypes without vitreous phenotyping or genetic testing","Overlooking hearing loss in children"],"management":["Prophylactic retinopexy and prompt surgical repair of giant retinal tears and detachment, the principal preventable morbidity","Structured lifelong ophthalmic surveillance with correction of congenital high myopia and clear detachment safety-netting","Audiological monitoring and hearing aids for sensorineural, often high-frequency, loss","Analgesia, physiotherapy and joint protection for early-onset osteoarthritis and epiphyseal dysplasia","Cleft palate repair and airway management for Pierre Robin sequence in infancy","Genetic counselling and cascade family screening for the autosomal dominant COL2A1 variant","Multidisciplinary coordination across ophthalmology, audiology, clinical genetics and orthopaedics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics reference texts","Ophthalmic genetics reference texts","Stickler syndrome surveillance and management guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"stickler_syndrome_type_2":{"name":"Stickler syndrome type 2","system":"Connective tissue / Skeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STL2","Stickler syndrome COL11A1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and ophthalmic genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant Stickler syndrome caused by mutations in COL11A1, encoding the alpha-1 chain of type XI collagen.","The beaded congenital vitreous anomaly is characteristic of type 2, contrasting with the membranous vitreous of type 1.","Sensorineural hearing loss tends to be more frequent and more pronounced than in type 1.","High myopia with retinal detachment risk, the Pierre Robin sequence, midface hypoplasia and cleft palate are shared with type 1.","It is allelic with, and clinically overlaps, Marshall syndrome (also COL11A1), which shows more striking facial and ectodermal features.","It must be distinguished from COL11A2-related Stickler syndrome (type 3), which spares the eye because the alpha-2 chain of type XI collagen is not expressed in vitreous.","Type XI collagen co-assembles with type II collagen to regulate fibril diameter, explaining the overlap in systemic features with type 1.","Lifelong retinal surveillance and regular audiology are required."],"pathophysiology":"Stickler syndrome type 2 is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in COL11A1, the gene for the alpha-1 chain of type XI collagen, a quantitatively minor fibrillar collagen that nucleates and co-polymerises with type II collagen to control the diameter and spacing of cartilage and vitreous fibrils. Pathogenic variants — frequently splice-site or in-frame changes acting through a dominant-negative mechanism — disrupt the type II/XI heterofibrillar network, degrading the architecture of the vitreous (producing the beaded vitreous anomaly and predisposing to giant retinal tears and detachment) and of cartilage (epiphyseal dysplasia, early osteoarthritis and craniofacial underdevelopment with the Robin sequence). Because type XI collagen is also important in the cochlea, sensorineural hearing loss is characteristically more prominent than in the COL2A1-related type 1. The same gene underlies Marshall syndrome, and the two conditions form an overlapping clinical spectrum.","additionalDifferentials":["Stickler syndrome type 1 (COL2A1, membranous vitreous)","Marshall syndrome (COL11A1, allelic and overlapping)","Stickler syndrome type 3 (COL11A2, non-ocular)","Recessive Stickler syndrome (COL9A1/2/3)","Wagner syndrome and Marfan syndrome","Otospondylomegaepiphyseal dysplasia (OSMED) and fibrochondrogenesis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundoscopy and slit-lamp examination with vitreous phenotyping (beaded anomaly) and refraction","Molecular genetic testing of COL11A1","Audiometry, with monitoring for progressive hearing loss","Skeletal survey for epiphyseal changes","Cleft palate and airway assessment in infancy","Craniofacial imaging where indicated"],"redFlags":["Symptoms of retinal detachment — flashes, floaters, a curtain-like field defect or sudden visual loss","Neonatal airway obstruction from the Robin sequence","Progressive or sudden hearing loss"],"prognosis":"Lifespan is normal; retinal detachment is the chief sight-threatening complication and hearing loss is often significant, but structured ophthalmic and audiological surveillance with timely intervention gives a good functional outlook.","pitfalls":["Assuming COL11A2-related (type 3) disease involves the eye — it does not","Difficulty separating type 2 from allelic Marshall syndrome","Relying on systemic features alone without vitreous phenotyping to distinguish from type 1","Underestimating the degree and progression of hearing loss"],"management":["Prophylactic retinopexy and urgent surgical repair of giant retinal tears and detachment","Lifelong ophthalmic surveillance with refractive correction of high myopia and detachment safety-netting","Close audiological monitoring and hearing amplification, as sensorineural loss is often more marked and progressive than in type 1","Manage early-onset osteoarthritis and epiphyseal dysplasia with analgesia, physiotherapy and joint protection","Cleft palate repair and airway management for Pierre Robin sequence in infancy","Genetic counselling and cascade family screening for the autosomal dominant COL11A1 variant, noting overlap with Marshall syndrome","Multidisciplinary care across ophthalmology, audiology, clinical genetics and orthopaedics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics reference texts","Ophthalmic genetics reference texts","Stickler syndrome surveillance and management guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"stickler_syndrome_type_3":{"name":"Stickler syndrome type 3","system":"Hearing / Vision genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Non-ocular Stickler syndrome","COL11A2-related Stickler syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Type 3 is the non-ocular variant of Stickler syndrome caused by dominant variants in COL11A2, the gene for the alpha-2 chain of type XI collagen.","Because COL11A2 is not expressed in the vitreous, this subtype characteristically spares the eye, so there is no vitreoretinopathy or high myopia and no retinal-detachment risk.","The distinguishing feature is the combination of sensorineural or mixed deafness, midface hypoplasia with cleft palate, and early-onset osteoarthritis without ocular disease.","Hearing loss is typically more consistent and often more pronounced than in the ocular subtypes and warrants early audiological follow-up.","Facial features overlap with other Sticklers: a flat midface, depressed nasal bridge and Pierre Robin sequence with micrognathia, cleft palate and glossoptosis in infancy.","Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia with mild vertebral flattening and premature joint degeneration produces stiffness and pain from early adulthood.","It is allelic and phenotypically overlapping with otospondylomegaepiphyseal dysplasia, which arises from recessive COL11A2 variants.","Distinguishing type 3 from the ocular types 1 and 2 is clinically important because retinal-detachment surveillance is not required."],"pathophysiology":"Stickler syndrome type 3 is an autosomal dominant connective-tissue disorder caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in COL11A2, which encodes the alpha-2(XI) chain of type XI collagen, a minor fibrillar collagen that co-assembles with type II and type XI alpha-1 chains to regulate the diameter and spacing of type II collagen fibrils in cartilage and other tissues. Abnormal or reduced alpha-2(XI) chains disrupt the fibrillar collagen network of cartilage, the cartilaginous growth plate and the inner ear, producing spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia, midfacial and palatal hypoplasia, and cochlear dysfunction with sensorineural or mixed hearing loss. Crucially, the vitreous humour of the eye assembles its type XI collagen using the alpha-2(V) chain of COL5A2 in place of alpha-2(XI), so COL11A2 is not required for normal vitreous architecture; this molecular substitution explains why type 3 disease, unlike the COL2A1-related type 1 and COL11A1-related type 2 forms, has a normal vitreous and does not confer the myopia, membranous or beaded vitreous phenotype, or the high risk of giant retinal tears and detachment that dominate the ocular subtypes. Dominant-negative and haploinsufficiency mechanisms both contribute, and the same gene produces a recessive, more severe skeletal-auditory phenotype when both alleles are affected.","additionalDifferentials":["Stickler syndrome type 1 (COL2A1) with membranous vitreous and retinal detachment","Stickler syndrome type 2 (COL11A1) with beaded vitreous and ocular involvement","Otospondylomegaepiphyseal dysplasia from recessive COL11A2 variants","Marshall syndrome with ectodermal features and hearing loss","Kniest and other type II collagenopathies with skeletal dysplasia","Isolated Pierre Robin sequence or non-syndromic cleft palate","22q11.2 deletion syndrome with palatal and craniofacial anomalies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of COL11A2 with analysis of COL2A1 and COL11A1 to subtype Stickler syndrome","Comprehensive audiometry and brainstem-evoked response testing","Skeletal survey and spine imaging for spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia","Dilated ophthalmological examination to confirm the expected absence of vitreoretinopathy","Cleft-palate and airway assessment in infants with Pierre Robin sequence","Joint imaging to document early osteoarthritis","Family evaluation and pedigree analysis for dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Neonatal airway obstruction from Pierre Robin sequence with micrognathia and glossoptosis","Feeding difficulty and aspiration associated with an unrepaired cleft palate","Progressive hearing loss impairing speech and language development","Disabling early joint pain suggesting advanced premature osteoarthritis","Recurrent otitis media with effusion compounding the sensorineural deficit"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is normal; the main burdens are progressive hearing loss and early osteoarthritis, and because the vitreous is spared the sight-threatening retinal complications of the ocular subtypes do not occur, so long-term outcome is generally more favourable for vision than in types 1 and 2.","pitfalls":["Labelling a child as ocular Stickler and imposing unnecessary retinal surveillance when the genotype is COL11A2","Conversely, withholding retinal surveillance before the subtype has been confirmed molecularly","Overlooking sensorineural hearing loss because attention is focused on the palate or joints","Confusing dominant type 3 with the more severe recessive otospondylomegaepiphyseal dysplasia","Missing Pierre Robin sequence as an early presenting feature in the newborn period"],"management":["Audiological rehabilitation with hearing aids, and cochlear implantation where appropriate, for the dominant sensorineural or mixed deafness","Regular audiological surveillance given the consistent and often pronounced progressive hearing loss","Manage early-onset osteoarthritis and spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia with analgesia, physiotherapy and joint protection","Cleft palate repair and airway management for Pierre Robin sequence in infancy","Confirmatory dilated ophthalmic assessment with reassurance that vitreoretinopathy and retinal-detachment risk are absent in this subtype","Genetic counselling and family screening for the autosomal dominant COL11A2 variant","Multidisciplinary care across audiology, rheumatology/orthopaedics and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical genetics reference texts","Ophthalmic and audiology reference texts","Skeletal dysplasia reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"sticky_platelet_syndrome":{"name":"Sticky platelet syndrome","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPS","platelet hyperaggregability syndrome","hereditary platelet hyperreactivity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A proposed inherited thrombophilia characterised by platelet hyperaggregability in response to low concentrations of ADP and adrenaline (epinephrine)","Associated in the literature with arterial and venous thrombosis, often at a young age and sometimes precipitated by emotional or physical stress","Reported clinical events include unexplained stroke, myocardial infarction, and recurrent pregnancy loss in the absence of other thrombophilias","The defining laboratory feature is increased platelet aggregation to low-dose agonists on light transmission aggregometry","It remains a debated and incompletely standardised entity, with no consensus genetic marker and variable diagnostic criteria","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, made after established inherited and acquired thrombophilias have been considered","When treatment is used, it is typically directed at reducing platelet reactivity with antiplatelet therapy rather than anticoagulation alone"],"pathophysiology":"Sticky platelet syndrome is described as a heritable tendency of platelets to aggregate excessively when exposed to low concentrations of ADP and adrenaline, suggesting heightened responsiveness of platelet activation pathways. This hyperreactivity is proposed to promote platelet-driven thrombus formation in both the arterial and venous circulations, particularly under conditions of physiological stress with catecholamine surges. The precise molecular basis is unresolved, no single causative gene has been established, and the entity remains debated, so it is best regarded as a clinico-laboratory phenotype pending further validation.","additionalDifferentials":["Factor V Leiden and prothrombin G20210A thrombophilias","Antithrombin, protein C, and protein S deficiencies","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Myeloproliferative neoplasms causing thrombocytosis and thrombosis","Hyperhomocysteinaemia","Acquired prothrombotic states such as malignancy or oestrogen exposure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Light transmission platelet aggregometry using low concentrations of ADP and adrenaline","Comprehensive inherited thrombophilia screen (factor V Leiden, prothrombin G20210A, antithrombin, protein C and S)","Antiphospholipid antibody testing","Full blood count to exclude a myeloproliferative neoplasm","Homocysteine measurement","Imaging to confirm and characterise a suspected thrombotic event"],"redFlags":["Arterial thrombosis such as stroke or myocardial infarction in a young patient","Recurrent venous thromboembolism without an obvious cause","Recurrent pregnancy loss after exclusion of other causes","Thrombosis recurring despite an otherwise negative thrombophilia workup"],"prognosis":"Because the entity is not firmly established, prognosis is uncertain; reported cases suggest that reducing platelet reactivity can lessen thrombotic events, but the evidence base is limited. Outcomes are strongly influenced by coexisting, better-defined thrombotic risk factors.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing it without first excluding established inherited and acquired thrombophilias","Relying on non-standardised aggregometry protocols with variable cut-offs","Overinterpreting transient platelet hyperreactivity from recent stress or sampling artefact","Attributing all thrombotic events to it while missing a coexisting definitive cause"],"management":["Low-dose aspirin as the mainstay to reduce platelet hyperaggregability and prevent thrombotic events","Standard anticoagulation for an established acute venous thromboembolic event","Treat acute arterial events such as stroke or myocardial infarction along standard antiplatelet-based pathways","Aspirin-based thromboprophylaxis in pregnancy for those with recurrent pregnancy loss","Modify coexisting cardiovascular and thrombotic risk factors, including smoking cessation and avoidance of catecholaminergic stimulants","Exclude and treat established inherited and acquired thrombophilias before attributing events to this entity","Haematology referral for aggregometry-guided assessment and long-term management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Hoffman: Hematology - Basic Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"stiff_limb_syndrome":{"name":"Stiff Limb Syndrome","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Stiff-limb syndrome","Focal stiff person syndrome","Stiff leg syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A focal variant of the stiff person spectrum in which rigidity and painful spasms are largely confined to one limb, most often a distal leg or foot, sometimes with fixed dystonic posturing such as an equinovarus deformity.","Continuous co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles produces board-like rigidity, with spontaneous and stimulus-sensitive painful spasms.","Electromyography shows continuous motor unit activity in the affected limb that persists at rest and is abolished by benzodiazepines, sleep or peripheral nerve block.","Compared with classic axial stiff person syndrome, glutamic acid decarboxylase antibodies are less consistently present; some cases carry glycine receptor or amphiphysin (paraneoplastic) antibodies.","Sphincter disturbance, brainstem signs or spread to other limbs and axial muscles may develop, and there can be overlap with progressive encephalomyelitis with rigidity and myoclonus.","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by electromyography, antibody testing and a therapeutic response to GABAergic agents such as benzodiazepines or baclofen.","Immunotherapy (corticosteroids, intravenous immunoglobulin, plasma exchange, rituximab) is used, but response is often less complete than in classic disease and fixed contractures may result.","Where paraneoplastic antibodies such as amphiphysin are found, an underlying malignancy (classically breast) should be sought."],"pathophysiology":"Stiff limb syndrome is an autoimmune disorder of impaired inhibitory neurotransmission within the spinal cord. Loss of GABAergic and glycinergic inhibition, driven by antibodies against glutamic acid decarboxylase (the enzyme synthesising GABA), the glycine receptor, or the presynaptic protein amphiphysin, releases spinal motor circuits from descending and segmental control. The result is continuous involuntary co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles that produces sustained rigidity with superimposed spasms, frequently triggered by touch, movement, noise or emotion. In the focal variant the process is regionally restricted, most often to a lower limb, although it can spread proximally or to the axial and brainstem musculature. Paraneoplastic cases, typically amphiphysin-associated, reflect an immune response directed at tumour antigens that are shared with neural tissue.","additionalDifferentials":["Classic axial stiff person syndrome","Progressive encephalomyelitis with rigidity and myoclonus","Focal or fixed dystonia (organic or functional)","Localised tetanus","Spinal cord pathology (compressive myelopathy or tumour) causing focal rigidity","Neuromyotonia (Isaacs syndrome) or cramp-fasciculation syndrome","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Needle electromyography demonstrating continuous motor unit activity abolished by benzodiazepines","Serum and cerebrospinal fluid glutamic acid decarboxylase, glycine receptor and amphiphysin antibodies","MRI of the spine and brain to exclude structural mimics","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis including oligoclonal bands and cell count","Screening for associated autoimmune disease such as thyroid disease or type 1 diabetes","Malignancy screening (including breast imaging) where paraneoplastic antibodies are present","Documented therapeutic response to a GABAergic agent as supportive evidence"],"redFlags":["Rapid spread with truncal or respiratory muscle involvement or brainstem signs, raising concern for progressive encephalomyelitis with rigidity","Autonomic instability or dysphagia","Features suggesting occult malignancy such as weight loss or a breast mass","Sudden violent spasms causing falls, fractures or joint dislocation","Development of fixed contractures threatening mobility"],"prognosis":"The course is variable; symptomatic and immunotherapy can reduce spasms and rigidity, but the limb variant often responds less completely than classic disease and may leave fixed deformity. Prognosis in paraneoplastic cases depends on the underlying tumour.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing as functional or psychogenic dystonia and delaying immunotherapy.","Assuming glutamic acid decarboxylase antibodies must be present, when their absence does not exclude the diagnosis.","Missing a paraneoplastic cause by omitting amphiphysin testing and malignancy screening.","Overlooking evolution into progressive encephalomyelitis with rigidity, with its life-threatening brainstem and autonomic involvement.","Abruptly withdrawing benzodiazepines or baclofen and precipitating severe rebound spasms."],"management":["GABAergic symptomatic therapy with benzodiazepines (diazepam, clonazepam) and/or baclofen to reduce rigidity and painful spasms","Immunotherapy with corticosteroids, intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange, escalating to rituximab in refractory disease","Botulinum toxin injection for focal dystonic posturing and fixed deformity such as equinovarus","Physiotherapy and regular stretching to preserve range of movement and prevent contractures","Investigate for and treat any underlying tumour in amphiphysin-associated paraneoplastic cases","Screen for and manage associated autoimmune disease such as type 1 diabetes and thyroid disorders","Neurology referral with monitoring for spread to axial and brainstem muscles or overlap with progressive encephalomyelitis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neuroimmunology and movement disorder textbooks","Stiff person spectrum disorder clinical reviews","Paraneoplastic neurological syndrome guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"stiff_person_syndrome":{"name":"Stiff-person syndrome","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPS","stiff-man syndrome","Moersch-Woltman syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An immune-mediated disorder of central nervous system inhibition characterised by progressive axial and proximal muscle rigidity with superimposed painful spasms","Rigidity typically involves the trunk and abdominal wall, producing an exaggerated lumbar lordosis and a stiff, board-like posture","Spasms are often triggered by startle, emotional stress, or sudden movement and can be severe enough to cause falls or fractures","Most cases are associated with high-titre antibodies to glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD65); a smaller subset relate to antibodies against amphiphysin or glycine receptors","It commonly coexists with other autoimmune conditions, particularly type 1 diabetes, thyroid disease, and pernicious anaemia","A paraneoplastic form, often linked to amphiphysin antibodies and breast or other tumours, should be considered especially with atypical or rapidly progressive disease","Electromyography characteristically shows continuous motor unit activity in agonist and antagonist muscles that persists at rest","Treatment principles combine GABA-enhancing symptomatic therapy for rigidity and spasm with immunotherapy directed at the underlying autoimmune process"],"pathophysiology":"Stiff-person syndrome is thought to arise from impaired GABAergic inhibition in the central nervous system, largely on an autoimmune basis. Antibodies to glutamic acid decarboxylase, the enzyme that synthesises the inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA, or to other synaptic proteins such as glycine receptors and amphiphysin, are associated with reduced inhibitory tone in spinal and brainstem circuits. The loss of descending and segmental inhibition leads to continuous co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles, producing sustained rigidity and stimulus-sensitive spasms.","additionalDifferentials":["Tetanus","Neuromyotonia and other peripheral hyperexcitability syndromes","Dystonia and other movement disorders","Parkinsonism and progressive supranuclear palsy with axial rigidity","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder","Myelopathy with spasticity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-GAD65 antibodies, with glycine receptor and amphiphysin antibodies when indicated","Electromyography demonstrating continuous motor unit activity","Screening for associated autoimmune disease including diabetes and thyroid disorders","Investigation for an underlying malignancy in suspected paraneoplastic cases","MRI to exclude structural spinal or cerebral causes of rigidity"],"redFlags":["Spasms severe enough to impair breathing or cause fractures and falls","Rapid progression or atypical features suggesting a paraneoplastic cause","Autonomic instability during severe spasm episodes","New systemic symptoms prompting malignancy search"],"prognosis":"The course is usually chronic and progressive but often responsive, at least partially, to symptomatic and immunomodulatory treatment; some patients stabilise or improve, while paraneoplastic cases depend on treatment of the underlying tumour and can be more refractory.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the disorder for a functional or psychiatric condition given the stress-sensitive spasms","Failing to check for associated autoimmune diseases and an occult malignancy","Overlooking the diagnostic value of anti-GAD antibodies and characteristic EMG findings","Relying on symptomatic drugs alone without considering immunotherapy"],"management":["GABAergic symptomatic therapy with benzodiazepines and/or baclofen to control axial rigidity and spasms","Immunotherapy with intravenous immunoglobulin, corticosteroids or plasma exchange, with rituximab for refractory disease","Physiotherapy, gait aids and fall/fracture prevention for spasm-related instability","Screen for and manage coexisting autoimmune conditions, particularly type 1 diabetes, thyroid disease and pernicious anaemia","Investigate for and treat an underlying malignancy in suspected paraneoplastic amphiphysin-associated disease, including breast imaging","Neurology referral with monitoring of disease progression and treatment response"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"still_disease_adult":{"name":"Adult-onset Still disease","system":"Rheumatology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AOSD","adult Still's disease","adult-onset Still's disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard rheumatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A systemic autoinflammatory disorder characterised by the classic triad of high spiking (quotidian) fevers, arthritis, and an evanescent salmon-coloured rash","The rash is typically transient, appearing with fever spikes and fading between them, often over the trunk and proximal limbs","Sore throat is a frequently reported early symptom","Strikingly high serum ferritin with a low glycosylated (fraction) ferritin is a supportive laboratory clue","Neutrophilic leukocytosis, elevated inflammatory markers, and deranged liver enzymes are common","It is fundamentally a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring careful exclusion of infection, malignancy (especially lymphoma), and other autoimmune disease","IL-1 and IL-6 directed biologic therapies are effective in refractory disease, reflecting the central role of these cytokines","Macrophage activation syndrome is a life-threatening complication that must be recognised promptly"],"pathophysiology":"Adult-onset Still disease is driven by innate immune activation with overproduction of proinflammatory cytokines, notably interleukin-1 beta, interleukin-6, and interleukin-18, producing systemic inflammation affecting joints, skin, reticuloendothelial system, and serosal surfaces. Uncontrolled activation of macrophages and cytotoxic lymphocytes can precipitate a cytokine-storm state (macrophage activation syndrome / secondary haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis).","additionalDifferentials":["Sepsis and other systemic infections (including viral and endocarditis)","Lymphoma and other malignancies","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other connective tissue diseases","Systemic vasculitis","Hereditary autoinflammatory / periodic fever syndromes","Reactive arthritis or other inflammatory arthritides"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ferritin (including glycosylated fraction) and acute-phase reactants","Full blood count, liver function tests, and blood film","Extensive infection screen and blood cultures to exclude sepsis","Autoantibodies (ANA, rheumatoid factor) which are characteristically negative","Imaging and, where indicated, lymph node or bone marrow biopsy to exclude malignancy and assess for haemophagocytosis"],"redFlags":["Features of macrophage activation syndrome: rapidly rising ferritin, falling cell counts, coagulopathy, hepatic dysfunction, and clinical deterioration","Persistent lymphadenopathy or atypical features raising concern for lymphoma","Haemodynamic instability suggesting sepsis or a cytokine-storm state","Cardiorespiratory involvement such as myocarditis, pericardial tamponade, or pleuritis"],"prognosis":"Course is variable, ranging from a self-limited monocyclic illness to a relapsing systemic pattern or a chronic destructive arthritis; overall outcomes are good with modern immunomodulatory and biologic therapy, though macrophage activation syndrome carries significant mortality if not treated urgently.","pitfalls":["Labelling the illness as Still disease before adequately excluding infection and malignancy","Overlooking evolving macrophage activation syndrome amid apparently worsening disease","Attributing the transient rash to a drug reaction","Missing the significance of markedly elevated ferritin"],"management":["NSAIDs for symptom control in mild disease and initial management","Systemic corticosteroids as the mainstay for active systemic and severe disease","Steroid-sparing DMARD therapy, principally methotrexate, for chronic and articular disease","Biologic therapy targeting IL-1 (anakinra, canakinumab) or IL-6 (tocilizumab) for refractory or corticosteroid-dependent disease","Urgent recognition and aggressive treatment of macrophage activation syndrome with high-dose corticosteroids, anakinra and ciclosporin","Exclude infection and malignancy, especially lymphoma, before and during immunomodulatory treatment","Rheumatology-led monitoring of disease activity, ferritin and organ involvement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"sting_associated_vasculopathy_infancy":{"name":"STING-associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy","system":"Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SAVI","STING1 gain-of-function disease","TMEM173-associated vasculopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A monogenic type I interferonopathy caused by gain-of-function variants in STING1 (TMEM173), usually autosomal dominant and frequently arising de novo.","Presents in early infancy with systemic inflammation, recurrent fevers and failure to thrive.","Hallmark acral vasculopathy affects cold-exposed sites (fingertips, toes, nose, ears and cheeks), progressing from violaceous plaques to ulceration, necrosis and digital loss.","Destructive nasal disease with septal perforation can closely mimic granulomatosis with polyangiitis.","Progressive interstitial lung disease is a major cause of morbidity and can culminate in pulmonary fibrosis and respiratory failure.","A strongly positive blood interferon signature supports the diagnosis, while positive autoantibodies (ANA, ANCA) frequently cause diagnostic confusion.","Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors that block downstream interferon signalling are the mainstay; conventional corticosteroids and cytotoxic immunosuppression are typically inadequate.","Cold exposure characteristically exacerbates the cutaneous vasculopathy."],"pathophysiology":"STING (stimulator of interferon genes), encoded by STING1/TMEM173, is an endoplasmic reticulum adaptor that normally senses cyclic dinucleotides generated by the cytosolic DNA sensor cGAS. Gain-of-function variants lock STING in a constitutively active conformation independent of its ligand, driving persistent TBK1-IRF3 activation and unrestrained transcription of type I interferons, predominantly interferon-beta. The resulting chronic interferon signalling through the JAK-STAT pathway upregulates interferon-stimulated genes in endothelium, leucocytes and lung, producing small-vessel inflammation, microthrombosis and tissue ischaemia that manifest as the characteristic acral vasculopathy and interstitial lung disease. Sustained interferon exposure also promotes a breach of self-tolerance with autoantibody production and T-cell cytopenia, explaining the frequent overlap with systemic autoimmune disease and the diagnostic confusion with vasculitis and lupus.","additionalDifferentials":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and other ANCA-associated vasculitides","Aicardi-Goutieres syndrome and other type I interferonopathies","CANDLE / proteasome-associated autoinflammatory syndrome (PRAAS)","Systemic lupus erythematosus with cutaneous vasculopathy or chilblain lupus","Cryoglobulinaemic or cryofibrinogenaemic vasculopathy","Neonatal-onset multisystem inflammatory disease (NOMID / CINCA)","Antiphospholipid syndrome with acral ischaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing for STING1 (TMEM173) variants","Blood interferon signature (interferon-stimulated gene expression score)","High-resolution CT of the chest for interstitial lung disease","Skin biopsy of affected acral lesions showing small-vessel vasculopathy and microthrombi","Autoantibody panel including ANA, ANCA and antiphospholipid antibodies","Pulmonary function testing including gas transfer","Lymphocyte subset quantification"],"redFlags":["Progressive digital ischaemia, necrosis or gangrene threatening tissue loss","Rapidly worsening breathlessness or hypoxia suggesting progressive interstitial lung disease","Nasal septal perforation or destructive midface involvement","Failure to thrive with unremitting systemic inflammation in an infant"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable and depends largely on the severity of interstitial lung disease, which is the principal determinant of mortality. Early JAK inhibition may stabilise disease, but established pulmonary fibrosis and digital loss are often irreversible.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the disease as ANCA-associated vasculitis or lupus because of positive autoantibodies with nasal and pulmonary involvement.","Expecting conventional corticosteroids and cytotoxic immunosuppression to control disease, which they rarely do.","Overlooking early interstitial lung disease when respiratory symptoms are still mild.","Attributing acral lesions to primary Raynaud phenomenon or frostbite."],"management":["JAK inhibitors (baricitinib, ruxolitinib or tofacitinib) as targeted therapy to block type I interferon JAK-STAT signalling, as conventional immunosuppression is often inadequate","Supportive wound care for acral ulceration and necrosis, with cold-avoidance measures and protection of the extremities","Monitor and manage interstitial lung disease with serial high-resolution CT and pulmonary function including gas transfer, this being the chief determinant of outcome","Vasodilator and antiplatelet measures to limit digital ischaemia and preserve tissue","Genetic counselling for the autosomal dominant, frequently de novo, STING1 variant with family assessment","Multidisciplinary care across clinical immunology, rheumatology, respiratory medicine and dermatology","Escalate early for respiratory failure and consider lung transplantation assessment in end-stage pulmonary fibrosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric rheumatology and clinical immunology textbooks","Autoinflammatory disease and interferonopathy reference sources","Inborn errors of immunity reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"stingray_injury":{"name":"Stingray injury","system":"Multisystem / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["stingray envenomation","stingray sting"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard toxicology and marine-envenomation references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Injury combines a penetrating or laceration wound from the barbed spine with envenomation by a heat-labile venom","Pain is typically immediate, intense and out of proportion to the visible wound","Immersion of the affected part in hot (not scalding) water helps relieve pain by denaturing the venom","Retained spine or sheath fragments in the wound are common and can be missed","Wounds are prone to secondary infection, including marine organisms, so wound care and consideration of antibiotics are important","Truncal or penetrating chest and abdominal wounds can be life-threatening and need urgent surgical assessment"],"pathophysiology":"Stingray injury results from the tail spine penetrating tissue while releasing venom from glandular tissue along the spine. The venom is a heat-labile mixture that causes intense local pain, vasoconstriction and tissue injury, while the barbed spine produces mechanical laceration and can retain fragments. Local necrosis and a high infection risk follow from the combination of envenomation, tissue trauma and contamination, and deep penetrating wounds may damage vital structures.","additionalDifferentials":["Other marine envenomation (stonefish, weeverfish)","Simple laceration or puncture wound","Retained foreign body without envenomation","Secondary wound infection or cellulitis","Allergic reaction to the sting"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wound exploration and imaging to identify retained spine fragments","Assessment of the depth and structures involved, especially for truncal wounds","Consideration of tetanus status and infection risk","Observation for systemic symptoms in severe envenoming","Surgical review for deep or penetrating body-cavity wounds"],"redFlags":["Penetrating chest, abdominal or neck wounds","Signs of systemic envenoming or haemodynamic compromise","Evidence of deep infection or necrosis","Neurovascular compromise of a limb"],"prognosis":"Most limb injuries do well with hot-water analgesia, wound care and removal of foreign material, but truncal penetrating wounds and severe infections can be serious or fatal.","pitfalls":["Failing to search for and remove retained spine fragments","Prematurely closing a contaminated marine wound","Underestimating pain and delaying hot-water immersion","Missing deep penetrating injury to vital structures"],"management":["Immediate hot-water immersion at a tolerable, non-scalding temperature to denature the heat-labile venom and relieve pain","Wound exploration, irrigation and removal of retained spine or sheath fragments, leaving wounds open to heal by secondary intention","Analgesia, including local anaesthetic infiltration or regional block for severe pain","Tetanus prophylaxis and thorough wound cleaning","Antibiotics covering marine organisms such as ciprofloxacin or doxycycline for Vibrio in high-risk or infected wounds","Urgent surgical assessment for truncal or body-cavity penetrating wounds that may injure vital structures","Observe for secondary infection and systemic features of severe envenoming"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"stonefish_envenomation":{"name":"Stonefish envenomation","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Synanceia sting","stonefish sting","stinging fish envenomation","reef stonefish injury","venomous fish spine injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / WHO envenoming guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Stonefish (Synanceia) have dorsal spines that inject venom when trodden on, causing immediate and often excruciating pain out of proportion to the wound","Local effects include intense pain, rapid swelling, discolouration and sometimes tissue necrosis, with retained spine fragments a common complication","Hot-water immersion of the affected part at a tolerable temperature is a key first-aid measure, as the venom is heat-labile and this can relieve pain","Systemic features may include nausea, sweating, weakness and, uncommonly, cardiovascular and respiratory effects with severe envenomation","Stonefish antivenom is available and used for severe pain or systemic envenomation; wound exploration for retained spine and foreign material is important","Secondary wound infection is common, so wound care, tetanus prophylaxis and monitoring for infection including marine organisms are essential","Distinguish envenomation pain and swelling from evolving secondary soft-tissue infection during follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"Stonefish venom, delivered through grooved dorsal spines, contains a pore-forming protein (verrucotoxin/stonustoxin family) that disrupts cell membranes and causes intense local pain, oedema and potential tissue necrosis. Systemic absorption can produce cardiovascular effects including hypotension and arrhythmia, and the venom has actions on skeletal and cardiac muscle. Many venom components are heat-labile, which underlies the analgesic benefit of hot-water immersion. Puncture wounds frequently retain spine fragments and are prone to secondary infection by marine and skin flora.","additionalDifferentials":["Other venomous fish spine injury (lionfish, scorpionfish, weeverfish, stingray)","Sea urchin spine injury","Simple puncture wound with secondary infection","Cellulitis or necrotising soft-tissue infection","Retained foreign body without envenomation","Marine bacterial infection (e.g. Vibrio, Mycobacterium marinum)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wound imaging (ultrasound or radiography) to detect retained spine fragments","Assessment of neurovascular status of the affected limb","Electrocardiography and monitoring in systemic envenomation","Inflammatory markers and wound cultures if infection is suspected","Renal function and creatine kinase in severe systemic cases"],"redFlags":["Systemic features such as hypotension, arrhythmia or respiratory compromise","Signs of compartment syndrome or extensive necrosis","Spreading soft-tissue infection or systemic sepsis","Retained deep foreign material near neurovascular structures","Uncontrolled severe pain despite first aid"],"prognosis":"Most stings cause severe transient pain that improves with hot-water immersion and analgesia, with full recovery. Complications arise chiefly from retained spines, secondary infection and, rarely, systemic envenomation; delayed or inadequate wound care prolongs morbidity.","pitfalls":["Using very hot water that scalds the anaesthetised, painful limb","Missing retained spine fragments leading to persistent pain and infection","Neglecting tetanus prophylaxis and marine wound care","Attributing later worsening solely to venom rather than recognising secondary infection"],"management":["Immediate hot-water immersion at a tolerable temperature to inactivate the heat-labile venom and relieve pain","Stonefish antivenom for severe pain unresponsive to immersion or for systemic envenomation","Local anaesthetic infiltration or regional block with systemic analgesia for pain control","Wound exploration and removal of retained spine fragments and foreign material","Tetanus prophylaxis and antibiotics covering marine organisms where infection risk is high or established","Cardiac monitoring and supportive care for cardiovascular and respiratory effects in systemic envenomation","Surgical review for deep wounds and ongoing monitoring for secondary infection and necrosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Australian marine envenomation management guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"storage_pool_disease":{"name":"Platelet storage pool disease","system":"Haematological system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Storage pool deficiency","Platelet granule deficiency","SPD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A heterogeneous group of inherited platelet disorders defined by deficient granule content, comprising dense granule (delta), alpha granule (grey platelet syndrome) and combined (alpha-delta) forms.","Patients present with lifelong mucocutaneous bleeding: easy bruising, epistaxis, menorrhagia and prolonged bleeding after surgery or dental extraction.","Platelet count is usually normal, though grey platelet syndrome is characteristically associated with mild thrombocytopenia and large agranular platelets.","Light transmission aggregometry typically shows absent second-wave aggregation and a blunted response to ADP, adrenaline and collagen.","Dense granule deficiency can be confirmed by whole-mount electron microscopy showing reduced granule number or by flow-cytometric mepacrine uptake studies.","Storage pool defects may be isolated or part of syndromes such as Hermansky-Pudlak (with oculocutaneous albinism) and Chediak-Higashi.","Grey platelet syndrome arises from NBEAL2 mutations and may progress to marrow fibrosis and splenomegaly.","Management centres on antifibrinolytics, desmopressin for minor procedures and platelet transfusion for major bleeding, with judicious use given alloimmunisation risk."],"pathophysiology":"Platelet storage pool disease results from defective formation, cargo loading or retention of platelet secretory granules. Dense (delta) granules normally store ADP, ATP, calcium and serotonin that amplify platelet activation and recruitment; their deficiency impairs the secondary wave of aggregation. Alpha granules store adhesive and haemostatic proteins including von Willebrand factor, fibrinogen, factor V and platelet factor 4; their absence, as in grey platelet syndrome from NBEAL2 mutation, produces agranular platelets and can drive marrow fibrosis through unregulated growth-factor release. In syndromic forms such as Hermansky-Pudlak and Chediak-Higashi, mutations in genes governing lysosome-related organelle biogenesis and vesicle trafficking affect melanosomes and granules alike, explaining the associated albinism. The common consequence is impaired granule secretion and a functional platelet defect causing mucocutaneous bleeding despite an often normal platelet count.","additionalDifferentials":["von Willebrand disease","Glanzmann thrombasthenia","Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Aspirin or other antiplatelet drug effect","Mild coagulation factor deficiency","Acquired platelet dysfunction from uraemia or myeloproliferative disease","Vascular or connective tissue bleeding disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film for platelet number and morphology","Light transmission platelet aggregometry with multiple agonists","Whole-mount transmission electron microscopy for dense granule count","Flow-cytometric mepacrine uptake and release studies","Platelet nucleotide (ADP/ATP) content and secretion assays","von Willebrand factor antigen and activity to exclude von Willebrand disease","Targeted genetic testing (for example NBEAL2, HPS genes, LYST)"],"redFlags":["Life-threatening haemorrhage after trauma, surgery or childbirth","Progressive splenomegaly and marrow fibrosis in grey platelet syndrome","Pulmonary fibrosis or colitis suggesting underlying Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome","Recurrent severe infections or lymphohistiocytosis suggesting Chediak-Higashi syndrome","Severe menorrhagia causing symptomatic anaemia"],"prognosis":"Bleeding is usually mild to moderate and lifelong, and most patients have a normal life expectancy with appropriate peri-procedural precautions. Prognosis is chiefly determined by any associated syndrome, such as pulmonary fibrosis in Hermansky-Pudlak or the immunodeficiency of Chediak-Higashi.","pitfalls":["Attributing mucocutaneous bleeding to a normal platelet count and overlooking a qualitative defect.","Missing an underlying syndrome such as Hermansky-Pudlak by not examining for oculocutaneous albinism.","Interpreting aggregometry without accounting for recent antiplatelet or non-steroidal drug exposure.","Repeated platelet transfusion leading to alloimmunisation and refractoriness.","Failing to give peri-operative antifibrinolytic cover for mucosal surgery."],"management":["Antifibrinolytics such as tranexamic acid for mucocutaneous bleeding and dental or surgical cover","Desmopressin (DDAVP) to enhance haemostasis for minor procedures and bleeding episodes","Platelet transfusion reserved for major bleeding or major surgery, minimising alloimmunisation","Avoid antiplatelet drugs including aspirin and NSAIDs that further impair platelet function","Hormonal therapy (combined oral contraceptive or levonorgestrel intrauterine system) with iron replacement for menorrhagia and iron-deficiency anaemia","Genetic counselling and evaluation for associated syndromes such as Hermansky-Pudlak and Chediak-Higashi","Haematology-led peri-procedural planning and surveillance for syndrome-specific complications such as pulmonary fibrosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology, current edition","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood, current edition","International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis guidance on inherited platelet function disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"strabismus":{"name":"Strabismus","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["squint","ocular misalignment","esotropia","exotropia","heterotropia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Strabismus is a misalignment of the visual axes and is described by direction: esotropia (inward), exotropia (outward), hypertropia (upward), and hypotropia (downward)","It is classified as concomitant (the deviation is similar in all directions of gaze, typical of childhood squints) or incomitant (varying with gaze, suggesting a nerve palsy or restriction)","In children the main concern is amblyopia in the deviating eye, whereas new-onset strabismus in adults usually causes diplopia","The cover test (cover-uncover and alternate cover) is the key clinical method for detecting and characterising a manifest or latent deviation","Accommodative esotropia is linked to uncorrected hypermetropia and often improves substantially with the appropriate glasses","A new incomitant squint, especially with a cranial nerve palsy pattern, warrants investigation for a neurological cause","Management principles include correcting refractive error, treating any amblyopia (e.g. occlusion), and surgical realignment of the extraocular muscles when indicated","Leukocoria or a sudden squint in a child requires urgent examination to exclude retinoblastoma or other serious pathology"],"pathophysiology":"Strabismus arises from a failure of the normal binocular alignment maintained by coordinated extraocular muscle action and central fusional control. Causes include refractive error (notably hypermetropia driving accommodative esotropia), sensory deprivation from poor vision in one eye, abnormalities of the neuromuscular pathways (cranial nerve palsies), mechanical restriction of muscles, and disorders of central fusion. In children, misalignment during the critical period of visual development leads to suppression of the deviating eye's image and can result in amblyopia and loss of binocular single vision.","additionalDifferentials":["Pseudostrabismus from prominent epicanthic folds or facial asymmetry","Cranial nerve palsy (third, fourth, or sixth nerve)","Restrictive myopathy (e.g. thyroid eye disease, orbital fracture)","Myasthenia gravis","Internuclear ophthalmoplegia and other supranuclear disorders","Retinoblastoma or other cause of sensory deprivation in a child"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cover-uncover and alternate cover tests to detect and measure the deviation","Assessment of visual acuity in each eye to detect amblyopia","Cycloplegic refraction to identify a correctable refractive cause","Ocular motility examination and assessment of binocular function/stereopsis","Neuroimaging when an incomitant or paralytic squint suggests a neurological lesion"],"redFlags":["New-onset incomitant squint with features of a cranial nerve palsy suggesting intracranial pathology","Leukocoria or a squint accompanying poor vision in a child, raising concern for retinoblastoma","Sudden diplopia with headache or other neurological signs","Painful or restricted eye movements suggesting orbital or inflammatory disease"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are generally favourable when strabismus and any associated amblyopia are detected and treated early in childhood, with good prospects for alignment and, in some cases, binocular vision; late detection reduces the chance of restoring binocularity, and adult acquired strabismus depends on the underlying cause.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a genuine squint as pseudostrabismus and delaying amblyopia treatment","Failing to perform cycloplegic refraction and missing correctable accommodative esotropia","Not investigating a new incomitant or paralytic squint for a neurological cause","Overlooking a serious underlying cause such as retinoblastoma in a child presenting with a squint"],"management":["Correct refractive error, particularly hypermetropia, with spectacles for accommodative esotropia","Treat amblyopia in children with occlusion (patching) or atropine penalisation of the better-seeing eye","Prisms incorporated into spectacles to relieve diplopia in acquired adult strabismus","Extraocular muscle surgery to realign the visual axes where non-surgical measures are insufficient","Botulinum toxin injection to extraocular muscles in selected cases","Investigate and manage any underlying neurological cause of an incomitant or paralytic squint","Orthoptic assessment and long-term follow-up of alignment, binocular function and stereopsis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"streptococcal_infections":{"name":"Streptococcal Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["group A strep","GAS","Streptococcus pyogenes","group B strep","GBS","Streptococcus agalactiae"],"chapter":153,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1206-1214","clinicalPearls":["Group A Streptococcus (GAS; S. pyogenes) is the only common cause of sore throat that should be treated with antibiotics; GAS accounts for 20-40% of exudative pharyngitis in children and is rare under age 3 (p.1208).","Streptococcal pharyngitis cannot be reliably diagnosed on clinical grounds alone given overlap with many viral and bacterial causes; throat culture remains the diagnostic gold standard, with rapid antigen tests (specificity >95%) a useful adjunct (p.1208).","Antibiotic treatment of GAS pharyngitis is given primarily to prevent suppurative complications and acute rheumatic fever; symptoms resolve in 3-5 days and are shortened little by treatment (p.1208).","Prevention of ARF depends on eradicating the organism from the pharynx (not just symptom resolution) and requires a 10-day penicillin course; a single dose or short course is inadequate (p.1208).","Penicillin treatment of streptococcal pharyngitis reduces the likelihood of ARF but NOT of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (p.1209).","Worldwide, GAS infections and their post-infectious sequelae (chiefly ARF and rheumatic heart disease) cause an estimated 500,000 deaths/year; incidence of GAS disease and RHD is roughly tenfold higher in resource-limited countries (p.1206-1207).","Scarlet fever is streptococcal infection (usually pharyngitis) plus a rash from pyrogenic exotoxins: blanching sandpaper-textured papular rash on trunk and extremities sparing palms and soles, with circumoral pallor, strawberry tongue, and Pastia's lines in skin folds, followed by desquamation of palms and soles (p.1209).","Impetigo is most often a superficial GAS infection (occasionally other streptococci or S. aureus) of young children; honey/amber crusted lesions on face and legs; lesions are generally NOT painful and fever is NOT a feature — if fever is present, suspect deeper infection or another diagnosis (p.1209-1210).","Erysipelas is a streptococcal cellulitis of the superficial dermis: bright red, warm, shiny, edematous plaque with a sharply demarcated raised border and peau d'orange texture, typically on the face (malar) or lower extremities, with abrupt onset, fever and chills; classic cases are almost always due to beta-hemolytic streptococci (usually GAS) (p.1210).","Streptococcal cellulitis favors sites of disrupted lymphatic drainage (prior cellulitis, post-mastectomy/axillary dissection arm, prior DVT or lymphedema leg, saphenous vein harvest site); treating tinea pedis can stop recurrent leg cellulitis after vein harvest (p.1210-1211).","GAS is among the few pathogens producing wound infection and surrounding cellulitis within the first 24 h after surgery, with thin exudate and rapid spread (p.1211).","Necrotizing fasciitis is a surgical emergency: GAS is implicated in ~60% of cases; early surgical exploration and extensive debridement are diagnostic, therapeutic, and life-saving, with antibiotics as a critical adjunct (p.1211).","Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome (TSS) features fever, hypotension, renal impairment, and respiratory distress; in contrast to staphylococcal TSS, most patients with streptococcal TSS are bacteremic, rash usually does NOT develop, and the most common associated infection is a soft tissue infection (necrotizing fasciitis, myositis, or cellulitis) (p.1211-1212).","Streptococcal TSS carries a mortality rate of >=30%, mostly from shock and respiratory failure; because of its rapidly progressive lethal course, early recognition is essential (p.1212).","Group B Streptococcus (GBS; S. agalactiae) is a leading cause of neonatal sepsis and meningitis and of peripartum fever; early-onset disease (first week, median 20 h) is acquired from the colonized maternal genital tract, while late-onset disease (1 week-3 months) most often presents as meningitis, frequently capsular type III (p.1212-1213).","GBS infection in adults increasingly affects the elderly and those with chronic illness (e.g., diabetes, malignancy), presenting as cellulitis/soft-tissue infection including diabetic ulcers, UTI, pneumonia, endocarditis, and septic arthritis (p.1213-1214)."],"pathophysiology":"GAS (S. pyogenes) is the single species of Lancefield group A, a beta-hemolytic, chain-forming, gram-positive facultative anaerobe. M protein (encoded by the emm gene, >200 emm types) is the major surface protein and chief virulence factor: it resists phagocytic killing partly by binding plasma fibrinogen and interfering with complement deposition, and type-specific anti-M antibody confers protection only against the same M type. A hyaluronic acid capsule (a weak immunogen owing to structural identity with mammalian connective tissue) protects against phagocytosis and aids pharyngeal colonization via CD44 binding; capsule is not essential for virulence. GAS elaborates streptolysins S and O, streptokinase, DNases, SpyCEP (cleaves IL-8 to impair neutrophil recruitment), and pyrogenic exotoxins (formerly erythrogenic toxins) that cause the scarlet fever rash and act as superantigens triggering T-cell cytokine release, implicated in severe invasive disease including necrotizing fasciitis and streptococcal TSS. The post-infectious sequelae ARF (uniquely associated with S. pyogenes) and PSGN are thought to result from immune responses to streptococcal infection. GBS is encapsulated by 1 of 10 polysaccharides; the capsule is a key virulence factor and type-specific anticapsular antibody is protective.","additionalDifferentials":["Scarlet fever differential: measles and other viral exanthems, Kawasaki disease, toxic shock syndrome, and systemic allergic/drug eruptions (p.1209)","Pharyngitis differential includes group C/G streptococci, mixed anaerobes (Vincent's angina), Arcanobacterium haemolyticum, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Corynebacterium diphtheriae, EBV/CMV/HIV, and other viruses (Table 153-2, p.1208)","Impetigo differential: bullous impetigo due to S. aureus, and herpetic lesions (orolabial HSV, chickenpox, or zoster), distinguished by grouped discrete vesicles and positive Tzanck/PCR (p.1210)"],"infectionMimics":["Staphylococcus aureus cellulitis/soft tissue infection mimicking streptococcal cellulitis (suspect S. aureus when cellulitis develops around a wound or ulcer) (p.1210-1211)","Bullous impetigo (S. aureus) mimicking streptococcal impetigo (p.1210)","Staphylococcal toxic shock syndrome (shares features with streptococcal TSS, but staph TSS patients are usually not bacteremic) (p.1211-1212)","Herpes simplex virus or varicella-zoster lesions mimicking impetigo (p.1210)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Kawasaki disease (mimics scarlet fever) (p.1209)","Systemic allergic reactions / drug eruptions (mimic scarlet fever) (p.1209)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Throat culture (gold standard; vigorous rubbing of both tonsillar pillars) and rapid antigen test (latex agglutination/EIA, specificity >95%, relative sensitivity 70-90%); confirm negative rapid test with culture in higher-risk individuals (p.1208)","Antibody tests to streptolysin O (ASO) and DNase B for serodiagnosis of preceding streptococcal infection in suspected ARF or PSGN (p.1207)","emm-typing by PCR/sequencing of the variable region of the emm gene (CDC database) for strain typing (p.1207)","In streptococcal TSS, laboratory findings: marked left shift with immature granulocytes, hypocalcemia, hypoalbuminemia, and thrombocytopenia (worsening on day 2-3) (p.1211)","Blood cultures (most streptococcal TSS patients are bacteremic) (p.1211-1212)","Early surgical exploration of suspected necrotizing fasciitis for diagnosis (reveals fascial-plane necrosis and inflammatory fluid without muscle involvement) (p.1211)","Cultures of vesicular fluid in difficult impetigo cases; culture to rule out MRSA if empirical treatment fails (p.1210)"],"redFlags":["Necrotizing fasciitis is a surgical emergency: severe pain out of proportion to physical findings, with only minimal early skin erythema; progression over hours to dusky/mottled erythema and edema; tenderness evolving into anesthesia from infarction of cutaneous nerves — demands early surgical exploration and debridement (p.1211)","Streptococcal TSS: rapidly progressive, lethal (mortality >=30%) with hypotension and multiorgan failure; early recognition essential (p.1211-1212)","Fever in a patient with impetigo suggests extension to deeper tissues or an alternative diagnosis (p.1210)","GAS bacteremia without an identified source raises the possibility of endocarditis, occult abscess, or osteomyelitis (p.1212)","Unusually severe or prolonged pharyngitis, or localized pain with high fever and toxic appearance, suggests a peritonsillar or parapharyngeal abscess (p.1209)"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated GAS pharyngitis is self-limited, with symptoms resolving in 3-5 days. Suppurative complications (peritonsillar/retropharyngeal abscess, cervical lymphadenitis, sinusitis, otitis, meningitis, bacteremia, endocarditis, pneumonia) are now uncommon with antibiotics; nonsuppurative sequelae are ARF and PSGN. Necrotizing fasciitis and streptococcal TSS are rapidly progressive and life-threatening; streptococcal TSS has a mortality rate of >=30%. Globally GAS sequelae account for ~500,000 deaths/year. Neonatal GBS incidence has fallen from ~2-3 to ~0.6 per 1000 live births with prenatal screening. Recurrent invasive GBS in adults occurs in ~4% of cases.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing streptococcal pharyngitis on clinical grounds alone is unreliable; clinicians over-prescribe antibiotics for sore throat (p.1208)","Rapid antigen tests are less sensitive than culture (relative sensitivity 70-90%), so a negative rapid test should be confirmed by throat culture in higher-risk patients (p.1208)","Improper swab technique lowers rapid-test sensitivity; both tonsillar pillars (and any exudate/ulceration) must be vigorously sampled (p.1208)","In necrotizing fasciitis, early physical findings may be unimpressive despite severe pain; relying on skin appearance delays diagnosis (p.1211)","Macrolide resistance is common in many countries and rising in invasive US GAS isolates (>30% since 2021); avoid macrolides where resistance exceeds 5-10% unless susceptibility is known (p.1208-1209)","Clindamycin resistance occurs in >=30% of invasive GAS isolates; susceptibility should not be assumed without in vitro testing before relying on clindamycin (p.1212)","Aggressive eradication of asymptomatic GAS pharyngeal carriage is usually not justified (carriers have lower risk of ARF and of transmission than symptomatic patients); exceptions arise when a carrier is a source of transmission to others (p.1209)","Endocarditis due to nonenterococcal group D streptococci (S. gallolyticus/S. bovis group) is associated with occult GI neoplasm (often colon carcinoma/polyp; abnormalities in >60%) and should prompt GI evaluation (p.1214)"],"management":["Penicillin, or amoxicillin, as first-line antibiotic for group A streptococcal pharyngitis, completing the full course to eradicate pharyngeal carriage and prevent acute rheumatic fever","A macrolide or first-generation cephalosporin for penicillin-allergic patients","For invasive disease and streptococcal toxic shock syndrome, combine a beta-lactam with clindamycin to suppress toxin production","Adjunctive intravenous immunoglobulin for streptococcal toxic shock syndrome","Urgent surgical exploration and debridement for necrotising fasciitis","Supportive intensive care with fluid resuscitation and organ support for invasive and toxic-shock disease","Drainage of suppurative complications such as peritonsillar abscess, and secondary antibiotic prophylaxis to prevent recurrent rheumatic fever"],"severityClassification":"Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome case definition (CDC, 1993; updated 2010; Table 153-4): I. Isolation of GAS from (A) a normally sterile site or (B) a nonsterile site; II. Clinical signs of severity = (A) hypotension AND (B) >=2 of: renal impairment, coagulopathy, liver function impairment, ARDS, generalized erythematous macular rash that may desquamate, or soft tissue necrosis (including necrotizing fasciitis or myositis) or gangrene. A DEFINITE case meets IA + IIA + IIB; a PROBABLE case meets IB + IIA + IIB if no other etiology is identified. Pharyngitis pretest risk is stratified clinically by the Centor criteria (fever history, absence of cough, tender anterior cervical adenopathy, tonsillar exudate/swelling).","references":["Wessels MR. Streptococcal Infections. In: Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd ed, Ch. 153, pp. 1206-1214.","Carapetis JR et al. The global burden of group A streptococcal diseases. Lancet Infect Dis 5:685, 2005.","Streptococcal Toxic Shock Syndrome (STSS) 2010 Case Definition, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention."],"crossLinks":[]},"stress_fracture":{"name":"Stress fracture","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fatigue fracture","insufficiency fracture","march fracture","bone stress injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon / standard sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as gradually worsening, activity-related localised bone pain that eases with rest, typically after a change or increase in training load","Fatigue fractures occur when normal bone is subjected to repetitive abnormal load, whereas insufficiency fractures occur when normal load acts on weakened bone such as osteoporotic or pathological bone","Common sites include the metatarsals (march fracture), tibia, navicular, and femoral neck, with the tibia most frequent in runners","Focal bony tenderness, pain on hopping or percussion, and pain reproduced by loading the bone support the diagnosis","Early plain radiographs are often normal, and MRI or bone scan is more sensitive for detecting bone stress injury","Certain high-risk sites, including the tension side of the femoral neck, anterior tibial cortex, navicular, and base of the fifth metatarsal, are prone to non-union or completion and need protected weight-bearing or surgical referral","Management of low-risk fractures is relative rest, activity modification, and graduated return to loading once pain-free","In female athletes consider the female athlete triad / relative energy deficiency in sport, with attention to energy availability, menstrual function, and bone health","Recurrent or multiple stress fractures warrant assessment for metabolic bone disease, vitamin D deficiency, and endocrine causes"],"pathophysiology":"A stress fracture develops when repetitive submaximal loading causes microdamage to accumulate faster than bone can remodel and repair it. Osteoclastic resorption temporarily outpaces osteoblastic formation, weakening the cortex and, if loading continues, progressing from a stress reaction to a frank fracture line. Fatigue fractures reflect excessive load on normal bone, while insufficiency fractures reflect normal load on abnormally weak bone.","additionalDifferentials":["Medial tibial stress syndrome (shin splints)","Chronic exertional compartment syndrome","Tendinopathy or muscle strain","Nerve entrapment","Osteomyelitis or bone infection","Osteoid osteoma or other bone tumour","Pathological fracture from metastasis or metabolic bone disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs, recognising they are frequently normal early and may show periosteal reaction or a fracture line later","MRI as the preferred sensitive test to grade bone marrow oedema and confirm the fracture","Radionuclide bone scan as an alternative sensitive investigation","CT for suspected navicular or other fractures needing detailed cortical assessment","Metabolic and endocrine workup, vitamin D, and bone density assessment in recurrent, atypical, or insufficiency fractures"],"redFlags":["High-risk sites such as the femoral neck (especially tension side), anterior tibia, tarsal navicular, and fifth metatarsal base that risk non-union or displacement","Groin or hip pain with weight-bearing suggesting femoral neck stress fracture requiring urgent assessment","Features of pathological or insufficiency fracture such as minimal trauma, older age, or known malignancy","Systemic symptoms or signs suggesting infection or tumour"],"prognosis":"Low-risk stress fractures generally heal fully within six to twelve weeks with load modification and graded return, whereas high-risk fractures may progress to non-union or complete fracture and can require prolonged protection or surgery.","pitfalls":["Excluding a stress fracture on the basis of a normal early radiograph","Failing to recognise high-risk sites and continuing loading with risk of completion or non-union","Overlooking relative energy deficiency in sport or metabolic bone disease in recurrent cases","Returning to full training too early before the bone has healed"],"management":["Relative rest and load modification, reducing the offending repetitive activity to allow remodelling to catch up with microdamage","Protected weight-bearing with a walking boot or crutches for painful lower-limb fractures","Analgesia with paracetamol for pain control","Graded, progressive return to activity once pain-free, correcting training errors and biomechanical factors","Surgical referral and fixation for high-risk sites prone to non-union, including the tension-side femoral neck, anterior tibial cortex, navicular and base of the fifth metatarsal","Assess and optimise bone health and energy availability, checking vitamin D, calcium and bone density and addressing relative energy deficiency in recurrent or insufficiency fractures","Physiotherapy for rehabilitation and prevention of recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"stress_incontinence":{"name":"Stress Urinary Incontinence","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["stress urinary incontinence","stress incontinence","SUI","effort urinary incontinence","exertional incontinence"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Stress urinary incontinence is involuntary leakage of urine on effort or exertion, such as coughing, sneezing, laughing, or physical activity, when abdominal pressure rises","It results from a weakened sphincter mechanism or poor support of the bladder neck and urethra, so that a rise in intra-abdominal pressure overcomes urethral closure","Key risk factors include vaginal childbirth, ageing and menopause, obesity, chronic cough or straining, and previous pelvic surgery","It must be distinguished from urgency incontinence (leakage with a sudden compelling desire to void); many women have a mixed picture with features of both","Diagnosis is largely clinical, supported by a bladder diary, cough stress test, and exclusion of infection and significant post-void residual urine","First-line management is conservative: supervised pelvic floor muscle training, weight loss, treatment of chronic cough or constipation, and lifestyle measures","Adjuncts include continence pessaries or devices, and duloxetine may be considered in selected cases where surgery is unsuitable or declined","Surgical options for persistent bothersome stress incontinence include mid-urethral slings, colposuspension, and urethral bulking agents, chosen according to patient factors and shared decision-making","Urodynamic testing is reserved for complex, mixed, or recurrent cases or before surgery where the diagnosis is uncertain, rather than being routine"],"pathophysiology":"Continence during exertion depends on adequate urethral closure pressure and on the bladder neck and urethra being well supported so that rises in abdominal pressure are transmitted equally to the bladder and proximal urethra. Two overlapping mechanisms cause stress incontinence: urethral hypermobility, in which weakened pelvic floor and endopelvic fascial support (often after childbirth or with ageing) allows the bladder neck to descend and the pressure transmission to become unequal; and intrinsic sphincter deficiency, in which the urethral closure mechanism itself is weakened, for example after surgery, radiation, or oestrogen deficiency. In either case a cough or exertion transiently raises bladder pressure above urethral resistance and urine escapes.","additionalDifferentials":["Urgency urinary incontinence / overactive bladder","Mixed urinary incontinence","Overflow incontinence from chronic retention","Urinary tract infection causing transient symptoms","Vesicovaginal or urethrovaginal fistula (continuous leakage)","Urethral diverticulum","Functional incontinence from mobility or cognitive impairment"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bladder (frequency-volume) diary to characterise the pattern of leakage","Cough stress test to demonstrate leakage with raised abdominal pressure","Urinalysis and culture to exclude infection","Post-void residual measurement to exclude incomplete emptying","Pad testing to quantify leakage where useful","Urodynamic studies in complex, mixed, recurrent, or pre-surgical cases"],"redFlags":["Visible haematuria requiring exclusion of urinary tract malignancy","Continuous leakage suggesting a fistula","Significant post-void residual or voiding difficulty indicating retention with overflow","New neurological symptoms suggesting a neurological cause","Recurrent urinary infections or pain warranting further evaluation"],"prognosis":"Stress incontinence is not life-threatening but can substantially impair quality of life. Many women improve significantly with conservative measures, particularly sustained pelvic floor muscle training and weight loss. Surgical procedures offer high rates of symptom improvement in appropriately selected patients, though some experience recurrence or complications, so treatment is individualised through shared decision-making.","pitfalls":["Labelling all incontinence as stress type without identifying a mixed or urgency component","Proceeding to surgery without a trial of conservative therapy or without clarifying the diagnosis","Missing overflow incontinence by not checking post-void residual","Overlooking a fistula in a patient with continuous leakage, especially after surgery or childbirth","Attributing symptoms to stress incontinence when haematuria or infection needs investigation"],"management":["Sustained supervised pelvic floor muscle training as the first-line mainstay of treatment","Lifestyle measures: weight reduction, treatment of chronic cough and constipation, and moderation of caffeine and fluid intake","Duloxetine as a pharmacological option where pelvic floor training is unsuccessful and surgery is declined or unsuitable","Topical vaginal oestrogen for postmenopausal urethral and vaginal atrophy, with a continence pessary or intravaginal device as a non-surgical adjunct","Surgery for persistent bothersome leakage: mid-urethral sling, colposuspension, autologous fascial sling, or urethral bulking agents for intrinsic sphincter deficiency","Reassess with a bladder diary and identify a coexisting urgency (mixed) component that may require separate treatment","Referral to urogynaecology or urology and specialist continence physiotherapy, escalating when conservative measures fail"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"stress_urinary_incontinence":{"name":"Stress urinary incontinence","system":"Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SUI","Stress incontinence","Effort incontinence"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Stress urinary incontinence is the involuntary leakage of urine on effort, exertion, coughing or sneezing, reflecting failure of the sphincteric closure mechanism to withstand rises in abdominal pressure.","In women it is usually related to urethral hypermobility from pelvic floor and support weakness after childbirth and with ageing, or to intrinsic sphincter deficiency; the two frequently coexist.","In men it is most commonly iatrogenic, particularly following radical prostatectomy or other prostatic surgery that damages the sphincteric mechanism.","Mixed urinary incontinence, combining stress and urgency components, is common, and the more bothersome component should guide initial treatment.","A cough stress test demonstrating synchronous leakage, together with a bladder diary and pad testing, supports the diagnosis and quantifies severity.","Supervised pelvic floor muscle training is the first-line treatment and benefits both women and men, including after prostatectomy.","Surgical options in women include the midurethral sling and colposuspension, while intrinsic sphincter deficiency and post-prostatectomy incontinence may require slings or an artificial urinary sphincter.","Urodynamic assessment is reserved for complex, mixed, recurrent or pre-surgical cases and to confirm the diagnosis when it is unclear."],"pathophysiology":"Continence during effort depends on the urethra remaining a competent, well-supported closure mechanism so that intraurethral pressure exceeds intravesical pressure when abdominal pressure suddenly rises. In stress urinary incontinence this mechanism fails by two overlapping routes. Loss of anatomical support, from weakening of the pelvic floor musculature, endopelvic fascia and vaginal wall attachments, allows the bladder neck and proximal urethra to descend and rotate (urethral hypermobility), so abdominal pressure is no longer transmitted equally to the urethra. Alternatively, or additionally, intrinsic sphincter deficiency, from denervation, scarring, atrophy or surgical damage, leaves the urethra unable to generate adequate closure pressure regardless of support. Contributing factors include vaginal childbirth, ageing and oestrogen deficiency, obesity, chronically raised intra-abdominal pressure, pelvic surgery and, in men, prostatic surgery. The result is leakage synchronous with transient increases in abdominal pressure and no accompanying detrusor contraction.","additionalDifferentials":["Urgency urinary incontinence / overactive bladder","Mixed urinary incontinence","Overflow incontinence from chronic retention","Urethral diverticulum or fistula (vesicovaginal, urethrovaginal)","Ectopic ureter presenting with continuous leakage","Functional incontinence from mobility or cognitive impairment","Extraurethral leakage or vaginal discharge mistaken for urine"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cough stress test with a comfortably full bladder","Frequency-volume bladder diary","Pad test to quantify leakage severity","Post-void residual measurement","Urinalysis and culture to exclude infection","Multichannel urodynamics in complex, mixed or recurrent cases","Cystourethroscopy or imaging when fistula, diverticulum or prior mesh complication is suspected"],"redFlags":["Continuous leakage suggesting a fistula or ectopic ureter rather than true stress incontinence","Associated haematuria, pain or a pelvic mass","Significant post-void residual indicating retention with overflow","New neurological symptoms suggesting a neurogenic cause","Recurrent incontinence or pain after previous continence surgery or mesh insertion"],"prognosis":"Conservative pelvic floor muscle training improves or cures symptoms in a substantial proportion of patients, and appropriately selected surgery achieves high continence rates. Outcomes are less favourable with severe intrinsic sphincter deficiency, obesity, previous failed surgery and a large mixed urgency component.","pitfalls":["Missing a coexisting overactive bladder component and operating on a patient whose main problem is urgency","Failing to exclude a fistula or ectopic ureter when leakage is actually continuous rather than effort related","Offering surgery before an adequate trial of supervised pelvic floor training","Overlooking chronic retention with overflow, which mimics stress leakage but requires very different management","Neglecting reversible contributors such as obesity, constipation, chronic cough and atrophic changes"],"management":["Supervised pelvic floor muscle training as first-line treatment for both women and men, including after radical prostatectomy","Lifestyle measures: weight reduction, treatment of chronic cough and constipation, and caffeine moderation","Duloxetine as a pharmacological adjunct where pelvic floor training is insufficient and surgery is not chosen","Topical vaginal oestrogen for postmenopausal atrophy, with a continence pessary or device as a non-surgical option","Surgery in women: mid-urethral or autologous fascial sling, colposuspension, or urethral bulking agents for intrinsic sphincter deficiency","Male stress incontinence: male sling or artificial urinary sphincter for persistent post-prostatectomy leakage","In mixed incontinence, direct initial treatment at the more bothersome component and reassess with bladder diary and pad testing","Referral to urogynaecology or urology and continence physiotherapy, escalating when conservative therapy fails"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology, current edition","European Association of Urology Guidelines on urinary incontinence","International Continence Society standardisation of terminology"],"crossLinks":[]},"striate_palmoplantar_keratoderma":{"name":"Striate palmoplantar keratoderma","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPPK","Brünauer-Fuhs-Siemens keratoderma","DSG1","DSP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant focal palmoplantar keratoderma with linear (striate) hyperkeratosis running along the palms and fingers and focal thickening on the soles","Caused by heterozygous variants in desmosomal genes, most often DSG1 (desmoglein 1) or DSP (desmoplakin), and occasionally KRT1","The linear pattern follows lines of mechanical stress, so the fingers and weight-bearing soles are typically involved","Because desmoplakin also functions in cardiac desmosomes, some DSP variants are associated with cardiocutaneous syndromes, so a cardiac history should be considered","Nails and hair are usually normal, helping to distinguish it from syndromic keratodermas","Diagnosis is clinical with family history; biopsy shows hyperkeratosis with widened intercellular spaces and can be supported by molecular testing","Management is supportive with keratolytics, emollients, mechanical paring and protective measures, with oral retinoids reserved for severe disease"],"pathophysiology":"Striate palmoplantar keratoderma results from haploinsufficiency or dominant-negative effects of variants in desmosomal proteins, chiefly desmoglein 1 and desmoplakin, which anchor keratin filaments to cell-cell junctions in the epidermis. Weakened desmosomal adhesion is most consequential in acral skin subjected to shearing and pressure, where impaired keratinocyte cohesion triggers compensatory hyperkeratosis along lines of mechanical stress, producing the characteristic striate pattern. Certain desmoplakin variants additionally impair cardiac desmosomes, linking the skin phenotype to arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy in some families.","additionalDifferentials":["Diffuse palmoplantar keratoderma (Unna-Thost / Vörner)","Focal palmoplantar keratoderma of pachyonychia congenita","Punctate palmoplantar keratoderma","Naxos disease and Carvajal syndrome (cardiocutaneous keratodermas)","Acquired keratoderma from friction or eczema","Keratosis palmoplantaris of Wachters"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of DSG1, DSP and related desmosomal genes","Skin biopsy showing hyperkeratosis with acantholytic-like widening of intercellular spaces","Family history and pedigree analysis","Cardiac assessment (history, ECG and echocardiography) where a DSP variant or cardiocutaneous syndrome is suspected","Nail and hair examination to exclude syndromic keratoderma","Clinical photography to document the striate pattern"],"redFlags":["Personal or family history of arrhythmia, syncope or cardiomyopathy suggesting a cardiocutaneous desmoplakin syndrome","Woolly hair with keratoderma raising concern for Carvajal or Naxos syndrome","Painful fissuring causing functional impairment","Secondary infection of fissured keratoderma"],"prognosis":"The cutaneous prognosis is good, with lifelong but manageable focal keratoderma; overall prognosis depends on excluding the cardiac involvement associated with some desmoplakin variants.","pitfalls":["Failing to consider cardiac evaluation in desmoplakin-related disease","Confusing the linear pattern with a friction or occupational keratoderma","Overlooking woolly hair as a clue to a cardiocutaneous syndrome","Relying on systemic retinoids before optimising topical and mechanical care"],"management":["Topical keratolytics such as urea, salicylic acid and lactic-acid preparations with regular emollients to soften and reduce hyperkeratosis","Mechanical measures: regular paring or debridement of thickened skin, cushioned footwear and orthotics to offload lines of mechanical stress","Oral retinoids such as acitretin considered for severe, disabling keratoderma, weighed against their side effects","Treat painful fissuring and any superimposed fungal or bacterial infection","Cardiac assessment with ECG and echocardiography where a desmoplakin (DSP) variant raises the risk of arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy","Molecular genetic confirmation of DSG1, DSP and related genes with referral to clinical genetics","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal dominant inheritance, extending cardiac screening to relatives where a DSP variant is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"stroke_rehabilitation":{"name":"Stroke rehabilitation","system":"Rehabilitation / Pain","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["post-stroke rehabilitation","CVA rehabilitation","cerebrovascular accident rehabilitation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braddom's Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation / pain medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rehabilitation should begin as soon as the patient is medically stable, with early coordinated multidisciplinary care in a dedicated stroke unit improving survival and functional independence","Recovery is driven by neuroplasticity, and gains are greatest through task-specific, repetitive, high-intensity practice directed at meaningful functional goals","A structured team assessment should cover motor function, sensation, tone, balance, swallowing, communication, cognition, mood, vision, and continence","Dysphagia screening before oral intake is essential to reduce the risk of aspiration pneumonia, with formal swallowing assessment where concerns arise","Common complications requiring proactive management include spasticity, shoulder subluxation and pain, falls, pressure injury, venous thromboembolism, post-stroke depression, and central post-stroke pain","Standardised outcome measures such as the modified Rankin Scale, Barthel Index, and Functional Independence Measure help track progress and guide discharge planning","Constraint-induced movement therapy, mirror therapy, and increasingly technologies such as robotics and functional electrical stimulation can augment recovery of the affected limb","Secondary prevention, patient and carer education, and planning for community reintegration and vocational return are integral to the rehabilitation programme"],"pathophysiology":"Stroke causes focal brain injury through either ischaemia or haemorrhage, producing loss of function referable to the affected vascular territory. The initial deficit reflects both the infarct or haematoma core and the surrounding penumbra and oedema. Functional recovery over subsequent weeks and months depends on resolution of oedema and diaschisis together with neuroplastic reorganisation, including recruitment of perilesional and contralateral networks, synaptic remodelling, and cortical remapping. Rehabilitation harnesses these adaptive processes through repetitive, goal-directed activity, while maladaptive changes such as spasticity and learned non-use can impede recovery.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent or extending stroke","Post-stroke seizure with transient worsening","Metabolic or toxic encephalopathy mimicking decline","Systemic infection causing functional deterioration","Depression or delirium presenting as reduced engagement","Subdural haematoma after a fall"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bedside dysphagia screening followed by formal videofluoroscopy or endoscopic swallow evaluation when indicated","Standardised functional and impairment scales such as the modified Rankin Scale, Barthel Index, and FIM","Cognitive, mood, and communication assessments including screening for post-stroke depression and aphasia","Neuroimaging repeated if there is unexplained neurological deterioration","Investigations for secondary prevention including cardiac rhythm monitoring, carotid imaging, and vascular risk profiling"],"redFlags":["New or worsening neurological deficit suggesting recurrent or extending stroke or haemorrhagic transformation","Signs of aspiration or pneumonia in a patient with dysphagia","Symptoms of pulmonary embolism or deep vein thrombosis in an immobile patient","Sudden severe headache, reduced consciousness, or new seizures","Rapidly developing pressure injury or sepsis"],"prognosis":"Recovery is most rapid in the first weeks to months and can continue for a year or more, though the extent varies with stroke size, location, initial severity, age, and comorbidity. Early, intensive, multidisciplinary rehabilitation improves functional outcome, while persisting severe deficits, cognitive impairment, and depression predict greater long-term disability.","pitfalls":["Allowing oral intake before dysphagia screening and risking aspiration","Delaying rehabilitation until the patient is deemed fully stable, losing the early window for gains","Neglecting shoulder positioning and support, leading to painful subluxation","Overlooking post-stroke depression and cognitive impairment that limit engagement","Failing to address secondary prevention and carer support alongside physical recovery"],"management":["Early coordinated multidisciplinary rehabilitation in a dedicated stroke unit, begun as soon as the patient is medically stable","Task-specific, repetitive, high-intensity practice directed at meaningful functional goals, delivered through physiotherapy, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy","Dysphagia screening before oral intake, with modified diet or textures and enteral feeding where needed to prevent aspiration pneumonia","Manage spasticity with stretching, splinting, botulinum toxin and antispasticity agents, and treat shoulder subluxation and pain","Proactively prevent and treat complications: venous thromboembolism prophylaxis, pressure-injury care, falls prevention, and treatment of post-stroke depression and central post-stroke pain","Secondary prevention with antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy, blood-pressure and lipid control, and management of atrial fibrillation or carotid disease","Track progress with standardised measures such as the modified Rankin Scale and Barthel Index, planning discharge with community rehabilitation and carer support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braddom's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation"],"crossLinks":[]},"strongyloidiasis":{"name":"Strongyloidiasis","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Strongyloides stercoralis infection","threadworm infection","strongyloides hyperinfection syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A soil-transmitted intestinal nematode acquired when larvae in contaminated soil penetrate skin, endemic in warm, humid parts of India","Uniquely among common helminths it can complete its life cycle within the host (autoinfection), so infection may persist silently for decades","Chronic infection is often mild, with intermittent abdominal symptoms, urticaria, and a characteristic migratory serpiginous rash (larva currens); eosinophilia is a common clue","The feared complication is hyperinfection/disseminated disease, precipitated by immunosuppression, especially corticosteroids and HTLV-1 co-infection","Screening and treating latent infection before starting immunosuppression is a key preventive principle","Disseminated strongyloidiasis often carries Gram-negative bacteraemia or meningitis as larvae transport gut bacteria","A single stool examination is insensitive; serology and repeated or concentrated stool studies improve detection","Antiparasitic therapy with ivermectin is the treatment principle, with prolonged/repeated courses needed in disseminated disease"],"pathophysiology":"Filariform larvae penetrate the skin, migrate through the circulation to the lungs, ascend the airways and are swallowed, maturing into adult worms in the small intestine. Females produce eggs that hatch into rhabditiform larvae; some transform into infective filariform larvae within the gut or perianal skin and reinvade the host, sustaining autoinfection. When host cellular immunity is impaired, this autoinfective cycle accelerates uncontrollably, causing massive larval migration through multiple organs (hyperinfection and dissemination) and carrying enteric bacteria that seed the bloodstream, meninges and other sites.","additionalDifferentials":["Other soil-transmitted helminth infections (hookworm, ascariasis)","Peptic ulcer disease or functional dyspepsia","Inflammatory bowel disease","Chronic urticaria of other cause","Hypereosinophilic syndromes"],"infectionMimics":["Drug-induced eosinophilia","Allergic/atopic disease with urticaria","Other causes of Gram-negative sepsis in the immunosuppressed","Eosinophilic gastroenteritis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serology for Strongyloides antibodies, which is more sensitive than single stool testing","Repeated stool microscopy with concentration or agar-plate culture to detect larvae","Full blood count looking for eosinophilia (which may be absent in dissemination)","Examination of sputum, duodenal aspirate or other fluids for larvae in suspected hyperinfection","Screening for HTLV-1 and assessment of immune status in severe disease"],"redFlags":["Hyperinfection or dissemination in an immunosuppressed patient, a life-threatening emergency","Gram-negative bacteraemia or meningitis complicating heavy larval migration","Planned corticosteroid or other immunosuppression in someone from an endemic area without prior screening","Rapid respiratory or gastrointestinal deterioration with detectable larvae"],"prognosis":"Chronic uncomplicated infection responds well to antiparasitic therapy and has an excellent outlook, though eradication should be confirmed given the autoinfection risk. Hyperinfection and disseminated disease, by contrast, carry very high mortality despite treatment, particularly when diagnosis is delayed.","pitfalls":["Starting corticosteroids or immunosuppression without screening at-risk patients, precipitating fatal hyperinfection","Relying on a single negative stool sample to exclude infection","Assuming eosinophilia will always be present, as it is often absent in dissemination","Failing to give a sufficiently prolonged antiparasitic course in disseminated disease"],"management":["Definitive anthelmintic therapy with ivermectin as first line for chronic infection, with albendazole as an alternative or adjunct","Screen for and treat latent infection before starting immunosuppression, particularly corticosteroids, and in HTLV-1 co-infection","Confirm cure with follow-up serology and stool examination given the risk of persistent autoinfection, repeating or extending treatment as required","Hyperinfection or disseminated disease: prolonged ivermectin continued until larvae are cleared, reduction or withdrawal of immunosuppression, and parenteral or veterinary-formulation ivermectin where enteral absorption is impaired","Treat accompanying Gram-negative bacteraemia or meningitis with broad-spectrum antibiotics alongside organ support in intensive care","Preventive advice on footwear and sanitation in endemic areas and screening of at-risk migrants and transplant or immunosuppression candidates","Involve infectious diseases specialists for severe or disseminated disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"struma_ovarii":{"name":"Struma ovarii","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ovarian struma","thyroid-type ovarian teratoma","monodermal teratoma with thyroid tissue"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare ovarian teratoma composed predominantly of mature thyroid tissue, classified as a monodermal teratoma","Most present as an incidental adnexal or pelvic mass rather than with thyroid symptoms","A minority secrete enough thyroid hormone to cause hyperthyroidism arising from ectopic ovarian tissue","Ectopic thyroid hormone production suppresses the eutopic thyroid, so neck radioiodine uptake is low while pelvic uptake may be demonstrable","It should be considered in unexplained thyrotoxicosis when the neck gland appears normal or shows suppressed uptake","Rarely it is associated with ascites and a raised tumour marker, mimicking ovarian malignancy","Malignant transformation into thyroid-type carcinoma can occur but is uncommon","Treatment is surgical removal of the affected ovary, with thyroid-directed management for any associated malignant histology"],"pathophysiology":"Struma ovarii is a germ-cell tumour of the ovary in which mature thyroid tissue forms the dominant or exclusive component. The ectopic thyroid follicular cells can retain the capacity to synthesise thyroid hormone, and when active in sufficient quantity they cause thyrotoxicosis independent of pituitary control, thereby suppressing thyroid-stimulating hormone and the normal cervical thyroid. Most tumours are benign, but the same follicular epithelium can rarely undergo malignant change resembling papillary or follicular thyroid carcinoma, with potential for spread. The mass itself may produce local pelvic symptoms or, uncommonly, ascites and pleural effusion.","additionalDifferentials":["Graves disease or toxic nodular thyroid disease","Other functioning or non-functioning ovarian tumours","Ovarian epithelial malignancy","Mature cystic teratoma without significant thyroid tissue","Factitious or exogenous thyrotoxicosis","Subacute or silent thyroiditis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thyroid function tests demonstrating thyrotoxicosis when present","Radioiodine or radionuclide imaging showing suppressed neck uptake with pelvic uptake","Pelvic ultrasound and cross-sectional imaging of the adnexal mass","Tumour markers where ovarian malignancy is in the differential","Histological examination of the resected specimen","Thyroglobulin monitoring if malignant struma is identified"],"redFlags":["Severe thyrotoxicosis with cardiovascular compromise","Features suggesting malignant transformation or metastatic spread","Rapidly enlarging pelvic mass with ascites","Ovarian torsion of the tumour-bearing ovary","Unexplained thyrotoxicosis with a normal-appearing neck gland"],"prognosis":"The great majority are benign and cured by surgical removal, with resolution of any associated thyrotoxicosis; malignant struma ovarii is uncommon and generally indolent, though it warrants thyroid-cancer-style follow-up.","pitfalls":["Attributing thyrotoxicosis solely to the neck thyroid and overlooking an ovarian source","Misinterpreting suppressed cervical radioiodine uptake without seeking a pelvic source","Assuming an adnexal mass with ascites is primary ovarian cancer","Failing to examine resected tissue for malignant thyroid histology","Neglecting thyroid-directed surveillance after removal of malignant struma"],"management":["Definitive surgical resection of the ovarian tumour by cystectomy or oophorectomy, which is curative and resolves any associated thyrotoxicosis","Control thyrotoxic symptoms preoperatively with beta-blockade, adding antithyroid drugs where needed to render the patient euthyroid before surgery","Histological examination of the resected specimen to exclude malignant struma resembling papillary or follicular thyroid carcinoma","For malignant struma, consider completion total thyroidectomy to enable radioiodine ablation and thyroglobulin surveillance","Monitor thyroid function postoperatively to confirm resolution of the thyrotoxicosis","Long-term surveillance with thyroglobulin and imaging where malignant struma is identified","Multidisciplinary input from gynaecology and endocrinology, with endocrine oncology involvement if malignant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","DeGroot's Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"struvite_nephrolithiasis":{"name":"Struvite (infection) nephrolithiasis","system":"Renal / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["struvite stones","infection stones","triple phosphate stones","magnesium ammonium phosphate stones","urease stones"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) stones form only in urine infected by urease-producing organisms, classically Proteus mirabilis and also Klebsiella, Pseudomonas and Ureaplasma","The enzyme urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, alkalinising the urine and supplying the ammonium and carbonate needed for crystallisation","These are the archetypal stones that grow rapidly and fill the collecting system as staghorn calculi","They occur more often in women and in patients with recurrent urinary infection, neurogenic bladder, indwelling catheters or urinary diversion","A persistently alkaline urine with recurrent urea-splitting organisms is an important diagnostic clue","Coffin-lid shaped crystals may be seen on urine microscopy","Cure requires complete surgical clearance of every fragment, because residual material harbours bacteria and seeds regrowth","Culture-directed antibiotics against the urease organism, sometimes with a urease inhibitor and urinary acidification, support treatment but cannot substitute for complete stone clearance"],"pathophysiology":"Struvite stones are a direct consequence of chronic urinary infection with bacteria that produce urease. Urease hydrolyses urea to ammonia and carbon dioxide; the ammonia raises the urine pH and generates ammonium while carbon dioxide yields carbonate. In this persistently alkaline environment magnesium ammonium phosphate (struvite) and carbonate apatite become supersaturated and precipitate rapidly, frequently incorporating bacteria within the stone matrix. Because organisms are sequestered inside the stone, infection cannot be eradicated by antibiotics alone, and any retained fragment allows the process to recur and the stone to enlarge.","additionalDifferentials":["Calcium stones forming a staghorn configuration","Cystine staghorn calculus","Uric acid stone with secondary infection","Renal tuberculosis with calcification","Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis","Medullary sponge kidney with stones"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Non-contrast CT to define the stone burden and staghorn configuration","Urine culture to identify the urease-producing organism and guide antibiotics","Urine pH, which is characteristically alkaline, and microscopy for crystals","Stone analysis confirming struvite and carbonate apatite composition","Serum renal function and, in bilateral disease, assessment of split renal function","Metabolic evaluation to exclude a coexisting metabolic stone nidus"],"redFlags":["Sepsis arising from an infected obstructed system","Rapid staghorn growth threatening renal function","Progressive loss of function in the affected kidney","Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis that may require nephrectomy"],"prognosis":"If every fragment is cleared and infection eradicated, cure is achievable; incomplete clearance leads to a high recurrence rate, and long-standing staghorn calculi can destroy renal function and, rarely, are associated with squamous cell carcinoma of the collecting system.","pitfalls":["Treating with antibiotics alone and expecting cure without complete stone removal","Leaving residual fragments that reseed both infection and stone","Missing a coexisting metabolic abnormality that acted as the initial nidus","Underestimating the sepsis risk when instrumenting an infected stone"],"management":["Definitive complete surgical stone clearance, principally percutaneous nephrolithotomy for staghorn calculi, aiming for a wholly stone-free state","Culture-directed antibiotics to treat the associated urinary infection perioperatively, recognising that antibiotics alone cannot eradicate organisms sequestered within the stone","Consider urease inhibition with acetohydroxamic acid in selected patients unfit for surgery or with unavoidable residual stone","Address predisposing factors: manage neurogenic bladder, change or remove indwelling catheters, and relieve urinary stasis and obstruction","Surveillance imaging and urine culture for residual fragments and recurrence given rapid regrowth from any retained nidus","Metabolic evaluation to identify and treat a coexisting metabolic stone-forming tendency","Nephrectomy considered for a non-functioning, chronically infected kidney, with urology referral for operative planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (Feehally et al.)","Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"strychnine_poisoning":{"name":"Strychnine poisoning","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["strychnine toxicity","strychnine intoxication","nux vomica poisoning","rodenticide strychnine poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Strychnine causes generalised muscle rigidity and violent, exaggerated startle-provoked spasms with a fully preserved level of consciousness between episodes","Classic signs include opisthotonos, risus sardonicus and trismus, closely mimicking tetanus but with much more rapid onset after ingestion","Spasms are triggered by minimal sensory stimulation, so a dark, quiet environment with minimal handling is an important supportive measure","Unlike a true seizure there is no post-ictal confusion, because the mechanism is spinal disinhibition rather than cortical discharge","Severe sustained contractions can cause hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis, lactic acidosis and myoglobinuric renal injury","Death results from respiratory failure due to spasm of the muscles of respiration and diaphragm rather than direct central depression","Management is supportive: aggressive control of spasms with sedation and neuromuscular blockade with ventilation when needed, plus treatment of hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis","Sources include some rodenticides and adulterated illicit drugs, and diagnosis is largely clinical with confirmatory analytical testing"],"pathophysiology":"Strychnine competitively antagonises glycine, the principal inhibitory neurotransmitter at postsynaptic receptors of spinal cord and brainstem motor neurons. Loss of this inhibitory tone removes the normal reciprocal control of motor neurons, so both agonist and antagonist muscle groups contract simultaneously in response to any stimulus. The result is generalised rigidity with intermittent violent extensor spasms while consciousness is spared, because the toxin acts on the spinal cord rather than the cortex. Sustained muscular hyperactivity generates heat, acid and muscle breakdown, and respiratory muscle involvement causes fatal hypoventilation.","additionalDifferentials":["Tetanus","Generalised seizure or status epilepticus","Dystonic reaction to antidopaminergic drugs","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome or serotonin syndrome","Stiff-person syndrome","Hypocalcaemic tetany"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Creatine kinase and myoglobin to assess rhabdomyolysis","Arterial blood gas and lactate for acidosis and ventilation","Renal function and electrolytes including potassium and calcium","Core temperature monitoring for hyperthermia","Analytical confirmation of strychnine in blood, urine or gastric contents","Electrocardiogram and continuous cardiac monitoring"],"redFlags":["Frequent or prolonged spasms involving respiratory muscles","Hyperthermia with rigidity","Marked rhabdomyolysis or myoglobinuric renal injury","Metabolic acidosis or haemodynamic instability","Inability to maintain the airway or adequate ventilation"],"prognosis":"If ventilation and spasms are controlled early and complications such as hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis are managed, recovery within a few days is usual as the toxin is metabolised; untreated severe poisoning is rapidly fatal from respiratory failure.","pitfalls":["Mistaking spasms for epileptic seizures and overlooking preserved consciousness","Excessive stimulation, examination or lighting that provokes further spasms","Failing to secure the airway and provide ventilation once neuromuscular blockade is used","Underestimating hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis","Delaying sedation while awaiting analytical confirmation"],"management":["Minimise sensory stimulation by nursing in a dark, quiet environment with minimal handling to reduce spasm-provoking triggers","Control muscle spasms with benzodiazepines as first line, escalating to neuromuscular blockade with mechanical ventilation for refractory spasms","Secure the airway early and provide controlled ventilation to prevent fatal respiratory-muscle spasm and hypoventilation","Aggressively manage hyperthermia with active cooling and treat rhabdomyolysis with intravenous fluids to protect renal function","Correct lactic acidosis and electrolyte disturbance and maintain continuous cardiac monitoring","Gastrointestinal decontamination with activated charcoal where the airway is protected and presentation is early, as no specific antidote exists","Manage in intensive care with continuous monitoring and involve the clinical toxicology or poisons service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","UpToDate: Strychnine poisoning"],"crossLinks":[]},"sturge_weber_syndrome":{"name":"Sturge-Weber syndrome","system":"Genetic / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SWS","encephalotrigeminal angiomatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard medical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Neurocutaneous disorder characterised by a facial capillary malformation (port-wine birthmark), leptomeningeal angiomatosis, and eye involvement","The facial birthmark typically follows the distribution of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve, and upper facial involvement raises the risk of brain and eye disease","Leptomeningeal angiomatosis predisposes to seizures, which often begin in infancy and can be difficult to control","Stroke-like episodes, hemiparesis, and progressive cognitive impairment may occur from impaired cortical perfusion","Glaucoma is a common ocular complication and requires ongoing monitoring of intraocular pressure","Caused by a somatic mosaic activating mutation in the GNAQ gene arising during early development, so it is not typically inherited","Characteristic tram-track cortical calcification may be seen on imaging in affected brain regions","A port-wine birthmark alone, without brain or eye involvement, does not constitute the full syndrome"],"pathophysiology":"Sturge-Weber syndrome is caused by a somatic activating mutation in the GNAQ gene occurring in a subset of cells during early embryogenesis, producing a mosaic distribution that explains the segmental capillary malformations. The mutation dysregulates signalling in vascular and other tissues, leading to abnormal capillary-venous malformations in the skin, leptomeninges, and eye. The leptomeningeal vascular malformation impairs normal venous drainage and cortical perfusion, causing chronic ischaemia, cortical atrophy, calcification, and a lowered seizure threshold. Because the mutation is somatic and mosaic rather than germline, the condition arises sporadically and is not inherited.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated port-wine birthmark without systemic involvement","Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome","PHACE syndrome","Other causes of infantile seizures with focal deficits","Megalencephaly-capillary malformation syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain magnetic resonance imaging with contrast to demonstrate leptomeningeal angiomatosis","Electroencephalography for seizure assessment","Regular ophthalmological review including intraocular pressure measurement for glaucoma","Neurodevelopmental assessment and monitoring","Imaging for cortical calcification when clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Poorly controlled or prolonged seizures requiring urgent treatment to limit neurological injury","Stroke-like episodes with acute focal deficits","Rising intraocular pressure or visual deterioration from glaucoma","Progressive developmental regression signalling worsening cortical involvement"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies with the extent of brain and eye involvement; some individuals have well-controlled seizures and near-normal development, while extensive leptomeningeal disease can lead to refractory epilepsy, hemiparesis, and cognitive impairment.","pitfalls":["Assuming any facial port-wine birthmark implies the full syndrome without assessing brain and eye involvement","Failing to monitor for glaucoma, which can present at any age","Undertreating early seizures with consequences for development","Not recognising the mosaic somatic cause and incorrectly counselling about heritability"],"management":["Antiseizure medication to control the often difficult-to-treat epilepsy, with epilepsy surgery including resection or hemispherectomy for refractory focal seizures","Consider low-dose aspirin to reduce recurrent stroke-like episodes arising from impaired cortical perfusion","Regular ophthalmological review with intraocular pressure monitoring and medical or surgical treatment of glaucoma","Pulsed-dye laser therapy for the facial capillary malformation (port-wine birthmark)","Neurodevelopmental assessment with early intervention, physiotherapy and educational support for cognitive and motor impairment","Prompt treatment of acute prolonged seizures or stroke-like episodes with seizure control and adequate hydration","Multidisciplinary care across neurology, ophthalmology, dermatology and genetics, counselling that the somatic mosaic GNAQ mutation is sporadic with a low recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"stxbp1_encephalopathy":{"name":"STXBP1 developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STXBP1","Munc18-1 encephalopathy","early infantile epileptic encephalopathy 4 (EIEE4)","STXBP1-related disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common cause of early-infantile developmental and epileptic encephalopathy, with seizures often beginning in the neonatal period or early infancy","Seizure types are varied and may include epileptic spasms with a burst-suppression or hypsarrhythmia EEG (Ohtahara or West syndrome patterns)","Some individuals have significant developmental impairment with little or no epilepsy, so the disorder spans an encephalopathy-with-or-without-seizures spectrum","Prominent features include hypotonia, movement disorders such as tremor, dystonia and ataxia, and later autistic behaviours","Caused by heterozygous loss-of-function or dominant-negative variants in STXBP1 (Munc18-1), usually de novo and autosomal dominant","Seizures may respond well to certain antiseizure medications even when development remains severely affected","Brain MRI is frequently normal or shows only non-specific findings"],"pathophysiology":"STXBP1 encodes Munc18-1, a syntaxin-binding protein essential for assembly of the SNARE complex that mediates synaptic vesicle docking and calcium-triggered neurotransmitter release. Heterozygous loss-of-function or dominant-negative variants reduce functional protein through haploinsufficiency and, in some cases, aggregation of misfolded protein, impairing synaptic transmission across excitatory and inhibitory networks. The resulting disturbance of neurotransmission produces cortical hyperexcitability and abnormal circuit function, manifesting as early epileptic encephalopathy, movement disorder and intellectual disability. The developmental impairment can be substantial even when seizures are controlled, reflecting the protein's ongoing role in synaptic function.","additionalDifferentials":["KCNQ2 or SCN2A neonatal epileptic encephalopathy","CDKL5 deficiency disorder","Ohtahara and West syndromes of other causes","Pyridoxine-dependent and other treatable metabolic epilepsies","Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Other developmental and epileptic encephalopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["EEG to identify burst-suppression, hypsarrhythmia or other epileptiform patterns","Brain MRI to exclude structural or acquired causes","Metabolic screen including a trial addressing treatable causes such as pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy","Chromosomal microarray and STXBP1-inclusive gene panel or exome sequencing","Developmental and movement-disorder assessment"],"redFlags":["Neonatal-onset seizures or epileptic spasms","Burst-suppression or hypsarrhythmia on EEG","Status epilepticus or clustering seizures","Feeding difficulty and failure to thrive"],"prognosis":"Most individuals have moderate to severe intellectual disability regardless of seizure control, though epilepsy can improve or remit over time in some. Outcome is variable across the spectrum, and management is supportive with attention to seizures, movement disorder and development.","pitfalls":["Assuming controlling the seizures will normalise development","Not considering STXBP1 in developmental impairment without epilepsy","Omitting a trial for treatable metabolic epilepsies before concluding the diagnosis","Interpreting a normal MRI as excluding a genetic encephalopathy"],"management":["Antiseizure medication tailored to seizure type and EEG pattern, with ACTH or vigabatrin considered for epileptic spasms and a West syndrome pattern","Trial treatable-cause therapy such as pyridoxine and complete a metabolic screen before attributing the picture solely to STXBP1","Manage the movement disorder (tremor, dystonia, ataxia) and hypotonia with physiotherapy and targeted agents","Developmental support with early intervention, occupational and speech therapy, and communication aids for intellectual disability and autistic features","Confirm the diagnosis with an STXBP1-inclusive gene panel or exome sequencing and refer to clinical genetics","Genetic counselling noting variants are usually de novo with a generally low recurrence risk, while discussing the possibility of parental germline mosaicism","Multidisciplinary neurodevelopmental care, escalating for status epilepticus or developmental regression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: STXBP1 Developmental and Epileptic Encephalopathy","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"subacromial_bursitis":{"name":"Subacromial bursitis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subdeltoid bursitis","subacromial-subdeltoid bursitis","SASD bursitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Inflammation or thickening of the subacromial-subdeltoid bursa, usually secondary to rotator cuff pathology rather than an isolated process","Presents with lateral shoulder pain on overhead activity, often with a painful arc and positive impingement signs","Frequently coexists with rotator cuff tendinopathy and shares its clinical picture, so the two are often inseparable","Ultrasound demonstrates bursal thickening or fluid and can show dynamic impingement","A diagnostic and therapeutic subacromial injection can confirm the pain source and relieve symptoms, but repeated corticosteroid use should be limited","Management targets the underlying cuff disorder with relative rest, load management and physiotherapy","Isolated septic subacromial bursitis is uncommon but must be considered when systemic features are present"],"pathophysiology":"The subacromial-subdeltoid bursa reduces friction between the rotator cuff and the overlying acromion and deltoid. With repetitive overhead loading, adjacent cuff tendinopathy, or mechanical irritation in a narrowed subacromial space, the bursa becomes thickened and inflamed. It is usually a secondary phenomenon accompanying rotator cuff related shoulder pain rather than a standalone diagnosis, and clinically the two are often indistinguishable. Rarely the bursa is involved in an inflammatory arthropathy or becomes infected, typically after a breach of overlying tissues.","additionalDifferentials":["Rotator cuff tendinopathy or tear","Calcific tendinitis","Adhesive capsulitis","Glenohumeral or acromioclavicular arthritis","Septic bursitis or septic arthritis","Cervical radiculopathy referring to the shoulder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound to assess bursal fluid and thickening and cuff integrity","Diagnostic subacromial local anaesthetic injection","Plain radiographs for acromial shape, arthritis and calcification","MRI where a cuff tear or other pathology is suspected","Inflammatory markers and bursal aspiration if infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Fever, erythema and marked tenderness suggesting septic bursitis","Acute loss of active movement after trauma suggesting a cuff tear","Systemic upset or immunosuppression raising infection risk","Progressive night pain unresponsive to treatment"],"prognosis":"Usually settles with treatment of the underlying cuff disorder and activity modification; recurrence relates to ongoing overload or unaddressed cuff pathology.","pitfalls":["Treating bursitis in isolation while ignoring the underlying cuff tendinopathy","Repeated corticosteroid injections without addressing load","Missing septic bursitis in an atypically hot, swollen presentation","Over-reliance on imaging findings that may be incidental"],"management":["Treat the underlying rotator cuff disorder with relative rest, activity and load modification, and structured physiotherapy for cuff and scapular strengthening","Simple analgesia and NSAIDs for pain and inflammation","Subacromial corticosteroid injection for diagnostic confirmation and symptom relief, limiting repeated corticosteroid use","Ultrasound assessment of bursal thickening or fluid and cuff integrity to guide treatment","Aspiration and culture-directed antibiotics if septic bursitis is suspected","Referral for a surgical opinion regarding subacromial decompression where symptoms persist despite conservative treatment or a significant cuff tear is present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","British Journal of Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"subacromial_impingement_syndrome":{"name":"subacromial impingement syndrome","system":"musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subacromial pain syndrome","rotator cuff impingement"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Trauma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a painful arc during mid-range abduction, typically with anterolateral shoulder pain that is worse on overhead reach and at night when lying on the affected side.","The term subacromial pain syndrome is increasingly preferred, reflecting a shift away from a purely mechanical model towards a spectrum including bursitis, tendinopathy and partial cuff pathology.","Neer and Hawkins-Kennedy provocation tests are sensitive but not specific; a positive subacromial injection test supports the diagnosis by transiently abolishing pain and improving cuff strength.","Acromial morphology matters: a hooked (Bigliami type III) acromion and a laterally downsloping acromion are associated with increased mechanical narrowing of the subacromial space.","Distinguish primary (extrinsic mechanical) impingement from secondary impingement arising from glenohumeral instability or scapular dyskinesis, as the latter requires rehabilitation rather than decompression.","Structured physiotherapy targeting rotator cuff and scapular stabiliser strengthening is first-line and resolves symptoms in the majority without surgery.","Consider an alternative or coexisting diagnosis when weakness is profound or there is loss of active elevation, suggesting a full-thickness cuff tear rather than isolated impingement.","Subacromial corticosteroid injection can provide short-term relief and aid engagement with rehabilitation, but repeated injections risk tendon deterioration."],"pathophysiology":"Impingement describes mechanical compression and irritation of the rotator cuff tendons (chiefly supraspinatus) and the subacromial bursa within the coracoacromial arch during elevation of the arm. Extrinsic factors include acromial shape, subacromial spurs and coracoacromial ligament thickening, which narrow the outlet, while intrinsic tendon degeneration, hypovascularity of the critical zone and age-related collagen changes weaken the tendon. Dynamic contributors such as scapular dyskinesis, glenohumeral instability and rotator cuff or periscapular muscle imbalance reduce effective humeral head depression, allowing superior migration and repetitive abrasion. The resulting inflammatory and degenerative cascade produces bursitis, tendinopathy and progressive partial tearing, forming part of a continuum that may culminate in cuff failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Rotator cuff tear (partial or full thickness)","Calcific tendinitis of the shoulder","Adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder)","Acromioclavicular joint osteoarthritis","Glenohumeral instability with secondary impingement","Cervical radiculopathy referring to the shoulder","Glenohumeral osteoarthritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs including an outlet (scapular Y) view to assess acromial morphology and spurs","Ultrasound of the rotator cuff and subacromial bursa, including dynamic assessment during elevation","MRI to characterise cuff tendinopathy, partial or full-thickness tears and bursal changes","Diagnostic subacromial local anaesthetic injection test","Assessment of scapular kinematics and posture","Cervical spine imaging if radicular features are present"],"redFlags":["Profound weakness or inability to actively elevate the arm suggesting a large or massive cuff tear","Rest and night pain unresponsive to conservative measures raising concern for tumour or infection","History of significant trauma with acute loss of function suggesting acute cuff rupture or fracture","Constitutional symptoms such as fever, weight loss or a palpable mass"],"prognosis":"Most patients improve substantially with a sustained programme of physiotherapy and activity modification, and outcomes of subacromial decompression are comparable to structured exercise in appropriately selected cases. Prognosis is less favourable when impingement is secondary to unaddressed instability, dyskinesis or an established cuff tear.","pitfalls":["Attributing all anterolateral shoulder pain to impingement while missing an underlying full-thickness cuff tear or glenohumeral pathology.","Proceeding to acromioplasty without first correcting scapular dyskinesis or instability, leading to persistent secondary impingement.","Over-reliance on radiographic acromial shape, which correlates poorly with symptoms in individual patients.","Repeated corticosteroid injections that mask progression and may compromise tendon integrity."],"management":["First-line structured physiotherapy targeting rotator cuff and scapular stabiliser strengthening, combined with activity modification","Analgesia and NSAIDs for symptom control","Subacromial corticosteroid injection for refractory pain, used judiciously","Manage secondary impingement from scapular dyskinesis or glenohumeral instability with rehabilitation rather than decompression","Correct posture and scapular kinematics with a graded return to overhead activity","Surgical subacromial decompression, with treatment of any cuff pathology, reserved for those failing sustained conservative therapy","Referral to orthopaedics or physiotherapy where symptoms persist or a rotator cuff tear is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Trauma","Miller's Review of Orthopaedics","British Elbow and Shoulder Society guidance on subacromial pain"],"crossLinks":[]},"subacute_combined_degeneration":{"name":"Subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCD","SACD","subacute combined degeneration","B12 myelopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by vitamin B12 (cobalamin) deficiency, which preferentially damages the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts of the spinal cord","Typically presents with symmetrical distal paraesthesiae, loss of vibration and joint-position sense, and a progressive spastic-ataxic gait","Combines upper motor neuron signs (spasticity, extensor plantars) with impaired proprioception and sometimes absent ankle reflexes, giving a mixed picture","Neuropsychiatric features such as cognitive slowing, irritability, or depression may accompany or precede the myelopathy","Serum B12 can be borderline or normal, so raised methylmalonic acid and homocysteine are more sensitive markers of tissue deficiency","Common causes include pernicious anaemia, malabsorption, strict vegan diet, gastric surgery, and prolonged nitrous oxide exposure","Early replacement of B12 can halt and partially reverse deficits; delayed treatment risks permanent disability","Macrocytic anaemia may be absent, so neurological disease can occur without haematological changes"],"pathophysiology":"Vitamin B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. Deficiency impairs myelin synthesis and maintenance, leading to vacuolar demyelination and axonal degeneration that begins in the dorsal columns and later involves the lateral (corticospinal) columns, hence the term 'combined'. Nitrous oxide irreversibly oxidises the cobalt core of cobalamin and can precipitate the disorder in those with marginal stores. The pathology accounts for the combination of sensory ataxia from posterior column loss and spasticity from corticospinal tract involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Copper deficiency myelopathy (clinically near-identical picture)","Multiple sclerosis and other demyelinating myelopathy","Compressive cervical myelopathy or spondylotic myelopathy","Tabes dorsalis and other causes of sensory ataxia","Hereditary spastic paraplegia","HTLV-1 associated myelopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum vitamin B12 with methylmalonic acid and homocysteine when B12 is borderline","Full blood count and blood film for macrocytosis and hypersegmented neutrophils","Intrinsic factor and parietal cell antibodies to identify pernicious anaemia","MRI of the spine showing high T2 signal in the dorsal columns (inverted V or dot sign)","Serum copper and caeruloplasmin to exclude copper deficiency mimicking the syndrome","Nerve conduction studies if a coexisting peripheral neuropathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive myelopathy causing gait failure or sphincter disturbance","Recent or repeated nitrous oxide use with acute neurological decline","Severe anaemia or pancytopenia requiring urgent evaluation","Coexisting cognitive decline suggesting advanced or long-standing deficiency"],"prognosis":"Recovery depends on how early replacement begins; symptoms present for less than a few months often improve substantially, whereas long-standing deficits may only stabilise, leaving residual sensory ataxia or spasticity.","pitfalls":["Relying on a normal serum B12 and missing functional deficiency demonstrable by methylmalonic acid","Assuming anaemia must be present, thereby overlooking purely neurological presentations","Failing to ask about recreational or repeated medical nitrous oxide exposure","Overlooking copper deficiency, which produces an indistinguishable myelopathy","Attributing spastic-ataxic gait solely to spondylosis and delaying B12 assessment"],"management":["Urgent vitamin B12 (hydroxocobalamin) replacement, initially by the parenteral route, started promptly to halt progression","Long-term maintenance cobalamin therapy, lifelong where the cause is pernicious anaemia or irreversible malabsorption","Identify and treat the underlying cause: pernicious anaemia, malabsorption, gastric surgery or dietary deficiency, with dietary advice or supplementation","Stop ongoing nitrous oxide exposure, which precipitates and worsens the disorder","Correct coexisting folate and iron deficiency, but replace B12 first to avoid precipitating neurological deterioration","Rehabilitation with physiotherapy and occupational therapy for residual sensory ataxia and spasticity, with falls prevention","Monitor the haematological and neurological response, escalating promptly since earlier treatment predicts better recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"subacute_cutaneous_lupus":{"name":"Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCLE","Subacute cutaneous LE"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A photosensitive, non-scarring cutaneous lupus subtype presenting in two morphologies: annular polycyclic lesions and papulosquamous (psoriasiform) plaques, favouring the upper back, shoulders, extensor arms and V of the neck while sparing the mid-face and knuckles.","Strongly associated with anti-Ro/SSA (and often anti-La/SSB) antibodies; most patients are anti-Ro positive.","Lesions characteristically heal without scarring but often leave persistent dyspigmentation and telangiectasia; central clearing gives annular lesions a distinctive rim.","A large proportion of cases are drug-induced, classically by thiazides, terbinafine, calcium-channel blockers, proton-pump inhibitors, antifungals, TNF inhibitors and chemotherapy agents.","Only a minority meet full criteria for systemic lupus erythematosus, and systemic involvement, when present, tends to be mild; nonetheless screening for systemic disease is essential.","Anti-Ro positivity carries a risk of neonatal lupus and congenital heart block in offspring, so pregnancy planning and fetal monitoring are important.","Photoprotection and photosensitivity counselling are cornerstones, since ultraviolet light reliably provokes and worsens lesions.","Antimalarials such as hydroxychloroquine are first-line systemic therapy, alongside topical corticosteroids and calcineurin inhibitors; smoking reduces antimalarial efficacy."],"pathophysiology":"Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus is an autoimmune interface dermatitis in which ultraviolet radiation, particularly UVB, drives keratinocyte apoptosis and translocation of intracellular Ro/SSA antigen to the cell surface. Anti-Ro/SSA autoantibodies then bind these exposed antigens, promoting antibody-dependent and cytotoxic T-cell-mediated destruction of basal keratinocytes at the dermo-epidermal junction. A type I interferon-rich milieu amplifies the response, and genetic predisposition (including HLA associations and complement deficiencies) increases susceptibility. Photosensitivity is therefore intrinsic to the disease mechanism. Numerous systemic drugs can trigger or unmask SCLE, presumably by enhancing photosensitivity or altering keratinocyte antigen expression, producing lesions clinically and serologically indistinguishable from idiopathic disease that typically remit after the culprit drug is withdrawn. Because the inflammatory attack is centred on the basal layer rather than the deeper appendages, lesions resolve without the dermal scarring and follicular destruction that characterise discoid lupus, though pigmentary change frequently persists.","additionalDifferentials":["Discoid lupus erythematosus and other chronic cutaneous lupus variants","Dermatomyositis (photodistributed poikiloderma and Gottron changes)","Psoriasis (papulosquamous variant overlap)","Tinea corporis and other annular dermatoses (granuloma annulare, erythema annulare centrifugum)","Polymorphic light eruption and other photodermatoses","Nummular eczema and pityriasis rosea","Secondary syphilis with annular plaques"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB antibodies with a full antinuclear antibody panel","Skin biopsy with histology showing interface dermatitis and direct immunofluorescence","Complement levels, full blood count, renal function and urinalysis to screen for systemic lupus","Detailed drug history and medication review for photosensitising culprit agents","Antiphospholipid antibody testing where thrombotic or obstetric risk is a concern","Assessment for neonatal lupus risk and fetal cardiac monitoring in anti-Ro-positive pregnancy"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting systemic lupus such as nephritis, cytopenias, serositis or neuropsychiatric symptoms","New or worsening eruption temporally linked to a recently started photosensitising drug","Anti-Ro positivity in pregnancy given the risk of congenital heart block","Extensive or rapidly progressive disease unresponsive to first-line therapy"],"prognosis":"The cutaneous prognosis is generally favourable, with non-scarring lesions that respond to photoprotection and antimalarials and, in drug-induced cases, remit after withdrawal of the culprit. A minority progress to or coexist with systemic lupus, so ongoing surveillance is warranted.","pitfalls":["Overlooking a drug trigger and continuing the offending photosensitising medication","Assuming all cutaneous lupus is systemic, or conversely failing to screen for systemic involvement","Missing anti-Ro-related neonatal lupus and heart-block risk in women of childbearing age","Confusing papulosquamous SCLE with psoriasis and treating with agents that may aggravate lupus","Neglecting rigorous photoprotection and smoking cessation, which undermines antimalarial response"],"management":["Strict photoprotection with broad-spectrum sunscreen, sun avoidance and protective clothing, since ultraviolet exposure is intrinsic to the disease","Identify and withdraw culprit photosensitising drugs such as thiazides, terbinafine, calcium-channel blockers, proton-pump inhibitors and TNF inhibitors in drug-induced cases","Antimalarials such as hydroxychloroquine as the mainstay systemic therapy, with topical corticosteroids and calcineurin inhibitors for individual lesions","Escalate refractory disease to systemic immunosuppression with agents such as methotrexate or mycophenolate mofetil","Screen for and monitor systemic lupus involvement with renal function, blood counts, complement and urinalysis","In anti-Ro-positive women, undertake pregnancy planning and foetal cardiac monitoring for neonatal lupus and congenital heart block","Dermatology and rheumatology referral for ongoing management and surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard dermatology textbooks","Cutaneous lupus erythematosus clinical guidelines","Peer-reviewed rheumatology and dermatology literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"subacute_sclerosing_panencephalitis":{"name":"Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (measles)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SSPE","Dawson encephalitis","measles inclusion encephalitis (chronic)","subacute sclerosing leukoencephalitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, progressive, and fatal encephalitis caused by persistence of a defective measles virus in the brain, typically developing years after the initial measles infection","The risk is greatest when natural measles is acquired in infancy, and the latent interval before SSPE is often 6 to 10 years or more","It evolves through recognisable stages, beginning with subtle cognitive and behavioural change and declining school performance","The hallmark second stage is periodic myoclonic jerks, followed by progressive dementia, spasticity, rigidity, and eventually a vegetative state","The electroencephalogram characteristically shows periodic complexes that coincide with the myoclonic jerks","Cerebrospinal fluid reveals markedly raised measles-specific antibody with oligoclonal bands and evidence of intrathecal antibody synthesis, with normal or mildly raised protein","There is no curative therapy; agents such as interferon and isoprinosine have been tried with limited benefit, and prevention through measles immunisation is paramount"],"pathophysiology":"Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis results from persistence of the measles virus in neurones and glia after apparently complete recovery from acute measles. Over the ensuing years the virus accumulates mutations, particularly affecting the matrix and other envelope genes, so that it can no longer produce complete infectious particles but continues to spread cell to cell within the brain. This defective, persistent infection provokes a chronic inflammatory and immune response, with intense intrathecal production of anti-measles antibody, alongside slowly progressive destruction of both grey and white matter, hence the term panencephalitis. Neurones and oligodendrocytes contain viral inclusions, and there is perivascular inflammation, gliosis, and demyelination. The relentless accumulation of neuronal loss underlies the stepwise clinical deterioration from cognitive decline through myoclonus to a vegetative state and death.","additionalDifferentials":["Progressive rubella panencephalitis","Other viral or autoimmune encephalitides","Neurodegenerative disorders and leukodystrophies of childhood and adolescence","Inborn errors of metabolism presenting with regression","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease and other prion disorders","Epileptic encephalopathies with myoclonus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electroencephalography demonstrating periodic complexes synchronous with myoclonus","Cerebrospinal fluid measles antibody titre with paired serum for antibody index and oligoclonal bands","MRI of the brain showing white-matter change and later cerebral atrophy","Detailed history of prior measles infection, especially in infancy, and immunisation status","Molecular detection of measles virus RNA in brain tissue in atypical cases","Exclusion of metabolic, prion, and autoimmune causes of progressive encephalopathy"],"redFlags":["Progressive cognitive decline with new periodic myoclonic jerks in a child or adolescent","Rapid neurological deterioration into rigidity or a vegetative state","Loss of safe swallowing and airway protection","Intractable seizures or autonomic instability in advanced disease"],"prognosis":"SSPE is usually relentlessly progressive and fatal, most patients dying within a few years of onset; occasional cases follow a more prolonged or fluctuating course, but spontaneous long-term remission is rare and no treatment reliably arrests it.","pitfalls":["Dismissing early behavioural and academic decline as a psychological or educational problem","Not recognising the diagnostic value of periodic EEG complexes coinciding with myoclonus","Relying on serum measles antibody alone rather than demonstrating intrathecal antibody synthesis","Underestimating the importance of measles vaccination in prevention, especially where infants are infected"],"management":["Recognise that no treatment reliably arrests the disease, so management is largely supportive and palliative","Disease-slowing regimens attempted in some cases, combining intrathecal or oral interferon-alpha with oral inosine pranobex (isoprinosine), which may slow progression in a minority","Symptomatic control of myoclonus and seizures with antiseizure medications such as clonazepam and valproate","Manage spasticity with antispasticity agents and physiotherapy, with careful positioning and contracture prevention","Nutritional support and aspiration prevention, progressing to enteral feeding as swallowing fails","Comprehensive palliative and end-of-life care with psychological support for the family given the progressive fatal course","Emphasise prevention through measles immunisation, as the disease follows measles infection acquired in infancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","UpToDate: Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis"],"crossLinks":[]},"subacute_thyroiditis":{"name":"Subacute thyroiditis","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["de Quervain thyroiditis","subacute granulomatous thyroiditis","giant cell thyroiditis","painful subacute thyroiditis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically follows a viral upper respiratory illness by a few weeks and presents with a painful, tender thyroid and referred pain to the jaw or ears","Classically evolves through phases: an early thyrotoxic phase from follicular disruption, followed by transient hypothyroidism, and usually a return to euthyroidism","In the thyrotoxic phase radioiodine uptake is characteristically low, which distinguishes it from Graves' disease where uptake is high","The erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are markedly elevated, reflecting the inflammatory process","Thyrotoxic symptoms arise from release of preformed hormone rather than increased synthesis, so antithyroid drugs are ineffective","Management is largely symptomatic, with anti-inflammatory agents for milder disease and corticosteroids reserved for severe pain, alongside beta-blockade for adrenergic symptoms","A minority of patients are left with permanent hypothyroidism, so thyroid function should be monitored during recovery"],"pathophysiology":"Subacute thyroiditis is thought to result from a viral infection or post-viral inflammatory response affecting the thyroid, producing granulomatous inflammation with giant cells and destruction of thyroid follicles. Disruption of follicles releases stored thyroid hormone into the circulation, causing an initial destructive thyrotoxicosis with suppressed TSH and low radioiodine uptake. As follicular stores are depleted and the gland heals, patients pass through a hypothyroid phase before glandular recovery restores normal hormone production in most cases.","additionalDifferentials":["Graves' disease","Painless (silent) thyroiditis and postpartum thyroiditis","Acute suppurative thyroiditis","Haemorrhage into a thyroid cyst or nodule","Toxic multinodular goitre or toxic adenoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thyroid function tests to characterise the phase of disease","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","Radioactive iodine uptake scan showing low uptake in the thyrotoxic phase","Thyroid ultrasound with reduced vascularity distinguishing it from Graves' disease","TSH-receptor antibody testing when Graves' disease is a serious consideration"],"redFlags":["Severe fever, fluctuance, or a rapidly enlarging tender gland suggesting suppurative thyroiditis needing drainage and antimicrobials","Marked thyrotoxic symptoms with cardiac compromise","Persistent hypothyroid symptoms indicating the need for hormone replacement","Compressive symptoms or an atypical mass raising concern for malignancy"],"prognosis":"The illness is usually self-limiting over weeks to months with full recovery of thyroid function in most patients, though pain can be prolonged, recurrences occur occasionally, and a small proportion develop permanent hypothyroidism.","pitfalls":["Mistaking destructive thyrotoxicosis for Graves' disease and prescribing ineffective antithyroid drugs","Failing to check radioiodine uptake or ultrasound vascularity to separate the two","Overlooking the later hypothyroid phase and not monitoring thyroid function through recovery","Attributing a painful gland to malignancy without recognising the inflammatory picture"],"management":["Anti-inflammatory analgesia with NSAIDs for thyroid pain in milder disease","Oral corticosteroids for severe or unremitting thyroid pain not controlled by NSAIDs","Beta-blockade for adrenergic thyrotoxic symptoms during the destructive phase, noting antithyroid drugs are ineffective and not indicated","Reassurance that the illness is self-limiting, with no antithyroid therapy for the transient thyrotoxic phase","Levothyroxine replacement during a symptomatic hypothyroid phase, which is usually temporary","Monitor thyroid function through the disease phases and to detect the small proportion who develop permanent hypothyroidism"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"subaortic_stenosis":{"name":"Subaortic stenosis","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subvalvular aortic stenosis","discrete subaortic stenosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Congenital heart disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Fixed obstruction below the aortic valve, most often from a discrete fibrous or fibromuscular ridge in the left ventricular outflow tract","It is usually acquired and progressive during childhood, and may recur after resection","The turbulent subvalvular jet can damage the aortic valve, causing progressive aortic regurgitation","It must be distinguished from valvular aortic stenosis and from hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (a dynamic obstruction)","An ejection systolic murmur is heard, and unlike valvular stenosis there is no ejection click","Surgical resection is considered for significant gradients or progressive aortic regurgitation, but recurrence is common"],"pathophysiology":"Discrete subaortic stenosis is characterised by a fibrous or fibromuscular membrane or ridge in the left ventricular outflow tract beneath the aortic valve, producing a fixed obstruction to ejection. Abnormal outflow tract geometry and shear stress are thought to stimulate proliferation of the obstructing tissue, which explains its progressive nature and tendency to recur after removal. The high-velocity subvalvular jet strikes the aortic valve leaflets, causing thickening and regurgitation over time, while the pressure-loaded left ventricle hypertrophies.","additionalDifferentials":["Valvular aortic stenosis","Supravalvular aortic stenosis","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (dynamic)","Coarctation (as another left-heart obstruction)","Tunnel-type left ventricular outflow obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography localising the obstruction and measuring the gradient","Assessment of aortic valve for secondary regurgitation","Evaluation of left ventricular hypertrophy and function","Exclusion of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and valvular stenosis","Serial follow-up given progression and recurrence"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive gradient","Developing or worsening aortic regurgitation","Symptoms of outflow obstruction (syncope, angina, breathlessness)","Recurrence after surgical resection"],"prognosis":"Resection relieves the obstruction and can protect the aortic valve, but the lesion may recur and aortic regurgitation can persist, so long-term surveillance is required.","pitfalls":["Confusing fixed subaortic stenosis with dynamic hypertrophic obstruction","Overlooking progressive aortic regurgitation from the subvalvular jet","Assuming cure after resection without follow-up for recurrence","Mislocalising the level of obstruction"],"management":["Surgical resection of the obstructing fibrous or fibromuscular membrane, with septal myectomy where fibromuscular, for a significant gradient or progressive aortic regurgitation","Serial echocardiographic surveillance of the outflow gradient, aortic valve and left ventricular hypertrophy given the tendency to progress and to recur after resection","Monitor for and manage progressive aortic regurgitation caused by the high-velocity subvalvular jet damaging the valve leaflets","Endocarditis prevention through good dental and oral hygiene, with antibiotic prophylaxis where indicated","Reoperation for recurrent obstruction, which is common after initial resection","Referral to congenital or structural cardiology and cardiac surgery for timing of intervention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Congenital heart disease references (standard reference)","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"subclavian_steal_syndrome":{"name":"Subclavian steal syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subclavian steal syndrome","subclavian steal phenomenon","vertebral-subclavian steal"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard vascular surgery and neurology reference texts and cerebrovascular consensus guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by proximal subclavian (or innominate) artery stenosis or occlusion, so blood is drawn in reverse down the ipsilateral vertebral artery to supply the arm, at the expense of the posterior cerebral circulation","Classically presents with arm claudication and posterior-circulation (vertebrobasilar) symptoms such as dizziness, vertigo, visual disturbance or syncope, provoked by exercising the affected arm","A hallmark examination finding is a significant inter-arm systolic blood pressure difference, with the lower pressure on the affected side","The affected arm may also have a weaker or delayed radial pulse and a supraclavicular bruit","It is far more common on the left, reflecting the higher frequency of proximal left subclavian atherosclerosis","The reversed vertebral flow (steal) is often an asymptomatic imaging finding; the term syndrome is reserved for when it produces symptoms","Treatment addresses the proximal lesion with revascularisation (endovascular angioplasty and stenting, or surgical bypass or transposition) alongside atherosclerotic risk reduction"],"pathophysiology":"A stenosis or occlusion of the subclavian artery proximal to the origin of the vertebral artery lowers pressure in the distal subclavian segment. During arm exercise, demand in the limb increases and the pressure gradient reverses flow in the ipsilateral vertebral artery, so blood is siphoned retrogradely from the basilar and contralateral vertebral system into the arm. This diversion can transiently reduce perfusion of the posterior cerebral circulation, producing vertebrobasilar symptoms, while the arm experiences exertional ischaemia. The underlying lesion is usually atherosclerotic, though large-vessel arteritis, prior surgery or congenital anomalies can be responsible.","additionalDifferentials":["Vertebrobasilar insufficiency from intrinsic posterior circulation atherosclerosis","Vestibular or inner-ear causes of vertigo","Cardiac arrhythmia or other causes of syncope","Thoracic outlet syndrome causing arm symptoms","Carotid territory transient ischaemic attack","Large-vessel vasculitis (e.g. Takayasu arteritis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bilateral arm blood pressure measurement to document an inter-arm gradient","Duplex ultrasound of the vertebral and subclavian arteries demonstrating reversed vertebral flow","CT or MR angiography of the aortic arch and great vessels to localise the proximal lesion","Catheter angiography when intervention is planned","Provocative arm-exercise testing to reproduce symptoms and flow reversal","Assessment for large-vessel arteritis when atherosclerosis is not the obvious cause"],"redFlags":["Recurrent syncope or focal neurological deficits from posterior-circulation hypoperfusion","Symptoms in the context of a prior coronary bypass using the internal mammary artery (coronary-subclavian steal causing angina)","Features suggesting large-vessel vasculitis such as systemic symptoms in a younger patient","Severe arm ischaemia or evidence of distal embolisation"],"prognosis":"Asymptomatic subclavian steal is generally benign and often needs no intervention, while symptomatic disease usually responds well to revascularisation with relief of arm and neurological symptoms; long-term outlook is largely determined by the burden of underlying atherosclerotic disease.","pitfalls":["Measuring blood pressure in only one arm and missing the diagnostic inter-arm gradient","Attributing dizziness to purely vestibular causes without considering vertebrobasilar steal","Overlooking coronary-subclavian steal in a patient with an internal mammary artery graft","Failing to consider large-vessel arteritis as an alternative to atherosclerosis"],"management":["Manage asymptomatic reversed vertebral flow expectantly, as it generally needs no intervention beyond surveillance","Best medical therapy with vascular risk-factor modification: antiplatelet agent, statin, blood-pressure and diabetes control, and smoking cessation","Revascularise symptomatic disease, with endovascular angioplasty and stenting of the proximal subclavian lesion as first-line intervention","Surgical bypass such as carotid-subclavian bypass where endovascular treatment is unsuitable or fails","Investigate and treat large-vessel arteritis, including immunosuppression, when it is the underlying cause rather than atherosclerosis","Monitor the inter-arm blood-pressure gradient and symptoms and refer to a vascular specialist for intervention planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Consensus guidance on extracranial cerebrovascular disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"subclinical_cushing_syndrome":{"name":"Subclinical (mild autonomous cortisol secretion) Cushing syndrome","system":"Endocrine / Adrenal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subclinical Cushing syndrome","mild autonomous cortisol secretion","MACS","autonomous cortisol secretion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / ESE-ENSAT Clinical Practice Guideline on the Management of Adrenal Incidentalomas","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Refers to autonomous cortisol secretion from an adrenal adenoma without the overt clinical features of Cushing syndrome; current guidance favours the term mild autonomous cortisol secretion (MACS)","It is typically identified during evaluation of an adrenal incidentaloma","The diagnosis is biochemical, resting principally on failure to suppress cortisol after an overnight low-dose dexamethasone suppression test, with a suppressed or low ACTH supporting adrenal autonomy","Although termed subclinical, it is associated with increased cardiometabolic morbidity, including hypertension, type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia, central obesity and reduced bone density","Post-dexamethasone cortisol thresholds stratify risk, with higher values indicating more definite autonomous secretion","Management is individualised: treat the associated comorbidities and consider adrenalectomy in younger patients with significant secretion and attributable disease","Perioperative and post-adrenalectomy glucocorticoid cover is needed because the contralateral adrenal gland may be suppressed"],"pathophysiology":"An adrenocortical adenoma secretes cortisol semi-autonomously, partly escaping normal hypothalamic-pituitary feedback. The circulating cortisol, although insufficient to produce classic Cushingoid features, suppresses pituitary ACTH and the contralateral adrenal cortex. Chronic mild cortisol excess drives insulin resistance, hypertension, visceral adiposity and bone loss, which explains the cardiometabolic and skeletal consequences despite the absence of florid clinical signs.","additionalDifferentials":["Overt clinical Cushing syndrome","Non-functioning adrenal adenoma","Pseudo-Cushing states related to alcohol, depression or obesity","Exogenous glucocorticoid effect","Metabolic syndrome of another cause","Phaeochromocytoma as part of the incidentaloma workup"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Overnight low-dose dexamethasone suppression test as the key screening test","Plasma ACTH, expected to be low or suppressed, and DHEAS","Late-night salivary cortisol and 24-hour urinary free cortisol to gauge overall cortisol burden","Assessment of comorbidities including blood pressure, glucose or HbA1c, lipids and bone density","Adrenal CT to characterise the adenoma","Metanephrines and aldosterone-to-renin ratio as part of the incidentaloma screen"],"redFlags":["Progression to overt Cushing syndrome","Marked post-dexamethasone cortisol elevation together with significant comorbidity","Imaging features suggesting malignancy in the adrenal lesion","Unrecognised adrenal insufficiency after adrenalectomy from contralateral suppression"],"prognosis":"Often stable, but untreated mild autonomous cortisol secretion carries excess cardiometabolic and fracture risk and higher mortality than a non-functioning adenoma; selected patients improve after adrenalectomy.","pitfalls":["Dismissing subclinical secretion as clinically irrelevant and neglecting cardiometabolic risk","Relying on a single test without confirming autonomy and excluding pseudo-Cushing states","Forgetting perioperative and postoperative steroid cover after adrenalectomy","Missing contralateral adrenal suppression and precipitating an adrenal crisis"],"management":["Confirm autonomous cortisol secretion biochemically with the overnight low-dose dexamethasone suppression test and characterise the adenoma before deciding management","Treat associated cardiometabolic comorbidities: hypertension, type 2 diabetes or glucose intolerance, dyslipidaemia and central obesity","Assess bone density and treat osteoporosis to reduce fracture risk","Adrenalectomy considered in younger patients with more definite autonomous secretion and attributable comorbidity","Perioperative glucocorticoid cover and postoperative steroid replacement for the suppressed contralateral adrenal after adrenalectomy","Surveillance of patients managed non-operatively, monitoring cortisol secretion, adenoma size and evolving comorbidities","Endocrinology referral for individualised decision-making"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","ESE-ENSAT Clinical Practice Guideline on the Management of Adrenal Incidentalomas"],"crossLinks":["adrenal_adenoma_nonfunctioning"]},"subconjunctival_hemorrhage":{"name":"Subconjunctival haemorrhage","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subconjunctival hemorrhage","hyposphagma","SCH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a painless, flat, bright red patch of blood beneath the conjunctiva with a sharply defined border and normal vision","Usually benign and self-limiting, resolving over one to two weeks as the blood is reabsorbed and changes colour","Common precipitants include Valsalva-type events such as coughing, sneezing, straining, or vomiting, minor trauma, and rubbing","Recurrent or bilateral haemorrhages should prompt review of hypertension, anticoagulant or antiplatelet use, and bleeding disorders","The absence of pain, discharge, and visual disturbance distinguishes it from more serious causes of a red eye","A haemorrhage without a visible posterior margin after trauma may indicate a source from behind the orbit, raising concern for orbital fracture or ruptured globe","Reassurance is the mainstay; lubricants may ease mild irritation"],"pathophysiology":"A subconjunctival haemorrhage is bleeding from small conjunctival or episcleral vessels into the potential space between the conjunctiva and the underlying sclera. Because blood is confined to this superficial layer, it appears as a well-demarcated red sheet without affecting the cornea or intraocular structures. Rises in venous pressure (Valsalva), fragile vessels, trauma, or impaired haemostasis predispose to rupture. The blood is gradually cleared by resorption, passing through the usual colour changes of a resolving bruise.","additionalDifferentials":["Conjunctivitis (bacterial, viral, haemorrhagic)","Ruptured globe with subconjunctival bleeding after trauma","Conjunctival or Kaposi lesion mimicking haemorrhage","Anterior uveitis or scleritis (painful red eye)","Bleeding from orbital or retrobulbar haemorrhage"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood pressure measurement, especially in recurrent cases","Review of anticoagulant and antiplatelet medication","Coagulation studies and platelet count when bleeding is recurrent or spontaneous","Slit-lamp examination to exclude other causes of red eye","Assessment for globe rupture or orbital injury when trauma is involved"],"redFlags":["History of significant trauma with a haemorrhage lacking a posterior border, suggesting globe rupture or orbital fracture","Associated pain, reduced vision, or a relative afferent pupillary defect","Recurrent or spontaneous bilateral haemorrhages indicating a systemic bleeding disorder or uncontrolled hypertension","Proptosis or restricted eye movements suggesting orbital pathology"],"prognosis":"Isolated spontaneous subconjunctival haemorrhage resolves completely without treatment and carries no visual risk; prognosis depends on any underlying systemic or traumatic cause.","pitfalls":["Attributing a traumatic haemorrhage to a benign cause and missing an occult globe rupture","Failing to check blood pressure and clotting in recurrent cases","Overlooking anticoagulant overtreatment as a contributor","Providing unnecessary antibiotics for a non-infective condition"],"management":["Reassurance that an isolated spontaneous haemorrhage is benign and self-limiting, resorbing without treatment","Ocular lubricants or artificial tears for any mild surface irritation","Identify and address precipitants, managing Valsalva triggers such as cough, straining, vomiting and constipation","Review blood pressure and anticoagulant or antiplatelet use in recurrent or bilateral cases","Coagulation studies and platelet count where haemorrhages are recurrent or spontaneous to exclude a bleeding disorder","Urgent ophthalmology referral where trauma raises concern for globe rupture or orbital fracture, particularly a haemorrhage without a visible posterior margin"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"subcorneal_pustular_dermatosis":{"name":"Subcorneal pustular dermatosis (Sneddon-Wilkinson)","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sneddon-Wilkinson disease","SPD","subcorneal pustulosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, chronic, relapsing sterile pustular eruption favouring the trunk, flexures, and intertriginous areas","Characterised by flaccid pustules that show a distinctive half-pus, half-clear-fluid level ('hypopyon' pustules) as they settle by gravity","Pustules coalesce into annular and serpiginous patterns and resolve with superficial scale-crust and hyperpigmentation, without scarring","Occurs most often in middle-aged and older women, and mucosal involvement is characteristically absent","Associated with monoclonal gammopathy (especially IgA) and other systemic disorders, so paraproteinaemia should be sought","Direct immunofluorescence is negative, which distinguishes it from the clinically similar IgA pemphigus (SPD type)","Dapsone is the treatment of choice, reflecting the neutrophil-rich, sterile nature of the pustules"],"pathophysiology":"Subcorneal pustular dermatosis is a neutrophilic dermatosis in which sterile neutrophils accumulate immediately beneath the stratum corneum to form subcorneal pustules, without acantholysis or infection. The precise trigger is unknown, but dysregulated neutrophil chemotaxis is central, and cytokine or immunoglobulin abnormalities, notably IgA monoclonal gammopathy, are implicated in a proportion of cases. Unlike IgA pemphigus it lacks demonstrable intercellular immunoglobulin deposition, indicating that the pustulation is driven by neutrophil recruitment rather than by autoantibodies to desmosomal adhesion molecules.","additionalDifferentials":["IgA pemphigus (subcorneal pustular dermatosis type)","Pustular psoriasis","Acute generalised exanthematous pustulosis","Pemphigus foliaceus","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Impetigo / bullous impetigo"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing subcorneal neutrophilic pustules without acantholysis","Direct immunofluorescence (negative) to distinguish from IgA pemphigus","Pustule swab and culture to confirm sterility and exclude infection","Serum protein electrophoresis and immunofixation for monoclonal gammopathy","Screening for associated conditions such as inflammatory bowel disease and lymphoproliferative disease","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase level before commencing dapsone"],"redFlags":["Detection of an IgA paraproteinaemia or other monoclonal gammopathy requiring haematological review","Widespread pustulation with systemic upset suggesting pustular psoriasis or infection","Secondary infection of eroded skin","Haemolysis or methaemoglobinaemia complicating dapsone therapy"],"prognosis":"The condition typically follows a benign but chronic, relapsing-remitting course over years; it responds well to dapsone, and outcome is largely influenced by any associated gammopathy or systemic disease.","pitfalls":["Failing to perform direct immunofluorescence and thereby missing IgA pemphigus","Treating sterile pustules as bacterial infection with antibiotics alone","Overlooking an associated monoclonal gammopathy","Starting dapsone without checking glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status"],"management":["First-line dapsone to suppress neutrophil recruitment, after checking glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status to reduce the risk of haemolysis","Alternative or adjunctive agents where dapsone is not tolerated, such as acitretin, colchicine or sulfapyridine","Narrowband UVB or PUVA phototherapy for widespread or treatment-resistant disease","Topical corticosteroids and emollients for symptomatic relief of pustulation and scaling","Monitor for dapsone-induced haemolytic anaemia and methaemoglobinaemia with regular blood counts","Investigate and monitor for associated IgA monoclonal gammopathy, and screen for inflammatory bowel and lymphoproliferative disease, with haematology referral if a paraprotein is found","Consider TNF-alpha inhibitors or other biologics for refractory disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"subcutaneous_fat_necrosis_newborn":{"name":"Subcutaneous fat necrosis of the newborn","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCFN","subcutaneous fat necrosis of the neonate","adiponecrosis subcutanea neonatorum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents in the first weeks of life as firm, indurated, red to violaceous nodules and plaques over the back, buttocks, shoulders, thighs and cheeks","It typically follows a difficult delivery, perinatal hypoxia, hypothermia, therapeutic cooling or other perinatal stress in an otherwise term or post-term infant","Lesions are usually painless or mildly tender and the baby is generally well at presentation","The most important complication is hypercalcaemia, which may appear weeks after the skin lesions and can be dangerous","Biopsy shows a lobular panniculitis with needle-shaped clefts within adipocytes and a granulomatous infiltrate with multinucleate giant cells","Affected infants need monitoring of serum calcium for several weeks to months even as the skin improves","Skin lesions resolve spontaneously, so treatment is largely supportive with active management of any hypercalcaemia"],"pathophysiology":"Subcutaneous fat necrosis of the newborn is an injury to neonatal fat that follows perinatal stresses such as hypoxia, hypothermia and trauma, often in the setting of therapeutic hypothermia. Neonatal fat is rich in saturated fatty acids with a higher solidification point, making it prone to crystallisation and necrosis when subjected to cold or ischaemia. The damaged adipocytes provoke a granulomatous inflammatory response with characteristic needle-shaped crystal clefts. Granulomatous tissue can produce excess 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, driving intestinal calcium absorption and the delayed hypercalcaemia that is the key systemic risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Sclerema neonatorum","Cold panniculitis of infancy","Neonatal cellulitis or abscess","Bacterial or other infective panniculitis","Haemangioma or other congenital nodule","Post-injection or traumatic fat necrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial serum calcium measurement over subsequent weeks to months","Serum phosphate, and vitamin D metabolites if hypercalcaemia occurs","Renal function and, if calcium is high, renal ultrasound for nephrocalcinosis","Skin biopsy for the characteristic crystalline lobular panniculitis when uncertain","Full blood count and platelet count (thrombocytopenia may occur)","Glucose and triglycerides in extensive disease"],"redFlags":["Hypercalcaemia with poor feeding, vomiting, irritability, lethargy or seizures","Nephrocalcinosis or renal impairment from sustained hypercalcaemia","Extensive or ulcerating lesions with possible secondary infection","Thrombocytopenia or metabolic derangement"],"prognosis":"The skin lesions almost always resolve spontaneously over weeks to months, sometimes with transient atrophy; the outlook is good provided hypercalcaemia is detected and managed, as untreated hypercalcaemia can cause significant morbidity.","pitfalls":["Discharging without arranging prolonged calcium monitoring, so delayed hypercalcaemia is missed","Mistaking the firm plaques for cellulitis and giving unnecessary antibiotics","Confusing it with the more diffuse and ominous sclerema neonatorum","Assuming resolution of the skin signals that the risk of hypercalcaemia has passed"],"management":["Manage the skin lesions conservatively, as the nodules resolve spontaneously and need only supportive skin care","Monitor serum calcium serially over weeks to months, because dangerous hypercalcaemia is characteristically delayed after the skin lesions appear","Treat significant hypercalcaemia with intravenous fluid rehydration and a loop diuretic to enhance calcium excretion","Use corticosteroids to reduce granulomatous 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D production, with calcitonin or a bisphosphonate for refractory cases","Restrict calcium and vitamin D intake and withhold vitamin D supplementation during the at-risk period","Check renal function and arrange renal ultrasound for nephrocalcinosis, and monitor the platelet count for thrombocytopenia","Involve neonatology and paediatric endocrinology to manage hypercalcaemia and its complications"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"subcutaneous_fat_necrosis_of_newborn":{"name":"subcutaneous fat necrosis of newborn","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subcutaneous fat necrosis of the newborn","SCFN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Weedon's Skin Pathology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A self-limiting lobular panniculitis of term and post-term neonates, typically appearing in the first days to weeks of life as firm, indurated, red-violaceous nodules and plaques.","Characteristic sites are areas rich in fat and subject to pressure: the back, buttocks, shoulders, cheeks and proximal limbs.","Risk factors include perinatal asphyxia, hypothermia (including therapeutic cooling), meconium aspiration, pre-eclampsia and maternal factors such as gestational diabetes.","The infant is usually otherwise well, in contrast to sclerema neonatorum which affects sick, premature neonates diffusely.","The principal and potentially life-threatening complication is hypercalcaemia, which may be delayed for weeks after the skin lesions appear.","Histology shows fat necrosis with a granulomatous infiltrate and pathognomonic needle-shaped clefts (radial crystals of triglyceride) within adipocytes and giant cells.","Other metabolic derangements include thrombocytopenia, hypertriglyceridaemia and hypoglycaemia, warranting monitoring.","Skin lesions resolve spontaneously over weeks to months, but serum calcium must be monitored well beyond cutaneous resolution."],"pathophysiology":"Neonatal subcutaneous fat has a high ratio of saturated (stearic and palmitic) to unsaturated fatty acids and a higher melting point than adult fat, predisposing it to crystallise and solidify under stress. Perinatal hypoxia, hypothermia and local pressure cause ischaemic injury to this metabolically vulnerable fat, triggering crystallisation and necrosis of adipocytes with liberation of fatty acids. A granulomatous inflammatory response ensues, with foreign-body giant cells containing characteristic radially arranged needle-shaped crystal clefts. The associated hypercalcaemia is attributed largely to extrarenal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity within the granulomatous infiltrate producing 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, augmented by prostaglandin E and release of calcium from necrotic fat.","additionalDifferentials":["sclerema neonatorum","neonatal cellulitis or abscess","cold panniculitis (popsicle panniculitis)","bacillus Calmette-Guerin or other iatrogenic injection-site reactions","congenital infantile haemangioma or other vascular tumours","poststeroid panniculitis (in infants weaned off corticosteroids)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["serial serum calcium (including ionised calcium) for several weeks to months","serum phosphate, magnesium, parathyroid hormone and vitamin D metabolites","full blood count for thrombocytopenia","serum triglycerides and glucose","skin biopsy for histopathology when diagnosis is uncertain (needle-shaped clefts, granulomatous panniculitis)","renal ultrasound and urinary calcium if nephrocalcinosis is suspected","electrocardiogram if significant hypercalcaemia is detected"],"redFlags":["hypercalcaemia causing irritability, poor feeding, vomiting, lethargy or seizures","nephrocalcinosis or renal impairment from sustained hypercalcaemia","extensive or ulcerating lesions predisposing to secondary infection","cardiac arrhythmia associated with severe hypercalcaemia","systemically unwell infant suggesting sclerema neonatorum or sepsis rather than isolated fat necrosis"],"prognosis":"The cutaneous prognosis is excellent, with spontaneous resolution over weeks to months, but the outcome hinges on detecting and managing hypercalcaemia, which can be delayed and, if unrecognised, cause metastatic calcification or renal damage.","pitfalls":["Discharging the infant once skin lesions improve without arranging prolonged calcium monitoring.","Confusing it with sclerema neonatorum, which carries a far graver prognosis and different clinical context.","Attributing hypercalcaemic symptoms to feeding difficulties or sepsis.","Failing to recognise therapeutic hypothermia as a precipitating factor.","Overlooking thrombocytopenia or hypertriglyceridaemia in the metabolic work-up."],"management":["Treat conservatively as a self-limiting panniculitis, with supportive skin care while the nodules involute spontaneously","Perform serial serum calcium monitoring for several weeks to months, since hypercalcaemia is often delayed and potentially life-threatening","Manage symptomatic hypercalcaemia with hyperhydration using intravenous fluids and a loop diuretic to promote calciuresis","Add corticosteroids to suppress extrarenal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity, escalating to calcitonin or a bisphosphonate if refractory","Limit dietary calcium and vitamin D and avoid supplements during the vulnerable period","Monitor renal function with renal ultrasound and urinary calcium for nephrocalcinosis, and check platelet count, glucose and triglycerides","Optimise perinatal care with correction of hypoxia and hypothermia, and involve neonatal and paediatric endocrine teams"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Weedon's Skin Pathology, current edition","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Textbook of Neonatal Dermatology, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"subcutaneous_panniculitis_like_t_cell_lymphoma":{"name":"Subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPTCL","subcutaneous panniculitic T-cell lymphoma","alpha/beta subcutaneous T-cell lymphoma","panniculitis-like cytotoxic T-cell lymphoma","CD8+/betaF1+/TCR alpha-beta subcutaneous lymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with deep subcutaneous nodules or plaques, often on the limbs and trunk, that clinically resemble a panniculitis","Systemic symptoms such as fever, fatigue, and weight loss are common, and a subset develop an associated haemophagocytic syndrome that dominates the clinical picture","By definition the neoplastic infiltrate is confined to subcutaneous fat, rimming individual adipocytes, without dermal or epidermal involvement","Tumour cells are cytotoxic T-lymphocytes of the alpha-beta lineage, typically CD8-positive and betaF1-positive, with expression of cytotoxic granule proteins","It must be separated from primary cutaneous gamma-delta T-cell lymphoma, which involves dermis and epidermis, is more aggressive, and carries a worse prognosis","An overlap exists with lupus erythematosus panniculitis, and the two can be difficult to distinguish or may coexist","In the absence of haemophagocytic syndrome the course is often relatively indolent"],"pathophysiology":"Subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma is a cytotoxic T-cell neoplasm derived from alpha-beta T-lymphocytes that selectively infiltrates subcutaneous adipose tissue. The malignant cells characteristically surround individual fat cells, producing a lobular panniculitis-like pattern with fat necrosis and karyorrhexis while sparing the overlying dermis and epidermis. Cytotoxic activity and cytokine release can drive macrophage activation, precipitating a haemophagocytic syndrome that accounts for much of the disease's morbidity. Some cases arise on a background of, or overlap with, autoimmune disease, blurring the boundary with lupus panniculitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Lupus erythematosus panniculitis","Primary cutaneous gamma-delta T-cell lymphoma","Erythema nodosum and other reactive panniculitides","Infective panniculitis","Cytophagic histiocytic panniculitis","Other cutaneous T-cell lymphomas with subcutaneous spread"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Deep incisional skin biopsy sampling subcutaneous fat with immunohistochemistry (CD8, betaF1, cytotoxic markers)","Assessment of the T-cell receptor lineage to exclude a gamma-delta phenotype","T-cell receptor clonality analysis","Ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen, and blood counts to screen for haemophagocytic syndrome","Autoimmune serology to evaluate for lupus panniculitis overlap","Cross-sectional imaging and bone marrow examination if systemic disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["Development of haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis with cytopenias, high ferritin, and organ dysfunction","Rapid dissemination or transformation to a more aggressive phenotype","Extensive ulcerating disease suggesting a gamma-delta lymphoma rather than SPTCL","Systemic deterioration with high fever and multi-organ involvement"],"prognosis":"Without haemophagocytic syndrome the prognosis is generally favourable and disease may be controlled with immunosuppression or systemic therapy, but an associated haemophagocytic syndrome markedly worsens outcome.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the lesions as benign panniculitis and delaying biopsy","Failing to distinguish SPTCL from the more aggressive gamma-delta cutaneous lymphoma","Overlooking a developing haemophagocytic syndrome","Confusing the condition with lupus panniculitis, which it can closely resemble or coexist with"],"management":["For indolent disease without haemophagocytic syndrome, use immunosuppression with systemic corticosteroids, often combined with ciclosporin, as first-line therapy","Reserve anthracycline-based multiagent chemotherapy for aggressive, progressive or haemophagocytic-syndrome-associated disease","Treat associated haemophagocytic syndrome promptly with immunosuppressive HLH-directed therapy including corticosteroids and etoposide","Consider localised radiotherapy for solitary or limited cutaneous lesions, and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation for refractory or relapsing disease","Monitor for evolution of haemophagocytic syndrome with ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen and blood counts","Distinguish from the more aggressive gamma-delta cutaneous lymphoma and evaluate for overlapping lupus panniculitis, as these alter management","Coordinate care within a specialist haemato-oncology multidisciplinary team with dermatology input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","Swerdlow SH et al. WHO monograph on lymphoid neoplasms"],"crossLinks":[]},"subependymal_giant_cell_astrocytoma":{"name":"Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma","system":"Neuro-oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SEGA","SGCA","Subependymal giant cell tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neuro-oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["WHO grade 1 tumour that is the characteristic CNS neoplasm of tuberous sclerosis complex, arising near the foramen of Monro at the caudothalamic groove.","Typically presents in childhood, adolescence or young adulthood with signs of obstructive hydrocephalus: worsening headache, morning vomiting, papilloedema, decline in behaviour or cognition, or an increase in seizures.","Growth of a subependymal lesion on serial imaging, or a lesion of sufficient size near the foramen of Monro, distinguishes a SEGA from a static subependymal nodule.","Driven by biallelic loss of TSC1 (hamartin) or TSC2 (tuberin) with constitutive activation of the mTOR complex 1 pathway.","mTOR inhibitors (for example everolimus) can reduce tumour volume and are an option for lesions not requiring emergency surgery.","Surgical resection is definitive and is indicated for acute or symptomatic hydrocephalus.","Regular surveillance MRI is recommended in tuberous sclerosis complex to detect enlarging lesions before they cause hydrocephalus.","Discovery of a SEGA should prompt evaluation for the wider features of tuberous sclerosis complex."],"pathophysiology":"Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma is a benign glioneuronal-astrocytic tumour that arises in tuberous sclerosis complex from the subependyma of the lateral ventricle adjacent to the foramen of Monro, characteristically at the caudothalamic groove. It results from biallelic inactivation of TSC1 (encoding hamartin, on chromosome 9q34) or TSC2 (encoding tuberin, on chromosome 16p13.3); loss of the hamartin-tuberin complex removes inhibition of Rheb and leads to constitutive activation of mTOR complex 1, driving cell growth and proliferation. The tumour is composed of large ganglion-like and plump gemistocyte-like astrocytic cells with variable calcification, and its periventricular location predisposes to obstruction of cerebrospinal fluid outflow and hydrocephalus.","additionalDifferentials":["Subependymal nodule (non-growing) of tuberous sclerosis complex","Central neurocytoma","Intraventricular ependymoma","Choroid plexus papilloma","Subependymoma","Astrocytoma","Intraventricular meningioma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced MRI of the brain showing an enhancing, often calcified mass at the foramen of Monro, compared with prior imaging to assess for growth","CT to demonstrate calcification and ventricular size","Surveillance neuroimaging as part of tuberous sclerosis complex follow-up","Genetic testing for TSC1 and TSC2 mutations","Assessment for other tuberous sclerosis complex features (skin, cardiac rhabdomyoma, renal angiomyolipoma, retinal hamartoma) with echocardiography, renal imaging and dermatological/ophthalmological review","Ophthalmological examination for papilloedema","Histopathology with immunohistochemistry where the lesion is resected"],"redFlags":["New or worsening headache, vomiting or papilloedema indicating obstructive hydrocephalus","Documented interval growth of a subependymal lesion near the foramen of Monro","Acute deterioration in conscious level from acute hydrocephalus","New focal deficit or a marked change in seizure pattern or behaviour"],"prognosis":"As a WHO grade 1 tumour the prognosis is good, with the main morbidity arising from obstructive hydrocephalus. Outcomes are favourable with timely surgery or mTOR-inhibitor therapy, though lesions may regrow and warrant ongoing surveillance.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling a growing SEGA as a stable subependymal nodule and missing impending hydrocephalus.","Assuming that absence of an obvious tuberous sclerosis complex phenotype excludes the diagnosis; rare cases occur without full clinical features.","Delaying surveillance imaging in tuberous sclerosis complex until symptoms develop.","Attributing new headaches or behavioural change solely to epilepsy rather than raised intracranial pressure."],"management":["mTOR inhibition with everolimus to reduce tumour volume for growing lesions that do not require emergency surgery","Microsurgical resection as definitive treatment for symptomatic tumours or acute obstructive hydrocephalus","Cerebrospinal fluid diversion by ventriculoperitoneal shunt or endoscopic third ventriculostomy when hydrocephalus needs relief","Serial contrast-enhanced MRI surveillance of the foramen of Monro region to detect growth or recurrence","Manage the wider tuberous sclerosis complex, including antiseizure therapy and monitoring of renal angiomyolipoma and cardiac rhabdomyoma","Genetic counselling and family screening for TSC1 and TSC2 mutations given autosomal dominant inheritance","Escalate urgently to neurosurgery for signs of raised intracranial pressure such as worsening headache, vomiting or declining consciousness"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Central Nervous System Tumours","International Tuberous Sclerosis Complex consensus and surveillance guidelines","Standard paediatric neuro-oncology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"subependymoma":{"name":"Subependymoma","system":"Oncology / Neuro-oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subependymoma","subependymal glioma","WHO grade 1 subependymoma","intraventricular subependymoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of the CNS","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign, slow-growing intraventricular tumour (CNS WHO grade 1), most often found in middle-aged and older adults","Characteristically located in the fourth ventricle or the lateral ventricles, attached to the ventricular wall","Frequently an incidental finding at imaging or autopsy, as many are asymptomatic","Symptoms, when present, usually reflect obstruction of cerebrospinal fluid flow with hydrocephalus and raised intracranial pressure","Histology shows clusters of bland glial cells within a dense fibrillary matrix, often with microcystic change and little mitotic activity","Mixed subependymoma-ependymoma tumours can occur, and the ependymoma component determines behaviour","Observation is reasonable for asymptomatic lesions, while symptomatic tumours are usually cured by surgical resection"],"pathophysiology":"Subependymoma is a slow-growing, benign glial tumour thought to arise from the subependymal glial cells lining the ventricles, possibly from a mix of ependymal and astrocytic progenitor cells. It grows as a well-demarcated nodular mass projecting into the ventricular lumen and is composed of small clusters of uniform, cytologically bland cells embedded in an abundant fibrillary glial matrix, commonly with microcysts and calcification. Proliferative activity is very low, accounting for its indolent behaviour, and it lacks the aggressive molecular features seen in higher-grade ependymal tumours. Clinical problems arise mechanically when the tumour is large enough to impede cerebrospinal fluid circulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Ependymoma","Central neurocytoma","Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma","Choroid plexus tumour","Intraventricular meningioma","Colloid cyst (for third ventricular lesions)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced brain MRI to characterise the well-defined intraventricular mass","Assessment of ventricular size for obstructive hydrocephalus","MRI features distinguishing it from ependymoma (typically minimal enhancement)","Histopathology confirming bland glial clusters in a fibrillary matrix","Immunohistochemistry (GFAP positivity) to confirm glial origin","Serial imaging surveillance for incidental asymptomatic lesions"],"redFlags":["Obstructive hydrocephalus with rising intracranial pressure requiring intervention","Sudden deterioration from acute cerebrospinal fluid obstruction","Atypical or contrast-avid features suggesting a mixed or higher-grade ependymal component","New brainstem signs from a fourth-ventricular mass"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent, as subependymoma is benign and slow-growing; complete surgical resection of a symptomatic tumour is generally curative, and incidental asymptomatic lesions can often simply be monitored. Recurrence after gross total resection is uncommon.","pitfalls":["Over-treating an incidental, asymptomatic benign lesion","Misinterpreting a mixed subependymoma-ependymoma as pure subependymoma and underestimating recurrence risk","Attributing symptoms to the tumour when they are due to unrelated pathology","Failing to recognise and relieve obstructive hydrocephalus in a symptomatic case"],"management":["Observe asymptomatic incidental lesions with serial surveillance MRI rather than intervening","Surgical resection, aiming for gross total removal, is definitive and generally curative for symptomatic tumours","Relieve obstructive hydrocephalus with cerebrospinal fluid diversion when required","Confirm histology and exclude a mixed subependymoma-ependymoma component, since an ependymoma element dictates further treatment","Reserve adjuvant radiotherapy for a higher-grade ependymal component, as pure subependymoma needs no chemo- or radiotherapy","Postoperative surveillance imaging to detect the uncommon recurrence after complete resection","Refer to neurosurgery and neuro-oncology for operative planning and follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System","Louis DN et al. The 2021 WHO Classification of Tumors of the Central Nervous System"],"crossLinks":[]},"subgaleal_haemorrhage":{"name":"Neonatal subgaleal haemorrhage","system":"Neonatal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subgaleal haematoma","subaponeurotic haemorrhage"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neonatology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Bleeding into the loose potential space between the scalp aponeurosis and the periosteum, which can hold a large volume of blood","It is the most dangerous of the extracranial birth haemorrhages because it can cause life-threatening blood loss","It is strongly associated with vacuum-assisted (and sometimes forceps) delivery","The swelling is diffuse, boggy and fluctuant, crosses sutures and may shift with position, sometimes with a fluid thrill","Signs of hypovolaemia (tachycardia, pallor, falling haematocrit) and expanding head circumference indicate ongoing bleeding","Management requires close monitoring, volume and blood-product resuscitation and correction of coagulopathy"],"pathophysiology":"Subgaleal haemorrhage occurs when shearing forces, characteristically from vacuum extraction, rupture the emissary veins traversing the subgaleal space. Because this space is large and unconfined by suture attachments, it can accommodate a substantial proportion of the neonate's blood volume, leading to significant, sometimes catastrophic, haemorrhage. The resulting hypovolaemia and any accompanying coagulopathy can cause shock, and breakdown of the extravasated blood contributes to jaundice.","additionalDifferentials":["Caput succedaneum","Cephalohaematoma","Coagulopathy causing bleeding","Intracranial haemorrhage","Other causes of neonatal shock"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial head circumference measurement and clinical assessment of the swelling","Full blood count and serial haematocrit","Coagulation studies","Continuous vital sign monitoring for hypovolaemia","Bilirubin monitoring during resorption"],"redFlags":["Expanding swelling with rising head circumference","Tachycardia, pallor or falling haematocrit indicating significant blood loss","Signs of shock or coagulopathy","Reduced conscious level"],"prognosis":"With early recognition and aggressive resuscitation most infants recover, but delayed diagnosis of a large or ongoing bleed can be fatal; the condition demands vigilance after assisted delivery.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for benign caput succedaneum and underestimating blood loss","Failing to monitor head circumference and haematocrit serially","Delaying resuscitation and blood products","Overlooking an associated coagulopathy"],"management":["Recognise early and admit to neonatal intensive care with continuous monitoring of heart rate, blood pressure and perfusion","Restore circulating volume with crystalloid and transfuse packed red cells for significant blood loss and anaemia","Correct coagulopathy with fresh frozen plasma, platelets and vitamin K as required","Track ongoing bleeding with serial head circumference measurement and serial haematocrit","Monitor and treat hyperbilirubinaemia with phototherapy, or exchange transfusion if severe, as the collection resorbs","Escalate to intensive support for hypovolaemic shock or disseminated intravascular coagulation","Image the cranium when an associated skull fracture or intracranial injury is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neonatology reference texts (standard reference)","Paediatric emergency references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"subgaleal_hemorrhage":{"name":"Neonatal subgaleal haemorrhage","system":"Neonatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subgaleal haemorrhage","subaponeurotic haemorrhage","subgaleal haematoma","SGH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cloherty and Stark Manual of Neonatal Care / Fanaroff and Martin's Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Subgaleal haemorrhage is bleeding into the loose potential space between the scalp aponeurosis and the periosteum, which can hold a large proportion of the neonate's blood volume","It is most strongly associated with instrumental delivery, particularly vacuum extraction, and with coagulopathy","Unlike a cephalhaematoma, the swelling crosses suture lines and is a boggy, fluctuant, shifting collection that can extend from the orbits to the nape of the neck","Because the space is large, blood loss can be substantial and lead to hypovolaemic shock and anaemia, making it a potentially life-threatening emergency","Serial measurement of head circumference and close monitoring of vital signs and perfusion are essential for early detection of ongoing bleeding","Management is largely supportive: prompt recognition, volume and blood replacement, correction of coagulopathy, and monitoring for hyperbilirubinaemia as the collection resorbs","A high index of suspicion after difficult or assisted delivery allows timely intervention before decompensation"],"pathophysiology":"Subgaleal haemorrhage results from rupture of the emissary veins that connect the scalp veins to the intracranial dural sinuses, or from shearing of these vessels during delivery. Blood collects in the subgaleal space, a large loosely bound plane beneath the epicranial aponeurosis that is not limited by cranial sutures, so bleeding can dissect widely across the scalp. Traction and shearing forces, especially from vacuum extraction, and impaired haemostasis from coagulopathy predispose to this injury. Because the space can accommodate a large volume, continued bleeding leads to progressive expansion of the swelling, significant blood loss, hypovolaemia, and anaemia, and later hyperbilirubinaemia as the extravasated blood is broken down.","additionalDifferentials":["Cephalhaematoma, which is confined by suture lines","Caput succedaneum, a superficial oedematous swelling that also crosses sutures but is not a large blood collection","Intracranial haemorrhage presenting with neurological signs","Scalp abscess or infected collection","Cranial or scalp swelling from birth trauma such as skull fracture","Other causes of neonatal hypovolaemia or anaemia such as feto-maternal haemorrhage"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial head circumference measurement to track expansion of the collection","Full blood count and serial haematocrit to detect and follow blood loss","Coagulation studies to identify a contributing bleeding disorder","Continuous monitoring of heart rate, blood pressure, and perfusion for hypovolaemia","Serum bilirubin monitoring as the haematoma resorbs","Cranial imaging when intracranial injury or skull fracture is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging, boggy scalp swelling crossing suture lines after assisted delivery","Tachycardia, pallor, hypotension, or poor perfusion indicating hypovolaemic shock","Falling haematocrit or laboratory evidence of significant blood loss","Associated coagulopathy or neurological signs suggesting intracranial injury"],"prognosis":"With early recognition and prompt supportive care most infants recover fully. However, unrecognised or large subgaleal haemorrhage can cause severe hypovolaemic shock, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and death, so mortality is significant when diagnosis is delayed. Survivors of severe blood loss are at risk of associated hypoxic-ischaemic injury.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a subgaleal haemorrhage for a benign caput or cephalhaematoma and underestimating blood loss","Failing to monitor for progressive expansion and cardiovascular compromise after assisted delivery","Delaying volume resuscitation and blood transfusion in a decompensating infant","Overlooking an underlying coagulopathy that perpetuates bleeding","Not anticipating later hyperbilirubinaemia during resorption of the collection"],"management":["Recognise promptly after assisted delivery and admit to neonatal intensive care with continuous monitoring of vital signs and perfusion","Resuscitate with crystalloid volume replacement and transfuse packed red cells for substantial blood loss and anaemia","Correct any coagulopathy with fresh frozen plasma, platelets and vitamin K","Follow expansion of the collection with serial head circumference measurement and serial haematocrit","Monitor serum bilirubin and treat hyperbilirubinaemia with phototherapy as the haematoma resorbs","Escalate to intensive care for hypovolaemic shock or disseminated intravascular coagulation","Arrange cranial imaging if a skull fracture or intracranial injury is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cloherty and Stark Manual of Neonatal Care","Fanaroff and Martin's Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"subglottic_hemangioma":{"name":"Subglottic haemangioma","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Laryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["infantile subglottic haemangioma","subglottic hemangioma","congenital subglottic haemangioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest cause of a vascular subglottic mass in infancy, being an infantile haemangioma of the subglottis that follows the typical proliferate-then-involute natural history","Presents in the first months of life with biphasic stridor and a barky cough, often initially misdiagnosed as croup","Symptoms characteristically worsen during the proliferative phase and with intercurrent upper respiratory infection or crying","A cutaneous haemangioma in a beard distribution should raise suspicion of airway involvement and prompt endoscopy","Large segmental facial haemangiomas may be part of PHACE association, warranting broader evaluation","Endoscopy typically reveals a compressible, often asymmetrical, reddish or bluish submucosal swelling, usually on the left posterolateral subglottis","Propranolol has become the mainstay of medical treatment, with surgery or airway support reserved for selected or resistant cases"],"pathophysiology":"A subglottic haemangioma is an infantile haemangioma, a benign proliferation of endothelial cells, located in the submucosa of the subglottic airway. Like cutaneous infantile haemangiomas it is typically absent or minimal at birth, proliferates over the first months of life, and then slowly involutes over subsequent years. During proliferation the lesion enlarges and encroaches on the already narrow infant subglottis, and because this is the least distensible part of the paediatric airway even modest swelling produces significant obstruction and biphasic stridor. Mucosal engorgement with crying or infection transiently worsens the narrowing, and spontaneous regression during the involutional phase underlies the tendency for symptoms to abate with time.","additionalDifferentials":["Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis)","Subglottic stenosis","Laryngomalacia","Subglottic cyst","Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis","Vascular malformation or other airway tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible fibreoptic laryngoscopy for initial airway assessment","Rigid endoscopy under anaesthesia, the key investigation to confirm a compressible subglottic vascular lesion","MRI to define the extent of a segmental or transspatial lesion","Cardiac, cerebrovascular, and ophthalmological evaluation where PHACE association is suspected","Examination of the skin for cutaneous haemangiomas in a beard distribution"],"redFlags":["Increasing biphasic stridor and respiratory distress during the proliferative phase","A beard-distribution cutaneous haemangioma indicating likely airway involvement","Features suggesting PHACE association requiring cardiovascular and intracranial assessment","Recurrent 'croup' that fails to resolve as expected, prompting airway endoscopy"],"prognosis":"The natural history is favourable because the lesion ultimately involutes, and with propranolol many infants avoid surgery and do well; the risk lies in airway obstruction during the proliferative phase, so timely diagnosis and treatment are important.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly diagnosing recurrent croup and missing an underlying subglottic haemangioma","Overlooking the beard-distribution skin clue to airway involvement","Failing to evaluate for PHACE association before commencing systemic treatment","Assuming spontaneous involution is safe without controlling airway obstruction during proliferation"],"management":["Oral propranolol is first-line to accelerate involution and relieve airway obstruction","Screen for PHACE association and perform baseline cardiovascular assessment before starting propranolol, then monitor for bradycardia and hypoglycaemia","Systemic or intralesional corticosteroids as an alternative or adjunct where beta-blockade is contraindicated or insufficient","Endoscopic intervention such as laser ablation, or open surgical excision, for lesions refractory to medical therapy","Secure the airway during the proliferative phase, reserving tracheostomy for severe obstruction unresponsive to other measures","Serial airway endoscopy to monitor response, with cautious weaning of propranolol after involution while watching for rebound growth","Manage within a paediatric otolaryngology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"subglottic_stenosis":{"name":"Subglottic stenosis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SGS","subglottic narrowing","laryngotracheal stenosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Narrowing of the airway at the subglottis, the region just below the vocal cords, which is the narrowest part of the paediatric airway and bounded by the complete cricoid ring","It may be congenital or acquired, and prolonged endotracheal intubation is the leading acquired cause","Presents with biphasic stridor, recurrent or prolonged croup-like episodes, exertional breathlessness, and in severe cases respiratory distress","In infants it can masquerade as recurrent croup or failure to extubate after ventilation","Endoscopic assessment of the airway defines the site, length, and severity of narrowing, and grading systems (such as Cotton-Myer) quantify the degree of luminal loss","Management depends on severity and ranges from observation and endoscopic dilatation or laser to open reconstruction such as laryngotracheal reconstruction or cricotracheal resection","A tracheostomy may be needed to secure the airway in severe or complex cases before or during definitive repair","Minimising intubation trauma and duration, and using appropriately sized tubes, helps prevent acquired stenosis"],"pathophysiology":"Subglottic stenosis is a fixed reduction in the cross-sectional area of the subglottic airway. The congenital form reflects abnormal development of the cricoid cartilage producing an inherently narrow lumen. The acquired form typically follows mucosal injury, most often from a tightly fitting or prolonged endotracheal tube, which causes pressure ischaemia, ulceration, perichondritis, and healing by fibrosis with scar contracture; trauma, infection, inflammatory disease, and prior airway surgery are other causes. Because the cricoid is a complete non-expansile ring, even modest circumferential scarring or oedema markedly reduces the lumen and airflow.","additionalDifferentials":["Laryngomalacia","Vocal cord palsy","Recurrent croup (laryngotracheobronchitis)","Subglottic haemangioma in an infant","Vascular ring or extrinsic tracheal compression","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis affecting the airway"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible and rigid endoscopy of the airway to assess and grade the stenosis","Sizing of the airway with endotracheal tubes to quantify the degree of narrowing","Cross-sectional imaging to map the length of the stenotic segment and exclude external compression","Evaluation for inflammatory or autoimmune disease when clinically suspected","Assessment of reflux, which can aggravate airway inflammation"],"redFlags":["Progressive stridor with respiratory distress indicating critical airway narrowing requiring urgent airway control","Failure to extubate or recurrent severe respiratory episodes in an infant","Rapidly worsening obstruction suggesting superimposed oedema or infection on a fixed stenosis","Signs of an underlying systemic inflammatory disease driving the stenosis"],"prognosis":"Mild congenital stenosis often improves as the airway grows, and mild acquired disease may respond to endoscopic measures; more severe or long-segment stenosis usually needs open reconstruction, which has good success in restoring an adequate airway, though multiple procedures and temporary tracheostomy are sometimes required.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating a child for croup while missing a fixed subglottic stenosis","Using oversized endotracheal tubes and prolonging intubation, promoting acquired stenosis","Underestimating severity by relying on symptoms alone without endoscopic grading","Overlooking an inflammatory cause such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis"],"management":["Observe mild congenital stenosis, which often improves as the airway grows","Endoscopic balloon dilatation, with laser scar division and adjunctive intralesional corticosteroid or mitomycin, for suitable short-segment disease","Open reconstruction by laryngotracheal reconstruction with cartilage grafting or cricotracheal resection for severe or long-segment stenosis","Tracheostomy to secure the airway in severe or acute obstruction","Treat aggravating gastro-oesophageal reflux with acid suppression and control airway inflammation","Institute immunosuppression where an underlying inflammatory or autoimmune disease such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis is identified","Manage within a specialist airway multidisciplinary team, anticipating that staged or repeated procedures may be needed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"substance_use_disorder":{"name":"Substance use disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SUD","addiction","substance dependence","substance abuse"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The disorder is a cluster of cognitive, behavioural, and physiological features indicating continued use of a substance despite significant harm, with severity graded from mild to severe by the number of features present","Core domains include impaired control over use, social impairment, risky use in hazardous situations, and pharmacological features such as tolerance and withdrawal","Tolerance and withdrawal alone in a patient taking a prescribed medication as directed do not by themselves indicate a use disorder","Craving, unsuccessful attempts to cut down, and continued use despite recognised harm are central to the behavioural picture","Screening should be routine and non-judgemental, using validated tools, and should cover alcohol, tobacco, prescription medicines, and illicit substances","Management integrates psychosocial interventions such as motivational interviewing and cognitive behavioural therapy with substance-specific pharmacotherapy and relapse-prevention strategies","Comorbid psychiatric illness is common, and integrated treatment of both the substance disorder and the mental disorder improves outcomes","Withdrawal from some substances, notably alcohol and sedatives, can be life-threatening and may require medically supervised detoxification"],"pathophysiology":"Substance use disorders arise from repeated activation of the mesolimbic dopaminergic reward pathway, producing neuroadaptations in reward, motivation, memory, and executive control circuits that drive compulsive use and craving. Genetic vulnerability, early exposure, psychiatric comorbidity, and environmental and social stressors interact with these neuroadaptations, while allostatic changes in stress and reward systems underlie tolerance, withdrawal, and relapse liability.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary mood or anxiety disorder driving or mimicking substance-related symptoms","Psychotic disorder versus substance-induced psychosis","Delirium or other cognitive disorder from intoxication or withdrawal","Other behavioural addictions","Non-adherence or physiological dependence on prescribed medication without a use disorder","Underlying medical illness producing similar symptoms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Validated screening instruments and a detailed substance use history","Urine or serum toxicology as appropriate","Liver function tests and other markers of organ damage","Screening for blood-borne viruses in injecting drug use","Assessment of withdrawal severity using standardised scales","Screening for comorbid psychiatric disorders and suicide risk"],"redFlags":["Signs of severe or complicated withdrawal such as seizures, autonomic instability, or delirium tremens","Overdose or respiratory depression requiring urgent intervention","Suicidal ideation or intent, which is elevated in substance use disorders","Injecting practices with risk of sepsis, endocarditis, or blood-borne infection"],"prognosis":"Substance use disorders are chronic, relapsing conditions, but many people achieve sustained remission, particularly with sustained treatment engagement and social support. Prognosis is worse with early onset, severe dependence, polysubstance use, untreated psychiatric comorbidity, and limited social stability, and better with strong recovery support and continuity of care.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling appropriate physiological dependence on prescribed medication as a use disorder","Underestimating the danger of alcohol or sedative withdrawal","Treating the substance disorder while ignoring comorbid psychiatric illness","A judgemental stance that undermines engagement and honest disclosure","Overlooking elevated suicide and overdose risk"],"management":["Combine psychosocial interventions — motivational interviewing, cognitive behavioural therapy, contingency management and mutual-support groups — as the foundation of care","Offer substance-specific pharmacotherapy: opioid agonist substitution with methadone or buprenorphine, or naltrexone; acamprosate, naltrexone or disulfiram for alcohol; and nicotine replacement, varenicline or bupropion for tobacco","Manage withdrawal safely with monitored detoxification, using benzodiazepines and thiamine for alcohol withdrawal","Provide harm-reduction measures including take-home naloxone, needle and syringe provision, and blood-borne virus screening and vaccination","Identify and treat comorbid psychiatric disorders and assess suicide risk","Emphasise relapse prevention, long-term treatment engagement and social support","Escalate urgently for overdose, severe withdrawal or acute suicide risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"subungual_exostosis":{"name":"Subungual exostosis","system":"Dermatology / Nail","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Dupuytren exostosis","subungual osteochondroma","subungual bony outgrowth"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Subungual exostosis is a benign bony outgrowth arising from the dorsal aspect of the distal phalanx, most often on the great toe","It classically presents in adolescents and young adults as a firm, tender, slowly enlarging swelling that lifts or distorts the nail","Pain is common, particularly with pressure from footwear, and the overlying nail may be elevated or destroyed","A history of trauma or chronic irritation is frequently reported, and the lesion is not a true nail tumour but a bone lesion presenting through the nail","A lateral radiograph of the toe is the key investigation and shows a bony projection continuous with the phalanx, capped by fibrocartilage","It is benign but is often mistaken for a wart, pyogenic granuloma, or ingrowing toenail, delaying correct diagnosis","Definitive treatment is surgical excision of the bony outgrowth, and incomplete removal may be followed by recurrence"],"pathophysiology":"Subungual exostosis is an outgrowth of mature trabecular bone capped by fibrocartilage that projects from the dorsal surface of the distal phalanx, usually of the hallux, into the space beneath or beside the nail. It is generally regarded as a reactive proliferation, often linked to previous trauma or chronic irritation, rather than an inherited skeletal disorder, and it differs from a true osteochondroma in its cartilage cap and clinical setting. As the bony mass enlarges it pushes upward against the nail bed and plate, causing elevation, distortion, or partial destruction of the nail and producing pain when compressed by shoes.","additionalDifferentials":["Viral wart (verruca) of the nail unit","Pyogenic granuloma","Ingrowing toenail with granulation tissue","Subungual osteochondroma or other bony lesion","Glomus tumour","Amelanotic subungual melanoma","Subungual keratoacanthoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lateral and oblique radiographs of the affected toe to demonstrate the bony outgrowth","Clinical inspection for nail elevation, distortion, or destruction","Onychoscopy to assess the surface and vascular pattern of the lesion","Histological examination of the excised specimen to confirm the diagnosis","Assessment for a history of trauma or chronic pressure","Consideration of cross-sectional imaging for atypical or recurrent lesions"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, ulceration, or bleeding raising concern for a malignant bone or soft-tissue lesion","Atypical radiographic features suggesting a more aggressive process","Recurrence after excision indicating incomplete removal","Persistent pain with destruction of the nail unit requiring surgical assessment"],"prognosis":"Subungual exostosis is benign and is cured by complete surgical excision, after which the nail usually regrows, although incomplete removal can lead to recurrence and some residual nail deformity may persist.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the lesion as a wart or pyogenic granuloma and treating it destructively without imaging","Omitting a radiograph, which is essential to reveal the bony origin","Incomplete excision of the bony base, leading to recurrence","Failing to send tissue for histology to exclude a malignant mimic"],"management":["Complete surgical excision of the bony outgrowth, including its fibrocartilage cap, is curative and the definitive treatment","Avulse or partially remove the overlying nail plate to access the lesion and curette the base to minimise recurrence","Confirm the diagnosis histologically on the excised specimen","Provide analgesia and advise roomy, pressure-relieving footwear for symptom control before and after surgery","Follow up for recurrence, which follows incomplete removal, counselling that the nail usually regrows though some deformity may persist","Refer for dermatological or podiatric surgery with a planning radiograph of the toe"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"subungual_haematoma":{"name":"Subungual haematoma","system":"Skin / Hair and nails","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Subungual haemorrhage","Bleeding under the nail"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A collection of blood beneath the nail plate, usually following acute crush or stubbing trauma, or repetitive microtrauma from ill-fitting footwear in runners and hikers.","Acute lesions are intensely painful because of pressure within the closed subungual space; chronic or repetitive ones may be painless.","Colour evolves over time from red-purple to blue-black and finally brown, and the pigment characteristically migrates distally with nail growth, moving away from the proximal fold.","Dermoscopy typically shows a homogeneous red-black globular or streaked pattern with peripheral fading and sometimes tiny blood spots, without the parallel pigment network of melanocytic lesions.","Acute painful haematomas involving a large fraction of the nail benefit from trephination (nail plate perforation) to release pressure and relieve pain.","A key clinical rule is that a pigmented subungual lesion that does not move distally over weeks, or that is associated with nail dystrophy or periungual pigment, warrants biopsy to exclude melanoma.","Associated finger or toe fractures, particularly distal phalangeal tuft fractures, should be considered and imaged when the mechanism is significant.","Complete resolution follows the pace of nail growth, which is slower in toenails than fingernails."],"pathophysiology":"Trauma to the nail apparatus ruptures the rich vascular network of the nail bed and matrix, and extravasated blood becomes trapped between the firmly adherent nail plate and the underlying bed. Because this compartment is relatively non-distensible, even a small volume of blood raises pressure sharply and stimulates nociceptors, producing the throbbing pain of an acute lesion. Haemoglobin breakdown accounts for the evolving colour changes. As the nail plate grows outward from the matrix, the adherent pigmented blood is carried distally, which is the basis of the diagnostically useful distal migration. Repetitive low-grade trauma, as in athletes, produces recurrent painless haemorrhage often affecting several toenails symmetrically.","additionalDifferentials":["Subungual melanoma","Melanocytic naevus of the nail matrix","Squamous cell carcinoma of the nail unit","Glomus tumour (painful lesions)","Subungual exostosis","Pseudomonas or fungal pigmentation","Splinter haemorrhages from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dermoscopy of the nail plate to characterise the pigment pattern and margins","Radiograph of the digit to exclude underlying phalangeal fracture","Serial review to confirm distal migration of the pigment with nail growth","Nail plate paring or clipping to assess whether pigment is blood or melanin","Nail matrix biopsy if melanoma cannot be excluded","Assessment of footwear and activity in recurrent toenail lesions"],"redFlags":["A pigmented band or lesion that fails to migrate distally over several weeks","Hutchinson sign with pigment extending onto the proximal or lateral nail fold","Subungual pigmentation without a convincing history of trauma, especially in a single digit","Associated nail plate dystrophy, ulceration or bleeding suggesting malignancy","Significant crush injury with suspected open fracture or nail bed laceration"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated subungual haematoma resolves completely as the nail grows out, though large matrix injuries can leave permanent nail dystrophy. The main clinical hazard is misattributing a subungual melanoma to trauma and delaying diagnosis.","pitfalls":["Reflexively attributing all subungual pigment to trauma and missing a melanoma","Failing to image a significant crush injury and overlooking a distal phalangeal fracture or nail bed laceration","Delaying trephination in a painful acute lesion when prompt drainage would relieve symptoms","Not arranging follow-up to confirm distal migration of the pigment"],"management":["Relieve pain in an acute haematoma involving a large portion of the nail by trephination to decompress the trapped blood","Provide simple analgesia and elevation for smaller or settling lesions","Radiograph after significant trauma to exclude a distal phalangeal fracture","For extensive nail bed laceration or an associated displaced fracture, remove the nail plate and repair the nail bed, with tetanus prophylaxis for open injury","Confirm resolution by observing distal migration of the pigment with nail growth","Biopsy the nail matrix if pigment fails to migrate or a subungual melanoma cannot be excluded","Advise correctly fitting footwear and activity modification for recurrent runner's toenail lesions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"subungual_hematoma":{"name":"Subungual haematoma","system":"Dermatology / Nail","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subungual hematoma","subungual haemorrhage","blood under the nail"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A subungual haematoma is a collection of blood beneath the nail plate, usually following acute trauma such as a crush injury or repetitive pressure","Acute lesions cause throbbing pain from pressure of the trapped blood, which appears as a red-black discolouration under the plate","Painful acute haematomas can be relieved by trephination (making a small hole in the nail) to drain the blood","Over time the pigment moves distally with nail growth and can be seen to advance, unlike a fixed melanocytic band","The main diagnostic concern is distinguishing a haematoma from a subungual melanoma, particularly when there is no clear history of trauma","Onychoscopy typically shows a homogeneous reddish-black colour with a globular or streaky periphery and, often, a filamentous distal edge, helping separate it from melanoma","A significant crush injury may be associated with an underlying phalangeal fracture or nail bed laceration, which should be assessed and managed appropriately"],"pathophysiology":"A subungual haematoma forms when trauma ruptures the vessels of the nail bed or matrix and blood accumulates in the confined space between the plate and the bed. The rigid nail plate prevents the blood from escaping, so pressure builds and causes the characteristic throbbing pain of an acute lesion. As the nail grows the trapped pigment is carried distally and eventually grows out, which distinguishes it from melanin deposited continuously by matrix melanocytes. A forceful crush mechanism can simultaneously fracture the underlying distal phalanx or lacerate the nail bed, so the haematoma may be a marker of deeper injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Subungual melanoma","Longitudinal melanonychia from a benign source","Subungual glomus tumour","Pseudomonas or fungal pigmentation","Junctional or other subungual naevus","Exogenous staining of the nail"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["History of trauma and the time course of the discolouration","Onychoscopy to identify features favouring haematoma over melanoma","Serial observation to confirm distal migration of pigment with nail growth","Radiograph after significant trauma to exclude an underlying phalangeal fracture","Assessment of the nail bed for laceration in acute crush injuries","Nail matrix or bed biopsy if pigment persists or melanoma cannot be excluded"],"redFlags":["Pigmented subungual lesion without a convincing history of trauma, raising concern for melanoma","Pigment that fails to migrate distally or grow out over time","Associated Hutchinson sign or nail dystrophy suggesting malignancy","Significant crush injury with suspected phalangeal fracture or extensive nail bed laceration","Signs of secondary infection following trauma or trephination"],"prognosis":"A simple traumatic subungual haematoma resolves as it grows out with the nail and has an excellent outcome, but a mistaken diagnosis that overlooks a subungual melanoma can have serious consequences, so persistent or unexplained pigment must be pursued.","pitfalls":["Attributing a pigmented lesion to trauma without a clear history and missing a melanoma","Failing to confirm distal migration of the pigment over follow-up","Overlooking an underlying fracture or nail bed laceration after a crush injury","Not offering trephination for a painful acute haematoma when it would relieve pressure"],"management":["Decompress a painful acute haematoma involving a large fraction of the nail by trephination to release the trapped blood","Offer simple analgesia and elevation for smaller or resolving lesions","Obtain a radiograph after significant crush trauma to exclude an underlying distal phalangeal fracture","Remove the nail plate and repair the nail bed where there is extensive laceration or a displaced fracture, giving tetanus prophylaxis for open wounds","Confirm a benign course by observing distal migration of pigment as the nail grows out","Perform nail matrix or bed biopsy if the pigment persists or melanoma cannot be excluded","Recommend well-fitting footwear and activity modification for recurrent traumatic toenail lesions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"succinate_dehydrogenase_deficient_gist":{"name":"Succinate Dehydrogenase-Deficient Gastrointestinal Stromal Tumour","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SDH-deficient GIST","Paediatric-type GIST","KIT/PDGFRA wild-type GIST (SDH-deficient)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Gastrointestinal oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distinct subset of gastrointestinal stromal tumours lacking KIT and PDGFRA mutations ('wild-type') and defined by loss of SDHB immunostaining.","Almost always gastric, typically multifocal and multinodular with a plexiform architecture and epithelioid or mixed cytology.","Shows a predilection for children, adolescents and young adults, with a female predominance, and was historically termed the 'paediatric GIST'.","May metastasise to lymph nodes, which is exceptional for conventional GIST, yet often follows an indolent course even in the presence of metastases.","Occurs sporadically, within the Carney triad (GIST, paraganglioma, pulmonary chondroma) and within the Carney–Stratakis dyad (GIST plus paraganglioma).","Germline SDHA/B/C/D variants underlie Carney–Stratakis and many apparently sporadic cases, whereas the Carney triad is usually driven by epigenetic SDHC promoter hypermethylation.","Responds poorly to imatinib because there is no KIT or PDGFRA target, so management centres on surgery with careful long-term follow-up.","Loss of SDHA staining in addition to SDHB specifically points to an underlying SDHA mutation."],"pathophysiology":"These tumours arise from the interstitial cells of Cajal but, unlike conventional gastrointestinal stromal tumours, are not driven by activating KIT or PDGFRA mutations. Instead they show deficiency of the succinate dehydrogenase complex, arising through germline SDHA/B/C/D mutation, epigenetic silencing of SDHC by promoter hypermethylation, or biallelic somatic events. Whichever mechanism operates, the complex is destabilised and SDHB protein is lost on immunohistochemistry. Resulting succinate accumulation inhibits alpha-ketoglutarate–dependent dioxygenases, stabilises hypoxia-inducible factors and produces a pseudohypoxic, pro-angiogenic and globally hypermethylated state that drives tumourigenesis independent of receptor tyrosine kinase signalling. This distinct biology underlies the gastric multifocality, the propensity for nodal spread and the relative resistance to tyrosine kinase inhibitors that characterise the group.","additionalDifferentials":["Conventional KIT-mutant gastrointestinal stromal tumour","PDGFRA-mutant gastrointestinal stromal tumour","NF1-associated gastrointestinal stromal tumour","Gastric leiomyoma or leiomyosarcoma","Gastric schwannoma","Gastric neuroendocrine tumour","Other Carney triad components such as paraganglioma or pulmonary chondroma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SDHB immunohistochemistry, with SDHA staining, on tumour tissue","KIT and PDGFRA mutation analysis to confirm wild-type status","Germline SDHx genetic testing with referral to clinical genetics","Endoscopy with endoscopic ultrasound and biopsy for gastric lesions","Cross-sectional staging imaging of the abdomen and pelvis with assessment for paraganglioma","Plasma or urinary metanephrines to screen for an associated paraganglioma or phaeochromocytoma","Chest imaging for pulmonary chondroma when the Carney triad is suspected"],"redFlags":["Gastrointestinal haemorrhage or symptomatic anaemia from an ulcerated gastric tumour","Multifocal gastric masses with regional lymphadenopathy in a young patient","Concurrent catecholamine symptoms suggesting an associated paraganglioma","Rapid tumour growth or new hepatic metastases indicating disease progression"],"prognosis":"SDH-deficient GIST typically behaves indolently, and patients may survive for many years even with nodal or hepatic metastases, although disease is often persistent and recurrent. Complete surgical resection is the mainstay because response to tyrosine kinase inhibitors is limited, so prolonged surveillance is essential.","pitfalls":["Applying conventional GIST risk-stratification based on size and mitotic count, which does not reliably predict behaviour in SDH-deficient tumours.","Prescribing imatinib as for typical GIST, when SDH-deficient tumours are largely unresponsive.","Failing to test for germline SDHx variants and to screen for paraganglioma, phaeochromocytoma and pulmonary chondroma.","Interpreting nodal metastasis as evidence of an aggressive conventional sarcoma and over-treating accordingly."],"management":["Complete surgical resection is the mainstay, as these tumours respond poorly to imatinib","For advanced or unresectable disease consider VEGFR-directed tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as sunitinib or regorafenib, recognising their limited efficacy","Adopt an indolent-disease approach with active surveillance for small asymptomatic or stable metastatic lesions, avoiding over-treatment","Confirm SDH deficiency with SDHB immunohistochemistry and arrange germline SDHx testing with clinical genetics referral and family screening","Screen for associated paraganglioma and phaeochromocytoma with plasma or urinary metanephrines and cross-sectional imaging, given the Carney triad and Carney-Stratakis associations","Undertake long-term imaging surveillance for the characteristic late nodal and hepatic recurrence","Manage within a specialist sarcoma and gastrointestinal stromal tumour multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Gastrointestinal and soft tissue pathology references","Hereditary endocrine tumour clinical guidelines","Oncology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"succinic_semialdehyde_dehydrogenase_deficiency":{"name":"Succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase deficiency (GABA metabolism)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase deficiency","SSADH deficiency","ALDH5A1 deficiency","4-hydroxybutyric aciduria","gamma-hydroxybutyric aciduria","GHB aciduria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by deficiency of succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH5A1), the second enzyme of GABA degradation, so succinic semialdehyde is diverted to gamma-hydroxybutyric acid (GHB)","The diagnostic biochemical hallmark is accumulation of 4-hydroxybutyric acid (gamma-hydroxybutyrate) in urine, plasma, and CSF, which is not detected on routine organic acid screening unless specifically sought","Typical features are global developmental delay, prominent expressive language delay, hypotonia, ataxia, and behavioural/psychiatric problems, with epilepsy in many","Both GABA and GHB accumulate, and the balance of GABA-B and GHB receptor effects is thought to explain the mixed excitatory and sedative neurological picture","Brain MRI often shows a characteristic symmetric high T2 signal in the globus pallidus, subthalamic nuclei, and dentate nuclei","It is generally a slowly progressive or static encephalopathy rather than an acute metabolic crisis illness; management is supportive and antiepileptic, with vigabatrin used cautiously given theoretical benefit and risk","Inheritance is autosomal recessive","British spelling and paediatric context apply"],"pathophysiology":"The inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA is catabolised via GABA transaminase to succinic semialdehyde, which succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase then oxidises to succinate for entry into the citric acid cycle. When this enzyme is deficient, succinic semialdehyde is instead reduced to gamma-hydroxybutyrate (GHB), which accumulates along with GABA in the brain and body fluids. GHB and elevated GABA act on GHB and GABA-B receptors, disturbing neurotransmission in ways that produce the developmental, motor, epileptic, and behavioural manifestations. The build-up of these neuroactive compounds, rather than an energy-failure crisis, characterises the disorder, giving a comparatively static or slowly evolving encephalopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of global developmental delay with prominent language impairment","Autism spectrum disorder with regression","Other neurotransmitter metabolism disorders","Mitochondrial disorders with bilateral pallidal signal change","Other genetic epileptic encephalopathies","GHB ingestion or intoxication (acquired) as a mimic of the biochemistry"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine organic acids with specific detection of 4-hydroxybutyric acid (may be missed on standard screens)","Quantification of gamma-hydroxybutyrate in plasma and CSF","Brain MRI for symmetric globus pallidus, subthalamic, and dentate signal change","EEG in those with seizures","Enzyme activity assay in leucocytes or lymphocytes","Molecular analysis of the ALDH5A1 gene"],"redFlags":["Worsening seizures or status epilepticus requiring urgent management","Significant neuropsychiatric deterioration or behavioural crisis","Regression or unexpected rapid decline prompting reassessment","Suspected GHB intoxication needing differentiation from the inherited disorder"],"prognosis":"The course is usually that of a static or slowly progressive encephalopathy with intellectual disability, language impairment, and variable epilepsy; many survive into adulthood. Outcome varies, and behavioural and psychiatric symptoms can be prominent later.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis because 4-hydroxybutyrate is not reliably seen on routine organic acid analysis","Overlooking the characteristic MRI pattern","Confusing the biochemical finding with exogenous GHB exposure","Using antiepileptic agents without regard to the disturbed GABA metabolism"],"management":["Manage epilepsy with antiepileptic drugs, avoiding valproate which may further inhibit residual enzyme activity","Consider cautious vigabatrin to lower gamma-hydroxybutyrate, balanced against its risk of visual field loss","Treat behavioural and psychiatric symptoms with appropriate psychotropic agents alongside behavioural support","Provide developmental support with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language therapy for delay, hypotonia and ataxia","Monitor with EEG for seizures and, when vigabatrin is used, regular ophthalmological visual field assessment","Offer genetic counselling and family testing given autosomal recessive inheritance of ALDH5A1 variants","Coordinate care through a metabolic medicine and neurology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase deficiency and GABA metabolism","Nyhan, Atlas of Inherited Metabolic Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"succinyl_coa_ketoacid_transferase_deficiency":{"name":"Succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase (SCOT) deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCOT deficiency","OXCT1 deficiency","3-oxoacid CoA transferase deficiency","succinyl-CoA:3-oxoacid CoA transferase deficiency","ketolysis defect"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive deficiency of SCOT (OXCT1 gene), the enzyme that activates acetoacetate for peripheral utilisation, is the classic pure ketolysis defect","Presents with recurrent, often severe ketoacidotic crises, characteristically beginning in the neonatal period or infancy and precipitated by fasting or infection","A hallmark is persistent ketosis even between crises and in the fed state, because ketone bodies cannot be used by peripheral tissues","Unlike beta-ketothiolase deficiency, urine organic acids show only the ketone bodies (large amounts of acetoacetate and 3-hydroxybutyrate) without the abnormal isoleucine metabolites","The acylcarnitine profile is typically normal, so the diagnosis is easily overlooked on newborn screening","Acute treatment reverses catabolism with intravenous glucose and corrects acidosis; long-term management relies on strict avoidance of fasting with an emergency illness regimen","Confirmation is by OXCT1 molecular testing or enzyme assay in fibroblasts, as biochemical markers can be subtle"],"pathophysiology":"SCOT (succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase) catalyses the rate-limiting first step of ketone-body utilisation in extrahepatic tissues, transferring coenzyme A from succinyl-CoA to acetoacetate to form acetoacetyl-CoA. When the enzyme is deficient, peripheral tissues cannot use ketone bodies for energy, so ketones accumulate and are not cleared even when glucose is available. This produces persistent ketosis and a tendency to severe, life-threatening ketoacidosis during any period of increased ketogenesis, such as fasting or intercurrent illness. Because only ketone bodies accumulate, the organic acid and acylcarnitine profiles lack the specific markers seen in the isoleucine-pathway ketolysis defect.","additionalDifferentials":["Beta-ketothiolase deficiency (T2/ACAT1)","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Ketotic hypoglycaemia and glycogen storage disorders","Organic acidaemias presenting with ketoacidosis","Sepsis with lactic and ketoacidosis","Salicylate poisoning"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and urine ketone quantification demonstrating persistent ketosis in the fed state","Blood gas, glucose, electrolytes and anion gap during acute crises","Urine organic acids showing marked ketonuria without abnormal isoleucine metabolites","Plasma acylcarnitine profile (often normal, helping distinguish from other defects)","OXCT1 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","SCOT enzyme activity assay in cultured fibroblasts where genetics are inconclusive"],"redFlags":["Neonatal or infant presentation with severe high-anion-gap ketoacidosis","Persistent ketosis that does not resolve with feeding","Rapid deterioration with obtundation or cardiorespiratory compromise during a crisis","Recurrent unexplained severe ketoacidotic episodes"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition and rigorous avoidance of fasting, many individuals do well and crises become less frequent with age, but early or severe ketoacidotic episodes can be fatal or cause neurological injury before diagnosis.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis because newborn screening and acylcarnitine profiles are typically normal","Interpreting persistent ketosis as dietary or presuming diabetes rather than a ketolysis defect","Allowing prolonged fasting for procedures without a protective glucose regimen","Assuming normal interictal biochemistry excludes the disorder when persistent low-grade ketosis is actually a clue"],"management":["Treat acute ketoacidotic crises with intravenous glucose to switch off ketogenesis and reverse catabolism, correcting acidosis and electrolyte disturbance","Prevent crises by strictly avoiding fasting, ensuring regular carbohydrate-containing feeds and frequent meals","Institute an emergency illness regimen with additional oral or intravenous glucose during intercurrent illness or reduced intake","Monitor blood and urine ketones, blood gases and anion gap, particularly during illness","Provide a written emergency management plan and educate the family to seek early treatment when unwell","Offer genetic counselling and family testing for autosomal recessive OXCT1 variants","Coordinate care through a specialist metabolic medicine team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: Disorders of Ketone Body Metabolism","Saudubray, Baumgartner & Walter, Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"succinyl_coa_synthetase_deficiency":{"name":"Succinyl-CoA synthetase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SUCLA2 deficiency","SUCLG1 deficiency","Succinate-CoA ligase deficiency","mtDNA depletion syndrome with methylmalonic aciduria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mitochondrial medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A deficiency of the tricarboxylic-acid-cycle enzyme succinyl-CoA synthetase (succinate-CoA ligase) arising from variants in SUCLA2 (a beta subunit) or SUCLG1 (the alpha subunit); it behaves as a mitochondrial DNA depletion syndrome.","SUCLA2 disease is an encephalomyopathic mtDNA depletion syndrome that typically begins in infancy with hypotonia, muscle atrophy, dystonia or another movement disorder, sensorineural hearing loss and feeding difficulty.","SUCLG1 disease tends to be more severe and earlier in onset — the alpha subunit is shared by both the ATP- and GTP-forming isoenzymes — often with fatal neonatal lactic acidosis and sometimes hepatic involvement.","A characteristic biochemical clue is mild-to-moderate methylmalonic aciduria with raised methylcitrate, plus elevated succinylcarnitine (a C4-dicarboxylylcarnitine) on the acylcarnitine profile, which helps separate it from classical methylmalonic acidaemia.","Lactic acidosis and Leigh-like basal ganglia signal change on MRI are common findings.","Depletion of mitochondrial DNA is demonstrable in skeletal muscle.","The methylmalonic aciduria is not cobalamin-responsive, distinguishing it from vitamin B12 (cobalamin) metabolic disorders.","Management is supportive and multidisciplinary; no curative therapy is available."],"pathophysiology":"Succinyl-CoA synthetase (succinate-CoA ligase) is a tricarboxylic-acid-cycle enzyme that reversibly converts succinyl-CoA to succinate with substrate-level phosphorylation of ADP or GDP. Its alpha subunit, encoded by SUCLG1, partners either with the ATP-forming beta subunit encoded by SUCLA2 or with the GTP-forming beta subunit encoded by SUCLG2, so loss of the shared alpha subunit tends to be more severe than loss of a single beta subunit. The enzyme associates with mitochondrial nucleoside diphosphate kinase to maintain the matrix nucleotide pool needed for mtDNA replication, and deficiency therefore causes mitochondrial DNA depletion with multiple respiratory-chain enzyme deficiencies. Reduced succinyl-CoA turnover shifts the methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA equilibrium, producing the characteristic mild methylmalonic aciduria, raised methylcitrate and elevated succinylcarnitine. Inheritance is autosomal recessive.","additionalDifferentials":["Classical methylmalonic acidaemia (MUT and cobalamin defects) — with much higher methylmalonic acid and cobalamin-responsive forms","Other mtDNA depletion syndromes (POLG, DGUOK, MPV17, TK2, RRM2B)","Leigh syndrome of other genetic cause","Combined oxidative phosphorylation deficiencies","Primary lactic acidosis of other mitochondrial aetiology","The sibling genotype (SUCLA2 versus SUCLG1), which differ in severity and organ involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine organic acids showing mild methylmalonic aciduria and raised methylcitrate","Plasma acylcarnitines demonstrating elevated succinylcarnitine (C4-dicarboxylylcarnitine)","Blood and CSF lactate","Brain MRI for basal ganglia and Leigh-like changes","Muscle biopsy with mtDNA quantification and respiratory-chain enzymology","SUCLA2 and SUCLG1 sequencing or a mitochondrial gene panel","Audiological assessment for sensorineural hearing loss"],"redFlags":["Neonatal lactic acidosis with rapid deterioration, which favours SUCLG1 disease","Progressive dystonia, hypotonia and loss of milestones in infancy","New or evolving basal ganglia signal change on MRI","Sensorineural hearing loss accompanying a movement disorder"],"prognosis":"The condition is serious: SUCLG1 disease is frequently fatal in the neonatal period or infancy, whereas SUCLA2 disease often allows survival into childhood or beyond, albeit with significant disability.","pitfalls":["Misreading the mild methylmalonic aciduria as classical methylmalonic acidaemia and giving cobalamin, which is ineffective here.","Missing the diagnosis when the acylcarnitine profile, and specifically succinylcarnitine, is not scrutinised.","Attributing the lactic acidosis solely to acute intercurrent illness.","Failing to quantify mitochondrial DNA in muscle when a depletion syndrome is suspected."],"management":["Provide supportive mitochondrial care, as no disease-modifying therapy exists for this mtDNA depletion syndrome","Manage lactic acidosis with fluids and buffering during metabolic decompensation and minimise catabolic stress","Ensure nutritional support with nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding for feeding difficulty and poor growth","Treat dystonia and movement disorder with appropriate agents alongside physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy","Rehabilitate sensorineural hearing loss with hearing aids or cochlear implantation","Avoid mitochondrial toxins such as sodium valproate and aminoglycosides, and a cofactor supplement regimen may be trialled","Offer genetic counselling and family testing for autosomal recessive SUCLA2 or SUCLG1 variants, with monitoring of development, hearing and neuroimaging","Coordinate multidisciplinary care through metabolic and mitochondrial medicine specialists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mitochondrial disease reference texts","Inherited metabolic disease review literature","mtDNA depletion syndrome clinical guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"sucrase_isomaltase_deficiency":{"name":"Congenital sucrase-isomaltase deficiency","system":"Gastroenterology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SI","CSID","genetic sucrase-isomaltase deficiency","GSID"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disaccharidase defect caused by SI variants that impair the brush-border sucrase-isomaltase enzyme","Symptoms typically appear when sucrose and starch are introduced, with watery osmotic diarrhoea, bloating, abdominal pain and failure to thrive","The osmotic diarrhoea abates during fasting or a sucrose-free diet, distinguishing it from secretory diarrhoeas","Unabsorbed sugars are fermented by colonic bacteria, giving acidic, frothy stool with a low stool pH and reducing substances","Diagnosis is supported by low sucrase activity on duodenal disaccharidase assay of biopsy tissue and by sucrose or starch breath testing","Management is dietary restriction of sucrose and starch, with enzyme substitution therapy as an adjunct to broaden dietary tolerance","Symptoms often become milder with age as diet and intestinal capacity adjust"],"pathophysiology":"Congenital sucrase-isomaltase deficiency is an osmotic malabsorption disorder of the small-intestinal brush border. Variants in SI reduce or abolish the activity of the sucrase-isomaltase complex, which normally hydrolyses sucrose and the alpha-1,6 and some alpha-1,4 bonds of starch-derived oligosaccharides. Undigested disaccharides and oligosaccharides remain in the lumen, drawing water osmotically into the bowel and passing to the colon where bacterial fermentation produces gas and short-chain acids. This yields acidic, watery, gassy diarrhoea that resolves when the offending carbohydrates are withheld.","additionalDifferentials":["Glucose-galactose malabsorption","Congenital or adult-type hypolactasia (lactase deficiency)","Coeliac disease with secondary disaccharidase deficiency","Functional gastrointestinal disorders and irritable bowel syndrome","Cow's milk protein or other food intolerances","Giardiasis and post-infective disaccharidase deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duodenal biopsy with disaccharidase enzyme assay showing reduced sucrase activity","Sucrose or starch hydrogen/methane breath testing","Stool pH and reducing substances after a carbohydrate challenge","Molecular genetic testing for SI variants","Trial of sucrose and starch elimination with symptom monitoring"],"redFlags":["Significant dehydration during acute diarrhoeal episodes in infants","Failure to thrive from chronic carbohydrate malabsorption","Persistent symptoms despite dietary measures suggesting an alternative or additional diagnosis","Onset of new alarm features prompting evaluation for coeliac or other mucosal disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent with appropriate dietary management, and many patients tolerate increasing amounts of sucrose and starch with age. Growth and quality of life are normal once symptoms are controlled.","pitfalls":["Attributing chronic osmotic diarrhoea to a functional disorder and not testing disaccharidase activity","Overlooking hidden sucrose and starch in processed foods and medicines during dietary restriction","Missing coeliac disease, which can cause a secondary reduction in disaccharidase activity","Assuming a negative single breath test excludes the condition without correlating with biopsy or genetics"],"management":["Restrict dietary sucrose and starch as the mainstay of treatment, tailored with specialist dietetic guidance","Use enzyme substitution therapy with sacrosidase as an adjunct to broaden carbohydrate tolerance","Ensure nutritional adequacy and correct any failure to thrive while monitoring growth","Gradually reintroduce and liberalise sucrose and starch as tolerance improves with age","Confirm the diagnosis and guide the diet using duodenal disaccharidase assay and carbohydrate breath testing","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive SI variants and assess affected family members"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"sudden_infant_death_syndrome":{"name":"Sudden Infant Death Syndrome","system":"Respiratory / General","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SIDS","cot death","crib death"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as the sudden and unexpected death of an infant under one year that remains unexplained after a thorough death scene investigation, review of history, and complete postmortem examination.","It is a diagnosis of exclusion and forms part of the broader category of sudden unexpected infant death (SUID).","Incidence peaks between about 2 and 4 months of age, and most deaths occur during sleep.","Prone and side sleeping positions are major modifiable risk factors; supine sleep is strongly protective.","Additional risks include soft bedding, bed-sharing, overheating, maternal smoking during and after pregnancy, prematurity, and low birth weight.","Protective measures include breastfeeding, immunisation, pacifier use at sleep, and room-sharing without bed-sharing.","The prevailing model proposes a vulnerable infant meeting an exogenous stressor during a critical developmental period (the triple-risk hypothesis).","Safe-sleep counselling for every family is a core preventive intervention, and any death requires a compassionate, thorough investigation to exclude other causes."],"pathophysiology":"SIDS is thought to arise when an underlying vulnerability, an environmental stressor, and a critical developmental window coincide (the triple-risk model). The vulnerability is believed to involve abnormalities in brainstem regions that regulate arousal, cardiorespiratory control, and responses to hypoxia and hypercarbia, including serotonergic network dysfunction. When such an infant encounters a stressor, for example a prone position, rebreathing of expired air, or overheating during sleep, an impaired protective arousal and autonomic response may fail to correct hypoxia, leading to death.","additionalDifferentials":["Accidental asphyxia or unsafe sleep-related suffocation","Undiagnosed infection or sepsis","Inborn errors of metabolism (e.g. fatty acid oxidation defects)","Cardiac channelopathy or arrhythmia (e.g. long QT syndrome)","Non-accidental injury or inflicted harm","Occult congenital or structural abnormality"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full postmortem examination (autopsy)","Detailed death scene investigation and review of circumstances","Comprehensive clinical and family history review","Metabolic screening including postmortem samples where indicated","Genetic testing for cardiac channelopathies in selected cases","Microbiology and toxicology as part of the exclusion process"],"redFlags":["Modifiable risk factors that mandate active safe-sleep counselling for prevention","Non-supine sleep position, soft bedding, or bed-sharing","Exposure to tobacco smoke before or after birth","History or scene findings suggesting suffocation or non-accidental injury","Recurrent unexplained infant deaths in a family, warranting careful evaluation","Prematurity or low birth weight increasing baseline risk"],"prognosis":"SIDS is by definition fatal; the clinical focus is prevention through consistent safe-sleep practices, which have substantially reduced incidence, and supportive bereavement care for affected families.","pitfalls":["Assigning the label SIDS without a complete investigation that excludes other causes","Failing to deliver safe-sleep advice at every relevant contact","Overlooking an unsafe sleep environment as a contributing factor","Not considering metabolic or cardiac causes in apparently unexplained deaths","Neglecting sensitive support and follow-up for the bereaved family"],"management":["Prioritise prevention through safe-sleep advice: supine sleeping on a firm flat surface, avoiding soft bedding and overheating","Promote room-sharing without bed-sharing, breastfeeding, pacifier use at sleep and routine immunisation","Counsel against maternal smoking, alcohol and drug use during pregnancy and around the infant","Provide compassionate bereavement support and referral to specialist support organisations for the family","Complete a thorough investigation — autopsy, death-scene review and, in selected cases, genetic testing for cardiac channelopathies — to exclude other causes and inform family counselling","Offer enhanced support and reinforced safe-sleep guidance for subsequent infants, such as a care-of-next-infant programme"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"sudden_sensorineural_hearing_loss":{"name":"Sudden sensorineural hearing loss","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SSNHL","sudden deafness","idiopathic sudden sensorineural hearing loss"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as a rapid-onset sensorineural hearing loss developing over hours to a few days, most often unilateral","It is an otological emergency where prompt evaluation and treatment offer the best chance of hearing recovery","Patients often report a blocked ear, tinnitus and sometimes vertigo, and may notice the loss on waking","Tuning fork tests help confirm a sensorineural pattern: Weber lateralises to the better-hearing ear and Rinne remains positive","The majority of cases are idiopathic, but identifiable causes include infection, vascular events, trauma, ototoxic drugs and, importantly, vestibular schwannoma","Management principles centre on early corticosteroid therapy, given systemically or intratympanically, after excluding a conductive cause","MRI of the internal auditory meatus is important to exclude a retrocochlear lesion such as acoustic neuroma","Delay in diagnosis and treatment reduces the likelihood of hearing recovery, so urgent referral is essential"],"pathophysiology":"Sudden sensorineural hearing loss reflects acute dysfunction of the cochlea or auditory nerve. Although often idiopathic, proposed mechanisms include viral infection or inflammation of the labyrinth, microvascular compromise of the cochlear blood supply, rupture of intracochlear membranes, and autoimmune injury. These processes damage the delicate hair cells and neural elements or disturb the cochlear ionic and metabolic environment, producing abrupt hearing loss. A minority result from identifiable structural causes such as a vestibular schwannoma compressing the cochlear nerve, which is why imaging is required.","additionalDifferentials":["Impacted cerumen or other conductive causes (must be excluded)","Vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma)","Meniere's disease","Perilymph fistula","Ototoxicity from drugs","Central causes such as stroke affecting auditory pathways"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy and tuning fork tests to distinguish sensorineural from conductive loss","Pure tone audiometry to confirm and quantify the sensorineural loss","MRI of the internal auditory meatus and posterior fossa to exclude retrocochlear pathology","Blood tests for infective, inflammatory or autoimmune causes as indicated","Vestibular assessment when vertigo accompanies the hearing loss"],"redFlags":["Sudden unilateral hearing loss itself, which is an emergency requiring urgent assessment and treatment","Accompanying neurological signs or severe vertigo suggesting a central or serious cause","Progressive or fluctuating loss with tinnitus indicating possible vestibular schwannoma","Bilateral sudden loss suggesting a systemic, autoimmune or ototoxic process"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable; a substantial proportion recover partially or fully, particularly with early treatment, but severe loss, delayed presentation, vertigo and poor initial audiometry are associated with worse recovery.","pitfalls":["Dismissing sudden hearing loss as wax or a benign blocked ear and delaying treatment","Failing to distinguish sensorineural from conductive loss with tuning fork tests","Not arranging MRI to exclude a vestibular schwannoma","Missing the narrow window in which corticosteroid therapy is most effective"],"management":["Commence early systemic corticosteroid therapy once a conductive cause is excluded, as the mainstay to maximise the chance of hearing recovery","Offer intratympanic corticosteroid injection as primary treatment where systemic steroids are contraindicated, or as salvage after an incomplete response","Consider adjunctive hyperbaric oxygen therapy in selected patients presenting early","Withdraw any ototoxic drugs and treat identifiable causes, using antiviral therapy for infection and immunosuppression for suspected autoimmune inner-ear disease","Arrange MRI of the internal auditory meatus to exclude vestibular schwannoma and other retrocochlear pathology","Track recovery with serial pure-tone audiometry and provide vestibular rehabilitation where vertigo is present","Refer to ENT and audiology for hearing rehabilitation with hearing aids or cochlear implantation if loss persists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"sulfite_oxidase_deficiency":{"name":"Isolated sulfite oxidase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ISOD","SUOX deficiency","sulphite oxidase deficiency","isolated sulphite oxidase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder caused by variants in SUOX, encoding the molybdenum-dependent enzyme sulphite oxidase","The enzyme catalyses the final step of sulphur amino acid catabolism, converting sulphite to sulphate; its deficiency causes accumulation of neurotoxic sulphite and S-sulphocysteine","Unlike molybdenum cofactor deficiency, xanthine oxidase is unaffected, so plasma uric acid is normal, which is the key distinguishing feature","The severe neonatal form presents with intractable seizures, feeding difficulty, hypotonia evolving to spasticity and rapidly progressive encephalopathy","Ectopia lentis (dislocation of the ocular lens) is a characteristic later finding","A fresh-urine sulphite dipstick may be positive, and raised urinary S-sulphocysteine supports the diagnosis; plasma total homocysteine is often low","Milder, later-onset phenotypes exist with developmental delay, movement disorder and a more indolent course","There is no established disease-modifying treatment, and dietary restriction of sulphur amino acids has limited proven benefit"],"pathophysiology":"Sulphite oxidase is a mitochondrial molybdoenzyme that oxidises sulphite to sulphate, the terminal reaction in the degradation of the sulphur-containing amino acids cysteine and methionine. When SUOX is defective, sulphite accumulates and reacts with cystine to form S-sulphocysteine, a structural analogue of glutamate that is thought to act as an excitotoxin at NMDA receptors. Sulphite is also directly toxic and can disrupt cerebral connective tissue and lens zonules, explaining the lens dislocation. The result is severe neuronal injury, cystic white matter change and progressive cerebral atrophy in the classic neonatal form.","additionalDifferentials":["Molybdenum cofactor deficiency (biochemically similar but with low uric acid)","Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Neonatal epileptic encephalopathies of other causes","Non-ketotic hyperglycinaemia","Homocystinuria (another cause of ectopia lentis)","Vitamin-responsive neonatal epilepsies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fresh urine sulphite testing and quantitation of urinary S-sulphocysteine","Plasma uric acid, which is normal (distinguishing it from molybdenum cofactor deficiency)","Plasma total homocysteine and cystine, which tend to be low","Neuroimaging showing early oedema followed by cystic change and atrophy","Molecular genetic testing of SUOX","Ophthalmological assessment for lens dislocation"],"redFlags":["Intractable neonatal seizures with rapidly evolving encephalopathy","Imaging suggesting hypoxic-ischaemic injury without a perinatal history","Ectopia lentis in an infant with neurological impairment","Progressive spasticity and acquired microcephaly"],"prognosis":"The classic neonatal form carries a poor prognosis, with severe neurodevelopmental impairment and often early death. Later-onset, attenuated forms follow a more variable course but still cause significant neurological disability; effective disease-modifying therapy is currently lacking.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the presentation for hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Failing to distinguish it from molybdenum cofactor deficiency by not checking uric acid","Relying on stored urine for sulphite testing, as sulphite degrades quickly","Overlooking milder late-onset phenotypes"],"management":["Attempt dietary restriction of the sulphur-containing amino acids methionine and cysteine as a substrate-reduction strategy, recognising its limited efficacy","Control intractable seizures with anticonvulsant therapy while avoiding agents that may worsen the metabolic state","Provide supportive nutrition via nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding and manage evolving spasticity with antispasticity agents and physiotherapy","Refer for ophthalmological assessment and management of ectopia lentis","Minimise catabolic stress from intercurrent illness and fasting","Coordinate multidisciplinary metabolic, neurology and palliative care given the severe neurodevelopmental impairment","Offer genetic counselling with carrier and prenatal testing for this autosomal recessive SUOX disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"sump_syndrome":{"name":"Sump syndrome","system":"Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["post-choledochoduodenostomy sump syndrome","biliary sump syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Blumgart's Surgery of the Liver, Biliary Tract and Pancreas","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sump syndrome is a late complication of side-to-side choledochoduodenostomy in which the segment of common bile duct distal to the anastomosis becomes a poorly drained blind pouch, or sump, that accumulates food debris, biliary sludge, and stones","The stagnant distal duct collects material that cannot clear through the papilla, leading to recurrent cholangitis, biliary obstruction, pain, and occasionally pancreatitis or hepatic abscess","It is chiefly of historical and residual relevance because side-to-side choledochoduodenostomy is now performed less often, having been largely superseded by Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy and endoscopic therapy","Symptoms may appear months to years after the original biliary bypass, so a history of previous biliary-enteric anastomosis is a crucial diagnostic clue","ERCP is the mainstay of both diagnosis and treatment, allowing sphincterotomy and clearance of debris and stones from the distal duct sump","Cross-sectional imaging and MRCP demonstrate the anastomosis, the distal ductal reservoir, and any pneumobilia, stones, or abscess","Pneumobilia is expected after a biliary-enteric anastomosis and should not by itself be interpreted as infection or gas-forming pathology","Refractory or recurrent disease may require surgical revision, typically conversion to a Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy with closure of the distal duct"],"pathophysiology":"A side-to-side choledochoduodenostomy joins the common bile duct to the duodenum above the papilla, leaving the segment of duct between the anastomosis and the sphincter of Oddi as a dependent cul-de-sac. Because bile preferentially drains through the more proximal, wider anastomosis, this distal segment is no longer flushed by normal bile flow and loses its physiological clearance. Reflux of enteric contents introduces food particles, vegetable matter, and bacteria into the poorly emptying pouch, where stasis promotes accumulation of debris and biliary sludge, precipitation of stones, and bacterial overgrowth. The trapped material intermittently obstructs the papilla or the anastomosis and provokes recurrent ascending cholangitis; persistent stasis and infection can extend to cause pancreatitis when the pancreatic orifice is involved, or seed hepatic abscesses. The clinical picture therefore reflects chronic stagnation and infection within an anatomically dependent, non-draining distal duct rather than a primary stone or stricture elsewhere.","additionalDifferentials":["Anastomotic stricture of the choledochoduodenostomy","Recurrent or retained choledocholithiasis","Cholangitis from another cause of biliary obstruction","Ascending cholangitis related to sphincter of Oddi dysfunction","Recurrent pyogenic cholangitis","Pancreatitis of biliary or other aetiology","Hepatic abscess of biliary origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Liver biochemistry showing a cholestatic pattern during symptomatic episodes","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers when cholangitis is suspected","Abdominal ultrasound to assess the biliary tree and detect stones or abscess","MRCP to delineate the anastomosis, the distal duct sump, and any retained debris or stones","Contrast-enhanced CT to identify complications such as hepatic abscess or pancreatitis","ERCP for direct visualisation, sump clearance, and therapeutic sphincterotomy"],"redFlags":["Acute cholangitis with fever, rigors, jaundice, and haemodynamic compromise","Development of hepatic abscess or persistent sepsis despite antibiotics","Acute pancreatitis complicating the obstruction","Progressive jaundice suggesting anastomotic stricture or fixed obstruction","Recurrent severe episodes indicating need for surgical revision"],"prognosis":"Endoscopic sphincterotomy with clearance of the sump is effective in most patients and relieves recurrent cholangitis, giving a good outlook; recurrent or refractory disease that requires surgical conversion to a Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy also generally does well once the dependent distal segment is eliminated.","pitfalls":["Failing to elicit the history of a previous side-to-side choledochoduodenostomy and so missing the diagnosis","Misinterpreting expected post-anastomotic pneumobilia as evidence of infection or gas-forming disease","Attributing recurrent cholangitis to simple retained stones without recognising the underlying sump anatomy","Overlooking a hepatic abscess or pancreatitis as a complication of chronic biliary stasis","Persisting with repeated endoscopic clearance when definitive surgical revision is warranted"],"management":["Perform ERCP with endoscopic sphincterotomy and clearance of retained debris and stones from the distal duct sump as the mainstay of treatment","Treat episodes of ascending cholangitis promptly with broad-spectrum antibiotics covering enteric organisms alongside biliary drainage","Drain any complicating hepatic abscess and manage associated pancreatitis supportively","Reserve surgical conversion to a Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy for recurrent or endoscopically refractory disease","Monitor liver biochemistry and inflammatory markers during symptomatic episodes and surveil for recurrence","Refer to hepatobiliary surgery and gastroenterology for combined endoscopic and surgical planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Blumgart's Surgery of the Liver, Biliary Tract and Pancreas","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"sunct_syndrome":{"name":"SUNCT/SUNA","system":"Nervous system / Headache","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SUNCT","SUNA","short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache attacks","short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache attacks with conjunctival injection and tearing"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / International Classification of Sleep Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache attacks lasting seconds to a few minutes and recurring many times an hour","SUNCT requires both conjunctival injection and tearing; SUNA has only one of these, or neither","The attacks are among the most frequent of all headaches, sometimes dozens or more per hour","Cutaneous or movement triggers can provoke attacks, overlapping with trigeminal neuralgia, but the autonomic features are prominent and there is typically no refractory period","The disorder is poorly responsive to indometacin, which helps separate it from paroxysmal hemicrania","Lamotrigine is often the preferred preventive, and intravenous lidocaine can help settle severe bouts","MRI, including views of the posterior fossa and pituitary, is important because symptomatic cases occur, sometimes from neurovascular contact with the trigeminal nerve"],"pathophysiology":"SUNCT and SUNA are trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias in which very brief paroxysms arise from trigeminovascular activation coupled to a cranial parasympathetic reflex, producing the ipsilateral autonomic signs. Posterior hypothalamic activation is seen on functional imaging, and neurovascular compression of the trigeminal nerve is identified in a proportion of cases, giving clinical overlap with trigeminal neuralgia.","additionalDifferentials":["First-division trigeminal neuralgia","Paroxysmal hemicrania","Cluster headache","Primary stabbing headache","Symptomatic trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia from a posterior fossa or pituitary lesion","Trigeminal neuralgia with autonomic symptoms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed characterisation of attack duration, frequency, triggers and autonomic features","MRI brain with dedicated pituitary and posterior fossa imaging and vascular sequences","Assessment for neurovascular contact of the trigeminal nerve","Trial of indometacin to help exclude paroxysmal hemicrania","Review for cutaneous trigger zones and the presence or absence of a refractory period"],"redFlags":["Abnormal neurological signs or an underlying structural lesion on imaging","First presentation in older age or rapidly escalating attacks","Features suggesting raised intracranial pressure","Attacks that do not fit the expected very-short, very-frequent profile"],"prognosis":"The course is variable, ranging from single bouts to a chronic unremitting pattern; many patients improve with preventive drugs such as lamotrigine, and identifying a symptomatic cause can allow targeted treatment.","pitfalls":["Confusing the disorder with trigeminal neuralgia and missing the prominent autonomic features","Expecting an indometacin response as seen in paroxysmal hemicrania","Omitting MRI and overlooking a symptomatic lesion or neurovascular contact","Escalating opioids for what is a neuralgiform, opioid-unresponsive disorder"],"management":["Start lamotrigine as the preferred preventive to reduce attack frequency","Use alternative preventives such as topiramate, gabapentin or oxcarbazepine when lamotrigine is ineffective or not tolerated","Settle severe exacerbations with intravenous lidocaine as a transitional measure","Arrange MRI to identify a symptomatic cause and treat any pituitary or posterior fossa lesion found","Consider microvascular decompression where trigeminal neurovascular contact is demonstrated, or neuromodulation such as occipital nerve stimulation in refractory cases","Refer to a specialist headache service for diagnosis and management of refractory disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (ICHD-3)","The Headaches (Olesen et al.)"],"crossLinks":["paroxysmal_hemicrania","hemicrania_continua"]},"superficial_fibromatosis":{"name":"Superficial fibromatosis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Palmar fibromatosis (Dupuytren disease)","Plantar fibromatosis (Ledderhose disease)"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours / Green's Operative Hand Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign fibroblastic and myofibroblastic proliferation of the palmar (Dupuytren) or plantar (Ledderhose) aponeurosis","Palmar disease typically affects the ring and little finger rays, progressing from nodules to cords and ultimately a fixed flexion contracture","It is commonest in older men of Northern European ancestry and is associated with diabetes, alcohol, smoking, antiepileptic use, and manual labour","Plantar fibromatosis produces firm nodules in the arch of the foot but far less often leads to contracture than its palmar counterpart","A strong diathesis is suggested by early onset, bilateral disease, knuckle pads (Garrod pads), and coexistent plantar or penile (Peyronie) fibromatosis","Microscopically there are cellular fascicles of bland myofibroblasts, many showing nuclear beta-catenin expression","Early or non-contractile disease is managed conservatively, with needle aponeurotomy, collagenase injection, or fasciectomy reserved for functionally limiting contracture","Recurrence after intervention is common, reflecting the field nature of the disease"],"pathophysiology":"Superficial fibromatosis is a benign but locally persistent proliferation of myofibroblasts within the palmar or plantar aponeurosis. The process evolves through a proliferative phase of cellular nodules, an involutional phase in which myofibroblasts align and contract, and a residual phase of dense, relatively acellular collagen forming cords. Myofibroblast contraction, mediated by their smooth muscle-like contractile apparatus and by remodelling of the surrounding collagen, produces the progressive flexion deformity that characterises palmar disease. Dysregulated Wnt and beta-catenin signalling, together with profibrotic growth factors, is implicated in driving the proliferation, and many lesions show nuclear beta-catenin. A genetic predisposition interacts with metabolic and lifestyle factors such as diabetes, alcohol, and smoking, explaining the familial clustering and the recognised disease associations.","additionalDifferentials":["Desmoid-type (deep) fibromatosis","Stenosing tenosynovitis or trigger finger","Ganglion or tendon sheath cyst","Giant cell tumour of the tendon sheath","Callus, plantar fibroma mimics, or foreign body granuloma in the foot","Epithelioid sarcoma or other soft tissue malignancy in atypical cases"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination including the tabletop test for palmar contracture","Goniometric measurement of joint contracture to monitor progression","Ultrasound or MRI to define nodules and cords when the picture is atypical","Histology in resected specimens to confirm the diagnosis and exclude a deep fibromatosis or sarcoma","Assessment for associated knuckle pads and plantar or penile involvement","Screening for associated conditions such as diabetes where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, pain, or a deep or fixed mass suggesting deep fibromatosis or malignancy","Severe progressive contracture threatening hand function","Atypical location or appearance not fitting classic palmar or plantar disease","Recurrent or aggressive disease after multiple interventions"],"prognosis":"Superficial fibromatosis is benign and does not metastasise, but it is often progressive and recurrent, and palmar disease in particular can cause substantial functional disability from fixed contracture. Outcome after treatment depends on disease severity and the strength of the underlying diathesis, with recurrence being frequent.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a palmar or plantar nodule for a ganglion or a soft tissue sarcoma","Intervening too early before a functionally significant contracture has developed","Underestimating the high recurrence rate when counselling patients before surgery","Failing to recognise a strong diathesis and its implications for prognosis and recurrence"],"management":["Observe mild, non-progressive disease with periodic goniometric assessment, reserving intervention for functionally significant contracture","Treat progressive palmar (Dupuytren) contracture with percutaneous needle aponeurotomy, collagenase injection or surgical fasciectomy","Consider radiotherapy in early proliferative nodular disease to slow progression","Manage plantar (Ledderhose) disease with cushioning orthotics, padding and physiotherapy, reserving surgery for refractory cases","Provide postprocedural hand therapy and night splinting to maintain extension","Address associated factors such as diabetes, alcohol and smoking, and counsel on the high recurrence risk where a strong diathesis exists","Refer to a hand surgeon for assessment when contracture limits function"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"superficial_fungal_infections":{"name":"Superficial Fungal Infections","system":"Skin and Soft Tissue","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Dermatophytosis","Ringworm","Tinea","Pityriasis versicolor","Tinea versicolor","Onychomycosis","Mucocutaneous candidiasis"],"chapter":225,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1722","clinicalPearls":["Superficial fungal infections are caused by molds and yeasts that colonize the outer skin layers, hair follicles, and nails without invading deeper tissue; they are the most common human fungal infections but rarely cause serious disease.","Dermatophytes (Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton) are not part of normal skin flora and confine themselves to keratinized structures; symptoms arise from inflammatory reaction to fungal antigens rather than tissue invasion, and an estimated billion people are affected worldwide.","The annular, ring-shaped cutaneous lesion ('ringworm') results from centrifugal outward growth of the organism in the stratum corneum; the term tinea (Latin for worm) is preferred and is paired with the affected body site.","Tinea capitis predominantly affects children aged 3 to 7 years, producing well-demarcated scaly patches with hair shafts broken off near the skin surface and possible alopecia.","Tinea pedis typically begins in the toe web spaces with peeling, maceration, and pruritus, and is a recognized portal of entry for streptococci and staphylococci, predisposing to lower-extremity cellulitis through interdigital fissures.","Onychomycosis affects toenails more often than fingernails, is most common in those with tinea pedis, and shows thickened, discolored, crumbling nails with onycholysis; older age, vascular disease, diabetes, and nail trauma are risk factors.","Malassezia yeasts (notably M. furfur, M. pachydermatis) are lipophilic commensals of sebum-rich areas (back, chest, scalp, face) and cause tinea/pityriasis versicolor, folliculitis, and seborrheic dermatitis; cutaneous IL-17 signaling normally controls them.","Tinea versicolor appears as flat round scaly hypo- or hyperpigmented patches on the neck, chest, or upper arms, usually asymptomatic, and can be confused with vitiligo (which is non-scaly).","KOH (potassium hydroxide) preparation of lesion-edge scrapings demonstrating hyphae is the key bedside diagnostic test; Malassezia shows a characteristic mix of budding bottle-shaped yeasts and short septate hyphae.","Mucocutaneous candidiasis is most often caused by Candida albicans; oral thrush yields white mucosal plaques that scrape off, and antibiotic use, diabetes, and pregnancy are major risk factors for vulvovaginal candidiasis.","Candida auris is an emerging multidrug-resistant species that persistently colonizes human skin, hair follicles, healthcare environments, and devices, grows avidly in human sweat, and drives nosocomial outbreaks.","Seborrheic dermatitis (a Malassezia-associated condition) can be especially severe in patients with AIDS, and Malassezia fungemia, though rare, occurs almost exclusively in premature neonates receiving parenteral lipid nutrition."],"pathophysiology":"Superficial fungi inhabit only the keratinized superficial layers (stratum corneum, hair follicles, nail plate) and do not invade beneath the stratum corneum. Dermatophytes live within keratin and produce disease through host inflammatory responses to fungal antigens rather than by direct tissue invasion; nail involvement begins via the lateral or superficial nail plate then spreads, and hair-shaft invasion may be intra- or peri-shaft. Host defense is mediated by IL-17 responses, which restrict dermatophyte growth and control cutaneous Malassezia; infections are more frequent in males, and progesterone can inhibit dermatophyte growth. Malassezia organisms are lipophilic commensals of sebaceous areas that elicit little inflammation but, when IL-17 responses are excessive, can exacerbate atopic inflammation. Candida species are mucosal/cutaneous commensals; control depends on IL-17-producing lymphoid cells (TH17, gamma-delta T cells, innate lymphoid cells) at barrier sites, and defects in IL-17 signaling predispose to oral and chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis.","additionalDifferentials":["Contact dermatitis","Eczema","Psoriasis (can mimic both tinea corporis and onychomycosis)","Erythrasma","Intertriginous candidiasis","Vitiligo (for tinea versicolor; differentiated by absence of scale)","Bacterial folliculitis","Seborrheic dermatitis / dandruff","Leukoplakia (for oral thrush)"],"infectionMimics":["Intertriginous candidiasis (vs tinea cruris)","Bacterial folliculitis (vs Malassezia folliculitis)","Oral leukoplakia from other infections vs Candida thrush"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Psoriasis","Eczema","Contact dermatitis","Vitiligo","Seborrheic dermatitis","Mucosal leukoplakia"],"additionalInvestigations":["KOH (potassium hydroxide) microscopy of scrapings from the lesion edge to detect hyphae","Fungal culture, indicated when an outbreak is suspected or when there is no response to therapy","KOH preparation showing budding bottle-shaped yeasts with short septate hyphae for Malassezia","Culture of Malassezia requires sterile olive oil added to the medium for suspected disseminated infection","Skin biopsy or nail clippings with culture and microscopy for skin and nail candidiasis","Biopsy of oral/esophageal tissue to distinguish invasive thrush (yeasts, pseudohyphae, and hyphae invading epithelium) from mere colonization (superficial yeast forms only) and from mimics such as leukoplakia","Culture and microscopy of vaginal secretions (swab) for vulvovaginal candidiasis"],"redFlags":["Tinea pedis with interdigital fissuring predisposing to lower-extremity cellulitis from streptococcal/staphylococcal entry","Severe seborrheic dermatitis as a clue to AIDS / advanced immunosuppression","Malassezia fungemia/disseminated infection in premature neonates on parenteral lipid nutrition","Candida auris colonization as a nosocomial multidrug-resistant outbreak risk","Recurrent or extensive mucocutaneous candidiasis suggesting inborn errors of immunity (e.g., STAT1 gain-of-function, IL-17 signaling defects) or AIDS","Moderate-to-severe oral thrush or esophageal involvement requiring systemic evaluation"],"prognosis":"Superficial fungal infections rarely cause serious disease. Malassezia skin infections are benign and self-limited, though recurrences are common. Dermatophyte infections generally respond well to therapy, but onychomycosis is slow to clear and prone to relapse. The outcome of rare systemic Malassezia infection depends on the host's underlying condition, though most affected neonates do well.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing onychomycosis clinically without confirmation, since psoriasis can mimic nail fungal disease (usually with associated skin lesions)","Mistaking tinea versicolor for vitiligo; the absence of scale distinguishes vitiligo","Interpreting superficial yeast forms on oral/esophageal biopsy as invasive candidiasis when they actually represent colonization only","Overlooking tinea pedis as a portal for bacterial cellulitis","Not pursuing culture when a patient fails to respond to empiric therapy or when an outbreak is suspected"],"management":["Treat cutaneous dermatophytosis such as tinea corporis, cruris and pedis with topical allylamine or azole antifungals","Use systemic antifungal therapy with terbinafine or an azole for onychomycosis and for tinea capitis, where topical treatment alone is inadequate","Manage Malassezia infections such as pityriasis versicolor with topical ketoconazole or selenium sulphide, reserving oral azoles for extensive disease","Keep affected skin dry and treat interdigital maceration to prevent bacterial cellulitis entering through toe-web fissures","Monitor liver function during prolonged oral terbinafine or azole courses and obtain fungal culture where there is no response to therapy","Advise on footwear hygiene, treatment of reservoirs and measures to reduce recurrence and reinfection"],"severityClassification":"Clinically stratified by extent and site: localized cutaneous tinea versus extensive tinea and onychomycosis; for mucocutaneous candidiasis, mild oral thrush is distinguished from moderate/severe or esophageal disease that requires systemic management.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1722","Harrison 22e p.1723","Harrison 22e p.1724"],"crossLinks":[]},"superficial_siderosis":{"name":"Superficial siderosis of the CNS","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["superficial siderosis","infratentorial superficial siderosis","central nervous system superficial siderosis","haemosiderin deposition of the CNS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic clinical triad is progressive sensorineural hearing loss, cerebellar ataxia and, later, myelopathy, evolving slowly over years","Results from chronic, repeated low-grade bleeding into the subarachnoid space with deposition of haemosiderin on the pial surfaces of the brain, brainstem, cerebellum, cranial nerves and spinal cord","The eighth cranial nerve and cerebellum are especially vulnerable, explaining the early prominence of hearing loss and ataxia","Susceptibility-weighted or gradient-echo MRI shows a characteristic rim of low signal (haemosiderin) coating the surface of the neuraxis","A meticulous search for the bleeding source is essential and may reveal a dural defect, prior trauma or surgery, a vascular lesion or a tumour","Anosmia and cognitive change can accompany the core triad in some patients","The infratentorial (classic) form differs from cortical superficial siderosis, which is more typically associated with cerebral amyloid angiopathy"],"pathophysiology":"Superficial siderosis of the central nervous system arises from persistent or recurrent haemorrhage into the subarachnoid space, releasing blood that breaks down to deposit haemosiderin along the pial and subpial surfaces of the neuraxis. Iron-laden macrophages and free iron promote oxidative injury and the accumulation of ferritin and haemosiderin in tissues rich in Bergmann glia and microglia, which accounts for the particular vulnerability of the cerebellum and the eighth cranial nerve. Progressive neuronal and glial damage produces the characteristic sensorineural deafness, cerebellar ataxia and myelopathy. Identifying and treating the chronic source of bleeding, such as a dural tear, is central to halting progression.","additionalDifferentials":["Cerebellar degeneration of other causes","Vestibular schwannoma or other causes of sensorineural hearing loss","Multiple sclerosis or other causes of progressive ataxia and myelopathy","Cerebral amyloid angiopathy causing cortical superficial siderosis","Chronic meningitis with meningeal involvement","Spinocerebellar ataxias and other hereditary ataxias","Compressive or inflammatory myelopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the whole neuraxis with susceptibility-weighted or gradient-echo sequences to demonstrate haemosiderin deposition","Dedicated spinal imaging and CT myelography to identify a dural defect or cerebrospinal fluid leak","Audiometry to characterise sensorineural hearing loss","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis, which may show xanthochromia or evidence of prior bleeding","Vascular imaging to exclude an underlying vascular malformation or aneurysm","Imaging for an occult tumour as a potential bleeding source"],"redFlags":["Progressive myelopathy with gait and sphincter deterioration","An identifiable and potentially treatable bleeding source such as a dural defect","Rapidly worsening hearing loss threatening communication","Features suggesting an underlying tumour or vascular malformation"],"prognosis":"Superficial siderosis is usually slowly progressive, and neurological deficits such as hearing loss and ataxia are often irreversible; identifying and repairing the bleeding source may arrest further deterioration, so prognosis depends heavily on early recognition and treatment of the cause.","pitfalls":["Failing to search thoroughly for the chronic bleeding source, including a spinal dural defect","Confusing infratentorial superficial siderosis with cortical siderosis of cerebral amyloid angiopathy","Attributing hearing loss and ataxia to ageing or unrelated causes and missing the imaging signature","Assuming the condition is untreatable and not pursuing a correctable dural or vascular lesion"],"management":["Identify and surgically repair the chronic source of subarachnoid bleeding, such as a dural defect or cerebrospinal fluid leak, to arrest progression","Consider the CNS-penetrant iron chelator deferiprone to reduce haemosiderin deposition","Provide hearing rehabilitation with hearing aids, and cochlear implantation for advanced sensorineural deafness","Offer physiotherapy and vestibular rehabilitation for cerebellar ataxia and gait impairment","Monitor disease with serial susceptibility-weighted or gradient-echo MRI and audiometry","Coordinate care across neurology, neurosurgery, ENT and audiology, and renew the search for a bleeding source if deterioration continues"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"superficial_thrombophlebitis":{"name":"Superficial thrombophlebitis","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["superficial venous thrombosis","SVT","superficial vein thrombophlebitis","phlebitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a tender, warm, erythematous, palpable cord along the course of a superficial vein, most often in the lower limb","Commonly arises in varicose veins, but can also follow intravenous cannulation or occur in otherwise normal veins","It is frequently a benign, self-limiting process, yet it should not be dismissed because it can coexist with or extend into deep vein thrombosis","Duplex ultrasound is used to confirm the diagnosis, define clot extent, and assess proximity to the saphenofemoral or saphenopopliteal junctions and the deep system","Thrombus close to a deep junction or of substantial length carries a higher risk of propagation and venous thromboembolism, influencing whether anticoagulation is used","Management ranges from symptomatic measures for limited disease to a course of anticoagulation for extensive or junction-approaching thrombus","Migratory or recurrent superficial thrombophlebitis without varicosities should prompt consideration of an underlying thrombophilia or occult malignancy","Suppurative (septic) thrombophlebitis is a distinct infective complication requiring urgent treatment and is not the typical bland form"],"pathophysiology":"Superficial thrombophlebitis is thrombosis with accompanying inflammation of a superficial vein. The elements of Virchow's triad apply: venous stasis (notably in varicose veins), endothelial injury (for example from cannulation), and hypercoagulability. Thrombus formation within the superficial vein triggers a local inflammatory response producing the characteristic tender palpable cord. Because superficial and deep systems communicate through perforators and saphenous junctions, thrombus can propagate into the deep veins, linking the condition to venous thromboembolism.","additionalDifferentials":["Cellulitis","Deep vein thrombosis","Lymphangitis","Ruptured Baker cyst","Erythema nodosum","Contact dermatitis or insect bite reaction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Venous duplex ultrasound to confirm diagnosis, measure clot length, and assess junctional and deep involvement","Assessment for deep vein thrombosis when clinical suspicion exists","Thrombophilia evaluation in recurrent or unprovoked cases without varicosities","Consideration of malignancy screening in migratory or recurrent disease","Review of intravenous access sites when catheter-related"],"redFlags":["Thrombus extending toward or reaching the saphenofemoral or saphenopopliteal junction, with risk of deep extension and pulmonary embolism","Symptoms or signs of concurrent deep vein thrombosis","Fever, purulence, or systemic upset suggesting septic (suppurative) thrombophlebitis","Migratory or recurrent phlebitis raising concern for occult malignancy or thrombophilia"],"prognosis":"Localised superficial thrombophlebitis in varicose veins is usually self-limiting with a good outcome, whereas extensive disease or thrombus near a deep junction carries a meaningful risk of progression to deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism. Recurrence is common when underlying venous disease or a prothrombotic state persists.","pitfalls":["Assuming all superficial thrombophlebitis is benign and failing to assess deep or junctional extension","Mistaking it for cellulitis and treating with antibiotics alone","Overlooking an underlying malignancy or thrombophilia in migratory or recurrent cases","Not arranging duplex assessment when clot proximity to the deep system would change management"],"management":["Manage limited disease symptomatically with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, warm compresses, compression stockings and continued mobilisation","Give anticoagulation at prophylactic intensity, such as fondaparinux or low-molecular-weight heparin, for extensive thrombus or thrombus approaching the saphenofemoral or saphenopopliteal junction","Escalate to therapeutic anticoagulation when thrombus extends into the deep venous system, treating as for deep vein thrombosis","Remove the offending cannula when the process is catheter-related","Confirm extent and junctional proximity with duplex ultrasound and repeat imaging if there is clinical extension","Investigate for thrombophilia or occult malignancy in recurrent, migratory or unprovoked cases","Treat the underlying varicose veins once the acute episode has settled to reduce recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"superficial_vein_thrombosis":{"name":"superficial vein thrombosis","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["superficial thrombophlebitis","superficial venous thrombophlebitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Superficial vein thrombosis is thrombosis with associated inflammation of a superficial vein, most often affecting varicose tributaries of the great saphenous vein in the lower limb and presenting as a tender, erythematous, palpable cord","Once regarded as benign, it is now recognised to carry a meaningful risk of concurrent or subsequent deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism, particularly when extensive or lying close to the saphenofemoral or saphenopopliteal junctions","Whole-leg compression ultrasound is advised to define thrombus extent, measure the distance to the deep venous junction and exclude coexisting deep vein thrombosis, which is present in a notable minority at diagnosis","Anticoagulation at prophylactic intensity for a defined period (for example fondaparinux or low-molecular-weight heparin) is recommended for thrombosis of significant length lying away from the junction, whereas thrombus close to or extending into the deep system warrants therapeutic anticoagulation as for deep vein thrombosis","Migratory or recurrent thrombophlebitis affecting otherwise normal veins (thrombophlebitis migrans, the Trousseau sign) should prompt evaluation for occult malignancy or thromboangiitis obliterans","Mondor disease denotes superficial vein thrombosis of the thoracoabdominal or breast wall veins; it is usually self-limiting, but breast wall involvement warrants exclusion of an underlying carcinoma","Upper limb superficial vein thrombosis is most often related to intravenous cannulae or infusions, and a suppurative (septic) thrombophlebitis must be distinguished as a separate infective emergency requiring antibiotics and sometimes vein excision"],"pathophysiology":"Superficial vein thrombosis develops through the components of the Virchow triad acting on a superficial vein: venous stasis (typically within tortuous varicosities), endothelial injury (from cannulation, trauma or chemical irritation) and hypercoagulability. Thrombus forms within the lumen and provokes an inflammatory response in the vein wall and surrounding tissue, producing the characteristic tender, indurated, erythematous cord. When the process involves the great or small saphenous trunk it can propagate proximally and extend through the saphenofemoral or saphenopopliteal junction into the deep venous system, or shed thrombus to the pulmonary circulation, which underlies the recognised association with deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.","additionalDifferentials":["Cellulitis or other soft tissue infection","Deep vein thrombosis","Lymphangitis","Erythema nodosum or other panniculitis","Ruptured popliteal (Baker) cyst","Insect bite or localised hypersensitivity reaction","Suppurative (septic) thrombophlebitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Whole-leg duplex ultrasound to confirm the diagnosis, map extent and measure distance to the deep junction","Duplex assessment for coexisting deep vein thrombosis","Thrombophilia screen in selected patients with recurrent, unprovoked or unusual-site thrombosis","Evaluation for occult malignancy when the pattern is migratory or unexplained","Full blood count and inflammatory markers if a septic (suppurative) process is suspected","Venous reflux (varicose vein) studies to assess underlying chronic venous disease","Blood cultures and imaging for suppurative thrombophlebitis where infection is likely"],"redFlags":["Thrombus extending towards or across the saphenofemoral or saphenopopliteal junction","Pleuritic chest pain or breathlessness suggesting pulmonary embolism","Fever, purulence or fluctuance indicating suppurative (septic) thrombophlebitis","Migratory pattern raising suspicion of underlying malignancy","Extensive or rapidly progressing thrombosis"],"prognosis":"Most localised superficial vein thrombosis settles over a few weeks with conservative measures and, where indicated, a course of anticoagulation. However, proximal extension and venous thromboembolism can occur, and recurrence is common in those with varicose veins or an underlying thrombophilia.","pitfalls":["Dismissing the condition as trivial and missing a concurrent deep vein thrombosis or junctional extension","Failing to image the whole leg or to measure the distance from the thrombus to the deep venous junction","Overlooking an underlying malignancy in migratory thrombophlebitis","Not recognising suppurative (septic) thrombophlebitis, which needs antibiotics and possible vein excision","Providing an inadequate duration or intensity of anticoagulation for extensive or junction-related disease"],"management":["Treat short-segment thrombosis remote from the deep junction with symptomatic measures such as non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, compression and mobilisation","Give a defined course of prophylactic-intensity anticoagulation, such as fondaparinux or low-molecular-weight heparin, for thrombosis of significant length lying away from the saphenous junctions","Use therapeutic anticoagulation as for deep vein thrombosis when thrombus lies close to or extends into the deep system","Perform whole-leg compression ultrasound to map extent, measure distance to the deep junction and exclude coexisting deep vein thrombosis","Investigate for occult malignancy or thromboangiitis obliterans in migratory or recurrent thrombophlebitis (Trousseau sign), and exclude an underlying carcinoma in Mondor disease of the chest wall","Undertake a thrombophilia screen in recurrent, unprovoked or unusual-site cases","Address underlying chronic venous disease and varicosities to reduce recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","European Society for Vascular Surgery clinical practice guidelines on the management of venous thrombosis","CHEST guidelines on antithrombotic therapy for venous thromboembolic disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"superior_laryngeal_neuralgia":{"name":"Superior laryngeal neuralgia","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SLN neuralgia","laryngeal neuralgia","superior laryngeal nerve neuralgia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Causes paroxysms of sharp, shooting pain in the lateral neck, over the thyrohyoid membrane, radiating to the angle of the jaw, ear, and supraclavicular region","Attacks are triggered by swallowing, phonation, coughing, yawning, or turning the head","A tender trigger point is often found over the thyrohyoid membrane where the internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve pierces it","It may follow upper respiratory infection, neck surgery (including thyroid surgery and endarterectomy), or trauma","Diagnostic and therapeutic superior laryngeal nerve block at the trigger point relieving the pain strongly supports the diagnosis","First-line medical therapy uses anticonvulsants such as carbamazepine; repeated nerve blocks or nerve sectioning help refractory cases","The distribution and swallow-triggered pattern distinguish it from glossopharyngeal neuralgia and carotidynia"],"pathophysiology":"Superior laryngeal neuralgia is a paroxysmal neuralgia of the superior laryngeal nerve, usually its internal branch, which supplies sensation to the supraglottic larynx and pierces the thyrohyoid membrane. Irritation, entrapment, or injury of the nerve, sometimes after infection, surgery, or trauma to the neck, is thought to generate ectopic afferent discharges triggered by laryngeal movement during swallowing and phonation. The resulting lancinating pain is referred throughout the sensory field of the nerve, and a focal trigger point overlying its entry through the thyrohyoid membrane is characteristic.","additionalDifferentials":["Glossopharyngeal neuralgia","Carotidynia","Eagle syndrome","Thyroid pathology (thyroiditis or nodular disease)","Laryngeal or hypopharyngeal malignancy","Cervical spine referred pain","Post-surgical neuroma of the neck"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Palpation for a trigger point over the thyrohyoid membrane reproducing the pain","Diagnostic superior laryngeal nerve block with local anaesthetic","Flexible laryngoscopy to exclude a mucosal or supraglottic lesion","Ultrasound of the neck and thyroid to exclude local pathology","MRI or CT of the neck if a structural or neoplastic cause is suspected","Review of any prior neck or thyroid surgery"],"redFlags":["A neck or laryngeal mass suggesting malignancy","Progressive hoarseness, stridor, or dysphagia","Constant rather than paroxysmal pain with systemic features","New cranial nerve deficits"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and frequently responds to anticonvulsant medication or superior laryngeal nerve blocks, with many cases settling over time; refractory pain can be controlled by repeated blocks or nerve sectioning.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with glossopharyngeal neuralgia given overlapping triggers","Overlooking laryngeal or thyroid malignancy as a cause of lateral neck pain","Failing to seek and test the thyrohyoid trigger point","Attributing the referred otalgia to primary ear disease"],"management":["Start an anticonvulsant such as carbamazepine as first-line medical therapy","Perform a superior laryngeal nerve block with local anaesthetic at the trigger point, which is both diagnostic and therapeutic","Offer repeated nerve blocks for pain that recurs after an initial response","Consider nerve sectioning or ablation of the superior laryngeal nerve for refractory pain","Exclude a mucosal or supraglottic lesion with flexible laryngoscopy and treat any identified precipitant such as prior neck surgery or infection","Refer to combined ENT, neurology and pain services for resistant cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"superior_mesenteric_artery_syndrome":{"name":"Superior mesenteric artery syndrome (Wilkie syndrome)","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Wilkie syndrome","SMA syndrome","cast syndrome","arteriomesenteric duodenal compression"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with postprandial epigastric pain, early satiety, nausea, bilious vomiting and weight loss from proximal small-bowel obstruction","The third part of the duodenum is compressed between the superior mesenteric artery in front and the aorta behind, in the angle where the artery leaves the aorta","Loss of the mesenteric fat pad from significant weight loss narrows the aorto-mesenteric angle, so it is often triggered by weight loss, creating a vicious cycle","Symptoms may be relieved by lying prone, in the knee-chest position, or on the left side, which opens the aorto-mesenteric angle","Associations include rapid weight loss, prolonged bed rest, spinal surgery or bracing, and eating disorders","Contrast studies and cross-sectional imaging show duodenal dilatation with an abrupt vertical cut-off and a reduced aorto-mesenteric angle and distance","Initial management is nutritional restoration to rebuild the mesenteric fat pad, with surgery such as duodenojejunostomy reserved for refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Superior mesenteric artery syndrome results from compression of the third part of the duodenum in the narrow angle between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta. Normally a pad of mesenteric and retroperitoneal fat holds the artery away from the aorta, keeping the aorto-mesenteric angle wide enough for the duodenum to pass. Marked weight loss, catabolic states or altered spinal anatomy reduce this fat cushion, narrowing the angle and trapping the duodenum. The resulting partial obstruction causes proximal dilatation, vomiting and further weight loss, which in turn worsens the compression in a self-perpetuating cycle.","additionalDifferentials":["Peptic ulcer disease with gastric outlet obstruction","Diabetic or other gastroparesis","Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction","Malrotation with duodenal obstruction","Pancreatic or duodenal malignancy","Eating disorder without mechanical obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper gastrointestinal contrast study showing duodenal dilatation with abrupt cut-off","CT or MR angiography measuring the aorto-mesenteric angle and distance","Upper endoscopy to exclude mucosal and obstructing lesions","Nutritional assessment including weight and body-mass index","Investigations to exclude gastroparesis and other dysmotility"],"redFlags":["Severe dehydration and electrolyte disturbance from persistent vomiting","Profound weight loss and malnutrition","Gastric or duodenal dilatation risking perforation or aspiration","Features of an underlying eating disorder requiring integrated care"],"prognosis":"Many patients improve with nutritional restoration and conservative measures; those with anatomical fixation or failure of conservative treatment may need surgery, which is generally effective.","pitfalls":["Dismissing symptoms as functional or as an eating disorder without imaging the duodenum","Overlooking the syndrome as a complication of rapid weight loss or spinal treatment","Proceeding to surgery before an adequate trial of nutritional restoration","Failing to correct dehydration and electrolytes before intervention"],"management":["Restore nutrition as first-line treatment, using nasojejunal feeding distal to the obstruction or parenteral nutrition to rebuild the mesenteric fat pad before graded oral refeeding","Advise postural measures after eating, adopting prone, left-lateral or knee-chest positioning to open the aorto-mesenteric angle","Decompress the stomach with a nasogastric tube during acute obstruction and correct fluid and electrolyte disturbances","Use small frequent meals and prokinetic agents for symptomatic relief","Address any underlying eating disorder or catabolic precipitant with psychiatric and dietetic support","Refer for surgical duodenojejunostomy when conservative measures fail or there is fixed anatomical obstruction","Monitor weight and body-mass index and involve gastroenterology, dietetics and surgery in a multidisciplinary approach"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Schwartz's Principles of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"superior_oblique_myokymia":{"name":"Superior oblique myokymia","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["superior oblique microtremor","SOM"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign paroxysmal disorder of the superior oblique muscle causing brief episodes of monocular oscillopsia and torsional-vertical diplopia","Attacks are typically short, frequent and precipitated by looking in the direction of the affected superior oblique","The high-frequency low-amplitude movements may be hard to see and are best appreciated on slit-lamp or fundus examination","It is usually idiopathic but has been attributed to neurovascular compression of the trochlear nerve","Most cases are benign, though MRI is warranted to exclude a posterior fossa lesion","Membrane-stabilising drugs or, in refractory cases, surgery or microvascular decompression may be considered"],"pathophysiology":"Superior oblique myokymia is thought to arise from ephaptic transmission or aberrant regeneration within the trochlear nerve, in some cases related to vascular compression at the nerve root exit, producing spontaneous high-frequency contraction of the superior oblique muscle. The resulting small torsional and vertical eye movements are perceived as shimmering monocular oscillopsia and transient diplopia.","additionalDifferentials":["Ocular neuromyotonia","Nystagmus (acquired pendular or see-saw)","Trochlear nerve palsy with aberrant regeneration","Orbital myopathy or restrictive strabismus","Functional eye-movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp or fundus observation of the fine torsional movements","MRI of the brain and orbits to exclude posterior fossa or trochlear-nerve lesions","Orthoptic assessment of ocular alignment and motility","Video-oculography where available to characterise the movements"],"redFlags":["Associated other cranial neuropathies suggesting a structural lesion","Progressive or persistent (non-paroxysmal) diplopia","New neurological signs","Onset after head trauma with additional deficits"],"prognosis":"The disorder is benign and may remit spontaneously or respond to medical therapy; symptoms can be intrusive but do not threaten sight.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the subtle movements and labelling symptoms functional","Omitting MRI and missing a compressive posterior fossa lesion","Confusing it with ocular neuromyotonia, which causes sustained rather than shimmering deviation","Assuming persistent diplopia rather than the characteristic paroxysms"],"management":["Reassure and observe, as the disorder is benign and may remit spontaneously","Offer membrane-stabilising drugs such as carbamazepine or gabapentin to suppress attacks","Consider a topical or systemic beta-blocker such as timolol or propranolol as an alternative medical option","Arrange MRI of the brain and orbits to exclude a posterior fossa or trochlear-nerve lesion","Reserve strabismus surgery or microvascular decompression for disabling, drug-refractory symptoms","Refer to neuro-ophthalmology for assessment and ongoing management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"superior_orbital_fissure_syndrome":{"name":"Superior orbital fissure syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rochon-Duvigneaud syndrome","SOF syndrome","sphenoidal fissure syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by ophthalmoplegia from involvement of the third, fourth, and sixth cranial nerves with sensory loss in the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve","A fixed dilated pupil and ptosis may be present from third nerve involvement, and sympathetic fibres passing through can also be affected","Vision is characteristically spared because the optic nerve exits through the separate optic canal, not the fissure","Progression to include the optic nerve signals extension to the orbital apex (orbital apex syndrome)","The syndrome may follow trauma with fractures involving the fissure, or result from tumour or inflammation","Periorbital pain, proptosis, and reduced corneal sensation are common accompanying features","Careful assessment of vision distinguishes this from orbital apex syndrome and guides urgency"],"pathophysiology":"The superior orbital fissure is the cleft between the greater and lesser wings of the sphenoid that transmits the oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nerves, the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve, sympathetic fibres, and the superior ophthalmic vein. A lesion here compresses or infiltrates these structures to produce ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, a dilated pupil, and forehead sensory loss, while the optic nerve is spared because it passes through the adjacent optic canal. Causes include trauma, tumour, granulomatous inflammation, infection, and vascular lesions in the region.","additionalDifferentials":["Orbital apex syndrome with added optic neuropathy","Cavernous sinus syndrome","Tolosa-Hunt granulomatous inflammation","Orbital cellulitis","Thyroid eye disease with restrictive ophthalmoplegia","Idiopathic orbital inflammatory disease","Multiple ocular motor nerve palsies from other skull base pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT of the orbit and skull base, particularly after trauma, to assess the fissure and fractures","MRI of the orbit and cavernous sinus with contrast","Assessment of visual acuity and the afferent pupillary pathway to exclude apex involvement","Inflammatory markers and infection screen","Screening for tumour or metastatic disease where relevant","Trigeminal sensory testing to map the deficit"],"redFlags":["Any decline in vision indicating extension to the orbital apex","Signs of orbital infection needing urgent treatment","A history of significant trauma with orbital fracture","Rapidly progressive cranial neuropathies suggesting tumour or aggressive inflammation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the cause; traumatic and inflammatory cases may recover partially or fully with appropriate management, whereas neoplastic causes are more guarded, and some ophthalmoplegia and sensory loss can persist.","pitfalls":["Failing to check vision and thereby missing progression to orbital apex syndrome","Confusing it with cavernous sinus syndrome, which more often involves the maxillary trigeminal division and can cause a Horner syndrome","Overlooking a treatable inflammatory or infective cause","Attributing all findings to trauma without imaging the fissure"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying cause, using corticosteroids for granulomatous or inflammatory disease, antimicrobials for infection and oncological therapy for tumour","Undertake urgent surgical decompression when trauma with a fracture or haematoma compresses the fissure and produces a progressive deficit","Protect the cornea with lubricants and lid taping where corneal sensation is reduced, to prevent exposure keratopathy","Provide analgesia for periorbital pain","Monitor visual acuity and the afferent pupillary response to detect extension to the orbital apex","Refer for combined neurosurgery, ophthalmology, oncology and maxillofacial input according to the aetiology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"superior_semicircular_canal_dehiscence":{"name":"Superior semicircular canal dehiscence","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Head and Neck","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SSCD","superior canal dehiscence syndrome","SCDS","Minor syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by absence of the bony roof over the superior semicircular canal, creating a pathological 'third window' into the labyrinth","Sound- or pressure-induced vertigo and oscillopsia (Tullio phenomenon and Hennebert sign) are characteristic and often triggered by loud noise, coughing or straining","Autophony (hearing one's own voice, heartbeat, or eye movements unusually loudly) is a common and distressing symptom","Audiometry classically shows a low-frequency air-bone gap with intact or supranormal bone conduction, mimicking conductive loss despite a normal mobile ossicular chain","Cervical and ocular vestibular-evoked myogenic potentials (VEMPs) show abnormally low thresholds and enlarged amplitudes on the affected side","High-resolution CT of the temporal bone with reformats in the planes of the superior canal (Poschl and Stenver views) confirms the bony defect","Many radiographically thin or dehiscent canals are asymptomatic, so imaging must be correlated with symptoms and physiology before intervention","Symptomatic cases may be managed conservatively or by surgical canal plugging or resurfacing via a middle-fossa or transmastoid approach"],"pathophysiology":"The superior semicircular canal normally lies enclosed within dense bone beneath the floor of the middle cranial fossa. When this bony covering is congenitally thin or eroded, a dehiscence creates an abnormal 'third mobile window' in addition to the oval and round windows. Acoustic and pressure energy that would normally drive the cochlea is instead partly shunted through the dehiscence, abnormally stimulating the vestibular apparatus and dissipating sound energy. This explains sound- and pressure-evoked vertigo, the low-frequency pseudo-conductive hearing pattern with preserved bone conduction, autophony, and the hypersensitive VEMP responses.","additionalDifferentials":["Otosclerosis or other causes of a conductive air-bone gap","Meniere disease","Perilymph fistula","Patulous eustachian tube","Enlarged vestibular aqueduct syndrome","Migraine-associated vertigo"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution temporal bone CT with Poschl and Stenver reformats","Pure-tone audiometry with masked bone conduction to demonstrate the low-frequency air-bone gap","Cervical and ocular vestibular-evoked myogenic potential testing","Tympanometry and acoustic reflex testing to show a mobile ossicular chain with preserved reflexes","Video-oculography to document the vertical-torsional nystagmus evoked by sound or pressure"],"redFlags":["Disabling vertigo or oscillopsia interfering with daily function or driving","Symptoms suggesting a coexisting intracranial process such as raised intracranial pressure or an encephalocoele through the defect","Progressive sensorineural hearing loss beyond the expected pseudo-conductive pattern","New cerebrospinal fluid leak or meningitic features"],"prognosis":"Many patients have mild symptoms that can be managed conservatively by avoiding provoking stimuli. When symptoms are disabling, surgical plugging or resurfacing of the canal relieves sound- and pressure-induced vestibular symptoms in most cases, though there is a small risk of hearing loss and symptoms may occasionally recur or arise on the contralateral side.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the low-frequency air-bone gap as otosclerosis and performing unnecessary stapes surgery","Relying on CT alone, since thin or dehiscent bone is common and may be asymptomatic or overcalled by volume averaging","Overlooking autophony and pulsatile or self-sound symptoms that point to a third-window disorder","Failing to counsel about the small operative risk to hearing before canal surgery"],"management":["Manage mild symptoms conservatively by avoiding provoking loud sounds, pressure changes and straining","Offer surgical plugging, capping or resurfacing of the superior canal via a middle fossa or transmastoid approach for disabling sound- and pressure-induced symptoms","Consider round window reinforcement as a less invasive surgical alternative","Provide vestibular rehabilitation to aid compensation for imbalance","Counsel on the small risk of hearing loss and symptom recurrence associated with surgery","Monitor with audiometry and vestibular-evoked myogenic potentials and refer to a neurotology service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"superior_vena_cava_obstruction":{"name":"superior vena cava obstruction","system":"Respiratory / Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SVCO","superior vena cava syndrome","SVC syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer: Principles and Practice of Oncology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Malignancy causes the majority of cases—lung cancer (particularly small-cell and squamous), lymphoma, thymoma and germ-cell tumours—while an increasing minority are due to thrombosis around indwelling central catheters or pacemaker leads.","Typical features are facial and neck swelling, plethora, distended non-pulsatile neck and chest-wall veins, headache worsened by bending forward, and a positive Pemberton's sign on raising the arms.","Contrast-enhanced CT of the thorax defines the level, cause and collateral circulation and guides the safest route to a tissue diagnosis.","Wherever the airway and haemodynamics permit, obtain a histological diagnosis before treatment, because chemosensitive tumours (lymphoma, small-cell lung cancer, germ-cell) are highly treatable and empirical radiotherapy can render subsequent biopsy uninterpretable.","Endovascular stenting provides the most rapid symptomatic relief and is increasingly first-line for severe or refractory obstruction irrespective of histology.","Definitive treatment is directed at the cause—systemic chemotherapy for small-cell lung cancer, lymphoma and germ-cell tumours, radiotherapy for radioresponsive disease, and anticoagulation with catheter management for thrombotic causes.","The routine benefit of corticosteroids is unproven except where the tumour is steroid-responsive (lymphoma) or to reduce radiotherapy-associated oedema.","Laryngeal or cerebral oedema and haemodynamic compromise convert a subacute presentation into a true emergency requiring immediate airway assessment and vascular intervention."],"pathophysiology":"Obstruction of the thin-walled, low-pressure superior vena cava—by external compression from a mediastinal mass, direct tumour invasion, or intraluminal thrombosis—impedes venous return from the head, neck and upper limbs. Pressure rises in the tributary veins, producing oedema, plethora and the recruitment of collateral pathways through the azygos, internal mammary and superficial chest-wall veins; the gradual development of these collaterals explains why chronic obstruction may be surprisingly well tolerated. When obstruction is rapid or complete, or extends to compromise the airway or raise intracranial venous pressure, life-threatening laryngeal and cerebral oedema can develop.","additionalDifferentials":["Cardiac tamponade or constrictive pericarditis","Congestive cardiac failure","Angioedema","Retrosternal thyroid goitre or other benign mediastinal mass","Fibrosing mediastinitis","Nephrotic syndrome with generalised oedema","Catheter-associated central venous thrombosis without malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the thorax","Chest radiograph for mediastinal widening or a right hilar mass","Tissue sampling by sputum cytology, bronchoscopy, EBUS, mediastinoscopy or core biopsy","Serum LDH, beta-hCG and alpha-fetoprotein when lymphoma or germ-cell tumour is possible","Venography or CT venography to delineate the obstruction and thrombus","Full blood count and coagulation screen","Cross-sectional staging to assess disease extent"],"redFlags":["Stridor or other signs of airway compromise","Laryngeal oedema","Reduced consciousness or other features of cerebral oedema","Haemodynamic instability","Rapidly progressive facial and upper-body swelling"],"prognosis":"Symptomatic prognosis is generally good because stenting and tumour-directed therapy relieve obstruction effectively, but overall survival is dictated by the underlying malignancy. Chemosensitive tumours may be curable, whereas SVCO from advanced non-small-cell lung cancer signals a poor outlook.","pitfalls":["Giving empirical radiotherapy or corticosteroids before histology in a potentially curable chemosensitive tumour","Assuming every case is malignant and missing catheter-associated thrombosis","Delaying airway and vascular assessment in a patient with stridor or cerebral symptoms","Attempting biopsy without imaging-guided planning of the safest approach","Overlooking concurrent venous thromboembolism requiring anticoagulation"],"management":["Secure a histological diagnosis before starting treatment wherever the airway and haemodynamics allow, since empirical radiotherapy can render subsequent biopsy uninterpretable","Relieve severe or refractory obstruction rapidly with endovascular stenting, increasingly first-line irrespective of histology","Direct definitive therapy at the cause, giving systemic chemotherapy for small-cell lung cancer, lymphoma and germ-cell tumours and radiotherapy for radioresponsive disease","Manage thrombotic obstruction with anticoagulation and removal or management of any offending central catheter","Provide supportive measures including head elevation, supplemental oxygen and corticosteroids for airway or cerebral oedema","Institute emergency airway management for laryngeal oedema or a critically compromised airway","Refer promptly to oncology for multidisciplinary treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["DeVita, Hellman and Rosenberg's Cancer: Principles and Practice of Oncology","Oxford Handbook of Oncology","British Thoracic Society guidance on the management of superior vena cava obstruction"],"crossLinks":[]},"superior_vena_cava_syndrome":{"name":"Superior vena cava syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SVC syndrome","SVC obstruction","superior vena cava obstruction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by obstruction of the superior vena cava, either extrinsic compression or invasion by tumour or intraluminal thrombosis","Malignancy is the leading cause, particularly bronchogenic carcinoma (small-cell and squamous) and non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Thrombotic causes are increasingly common with indwelling central venous catheters and pacemaker leads","Presents with facial and neck swelling and plethora, distended neck and chest wall veins, arm swelling, headache, and dyspnoea, typically worse on bending forward or lying flat","Pemberton's sign (facial congestion on raising the arms) supports the diagnosis","Contrast-enhanced CT of the chest defines the level and cause of obstruction and guides biopsy; a tissue diagnosis is important because management depends on tumour type","Management is directed at the cause, treating the underlying malignancy (chemotherapy or radiotherapy) or thrombus (anticoagulation), with endovascular stenting for prompt symptom relief in severe or refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Superior vena cava syndrome results from obstruction to venous return through the SVC to the right atrium. Most cases are due to malignant mediastinal disease, commonly lung cancer or lymphoma, that compresses or invades the thin-walled, low-pressure SVC; thrombosis, frequently related to intravascular devices, is a growing non-malignant cause. Obstruction raises venous pressure in the head, neck, and upper limbs, producing oedema, venous distension, and the recruitment of collateral chest wall veins. The severity of symptoms depends on how rapidly the obstruction develops and whether collaterals have had time to form, with airway and cerebral oedema being the most dangerous consequences.","additionalDifferentials":["Congestive cardiac failure","Cardiac tamponade or constrictive pericarditis","Mediastinal fibrosis (for example fibrosing mediastinitis or post-radiation)","Nephrotic syndrome or other causes of facial oedema","Angioedema","Bilateral internal jugular vein thrombosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the chest to define the level, extent, and cause","Tissue diagnosis (sputum cytology, bronchoscopy, or biopsy of a nodal or mediastinal mass) to guide oncological treatment","CT or MR venography to map the obstruction and collaterals","Chest radiograph as an initial screen for mediastinal widening or mass","Venography at the time of stenting","Assessment for catheter- or lead-related thrombosis where relevant"],"redFlags":["Stridor, laryngeal oedema, or respiratory compromise (an airway emergency)","Cerebral oedema with confusion, severe headache, or altered consciousness","Haemodynamic instability","Rapid onset of severe symptoms without collateral formation"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends chiefly on the underlying cause; malignant SVC syndrome reflects the prognosis of the tumour, although stenting and oncological therapy give rapid symptom relief, whereas benign thrombotic causes generally respond well to anticoagulation and stenting.","pitfalls":["Starting empirical radiotherapy before obtaining a tissue diagnosis in a stable patient, potentially obscuring histology","Overlooking a catheter- or lead-related thrombus as the cause","Failing to recognise airway or cerebral compromise requiring urgent intervention","Assuming all SVC obstruction is malignant"],"management":["Obtain a tissue diagnosis before treatment wherever it is clinically safe, as management depends on the tumour type","Use endovascular stent placement for prompt relief of severe symptoms regardless of the underlying histology","Treat the cause definitively, with chemotherapy for small-cell bronchogenic carcinoma and non-Hodgkin lymphoma and radiotherapy for radiosensitive tumours","Manage catheter- or lead-related thrombotic obstruction with anticoagulation and device removal where appropriate","Give supportive care with head-of-bed elevation, oxygen and corticosteroids where there is airway or cerebral oedema","Escalate urgently for airway protection when stridor or laryngeal oedema develops","Refer to a multidisciplinary oncology team to guide tumour-directed treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","ESVS Clinical Practice Guidelines on Venous Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"superwarfarin_poisoning":{"name":"Superwarfarin (long-acting anticoagulant rodenticide) poisoning","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["long-acting anticoagulant rodenticide poisoning","brodifacoum poisoning","superwarfarin toxicity","bromadiolone poisoning","long-acting vitamin K antagonist poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Superwarfarins such as brodifacoum are far more potent and vastly longer acting than warfarin, so coagulopathy can persist for weeks to months","Coagulopathy is delayed, typically appearing a day or two after ingestion, because existing clotting factors must first be depleted","A single acute exploratory ingestion by a child rarely causes clinically significant coagulopathy, whereas large or repeated ingestion can","Consider covert or intentional exposure in an adult with unexplained prolonged coagulation and no prescribed anticoagulant","Bleeding can be occult, so haematuria, bruising, gum bleeding, gastrointestinal or intracranial haemorrhage should prompt assessment of coagulation","Vitamin K1 (phytomenadione) is the specific antidote and must often be continued in high oral doses for a prolonged period given the long half-life","Acute major bleeding is treated with factor replacement such as prothrombin complex concentrate or fresh frozen plasma in addition to vitamin K","Prophylactic vitamin K should not be started routinely after a trivial ingestion, as it can mask evolving coagulopathy; the INR is followed instead"],"pathophysiology":"Superwarfarins inhibit vitamin K epoxide reductase, the enzyme that regenerates reduced vitamin K, which is the essential cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors II, VII, IX and X and of proteins C and S. Without functional vitamin K these factors cannot be activated, producing an anticoagulant state. Because factor VII has the shortest half-life, the prothrombin time and INR prolong first, but full coagulopathy is delayed until preformed factors are consumed. Unlike warfarin, superwarfarins are highly lipophilic, extensively tissue-bound and slowly eliminated, so their anticoagulant effect is markedly prolonged.","additionalDifferentials":["Therapeutic warfarin over-anticoagulation","Direct oral anticoagulant or heparin effect","Liver disease with impaired synthetic function","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Vitamin K deficiency from malabsorption or poor intake","Inherited or acquired factor deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Prothrombin time and INR, measured serially over an appropriate delayed window","Activated partial thromboplastin time and fibrinogen","Full blood count to assess for anaemia from blood loss","Specific clotting factor assays and mixing studies where the cause is unclear","Superwarfarin concentration in plasma where available to confirm and monitor","Investigations for the site of bleeding, including imaging when haemorrhage is suspected"],"redFlags":["Active bleeding, especially intracranial or gastrointestinal haemorrhage","Markedly prolonged INR with clinical bleeding","Large deliberate ingestion or repeated exposure","Falling haemoglobin or haemodynamic instability","Rebound coagulopathy when vitamin K is reduced or stopped too early"],"prognosis":"With adequate and sustained vitamin K therapy and treatment of any acute bleeding, prognosis is generally good, although therapy and monitoring may be required for weeks to months; life-threatening haemorrhage before treatment carries significant risk.","pitfalls":["Checking coagulation only at presentation and missing delayed coagulopathy","Stopping vitamin K prematurely and allowing rebound anticoagulation","Giving prophylactic vitamin K after a trivial ingestion, masking evolving coagulopathy","Relying on vitamin K alone in major bleeding rather than replacing clotting factors","Failing to consider covert or intentional poisoning in unexplained coagulopathy"],"management":["Give vitamin K1 (phytomenadione) as the specific antidote, typically in high oral doses continued for a prolonged period because of the drug's long half-life","Reverse active or life-threatening haemorrhage urgently with prothrombin complex concentrate or fresh frozen plasma","Consider activated charcoal for a recent substantial ingestion presenting early","Monitor prothrombin time and INR serially over an extended window, titrating and tapering vitamin K to the coagulation response rather than treating pre-emptively","Identify and manage the site of bleeding and provide transfusion support where anaemia results","Arrange psychiatric assessment for intentional ingestion and a safeguarding review for childhood exposure, and identify the source","Involve clinical toxicology and haematology in ongoing management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","UpToDate: Anticoagulant rodenticide (long-acting) poisoning"],"crossLinks":[]},"suprascapular_neuropathy":{"name":"suprascapular neuropathy","system":"Nervous system / Peripheral nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["suprascapular nerve entrapment","suprascapular nerve palsy","spinoglenoid notch syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Entrapment or injury of the suprascapular nerve, most often at the suprascapular notch beneath the transverse scapular ligament or more distally at the spinoglenoid notch","The level of the lesion determines the deficit: a suprascapular notch lesion weakens both supraspinatus and infraspinatus, whereas a spinoglenoid notch lesion selectively weakens infraspinatus, sparing supraspinatus","Clinically there is weakness of shoulder external rotation and of the initial phase of abduction, with a deep, poorly localised posterolateral shoulder ache; the nerve carries no cutaneous sensory territory, so there is no skin numbness","A spinoglenoid notch ganglion cyst, frequently associated with a posterosuperior glenoid labral tear, is a classic and treatable cause of isolated infraspinatus wasting","Repetitive overhead activity, particularly in volleyball players and other overhead athletes, and traction injuries or scapular fractures are recognised mechanisms","Visible infraspinatus wasting in the infraspinatus fossa is often the presenting sign, and can be mistaken for a rotator cuff tear or C5-C6 radiculopathy","Management is conservative with physiotherapy and activity modification for traction or overuse cases, decompression or aspiration for a compressive ganglion, and repair of an associated labral tear where present"],"pathophysiology":"The suprascapular nerve arises from the upper trunk of the brachial plexus (C5-C6, occasionally C4) and runs laterally to pass through the suprascapular notch beneath the transverse scapular ligament, where it gives motor branches to supraspinatus, before curving around the base of the scapular spine through the spinoglenoid notch to supply infraspinatus. Both notches are relatively fixed fibro-osseous tunnels that tether the nerve, making it vulnerable to compression and to repetitive traction during overhead and cross-body movement. A ganglion cyst arising from a labral tear characteristically compresses the nerve at the spinoglenoid notch, selectively denervating infraspinatus, while ligamentous or bony narrowing at the suprascapular notch affects both muscles. Because the nerve is essentially purely motor, injury produces weakness and muscle wasting with pain but without cutaneous sensory loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Rotator cuff tear (supraspinatus or infraspinatus)","C5-C6 cervical radiculopathy","Neuralgic amyotrophy (Parsonage-Turner syndrome)","Upper-trunk brachial plexopathy","Glenohumeral or acromioclavicular joint pathology","Quadrilateral space syndrome (axillary neuropathy) as an alternative cause of shoulder wasting"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Needle electromyography of supraspinatus and infraspinatus to localise the lesion to the suprascapular or spinoglenoid notch","Nerve conduction studies measuring suprascapular motor latencies to the two muscles","MRI of the shoulder to detect a ganglion cyst, labral tear and the pattern of muscle oedema or fatty atrophy","Magnetic resonance or ultrasound arthrography if a posterosuperior labral tear is suspected","Cervical spine imaging when C5-C6 radiculopathy is a competing diagnosis","Diagnostic suprascapular nerve block in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Acute severe shoulder-girdle pain followed by weakness and wasting, suggesting neuralgic amyotrophy","A spinoglenoid or suprascapular notch mass on imaging indicating a compressive ganglion or tumour","Rapidly progressive or profound wasting despite conservative treatment","Associated mechanical symptoms of a labral tear such as clicking, catching or instability"],"prognosis":"Traction and overuse cases and those with a decompressed or aspirated ganglion generally recover strength over months, particularly when an associated labral tear is addressed. Long-standing entrapment with established fatty atrophy of the muscle recovers less completely, leaving persistent weakness of external rotation.","pitfalls":["Attributing external rotation weakness and infraspinatus wasting to a rotator cuff tear without imaging or electrophysiology","Missing a spinoglenoid notch ganglion and its associated labral tear as a treatable cause","Confusing the deep aching pain with primary glenohumeral joint disease","Overlooking neuralgic amyotrophy in acute painful presentations","Failing to localise the lesion by not distinguishing isolated infraspinatus from combined supraspinatus and infraspinatus involvement"],"management":["Manage traction and overuse cases conservatively with activity modification, avoidance of provocative overhead movement, physiotherapy and analgesia","Treat a spinoglenoid or suprascapular notch ganglion cyst by image-guided aspiration or surgical excision, addressing any associated glenoid labral tear","Offer surgical decompression, including release of the transverse scapular ligament, for entrapment that fails conservative management","Consider a diagnostic and therapeutic suprascapular nerve block in selected patients","Monitor recovery with serial needle electromyography for evidence of reinnervation","Refer earlier for surgery where established fatty atrophy predicts poorer spontaneous recovery, involving shoulder and orthopaedic services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"supraventricular_tachycardia":{"name":"Supraventricular tachycardia","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SVT","paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia","PSVT","AVNRT","AVRT"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["SVT is a broad term for regular, usually narrow-complex tachycardias originating at or above the atrioventricular node, most commonly AV nodal reentrant tachycardia and AV reentrant tachycardia using an accessory pathway","Typical presentation is abrupt-onset, regular palpitations with sudden termination, often with lightheadedness, chest discomfort, or breathlessness","The QRS is usually narrow unless there is aberrant conduction or a pre-existing bundle branch block, when it can mimic ventricular tachycardia","Vagal manoeuvres are the first-line acute intervention to interrupt reentry through the AV node","When vagal manoeuvres fail in a stable patient, an AV nodal blocking agent given under monitoring is the usual next step","Synchronised cardioversion is indicated when the patient is haemodynamically unstable","Catheter ablation offers a high cure rate for recurrent AVNRT and AVRT and is a definitive long-term option","A regular narrow-complex tachycardia should be distinguished from atrial flutter and atrial fibrillation, which have different management"],"pathophysiology":"Most paroxysmal SVT is caused by reentry. In AV nodal reentrant tachycardia a functional circuit forms within or near the AV node using fast and slow pathways. In AV reentrant tachycardia the circuit incorporates an accessory pathway connecting atrium and ventricle, as in Wolff-Parkinson-White. Atrial tachycardias instead arise from an ectopic atrial focus with enhanced automaticity or microreentry. In reentrant mechanisms a premature beat finds one limb refractory and conducts down the other, then returns retrogradely, establishing a self-sustaining loop that the AV node often participates in, which is why AV nodal blockade frequently terminates the arrhythmia.","additionalDifferentials":["Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter","Ventricular tachycardia (especially with aberrant conduction)","Sinus tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Pre-excited tachycardia in Wolff-Parkinson-White"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG during tachycardia and in sinus rhythm to characterise the mechanism and detect pre-excitation","Continuous rhythm monitoring or ambulatory ECG for paroxysmal episodes","Electrolyte and thyroid function assessment to identify reversible triggers","Echocardiography to exclude structural heart disease","Electrophysiology study to define the circuit when ablation is considered"],"redFlags":["Haemodynamic instability with hypotension, ischaemic chest pain, pulmonary oedema, or reduced consciousness, requiring urgent synchronised cardioversion","Wide-complex tachycardia that cannot be confidently distinguished from ventricular tachycardia, which should be treated as VT until proven otherwise","Pre-excited tachycardia where AV nodal blocking drugs may be hazardous","Recurrent syncope during episodes"],"prognosis":"Most forms of SVT are not life-threatening and carry an excellent prognosis, with catheter ablation curing the majority of reentrant cases; prognosis worsens when SVT complicates structural heart disease or occurs as pre-excited tachycardia with a risk of rapid ventricular rates.","pitfalls":["Giving AV nodal blocking drugs in a pre-excited tachycardia, which can accelerate ventricular response","Misdiagnosing a wide-complex tachycardia as SVT with aberrancy when it is actually ventricular tachycardia","Delaying cardioversion in an unstable patient while attempting pharmacological control","Overlooking reversible precipitants such as stimulants, thyroid disease, or electrolyte disturbance"],"management":["Terminate acute stable episodes first with vagal manoeuvres to interrupt reentry through the AV node","Give an AV nodal blocking agent such as adenosine under continuous monitoring when vagal manoeuvres fail, using verapamil, diltiazem or a beta-blocker as alternatives","Perform immediate synchronised cardioversion when the patient is haemodynamically unstable","Offer catheter ablation as curative definitive treatment for recurrent reentrant SVT","Use long-term beta-blockers or calcium-channel blockers for prevention when ablation is declined or unsuitable","Avoid AV nodal blocking drugs in pre-excited atrial fibrillation associated with an accessory pathway","Identify and correct reversible triggers such as electrolyte disturbance, thyroid dysfunction and stimulants, and refer to cardiology electrophysiology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"surf1_leigh_syndrome":{"name":"SURF1-related Leigh syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SURF1 deficiency","cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV) deficiency","COX-deficient Leigh syndrome","subacute necrotising encephalomyelopathy (SURF1)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID) / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive Leigh syndrome caused by SURF1 mutations, in which a complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase) assembly factor is deficient, giving isolated COX deficiency","Typically presents in infancy or early childhood with psychomotor regression, hypotonia, feeding and swallowing difficulty, and brainstem signs, often triggered or worsened by intercurrent illness","Brain MRI classically shows symmetrical T2-hyperintense lesions of the basal ganglia and brainstem, the imaging hallmark of Leigh syndrome","Elevated lactate in blood and cerebrospinal fluid supports a mitochondrial energy-generation defect, and CSF lactate can be raised even when blood is near-normal","SURF1 disease tends to produce a relatively consistent phenotype with hypertrichosis reported in some patients and a notably poor tolerance of anaesthesia and metabolic stress","Diagnosis rests on the MRI pattern, isolated COX deficiency on muscle histochemistry/biochemistry, and SURF1 sequencing; management is supportive with avoidance of catabolic stress"],"pathophysiology":"SURF1 encodes an assembly factor required for correct biogenesis of complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase), the terminal enzyme of the mitochondrial respiratory chain. Biallelic loss-of-function mutations reduce assembled, functional complex IV, impairing oxidative phosphorylation and cellular ATP production. Highly energy-dependent neurons, particularly in the basal ganglia and brainstem, undergo the characteristic subacute symmetrical necrosis of Leigh syndrome, with reactive lactate accumulation. Metabolic crises are commonly precipitated by infection, fasting, or other catabolic stress that further strains oxidative metabolism.","additionalDifferentials":["Leigh syndrome from other nuclear or mitochondrial DNA mutations","Other complex IV assembly-factor defects (e.g. SCO2, COX10, COX15)","MELAS and other mitochondrial encephalopathies","Biotinidase deficiency and biotin-thiamine-responsive basal ganglia disease (treatable mimics)","Wilson disease and other basal ganglia disorders in older children","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis or infective encephalitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and CSF lactate and pyruvate concentrations","Brain MRI to demonstrate symmetrical basal ganglia and brainstem lesions","Muscle biopsy with histochemistry showing cytochrome c oxidase deficiency and respiratory-chain enzymology","SURF1 gene sequencing and broader mitochondrial gene panels","Plasma amino acids and urine organic acids to exclude treatable metabolic mimics","Cardiac and ophthalmological assessment given multisystem mitochondrial involvement"],"redFlags":["Acute neurological deterioration during intercurrent illness signalling a metabolic crisis","Brainstem dysfunction with respiratory irregularity or apnoea","Progressive bulbar involvement compromising airway and feeding safety","Anaesthetic exposure without mitochondrial precautions, risking decompensation"],"prognosis":"SURF1-related Leigh syndrome is typically progressive with a poor prognosis, and many affected children die in early childhood, often during metabolic crises, although the rate of decline varies. Care is supportive, aiming to prevent and manage crises and optimise nutrition and neurological function.","pitfalls":["Overlooking treatable mimics of Leigh syndrome (such as biotin-thiamine-responsive basal ganglia disease) before concluding an untreatable defect","Missing raised CSF lactate when blood lactate is normal","Failing to take mitochondrial anaesthetic and fasting precautions, precipitating crises","Attributing acute deterioration solely to infection without recognising metabolic decompensation"],"management":["Prevent metabolic crises by avoiding fasting and catabolic stress and treating intercurrent illness promptly with adequate glucose-containing fluids","Offer supportive vitamin and cofactor supplementation such as thiamine, riboflavin and coenzyme Q10, recognising its unproven efficacy","Avoid mitochondrial-toxic drugs, particularly sodium valproate, and use anaesthesia cautiously given poor tolerance","Control seizures with anticonvulsants while avoiding valproate, and manage dystonia and spasticity","Provide nutritional support with gastrostomy feeding for swallowing difficulty and manage lactic acidosis during crises by treating the precipitant","Coordinate multidisciplinary neurology, metabolic, cardiology, ophthalmology, respiratory and palliative care","Offer genetic counselling with carrier and prenatal testing for this autosomal recessive SURF1 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"surfactant_protein_b_deficiency":{"name":"Surfactant protein B deficiency","system":"Respiratory / Genetic interstitial lung disease","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SP-B deficiency","SFTPB deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric respiratory and genetic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal recessive disorder of pulmonary surfactant metabolism caused by biallelic SFTPB mutations","Typically presents in term newborns with severe, progressive respiratory distress unresponsive to conventional surfactant replacement","It is a leading inherited cause of fatal neonatal respiratory failure of unclear initial cause","Chest imaging resembles neonatal respiratory distress syndrome despite the infant being at term","Diagnosis rests on genetic testing, with supportive lung histology and absent mature surfactant protein B","Lung transplantation is the only definitive treatment, as the deficiency is not corrected by exogenous surfactant","Carrier parents are usually asymptomatic, and recurrence risk in future pregnancies is significant"],"pathophysiology":"Surfactant protein B is essential for the formation and stability of the surfactant film that lowers alveolar surface tension. Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in SFTPB abolish functional protein, so surfactant cannot organise normally and lamellar bodies are malformed. The result is widespread alveolar collapse, impaired gas exchange and a histological pattern of alveolar proteinosis with accumulated abnormal surfactant material. Because the defect is intrinsic to surfactant assembly, exogenous surfactant provides only transient benefit, and progressive respiratory failure ensues.","additionalDifferentials":["Surfactant protein C deficiency","ABCA3 deficiency","Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome of prematurity","Congenital pneumonia or sepsis","Congenital alveolar proteinosis of other cause","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for SFTPB mutations","Chest imaging showing a diffuse ground-glass or granular pattern","Lung biopsy with immunostaining for surfactant proteins when needed","Assessment for the common recurrent SFTPB mutation","Exclusion of infection and structural cardiac disease"],"redFlags":["Severe respiratory failure in a term neonate without another cause","Poor or only transient response to exogenous surfactant","Rapidly progressive hypoxaemia requiring escalating support","Family history of unexplained neonatal respiratory death"],"prognosis":"Classic complete surfactant protein B deficiency is usually fatal in infancy without lung transplantation; partial or milder variants may have a more protracted course.","pitfalls":["Attributing term-infant respiratory failure solely to infection or transient tachypnoea","Assuming failure to respond to surfactant excludes a surfactant disorder","Delaying genetic testing in unexplained severe neonatal lung disease","Overlooking the recurrence risk and the value of genetic counselling"],"management":["Definitive treatment is lung transplantation, since exogenous surfactant replacement gives only transient benefit and cannot correct the intrinsic surfactant protein B defect","Provide intensive respiratory support with mechanical ventilation and oxygen, using exogenous surfactant only as a temporising measure","Consider extracorporeal membrane oxygenation as a bridge to transplantation in refractory respiratory failure","Optimise nutrition and growth and treat intercurrent infection promptly to keep the infant transplant-eligible","Monitor gas exchange and screen for pulmonary hypertension with serial echocardiography","Coordinate care across neonatology, paediatric respiratory medicine and a lung transplant centre","Offer genetic counselling with carrier and family testing given autosomal recessive inheritance, and discuss prenatal testing in future pregnancies","Introduce palliative care and honest prognostic discussion where transplantation is not feasible"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kendig's Disorders of the Respiratory Tract in Children (standard reference)","Paediatric interstitial lung disease guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"surfactant_protein_c_deficiency":{"name":"Surfactant protein C deficiency","system":"Respiratory / Genetic interstitial lung disease","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SP-C deficiency","SFTPC-related interstitial lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Respiratory and genetic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by mutations in SFTPC and usually inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, often with variable expression and de novo cases","Presentation ranges widely, from severe neonatal respiratory disease to childhood or adult-onset interstitial lung disease","A dominant-negative mechanism means that even a single abnormal allele can cause disease, unlike the recessive surfactant protein B deficiency","Imaging typically shows diffuse interstitial change, ground-glass opacity and, over time, cysts or fibrosis","The clinical course is highly variable within and between families carrying the same mutation","Diagnosis combines genetic testing with imaging and, where needed, lung biopsy","Management is supportive, sometimes with immunomodulatory therapy, and lung transplantation for advanced disease"],"pathophysiology":"Surfactant protein C is a hydrophobic protein that contributes to surfactant function and is produced by alveolar type II cells. Many SFTPC mutations lead to misfolding of the proprotein, which accumulates within type II cells and triggers endoplasmic reticulum stress, cellular injury and an abnormal repair response. This cellular toxicity, rather than simple loss of protein, drives progressive interstitial inflammation and fibrosis. The dominant-negative and gain-of-toxicity effects explain the autosomal dominant inheritance and the wide variability in age of onset and severity.","additionalDifferentials":["Surfactant protein B deficiency","ABCA3 deficiency","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis in adults","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Other childhood interstitial lung disease (chILD) entities","Connective tissue disease-associated interstitial lung disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for SFTPC mutations","High-resolution CT of the chest","Lung biopsy when the diagnosis is unclear","Pulmonary function testing in older children and adults","Family screening and assessment given dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive respiratory failure at any age","Severe neonatal presentation","Progressive fibrosis with declining lung function","Superimposed respiratory infection causing acute deterioration"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is variable, ranging from severe fatal neonatal disease to a chronic course into adulthood; some patients stabilise while others progress to fibrosis requiring transplantation.","pitfalls":["Assuming an adult with interstitial lung disease cannot have a genetic surfactant disorder","Expecting a uniform course from a given mutation","Missing the diagnosis in a family labelled as having idiopathic fibrosis","Overlooking the need to screen relatives for dominant inheritance"],"management":["Trial empirical anti-inflammatory therapy with systemic corticosteroids, sometimes combined with hydroxychloroquine, to modify the interstitial inflammation, though evidence is limited","Provide supplemental oxygen and other supportive care for chronic interstitial lung disease","Ensure routine, influenza and pneumococcal immunisation and treat respiratory infections promptly to limit exacerbations","Reserve lung transplantation for progressive end-stage fibrotic disease","Monitor disease with serial pulmonary function testing and high-resolution CT","Manage care through a paediatric or adult interstitial lung disease multidisciplinary team","Offer genetic counselling and family assessment given autosomal dominant inheritance with de novo cases and variable expression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kendig's Disorders of the Respiratory Tract in Children (standard reference)","Interstitial lung disease guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"surfactant_protein_deficiency":{"name":"Surfactant protein deficiency","system":"Respiratory / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SFTPB deficiency","SFTPC deficiency","ABCA3 deficiency","surfactant dysfunction disorder","genetic surfactant deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATS/ERS interstitial lung disease guidance and respiratory genetics texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider in a term newborn with severe unexplained respiratory distress and diffuse infiltrates that fails to resolve like typical prematurity-related disease","Surfactant protein B and ABCA3 deficiency typically present in the neonatal period, often with rapidly progressive and severe disease","Surfactant protein C mutations and some ABCA3 variants can present later in childhood or in adults as chronic interstitial lung disease","Inheritance differs by gene: SFTPB and ABCA3 are autosomal recessive, whereas SFTPC disease is autosomal dominant and may arise de novo","High-resolution imaging shows diffuse ground-glass change and, over time, features of chronic interstitial lung disease","Genetic testing has largely replaced lung biopsy as the confirmatory investigation where the phenotype is suggestive","Family history of neonatal death or childhood interstitial lung disease supports a heritable surfactant disorder"],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonary surfactant lowers alveolar surface tension and prevents alveolar collapse. Mutations in SFTPB and SFTPC (encoding surfactant proteins B and C) or in ABCA3 (a lipid transporter required for surfactant assembly and packaging within lamellar bodies of type II pneumocytes) impair production, processing, or secretion of functional surfactant. The resulting surfactant dysfunction causes alveolar instability, impaired gas exchange, and accumulation of abnormal proteinaceous and lipid material, driving neonatal respiratory failure or a chronic fibrosing interstitial lung disease depending on the gene and specific variant.","additionalDifferentials":["Respiratory distress syndrome of prematurity","Neonatal pneumonia or sepsis","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Congenital pulmonary alveolar proteinosis from CSF2 receptor defects","Other childhood interstitial lung diseases (for example NKX2-1 related disease)","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of SFTPB, SFTPC and ABCA3","High-resolution chest computed tomography","Echocardiography to assess for pulmonary hypertension and exclude cardiac disease","Infection and sepsis screen to exclude mimics","Lung biopsy with electron microscopy in selected undiagnosed cases","Blood gas analysis and oxygenation assessment"],"redFlags":["Refractory hypoxaemic respiratory failure in a term neonate not responding to standard support","Progressive interstitial lung disease with declining exercise tolerance","Development of pulmonary hypertension","Family history of neonatal respiratory death"],"prognosis":"Surfactant protein B and severe ABCA3 deficiency are frequently fatal in infancy without lung transplantation, whereas SFTPC and milder ABCA3 disease follow a more variable chronic course; overall outcome depends heavily on the specific gene and mutation.","pitfalls":["Assuming term neonatal respiratory distress is simply delayed transition or infection and missing a genetic surfactant disorder","Overlooking a dominant SFTPC diagnosis because a parent has mild or subclinical lung disease","Attributing later-onset interstitial lung disease solely to environmental causes","Delaying transplant referral in severe neonatal forms"],"management":["Tailor definitive management to the gene: lung transplantation for severe neonatal surfactant protein B and ABCA3 deficiency, where exogenous surfactant gives only transient benefit","Trial empirical anti-inflammatory therapy such as corticosteroids, hydroxychloroquine or azithromycin in the chronic interstitial forms (surfactant protein C and milder ABCA3 disease)","Provide respiratory support with oxygen and mechanical ventilation, with extracorporeal membrane oxygenation as a bridge in refractory neonatal failure","Maintain immunisation, treat intercurrent infection promptly and optimise nutrition and growth","Monitor with serial high-resolution CT, pulmonary function testing and echocardiographic surveillance for pulmonary hypertension","Coordinate care across neonatology or respiratory medicine, an interstitial lung disease service and a transplant centre","Provide genetic counselling and family screening tailored to inheritance (autosomal recessive for SFTPB and ABCA3, autosomal dominant for SFTPC)"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["American Thoracic Society statement on childhood interstitial lung disease","Standard respiratory medicine and paediatric pulmonology texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"susac_syndrome":{"name":"Susac syndrome","system":"Rheumatology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["retinocochleocerebral vasculopathy","SICRET syndrome","Susac's syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by a clinical triad of encephalopathy, branch retinal artery occlusions, and sensorineural hearing loss, though all three are often not present at onset","Predominantly affects young women and may be misdiagnosed as multiple sclerosis or migraine with aura","Encephalopathy can include headache, confusion, cognitive and behavioural change, and focal deficits","MRI characteristically shows central callosal lesions described as 'snowball' or 'icicle' lesions, differing from the peripheral callosal lesions of multiple sclerosis","Fluorescein angiography reveals branch retinal artery occlusions and arterial wall hyperfluorescence even in asymptomatic segments","Audiometry typically shows low- to mid-frequency sensorineural hearing loss from cochlear microinfarction","Early aggressive immunosuppression aims to limit permanent cognitive, visual, and auditory damage"],"pathophysiology":"Susac syndrome is an immune-mediated microangiopathy (endotheliopathy) affecting the small vessels (precapillary arterioles) of the brain, retina, and inner ear. Antiendothelial cell antibodies and cytotoxic lymphocyte-mediated injury to microvascular endothelium are implicated, producing microvascular occlusion and ischaemic damage in these three organs. Involvement of the corpus callosum microvasculature yields the characteristic central callosal MRI lesions, retinal arteriolar occlusion causes visual deficits, and cochlear microinfarction produces hearing loss and vertigo.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple sclerosis and acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Primary central nervous system vasculitis","Cogan syndrome","Migraine with aura","Antiphospholipid syndrome and other thrombotic microangiopathies","Neuro-Behcet disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain looking for central corpus callosum 'snowball' lesions and leptomeningeal enhancement","Fluorescein angiography to detect branch retinal artery occlusions and arterial wall hyperfluorescence","Audiometry to identify and monitor sensorineural hearing loss","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis showing raised protein but usually without oligoclonal bands","Autoimmune and thrombophilia screen to exclude mimics","Serial ophthalmological review including retinal imaging"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive encephalopathy with cognitive decline","Acute visual loss from retinal artery occlusion","Sudden or progressive hearing loss","New focal neurological deficits indicating ongoing microinfarction"],"prognosis":"The disease is often self-limiting over months to a few years but can leave permanent cognitive impairment, visual field loss, and hearing deficits; early and sustained immunosuppression improves outcomes, though relapses occur.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as multiple sclerosis and missing the distinguishing central callosal lesions","Not performing fluorescein angiography, thereby missing subclinical retinal artery occlusions","Overlooking the diagnosis when the full triad is not present at onset","Under-treating early disease and permitting irreversible organ damage","Attributing cognitive symptoms to a psychiatric cause"],"management":["Institute prompt, aggressive immunosuppression with high-dose corticosteroids, usually combined with intravenous immunoglobulin, to halt the microvascular injury","Add steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as mycophenolate, azathioprine, rituximab or cyclophosphamide in severe or refractory encephalopathic disease","Escalate to pulse corticosteroids with intravenous immunoglobulin or cyclophosphamide during acute encephalopathic deterioration","Provide rehabilitation for cognitive deficits and hearing rehabilitation with hearing aids or cochlear implantation for sensorineural loss","Monitor disease activity with serial brain MRI, fluorescein angiography and audiometry, since subclinical relapse is common","Manage through a multidisciplinary team spanning neurology, ophthalmology and audiology or ENT","Continue prolonged immunosuppression and surveillance because relapses occur after apparent remission"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"sustained_ventricular_tachycardia":{"name":"Sustained Ventricular Tachycardia","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sustained monomorphic VT","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","Idiopathic VT","Belhassen's VT","Verapamil-sensitive VT","Bundle branch reentry VT","Outflow tract VT"],"chapter":261,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1963","clinicalPearls":["Sustained monomorphic VT is a wide-QRS ventricular arrhythmia lasting at least 30 seconds, or one requiring intervention (antitachycardia pacing or cardioversion) for termination; each QRS resembles the others, pointing to a single focal origin or repetitive exit from a fixed reentry circuit.","In structural heart disease the usual substrate is patchy replacement fibrosis from prior infarction, fibrosis, inflammation, or previous cardiac surgery, which sets up anatomic or functional reentry; less often VT arises from reentry or automaticity in diseased Purkinje conduction tissue.","Clinical tolerance depends on rate, baseline ventricular function, and autonomic response: rapid VT may cause hypotension and syncope (especially with poor LV function), while patients with normal function may have only palpitations despite fast rates.","Hemodynamic stability during a wide-complex tachycardia does NOT distinguish VT from supraventricular tachycardia with aberrancy; tolerance of the rhythm should never be used to exclude VT.","Ventriculoatrial (AV) dissociation is a reliable marker of VT, provided the atrial rate is slower than the ventricular rate; however a P wave following every QRS does not exclude VT because 1:1 ventriculo-atrial conduction can occur.","A monophasic R or Rs complex in lead aVR, or concordance (all monophasic R or all S waves) across V1 to V6, is relatively specific for VT.","In a patient with known structural heart disease, a wide-QRS tachycardia is most likely VT regardless of QRS morphology; depressed LV function or evidence of structural disease makes scar-related reentry the most likely mechanism of sustained monomorphic VT.","Scar is suggested by pathologic Q waves on ECG, segmental wall-motion abnormalities on echo or nuclear imaging, and areas of delayed gadolinium enhancement on cardiac MRI.","Idiopathic VT occurs in structurally normal hearts (normal sinus ECG, normal function and no scar on imaging), is provoked by sympathetic stimulation or acute sympathetic withdrawal (e.g. immediately post-exercise), and is generally benign with low risk of sudden death.","Outflow tract VT most often arises in the right ventricular outflow tract, producing a left bundle branch block pattern in V1 with an inferiorly directed axis (tall R waves in II, III, aVF); a prominent R wave in V1 or V2 suggests a left ventricular outflow tract or aortic-root origin.","LV fascicular VT (Belhassen's / verapamil-sensitive VT) is the second most common idiopathic form: it shows a right bundle branch block-like configuration negative in the inferior leads and is due to a small reentry circuit near the septal ramifications of the left Purkinje system; despite being reentrant its course is typically benign.","Repeated incessant bursts of nonsustained monomorphic VT (repetitive monomorphic VT) can produce a tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy with depressed ventricular function that recovers once the arrhythmia is suppressed."],"pathophysiology":"Sustained monomorphic VT most commonly results from reentry around a fixed substrate. In structural heart disease this substrate is an area of patchy replacement fibrosis (from infarction, inflammation, fibrosis, or surgical scar) creating anatomic or functional reentrant circuits; the uniform QRS reflects a single focal origin or repetitive exit from a stable circuit. In coronary artery disease the typical setting is a remote large myocardial infarction with a remodeled ventricle and depressed LV function, where the infarct scar provides a durable reentry substrate that is not generally dependent on ongoing acute ischemia. In nonischemic dilated cardiomyopathy, scar-related reentry from progressive replacement fibrosis underlies VT, with scars often intramural or subepicardial and frequently adjacent to a valve annulus; sarcoidosis, Chagas' disease, and Lamin A/C mutation cardiomyopathy are particularly associated. Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy involves fibrofatty replacement chiefly of right ventricular myocardium producing a left bundle branch block-like VT. Bundle branch reentry VT (about 5% of monomorphic VT with structural disease) uses a macroreentrant circuit through the Purkinje system, usually retrograde up the left bundle and anterograde down the right bundle, generating a left bundle branch block configuration. Idiopathic VT in structurally normal hearts arises either from focal triggered automaticity (outflow tract foci near the pulmonic or aortic annuli) or from small reentry circuits in the Purkinje system (fascicular VT). Rapid or scar-associated monomorphic VT may degenerate into ventricular fibrillation.","additionalDifferentials":["Supraventricular tachycardia with left or right bundle branch block aberrancy","Supraventricular tachycardia conducted to the ventricles over an accessory pathway (pre-excited tachycardia)","Rapid ventricular pacing (appropriate or inappropriate) in a patient with a pacemaker or defibrillator","Telemetry noise and movement artifact simulating VT"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["SVT with aberrant conduction (bundle branch block)","Antidromic AV reentrant tachycardia / pre-excited SVT over an accessory pathway","Pacemaker-mediated rapid ventricular pacing"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG during tachycardia analyzing AV dissociation, aVR morphology, and V1-V6 concordance to differentiate VT from SVT with aberrancy","Comparison of tachycardia QRS morphology with the baseline sinus-rhythm QRS (identical morphology in a known bundle branch block favors SVT but is not definitive)","Echocardiography to assess ventricular function and detect segmental wall-motion abnormalities indicating scar","Cardiac MRI for delayed gadolinium enhancement to identify and localize fibrosis (intramural/subepicardial in nonischemic cardiomyopathy)","Nuclear imaging for wall-motion abnormalities","Cardiac biomarkers (troponin, CK-MB) to evaluate myocardial injury, recognizing that acute MI rarely causes sustained monomorphic VT and modest elevations usually reflect demand ischemia during the arrhythmia rather than an acute coronary event","Electrophysiologic study when noninvasive criteria leave the diagnosis uncertain"],"redFlags":["Hypotension, impaired consciousness, or pulmonary edema accompanying the tachycardia (hemodynamic instability)","Rapid VT in a patient with significant ventricular dysfunction (high risk of syncope and degeneration to VF)","VT presenting as resuscitated out-of-hospital cardiac arrest where VF was the initial recorded rhythm","Sustained monomorphic VT in the setting of structural heart disease (high recurrence and sudden-death risk)","Frequent recurrent or incessant VT episodes","Syncope or cardiac arrest as the presenting event in inherited cardiomyopathies (ARVC, repaired tetralogy of Fallot)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis hinges on the presence and type of underlying heart disease. Scar-related monomorphic VT in structural heart disease carries a high recurrence risk; with ischemic cardiomyopathy and a prior large MI, up to 70% of patients have arrhythmia recurrence within 2 years, and even after device-based secondary prevention the depressed-ejection-fraction population remains at risk for heart failure, recurrent ischemic events, and recurrent VT, with a 5-year mortality exceeding 30%. Device shocks and antitachycardia pacing are themselves adverse events linked to higher rates of heart failure, mortality, and psychological stress. By contrast, idiopathic VT in a structurally normal heart is generally benign, sudden death is rare, and a defibrillator is not recommended; LV fascicular (Belhassen's) VT likewise follows a benign course despite being reentrant. In ARVC, clinical heart failure is rare until late stages and survival to advanced age can be expected provided the VT is controlled. In repaired tetralogy of Fallot the risk of sudden cardiac death may reach roughly 1% per year in adulthood by the fourth or fifth decade.","pitfalls":["Using hemodynamic stability to rule out VT - tolerance of the rhythm does not exclude VT.","Assuming a P wave after every QRS excludes VT, when 1:1 ventriculo-atrial conduction can mimic this.","Relying on a single QRS-morphology criterion; all such criteria have limitations and are unreliable in severe heart disease, and bundle branch reentry VT can closely resemble the native sinus QRS.","Interpreting troponin/CK-MB elevations during VT as proof of an acute coronary event - these usually reflect demand ischemia from hypotension and fixed coronary disease; acute MI is rarely the cause of sustained monomorphic VT.","Mistaking telemetry noise or movement artifact for VT, leading to unnecessary tests and interventions.","Attributing a typical outflow-tract QRS morphology automatically to benign idiopathic VT, since cardiomyopathies (notably ARVC) can produce PVCs/VT from the same region and must be excluded.","Failing to recognize repetitive monomorphic (incessant nonsustained) VT as a reversible cause of tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy."],"management":["Perform immediate synchronised direct-current cardioversion for haemodynamically unstable sustained ventricular tachycardia","For stable monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, use intravenous antiarrhythmics such as procainamide or amiodarone to terminate the arrhythmia","Correct reversible contributors including electrolyte disturbance and ischaemia, and optimise guideline-directed heart-failure therapy for the underlying cardiomyopathy","Implant a cardioverter-defibrillator for secondary prevention in structural heart disease, as recurrence risk is high","Suppress recurrent episodes with antiarrhythmic drugs such as amiodarone, sotalol or beta-blockers, with device antitachycardia pacing to terminate detected episodes","Offer catheter ablation of the scar-related reentry substrate for recurrent tachycardia or frequent device therapies","Refer to a cardiac electrophysiology service, and screen family members where an inherited cardiomyopathy is identified"],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies sustained ventricular arrhythmias by substrate/etiology rather than a graded severity scale: (1) idiopathic VT without structural heart disease - outflow tract (RV outflow: LBBB pattern in V1 with inferior axis and late precordial transition; LV outflow: inferior axis with early precordial transition and prominent R in V2-V3), LV fascicular VT (RBBB pattern in V1 with left axis deviation), and papillary muscle VT; (2) ischemic cardiomyopathy (monomorphic VT common after large prior MI); (3) nonischemic cardiomyopathy (fibrotic scar VT, e.g. sarcoidosis, Chagas', Lamin A/C); (4) arrhythmogenic RV cardiomyopathy (RV-origin LBBB-pattern VT); (5) repaired tetralogy of Fallot (RV-origin VT); (6) hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; and (7) genetic arrhythmia syndromes (long QT, Brugada, catecholaminergic polymorphic VT, short QT/early repolarization). Practically, the most important severity distinction is between benign idiopathic VT in a structurally normal heart and scar-related VT in structural heart disease, the latter conferring substantial sudden-death risk.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1963","Harrison 22e p.1964","Harrison 22e p.1965","Harrison 22e p.1966","Harrison 22e p.1967","Harrison 22e p.1968"],"crossLinks":[]},"swan_neck_deformity":{"name":"Swan-neck deformity","system":"Musculoskeletal / Hand & Peripheral nerve","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["swan neck deformity","proximal interphalangeal hyperextension deformity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by proximal interphalangeal joint hyperextension with distal interphalangeal joint flexion, the reciprocal of a boutonniere deformity","It has several possible origins, including a chronic mallet finger, volar plate laxity at the proximal interphalangeal joint, intrinsic muscle tightness, flexor digitorum superficialis insufficiency, and rheumatoid arthritis","The common final pathway is an extensor imbalance that favours proximal interphalangeal extension and distal interphalangeal flexion","Assessing proximal interphalangeal flexibility with the metacarpophalangeal joint held in different positions helps identify intrinsic tightness (the Bunnell test)","In rheumatoid disease, metacarpophalangeal volar subluxation and intrinsic contracture drive the deformity","The Nalebuff classification grades rheumatoid swan-neck deformity according to proximal interphalangeal flexibility and joint condition","Generic management ranges from figure-of-eight or dorsal-blocking splints for flexible deformities to surgical soft-tissue rebalancing or joint fusion or arthroplasty for severe or arthritic joints","Identifying and treating the underlying cause is essential rather than addressing the posture alone"],"pathophysiology":"Swan-neck deformity reflects an imbalance of the extensor mechanism producing proximal interphalangeal hyperextension and distal interphalangeal flexion. It may begin distally, where a chronic mallet finger allows extensor force to concentrate on the proximal interphalangeal joint and hyperextend it, or at the proximal interphalangeal joint itself through volar plate attenuation, flexor digitorum superficialis insufficiency or intrinsic muscle tightness, with the lateral bands displacing dorsally. It is common in rheumatoid arthritis, where synovitis, metacarpophalangeal joint subluxation and intrinsic contracture combine to create and perpetuate the deformity.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic mallet finger with a secondary swan-neck","Rheumatoid arthritis or other connective tissue disease such as lupus (Jaccoud arthropathy)","Spasticity-related intrinsic tightness, for example in cerebral palsy","Volar plate laxity associated with generalised joint hypermobility","Post-traumatic flexor digitorum superficialis injury","Malunion of a phalangeal fracture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of proximal interphalangeal flexibility with the metacarpophalangeal joint flexed and extended to detect intrinsic tightness","Plain radiographs to assess joint surfaces, arthritis and subluxation","Examination of the distal interphalangeal joint to identify an underlying mallet","Screening for rheumatoid arthritis or connective tissue disease when the deformity is atraumatic","Application of the Nalebuff classification in rheumatoid cases to guide treatment"],"redFlags":["An underlying inflammatory arthropathy requiring systemic disease-modifying treatment","A rigid, stiff or arthritic proximal interphalangeal joint that limits reconstructive options","Rapidly progressive deformity affecting multiple digits","Skin or joint compromise"],"prognosis":"The outlook varies with the cause and the flexibility of the joints; flexible deformities respond well to splinting and soft-tissue procedures, whereas established or arthritic deformities usually require joint surgery and give less predictable results.","pitfalls":["Treating the deformity without identifying the primary cause, whether mallet, volar plate or rheumatoid disease","Overlooking intrinsic muscle tightness as a contributor","Ignoring the metacarpophalangeal joint in rheumatoid arthritis","Attempting to splint a rigid joint that will not correct"],"management":["Treat the underlying cause, correcting a chronic mallet finger with distal interphalangeal extension splinting and controlling rheumatoid synovitis with disease-modifying therapy","Manage flexible deformities non-operatively with proximal interphalangeal extension-block splinting (such as figure-of-eight or silver-ring splints) and hand therapy","Release intrinsic tightness and rebalance the extensor mechanism with soft-tissue procedures such as flexor digitorum superficialis tenodesis or lateral band relocation for correctable deformity","Reserve proximal interphalangeal arthrodesis or arthroplasty for fixed or arthritic joints","Address associated metacarpophalangeal subluxation and intrinsic contracture in rheumatoid disease as part of the reconstruction","Use the Nalebuff classification to guide the choice of procedure in rheumatoid cases","Refer to a hand surgery and hand therapy service for assessment and postoperative rehabilitation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"sweet_syndrome":{"name":"Sweet syndrome (acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis)","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis","Sweet's syndrome","neutrophilic dermatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Bolognia Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with the abrupt onset of tender, red-to-violaceous plaques and nodules, often with fever and a raised neutrophil count","Lesions typically favour the face, neck, and upper limbs and may look oedematous or pseudovesicular ('juicy' plaques)","Histology shows a dense dermal infiltrate of neutrophils without evidence of true vasculitis","Recognised subtypes are classic or idiopathic (often post-infectious or associated with inflammatory bowel disease and pregnancy), malignancy-associated (notably acute myeloid leukaemia), and drug-induced","Drug-induced disease is classically linked to granulocyte colony-stimulating factor and several other medications","Pathergy may occur, with new lesions arising at sites of trauma, and extracutaneous neutrophilic involvement of eyes, joints, lungs, and other organs can accompany the skin disease","Responds briskly to systemic glucocorticoids, with steroid-sparing agents used for recurrent or refractory disease and treatment of any underlying trigger"],"pathophysiology":"Sweet syndrome is a reactive neutrophilic dermatosis in which activated neutrophils accumulate in the dermis, producing tender inflammatory plaques, fever, and systemic inflammation. It is thought to reflect a hypersensitivity-type response driven by cytokines and colony-stimulating factors that recruit and activate neutrophils, rather than by infection or primary vasculitis. Triggers include preceding infections, inflammatory and autoimmune diseases, pregnancy, certain drugs, and, importantly, underlying malignancy, most often myeloid haematological disorders, where the syndrome can be paraneoplastic. Dysregulated innate immune activation and possible clonal myeloid contributions underlie the neutrophil-rich inflammation.","additionalDifferentials":["Erythema nodosum and other panniculitides","Cutaneous leukocytoclastic vasculitis","Pyoderma gangrenosum (another neutrophilic dermatosis)","Cellulitis or other skin infection","Bullous or atypical erythema multiforme","Leukaemia cutis","Behcet disease with cutaneous lesions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing dense dermal neutrophilic infiltrate without vasculitis","Full blood count with differential for neutrophilia and to screen for haematological disease","Inflammatory markers such as ESR and CRP","Blood film and, where indicated, haematology referral to exclude myeloid malignancy","Review of recent medications, especially colony-stimulating factors","Investigations for associated infection, inflammatory bowel disease, or other trigger as guided by history"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting underlying acute myeloid leukaemia or myelodysplasia such as cytopenias or abnormal blood film","Systemic neutrophilic involvement of lungs, eyes, or central nervous system","Recurrent or treatment-resistant disease prompting a search for occult malignancy","Severe systemic upset with high fever mimicking sepsis"],"prognosis":"Classic and drug-induced disease usually responds rapidly to glucocorticoids and often resolves, though recurrences occur; malignancy-associated disease tends to follow the course of the underlying cancer and may relapse with disease activity.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing tender plaques as cellulitis and treating with antibiotics alone","Failing to investigate for an underlying haematological malignancy, particularly in recurrent or atypical cases","Overlooking a recent drug, such as a colony-stimulating factor, as the cause","Not recognising extracutaneous neutrophilic organ involvement"],"management":["Systemic corticosteroids are the mainstay and typically produce rapid resolution of skin lesions and systemic inflammation","Identify and remove or treat the trigger, withdrawing a culprit drug such as granulocyte colony-stimulating factor and treating associated infection or inflammatory bowel disease","Use steroid-sparing agents such as colchicine, dapsone or potassium iodide for recurrent, refractory or steroid-intolerant disease","Treat localised disease with potent topical or intralesional corticosteroids","Investigate for and manage an underlying malignancy, particularly acute myeloid leukaemia, since paraneoplastic disease tracks the cancer","Monitor for recurrence during corticosteroid tapering and with reactivation of any associated disease","Involve dermatology and, where malignancy is suspected, haematology in ongoing care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"swimming_induced_pulmonary_edema":{"name":"Swimming-induced pulmonary oedema","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SIPE","immersion pulmonary oedema"],"chapter":null,"source":"Diving and sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Acute pulmonary oedema arising during surface swimming or diving in the absence of water aspiration or a primary cardiac event","Presents with breathlessness, cough, frothy or blood-tinged sputum and hypoxaemia during or soon after immersion","It typically affects otherwise healthy swimmers, divers and triathletes, sometimes recurrently","Cold water, exertion, overhydration and tight wetsuits are recognised contributing factors","Symptoms usually improve rapidly on leaving the water and with oxygen and rest","Recurrence is common, and evaluation for hypertension or underlying cardiac disease is advised"],"pathophysiology":"Swimming-induced pulmonary oedema is attributed to the combination of immersion and exertion raising pulmonary capillary pressure. Water immersion and cold shift blood centrally and cause peripheral vasoconstriction, while exertion increases cardiac output and pulmonary blood flow; the resulting rise in pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure exceeds the threshold for fluid to leak into the alveoli. Factors that further raise preload or afterload, such as overhydration, cold and constrictive suits, and any underlying cardiac or hypertensive tendency, augment the pressure and precipitate oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Aspiration or near-drowning","Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema from primary cardiac disease","Pulmonary barotrauma or decompression illness in divers","Exercise-induced bronchoconstriction","Pneumonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment and oxygen saturation during and after the event","Chest imaging showing pulmonary oedema without aspiration","Cardiac evaluation including echocardiography for underlying disease","Blood pressure assessment for hypertension","Exclusion of diving-specific causes such as barotrauma in divers"],"redFlags":["Severe hypoxaemia or respiratory distress","Features suggesting a primary cardiac event","Haemoptysis with haemodynamic compromise","Recurrent episodes indicating an underlying cardiovascular disorder"],"prognosis":"The acute episode usually resolves quickly with removal from water, oxygen and rest, but recurrence is frequent and an identified cardiac or hypertensive predisposition needs management.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with near-drowning from aspiration","Overlooking an underlying cardiac or hypertensive cause","In divers, missing barotrauma or decompression illness","Underestimating the risk of recurrence with continued open-water activity"],"management":["Remove the person from the water immediately and give rest, warming and supplemental oxygen, which usually brings rapid resolution","Provide supportive care for hypoxaemia, escalating to non-invasive positive-pressure ventilation in severe cases","Advise on preventive measures including avoiding overhydration, over-tight wetsuits, cold exposure and excessive exertion","Evaluate for and treat underlying hypertension or cardiac disease, including echocardiography","Consider pharmacological prophylaxis such as sildenafil or an antihypertensive agent for recurrent episodes in susceptible individuals","Counsel on the high recurrence risk and advise on fitness to continue swimming or diving"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Diving and sports medicine references (standard reference)","Respiratory medicine reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"swyer_james_syndrome":{"name":"swyer james syndrome","system":"respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome","unilateral hyperlucent lung"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome is a form of post-infectious constrictive (obliterative) bronchiolitis producing a hyperlucent, hypoperfused lung or lobe that is usually smaller than normal.","It follows a severe lower respiratory infection in early childhood — often viral (adenovirus, measles) or Mycoplasma — that injures the developing distal airways and alveoli.","The classic chest radiograph shows unilateral hyperlucency with a small or normal-sized lung and reduced vascular markings, distinguishing it from compensatory overinflation.","Air trapping is the functional hallmark: expiratory imaging shows the affected side remaining lucent and failing to empty, sometimes with mediastinal shift on expiration.","CT typically reveals mosaic attenuation, bronchiectasis, reduced vessel calibre and areas of low attenuation reflecting the obliterated small airways.","Many patients are asymptomatic and diagnosed incidentally; others have recurrent infection, chronic cough, exertional dyspnoea or bronchiectasis.","Perfusion imaging demonstrates markedly reduced perfusion to the affected lung, matching the ventilation abnormality.","It must be distinguished from other causes of a unilateral hyperlucent lung, including endobronchial obstruction, congenital lobar emphysema, absent pulmonary artery and prior pneumonectomy or lobar disease."],"pathophysiology":"Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome arises when a severe lower respiratory infection during the period of ongoing lung growth in early childhood injures the terminal and respiratory bronchioles. Healing produces constrictive (obliterative) bronchiolitis, with fibrous narrowing and obliteration of small airways that impairs both ventilation and normal alveolar multiplication in the affected region. The damaged lung is chronically underventilated, and reflex and structural reduction of the pulmonary vascular bed leads to diminished perfusion and attenuated vessels, accounting for the radiographic hyperlucency. Collateral air drift allows air to enter distal units but the obliterated broncholes impede its egress, so air trapping develops and the affected lung fails to deflate normally on expiration. Because the insult occurs during development, the affected lung or lobe often remains smaller than normal, and coexisting bronchiectasis reflects proximal airway damage from the original infection and subsequent recurrent infection.","additionalDifferentials":["endobronchial obstruction by tumour or foreign body causing air trapping","congenital lobar emphysema","congenital absence or hypoplasia of a pulmonary artery","compensatory overinflation after contralateral lobar collapse or resection","bullous or panlobular emphysema (including alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency)","pulmonary embolism with regional oligaemia","technical or rotational causes of asymmetric radiographic lucency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["inspiratory and expiratory CT to demonstrate air trapping and mosaic attenuation","ventilation-perfusion scintigraphy showing matched reduction on the affected side","chest radiograph in inspiration and expiration","pulmonary function testing including assessment of airflow obstruction and gas trapping","bronchoscopy where endobronchial obstruction needs exclusion","CT pulmonary angiography if absent or hypoplastic pulmonary artery is considered","assessment for bronchiectasis-related infection with sputum culture"],"redFlags":["recurrent or severe lower respiratory infections with declining lung function","haemoptysis prompting exclusion of another cause of hyperlucency","progressive dyspnoea suggesting extension of disease or a superimposed process","new focal obstruction raising concern for an endobronchial lesion"],"prognosis":"Many patients remain stable and minimally symptomatic for life, but those with extensive bronchiectasis or recurrent infection may have progressive symptoms; management is largely supportive, with treatment of infection and, rarely, resection for a destroyed lobe causing intractable problems.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the hyperlucency as emphysema, compensatory overinflation or a technical artefact.","Omitting expiratory imaging, without which the diagnostic air trapping may be missed.","Overlooking an endobronchial obstruction that can mimic the appearance.","Failing to recognise and treat associated bronchiectasis and recurrent infection.","Assuming the small hyperlucent lung is congenitally absent tissue rather than post-infectious small-airway obliteration."],"management":["Management is largely supportive, as the obliterative bronchiolitis cannot be reversed","Treat respiratory infections promptly with antimicrobials and ensure influenza and pneumococcal immunisation to prevent further airway damage","Provide airway clearance physiotherapy and manage any associated bronchiectasis","Use inhaled bronchodilators where there is demonstrable airflow obstruction, and advise avoidance of smoking","Monitor with serial pulmonary function testing and review for recurrent infection or progressive symptoms","Reserve surgical resection such as lobectomy for a destroyed lobe causing intractable infection or haemoptysis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders, current edition","Murray and Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine, current edition","Standard thoracic radiology reference texts on the unilateral hyperlucent lung"],"crossLinks":[]},"swyer_syndrome":{"name":"Swyer syndrome (46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis)","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis","XY pure gonadal dysgenesis","46,XY CGD","XY female"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Endocrine Society clinical guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A 46,XY individual with an entirely female phenotype, normal or tall stature, unambiguous female external genitalia and a normally formed uterus and vagina","Bilateral streak gonads produce neither testosterone nor anti-mullerian hormone, so mullerian structures persist and no virilisation occurs","Typically presents with primary amenorrhoea and absent or arrested pubertal development in an otherwise phenotypically female adolescent","About 15 to 20 per cent of cases are caused by loss-of-function variants in the SRY gene, with other cases involving genes such as NR5A1, MAP3K1, DHH and duplications affecting the ovarian-determining pathway","Streak gonads carrying Y-chromosome material carry a substantial risk of gonadoblastoma and dysgerminoma, so gonadectomy of the dysgenetic gonads is recommended","Oestrogen and progestogen replacement is needed to induce and maintain secondary sexual characteristics and protect bone health","Because the uterus is present, pregnancy through oocyte donation and assisted reproduction is possible after hormonal preparation"],"pathophysiology":"In Swyer syndrome the karyotype is 46,XY but the bipotential gonad fails to differentiate into a testis, resulting in fibrous streak gonads. This failure of testis determination most often reflects disruption of the SRY gene or of downstream sex-determining transcription factors. Without functioning Sertoli cells there is no anti-mullerian hormone, so the mullerian ducts develop into a uterus, cervix and upper vagina; without Leydig cell testosterone the wolffian ducts regress and the external genitalia default to a female pattern. The streak gonads retain Y-derived germ cell material, which predisposes to malignant germ cell tumours, chiefly gonadoblastoma and dysgerminoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (absent uterus, testes present)","46,XX gonadal dysgenesis","Turner syndrome and its mosaic forms","Mullerian agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser)","Congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Ovotesticular disorder of sex development"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood karyotype with SRY analysis and, where indicated, gene panel testing (NR5A1, MAP3K1, DHH)","Serum gonadotropins showing hypergonadotropic hypogonadism with low oestradiol","Pelvic ultrasound or MRI to confirm a uterus and characterise streak gonads","Tumour markers and imaging surveillance for germ cell malignancy in retained gonads","Bone mineral density assessment given oestrogen deficiency","Histological examination of removed gonadal tissue"],"redFlags":["A palpable or radiologically enlarging gonadal mass suggesting gonadoblastoma or dysgerminoma","Signs of virilisation, which would suggest an alternative diagnosis or a hormonally active tumour","Severe osteoporosis or fragility fracture from untreated oestrogen deficiency","Delayed diagnosis leaving Y-bearing gonads in situ into adulthood"],"prognosis":"With timely gonadectomy of the dysgenetic gonads and appropriate hormone replacement, individuals have a good long-term outlook and can achieve pregnancy via oocyte donation. The main threat is malignant transformation of retained Y-bearing streak gonads, which is largely preventable with early gonadectomy.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the condition as simple delayed puberty and missing the karyotype","Leaving dysgenetic gonads with Y material in place and overlooking tumour risk","Confusing it with androgen insensitivity, in which no uterus is present","Neglecting bone protection and psychological support during hormone induction"],"management":["Perform gonadectomy of the dysgenetic streak gonads to eliminate the substantial risk of gonadoblastoma and dysgerminoma from retained Y-bearing tissue","Induce puberty with oestrogen and then maintain secondary sexual characteristics with combined oestrogen and progestogen replacement, the progestogen protecting the retained uterus","Offer assisted reproduction through oocyte or embryo donation, as the uterus is present and can support pregnancy","Monitor and protect bone health with bone mineral density assessment given oestrogen deficiency","Provide psychological support and address disclosure and identity issues","Offer genetic counselling and consider testing for SRY and related gene variants","Manage through a multidisciplinary team of reproductive endocrinology, gynaecology, clinical genetics and psychology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society clinical practice guidance on disorders of sex development"],"crossLinks":[]},"sydenham_chorea":{"name":"sydenham chorea","system":"nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chorea minor","rheumatic chorea","St Vitus dance"],"chapter":null,"source":"Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Post-streptococcal autoimmune chorea and the commonest cause of acquired chorea in childhood, typically arising 1-6 months after group A streptococcal pharyngitis.","One of the major Jones criteria for acute rheumatic fever, and may occur in isolation (pure chorea) when other manifestations have resolved.","Peaks between ages 5 and 15 with a female preponderance; onset is often subacute over days to weeks.","Clinical triad of chorea, hypotonia and emotional lability; obsessive-compulsive symptoms and attention difficulties frequently accompany the movement disorder.","Classic bedside signs include the 'milkmaid grip' (rhythmic squeezing on sustained grasp), 'darting' or serpentine tongue on protrusion, and pronator (spooning) sign with wrist flexion on arm elevation.","Chorea may be markedly asymmetrical or strictly unilateral (hemichorea) in up to a fifth of cases.","Antineuronal antibodies cross-reacting with basal ganglia epitopes are implicated; anti-streptolysin O and anti-DNase B titres may already be falling by the time chorea appears.","Secondary penicillin prophylaxis against recurrent streptococcal infection and rheumatic carditis is essential regardless of whether cardiac involvement is evident."],"pathophysiology":"Sydenham chorea is an autoimmune disorder triggered by group A beta-haemolytic streptococcal infection in a genetically susceptible host. Molecular mimicry between streptococcal antigens (notably M protein and N-acetyl-beta-D-glucosamine) and neuronal epitopes generates cross-reactive antibodies that bind surface antigens on basal ganglia neurons, particularly within the caudate and putamen. Antibody binding activates calcium/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase II signalling and disturbs dopaminergic neurotransmission, producing dysfunction of the striatal indirect pathway and the disinhibited, involuntary movements characteristic of chorea. Neuroimaging and functional studies show reversible striatal inflammation and hypermetabolism, correlating with the usually self-limiting course as the immune response wanes.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic lupus erythematosus and antiphospholipid antibody syndrome chorea","Chorea gravidarum and oral contraceptive-related chorea","Drug-induced chorea (neuroleptics, stimulants, anticonvulsants)","Benign hereditary chorea and other genetic choreas","Wilson disease","Tics and Tourette syndrome","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Throat swab culture and anti-streptolysin O / anti-DNase B titres","Echocardiography to detect subclinical rheumatic valvulitis","Inflammatory markers (ESR, C-reactive protein) and full blood count","Autoantibody screen (antinuclear, antiphospholipid antibodies) to exclude lupus","Serum caeruloplasmin and copper studies to exclude Wilson disease","MRI brain (often normal or showing reversible striatal signal change)","Electrocardiogram for evidence of rheumatic carditis"],"redFlags":["Signs of active carditis (new murmur, cardiac failure, pericardial rub) requiring urgent cardiology assessment","Severe generalised chorea causing dehydration, exhaustion or injury (chorea paralytica)","Fever, encephalopathy or focal deficits suggesting an alternative encephalitic process","Marked psychiatric disturbance or self-harm risk from emotional lability","Recurrence of chorea suggesting inadequate streptococcal prophylaxis"],"prognosis":"Most cases are self-limiting and resolve over weeks to months, though symptoms may persist up to two years and recur in a third of patients, particularly during pregnancy or with oestrogen exposure. Long-term outcome is dominated by the risk of rheumatic heart disease, making secondary antibiotic prophylaxis the key determinant of prognosis.","pitfalls":["Attributing fidgetiness or emotional change to behavioural problems and missing an evolving chorea.","Excluding rheumatic fever because pharyngitis was not recalled or streptococcal titres have already normalised.","Omitting echocardiography because a murmur is not audible, thereby missing subclinical valvulitis.","Failing to institute or continue secondary penicillin prophylaxis, risking carditis with recurrent infection.","Over-sedating with dopamine antagonists when symptoms are mild and would settle spontaneously."],"management":["Eradicate group A streptococcus with a penicillin course and start long-term secondary antibiotic prophylaxis to prevent recurrence and rheumatic heart disease","Treat disabling chorea symptomatically with dopamine-blocking agents such as haloperidol or risperidone, or antiepileptics such as sodium valproate or carbamazepine","Reserve immunomodulation with corticosteroids, intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange for severe or refractory chorea","Screen for and manage associated rheumatic carditis with echocardiography","Address accompanying obsessive-compulsive symptoms, emotional lability and attention difficulties","Provide a safe, low-stimulation environment and supportive care during the movement disorder","Monitor for relapse, including with pregnancy or oestrogen exposure, and coordinate paediatric neurology and cardiology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","World Heart Federation criteria for acute rheumatic fever"],"crossLinks":[]},"symmetrical_drug_related_intertriginous_exanthema":{"name":"Symmetrical drug-related intertriginous and flexural exanthema (SDRIFE)","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SDRIFE","baboon syndrome","symmetrical drug-related intertriginous and flexural exanthema","systemic contact-type baboon syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as symmetrical, sharply demarcated erythema of the buttocks and perianal or genital area, evoking the historical term baboon syndrome","There is additional symmetrical involvement of at least one other flexural or intertriginous site such as the groins, axillae or antecubital fossae","By definition it follows exposure to a systemic drug, without a preceding external contact allergen at the affected sites","Commonly implicated agents include beta-lactam antibiotics and other antimicrobials, and the reaction often occurs after the first systemic exposure","Characteristically there is an absence of systemic symptoms and no significant fever or organ involvement, distinguishing it from more serious drug reactions","The palms, soles and face are usually spared, and mucosal surfaces are not involved","Histology is non-specific, showing a mild superficial perivascular dermatitis, so diagnosis rests on the clinical pattern and drug history","Management is withdrawal of the responsible drug, after which the eruption settles, with topical corticosteroids to relieve symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"Symmetrical drug-related intertriginous and flexural exanthema is regarded as a systemically induced delayed-type (type IV) hypersensitivity reaction to a drug, without the need for prior sensitisation through skin contact. A systemically administered drug or its metabolite is thought to reach the flexural skin and provoke a T-cell-mediated inflammatory response, with the characteristic predilection for the buttocks and major body folds possibly reflecting local factors such as occlusion, sweat, and a higher density of eccrine structures or drug delivery to these sites. The reaction is confined to the skin, and the lack of systemic features and organ involvement distinguishes it mechanistically from severe cutaneous adverse reactions.","additionalDifferentials":["Allergic or irritant intertrigo","Inverse (flexural) psoriasis","Candidal or dermatophyte intertrigo","Allergic contact dermatitis to a topical agent","Acute generalised exanthematous pustulosis in flexural sites","Erythrasma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed systemic drug history correlating exposure with onset","Clinical mapping of the symmetrical flexural and gluteal distribution","Skin swabs or scrapings to exclude candidal, dermatophyte or bacterial intertrigo","Skin biopsy in atypical cases to exclude other flexural dermatoses","Patch and, where appropriate, systemic provocation testing after recovery to confirm the culprit","Assessment for systemic features to exclude a more serious drug reaction"],"redFlags":["Development of pustulation, systemic upset or fever suggesting an alternative severe drug reaction","Mucosal involvement or skin detachment inconsistent with the diagnosis","Failure to settle after drug withdrawal","Signs of secondary infection in the affected folds"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent, with resolution over one to two weeks once the responsible drug is stopped and no long-term sequelae, although the reaction will recur on re-exposure.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for an infective or inverse-psoriasis intertrigo and missing the drug cause","Failing to recognise that no external contact allergen is required for the flexural eruption","Overlooking that recurrence follows re-exposure to the culprit drug","Confusing it with acute generalised exanthematous pustulosis when pustules develop"],"management":["Identify and withdraw the responsible systemic drug, often a beta-lactam antibiotic, which is the definitive step","Relieve symptoms with topical corticosteroids and emollients, and antihistamines for pruritus","Document the drug allergy and advise avoidance of the culprit and cross-reactive agents to prevent recurrence","Confirm the culprit where needed with patch testing or supervised provocation after recovery","Reassure the patient given the benign, self-limiting course that settles within a couple of weeks of withdrawal","Assess for systemic features to exclude a more serious cutaneous adverse drug reaction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","British Association of Dermatologists guidance on cutaneous drug reactions"],"crossLinks":[]},"symmetrical_drug_related_intertriginous_flexural_exanthema":{"name":"Symmetrical drug-related intertriginous and flexural exanthema","system":"Skin","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SDRIFE","baboon syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distinctive drug eruption with symmetrical erythema of the gluteal/perianal area and the major flexures","The gluteal V-shaped erythema gives the historical name baboon syndrome","It occurs after systemic exposure to a drug, without prior sensitisation at those sites","There are typically no systemic symptoms and no mucosal involvement","Common culprits include beta-lactam antibiotics and other systemic drugs","It resolves on stopping the culprit drug and has a benign course"],"pathophysiology":"Symmetrical drug-related intertriginous and flexural exanthema is a T-cell-mediated delayed hypersensitivity reaction to a systemically administered drug (or occasionally systemically absorbed contact allergen). For reasons that are not fully understood, the reaction localises symmetrically to the gluteal region and flexures. The immune response produces the characteristic well-demarcated erythema without the systemic features or mucosal involvement of more severe cutaneous adverse reactions, and it settles when the drug is withdrawn.","additionalDifferentials":["Intertrigo","Inverse psoriasis","Allergic contact dermatitis","Fixed drug eruption","Other drug exanthems"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Drug history correlating onset with a systemic drug","Clinical recognition of the symmetrical flexural distribution","Skin biopsy in atypical cases","Patch or drug testing where confirmation is needed","Exclusion of intertrigo and inverse psoriasis"],"redFlags":["Systemic symptoms or mucosal involvement suggesting a more serious reaction","Extensive or atypical distribution","Features suggesting a severe cutaneous adverse reaction","Failure to resolve after stopping the drug"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limiting, clearing within days to a couple of weeks after the culprit drug is discontinued.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with intertrigo or inverse psoriasis","Failing to identify the systemic drug trigger","Overtreating a benign self-limiting eruption","Not documenting the culprit for future avoidance"],"management":["Discontinue the offending systemic drug, commonly a beta-lactam antibiotic, which leads to resolution","Provide symptomatic treatment with topical corticosteroids and emollients","Record the culprit drug as an allergy and counsel on avoiding re-exposure, since the eruption recurs on rechallenge","Confirm causality with patch or drug testing when the culprit is uncertain","Exclude intertrigo, inverse psoriasis and more serious cutaneous drug reactions","Reassure regarding the benign, self-limiting prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dermatology reference texts (standard reference)","Drug eruption references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"sympathetic_ophthalmia":{"name":"Sympathetic ophthalmia","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sympathetic ophthalmitis","sympathetic uveitis","SO"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare bilateral granulomatous panuveitis that follows penetrating ocular trauma or intraocular surgery to one eye","The injured eye is termed the exciting eye and the fellow, initially uninjured eye that becomes inflamed is the sympathising eye","Onset is usually within weeks to months of the inciting injury, though it can occur many years later","Classic fundus findings include Dalen-Fuchs nodules (yellow-white deposits at the level of the retinal pigment epithelium) and a diffuse choroiditis","Presentation may include bilateral blurred vision, photophobia, pain and, in some cases, features overlapping with Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada disease","Management principles rely on prompt, high-intensity systemic corticosteroids followed by steroid-sparing systemic immunosuppression to preserve the sympathising eye","Early enucleation of a blind, severely injured exciting eye may be considered to prevent onset, but once inflammation begins its benefit is uncertain","Lifelong monitoring is needed because the disease is chronic and prone to recurrent flares"],"pathophysiology":"Sympathetic ophthalmia is a T-cell-mediated autoimmune granulomatous inflammation directed against previously sequestered uveal and retinal antigens. Penetrating trauma or surgery exposes these ocular antigens to the systemic immune system, breaking immune privilege and sensitising the host. The resulting autoreactive response attacks the uvea of both eyes, producing a bilateral granulomatous panuveitis characterised by diffuse choroidal infiltration by lymphocytes and epithelioid cells and by Dalen-Fuchs nodules. Because the response targets shared antigens, the uninjured sympathising eye is affected in parallel with the exciting eye.","additionalDifferentials":["Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada disease","Sarcoid uveitis","Tuberculous or syphilitic granulomatous uveitis","Phacoanaphylactic (lens-induced) uveitis","Intraocular lymphoma masquerade","Post-operative or post-traumatic endophthalmitis","Birdshot chorioretinopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fundus fluorescein angiography showing multiple early hyperfluorescent leakage points and late pooling","Indocyanine green angiography demonstrating hypofluorescent choroidal lesions","Optical coherence tomography to detect subretinal fluid and choroidal thickening","Fundus autofluorescence to map Dalen-Fuchs nodules and outer retinal changes","Ultrasonography where media are opaque, to assess choroidal thickening","Systemic and infective screening (including tuberculosis, syphilis and sarcoidosis) to exclude mimics"],"redFlags":["Any new inflammation in the fellow eye after penetrating trauma or surgery, which is sympathetic ophthalmia until proven otherwise","Rapidly progressive bilateral visual loss requiring urgent immunosuppression","Exudative retinal detachment threatening the macula","Development of the disease in a patient's only functioning eye"],"prognosis":"With prompt and sustained immunosuppression, many patients retain useful vision, but the disease is chronic and relapsing; delayed treatment or recurrent flares can lead to severe bilateral visual loss and secondary complications such as cataract, glaucoma and macular scarring.","pitfalls":["Attributing fellow-eye inflammation to something benign and missing early sympathetic ophthalmia","Under-dosing or prematurely tapering immunosuppression and precipitating a relapse","Assuming enucleation of the exciting eye after onset will halt established disease","Failing to exclude infective and neoplastic mimics before committing to long-term immunosuppression","Overlooking the risk following seemingly minor intraocular surgery, not just major trauma"],"management":["Institute prompt high-intensity systemic corticosteroids to control the bilateral granulomatous panuveitis and preserve the sympathising eye","Add steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as mycophenolate, azathioprine, methotrexate or ciclosporin, with TNF-inhibitor biologics for refractory disease","Use topical corticosteroids and cycloplegics as adjuncts for anterior segment inflammation","Consider enucleation of a blind, painful exciting eye, while exhausting the visual potential before removing any seeing eye","Exclude infective mimics such as tuberculosis, syphilis and sarcoidosis before and during immunosuppression","Monitor for and manage complications including cataract, glaucoma and macular scarring, and screen for immunosuppressant toxicity","Manage under an ophthalmology and uveitis specialist with long-term surveillance for relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina"],"crossLinks":[]},"sympathomimetic_toxidrome":{"name":"Sympathomimetic toxidrome","system":"Multisystem / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["adrenergic toxidrome","stimulant toxidrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard toxicology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by agitation, tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, dilated pupils and prominent sweating","Typical causes include cocaine, amphetamines and related stimulants and some decongestants","The presence of sweating and usually preserved bowel sounds distinguishes it from the dry anticholinergic toxidrome","Benzodiazepines are the cornerstone of treatment, controlling agitation, tachycardia and hypertension","Complications include seizures, myocardial ischaemia, hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis and intracranial haemorrhage","Traditional teaching cautions against beta-blocker monotherapy in cocaine toxicity because of concern about unopposed alpha stimulation"],"pathophysiology":"The sympathomimetic toxidrome results from excessive stimulation of adrenergic receptors through increased release or reduced reuptake of catecholamines, or direct receptor agonism. The resulting adrenergic overdrive raises heart rate, blood pressure and temperature, dilates pupils and stimulates sweating, while central stimulation causes agitation and can provoke seizures. End-organ complications arise from vasoconstriction and increased metabolic demand, including myocardial ischaemia, cerebral haemorrhage and hyperthermia with rhabdomyolysis.","additionalDifferentials":["Anticholinergic toxidrome","Serotonin syndrome","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Thyroid storm","Alcohol or sedative withdrawal","Sepsis or CNS infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical recognition and a substance-use history","Electrocardiogram and cardiac assessment for ischaemia and arrhythmia","Temperature, creatine kinase and renal function for hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis","Neuroimaging if focal signs or severe headache suggest intracranial haemorrhage","Glucose and electrolytes to exclude mimics"],"redFlags":["Chest pain or electrocardiographic evidence of ischaemia","Severe hyperthermia","Seizures or focal neurological signs","Uncontrolled hypertension with end-organ compromise"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover with sedation and supportive care, but severe hyperthermia, myocardial infarction, intracranial haemorrhage or status epilepticus can be fatal.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with the anticholinergic toxidrome (sweaty versus dry skin)","Under-sedating agitated patients and allowing hyperthermia to progress","Overlooking myocardial ischaemia or intracranial haemorrhage","Giving beta-blocker monotherapy in cocaine toxicity against traditional caution"],"management":["Give benzodiazepines as first-line treatment to control agitation, tachycardia, hypertension and seizures","Treat significant hyperthermia with rapid external cooling and manage rhabdomyolysis with intravenous fluids","Control persistent hypertension or myocardial ischaemia with vasodilators such as nitrates or phentolamine, avoiding beta-blocker monotherapy in suspected cocaine toxicity because of unopposed alpha stimulation","Provide supportive care with cardiac monitoring and correction of glucose and electrolyte abnormalities","Consider gastrointestinal decontamination for recent oral ingestion or drug body-packing","Investigate and treat complications including seizures, arrhythmia and intracranial haemorrhage","Involve a clinical toxicology or poisons service for guidance in severe cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"synchysis_scintillans":{"name":"Synchysis scintillans","system":"Special senses / Eye (vitreous)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cholesterolosis bulbi","cholesterol crystals of the vitreous"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consists of cholesterol crystals that form within a liquefied (synchytic) vitreous, characteristically in an eye already damaged by previous trauma, chronic vitreous haemorrhage or long-standing severe intraocular disease","Crystals are flat, angular and golden-brown or multicoloured with a highly refractile glitter, in contrast to the round white spheres of asteroid hyalosis","Because the vitreous is liquefied the crystals move freely and settle inferiorly under gravity when the eye is still, then swirl through the cavity on movement like a snow-globe","Often unilateral when secondary to prior injury or haemorrhage in one eye, but may be bilateral where systemic or metabolic factors contribute","Typically arises in an eye with vision already reduced by the underlying disease; the crystals themselves seldom cause additional visual loss","Cholesterol crystals may also accumulate in the anterior chamber (cholesterolosis) in a blind or severely damaged eye","Diagnosis is essentially clinical, and management is directed at the underlying condition rather than at the inert crystals themselves"],"pathophysiology":"Synchysis scintillans, or cholesterolosis bulbi, develops when cholesterol released from the breakdown of blood or degenerating tissue after haemorrhage, trauma or chronic intraocular disease crystallises within a vitreous that has already undergone liquefaction. Loss of the normal gel structure allows the dense crystals to move freely and to sediment inferiorly under gravity between eye movements. It therefore signifies a previously diseased eye, distinguishing it from asteroid hyalosis, in which calcium-phospholipid bodies remain suspended in a still-formed gel and do not settle.","additionalDifferentials":["Asteroid hyalosis (calcium soaps suspended in a formed gel that do not sediment)","Vitreous or anterior chamber haemorrhage","Vitreous amyloidosis","Inflammatory vitreous cells or uveitis","Retained intraocular foreign body or changes of long-standing retinal detachment","Crystalline retinopathies with intraretinal deposits"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp and dilated fundus examination noting settling of crystals inferiorly at rest","B-scan ultrasonography demonstrating mobile echoes that settle with gravity, particularly where the fundus view is poor","Detailed history and examination for previous trauma, haemorrhage or chronic intraocular disease","Examination of the fellow eye to assess laterality and any systemic pattern","Investigation of the underlying cause of recurrent or chronic vitreous haemorrhage where relevant","Fundus imaging where the media are clear enough to document the crystals and underlying retina"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or new vitreous haemorrhage indicating active underlying disease such as proliferative retinopathy or a retinal tear","Signs of a retained intraocular foreign body or occult penetrating trauma","A painful blind eye raising concern about other end-stage complications","New visual symptoms in the fellow eye"],"prognosis":"The cholesterol crystals themselves are inert and generally do not threaten vision; the visual outcome is determined almost entirely by the underlying ocular disease that gave rise to them. Many affected eyes already have poor vision from the causative pathology.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with asteroid hyalosis and missing the clue that a liquefied vitreous with sedimenting crystals signifies a previously diseased eye","Overlooking a treatable underlying cause of recurrent vitreous haemorrhage","Attempting to treat the crystals rather than the primary pathology","Failing to examine the fellow eye and assess for systemic contributors"],"management":["No treatment is directed at the inert cholesterol crystals themselves, and asymptomatic eyes are simply observed","Direct management at the underlying ocular disease, treating the cause of recurrent or chronic vitreous haemorrhage and the sequelae of prior trauma","Reserve pars plana vitrectomy for cases where crystals or media opacity obscure the view or where vitreoretinal pathology needs surgical treatment","Investigate the cause of recurrent vitreous haemorrhage where relevant","Monitor the affected eye and examine the fellow eye to assess laterality and any systemic contribution","Set realistic expectations, as visual prognosis is determined by the underlying disease rather than the crystals"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","Ryan's Retina"],"crossLinks":[]},"syndactyly":{"name":"Syndactyly","system":"Paediatric orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["webbed fingers","simple syndactyly","complex syndactyly","complete syndactyly","incomplete syndactyly"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery / Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Syndactyly is failure of separation of adjacent digits and is the commonest congenital hand difference; it is classified as simple (soft tissue only) or complex (with bony fusion), and as complete (to the fingertips) or incomplete","Complicated syndactyly involves additional or abnormal bones, tendons, or neurovascular structures beyond a simple bony bridge","The third web space (long-ring) is most often affected; the condition is frequently bilateral and may be familial (autosomal dominant with variable penetrance)","Border-digit and unequal-length pairings (such as ring-little or thumb-index) should be separated earlier, because tethering causes progressive angular and rotational deformity as the digits grow at different rates","Syndromic links include Apert syndrome (complex symmetrical syndactyly producing a 'mitten hand'), Poland syndrome (with chest-wall and pectoral deficiency), and constriction ring (amniotic band) sequence","Surgical separation uses interdigitating (zig-zag) incisions to resurface the sides of the digits and reconstructs the web (commissure) with local flaps, frequently supplemented by skin grafts","The two sides of a single digit are generally not released at the same operation, so as to protect that digit's blood supply"],"pathophysiology":"Syndactyly results from failure of the normal programmed cell death (apoptosis) that separates the digital rays during limb development, so adjacent digits remain joined by skin and, in complex forms, by bone. It represents a failure of differentiation and may occur in isolation, be inherited, or form part of a syndrome in which broader signalling defects affect both the hand and other structures, as in the acrocephalosyndactyly of Apert syndrome or the vascular disruption of constriction ring sequence.","additionalDifferentials":["Polysyndactyly (syndactyly combined with duplication)","Constriction ring (amniotic band) sequence with acrosyndactyly","Symphalangism (congenitally stiff interphalangeal joints)","Camptodactyly or clinodactyly (angular deformity without fusion)","Acquired web contracture from burn or trauma","Cleft hand with abnormal web spaces"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Radiographs to distinguish simple from complex (bony) syndactyly and to define shared skeletal structures","Careful clinical assessment of the affected web pairing and relative digit lengths","Screening for syndromic features (craniofacial in Apert, chest wall in Poland)","Vascular assessment and staged planning for complex or complete syndactyly","Genetic evaluation where the pattern is syndromic or strongly familial"],"redFlags":["Complex or complicated syndactyly with a shared neurovascular bundle risking devascularisation at separation","Progressive deformity of border digits from tethered unequal growth","Features of Apert or another syndrome requiring multidisciplinary care"],"prognosis":"Simple isolated syndactyly has excellent results after zig-zag separation and web reconstruction; complex and syndromic forms, especially Apert syndrome, are more likely to need multiple procedures and to leave residual stiffness, distal migration of the web ('web creep'), and imperfect appearance.","pitfalls":["Releasing both sides of the same digit at one operation and compromising its blood supply","Delaying separation of unequal-length border digits, allowing fixed deformity to develop","Reconstructing the web too shallow or narrow, leading to web creep","Underestimating shared bony or neurovascular anatomy in complex syndactyly"],"management":["Definitive treatment is surgical separation using interdigitating zig-zag incisions with reconstruction of the web commissure by local flaps, frequently supplemented by skin grafts","Time surgery by anatomy, separating border-digit and unequal-length pairs earlier to prevent progressive angular and rotational deformity, and avoiding operating on both sides of a digit at once to protect its blood supply","Plan complex or bony syndactyly carefully with attention to shared skeletal, tendon and neurovascular structures","Provide postoperative splinting, hand therapy and scar management, and monitor for web creep and joint contracture","Screen for and manage syndromic associations such as Apert and Poland syndromes","Offer genetic evaluation and counselling where the pattern is syndromic or strongly familial","Manage through a paediatric hand and upper-limb surgical service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"syne1_ataxia":{"name":"SYNE1-Related Ataxia","system":"Neurology / Hereditary ataxia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ARCA1","SCAR8","Autosomal recessive cerebellar ataxia type 1","Recessive ataxia of Beauce"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive ataxia caused by biallelic SYNE1 mutations affecting nesprin-1, a component of the LINC complex that tethers the nuclear envelope to the cytoskeleton.","Originally described as a relatively pure, adult-onset, slowly progressive cerebellar ataxia in a French-Canadian population (the recessive ataxia of Beauce).","Dysarthria is common and often prominent, accompanying gait and limb ataxia and cerebellar oculomotor signs such as nystagmus and saccadic pursuit.","Beyond the pure cerebellar form, an expanded multisystem phenotype includes upper and lower motor neuron signs, brainstem involvement and occasional cognitive change, reported more often outside Quebec.","Brain MRI typically shows cerebellar atrophy, with relative preservation of other structures in the pure form.","SYNE1 is an exceptionally large gene, and distinct mutations underlie different disorders including arthrogryposis and Emery-Dreifuss-like presentations.","Tendon reflexes may be normal or brisk when pyramidal involvement is present in the complex phenotype.","Peripheral sensation is often relatively spared compared with several other autosomal recessive ataxias."],"pathophysiology":"SYNE1 encodes nesprin-1, a giant spectrin-repeat protein of the outer nuclear membrane that, together with SUN-domain partners, forms the LINC complex physically coupling the nucleoskeleton to the cytoskeleton. Nesprin-1 is highly expressed in cerebellar Purkinje cells and is also enriched at the neuromuscular junction, where it contributes to nuclear positioning and cytoskeletal organisation. Loss of functional nesprin-1 disrupts these structural links, and the resulting impairment of nuclear anchoring and cytoskeletal integrity is associated with degeneration of the cerebellar cortex, producing the characteristic ataxia. In the expanded phenotypes, additional involvement of motor neurons and brainstem structures is thought to reflect the wider distribution and function of nesprin-1, accounting for the pyramidal, lower motor neuron and bulbar features that can accompany the cerebellar syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal recessive spastic ataxia of Charlevoix-Saguenay","Ataxia with oculomotor apraxia types 1 and 2","Sporadic adult-onset cerebellar ataxia and multiple system atrophy of cerebellar type","Autosomal dominant spinocerebellar ataxias","Friedreich ataxia","Motor neuron disease when lower and upper motor neuron signs dominate","Acquired ataxias including alcohol-related, gluten and paraneoplastic causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI assessing cerebellar atrophy","SYNE1 sequencing accounting for its very large size, or exome/recessive ataxia panel","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to assess neuropathy and motor neuron involvement","Creatine kinase where muscle or neuromuscular junction involvement is suspected","Screening for acquired causes of ataxia","Cognitive assessment where a complex multisystem phenotype is suspected"],"redFlags":["Emergence of prominent lower motor neuron or bulbar signs suggesting motor neuron involvement","Respiratory or swallowing compromise","Rapid deterioration atypical for the usually slow cerebellar course"],"prognosis":"The disorder is usually slowly progressive with relatively preserved lifespan, particularly in the pure cerebellar form. Complex multisystem forms with motor neuron involvement may carry greater disability.","pitfalls":["Assuming SYNE1 ataxia is always a pure cerebellar syndrome, when motor neuron and brainstem involvement can occur.","Missing the diagnosis because the very large gene poses technical sequencing challenges.","Mistaking complex SYNE1 phenotypes for primary motor neuron disease.","Assuming the disorder is confined to French-Canadian populations, when it is reported worldwide."],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so care is supportive and directed at symptoms","Provide physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain gait, balance and function, with mobility aids and falls prevention","Offer speech and language therapy for dysarthria and assessment of swallowing safety","Manage features of the expanded phenotype such as spasticity and lower motor neuron involvement symptomatically","Monitor for progression of swallowing, respiratory and motor-neuron complications over time","Offer genetic counselling and family testing given autosomal recessive inheritance","Coordinate long-term care through a neurology-led multidisciplinary ataxia service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics reference texts","Hereditary ataxia consensus reviews","Clinical neurology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"syngap1_encephalopathy":{"name":"SYNGAP1-related intellectual disability","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SYNGAP1","SYNGAP1-related developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","SYNGAP1 encephalopathy","mental retardation autosomal dominant 5 (MRD5)"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Combines moderate to severe intellectual disability, autism spectrum features and a distinctive epilepsy","The epilepsy is generalised, typically with myoclonic-atonic (drop) seizures, atypical absences and eyelid myoclonia often resistant to treatment","Reflex seizures triggered by eating (and sometimes by other oral or tactile stimuli) are a suggestive clue","Early hypotonia, ataxic or unsteady gait, and marked feeding and oromotor difficulties are common","Behavioural problems include autistic features, aggression, high pain threshold and disordered sleep","Caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in SYNGAP1, usually de novo and autosomal dominant, encoding a synaptic Ras/Rap GTPase-activating protein","Language is often more impaired than expected for the overall cognitive level"],"pathophysiology":"SYNGAP1 encodes a synaptic Ras/Rap GTPase-activating protein highly enriched at excitatory glutamatergic postsynaptic densities, where it regulates AMPA-receptor trafficking and the maturation of dendritic spines. Haploinsufficiency from loss-of-function variants disturbs the balance of synaptic excitation, accelerating spine maturation and impairing synaptic plasticity underlying learning. The resulting cortical hyperexcitability and abnormal circuit development produce the intellectual disability, autistic features and generalised, frequently reflex-triggered epilepsy. The gene is dosage-sensitive, so a single functional copy is insufficient for normal synaptic function.","additionalDifferentials":["Myoclonic-atonic epilepsy (Doose syndrome)","Lennox-Gastaut syndrome","Angelman syndrome","Other autism spectrum disorders with epilepsy","STXBP1 or SLC6A1-related encephalopathy","Idiopathic generalised epilepsy with intellectual disability"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["EEG, including capture during eating to document reflex seizures","Brain MRI, which is often normal or shows non-specific changes","Chromosomal microarray to detect 6p21 deletions involving SYNGAP1","Gene panel or exome sequencing for SYNGAP1 and other epileptic-encephalopathy genes","Developmental, speech and autism assessment"],"redFlags":["Frequent drop attacks risking injury","Status epilepticus or clusters of atypical absences","Feeding difficulty with aspiration or faltering growth","Escalating aggression or self-injury affecting safety"],"prognosis":"Most individuals have lifelong intellectual disability and ongoing, often drug-resistant epilepsy, with variable but usually limited functional independence. Seizure burden and behaviour tend to be the main determinants of quality of life, and care is supportive and multidisciplinary.","pitfalls":["Attributing the epilepsy to a generic idiopathic syndrome and not testing for SYNGAP1","Missing eating-triggered reflex seizures because EEG is not recorded around meals","Assuming a normal MRI excludes a monogenic cause","Underestimating the disproportionate language and communication impairment"],"management":["Treat the generalised epilepsy with broad-spectrum antiseizure medication such as sodium valproate, lamotrigine, levetiracetam or ethosuximide, adding the ketogenic diet for drug-resistant seizures","Recognise and address reflex seizures triggered by eating and other oral or tactile stimuli","Provide early developmental intervention with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech and language therapy and special educational support","Manage behavioural and autistic features, aggression and sleep disturbance, using melatonin for sleep, and remain alert to a high pain threshold masking injury","Support feeding and oromotor difficulties with dietetic and feeding-therapy input","Offer genetic counselling given the usually de novo, autosomal dominant, dosage-sensitive loss-of-function variants","Coordinate care through neurology, clinical genetics and neurodevelopmental services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: SYNGAP1-Related Intellectual Disability","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"synovial_chondromatosis":{"name":"Synovial chondromatosis","system":"Rheumatology / Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["synovial osteochondromatosis","primary synovial chondromatosis","Reichel syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign proliferative disorder in which the synovium undergoes cartilaginous metaplasia, forming multiple cartilage nodules that detach as loose bodies","Typically monoarticular, most often affecting the knee, followed by the hip, elbow and shoulder","Presents with joint pain, swelling, stiffness, crepitus and mechanical symptoms such as locking or catching from the loose bodies","When the loose bodies calcify or ossify they become visible on plain radiographs as multiple similarly sized calcified bodies within the joint","Non-mineralised cartilaginous bodies may be radiographically occult, so MRI is valuable to demonstrate them and the synovial disease","Diagnosis is confirmed histologically, and malignant transformation to synovial chondrosarcoma is rare but recognised","Treatment is surgical removal of loose bodies with synovectomy, and recurrence can occur if active synovial disease remains"],"pathophysiology":"Synovial chondromatosis is a benign neoplastic or metaplastic process in which the synovial membrane produces multiple foci of hyaline cartilage. These nodules enlarge, may calcify or ossify, and can detach from the synovium to become free-floating intra-articular loose bodies that continue to grow by drawing nutrition from the synovial fluid. Primary synovial chondromatosis arises de novo from the synovium and is now recognised to be a true clonal neoplastic process in many cases, whereas secondary chondromatosis develops from cartilage and bone fragments in a joint already damaged by osteoarthritis or trauma. The loose bodies mechanically damage articular cartilage and produce the locking, effusion and secondary degenerative change seen clinically.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary osteochondromatosis from osteoarthritis or trauma","Pigmented villonodular synovitis","Loose bodies from osteochondritis dissecans","Synovial chondrosarcoma","Rheumatoid or other inflammatory synovitis","Intra-articular tumour or synovial sarcoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs showing multiple calcified intra-articular bodies of similar size","MRI to detect non-calcified cartilaginous bodies and characterise the synovial disease","CT to define ossified loose bodies and bony involvement","Ultrasound to demonstrate effusion and intra-articular bodies","Histological examination of resected tissue to confirm the diagnosis and exclude chondrosarcoma"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, aggressive bone destruction or a soft-tissue mass suggesting chondrosarcoma","Recurrent disease after surgery raising concern for persistent active or malignant synovium","Progressive joint destruction with severe mechanical impairment","Atypical extra-articular extension of the process"],"prognosis":"Primary synovial chondromatosis is benign and treatable with surgery, but recurrence is not uncommon when active synovium persists and untreated disease can cause secondary osteoarthritis; malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma is rare but carries a worse outlook.","pitfalls":["Missing non-calcified cartilaginous loose bodies on plain radiographs and relying on them alone","Confusing it with pigmented villonodular synovitis on imaging","Failing to consider malignant transformation in aggressive or recurrent disease","Removing loose bodies without addressing the active synovium, leading to recurrence"],"management":["Definitive treatment is surgical removal of the intra-articular loose bodies combined with synovectomy to remove the active synovium, performed arthroscopically or open","In secondary disease, manage the underlying joint pathology such as osteoarthritis that generated the cartilage fragments","Manage secondary degenerative change with analgesia and physiotherapy, and joint replacement for advanced arthritis","Monitor for recurrence, which is common when active synovium persists after incomplete synovectomy","Submit resected tissue for histological examination and remain alert to rare malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma","Refer to orthopaedic surgery, with rheumatology input as appropriate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Resnick's Diagnosis of Bone and Joint Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"synovial_sarcoma":{"name":"Synovial sarcoma (SS18-SSX)","system":"Oncology / Sarcoma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SS18-SSX fusion sarcoma","SYT-SSX sarcoma","monophasic synovial sarcoma","biphasic synovial sarcoma","poorly differentiated synovial sarcoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically affects adolescents and young adults, though it can occur at any age, without a strong sex predilection","Characteristically arises in para-articular deep soft tissue of the extremities, especially around the knee and thigh, but despite the name does not originate from synovium","Often presents as a slowly enlarging, sometimes long-standing and painful deep mass, which can lead to misdiagnosis as a benign lesion","Calcification within the tumour on imaging is a helpful, relatively characteristic feature","Defined by the t(X;18) translocation producing an SS18-SSX fusion gene (SSX1, SSX2, or less often SSX4), demonstrable by molecular testing and increasingly by SSX and SS18-SSX antibody immunohistochemistry","Morphology ranges from monophasic spindle cell to biphasic (spindle plus epithelial) and poorly differentiated patterns, with variable cytokeratin and EMA positivity","Local control relies on wide surgical excision, frequently combined with radiotherapy, and multi-agent chemotherapy is considered in selected higher-risk cases","A tendency to late local recurrence and metastasis, chiefly to lung, mandates prolonged follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"Synovial sarcoma is a translocation-associated malignancy of uncertain differentiation defined by fusion of SS18 on chromosome 18 with an SSX gene on the X chromosome. The resulting SS18-SSX fusion protein disrupts normal chromatin-remodelling complexes of the SWI/SNF (BAF) family, hijacking transcriptional programmes and derepressing target genes that drive proliferation and block normal differentiation. This epigenetic dysregulation, rather than any true synovial origin, underlies the tumour and its capacity for both spindle-cell and epithelial (biphasic) phenotypes; detection of the fusion is the diagnostic gold standard.","additionalDifferentials":["Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour","Fibrosarcoma and other spindle-cell sarcomas","Solitary fibrous tumour","Leiomyosarcoma","Mesothelioma (for biphasic pleural lesions)","Benign nerve sheath tumours and other slow-growing soft-tissue masses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the primary site to assess deep extent and relation to joint and neurovascular structures","Plain radiographs to detect characteristic soft-tissue calcification","Chest CT for pulmonary metastatic surveillance","Whole-body functional imaging in higher-risk or large tumours","Core or open biopsy with careful tract planning and molecular testing for SS18 rearrangement","Immunohistochemistry including cytokeratins, EMA, TLE1, and SS18-SSX-specific antibodies"],"redFlags":["A deep-seated, enlarging soft-tissue mass, especially near a joint in a young patient, wrongly labelled benign","A long-standing mass that begins to grow more rapidly or becomes painful","New respiratory symptoms suggesting pulmonary metastases","Recurrence at the primary site after apparently adequate excision"],"prognosis":"Outcome is influenced by tumour size, depth, grade, patient age, and adequacy of resection; smaller, completely excised tumours in younger patients fare better, but late recurrence and pulmonary metastasis are well recognised, so long-term survival can be worse than early results suggest.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a deep soft-tissue mass as benign because of slow growth or long duration","Failing to obtain molecular confirmation of the SS18 fusion when morphology is ambiguous","Poorly planned biopsy that contaminates compartments and complicates limb-sparing surgery","Curtailing follow-up too early, missing late local or pulmonary recurrence","Interpreting focal keratin positivity in isolation without the full immunohistochemical and molecular context"],"management":["Wide en-bloc surgical resection with tumour-free margins, aiming for limb-sparing where feasible, is the curative mainstay","Peri-operative radiotherapy for local control in large, deep, high-grade tumours or where margins are close","Anthracycline- and ifosfamide-based chemotherapy for large, high-grade, or metastatic disease given its relative chemosensitivity","Management from diagnosis within a specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team, including planned excision of the biopsy tract","Long-term surveillance with cross-sectional imaging of the primary site and chest imaging for late local recurrence and pulmonary metastasis","Pulmonary metastasectomy or systemic therapy for oligometastatic lung disease","Referral to clinical trials of targeted agents where standard options are exhausted"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"syphilis":{"name":"Syphilis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lues","Treponema pallidum infection"],"chapter":187,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1428-1436","clinicalPearls":["Chronic systemic infection by Treponema pallidum subsp. pallidum, usually sexually transmitted, characterized by episodes of active disease separated by asymptomatic latent periods (Harrison 22e p.1428).","Staging is the organizing framework: a primary chancre at the site of inoculation, then generalized secondary mucocutaneous disease, followed by latency, and in a minority of untreated patients tertiary disease (gumma, cardiovascular, late neurosyphilis) (Harrison 22e p.1428, p.1430).","Primary chancre is typically a single painless papule that erodes into an indurated ulcer with a firm cartilaginous edge; it is accompanied by regional (usually inguinal) lymphadenopathy that is firm, nonsuppurative, and painless, and heals spontaneously in about 4-6 weeks (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Secondary syphilis classically produces a widespread macular-to-papular, nonpruritic rash that frequently involves the palms and soles, plus generalized nontender lymphadenopathy (noted in ~85% of patients) (Harrison 22e p.1430-1431).","Condylomata lata (broad, moist, highly infectious lesions in intertriginous areas, ~10% of secondary cases) and mucous patches (painless silver-gray erosions of oral/genital mucosa) are characteristic secondary lesions (Harrison 22e p.1431).","Latent syphilis is defined by reactive serology with a normal CSF and no clinical manifestations; early latent is within the first year, late latent is >=1 year or of unknown duration (Harrison 22e p.1431).","Two classes of serologic tests: lipoidal (nontreponemal) RPR/VDRL, which are quantitative and reflect disease activity, and treponemal tests (FTA-ABS, TPPA, EIA/CIA); a reactive lipoidal screen must be confirmed by a treponemal test (Harrison 22e p.1433).","RPR is preferred for rapid serologic diagnosis; the VDRL remains the standard for CSF examination and is superior to RPR for that purpose (Harrison 22e p.1433-1434).","After treatment of early syphilis a fourfold (two-dilution) fall in VDRL/RPR titer (e.g., 1:32 to 1:8) signals adequate response; treponemal tests typically remain reactive and are not useful for monitoring therapy (Harrison 22e p.1433, p.1435).","Penicillin is the drug of choice for all stages as a principle; its efficacy against T. pallidum remains undiminished after decades of use (Harrison 22e p.1434).","Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction (fever, chills, myalgia, headache, tachycardia) may follow the start of therapy, thought to be a response to lipoproteins released by dying treponemes; it occurs in ~50% of primary and ~90% of secondary cases, defervesces within 12-24 h, is self-limited, and does not require steroids (Harrison 22e p.1435).","Earliest manifestations of early congenital syphilis include rhinitis ('snuffles'), mucocutaneous lesions, periostitis/bone changes, and hepatosplenomegaly; classic late stigmata include Hutchinson's teeth, mulberry molars, saddle nose, and saber shins (Harrison 22e p.1432-1433)."],"pathophysiology":"Treponema pallidum subsp. pallidum is a thin spiral organism with a lipid-rich outer membrane and periplasmic endoflagella that drive motility; it has severely limited metabolic capacity and depends on host amino acids, carbohydrates, and lipids. Humans are the only known natural host. After penetrating intact mucous membranes or skin abrasions, treponemes reach the lymphatics and blood within hours, producing systemic dissemination and metastatic foci well before the primary lesion appears (median incubation ~21 days, rarely >6 weeks). Tissue damage reflects the host immune response rather than a direct treponemal toxin: lesions show perivascular infiltration by CD4+ and CD8+ T cells, plasma cells, and macrophages with endarteritis obliterans (obliteration of small vessels). Antigenic variation of the TprK outer-membrane protein allows immune evasion and persistence, explaining the paradoxical appearance of secondary lesions after primary lesions clear. Tertiary lesions (gumma, aortitis of the vasa vasorum of the ascending aorta, parenchymal neurosyphilis) arise from a slowly progressive inflammatory process that begins early in infection (Harrison 22e p.1428-1430, p.1432).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary chancre vs herpes simplex virus genital ulceration (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Primary chancre vs chancroid (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Primary chancre vs donovanosis (granuloma inguinale) (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Primary chancre vs traumatic genital injury (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Neonatal congenital syphilis vs rubella, cytomegalovirus, herpes simplex, toxoplasmosis, and erythroblastosis fetalis (Harrison 22e p.1432)"],"infectionMimics":["Genital herpes simplex virus infection (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Chancroid (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Donovanosis (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Other treponemal infections (yaws, pinta, bejel) that also give reactive treponemal serology (Harrison 22e p.1433)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Traumatic genital injury mimicking a primary chancre (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Rectosigmoid mass of secondary syphilis mistaken for malignancy (Harrison 22e p.1431)","Ocular syphilis (uveitis/iritis) mistaken for non-infectious uveitis when it fails to respond to topical steroids (Harrison 22e p.1431)"],"additionalInvestigations":["RPR or VDRL lipoidal (nontreponemal) serology for screening and quantitation, confirmed by a treponemal test (FTA-ABS or TPPA) (Harrison 22e p.1433)","Treponemal EIA/CIA automated assays as screening tests in large laboratories, with reflex TPPA confirmation (Harrison 22e p.1433-1434)","Dark-field microscopy or direct fluorescent antibody of moist lesions to demonstrate T. pallidum (rarely available outside research settings) (Harrison 22e p.1433)","PCR of lesion swabs, tissue, blood, CSF, ocular fluid, urine, or oropharyngeal/rectal swabs (not commercially available; in-house validated assays) (Harrison 22e p.1433)","CSF examination (mononuclear pleocytosis >5/uL, protein >45 mg/dL, CSF VDRL) for suspected neurosyphilis; CSF VDRL is highly specific but insensitive (Harrison 22e p.1432, p.1434)","HIV testing in all patients with newly diagnosed syphilis (Harrison 22e p.1434)"],"redFlags":["Neurosyphilis at any stage: meningeal syphilis (headache, neck stiffness, cranial nerve involvement, seizures, mental status change), meningovascular syphilis (stroke syndrome in a relatively young adult), or late parenchymatous disease (general paresis, tabes dorsalis) (Harrison 22e p.1432)","Ocular syphilis (uveitis, iritis, optic neuritis, pupillary abnormalities)—permanent blindness may result without prompt diagnosis and treatment (Harrison 22e p.1431)","Otic syphilis (sensorineural hearing loss, vertigo, tinnitus)—deafness may result if untreated (Harrison 22e p.1431)","Cardiovascular syphilis: aortitis with aortic regurgitation, saccular aneurysm of the ascending aorta, or coronary ostial stenosis (Harrison 22e p.1432)","Maternal syphilis in pregnancy: high risk of fetal loss, stillbirth, prematurity, neonatal death, and congenital syphilis (Harrison 22e p.1432)","Serum RPR titer >=1:32 marks higher risk of neurosyphilis (Harrison 22e p.1432, p.1434)"],"prognosis":"Untreated primary and secondary lesions resolve spontaneously, but infection persists. In the preantibiotic era about one-third of untreated patients with latent infection progressed to tertiary disease; the Tuskegee Study showed untreated syphilis raised the death rate ~17%, largely from cardiovascular disease. With early treatment, prognosis is excellent and tertiary syphilis is now nearly eliminated in Western countries; the clinical response to therapy for benign tertiary (gumma) disease is usually impressive. Congenital syphilis prognosis is poor when fulminant disease is clinically apparent at birth, but adequate maternal treatment before the 16th week of pregnancy should prevent fetal damage. Response to therapy is monitored by a fourfold decline in VDRL/RPR titer (Harrison 22e p.1430, p.1432, p.1435).","pitfalls":["Prozone phenomenon: a high-titer specimen may give a nonreactive or only weakly reactive lipoidal test with undiluted serum that becomes reactive only on dilution—a falsely negative screen if dilution is not performed (Harrison 22e p.1433).","Primary syphilis is often missed in women and in MSM because chancres on the cervix, vaginal wall, anal canal, rectum, or mouth go unseen; atypical or trivial dark-field-negative genital lesions should still prompt consideration of syphilis (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Both lipoidal and treponemal tests may be nonreactive in early primary syphilis; an early primary ulcer can present with nonreactive serology, so negative serology does not exclude it (Harrison 22e p.1431-1433).","Biological false-positive lipoidal tests occur (usually titer <=1:8, e.g., in autoimmune disease or injection drug use); a nonreactive treponemal test excludes syphilis in this setting (Harrison 22e p.1433-1434).","Treponemal tests usually remain reactive for life after treatment and cannot distinguish active from past infection or gauge response to therapy (Harrison 22e p.1433, p.1435).","CSF VDRL is highly specific but insensitive—it may be nonreactive even in symptomatic neurosyphilis—and RPR must not be substituted for VDRL on CSF (Harrison 22e p.1434).","Benzathine penicillin does not achieve treponemicidal levels in CSF even at high doses, so it is inadequate for neurosyphilis and viable T. pallidum has been recovered from CSF after such treatment (Harrison 22e p.1434).","Syphilis is a 'great imitator': its protean mucocutaneous and systemic features overlap with many other diseases, so it must be considered broadly (Harrison 22e p.1430-1431)."],"management":["Stage-directed parenteral penicillin is the treatment of choice, with benzathine penicillin for early disease and a prolonged course for late latent infection","Intravenous aqueous crystalline (or intramuscular procaine) penicillin for neurosyphilis, ocular or otic disease","Doxycycline as an alternative in non-pregnant penicillin-allergic patients, with penicillin desensitisation in pregnancy as penicillin remains the only reliable agent","Counsel on the self-limiting Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction that may follow the first dose","Serological follow-up with quantitative non-treponemal titres to confirm response and detect treatment failure or reinfection","Partner notification, contact tracing, and presumptive treatment of sexual contacts","Test for HIV and other sexually transmitted infections, with repeat HIV testing where appropriate"],"severityClassification":"Disease is described by clinical stage rather than a graded severity score: primary syphilis (chancre with regional lymphadenopathy); secondary syphilis (generalized mucocutaneous rash including palms and soles, condylomata lata, mucous patches, constitutional symptoms); latent syphilis (reactive serology, normal CSF, no clinical signs; early latent <1 year vs late latent >=1 year or unknown duration); tertiary/late syphilis (gumma, cardiovascular syphilis, late neurosyphilis). Neurosyphilis spans a continuum: early (asymptomatic meningitis, meningeal and meningovascular syphilis) and late parenchymatous disease (general paresis, tabes dorsalis). Congenital syphilis is divided into early (within first 2 years) and late (after 2 years) (Harrison 22e p.1428, p.1431-1433).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1428-1436"],"crossLinks":[]},"syringobulbia":{"name":"Syringobulbia","system":"Nervous system / Spinal cord and brainstem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["brainstem syrinx","medullary syrinx","bulbar syringomyelia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fluid-filled cavity within the brainstem, usually the medulla, most often representing rostral extension of a cervical syringomyelia along the tegmentum rather than an isolated lesion","Involvement of the lower cranial nerve nuclei produces dysphagia, dysarthria, palatal and pharyngeal weakness, and tongue wasting with fasciculation, so the picture can superficially resemble a bulbar motor syndrome","Damage to the descending spinal tract and nucleus of the trigeminal nerve gives a dissociated facial sensory loss in an onion-skin or balaclava distribution, a highly suggestive sign","Nystagmus, particularly downbeat, together with vertigo and oscillopsia reflects involvement of vestibular connections and the medial longitudinal fasciculus","An ipsilateral Horner syndrome may occur from interruption of descending sympathetic fibres in the brainstem","The disorder is strongly associated with Chiari I malformation and obstruction of cerebrospinal fluid flow at the foramen magnum, and less often with trauma or intramedullary tumour","MRI of the brain and whole spine is the investigation of choice and typically shows the brainstem cavity in continuity with a cervical cord syrinx","Progressive bulbar involvement threatens the airway through aspiration, vocal cord palsy and central respiratory disturbance, so surveillance of swallowing and breathing is essential"],"pathophysiology":"Syringobulbia is a slit-like, gliosis-lined cavity within the brainstem, most commonly dissecting through the medullary tegmentum, and in the majority of cases it is continuous with a cervical cord syrinx. It is thought to arise from disordered cerebrospinal fluid dynamics at the craniovertebral junction, classically in association with a Chiari I malformation, which alters pressure gradients and drives fluid into and along the neuraxis. As the cavity enlarges it disrupts adjacent structures, including the nuclei and intramedullary fibres of the glossopharyngeal, vagus and hypoglossal nerves, the spinal tract and nucleus of the trigeminal nerve, descending sympathetic pathways, the medial longitudinal fasciculus and vestibular connections, producing the characteristic constellation of bulbar, sensory, oculomotor and autonomic signs.","additionalDifferentials":["Brainstem glioma or other intrinsic brainstem tumour","Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome","Bulbar-onset motor neurone disease","Multiple sclerosis with brainstem demyelination","Foramen magnum or craniovertebral junction tumour","Brainstem cavernoma or other vascular malformation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain and whole spine with contrast to define the brainstem cavity and any continuous cord syrinx","Cine phase-contrast (CSF flow) MRI at the craniovertebral junction to assess for obstruction and a Chiari I malformation","Dedicated imaging of the craniovertebral junction and skull base to identify structural causes","Videofluoroscopic or fibreoptic swallowing assessment to quantify aspiration risk","Laryngoscopy to evaluate vocal cord and palatal function","Overnight oximetry or polysomnography where central sleep-disordered breathing is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive bulbar dysfunction with clinical or objective evidence of aspiration","Stridor or vocal cord paralysis, or symptoms of central sleep apnoea suggesting respiratory compromise","Rapid neurological deterioration indicating cavity expansion","New or worsening brainstem signs in a patient with known syringomyelia"],"prognosis":"The course is usually slowly progressive over years, and treatment directed at the underlying cause, such as posterior fossa decompression for Chiari I malformation, may arrest or partially reverse deficits. Established bulbar and cranial nerve deficits often persist, and advanced disease carries a risk of life-threatening aspiration and respiratory compromise.","pitfalls":["Attributing tongue wasting and bulbar weakness to motor neurone disease without imaging the brainstem and cervical cord","Failing to recognise the onion-skin facial sensory loss as a localising trigeminal tract sign","Overlooking an associated Chiari I malformation or craniovertebral junction abnormality that is amenable to treatment","Not imaging the whole neuraxis and thereby missing a continuous cervical syrinx","Underestimating occult central sleep-disordered breathing and aspiration risk"],"management":["Neurosurgical treatment of the underlying craniovertebral abnormality, typically posterior fossa decompression for Chiari I malformation, to restore cerebrospinal fluid flow and arrest cavity progression","Syrinx drainage or shunting when a symptomatic cavity persists or enlarges despite decompression","Swallowing assessment with texture modification, safe-swallow strategies, and enteral feeding when aspiration risk is high","Speech and language therapy for dysarthria and palatopharyngeal weakness","Surveillance for central sleep-disordered breathing with ventilatory support where hypoventilation develops","Protective measures for dissociated facial sensory loss and monitoring of vocal cord function by laryngoscopy","Serial MRI surveillance with multidisciplinary follow-up across neurosurgery, neurology, ENT and respiratory teams"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Youmans and Winn Neurological Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"syringoma":{"name":"Syringoma","system":"Dermatology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["eccrine syringoma","periorbital syringoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / WHO Classification of Skin Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign adnexal tumour of eccrine sweat-duct differentiation, presenting as small skin-coloured to yellowish papules","Most characteristically clustered symmetrically on the lower eyelids and periorbital skin, more common in women","Eruptive syringomas can appear in crops on the trunk, and there is an association with Down syndrome","Lesions are usually asymptomatic and stable, sought mainly for cosmetic reasons rather than medical concern","Histology shows small ducts lined by two layers of epithelial cells, some with a comma-like or tadpole tail, embedded in a sclerotic dermal stroma","The tadpole/comma-shaped ducts distinguish syringoma from other basaloid adnexal tumours","Treatment is optional and cosmetic; options include ablative or destructive methods, with recurrence common because lesions are numerous"],"pathophysiology":"Syringoma is a benign adnexal neoplasm derived from the intraepidermal portion of the eccrine sweat duct. Histologically it consists of numerous small ducts and solid epithelial strands set within a fibrous, often sclerotic, dermal stroma; the ducts are lined by two layers of cuboidal cells and many acquire a characteristic comma-like or tadpole configuration. The lesions represent a proliferation of well-differentiated ductal structures rather than an aggressive process, so they remain confined to the dermis without invasion or metastatic potential. Hormonal and genetic influences are thought to contribute, reflected in the female predominance, the tendency to periorbital clustering, and the eruptive forms associated with certain systemic conditions.","additionalDifferentials":["Xanthelasma (periorbital)","Trichoepithelioma","Milia and small epidermoid cysts","Basal cell carcinoma (superficial or micronodular)","Microcystic adnexal carcinoma (histological mimic)","Sebaceous hyperplasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination of the periorbital and eyelid skin for characteristic clustered papules","Skin biopsy with histopathology showing tadpole-shaped ducts in sclerotic stroma","Dermoscopy to help distinguish from xanthelasma and other papules","Immunohistochemistry in equivocal cases to distinguish from microcystic adnexal carcinoma","Assessment for associated conditions when lesions are eruptive or widespread"],"redFlags":["An indurated infiltrative plaque rather than discrete papules, raising concern for microcystic adnexal carcinoma","Rapid change or ulceration atypical for benign syringoma","Widespread eruptive lesions prompting evaluation for an associated systemic condition"],"prognosis":"Syringomas are entirely benign with no malignant potential; they are cosmetically bothersome and tend to persist, and treatment can be complicated by recurrence and scarring given the number and depth of lesions.","pitfalls":["Confusing periorbital syringomas with xanthelasma and pursuing lipid-lowering rather than dermatological assessment","Misinterpreting the sclerotic ductal histology as microcystic adnexal carcinoma or morphoeic basal cell carcinoma","Promising complete cosmetic clearance despite a high recurrence rate","Overlooking the association with systemic conditions in eruptive presentations"],"management":["Reassurance that the lesions are entirely benign with no malignant potential, so treatment is elective and cosmetic","Ablative destruction with carbon dioxide laser, electrodesiccation, or dermabrasion for bothersome clustered periorbital lesions","Surgical excision or cautery for isolated papules, accepting the depth-related risk of incomplete clearance","Counsel on the high likelihood of recurrence and the risk of scarring or dyspigmentation, especially on thin periorbital skin","Histological confirmation to exclude microcystic adnexal carcinoma before destructive treatment where a lesion is atypical","Evaluate for associated conditions such as Down syndrome or a metabolic trigger when lesions are eruptive or widespread"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","WHO Classification of Skin Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"syringoma_eyelid":{"name":"Syringoma (eyelid)","system":"Ophthalmic / integumentary (eyelid)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["eyelid syringoma","eccrine syringoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Syringomas are benign adnexal tumours of the eccrine sweat ducts presenting as multiple small, soft, skin-coloured to yellowish papules clustered on the lower eyelids and upper cheeks.","They are typically bilateral and symmetrical, appear around puberty or in early adulthood, and are commoner in women.","Individual papules are small and firm; they are usually asymptomatic and the concern is almost entirely cosmetic.","The periorbital region, especially the lower lids, is the classic site, and the clustered, symmetrical distribution is a helpful diagnostic clue.","There is a recognised association with Down syndrome, and an eruptive variant can appear on the trunk; diabetes has been linked to a clear-cell histological variant.","Diagnosis is usually clinical, but biopsy with histology confirms the characteristic ductal structures when there is doubt.","Treatment is for cosmesis only and is difficult, as lesions are dermal and multiple; options include ablative laser, electrocautery, excision and chemical peels, all with a tendency to recur and a risk of eyelid scarring.","Syringomas must be distinguished from xanthelasma, milia, sebaceous hyperplasia and, importantly, from the more aggressive microcystic adnexal carcinoma and morphoeic basal cell carcinoma when a lesion is solitary, indurated or growing."],"pathophysiology":"Syringomas are benign proliferations (hamartomatous or adenomatous) of the intraepidermal eccrine sweat duct. Histology shows small ducts and solid epithelial cords embedded in a fibrous dermal stroma, with characteristic comma-shaped or tadpole-like ductal profiles lined by two layers of cuboidal cells. The proliferation is confined to the superficial to mid dermis without invasion, and hormonal influence is implicated by the tendency to appear or worsen around puberty and in women. Because the lesions are dermal and multifocal, they neither regress spontaneously nor respond to superficial treatment, and their depth explains the difficulty of complete cosmetic clearance.","additionalDifferentials":["Xanthelasma palpebrarum","Milia","Sebaceous hyperplasia","Trichoepithelioma and other benign adnexal tumours","Microcystic adnexal carcinoma","Morphoeic (sclerosing) basal cell carcinoma","Flat warts or lichen planus papules"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp and external examination of the periorbital distribution","Dermoscopy to assess surface and vascular pattern","Skin biopsy with histopathology when diagnosis is uncertain or a lesion is atypical","External photography to document distribution and monitor after treatment","Evaluation for associated conditions (e.g. Down syndrome) where clinically indicated","Deeper or excisional biopsy if microcystic adnexal carcinoma is suspected"],"redFlags":["A solitary, indurated, enlarging or ill-defined lesion suggesting microcystic adnexal carcinoma or morphoeic basal cell carcinoma","Ulceration, bleeding or loss of eyelashes","Rapid change in a previously stable lesion","Deep infiltration or recurrence after excision suggesting a more aggressive adnexal tumour"],"prognosis":"Benign with an excellent prognosis and no threat to vision; lesions persist indefinitely. Cosmetic treatment is often only partially effective and recurrence is common, with a risk of scarring on the delicate eyelid skin.","pitfalls":["Confusing syringomas with xanthelasma and thereby missing an opportunity to counsel on lipids, or vice versa","Mistaking an infiltrative microcystic adnexal carcinoma for benign syringomas and delaying appropriate excision","Aggressive ablative treatment of lower-lid lesions causing scarring, dyspigmentation or ectropion","Promising complete cosmetic clearance despite the high recurrence rate of dermal lesions","Failing to biopsy an atypical or solitary lesion before cosmetic destruction"],"management":["Reassurance that eyelid syringomas are benign and pose no threat to vision, so intervention is cosmetic and optional","Careful ablative treatment with carbon dioxide laser, electrodesiccation, or fine cautery, using corneal shielding to protect the ocular surface","Surgical excision reserved for isolated lesions, mindful of the delicate eyelid skin and the risk of lid-margin scarring or ectropion","Counsel on frequent recurrence and the risk of scarring and pigmentary change on periorbital skin","Histological confirmation to distinguish from microcystic adnexal carcinoma when a lesion is atypical","Assessment for associated conditions such as Down syndrome where clinically indicated, with ophthalmology-led follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course — Oculofacial Plastic and Orbital Surgery","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"syringomyelia":{"name":"Syringomyelia","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["syrinx","spinal cord cavitation","hydromyelia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fluid-filled cavity (syrinx) within the spinal cord that classically produces a dissociated sensory loss, with impaired pain and temperature but preserved light touch and proprioception","The dissociated sensory loss often has a cape-like distribution over the shoulders and arms as the central cord and crossing spinothalamic fibres are affected first","Painless burns or injuries and neuropathic joint degeneration (Charcot joints) can result from the loss of protective pain sensation","Progressive lower motor neuron signs in the hands and arms occur as the syrinx expands into the anterior horns","Frequently associated with a Chiari I malformation at the craniocervical junction, and also with prior trauma, tumour, or arachnoiditis","MRI of the whole spine and craniocervical junction is the definitive investigation and defines the syrinx and any underlying cause","Management targets the underlying cause, for example posterior fossa decompression for Chiari-associated syrinx, rather than the cavity alone","Extension of the cavity into the brainstem (syringobulbia) can cause lower cranial nerve and bulbar dysfunction"],"pathophysiology":"Syringomyelia is characterised by a longitudinal cavity within the spinal cord that disrupts neural tissue from the centre outward. Altered cerebrospinal fluid dynamics, often related to obstruction at the craniocervical junction as in Chiari I malformation, are thought to drive fluid accumulation and cavity expansion. The central location preferentially damages the decussating spinothalamic fibres, producing dissociated sensory loss, and progressive enlargement affects anterior horn cells, long tracts, and autonomic pathways.","additionalDifferentials":["Intramedullary spinal cord tumour","Multiple sclerosis and other myelopathies","Cervical spondylotic myelopathy","Motor neuron disease when lower motor neuron hand wasting predominates","Peripheral neuropathy","Leprosy and other causes of dissociated sensory loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the entire spine and craniocervical junction to define the syrinx and identify Chiari malformation or other cause","MRI of the brain to assess the posterior fossa and hindbrain","Contrast-enhanced imaging when an underlying tumour is suspected","CSF flow studies in selected cases to assess craniocervical junction dynamics","Neurophysiological studies where the differential includes motor neuron disease or neuropathy"],"redFlags":["Rapid neurological deterioration or new bulbar symptoms from syringobulbia requiring urgent neurosurgical assessment","Progressive weakness, sensory loss, or sphincter disturbance indicating cord compromise","Associated Chiari malformation with brainstem compression signs","An underlying spinal cord tumour presenting as a secondary syrinx"],"prognosis":"Syringomyelia often progresses slowly over years, with a stepwise or gradual accumulation of deficits. Treating the underlying cause, particularly decompressing a Chiari malformation, can stabilise or improve symptoms, but established neurological deficits may not fully reverse and some patients continue to deteriorate despite surgery.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise dissociated sensory loss and its localising value","Overlooking an associated Chiari malformation or underlying tumour as the treatable cause","Attributing hand wasting solely to motor neuron disease without imaging the cord","Not counselling patients about protecting insensate areas from burns and injury"],"management":["Neurosurgical correction of the underlying cause, such as craniocervical decompression for Chiari I malformation or detethering and adhesiolysis for post-traumatic or arachnoiditis-related syrinx, to halt progression","Syrinx drainage or shunting when a symptomatic cavity persists or expands","Neuropathic pain control with anticonvulsant and antidepressant agents","Protective skin and joint care given loss of protective pain sensation, to prevent painless burns, trophic ulcers, and Charcot joints","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to preserve function and manage weakness","Serial MRI surveillance of the syrinx and craniocervical junction to monitor size and guide re-intervention","Multidisciplinary follow-up across neurology, neurosurgery, rehabilitation and pain services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"systemic_ebv_t_cell_lymphoma_childhood":{"name":"Systemic EBV-positive T-cell lymphoma of childhood","system":"Oncology / Haematologic malignancy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["systemic EBV-positive T-cell lymphoma of childhood","systemic EBV-positive T-cell lymphoproliferative disease of childhood","fulminant EBV-positive T-cell lymphoproliferative disorder","EBV-associated haemophagocytic T-cell lymphoma of children"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare, fulminant clonal T-cell proliferation of children and young adults that follows acute or chronic active Epstein-Barr virus infection","It is classified as a lymphoid neoplasm because of the clonal, aggressive nature of the process despite its viral driver","Presents with a rapidly progressive systemic illness including fever, hepatosplenomegaly, liver dysfunction and cytopenias","Almost always complicated by haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, which dominates the acute clinical picture","The neoplastic cells are Epstein-Barr virus-positive (EBER-positive) cytotoxic T cells, usually CD8-positive, and are clonal by T-cell receptor analysis","It is far more common in East Asian and Latin American populations, indicating a genetic predisposition to abnormal Epstein-Barr virus handling","Requires urgent immunochemotherapy directed at both the lymphoma and the haemophagocytic syndrome, with allogeneic HSCT offering the main chance of cure"],"pathophysiology":"Systemic EBV-positive T-cell lymphoma of childhood develops when Epstein-Barr virus infects T cells and, against a background of genetically impaired antiviral immunity, drives a clonal and rapidly proliferating population of virus-infected cytotoxic T lymphocytes. The infected clone and the surrounding immune response release large quantities of inflammatory cytokines, activating macrophages and precipitating haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis with its attendant cytopenias, coagulopathy and multi-organ dysfunction. The neoplastic cells infiltrate the liver, spleen, marrow and lymphoid tissue, and the combination of clonal proliferation and cytokine-driven hyperinflammation produces the characteristically fulminant course that can progress to multi-organ failure within days to weeks.","additionalDifferentials":["Infection-associated (reactive) haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","Chronic active Epstein-Barr virus infection of T/NK-cell type","Aggressive NK-cell leukaemia","Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type","Primary (familial) haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","Acute leukaemia or other aggressive systemic lymphoma in a child"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Epstein-Barr virus studies including EBER in situ hybridisation on tissue and viral load in blood","Immunophenotyping showing EBV-positive cytotoxic T cells, usually CD8-positive","T-cell receptor clonality analysis","Haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis workup including ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen, soluble interleukin-2 receptor and bone marrow examination","Liver function and coagulation studies given frequent hepatic involvement","Imaging to assess hepatosplenomegaly and disease extent"],"redFlags":["Fulminant haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis with multi-organ dysfunction","Rapidly worsening liver failure and coagulopathy","Severe cytopenias with bleeding or overwhelming infection","Haemodynamic instability from cytokine storm"],"prognosis":"The disorder is highly aggressive and frequently fatal within a short period, often from fulminant haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis and multi-organ failure; survival is poor without prompt intensive therapy, and allogeneic HSCT offers the main prospect of long-term cure in those who respond.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the illness for a self-limiting viral infection or purely reactive haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis and delaying treatment","Failing to demonstrate the clonal EBV-positive T-cell population","Underestimating the speed of clinical deterioration","Confusing it with chronic active Epstein-Barr virus infection, which has a different course","Not recognising the higher incidence and predisposition in East Asian and Latin American children"],"management":["Urgent haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis-directed immunochemotherapy, using etoposide-based regimens with corticosteroids and ciclosporin, to control the cytokine-driven hyperinflammation","Combination lymphoma chemotherapy directed against the clonal Epstein-Barr virus-positive cytotoxic T-cell population","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the main route to durable cure in responders","Intensive supportive care for multi-organ dysfunction, including correction of coagulopathy and cytopenias and organ support in a critical-care setting","Blood-product support and infection prophylaxis during profound cytopenia and immunosuppression","Management in a specialist paediatric haemato-oncology centre with early transplant referral","Family counselling and evaluation for an underlying genetic predisposition to impaired Epstein-Barr virus handling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours","British Society for Haematology and international guidance on EBV-associated lymphoproliferative disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"systemic_juvenile_idiopathic_arthritis":{"name":"Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis (Still disease)","system":"Rheumatology / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["systemic JIA","sJIA","Still disease","systemic-onset juvenile idiopathic arthritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology / ILAR & PRINTO criteria","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic triad is a quotidian (once- or twice-daily spiking) fever, an evanescent salmon-pink rash that appears with fever peaks, and arthritis, though joint involvement may lag behind the systemic features","Regarded as an autoinflammatory rather than a classically autoantibody-driven disease, so antinuclear antibody and rheumatoid factor are typically negative and unhelpful diagnostically","Markedly raised inflammatory markers, neutrophilia, thrombocytosis, anaemia of inflammation and a strikingly high serum ferritin are characteristic","Serositis (pericarditis, pleuritis), hepatosplenomegaly and generalised lymphadenopathy are common systemic accompaniments","Macrophage activation syndrome (MAS) is a life-threatening complication signalled by falling cell counts, falling erythrocyte sedimentation rate with rising ferritin, hypofibrinogenaemia, hypertriglyceridaemia and hepatic dysfunction","IL-1 and IL-6 blockade have transformed management and are used early in systemic disease, alongside systemic corticosteroids for severe presentations","Chronic anterior uveitis is uncommon in the systemic subtype compared with oligoarticular disease, but ophthalmology review remains part of overall JIA surveillance","A biphasic course is described, with an early systemic-inflammatory phase and a later chronic destructive arthritis phase in a subset of children"],"pathophysiology":"Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis is best understood as an autoinflammatory disorder driven by dysregulated innate immunity rather than adaptive autoimmunity. Excess production of the proinflammatory cytokines interleukin-1, interleukin-6 and interleukin-18, together with activated neutrophils and macrophages, produces the systemic features of fever, rash, serositis and reticuloendothelial activation. Uncontrolled macrophage and T-cell activation can tip into a cytokine storm known as macrophage activation syndrome, a form of secondary haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis. Persistent joint inflammation in the chronic phase may reflect a shift towards adaptive immune mechanisms and can lead to erosive, destructive arthritis if inadequately controlled.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic infection or occult sepsis","Leukaemia or lymphoma presenting with fever and cytopenias","Kawasaki disease","Primary haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other connective tissue disease","Inflammatory bowel disease with systemic features","Monogenic autoinflammatory (periodic fever) syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with differential looking for neutrophilia, thrombocytosis and anaemia (and cytopenias suggesting MAS)","Inflammatory markers including erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","Serum ferritin, fibrinogen, triglycerides and liver function tests to detect macrophage activation syndrome","Antinuclear antibody and rheumatoid factor (usually negative) to help exclude other subtypes and connective tissue disease","Blood cultures and infection screen, plus bone marrow examination when malignancy or HLH is a concern","Echocardiography and chest imaging when serositis is suspected","Slit-lamp ophthalmology assessment as part of JIA uveitis surveillance"],"redFlags":["Falling blood counts, rising ferritin and falling erythrocyte sedimentation rate suggesting macrophage activation syndrome","Bleeding, encephalopathy or multiorgan dysfunction indicating severe MAS","Cardiac tamponade from large pericardial effusion","Cytopenias, bone pain or blast cells raising concern for underlying malignancy","Persistent unexplained fever with clinical deterioration despite treatment"],"prognosis":"Outcomes have improved substantially with early cytokine-targeted therapy; some children achieve durable remission, whereas others develop persistent destructive polyarthritis or recurrent systemic flares. Macrophage activation syndrome carries appreciable mortality and requires prompt recognition and intensive immunosuppression.","pitfalls":["Attributing persistent fever and raised inflammatory markers to infection and delaying rheumatological assessment","Overlooking evolving macrophage activation syndrome when the erythrocyte sedimentation rate paradoxically falls","Assuming a negative antinuclear antibody and rheumatoid factor exclude the diagnosis","Failing to exclude leukaemia before starting corticosteroids in a febrile child with cytopenias","Neglecting long-term monitoring for growth impairment and joint damage"],"management":["Early cytokine-targeted biologic therapy with interleukin-1 blockade (anakinra, canakinumab) or interleukin-6 blockade (tocilizumab) as the disease-modifying mainstay","Systemic corticosteroids for severe systemic presentations and to bridge to biologic control","NSAIDs for symptomatic relief of fever and arthralgia in milder disease","Methotrexate or continued biologic therapy for persistent, potentially erosive arthritis","Vigilance for macrophage activation syndrome, treated urgently with high-dose corticosteroids, anakinra and ciclosporin","Monitoring of growth, bone health, and drug toxicity, with physiotherapy to preserve joint function","Management within a paediatric rheumatology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology","ILAR classification of juvenile idiopathic arthritis","PRINTO/PReS consensus on systemic JIA and MAS"],"crossLinks":[]},"systemic_lupus_erythematosus":{"name":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus","system":"Immune-Mediated / Rheumatologic (multisystem)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SLE","Lupus","Lupus erythematosus, systemic"],"chapter":368,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2827","clinicalPearls":["SLE is an autoimmune disease driven by overactive innate and adaptive immunity, with tissue injury mediated by autoantibodies and immune complexes; cutaneous, musculoskeletal, and renal systems are most often affected, followed by pulmonary, hematologic, cardiovascular, serosal, and CNS involvement (p.2827).","Roughly 90% of patients are women, most of childbearing age; in the US the highest prevalence is in Black women, then Hispanic, White, and Asian/Pacific Islander women, and non-White patients tend to have more severe disease (p.2827).","Autoantibodies can be detectable years before clinical diagnosis, so seropositivity helps attribute nonspecific constitutional symptoms to SLE (p.2827, p.2830).","ANA at a titer of at least 1:80 is the best screening test and is present in nearly all patients; repeatedly negative immunofluorescence ANA makes the diagnosis unlikely, though about 5% of patients are ANA-negative (p.2832).","Anti-dsDNA (present in ~40-60%, specificity ~75-99%) and anti-Sm (present in ~30%, highly specific) are the only ANA subtypes in the EULAR/ACR criteria; rising anti-dsDNA with falling C3/C4 signals active disease, especially impending nephritis (p.2832, p.2830).","Lupus arthritis is a nonerosive polyarthritis of wrists, MCP/PIP joints, and knees; periarticular ligament damage can cause reversible Jaccoud-like deformities, and overlap with rheumatoid arthritis is termed 'rhupus' (p.2829).","The classic malar 'butterfly' rash spares the nasolabial folds (distinguishing it from rosacea); subacute cutaneous lupus is photosensitive and linked to anti-Ro/SSA, while discoid lesions are the most common chronic cutaneous form (p.2829).","Nephritis occurs in about 35% of patients, may be asymptomatic, and warrants screening every 3 months with a urine protein-to-creatinine ratio; biopsy is generally prompted by UPCr >0.5 or declining renal function (p.2830, p.2833).","Antiphospholipid antibodies are present in ~50% of patients and markedly raise risk of arterial/venous thrombosis, thrombocytopenia, recurrent fetal loss, Libman-Sacks endocarditis, and stroke (p.2830, p.2832).","Anti-Ro/SSA antibodies can cross the placenta and cause neonatal lupus (transient rash/lab abnormalities or, less commonly, congenital heart block); high-titer Ro warrants fetal echocardiography screening (p.2836).","Avascular (osteo)necrosis should be suspected when pain is out of proportion in a single hip, shoulder, or knee, particularly with prior corticosteroid use (p.2829).","Some patients are categorized as 'type 1 lupus' (classic immune-driven features such as nephritis, arthritis, vasculitis, generally steroid-responsive) versus 'type 2 lupus' (fatigue, diffuse pain, cognitive dysfunction/'brain fog', less responsive to immunosuppression) (p.2827-2828)."],"pathophysiology":"SLE arises from multiple immunoregulatory defects in genetically predisposed individuals interacting with environmental triggers. There is a strong genetic component (24% vs 2% concordance in monozygotic vs dizygotic twins; >150 GWAS susceptibility loci), with the MHC the most common association and nearly half of loci tied to type 1 interferon production or signaling (e.g., TLR7, IRF5/7/8, STAT4); complement-cascade defects (C1q, C1r, C1s, C2, C4) impair clearance of apoptotic debris and immune complexes and underlie severe monogenic disease. Environmental contributors include UV light, crystalline silica, smoking, sex hormones, Epstein-Barr virus, and gut-microbiome-related increased intestinal permeability. Innate defects cause excess cellular debris (high neutrophil turnover and NETosis) that overwhelms macrophage/dendritic clearance, triggering nucleic-acid sensors and TLRs in plasmacytoid dendritic cells to produce type 1 interferon, which sensitizes the adaptive immune system. Loss of B- and T-cell tolerance (including expansion of age-associated B cells, T follicular/peripheral helper cell help, and defective regulatory T cells) drives production of pathogenic autoantibodies; autoantibody-nucleic acid immune complexes deposit in tissue, activate monocytes, and produce inflammation, repair, and eventually fibrosis after recurrent flares.","additionalDifferentials":["Drug-induced lupus (antihistone-antibody positive; rarely renal or CNS; resolves after stopping the offending drug)","Rheumatoid arthritis (and rhupus overlap)","Mixed connective tissue disease / anti-U1-RNP-associated disease","Sjogren's syndrome","Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)","Inflammatory myositis (dermatomyositis/polymyositis)","Antiphospholipid syndrome (primary)","Rosacea (mimics malar rash)","Other ANA-associated autoimmune conditions: autoimmune hepatitis, Hashimoto's thyroiditis, primary biliary cholangitis"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious encephalitis (must be excluded when SLE psychosis/confusional state is considered)","Pulmonary infection (acute lupus pneumonitis can appear indistinguishable from infection on imaging)","Viral infection-related cytopenias (cytopenias in SLE may be due to infection rather than disease activity)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Glucocorticoid-induced psychosis (mimics neuropsychiatric lupus psychosis)","Migraine or tension headache (overlaps with neuropsychiatric lupus headache)","Rosacea (mimics the malar/butterfly rash)","Drug-induced lupus (clinically and serologically overlaps with SLE)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura / atypical hemolytic-uremic syndrome (microangiopathy mimic)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ANA by immunofluorescence with titer (screening; positive threshold 1:80 or higher)","Anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm antibodies (specific subtypes; anti-dsDNA also tracks disease activity)","Anti-Ro/SSA antibodies (useful in ANA-negative suspected SLE; neonatal-lupus risk)","Antihistone antibodies (drug-induced lupus, positive in ~75% of DIL)","Antiphospholipid panel: anticardiolipin, anti-beta-2-glycoprotein-1 (IgG/IgM/IgA), and lupus anticoagulant via dilute Russell viper venom time","Complement levels C3 and C4 (fall with active disease / immune-complex consumption)","Complete blood count for cytopenias; peripheral smear for schistocytes and ADAMTS13 activity plus LDH if microangiopathy suspected","Renal assessment: eGFR, serum creatinine, random or 24-h urine protein-to-creatinine ratio, screened quarterly","Renal biopsy (ISN/RPS classification) when UPCr exceeds 0.5 or renal function declines","Emerging urinary biomarkers when available (urinary IL-16, CD163, type 1 IFN, TGF-beta)","Creatine kinase and aldolase with muscle biopsy if inflammatory myopathy suspected","Baseline and yearly retinal examination when on hydroxychloroquine (maculopathy surveillance)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining renal function or rising proteinuria suggesting class III/IV proliferative lupus nephritis (~20% of class IV cases die or reach ESRD within 10 years)","Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage or acute pneumonitis (life-threatening pulmonary involvement)","Severe neuropsychiatric disease: psychosis, seizures, transverse myelitis, stroke","Severe thrombocytopenia (<20,000-50,000/uL) with bleeding risk, or life-threatening hemolytic anemia","Atypical hemolytic-uremic syndrome / thrombotic microangiopathy (hemolysis, thrombocytopenia, schistocytes, low ADAMTS13, high LDH)","Mesenteric vasculitis (risk of intestinal perforation, bleeding, ischemia)","Antiphospholipid syndrome with arterial/venous thrombosis or catastrophic APS","Pulmonary arterial hypertension or prior arterial thrombosis in pregnancy (highest maternal mortality risk)","New chest pain in a young patient given accelerated atherosclerosis and premature coronary disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis and survival vary widely by geographic region, race/ethnicity, and access to care and medications. Most patients have active disease despite therapy or recurrent flares; about 15% have relatively mild disease. Renal disease is a major determinant of outcome: roughly 20% of US patients with class IV lupus nephritis die or develop ESRD within 10 years, and non-White patients more often progress to ESRD. Accelerated atherosclerosis becomes a leading cause of death after about 5 years of disease, with elevated myocardial infarction and stroke rates driven by both traditional risk factors and lupus-specific factors (antiphospholipid antibodies, high disease activity, low C3, high glucocorticoid dose, elevated homocysteine/leptin). Glucocorticoids contribute to irreversible, dose-dependent organ damage (cataracts, osteoporotic fractures, avascular necrosis, diabetes) even at low doses; class V nephritis carries a better renal prognosis than class III/IV. Sustained low disease activity (LLDAS) for 2 or more years is associated with significantly less damage, and meta-analyses suggest an increased overall risk of cancer and cancer-related death.","pitfalls":["Treating ANA positivity as diagnostic: ANA occurs at low titer in healthy people and in many other autoimmune diseases; diagnosis ultimately rests on clinical judgment, and classification criteria were designed for trial enrollment, not diagnosis.","Excluding SLE because ANA is negative: about 5% of patients are ANA-negative, where anti-Ro/SSA can support the diagnosis.","Missing asymptomatic lupus nephritis or cytopenias by not screening quarterly, since renal and hematologic disease can be present without symptoms.","Attributing acute pulmonary infiltrates to lupus pneumonitis when infection looks identical on imaging.","Ascribing psychosis or confusion to neuropsychiatric lupus without ruling out glucocorticoid-induced psychosis and infectious encephalitis.","Overlooking avascular necrosis as a cause of disproportionate single-joint pain in patients on corticosteroids.","Confusing drug-induced lupus (antihistone-positive, usually sparing kidney/brain, resolving after withdrawal) with idiopathic SLE.","Using estrogen-containing contraceptives or HRT in patients with antiphospholipid antibodies or active disease, increasing thromboembolism risk.","Using direct oral anticoagulants instead of warfarin for concomitant APS, or continuing teratogenic agents (mycophenolate, methotrexate, warfarin) in pregnancy."],"management":["Hydroxychloroquine as background therapy for essentially all patients to reduce flares and organ damage","Glucocorticoids for active disease, tapered to the lowest effective dose, with steroid-sparing immunosuppression","Immunosuppressants such as mycophenolate, azathioprine or cyclophosphamide for major organ involvement including lupus nephritis","Biologic therapy with belimumab, anifrolumab, or rituximab for refractory or severe disease","Photoprotection, cardiovascular risk reduction, bone protection, and vaccination given infection and accelerated atherosclerotic risk","Monitoring of disease activity with anti-dsDNA titres, complement levels and renal parameters to detect flares and impending nephritis","Antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy where antiphospholipid antibodies confer thrombotic risk, with pre-conception planning and pregnancy co-management","Multidisciplinary care spanning rheumatology, nephrology and obstetrics as needed"],"severityClassification":"Lupus nephritis is graded by the International Society of Nephrology/Renal Pathology Society (ISN/RPS) classes: I (mesangial deposits without hypercellularity), II (mesangial proliferative), III (focal proliferative glomerulonephritis), IV (diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis), and V (membranous), with III/IV +/- V combinations carrying the highest risk; class V (~20% of nephritis) more often presents with nephrotic syndrome but has a better prognosis than III/IV. Disease activity is quantified with the SLEDAI (and SLEDAI-2K), where low disease activity is a score of 0-4 and remission a score of 0 (with stable hydroxychloroquine/immunosuppressive use and prednisone equivalent 5 mg/day or less). Overall disease severity is also stratified clinically as mild (constitutional symptoms, mild arthritis, rash <=9% BSA, platelets 50-100, SLEDAI <=6), moderate (rheumatoid-like arthritis, rash 9-18% BSA, platelets 20-50, serositis, SLEDAI 7-12), and severe (major organ-threatening disease such as cerebritis, myelitis, pneumonitis, mesenteric vasculitis; platelets <20; TTP-like or hemophagocytic syndrome; rash >18% BSA; SLEDAI >12). Treat-to-target endpoints include LLDAS and DORIS remission, and trials use SRI, BICLA, and CLASI instruments.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2827","Harrison 22e p.2828","Harrison 22e p.2829","Harrison 22e p.2830","Harrison 22e p.2831","Harrison 22e p.2832","Harrison 22e p.2833","Harrison 22e p.2835","Harrison 22e p.2836"],"crossLinks":[]},"systemic_mastocytosis":{"name":"Systemic mastocytosis","system":"Haematology / Multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SM"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO haematolymphoid classification / mastocytosis literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A clonal mast cell disorder with infiltration of the bone marrow and often other organs beyond the skin","Symptoms arise from mast cell mediator release (flushing, pruritus, abdominal pain, diarrhoea, anaphylaxis) and from organ infiltration","The great majority carry an activating KIT D816V mutation","A raised baseline serum tryptase supports the diagnosis and is one of the diagnostic criteria","Subtypes range from indolent systemic mastocytosis to aggressive disease and mast cell leukaemia, with markedly different prognoses","Management combines mediator-targeted symptom control and anaphylaxis preparedness with cytoreductive or KIT-targeted therapy in advanced disease"],"pathophysiology":"Systemic mastocytosis is driven by clonal proliferation of abnormal mast cells, in most cases due to an activating mutation of the KIT receptor tyrosine kinase (classically D816V), which promotes mast cell survival and accumulation. These mast cells infiltrate the bone marrow and frequently the gastrointestinal tract, liver, spleen and bone, and release mediators such as histamine and tryptase either spontaneously or in response to triggers. Symptoms therefore reflect both episodic mediator release and, in advanced forms, organ dysfunction from mast cell infiltration.","additionalDifferentials":["Cutaneous mastocytosis without systemic involvement","Mast cell activation syndrome","Carcinoid syndrome","Phaeochromocytoma","Hereditary alpha-tryptasaemia","Idiopathic anaphylaxis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum baseline tryptase","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine with mast cell immunophenotyping","KIT D816V mutation analysis","Assessment for organ involvement (blood counts, liver and spleen, bone imaging)","Allergy and anaphylaxis evaluation including trigger identification"],"redFlags":["Recurrent anaphylaxis or severe mediator crises","Cytopenias, hepatosplenomegaly or malabsorption suggesting aggressive disease","Pathological fractures from bone involvement","Features suggesting mast cell leukaemia"],"prognosis":"Indolent systemic mastocytosis has a near-normal life expectancy with good symptom control, whereas aggressive systemic mastocytosis and mast cell leukaemia carry a poor prognosis.","pitfalls":["Attributing recurrent anaphylaxis to idiopathic causes without checking tryptase","Missing an associated haematological neoplasm","Overlooking osteoporosis and fracture risk","Failing to provide anaphylaxis rescue medication and a trigger-avoidance plan"],"management":["Mediator-directed symptom control with H1 and H2 antihistamines, mast cell stabilisers such as sodium cromoglicate, and leukotriene receptor antagonists","Trigger avoidance and anaphylaxis preparedness, including a self-injectable adrenaline device","KIT-targeted tyrosine kinase inhibitors (avapritinib, midostaurin) for advanced or symptomatic disease driven by the KIT D816V mutation","Cytoreductive therapy with cladribine or interferon alfa in aggressive systemic mastocytosis or mast cell leukaemia","Surveillance and treatment of mastocytosis-related osteoporosis with bone-protective agents","Monitoring of baseline tryptase, blood counts and organ involvement to detect progression to advanced disease","Multidisciplinary care across haematology, allergy and dermatology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","Mastocytosis clinical guidelines (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"systemic_sclerosis":{"name":"Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma)","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scleroderma"],"chapter":372,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2859-2872","clinicalPearls":["Two major subsets: limited cutaneous SSc (lcSSc) with skin confined to fingers, distal limbs and face (trunk spared) and an indolent course; and diffuse cutaneous SSc (dcSSc) with rapidly progressive skin involvement of fingers, extremities, face and trunk (p.2859, 2866).","In lcSSc, Raynaud's phenomenon typically precedes sclerodactyly and other manifestations, sometimes by years; the CREST constellation (calcinosis cutis, Raynaud's, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, telangiectasia) is a distinct lcSSc subset (p.2859).","Bilateral symmetrical skin thickening starting in the fingers and advancing distal-to-proximal is the hallmark distinguishing SSc from other connective tissue diseases (p.2867).","SSc-specific autoantibodies are mutually exclusive and aid risk stratification: anti-centromere is associated with lcSSc and isolated PAH (renal crisis rare); anti-topoisomerase I (Scl-70) with dcSSc, extensive skin, early ILD and cardiac involvement; anti-RNA polymerase III with rapidly progressive skin, renal crisis and contemporaneous cancer (p.2864, 2871).","Antinuclear antibodies (ANA) are detectable in virtually all SSc patients, even in early/preclinical stages (p.2864, 2871).","Nailfold capillaroscopy distinguishes primary Raynaud's disease (regular, evenly spaced parallel hairpin loops) from secondary Raynaud's/SSc (giant capillaries, dilated and distorted loops, microhemorrhages, capillary dropout) and helps establish early SSc diagnosis (p.2867, 2871).","ILD and pulmonary vascular disease together account for the majority of SSc-related deaths; survival is particularly poor when both coexist (p.2868).","Scleroderma renal crisis presents with accelerated hypertension plus acute kidney injury, occurs in <15% of patients almost always within 4 years of onset, and is associated with diffuse/progressive skin involvement and anti-RNA polymerase III (up to 50%); anti-centromere appears protective (p.2870-2871).","Palpable tendon friction rubs are associated with increased risk of renal and cardiac complications and reduced survival, and may be a harbinger of impending renal crisis (p.2871).","SSc patients should be screened for PAH at initial evaluation and annually thereafter, and screened for ILD with regular pulmonary monitoring for several years after diagnosis (p.2868, 2872).","SSc sine scleroderma: characteristic Raynaud's plus typical clinical/serologic features of SSc in the absence of detectable skin thickening (~10% of patients) (p.2859, 2866, 2872)."],"pathophysiology":"SSc is a chronic, often progressive disease of unknown etiology defined pathologically by widespread microangiopathy (fibroproliferative vasculopathy with capillary loss and obliteration) combined with multiorgan fibrosis, on a background of autoimmunity. An initial vascular insult in a genetically predisposed individual (modest HLA class II and immune-regulatory gene associations) triggers inflammation, generation of SSc-specific autoantibodies, and sustained activation of fibroblasts/myofibroblasts driven by mediators such as TGF-beta, IL-6, CTGF, PDGF and endothelin. Maladaptive repair leads to replacement of normal tissue with rigid, avascular, acellular connective tissue affecting skin, lungs, heart, GI tract, kidneys and other organs (p.2859-2866).","additionalDifferentials":["Localized scleroderma (morphea, linear scleroderma, coup de sabre) - generally not complicated by Raynaud's or internal organ involvement (p.2860, 2871)","Mixed connective tissue disease and SSc overlap syndromes (with polymyositis, Sjogren's, SLE, polyarthritis, autoimmune liver disease) (p.2860, 2866)","Eosinophilic fasciitis (Shulman's disease) (p.2860)","Scleromyxedema (papular mucinosis) and scleredema (Buschke / diabetic) (p.2860)","Chronic graft-versus-host disease (p.2860)","Primary Raynaud's disease - benign, must be differentiated from early/limited SSc (p.2867, 2871)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis (associated with gadolinium exposure) (p.2860)","Vinyl chloride-induced disease and other chemically/drug-associated scleroderma-like conditions (p.2860)","Eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome (L-tryptophan contaminant) (p.2860)","Stiff skin syndrome and pachydermoperiostosis (p.2860)","Paraneoplastic scleroderma-like syndrome (p.2860)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura / thrombotic microangiopathy - scleroderma renal crisis is sometimes initially misdiagnosed as TTP (p.2871)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ANA and SSc-specific autoantibodies (anti-centromere, anti-topoisomerase I/Scl-70, anti-RNA polymerase III, U3-RNP/fibrillarin, Th/To, PM/Scl) for diagnosis and risk stratification (p.2864, 2871)","Nailfold capillaroscopy (low-power stereomicroscope or videocapillaroscopy under immersion oil) (p.2867)","High-resolution chest CT (HRCT) for ILD detection and prognostic assessment of fibrosis extent (p.2868)","Pulmonary function testing including FVC and DLCO for ILD monitoring (a DLCO reduction out of proportion to lung volumes suggests pulmonary vascular disease) (p.2868)","Doppler echocardiography for PAH screening; right heart (cardiac) catheterization to confirm PAH and exclude venoocclusive/postcapillary causes (p.2868)","NT-proBNP correlates with presence/severity of PAH and survival (not specific) (p.2868-2869, 2871)","Cardiac MRI / tissue Doppler / SPECT for occult myocardial involvement (p.2871)","Blood film for microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, platelet count, urinalysis and creatinine in suspected renal crisis (p.2871)"],"redFlags":["Scleroderma renal crisis: accelerated/new hypertension (generally >150/90 mmHg) with progressive oliguric renal failure - a medical emergency (note ~10% present normotensive, with poorer outcome) (p.2870-2871)","Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with fragmented red cells and thrombocytopenia (hallmark of renal crisis) (p.2871)","New unexplained anemia, thrombocytopenia, pericardial effusion or new tendon friction rubs as harbingers of impending renal crisis (p.2871)","Pulmonary arterial hypertension: exertional dyspnea, near-syncope, loud P2, right heart failure - 3-year survival of untreated PAH <50% (p.2868)","Rapidly progressive ILD with declining FVC (can fall ~30%/year), most rapid in the first 3-5 years (p.2868)","Critical digital ischemia with fingertip ulceration/necrosis and acro-osteolysis (p.2867)"],"prognosis":"Highly variable; dcSSc carries a more rapidly progressive course and worse prognosis than lcSSc. SSc confers a fivefold to eightfold higher age/sex-adjusted mortality, with median survival ~11 years; 5- and 10-year survival are ~70%/55% in dcSSc and ~90%/75% in lcSSc. Major causes of death are PAH, pulmonary fibrosis, GI involvement and cardiac disease. Scleroderma renal crisis carries ~30% 3-year mortality (markedly improved by ACE inhibitors). Poor-prognosis markers include male sex, African ancestry, older age at onset, extensive truncal skin involvement, palpable tendon friction rubs, significant/progressive visceral involvement, and laboratory findings of elevated ESR, anemia, proteinuria and anti-topoisomerase I antibodies (p.2873).","pitfalls":["Early dcSSc may present with only upper-extremity edema and puffy fingers and Raynaud's may be absent, leading to misdiagnosis as arthritis, SLE, myositis or undifferentiated connective tissue disease (p.2872)","Scleroderma renal crisis can be misdiagnosed as TTP or other thrombotic microangiopathy; renal biopsy and von Willebrand factor-cleaving protease activity may help (p.2871)","Glucocorticoids (especially prednisone >=10 mg/d) are associated with precipitating scleroderma renal crisis and should be used only when essential and at low dose in high-risk patients (p.2870)","PFTs and conventional echocardiography are relatively insensitive for early ILD and early cardiac involvement, respectively (p.2868, 2871)","Echocardiography can over- or underestimate pulmonary artery pressures, so PAH must be confirmed by cardiac catheterization (p.2868)","SSc sine scleroderma can be missed because diagnostic skin induration is absent despite organ involvement (p.2872)"],"management":["Vasodilator therapy for Raynaud phenomenon and digital ulcers, using calcium channel blockers, phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors, and endothelin receptor antagonists or prostacyclin analogues for severe ischaemia","Immunosuppression for skin and interstitial lung disease with mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, or rituximab and tocilizumab, adding the antifibrotic nintedanib for progressive fibrosis","Targeted pulmonary vasodilator therapy for pulmonary arterial hypertension, guided by right heart catheterisation","Prompt ACE-inhibitor therapy for scleroderma renal crisis, while avoiding high-dose glucocorticoids that precipitate it","Proton pump inhibitors and prokinetics for reflux and gastrointestinal dysmotility","Structured surveillance with pulmonary function tests and echocardiography to detect lung and pulmonary vascular disease early","Multidisciplinary care across rheumatology, respiratory, cardiology and gastroenterology"],"severityClassification":"ACR/EULAR classification criteria are >90% specific and sensitive but are intended for research rather than clinical practice; weighted items include skin thickening proximal to MCP joints (score 9), sclerodactyly/puffy fingers, fingertip lesions, telangiectasia, abnormal nailfold capillaries, ILD/PAH, Raynaud's phenomenon, and SSc-specific autoantibodies (anti-centromere, Scl-70, RNA polymerase III). Clinically, patients are stratified into limited cutaneous and diffuse cutaneous subsets by extent of skin involvement (p.2860).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2859-2873"],"crossLinks":["ild"]},"systemic_sclerosis_diffuse":{"name":"Diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis","system":"Rheumatology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["diffuse scleroderma","dcSSc","anti-Scl-70","anti-topoisomerase I","anti-RNA polymerase III"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology / EULAR systemic sclerosis guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Skin thickening extends proximal to the elbows and knees and onto the trunk, often progressing rapidly in the first years","Raynaud phenomenon typically appears close in time to skin change rather than long before it","Anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) associates with interstitial lung disease, while anti-RNA polymerase III associates with rapid skin progression and renal crisis","Interstitial lung disease is a leading cause of mortality and warrants early screening and monitoring","The risk of scleroderma renal crisis is highest early in diffuse disease and is increased by glucocorticoid exposure","Tendon friction rubs, arthralgia, and rapidly worsening skin score suggest active, progressive disease","The modified Rodnan skin score is used to track cutaneous extent and disease activity"],"pathophysiology":"Diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis is characterised by widespread immune-driven fibrosis and obliterative vasculopathy. Endothelial injury, T-cell and B-cell activation, and profibrotic mediators including transforming growth factor beta drive fibroblast-to-myofibroblast transformation and excessive collagen deposition across skin and internal organs. Autoantibodies such as anti-topoisomerase I and anti-RNA polymerase III mark distinct clinical phenotypes. Rapidly progressive fibrosis of the lungs produces interstitial disease, while intimal proliferation in renal vessels predisposes to renal crisis.","additionalDifferentials":["Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis","Mixed connective tissue disease","Eosinophilic fasciitis","Scleromyxoedema","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis","Chronic graft-versus-host disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antinuclear antibody with anti-Scl-70 and anti-RNA polymerase III testing","High-resolution CT of the chest for interstitial lung disease","Pulmonary function tests including forced vital capacity and diffusing capacity","Transthoracic echocardiography to assess for pulmonary hypertension","Blood pressure and renal function monitoring for early renal crisis","Nailfold capillaroscopy for scleroderma-pattern microangiopathy"],"redFlags":["Abrupt hypertension with rising creatinine indicating scleroderma renal crisis","Rapidly declining forced vital capacity from progressive interstitial lung disease","New exertional breathlessness suggesting pulmonary hypertension","Fulminant skin progression with tendon friction rubs","Digital gangrene from severe vasculopathy"],"prognosis":"Diffuse disease carries higher morbidity and mortality than limited disease, driven mainly by interstitial lung disease, pulmonary hypertension, and renal crisis. Skin may stabilise or soften after the early active phase, and early organ-directed therapy improves outcomes.","pitfalls":["Using higher-dose glucocorticoids and precipitating scleroderma renal crisis","Delaying lung disease screening until symptoms are advanced","Misreading softening skin as remission while internal organ disease progresses","Failing to recognise anti-RNA polymerase III as a marker of renal crisis risk"],"management":["Immunosuppression to control rapidly progressive skin and interstitial lung disease with mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, rituximab or tocilizumab, adding nintedanib for progressive pulmonary fibrosis","Early ACE-inhibitor treatment for scleroderma renal crisis, and avoidance of high-dose corticosteroids given their role in precipitating it","Vasodilators for Raynaud phenomenon and digital ulcers, including calcium channel blockers, phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors and endothelin receptor antagonists","Proton pump inhibitors and prokinetics for oesophageal and gastrointestinal dysmotility","Blood-pressure and renal monitoring, especially early in diffuse disease, alongside pulmonary function and echocardiographic surveillance","Consideration of autologous haematopoietic stem cell transplantation in selected patients with rapidly progressive disease","Multidisciplinary specialist care with physiotherapy for skin and joint contractures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology","EULAR recommendations for the treatment of systemic sclerosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"systemic_sclerosis_limited":{"name":"Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis (CREST)","system":"Rheumatology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CREST syndrome","limited scleroderma","lcSSc","anticentromere antibody","ACA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology / EULAR systemic sclerosis guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Skin thickening is confined distal to the elbows and knees (with facial involvement) and often follows years of longstanding Raynaud phenomenon","The CREST constellation summarises Calcinosis, Raynaud, oEsophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, and Telangiectasia","Anticentromere antibodies are the characteristic serological marker and correlate with the limited subset","Pulmonary arterial hypertension is the leading disease-specific cause of late mortality and may develop insidiously after many years","Regular echocardiographic and pulmonary function screening is used to detect pulmonary vascular disease before it becomes symptomatic","Digital pitting scars, ischaemic ulcers, and telangiectasia over the face and hands are common examination findings","Cutaneous change progresses slowly, so internal organ surveillance rather than skin score often drives management"],"pathophysiology":"Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis is a chronic autoimmune connective tissue disorder in which immune activation, small-vessel endothelial injury, and progressive fibrosis interact. Persistent vascular damage drives Raynaud phenomenon and a proliferative vasculopathy, while activated fibroblasts and myofibroblasts deposit excessive collagen in a distal-limited distribution. Autoantibodies, classically anticentromere, reflect the underlying immune dysregulation. Over years the vasculopathy may extend to the pulmonary arteries, producing pulmonary arterial hypertension, and to the gastrointestinal tract, causing oesophageal and gut dysmotility.","additionalDifferentials":["Diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis","Mixed connective tissue disease","Primary Raynaud phenomenon","Eosinophilic fasciitis","Scleromyxoedema or scleroedema","Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antinuclear antibody with anticentromere and extractable nuclear antigen profiling","Nailfold capillaroscopy to demonstrate scleroderma-pattern microvascular change","Transthoracic echocardiography to screen for pulmonary arterial hypertension","Pulmonary function tests with diffusing capacity to detect vascular or interstitial disease","Right heart catheterisation when non-invasive screening suggests pulmonary hypertension","Oesophageal studies for reflux and dysmotility when symptomatic"],"redFlags":["Progressive breathlessness or reduced diffusing capacity suggesting pulmonary arterial hypertension","Critical digital ischaemia or non-healing ulceration risking tissue loss","Recurrent aspiration or severe reflux from oesophageal dysmotility","Rapidly progressive skin involvement suggesting evolution toward diffuse disease"],"prognosis":"Limited cutaneous disease generally follows an indolent course with better survival than diffuse disease, but the development of pulmonary arterial hypertension markedly worsens outcome. Early detection through surveillance and targeted pulmonary vasodilator therapy improves prognosis.","pitfalls":["Attributing longstanding Raynaud to a primary cause and missing evolving systemic sclerosis","Failing to screen periodically for pulmonary arterial hypertension because skin disease appears stable","Overlooking silent oesophageal dysmotility and its aspiration risk","Assuming the limited subset never causes serious internal organ disease"],"management":["Vasodilator therapy for Raynaud phenomenon and digital ulcers, using calcium channel blockers, phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors, and endothelin receptor antagonists or prostacyclin analogues","Targeted pulmonary vasodilator therapy for pulmonary arterial hypertension, the leading disease-specific cause of late death","Proton pump inhibitors and prokinetics for oesophageal dysmotility and reflux","Regular echocardiographic and pulmonary function surveillance to detect pulmonary arterial hypertension before it becomes symptomatic","Management of calcinosis, telangiectasia and digital ischaemia, with wound care for ischaemic ulcers","Prompt ACE-inhibitor therapy should renal crisis occur, though it is uncommon in the limited subset","Multidisciplinary follow-up across rheumatology, respiratory and cardiology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology","EULAR recommendations for the treatment of systemic sclerosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"systemic_sclerosis_sine_scleroderma":{"name":"Systemic sclerosis sine scleroderma","system":"Rheumatology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ssSSc","scleroderma sine scleroderma","systemic sclerosis without skin thickening","anticentromere antibody","anti-Scl-70"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology / EULAR systemic sclerosis guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Patients have the internal organ and vascular features of systemic sclerosis without clinically detectable skin thickening","Raynaud phenomenon, a scleroderma-pattern nailfold capillary picture, and systemic sclerosis-specific autoantibodies support the diagnosis","Interstitial lung disease, pulmonary arterial hypertension, oesophageal dysmotility, or renal crisis may still occur","The absence of skin change frequently delays recognition until an internal organ complication declares itself","Autoantibody profiles resemble those of cutaneous systemic sclerosis, including anticentromere and anti-topoisomerase I patterns","Organ-based surveillance mirrors that used in cutaneous systemic sclerosis despite normal skin","It should be considered in unexplained pulmonary hypertension or interstitial lung disease with Raynaud"],"pathophysiology":"Systemic sclerosis sine scleroderma shares the immune activation, small-vessel vasculopathy, and internal fibrosis of cutaneous systemic sclerosis but without the dermal fibrosis that produces visible skin thickening. Endothelial injury, autoantibody production, and profibrotic signalling target the lungs, gastrointestinal tract, heart, and kidneys while sparing the skin clinically. The same pathogenic mechanisms therefore generate pulmonary vascular disease, interstitial fibrosis, and dysmotility, meaning the visceral risk profile parallels that of overt scleroderma.","additionalDifferentials":["Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis","Idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension","Idiopathic interstitial pneumonia","Mixed connective tissue disease","Undifferentiated connective tissue disease","Primary Raynaud phenomenon"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antinuclear antibody with systemic sclerosis-specific antibody profiling","Nailfold capillaroscopy for scleroderma-pattern change","High-resolution CT and pulmonary function tests for interstitial lung disease","Transthoracic echocardiography screening for pulmonary hypertension","Oesophageal function studies when dysmotility is suspected","Renal function and blood pressure monitoring"],"redFlags":["Unexplained progressive breathlessness from pulmonary vascular or interstitial disease","Hypertensive crisis with rising creatinine suggesting renal crisis","Recurrent aspiration from oesophageal dysmotility","Critical digital ischaemia"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the specific internal organ involvement; the visceral complications carry the same risks as in cutaneous disease, and pulmonary vascular or interstitial disease dominates prognosis. Early recognition despite normal skin allows timely organ-directed treatment.","pitfalls":["Excluding systemic sclerosis simply because the skin is normal","Labelling pulmonary hypertension as idiopathic without checking autoantibodies and capillaroscopy","Delaying surveillance for internal organ disease","Overlooking the risk of renal crisis in the absence of skin change"],"management":["Vasodilator therapy for Raynaud phenomenon and digital ischaemia with calcium channel blockers and phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors","Immunosuppression and antifibrotic therapy (mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, rituximab, nintedanib) for interstitial lung disease","Targeted pulmonary vasodilators for pulmonary arterial hypertension confirmed on right heart catheterisation","Proton pump inhibitors and prokinetics for oesophageal and gastrointestinal dysmotility","ACE-inhibitor therapy for scleroderma renal crisis, with avoidance of high-dose corticosteroids","Organ-based surveillance mirroring cutaneous disease, with pulmonary function tests, echocardiography, and blood pressure and renal monitoring despite normal skin","Multidisciplinary specialist follow-up to enable early detection of visceral complications"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology","EULAR recommendations for the treatment of systemic sclerosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"t_cell_prolymphocytic_leukemia":{"name":"T-cell prolymphocytic leukaemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["T-PLL","prolymphocytic leukaemia T-cell type","TCL1-rearranged T-cell leukaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An aggressive mature T-cell leukaemia typically presenting with a very high lymphocyte count, hepatosplenomegaly and generalised lymphadenopathy","Skin infiltration and serous effusions are recognised and relatively distinctive extranodal features","The blood film shows small to medium prolymphocytes with a visible nucleolus and characteristic cytoplasmic blebbing or protrusions","The cells are usually mature T cells expressing CD2, CD3, CD5 and CD7, most often CD4 positive, and frequently co-express CD4 and CD8","Rearrangement or overexpression of the TCL1 oncogene, commonly through inversion or translocation of chromosome 14, is the defining molecular lesion","Association with ataxia-telangiectasia and somatic ATM abnormalities is well recognised","The course is usually rapidly progressive, and durable control generally requires monoclonal antibody therapy followed by allogeneic transplantation in fit patients"],"pathophysiology":"T-cell prolymphocytic leukaemia is a clonal malignancy of mature post-thymic T lymphocytes. The hallmark oncogenic event is aberrant activation of the TCL1 family of oncogenes, most often through chromosome 14 inversions or translocations that juxtapose TCL1 with T-cell receptor regulatory elements, driving proliferation via enhanced AKT signalling. Additional abnormalities of the ATM gene on chromosome 11, echoing the germline defect of ataxia-telangiectasia, impair DNA damage responses and contribute to genomic instability. The resulting rapidly dividing clone accumulates in blood, marrow, spleen, liver, lymph nodes and skin.","additionalDifferentials":["B-cell prolymphocytic leukaemia","Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia with increased prolymphocytes","Adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma","Sezary syndrome","T-cell large granular lymphocytic leukaemia","Mantle cell lymphoma in leukaemic phase"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood film to identify prolymphocyte morphology","Flow cytometry immunophenotyping including CD4/CD8 and TCL1 expression","Cytogenetics and FISH for chromosome 14 (TCL1) and chromosome 11 (ATM) abnormalities","T-cell receptor gene rearrangement to confirm clonality","Cross-sectional imaging to assess organomegaly and effusions","Bone marrow examination to document infiltration"],"redFlags":["Rapidly doubling lymphocyte count with progressive organomegaly","Development of effusions or symptomatic skin infiltration","Marrow failure with cytopenias and infection or bleeding","Central nervous system or other extranodal spread"],"prognosis":"T-cell prolymphocytic leukaemia is aggressive with historically short survival; monoclonal antibody induction can produce remission, but long-term disease control usually depends on allogeneic transplantation in eligible patients.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling it as an atypical B-cell chronic lymphocytic leukaemia without immunophenotyping","Adopting a watch-and-wait strategy suited to indolent disease in this aggressive entity","Overlooking TCL1 and ATM testing that confirm the diagnosis","Underestimating the poor prognosis when counselling and planning transplant early"],"management":["Intravenous alemtuzumab, an anti-CD52 monoclonal antibody, as induction of choice to achieve remission","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation to consolidate remission in eligible, fit patients","Antimicrobial prophylaxis against Pneumocystis, cytomegalovirus and fungal infection during profound alemtuzumab-induced immunosuppression, with cytomegalovirus viral-load monitoring","Blood-product support and management of cytopenias and organomegaly-related symptoms","Consideration of novel agents or clinical trials, such as BCL2 inhibition, for relapsed or refractory disease","Regular monitoring for relapse given the high relapse rate after remission","Management within a specialist haematology and transplant centre"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"tabes_dorsalis":{"name":"Tabes dorsalis","system":"Neurology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tabetic neurosyphilis","syphilitic myelopathy","progressive locomotor ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A late (tertiary) form of neurosyphilis caused by Treponema pallidum, developing years to decades after untreated infection","Results from degeneration of the dorsal columns and dorsal roots, producing a progressive sensory ataxia with a broad-based, stamping gait","Lightning pains, lancinating paroxysms in the limbs or trunk, are a characteristic feature","Loss of proprioception and vibration causes a positive Romberg sign, and deep tendon reflexes are often absent","The Argyll Robertson pupil accommodates but does not react to light and is a classic associated sign","Impaired pain and position sense can lead to painless Charcot joints and trophic foot ulcers","Visceral crises, such as sudden severe abdominal pain, and bladder dysfunction may occur","Diagnosis rests on positive serum and cerebrospinal fluid treponemal testing, and penicillin-based therapy halts progression but may not reverse established deficits"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic infection with Treponema pallidum leads, in the tertiary phase, to inflammatory degeneration of the posterior (dorsal) columns and the dorsal nerve roots and ganglia. Loss of large sensory fibres carrying proprioception and vibration produces sensory ataxia, areflexia, and the characteristic gait, while dorsal root involvement generates the lancinating lightning pains. The associated pupillary and autonomic findings reflect more widespread involvement of the nervous system. The condition is now rare where syphilis is treated early but persists in undertreated populations and those with HIV co-infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Subacute combined degeneration from vitamin B12 deficiency","Sensory ganglionopathy (paraneoplastic or toxic)","Diabetic pseudotabes","Hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathies","Other causes of dorsal column dysfunction and sensory ataxia","Compressive posterior column myelopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum treponemal and non-treponemal serology (e.g. TPPA and RPR/VDRL)","Cerebrospinal fluid examination including CSF-VDRL, cell count, and protein","HIV testing given frequent co-infection and altered natural history","MRI of the spine to demonstrate posterior column change and exclude other myelopathy","Nerve conduction studies to characterise the sensory involvement","Ophthalmological assessment of pupillary abnormalities"],"redFlags":["New or evolving neurological deficits in a patient with untreated or inadequately treated syphilis","Concurrent HIV infection, which accelerates and complicates neurosyphilis","Visceral crises or autonomic dysfunction requiring exclusion of other acute pathology","Neuropathic joint destruction or non-healing trophic ulcers indicating advanced sensory loss"],"prognosis":"Antibiotic treatment arrests further progression and treats active infection, but long-established dorsal column and root damage often persists, so residual ataxia, pain, and pupillary abnormalities are common; earlier treatment yields better outcomes.","pitfalls":["Overlooking syphilis as a cause of sensory ataxia in the modern era","Confusing the picture with B12 deficiency and not sending treponemal serology","Failing to test for and address concurrent HIV infection","Misattributing lightning pains to a primary painful neuropathy","Expecting antibiotics to reverse fixed neurological deficits and neglecting supportive care"],"management":["Parenteral penicillin using a neurosyphilis regimen, namely intravenous aqueous crystalline penicillin, as the specific antitreponemal treatment","Penicillin desensitisation in allergic patients, as penicillin remains the only reliable agent for neurosyphilis","Neuropathic and lightning-pain control with anticonvulsant agents such as carbamazepine","Physiotherapy and gait rehabilitation with walking aids for sensory ataxia","Protective foot care and orthopaedic management of trophic ulcers and Charcot joints from loss of protective sensation","Follow-up cerebrospinal fluid examination and serial serology to confirm treatment response","HIV testing and co-management given frequent co-infection and altered natural history, with ophthalmology input for pupillary abnormalities"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"taco":{"name":"Transfusion-associated circulatory overload","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TACO","transfusion-associated circulatory overload"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AABB Technical Manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as cardiogenic pulmonary oedema from volume excess during or within about six to twelve hours of transfusion","Features favouring TACO over TRALI include hypertension, raised jugular venous pressure, a third heart sound, and elevated natriuretic peptides","Elderly patients, small adults, infants, and those with cardiac or renal impairment are at greatest risk, particularly with rapid or large-volume transfusion","One of the most frequently reported serious transfusion hazards and a leading cause of transfusion-associated death","Response to slowing or stopping the transfusion, sitting the patient up, giving oxygen, and diuresis supports the diagnosis","Prevention relies on transfusing the minimum necessary volume slowly, one unit at a time with reassessment, and considering pre-emptive diuretics in at-risk patients","Chest imaging shows features of volume overload such as cardiomegaly, vascular redistribution, and pleural effusions"],"pathophysiology":"TACO results from an acute increase in intravascular volume that exceeds the recipient's cardiovascular capacity, raising pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure and causing transudation of fluid into the alveoli. Patients with reduced cardiac reserve, renal impairment, or extremes of body size are least able to accommodate the transfused volume, especially when infusion is rapid. Unlike TRALI, the alveolar fluid reflects hydrostatic pressure rather than increased capillary permeability, so the oedema is cardiogenic and responds to volume reduction.","additionalDifferentials":["Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI)","Decompensated heart failure from other causes","Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Anaphylactic transfusion reaction","Pneumonia or aspiration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph showing cardiogenic oedema and cardiomegaly","Natriuretic peptide measurement, ideally with a pre-transfusion comparison","Echocardiography to assess ventricular function and filling pressures","Fluid balance review and vital sign trends around the transfusion","Exclusion of TRALI and other causes of respiratory distress"],"redFlags":["Acute respiratory distress with hypoxaemia during transfusion requiring urgent oxygen and diuresis","Severe hypertension and pulmonary oedema in a patient with limited cardiac reserve","Failure to improve with volume reduction, prompting reconsideration of TRALI or other diagnoses","Recurrent overload in a patient needing ongoing transfusion, requiring a modified transfusion plan"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally good when recognised early and treated with volume reduction, oxygen, and diuresis, but delayed recognition can lead to respiratory failure and death, particularly in frail patients with cardiac disease.","pitfalls":["Transfusing at-risk patients rapidly or in large volumes without reassessment between units","Confusing TACO with TRALI and withholding beneficial diuresis","Overlooking cumulative volume from concurrent intravenous fluids","Failing to document and report the reaction, missing prevention opportunities for future transfusions"],"management":["Stop or slow the transfusion immediately at the first sign of circulatory overload","Sit the patient upright, give supplemental oxygen, and administer a loop diuretic to reduce intravascular volume","Ventilatory support, non-invasive or invasive, for severe respiratory compromise","Preventive strategy of transfusing the minimum necessary volume slowly, giving single units with reassessment between each","Pre-emptive diuretic cover for patients with cardiac or renal impairment or at extremes of body size","Distinguish from TRALI and other transfusion reactions to guide correct management","Report the event to transfusion and haemovigilance services and review future transfusion planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"taeniasis":{"name":"Taeniasis (Taenia solium/saginata)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tapeworm infection","Taenia solium infection","Taenia saginata infection","beef tapeworm","pork tapeworm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell/Manson's Tropical Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Intestinal taeniasis is acquired by eating raw or undercooked meat containing larval cysts, beef for Taenia saginata and pork for Taenia solium","The adult tapeworm anchors to the small intestine by its scolex and can grow to several metres, yet often causes only mild or no symptoms","Patients may notice motile proglottids passed in the stool or emerging spontaneously, which is frequently the reason for presentation","The critical distinction is that Taenia solium eggs, if ingested by a human, cause cysticercosis, including neurocysticercosis, a leading cause of acquired epilepsy in endemic regions","Autoinfection or faecal-oral spread of Taenia solium eggs from a tapeworm carrier can therefore endanger the carrier and close contacts, independent of pork consumption","Species differentiation matters because it guides the risk assessment for cysticercosis, and is aided by examining proglottid or scolex morphology or by molecular testing","Praziquantel is the usual treatment for intestinal infection, with careful attention to the separate management considerations of cysticercosis"],"pathophysiology":"Humans are the definitive host for both Taenia saginata and Taenia solium. Ingesting viable cysticerci in undercooked beef or pork releases a larva whose scolex attaches to the small-intestinal wall and matures into an adult tapeworm producing a chain of egg-laden proglottids shed in the faeces. For Taenia saginata the human is only the definitive host, so disease is confined to the gut. Taenia solium is more dangerous because, in addition to the intestinal adult, humans can act as an intermediate host: ingested eggs hatch, and the oncospheres disseminate to encyst in tissues including muscle, subcutaneous tissue, eye and brain, producing cysticercosis. Neurocysticercosis results when cysts lodge in the central nervous system, and the inflammatory response as they degenerate provokes seizures and other neurological effects.","additionalDifferentials":["Diphyllobothriasis and other tapeworm infections","Hymenolepiasis","Other intestinal helminthiases","Irritable bowel syndrome","For neurological presentations, other causes of focal brain lesions and epilepsy","Functional abdominal pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy for taenid eggs, recognising that species cannot be told apart by egg morphology alone","Examination of expelled proglottids or scolex for species identification","Molecular PCR for species differentiation","Serology for cysticercosis when Taenia solium exposure is suspected","Neuroimaging with computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging when neurocysticercosis is a concern","Full blood count including eosinophil count"],"redFlags":["New-onset seizures or focal neurological signs suggesting neurocysticercosis","Raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus from intraventricular cysts","Ocular cysticercosis threatening vision","Identification of Taenia solium in a household, placing contacts at risk of cysticercosis"],"prognosis":"Intestinal taeniasis is readily cured and generally benign; the outlook is dominated by Taenia solium cysticercosis, where neurological involvement can cause significant long-term morbidity despite treatment.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish Taenia solium from Taenia saginata and thereby missing the cysticercosis risk","Overlooking that a tapeworm carrier can transmit cysticercosis-causing eggs to themselves and others","Treating intestinal infection without evaluating for or counselling about neurocysticercosis when appropriate","Assuming egg morphology on stool microscopy identifies the species"],"management":["Single-dose anthelmintic, praziquantel or niclosamide, to eradicate the intestinal adult tapeworm","Confirm species and, where Taenia solium is suspected, exclude neurocysticercosis before treatment given the risk of an inflammatory reaction to dying cysts","For neurocysticercosis, antiparasitic therapy with albendazole (with or without praziquantel) under corticosteroid cover, plus antiepileptic drugs for seizures","Screen and treat household contacts of a Taenia solium carrier to prevent cysticercosis from faecal-oral egg spread","Confirmation of cure by follow-up stool examination","Preventive advice on thorough cooking of beef and pork, hand hygiene and sanitation","Specialist infectious diseases and neurology referral when cysticercosis or neurological involvement is present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"tafro_syndrome":{"name":"TAFRO syndrome","system":"Multisystem / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TAFRO","iMCD-TAFRO"],"chapter":null,"source":"Haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["TAFRO is an acronym for Thrombocytopenia, Anasarca (oedema, ascites and effusions), Fever, Reticulin fibrosis of the marrow (with Renal dysfunction) and Organomegaly.","It is regarded as a distinct, aggressive clinical subtype within the spectrum of HHV-8-negative idiopathic multicentric Castleman disease (iMCD-TAFRO), although some cases do not fully meet Castleman criteria.","It is distinguished from other idiopathic multicentric Castleman disease by thrombocytopenia rather than thrombocytosis, severe anasarca, only mild lymphadenopathy and relatively preserved immunoglobulins instead of marked hypergammaglobulinaemia.","The clinical picture is driven by a cytokine storm, especially interleukin-6 and vascular endothelial growth factor, causing systemic inflammation and vascular hyperpermeability.","Lymph node histology resembles hyaline-vascular or mixed Castleman disease with atretic germinal centres, while the bone marrow shows megakaryocytic hyperplasia and reticulin fibrosis.","It is a diagnosis of exclusion — infection (HHV-8, HIV, EBV), malignancy (especially lymphoma), autoimmune disease and POEMS syndrome must be ruled out.","Presentation is often acute with rapidly progressive renal impairment and effusions, and the syndrome can be life-threatening.","Treatment mainstays include corticosteroids and anti-interleukin-6 therapy (for example tocilizumab or siltuximab), with rituximab, ciclosporin or intensive immunosuppression reserved for refractory or critically ill patients."],"pathophysiology":"TAFRO syndrome is a systemic hyperinflammatory disorder thought to arise from dysregulated cytokine production, in which interleukin-6 and vascular endothelial growth factor play central roles. Excess interleukin-6 drives fever, an acute-phase response and reactive changes in lymphoid tissue and marrow, while vascular endothelial growth factor increases vascular permeability, producing the striking anasarca with ascites and pleural effusions that characterises the condition. Platelet consumption together with marrow megakaryocytic hyperplasia and reticulin fibrosis contributes to thrombocytopenia, and microvascular and immune-mediated injury underlie the frequent renal dysfunction. The initiating trigger is unknown, with autoimmune and antigenic or viral stimuli postulated, but by definition recognised causes such as HHV-8 infection are absent. The syndrome overlaps with idiopathic multicentric Castleman disease, sharing reactive lymph node histology with atretic germinal centres, yet it is set apart clinically by thrombocytopenia, severe fluid overload, comparatively small lymph nodes and the absence of marked hypergammaglobulinaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic multicentric Castleman disease, non-TAFRO/plasma cell type","HHV-8-associated multicentric Castleman disease","POEMS syndrome","Autoimmune disease (systemic lupus erythematosus, adult-onset Still's disease, ANCA-associated vasculitis)","Lymphoma and other malignancies","Haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","IgG4-related disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count (thrombocytopenia, anaemia) with a blood film","Inflammatory markers (C-reactive protein) alongside interleukin-6 and vascular endothelial growth factor levels","Renal function testing and urinalysis","Cross-sectional imaging for effusions, ascites, organomegaly and lymphadenopathy","Excisional lymph node biopsy for histology","Bone marrow examination (megakaryocytic hyperplasia and reticulin fibrosis)","Exclusion work-up including HHV-8 and HIV testing, autoimmune serology, and serum immunoglobulins with free light chains to exclude POEMS"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive renal failure requiring renal replacement therapy","Refractory anasarca with large effusions and respiratory compromise","Multi-organ dysfunction or critical illness","Severe thrombocytopenia with bleeding","Features suggesting overlapping haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis"],"prognosis":"TAFRO syndrome is more acute and aggressive than classic idiopathic multicentric Castleman disease and can be fatal from multi-organ failure. Timely immunosuppression and anti-interleukin-6 therapy nonetheless induce remission in a substantial proportion of patients.","pitfalls":["Attributing isolated thrombocytopenia to immune thrombocytopenia and missing the wider systemic syndrome.","Delaying the lymph node or bone marrow biopsy needed to establish the diagnosis.","Failing to exclude HHV-8 and HIV, which would change classification and management.","Confusing it with POEMS syndrome or an autoimmune connective tissue disease.","Underestimating severity given the potential for rapid multi-organ deterioration."],"management":["High-dose corticosteroids as first-line immunosuppression to dampen the cytokine storm","Anti-interleukin-6 therapy with tocilizumab or siltuximab targeting the central driver cytokine","Escalation to ciclosporin, rituximab or cytotoxic chemotherapy in corticosteroid-refractory or severe disease","Supportive care for anasarca, renal dysfunction including renal replacement therapy, and thrombocytopenia with platelet support as needed","Intensive organ support for multi-organ failure in critically ill patients","Rigorous exclusion of infection, lymphoma, autoimmune disease and POEMS syndrome, which is integral to the diagnosis","Monitoring of platelets, inflammatory markers, interleukin-6 and renal function to track response","Multidisciplinary haematology-led care with critical care support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International consensus diagnostic and treatment guidelines for idiopathic multicentric Castleman disease","Castleman Disease Collaborative Network resources","Wintrobe's Clinical Hematology"],"crossLinks":[]},"tailors_bunion":{"name":"Tailor's bunion","system":"Musculoskeletal system / Foot and ankle","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bunionette","fifth metatarsal head prominence","digitus quintus varus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle / Apley and Solomon's System of Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A symptomatic prominence of the fifth metatarsal head on the lateral border of the forefoot, historically attributed to tailors sitting cross-legged with pressure on the outer foot","The lateral eminence causes a painful adventitial bursa, keratotic callus and skin irritation from shoe pressure, mirroring hallux valgus on the medial side","The Fallat and Coleman radiographic classification distinguishes an enlarged or laterally spurred fifth metatarsal head (type I), lateral bowing of the metatarsal shaft (type II) and a widened fourth-to-fifth intermetatarsal angle (type III, the commonest)","It frequently coexists with hallux valgus as part of a splayed forefoot, so both borders should be assessed together","Plantar or plantar-lateral keratosis suggests an associated plantarflexed or prominent fifth metatarsal head rather than a purely lateral deformity","First-line management is non-operative with wide toe-box footwear, protective padding, callus care and accommodative orthoses","When surgery is needed, the osteotomy is chosen by deformity type, ranging from lateral condylectomy for a bony spur to distal chevron or diaphyseal osteotomy to correct shaft bowing or a wide intermetatarsal angle"],"pathophysiology":"A tailor's bunion arises from a prominent lateral fifth metatarsal head, which may reflect a congenitally enlarged or laterally spurred head, congenital lateral bowing of the metatarsal shaft, or an increased fourth-to-fifth intermetatarsal angle that splays the ray outward. Chronic pressure from footwear against this prominence provokes an adventitial bursa, soft-tissue thickening and hyperkeratotic callus. Contributory biomechanical factors include a splayed forefoot with associated hallux valgus, pes planovalgus, hindfoot or forefoot varus increasing lateral loading, and ligamentous laxity. Over time the fifth toe drifts into varus while the metatarsal head deviates laterally, and abnormal plantar loading can produce an intractable plantar keratosis beneath the head.","additionalDifferentials":["Hallux valgus with generalised forefoot splaying","Fifth metatarsal stress fracture or non-united avulsion at the base","Plantar or interdigital keratoma and intractable plantar keratosis","Ganglion or adventitial bursa over the metatarsophalangeal joint","Gouty tophus or inflammatory arthropathy of the fifth metatarsophalangeal joint","Soft-tissue lesion such as a lipoma or fibroma over the lateral border"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Weight-bearing dorsoplantar and oblique foot radiographs to measure the fourth-to-fifth intermetatarsal and lateral deviation angles","Assessment of the fifth metatarsal head morphology and any lateral exostosis on the oblique view","Clinical evaluation of hindfoot alignment and concurrent hallux valgus","Skin and callus mapping to localise lateral versus plantar pressure","Vascular and neurological assessment of the foot before considering surgery","Pedobarography or gait assessment where abnormal loading is suspected"],"redFlags":["Ulceration or deep infection over the prominence, particularly in a person with diabetes or peripheral vascular disease","Sensory loss or ischaemia indicating neuropathic or vascular compromise","Rest pain, night pain or a rapidly enlarging mass suggesting a bony or soft-tissue tumour","Signs of acute inflammatory or crystal arthropathy rather than mechanical deformity"],"prognosis":"Most patients settle with footwear modification and padding, and surgical correction with an appropriately selected osteotomy gives reliable relief of the lateral prominence; recurrence and transfer plantar lesions are uncommon when the deformity type is matched to the procedure.","pitfalls":["Performing an isolated condylectomy when the underlying problem is a widened intermetatarsal angle, leading to recurrence","Overlooking a coexisting plantarflexed metatarsal head and intractable plantar keratosis","Operating on a neuropathic or ischaemic foot without first assessing vascular and sensory status","Neglecting concurrent hallux valgus so the forefoot remains splayed after treatment"],"management":["First-line non-operative care with wide toe-box footwear to offload the lateral prominence","Protective padding and regular callus and skin care over the fifth metatarsal head","Accommodative orthoses to redistribute abnormal plantar and lateral loading","Simple analgesics or NSAIDs and treatment of any inflamed adventitial bursa","Surgical correction with a fifth metatarsal osteotomy selected to match the deformity type, or lateral condylectomy, when conservative measures fail","Assessment and concurrent management of coexisting hallux valgus and hindfoot malalignment","Vascular and neurological assessment of the foot before contemplating surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mann's Surgery of the Foot and Ankle","Apley and Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Trauma"],"crossLinks":[]},"takayasu_arteritis":{"name":"Takayasu arteritis","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TAK","pulseless disease","aortic arch syndrome","large-vessel vasculitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A large-vessel vasculitis affecting the aorta and its major branches, characteristically in young women under 40, often of Asian descent","An early systemic phase of malaise, fever, and arthralgia may precede a later occlusive phase with vascular symptoms","Classic signs include diminished or absent peripheral pulses, blood pressure asymmetry between limbs, vascular bruits, and limb claudication","Renovascular involvement is an important cause of secondary hypertension in young patients","Inflammatory markers are often raised but can be normal despite ongoing vascular damage","Cross-sectional vascular imaging (MR or CT angiography, PET) demonstrates wall thickening, stenoses, occlusions, and aneurysms","Blood pressure should be measured in the arm with the least affected artery, as readings can be falsely low with subclavian stenosis","Corticosteroids are first-line, with steroid-sparing immunosuppressants or biologics for relapsing disease, and revascularisation reserved for critical lesions once inflammation is controlled"],"pathophysiology":"Takayasu arteritis is a chronic granulomatous large-vessel vasculitis of unknown cause. Cell-mediated immunity drives infiltration of the arterial wall by T cells, macrophages, and giant cells, beginning in the adventitia and vasa vasorum and extending through the media. Chronic inflammation produces intimal proliferation, medial destruction, and fibrosis, leading to segmental stenosis, occlusion, aneurysm formation, and thrombosis of the aorta and its principal branches. The resulting reduced or absent blood flow causes the ischaemic and 'pulseless' clinical manifestations.","additionalDifferentials":["Giant cell arteritis (large-vessel involvement in older patients)","Fibromuscular dysplasia","Atherosclerotic occlusive disease","IgG4-related aortitis and other aortitides","Behçet disease with vascular involvement","Infective aortitis (e.g. syphilis, tuberculosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bilateral limb blood pressures and pulse examination with auscultation for bruits","MR or CT angiography of the aorta and branches for wall thickening, stenosis, and aneurysm","FDG-PET to assess vascular wall inflammatory activity","Inflammatory markers (ESR and CRP) recognising they may be normal","Assessment for renovascular hypertension and end-organ ischaemia"],"redFlags":["Cerebrovascular symptoms, visual loss, or syncope from carotid or vertebral artery involvement","Severe or refractory hypertension from renal artery stenosis with risk of end-organ damage","Critical limb or mesenteric ischaemia","Aortic aneurysm with risk of dissection or rupture, or significant aortic regurgitation","Myocardial ischaemia from coronary ostial involvement"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and immunosuppression many patients do well, but relapses are common and progressive vascular damage can cause hypertension, ischaemic complications, and aneurysms. Long-term morbidity relates to arterial stenoses, aortic regurgitation, and cardiovascular events.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis in a young woman with nonspecific systemic symptoms before vascular signs appear","Relying on a single-arm blood pressure that is falsely low due to subclavian stenosis","Assuming normal inflammatory markers exclude active disease","Attempting revascularisation during active inflammation with high restenosis risk","Overlooking renovascular hypertension as a treatable cause of raised blood pressure"],"management":["Induce remission with systemic glucocorticoids as first-line therapy for active large-vessel inflammation","Add a steroid-sparing agent such as methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate to maintain remission and limit corticosteroid toxicity","Reserve biologic therapy with tocilizumab or a TNF inhibitor for relapsing or refractory disease","Control renovascular and systemic hypertension, measuring pressure in an unaffected limb to avoid underestimation from subclavian stenosis","Monitor disease activity with serial inflammatory markers and cross-sectional vascular imaging (MR or CT angiography, or PET), recognising markers may be normal despite progression","Use antiplatelet therapy for ischaemic complications and manage cardiovascular risk factors","Defer revascularisation by angioplasty or bypass for critical stenoses to periods of disease quiescence, coordinated with vascular surgery","Manage through a rheumatology-led multidisciplinary team with vascular surgery and cardiology input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"takenouchi_kosaki_syndrome":{"name":"Takenouchi-Kosaki syndrome (CDC42 macrothrombocytopenia)","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CDC42","Takenouchi-Kosaki syndrome","TKS","CDC42-related macrothrombocytopenia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / Nathan and Oski","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal dominant multisystem disorder caused by heterozygous mutations in CDC42, presenting with macrothrombocytopenia alongside developmental and structural anomalies","Haematologically it produces mild-to-moderate thrombocytopenia with large platelets","Extra-haematological features include developmental delay, distinctive facial features, lymphoedema, and structural brain, cardiac, or skeletal anomalies","Some CDC42 variants cause an autoinflammatory phenotype with recurrent fevers and macrophage activation","The specific mutation influences whether the picture is predominantly developmental, autoinflammatory, or haematological","Bleeding tendency from the macrothrombocytopenia is usually mild","It sits within the expanding group of transcription-factor and cytoskeletal-regulator inherited thrombocytopenias","Diagnosis rests on CDC42 genetic testing supported by the platelet findings and syndromic features"],"pathophysiology":"CDC42 encodes a Rho-family small GTPase that governs actin cytoskeletal dynamics, cell polarity, and intracellular signalling across many tissues. Heterozygous mutations perturb these functions, and in megakaryocytes disrupted cytoskeletal regulation impairs proplatelet formation and platelet sizing, producing macrothrombocytopenia. Because CDC42 is critical in neural, lymphatic, and skeletal development and in immune regulation, the same mutations give rise to the developmental anomalies, lymphoedema, and, for certain variants, an autoinflammatory phenotype, explaining the multisystem nature of the syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["MYH9-related disorder","Other syndromic inherited macrothrombocytopenias","Noonan syndrome and related RASopathies (for overlapping features)","Wiskott-Aldrich spectrum disorders","Autoinflammatory syndromes with cytopenias","Immune thrombocytopenia (acquired)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and film confirming macrothrombocytopenia","CDC42 genetic testing","Developmental assessment and dysmorphology review","Neuroimaging, echocardiography, and skeletal evaluation for structural anomalies","Inflammatory markers and assessment for macrophage activation where an autoinflammatory phenotype is suspected","Platelet function testing where bleeding is disproportionate"],"redFlags":["Features of macrophage activation syndrome or severe autoinflammation with certain CDC42 variants","Significant structural anomalies such as major cardiac or brain malformations","Bleeding disproportionate to the platelet count","Progressive neurological or growth concerns requiring multidisciplinary care"],"prognosis":"Outlook varies with the specific variant and the severity of associated anomalies; the thrombocytopenia itself is usually mild, but developmental impairment and, in autoinflammatory variants, severe inflammation can dominate the clinical course.","pitfalls":["Attributing the isolated low platelet count to immune thrombocytopenia and overlooking the syndromic features","Failing to recognise the autoinflammatory phenotype in the relevant CDC42 variants","Not investigating for associated structural anomalies once the diagnosis is made","Assuming a purely haematological disorder rather than a multisystem condition"],"management":["Manage the usually mild bleeding tendency conservatively, reserving platelet transfusion for significant haemorrhage or surgical cover","Treat the autoinflammatory phenotype of specific variants with interleukin-1 or interleukin-18 directed biologic therapy","Provide developmental support with early intervention, physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy","Manage lymphoedema with skin care, compression and specialist lymphoedema therapy","Monitor for and manage structural cardiac, cerebral and skeletal anomalies with appropriate specialist follow-up","Watch for macrophage activation syndrome in autoinflammatory variants and escalate urgently with immunosuppression if it develops","Coordinate care through haematology, clinical genetics and relevant paediatric specialists","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant condition with testing of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"takotsubo_cardiomyopathy":{"name":"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["stress cardiomyopathy","apical ballooning syndrome","broken heart syndrome","takotsubo syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC position statement on Takotsubo syndrome / Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acute, usually reversible left ventricular dysfunction that mimics acute coronary syndrome, occurring predominantly in postmenopausal women","Typically triggered by intense emotional or physical stress, though a trigger is not always identified","Presents with chest pain, breathlessness, ECG changes such as ST elevation or deep T-wave inversion, and a modest troponin rise disproportionately small for the extent of wall-motion abnormality","The characteristic pattern is apical ballooning with basal hypercontractility, producing the octopus-pot shape that gives the syndrome its name, though midventricular and other variants occur","Coronary angiography shows no obstructive coronary disease sufficient to explain the regional dysfunction, which typically extends beyond a single coronary territory","Left ventricular systolic function usually recovers over days to weeks, distinguishing it from myocardial infarction","Complications during the acute phase include heart failure, dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction, arrhythmia, thrombus formation, and, rarely, free-wall rupture","Management is supportive and tailored to complications, with caution regarding inotropes when there is outflow obstruction"],"pathophysiology":"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy is thought to arise from a surge in circulating catecholamines, often provoked by acute emotional or physical stress, that produces transient myocardial stunning. The apex of the left ventricle, with its relatively high density of adrenergic receptors, is particularly susceptible, leading to the characteristic apical ballooning with preserved or hypercontractile basal segments. Proposed mechanisms include catecholamine-mediated microvascular dysfunction, direct myocyte injury, and transient coronary vasospasm, without a fixed obstructive coronary lesion to explain the regional dysfunction. Because the injury is functional rather than infarction, systolic function typically recovers once the catecholamine stress subsides.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute coronary syndrome / ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Acute myocarditis","Phaeochromocytoma-related cardiomyopathy","Coronary vasospasm","Neurogenic stunned myocardium (e.g. subarachnoid haemorrhage)","Other stress-related or catecholamine-driven cardiomyopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Coronary angiography to exclude obstructive coronary disease as the cause of regional dysfunction","Echocardiography or ventriculography to demonstrate the apical ballooning pattern and assess outflow obstruction","Cardiac MRI to show oedema without the late gadolinium enhancement pattern of infarction or myocarditis","Serial ECGs and cardiac biomarkers, noting the disproportionately small troponin rise","Assessment for a precipitating stressor and for phaeochromocytoma when clinically suggested","Follow-up imaging to confirm recovery of left ventricular function"],"redFlags":["Cardiogenic shock or acute pulmonary oedema during the acute phase","Dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction, which alters treatment choices","Malignant ventricular arrhythmia or marked QT prolongation","Left ventricular thrombus or, rarely, free-wall rupture"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover left ventricular function within days to weeks with a generally favourable outlook, but the acute phase can be complicated by heart failure, arrhythmia, and shock, and recurrence occurs in a minority.","pitfalls":["Assuming benignity and underestimating serious acute complications such as shock and outflow obstruction","Giving inotropes when dynamic outflow tract obstruction is present, potentially worsening the situation","Diagnosing it without excluding obstructive coronary disease by angiography","Overlooking phaeochromocytoma or another catecholamine source as an underlying cause"],"management":["Manage initially as suspected acute coronary syndrome until angiography excludes obstructive coronary disease","Identify and treat the precipitating emotional or physical stressor","Provide supportive heart-failure therapy with an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker and a beta-blocker during the recovery phase","Manage dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction with cautious beta-blockade, careful fluid loading and a pure vasoconstrictor, avoiding inotropes and vasodilators","Consider anticoagulation where apical akinesia raises the risk of left ventricular thrombus","Monitor for and treat acute complications including arrhythmia, cardiogenic shock and heart failure with intensive support as needed","Arrange follow-up echocardiography to confirm recovery of left ventricular function","Manage in cardiology with continued attention to recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC Heart Failure Association position statement on Takotsubo syndrome","Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"talaromycosis":{"name":"Talaromycosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["penicilliosis","Talaromyces marneffei infection","Penicillium marneffei infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India NTEP","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An invasive mycosis caused by the dimorphic fungus Talaromyces marneffei, endemic to South-East Asia, southern China and parts of north-eastern India","It occurs predominantly in people with advanced HIV and low CD4 counts, and is an AIDS-defining opportunistic infection in endemic regions","Typical features are fever, weight loss, anaemia, hepatosplenomegaly, lymphadenopathy and characteristic umbilicated, molluscum-like skin papules often on the face and trunk","Exposure to bamboo rats and their habitats is epidemiologically linked, though the precise mode of human acquisition is thought to be inhalational","Diagnosis is confirmed by culture from blood, skin, bone marrow or lymph node, with the fungus showing characteristic fission-yeast morphology and a diffusible red pigment in culture","It closely mimics disseminated tuberculosis, histoplasmosis and cryptococcosis, so a high index of suspicion is essential in HIV-positive travellers or residents from endemic areas","Treatment follows an induction phase with amphotericin-based therapy followed by azole consolidation and, in HIV, secondary prophylaxis until immune reconstitution"],"pathophysiology":"Talaromyces marneffei is inhaled as conidia that convert to a pathogenic yeast-like form dividing by fission at body temperature. In hosts with intact cell-mediated immunity infection is usually contained, but in advanced HIV or other T-cell deficiency the organism proliferates within macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system. Haematogenous dissemination follows, seeding skin, bone marrow, liver, spleen and lymph nodes, producing a granulomatous, suppurative or necrotising reaction depending on immune status. Uncontrolled fungaemia leads to sepsis and multiorgan involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Disseminated tuberculosis","Disseminated histoplasmosis","Cryptococcosis","Molluscum contagiosum (for skin lesions)","Disseminated non-tuberculous mycobacterial infection"],"infectionMimics":["Disseminated tuberculosis","Histoplasmosis","Cryptococcosis with umbilicated skin lesions","Molluscum contagiosum in HIV"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fungal culture of blood, skin lesion, bone marrow or lymph node aspirate","Microscopy of skin scrapings or tissue for intracellular fission yeasts","HIV testing and CD4 count in all suspected cases","Antigen-based assays where available for earlier diagnosis","Assessment for concurrent opportunistic infections such as tuberculosis"],"redFlags":["Disseminated disease with sepsis and multiorgan involvement","Advanced HIV with very low CD4 count and high fungal burden","Respiratory failure or haemodynamic instability","Delayed diagnosis due to mimicry of tuberculosis, which markedly increases mortality"],"prognosis":"Untreated disseminated talaromycosis is almost uniformly fatal, but early antifungal induction and consolidation with antiretroviral therapy and secondary prophylaxis achieve good outcomes; mortality remains high when diagnosis is delayed or the patient presents with sepsis.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as tuberculosis and delaying antifungal therapy","Stopping antifungals or secondary prophylaxis prematurely before immune reconstitution","Overlooking the diagnosis in HIV patients with relevant travel or residence history","Attributing umbilicated skin lesions solely to molluscum contagiosum"],"management":["Treat disseminated disease with induction antifungal therapy using liposomal amphotericin B","Follow induction with consolidation itraconazole therapy, with voriconazole as an alternative","Continue secondary antifungal prophylaxis with itraconazole until immune reconstitution on antiretroviral therapy","Initiate or optimise antiretroviral therapy in HIV-associated disease, timing it to reduce immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome","Provide supportive care for sepsis, anaemia and organ dysfunction during the acute illness","Screen for and manage concurrent opportunistic infections such as tuberculosis","Monitor CD4 count and treatment response, escalating for severe sepsis or multiorgan involvement","Coordinate care between infectious diseases and HIV services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/NTEP guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"talcosis":{"name":"Talcosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["talc pneumoconiosis","talco-silicosis","intravenous talcosis","talc granulomatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Occupational and Environmental Respiratory Medicine reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by exposure to talc (hydrated magnesium silicate), either inhaled occupationally (mining, milling, ceramics, cosmetics, rubber) or introduced intravenously","Intravenous talcosis occurs when injection drug users inject crushed oral tablets containing talc filler, producing foreign-body granulomas around vascular deposits","Occupational talc is often contaminated with silica or asbestos fibres, so disease may reflect talco-silicosis or talco-asbestosis rather than pure talc effect","Birefringent talc particles are seen under polarised light within granulomas and giant cells on biopsy","Intravenous disease characteristically causes lower-zone or perihilar micronodularity that may coalesce into masses resembling progressive massive fibrosis","Panacinar lower-zone emphysema is a recognised feature of intravenous talcosis, particularly with methylphenidate injection","Pulmonary hypertension can develop from vascular talc granulomas obstructing small pulmonary arteries"],"pathophysiology":"Talc particles reaching the lung, whether inhaled or delivered intravenously, are engulfed by macrophages and provoke a foreign-body granulomatous reaction with multinucleated giant cells surrounding birefringent crystals. In inhalational disease the particles deposit in airways and interstitium, and any accompanying silica or asbestos amplifies fibrosis. In intravenous talcosis, filler particles lodge in pulmonary arterioles and capillaries, seeding perivascular granulomas that can coalesce into fibrotic conglomerate masses, obliterate the vasculature to cause pulmonary hypertension, and, through mechanisms not fully understood, promote basal panacinar emphysema. Progressive interstitial fibrosis may result from ongoing exposure.","additionalDifferentials":["Silicosis and mixed-dust pneumoconiosis","Asbestosis","Sarcoidosis","Progressive massive fibrosis from other dusts","Metastatic malignancy (masses)","Other causes of pulmonary hypertension","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution CT to define micronodularity, conglomerate masses, and emphysema distribution","Occupational history and, where relevant, a sensitive history of injection drug use","Lung biopsy with polarised-light microscopy demonstrating birefringent talc crystals","Bronchoalveolar lavage in selected cases","Pulmonary function tests and diffusing capacity","Echocardiography or right heart assessment for pulmonary hypertension"],"redFlags":["Progressive pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain","Rapidly enlarging masses requiring exclusion of malignancy","Severe basal emphysema with respiratory failure in injection drug users","Signs of ongoing injection drug use with attendant infective risks"],"prognosis":"Pure occupational talcosis is often indolent, but intravenous talcosis and talc-silicosis can progress to disabling fibrosis, emphysema, pulmonary hypertension, and respiratory failure; cessation of exposure or drug injection is essential to limit progression.","pitfalls":["Failing to elicit a history of injection drug use in a patient with unexplained micronodular disease","Attributing conglomerate masses to malignancy without considering talcosis","Assuming occupational talc disease is pure when silica or asbestos contamination is common","Overlooking developing pulmonary hypertension in intravenous talcosis"],"management":["Eliminate ongoing exposure by removing the patient from occupational talc and supporting cessation of injection drug use, the essential step to limit progression","Offer addiction support and harm-reduction services where intravenous disease relates to injection of crushed oral tablets","Provide symptomatic support with supplemental oxygen for hypoxaemia, bronchodilators and pulmonary rehabilitation","Consider a trial of corticosteroids for progressive granulomatous inflammation, recognising the evidence is limited","Encourage smoking cessation and give influenza and pneumococcal vaccination to protect remaining lung function","Screen for and manage pulmonary hypertension arising from vascular obliteration","Refer advanced fibrotic or emphysematous disease for lung transplantation assessment","Manage through respiratory and occupational lung disease specialists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Parkes' Occupational Lung Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"tangier_disease":{"name":"Tangier disease (ABCA1, analphalipoproteinaemia neuropathy)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["familial high-density lipoprotein deficiency","ABCA1 deficiency","Tangier neuropathy","ABCA1","alpha-lipoprotein deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley and Daroff Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder of cholesterol efflux caused by variants in ABCA1, producing very low high-density lipoprotein and low total cholesterol with near-absent apolipoprotein A-I","Cholesteryl ester accumulates in the reticuloendothelial system, giving the hallmark enlarged orange or yellow-grey tonsils, hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy","Peripheral neuropathy is a defining feature and takes two main forms: a relapsing-remitting multifocal (mononeuritis multiplex-like) neuropathy and a slowly progressive syringomyelia-like pattern with dissociated sensory loss","The pseudosyringomyelic form causes loss of pain and temperature sensation with relative sparing of light touch and proprioception, mimicking a central cord lesion","A markedly low HDL cholesterol in a patient with neuropathy and prominent tonsils should prompt consideration of Tangier disease","Despite low overall cholesterol, patients have an increased risk of premature atherosclerotic vascular disease because protective HDL is deficient","Diagnosis rests on the characteristic lipid profile and clinical features, confirmed by ABCA1 genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Tangier disease is caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in ABCA1, a transporter that mediates the efflux of cellular cholesterol and phospholipid onto lipid-poor apolipoprotein A-I to form nascent high-density lipoprotein. Without functional ABCA1, apolipoprotein A-I is not lipidated and is rapidly catabolised, so plasma HDL and apolipoprotein A-I fall to very low levels and total cholesterol is reduced. Unesterified cholesterol and cholesteryl esters accumulate within macrophages throughout the reticuloendothelial system, producing the enlarged orange tonsils, hepatosplenomegaly and foam-cell infiltration. Lipid accumulation within Schwann cells and nerves is thought to underlie the demyelinating and axonal neuropathy, which can be multifocal and relapsing or produce a syringomyelia-like dissociated sensory loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Analphalipoproteinaemia and other severe HDL-deficiency states","Syringomyelia and other intramedullary cord lesions","Leprous neuropathy with dissociated sensory loss","Mononeuritis multiplex from vasculitis","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy","Hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting lipid panel showing very low HDL cholesterol, low total cholesterol and reduced apolipoprotein A-I","ABCA1 sequencing to confirm the diagnosis","Nerve conduction studies characterising the multifocal or sensory-predominant neuropathy","Nerve biopsy showing lipid-laden Schwann cells in selected cases","Examination and imaging of tonsils, liver and spleen for lipid deposition","Cardiovascular risk assessment given the predisposition to atherosclerosis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or severe multifocal neuropathy causing functional loss","Symptoms of premature coronary or cerebrovascular disease","Progressive dissociated sensory loss raising concern for a cord lesion needing imaging","Painless injuries or burns from unrecognised pain and temperature loss"],"prognosis":"The neuropathy may relapse and remit or slowly progress and can cause substantial disability; long-term outlook is also influenced by an increased risk of premature atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease.","pitfalls":["Overlooking a very low HDL as a clue and attributing neuropathy to a more common cause","Mistaking the pseudosyringomyelic sensory loss for a primary spinal cord lesion","Assuming low total cholesterol is protective and neglecting cardiovascular risk","Failing to examine the tonsils, which show the characteristic orange discolouration"],"management":["Recognise there is no therapy to correct the ABCA1 defect, so management centres on complications and cardiovascular risk reduction","Aggressively manage atherosclerotic risk with lifestyle measures, LDL-lowering therapy and control of other risk factors despite the low total cholesterol","Provide symptomatic and rehabilitative care for the peripheral neuropathy with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and neuropathic pain agents","Consider tonsillectomy where enlarged tonsils cause obstructive symptoms","Monitor neurological progression with periodic clinical and nerve conduction assessment","Undertake regular cardiovascular surveillance for premature atherosclerotic disease","Coordinate care across neurology, lipidology and cardiology","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder with carrier testing of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews / OMIM: Tangier Disease (ABCA1)","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"tardive_akathisia":{"name":"tardive akathisia","system":"nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tardive akathisic syndrome","chronic neuroleptic-induced akathisia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A persistent, late-onset movement disorder of inner restlessness and a compelling urge to move, developing after prolonged exposure to dopamine-receptor-blocking agents and continuing well after acute akathisia would be expected to settle.","Distinguished from acute akathisia by its delayed emergence, chronicity, and tendency to persist or worsen when the offending drug is reduced or withdrawn, rather than on initiation or dose increase.","The subjective distress (tension, dysphoria, inability to keep still) is often more prominent than in tardive dyskinesia and can be mistaken for anxiety, agitation or worsening of the underlying psychiatric illness.","Characteristic motor features include marching on the spot, shifting weight, crossing and uncrossing legs, truncal rocking and inability to remain seated.","It frequently coexists with tardive dyskinesia and tardive dystonia, and complex stereotypic leg movements may blur the boundary between restlessness and dyskinesia.","Increasing the causative antipsychotic may transiently suppress symptoms (masking), reinforcing a cycle of escalating exposure; the diagnosis requires a careful temporal drug history.","Recognised because of its strong association with dysphoria, impulsivity and heightened suicide risk, warranting proactive mood and safety assessment.","Management centres on reviewing the need for and dose of the dopamine blocker, considering a switch to an agent with lower liability, and specific pharmacological strategies including VMAT2 inhibitors and, in selected cases, beta-adrenergic blockade."],"pathophysiology":"Tardive akathisia is attributed to chronic postsynaptic dopamine D2-receptor blockade producing receptor supersensitivity and dysregulation of mesocortical, mesolimbic and nigrostriatal dopaminergic pathways that subserve motor drive and reward. Imbalance between dopaminergic and noradrenergic tone, and altered signalling in ventral striatal and limbic circuits, is thought to generate both the subjective restlessness and the semi-voluntary movements. As with other tardive syndromes, cumulative and high-potency drug exposure, and individual genetic and neurochemical vulnerability, shape the risk and persistence.","additionalDifferentials":["acute drug-induced akathisia","psychomotor agitation of the underlying psychiatric disorder","restless legs syndrome and periodic limb movements","tardive dyskinesia with stereotypic leg movements","anxiety or agitated depression","hyperthyroidism or stimulant/substance-related restlessness","drug-withdrawal states"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["structured temporal history of all dopamine-blocking agents including antiemetics and any recent dose changes","standardised assessment with the Barnes Akathisia Rating Scale for severity and monitoring","mood, impulsivity and suicide-risk evaluation given the recognised association","screening for restless legs syndrome including iron studies where the history overlaps","thyroid function tests to exclude a metabolic driver of restlessness","review for coexisting tardive dyskinesia, dystonia or parkinsonism on examination"],"redFlags":["emergent suicidal ideation, severe dysphoria or impulsive behaviour","escalating distress prompting inappropriate increases in the causative drug","features suggesting an evolving neuroleptic malignant syndrome such as rigidity, fever and autonomic instability","profound functional impairment or inability to rest and sleep"],"prognosis":"Tardive akathisia is often chronic and may persist for years, and can worsen transiently on withdrawal of the causative agent; some patients improve gradually once the offending drug is stopped or switched. The associated dysphoria and suicide risk make early recognition clinically important.","pitfalls":["misattributing the restlessness to worsening psychosis, anxiety or agitation and escalating the antipsychotic dose","overlooking the heightened suicide and impulsivity risk associated with the disorder","failing to distinguish tardive from acute akathisia by ignoring the temporal relationship to drug changes","missing coexisting restless legs syndrome that may respond to iron repletion or dopaminergic strategies","not recognising covert dopamine antagonists such as metoclopramide as the responsible agent"],"management":["Review and, where clinically safe, gradually withdraw or reduce the causative dopamine-blocking agent, avoiding dose increases that merely mask symptoms","Where antipsychotic treatment must continue, switch to a lower-potency agent or to clozapine","Consider a VMAT2 inhibitor such as valbenazine, deutetrabenazine or tetrabenazine to suppress the movement disorder","Trial adjuncts such as a beta-blocker or clonidine for the subjective restlessness","Discontinue offending antiemetics such as metoclopramide and prochlorperazine and use non-dopaminergic alternatives","Assess and manage the associated dysphoria and suicide risk, treating comorbid mood symptoms","Monitor severity with a structured akathisia rating scale over time","Manage jointly with neurology and psychiatry"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice, current edition","Fahn and Jankovic, Principles and Practice of Movement Disorders, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"tardive_dyskinesia":{"name":"Tardive dyskinesia","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tardive dyskinesia","neuroleptic-induced tardive dyskinesia","TD","orofacial dyskinesia (drug-induced)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A delayed, drug-induced movement disorder that emerges after prolonged exposure to dopamine-receptor blocking agents","The classic picture is orofacial (bucco-linguo-masticatory) choreoathetosis with chewing, lip-smacking, tongue protrusion and grimacing","Antipsychotics are the commonest culprits, but antiemetics such as metoclopramide and prochlorperazine are also important causes","Onset is typically after months to years of exposure, and movements may first appear or worsen when the offending drug is reduced or withdrawn","Older age, female sex, longer exposure and use of first-generation agents increase the risk","Movements may persist, or be irreversible, even after the causative drug is stopped","It should be distinguished from acute dystonia, akathisia and drug-induced parkinsonism, which have different time courses"],"pathophysiology":"Tardive dyskinesia is thought to develop from chronic blockade of dopamine D2 receptors, leading to postsynaptic dopamine-receptor supersensitivity and up-regulation, altered signalling within striatal output pathways, and possible oxidative injury to vulnerable neurones. This maladaptive plasticity disturbs basal ganglia control of movement and produces the characteristic involuntary, often orofacial, hyperkinesias. Because the underlying changes can become fixed, the movements may persist long after the offending drug has been withdrawn.","additionalDifferentials":["Huntington's disease and other genetic choreas","Spontaneous orofacial dyskinesia of the elderly and edentulous (denture-related) dyskinesia","Wilson's disease","Levodopa-induced dyskinesia in Parkinson's disease","Focal or segmental dystonia of other cause","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful drug history documenting cumulative exposure to dopamine-blocking agents","Structured rating of abnormal involuntary movements to monitor severity over time","Bloods to exclude metabolic and other secondary causes of chorea","Copper studies where Wilson's disease is plausible in a younger patient","Genetic testing for Huntington's disease when history or examination suggest it","Neuroimaging where the presentation is atypical or focal signs are present"],"redFlags":["Severe orofacial, pharyngeal or truncal movements interfering with speech, swallowing or breathing","Rapid progression or prominent cognitive or psychiatric change suggesting Huntington's disease","Young patient with chorea and hepatic or psychiatric features suggesting Wilson's disease","Coexisting fever, rigidity and autonomic instability suggesting a neuroleptic emergency"],"prognosis":"The course is variable; some patients improve or remit after the offending agent is reduced or stopped, but a substantial proportion have persistent or irreversible movements. Early recognition and review of the causative drug improve outcome, and VMAT2-inhibiting therapy can meaningfully reduce symptoms. Prevention through judicious prescribing and regular monitoring remains the most effective strategy.","pitfalls":["Abruptly stopping the causative drug, which can transiently worsen the dyskinesia","Increasing the antipsychotic dose to mask movements, which perpetuates the underlying problem","Misdiagnosing spontaneous orofacial dyskinesia or Huntington's disease as tardive dyskinesia","Failing to review the ongoing need for, and dose of, dopamine-blocking medication"],"management":["Treat with a VMAT2 inhibitor such as valbenazine or deutetrabenazine as the evidence-based mainstay of symptomatic therapy","Review the need for the causative agent and, where possible, reduce the dose or switch to a lower-risk agent such as clozapine, avoiding abrupt withdrawal that can transiently worsen movements","Discontinue causative antiemetics such as metoclopramide and prochlorperazine where they can be stopped","Withdraw anticholinergic drugs, which tend to worsen orofacial dyskinesia","Consider botulinum toxin injection for focal or troublesome regional movements","Monitor severity with structured rating of abnormal involuntary movements over time","Prevent progression by prescribing dopamine-blocking agents at the lowest effective dose with regular review","Refer to neurology for specialist movement-disorder management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice, current edition","Fahn and Jankovic, Principles and Practice of Movement Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"tardive_dystonia":{"name":"tardive dystonia","system":"nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tardive dystonic syndrome","neuroleptic-induced tardive dystonia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A form of tardive syndrome characterised by sustained or repetitive muscle contractions producing twisting, patterned postures, arising after prolonged exposure to dopamine-receptor-blocking agents (typical and atypical antipsychotics, metoclopramide, prochlorperazine).","Unlike classic tardive dyskinesia, it favours younger adults and men, and frequently involves axial and cranial musculature, producing retrocollis, opisthotonic trunk posturing, and characteristic arm extension with internal rotation and wrist flexion.","Onset may follow months of exposure rather than years, and by definition persists beyond a defined interval after the offending drug is stopped; it may be irreversible.","Blepharospasm, oromandibular dystonia and a fixed 'tardive' grimace overlap with tardive dyskinesia, but the sustained, directional, action- or posture-related nature distinguishes dystonia.","Sensory tricks (geste antagoniste) may transiently relieve the posturing, mirroring idiopathic focal dystonias.","Paradoxically, cautious increase of the causative dopamine blocker can mask symptoms while worsening the underlying disorder ('masking'); withdrawal often unmasks or intensifies the dystonia.","Anticholinergics may help tardive dystonia (in contrast to tardive dyskinesia, which they often worsen), and this differential drug response is a useful bedside clue.","VMAT2 inhibitors, tetrabenazine-class agents, botulinum toxin for focal distributions, and deep brain stimulation of the globus pallidus internus are recognised options for refractory disease."],"pathophysiology":"Prolonged blockade of striatal postsynaptic dopamine D2 receptors by antipsychotics or antiemetic dopamine antagonists is thought to induce receptor supersensitivity and maladaptive plasticity within striato-pallido-thalamocortical motor circuits, alongside altered signalling in the indirect and direct basal ganglia pathways. Compared with tardive dyskinesia, tardive dystonia is postulated to reflect a distinct imbalance favouring sustained co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles, with contributions from dysregulated GABAergic and cholinergic tone and possible oxidative injury to striatal neurones. Individual susceptibility is modulated by genetic factors, cumulative and potency-weighted drug exposure, and coexisting mood or psychotic illness.","additionalDifferentials":["idiopathic (primary) focal or segmental dystonia","acute dystonic reaction to dopamine-blocking drugs","Wilson disease and other inherited metabolic dystonias","tardive dyskinesia and other tardive syndromes","dystonia of Huntington disease or neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation","functional (psychogenic) dystonia","structural basal ganglia lesion or paraneoplastic movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["detailed longitudinal drug-exposure history including antiemetics and hidden dopamine blockers","serum caeruloplasmin, copper studies and slit-lamp examination to exclude Wilson disease in younger patients","MRI brain to exclude structural, iron-deposition or degenerative pathology","genetic testing for inherited dystonia syndromes where phenotype or family history suggests","standardised rating with the Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale and dystonia severity scales for monitoring","review for coexisting parkinsonism or tardive dyskinesia on structured examination"],"redFlags":["laryngeal or pharyngeal dystonia threatening the airway or causing stridor and dysphagia","opisthotonic or severe axial posturing risking respiratory compromise or spinal injury","rapidly progressive dystonia with autonomic instability or rigidity suggesting an emerging neuroleptic malignant syndrome","new focal neurological deficit or atypical features prompting urgent structural imaging"],"prognosis":"Tardive dystonia is frequently persistent and may be permanent even after the offending agent is withdrawn, though a minority remit over months to years. Early recognition and cessation of the causative drug improve the chance of partial or complete recovery.","pitfalls":["mislabelling sustained posturing as tardive dyskinesia and prescribing anticholinergics inappropriately, or withholding them when they might help dystonia","increasing the causative antipsychotic to 'treat' worsening movements, thereby masking and perpetuating the disorder","overlooking covert dopamine antagonists such as metoclopramide and prochlorperazine in the exposure history","attributing early or reversible posturing to a functional disorder and delaying drug withdrawal","failing to screen younger patients for Wilson disease before ascribing dystonia solely to drug exposure"],"management":["Review and, where safe, withdraw or reduce the causative dopamine-blocking agent, switching to clozapine if antipsychotic cover remains necessary","Use botulinum toxin injection targeted to the overactive muscles as a mainstay for focal or segmental dystonia","Consider a VMAT2 inhibitor or tetrabenazine for generalised or refractory movements","Trial anticholinergic therapy, which unlike in tardive dyskinesia may relieve the dystonic posturing","Discontinue causative antiemetics such as metoclopramide and prochlorperazine where possible","Refer severe, disabling and drug-refractory dystonia for pallidal deep brain stimulation assessment","Monitor with structured involuntary-movement and dystonia severity scales","Manage through neurology with psychiatric liaison"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice, current edition","Fahn and Jankovic, Principles and Practice of Movement Disorders, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"tarlov_cyst":{"name":"Tarlov (perineural) cyst","system":"Musculoskeletal / Spine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["perineural cyst","sacral nerve root cyst","Tarlov perineural cyst","sacral meningeal cyst type II"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Perineural (Tarlov) cysts are cerebrospinal-fluid-filled dilatations of the nerve-root sheath, most commonly at the sacral (S2/S3) levels","The majority are incidental and asymptomatic, found on MRI performed for other reasons","When symptomatic they can cause sacral or perineal pain, radiculopathy, and sometimes bladder, bowel, or sexual dysfunction","Symptoms may be positional, worsening with standing, the Valsalva manoeuvre, or activities that raise cerebrospinal fluid pressure","They are distinguished from other meningeal cysts by containing nerve-root fibres within the cyst wall or cavity","MRI shows a well-defined cystic lesion following cerebrospinal fluid signal, often with bony scalloping of the sacrum","Because incidental cysts are common, symptoms must be carefully correlated before attributing them to the cyst","Most are managed conservatively, and selected symptomatic cysts may be treated with image-guided aspiration or surgical fenestration and imbrication, although recurrence is a recognised problem"],"pathophysiology":"Tarlov (perineural) cysts are dilatations of the meningeal sheath around the posterior nerve root and dorsal root ganglion, most often in the sacral region. They contain cerebrospinal fluid and, characteristically, nerve-root fibres within their wall or lumen. A ball-valve mechanism is proposed, whereby cerebrospinal fluid enters the cyst but leaves less readily, allowing gradual enlargement, and raised cerebrospinal fluid pressure such as during the Valsalva manoeuvre may drive expansion. Progressive growth can compress adjacent nerve roots and erode the surrounding bone, producing radicular or sphincter symptoms in the minority who become symptomatic.","additionalDifferentials":["Other meningeal or arachnoid cysts","Lumbosacral disc herniation or stenosis with radiculopathy","Coccydynia or sacroiliac joint pain","Sacral tumour such as chordoma or schwannoma, or an anterior sacral meningocele","Endometriosis for cyclical perineal or pelvic symptoms","Pelvic floor or gynaecological and urological pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the lumbosacral spine and sacrum, following cerebrospinal fluid signal and showing nerve-root fibres","CT or CT myelography to assess bony scalloping and any communication with the thecal sac","Careful clinical correlation of symptoms with the cyst location and level","Urodynamic studies if bladder dysfunction is present","Imaging to exclude alternative sacral pathology"],"redFlags":["New or progressive bladder, bowel, or sexual dysfunction","Progressive neurological deficit","Features suggesting a solid sacral tumour rather than a simple cyst"],"prognosis":"Most perineural cysts are asymptomatic and require only reassurance; symptomatic cysts have a more variable course, and although intervention can relieve symptoms in selected patients, recurrence and cerebrospinal-fluid-related complications temper outcomes.","pitfalls":["Attributing symptoms to an incidental cyst without careful correlation","Overlooking an alternative, treatable cause of sacral radicular symptoms","Underestimating the risk of cerebrospinal fluid leak and recurrence after intervention"],"management":["Reassure and observe the majority, which are incidental and asymptomatic, without intervention","Manage symptomatic cysts conservatively with analgesia, neuropathic pain agents, physiotherapy and avoidance of activities that raise cerebrospinal fluid pressure","Consider image-guided percutaneous aspiration, often with fibrin glue injection, for selected symptomatic cysts","Reserve surgical options such as cyst fenestration, imbrication or wrapping for refractory cases, accepting a risk of recurrence and cerebrospinal-fluid complications","Assess and manage bladder, bowel and sexual dysfunction, with urodynamic studies where indicated","Escalate urgently for progressive neurological deficit or new sphincter disturbance","Refer symptomatic or enlarging cysts to spinal neurosurgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","Osborn: Diagnostic Imaging: Spine"],"crossLinks":["coccydynia","lumbar_disc_herniation"]},"tarsal_coalition":{"name":"Tarsal coalition","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["peroneal spastic flatfoot","calcaneonavicular coalition","talocalcaneal coalition","tarsal bar"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An abnormal bony, cartilaginous or fibrous union between two or more tarsal bones arising from a failure of embryonic segmentation, not an osteonecrosis or apophysitis","The two most common types are calcaneonavicular and talocalcaneal (subtalar) coalitions","Typically becomes symptomatic in later childhood or adolescence, roughly eight to sixteen years, as a fibrous or cartilaginous bar ossifies and stiffens","Presents with a rigid flatfoot, recurrent ankle sprains, activity-related hindfoot pain and limited subtalar motion","The classic clinical picture is a peroneal spastic flatfoot, in which the peroneal muscles are held in protective spasm and the hindfoot does not invert on tiptoe standing","Radiographic clues include the C-sign and talar beaking in talocalcaneal coalition and the anteater nose sign in calcaneonavicular coalition","Computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging confirms the coalition, defines its type and extent and detects secondary joint changes","Management ranges from activity modification, orthoses and immobilisation for symptom control to surgical resection or arthrodesis as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Tarsal coalition results from a failure of normal segmentation of the primitive mesenchyme during foetal development, so that two or more tarsal bones remain abnormally joined by bone (synostosis), cartilage (synchondrosis) or fibrous tissue (syndesmosis). Early in life a fibrous or cartilaginous coalition may permit some movement and remain asymptomatic, but as the child grows the bar tends to ossify and progressively restrict motion at the affected joint, most often the subtalar or calcaneonavicular articulation. The loss of hindfoot mobility transfers abnormal stress to adjacent joints and provokes protective spasm of the peroneal muscles, producing the characteristic rigid, everted flatfoot. Over time the restricted, abnormally loaded joints are prone to secondary degenerative change.","additionalDifferentials":["Flexible flatfoot","Inflammatory or juvenile idiopathic arthritis of the hindfoot","Recurrent lateral ankle instability","Accessory navicular syndrome","Post-traumatic subtalar stiffness or arthritis","Infection or tumour of the hindfoot"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Weight-bearing foot radiographs including an oblique view for calcaneonavicular coalition","Assessment for the C-sign, talar beaking and the anteater nose sign","Computed tomography to define bony coalitions and their extent","Magnetic resonance imaging to detect fibrous or cartilaginous coalitions and marrow oedema","Clinical assessment of subtalar motion and hindfoot flexibility on tiptoe standing","Examination for peroneal muscle spasm reproducing the rigid flatfoot"],"redFlags":["A rigid, painful flatfoot that does not correct on tiptoe standing, indicating a fixed structural cause","Established secondary degenerative change in the subtalar or adjacent joints","Progressive pain and deformity limiting walking despite conservative measures","Atypical features such as warmth, swelling or systemic upset suggesting infection or inflammatory arthritis"],"prognosis":"Many patients improve with conservative measures, particularly when a limited coalition is treated before secondary arthritis develops; larger coalitions, established degenerative change or persistent pain may require surgery, with resection favoured for smaller bars and arthrodesis for extensive coalitions or arthritic joints.","pitfalls":["Labelling a rigid flatfoot as a simple flexible flatfoot and missing an underlying coalition","Relying on plain radiographs alone and overlooking a fibrous or cartilaginous coalition best seen on cross-sectional imaging","Attributing recurrent ankle sprains to instability without recognising restricted subtalar motion","Proceeding to resection when extensive coalition or established arthritis would be better served by arthrodesis"],"management":["Begin with conservative management using activity modification, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory analgesia and supportive orthoses to offload the hindfoot","Use a period of cast or walking-boot immobilisation to settle acute pain and peroneal spasm","Provide physiotherapy for hindfoot stretching, peroneal spasm and strengthening once acute symptoms settle","Consider a corticosteroid injection to the coalition for persistent inflammatory pain","Offer surgical resection of the bar with interposition of fat or muscle for smaller symptomatic coalitions without established arthritis","Reserve arthrodesis for extensive coalitions or established secondary degenerative change","Refer persistent or disabling cases to orthopaedic foot and ankle surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"tarsal_tunnel_posterior":{"name":"tarsal tunnel posterior","system":"nervous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["posterior tarsal tunnel syndrome","tibial nerve entrapment at the ankle"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Entrapment of the posterior tibial nerve or its terminal branches beneath the flexor retinaculum behind the medial malleolus, producing burning plantar pain and paraesthesiae.","Symptoms are typically worse with standing and walking and may radiate proximally along the medial calf (the Valleix phenomenon).","A Tinel sign behind the medial malleolus, and reproduction of symptoms with sustained eversion and dorsiflexion, support the diagnosis.","Selective involvement of the medial or lateral plantar branch, or the calcaneal branch, produces a restricted sensory pattern that aids localisation.","A space-occupying lesion such as a ganglion, varicosity, lipoma, accessory muscle or tenosynovitis is identified in a substantial proportion of cases and should be actively sought.","The condition is markedly less common than carpal tunnel syndrome, and overdiagnosis is a recognised problem when plantar symptoms have another cause.","Electrodiagnostic studies with distal tibial motor and mixed or sensory plantar conductions help confirm the diagnosis but can be technically difficult.","Hindfoot malalignment, particularly a valgus or planovalgus foot, increases traction on the nerve and predisposes to entrapment."],"pathophysiology":"The posterior tibial nerve passes through the fibro-osseous tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus, bounded by the flexor retinaculum, where it divides into the medial and lateral plantar and calcaneal branches. Increased pressure within this confined space from a ganglion, tenosynovitis, varicosities, an accessory or hypertrophied muscle, post-traumatic fibrosis, or biomechanical traction in hindfoot valgus produces focal demyelination and axonal injury. Branch-specific compression within separate compartments accounts for the variable sensory patterns, and systemic conditions causing nerve vulnerability may lower the threshold for symptomatic entrapment.","additionalDifferentials":["plantar fasciitis","peripheral polyneuropathy, including diabetic","S1 lumbosacral radiculopathy","Morton neuroma","medial plantar nerve entrapment (jogger's foot)","calcaneal stress fracture","posterior tibial tendinopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["nerve conduction studies of medial and lateral plantar nerves with tibial motor studies","needle electromyography of intrinsic foot muscles","magnetic resonance imaging of the ankle to identify a space-occupying lesion","high-resolution ultrasound of the tarsal tunnel","weight-bearing foot radiographs to assess alignment and bony anomalies","screening for diabetes and other causes of polyneuropathy","contralateral comparison electrodiagnostic studies"],"redFlags":["a rapidly enlarging or firm mass within the tarsal tunnel suggesting neoplasm","progressive motor weakness or intrinsic foot muscle wasting","constitutional symptoms or features of systemic inflammatory or infiltrative disease","bilateral or diffuse sensory loss pointing to a generalised neuropathy rather than local entrapment"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are best when a discrete compressive lesion is found and released, whereas idiopathic cases respond less predictably to decompression. Conservative measures including orthoses and corticosteroid injection help many patients with milder or biomechanical presentations.","pitfalls":["diagnosing tarsal tunnel syndrome when a distal polyneuropathy is the true cause of plantar symptoms","failing to image the tunnel and thereby missing a treatable space-occupying lesion","over-interpreting technically difficult plantar nerve conduction studies","neglecting hindfoot malalignment that will cause recurrence after surgery"],"management":["Identify and surgically excise any space-occupying cause such as a ganglion, lipoma, varicosity or hypertrophied accessory muscle, which affords the best outcomes","Correct biomechanical contributors including hindfoot valgus with orthoses and footwear modification","Provide conservative measures including activity modification, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory analgesia and neuropathic pain agents","Consider a corticosteroid injection into the tarsal tunnel for milder or inflammatory presentations","Offer surgical decompression by release of the flexor retinaculum when conservative measures fail and a compressive lesion is confirmed","Screen for and manage diabetes and other systemic causes of neuropathy","Monitor recovery and refer to a peripheral nerve or foot and ankle surgeon for persistent symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies","Dawson, Hallett and Wilbourn: Entrapment Neuropathies","Preston and Shapiro: Electromyography and Neuromuscular Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"tarsal_tunnel_syndrome":{"name":"Tarsal tunnel syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Hand & Peripheral nerve","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["posterior tibial nerve entrapment","tibial nerve compression at the ankle","posterior tarsal tunnel syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery / Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Entrapment of the posterior tibial nerve or its branches beneath the flexor retinaculum (laciniate ligament) behind and below the medial malleolus","Presents with burning pain, paraesthesiae and numbness of the sole and toes, often worse on standing or walking and sometimes nocturnal","A Tinel sign behind the medial malleolus that radiates into the foot supports the diagnosis","The tunnel contents from anterior to posterior are tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, the posterior tibial artery and vein, the tibial nerve and flexor hallucis longus","A space-occupying lesion such as a ganglion, varicosities, lipoma, accessory muscle or tenosynovitis is identifiable in a large proportion of cases","The nerve divides into medial and lateral plantar and calcaneal branches, so symptoms may be confined to part of the sole depending on the branch involved","A dorsiflexion-eversion manoeuvre that tightens the tunnel can reproduce symptoms","Generic management includes orthoses and activity modification, corticosteroid injection, and surgical tarsal tunnel release, especially when a space-occupying lesion is present"],"pathophysiology":"Tarsal tunnel syndrome results from compression of the tibial nerve within the fibro-osseous tunnel behind the medial malleolus, deep to the flexor retinaculum. Raised pressure within this confined space may arise from space-occupying lesions, hindfoot valgus or other deformity, post-traumatic scarring, tenosynovitis or venous engorgement. Sustained compression impairs axonal transport and causes local demyelination, producing sensory disturbance in the plantar distribution and, in advanced cases, weakness and wasting of the intrinsic foot muscles.","additionalDifferentials":["Plantar fasciitis","Peripheral polyneuropathy, for example diabetic neuropathy","S1 lumbosacral radiculopathy","Morton (interdigital) neuroma","Distal medial plantar nerve entrapment (jogger's foot)","Referred pain or peripheral vascular claudication"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies and electromyography, recognising that results can be less reliable than at other sites","MRI to identify a space-occupying lesion or accessory muscle within the tunnel","Ultrasound to assess the nerve and surrounding structures dynamically","Weightbearing plain radiographs for bony deformity, coalition or osteophytes","Clinical provocative testing with the Tinel sign and dorsiflexion-eversion manoeuvre","Screening for diabetes mellitus or other systemic causes of neuropathy"],"redFlags":["A mass lesion within the tunnel that may represent a tumour","Rapidly progressive motor loss or intrinsic foot muscle wasting","Features suggesting a systemic neuropathy that requires separate investigation","Evidence of infection or acute trauma around the medial ankle"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are good when a discrete compressive cause such as a ganglion is identified and released, whereas idiopathic cases are less predictable and may have incomplete relief of symptoms.","pitfalls":["Confusing the condition with plantar fasciitis or a generalised polyneuropathy and treating the wrong diagnosis","Failing to image the tunnel and missing a space-occupying lesion","Relying on nerve conduction studies, which can be falsely normal, to exclude the diagnosis","Incomplete surgical release that leaves the distal medial and lateral plantar branches decompressed inadequately"],"management":["Excise an identifiable space-occupying lesion such as a ganglion, lipoma, varicosities or accessory muscle, which affords the most reliable outcome","Correct hindfoot deformity and biomechanical factors with orthoses and appropriate footwear","Offer conservative care with activity modification, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory analgesia and neuropathic pain medication","Consider a corticosteroid injection into the tunnel for inflammatory or milder cases","Perform surgical decompression by division of the flexor retinaculum when conservative treatment fails","Screen for and treat diabetes mellitus and other systemic neuropathies contributing to nerve vulnerability","Refer to hand or foot and ankle surgery, counselling that idiopathic cases respond less predictably"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell's Operative Orthopaedics","Green's Operative Hand Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"tarui_disease":{"name":"Tarui disease (glycogen storage disease type VII)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["glycogen storage disease type VII","GSD VII","muscle phosphofructokinase deficiency","PFK deficiency","PFKM deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Scriver's Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive glycogenosis caused by deficiency of the muscle isoform of phosphofructokinase (PFKM gene), a key regulatory glycolytic enzyme","Presents like McArdle disease with exercise intolerance, cramps, and exertional muscle pain, but symptoms are often worse after a carbohydrate-rich meal","A carbohydrate load does not improve and may worsen exertional symptoms (the 'out of wind' effect), unlike the fuel benefit seen in some other glycogenoses","Because the enzyme is also partially deficient in red cells, a compensated haemolytic anaemia with reticulocytosis and hyperuricaemia frequently coexists","The forearm exercise test shows a flat lactate response, similar to McArdle disease","Exertional rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria can occur, and some patients develop gout from the associated hyperuricaemia","Management is supportive with avoidance of intense exertion and, in contrast to some other glycogenoses, avoidance of high-carbohydrate pre-exercise loading"],"pathophysiology":"Tarui disease is caused by deficiency of the muscle subunit of phosphofructokinase, which catalyses the conversion of fructose-6-phosphate to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate, a committed and rate-limiting step of glycolysis. The block prevents muscle from using both stored glycogen and blood glucose for anaerobic energy production, so exercise capacity is limited and lactate fails to rise with exertion. Because ingested glucose cannot be utilised and instead suppresses the alternative free-fatty-acid fuel supply, a carbohydrate meal can paradoxically worsen exercise tolerance. The muscle-type PFK subunit is also expressed in erythrocytes, so its deficiency reduces red-cell glycolysis and causes a compensated haemolysis, with increased purine turnover contributing to hyperuricaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["McArdle disease (GSD V)","Other distal glycolytic enzyme defects (e.g. phosphoglycerate kinase or mutase deficiency)","Fatty acid oxidation disorders","Mitochondrial myopathies","Haemolytic anaemias from other causes","Exertional rhabdomyolysis of other aetiology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Forearm exercise test showing a flat lactate response","Full blood count and reticulocyte count for compensated haemolysis","Serum uric acid and bilirubin","Creatine kinase at rest and after exertion, with urine myoglobin during episodes","Molecular genetic testing of the PFKM gene","Muscle biopsy with phosphofructokinase enzyme assay"],"redFlags":["Severe exertional rhabdomyolysis with pigmenturia and risk of acute kidney injury","Symptomatic anaemia or marked reticulocytosis","Gout or urate nephropathy from hyperuricaemia"],"prognosis":"Most patients have a normal life expectancy with lifestyle adaptation, though exercise capacity is limited; recurrent rhabdomyolysis, complications of haemolysis, and gout are the main long-term concerns, and rare infantile-onset forms carry a poorer prognosis.","pitfalls":["Advising carbohydrate loading before exercise, which can worsen symptoms in this glycogenosis","Confusing it with McArdle disease and missing the associated haemolysis and hyperuricaemia","Overlooking gout or urate nephropathy as a complication","Failing to counsel about rhabdomyolysis and acute kidney injury risk during strenuous activity"],"management":["Advise avoidance of strenuous and high-intensity exercise to prevent cramps and exertional rhabdomyolysis","Counsel against pre-exercise carbohydrate loading, which worsens exertional tolerance in the out-of-wind effect, unlike in McArdle disease","Consider a high-protein or ketogenic dietary approach to provide alternative muscle fuel","Manage acute exertional rhabdomyolysis with generous hydration and monitoring for acute kidney injury and hyperkalaemia","Treat associated hyperuricaemia and gout with urate-lowering therapy such as allopurinol","Monitor the compensated haemolytic anaemia and reticulocytosis with periodic blood counts","Coordinate care through metabolic medicine","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder with carrier testing of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Scriver's The Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"task_specific_dystonia":{"name":"Task-specific (focal) dystonia","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["task-specific dystonia","occupational dystonia","musician's dystonia","focal task-specific dystonia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Produces abnormal posturing or spasm only during a particular skilled, often over-practised, motor task","Writer's cramp and musician's dystonia are the classic examples; embouchure dystonia and the sporting yips are others","The same muscles function normally during other activities, which is the defining feature","Symptoms often begin as loss of fine control or cramping and progress to overt posturing as the task continues","A sensory trick may temporarily relieve the dystonia in some patients","It typically affects adults engaged in repetitive precision tasks and can be occupationally disabling","Over time the dystonia may spread to related tasks, with loss of the original task specificity"],"pathophysiology":"Task-specific focal dystonia is thought to arise from maladaptive plasticity within the sensorimotor system, with reduced inhibition at cortical, subcortical and spinal levels and abnormal integration of sensory feedback with motor output. Extensive repetitive practice of a skilled task appears to drive aberrant cortical reorganisation that blurs the normally distinct representations of individual digits or muscles, so that performing the specific over-learned movement triggers involuntary co-contraction and abnormal posturing.","additionalDifferentials":["Overuse or repetitive strain injury and tenosynovitis","Peripheral nerve entrapment or focal neuropathy","Essential tremor affecting the task","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder","Early features of a generalised or secondary dystonia","Orthopaedic or musculoskeletal disorders of the hand"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Task-based clinical examination, ideally observing the patient performing the triggering activity such as writing or playing an instrument","Surface electromyography demonstrating co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles","Nerve conduction studies to exclude focal neuropathy or entrapment","MRI where a structural or secondary cause is suspected","Copper studies and genetic testing in younger patients or those with wider features","Video recording of the task to document and monitor the dystonia"],"redFlags":["Dystonia at rest or spreading beyond the specific task, suggesting a secondary or generalising process","Sensory loss, weakness or wasting suggesting a compressive or neuropathic lesion","Young onset with additional neurological features suggesting an inherited dystonia","Features of Wilson's disease in a younger patient"],"prognosis":"Task-specific dystonia is generally chronic and spontaneous remission is uncommon, though severity can fluctuate. It is not life-threatening but may curtail or end a career dependent on the affected skill. Botulinum toxin injection targeted to the overactive muscles is a mainstay of treatment, and some patients benefit from retraining, ergonomic modification or a change of technique, with results that vary between individuals.","pitfalls":["Dismissing early symptoms as overuse injury or anxiety","Not observing the patient performing the actual triggering task","Neglecting the occupational and psychological impact","Assuming permanent task specificity, when the dystonia can spread to related tasks"],"management":["Use botulinum toxin injection targeted to the overactive muscles as the mainstay of treatment","Modify the triggering task with ergonomic aids, altered technique, grip adaptations or switching to the other hand","Provide sensorimotor retraining and physiotherapy, including constraint and rehabilitation strategies to restore normal control","Trial oral anticholinergic therapy such as trihexyphenidyl in selected patients","Teach use of any effective sensory trick to relieve posturing","Consider copper studies and genetic testing in younger patients or those with wider dystonic features","Refer to neurology with movement-disorder expertise and involve occupational or performing-arts medicine support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice, current edition","Fahn and Jankovic, Principles and Practice of Movement Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"tatton_brown_rahman_syndrome":{"name":"Tatton-Brown-Rahman syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBRS","DNMT3A overgrowth syndrome","DNMT3A-related overgrowth and intellectual disability"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is an overgrowth-with-intellectual-disability syndrome featuring tall stature, macrocephaly, and a recognisable facial appearance with heavy, straight eyebrows and narrow palpebral fissures","It is caused by heterozygous variants in DNMT3A, which encodes a DNA methyltransferase central to establishing DNA methylation patterns during development","Intellectual disability is usually mild to moderate, and behavioural features including autistic traits and psychiatric symptoms are frequently reported","Obesity and a tendency to a heavier build are common, and some individuals have joint hypermobility, scoliosis, or congenital heart anomalies","Because somatic DNMT3A variants are recurrent drivers in haematological malignancy, there is a recognised predisposition to acute myeloid leukaemia and other blood cancers, warranting clinical vigilance","Diagnosis is molecular, typically via exome or genome sequencing or an overgrowth/intellectual disability panel, after considering Sotos and Weaver syndromes","There is no disease-specific therapy; management is anticipatory and multidisciplinary with developmental support and awareness of the haematological risk"],"pathophysiology":"Tatton-Brown-Rahman syndrome is a chromatin-related overgrowth disorder caused by germline heterozygous variants in DNMT3A. This gene encodes a DNA methyltransferase responsible for de novo methylation of DNA, an epigenetic process that governs gene expression programmes during growth and neurodevelopment. Impaired DNMT3A function disturbs these methylation patterns, producing generalised overgrowth, macrocephaly, and neurodevelopmental features. Because the same gene is a recurrently mutated driver in myeloid malignancy at the somatic level, constitutional variants confer an increased lifetime risk of haematological cancers. Inheritance is autosomal dominant, and the majority of cases are de novo.","additionalDifferentials":["Sotos syndrome (NSD1-related)","Weaver syndrome (EZH2-related)","Malan syndrome (NFIX-related)","Cohen syndrome and other obesity-associated intellectual disability syndromes","Fragile X syndrome","Other chromatin- or methylation-related overgrowth disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or genome sequencing, or a targeted overgrowth/intellectual disability gene panel","Chromosomal microarray to exclude copy number abnormalities","Developmental, behavioural, and psychiatric assessment","Full blood count and clinical review with a low threshold for haematology referral given malignancy risk","Echocardiography if a congenital heart anomaly is suspected","Spinal assessment for scoliosis"],"redFlags":["Unexplained cytopenias, easy bruising, recurrent infection, or constitutional symptoms suggesting haematological malignancy","Rapid neurological change or regression prompting re-evaluation","Progressive scoliosis with respiratory or neurological compromise","Symptoms of a significant congenital cardiac lesion"],"prognosis":"Many individuals have a relatively stable course determined mainly by their level of intellectual disability and associated features. The recognised predisposition to haematological malignancy is an important long-term consideration and should prompt prompt evaluation of suggestive symptoms, though most patients do not develop cancer.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing Sotos syndrome clinically and failing to test DNMT3A","Overlooking the haematological malignancy predisposition when investigating cytopenias or constitutional symptoms","Attributing obesity and behavioural difficulties to unrelated causes rather than the underlying syndrome","Neglecting genetic counselling regarding the autosomal dominant, usually de novo, inheritance"],"management":["Provide developmental and educational support with early intervention and individualised learning plans for intellectual disability","Assess and manage behavioural and psychiatric features including autistic traits with appropriate therapy and psychiatric input","Maintain haematological vigilance for acute myeloid leukaemia and other blood cancers, with a low threshold for full blood count and haematology referral for suggestive symptoms","Support weight management to address the tendency to obesity","Screen for and manage scoliosis, joint hypermobility and any congenital cardiac anomaly with echocardiography and orthopaedic review as indicated","Coordinate care through clinical genetics and relevant paediatric and adult specialists","Offer genetic counselling, noting autosomal dominant inheritance with most cases arising de novo"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (University of Washington)","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"tay_sachs_disease":{"name":"Tay-Sachs disease","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hexosaminidase A deficiency","GM2 gangliosidosis type 1","beta-hexosaminidase A deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard metabolic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive GM2 gangliosidosis caused by deficiency of the enzyme hexosaminidase A due to pathogenic variants in the HEXA gene","Carrier frequency is increased in the Ashkenazi Jewish population, making targeted carrier screening and prenatal diagnosis important","The classic infantile form presents around 3 to 6 months with loss of milestones, hypotonia progressing to spasticity, and an exaggerated startle response to sound","A cherry-red spot at the macula is a hallmark ophthalmological finding","Unlike many storage disorders, there is characteristically no hepatosplenomegaly, which helps distinguish it clinically","Progressive macrocephaly, seizures, blindness, and neurodegeneration follow, with death usually in early childhood","Juvenile and late-onset variants exist and can present with slower motor and psychiatric or cerebellar symptoms","No disease-modifying therapy is established, so management is supportive and genetic counselling is central"],"pathophysiology":"Tay-Sachs disease results from biallelic pathogenic variants in HEXA, which encodes the alpha subunit of the enzyme beta-hexosaminidase A. Deficient enzyme activity prevents degradation of GM2 ganglioside, which accumulates progressively within neuronal lysosomes of the central nervous system. Neuronal engorgement and dysfunction drive relentless neurodegeneration. Because storage is largely confined to neurons, visceral organomegaly is typically absent, distinguishing it from disorders such as Sandhoff disease, in which both hexosaminidase A and B are deficient.","additionalDifferentials":["Sandhoff disease","GM1 gangliosidosis","Niemann-Pick disease","Other causes of infantile neurodegeneration with cherry-red spot","Leukodystrophies presenting with developmental regression"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assay of hexosaminidase A activity in serum or leukocytes","HEXA molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis and enable carrier detection","Ophthalmological examination for the macular cherry-red spot","Neuroimaging to characterise neurodegeneration and exclude alternatives","Carrier screening and prenatal or preimplantation testing for at-risk families"],"redFlags":["Rapid developmental regression with exaggerated startle in an infant","Emerging seizures or loss of vision","Progressive swallowing difficulty with aspiration risk","Family history in an at-risk population without prior carrier screening"],"prognosis":"The infantile form is progressive and fatal, typically within the first few years of life. Juvenile-onset disease progresses more slowly with death in later childhood or adolescence, while late-onset forms have a more variable and protracted course. Management remains supportive.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis because hepatosplenomegaly is absent","Failing to distinguish it from Sandhoff disease, which additionally involves visceral storage","Not offering carrier screening to at-risk populations before conception","Attributing the exaggerated startle to a benign cause and delaying evaluation"],"management":["Recognise there is no curative therapy, so care is supportive and palliative, centred on comfort and quality of life","Control seizures with anticonvulsant therapy","Manage feeding difficulty and aspiration risk with feeding support and gastrostomy where needed","Treat spasticity and manage respiratory secretions and recurrent chest infections","Involve paediatric palliative care early for symptom control and family support","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across metabolic medicine, neurology and allied health","Offer genetic counselling with carrier screening, particularly in Ashkenazi Jewish families, and prenatal or preimplantation diagnosis for at-risk families"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"tbck_related_disorder":{"name":"TBCK-related intellectual disability syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBCK-related disorder","hypotonia, infantile, with psychomotor retardation and characteristic facies (IHPRF3)","TBCK encephaloneuropathy","TBCKE","TBCK syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nature Reviews chromatinopathy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in TBCK, which encodes a protein implicated in the regulation of mTOR signalling and autophagy, so it is a metabolic-neurodegenerative disorder rather than a chromatin-modifier syndrome","Profound infantile hypotonia, global developmental delay and intellectual disability with a distinctive facial appearance are the core features","Many children have severe feeding and respiratory difficulty, and progressive, neurodegenerative decline can occur in more severely affected individuals","A recurrent founder variant is prevalent in individuals of Puerto Rican ancestry and is associated with a severe, progressive phenotype","Neuroimaging may show white matter abnormalities (leukoencephalopathy), a thin corpus callosum and brain atrophy","Epilepsy, hyporeflexia and features of a motor neuronopathy or neuropathy may develop over time","Diagnosis rests on identifying biallelic pathogenic TBCK variants, and inheritance is autosomal recessive with implications for carrier and reproductive counselling"],"pathophysiology":"TBCK encodes a multidomain protein carrying a TBC domain and a pseudokinase region that is thought to participate in regulating the mTOR signalling pathway and cellular autophagy, processes central to cell growth, protein turnover and the clearance of cellular waste. Biallelic loss-of-function variants reduce or abolish functional protein, dysregulating mTOR-related signalling and impairing autophagy so that abnormal material accumulates within cells, with some findings suggesting a lysosomal storage-like component. The nervous system is especially vulnerable, producing profound hypotonia, developmental impairment, white matter disease and, in severe cases, progressive neurodegeneration, while the severity varies with the specific genotype including the severe founder variant.","additionalDifferentials":["Other genetic causes of severe infantile hypotonia (e.g. spinal muscular atrophy)","Congenital myopathies and congenital muscular dystrophies","Peroxisomal and mitochondrial disorders","Lysosomal storage disorders","Pontocerebellar hypoplasias","Other leukoencephalopathies of infancy","Prader-Willi syndrome and other causes of neonatal hypotonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or genome sequencing to identify biallelic TBCK variants, with targeted testing for the founder variant in relevant ancestries","Brain MRI to characterise leukoencephalopathy, corpus callosum thinning and atrophy","Electroencephalography where seizures are present","Assessment of feeding and swallowing with respiratory evaluation","Neuromuscular assessment including nerve conduction studies if a neuropathy is suspected","Creatine kinase and metabolic screening to help exclude alternative diagnoses","Ophthalmological and audiological review"],"redFlags":["Respiratory insufficiency or recurrent aspiration requiring urgent support","Developmental regression or loss of skills indicating progression","Refractory or worsening seizures","Severe feeding failure with faltering growth","Rapid neurological deterioration in the severe (founder-variant) phenotype"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies widely with genotype: milder cases have static severe developmental impairment, whereas the severe form, including the founder variant, is often progressive with neurodegeneration, respiratory compromise and shortened survival; care is supportive, centring on feeding, respiratory management, seizure control and developmental support.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the recurrent founder variant in individuals of Puerto Rican ancestry","Mistaking the disorder for a static encephalopathy and missing its potential to progress","Attributing the hypotonia to a primary neuromuscular disease without genetic testing","Failing to provide recurrence-risk counselling appropriate to autosomal recessive inheritance","Underestimating respiratory risk that drives much of the morbidity"],"management":["Recognise there is no disease-modifying therapy, so management is supportive and directed at the neurological, feeding and respiratory manifestations","Support feeding and nutrition with swallowing assessment and gastrostomy where oral intake is unsafe","Provide respiratory care including secretion management, prompt infection treatment and ventilatory support as decline progresses","Manage hypotonia and developmental impairment with physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy","Control epilepsy with anticonvulsant therapy where seizures occur","Monitor for neurodegenerative progression and involve palliative care in severe, progressive disease","Coordinate multidisciplinary paediatric care across neurology, respiratory and allied health","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, with targeted founder-variant testing in Puerto Rican families and carrier testing of relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews — TBCK-Related Intellectual Disability Syndrome","Nature Reviews — mTOR signalling, autophagy and neurodevelopmental disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"tbck_syndrome":{"name":"TBCK-Related Intellectual Disability Syndrome","system":"Neurology / Neurodevelopmental disorder","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBCK syndrome","TBCK encephaloneuronopathy","HISHDT","Boricua founder syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["TBCK encodes TBC1-domain-containing kinase, which regulates mTORC1 signalling and Rab-mediated intracellular trafficking; inheritance is autosomal recessive.","A recurrent founder nonsense variant is prevalent in individuals of Puerto Rican (Boricua) ancestry.","Presentation is with profound neonatal or infantile hypotonia, global developmental delay and often absent speech and independent walking.","Distinctive coarse facial features are characteristic, sometimes with macroglossia and an open-mouth, full-faced appearance.","The severe phenotype is progressive, with leukoencephalopathy, epilepsy, respiratory insufficiency and premature death.","Reduced mTORC1 signalling with disturbed autophagy is the proposed mechanism, and some features overlap with storage and neurodegenerative disorders.","Respiratory failure and recurrent infection are major sources of morbidity and mortality.","Additional features may include optic atrophy, central or sensory visual impairment and dysautonomia."],"pathophysiology":"TBCK on chromosome 4q24 encodes TBCK (TBC1-domain-containing kinase), a multidomain protein comprising a pseudokinase domain, a TBC (Tre2-Bub2-Cdc16) Rab-GAP domain and a rhodanese-like domain that regulates mammalian target of rapamycin complex 1 (mTORC1) signalling and Rab-GTPase-mediated endolysosomal and vesicular trafficking, thereby shaping cell growth, autophagy and actin organisation. Biallelic loss-of-function variants, including a recurrent nonsense founder allele in the Puerto Rican population, reduce TBCK protein and downregulate mTORC1 signalling, while impaired autophagy and endolysosomal handling generate storage-like neuronal pathology. The consequence is a syndromic infantile hypotonia with global developmental delay and characteristic facies that, in severely affected children, evolves into a progressive encephaloneuronopathy with leukoencephalopathy and neurodegeneration.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital myopathies and congenital muscular dystrophies","Spinal muscular atrophy","Prader-Willi syndrome","Lysosomal storage disorders such as the mucopolysaccharidoses (given the coarse facies)","Other mTOR-pathway or leukoencephalopathy disorders","Mitochondrial and peroxisomal disorders","Other syndromic causes of infantile hypotonia with developmental delay"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or genome sequencing or a hypotonia gene panel (biallelic TBCK); targeted founder-variant testing where ancestry fits","MRI brain (white-matter or leukoencephalopathy changes, atrophy)","EEG if seizures are present","Creatine kinase and neuromuscular assessment to evaluate the hypotonia (qualitative)","Respiratory and ventilation/sleep assessment","Ophthalmology and hearing evaluation"],"redFlags":["Respiratory insufficiency or recurrent aspiration","Neurological or developmental regression suggesting the progressive form","New or worsening seizures","Bulbar dysfunction with feeding difficulty and faltering growth"],"prognosis":"Highly variable; milder cases survive into adulthood with a largely static intellectual disability, whereas the severe progressive (encephaloneuronopathy) form leads to neurodegeneration, respiratory failure and death in childhood.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling as a primary neuromuscular disorder (such as a congenital myopathy) and halting the workup","Overlooking the recurrent founder variant in individuals of Puerto Rican ancestry","Allowing coarse facies to prompt an exclusively lysosomal-storage workup","Underestimating respiratory risk in an apparently stable child"],"management":["Manage supportively, as no disease-modifying treatment exists, focusing on the hypotonia, developmental, feeding and respiratory needs","Provide developmental therapies including physiotherapy, occupational and speech and language therapy for the profound hypotonia and global delay","Ensure safe nutrition with feeding and swallow assessment and gastrostomy feeding when required","Deliver respiratory support with secretion clearance, prompt infection treatment and assisted ventilation as respiratory insufficiency develops","Treat epilepsy with anticonvulsants under EEG-guided review","Involve paediatric palliative care in the severe progressive encephaloneuronopathy form","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance, with founder-variant testing in individuals of Puerto Rican ancestry","Coordinate care through paediatric neurology and a multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics reference texts","Paediatric neurology reference texts","Neuromuscular and neurodevelopmental genetics review literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"telangiectasia_macularis_eruptiva_perstans":{"name":"Telangiectasia macularis eruptiva perstans","system":"Skin","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TMEP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology references / mastocytosis literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare adult form of cutaneous mastocytosis characterised by persistent telangiectatic red-brown macules","Lesions are typically on the trunk and may be widespread, with fine telangiectasia within them","The Darier sign is often subtle or absent, unlike in typical urticaria pigmentosa","It occurs almost exclusively in adults and may be associated with systemic mastocytosis","Diagnosis relies on biopsy demonstrating increased mast cells around dilated dermal vessels","Evaluation for systemic disease and mediator symptoms is appropriate given the adult onset"],"pathophysiology":"Telangiectasia macularis eruptiva perstans is a variant of cutaneous mastocytosis in which increased dermal mast cells cluster around dilated superficial blood vessels, producing persistent telangiectatic pigmented macules. As in other mastocytosis, clonal mast cells (often KIT-associated) release mediators, though the weal-and-flare response is frequently muted. Because it presents in adults, it is regarded, like other adult cutaneous mastocytosis, as potentially reflecting underlying systemic mast cell disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Other cutaneous or systemic mastocytosis","Generalised essential telangiectasia","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia","Poikiloderma","Connective tissue disease-related telangiectasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with mast cell staining around dermal vessels","Serum baseline tryptase","Assessment for systemic mastocytosis in view of adult onset","History of mediator symptoms and triggers","Dermoscopy to characterise the telangiectasia"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting systemic mastocytosis","Recurrent mediator or anaphylactic symptoms","A markedly raised tryptase","Rapidly progressive or extensive disease"],"prognosis":"The cutaneous lesions are persistent but benign; prognosis is influenced by any associated systemic mastocytosis, which should be sought given the adult onset.","pitfalls":["Missing associated systemic mastocytosis","Expecting a florid Darier sign, which is often absent","Confusing it with non-mast-cell telangiectatic disorders","Not measuring tryptase in an adult with suggestive lesions"],"management":["Advise avoidance of mast-cell degranulation triggers such as heat, friction, alcohol, opioids and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","Control mediator symptoms with H1 and H2 antihistamines","Add a mast-cell stabiliser such as sodium cromoglicate, or consider a leukotriene antagonist, for refractory symptoms","Offer phototherapy for widespread or cosmetically troublesome cutaneous lesions","Prescribe an adrenaline auto-injector and an anaphylaxis action plan for patients at risk of systemic reactions","Evaluate for underlying systemic mastocytosis with baseline serum tryptase given the adult onset, referring to haematology if suspected","Escalate urgently and treat anaphylaxis with intramuscular adrenaline if systemic mast-cell mediator release occurs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dermatology reference texts (standard reference)","Mastocytosis clinical guidelines (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"telogen_effluvium":{"name":"Telogen effluvium","system":"Dermatology / Trichology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TE","telogen shedding","acute telogen effluvium","chronic telogen effluvium"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Diffuse, non-scarring hair shedding that classically begins around two to three months after a triggering event such as childbirth, severe febrile illness, major surgery, crash dieting, or starting or stopping a drug","Patients often report alarming quantities of hair on the pillow, in the shower, and on the brush, yet visible baldness is unusual because shedding rarely involves more than about half of the scalp hair","The hair-pull test is positive with an increased proportion of club (telogen) hairs, and trichoscopy shows numerous empty follicles and short upright regrowing hairs","Follicular ostia are preserved, confirming a non-scarring process with excellent potential for full regrowth, which distinguishes it from cicatricial alopecias","Acute forms are self-limiting and settle within a few months once the trigger has passed, whereas chronic telogen effluvium persists beyond six months, often in middle-aged women, and fluctuates","A careful history covering events in the preceding few months, a drug review, and screening for iron deficiency, thyroid dysfunction and nutritional causes usually identifies the precipitant","Reassurance about the reversible nature is central, and treating any identified underlying cause such as iron repletion or thyroid correction is the mainstay rather than specific hair therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Telogen effluvium results from a disturbance of the normal hair cycle in which an abnormally large proportion of follicles are prematurely shifted from the growing (anagen) phase into the resting (telogen) phase. After a latency of roughly two to three months these synchronised telogen hairs are shed as new anagen hairs emerge beneath them. Recognised triggers include physiological stress such as childbirth, febrile illness and major surgery, rapid weight loss, iron and other nutritional deficiency, thyroid disturbance, and numerous drugs. Because the follicles themselves are not destroyed and the ostia remain intact, the process is non-scarring and reverses once the precipitant resolves.","additionalDifferentials":["Androgenetic alopecia, which may be unmasked or accelerated by an effluvium","Alopecia areata, especially the diffuse (incognita) variant","Anagen effluvium from chemotherapy or toxin exposure","Early scarring alopecia presenting with shedding","Diffuse hair loss of systemic disease such as thyroid disorder or lupus","Nutritional or iron-deficiency related hair loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Hair-pull test to confirm increased telogen shedding","Trichoscopy to assess follicular ostia, hair-shaft calibre and regrowth","Full blood count and ferritin to detect iron deficiency","Thyroid function tests","Targeted screening for other nutritional causes such as vitamin D or zinc where clinically indicated","Trichogram or scalp biopsy in uncertain or chronic cases to confirm the telogen ratio and exclude other diagnoses"],"redFlags":["Loss of follicular ostia, scaling, or perifollicular erythema suggesting a scarring alopecia rather than an effluvium","Shedding that continues beyond six months or progresses despite removal of the presumed trigger","Associated systemic symptoms pointing to occult illness or malignancy","Patchy rather than diffuse loss suggesting alopecia areata or tinea capitis"],"prognosis":"Acute telogen effluvium is self-limiting and typically resolves with full regrowth over several months once the trigger has passed; chronic telogen effluvium may fluctuate over years but does not itself cause permanent baldness, although it can unmask underlying androgenetic thinning.","pitfalls":["Attributing all diffuse shedding to effluvium and overlooking early androgenetic alopecia or diffuse alopecia areata","Looking back only to when shedding began rather than two to three months earlier for the true trigger","Missing a treatable systemic cause such as iron deficiency or thyroid disease","Reassuring a patient without excluding a scarring process on close scalp examination"],"management":["Identify and remove the precipitating trigger such as recent childbirth, febrile illness, surgery, crash dieting or a newly started drug, as the mainstay since shedding reverses once it resolves","Correct identified nutritional deficiency, replacing iron where ferritin is low and addressing deficiencies such as vitamin D or zinc","Treat any underlying thyroid dysfunction, since restoring euthyroid status allows the hair cycle to normalise","Review medication and withdraw or substitute a culprit drug where clinically feasible","Reassure the patient that the process is non-scarring with excellent regrowth potential, and advise gentle hair care avoiding traction and harsh chemical treatments","Consider topical minoxidil to encourage regrowth in persistent or chronic telogen effluvium","Refer to dermatology and reassess with trichoscopy or scalp biopsy if shedding is chronic, uncertain, or unmasking androgenetic thinning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"telomere_biology_disorder":{"name":"Telomere biology disorder","system":"Haematological / multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBD","Telomeropathy","Short telomere syndrome","Dyskeratosis congenita spectrum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Inherited bone marrow failure and telomere biology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Umbrella term for disorders of telomere maintenance producing abnormally short telomeres; it encompasses dyskeratosis congenita and its severe variants (Hoyeraal-Hreidarsson, Revesz and Coats plus syndromes).","Caused by variants in telomere-maintenance genes including TERT, TERC, DKC1, TINF2, RTEL1, PARN, NAF1, NOP10, NHP2, WRAP53, ACD, CTC1 and STN1; inheritance may be X-linked (DKC1), autosomal dominant or autosomal recessive.","The classic dyskeratosis congenita mucocutaneous triad is nail dystrophy, oral leucoplakia and reticulate skin pigmentation, but it is frequently incomplete or absent, especially in adults.","Bone marrow failure with single or multilineage cytopenias and aplastic anaemia is a leading cause of early morbidity and mortality.","Adult presentations are often cryptic: idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, unexplained cirrhosis or apparently isolated cytopenias, sometimes with premature greying of hair.","Genetic anticipation, with earlier onset and greater severity in successive generations, reflects progressive telomere shortening.","There is a high lifetime risk of myelodysplastic syndrome, acute myeloid leukaemia and squamous cell carcinomas of the head and neck and anogenital region.","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating abnormally short leucocyte telomeres (flow-FISH), interpreted against age-matched values, together with genetic testing."],"pathophysiology":"Telomeres are repetitive TTAGGG nucleoprotein caps that protect chromosome ends. They are maintained by the telomerase enzyme complex (the catalytic reverse transcriptase TERT, the RNA template TERC, and dyskerin/DKC1 with NOP10, NHP2 and GAR1 that stabilise TERC), by telomerase biogenesis and trafficking factors (WRAP53/TCAB1, PARN and NAF1), by the shelterin complex (including TINF2/TIN2 and ACD/TPP1) that caps the ends and recruits telomerase, and by the CST complex (CTC1-STN1-TEN1) that supports replication of the telomeric C-strand. Loss-of-function or dominant-negative variants in these genes accelerate telomere attrition; once telomeres become critically short they trigger a DNA-damage response leading to cellular senescence or apoptosis, depleting rapidly dividing stem-cell compartments. This preferentially affects haematopoietic stem cells (marrow failure), alveolar and airway epithelium (pulmonary fibrosis), hepatocytes and vascular endothelium (liver disease) and skin and mucosa, while genomic instability arising from dysfunctional telomeres underlies the elevated cancer risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Fanconi anaemia and other inherited bone marrow failure syndromes (Shwachman-Diamond, Diamond-Blackfan anaemia)","Acquired aplastic anaemia and idiopathic myelodysplastic syndrome","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis without a recognised telomere cause","GATA2 deficiency and SAMD9/SAMD9L-related disorders","Cryptogenic cirrhosis of alternative aetiology","Poikiloderma syndromes such as Rothmund-Thomson syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Leucocyte telomere length by flow-FISH, interpreted against age-matched percentiles","Targeted telomere-gene panel or whole-exome sequencing","Full blood count with reticulocytes and bone marrow aspirate and trephine with cytogenetics","Chromosome breakage testing to exclude Fanconi anaemia","High-resolution chest CT and pulmonary function tests","Liver function tests with hepatic imaging and elastography","Structured surveillance for myelodysplastic syndrome, leukaemia and mucosal squamous cell carcinoma"],"redFlags":["New or progressive cytopenias or other features of marrow failure","Progressive breathlessness or radiological pulmonary fibrosis","Signs of portal hypertension or hepatopulmonary syndrome","New oral, anogenital or cutaneous lesions suspicious for squamous cell carcinoma","Severe infantile presentation with cerebellar hypoplasia and immunodeficiency suggesting Hoyeraal-Hreidarsson syndrome"],"prognosis":"Highly variable, ranging from early childhood death in severe infantile forms to adult-onset organ fibrosis. Overall life expectancy is reduced, driven by marrow failure, pulmonary and hepatic fibrosis and malignancy.","pitfalls":["Expecting the full mucocutaneous triad; many patients, particularly adults, lack the classic skin and nail findings","Attributing pulmonary fibrosis or cirrhosis to another cause and missing the underlying telomeropathy","Using standard-intensity conditioning for haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, which is poorly tolerated; reduced-intensity approaches are preferred","Overlooking at-risk relatives who need counselling and telomere-aware screening","Relying solely on telomere length, since results may fall within the normal range for certain genotypes, so genetic testing remains essential"],"management":["Treat bone marrow failure with androgen therapy such as danazol to stimulate residual marrow, reserving allogeneic haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation for severe or transfusion-dependent cytopenias","Use reduced-intensity conditioning for transplantation because these patients are exquisitely sensitive to DNA-damaging chemotherapy and radiotherapy","Manage pulmonary fibrosis with best supportive respiratory care and lung transplantation, and consider liver transplantation for progressive hepatic fibrosis or cirrhosis","Provide transfusion and infection support for cytopenias using leucodepleted, irradiated blood products, with iron chelation where transfusion burden is high","Advise strict avoidance of telomere- and DNA-damaging exposures including smoking, excess alcohol and unnecessary radiation","Institute lifelong surveillance for the associated malignancies including myelodysplasia, acute leukaemia and squamous cell carcinomas, with regular mucocutaneous, marrow and imaging review","Offer genetic counselling and family screening including telomere-length testing, given the heritable disorder that shows genetic anticipation","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across haematology, respiratory medicine, hepatology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited bone marrow failure syndrome guidelines","Dyskeratosis congenita and telomere biology disorder reviews","Haematology and medical genetics textbooks"],"crossLinks":[]},"temple_syndrome":{"name":"Temple syndrome (maternal UPD14)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TS14","maternal UPD14 syndrome","Temple syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by loss of the paternally expressed 14q32 genes, through maternal uniparental disomy 14, a paternal 14q32 deletion, or an imprinting defect","Pre- and postnatal growth restriction with short stature and small hands and feet is typical","Neonatal hypotonia and feeding difficulty overlap strongly with Prader-Willi syndrome in infancy","Precocious or early puberty is characteristic and a useful distinguishing clue","Truncal obesity and increased weight tend to develop in later childhood","Motor delay is usually mild and intellectual disability mild or absent, while a triangular face and frontal bossing may resemble Silver-Russell syndrome","It is often under-diagnosed because it overlaps phenotypically with both Prader-Willi and Silver-Russell syndromes"],"pathophysiology":"Temple syndrome is an imprinting disorder caused by absent expression of the paternally expressed genes at the imprinted 14q32 locus, such as DLK1 and RTL1, with relative excess of the maternally expressed gene MEG3. This most commonly results from maternal uniparental disomy 14, but the identical picture follows a paternal deletion of 14q32 or a primary imprinting (methylation) defect. Disturbed dosage of these growth- and metabolism-related imprinted genes produces the growth restriction, hypotonia, early puberty, and later obesity.","additionalDifferentials":["Prader-Willi syndrome, the major infancy mimic","Silver-Russell syndrome, given growth restriction and a triangular face","Kagami-Ogata syndrome, the reciprocal paternal-UPD14 disorder","Other causes of precocious puberty","Constitutional or idiopathic short stature"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Methylation analysis of the 14q32 (MEG3/DLK1) differentially methylated region as the first-line test","Microsatellite or SNP analysis to confirm maternal uniparental disomy 14 and distinguish it from deletion or epimutation","Karyotype to detect a Robertsonian translocation involving chromosome 14","Chromosomal microarray, usually normal but useful to exclude deletions and duplications","Growth monitoring and endocrine or pubertal assessment, including bone age","Developmental assessment"],"redFlags":["Precocious puberty requiring endocrine intervention","Severe neonatal hypotonia with feeding failure","Rapid weight gain with obesity-related complications"],"prognosis":"Generally favourable, with usually mild or no intellectual disability; the main issues are early puberty, short stature, and a tendency to obesity, all of which respond to appropriate endocrine and dietary management.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis because it mimics Prader-Willi and Silver-Russell syndromes","Relying on karyotype or microarray, which miss uniparental disomy and imprinting defects","Overlooking precocious puberty as a distinguishing feature","Failing to test for uniparental disomy 14 when a chromosome 14 Robertsonian translocation is present"],"management":["Support neonatal feeding difficulty and hypotonia with assisted or nasogastric feeding and early physiotherapy until oromotor function improves","Monitor growth closely and consider growth hormone therapy for the associated short stature under endocrine supervision","Treat precocious or early puberty with a GnRH analogue to arrest premature pubertal progression and protect adult height","Institute early dietary and lifestyle measures to prevent the truncal obesity that emerges in later childhood","Provide developmental assessment with physiotherapy and educational support for motor delay","Coordinate multidisciplinary paediatric, endocrine and dietetic follow-up","Offer genetic counselling clarifying recurrence risk according to the underlying mechanism of uniparental disomy, deletion or imprinting defect, and test for a Robertsonian translocation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Cassidy and Allanson's Management of Genetic Syndromes"],"crossLinks":[]},"tennis_elbow":{"name":"Tennis elbow","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lateral epicondylitis","lateral epicondylalgia","lateral elbow tendinopathy","common extensor origin tendinopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon / standard sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pain is centred over the lateral epicondyle and the origin of the common wrist extensors, often radiating down the back of the forearm","Symptoms are provoked by gripping, lifting with the palm down, and resisted wrist or middle-finger extension","Despite the older term epicondylitis, the underlying process is a degenerative tendinopathy rather than acute inflammation","It most commonly affects the extensor carpi radialis brevis origin and peaks in middle age, frequently in people doing repetitive forearm work rather than tennis players","Diagnosis is clinical; local tenderness just distal to the epicondyle with pain on resisted extension is characteristic","First-line management is activity modification, load reduction, and a progressive eccentric or graded strengthening programme, which addresses the underlying tendon problem","Counterforce bracing and attention to technique and equipment can offload the tendon during aggravating activities","Corticosteroid injection may give short-term relief but is associated with poorer long-term outcomes and higher recurrence than exercise-based rehabilitation","Most cases are self-limiting over months to a couple of years, and surgery is reserved for the small minority with refractory symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"Tennis elbow is an overuse tendinopathy of the common extensor origin at the lateral epicondyle, most often involving extensor carpi radialis brevis. Repetitive gripping and wrist extension produce microtears and a failed healing response, with angiofibroblastic (mucoid) degeneration, disorganised collagen, and neovascularisation rather than true inflammatory change. The weakened tendon becomes painful and mechanically vulnerable to further load.","additionalDifferentials":["Radial tunnel syndrome (posterior interosseous nerve entrapment)","Radiocapitellar osteoarthritis or a plica","Cervical radiculopathy referring pain to the lateral forearm","Posterolateral rotatory instability of the elbow","Referred pain from the shoulder or myofascial trigger points"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Usually a clinical diagnosis requiring no imaging","Ultrasound can confirm tendon thickening, hypoechoic change, or tears and guide injection when the diagnosis is unclear","MRI in atypical or refractory cases to characterise the tendon and exclude intra-articular pathology","Plain radiographs if bony pathology or calcification is suspected","Nerve conduction studies if radial tunnel syndrome or radiculopathy is considered"],"redFlags":["Night pain, systemic symptoms, or a mass suggesting infection or tumour rather than tendinopathy","Neurological symptoms pointing to nerve entrapment or cervical radiculopathy","History of significant trauma raising the possibility of instability or fracture","Failure to improve despite appropriate rehabilitation, prompting reassessment of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The natural history is generally favourable, with most patients recovering within a year regardless of specific treatment, though symptoms can persist or recur; structured loading rehabilitation improves long-term outcomes compared with steroid injection.","pitfalls":["Assuming an inflammatory process and relying on rest and anti-inflammatories rather than progressive loading","Repeated corticosteroid injections, which can worsen the tendon and long-term prognosis","Overlooking radial tunnel syndrome or cervical radiculopathy in atypical or persistent cases","Neglecting kinetic-chain, technique, and workplace factors that perpetuate overload"],"management":["Base treatment on activity modification and load reduction, avoiding the repetitive gripping and wrist extension that drive the tendinopathy","Prescribe a progressive eccentric or graded resistance strengthening programme for the wrist extensors as the definitive rehabilitation, addressing the failed tendon healing","Provide short-term analgesia with topical or oral NSAIDs for pain control","Consider a counterforce forearm brace to offload the common extensor origin during aggravating activity","Reassure the patient about the generally favourable, self-limiting natural history over months to a year","Reserve corticosteroid injection for short-term relief only, counselling that it can worsen long-term outcome compared with loading rehabilitation","Refer for physiotherapy, and consider extracorporeal shockwave therapy or surgical debridement and release in refractory, prolonged cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"tenosynovial_giant_cell_tumor_diffuse":{"name":"Diffuse-type tenosynovial giant cell tumour","system":"Musculoskeletal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pigmented villonodular synovitis","PVNS","diffuse-type TGCT","CSF1-rearranged giant cell tumour of tendon sheath"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone / Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A locally aggressive proliferation of the synovium of joints, bursae and tendon sheaths, most often affecting the knee and hip of young to middle-aged adults","The diffuse form, historically called pigmented villonodular synovitis, involves the joint extensively and behaves more aggressively than the localised form","Presents with recurrent joint swelling, pain, stiffness and often haemorrhagic or brownish effusions","MRI characteristically shows low signal on all sequences with blooming artefact from haemosiderin deposition","Driven by a CSF1 gene translocation in a minority of cells, which recruits abundant non-neoplastic CSF1-receptor-bearing macrophages, a landscape effect","Histology shows mononuclear cells, multinucleated osteoclast-type giant cells, foamy macrophages and haemosiderin-laden cells","Surgical synovectomy is standard, but recurrence is common, and CSF1R-targeted therapy is an option for diffuse or unresectable disease"],"pathophysiology":"Diffuse-type tenosynovial giant cell tumour is a locally aggressive neoplasm of synovial-lined structures underpinned by a translocation involving the CSF1 gene, most commonly a COL6A3-CSF1 fusion, in a small subset of the tumour cells. Overexpression of colony-stimulating factor 1 recruits large numbers of non-neoplastic macrophages that bear the CSF1 receptor, so the bulk of the lesion is a reactive inflammatory infiltrate driven by a minority neoplastic population, the so-called landscape effect. Chronic proliferation and recurrent haemorrhage lead to haemosiderin deposition, villous and nodular synovial thickening, and progressive joint destruction. This biology provides the rationale for CSF1 receptor inhibition in advanced disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Localised-type tenosynovial giant cell tumour","Synovial chondromatosis","Rheumatoid or other inflammatory synovitis","Haemophilic arthropathy","Synovial haemangioma","Synovial sarcoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI demonstrating haemosiderin-related blooming artefact and extent of synovial involvement","Plain radiography to assess secondary bone erosions and joint-space changes","Arthroscopic or open synovial biopsy for histological confirmation","Histology showing giant cells, foamy macrophages and haemosiderin pigment","Molecular assessment for CSF1 rearrangement in difficult or diffuse cases"],"redFlags":["Extensive extra-articular soft-tissue extension in diffuse disease","Progressive bone erosion and joint destruction","Repeated recurrence after synovectomy","Atypical features or rapid growth raising concern for a malignant variant"],"prognosis":"The tumour is benign but locally aggressive, and while the localised form is usually cured by excision, the diffuse form recurs frequently and can cause substantial joint damage. Malignant transformation is rare, and CSF1R-directed therapy has improved outcomes in advanced diffuse disease.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting recurrent haemorrhagic effusions as inflammatory or haemophilic arthropathy","Incomplete synovectomy leading to a high recurrence rate in diffuse disease","Overlooking the diagnosis when haemosiderin blooming on MRI is not appreciated","Assuming the tumour is purely reactive rather than a CSF1-driven neoplasm"],"management":["Perform surgical synovectomy, often combined open and arthroscopic and sometimes anterior and posterior, as the mainstay to remove the diffuse synovial disease","Offer a CSF1-receptor inhibitor such as pexidartinib for inoperable, recurrent or extensive diffuse disease, exploiting the CSF1-driven macrophage biology, with monitoring of liver function","Consider adjuvant external-beam radiotherapy or radiosynovectomy to reduce recurrence after incomplete excision","Manage end-stage joint destruction with arthroplasty where the joint is irreparably damaged","Provide physiotherapy to restore range of motion and function after synovectomy","Undertake long-term surveillance with MRI for the frequent local recurrence characteristic of the diffuse form","Manage within an orthopaedic oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"tension_pneumothorax":{"name":"Tension pneumothorax","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tension PTX"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is a clinical diagnosis to be treated immediately, without waiting for imaging, because delay is fatal","Suspect it in a shocked, breathless patient with reduced breath sounds and hyperresonance on one side, especially after trauma or positive-pressure ventilation","Distended neck veins, tracheal deviation away from the affected side, and hypotension are supportive but often late or absent signs","Immediate treatment is emergency decompression of the affected side, followed by definitive chest drain insertion","In a mechanically ventilated patient, sudden hypotension with high airway pressures should prompt consideration of tension pneumothorax","It arises during the breathing step of the primary survey and takes priority over other breathing problems","Reassess after decompression to confirm improvement and to guard against recurrence or a drain that has stopped working"],"pathophysiology":"A one-way valve mechanism allows air to enter the pleural space during inspiration but prevents its escape, so intrapleural pressure rises progressively. The affected lung collapses, and mounting pressure pushes the mediastinum towards the opposite side, compressing the other lung and, critically, kinking the great veins to impair venous return to the heart. The combination of impaired gas exchange and obstructed venous return produces profound hypoxia and obstructive shock. Positive-pressure ventilation can accelerate air accumulation and the development of tension physiology.","additionalDifferentials":["Simple or open pneumothorax without tension physiology","Massive haemothorax","Cardiac tamponade","Massive pulmonary embolism causing obstructive shock","Right main bronchus intubation causing unilateral reduced breath sounds"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination is the basis for immediate treatment; do not delay for imaging","Bedside thoracic ultrasound can rapidly confirm absent lung sliding when immediately available","Chest radiograph only after decompression or when the diagnosis is genuinely uncertain and the patient is stable","Continuous monitoring of oxygenation and haemodynamics","Post-procedure imaging to confirm lung re-expansion and drain position"],"redFlags":["Shock with unilateral absent breath sounds requiring immediate needle or finger decompression before any imaging","Sudden hypotension and rising airway pressures in a ventilated patient","Distended neck veins with tracheal deviation and respiratory distress","Rapid clinical deterioration in a patient with known chest injury or pneumothorax","Recurrence of tension physiology after apparent treatment, indicating a blocked or displaced drain"],"prognosis":"With immediate recognition and decompression the outlook is excellent, but even a short delay can be rapidly fatal because of progressive obstructive shock and hypoxia.","pitfalls":["Waiting for a chest radiograph to confirm the diagnosis before treating","Relying on late signs such as tracheal deviation, which may never appear","Failing to consider it in a deteriorating ventilated patient","Assuming decompression is definitive and not proceeding to a chest drain","Not reassessing for recurrence after initial treatment"],"management":["Perform immediate emergency decompression of the affected haemithorax by needle or finger thoracostomy on clinical suspicion without waiting for imaging, as delay is rapidly fatal","Give high-flow supplemental oxygen and resuscitate for the accompanying obstructive shock","Insert a definitive intercostal chest drain after decompression to re-expand the lung","In a mechanically ventilated patient with sudden hypotension and high airway pressures, decompress promptly and adjust ventilator settings to limit further air accumulation","Treat the underlying cause such as chest trauma and address any ongoing air leak","Confirm lung re-expansion and drain position with post-procedure imaging and continue haemodynamic and oxygenation monitoring","Escalate to thoracic surgery and critical care for a persistent air leak or failure to re-expand"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"terminal_complement_deficiency":{"name":"Terminal complement (C5-C9) deficiency","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["membrane attack complex deficiency","MAC deficiency","C5 deficiency","C6 deficiency","C7 deficiency","C8 deficiency","C9 deficiency","terminal pathway complement deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies / Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The signature clinical association is recurrent invasive Neisseria infection, especially meningococcal disease and disseminated gonococcal infection","Meningococcal disease in these patients is often caused by uncommon serogroups and may recur or present at an atypical age","Inheritance is autosomal recessive, so consanguinity or affected siblings increase suspicion","The defect lies in assembling the membrane attack complex, so bacteriolysis of Neisseria is impaired despite otherwise intact immunity","A low CH50 (classical pathway) with normal individual early components points to a terminal pathway defect and should trigger measurement of individual terminal components","Properdin deficiency (X-linked) and early complement defects can produce overlapping neisserial susceptibility and should be considered","Prevention centres on meningococcal vaccination including serogroup B and quadrivalent conjugate vaccines, and prompt treatment of febrile illness"],"pathophysiology":"The terminal complement components C5 through C9 assemble on microbial surfaces to form the membrane attack complex, a pore that lyses susceptible organisms. Neisseria species are unusually dependent on this direct complement-mediated bacteriolysis for their clearance. When any terminal component is genetically deficient, the classical, lectin and alternative pathways can still opsonise but cannot complete lytic killing, so the host is left disproportionately vulnerable to Neisseria meningitidis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae. Because early complement functions such as opsonisation and immune-complex handling are preserved, patients typically do not have the encapsulated-organism infection pattern or autoimmune features seen with early classical pathway defects.","additionalDifferentials":["Properdin deficiency (X-linked neisserial susceptibility)","Early classical pathway component deficiency (C1, C2, C4)","Factor D or other alternative pathway defects","Asplenia or hyposplenism","Antibody deficiency predisposing to bacterial infection","Complement consumption from active immune-complex disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CH50 (total classical haemolytic complement) as a screen for terminal pathway integrity","AH50 (alternative pathway haemolytic assay) to help localise the defect","Measurement of individual terminal components C5 to C9","Properdin and early component (C3, C4, C2) levels to exclude other complement defects","Microbiological culture and serogrouping of any neisserial isolate","Genetic confirmation of the deficient component where available"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or second episode of invasive meningococcal disease","Meningococcal infection caused by an unusual serogroup or occurring in an adolescent/adult","Family history of invasive neisserial infection or unexplained meningitis deaths","Fulminant sepsis with purpura requiring immediate treatment"],"prognosis":"Individual invasive episodes tend to be less fulminant than in complement-sufficient patients, but the lifelong risk of recurrent neisserial infection persists, and outcome depends heavily on vaccination, patient education, and rapid treatment of febrile illness.","pitfalls":["Attributing a single meningococcal episode to bad luck and not screening the complement system after recurrent or atypical disease","Measuring only individual components without a functional CH50/AH50 to guide localisation","Assuming vaccination gives complete protection and failing to counsel on urgent care for fevers","Overlooking properdin deficiency, which mimics the neisserial phenotype"],"management":["Prioritise immunisation against Neisseria meningitidis with both quadrivalent conjugate (MenACWY) and serogroup B vaccines, with boosters, as the cornerstone of prevention","Complete vaccination against other encapsulated organisms including pneumococcus and Haemophilus influenzae type b","Consider long-term prophylactic penicillin to reduce the risk of invasive meningococcal disease","Educate the patient to seek urgent medical care for any febrile illness and to carry an alert document, given the lifelong recurrent-infection risk","Treat suspected invasive meningococcal or gonococcal infection promptly with a third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone","Screen and vaccinate at-risk family members and offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive defect","Provide ongoing clinical immunology follow-up and culture and serogroup any neisserial isolate to guide public-health action"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"terrible_triad_elbow":{"name":"Terrible triad of the elbow","system":"Musculoskeletal / Trauma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["elbow terrible triad","elbow dislocation with radial head and coronoid fractures","posterolateral rotatory instability injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The triad comprises a posterior or posterolateral elbow dislocation with fractures of the radial head and the coronoid process","It arises from a posterolateral rotatory mechanism, typically an axial load combined with valgus and external rotation","The elbow is highly unstable because both bony buttresses (radial head and coronoid) and the lateral collateral ligament are compromised","Management is usually surgical, fixing or replacing the radial head, addressing the coronoid and anterior capsule, and repairing the lateral collateral ligament, adding a medial repair or hinged fixator if instability persists","The radial head should be preserved or replaced rather than simply excised, in order to maintain stability","The goal is a concentrically reduced joint with a stable arc of motion permitting early mobilisation","CT is valuable to characterise the radial head and coronoid fractures and plan reconstruction"],"pathophysiology":"A fall generating axial loading with valgus and external rotation carries the elbow through posterolateral rotatory subluxation to frank dislocation. As the joint displaces, the radial head is driven against the capitellum and fractures, the coronoid process fractures as the trochlea rides over it, and the lateral collateral ligament complex avulses from the lateral epicondyle. The loss of these anterior, lateral and radiocolumnar stabilisers leaves the elbow grossly unstable and prone to redislocation, so restoration of each element is required to re-establish a stable, congruent joint.","additionalDifferentials":["Simple elbow dislocation without fracture","Isolated radial head fracture","Isolated coronoid process fracture","Trans-olecranon fracture-dislocation or Monteggia variant","Capitellar fracture","Essex-Lopresti injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anteroposterior and lateral elbow radiographs","CT to characterise the radial head and coronoid fractures and plan fixation","Post-reduction radiographs to confirm concentric reduction","Examination and documentation of neurovascular status","Intra-operative assessment of the stable arc of motion"],"redFlags":["Persistent instability or subluxation after reduction","Neurovascular injury about the elbow","Open injury requiring urgent management","Non-concentric reduction with a widened joint (drop sign)"],"prognosis":"Historically a difficult injury with poor results, but a systematic surgical protocol restoring the bony and ligamentous stabilisers has improved outcomes; stiffness, recurrent instability and post-traumatic arthritis remain recognised risks.","pitfalls":["Treating the injury as a simple dislocation and mobilising an unstable joint","Excising the radial head rather than preserving or replacing it","Missing the coronoid fracture","Failing to confirm a concentric reduction on post-reduction imaging"],"management":["Perform urgent closed reduction of the dislocation with documentation of neurovascular status and post-reduction radiographs","Undertake surgical reconstruction of all injured stabilisers as the definitive treatment, with fixation or replacement of the radial head, fixation of the coronoid and repair of the anterior capsule, and repair of the lateral collateral ligament","Preserve or replace the radial head rather than excising it, to maintain longitudinal and valgus stability","Add a medial collateral ligament repair or a hinged external fixator if instability persists after the primary repair","Aim for a concentrically reduced joint with a stable arc of motion permitting early protected mobilisation","Provide supervised physiotherapy and monitor for the recognised complications of stiffness, recurrent instability and post-traumatic arthritis","Manage under specialist orthopaedic trauma care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults","AO Principles of Fracture Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"terrien_marginal_degeneration":{"name":"Terrien marginal degeneration","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Terrien's marginal degeneration","TMD","peripheral non-inflammatory corneal thinning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology / Cornea by Krachmer","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A slowly progressive, usually painless, bilateral but often asymmetric peripheral corneal thinning, classically beginning superiorly","The epithelium remains intact over the thinned gutter, distinguishing it from ulcerative processes such as Mooren ulcer","A fine yellow-white lipid deposit is often seen at the advancing central edge of the thinning","Superficial vascularisation (pannus) crosses into the area of thinning as the degeneration progresses","Progressive thinning induces marked against-the-rule or oblique astigmatism, which is often the main cause of reduced vision","Typically affects middle-aged adults and is generally non-inflammatory, though an uncommon inflammatory variant with episodic redness and pain exists","Management is largely optical (spectacles or contact lenses for astigmatism), reserving lamellar keratoplasty or tectonic grafting for severe thinning or perforation risk","Spontaneous or minor-trauma perforation can occur in advanced disease because of the marked thinning"],"pathophysiology":"Terrien marginal degeneration is a non-inflammatory (in its usual form) peripheral corneal thinning of uncertain cause, in which the peripheral stroma progressively degenerates and thins while the overlying epithelium stays intact. Lipid is deposited at the leading edge and superficial vessels grow into the gutter. The thinning typically starts superiorly and gradually extends circumferentially, producing a shallow peripheral trough. Progressive structural weakening flattens the peripheral cornea and steepens the meridian at right angles, generating high against-the-rule or oblique astigmatism, and severe thinning predisposes to perforation.","additionalDifferentials":["Mooren ulcer and other peripheral ulcerative keratitis","Pellucid marginal degeneration","Peripheral ulcerative keratitis from systemic vasculitis","Marginal furrow degeneration in the elderly","Dellen (localised thinning from adjacent surface elevation)","Keratoconus and other ectatic disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination documenting peripheral thinning with intact epithelium and a lipid line","Corneal topography to characterise the induced astigmatism","Anterior segment optical coherence tomography or pachymetry to quantify thinning","Inflammatory and autoimmune workup to exclude peripheral ulcerative keratitis when signs are atypical","Serial imaging to monitor progression","Refraction to assess the visual impact of astigmatism"],"redFlags":["Severe thinning with risk of spontaneous or traumatic perforation","New pain and redness suggesting the uncommon inflammatory variant or an alternative diagnosis","Rapid progression atypical for this usually indolent degeneration","Epithelial breakdown raising concern for an ulcerative or infective process"],"prognosis":"The degeneration is usually slowly progressive with vision limited mainly by astigmatism, which can often be managed optically. Advanced thinning carries a risk of perforation and may require tectonic surgery, but the condition rarely leads to central corneal opacification.","pitfalls":["Confusing painless thinning with the painful ulceration of Mooren ulcer","Missing high against-the-rule astigmatism as the true cause of reduced vision","Underestimating perforation risk in advanced thinning","Failing to recognise the rarer inflammatory variant"],"management":["Correct the induced against-the-rule or oblique astigmatism optically with spectacles or rigid contact lenses as the main means of preserving vision","Monitor progression with serial corneal topography, pachymetry and anterior-segment imaging","Undertake tectonic surgery such as crescentic lamellar keratoplasty or a corneoscleral graft for severe thinning threatening perforation","Manage frank perforation urgently with tissue adhesive or a patch graft","Treat the uncommon inflammatory variant with topical or systemic anti-inflammatory therapy during episodes of redness and pain","Advise protective eyewear to reduce the risk of traumatic rupture of the thinned peripheral cornea","Refer to a corneal ophthalmology specialist for ongoing management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Cornea by Krachmer, Mannis and Holland"],"crossLinks":[]},"terry_nails":{"name":"Terry nails","system":"Skin / Hair and nails","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Terry's nails","White nails of Terry"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Terry nails show a ground-glass white opacification of most of the nail bed with a preserved narrow distal band of normal pink to brown, usually only a couple of millimetres wide.","The whitening obscures the lunula and typically affects all nails uniformly, being best appreciated on the fingernails.","It is an apparent leukonychia of nail bed origin, so the white area does not migrate distally with nail growth.","Classic associations include cirrhosis and chronic liver disease, but Terry nails are also seen in congestive cardiac failure, type 2 diabetes and with advancing age.","The finding is asymptomatic and matters chiefly as a pointer to underlying systemic disease.","It is distinguished from half-and-half nails by the position and width of the distal coloured band and the absence of the sharp demarcation seen in renal disease.","Terry nails can occur in otherwise healthy older people, so context and additional signs guide the extent of investigation.","Bedside recognition can prompt earlier detection of occult hepatic or cardiac disease."],"pathophysiology":"Terry nails represent an apparent leukonychia in which the nail plate is normal but the nail bed is altered. The favoured explanation is a combination of reduced nail bed vascularity and an increase in connective tissue within the bed, which together diminish the normal pink translucency and produce a uniform white, ground-glass appearance. A narrow distal zone retains its normal vascular pattern, giving the characteristic band of preserved colour at the free margin. The changes are thought to reflect systemic alterations in the microvasculature and are commonly encountered where there is impaired hepatic function, cardiac failure or the vascular changes of diabetes and ageing. Because the abnormality lies in the bed rather than the plate, the pattern remains fixed as the nail grows.","additionalDifferentials":["Half-and-half (Lindsay) nails","Muehrcke lines","True leukonychia","Anaemia-related nail pallor","Normal ageing nail changes","Neapolitan nails of the elderly"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Liver function tests and assessment for chronic liver disease","Fasting glucose or HbA1c for diabetes","Cardiac assessment where heart failure is suspected","Full blood count to exclude anaemia","Clinical examination of all nails and lunulae","Review of age and comorbidities to gauge significance"],"redFlags":["New Terry nails with stigmata of chronic liver disease or decompensation","Accompanying breathlessness or oedema suggesting cardiac failure","Rapid onset in a previously well younger patient","Coexisting features suggesting the sharp demarcation of half-and-half nails and renal disease"],"prognosis":"Terry nails are benign in themselves and require no treatment, but they can be an early clue to significant hepatic, cardiac or metabolic disease whose prognosis they reflect. In healthy older individuals they carry no adverse significance.","pitfalls":["Confusing Terry nails with half-and-half nails and misdirecting the systemic workup","Overlooking the sign in older patients where it may still indicate occult disease","Assuming the change is a primary nail disorder rather than a systemic marker","Attempting to treat the nail rather than the underlying condition"],"management":["Recognise the finding as a pointer to systemic disease and investigate accordingly, rather than treating the nails, which need no specific therapy","Identify and manage underlying chronic liver disease or cirrhosis with hepatology input","Assess for and treat congestive cardiac failure where suspected","Optimise glycaemic control where type 2 diabetes is identified","Reassure the patient that the nail change is asymptomatic and benign in itself","Arrange follow-up directed by the underlying systemic condition, recognising the finding may be an early clue to significant disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"terson_syndrome":{"name":"Terson syndrome","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Terson's syndrome","intraocular haemorrhage of subarachnoid haemorrhage"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Refers to intraocular haemorrhage, most often vitreous or pre-retinal, occurring in association with subarachnoid or other intracranial haemorrhage or an acute rise in intracranial pressure","The haemorrhage may be unilateral or bilateral and can involve the vitreous, the sub-hyaloid or sub-internal limiting membrane space, or the retina itself","It is frequently discovered when a neurologically recovering patient reports blurred vision or floaters","Its presence is a marker of the severity of the intracranial event and is associated with higher mortality","Fundus examination and, when the media are opaque, B-scan ultrasound establish the diagnosis","Management is observation for spontaneous clearance, with vitrectomy considered for non-clearing vitreous haemorrhage","In young children, early vitrectomy is important to prevent deprivation amblyopia"],"pathophysiology":"An abrupt rise in intracranial pressure, typically from subarachnoid haemorrhage, is transmitted along the optic nerve sheath to compress the central retinal vein and the retinochoroidal venous anastomoses. The resulting acute venous congestion and stasis rupture peripapillary and retinal capillaries, releasing blood into the retina, the pre-retinal spaces and the vitreous. The intraocular blood therefore arises locally rather than tracking directly from the cranial cavity.","additionalDifferentials":["Valsalva retinopathy","Posterior vitreous detachment with haemorrhage","Proliferative diabetic retinopathy","Retinal vein occlusion","Non-accidental injury in an infant","Ocular trauma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination to characterise the intraocular haemorrhage","Neuroimaging with computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging for the intracranial haemorrhage","Optical coherence tomography to localise pre-retinal or sub-internal limiting membrane blood","B-scan ultrasound when the media are too opaque for a fundus view","Coordination with neurosurgery and neurology for the underlying condition"],"redFlags":["Reduced consciousness or neurological deterioration from the underlying intracranial haemorrhage","Bilateral dense intraocular haemorrhage","Suspected non-accidental injury in an infant","Signs of persistently raised intracranial pressure"],"prognosis":"The visual prognosis is often good once the haemorrhage clears spontaneously or after vitrectomy, but the overall outcome is dominated by the severity of the underlying neurological injury.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the ocular findings in an unconscious or sedated patient","Failing to recognise an underlying intracranial haemorrhage when the eye signs present first","Delaying vitrectomy in a young child at risk of amblyopia","Assuming the haemorrhage is due to a simple Valsalva effort"],"management":["Prioritise treatment of the underlying subarachnoid or intracranial haemorrhage and raised intracranial pressure with neurosurgery and neurology","Observe most intraocular haemorrhage for spontaneous clearance, which commonly occurs over weeks to months","Perform pars plana vitrectomy for non-clearing vitreous haemorrhage, dense bilateral involvement, or where earlier visual rehabilitation is needed","Consider earlier vitrectomy in affected children to prevent deprivation amblyopia","Monitor for the complications of retinal detachment, epiretinal membrane and raised intraocular pressure during follow-up","Coordinate ophthalmology follow-up with the neurosurgical and neurological team managing the primary injury"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course: Retina and Vitreous"],"crossLinks":[]},"tertiary_hyperparathyroidism":{"name":"Tertiary hyperparathyroidism","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tertiary HPT","autonomous hyperparathyroidism of renal disease","refractory renal hyperparathyroidism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / WHO Classification of Endocrine Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Develops when long-standing secondary hyperparathyroidism, usually from chronic kidney disease, progresses to autonomous PTH secretion that persists despite correction of the original stimulus","Characterised biochemically by hypercalcaemia with an inappropriately high PTH, in contrast to the hypocalcaemia or normocalcaemia of secondary disease","Classically emerges after successful renal transplantation, when the previously enlarged, autonomous glands continue to oversecrete despite restored renal function","Chronically stimulated glands undergo nodular hyperplasia with reduced calcium-sensing receptor and vitamin D receptor expression, driving the shift to autonomy","Persistent hypercalcaemia threatens the transplanted kidney and accelerates vascular and soft-tissue calcification and bone disease","Medical therapy with calcimimetics can lower calcium and PTH, but truly autonomous, refractory disease often requires parathyroidectomy","Surgical options include subtotal parathyroidectomy or total parathyroidectomy with autotransplantation, balancing recurrence against the risk of permanent hypoparathyroidism"],"pathophysiology":"Tertiary hyperparathyroidism represents the transition of chronically stimulated parathyroid glands to autonomous hormone secretion. In chronic kidney disease, persistent hyperphosphataemia, reduced active vitamin D, and hypocalcaemia drive secondary hyperparathyroidism with diffuse then nodular parathyroid hyperplasia. Over years, nodular areas develop monoclonal expansion with down-regulation of the calcium-sensing and vitamin D receptors, so the glands no longer respond appropriately to normalised calcium and continue to oversecrete PTH. When the underlying stimulus is removed, most notably after renal transplantation, this autonomous secretion produces frank hypercalcaemia and ongoing skeletal and vascular injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary hyperparathyroidism of chronic kidney disease","Primary hyperparathyroidism from an adenoma","Parathyroid carcinoma","Immobilisation or vitamin D-related hypercalcaemia in renal patients","Calcium and vitamin D over-supplementation","Aluminium or adynamic bone disease affecting calcium handling"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium, phosphate, and intact PTH demonstrating hypercalcaemia with inappropriately high PTH","Assessment of renal function and transplant status","Vitamin D status and markers of bone turnover","Neck ultrasound and sestamibi or 4D-CT to assess gland number and enlargement before surgery","Bone density and skeletal imaging for renal osteodystrophy and fractures","Imaging for vascular and soft-tissue calcification when clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Progressive hypercalcaemia threatening a transplanted kidney","Calciphylaxis or extensive vascular and soft-tissue calcification","Severe bone disease with pathological fractures","Symptomatic hypercalcaemic crisis requiring urgent treatment"],"prognosis":"Autonomous glands may occasionally regress after transplantation, but persistent tertiary disease generally requires calcimimetics or parathyroidectomy; surgery is usually curative, with the main trade-off being recurrence versus permanent hypoparathyroidism.","pitfalls":["Confusing tertiary disease with primary hyperparathyroidism and overlooking the underlying renal history","Persisting with medical therapy alone in truly autonomous, refractory disease that warrants surgery","Failing to monitor calcium after transplantation and missing evolving autonomy","Underestimating the risk of profound postoperative hypocalcaemia and hungry bone syndrome after parathyroidectomy"],"management":["Initiate a calcimimetic such as cinacalcet to lower serum calcium and PTH in autonomous disease, as a bridge or alternative to surgery","Optimise mineral metabolism by controlling hyperphosphataemia and using active vitamin D judiciously, avoiding aggravation of the hypercalcaemia","Perform parathyroidectomy, subtotal or total with autotransplantation, for refractory autonomous disease as the definitive treatment","Localise abnormal glands preoperatively with neck ultrasound and sestamibi or 4D-CT scanning","Monitor closely after parathyroidectomy for hypocalcaemia and hungry-bone syndrome, replacing calcium and vitamin D","Protect the transplanted kidney and vasculature by correcting persistent hypercalcaemia that accelerates graft dysfunction and soft-tissue calcification","Manage the associated renal osteodystrophy with bone-density and skeletal surveillance","Coordinate care between nephrology or transplant medicine and endocrine surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","WHO Classification of Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"testicular_appendage_torsion":{"name":"Torsion of a testicular appendage","system":"Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["torsion of the appendix testis","torted hydatid of Morgagni"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric urology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common cause of acute scrotal pain in prepubertal boys, from twisting of an embryological remnant (usually the appendix testis)","Pain is usually more gradual and less severe than in testicular torsion, and systemic upset is uncommon","The classic sign is a tender blue dot at the upper pole of the testis seen through the scrotal skin","The testis itself lies normally with a normal cremasteric reflex, unlike in testicular torsion","It is a key differential of the acute scrotum, where the priority is to exclude testicular torsion","It is self-limiting and managed conservatively once torsion of the testis is excluded"],"pathophysiology":"The testis and epididymis carry small vestigial embryological appendages. Torsion of one of these pedunculated appendages, most often the appendix testis (hydatid of Morgagni), causes ischaemia and infarction of the remnant with local inflammation and pain. Because the testicular blood supply is unaffected, the testis remains viable, and the process settles as the infarcted appendage necroses and resolves. The main clinical importance is distinguishing it from true testicular torsion, which is a surgical emergency.","additionalDifferentials":["Testicular torsion","Epididymo-orchitis","Incarcerated inguinal hernia","Idiopathic scrotal oedema","Henoch-Schonlein purpura affecting the scrotum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment, including looking for the blue dot sign","Doppler ultrasound showing normal testicular blood flow when the diagnosis is uncertain","Assessment of the cremasteric reflex and testicular lie","Urinalysis to help exclude infection","Surgical exploration if testicular torsion cannot be excluded"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting testicular torsion (severe sudden pain, high-riding testis, absent cremasteric reflex)","Diagnostic uncertainty requiring exploration","Signs of infection","Worsening rather than settling pain"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limiting, resolving over days with conservative management and analgesia, with no long-term testicular consequences.","pitfalls":["Missing testicular torsion by over-diagnosing appendage torsion","Delaying exploration when torsion cannot be excluded","Relying on the blue dot sign, which is not always present","Attributing pain to infection without considering torsion"],"management":["First exclude true testicular torsion, since distinguishing the two is the key priority in the acute scrotum","Manage conservatively once torsion is excluded, with simple analgesia or NSAIDs, rest and scrotal support, as the condition is self-limiting","Reassure that the infarcted appendage necroses and resolves over days without long-term testicular consequence","Reserve surgical exploration and excision of the appendage for cases where testicular torsion cannot be excluded or where pain is severe or persistent","Give safety-net advice to return urgently if pain worsens or acute features develop","Involve paediatric surgery or urology in assessment of the acute scrotum"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric urology references (standard reference)","Emergency urology references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"testicular_atrophy":{"name":"Testicular atrophy","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["testicular atrophy","atrophic testis","shrunken testis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Testicular atrophy describes a testis that has become abnormally small and soft, reflecting loss of seminiferous tubule volume and often impaired spermatogenesis","Causes are diverse and include prior testicular torsion, trauma, orchitis (notably mumps), varicocele, cryptorchidism, previous surgery, and vascular injury","Endocrine and toxic causes include hypogonadotropic states, exogenous androgens or anabolic steroids, and chemotherapy or radiotherapy","Bilateral atrophy raises concern for a systemic, endocrine, or genetic cause such as Klinefelter syndrome, whereas unilateral atrophy often follows a local insult","Assessment includes measurement of testicular volume, a semen analysis when fertility is relevant, and hormone profiling with FSH, LH, and testosterone","An atrophic testis still warrants examination and ultrasound because a small tumour or the increased malignancy risk of an atrophic or previously undescended testis should not be missed","Management is directed at the underlying cause, with attention to fertility preservation, hormone replacement where hypogonadism is confirmed, and counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Testicular atrophy results from injury to or loss of the germinal epithelium and, in some cases, the Leydig cells, leading to shrinkage of testicular volume. Ischaemic insults such as torsion or vascular injury, inflammatory damage from orchitis, the raised scrotal temperature and venous stasis of a varicocele, obstruction or heat effects, direct trauma, and gonadotoxic drugs or radiation all damage the seminiferous tubules. Endocrine causes act by removing the gonadotropin drive needed to maintain the testis, as in hypogonadotropic hypogonadism or suppression by exogenous androgens, while genetic conditions such as Klinefelter syndrome cause progressive tubular sclerosis. The common endpoint is a small, firm-to-soft testis with reduced or absent spermatogenesis.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital small testis or hypogonadism","Klinefelter syndrome and other genetic causes","Post-torsion or post-traumatic atrophy","Post-orchitis atrophy (e.g. mumps)","Varicocele-associated volume loss","Cryptorchidism with an intrinsically small testis","Anorchia (absent rather than atrophic testis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Measurement of testicular volume by examination and orchidometer or ultrasound","Scrotal ultrasound to assess parenchyma and exclude a mass","Hormone profile including FSH, LH, and testosterone","Semen analysis when fertility is a concern","Karyotype or genetic testing when a chromosomal cause is suspected","History of medications, anabolic steroid use, and previous gonadotoxic treatment"],"redFlags":["A discrete mass within an atrophic testis suggesting a tumour","Rapid or progressive bilateral atrophy without an obvious cause","Features of hypogonadism such as loss of libido, fatigue, or reduced secondary sexual characteristics","History suggesting an untreated or missed testicular torsion"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the cause and its reversibility: atrophy from established ischaemic, inflammatory, or genetic damage is usually permanent with impaired fertility, whereas atrophy from reversible factors such as varicocele or suppressive drugs may improve when the cause is addressed; hormone replacement can manage hypogonadism.","pitfalls":["Overlooking a tumour within a small or previously undescended testis","Attributing atrophy to a single cause without a systematic endocrine and genetic assessment","Missing anabolic steroid use as a reversible contributor","Failing to discuss and arrange fertility preservation before it is too late"],"management":["Address reversible causes directly, repairing a symptomatic varicocele and withdrawing anabolic steroids or other gonadotoxic agents where possible","Treat hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with gonadotropin therapy using hCG with FSH when fertility is desired, restoring the trophic drive to the testis","Provide androgen replacement with testosterone for symptomatic hypogonadism where fertility is not the immediate goal","Manage fertility with semen analysis, sperm cryopreservation and surgical sperm retrieval with assisted reproduction where indicated","Maintain examination and ultrasound surveillance, since an atrophic or previously undescended testis carries an increased malignancy risk","Arrange karyotyping and genetic counselling where a chromosomal cause such as Klinefelter syndrome is suspected","Coordinate care across urology, endocrinology and fertility services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology, Male Reproductive and Sexual Medicine section"],"crossLinks":[]},"testicular_cancer":{"name":"Testicular Cancer","system":"Genitourinary / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Testicular germ cell tumor","Testicular GCT","Germ cell tumor of the testis"],"chapter":93,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.704","clinicalPearls":["Germ cell tumors (GCTs) account for about 95% of all testicular neoplasms; non-GCTs of the testis are much less common, and roughly 5% of GCTs arise in extragonadal sites such as the mediastinum, retroperitoneum, and pineal gland.","Testicular cancer is regarded as a model curable neoplasm: about 95% of newly diagnosed patients are cured, and treatment is dictated by pathology (seminoma vs nonseminoma) and stage.","It is the most common malignancy in adolescent and young adult males (age 15-39), most often diagnosed in Caucasians and much less common in African Americans; incidence in those over 50 is rising.","Strongest risk factors are a prior history of GCT, cryptorchidism, and germ cell neoplasia in situ; a prior GCT carries a 1-2% risk of a contralateral tumor (usually metachronous), and cryptorchidism raises risk roughly four- to sixfold.","Orchidopexy before puberty reduces but does not eliminate the cryptorchidism-associated risk, and the contralateral normally descended testis also remains at risk.","A solid mass on scrotal ultrasound (typically hypoechoic, sometimes multifocal) must be considered malignant until proven otherwise; transscrotal aspiration or biopsy must never be performed because it risks seeding the scrotum or inguinal nodes.","Tumor marker biology is key: seminomas never secrete AFP, so a histologic seminoma with elevated AFP is managed as a nonseminomatous GCT; hCG can be elevated in both seminoma and nonseminoma, and choriocarcinoma may produce markedly high hCG.","Serum tumor markers (AFP, hCG, LDH) should be measured before and after orchiectomy; AFP is elevated in roughly 60-70% of nonseminomatous GCTs, while most seminomas and up to a third of nonseminomas have no marker elevation.","Lymphatic spread follows a predictable pattern: left-sided tumors drain first to left paraaortic nodes below the left renal vessels, right-sided tumors to aortocaval nodes below the right renal vessels, then to retrocrural, mediastinal, and supraclavicular nodes; the lung is the most common hematogenous site.","Genetically, most GCTs show excess copies of isochromosome 12p (i[12p]); the strongest heritable-risk locus identified is KITLG (KIT ligand) on chromosome 12, and overall heritability is nearly 50%.","Teratoma is chemotherapy-resistant and must be managed surgically; the other nonseminomatous subtypes (embryonal carcinoma, yolk sac tumor, choriocarcinoma) and seminomas are chemosensitive.","Sperm banking should be offered before chemotherapy: about 30% of newly diagnosed patients already have severe oligospermia or azoospermia, and treatment-related sterility is common (though ~80% recover spermatogenesis over years)."],"pathophysiology":"GCTs originate from the primordial germ cell, and most malignant GCTs arise from germ cell neoplasia in situ; the precise molecular steps driving progression from in situ neoplasia to invasive tumor are not fully defined. Genetic analysis shows excess copy number of isochromosome 12p in most cases, and genome-wide studies implicate several loci, most strongly KITLG on chromosome 12. Tumors are classified as seminoma (must be 100% seminoma; any mixed tumor is treated as nonseminomatous) or nonseminoma. Seminomas may contain syncytiotrophoblastic cells that secrete hCG but never secrete AFP. Nonseminomatous subtypes include embryonal carcinoma (the most undifferentiated, able to differentiate into the others, and may secrete AFP and/or hCG), yolk sac tumor (often AFP-secreting), choriocarcinoma (aggressive, often very high hCG), and teratoma (mature, immature, or malignant somatic tissue derived from two or more germ layers).","additionalDifferentials":["Epididymitis","Orchitis","Testicular atrophy","Hereditary persistence of AFP or hepatic disease (false AFP elevation)","Non-germ-cell testicular tumors"],"infectionMimics":["Epididymitis (a trial of antibacterials may be considered, but failure to respond should prompt scrotal ultrasound)","Orchitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Testicular atrophy","Hereditary persistence of AFP (mildly elevated baseline AFP)","Hepatic disease causing false AFP elevation","False hCG elevation from hypogonadism, marijuana use, or assay interference"],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound of both testicles (GCT typically hypoechoic, possibly multifocal)","Serum AFP, hCG, and LDH before and after orchiectomy","CT scan of abdomen and pelvis for staging","Chest x-ray, with chest CT if retroperitoneal disease or lung nodules are present","Pathologic review of the entire orchiectomy specimen by an experienced pathologist","Serum microRNA miR-371a-3p (a promising investigational biomarker undergoing validation)"],"redFlags":["Painless testicular mass (pathognomonic of GCT) or testicular swelling, firmness, or discomfort","Back or flank pain suggesting retroperitoneal metastases","Cough, dyspnea, or hemoptysis indicating lung metastases","Gynecomastia in the setting of elevated hCG","Suspected epididymitis/orchitis that fails to respond to antibiotics","Rapidly progressive disease with life-threatening symptoms (e.g., hemoptysis) warranting emergent treatment even without tissue diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are excellent overall, with about 95% of newly diagnosed patients cured. Nearly all stage I patients (seminoma and nonseminoma) achieve cure. In metastatic (stage III) disease classified by the International Germ Cell Consensus Classification, most patients have good-risk disease with >90% cure; intermediate- and poor-risk groups have approximately 80% and 50% five-year survival, respectively. About 20-30% of patients with metastatic GCT treated with cisplatin-based chemotherapy do not achieve durable control, with most relapses occurring within 2 years of completing treatment.","pitfalls":["Performing transscrotal biopsy or aspiration of a testicular mass, which can seed the scrotum or inguinal nodes and should never be done.","Misclassifying a histologic seminoma with elevated AFP as a true seminoma, when it must be approached as a nonseminomatous GCT.","Over-relying on tumor markers for diagnosis, since most seminomas and up to a third of nonseminomas have no marker elevation.","Diagnostic delay (not uncommon) leading to more advanced stage at presentation, including mislabeling a GCT as epididymitis/orchitis.","Assuming residual postchemotherapy masses are benign: imaging cannot distinguish necrosis/fibrosis from teratoma or viable tumor, and enlarging cystic retroperitoneal masses with normalized markers may represent growing teratoma syndrome.","Forgetting that LDH is a nonspecific marker whose main role is risk classification in metastatic disease, not diagnosis."],"management":["Perform radical inguinal orchidectomy for diagnosis and local control, never a trans-scrotal biopsy which risks tumour seeding","Offer sperm banking before orchidectomy or systemic therapy, as both disease and treatment threaten fertility","Direct subsequent treatment by histology and stage: active surveillance for many stage I tumours, cisplatin-based combination chemotherapy such as BEP for metastatic disease, and radiotherapy as an option for seminoma","Consider retroperitoneal lymph-node dissection for selected non-seminomatous tumours or residual post-chemotherapy masses","Track serum AFP, beta-hCG and LDH before and serially after treatment to guide staging, response and relapse detection","Maintain long-term surveillance for relapse, a metachronous contralateral tumour, and late effects of platinum chemotherapy and radiotherapy","Provide androgen replacement if hypogonadal after treatment and manage within a uro-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":"Three main AJCC TNM-based stages: Stage I limited to the testis; Stage II involving retroperitoneal lymph nodes (subgrouped IIA/IIB/IIC by node size: <=2 cm, >2-5 cm, >5 cm); Stage III with lymph node involvement beyond the retroperitoneum and/or distant metastases. Stage III is further stratified into good-, intermediate-, and poor-risk groups by the International Germ Cell Consensus Classification using histology, primary site, presence of nonpulmonary visceral metastases, and postorchiectomy marker levels (e.g., for NSGCT good-risk: AFP <1000 ng/mL, hCG <5000 mIU/mL, LDH <1.5x ULN; poor-risk: AFP >10,000 ng/mL, hCG >50,000 mIU/mL, LDH >10x ULN, mediastinal primary, or nonpulmonary visceral metastases). Marker half-lives: AFP 5-7 days, hCG 24-36 hours.","references":["Harrison 22e p.704","Harrison 22e p.705","Harrison 22e p.706","Harrison 22e p.707","Harrison 22e p.708"],"crossLinks":[]},"testicular_germ_cell_tumor":{"name":"Testicular germ cell tumour (overview)","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["testicular cancer","germ cell tumour of the testis","TGCT","GCT"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents as a painless, firm, unilateral testicular mass or diffuse enlargement in a young or middle-aged man, and any solid intratesticular lesion is malignant until proven otherwise","Germ cell tumours are broadly divided into seminoma and non-seminomatous germ cell tumour (NSGCT), a distinction that drives staging, treatment, and follow-up","Serum tumour markers (AFP, beta-hCG, LDH) should always be measured before and after treatment: seminoma may raise beta-hCG and LDH but keeps a normal AFP, whereas a raised AFP indicates a non-seminomatous element regardless of histology","A history of cryptorchidism (including the contralateral, normally descended testis), prior contralateral tumour, family history, and infertility are recognised risk factors","Scrotal ultrasound is the first-line imaging test and radical inguinal orchidectomy is the standard approach for diagnosis and local control; trans-scrotal biopsy is avoided to prevent tumour seeding","Sperm banking should be offered before orchidectomy or systemic therapy because both the disease and its treatment threaten fertility","Staging with CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis assesses retroperitoneal nodal and visceral spread, and post-orchidectomy marker kinetics guide risk stratification"],"pathophysiology":"Most testicular germ cell tumours arise from a precursor lesion, germ cell neoplasia in situ, in which malignant transformation of primordial germ cells within the seminiferous tubules is thought to begin in fetal life. Progression yields either seminoma, which resembles undifferentiated germ cells, or non-seminomatous tumours that recapitulate embryonic and extra-embryonic differentiation (embryonal carcinoma, yolk sac tumour, choriocarcinoma, teratoma), often as mixed tumours. Isochromosome 12p is a characteristic cytogenetic marker. Spread is typically lymphatic to the retroperitoneal nodes following gonadal drainage, with later haematogenous dissemination to lung and other viscera; choriocarcinoma has a particular tendency to early blood-borne spread.","additionalDifferentials":["Epididymo-orchitis or an inflammatory testicular mass","Testicular lymphoma, especially in older men","Sex cord-stromal tumours (Leydig or Sertoli cell tumour)","Spermatocele or epididymal cyst","Hydrocele obscuring an underlying tumour","Testicular torsion presenting as acute swelling","Metastasis to the testis from another primary"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound to confirm an intratesticular solid lesion","Serum tumour markers AFP, beta-hCG, and LDH before and serially after orchidectomy","Radical inguinal orchidectomy for histological diagnosis and local control","CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis for nodal and visceral staging","Semen analysis and sperm banking prior to treatment","MRI or brain imaging when choriocarcinoma or neurological symptoms raise concern for cerebral metastases"],"redFlags":["Bulky retroperitoneal disease or a supraclavicular node indicating advanced spread","Respiratory compromise or haemoptysis from pulmonary metastases","Very high beta-hCG with gynaecomastia or thyrotoxic features suggesting large tumour burden","Neurological symptoms raising suspicion of cerebral metastases from choriocarcinoma","Markers that fail to normalise after orchidectomy, indicating residual or metastatic disease"],"prognosis":"Testicular germ cell tumour is among the most curable solid malignancies; the great majority of patients, including many with metastatic disease, achieve long-term cure with stage-appropriate surgery, surveillance, cisplatin-based chemotherapy, or radiotherapy, though prognosis worsens with high-risk marker elevation and non-pulmonary visceral metastases.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a tumour for epididymo-orchitis and delaying ultrasound and orchidectomy","Performing a trans-scrotal biopsy or orchidectomy and risking tumour seeding and altered lymphatic drainage","Omitting pre-treatment tumour markers, losing prognostic and monitoring information","Failing to offer sperm banking before orchidectomy or chemotherapy","Overlooking a small contralateral lesion or the increased contralateral risk in men with cryptorchidism"],"management":["Perform radical inguinal orchidectomy for histological diagnosis and local control, avoiding trans-scrotal biopsy to prevent tumour seeding","Offer sperm banking before orchidectomy or systemic therapy given the fertility threat from disease and treatment","Measure serum AFP, beta-hCG and LDH before and serially after orchidectomy to guide staging, risk stratification and monitoring","Tailor treatment to seminoma-versus-non-seminoma histology and stage: active surveillance for many stage I tumours, cisplatin-based combination chemotherapy for metastatic disease, and radiotherapy as an option for seminoma","Consider retroperitoneal lymph-node dissection for selected non-seminomatous disease and resect residual post-chemotherapy masses","Maintain prolonged surveillance for relapse and a metachronous contralateral tumour, with androgen replacement if hypogonadism develops","Manage within a uro-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","EAU Guidelines on Testicular Cancer"],"crossLinks":[]},"testicular_lymphoma":{"name":"Testicular lymphoma","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["primary testicular lymphoma","testicular diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","PTL"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Testicular lymphoma is the commonest testicular tumour in men over about 60 years and the most frequent bilateral testicular tumour","It usually presents as a painless testicular mass, sometimes with B symptoms such as fever, night sweats, or weight loss","Most cases are diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, and involvement may be a primary extranodal presentation or part of disseminated systemic lymphoma","Germ cell tumour markers (AFP, beta-hCG) are characteristically normal, which helps distinguish it from germ cell tumours in the older patient","Radical inguinal orchidectomy provides tissue diagnosis and local control, after which management is systemic and led by haemato-oncology","There is a notable risk of relapse in sanctuary sites, particularly the central nervous system and the contralateral testis, prompting CNS-directed prophylaxis and consideration of contralateral scrotal radiotherapy","Staging includes CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis, and CNS assessment given the relapse pattern"],"pathophysiology":"Primary testicular lymphoma is a predominantly diffuse large B-cell lymphoma that infiltrates the interstitium between seminiferous tubules, often with vascular invasion. The testis behaves as an immune-privileged sanctuary site, which is thought to contribute to the characteristic pattern of relapse in other sanctuary sites such as the contralateral testis and the central nervous system even after apparent local control. Disease may be confined to the testis at presentation or reflect systemic nodal or extranodal lymphoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Germ cell tumour (seminoma or NSGCT)","Epididymo-orchitis","Leukaemic infiltration of the testis","Sex cord-stromal tumour","Metastasis to the testis","Granulomatous orchitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound demonstrating diffuse or multifocal hypoechoic infiltration, sometimes bilateral","Serum AFP and beta-hCG (typically normal) to distinguish from germ cell tumour, plus LDH","Radical inguinal orchidectomy for histology and immunophenotyping","CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis, often with PET, for systemic staging","Cerebrospinal fluid examination and CNS imaging given the relapse pattern","Bone marrow assessment as part of lymphoma staging"],"redFlags":["B symptoms or rapid bilateral testicular enlargement suggesting aggressive systemic disease","Neurological symptoms indicating central nervous system involvement","Contralateral testicular enlargement suggesting sanctuary-site relapse","Extensive nodal or extranodal disease on staging"],"prognosis":"Primary testicular lymphoma tends to behave aggressively with a significant risk of relapse in the central nervous system and contralateral testis; outcomes have improved with combined immunochemotherapy plus CNS prophylaxis and contralateral radiotherapy, but late relapses mean prolonged follow-up is required.","pitfalls":["Assuming a testicular mass in an older man is a germ cell tumour and overlooking lymphoma","Failing to arrange systemic and CNS staging after orchidectomy","Neglecting CNS prophylaxis and contralateral testicular protection given the relapse pattern","Attributing bilateral disease to benign inflammation"],"management":["Perform radical inguinal orchidectomy for tissue diagnosis, immunophenotyping and local control","Treat with systemic immunochemotherapy, combining rituximab with an anthracycline-based regimen such as R-CHOP, as the mainstay for this diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, led by haemato-oncology","Give central-nervous-system-directed prophylaxis with intrathecal or high-dose methotrexate because of the propensity for CNS sanctuary relapse","Deliver prophylactic radiotherapy to the contralateral testis, another recognised sanctuary site of relapse","Complete lymphoma staging including cerebrospinal fluid examination, bone marrow assessment and PET-CT","Maintain prolonged surveillance for late relapse in the central nervous system and contralateral testis","Manage within a haemato-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","WHO Classification of Tumours: Urinary and Male Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"testicular_microlithiasis":{"name":"Testicular microlithiasis","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TML","testicular microliths","microlithiasis of the testis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An incidental ultrasound finding of multiple tiny, usually sub-millimetre to a few millimetres, non-shadowing echogenic foci scattered through the testicular parenchyma","Classically described as a 'starry sky' appearance, with classic or diffuse microlithiasis defined as five or more microliths per ultrasound field","Usually asymptomatic and discovered during scrotal imaging performed for another reason such as pain, subfertility, or a lump","The association with testicular germ-cell tumour is real, but its clinical significance and the need for surveillance are debated, since most men with microlithiasis never develop cancer","Risk is considered higher when microlithiasis coexists with other factors - subfertility, testicular atrophy, cryptorchidism or prior undescended testis, a personal or family history of germ-cell tumour, or a contralateral tumour","In the absence of additional risk factors most guidance advises teaching testicular self-examination rather than routine repeat ultrasound, reserving surveillance imaging for higher-risk men","Microlithiasis is not itself a premalignant lesion and needs no treatment; the emphasis is on appropriate risk stratification"],"pathophysiology":"Testicular microlithiasis represents calcified concretions within the lumen of the seminiferous tubules. Cells that degenerate within a tubule fail to be cleared and act as a nidus for laminated calcium deposition surrounded by a glycoprotein matrix, producing the characteristic tiny echogenic foci seen on ultrasound. The same disordered tubular environment that favours these calcifications is thought to be shared with abnormalities of the germinal epithelium, which may explain the statistical association with germ-cell neoplasia and with subfertility, even though the microliths themselves are not a precursor lesion.","additionalDifferentials":["Large-cell calcifying Sertoli cell tumour","Calcification from prior granulomatous orchitis","Burnt-out (regressed) germ-cell tumour","Tunica albuginea calcification","Testicular scarring or old infarct","Calcified intratesticular haematoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound to characterise and quantify the foci and assess for any associated mass","Testicular tumour markers (AFP, beta-hCG, LDH) if a discrete lesion is seen","Semen analysis in subfertile men","Clinical examination with teaching of testicular self-examination","Interval ultrasound surveillance in higher-risk patients"],"redFlags":["A discrete focal testicular mass on ultrasound","Elevated testicular tumour markers","A rapidly changing testicular size or a new hard lump","Coexisting cryptorchidism or a prior germ-cell tumour","Testicular atrophy accompanying the microlithiasis"],"prognosis":"In isolation the prognosis is excellent and the great majority of men remain free of malignancy; the outlook is chiefly determined by the presence or absence of additional germ-cell-tumour risk factors.","pitfalls":["Over-investigating low-risk men with repeated ultrasounds and provoking unnecessary anxiety","Conversely, failing to arrange surveillance in men who have microlithiasis together with significant risk factors","Assuming microlithiasis is itself premalignant and requires treatment","Not teaching and documenting testicular self-examination as the key safety-net"],"management":["Risk-stratify each patient by the presence of additional germ-cell-tumour risk factors such as subfertility, testicular atrophy, cryptorchidism or a personal or family history of germ-cell tumour","Teach regular testicular self-examination as the principal strategy for men with isolated microlithiasis and no additional risk factors","Reserve interval scrotal ultrasound surveillance for higher-risk men rather than applying it routinely","Investigate any discrete mass promptly with tumour markers AFP, beta-hCG and LDH and urological assessment to exclude germ-cell tumour","Offer semen analysis to subfertile men, given the association with impaired spermatogenesis","Reassure low-risk men that the great majority never develop malignancy","Refer to urology when additional risk factors or a suspicious lesion are present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","European Society of Urogenital Radiology guidance on testicular microlithiasis"],"crossLinks":[]},"testicular_torsion":{"name":"Testicular torsion","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["torsion of the testis","spermatic cord torsion","testis torsion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic presentation is sudden, severe unilateral scrotal pain of rapid onset, often with nausea and vomiting, most commonly in adolescents and young men but also seen in neonates","Examination typically shows a swollen, exquisitely tender, high-riding testis that may lie transversely (horizontal lie) with an absent cremasteric reflex","It is a time-critical surgical emergency: testicular salvage is high if detorsion occurs within about six hours and falls sharply thereafter","The bell-clapper anomaly, in which the tunica vaginalis invests the testis abnormally, predisposes to intravaginal torsion and is often bilateral","Diagnosis is fundamentally clinical; imaging should never delay surgery when torsion is strongly suspected","Colour Doppler ultrasound showing absent or reduced intratesticular blood flow supports the diagnosis, but a normal study does not reliably exclude it","Definitive treatment is emergency scrotal exploration with detorsion and orchidopexy of the affected testis, plus fixation of the contralateral testis because the predisposing anomaly is usually bilateral","Manual detorsion may be attempted as a temporising measure but does not replace surgical exploration and fixation"],"pathophysiology":"Testicular torsion is twisting of the spermatic cord that obstructs venous drainage first and then arterial inflow, producing ischaemia and, if unrelieved, haemorrhagic infarction of the testis. The intravaginal form, most common in adolescents, is facilitated by the bell-clapper deformity where the tunica vaginalis surrounds the testis and epididymis completely, allowing the testis to rotate freely within it. Extravaginal torsion occurs in neonates when the entire tunica and its contents twist above the level of attachment. The degree of twisting and the duration of ischaemia determine the likelihood of viability.","additionalDifferentials":["Epididymo-orchitis","Torsion of a testicular or epididymal appendage (appendix testis)","Incarcerated or strangulated inguinal hernia","Idiopathic scrotal oedema","Testicular trauma or haematoma","Testicular tumour with haemorrhage","Henoch-Schonlein purpura involving the scrotum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent surgical exploration when clinical suspicion is high (imaging must not delay this)","Colour Doppler ultrasound to assess intratesticular blood flow when the diagnosis is uncertain and readily available","Urinalysis, which is typically normal and helps distinguish from infection","Assessment of the contralateral testis given the frequently bilateral anatomical predisposition"],"redFlags":["Testicular torsion is a urological emergency; salvage depends on detorsion within roughly six hours, so exploration must not be delayed for investigations","Sudden severe scrotal pain with a high-riding testis and absent cremasteric reflex until proven otherwise","Neonatal torsion, which may present with a firm, discoloured, painless scrotal swelling","Progressive testicular ischaemia leading to non-viable testis and need for orchidectomy"],"prognosis":"Testicular viability depends critically on time to detorsion, with excellent salvage within a few hours and progressively poorer outcomes beyond six to twelve hours; a non-viable testis requires orchidectomy, and infarction may later affect fertility.","pitfalls":["Attributing acute scrotal pain to epididymo-orchitis and delaying surgical exploration","Waiting for or relying on ultrasound when clinical suspicion is high","Assuming a normal Doppler study excludes torsion, especially in intermittent or partial torsion","Failing to fix the contralateral testis, leaving it at risk of subsequent torsion","Dismissing intermittent testicular pain that may represent recurrent torsion-detorsion"],"management":["Undertake immediate surgical scrotal exploration and detorsion as a time-critical emergency, since salvage falls sharply with delay and imaging must not hold up surgery","Fix a viable testis with orchidopexy, or perform orchidectomy if the testis is non-viable at exploration","Fix the contralateral testis at the same operation, as the predisposing bell-clapper anomaly is frequently bilateral","Attempt manual detorsion with analgesia as a temporising measure only if definitive surgery will be delayed","Use colour Doppler ultrasound only when the diagnosis is genuinely uncertain and immediately available, never delaying exploration","Counsel on possible effects on fertility and, after orchidectomy, on cosmesis and prosthesis, with appropriate follow-up","Refer urgently to urology or surgery on clinical suspicion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"tetanus":{"name":"Tetanus","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lockjaw"],"chapter":157,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1230-1233","clinicalPearls":["Trismus (lockjaw) is typically the earliest symptom, progressing to neck and trunk rigidity, dysphagia, and pharyngeal/laryngeal spasm; risus sardonicus and opisthotonos reflect the characteristic sustained muscle rigidity.","Generalized tetanus is the most common form, characterized by muscle rigidity with generalized spasms; localized tetanus affects only isolated muscle groups but can progress to the generalized form.","Cephalic tetanus is a localized form involving the cranial nerves, which can cause pharyngeal or laryngeal spasm leading to aspiration, airway obstruction, or respiratory failure.","Neonatal tetanus classically presents with loss of the ability to suck and cry (a newborn normal in the first 2 days who deteriorates between days 3 and 28), poor feeding, rigidity, and generalized spasms.","Laryngeal spasm causing apnea or respiratory failure is a life-threatening event and, without immediate respiratory support, is the most common cause of death.","Autonomic nervous system disturbance characteristically emerges in the second week of severe disease, with fluctuating heart rate and blood pressure, sweating, gastrointestinal stasis, and increased tracheal secretions; cardiovascular events then become the major threat.","Disease severity is graded with the Ablett classification (Grades I-IV), where Grade IV is Grade III plus autonomic dysfunction.","Abdominal rigidity in tetanus is characteristically continuous, distinguishing it from the rigidity of peritonitis or an acute abdomen.","Roughly 20-30% of cases have no identifiable entry wound; superficial limb abrasions are the most common portal of entry in adults, while deeper injuries (open fractures, drug injection) are linked to more severe disease.","Natural infection does not confer protective immunity because tetanus toxin is poorly immunogenic, so survivors still require a full primary immunization course."],"pathophysiology":"Tetanus is caused by tetanus toxin (tetanospasmin) produced by Clostridium tetani, an anaerobic, gram-positive, spore-forming rod whose resilient environmental spores enter through wounds, abrasions, or the umbilical stump and germinate in anaerobic tissue. Only toxin-producing organisms cause disease. The toxin is a 150-kDa protein cleaved into a heavy (100-kDa) and light (50-kDa) chain joined by a disulfide bond; the heavy-chain carboxy terminal binds presynaptic motor nerve terminals, leading to internalization and retrograde intra-axonal transport to motor nuclei of cranial nerves and spinal cord ventral horns. The toxin then crosses the synapse into GABA-ergic presynaptic inhibitory interneurons, where the light chain (a zinc-dependent endopeptidase) cleaves VAMP2 (synaptobrevin), blocking inhibitory neurotransmitter release. Loss of inhibition produces unregulated motor activity (muscle rigidity and spasms), and similar disinhibition in the autonomic system produces autonomic instability; elevated circulating catecholamines in severe disease drive cardiovascular complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Strychnine poisoning","Dystonic reactions to antidopaminergic (antidopaminergic drug) agents","Stiff person syndrome","Somatic symptom disorder","Hypocalcemia (in neonatal tetanus)","Meningoencephalitis (in neonatal tetanus)","Oropharyngeal, peritonsillar, or submaxillary infection / lymphadenitis and cranial nerve disease (mimics of cephalic tetanus / trismus)","Intracranial hemorrhage","Peritonitis or acute abdominal emergency (versus continuous abdominal rigidity of tetanus)"],"infectionMimics":["Oropharyngeal infection","Peritonsillar infection","Submaxillary lymphadenitis","Meningoencephalitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Strychnine poisoning","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Dystonic reaction to antidopaminergic drugs","Stiff person syndrome","Somatic symptom disorder","Hypocalcemia","Intracranial hemorrhage"],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical; confirmatory laboratory testing is limited.","Wound culture of C. tetani cannot confirm the diagnosis (a positive culture is non-confirmatory).","Serum antitetanus IgG measured before antitoxin/immunoglobulin administration: levels >0.1 IU/mL (standard ELISA) are deemed protective and argue against tetanus.","Serum tetanus toxin bioassay may help if antibody is below the protective threshold, but a negative result does not exclude tetanus and the assay is not generally performed.","PCR and recombinase polymerase amplification to detect the tetanus neurotoxin gene are promising but emerging techniques."],"redFlags":["Laryngeal spasm with apnea or respiratory failure - life-threatening and the most common cause of death.","Autonomic nervous system disturbance (labile heart rate and blood pressure, sweating, GI stasis, increased tracheal secretions), typically in the second week, marking high cardiovascular mortality risk.","Short incubation period (wound to first symptom) and short period of onset (first symptom to first generalized/pharyngeal/laryngeal spasm) - rapid progression predicts worse outcome.","Dysphagia with laryngeal hyperactivity, which makes airway management difficult and raises aspiration risk."],"prognosis":"Tetanus has a high survival rate when complications are controlled and interventions are prompt, though recovery is prolonged, typically taking about 4-6 weeks. Rapid onset predicts more severe disease: the shorter the incubation period (wound to first symptom) and the period of onset (first symptom to first generalized or pharyngeal/laryngeal spasm), the worse the prognosis. The Tetanus Severity Score (TSS) outperforms the Dakar and Philips scores for predicting severity and outcome (sensitivity ~66%, specificity ~91%; a cutoff score of >=8 predicts death). Surviving adults, especially the elderly, may have reduced long-term functional outcomes, while affected children and neonates have a higher incidence of neurologic sequelae (neonates may face increased risk of learning disabilities, behavioral problems, cerebral palsy, and deafness).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis is clinical - waiting for laboratory confirmation is misguided; a positive wound culture does not confirm and a protective antitetanus IgG (>0.1 IU/mL) argues against the diagnosis.","Treatment should not be delayed once tetanus is clinically diagnosed.","Assuming natural infection confers immunity: tetanus toxin is poorly immunogenic, so survivors still require a full primary immunization course.","Overlooking absence of a wound: about 20-30% of cases have no identifiable entry site, so a negative wound history does not exclude tetanus.","Failure to identify and debride the entry wound (to remove anaerobic foci) can lead to recurrent or prolonged disease.","Long-term ICU care with deep sedation predisposes to hospital-acquired infections (ventilator-associated pneumonia, bloodstream and urinary tract infection, sepsis), venous thromboembolism, and other complications - prognosis depends on controlling these."],"management":["Neutralise circulating unbound toxin promptly with human tetanus immunoglobulin","Eradicate the organism with wound debridement and antibiotic therapy, metronidazole being the preferred agent","Control muscle rigidity and spasms with benzodiazepine sedation and adjuncts such as magnesium sulphate and muscle relaxants","Secure the airway and provide mechanical ventilation, often with neuromuscular blockade, for laryngeal spasm and respiratory failure","Manage the autonomic instability of severe disease with magnesium, sedation and alpha- and beta-adrenergic blockade","Nurse in a quiet environment that minimises stimuli, with intensive supportive care including nutrition and thromboprophylaxis","Administer tetanus toxoid to actively immunise during recovery, since natural infection does not confer immunity, and complete the vaccination schedule","Reinforce prevention through vaccination and appropriate wound tetanus prophylaxis in the community"],"severityClassification":"Ablett classification of tetanus severity: Grade I (mild) - mild trismus, general spasticity, no respiratory compromise, no spasms, no dysphagia; Grade II (moderate) - moderate trismus and rigidity, short spasms, mild dysphagia, moderate respiratory involvement, respiratory rate >30/min; Grade III (severe) - severe trismus, generalized rigidity, prolonged spasms, severe dysphagia, apneic spells, pulse >120/min, respiratory rate >40/min; Grade IV (very severe) - Grade III features plus autonomic dysfunction. Prognostic scoring is provided by the Tetanus Severity Score (TSS), which is superior to the Dakar and Philips scores.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1230-1233","Harrison 22e p.1233"],"crossLinks":[]},"tethered_cord_syndrome":{"name":"Tethered cord syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Spine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TCS","tethered spinal cord","cord tethering","filum terminale syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A stretch-induced disorder of the spinal cord, typically from a low-lying conus medullaris and/or a thickened, fatty, or tight filum terminale","Often associated with spinal dysraphism such as lipomyelomeningocele, dermal sinus, split-cord malformation, or a fatty filum","Cutaneous stigmata over the lower back (a hairy patch or hypertrichosis, a dimple or sinus, a lipoma, a haemangioma, or a skin tag) are important clues, especially in children","Children may present with delayed milestones, gait abnormality, foot deformity, scoliosis, limb or muscle asymmetry, and bladder dysfunction","Adults may present later with progressive back and leg pain, sensory and motor changes, and bladder, bowel, or sexual dysfunction, sometimes provoked by growth or activity","MRI typically shows the conus below the L1/2 level and/or a filum thicker than about 2 mm, often with a fatty filum","Urodynamic studies are valuable for detecting and monitoring neurogenic bladder involvement","Surgical detethering (release of the filum or untethering of the cord) aims to halt progression, and because established deficits may not fully reverse the timing of surgery is important"],"pathophysiology":"Tethered cord syndrome results from abnormal caudal fixation of the spinal cord so that it is placed under longitudinal traction. Normally the conus ascends to around the L1/2 level; when it is anchored, whether by a short, thick, or fatty filum terminale, a dysraphic lesion such as a lipoma, a dermal sinus tract, or a split-cord malformation, normal movement is restricted and the cord is stretched, particularly with growth and flexion. The resulting mechanical strain impairs neuronal metabolism and blood flow in the conus, producing progressive neurological, urological, and orthopaedic dysfunction, and overlying cutaneous stigmata reflect the associated dysraphism.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of neurogenic bladder in childhood","Cerebral palsy or another central cause of gait and limb abnormality","Lumbosacral disc or stenotic pathology in adults","Spinal cord tumour or syrinx","Peripheral neuropathy","Hereditary spastic paraplegia for progressive lower-limb signs"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the whole spine to show the conus level, filum thickness or fat, and any associated dysraphism","Urodynamic studies to assess and monitor bladder function","Examination and documentation of cutaneous stigmata over the spine","Neurophysiology such as lower-limb studies where indicated to characterise deficits","Plain radiographs or scoliosis imaging to assess associated bony deformity"],"redFlags":["Progressive neurological deterioration such as weakness, sensory loss, or worsening gait","New or worsening bladder or bowel dysfunction","Rapidly progressive scoliosis or foot deformity in a child with cutaneous stigmata"],"prognosis":"Detethering usually arrests progression and often relieves pain; motor and sensory recovery is variable and bladder dysfunction may only stabilise rather than reverse, so outcomes are best when surgery is performed before significant fixed deficits develop. Re-tethering can occur and require further surgery.","pitfalls":["Dismissing lumbosacral cutaneous stigmata that signal occult dysraphism","Delaying imaging in a child with unexplained gait, foot, or bladder problems","Expecting full reversal of long-standing deficits after detethering"],"management":["Microsurgical detethering — division of a tight or fatty filum terminale and untethering of the cord from any associated lipoma, dermal sinus or split-cord malformation — is the definitive treatment to relieve longitudinal traction","Operate before fixed neurological, urological or orthopaedic deficits develop, as this offers the best chance of arresting progression","Manage the neurogenic bladder with intermittent catheterisation and antimuscarinic therapy, guided by serial urodynamics","Symptomatic care with analgesia for back and leg pain, physiotherapy, and orthotic or surgical correction of foot deformity and scoliosis","Lifelong surveillance with periodic neurological examination, urodynamic studies and MRI to detect re-tethering","Multidisciplinary care coordinating neurosurgery, urology, orthopaedics and physiotherapy","Escalate promptly for repeat detethering if progressive motor, sensory or sphincter deterioration occurs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","Winn: Youmans and Winn Neurological Surgery"],"crossLinks":["thoracic_disc_herniation"]},"tetralogy_of_fallot":{"name":"Tetralogy of Fallot","system":"Paediatric cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TOF","ToF"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common cyanotic congenital heart lesion beyond infancy, comprising four features: a large ventricular septal defect, an overriding aorta, right ventricular outflow tract obstruction, and right ventricular hypertrophy.","The degree of cyanosis reflects the severity of right ventricular outflow obstruction; some infants are minimally cyanotic (so-called pink Fallot) while others present with marked desaturation.","A harsh systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border arises from the outflow obstruction, not from the septal defect itself; the murmur softens during a hypercyanotic spell as flow across the outflow tract falls.","Hypercyanotic (tet) spells are episodes of sudden, worsening cyanosis with agitation and deep breathing, often on waking or after feeding or crying, and can be life-threatening.","Calming manoeuvres for a spell aim to increase systemic vascular resistance and outflow through the lungs: comforting the child, bringing the knees to the chest, and giving oxygen, with pharmacological support as needed.","The chest radiograph classically shows a boot-shaped heart with a concave main pulmonary artery segment and normal or reduced pulmonary vascularity.","Echocardiography confirms the anatomy and defines the outflow tract, pulmonary arteries, and coronary artery course before surgery.","Older, unrepaired children may adopt squatting to relieve exertional cyanosis by transiently raising systemic resistance and pulmonary blood flow.","Complete surgical repair, involving closure of the septal defect and relief of outflow obstruction, is the definitive treatment and is usually performed in infancy."],"pathophysiology":"Anterior and cephalad deviation of the infundibular septum during development produces right ventricular outflow tract obstruction, a malaligned ventricular septal defect, and an aorta that overrides the defect. Obstruction to pulmonary outflow, combined with the large communication between the ventricles, allows deoxygenated right ventricular blood to shunt right-to-left into the aorta, producing systemic desaturation. The volume of pulmonary blood flow and the resulting cyanosis depend dynamically on the balance between the fixed and dynamic outflow obstruction and systemic vascular resistance; a fall in systemic resistance or a spasm of the infundibulum increases right-to-left shunting and precipitates a hypercyanotic spell.","additionalDifferentials":["Transposition of the great arteries","Tricuspid atresia","Pulmonary atresia with ventricular septal defect","Truncus arteriosus","Severe isolated pulmonary stenosis","Double-outlet right ventricle"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to define the outflow tract, septal defect, aortic override, and pulmonary artery anatomy","Chest radiograph looking for a boot-shaped heart and pulmonary vascularity","Electrocardiogram showing right axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy","Pulse oximetry and, in spells, assessment of acidosis","Cardiac catheterisation or CT/MR angiography when coronary or pulmonary artery anatomy is unclear","Genetic evaluation for 22q11.2 deletion when associated features are present"],"redFlags":["Hypercyanotic (tet) spell with deepening cyanosis, agitation, and a disappearing murmur","Loss of consciousness, seizures, or limpness during a spell","Duct-dependent presentation with severe outflow obstruction and cardiovascular collapse as the ductus closes","Profound or worsening hypoxaemia unresponsive to calming measures","Metabolic acidosis indicating inadequate pulmonary blood flow"],"prognosis":"With timely complete surgical repair the long-term outlook is generally good, though survivors need lifelong cardiology follow-up for pulmonary regurgitation, residual outflow obstruction, arrhythmia, and possible need for pulmonary valve replacement. Untreated disease carries high morbidity and mortality from progressive cyanosis, spells, and complications.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a softening or absent murmur reassuringly when it actually signals a severe hypercyanotic spell","Aggressively sedating or agitating an infant in a spell rather than using calming, resistance-raising manoeuvres","Overlooking a duct-dependent lesion in a neonate whose cyanosis worsens as the ductus arteriosus closes","Attributing exertional symptoms or squatting in an older child to behaviour rather than underlying cyanotic heart disease","Failing to screen for associated 22q11.2 deletion and other anomalies"],"management":["Complete surgical repair — closure of the ventricular septal defect and relief of right ventricular outflow tract obstruction — is the definitive treatment, with a palliative systemic-to-pulmonary (Blalock-Taussig) shunt in neonates unsuitable for early repair","In duct-dependent presentation maintain ductal patency with a prostaglandin infusion until intervention","Abort a hypercyanotic spell by placing the child knee-to-chest, giving oxygen, opioid sedation and fluids, and a vasopressor (phenylephrine) or beta-blocker to raise systemic resistance and relax the infundibulum, correcting any metabolic acidosis","Prophylactic beta-blockade may reduce infundibular spasm and the frequency of spells while awaiting repair","Lifelong cardiology surveillance with echocardiography for pulmonary regurgitation, residual outflow obstruction and arrhythmia","Timely pulmonary valve replacement for significant pulmonary regurgitation, with electrophysiological assessment for ventricular arrhythmia","Infective endocarditis prophylaxis and coordinated transition from paediatric to adult congenital cardiology follow-up","Genetic testing and counselling for 22q11.2 deletion when associated features are present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"tetrasomy_18p":{"name":"Tetrasomy 18p","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["isochromosome 18p","i(18p) syndrome","tetrasomy 18p syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by a supernumerary isochromosome made up of two short arms of chromosome 18, giving four copies of 18p","Presents with developmental delay or intellectual disability, usually moderate, with frequent speech delay","Neonatal feeding difficulties and hypotonia, sometimes later evolving into spasticity, are common","Microcephaly and characteristic facial features such as a narrow or oval face, strabismus, and a high nasal bridge are typical","Behavioural difficulties and, in some, a movement disorder or seizures may occur","Growth restriction and low birth weight are frequent","The isochromosome usually arises de novo, most often from a maternal meiotic error, so recurrence risk is low but not zero"],"pathophysiology":"Tetrasomy 18p results from a supernumerary marker chromosome that is an isochromosome of the short arm of chromosome 18, so that four copies of 18p are present, comprising the two normal chromosome 18s plus the two arms of the isochromosome. The extra dosage of genes on 18p disturbs neurodevelopment and growth, producing the characteristic pattern of intellectual disability, hypotonia evolving to spasticity, and dysmorphism. It generally occurs sporadically through a meiotic or post-meiotic error, commonly of maternal origin.","additionalDifferentials":["Other supernumerary marker chromosome syndromes","18p deletion syndrome, which reflects the opposite dosage change","Cerebral palsy, given the tone abnormalities","Non-specific syndromic intellectual disability","Other causes of neonatal hypotonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype to identify the marker chromosome","FISH or microarray to confirm the marker is derived from 18p","Chromosomal microarray to characterise copy number","MRI of the brain if seizures or a movement disorder are present","Developmental and speech and language assessment","Feeding and swallow evaluation in infancy"],"redFlags":["Severe neonatal feeding difficulty with failure to thrive","Seizures","Progressive spasticity affecting mobility"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is generally not markedly reduced; most individuals have moderate intellectual disability with ongoing motor and speech difficulties, and outcome depends on the severity of neurological involvement.","pitfalls":["Reporting a marker chromosome without identifying its origin, which requires FISH or microarray","Misattributing tone abnormalities to cerebral palsy without cytogenetic testing","Overlooking spasticity that evolves after initial hypotonia","Assuming a high recurrence risk when most cases are de novo"],"management":["No disease-specific therapy exists; management centres on early multidisciplinary developmental support with physiotherapy, occupational therapy and speech and language therapy","Manage evolving spasticity with physiotherapy, orthoses and, where needed, botulinum toxin or an antispasticity agent such as baclofen","Support neonatal feeding difficulties with swallow assessment, nutritional optimisation and tube feeding if required","Treat seizures, when present, with appropriate antiepileptic therapy and correlate with neuroimaging","Ophthalmology referral for strabismus and behavioural support for associated difficulties","Ongoing surveillance of growth, development and neurological status through paediatric and community teams","Genetic counselling: the isochromosome usually arises de novo with low recurrence risk, but family evaluation and karyotype interpretation should be offered"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Gardner and Sutherland's Chromosome Abnormalities and Genetic Counseling"],"crossLinks":[]},"tetrodotoxin_poisoning":{"name":"Tetrodotoxin (pufferfish) poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pufferfish poisoning","fugu poisoning","TTX poisoning","tetraodon poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / WHO envenoming guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Follows ingestion of pufferfish (fugu) or other tetrodotoxin-containing seafood, especially when improperly prepared","Symptoms begin within minutes to a few hours with perioral and lingual paraesthesiae, then spread to the face and extremities","Progresses to nausea, vomiting, ascending flaccid paralysis, bulbar weakness and respiratory failure in severe cases","Consciousness is usually preserved even in advanced paralysis, as the toxin has limited central effect","There is no antidote; management is supportive with airway protection and mechanical ventilation until the toxin clears","Early gastrointestinal decontamination may be considered soon after ingestion, and cardiovascular support given for hypotension and bradyarrhythmia","Severity grading based on progression of paraesthesiae, weakness and cardiorespiratory signs helps guide monitoring and disposition"],"pathophysiology":"Tetrodotoxin is a potent, heat-stable toxin concentrated in the liver, gonads, skin and viscera of pufferfish and produced by symbiotic bacteria. It binds with high affinity to voltage-gated sodium channels on nerve and skeletal muscle membranes, blocking sodium influx and preventing action potential generation and propagation. The result is a rapidly progressive sensory disturbance and flaccid paralysis, including of the respiratory muscles, with respiratory failure the main cause of death. Autonomic effects can cause hypotension and bradycardia, while relative sparing of the central nervous system leaves consciousness intact.","additionalDifferentials":["Paralytic shellfish (saxitoxin) poisoning","Blue-ringed octopus envenomation","Guillain-Barre syndrome or botulism","Ciguatera poisoning (overlapping paraesthesiae)","Myasthenic crisis","Other cause of acute neuromuscular respiratory failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on ingestion history and characteristic progression","Continuous respiratory monitoring and serial forced vital capacity","Pulse oximetry and arterial blood gases for ventilatory failure","Electrocardiography and cardiac monitoring for bradyarrhythmia","Electrolytes and baseline bloods to exclude alternative causes","Toxin detection in food or biological samples where laboratory capability exists"],"redFlags":["Spreading paraesthesiae with progressive limb and bulbar weakness","Declining respiratory effort or oxygen saturation","Hypotension or significant bradycardia","Rapid symptom onset after ingestion (suggesting a large dose)","Preserved awareness with advancing paralysis heralding respiratory arrest"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on timely airway and ventilatory support; patients who survive the first hours with adequate ventilation generally recover fully within days, whereas untreated respiratory failure is rapidly fatal.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the speed of progression from paraesthesiae to respiratory failure","Assuming immobility reflects unconsciousness, when awareness is usually retained","Waiting for an antidote instead of instituting supportive ventilation","Failing to recognise the diagnosis in the absence of a clear pufferfish history"],"management":["There is no antidote; the mainstay is meticulous airway protection with early intubation and mechanical ventilation, as respiratory-muscle paralysis is the main cause of death","Consider early gastrointestinal decontamination with activated charcoal soon after ingestion","Treat autonomic instability with intravenous fluids and vasopressors for hypotension and atropine for symptomatic bradycardia","Continuous respiratory and cardiac monitoring with serial forced vital capacity to detect deteriorating ventilation","Provide supportive intensive care until the toxin clears, with full recovery expected in survivors who are adequately ventilated","Manage in a critical care setting with early escalation for ventilatory failure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","WHO/food-safety guidance on marine biotoxins"],"crossLinks":[]},"thallium_poisoning":{"name":"Thallium poisoning","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thallium toxicity","thallotoxicosis","thallium salt poisoning","rodenticide thallium poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic triad is gastrointestinal upset, a painful ascending sensory-predominant peripheral neuropathy and, later, alopecia","Alopecia is a hallmark but is delayed by one to several weeks, so early diagnosis rests on the painful neuropathy and gastrointestinal symptoms","Consider deliberate poisoning, as thallium is odourless and tasteless and has been used for homicidal and suicidal intent","Autonomic features, tachycardia, hypertension and constipation, together with painful acral dysaesthesiae, are useful early clues","Mees' lines (transverse white bands) may appear on the nails weeks after exposure","Diagnosis is confirmed by elevated urine (and blood) thallium concentrations, ideally with a 24-hour urine collection","Prussian blue is the specific therapy, binding thallium in the gut to interrupt enterohepatic and enteroenteric recirculation and enhance faecal elimination, alongside supportive care","Multi-dose oral binding, generous bowel regimen and, in severe cases, extracorporeal removal are considered under poisons-centre guidance"],"pathophysiology":"Thallium is a heavy metal whose monovalent ion mimics potassium, allowing it to enter cells through potassium transport pathways and disrupt potassium-dependent processes. It interferes with mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation, binds sulfhydryl groups on enzymes and structural proteins, and disrupts ribonucleic acid and protein synthesis. High metabolic demand tissues, including neurons and the actively dividing cells of hair follicles, are especially vulnerable, explaining the prominent peripheral neuropathy and characteristic delayed alopecia. Thallium undergoes enterohepatic recirculation, which sustains systemic exposure.","additionalDifferentials":["Guillain-Barre syndrome or other acute polyneuropathy","Arsenic or other heavy-metal poisoning","Vasculitic or paraneoplastic neuropathy","Vitamin deficiency neuropathy (e.g. thiamine)","Porphyria","Other causes of alopecia such as chemotherapy or autoimmune disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Twenty-four-hour urinary thallium concentration and blood thallium level","Nerve conduction studies demonstrating axonal sensorimotor neuropathy","Full blood count, renal and liver function and electrolytes","Electrocardiogram for conduction disturbance and repolarisation changes","Abdominal radiograph, as some thallium salts are radio-opaque","Examination of nails and hair for Mees' lines and follicular changes"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive peripheral neuropathy or ascending weakness","Respiratory muscle involvement or bulbar dysfunction","Seizures, encephalopathy or coma","Cardiac arrhythmia or autonomic instability","Evidence of deliberate poisoning requiring safeguarding and forensic involvement"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on dose and timeliness of chelation; mild cases may recover fully, but severe poisoning can leave permanent neurological deficits or be fatal, and hair usually regrows in survivors over subsequent months.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis before alopecia develops and mislabelling it as Guillain-Barre syndrome","Failing to consider intentional poisoning and to involve safeguarding or forensic services","Not sending appropriate urine and blood specimens for heavy-metal analysis","Overlooking enterohepatic recirculation and stopping binding therapy prematurely","Attributing painful paraesthesiae to a psychiatric or functional cause"],"management":["Give oral Prussian blue (insoluble ferric hexacyanoferrate), which binds thallium in the gut and interrupts its enterohepatic recirculation to enhance faecal elimination","Consider multi-dose activated charcoal, and early haemodialysis in severe poisoning to augment clearance","Provide supportive care with fluids, correction of electrolyte disturbance and neuropathic analgesia for the painful neuropathy","Remove and secure the source of exposure, and involve safeguarding or the authorities where deliberate poisoning is suspected","Continuous cardiac monitoring for conduction and repolarisation disturbances","Serial urinary thallium concentrations to monitor the response to decontamination and binding therapy","Neurological rehabilitation and multidisciplinary follow-up, as severe cases may leave permanent deficits"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","UpToDate: Thallium poisoning"],"crossLinks":[]},"thanatophoric_dysplasia":{"name":"Thanatophoric dysplasia","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FGFR3","TD","TD type I","TD type II"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common lethal skeletal dysplasia, typically incompatible with survival beyond the neonatal period because of a severely small thorax and pulmonary hypoplasia","Caused by activating variants in FGFR3 that produce far stronger receptor signalling than achondroplasia, arising almost always de novo in an autosomal dominant manner","Features include extreme micromelia, a narrow bell-shaped thorax, a large head with frontal bossing and depressed nasal bridge, and redundant skin folds on the limbs","Type I is characterised by curved, short femora resembling telephone receivers with a normally shaped skull, whereas type II features straight femora with a cloverleaf skull deformity","Radiographs show platyspondyly with markedly flattened vertebrae, short ribs, small squared iliac bones, and short curved or straight long bones","Prenatal ultrasound often detects severe limb shortening, a small chest, and polyhydramnios, prompting molecular confirmation and genetic counselling","Management is directed toward confirming the diagnosis, counselling, and compassionate perinatal care given the lethal prognosis"],"pathophysiology":"Thanatophoric dysplasia is caused by strongly activating variants in FGFR3. The resulting profound constitutive receptor signalling severely disrupts growth-plate chondrocyte proliferation and endochondral ossification, producing extreme limb shortening and a markedly hypoplastic thoracic cage. The small thorax restricts lung development, and the consequent pulmonary hypoplasia is the usual cause of neonatal death. The same signalling excess also affects cortical brain development in some cases, contributing to the cloverleaf skull and neurological abnormalities seen particularly in type II.","additionalDifferentials":["Homozygous achondroplasia","Achondrogenesis","Osteogenesis imperfecta type II","Short-rib polydactyly syndromes","Campomelic dysplasia","Hypophosphatasia (severe perinatal form)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Prenatal ultrasound and, where available, fetal MRI to characterise limb, thorax, and skull findings","Postnatal skeletal survey demonstrating platyspondyly, short ribs, and characteristic femoral shape","FGFR3 molecular testing to confirm the diagnosis and subtype","Fetal or neonatal autopsy with placental examination when consent is given","Genetic counselling with discussion of recurrence risk and prenatal options","Neuroimaging where cloverleaf skull or neurological abnormality is present"],"redFlags":["Severe respiratory failure at birth from pulmonary hypoplasia","Prenatal detection of a severely constricted thorax and extreme micromelia indicating a likely lethal dysplasia","Cloverleaf skull with signs of raised intracranial pressure","Feeding and airway compromise requiring urgent palliative planning"],"prognosis":"Thanatophoric dysplasia is almost universally lethal in the perinatal period, with most affected infants dying shortly after birth from respiratory failure. Rare survivors with intensive respiratory support have severe disability and profound growth restriction.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with severe achondroplasia or homozygous achondroplasia without molecular confirmation","Underestimating the lethality and delaying appropriate counselling and palliative planning","Missing the distinction between type I and type II femoral and skull morphology","Failing to offer autopsy and genetic counselling to inform recurrence risk"],"management":["Confirm the diagnosis with FGFR3 molecular testing and characteristic prenatal and postnatal imaging to inform prognosis and counselling","Given near-universal perinatal lethality from pulmonary hypoplasia, the mainstay is compassionate perinatal palliative and comfort care with clear goals-of-care discussions","Offer non-directive genetic counselling with discussion of prognosis and, antenatally, the option of termination","Plan the delivery and birth setting with the family, agreeing ceilings of care and avoiding futile intervention","For the rare survivor pursuing active support, manage respiratory failure with ventilatory support and coordinate complex disability care","Provide bereavement support and offer autopsy with placental examination where consent is given","Genetic counselling on recurrence: variants are almost always de novo, but rare parental germline mosaicism means prenatal testing can be offered in future pregnancies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"the_bradyarrhythmias_disorders_of_the":{"name":"The Bradyarrhythmias: Disorders of the Atrioventricular Node","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AV block","Atrioventricular block","AV conduction block","Heart block","Bradyarrhythmia due to AV node disease"],"chapter":252,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1924","clinicalPearls":["The AV node is specialized for slow conduction, creating a delay between atrial and ventricular activation and acting as the electrical gatekeeper to the ventricle; its intrinsic backup pacemaker (escape) rate is roughly 20-60 beats/min, normally overridden by the faster sinus node (60-100 beats/min).","First-degree AV block means every atrial impulse still conducts but with an abnormally prolonged PR interval (>200 ms); the term block is a misnomer since conduction is delayed, not interrupted.","Mobitz I (Wenckebach) second-degree block shows progressive PR lengthening before a dropped beat with grouped beating and progressive RR shortening; it is typically within the AV node, hemodynamically stable, and usually does not warrant pacing when asymptomatic.","Mobitz II second-degree block shows a fixed PR interval before a dropped beat, is usually infranodal with a wider QRS, carries a risk of sudden death from an unreliable escape rhythm, and warrants permanent pacing even without symptoms.","In 2:1 AV block the ECG cannot reliably distinguish type I from type II; a PR <160 ms with a wide QRS before the conducted beat favors infranodal (type II) block.","QRS width of the escape rhythm localizes the block: a narrow QRS escape implies block in the AV node or proximal His with a faster, more stable junctional rhythm, whereas a wide QRS escape (without preexisting bundle branch block) implies distal His or bundle branch block and a slower, less reliable rhythm.","Vagal maneuvers and carotid sinus massage slow AV nodal conduction but may paradoxically improve infranodal conduction, while atropine, isoproterenol, and exercise improve AV nodal conduction but may worsen infranodal conduction - useful for localizing the level of block.","On the His bundle electrogram, the AH interval (AV nodal conduction) is normally <130 ms and the HV interval (His-Purkinje conduction) is normally <=55 ms; a markedly prolonged HV interval predicts progression to higher-grade block.","Functional causes of AV block (autonomic, metabolic/endocrine, drug-related) tend to be reversible, whereas most structural causes producing fibrosis are permanent.","Congenital complete heart block with a narrow QRS typically increases heart rate with exercise, whereas acquired complete heart block (especially wide QRS) does not respond to exercise.","Acquired persistent AV conduction failure is rare in healthy adults (estimated incidence about 1 per 5000 per year in the U.S.), but becomes much more common with ischemia, aging/fibrosis, or infiltrative disease.","Idiopathic progressive fibrosis/sclerosis of the conduction system is the most common acquired cause, accounting for roughly 50% of AV block; it often begins in the fourth decade and is accelerated by atherosclerosis, hypertension, and diabetes."],"pathophysiology":"AV nodal myocytes sit at a relatively high resting membrane potential (~-60 mV), exhibit slow phase-0 upstrokes mediated by L-type calcium current (with a relative lack of fast sodium current), and show spontaneous phase-4 depolarization, conferring backup automaticity. Weak electrical coupling from sparse gap junctions (predominantly connexin-40) plus increased extracellular volume slows conduction and protects the ventricle. The AV junction comprises a transitional (atrionodal) cell zone showing decremental conduction, the compact node, and the penetrating His bundle; conduction continues through the His, right and left bundle branches, and the fast-conducting Purkinje network (1-3 m/s). Bradycardia and block arise when conduction across this axis is compromised by fibrosis/sclerosis, calcification (Lev, Lenegre disease), ischemia/infarction, infiltrative or inflammatory disease, drugs, heightened vagal tone, or congenital/genetic abnormalities; consequences range from fatigue and syncope to asystolic death if no reliable escape emerges. Functional (autonomic/metabolic/drug) causes are often reversible while structural fibrosis is typically permanent.","additionalDifferentials":["Sinus node dysfunction / sinus bradycardia (sick sinus syndrome) as a competing cause of symptomatic bradycardia","Drug effect (beta blockers, verapamil, diltiazem, digoxin) producing reversible AV conduction slowing","Heightened vagal tone (sleep, well-conditioned athletes, carotid sinus hypersensitivity, vasovagal/cough/micturition syncope)","Acute myocardial ischemia/infarction-related conduction block","Infiltrative cardiomyopathy (sarcoidosis, amyloidosis, hemochromatosis)","Congenital complete heart block (e.g., maternal lupus)"],"infectionMimics":["Lyme carditis (Borrelia burgdorferi) - a common infectious cause of AV block, typically AV-nodal with narrow junctional escape >40 beats/min, usually improving within about a week of antibiotic therapy","Infective endocarditis with perivalvular abscess - AV block here should prompt urgent surgical consultation","Viral myocarditis","Chagas disease","Toxoplasmosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Senile degenerative fibrosis/calcification of the conduction system (Lev disease)","Lenegre disease (sclerodegenerative distal bundle branch disease in younger patients)","Aortic (and less commonly mitral) valve annular calcification encroaching on the conduction system","Cardiac sarcoidosis and amyloidosis","Autoimmune/rheumatologic disease (SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, mixed connective tissue disease, scleroderma)","Neuromuscular disease (myotonic dystrophy, Kearns-Sayre syndrome, muscular dystrophies)","Iatrogenic injury (valve/cardiac surgery, TAVR, septal myectomy/alcohol septal ablation, catheter ablation)","Hereditary progressive familial heart block (e.g., SCN5A mutations) and lamin A/C cardiomyopathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG to classify the degree and pattern of block and assess QRS width/escape rhythm","Transthoracic echocardiography to assess for structural/valvular disease, ejection fraction, and wall motion","Advanced imaging (cardiac MRI, CT, FDG-PET, technetium pyrophosphate scanning) for infiltrative disease, especially when block develops at age <=60 years","Ambulatory/continuous ECG monitoring to correlate symptoms with bradyarrhythmia","Provocative testing - vagal maneuvers, carotid sinus massage, exercise, atropine, isoproterenol - to localize the level of block","Invasive electrophysiologic study with His bundle electrogram (AH and HV interval measurement) in syncope with suspected high-grade or infranodal block","Evaluation for ischemia when clinical suspicion is present"],"redFlags":["Wide-complex escape rhythm at a slow rate (e.g., ~30 beats/min) implying infranodal block and an unreliable escape","Mobitz II second-degree or complete (third-degree) heart block","Syncope or presyncope attributable to bradycardia","Markedly prolonged HV interval (>100 ms carries an annual incidence of complete AV block approaching 10%)","High-grade AV block complicating anterior MI (distal block, wide unstable escape, high mortality)","New AV block with infective endocarditis suggesting perivalvular abscess","Alternating bundle branch block or bifascicular/trifascicular block with intermittent third-degree block"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends heavily on the level of block. AV-nodal (proximal) block - including Mobitz I and most inferior-MI-related block - tends to have a faster, stable narrow-complex escape and is often reversible with a favorable outcome. Infranodal (distal His-Purkinje) block, Mobitz II, and high-grade block in anterior MI carry unstable wide-complex escape rhythms, higher mortality, and a risk of sudden asystolic death, so pacing is indicated even when asymptomatic. Functional/reversible causes generally resolve; structural fibrotic disease is usually permanent and progressive.","pitfalls":["Mislabeling 2:1 AV block as type I when it may be infranodal type II - the surface ECG cannot reliably distinguish them","Assuming all AV block is benign vagal phenomenon and missing serious infranodal disease that warrants pacing despite absent symptoms","Overlooking reversible/treatable causes (offending drugs, electrolyte derangement, ischemia, hypothyroidism, sleep apnea, Lyme carditis) before committing to permanent therapy","Failing to pursue advanced imaging for infiltrative disease (e.g., sarcoidosis) when AV block presents at a younger age (<=60 years)","Misinterpreting Mobitz I at very short paced cycle lengths as pathologic - it can be a normal physiologic response; it is abnormal at cycle lengths >500 ms (<120 beats/min) without high vagal tone","Not recognizing that a new perivalvular abscess in endocarditis can present as AV block requiring urgent surgery"],"management":["Identify and reverse treatable causes first — withdraw AV-nodal blocking agents (beta-blockers, non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers, digoxin), treat ischaemia, correct electrolyte disturbance and hypothyroidism, and treat Lyme carditis or infiltrative disease","For acute symptomatic bradycardia give atropine and, if inadequate, a chronotrope such as isoprenaline while arranging temporary transcutaneous or transvenous pacing","Implant a permanent pacemaker for symptomatic AV block, and for Mobitz II or high-grade/complete infranodal block even when asymptomatic given the unreliable escape rhythm","Observe asymptomatic first-degree and Mobitz I (Wenckebach) block, which are usually nodal and stable and do not require pacing","Consider conduction-system (His-bundle or left-bundle-branch) pacing or biventricular pacing where a high ventricular-pacing burden or impaired function is anticipated","Investigate for underlying structural or infiltrative disease with echocardiography and advanced imaging, particularly when block occurs in younger patients","Refer to electrophysiology for device implantation and for His-bundle electrogram assessment in unexplained syncope with suspected infranodal block"],"severityClassification":"Electrocardiographic grading: First-degree AV block - all atrial impulses conduct but PR is prolonged (>200 ms), delay usually within the AV node. Second-degree AV block - intermittent failure of AV conduction; Type I/Mobitz I/Wenckebach (progressive PR prolongation until a dropped beat, usually AV-nodal) and Type II/Mobitz II (fixed PR before a dropped beat, usually infranodal, often with wide QRS). Third-degree (complete) AV block - complete AV dissociation with a ventricular (escape) rate slower than the atrial rate.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1924","Harrison 22e p.1925","Harrison 22e p.1926","Harrison 22e p.1927","Harrison 22e p.1928","Harrison 22e p.1929","Harrison 22e p.1930"],"crossLinks":[]},"the_hyperbilirubinemias":{"name":"The Hyperbilirubinemias","system":"Hepatobiliary / Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Gilbert syndrome","Crigler-Najjar syndrome","Dubin-Johnson syndrome","Rotor syndrome","unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","conjugated hyperbilirubinemia","inherited hyperbilirubinemias"],"chapter":349,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2637","clinicalPearls":["Bilirubin is the terminal product of heme catabolism, with roughly 70-90% derived from breakdown of hemoglobin in senescent red cells; hepatic handling involves four linked steps: carrier-mediated uptake, intracellular binding to glutathione-S-transferases (ligandins), conjugation by UGT1A1, and canalicular excretion (Harrison 22e p.2637-2638).","Conjugation by UGT1A1 adds one or two glucuronic acid moieties, disrupting internal hydrogen bonding so the resulting glucuronides become water-soluble; conjugation is obligatory for biliary excretion (Harrison 22e p.2638).","Unconjugated bilirubin is too tightly albumin-bound to be filtered and does not appear in urine, whereas conjugated (water-soluble) bilirubin is readily filtered, so bilirubinuria indicates a conjugated (direct) hyperbilirubinemia; the kidney acts as an overflow valve so renal failure can amplify jaundice in conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (Harrison 22e p.2638).","Isolated hemolysis in an otherwise healthy person produces a purely unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia with direct fraction <=15% of total; because marrow erythropoiesis can rise only about eightfold, hemolysis alone rarely sustains a bilirubin above ~68 micromol/L (4 mg/dL), so higher values imply concomitant hepatic dysfunction (Harrison 22e p.2638-2639).","Gilbert syndrome is a common, benign cause of mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (usually <51 micromol/L, <3 mg/dL) with normal liver tests and histology; levels rise with fasting, stress, fatigue, alcohol, and intercurrent illness and fall with caloric intake or enzyme inducers (Harrison 22e p.2640).","Crigler-Najjar type I shows extreme lifelong unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (~340-765 micromol/L, 20-45 mg/dL) with essentially absent UGT1A1 activity, no response to phenobarbital, and high kernicterus risk; type II has lower levels, detectable enzyme, pigmented bile, and a >25% bilirubin fall with phenobarbital (Harrison 22e p.2639-2640).","Dubin-Johnson syndrome is a benign, autosomal recessive predominantly conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (typically 34-85 micromol/L) caused by mutation of the canalicular transporter MRP2 (ABCC2); the liver is grossly black from lysosomal pigment and urinary coproporphyrin is >=80% isomer I despite a normal total (Harrison 22e p.2640-2641).","Rotor syndrome resembles Dubin-Johnson clinically (conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, bilirubinuria) but the liver is normal in color, the gallbladder is visualized on oral cholecystography, and total urinary coproporphyrin is markedly increased with isomer I usually <70%; it results from combined deficiency of OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 (Harrison 22e p.2641-2642).","Most neonates develop mild physiologic unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia between days 2 and 5 (peak typically <85-170 micromol/L, 5-10 mg/dL) due to low UGT1A1 and enterohepatic recycling, resolving within about 2 weeks (Harrison 22e p.2639).","A rapidly rising neonatal unconjugated bilirubin, or absolute levels above ~340 micromol/L (20 mg/dL), risks bilirubin encephalopathy (kernicterus) as pigment crosses an immature blood-brain barrier and deposits in the basal ganglia (Harrison 22e p.2639).","Acquired liver disease (acute hepatitis, common bile duct stone) generally produces a mixed conjugated and unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, and serum levels or fractions cannot reliably distinguish intrahepatic from extrahepatic obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2640).","Gilbert syndrome impairs glucuronidation of the irinotecan (CPT-11) active metabolite SN-38, predisposing to severe diarrhea and myelosuppression; the HIV protease inhibitors indinavir and atazanavir inhibit UGT1A1 and worsen hyperbilirubinemia, most markedly in preexisting Gilbert syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2640)."],"pathophysiology":"Bilirubin, the end product of heme degradation (chiefly from senescent erythrocyte hemoglobin), circulates tightly bound to albumin and is processed by hepatocytes in four steps: carrier-mediated uptake, intracellular binding to glutathione-S-transferases, conjugation with glucuronic acid by UGT1A1 (the physiologically critical isoform encoded by exon A1 plus shared exons 2-5 of the UGT1 gene complex on chromosome 2), and ATP-dependent canalicular excretion of mono- and diglucuronides via MRP2. A portion of glucuronides is also exported into the portal circulation by MRP3 and recaptured by sinusoidal OATP1B1/OATP1B3. Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia arises from overproduction (hemolysis, ineffective erythropoiesis, resorption of large hematomas/infarcts), decreased hepatic uptake (drugs such as rifampin, novobiocin; contributory in Gilbert syndrome), or impaired conjugation (neonatal immaturity, breast-milk jaundice, drug inhibition of UGT1A1, and hereditary UGT1A1 defects of Crigler-Najjar I/II and Gilbert syndrome). Gilbert syndrome most often reflects a homozygous extra TA insertion (A[TA]7TAA rather than A[TA]6TAA) in the UGT1A1 promoter reducing enzyme expression; Crigler-Najjar reflects coding/structural UGT1 mutations causing absent (type I) or markedly reduced (type II) activity. Predominantly conjugated familial hyperbilirubinemias result from defective canalicular excretion: Dubin-Johnson from MRP2 (ABCC2) mutation and Rotor from combined OATP1B1/OATP1B3 deficiency impairing reuptake of refluxed conjugated bilirubin.","additionalDifferentials":["Hemolytic anemias (hereditary spherocytosis, G6PD deficiency, beta-thalassemia) causing unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","Ineffective erythropoiesis (thalassemia major, megaloblastic anemia, congenital dyserythropoietic anemias, lead poisoning)","Drug-induced unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (rifampin, novobiocin, atazanavir, indinavir, chloramphenicol, gentamicin)","Physiologic, breast-milk, and Lucey-Driscoll neonatal jaundice","Acquired hepatocellular or cholestatic liver disease (acute viral hepatitis, common bile duct stone) with mixed hyperbilirubinemia","Benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis (BRIC) and progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis (PFIC types 1-3)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute viral hepatitis, which the first cholestatic episodes of benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis may mimic"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Extrahepatic biliary obstruction (e.g., choledocholithiasis) presenting with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia","Intrahepatic cholestatic injury","Resorbing large hematoma or massive tissue infarction causing transient unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fractionation of serum bilirubin into conjugated (direct) and unconjugated (indirect) components","Urinalysis for bilirubinuria (present in conjugated, absent in unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia)","Standard liver biochemistries (aminotransferases, alkaline phosphatase, albumin, prothrombin time) and hemolysis workup (reticulocytes, haptoglobin)","Urinary coproporphyrin isomer analysis to distinguish Dubin-Johnson (>=80% isomer I, normal total) from Rotor (markedly increased total, isomer I usually <70%)","Phenobarbital challenge to separate Crigler-Najjar type II (>25% fall) from type I (no response)","UGT1A1 genotyping (e.g., A[TA]7TAA promoter for Gilbert syndrome); oral cholecystography (gallbladder non-visualized in Dubin-Johnson, visualized in Rotor)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising neonatal unconjugated bilirubin or absolute level above ~340 micromol/L (20 mg/dL) signaling risk of kernicterus","Sustained unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia above ~68 micromol/L (4 mg/dL) attributed to hemolysis, implying additional hepatic dysfunction","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with bilirubinuria, prompting evaluation for hepatocellular or obstructive disease","Intercurrent febrile illness in Crigler-Najjar that can precipitate late-onset bilirubin encephalopathy","Prolonged, debilitating cholestatic attacks in BRIC and progression of PFIC to end-stage liver disease"],"prognosis":"Gilbert, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes are benign with normal life expectancy and no progression to chronic liver disease. Crigler-Najjar type I carries a grave prognosis: before phototherapy most patients died of kernicterus in infancy or early childhood, and even survivors developed bilirubin-induced neurologic dysfunction, with early liver transplantation the best means to prevent brain injury and death. Crigler-Najjar type II is far less often complicated by kernicterus and is generally compatible with normal life, though intercurrent illness, fasting, or low albumin can precipitate dangerous bilirubin elevations. BRIC is benign and does not cause cirrhosis, whereas PFIC progresses to end-stage liver disease in childhood.","pitfalls":["Assuming hemolysis alone explains a markedly elevated bilirubin; sustained levels above ~68 micromol/L (4 mg/dL) imply coexisting hepatic dysfunction","Using serum bilirubin levels or conjugated/unconjugated proportions to differentiate intrahepatic from extrahepatic causes of jaundice, which is not reliable","Overdiagnosing Gilbert syndrome from a single value, since levels fluctuate widely and at least 25% of patients show transiently normal bilirubin on follow-up","Male predominance in Gilbert syndrome may be artifactual, reflecting use of male-derived reference ranges","Mistaking the first episodes of benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis for acute viral hepatitis","Prescribing drugs cleared by glucuronidation (notably irinotecan) without caution in Gilbert syndrome given risk of severe toxicity"],"management":["Reassurance without treatment for the benign syndromes (Gilbert, Dubin-Johnson and Rotor), advising avoidance of precipitants such as fasting, dehydration and intercurrent-illness stress in Gilbert syndrome","Induce residual UGT1A1 activity with phenobarbital in Crigler-Najjar type II to lower bilirubin","In Crigler-Najjar type I prevent kernicterus with intensive phototherapy and exchange transfusion for acute rises, as the enzyme is absent and phenobarbital is ineffective","Reserve liver transplantation as the definitive cure for Crigler-Najjar type I, ideally before neurological injury develops","Avoid drugs that displace bilirubin from albumin and, in Dubin-Johnson, oral oestrogens that can precipitate jaundice","Monitor unconjugated bilirubin and neurological status, particularly in neonates and during intercurrent illness, given kernicterus risk in Crigler-Najjar","Offer genetic counselling, as Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson and Rotor are autosomal recessive and Gilbert relates to a UGT1A1 promoter polymorphism"],"severityClassification":"Inherited unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia forms a severity continuum by residual UGT1A1 activity: Crigler-Najjar type I (activity absent; bilirubin ~340-765 micromol/L, 20-45 mg/dL; no phenobarbital response; usual kernicterus), Crigler-Najjar type II (reduced activity, typically <=10% of normal; bilirubin ~100-430 micromol/L, 6-25 mg/dL; >25% phenobarbital response; rare kernicterus), and Gilbert syndrome (activity ~10-35% of normal; bilirubin usually <=70 micromol/L, 4 mg/dL absent fasting/hemolysis; normal histology). Familial conjugated hyperbilirubinemias of canalicular function (Dubin-Johnson, Rotor) are benign, distinguished from the progressive cholestatic disorders BRIC (episodic, non-progressive) and PFIC (progressive to end-stage liver disease).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2637","Harrison 22e p.2638","Harrison 22e p.2639","Harrison 22e p.2640","Harrison 22e p.2641","Harrison 22e p.2642"],"crossLinks":[]},"the_metabolic_syndrome":{"name":"The Metabolic Syndrome","system":"Endocrine/Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Syndrome X","Insulin resistance syndrome","MetS"],"chapter":420,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3252","clinicalPearls":["Metabolic syndrome is a clustering of metabolic abnormalities (central obesity, hypertriglyceridemia, low HDL cholesterol, hyperglycemia, and hypertension) that together raise the risk of cardiovascular disease and diabetes.","Diagnosis under NCEP:ATPIII (2001) requires three or more of: waist circumference >102 cm in men or >88 cm in women; triglycerides >=150 mg/dL; HDL <40 mg/dL (men) or <50 mg/dL (women); BP >=130/85 mmHg; fasting glucose >=100 mg/dL (or specific medication for, or prior diagnosis of, the relevant condition).","The Harmonizing definition uses ethnicity-specific waist circumference cutoffs (e.g., for South Asian and Chinese men >=90 cm, Europid men >=94 cm, Japanese men >=85 cm), reflecting that risk at a given waist circumference differs between populations.","Visceral (intra-abdominal) adipose tissue is more strongly linked to insulin resistance, diabetes, and CVD than subcutaneous fat, but waist circumference alone cannot reliably distinguish the two depots; DEXA, CT, or MRI is needed for that distinction.","Normal-weight individuals can still be insulin-resistant and meet criteria for metabolic syndrome, a phenotype that is particularly evident in populations from India, Southeast Asia, and Central America.","The syndrome is typically asymptomatic; physical examination often shows only elevated waist circumference and blood pressure, which should trigger a search for the other biochemical components.","Lipoatrophy and acanthosis nigricans, when present, signal severe insulin resistance, though they are far less common than the standard components.","Hypertriglyceridemia is an excellent marker of the insulin-resistant state; fasting triglycerides above roughly 180 mg/dL (~2.0 mM) are typically accompanied by a predominance of small, dense, more atherogenic LDL particles.","Most patients (~75%) with type 2 diabetes or impaired glucose tolerance have metabolic syndrome, and its presence in these groups marks a higher CVD burden than in those without the syndrome.","Conditions strongly associated with insulin resistance and the syndrome include metabolic-associated fatty liver disease (MAFLD/MASH), polycystic ovary syndrome, and obstructive sleep apnea, which should be screened for during evaluation.","Obstructive sleep apnea can predict metabolic syndrome even without excess adiposity, and insulin resistance tends to be more severe in apnea patients than in weight-matched controls.","Hyperuricemia reflects insulin-resistance-related defects in renal tubular handling of uric acid and may itself contribute to hypertension via effects on endothelial nitric oxide synthase and activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system."],"pathophysiology":"Insulin resistance is the most widely accepted unifying mechanism. Early insulin resistance manifests as postprandial then fasting hyperinsulinemia, eventually progressing to hyperglycemia as compensatory beta-cell secretion fails. An overabundance of circulating free fatty acids (FFAs), released from expanded adipose tissue and from lipoprotein-lipase-mediated lipolysis, drives the process: FFAs impair insulin-mediated glucose uptake in muscle, promote intramuscular and cardiac triglyceride accumulation, and increase hepatic glucose and triglyceride production with VLDL secretion. Insulin normally suppresses lipolysis (its most sensitive action), so resistance amplifies FFA release in a self-perpetuating loop. Increased VLDL/apoB and elevated apoC-III inhibit lipoprotein lipase, raising triglyceride-rich remnants; HDL becomes small, dense, and cleared faster, lowering HDL cholesterol. Loss of insulin-mediated vasodilation (nitric oxide) with preserved renal sodium reabsorption and sympathetic activation, plus adipose angiotensinogen expression, contributes to hypertension. Contributing/associated mechanisms include leptin resistance, reduced anti-inflammatory adiponectin, increased proinflammatory cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, IL-18, TNF-alpha, resistin) from adipose-derived macrophages, a prothrombotic state (fibrinogen, PAI-1), oxidative stress with mitochondrial dysfunction, and alterations in the gut microbiome (increased Firmicutes/Bacteroidetes ratio).","additionalDifferentials":["Type 2 diabetes mellitus / impaired glucose tolerance","Genetic lipodystrophies (e.g., Berardinelli-Seip congenital, Dunnigan familial partial lipodystrophy)","Acquired lipodystrophy (e.g., HIV-related and antiretroviral-associated)","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Cushing's syndrome / other endocrine causes of central obesity and hypertension","Primary dyslipidemias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lipodystrophy syndromes (severe insulin resistance with many overlapping components)","Cushing's syndrome (central adiposity, hypertension, hyperglycemia)","Polycystic ovary syndrome with insulin resistance"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting lipid panel (triglycerides, HDL cholesterol) and fasting glucose are required for diagnosis","Waist circumference measurement and blood pressure","Optional insulin-resistance biomarkers individualized to the patient: apoB, hsCRP, fibrinogen, uric acid, urinary albumin/creatinine ratio, liver function tests","Sleep study if symptoms of obstructive sleep apnea are present","If PCOS suspected (clinical features and anovulation): testosterone, luteinizing hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone","MAFLD assessment via FIB-4 fibrosis score or elastography","DEXA, CT, or MRI if discrimination of visceral versus subcutaneous fat is needed"],"redFlags":["Acanthosis nigricans or lipoatrophy indicating severe insulin resistance","Established type 2 diabetes or impaired glucose tolerance (markedly elevated diabetes and CVD risk)","Coexisting metabolic syndrome and elevated hsCRP (associated with increased all-cause mortality)","Features suggesting MASH/advanced liver fibrosis (precursor of cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma)","Symptoms of obstructive sleep apnea"],"prognosis":"Metabolic syndrome roughly doubles the risk of cardiovascular death and confers about a threefold higher risk of acute myocardial infarction or stroke; the risk for type 2 diabetes is increased three- to fivefold (fasting glucose explaining most of this). In the REGARDS cohort, metabolic syndrome carried a relative risk of about 1.34 for all-cause mortality, with the relationship to hsCRP and mortality stronger in white than in black participants. Prevalence among patients with coronary heart disease may reach up to 60% in those over 75. Risk and prevalence are modifiable: cardiac rehabilitation and lifestyle change (improved nutrition, physical activity, weight reduction) can reduce the prevalence of the syndrome.","pitfalls":["Waist circumference does not reliably separate subcutaneous from visceral fat, so it can misestimate true metabolic risk without imaging.","A single ethnicity-blind waist cutoff under- or over-estimates risk across populations; ethnicity-specific thresholds are needed.","Normal body weight does not exclude the syndrome, so insulin resistance can be missed in lean individuals.","Whether metabolic syndrome adds CVD or diabetes risk beyond the simple sum of its individual components remains debated, so it should not be over-interpreted as an independent diagnosis.","Fasting insulin levels or HOMA capture only part of insulin resistance, so they inadequately quantify the contribution of resistance to hypertension.","Obstructive sleep apnea and PCOS are commonly overlooked associated conditions if not actively screened for."],"management":["Intensive lifestyle modification with a calorie-reduced diet, increased physical activity and weight loss is the first-line mainstay targeting the underlying insulin resistance","Treat atherogenic dyslipidaemia with a statin, adding a fibrate or other agent for persistent hypertriglyceridaemia","Control blood pressure, favouring renin-angiotensin system inhibitors given their metabolic profile","Address hyperglycaemia and insulin resistance with metformin alongside lifestyle measures to reduce progression to type 2 diabetes","Consider weight-loss pharmacotherapy such as a GLP-1 receptor agonist, or bariatric surgery, for obesity not controlled by lifestyle alone","Screen for and treat associated conditions — obstructive sleep apnoea and metabolic-dysfunction-associated fatty liver disease","Reduce global cardiovascular risk with smoking cessation and periodic monitoring of glycaemia, lipids and blood pressure"],"severityClassification":"There is no graded severity scale; the syndrome is defined categorically by meeting three or more of the five diagnostic components (NCEP:ATPIII 2001 or the ethnicity-adjusted Harmonizing definition, Table 420-1). Risk rises with the number of components present and with associated markers such as elevated hsCRP.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3252","Harrison 22e p.3253","Harrison 22e p.3254","Harrison 22e p.3255","Harrison 22e p.3256"],"crossLinks":[]},"the_vasculitis_syndromes":{"name":"The Vasculitis Syndromes","system":"Multisystem / Rheumatologic (Immune-Mediated Vascular)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Vasculitis","Systemic vasculitis","ANCA-associated vasculitis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Microscopic polyangiitis","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss)","Polyarteritis nodosa","Giant cell (temporal) arteritis","Takayasu arteritis","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein)","Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis","Polymyalgia rheumatica"],"chapter":375,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2893","clinicalPearls":["Vasculitis is inflammation of and damage to blood vessel walls that typically narrows the lumen and produces downstream tissue ischemia; it may be the sole disease, may be secondary to another disease, and may be single-organ or multisystem.","Suspect vasculitis in any unexplained systemic illness; clinical clues include palpable purpura, pulmonary infiltrates with microscopic hematuria, chronic sinusitis, mononeuritis multiplex, unexplained ischemic events, and glomerulonephritis with multisystem disease.","Definitive diagnosis usually rests on biopsy of clinically involved tissue; 'blind' biopsy of organs without subjective or objective involvement has very low yield and should be avoided. Catheter-directed arteriography is used when PAN, Takayasu, or primary CNS vasculitis is suspected.","ANCA subtypes: cANCA targets proteinase-3 (>90% positive in active granulomatosis with polyangiitis); pANCA most relevantly targets myeloperoxidase (variably positive in microscopic polyangiitis, EGPA, and isolated necrotizing crescentic glomerulonephritis). Only anti-MPO and anti-PR3 are convincingly vasculitis-associated.","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis classically combines granulomatous vasculitis of the upper and lower respiratory tracts with glomerulonephritis; upper airway involvement occurs in ~95% and renal disease (~77% over course) dominates prognosis. Renal lesion is pauci-immune (no immune-complex deposition).","Microscopic polyangiitis resembles GPA but lacks granulomatous inflammation; it favors small vessels (capillaries, venules, arterioles), with glomerulonephritis in at least 79% and alveolar hemorrhage in ~12%. Upper airway disease and lung nodules are atypical and, if present, point instead to GPA. ANCA (usually anti-MPO) in ~75%.","EGPA (Churg-Strauss) is defined by asthma, blood and tissue eosinophilia, and vasculitis; eosinophilia exceeds 1000 cells/uL in >80% and ANCA (usually anti-MPO) is present in ~48%. Cardiac involvement is the leading cause of death, so echocardiography is advised at diagnosis.","Polyarteritis nodosa is a necrotizing vasculitis of small- and medium-sized muscular arteries that spares pulmonary arteries and does NOT cause glomerulonephritis, granuloma, or significant eosinophilia; renal involvement is arteritis (microaneurysms, hypertension), not glomerular. ANCA are rarely found; screen all patients for hepatitis B and C.","Giant cell arteritis occurs almost exclusively after age 50, presents with fever, anemia, high ESR/CRP, and headache; ischemic optic neuropathy with sudden blindness is the dreaded complication, and patients carry an ~18-fold increased risk of thoracic aortic aneurysm. Treatment should not be delayed for biopsy.","Polymyalgia rheumatica (aching/stiffness of neck, shoulder and hip girdles with raised ESR/CRP) coexists with GCA in 40-50% of GCA patients; creatine kinase is normal and RF/anti-CCP should be negative. ~10-20% with isolated PMR later develop GCA.","Takayasu arteritis is a large-vessel panarteritis with strong predilection for the aortic arch and its branches, classically in adolescent girls and young women; suspect it in a young woman with diminished or absent pulses, an interarm blood-pressure discrepancy, or arterial bruits.","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein) is a small-vessel, immune-complex (IgA) vasculitis most common in children 4-7 years, with palpable purpura over buttocks/lower limbs, arthralgias, colicky GI symptoms (intussusception risk), and usually mild self-limited glomerulonephritis; serum complement is normal and IgA is elevated in about half.","Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis is most often associated with hepatitis C (estimated in ~5% of chronic HCV); rheumatoid factor is almost always present, hypocomplementemia occurs in ~90%, and membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis accounts for ~80% of renal lesions.","Temporal artery biopsy yield improves with a 3-5 cm segment and serial sectioning because involvement is segmental; biopsy may still show arteritis after about 14 days of glucocorticoid therapy, so starting treatment promptly does not preclude later diagnosis."],"pathophysiology":"Most vasculitic syndromes are thought to be at least partly immune-mediated responses to antigenic stimuli, with the precise mechanism varying by disease. Three principal hypothesized mechanisms are recognized. (1) Pathogenic immune-complex formation/deposition (e.g., IgA vasculitis, lupus vasculitis, serum-sickness/cutaneous syndromes, HCV-associated cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, HBV-associated vasculitis): antigen-antibody complexes formed in antigen excess deposit in vessel walls whose permeability has been raised by vasoactive amines, activate complement (notably chemotactic C5a), recruit neutrophils that phagocytose complexes and release intracytoplasmic enzymes damaging the wall, with later mononuclear infiltration and luminal compromise. (2) ANCA production (GPA, microscopic polyangiitis, EGPA): when neutrophils/monocytes are primed by TNF-alpha or IL-1 or alternative-complement activation, PR3 and MPO translocate to the cell membrane where ANCA can engage them, triggering degranulation, reactive oxygen species, endothelial injury, and release of IL-1/IL-8; however, some patients have active GPA without ANCA and titers correlate poorly with activity, so a primary pathogenic role remains debated. (3) Pathogenic T-lymphocyte responses and granuloma formation (giant cell arteritis, Takayasu arteritis, GPA, EGPA): cytokine-activated endothelium expresses HLA class II and secretes IL-1, enabling CD4+ T-cell interactions; IL-1 and TNF-alpha upregulate ELAM-1 and VCAM-1 to enhance leukocyte adhesion. In GCA the disease is antigen-driven, with clonally restricted T cells suggesting an arterial-wall antigen, and both TH1 (IFN-gamma) and TH17 (IL-17) CD4 lineages contribute. The size, type, and location of affected vessels, host genetic predisposition, environmental exposures, reticuloendothelial clearance of complexes, hemodynamic factors, and endothelial integrity all influence the ultimate phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Other primary vasculitides when the full classic syndrome is incomplete","Anti-glomerular basement membrane (Goodpasture's) disease","Relapsing polychondritis","IgG4-related disease (aortitis/periaortitis mimicking Takayasu)","Connective tissue disease-associated (lupus, rheumatoid, sarcoid) vasculitis","Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis or other inflammatory arthropathy (vs polymyalgia rheumatica)","Lymphomatoid granulomatosis and extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma (vs GPA midline destructive disease)"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial endocarditis","Disseminated gonococcal infection","Pulmonary histoplasmosis","Coccidioidomycosis","Syphilis","Lyme disease","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Whipple's disease","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis and rhinoscleroma (midline destructive mimics of GPA)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Antiphospholipid syndrome","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura / thrombotic microangiopathies","Atrial myxoma","Lymphoma and carcinomatosis","Drug toxicity (cocaine, levamisole-adulterated cocaine, amphetamines, ergot alkaloids, methysergide, arsenic)","Sarcoidosis","Atheroembolic disease","Amyloidosis","Migraine","Fibromuscular dysplasia","Heritable connective tissue disorders","Segmental arterial mediolysis","Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome"],"additionalInvestigations":["Tissue biopsy of clinically involved organ (highest yield: lung for GPA; symptomatic skin nodules, painful testes, or nerve/muscle for PAN)","ANCA testing (anti-PR3 / cANCA and anti-MPO / pANCA) with awareness of variable predictive value by presentation","Catheter-directed (dye) arteriography for suspected PAN; MR or CT arteriography of aorta and branches for Takayasu and large-vessel GCA","Temporal artery biopsy (3-5 cm with serial sections) and/or temporal +/- axillary artery ultrasound 'halo' sign for GCA","ESR and/or CRP, complete blood count (anemia, leukocytosis, thrombocytosis, eosinophilia in EGPA)","Urinalysis for hematuria, proteinuria, and red-cell casts; serum creatinine","Hepatitis B and C serologies/RNA (PAN, cryoglobulinemic vasculitis)","Serum cryoglobulins, rheumatoid factor, and complement levels for cryoglobulinemic vasculitis","Skin biopsy with immunofluorescence (IgA and C3 deposition in IgA vasculitis; leukocytoclastic vasculitis in cutaneous vasculitis)","Echocardiography at diagnosis in EGPA given cardiac mortality risk","Consider DADA2 (adenosine deaminase type 2 deficiency) evaluation in early-onset PAN-like vasculitis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis or rising creatinine (GPA, microscopic polyangiitis, cryoglobulinemic vasculitis)","Hemoptysis / diffuse alveolar hemorrhage (microscopic polyangiitis, GPA)","New or worsening headache, jaw or tongue claudication, scalp tenderness, or any visual symptom in a patient over 50 (impending GCA-related blindness)","Subglottic stenosis or progressive airway obstruction in GPA","Mononeuritis multiplex","Abdominal pain with GI bleeding suggesting bowel infarction/perforation (PAN, EGPA, IgA vasculitis with intussusception)","Cardiac involvement (myocarditis/coronary vasculitis) in EGPA or PAN","Interarm blood-pressure discrepancy, absent pulses, or aortic aneurysm/dissection in large-vessel disease","Concurrent anti-GBM antibody positivity in ANCA-associated vasculitis"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies markedly by syndrome and by treatment. Untreated granulomatosis with polyangiitis was once uniformly fatal within months; modern cyclophosphamide- or rituximab-based regimens with glucocorticoids achieve improvement in >90% and remission in ~75%, with 5-year survival >80%, though 50-70% of remissions are followed by relapse and many patients accrue irreversible damage (renal insufficiency, hearing loss, subglottic stenosis, saddle-nose deformity). Treated microscopic polyangiitis has ~74% 5-year survival with relapse in at least 34%, mortality from alveolar hemorrhage or GI/cardiac/renal disease. Untreated EGPA has a ~25% 5-year survival; with treatment about 72% survive at 78 months, and myocardial involvement causes ~39% of deaths. Untreated PAN carries a 10-20% 5-year survival (death from bowel infarction/perforation and cardiovascular causes); combination immunosuppression markedly improves survival, with relapse in ~10-20%. GCA seldom causes acute death but carries late risk of aortic aneurysm rupture/dissection; PMR generally responds well. Takayasu is usually chronic and relapsing with widely variable reported survival. IgA vasculitis prognosis is excellent (mortality exceedingly rare; 1-5% of children reach end-stage renal disease, possibly higher in adults; recurrences in 10-40%). In cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, glomerulonephritis is a poor prognostic sign (~15% progress to end-stage renal disease, 40% of whom later die of cardiovascular disease, infection, or liver failure).","pitfalls":["Do NOT treat a rise in ANCA titer alone as evidence of relapse; only ~43% of patients relapsed within a year of an ANCA rise, and many remain ANCA-positive for years in remission.","ANCA predictive value falls sharply when renal disease is absent (~30-60% with sinus/lung-only disease vs ~90% with active glomerulonephritis), so biopsy is needed to exclude infection or malignancy that can cause false-positive ANCA.","Upper airway lesions in GPA should NEVER be irradiated; midline destructive disease mimics include NK/T-cell lymphoma and cocaine/levamisole injury (anti-neutrophil-elastase ANCA, granulocytopenia point to levamisole).","Eosinophilia is rare in PAN; when markedly elevated it should redirect toward EGPA.","In PAN, MR and CT arteriography lack the resolution to visualize the small/medium aneurysms, so catheter-directed dye arteriography is required.","When tocilizumab or other IL-6 blockade is used in GCA, ESR and CRP normalize and can no longer be used to gauge disease activity or detect infection.","TMP-SMX should never be used alone to treat active GPA involving organs beyond the sinonasal tissue.","Failure to exclude infection (especially endocarditis) before starting empirical immunosuppression in a rapidly deteriorating patient can be catastrophic.","Glucocorticoids do not reliably benefit the skin or renal disease of IgA vasculitis and do not shorten its course or reduce recurrence."],"management":["Induce remission in severe, organ-threatening ANCA-associated vasculitis with high-dose glucocorticoids combined with either rituximab or cyclophosphamide","Maintain remission with rituximab, azathioprine or methotrexate while tapering glucocorticoids to the lowest effective dose","Add plasma exchange in selected cases such as rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis or alveolar haemorrhage","Treat the underlying trigger: antiviral therapy for hepatitis-B- or hepatitis-C-associated (cryoglobulinaemic) vasculitis, and withdrawal of the offending agent in drug-induced disease","Start glucocorticoids urgently in giant cell arteritis to prevent visual loss, using tocilizumab as a steroid-sparing agent","Give infection prophylaxis, including against Pneumocystis, and monitor for treatment toxicity and disease relapse","Coordinate multidisciplinary specialist care across rheumatology, nephrology, ENT and respiratory medicine, with organ support for major complications"],"severityClassification":"For ANCA-associated vasculitis (GPA/microscopic polyangiitis), Harrison frames management by severe vs nonsevere disease. Severe disease involves life- or organ-threatening manifestations and is judged by both extent and the specific organ affected: pulmonary disease may be nonsevere (nodules without respiratory compromise) or severe (alveolar hemorrhage), whereas active glomerulonephritis is ALWAYS considered severe even when renal function is normal. Treatment is conceptually divided into a remission-induction phase followed by a maintenance phase, with regimen choice stratified by this severity assessment along with relapse history and comorbidities.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2893","Harrison 22e p.2894","Harrison 22e p.2895","Harrison 22e p.2896","Harrison 22e p.2897","Harrison 22e p.2898","Harrison 22e p.2899","Harrison 22e p.2900","Harrison 22e p.2901","Harrison 22e p.2902","Harrison 22e p.2903","Harrison 22e p.2904","Harrison 22e p.2905","Harrison 22e p.2906"],"crossLinks":[]},"theca_lutein_cyst":{"name":"Theca lutein cyst","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["theca-lutein cysts","hyperreactio luteinalis","luteinised follicular cysts"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology reference textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically bilateral and multicystic, arising from marked luteinisation of the ovaries driven by beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin","Strongly associated with high or sustained beta-hCG levels, as seen in gestational trophoblastic disease, molar pregnancy, and multiple pregnancy","Also occurs in ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome following ovulation induction, where exogenous stimulation and hCG drive cyst formation","Often asymptomatic and discovered incidentally, though the enlarged ovaries may cause a dragging pelvic discomfort or pressure symptoms","The ovaries commonly show a multilocular, spoke-wheel appearance on ultrasound with numerous thin-walled cysts","The cysts characteristically regress spontaneously once the beta-hCG level falls, so expectant management is usually appropriate","Complications include torsion, haemorrhage, or rupture of the enlarged ovaries, which may require intervention"],"pathophysiology":"Excessive circulating beta-hCG, or heightened ovarian sensitivity to normal levels, over-stimulates the theca interna cells lining the ovarian follicles. This drives exaggerated luteinisation and the formation of multiple fluid-filled cysts, usually affecting both ovaries and producing considerable ovarian enlargement. Because the process is hormonally driven, the cysts involute as the beta-hCG concentration declines, for example after evacuation of a molar pregnancy or delivery.","additionalDifferentials":["Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome","Bilateral cystadenomas","Polycystic ovaries","Ovarian malignancy with bilateral cystic involvement","Bilateral functional cysts from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum beta-hCG to confirm and quantify the hormonal driver","Transvaginal or transabdominal ultrasound showing bilateral multilocular cysts","Pelvic ultrasound of the uterus to exclude a molar pregnancy or confirm a multiple pregnancy","Thyroid function tests, given the potential thyrotropic effect of very high hCG","Full blood count and observation for features of ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome"],"redFlags":["Ovarian torsion presenting with acute severe pelvic pain","Haemorrhage or rupture of an enlarged ovary","Evidence of gestational trophoblastic disease requiring specialist management","Severe ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome with tense ascites, breathlessness, or thromboembolic risk"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent, as theca lutein cysts almost always resolve spontaneously once the underlying hCG stimulus subsides, without the need for surgery.","pitfalls":["Performing unnecessary surgery on cysts that would resolve spontaneously","Failing to identify an underlying molar pregnancy or gestational trophoblastic disease","Overlooking evolving ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome in an assisted-conception setting","Missing torsion or haemorrhage in a woman with markedly enlarged ovaries"],"management":["Expectant, conservative management is the mainstay, as the cysts involute spontaneously once the beta-hCG stimulus falls","Treat the underlying driver — evacuation of a hydatidiform mole, delivery, or chemotherapy for gestational trophoblastic neoplasia","Manage associated ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome supportively where that is the cause","Monitor with serial beta-hCG and pelvic ultrasound to confirm regression and exclude trophoblastic disease","Check thyroid function given the weak thyrotropic effect of very high hCG","Reserve surgery for acute complications such as torsion, rupture or haemorrhage, conserving ovarian tissue wherever possible"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference textbook","Reproductive medicine reference text"],"crossLinks":[]},"theca_lutein_cysts":{"name":"theca lutein cysts","system":"Reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["theca-lutein cysts","hCG-associated luteinised ovarian cysts"],"chapter":null,"source":"Berek & Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Theca-lutein cysts are the largest of the functional ovarian cysts and are characteristically bilateral and multilocular, arising from excessive or prolonged stimulation of ovarian theca cells by human chorionic gonadotrophin.","They are the classic ovarian finding of gestational trophoblastic disease (complete hydatidiform mole and choriocarcinoma) and also accompany multiple gestation, fetal hydrops, ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome, and exogenous gonadotrophin or clomifene therapy.","On ultrasound the enlarged ovaries show numerous thin-walled locules in a 'spoke-wheel' pattern, usually without solid components.","Most are asymptomatic and found incidentally, but large cysts may cause pelvic pressure, and complications include torsion, rupture, intraperitoneal haemorrhage and occasionally ascites.","Because they are hCG-dependent, they regress once the stimulus is removed (after evacuation of a mole, delivery, or resolution of the underlying cause), although regression may lag behind the fall in hCG by weeks to months.","Management is conservative and expectant; surgery is reserved for acute complications such as torsion or haemorrhage, ideally with ovarian conservation.","In gestational trophoblastic disease, persistent or re-enlarging theca-lutein cysts should prompt reassessment for persistent trophoblastic activity.","Maternal virilisation and clinical hyperthyroidism (from the weak thyrotrophic activity of hCG) are occasionally seen when hCG concentrations are very high."],"pathophysiology":"Theca-lutein cysts develop when ovarian theca-interna cells are excessively stimulated by human chorionic gonadotrophin, which acts on the luteinising-hormone receptor to drive luteinisation, follicular over-recruitment and cystic dilatation. Markedly raised hCG, as in complete molar pregnancy, choriocarcinoma or multiple gestation, is the usual driver, although heightened ovarian sensitivity can produce them at more modest levels. The affected ovaries enlarge and become studded with multiple thin-walled, fluid-filled cysts lined by luteinised theca and granulosa cells, frequently bilateral and symmetrical. The luteinised cells may secrete androgens, and the high hCG can exert weak thyroid-stimulating activity. Because the cysts are functional and hormone-dependent, removal or decline of the hCG source allows involution.","additionalDifferentials":["Hyperreactio luteinalis (florid theca-lutein cysts in pregnancy without trophoblastic disease)","Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome","Bilateral cystic ovarian neoplasm (mucinous or serous cystadenoma, borderline tumour)","Polycystic ovaries","Bilateral haemorrhagic corpus luteum cysts or endometriomas","Ovarian torsion","Simple follicular cysts"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative serum hCG and its trend","Pelvic ultrasound with Doppler to assess cyst morphology, ovarian perfusion and possible torsion","Uterine evaluation for hydatidiform mole or retained trophoblastic tissue","Thyroid function tests if clinically hyperthyroid","Serum androgens if virilisation is present","Chest imaging where choriocarcinoma or metastatic trophoblastic disease is suspected","Interval imaging to document regression"],"redFlags":["Acute severe pelvic pain with peritonism suggesting torsion, rupture or haemorrhage","Haemodynamic instability from intraperitoneal bleeding","Plateauing or rising hCG after molar evacuation indicating persistent gestational trophoblastic neoplasia","Solid or papillary components, or failure to regress, raising suspicion of neoplasm"],"prognosis":"The cysts are benign and self-limiting and almost always involute spontaneously once the hCG source resolves. The outlook is excellent provided complications such as torsion or haemorrhage are managed and any underlying trophoblastic disease is treated.","pitfalls":["Resecting hormone-dependent cysts that would regress spontaneously, sacrificing ovarian tissue","Failing to investigate for gestational trophoblastic disease when bilateral theca-lutein cysts are found","Misinterpreting the multicystic ovaries as malignancy","Attributing all pelvic pain to the cysts and missing a coexisting torsion","Expecting immediate regression and re-intervening before the natural lag after hCG decline"],"management":["Conservative, expectant management is appropriate, as these hCG-dependent functional cysts regress once the underlying stimulus resolves, though regression may lag the fall in hCG by weeks to months","Treat the underlying cause — evacuation of a hydatidiform mole or retained trophoblastic tissue, delivery, or chemotherapy for choriocarcinoma","Manage ovulation-induction-related cases as part of ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome with supportive care","Monitor with serial quantitative hCG and Doppler pelvic ultrasound to track regression and assess ovarian perfusion","Reserve surgery, with ovarian conservation, for acute complications such as torsion, rupture or intraperitoneal haemorrhage","Check thyroid function if clinically hyperthyroid and serum androgens if virilisation is present","Refer to gynaecology, with oncology input where trophoblastic malignancy is confirmed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Berek & Novak's Gynecology","Williams Obstetrics","Blaustein's Pathology of the Female Genital Tract"],"crossLinks":[]},"thelaziasis":{"name":"Thelaziasis (eye worm)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Thelazia callipaeda","Thelazia californiensis","oriental eye worm","ocular thelaziasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Thelaziasis is infestation of the eye by Thelazia nematodes, transmitted by fruit-feeding flies that deposit larvae in the conjunctival secretions","Thelazia callipaeda predominates across parts of Asia and Europe, while Thelazia californiensis occurs in North America","Adult worms live in the conjunctival sac, under the eyelids and around the lacrimal apparatus","Symptoms include foreign-body sensation, excessive tearing, conjunctival redness, itching and sometimes visible moving worms","Complications may include keratitis, corneal ulceration and, rarely, more serious ocular damage","Diagnosis is clinical, by directly visualising and removing the slender white worms during slit-lamp or eyelid examination","Treatment is mechanical removal of the worms, with topical measures to relieve irritation and treat any secondary infection"],"pathophysiology":"Thelaziasis results from the deposition of infective Thelazia larvae onto the conjunctiva by drosophilid flies that feed on lacrimal secretions. The larvae mature into adult worms within the conjunctival sac and adnexal structures, where their movement and cuticular surface mechanically irritate the ocular surface. This provokes reflex tearing, conjunctival inflammation and a foreign-body reaction; persistent presence of worms can lead to keratitis and corneal ulceration. Because the parasite remains largely superficial in the eye, disease is usually localised rather than systemic.","additionalDifferentials":["Allergic or infective conjunctivitis","Foreign body on the ocular surface","Other ocular parasitoses such as loiasis or ocular larva migrans","Bacterial or viral keratitis","Dry eye disease with surface irritation","Blepharitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination of the conjunctival sac and eyelid margins to identify worms","Careful eversion of the eyelids to inspect the fornices","Morphological identification of extracted worms under microscopy","Ocular surface staining to detect corneal involvement","Conjunctival swab if secondary bacterial infection is suspected","Molecular identification of the species where available"],"redFlags":["Corneal ulceration or visual impairment suggesting keratitis","Severe or persistent ocular pain and photophobia","Signs of secondary bacterial infection with purulent discharge","Multiple or recurrent worm infestation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent once the worms are removed, with rapid symptomatic relief and full recovery unless corneal damage has already occurred.","pitfalls":["Treating persistent conjunctivitis as allergic or bacterial without inspecting for worms","Incomplete removal leaving residual worms and continuing symptoms","Overlooking the fornices and lacrimal region where worms hide","Missing secondary corneal involvement"],"management":["Mechanical removal of the adult worms from the conjunctival sac and fornices under topical anaesthesia, using forceps or a cotton swab at slit-lamp examination, is the definitive treatment","Irrigate the conjunctival sac to flush out larvae and any remaining worms","Consider an anthelmintic (a topical or systemic macrocyclic lactone such as moxidectin or ivermectin) as an adjunct in heavy or recurrent infestation","Treat or prevent secondary bacterial infection with topical antibiotics","Manage complications such as keratitis and corneal ulceration with ocular-surface care and ophthalmology review","Advise fly-control and hygiene measures to reduce reinfection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"theophylline_toxicity":{"name":"Theophylline toxicity","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["theophylline poisoning","theophylline overdose","aminophylline toxicity","methylxanthine toxicity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Theophylline has a narrow therapeutic window and toxicity may be acute or chronic, with the two presenting differently","Chronic toxicity, often in older patients or after a change in metabolism or an interacting drug, causes serious effects at lower concentrations than acute overdose","Prominent features are vomiting, tremor, agitation, tachycardia and, in severe cases, seizures and arrhythmias","Hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hyperglycaemia and a metabolic (lactic) acidosis reflect intense beta-adrenergic and metabolic stimulation","Seizures in significant theophylline poisoning can be refractory and portend a poor outcome","Slow-release preparations cause delayed and prolonged absorption, so levels should be measured serially and can continue to rise","Multiple-dose activated charcoal enhances elimination, and extracorporeal removal such as haemodialysis is indicated for very high concentrations or severe clinical toxicity","Beta-blockade may be considered cautiously for tachydysrhythmias driven by beta-adrenergic excess, under specialist guidance"],"pathophysiology":"Theophylline is a methylxanthine that inhibits phosphodiesterase, raising intracellular cyclic adenosine monophosphate, and antagonises adenosine receptors. It also releases endogenous catecholamines, producing a state of intense beta-adrenergic stimulation. The result is central nervous system excitation with tremor, agitation and seizures, cardiovascular effects including tachycardia and dysrhythmias, and metabolic disturbances such as hypokalaemia from intracellular potassium shift, hyperglycaemia and lactic acidosis. Adenosine antagonism lowers the seizure threshold. In chronic toxicity, accumulation from impaired clearance produces serious effects at lower measured concentrations than in acute single overdose.","additionalDifferentials":["Sympathomimetic poisoning (e.g. amphetamines, cocaine)","Caffeine or other methylxanthine toxicity","Thyroid storm","Sepsis with lactic acidosis","Salicylate poisoning","Serotonin syndrome or other agitated hyperadrenergic states"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial serum theophylline concentrations, especially with modified-release preparations","Serum potassium, magnesium, glucose and other electrolytes","Arterial blood gas and lactate","Electrocardiogram and continuous cardiac monitoring for arrhythmia","Renal and liver function to assess clearance","Paracetamol and salicylate levels in intentional overdose"],"redFlags":["Seizures, particularly if recurrent or refractory","Ventricular or unstable supraventricular arrhythmia","Very high serum concentration, especially after modified-release ingestion","Severe hypokalaemia, acidosis or haemodynamic instability","Chronic toxicity in an older or comorbid patient even at moderate levels"],"prognosis":"Mild toxicity generally resolves with supportive care and gastrointestinal decontamination, but severe poisoning with seizures or arrhythmias, and chronic toxicity in vulnerable patients, carries significant mortality; prompt enhanced elimination improves outcome in severe cases.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single level and missing continued absorption from slow-release tablets","Underestimating chronic toxicity because the concentration seems only modestly raised","Neglecting to correct hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia driving arrhythmia","Delaying extracorporeal removal in severe or very high-level poisoning","Using unopposed agents that worsen catecholamine-driven cardiovascular effects"],"management":["Stop theophylline and give multi-dose activated charcoal to enhance elimination by interrupting enterohepatic recirculation, with whole-bowel irrigation considered for modified-release preparations","Correct metabolic disturbance — replace potassium (recognising it reflects intracellular shift) and magnesium, and treat hypoglycaemia or acidosis","Control seizures with benzodiazepines then barbiturates; phenytoin is ineffective and should be avoided","Give antiemetics for the prominent vomiting and treat tachyarrhythmias, using a beta-blocker cautiously for beta-adrenergic-driven supraventricular arrhythmia with haemodynamic compromise","Institute haemodialysis for severe poisoning — refractory seizures, life-threatening arrhythmia, markedly elevated or rising concentrations, or chronic toxicity in vulnerable patients","Measure serial theophylline concentrations, especially with slow-release preparations where absorption is delayed and prolonged","Manage in a monitored critical care setting with continuous cardiac monitoring and early escalation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","UpToDate: Theophylline poisoning"],"crossLinks":[]},"thermal_burns":{"name":"Thermal burns","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["burn injury","burns","thermal injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Approach the burned patient as a trauma patient first, following the primary survey, because associated injuries and airway compromise can be immediately lethal","Assess the airway early: facial burns, singed nasal hair, soot in the mouth, hoarseness, and stridor warn of inhalational injury and the need for early intubation before oedema closes the airway","Estimate burn size using validated methods (rule of nines or a palm-based estimate) counting only partial- and full-thickness burns","Burn depth guides prognosis: superficial burns blanch and are painful, whereas full-thickness burns are leathery, insensate, and non-blanching","Large burns require fluid resuscitation titrated to urine output using a body-surface-area and weight-based formula as a starting estimate","Circumferential full-thickness burns of a limb or the chest can cause a compartment or ventilatory crisis requiring escharotomy","Suspect carbon monoxide or cyanide toxicity in enclosed-space fires, since pulse oximetry can read falsely normal","Keep the patient warm and provide adequate analgesia, as burns cause rapid heat and fluid loss and severe pain"],"pathophysiology":"Heat denatures proteins and causes coagulative necrosis of skin, with a central zone of coagulation surrounded by zones of stasis and hyperaemia that can progress to necrosis if perfusion falls. Loss of the skin barrier and a systemic inflammatory response increase capillary permeability, causing large fluid shifts, intravascular volume depletion, and burn shock in extensive injuries. Inhalational injury combines direct thermal damage to the upper airway, chemical injury to the lower airways from combustion products, and systemic asphyxiants such as carbon monoxide and cyanide. Later, the denuded surface predisposes to infection and hypermetabolism.","additionalDifferentials":["Chemical or electrical burns with different management priorities","Scald injury versus non-accidental injury patterns","Toxic epidermal necrolysis or Stevens-Johnson syndrome mimicking a burn","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Cold injury (frostbite) in the differential of tissue necrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Carboxyhaemoglobin level and arterial blood gas where inhalation is suspected","Serial urine output monitoring to titrate fluid resuscitation","Baseline bloods including electrolytes, renal function, and haematocrit","Bronchoscopy to assess and confirm inhalational airway injury in selected cases","Serial examination of circumferential burns for compartment compromise"],"redFlags":["Signs of inhalational injury (hoarseness, stridor, soot, facial burns) demanding early airway control before oedema progresses","Extensive burns with evolving hypovolaemic shock requiring aggressive titrated resuscitation","Circumferential full-thickness burns threatening limb perfusion or ventilation and needing escharotomy","Suspected carbon monoxide or cyanide poisoning from an enclosed-space fire","Rapidly rising resuscitation fluid requirements or falling urine output indicating under-resuscitation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on burn size and depth, age, inhalational injury, and comorbidity. Small partial-thickness burns heal well, whereas large or full-thickness burns and inhalational injury carry higher mortality and long-term morbidity, including scarring and functional loss.","pitfalls":["Delaying airway assessment until oedema makes intubation difficult or impossible","Under- or over-estimating burn area, leading to incorrect fluid resuscitation","Relying on pulse oximetry, which can appear normal in carbon monoxide poisoning","Missing associated trauma by focusing only on the visible burn","Allowing hypothermia during exposure and fluid administration"],"management":["Manage as trauma with a primary survey and secure the airway early — intubating before oedema develops — when inhalational injury is suspected","Resuscitate large burns with warmed crystalloid titrated to urine output using a body-surface-area and weight-based formula as the starting estimate","Treat systemic asphyxiants: high-flow oxygen for carbon monoxide and hydroxocobalamin for suspected cyanide toxicity","Provide burn wound care with cleaning, topical antimicrobials and dressings, and early tangential excision with skin grafting for deep partial- and full-thickness burns","Perform escharotomy for circumferential full-thickness burns causing limb ischaemia or ventilatory compromise","Give adequate analgesia, tetanus prophylaxis, early high-protein nutritional support for the hypermetabolic response, and vigilance for wound infection","Refer major burns to a specialist burns centre for multidisciplinary care, rehabilitation and scar management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"thiamine_responsive_megaloblastic_anaemia":{"name":"Thiamine-responsive megaloblastic anaemia","system":"Haematological / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TRMA","Rogers syndrome","SLC19A2 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Haematology and metabolic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in SLC19A2, encoding a high-affinity thiamine transporter","Classic triad: megaloblastic anaemia, diabetes mellitus and sensorineural hearing loss","The megaloblastic anaemia responds to pharmacological doses of thiamine, unlike vitamin B12 or folate deficiency","Hearing loss is usually permanent and often not reversed by thiamine","Diabetes typically develops in childhood and may partially respond to thiamine early on","The bone marrow shows megaloblastic changes and often ringed sideroblasts","Presentation is usually in infancy or early childhood"],"pathophysiology":"SLC19A2 encodes thiamine transporter 1, which mediates high-affinity cellular uptake of thiamine. Loss of function impairs intracellular thiamine availability in tissues that depend on this transporter, particularly haematopoietic precursors, pancreatic beta cells and the inner ear. Thiamine is a cofactor for enzymes of carbohydrate and nucleic acid metabolism, so its intracellular deficiency impairs DNA synthesis in erythroid precursors (megaloblastic anaemia), beta-cell function (diabetes) and cochlear function (deafness). High-dose thiamine can partially overcome the transport defect through low-affinity routes.","additionalDifferentials":["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency","Congenital sideroblastic anaemia","Pearson marrow-pancreas syndrome","Other syndromic diabetes with deafness (including mitochondrial diabetes)","Myelodysplasia in a child"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film showing macrocytic/megaloblastic anaemia","Bone marrow examination (megaloblastic change, ringed sideroblasts)","Normal serum vitamin B12 and folate","Genetic testing of SLC19A2","Audiometry and assessment of glucose metabolism","Trial of thiamine with monitoring of the haematological response"],"redFlags":["Progressive hearing loss","Poorly controlled diabetes","Severe anaemia requiring transfusion before diagnosis","Additional features such as optic atrophy or cardiac involvement in some cases"],"prognosis":"With early recognition and thiamine supplementation the anaemia and, to some extent, the diabetes can be improved, but sensorineural hearing loss is generally permanent; lifelong follow-up is required.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for ordinary B12 or folate deficiency and missing the transporter defect","Not trialling thiamine in an unexplained childhood megaloblastic anaemia with diabetes and deafness","Expecting thiamine to reverse established hearing loss","Overlooking the diagnosis when only one or two triad features are present initially"],"management":["Lifelong pharmacological (high-dose) thiamine supplementation is the mainstay, correcting the megaloblastic anaemia and often improving early glycaemic control","Manage the diabetes mellitus with insulin as required, recognising that thiamine may reduce insulin needs early in the course","Provide audiological rehabilitation with hearing aids or cochlear implantation, as the sensorineural deafness is usually permanent and thiamine-unresponsive","Monitor the haematological response (full blood count and film) and glycaemic control to titrate thiamine and diabetes therapy","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across haematology, metabolic medicine/endocrinology and audiology","Offer genetic counselling and family screening, as this is an autosomal recessive SLC19A2 disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Wintrobe's Clinical Hematology (standard reference)","GeneReviews (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"thiamine_responsive_megaloblastic_anemia":{"name":"Thiamine-responsive megaloblastic anaemia","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rogers syndrome","TRMA","SLC19A2","thiamine transporter 1 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews / Nathan and Oski's Haematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Recognise the classic triad: megaloblastic anaemia, diabetes mellitus and progressive sensorineural deafness, all presenting in infancy or early childhood","Autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in SLC19A2, encoding high-affinity thiamine transporter 1 (THTR1)","The megaloblastic anaemia does not respond to vitamin B12 or folate, but does respond to pharmacological thiamine supplementation","Thiamine treatment can correct the anaemia and improve glycaemic control, though it usually does not reverse the sensorineural hearing loss","The bone marrow shows megaloblastic changes and often ringed sideroblasts","Diabetes is typically non-autoimmune and may become insulin-dependent over time","Additional features can include optic atrophy, retinal changes, cardiac defects and short stature","Lifelong thiamine supplementation is required; interruption can allow anaemia and hyperglycaemia to recur"],"pathophysiology":"Rogers syndrome results from loss of function of the high-affinity thiamine transporter THTR1 encoded by SLC19A2. Cells that depend heavily on this transporter for thiamine uptake, notably haematopoietic precursors, pancreatic beta cells and cochlear cells, become thiamine deficient at the intracellular level despite normal systemic thiamine. Impaired thiamine-dependent enzyme activity (including transketolase and the pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complexes) disrupts nucleic acid ribose synthesis and energy metabolism, producing megaloblastic dyserythropoiesis, beta-cell failure with diabetes, and cochlear degeneration with deafness. Supraphysiological thiamine can drive residual low-affinity transport and restore intracellular thiamine, correcting the haematological and metabolic features.","additionalDifferentials":["Vitamin B12 or folate deficiency megaloblastic anaemia","Congenital sideroblastic anaemias","Myelodysplastic syndrome in childhood","Pearson marrow-pancreas syndrome and other mitochondrial disorders","Wolfram syndrome (DIDMOAD) given diabetes with deafness","Neonatal or MODY-type diabetes syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SLC19A2 molecular genetic testing","Full blood count and blood film showing macrocytic anaemia","Bone marrow examination for megaloblastic change and ringed sideroblasts","Fasting glucose and glycated haemoglobin for diabetes","Audiometry for sensorineural hearing loss","Ophthalmological and echocardiographic assessment for associated anomalies"],"redFlags":["Severe symptomatic anaemia requiring transfusion in an infant","Uncontrolled hyperglycaemia or diabetic ketoacidosis","Rapidly progressive hearing loss","Cardiac decompensation from associated structural defects"],"prognosis":"Thiamine supplementation typically corrects the anaemia and improves diabetes control, but sensorineural deafness is usually irreversible and lifelong treatment with monitoring is needed to maintain remission.","pitfalls":["Treating the anaemia with B12 or folate and concluding the marrow is unresponsive","Failing to link the anaemia, diabetes and deafness as one syndrome","Stopping thiamine once counts normalise, allowing relapse","Missing the diagnosis in a child labelled as having early-onset diabetes alone"],"management":["Start and continue lifelong high-dose thiamine supplementation, which drives residual low-affinity transport to correct the anaemia and improve diabetes control","Recognise that the anaemia does not respond to vitamin B12 or folate, so thiamine is the specific treatment","Treat the non-autoimmune diabetes with insulin as it becomes insulin-dependent, adjusting as thiamine improves early glycaemic control","Provide hearing aids or cochlear implantation for the progressive sensorineural deafness, which is usually irreversible","Monitor the full blood count, glycated haemoglobin and hearing, and screen for associated cardiac and ophthalmological anomalies","Offer genetic counselling and testing for biallelic SLC19A2 variants, with screening of at-risk relatives given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Thiamine-Responsive Megaloblastic Anemia Syndrome","OMIM entry for SLC19A2","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"thiel_behnke_corneal_dystrophy":{"name":"Thiel-Behnke corneal dystrophy","system":"Eye / Cornea","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["corneal dystrophy of Bowman layer type 2","honeycomb corneal dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / corneal dystrophy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant Bowman layer dystrophy, generally associated with the TGFBI gene","Produces a characteristic honeycomb pattern of subepithelial opacities","Presents in childhood or adolescence with recurrent corneal erosions","It runs a more slowly progressive and generally milder course than Reis-Bucklers dystrophy","Distinguishing it from Reis-Bucklers can require correlation of clinical, imaging, histological and genetic findings","Recurrent erosions are managed conservatively, with superficial ablation for visually significant opacity"],"pathophysiology":"Thiel-Behnke corneal dystrophy is a Bowman layer dystrophy, in most families linked to mutations in the TGFBI gene (distinct in phenotype from Reis-Bucklers). Abnormal protein deposition disrupts the Bowman layer, producing a honeycomb pattern of subepithelial opacities and impairing epithelial adhesion, which leads to recurrent corneal erosions from childhood. Opacification progresses slowly, gradually affecting vision, and, as with other anterior TGFBI dystrophies, the abnormality can recur after superficial treatment or grafting.","additionalDifferentials":["Reis-Bucklers corneal dystrophy","Granular and lattice corneal dystrophies","Map-dot-fingerprint dystrophy","Recurrent erosion from other cause","Anterior corneal scarring"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination of the honeycomb subepithelial opacities","TGFBI genetic testing to confirm and distinguish from Reis-Bucklers","Anterior segment imaging and topography","Assessment of erosion frequency and visual impact","Family history and examination of relatives"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual loss from opacification","Infective keratitis complicating an erosion","Recurrence within a graft","Frequent disabling erosions"],"prognosis":"The course is slowly progressive and milder than Reis-Bucklers; vision is often preserved for longer, and superficial ablation can help, though recurrence is possible.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish it from Reis-Bucklers, which affects prognosis and surgical planning","Choosing penetrating keratoplasty when a superficial approach suffices","Missing infective keratitis during erosions","Overlooking the family history"],"management":["Manage recurrent corneal erosions conservatively with ocular lubricants, hypertonic saline, bandage contact lenses and prophylactic topical antibiotics during epithelial breakdown","Treat visually significant subepithelial opacity with superficial ablation — phototherapeutic keratectomy or superficial keratectomy","Reserve lamellar keratoplasty for advanced opacification not amenable to superficial treatment","Counsel that the dystrophy can recur in the treated cornea or graft, so plan for possible repeat procedures","Monitor visual acuity and erosion frequency at the slit lamp with anterior-segment imaging","Offer genetic counselling and examination of relatives, as this is an autosomal dominant TGFBI-associated dystrophy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","IC3D classification of corneal dystrophies"],"crossLinks":[]},"thiemann_disease":{"name":"Thiemann disease","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Thiemann epiphyseal osteochondrosis","osteochondrosis of the phalangeal epiphyses"],"chapter":null,"source":"Orthopaedic and rheumatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare osteochondrosis affecting the epiphyses of the phalanges of the hands (and sometimes toes)","It typically presents in adolescence with painless or mildly painful swelling of the interphalangeal joints","There may be a familial (autosomal dominant) pattern in some cases","Radiographs show irregularity, fragmentation and flattening of the phalangeal epiphyses","It can be mistaken for juvenile inflammatory arthritis, but inflammatory markers are normal and there is no true synovitis","The course is usually benign, though residual joint deformity or early osteoarthritis may occur"],"pathophysiology":"Thiemann disease is an osteochondrosis in which the epiphyses of the phalanges undergo avascular change with disturbed endochondral ossification during growth. The affected epiphyses become fragmented and flattened before reconstituting, and the process is thought to reflect ischaemia of the growing epiphysis, sometimes with a hereditary predisposition. The result is enlargement and deformity of the interphalangeal joints, which can lead to residual deformity or secondary degenerative change.","additionalDifferentials":["Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Other osteochondroses","Erosive or inflammatory arthritis","Skeletal dysplasia with epiphyseal involvement","Frostbite epiphyseal injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Radiographs of the hands showing epiphyseal changes","Inflammatory markers and autoantibodies (normal, to exclude arthritis)","Family history and, where relevant, assessment for a hereditary pattern","Clinical assessment of the interphalangeal joints","Exclusion of skeletal dysplasia if widespread"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting true inflammatory arthritis","Widespread epiphyseal disease suggesting a dysplasia","Significant functional impairment or deformity","Atypical distribution"],"prognosis":"The condition is generally benign and self-limiting with skeletal maturity, although some patients are left with digital deformity or early interphalangeal osteoarthritis.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as juvenile idiopathic arthritis and giving unnecessary treatment","Overlooking a hereditary pattern","Confusing it with skeletal dysplasia","Attributing normal-marker joint swelling to inflammation"],"management":["Reassurance and conservative management are the mainstay, as the osteochondrosis is generally benign and self-limiting at skeletal maturity","Provide symptomatic relief with simple analgesia or anti-inflammatories, activity modification and splinting during painful phases","Confirm normal inflammatory markers and autoantibodies to distinguish it from juvenile inflammatory arthritis and avoid unnecessary immunosuppression","Offer hand therapy and physiotherapy to maintain interphalangeal joint function","Monitor radiographically for residual digital deformity or early interphalangeal osteoarthritis","Refer to orthopaedics for persistent deformity, and consider genetic counselling where an autosomal dominant familial pattern is evident"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Orthopaedic reference texts (standard reference)","Paediatric rheumatology references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"thin_basement_membrane":{"name":"Thin basement membrane nephropathy","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBMN","thin basement membrane disease","benign familial haematuria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common cause of persistent or recurrent glomerular (dysmorphic) microscopic haematuria, frequently found in several family members","Often caused by heterozygous mutations in the COL4A3 or COL4A4 genes encoding type IV collagen, the same collagen family affected in Alport syndrome","The hallmark on electron microscopy is diffuse, uniform thinning of the glomerular basement membrane","Most patients have isolated haematuria with normal or only minimal proteinuria, normal blood pressure, and preserved kidney function","Unlike Alport syndrome, it is not typically associated with sensorineural hearing loss or ocular abnormalities","It is largely a diagnosis of exclusion once IgA nephropathy, Alport syndrome, and urological causes have been considered","Biopsy is not always required; the diagnosis is often based on the clinical picture and family history of benign haematuria","Management is usually limited to reassurance and periodic monitoring of blood pressure, proteinuria, and kidney function"],"pathophysiology":"Thin basement membrane nephropathy is a hereditary disorder of the glomerular basement membrane, commonly due to heterozygous mutations in COL4A3 or COL4A4. These genes encode alpha chains of type IV collagen that form the structural scaffold of the basement membrane; the mutations produce a thin but generally intact membrane. The attenuated barrier allows red cells to escape into the urinary space, causing glomerular haematuria, yet the membrane usually retains enough integrity to preserve filtration and limit protein loss. Because the same collagen genes underlie Alport syndrome, TBMN can be viewed as part of a spectrum of type IV collagen disease, and a minority of carriers may show a more progressive course.","additionalDifferentials":["IgA nephropathy","Alport syndrome (X-linked or autosomal)","Urological causes of haematuria (stones, tumours, infection)","Other glomerulonephritides presenting with haematuria","Exercise- or menstruation-related haematuria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine microscopy for dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts","Quantification of proteinuria and monitoring of kidney function","Blood pressure assessment","Family screening for haematuria","Kidney biopsy with electron microscopy in selected cases, and genetic testing for COL4A3/COL4A4 where appropriate"],"redFlags":["Development of significant or rising proteinuria","New hypertension or declining kidney function","Hearing loss or ocular findings suggesting Alport syndrome instead","Gross haematuria requiring urological evaluation"],"prognosis":"The great majority of patients have a benign course with lifelong isolated haematuria and stable kidney function, although a subset with underlying collagen IV mutations may progress and warrants ongoing surveillance.","pitfalls":["Labelling haematuria as benign without excluding Alport syndrome, especially when proteinuria or family kidney failure is present","Missing the overlap with type IV collagen disease and its potential for progression","Failing to check for proteinuria and hypertension on follow-up","Overlooking coexisting IgA nephropathy in a patient with thin membranes"],"management":["Reassurance without specific drug therapy for the typical case of isolated microscopic haematuria with preserved renal function","Institute renoprotection with a renin-angiotensin system inhibitor (ACE inhibitor or ARB) if proteinuria or hypertension develops","Monitor periodically with blood pressure, quantification of proteinuria and renal function to detect the minority who progress","Avoid nephrotoxic drugs and manage cardiovascular risk factors","Screen family members for haematuria and offer genetic testing for COL4A3/COL4A4 variants, given overlap with the Alport spectrum","Refer to nephrology for atypical features — significant proteinuria, declining function, or hearing or ocular signs suggesting Alport syndrome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"thin_basement_membrane_nephropathy":{"name":"Thin basement membrane nephropathy","system":"Renal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBMN","benign familial haematuria","thin basement membrane disease","COL4A3","COL4A4","autosomal dominant Alport-spectrum carrier"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Oxford Handbook of Nephrology and Hypertension","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Thin basement membrane nephropathy typically causes persistent or intermittent isolated microscopic haematuria, often discovered incidentally and frequently familial","It is usually associated with heterozygous variants in the type IV collagen genes COL4A3 or COL4A4, placing it at the mild end of the Alport spectrum","The defining feature on electron microscopy is diffuse thinning of the glomerular basement membrane without the lamellation seen in classic Alport syndrome","Blood pressure, renal function and proteinuria are characteristically normal, and the traditional term benign familial haematuria reflects the usually favourable course","Hearing and eyes are typically unaffected, in contrast to Alport syndrome","Because carriers of COL4A3/COL4A4 variants can occasionally develop proteinuria, hypertension or progressive disease, long-term monitoring is prudent","Family screening and genetic counselling help distinguish it from progressive autosomal Alport disease within the same gene family"],"pathophysiology":"Thin basement membrane nephropathy is a disorder of the type IV collagen network that forms the glomerular basement membrane. Heterozygous variants in COL4A3 or COL4A4, which encode alpha chains of type IV collagen, produce a structurally thinner but largely functional basement membrane. The attenuated membrane is more permeable to red cells, giving rise to persistent glomerular (dysmorphic) microscopic haematuria, but in most people the filtration barrier otherwise functions normally so proteinuria, hypertension and renal impairment are absent or minimal. Because the same genes, when affected biallelically or with certain variants, cause autosomal recessive or dominant Alport syndrome, the condition represents the mild end of a continuous type IV collagen disease spectrum, and a minority of carriers show slow progression over decades.","additionalDifferentials":["Alport syndrome (X-linked or autosomal, with progressive disease and extrarenal features)","IgA nephropathy","Other causes of glomerular haematuria","Urological (non-glomerular) causes of haematuria such as stones or tumours","Post-infectious or other glomerulonephritis","Hypercalciuria or hyperuricosuria causing haematuria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and phase-contrast microscopy to confirm glomerular (dysmorphic) red cells","Quantification of proteinuria (urine albumin-to-creatinine or protein-to-creatinine ratio)","Renal function and blood pressure monitoring over time","Family history and screening of relatives for haematuria","Audiometry and ophthalmological review to exclude Alport features when indicated","Kidney biopsy with electron microscopy, or genetic testing of COL4A3/COL4A4, when the diagnosis or prognosis is uncertain"],"redFlags":["Development of significant proteinuria","Rising blood pressure or declining renal function","Hearing loss or ocular abnormalities suggesting Alport syndrome","Family history of end-stage kidney disease","Visible (macroscopic) or non-glomerular haematuria requiring urological evaluation"],"prognosis":"The great majority of individuals maintain normal renal function lifelong with isolated haematuria, but because COL4A3/COL4A4 carriers occasionally progress, ongoing surveillance for proteinuria, hypertension and renal decline is warranted.","pitfalls":["Labelling the condition entirely benign and discharging without any follow-up","Failing to recognise it as part of the Alport type IV collagen spectrum","Overlooking a urological cause when haematuria is non-glomerular or visible","Not screening the family or offering genetic counselling","Missing early extrarenal features that would reclassify the patient as having Alport syndrome"],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy is needed for isolated glomerular microscopic haematuria; provide reassurance and avoid nephrotoxic agents","Introduce renin-angiotensin blockade with an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin-receptor blocker only if proteinuria or hypertension develops","Periodically monitor blood pressure, urinary protein quantification and renal function to detect the minority who progress along the type IV collagen spectrum","Screen first-degree relatives for haematuria and offer clinical-genetics counselling given the heritable COL4A3/COL4A4 basis","Arrange audiometry and ophthalmological review if extrarenal features raise the possibility of Alport syndrome","Refer to nephrology and escalate investigation (biopsy or COL4A3/COL4A4 genetic testing) if proteinuria, hypertension or declining renal function emerge"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (Feehally, Floege, Tonelli, Johnson)","Expert consensus guidance on Alport spectrum / type IV collagen disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"thiopurine_tpmt_toxicity":{"name":"Thiopurine (TPMT/NUDT15) myelotoxicity","system":"Clinical pharmacology / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thiopurine myelosuppression","azathioprine toxicity","mercaptopurine toxicity","TPMT deficiency","NUDT15 deficiency","thiopurine methyltransferase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Goldman-Cecil Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Thiopurines (azathioprine, mercaptopurine, thioguanine) can cause severe, sometimes life-threatening bone marrow suppression","Patients with low or absent thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT) activity accumulate cytotoxic thioguanine nucleotides and are at high risk of profound myelotoxicity","NUDT15 variants are an additional important genetic cause of thiopurine-induced myelosuppression, particularly relevant in East Asian and Hispanic populations","Pre-treatment testing of TPMT (and where available NUDT15) status guides dosing and identifies patients needing reduced doses or avoidance","Myelosuppression can present with neutropenia, thrombocytopenia or pancytopenia, sometimes with infection or bleeding","Regular full blood count monitoring is essential throughout treatment, as marrow suppression can occur even with normal enzyme activity","Allopurinol markedly increases thiopurine toxicity by blocking xanthine oxidase, an important interaction requiring dose reduction","Management of established toxicity is drug withdrawal, supportive care, and treatment of neutropenic sepsis or bleeding"],"pathophysiology":"Thiopurines are prodrugs metabolised along competing pathways. One route, via hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase, generates cytotoxic thioguanine nucleotides that are incorporated into DNA and mediate both the therapeutic immunosuppressive effect and marrow toxicity. Two enzymes divert drug away from this cytotoxic route: thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT), which methylates the drug, and xanthine oxidase, which oxidises it. Individuals with genetically low TPMT activity shunt more drug into thioguanine nucleotide production, causing accumulation and severe myelosuppression at standard doses. NUDT15 normally dephosphorylates active thioguanine metabolites, and loss-of-function variants similarly increase active drug and marrow toxicity. Inhibition of xanthine oxidase by allopurinol removes another escape route, greatly amplifying toxicity unless the thiopurine dose is reduced.","additionalDifferentials":["Other drug-induced marrow suppression","Bone marrow infiltration or primary haematological malignancy","Aplastic anaemia from other causes","Sepsis-related cytopenias","Nutritional deficiency (B12, folate) causing cytopenias","Viral marrow suppression"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["TPMT enzyme activity or genotype before starting therapy","NUDT15 genotyping where available and indicated","Regular full blood count with differential during treatment","Assessment for drug interactions, especially allopurinol","Thiopurine metabolite levels in specialist monitoring","Bone marrow examination in unexplained or severe cytopenias"],"redFlags":["Severe neutropenia with fever or sepsis","Profound thrombocytopenia with bleeding","Pancytopenia developing after starting or dose-escalating a thiopurine","Concurrent allopurinol without appropriate dose reduction","Rapidly falling counts on routine monitoring"],"prognosis":"Marrow toxicity usually recovers after withdrawal and supportive care, but severe neutropenic sepsis can be fatal. Identifying at-risk genotypes and monitoring counts allows most patients to be treated safely.","pitfalls":["Starting standard doses without checking TPMT (and where relevant NUDT15) status","Co-prescribing allopurinol without reducing the thiopurine dose","Assuming normal enzyme activity removes the need for blood count monitoring","Attributing cytopenias to disease activity rather than drug toxicity"],"management":["Test TPMT activity (and NUDT15 genotype where available) before starting a thiopurine, reducing the dose or avoiding azathioprine, mercaptopurine or thioguanine in deficient patients","Withhold or permanently discontinue the offending thiopurine as soon as significant myelosuppression is detected","Avoid concurrent allopurinol or febuxostat, since xanthine oxidase inhibition diverts drug into cytotoxic thioguanine nucleotides; markedly reduce the thiopurine dose if co-prescription is unavoidable","Give supportive care for neutropenic sepsis with broad-spectrum antibiotics and granulocyte colony-stimulating factor, with platelet and red cell transfusion for bleeding or symptomatic anaemia","Monitor the full blood count regularly throughout treatment, as myelosuppression can occur even with normal enzyme activity","Use thiopurine metabolite level monitoring in specialist practice to guide dose adjustment","Escalate to haematology with bone marrow examination for profound, prolonged or unexplained pancytopenia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","CPIC Guideline for thiopurines and TPMT/NUDT15"],"crossLinks":[]},"thoracic_aortic_aneurysm":{"name":"Thoracic aortic aneurysm","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TAA","thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm","TAAA","ascending aortic aneurysm","descending thoracic aortic aneurysm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most thoracic aortic aneurysms are asymptomatic and discovered incidentally on chest imaging performed for other reasons","Symptoms, when present, reflect local compression and may include chest or back pain, hoarseness, dysphagia, or stridor","Ascending and arch aneurysms are often linked to degeneration of the aortic media, while descending aneurysms are more commonly atherosclerotic","Connective tissue disorders such as Marfan and Loeys-Dietz syndromes, bicuspid aortic valve, and a family history predispose to earlier and more aggressive disease","CT angiography and MR angiography define aneurysm extent, diameter, and involvement of aortic branches for surveillance and operative planning","Repair thresholds are based on maximal diameter, growth rate, symptoms, and underlying aetiology, with lower thresholds for connective tissue disease","Blood pressure control and cardiovascular risk-factor management slow expansion and reduce dissection risk during surveillance","Open surgical and endovascular (thoracic endovascular aortic repair) approaches are selected according to aneurysm location, anatomy, and patient fitness"],"pathophysiology":"Thoracic aortic aneurysm is a permanent localised dilatation of the thoracic aorta resulting from weakening of the aortic wall. Degeneration of medial elastin and smooth muscle, driven by genetic connective tissue disorders, bicuspid valve-associated aortopathy, atherosclerosis, or ageing, reduces wall integrity. According to the law of Laplace, increasing diameter raises wall tension, promoting further expansion in a self-perpetuating cycle. As the wall weakens it becomes vulnerable to acute dissection, in which an intimal tear allows blood to track within the media, and ultimately to rupture.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute aortic dissection","Aortic intramural haematoma or penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer","Mediastinal mass or lymphadenopathy","Aortic tortuosity or pseudoaneurysm","Acute coronary syndrome (for chest pain presentations)","Pulmonary embolism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT angiography of the thoracic aorta for diameter, extent, and branch involvement","MR angiography as a radiation-sparing alternative for surveillance","Transthoracic and transoesophageal echocardiography for the aortic root and valve","Genetic evaluation and family screening when a heritable aortopathy is suspected","Assessment of cardiac and respiratory fitness prior to intervention"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe chest or back pain suggesting acute dissection or impending or actual rupture, a surgical emergency","Haemodynamic instability or syncope indicating rupture or tamponade","New hoarseness, stridor, or dysphagia from rapid enlargement","Rapid interval growth or diameter exceeding operative thresholds"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on aneurysm size, growth rate, and aetiology; small aneurysms often remain stable under surveillance, whereas large or rapidly expanding lesions carry a high risk of dissection or rupture. Elective repair markedly improves survival compared with the very high mortality of rupture, though operative risk varies with aneurysm extent and comorbidity.","pitfalls":["Dismissing incidental thoracic aortic dilatation without arranging appropriate surveillance","Applying general size thresholds to patients with connective tissue disease who warrant earlier repair","Attributing acute chest and back pain solely to cardiac causes and missing dissection","Neglecting family screening in heritable aortopathies"],"management":["Reduce aortic wall stress with antihypertensive therapy, using beta-blockers and, in Marfan syndrome, angiotensin-receptor blockers, alongside smoking cessation and statin therapy","Surveillance with CT or MR angiography to track maximal diameter and growth rate and plan intervention","Elective repair by open graft replacement or thoracic endovascular aortic repair, with thresholds set by diameter, growth, symptoms and aetiology and lowered in connective tissue disease","Advise avoidance of heavy isometric straining and stimulant drugs that provoke blood-pressure surges","Treat acute dissection or rupture as an emergency with urgent blood-pressure and heart-rate control, analgesia and emergency surgical or endovascular intervention","Offer genetic evaluation and family screening when heritable aortopathy such as Marfan, Loeys-Dietz or bicuspid-valve disease is suspected","Manage within a multidisciplinary aortic team spanning cardiothoracic and vascular surgery, cardiology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"thoracic_disc_herniation":{"name":"Thoracic disc herniation","system":"Musculoskeletal / Spine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thoracic disc prolapse","thoracic disc protrusion","herniated thoracic disc","thoracic disc extrusion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Relatively uncommon and often asymptomatic, with symptomatic herniations occurring most frequently in the lower thoracic spine","Presentations range from axial or band-like radicular (intercostal) thoracic pain to myelopathy from cord compression","Because the thoracic canal is relatively narrow and the cord's blood supply is a watershed, central herniations can cause myelopathy with lower-limb spasticity, gait disturbance, and sphincter dysfunction","A sensory level, hyperreflexia, and extensor plantar responses point to cord compression rather than an isolated nerve-root lesion","A high proportion of thoracic disc herniations are calcified, which influences surgical planning","Thoracic disc herniation can mimic intra-abdominal or intrathoracic (cardiac or pulmonary) disease when pain is referred anteriorly","MRI is the investigation of choice, and CT helps characterise disc calcification","Asymptomatic or purely painful herniations are managed conservatively, whereas progressive myelopathy warrants surgical decompression, often by an anterior or lateral approach to avoid cord retraction"],"pathophysiology":"Thoracic disc herniation occurs when nucleus pulposus displaces beyond the annulus in the thoracic spine. The rigid rib cage and the orientation of the facets make this region relatively stable, so herniations are less frequent than in the cervical or lumbar spine, and the lower thoracic levels, where mobility increases towards the thoracolumbar junction, are most often involved. A narrow thoracic canal and the tenuous, watershed vascular supply of the thoracic cord mean that central herniations readily compress the cord and compromise its perfusion, producing myelopathy. A substantial proportion of thoracic herniations calcify and may adhere to or erode through the dura.","additionalDifferentials":["Intercostal neuralgia or rib pathology","Cardiac, pulmonary, or oesophageal disease for referred anterior chest pain","Intra-abdominal pathology such as cholecystitis or renal colic for lower thoracic referral","Spinal tumour or metastasis","Herpes zoster (shingles) in a dermatomal band","Other causes of thoracic myelopathy such as demyelination or an epidural lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the thoracic spine as the investigation of choice with clear level localisation","CT to assess disc calcification and bony detail for surgical planning","Plain radiographs for alignment and to count levels accurately","Careful level marking, correlating with a whole-spine image, to avoid wrong-level surgery","Investigations to exclude visceral mimics when pain is referred anteriorly"],"redFlags":["Signs of myelopathy such as lower-limb weakness or spasticity, gait disturbance, a sensory level, or sphincter dysfunction","Rapidly progressive neurological deficit","Constitutional symptoms suggesting malignancy or infection"],"prognosis":"Most thoracic disc herniations are asymptomatic or cause pain that settles with conservative care; when myelopathy is present, timely decompression can halt progression and often improves function, although established cord damage may not fully recover.","pitfalls":["Wrong-level surgery due to difficulty counting thoracic levels","Underestimating the risk of cord injury with posterior approaches that require cord retraction","Misattributing referred thoracic or abdominal pain to visceral disease and missing the spinal cause"],"management":["Manage axial or radicular pain without myelopathy conservatively with analgesia, activity modification and physiotherapy","Add neuropathic analgesics and consider image-guided intercostal or epidural corticosteroid injection for refractory radicular thoracic pain","Undertake surgical decompression by an anterior, thoracoscopic or posterolateral approach for myelopathy or progressive neurological deficit, tailored to whether the disc is calcified or dural-adherent","Perform meticulous pre-operative level marking correlated with a whole-spine image to avoid wrong-level surgery","Monitor neurological status for evolving cord compression such as lower-limb spasticity, gait disturbance and sphincter dysfunction","Exclude visceral cardiac, pulmonary or intra-abdominal mimics when pain is referred anteriorly","Refer to spinal surgery or neurosurgery for myelopathic or refractory cases and escalate urgently if the deficit progresses rapidly"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rothman-Simeone and Herkowitz's The Spine","Benzel: Spine Surgery: Techniques, Complication Avoidance, and Management"],"crossLinks":["lumbar_disc_herniation","cervical_spondylotic_myelopathy"]},"thoracic_outlet_neurogenic":{"name":"thoracic outlet neurogenic","system":"Nervous system / Peripheral nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neurogenic thoracic outlet syndrome","true neurogenic TOS","Gilliatt-Sumner hand"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["True neurogenic thoracic outlet syndrome is a rare lower-trunk (C8-T1) brachial plexopathy caused by chronic compression, classically by a cervical rib with a taut fibrous band inserting on the first rib","The characteristic Gilliatt-Sumner hand shows thenar wasting that is most marked over abductor pollicis brevis, with lesser hypothenar and interosseous wasting, reflecting predominant T1 and C8 involvement","Sensory symptoms and any sensory loss follow a medial forearm and medial hand (medial antebrachial and ulnar) distribution, and aching pain is common along the medial arm","It occurs most often in young to middle-aged women, is usually unilateral, and should be sharply distinguished from the far commoner disputed or non-specific thoracic outlet syndrome, which lacks objective neurological or electrophysiological signs","The classic electrophysiological signature is a reduced median motor response (thenar wasting) with a reduced ulnar sensory response, plus a relatively preserved median sensory and reduced ulnar motor response, indicating a lower-trunk lesion","Provocative manoeuvres such as the elevated arm stress test and Adson test are suggestive but non-specific and cannot confirm the diagnosis alone","Imaging looks for a cervical rib, an elongated C7 transverse process or a fibrous band; surgical decompression by band and rib resection is indicated for confirmed true neurogenic disease with objective deficits"],"pathophysiology":"The brachial plexus and subclavian vessels traverse the thoracic outlet through the interscalene triangle, the costoclavicular space and the retropectoralis minor space. In true neurogenic thoracic outlet syndrome, a congenital cervical rib or elongated C7 transverse process gives rise to a fibrous band that stretches to the first rib and angulates the lowest components of the plexus, chiefly the T1 fibres and to a lesser extent C8, which form the lower trunk. Chronic traction and compression at this point cause progressive axonal loss preferentially affecting the T1-derived median motor fibres to the thenar muscles, together with C8-T1 sensory fibres, producing the characteristic pattern of thenar-predominant wasting and medial hand sensory loss. This structural, fixed compression contrasts with the intermittent, positional and often purely subjective symptoms of non-specific thoracic outlet syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Ulnar neuropathy at the elbow","C8-T1 radiculopathy from cervical spondylosis or disc","Distal median neuropathy (carpal tunnel syndrome) causing thenar wasting","Pancoast (superior sulcus) tumour infiltrating the lower plexus","Motor neurone disease presenting with focal hand wasting","Disputed or non-specific thoracic outlet syndrome","Brachial plexus tumour such as a schwannoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies demonstrating a low median motor and low ulnar sensory response with relative median sensory preservation","Needle electromyography of C8-T1 muscles including abductor pollicis brevis and first dorsal interosseous to confirm a lower-trunk pattern","Cervical spine and chest radiographs to detect a cervical rib or elongated C7 transverse process","MRI of the brachial plexus and thoracic outlet to identify fibrous bands, a mass or a Pancoast tumour","Vascular studies if concurrent arterial or venous compression is suspected","Cervical spine MRI if radiculopathy is a competing diagnosis"],"redFlags":["A supraclavicular or apical mass, or Horner syndrome, raising concern for a Pancoast tumour","Rapidly progressive weakness or widespread wasting suggesting motor neurone disease","Signs of acute limb ischaemia, distal emboli or subclavian vein thrombosis indicating vascular thoracic outlet compromise","Constitutional symptoms or weight loss suggesting malignancy"],"prognosis":"Surgical resection of the responsible cervical rib and fibrous band typically halts progression and relieves pain, but established thenar wasting and axonal sensory loss often recover only partially. The much commoner non-specific form has a variable outcome and does not carry the same structural prognosis.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing true neurogenic disease on positional symptoms and provocative tests alone, without objective wasting or electrophysiological confirmation","Overlooking a Pancoast tumour as a cause of lower-plexus signs","Confusing thenar wasting with carpal tunnel syndrome by not recognising the accompanying medial hand sensory loss","Mislabelling early motor neurone disease as thoracic outlet syndrome","Operating on patients with the disputed, non-specific form who lack objective neurological deficits"],"management":["Definitive treatment is surgical decompression with resection of the responsible cervical rib and division of the taut fibrous band, usually with first-rib resection, to relieve traction on the lower trunk","Use physiotherapy for posture and shoulder-girdle strengthening as an adjunct and while awaiting surgery","Provide analgesia and neuropathic pain control for the aching medial arm pain","Offer hand and occupational therapy with rehabilitation for thenar wasting and functional loss","Exclude a Pancoast tumour or other mass on brachial plexus imaging before attributing symptoms to a cervical rib","Refer early for surgery and escalate promptly, since established thenar wasting and axonal sensory loss recover only partially once decompression halts progression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Stewart's Focal Peripheral Neuropathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"thoracic_outlet_syndrome":{"name":"Thoracic outlet syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TOS","thoracic outlet syndrome","neurogenic thoracic outlet syndrome","Paget-Schroetter syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy / Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Compression of the neurovascular structures traversing the thoracic outlet, classified into neurogenic, venous, and arterial subtypes according to the structure involved","Neurogenic disease is by far the commonest, causing arm and hand pain, paraesthesiae, and weakness, often with wasting of the thenar or intrinsic hand muscles in advanced true neurogenic cases","Venous disease (Paget-Schroetter or effort thrombosis) presents with acute arm swelling, cyanosis, and distended superficial veins, typically after vigorous or repetitive arm use in young active people","Arterial disease is the rarest but most dangerous, causing hand ischaemia, digital emboli, or a subclavian aneurysm, and is strongly associated with a cervical rib or bony anomaly","Predisposing factors include a cervical rib, anomalous first rib, fibrous bands, and hypertrophied scalene muscles, often with a history of repetitive overhead activity or trauma","Provocative manoeuvres and posture-dependent symptoms aid clinical assessment, but imaging and nerve studies are needed to confirm the subtype","Management ranges from physiotherapy and postural correction in neurogenic disease to anticoagulation with venous decompression in effort thrombosis and prompt surgical decompression and arterial repair in the arterial form"],"pathophysiology":"Thoracic outlet syndrome arises from compression of the brachial plexus, subclavian vein, or subclavian artery as they pass through the confined space bounded by the scalene muscles, the first rib, and the clavicle. Congenital factors such as a cervical rib, an anomalous first rib, or fibrous bands narrow this space, while acquired factors including scalene muscle hypertrophy, poor posture, and post-traumatic scarring can further reduce it. Neurogenic compression of the lower brachial plexus produces sensory and motor symptoms in the arm and hand. Venous compression, aggravated by repetitive arm effort, promotes turbulence, intimal injury, and thrombosis of the subclavian vein, the basis of Paget-Schroetter effort thrombosis. Arterial compression, usually against a bony anomaly, damages the subclavian artery wall and can lead to post-stenotic aneurysm, mural thrombus, and distal embolisation causing hand ischaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Cervical radiculopathy or cervical spondylosis","Carpal or cubital tunnel syndrome and other peripheral entrapment neuropathies","Rotator cuff disease or other shoulder pathology","Complex regional pain syndrome","Raynaud phenomenon or other cause of digital ischaemia","Upper limb deep vein thrombosis of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound with positional manoeuvres to assess subclavian vessel compression or thrombosis","CT or MR angiography and venography to define vascular anatomy and bony anomalies","Chest and cervical spine radiographs to identify a cervical rib or first-rib anomaly","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to characterise neurogenic involvement","Catheter venography or arteriography in venous and arterial subtypes, particularly before intervention","MRI of the brachial plexus and thoracic outlet in diagnostically difficult neurogenic cases"],"redFlags":["Hand or digital ischaemia, or a subclavian aneurysm, indicating arterial thoracic outlet syndrome","Acute painful arm swelling and cyanosis suggesting subclavian vein thrombosis","Progressive muscle wasting and weakness in true neurogenic disease","Distal embolic events threatening the hand or fingers"],"prognosis":"Neurogenic thoracic outlet syndrome often improves with conservative physiotherapy and postural measures, with surgery reserved for refractory or true neurogenic cases. Venous effort thrombosis generally does well with anticoagulation and decompression, while arterial disease requires prompt surgical treatment and, once complicated by embolisation or aneurysm, carries the greatest risk to the limb.","pitfalls":["Overdiagnosing neurogenic disease without objective imaging or neurophysiological support","Missing arterial thoracic outlet syndrome as a source of unexplained hand or digital ischaemia","Failing to identify a cervical rib or bony anomaly driving vascular compression","Treating effort thrombosis with anticoagulation alone without considering decompression","Confusing the syndrome with cervical radiculopathy or distal nerve entrapment"],"management":["Confirm the subtype with imaging and nerve studies first, since neurogenic, venous and arterial disease need different treatment","Treat the neurogenic subtype initially with physiotherapy, postural correction and activity modification, reserving surgical decompression by scalenectomy and first-rib or cervical-rib resection for refractory or true neurogenic cases","Manage venous effort thrombosis (Paget-Schroetter) with anticoagulation and catheter-directed thrombolysis followed by thoracic outlet decompression","Treat the arterial subtype with resection of the bony anomaly and repair or reconstruction of the subclavian artery, with thromboembolectomy for distal emboli","Provide analgesia and neuropathic pain control for neurogenic symptoms","Escalate arterial disease urgently given the risk of hand ischaemia, digital embolism and limb loss","Manage within a multidisciplinary team of vascular medicine, vascular or thoracic surgery and neurology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"three_m_syndrome":{"name":"3M syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["three-M syndrome","CUL7/OBSL1/CCDC8-related primordial short stature","gloomy face syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive primordial growth disorder causing pre- and postnatal proportionate short stature with normal intelligence","Caused by biallelic mutations in one of three genes: CUL7, OBSL1 or CCDC8 (the initials of which give the syndrome its name)","Birth length and weight are low and severe short stature persists, but head circumference is relatively preserved, giving a relatively large head appearance","Facial features include a triangular face, a prominent forehead, a fleshy-tipped nose, full lips and a pointed chin (historically described as a 'gloomy' facial appearance)","Skeletal features include slender long bones with thin cortices, tall (flat, wedge-shaped) vertebral bodies and slender ribs on radiographs","Growth-hormone testing is usually normal and children generally respond poorly to growth-hormone therapy, distinguishing it from classic growth-hormone deficiency","It is an important differential of Silver-Russell syndrome, but 3M syndrome lacks the marked body asymmetry and has a distinct radiographic and molecular basis","Diagnosis is confirmed by molecular testing of CUL7, OBSL1 and CCDC8"],"pathophysiology":"3M syndrome is caused by loss of function of components of a protein complex (CUL7, OBSL1 and CCDC8) that regulates cell growth, cytoskeletal organisation and protein turnover, and interacts with pathways controlling growth and the response to growth factors. CUL7 is part of an E3 ubiquitin-ligase complex, while OBSL1 and CCDC8 form a scaffold that maintains the stability and localisation of the complex. Disruption impairs normal cellular growth signalling from before birth, producing severe primordial short stature with characteristic skeletal changes, while sparing cognitive development.","additionalDifferentials":["Silver-Russell syndrome (asymmetry, feeding difficulty)","Growth-hormone deficiency and insensitivity (Laron syndrome)","IGF1 or IGF1-receptor defects","Mulibrey nanism (TRIM37, primordial short stature)","Microcephalic primordial dwarfism syndromes (e.g. MOPD, though head size differs)","Other proportionate short-stature syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of CUL7, OBSL1 and CCDC8 to confirm the diagnosis","Serial growth measurements with attention to relatively preserved head circumference","Skeletal radiographs to document slender long bones, tall vertebrae and slender ribs","Growth-hormone and IGF-1 axis assessment to exclude classic growth-hormone deficiency","Assessment for hypogonadism where indicated in affected males","Genetic counselling given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"redFlags":["Failure to thrive or feeding difficulty in infancy requiring nutritional support","Misdiagnosis leading to prolonged, ineffective growth-hormone treatment","Airway or anaesthetic concerns related to facial and skeletal features","Associated hypogonadism affecting pubertal development"],"prognosis":"Cognition and life expectancy are normal; the main issue is marked short stature, which responds poorly to growth hormone, so management focuses on accurate diagnosis, realistic counselling and monitoring for skeletal and endocrine issues.","pitfalls":["Labelling the child as growth-hormone deficient and expecting a good response to therapy","Confusing 3M syndrome with Silver-Russell syndrome without noting the absence of asymmetry","Overlooking the characteristic radiographic features that support the diagnosis","Failing to recognise autosomal recessive inheritance and its recurrence risk"],"management":["There is no effective disease-specific pharmacotherapy; growth-hormone therapy is generally ineffective, so counsel families realistically rather than raising expectations","Undertake serial growth monitoring with attention to the relatively preserved head circumference and proportionate short stature","Assess for and manage hypogonadism in affected males","Provide skeletal surveillance for slender long bones and vertebral changes, with orthopaedic input as needed","Exercise anaesthetic caution during procedures given the facial features and small stature that may complicate the airway","Offer genetic counselling for the autosomal recessive inheritance of CUL7, OBSL1 and CCDC8 variants, with testing of at-risk relatives","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary skeletal-dysplasia and paediatric endocrine service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: 3-M syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"thromboangiitis_obliterans":{"name":"thromboangiitis obliterans","system":"Cardiovascular and vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Buerger disease","Buerger's disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A non-atherosclerotic, segmental inflammatory occlusive disease affecting the small and medium arteries and veins of the extremities.","Occurs almost exclusively in tobacco users, characteristically young men, though incidence in women is rising with changing smoking patterns.","Presents with distal extremity ischaemia — digital ischaemia, foot or instep claudication, rest pain, ulceration and gangrene.","Superficial migratory thrombophlebitis and Raynaud phenomenon are common accompanying features.","Both upper and lower limbs are typically involved, and the Allen test is frequently abnormal, so the hands must always be examined.","Angiography classically shows segmental occlusions with 'corkscrew' collaterals and normal, smooth proximal vessels free of atheroma.","Complete and permanent tobacco cessation, including smokeless products and nicotine replacement, is the only proven measure that halts progression and prevents amputation.","Diagnosis is essentially one of exclusion, guided by criteria such as those of Olin and Shionoya."],"pathophysiology":"Thromboangiitis obliterans is characterised by a highly cellular, inflammatory occlusive thrombus within small and medium arteries and veins, with relative preservation of the vessel wall and internal elastic lamina, distinguishing it from both atherosclerosis and classic necrotising vasculitis. Tobacco exposure is central: constituents of tobacco are thought to provoke endothelial dysfunction and an immune-mediated inflammatory response, possibly involving hypersensitivity to tobacco antigens and impaired endothelium-dependent vasodilatation. The result is segmental thrombotic occlusion of distal vessels with sparing of proximal arteries.","additionalDifferentials":["Atherosclerotic peripheral arterial disease","Thromboembolic arterial occlusion","Systemic vasculitides such as polyarteritis nodosa or ANCA-associated vasculitis","Connective tissue disease vasculopathy (systemic sclerosis, systemic lupus erythematosus)","Antiphospholipid syndrome and hypercoagulable states","Popliteal artery entrapment or adventitial cystic disease","Ergotism, cocaine or cannabis-associated arteritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography and proximal arterial imaging to exclude an embolic source","Autoimmune and vasculitis serology (antinuclear antibodies, ANCA, complement, rheumatoid factor)","Antiphospholipid antibodies and a thrombophilia screen","Catheter or CT/MR arteriography of both upper and lower limbs seeking corkscrew collaterals","Urine and serum toxicology for cocaine and cannabis","Screening for diabetes and hyperlipidaemia to exclude atherosclerotic disease"],"redFlags":["Ischaemic rest pain or tissue loss with digital ulceration or gangrene","Ascending infection or spreading cellulitis of an ischaemic digit","Continued tobacco use despite established disease","Rapidly progressive multi-limb ischaemia"],"prognosis":"Limb prognosis is excellent with complete tobacco abstinence, whereas continued smoking leads to recurrent ischaemia and a high risk of amputation; overall life expectancy is close to normal as the disease spares the coronary and cerebral circulations.","pitfalls":["Misattributing distal ischaemia in a young smoker to premature atherosclerosis.","Underestimating ongoing nicotine exposure from smokeless tobacco or nicotine replacement as a driver of progression.","Failing to consider the diagnosis in young women.","Examining only the lower limbs and missing upper-limb and Allen test involvement."],"management":["Complete and permanent cessation of all tobacco and nicotine exposure is the definitive disease-modifying intervention and the single strongest determinant of limb salvage","Give intravenous prostanoids such as iloprost for critical ischaemia, ischaemic rest pain and ulceration","Provide meticulous foot and wound care, analgesia and protection from cold and trauma to the affected limbs","Manage the accompanying superficial migratory thrombophlebitis and Raynaud phenomenon","Recognise that conventional surgical revascularisation is rarely feasible because of distal, segmental disease; consider sympathectomy or intermittent pneumatic compression for refractory ischaemia","Reserve amputation for intractable gangrene or infection","Coordinate multidisciplinary vascular and intensive smoking-cessation support and escalate promptly for critical limb ischaemia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","Circulation"],"crossLinks":[]},"thrombocytopenia_absent_radius":{"name":"Thrombocytopenia-absent radius syndrome","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TAR syndrome","TAR","RBM8A-related thrombocytopenia","radial aplasia-thrombocytopenia syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / Nathan and Oski's Hematology of Infancy and Childhood","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by bilateral absence of the radii with characteristically preserved thumbs, an important distinction from Fanconi anaemia in which thumbs are typically abnormal","Thrombocytopenia is present from birth or early infancy and is usually most severe in the first year, then tends to improve with age","Most cases involve a low-frequency null allele of RBM8A together with a common hypomorphic non-coding variant on the other allele, giving a compound inheritance pattern","Cow's milk protein intolerance can precipitate acute drops in the platelet count and gastrointestinal symptoms","Other skeletal and cardiac anomalies may accompany the radial defect, but the humeri and thumbs are relatively spared","Bleeding risk is greatest in infancy and platelet transfusion support is used for significant haemorrhage or before procedures","Diagnosis rests on the clinical combination of radial aplasia with preserved thumbs plus thrombocytopenia, confirmed by RBM8A analysis"],"pathophysiology":"TAR syndrome is a disorder of megakaryopoiesis and limb development linked to reduced function of RBM8A, a gene encoding a core component of the exon junction complex that regulates messenger RNA processing. Affected individuals typically inherit a rare loss-of-function allele on one chromosome and a common low-expressing regulatory variant on the other, so the gene product is insufficient in the tissues that depend most on it. The resulting deficiency impairs megakaryocyte production and maturation, producing a hypomegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia, while parallel effects on limb morphogenesis cause bilateral radial aplasia with sparing of the thumbs. Unlike the classic inherited bone-marrow failure syndromes, the thrombocytopenia is usually isolated and tends to remit over the first years of life rather than progressing to pancytopenia.","additionalDifferentials":["Fanconi anaemia (radial anomalies with absent or hypoplastic thumbs)","Congenital amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia (MPL)","Amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia with radioulnar synostosis","Holt-Oram syndrome","VACTERL association","Neonatal alloimmune or autoimmune thrombocytopenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film confirming thrombocytopenia with otherwise preserved lineages","Bone marrow examination showing reduced or absent megakaryocytes","Upper limb and skeletal radiographs documenting radial aplasia with preserved thumbs","RBM8A molecular analysis including the null allele and the common non-coding variant","Echocardiography and renal imaging for associated anomalies","Chromosome breakage testing where Fanconi anaemia needs to be excluded"],"redFlags":["Significant haemorrhage, including intracranial bleeding, during the high-risk first year of life","Acute fall in platelet count associated with cow's milk exposure or intercurrent infection","Evolving pancytopenia, which should prompt reconsideration of an alternative marrow-failure diagnosis","Abnormal thumbs or positive chromosome breakage suggesting Fanconi anaemia instead"],"prognosis":"The thrombocytopenia is typically most dangerous in infancy and generally improves toward near-normal platelet counts in later childhood, so with careful support through the early high-risk period the long-term haematological outlook is good.","pitfalls":["Confusing TAR with Fanconi anaemia; checking the thumbs and performing breakage testing avoids this error","Overlooking cow's milk protein intolerance as a trigger for acute thrombocytopenic episodes","Assuming persistent severe thrombocytopenia, when counts usually improve over time","Neglecting associated cardiac or renal anomalies during the neonatal assessment"],"management":["Support significant haemorrhage and cover procedures with platelet transfusion, concentrating vigilance on the high-risk infancy period","Exclude cow's milk protein from the diet, since it can precipitate acute falls in the platelet count and gastrointestinal symptoms","Avoid antiplatelet agents and unnecessary trauma, and manage bleeding episodes promptly","Provide orthopaedic and occupational-therapy management of the bilateral radial aplasia, including adaptive aids and corrective limb surgery","Screen with echocardiography and renal imaging and manage any associated cardiac or renal anomalies","Monitor the full blood count over time, as the thrombocytopenia characteristically improves towards near-normal counts in later childhood","Offer genetic counselling for the RBM8A compound inheritance and testing of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"thrombocytopenia_radioulnar_synostosis":{"name":"Amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia with radioulnar synostosis (MECOM/HOXA11)","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ATRUS","MECOM","HOXA11","amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia with radio-ulnar synostosis","EVI1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / Nathan and Oski","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare congenital marrow-failure disorder combining amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia with proximal radioulnar synostosis (bony fusion limiting forearm rotation)","Caused by mutations in MECOM (EVI1) in most cases, or in HOXA11 in some families","Presents in infancy with thrombocytopenia and bleeding, often with limited pronation and supination of the forearm","The bone marrow characteristically shows reduced or absent megakaryocytes","There is a tendency for the cytopenia to progress to broader bone-marrow failure (aplastic anaemia) over time","Additional features can include clinodactyly, sensorineural hearing loss, and cardiac or renal anomalies","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation is the definitive treatment for evolving marrow failure","Diagnosis integrates the skeletal anomaly, marrow findings, and genetic testing of MECOM and HOXA11"],"pathophysiology":"MECOM (EVI1) and HOXA11 encode transcriptional regulators critical for haematopoietic stem-cell maintenance and megakaryocyte development, and MECOM also has roles in limb patterning. Pathogenic mutations impair the transcriptional programme that sustains stem-cell self-renewal and megakaryopoiesis, producing amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia that may extend to trilineage marrow failure. The overlapping developmental role in the upper limb explains the associated proximal radioulnar synostosis, linking the haematological and skeletal manifestations to disruption of shared regulatory pathways.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia (MPL mutations)","Thrombocytopenia-absent radius (TAR) syndrome","Fanconi anaemia and other inherited bone-marrow failure syndromes","Diamond-Blackfan anaemia (when broader cytopenias are considered)","Radioulnar synostosis without haematological disease","Acquired aplastic anaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and film documenting thrombocytopenia and any additional cytopenias","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine showing reduced or absent megakaryocytes","Forearm and elbow radiographs to demonstrate proximal radioulnar synostosis","Genetic testing of MECOM and HOXA11","Audiological, cardiac, and renal assessment for associated anomalies","Cytogenetics and marrow surveillance for evolving failure or clonal change"],"redFlags":["Progression from isolated thrombocytopenia to pancytopenia indicating evolving marrow failure","Severe bleeding from profound thrombocytopenia","Emergence of clonal cytogenetic abnormalities or dysplasia","Signs of an associated anomaly such as significant cardiac or renal dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Outlook is determined by progression to marrow failure; with vigilant monitoring and timely allogeneic stem-cell transplantation, outcomes can be good, whereas untreated evolving aplasia carries substantial risk.","pitfalls":["Attributing the thrombocytopenia to immune causes and overlooking the skeletal clue of limited forearm rotation","Failing to anticipate and monitor for progression to full marrow failure","Not screening for associated cardiac, renal, and hearing abnormalities","Missing the diagnosis when radioulnar synostosis is subtle or not actively sought"],"management":["Allogeneic haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation is the definitive treatment for progressive marrow failure","Support bleeding and thrombocytopenia with platelet transfusion and give red cell transfusion as trilineage marrow failure evolves","Undertake regular full blood count and bone marrow surveillance, including cytogenetics, for progression to aplasia or clonal change","Provide orthopaedic and occupational-therapy management of the proximal radioulnar synostosis that limits forearm rotation","Assess and manage associated sensorineural hearing loss and cardiac or renal anomalies","Offer genetic counselling and family testing for MECOM (EVI1) or HOXA11 variants","Refer early to a bone-marrow-failure and transplant centre and escalate at the first signs of evolving trilineage failure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"thrombophilia":{"name":"Thrombophilia","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hypercoagulable state","inherited thrombophilia","prothrombotic state"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AABB Technical Manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Thrombophilia denotes an inherited or acquired tendency to venous, and less often arterial, thrombosis","Common inherited causes include factor V Leiden and the prothrombin G20210A variant; deficiencies of antithrombin, protein C, and protein S are rarer but more strongly prothrombotic","Acquired causes include the antiphospholipid syndrome, malignancy, pregnancy, oestrogen therapy, nephrotic syndrome, and immobility","Clinical features that suggest an underlying thrombophilia include venous thromboembolism at a young age, recurrent events, unusual sites such as cerebral or splanchnic veins, a strong family history, and warfarin-induced skin necrosis","Testing rarely changes acute management and is confounded by the acute event, anticoagulation, and pregnancy, so it should be timed carefully and directed by whether the result will alter decisions","Antithrombin, protein C, and protein S levels are unreliable during acute thrombosis and while on anticoagulation, whereas genetic tests for factor V Leiden and prothrombin variants are unaffected","Management centres on anticoagulation for events and on risk assessment for prophylaxis during high-risk periods rather than on the laboratory label itself"],"pathophysiology":"Thrombosis arises when the balance of Virchow's triad shifts toward clot formation through hypercoagulability, stasis, or endothelial injury. Inherited thrombophilias tilt this balance by either increasing procoagulant activity or reducing natural anticoagulant function: factor V Leiden and prothrombin variants enhance clotting, while deficiencies of antithrombin, protein C, and protein S remove restraints on thrombin generation. Acquired states add further procoagulant stimuli, such as antiphospholipid antibodies, tumour-derived tissue factor, or oestrogen effects. Most thrombotic events reflect the interaction of one or more of these predispositions with a transient provoking factor.","additionalDifferentials":["Antiphospholipid syndrome","Occult malignancy","Myeloproliferative neoplasm including JAK2-associated disease","Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria","Provoked thrombosis from surgery, immobility, or hormonal therapy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for factor V Leiden and prothrombin G20210A","Functional antithrombin, protein C, and protein S assays timed away from acute events and anticoagulation","Antiphospholipid antibody panel including lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, and anti-beta-2-glycoprotein I","Full blood count and JAK2 testing where a myeloproliferative disorder is suspected","Age-appropriate malignancy evaluation in unprovoked events"],"redFlags":["Extensive or life-threatening thrombosis such as massive pulmonary embolism or cerebral venous thrombosis","Thrombosis at unusual sites suggesting an underlying myeloproliferative disorder or malignancy","Skin necrosis after starting a vitamin K antagonist, suggesting protein C or S deficiency","Recurrent pregnancy loss or arterial events raising concern for antiphospholipid syndrome"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the specific defect and on whether events are provoked; many people with common low-risk variants never have a thrombosis, whereas antithrombin deficiency and antiphospholipid syndrome carry higher recurrence risk and may warrant extended anticoagulation.","pitfalls":["Testing during the acute event or while on anticoagulation, producing misleading levels","Allowing a positive result to prolong anticoagulation unnecessarily when clinical risk is low","Overlooking acquired causes such as malignancy or antiphospholipid syndrome by focusing only on inherited defects","Testing asymptomatic relatives without clear benefit or counselling"],"management":["Treat acute venous thromboembolism with anticoagulation, using initial low-molecular-weight heparin followed by a direct oral anticoagulant or a vitamin K antagonist","Use a vitamin K antagonist rather than a direct oral anticoagulant in antiphospholipid syndrome, and consider extended or indefinite anticoagulation for high-risk defects such as antithrombin deficiency, antiphospholipid syndrome or recurrent unprovoked events","Provide low-molecular-weight heparin thromboprophylaxis during high-risk periods such as surgery and immobility, and avoid oestrogen-containing contraceptives","Time thrombophilia testing away from the acute event and anticoagulation, since it rarely alters acute management and functional assays are unreliable during these periods","Manage pregnancy with heparin-based prophylaxis under specialist obstetric-haematology care, adding low-dose aspirin with heparin in obstetric antiphospholipid syndrome","Offer family counselling and testing for inherited defects where the result will change decisions","Undertake age-appropriate malignancy evaluation after an unprovoked event"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"thrombotic_renovascular_disorders":{"name":"Thrombotic Renovascular Disorders","system":"Renal/Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Thrombotic microangiopathy of the kidney","Renal TMA","Renal microvascular thrombotic disorders"],"chapter":329,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2444","clinicalPearls":["Thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA) is a pathologic lesion of endothelial injury in terminal arterioles and capillaries, with platelet and hyaline thrombi causing partial or complete occlusion; it is usually but not always accompanied by microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA) featuring thrombocytopenia and schistocytes.","Renal histology of TMA shows swollen endocapillary cells (endotheliosis), fibrin thrombi, platelet plugs, arterial intimal fibrosis, and a membranoproliferative glomerular pattern; fibrin thrombi may extend into the arteriolar pole producing glomerular collapse and at times cortical necrosis, and recovered kidneys may develop secondary focal segmental glomerulosclerosis.","The classic TTP pentad (MAHA, thrombocytopenia, neurologic symptoms, fever, renal failure) is rare: fewer than 5% of immune-mediated TTP cases show the full pentad, so do not wait for all five to act.","TTP is defined as MAHA associated with ADAMTS13 activity below 5-10%; ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers accumulate, form clots, and shear erythrocytes, but loss of ADAMTS13 alone may need an additional trigger (infection, surgery, pancreatitis, pregnancy) to provoke clinical disease.","Atypical (complement-mediated) HUS reflects alternative complement pathway dysregulation; affected patients often have low C3 with normal C4, and factor H deficiency is the most common defect, with more than 70 factor H gene mutations described.","Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (STEC) HUS, classically D+ (diarrhea-associated) HUS, mostly affects children under 5 but can affect adults; bloody diarrhea precedes MAHA within one week in over 80% of cases, fever is typically absent, and neurologic involvement is common, especially in adults.","Scleroderma renal crisis presents with acute kidney injury, abrupt-onset hypertension, and a normal urine sediment (though none is strictly required); it occurs in about 12% of diffuse but only ~2% of limited systemic sclerosis, and its renal histology of 'onion-skinning' arcuate artery intimal/medial proliferation is indistinguishable from malignant hypertension.","Antiphospholipid syndrome produces a predisposition to arterial and venous thrombosis and recurrent fetal loss; 'triple positivity' (lupus anticoagulant, anti-beta-2 glycoprotein I, and anticardiolipin antibodies) carries the highest thrombosis risk, and APLS is the leading cause of stroke in patients under 45.","HELLP syndrome (hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets) is a microvascular pregnancy complication; the defining organ is the liver (subcapsular hematoma with potential rupture), renal failure occurs in about half of patients, and proteinuria is present in 86-100%.","Renal vein thrombosis can present with flank pain, tenderness, hematuria, rapid renal decline, and proteinuria, or be silent and discovered during a pulmonary embolism workup; the left renal vein is more commonly involved and two-thirds of cases are bilateral.","Sickle cell nephropathy arises from occlusion of the vasa recta in the renal medulla, where low oxygen tension and high osmolarity favor hemoglobin S polymerization and sickling; sequelae include hyposthenuria, hematuria, and papillary necrosis (which can also occur in sickle trait).","Drug-induced TMA occurs via two mechanisms: dose-dependent toxic/endothelial damage (chemotherapeutics such as proteasome inhibitors, mitomycin C, gemcitabine; immunosuppressives such as calcineurin inhibitors, interferon, sirolimus) or non-dose-dependent drug-induced autoantibodies (e.g., quinine, which can react even after a single re-exposure)."],"pathophysiology":"Thrombotic renovascular disorders share endothelial injury of the renal microvasculature (terminal arterioles and capillaries) leading to platelet/hyaline thrombi and microangiopathic hemolysis. In TTP, deficient ADAMTS13 activity (below ~5-10%, from congenital mutation in cTTP/Upshaw-Schulman syndrome or acquired autoantibody in iTTP) allows ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers to accumulate, forming platelet-rich thrombi that shear red cells; an inflammatory trigger is often required to manifest disease. In atypical HUS, dysregulation of the alternative complement pathway (commonly factor H deficiency, or defects in factor I, factor B, CD46/membrane cofactor protein, C3, CFHR1/3/5, thrombomodulin, or autoantibody to factor H in DEAP HUS) causes complement-mediated endothelial damage. STEC HUS results from Shiga toxins (Stx1/Stx2, verotoxins) binding the glycolipid receptor Gb3 on renal microvascular cells, inducing inflammatory cytokines (IL-8, MCP-1, SDF-1) and chemokine receptors (CXCR4/CXCR7) with platelet aggregation; Streptococcus pneumoniae neuraminidase can expose the cryptic Thomsen-Friedenreich antigen so preformed IgM triggers MAHA. Scleroderma renal crisis features arcuate artery intimal/medial proliferation with onion-skinning, fibrinoid necrosis, and thrombosis. Antiphospholipid syndrome causes intralobular fibrous intimal hyperplasia and arterial/arteriolar occlusion. Renal vein thrombosis results from endothelial damage, venous stasis, or hypercoagulability (notably nephrotic syndrome, especially membranous nephropathy).","additionalDifferentials":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (congenital cTTP and immune iTTP)","Hemolytic-uremic syndrome (STEC/D+ HUS, pneumococcal neuraminidase-associated, atypical complement-mediated/aHUS, DEAP HUS)","Malignant hypertension","Scleroderma renal crisis","Antiphospholipid syndrome (including catastrophic APLS)","Preeclampsia/HELLP syndrome","Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation-associated TMA","Cancer-associated TMA","Drug-induced TMA","POEMS syndrome","Sickle cell nephropathy","Renal vein thrombosis","COVID-19 (SARS-CoV-2) microvascular disease"],"infectionMimics":["Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (EHEC/STEC) HUS","Streptococcus pneumoniae neuraminidase-associated HUS","HIV-related thrombotic microangiopathy (advanced AIDS, low CD4 count)","COVID-19/SARS-CoV-2 microvascular thrombosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Malignant hypertension (histologically indistinguishable from scleroderma renal crisis)","Preeclampsia/HELLP syndrome","Cancer-associated TMA (tumor microvascular obstruction)","Drug-induced TMA","Acute tubular necrosis (contributes to renal failure in HELLP)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral smear for schistocytes and microangiopathic hemolytic anemia","ADAMTS13 activity assay (markedly reduced, below ~5-10%, in TTP; reduced only 30-60% in HELLP)","Platelet count and serial monitoring","LDH (elevated in hemolysis/TMA), haptoglobin (decreased), bilirubin","Direct and indirect Coombs test (negative in microangiopathic hemolysis)","Complement studies including C3 and C4 (low C3 with normal C4 suggests alternative pathway activation in aHUS); complement-regulatory gene mutation testing","Stool studies for Shiga toxin / E. coli O157:H7 in suspected STEC HUS","Antiphospholipid antibody panel: lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, anti-beta-2 glycoprotein I antibodies","Antinuclear antibody pattern and anti-RNA polymerase I/III, anti-U3-RNP for scleroderma renal crisis","Doppler ultrasonography, CT angiography (nearly 100% sensitive), or MR angiography for renal vein thrombosis","Renal biopsy when diagnosis is uncertain (required for HIV-related TMA; recommended in atypical APLS or scleroderma renal involvement without hypertension)","Urinalysis and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (screening in sickle cell nephropathy)","LDH-to-AST ratio (about 13:1 in HELLP/preeclampsia vs about 29:1 without preeclampsia), antithrombin III (decreased in HELLP not TTP), D-dimer (elevated in HELLP not TTP)"],"redFlags":["Neurologic deterioration (seizures, encephalopathy, cerebral infarction) in HUS/TTP, especially in adults","Untreated immune-mediated TTP carries a mortality exceeding 90%, and 20% die within the first month even with modern therapy","Catastrophic antiphospholipid syndrome: rapid onset, multiorgan failure, and severe thrombotic events","Scleroderma renal crisis with cardiac involvement (myocarditis, pericarditis, arrhythmias) indicating an especially poor prognosis, or with pulmonary edema","HELLP syndrome with subcapsular hepatic hematoma/spontaneous liver rupture, cerebral infarction/hemorrhage/edema, or placental abruption","HSCT-associated TMA, which carries a 75% mortality within 3 months","Bilateral renal vein thrombosis with rapid decline in renal function or life-threatening complications"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies by subtype. Untreated immune-mediated TTP has a mortality exceeding 90%, and even with modern therapy about 20% die within the first month from microvascular thrombosis. HSCT-associated TMA is highly lethal, with roughly 75% mortality within 3 months. Scleroderma renal crisis historically had over 90% mortality at 1 month, reduced to about 30% at 3 years with renin-angiotensin system blockade; nearly two-thirds of patients may require dialysis, with renal recovery in about half (median ~1 year). HELLP syndrome carries a mortality of about 7.4-34% and usually resolves spontaneously after delivery. POEMS syndrome rarely progresses to end-stage renal disease. Renal injury in aHUS can be severe and progress to end-stage renal disease, with recurrence risk after transplant depending on the complement-regulatory protein involved. Chronic kidney disease develops in roughly 25-33% of sickle cell nephropathy patients, and nephrotic-range proteinuria predicts progression to renal failure.","pitfalls":["Waiting for the full TTP pentad before acting is a mistake, since fewer than 5% of immune-mediated TTP cases display all five features.","Assuming TMA always shows MAHA can mislead: TMA is usually but not always accompanied by microangiopathic hemolysis, and in antiphospholipid syndrome renal TMA is common while signs of MAHA and platelet consumption are usually absent.","Scleroderma renal crisis is histologically indistinguishable from malignant hypertension and the two can coexist, so renal biopsy is recommended for atypical renal involvement, especially when hypertension is absent.","ADAMTS13 reduction in HELLP (30-60%) is milder than in TTP (below ~10%), so interpreting modest reductions as diagnostic of TTP can mislead during pregnancy when aHUS and TTP can also be triggered.","Thrombocytopenia in HIV-related TMA may prohibit renal biopsy, yet biopsy is required for diagnosis because other HIV-associated renal diseases mimic it; ADAMTS13 activity is not reduced in HIV-related TMA.","In drug-induced autoantibody TMA (e.g., quinine), disease can recur after even a single re-exposure in a previously sensitized patient, so it is not strictly dose-dependent.","Distinguishing HSCT-associated TMA is difficult because thrombocytopenia, anemia, and renal insufficiency are common after transplantation for other reasons."],"management":["Treat thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura urgently with plasma exchange plus corticosteroids, adding the anti-von Willebrand factor agent caplacizumab and rituximab for immune-mediated disease","Treat atypical complement-mediated haemolytic uraemic syndrome with terminal complement blockade using eculizumab, after meningococcal vaccination and antibiotic prophylaxis","Manage Shiga toxin-associated (STEC) haemolytic uraemic syndrome supportively with careful fluid and electrolyte balance and renal replacement therapy, avoiding antimotility agents and generally antibiotics","Provide renal support with dialysis for acute kidney injury, blood-pressure control and management of the microangiopathic haemolysis","Avoid platelet transfusion unless there is life-threatening haemorrhage, as it may aggravate microvascular thrombosis","Monitor serial platelet count, lactate dehydrogenase, haptoglobin, schistocytes, ADAMTS13 activity and complement studies to guide therapy and detect relapse","Refer to specialist nephrology and haematology and offer genetic counselling for complement-regulatory or ADAMTS13 mutations in congenital forms"],"severityClassification":"No single unified severity scale; classification is by etiologic subtype and laboratory criteria. TTP is defined as MAHA with ADAMTS13 activity below 5-10% and subdivided into congenital (cTTP/Upshaw-Schulman) and immune (iTTP) forms. HUS variants include STEC/D+ HUS, pneumococcal neuraminidase-associated HUS, atypical complement-mediated HUS (aHUS), and DEAP HUS. HSCT-associated TMA has formal diagnostic criteria (Harrison Table 329-1): the International Working Group requires schistocytes (over 4% in one set, or RBC fragmentation with at least 2 schistocytes per high-power field), de novo/progressive thrombocytopenia, rising LDH, falling hemoglobin or increased transfusion need, negative Coombs, falling haptoglobin, and concurrent renal/neurologic dysfunction without other explanation. Antiphospholipid syndrome severity is stratified by antibody profile, with 'triple positivity' conferring the highest thrombosis risk and a catastrophic variant defined by rapid multiorgan failure.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2444","Harrison 22e p.2445","Harrison 22e p.2446","Harrison 22e p.2447","Harrison 22e p.2448"],"crossLinks":[]},"thrombotic_thrombocytopenic_purpura":{"name":"thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","system":"Haematological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TTP","Moschcowitz syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hoffbrand's Essential Haematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["TTP results from a severe deficiency of the von Willebrand factor-cleaving protease ADAMTS13, either acquired through an autoantibody or, rarely, congenital (Upshaw-Schulman syndrome).","The classic pentad of fever, microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, renal impairment and fluctuating neurological signs is seldom complete; unexplained microangiopathic haemolysis with thrombocytopenia is sufficient to act.","The blood film is central to diagnosis, showing prominent schistocytes, thrombocytopenia and polychromasia, with a raised lactate dehydrogenase, raised unconjugated bilirubin, low haptoglobin and a negative direct antiglobulin test.","Neurological features characteristically fluctuate and range from headache and confusion to seizures, focal deficits and coma, and may be transient.","TTP is a haematological emergency: plasma exchange should be started urgently on clinical suspicion, without waiting for the ADAMTS13 result to return.","Platelet transfusion is generally avoided unless there is life-threatening haemorrhage, as it may aggravate microvascular thrombosis.","Standard adjuncts to plasma exchange in acquired TTP include corticosteroids, rituximab and the anti-von Willebrand factor agent caplacizumab.","The PLASMIC score aids estimation of the likelihood of severe ADAMTS13 deficiency while the confirmatory assay is awaited, and congenital TTP is managed with plasma infusion rather than exchange."],"pathophysiology":"Deficient activity of ADAMTS13, the metalloprotease that cleaves ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers as they are released from endothelium, allows these hyperadhesive multimers to persist in the circulation. They spontaneously bind and aggregate platelets under high shear, generating platelet-rich microthrombi in the arterioles and capillaries of multiple organs, especially the brain, heart and kidneys. Passage of red cells across these partially occluded, fibrin- and platelet-laden vessels causes mechanical fragmentation and a microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, while platelet consumption produces thrombocytopenia. In acquired disease an IgG autoantibody inhibits or clears ADAMTS13, whereas the congenital form is due to biallelic mutations in the ADAMTS13 gene. Unlike DIC, the coagulation cascade is not primarily activated, so the clotting screen is typically normal.","additionalDifferentials":["haemolytic uraemic syndrome (including Shiga toxin-associated and complement-mediated forms)","disseminated intravascular coagulation","HELLP syndrome and severe pre-eclampsia","malignant hypertension","catastrophic antiphospholipid syndrome","drug-induced thrombotic microangiopathy","disseminated malignancy with microangiopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["blood film and reticulocyte count","lactate dehydrogenase, haptoglobin and unconjugated bilirubin","direct antiglobulin test","ADAMTS13 activity with anti-ADAMTS13 antibody or inhibitor assay","coagulation screen (characteristically normal or near-normal)","renal function and urinalysis","troponin and electrocardiogram to detect cardiac involvement"],"redFlags":["any new or fluctuating neurological deficit, seizure or reduced consciousness","chest pain, arrhythmia or other evidence of cardiac involvement","severe thrombocytopenia with active bleeding","a rapidly rising lactate dehydrogenase indicating ongoing microangiopathy","haemodynamic instability or evolving multi-organ dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Untreated TTP carries a very high mortality, but prompt plasma exchange with immunosuppression allows most patients to survive the acute episode. Acquired TTP is prone to relapse and warrants long-term monitoring of ADAMTS13 activity.","pitfalls":["giving prophylactic platelet transfusions, which may worsen microthrombosis","delaying plasma exchange while awaiting the ADAMTS13 result","confusing TTP with DIC, forgetting that the coagulation screen is usually normal in TTP","overlooking subtle or transient neurological signs and clinically silent cardiac involvement","attributing the picture to sepsis or malignancy and missing a treatable thrombotic microangiopathy"],"management":["Start plasma exchange urgently on clinical suspicion without waiting for the ADAMTS13 result, as it is the definitive mainstay of treatment","Give corticosteroid immunosuppression for acquired autoantibody-mediated disease","Add the anti-von Willebrand factor agent caplacizumab to reduce microthrombosis, with rituximab for refractory or relapsing immune disease","Manage congenital (Upshaw-Schulman) disease with plasma infusion or recombinant ADAMTS13 replacement","Avoid platelet transfusion unless there is life-threatening haemorrhage, since it may aggravate microvascular thrombosis","Provide supportive care with folate, red cell transfusion for symptomatic anaemia and organ support for neurological or cardiac involvement","Monitor ADAMTS13 activity long term under haematology follow-up to detect and pre-empt relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["British Society for Haematology guideline on the diagnosis and management of thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura and other thrombotic microangiopathies","Hoffbrand's Essential Haematology","International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis guidelines on TTP"],"crossLinks":[]},"thymic_carcinoma":{"name":"Thymic carcinoma","system":"Respiratory / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["type C thymoma","malignant thymic epithelial tumour","thymic squamous cell carcinoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Shields' General Thoracic Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An aggressive malignant thymic epithelial tumour that is biologically distinct from and behaves far worse than thymoma","Presents as an anterior mediastinal mass, often with chest pain, cough, breathlessness, or superior vena caval obstruction","Unlike thymoma, it is rarely associated with myasthenia gravis or other paraneoplastic autoimmune syndromes","Shows infiltrative growth with a tendency to invade adjacent structures and to metastasise to lymph nodes and distant sites","Squamous cell carcinoma is the commonest histological subtype, and loss of thymoma-associated markers helps distinguish it pathologically","Complete surgical resection when feasible offers the best chance of cure and is often combined with radiotherapy and chemotherapy","Multimodality therapy is frequently required because many tumours are locally advanced or metastatic at diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Thymic carcinoma arises from the epithelial cells of the thymus but, unlike thymoma, shows overtly malignant cytological features, loss of the organotypic thymic architecture, and an infiltrative, destructive growth pattern. The tumour cells lose the immature T-lymphocyte-rich background characteristic of thymoma, which underlies both the different immunohistochemical profile and the rarity of associated autoimmune syndromes. It invades local mediastinal structures including the pericardium, great vessels, and lung, and disseminates via lymphatics and the bloodstream, so a substantial proportion present with advanced or metastatic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Thymoma","Malignant mediastinal germ cell tumour","Lymphoma involving the anterior mediastinum","Metastasis to the anterior mediastinum","Mediastinal extension of lung carcinoma","Substernal thyroid malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the thorax to assess the mass and local invasion","MRI to clarify vascular or pericardial involvement","PET-CT for staging and detection of nodal and distant metastases","Core or surgical biopsy with immunohistochemistry to distinguish from thymoma and germ cell tumour","Serum germ cell tumour markers to exclude a germ cell tumour in the differential"],"redFlags":["Superior vena caval obstruction with facial and upper limb swelling","Invasion of great vessels, pericardium, or airway on imaging","Distant metastatic disease at presentation","Rapidly enlarging anterior mediastinal mass with constitutional symptoms"],"prognosis":"Thymic carcinoma carries a markedly poorer prognosis than thymoma, driven by frequent local invasion and metastasis; completely resected, early-stage disease has the best outcome, while advanced disease has limited survival despite multimodality treatment.","pitfalls":["Assuming an anterior mediastinal epithelial tumour is an indolent thymoma without adequate histological assessment","Expecting myasthenia gravis to be present and being falsely reassured by its absence","Understaging by omitting PET-CT and missing metastatic disease","Proceeding to incomplete resection without considering neoadjuvant therapy for locally advanced tumours"],"management":["Complete surgical resection by radical thymectomy when feasible offers the best chance of cure","Give adjuvant mediastinal radiotherapy, particularly after incomplete resection or for locally invasive disease","Use platinum-based combination chemotherapy for advanced, unresectable or metastatic disease, sometimes neoadjuvantly to downstage before surgery","Manage local complications such as superior vena cava obstruction","Stage and monitor with contrast CT and PET-CT for nodal and distant metastasis and local recurrence","Provide palliative and supportive care for advanced or progressive disease","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary thoracic oncology team of thoracic surgery, medical oncology and radiation oncology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Shields' General Thoracic Surgery","Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"thymic_cyst":{"name":"Thymic cyst","system":"Respiratory / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["congenital thymic cyst","acquired thymic cyst","cystic thymic lesion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Shields' General Thoracic Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A usually benign fluid-filled cyst of the thymus, presenting in the anterior mediastinum or occasionally the neck","Congenital cysts arise from remnants of the thymopharyngeal duct, while acquired cysts follow inflammation, surgery, or radiotherapy","Most are asymptomatic and found incidentally on imaging performed for another reason","Cross-sectional imaging shows a well-defined, thin-walled, homogeneous fluid-density lesion without a solid enhancing component","A simple, uncomplicated cyst can often be managed conservatively with surveillance","Solid components, thick or irregular walls, or internal nodularity should raise concern for a cystic thymic neoplasm and prompt resection","Surgical excision is both diagnostic and curative when there is diagnostic uncertainty or symptomatic mass effect"],"pathophysiology":"Congenital thymic cysts develop from persistent epithelial remnants of the thymopharyngeal duct along the line of embryological thymic descent from the neck to the anterior mediastinum. Acquired cysts instead form through cystic degeneration provoked by inflammation, previous thoracic surgery or radiotherapy, or an adjacent neoplasm, and are often multilocular. In both cases the cyst is lined by thymic epithelium and filled with clear or proteinaceous fluid, and remains benign unless associated with an underlying tumour such as a thymoma or lymphoma undergoing cystic change.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystic thymoma or thymic carcinoma","Mature cystic teratoma","Lymphangioma or other lymphatic malformation","Bronchogenic or other foregut duplication cyst","Pericardial cyst","Lymphoma with cystic change"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the thorax to confirm fluid density and a thin wall","MRI to characterise cyst contents and exclude a solid component","Ultrasound for cervically extending or superficial lesions","Surgical excision with histology when the diagnosis is uncertain","PET-CT where a cystic neoplasm is suspected"],"redFlags":["A solid, enhancing, or nodular component within the cyst","Thick, irregular walls or internal septation suggesting neoplasm","Rapid enlargement or compressive symptoms","Associated lymphadenopathy raising concern for lymphoma"],"prognosis":"Simple thymic cysts are benign with an excellent prognosis, and complete excision of a symptomatic or indeterminate cyst is curative; the outlook depends on any underlying neoplasm if the cyst proves to be a cystic tumour.","pitfalls":["Assuming every cystic anterior mediastinal lesion is a benign cyst and missing a cystic thymoma or lymphoma","Failing to characterise a solid component on adequate cross-sectional imaging","Aspirating a cyst without excluding malignancy when features are indeterminate","Overlooking that acquired cysts may accompany an adjacent tumour"],"management":["Manage a simple, thin-walled, uncomplicated cyst conservatively with periodic surveillance imaging","Undertake surgical excision with histology for symptomatic, enlarging or diagnostically indeterminate cysts","Resect promptly when solid components, thick or irregular walls or internal nodularity raise concern for a cystic thymoma or lymphoma","Characterise and follow lesions with contrast CT or MRI, adding PET-CT where a cystic neoplasm is suspected","Refer indeterminate anterior mediastinal lesions to thoracic surgery","Direct further management according to any underlying neoplasm if the cyst proves to be a cystic tumour"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Shields' General Thoracic Surgery","Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"thymoma":{"name":"Thymoma","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thymic epithelial tumour","thymic carcinoma","thymoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Thymomas are the most common tumours of the anterior mediastinum in adults and are frequently discovered incidentally on chest imaging","There is a strong association with myasthenia gravis, and a proportion of patients present first with neuromuscular symptoms; other paraneoplastic syndromes include pure red cell aplasia and hypogammaglobulinaemia","Local symptoms, when present, arise from mass effect and include chest pain, cough, dyspnoea, and features of superior vena cava obstruction","Thymic carcinoma is a distinct and more aggressive entity than thymoma, with greater invasiveness, higher metastatic potential, and worse prognosis","Staging emphasises the degree of local invasion beyond the capsule and into surrounding structures, which is the main determinant of behaviour and treatment","Complete surgical resection is the cornerstone of treatment for resectable disease, and completeness of resection strongly influences outcome","Radiotherapy is used adjuvantly for invasive or incompletely resected tumours, and chemotherapy, often platinum-based, is used for advanced or unresectable disease","Patients with associated myasthenia gravis require optimisation of neuromuscular status before surgery to reduce perioperative respiratory risk"],"pathophysiology":"Thymomas are neoplasms of the thymic epithelial cells, typically accompanied by variable numbers of non-neoplastic immature T lymphocytes. They are classified histologically by epithelial morphology and lymphocyte content, while thymic carcinoma shows overt cytological malignancy and loss of organotypic features. The abnormal thymic microenvironment can disrupt immune tolerance, accounting for the frequent paraneoplastic autoimmune phenomena, most notably myasthenia gravis mediated by anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies. Tumours may remain encapsulated or invade the capsule, mediastinal fat, pleura, pericardium, and adjacent great vessels.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphoma involving the anterior mediastinum","Germ cell tumour of the mediastinum","Retrosternal or ectopic thyroid mass","Thymic cyst or thymic hyperplasia","Metastatic disease to the mediastinum","Parathyroid adenoma or other mediastinal masses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the chest to characterise the anterior mediastinal mass and local invasion","MRI to clarify cystic versus solid components and vascular involvement","Testing for anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies and evaluation for myasthenia gravis","Full blood count and immunoglobulin levels to detect associated red cell aplasia or hypogammaglobulinaemia","Biopsy when the diagnosis is uncertain or the tumour appears unresectable, and PET-CT for suspected thymic carcinoma"],"redFlags":["Superior vena cava obstruction with facial swelling and distended neck veins","Respiratory compromise or myasthenic crisis in a patient with a mediastinal mass","Rapidly enlarging or invasive anterior mediastinal mass suggesting thymic carcinoma","New pleural or pericardial effusion indicating advanced spread"],"prognosis":"Encapsulated, completely resected thymomas have an excellent long-term prognosis, whereas invasive tumours and especially thymic carcinoma carry higher recurrence and mortality; degree of invasion and completeness of resection are the key prognostic factors.","pitfalls":["Overlooking underlying myasthenia gravis and precipitating perioperative respiratory failure","Failing to distinguish aggressive thymic carcinoma from indolent thymoma","Biopsying a potentially resectable encapsulated tumour when upfront resection is preferable","Missing associated paraneoplastic cytopenias or immunodeficiency"],"management":["Complete surgical resection by thymectomy is the cornerstone for resectable disease, and completeness of resection strongly drives outcome","Give adjuvant radiotherapy for invasive or incompletely resected tumours","Use platinum-based combination chemotherapy for advanced or unresectable disease and as neoadjuvant therapy to enable resection","Identify and optimise associated myasthenia gravis with acetylcholinesterase inhibitors and immunotherapy before surgery to prevent perioperative myasthenic crisis","Manage other paraneoplastic syndromes, treating pure red cell aplasia with immunosuppression and hypogammaglobulinaemia with immunoglobulin replacement","Undertake long-term surveillance imaging for late recurrence","Coordinate multidisciplinary thoracic oncology and neurology care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroglossal_carcinoma":{"name":"Thyroglossal duct cyst carcinoma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thyroglossal duct carcinoma","carcinoma arising in thyroglossal cyst","thyroglossal cyst carcinoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / WHO Classification of Endocrine Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare malignancy arising within the ectopic thyroid tissue of a thyroglossal duct cyst, usually discovered incidentally after excision of a presumed benign midline neck cyst","Presents as a painless midline neck swelling that classically moves upward with tongue protrusion and swallowing, indistinguishable clinically from a benign cyst","The great majority are papillary carcinomas, reflecting their origin from displaced thyroid follicular tissue along the tract of thyroid descent","Diagnosis is usually histological after Sistrunk excision, since preoperative aspiration rarely identifies the malignancy within an inflamed cyst","When cancer is confirmed, evaluation of the orthotopic thyroid gland is important, as synchronous papillary carcinoma may be present","Management combines the Sistrunk operation with individualised consideration of thyroidectomy, radioactive iodine, and TSH suppression depending on risk features","Prognosis is generally very good, mirroring the favourable behaviour of well-differentiated papillary thyroid carcinoma"],"pathophysiology":"A thyroglossal duct cyst is a remnant of the embryological tract along which the thyroid descends from the foramen caecum of the tongue to its cervical position, and it may retain ectopic thyroid follicular tissue. Malignant transformation of this tissue, most often into papillary carcinoma, gives rise to thyroglossal duct carcinoma. Because the epithelium is of thyroid follicular type, the tumour behaves like a well-differentiated thyroid cancer and can, in a minority of cases, coexist with a primary carcinoma in the orthotopic gland, raising the question of whether disease is truly primary within the cyst or represents metastatic spread.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign thyroglossal duct cyst","Ectopic or lingual thyroid","Dermoid or epidermoid cyst of the midline neck","Enlarged delphian or pretracheal lymph node","Papillary carcinoma of the orthotopic thyroid with nodal spread","Midline branchial or other developmental neck cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Neck ultrasound to characterise the midline cyst and assess the thyroid gland and lymph nodes","Assessment of the orthotopic thyroid for a synchronous primary carcinoma","Histological examination of the Sistrunk excision specimen for definitive diagnosis","Serum TSH and thyroid function to confirm euthyroid status","Serum thyroglobulin as a post-treatment marker when thyroidectomy is performed","Cross-sectional imaging if invasive or nodal disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapid enlargement, fixation, or irregularity of a previously stable midline cyst","Associated cervical lymphadenopathy suggesting nodal spread","A synchronous suspicious nodule in the orthotopic thyroid gland","Invasion of adjacent structures on imaging"],"prognosis":"Outcome is generally excellent given the predominantly papillary, well-differentiated histology, with adverse features being invasive disease, nodal or distant spread, and a coexisting higher-risk primary thyroid cancer.","pitfalls":["Assuming every midline neck cyst is benign and omitting careful histological examination of the excised specimen","Failing to assess the orthotopic thyroid for a synchronous carcinoma once cyst malignancy is found","Relying on preoperative aspiration, which frequently fails to detect malignancy in an inflamed cyst","Performing simple cystectomy rather than the Sistrunk procedure, risking incomplete excision and recurrence"],"management":["The Sistrunk operation, excising the cyst, the whole tract and the central portion of the hyoid, is the primary surgical treatment","Evaluate the orthotopic thyroid gland and consider total thyroidectomy where a synchronous primary carcinoma or high-risk features are present","Give radioactive iodine ablation after thyroidectomy for higher-risk differentiated disease","Provide TSH-suppressive levothyroxine therapy in appropriate risk categories","Assess cervical lymph nodes and undertake neck dissection where nodal disease is confirmed","Follow up with serum thyroglobulin and neck ultrasound for recurrence","Manage within a multidisciplinary endocrine surgery and oncology team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","WHO Classification of Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroglossal_cyst":{"name":"Thyroglossal cyst","system":"Paediatric surgery / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thyroglossal duct cyst","TGDC","thyroglossal duct remnant"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Bailey & Love (paediatric surgery)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common congenital midline neck cyst in children, usually presenting as a painless swelling near or just below the hyoid bone","A characteristic sign is that the lump moves upward on swallowing and on protrusion of the tongue, reflecting its attachment to the tract from the foramen caecum","It arises from remnants of the thyroglossal duct along the path of embryological descent of the thyroid gland","Cysts may present acutely when they become infected, appearing as a tender, red, sometimes discharging midline mass","Before excision it is important to confirm the presence of normally located thyroid tissue, because rarely the cyst contains the only functioning thyroid (ectopic thyroid)","Ultrasound is the preferred first investigation and helps confirm normal thyroid gland location","Definitive treatment is the Sistrunk operation, which removes the cyst together with the central portion of the hyoid bone and the tract up to the tongue base to minimise recurrence","Simple incision or local excision without removing the tract and mid-hyoid leads to high recurrence rates"],"pathophysiology":"During development the thyroid gland descends from the foramen caecum at the tongue base to its final pretracheal position, leaving behind the thyroglossal duct, which normally involutes. Persistence of epithelial remnants along this tract allows a fluid-filled cyst to form anywhere from the tongue base to the thyroid, but most commonly at or near the hyoid bone. Its intimate relationship with the hyoid explains the movement with swallowing and tongue protrusion, and retained secretions or infection cause enlargement and inflammation.","additionalDifferentials":["Ectopic or lingual thyroid","Dermoid cyst of the midline neck","Enlarged or aberrant thyroid nodule","Submental lymphadenopathy","Branchial or other lateral neck cyst (if off midline)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Neck ultrasound to characterise the cyst and confirm a normally sited thyroid gland","Thyroid function tests where ectopic thyroid is a concern","Cross-sectional imaging (CT/MRI) in atypical, recurrent, or deep lesions","Fine-needle aspiration reserved for suspicious or atypical masses","Radionuclide thyroid scan if ectopic functioning tissue must be confirmed"],"redFlags":["Acute infection with abscess formation requiring drainage and antibiotic treatment","A solid or rapidly enlarging component, or the presence of a suspicious nodule, raising the rare possibility of malignancy","Evidence that the cyst represents the only functioning thyroid tissue, which contraindicates simple excision","Airway compromise from a large lingual or tongue-base lesion"],"prognosis":"Following a properly performed Sistrunk procedure the outlook is excellent with low recurrence. Recurrence is more likely after inadequate surgery or when a preceding infection has distorted the tissue planes, and malignant change within the cyst is rare.","pitfalls":["Excising the cyst alone without the mid-hyoid and tract, leading to recurrence","Operating without first confirming a normally located, functioning thyroid gland","Attempting definitive surgery during acute infection rather than controlling infection first","Mistaking a midline dermoid or ectopic thyroid for a simple thyroglossal cyst"],"management":["Definitive treatment is the Sistrunk procedure, excising the cyst, its tract and the central portion of the hyoid, which minimises recurrence","Confirm a normally sited, functioning orthotopic thyroid gland on ultrasound before excision, since the cyst rarely contains the only functioning thyroid tissue","Treat acute infection with antibiotics and drainage of any abscess, allowing inflammation to settle before definitive surgery","Assess thyroid function and provide thyroxine replacement if the orthotopic thyroid is ectopic or absent","Submit the excised specimen for histology given the rare possibility of malignant change","Refer to paediatric surgery or otolaryngology for planned excision"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroglossal_duct_cyst":{"name":"Thyroglossal duct cyst","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Head and Neck","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thyroglossal cyst","thyroglossal tract cyst","midline neck cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common congenital midline neck cyst, arising from remnants of the thyroglossal duct along the thyroid's embryological descent","Typically presents as a smooth, painless midline or paramedian swelling near the hyoid bone, often in childhood","Characteristically moves upward with swallowing and with tongue protrusion because of its attachment to the hyoid and tongue base","May present acutely when infected, becoming tender and enlarged, sometimes discharging via a sinus","Ultrasound is used to characterise the cyst and, importantly, to confirm the presence of a normal orthotopic thyroid gland","Rarely the cyst contains the only functioning thyroid tissue, so demonstrating a normal thyroid before surgery is essential","Definitive treatment is excision including the central portion of the hyoid bone and the tract to the foramen caecum (Sistrunk procedure) to minimise recurrence","Papillary thyroid carcinoma can rarely arise within a thyroglossal duct cyst"],"pathophysiology":"During embryogenesis the thyroid gland descends from the foramen caecum at the tongue base to its final pretracheal position, leaving behind the thyroglossal duct, which normally involutes. Persistence of epithelial remnants of this duct allows a cyst to form anywhere along the tract, most often at or just below the hyoid bone in the midline. Because the tract passes intimately through the body of the hyoid, the cyst elevates with swallowing and tongue protrusion. Retained secretions expand the cyst, and communication with the oral cavity predisposes to infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Dermoid or epidermoid cyst of the midline neck","Ectopic or lingual thyroid tissue","Enlarged delphian or submental lymph node","Thyroid nodule or pyramidal lobe lesion","Branchial cleft cyst when more lateral","Plunging ranula or other cystic neck lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Neck ultrasound to characterise the cyst and confirm a normal orthotopic thyroid gland","Thyroid function tests where ectopic or absent thyroid is a concern","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) in atypical or recurrent cases","Fine-needle aspiration cytology if malignancy or an atypical mass is suspected","Radionuclide thyroid scanning when ectopic functioning tissue must be excluded"],"redFlags":["Solid or irregular components, or fixation, suggesting malignancy within the cyst","Rapid enlargement or associated lymphadenopathy","Acute infection with abscess formation and airway or swallowing compromise","Imaging showing the cyst may contain the only functioning thyroid tissue"],"prognosis":"Excision by the Sistrunk procedure gives a low recurrence rate, whereas simple cystectomy that leaves the tract or hyoid segment recurs more often. Carcinoma within a thyroglossal duct cyst is rare and, when confined, generally has a favourable outlook.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to surgery without confirming a normal thyroid gland and inadvertently removing the only functioning thyroid tissue","Performing simple excision rather than removing the central hyoid and tract, leading to recurrence","Repeated incision and drainage of an infected cyst instead of definitive excision once settled","Overlooking malignant transformation in a cyst with solid components"],"management":["The Sistrunk procedure is the definitive treatment; simple cystectomy that leaves the tract or hyoid segment recurs more often","Confirm a normal, functioning orthotopic thyroid gland on ultrasound before surgery to avoid excising ectopic sole functioning thyroid tissue","Treat acute infection with antibiotics and drainage, delaying definitive excision until the inflammation resolves","Check thyroid function and provide thyroxine replacement where the orthotopic thyroid is ectopic or absent","Examine the excised specimen histologically to exclude rare carcinoma within the cyst","Refer to otolaryngology or head and neck surgery for excision"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroid_cancer":{"name":"Thyroid Nodular Disease and Cancer","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thyroid nodule","thyroid carcinoma","papillary thyroid cancer","medullary thyroid cancer"],"chapter":397,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3040-3050","clinicalPearls":["Thyroid nodules are common: ~3-7% of adults by palpation, but up to 50% have nodules on ultrasound, with the majority <1 cm. The vast majority of nodules are benign (Harrison 22e p.3040, p.3044, p.3048).","Initial workup of a nodule begins with a TSH level. If TSH is suppressed, a radionuclide scan is done; a hyperfunctioning ('hot') nodule is almost never malignant, so FNA is unnecessary. Otherwise proceed to thyroid ultrasound (Harrison 22e p.3048).","Thyroid ultrasound with a validated risk-stratification system (e.g., ACR TI-RADS, TR1-TR5) is used to decide which nodules need fine-needle aspiration (FNA), combining sonographic pattern with nodule size; more suspicious patterns warrant FNA at lower size cutoffs (Harrison 22e p.3042, p.3048).","FNA, ideally ultrasound-guided, is the diagnostic procedure of choice for nodules. Cytology is reported with the six-tier Bethesda System (version 3): benign ~4% malignancy risk, AUS ~22%, follicular neoplasm ~30%, suspicious for malignancy ~74%, malignant ~97% (Harrison 22e p.3044, p.3049).","Papillary thyroid cancer (PTC) is the most common type (80-85% of well-differentiated cancers); classic and follicular variants are usually indolent, spread mainly via lymphatics, and have an excellent prognosis (>95% present at stage I or II) (Harrison 22e p.3046).","Follicular thyroid cancer (FTC) accounts for ~5% of US thyroid cancers and cannot be distinguished from benign follicular lesions by FNA cytology alone; diagnosis requires histologic evidence of capsular and/or vascular invasion (Harrison 22e p.3046-3047, p.3048).","Medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) arises from calcitonin-producing C cells, accounts for ~5% of thyroid cancers, and can be sporadic or familial (MEN 2A, MEN 2B, familial MTC); elevated serum calcitonin is a marker of residual/recurrent disease (Harrison 22e p.3048).","All patients with MTC should be tested for RET mutations so that genetic counseling and family testing can be offered; pheochromocytoma must be excluded before surgery in any patient with a RET mutation (Harrison 22e p.3048).","Anaplastic thyroid cancer (ATC) is poorly differentiated and aggressive with a bleak prognosis; most patients die within 6 months of diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.3040, p.3048).","Serum thyroglobulin (Tg) is a sensitive marker of residual/recurrent disease for PTC, FTC, and oncocytic carcinoma after total thyroidectomy and radioablation, while calcitonin serves this role for MTC (Harrison 22e p.3045, p.3047).","Molecular diagnostic panels (next-generation sequencing, microRNA, gene expression) are increasingly used on FNA samples with indeterminate cytology (AUS, follicular neoplasm) to distinguish benign from malignant lesions and reduce unnecessary diagnostic surgery; current validated tests have negative predictive values >95% (Harrison 22e p.3046, p.3050)."],"pathophysiology":"Thyroid nodular disease reflects disordered growth of thyroid cells that may be hyperplastic or neoplastic. Thyroid neoplasms can arise from follicular cells (papillary, follicular, oncocytic, poorly differentiated, anaplastic carcinomas), calcitonin-producing C cells (medullary carcinoma), lymphocytes (lymphoma), or stromal/vascular elements. Cancers are monoclonal, arising from mutations conferring a growth advantage or resistance to cell death. Activation of the RET-RAS-BRAF (MAPK) signaling pathway is seen in up to 70% of PTCs; BRAF V600E mutations occur in up to 60% of PTC, RET rearrangements in ~15% (more frequent after Chernobyl fallout), and RAS mutations in ~15%. FTCs harbor BRAF/RAS mutations plus PAX8-PPARgamma rearrangements (~70% have a mutation/rearrangement). Anaplastic cancers accumulate additional TERT promoter, BRAF, CTNNB1 (beta-catenin), and TP53 mutations driving dedifferentiation and genetic instability. MEN 2-associated MTC carries inherited activating RET point mutations and is preceded by C-cell hyperplasia. External and fallout radiation predispose to chromosomal breaks and increase risk of benign and malignant nodules, especially in children. Most differentiated cancers retain TSH receptor expression and respond to TSH, the basis for both LT4 suppression and radioiodine uptake.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign hyperplastic (colloid) nodule","Follicular adenoma","Oncocytic (Hurthle cell) adenoma","Thyroid cyst (colloid cyst)","Multinodular goiter (nodule within an MNG)","Toxic adenoma / hyperfunctioning ('hot') nodule","Noninvasive follicular thyroid neoplasm with papillary-like nuclear features (NIFTP) - no longer considered malignant","Thyroglossal duct cyst / developmental abnormality","Thyroid lymphoma","Metastasis to the thyroid from another primary"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hashimoto's thyroiditis (background for thyroid lymphoma; may produce a goitrous, nodular-feeling gland)","Subclinical thyrotoxicosis from autonomous nodule (mimics need for cancer workup but reflects functioning, benign nodule)","Hoarseness from gastroesophageal reflux rather than laryngeal nerve invasion by tumor"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum TSH (first test in nodule evaluation)","Radionuclide (I-123) scan if TSH suppressed, to identify hyperfunctioning 'hot' nodule","Thyroid and cervical lymph node ultrasound with risk-stratification (ACR TI-RADS / ATA / EU-TIRADS)","Ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration (FNA) with Bethesda System cytology reporting","Molecular testing of indeterminate FNA samples (next-generation sequencing, microRNA, gene-expression panels)","Serum thyroglobulin (Tg) for surveillance of differentiated cancer after thyroidectomy/ablation","Serum calcitonin (and RET mutation testing) for medullary thyroid carcinoma","Whole-body radioiodine scan (WBS) for iodine-avid metastases or elevated Tg with negative imaging","Neck/chest cross-sectional imaging (CT/MRI) and PET-CT (18FDG or Ga-68 Dotatate avidity raises malignancy suspicion)"],"redFlags":["Nodule fixed to adjacent structures (firm/hard, immobile)","Vocal cord paralysis or hoarse voice (suggests recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement)","Lateral cervical lymphadenopathy ipsilateral to the nodule","Rapidly enlarging neck mass","Prior head/neck irradiation before age 18 (e.g., mantle radiation for Hodgkin's, cranial radiation for childhood leukemia)","Childhood/adolescent exposure to ionizing radiation from fallout","Age <20 or >65 years","Male gender","Family history of PTC in two or more first-degree relatives, MEN 2, or syndromes (Cowden's, familial adenomatous polyposis, Carney complex, PTEN hamartoma)","18FDG or Ga-68 Dotatate PET avidity of the nodule"],"prognosis":"Prognosis spans a wide range by histologic type. Differentiated cancers (PTC and FTC) are often curable with good prognosis in early-stage disease; the 20-year disease-specific mortality for low-risk thyroid cancer is ~1%, and most PTC present at stage I/II with survival similar to expected. Mortality rises markedly in stage IV disease, especially with distant metastases (stage IVB, ~1% of patients). FTC, particularly angioinvasive, carries less favorable outcomes than PTC partly due to more stage IV presentation; poor features include distant metastases, age >55, tumor >4 cm, and marked vascular invasion. MTC is more aggressive in MEN 2B than MEN 2A, and familial MTC more aggressive than sporadic. Anaplastic thyroid cancer is aggressive with a bleak prognosis, most patients dying within 6 months. Older age (>65) and male gender are associated with worse outcomes overall.","pitfalls":["Thyroid cancer is being overdiagnosed: rising incidence is driven by small T1 papillary tumors while disease-specific mortality has not changed in 40 years; routine FNA is not recommended for nodules <1 cm unless metastatic cervical nodes are present.","A hyperfunctioning ('hot') nodule on radionuclide scan is almost never malignant; FNA is unnecessary and should not be performed for these.","FNA cytology cannot reliably distinguish benign follicular adenoma from follicular carcinoma because nuclear features do not differ; the distinction requires histologic evidence of capsular/vascular invasion.","LT4 (TSH-suppression) therapy does NOT shrink nodules in iodine-replete (iodine-sufficient) populations and should not be used for that purpose; if it has not reduced nodule size after 6-12 months it should be stopped.","Spongiform nodules (TR1) have <3% cancer risk and warrant observation rather than FNA.","Iodine-containing contrast agents should be avoided in nodular goiter due to the risk of the Jod-Basedow effect (iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis from autonomous nodules).","NIFTP can only be diagnosed on surgical pathology, so it is still included within Bethesda malignancy risk estimates.","Hoarseness more commonly reflects benign causes (e.g., reflux) than malignancy, but new vocal cord paralysis is a genuine red flag.","In MTC, surgery should not proceed until pheochromocytoma has been excluded in any patient with a RET mutation.","For thyroid lymphoma, initial surgical resection should be avoided because it may spread otherwise localized disease."],"management":["Surgery is the mainstay for malignant nodules, with lobectomy or total thyroidectomy according to size and risk and lymph-node dissection for nodal disease","Give radioactive iodine ablation of residual differentiated disease after total thyroidectomy in appropriate risk groups","Provide TSH-suppressive levothyroxine for differentiated cancer, with lifelong thyroid hormone replacement after thyroidectomy","Treat medullary carcinoma with total thyroidectomy and central-compartment node dissection, screening for and treating any associated phaeochromocytoma before operating and undertaking RET testing","Manage anaplastic and advanced disease with multimodality chemoradiotherapy and targeted kinase inhibitors such as BRAF and MEK inhibitors for BRAF-mutant tumours or RET inhibitors, often with palliative intent","Offer active surveillance for selected low-risk papillary microcarcinoma and monitor benign nodules","Follow up with serum thyroglobulin and neck ultrasound, using calcitonin and CEA for medullary disease, and manage post-operative hypoparathyroidism and hypocalcaemia","Provide genetic counselling and RET testing for medullary carcinoma and multiple endocrine neoplasia syndromes"],"severityClassification":"Histologic classification (WHO/Table 397-1): follicular cell-derived (benign follicular/oncocytic adenoma; low-risk neoplasms including NIFTP; malignant - papillary, follicular, oncocytic, differentiated/poorly differentiated high-grade, anaplastic), C-cell-derived medullary carcinoma, mixed medullary-follicular, lymphoma, and others. Staging uses the AJCC/UICC 8th-edition TNM system (tumor size and extrathyroidal invasion, regional nodal metastases, distant metastases) for differentiated and anaplastic cancers. Sonographic risk stratification uses ACR TI-RADS levels TR1 (benign) to TR5 (highly suspicious). FNA cytology uses the six-tier Bethesda System v3 (I nondiagnostic ~13%, II benign ~4%, III AUS ~22%, IV follicular neoplasm ~30%, V suspicious for malignancy ~74%, VI malignant ~97% malignancy risk). MTC risk in MEN 2 is further stratified by specific RET mutation.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3040-3050"],"crossLinks":["multiple_endocrine_neoplasia","hyperthyroidism"]},"thyroid_dyshormonogenesis":{"name":"Thyroid dyshormonogenesis","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dyshormonogenetic goitre","thyroid hormone synthesis defect","TPO deficiency","TG deficiency","Pendred syndrome (SLC26A4)","DUOX2 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard endocrinology texts and congenital hypothyroidism guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A cause of congenital hypothyroidism in which the thyroid gland is present, often enlarged (goitre), but cannot make hormone normally","Usually autosomal recessive, arising from defects in steps of hormone synthesis such as iodide transport, organification (thyroid peroxidase), thyroglobulin, or hydrogen peroxide generation (DUOX2)","Detected in many countries through newborn screening showing a raised thyroid-stimulating hormone","Distinguished from thyroid dysgenesis by the presence of a normally sited or enlarged gland on imaging","Pendred syndrome combines an organification defect with sensorineural hearing loss, so hearing should be assessed","A perchlorate discharge test can demonstrate an organification defect where such testing is available","Prompt levothyroxine replacement started in the neonatal period prevents the neurodevelopmental consequences of untreated congenital hypothyroidism"],"pathophysiology":"Thyroid dyshormonogenesis is caused by inherited defects in the enzymatic machinery the thyroid uses to synthesise hormone. Depending on the gene involved, iodide may not be trapped or organified, thyroglobulin may be abnormal, or the hydrogen peroxide needed for organification may be deficient. Impaired hormone output lowers circulating thyroid hormone and, through loss of negative feedback, raises thyroid-stimulating hormone. The resulting chronic stimulation causes the gland to enlarge into a goitre, while the hormone deficiency itself threatens growth and, critically in infancy, neurodevelopment.","additionalDifferentials":["Thyroid dysgenesis (aplasia, hypoplasia, or ectopia)","Transient neonatal hypothyroidism from maternal or iodine factors","Central (secondary) hypothyroidism","Iodine deficiency or excess","Resistance to thyroid hormone","Maternal antithyroid drug or antibody effects"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum thyroid-stimulating hormone and free thyroxine","Thyroid ultrasound to confirm a present or enlarged gland","Radionuclide uptake scan to assess trapping and gland location","Perchlorate discharge test for organification defects","Serum and urine thyroglobulin and iodine studies","Genetic testing of relevant synthesis genes and audiology for suspected Pendred syndrome"],"redFlags":["Delayed recognition or treatment in a neonate risking irreversible neurodevelopmental impairment","A rapidly enlarging or compressive goitre","Associated sensorineural hearing loss suggesting Pendred syndrome","Severe hypothyroid features such as feeding difficulty and hypotonia in infancy"],"prognosis":"When congenital hypothyroidism is detected by screening and treated promptly with adequate replacement, growth and neurodevelopment are usually normal; delayed treatment risks permanent cognitive impairment, and the goitre generally regresses once hormone levels are restored.","pitfalls":["Assuming a present gland excludes a hormone synthesis defect","Missing associated deafness in Pendred syndrome","Delaying replacement while awaiting confirmatory imaging or genetics in a neonate","Under-dosing replacement and allowing persistent goitre or hypothyroidism"],"management":["Start prompt, lifelong levothyroxine replacement titrated to restore euthyroidism as soon as congenital hypothyroidism is confirmed on newborn screening","Titrate the levothyroxine dose against thyroid function and clinical response, ensuring adequate replacement through infancy to protect neurodevelopment","Monitor growth, developmental milestones and thyroid function at regular intervals, expecting the goitre to regress once hormone levels are restored","Assess hearing with audiology where Pendred syndrome is suspected, and provide aural rehabilitation for sensorineural hearing loss","Adjust replacement during intercurrent illness, growth and puberty to avoid under-treatment","Offer genetic counselling and family screening, as the disorder is usually autosomal recessive","Refer to paediatric endocrinology and clinical genetics for coordinated care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["European Society for Paediatric Endocrinology congenital hypothyroidism guidance","Standard endocrinology texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroid_eye_disease":{"name":"Thyroid eye disease","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thyroid-associated orbitopathy","Graves ophthalmopathy","thyroid orbitopathy","TED","TAO"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autoimmune orbitopathy most often associated with Graves disease, though patients may be hyperthyroid, euthyroid, or hypothyroid at presentation","Cardinal features include eyelid retraction, lid lag on downgaze, proptosis, restrictive strabismus with diplopia, and ocular surface exposure","It follows an active inflammatory phase lasting months to a couple of years, followed by a quiescent fibrotic phase; treatment strategy depends on which phase the patient is in","Smoking strongly worsens the disease and its response to treatment, so cessation is a key intervention","Disease activity and severity are assessed separately, for example using a clinical activity score and grading of soft tissue, proptosis, motility, corneal, and optic nerve findings","Active moderate-to-severe disease is managed with immunosuppression and orbital measures, while stable disease may require rehabilitative surgery in the sequence of orbital decompression, then squint, then eyelid surgery","The most feared complication is dysthyroid optic neuropathy from apical crowding, which threatens sight and needs urgent treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Thyroid eye disease is an autoimmune inflammatory disorder of the orbit linked to Graves disease. Autoantibodies against the thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor, expressed on orbital fibroblasts, drive activation and differentiation of these cells. The resulting inflammation causes accumulation of glycosaminoglycans, oedema, and enlargement of the extraocular muscles and orbital fat. The expanded orbital contents within a fixed bony cavity produce proptosis, restrictive myopathy, and, when the crowded orbital apex compresses the optic nerve, dysthyroid optic neuropathy. Over time inflammation gives way to fibrosis, leaving fixed muscle restriction and lid changes.","additionalDifferentials":["Orbital cellulitis","Orbital inflammatory syndrome (orbital pseudotumour)","Orbital tumour or lymphoma","Carotid-cavernous fistula","IgG4-related orbital disease","Myasthenia gravis (as a cause of diplopia and lid signs, and a recognised association)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thyroid function tests and thyroid autoantibodies including TSH-receptor antibodies","Assessment of clinical activity score and disease severity grading","Exophthalmometry to quantify proptosis","Orbital CT or MRI to assess muscle enlargement and apical crowding","Visual acuity, colour vision, visual fields, and pupillary assessment to detect optic neuropathy","Ocular motility and diplopia assessment"],"redFlags":["Dysthyroid optic neuropathy with reduced acuity, impaired colour vision, a relative afferent pupillary defect, or disc swelling requiring urgent treatment","Corneal exposure or ulceration from severe proptosis and lagophthalmos","Rapidly progressive proptosis or globe subluxation","Marked orbital congestion with raised intraocular pressure"],"prognosis":"Many patients have mild, self-limiting disease, but moderate-to-severe cases can leave lasting proptosis, diplopia, and disfigurement; optic neuropathy risks permanent visual loss if not treated promptly, and smoking cessation improves the overall outlook.","pitfalls":["Assuming euthyroid patients cannot have thyroid eye disease and delaying diagnosis","Missing early optic neuropathy because central acuity can be preserved while colour vision and fields deteriorate","Performing rehabilitative surgery during the active inflammatory phase","Neglecting smoking cessation and ocular surface protection"],"management":["Advise smoking cessation and restore and maintain euthyroidism, as both strongly influence disease course and treatment response","Treat active moderate-to-severe disease with immunosuppression, typically intravenous glucocorticoids, with the IGF-1 receptor antibody teprotumumab, rituximab or orbital radiotherapy as further options","Protect the ocular surface with lubricants, manage diplopia with prisms or occlusion, and consider selenium in mild disease","Grade disease activity and severity and track visual acuity, colour vision and fields to detect optic neuropathy","Treat dysthyroid optic neuropathy urgently with high-dose intravenous glucocorticoids and surgical orbital decompression if the response is inadequate","Undertake rehabilitative surgery only once disease is quiescent, in the sequence of orbital decompression, then squint correction, then eyelid surgery","Coordinate care through a combined thyroid eye clinic with ophthalmology and endocrinology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroid_gland_physiology_and_testing":{"name":"Thyroid Gland Physiology and Testing","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Thyroid axis physiology","Thyroid function testing","HPT axis"],"chapter":394,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3020","clinicalPearls":["TSH is the single most useful physiologic marker of thyroid hormone action; because of its long plasma half-life (~50 min) a single measurement reliably reflects circulating levels (p.3021).","A normal TSH essentially excludes a primary thyroid abnormality, so the logical first step in evaluation is to determine whether TSH is suppressed, normal, or elevated (p.3026).","Primary hyperthyroidism produces a low/suppressed TSH and primary hypothyroidism produces an elevated TSH because thyroid hormone exerts dominant negative feedback on TSH gene expression (p.3021, p.3026).","TSH is a glycoprotein with an alpha subunit shared with LH, FSH, and hCG and a unique beta subunit; the structurally similar hCG weakly stimulates the TSH-R, lowering TSH in the first trimester of pregnancy (p.3021, p.3024).","T4 is secreted in roughly 15-fold excess over T3 and acts largely as a prohormone, being converted peripherally to the more potent T3 by deiodinases (p.3023-3024).","Nuclear thyroid hormone receptors silence target genes when unliganded by binding co-repressors, so hormone deficiency causes both loss of stimulation and active gene repression, giving deficiency a more profound effect than receptor gene deletion (p.3025).","About 5-15% of euthyroid women and up to 2% of euthyroid men carry thyroid (anti-TPO) antibodies and are at increased risk of future thyroid dysfunction; nearly all autoimmune hypothyroid patients and up to 80% of Graves' patients have TPO antibodies (p.3026).","Functioning (hot) thyroid nodules are almost never malignant and do not require fine-needle aspiration, whereas non-functioning (cold) nodules carry a ~5-10% malignancy risk (p.3026-3027).","Serum thyroglobulin is elevated in all forms of thyrotoxicosis except factitious thyrotoxicosis (exogenous hormone), and its principal clinical use is surveillance after thyroidectomy/radioablation for thyroid cancer (p.3026).","After total thyroidectomy and radioablation for thyroid cancer, thyroglobulin should be <0.2 ng/mL in the absence of anti-Tg antibodies; a measurable level suggests incomplete ablation or recurrence (p.3026)."],"pathophysiology":"The hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis is a classic endocrine feedback loop. Hypothalamic TRH stimulates pituitary thyrotrope production of TSH, which binds the TSH-R (a Gs-coupled seven-transmembrane GPCR) on the basolateral surface of thyroid follicular cells, activating adenylyl cyclase/cAMP and phospholipase C to drive gland growth and hormone synthesis. Iodide is actively trapped by the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS), transported apically via pendrin, and oxidized/organified onto tyrosyl residues of thyroglobulin by thyroid peroxidase using DUOX-generated hydrogen peroxide; iodotyrosines are coupled to form T4 and T3, which are released after lysosomal proteolysis of reabsorbed thyroglobulin. Circulating hormone is largely protein-bound (TBG, transthyretin, albumin) with only the small unbound fraction biologically available; deiodinases (types I, II, III) regulate peripheral conversion of T4 to active T3 or inactivating reverse T3. T3 acts mainly through nuclear receptors TRalpha and TRbeta, which heterodimerize with RXR on thyroid response elements, exchanging co-repressors for co-activators upon ligand binding. Thyroid hormones feed back, predominantly via TRbeta2 in hypothalamus and pituitary, to suppress TRH and TSH, establishing the axis set point.","additionalDifferentials":["Euthyroid hyperthyroxinemia (familial dysalbuminemic hyperthyroxinemia, TBG excess, transthyretin variants) with elevated total but normal free T4/T3","Resistance to thyroid hormone (TRbeta mutation) with high free T4/T3 and inappropriately normal/elevated TSH","TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma causing inappropriate TSH with high thyroid hormones","Sick euthyroid (nonthyroidal illness) syndrome altering TSH and T4-to-T3 conversion","MCT8 transporter mutation (X-linked) with low T4, high T3, high TSH and psychomotor retardation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["TBG excess from estrogen (pregnancy, oral contraceptives, tamoxifen) raising total T4/T3 while free hormone stays normal","TBG deficiency (X-linked) lowering total T4/T3 with normal free levels and euthyroid state","Biotin supplement interference falsely lowering TSH and falsely raising T4/T3 in biotinylated assays","Drugs displacing hormone from binding proteins (salicylates, salsalate) or impairing T4-to-T3 conversion (propranolol, amiodarone, glucocorticoids, propylthiouracil, oral contrast agents)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Sensitive third-generation TSH immunoradiometric assay (detects to <=0.01 mIU/L) as the screening test","Free (unbound) T4 by immunoassay or equilibrium dialysis; free T3 when TSH is suppressed but free T4 is normal (to detect T3 toxicosis)","Anti-TPO (and anti-thyroglobulin) antibodies for autoimmune thyroid disease","TRAb / thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin assay for Graves' disease and to predict fetal/neonatal thyrotoxicosis","Serum thyroglobulin for thyroiditis and post-thyroidectomy cancer surveillance","Radioiodine (123I/131I) uptake and thyroid scan, or 99mTc pertechnetate imaging, to define etiology of thyrotoxicosis","Thyroid ultrasound (10-12 MHz) with risk-stratification systems (TI-RADS) and ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration for nodules"],"redFlags":["Retrosternal goiter causing venous distention and dyspnea on raising the arms (Pemberton's sign)","Suspicious sonographic nodule features (hypoechoic solid nodule, irregular margins, punctate echogenic microcalcifications) with >90% cancer risk","Cervical or supraclavicular lymphadenopathy on thyroid examination suggesting malignancy","High maternal TRAb/TSI (>3x upper limit of normal) in the third trimester predicting fetal/neonatal thyrotoxicosis","Untreated maternal/neonatal hypothyroidism in iodine-deficient regions risking cretinism (intellectual disability, growth retardation)"],"prognosis":"Most physiologic and binding-protein variants (TBG abnormalities, familial dysalbuminemic hyperthyroxinemia, classic TRbeta resistance) are benign and require no treatment once recognized, the main goal being avoidance of inappropriate therapy. Early thyroid hormone replacement in congenital hypothyroidism prevents severe developmental abnormality, whereas untreated iodine deficiency in early life causes irreversible cretinism and even mild deficiency can subtly reduce IQ.","pitfalls":["Treating low total T4 in TBG deficiency: attempts to normalize total T4 cause thyrotoxicosis and are futile due to rapid clearance.","Relying on TSH alone in suspected hypothalamic-pituitary (secondary) hypothyroidism, where TSH is inappropriately low-to-normal for a low T4; free T4 must also be measured.","Failing to stop biotin supplements >=2 days before testing, leading to falsely low TSH and falsely high T4/T3.","Missing T3 toxicosis (2-5% of cases) if only free T4 is checked when TSH is suppressed.","Misinterpreting transiently low TSH (0.01-0.1 mIU/L) of early pregnancy, recent hyperthyroidism treatment, or glucocorticoid/dopamine therapy as true thyrotoxicosis.","Performing fine-needle aspiration on a hot/functioning nodule, which is almost never malignant; scintigraphy should precede biopsy when TSH is subnormal.","Confusing thyroid hormone resistance or a TSH-secreting adenoma with simple primary hyperthyroidism because of elevated hormones with non-suppressed TSH."],"management":["Use serum thyroid-stimulating hormone as the first-line test, reserving free thyroxine and free triiodothyronine to confirm and characterise abnormal results","Once recognised, leave benign euthyroid variants such as thyroxine-binding globulin abnormalities and familial dysalbuminaemic hyperthyroxinaemia untreated, since therapy is inappropriate and harmful","Distinguish inappropriate-TSH pictures such as thyroid hormone resistance from a thyrotropin-secreting adenoma before committing to treatment, using antibody, alpha-subunit and dynamic testing with imaging","Interpret results in physiological and clinical context, allowing for the hCG-driven fall in TSH in early pregnancy and the changes of non-thyroidal illness","Ensure early levothyroxine replacement where congenital hypothyroidism is detected, to protect neurodevelopment","Refer discordant or unexplained results to endocrinology for specialist interpretation and genetic evaluation"],"severityClassification":"Not applicable as a discrete disease; thyroid status is categorized biochemically by TSH and free hormone levels into euthyroid, subclinical hypo-/hyperthyroidism (abnormal TSH with normal free hormones), and overt hypo-/hyperthyroidism (abnormal TSH with abnormal free hormones), with trimester-specific reference ranges applied in pregnancy.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3020","Harrison 22e p.3021","Harrison 22e p.3022","Harrison 22e p.3023","Harrison 22e p.3024","Harrison 22e p.3025","Harrison 22e p.3026","Harrison 22e p.3027"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroid_hormone_cell_transporter_defect":{"name":"Thyroid hormone cell-membrane transporter defect","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCT8 deficiency","SLC16A2 defect","Allan-Herndon-Dudley syndrome","monocarboxylate transporter 8 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard endocrinology texts and thyroid transporter disorder literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked disorder affecting boys, combining severe neurodevelopmental impairment with a characteristic thyroid function pattern","Presents with central hypotonia evolving to spasticity, profound developmental delay, and poor head control (Allan-Herndon-Dudley syndrome)","The hallmark biochemistry is a raised free triiodothyronine, low or low-normal free thyroxine, and normal or slightly raised thyroid-stimulating hormone","Caused by mutations in SLC16A2, encoding the MCT8 transporter that carries thyroid hormone into cells, particularly neurones","Peripheral tissues are relatively thyrotoxic while the brain is hypothyroid, so the clinical picture mixes hypermetabolic and hypothyroid features","Standard levothyroxine does not correct the neurological disease because the transporter needed to deliver hormone to neurones is defective","Diagnosis should be considered in any boy with unexplained developmental delay and this distinctive thyroid function profile"],"pathophysiology":"MCT8, encoded by SLC16A2 on the X chromosome, is a transmembrane transporter that moves thyroid hormone into cells, and it is critical for delivering hormone across the blood-brain barrier and into neurones. When it is defective, thyroid hormone cannot reach the developing brain, producing a state of cerebral hypothyroidism and severe neurodevelopmental impairment. At the same time excess hormone circulates and enters tissues that rely on other transporters, and increased peripheral conversion generates high triiodothyronine, giving a thyrotoxic effect on tissues such as muscle and driving the paradoxical thyroid function tests.","additionalDifferentials":["Resistance to thyroid hormone (THRB)","Other causes of X-linked or syndromic developmental delay with hypotonia","Cerebral palsy of other aetiology","Primary or central hypothyroidism","Mitochondrial and neurometabolic disorders","Thyroid hormone metabolism defects such as SBP2/SECISBP2 disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full thyroid function profile including free triiodothyronine, free thyroxine, and thyroid-stimulating hormone","SLC16A2 genetic testing","Reverse triiodothyronine measurement","Brain magnetic resonance imaging including assessment of myelination","Developmental and neurological assessment","Assessment for hypermetabolic effects such as poor weight gain and tachycardia"],"redFlags":["Failure to thrive with a hypermetabolic state despite apparent hypothyroid features","Progressive spasticity and loss of developmental milestones","Recurrent aspiration or feeding difficulty from severe hypotonia","Misdiagnosis leading to inappropriate high-dose thyroxine that worsens thyrotoxic effects"],"prognosis":"The neurodevelopmental impairment is severe and largely irreversible, and most affected boys have profound disability; management is supportive, and experimental thyroid hormone analogues aim mainly to reduce the peripheral thyrotoxic burden rather than restore neurological function.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the raised triiodothyronine for ordinary thyrotoxicosis and treating with antithyroid drugs","Giving standard levothyroxine and expecting neurological improvement","Overlooking the diagnosis in a boy labelled as cerebral palsy","Failing to recognise and manage the peripheral hypermetabolic state"],"management":["Consider the thyroid hormone analogue tiratricol (TRIAC) to lower the peripheral thyrotoxic burden, recognising that standard levothyroxine cannot correct the neurological disease","Manage peripheral hypermetabolism with beta-blockade for tachycardia and nutritional support to counter poor weight gain","Provide supportive neurorehabilitation including physiotherapy for hypotonia and spasticity, feeding assessment and management of any seizures","Monitor thyroid function, weight, growth and cardiac status while on hormone-analogue therapy","Offer genetic counselling and carrier testing to female relatives, as this is an X-linked condition","Coordinate care through metabolic medicine, paediatric neurology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Specialist reviews of MCT8 deficiency / Allan-Herndon-Dudley syndrome","Standard endocrinology texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroid_lymphoma":{"name":"Primary thyroid lymphoma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["primary thyroid lymphoma","thyroid non-Hodgkin lymphoma","thyroid MALT lymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / WHO Classification of Endocrine Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents as a rapidly enlarging goitre or neck mass, often with compressive symptoms, in an older patient with long-standing Hashimoto thyroiditis","Almost always a non-Hodgkin B-cell lymphoma, most commonly diffuse large B-cell lymphoma or a marginal-zone (MALT) lymphoma arising in the setting of autoimmune thyroiditis","Chronic autoimmune inflammation in Hashimoto thyroiditis provides the lymphoid tissue from which MALT lymphoma can develop, so a history of hypothyroidism and positive thyroid antibodies is common","Because treatment is chemotherapy and radiotherapy rather than surgery, obtaining an adequate tissue diagnosis is critical and often needs core or open biopsy with flow cytometry, as aspiration may not suffice","May clinically mimic anaplastic carcinoma, but immunophenotyping distinguishes lymphoid from epithelial malignancy and carries a far better prognosis","Compressive features such as stridor, dysphagia, or hoarseness can develop quickly and require prompt airway assessment","Staging and management follow lymphoma principles, so haematology-oncology involvement and systemic assessment are essential"],"pathophysiology":"Primary thyroid lymphoma is a non-Hodgkin B-cell malignancy arising within the thyroid, almost invariably against a background of chronic lymphocytic (Hashimoto) thyroiditis. Persistent autoimmune stimulation recruits and expands intrathyroidal lymphoid tissue, and acquired genetic changes in this population can give rise to a clonal marginal-zone (MALT) lymphoma or transform into an aggressive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. The proliferating lymphoid cells rapidly efface and expand the gland, producing a fast-growing mass and compressive symptoms, but the disease responds to lymphoma-directed chemotherapy and radiotherapy rather than to thyroidectomy.","additionalDifferentials":["Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma","Poorly differentiated or medullary thyroid carcinoma","Hashimoto thyroiditis with diffuse goitre","Riedel invasive fibrous thyroiditis","Reactive lymphoid hyperplasia of the thyroid","Secondary or systemic lymphoma involving the thyroid"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Core needle or open biopsy with histology, immunohistochemistry, and flow cytometry for definitive diagnosis","Thyroid autoantibodies and thyroid function, given the strong association with Hashimoto thyroiditis","Neck ultrasound and cross-sectional imaging to define extent and compression","Systemic staging imaging such as CT and functional imaging per lymphoma protocols","Bone marrow assessment and lactate dehydrogenase as part of lymphoma staging","Airway assessment including laryngoscopy when compressive symptoms are present"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging mass with stridor or airway compromise requiring urgent assessment","New dysphagia or hoarseness from compression or invasion","Systemic B symptoms such as fever, night sweats, and weight loss indicating aggressive lymphoma","Superior vena cava obstruction from bulky mediastinal extension"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally good and far better than for anaplastic carcinoma, particularly for localised MALT lymphoma; aggressive diffuse large B-cell histology, advanced stage, and extrathyroidal spread reduce response and survival.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing lymphoma as anaplastic carcinoma and thereby denying potentially curative lymphoma therapy","Relying on aspiration cytology alone, which may be insufficient to subtype lymphoma without flow cytometry or core biopsy","Proceeding to thyroidectomy for a disease that is treated medically","Overlooking an underlying Hashimoto thyroiditis that provides the diagnostic context"],"management":["Treat aggressive diffuse large B-cell histology with rituximab-based immunochemotherapy (an anthracycline-containing regimen) combined with involved-site radiotherapy rather than thyroidectomy","Treat localised marginal-zone (MALT) lymphoma with radiotherapy, with rituximab as an option, reserving surgery for diagnosis only","Secure an adequate tissue diagnosis by core or open biopsy with immunophenotyping and flow cytometry before starting treatment","Assess and protect the airway urgently when compressive stridor, dysphagia or hoarseness develops, using corticosteroids and airway support as needed","Replace thyroid hormone for the coexisting Hashimoto hypothyroidism and provide supportive care through chemotherapy","Stage and manage through a haemato-oncology multidisciplinary team, with post-treatment surveillance for relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","WHO Classification of Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroid_nodule":{"name":"Thyroid nodule","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thyroid lump","solitary thyroid nodule","thyroid mass","nodular goitre"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Thyroid nodules are common and usually benign, but the central task is to identify the minority that are malignant","Initial evaluation begins with a serum TSH; a suppressed value points towards an autonomously functioning (usually benign) nodule and warrants a radionuclide scan","Ultrasound characterises nodules and stratifies malignancy risk, with features such as marked hypoechogenicity, microcalcifications, irregular margins, and a taller-than-wide shape raising concern","Fine-needle aspiration is the key diagnostic test, with results reported using standardised cytology categories that guide management","Hot (functioning) nodules on scanning rarely harbour malignancy, so aspiration is generally directed at non-functioning nodules","History of childhood neck irradiation, family history of thyroid cancer, rapid growth, hoarseness, or fixed hard nodules increase suspicion of cancer","Molecular testing of indeterminate cytology samples can refine the risk of malignancy and help avoid unnecessary surgery"],"pathophysiology":"Thyroid nodules arise from focal proliferation of follicular cells and may be colloid nodules, adenomas, cysts, areas of thyroiditis, or carcinomas. Autonomously functioning nodules develop constitutive activation of thyroid hormone production, often through activating mutations of the TSH-receptor or G-protein signalling pathway, suppressing TSH and appearing 'hot' on scanning. Malignant nodules result from oncogenic alterations (for example in the MAPK pathway) driving unregulated growth, and their behaviour ranges from indolent differentiated cancers to aggressive anaplastic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign colloid or hyperplastic nodule","Follicular adenoma","Differentiated thyroid carcinoma (papillary or follicular)","Medullary or anaplastic thyroid carcinoma","Thyroid cyst or focal thyroiditis","Parathyroid or other neck mass mimicking a thyroid nodule"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum TSH as the first-line test","Thyroid ultrasound to characterise the nodule and assess cervical lymph nodes","Radionuclide thyroid scan when TSH is suppressed","Ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration cytology","Molecular marker testing for indeterminate cytology","Serum calcitonin when medullary carcinoma is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging, hard, or fixed nodule","New hoarseness or vocal cord palsy suggesting recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement","Cervical lymphadenopathy","History of head and neck irradiation or family history of thyroid cancer or MEN syndromes","Compressive symptoms such as dysphagia or stridor"],"prognosis":"Most nodules are benign and can be followed conservatively, and even most malignant nodules are differentiated cancers with an excellent prognosis after treatment, though anaplastic and some medullary cancers carry a poor outlook.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to biopsy before checking TSH and missing an autonomous nodule that does not need aspiration","Relying on nodule size alone rather than sonographic risk features to decide on aspiration","Falsely reassuring the patient after a non-diagnostic aspirate without repeating sampling","Overlooking calcitonin measurement when clinical features suggest medullary carcinoma"],"management":["Stratify malignancy risk with serum thyroid-stimulating hormone, ultrasound characterisation and ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration cytology to direct management","Manage cytologically benign nodules with clinical and ultrasound surveillance rather than intervention","Refer nodules with malignant or suspicious cytology for surgery (diagnostic lobectomy or thyroidectomy), using molecular testing to guide indeterminate results","Treat autonomously functioning (hot) nodules causing hyperthyroidism with radioiodine or surgery","Offer surgery for large or compressive nodules causing local symptoms","Refer suspected malignancy to a thyroid multidisciplinary team, and measure calcitonin where medullary carcinoma is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroid_orbitopathy":{"name":"Thyroid eye disease","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thyroid orbitopathy","thyroid-associated orbitopathy","Graves ophthalmopathy","dysthyroid eye disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autoimmune inflammatory orbitopathy most often associated with Graves disease, though patients may be hyperthyroid, euthyroid, or hypothyroid at presentation","Characteristic features include eyelid retraction, lid lag, proptosis (exophthalmos), and restrictive myopathy causing diplopia","The enlarged extraocular muscles typically spare their tendinous insertions on orbital imaging, a feature that helps distinguish it from orbital myositis","Disease runs an active inflammatory phase followed by a quiescent fibrotic phase, and treatment strategy depends on which phase and how severe the disease is","Smoking is a major modifiable risk factor that worsens severity and reduces treatment response","The sight-threatening complications are dysthyroid optic neuropathy from apical crowding and corneal exposure from severe proptosis and lid retraction","Management includes restoring and stabilising thyroid status, ocular surface lubrication, and, in active moderate-to-severe disease, immunosuppression with corticosteroids and, where appropriate, orbital radiotherapy","Orbital decompression relieves optic-nerve compression or disfiguring proptosis, with squint and lid surgery reserved for the stable phase"],"pathophysiology":"Thyroid eye disease is an autoimmune process in which autoantibodies, particularly against the thyrotropin receptor, activate orbital fibroblasts that share antigens with the thyroid gland. Activated fibroblasts proliferate, secrete glycosaminoglycans that draw water into the tissues, and can differentiate into fat cells, causing the extraocular muscles and orbital fat to swell within the bony orbit. This expansion produces proptosis, congestion, and restrictive myopathy, while the muscle bellies enlarge with relative sparing of their tendons. Crowding at the orbital apex can compress the optic nerve, and proptosis with lid retraction exposes the cornea. After the active inflammatory phase the enlarged muscles may fibrose, leaving fixed restriction and deformity.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic orbital inflammation (orbital pseudotumour)","Orbital myositis","Orbital cellulitis","Carotid-cavernous fistula","Orbital tumour or lymphoma","Orbital metastasis or IgG4-related orbital disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thyroid function tests and thyroid autoantibodies including thyrotropin-receptor antibodies","Orbital CT or MRI showing fusiform muscle enlargement with tendon sparing","Assessment of visual acuity, colour vision, pupil reactions, and visual fields to detect optic neuropathy","Measurement of proptosis (exophthalmometry) and ocular motility with diplopia assessment","Corneal and ocular surface evaluation for exposure keratopathy","Disease activity and severity grading to guide treatment"],"redFlags":["Reduced acuity, dyschromatopsia, or a relative afferent pupillary defect indicating dysthyroid optic neuropathy","Severe corneal exposure with breakdown from marked proptosis and lid retraction","Rapidly progressive proptosis or congestion in the active phase","Choroidal folds or disc swelling suggesting posterior compression"],"prognosis":"Many patients stabilise after the active phase with good outcomes, especially with smoking cessation and prompt treatment, but a minority develop sight-threatening optic neuropathy or corneal breakdown, and residual proptosis, lid malposition, and diplopia often require surgical rehabilitation in the stable phase.","pitfalls":["Assuming normal thyroid function excludes the diagnosis, since patients can be euthyroid","Missing early dysthyroid optic neuropathy because proptosis and external signs may be modest","Not counselling about smoking as a key modifiable risk factor","Operating on lids or muscles before the disease has become inactive","Confusing tendon-sparing muscle enlargement with orbital myositis, which involves the tendons"],"management":["Advise smoking cessation and restore stable euthyroidism, as smoking worsens severity and reduces treatment response","Treat active moderate-to-severe orbitopathy with immunosuppression, principally intravenous glucocorticoids, with orbital radiotherapy, rituximab or the IGF-1 receptor antibody teprotumumab as further options","Protect the ocular surface with lubricants and manage diplopia with prisms, considering selenium in mild disease","Grade disease activity and severity and monitor visual acuity, colour vision and pupillary responses to detect dysthyroid optic neuropathy","Treat sight-threatening optic neuropathy urgently with high-dose intravenous glucocorticoids and surgical orbital decompression when needed","Defer rehabilitative surgery until the disease is quiescent, performing orbital decompression, then strabismus, then eyelid surgery in sequence","Coordinate care in a joint ophthalmology-endocrinology clinic"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyrotoxic_periodic_paralysis":{"name":"Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TPP","thyrotoxic hypokalaemic periodic paralysis","thyrotoxic periodic paralysis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / endocrine and neuromuscular reference literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with acute episodes of proximal, often ascending, muscle weakness or flaccid paralysis in the setting of thyrotoxicosis and profound hypokalaemia","Characteristically affects young Asian men, most often on a background of Graves disease, although any cause of thyrotoxicosis can be responsible","Attacks are commonly triggered by a high-carbohydrate meal, strenuous exercise followed by rest, or stress, typically overnight or in the early morning","The hypokalaemia reflects an intracellular shift of potassium rather than true depletion, driven by thyroid-hormone-enhanced sodium-potassium-ATPase activity","Thyrotoxic symptoms may be subtle, so thyroid function should be checked in any young man with hypokalaemic paralysis","Acute treatment uses cautious potassium and beta-blockade for thyrotoxic periodic paralysis, with close monitoring to avoid rebound hyperkalaemia","Definitive prevention depends on rendering the patient euthyroid, after which attacks cease"],"pathophysiology":"Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis arises when excess thyroid hormone increases the activity of the sodium-potassium-ATPase pump in skeletal muscle, driving potassium into cells. This intracellular shift is amplified by insulin and adrenergic stimulation, explaining the triggering by carbohydrate loads, exercise and stress. The resulting acute hypokalaemia hyperpolarises and then paradoxically renders muscle membranes inexcitable, producing flaccid weakness. Because potassium is redistributed rather than lost, total body potassium is normal, which underlies the risk of rebound hyperkalaemia if too much potassium is administered. A genetic predisposition affecting muscle ion channels is thought to contribute in susceptible individuals.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial (hypokalaemic) periodic paralysis","Hypokalaemia from gastrointestinal or renal potassium loss","Guillain-Barre syndrome","Renal tubular acidosis or Gitelman/Bartter syndromes","Myasthenic crisis","Acute intermittent porphyria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum potassium and full electrolyte panel during the attack","Thyroid function tests confirming thyrotoxicosis","Electrocardiogram to detect hypokalaemic changes and arrhythmia","Assessment of urinary potassium and acid-base status to confirm a shift rather than loss","TSH-receptor antibodies and thyroid imaging to define the cause of thyrotoxicosis","Magnesium and phosphate measurement"],"redFlags":["Respiratory muscle involvement or bulbar weakness compromising ventilation","Severe hypokalaemia with cardiac arrhythmia","Rebound hyperkalaemia during potassium replacement","Coexisting features of thyroid storm"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent once euthyroidism is achieved, as attacks resolve completely; recurrence is the rule while thyrotoxicosis persists.","pitfalls":["Overzealous potassium replacement causing dangerous rebound hyperkalaemia as potassium redistributes","Missing subtle thyrotoxicosis in a young man presenting with paralysis","Confusing the condition with familial periodic paralysis and overlooking the thyroid cause","Failing to treat the underlying thyrotoxicosis, leaving the patient prone to further attacks","Neglecting cardiac monitoring during acute hypokalaemia"],"management":["Treat the acute attack with cautious potassium replacement and close monitoring, since the hypokalaemia reflects an intracellular shift and risks rebound hyperkalaemia","Give non-selective beta-blockade (such as propranolol) to blunt the adrenergically driven potassium shift and hasten recovery","Render the patient euthyroid with a thionamide antithyroid drug such as carbimazole, as attacks resolve completely once thyrotoxicosis is controlled","Arrange definitive treatment of the thyrotoxicosis with radioiodine or thyroidectomy to prevent recurrence","Advise avoidance of precipitants such as high-carbohydrate meals and strenuous exercise followed by rest","Monitor serum potassium and the electrocardiogram during attacks for arrhythmia","Refer to endocrinology to identify and manage the underlying cause of thyrotoxicosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Reference literature on thyrotoxic periodic paralysis"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyrotropinoma":{"name":"Thyrotropinoma (TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma","TSHoma","thyrotroph adenoma","central hyperthyroidism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard endocrinology reference texts and pituitary tumour consensus guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare cause of hyperthyroidism in which a pituitary adenoma inappropriately secretes TSH, driving the thyroid to overproduce hormone","The biochemical hallmark is a raised or inappropriately normal (non-suppressed) TSH in the presence of elevated free thyroxine and free triiodothyronine","This inappropriate TSH pattern must be distinguished from resistance to thyroid hormone, which shows a similar biochemical picture without a pituitary tumour","An elevated alpha-subunit and a raised alpha-subunit to TSH molar ratio support a thyrotropinoma over other causes of inappropriate TSH secretion","Many are macroadenomas at diagnosis and may cause headache, visual field defects and features of other hormone co-secretion (such as growth hormone or prolactin)","Failure of TSH to respond normally to dynamic testing and pituitary MRI showing an adenoma help confirm the diagnosis","Transsphenoidal surgery is the primary treatment, with somatostatin analogue therapy to control hormone secretion and radiotherapy reserved for residual disease"],"pathophysiology":"A thyrotropinoma is a benign monoclonal tumour of pituitary thyrotroph cells that secretes biologically active TSH in an unregulated manner, escaping the normal negative feedback of thyroid hormones. The persistent TSH drive stimulates the thyroid gland to enlarge and to overproduce thyroxine and triiodothyronine, producing central (secondary) hyperthyroidism with a goitre. Because the tumour often retains the capacity to release the glycoprotein hormone alpha-subunit disproportionately, the alpha-subunit to TSH ratio is typically increased. Larger tumours also exert local mass effect and may co-secrete other anterior pituitary hormones.","additionalDifferentials":["Resistance to thyroid hormone (thyroid hormone receptor beta mutation)","Assay interference or heterophile antibody artefact","Graves disease and other causes of primary hyperthyroidism","Familial dysalbuminaemic hyperthyroxinaemia","Non-thyroidal illness with abnormal thyroid function","Recent alteration of thyroid hormone replacement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Free thyroxine, free triiodothyronine and TSH demonstrating the inappropriate TSH pattern","Serum glycoprotein hormone alpha-subunit and calculation of the alpha-subunit to TSH molar ratio","Dynamic pituitary testing (e.g. TRH stimulation and thyroid hormone suppression) where available","Pituitary MRI to identify and characterise the adenoma","Genetic testing for thyroid hormone resistance when the biochemistry is ambiguous","Assessment of other anterior pituitary axes for co-secretion or hypopituitarism"],"redFlags":["Visual field loss or other mass effect from a pituitary macroadenoma","Severe thyrotoxicosis with cardiac arrhythmia or thyroid storm","Pituitary apoplexy causing sudden headache and visual or ocular motor deficits","Inadvertent thyroid ablation for misdiagnosed primary hyperthyroidism worsening the pituitary tumour drive"],"prognosis":"With complete surgical removal many patients achieve biochemical cure and resolution of hyperthyroidism, but large or invasive tumours may leave residual disease requiring medical therapy or radiotherapy, and lifelong follow-up is needed to detect recurrence.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting a raised free thyroxine with non-suppressed TSH as an assay error and ignoring it","Treating the thyroid (with ablation or thyroidectomy) instead of addressing the pituitary source","Confusing a thyrotropinoma with resistance to thyroid hormone and missing the tumour","Overlooking co-secretion of growth hormone or prolactin from the same adenoma"],"management":["Offer transsphenoidal surgical resection as the first-line, potentially curative treatment","Use somatostatin analogues (such as octreotide or lanreotide) to lower TSH secretion, control hyperthyroidism and shrink residual or unresectable tumour","Control thyrotoxic symptoms preoperatively with beta-blockade and measures to restore euthyroidism before surgery","Consider pituitary radiotherapy for residual or invasive disease not controlled by surgery and medical therapy","Assess and replace any coexisting anterior pituitary hormone deficiencies","Monitor thyroid function, alpha-subunit, pituitary imaging and visual fields long term to detect recurrence","Manage through a specialist pituitary multidisciplinary team with neurosurgery and endocrinology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","European Thyroid Association guideline on TSH-secreting pituitary tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"tibial_torsion":{"name":"Tibial torsion","system":"Paediatric orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["internal tibial torsion","external tibial torsion","tibial version","medial tibial torsion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Internal tibial torsion is the commonest cause of in-toeing in toddlers and typically becomes apparent when the child begins to walk","The deformity is assessed on the rotational profile by the thigh-foot angle and the transmalleolar axis, with the child prone and the knee flexed","Tibial torsion normally shifts outward with growth, so mild internal torsion in infancy corrects to a modest external value by later childhood","The condition is usually physiological, frequently bilateral or asymmetrical, and resolves spontaneously without treatment in the great majority","Reassurance and observation are the mainstays, and passive stretching, special shoes, braces, and night splints have not been shown to alter the natural history","External tibial torsion is less common, tends to worsen with growth, and may contribute to patellofemoral maladjustment or an out-toeing gait","Surgical derotation osteotomy is reserved for the rare severe, persistent, and functionally significant deformity in an older child"],"pathophysiology":"Tibial torsion describes the normal axial rotation of the tibia between the knee and ankle, which in fetal life is internally rotated and gradually rotates externally through infancy and childhood as part of normal skeletal development. Persistence of the immature internally rotated pattern produces internal tibial torsion and an in-toeing gait, while excessive external rotation produces external torsion and out-toeing. It represents a variation of normal maturation influenced by intrauterine positioning and sleeping or sitting postures rather than a structural disease, and the growing bone remodels along the physiological path in the overwhelming majority of children.","additionalDifferentials":["Increased femoral anteversion as an alternative cause of in-toeing","Metatarsus adductus producing in-toeing at the level of the foot","Residual clubfoot deformity","External tibial torsion or femoral retroversion causing out-toeing","Neuromuscular gait abnormality such as cerebral palsy","Blount disease or other tibial deformity mimicking torsional malalignment"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full rotational profile assessment including thigh-foot angle and transmalleolar axis","Foot progression angle observed during gait","Assessment of hip rotation to exclude a coexisting femoral cause","Examination of the feet for metatarsus adductus or other deformity","Standing lower-limb radiographs only when angular deformity or a pathological cause is suspected","Computed tomography rotational study reserved for surgical planning in severe cases"],"redFlags":["Progressive worsening rather than the expected spontaneous improvement","Marked asymmetry between the two limbs","Associated pain, limp, or functional limitation","Short stature, disproportion, or other features suggesting a skeletal dysplasia or metabolic bone disease","Neurological signs indicating an underlying neuromuscular disorder"],"prognosis":"The natural history is overwhelmingly benign, with internal tibial torsion resolving spontaneously in almost all children as the tibia derotates with growth, and lasting functional or arthritic consequences are not expected.","pitfalls":["Prescribing braces, special shoes, or night splints that do not change the natural course and burden the family","Failing to complete a full rotational profile and thereby missing a coexisting femoral cause","Reassuring without follow-up when the deformity is progressive or asymmetrical","Attributing a pathological angular deformity such as Blount disease to simple torsion","Recommending surgery before the natural resolution that occurs with skeletal growth has been allowed"],"management":["Reassure families and observe, as internal tibial torsion is physiological and resolves spontaneously in almost all children as the tibia derotates with growth","Avoid braces, night splints, special shoes and passive stretching, which do not alter the natural history","Monitor the rotational profile (thigh-foot angle and transmalleolar axis) over serial visits to confirm expected external derotation","Reserve derotational tibial osteotomy for the rare severe, persistent deformity causing functional or cosmetic problems in an older child","Follow external tibial torsion more closely, as it tends to worsen with growth and may contribute to patellofemoral maladjustment","Refer atypical, markedly asymmetrical, worsening or pathological cases to paediatric orthopaedics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","Staheli LT, rotational problems of the lower extremity"],"crossLinks":[]},"tick_borne_encephalitis":{"name":"Tick-borne encephalitis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBE","tick-borne encephalitis virus infection","TBEV infection","Central European encephalitis","Russian spring-summer encephalitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases / WHO guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A flavivirus transmitted by Ixodes ticks across endemic parts of Europe and Asia, with European, Siberian, and Far Eastern subtypes of differing severity","The illness is classically biphasic: an initial viraemic flu-like phase, a symptom-free interval of about a week, then a second neurological phase with meningitis, encephalitis, or myelitis","An important additional route is consumption of unpasteurised milk or dairy products from infected goats, sheep, or cattle (alimentary transmission)","The Far Eastern subtype tends to cause more severe monophasic disease with higher mortality and sequelae, while the European subtype more often shows the classic biphasic pattern","Neurological sequelae such as cognitive impairment, ataxia, and flaccid paralysis (especially of the shoulder girdle) can be long-lasting","There is no specific antiviral; management is supportive, and an effective inactivated vaccine is the mainstay of prevention for residents and travellers to endemic areas","Diagnosis is usually serological (IgM and IgG in serum and cerebrospinal fluid), as viraemia and detectable RNA have often cleared by the neurological phase"],"pathophysiology":"Tick-borne encephalitis virus is inoculated during the bite of an infected Ixodes tick, or ingested in unpasteurised dairy products. It first replicates locally and in regional lymph nodes, producing the initial viraemic phase with nonspecific febrile symptoms. In a proportion of patients the virus subsequently crosses the blood-brain barrier and infects neurons, particularly in the brainstem, cerebellum, and anterior horns of the spinal cord, provoking an inflammatory meningoencephalitis or myelitis. By the time neurological disease appears, viraemia has typically resolved and the host antibody response is detectable, which is why diagnosis is largely serological.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex virus and other viral encephalitides","Bacterial meningitis or meningoencephalitis","Lyme neuroborreliosis (also Ixodes-transmitted)","Enteroviral meningitis","West Nile virus and other flaviviral neuroinvasive disease","Guillain-Barre syndrome when flaccid paralysis predominates"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and cerebrospinal fluid IgM and IgG serology for tick-borne encephalitis virus","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis showing lymphocytic pleocytosis and raised protein","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain and spinal cord for thalamic, cerebellar, brainstem, or anterior-horn involvement","RT-PCR on blood during the early viraemic phase, recognising it is often negative by the neurological phase","Testing for co-transmitted Lyme borreliosis given shared Ixodes vectors","Electrophysiology when myelitis or peripheral involvement is suspected"],"redFlags":["Declining consciousness or brainstem signs indicating severe encephalitis","Flaccid limb or respiratory-muscle weakness from myelitis","Seizures or status epilepticus","Rapidly progressive monophasic course suggesting a more severe viral subtype","Signs of raised intracranial pressure"],"prognosis":"Many patients recover fully, but a substantial minority, especially with the Siberian or Far Eastern subtypes or with encephalitic or myelitic involvement, are left with persistent neurological sequelae, and mortality is highest with the Far Eastern subtype.","pitfalls":["Discharging a patient during the afebrile interval and missing the second neurological phase","Relying on RT-PCR in the neurological phase, when serology is the appropriate test","Overlooking alimentary transmission from unpasteurised dairy products","Failing to consider co-infection with Lyme borreliosis from the same tick exposure","Not offering vaccination to at-risk residents and travellers as prevention"],"management":["Provide supportive care, since there is no specific antiviral, with analgesia, antipyretics and fluid and electrolyte management","Prevent disease with the inactivated tick-borne encephalitis vaccine for residents of and travellers to endemic areas, alongside tick-bite avoidance and avoiding unpasteurised dairy products","Manage severe encephalitic or myelitic disease in critical care, with ventilatory support for bulbar or respiratory involvement and control of raised intracranial pressure and seizures","Monitor neurological status, swallowing and airway safety through the illness","Arrange neurorehabilitation for lasting sequelae such as cognitive impairment, ataxia and flaccid paralysis","Test for co-transmitted Lyme borreliosis given the shared Ixodes vector, and treat if confirmed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","World Health Organization tick-borne encephalitis position paper"],"crossLinks":[]},"tick_borne_relapsing_fever":{"name":"Tick-borne relapsing fever","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBRF","endemic relapsing fever","Borrelia hermsii infection","Borrelia duttonii infection","spirochaetal relapsing fever"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by several Borrelia species (such as Borrelia hermsii in North America and Borrelia duttonii in Africa) and transmitted by the bite of soft (argasid) Ornithodoros ticks, whose bites are brief and painless and often not recalled","Exposure is classically linked to sleeping in rustic cabins or dwellings harbouring rodent-associated ticks, so a careful environmental history is important","After an incubation of about a week there is abrupt high fever, rigors, headache, myalgia and arthralgia lasting a few days, followed by an afebrile interval and then one or more relapses","The relapsing pattern reflects successive waves of spirochaetaemia as the organism undergoes antigenic variation to evade the developing antibody response","Diagnosis is often made by seeing spirochaetes on a peripheral blood film (thick and thin films, ideally taken during a febrile spike) using Giemsa or Wright staining","Tetracyclines such as doxycycline are the mainstay of treatment, with beta-lactams as alternatives, and treatment is usually given empirically once the diagnosis is suspected","A Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction is common on starting treatment, so patients should be observed and supported through it"],"pathophysiology":"Relapsing fever borreliae are spirochaetes inoculated during the brief feeding of soft ticks. They multiply in the bloodstream to produce high-grade spirochaetaemia, which drives the febrile episodes. As host antibodies clear the dominant serotype, the organism switches its surface antigens through programmed antigenic variation, allowing an antigenically distinct population to expand and cause a further febrile relapse; this cycle repeats, producing the characteristic relapsing pattern. High spirochaetal loads can involve the spleen, liver, central nervous system and heart, and the sudden release of bacterial products on antibiotic-induced lysis provokes the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction.","additionalDifferentials":["Louse-borne relapsing fever","Malaria","Leptospirosis","Typhoid fever","Rickettsial infections","Dengue and other arboviral fevers","Brucellosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thick and thin peripheral blood films taken during fever to demonstrate spirochaetes","PCR of blood for Borrelia species where available","Serology, interpreted with caution owing to cross-reactivity and variable performance","Full blood count, which may show thrombocytopenia","Liver enzymes and renal function","Lumbar puncture if central nervous system involvement is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neurological features such as meningism, cranial nerve palsies or altered consciousness","Myocarditis with heart failure or arrhythmia","Marked haemorrhagic tendency or profound thrombocytopenia","Severe Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction with hypotension after the first antibiotic dose","Pregnancy, in which disease and vertical transmission carry added risk"],"prognosis":"With appropriate antibiotics tick-borne relapsing fever generally has a good prognosis and low mortality, though the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction can be severe; untreated or complicated disease, particularly with cardiac or neurological involvement, carries higher risk.","pitfalls":["Not examining blood films during a febrile spike, when spirochaetaemia is highest","Failing to warn about and manage the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction","Missing the diagnosis because the painless soft-tick bite is not recalled","Attributing recurrent fevers to a relapsing viral illness or malaria without a film","Overlooking central nervous system or cardiac complications in severe cases"],"management":["Treat with a tetracycline such as doxycycline as the mainstay, with a beta-lactam (penicillin or ceftriaxone) as an alternative, often started empirically once the diagnosis is suspected","Use parenteral ceftriaxone or penicillin where there is central nervous system involvement","Anticipate and support the patient through a Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction after the first antibiotic dose, with observation and supportive measures","Provide supportive care with antipyretics and fluid replacement","Monitor the full blood count for thrombocytopenia and assess for cardiac, hepatic and neurological involvement","Advise environmental measures such as rodent and tick control and avoiding tick-infested rustic dwellings to prevent reinfection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"tick_paralysis":{"name":"Tick paralysis","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tick-borne paralysis","Ixodes holocyclus paralysis","Dermacentor tick paralysis","tick toxicosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / WHO envenoming guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with an ascending, symmetrical flaccid paralysis that can closely mimic Guillain-Barre syndrome","Caused by a neurotoxin in the saliva of a feeding, engorged female tick, most often in children","A careful whole-body skin search, including the scalp, hairline, ears and skin folds, is essential to find the attached tick","In the North American form (Dermacentor and others), removing the tick usually produces rapid improvement over hours to days","In the Australian form (Ixodes holocyclus), paralysis may worsen after tick removal, and specific measures differ","Sensation is typically preserved and reflexes are reduced or absent, helping distinguish it from other causes","The principal danger is progression to bulbar and respiratory muscle weakness requiring ventilatory support"],"pathophysiology":"During prolonged attachment and feeding, certain ticks secrete a salivary neurotoxin that impairs neuromuscular transmission, principally by interfering with presynaptic release of acetylcholine and with nerve conduction. The result is a progressive, ascending, symmetrical flaccid paralysis with hyporeflexia but preserved sensation. Toxin production relates to the duration of feeding and the engorgement of the tick, and the clinical course after tick removal differs between the North American species, where recovery is usually prompt, and Ixodes holocyclus, in which deterioration can continue for a period after removal.","additionalDifferentials":["Guillain-Barre syndrome","Botulism","Myasthenia gravis or myasthenic crisis","Spinal cord compression or transverse myelitis","Poliomyelitis or other acute flaccid paralysis","Electrolyte disturbance or periodic paralysis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thorough skin and scalp examination to locate the tick (the key diagnostic step)","Serial forced vital capacity and respiratory monitoring","Nerve conduction studies to help differentiate from Guillain-Barre syndrome","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis to exclude inflammatory neuropathy","Electrolytes including potassium and calcium","Pulse oximetry and arterial blood gases if respiratory involvement"],"redFlags":["Bulbar weakness with impaired swallowing or airway protection","Declining respiratory function or vital capacity","Rapidly ascending or worsening paralysis","Deterioration after tick removal (notably with Ixodes holocyclus)","Very young child with progressive weakness"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent once the tick is found and removed, with most patients (particularly the North American form) recovering fully over days; the main risk is respiratory failure before diagnosis, and the Australian form may deteriorate transiently after removal.","pitfalls":["Missing the attached tick, especially in the scalp, and misdiagnosing Guillain-Barre syndrome","Assuming immediate improvement in all cases, when Ixodes holocyclus paralysis can worsen after removal","Crushing or incompletely removing the tick, potentially leaving mouthparts or expressing more saliva","Underestimating the speed of respiratory decline in a young child"],"management":["Locate and completely remove the attached, engorged tick by thorough whole-body and scalp examination, which is curative and produces rapid recovery in the North American form","Monitor respiratory function with serial forced vital capacity and provide ventilatory support if respiratory muscles are involved","Observe carefully after removal in the Australian Ixodes holocyclus form, in which paralysis may transiently worsen, and avoid squeezing the tick during removal","Provide supportive nursing and airway protection while weakness resolves","Confirm neurological recovery and reconsider alternative diagnoses such as Guillain-Barre syndrome if there is no improvement after tick removal"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Clinical guidance on tick paralysis"],"crossLinks":[]},"tietze_syndrome":{"name":"Tietze syndrome","system":"Rheumatology / Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tietze's syndrome","costochondral junction syndrome","chondropathia tuberosa"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign inflammatory condition producing painful, non-suppurative swelling of the upper costal cartilages, most often the second or third","The presence of visible and palpable localised swelling distinguishes Tietze syndrome from costochondritis, which causes tenderness without swelling","Usually affects a single site and tends to occur in younger adults, in contrast to the more diffuse, multi-level costochondritis","Pain is reproduced by palpation of the affected costal cartilage and may be aggravated by coughing, deep breathing or movement","It is fundamentally a clinical diagnosis, made after excluding cardiac, pulmonary and other serious causes of chest pain","Imaging is used chiefly to exclude other pathology, though MRI or CT may show cartilage enlargement and oedema","Management is reassurance, activity modification, NSAIDs, and local corticosteroid injection for resistant cases"],"pathophysiology":"Tietze syndrome is a localised, benign inflammation of the costochondral, costosternal or sternoclavicular cartilage of unknown precise cause. Repetitive minor trauma, coughing or physical strain is thought to trigger a self-limiting inflammatory and possibly reparative response within the costal cartilage, producing cartilage enlargement with surrounding soft-tissue oedema. Unlike infective or destructive processes there is no suppuration or bony erosion; the visible fusiform swelling reflects benign cartilage and perichondrial thickening rather than joint destruction, and the condition resolves once the inflammation settles although the swelling may persist for some time.","additionalDifferentials":["Costochondritis (tenderness without swelling)","Cardiac ischaemia or myocardial infarction","Rib fracture or costal cartilage injury","Anterior chest wall involvement of SAPHO syndrome or seronegative arthropathy","Chest wall tumour or metastasis","Sternoclavicular joint infection or arthritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["ECG and cardiac assessment to exclude ischaemic chest pain","Chest radiograph to exclude pulmonary and rib pathology","MRI or CT of the chest wall to characterise cartilage swelling and exclude tumour or infection","Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP) when an inflammatory or infective process is considered","Ultrasound of the affected costal cartilage to confirm localised swelling"],"redFlags":["Chest pain with cardiac features (exertional, radiating, associated with breathlessness or diaphoresis)","Constitutional symptoms or a hard fixed mass suggesting malignancy","Fever and progressive swelling raising concern for infection","Pain unresponsive to conservative measures with atypical features"],"prognosis":"Tietze syndrome is benign and usually self-limiting, with pain settling over weeks to months, although the palpable swelling can persist for a prolonged period; recurrence is possible but serious sequelae do not occur.","pitfalls":["Anchoring on a musculoskeletal cause and missing cardiac or pulmonary disease","Confusing it with costochondritis by overlooking the characteristic localised swelling","Ordering unnecessary invasive investigation for a benign self-limiting condition","Failing to consider SAPHO syndrome or malignancy when swelling is atypical or persistent"],"management":["Reassure the patient after excluding cardiac, pulmonary and other serious causes, as the condition is benign and self-limiting","Relieve pain with simple analgesia and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, together with local heat and activity modification","Advise avoidance of aggravating movements, coughing strain and repetitive loading of the chest wall","Offer a local corticosteroid and anaesthetic injection into the affected cartilage for pain that does not settle","Explain that the palpable swelling may persist after the pain resolves and does not indicate serious disease","Re-evaluate if new or atypical features emerge that suggest an alternative diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Oxford Textbook of Rheumatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"tilted_disc_syndrome":{"name":"tilted disc syndrome","system":"ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Fuchs coloboma","congenital tilted disc"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Congenital anomaly caused by oblique insertion of the optic nerve into the globe, producing an ovoid disc with a raised superotemporal and depressed inferonasal margin.","Almost always bilateral though frequently asymmetric; the disc long axis is typically directed inferonasally.","Situs inversus of the retinal vessels is a characteristic sign, with vessels emerging in an anomalous nasal direction before doubling back.","An inferonasal fundus ectasia (staphylomatous crescent) and thinning of the retinal pigment epithelium and choroid accompany the tilt.","Refractive error is usually myopic astigmatism with the astigmatic axis oriented obliquely along the ectasia.","Superotemporal visual field defects that characteristically do NOT respect the vertical midline help distinguish it from chiasmal compression.","Refracting the patient and repeating perimetry after correcting the induced myopic defocus can abolish the pseudo-bitemporal field loss.","Considered a mild form of the optic disc dysplasia/coloboma spectrum and is usually a benign, non-progressive finding."],"pathophysiology":"Tilted disc syndrome arises from incomplete or abnormal closure of the embryonic fetal fissure and an oblique entry of the optic nerve through the scleral canal during development. The result is an obliquely inserted, ovoid optic disc with the superotemporal portion elevated and the inferonasal portion recessed, accompanied by an inferonasal ectasia in which the retinal pigment epithelium, choroid and sclera are attenuated. This localised ectasia distorts the regional refractive geometry, producing myopic astigmatism, and the associated retinal thinning creates relative field depression corresponding to the ectatic zone. Because the anomaly straddles but does not strictly honour the vertical raphe, field defects mimic but do not truly respect the vertical meridian.","additionalDifferentials":["Chiasmal compression from pituitary adenoma or craniopharyngioma","Optic nerve coloboma","Peripapillary staphyloma","Myopic degeneration with peripapillary atrophy","Optic disc hypoplasia","Glaucomatous optic neuropathy","Retinoschisis producing field loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Automated static perimetry with refractive correction in place","Optical coherence tomography of the optic nerve head and peripapillary retinal nerve fibre layer","Cycloplegic refraction to characterise myopic astigmatism","Fundus photography documenting situs inversus and inferonasal crescent","Fundus autofluorescence to delineate peripapillary RPE changes","Neuroimaging (MRI of the sella/chiasm) when field defect respects the vertical midline or is progressive"],"redFlags":["Visual field defect that strictly respects the vertical midline, suggesting a chiasmal lesion rather than the disc anomaly","Progressive field loss or new dyschromatopsia on follow-up","Development of a serous macular detachment or subretinal fluid","Choroidal neovascular membrane presenting with sudden central distortion"],"prognosis":"Vision is generally stable and good once refractive error is corrected, and the anomaly itself is non-progressive. Rarely, associated maculopathy such as serous detachment or choroidal neovascularisation may threaten central acuity.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting the pseudo-bitemporal field defect as chiasmal compression and initiating unnecessary neuroimaging without first correcting refraction and repeating perimetry.","Overlooking associated inferonasal serous retinal detachment or choroidal neovascularisation as the cause of new symptoms.","Attributing the ovoid disc appearance to glaucoma and treating for raised intraocular pressure inappropriately.","Failing to recognise associated systemic or midline associations in syndromic cases."],"management":["Correct the myopic astigmatism with spectacles or contact lenses, which is the mainstay and generally restores good vision","Reassure and observe, as the anomaly is congenital and non-progressive","Confirm that visual field defects do not respect the vertical midline, arranging MRI of the sella and chiasm if a defect is progressive or truly respects the midline to exclude chiasmal compression","Monitor the macula for complications such as serous retinal detachment or choroidal neovascularisation","Treat choroidal neovascularisation, if it develops, with intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy","Follow with automated perimetry and optical coherence tomography to track fields and the peripapillary retina"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Neuro-Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"timothy_syndrome":{"name":"Timothy syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CACNA1C-related disorder","long QT syndrome type 8","LQT8","Timothy syndrome type 1"],"chapter":null,"source":"ESC/AHA guidelines on ventricular arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death; specialist inherited arrhythmia literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare multisystem disorder caused by gain-of-function variants in the L-type calcium channel gene CACNA1C, classified as long QT syndrome type 8","Marked QT prolongation predisposes to life-threatening ventricular arrhythmia and sudden death, often in infancy or early childhood","Extracardiac features commonly include cutaneous syndactyly of the fingers and toes, facial dysmorphism, and neurodevelopmental problems such as autism spectrum features","Congenital heart defects, immune dysfunction, hypoglycaemia and dental abnormalities can also occur","The classic form usually arises from de novo variants, so a negative family history does not exclude it","Management focuses on preventing arrhythmia with beta-blockers, careful avoidance of QT-prolonging drugs and an implantable cardioverter defibrillator in high-risk children","Functional 2:1 atrioventricular block can occur because the very long ventricular repolarisation leaves the ventricle refractory to alternate atrial beats","Care is multidisciplinary given the cardiac, developmental and systemic involvement"],"pathophysiology":"Timothy syndrome is caused by gain-of-function variants in CACNA1C, which encodes the pore-forming subunit of the L-type calcium channel (CaV1.2). The variants impair voltage-dependent inactivation of the channel, so calcium entry into cells is prolonged. In cardiomyocytes this lengthens the action potential and markedly prolongs the QT interval, promoting early afterdepolarisations and torsades-de-pointes ventricular arrhythmia. Because CaV1.2 is expressed widely, the same channel dysfunction disrupts development and function of other tissues, producing the syndactyly, neurodevelopmental, immune and other systemic features that distinguish the syndrome from isolated long QT syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Other genetic long QT syndrome subtypes","Acquired QT prolongation from drugs or electrolyte disturbance","Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome","Andersen-Tawil syndrome","Other syndromic causes of syndactyly with cardiac disease","Congenital heart block from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG demonstrating marked QT prolongation and any functional 2:1 atrioventricular block","Ambulatory monitoring for ventricular arrhythmia","Echocardiography for associated congenital heart defects","Genetic testing for CACNA1C variants","Developmental and neurological assessment","Evaluation of immune function, glucose homeostasis and dental health as indicated"],"redFlags":["Marked QT prolongation with syncope or documented ventricular arrhythmia","Functional 2:1 atrioventricular block in an infant","Aborted cardiac arrest","Syndactyly with cardiac and neurodevelopmental features prompting urgent cardiac evaluation"],"prognosis":"Timothy syndrome carries a high risk of early sudden death from malignant ventricular arrhythmia, and survivors face substantial neurodevelopmental and systemic morbidity; multidisciplinary care and arrhythmia prevention improve but do not eliminate the risk.","pitfalls":["Assuming a negative family history excludes the diagnosis, when most cases are de novo","Attributing an infant's QT prolongation to reversible causes and missing the genetic syndrome","Focusing on the cardiac problem and overlooking the syndactyly and developmental clues","Using QT-prolonging medications that can precipitate torsades","Underestimating the systemic and immune complications"],"management":["Prevent arrhythmia with beta-blockade as the cornerstone of cardiac management","Strictly avoid QT-prolonging drugs and correct electrolyte disturbances that further lengthen the QT interval","Implant a cardioverter defibrillator in high-risk children, and consider left cardiac sympathetic denervation for breakthrough arrhythmia","Treat acute torsades-de-pointes with magnesium and defibrillation, with a calcium channel blocker such as verapamil considered given the underlying L-type calcium channel gain of function","Provide multidisciplinary support for extracardiac features, including surgical release of syndactyly, neurodevelopmental and autism support, and attention to immune, glucose and dental problems","Monitor with serial ECG, QT assessment and ambulatory rhythm recording, and screen for congenital heart defects with echocardiography","Offer genetic counselling and cardiac evaluation of relatives, recognising that most cases arise from de novo variants so a negative family history does not exclude it"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with ventricular arrhythmias and the prevention of sudden cardiac death","GeneReviews: Timothy syndrome / CACNA1C-related disorder"],"crossLinks":[]},"tinea_versicolor":{"name":"Tinea Versicolor","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pityriasis versicolor","PV","Malassezia infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as multiple well-defined oval macules and patches on the upper trunk, shoulders, and neck, which coalesce and carry a fine, powdery scale revealed by gently stretching or scratching the skin","Lesions vary in colour, appearing hypopigmented on tanned or darker skin and pink-brown on paler skin, which explains the name versicolor","It is caused by an overgrowth of commensal Malassezia yeast rather than a true dermatophyte, so despite the name it is not classic ringworm","Warmth, humidity, sweating, oily skin, and immunosuppression favour the shift from yeast to the pathogenic filamentous form","Microscopy of scale shows the typical spaghetti-and-meatballs pattern of short hyphae and spores, confirming the diagnosis","Treatment relies on topical antifungal or keratolytic agents, with systemic antifungals reserved for extensive or recurrent disease; recurrence is common and prophylactic regimens may be needed","Warn patients that pigmentary changes can persist for weeks to months after successful treatment, as this does not indicate treatment failure","A Wood's lamp may show a coppery-orange or pale fluorescence, aiding assessment"],"pathophysiology":"Tinea versicolor results from overgrowth of lipophilic Malassezia yeasts that are normal residents of sebum-rich skin. Under predisposing conditions such as heat, humidity, occlusion, and increased sebum, the yeast converts to its hyphal, pathogenic form and proliferates within the stratum corneum. The organism produces dicarboxylic acids, notably azelaic acid, that inhibit melanocyte tyrosinase and cause the hypopigmented patches, while an associated low-grade inflammatory and hyperkeratotic response produces the scale and, in some, hyperpigmentation. Because the yeast is part of the normal flora, the condition is more a disturbance of host-commensal balance than a transmissible infection, and it readily recurs.","additionalDifferentials":["Vitiligo (complete depigmentation without scale)","Pityriasis alba","Post-inflammatory hypo- or hyperpigmentation","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Tinea corporis due to a true dermatophyte","Erythrasma or, when hypopigmented patches are extensive, early indeterminate leprosy in endemic areas"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Usually clinical, confirmed by microscopy of skin scrapings showing hyphae and spores","Wood's lamp examination for characteristic fluorescence","Culture is generally unnecessary because the organism is a normal commensal","Consideration of underlying immunosuppression or endocrine factors in extensive or recalcitrant disease"],"redFlags":["Widespread or unusually treatment-resistant disease prompting evaluation for immunosuppression","Persistent hypopigmented patches that lack scale and could represent another disorder such as vitiligo or, in endemic settings, leprosy","Signs of deeper Malassezia infection such as folliculitis with pustules in an immunocompromised patient"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and responds well to antifungal therapy, but recurrence is frequent because the causative yeast is part of the normal skin flora and predisposing conditions persist; residual pigmentary change resolves gradually over weeks to months once treated.","pitfalls":["Interpreting residual hypopigmentation after treatment as treatment failure","Confusing hypopigmented patches with vitiligo and omitting to examine for scale","Failing to counsel about the high recurrence rate and the role of maintenance therapy","Overlooking predisposing factors such as heat, humidity, or immunosuppression that drive relapse"],"management":["Treat localised disease with topical antifungals such as ketoconazole or other azole creams and shampoos, selenium sulphide or zinc pyrithione preparations applied across the affected trunk and shoulders, or with keratolytic agents.","Reserve short courses of an oral azole such as itraconazole or fluconazole for extensive, recalcitrant or frequently recurring disease, avoiding oral terbinafine, which is ineffective against Malassezia.","Counsel on modifiable triggers including heat, humidity, sweating, occlusive clothing and oily skin to reduce yeast overgrowth and recurrence.","Set expectations that hypopigmentation persists for weeks to months after successful treatment and does not indicate failure, whereas scaling and itch resolve first.","For frequent recurrence, use intermittent prophylactic topical antifungal regimens, or periodic oral therapy where topical measures are insufficient.","In extensive or treatment-resistant disease, investigate and address underlying immunosuppression or predisposing endocrine factors.","Confirm the diagnosis and treatment response with microscopy of skin scale showing short hyphae and spores, supported by Wood's-lamp examination where the clinical picture is unclear."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"tinnitus":{"name":"Tinnitus","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ringing in the ears","subjective tinnitus","objective tinnitus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Tinnitus is the perception of sound in the absence of an external source and is a symptom rather than a disease in itself","It is usefully divided into subjective tinnitus, heard only by the patient, and the much rarer objective tinnitus, which can also be detected by the examiner","Most subjective tinnitus accompanies sensorineural hearing loss from noise exposure, presbycusis, ototoxic drugs or Meniere disease","Pulsatile tinnitus that is synchronous with the heartbeat suggests a vascular cause such as a glomus tumour, an arteriovenous malformation or a dural sinus abnormality and needs vascular imaging","Rhythmic clicking that is not pulse-synchronous may be due to palatal or middle ear muscle myoclonus or a patulous Eustachian tube","Unilateral tinnitus with asymmetrical hearing loss should prompt evaluation for a retrocochlear lesion such as an acoustic neuroma","Aggravating factors such as ototoxic and other implicated medications, caffeine, stress and poor sleep should be reviewed","Because there is often no curative treatment, management centres on treating any underlying cause, hearing aids or sound therapy for masking, and cognitive and habituation-based approaches such as tinnitus retraining therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Subjective tinnitus is thought to arise from aberrant neural activity generated when the cochlea or auditory nerve is damaged; loss of input leads to maladaptive changes and increased spontaneous firing and reorganisation in the central auditory pathways, so the brain perceives sound that is not present. Emotional and attentional networks amplify and sustain the perception, which explains why distress and the loudness of tinnitus are only loosely related. Objective tinnitus, in contrast, has a genuine internal acoustic source: turbulent or pulsatile blood flow in vascular lesions, or repetitive contraction of palatal, tensor tympani or stapedius muscles, generates a real sound transmitted to the ear.","additionalDifferentials":["Noise-induced or age-related sensorineural hearing loss","Meniere disease","Ototoxicity from drugs","Otosclerosis or middle ear effusion","Acoustic neuroma or other retrocochlear lesion","Vascular causes (glomus tumour, arteriovenous malformation, carotid or dural sinus disease)","Palatal or middle ear myoclonus and patulous Eustachian tube"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy and pure tone audiometry to characterise associated hearing loss","Tympanometry and acoustic reflex testing where middle ear or myoclonic causes are suspected","Tinnitus pitch and loudness matching for assessment and rehabilitation","MRI of the internal auditory meatus for unilateral or asymmetrical tinnitus","CT/MR angiography or venography for pulsatile tinnitus","Blood tests for anaemia, thyroid dysfunction or vascular risk factors where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Unilateral tinnitus with asymmetrical hearing loss suggesting a retrocochlear tumour","Pulsatile tinnitus requiring vascular imaging to exclude a treatable vascular lesion","Associated neurological signs, facial numbness or weakness","Sudden hearing loss accompanying the tinnitus, which is an emergency","Tinnitus with severe distress, depression or suicidal ideation needing urgent mental health support"],"prognosis":"Many patients habituate over time and cope well with reassurance, sound enrichment and psychological approaches, and tinnitus linked to a treatable cause may improve when that cause is addressed; however, some individuals have persistent, distressing tinnitus that requires ongoing support.","pitfalls":["Missing an acoustic neuroma behind unilateral tinnitus","Failing to image pulsatile tinnitus for a vascular source","Dismissing the symptom without exploring its psychological impact and sleep disturbance","Overlooking ototoxic medication or reversible contributors","Promising a cure rather than framing management around control and habituation"],"management":["Identify and treat any underlying cause, such as fitting hearing aids for sensorineural loss, managing Meniere disease or stopping ototoxic drugs","Investigate and refer red-flag patterns: unilateral or asymmetrical tinnitus for MRI of the internal auditory meatus to exclude an acoustic neuroma, and pulsatile tinnitus for vascular imaging","Offer sound enrichment or masking to reduce the prominence of the tinnitus","Provide psychological therapies such as cognitive behavioural therapy and tinnitus retraining therapy to aid habituation and reduce distress","Give reassurance and education, explaining that tinnitus is a symptom and that most people habituate over time","Treat associated anxiety, depression and insomnia, which amplify and sustain the perception","Consider treatment directed at objective causes, such as intervention for a vascular lesion or management of palatal or middle-ear myoclonus"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"titinopathy":{"name":"Titinopathy (TTN-related myopathy and cardiomyopathy)","system":"Neuromuscular / Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TTN myopathy","Titin myopathy","Tibial muscular dystrophy (Udd myopathy)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neuromuscular and cardiomyopathy genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by variants in TTN, which encodes titin — the giant sarcomeric protein spanning half the sarcomere from Z-disc to M-band that governs passive elasticity and sarcomere assembly.","The clinical spectrum is exceptionally broad and highly domain- and inheritance-dependent, ranging from dominant late-onset distal disease to severe recessive congenital and limb-girdle myopathies.","Tibial muscular dystrophy (Udd myopathy) is a dominant, adult-onset distal myopathy from C-terminal M-band variants, beginning with anterior lower-leg weakness and foot drop.","Recessive titinopathies include early-onset myopathy with fatal cardiomyopathy, congenital core-like myopathy, and Emery-Dreifuss-like phenotypes with contractures.","Heterozygous truncating TTN variants in the A-band are the single commonest genetic cause of dilated cardiomyopathy, often without skeletal muscle disease.","Muscle biopsy may show dystrophic changes, rimmed vacuoles or central cores, and calpain-3 can be secondarily reduced, mimicking primary calpainopathy.","Because TTN is enormous, deep intronic and structural variants can be missed, and pathogenicity interpretation is challenging given frequent benign truncations in the population.","Cardiac surveillance is essential in recessive titinopathy and in carriers of A-band truncating variants given the risk of dilated cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia."],"pathophysiology":"Titin is the largest protein in the human body and forms a continuous filament from the Z-disc to the M-band, acting as a molecular spring that sets the sarcomere's passive tension and resting length, serving as a template for sarcomere assembly and participating in mechanosensing and signalling. Its extreme size and multiple isoforms mean that the functional consequence of a variant depends critically on which domain and which exons are affected and whether those exons are constitutively expressed. M-band variants disrupt the C-terminal kinase region and interaction with calpain-3 and are responsible for dominant tibial muscular dystrophy and, in the homozygous or compound state, more severe limb-girdle and early-onset phenotypes; secondary calpain-3 deficiency explains overlap with calpainopathy on biopsy. Variants affecting the elastic I-band or the structural A-band, and biallelic combinations, produce congenital myopathy, cardiomyopathy and contractural (Emery-Dreifuss-like) phenotypes. In the heart, heterozygous truncating variants clustered in the A-band impair sarcomere integrity under haemodynamic load, producing dilated cardiomyopathy with a propensity to arrhythmia, whereas equivalent truncations in exons not highly expressed in the heart may be clinically silent.","additionalDifferentials":["Calpainopathy and other limb-girdle muscular dystrophies","Distal myopathies (GNE, dysferlin, anoctamin-5, myotilin)","Myofibrillar myopathies including filaminopathy and desminopathy","Congenital myopathies with cores (e.g. RYR1-related)","Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy (emerin, lamin A/C)","Other genetic dilated cardiomyopathies (lamin A/C, filamin C)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive TTN analysis (next-generation sequencing with copy-number and, where possible, deep-intronic assessment) and segregation studies","Muscle biopsy with histochemistry, including calpain-3 immunoblot and assessment for cores/rimmed vacuoles","Muscle MRI to characterise the selective pattern of muscle involvement","Echocardiography and cardiac MRI for dilated cardiomyopathy and fibrosis","ECG and ambulatory rhythm monitoring for arrhythmia and conduction disease","Respiratory function testing in congenital and severe recessive forms"],"redFlags":["Signs of dilated cardiomyopathy or new arrhythmia, particularly with A-band truncating variants","Early-onset severe myopathy with cardiac involvement in infancy","Progressive respiratory muscle weakness","Contractures with cardiac conduction disease suggesting an Emery-Dreifuss-like course","Family history of premature sudden cardiac death or unexplained heart failure"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies enormously by phenotype: dominant tibial muscular dystrophy is slowly progressive with preserved lifespan and ambulation into later life, whereas recessive early-onset forms with cardiomyopathy can be severe or fatal. Cardiac and respiratory involvement are the principal determinants of outcome.","pitfalls":["Misattributing secondary calpain-3 reduction to primary calpainopathy and stopping the diagnostic work-up","Overcalling pathogenicity of TTN truncations given frequent benign variants in the general population","Missing deep intronic or structural variants because of incomplete sequencing coverage","Neglecting cardiac screening in recessive disease and in A-band truncation carriers","Assuming a single uniform phenotype rather than recognising strong domain-dependent variability"],"management":["Manage dilated cardiomyopathy with guideline-directed heart failure therapy, as there is no disease-specific drug for the underlying titin defect","Treat arrhythmia and conduction disease and offer an implantable defibrillator or pacemaker where indicated for high-risk cardiac phenotypes","Support weakness with physiotherapy, ankle-foot orthoses for foot drop, occupational therapy and management of contractures","Monitor respiratory function and provide non-invasive ventilation for respiratory muscle failure, particularly in congenital and severe recessive forms","Undertake cardiac surveillance with echocardiography, cardiac MRI and ambulatory rhythm monitoring, including in relatives","Offer genetic counselling and cascade screening, tailoring advice to the domain and inheritance pattern of the variant","Coordinate care between neuromuscular neurology and cardiology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard neuromuscular disease textbooks","Inherited cardiomyopathy and cardiovascular genetics references","Titin-related myopathy review literature (source-neutral)"],"crossLinks":[]},"tmem165_cdg":{"name":"TMEM165-CDG (CDG-IIk)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TMEM165","congenital disorder of glycosylation type IIk","CDG-IIk","Golgi manganese/calcium transporter defect"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A type II CDG caused by defects in TMEM165, a Golgi transporter involved in manganese and calcium homeostasis required for glycosyltransferase activity","Distinguished by a prominent skeletal phenotype with spondylo-epi-metaphyseal dysplasia, short stature and skeletal dysplasia in addition to developmental delay","Hepatic involvement, growth failure and variable dysmorphism accompany the skeletal features","Transferrin analysis shows a type II pattern reflecting defective galactosylation of complex glycan antennae","Because the defect involves Golgi manganese handling, in vitro and some clinical data suggest galactose or manganese supplementation can improve glycosylation markers","Management is supportive and multidisciplinary; galactose supplementation may be trialled with biochemical monitoring","Diagnosis is confirmed by TMEM165 molecular genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"TMEM165 is a Golgi membrane protein that regulates manganese and calcium homeostasis in the secretory pathway. Many Golgi glycosyltransferases, particularly galactosyltransferases, depend on manganese as a cofactor, so loss of TMEM165 function impairs terminal galactosylation of complex N-glycans, producing a type II processing defect on transferrin analysis. The manganese dependence explains why supplying galactose or manganese can partially restore glycosylation. Hypoglycosylation of bone matrix and other proteins is thought to drive the characteristic skeletal dysplasia.","additionalDifferentials":["Other type II CDG subtypes (e.g. MGAT2-CDG, B4GALT1-CDG, COG-complex CDG, SLC35A2-CDG)","Primary skeletal dysplasias and spondylo-epi-metaphyseal dysplasias","Mucopolysaccharidoses and other storage disorders with bone involvement","Type I CDGs when the transferrin pattern is ambiguous","Syndromic short stature","Hepatic involvement from other metabolic causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transferrin isoelectric focusing or mass spectrometry showing a type II pattern","TMEM165 molecular genetic testing","Skeletal survey for spondylo-epi-metaphyseal dysplasia","Liver function tests and imaging","Growth and endocrine assessment","Biochemical monitoring during a galactose supplementation trial"],"redFlags":["Progressive skeletal deformity or severe short stature","Significant hepatic dysfunction","Developmental regression","Failure to thrive with multisystem involvement"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is variable; the skeletal dysplasia and growth failure are often prominent, and biochemical improvement with galactose supplementation has been reported, although its long-term clinical benefit is still being defined. Neurodevelopmental outcome depends on disease severity.","pitfalls":["Investigating the skeletal dysplasia in isolation without considering a glycosylation disorder","Not distinguishing type I from type II defects on transferrin analysis","Overlooking a potential galactose or manganese-responsive component","Interpreting biochemical response as proven clinical cure"],"management":["Trial oral galactose supplementation, given the Golgi manganese dependence of galactosylation, and monitor transferrin glycosylation markers and clinical response during the trial","Provide multidisciplinary supportive care directed at the skeletal, hepatic and developmental manifestations","Manage the spondylo-epi-metaphyseal dysplasia and short stature with orthopaedic input and physiotherapy","Monitor liver function and imaging for hepatic involvement","Assess growth, endocrine status and development, providing developmental support as needed","Offer genetic counselling and family screening, as the disorder is inherited"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID, congenital disorders of glycosylation","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics, disorders of glycosylation"],"crossLinks":[]},"tmj_disorder":{"name":"Temporomandibular joint disorder","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TMD","temporomandibular disorder","TMJ dysfunction","myofascial pain dysfunction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Temporomandibular disorders are a group of musculoskeletal conditions affecting the jaw joints, the muscles of mastication, or both","Typical features are jaw pain, joint sounds such as clicking or crepitus, limited or deviated mouth opening, and pain on chewing","Pain is often localised to the preauricular region and may radiate to the temple, ear, or face, and can mimic ear or dental pathology","Most cases are muscular (myofascial) rather than intra-articular, and parafunctional habits like clenching and grinding are common contributors","Disc displacement with reduction typically produces reciprocal clicking, whereas displacement without reduction causes locking and restricted opening","It is largely a clinical diagnosis; imaging is reserved for atypical, persistent, or red-flag presentations","Initial management is conservative, including reassurance, jaw rest, soft diet, physiotherapy, occlusal splints, and stress and habit control","Psychological factors, stress, and chronic pain behaviours strongly influence symptoms and treatment response"],"pathophysiology":"Temporomandibular disorders arise from a multifactorial interplay of biomechanical overload, parafunctional habits, trauma, and psychosocial stress acting on the masticatory system. Sustained muscle hyperactivity from clenching or grinding produces myofascial pain and fatigue, while abnormal loading and internal derangement of the joint can disturb the normal disc-condyle relationship, causing clicking, locking, or degenerative change. Central sensitisation and psychological factors can amplify and perpetuate pain. The result is a spectrum ranging from purely muscular pain to intra-articular disc displacement and degenerative joint disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Odontogenic pain from pulpal or periapical disease","Otitis or other ear pathology","Trigeminal neuralgia or other neuropathic facial pain","Giant cell (temporal) arteritis in older patients","Salivary gland disease","Referred pain from cervical spine or headache disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of jaw movement, joint sounds, and muscle tenderness","Evaluation of occlusion and parafunctional habits","MRI for suspected internal derangement when symptoms persist","CT or cone-beam imaging for bony or degenerative joint changes","Inflammatory markers if temporal arteritis is a concern in older patients"],"redFlags":["New onset facial pain with headache, scalp tenderness, or visual symptoms in an older patient suggesting giant cell arteritis","Progressive limitation of opening, a hard end-feel, or a mass suggesting joint ankylosis or neoplasia","Unilateral worsening symptoms with cranial nerve signs pointing to a sinister cause","Recent significant trauma raising the possibility of condylar fracture"],"prognosis":"Most patients improve with conservative, reversible measures and the condition often follows a self-limiting or fluctuating course, though a minority develop chronic pain or progressive degenerative joint change.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to irreversible occlusal or surgical treatment before trying conservative care","Missing giant cell arteritis in an older patient with jaw and temporal symptoms","Attributing all facial pain to the joint and overlooking dental or neuropathic causes","Neglecting parafunctional habits and psychosocial factors that drive symptoms"],"management":["Reassurance and patient education with jaw rest, a soft diet, and elimination of parafunctional habits such as clenching and grinding as first-line reversible therapy","Short-term simple analgesia with NSAIDs and, for muscular pain, a trial of a muscle relaxant","Supervised jaw physiotherapy including range-of-motion and stretching exercises","Occlusal splint or stabilisation appliance to offload the joint and protect against nocturnal bruxism","Address psychosocial contributors with stress management and cognitive behavioural therapy for chronic or centrally sensitised pain","Intra-articular corticosteroid injection or arthrocentesis for refractory intra-articular pain or persistent locking","Refer to oral and maxillofacial surgery for arthroscopy or open surgery when internal derangement or degenerative disease fails conservative care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"tnf_receptor_associated_periodic_syndrome":{"name":"TNF Receptor-Associated Periodic Syndrome","system":"Multisystem / Immune","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TRAPS","Familial Hibernian Fever","TNFRSF1A-associated periodic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rheumatology and clinical immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant autoinflammatory disorder caused by mutations in the TNFRSF1A gene encoding the type 1 tumour necrosis factor receptor (p55 TNFR).","Characterised by recurrent, often prolonged febrile attacks that typically last longer than the febrile episodes seen in most other periodic fever syndromes.","Migratory myalgia overlying a centrifugally spreading erythematous macular or patchy rash is a highly characteristic feature, reflecting monocytic fasciitis rather than true myositis.","Periorbital oedema with conjunctivitis and periorbital pain is a classic and relatively distinctive sign that should prompt consideration of TRAPS.","Serosal involvement is common, producing abdominal pain (peritoneal inflammation) and pleuritic chest pain; attacks are accompanied by a marked acute-phase response.","AA (secondary) amyloidosis is the most serious long-term complication, presenting with proteinuria and progressive renal impairment, and is more likely with cysteine-residue mutations.","Cysteine and other structural mutations tend to produce a more severe, penetrant phenotype, whereas low-penetrance variants such as R92Q may cause milder or atypical disease.","Interleukin-1 blockade is generally effective for controlling attacks and the acute-phase response, and corticosteroids can abort or attenuate individual episodes."],"pathophysiology":"TRAPS results from heterozygous mutations in TNFRSF1A, which encodes the p55 type 1 receptor for tumour necrosis factor. Many pathogenic variants disrupt conserved cysteine residues within the extracellular cysteine-rich domains, causing receptor misfolding and retention within the endoplasmic reticulum rather than a simple defect of receptor shedding as originally proposed. The accumulated misfolded receptor triggers a maladaptive cellular stress response with enhanced mitochondrial reactive oxygen species production, which in turn potentiates innate immune signalling and augments secretion of proinflammatory cytokines, particularly interleukin-1 beta, on stimulation. The result is dysregulated, largely antigen-independent activation of the innate immune system with an exuberant acute-phase response during attacks. Persistent subclinical inflammation between overt episodes, together with sustained serum amyloid A elevation, drives deposition of AA amyloid fibrils in the kidneys and other organs in a substantial minority of untreated patients, underlying the principal cause of long-term morbidity and mortality.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial Mediterranean fever","Mevalonate kinase deficiency (hyperimmunoglobulinaemia D syndrome)","Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes","PFAPA syndrome","Adult-onset Still disease / systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Systemic vasculitis or connective tissue disease","Occult infection or lymphoma presenting with recurrent fever"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing of the TNFRSF1A gene for pathogenic variants","Acute-phase reactants (C-reactive protein and serum amyloid A) measured during and between attacks","Full blood count showing leucocytosis and reactive changes during episodes","Serum immunoglobulin D and urinary mevalonic acid to help exclude mevalonate kinase deficiency","Urinalysis and urine protein quantification to screen for amyloid-related proteinuria","Renal or rectal/subcutaneous fat biopsy with Congo red staining if amyloidosis is suspected","Serum amyloid P component scintigraphy where available to assess amyloid burden"],"redFlags":["New or increasing proteinuria or declining renal function suggesting AA amyloidosis","Prolonged unremitting febrile attacks with severe systemic upset","Nephrotic-range protein loss with oedema and hypoalbuminaemia","Signs of serosal complications such as severe abdominal pain mimicking a surgical abdomen","Failure to respond to standard attack-aborting therapy, prompting reassessment of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"With modern interleukin-1-directed therapy, attack frequency and the chronic acute-phase response can usually be well controlled, and the outlook is favourable. The main determinant of long-term prognosis is the development of AA amyloidosis, which historically caused significant renal morbidity and premature death in untreated patients.","pitfalls":["Assuming the febrile attacks are always short; TRAPS episodes are frequently prolonged and may be misattributed to infection.","Interpreting a low-penetrance variant such as R92Q as definitively causative without correlating with the clinical picture.","Overlooking migratory myalgia with overlying rash and periorbital oedema, which are strong diagnostic clues.","Failing to monitor urine for proteinuria, allowing amyloid nephropathy to progress silently.","Attributing recurrent abdominal pain to a surgical cause and proceeding to unnecessary laparotomy."],"management":["Interleukin-1 blockade with anakinra or canakinumab as the mainstay for controlling attacks and suppressing the chronic acute-phase response","Corticosteroids to abort or shorten acute febrile attacks, with NSAIDs for symptomatic relief of fever and serositis","Consider etanercept where IL-1 inhibition is unavailable, avoiding monoclonal anti-TNF agents such as infliximab which can paradoxically worsen disease","Monitor acute-phase reactants including serum amyloid A between attacks and screen urine for proteinuria to detect AA amyloidosis early","Autosomal dominant inheritance warrants genetic counselling and testing of at-risk relatives","Coordinate care through rheumatology or clinical immunology, with nephrology referral if amyloid nephropathy develops","Escalate to sustained biologic therapy for frequent attacks, persistent subclinical inflammation, or evidence of amyloid deposition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rheumatology (standard reference)","Textbook of autoinflammatory diseases (standard reference)","International periodic fever and autoinflammatory syndrome guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"tnf_receptor_periodic":{"name":"TNF receptor-associated periodic syndrome","system":"Rheumatology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TRAPS","familial Hibernian fever","TNFRSF1A-associated periodic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard rheumatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant autoinflammatory disorder caused by mutations in TNFRSF1A, the gene for the type 1 tumour necrosis factor receptor","Febrile attacks are notably longer than in most other periodic fever syndromes, often lasting one to three weeks","Migratory myalgia overlying a spreading erythematous rash is characteristic and reflects underlying fasciitis","Periorbital oedema with conjunctivitis and periorbital pain is a distinctive clue","Serositis causing abdominal or chest pain and arthralgia commonly accompany attacks","Acute-phase reactants are markedly elevated during flares and may remain elevated between them","Secondary AA amyloidosis is an important long-term risk, particularly with certain high-penetrance mutations","IL-1 inhibition is generally effective; TNF-directed therapy has variable and sometimes paradoxical responses"],"pathophysiology":"Mutations in TNFRSF1A impair normal shedding and folding of the type 1 TNF receptor, causing intracellular retention and misfolding of the receptor with activation of stress and inflammatory signalling pathways. Enhanced production of interleukin-1 and other proinflammatory mediators drives prolonged systemic and localised inflammation of skin, muscle fascia, serosal surfaces, and joints.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial Mediterranean fever and other hereditary periodic fevers","Adult-onset Still disease","Systemic vasculitis","Cellulitis or infective myositis (given the rash and myalgia)","Recurrent or chronic serositis of other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["TNFRSF1A genetic testing","Acute-phase reactants (CRP, ESR, serum amyloid A) during and between attacks","Full blood count showing leukocytosis during flares","Urinalysis for proteinuria to screen for amyloidosis","Imaging or biopsy of affected muscle/fascia when the diagnosis is uncertain"],"redFlags":["Persistent proteinuria indicating secondary AA amyloidosis with risk of renal failure","Prolonged unremitting fever prompting exclusion of infection or malignancy","High-penetrance mutation genotype associated with greater amyloidosis risk","Severe serositis mimicking a surgical or cardiac emergency"],"prognosis":"Attacks tend to recur over many years; with effective IL-1 targeted therapy inflammation is well controlled and amyloidosis risk reduced, but poorly controlled disease, especially with high-penetrance genotypes, carries a meaningful risk of amyloid nephropathy.","pitfalls":["Confusing the migratory rash and myalgia with recurrent cellulitis and giving repeated antibiotics","Assuming attacks are too long to represent a periodic fever syndrome","Overlooking amyloidosis screening in long-standing disease","Expecting a uniform response to TNF inhibitors, which can be unpredictable"],"management":["Interleukin-1-targeted therapy with anakinra or canakinumab as the mainstay to control recurrent attacks and dampen chronic inflammation","Corticosteroids to terminate or attenuate acute febrile attacks, with NSAIDs for fever, myalgia, and serositis","Reserve etanercept for where IL-1 inhibition is unavailable, and avoid monoclonal anti-TNF agents such as infliximab which may exacerbate disease","Serial monitoring of C-reactive protein and serum amyloid A, with urinalysis for proteinuria to detect AA amyloidosis","Offer genetic counselling and cascade testing of relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance","Multidisciplinary rheumatology-led care with nephrology input should amyloid nephropathy arise","Escalate biologic therapy for high-penetrance genotypes or breakthrough inflammation to reduce amyloidosis risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"tnf_receptor_periodic_syndrome":{"name":"TNF-receptor-associated periodic syndrome (TRAPS)","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TRAPS","TNFRSF1A","familial Hibernian fever","TNF receptor 1-associated periodic syndrome","autosomal dominant periodic fever"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology / EULAR recommendations for autoinflammatory diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant autoinflammatory disorder caused by mutations in TNFRSF1A, encoding the type 1 tumour necrosis factor receptor","Fever episodes are characteristically prolonged, often lasting one to three weeks, distinguishing it from the shorter attacks of other periodic fever syndromes","A migratory, tender erythematous rash overlying underlying myalgia, typically moving distally along a limb, is a suggestive feature","Periorbital oedema with conjunctivitis or periorbital pain is a classic ocular manifestation","Attacks commonly include serositis (abdominal and chest pain), arthralgia and monocytic fasciitis","AA (secondary) amyloidosis is the major long-term complication, driven by chronic inflammation, and warrants surveillance","Therapy is directed at the interleukin-1 axis and, in some cases, interleukin-6 blockade, whereas continuous tumour necrosis factor inhibition has been less reliably effective"],"pathophysiology":"TRAPS is an autosomal dominant autoinflammatory syndrome caused by mutations in TNFRSF1A, the gene for the 55-kDa tumour necrosis factor receptor 1. Rather than a simple loss of receptor shedding, the mutant receptor misfolds and is retained within the endoplasmic reticulum, provoking a cellular stress response, enhanced production of reactive oxygen species, and ligand-independent, exaggerated pro-inflammatory signalling. This drives excess innate cytokine output, particularly along the interleukin-1 pathway, producing recurrent systemic inflammation. Persistent elevation of acute phase proteins over years predisposes to deposition of serum amyloid A protein in tissues and the development of AA amyloidosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial Mediterranean fever","Hyper-IgD syndrome / mevalonate kinase deficiency","Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis or adult-onset Still disease","Cyclical or infective causes of recurrent fever","Inflammatory bowel disease presenting with recurrent abdominal pain and fever"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Acute phase reactants (C-reactive protein and serum amyloid A) during and between attacks","Genetic testing of TNFRSF1A","Full blood count showing neutrophilia during attacks","Urinalysis for proteinuria as an early marker of AA amyloidosis","Serum immunoglobulins including IgD to help distinguish related syndromes","Tissue biopsy with amyloid staining where amyloidosis is suspected"],"redFlags":["New or increasing proteinuria suggesting evolving AA amyloidosis","Persistently elevated inflammatory markers between attacks indicating uncontrolled subclinical inflammation","Severe serositis mimicking an acute surgical abdomen","Progressive renal impairment"],"prognosis":"With effective anti-inflammatory therapy attacks can be controlled and quality of life preserved, but untreated or poorly controlled disease carries a meaningful risk of AA amyloidosis and consequent renal failure, which is the principal determinant of long-term outcome.","pitfalls":["Expecting brief attacks and dismissing TRAPS because fevers are unusually prolonged","Failing to monitor for proteinuria and missing early amyloidosis","Assuming tumour necrosis factor blockade is always effective, when interleukin-1 targeting is often preferred","Repeated surgery for recurrent serositis mistaken for a surgical abdomen"],"management":["Interleukin-1 inhibition with anakinra or canakinumab as the mainstay to suppress attacks and normalise acute-phase proteins","Corticosteroids to shorten acute attacks and NSAIDs for symptomatic control of fever and serositis","Consider etanercept where IL-1 blockade is not accessible, avoiding monoclonal anti-TNF agents such as infliximab","Surveillance for AA amyloidosis with serum amyloid A between attacks and urinary protein screening for early nephropathy","Genetic counselling and TNFRSF1A testing of at-risk family members given autosomal dominant transmission","Rheumatology or clinical immunology coordination with nephrology referral if renal amyloid develops","Escalate to continuous biologic therapy for persistent subclinical inflammation to protect against amyloid deposition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","EULAR recommendations for the management of autoinflammatory diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"toddler_diarrhea":{"name":"Toddler's diarrhoea (chronic nonspecific diarrhoea)","system":"Paediatrics / Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic nonspecific diarrhoea of childhood","toddler's diarrhoea","functional diarrhoea"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard paediatric gastroenterology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common cause of chronic loose stools in an otherwise well, thriving pre-school child, typically between about one and five years of age","Stools are frequent and loose and may contain recognisable undigested food, giving the classic peas-and-carrots appearance","By definition the child grows well, is not dehydrated, and has no blood in the stool or systemic symptoms","It is often linked to dietary factors summarised as the four Fs: excessive fluid, fruit juice, low fat, and high fibre","It reflects rapid intestinal transit rather than malabsorption or infection","Diagnosis is clinical in a thriving child with a normal examination, and extensive investigation is unnecessary in the absence of red flags","Management is reassurance and dietary adjustment, normalising fluid intake, reducing fruit juice and sorbitol-containing drinks, ensuring adequate dietary fat, and avoiding excessive fibre","The condition is self-limiting and typically resolves by school age"],"pathophysiology":"Toddler's diarrhoea is a functional disorder of accelerated gastrointestinal transit rather than a disease of the gut mucosa. Excessive intake of fluids and of fructose- or sorbitol-containing drinks, together with a relatively low-fat diet, speeds transit and reduces the time available for water and nutrient reabsorption. The result is frequent loose stools containing undigested food residue, occurring in a child who is otherwise thriving with no evidence of malabsorption, inflammation, or infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Coeliac disease","Cow's milk protein allergy","Chronic giardiasis or other gut infection","Carbohydrate malabsorption such as lactose intolerance or excess fructose or sorbitol intake","Inflammatory bowel disease in the older child","Cystic fibrosis or other pancreatic insufficiency when there is failure to thrive","Overflow diarrhoea complicating constipation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful growth monitoring and a detailed dietary history, which are the mainstay","Coeliac serology if there are any concerns about growth or symptoms","Stool microscopy, culture, and parasite testing if infection is suspected","Faecal elastase if pancreatic insufficiency is a concern","Full blood count and inflammatory markers if organic disease is suspected","Faecal calprotectin where inflammatory bowel disease is considered","Assessment of fluid and fruit-juice intake"],"redFlags":["Faltering growth or weight loss","Blood or mucus in the stool","Nocturnal diarrhoea or systemic symptoms such as fever or vomiting","Signs of dehydration or malnutrition","Onset in early infancy or persistence beyond the expected age"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent: this is a benign, self-limiting condition that resolves spontaneously, usually by around school age, without long-term consequences.","pitfalls":["Subjecting a thriving child to unnecessary invasive investigation","Over-restricting the diet and causing nutritional harm, particularly inappropriate fat or calorie restriction","Missing coeliac disease or another organic cause when growth is faltering","Reflexly excluding milk without supporting evidence","Failing to address excessive fruit juice and sugary-drink intake"],"management":["Reassure parents that this is a benign, self-limiting functional disorder in a thriving child that requires no medication","Dietary modification targeting the four Fs: reduce excessive fluid and fruit-juice intake and cut fructose- and sorbitol-containing drinks","Normalise dietary fat and avoid an excessively low-fat, high-fibre toddler diet to slow intestinal transit","Maintain regular growth monitoring and plotting to confirm continued thriving","Avoid unnecessary investigation and restrictive elimination diets in the absence of red flags","Safety-net with review if faltering growth, blood in the stool, nocturnal symptoms, or systemic features emerge, prompting evaluation for organic disease such as coeliac disease or inflammatory bowel disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rome diagnostic criteria for paediatric functional gastrointestinal disorders","Standard paediatric gastroenterology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"toddlers_diarrhoea":{"name":"Toddler's diarrhoea","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Chronic nonspecific diarrhoea of childhood","Functional diarrhoea","Peas-and-carrots diarrhoea"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common cause of chronic diarrhoea in a well, thriving preschool child, broadly between six months and five years of age.","Passage of several loose stools daily, often containing recognisable undigested food such as vegetable fragments (the 'peas and carrots' sign).","Classic diurnal pattern: the first stool of the day is larger and more formed, with subsequent stools becoming progressively looser, and no diarrhoea overnight.","Normal growth and the absence of nocturnal symptoms are the cardinal reassuring features and are central to the diagnosis.","Strongly linked to excessive intake of fruit juice and squash rich in fructose and sorbitol, overhydration and paradoxically low-fat, high-fibre 'toddler' diets.","It is a functional disorder of rapid intestinal transit; there is no malabsorption and nutrient absorption is preserved.","Management is dietary through the 'four Fs': ensure adequate Fat, reduce excess Fluid, limit Fruit juice, and provide appropriate rather than excessive Fibre.","Investigations are usually unnecessary when the history is classic and the growth trajectory is normal."],"pathophysiology":"Toddler's diarrhoea is a functional disorder attributed to dysregulated intestinal motility with rapid small- and large-bowel transit, in the absence of mucosal, infective or malabsorptive disease. Excessive ingestion of fluids, particularly fruit juices and squashes rich in poorly absorbed carbohydrates such as fructose and sorbitol, delivers an osmotic carbohydrate load to the colon that exceeds absorptive and fermentative capacity, drawing water into the lumen and accelerating transit. Diets low in fat, which would normally slow gastric emptying and transit, and disproportionately high in fibre further shorten transit time. The net effect is frequent loose stools containing undigested food, without impaired nutrient absorption, so growth and development are preserved.","additionalDifferentials":["Coeliac disease","Cow's milk or other food protein allergy","Giardiasis and other chronic enteric infections","Carbohydrate malabsorption (lactose intolerance, congenital sucrase-isomaltase deficiency)","Cystic fibrosis and other causes of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","Inflammatory bowel disease","Constipation with overflow soiling masquerading as diarrhoea"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plotting and review of the growth chart (central to the diagnosis)","Coeliac serology (tissue transglutaminase IgA with total IgA)","Stool microscopy, culture and Giardia antigen testing","Faecal calprotectin if inflammatory bowel disease is suspected","Faecal elastase or sweat test if pancreatic insufficiency or cystic fibrosis is suspected","Full blood count, inflammatory markers and ferritin","Dietary diary review of fluid, juice, fat and fibre intake"],"redFlags":["Faltering growth or weight loss","Nocturnal diarrhoea waking the child","Blood or mucus in the stool","Bilious or persistent vomiting, significant abdominal pain or distension","Steatorrhoea with bulky, pale, greasy, offensive stools"],"prognosis":"A benign, self-limiting condition that resolves spontaneously by school age, typically around four to five years, as bowel habit matures and continence is established; growth and development are unaffected.","pitfalls":["Undertaking extensive investigations or imposing restrictive diets in a thriving child with a classic history.","Over-restricting dietary fat, which can worsen symptoms and compromise energy intake.","Assuming a functional cause without checking growth and thereby missing coeliac disease or giardiasis.","Overlooking constipation with overflow soiling presenting as apparent diarrhoea.","Escalating fibre inappropriately in the belief that it will firm the stools."],"management":["Reassure the family that this is a benign functional disorder of rapid transit in a well, growing child needing no drug therapy","Reduce excessive fluid intake and limit fructose- and sorbitol-rich fruit juices and squashes that deliver an osmotic carbohydrate load to the colon","Increase dietary fat and moderate excessive fibre to lengthen intestinal transit time","Monitor and plot growth serially, as preserved growth is central to confirming the diagnosis","Avoid unwarranted investigation and empirical exclusion diets when there are no red flags","Review and investigate for organic causes such as coeliac disease, giardiasis, or inflammatory bowel disease only if growth falters or nocturnal or systemic symptoms appear"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Walker's Pediatric Gastrointestinal Disease","Rome IV criteria for paediatric functional gastrointestinal disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"tolosa_hunt_syndrome":{"name":"Tolosa-Hunt syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["THS","painful ophthalmoplegia","idiopathic granulomatous cavernous sinus inflammation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with unilateral, often severe, retro-orbital or periorbital pain accompanied by ipsilateral ophthalmoplegia","Caused by idiopathic granulomatous inflammation of the cavernous sinus or superior orbital fissure","Any combination of the third, fourth, and sixth cranial nerves may be involved, sometimes with the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve","Sympathetic fibres may be affected, producing a Horner syndrome, and optic nerve involvement can occur if the inflammation extends to the orbital apex","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring careful ruling out of tumour, aneurysm, infection, and other inflammatory disease","MRI may show abnormal soft tissue in the cavernous sinus that enhances with contrast","The pain and ophthalmoplegia typically respond promptly to corticosteroids, and a dramatic response supports the diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Tolosa-Hunt syndrome is a non-specific granulomatous inflammation involving the cavernous sinus, superior orbital fissure, or orbital apex. The inflammatory tissue compresses and irritates the ocular motor nerves and the trigeminal fibres passing through this compartment, producing painful ophthalmoplegia. The cause is unknown and the histology resembles that of idiopathic orbital inflammation. Because the same anatomical region can be affected by tumour, aneurysm, infection, and systemic granulomatous disease, the syndrome remains a diagnosis of exclusion supported by imaging and steroid responsiveness.","additionalDifferentials":["Cavernous sinus tumour or metastasis","Intracavernous carotid aneurysm","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Sarcoidosis or granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma with skull base extension","Diabetic ocular motor nerve palsy","Orbital apex fungal infection such as mucormycosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain and orbits with contrast focused on the cavernous sinus","Magnetic resonance or CT angiography to exclude an aneurysm","Inflammatory markers including erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Screening for systemic granulomatous disease such as sarcoidosis and vasculitis","Blood glucose to consider a diabetic nerve palsy","Biopsy in atypical or steroid-unresponsive cases"],"redFlags":["Failure to respond to corticosteroids prompting reconsideration of tumour or infection","Signs of orbital apex involvement with visual loss","Features suggesting an aneurysm or invasive infection","Systemic symptoms pointing to malignancy or vasculitis"],"prognosis":"The syndrome usually responds well to corticosteroids with resolution of pain and gradual recovery of ophthalmoplegia, but recurrences are common and some patients retain residual nerve deficits.","pitfalls":["Accepting the diagnosis without adequately excluding tumour, aneurysm, and infection","Interpreting any improvement on steroids as confirmation, since some tumours and lymphoma also respond transiently","Missing orbital apex extension threatening vision","Overlooking invasive fungal infection in an immunocompromised or diabetic patient"],"management":["Systemic corticosteroids are the mainstay, typically producing rapid relief of pain and gradual recovery of the ophthalmoplegia","Confirm exclusion of tumour, aneurysm, infection, and systemic granulomatous disease before attributing symptoms and committing to immunosuppression","Analgesia for the severe retro-orbital pain during the acute phase","Slow corticosteroid taper guided by symptoms to reduce the risk of relapse","Steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as azathioprine or methotrexate for recurrent or corticosteroid-dependent disease","Serial clinical review and contrast MRI surveillance to confirm resolution of cavernous sinus inflammation and detect relapse","Refer to neurology and ophthalmology, with neurosurgical or biopsy input for atypical or treatment-resistant cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","International Classification of Headache Disorders criteria"],"crossLinks":[]},"top_of_the_basilar_syndrome":{"name":"Top-of-the-basilar syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rostral basilar artery syndrome","top of the basilar syndrome","basilar apex syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from occlusion of the rostral (apical) basilar artery, usually embolic, causing ischaemia of the midbrain, thalamus, and occipitotemporal lobes","Produces a striking combination of visual, oculomotor, behavioural, and consciousness disturbances rather than prominent limb weakness","Vertical gaze palsy, convergence abnormalities, and pupillary changes reflect involvement of the rostral midbrain and pretectal region","Somnolence, hypersomnolence, or fluctuating consciousness follows bilateral paramedian thalamic and reticular involvement","Cortical visual loss, visual hallucinations (peduncular hallucinosis), and memory disturbance arise from occipitotemporal and thalamic infarction","Motor deficits may be minimal, so the diagnosis is easily missed if attention focuses only on limb power","It is a neurological emergency because it often reflects distal basilar embolism amenable to recanalisation"],"pathophysiology":"Top-of-the-basilar syndrome follows occlusion, typically embolic, of the distal basilar artery and its terminal branches, which supply the midbrain, thalamus, and the medial temporal and occipital lobes through the posterior cerebral and thalamoperforating arteries. Ischaemia of the rostral midbrain and pretectum disrupts vertical gaze, convergence, and pupillary control, while bilateral paramedian thalamic and reticular infarction impairs arousal and produces somnolence and behavioural change. Involvement of the occipitotemporal cortex causes cortical visual loss, visual hallucinations, and amnesia. The clustering of eye movement, arousal, visual, and memory deficits with relatively preserved strength reflects this rostral posterior-circulation territory.","additionalDifferentials":["Bilateral paramedian thalamic infarction from an artery of Percheron occlusion","Metabolic or toxic encephalopathy","Non-convulsive status epilepticus","Wernicke encephalopathy","Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome","Basilar migraine or other transient posterior circulation events"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diffusion-weighted MRI showing infarction across midbrain, thalamus, and occipitotemporal regions","CT or MR angiography of the basilar apex and posterior circulation to identify occlusion","Urgent vascular imaging to assess suitability for thrombolysis or thrombectomy","Assessment of vertical gaze, pupils, and level of consciousness","Cardiac evaluation for an embolic source including ECG and echocardiography","Thiamine status and metabolic screen where an encephalopathic mimic is possible"],"redFlags":["Fluctuating or declining consciousness signalling active basilar apex occlusion","Progressive brainstem signs indicating evolving basilar thrombosis needing urgent intervention","Bilateral thalamic involvement causing profound hypersomnolence","Cortical blindness with denial of deficit that may mask serious infarction"],"prognosis":"Outcome hinges on the extent of infarction and speed of recanalisation; timely reperfusion can yield good recovery, but extensive rostral brainstem and bilateral thalamic infarction carries high morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Attributing somnolence and confusion to a metabolic cause and missing basilar occlusion","Focusing on limb power and overlooking the diagnostic eye movement and visual signs","Delaying vascular imaging in a patient without obvious hemiparesis","Mistaking peduncular hallucinosis for a primary psychiatric disturbance"],"management":["Emergency reperfusion with intravenous thrombolysis and/or endovascular mechanical thrombectomy for the occluded basilar apex, treated as time-critical","Admit to a stroke unit with airway protection and ventilatory support given the risk of depressed consciousness from bilateral thalamic involvement","Investigate and treat the embolic source, with anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation or antiplatelet therapy for atherosclerotic disease as secondary prevention","Optimise vascular risk factors including blood pressure, statin therapy for lipids, and glycaemic control","Monitor for and manage complications such as aspiration, raised intracranial pressure, and haemorrhagic transformation","Early multidisciplinary neurorehabilitation addressing visual, oculomotor, cognitive, and memory deficits","Escalate to neurocritical care for deteriorating consciousness or brainstem compromise"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":["dejerine_roussy_syndrome","weber_syndrome","locked_in_syndrome"]},"tophaceous_gout":{"name":"Chronic tophaceous gout","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tophaceous gout","chronic gout","gouty tophi","chronic gouty arthropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Chronic tophaceous gout is the advanced stage in which longstanding, poorly controlled hyperuricaemia leads to visible or palpable deposits of monosodium urate (tophi) in and around joints and soft tissues","Tophi commonly form over the first metatarsophalangeal joint, the fingers, the olecranon bursa, the Achilles tendon, and the helix of the ear, and may ulcerate to discharge chalky white material","The condition reflects a large total-body urate burden and usually indicates years of untreated or undertreated gout","Tophi cause joint-destroying erosions, deformity, chronic pain, and reduced function, and can restrict movement or entrap nerves","Radiographs show characteristic well-defined 'punched-out' erosions with sclerotic margins and overhanging edges, with joint space relatively preserved until late","Aspiration or biopsy of a tophus reveals abundant needle-shaped, negatively birefringent monosodium urate crystals","The cornerstone of management is long-term urate-lowering therapy to a low target (treat-to-target), which over time dissolves tophi and prevents further damage; it is not started or stopped during an acute flare and is continued through flares"],"pathophysiology":"Prolonged hyperuricaemia produces a large body burden of monosodium urate that precipitates as aggregated crystal masses (tophi) surrounded by a chronic granulomatous, foreign-body inflammatory reaction. Tophi within and around joints erode bone and cartilage, generating the characteristic punched-out erosions and progressive structural damage, while in soft tissues they form firm nodules that may ulcerate and discharge. Sustained lowering of the serum urate below the saturation point reverses the process by allowing the crystals to dissolve gradually.","additionalDifferentials":["Rheumatoid arthritis with nodules","Calcium pyrophosphate crystal arthropathy","Osteoarthritis with Heberden and Bouchard nodes","Chronic infection or osteomyelitis","Xanthomas or other soft-tissue nodules","Basic calcium phosphate periarticular deposition"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Aspiration or biopsy of a tophus or joint with polarised microscopy for negatively birefringent urate crystals","Serum urate to guide and monitor treat-to-target therapy","Radiographs for punched-out erosions with overhanging edges","Dual-energy CT to quantify and map urate deposits","Ultrasound for tophi and the double-contour sign","Renal function and assessment of cardiovascular and metabolic comorbidity","Swab or culture if an ulcerated tophus raises suspicion of secondary infection"],"redFlags":["An ulcerated or discharging tophus with possible secondary infection","Nerve compression caused by a tophaceous mass","Coexistent septic arthritis in a chronically damaged joint","Significant renal impairment influencing the choice of therapy"],"prognosis":"With sustained urate-lowering therapy to target, tophi gradually resolve, attacks stop, and further joint damage is prevented; established erosions and deformity may nonetheless be permanent, and poor adherence leads to ongoing destruction and disability.","pitfalls":["Failing to commit to long-term treat-to-target urate-lowering therapy","Stopping urate-lowering therapy once tophi shrink, allowing recurrence","Starting or stopping urate-lowering therapy during an acute flare","Missing secondary infection of an ulcerated tophus","Attributing the deformity to another arthropathy without confirming urate crystals"],"management":["Long-term urate-lowering therapy with a xanthine oxidase inhibitor such as allopurinol or febuxostat, titrated treat-to-target to dissolve tophi","Add a uricosuric such as probenecid, or consider pegloticase, for refractory disease or intolerance of xanthine oxidase inhibitors","Anti-inflammatory flare prophylaxis with colchicine or an NSAID when starting urate-lowering therapy to prevent mobilisation flares","Treat acute flares with an NSAID, colchicine, or corticosteroids","Lifestyle measures including reduced alcohol and purine-rich food intake, weight loss, and review of urate-raising drugs such as diuretics","Address concurrent cardiovascular, renal, and metabolic comorbidity","Refer for surgical debulking of tophi that ulcerate, become infected, entrap nerves, or impair joint function","Monitor serum urate to confirm sustained suppression below the crystal-saturation point"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Oxford Textbook of Rheumatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"torsion_appendix_testis":{"name":"Torsion of the appendix testis","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["torsion of the testicular appendage","torted appendix testis","appendix testis torsion","blue-dot sign"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Torsion of the appendix testis is a common cause of acute scrotal pain in prepubertal boys and is a self-limiting, benign condition, in contrast to testicular torsion","The classic sign is a small tender nodule at the upper pole of the testis with a bluish discolouration visible through the scrotal skin, the blue-dot sign","Pain tends to be more gradual and less severe than in testicular torsion, and the testis itself usually lies in a normal vertical position with a preserved cremasteric reflex","The key clinical priority is to exclude testicular torsion, which is a surgical emergency, and diagnostic uncertainty should prompt scrotal exploration","Colour Doppler scrotal ultrasound typically shows normal or increased testicular blood flow with a non-vascular tender appendage, supporting the diagnosis","Management is usually conservative with rest, scrotal support, and simple analgesia, as symptoms settle over days as the appendage infarcts and involutes","Surgical excision of the twisted appendage is reserved for severe or persistent pain or when torsion cannot be excluded"],"pathophysiology":"The appendix testis is a small pedunculated Mullerian duct remnant at the upper pole of the testis. Its narrow stalk predisposes it to twisting, which cuts off its blood supply and causes ischaemia and infarction of the appendage. The resulting inflammation produces localised pain and tenderness, and the infarcted appendage may be visible as a bluish nodule beneath the scrotal skin. Because the testis itself is not compromised, the process is self-limiting and resolves as the appendage necroses and is reabsorbed.","additionalDifferentials":["Testicular torsion (must be excluded urgently)","Epididymo-orchitis","Torsion of the appendix epididymis","Incarcerated inguinal hernia","Idiopathic scrotal oedema","Henoch-Schonlein purpura affecting the scrotum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful clinical examination for the blue-dot sign, testicular lie, and cremasteric reflex","Colour Doppler scrotal ultrasound to confirm preserved testicular perfusion","Urinalysis to help exclude infection","Assessment of the contralateral testis","Scrotal exploration when testicular torsion cannot be reliably excluded"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe pain, high-riding or transversely lying testis, or absent cremasteric reflex suggesting testicular torsion","Reduced or absent testicular blood flow on Doppler","Diagnostic uncertainty in a boy with an acute scrotum","Systemic features raising concern for another surgical or infective cause"],"prognosis":"Torsion of the appendix testis is benign and self-limiting; symptoms typically resolve within one to two weeks with conservative care and there is no threat to testicular viability or future fertility.","pitfalls":["Attributing an acute scrotum to appendage torsion and missing testicular torsion","Relying on ultrasound to exclude torsion when clinical suspicion is high, rather than exploring","Over-diagnosing epididymo-orchitis and giving unnecessary antibiotics","Failing to reassess a boy whose pain worsens or fails to settle"],"management":["Exclude testicular torsion urgently, proceeding to scrotal exploration whenever it cannot be reliably ruled out","Confirm preserved testicular perfusion with colour Doppler ultrasound when the diagnosis is suggested clinically by a blue-dot sign","Conservative management with rest, scrotal support, and simple analgesia such as an NSAID once torsion is excluded","Reassure that the condition is benign and self-limiting as the appendage infarcts and involutes over days to weeks","Surgical excision of the twisted appendage for severe or persistent pain not settling with conservative care","Clinical follow-up to confirm symptom resolution and reassess if pain worsens"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology, Pediatric Urology section"],"crossLinks":[]},"torus_palatinus":{"name":"Torus palatinus","system":"Oral & Maxillofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["palatal torus","palatine torus","oral exostosis of the palate"],"chapter":null,"source":"Burket's Oral Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common, benign bony outgrowth (exostosis) arising in the midline of the hard palate","Presents as a slow-growing, bony-hard, sessile or lobulated swelling covered by normal mucosa","More frequent in females and shows variation in prevalence between ethnic groups","Usually asymptomatic and often discovered incidentally","The thin overlying mucosa is prone to traumatic ulceration that can be slow to heal","Its main clinical importance lies in interference with the fitting and construction of dentures","The diagnosis is clinical; a hard midline palatal swelling with normal mucosa needs no biopsy","No treatment is required unless prosthetic needs, recurrent trauma, or patient concern warrant surgical removal"],"pathophysiology":"A torus palatinus is a developmental, benign exostosis composed of dense cortical bone with a variable amount of trabecular bone, arising along the midline palatal suture. Its growth is slow and it typically becomes apparent from adolescence or early adulthood; both genetic predisposition and functional or masticatory factors have been proposed as contributors. The lesion is not neoplastic and has no malignant potential, and the thin mucosa stretched over the bony prominence is easily traumatised, which accounts for the occasional ulceration seen clinically.","additionalDifferentials":["Torus mandibularis and other oral exostoses","Palatal abscess or dental infection","Salivary gland tumour of the palate such as pleomorphic adenoma","Osteoma or osteoid osteoma","Nasopalatine duct cyst","Squamous cell carcinoma or other palatal neoplasm if the mucosa is abnormal"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination confirming a bony-hard midline swelling with normal overlying mucosa","Palpation to assess consistency, extent, and mucosal integrity","Radiographs or cross-sectional imaging only if the diagnosis is uncertain or for surgical planning","Study models for denture planning when a prosthesis is required","Biopsy reserved for lesions with atypical, non-bony, ulcerated, or soft features","Review of the mucosa for traumatic ulceration"],"redFlags":["Ulceration that fails to heal or shows induration, raising suspicion of neoplasia","Rapid growth, which is atypical for a torus","A soft or fluctuant rather than bony-hard swelling suggesting another lesion","Signs of infection or abscess formation"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent as the torus is entirely benign; although it continues to grow slowly it causes no harm, and surgical removal, when indicated, is generally curative with uncommon recurrence.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a torus for a neoplasm or abscess and over-investigating","Missing a genuine palatal tumour by assuming any midline swelling is a torus","Traumatising the thin overlying mucosa during denture fitting","Recommending surgery when observation would suffice"],"management":["Reassure that the lesion is a benign bony exostosis with no malignant potential and needs no treatment when asymptomatic","Observation with periodic review, as intervention is unnecessary for the incidental, asymptomatic torus","Manage traumatic ulceration of the thin overlying mucosa with local protective measures until healing","Surgical reduction or excision reserved for tori that interfere with denture construction, cause recurrent ulceration, or impair speech and function","Take study models and involve prosthodontics when a denture is required over a prominent torus","Biopsy only if features become atypical, non-bony, or fail to heal, to exclude other pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Cawson's Essentials of Oral Pathology and Oral Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"total_anomalous_pulmonary_venous_return":{"name":"Total anomalous pulmonary venous return","system":"Cardiovascular / Congenital","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TAPVR","TAPVC","total anomalous pulmonary venous connection","anomalous pulmonary venous drainage"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["All pulmonary veins drain into the systemic venous circulation rather than the left atrium, so an atrial-level communication is obligatory for survival","Anatomical types are supracardiac, cardiac, infracardiac and mixed, classified by the site of anomalous drainage","Obstructed drainage, most typical of the infracardiac type, produces a critically cyanotic neonate with severe pulmonary oedema and respiratory distress","Unobstructed drainage presents more insidiously with mild cyanosis, tachypnoea, failure to thrive and heart failure from pulmonary overcirculation","Complete mixing means systemic saturation reflects the balance of pulmonary and systemic venous return across the atrial septum","The obstructed form is a true surgical emergency and is not relieved by prostaglandin, which is reserved for duct-dependent lesions","Repair anastomoses the pulmonary venous confluence to the left atrium, with pulmonary vein stenosis being the main late concern"],"pathophysiology":"In total anomalous pulmonary venous return the pulmonary veins fail to connect to the left atrium and instead drain into the systemic venous system, so oxygenated and deoxygenated blood mix within the right atrium. Survival depends on an obligatory right-to-left shunt through a patent foramen ovale or atrial septal defect to fill the left heart and systemic circulation. When the anomalous pathway is obstructed, as often occurs when an infracardiac vein passes through the diaphragm, pulmonary venous pressure rises steeply, causing pulmonary oedema, pulmonary hypertension and profound cyanosis. Unobstructed connections instead deliver a large volume of recirculated blood to the lungs, producing pulmonary overcirculation, right heart volume loading and congestive failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome or pneumonia","Other cyanotic congenital heart disease such as transposition of the great arteries","Cor triatriatum","Hypoplastic left heart syndrome","Partial anomalous pulmonary venous return with large shunt"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to identify the pulmonary venous confluence, drainage site and obstruction","CT or MR angiography to map the anomalous venous pathway when echo is inconclusive","Chest radiograph showing pulmonary oedema in obstructed disease or a snowman contour in the supracardiac type","Cardiac catheterisation in selected cases to define anatomy and pressures","Arterial blood gas and preductal and postductal saturations to assess cyanosis","Electrocardiogram for right ventricular hypertrophy"],"redFlags":["Severe cyanosis with pulmonary oedema in a neonate indicating obstructed drainage","Rapidly worsening respiratory distress unresponsive to ventilation","Signs of low cardiac output and metabolic acidosis","A restrictive atrial communication limiting left heart filling"],"prognosis":"With timely surgical repair the outlook is good, but obstructed neonatal presentations carry significant early risk, and postoperative pulmonary vein stenosis is a serious complication that can recur and worsen prognosis.","pitfalls":["Mistaking obstructed disease for primary lung disease or persistent pulmonary hypertension and delaying surgery","Expecting prostaglandin to help, when the lesion is not duct-dependent and prostaglandin may worsen pulmonary congestion","Overlooking a restrictive atrial septum that critically limits systemic output","Failing to monitor for recurrent pulmonary vein stenosis after repair"],"management":["Surgical anastomosis of the pulmonary venous confluence to the left atrium is the definitive treatment, performed emergently in the obstructed infracardiac form","Preoperative stabilisation with mechanical ventilation, inotropic support, and correction of metabolic acidosis, escalating to extracorporeal membrane oxygenation in the critically obstructed neonate","Avoid relying on prostaglandin, which does not relieve pulmonary venous obstruction, and treat pulmonary overcirculation in unobstructed disease with diuretics and anti-failure measures","Maintain an adequate atrial-level communication to allow left heart filling until surgical repair","Postoperative surveillance for recurrent or de novo pulmonary vein stenosis, arrhythmia, and pulmonary hypertension","Coordinate care through a paediatric congenital cardiac surgical, cardiology, and intensive care multidisciplinary team","Long-term echocardiographic follow-up to detect late pulmonary vein stenosis and residual lesions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","Anderson's Paediatric Cardiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"tourette_syndrome":{"name":"Tourette syndrome","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tourette's disorder","Gilles de la Tourette syndrome","TS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The diagnosis requires both multiple motor tics and one or more vocal (phonic) tics, present at some time during the illness, with onset before age 18 and persistence for more than a year","Tics are sudden, rapid, recurrent, nonrhythmic movements or vocalisations that wax and wane in severity and often shift in type over time","Many patients experience premonitory urges, an uncomfortable sensation relieved by performing the tic, and can suppress tics temporarily at the cost of rising inner tension","Coprolalia, the involuntary utterance of obscenities, is popularly associated with the condition but actually occurs in only a minority of patients","Comorbidity is the rule rather than the exception, particularly attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder and obsessive-compulsive disorder, which often cause more impairment than the tics","Behavioural treatment, especially comprehensive behavioural intervention for tics including habit reversal training, is first-line; medication is reserved for troublesome tics","Pharmacological options for severe tics include agents acting on dopaminergic transmission and certain alpha-adrenergic agents, chosen by symptom burden and comorbidity","Psychoeducation, school liaison, and treating comorbid conditions usually improve function more than suppressing the tics themselves"],"pathophysiology":"Tourette syndrome is a neurodevelopmental disorder involving dysfunction of cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical circuits, with abnormalities in dopaminergic neurotransmission within the basal ganglia thought to underlie the generation of tics. It is highly heritable with a complex polygenic basis, and environmental and possibly immune-mediated factors may modulate expression, while overlapping circuitry accounts for the frequent association with obsessive-compulsive and attentional symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Provisional (transient) tic disorder or persistent motor or vocal tic disorder","Stereotypic movement disorder","Myoclonus, dystonia, or chorea from a neurological cause","Obsessive-compulsive disorder with compulsions resembling tics","Medication-induced or substance-induced movement disorder","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed clinical history and observation of tics over time","Screening for comorbid ADHD, obsessive-compulsive disorder, anxiety, and mood disorders","Standardised tic severity rating scales to monitor course","Neurological examination to exclude other movement disorders","Further neurological workup only if atypical features suggest a secondary cause"],"redFlags":["Self-injurious tics causing physical harm","Abrupt, explosive onset or neurological signs suggesting a secondary or acquired cause","Severe comorbid depression with suicidal ideation","Marked functional deterioration from comorbid ADHD or obsessive-compulsive symptoms"],"prognosis":"Tics typically emerge in childhood, peak in severity in early adolescence, and then decline in many patients, with a substantial proportion experiencing marked improvement or near-resolution by adulthood. Prognosis for overall function depends heavily on comorbid conditions, which often persist and drive impairment, so their recognition and treatment strongly influence outcome.","pitfalls":["Focusing on tic suppression while neglecting more impairing comorbid ADHD or obsessive-compulsive disorder","Assuming coprolalia is required for diagnosis when it is uncommon","Overmedicating mild tics that do not warrant pharmacotherapy","Misinterpreting suppressible tics as voluntary or attention-seeking","Missing an atypical presentation that points to a secondary movement disorder"],"management":["Psychoeducation and reassurance about the natural, waxing-and-waning course, with school and family support","Comprehensive behavioural intervention for tics, including habit reversal training, as first-line treatment","Identify and treat comorbid attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder and obsessive-compulsive disorder, which often cause greater impairment than the tics","Reserve medication for troublesome tics, using an alpha-2 agonist such as clonidine or guanfacine, or a dopamine-blocking agent such as aripiprazole or risperidone","Botulinum toxin injection for disabling focal motor or phonic tics","Consider deep brain stimulation for severe, treatment-refractory tics in adults","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across psychiatry, neurology, and psychology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"townes_brocks_syndrome":{"name":"Townes-Brocks syndrome (SALL1)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBS","SALL1-related disorder","anus-hand-ear syndrome","renal-ear-anal-radial syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant disorder caused by heterozygous truncating variants in SALL1","The classic triad comprises anal, ear, and thumb anomalies, most memorably imperforate anus","Ear findings include dysplastic or lop ears, preauricular tags or pits, and sensorineural or conductive hearing loss","Thumb anomalies feature a triphalangeal or duplicated thumb, distinguishing it from disorders with thumb hypoplasia","Renal anomalies, which can progress to renal impairment, are an important and potentially serious component","Congenital heart defects and, less commonly, foot anomalies and eye findings may occur","There is phenotypic overlap with VACTERL association and with SALL4-related Duane-radial ray syndrome","Diagnosis is confirmed by identifying a SALL1 variant"],"pathophysiology":"Townes-Brocks syndrome is caused by heterozygous, usually truncating, variants in SALL1, a zinc-finger transcription factor of the spalt-like family that regulates gene expression during development of the anus, ears, limbs, kidneys, and heart. The truncated protein is thought to act in a dominant-negative fashion in addition to reducing normal gene dosage, disturbing the developmental programmes SALL1 controls. This disruption produces the anorectal malformation, characteristic ear anomalies with hearing loss, preaxial limb anomalies such as triphalangeal thumbs, and the renal and cardiac defects that define the condition. Inheritance is autosomal dominant, and expression is variable, so affected relatives may show only subtle features.","additionalDifferentials":["VACTERL association with overlapping anorectal, cardiac, renal, and limb anomalies","Duane-radial ray (Okihiro) syndrome from SALL4 variants","Baller-Gerold and other syndromes with preaxial limb anomalies","Branchio-oto-renal syndrome with ear and renal involvement","Fanconi anaemia with limb and renal anomalies","Isolated anorectal malformation of non-syndromic cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of SALL1 to confirm the diagnosis","Renal ultrasound and assessment of renal function","Audiological evaluation and, where indicated, temporal bone imaging","Limb radiography to characterise thumb and other skeletal anomalies","Echocardiography for congenital heart disease","Assessment and surgical planning for anorectal malformation"],"redFlags":["Renal impairment progressing towards kidney failure","Haemodynamically significant congenital heart disease","Anorectal malformation requiring urgent neonatal surgical management","Progressive hearing loss impairing language development"],"prognosis":"Lifespan is usually normal, but long-term outcome is influenced most by renal involvement, which can progress to kidney failure and warrants lifelong monitoring; the anorectal, ear, and limb anomalies are generally amenable to surgical and rehabilitative treatment, and intellect is typically normal in most individuals.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing VACTERL association and missing the specific SALL1 disorder with its renal risk","Failing to monitor renal function over time, allowing progressive impairment to go undetected","Confusing it with SALL4-related disease, which more often shows thumb hypoplasia rather than triphalangeal thumb","Overlooking subtle features in a parent, leading to an incorrect assumption of a de novo event"],"management":["Surgical correction of the anorectal malformation, including repair of imperforate anus, in the neonatal period","Lifelong monitoring of renal function with management of chronic kidney disease and renal replacement therapy or transplantation if it progresses","Audiological assessment with hearing aids and speech support for sensorineural or conductive hearing loss","Cardiology evaluation and surgical repair of congenital heart defects as indicated","Orthopaedic and hand-surgery management of triphalangeal or duplicated thumbs and other limb anomalies","Genetic counselling with SALL1 testing and evaluation of parents given autosomal dominant inheritance and variable expression","Coordinate lifelong care through a multidisciplinary team with clinical genetics oversight"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Townes-Brocks syndrome","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"toxic_adenoma_thyroid":{"name":"Toxic thyroid adenoma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["autonomously functioning thyroid nodule","hot nodule","solitary toxic adenoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / WHO Classification of Endocrine Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A single autonomously functioning benign follicular adenoma that produces thyroid hormone independent of TSH, causing hyperthyroidism without the eye or skin signs of Graves disease","Biochemistry shows a suppressed TSH with raised free thyroid hormones, and there are no TSH-receptor antibodies since the process is autonomous rather than autoimmune","A radioiodine (or technetium) uptake scan demonstrates a focal 'hot' nodule with suppression of uptake in the surrounding normal gland","Most autonomous nodules harbour activating somatic mutations of the TSH receptor or the Gs-alpha subunit, driving constitutive stimulation of the cyclic-AMP pathway","A hot nodule on isotope scanning is almost always benign, so fine-needle aspiration is generally not required for a scintigraphically hyperfunctioning nodule","Definitive options include radioactive iodine, which is concentrated by the autonomous nodule, or surgical removal, both preceded by rendering the patient euthyroid","Excess iodine exposure, including iodinated contrast or amiodarone, can precipitate overt thyrotoxicosis in a previously subclinical autonomous nodule"],"pathophysiology":"A toxic adenoma is a benign monoclonal follicular tumour whose cells acquire somatic gain-of-function mutations, most often in the TSH-receptor gene or the stimulatory Gs-alpha subunit. These mutations lock the cyclic-AMP signalling pathway in an active state, so the nodule proliferates and secretes thyroid hormone independently of pituitary TSH. The resulting rise in circulating thyroid hormone suppresses TSH, which in turn switches off the surrounding normal thyroid, explaining the focal uptake on isotope imaging. Because the nodule handles iodine avidly, a sudden iodine load can markedly increase hormone output and precipitate clinical thyrotoxicosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Graves disease","Toxic multinodular goitre","Thyroiditis with a hyperthyroid phase","Non-functioning (cold) thyroid nodule","Exogenous or iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis","TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum TSH with free thyroxine and free triiodothyronine to confirm and characterise thyrotoxicosis","Radioiodine or technetium uptake scan to demonstrate a focal hot nodule with suppressed background","TSH-receptor antibodies to distinguish autonomy from Graves disease when uncertain","Neck ultrasound to define nodule size and structure","Assessment of recent iodine exposure including contrast and amiodarone","Fine-needle aspiration only if a coexisting non-functioning suspicious nodule is present"],"redFlags":["Features of thyroid storm such as fever, tachyarrhythmia, and confusion","Atrial fibrillation or heart failure precipitated by thyrotoxicosis, especially in the elderly","Recent iodinated contrast or amiodarone triggering severe thyrotoxicosis","Significant compressive symptoms from a large nodule"],"prognosis":"The nodule itself is benign and hyperthyroidism is reliably controlled by radioactive iodine or surgery; untreated thyrotoxicosis, however, carries cardiovascular and bone risks, particularly in older patients.","pitfalls":["Performing fine-needle aspiration of a scintigraphically hot nodule, which is almost always benign, and misinterpreting reactive cytology","Giving iodinated contrast or amiodarone to a patient with an autonomous nodule and precipitating thyrotoxicosis","Confusing autonomous nodular disease with Graves disease and mismanaging with antibody-directed reasoning","Treating with radioactive iodine without first achieving biochemical control in a symptomatic patient"],"management":["Definitive treatment with radioactive iodine, concentrated by the autonomous nodule, or surgical lobectomy","Render the patient euthyroid before definitive therapy using an antithyroid drug such as carbimazole or propylthiouracil","Beta-blockade with propranolol for adrenergic symptoms and to control tachycardia","Avoid iodine-rich contrast and amiodarone, which can precipitate thyrotoxicosis in autonomous tissue","Consider percutaneous ethanol or thermal ablation as an alternative for a solitary nodule in selected patients","Monitor thyroid function after treatment for resolution of thyrotoxicosis and for later hypothyroidism requiring levothyroxine replacement","Refer to endocrinology or endocrine surgery for definitive management planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","WHO Classification of Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"toxic_and_drug_induced_hepatitis":{"name":"Toxic and Drug-Induced Hepatitis","system":"Hepatobiliary / Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Drug-induced liver injury","DILI","Hepatotoxicity","Toxic hepatitis"],"chapter":351,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2665-2672","clinicalPearls":["Drug-induced liver injury (DILI) is the single most common cause of acute liver failure, and hepatotoxicity signals are the leading reason candidate compounds fail to gain approval during drug development; a meticulous history covering prescription, over-the-counter, herbal, dietary, occupational and household chemical exposures is essential (Harrison 22e p.2665).","Two broad categories are recognized: direct (intrinsic) toxic injury, which is predictable, dose-dependent and has a short latency (often hours), and idiosyncratic injury, which is infrequent (roughly 1 in 1,000 to 1 in 100,000 exposures), less clearly dose-related and typically appears 5-90 days after starting the drug (Harrison 22e p.2665-2666).","The R value, the ratio of ALT to alkaline phosphatase (each as a multiple of the upper limit of normal), classifies the injury pattern: R greater than 5 indicates hepatocellular injury, R less than 2 indicates cholestatic injury, and R of 2-5 indicates a mixed pattern (Harrison 22e p.2667).","Hy's Law, named for Hyman Zimmerman, holds that drug-induced jaundice during a trial predicts serious liver injury, with roughly a 10-to-1 ratio of jaundiced cases to cases of acute liver failure; such cases often portend failure of drug approval (Harrison 22e p.2667).","Acetaminophen is the prototypic direct hepatotoxin and the most prevalent cause of acute liver failure in the Western world; it produces dose-related centrilobular necrosis, and aminotransferases can exceed 10,000 IU/L, far above levels seen in viral hepatitis, often with a relatively low bilirubin in this hyperacute injury (Harrison 22e p.2668).","A characteristic biochemical clue to acetaminophen toxicity is the combination of extremely high aminotransferases with low bilirubin; clinical suspicion should prompt empiric N-acetylcysteine even before history is confirmed, since denial or altered mentation can obscure the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2668).","Isoniazid causes transient mild aminotransferase elevation in about 10% of recipients early in therapy (an 'adaptive' response that usually resolves despite continued use); clinically significant hepatocellular injury can appear over a latency of up to 6 months and rises sharply with age, being highest in those over 50 and lowest in those under 20 (Harrison 22e p.2670).","Amoxicillin-clavulanate is the most commonly implicated single agent in DILI in the United States and Europe, producing a fairly specific mixed or predominantly cholestatic injury that may begin after the drug has already been stopped owing to a long latency (Harrison 22e p.2667, 2670-2671).","Complementary and alternative medicines, including herbal and dietary weight-loss and body-building products, now account for more than 20% of DILI cases in the United States; implicated agents include green tea extract (Camellia sinensis), kava, germander, chaparral, comfrey (pyrrolizidine alkaloids), Hydroxycut and OxyElite Pro (Harrison 22e p.2671).","Certain drugs produce an autoimmune-hepatitis-like phenotype with positive antinuclear antibodies, classically nitrofurantoin, minocycline, hydralazine and alpha-methyldopa (Harrison 22e p.2667, 2670-2671).","Immune checkpoint inhibitors (anti-CTLA-4 ipilimumab; anti-PD-1 pembrolizumab and nivolumab) cause an immune-mediated hepatitis within weeks, often ANA-positive, mediated predominantly by CD8+ lymphocytes and responsive to glucocorticoids; concurrent immune injury to thyroid, muscle and colon may occur (Harrison 22e p.2671-2672).","Withdrawal of the suspected agent at the first sign of an adverse reaction, or when aminotransferases reach five times the upper limit of normal, is critical, because continued use despite symptoms and signs is associated with lethal outcomes (Harrison 22e p.2668)."],"pathophysiology":"Hepatotoxicity arises from direct hepatocyte injury (free radicals or reactive metabolites causing membrane lipid peroxidation) or from drug/metabolite activation of innate or adaptive immune pathways, apoptotic signaling, and disruption of bile excretory transport. Interference with canalicular pumps allows injurious endogenous bile acids to accumulate, producing cholestasis; other mechanisms inhibit protein synthesis or impair mitochondrial fatty-acid oxidation, leading to lactic acidosis and microvesicular steatosis. Most drugs are metabolized via phase I cytochrome P450 (oxidation) followed by phase II conjugation (glucuronidation, sulfation) or glutathione inactivation; most toxicity reflects formation of a reactive phase I metabolite, with glutathione depletion removing a protective pathway. For acetaminophen, phase II conjugation yields harmless metabolites, but a fraction is converted by CYP2E1 to the toxic intermediate NAPQI, normally detoxified by glutathione; when glutathione is overwhelmed or depleted, NAPQI binds covalently to hepatocyte macromolecules forming protein adducts that drive necrosis. Susceptibility to idiosyncratic injury is influenced by HLA haplotypes (e.g., amoxicillin-clavulanate, flucloxacillin), polymorphisms in metabolizing pathways and protective cytokines, and activation of nuclear receptors such as CAR and PXR. Factors potentiating acetaminophen injury include enzyme induction (alcohol via CYP2E1, phenobarbital, isoniazid), fasting/starvation and other states that lower hepatic glutathione (Harrison 22e p.2665-2666, 2668-2670).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute viral hepatitis (A, B, C) - idiosyncratic hepatocellular DILI can be clinically and histologically indistinguishable","Autoimmune hepatitis (especially with nitrofurantoin, minocycline, hydralazine, methyldopa)","Alcoholic hepatitis (amiodarone produces a pseudoalcoholic picture)","Ischemic hepatitis / shock liver","Biliary obstruction and primary sclerosing cholangitis (DILI can mimic sclerosing cholangitis after floxuridine or long-term ketamine)","Cholestasis of pregnancy (shares genetic canalicular transporter defects with estrogen-induced cholestasis)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute viral hepatitis A, B, and C"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune hepatitis","Alcoholic / nonalcoholic steatohepatitis","Ischemic hepatopathy","Mechanical biliary obstruction"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin to define pattern and compute the R value (ALT/ALP as multiples of upper limit of normal)","Serum acetaminophen level timed 4-8 h after ingestion, interpreted against the treatment nomogram (levels above 300 mcg/mL at 4 h predict severe injury; below 150 mcg/mL injury unlikely)","Acetaminophen-protein (acetaminophen-Cys) adduct measurement by HPLC as an investigational diagnostic marker","Arterial blood lactate in acute liver failure (above 3.5 mmol/L suggests likely need for transplantation)","Autoantibodies (ANA, anti-smooth muscle, anti-LKM2, anti-mitochondrial) when an autoimmune-pattern or checkpoint-inhibitor injury is suspected","Liver biopsy when the pattern is uncertain (may show centrilobular necrosis, microvesicular steatosis, granulomas, phospholipid-laden lamellar bodies in amiodarone, or vanishing bile ducts)","Exclusion of viral, autoimmune and biliary causes; structured causality assessment (DILIN expert opinion, RUCAM, or newer RECAM)"],"redFlags":["Jaundice arising during drug exposure (Hy's Law) predicts serious injury and possible liver failure","Coagulopathy and hepatic encephalopathy - markers of acute liver failure, which develops in about 10% of DILI cases","Aminotransferases above 10,000 IU/L with a low bilirubin - hyperacute acetaminophen pattern","Continued ingestion despite nausea, fatigue or jaundice (common scenario in fatal isoniazid and acetaminophen toxicity)","Acetaminophen ingestion of 25 g or more, or high timed plasma levels on the nomogram","Arterial lactate above 3.5 mmol/L in acetaminophen acute liver failure","Concurrent renal failure or myocardial injury in acetaminophen overdose"],"prognosis":"Most DILI is self-limited and resolves after withdrawal of the offending agent. Acute liver failure develops in about 10% of DILI cases; once that threshold (coagulopathy plus encephalopathy) is reached, spontaneous recovery occurs in fewer than 30%, and over 40% of those patients undergo liver transplantation. Survivors of acetaminophen overdose rarely have lasting liver injury, though the associated acute kidney injury (seen in nearly 75% of severe cases) is almost always self-limited. Anabolic steroid cholestasis typically resolves over weeks to months with prolonged jaundice. Some agents cause chronic injury: methotrexate, tamoxifen and amiodarone can lead to cirrhosis, and several drugs cause chronic autoimmune-like hepatitis (Harrison 22e p.2668-2670).","pitfalls":["DILI is nearly always a diagnosis of exclusion; causality is hard to prove and many disorders produce an identical clinicopathologic picture","Mild transient aminotransferase elevations that resolve during continued therapy ('adaptation,' seen with isoniazid, valproate, phenytoin and statins) can be mistaken for significant toxicity","Long latency drugs (notably amoxicillin-clavulanate and nitrofurantoin) may cause injury that manifests only after the drug has been stopped","Idiosyncratic reactions occurring in under 1 in 10,000 recipients are missed by clinical trials and only detected through postmarketing surveillance (e.g., troglitazone)","In chronic alcohol users, glutathione depletion and CYP2E1 induction can make the toxic acetaminophen dose as low as 2 g","Activated charcoal or cholestyramine are ineffective if given more than 30 minutes after acetaminophen ingestion","Routine liver test monitoring during statin therapy is not recommended, and asymptomatic isolated aminotransferase elevations need not prompt discontinuation","Herbal/dietary supplements may contain undeclared pharmaceuticals (e.g., diclofenac in antirheumatic 'herbs'), so the history must specifically probe alternative-medicine use"],"management":["Immediate withdrawal of the suspected offending drug or toxin as the essential first step","N-acetylcysteine for paracetamol toxicity, given empirically when suspected without awaiting confirmation","Supportive care with correction of fluid, electrolyte, and metabolic derangement, and cholestyramine for cholestatic pruritus","Corticosteroids for hypersensitivity or autoimmune-pattern injury, including checkpoint-inhibitor hepatitis","Serial monitoring of transaminases, bilirubin, and coagulation, watching for the coagulopathy and encephalopathy that herald acute liver failure","Early referral to a specialist liver and transplant centre for acute liver failure, with consideration of emergency transplantation","Document the reaction and counsel the patient to avoid re-exposure to the causative agent and related compounds"],"severityClassification":"Injury pattern is graded by the R value (ALT/ALP as multiples of ULN): hepatocellular (R > 5), cholestatic (R < 2), or mixed (R 2-5). Severity is judged clinically by progression to acute liver failure (coagulopathy plus encephalopathy). Causality likelihood is assessed by structured tools including the DILIN expert-opinion scale (definite, highly likely, probable, possible, unlikely), RUCAM, and the newer RECAM. Hy's Law identifies high-risk hepatocellular injury when jaundice accompanies hepatocellular enzyme elevation (Harrison 22e p.2667-2668).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2665","Harrison 22e p.2666","Harrison 22e p.2667","Harrison 22e p.2668","Harrison 22e p.2669","Harrison 22e p.2670","Harrison 22e p.2671","Harrison 22e p.2672"],"crossLinks":[]},"toxic_multinodular_goiter":{"name":"Toxic multinodular goitre","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["toxic multinodular goiter","Plummer disease","toxic nodular goitre"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / WHO Classification of Endocrine Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common cause of hyperthyroidism in older adults, arising in a long-standing multinodular goitre when one or more nodules become autonomously hyperfunctioning","Thyrotoxicosis is often insidious and may be 'apathetic', dominated by atrial fibrillation, weight loss, or heart failure rather than classic adrenergic features","Biochemistry shows suppressed TSH with elevated thyroid hormones, and TSH-receptor antibodies are absent because the disorder is autonomous, not autoimmune","Isotope uptake scanning demonstrates patchy uptake with multiple hot areas and suppressed internodular tissue, distinguishing it from the diffuse uptake of Graves disease","Autonomy commonly reflects accumulated activating TSH-receptor mutations within nodules developing over years of goitrous change","A large retrosternal goitre can produce compressive symptoms and positional airway narrowing, which surgery addresses better than radioactive iodine","Iodine loads from contrast media or amiodarone frequently precipitate overt thyrotoxicosis in these autonomous glands"],"pathophysiology":"Toxic multinodular goitre develops when a long-standing multinodular goitre acquires foci of autonomous function. Over time, subpopulations of follicular cells accumulate somatic activating mutations, particularly of the TSH receptor, so that individual nodules secrete thyroid hormone independently of TSH. As autonomous mass increases, circulating thyroid hormone rises and suppresses pituitary TSH, silencing the internodular parenchyma and producing the characteristic patchy scintigraphic pattern. Because the autonomous tissue avidly traps iodine, exposure to iodinated contrast or iodine-rich drugs such as amiodarone can abruptly increase hormone synthesis and precipitate thyrotoxicosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Graves disease","Solitary toxic adenoma","Thyroiditis with transient hyperthyroidism","Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis","Non-toxic multinodular goitre","Exogenous thyrotoxicosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum TSH with free thyroxine and free triiodothyronine to confirm thyrotoxicosis","Radioiodine or technetium uptake scan showing patchy multifocal uptake","Neck ultrasound to characterise the nodular gland and identify any dominant or suspicious nodule","Cross-sectional imaging when retrosternal extension or compression is suspected","TSH-receptor antibodies to exclude coexisting Graves disease where uncertain","Review of iodine exposure including contrast and amiodarone"],"redFlags":["New atrial fibrillation, heart failure, or thromboembolism from occult thyrotoxicosis in an older patient","Thyroid storm features such as fever, delirium, and cardiovascular collapse","Compressive airway symptoms or stridor from a large or retrosternal goitre","Severe thyrotoxicosis precipitated by iodinated contrast or amiodarone"],"prognosis":"With definitive treatment by radioactive iodine or surgery the outlook is good; untreated disease, especially in the elderly, carries substantial cardiovascular and bone morbidity, and large goitres may cause progressive compression.","pitfalls":["Overlooking apathetic thyrotoxicosis presenting only as atrial fibrillation or weight loss in an older patient","Administering iodinated contrast or amiodarone without recognising the risk of precipitating thyrotoxicosis","Choosing radioactive iodine for a large compressive or retrosternal goitre better managed surgically","Assuming diffuse Graves-type uptake and missing the patchy autonomous pattern on scanning"],"management":["Definitive treatment with radioactive iodine, or total or near-total thyroidectomy which is preferred for large, retrosternal, or compressive goitres","Render the patient euthyroid before definitive therapy with an antithyroid drug such as carbimazole","Beta-blockade to control adrenergic symptoms and to rate-control associated atrial fibrillation","Assess for anticoagulation in thyrotoxic atrial fibrillation to reduce thromboembolic risk","Avoid iodinated contrast and amiodarone, which can precipitate thyrotoxicosis","Cross-sectional imaging and airway assessment where retrosternal extension causes compression","Monitor thyroid function for resolution and for post-treatment hypothyroidism needing levothyroxine, and refer to endocrinology and endocrine surgery for definitive management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","WHO Classification of Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"toxic_optic_neuropathy":{"name":"toxic optic neuropathy","system":"Ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["toxic optic neuritis","drug-induced optic neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically bilateral, symmetric, painless and progressive visual loss with a centrocaecal scotoma and dyschromatopsia, closely overlapping the nutritional form.","Frequent offenders include ethambutol, methanol, amiodarone, prolonged linezolid, isoniazid, disulfiram, tobacco and heavy metals such as lead and thallium.","Ethambutol toxicity is dose- and duration-dependent, is often reversible if detected early, and mandates baseline and periodic monitoring of acuity and colour vision.","Methanol poisoning causes acute severe loss with disc oedema and a high anion-gap metabolic acidosis and may be permanent or fatal.","The papillomacular bundle is preferentially affected, producing temporal disc pallor as atrophy develops.","A meticulous drug, occupational and social history is essential, and removal of the offending agent is the mainstay of management.","Pupillary responses are relatively spared and a relative afferent pupillary defect is minimal when the process is symmetric.","Coexisting nutritional deficiency frequently aggravates toxic injury and should be sought and corrected."],"pathophysiology":"Most toxins injure the optic nerve by impairing mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation and generating oxidative stress within the metabolically demanding papillomacular bundle. Ethambutol chelates copper and iron and interferes with metal-dependent respiratory chain enzymes, while the methanol metabolite formic acid directly inhibits cytochrome c oxidase. The energy failure preferentially damages small central fibres, accounting for the characteristic central and centrocaecal field defects and eventual temporal atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Nutritional optic neuropathy","Leber hereditary optic neuropathy","Compressive or chiasmal lesion","Demyelinating optic neuritis","Maculopathy","Functional (non-organic) visual loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed medication, occupational and toxin exposure history","Colour vision testing and automated perimetry","Optical coherence tomography of the retinal nerve fibre layer","Magnetic resonance imaging to exclude a compressive lesion","Serum or urine toxicology and heavy-metal levels where indicated","Arterial blood gas and osmolar gap in suspected methanol poisoning","Vitamin B12 and folate to exclude a coexisting nutritional cause"],"redFlags":["Acute severe visual loss with metabolic acidosis indicating a methanol emergency","Rapidly progressive loss or systemic signs of toxicity","No recovery despite withdrawal of the suspected agent","Asymmetry or focal neurological signs suggesting an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Many cases, particularly ethambutol-related, improve after early withdrawal of the offending agent, whereas methanol poisoning frequently causes permanent, severe loss. Chronic unrecognised exposure results in irreversible optic atrophy.","pitfalls":["Continuing the offending drug because of inadequate monitoring, as with ethambutol","Failing to recognise methanol toxicity as a treatable emergency","Ignoring a concurrent nutritional deficiency that worsens the injury","Omitting baseline visual assessment before commencing a known optic-toxic agent"],"management":["Prompt identification and withdrawal of the offending agent such as ethambutol, linezolid, or amiodarone is the mainstay","In methanol poisoning, urgent inhibition of alcohol dehydrogenase with fomepizole or ethanol, correction of acidosis with sodium bicarbonate, folinic acid, and haemodialysis to clear formate","Baseline and periodic monitoring of visual acuity and colour vision in patients on potentially toxic drugs such as ethambutol","Nutritional support with B-complex vitamins and folate where a coexisting nutritional deficiency contributes","Counsel on avoidance of contributory toxins including tobacco and alcohol","Refer to neuro-ophthalmology with low-vision rehabilitation for established visual loss"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course, Neuro-Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"toxic_shock_streptococcal":{"name":"Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STSS","streptococcal TSS","group A streptococcal toxic shock","invasive GAS toxic shock"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Driven by invasive group A Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes) whose exotoxins act as superantigens, provoking massive cytokine release and rapid-onset shock","Frequently arises from a soft-tissue focus such as necrotising fasciitis or cellulitis, though a portal of entry is not always evident","Pain out of proportion to visible skin findings is an early warning sign and should prompt urgent surgical assessment","Hypotension progresses quickly to multi-organ dysfunction, with renal impairment often preceding the drop in blood pressure","A generalised erythematous rash may be subtle or absent compared with staphylococcal toxic shock; desquamation can occur late","Management combines aggressive fluid resuscitation, prompt source control, and antibiotic principles pairing a beta-lactam with a protein-synthesis inhibitor such as clindamycin to switch off toxin production","Intravenous immunoglobulin is considered as an adjunct to neutralise circulating superantigens in severe disease","Mortality is high, so early recognition, critical-care support, and surgical debridement of any necrotising focus are decisive"],"pathophysiology":"Invasive strains of Streptococcus pyogenes release pyrogenic exotoxins and other superantigens that bind directly to MHC class II molecules and T-cell receptors outside the normal antigen groove. This triggers polyclonal T-cell activation and an overwhelming release of pro-inflammatory cytokines (a cytokine storm), producing capillary leak, vasodilatation, hypotension, and multi-organ failure. Bacteraemia and deep tissue invasion, often within necrotising soft-tissue infection, amplify the toxin load and accelerate the shock state.","additionalDifferentials":["Staphylococcal toxic shock syndrome","Septic shock from other Gram-positive or Gram-negative organisms","Meningococcaemia","Necrotising fasciitis without established shock","Severe drug reaction with systemic features"],"infectionMimics":["Anaphylaxis with distributive shock","Heat stroke with multi-organ dysfunction","Adrenal crisis","Severe acute pancreatitis with systemic inflammatory response"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures and cultures from any surgical or wound specimen","Serial lactate, renal and liver function, coagulation profile and creatine kinase to track organ involvement","Imaging (CT or MRI) of a suspected deep soft-tissue focus, without delaying surgery when necrotising infection is likely","Surgical exploration for tissue diagnosis when necrotising fasciitis is suspected","Assessment for disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"redFlags":["Pain disproportionate to examination findings suggesting underlying necrotising infection requiring urgent debridement","Rapidly progressing hypotension and organ failure indicating established septic shock","Skin crepitus, bullae, dusky discolouration or rapidly advancing erythema pointing to deep tissue necrosis","Falling urine output and rising creatinine signalling early multi-organ dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Even with intensive treatment, mortality remains substantial and is highest when there is an associated necrotising soft-tissue infection or delayed source control; survival depends on early recognition, rapid surgery, and organ support.","pitfalls":["Attributing severe limb pain to a minor injury and missing early necrotising infection","Relying on antibiotics alone without urgent surgical source control","Omitting a protein-synthesis inhibitor such as clindamycin, forgoing its anti-toxin effect","Underestimating fluid requirements and delaying critical-care escalation"],"management":["Prompt combination antibiotics pairing a beta-lactam such as benzylpenicillin with clindamycin to switch off exotoxin production","Urgent surgical source control with debridement of any necrotising soft-tissue focus","Aggressive intravenous fluid resuscitation with vasopressors and organ support in intensive care for refractory shock","Consider intravenous immunoglobulin to neutralise circulating superantigen toxins in severe disease","Monitor and manage multi-organ dysfunction including acute kidney injury and disseminated intravascular coagulation","Escalate early to critical care and surgery given rapid progression, with a low threshold for repeat exploration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"toxoplasmosis":{"name":"Toxoplasmosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Toxoplasma gondii infection"],"chapter":235,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1797","clinicalPearls":["Caused by the obligate intracellular parasite Toxoplasma gondii; up to a third of the world's population is latently infected (Harrison 22e p.1797).","Two transmissible stages reach humans: tissue cysts containing bradyzoites in undercooked meat (e.g., lamb, pork, venison, ground beef), and sporulated oocysts shed in cat feces that contaminate soil, food, or water; sporulated oocysts are environmentally hardy and highly infectious (Harrison 22e p.1797).","In the immunocompetent host, acute infection is usually asymptomatic and self-limited, going unrecognized in 80-90% of acquired infections (Harrison 22e p.1799).","The most common manifestation of symptomatic acute toxoplasmosis in immunocompetent patients is cervical lymphadenopathy; nodes are usually nontender and discrete, with associated headache, malaise, fatigue, and low-grade fever (temperature usually <40 degrees C) (Harrison 22e p.1799).","The classic severe presentation is reactivation encephalitis in immunocompromised hosts (especially HIV-positive individuals or transplant recipients) in whom latent infection has reactivated (Harrison 22e p.1797).","In AIDS, >95% of Toxoplasma encephalitis cases are due to recrudescent infection, typically developing when the CD4+ T-cell count falls below 100/microL; encephalitis is the predominant manifestation while meningeal involvement is uncommon (Harrison 22e p.1800).","On MRI/CT, Toxoplasma encephalitis classically produces multiple ring-enhancing lesions, most often in the basal ganglia and corticomedullary junction; a single lesion increases the likelihood of primary CNS lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.1801-1802).","T. gondii is the most common cause of posterior uveitis in immunocompetent individuals and is estimated to cause about 35% of all chorioretinitis cases in the US and Europe (Harrison 22e p.1799, p.1801).","Congenital toxoplasmosis results from transplacental passage during primary maternal infection; infants may be asymptomatic at birth but later manifest chorioretinitis, strabismus, epilepsy, hydrocephalus, microcephaly, intellectual disability, and psychomotor retardation (Harrison 22e p.1797, p.1799).","Antibody titers are NOT useful for establishing reactivation or following infection activity in immunocompromised patients; however, an absence of positive serology suggests an alternative diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1800).","Diagnosis rests on serology (combined IgG and IgM, with circulating IgA favoring acute infection) and PCR; PCR of body fluids is specific but its sensitivity may be low (Harrison 22e p.1801).","A therapeutic trial of anti-Toxoplasma medications is frequently used in AIDS patients with CNS mass lesions: >50% improve by day 3 and >90% by day 7, whereas worsening by day 7 (or lymphoma) prompts brain biopsy (Harrison 22e p.1802)."],"pathophysiology":"T. gondii is an intracellular coccidian. Ingested tissue cysts (bradyzoites) or oocysts (sporozoites) release organisms that invade gut epithelium and transform into rapidly dividing tachyzoites, which can infect and replicate in all nucleated mammalian cells via a parasitophorous vacuole, causing cell death and focal necrosis with an acute mononuclear inflammatory response. Parasites disseminate as free tachyzoites or within phagocytic 'Trojan horse' cells through blood and lymphatics to lymphatic tissue, skeletal muscle, myocardium, retina, placenta, and CNS. In the immunocompetent host, humoral and cell-mediated immunity (parasiticidal antibody, macrophage activation, IFN-gamma, IL-12-driven TH1 response, CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes) controls infection; tissue cysts containing bradyzoites then form 7-10 days after systemic infection and persist principally in the CNS and muscle, completing the asexual cycle and establishing lifelong chronic infection. Subclinical cycles of cyst rupture and reformation are the probable source of recrudescent infection. Immunocompromised or fetal hosts lack the immune factors (notably CD8+ T cells; reactivating T cells fail to secrete IFN-gamma and IL-2) needed to contain tachyzoite spread, allowing progressive focal destruction (necrotizing encephalitis, pneumonia, myocarditis). The sexual stage occurs only in the cat (definitive host), producing oocysts shed in feces (Harrison 22e p.1797-1800).","additionalDifferentials":["Mononucleosis-like syndrome from acute toxoplasmic lymphadenopathy: EBV infection, CMV infection, HIV infection, Bartonella (cat-scratch disease), and lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.1800-1801).","CNS mass lesions in an AIDS patient: primary CNS lymphoma, metastatic tumor, brain abscess, progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (JC virus), fungal infection, and mycobacterial infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Chorioretinitis in an immunocompetent individual: tuberculosis, syphilis, and histoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Chorioretinitis in an AIDS patient: CMV, syphilis, HSV, VZV, and fungal infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Cerebral toxoplasmosis must also be differentiated from herpes simplex encephalitis, cryptococcal meningitis, and HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder (HAND) (Harrison 22e p.1800).","Congenital infection: CMV, HSV, rubella, syphilis, and listeriosis (Harrison 22e p.1801)."],"infectionMimics":["Primary CNS lymphoma (EBV-associated) presenting as ring-enhancing brain lesion(s) in AIDS","Cytomegalovirus infection","Epstein-Barr virus infection (infectious mononucleosis)","Bartonella henselae (cat-scratch disease)","Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (JC virus)","Cryptococcal meningitis","Herpes simplex encephalitis","Tuberculous, fungal, or bacterial brain abscess","Pneumocystis pneumonia (Toxoplasma pneumonia can be confused with it)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary CNS lymphoma","Metastatic brain tumor / other CNS malignancy","Lymphoma (as a cause of lymphadenopathy)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serology: combined IgG and IgM antibody to Toxoplasma; circulating IgA favors acute infection; Sabin-Feldman dye test, indirect fluorescent antibody test, and ELISA measure IgG; double-sandwich IgM-ELISA or IgM-ISAGA preferred for IgM (Harrison 22e p.1801).","IgG avidity testing to help time infection, particularly useful in pregnancy (Harrison 22e p.1801).","PCR amplification of T. gondii DNA from blood, CSF, ocular fluid, bronchoalveolar lavage, or amniotic fluid; PCR is the mainstay for detecting organisms but a negative PCR does not rule out toxoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Double-dose contrast CT and contrast MRI of the brain (standard imaging for Toxoplasma encephalitis); MRI is more sensitive for monitoring response (Harrison 22e p.1802).","CSF analysis in suspected CNS disease (modest pleocytosis and protein elevation without glucose change; CSF PCR specific but not sensitive) (Harrison 22e p.1800-1801).","Lymph node biopsy / histology showing follicular hyperplasia and clusters of epithelioid macrophages; demonstration of tachyzoites establishes acute infection (Harrison 22e p.1799, p.1801).","Dilated ophthalmologic examination for chorioretinitis; ocular fluid PCR, Western blot, or Goldmann-Witmer coefficient for ocular disease (Harrison 22e p.1801-1802).","In HIV-infected persons, screening for IgG antibody to Toxoplasma at the time of HIV diagnosis to identify latent infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Weekly blood PCR screening after allogeneic HSCT in patients seropositive before transplant; pre-transplant donor and recipient serologies (Harrison 22e p.1800).","Next-generation sequencing (metagenomics) of blood or body fluids, especially in immunocompromised individuals (Harrison 22e p.1801)."],"redFlags":["Focal neurologic deficits, seizures, altered mental status, or ring-enhancing CNS mass lesion(s) in an HIV/AIDS patient (especially CD4+ <100/microL) - reactivation Toxoplasma encephalitis, which is rapidly fatal if untreated (Harrison 22e p.1800).","A solitary ring-enhancing CNS lesion - increases the likelihood of primary CNS lymphoma over toxoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.1802).","Failure to improve clinically by day 7 of empiric anti-Toxoplasma therapy - prompts brain biopsy and consideration of lymphoma or alternative diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1802).","Fulminant sepsis, fever of unknown origin, or pneumonia in an immunocompetent person with travel to or residence in South/Central America - virulent atypical genotypes (Harrison 22e p.1797-1799).","Toxoplasma pneumonia / disseminated disease in immunocompromised hosts may progress to acute respiratory failure with hemoptysis, metabolic acidosis, hypotension, and disseminated intravascular coagulation (Harrison 22e p.1800).","Acute maternal infection in pregnancy (risk of transplacental transmission; severity highest with first-trimester infection) (Harrison 22e p.1798)."],"prognosis":"In immunocompetent individuals, acute infection is usually asymptomatic and self-limited, resolving within several weeks though lymphadenopathy may persist for months; chronic infection persists lifelong with little consequence. Ocular disease can be self-limited but episodic chorioretinitis flares progressively destroy retinal tissue and may lead to glaucoma. In immunocompromised hosts, disease may be rapidly fatal if untreated, mandating prompt diagnosis and therapy; with treatment of Toxoplasma encephalitis, >50% improve by day 3 and >90% by day 7, but relapses are common and maintenance/secondary prophylaxis is needed while CD4+ counts remain low. For congenital toxoplasmosis, factors associated with severe disability include delayed diagnosis/treatment, neonatal hypoxia and hypoglycemia, profound visual impairment, uncorrected hydrocephalus, and raised intracranial pressure; with appropriate treatment upward of 70% of children have normal developmental, neurologic, and ophthalmologic findings at follow-up. Infected infants normal at birth may still have higher rates of learning disabilities and chronic neurologic sequelae (Harrison 22e p.1797-1801).","pitfalls":["Acute toxoplasmosis is asymptomatic and self-limited in 80-90% of immunocompetent adults and children, so the diagnosis is easily missed (in one epidemic, toxoplasmosis was correctly diagnosed in only 3 of 25 patients who sought care) (Harrison 22e p.1799).","Antibody titers do not establish reactivation or track infection activity in immunocompromised patients; do not rely on serial titers to gauge active disease (Harrison 22e p.1800).","A negative PCR does not rule out toxoplasmosis - PCR is specific but may be of low sensitivity depending on the body fluid (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Isolation/demonstration of tissue cysts (bradyzoites) from biopsied tissue indicates only the presence of cysts and should NOT be misinterpreted as evidence of acute toxoplasmosis; only demonstration of tachyzoites (e.g., in lymph nodes) establishes acute infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","IgM can persist for >1 year, so a positive IgM with positive IgG should not necessarily be interpreted as acute disease; a negative IgM with positive IgG indicates distant infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","MRI/CT findings are not pathognomonic - 40% of CNS lymphomas are multifocal and 50% are ring-enhancing, and the false-negative rate for both MRI and CT is ~10% (Harrison 22e p.1802).","In AIDS patients with TE, IgM is usually undetectable and titers may be borderline/low (or transplant patients on B-cell-targeted immunosuppression may become seronegative); fewer than 3% have no demonstrable antibody at diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1800, p.1801).","Up to 25% of congenitally infected newborns may be seronegative with normal routine physical examinations, so eye, brain, CSF, and radiologic assessment are needed (Harrison 22e p.1802).","Toxoplasma pneumonia can be confused with Pneumocystis pneumonia, and organisms may also be found in healthy tissue, complicating interpretation (Harrison 22e p.1800).","If a patient is seronegative for T. gondii, the likelihood that a CNS mass lesion is due to toxoplasmosis is <10%, favoring earlier biopsy (Harrison 22e p.1802)."],"management":["Withhold antimicrobials in immunocompetent patients with mild, self-limited acute infection, treating only severe, persistent, or organ-threatening disease","Combination pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine with folinic acid to protect the marrow as the mainstay for toxoplasma encephalitis and severe or ocular disease","Add corticosteroids for sight-threatening chorioretinitis or CNS mass lesions with significant surrounding oedema","Alternative regimens such as pyrimethamine plus clindamycin, or co-trimoxazole, for sulfonamide intolerance","In immunocompromised hosts continue maintenance secondary prophylaxis until immune reconstitution, with primary co-trimoxazole prophylaxis in advanced HIV","In pregnancy use spiramycin to reduce vertical transmission, switching to pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine with folinic acid if foetal infection is confirmed","Preventive counselling to avoid undercooked meat and cat-litter or soil exposure, especially in pregnancy and immunosuppression","Monitor treatment response with clinical and radiological follow-up"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity grading system for toxoplasmosis. Clinical stratification is by host immune status and syndrome: (1) immunocompetent acute infection - usually asymptomatic/self-limited (most commonly cervical lymphadenopathy); (2) ocular toxoplasmosis (chorioretinitis); (3) reactivation disease in the immunocompromised host - most importantly Toxoplasma encephalitis in AIDS (typically CD4+ <100/microL), which is rapidly fatal if untreated, plus pneumonitis, myocarditis, and disseminated disease; and (4) congenital toxoplasmosis, with severity inversely related to gestational age at maternal infection (earliest infection = lowest transmission but most severe fetal disease) (Harrison 22e p.1797-1800).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1797"],"crossLinks":["BRAIN_ABSCESS","ENCEPHALITIS"]},"tracheal_stenosis":{"name":"tracheal stenosis","system":"respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tracheal narrowing","post-intubation tracheal stenosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray and Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fixed narrowing of the tracheal lumen; the commonest acquired cause is post-intubation or post-tracheostomy injury, followed by inflammatory, infiltrative and neoplastic disease.","Post-intubation stenosis typically develops at the level of the cuff or the stoma, where mucosal ischaemia from pressure leads to ulceration, cartilage damage and circumferential fibrotic scarring.","Symptoms often appear weeks to months after extubation, with progressive exertional dyspnoea, stridor and a sensation of difficulty moving air; wheeze frequently prompts misdiagnosis as asthma.","Biphasic stridor suggests a fixed lesion at the thoracic inlet; stridor usually becomes audible once the lumen is critically narrowed, so its absence does not exclude significant stenosis.","The flow-volume loop classically shows flattening of both inspiratory and expiratory limbs, the pattern of a fixed extrathoracic or intrathoracic upper-airway obstruction.","Consider granulomatosis with polyangiitis (subglottic involvement), amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, relapsing polychondritis and tracheobronchopathia osteochondroplastica among non-iatrogenic causes.","Bronchoscopy defines the length, calibre, maturity and location of the stricture and distinguishes simple web-like from complex cartilaginous stenoses, guiding whether endoscopic dilation or resection is appropriate.","Idiopathic subglottic stenosis characteristically affects women in mid-adult life and tends to recur after endoscopic treatment."],"pathophysiology":"Acquired tracheal stenosis most often follows mucosal injury from an endotracheal or tracheostomy tube. Sustained cuff or tube pressure exceeding mucosal perfusion causes ischaemic ulceration; the exposed cartilage undergoes chondritis and may soften or fragment, and subsequent healing produces circumferential granulation and mature fibrous scar that contracts the lumen. Depending on the site and mechanism, stenosis may be a thin fibrous web, a longer cartilaginous 'A-frame' collapse, or a complex segment with associated malacia. Inflammatory causes (granulomatosis with polyangiitis, relapsing polychondritis, sarcoidosis) narrow the airway through mucosal and cartilaginous inflammation and fibrosis, while infiltrative diseases such as amyloidosis and tracheobronchopathia osteochondroplastica deposit material within the submucosa. Regardless of cause, the reduced cross-sectional area sharply increases airflow resistance; because resistance rises steeply as the radius falls, patients may be nearly asymptomatic until the lumen is severely compromised, after which small further reductions cause marked dyspnoea and stridor.","additionalDifferentials":["asthma or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","tracheomalacia or excessive dynamic airway collapse","extrinsic compression by goitre, mediastinal mass or vascular anomaly","primary or secondary tracheal tumour","granulomatosis with polyangiitis with subglottic stenosis","relapsing polychondritis","tracheobronchial amyloidosis or tracheobronchopathia osteochondroplastica"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["flexible and rigid bronchoscopy to map length, calibre and maturity of the stricture","multidetector CT of the neck and chest with multiplanar and virtual bronchoscopic reconstruction","pulmonary function testing with inspiratory and expiratory flow-volume loops","anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibody testing where vasculitis is suspected","mucosal biopsy for amyloid, granulomatous or neoplastic disease","serum inflammatory markers and autoimmune serology as indicated","laryngoscopic assessment of glottic and subglottic involvement"],"redFlags":["stridor at rest or use of accessory muscles indicating critical airway narrowing","rapidly progressive dyspnoea or inability to lie flat","hypoxaemia, exhaustion or altered consciousness suggesting impending airway failure","haemoptysis or systemic features raising concern for malignancy or active vasculitis","recent extubation with worsening breathlessness suggesting evolving high-grade stenosis"],"prognosis":"Short, mature, web-like stenoses often respond durably to endoscopic dilation, whereas complex, long or cartilaginous strictures usually require segmental tracheal resection with reconstruction, which achieves good long-term results in experienced centres; inflammatory and idiopathic subglottic disease is prone to recurrence and needs ongoing surveillance.","pitfalls":["Attributing fixed wheeze and dyspnoea to asthma and delaying airway imaging or bronchoscopy.","Underestimating severity because symptoms are minimal until the lumen is critically reduced.","Performing repeated dilations of a lesion that has a treatable systemic inflammatory cause without addressing the underlying disease.","Overlooking concurrent malacia, which limits the durability of dilation alone.","Inserting a stent in benign disease without strong indication, given the risk of granulation, migration and secondary infection."],"management":["Restore the lumen: endoscopic balloon dilation for short, mature, web-like strictures, reserving segmental tracheal resection with end-to-end reconstruction for long, complex or cartilaginous stenoses","Adjunctive endoscopic techniques including radial laser incision of fibrous webs and topical mitomycin to relieve obstruction and limit recurrent granulation, with airway stenting for malacic or non-operable segments","Treat the underlying inflammatory cause where present, with glucocorticoids and steroid-sparing immunosuppression for granulomatosis with polyangiitis or relapsing polychondritis","Supportive airway measures including humidified air, nebulised saline and secretion clearance, with heliox to reduce work of breathing during critical narrowing","Surveillance with serial bronchoscopy and inspiratory-expiratory flow-volume loops to detect restenosis after dilation or resection","Plan for acute critical obstruction with urgent rigid bronchoscopy to secure and dilate the airway, avoiding blind intubation through a tight stenosis","Manage within a multidisciplinary airway team including thoracic surgery, interventional respiratory medicine and ENT, individualising intervention to stenosis morphology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray and Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine, current edition","Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders, current edition","European Respiratory Society / American Thoracic Society guidance on central airway obstruction"],"crossLinks":[]},"tracheobronchomalacia":{"name":"Tracheobronchomalacia","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBM","tracheomalacia","bronchomalacia","excessive dynamic airway collapse (related)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Respiratory Medicine and Bronchoscopy reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by excessive expiratory narrowing of the central airways due to weakness of the airway wall, causing dynamic collapse with cough, forced expiration, or Valsalva","Causes a harsh barking or seal-like cough, breathlessness, wheeze, difficulty clearing secretions, and recurrent infections","May be congenital (including association with tracheo-oesophageal anomalies) or acquired from prolonged intubation, chronic inflammation, COPD, trauma, or extrinsic compression","Excessive dynamic airway collapse describes bulging of the posterior membrane, distinct from cartilage-based malacia, though the two overlap clinically","Dynamic imaging or bronchoscopy is essential, as the collapse is seen during expiration or coughing and may be missed on static inspiratory studies","A common diagnostic threshold is more than about half expiratory reduction in luminal cross-section, ideally confirmed dynamically","Management ranges from treating underlying disease and positive-pressure support to airway stenting or surgical tracheobronchoplasty in severe cases"],"pathophysiology":"Tracheobronchomalacia results from structural weakness of the central airway wall, either from deficient or softened cartilaginous support or from laxity and redundancy of the posterior membranous wall. During expiration and coughing, when intrathoracic pressure rises above intraluminal pressure, the weakened airway collapses excessively, obstructing airflow and impairing the shear forces needed to clear secretions. Congenital forms reflect immature or deficient cartilage, while acquired forms follow injury from prolonged intubation or tracheostomy, chronic inflammation, recurrent infection, emphysema, trauma, or chronic compression by adjacent structures. The dynamic nature of the collapse explains why symptoms are provoked by exertion and cough and why static imaging can underestimate severity.","additionalDifferentials":["Asthma","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Fixed central airway stenosis or tumour","Vocal cord dysfunction","Extrinsic airway compression (goitre, vascular ring, mediastinal mass)","Relapsing polychondritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dynamic expiratory CT of the airways to quantify collapse","Bronchoscopy with observation during forced expiration and coughing","Pulmonary function tests with flow-volume loops","Assessment for underlying causes such as prior intubation, COPD, or compression","Evaluation for tracheo-oesophageal anomalies in congenital cases","Trial of positive airway pressure to assess symptomatic and functional response"],"redFlags":["Recurrent respiratory failure or life-threatening episodes of near-complete airway collapse","Severe secretion retention with recurrent pneumonia","Rapidly worsening symptoms suggesting an evolving compressive lesion","Syncope or exercise-limiting obstruction from severe dynamic collapse"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on severity and cause; mild disease is often managed conservatively with good quality of life, while severe symptomatic malacia can be disabling but may improve substantially with positive-pressure support, stenting, or tracheobronchoplasty.","pitfalls":["Relying on static inspiratory imaging and missing the dynamic collapse","Mislabelling the disease as refractory asthma or COPD and escalating ineffective therapy","Overlooking a treatable underlying cause such as extrinsic compression","Proceeding to airway stenting without a trial confirming benefit, given stent-related complications"],"management":["Continuous positive airway pressure as a pneumatic stent to splint the central airways open during expiration and improve secretion clearance in symptomatic disease","Optimise airway clearance with cough-assist techniques, mucolytics and pulmonary rehabilitation to counter impaired secretion clearance","Treat contributing conditions including inhaled therapy for coexisting COPD and relief of extrinsic compression where present","Prompt culture-guided antibiotics for the recurrent lower respiratory infections driven by retained secretions","Short-term silicone airway stent trial to identify patients likely to benefit from surgery","Surgical tracheobronchoplasty to stabilise the posterior membranous wall in severe, disabling, well-selected disease","Manage in a multidisciplinary airway service spanning interventional pulmonology and thoracic surgery, reserving intervention for genuinely symptomatic collapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Flexible Bronchoscopy (Wang, Mehta, Turner)"],"crossLinks":[]},"tracheobronchomegaly":{"name":"tracheobronchomegaly","system":"respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mounier-Kuhn syndrome","tracheobronchial dilatation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Tracheobronchomegaly (Mounier-Kuhn syndrome) is marked abnormal dilatation of the trachea and main bronchi due to atrophy or deficiency of the elastic and smooth muscle of the airway wall.","The dilated airway is abnormally compliant, so it collapses on expiration and cough, impairing clearance and predisposing to recurrent infection and bronchiectasis.","Corrugation of the airway wall between the cartilage rings produces mucosal outpouchings (tracheal diverticulosis), classically giving a scalloped contour on imaging.","It usually presents in adult men with chronic productive cough, recurrent lower respiratory infection, breathlessness and, in some, a loud ineffective cough.","Diagnosis rests on measuring the tracheal and main bronchial diameters on CT, which exceed defined upper limits of normal.","Dynamic imaging or bronchoscopy demonstrates the excessive expiratory collapse that accompanies the fixed dilatation.","Associations include connective-tissue and cartilage disorders (such as Ehlers-Danlos and cutis laxa) and, in children, certain congenital syndromes.","Management is largely supportive — airway clearance, prompt treatment of infection and immunisation — as no therapy reverses the structural abnormality; positive-pressure support may aid selected patients."],"pathophysiology":"Tracheobronchomegaly results from deficiency or atrophy of the elastic fibres and smooth muscle within the membranous and cartilaginous portions of the tracheal and main bronchial walls. The weakened wall dilates progressively under normal transmural pressures, and the redundant, poorly supported tissue between cartilage rings herniates outward to form the characteristic corrugations and diverticula. The enlarged, flaccid airway loses its structural rigidity and collapses during expiration and coughing; because cough depends on generating high linear airflow velocities in a stable airway, the dynamic collapse renders cough ineffective and severely impairs mucus clearance. Retained secretions and recurrent infection damage the distal airways, producing bronchiectasis and further inflammation in a self-perpetuating cycle. The condition may be idiopathic, familial, or associated with heritable connective-tissue disorders in which generalised deficiency of elastic tissue affects the airway as one of several sites.","additionalDifferentials":["bronchiectasis from other causes (cystic fibrosis, post-infective, immunodeficiency)","tracheomalacia or excessive dynamic airway collapse without dilatation","chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with recurrent infection","saber-sheath trachea","tracheobronchial diverticula from other causes","connective-tissue disease with airway involvement","acquired tracheal dilatation adjacent to fibrotic lung disease (traction)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["chest CT with measurement of tracheal and main bronchial diameters","dynamic expiratory CT to demonstrate excessive airway collapse","flexible bronchoscopy to assess wall flaccidity, diverticula and collapse","pulmonary function testing including flow-volume loop analysis","sputum culture and assessment of bronchiectasis-related infection","evaluation for associated connective-tissue or cartilage disorder","assessment of immune function where recurrent infection is prominent"],"redFlags":["recurrent or severe lower respiratory infections with clinical deterioration","progressive respiratory failure from ineffective cough and secretion retention","haemoptysis complicating established bronchiectasis","acute large-airway obstruction from retained secretions or mucus plugging","features of a systemic connective-tissue disorder requiring broader assessment"],"prognosis":"The course is variable: some patients remain stable with mild symptoms for years, whereas those with extensive bronchiectasis and recurrent infection may progress to chronic respiratory impairment; management is supportive, as the structural airway abnormality is not reversible.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis by not measuring airway calibre on CT in a patient with recurrent infection and bronchiectasis.","Attributing the ineffective cough and recurrent infection solely to bronchiectasis without recognising the underlying megatrachea.","Relying on static imaging and missing the dynamic expiratory collapse.","Inserting airway stents without strong indication, given the poor structural support and complication risk.","Failing to screen for an associated heritable connective-tissue disorder."],"management":["Recognise there is no therapy to reverse the structural dilatation, so care centres on airway clearance with chest physiotherapy, postural drainage and mucolytics to counter impaired mucus clearance","Prompt, culture-guided antibiotics for recurrent lower respiratory infections, with active management of established bronchiectasis","Positive-pressure ventilation to splint the flaccid, collapsing airway and aid secretion clearance","Immunisation against influenza and pneumococcus to reduce infective exacerbations","Airway stenting or tracheobronchoplasty reserved for selected patients with severe, refractory expiratory collapse","Surveillance for progressive bronchiectasis and respiratory impairment with serial imaging, spirometry and sputum monitoring","Assess for an associated connective-tissue or cartilage disorder and maintain long-term respiratory specialist follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders, current edition","Murray and Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine, current edition","Contemporary reviews of Mounier-Kuhn syndrome and central airway disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"tracheobronchopathia_osteochondroplastica":{"name":"Tracheobronchopathia osteochondroplastica","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TPO","TBO","tracheopathia osteoplastica","osteochondroplastic tracheobronchopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Respiratory Medicine and Bronchoscopy reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare benign condition characterised by multiple submucosal cartilaginous and bony nodules projecting into the lumen of the trachea and large bronchi","Characteristically spares the posterior membranous wall of the trachea because the nodules arise from cartilage, which is absent posteriorly","Often asymptomatic and found incidentally; when symptomatic it causes chronic cough, wheeze, haemoptysis, hoarseness, and recurrent infections","Bronchoscopy shows a distinctive cobblestone or rock-garden appearance with hard nodules that can make instrumentation difficult","CT demonstrates calcified nodules along the anterolateral tracheal walls with sparing of the posterior membrane","Usually a disease of older adults and generally follows a slow, benign course","Diagnosis is typically made bronchoscopically or on imaging, with biopsy confirming cartilage and bone within the nodules"],"pathophysiology":"Tracheobronchopathia osteochondroplastica is defined by the formation of multiple submucosal nodules composed of cartilage and heterotopic bone, sometimes with marrow, that bulge into the airway lumen. These nodules are thought to arise from the perichondrium and cartilaginous rings, which explains their anterolateral distribution and the characteristic sparing of the posterior membranous wall that lacks cartilage. The cause is unknown, with proposed contributions from chronic inflammation, metabolic and degenerative processes, and abnormal bone morphogenetic signalling within airway connective tissue. Over time the nodules can narrow and stiffen the airway, causing airflow obstruction, impaired secretion clearance, and recurrent infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Amyloidosis of the airway","Tracheobronchial calcification from other causes","Endobronchial tuberculosis","Papillomatosis or other endobronchial tumours","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis affecting the airway","Relapsing polychondritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bronchoscopy revealing the cobblestone nodular appearance and sparing of the posterior wall","CT of the airways showing calcified submucosal nodules","Biopsy demonstrating cartilage and bone within nodules","Pulmonary function tests including flow-volume loops for fixed obstruction","Assessment for recurrent infection and secretion retention","Exclusion of amyloidosis and other infiltrative airway diseases"],"redFlags":["Significant central airway obstruction causing stridor or exertional breathlessness","Recurrent or severe lower respiratory infections from impaired clearance","Haemoptysis requiring exclusion of other airway pathology","Difficult airway during intubation or bronchoscopy due to rigid nodules"],"prognosis":"The condition is usually benign and slowly progressive; most patients have a good prognosis with symptomatic management, and significant airway compromise requiring intervention is uncommon.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the nodular airway appearance for malignancy or amyloidosis without biopsy","Failing to recognise the diagnostic clue of posterior membrane sparing","Underestimating the risk of a difficult airway during instrumentation","Attributing chronic cough or recurrent infection to more common causes and overlooking the diagnosis"],"management":["Observation alone for the many asymptomatic patients, given the benign, slowly progressive course","Symptomatic relief of cough and wheeze with inhaled bronchodilators, humidification and cough suppression as needed","Prompt antibiotics and airway clearance for recurrent infections arising from impaired secretion clearance","Bronchoscopic debulking by laser, cryotherapy or mechanical removal of nodules for significant airway obstruction, anticipating hard tissue that makes instrumentation difficult","Periodic bronchoscopic and CT surveillance to monitor luminal narrowing and secretion retention","Exclude infiltrative mimics, particularly amyloidosis, given the overlapping submucosal nodular appearance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Flexible Bronchoscopy (Wang, Mehta, Turner)"],"crossLinks":[]},"tracheoesophageal_fistula":{"name":"Tracheoesophageal Fistula","system":"Neonatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TEF","TOF/TEF","esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula","EA/TEF"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An abnormal congenital communication between the trachea and oesophagus, most often occurring together with oesophageal atresia; the commonest anatomical type is proximal oesophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula.","Typical newborn features include excessive drooling and frothy secretions, choking, coughing, and cyanosis with feeds, and inability to pass a nasogastric tube into the stomach.","A characteristic radiographic clue is a feeding tube coiling in the upper oesophageal pouch; gas in the stomach and bowel indicates a distal fistula, while a gasless abdomen suggests atresia without a distal fistula.","Aspiration of saliva and, via the fistula, gastric contents into the airway causes respiratory compromise and pneumonitis.","An isolated H-type fistula without atresia may present later with recurrent coughing during feeds, cyanotic spells, and recurrent respiratory infections.","Initial management protects the airway and lungs: keep the infant nil by mouth, apply continuous suction to the upper pouch, and position to reduce aspiration and reflux through the fistula.","It is frequently part of the VACTERL association, so evaluation for vertebral, anorectal, cardiac, renal, and limb anomalies is important.","Definitive treatment is surgical division of the fistula and repair of the oesophageal atresia, timed according to the infant's stability and anatomy.","Long-term issues can include anastomotic stricture, gastro-oesophageal reflux, tracheomalacia, and dysphagia requiring ongoing follow-up."],"pathophysiology":"Abnormal separation of the primitive foregut into trachea and oesophagus during embryogenesis leaves an abnormal connection between the two and, commonly, a discontinuous oesophagus (atresia). In the common configuration, the upper oesophagus ends in a blind pouch while the lower oesophagus connects to the trachea. Swallowed secretions collect in and overflow from the upper pouch into the airway, and the distal fistula allows gastric contents to reflux into the trachea and air to distend the stomach. These mechanisms produce aspiration, chemical pneumonitis, and, with a large distal fistula, gaseous abdominal distension that further impairs ventilation.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated oesophageal atresia without fistula","Laryngeal cleft","Choanal atresia","Severe gastro-oesophageal reflux with aspiration","Vascular ring causing feeding difficulty","Swallowing dysfunction of neurological origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Attempted passage of a nasogastric tube with radiograph showing tube coiling in the upper pouch","Chest and abdominal radiograph to assess the pouch and bowel gas pattern","Echocardiography to identify cardiac anomalies and aortic arch side before surgery","Renal ultrasound and spinal and limb assessment for VACTERL anomalies","Bronchoscopy or contrast study to define an H-type fistula when suspected"],"redFlags":["Choking, cyanosis, and respiratory distress with attempted feeding","Aspiration pneumonitis from overflow of secretions or reflux through the fistula","Progressive gastric and bowel distension impairing ventilation via a large distal fistula","Recurrent respiratory infections and cyanotic spells suggesting a missed H-type fistula","Associated critical cardiac or other major anomalies within the VACTERL spectrum"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and surgical repair the outlook is generally favourable, and prognosis is influenced strongly by prematurity, birth weight, and associated anomalies, particularly cardiac lesions. Many children require management of later complications such as strictures, reflux, and tracheomalacia but achieve good long-term function.","pitfalls":["Feeding a newborn with excessive drooling before excluding oesophageal atresia, precipitating aspiration","Missing a delayed-presenting H-type fistula in a child with recurrent feeding-related respiratory symptoms","Failing to search for associated VACTERL anomalies, especially cardiac lesions, before surgery","Overlooking that a gasless abdomen indicates atresia without a distal fistula","Underestimating long-term reflux, stricture, and tracheomalacia complications requiring follow-up"],"management":["Immediate airway and lung protection: keep the infant nil by mouth, apply continuous suction to the upper oesophageal pouch with a Replogle tube, and nurse head-up to limit aspiration and reflux through the fistula","Definitive surgical correction with ligation and division of the tracheoesophageal fistula and primary oesophageal anastomosis","Preoperative echocardiography to define cardiac anomalies and aortic arch side, with screening for associated VACTERL anomalies","Respiratory support and treatment of aspiration pneumonitis, ventilating carefully to avoid distending the stomach through a distal fistula","Anticipate and manage postoperative complications, dilating anastomotic strictures endoscopically, controlling gastro-oesophageal reflux with acid suppression or fundoplication, and treating residual tracheomalacia","Manage within a paediatric surgical and neonatal multidisciplinary team, with long-term follow-up of feeding, growth and respiratory status"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"tracheomalacia":{"name":"tracheomalacia","system":"respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tracheobronchomalacia","expiratory central airway collapse"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray and Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by excessive expiratory collapse of the trachea (and often main bronchi) due to weakness of the cartilaginous rings, the posterior membranous wall, or both, producing a crescentic or 'frown-shaped' lumen on dynamic imaging.","Congenital forms associate with prematurity, tracheo-oesophageal fistula/oesophageal atresia, vascular rings and cartilage disorders; acquired forms follow prolonged intubation, tracheostomy, chronic inflammation (relapsing polychondritis), COPD or extrinsic compression.","The cardinal symptom is a harsh, barking or 'seal-like' cough, often with expiratory wheeze, exertional dyspnoea and difficulty clearing secretions leading to recurrent lower respiratory infection.","Symptoms characteristically worsen with any manoeuvre that raises intrathoracic pressure — coughing, forced expiration, crying in infants, or supine posture.","Dynamic (inspiratory-expiratory or cine) CT and awake flexible bronchoscopy with the patient breathing spontaneously are the reference investigations; static end-inspiratory imaging alone frequently misses the diagnosis.","Flow-volume loops may show expiratory flow oscillations or a characteristic notch, but normal spirometry does not exclude the condition.","Distinguish primary cartilage/membranous weakness (tracheomalacia) from dynamic excessive collapse driven by an over-compliant posterior membrane (excessive dynamic airway collapse), as management emphasis differs.","Continuous positive airway pressure can act as a pneumatic stent, relieving symptoms and guiding suitability for surgical stabilisation such as posterior tracheobronchopexy."],"pathophysiology":"Tracheomalacia results from structural weakness of the tracheal wall so that it cannot maintain patency against the transmural pressure swings of respiration. Weakness may lie in deficient or soft cartilaginous rings (reducing the normal cartilage-to-membrane ratio) or in an excessively lax, redundant posterior membranous wall. During expiration and cough, positive intrathoracic pressure exceeds the airway's structural resistance, so the intrathoracic trachea collapses inward, dynamically narrowing the lumen; the extrathoracic trachea, by contrast, tends to collapse on inspiration. This flow limitation impairs secretion clearance, promotes air trapping and turbulent flow, and predisposes to mucus retention and recurrent infection. Congenital disease reflects intrinsic cartilage immaturity or maldevelopment (often with associated foregut or vascular anomalies), whereas acquired disease reflects cartilage destruction or degeneration from pressure necrosis, chronic inflammation, or long-standing extrinsic compression.","additionalDifferentials":["asthma with poorly reversible airflow obstruction","chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","fixed tracheal stenosis","vocal cord dysfunction / inducible laryngeal obstruction","extrinsic tracheal compression by mediastinal mass or vascular ring","excessive dynamic airway collapse from an over-compliant membranous wall","relapsing polychondritis affecting the airway"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["dynamic inspiratory-expiratory (cine) CT of the central airways","awake flexible bronchoscopy during spontaneous and forced breathing","pulmonary function testing with flow-volume loop analysis","trial of continuous positive airway pressure as a diagnostic-therapeutic stent","echocardiography or CT angiography where vascular ring or compression is suspected","evaluation for relapsing polychondritis where cartilage inflammation is suspected","contrast oesophagram if tracheo-oesophageal fistula or vascular ring is possible"],"redFlags":["recurrent 'dying spells', cyanotic or apparent life-threatening events in an infant","acute respiratory distress with inability to clear secretions","recurrent or non-resolving lower respiratory tract infection","syncope or near-syncope precipitated by cough (cough syncope)","rapidly progressive dyspnoea suggesting associated systemic cartilage disease"],"prognosis":"Congenital tracheomalacia frequently improves as the airway cartilage matures over the first one to two years of life, whereas acquired and syndromic forms tend to be persistent and may progress; severe or diffuse disease carries appreciable morbidity but can respond well to positive-pressure support or surgical stabilisation in selected patients.","pitfalls":["Relying on static end-inspiratory imaging or a single bronchoscopic snapshot, which can miss dynamic expiratory collapse.","Misdiagnosing the barking cough and wheeze as refractory asthma and escalating bronchodilators or steroids without benefit.","Overlooking a treatable underlying cause such as a vascular ring, mediastinal mass or relapsing polychondritis.","Placing airway stents indiscriminately, as they carry substantial complications including granulation, migration and infection.","Failing to recognise associated gastro-oesophageal reflux or aspiration that perpetuates airway inflammation."],"management":["Conservative, expectant management for congenital disease, since airway cartilage commonly matures and symptoms improve over the first year or two of life, supported by humidified air and physiotherapy","Continuous positive airway pressure as a pneumatic stent for severe or symptomatic collapse, aiding both patency and secretion clearance","Airway clearance and prompt antibiotics for the recurrent lower respiratory infections caused by impaired secretion clearance","Treat the underlying cause, with immunosuppression for relapsing polychondritis and surgical relief of a vascular ring or other extrinsic compression","Surgical options for severe disease including aortopexy for anterior compression, tracheobronchoplasty, or airway stenting in selected cases","Plan for acute events by avoiding manoeuvres that raise intrathoracic pressure, managing within a multidisciplinary airway team with dynamic bronchoscopic surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray and Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine, current edition","European Respiratory Society statement on tracheobronchomalacia and excessive dynamic airway collapse","Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"trachyonychia":{"name":"Trachyonychia (twenty-nail dystrophy)","system":"Dermatology / Nail","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["twenty-nail dystrophy","rough nails","sandpaper nails"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Trachyonychia produces rough, brittle, longitudinally ridged nails with a sandpaper-like surface, often with a loss of lustre","It may affect several or all twenty nails, hence the older term twenty-nail dystrophy, and is more common in children","It is a reaction pattern of the nail matrix rather than a single disease, and is associated with alopecia areata, lichen planus, and psoriasis","Two clinical forms are recognised: an opaque, rough sandpaper type and a shinier variety with numerous small superficial pits","It is usually a benign, cosmetic problem that does not scar the nail, particularly in the idiopathic childhood form","Diagnosis is generally clinical, and matrix biopsy is reserved for atypical cases because the appearance rarely predicts the underlying association","Management is largely supportive with reassurance and nail protection, as many childhood cases improve spontaneously over years"],"pathophysiology":"Trachyonychia reflects inflammation of the nail matrix that disturbs the orderly production of the nail plate, so that the emerging nail is irregular, rough, and ridged. The matrix inflammation is a shared final pathway for several conditions, including alopecia areata, lichen planus, psoriasis, and eczema, which is why the clinical appearance alone seldom identifies the cause. Because the inflammation usually affects the matrix without destroying it, the process is generally non-scarring and the nail can recover, although the widespread involvement of many nails reflects a diffuse matrix response.","additionalDifferentials":["Nail lichen planus (with risk of scarring and pterygium in severe cases)","Nail psoriasis","Alopecia areata-associated nail changes","Chronic eczema affecting the nail matrix","Idiopathic twenty-nail dystrophy of childhood","Nail changes of lichen striatus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination of all twenty nails and the hair and skin for associated disease","Onychoscopy to characterise pitting, ridging, and surface changes","Assessment for alopecia areata, lichen planus, and psoriasis elsewhere","Nail matrix biopsy reserved for atypical, scarring, or diagnostically uncertain cases","Mycology if fungal infection needs to be excluded","Longitudinal review to monitor spontaneous improvement, especially in children"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting nail lichen planus with scarring, such as pterygium formation, which needs prompt attention","Rapidly progressive or destructive nail change inconsistent with benign trachyonychia","Associated systemic or extensive cutaneous disease requiring specific treatment","Single-nail atypical change that does not fit the diffuse pattern and warrants biopsy"],"prognosis":"Trachyonychia is generally benign and non-scarring, and the idiopathic childhood form often resolves spontaneously over several years, although nails associated with lichen planus can occasionally scar and warrant closer follow-up.","pitfalls":["Over-investigating a benign, self-limiting childhood presentation","Missing scarring lichen planus of the nail, which requires earlier intervention","Assuming a fungal cause and treating with antifungals without confirmation","Promising rapid resolution when recovery is typically slow over months to years"],"management":["Reassure and observe, particularly in the idiopathic childhood form, which is benign, non-scarring and usually resolves spontaneously over several years","General nail care with emollients and hydration, nail-hardening lacquers, and avoidance of trauma and irritants to limit brittleness","Treat any associated matrix dermatosis, using topical or intralesional corticosteroids for lichen planus and vitamin D analogues or corticosteroids for psoriasis","Intralesional corticosteroid injection to the nail matrix for troublesome or refractory disease","Screen for and manage coexisting alopecia areata, lichen planus and psoriasis elsewhere on the skin and hair","Reserve nail matrix biopsy for atypical or scarring cases, with closer follow-up where lichen planus threatens permanent nail damage"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Baran and Dawber's Diseases of the Nails and their Management"],"crossLinks":[]},"traction_alopecia":{"name":"Traction alopecia","system":"Dermatology / Trichology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["marginal alopecia","hairstyle-induced hair loss","traumatic marginal alopecia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hair loss caused by prolonged or repetitive tension on the hair from tight hairstyles such as braids, weaves, ponytails and buns, and from the use of extensions","Classically affects the frontotemporal and marginal (hairline) scalp, producing a band of thinning with retention of a rim of fine hairs at the very margin, the useful fringe sign","Early disease is non-scarring and reversible, but persistent traction eventually destroys follicles and leads to permanent scarring alopecia","Hair casts, which are cylindrical concretions encircling the shaft, together with perifollicular erythema or pustules, indicate ongoing traction damage","It is more common where cultural or occupational styling imposes chronic tension, and is also seen with tight religious head coverings and turbans","Diagnosis is clinical from the pattern and history, and trichoscopy shows hair casts and reduced density with early preservation of ostia","The key intervention is to release the tension and change styling early, before follicular loss becomes permanent"],"pathophysiology":"Traction alopecia results from sustained mechanical tension transmitted to the hair follicle by tight hairstyling. Repeated pulling first produces perifollicular inflammation and extrusion of hairs, then, if traction continues over months to years, progressive follicular miniaturisation, atrophy and eventual fibrous replacement. Loss predominates at the margins where tension is greatest, and the process converts from a reversible non-scarring phase to an irreversible scarring one as follicular ostia are lost.","additionalDifferentials":["Frontal fibrosing alopecia, with band-like frontotemporal recession, scarring and eyebrow loss","Androgenetic alopecia","Alopecia areata in an ophiasis pattern","Trichotillomania","Telogen effluvium"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed hairstyling and cultural or occupational history","Trichoscopy to assess hair casts, density and follicular ostia","Recognition of marginal loss with a retained fringe sign","Scalp biopsy where scarring or an alternative diagnosis such as frontal fibrosing alopecia is suspected","Photographic documentation to monitor progression"],"redFlags":["Loss of follicular ostia indicating conversion to permanent scarring alopecia","Perifollicular erythema and scaling suggesting a coexisting lymphocytic scarring alopecia such as frontal fibrosing alopecia","Rapidly progressive marginal loss despite change of hairstyle"],"prognosis":"If tension is relieved during the early inflammatory phase hair usually regrows, but longstanding traction causes irreversible scarring alopecia, so prevention through early modification of hairstyling is essential.","pitfalls":["Assuming marginal recession is always traction and missing frontal fibrosing alopecia, which also affects the hairline and eyebrows","Failing to advise a change of hairstyle early, allowing reversible loss to become permanent","Overlooking the fringe sign, a helpful clue to the diagnosis","Not recognising that pustules may reflect secondary folliculitis rather than a primary infection"],"management":["Remove the mechanical cause by stopping tight braids, weaves, ponytails, buns and extensions and adopting low-tension hairstyles, the essential step while follicles remain viable","Reduce active follicular inflammation with topical or intralesional corticosteroids during the early, reversible phase","Promote regrowth with topical minoxidil where follicles are still preserved","Treat associated folliculitis or pustules with topical or oral antibiotics","Education and behavioural change around styling practices to prevent recurrence and progression to scarring","Consider surgical hair transplantation or camouflage for established, irreversible scarring alopecia, with photographic monitoring of progress"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"trali":{"name":"Transfusion-related acute lung injury","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TRALI","transfusion-related acute lung injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AABB Technical Manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as acute hypoxaemia and non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema developing during or within about six hours of transfusion","Bilateral pulmonary infiltrates appear on chest imaging without evidence of left atrial hypertension or fluid overload","A key distinction from TACO is the absence of features of circulatory overload; jugular venous pressure and natriuretic peptides are typically not elevated","Often mediated by donor anti-leukocyte (anti-HLA or anti-neutrophil) antibodies, so plasma-rich components from multiparous or previously transfused donors carry higher risk","Historically a leading cause of transfusion-related mortality, which fell substantially after mitigation strategies such as using predominantly male-donor plasma","Management is supportive with oxygen and lung-protective ventilation as needed; diuretics are unhelpful and the picture is one of capillary leak rather than volume excess","Most patients improve within one to three days, distinguishing it from other causes of ARDS"],"pathophysiology":"TRALI reflects acute injury to the pulmonary capillary endothelium leading to increased permeability and protein-rich alveolar oedema. A widely used two-hit model proposes that a predisposing condition (such as sepsis, surgery, or inflammation) primes and sequesters neutrophils in the lung; a second hit from transfused antibodies against recipient leukocyte antigens, or from biologically active lipids and cytokines accumulated in stored components, then activates these neutrophils. The resulting release of reactive oxygen species and proteases damages the endothelium and produces non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO)","Acute respiratory distress syndrome from other causes","Anaphylactic transfusion reaction","Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema","Bacterial contamination of a component with sepsis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph or CT showing bilateral infiltrates","Arterial blood gas or pulse oximetry to quantify hypoxaemia","Natriuretic peptide levels and echocardiography to help exclude circulatory overload","Investigation of implicated donors for anti-HLA and anti-neutrophil antibodies via the transfusion service","Exclusion of infective and cardiac causes"],"redFlags":["Acute severe hypoxaemia and respiratory distress during or shortly after transfusion requiring urgent oxygen and possible ventilatory support","Rapid clinical deterioration necessitating critical care","Diagnostic uncertainty with concurrent features of overload or sepsis","Recurrence on re-exposure to a component from an implicated donor"],"prognosis":"Most cases resolve within one to three days with supportive care and hypoxaemia improves, but severe episodes can be fatal; identification and deferral of implicated donors prevents further cases.","pitfalls":["Misattributing the picture to fluid overload and giving diuretics, delaying appropriate supportive care","Failing to stop the transfusion and report the reaction to the transfusion service","Not investigating and deferring implicated donors, risking further cases","Overlooking TRALI in ventilated critically ill patients where hypoxaemia is attributed solely to underlying illness"],"management":["Stop the transfusion immediately and notify the transfusion service when acute hypoxaemia develops within hours of a component","Supportive respiratory care with supplemental oxygen, escalating to non-invasive or lung-protective invasive ventilation for the non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema","Support the circulation with fluids and vasopressors for hypotension, recognising this is capillary leak rather than volume overload","Avoid diuretics, which are unhelpful because the oedema is inflammatory and permeability-driven rather than hydrostatic","Report the reaction and investigate implicated donors for anti-HLA and anti-neutrophil antibodies, deferring them to prevent further cases","Preventive transfusion practice using predominantly male-donor plasma and restrictive component use to lower risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"transaldolase_deficiency":{"name":"Transaldolase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TALDO deficiency","TALDO1 deficiency","transaldolase 1 deficiency","pentose phosphate pathway transaldolase defect"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive defect of transaldolase (TALDO1 gene) in the non-oxidative branch of the pentose phosphate pathway, leading to accumulation of polyols and seven-carbon sugars","Often presents in the neonatal or antenatal period with hepatosplenomegaly, liver dysfunction, coagulopathy and thrombocytopenia, sometimes with hydrops","A multisystem disorder that can include dysmorphic features, cutis laxa or skin changes, cardiac and renal anomalies, and anaemia","Urine polyol analysis shows elevated erythritol, arabitol and ribitol, and characteristic seven-carbon sugars such as sedoheptulose and its derivatives","Progressive liver disease with fibrosis or cirrhosis is a major feature, and hepatocellular carcinoma has been reported","There is no specific enzyme-replacement therapy, so management is supportive with monitoring of hepatic, renal, haematological and cardiac function, and antioxidant approaches have been explored","Diagnosis relies on the urinary polyol/sugar pattern with confirmatory TALDO1 molecular testing"],"pathophysiology":"Transaldolase catalyses a reversible step in the non-oxidative branch of the pentose phosphate pathway that interconverts sugar phosphates and links the pathway with glycolysis. Its deficiency disrupts the balance of pentose phosphate intermediates, causing accumulation of polyols (such as erythritol, arabitol and ribitol) and seven-carbon sugars (including sedoheptulose). The disturbed pathway is thought to increase oxidative stress and impair regeneration of reducing equivalents, contributing to progressive injury of the liver, kidneys, heart and blood cells. The result is a multisystem disorder dominated by hepatosplenomegaly, hepatic fibrosis, coagulopathy and cytopenias, often evident from birth.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital disorders of glycosylation","Neonatal haemochromatosis and other neonatal liver failure syndromes","Congenital infections causing hepatosplenomegaly (TORCH)","Other pentose phosphate pathway defects (ribose-5-phosphate isomerase deficiency)","Cutis laxa syndromes with systemic involvement","Mitochondrial hepatopathies","Tyrosinaemia type I and other metabolic liver diseases"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine polyol analysis for erythritol, arabitol and ribitol","Urine and body-fluid sugar analysis for sedoheptulose and seven-carbon sugar derivatives","Liver function tests, coagulation profile and full blood count","Abdominal imaging to assess hepatosplenomegaly and hepatic architecture","Renal and cardiac assessment for associated anomalies","TALDO1 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Neonatal liver failure with coagulopathy or bleeding","Hydrops fetalis or severe antenatal hepatosplenomegaly","Progressive hepatic fibrosis or signs suggesting hepatocellular carcinoma","Multisystem deterioration with cardiac or renal failure"],"prognosis":"The disorder is variable but often serious, with progressive liver disease, coagulopathy and multisystem involvement; some individuals survive into later childhood or adulthood while others succumb to neonatal liver failure or complications such as hepatocellular carcinoma.","pitfalls":["Overlooking transaldolase deficiency in neonatal liver failure by not requesting urinary polyol analysis","Attributing hepatosplenomegaly and cytopenias solely to congenital infection","Failing to monitor for evolving hepatic fibrosis and hepatocellular carcinoma over time","Missing associated renal, cardiac or dysmorphic features that point to a multisystem metabolic disorder"],"management":["Understand there is no enzyme-replacement or curative therapy, so management is supportive across affected organs, with antioxidant approaches such as N-acetylcysteine explored experimentally","Manage progressive liver disease by correcting coagulopathy with vitamin K and clotting-factor support, treating complications of fibrosis and cirrhosis, and considering liver transplantation for end-stage failure","Support the haematological features with transfusion for symptomatic anaemia and thrombocytopenia","Surveillance for hepatocellular carcinoma with alpha-fetoprotein and liver imaging given the recognised malignant risk","Monitor renal and cardiac function for associated anomalies and treat them symptomatically","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive TALDO1 disorder, with carrier testing and family screening","Coordinate care through metabolic medicine and clinical genetics with multidisciplinary organ-specific input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: Disorders of the Pentose Phosphate Pathway","Saudubray, Baumgartner & Walter, Inborn Metabolic Diseases: Diagnosis and Treatment"],"crossLinks":[]},"transcobalamin_deficiency":{"name":"Transcobalamin deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["transcobalamin II deficiency","TCN2 deficiency","TC deficiency","congenital transcobalamin deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews / Nathan and Oski's Haematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents in early infancy with failure to thrive, vomiting, weakness, pancytopenia or megaloblastic anaemia and an evolving encephalopathy","Autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in TCN2, encoding transcobalamin, the plasma protein that delivers vitamin B12 to tissues","A diagnostic pitfall is that total serum vitamin B12 is often normal, because most circulating B12 is bound to haptocorrin rather than transcobalamin","Functional intracellular B12 deficiency causes raised plasma homocysteine and methylmalonic acid despite normal serum B12","Requires early and generous parenteral B12 (usually hydroxocobalamin or cyanocobalamin) to overcome the transport defect and maintain intracellular cobalamin","Untreated or late-treated disease leads to irreversible neurological injury and immune dysfunction with recurrent infection","Absolute transcobalamin level or its saturation, and molecular testing, confirm the diagnosis","Distinguish from inherited intracellular cobalamin metabolism defects (cbl complementation groups), which are managed differently"],"pathophysiology":"Transcobalamin is the transport protein that binds newly absorbed vitamin B12 and delivers it to cells via receptor-mediated endocytosis. In transcobalamin deficiency, biallelic TCN2 variants abolish functional transcobalamin, so although B12 is absorbed and present in plasma bound to haptocorrin, it cannot enter tissues. The resulting intracellular cobalamin deficiency impairs the two B12-dependent reactions, methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, causing accumulation of homocysteine and methylmalonic acid. This disrupts DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing cells (producing megaloblastic anaemia and immune deficiency) and impairs myelin and neuronal metabolism, giving a progressive neurological picture. High-dose parenteral B12 raises free cobalamin sufficiently to enter cells by alternative means and reverse the deficiency.","additionalDifferentials":["Nutritional or maternal vitamin B12 deficiency","Intrinsic factor deficiency and other causes of B12 malabsorption","Inherited disorders of intracellular cobalamin metabolism (cblC, cblE, cblG and related)","Imerslund-Grasbeck syndrome","Folate metabolism disorders","Combined immunodeficiency of other cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["TCN2 molecular genetic testing","Plasma total homocysteine and methylmalonic acid (both elevated)","Serum transcobalamin level and unsaturated B12 binding capacity","Full blood count and blood film for megaloblastic/pancytopenic changes","Bone marrow examination when the blood picture is unclear","Immunoglobulin levels and immune function assessment"],"redFlags":["Progressive encephalopathy or developmental regression in infancy","Severe pancytopenia with bleeding or infection risk","Recurrent or severe infections from immune dysfunction","Failure to thrive with persistent vomiting"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and adequate parenteral B12 the haematological and immune features resolve and neurological outcome is good, but delayed treatment risks permanent neurodevelopmental damage.","pitfalls":["Excluding B12 deficiency because total serum B12 is normal","Under-dosing or spacing parenteral B12 too widely to keep intracellular cobalamin adequate","Confusing it with intracellular cobalamin metabolism disorders that need additional therapy","Missing associated immunodeficiency and its infection risk"],"management":["Lifelong high-dose parenteral vitamin B12, as hydroxocobalamin or cyanocobalamin, begun as early as possible to raise free cobalamin enough to enter cells and bypass the transport defect","Titrate treatment to biochemical response by monitoring plasma homocysteine and methylmalonic acid rather than total serum B12, which is misleadingly normal","Monitor the full blood count and neurodevelopment to confirm resolution of megaloblastic anaemia and pancytopenia and to detect neurological sequelae","Vigilant management of infections given the associated immune deficiency, with assessment of immunoglobulin levels","Ensure an uninterrupted supply and adherence to frequent parenteral dosing, intensifying the regimen if biochemical markers rise","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive TCN2 disorder, with family screening so affected siblings are treated before irreversible neurological injury"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews / OMIM entry for TCN2 (Transcobalamin Deficiency)","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"transfusion_associated_circulatory_overload":{"name":"Transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO)","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TACO","transfusion circulatory overload","volume overload transfusion reaction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / AABB technical manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as acute dyspnoea, hypoxaemia, hypertension and features of cardiogenic pulmonary oedema during or shortly after transfusion","It is essentially hydrostatic pulmonary oedema caused by an inability to accommodate the transfused volume","Raised jugular venous pressure and a positive fluid balance help distinguish it from lung injury","Natriuretic peptide levels are typically elevated, reflecting cardiac strain, in contrast to acute lung injury","Risk is greatest in the elderly, in small patients, and in those with cardiac or renal impairment","Management is to stop or slow the transfusion, sit the patient upright, give oxygen and administer diuretics","It responds to diuresis, which is the key clinical distinction from transfusion-related acute lung injury","Prevention rests on slow transfusion rates, single-unit prescribing and consideration of prophylactic diuretics in at-risk patients"],"pathophysiology":"Transfusion-associated circulatory overload results from the intravascular volume of a transfusion exceeding what the circulation can accommodate. In patients with limited cardiac reserve or impaired renal handling of fluid, the added volume raises left atrial and pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure, forcing fluid across an intact alveolar-capillary membrane into the alveoli. The oedema is therefore hydrostatic and cardiogenic in nature, accompanied by raised filling pressures and cardiac strain that elevate natriuretic peptides. Rapid infusion rates, large volumes and pre-existing cardiac or renal disease are the principal contributors, and because the mechanism is volume-related the process reverses with diuresis and reduction of the circulating load.","additionalDifferentials":["Transfusion-related acute lung injury","Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema from decompensated heart failure","Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Anaphylactic transfusion reaction","Pulmonary embolism","Pneumonia or aspiration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest imaging showing pulmonary oedema, cardiomegaly and upper-lobe blood diversion","Natriuretic peptide levels, typically raised, to support a cardiogenic mechanism","Assessment of fluid balance and jugular venous pressure","Echocardiography to evaluate ventricular function and filling pressures","Arterial blood gas or oximetry to quantify hypoxaemia","Renal function to gauge fluid-handling capacity"],"redFlags":["Acute breathlessness with hypertension and raised jugular venous pressure during transfusion","Rapidly progressive hypoxaemia in an elderly or fluid-overloaded patient","Frank pulmonary oedema requiring urgent oxygen and diuresis","Failure to improve with diuretics, prompting reconsideration of lung injury"],"prognosis":"Recognised promptly and treated with diuresis and respiratory support, most patients recover quickly, but it is a common and under-recognised cause of transfusion-related morbidity and death, particularly in frail patients with cardiac disease.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with transfusion-related acute lung injury and withholding the diuretics that would help","Transfusing at excessive rates or in multiple units where a single unit would suffice","Overlooking the elderly and small patients who are most vulnerable to volume overload","Ignoring a raised natriuretic peptide and pre-existing cardiac history when assessing dyspnoea"],"management":["Stop or slow the transfusion at once and sit the patient upright when acute dyspnoea and hypertension develop during or after a component","Give supplemental oxygen and loop diuretics to offload the hydrostatic pulmonary oedema, with nitrates as an adjunct for severe cardiogenic oedema","Escalate to non-invasive ventilation such as continuous positive airway pressure for refractory hypoxaemia","Monitor fluid balance and cardiac status, treating this as a volume-overload state that reverses with diuresis","Prevent in at-risk patients by transfusing single units slowly and spread over time, with pre-emptive diuretic cover and restrictive thresholds, especially in the elderly, small patients and those with cardiac or renal impairment","Report the reaction to the transfusion service and review future transfusion planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"transfusion_associated_graft_versus_host_disease":{"name":"Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease","system":"Haematology / Transfusion medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TA-GvHD","Transfusion-associated GvHD"],"chapter":null,"source":"AABB Technical Manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare but almost uniformly fatal complication caused by viable donor T lymphocytes engrafting and attacking recipient tissues.","Occurs when the recipient cannot reject donor lymphocytes, either through immunosuppression or when the donor is homozygous for an HLA haplotype the recipient carries, as in directed donations from relatives.","Typically presents one to six weeks after transfusion with fever, a maculopapular rash, diarrhoea, deranged liver function and progressive pancytopenia.","The defining and lethal feature is marrow aplasia, distinguishing it from graft-versus-host disease after stem-cell transplantation where the graft marrow is spared.","Gamma or X-ray irradiation of cellular components to inactivate donor lymphocytes is the mainstay of prevention; leucoreduction alone is insufficient.","At-risk groups include recipients of directed donations from first-degree relatives, HLA-matched platelets, intrauterine or neonatal exchange transfusions, and the severely immunocompromised.","Diagnosis is supported by skin biopsy and by demonstrating donor-derived lymphocytes or chimerism in the recipient.","Established disease responds poorly to immunosuppression, underscoring that prevention is essential."],"pathophysiology":"Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease results from immunocompetent donor T lymphocytes that are transfused within a cellular blood component and are not eliminated by the recipient's immune system. These viable donor lymphocytes recognise recipient HLA antigens as foreign, proliferate and mount a cytotoxic attack against host tissues bearing those antigens, principally skin, gut, liver and, critically, the bone marrow. Engraftment is favoured either by profound recipient immunosuppression or by a one-way HLA match, in which the donor is homozygous for an HLA haplotype shared with the recipient so that the recipient does not perceive the donor cells as foreign while the donor cells still attack the recipient. The consequent immune destruction of the marrow produces intractable aplasia and overwhelming infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms or other drug eruption","Viral infection such as cytomegalovirus with marrow suppression","Sepsis with multi-organ dysfunction","Chemotherapy or radiotherapy toxicity","Graft-versus-host disease after haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation","Autoimmune or paraneoplastic marrow failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing basal vacuolar change, dyskeratosis and lymphocytic infiltrate","Bone marrow examination demonstrating hypoplasia or aplasia","HLA typing and chimerism studies to detect donor lymphocytes in recipient blood or tissue","Full blood count tracking progressive pancytopenia","Liver function tests documenting cholestatic derangement","Review of transfusion history for non-irradiated cellular components in an at-risk recipient"],"redFlags":["Progressive pancytopenia developing one to six weeks after transfusion","Fever with rash, diarrhoea and deranged liver function in an at-risk recipient","Transfusion of non-irradiated components to a severely immunocompromised patient","Receipt of a directed donation from a blood relative","Marrow aplasia without an alternative explanation"],"prognosis":"The condition is almost always fatal, usually within weeks, from marrow failure with overwhelming infection and bleeding, because established disease responds poorly to treatment. This dismal outlook makes irradiation of at-risk cellular components the essential intervention.","pitfalls":["Assuming leucoreduction alone prevents the disease when irradiation is required.","Failing to identify at-risk recipients who require irradiated components.","Overlooking the danger of directed donations from first-degree relatives.","Attributing the rash, diarrhoea and cytopenia to drugs or infection and delaying diagnosis.","Expecting immunosuppressive treatment to reverse established disease."],"management":["Prevention is the essential intervention: gamma or X-ray irradiation of cellular blood components for at-risk recipients, since leucoreduction alone is insufficient","Identify and flag at-risk groups including the severely immunocompromised, recipients of directed donations from relatives, HLA-matched platelets, and intrauterine or neonatal exchange transfusions, so they always receive irradiated components","Attempt aggressive immunosuppression with corticosteroids, ciclosporin and antithymocyte globulin for established disease, recognising response is generally poor","Intensive supportive care for the resulting marrow aplasia, including transfusion support, haematopoietic growth factors and management of skin, gut and hepatic involvement","Aggressive prophylaxis and treatment of infection, as overwhelming infection with bleeding drives the near-uniform mortality","Consider haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation in rare, appropriately selected cases, involving transfusion medicine and haematology throughout"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["AABB Technical Manual, current edition","British Society for Haematology guidelines on the use of irradiated blood components","Mollison's Blood Transfusion in Clinical Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"transfusion_associated_gvhd":{"name":"Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TA-GVHD","transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AABB Technical Manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare but almost uniformly fatal complication caused by viable donor T lymphocytes engrafting and attacking recipient tissues","Typically appears one to six weeks after transfusion with fever, a maculopapular rash, diarrhoea, hepatitis, and progressive pancytopenia","Marrow aplasia distinguishes it from graft-versus-host disease after stem cell transplant, where the graft spares recipient marrow","Highest risk in severely immunocompromised recipients and when donor and recipient share an HLA haplotype, as with directed donations from blood relatives","Gamma or X-ray irradiation of cellular components to inactivate donor lymphocytes is the cornerstone of prevention; leukoreduction alone is insufficient","Diagnosis is supported by skin biopsy and by demonstrating donor-derived lymphocytes or chimerism in the recipient","Established disease responds poorly to immunosuppression, so prevention through irradiation of at-risk transfusions is paramount"],"pathophysiology":"TA-GVHD occurs when immunologically competent donor T lymphocytes contained in a cellular blood component are not eliminated by the recipient's immune system and instead engraft, proliferate, and mount an immune attack against host tissues bearing foreign HLA antigens. This happens when the recipient is profoundly immunosuppressed or when the donor is homozygous for an HLA haplotype shared with the recipient, so the recipient fails to recognise the donor cells as foreign. The activated donor lymphocytes target skin, gut, liver, and bone marrow, with marrow involvement producing the aplasia that drives the high mortality.","additionalDifferentials":["Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS) or other drug eruptions","Viral infection with rash and cytopenias","Marrow failure or aplastic anaemia from other causes","Post-transplant graft-versus-host disease","Sepsis with multi-organ dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing characteristic histology of graft-versus-host disease","Full blood count documenting progressive pancytopenia","Liver function tests reflecting hepatitis","HLA typing and detection of donor lymphocyte chimerism in blood or affected tissue","Bone marrow examination showing hypoplasia or aplasia"],"redFlags":["Fever, rash, diarrhoea, and falling blood counts within weeks of transfusion in an at-risk patient","Progressive pancytopenia signalling marrow aplasia","Any cellular transfusion given without irradiation to a severely immunocompromised recipient or from a first-degree relative","Rapid multi-organ deterioration requiring urgent haematology involvement"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is extremely poor once established, with mortality generally exceeding ninety per cent largely from marrow aplasia and infection; the emphasis therefore lies entirely on prevention by irradiating cellular components for at-risk patients.","pitfalls":["Failing to irradiate cellular components for immunocompromised recipients or directed family donations","Assuming leukoreduction alone prevents the disease","Attributing the rash, diarrhoea, and cytopenias to drugs or infection and delaying recognition","Not flagging patients who require lifelong irradiated components in their transfusion records"],"management":["Focus care on prevention by irradiating cellular components with gamma or X-ray to inactivate donor T lymphocytes, as leukoreduction alone does not prevent it","Ensure severely immunocompromised recipients and those receiving HLA-matched or blood-relative directed donations are reliably identified to receive irradiated components","Treat established disease with intensive immunosuppression such as corticosteroids and ciclosporin, accepting that established disease responds poorly","Provide supportive care for marrow aplasia and pancytopenia, including transfusion support and haematopoietic growth factors","Prevent and treat infection and bleeding vigorously, the usual causes of death","Manage jointly with haematology and transfusion medicine, reviewing the transfusion history to ensure at-risk recipients receive irradiated blood in future"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"transfusion_hyperkalaemia":{"name":"Transfusion-associated hyperkalaemia","system":"Haematology / Transfusion medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Transfusion hyperkalaemia","Hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest of transfusion"],"chapter":null,"source":"AABB Technical Manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Potassium leaks from stored red cells into the supernatant over time, so older units carry a higher extracellular potassium load.","Clinically important hyperkalaemia is most likely with rapid, large-volume transfusion through central lines, and in neonatal and paediatric large-volume or exchange transfusion.","Irradiation of red cells accelerates potassium leakage, so irradiated units are best transfused promptly and within a shortened shelf life.","The feared outcome is transfusion-associated hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest, particularly in small infants during rapid infusion.","Risk is compounded by renal impairment, acidosis, hypocalcaemia and hypothermia which impair potassium handling and cardiac stability.","Electrocardiographic changes progress from peaked T waves to widened QRS and sine-wave patterns before arrest.","Preventive measures include using fresher or washed red cells for at-risk recipients and controlling infusion rate and line position.","Emergency management follows standard hyperkalaemia treatment with cardiac membrane stabilisation and measures to shift and remove potassium."],"pathophysiology":"During refrigerated storage, red cells progressively lose intracellular potassium because the energy-dependent sodium-potassium membrane pump is inactive at cold temperatures, so potassium accumulates in the unit's supernatant, increasing with storage age and after irradiation, which damages the membrane. When such units are transfused slowly the load is trivial, but rapid infusion of large volumes, especially directly into the central circulation, can deliver a potassium bolus faster than it can be redistributed or excreted. Neonates and infants are especially vulnerable because of their small blood volume and the relatively large transfused volumes used in exchange and massive transfusion. Superimposed renal impairment, acidosis, hypocalcaemia and hypothermia reduce potassium tolerance and myocardial stability, and the resulting acute hyperkalaemia can cause conduction disturbance and cardiac arrest.","additionalDifferentials":["Hyperkalaemia from renal failure","Hyperkalaemia from tissue injury, rhabdomyolysis or tumour lysis","Citrate toxicity causing hypocalcaemic arrhythmia","Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction releasing intracellular potassium","Acidosis-driven potassium shift","Drug-induced hyperkalaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum or point-of-care potassium during rapid or large-volume transfusion","Electrocardiogram for peaked T waves and QRS widening","Ionised calcium given the interplay with citrate","Arterial blood gas for acidosis and potassium","Renal function tests","Review of red-cell storage age and irradiation status in at-risk recipients"],"redFlags":["Peaked T waves or widening QRS during transfusion","Bradycardia or cardiac arrest during rapid transfusion in an infant","Rapid central infusion of old or irradiated red cells","Hyperkalaemia in a recipient with renal impairment or acidosis","Large-volume or exchange transfusion in a neonate"],"prognosis":"When anticipated and prevented by choice of fresher or washed cells and controlled infusion, the risk is low; however, transfusion-associated hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest, though rare, has a high mortality, particularly in infants. Prompt recognition and standard hyperkalaemia management are life-saving.","pitfalls":["Using the oldest or freshly irradiated units for rapid large-volume paediatric transfusion.","Assuming a normal baseline potassium removes the risk during rapid infusion.","Overlooking the additive risks of acidosis, hypocalcaemia and hypothermia.","Failing to monitor the electrocardiogram and potassium during high-risk transfusion.","Confusing hyperkalaemic changes with citrate-induced hypocalcaemic arrhythmia."],"management":["Prevention through component choice: transfuse fresher, washed or volume-reduced red cells for neonatal, exchange and massive transfusion, and use irradiated units promptly within a shortened shelf life","Control delivery by avoiding rapid, large-volume boluses through central lines and using slower controlled infusion in vulnerable patients","Monitor potassium, ionised calcium and the electrocardiogram during rapid or large-volume transfusion, watching for peaked T waves and QRS widening","Treat established hyperkalaemia urgently with calcium salts for myocardial stabilisation, then insulin-dextrose and nebulised salbutamol to shift potassium intracellularly","Correct compounding factors of acidosis, hypocalcaemia and hypothermia that reduce potassium tolerance and cardiac stability","Be prepared to manage transfusion-associated hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest, especially in small infants during rapid infusion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["AABB Technical Manual, current edition","British Society for Haematology guidelines on transfusion for neonates and older children","Mollison's Blood Transfusion in Clinical Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"transfusion_reactions":{"name":"Transfusion Reactions","system":"Hematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hemolytic transfusion reaction","TRALI","TACO"],"chapter":118,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.901","clinicalPearls":["Acute immune-mediated haemolytic reactions occur when recipient preformed antibodies lyse transfused donor RBCs, during or up to 24 h after transfusion; anti-A/anti-B antibodies cause most of the most severe (potentially fatal) reactions, though alloantibodies against Rh, Kell, and Duffy antigens can also cause severe haemolysis (Harrison 22e p.906).","The most dramatic acute haemolytic reactions are usually caused by a failure in product or patient identification, erroneous blood grouping, or unidentified recipient anti-RBC alloimmunisation - i.e. a clerical/identification error (Harrison 22e p.906).","Acute haemolytic reactions may present with hypotension, tachypnoea, tachycardia, fever (rise of 1-2 degrees C), chills, chest and back/flank pain, haemoglobinuria, and haemoglobinemia; in the most severe cases DIC, acute renal failure, shock, and death may occur (Harrison 22e p.906).","RED FLAG: when an adverse reaction is suspected the transfusion must be STOPPED, the recipient's clinical status assessed, and supportive care initiated; a thorough clerical check is crucial to prevent a possible second misidentified transfusion (Harrison 22e p.908).","Management of acute immune haemolytic reaction is mainly supportive: prompt interruption of transfusion, biological workup, vigorous isotonic saline hydration with diuretics to maintain urine output; may require forced alkaline diuresis, electrolyte correction, and pressor support, and in DIC with bleeding, platelet/plasma/cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen (Harrison 22e p.908).","Diagnosis of transfusion-associated haemolysis relies on persistent/worsening anaemia, depleted haptoglobin, haemoglobinemia and haemoglobinuria, and elevated LDH and unconjugated bilirubin; the direct antiglobulin test (DAT/direct Coombs) is most often positive, as is the indirect antiglobulin test (IAT) (Harrison 22e p.908).","Delayed haemolytic reactions present with icterus and persisting or worsening anaemia and result from an anamnestic response in previously sensitised patients who had a negative alloantibody screen at transfusion (low antibody levels); the alloantibody becomes detectable 1-2 weeks after transfusion (Harrison 22e p.906).","FNHTR (febrile non-haemolytic transfusion reaction) is the most frequent reaction to cellular components (up to 300 per 100,000), characterised by chills, rigors and a rise of at least 1 degree C, caused by proinflammatory cytokines in the component or recipient antibodies against donor antigens; it is a diagnosis of exclusion once infection and haemolysis are ruled out (Harrison 22e p.911).","Leukocyte reduction (especially prestorage) can prevent FNHTR; premedication with antipyretics has generally proven ineffective and may mask relevant clinical symptoms (Harrison 22e p.911).","Most allergic transfusion reactions are mild (rash, pruritus, urticaria, localised oedema) and relate to plasma proteins; rarely they are severe/anaphylactic with bronchospasm, respiratory distress, hypotension, nausea, vomiting and shock - anaphylaxis can occur after only a few millilitres of component (Harrison 22e p.911).","TRALI is characterised by occurrence or worsening of hypoxia and NONcardiogenic pulmonary oedema with bilateral interstitial infiltrates on chest x-ray during or within 6 h of transfusion (delayed cases up to 72 h); it is among the most common causes of transfusion-related fatalities and treatment is supportive only (Harrison 22e p.911).","TRALI usually results from donor plasma containing high-titre anti-HLA class II antibodies binding recipient cognate antigen (anti-HLA class I and anti-HNA antibodies may also be involved); a cytokine/chemokine-mediated mechanism without HLA interaction can also occur (Harrison 22e p.911).","TACO (transfusion-associated circulatory overload) is common and underrecognised and is now the main cause of transfusion-related death since TRALI risk was mitigated; it presents with dyspnoea, hypoxia, bilateral and predominantly ALVEOLAR infiltrates on chest x-ray, frequent systolic hypertension, and elevated BNP (fever may also occur) (Harrison 22e p.912).","TRALI vs TACO distinction: TRALI gives noncardiogenic oedema with predominantly interstitial infiltrates within 6 h, whereas TACO is volume overload with predominantly alveolar infiltrates (within 12 h), systolic hypertension and elevated BNP; TACO risk factors include older age, renal failure, preexisting fluid overload, cardiac dysfunction, large transfusion volume and excessive transfusion rate (Harrison 22e p.911-912).","Transfusion-related GVHD is extremely rare but fatal in over 90% of cases; mediated by engrafted donor T lymphocytes in a recipient unable to reject them, it presents 5-10 days post-transfusion with cytopenia, fever, characteristic skin rash, diarrhoea and liver function abnormalities, and is prevented by irradiation (minimum 25 Gy) of cellular components in at-risk patients (Harrison 22e p.911).","Transfusion-transmitted bacterial infection remains a significant concern, notably with platelet concentrates stored at room temperature; some gram-negatives such as Yersinia grow at 4 degrees C and can contaminate RBC units - recipients may develop abrupt fever and chills deteriorating to septic shock, DIC and death, and after culture sampling broad-spectrum antibiotics should be started promptly (Harrison 22e p.913).","Iron overload: each RBC concentrate contains 200-250 mg of iron; in frequently transfused recipients untreated accumulation impairs endocrine, hepatic and cardiac function (death may occur from cardiac failure or arrhythmia), assessed by serum ferritin, MRI and liver biopsy, and prevented/treated by monitoring and iron chelation (Harrison 22e p.912).","Acute hypotensive transfusion reactions are an abrupt drop in systolic blood pressure of more than 30 mmHg early after starting transfusion that resolves once transfusion is stopped; they are more likely in patients taking ACE inhibitors (impaired bradykinin metabolism) and the same product should NOT be restarted (Harrison 22e p.912)."],"pathophysiology":"Adverse reactions to blood components arise via immune or nonimmune mechanisms. Immune-mediated acute haemolysis occurs when recipient preformed antibodies (anti-A/anti-B IgM/IgG, or alloantibodies to Rh, Kell, Duffy) lyse transfused donor RBCs, with complement activation; ABO incompatibility classically results from clerical/identification error. Delayed haemolysis reflects an anamnestic alloantibody response (neoformed allogeneic IgG, C3b-mediated opsonisation and ADCC-mediated phagocytosis) detectable 1-2 weeks later. FNHTR is driven by proinflammatory cytokines accumulated in the component or recipient antibodies against donor leukocyte antigens. Allergic/anaphylactic reactions relate to plasma proteins and soluble mediators triggering histamine/serotonin release (IgA-deficient recipients may react to IgA). TRALI results from donor anti-HLA (class II, also class I) or anti-HNA antibodies binding recipient antigen, or cytokine/chemokine priming, causing neutrophil aggregation in pulmonary vasculature and release of inflammatory mediators producing noncardiogenic pulmonary oedema. TACO is hydrostatic circulatory overload from excessive volume/rate relative to cardiac/renal reserve. Transfusion-associated GVHD is mediated by engrafted donor T lymphocytes attacking host HLA antigens. (Harrison 22e p.906-912)","additionalDifferentials":["Febrile non-haemolytic transfusion reaction (diagnosis of exclusion once infection and haemolysis excluded)","Acute immune-mediated haemolytic transfusion reaction (ABO/clerical error)","Delayed haemolytic transfusion reaction (anamnestic alloantibody response)","Hyperhaemolytic transfusion reaction in haemoglobinopathy/sickle cell disease (bystander haemolysis)","Allergic / anaphylactic transfusion reaction","TRALI (transfusion-related acute lung injury)","TACO (transfusion-associated circulatory overload)","Transfusion-transmitted bacterial infection / septic transfusion reaction","Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease","Posttransfusion purpura","Acute hypotensive transfusion reaction (bradykinin-related, ACE inhibitor)"],"infectionMimics":["Transfusion-transmitted bacterial contamination/sepsis presenting with abrupt fever, chills, septic shock and DIC can mimic an acute haemolytic reaction (fever over 2 degrees C is a warning sign favouring bacterial contamination/haemolysis)","Septic shock from a contaminated component can mimic anaphylactic or haemolytic shock"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["TRALI may be difficult to distinguish from other causes of hypoxia, particularly circulatory overload (TACO)","G6PD/enzymatic RBC defects and underlying haemolytic conditions exacerbated by transfusion can mimic immune-mediated haemolytic transfusion reaction","Hyperhaemolysis in sickle cell disease can mimic and be exacerbated by transfusion"],"additionalInvestigations":["Direct antiglobulin test (DAT / direct Coombs) detecting immunoglobulin and possibly complement (C3d) on recipient RBCs","Indirect antiglobulin test (IAT / indirect Coombs) detecting anti-RBC alloantibody in serum, with antibody elution to identify the culprit alloantibody","Plasma haptoglobin (depleted), LDH and unconjugated bilirubin (elevated), haemoglobinemia and haemoglobinuria","Repeat ABO determination and thorough clerical check; alloantibody screen and crossmatch","Bacterial culture of the component and recipient before starting broad-spectrum antibiotics when bacterial contamination suspected","Chest x-ray (bilateral interstitial infiltrates in TRALI; predominantly alveolar infiltrates in TACO) and brain natriuretic peptide (elevated in TACO)","Serum ferritin, MRI and liver biopsy to assess iron overload in frequently transfused patients","Test for IgA deficiency in patients with anaphylactic or repeated allergic reactions"],"redFlags":["STOP the transfusion immediately when any adverse reaction is suspected","Fever, flank/back pain and haemoglobinuria suggesting acute haemolytic (ABO-incompatible) reaction - perform an urgent clerical/identification check to prevent a second misidentified transfusion","Hypotension (systolic drop of at least 30 mmHg) - haemolytic, anaphylactic or septic shock","Dyspnoea within 6 h (TRALI) or within 12 h (TACO) of transfusion","Temperature rise over 2 degrees C - favours transfusion-transmitted bacterial infection or haemolysis","Rash over two-thirds of body within 5 minutes with dyspnoea and shock - anaphylaxis","Development of DIC, acute renal failure or shock during/after transfusion"],"prognosis":"Most adverse reactions are non-life-threatening, though serious reactions can present with mild symptoms. An average of ~37 transfusion-associated fatalities (possible-to-definite imputability) were reported yearly to the FDA between 2017-2021 among ~15 million transfused components, with leading causes of death being TACO (32%), TRALI (21%), haemolysis (21%) and microbial contamination (13%). Acute haemolysis is often self-limiting but can be fatal (DIC, acute renal failure, shock). Transfusion-associated GVHD is fatal in over 90% of cases and highly resistant to treatment. (Harrison 22e p.906-911)","pitfalls":["Premedication with antipyretics for FNHTR is generally ineffective and may mask relevant clinical warning symptoms","FNHTR must not be diagnosed until infection and haemolysis are excluded","Failing to perform a clerical/identification check risks a second misidentified (incompatible) transfusion","TRALI and TACO are easily confused; both cause hypoxia and bilateral infiltrates - TACO is now the leading cause of transfusion death and is underrecognised","In acute hypotensive reactions the same blood product should not be restarted; consider switching ACE inhibitor for patients needing further transfusion","Delayed haemolytic reactions occur despite a negative pretransfusion alloantibody screen (low antibody levels) - icterus and worsening anaemia 1-2 weeks later","Iron overload is frequently underreported in chronically transfused patients","Further RBC transfusion in hyperhaemolytic reaction (sickle cell disease) typically exacerbates ongoing haemolysis"],"management":["Stop the transfusion immediately at any suspected reaction, maintain venous access, and perform a rigorous clerical and identity check to prevent a second misidentified transfusion","For acute haemolytic reactions give vigorous isotonic saline to maintain urine output with diuretics as needed, support blood pressure with vasopressors, and correct electrolytes","Manage haemolysis-associated disseminated intravascular coagulation with platelets, plasma, cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen when there is bleeding","Treat febrile non-haemolytic reactions with antipyretics after excluding haemolysis, and allergic reactions with antihistamines, escalating to adrenaline and corticosteroids for anaphylaxis","When bacterial contamination is suspected, culture the component and recipient and start broad-spectrum antibiotics","Send diagnostic samples including direct and indirect antiglobulin tests, repeat ABO grouping and crossmatch, and report to the transfusion service","Prevent recurrence with appropriate component selection using leucoreduced units, washed or IgA-deficient components for IgA-deficient recipients, and premedication where indicated"],"severityClassification":"Warning-sign based recognition of transfusion adverse reactions (Harrison Table 118-5): fever of at least 38 degrees C (rise of 1-2 degrees C within 4 h favours FNHTR or TRALI; rise over 2 degrees C favours bacterial infection/haemolysis); hypotension (systolic drop of at least 30 mmHg) suggesting haemolytic, anaphylactic or septic shock; dyspnoea (TRALI within 6 h, TACO within 12 h, severe allergy within 4 h); haemoglobinuria (intravascular haemolysis); rash (minor allergy if under two-thirds body, severe if over two-thirds, anaphylaxis if with dyspnoea/shock within 5 min); icterus (delayed haemolysis); rash/diarrhoea/fever 2 days-6 weeks (GVHD); gum bleeding/purpura 5-12 days (posttransfusion purpura). Reactions are also categorised as immune-mediated vs nonimmunologic, and by timing (acute vs delayed). (Harrison 22e p.908)","references":["Harrison 22e p.901","Harrison 22e p.906","Harrison 22e p.908","Harrison 22e p.911","Harrison 22e p.912","Harrison 22e p.913","Delaney M et al: Transfusion reactions: Prevention, diagnosis, and treatment","Panch SR et al: Hemolytic transfusion reactions. N Engl J Med"],"crossLinks":["anaphylaxis","hemolytic_anemia","ards"]},"transfusion_related_acute_lung_injury":{"name":"Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI)","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TRALI","transfusion-related lung injury","antibody-mediated transfusion lung injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / AABB technical manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as acute hypoxaemia and bilateral pulmonary infiltrates developing within about six hours of transfusion","It is a form of non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, so filling pressures are normal and there is no evidence of volume overload","Often accompanied by fever, hypotension and, in severe cases, transient reduction in the circulating white-cell count","Classically attributed to donor antibodies against recipient leucocyte antigens, with a two-hit model in which patient predisposition primes the pulmonary endothelium","Plasma-rich components carry the greatest risk, and preferential use of male donor plasma has reduced incidence","Management is supportive with oxygen and respiratory support, escalating to non-invasive or invasive ventilation as required","Distinguishing it from circulatory overload is essential because diuresis is the treatment for overload but not for this injury","Report suspected cases to the blood service so implicated donors can be investigated and deferred"],"pathophysiology":"Transfusion-related acute lung injury is an acute inflammatory injury of the pulmonary capillary endothelium. In the antibody-mediated form, donor anti-leucocyte antibodies bind recipient neutrophil or human leucocyte antigens, activating neutrophils that are already sequestered in the pulmonary microvasculature. A widely used two-hit model holds that an initial insult such as sepsis or surgery primes and sequesters neutrophils, after which the transfused antibody or accumulated biological response modifiers provides the second hit. The resulting neutrophil activation damages the endothelium, increasing permeability so that protein-rich fluid floods the alveoli. Because the mechanism is inflammatory rather than hydrostatic, cardiac filling pressures remain normal.","additionalDifferentials":["Transfusion-associated circulatory overload","Acute respiratory distress syndrome from another cause","Anaphylactic transfusion reaction with bronchospasm","Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema","Bacterial contamination with septic reaction and shock","Aspiration pneumonitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest imaging demonstrating bilateral infiltrates without cardiomegaly or upper-lobe diversion","Arterial blood gas or pulse oximetry to quantify hypoxaemia","Natriuretic peptide levels, typically not markedly raised, to help distinguish from circulatory overload","Echocardiography or assessment of filling pressures to confirm normal cardiac function","Full blood count, which may show transient neutropenia","Testing of donor and recipient for leucocyte antibodies in conjunction with the blood service"],"redFlags":["Rapidly worsening hypoxaemia within hours of transfusion requiring ventilatory support","Bilateral pulmonary infiltrates with hypotension rather than hypertension","Failure to improve with diuresis, arguing against circulatory overload","Progressive respiratory failure despite high-flow oxygen"],"prognosis":"Most patients improve within a few days with supportive respiratory care and the pulmonary infiltrates resolve, but severe cases requiring mechanical ventilation carry appreciable mortality and it remains a leading cause of transfusion-related death.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the picture as circulatory overload and treating with diuretics that do not help","Attributing the hypoxaemia solely to the underlying illness and missing the transfusion trigger","Failing to report the reaction, so implicated donors are not investigated","Assuming a normal natriuretic peptide excludes any transfusion reaction"],"management":["Stop the transfusion immediately and alert the transfusion service when hypoxaemia and bilateral infiltrates arise within hours of a component","Provide supportive respiratory care with supplemental oxygen escalating to non-invasive or lung-protective invasive ventilation as hypoxaemia dictates","Support hypotension with fluids and vasopressors, recognising the picture is inflammatory capillary leak with normal filling pressures","Avoid diuretic-driven fluid removal, which does not address the permeability oedema","Investigate implicated donors with the blood service for anti-leucocyte antibodies and defer them from future donation","Reduce risk through preferential use of male-donor plasma and restrictive transfusion practice"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"transfusion_related_immunomodulation":{"name":"Transfusion-related immunomodulation","system":"Haematology / Transfusion medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TRIM","Transfusion-associated immunomodulation"],"chapter":null,"source":"AABB Technical Manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Refers to the immunosuppressive and pro-inflammatory effects of allogeneic transfusion rather than an acute clinical reaction.","First recognised through improved renal allograft survival in patients transfused before transplantation, an observation that led to the concept.","Proposed adverse associations include increased postoperative infection and possibly cancer recurrence, though causation remains debated and confounded by the reasons for transfusion.","Donor leucocytes and their soluble products are considered central mediators, providing the rationale for universal leucoreduction.","Effects are thought to involve shifts in the balance of regulatory and effector T cells, altered cytokine profiles and suppression of natural-killer-cell and macrophage function.","Older or non-leucoreduced red cells and the storage lesion may amplify the immunomodulatory effect.","The strongest practical implication is support for restrictive transfusion thresholds and blood conservation strategies.","It is a chronic, epidemiological phenomenon and should not be confused with acute transfusion reactions."],"pathophysiology":"Transfusion-related immunomodulation describes durable alterations in recipient immunity following exposure to allogeneic blood. Several mechanisms are proposed and probably act together. Transfused donor leucocytes present alloantigen and, in the absence of appropriate costimulation, may induce clonal deletion or anergy and expand regulatory T-cell populations, biasing the recipient toward tolerance and immunosuppression. Soluble mediators accumulating during storage, including cytokines, soluble HLA molecules and bioactive lipids, further modulate antigen-presenting-cell and natural-killer-cell function and shift the cytokine balance away from a T-helper-1 pattern. The net effect is downregulation of cell-mediated immunity, which may explain both the beneficial tolerance seen in transplantation and the postulated harms of increased infection and tumour recurrence.","additionalDifferentials":["Postoperative infection from surgical or patient-related factors","Immunosuppression from underlying malignancy or its treatment","Confounding by indication where sicker patients receive more blood","Nutritional or metabolic causes of impaired immunity","Hospital-acquired infection unrelated to transfusion","Cancer recurrence driven by tumour biology rather than transfusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Review of transfusion exposure including number of units and leucoreduction status","Assessment of cytokine and lymphocyte-subset changes in research settings","Natural-killer-cell activity assays in study contexts","Audit of postoperative infection rates against transfusion exposure","Correlation with red-cell storage age where data are available","Multivariable analysis to adjust for confounding by indication"],"redFlags":["Rising postoperative infection rates linked to liberal transfusion practice","Use of non-leucoreduced components where leucoreduction is standard","Unnecessary transfusion above evidence-based thresholds","Failure to apply blood conservation in surgery with high transfusion demand","Repeated allogeneic exposure in patients with malignancy where recurrence is a concern"],"prognosis":"As a chronic epidemiological effect rather than an acute event, the clinical impact varies and remains contested; the pragmatic response is to minimise unnecessary allogeneic exposure through restrictive thresholds and leucoreduction. Definitive causal attribution of individual outcomes is generally not possible.","pitfalls":["Treating transfusion-related immunomodulation as an acute reaction with immediate symptoms.","Overstating causation given substantial confounding by indication.","Ignoring the case for restrictive transfusion thresholds and blood conservation.","Assuming leucoreduction abolishes all immunomodulatory effects.","Neglecting other explanations for postoperative infection or cancer recurrence."],"management":["Minimise unnecessary allogeneic exposure through restrictive transfusion thresholds and single-unit transfusion, the principal pragmatic response to a contested chronic effect","Universal leucoreduction of cellular components, since donor leucocytes and their soluble products are considered central mediators","Apply patient blood management by correcting anaemia with iron and erythropoiesis-stimulating agents, using cell salvage and autologous transfusion where appropriate, and giving tranexamic acid to reduce surgical blood loss","Prefer fresher components and consider washed products in selected high-exposure settings","Audit postoperative infection and outcome data against transfusion exposure to inform local practice","Weigh transfusion decisions against the underlying indication, avoiding attribution of individual outcomes to immunomodulation alone"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["AABB Technical Manual, current edition","British Society for Haematology guidelines on red cell transfusion","Mollison's Blood Transfusion in Clinical Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"transfusional_iron_overload":{"name":"Transfusional iron overload","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["transfusional haemosiderosis","secondary haemochromatosis","transfusion iron overload"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / AABB technical manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Develops in patients dependent on long-term red-cell transfusion, since the body has no effective route to excrete the iron delivered by each unit","Common in thalassaemia major, sickle cell disease, myelodysplastic syndromes and other chronic transfusion-dependent anaemias","Iron accumulates first in the reticuloendothelial system and then in parenchymal organs, causing a secondary haemochromatosis","Target organs are the heart, liver and endocrine glands, producing cardiomyopathy, cirrhosis, diabetes and hypogonadism","Serum ferritin is used to monitor iron burden, and cardiac and hepatic magnetic resonance imaging quantifies tissue iron more directly","Management is iron chelation therapy, with the aim of keeping iron burden below the threshold for organ damage","Cardiac iron loading is the leading cause of death, so cardiac monitoring is central to surveillance"],"pathophysiology":"Each unit of transfused red cells delivers a substantial quantity of iron, and because humans lack a regulated pathway for iron excretion, repeated transfusion inexorably expands total body iron. Once transferrin becomes saturated, non-transferrin-bound iron appears in plasma and is taken up by parenchymal cells, where it catalyses the generation of reactive oxygen species. This oxidative injury damages the myocardium, hepatocytes and endocrine tissue, leading over time to cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia, hepatic fibrosis and cirrhosis, and endocrine failure including diabetes and hypogonadism. Coexisting ineffective erythropoiesis in some underlying disorders suppresses hepcidin and further increases dietary iron absorption, compounding the transfusional load.","additionalDifferentials":["Hereditary haemochromatosis","Alcoholic or non-alcoholic fatty liver disease","Other causes of dilated or restrictive cardiomyopathy","Endocrine failure from unrelated causes","Porphyria cutanea tarda with iron loading","Chronic liver disease from viral hepatitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ferritin trend to estimate iron burden over time","Transferrin saturation, characteristically high in iron overload","Cardiac magnetic resonance to quantify myocardial iron","Hepatic magnetic resonance or, less commonly, biopsy to assess liver iron concentration","Liver function tests and assessment for fibrosis","Endocrine screening including glucose, thyroid and gonadal function"],"redFlags":["New heart failure or arrhythmia suggesting cardiac iron loading","Rising ferritin despite chelation indicating inadequate iron control","Deranged liver function or signs of cirrhosis","Emerging diabetes or hypogonadism from endocrine iron deposition"],"prognosis":"With effective chelation and monitoring, organ damage can be prevented or partly reversed and survival is greatly improved, but poor adherence or late intervention leads to progressive cardiac and hepatic disease and premature death.","pitfalls":["Delaying chelation until organ damage is already established","Relying on ferritin alone without imaging to assess cardiac and hepatic iron","Underestimating cardiac risk, which is the principal cause of mortality","Poor adherence to chelation going unrecognised during follow-up"],"management":["Iron chelation is the mainstay, using deferoxamine, deferasirox or deferiprone to remove accumulated iron and keep the burden below the threshold for organ damage","Intensified or combination chelation for high cardiac iron loading or established iron cardiomyopathy","Monitor iron burden with serial serum ferritin, transferrin saturation, and cardiac and hepatic magnetic resonance to guide chelation intensity","Screen for and treat endocrine complications, giving insulin or other therapy for diabetes, sex-hormone replacement for hypogonadism, and thyroid and parathyroid replacement as needed","Manage iron cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia with cardiology input alongside aggressive chelation","Reduce the transfusion burden where feasible by optimising treatment of the underlying anaemia","Support long-term adherence to chelation, the key determinant of preventing progressive cardiac and hepatic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"transient_hypogammaglobulinaemia_of_infancy":{"name":"Transient Hypogammaglobulinaemia of Infancy","system":"Immunology / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["THI","Transient hypogammaglobulinemia of infancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A self-limiting delay in endogenous immunoglobulin production that prolongs and exaggerates the physiological nadir of IgG seen in infancy, with eventual spontaneous recovery.","Defined by low IgG (often with low IgA) in an infant who has normal B-cell numbers and, importantly, retains the ability to make specific antibody to vaccines.","Most children are healthy or have only mild recurrent upper respiratory and ear infections; some present with atopic features such as eczema or wheeze.","It is fundamentally a diagnosis of exclusion and can only be confirmed retrospectively once immunoglobulin levels normalise, typically within the first few years of life.","Preserved specific antibody responses (for example to tetanus or conjugate vaccines) are the key feature separating it from true antibody-deficiency syndromes.","B-cell numbers and T-cell function are normal, distinguishing it from agammaglobulinaemia and combined immunodeficiencies.","Routine immunoglobulin replacement is usually unnecessary; management is watchful monitoring with prompt treatment of infections and, occasionally, antibiotic prophylaxis.","Persistent hypogammaglobulinaemia beyond early childhood or failure of specific antibody responses should prompt re-evaluation for common variable immunodeficiency or another primary immunodeficiency."],"pathophysiology":"Transient hypogammaglobulinaemia of infancy represents a maturational lag rather than an intrinsic structural defect of the immune system. All infants experience a physiological trough in serum IgG as transplacentally acquired maternal IgG is catabolised over the first months of life before endogenous synthesis takes over. In affected infants, the onset of adequate independent IgG (and often IgA) production is delayed, deepening and prolonging this nadir. B lymphocytes are present in normal numbers and are intrinsically capable of maturing into antibody-secreting plasma cells, and the capacity to mount protective specific antibody responses is generally retained, which is why most children remain well or only mildly symptomatic. The mechanism is incompletely understood but is thought to involve delayed maturation of T-cell help, transient dysregulation of cytokines supporting immunoglobulin class-switching, or slow expansion of the memory B-cell compartment. Because the underlying machinery is intact, immunoglobulin concentrations rise into the normal range as the child grows, distinguishing the condition from the fixed defects of agammaglobulinaemia and from the progressive humoral failure of common variable immunodeficiency, with which it can initially be confused.","additionalDifferentials":["Common variable immunodeficiency","X-linked or autosomal recessive agammaglobulinaemia","Selective IgA deficiency","Specific antibody deficiency with normal immunoglobulins","Secondary hypogammaglobulinaemia (protein loss, drugs)","Physiological immunoglobulin nadir of normal infancy","Combined immunodeficiency with humoral component"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial serum immunoglobulins (IgG, IgA, IgM) interpreted against age-matched ranges","Specific antibody titres to protein and polysaccharide-conjugate vaccines","Lymphocyte subsets confirming normal B-cell numbers","Isohaemagglutinin titres as a measure of IgM antibody function","Response to a booster immunisation where specific titres are borderline","Exclusion of protein-losing enteropathy or nephrotic-range protein loss","Longitudinal follow-up to document spontaneous normalisation"],"redFlags":["Severe, invasive or opportunistic infection out of keeping with a benign transient process","Failure to thrive or chronic diarrhoea suggesting a more serious immunodeficiency","Absent specific antibody responses despite adequate immunisation","Persistently low immunoglobulins continuing well beyond early childhood","Very low or absent circulating B cells on immunophenotyping"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent, with spontaneous resolution and normalisation of immunoglobulin levels in the majority during early childhood. A minority who fail to recover or lose specific antibody responses may ultimately be reclassified as having a persistent primary immunodeficiency.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing it prematurely and definitively when only prolonged follow-up can confirm resolution","Starting unnecessary immunoglobulin replacement in a child with preserved specific antibody responses","Failing to reassess a child whose hypogammaglobulinaemia persists, thereby missing evolving common variable immunodeficiency","Interpreting low immunoglobulins without checking functional specific antibody titres","Overlooking secondary causes such as protein loss before labelling the process transient"],"management":["Manage most infants expectantly, as this is a self-limiting maturational lag that resolves spontaneously, so immunoglobulin replacement is rarely required.","Treat intercurrent bacterial infections, particularly recurrent otitis media and respiratory infections, promptly with appropriate antibiotics.","Continue the routine immunisation schedule, including protein and conjugate-polysaccharide vaccines, and use the resulting specific antibody responses to confirm intact humoral function.","Monitor with serial age-matched immunoglobulins (IgG, IgA, IgM) and specific vaccine antibody titres until values normalise, confirming the diagnosis retrospectively.","For troublesome recurrent infections, consider prophylactic antibiotics as an interim measure while awaiting spontaneous recovery.","Maintain immunology follow-up and reserve immunoglobulin replacement for the uncommon child with severe or recurrent infection and poor specific antibody responses.","Escalate and reclassify as a persistent primary immunodeficiency if immunoglobulin levels fail to normalise or specific antibody responses are lost over time."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical immunology textbook","Paediatric immunology reference","International primary immunodeficiency classification guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"transient_neonatal_diabetes_mellitus":{"name":"Transient neonatal diabetes mellitus","system":"Endocrine / Monogenic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TNDM","6q24-related neonatal diabetes","Transient neonatal diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Monogenic diabetes references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Insulin-requiring diabetes appears in early infancy but remits, usually within the first months of life, distinguishing it clinically from the permanent forms.","The commonest cause is overexpression of the imprinted 6q24 locus (PLAGL1/ZAC and HYMAI), through paternal uniparental disomy, paternal duplication, or loss of maternal methylation.","Remission is not cure: a substantial proportion relapse with diabetes later in childhood or around puberty, so lifelong follow-up is essential.","Macroglossia and umbilical hernia are recognised clues in 6q24-related disease and help separate it from other neonatal diabetes.","Potassium-channel mutations (KCNJ11, ABCC8) can also produce a transient course, and these cases may respond to sulfonylureas at presentation and relapse.","Marked intrauterine growth restriction reflects deficient foetal insulin, mirroring the permanent forms.","A generalised imprinting disturbance (for example ZFP57-related) can accompany maternal hypomethylation and cause additional features."],"pathophysiology":"Transient neonatal diabetes is dominated by disturbance of an imprinted region on chromosome 6q24 that normally shows monoallelic paternal expression. Excess dosage of the transcription factor PLAGL1 (ZAC) together with HYMAI, arising from paternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 6, paternal duplication of the 6q24 segment, or loss of methylation on the maternal allele, impairs the developmental regulation of insulin secretion during a critical perinatal window. Insulin deficiency during this period causes hyperglycaemia and poor growth, but as beta-cell regulation matures the requirement for exogenous insulin falls and diabetes remits. Because the underlying epigenetic or dosage lesion persists, beta-cell reserve remains vulnerable, and physiological stress or the insulin resistance of puberty can precipitate relapse. A minority of cases instead reflect activating potassium-channel mutations that transiently impair insulin release. Maternal hypomethylation may extend beyond 6q24 to other imprinted loci, producing a multilocus imprinting disturbance with a broader phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Permanent neonatal diabetes mellitus (non-remitting monogenic disease)","Beckwith-Wiedemann spectrum where macroglossia and overgrowth predominate","Potassium-channel neonatal diabetes with a transient presentation","Stress or iatrogenic hyperglycaemia in the sick or preterm neonate","Small-for-gestational-age infants with transient glucose dysregulation","Early autoimmune diabetes presenting in later infancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Methylation-specific analysis of the 6q24 differentially methylated region","Testing for paternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 6 and 6q24 duplication","Sequencing of KCNJ11 and ABCC8 to identify transient channel forms","Serum C-peptide with paired glucose to gauge residual beta-cell function","Islet autoantibody panel to exclude autoimmune diabetes","Serial glucose monitoring through remission and structured follow-up for relapse"],"redFlags":["Dehydration or ketoacidosis at initial presentation despite the usually milder course","Failure to thrive with persistent hyperglycaemia in early infancy","Return of hyperglycaemic symptoms in later childhood or adolescence indicating relapse","Features suggesting a wider imprinting disorder beyond isolated diabetes"],"prognosis":"Neonatal diabetes typically remits within the first year with good short-term outcomes, but relapse later in childhood or at puberty is common and may require renewed treatment. Long-term prognosis is favourable when relapse is anticipated and managed, though lifelong metabolic surveillance is warranted.","pitfalls":["Regarding remission as permanent cure and discharging the child from follow-up.","Failing to counsel families that diabetes frequently returns around puberty.","Not distinguishing 6q24 disease from potassium-channel forms, which alters treatment options.","Attributing macroglossia and umbilical hernia to an unrelated overgrowth syndrome.","Assuming a benign transient course means genetic confirmation is unnecessary."],"management":["Insulin replacement during the hyperglycaemic phase by continuous subcutaneous or intravenous insulin infusion, titrated to glycaemia and weaned as beta-cell function matures and diabetes remits","Trial of an oral sulfonylurea where a KCNJ11 or ABCC8 potassium-channel mutation is identified, as these transient forms may respond and permit insulin withdrawal","Optimise nutrition to correct the intrauterine growth restriction, with careful fluid and electrolyte balance in the neonatal period","Confirm the molecular basis (6q24 methylation defect, paternal uniparental disomy or duplication, or a channel gene) and involve clinical genetics with family counselling","Continuous glucose monitoring through remission, with C-peptide and paired glucose to gauge residual beta-cell reserve","Lifelong metabolic surveillance because remission is not cure, anticipating relapse in later childhood and around puberty","Reintroduce glucose-lowering therapy promptly at relapse and involve paediatric endocrinology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric endocrinology reference texts","International consensus guidance on monogenic and neonatal diabetes","Clinical genetics reference resources on imprinting disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"transient_neonatal_pustular_melanosis":{"name":"transient neonatal pustular melanosis","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["transient neonatal pustulosis","TNPM"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neonatal and Infant Dermatology (Eichenfield, Frieden)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign self-limiting neonatal eruption that, unlike erythema toxicum, is typically present at birth and lacks a surrounding erythematous flare.","It evolves through three overlapping stages: fragile superficial pustules, then a collarette of fine scale, then residual hyperpigmented macules.","The pigmented macules can persist for weeks to months and are frequently the only finding by the time the infant is examined.","Unlike erythema toxicum, it commonly involves the palms, soles, chin, forehead, neck and lower back, and shows no age or maturity predilection.","It is disproportionately common in infants with darker, more heavily pigmented skin.","Pustule cytology shows a predominance of neutrophils, in contrast to the eosinophil-rich smear of erythema toxicum neonatorum.","Diagnosis is clinical in a well infant; the pustules are sterile and no treatment is required beyond parental reassurance.","The residual hyperpigmentation represents postinflammatory change and fades completely without intervention."],"pathophysiology":"Transient neonatal pustular melanosis is a benign, idiopathic neonatal pustular dermatosis. The early pustule is a sterile, subcorneal or intracorneal collection of neutrophils, occasionally with a few eosinophils, without evidence of infection or follicular targeting in the way erythema toxicum shows. As the fragile pustule ruptures, a fine collarette of scale remains, and localised postinflammatory increase in epidermal melanin produces the characteristic hyperpigmented macule. The cause of the initial sterile neutrophilic pustulation is unknown, and it is regarded as a transient reactive phenomenon of newborn skin. The prominence of residual pigmentation in more darkly pigmented infants reflects their greater melanocyte activity and readiness to develop postinflammatory hyperpigmentation.","additionalDifferentials":["erythema toxicum neonatorum","neonatal herpes simplex infection","bullous impetigo or staphylococcal pustulosis","congenital or neonatal candidiasis","infantile acropustulosis","miliaria","incontinentia pigmenti (vesicular stage)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wright or Giemsa stain of pustule contents showing predominant neutrophils","Gram stain and bacterial culture to exclude staphylococcal infection","viral swabs and PCR for herpes simplex if vesicular and clinically suspected","potassium hydroxide preparation to exclude candidal pustulosis","septic screen if the neonate is systemically unwell","skin biopsy only in atypical or uncertain cases","clinical follow-up to confirm expected fading of pigmented macules"],"redFlags":["an unwell, febrile or poorly feeding neonate, which is incompatible with this benign diagnosis","grouped vesicles suggesting neonatal herpes simplex infection","positive Gram stain or bacterial culture of pustule contents","linear or blaschkoid vesicular lesions suggesting incontinentia pigmenti","progressive or atypical eruption not following the expected pustule-scale-macule sequence"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; the pustular and scaling phases resolve within days and the residual hyperpigmented macules fade completely over several weeks to a few months without treatment or scarring.","pitfalls":["confusing residual hyperpigmented macules with a pathological pigmentary disorder or bruising","mislabelling the sterile pustules as bacterial infection and treating unnecessarily","failing to distinguish it from erythema toxicum, which is not usually present at birth and is eosinophil-predominant","overlooking neonatal herpes simplex in a vesicular eruption","over-investigating a well infant with classic features"],"management":["Reassurance and observation only, as the eruption is benign and self-resolving without active dermatological treatment","Confirm the sterile nature with a Wright or Giemsa smear showing predominant neutrophils, and exclude infective mimics by Gram stain and culture, herpes simplex PCR and potassium hydroxide preparation before withholding treatment","Routine gentle neonatal skin care, with no need for topical or systemic agents","Counsel parents that the pustular and scaling phases settle within days while residual hyperpigmented macules fade over weeks to months without scarring","Reserve a full septic screen and empirical antimicrobials for the infant who becomes systemically unwell"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neonatal and Infant Dermatology (Eichenfield, Frieden)","Harper's Textbook of Paediatric Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"transient_osteoporosis_of_the_hip":{"name":"Transient osteoporosis of the hip","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["transient osteoporosis","transient bone marrow oedema of the hip"],"chapter":null,"source":"Orthopaedic and rheumatology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A self-limiting cause of hip pain characterised by transient bone marrow oedema and reversible osteopenia","Classically affects middle-aged men and women in the third trimester of pregnancy","Presents with progressive hip pain and limp without preceding trauma","Radiographs show osteopenia of the femoral head after some weeks, while MRI shows marrow oedema without the necrosis of avascular necrosis","It is considered part of the bone marrow oedema syndrome spectrum and is a key differential of early avascular necrosis","It resolves spontaneously over months with protected weight bearing and analgesia"],"pathophysiology":"Transient osteoporosis of the hip is characterised by self-limiting bone marrow oedema of the femoral head and neck with associated localised, reversible bone loss. The cause is uncertain; proposed mechanisms include transient ischaemia, altered local blood flow or a neurovascular response, and in pregnancy mechanical and hormonal factors may contribute. Unlike avascular necrosis, there is no established osteonecrosis, and the oedema and osteopenia resolve completely, distinguishing it on MRI and by its benign natural history.","additionalDifferentials":["Avascular necrosis of the femoral head","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis","Subchondral insufficiency fracture","Inflammatory arthritis","Complex regional pain syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI showing bone marrow oedema without necrosis","Radiographs (osteopenia appears later)","Inflammatory markers to exclude infection","Assessment for pregnancy and osteoporosis risk factors","Follow-up imaging to confirm resolution"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting septic arthritis or osteomyelitis","Imaging evidence of established avascular necrosis or subchondral collapse","A subchondral fracture line","Failure to resolve as expected"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limiting, typically resolving over six to twelve months with conservative management and no long-term sequelae.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for avascular necrosis and intervening unnecessarily","Missing a subchondral insufficiency fracture","Overlooking infection when there are systemic features","Failing to protect the hip, risking fracture during the osteopenic phase"],"management":["Protected weight-bearing with crutches or a walking aid to offload the femoral head and reduce fracture risk while the marrow oedema resolves","Analgesia for pain control during the symptomatic phase","Consider bisphosphonate therapy with calcium and vitamin D repletion to hasten resolution of the bone marrow oedema in selected cases","Physiotherapy to preserve hip range of movement and muscle strength while weight-bearing is restricted","Serial MRI follow-up to confirm resolution and to distinguish the condition from evolving avascular necrosis","In pregnancy-associated disease, coordinate analgesia and mobility support with obstetric care, expecting spontaneous resolution after delivery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Orthopaedic reference texts (standard reference)","Musculoskeletal imaging references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"transient_synovitis_hip":{"name":"transient synovitis hip","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Irritable hip","Toxic synovitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Transient synovitis is the commonest cause of acute atraumatic hip pain and limp in young children, typically affecting the pre-school and early school-age group, and is more common in boys.","A preceding or concurrent viral upper respiratory tract illness is frequently reported, supporting a post-infectious reactive aetiology.","Affected children are usually well or only mildly unwell, afebrile or with low-grade temperature, and tend to limp or remain weight-bearing rather than refuse to move the limb entirely.","The hip is characteristically held flexed, abducted and externally rotated, with pain and guarding most marked at the extremes of internal rotation.","The central clinical task is to distinguish it from septic arthritis; validated prediction rules combining weight-bearing status, fever and inflammatory markers help stratify risk.","Inflammatory markers and the white cell count are normal or only mildly raised, in contrast to the marked elevation typical of septic arthritis.","Ultrasound commonly demonstrates a hip joint effusion but cannot reliably differentiate a sterile from an infected collection.","It remains a diagnosis of exclusion; management is supportive with rest and simple analgesia or anti-inflammatory agents, with planned review to confirm resolution."],"pathophysiology":"Transient synovitis is a self-limiting, non-pyogenic inflammation of the hip synovium that produces a sterile reactive effusion and consequent joint irritability. It is widely regarded as a post-infectious or reactive phenomenon, frequently following a viral illness, in which transient immune-mediated synovial inflammation and effusion raise intra-articular pressure and provoke pain, guarding and a characteristic protective posture. The synovial reaction resolves spontaneously without cartilage destruction, distinguishing it fundamentally from the pyogenic, cartilage-damaging process of septic arthritis with which it is most often confused.","additionalDifferentials":["Septic arthritis of the hip","Osteomyelitis of the proximal femur or pelvis","Legg-Calve-Perthes disease","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis, particularly in older or heavier children","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Reactive or post-streptococcal arthritis and acute rheumatic fever","Occult trauma or malignancy such as leukaemia presenting with bone or joint pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Hip ultrasound to detect and quantify effusion and to guide aspiration where needed","CRP, ESR and full blood count","Blood cultures where infection is suspected","Plain radiographs of the pelvis and hips, including anteroposterior and frog-lateral views, to exclude Perthes, slipped epiphysis and fracture","Joint aspiration with Gram stain, culture and cell count when septic arthritis cannot be excluded","MRI where osteomyelitis, Perthes disease or occult pathology is considered","Peripheral blood film and further haematological work-up if atypical or systemic features are present"],"redFlags":["Inability to bear weight combined with fever and markedly raised inflammatory markers, suggesting septic arthritis","Systemic toxicity, rigors or a rapidly deteriorating child","Night pain, weight loss, bruising or pallor suggesting malignancy","Symptoms failing to settle within the expected short timeframe","Migratory or multi-joint involvement"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent and the condition is self-limiting, with most children recovering fully within one to two weeks; a minority experience recurrent episodes. Rarely, a small proportion of children initially labelled as having transient synovitis are subsequently diagnosed with Perthes disease.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing early septic arthritis as transient synovitis and delaying intervention","Relying on ultrasound to differentiate a sterile from a septic effusion","Missing Perthes disease or a slipped epiphysis on inadequate radiographs that omit frog-lateral views","Failing to safety-net and arrange review for non-resolving symptoms","Overlooking leukaemia presenting as bone or joint pain"],"management":["Rest, activity limitation and simple analgesia or a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug for symptomatic relief, as the synovitis is self-limiting","Actively exclude septic arthritis using validated prediction rules combining weight-bearing status, fever and inflammatory markers, with hip ultrasound and joint aspiration where infection cannot be ruled out","Reassurance with outpatient management for the well, afebrile, weight-bearing child","Clear safety-netting to re-present with fever, increasing pain or refusal to weight-bear, prompting re-evaluation for septic arthritis","Clinical follow-up to confirm resolution within one to two weeks and to detect the minority who later declare Perthes disease","Refer to paediatric orthopaedics when the diagnosis is uncertain, symptoms persist, or septic arthritis is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Kocher criteria for differentiating septic arthritis from transient synovitis of the hip","Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"transient_tachypnea_of_newborn":{"name":"Transient Tachypnea of the Newborn","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TTN","transient tachypnoea of the newborn","wet lung","retained fetal lung fluid"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common, benign, self-limited cause of respiratory distress in term and late-preterm newborns caused by delayed clearance of fetal lung fluid.","It typically presents soon after birth with tachypnoea, mild grunting, nasal flaring, and mild recession, usually without severe distress.","Delivery by caesarean section (especially without labour), maternal diabetes, and rapid or precipitous delivery are recognised risk factors.","The chest radiograph classically shows prominent perihilar vascular markings, fluid in the interlobar fissures, hyperinflation, and sometimes small pleural effusions.","It is largely a diagnosis of exclusion, and sepsis and pneumonia should be considered, particularly if distress is marked or prolonged.","Management is supportive with oxygen as required and attention to feeding; the condition usually resolves within the first day or two of life.","Oxygen requirement is generally modest, and the need for high oxygen or escalating support should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis.","Distress persisting well beyond 2-3 days argues against transient tachypnoea and warrants further evaluation."],"pathophysiology":"During fetal life the lungs are filled with fluid that is normally cleared around birth by labour-associated catecholamine and hormonal surges that switch alveolar epithelial cells from fluid secretion to sodium-driven absorption, aided by mechanical squeeze during vaginal delivery. When this clearance is delayed or incomplete, residual fluid remains in the interstitium and air spaces, reducing lung compliance and causing tachypnoea and mild hypoxaemia until the fluid is reabsorbed.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome","Early-onset neonatal pneumonia or sepsis","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","Congenital heart disease","Air-leak syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph showing wet-lung features","Pulse oximetry and monitoring of oxygen requirement","Blood gas if distress is significant","Sepsis screen and consideration of antibiotics when infection cannot be excluded","Blood glucose monitoring"],"redFlags":["High or rising oxygen requirement","Distress persisting beyond the first few days of life","Marked recession, grunting, or signs of respiratory failure","Features suggesting sepsis or pneumonia","Differential pre- and post-ductal saturations suggesting pulmonary hypertension or cardiac disease"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limited, resolving within hours to a few days with supportive care and no long-term respiratory sequelae in the great majority of infants.","pitfalls":["Anchoring on transient tachypnoea and missing early sepsis or pneumonia","Overlooking congenital heart disease presenting with tachypnoea","Failing to reconsider the diagnosis when oxygen needs are high or distress is prolonged","Unnecessarily delaying feeding or investigation in a stable infant","Attributing every caesarean-born tachypnoeic infant to TTN without appropriate observation"],"management":["Supportive respiratory care with supplemental oxygen titrated to saturations, and continuous positive airway pressure if distress is more marked","Withhold oral feeds while tachypnoea limits safe feeding, providing nasogastric feeds or intravenous fluids to avoid aspiration","Continuous pulse oximetry and serial assessment of respiratory effort, with blood gas if distress is significant","Perform a sepsis screen and start empirical antibiotics until pneumonia or sepsis is confidently excluded, given the overlapping presentation","Monitor blood glucose, particularly in the infant of a diabetic mother","Anticipate resolution within the first day or two and escalate, reconsidering the diagnosis including persistent pulmonary hypertension, if the oxygen requirement rises or distress persists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"transient_tachypnoea_of_newborn":{"name":"Transient tachypnoea of the newborn","system":"Neonatal / Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TTN","wet lung","retained fetal lung fluid"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neonatology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common, self-limiting cause of neonatal respiratory distress from delayed clearance of fetal lung fluid","Typically presents soon after birth with tachypnoea, mild recession and grunting in a near-term or term baby","Elective caesarean birth without labour is a major risk factor, as is late prematurity and maternal diabetes","It is largely a diagnosis of exclusion, with infection and other causes of distress ruled out","Chest radiography classically shows prominent perihilar streaking and fluid in the horizontal fissure","Management is supportive with oxygen and respiratory support as needed, and it usually resolves within a day or two"],"pathophysiology":"Transient tachypnoea of the newborn results from delayed absorption of the fluid that normally fills the fetal lungs. During labour and vaginal birth, hormonal changes and thoracic compression promote clearance of this fluid via epithelial sodium channels and the lymphatics; when this process is incomplete, as after caesarean birth without labour, residual fluid in the airspaces and interstitium impairs gas exchange and reduces compliance, producing tachypnoea and mild distress. As the fluid is reabsorbed over hours to a couple of days, the symptoms resolve.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome","Early-onset neonatal sepsis or pneumonia","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","Congenital heart disease","Pneumothorax"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of respiratory rate, effort and oxygenation","Chest radiograph showing perihilar streaking and fissural fluid","Sepsis screen where infection cannot be excluded","Blood gas to assess gas exchange","Pre- and post-ductal saturations if pulmonary hypertension or cardiac disease is considered"],"redFlags":["Worsening or persistent distress beyond the expected short course","Hypoxaemia out of proportion suggesting pulmonary hypertension or cardiac disease","Features suggesting sepsis or pneumonia","Sudden deterioration suggesting pneumothorax"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limiting, resolving within one to three days with supportive care and no long-term respiratory sequelae.","pitfalls":["Attributing distress to TTN and missing early sepsis or pneumonia","Overlooking a cardiac cause of persistent hypoxaemia","Assuming resolution without reassessing worsening distress","Failing to recognise persistent pulmonary hypertension"],"management":["Supportive care with supplemental oxygen and non-invasive respiratory support such as continuous positive airway pressure, titrated to the infant's oxygen requirement","Provide nasogastric or intravenous nutrition and fluids while tachypnoea precludes safe oral feeding","Monitor respiratory rate, effort, oxygenation and blood gas, using pre- and post-ductal saturations to help exclude pulmonary hypertension or cardiac disease","Screen for infection and cover empirically with antibiotics until sepsis and pneumonia are excluded, as it is a diagnosis of exclusion","Provide parental reassurance and expect spontaneous resolution within one to three days as foetal lung fluid is reabsorbed","Escalate to neonatal intensive care and reconsider alternative diagnoses if respiratory distress worsens or fails to settle as expected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neonatology reference texts (standard reference)","Paediatric respiratory references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"transposition_of_great_arteries":{"name":"Transposition of the Great Arteries","system":"Paediatric cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TGA","d-TGA","transposition of great vessels"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["In d-transposition the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle, so the systemic and pulmonary circulations run in parallel rather than in series.","It is a leading cause of cyanosis presenting in the newborn period, often in an otherwise well-grown, term infant.","Survival depends on mixing between the two parallel circuits through a patent foramen ovale, atrial or ventricular septal defect, or the ductus arteriosus.","Cyanosis is typically profound and, unlike many respiratory causes, does not improve substantially with supplemental oxygen.","A prostaglandin infusion is started to maintain ductal patency and improve intercirculatory mixing while the infant is stabilised.","Balloon atrial septostomy may be performed to enlarge the atrial communication and improve mixing when cyanosis is severe.","Chest radiograph may show an egg-on-a-string cardiac silhouette with a narrow mediastinum, though this is not always present.","Echocardiography confirms the discordant great-artery connections, defines associated defects, and maps the coronary arteries before surgery.","The arterial switch operation, restoring normal ventriculoarterial connections, is the definitive repair and is performed in the neonatal period."],"pathophysiology":"Abnormal division of the truncus arteriosus results in the aorta connecting to the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery to the left ventricle. As a consequence, deoxygenated systemic venous blood is pumped back to the body and oxygenated pulmonary venous blood is returned to the lungs, forming two separate parallel circuits. Life is only possible if oxygenated and deoxygenated blood mix through a communication at the atrial, ventricular, or ductal level. Inadequate mixing produces severe systemic hypoxaemia and progressive metabolic acidosis, which worsens as the ductus arteriosus and foramen ovale close after birth.","additionalDifferentials":["Tetralogy of Fallot","Total anomalous pulmonary venous return","Tricuspid atresia","Truncus arteriosus","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","Severe respiratory disease causing cyanosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to confirm ventriculoarterial discordance, mixing sites, and coronary anatomy","Hyperoxia test showing little rise in oxygenation with supplemental oxygen (suggesting a cardiac cause)","Chest radiograph looking for an egg-on-a-string silhouette","Blood gas to assess hypoxaemia and acidosis","Pulse oximetry, including pre- and post-ductal comparison"],"redFlags":["Severe cyanosis in a newborn that fails to improve with supplemental oxygen","Rapid deterioration and acidosis as the ductus arteriosus and foramen ovale close, restricting mixing","Duct-dependent collapse before prostaglandin is established","Worsening metabolic acidosis indicating inadequate systemic oxygen delivery","Inadequate intercirculatory mixing requiring urgent septostomy"],"prognosis":"With prompt stabilisation and an arterial switch operation in the neonatal period, most children do well with good long-term function, though follow-up is required for coronary, valve, and great-artery complications. Without intervention the condition is rapidly fatal.","pitfalls":["Attributing severe neonatal cyanosis to lung disease and delaying cardiac evaluation because oxygen does not help","Failing to start prostaglandin promptly, allowing the ductus to close and mixing to fail","Overlooking the need to enhance mixing (septostomy) in a critically hypoxaemic infant","Assuming a normal chest radiograph excludes the diagnosis","Not mapping coronary anatomy before surgical planning"],"management":["Commence a prostaglandin infusion at presentation to maintain ductal patency and improve intercirculatory mixing during stabilisation","Perform balloon atrial septostomy to enlarge the atrial communication and improve mixing when cyanosis remains severe","Correct metabolic acidosis and hypoxaemia with supportive measures, including mechanical ventilation and inotropic support as required","Undertake the arterial switch operation in the neonatal period as the definitive corrective repair, with careful coronary artery transfer","Manage within a specialist paediatric cardiology and cardiac surgery multidisciplinary team","Provide lifelong cardiology follow-up for coronary, neo-aortic valve and great-artery complications after repair"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"transverse_myelitis":{"name":"Transverse myelitis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute transverse myelitis","ATM","transverse myelopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acute or subacute inflammatory myelopathy producing bilateral motor, sensory, and autonomic dysfunction below the level of the lesion","A clearly demarcated sensory level, with bladder and bowel involvement, is a key clue to a spinal cord process rather than a peripheral one","Rapidly evolving cord compression must be excluded urgently with imaging, because it may be surgically treatable and demands emergency intervention","Longitudinally extensive lesions spanning three or more vertebral segments should raise suspicion of neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder and prompt aquaporin-4 antibody testing","It may be idiopathic/post-infectious or the presenting event of multiple sclerosis, NMOSD, MOG-associated disease, or a systemic autoimmune disorder","MRI of the cord with contrast and cerebrospinal fluid analysis are central to diagnosis and to identifying the underlying cause","High-dose corticosteroids are the usual first-line treatment, with plasma exchange considered for severe or steroid-refractory cases","Identifying whether the episode is monophasic or part of a relapsing disorder guides long-term immunotherapy"],"pathophysiology":"Transverse myelitis is focal inflammation of the spinal cord causing demyelination and axonal injury across one or more segments, disrupting ascending sensory and descending motor tracts as well as autonomic pathways. It may follow infection or vaccination as a para- or post-infectious immune response, arise from autoimmune demyelinating disease such as multiple sclerosis, NMOSD, or MOG antibody disease, or occur with systemic autoimmune conditions. Breakdown of the blood-cord barrier and inflammatory cell infiltration produce cord swelling and dysfunction.","additionalDifferentials":["Compressive myelopathy from disc, tumour, abscess, or haematoma","Spinal cord infarction (anterior spinal artery syndrome)","Multiple sclerosis and NMOSD","Infective myelitis (e.g. viral)","Nutritional or metabolic myelopathy such as B12 deficiency","Guillain-Barre syndrome when presenting with ascending weakness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent MRI of the spine with and without contrast to exclude compression and define the inflammatory lesion","MRI of the brain to assess for demyelinating lesions suggesting multiple sclerosis","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis including cell count, protein, oligoclonal bands, and infective studies","Serum aquaporin-4 and MOG antibodies","Autoimmune and infective serology, and vitamin B12, as guided by the clinical picture"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive weakness with a sensory level and sphincter disturbance requiring emergency MRI to exclude compressive cord lesions amenable to surgery","Ascending deficit approaching the cervical cord threatening respiratory function","New bladder or bowel dysfunction and saddle sensory loss indicating significant cord involvement","Fever and back pain with neurological deficit suggesting epidural abscess as a surgical emergency"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable and depends on cause, severity, and rapidity of onset. Some patients recover substantially, while others are left with significant residual deficits. Recurrent disease and poorer recovery are more typical of NMOSD and longitudinally extensive lesions, underscoring the importance of accurate aetiological diagnosis and timely immunotherapy.","pitfalls":["Delaying MRI and missing a compressive lesion that requires emergency decompression","Failing to test for aquaporin-4 and MOG antibodies and misclassifying the disorder","Attributing the presentation to a peripheral cause and overlooking the sensory level and cord signs","Not investigating for an infective cause before or alongside immunosuppression when indicated"],"management":["Exclude compressive myelopathy urgently with spinal MRI, as it may be a surgically treatable emergency","Treat the acute inflammatory attack with high-dose intravenous corticosteroids (methylprednisolone)","Escalate to plasma exchange in steroid-refractory or severe cases","Direct treatment at the underlying cause: maintenance immunosuppression for aquaporin-4-positive NMOSD and MOG-antibody disease, disease-modifying therapy for multiple sclerosis, and specific treatment of any infective or systemic autoimmune trigger","Supportive care for the neurogenic bladder and bowel, pressure-area protection, venous thromboembolism prophylaxis, and management of neuropathic pain and spasticity","Early multidisciplinary neurorehabilitation with physiotherapy and occupational therapy","Long-term relapse-prevention immunotherapy and neurology follow-up for relapsing disorders such as NMOSD"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"transverse_vaginal_septum":{"name":"Transverse vaginal septum","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["transverse vaginal septum","vaginal septum","transverse septum of the vagina"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology textbook and paediatric and adolescent gynaecology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A transverse vaginal septum is a horizontal partition across the vaginal canal caused by faulty fusion or canalisation between the Mullerian ducts and the urogenital sinus","When the septum is complete it obstructs menstrual outflow, so adolescents typically present with primary amenorrhoea and cyclical pelvic pain despite normal secondary sexual characteristics","Retained menstrual blood accumulates above the septum causing haematocolpos and haematometra, mirroring an imperforate hymen but at a higher level in the vagina","The distinguishing feature from imperforate hymen is that the introitus and hymenal ring look normal, and the obstruction is felt higher up rather than as a bulging membrane at the opening","A perforate (incomplete) septum may allow menstruation but can cause obstructed drainage, chronic discharge, or difficulty with tampon use and intercourse","The thickness and level of the septum, which determine surgical difficulty, are best defined before treatment, usually with magnetic resonance imaging","Treatment is surgical excision of the septum with re-anastomosis of the upper and lower vaginal segments, and thick or high septa may require complex reconstruction","Because it is a Mullerian anomaly, associated renal and skeletal malformations should be sought"],"pathophysiology":"The vagina forms from fusion of the paired Mullerian (paramesonephric) ducts with the urogenital sinus, followed by canalisation of the intervening vaginal plate. A transverse septum arises when there is incomplete fusion at the junction of these components or failure of the vaginal plate to canalise fully, leaving a fibrous or fibromuscular partition at any level of the vagina. A complete septum blocks the egress of menstrual blood once cycles begin, producing haematocolpos above the obstruction and, with time, haematometra and possible retrograde menstruation. Ovarian and endocrine function are normal, so puberty progresses despite the outflow obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Imperforate hymen","Distal vaginal atresia or agenesis","Cervical agenesis or dysgenesis","Mullerian (vaginal and uterine) agenesis","Obstructed hemivagina with a longitudinal septum","Androgen insensitivity syndrome and other causes of primary amenorrhoea"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genital examination confirming a normal introitus with obstruction higher in the vagina","Pelvic ultrasound to demonstrate haematocolpos and haematometra","Magnetic resonance imaging to define the level, thickness, and completeness of the septum and plan surgery","Assessment of pubertal development and secondary sexual characteristics","Renal tract and skeletal imaging for associated congenital anomalies"],"redFlags":["A thick or high septum implying complex reconstructive surgery and risk of stenosis","Acute urinary retention or severe pain from a large obstructed collection","Signs of infection within retained blood","Uncertainty distinguishing septum from cervical or vaginal agenesis, which alters management"],"prognosis":"After appropriate surgical correction the outlook for menstruation and fertility is generally good, but thick or high septa carry a higher risk of postoperative stenosis and may need repeat procedures, and delayed treatment increases the chance of endometriosis from retrograde flow.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a transverse septum for an imperforate hymen and planning inadequate surgery","Operating without magnetic resonance imaging to characterise septal thickness and level","Underestimating the risk of postoperative vaginal stenosis after excision of a thick septum","Attributing primary amenorrhoea with normal development to hormonal causes and missing the obstruction","Overlooking associated renal or skeletal anomalies"],"management":["Surgical resection of the septum with re-anastomosis of the upper and lower vaginal segments as the definitive treatment, ideally in a specialist centre","Define the level, thickness and completeness of the septum with MRI to plan the operative approach before intervention","Relieve the obstruction and drain the accumulated haematocolpos and haematometra at operation","Postoperative vaginal dilator therapy to reduce the risk of stricture, particularly with thick or high septa prone to stenosis and repeat procedures","Screen for and manage endometriosis arising from retrograde menstruation, especially where treatment has been delayed","Investigate for associated renal tract and skeletal anomalies","Provide psychological support and counselling to the adolescent and family around diagnosis and fertility"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference text on Mullerian anomalies","Paediatric and adolescent gynaecology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"trapped_lung":{"name":"Trapped lung","system":"Respiratory / Thoracic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["non-expandable lung","unexpandable lung","visceral pleural peel"],"chapter":null,"source":"Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic, non-expandable lung caused by a fibrous visceral pleural peel that prevents the lung re-expanding to the chest wall","Results from a remote, now-quiescent pleural insult such as prior infection, haemothorax, or inflammation, in contrast to active lung entrapment where inflammation is ongoing","Produces a persistent, typically small to moderate pleural effusion that reaccumulates after drainage","The fluid is characteristically a transudate or borderline exudate formed by negative pleural pressure (ex vacuo effusion)","Pleural manometry shows a rapid, excessive fall in pleural pressure during fluid removal, and imaging after drainage reveals failure of the lung to fully expand","Repeated therapeutic thoracocentesis gives little lasting symptomatic benefit because the lung cannot expand","Definitive treatment when symptomatic is surgical decortication to release the visceral peel"],"pathophysiology":"Following a resolved pleural inflammatory process, a mature, relatively acellular fibrous membrane forms over the visceral pleura. This inelastic peel mechanically restrains the lung so that it cannot expand to fill the hemithorax. The persistently unexpanded lung creates an abnormally negative intrapleural pressure, which draws a transudative effusion into the space through an imbalance of hydrostatic forces rather than active inflammation. Because the driving mechanism is mechanical, the fluid recurs after drainage and the lung fails to re-expand, distinguishing established trapped lung from active entrapment where ongoing pleural inflammation is present.","additionalDifferentials":["Active lung entrapment from ongoing pleural inflammation or malignancy","Fibrothorax after empyema or haemothorax","Malignant pleural effusion with visceral pleural involvement","Chronic organising empyema","Endobronchial obstruction preventing lung expansion","Chylothorax or other persistent effusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph before and after thoracocentesis to assess lung re-expansion","CT of the thorax to demonstrate visceral pleural thickening and exclude malignancy or endobronchial obstruction","Pleural fluid analysis, typically showing a transudate or borderline exudate","Pleural manometry demonstrating a steep pressure drop during fluid removal","Bronchoscopy where an endobronchial cause of non-expansion is suspected"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting an underlying malignancy such as a bloody effusion or pleural nodularity","Signs of active or recurrent pleural infection","Development of a hydropneumothorax after attempted drainage","Progressive breathlessness with an enlarging pleural collection"],"prognosis":"Trapped lung itself is a stable, benign mechanical problem; symptomatic cases can improve after decortication if the underlying lung is healthy, whereas the outlook is dominated by any underlying malignant or infective cause when present.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly draining the effusion without recognising that the lung cannot re-expand","Mislabelling a mechanical ex vacuo transudate as an unexplained exudate","Attempting pleurodesis when the lung will not appose the chest wall","Failing to distinguish quiescent trapped lung from active entrapment that needs treatment of ongoing disease"],"management":["Surgical decortication to strip the fibrous visceral pleural peel and allow lung re-expansion in symptomatic patients whose underlying lung is healthy","Confirm the diagnosis with pleural manometry showing an excessive pressure drop on fluid removal and post-drainage imaging showing failure of re-expansion","Avoid repeated therapeutic thoracocentesis, which gives little lasting benefit because the lung cannot expand","Identify and treat any underlying malignant or infective process driving the pleural disease","Observe asymptomatic patients, reserving intervention for breathlessness or diagnostic uncertainty","Refer to thoracic surgery for assessment of decortication and to distinguish established trapped lung from active lung entrapment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Shields' General Thoracic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"traps_syndrome":{"name":"traps syndrome","system":"Immunological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TNF receptor-associated periodic syndrome","familial Hibernian fever","TNFRSF1A-associated periodic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant disorder caused by variants in TNFRSF1A encoding the p55 tumour necrosis factor receptor 1; originally described in families of northern European (Irish and Scottish) ancestry as familial Hibernian fever.","Attacks are notably prolonged compared with other periodic fevers, frequently lasting a week or more and sometimes several weeks, which can lead to repeated investigation for infection or malignancy.","Migratory myalgia with a warm, tender overlying erythematous patch that moves centrifugally down a limb is highly characteristic and reflects a monocytic fasciitis rather than true myositis.","Periorbital oedema, often with conjunctivitis and periorbital pain, is a distinctive sign that is uncommon in the other hereditary periodic fevers.","Serositis with abdominal pain, pleuritic chest pain and arthralgia is common, and reactive AA amyloidosis is an important long-term complication, particularly with cysteine-residue variants.","Interleukin-1 blockade is the most reliably effective treatment; corticosteroids attenuate attacks but with escalating requirements, and anti-TNF responses are inconsistent with some agents paradoxically worsening disease.","Low-penetrance variants such as R92Q and P46L are common in the general population and may produce milder, atypical or incompletely penetrant phenotypes.","Reduced shedding of soluble TNF receptor 1 between attacks can support the diagnosis but is neither fully sensitive nor specific."],"pathophysiology":"Variants in TNFRSF1A, especially those affecting conserved cysteine residues in the extracellular domain, cause misfolding of the mutant receptor with intracellular retention and aggregation in the endoplasmic reticulum rather than defective receptor shedding alone. The retained receptor provokes endoplasmic reticulum stress, augmented mitochondrial reactive oxygen species and ligand-independent pro-inflammatory signalling that enhances responsiveness to inflammatory stimuli. The net effect is exaggerated innate immune activation with prominent interleukin-1 beta-driven inflammation, accounting for both the prolonged inflammatory attacks and the responsiveness to interleukin-1 blockade, while sustained serum amyloid A elevation underlies the risk of AA amyloidosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial Mediterranean fever","Mevalonate kinase deficiency (hyper-IgD syndrome)","Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndrome spectrum","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis and adult-onset Still's disease","PFAPA syndrome","Cyclic neutropenia","Occult infection or lymphoma causing prolonged fever"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["TNFRSF1A gene sequencing, interpreting low-penetrance variants with caution","Serial acute-phase reactants (C-reactive protein, serum amyloid A) during and between episodes","Serum soluble TNF receptor 1 concentration between attacks","Magnetic resonance imaging of affected muscle groups to demonstrate fasciitis rather than myositis","Urinalysis for proteinuria and, if positive, tissue biopsy with Congo red staining for amyloid","Ophthalmological assessment when periorbital oedema and conjunctivitis are prominent"],"redFlags":["Emergence of proteinuria indicating renal AA amyloidosis","Escalating corticosteroid dependence to control increasingly frequent or prolonged attacks","A prolonged febrile episode where serious infection or malignancy has not been adequately excluded","Clinical deterioration after starting certain anti-TNF agents"],"prognosis":"Interleukin-1 blockade controls attacks and inflammation in most patients, allowing corticosteroid sparing and reducing the risk of amyloidosis. Untreated disease, especially with high-penetrance cysteine variants, carries a meaningful risk of AA amyloidosis and progressive renal impairment.","pitfalls":["Misattributing prolonged febrile attacks to recurrent infection and administering repeated antibiotic courses","Reporting a low-penetrance variant such as R92Q as definitively diagnostic without correlating with phenotype","Mistaking periorbital oedema for allergy or angio-oedema","Interpreting migratory limb erythema and myalgia as cellulitis or thrombophlebitis"],"management":["Interleukin-1 blockade (anakinra or canakinumab) as the mainstay, being the most reliably effective therapy for controlling attacks and inflammation","Corticosteroids to attenuate acute attacks, while limiting reliance given escalating requirements","Consider the TNF-inhibitor etanercept where interleukin-1 blockade is unsuitable, but avoid infliximab, which can paradoxically worsen disease","Monitor C-reactive protein and serum amyloid A during and between episodes, and screen the urine for proteinuria to detect reactive AA amyloidosis","Confirm the diagnosis by TNFRSF1A sequencing and provide genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant disorder","Involve nephrology and consider tissue biopsy with Congo red staining if amyloidosis or renal impairment develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology (current edition)","Textbook of Pediatric Rheumatology, Cassidy (current edition)","Eurofever and PRINTO consensus classification criteria for hereditary recurrent fevers"],"crossLinks":[]},"traumatic_brain_injury":{"name":"Traumatic brain injury","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBI","head injury","traumatic head injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Severity is graded with the Glasgow Coma Scale, and a trend in the score matters more than any single reading","The overriding aim is to prevent secondary brain injury by avoiding hypoxia and hypotension, which independently worsen outcome","Maintain adequate oxygenation and cerebral perfusion pressure while controlling intracranial pressure","Unequal or fixed dilated pupils, deteriorating consciousness, and a lateralising motor deficit suggest an expanding mass lesion and impending herniation","Cushing's response (hypertension with bradycardia) is a late and ominous sign of dangerously raised intracranial pressure","Early CT imaging identifies surgically evacuable lesions such as extradural and subdural haematomas","Head-injured patients frequently have concomitant injuries, and hypotension should prompt a search for extracranial bleeding rather than being attributed to the brain injury alone","Basic measures to lower intracranial pressure include head-of-bed elevation, avoiding venous obstruction, and preventing fever, seizures, and hypercarbia"],"pathophysiology":"The primary injury is the mechanical damage sustained at impact, which cannot be reversed. Secondary injury develops over hours to days from hypoxia, hypotension, oedema, haematoma expansion, seizures, and disturbed autoregulation. Because the skull is a fixed compartment, expanding blood or oedema raises intracranial pressure; once compensatory mechanisms are exhausted, small volume increases cause steep pressure rises. Rising intracranial pressure reduces cerebral perfusion pressure and drives ischaemia, and pressure gradients can force brain tissue across dural folds or the foramen magnum, producing herniation and brainstem compression.","additionalDifferentials":["Ischaemic or haemorrhagic stroke presenting with a fall or collapse","Hypoglycaemia or other metabolic causes of reduced consciousness","Intoxication or drug overdose","Post-ictal state after seizure","Non-traumatic intracranial haemorrhage (e.g. aneurysmal subarachnoid haemorrhage)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Non-contrast CT head to detect haemorrhage, contusion, oedema, and skull fracture","Cervical spine imaging given the frequent association of head and neck injury","Blood glucose and coagulation studies, including reversal assessment for anticoagulated patients","Invasive intracranial pressure monitoring in selected severe cases","Repeat imaging if neurological status deteriorates or bleeding may progress"],"redFlags":["Falling GCS or a drop of two or more points indicating deterioration","Unequal, dilated, or unreactive pupil suggesting uncal herniation from a mass lesion needing urgent decompression","Cushing's response (hypertension with bradycardia) signalling critically raised intracranial pressure","New focal neurological deficit or seizures","Hypoxia or hypotension, which must be corrected immediately to limit secondary injury"],"prognosis":"Outcome ranges from full recovery in mild injury to death or severe disability in severe injury. Prevention of secondary insults, timely evacuation of surgical lesions, and control of intracranial pressure improve results, whereas sustained raised pressure and low cerebral perfusion predict poor recovery.","pitfalls":["Attributing hypotension to the brain injury and overlooking extracranial haemorrhage","Allowing hypoxia, hypercarbia, or hypotension that aggravate secondary injury","Missing an expanding haematoma by not repeating imaging after clinical deterioration","Over-sedating so that neurological reassessment becomes impossible","Failing to reverse anticoagulation promptly in a bleeding head injury"],"management":["Prevent secondary brain injury by maintaining oxygenation and blood pressure, avoiding hypoxia and hypotension","Optimise cerebral perfusion pressure and control raised intracranial pressure with head-of-bed elevation, sedation and analgesia, and osmotherapy using hypertonic saline or mannitol","Obtain urgent non-contrast CT head and refer for neurosurgical evacuation of surgically accessible lesions such as extradural and subdural haematomas","Reverse anticoagulation, correct coagulopathy and give antiseizure prophylaxis to reduce early post-traumatic seizures","Institute invasive intracranial pressure monitoring in selected severe injuries and manage within a neurocritical care and trauma team","Repeat imaging and reassess if the Glasgow Coma Scale deteriorates or bleeding may progress, watching for pupillary change and the Cushing response","Arrange multidisciplinary neurorehabilitation for surviving patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"traumatic_optic_neuropathy":{"name":"traumatic optic neuropathy","system":"Ophthalmic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TON","post-traumatic optic neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Follows blunt or penetrating head and orbital trauma, classically frontal or brow impact that transmits force to the tightly tethered intracanalicular optic nerve.","Distinguish direct injury, such as nerve laceration, avulsion, bony fragment or sheath haematoma, from indirect injury from deceleration and shearing forces.","There is acute visual loss with a relative afferent pupillary defect, while the disc is often normal at onset and becomes pale after several weeks.","It is commonly associated with maxillofacial and orbital fractures and closed head injury and may be masked in an unconscious patient.","Computed tomography of the orbits and optic canal with fine cuts is central to assessment, identifying fractures, haematoma and nerve impingement.","Management is controversial and includes observation, high-dose corticosteroids or optic canal decompression, with no proven benefit from routine steroids.","High-dose corticosteroids are potentially harmful in the setting of significant head injury, as shown in major head-injury trials.","An orbital compartment syndrome from retrobulbar haemorrhage is a treatable emergency requiring urgent lateral canthotomy and cantholysis."],"pathophysiology":"Traumatic optic neuropathy involves both primary and secondary mechanisms. Primary injury results from direct disruption of axons or from mechanical force transmitted to the firmly anchored intracanalicular segment, where the nerve is least able to dissipate energy. Secondary injury then evolves within the confined bony optic canal through oedema, ischaemia, oxidative stress and apoptotic cell death, causing progressive axonal loss. A retrobulbar haemorrhage can additionally compress the nerve and its blood supply, producing a compartment-type ischaemic insult.","additionalDifferentials":["Orbital compartment syndrome from retrobulbar haemorrhage","Globe rupture or severe anterior segment injury","Commotio retinae or traumatic maculopathy","Retinal detachment","Occipital or cortical visual injury","Functional (non-organic) visual loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Computed tomography of the head and orbits with dedicated fine optic canal views","Assessment of visual acuity and pupils for a relative afferent pupillary defect","Colour vision testing and visual fields when the patient is able to cooperate","Measurement of intraocular pressure and assessment for proptosis and a tense orbit","Magnetic resonance imaging when a sheath haematoma or soft-tissue injury is suspected","Optical coherence tomography of the retinal nerve fibre layer at follow-up"],"redFlags":["Proptosis with raised intraocular pressure and a tense orbit indicating a compartment syndrome needing emergency canthotomy","An expanding orbital haematoma","Bilateral visual loss or no perception of light","Associated significant intracranial injury"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable; poor prognostic features include no perception of light at presentation, an optic canal fracture, older age and loss of consciousness. Some patients recover useful vision spontaneously.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis in an unconscious or multiply injured patient","Administering high-dose corticosteroids in significant head injury despite evidence of harm","Overlooking a treatable retrobulbar haemorrhage requiring urgent orbital decompression","Delaying surgical decompression when a clear compressive lesion is present"],"management":["Relieve orbital compartment pressure emergently with lateral canthotomy and cantholysis when a retrobulbar haemorrhage threatens the nerve","Consider observation, high-dose corticosteroids or optic canal decompression on an individualised basis, recognising that routine steroids are of unproven benefit","Image the head and orbits with fine-cut CT of the optic canal to identify fracture, haematoma or nerve impingement amenable to surgery","Undertake surgical decompression of the optic canal or evacuation of a compressive sheath haematoma in selected cases with impingement","Serially monitor visual acuity, pupillary responses and colour vision to track the afferent defect and treatment response","Manage jointly with neuro-ophthalmology and maxillofacial or orbital surgery, alongside treatment of associated head and facial injuries"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","International Optic Nerve Trauma Study"],"crossLinks":[]},"traumatic_panniculitis":{"name":"traumatic panniculitis","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mobile encapsulated lipoma","post-traumatic fat necrosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Weedon's Skin Pathology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An inflammation of subcutaneous fat provoked by mechanical injury, blunt trauma, pressure or repetitive friction, producing tender nodules or plaques at the site of insult.","Common sites reflect exposure to trauma: the breasts, thighs, buttocks and shins; a history of a precipitating event is often but not always elicited.","Cold-related and pressure-related forms overlap clinically; the breast is a classic site where post-traumatic fat necrosis can mimic carcinoma.","Lesions evolve from firm tender nodules to indurated, sometimes fibrotic or calcified masses, and may become mobile and encapsulated over time.","Histology shows a mostly lobular panniculitis with fat necrosis, lipophages (foamy macrophages), lipomembranous (membranocystic) change and later fibrosis.","Imaging of breast lesions may reveal an oil cyst with rim calcification, a helpful clue to a benign traumatic aetiology.","Diagnosis rests on correlating a clear mechanical history with compatible histology after excluding infective and systemic panniculitides.","Most lesions are self-limiting and resolve with conservative care; the main clinical imperative is to avoid mistaking them for malignancy."],"pathophysiology":"Mechanical trauma, sustained pressure or repetitive friction disrupts adipocytes and their vascular supply, releasing intracellular lipid into the interstitium. Free triglycerides are hydrolysed by tissue lipases into free fatty acids, which incite a lobular inflammatory response dominated by lipophagic macrophages that engulf the liberated lipid. Ischaemia from injured vessels contributes to fat necrosis. Over time the infiltrate gives way to foreign-body granulomas, lipomembranous change and fibrosis, with occasional dystrophic calcification and oil-cyst formation. In the breast this reparative fibrosis can produce a firm, spiculated mass that clinically and radiologically simulates carcinoma.","additionalDifferentials":["erythema nodosum","breast carcinoma (for breast lesions)","infective panniculitis","pancreatic panniculitis","lupus panniculitis","factitial panniculitis","subcutaneous sarcoidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["deep incisional or excisional biopsy including subcutaneous fat for histopathology","ultrasound or mammography for breast lesions (oil cyst, rim calcification)","tissue cultures to exclude infection when clinically indicated","MRI for deep or diagnostically uncertain lesions","core biopsy of suspicious breast masses to exclude malignancy","review of medication and occupational or activity history for repetitive trauma"],"redFlags":["a firm, fixed or enlarging breast mass that cannot be confidently distinguished from carcinoma","systemic features (fever, weight loss) suggesting infection or an alternative systemic panniculitis","ulceration or discharge raising concern for infection or factitial disease","absence of any plausible traumatic history prompting a search for another cause"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and usually self-limiting, resolving over weeks to months, though residual fibrosis, calcification or an encapsulated fatty nodule may persist.","pitfalls":["Misinterpreting a post-traumatic breast mass as carcinoma, or vice versa, without appropriate imaging and biopsy.","Accepting a trivial or coincidental trauma history and overlooking a systemic panniculitis.","Sampling too superficially and missing the lobular fat pathology.","Assuming all fat necrosis is traumatic and failing to consider pancreatic or infective causes.","Attributing recurrent lesions to trauma when factitial disease is responsible."],"management":["Conservative management with analgesia, warm compresses and rest, as the panniculitis is self-limiting","Identify and remove the precipitating mechanical cause, whether blunt trauma, sustained pressure or repetitive friction","Exclude malignancy in breast lesions that mimic carcinoma, using mammography or ultrasound and core biopsy where imaging is suspicious","Confirm the diagnosis with deep incisional or excisional biopsy including subcutaneous fat in atypical or uncertain lesions","Reassure that lesions resolve over weeks to months, while explaining that residual fibrosis, calcification or an encapsulated fatty nodule may persist"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Weedon's Skin Pathology, current edition","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology, current edition","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine, current edition"],"crossLinks":[]},"treacher_collins_syndrome":{"name":"Treacher Collins syndrome (TCOF1)","system":"Genetics / Craniofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mandibulofacial dysostosis","Treacher Collins-Franceschetti syndrome","TCS","Franceschetti-Klein syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A symmetrical mandibulofacial dysostosis, in contrast to the asymmetry of the oculo-auriculo-vertebral spectrum","Hallmark features are down-slanting palpebral fissures, lower eyelid colobomata with deficient lower lash medial to the defect, and malar (zygomatic) hypoplasia","External ear anomalies with microtia and conductive hearing loss are typical, and audiological support is a priority for language development","Micrognathia and cleft palate contribute to neonatal airway and feeding problems","Intellect is characteristically normal, an important point for family counselling","Most cases follow autosomal dominant inheritance through TCOF1 variants, with variable expressivity even within families","Recessive forms linked to POLR1C and POLR1D and other polymerase-related genes are recognised, so a normal TCOF1 result does not exclude the diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"The condition results principally from haploinsufficiency of the treacle protein encoded by TCOF1, which participates in ribosomal RNA transcription and processing within the nucleolus. Reduced ribosome biogenesis is thought to trigger nucleolar stress and stabilisation of p53, leading to excessive apoptosis of cranial neural crest cells during early development. Because these neural crest cells populate the first and second branchial arches that form the midface, mandible, and external and middle ear, their depletion produces the symmetrical hypoplasia of the malar bones, mandible, and ear structures. Rarer forms arise from variants in RNA polymerase I and III subunit genes affecting the same pathway.","additionalDifferentials":["Nager acrofacial dysostosis (mandibulofacial dysostosis with preaxial limb defects)","Miller syndrome (mandibulofacial dysostosis with postaxial limb defects)","Goldenhar spectrum (asymmetric involvement)","Acrofacial dysostosis of other subtypes","Isolated Pierre Robin sequence","Maternal retinoid embryopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of TCOF1 with reflex analysis of POLR1C and POLR1D when negative","Audiological assessment including bone-conduction and brainstem-evoked response testing","Craniofacial computed tomography to define zygomatic, orbital, and middle-ear anatomy","Sleep study to assess for obstructive sleep apnoea related to airway hypoplasia","Ophthalmological review for eyelid coloboma and ocular surface protection","Genetic counselling and cascade testing of at-risk relatives"],"redFlags":["Neonatal airway obstruction from combined micrognathia, choanal narrowing, and pharyngeal hypoplasia","Feeding failure and poor weight gain requiring nutritional intervention","Exposure keratopathy from lower eyelid colobomata threatening the cornea","Progressive obstructive sleep apnoea with growth or cardiorespiratory consequences","Difficult airway anticipated during any anaesthetic"],"prognosis":"With multidisciplinary craniofacial care, airway and hearing support, and staged reconstruction, most individuals achieve good function and normal cognition, though repeated surgery over childhood and adolescence is often required.","pitfalls":["Assuming intellectual disability and thereby underestimating the child's potential","Failing to anticipate a difficult airway before sedation or surgery","Overlooking recessively inherited forms when TCOF1 testing is negative","Neglecting corneal protection in the presence of eyelid colobomata","Delaying hearing rehabilitation, harming speech and language outcomes"],"management":["Secure the neonatal airway compromised by micrognathia and cleft palate, ranging from positioning and a nasopharyngeal airway to mandibular distraction osteogenesis or tracheostomy in severe obstruction","Support feeding and nutrition, addressing the cleft palate with feeding aids and speech and language therapy input","Provide early audiological rehabilitation, typically bone-conduction hearing aids, to protect language development given conductive hearing loss and microtia","Undertake staged craniofacial reconstruction of the zygoma, mandible, eyelids and external ear through childhood and adolescence","Repair lower eyelid colobomata and protect the ocular surface under ophthalmology guidance","Investigate and treat obstructive sleep apnoea with a sleep study and continuous positive airway pressure where indicated","Coordinate care through a craniofacial multidisciplinary team","Provide genetic counselling and cascade testing of at-risk relatives for this autosomal dominant TCOF1-related disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: Treacher Collins Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"trehalase_deficiency":{"name":"Trehalase deficiency","system":"Gastroenterology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TREH","trehalose intolerance","mushroom intolerance","congenital trehalase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare disaccharidase deficiency, attributed to reduced brush-border trehalase encoded by TREH","Trehalose is found chiefly in mushrooms and some processed foods, so symptoms follow their ingestion","Presents with osmotic diarrhoea, bloating, cramping and flatulence after trehalose-containing meals","The mechanism is failure to hydrolyse trehalose to glucose, leaving an osmotically active, fermentable sugar in the lumen","Symptoms are dose-dependent and resolve on avoidance of trehalose-rich foods","Diagnosis can be supported by low trehalase activity on duodenal disaccharidase assay and by a trehalose breath test","The condition is often under-recognised and may be mistaken for a functional bowel disorder"],"pathophysiology":"Trehalase deficiency is an osmotic malabsorption disorder of the intestinal brush border. Trehalase, encoded by TREH, hydrolyses the disaccharide trehalose into two glucose molecules for absorption. When trehalase activity is deficient, ingested trehalose, present mainly in mushrooms and certain processed foods, is not digested and remains in the lumen. The unabsorbed sugar draws water osmotically into the bowel and is fermented by colonic bacteria into gas and short-chain acids, producing diarrhoea, bloating and flatulence after trehalose-containing meals. Because exposure is intermittent and food-specific, symptoms occur only after relevant ingestions.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital sucrase-isomaltase deficiency","Lactase deficiency (congenital or adult-type)","Glucose-galactose malabsorption","Functional gastrointestinal disorders and irritable bowel syndrome","Other food intolerances and FODMAP sensitivity","Post-infective carbohydrate malabsorption"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dietary history correlating symptoms with mushroom or trehalose-containing food intake","Trehalose hydrogen breath test","Duodenal biopsy with disaccharidase assay including trehalase activity","Stool pH and reducing substances after a trehalose challenge","Molecular assessment of TREH where available and trial of trehalose elimination"],"redFlags":["Dehydration during acute diarrhoeal episodes, particularly in young children","Weight loss or failure to thrive suggesting a broader malabsorptive process","Persistent symptoms despite trehalose avoidance indicating another diagnosis","New alarm features prompting evaluation for mucosal disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent, as symptoms are entirely controlled by avoiding trehalose-rich foods and there are no systemic consequences. Recognition prevents unnecessary investigation for functional disorders.","pitfalls":["Attributing recurrent post-meal symptoms to a functional disorder without taking a focused dietary history","Overlooking trehalose as a hidden ingredient in some processed foods","Failing to include trehalase in the disaccharidase assay panel","Assuming symptoms reflect a more common sugar intolerance without confirmatory testing"],"management":["Dietary elimination of trehalose-rich foods, chiefly mushrooms and certain processed foods, as the definitive and curative measure","Dietitian support to identify hidden dietary sources of trehalose and maintain nutritional adequacy","Educate that symptoms are dose-dependent and resolve fully on avoidance, so strict blanket restriction is not always required","Confirm the diagnosis with a duodenal disaccharidase assay showing low trehalase activity or a trehalose breath test, preventing unnecessary investigation for functional bowel disease","Reassure that the condition is benign with no systemic consequences once relevant foods are avoided"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"trench_fever":{"name":"Trench fever","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Bartonella quintana infection","quintan fever","five-day fever","urban trench fever"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by Bartonella quintana and transmitted by the human body louse, with organisms shed in louse faeces and inoculated through scratched skin","Historically a major cause of illness among soldiers in the trenches of the First World War, it persists today among homeless people, heavy drinkers and others exposed to body lice, hence the term urban trench fever","After an incubation of around 1-2 weeks patients develop fever, severe headache, bone pain (classically shin pain), dizziness and malaise","The fever may be a single episode or recur at roughly five-day intervals, giving rise to the names five-day and quintan fever","Infection ranges from mild self-limiting illness to prolonged or relapsing bacteraemia, and Bartonella quintana is an important cause of culture-negative endocarditis","The same organism causes bacillary angiomatosis in immunocompromised hosts, so the clinical spectrum depends heavily on host immunity","Diagnosis relies on serology, PCR and prolonged-incubation blood cultures, and treatment uses prolonged antimicrobial courses, with delousing essential to prevent reinfection and spread"],"pathophysiology":"Bartonella quintana is a fastidious Gram-negative bacterium maintained in humans and transmitted by the body louse. Following inoculation through louse-faeces contamination of broken skin, the organism enters the bloodstream and characteristically infects erythrocytes and vascular endothelial cells, establishing a persistent or relapsing intraerythrocytic bacteraemia that accounts for the recurrent febrile episodes. In immunocompetent hosts this produces the self-limiting or relapsing febrile illness of trench fever, whereas the ability to adhere to cardiac valves underlies its role in chronic culture-negative endocarditis.","additionalDifferentials":["Epidemic or murine typhus","Relapsing fever due to Borrelia","Malaria in returning travellers","Infective endocarditis of other cause","Brucellosis","Undifferentiated viral febrile illness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serology for Bartonella quintana","PCR of blood or tissue","Prolonged-incubation blood cultures using enriched media","Full blood count and inflammatory markers","Echocardiography where endocarditis is suspected","Examination for body lice to support the epidemiological diagnosis"],"redFlags":["New murmur, heart failure or embolic events suggesting endocarditis","Prolonged or relapsing bacteraemia despite treatment","Profound immunosuppression predisposing to bacillary angiomatosis","Occurrence in a homeless or crowded population signalling wider transmission risk","Severe or persistent constitutional symptoms with weight loss"],"prognosis":"Acute trench fever is generally self-limiting with a good outcome, but Bartonella quintana bacteraemia and endocarditis are serious, may require valve surgery, and mandate prolonged antibiotic therapy; reinfection is likely if louse infestation is not addressed.","pitfalls":["Not considering the diagnosis in homeless or louse-infested patients with fever","Relying on routine short-incubation blood cultures, which are typically negative","Failing to investigate for endocarditis in persistent bacteraemia","Overlooking delousing and social measures, leading to reinfection","Confining the differential to typhus and missing this treatable Bartonella infection"],"management":["Antimicrobial therapy with a tetracycline such as doxycycline, combined with an aminoglycoside (gentamicin) for confirmed bacteraemia, to clear the intraerythrocytic Bartonella quintana infection","Prolonged combination antibiotic therapy for Bartonella quintana endocarditis, with valve surgery where there is significant valvular damage or heart failure","Eradicate the body louse infestation through delousing, laundering or disposal of clothing and improved hygiene to prevent relapse and reinfection","Address underlying social determinants such as homelessness and alcohol dependence that predispose to louse exposure","Perform echocardiography and monitor when endocarditis is suspected, given the risk of culture-negative valvular infection","Provide supportive care and analgesia for the self-limiting febrile illness in the immunocompetent host"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"trench_foot":{"name":"Trench foot (non-freezing cold injury)","system":"Environmental / Emergency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["non-freezing cold injury","NFCI","cold immersion foot"],"chapter":null,"source":"Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine / Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A non-freezing cold injury caused by prolonged exposure of the feet to cold, wet conditions, classically described in soldiers standing in cold water-filled trenches","Develops at temperatures above freezing, so tissue does not freeze; the injury is from sustained cold and damp rather than ice crystal formation","Early on the foot is cold, numb, pale, and often swollen; on rewarming it becomes hyperaemic, red, painful, and may blister","A characteristic biphasic course of initial anaesthesia followed by a painful hyperaemic phase is typical","Management is gradual rewarming, keeping the foot dry, elevation, meticulous foot care, and analgesia for the painful phase","Prevention centres on keeping feet dry and warm, regularly changing wet socks, and avoiding constrictive footwear","Severe or repeated injury can leave long-term cold sensitivity, chronic pain, and sensory disturbance"],"pathophysiology":"Trench foot follows prolonged exposure of tissue to cold, wet conditions without freezing. Sustained cold-induced vasoconstriction reduces perfusion, and the combination of hypoxia, endothelial injury, and alternating vasoconstriction and reperfusion damages nerves, small vessels, and soft tissue. Because temperatures remain above freezing there is no ice crystal formation, distinguishing it from frostbite; instead the injury is a microvascular and neural insult that, when severe, can progress to tissue necrosis and leaves the characteristic biphasic clinical course of numb ischaemia followed by a painful hyperaemic reperfusion phase.","additionalDifferentials":["Frostbite (a true freezing injury)","Immersion foot from warm-water exposure","Chilblains (pernio)","Cellulitis or other soft tissue infection","Peripheral vascular disease with critical ischaemia","Compartment syndrome after prolonged immobility"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from history of prolonged cold, wet exposure and characteristic foot findings","Assessment of peripheral pulses, capillary refill, and neurological (sensory) function","Skin and soft tissue examination for blistering, necrosis, or secondary infection","Inflammatory markers and wound swabs if secondary infection is suspected","Vascular imaging where an alternative ischaemic cause is considered"],"redFlags":["Tissue necrosis, deep blistering, or evidence of secondary infection","Features suggesting a true freezing injury (frostbite) requiring different management","Signs of systemic infection or sepsis from an infected wound","Severe, persistent pain or sensory loss suggesting significant nerve injury"],"prognosis":"Mild cases recover over days to weeks with rewarming and foot care, but moderate to severe non-freezing cold injury may leave prolonged cold hypersensitivity, chronic neuropathic pain, and sensory deficits that can persist for months or longer.","pitfalls":["Confusing trench foot with frostbite and mismanaging the rewarming approach","Applying rapid or excessive direct heat, worsening reperfusion injury","Underestimating the painful hyperaemic phase and providing inadequate analgesia","Neglecting preventive foot care, allowing recurrence"],"management":["Gradual rewarming with the foot kept dry, warm and elevated, avoiding rapid heat or rubbing","Meticulous foot and wound care with monitoring for blistering, tissue necrosis and secondary infection","Analgesia for the painful hyperaemic reperfusion phase, including agents for neuropathic pain","Treat secondary soft-tissue infection with antibiotics guided by wound swabs where it develops","Prevention through keeping the feet dry and warm, regularly changing wet socks and avoiding constrictive footwear","Recognise and manage chronic sequelae such as persistent cold hypersensitivity and neuropathic pain, with specialist referral for severe or non-healing injury"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Auerbach's Wilderness Medicine","Bove and Davis' Diving Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"triangular_fibrocartilage_complex_injury":{"name":"Triangular fibrocartilage complex injury","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TFCC injury","TFCC tear"],"chapter":null,"source":"Hand surgery and sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The triangular fibrocartilage complex stabilises the distal radioulnar joint and cushions the ulnar wrist, and its injury causes ulnar-sided wrist pain","Symptoms include ulnar wrist pain worse with rotation and gripping, clicking and sometimes distal radioulnar joint instability","Injuries are broadly traumatic or degenerative, the latter associated with positive ulnar variance and ulnar impaction","The fovea sign, with tenderness in the soft spot between the ulnar styloid and flexor carpi ulnaris, supports the diagnosis","MRI, and definitively wrist arthroscopy, characterise the tear and its location","Peripheral traumatic tears in the vascular zone can heal or be repaired, whereas central and degenerative tears are managed differently"],"pathophysiology":"The triangular fibrocartilage complex comprises the articular disc, ligaments and sheath that support the distal radioulnar joint and transmit load across the ulnar wrist. Acute injury from a fall onto the extended wrist or forceful rotation tears the complex, while chronic degenerative tears develop where excess ulnar length increases load on the disc (ulnar impaction). The peripheral portion is vascular and can heal, whereas the central avascular disc does not, which determines whether repair or debridement is appropriate. Loss of complex integrity may destabilise the distal radioulnar joint.","additionalDifferentials":["Ulnar impaction syndrome","Distal radioulnar joint instability of other cause","Extensor carpi ulnaris tendon pathology","Lunotriquetral ligament injury","Hook of hamate fracture or pisotriquetral pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination including the fovea sign and distal radioulnar joint stability testing","Radiographs assessing ulnar variance","MRI, ideally with arthrography, to image the tear","Wrist arthroscopy as the definitive diagnostic and often therapeutic tool","Comparison with the contralateral wrist for instability"],"redFlags":["Distal radioulnar joint instability","Associated fracture","Progressive pain and weakness despite conservative care","Features suggesting an alternative structural injury"],"prognosis":"Many injuries respond to a period of immobilisation and rehabilitation; peripheral repairs and procedures to address ulnar impaction generally do well, whereas untreated instability can lead to chronic pain and arthritis.","pitfalls":["Attributing ulnar wrist pain to a simple sprain and missing the diagnosis","Overlooking distal radioulnar joint instability","Not recognising ulnar impaction as the driver of a degenerative tear","Expecting central avascular tears to heal like peripheral ones"],"management":["Initial conservative management with wrist immobilisation in a splint or cast, activity modification and analgesia or a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug","Rehabilitation and physiotherapy to restore range of movement, grip strength and forearm rotation","Arthroscopic repair of peripheral traumatic tears in the vascular zone, which have healing potential","Arthroscopic debridement of irreparable central and degenerative tears","Ulnar shortening osteotomy or a wafer procedure to offload the disc in positive ulnar variance with ulnar impaction","Stabilise the distal radioulnar joint where the injury has caused instability","Refer to hand surgery for wrist arthroscopy, which serves as both definitive diagnosis and treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Hand surgery reference texts (standard reference)","Sports medicine references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichiasis":{"name":"Trichiasis","system":"Eye / Orbit and adnexa","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["misdirected eyelashes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Posterior misdirection of eyelashes from a normally positioned lid margin, so lashes rub against the ocular surface","Causes foreign-body sensation, watering, redness and, if chronic, corneal abrasion and scarring","It differs from entropion, in which the whole lid margin turns inward","Common causes include chronic blepharitis, cicatrising conditions and, globally, trachoma","Trachomatous trichiasis is a leading infective cause of blindness worldwide and a target of elimination programmes","Management ranges from epilation and cryotherapy or electrolysis to lid surgery for cicatricial disease"],"pathophysiology":"Trichiasis arises when eyelash follicles or the lid margin are distorted so that lashes grow toward the globe despite a normally positioned lid. Chronic inflammation, scarring or cicatrisation of the lid margin, as in trachoma, chronic blepharitis or other cicatrising conjunctival diseases, redirects the lashes posteriorly. The abrasive lashes irritate the cornea and conjunctiva, and persistent rubbing can cause epithelial breakdown, corneal scarring and visual loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Entropion","Epiblepharon","Distichiasis","Cicatricial conjunctival disease (for example trachoma, ocular mucous membrane pemphigoid)","Chronic blepharitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination of lash direction, lid margin and cornea","Fluorescein staining to assess corneal involvement","Assessment for underlying cicatricial or infective cause","Eversion of the lid to exclude entropion and scarring","Consideration of biopsy where a cicatrising immunobullous disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["Corneal ulceration or scarring threatening vision","Features of a progressive cicatrising conjunctival disease","Extensive or recurrent disease","Signs suggesting ocular mucous membrane pemphigoid"],"prognosis":"Isolated trichiasis is readily treated by lash removal or ablation, though recurrence is common; cicatricial disease requires treating the underlying cause and may need lid surgery to protect the cornea.","pitfalls":["Confusing trichiasis with entropion, which needs different surgery","Missing an underlying cicatrising disease such as mucous membrane pemphigoid","Neglecting corneal protection in chronic cases","Relying on simple epilation alone, which is often followed by recurrence"],"management":["Mechanical lash removal by epilation for immediate relief, recognising that recurrence follows regrowth","Definitive ablation of aberrant follicles by electrolysis, cryotherapy or laser for localised, recurrent lashes","Lid rotation surgery such as bilamellar tarsal rotation for cicatricial and trachomatous trichiasis affecting many lashes","Treat the underlying cause, whether chronic blepharitis, cicatrising conjunctival disease, or trachoma with its antibiotic (azithromycin) and the wider SAFE elimination strategy","Protect the ocular surface with lubricants and, where needed, a bandage contact lens to prevent corneal abrasion and scarring","Monitor the cornea at the slit lamp with fluorescein staining for epithelial breakdown and scarring","Refer to oculoplastics for surgical correction and follow-up given the high recurrence rate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","WHO trachoma elimination guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichinellosis":{"name":"Trichinellosis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["trichinosis","Trichinella spiralis infection","trichiniasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases; CDC parasitic disease guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A zoonotic tissue nematode infection acquired by eating raw or undercooked meat containing Trichinella larvae, classically pork but also wild game such as bear and boar","The early intestinal phase causes diarrhoea, abdominal pain and nausea within days of ingestion","The later muscle-invasion phase produces fever, myalgia, weakness and the characteristic periorbital and facial oedema","Marked peripheral eosinophilia and raised muscle enzymes (creatine kinase) are typical laboratory findings","Splinter haemorrhages, conjunctival and subungual haemorrhages, and a rash may be seen","Serology becomes positive over the first weeks; muscle biopsy showing encysted larvae is diagnostic when needed","Treatment combines anthelmintics such as albendazole or mebendazole with corticosteroids to control the inflammatory reaction in severe disease","Serious complications include myocarditis, encephalitis and respiratory muscle involvement, which are the main causes of death"],"pathophysiology":"Trichinellosis begins when a person eats raw or undercooked meat containing encysted Trichinella larvae. Gastric digestion frees the larvae, which mature into adult worms in the small intestine; the females release newborn larvae that penetrate the mucosa and disseminate through the bloodstream and lymphatics. These larvae preferentially invade striated skeletal muscle, where they encyst within modified muscle cells (nurse cells), provoking an intense eosinophilic and inflammatory response. This muscle invasion accounts for the myositis, myalgia and periorbital oedema, while migration through other tissues can inflame the myocardium, central nervous system and respiratory muscles, explaining the potentially fatal complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Dermatomyositis or polymyositis","Other causes of eosinophilia with myalgia (eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome, hypereosinophilic syndrome)","Influenza or other viral myositis","Leptospirosis","Angioedema or allergic reaction (periorbital swelling)","Other tissue-invasive helminth infections","Systemic vasculitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film for eosinophilia","Serum creatine kinase and other muscle enzymes","Trichinella serology (antibody testing)","Detailed dietary history for raw or undercooked pork or game","Muscle biopsy for encysted larvae in uncertain cases","ECG and cardiac assessment, and neurological evaluation, when complications are suspected"],"redFlags":["Chest pain, arrhythmia or heart failure suggesting myocarditis","Headache, confusion or focal deficits suggesting encephalitis","Breathlessness from respiratory muscle involvement","Severe systemic illness with very high eosinophilia and muscle enzyme elevation"],"prognosis":"Most cases are mild and self-limiting with a good outcome, but heavy infections with myocardial, neurological or respiratory involvement can be life-threatening; prompt treatment shortens illness and reduces complications.","pitfalls":["Missing the dietary history of undercooked pork or wild game","Mistaking the myositis and periorbital oedema for dermatomyositis or an allergic reaction","Relying on early serology, which may be negative in the first days of illness","Failing to give anthelmintics early, before larvae encyst in muscle, when the diagnosis is suspected","Overlooking cardiac or neurological complications that determine prognosis"],"management":["Start a benzimidazole anthelmintic (albendazole or mebendazole) promptly to kill intestinal adult worms and curtail release of newborn larvae; efficacy is greatest early before muscle encystment","Add systemic corticosteroids for severe inflammatory disease, particularly myocarditis, central nervous system involvement or marked myositis, to suppress the intense eosinophilic hypersensitivity response","Provide supportive symptomatic care with antipyretics and simple analgesia for fever and myalgia, together with bed rest and adequate hydration","Institute early ECG and cardiac monitoring and watch for myocardial, neurological and respiratory-muscle complications, tracking eosinophilia and creatine kinase","Escalate to critical care for myocarditis with arrhythmia or heart failure, encephalitis or respiratory-muscle weakness","Refer to infectious diseases or tropical medicine, involving cardiology and neurology when organ involvement is suspected","Notify public health, trace the implicated meat source and advise thorough cooking or freezing of pork and game to prevent further cases and treat co-exposed individuals"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","CDC guidance on trichinellosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichinellosis_and_other_tissue_nematode":{"name":"Trichinellosis and Other Tissue Nematode Infections","system":"Infectious Disease (Parasitology / Tissue Helminths)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Trichinosis","Trichinella infection","Tissue nematode infections","Visceral larva migrans","Ocular larva migrans","Toxocariasis","Cutaneous larva migrans","Creeping eruption","Cerebral angiostrongyliasis","Rat lungworm disease","Gnathostomiasis"],"chapter":238,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1810","clinicalPearls":["Trichinellosis follows ingestion of meat (classically pork, but increasingly wild game such as bear and walrus) containing encysted Trichinella larvae; most infections are mild or asymptomatic, but heavy burdens can be life-threatening (Harrison 22e p.1810).","Disease severity tracks larval burden: light infections (<10 larvae/g muscle) tend to be asymptomatic, whereas heavy infections (>50 larvae/g muscle) can be life-threatening (Harrison 22e p.1811).","Clinical course is biphasic: an early enteric phase (diarrhea, abdominal pain, constipation, nausea) in the first weeks, followed in the second week by larval-migration symptoms (fever, periorbital/facial edema, eosinophilia) and then a myositis phase as larvae encyst (Harrison 22e p.1811).","Periorbital and facial edema with subconjunctival, retinal, and nail-bed (splinter) hemorrhages are characteristic of the migratory phase of trichinellosis (Harrison 22e p.1811).","Most deaths in trichinellosis result from myocarditis (with tachyarrhythmias or heart failure) and, less commonly, encephalitis or pneumonitis (Harrison 22e p.1811).","Muscle involvement preferentially affects the extraocular muscles, biceps, and the jaw, neck, lower back, and diaphragm muscles, with symptoms peaking around 3 weeks (Harrison 22e p.1811).","Visceral larva migrans (most often Toxocara canis) chiefly affects preschool children who ingest soil contaminated with embryonated eggs, producing fever, hepatosplenomegaly, wheezing, and extraordinary eosinophilia that can approach 90% (Harrison 22e p.1812).","Ocular larva migrans typically occurs in older children/young adults without pica and usually lacks eosinophilia or visceral signs; the retinal granuloma can mimic retinoblastoma and lead to unnecessary enucleation if misdiagnosed (Harrison 22e p.1812).","Cutaneous larva migrans ('creeping eruption'), usually from the dog/cat hookworm Ancylostoma braziliense, produces an intensely pruritic serpiginous track that advances several centimeters per day; the larvae cannot mature in humans and self-resolve over weeks to a couple of months (Harrison 22e p.1812).","Angiostrongylus cantonensis (rat lungworm) is the most common cause of human eosinophilic meningitis; CSF shows elevated opening pressure, pleocytosis (150-2000 cells/uL) with eosinophilic pleocytosis >20%, elevated protein, and normal glucose (Harrison 22e p.1813).","Gnathostomiasis is acquired from raw fish/poultry (e.g., som fak, sashimi) and causes migratory, recurrent pruritic cutaneous swellings; CNS invasion produces eosinophilic myeloencephalitis with agonizing radicular pain followed by paraplegia, and cerebral involvement is often fatal (Harrison 22e p.1813).","Stool examination is worthless in toxocariasis because the larvae never develop into egg-producing adults in the human host (Harrison 22e p.1812)."],"pathophysiology":"These are zoonotic tissue-dwelling nematode infections arising from incidental human exposure; symptoms derive largely from invasive larval stages that (except for Trichinella) do not mature in humans. In trichinellosis, ingested encysted larvae are freed by gastric acid and proteases, invade small-bowel mucosa, mature to adults, and after about one week females release newborn larvae that migrate via the circulation to striated muscle, where most species encyst by radically transforming muscle-cell architecture into a 'nurse cell'; host immune responses help expel adult intestinal worms but have little effect on muscle larvae. Migrating larvae provoke a marked local and systemic hypersensitivity reaction with eosinophilia. In visceral larva migrans, ingested Toxocara eggs hatch, penetrate the gut, and disseminate to liver, lungs, CNS, and other tissues, eliciting intense eosinophilic granulomatous inflammation. In cutaneous larva migrans, animal hookworm larvae penetrate skin and burrow along the dermal-epidermal junction. In angiostrongyliasis, ingested infective larvae migrate to the brain and die there, inciting eosinophilic inflammation, hemorrhage, necrosis, and granuloma formation. In gnathostomiasis, aberrant migration of a single larva into cutaneous, visceral, neural, or ocular tissue causes local inflammation, including ascending migration along nerve tracts producing myeloencephalitis (Harrison 22e p.1810-1813).","additionalDifferentials":["Polymyositis / dermatomyositis (myalgia, weakness, elevated muscle enzymes) - note T. pseudospiralis can mimic a prolonged polymyositis-like illness","Periorbital edema from other causes (allergic, nephrotic, angioedema)","Acute myocarditis of other etiology","Eosinophilic meningitis from other parasites (see infectionMimics)","Retinoblastoma (mimicked by ocular toxocariasis retinal granuloma)","Other hypereosinophilic syndromes"],"infectionMimics":["Other causes of eosinophilic meningitis in endemic areas: gnathostomiasis, paragonimiasis, schistosomiasis, neurocysticercosis, and coccidioidomycosis","Other eosinophilia-associated helminthiases (e.g., strongyloidiasis, filariasis)","Pleurodynia (coxsackievirus B) and bacterial myositis as causes of myalgia overlapping with trichinellosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Idiopathic inflammatory myopathies (polymyositis, dermatomyositis)","Retinoblastoma (versus ocular larva migrans)","Allergic/angioedematous periorbital swelling","Idiopathic hypereosinophilic syndrome"],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood eosinophilia (develops in >90% of symptomatic trichinellosis, may peak at >50% at 2-4 weeks)","Serum muscle enzymes including creatine phosphokinase (elevated in most symptomatic trichinellosis)","Trichinella parasite-specific serology (titer rise usually only after the third week of infection)","Muscle biopsy (>=1 g, near tendon insertions in symptomatic muscle) with fresh tissue compressed between glass slides and examined microscopically, since routine histology may miss larvae","ELISA for toxocaral antibodies (may be negative in ocular larva migrans); leukocytosis and hypergammaglobulinemia in visceral larva migrans; chest x-ray for transient pulmonary infiltrates","CSF examination for angiostrongyliasis (opening pressure, cell count, eosinophilic pleocytosis, protein, glucose); A. cantonensis PCR on CSF/tissue (available via CDC and Hawaii Dept of Public Health)"],"redFlags":["Myocarditis with tachyarrhythmias or heart failure (leading cause of death in trichinellosis)","Encephalitis or pneumonitis in trichinellosis","Dysphagia, dyspnea, or diaphragmatic muscle involvement","Severe neurologic, pneumonic, or myocardial involvement in visceral larva migrans (rare deaths)","Ascending radicular pain and paresthesias followed by paraplegia in gnathostomiasis (myeloencephalitis)","Cerebral involvement with focal hemorrhages in gnathostomiasis (often fatal)","Permanent neurologic sequelae from heavy CNS angiostrongyliasis"],"prognosis":"Most lightly infected trichinellosis patients recover uneventfully, with symptoms peaking near 3 weeks and subsiding gradually over a prolonged convalescence; heavy infections can be life-threatening, with death usually due to myocarditis. Most visceral larva migrans infections are self-limited (rare deaths from severe organ involvement). Cutaneous larva migrans self-resolves over weeks to a couple of months as the larvae die. Cerebral angiostrongyliasis is usually self-limited with complete recovery, though heavy infections can leave permanent neurologic sequelae or cause death. Gnathostomiasis cutaneous swellings can recur intermittently over years; CNS gnathostomiasis is a serious complication and cerebral involvement is often fatal (Harrison 22e p.1811-1813).","pitfalls":["Most mild trichinellosis cases remain undiagnosed; a high index of suspicion and a careful dietary/exposure history are needed.","Trichinella serology is typically negative until after the third week, so a presumptive clinical diagnosis (fever, eosinophilia, periorbital edema, myalgia after a suspect meal) may be required early.","Stool examination is useless in toxocariasis because larvae do not mature into egg-producing adults in humans.","Ocular toxocariasis can be mistaken for retinoblastoma, risking unnecessary enucleation; it often lacks eosinophilia and visceral signs.","In angiostrongyliasis, A. cantonensis larvae are only rarely seen in CSF and serologic tests are unstandardized and may be negative at symptom onset.","Arctic T. nativa larvae in walrus or bear meat are relatively freeze-resistant and may remain viable despite freezing, so usual freezing prevention may fail.","Gnathostomiasis may present with ocular or CNS involvement without antecedent cutaneous swellings."],"management":["For trichinellosis, give a benzimidazole anthelmintic (albendazole or mebendazole) early to eradicate intestinal adults; for symptomatic visceral or ocular Toxocara larva migrans use albendazole, and treat trichostrongyle-type infections with the same benzimidazole class","Use systemic corticosteroids to dampen the marked larval-migration hypersensitivity in severe trichinellosis (myocarditis, encephalitis) and in ocular or severe visceral larva migrans","In angiostrongyliasis-type eosinophilic meningitis, prioritise supportive care with analgesia, corticosteroids and repeated therapeutic lumbar puncture for headache relief, as anthelmintics are of uncertain benefit","Provide symptomatic care with antipyretics, analgesia and hydration, and correct anaemia or malnutrition in heavier intestinal infections","Monitor for cardiac, neurological, respiratory and ocular complications, following eosinophilia and muscle enzymes, and escalate to critical care for organ-threatening disease","Refer to infectious diseases or tropical medicine, engaging cardiology, neurology or ophthalmology according to the tissue involved","Advise prevention through thorough cooking or freezing of meat, avoidance of raw snails, slugs and contaminated produce, hand hygiene and deworming of pets to interrupt zoonotic transmission"],"severityClassification":"Trichinellosis severity correlates with muscle larval burden: light/asymptomatic infection <10 larvae per gram of muscle; heavy, potentially life-threatening infection >50 larvae per gram of muscle. Clinically graded as mild, moderate, and severe (severe = significant myositis/myocarditis) per Harrison's tissue nematode framework (Harrison 22e p.1811-1812).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1810","Harrison 22e p.1811","Harrison 22e p.1812","Harrison 22e p.1813"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichodysplasia_spinulosa":{"name":"Trichodysplasia spinulosa (polyomavirus)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["trichodysplasia spinulosa polyomavirus infection","TSPyV infection","viral-associated trichodysplasia","pilomatrix dysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare skin disorder caused by the trichodysplasia spinulosa polyomavirus, which infects the inner root sheath cells of hair follicles","It occurs almost exclusively in immunocompromised patients, particularly solid-organ transplant recipients and those on intensive immunosuppression for haematological disease","The characteristic finding is numerous folliculocentric papules bearing keratinous spines, concentrated on the central face, especially the nose, eyebrows, and ears","Progressive involvement produces a thickened, spiny (leonine) facial appearance with loss of eyebrow and eyelash hair (madarosis) and alopecia","Histology shows dystrophic, enlarged hair follicles with abnormally proliferating inner root sheath cells containing trichohyaline granules and viral inclusions","Diagnosis is supported by skin biopsy, electron microscopy showing polyomavirus particles, and PCR detection of the trichodysplasia spinulosa polyomavirus","Management centres on reducing immunosuppression where possible, with topical antiviral or retinoid therapy reported to help; it can regress as immune function recovers"],"pathophysiology":"Trichodysplasia spinulosa is driven by active replication of a specific human polyomavirus that is probably acquired asymptomatically and remains latent in the skin of most people. When cell-mediated immunity is suppressed, as after organ transplantation, the virus reactivates and productively infects the inner root sheath cells of hair follicles. Uncontrolled viral replication drives abnormal proliferation and differentiation of these cells, with excessive accumulation of trichohyaline and formation of keratinous spines that protrude from distended, dystrophic follicles. The follicular architecture is disrupted, producing the spiny papules, facial thickening, and loss of hair characteristic of the condition. Because the disease depends on the failure of immune control, restoring immune function or reducing immunosuppression typically allows the follicular changes to regress.","additionalDifferentials":["Lichen spinulosus and keratosis pilaris","Multiple trichoepitheliomas or other adnexal tumours","Follicular mycosis fungoides","Phrynoderma (vitamin A deficiency) and other follicular hyperkeratoses","Sarcoidosis or other granulomatous facial dermatoses","Drug-induced follicular eruptions in transplant recipients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with histopathology of dystrophic follicles and abnormal inner root sheath proliferation","Electron microscopy demonstrating intranuclear polyomavirus particles","PCR for trichodysplasia spinulosa polyomavirus DNA from skin or spines","Review of the immunosuppressive regimen and degree of net immunosuppression","Assessment of underlying transplant or haematological condition","Dermatological examination to map the extent of facial and hair involvement"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive facial disfigurement and hair loss","Extensive disease indicating heavy net immunosuppression","Diagnostic uncertainty raising concern for a follicular malignancy","Secondary bacterial infection of affected skin"],"prognosis":"The condition is disfiguring but not life-threatening, and it commonly improves or regresses when immunosuppression is reduced or immune function recovers; without such change it tends to persist and progress.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the facial papules for keratosis pilaris or a benign follicular disorder and missing the diagnosis","Failing to link the eruption to the patient's immunosuppression","Not obtaining a biopsy or molecular testing to confirm the polyomavirus","Overlooking the potential for regression on reducing immunosuppression and pursuing unnecessary aggressive treatment"],"management":["Reduce the net immunosuppression wherever transplant graft safety allows, as restoring cell-mediated immunity to control polyomavirus replication is the mainstay and often drives regression","Apply topical cidofovir to affected skin, or consider oral valganciclovir, as antiviral options reported to improve the folliculocentric spiny papules","Use topical retinoids or keratolytics and gentle physical removal of the keratinous spines for symptomatic and cosmetic improvement","Coordinate any change in immunosuppressive regimen with the transplant or haematology team, balancing viral control against the risk of graft rejection or disease relapse","Provide dermatological care with mapping and monitoring of facial and hair involvement, and surveillance for recurrence if immunosuppression is later intensified","Manage jointly through dermatology and the underlying transplant or haematology service, with counselling about the disfiguring but non-life-threatening nature of the condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","UpToDate: Trichodysplasia spinulosa and human polyomavirus skin disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichoepithelioma":{"name":"Trichoepithelioma","system":"Dermatology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["trichoepithelioma","multiple familial trichoepithelioma","epithelioma adenoides cysticum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / WHO Classification of Skin Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign tumour of follicular (hair-germ) differentiation, presenting as skin-coloured firm papules or nodules","The multiple familial form clusters symmetrically on the central face, especially the nasolabial folds, nose, and forehead, appearing around puberty","Multiple familial trichoepithelioma is linked to CYLD mutations and overlaps with Brooke-Spiegler syndrome and familial cylindromatosis","The chief diagnostic challenge is distinguishing it histologically from basal cell carcinoma, which it closely mimics","Helpful discriminators favouring trichoepithelioma include papillary mesenchymal bodies, a fibrocytic stroma, absence of a peritumoural cleft, and stromal amyloid","Immunohistochemistry, for example CD10 stromal versus tumoural patterns and other markers, can aid the distinction from basal cell carcinoma in difficult cases","Solitary lesions are usually excised, while multiple facial lesions are managed for cosmetic reasons and tend to recur"],"pathophysiology":"Trichoepithelioma is a benign adnexal neoplasm derived from hair-germ (follicular) epithelium. It is composed of basaloid cells arranged in nests and cords with follicular differentiation, characteristically forming keratin-filled horn cysts and papillary mesenchymal bodies within a cellular fibrocytic stroma. The multiple familial variant arises from germline loss-of-function mutations in the CYLD tumour-suppressor gene, the same gene underlying cylindromas and spiradenomas, so it forms part of the CYLD-related spectrum with Brooke-Spiegler syndrome; loss of CYLD deregulates nuclear factor-kappa B signalling and promotes follicular tumour formation. Although architecturally and cytologically it resembles basal cell carcinoma, it lacks the aggressive infiltrative and destructive behaviour of that malignancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Basal cell carcinoma (especially the keratotic and infiltrative types)","Cylindroma","Trichoblastoma","Desmoplastic trichoepithelioma versus microcystic adnexal carcinoma","Syringoma","Angiofibromas (as in tuberous sclerosis) for facial papules"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin examination noting central-facial symmetry and multiplicity","Skin biopsy with histopathology showing horn cysts and papillary mesenchymal bodies","Immunohistochemistry (for example CD10 and other markers) to distinguish from basal cell carcinoma","Family history and examination for cylindromas and spiradenomas","CYLD genetic testing when multiple familial trichoepithelioma or Brooke-Spiegler syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["A solitary lesion enlarging, ulcerating, or behaving aggressively, raising concern for basal cell carcinoma","New infiltrative or destructive change in a previously stable lesion","Family clustering of adnexal tumours indicating a hereditary CYLD syndrome"],"prognosis":"Trichoepitheliomas are benign with an excellent prognosis; solitary lesions are cured by excision, whereas multiple familial lesions persist and recur and cause cosmetic morbidity. Malignant transformation is rare.","pitfalls":["Overdiagnosing basal cell carcinoma on a superficial or partial biopsy and pursuing unnecessary aggressive treatment","Conversely, dismissing a genuine basal cell carcinoma as a trichoepithelioma","Missing the hereditary CYLD context in patients with multiple facial lesions","Confusing desmoplastic trichoepithelioma with microcystic adnexal carcinoma on limited tissue"],"management":["Manage a solitary lesion by complete surgical excision, which is curative and provides histology to confirm the diagnosis","For multiple familial lesions, use ablative or destructive techniques such as carbon dioxide laser, electrosurgery or dermabrasion to reduce cosmetic burden, accepting that lesions tend to recur","Maintain histological vigilance with biopsy of atypical or changing lesions to distinguish from and detect basal cell carcinoma and the rare malignant transformation","Offer CYLD genetic testing and counselling, and screen relatives and the patient for cylindromas and spiradenomas within the Brooke-Spiegler spectrum","Provide psychological support and reassurance for the cosmetic morbidity of extensive central-facial disease","Follow up through dermatology and dermatopathology, with plastic surgery input for reconstruction where lesions are numerous or disfiguring"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","WHO Classification of Skin Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichomoniasis":{"name":"Trichomoniasis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["trich","Trichomonas vaginalis infection","trichomonal vaginitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India NTEP","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by the flagellated protozoan Trichomonas vaginalis and spread almost exclusively through sexual contact, so it should prompt screening for other sexually transmitted infections","Women often present with a malodorous, frothy yellow-green discharge, vulvovaginal itching and dysuria, though a large proportion are asymptomatic","The classic 'strawberry cervix' (punctate cervical haemorrhages) is highly suggestive but seen in only a minority of cases","Most infected men are asymptomatic carriers but may develop urethritis, making partner treatment essential to prevent reinfection","Diagnosis is traditionally by observing motile trophozoites on saline wet mount, but nucleic acid amplification testing is far more sensitive and is now preferred where available","Treatment is with an oral nitroimidazole (metronidazole or tinidazole); partners should be treated simultaneously and intercourse avoided until both complete therapy","Infection in pregnancy is associated with preterm birth and low birth weight, and it increases susceptibility to HIV acquisition and transmission"],"pathophysiology":"Trichomonas vaginalis is an anaerobic, flagellated protozoan that adheres to and damages squamous epithelium of the urogenital tract. Cytoadherence and release of proteinases and other virulence factors trigger a local inflammatory response with neutrophil infiltration, producing the characteristic discharge and mucosal microhaemorrhages. The resulting disruption of the epithelial barrier and mucosal inflammation facilitates acquisition and shedding of HIV and other pathogens. The organism cannot survive long outside a moist environment, which underlies its predominantly sexual mode of transmission.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial vaginosis","Vulvovaginal candidiasis","Gonococcal or chlamydial cervicitis","Atrophic vaginitis","Non-specific urethritis in men"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial vaginosis with malodorous discharge","Candida vulvovaginitis causing itch and discharge","Chlamydial or gonococcal cervicitis or urethritis","Mycoplasma genitalium urethritis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Saline wet-mount microscopy for motile trophozoites and increased polymorphs","Nucleic acid amplification testing of vaginal or urethral specimens as the most sensitive method","Vaginal pH and whiff testing to help distinguish from bacterial vaginosis","Concurrent screening for HIV, syphilis, gonorrhoea and chlamydia","Culture in selective medium where molecular testing is unavailable"],"redFlags":["Infection during pregnancy given the association with preterm delivery and low birth weight","Persistent symptoms after treatment suggesting reinfection or nitroimidazole resistance","Coexisting HIV infection or features suggesting other serious sexually transmitted disease","Signs of pelvic inflammatory disease indicating an alternative or additional pathogen"],"prognosis":"With appropriate nitroimidazole therapy and simultaneous partner treatment the cure rate is high; recurrence usually reflects reinfection or non-adherence rather than true drug resistance, which is uncommon but increasingly recognised.","pitfalls":["Relying on wet-mount microscopy alone, which misses many infections because of its limited sensitivity","Treating the index patient without treating sexual partners, leading to reinfection","Overlooking asymptomatic infection, particularly in men, who sustain transmission","Failing to screen for coexisting sexually transmitted infections and HIV"],"management":["Treat with an oral nitroimidazole (metronidazole or tinidazole) as definitive antiprotozoal therapy, counselling on avoidance of alcohol during and shortly after treatment","Treat all recent sexual partners simultaneously and advise abstaining from intercourse until both partners have completed therapy, to prevent reinfection","Screen for and manage coexisting sexually transmitted infections including HIV, syphilis, gonorrhoea and chlamydia","Arrange retesting for cure given the high reinfection rate, and treat symptomatic pregnant women, involving obstetrics for regimen choice","For persistent infection after adherent treatment, use a prolonged or higher-intensity tinidazole-based regimen and seek specialist advice on the uncommon nitroimidazole resistance","Counsel on consistent condom use and safer-sex practices to reduce recurrence and onward transmission"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/NTEP guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichorhinophalangeal_syndrome":{"name":"Trichorhinophalangeal syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TRPS","Langer-Giedion syndrome (TRPS type II)","trichorhinophalangeal syndrome types I-III","TRPS1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The name captures the triad of hair (tricho-), nose (rhino-), and digit (phalangeal) abnormalities","Characteristic features are sparse, slow-growing scalp hair, a bulbous pear-shaped nose, and a long flat philtrum","The hallmark radiographic finding is cone-shaped epiphyses of the phalanges, leading to shortened fingers and deviation of the digits","Type I is caused by TRPS1 variants; type III is a more severe TRPS1 phenotype with pronounced brachydactyly and short stature","Type II (Langer-Giedion syndrome) results from a contiguous deletion involving TRPS1 and the adjacent EXT1 gene, adding multiple cartilaginous exostoses and intellectual disability","Premature degenerative joint disease of the hands and hips can develop","Hip abnormalities resembling Perthes disease may occur","Diagnosis is clinical and radiographic, confirmed by TRPS1 testing or deletion analysis; management is supportive and orthopaedic"],"pathophysiology":"Trichorhinophalangeal syndrome is caused by loss of function of TRPS1, a zinc-finger transcription factor that regulates chondrocyte and perichondrial development and other aspects of skeletal and ectodermal patterning. Reduced TRPS1 activity disturbs growth-plate function, producing the characteristic cone-shaped epiphyses, brachydactyly, short stature, and hip abnormalities, and also affects hair follicle development, giving the sparse fine hair. Type I arises from intragenic TRPS1 variants, while type III reflects variants with a more severe skeletal effect. Type II (Langer-Giedion syndrome) is a contiguous gene deletion that removes both TRPS1 and the neighbouring EXT1 gene, so the exostoses and cognitive impairment reflect loss of EXT1 in addition to the TRPS1 phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Acrodysostosis and other acromelic dysplasias","Ectodermal dysplasias with sparse hair","Multiple hereditary exostoses (isolated EXT1/EXT2)","Brachydactyly syndromes","Cartilage-hair hypoplasia","Peripheral dysostosis of other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Hand and skeletal radiographs demonstrating cone-shaped epiphyses","TRPS1 molecular genetic testing","Chromosomal microarray or deletion analysis for suspected Langer-Giedion syndrome","Hip imaging for degenerative or Perthes-like change","Developmental assessment where type II is suspected","Dermatological and hair assessment"],"redFlags":["Nerve or spinal-cord compression from exostoses in Langer-Giedion syndrome","Malignant transformation of an exostosis (rare) with new pain or growth","Progressive disabling hip or hand arthropathy","Significant developmental delay prompting evaluation for type II"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is normal; morbidity arises from short stature, hand deformity, and early degenerative joint disease, with additional risks from exostoses and cognitive impairment in the Langer-Giedion form.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnostic cone-shaped epiphyses on hand radiographs","Failing to distinguish type II (contiguous deletion with exostoses) from types I and III","Attributing the sparse hair to an isolated ectodermal disorder","Not arranging surveillance of exostoses in Langer-Giedion syndrome"],"management":["Recognise there is no disease-specific drug therapy, so care is supportive and directed at the skeletal, ectodermal and, in type II, exostosis manifestations","Manage hip disease and premature degenerative joint disease of the hands and hips with physiotherapy, analgesia and orthopaedic surgery including joint replacement where indicated","Monitor growth and short stature and assess through paediatric endocrinology, with hand therapy for brachydactyly and digital deformity","In Langer-Giedion (type II) disease, monitor and surgically excise symptomatic osteochondromas, remaining alert to malignant change, and provide developmental and educational support for intellectual disability","Offer dermatological and trichological advice for sparse, slow-growing hair, including camouflage or hairpieces","Coordinate care across clinical genetics, orthopaedics, endocrinology and dermatology","Provide genetic counselling and offer family screening with TRPS1 testing, or deletion and microarray analysis where the contiguous-gene form is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichorrhexis_nodosa":{"name":"Trichorrhexis nodosa","system":"Dermatology / Trichology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TN","hair-shaft nodal defect"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common hair-shaft abnormality in which small greyish-white nodes along the shaft represent sites of cortical fracture, giving hair that breaks easily","Under the microscope the nodes resemble the ends of two brushes or brooms pushed together at the fracture point, the classic paintbrush appearance","It may be proximal, distal or localised, and distal trichorrhexis nodosa from cumulative cosmetic and physical trauma such as heat, bleaching and brushing is the commonest form","Acquired forms follow physical or chemical trauma to otherwise normal or inherently weakened hair, whereas congenital forms accompany metabolic disorders such as argininosuccinic aciduria and other hair-shaft syndromes","Trichoscopy shows the nodes and transverse fractures with brush-like broken ends","Proximal trichorrhexis nodosa is more common in afro-textured hair and with certain metabolic conditions","Management focuses on eliminating traumatic hair practices and treating any underlying disorder, and hair usually recovers once trauma stops in acquired cases"],"pathophysiology":"Trichorrhexis nodosa arises when the protective cuticle is disrupted, exposing the cortex, whose fibres then splay apart to form the characteristic nodes and fracture transversely. It may reflect an inherent weakness of the shaft, as in inherited metabolic disorders affecting keratin sulphur content or amino-acid metabolism, or, more often, cumulative physical and chemical trauma from grooming, heat and cosmetic processing acting on normal or vulnerable hair. The result is localised structural failure and breakage.","additionalDifferentials":["Pili torti, with twisting but no nodes","Monilethrix, with regular beading","Trichoschisis and trichothiodystrophy","Weathering of the hair tip","Other causes of hair fragility and breakage"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Light microscopy of the shaft showing paired brush-like fracture ends at the nodes","Trichoscopy demonstrating nodes and transverse fractures","Detailed hair-care and cosmetic-processing history","Metabolic screening, for example for argininosuccinic aciduria, where a congenital cause is suspected","Assessment for associated hair-shaft syndromes or systemic disease"],"redFlags":["Widespread congenital fragility suggesting an underlying metabolic or genetic disorder","Associated developmental or neurological features","Failure to improve despite stopping trauma, prompting a search for an inherent shaft defect"],"prognosis":"Acquired distal trichorrhexis nodosa usually recovers once traumatic practices cease, whereas congenital or metabolic-associated forms depend on the underlying disorder and may be more persistent.","pitfalls":["Attributing breakage to hair loss rather than a shaft defect and so missing the true cause","Overlooking an underlying metabolic disorder in congenital cases","Not taking a full cosmetic and grooming history in acquired disease","Failing to examine the shaft microscopically to confirm the diagnosis"],"management":["Remove the causative mechanical and chemical trauma by stopping heat styling, bleaching, chemical relaxing and vigorous brushing, which is the mainstay for the common acquired distal form","Adopt gentle hair-care measures with mild shampoos, conditioners and low-tension styling to minimise cortical fracture and allow the shaft to recover","Investigate and treat an underlying metabolic disorder in congenital cases, such as dietary and substrate management of argininosuccinic aciduria under a metabolic team","Identify and correct associated systemic contributors such as iron deficiency and hypothyroidism","Monitor for regrowth of intact hair after harmful practices cease, which confirms the acquired mechanism","Refer to clinical genetics or metabolic medicine when a congenital hair-shaft or amino-acid disorder is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichostrongyliasis":{"name":"Trichostrongyliasis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Trichostrongylus infection","trichostrongylosis","pseudohookworm infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / Mandell","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by Trichostrongylus species, small nematodes chiefly of herbivores, with human infection common in rural farming communities of Asia, the Middle East and elsewhere","Acquired by ingesting infective larvae on contaminated vegetables and greens, in contrast to the skin-penetrating route of true hookworms","Most infections are light and asymptomatic; heavier burdens cause abdominal pain, diarrhoea, nausea and, occasionally, mild anaemia","Peripheral eosinophilia is common and may be the clue that prompts stool examination","The eggs resemble hookworm eggs but are larger with more pointed ends, and can be mistaken for them on routine microscopy","Treatment is with a benzimidazole anthelmintic such as albendazole, which is highly effective","Prevention relies on adequate sanitation, avoiding use of animal manure on food crops and washing or cooking vegetables"],"pathophysiology":"Trichostrongylus species are small nematodes that normally parasitise the intestines of sheep, goats, cattle and other herbivores. Eggs passed in the faeces of these animals develop in soil into infective larvae that contaminate low-growing vegetables and herbage. Humans acquire infection by ingesting these larvae rather than through skin penetration. In the small intestine the larvae mature into adult worms that attach superficially to the mucosa, where light infections cause little harm. Heavier worm burdens produce localised mucosal irritation and inflammation with abdominal symptoms and diarrhoea, and chronic infection can contribute to mild blood and protein loss and anaemia. Eggs shed in the faeces return to the soil to continue the cycle, sustained by both animal reservoirs and human contamination.","additionalDifferentials":["Hookworm infection (Ancylostoma or Necator), given similar eggs","Strongyloidiasis and other intestinal nematode infection","Giardiasis and other protozoal enteritis","Irritable bowel syndrome or functional dyspepsia","Other causes of eosinophilia with gastrointestinal symptoms","Coeliac disease or another cause of mild malabsorption"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy for ova and parasites, with concentration methods","Careful egg morphometry to distinguish from hookworm eggs","Full blood count for eosinophilia and anaemia","Larval culture of stool to confirm the species where required","Repeat stool sampling in light infection","Dietary and occupational history of exposure to livestock and raw vegetables"],"redFlags":["Significant anaemia in heavy or chronic infection","Persistent diarrhoea with weight loss","Symptoms unresponsive to appropriate anthelmintic therapy","Coexisting malnutrition, particularly in children"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent, as infection is usually mild and clears readily with anthelmintic treatment; heavy chronic infection resolves once the worm burden is eradicated.","pitfalls":["Misidentifying the eggs as hookworm and misdirecting management","Missing light infections on a single stool sample","Attributing eosinophilia to allergy rather than looking for intestinal helminths","Overlooking the animal reservoir and manure exposure when advising on prevention"],"management":["Treat with a benzimidazole anthelmintic such as albendazole or mebendazole, with pyrantel as an alternative, to eradicate the intestinal worm burden","Provide supportive care by correcting iron-deficiency anaemia and supporting nutrition in heavier or chronic infection","Confirm clearance with follow-up stool microscopy, repeating treatment if eggs persist","Screen and treat exposed household or community members in endemic farming settings","Advise prevention through thorough washing or cooking of vegetables and greens, improved sanitation and avoidance of fresh animal manure on crops to interrupt the soil cycle","Seek infectious diseases or tropical medicine input for heavy or diagnostically uncertain infections"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichothiodystrophy":{"name":"Trichothiodystrophy","system":"Genetics / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TTD","sulphur-deficient brittle hair syndrome","Tay syndrome","ERCC2","ERCC3","GTF2H5"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews (NCBI) and standard clinical genetics texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive multisystem disorder defined by sulphur-deficient brittle hair, frequently caused by variants in nucleotide excision repair genes such as ERCC2 (XPD), ERCC3 (XPB) or GTF2H5","Hair shows a diagnostic alternating light and dark banding (tiger-tail pattern) under polarised light microscopy","The clinical spectrum is captured by the mnemonic PIBIDS: photosensitivity, ichthyosis, brittle hair, intellectual impairment, decreased fertility and short stature","Photosensitivity occurs in a subset but, unlike xeroderma pigmentosum, is not associated with a raised skin-cancer risk","Neurological features include developmental delay, ataxia and dysmyelination on neuroimaging","Recurrent infections and a tendency to febrile illness are recognised, and some patients have features overlapping immunodeficiency","Analysis of hair amino acid content demonstrates a low cysteine and sulphur level, supporting the diagnosis","Diagnosis integrates the hair microscopy pattern, biochemical hair analysis, DNA-repair testing and molecular confirmation"],"pathophysiology":"Trichothiodystrophy most commonly results from variants affecting components of the transcription factor TFIIH, which functions in both nucleotide excision repair and basal transcription. Impaired TFIIH activity reduces synthesis of cysteine-rich structural proteins of the hair shaft, producing the sulphur deficiency and brittleness, while defective repair and transcription in other tissues account for the neuroectodermal, cutaneous and developmental manifestations. Non-photosensitive forms arise from variants in genes affecting hair-specific structural or regulatory proteins.","additionalDifferentials":["Xeroderma pigmentosum","Cockayne syndrome","Netherton syndrome","Menkes disease and other hair-shaft dysplasias","Congenital ichthyoses","Other syndromic causes of brittle hair with developmental delay"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Polarised light microscopy of the hair shaft for the tiger-tail banding pattern","Biochemical analysis of hair cysteine and sulphur content","Molecular genetic testing including ERCC2, ERCC3 and GTF2H5","Functional DNA-repair assay in photosensitive forms","MRI brain for dysmyelination","Developmental, immunological and growth assessment"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or severe infections suggesting associated immune compromise","Failure to thrive or feeding difficulty in infancy","Progressive neurological decline","Severe ichthyosis with skin barrier failure in the neonate"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies widely with severity, from early death in severe neonatal ichthyosiform presentations to survival into adulthood with milder disease. Recurrent infection contributes significantly to morbidity and mortality, and care is supportive.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis without polarised light hair microscopy","Assuming photosensitivity implies xeroderma pigmentosum and a high cancer risk","Missing associated immune deficiency and infection risk","Attributing the brittle hair to an acquired or nutritional cause"],"management":["Recognise there is no curative or disease-specific therapy, so provide coordinated supportive multisystem care tailored to each patient's manifestations","Institute strict photoprotection with broad-spectrum sunscreen, protective clothing and ultraviolet avoidance in the photosensitive subgroup","Manage the ichthyosis with emollients and keratolytics and provide gentle skin and hair care","Treat infections promptly and aggressively, ensure immunisations are up to date and monitor for features of immunodeficiency, given recurrent infection is a major cause of morbidity","Support neurodevelopment with physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech therapy and special educational input for intellectual impairment, ataxia and dysmyelination, alongside nutritional and growth monitoring","Coordinate care through clinical genetics, dermatology, neurology, immunology and paediatrics","Offer genetic counselling and family and prenatal testing, reflecting autosomal recessive inheritance and the nucleotide-excision-repair genes ERCC2, ERCC3 and GTF2H5"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (NCBI): Trichothiodystrophy","OMIM: Trichothiodystrophy"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichotillomania":{"name":"Trichotillomania","system":"Dermatology / Trichology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hair-pulling disorder","trichotillosis","traumatic hair-pulling alopecia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A compulsive hair-pulling disorder producing irregular, often bizarre patches of incomplete hair loss with hairs broken at varying lengths within the same area","Classically affects easily reachable areas, so the tonsure or Friar Tuck pattern spares the occiput and peripheries that are harder to grasp","Eyebrows and eyelashes may also be plucked, and ingestion of pulled hair (trichophagia) can rarely form a gastric trichobezoar, described in Rapunzel syndrome","Trichoscopy shows broken hairs of differing lengths, coiled hairs, flame hairs, V-signs and hair powder, with preserved follicular ostia","The hair-pull test is negative in pulled areas and the follicular ostia remain intact, confirming a non-scarring mechanical cause","It is more common in children and adolescents and is often associated with anxiety, obsessive-compulsive traits or psychosocial stress","Habit-reversal training and psychological therapy are first-line, and because the process is non-scarring hair regrows once pulling stops, though very prolonged traction may eventually scar"],"pathophysiology":"Trichotillomania is a body-focused repetitive behaviour in which recurrent pulling, twisting or plucking of hair produces mechanical breakage of shafts and traumatic disruption of follicles. It is classified among the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders and often serves to relieve tension or anxiety. Because the follicles are mechanically injured rather than inflamed or destroyed, the ostia are preserved and regrowth occurs once the behaviour ceases, although very prolonged or forceful pulling can occasionally cause permanent follicular damage and secondary scarring.","additionalDifferentials":["Alopecia areata, with smooth patches, exclamation-mark hairs and a positive pull test at margins","Tinea capitis, with scaling, broken hairs and positive mycology","Traction alopecia, which is marginal and tension-related","Early scarring alopecia","Congenital triangular (temporal) alopecia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Trichoscopy to identify broken hairs of varying length, flame hairs and V-signs","Mycological examination to exclude tinea capitis","Scalp biopsy in equivocal cases showing pigment casts, trichomalacia and empty follicles without significant inflammation","Psychological or psychiatric assessment for comorbid anxiety or obsessive-compulsive disorder","Abdominal imaging if trichophagia with a bezoar is suspected"],"redFlags":["Abdominal pain, a palpable mass or obstruction suggesting a trichobezoar (Rapunzel syndrome)","Signs of significant underlying psychiatric distress or self-harm","Loss of follicular ostia indicating secondary scarring from very prolonged pulling"],"prognosis":"In young children the behaviour is frequently transient and resolves with reassurance and simple behavioural measures; in adolescents and adults it tends to be more chronic and relapsing, but the alopecia is reversible unless prolonged trauma has caused scarring.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the bizarre incomplete patches for alopecia areata and missing the behavioural cause","Overlooking trichophagia and the risk of a gastric bezoar","Confronting the patient or family unsympathetically, undermining engagement with behavioural therapy","Attributing failure of regrowth to the diagnosis rather than considering secondary scarring"],"management":["Use behavioural therapy as first-line treatment, principally habit-reversal training and cognitive behavioural therapy targeting the body-focused repetitive behaviour","Consider pharmacological adjuncts such as N-acetylcysteine, or an SSRI or clomipramine where there is comorbid anxiety or obsessive-compulsive disorder, recognising the evidence is modest","Identify and treat comorbid anxiety, depression or obsessive-compulsive disorder that drive the pulling","Apply simple stimulus-control and barrier measures, and offer reassurance and watchful support in young children in whom the behaviour is often transient","Screen for trichophagia and investigate for a gastric trichobezoar with abdominal imaging, arranging endoscopic or surgical removal if the Rapunzel syndrome is present","Refer to psychology or psychiatry for structured therapy and provide family support","Follow up for relapse, as adolescent and adult disease tends to be chronic and remitting"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichuriasis":{"name":"Trichuriasis (whipworm)","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["whipworm infection","Trichuris trichiura infection","trichocephaliasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell/Manson's Tropical Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Trichuriasis is caused by the whipworm Trichuris trichiura, a soil-transmitted nematode acquired by ingesting embryonated eggs from faecally contaminated soil, food or water","The adult worm has a slender whip-like anterior threaded into the colonic mucosa and a thicker posterior end, and lives predominantly in the caecum and ascending colon","Light infections are usually asymptomatic, whereas heavy burdens cause chronic colitis with abdominal pain, tenesmus and mucoid, sometimes bloody, diarrhoea","Massive infection in children can produce the Trichuris dysentery syndrome with rectal prolapse, anaemia and growth and cognitive impairment","Diagnosis is by identifying the characteristic barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar mucous plugs on stool microscopy","Adult worms may be seen on colonoscopy attached to an inflamed mucosa","Albendazole or mebendazole is used, though whipworm responds less reliably to single-dose therapy than other soil-transmitted helminths and may need repeated or combination courses"],"pathophysiology":"Trichuris trichiura eggs passed in faeces embryonate in warm, moist soil and become infective over several weeks; once ingested they hatch in the small intestine and the larvae mature as they move to the large bowel. The adult worm embeds its narrow anterior portion within a syncytial tunnel in the colonic epithelium while its posterior lies free in the lumen. Heavy worm burdens cause a chronic inflammatory colitis with mucosal oedema, friability and bleeding; the resulting colorectal inflammation, chronic blood loss and, in intense infection, mechanical and inflammatory effects on the rectum contribute to dysentery, rectal prolapse, anaemia and, in children, impaired growth and development.","additionalDifferentials":["Amoebic or bacillary dysentery","Inflammatory bowel disease","Other soil-transmitted helminthiases","Schistosomal colitis","Rectal prolapse from other causes","Colonic polyps or other mucosal disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy for barrel-shaped bipolar-plugged eggs","Faecal egg quantification such as Kato-Katz to gauge burden","Full blood count for anaemia and eosinophilia","Colonoscopy or sigmoidoscopy visualising adult worms in heavy infection","Assessment of growth and nutritional status in children","Iron studies where anaemia is present"],"redFlags":["Trichuris dysentery syndrome with rectal prolapse in a child","Significant anaemia from chronic colonic blood loss","Growth faltering and developmental impairment with heavy burden","Severe or persistent dysenteric symptoms despite treatment"],"prognosis":"Light infections are benign; heavy infections respond to treatment with reversal of dysentery, anaemia and growth effects, although eradication may require repeated courses and reinfection is common without sanitation.","pitfalls":["Expecting reliable cure from single-dose therapy, to which whipworm responds relatively poorly","Overlooking whipworm as a cause of paediatric rectal prolapse","Missing coexisting soil-transmitted helminths","Attributing chronic colitis to inflammatory bowel disease without stool parasitology in endemic settings"],"management":["Treat with a benzimidazole anthelmintic (albendazole or mebendazole), favouring an extended course of mebendazole or combination with ivermectin for heavy colonic infection","Correct anaemia with iron supplementation and provide nutritional rehabilitation and growth support in affected children","In the Trichuris dysentery syndrome, reduce the worm burden with anthelmintics and manage rectal prolapse with gentle manual reduction","Confirm cure with follow-up stool microscopy and repeat treatment, as clearance in heavy infection may require repeated courses","Advise prevention through improved sanitation, hand hygiene and safe food and water, supported by community deworming programmes to limit reinfection","Involve paediatrics and infectious diseases for severe disease with dysentery, prolapse or growth and cognitive impairment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"tricuspid_atresia":{"name":"Tricuspid atresia","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["absent tricuspid valve"],"chapter":null,"source":"Congenital heart disease references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Congenital absence of the tricuspid valve with no direct connection between the right atrium and right ventricle","It is a cyanotic lesion and a form of functionally single ventricle","An obligatory atrial communication allows systemic venous blood to reach the left heart, and a ventricular septal defect or duct supplies pulmonary flow","Degree of cyanosis depends on the amount of pulmonary blood flow, which may be duct-dependent","The electrocardiogram often shows left-axis deviation with left ventricular dominance, unusual for a cyanotic newborn","Management follows the single-ventricle palliation pathway culminating in a Fontan circulation"],"pathophysiology":"In tricuspid atresia the absent tricuspid connection forces all systemic venous return to cross the atrial septum into the left atrium, where it mixes with pulmonary venous blood, producing cyanosis. The right ventricle is hypoplastic, and pulmonary blood flow depends on a ventricular septal defect, the arterial duct or associated lesions. Because a single functional (left) ventricle supports both circulations, the physiology and surgical approach follow the single-ventricle pathway, ultimately routing systemic venous blood directly to the lungs (Fontan).","additionalDifferentials":["Other single-ventricle lesions","Pulmonary atresia with intact septum","Ebstein anomaly","Transposition with restricted pulmonary flow","Other cyanotic congenital heart disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography defining the atretic valve and associated defects","Assessment of pulmonary blood flow and ductal dependence","Electrocardiogram (left-axis deviation)","Pre-operative anatomical imaging","Single-ventricle pathway evaluation"],"redFlags":["Severe cyanosis from inadequate pulmonary blood flow (duct closing)","Restrictive atrial septum impeding systemic venous egress","Excessive pulmonary flow causing heart failure","Failing Fontan circulation later in life"],"prognosis":"Staged single-ventricle palliation allows survival into adulthood for many, but the Fontan circulation carries long-term risks including arrhythmia, protein-losing enteropathy and circulatory failure.","pitfalls":["Not maintaining ductal patency when pulmonary flow is duct-dependent","Overlooking a restrictive atrial communication","Misreading the unusual left-axis electrocardiogram","Underestimating long-term Fontan complications"],"management":["Stabilise the duct-dependent neonate with a prostaglandin E infusion to maintain ductal patency and pulmonary blood flow until intervention","Perform balloon atrial septostomy if the obligatory atrial communication is restrictive and limits systemic venous egress to the left heart","Pursue the staged single-ventricle palliation pathway, using an initial aortopulmonary shunt or pulmonary artery band as needed, then a bidirectional Glenn and finally Fontan completion","Give anticongestive therapy with diuretics for heart failure and institute antithrombotic therapy after Fontan completion to reduce thromboembolic risk","Undertake lifelong surveillance for Fontan complications including atrial arrhythmia, protein-losing enteropathy, plastic bronchitis, thromboembolism, hepatic disease and ventricular dysfunction, with consideration of transplantation for the failing circulation","Support with nutrition, immunisations, endocarditis prophylaxis and individualised exercise advice","Manage through a paediatric and congenital cardiology and cardiac surgery team, with structured transition to adult congenital heart disease services and family counselling with foetal echocardiography in future pregnancies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Congenital heart disease references (standard reference)","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"tricuspid_regurgitation":{"name":"Tricuspid regurgitation","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tricuspid insufficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Valvular heart disease guidelines / cardiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Backflow across the tricuspid valve, most commonly functional (secondary) from right ventricular or annular dilatation rather than primary valve disease","Primary causes include endocarditis, rheumatic disease, Ebstein anomaly, carcinoid and device leads","Signs include a raised jugular venous pressure with a prominent v wave, a pansystolic murmur that increases on inspiration, pulsatile hepatomegaly and peripheral oedema","It is frequently associated with left-sided heart disease and pulmonary hypertension","Severe chronic regurgitation leads to right heart failure and hepatic congestion","Management addresses the underlying cause and pulmonary hypertension, with valve repair or replacement in selected severe cases"],"pathophysiology":"Tricuspid regurgitation most often arises secondarily when right ventricular pressure or volume overload, commonly from left heart disease or pulmonary hypertension, dilates the right ventricle and tricuspid annulus so the leaflets fail to coapt. Primary (structural) regurgitation results from direct valve damage. The regurgitant volume overloads the right atrium and ventricle, raising systemic venous pressure to cause hepatic congestion, ascites and peripheral oedema, and chronic overload progressively impairs right ventricular function.","additionalDifferentials":["Right heart failure of other cause","Constrictive pericarditis","Pulmonary hypertension","Mitral valve disease with secondary effects","Hepatic disease causing oedema and ascites"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography grading severity and assessing the right ventricle and annulus","Assessment of pulmonary artery pressure","Evaluation for left-sided valve or myocardial disease","Assessment for primary causes (endocarditis, carcinoid, leads)","Right heart and hepatic assessment in chronic disease"],"redFlags":["Progressive right heart failure with congestion and ascites","Severe pulmonary hypertension","Endocarditis as a cause (fever, vegetations)","Rapidly worsening functional status"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends heavily on the underlying cause and right ventricular function; significant secondary regurgitation is associated with worse outcomes, and intervention decisions are individualised.","pitfalls":["Overlooking treatable left-sided or pulmonary causes of functional regurgitation","Attributing congestion and ascites to liver disease alone","Delaying intervention until right ventricular function is severely impaired","Missing endocarditis or carcinoid as a primary cause"],"management":["Treat the underlying driver of functional regurgitation by managing left-sided heart disease and lowering pulmonary artery pressure with pulmonary hypertension therapy where indicated","Relieve systemic venous congestion, hepatic congestion and oedema with loop diuretics, adding an aldosterone antagonist as needed","Provide rate or rhythm control and anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation contributing to atrial and annular remodelling","Address primary valve causes directly, with antimicrobials for endocarditis and management of carcinoid or lead-related regurgitation","Offer surgical tricuspid annuloplasty or valve replacement in selected severe cases, particularly at the time of left-sided valve surgery, and consider transcatheter tricuspid repair for high-risk patients","Perform serial echocardiographic surveillance of severity and right ventricular function, watching for progression to right heart failure","Manage decisions through a multidisciplinary heart valve team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Valvular heart disease guidelines (standard reference)","Braunwald's Heart Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"tricuspid_valve_disease":{"name":"Tricuspid Valve Disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tricuspid stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","TS","TR"],"chapter":277,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2059","clinicalPearls":["Tricuspid stenosis is far less common than mitral stenosis in North America and Western Europe, is usually rheumatic in origin, occurs more often in women, and essentially never appears as an isolated lesion (it is typically combined with mitral stenosis) (Harrison 22e p.2059).","Hemodynamically significant tricuspid stenosis is seen in roughly 5-10% of patients with severe mitral stenosis, and rheumatic tricuspid stenosis is commonly accompanied by some tricuspid regurgitation; nonrheumatic causes of stenosis are rare (Harrison 22e p.2059).","More than 85% of tricuspid regurgitation encountered clinically is secondary (functional), arising from tricuspid annular dilation and leaflet tethering in the setting of RV remodeling from pressure/volume overload, MI, or trauma rather than from intrinsic valve disease (Harrison 22e p.2060).","New-onset atrial fibrillation, especially in older patients, can produce 'atrial functional' tricuspid regurgitation through atrial and annular remodeling (Harrison 22e p.2060-2061).","Patients with severe tricuspid stenosis characteristically complain of relatively little dyspnea given the marked hepatomegaly, ascites, and edema present, because the right-sided obstruction limits pulmonary congestion (Harrison 22e p.2059).","Tricuspid regurgitation is more often detected by inspecting the neck veins (prominent c-v waves with rapid y descent) than by cardiac auscultation (Harrison 22e p.2061).","Carvallo's sign - inspiratory accentuation of the holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border - helps distinguish the murmur of tricuspid regurgitation from that of mitral regurgitation; it diminishes on expiration or during the Valsalva strain phase (Harrison 22e p.2061).","The murmur of tricuspid stenosis resembles that of mitral stenosis but is best heard at the lower left sternal border/xiphoid, is most prominent in presystole during sinus rhythm, and increases with inspiration while decreasing during expiration and Valsalva strain (Harrison 22e p.2060).","In tricuspid stenosis with sinus rhythm, the right atrial a wave can be enormous (approaching RV systolic pressure) and the y descent is prolonged; presystolic pulsations of an enlarged liver may also be palpable (Harrison 22e p.2059).","On ECG, absence of right ventricular hypertrophy in a patient with right-sided heart failure who is presumed to have mitral stenosis should raise suspicion for coexisting tricuspid valve disease; right atrial enlargement gives tall peaked P waves in lead II and prominent upright P waves in V1 (Harrison 22e p.2060).","A distinct category of tricuspid regurgitation is caused by cardiac implantable electronic device (CIED) leads crossing the valve, producing leaflet entrapment or perforation; right ventricular apical pacing can additionally cause dyssynchronous contraction and functional TR (Harrison 22e p.2060-2061).","Coexisting tricuspid stenosis can mask the hemodynamic and clinical features of associated mitral stenosis by lowering cardiac output and keeping left atrial, pulmonary artery, and RV systolic pressures only normal or mildly elevated (Harrison 22e p.2059)."],"pathophysiology":"Tricuspid stenosis is defined by a diastolic pressure gradient between the right atrium and right ventricle that increases with inspiration (greater transvalvular flow) and falls with expiration. A mean diastolic gradient of about 4 mmHg is usually enough to raise mean right atrial pressure to levels causing systemic venous congestion - hepatomegaly, ascites, and edema. Resting cardiac output is typically depressed and fails to rise with exercise. In tricuspid regurgitation, the incompetent valve allows backward flow from RV to RA, with the regurgitant volume governed by RV systolic driving pressure and the size of the regurgitant orifice; severity is modulated by pulmonary artery systolic pressure, annular dimensions, respiratory changes in RV preload, and RA compliance. Significant TR enlarges the right atrium, raises RA and jugular venous pressures with prominent c-v waves, can 'ventricularize' the RA waveform, and leads to RV volume overload, dilation, and eventual systolic dysfunction - accelerated by any added pressure load from pulmonary hypertension or prior myocardial fibrosis (Harrison 22e p.2059-2061).","additionalDifferentials":["Mitral stenosis (frequently coexists and dominates the picture)","Mitral regurgitation (murmur can be confused with TR without attention to respiratory variation)","Constrictive pericarditis / restrictive cardiomyopathy as a cause of right-sided venous congestion","Other causes of right heart failure with hepatic congestion and ascites"],"infectionMimics":["Infective endocarditis of the tricuspid valve causing primary regurgitation"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Carcinoid heart disease causing tricuspid leaflet retraction and regurgitation","Ebstein's malformation and other congenital tricuspid deformities","Myxomatous tricuspid valve prolapse","Radiation-induced valve disease","Endomyocardial fibrosis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography (Doppler) - assesses leaflet thickening/doming, transvalvular gradient, RA and IVC size, associated TR severity, mitral and LV/RV function, and PA pressure","Continuous-wave Doppler of the TR jet velocity to estimate PA systolic pressure (may be blunted when TR is very severe)","ECG for right atrial enlargement, RVH, prior inferior/RV MI pattern, atrial fibrillation, or preexcitation in Ebstein's anomaly","Chest x-ray - RA and superior vena cava prominence with azygos engorgement in combined TS/MS; RA/RV enlargement in TR","Real-time three-dimensional echocardiography and cardiac MR for difficult assessment of TR severity, PA pressures, and RV size/function","Exercise testing to assess functional capacity in asymptomatic severe TR","Cardiac catheterization - not routinely needed for TS assessment"],"redFlags":["Severe hepatic congestion progressing to cirrhosis, jaundice, serious malnutrition, anasarca, and ascites in severe tricuspid valve disease","Persistent right-sided failure after an adequate mitral commissurotomy, suggesting unrecognized tricuspid stenosis","Progressive RV dilation and declining systolic function in severe TR","Marked reduction in cardiac output with prominent c-v wave and rapid y descent and loss of the x descent in severe TR"],"prognosis":"Mild-to-moderate tricuspid regurgitation is generally well tolerated when there is no other hemodynamic disturbance. Because TR commonly coexists with left-sided valve lesions, LV dysfunction, or pulmonary hypertension, those conditions often dominate the clinical course. As isolated severe TR progresses, reduced forward cardiac output causes fatigue and exertional dyspnea, and declining RV function leads to cervical pulsations, abdominal fullness, anorexia, muscle wasting, weight gain, and painful lower-extremity swelling. Severe TR is associated with markedly reduced cardiac output and elevated mean RA and RV end-diastolic pressures (Harrison 22e p.2061-2062).","pitfalls":["Tricuspid stenosis is easily overlooked because it almost always occurs alongside obvious mitral disease and its diastolic murmur resembles that of mitral stenosis - the diagnosis must be deliberately considered (Harrison 22e p.2059-2060).","The TR murmur may be mistaken for mitral regurgitation unless its inspiratory accentuation and the degree of RV enlargement are appreciated (Harrison 22e p.2061).","Accurate echocardiographic quantification of TR severity, PA pressure, and RV size/function is genuinely difficult in many patients (Harrison 22e p.2062).","Continuous-wave Doppler estimation of PA systolic pressure can be falsely low when TR is very severe because the jet velocity is blunted by rapidly rising RA pressure (Harrison 22e p.2062)."],"management":["For tricuspid stenosis, relieve systemic venous congestion with loop diuretics and sodium restriction and treat the underlying rheumatic disease that usually coexists with mitral stenosis","Correct significant tricuspid stenosis with balloon valvuloplasty or surgical repair or replacement, typically undertaken at the time of mitral valve surgery","For functional regurgitation, address the driving pulmonary hypertension and left-sided heart disease and relieve congestion with diuretics","Provide rate or rhythm control and anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation, which can itself generate atrial functional regurgitation through annular remodelling","Offer surgical annuloplasty, repair or valve replacement for severe disease, with a bioprosthesis often preferred in the tricuspid position, and consider transcatheter intervention in selected high-risk patients","Give secondary antibiotic prophylaxis with penicillin for rheumatic disease and address endocarditis prophylaxis considerations","Undertake serial echocardiographic surveillance of valve gradient, regurgitation severity, right ventricular function and exercise tolerance, managing decisions through a heart valve team"],"severityClassification":"Severe tricuspid stenosis is defined echocardiographically by a tricuspid valve area of 1 cm2 or less, or a pressure half-time of 190 ms or more; a mean diastolic gradient around 4 mmHg is hemodynamically significant. For tricuspid regurgitation, severity is staged (progressive Stage B, asymptomatic severe Stage C, symptomatic severe Stage D); annular dilation is defined as >40 mm (>21 mm/m2) on transthoracic echocardiography or >70 mm on direct intraoperative measurement, and severe TR is accompanied by hepatic vein systolic flow reversal (Harrison 22e p.2060-2062).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2059","Harrison 22e p.2060","Harrison 22e p.2061","Harrison 22e p.2062"],"crossLinks":[]},"tricyclic_antidepressant_overdose":{"name":"Tricyclic antidepressant overdose","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TCA overdose","tricyclic poisoning","amitriptyline overdose","tricyclic antidepressant toxicity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Toxicity combines an anticholinergic toxidrome (dry flushed skin, dilated pupils, urinary retention, agitation, delirium) with the far more dangerous cardiovascular and neurological effects","Sodium-channel blockade widens the QRS complex; a broad QRS is the key marker of cardiotoxicity and predicts arrhythmia and seizures","A prominent terminal R wave in lead aVR is a supportive ECG sign of sodium-channel blockade","Life-threatening features include ventricular arrhythmias, hypotension from myocardial depression and vasodilatation, seizures, and rapid deterioration in conscious level","Intravenous sodium bicarbonate is the cornerstone treatment for a widening QRS, ventricular arrhythmia, or hypotension, working by sodium loading and alkalinisation","Deterioration can be abrupt; a patient who looks well early can rapidly progress to coma, seizures, and cardiac arrest, so early cardiac monitoring is essential","Class Ia and Ic antiarrhythmics are contraindicated as they aggravate sodium-channel blockade; refractory arrhythmia may be treated with additional bicarbonate and supportive measures","Intravenous lipid emulsion may be considered as a rescue therapy in refractory cardiovascular collapse"],"pathophysiology":"Tricyclic antidepressants exert several toxic actions. Blockade of fast cardiac sodium channels slows myocardial depolarisation, widening the QRS and predisposing to ventricular arrhythmia and myocardial depression; this effect is worsened by acidosis. Anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) activity produces the classic peripheral and central anticholinergic features. Antagonism of peripheral alpha-adrenergic receptors causes vasodilatation and hypotension, and central effects plus GABA and other receptor actions lower the seizure threshold. Seizures worsen acidosis, which in turn intensifies sodium-channel blockade in a dangerous positive-feedback loop.","additionalDifferentials":["Other sodium-channel-blocking drug toxicity (some antiarrhythmics, carbamazepine)","Anticholinergic poisoning from other agents","Sympathomimetic toxicity","Other causes of wide-complex tachycardia","Hyperkalaemia causing QRS widening"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial 12-lead ECG focusing on QRS width and the aVR terminal R wave","Continuous cardiac monitoring","Arterial blood gas and serum electrolytes including potassium","Capillary glucose and paracetamol/salicylate co-ingestant screen","Creatine kinase if prolonged seizures or immobility raise concern for rhabdomyolysis"],"redFlags":["QRS widening on the ECG indicating significant cardiotoxicity","Ventricular arrhythmia or haemodynamic instability","Seizures or rapidly declining conscious level","Refractory hypotension unresponsive to fluids","Rapid clinical deterioration in a patient who initially appeared stable"],"prognosis":"Most deaths occur early from arrhythmia, refractory hypotension, or seizures, often before or soon after arrival; patients who reach hospital and receive prompt bicarbonate and supportive care and survive the first day generally recover well.","pitfalls":["Being reassured by an initially well-appearing patient and delaying continuous cardiac monitoring","Using class Ia or Ic antiarrhythmics, which worsen sodium-channel blockade","Failing to give sodium bicarbonate promptly for a widening QRS or arrhythmia","Allowing acidosis from seizures or hypoventilation to persist and aggravate cardiotoxicity"],"management":["Secure the airway with early intubation for depressed consciousness or seizures, obtain intravenous access and start continuous cardiac monitoring, anticipating abrupt deterioration","Give intravenous sodium bicarbonate as the cornerstone for a widening QRS, ventricular arrhythmia or hypotension, working through sodium loading and serum alkalinisation, and correct hypoxia and acidosis that intensify sodium-channel blockade","Administer activated charcoal for early presentations once the airway is protected","Control seizures with benzodiazepines as first-line agents, avoiding class Ia, Ic and III antiarrhythmics which worsen sodium-channel blockade","Treat hypotension with intravenous crystalloid then a vasopressor such as noradrenaline, and consider intravenous lipid emulsion or extracorporeal life support for refractory cardiotoxicity","Avoid physostigmine and manage hyperthermia and electrolyte disturbance including potassium, checking capillary glucose and a paracetamol and salicylate co-ingestant screen","Observe with cardiac monitoring until the ECG and mental status normalise, then arrange psychiatric assessment and risk evaluation for intentional overdose, with toxicology or poisons-centre and critical-care input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"trigeminal_autonomic_cephalalgia":{"name":"Trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TACs","trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia"],"chapter":null,"source":"ICHD-3 / Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of primary headaches characterised by strictly unilateral pain in the trigeminal distribution, usually the ophthalmic division, with prominent ipsilateral cranial autonomic features","The group comprises cluster headache, paroxysmal hemicrania, short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache attacks (SUNCT and SUNA) and hemicrania continua","Ipsilateral autonomic signs include conjunctival injection, lacrimation, nasal congestion or rhinorrhoea, eyelid oedema, forehead and facial sweating, miosis and ptosis, while restlessness and agitation are typical of cluster headache","The subtypes are distinguished mainly by attack duration and frequency: cluster is the longest with circadian and circannual clustering, paroxysmal hemicrania has many shorter attacks per day, SUNCT and SUNA are very brief but very frequent, and hemicrania continua is continuous with exacerbations","Treatment response is a defining clue, as paroxysmal hemicrania and hemicrania continua show an absolute response to indometacin, while cluster headache responds to high-flow oxygen and triptans with preventives such as verapamil","SUNCT and SUNA can be triggered by cutaneous stimuli and may mimic trigeminal neuralgia, but the conjunctival injection and tearing help differentiate them","A symptomatic (secondary) trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia, especially from a pituitary or posterior fossa lesion, must be excluded, so MRI including dedicated pituitary views is recommended, particularly with atypical features","Accurate classification is essential because management differs markedly between the subtypes"],"pathophysiology":"The trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias arise from activation of the trigeminovascular system, referring pain to the ophthalmic trigeminal territory, together with activation of the trigeminal-autonomic (parasympathetic) reflex that produces the ipsilateral cranial autonomic features. Functional imaging implicates the posterior hypothalamus as a generator or permissive region, which underlies the striking circadian and circannual rhythmicity of cluster headache. Parasympathetic outflow via the sphenopalatine ganglion mediates the lacrimation and nasal symptoms, while associated sympathetic dysfunction produces the partial Horner features of ptosis and miosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Trigeminal neuralgia, especially in relation to SUNCT and SUNA","Migraine, which may have some autonomic features but a different phenotype","Symptomatic trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia from a pituitary tumour, cavernous sinus or posterior fossa lesion or carotid dissection","Primary stabbing headache","Acute angle-closure glaucoma or other ocular and orbital pathology","Sinusitis or dental pathology as non-neurological causes of facial pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI brain with dedicated pituitary and cavernous sinus views to exclude a symptomatic cause","MR or CT angiography if arterial dissection or a vascular lesion is suspected","Detailed headache diary documenting attack duration, frequency, timing and autonomic features","A trial of indometacin where paroxysmal hemicrania or hemicrania continua is suspected","Pituitary function tests if a sellar lesion is found or suspected","Ophthalmological or ENT assessment to exclude a local cause of a red, watering eye"],"redFlags":["A new or atypical presentation, or a change in an established pattern, suggesting a secondary cause","Persistent focal neurological deficit, visual field loss or a fixed Horner syndrome","Features of raised intracranial pressure or a posterior fossa or sellar mass","An unusual age of onset or accompanying systemic symptoms","A painful red eye requiring exclusion of acute glaucoma"],"prognosis":"The trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias are typically chronic or relapsing conditions; with correct classification most respond well to targeted therapy, although some patients develop chronic or refractory disease requiring specialist or neuromodulation approaches.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing cluster headache as migraine or sinusitis and delaying effective treatment","Not performing an indometacin trial and missing an eminently treatable paroxysmal hemicrania or hemicrania continua","Failing to image, including the pituitary, and overlooking a symptomatic trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia","Confusing SUNCT or SUNA with trigeminal neuralgia and managing it incorrectly","Overusing acute medications and precipitating medication-overuse headache"],"management":["Abort cluster headache attacks with high-flow oxygen and a fast-acting subcutaneous or intranasal triptan such as sumatriptan or zolmitriptan","Prevent cluster headache with verapamil under ECG monitoring, using a short corticosteroid course or greater occipital nerve block as a transitional bridge, and lithium, topiramate or a CGRP monoclonal antibody as alternatives","Treat suspected paroxysmal hemicrania and hemicrania continua with indometacin under gastroprotection, exploiting the absolute response that is both therapeutic and diagnostic","Manage SUNCT and SUNA with lamotrigine or other antiseizure agents, reserving intravenous lidocaine for severe exacerbations","Exclude a symptomatic secondary cause with brain MRI including dedicated pituitary and cavernous-sinus views before committing to long-term treatment","Offer neuromodulation such as occipital nerve or sphenopalatine ganglion stimulation or non-invasive vagus nerve stimulation for refractory disease","Advise avoidance of alcohol and other triggers during cluster bouts, keep a headache diary and refer to a neurology or headache specialist"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (ICHD-3)","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"trigeminal_neuralgia_bell_s_palsy":{"name":"Trigeminal Neuralgia, Bell’s Palsy, and Other Cranial Nerve Disorders","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tic douloureux","Idiopathic facial paralysis","Cranial neuropathy","Glossopharyngeal neuralgia","Cavernous sinus syndrome","Tolosa-Hunt syndrome"],"chapter":452,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3551","clinicalPearls":["Trigeminal neuralgia produces brief (seconds to a minute or two) paroxysms of excruciating, superficial, stabbing pain usually in the lips, gums, cheek, or chin (V2/V3); ophthalmic-division (V1) involvement is rare. Attacks may be triggered by light tactile stimuli at facial, lip, or tongue trigger zones (washing, brushing teeth, a draft of air) and by speaking, chewing, or smiling.","An essential diagnostic feature of classic trigeminal neuralgia is the ABSENCE of demonstrable objective sensory loss on examination; demonstrable trigeminal sensory or motor deficit instead points to a structural trigeminal neuropathy or secondary cause.","Trigeminal neuralgia is relatively common (incidence ~4–8 per 100,000/year), affects mainly middle-aged and elderly persons, and roughly 60% of cases occur in women; bouts persist for weeks to months and tend to remit then recur.","When trigeminal neuralgia occurs in a young adult or is bilateral, suspect multiple sclerosis (demyelinating plaque near the trigeminal root entry zone in the pons), especially if subtle facial sensory loss is present.","Bell’s palsy is the most common form of facial paralysis (annual incidence ~25 per 100,000; lifetime risk ~1 in 60); onset is abrupt with maximal weakness by 48 h, often preceded by retroauricular pain for a day or two, and may include loss of taste and hyperacusis.","A peripheral (lower motor neuron) facial palsy weakens the whole hemiface including the forehead and eyelid closure, whereas a supranuclear (central) lesion relatively spares the forehead and orbicularis oculi because upper-face muscles receive bilateral corticobulbar innervation.","Localizing the seventh-nerve lesion: loss of taste over the anterior two-thirds of the tongue points to a middle-ear-segment lesion; hyperacusis indicates involvement of the nerve to stapedius; accompanying deafness, tinnitus, or vertigo suggests an internal auditory meatus lesion affecting the eighth nerve.","Glossopharyngeal neuralgia resembles trigeminal neuralgia but is much less common; pain is paroxysmal and intense, arises in the tonsillar fossa/throat, may radiate to the ear, and is provoked by swallowing or coughing; rarely it triggers bradycardia/asystole, hypotension, and syncope.","Anatomic clustering of cranial nerves yields recognizable syndromes: jugular foramen (IX, X, XI), cavernous sinus (III, IV, V1, VI), pontocerebellar angle (V, VI, VII, VIII), and retroparotid space (IX, X, XI, XII plus Horner’s).","Cavernous sinus syndrome is frequently life-threatening: orbital/facial pain, orbital swelling, chemosis, fever, ophthalmoplegia (III, IV, VI), and V1 (±V2) trigeminal involvement—cavernous sinus thrombosis from facial/orbital/sinus infection (including mucormycosis in diabetics) is the most common cause and demands early diagnosis.","Tolosa-Hunt syndrome (idiopathic granulomatous cavernous-sinus/orbital inflammation) causes painful ophthalmoparesis with V1/V2 numbness and shows a dramatic response to glucocorticoids, but its picture can be mimicked by sarcoid, vasculitis, IgG4-related disease, lymphoma, and fungal infection—so confirm before attributing to it.","An eleventh-nerve (spinal accessory) palsy causes little overt neck weakness because other muscles assist head turning; detection relies on palpating absent sternocleidomastoid contraction during head turning and on noting a lower shoulder, scapular winging, and inability to abduct the arm beyond 90 degrees."],"pathophysiology":"The cranial nerves are 12 paired nerves with mixed motor, sensory, and autonomic functions linked by their close brainstem relationships; nine connect directly to brainstem nuclei, while CN I and II are effectively brain fiber tracts and CN XI arises largely from upper cervical cord. In trigeminal neuralgia, symptoms arise from ectopic action-potential generation in pain-sensitive afferents of the fifth-nerve root just proximal to its entry at the lateral pons; demyelination of large myelinated (non-nociceptive) fibers renders them hyperexcitable and electrically coupled to adjacent small unmyelinated/poorly myelinated pain fibers, so tactile input can ignite paroxysms of pain. Compression of the root by a blood vessel—most often the superior cerebellar artery (or a tortuous vein)—is implicated in most cases; age-related brain sagging and increased vascular tortuosity may explain its predilection for later life. The trigeminal nerve carries oligodendrocyte-derived (central-type) myelin for up to ~7 mm beyond the brainstem, which may underlie trigeminal neuralgia in MS. In Bell’s palsy there is inflammation of the facial nerve with mononuclear cell infiltration suggesting an infectious/immune mechanism; reactivation of herpes simplex virus type 1 in the geniculate ganglion is thought to cause most cases, with varicella-zoster reactivation implicated in up to one-third (Ramsay Hunt syndrome). The vagus and other lower cranial nerves can be injured along their medullary, meningeal, foraminal, or extracranial courses; the recurrent laryngeal nerve (especially the left) is most often damaged by intrathoracic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Migraine and cluster headache (deep, steady pain rather than superficial stabbing; cluster-tic overlap exists)","Temporal (giant cell) arteritis (superficial facial pain with myalgias/systemic symptoms and elevated ESR/CRP)","Short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache (SUNCT/SUNA) and paroxysmal hemicrania","Dental, jaw, or sinus disease as a source of face pain","Trigeminal neuropathy from tumor (meningioma, schwannoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, metastasis), Sjögren’s/connective-tissue disease, sarcoidosis, leprosy","Supranuclear facial palsy from stroke/structural hemispheric lesion (forehead-sparing, often with limb weakness or aphasia)","Lyme disease facial palsy (unilateral or bilateral) in endemic areas","Guillain-Barré syndrome (often bilateral facial palsy; Fisher variant with ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia)","Myasthenia gravis for pure motor cranial weakness without atrophy","Polymyositis/dermatomyositis and myotonic dystrophy mimicking vagal dysphagia/dysphonia","Progressive bulbar palsy (motor neuron disease) for multiple lower cranial motor involvement"],"infectionMimics":["Ramsay Hunt syndrome (geniculate herpes zoster) mimicking Bell’s palsy, with auricular/pharyngeal vesicles and often eighth-nerve involvement","Lyme disease (Borrelia burgdorferi) causing uni- or bilateral facial palsy","HIV seroconversion-associated facial palsy","Herpes zoster involving the trigeminal ganglion (acute or postherpetic) or lower cranial nerves","Leprosy involving the facial or trigeminal nerve","Chronic/carcinomatous meningitis, tuberculosis, otitis media/mastoiditis spread to the semilunar ganglion","Diphtheria affecting the pharyngeal branches of the vagus (nasal voice, nasal regurgitation)","Mucormycosis and other invasive fungal infections at the cavernous sinus/orbital apex (especially in diabetics)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis (often bilateral facial palsy; cause of multiple cranial neuropathy)","Tolosa-Hunt syndrome and other idiopathic granulomatous orbital/cavernous inflammation","IgG4-related disease, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, and other vasculitides","Lymphoma and metastatic/locally infiltrating tumors (nasopharyngeal carcinoma, neurofibroma, meningioma, chordoma, cholesteatoma)","Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome (recurrent facial palsy, recurrent facial/labial edema, fissured tongue)","Diabetes mellitus, amyloidosis, and connective-tissue/collagen-vascular disease (e.g., Sjögren’s, SLE, scleroderma, MCTD)","Skull-base and posterior-fossa structural lesions (acoustic neuroma, platybasia, basilar invagination, Paget’s disease, Chiari malformation)","Carotid-cavernous fistula or carotid aneurysm","Temporal bone fracture or blunt head injury","Iatrogenic/traumatic injury (neck surgery, jugular vein cannulation, carotid endarterectomy/dissection, post-intubation Tapia syndrome)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ESR and/or CRP when temporal arteritis is suspected as a cause of facial pain or in atypical facial palsy","Neuroimaging (MRI/MR angiography) to exclude secondary causes of trigeminal neuralgia and to map the relationship of the fifth-nerve root to adjacent vessels for surgical planning","MRI of the facial nerve, which in idiopathic Bell’s palsy often shows smooth linear enhancement and swelling of the geniculate ganglion and facial nerve (genu, tympanic, mastoid segments); similar enhancement can occur in Lyme disease, sarcoidosis, and perineural malignant spread","Electromyography for prognosis in Bell’s palsy (denervation evident after 10 days indicates axonal degeneration and a longer, possibly incomplete recovery)","In atypical/uncertain facial palsy: testing for diabetes mellitus, Lyme serology, HIV serology, ACE level with chest imaging for sarcoidosis, and lumbar puncture if Guillain-Barré is suspected","Lumbar puncture/CSF examination (mild CSF lymphocytosis may occur in Bell’s palsy; useful for meningeal/inflammatory causes)","Laryngoscopy and chest/mediastinal imaging for vocal cord palsy to evaluate intrathoracic causes of recurrent laryngeal nerve injury","Biopsy when granulomatous/infiltrative cavernous sinus or orbital apex disease (e.g., Tolosa-Hunt mimics, IgG4-related disease) is suspected"],"redFlags":["Objective trigeminal sensory loss, jaw weakness, or jaw deviation accompanying facial pain (suggests structural trigeminal neuropathy rather than classic neuralgia)","Bilateral trigeminal neuralgia or onset in a young adult (consider multiple sclerosis)","Isolated numbness over the chin (mental neuropathy), which can be the sole manifestation of systemic malignancy","Forehead-sparing facial weakness with limb weakness or aphasia (central/supranuclear lesion such as stroke)","Facial palsy with vesicles in the ear canal/palate, severe pain, or eighth-nerve signs (Ramsay Hunt syndrome)","Slowly progressive or insidious facial weakness (suggests tumor invading the temporal bone rather than Bell’s palsy)","Orbital/facial pain with orbital swelling, chemosis, fever, and ophthalmoplegia (cavernous sinus thrombosis—life-threatening)","Glossopharyngeal neuralgia with bradycardia, asystole, hypotension, or syncope","Successive involvement of multiple cranial nerves, especially with bone erosion or foraminal enlargement (infiltrating tumor, granulomatous or neoplastic meningitis)","Corneal anesthesia (loss of V1 corneal sensation) increasing risk of ulceration (neurokeratitis)"],"prognosis":"Trigeminal neuralgia characteristically remits spontaneously after bouts lasting weeks to months, and remissions can be long-lasting, though the disorder ultimately recurs in most patients. In Bell’s palsy, about 80% of patients recover within a few weeks to months; incomplete paralysis during the first week is the most favorable prognostic sign, whereas EMG evidence of denervation after 10 days predicts axonal degeneration with delayed (typically ~3 months) and possibly incomplete recovery, and recurrences occur in roughly 7% of cases. Idiopathic trigeminal neuropathy usually recovers gradually. Most patients with isolated spinal accessory neuropathy recover. Post-intubation Tapia syndrome (vagal plus hypoglossal palsy) usually resolves within a few months.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing classic trigeminal neuralgia despite objective trigeminal sensory loss—such findings indicate trigeminal neuropathy from a structural/secondary cause (aneurysm, neurofibroma, acoustic schwannoma, meningioma).","Overlooking multiple sclerosis as the cause of trigeminal neuralgia in a young adult or when symptoms are bilateral.","Mistaking a central (supranuclear) forehead-sparing facial weakness for peripheral Bell’s palsy and missing a stroke or hemispheric lesion.","Failing to examine the eighth and other cranial nerves in a patient with facial palsy—the eighth nerve runs close to the facial nerve at the pontomedullary junction and in the temporal bone.","Attributing an insidious, progressive facial weakness to Bell’s palsy when it actually reflects a temporal bone or pontine tumor.","Missing Ramsay Hunt syndrome by not inspecting the external auditory canal and palate for zoster vesicles.","Misdiagnosing vagal/laryngeal or pharyngeal muscle disease (polymyositis, dermatomyositis, myotonic dystrophy) as a primary vagus nerve lesion in dysphagia/dysphonia.","Searching intracranially for an isolated vocal cord palsy when intrathoracic disease (aortic aneurysm, enlarged left atrium, mediastinal/bronchial tumor) is the far more common cause.","Delaying recognition and treatment of cavernous sinus thrombosis, an early-diagnosis emergency."],"management":["Treat trigeminal neuralgia first-line with a sodium-channel-blocking antiseizure drug, carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine, using lamotrigine, baclofen or gabapentin as alternatives or add-ons","Offer microvascular decompression for fit patients with neurovascular contact, or an ablative procedure such as percutaneous rhizotomy or stereotactic radiosurgery, when trigeminal neuralgia is refractory to medication","Treat Bell's palsy with an early course of oral corticosteroids, adding an antiviral agent in severe cases","Protect the eye in facial palsy with lubricating drops and ointment, and taping or patching to prevent exposure keratopathy from incomplete lid closure","Investigate for and treat secondary causes such as multiple sclerosis, a compressive tumour, Lyme disease or herpes zoster with appropriate imaging and serology, particularly with objective sensory loss, young or bilateral trigeminal neuralgia, or atypical facial palsy","Provide facial physiotherapy and rehabilitation for Bell's palsy and monitor recovery, referring for specialist input if there is incomplete recovery or aberrant reinnervation","Coordinate care across neurology, neurosurgery, ophthalmology and ENT as dictated by the underlying disorder"],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scale is presented in this chapter. In Bell’s palsy, clinical severity is gauged by completeness of paralysis, with incomplete paralysis in the first week being the most favorable prognostic indicator and electromyographic denervation after 10 days indicating axonal degeneration and a worse, more prolonged recovery. The diagnosis of Bell’s palsy is supported clinically by a typical presentation, absence of other risk factors/symptoms, no zoster lesions in the ear canal, and a normal neurologic examination apart from the facial nerve.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3551","Harrison 22e p.3552","Harrison 22e p.3553","Harrison 22e p.3554","Harrison 22e p.3555","Harrison 22e p.3556","Harrison 22e p.3557","Harrison 22e p.3558","Harrison 22e p.3559"],"crossLinks":[]},"trigeminal_trophic_syndrome":{"name":"Trigeminal trophic syndrome","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TTS","trigeminal trophic ulceration","trophic ulceration of the nasal ala"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by a triad of trigeminal sensory loss, facial paraesthesia (often a crawling or itching dysaesthesia), and a self-induced ulcer, classically of the nasal ala","The crescentic ulcer typically spares the nasal tip because the tip is supplied by a different sensory branch, an important diagnostic clue","It follows damage to the trigeminal nerve or its ganglion, commonly after trigeminal ablative procedures for neuralgia, stroke, herpes zoster, or tumour","The ulceration is caused by repetitive self-manipulation (rubbing, picking, scratching) of anaesthetic and paraesthetic skin rather than by primary tissue breakdown","There may be a long latency, from months to years, between the nerve injury and the appearance of the ulcer","Diagnosis is clinical but requires biopsy and investigation to exclude malignancy, infection, and vasculitis, which can mimic it","Treatment centres on protecting the area and interrupting the itch-scratch cycle (barriers, behavioural measures, and neuropathic agents such as carbamazepine); surgical repair fails unless self-manipulation is stopped"],"pathophysiology":"Trigeminal trophic syndrome develops after injury to the trigeminal nerve, ganglion, or its central connections, producing sensory loss and abnormal paraesthetic sensations in the affected dermatome. The disordered sensation drives compulsive rubbing, picking, and scratching of the numb skin, and this repetitive self-inflicted trauma to tissue that lacks protective sensation produces a chronic, painless ulcer, most often on the ala of the nose. Impaired trophic and sensory function of the denervated skin contributes to poor healing, but the primary driver is mechanical self-manipulation rather than an intrinsic ischaemic or infective process. Characteristic sparing of the nasal tip reflects its innervation by a separate nerve branch.","additionalDifferentials":["Basal cell or squamous cell carcinoma of the nose","Cutaneous infection (including mucormycosis and atypical mycobacteria)","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and other vasculitides","Pyoderma gangrenosum","Factitious dermatitis (dermatitis artefacta) elsewhere","Leprosy or other neuropathic ulceration","Cutaneous leishmaniasis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy of the ulcer edge to exclude malignancy and specific infection","Microbiological cultures including fungal and mycobacterial studies","MRI or CT of the brain and skull base to identify the underlying trigeminal lesion","Vasculitis screen including ANCA where relevant","Detailed sensory examination of the trigeminal territory","Review of prior trigeminal procedures, zoster, or stroke history"],"redFlags":["An indurated or non-healing ulcer suggesting malignancy requiring biopsy","Rapidly progressive necrosis raising concern for invasive fungal infection","Systemic features suggesting vasculitis","A new intracranial lesion accounting for the trigeminal injury"],"prognosis":"The ulcer is chronic and notoriously difficult to heal because it depends on stopping self-manipulation of insensate skin; with effective protection and behavioural interruption of the itch-scratch cycle healing can occur, but recurrence is common and reconstructive surgery fails if manipulation continues.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the ulcer for a skin malignancy or infection, or vice versa, without biopsy","Attempting surgical reconstruction without first controlling the self-manipulation","Failing to recognise nasal tip sparing as a diagnostic clue","Overlooking the often long latency between nerve injury and ulceration","Not addressing the underlying trigeminal lesion or the paraesthesia driving the behaviour"],"management":["Interrupt the self-manipulation that drives the ulcer, using behavioural modification, physical barriers, occlusive dressings and protective devices to break the itch-scratch-pick cycle in the anaesthetic skin","Provide meticulous wound care with dressings and treat secondary bacterial infection with appropriate antimicrobials","Control the neuropathic dysaesthesia with agents such as carbamazepine, gabapentin, pregabalin or amitriptyline, and consider an antipsychotic such as pimozide when a compulsive picking element predominates","Exclude and treat mimics by biopsy to rule out malignancy, deep infection and vasculitis before attributing the ulcer to the syndrome","Identify and address the underlying trigeminal lesion, whether post-ablative, post-herpetic, vascular or neoplastic","Defer reconstructive surgery until self-manipulation is reliably controlled, as flaps and grafts fail if picking continues, and consider innervated flaps when repair is undertaken","Manage jointly across dermatology, neurology, plastic surgery and psychology or psychiatry"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"trigger_finger":{"name":"Trigger finger","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["stenosing flexor tenosynovitis","digital tenovaginitis stenosans","trigger thumb","snapping finger"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon / standard sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The finger catches, clicks, or locks in flexion and may need to be actively or passively straightened, often with pain at the base of the digit","The problem lies at the A1 pulley over the metacarpophalangeal joint, where the flexor tendon cannot glide smoothly through a narrowed sheath","A tender nodule may be palpable over the A1 pulley and can be felt to move with the tendon","Symptoms are frequently worse in the morning, with the digit locked, and it is most common in the thumb, ring, and middle fingers","It is associated with diabetes and inflammatory arthritis, and these patients tend to respond less well to conservative treatment","Severity is graded from painful catching without locking through to a fixed, non-reducible digit","Initial management includes activity modification and splinting, with corticosteroid injection into the sheath being effective in many cases","Injection tends to be less successful in people with diabetes and in longstanding or multiple-digit disease","Surgical or percutaneous release of the A1 pulley is used for cases that fail injection or present with fixed locking"],"pathophysiology":"Trigger finger is a stenosing tenosynovitis at the first annular (A1) pulley. Thickening and fibrocartilaginous change of the pulley and of the flexor tendon or its sheath create a mismatch in size, so the tendon catches as it passes through. A nodule on the tendon may become trapped proximal to the pulley, producing the characteristic triggering, locking, and pain, with a mechanical block rather than a simple inflammatory process.","additionalDifferentials":["Dupuytren disease","Metacarpophalangeal or interphalangeal joint osteoarthritis","Flexor tendon or sheath infection","Ganglion of the tendon sheath","Focal dystonia or extensor tendon subluxation","Locked metacarpophalangeal joint from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["A clinical diagnosis based on the history and demonstrable triggering","Ultrasound to confirm tendon or pulley thickening and guide injection in uncertain cases","Screening for diabetes given the association and its effect on outcome","Plain radiographs only if joint pathology is suspected","Assessment for inflammatory arthritis when multiple digits are affected"],"redFlags":["Signs of flexor sheath infection such as fusiform swelling, severe pain on passive extension, and erythema","A fixed, non-reducible locked digit requiring timely surgical referral","Rapidly progressive or multi-digit involvement suggesting inflammatory arthritis","Neurovascular compromise of the digit"],"prognosis":"Many cases resolve with splinting or a single corticosteroid injection, though recurrence can occur; response is poorer in diabetic patients and longstanding disease, and surgical release is highly effective when needed.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a locked digit for a joint problem rather than tendon triggering","Missing flexor sheath infection presenting with a swollen painful digit","Repeated injections in diabetic patients with limited benefit and glycaemic disturbance","Injuring the neurovascular bundle or digital nerve during release"],"management":["Begin conservative treatment with activity modification and a splint holding the metacarpophalangeal joint in extension, using NSAIDs for pain","Give a corticosteroid injection into the flexor tendon sheath at the A1 pulley as the mainstay of treatment, which is often curative","Refer for surgical A1 pulley release, open or percutaneous, for refractory triggering or a fixed, locked digit","Screen for and optimise diabetes and inflammatory arthritis, which are associated and predict a poorer response to conservative care","Provide hand therapy with tendon-gliding exercises to restore smooth excursion","Monitor for recurrence and escalate to surgical release when repeated injections fail"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"trigger_thumb":{"name":"Trigger thumb","system":"musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["stenosing tenosynovitis of the thumb","pollex flexus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Stenosing tenosynovitis of flexor pollicis longus at the first annular (A1) pulley, causing catching, locking or triggering of the thumb interphalangeal joint on flexion and extension.","A tender palpable nodule (Notta node) is often felt over the A1 pulley at the metacarpophalangeal flexion crease of the thumb.","Adult disease is most common in middle-aged women and is associated with diabetes mellitus, rheumatoid arthritis, hypothyroidism and repetitive gripping; diabetic triggering responds less well to conservative measures.","Paediatric (congenital) trigger thumb typically presents as a fixed flexion deformity of the interphalangeal joint rather than true triggering, and a proportion resolve spontaneously in early childhood.","Grading progresses from painful triggering, to demonstrable catching that self-corrects, to locking requiring passive release, and finally to a fixed contracture.","First-line management in adults is activity modification, splinting and corticosteroid injection into the flexor sheath; injection is highly effective though less so in diabetics.","Surgical A1 pulley release (open or percutaneous) is definitive for refractory or recurrent cases; the radial digital nerve to the thumb lies close to the sheath and is at risk.","Persistent triggering after apparently adequate release should raise suspicion of an incompletely divided pulley or an unrecognised trigger at a more distal site."],"pathophysiology":"Trigger thumb arises from a size mismatch between flexor pollicis longus and the mouth of the fibro-osseous A1 pulley. Repetitive loading and shear at the pulley provoke fibrocartilaginous metaplasia and thickening of the pulley together with tenosynovial hyperplasia of the tendon, producing a localised nodule. As the thickened tendon segment is forced through the constricted pulley it catches, generating the characteristic triggering; progressive stenosis leads to locking and, ultimately, a fixed flexion contracture. Systemic conditions such as diabetes mellitus promote glycation and matrix changes that predispose to and perpetuate the stenosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Thumb metacarpophalangeal or interphalangeal joint osteoarthritis","Flexor pollicis longus tendon rupture or partial laceration","De Quervain tenosynovitis","Sesamoid pathology at the thumb MCP joint","Dupuytren nodule of the thumb ray","Retained foreign body or infective flexor tenosynovitis","Congenital clasped thumb in the paediatric setting"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical from history and examination of triggering and the Notta node","Ultrasound to confirm pulley thickening, tenosynovitis or a discrete nodule in equivocal cases","Screening for diabetes mellitus in recurrent or multi-digit disease","Thyroid function assessment where clinically suggested","Plain radiographs if concomitant joint arthritis or bony pathology is suspected","Inflammatory markers and rheumatoid screen when an inflammatory arthropathy is considered","Documentation of triggering grade and interphalangeal joint range of motion"],"redFlags":["Fixed interphalangeal flexion contracture that will not passively extend","Signs of infective flexor tenosynovitis (fusiform swelling, pain on passive extension, disproportionate tenderness)","Loss of active interphalangeal flexion suggesting tendon rupture rather than triggering","Sensory disturbance in the thumb suggesting digital nerve involvement"],"prognosis":"Generally excellent; most adults respond to corticosteroid injection and the majority of the remainder are cured by A1 pulley release, while a substantial proportion of congenital cases resolve spontaneously or after release. Diabetic patients have higher recurrence and lower injection success.","pitfalls":["Injecting or releasing without recognising an associated fixed contracture that requires additional therapy or splinting","Injuring the radial digital nerve, which crosses obliquely near the thumb A1 pulley, during percutaneous release","Dividing the oblique pulley in error, which can cause flexor pollicis longus bowstringing and loss of interphalangeal flexion power","Attributing a locked thumb to triggering when the underlying problem is joint arthritis or tendon rupture","Assuming poor injection response in diabetics reflects wrong diagnosis rather than expected reduced efficacy"],"management":["Manage adult disease first-line with activity modification, splinting and a corticosteroid injection into the flexor sheath at the A1 pulley, which is highly effective","Refer for surgical A1 pulley release for triggering refractory to injection or a fixed interphalangeal flexion contracture","For paediatric congenital trigger thumb, observe initially as a proportion resolve spontaneously, reserving surgical release for a persistent fixed flexion deformity","Screen for and optimise associated diabetes mellitus, hypothyroidism and rheumatoid arthritis","Provide hand therapy with stretching and tendon-gliding, particularly after injection or surgical release","Monitor for recurrence, which is higher in diabetic patients, and escalate to surgery when injection fails"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","British Society for Surgery of the Hand patient and clinical guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"trigger_thumb_pediatric":{"name":"Paediatric trigger thumb","system":"Paediatric orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["congenital trigger thumb","paediatric trigger thumb","pollex flexus","Notta's node","stenosing tenosynovitis of the thumb in children"],"chapter":null,"source":"Green's Operative Hand Surgery / Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a fixed or catching flexion of the interphalangeal joint of the thumb, usually noticed in toddlers rather than at birth, so it is frequently not truly congenital","A palpable nodule (Notta's node) is felt at the level of the A1 pulley over the volar aspect of the metacarpophalangeal region, representing thickening of the flexor pollicis longus tendon","The underlying problem is a size mismatch or stenosis between the flexor pollicis longus tendon and the A1 pulley, restricting smooth tendon gliding","Unlike in adults, painful triggering is uncommon; the usual presentation is a thumb held flexed at the interphalangeal joint, sometimes discovered when the child cannot straighten it","It is often bilateral, so both thumbs should be examined; the differential includes a congenital clasped thumb and a thumb-in-palm deformity","Many cases resolve spontaneously, particularly in the youngest children, so a period of observation with or without stretching and splinting is reasonable","Persistent fixed deformity is treated by surgical release of the A1 pulley, which is highly effective and durable"],"pathophysiology":"The condition arises from a mismatch between the flexor pollicis longus tendon and the mouth of the A1 pulley at the base of the thumb. Thickening of the tendon, forming the palpable Notta's node, together with relative narrowing of the pulley, impedes smooth gliding so that the thumb catches or becomes locked in flexion at the interphalangeal joint. Because the deformity is commonly absent at birth and only appears later in infancy or early childhood, it is regarded as a developmental or acquired stenosing tenosynovitis rather than a truly congenital anomaly.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital clasped thumb (an extensor deficiency)","Thumb-in-palm deformity, for example in cerebral palsy or spasticity","Trigger finger affecting the other digits","Arthrogryposis with thumb contracture","Post-traumatic tendon or joint injury","A skeletal anomaly limiting interphalangeal joint extension"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis by palpation of Notta's node and demonstration of fixed interphalangeal flexion or triggering","Examination of both thumbs given frequent bilaterality, and of the other digits","Assessment for associated neurological or arthrogrypotic conditions when the presentation is atypical","Radiographs only if a bony abnormality or an alternative diagnosis is suspected","Serial documentation of passive and active interphalangeal extension to track spontaneous resolution"],"redFlags":["A thumb that cannot be passively extended, suggesting a fixed contracture rather than simple triggering","Atypical features suggesting a neurological or syndromic cause","Multiple-digit involvement raising suspicion of a metabolic storage disorder (such as a mucopolysaccharidosis)"],"prognosis":"A substantial proportion resolve spontaneously, especially when identified in infancy; for thumbs that remain fixed, release of the A1 pulley gives reliable and lasting correction with a low complication rate.","pitfalls":["Assuming the condition is present from birth and labelling it congenital, when it usually appears later","Confusing it with a congenital clasped thumb, which is an extensor problem","Operating too early, before allowing a reasonable chance of spontaneous resolution","Injuring the digital nerves, which lie close to the A1 pulley, during release"],"management":["Offer initial observation, as many cases resolve spontaneously, particularly in the youngest children","Trial passive interphalangeal extension stretching and night extension splinting during the observation period","Reserve open release of the stenosed A1 pulley for thumbs that remain fixed in flexion or fail to resolve","Examine both thumbs and the other digits at each review given the frequent bilaterality","Document serial passive and active interphalangeal extension to track spontaneous resolution","Refer to a paediatric hand surgeon when the thumb stays locked or persistent triggering impairs function"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Green's Operative Hand Surgery","Tachdjian's Pediatric Orthopaedics"],"crossLinks":[]},"trimethylaminuria":{"name":"Trimethylaminuria (fish-odour syndrome, FMO3)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["trimethylaminuria","fish-odour syndrome","fish malodour syndrome","FMO3 deficiency","flavin-containing monooxygenase 3 deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by deficient flavin-containing monooxygenase 3 (FMO3), which normally oxidises malodorous trimethylamine to the odourless trimethylamine N-oxide (TMAO)","The dominant feature is a strong fishy body odour in sweat, breath, urine, and other secretions, which is socially and psychologically distressing despite being medically benign","The key diagnostic measure is the ratio of trimethylamine to trimethylamine N-oxide in urine, ideally after a modest choline/trimethylamine precursor load","Trimethylamine is produced by gut bacterial metabolism of dietary precursors such as choline, lecithin, carnitine, and trimethylamine N-oxide from fish","Management centres on dietary restriction of precursors, sometimes short courses of gut-flora-modifying antibiotics, and measures to lower skin surface pH such as slightly acidic soaps","Symptoms can fluctuate and may worsen around menstruation, with fever, stress, or high-precursor meals","Inheritance is autosomal recessive, and heterozygotes may have transient mild odour under precursor loading","British spelling applies (odour)"],"pathophysiology":"Dietary precursors including choline, lecithin and carnitine are metabolised by gut bacteria to trimethylamine, a volatile compound with a strong fishy smell. The hepatic enzyme flavin-containing monooxygenase 3 normally oxidises absorbed trimethylamine to the non-odorous trimethylamine N-oxide, which is excreted. When FMO3 activity is deficient, unoxidised trimethylamine accumulates and is excreted in sweat, urine, and breath, producing the characteristic odour. The condition is a metabolic capacity defect rather than a toxic disorder, so it causes social and emotional morbidity rather than organ damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Poor personal hygiene or exogenous malodour","Bacterial vaginosis or other localised infective odour","Secondary trimethylaminuria from heavy dietary precursor intake or liver disease","Transient trimethylaminuria in infancy or during febrile illness","Other metabolic malodour syndromes","Delusional or perceived body odour without objective malodour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinary trimethylamine and trimethylamine N-oxide measurement with calculation of their ratio","Precursor (choline) loading test to unmask reduced oxidising capacity","Molecular analysis of the FMO3 gene","Assessment of dietary precursor intake","Exclusion of hepatic dysfunction contributing to secondary odour","Screening for infective sources of malodour"],"redFlags":["Significant psychological distress, depression, or social withdrawal warranting support","Odour with systemic illness suggesting a secondary cause such as liver disease","Misdiagnosis leading to unnecessary or repeated antimicrobial treatment"],"prognosis":"The disorder is medically benign with a normal lifespan, but the persistent odour can cause considerable psychosocial distress. Symptoms are often controllable with dietary and hygiene measures, though not fully curable.","pitfalls":["Dismissing the complaint as poor hygiene or a psychiatric symptom without objective testing","Failing to standardise precursor intake before measuring the trimethylamine/TMAO ratio","Overlooking the substantial psychological impact and need for support","Attributing all odour to FMO3 defect without excluding secondary causes"],"management":["Restrict dietary precursors of trimethylamine, limiting choline, lecithin, marine fish and other sources rich in trimethylamine N-oxide","Use short courses of gut-flora-modifying antibiotics such as metronidazole or neomycin to suppress bacterial trimethylamine production during flares","Lower skin-surface pH with slightly acidic soaps and lotions to trap and reduce the odour","Offer riboflavin supplementation to maximise residual FMO3 activity, with sequestrants such as activated charcoal or copper chlorophyllin as adjuncts","Provide psychological support and counselling for the social and emotional distress caused by the persistent odour","Advise avoidance of recognised aggravators including stress and high-precursor meals, with attention to perimenstrual worsening","Arrange clinical genetics input and family counselling given the autosomal recessive FMO3 basis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID: trimethylaminuria and FMO3","Nyhan, Atlas of Inherited Metabolic Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"triosephosphate_isomerase_deficiency":{"name":"Triosephosphate isomerase deficiency","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TPI deficiency","triose phosphate isomerase deficiency","TPI1 gene"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most severe of the glycolytic enzymopathies, combining chronic non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia with progressive neuromuscular disease","Inherited as an autosomal recessive trait, frequently with consanguinity, and most patients present in infancy","A progressive neurological syndrome with spasticity, dystonia, tremor and motor neurone-type weakness typically emerges after the first months of life","Recurrent infections and increased susceptibility to bacterial illness are characteristic and contribute to early mortality","Cardiac involvement and sudden death may occur, adding to the poor prognosis","The metabolic hallmark is a marked accumulation of dihydroxyacetone phosphate in red cells, with reduced enzyme activity on assay and a confirmatory TPI1 mutation","Management is supportive with transfusion, folate and vigilant treatment of infections; no disease-modifying therapy exists and splenectomy offers limited benefit"],"pathophysiology":"Triosephosphate isomerase interconverts dihydroxyacetone phosphate and glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, ensuring both triose phosphates feed into the ATP-yielding steps of glycolysis. Deficiency causes accumulation of dihydroxyacetone phosphate and a fall in glycolytic energy production; in the red cell this shortens survival to produce a chronic non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia, while in neural and muscle tissue the same energy failure, together with toxic effects of the accumulated substrate, drives a progressive and often fatal neuromuscular disorder with heightened susceptibility to infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Pyruvate kinase deficiency","Glucose phosphate isomerase deficiency with neuromuscular features","Congenital neurodegenerative and mitochondrial disorders","Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","Other severe glycolytic enzymopathies","Hereditary spastic paraplegia and motor neurone disorders of infancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Red-cell triosephosphate isomerase enzyme assay","Measurement of red-cell dihydroxyacetone phosphate accumulation","Full blood count, reticulocytes and blood film","Haemolysis screen with bilirubin, lactate dehydrogenase and haptoglobin","Neurological assessment with neurophysiology and imaging","Molecular testing of the TPI1 gene"],"redFlags":["Progressive neuromuscular deterioration with respiratory compromise","Recurrent or severe bacterial infections","Cardiac involvement predisposing to sudden death","Severe neonatal jaundice requiring exchange transfusion","Aplastic crisis after parvovirus B19 infection"],"prognosis":"The classic infantile form carries a poor prognosis, with progressive neurological decline, susceptibility to infection and frequent death in early childhood; rare milder variants with residual enzyme activity have a better course.","pitfalls":["Focusing on the anaemia while missing the emerging neuromuscular syndrome","Underestimating infection risk and delaying antimicrobial treatment","Expecting splenectomy to substantially alter the systemic disease","Failing to offer genetic counselling given the recessive inheritance and severe phenotype"],"management":["Manage the chronic non-spherocytic haemolytic anaemia with folic acid supplementation and red-cell transfusion during severe anaemia or aplastic crises","Prevent and treat infection aggressively with vaccination and prompt antibiotics given the marked susceptibility to bacterial illness","Provide neuromuscular supportive care with physiotherapy and management of spasticity and dystonia, adding respiratory support as weakness progresses","Undertake cardiac surveillance for arrhythmia and cardiomyopathy given the risk of sudden death","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across haematology, neurology, cardiology and paediatrics, with early palliative involvement in the severe infantile form","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for the autosomal recessive TPI1 defect"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Wintrobe's Clinical Hematology"],"crossLinks":[]},"tripe_palms":{"name":"tripe palms","system":"Integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acanthosis palmaris","pachydermatoglyphy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by thickened, velvety palms with exaggerated dermatoglyphics giving a ridged, honeycomb appearance resembling the surface of bovine foregut (tripe).","Strongly paraneoplastic, with the great majority of reported cases occurring in association with an underlying malignancy.","The tumour association depends on coexisting acanthosis nigricans: tripe palms with acanthosis nigricans most often signals gastric carcinoma, whereas isolated tripe palms more often signals pulmonary carcinoma.","May be accompanied by the sign of Leser-Trelat and florid cutaneous papillomatosis as part of the same paraneoplastic spectrum.","Yellowish keratotic thickening of the palm with accentuated ridges over the finger pulps is typical.","Onset may precede diagnosis of the tumour and should trigger investigation for gastric and pulmonary primaries.","Skin changes may regress with treatment of the underlying malignancy and recur with relapse."],"pathophysiology":"Tripe palms share the growth-factor-driven mechanism of malignant acanthosis nigricans, with which they frequently coexist. Tumour secretion of transforming growth factor alpha, acting through the epidermal growth factor receptor, together with fibroblast growth factor and insulin-like growth factor 1, stimulates keratinocyte and dermal fibroblast proliferation. The result is marked palmar epidermal hyperplasia with accentuation of the normal dermatoglyphic ridges, producing the characteristic rugose, velvety surface.","additionalDifferentials":["Hereditary or acquired palmoplantar keratoderma","Malignant acanthosis nigricans (frequently coexists)","Pachydermoperiostosis","Hyperkeratotic hand eczema or palmar psoriasis","Occupational or frictional keratoderma","Keratoderma climactericum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT of the chest to identify lung carcinoma, especially with isolated disease","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with biopsy for gastric carcinoma when acanthosis nigricans coexists","CT of abdomen and pelvis for other primaries","Skin biopsy showing hyperkeratosis, papillomatosis and acanthosis","Sputum cytology or bronchoscopy where a lung primary is suspected","Tumour markers directed by the suspected primary"],"redFlags":["New tripe palms in an adult, particularly a smoker","Coexistent acanthosis nigricans or the sign of Leser-Trelat","Associated weight loss or systemic symptoms","Rapidly progressive palmar thickening"],"prognosis":"Tripe palms indicate an underlying, frequently advanced gastric or pulmonary malignancy; prognosis is that of the associated tumour and is often poor.","pitfalls":["Mistaking tripe palms for chronic eczema or occupational keratoderma","Not tailoring the tumour search to whether acanthosis nigricans coexists (gastric versus lung)","Missing an occult lung primary in a patient with isolated tripe palms","Failing to recognise recurrence as a sign of tumour relapse"],"management":["Direct treatment at the underlying malignancy, since the palmar changes typically improve when the causative gastric or pulmonary tumour is controlled","Investigate urgently for an occult primary, prioritising pulmonary carcinoma in isolated disease and gastric carcinoma when acanthosis nigricans coexists","Offer symptomatic skin softening with topical keratolytics such as urea, salicylic acid or topical retinoids","Monitor the palmar changes as a cutaneous marker, with recurrence prompting a search for tumour relapse or progression","Look for and address associated paraneoplastic signs including the sign of Leser-Trelat and malignant acanthosis nigricans","Coordinate care between dermatology and oncology, with tumour-directed treatment guided by stage"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"triple_a_syndrome":{"name":"Triple A syndrome","system":"Endocrine / Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Allgrove syndrome","AAA syndrome","4A syndrome","Achalasia-addisonianism-alacrima syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Endocrinology and gastroenterology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the AAAS gene (chromosome 12q13) encoding the nucleoporin ALADIN.","Classic triad: alacrima (deficient tear production), achalasia of the cardia, and adrenocorticotrophin-resistant adrenal insufficiency.","Alacrima is usually the earliest and most consistent feature, often present from infancy, and may precede other manifestations by years.","Adrenal insufficiency is characteristically an isolated glucocorticoid deficiency, with mineralocorticoid function often preserved initially but able to fail later.","Achalasia produces dysphagia, regurgitation, recurrent chest infections and failure to thrive, and may present with hypoglycaemia-related collapse when combined with cortisol deficiency.","Progressive neurological involvement (autonomic dysfunction, sensorimotor polyneuropathy, optic atrophy, ataxia, dysarthria and mild cognitive impairment) leads to the broader '4A' description with autonomic dysfunction.","Reduced tear production can be demonstrated at the bedside with a Schirmer test.","Consider the diagnosis in any child with unexplained adrenal insufficiency, hypoglycaemic seizures or early-onset achalasia."],"pathophysiology":"The AAAS gene on chromosome 12q13 encodes ALADIN, a WD-repeat protein that is a component of the nuclear pore complex. Pathogenic variants mislocalise ALADIN away from the nuclear envelope and impair nucleocytoplasmic transport, particularly of proteins involved in DNA repair and defence against oxidative stress, rendering neuroendocrine, neuronal and exocrine tissues selectively vulnerable. In the adrenal cortex the zona fasciculata becomes resistant to adrenocorticotrophin stimulation, producing glucocorticoid deficiency; degeneration of lacrimal gland function produces alacrima; and progressive loss of oesophageal myenteric plexus neurons produces achalasia with failure of lower oesophageal sphincter relaxation. A slowly progressive neurodegenerative process affects autonomic, sensory and motor pathways as well as the optic nerves, accounting for the multisystem and evolving nature of the disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial glucocorticoid deficiency (MC2R or MRAP mutations)","Autoimmune adrenalitis and autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes","X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Idiopathic primary achalasia","Sjogren syndrome and other causes of keratoconjunctivitis sicca","Adrenal hypoplasia congenita (NR0B1/DAX1)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Morning serum cortisol with adrenocorticotrophin (short cosyntropin) stimulation demonstrating a subnormal cortisol response","Plasma adrenocorticotrophin (markedly raised) with renin and aldosterone to assess the mineralocorticoid axis","Schirmer test and ophthalmology review for alacrima and keratoconjunctivitis","Oesophageal manometry (absent peristalsis, non-relaxing lower oesophageal sphincter) and barium swallow","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy to exclude pseudoachalasia and mucosal disease","AAAS gene molecular genetic testing","Nerve conduction studies and autonomic function testing to characterise neurological involvement"],"redFlags":["Adrenal crisis with hypotension, hypoglycaemia, collapse or seizures, which may be the presenting event and is life-threatening","Hypoglycaemic seizures in an infant or young child","Aspiration pneumonia secondary to achalasia","Autonomic instability with postural symptoms and cardiovascular lability","New or progressive neurological deterioration"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is variable: the life-threatening adrenal insufficiency is manageable with glucocorticoid replacement and achalasia is treatable, but the neurodegenerative features are progressive and represent the principal long-term morbidity.","pitfalls":["Attributing isolated dry eyes or alacrima to benign causes and missing the underlying syndrome.","Failing to screen for adrenal insufficiency, a preventable cause of sudden death.","Assuming mineralocorticoid function is always intact, as it can decline over time.","Overlooking the diagnosis in adults, in whom presentation may be delayed and incomplete.","Misdiagnosing achalasia-related respiratory symptoms as asthma or recurrent chest infection."],"management":["Replace glucocorticoid with hydrocortisone for the adrenocorticotrophin-resistant adrenal insufficiency, adding fludrocortisone if mineralocorticoid function later fails","Educate on sick-day rules, emergency parenteral hydrocortisone and a steroid alert card to prevent and treat adrenal crisis","Treat achalasia with pneumatic dilatation or Heller myotomy to relieve dysphagia and reduce aspiration","Manage alacrima with regular ocular lubricants and artificial tears to protect the cornea","Provide neurological and physiotherapy support for the progressive polyneuropathy, ataxia and autonomic dysfunction","Monitor the mineralocorticoid axis over time as aldosterone deficiency can emerge later","Coordinate endocrinology, gastroenterology, ophthalmology and neurology, and offer genetic counselling for the autosomal recessive AAAS defect"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology (standard reference)","Sperling Pediatric Endocrinology (standard reference)","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"triple_x_syndrome":{"name":"Triple X syndrome (47,XXX)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["trisomy X","47,XXX","triplo-X syndrome","XXX syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Often clinically unremarkable and frequently undiagnosed, so many affected females are never identified","Tall stature, especially long legs, is the most consistent physical feature","Learning difficulties, particularly language-based, and delayed speech are common, though IQ usually lies within the normal range while being somewhat lower than that of siblings","There is an increased risk of motor coordination difficulties and of behavioural and emotional problems, including anxiety","Fertility is usually normal, although premature ovarian insufficiency is somewhat more frequent","Most cases arise from maternal meiotic non-disjunction, with a mild maternal-age effect","Each additional X chromosome tends to be associated with a further reduction in IQ and more physical features, reflecting the general sex-chromosome aneuploidy trend"],"pathophysiology":"Triple X syndrome results from an additional X chromosome in a female, usually from maternal meiotic non-disjunction. Although the surplus X chromosomes are largely inactivated, genes that escape X-inactivation are present in extra dosage, which is thought to underlie the tall stature and the neurodevelopmental effects on language and learning. Phenotypic severity broadly increases with the number of extra X chromosomes, and mosaicism can attenuate the picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Constitutional or familial tall stature","Marfan syndrome and other overgrowth syndromes, for tall stature","Other sex-chromosome aneuploidies, including higher-grade X polysomies","Idiopathic developmental language disorder","Fragile X syndrome and other causes of learning difficulty"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype, which is diagnostic and often prompted by a prenatal screening result or a learning or behavioural assessment","Chromosomal microarray, which will detect the extra X","Developmental, speech and language, and educational assessment","Assessment of ovarian function if there is menstrual irregularity or subfertility","Mental-health assessment where indicated"],"redFlags":["Significant emotional or behavioural disturbance or a mental-health crisis","Features suggesting premature ovarian insufficiency","Marked developmental delay out of keeping with expectation, prompting a search for an additional diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Generally good; most individuals live independent lives with a normal lifespan, though learning and emotional support is often beneficial, and outcomes are better with earlier recognition and support.","pitfalls":["Assuming all individuals are affected equally, when many are asymptomatic","Overstating the severity to families and causing unnecessary alarm after an incidental prenatal finding","Missing language-based learning difficulties that respond to early intervention","Neglecting to counsel about the usually normal fertility and the small risk of ovarian insufficiency"],"management":["Provide early speech and language therapy and individualised educational support for language-based learning difficulties","Offer occupational therapy and physiotherapy for motor coordination difficulties","Provide mental-health support and monitoring for anxiety and emotional or behavioural problems","Monitor ovarian function and menstrual pattern given the increased risk of premature ovarian insufficiency, with fertility counselling as needed","Offer genetic counselling, reassuring that most cases arise sporadically from non-disjunction with low recurrence risk","Coordinate developmental follow-up so difficulties are recognised and supported early, which improves outcomes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"trismus_pseudocamptodactyly_syndrome":{"name":"Trismus-pseudocamptodactyly syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["distal arthrogryposis type 7","DA7","Hecht syndrome","Dutch-Kentucky syndrome","MYH8"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / Gorlin's Syndromes of the Head and Neck","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distal arthrogryposis characterised by limited mouth opening (trismus) and pseudocamptodactyly, in which the fingers flex only when the wrist is extended","The finger flexion is 'pseudo' because it is provoked by dorsiflexing the wrist and disappears when the wrist is flexed, reflecting short flexor tendons rather than fixed joint contracture","Trismus limits mouth opening and can impede dental care, feeding and intubation","Foot deformities such as talipes and short muscles may also occur","Caused by autosomal dominant variants in MYH8, encoding perinatal myosin heavy chain","Intellect is normal and the condition is non-progressive","Management is supportive with physiotherapy, stretching and occasional surgical release of tendons or the temporomandibular apparatus"],"pathophysiology":"Trismus-pseudocamptodactyly syndrome is caused by autosomal dominant variants in MYH8, which encodes perinatal myosin heavy chain expressed in developing and neonatal skeletal muscle. Abnormal myosin function alters muscle length and tone during development, leaving the long finger flexors and the muscles of mastication functionally short. The short flexor-digitorum tendons produce the characteristic wrist-dependent finger flexion (pseudocamptodactyly), while shortening around the temporomandibular apparatus limits mouth opening (trismus). Unlike true camptodactyly there is no fixed interphalangeal joint contracture, and the condition remains static after birth.","additionalDifferentials":["Other distal arthrogryposis subtypes with true camptodactyly","Isolated congenital trismus or temporomandibular joint ankylosis","Schwartz-Jampel syndrome","Freeman-Sheldon syndrome with microstomia","Camptodactyly from other causes","Dupuytren-like contractures"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical demonstration of wrist-dependent finger flexion and measurement of maximal mouth opening","Molecular genetic testing for MYH8 and a distal arthrogryposis panel","Three-generation pedigree and examination of relatives","Radiographs or imaging of the temporomandibular joint and hands where surgery is considered","Dental and maxillofacial assessment for trismus","Airway/anaesthetic evaluation before procedures given limited mouth opening"],"redFlags":["Anticipated difficult intubation from restricted mouth opening","Impaired dental hygiene and access leading to dental disease","Feeding difficulty in infancy","Progressive limitation of hand or jaw function"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is good, with normal intellect and lifespan; symptoms are static and functional limitations from trismus and finger tightness can be improved with stretching, physiotherapy and, in selected cases, surgery.","pitfalls":["Mistaking pseudocamptodactyly for a fixed joint contracture and misdirecting treatment","Proceeding to anaesthesia without planning for the restricted mouth opening","Overlooking the autosomal dominant inheritance in counselling","Neglecting dental access problems caused by trismus"],"management":["Use stretching exercises and physiotherapy to improve mouth opening and reduce finger flexor tightness","Consider surgical lengthening of the short finger flexor tendons or release procedures in selected cases with functional limitation","Correct associated foot deformities such as talipes with orthopaedic input where present","Provide dental and maxillofacial care adapted to the limited mouth opening","Flag the difficult airway from restricted mouth opening and plan anaesthesia carefully before any procedure","Offer genetic counselling and family assessment for the autosomal dominant MYH8 variant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Gorlin's Syndromes of the Head and Neck"],"crossLinks":[]},"trisomy_8_mosaicism":{"name":"Trisomy 8 mosaicism (Warkany syndrome 2)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Warkany syndrome 2","mosaic trisomy 8","T8M","mosaic trisomy 8 syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Deep, longitudinal plantar and palmar furrows are the most characteristic and useful clinical clue","Intelligence ranges from normal to moderate impairment, often milder than expected for an autosomal trisomy","Facial features include a prominent forehead, deep-set eyes, a broad upturned nose, and full lips","Skeletal anomalies are common, including joint contractures, restricted movement, vertebral and rib anomalies, and absent or hypoplastic patellae","Camptodactyly and other digital anomalies are frequent","Renal, ureteric and cardiac malformations should be actively sought","Complete non-mosaic trisomy 8 is almost always lethal in utero, so survival essentially implies mosaicism","There is a recognised, though small, association with haematological malignancy that warrants awareness"],"pathophysiology":"Trisomy 8 mosaicism reflects two cell lines, one with a normal chromosome complement and one carrying an extra chromosome 8, usually arising from post-zygotic mitotic non-disjunction. Because complete trisomy 8 is incompatible with survival, the viable phenotype depends on the proportion and tissue distribution of trisomic cells, which are highly variable and explain the wide clinical spectrum. The trisomic fraction detectable in blood often falls with age, so skin fibroblast analysis may be needed, and the tissue-limited nature accounts for the frequently milder-than-expected outcome.","additionalDifferentials":["Other autosomal mosaic trisomies","Overgrowth and connective-tissue syndromes featuring deep palmar or plantar creases","Distal arthrogryposis syndromes, for the contractures","Isolated congenital heart or renal malformation syndromes","Other dysmorphology syndromes such as Kabuki syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood karyotype, but with a low threshold for skin fibroblast or buccal karyotype and FISH because blood mosaicism may be low or lost","Chromosomal microarray, recognising low-level mosaicism can be missed","Renal tract ultrasound","Echocardiography","Skeletal and spinal imaging for vertebral and patellar anomalies","Developmental assessment","Awareness of and surveillance for haematological abnormalities"],"redFlags":["Features of a haematological malignancy such as unexplained cytopenias or bruising","Significant congenital heart disease","Progressive renal impairment from a structural anomaly"],"prognosis":"The outlook is generally more favourable than for most autosomal trisomies, with many individuals having normal or only mildly impaired intellect; prognosis depends chiefly on the severity of associated malformations and on any haematological complication.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis on a normal blood karyotype without testing fibroblasts","Missing the fall in blood mosaicism that occurs with age","Overlooking the recognised association with haematological disease","Underrecognising the diagnostic value of deep plantar furrows"],"management":["Screen for and manage renal, ureteric and cardiac malformations with imaging-guided surgical or medical correction as needed","Provide orthopaedic and physiotherapy management of joint contractures, vertebral and patellar anomalies and restricted movement","Offer developmental support and early intervention tailored to the degree of intellectual impairment","Maintain haematological surveillance given the recognised association of trisomy 8 with myelodysplasia and myeloid leukaemia","Coordinate multidisciplinary follow-up across cardiology, nephrology, orthopaedics and developmental paediatrics","Offer genetic counselling, noting the usually sporadic post-zygotic origin and low recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"trisomy_9_mosaicism":{"name":"Trisomy 9 mosaicism","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mosaic trisomy 9","T9M","mosaic trisomy 9 syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically severe, with marked developmental delay or intellectual disability and pre- and postnatal growth restriction","Congenital heart defects are frequent, including septal and conotruncal anomalies, and are a major determinant of survival","Central nervous system malformations are common, such as Dandy-Walker malformation, ventriculomegaly, and corpus callosum anomalies","Characteristic craniofacial features include microcephaly, a sloping forehead, deep-set eyes with small palpebral fissures, a bulbous nose, micrognathia, and low-set malformed ears","Skeletal and joint anomalies, including contractures or arthrogryposis and abnormally positioned digits, are typical","Genitourinary anomalies such as cryptorchidism and renal malformation are common","Complete trisomy 9 is usually lethal, and even in mosaic cases the prognosis is generally poor and correlates with trisomic-cell burden and malformation severity"],"pathophysiology":"Mosaic trisomy 9 reflects a normal cell line coexisting with a trisomy 9 line, generally through post-zygotic mitotic error or, less often, partial correction of a fully trisomic zygote. Because full trisomy 9 is largely incompatible with life, the surviving phenotype is shaped by the proportion of trisomic cells and their tissue distribution, which vary widely; even so, the disorder tends to be severe, with prominent effects on the developing brain, heart, and skeleton. As with trisomy 8, the trisomic fraction in blood may decline over time, so alternative tissues may be needed for confirmation.","additionalDifferentials":["Other autosomal trisomies, notably trisomy 13 and 18 and their mosaic forms","CHARGE syndrome and other malformation syndromes","Chromosomal microdeletion or microduplication syndromes","Syndromic Dandy-Walker malformation of other cause","Arthrogryposis syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood karyotype, with fibroblast karyotype and FISH if blood is normal or mosaicism is low","Chromosomal microarray","Echocardiography","Cranial imaging, ultrasound then MRI, for CNS malformation","Renal tract ultrasound","Skeletal assessment for contractures and other anomalies","Developmental evaluation"],"redFlags":["Critical congenital heart disease","Severe CNS malformation with hydrocephalus","Failure to thrive with recurrent aspiration"],"prognosis":"Generally poor, particularly with a high trisomic-cell fraction and major cardiac or CNS malformation; many affected pregnancies miscarry and surviving infants often have severe disability, though milder outcomes occur with low-level mosaicism.","pitfalls":["Relying on a normal blood karyotype and not proceeding to fibroblast analysis","Underestimating the trisomic fraction because it falls in blood with age","Missing associated cardiac or CNS anomalies on initial assessment","Assuming uniform severity when the mosaic burden varies widely"],"management":["Assess and manage congenital heart defects, including surgical repair of septal and conotruncal anomalies, as a key determinant of survival","Manage central nervous system malformations such as Dandy-Walker malformation and ventriculomegaly, with neurosurgical shunting for hydrocephalus where indicated","Address genitourinary and renal anomalies including cryptorchidism and renal malformation","Provide feeding support, physiotherapy and developmental intervention for the growth restriction and marked developmental delay","Coordinate multidisciplinary care and involve palliative services early in severely affected infants","Offer genetic counselling covering the mosaic post-zygotic origin and prenatal options in future pregnancies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"trochanteric_bursitis":{"name":"Greater trochanteric pain syndrome (trochanteric bursitis)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["greater trochanteric pain syndrome","GTPS","lateral hip pain","trochanteric bursopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Now understood as greater trochanteric pain syndrome, in which gluteal tendinopathy rather than primary bursal inflammation is the usual driver","Presents with lateral hip pain aggravated by lying on the affected side, prolonged standing, stairs and single-leg loading","Tenderness is localised over the greater trochanter","More common in women and in middle to older age","Provocative tests include resisted hip abduction and prolonged single-leg stance","Ultrasound or MRI can show gluteus medius and minimus tendinopathy with secondary bursal change","Management centres on load modification, restoring hip abductor function with physiotherapy and avoiding provocative positions rather than rest alone","Corticosteroid injection may give short-term relief but does not address the underlying tendon problem and carries a recurrence risk"],"pathophysiology":"Lateral hip pain historically attributed to inflammation of the trochanteric bursae is now recognised in most cases as greater trochanteric pain syndrome, driven by degenerative tendinopathy of the gluteus medius and minimus tendons at their insertion on the greater trochanter, often with secondary thickening of the adjacent bursae. Compressive load from hip adduction, such as crossing the legs or lying on the side, combined with tensile overload contributes to the tendon change. As with other tendinopathies the tissue shows disorganised collagen and failed healing rather than a predominantly inflammatory bursitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Hip osteoarthritis","Gluteal (abductor) tendon tear","Lumbar radiculopathy or referred spinal pain","Iliotibial band syndrome","Femoral neck stress fracture","Meralgia paraesthetica"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound to assess the gluteal tendons and bursal change and to guide injection","MRI for tendinopathy and tears and to exclude hip joint or bony pathology","Plain radiographs of the hip and pelvis to assess the joint and enthesophytes","Clinical provocative testing (resisted abduction, single-leg stance)","Assessment of the lumbar spine as a source of referred pain"],"redFlags":["Rest or night bone pain with systemic features suggesting infection or malignancy","Inability to weight bear or perform single-leg stance suggesting a significant abductor tear","Neurological signs pointing to lumbar radiculopathy","History suggesting a femoral neck stress fracture in an at-risk athlete"],"prognosis":"Most improve with load management and abductor rehabilitation, although symptoms can be persistent; refractory cases may benefit from shockwave therapy or, rarely, surgery for a confirmed tendon tear.","pitfalls":["Labelling the problem 'bursitis' and treating with rest or injection rather than progressive abductor loading","Missing a gluteal tendon tear or hip joint pathology","Overlooking referred pain from the lumbar spine","Repeated corticosteroid injections without accompanying rehabilitation"],"management":["Base treatment on load management and a progressive gluteal abductor tendon rehabilitation programme","Advise posture and activity modification to reduce compressive load, avoiding leg-crossing and prolonged lying on the affected side","Use simple analgesia and short-term non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for symptom control","Consider a peritrochanteric corticosteroid injection for short-term relief in refractory pain","Offer extracorporeal shockwave therapy for symptoms persisting despite rehabilitation","Reserve surgery for a confirmed gluteal tendon tear failing conservative care, and assess the lumbar spine as a source of referred pain"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","British Journal of Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"trochlear_nerve_palsy":{"name":"Fourth nerve palsy","system":"Nervous system / Neuro-ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["trochlear palsy","superior oblique palsy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Weakness of the superior oblique produces vertical and torsional diplopia that is worse on looking down and in, as when reading or descending stairs","Patients typically adopt a compensatory head tilt away from the affected side to control the diplopia","The Parks-Bielschowsky three-step test localises the palsy and shows worsening hypertropia on head tilt toward the affected side","It is the commonest cause of vertical diplopia and, because the nerve is thin with a long course, is frequently caused by head trauma","Congenital fourth nerve palsy is common and may decompensate in adulthood; old photographs showing a long-standing head tilt support this","Bilateral palsies, often traumatic, cause alternating hypertropia and prominent torsion"],"pathophysiology":"The trochlear nerve supplies the superior oblique, which intorts, depresses and abducts the eye; its palsy leaves the eye relatively elevated and extorted with impaired depression in adduction. The nerve is uniquely long, thin and the only cranial nerve to exit dorsally after decussating, crossing the tentorial edge, which makes it especially susceptible to closed head injury. Microvascular ischaemia is another common cause, while congenital laxity or absence of the tendon can present later when fusional reserves that had compensated finally fail.","additionalDifferentials":["Skew deviation","Myasthenia gravis","Thyroid eye disease affecting the inferior rectus","Partial third nerve palsy","Orbital floor fracture with inferior rectus restriction","Ocular tilt reaction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Orthoptic assessment with the three-step test and measurement of torsion","Comparison with old photographs for a long-standing head tilt","MRI of the brain and orbits when the palsy is not clearly traumatic or vasculopathic","Vasculopathic risk-factor evaluation","Acetylcholine-receptor antibody testing if myasthenia is possible"],"redFlags":["Additional neurological signs or other cranial neuropathies","Bilateral palsy without a clear traumatic explanation","Progressive deviation or associated headache","Features suggesting skew deviation from posterior fossa disease"],"prognosis":"Microvascular and many traumatic palsies improve over weeks to months; decompensated congenital and persistent palsies are well managed with prisms or superior oblique strengthening or other strabismus surgery.","pitfalls":["Confusing a decompensated congenital palsy with a new acquired lesion","Overlooking skew deviation as a mimic of vertical diplopia","Missing subtle torsion that helps localise the palsy","Attributing bilateral palsy to a unilateral cause"],"management":["Treat the underlying cause, optimising vascular risk factors in microvascular palsy and managing any lesion identified on imaging","Observe microvascular and many traumatic palsies, which commonly recover over weeks to months","Relieve diplopia symptomatically with Fresnel or ground-in prisms, or temporary monocular occlusion","Refer for strabismus surgery such as superior oblique strengthening or inferior oblique weakening when the deviation is stable but persistent","Recognise and treat decompensated congenital palsy, supported by old photographs, with prisms or surgery","Coordinate orthoptic, ophthalmology and neurology follow-up to monitor recovery and re-image if the palsy progresses or fails to resolve"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Walsh and Hoyt's Clinical Neuro-Ophthalmology","Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"tropheryma_endocarditis":{"name":"Tropheryma whipplei endocarditis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tropheryma whipplei endocarditis","Whipple endocarditis","blood-culture-negative endocarditis (Tropheryma whipplei)","cardiac Whipple disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by Tropheryma whipplei, the fastidious actinobacterium responsible for classic Whipple disease, which can localise to the heart valves","It is an important cause of blood-culture-negative endocarditis and should be considered when standard cultures remain sterile","Unlike classic Whipple disease, cardiac cases often lack the typical gastrointestinal symptoms of diarrhoea, weight loss, and arthralgia","It characteristically affects previously normal valves and may present insidiously with heart failure rather than the classic stigmata of endocarditis, often without fever","Blood cultures are negative, so diagnosis depends on examining excised valve tissue with PAS staining and, crucially, PCR for Tropheryma whipplei","Serology and echocardiographic evidence of valvular vegetation or destruction support the diagnosis alongside molecular confirmation","Treatment involves prolonged antibiotic therapy, often an initial intravenous agent followed by long-term oral co-trimoxazole, with valve surgery frequently required"],"pathophysiology":"Tropheryma whipplei is a slow-growing, environmentally acquired actinobacterium that cannot be recovered on routine blood-culture media, which is why the endocarditis it causes is culture-negative. The organism is taken up by macrophages, in which it persists, and a subtle defect in host immune handling is thought to allow chronic infection. In cardiac disease the bacterium colonises heart valves, driving a low-grade inflammatory process that produces valvular vegetations and progressive tissue destruction, frequently on valves that were previously structurally normal. The characteristic histological finding is an accumulation of foamy macrophages containing periodic acid-Schiff-positive material within the valve tissue. Because the illness is indolent and often lacks fever or the gastrointestinal features of classic Whipple disease, it commonly presents late with valvular regurgitation and heart failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of blood-culture-negative endocarditis (Coxiella burnetii, Bartonella species)","Culture-positive bacterial endocarditis with prior antibiotic exposure","Non-bacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis","Libman-Sacks endocarditis and other immune-mediated valvular disease","Classic Whipple disease with cardiac involvement","Degenerative valvular disease with heart failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Excised valve tissue examination with PAS staining and PCR for Tropheryma whipplei","Serology and specific molecular testing on blood where valve tissue is unavailable","Transthoracic and transoesophageal echocardiography to demonstrate vegetations and valvular damage","Multiple sets of blood cultures with extended incubation to document culture negativity","Broad workup for other fastidious causes of culture-negative endocarditis","Cerebrospinal fluid PCR if central nervous system involvement is suspected, given the risk of neurological Whipple disease"],"redFlags":["Progressive heart failure from valvular destruction","Embolic phenomena or new murmur with sterile blood cultures","Neurological features suggesting central nervous system involvement","Haemodynamic deterioration requiring urgent valve surgery"],"prognosis":"With valve surgery where indicated and prolonged appropriate antibiotics the prognosis is generally favourable, but delayed diagnosis, extensive valvular destruction, or central nervous system involvement worsen the outlook, and relapse can occur if therapy is insufficient.","pitfalls":["Failing to consider Tropheryma whipplei in blood-culture-negative endocarditis and omitting valve PCR","Excluding the diagnosis because classic gastrointestinal symptoms are absent","Discarding or fixing excised valve tissue in a way that prevents molecular testing","Using too short a course of antibiotics, risking relapse and neurological disease"],"management":["Give prolonged antibiotic therapy, typically induction with a parenteral agent such as ceftriaxone followed by long-term oral co-trimoxazole","Consider doxycycline combined with hydroxychloroquine as an alternative prolonged regimen with good tissue penetration","Refer for valve surgery where there is significant valvular destruction, heart failure or persistent infection","Ensure the regimen penetrates the central nervous system and assess cerebrospinal fluid given the risk of neurological Whipple disease","Manage heart failure medically alongside definitive antimicrobial treatment","Arrange prolonged clinical and echocardiographic follow-up to detect relapse, which occurs if therapy is insufficient","Coordinate infectious diseases, cardiology and cardiac surgery in a multidisciplinary approach"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","UpToDate: Whipple disease and Tropheryma whipplei infection"],"crossLinks":[]},"tropical_pulmonary_eosinophilia":{"name":"Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TPE","Weingarten syndrome","occult filarial lung disease","filarial hypereosinophilic lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases; WHO guidance on lymphatic filariasis","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A hypersensitivity reaction to lymphatic filarial parasites (Wuchereria bancrofti and Brugia species) affecting the lungs","Typically presents with paroxysmal nocturnal cough, wheeze and breathlessness that can mimic asthma","Occurs in residents of, or travellers from, filariasis-endemic areas of South Asia, Southeast Asia, Africa and the Pacific","Marked peripheral blood eosinophilia and a very high serum immunoglobulin E are hallmark laboratory findings","Antifilarial antibody titres are strongly positive, whereas microfilariae are characteristically absent from the peripheral blood because they are trapped and destroyed in the tissues","Chest imaging often shows diffuse reticulonodular or miliary shadowing, predominantly in the mid and lower zones","Diethylcarbamazine is the mainstay of treatment and usually produces rapid clinical improvement","Delayed or inadequate treatment can lead to chronic interstitial lung disease and fibrosis"],"pathophysiology":"Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia is an immunological hyperresponsiveness to lymphatic filarial worms rather than a direct tissue infection by adult worms in the lung. Microfilariae released into the circulation are rapidly cleared from the blood by an exuberant immune response and become trapped in the pulmonary vasculature and interstitium. There they trigger intense eosinophil recruitment and release of eosinophil granule proteins and inflammatory mediators, driven by a strong immunoglobulin E and antifilarial antibody response. This causes bronchospasm, alveolar and interstitial inflammation, and, if untreated, progressive interstitial fibrosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Bronchial asthma","Acute and chronic eosinophilic pneumonia","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Loeffler syndrome from transpulmonary helminth migration (Ascaris, hookworm, Strongyloides)","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Drug-induced pulmonary eosinophilia","Pulmonary tuberculosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film demonstrating marked eosinophilia","Serum total immunoglobulin E and antifilarial antibody serology","Chest radiograph and, where needed, high-resolution CT of the chest","Examination for the absence of circulating microfilariae (including night blood films in the filarial context)","Pulmonary function testing showing restrictive or mixed patterns","Exclusion of other causes of pulmonary eosinophilia (sputum for fungi and mycobacteria, stool for helminths)"],"redFlags":["Progressive breathlessness or hypoxaemia despite treatment, suggesting established fibrosis","Failure to respond to antifilarial therapy, prompting review of the diagnosis","Very high eosinophil counts with systemic features raising concern for a hypereosinophilic syndrome","Signs of an alternative serious diagnosis such as tuberculosis or vasculitis"],"prognosis":"With prompt antifilarial treatment most patients recover fully, but diagnostic delay or recurrent untreated episodes can result in chronic interstitial lung disease and irreversible pulmonary fibrosis.","pitfalls":["Managing the illness as ordinary asthma and missing the filarial cause","Expecting to find microfilariae in the blood, whose characteristic absence supports the diagnosis","Overlooking a relevant travel or residence history in an endemic region","Attributing the eosinophilia to allergy alone without serological confirmation","Under-treating and allowing progression to fibrosis"],"management":["Give a course of the antifilarial diethylcarbamazine as the mainstay to eliminate the microfilarial trigger","Add bronchodilators and, in severe or slowly responding disease, corticosteroids to control airway and interstitial inflammation","Monitor symptoms, eosinophil count and lung function for relapse, repeating the antifilarial course if symptoms recur","Watch for progression to chronic interstitial lung disease and pulmonary fibrosis when treatment is delayed","Exclude other causes of pulmonary eosinophilia before and during treatment","Advise on vector avoidance and filariasis exposure in endemic areas"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","WHO guidance on lymphatic filariasis"],"crossLinks":[]},"tropical_sprue":{"name":"Tropical sprue","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["post-infective tropical malabsorption","tropical enteropathy with malabsorption"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs in residents of, or long-stay travellers to, endemic tropical regions and presents with chronic diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, weight loss and fatigue","Characteristically causes malabsorption of both folate and vitamin B12, producing a macrocytic megaloblastic anaemia","Unlike coeliac disease it involves the entire small bowel including the ileum, which explains the B12 deficiency","Diagnosis is one of exclusion, requiring documented malabsorption and villous changes on small-bowel biopsy after ruling out parasitic and other infective causes","Small-bowel histology shows partial villous atrophy with crypt changes, generally milder than the total atrophy of coeliac disease","Management combines antibiotic courses for bacterial overgrowth of the small bowel together with folate and vitamin B12 replacement","A history of residence in the tropics, sometimes months or years earlier, is a key diagnostic clue"],"pathophysiology":"Tropical sprue is an acquired malabsorptive disorder of the small intestine occurring in tropical regions, believed to follow an enteric infection that leads to persistent colonisation of the upper small bowel by abnormal coliform bacteria. This chronic overgrowth, together with resulting mucosal injury and impaired motility, damages the villi throughout the small intestine and impairs absorption of nutrients including folate and vitamin B12. Deficiency of these vitamins further impairs enterocyte turnover, perpetuating villous atrophy in a self-sustaining cycle until the abnormal flora and deficiencies are corrected.","additionalDifferentials":["Coeliac disease","Giardiasis and other parasitic infections","Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth from other causes","Chronic pancreatic exocrine insufficiency","HIV enteropathy and other immunodeficiency-related enteropathies","Whipple disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and film with folate and vitamin B12 levels to detect megaloblastic anaemia","Small-bowel biopsy showing partial villous atrophy","Stool microscopy and specific tests to exclude parasitic and other infections","Coeliac serology to exclude coeliac disease","Assessment of fat malabsorption and other nutritional deficiencies"],"redFlags":["Profound weight loss and cachexia from prolonged malabsorption","Severe anaemia with symptoms from combined folate and B12 deficiency","Dehydration and electrolyte disturbance from persistent diarrhoea","Failure to respond to treatment, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"With appropriate antibiotic therapy and vitamin replacement the prognosis is generally excellent and relapse is uncommon once the patient has left the endemic area, though untreated disease can cause severe nutritional depletion.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with coeliac disease and missing the ileal involvement and B12 deficiency","Failing to exclude parasitic infection before making the diagnosis","Overlooking a remote history of tropical residence","Neglecting to replace both folate and vitamin B12 alongside antibiotics"],"management":["Give a prolonged course of a tetracycline such as tetracycline or doxycycline to eradicate the abnormal small-bowel coliform overgrowth","Replace folate and vitamin B12 to correct the megaloblastic anaemia and support enterocyte recovery","Correct associated nutritional deficiencies and provide nutritional rehabilitation with fluid and electrolyte replacement","Advise that leaving the endemic area aids recovery and reduces relapse","Monitor symptoms, haematological indices and nutritional status to confirm response to treatment","Reassess the diagnosis, excluding coeliac disease and persistent parasitic infection, if there is no response"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"troyer_syndrome":{"name":"Troyer syndrome (SPG20, SPART)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SPG20","SPART-related HSP","spartin deficiency","Troyer syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Troyer syndrome (SPG20) is an autosomal recessive complicated hereditary spastic paraplegia caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in SPART, encoding spartin","It was first described in an Old Order Amish population and follows recessive inheritance, so a consanguineous or founder-population background may be a clue","The phenotype combines childhood-onset spastic paraparesis with dysarthria, distal muscle wasting, and short stature","Developmental delay, cognitive impairment, and emotional lability are frequently part of the picture","Skeletal features and a characteristic slowly progressive gait disorder help distinguish it from pure HSP","Diagnosis rests on the clinical pattern supported by molecular confirmation of SPART variants","Management is symptomatic and multidisciplinary, targeting spasticity, mobility, speech, and developmental support"],"pathophysiology":"Troyer syndrome shares the core hereditary spastic paraplegia mechanism of length-dependent degeneration of the longest corticospinal axons, producing lower-limb-predominant spasticity. SPART encodes spartin, a protein implicated in endosomal trafficking, epidermal growth factor receptor turnover, mitochondrial function, and lipid droplet regulation. Loss of spartin disrupts these processes within neurones, and the extensive functions of the protein are thought to underlie the additional developmental, cognitive, and somatic features that make this a complicated rather than pure form of HSP.","additionalDifferentials":["Other complicated recessive hereditary spastic paraplegias (e.g. SPG11, SPG15)","Cerebral palsy with spastic diplegia","Metabolic or lysosomal storage disorders with spasticity and developmental delay","Mitochondrial encephalomyopathy","Dopa-responsive dystonia","Distal hereditary motor neuropathy when wasting predominates"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain and spine MRI to exclude structural causes and characterise any white-matter change","Molecular genetic testing of SPART, particularly in founder or consanguineous populations","Nerve conduction studies and EMG to assess distal wasting","Neuropsychological and developmental assessment","Metabolic screen to exclude treatable mimics presenting with spasticity and delay"],"redFlags":["Regression of previously acquired skills suggesting a progressive metabolic or storage disorder","Rapid neurological deterioration inconsistent with the usually slow course","New seizures or focal deficits prompting reassessment","Feeding or respiratory difficulty from bulbar involvement in advanced disease"],"prognosis":"Troyer syndrome progresses slowly; many affected individuals remain ambulant into adulthood, though gait, speech, and cognition decline gradually and disability increases with age.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing static cerebral palsy in a child with a slowly progressive genetic disorder","Not recognising the founder-population context that raises the pre-test probability","Attributing short stature and developmental features to unrelated causes rather than the syndrome","Overlooking the need for coordinated developmental and rehabilitation support"],"management":["Manage lower-limb spasticity with antispasticity agents such as baclofen or tizanidine, and focal botulinum toxin injections where appropriate","Provide physiotherapy, gait training, mobility aids and occupational therapy to preserve ambulation and function","Offer speech and language therapy for dysarthria and swallow assessment as bulbar features progress","Support cognition and address emotional lability with appropriate psychological and psychiatric input","Manage distal wasting, contractures and skeletal features with orthopaedic and rehabilitation input","Offer genetic counselling and family screening for the autosomal recessive SPART variant, especially in founder or consanguineous populations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Troyer Syndrome (SPG20)","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"truncus_arteriosus":{"name":"Truncus arteriosus","system":"Cardiovascular / Congenital","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["common arterial trunk","persistent truncus arteriosus","truncus arteriosus communis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A single arterial trunk arises from the heart and gives rise to the coronary, pulmonary and systemic circulations, overriding a ventricular septal defect","A single truncal valve, often abnormal and regurgitant or stenotic, guards the common trunk","Complete mixing at the trunk produces mild cyanosis combined with heavy pulmonary overcirculation once pulmonary resistance falls","Untreated infants develop early heart failure and are at risk of rapidly progressive pulmonary vascular disease","There is a strong association with 22q11.2 deletion and the DiGeorge phenotype, warranting evaluation for immune and calcium abnormalities","Classification is based on how the pulmonary arteries branch from the common trunk","Early surgical repair separates the pulmonary arteries from the trunk, closes the ventricular septal defect and interposes a valved conduit to the pulmonary circulation"],"pathophysiology":"Truncus arteriosus results from failure of the embryonic truncus to septate into separate aorta and pulmonary artery, leaving a single great vessel overriding a ventricular septal defect and receiving output from both ventricles. Complete mixing of systemic and pulmonary venous blood occurs at the trunk, so arterial saturation reflects the ratio of pulmonary to systemic flow. As neonatal pulmonary vascular resistance falls, blood floods the pulmonary arteries, causing marked pulmonary overcirculation, torrential left-to-right shunting and congestive heart failure with only mild cyanosis. The frequently abnormal truncal valve may be regurgitant or stenotic, adding to ventricular loading. Persistent high pulmonary flow accelerates pulmonary vascular obstructive disease, which becomes irreversible if repair is delayed. The lesion is strongly linked to 22q11.2 microdeletion.","additionalDifferentials":["Tetralogy of Fallot with pulmonary atresia and major aortopulmonary collaterals","Aortopulmonary window with ventricular septal defect","Large ventricular septal defect","Transposition of the great arteries with ventricular septal defect","Double outlet right ventricle","Interrupted aortic arch (which may coexist)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to demonstrate the single trunk, truncal valve, ventricular septal defect and pulmonary artery origins","Cardiac CT or MR angiography to define pulmonary artery and arch anatomy for surgery","Cardiac catheterisation to assess pulmonary vascular resistance when reversibility is uncertain","Genetic testing for 22q11.2 deletion with immune and calcium evaluation","Chest radiograph showing cardiomegaly and pulmonary plethora","Electrocardiogram for biventricular hypertrophy"],"redFlags":["Early severe heart failure from unrestricted pulmonary overcirculation","Signs of advancing pulmonary vascular obstructive disease","Significant truncal valve regurgitation or stenosis with ventricular dysfunction","Hypocalcaemia, immune deficiency or coexisting interrupted aortic arch in the DiGeorge spectrum"],"prognosis":"Without surgery the outlook is poor because of early heart failure and irreversible pulmonary vascular disease; with early repair survival is good, though conduit replacement and truncal valve problems commonly require reintervention.","pitfalls":["Delaying repair and allowing fixed pulmonary vascular disease to develop","Overlooking truncal valve dysfunction as a driver of ventricular failure","Missing associated 22q11.2 deletion with its hypocalcaemia and immune risks, including the need for irradiated blood products","Failing to detect a coexisting interrupted aortic arch"],"management":["Undertake early complete surgical repair with ventricular septal defect closure and a right-ventricle-to-pulmonary-artery conduit","Control preoperative heart failure medically with diuretics and afterload reduction to limit pulmonary overcirculation","Repair or replace the truncal valve when significant regurgitation or stenosis is present","Screen for 22q11.2 deletion and manage hypocalcaemia and immunodeficiency, using irradiated CMV-negative blood products until immune status is known","Provide lifelong surveillance for conduit stenosis needing reintervention and for progressive pulmonary vascular disease","Give endocarditis prophylaxis and coordinate congenital cardiology follow-up","Offer genetic counselling in view of the strong 22q11.2 deletion association"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","Anderson's Paediatric Cardiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"tubb4a_hypomyelinating_leukodystrophy":{"name":"TUBB4A-related hypomyelinating leukodystrophy","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TUBB4A leukodystrophy","HLD6","tubulinopathy hypomyelination","isolated hypomyelination with TUBB4A","DYT4-associated leukodystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A hypomyelinating leukodystrophy caused by variants in TUBB4A, encoding a central-nervous-system beta-tubulin isotype, spanning a wide clinical spectrum","At one end is severe hypomyelination with atrophy of the basal ganglia and cerebellum (H-ABC); at the other are milder or isolated hypomyelination and the dominant dystonia syndrome DYT4 (whispering dysphonia with generalised dystonia and ataxia)","Milder cases may show hypomyelination on MRI with little or no striatal atrophy, so imaging appearances range from pure hypomyelination to the full H-ABC pattern of a shrinking putamen and caudate","Clinical features commonly include a movement disorder (dystonia, spasticity, chorea), cerebellar ataxia, dysarthria and variable developmental involvement","Most disease-causing variants arise de novo and are dominant, though inherited variants (including the DYT4 variant) occur, so family history may be positive or negative","Diagnosis is guided by recognition of a hypomyelinating pattern with or without basal ganglia and cerebellar atrophy, confirmed by TUBB4A sequencing","There is no specific therapy; management is supportive and multidisciplinary, targeting dystonia and spasticity, communication, nutrition and physiotherapy"],"pathophysiology":"TUBB4A-related disease is caused by dominant variants in TUBB4A, which encodes a beta-tubulin isotype highly expressed in the brain. Tubulin heterodimers assemble into microtubules that are essential for cell shape, intracellular transport and division. Variant tubulin perturbs microtubule dynamics in oligodendrocytes and neurons; disrupted oligodendrocyte function impairs myelin formation to produce hypomyelination, while neuronal microtubule dysfunction contributes to degeneration of the striatum and cerebellum in more severe cases. The particular variant strongly influences where on the spectrum a patient falls, from isolated hypomyelination to full H-ABC.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypomyelination with atrophy of basal ganglia and cerebellum (the severe TUBB4A phenotype)","Pelizaeus-Merzbacher and Pelizaeus-Merzbacher-like disease","POLR3-related (4H) leukodystrophy","Hypomyelination with congenital cataract (FAM126A)","Other tubulinopathies and cortical malformation syndromes","Primary dystonia syndromes when the movement disorder dominates"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to characterise the degree of hypomyelination and any basal ganglia or cerebellar atrophy","TUBB4A sequencing, including analysis for the recurrent H-ABC variant and the DYT4 variant","Hypomyelinating leukodystrophy gene panel or exome if TUBB4A is negative","Metabolic screen to exclude treatable causes of a hypomyelinating or movement-disorder phenotype","Movement-disorder assessment to guide symptomatic management","Speech, swallow and developmental evaluation as clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Status dystonicus with severe dystonia, pain and metabolic disturbance","Progressive dysphagia with aspiration and faltering growth","Rapid neurological regression suggesting intercurrent illness or an alternative cause","Respiratory compromise from bulbar and truncal involvement"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies markedly across the TUBB4A spectrum: severe early-onset (H-ABC) disease is progressive with substantial motor and cognitive disability and reduced life expectancy, whereas milder hypomyelination and the DYT4 dystonia phenotype can follow a more indolent course. The specific variant is the main determinant of severity and rate of progression.","pitfalls":["Not appreciating the breadth of the TUBB4A spectrum and expecting only the severe H-ABC picture","Overlooking basal ganglia atrophy or, conversely, excluding the diagnosis when striatal atrophy is absent in milder cases","Assuming autosomal recessive inheritance when most variants are de novo dominant","Missing the DYT4 dystonia phenotype as part of the same gene's spectrum","Failing to counsel families about recurrence risk, including germline mosaicism"],"management":["Manage the movement disorder symptomatically, treating dystonia with agents such as trihexyphenidyl or baclofen and using botulinum toxin for focal dystonia","Provide physiotherapy and occupational therapy for spasticity, ataxia and mobility, with orthoses and aids as needed","Offer speech and language therapy for dysarthria and swallow assessment with nutritional support where feeding is impaired","Provide developmental support and manage any seizures","Coordinate multidisciplinary neurology-led follow-up across the wide clinical spectrum","Offer genetic counselling, noting most variants arise de novo while some, including the DYT4 variant, are inherited dominantly"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":["hypomyelination_atrophy_basal_ganglia_cerebellum"]},"tuberculous_lymphadenitis":{"name":"Tuberculous lymphadenitis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scrofula","TB lymphadenitis","tubercular lymphadenopathy","cervical TB adenitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India NTEP","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common form of extrapulmonary tuberculosis, especially in India, most often involving the cervical lymph nodes (historically termed scrofula)","Typically presents as a firm, painless, gradually enlarging node or matted nodes, sometimes progressing to a fluctuant cold abscess or discharging sinus","Constitutional symptoms such as fever, night sweats and weight loss may be absent, so a chronic painless node warrants consideration of tuberculosis in endemic settings","Fine-needle aspiration cytology showing granulomas with caseation is a key first-line, minimally invasive diagnostic step","Molecular testing such as cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification on aspirate improves sensitivity and detects rifampicin resistance","Paradoxical enlargement of nodes during or after treatment is common and does not necessarily indicate treatment failure","Treatment is with the standard multidrug anti-tuberculosis regimen, with surgery reserved for selected complications rather than routine care"],"pathophysiology":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis reaches lymph nodes via lymphatic or haematogenous spread, often from a primary pulmonary focus. Within the node, a cell-mediated immune response forms granulomas with epithelioid macrophages, Langhans giant cells and central caseous necrosis. Progressive necrosis can liquefy to form a cold abscess that may track to the skin and produce a chronically discharging sinus. The relatively contained immune response accounts for the frequent absence of systemic symptoms, while immune reconstitution or the intense host response during therapy can transiently worsen node size (paradoxical reaction).","additionalDifferentials":["Reactive or pyogenic bacterial lymphadenitis","Lymphoma","Metastatic carcinoma to lymph nodes","Non-tuberculous mycobacterial lymphadenitis (especially in children)","Sarcoidosis or cat-scratch disease"],"infectionMimics":["Non-tuberculous mycobacterial lymphadenitis","Pyogenic bacterial lymphadenitis","Cat-scratch disease","Fungal lymphadenitis (e.g. histoplasmosis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fine-needle aspiration cytology for granulomas and caseation","Molecular testing (cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification) and culture of aspirate or tissue","Excisional node biopsy with histopathology when cytology is inconclusive","Chest imaging to assess for concurrent pulmonary or mediastinal disease","HIV testing given the association with extrapulmonary tuberculosis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging or systemically unwell patient suggesting alternative diagnosis such as lymphoma","Airway or vascular compression from massive mediastinal or cervical nodes","Coexisting HIV with disseminated tuberculosis","Rifampicin resistance on molecular testing requiring a drug-resistant pathway"],"prognosis":"With the standard multidrug regimen the outlook is excellent, though nodes may enlarge paradoxically during treatment and residual firm nodes or scarring can persist; relapse is uncommon with adherence, and drug resistance worsens outcomes.","pitfalls":["Interpreting paradoxical node enlargement during treatment as failure and inappropriately changing therapy","Incising and draining a cold abscess routinely, which risks chronic sinus formation","Missing lymphoma or metastatic carcinoma by not biopsying when cytology is atypical","Overlooking drug-susceptibility testing and HIV status"],"management":["Treat with the standard multidrug antituberculous regimen of rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide and ethambutol in an intensive phase followed by a continuation phase","Escalate to a multidrug-resistant regimen guided by susceptibility when rifampicin resistance is detected","Recognise paradoxical node enlargement during treatment as usually not treatment failure, continuing therapy and adding corticosteroids or aspiration only if required","Perform aspiration or excision of a large fluctuant cold abscess or a persistently discharging sinus","Support adherence, including directly observed therapy, and monitor for drug toxicity and treatment response","Test for HIV and manage co-infection given the association with extrapulmonary tuberculosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/NTEP guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"tuberculous_meningitis":{"name":"Tuberculous meningitis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBM","TB meningitis","tubercular meningitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India NTEP","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most severe form of tuberculosis, carrying high mortality and long-term neurological morbidity, and a medical emergency where treatment must not await confirmation","Onset is typically subacute over one to three weeks with fever, headache, vomiting, altered behaviour and progressive decline, in contrast to the abrupt onset of pyogenic meningitis","Cranial nerve palsies, particularly of the sixth nerve, and features of raised intracranial pressure are common, reflecting basal meningeal involvement","Cerebrospinal fluid classically shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, high protein and low glucose, though early cellular responses may be mixed","Basal meningeal enhancement, hydrocephalus, infarcts and tuberculomas on neuroimaging support the diagnosis","Cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification on cerebrospinal fluid aids rapid diagnosis and detects rifampicin resistance, but a negative result does not exclude the disease","Management combines prompt multidrug anti-tuberculosis therapy with adjunctive corticosteroids and neurosurgical intervention for hydrocephalus, guided under India's NTEP for extrapulmonary tuberculosis"],"pathophysiology":"Tuberculous meningitis usually develops when a subependymal or subpial focus (a Rich focus), seeded during earlier haematogenous dissemination, ruptures into the subarachnoid space. The released mycobacterial antigens provoke an intense inflammatory exudate that is most marked at the base of the brain. This basal exudate entraps cranial nerves, obstructs cerebrospinal fluid flow to cause hydrocephalus, and induces a vasculitis of perforating arteries leading to ischaemic infarction. The combination of inflammation, raised intracranial pressure and vascular compromise underlies the cranial neuropathies, strokes and depressed consciousness that characterise severe disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Partially treated pyogenic bacterial meningitis","Cryptococcal or other fungal meningitis","Viral meningoencephalitis","Carcinomatous or lymphomatous meningitis","Neurosarcoidosis"],"infectionMimics":["Cryptococcal meningitis","Partially treated bacterial meningitis","Viral meningoencephalitis","Neurobrucellosis or neurosyphilis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lumbar puncture with cerebrospinal fluid cell count, protein, glucose and opening pressure","Cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification and mycobacterial culture of cerebrospinal fluid","Contrast neuroimaging (CT or MRI) for basal enhancement, hydrocephalus, infarcts and tuberculomas","HIV testing and evaluation for tuberculosis elsewhere in the body","Repeat or large-volume cerebrospinal fluid sampling to improve diagnostic yield"],"redFlags":["Declining consciousness, seizures or focal neurological deficit indicating advanced disease","Hydrocephalus or signs of raised intracranial pressure requiring urgent neurosurgical assessment","Rapid clinical deterioration mandating immediate empirical treatment before confirmation","Rifampicin resistance or HIV co-infection worsening prognosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on the neurological stage at which treatment begins; early treatment offers good recovery, whereas presentation with coma, deficits or advanced stage carries high mortality and frequent long-term sequelae such as cognitive impairment, hydrocephalus and paresis.","pitfalls":["Withholding anti-tuberculosis therapy while awaiting confirmatory tests, allowing irreversible deterioration","Relying on a negative molecular test or culture to exclude the diagnosis","Missing hydrocephalus that needs neurosurgical drainage","Omitting adjunctive corticosteroids where indicated or overlooking HIV co-infection"],"management":["Start antituberculous therapy urgently without awaiting confirmation, using a prolonged regimen with agents that penetrate the central nervous system","Add adjunctive corticosteroids such as dexamethasone to reduce mortality and neurological morbidity","Treat hydrocephalus with cerebrospinal fluid diversion such as an external ventricular drain or shunt, and manage raised intracranial pressure and seizures","Adjust therapy for drug resistance guided by rapid molecular testing and culture","Test for HIV and coordinate antiretroviral therapy, with attention to timing and immune reconstitution","Monitor for complications and long-term sequelae such as hydrocephalus, cognitive impairment and paresis, with rehabilitation input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/NTEP guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"tuberous_sclerosis":{"name":"Tuberous sclerosis complex","system":"Genetic / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TSC","tuberous sclerosis","Bourneville disease","epiloia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard medical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Multisystem disorder characterised by benign hamartomas affecting the brain, skin, kidneys, heart, and eyes","Skin findings include hypomelanotic macules (ash-leaf spots), facial angiofibromas, shagreen patches, and periungual fibromas","Neurological features include cortical tubers, subependymal nodules, epilepsy (often including infantile spasms), and cognitive and behavioural difficulties within the TSC-associated neuropsychiatric spectrum","Cardiac rhabdomyomas may be detected antenatally or in infancy and often regress spontaneously","Renal angiomyolipomas and cysts can bleed or impair function and require surveillance","Caused by mutations in TSC1 (hamartin) or TSC2 (tuberin), tumour suppressors that normally restrain the mTOR signalling pathway","Autosomal dominant inheritance with variable expressivity and a high rate of de novo mutations","mTOR inhibitor therapy can shrink certain lesions such as subependymal giant cell astrocytomas and angiomyolipomas under specialist care"],"pathophysiology":"Tuberous sclerosis complex is caused by inactivating mutations in either TSC1, encoding hamartin, or TSC2, encoding tuberin. Together these proteins form a complex that inhibits the mechanistic target of rapamycin (mTOR) signalling pathway, which controls cell growth and proliferation. Loss of function releases this inhibition, leading to constitutive mTOR activation and the growth of benign hamartomatous lesions across multiple organs. Inheritance is autosomal dominant, though many cases are de novo, and lesion formation is consistent with a second somatic hit inactivating the remaining allele. The mechanistic understanding underpins the therapeutic use of mTOR inhibitors.","additionalDifferentials":["Neurofibromatosis type 1","Isolated epilepsy or infantile spasms of other cause","Multiple endocrine neoplasia and other hamartoma syndromes","Sporadic angiomyolipoma or lymphangioleiomyomatosis","Vitiligo or other causes of hypopigmented macules"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assessment against established clinical diagnostic criteria supported by TSC1 or TSC2 molecular testing","Brain magnetic resonance imaging for tubers, subependymal nodules, and giant cell astrocytomas","Electroencephalography for seizure evaluation","Renal imaging for angiomyolipomas and cysts","Echocardiography, ophthalmological examination, and skin assessment, with neurodevelopmental evaluation"],"redFlags":["Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma causing hydrocephalus and raised intracranial pressure requiring urgent intervention","Poorly controlled seizures, including infantile spasms, which need prompt treatment to protect development","Large or bleeding renal angiomyolipomas with risk of life-threatening haemorrhage","Cardiac rhabdomyomas causing outflow obstruction or arrhythmia in infancy"],"prognosis":"Outcome is highly variable; many individuals live full lives, but epilepsy severity, cognitive impairment, and organ complications such as giant cell astrocytomas and renal or pulmonary disease determine morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Overlooking subtle skin signs such as hypomelanotic macules that aid early diagnosis","Failing to arrange surveillance for growing subependymal giant cell astrocytomas and renal lesions","Delaying treatment of infantile spasms with consequences for development","Not recognising the neuropsychiatric spectrum and its impact on quality of life"],"management":["Use mTOR inhibitors such as everolimus for subependymal giant cell astrocytomas and growing renal angiomyolipomas, and topical sirolimus for facial angiofibromas","Manage epilepsy with antiseizure medication, using vigabatrin as first-line for infantile spasms, and consider epilepsy surgery or vagus nerve stimulation in refractory cases","Treat renal angiomyolipomas at risk of haemorrhage with an mTOR inhibitor or selective arterial embolisation, preserving nephrons","Undertake regular surveillance with brain MRI, renal imaging, echocardiography, and ophthalmological and skin review","Assess and support the TSC-associated neuropsychiatric disorders including learning, behavioural and cognitive difficulties","Coordinate multidisciplinary care and offer genetic counselling and family screening for the autosomal dominant TSC1 or TSC2 mutation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"tuberous_sclerosis_complex":{"name":"Tuberous sclerosis complex","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TSC","tuberous sclerosis","Bourneville disease","TSC1","TSC2","hamartin","tuberin"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A multisystem disorder characterised by benign hamartomas in the brain, skin, heart, kidneys, lungs and eyes, following autosomal dominant inheritance with a high de novo rate","Skin findings are key diagnostic clues: hypomelanotic macules (best seen under Wood's lamp), facial angiofibromas, shagreen patches and periungual fibromas","Neurological features include cortical tubers, subependymal nodules and subependymal giant cell astrocytomas, with epilepsy (often infantile spasms) and TSC-associated neuropsychiatric disorders","Cardiac rhabdomyomas may be detected antenatally or in infancy and usually regress spontaneously","Renal angiomyolipomas and cysts require surveillance because of the risk of haemorrhage and, rarely, malignancy, and are a leading cause of adult morbidity","Lymphangioleiomyomatosis, causing progressive cystic lung disease, predominantly affects women and warrants respiratory monitoring","The mTOR pathway is constitutively activated, and mTOR inhibitors are an established targeted therapy for several manifestations"],"pathophysiology":"Tuberous sclerosis complex is caused by loss-of-function variants in TSC1 (encoding hamartin) or TSC2 (encoding tuberin), whose protein product forms a complex that inhibits the mechanistic target of rapamycin (mTOR) signalling pathway. Loss of this inhibitory brake leads to constitutive mTOR activation, driving unregulated cell growth and proliferation and the formation of hamartomas in multiple organs. A second-hit somatic inactivation of the remaining allele in individual cells underlies lesion development, and TSC2 variants generally produce a more severe phenotype than TSC1.","additionalDifferentials":["Neurofibromatosis type 1","Sturge-Weber syndrome","Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome","Multiple endocrine neoplasia and other hamartoma syndromes","Isolated infantile spasms of other cause","Sporadic renal angiomyolipoma or lymphangioleiomyomatosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis of TSC1 and TSC2","Brain MRI for tubers, subependymal nodules and giant cell astrocytoma, with electroencephalography for seizures","Renal imaging (ultrasound or MRI) for angiomyolipomas and cysts with periodic surveillance","Echocardiography and electrocardiography for rhabdomyomas and arrhythmia","Dermatological and ophthalmological (retinal hamartoma) examination","Pulmonary function testing and chest imaging for lymphangioleiomyomatosis in at-risk individuals"],"redFlags":["New or worsening headache, vomiting or focal deficit suggesting an enlarging subependymal giant cell astrocytoma with hydrocephalus","Flank pain, haematuria or shock from angiomyolipoma haemorrhage","Progressive breathlessness or pneumothorax from lymphangioleiomyomatosis","Infantile spasms requiring prompt recognition and treatment"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is highly variable and depends on the burden of neurological, renal and pulmonary disease; epilepsy and neuropsychiatric disorders drive childhood morbidity, while renal and pulmonary complications dominate in adults. Targeted mTOR-inhibitor therapy and structured surveillance have improved outcomes, and many individuals live into adulthood.","pitfalls":["Overlooking subtle hypomelanotic macules without Wood's-lamp examination","Failing to establish lifelong renal surveillance for angiomyolipoma growth and bleeding risk","Missing an enlarging subependymal giant cell astrocytoma causing obstructive hydrocephalus","Not recognising TSC-associated neuropsychiatric disorders, which are frequently under-assessed and undertreated"],"management":["Everolimus or another mTOR inhibitor as disease-modifying therapy for subependymal giant cell astrocytomas, enlarging renal angiomyolipomas and lymphangioleiomyomatosis","Epilepsy control with antiseizure medication, using vigabatrin as first-line for infantile spasms and considering epilepsy surgery or vagus nerve stimulation in drug-resistant seizures","Topical mTOR inhibitor (sirolimus) or laser therapy for facial angiofibromas, with screening and support for TSC-associated neuropsychiatric disorders","Structured surveillance with serial brain MRI, renal imaging, echocardiography and pulmonary function testing for the multisystem complications","Manage acute angiomyolipoma haemorrhage with selective arterial embolisation and nephron-sparing measures rather than nephrectomy where possible","Multidisciplinary care across neurology, nephrology, dermatology, respiratory and ophthalmology","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal dominant inheritance and the high de novo rate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","International Tuberous Sclerosis Complex Consensus Group recommendations"],"crossLinks":[]},"tuberous_sclerosis_skin":{"name":"tuberous sclerosis skin","system":"integumentary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cutaneous manifestations of tuberous sclerosis complex","tuberous sclerosis dermatological features"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Cutaneous signs are among the earliest and most useful diagnostic clues to tuberous sclerosis complex and feature prominently in the diagnostic criteria.","Hypomelanotic (ash-leaf) macules are frequently present from infancy and are best demonstrated under Wood's lamp examination.","Facial angiofibromas develop over the nasolabial folds, cheeks and chin in childhood and are commonly mistaken for acne.","A shagreen patch is a connective-tissue naevus, typically a raised plaque over the lumbosacral region with an orange-peel texture.","Peri- and subungual fibromas (Koenen tumours) tend to appear later, around puberty and adulthood.","A fibrous cephalic (forehead) plaque is a specific skin marker and may precede other features.","The disorder arises from loss-of-function mutations in TSC1 or TSC2 with consequent overactivation of the mTOR pathway.","Topical or systemic mTOR inhibitors can reduce facial angiofibromas and address systemic manifestations under specialist supervision."],"pathophysiology":"Tuberous sclerosis complex is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by inactivating mutations in TSC1 (encoding hamartin) or TSC2 (encoding tuberin). The hamartin-tuberin complex normally inhibits the mechanistic target of rapamycin (mTOR) signalling pathway; its loss releases constitutive mTOR activation, driving unchecked cell growth and the formation of hamartomas across multiple organs. In the skin this dysregulated growth produces the characteristic lesions: hypomelanotic macules reflecting reduced melanogenesis, angiofibromas composed of fibrovascular proliferation, connective-tissue naevi (shagreen patches), and periungual fibromas. The same pathway underlies the systemic hamartomas of brain, kidney, heart and lung, which is why recognition of the skin signs prompts wider evaluation.","additionalDifferentials":["Acne vulgaris or rosacea (mimicking facial angiofibromas)","Isolated hypopigmentation such as naevus depigmentosus or vitiligo","Birt-Hogg-Dubé syndrome (fibrofolliculomas)","Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 with cutaneous collagenomas","Neurofibromatosis type 1 with café-au-lait macules","Idiopathic periungual fibromas from trauma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wood's lamp examination to highlight hypomelanotic macules","Genetic testing for TSC1 and TSC2 mutations","Brain MRI for cortical tubers, subependymal nodules and giant cell astrocytoma","Renal imaging for angiomyolipomas and cysts","Echocardiography for cardiac rhabdomyomas, especially in infancy","Ophthalmological review for retinal hamartomas"],"redFlags":["New focal neurological deficit or rising intracranial pressure suggesting subependymal giant cell astrocytoma","Flank pain or haematuria indicating angiomyolipoma haemorrhage","Respiratory symptoms in women suggesting lymphangioleiomyomatosis","Refractory seizures or developmental regression requiring urgent neurological input"],"prognosis":"Cutaneous lesions themselves are benign but can cause considerable cosmetic and psychological burden; overall prognosis is determined by systemic involvement, particularly neurological disease, renal angiomyolipomas and pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis, and has improved with mTOR-inhibitor therapy and multidisciplinary surveillance.","pitfalls":["Treating facial angiofibromas as acne and thereby missing the underlying diagnosis.","Overlooking subtle hypomelanotic macules without Wood's lamp examination.","Assessing skin in isolation and failing to arrange systemic screening once the diagnosis is suspected.","Attributing a solitary skin finding to tuberous sclerosis without applying the full diagnostic criteria.","Neglecting genetic counselling given the autosomal dominant inheritance and variable expressivity."],"management":["Topical mTOR inhibitor (sirolimus) applied to facial angiofibromas to reduce their size and vascularity","Ablative or vascular laser therapy, and surgical excision, dermabrasion or curettage for angiofibromas, fibrous cephalic plaques and Koenen tumours","Skin camouflage and psychological support for the cosmetic and social burden of visible lesions","Recognition of the cutaneous signs as a trigger for full multisystem evaluation and systemic mTOR inhibitor therapy where brain, renal or pulmonary disease is present","Periodic dermatological review as lesions such as periungual fibromas emerge and evolve through adolescence and adulthood","Multidisciplinary referral to clinical genetics, neurology and nephrology","Genetic counselling and family screening for autosomal dominant tuberous sclerosis complex"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine","International Tuberous Sclerosis Complex Consensus Group diagnostic criteria"],"crossLinks":[]},"tubular_aggregate_myopathy":{"name":"Tubular aggregate myopathy (STIM1/ORAI1)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TAM","STIM1-related tubular aggregate myopathy","ORAI1-related myopathy","Stormorken syndrome spectrum","store-operated calcium entry myopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused most often by gain-of-function variants in STIM1 or ORAI1, the key components of store-operated calcium entry; inheritance is usually autosomal dominant","Presents with slowly progressive proximal limb weakness, muscle cramps, myalgia, and exercise intolerance, often from childhood or early adulthood","STIM1 gain-of-function variants can cause Stormorken syndrome, a multisystem disorder combining myopathy with thrombocytopenia, bleeding tendency, miosis, and other features","Creatine kinase is often mildly to moderately elevated and electromyography shows a myopathic pattern","The defining biopsy feature is tubular aggregates, densely packed arrays of tubules derived from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, typically in type 2 fibres and best seen with oxidative and Gomori trichrome stains and electron microscopy","Tubular aggregates are a non-specific reaction pattern that also occurs in other conditions, so genetic and clinical correlation is needed","Assessment should include a platelet count and bleeding history given the Stormorken-syndrome association with STIM1 variants","Diagnosis combines the myopathic phenotype, tubular aggregates on biopsy, and STIM1 or ORAI1 genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Tubular aggregate myopathy arises from dysregulated store-operated calcium entry, the process by which depletion of sarcoplasmic reticulum calcium is sensed by STIM1 and triggers calcium influx through the ORAI1 channel. Gain-of-function variants in STIM1 or ORAI1 cause constitutive or excessive calcium entry, leading to calcium overload and abnormal proliferation and packing of sarcoplasmic reticulum membranes that form the characteristic tubular aggregates. Disturbed calcium homeostasis impairs muscle function, producing weakness, cramps, and exercise intolerance, and the same pathway abnormality accounts for the platelet and other systemic features of Stormorken syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Metabolic myopathies with exercise intolerance and cramps","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","Periodic paralysis and other channelopathies","Congenital myasthenic syndrome","Inflammatory myopathy","Other causes of tubular aggregates such as periodic paralysis or drug/toxin exposure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography","Muscle biopsy with oxidative enzyme and Gomori trichrome staining and electron microscopy to demonstrate tubular aggregates","Full blood count and bleeding assessment to detect thrombocytopenia in the Stormorken spectrum","Muscle MRI to characterise involvement","Genetic testing of STIM1 and ORAI1 and a broader myopathy panel"],"redFlags":["Bleeding tendency or thrombocytopenia suggesting Stormorken syndrome","Multisystem features such as miosis, asplenia, or ichthyosis in the STIM1 spectrum","Rapidly progressive weakness atypical for the usual slow course","Respiratory or cardiac involvement warranting evaluation"],"prognosis":"Muscle weakness typically progresses slowly and most patients remain ambulant, giving a generally favourable neuromuscular prognosis; in the Stormorken-syndrome end of the STIM1 spectrum, the bleeding tendency and multisystem involvement add to morbidity and require additional management.","pitfalls":["Regarding tubular aggregates as diagnostic of a single disease when they are a non-specific reaction pattern","Missing the associated thrombocytopenia and bleeding risk of Stormorken syndrome in STIM1 disease","Overlooking a treatable alternative such as a metabolic myopathy or channelopathy","Failing to confirm the diagnosis genetically after finding tubular aggregates on biopsy"],"management":["No disease-specific pharmacotherapy exists; management centres on tailored physiotherapy and graded low-impact exercise to preserve proximal strength and limit deconditioning","Symptomatic management of muscle cramps, myalgia and exercise intolerance with activity pacing","Occupational therapy, orthoses and mobility aids to maintain function and ambulation","Periodic monitoring of muscle strength and of respiratory and bulbar function as weakness progresses","In the Stormorken/STIM1 spectrum, surveillance for thrombocytopenia with bleeding precautions and platelet support around surgery or bleeding episodes","Multidisciplinary neuromuscular team involvement","Genetic counselling given the usually autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: STIM1- and ORAI1-related tubular aggregate myopathy and Stormorken syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"tubulointerstitial_disease":{"name":"Tubulointerstitial Kidney Disease","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["interstitial nephritis","acute interstitial nephritis","renal tubular acidosis"],"chapter":328,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2437-2444","clinicalPearls":["Tubulointerstitial diseases primarily affect the tubules and interstitium with relative sparing of the glomeruli and renal vessels, and are divided into acute and chronic tubulointerstitial nephritis (TIN) (Harrison 22e p.2437).","Classic allergic acute interstitial nephritis (AIN) presents with fever, rash, peripheral eosinophilia, and oliguric acute kidney injury occurring after 7-10 days of a beta-lactam (e.g., methicillin) - but this classic tetrad is the exception rather than the rule; patients are more often found incidentally to have a rising creatinine (Harrison 22e p.2438).","Acute TIN most often presents as acute kidney injury; today AIN is far more often an allergic reaction to a drug than a postinfectious phenomenon (Harrison 22e p.2437).","Urinalysis in allergic AIN shows pyuria with white blood cell casts and hematuria; white blood cell (leukocyte) casts are a characteristic finding of interstitial nephritis (Harrison 22e p.2438, p.2456).","The mainstay of treatment for drug-induced AIN is discontinuation of the offending agent, which often leads to reversal of kidney injury; glucocorticoids may accelerate recovery but are NOT required in most cases and are reserved for severe injury (Harrison 22e p.2438).","Chronic TIN is more indolent and manifests with disorders of tubular function: polyuria from impaired concentrating ability (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus), Fanconi's syndrome from defective proximal reabsorption (glycosuria, phosphaturia, aminoaciduria, hypokalemia, and type II/proximal RTA from bicarbonaturia), or non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with hyperkalemia (type IV RTA) from impaired ammoniagenesis (Harrison 22e p.2437).","Proteinuria in chronic TIN is usually modest (rarely >2 g/d) from decreased tubular reabsorption of filtered proteins, but nephrotic-range albuminuria may occur when secondary focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS) develops (Harrison 22e p.2437).","Tubulointerstitial nephritis with uveitis (TINU) accounts for <5% of AIN, affects females 3x more than males, with median onset age 15 years; hallmark is painful anterior uveitis plus lymphocyte-predominant interstitial nephritis, with sterile pyuria, mild proteinuria, features of Fanconi's syndrome, and elevated ESR (Harrison 22e p.2439).","Sterile pyuria with elevated creatinine and features of Fanconi's syndrome should raise suspicion for TINU; ocular symptoms precede or accompany kidney disease in only one-third of cases (Harrison 22e p.2439).","Papillary necrosis is a key feature of analgesic nephropathy; a sloughed necrotic papilla can cause gross hematuria and ureteric colic from ureteral obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2442).","Renal ultrasound in reflux nephropathy characteristically shows asymmetric small kidneys with irregular outlines, thinned cortices, and regions of compensatory hypertrophy (Harrison 22e p.2441)."],"pathophysiology":"A group of disorders that primarily affect the renal tubules and interstitium with relative sparing of the glomeruli and vessels, divided into acute and chronic tubulointerstitial nephritis (TIN) (Harrison 22e p.2437). Acute TIN causes AKI either via aggressive inflammatory infiltrates producing tissue edema, tubular cell injury, and compromised tubular flow, or via frank tubular obstruction by casts, cellular debris, or crystals; flank pain may arise from renal capsule distention (Harrison 22e p.2437). Acute interstitial nephritis (AIN) is most commonly an allergic, immune-mediated drug reaction (antibiotics such as beta-lactams/sulfonamides/quinolones/vancomycin/rifampin, NSAIDs/COX-2 inhibitors, proton pump inhibitors, diuretics, anticonvulsants, allopurinol, immune checkpoint inhibitors), but may also be immune-mediated within autoimmune syndromes (Sjogren's, SLE, TINU, granulomatous/sarcoid, IgG4-related disease) or infection-associated (Harrison 22e p.2437-2438). Chronic TIN produces interstitial fibrosis with patchy mononuclear cell infiltration, widespread tubular atrophy, luminal dilation, and thickened tubular basement membranes, manifesting as tubular dysfunction (nephrogenic DI, Fanconi's syndrome, RTA) and progressive azotemia (Harrison 22e p.2437). Because the chronic histopathology is nonspecific, biopsy rarely yields a specific diagnosis, so diagnosis relies on history, drug/toxin exposure, associated symptoms, and imaging (Harrison 22e p.2437).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute interstitial nephritis (drug-induced/allergic) (Harrison 22e p.2437)","Tubulointerstitial nephritis with uveitis (TINU) (Harrison 22e p.2439)","Sjogren's syndrome with tubulointerstitial nephritis, distal RTA, and nephrogenic DI (Harrison 22e p.2438)","SLE with tubulointerstitial predominance and type IV RTA (Harrison 22e p.2439)","Granulomatous interstitial nephritis / sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.2439)","IgG4-related systemic disease (Harrison 22e p.2439)","Immune checkpoint inhibitor-associated AIN (Harrison 22e p.2440)","Light chain cast nephropathy / myeloma kidney (Harrison 22e p.2440)","Crystal deposition / obstructive tubulopathies (acute urate, acute phosphate, oxalate, drug crystals) (Harrison 22e p.2440)","Reflux nephropathy (vesicoureteral reflux) (Harrison 22e p.2441)","Sickle cell nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2442)","Analgesic nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2442)","Aristolochic acid nephropathy (Chinese herbal / Balkan endemic) (Harrison 22e p.2442)","Lithium-associated nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2442)","Calcineurin inhibitor nephrotoxicity (Harrison 22e p.2443)","Heavy metal (lead, cadmium) nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2443)","Hypercalcemic and hypokalemic nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2443)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute bacterial pyelonephritis - infection-associated AIN should be distinguished from it; pyelonephritis does not generally cause AKI unless bilateral or causing septic shock (Harrison 22e p.2440)","Reflux nephropathy was previously mislabeled 'chronic pyelonephritis' because it was thought to result from recurrent childhood UTIs (Harrison 22e p.2441)","Infection-associated AIN, currently most often polyomavirus BK reactivation in kidney transplant recipients (Harrison 22e p.2440)","Granulomatous interstitial nephritis - tuberculosis must be ruled out before treatment, as it is a rare cause (Harrison 22e p.2439)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute tubular necrosis and other causes of unexplained AKI (biopsy-proven AIN accounts for no more than ~15% of unexplained AKI, likely an underestimate) (Harrison 22e p.2437)","Primary glomerulonephritis causing secondary tubulointerstitial damage; in advanced disease the initial cause is often difficult to discern on biopsy (Harrison 22e p.2442)","Behcet's disease and sarcoidosis as alternative causes of uveitis when considering TINU (Harrison 22e p.2439)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis - active sediment with leukocytes and cellular casts in acute TIN; pyuria with white blood cell casts and hematuria in allergic AIN; bland sediment in aristolochic acid nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2437-2438, p.2442)","Peripheral blood eosinophil count - supports AIN but present in only a minority of patients (Harrison 22e p.2438)","Urinary eosinophils - NOT recommended; neither sensitive nor specific for AIN (Harrison 22e p.2438)","Serum creatinine and BUN to assess azotemia (Harrison 22e p.2437)","Anti-Ro (SS-A) and anti-La (SS-B) antibodies when Sjogren's syndrome suspected (Harrison 22e p.2438)","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis and immunofixation, plus serum/urine free light chains, when light chain cast nephropathy suspected (Harrison 22e p.2440)","Renal ultrasonography - 'medical renal disease' changes (increased echogenicity, loss of corticomedullary differentiation); asymmetric scarred kidneys in reflux nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2437, p.2441)","Kidney biopsy - not usually required for AIN diagnosis but shows interstitial/tubular leukocyte infiltration including eosinophils; required (with other criteria) for aristolochic acid nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2438, p.2442)"],"redFlags":["Classic allergic AIN tetrad: fever, rash, peripheral eosinophilia, and oliguric AKI after a drug (Harrison 22e p.2438)","Particularly severe and rapid-onset AIN upon reintroduction of rifampin after a drug-free period (Harrison 22e p.2438)","Painful anterior uveitis (often bilateral, with blurred vision and photophobia) with sterile pyuria and AKI - suggests TINU (Harrison 22e p.2439)","Gross hematuria with ureteric colic from a sloughed necrotic papilla in analgesic or sickle cell nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2442)","Triad of saturnine gout, hypertension, and impaired kidney function suggesting lead nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2443)","Anemia disproportionately severe relative to kidney dysfunction in aristolochic acid nephropathy (with very high risk of upper urinary tract urothelial cancer) (Harrison 22e p.2442)"],"prognosis":"In allergic/drug-induced AIN, discontinuation of the offending agent often reverses the kidney injury, though recovery may be incomplete depending on duration of exposure and degree of tubular atrophy and interstitial fibrosis (Harrison 22e p.2438). Granulomatous, idiopathic, TINU, and Sjogren's-related interstitial nephritis generally respond to glucocorticoids, especially when treated early before significant fibrosis, but may relapse and require maintenance immunosuppression (Harrison 22e p.2438-2439). Chronic TIN damage that has already occurred is impossible to reverse, but further deterioration may be slowed by treating glomerular hypertension (Harrison 22e p.2440). Reflux nephropathy affecting both kidneys often progresses inexorably to ESRD over years even without ongoing infection or reflux (Harrison 22e p.2441). Lithium-associated CKD is often irreversible and slowly progresses to ESRD even after lithium is stopped (Harrison 22e p.2443). AKI from acute hypercalcemia may be fully reversible, whereas chronic hypercalcemic kidney dysfunction may not improve even after correcting the calcium disorder (Harrison 22e p.2443).","pitfalls":["The classic AIN tetrad (fever, rash, eosinophilia, oliguric AKI) is the exception rather than the rule; relying on it misses most cases (Harrison 22e p.2438).","NSAID-induced AIN often lacks fever, rash, and eosinophilia but presents with AKI and heavy proteinuria (Harrison 22e p.2438).","Urinary eosinophil testing is neither sensitive nor specific for AIN and is not recommended (Harrison 22e p.2438).","Absence of peripheral eosinophilia does not exclude AIN, as it is present in only a minority of patients (Harrison 22e p.2438).","A negative urine dipstick for protein with elevated total spot urine protein is highly suggestive of Bence-Jones protein (light chains), which dipsticks do not detect - missing myeloma kidney (Harrison 22e p.2440).","Reflux nephropathy may go unnoticed until adulthood and can be mistaken for 'chronic pyelonephritis'; a single affected kidney may be silent except for hypertension (Harrison 22e p.2441).","Because chronic TIN histopathology is nonspecific, biopsy rarely gives a specific diagnosis; over-reliance on biopsy without history/exposure analysis is a pitfall (Harrison 22e p.2437)."],"management":["Prompt withdrawal of the causative drug (implicated beta-lactam, sulfonamide, NSAID, proton pump inhibitor, allopurinol or immune checkpoint inhibitor) as the mainstay of drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis","A course of corticosteroids for severe or non-resolving acute interstitial nephritis to accelerate renal recovery","Treatment of the underlying cause in secondary disease, such as immunosuppression for sarcoidosis or Sjogren's and cessation of exposure in aristolochic-acid or heavy-metal nephropathy","Supportive correction of tubular dysfunction in chronic disease, with electrolyte and bicarbonate replacement for renal tubular acidosis and management of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and Fanconi features","Avoidance of further nephrotoxins and dose adjustment of renally cleared drugs","Monitoring of renal function and electrolytes, with dialysis support for severe acute kidney injury","Nephrology referral and, where the diagnosis is uncertain, renal biopsy to confirm the diagnosis and guide treatment"],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies tubulointerstitial diseases into Acute Tubulointerstitial Disorders - acute interstitial nephritis (therapeutic agents/drugs, infection, autoimmune including TINU, Sjogren's, SLE, granulomatous, IgG4-related) and acute obstructive disorders (light chain cast nephropathy, acute phosphate nephropathy, acute urate nephropathy) - versus Chronic Tubulointerstitial Disorders (reflux nephropathy, sickle cell disease, chronic toxin/drug exposure such as analgesics, lithium, heavy metals, aristolochic acid, calcineurin inhibitors), metabolic disturbances (hypercalcemia/nephrocalcinosis, hyperuricemia, hypokalemia, hyperoxaluria, cystinosis), cystic/hereditary disorders, and miscellaneous causes (aging, chronic glomerulonephritis, obstruction, ischemia, radiation) (Harrison 22e p.2437).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2437","Harrison 22e p.2438","Harrison 22e p.2439","Harrison 22e p.2440","Harrison 22e p.2441","Harrison 22e p.2442","Harrison 22e p.2443","Harrison 22e p.2444"],"crossLinks":["aki","ckd"]},"tubulointerstitial_nephritis_uveitis":{"name":"tubulointerstitial nephritis uveitis","system":"ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TINU syndrome","tubulointerstitial nephritis and uveitis syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A distinct oculorenal syndrome combining acute tubulointerstitial nephritis with uveitis, most often in adolescent girls and young women.","The uveitis is typically bilateral, non-granulomatous and anterior, though intermediate and posterior involvement can occur.","Ocular inflammation may precede, coincide with or, most commonly, follow the renal disease, so uveitis can be the presenting feature of occult nephritis.","Systemic prodrome of fever, weight loss, fatigue, anorexia and myalgia is common and helps flag the diagnosis.","Urinary beta-2 microglobulin is a sensitive marker of tubular injury and is characteristically elevated.","Renal features include a raised creatinine, sterile pyuria, low-grade proteinuria, glycosuria and features of tubular dysfunction.","It is a diagnosis of exclusion; sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, syphilis, Sjögren syndrome and other systemic causes of oculorenal disease must be ruled out.","The nephritis is usually managed with systemic corticosteroids and often recovers, whereas the uveitis tends to be chronic or relapsing and may need prolonged topical and steroid-sparing therapy."],"pathophysiology":"Tubulointerstitial nephritis and uveitis syndrome is believed to be an immune-mediated, likely T-cell driven, hypersensitivity disorder in which a shared autoantigen or cross-reactive immune response targets both the renal tubulointerstitium and the uveal tract. The kidney shows an acute interstitial infiltrate of lymphocytes, plasma cells and eosinophils with tubular injury, sometimes with non-caseating granulomata, producing tubular dysfunction and a rise in creatinine. A parallel immune response in the eye generates non-granulomatous uveitis. Cell-mediated immunity, circulating autoantibodies against a modified C-reactive protein found in both renal tubular cells and uveal tissue, and genetic susceptibility through certain HLA associations have all been implicated. Preceding drug exposure or infection may act as a trigger in susceptible individuals.","additionalDifferentials":["sarcoidosis with renal and ocular involvement","drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis with separate uveitis","systemic lupus erythematosus","Sjögren syndrome","granulomatosis with polyangiitis and other ANCA-associated vasculitis","ocular tuberculosis or syphilis with renal disease","Behçet disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["serum creatinine and estimated glomerular filtration rate","urinary beta-2 microglobulin and urinalysis for sterile pyuria, glycosuria and proteinuria","renal biopsy showing acute tubulointerstitial nephritis when the diagnosis is uncertain","serum angiotensin-converting enzyme and chest imaging to exclude sarcoidosis","autoimmune serology including antinuclear and ANCA antibodies","syphilis serology and interferon-gamma release assay","dilated fundus examination and optical coherence tomography for macular oedema"],"redFlags":["acute kidney injury with a rising creatinine requiring urgent nephrology input","bilateral uveitis with systemic constitutional symptoms in an adolescent","features suggesting systemic vasculitis such as haematuria and pulmonary involvement","sight-threatening posterior involvement or persistent macular oedema"],"prognosis":"The renal component usually responds well to corticosteroids and often recovers fully, though a minority develop chronic renal impairment; the uveitis frequently follows a chronic or relapsing course and requires longer treatment than the nephritis.","pitfalls":["Treating the uveitis in isolation and missing coexisting or subsequent renal disease.","Failing to measure renal function and urinary beta-2 microglobulin in a young patient with bilateral anterior uveitis.","Attributing the syndrome to a drug reaction without excluding sarcoidosis and other systemic causes.","Assuming the uveitis will resolve with the nephritis, when it commonly relapses and outlasts the renal disease.","Overlooking posterior segment involvement and chronic macular oedema as causes of persistent visual loss."],"management":["Systemic corticosteroids as first-line therapy for the tubulointerstitial nephritis, usually producing good renal recovery","Withdrawal of any implicated triggering drug and supportive correction of tubular dysfunction with electrolyte and bicarbonate replacement","Topical corticosteroids and mydriatics for the anterior uveitis, escalating to periocular or systemic steroids for intermediate or posterior involvement","Steroid-sparing immunosuppression such as methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate for chronic, relapsing or steroid-dependent ocular or renal disease","Exclusion of mimics such as sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, syphilis and other autoimmune disease before committing to prolonged immunosuppression","Joint ophthalmology and nephrology follow-up, monitoring renal function and for relapsing ocular inflammation","Escalation to a specialist uveitis and renal service for refractory disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology Basic and Clinical Science Course, Uveitis and Ocular Inflammation","Standardisation of Uveitis Nomenclature classification criteria"],"crossLinks":[]},"tufted_angioma":{"name":"Tufted angioma","system":"Skin / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Angioblastoma of Nakagawa","Acquired tufted angioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Regarded as part of a continuum with kaposiform haemangioendothelioma, sharing histological and clinical features.","Characterised by discrete capillary tufts scattered through the dermis in a cannonball pattern on histology.","Usually presents in infancy or early childhood as a slowly enlarging dull-red to violaceous plaque, sometimes with overlying hypertrichosis and hyperhidrosis.","Can trigger the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon with profound thrombocytopenia and consumptive coagulopathy, though less often than kaposiform haemangioendothelioma.","GLUT1-negative; positivity for the lymphatic marker podoplanin (D2-40) supports the diagnosis.","Tenderness and induration may accompany platelet trapping.","Spontaneous regression can occur, particularly in congenital cases, but many lesions persist.","mTOR inhibition with sirolimus is increasingly used for complicated or coagulopathic lesions."],"pathophysiology":"Tufted angioma is a benign but occasionally locally aggressive vascular tumour characterised by discrete lobules or tufts of tightly packed capillaries dispersed through the dermis in a cannonball pattern, often bulging into dilated lymphatic-like channels. It expresses lymphatic endothelial markers such as podoplanin (D2-40), is GLUT1-negative, and shares pathological and behavioural overlap with kaposiform haemangioendothelioma, including the capacity to sequester and consume platelets, producing the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon. Somatic GNA14 mutations have been implicated in a subset of lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["Kaposiform haemangioendothelioma","Infantile haemangioma","Kaposi sarcoma","Capillary or venous malformation","Fibrous hamartoma of infancy","Plaque-type infantile myofibromatosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing cannonball tufts with podoplanin positivity","Full blood count and coagulation studies (fibrinogen, D-dimer) to detect the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon","MRI to define depth and soft-tissue extent","Doppler ultrasound","Serial platelet monitoring in at-risk lesions"],"redFlags":["Rapid enlargement with a tense, purpuric and painful lesion","Falling platelet count with hypofibrinogenaemia (Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon)","Spontaneous bleeding or extensive bruising","Functional impairment from a large or periarticular lesion"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally favourable, with some lesions regressing spontaneously, but persistent pain, disfigurement and the risk of the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon may complicate the course and require systemic therapy.","pitfalls":["Failing to check the platelet count and coagulation in a growing violaceous plaque.","Confusing the lesion with a simple bruise or an infantile haemangioma.","Not recognising the continuum with kaposiform haemangioendothelioma.","Giving platelet transfusion in the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon except during active bleeding or surgery, since it fuels further trapping."],"management":["Observation for small, asymptomatic lesions, a proportion of which regress spontaneously","Systemic sirolimus (mTOR inhibitor) as targeted therapy for extensive, symptomatic or Kasabach-Merritt-complicated lesions","Corticosteroids alongside sirolimus for the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon, with platelet transfusion reserved for active bleeding rather than routine correction","Surgical excision for localised, resectable and symptomatic lesions","Analgesia for pain, tenderness and induration","Serial monitoring of platelet count and coagulation studies in at-risk lesions to detect the Kasabach-Merritt phenomenon early","Referral to a specialist vascular anomalies multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of paediatric dermatology","ISSVA classification of vascular anomalies","Consensus reviews on the tufted angioma and kaposiform haemangioendothelioma spectrum"],"crossLinks":[]},"tufting_enteropathy":{"name":"Tufting enteropathy","system":"Gastroenterology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["EPCAM","congenital tufting enteropathy","intestinal epithelial dysplasia","CTE"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive congenital enteropathy caused chiefly by biallelic EPCAM variants, with a syndromic form linked to SPINT2","Presents in early infancy with severe watery diarrhoea and failure to thrive requiring parenteral nutrition","Small-bowel biopsy shows villus atrophy, crypt abnormalities and characteristic focal epithelial tufts of crowded, disorganised enterocytes","EpCAM immunostaining is typically reduced or absent at the enterocyte membrane, supporting the diagnosis","The diarrhoea reflects disrupted enterocyte adhesion and impaired absorptive surface rather than a single transporter defect","The SPINT2-related variant may be accompanied by choanal atresia, keratitis and other congenital anomalies","Long-term nutritional dependence is common, and severe refractory cases may require intestinal transplantation"],"pathophysiology":"Tufting enteropathy results from defective enterocyte adhesion and epithelial organisation. Loss-of-function variants in EPCAM disrupt the epithelial cell adhesion molecule, impairing cell-cell contacts and the normal architecture of the intestinal epithelium. Enterocytes crowd and pile into focal tufts at the villus surface, with associated villus atrophy and crypt changes, so the absorptive surface is compromised. The syndromic SPINT2-associated form involves a serine protease inhibitor and additional developmental anomalies. The net effect is failure of fluid and nutrient absorption producing chronic diarrhoea from early infancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Microvillus inclusion disease","Autoimmune enteropathy and IPEX syndrome","Congenital chloride or sodium diarrhoea","Enteroendocrine cell dysgenesis (NEUROG3)","Trichohepatoenteric syndrome","Coeliac disease and other later-onset enteropathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Small-bowel mucosal biopsy with assessment for epithelial tufts and villus atrophy","EpCAM immunohistochemistry of biopsy tissue","Stool electrolytes and faecal osmotic gap to characterise the diarrhoea","Molecular testing for EPCAM and SPINT2 variants","Assessment for extraintestinal anomalies (e.g. choanal, ophthalmic) in suspected syndromic disease"],"redFlags":["Severe dehydration and electrolyte disturbance in a young infant with intractable diarrhoea","Failure to thrive despite optimised feeding, indicating a congenital enteropathy","Associated congenital anomalies suggesting the syndromic SPINT2-related form","Complications of long-term parenteral nutrition such as line sepsis or cholestasis"],"prognosis":"Most patients require prolonged or indefinite parenteral nutrition; some achieve partial enteral tolerance while severe refractory disease may progress to intestinal transplantation. Outcome is influenced by nutritional complications and any associated anomalies.","pitfalls":["Confusing the histology with coeliac disease or other villus-atrophy enteropathies without recognising the diagnostic tufts","Relying on light microscopy alone rather than adding EpCAM staining and genetics","Attributing chronic diarrhoea to feed intolerance and delaying definitive evaluation","Missing associated congenital anomalies that point to a syndromic cause"],"management":["Long-term parenteral nutrition as the mainstay to sustain fluid, electrolyte and nutritional needs from early infancy","Meticulous central venous catheter care with prevention and prompt treatment of catheter-related bloodstream infection and line complications","Cautious introduction and advancement of enteral feeds to promote any achievable enteral tolerance","Monitoring and management of intestinal-failure-associated liver disease, growth, and micronutrient and trace-element status","Consideration of intestinal or combined liver-intestinal transplantation for refractory disease or failure of parenteral nutrition","Assessment and management of associated anomalies (choanal atresia, keratitis) in SPINT2-related syndromic disease","Multidisciplinary intestinal-failure and gastroenterology care, with genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"tularemia":{"name":"Tularemia","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Francisella tularensis infection"],"chapter":175,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1335-1340","clinicalPearls":["Zoonosis caused by Francisella tularensis, a small facultatively intracellular aerobic gram-negative coccobacillus; humans are infected via arthropod bites (ticks, tabanid/deer flies, mosquitoes), inhalation of infectious aerosols, handling infected animals, or ingestion of contaminated water/food (Harrison 22e p.1335-1336).","Take a deliberate exposure history: wild animal contact (rodents, rabbits, hares), inhalation of dust contaminated with their urine or feces, recent tick/fly/mosquito bites, brush-cutting or grass-trimming, handling wood or hay, and walking or hunting in wildlife habitats; cluster cases sharing the same exposure are a clue (Harrison 22e p.1337).","The most common initial manifestation is an influenza-like illness with high fever (acute onset, myalgia, headache, constitutional symptoms); fever may be persistent or intermittent. Incubation is usually 3-5 days but may range from 1 to 21 days (Harrison 22e p.1337).","Inflamed, swollen, tender regional lymph nodes are typical; the initial infection route determines which lymphoid tissue is engaged (skin or pharyngeal entry gives regional nodes; inhalation gives mediastinal/hilar nodes) (Harrison 22e p.1337).","Ulceroglandular form (most common, ~47% of US cases): skin inoculation by a blood-feeding arthropod or direct contact with an infected animal/contaminated material produces a primary ulcer plus tender regional lymphadenopathy; the primary ulcer is often a small skin break with limited inflammation that requires careful examination to detect (Harrison 22e p.1337-1338).","Glandular form (~18% of US cases): regional lymphadenopathy without a detectable primary skin ulcer (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Oculoglandular form (~2% of US cases): conjunctivitis with pronounced local inflammation plus regional (e.g., preauricular) lymphadenopathy, acquired by touching the eye with contaminated fingers or by contaminated aerosol (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Oropharyngeal form (~2% of US cases): acute pharyngitis with pronounced local inflammation following ingestion of contaminated water or food, with cervical/tonsillar lymphadenopathy (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Respiratory/pneumonic form (~17% of US cases): acquired by inhaling contaminated aerosol; more often bacteremic and more likely to progress to severe disease requiring inpatient care, particularly in patients aged 65 and over and those with comorbidities. Onset of a dry cough may occur more than 7 days after the influenza-like illness; engaged lymphoid tissue is in the mediastinum or lung hila (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Typhoidal form (~14% of US cases): an older term for a typhoid-like syndrome with prolonged high fever, fatigue, headache, and nausea; Harrison recommends avoiding the term because most historic typhoidal cases were in fact respiratory/pneumonic tularemia acquired by inhalation (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Routine blood biochemistry is generally unhelpful; findings are nonspecific and may or may not show elevated liver enzymes or hematuria. In a Swedish type B outpatient series, white-cell counts stayed within normal limits in 70% of patients and CRP was only moderately elevated (Harrison 22e p.1338).","When tularemia is clinically suspected, communicate the suspicion to the microbiology laboratory in advance so that cultures are handled safely, because F. tularensis causes laboratory-acquired infection and culture requires BSL-3 containment; it is also a US HHS Tier 1 Select Agent (Harrison 22e p.1335, 1339).","For ulcer sampling, swab the edge of the wound (remove any scab first) and rub firmly so the specimen is slightly blood-tinged to maximize yield (Harrison 22e p.1339)."],"pathophysiology":"Natural transmission is usually through the skin or by inhalation of aerosols, but ingestion of contaminated food/water can also infect. An extremely low dose of about 10-50 organisms inoculated through skin microtrauma or inhaled suffices to cause disease, whereas roughly 10^6-10^8 organisms are required by the oral route. Tissue-resident macrophages (alveolar macrophages in lung, Langerhans cells in skin) transport the organism via blood and lymphatics to lymph nodes, spleen, and bone marrow; in skin, neutrophils phagocytose F. tularensis early. After uptake, the bacterium escapes the phagosome into the cytosol, replicates, exits the cell, and disseminates. F. tularensis is a 'stealth pathogen' that manipulates host-cell signaling, delaying the immune response and permitting rapid systemic spread. Characteristic tissue reactions are granuloma formation and necrotic foci surrounded by macrophages, lymphocytes, and giant cells in lymph nodes, lung, spleen, kidney, and liver; pulmonary autopsy findings show necrotizing pneumonia with fibrin, cellular debris, and neutrophils. Effective protective immunity requires both B and T cells, and antigen-specific recall T-cell responses can persist 25-30 years after infection or vaccination (Harrison 22e p.1337, 1339).","additionalDifferentials":["Lung cancer (pulmonary nodules or dense consolidation can mimic malignancy, prompting CT/PET and biopsy before tularemia is suspected) (Harrison 22e p.1337, 1339)","Head and neck cancer (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Tuberculosis (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Unspecified viral infection / 'a severe virus' (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Common skin and soft tissue infection (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Community-acquired pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Undifferentiated fever or sepsis (Harrison 22e p.1337)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Community-acquired pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Common skin and soft tissue infection (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Unspecified viral infection (Harrison 22e p.1337)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lung cancer (Harrison 22e p.1337, 1339)","Head and neck cancer (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Suspected tumor with lymphoid tissue engagement (Harrison 22e p.1337)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Laboratory confirmation by recovery of an F. tularensis isolate, detection of F. tularensis-specific antigens or nucleic acids, or detection of specific antibodies by serology (Harrison 22e p.1338-1339)","Direct detection by PCR/nucleic acid testing of clinical specimens is rapid and reliable; for ulcer specimens it is comparable to culture in sensitivity and specificity (Harrison 22e p.1339)","Appropriate specimens for direct detection or culture: ulcer specimens (sampled from the wound edge), lower respiratory tract specimens (sputum, tracheal washing, bronchoalveolar lavage), lymph node biopsies or aspirates, and blood cultures (Harrison 22e p.1339)","Blood culture: F. tularensis is slow-growing and a 10-day incubation is recommended for optimal sensitivity; MALDI-TOF identification from blood cultures is an increasingly common, sometimes unexpected, finding (Harrison 22e p.1339)","Serology (ELISA or microagglutination) is the mainstay in many laboratories and is especially useful when tularemia is suspected at a later stage; antibodies are generally detectable 10-20 days post-infection (Harrison 22e p.1339)","Chest imaging in respiratory tularemia: single or multifocal consolidation, pleural effusion, and hilar/mediastinal lymphadenopathy; CT may show rounded subpleural consolidations and necrotic lymph nodes; pathology resolution on imaging can be slow and take months (Harrison 22e p.1339)"],"redFlags":["Respiratory/pneumonic form, which is more often bacteremic and may progress to severe disease requiring inpatient care, particularly in patients aged 65 and over and those with comorbidities (Harrison 22e p.1338)","Multiorgan involvement or hemodynamic instability, indicating need for inpatient care (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Host factors increasing risk of severe infection: geriatric frailty, immunosuppression, severe lung and/or heart impairment, and other significant comorbidities (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Lymph node abscess formation, which occurs in about one-third of patients and is associated with antibiotic treatment delay of more than 2-3 weeks after disease onset (Harrison 22e p.1340)","Severe complications reported: adult respiratory distress syndrome, myocarditis, pericarditis, endocarditis, meningitis, osteomyelitis, hepatitis, and renal failure (Harrison 22e p.1340)"],"prognosis":"Before antibiotics, US case-fatality rates were typically 5-15% and could reach as high as 60% in respiratory/pneumonic tularemia. Recent US surveillance (2006-2021) showed 27 of 903 patients (3.0%) with available outcome data died; treatment with at least one high-efficacy antimicrobial class was independently associated with greatly increased odds of survival. Type A is generally more lethal than type B, though there is heterogeneity among type A genetic varieties. Overall type B fatality is estimated at less than 1% in Sweden, where severe respiratory type B tularemia carried about 1.5% fatality. Recovery (and resolution of imaging abnormalities) may be slow, and some patients report postinfectious persisting fatigue and disability of uncertain cause (Harrison 22e p.1339-1340).","pitfalls":["Clinical recognition is challenging, especially if the patient does not spontaneously report fever; tularemia may not be suspected until after extensive and costly investigations, and even in high-endemic areas patients are often initially mistreated for other conditions. A high index of suspicion is critical (Harrison 22e p.1337).","If care is sought several weeks after symptom onset, the acute fever onset may not be reported spontaneously and must be actively elicited (Harrison 22e p.1337).","The primary ulcer can be a small, minimally inflamed skin break that is easily missed or may be undetectable, in which case the disease is classified as glandular rather than ulceroglandular (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Failure to alert the microbiology laboratory to suspected tularemia risks laboratory-acquired infection, as F. tularensis is highly virulent and requires BSL-3 culture conditions (Harrison 22e p.1335, 1339).","Acute-phase serology can be misleading because F. tularensis antibodies persist (single titers can remain above the diagnostic cutoff for at least 1 year), so reliable acute diagnosis should rely on a significant rise between paired sera taken 2-4 weeks apart (Harrison 22e p.1339).","Culture specimens taken from an established lymph node abscess are typically F. tularensis negative (Harrison 22e p.1340).","Tularemia patients do not require isolation because there is no human-to-human transmission (Harrison 22e p.1340)."],"management":["Aminoglycoside antimicrobials (streptomycin or gentamicin) as first-line treatment, particularly for severe or pneumonic disease","Fluoroquinolones or tetracyclines (ciprofloxacin or doxycycline) as oral alternatives for milder disease, accepting a higher relapse rate with bacteriostatic agents","Supportive care with antipyretics, analgesia and rehydration","Drainage of suppurative, fluctuant lymph nodes that fail to resolve with antimicrobial therapy","Alert the laboratory to the suspected diagnosis given aerosol infectivity, with public-health notification and consideration of deliberate release when clusters occur","Post-exposure antimicrobial prophylaxis with a fluoroquinolone or tetracycline after a recognised high-risk exposure","Prevention advice including protective clothing, insect-bite avoidance and care when handling wild animals in endemic areas"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity score. Clinically, management level is decided by physical examination of the anatomic site and extent of inflammation (pneumonitis, lymph node inflammation, skin ulcers/pustules, pharyngitis), the rate of progression, and host risk factors: localized symptoms are generally suitable for outpatient care, whereas multiorgan involvement or hemodynamic instability suggests inpatient care. The recognized clinical forms (ulceroglandular, glandular, oropharyngeal, oculoglandular, respiratory/pneumonic, typhoidal) are classified by F. tularensis infection route rather than by severity (Harrison 22e p.1337-1338).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1335-1340"],"crossLinks":[]},"tumefactive_demyelination":{"name":"Tumefactive demyelination","system":"Nervous system / Neuroimmunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tumefactive multiple sclerosis","tumefactive demyelinating lesion","demyelinating pseudotumour","tumour-like demyelination"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A large (typically over 2 cm) demyelinating lesion that mimics a brain tumour clinically and radiologically","Presents with focal deficits, headache, cognitive change, or seizures depending on location and mass effect","Characteristic MRI features include an incomplete (open) ring of enhancement with the open edge facing the cortex or grey matter","Mass effect and surrounding oedema are usually less than expected for the lesion size, a helpful clue against neoplasm","May occur in isolation or as a presentation of multiple sclerosis, and can be triggered in the context of certain disease-modifying drugs","Corticosteroids are first-line, with plasma exchange for steroid-refractory disease","Biopsy is sometimes needed but carries the risk of misinterpretation, as active demyelination can resemble neoplasm histologically"],"pathophysiology":"Tumefactive demyelination is a focal region of intense inflammatory demyelination large enough to produce a space-occupying appearance. Macrophage-rich inflammation strips myelin while relatively sparing axons, and the advancing inflammatory edge produces the characteristic open-ring pattern of contrast enhancement. Because the pathology is inflammatory rather than neoplastic, restricted diffusion at the rim, relative lack of mass effect for size, and the open-ring sign help distinguish it from tumour, although overlap with the multiple sclerosis spectrum and with Balo and Marburg variants exists.","additionalDifferentials":["High-grade glioma","Primary central nervous system lymphoma","Cerebral abscess","Metastasis","Balo concentric sclerosis","Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy","Neurosarcoidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain with contrast, diffusion, perfusion, and spectroscopy to seek the open-ring sign and features favouring demyelination","MRI of the spinal cord and orbits to look for additional demyelinating lesions","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis including oligoclonal bands","Aquaporin-4 and MOG antibody testing","Brain biopsy in genuinely indeterminate cases","Serial imaging to demonstrate the expected evolution of a demyelinating lesion"],"redFlags":["Progressive mass effect with raised intracranial pressure","Rapid neurological deterioration","Imaging that cannot confidently exclude malignancy or infection","Steroid-refractory progression requiring escalation"],"prognosis":"Many patients recover well with corticosteroids, and lesions typically shrink over time; long-term outcome depends on whether the disorder declares itself as multiple sclerosis or remains a solitary event.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to resection or radiotherapy on the assumption of a tumour","Misreading a demyelinating biopsy as glioma","Overlooking associated demyelinating lesions elsewhere","Failing to consider a drug-associated trigger in treated multiple sclerosis"],"management":["High-dose corticosteroids as first-line treatment of the acute demyelinating lesion","Plasma exchange for steroid-refractory disease","Careful exclusion of tumour and infection to avoid unnecessary resection, with brain biopsy reserved for genuinely indeterminate cases","Review of any disease-modifying drug that may have precipitated the lesion","Initiation of long-term disease-modifying therapy if the disorder declares itself as multiple sclerosis","Supportive management of mass effect and oedema, with symptomatic treatment of seizures or focal deficits and neurorehabilitation","Serial imaging follow-up to confirm the expected evolution, with neurology and neuroimmunology referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Continuum: Multiple Sclerosis and Related Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"tumefactive_multiple_sclerosis":{"name":"Tumefactive multiple sclerosis","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tumefactive demyelination"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A large (typically over two centimetres) demyelinating brain lesion that mimics a tumour or abscess","It may be the first presentation of multiple sclerosis or occur in established disease","Characteristic MRI features include an incomplete (open) ring of enhancement and relatively little mass effect or oedema for the lesion size","The main concern is distinguishing it from a high-grade glioma, lymphoma or abscess to avoid unnecessary resection","Advanced imaging and, when needed, biopsy help confirm demyelination","It usually responds to corticosteroids, and management otherwise follows that of multiple sclerosis"],"pathophysiology":"Tumefactive multiple sclerosis is a form of inflammatory demyelination in which a large focus of myelin breakdown, perivascular inflammation and reactive astrocytosis forms a mass-like lesion. The active demyelinating edge, where the blood-brain barrier is disrupted, produces the characteristic open-ring enhancement facing the cortex, while the relatively preserved deep margin and modest mass effect reflect the non-neoplastic, inflammatory nature. Recognising these features distinguishes it from neoplasia and infection.","additionalDifferentials":["High-grade glioma","Primary CNS lymphoma","Cerebral abscess","Cerebral metastasis","Other inflammatory demyelinating disease (ADEM, neuromyelitis optica)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI with contrast, including advanced sequences (perfusion, spectroscopy)","Cerebrospinal fluid for oligoclonal bands","Assessment for dissemination in space and time","Brain biopsy where malignancy or infection cannot be excluded","Aquaporin-4 and MOG antibodies to exclude mimics"],"redFlags":["Imaging or clinical features favouring malignancy or abscess","Rapid neurological deterioration or raised intracranial pressure","Atypical features suggesting neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder","Poor response to corticosteroids"],"prognosis":"Many lesions respond well to corticosteroids with good recovery, and the longer-term course follows that of the underlying demyelinating disease; misdiagnosis as tumour risks harmful surgery.","pitfalls":["Resecting the lesion as a presumed tumour","Not recognising the open-ring enhancement pattern","Overlooking neuromyelitis optica or MOG-associated disease","Failing to obtain cerebrospinal fluid and antibody studies"],"management":["High-dose corticosteroids as first-line treatment of the acute tumefactive lesion","Plasma exchange for steroid-refractory episodes","Advanced imaging (perfusion, spectroscopy) and, when necessary, biopsy to confirm demyelination and avoid unnecessary neurosurgical resection","Establishment of disease-modifying therapy for the underlying multiple sclerosis once confirmed","Symptomatic management of seizures, spasticity and focal deficits with neurorehabilitation","Review of any disease-modifying drug implicated in triggering the lesion and monitoring of treatment response","Neurology and neuroimmunology referral with structured multiple sclerosis follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurology reference texts (standard reference)","Multiple sclerosis diagnostic guidelines (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"tumor_induced_osteomalacia":{"name":"Tumour-induced (oncogenic) osteomalacia (FGF23)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oncogenic osteomalacia","TIO","paraneoplastic osteomalacia","phosphaturic mesenchymal tumour syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acquired paraneoplastic syndrome in which a usually small, benign mesenchymal tumour secretes fibroblast growth factor 23 (FGF23), driving renal phosphate wasting","Typically presents in adults with insidious bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, fatigue, and recurrent fragility fractures often misattributed to other rheumatological disease","Biochemistry shows hypophosphataemia with inappropriate phosphaturia and a low or inappropriately normal 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D despite the low phosphate","Serum calcium and parathyroid hormone are usually normal, and alkaline phosphatase is commonly raised reflecting osteomalacia","The culprit phosphaturic mesenchymal tumour is often tiny and occult, hiding in the extremities, craniofacial region, or acral soft tissue and bone","Functional imaging exploiting somatostatin-receptor expression, alongside FGF23-guided venous sampling, aids localisation of an elusive tumour","Complete surgical resection is curative and normalises phosphate; when the tumour cannot be found or removed, phosphate salts with active vitamin D or FGF23-directed antibody therapy are used"],"pathophysiology":"Phosphaturic mesenchymal tumours ectopically overproduce FGF23 (and related phosphatonins). Circulating FGF23 acts on the proximal renal tubule to downregulate sodium-phosphate cotransporters, causing renal phosphate wasting and hypophosphataemia. FGF23 also suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase and stimulates 24-hydroxylase, so 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is inappropriately low or normal for the degree of hypophosphataemia, limiting compensatory intestinal phosphate absorption. Chronic hypophosphataemia impairs mineralisation of osteoid, producing osteomalacia with bone pain, weakness, and fractures. Because the mechanism mirrors the inherited FGF23-driven rickets syndromes, the biochemical fingerprint is shared, but here it is acquired and reversible with tumour removal.","additionalDifferentials":["X-linked hypophosphataemic rickets and other inherited FGF23-mediated phosphate-wasting disorders","Nutritional or malabsorptive vitamin D deficiency osteomalacia","Fanconi syndrome with generalised proximal tubulopathy","Primary hyperparathyroidism and other metabolic bone disease","Polymyalgia rheumatica or inflammatory myopathy given the proximal weakness","Metastatic bone disease or multiple myeloma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting serum phosphate with paired urine studies (tubular reabsorption of phosphate / TmP-GFR) to confirm renal phosphate wasting","Serum calcium, parathyroid hormone, alkaline phosphatase, and 25- and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D metabolites","Measurement of intact FGF23, which is elevated or inappropriately normal","Functional somatostatin-receptor imaging (e.g. gallium-labelled DOTA-peptide PET) to localise the occult tumour","Cross-sectional anatomical imaging of suspicious sites and selective venous sampling for FGF23 when localisation is difficult","Histological confirmation of a phosphaturic mesenchymal tumour after resection"],"redFlags":["Recurrent low-trauma fractures with unexplained persistent hypophosphataemia","Progressive proximal weakness leading to falls and immobility","Failure of symptoms to resolve after apparent resection, suggesting incomplete removal or malignant behaviour","Nephrocalcinosis or hypercalciuria complicating high-dose phosphate and active vitamin D therapy"],"prognosis":"Complete resection of the responsible tumour is curative, with rapid normalisation of phosphate and gradual healing of osteomalacia; when the tumour is unresectable or occult, long-term medical therapy controls symptoms but requires monitoring for secondary hyperparathyroidism and nephrocalcinosis.","pitfalls":["Overlooking hypophosphataemia because routine panels omit phosphate or the low value is dismissed as artefact","Attributing symptoms to primary rheumatological or psychiatric illness, delaying diagnosis for years","Abandoning the search for an occult tumour before functional and venous sampling techniques are exhausted","Inducing tertiary hyperparathyroidism and renal calcinosis with unmonitored phosphate and calcitriol supplementation"],"management":["Complete surgical resection of the culprit phosphaturic mesenchymal tumour, which is curative and rapidly normalises phosphate","Oral phosphate supplementation combined with active vitamin D (calcitriol or alfacalcidol) when the tumour is occult or unresectable","The anti-FGF23 monoclonal antibody burosumab as targeted medical therapy where conventional replacement is inadequate","Functional somatostatin-receptor imaging with anatomical imaging and selective venous FGF23 sampling to localise the elusive tumour","Ablative approaches such as radiofrequency ablation for tumours not amenable to surgical resection","Surveillance for the complications of medical therapy, namely secondary and tertiary hyperparathyroidism and nephrocalcinosis","Multidisciplinary metabolic bone, surgical and radiology input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism"],"crossLinks":[]},"tumors_of_the_liver_and":{"name":"Tumors of the Liver and Biliary Tree","system":"Hepatobiliary / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Hepatocellular carcinoma","HCC","Cholangiocarcinoma","Gallbladder cancer","Liver cancer","Hepatic tumors","Biliary tract cancer"],"chapter":87,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.659-670","clinicalPearls":["Liver cancer is the sixth most common cancer worldwide and the third leading cause of cancer-related death; hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) makes up about 85% of primary liver cancers, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma about 10%, with rarer entities including fibrolamellar HCC, mixed HCC-iCCA, epithelioid hemangioendothelioma, and pediatric hepatoblastoma (Harrison 22e p.659).","Cirrhosis of any cause is the dominant risk factor for HCC; about one-third of cirrhotic patients develop HCC over their lifetime, with an annual risk of roughly 3-8% in HBV- or HCV-related cirrhosis and a lower 1-2% per year in cirrhosis from alcohol, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH), alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency, autoimmune hepatitis, Wilson's disease, and cholestatic disorders (Harrison 22e p.659-660).","HBV accounts for roughly 50% of HCC cases globally (about 60% in Asia and Africa, about 20% in the West) and can cause tumors via insertional mutagenesis; importantly, 25-30% of MASH-associated HCCs arise in the absence of cirrhosis (Harrison 22e p.660).","HCC has a strong male preponderance (male-to-female ratio about 2.5:1) and incidence peaks at 65-70 years, though it presents earlier (40-50 years) in populations with vertical HBV transmission (Harrison 22e p.659).","The radiologic hallmark of HCC on four-phase CT or dynamic contrast-enhanced MRI is arterial-phase contrast uptake with washout in the portal venous or delayed phase, a pattern with about 95-100% specificity that can make biopsy unnecessary in cirrhotic patients (Harrison 22e p.661).","The Barcelona Clinic Liver Cancer (BCLC) system is the most accepted staging scheme; the TNM staging system is NOT used in HCC because it does not capture liver function and performance status, the key prognostic variables (Harrison 22e p.662-664).","Cholangiocarcinoma is the second most common primary liver cancer and is classified by location into intrahepatic (iCCA, ~20-30%), perihilar (pCCA, ~50-60%), and distal (dCCA, ~20-30%); perihilar and distal forms together are termed extrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma (Harrison 22e p.667).","FGFR2 fusions and IDH1/2 mutations occur mostly in iCCA, whereas ERBB2/3 amplifications and SMAD4 aberrations characterize extrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma; up to about 40% of iCCAs carry a targetable mutation (Harrison 22e p.667).","Acute painless jaundice occurs in about 90% of perihilar cholangiocarcinoma patients, while roughly 10% present with cholangitis; distal cholangiocarcinoma can be hard to distinguish from early pancreatic cancer (Harrison 22e p.668-669).","Gallbladder cancer is the most common biliary tract malignancy worldwide, has a female-to-male ratio of about 3:1, and cholelithiasis is the major risk factor though fewer than 1% of patients with gallstones develop it; gallbladder polyps 10 mm or larger carry transformation risk (Harrison 22e p.669-670).","Fibrolamellar HCC typically affects children and young adults (10-30 years) without underlying liver disease, carries the PRKACA-DNAJB1 fusion oncogene in 80-100% of cases, and has a better prognosis than conventional HCC (Harrison 22e p.670).","Hepatic (hepatocellular) adenomas occur predominantly in women aged 35-40 (female-to-male ratio ~10:1), are linked to oral contraceptive use in women and anabolic androgenic steroid use in male bodybuilders, and carry risk of hemorrhage and malignant transformation, particularly when larger than 5 cm (Harrison 22e p.670)."],"pathophysiology":"HCC develops through a multistep process beginning with precancerous low-grade dysplastic nodules that progress to high-grade dysplastic nodules and then early HCC; adult hepatocytes are the principal cell of origin, either transforming directly or de-differentiating into precursor cells, with progenitor cells contributing in a subset. It is a prototypical inflammation-associated cancer in which oxidative stress and the immune microenvironment of chronically damaged liver drive mutation. Tumors accumulate roughly 40-60 somatic alterations, of which 4-8 are driver genes; the leading drivers are TERT promoter mutations (~56%), TP53 (~27%), and CTNNB1 (~26%), all currently non-actionable. Aflatoxin B1 produces a characteristic TP53 R249S hotspot mutation. Only about 20-25% of HCCs carry an actionable mutation. Two molecular subclasses exist: a proliferative class (poor outcome, HBV-related, AFP overexpression, Ras/mTOR/IGF activation) and a nonproliferative class (often CTNNB1-mutated, better outcome). Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma arises from adult cholangiocytes, transdifferentiated hepatocytes, or progenitor-cholangiocyte precursors, whereas extrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma arises from biliary epithelium and peribiliary glands. Hepatic adenomas are clonal proliferations from single-gene driver mutations (e.g., CTNNB1, HNF1A inactivation, inflammatory or Hedgehog pathway activation), and focal nodular hyperplasia is a polyclonal hepatocellular proliferation secondary to an arterial malformation (Harrison 22e p.660-670).","additionalDifferentials":["Low-grade and high-grade dysplastic nodules versus early HCC in a cirrhotic liver","Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma versus metastatic adenocarcinoma (colorectal, breast, lung) versus mixed HCC-iCCA","Distal cholangiocarcinoma versus early pancreatic cancer","Benign liver lesions: hemangioma, focal nodular hyperplasia, hepatocellular adenoma","Mixed HCC-iCCA and cholangiolocellular carcinoma","IgG4-associated cholangiopathy mimicking cholangiocarcinoma stricture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hepatic hemangioma","Focal nodular hyperplasia","Hepatocellular adenoma","Dysplastic regenerative nodules in cirrhosis","Metastatic adenocarcinoma to the liver"],"additionalInvestigations":["Six-monthly abdominal ultrasound with serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) for HCC surveillance in high-risk cirrhotic patients (sensitivity ~65%, specificity >90%)","Four-phase contrast-enhanced CT or dynamic/gadoxetic-enhanced MRI for noninvasive HCC diagnosis using the arterial uptake/washout hallmark","LI-RADS categorization of liver nodules (LI-RADS-5 ~95% probability HCC; LI-RADS-4 ~60-70%; LI-RADS-1 0%)","Liver biopsy with hepatopathologist review when noncirrhotic, atypical imaging, or LI-RADS-4; supportive stains GPC3, glutamine synthetase, HSP70, clathrin heavy chain (2 of 4 positive is highly specific for HCC); a negative biopsy does NOT exclude HCC","CA 19-9 (prognostic at cutoffs ~100-129 U/mL for cholangiocarcinoma but poor for early diagnosis, sensitivity/specificity ~60%)","MRI/MRCP and chest-abdomen CT and PET for cholangiocarcinoma staging; PET helps detect occult lymph node metastasis","Endoscopic retrograde cholangiography with brushing cytology plus FISH for polysomy in perihilar/distal cholangiocarcinoma (FISH raises cytology sensitivity from ~20% to ~40%); serum IgG4 to exclude IgG4 cholangiopathy","Child-Pugh score (bilirubin, albumin, prothrombin time, ascites, encephalopathy) and ECOG performance status for prognostic assessment"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic HCC (malaise, weight loss, anorexia, abdominal discomfort, or signs of liver dysfunction) indicates advanced disease with median survival under 1 year","Macrovascular (portal, hepatic, or branch vein) invasion, lymph node involvement, or extrahepatic spread denotes advanced BCLC C disease","Child-Pugh class C / severe liver dysfunction (BCLC D)","Rising or markedly elevated AFP (>=20 ng/mL prompting imaging; >=400 ng/dL highly suspicious but not diagnostic)","Painless jaundice or cholangitis suggesting biliary tract malignancy","Hepatic adenoma larger than 5 cm (hemorrhage and malignant transformation risk); active adenoma bleeding"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on stage, tumor burden, liver function, and performance status. About 40% of HCC patients are diagnosed at BCLC stage 0 or A and are eligible for potentially curative resection, transplantation, or ablation, with median overall survival beyond 5 years (up to ~10 years after transplantation). Optimal surgical resection candidates have 5-year survival of 60-70% (35-55% in suboptimal candidates); RFA achieves ~60% 5-year survival; liver transplantation within Milan criteria yields 70-80% 5-year survival with ~10-15% recurrence at 5 years. Intermediate-stage (BCLC B) untreated natural history is about 16 months, improved to ~25-30 months with TACE. Symptomatic/advanced disease has median survival under 1 year; end-stage (BCLC D) survival is about 3-6 months. Cholangiocarcinoma overall carries a 5-year survival of about 10%; resectable iCCA single tumors have median survival ~40-50 months. Gallbladder cancer 5-year survival ranges 60-90% in resected localized disease but only ~10-12 months median in advanced disease. Fibrolamellar HCC has better prognosis (median ~70 months after resection). Hepatoblastoma overall 5-year survival is ~70% (50-90% by PRETEXT stage); notably, low AFP (<100 ng/mL) indicates poor prognosis in hepatoblastoma, opposite to HCC.","pitfalls":["A negative liver biopsy does NOT exclude HCC; sensitivity falls below 50% for tumors 1-2 cm, and a second biopsy is advised if findings are inconclusive or the lesion grows/changes enhancement","Nodules under 1 cm are unlikely to be HCC and are difficult to diagnose; short-interval ultrasound follow-up (every 3-4 months) is recommended rather than immediate biopsy","PET and contrast-enhanced ultrasound perform poorly for early HCC diagnosis and are not recommended as primary diagnostic tools","An AFP of >=400 ng/dL is highly suspicious but NOT diagnostic of HCC by guidelines","TNM staging should NOT be applied to HCC because it omits liver function and performance status","About 25-30% of MASH-related HCCs occur without cirrhosis, so absence of cirrhosis does not exclude HCC in this group","Surveillance is generally NOT recommended in advanced (Child-Pugh C) cirrhosis since curative therapy is precluded, except for liver transplant waiting-list patients","Liver biopsy carries a ~2-3% risk of complications including tumor seeding and bleeding"],"management":["Stage-directed treatment using the Barcelona Clinic Liver Cancer framework, with surgical resection, liver transplantation or local ablation for early-stage hepatocellular carcinoma","Transarterial chemoembolisation for intermediate-stage, liver-confined disease","Systemic therapy for advanced hepatocellular carcinoma, either combination immunotherapy (atezolizumab with bevacizumab) or multikinase inhibitors such as sorafenib or lenvatinib","Treatment of the underlying liver disease and its cause, including antiviral therapy for hepatitis B or C and alcohol cessation, with management of cirrhosis and portal hypertension","Surgical resection with lymphadenectomy and gemcitabine-based chemotherapy for cholangiocarcinoma, with endoscopic biliary drainage or stenting for obstructive jaundice","Regular surveillance ultrasound with serum alpha-fetoprotein in at-risk cirrhotic patients to detect tumours at a curable stage","Hepatobiliary multidisciplinary team management with best supportive and palliative care where appropriate"],"severityClassification":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is staged by the Barcelona Clinic Liver Cancer (BCLC) system into five stages: very early (BCLC 0, single nodule <=2 cm, Child-Pugh A, ECOG 0), early (BCLC A, single or up to three nodules <=3 cm, Child-Pugh A-B, ECOG 0), intermediate (BCLC B, multinodular, Child-Pugh A-B, ECOG 0), advanced (BCLC C, portal/macrovascular invasion, N1 or M1, or ECOG 1-2), and terminal (BCLC D, Child-Pugh C or ECOG >2). Liver function is graded by the Child-Pugh score (classes A-C) and performance by ECOG. Milan criteria (single tumor <=5 cm, or up to three nodules each <=3 cm, no macrovascular invasion or extrahepatic spread) define transplant eligibility. Cholangiocarcinoma is classified anatomically into intrahepatic, perihilar, and distal subtypes; hepatoblastoma uses the PRETEXT (Pretreatment Extent of Tumor) classification (Harrison 22e p.661-669).","references":["Harrison 22e p.659","Harrison 22e p.660","Harrison 22e p.661","Harrison 22e p.662","Harrison 22e p.663","Harrison 22e p.664","Harrison 22e p.667","Harrison 22e p.668","Harrison 22e p.669","Harrison 22e p.670"],"crossLinks":[]},"tumour_induced_osteomalacia":{"name":"tumour induced osteomalacia","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oncogenic osteomalacia","TIO","phosphaturic mesenchymal tumour syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Oxford Textbook of Endocrinology and Diabetes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Tumour-induced osteomalacia is a rare acquired paraneoplastic syndrome caused by FGF23, and other phosphatonins, secreted by usually small benign phosphaturic mesenchymal tumours of the mixed connective tissue variant.","Excess FGF23 produces renal phosphate wasting, hypophosphataemia and low 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, with normal calcium and PTH.","Adults present insidiously with bone pain, muscle weakness, fatigue, height loss and fragility or insufficiency fractures, and are frequently misdiagnosed for years.","The biochemistry mirrors X-linked hypophosphataemia but is acquired in adulthood with no family history; a raised or inappropriately normal intact FGF23 supports the diagnosis.","Tumours are often tiny and hidden in the extremities, craniofacial bones or sinonasal tract, so localisation depends on functional imaging; 68-gallium DOTATATE PET/CT is the most sensitive, supplemented by MRI.","Complete surgical resection is curative, with rapid normalisation of phosphate; incomplete localisation is the main obstacle to cure.","When the tumour cannot be found or resected, medical therapy with oral phosphate and active vitamin D, or burosumab, controls the biochemistry.","Consider this diagnosis in any adult-onset hypophosphataemic osteomalacia without a hereditary or nutritional explanation."],"pathophysiology":"Phosphaturic mesenchymal tumours secrete fibroblast growth factor 23 (and less commonly other phosphatonins such as sFRP4 and MEPE). Circulating FGF23 downregulates the proximal tubular sodium-phosphate cotransporters, causing renal phosphate wasting and hypophosphataemia, and suppresses renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase so that 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is inappropriately low. The resulting chronic hypophosphataemia impairs mineralisation of osteoid, producing osteomalacia. Because the tumours are typically small, slow-growing and anatomically obscure, the phosphaturic state persists and drives progressive skeletal disease until the lesion is identified and removed.","additionalDifferentials":["X-linked or other hereditary hypophosphataemia presenting late","autosomal dominant hypophosphataemic rickets","Fanconi syndrome","nutritional osteomalacia","drug-induced phosphate wasting (intravenous iron, tenofovir)","fibrous dysplasia or McCune-Albright syndrome","multiple myeloma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["serum phosphate, calcium, alkaline phosphatase and PTH","25-hydroxy and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D","renal tubular phosphate reabsorption (TmP/GFR)","intact FGF23","68-gallium DOTATATE PET/CT for tumour localisation","whole-body MRI and selective venous sampling for FGF23 if required","histology of the resected tumour"],"redFlags":["recurrent insufficiency fractures","severe refractory hypophosphataemia","profound proximal myopathy causing immobility","failure to localise a strongly suspected tumour","rare malignant phosphaturic mesenchymal tumour with metastases"],"prognosis":"The condition is curable with complete tumour resection, after which symptoms and biochemistry resolve, although localisation may take years and rare malignant variants carry a worse outlook.","pitfalls":["prolonged diagnostic delay from attributing symptoms to osteoporosis or fibromyalgia","not measuring phosphate and FGF23 in unexplained bone pain","relying on anatomical imaging alone and missing small tumours","assuming a drug cause is excluded once the agent is stopped without rechecking phosphate","incomplete resection leading to biochemical recurrence"],"management":["Complete surgical resection of the FGF23-secreting phosphaturic mesenchymal tumour, which reverses the biochemistry and heals the osteomalacia","Oral phosphate replacement together with active vitamin D (alfacalcidol or calcitriol) while the tumour remains occult or after incomplete resection","The anti-FGF23 antibody burosumab as an alternative targeted treatment when the tumour cannot be localised or removed","Localisation with functional gallium-68 DOTATATE PET/CT supplemented by whole-body MRI and, if needed, selective venous FGF23 sampling","Physiotherapy, analgesia and fracture prevention for bone pain and proximal myopathy","Surveillance for secondary hyperparathyroidism and nephrocalcinosis during long-term phosphate and vitamin D therapy","Multidisciplinary metabolic bone and surgical care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Oxford Textbook of Endocrinology and Diabetes","Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"tumour_lysis_syndrome":{"name":"tumour lysis syndrome","system":"Renal and metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TLS","acute tumour lysis syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"British Committee for Standards in Haematology / Cairo-Bishop tumour lysis guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the Cairo-Bishop criteria as laboratory TLS (two or more of hyperuricaemia, hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia and hypocalcaemia within a defined window) progressing to clinical TLS when renal impairment, arrhythmia, seizure or death supervene.","Highest risk with bulky, rapidly proliferating and chemosensitive malignancies: Burkitt and other high-grade non-Hodgkin lymphomas, acute lymphoblastic leukaemia, and acute leukaemias with high white-cell counts or high LDH.","Spontaneous TLS may occur before any cytotoxic therapy (classically Burkitt lymphoma) and characteristically shows hyperuricaemia without the hyperphosphataemia typical of treatment-induced TLS, because released phosphate is reincorporated into new tumour cells.","Secondary hypocalcaemia results from calcium-phosphate precipitation; give calcium only for symptomatic hypocalcaemia because supplementation risks metastatic calcification and worsening acute kidney injury.","Rasburicase (recombinant urate oxidase) rapidly degrades existing urate and is preferred for high-risk or established TLS; it is contraindicated in G6PD deficiency owing to the risk of haemolysis and methaemoglobinaemia.","Allopurinol or febuxostat prevents new urate formation but does not lower an existing urate load, so it is a prophylactic (not rescue) agent in lower-risk patients.","Aggressive isotonic intravenous fluid to maintain a high urine output is the cornerstone; routine urinary alkalinisation is no longer recommended because it promotes calcium-phosphate deposition in the kidney.","Early nephrology involvement and low threshold for renal replacement therapy are warranted for refractory hyperkalaemia, symptomatic hypocalcaemia with hyperphosphataemia, volume overload or oligoanuric acute kidney injury."],"pathophysiology":"Rapid lysis of a large, proliferative tumour burden—either spontaneously or precipitated by cytotoxic therapy, corticosteroids or radiotherapy—releases intracellular potassium, phosphate and nucleic acids into the circulation faster than renal excretion can clear them. Purine catabolism generates uric acid that precipitates in the distal tubules and collecting ducts, while hyperphosphataemia drives calcium-phosphate crystal deposition; both cause intrarenal obstruction, vasoconstriction and inflammation leading to acute kidney injury, which in turn compounds the metabolic derangement. The resulting hyperkalaemia and hypocalcaemia produce the cardiac and neuromuscular complications that define clinical TLS.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute kidney injury from sepsis, nephrotoxins or contrast","Rhabdomyolysis with hyperkalaemia and hyperphosphataemia","Pre-existing chronic kidney disease with electrolyte derangement","Lactic acidosis of malignancy","Obstructive uropathy from retroperitoneal disease","Pseudohyperkalaemia from sample haemolysis or extreme leucocytosis","Primary hyperphosphataemia from exogenous phosphate administration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial urea, electrolytes and creatinine with urate, phosphate, calcium and magnesium","Lactate dehydrogenase and uric acid to gauge tumour burden and risk","Twelve-lead ECG for hyperkalaemic changes and QT prolongation from hypocalcaemia","Venous or arterial blood gas for acid-base status","Strict fluid balance and hourly urine output monitoring","G6PD assay before rasburicase where clinically feasible","Full blood count and coagulation screen to detect concurrent DIC or leukostasis"],"redFlags":["ECG changes or arrhythmia from severe hyperkalaemia","Tetany, carpopedal spasm or seizures from acute hypocalcaemia","Oliguria or anuria signalling established acute kidney injury","Rapidly rising potassium or phosphate despite fluids","Reduced consciousness or cardiovascular collapse"],"prognosis":"With pre-emptive risk stratification, hydration and urate-lowering therapy most cases are preventable or reversible, but established clinical TLS with acute kidney injury carries significant morbidity and mortality. Outcome depends on early recognition, control of the underlying malignancy and timely renal support.","pitfalls":["Administering intravenous calcium for asymptomatic hypocalcaemia, precipitating calcium-phosphate deposition","Relying on allopurinol to treat an already elevated urate load instead of using rasburicase","Giving rasburicase without excluding G6PD deficiency","Continuing urinary alkalinisation, which worsens calcium-phosphate precipitation","Misinterpreting spurious hyperkalaemia from a haemolysed or delayed sample and either over- or under-treating"],"management":["Risk stratification with aggressive intravenous hydration to maintain high urine output and enhance clearance of urate and phosphate","Rasburicase (recombinant urate oxidase) for high-risk or established disease to rapidly degrade existing uric acid, avoiding it in G6PD deficiency because of haemolysis and methaemoglobinaemia","Allopurinol or febuxostat as prophylaxis in lower-risk patients to prevent new urate formation","Emergency management of hyperkalaemia and treatment of hyperphosphataemia with phosphate binders","Calcium replacement reserved for symptomatic hypocalcaemia, avoiding routine supplementation to prevent metastatic calcification","Renal replacement therapy for refractory electrolyte disturbance, severe acute kidney injury or fluid overload","Close monitoring with serial electrolytes, urate, phosphate and calcium plus continuous cardiac monitoring, coordinated with the oncology and haematology team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cairo MS, Bishop M. Tumour lysis syndrome: classification and consensus grading","British Committee for Standards in Haematology guideline on management of tumour lysis syndrome","Coiffier B et al. Guidelines for the management of paediatric and adult tumour lysis syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"tungiasis":{"name":"Tungiasis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tunga penetrans","jigger flea infestation","sand flea disease","chigoe flea","pique"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by penetration of the skin by the gravid female sand flea Tunga penetrans, endemic in sub-Saharan Africa, Latin America and the Caribbean","Affects impoverished communities where people walk barefoot on sandy or dusty soil; lesions concentrate on the feet, especially the toes, periungual folds and soles","The classic lesion is a white papule with a central black dot (the flea's posterior/exsanguination pore) that enlarges into a painful, itchy nodule as eggs develop","Lesions can be staged (Fortaleza classification) from penetration through to involution and residual skin changes","Diagnosis is clinical, based on typical lesions in an epidemiological context; dermoscopy can confirm the embedded flea","Secondary bacterial infection is common and can lead to abscess, ulceration, lymphangitis, and in neglected cases tetanus or gangrene","Treatment is careful surgical extraction of the flea under sterile conditions, wound care, and ensuring tetanus immunisation; occlusive topical agents may kill the embedded flea"],"pathophysiology":"The impregnated female flea burrows head-first into the epidermis, usually of the foot, leaving its posterior end at the skin surface for respiration, defecation and egg expulsion. Over one to two weeks the flea's abdomen swells enormously as eggs mature, producing the characteristic expanding nodule with a central pore. Local inflammation, pain and pruritus result from the parasite and its by-products. Once eggs are shed the flea dies and the lesion involutes, but the breach in the skin and retained flea remnants predispose to secondary bacterial infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Plantar wart (verruca)","Foreign body granuloma or splinter reaction","Myiasis (furuncular)","Paronychia or bacterial abscess","Scabies or other pruritic dermatoses of the feet","Melanoma or pigmented lesion (when the black dot is prominent)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical and epidemiological assessment (travel/residence, barefoot exposure)","Dermoscopy to visualise the embedded flea and its pore","Examination for multiple lesions and secondary infection","Assessment of tetanus immunisation status","Swab and culture if secondary bacterial infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Spreading cellulitis, lymphangitis or abscess formation","Signs suggestive of tetanus in an unimmunised patient","Gangrene or deep tissue destruction from heavy or neglected infestation","Widespread multiple lesions causing difficulty walking and disability"],"prognosis":"Isolated lesions resolve well after extraction and wound care, but heavy or repeated infestation in endemic settings causes chronic morbidity, deformity and disability, and secondary infection can be limb- or life-threatening if neglected.","pitfalls":["Incomplete extraction leaving flea parts that provoke persistent inflammation and infection","Performing non-sterile 'home' extraction, introducing secondary infection or tetanus risk","Overlooking tetanus prophylaxis","Mistaking a solitary lesion for a wart and applying inappropriate destructive treatment","Failing to advise on footwear and environmental measures, allowing reinfestation"],"management":["Sterile surgical extraction of the embedded flea in its entirety, followed by wound cleaning and dressing","Topical treatment such as occlusive dimeticone or topical ivermectin for multiple lesions where individual extraction is impractical","Assessment and updating of tetanus immunisation given the breach in the skin","Treatment of secondary bacterial infection with appropriate antibiotics and drainage of any abscess","Examination for and management of multiple or heavily infested lesions","Prevention through closed footwear, treatment of animal reservoirs and environmental measures in endemic communities","Follow-up to ensure healing and detect complications such as lymphangitis, ulceration or deformity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"turf_toe":{"name":"Turf toe (first MTP hyperextension sprain)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Sports & Soft tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["first MTP hyperextension sprain","great toe hyperextension injury","plantar plate injury of the hallux","metatarsophalangeal sprain"],"chapter":null,"source":"Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sprain of the plantar capsuloligamentous complex (plantar plate) of the first metatarsophalangeal joint from forced hyperextension of the great toe","Common on hard artificial surfaces ('turf') and in sports involving push-off with the foot fixed in dorsiflexion","The plantar plate, sesamoid complex and collateral ligaments may be injured; severe cases can involve sesamoid fracture, diastasis or dislocation","Presents with pain, swelling and bruising at the first MTP joint and painful, limited toe extension","Graded by severity, with assessment of sesamoid position and joint stability","Radiographs assess sesamoid position and fracture, and MRI best characterises plantar plate and sesamoid injury","Management is graded, with relative rest, protection using stiff-soled footwear or taping to limit dorsiflexion, and progressive rehabilitation; higher grades and structural injury such as sesamoid disruption or plate rupture may need surgery","Inadequately managed injuries can lead to persistent pain, stiffness and hallux deformity"],"pathophysiology":"Turf toe is an injury to the plantar capsuloligamentous complex of the first metatarsophalangeal joint, caused by forced hyperextension (dorsiflexion) of the hallux while the forefoot is fixed on the ground, as in push-off on a hard surface. The plantar plate, the sesamoid apparatus and flexor mechanism, and the collateral ligaments may be stretched or torn; in severe injury there can be sesamoid fracture, retraction or diastasis and joint instability or dislocation. The sesamoids, embedded within the flexor hallucis brevis mechanism, are integral to the injured complex, so their position and integrity guide grading and management.","additionalDifferentials":["Sesamoid fracture or sesamoiditis","First MTP joint dislocation","Hallux (proximal phalanx) fracture","Gout of the first MTP joint","Hallux rigidus or first MTP osteoarthritis","Metatarsophalangeal sprain of a lesser toe"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Weight-bearing radiographs including assessment of sesamoid position, with comparison views","MRI to characterise plantar plate, sesamoid and capsular injury","Clinical assessment of first MTP stability and range of movement","Ultrasound in some settings to assess the plantar plate","Bone scan or CT where an occult sesamoid fracture is suspected"],"redFlags":["Gross instability or dislocation of the first MTP joint","Sesamoid diastasis, retraction or fracture suggesting complete plantar plate rupture","Inability to push off or bear weight","Failure to improve, risking chronic instability and deformity"],"prognosis":"Low-grade sprains generally recover with protected rehabilitation over weeks, whereas high-grade injuries with plantar plate rupture or sesamoid disruption may need surgery and can cause prolonged loss of play and long-term stiffness or deformity.","pitfalls":["Underestimating a high-grade injury and returning to play too soon","Missing a sesamoid fracture, diastasis or plantar plate rupture","Inadequate protection allowing recurrent hyperextension","Overlooking progression to hallux rigidus or deformity"],"management":["Initial rest, ice, compression, elevation and relative offloading with protected weight-bearing for the acute injury","Immobilisation in stiff-soled footwear, turf-toe taping or a rigid orthosis to limit painful hallux dorsiflexion","Analgesia and anti-inflammatory measures for pain and swelling","Graded rehabilitation restoring first metatarsophalangeal range of movement and strength before return to sport","Grading-based management, with surgical repair reserved for high-grade injuries involving plantar-plate rupture, sesamoid fracture, diastasis or joint instability","Imaging-guided assessment of sesamoid position and joint stability to direct treatment","Sports-medicine and, where indicated, orthopaedic foot-and-ankle referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Brukner and Khan's Clinical Sports Medicine","British Journal of Sports Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"turner_mosaicism":{"name":"Turner syndrome mosaicism (45,X/46,XX)","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mosaic Turner syndrome","45,X/46,XX","Turner mosaic karyotype","mosaic monosomy X"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Endocrine Society clinical guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mosaic karyotype combining a 45,X line with a normal 46,XX line, generally producing a milder and more variable phenotype than classic monosomy X","Short stature and gonadal insufficiency remain the most consistent features, but their severity depends on the proportion of 45,X cells","Some individuals retain enough ovarian tissue for spontaneous puberty and even fertility, though premature ovarian insufficiency is common","Classic Turner stigmata such as webbed neck, lymphoedema, cardiac and renal anomalies occur less frequently than in non-mosaic disease","Because there is no Y-chromosome line, the gonadoblastoma risk that mandates gonadectomy in Y-bearing dysgenesis does not apply","Cardiac surveillance (particularly for bicuspid aortic valve and aortic dilatation) and renal, thyroid, hearing and metabolic monitoring remain important","Management includes growth optimisation, timely oestrogen replacement when ovarian function is insufficient, and fertility counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Turner syndrome mosaicism results from the coexistence of a 45,X cell line, usually arising from postzygotic loss of an X chromosome, alongside a normal 46,XX line. Haploinsufficiency of X-linked genes that escape inactivation, notably SHOX, contributes to short stature and skeletal features, while accelerated loss of oocytes leads to varying degrees of ovarian insufficiency. Because the phenotype reflects the tissue distribution and proportion of the 45,X line, presentations range from near-normal to features resembling classic Turner syndrome. The absence of any Y-bearing cell line means the streak-gonad tumour risk associated with Y material is not a concern in this karyotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-mosaic Turner syndrome (45,X)","45,X/46,XY mixed gonadal dysgenesis","Noonan syndrome (phenotypic overlap with normal karyotype)","46,XX gonadal dysgenesis and premature ovarian insufficiency of other cause","Constitutional short stature or growth hormone deficiency","Other sex-chromosome aneuploidies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype with adequate cell counts (and FISH where needed) to detect and quantify mosaicism","Serum gonadotropins and oestradiol to assess ovarian function","Echocardiography and, in adults, aortic imaging for cardiovascular anomalies","Renal ultrasound for structural anomalies","Thyroid function, glucose and lipid screening, and audiometry","Bone mineral density assessment and anti-mullerian hormone for fertility counselling"],"redFlags":["Aortic root dilatation or bicuspid aortic valve raising the risk of dissection","Undiagnosed renal or cardiac structural anomaly","Untreated oestrogen deficiency leading to osteoporosis","Signs of autoimmune thyroid disease or impaired glucose tolerance"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally more favourable than in non-mosaic Turner syndrome, with a proportion achieving spontaneous puberty and occasionally fertility, though ovarian insufficiency and short stature are frequent. Long-term outcome depends on cardiovascular surveillance and management of associated endocrine and metabolic conditions.","pitfalls":["Assuming a mild phenotype excludes cardiovascular and renal anomalies that still require screening","Overlooking premature ovarian insufficiency in someone who underwent spontaneous puberty","Applying the gonadectomy rule inappropriately when there is no Y-chromosome line","Insufficient karyotype sampling missing a low-level 45,X clone"],"management":["Recombinant growth hormone to improve height where short stature is present","Oestrogen replacement to induce and maintain puberty followed by combined oestrogen-progesterone therapy, individualised because some retain ovarian function","Fertility counselling with consideration of oocyte cryopreservation given the risk of premature ovarian insufficiency, and assisted reproduction where needed","Cardiac surveillance for bicuspid aortic valve and aortic dilatation with echocardiography and aortic imaging","Monitoring for renal anomalies, thyroid disease, hearing loss, metabolic disturbance and bone health","No gonadectomy is required because there is no Y-chromosome line and hence no gonadoblastoma risk","Genetic counselling and multidisciplinary care coordinated across endocrinology, cardiology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","International Turner syndrome clinical practice guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"turner_syndrome":{"name":"Turner Syndrome","system":"Genetic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["45,X","monosomy X","Ullrich-Turner syndrome","gonadal dysgenesis (45,X)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chromosomal disorder affecting females caused by complete or partial absence of one X chromosome, most commonly a 45,X karyotype, with mosaic forms also occurring.","The two most consistent features are short stature and gonadal dysgenesis leading to ovarian failure.","Newborns may show puffiness of the hands and feet (lymphoedema) and redundant nuchal skin; a fetal cystic hygroma may be detected antenatally.","Characteristic features can include a webbed neck, low posterior hairline, widely spaced nipples on a broad chest, and a wide carrying angle at the elbows.","Congenital cardiovascular anomalies, particularly bicuspid aortic valve and coarctation of the aorta, carry a risk of aortic dilatation and dissection and require ongoing surveillance.","Renal anomalies such as a horseshoe kidney are common, as are recurrent otitis media and hearing loss.","Most girls have normal intelligence but may have specific difficulties with visuospatial and mathematical tasks.","Growth hormone therapy is used to improve final height, and oestrogen replacement is used to induce puberty and maintain bone and uterine health.","Diagnosis is confirmed by karyotype, and lifelong multidisciplinary follow-up addresses cardiac, endocrine, and reproductive needs."],"pathophysiology":"Loss of all or part of the second sex chromosome causes haploinsufficiency of genes that normally escape X-inactivation and are needed in two copies. Deficiency of the SHOX gene contributes to short stature and skeletal features, while accelerated loss of ovarian follicles leads to gonadal dysgenesis and oestrogen deficiency. Abnormal fetal lymphatic development produces the lymphoedema, nuchal changes, and associated features such as the webbed neck, and disrupted development contributes to the cardiovascular and renal malformations.","additionalDifferentials":["Noonan syndrome (overlapping webbed neck and short stature, normal karyotype)","Other causes of short stature (growth hormone deficiency, constitutional delay)","Other causes of primary ovarian insufficiency or delayed puberty","Mixed gonadal dysgenesis","Skeletal dysplasias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype to confirm monosomy X or mosaicism (with additional cells if mosaicism suspected)","Echocardiography and cardiac MRI to assess aortic valve, arch, and aortic dimensions","Renal ultrasound for structural anomalies","Gonadotrophins (FSH, LH) and thyroid function testing","Audiological assessment","Bone age and growth monitoring; screening for coeliac disease"],"redFlags":["Aortic dilatation or symptoms suggesting aortic dissection","Severe coarctation or critical cardiac lesions in infancy","Progressive hearing loss","Features suggesting Y-chromosome material, which raises gonadoblastoma risk","Signs of untreated hypothyroidism or coeliac disease"],"prognosis":"With appropriate hormone therapy and surveillance most girls achieve improved height, undergo induced puberty, and have a good quality of life; long-term outlook is influenced chiefly by cardiovascular complications, and fertility is usually impaired though assisted reproduction may be possible.","pitfalls":["Not considering the diagnosis in a girl with unexplained short stature or delayed puberty","Missing mosaicism when only a limited number of cells are analysed","Overlooking the need for lifelong aortic surveillance","Failing to screen for associated hypothyroidism, coeliac disease, and hearing loss"],"management":["Recombinant growth hormone therapy in childhood to improve final adult height, with oxandrolone as a possible adjunct","Oestrogen replacement to induce puberty followed by maintenance combined oestrogen-progesterone hormone therapy for gonadal failure","Lifelong cardiovascular surveillance with echocardiography and aortic MRI for bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation and aortic dilatation or dissection risk","Monitoring and management of associated conditions including renal anomalies, hypothyroidism, coeliac disease, recurrent otitis media, hearing loss and reduced bone density","Fertility counselling with assisted reproduction via oocyte donation and pre-pregnancy cardiac assessment given dissection risk","Urgent evaluation for acute aortic dissection in any patient presenting with acute chest pain","Multidisciplinary care with karyotype confirmation and genetic counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"twin_twin_transfusion_syndrome":{"name":"Twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome","system":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TTTS","twin-twin transfusion syndrome","feto-fetal transfusion syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / RCOG and international fetal medicine guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["TTTS is a complication of monochorionic twin pregnancies caused by unbalanced blood flow across shared placental vascular anastomoses","The donor twin becomes hypovolaemic, oliguric, and growth-restricted with oligohydramnios, appearing 'stuck' against the uterine wall","The recipient twin becomes hypervolaemic and polyuric with polyhydramnios and may develop cardiac overload and hydrops","Determining chorionicity in the first trimester is essential, as only monochorionic pregnancies are at risk","Diagnosis relies on the combination of oligohydramnios in one sac and polyhydramnios in the other on ultrasound","The Quintero system stages severity from amniotic fluid discordance through absent bladder filling, abnormal Dopplers, hydrops, to demise","Fetoscopic laser photocoagulation of the connecting placental anastomoses is the preferred treatment for significant disease","Amnioreduction and, in selected cases, selective reduction are alternative or adjunctive options depending on stage and gestation"],"pathophysiology":"In monochorionic twins a single shared placenta contains vascular anastomoses connecting the two foetal circulations. When net flow through unbalanced arteriovenous connections favours one twin, blood is chronically shunted from a donor to a recipient. The donor responds to relative hypovolaemia with reduced urine output and oligohydramnios and becomes growth-restricted, while the recipient develops volume overload, polyuria, and polyhydramnios, with activation of vasoactive and natriuretic pathways that can lead to cardiac hypertrophy, dysfunction, and hydrops. Without treatment the haemodynamic imbalance progresses and threatens both twins.","additionalDifferentials":["Selective foetal growth restriction in monochorionic twins","Twin anaemia-polycythaemia sequence","Discordant twins from other causes","Structural or chromosomal anomaly in one twin","Isolated polyhydramnios or oligohydramnios","Twin reversed arterial perfusion sequence"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial ultrasound assessment of amniotic fluid volumes in each sac","Doppler studies of umbilical artery, middle cerebral artery, and ductus venosus","Foetal echocardiography, particularly of the recipient twin","Assessment of bladder filling and growth in each twin","Confirmation of chorionicity from early pregnancy imaging","Foetal anatomy survey to exclude coexisting anomalies"],"redFlags":["Rapidly worsening polyhydramnios causing maternal discomfort or threatened preterm labour","Abnormal Doppler waveforms or absent bladder filling indicating advancing stage","Signs of foetal hydrops in the recipient","Sudden demise of one twin threatening the co-twin"],"prognosis":"Untreated advanced TTTS carries a high risk of loss of one or both twins. Fetoscopic laser therapy substantially improves survival and reduces neurological morbidity, though outcomes depend on stage at treatment and gestational age, and long-term neurodevelopmental follow-up is advised.","pitfalls":["Failing to establish chorionicity early and missing the at-risk monochorionic pregnancy","Attributing amniotic fluid discordance to growth restriction alone and delaying referral","Not referring promptly to a fetal medicine centre capable of laser therapy","Overlooking the co-twin's risk after single intrauterine death"],"management":["Fetoscopic laser photocoagulation of the placental vascular anastomoses as the definitive treatment for advanced (Quintero stage II and above) disease","Amnioreduction or, in selected cases, amniotic septostomy where laser therapy is unavailable or unsuitable","Determination of chorionicity and Quintero staging to guide the timing and choice of intervention","Serial ultrasound and Doppler surveillance of amniotic fluid, bladder filling, growth and foetal cardiac function","Consideration of selective reduction by cord occlusion in severe cases with impending demise of one twin","Planning of the timing and mode of delivery in a tertiary unit with neonatal support, and treatment of preterm labour","Foetal medicine referral and long-term neurodevelopmental follow-up of survivors"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","RCOG guidance on monochorionic twin pregnancy","International fetal medicine consensus on twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"tympanic_membrane_perforation":{"name":"Tympanic membrane perforation","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["perforated eardrum","ruptured tympanic membrane","TM perforation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Perforations arise most often from infection (acute or chronic otitis media) but also from trauma such as a slap to the ear, blast injury, barotrauma or instrumentation","Central perforations of the pars tensa are generally safe, whereas attic or marginal (posterosuperior) perforations are unsafe because they associate with cholesteatoma","The degree of conductive hearing loss reflects both the size and site of the perforation, being greater with larger and posterior defects","A discharging perforation suggests active middle ear disease and warrants aural toilet and treatment of infection","Many traumatic and small perforations heal spontaneously if the ear is kept dry and free of infection","Water precautions are important because contamination can precipitate recurrent infection and discharge","Persistent dry perforations affecting hearing or causing recurrent infection can be repaired by myringoplasty or tympanoplasty","Examination should assess the size, site and margins of the defect and the state of the middle ear mucosa and ossicles"],"pathophysiology":"The tympanic membrane can be breached when middle ear pressure rises from suppurative infection until necrosis of the drum produces a perforation, or when external forces (direct trauma, sudden pressure change, or a foreign body) tear it mechanically. Loss of an intact vibrating surface and of the round window sound-shielding effect reduces the efficiency of sound transfer to the cochlea, producing conductive hearing loss proportional to the area lost and its position. Small tears usually re-epithelialise and heal, but persistent perforation, ongoing infection, or ingrowth of squamous epithelium through a marginal or attic defect can prevent closure and predispose to cholesteatoma and chronic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Retracted but intact tympanic membrane simulating a defect","Healed thin (dimeric) segment mimicking a perforation","Cholesteatoma with a marginal or attic defect","Grommet (ventilation tube) in situ","Otitis externa with debris obscuring the drum","Congenital ossicular anomaly causing conductive loss with an intact drum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy and, ideally, microscopy to define size, site and margins of the perforation","Pure tone audiometry and tuning fork tests to quantify conductive loss","Tympanometry, which shows a large ear canal volume with a perforation","Culture of any discharge in active infection","CT of the temporal bone if cholesteatoma or ossicular pathology is suspected"],"redFlags":["Marginal or attic perforation with foul discharge or crusting suggesting cholesteatoma","Vertigo, sensorineural loss or facial weakness indicating inner ear or facial nerve involvement","Perforation following head trauma with cerebrospinal fluid otorrhoea or associated fracture","Persistent bloody discharge or granulations raising concern for neoplasm"],"prognosis":"Small, uncomplicated and traumatic perforations often heal spontaneously with good recovery of hearing, and surgical repair of dry persistent defects has a high success rate; outcomes are worse when there is ongoing infection, ossicular damage or an underlying cholesteatoma.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish a dangerous marginal or attic perforation from a safe central one","Allowing water to enter the ear and precipitate recurrent infection","Assuming all perforations need surgery when many heal with conservative care","Overlooking ossicular discontinuity as an additional cause of hearing loss"],"management":["Watchful waiting with strict dry-ear precautions for small traumatic or infective perforations, most of which heal spontaneously","Treatment of active middle-ear infection with aural toilet and topical or systemic antibiotics, avoiding potentially ototoxic drops reaching the middle ear","Water precautions to prevent contamination and recurrent discharge","Surgical repair by myringoplasty or tympanoplasty for persistent dry perforations, with ossicular reconstruction where required","Urgent otological assessment and temporal-bone CT for marginal or attic (unsafe) perforations given the risk of cholesteatoma","Audiometric assessment and hearing rehabilitation for conductive hearing loss","ENT referral for non-healing, discharging or complicated perforations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"type1_diabetes_child":{"name":"Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (Child)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["T1DM","type 1 diabetes","insulin-dependent diabetes","juvenile diabetes","paediatric type 1 diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common form of diabetes in children, caused by autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells leading to absolute insulin deficiency.","Classic presenting features are polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, and fatigue, often over a few weeks; new secondary enuresis in a previously dry child is a useful clue.","A substantial proportion of children present in diabetic ketoacidosis, which can be the first manifestation of the disease.","Lifelong insulin replacement is essential; unlike type 2 diabetes, it cannot be managed by lifestyle measures or oral agents alone.","A 'honeymoon' period of partial remission with reduced insulin needs may follow initial stabilisation but is temporary.","Management is multidisciplinary, combining insulin, carbohydrate awareness, glucose monitoring, and structured education for the child and family.","Careful attention to preventing and recognising hypoglycaemia is central to safe day-to-day care.","Long-term care includes screening for associated autoimmune conditions (such as thyroid disease and coeliac disease) and for microvascular complications over time."],"pathophysiology":"Type 1 diabetes results from T-cell mediated autoimmune destruction of insulin-producing pancreatic beta cells in genetically predisposed children, often triggered by environmental factors. Progressive beta-cell loss produces absolute insulin deficiency, so glucose cannot enter cells and hyperglycaemia develops. Without insulin the body shifts to lipolysis and ketogenesis; unrestrained ketone production causes metabolic acidosis, while osmotic diuresis from glycosuria leads to dehydration and electrolyte loss, culminating in diabetic ketoacidosis if untreated.","additionalDifferentials":["Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Monogenic diabetes (MODY) and neonatal diabetes","Secondary diabetes (e.g. cystic fibrosis-related, steroid-induced)","Diabetes insipidus (for polyuria and polydipsia)","Transient stress hyperglycaemia during acute illness","Urinary tract infection (for enuresis or dysuria)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood glucose and HbA1c","Blood and/or urine ketones","Venous blood gas and electrolytes to assess for ketoacidosis","Pancreatic autoantibodies (e.g. GAD, IA-2, ZnT8, islet cell)","Thyroid function and coeliac screening at diagnosis and during follow-up","C-peptide where the diabetes type is uncertain"],"redFlags":["New-onset type 1 diabetes presenting in diabetic ketoacidosis (vomiting, abdominal pain, deep sighing breathing, dehydration, drowsiness) is a medical emergency","Reduced consciousness or focal neurological signs suggesting cerebral oedema during DKA management","Severe hypoglycaemia with seizures or loss of consciousness","Signs of significant dehydration or shock","Persistent vomiting in a known diabetic child, which may signal evolving ketoacidosis"],"prognosis":"With modern insulin regimens, glucose monitoring, and multidisciplinary support most children live full lives; long-term outcome depends heavily on sustained glycaemic control, which reduces the risk of microvascular and macrovascular complications. Acute risks include ketoacidosis and severe hypoglycaemia.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early symptoms for a urinary tract infection or behavioural enuresis and delaying diagnosis","Failing to check ketones and acid-base status in a hyperglycaemic, unwell child","Correcting fluids and glucose too aggressively in DKA and precipitating cerebral oedema","Assuming the honeymoon phase means resolution and reducing vigilance","Overlooking associated autoimmune conditions during follow-up"],"management":["Lifelong subcutaneous insulin replacement using a basal-bolus multiple daily injection regimen or continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion pump, as absolute insulin deficiency cannot be managed by lifestyle or oral agents","Structured carbohydrate awareness and dietary education, matching prandial insulin to carbohydrate intake and adjusting for exercise","Glucose monitoring with capillary testing or continuous glucose monitoring, with periodic HbA1c to guide dose titration and target glycaemic control","Prompt treatment of hypoglycaemia with fast-acting oral carbohydrate, and parenteral glucagon for severe episodes, with sick-day rules taught to the family","Emergency management of diabetic ketoacidosis with careful rehydration, insulin infusion and potassium replacement, avoiding overly rapid correction to reduce cerebral oedema risk","Surveillance for associated autoimmune conditions with thyroid and coeliac screening, and later screening for retinopathy, nephropathy and neuropathy","Multidisciplinary care through a paediatric diabetes team including diabetes specialist nurse, dietitian and psychological support for the child and family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"type_a_insulin_resistance":{"name":"Type A insulin resistance syndrome","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["type A insulin resistance","INSR mutation","HAIR-AN syndrome (overlap)","severe insulin resistance without obesity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Endocrine Society clinical guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The mildest of the inherited insulin-receptor disorders, typically from heterozygous INSR mutations and often presenting in adolescent or young adult women","Classic triad of severe insulin resistance, acanthosis nigricans, and hyperandrogenism (hirsutism, oligomenorrhoea, polycystic ovaries) in a lean, non-obese individual","Distinguished from ordinary polycystic ovary syndrome by the disproportionate degree of insulin resistance relative to body weight","Marked hyperinsulinaemia is present, and glucose tolerance may be normal early before overt diabetes develops","Ovarian theca-cell stimulation by high insulin drives the hyperandrogenism (an overlap with the acquired HAIR-AN picture)","Because the defect is at the insulin receptor, management uses insulin sensitisers and, in more severe cases, recombinant IGF-1 for severe insulin resistance; leptin has a limited role when leptin is not deficient","It sits at the mild end of the INSR spectrum, far less severe than Rabson-Mendenhall or Donohue syndromes","Diagnosis rests on the lean hyperandrogenic phenotype with disproportionate hyperinsulinaemia and INSR genetics"],"pathophysiology":"Type A insulin resistance syndrome is a genetic insulin-signalling defect, usually from heterozygous mutations in the insulin receptor gene (INSR), that impairs insulin action without the obesity, autoimmunity, or lipodystrophy that cause other insulin-resistant states. Reduced receptor function forces compensatory hyperinsulinaemia; the excess insulin acts on ovarian theca cells to stimulate androgen production, generating hirsutism, menstrual disturbance, and polycystic ovaries, while impaired insulin action on skin promotes acanthosis nigricans. Because affected individuals are typically lean, the severity of insulin resistance is out of proportion to body weight, and overt insulin-resistant diabetes may emerge over time as compensation fails.","additionalDifferentials":["Polycystic ovary syndrome with ordinary insulin resistance","HAIR-AN syndrome (acquired hyperandrogenism-insulin resistance-acanthosis)","Type B insulin resistance from anti-insulin-receptor antibodies","Rabson-Mendenhall syndrome (more severe INSR defect)","Familial partial lipodystrophy","Cushing syndrome","Androgen-secreting ovarian or adrenal tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting insulin and C-peptide demonstrating marked hyperinsulinaemia","Glucose/HbA1c and glucose tolerance testing","Androgen profile (testosterone and related androgens) for hyperandrogenism","Pelvic ultrasound for polycystic ovarian morphology","INSR gene sequencing, with autoantibody testing to exclude type B resistance","Lipid profile and leptin to characterise the metabolic state"],"redFlags":["Progression to overt, difficult-to-control insulin-resistant diabetes","Rapidly worsening virilisation suggesting an alternative androgen source","Features suggesting type B (autoimmune) insulin resistance","Severe metabolic derangement disproportionate to body habitus"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is more favourable than the severe childhood INSR disorders; many patients are managed for years with insulin sensitisers, though progression to overt diabetes and the burden of hyperandrogenism require ongoing care.","pitfalls":["Labelling the presentation as ordinary polycystic ovary syndrome and missing the disproportionate insulin resistance","Overlooking a lean body habitus as a clue against obesity-driven resistance","Failing to separate genetic type A from autoimmune type B insulin resistance","Assuming leptin replacement will help when leptin is not deficient","Not confirming the diagnosis with INSR genetic testing"],"management":["Insulin sensitisers, principally metformin and where appropriate a thiazolidinedione, to counter the receptor-level insulin resistance and compensatory hyperinsulinaemia","Recombinant IGF-1 (mecasermin) as targeted therapy in more severe insulin resistance, bypassing the defective insulin receptor via the IGF-1 receptor","Management of hyperandrogenism with a combined oral contraceptive and an anti-androgen such as spironolactone for hirsutism and menstrual disturbance","Surveillance for progression to overt insulin-resistant diabetes with periodic glucose and HbA1c monitoring as beta-cell compensation fails","Fertility and reproductive care addressing oligomenorrhoea and polycystic ovarian dysfunction","Multidisciplinary input from endocrinology and clinical genetics, with dietetic support even in lean patients","Genetic counselling and family assessment given the heritable INSR mutation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Endocrine Society clinical practice guidance on severe insulin resistance"],"crossLinks":[]},"tyrosinaemia":{"name":"tyrosinaemia","system":"metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hereditary tyrosinaemia type 1","hepatorenal tyrosinaemia","fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics; Scriver's The Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hereditary tyrosinaemia type 1 (HT1) results from deficiency of fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase, the terminal enzyme of tyrosine catabolism; accumulated fumarylacetoacetate and succinylacetone are the toxic culprits, so the plasma tyrosine level does not itself reflect disease severity.","Succinylacetone in urine or blood is the pathognomonic and most sensitive diagnostic marker; it is the analyte used in expanded newborn screening and should be requested specifically, as a raised tyrosine alone is non-specific and seen in many other states.","Nitisinone (NTBC), which blocks the pathway upstream at 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase, has transformed prognosis and is combined with a tyrosine- and phenylalanine-restricted diet; it must not be given without dietary control or iatrogenic tyrosine crystal keratopathy and skin lesions ensue.","Succinylacetone inhibits porphobilinogen synthase (delta-aminolaevulinate dehydratase), producing acute porphyria-like neurological crises with abdominal pain, autonomic instability, hypertension and painful peripheral neuropathy that can mimic Guillain-Barre syndrome or acute intermittent porphyria.","Untreated HT1 carries a very high lifetime risk of hepatocellular carcinoma, which may arise even in childhood; alpha-fetoprotein trends and cross-sectional imaging surveillance are central to long-term follow-up, and adults surviving on treatment remain under cancer surveillance.","Renal involvement is a proximal (Fanconi-type) tubulopathy causing hypophosphataemic rickets, glycosuria, aminoaciduria and renal tubular acidosis; growth failure and bone disease may be presenting features in the milder chronic form.","Tyrosinaemia type 2 (Richner-Hanhart, tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency) is a distinct disorder causing painful palmoplantar hyperkeratosis, dendritic (pseudoherpetic) keratitis and variable intellectual disability, treated by diet alone without hepatorenal or malignancy risk.","Transient tyrosinaemia of the newborn and acquired hypertyrosinaemia from advanced liver disease are far commoner than the inherited enzymopathies and must be distinguished, as they lack succinylacetone."],"pathophysiology":"Tyrosine is degraded through a five-enzyme hepatic and renal pathway. In hereditary tyrosinaemia type 1, biallelic loss-of-function variants in FAH abolish fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase, the final step, causing accumulation of the reactive electrophilic intermediates fumarylacetoacetate and maleylacetoacetate and their derivative succinylacetone. These compounds deplete glutathione, form adducts with cellular macromolecules and are mutagenic, injuring hepatocytes and proximal renal tubular cells and predisposing to malignant transformation. Succinylacetone additionally inhibits porphobilinogen synthase, causing accumulation of the neurotoxic haem precursor delta-aminolaevulinic acid that drives porphyria-like neurological crises, and it impairs renal tubular function producing a Fanconi syndrome. Tyrosinaemia type 2 reflects cytosolic tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency with tyrosine crystal deposition in skin and cornea, while type 3 (4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase deficiency) is milder and chiefly neurological; none of the toxic distal intermediates accumulate in these forms.","additionalDifferentials":["acute intermittent porphyria (crises are clinically similar)","Guillain-Barre syndrome (during neurological crisis with weakness)","other causes of infantile hepatic failure and cirrhosis (galactosaemia, hereditary fructose intolerance, neonatal haemochromatosis)","Wilson disease and other inherited causes of hepatocellular carcinoma predisposition","distal or proximal renal tubular acidosis and other causes of hypophosphataemic rickets","transient tyrosinaemia of the newborn and hypertyrosinaemia secondary to hepatic failure","tyrosinaemia type 2 and type 3 (as differentials of hypertyrosinaemia)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["quantitative urine and blood succinylacetone (diagnostic marker)","plasma amino acids showing raised tyrosine, methionine and phenylalanine","urine organic acids and delta-aminolaevulinic acid","serum alpha-fetoprotein and liver synthetic function including coagulation","FAH gene sequencing and, where relevant, erythrocyte FAH enzyme assay","liver ultrasound and MRI for nodules and hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance","renal tubular profile (phosphate, bicarbonate, glucose, generalised aminoaciduria) and bone imaging for rickets"],"redFlags":["acute neurological crisis with severe abdominal pain, hypertension, autonomic instability and progressive weakness suggesting respiratory compromise","rapidly deteriorating synthetic liver function with coagulopathy unresponsive to vitamin K","rising or newly elevated alpha-fetoprotein or a new hepatic nodule indicating possible hepatocellular carcinoma","corneal pain, photophobia and dendritic keratitis suggesting tyrosine keratopathy (type 2 or nitisinone overexposure without diet)","hypophosphataemic rickets with fractures and marked growth failure"],"prognosis":"With early nitisinone and dietary therapy, survival and freedom from liver failure and neurological crises are excellent and the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma is greatly reduced, particularly when treatment begins in the neonatal period. Late-treated or untreated disease carries high mortality from hepatic failure, malignancy or neurological crisis, and lifelong biochemical, hepatic imaging and malignancy surveillance remains necessary.","pitfalls":["Relying on plasma tyrosine to diagnose or exclude HT1; succinylacetone is the definitive marker and tyrosine may be only modestly raised.","Attributing an acute painful neuropathy or abdominal crisis to porphyria or Guillain-Barre syndrome without considering underlying tyrosinaemia.","Starting or continuing nitisinone without strict tyrosine restriction, risking tyrosine crystal keratopathy and hyperkeratotic skin lesions.","Discontinuing malignancy surveillance in stable treated adults, in whom hepatocellular carcinoma can still arise.","Confusing the benign transient tyrosinaemia of the newborn or hepatic-failure-related hypertyrosinaemia with inherited disease."],"management":["Nitisinone as disease-specific therapy, blocking 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase upstream to prevent formation of toxic fumarylacetoacetate and succinylacetone","Concurrent tyrosine- and phenylalanine-restricted diet with specialist metabolic amino-acid formula, which must accompany nitisinone to prevent tyrosine crystal keratopathy and skin lesions","Biochemical monitoring of succinylacetone and plasma tyrosine to confirm metabolic control and guide dietary and drug adjustment","Hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance with serial alpha-fetoprotein trends and cross-sectional liver imaging, continuing lifelong even on treatment","Supportive management of acute porphyria-like neurological crises and correction of the renal Fanconi tubulopathy including phosphate and vitamin D for hypophosphataemic rickets","Consideration of liver transplantation for hepatocellular carcinoma or decompensated liver failure unresponsive to therapy","Multidisciplinary care with metabolic medicine, hepatology, dietetics and ophthalmology, plus slit-lamp review for corneal deposits","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder, with family testing and newborn screening for succinylacetone"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, current edition","Scriver's The Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","Chinsky et al. Diagnosis and management of tyrosinaemia type 1: consensus guideline (Genetics in Medicine)"],"crossLinks":[]},"tyrosine_hydroxylase_deficiency":{"name":"Tyrosine hydroxylase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TH deficiency","autosomal recessive dopa-responsive dystonia","Segawa-like syndrome (recessive)","dopamine biosynthesis defect (TH)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID) / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder of dopamine synthesis caused by TH mutations, in which tyrosine hydroxylase fails to convert tyrosine to L-dopa, reducing dopamine and downstream noradrenaline/adrenaline","It is an important treatable cause of a levodopa-responsive movement disorder, and a therapeutic trial of levodopa is central to both diagnosis and management","Presentations range from a milder infantile-onset dopa-responsive dystonia/parkinsonism to a severe early-onset encephalopathy with hypokinesia, oculogyric crises, ptosis, and autonomic features","The biochemical signature in cerebrospinal fluid is low homovanillic acid (a dopamine metabolite) with a normal 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (serotonin metabolite) and normal pterins, distinguishing it from biopterin defects","Because pterin metabolism is intact, blood phenylalanine and pterin profiles are normal, contrasting with tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) disorders","Some patients, particularly the severe phenotype, respond only partially to levodopa and require careful, gradual dose titration to avoid dyskinesia and irritability"],"pathophysiology":"Tyrosine hydroxylase catalyses the rate-limiting, tetrahydrobiopterin-dependent conversion of tyrosine to L-dopa, the precursor of dopamine and, in turn, noradrenaline and adrenaline. Biallelic TH mutations reduce enzyme activity, causing a central deficiency of catecholaminergic neurotransmission. Dopaminergic hypofunction produces the extrapyramidal features (dystonia, parkinsonism, hypokinesia, oculogyric crises), while reduced noradrenergic and adrenergic signalling contributes to ptosis, autonomic instability, and, in severe cases, encephalopathy. Because the pterin cofactor pathway is unaffected, phenylalanine handling and pterin concentrations remain normal, and the CSF shows selectively low dopamine metabolites.","additionalDifferentials":["GTP cyclohydrolase I deficiency (autosomal dominant dopa-responsive/Segawa dystonia)","Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AADC) deficiency","Tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) synthesis/recycling defects (PTPS, DHPR, sepiapterin reductase)","Cerebral palsy of dystonic type","Early-onset idiopathic dystonia or juvenile parkinsonism","Neurotransmitter-related epileptic encephalopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cerebrospinal fluid neurotransmitter metabolites showing low homovanillic acid with normal 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid","CSF pterins (biopterin and neopterin) to confirm a normal pterin profile","TH gene sequencing to confirm the molecular diagnosis","Plasma phenylalanine (normal) to distinguish from BH4 disorders detected on newborn screening","Documented clinical and biochemical response to a levodopa trial","Brain MRI, usually unremarkable, to exclude structural causes of the movement disorder"],"redFlags":["Severe neonatal or infantile hypokinetic-rigid encephalopathy with autonomic instability","Recurrent oculogyric crises causing distress and feeding difficulty","Failure to consider a treatable dopa-responsive disorder in a child labelled as cerebral palsy","Marked levodopa-induced dyskinesia or behavioural disturbance during titration"],"prognosis":"The milder dopa-responsive phenotype often improves markedly and durably with levodopa and BH4-independent dopaminergic therapy, whereas the severe encephalopathic form responds less completely and may leave residual disability. Early recognition and treatment improve motor and developmental outcomes.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis by not offering a levodopa trial in an unexplained childhood movement disorder","Starting levodopa at too high a dose and provoking dyskinesia, irritability, or dystonic storms","Confusing the disorder with BH4 defects, which additionally require cofactor and sometimes serotonin-precursor therapy","Overlooking autonomic and ptosis features that reflect noradrenergic deficiency"],"management":["Levodopa combined with a peripheral dopa decarboxylase inhibitor as the mainstay, replacing the deficient dopamine that tyrosine hydroxylase can no longer synthesise","Cautious, gradual dose titration, particularly in the severe phenotype, to achieve motor benefit while avoiding dyskinesia and irritability","Adjunctive dopaminergic agents such as dopamine agonists, and anticholinergics where dystonia persists despite levodopa","Supportive management of oculogyric crises, autonomic instability and feeding difficulty in the encephalopathic form","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and developmental support to optimise motor function and milestones","Multidisciplinary follow-up through paediatric neurology and metabolic medicine","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder with family assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease (OMMBID)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"tyrosinemia":{"name":"Tyrosinemia","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hereditary tyrosinemia","tyrosinaemia type 1","hepatorenal tyrosinemia","fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase deficiency","FAH deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Type 1 (hepatorenal) is the most severe form and stems from deficiency of fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase in the tyrosine degradation pathway","Acute infantile presentation features liver failure, coagulopathy, ascites and a cabbage-like or boiled-cabbage odour","Chronic disease produces cirrhosis, renal tubular dysfunction with hypophosphataemic rickets (renal Fanconi syndrome), and a very high risk of hepatocellular carcinoma","Painful neurological crises resembling acute porphyria can occur due to accumulated succinylacetone inhibiting porphyrin synthesis","Succinylacetone in blood or urine is the key diagnostic marker and is now used in newborn screening","Type 2 causes eye and skin lesions with keratitis, and type 3 is a rare neurological form","Management is centred on nitisinone (NTBC), which blocks tyrosine degradation upstream, combined with dietary restriction of phenylalanine and tyrosine","Nitisinone therapy has transformed outcomes and reduced the need for liver transplantation, though HCC surveillance is still required"],"pathophysiology":"Hereditary tyrosinemia type 1 is an autosomal recessive defect of fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase, the final enzyme of tyrosine catabolism. Its deficiency causes accumulation of the upstream intermediates maleylacetoacetate and fumarylacetoacetate, which are converted to the toxic metabolites succinylacetone and succinylacetoacetate. These compounds are highly reactive and injure hepatocytes and renal tubular cells, driving hepatic failure, cirrhosis, malignant transformation and renal Fanconi syndrome. Succinylacetone also inhibits delta-aminolaevulinic acid dehydratase, causing porphyrin precursor accumulation and porphyria-like neurological crises. Nitisinone works by inhibiting 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase upstream, preventing formation of the toxic distal metabolites.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal hepatitis and other causes of infantile liver failure","Galactosemia and hereditary fructose intolerance","Neonatal haemochromatosis","Other causes of renal Fanconi syndrome (cystinosis, Lowe syndrome)","Transient tyrosinemia of the newborn","Acute intermittent porphyria (for the neurological crises)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine and blood succinylacetone (diagnostic hallmark)","Newborn screening incorporating succinylacetone measurement","Plasma amino acids showing elevated tyrosine (and often methionine)","Liver function tests, coagulation profile and alpha-fetoprotein (markedly raised, and a tumour marker)","Renal tubular studies for Fanconi syndrome and assessment for rickets","Liver imaging and AFP for hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance; FAH molecular testing"],"redFlags":["Acute liver failure with coagulopathy in an infant is a metabolic emergency","Porphyria-like neurological crises with pain, weakness and possible respiratory compromise","Rising or persistently elevated alpha-fetoprotein or hepatic nodules suggesting hepatocellular carcinoma","Severe electrolyte and phosphate loss from renal tubulopathy"],"prognosis":"Untreated type 1 disease historically carried a poor prognosis with death from liver failure in infancy or later hepatocellular carcinoma, but early newborn detection with nitisinone and dietary therapy markedly improves survival and reduces cancer risk; lifelong monitoring remains essential.","pitfalls":["Relying on plasma tyrosine alone, which is non-specific, rather than measuring succinylacetone","Attributing infantile liver failure to infection or sepsis and missing the treatable metabolic cause","Assuming nitisinone eliminates hepatocellular carcinoma risk and stopping surveillance","Neglecting phenylalanine and tyrosine dietary control while on nitisinone, risking corneal and skin complications"],"management":["Nitisinone as disease-specific therapy, inhibiting 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase to prevent accumulation of the hepatotoxic and nephrotoxic metabolite succinylacetone","Lifelong tyrosine- and phenylalanine-restricted diet with a specialist metabolic formula, which must be maintained alongside nitisinone to prevent iatrogenic tyrosinaemia","Biochemical monitoring of blood and urine succinylacetone and plasma amino acids to confirm adequate metabolic control","Hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance with serial alpha-fetoprotein and liver imaging, alongside monitoring of synthetic function and coagulation","Supportive care of the renal Fanconi syndrome with electrolyte, phosphate and vitamin D replacement for hypophosphataemic rickets, and management of acute porphyria-like crises","Liver transplantation reserved for hepatocellular carcinoma or irreversible liver failure","Multidisciplinary management with metabolic medicine, hepatology and dietetics, and genetic counselling with family screening for this autosomal recessive condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"udd_myopathy":{"name":"Udd distal myopathy / tibial muscular dystrophy (titin)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tibial muscular dystrophy","TMD","Udd distal myopathy","titinopathy","TTN-related distal myopathy","Finnish distal myopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant late-onset distal myopathy caused by variants in the C-terminal (M-line) region of the titin gene (TTN), with a well-described Finnish founder mutation","Onset is usually after the age of 35-40 years, with slowly progressive weakness of the anterior lower-leg muscles, particularly the tibialis anterior, causing foot drop","Weakness and atrophy are often relatively selective for the tibialis anterior on imaging, with the deep posterior and other compartments spared until later","Heterozygotes typically have a mild distal phenotype and retain ambulation, whereas homozygous or compound heterozygous TTN variants cause a severe limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (LGMD R10, formerly LGMD2J)","Creatine kinase is normal or mildly elevated and electromyography confirms a myopathic process","Muscle biopsy shows dystrophic or myopathic changes, often with rimmed vacuoles","Muscle MRI demonstrating selective anterior tibial involvement is a useful diagnostic pointer","Diagnosis rests on the anterior-compartment distal weakness pattern together with TTN genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Udd myopathy results from dominant variants affecting the C-terminal M-line region of titin, the giant sarcomeric protein that spans from the Z-disc to the M-line and provides passive elasticity and a scaffold for sarcomere assembly and signalling. Mutations disrupt the interaction of titin with M-line binding partners such as calpain-3, impairing sarcomeric integrity and maintenance. The anterior lower-leg muscles are affected earliest, producing distal weakness and foot drop, while a double dose of TTN variants causes more widespread muscle breakdown and a severe limb-girdle phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Laing distal myopathy and other anterior-compartment distal myopathies","Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease and peroneal neuropathy causing foot drop","Inclusion body myositis","Myotonic dystrophy","L5 radiculopathy or common peroneal nerve palsy","Distal spinal muscular atrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to distinguish myopathy from neuropathy","Muscle MRI showing selective tibialis anterior involvement","Muscle biopsy for dystrophic changes and rimmed vacuoles","Targeted TTN M-line sequencing or a comprehensive distal myopathy/LGMD panel","Family evaluation given dominant inheritance and the severe recessive limb-girdle phenotype"],"redFlags":["Severe early-onset generalised weakness suggesting a homozygous limb-girdle titinopathy","Cardiac symptoms, since some TTN variants are associated with cardiomyopathy","Respiratory muscle weakness with declining vital capacity","Rapid progression atypical for the classic heterozygous phenotype"],"prognosis":"The heterozygous tibial muscular dystrophy phenotype progresses slowly, and most patients retain independent walking with a normal lifespan; homozygous or compound heterozygous disease produces a much more severe, disabling limb-girdle muscular dystrophy.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the foot drop for a peroneal nerve palsy or radiculopathy","Failing to consider a severe recessive titinopathy in relatives when counselling families","Assuming normal creatine kinase excludes myopathy","Overlooking possible cardiac involvement associated with certain titin variants"],"management":["No disease-specific pharmacotherapy exists, so care centres on physiotherapy and a stretching programme to preserve ankle range and function against the tibialis anterior weakness","Ankle-foot orthoses to correct foot drop and improve gait stability and safety","Occupational therapy and mobility aids to maintain independence as distal weakness progresses","Respiratory and cardiac assessment, particularly in the severe homozygous or compound heterozygous limb-girdle phenotype where more widespread muscle involvement occurs","Multidisciplinary follow-up through a neuromuscular clinic with periodic reassessment of functional status","Genetic counselling and family evaluation given autosomal dominant inheritance and the severe recessive limb-girdle phenotype in offspring of two carriers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: Udd distal myopathy / titinopathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"uhl_anomaly":{"name":"Uhl anomaly","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["parchment right ventricle","parchment heart","congenital absence of right ventricular myocardium"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease / congenital cardiomyopathy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare congenital disorder marked by near-total absence of the right ventricular myocardium","The right ventricular free wall is reduced to apposed layers of endocardium and epicardium, giving a paper-thin parchment appearance","Presents with right heart failure, often in infancy or childhood, and can cause arrhythmia and sudden death","The thin-walled right ventricle dilates and fails to contract, producing markedly impaired right ventricular function","Must be distinguished from arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy, in which myocardium is replaced by fibrofatty tissue","Imaging shows an aneurysmal, akinetic, extremely thin right ventricular free wall with preserved left ventricle early on","Management is supportive for heart failure and arrhythmia, with transplantation considered in end-stage disease"],"pathophysiology":"Uhl anomaly is a congenital cardiomyopathy in which the right ventricular myocardium is almost entirely absent, so the free wall consists of directly apposed endocardium and epicardium with little or no intervening muscle. This is thought to arise from an excessive developmental loss of right ventricular myocytes, in contrast to the progressive fibrofatty myocardial replacement of arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy. The resulting parchment-thin wall cannot generate contractile force, leading to a dilated, akinetic right ventricle, progressive right heart failure, and a substrate for ventricular arrhythmia.","additionalDifferentials":["Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy","Ebstein anomaly of the tricuspid valve","Right ventricular infarction","Idiopathic dilatation of the right atrium and ventricle","Severe pulmonary hypertension with right ventricular failure","Congenital tricuspid regurgitation from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography demonstrating a dilated, thin-walled, akinetic right ventricle","Cardiac MRI to characterise wall thinning and distinguish it from fibrofatty replacement","ECG and ambulatory monitoring to detect right-sided conduction and arrhythmia","Assessment of right ventricular function and heart-failure severity","Family and genetic evaluation to help separate it from arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy"],"redFlags":["Progressive right heart failure in infancy or childhood","Ventricular arrhythmia or syncope","Signs suggesting risk of sudden cardiac death","Severe right ventricular dilatation with haemodynamic compromise"],"prognosis":"The outlook is generally poor, with many patients developing progressive right heart failure and arrhythmia; survival varies with severity, and advanced disease may ultimately require cardiac transplantation.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy, which has different genetics and management","Overlooking the arrhythmic and sudden-death risk while focusing on heart failure","Misattributing right heart failure to pulmonary or valvular causes","Underestimating disease severity from a preserved left ventricle early in the course"],"management":["Heart failure therapy directed at the failing, dilated right ventricle, using diuretics for congestion and standard neurohormonal medical therapy","Antiarrhythmic management and rhythm surveillance for the ventricular arrhythmias arising from the thin, akinetic free wall","Anticoagulation to reduce thromboembolism from the dilated, poorly contractile right ventricle","Consideration of an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator for those at high risk of sudden cardiac death","Serial imaging with echocardiography and cardiac MRI to monitor right ventricular function and heart-failure progression","Cardiac transplantation as the definitive option for progressive, refractory right heart failure","Genetic and family evaluation to distinguish the condition from arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy, with specialist cardiology and cardiac surgery input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease","Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children, and Adolescents"],"crossLinks":[]},"ullrich_congenital_muscular_dystrophy":{"name":"Ullrich congenital muscular dystrophy","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UCMD","COL6A1","COL6A2","COL6A3","collagen VI-related muscular dystrophy, severe","Ullrich scleroatonic muscular dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The severe end of the collagen VI-related spectrum, of which Bethlem myopathy is the milder counterpart, usually presenting at birth or in early infancy","Neonates show marked hypotonia and weakness together with the striking combination of proximal joint contractures (hips, knees, elbows) and hyperlaxity of the distal joints (wrists, fingers, ankles)","Congenital features may include torticollis, kyphoscoliosis, hip dislocation and prominent calcanei","Motor milestones are delayed and many children achieve, but may later lose, independent walking","Caused by mutations in the collagen VI genes COL6A1, COL6A2 and COL6A3, with both recessive and dominant (often de novo) inheritance recognised","Progressive respiratory failure from diaphragmatic and chest-wall weakness is a leading concern and often necessitates non-invasive nocturnal ventilatory support","Soft, velvety palms and follicular hyperkeratosis with abnormal scarring reflect the underlying connective-tissue defect"],"pathophysiology":"Ullrich congenital muscular dystrophy is caused by mutations in the collagen VI genes COL6A1, COL6A2 and COL6A3, which more severely disrupt the assembly or function of the collagen VI microfibrillar network than the milder variants causing Bethlem myopathy. Collagen VI links the muscle-fibre basement membrane to the surrounding interstitial matrix, and its severe deficiency impairs the structural and signalling relationship between fibres and their environment, with dysregulated mitochondrial function and increased apoptosis contributing to early and profound muscle degeneration. Because collagen VI is widespread in connective tissue, the disorder also produces the characteristic joint contractures and hyperlaxity, skeletal deformity and skin abnormalities, and the early respiratory failure that dominates its natural history.","additionalDifferentials":["Bethlem myopathy","Other congenital muscular dystrophies including merosin-deficient forms","Congenital myopathies such as core and nemaline myopathies","Spinal muscular atrophy","Congenital myasthenic syndromes","Distal arthrogryposis and syndromic contracture disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase, usually normal or only mildly elevated","Muscle biopsy with collagen VI immunohistochemistry, often supplemented by fibroblast culture studies","Targeted genetic testing of COL6A1, COL6A2 and COL6A3","Muscle magnetic resonance imaging showing the characteristic pattern of muscle involvement","Respiratory function tests and overnight monitoring for hypoventilation from early in the disease","Spinal and hip imaging to assess scoliosis and dislocation"],"redFlags":["Progressive respiratory failure or nocturnal hypoventilation, which may occur even before loss of walking","Rapidly progressive scoliosis compromising respiratory function","Feeding difficulty and failure to thrive in infancy","Loss of ambulation requiring orthopaedic and rehabilitation planning"],"prognosis":"Ullrich congenital muscular dystrophy is more severe than Bethlem myopathy, with many affected children losing ambulation in the first or second decade and developing progressive respiratory insufficiency that determines survival, although proactive respiratory and orthopaedic care can substantially prolong and improve life.","pitfalls":["Delaying respiratory surveillance because motor function seems relatively preserved","Overlooking the diagnostic combination of proximal contractures with distal hyperlaxity in a hypotonic infant","Assuming normal collagen VI immunostaining excludes the diagnosis, as staining can be only partially reduced","Not recognising that dominant de novo mutations can cause the disorder in a child with no family history"],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy exists for this collagen VI deficiency, so care is anticipatory and supportive around respiratory, orthopaedic and functional needs","Proactive respiratory management with non-invasive nocturnal ventilation for hypoventilation and airway-clearance techniques, given progressive diaphragmatic and chest-wall weakness","Physiotherapy with stretching, serial casting and orthoses to manage proximal contractures while preserving distal hyperlaxity","Orthopaedic surveillance and intervention for kyphoscoliosis and hip dislocation","Regular monitoring of respiratory function and overnight oximetry from early in the disease, with nutritional and feeding support","Multidisciplinary care through neuromuscular, respiratory, orthopaedic, physiotherapy and occupational therapy teams to maintain ambulation as long as possible","Genetic counselling given COL6A1, COL6A2 and COL6A3 mutations with both dominant and recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: Collagen VI-Related Dystrophies"],"crossLinks":[]},"umbilical_cord_prolapse":{"name":"Umbilical cord prolapse","system":"Reproductive / Obstetrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cord prolapse","funic prolapse"],"chapter":null,"source":"RCOG Green-top Guideline / Williams Obstetrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Cord prolapse is descent of the umbilical cord below the presenting part once the membranes have ruptured (overt prolapse); a cord lying beside the presenting part is occult prolapse, and a cord below the presenting part with intact membranes is a cord presentation","It is an acute obstetric emergency because cord compression, together with vasospasm from cooling and handling, interrupts fetal oxygenation and produces rapid fetal hypoxia and bradycardia","Risk factors reflect a poorly applied presenting part: malpresentation such as breech or transverse lie, prematurity, polyhydramnios, a high or unengaged presenting part, multiparity, multiple pregnancy, and a long cord","Iatrogenic cases follow artificial rupture of membranes with a high presenting part, amnioinfusion, external cephalic version, or other intrauterine manipulation","Diagnosis is by seeing or palpating the cord in the vagina or at the introitus, or by an abrupt fetal heart rate abnormality such as variable decelerations or bradycardia soon after membrane rupture","Immediate measures relieve cord compression: elevate the presenting part manually or by filling the bladder, position the woman knee-chest or head-down in the left lateral posture, minimise handling of the cord, and consider tocolysis","Definitive management is expedited delivery, most often by emergency caesarean section, unless vaginal birth is imminent and would be quicker","Outcome depends chiefly on the interval from diagnosis to delivery, so regular drills, early recognition, and rapid transfer are central to safe management"],"pathophysiology":"The umbilical cord supplies the fetus with oxygenated blood, and vessel patency depends on the cord not being compressed. A presenting part that does not fill the pelvic inlet, as in malpresentation, prematurity, an unengaged head, or polyhydramnios with sudden decompression at membrane rupture, leaves a space through which the cord can slip below or beside the fetus. Once the cord lies below the presenting part with ruptured membranes, descent of the fetus and uterine contractions compress the cord against the bony pelvis and occlude the umbilical vessels. In addition, exposure of the cord to the cooler vaginal environment and physical handling provoke reflex vasospasm. The combination of mechanical occlusion and vasospasm reduces or abolishes umbilical blood flow, causing fetal hypoxaemia, bradycardia, and, if unrelieved, hypoxic-ischaemic injury or death.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of acute fetal bradycardia such as placental abruption, uterine rupture, or maternal hypotension","Cord presentation with intact membranes rather than frank prolapse","Vasa praevia with vessel rupture","Prolonged deceleration from rapid cervical dilatation or uterine hyperstimulation","Maternal supine hypotension reducing uteroplacental flow","Prolapse of another structure mistaken for cord at examination"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Immediate vaginal examination to confirm the cord below or alongside the presenting part and to assess cervical dilatation","Palpation of the cord for pulsation as a bedside marker of fetal viability","Continuous cardiotocography or fetal heart rate monitoring","Bedside ultrasound to confirm presentation and fetal viability if immediately available","Maternal observations of pulse and blood pressure to exclude contributory hypotension","Urgent preoperative bloods including full blood count and group-and-save or crossmatch","Umbilical cord arterial blood gas at delivery to document the degree of acidaemia"],"redFlags":["Fetal bradycardia or deep variable decelerations following rupture of the membranes","Visible or palpable cord at or beyond the introitus","Loss of cord pulsation","A prolonged diagnosis-to-delivery interval","Cord prolapse occurring remote from theatre or skilled help"],"prognosis":"When recognised promptly and followed by rapid delivery, most babies do well. Delay in relieving cord compression is associated with hypoxic-ischaemic injury and perinatal death, and outcomes are worse when prolapse occurs outside hospital.","pitfalls":["Performing artificial rupture of membranes with a high or ill-applied presenting part, precipitating prolapse","Attempting to replace or excessively handle the cord, which aggravates vasospasm","Failing to maintain continuous elevation of the presenting part until delivery","Delaying delivery to complete investigations or transfer without maintaining decompression measures","Overlooking a cord presentation on examination in a woman with risk factors before the membranes rupture"],"management":["Immediate relief of cord compression by manually elevating the presenting part or filling the bladder, and minimising handling of the cord to avoid vasospasm","Positioning the woman knee-chest or head-down in the left lateral position to reduce pressure on the cord","Tocolysis to abolish uterine contractions and improve foetal oxygenation while preparing for delivery","Continuous cardiotocographic foetal heart rate monitoring, with cord pulsation used as a bedside marker of viability","Urgent delivery, usually by category-one caesarean section, or assisted vaginal birth if the cervix is fully dilated and delivery is imminent","Activation of the obstetric emergency response with neonatal resuscitation team present at delivery and anaesthetic support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["RCOG Green-top Guideline on umbilical cord prolapse","Williams Obstetrics","Oxford Handbook of Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"umbilical_hernia":{"name":"Umbilical hernia","system":"GI / Abdominal wall / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["paraumbilical hernia","supraumbilical hernia","congenital umbilical hernia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["True umbilical hernias in infants protrude through the umbilical ring and most close spontaneously within the first few years of life","In adults the defect is usually paraumbilical, occurring through the linea alba just above or below the umbilicus, and does not resolve without surgery","Adult paraumbilical hernias are more frequent in women, and predisposing factors include obesity, multiparity, ascites, and chronically raised intra-abdominal pressure","The neck is often narrow relative to the sac, so incarceration and strangulation are relatively common, particularly with a fibrous fatty omental content","Contents may include omentum, small bowel, or transverse colon, and long-standing hernias can become multiloculated","Elective repair (suture repair for small defects, mesh for larger ones) is advised for symptomatic or enlarging adult hernias to avoid emergency complications","In children, repair is usually deferred and reserved for defects that persist beyond a few years, are large, or become complicated","Skin changes over a large tense hernia, such as thinning or ulceration, warrant urgent assessment"],"pathophysiology":"Umbilical and paraumbilical hernias arise from a defect at or adjacent to the umbilical cicatrix. In infants the umbilical ring fails to close fully after cord separation. In adults, weakening and separation of the linea alba fibres around the umbilicus, combined with sustained raised intra-abdominal pressure from obesity, pregnancy, or ascites, allows protrusion of preperitoneal fat, omentum, or bowel through a paraumbilical defect. The characteristically narrow, fibrous neck restricts reduction and readily compromises the vascular supply of trapped contents, predisposing to incarceration and strangulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Epigastric hernia","Incisional hernia near the umbilicus","Divarication of the recti","Umbilical granuloma or omphalitis in infants","Metastatic umbilical nodule (Sister Mary Joseph nodule)","Lipoma or sebaceous cyst of the umbilicus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination for reducibility, cough impulse, and defect size","Ultrasound to confirm the defect and characterise contents when uncertain","CT in large, recurrent, or complex hernias and to assess in patients with ascites","Assessment for underlying causes of raised intra-abdominal pressure such as ascites"],"redFlags":["Irreducible, tense, tender umbilical swelling indicating incarceration or strangulation (surgical emergency)","Features of bowel obstruction with an irreducible hernia","Overlying skin ischaemia, ulceration, or impending rupture over a large hernia","Systemic sepsis or peritonism suggesting strangulated bowel"],"prognosis":"Most infantile umbilical hernias close spontaneously. Adult paraumbilical hernias do not resolve and carry a meaningful risk of incarceration and strangulation; elective mesh repair gives good results, whereas emergency surgery, especially in obese or comorbid patients, is associated with higher complication rates.","pitfalls":["Assuming an adult paraumbilical hernia will behave like a self-resolving infantile hernia","Underestimating strangulation risk because of the narrow neck","Overlooking uncontrolled ascites, which increases recurrence and complicates repair","Missing a Sister Mary Joseph nodule by attributing all umbilical lumps to hernia"],"management":["Conservative observation of infantile umbilical hernias, most of which close spontaneously through the umbilical ring in the first few years","Elective surgical repair for adult paraumbilical hernias, which do not resolve, using suture repair for small defects and mesh for larger ones","Addressing contributory causes of raised intra-abdominal pressure such as obesity and ascites to reduce recurrence risk","Emergency surgical repair for incarceration or strangulation, given the narrow neck and risk of compromise to trapped omentum or bowel","Prompt escalation and surgical referral for an irreducible, painful or tender hernia suggesting strangulation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"uncommon_disseminated_fungal_infections":{"name":"Uncommon Disseminated Fungal Infections","system":"Multisystem (Infectious Diseases / Mycoses)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Paracoccidioidomycosis","Talaromycosis","Penicilliosis","Emergomycosis","Disseminated emmonsiosis","Adiaspiromycosis","Phaeohyphomycosis","Fusariosis","Scedosporiosis","Lomentosporiosis","Trichosporonosis"],"chapter":226,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1725","clinicalPearls":["This group spans endemic dimorphic fungi (paracoccidioidomycosis, talaromycosis, emergomycosis, adiaspiromycosis), hyaline and dematiaceous molds (fusariosis, scedosporiosis/lomentosporiosis, phaeohyphomycoses), and the yeast-like Trichosporon; the more common invasive mycoses (histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis, blastomycosis, sporotrichosis, candidiasis, cryptococcosis, aspergillosis, mucormycosis) are covered separately (Harrison 22e p.1725).","Dimorphic fungi exist as conidia-producing molds in the environment (the infectious form) and convert to yeast (or spherules/adiaspores) at temperatures above about 35 degrees C in tissue; infection generally begins with inhalation of conidia (Harrison 22e p.1725).","Paracoccidioidomycosis (Paracoccidioides brasiliensis complex, P. lutzii) is acquired by inhalation in humid Central/South American forests, with highest incidence in Brazil; chronic disease shows a striking male predominance (reported male-to-female ratios from 14:1 up to ~70:1) (Harrison 22e p.1726).","The acute/juvenile form of paracoccidioidomycosis is uncommon, occurs typically in those under 30, primarily disseminates through the reticuloendothelial system, and often shows peripheral eosinophilia; the chronic/adult form accounts for ~90% of cases (Harrison 22e p.1726).","On tissue, Paracoccidioides yeast classically appears as a central yeast ringed by narrow-necked daughter buds (the 'mariner's wheel'); some commercial serologic tests may fail to detect P. lutzii (Harrison 22e p.1726).","Talaromycosis (Talaromyces marneffei, formerly Penicillium marneffei) is endemic in Vietnam, Thailand, and other Southeast Asian countries, is epidemiologically linked to bamboo rats, and occurs mostly in advanced AIDS; its presentation mimics disseminated histoplasmosis with umbilicated molluscum-like skin papules (Harrison 22e p.1727).","In culture T. marneffei produces a distinctive diffusible red pigment, and tissue/blood smears show yeast-like organisms reproducing by bipolar fission (two ellipses with a central septum) (Harrison 22e p.1727).","Emergomycosis (Emergomyces, several formerly Emmonsia) is an emerging dimorphic-fungus infection of patients with impaired cellular immunity; cutaneous involvement (disseminated ulcers, papules, nodules) accompanies pulmonary disease in most patients, while CNS spread is rare (Harrison 22e p.1727).","Fusariosis is angioinvasive and mimics aspergillosis, but is distinguished by very common, often painful, nodular or necrotic skin lesions appearing around the onset of fever and by frequently positive blood cultures (~50%); banana-shaped macroconidia are characteristic on culture (Harrison 22e p.1728).","Phaeohyphomycoses are caused by melanin-producing (dematiaceous/brown-black) molds; melanin is a virulence factor, certain species (Cladophialophora bantiana, Rhinocladiella mackenziei) are neurotropic, and golden-brown hyphae are highlighted by the Fontana-Masson melanin stain (Harrison 22e p.1728).","Trichosporonosis (most often Trichosporon asahii) resembles invasive candidiasis with fungemia, but the presence of arthroconidia helps distinguish it from candidemia, and the serum cryptococcal antigen latex agglutination test can be falsely positive because T. asahii shares polysaccharide antigens with Cryptococcus neoformans (Harrison 22e p.1729).","Scedosporiosis (Scedosporium apiospermum complex) and lomentosporiosis (Lomentospora prolificans) mimic aspergillosis radiographically and histologically; L. prolificans can grow in blood cultures whereas S. apiospermum usually does not (Harrison 22e p.1729)."],"pathophysiology":"Most of these fungi enter through inhalation of environmental conidia, with a smaller number introduced by traumatic skin or corneal inoculation or by injection of contaminated material. Dimorphic fungi (Paracoccidioides, Talaromyces, Emergomyces, Emmonsia crescens) convert from mold to invasive yeast/adiaspore form under thermal pressure in the lungs and then disseminate hematogenously to the reticuloendothelial system and other organs; granulomatous inflammation is typical. Disease frequently reflects host immune deficits: inborn errors affecting the IL-12/IFN-gamma axis predispose to severe paracoccidioidomycosis and talaromycosis, and IFN-gamma-primed monocytes/macrophages and neutrophils control Talaromyces. Molds such as Fusarium, Scedosporium, and Lomentospora are angioinvasive, and protection against Fusarium and Trichosporon depends heavily on neutrophils and their oxidative burst, explaining the strong link with profound prolonged neutropenia and with chronic granulomatous disease (defective NADPH oxidase). Dematiaceous molds use melanin as a virulence factor; defense against disseminated phaeohyphomycosis involves Dectin-1 (CLEC7A) and the adaptor CARD9, and deficiencies in this pathway predispose to disseminated and brain infection even in otherwise immunocompetent hosts. In adiaspiromycosis, inhaled E. crescens conidia enlarge into nonreplicating thick-walled adiaspores (up to ~500 micrometers) that drive a granulomatous inflammatory response.","additionalDifferentials":["Disseminated histoplasmosis (talaromycosis and emergomycosis mimic it clinically and the Histoplasma antigen test cross-reacts with Emergomyces)","Invasive aspergillosis (fusariosis, scedosporiosis, and lomentosporiosis mimic it clinically, radiographically, and histologically)","Invasive candidiasis / candidemia (resembled by trichosporonosis)","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis and squamous cell carcinoma (mimic the mucocutaneous lesions of chronic paracoccidioidomycosis)","Molluscum contagiosum (umbilicated skin papules of talaromycosis)"],"infectionMimics":["Disseminated histoplasmosis","Invasive aspergillosis","Invasive candidiasis","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis","Molluscum contagiosum","Cryptococcosis (cross-reacting cryptococcal antigen in trichosporonosis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Squamous cell carcinoma (mucocutaneous lesions of paracoccidioidomycosis; rarely arises in chronic chromoblastomycosis)","Necrotic/embolic skin lesions of other causes (mimicked by painful necrotic lesions of fusariosis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Culture from blood and/or tissue is central; identification to mold phase is often required because yeast/hyphal tissue forms can be misidentified (e.g., Emergomyces resembling Histoplasma; Fusarium/Scedosporium resembling Aspergillus)","Histopathology with special stains, including Fontana-Masson for melanized (dematiaceous) molds","Blood cultures (positive in up to ~50% of disseminated fusariosis and can grow Lomentospora prolificans; S. apiospermum usually negative)","Serology for Paracoccidioides (with the caveat that some assays miss P. lutzii)","PCR-based molecular identification (including 28S rDNA and rRNA ITS) through fungal reference laboratories, useful in culture-negative or difficult cases","Awareness of cross-reactivity: Histoplasma antigen test cross-reacts with Emergomyces; cryptococcal antigen can be positive in Trichosporon asahii infection","Species-level identification and susceptibility testing in a reference laboratory to guide management of resistant molds"],"redFlags":["Persistent fever in a profoundly neutropenic patient despite broad-spectrum antibiotics (suggests angioinvasive mold such as Fusarium or Scedosporium/Lomentospora)","New painful nodular or necrotic skin lesions in a neutropenic or leukemic patient, especially appearing with the onset of fever (fusariosis)","Positive fungal blood culture in an immunocompromised host (disseminated fusariosis or Lomentospora)","Disseminated cutaneous ulcers/papules/nodules with pulmonary disease in an HIV/AIDS or transplant patient (emergomycosis, talaromycosis)","Indolent brain abscess with golden-brown pigmented hyphae, or intraventricular CNS spread (neurotropic dematiaceous molds such as Cladophialophora bantiana)","CNS involvement in any of these disseminated infections, which carries notably high mortality"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely by organism and host immune status. Paracoccidioidomycosis generally responds well, though pulmonary fibrosis can progress in chronic disease. Untreated disseminated talaromycosis is usually fatal, but treated mortality is about 10%. Disseminated fusariosis carries high mortality (historically up to ~85%, now around 50% with better therapy) and approaches 100% if neutropenia persists; survival hinges on neutrophil recovery. Invasive S. apiospermum infection has roughly 50% mortality, whereas L. prolificans remains about 85-100% lethal. Disseminated trichosporonosis mortality has been as high as 70% and remains very high with persistent neutropenia. Disseminated and visceral phaeohyphomycoses, particularly with CNS involvement, have high mortality.","pitfalls":["Misidentifying tissue forms: Emergomyces can be mistaken for Histoplasma, and Fusarium/Scedosporium/Lomentospora are difficult to distinguish from Aspergillus on histology, so culture identification is essential","Cross-reacting antigen tests can mislead: Histoplasma antigen cross-reacts with Emergomyces, and cryptococcal antigen may be positive in Trichosporon asahii infection","Assuming AmB activity is universal: many of these molds (Scedosporium, Lomentospora) are intrinsically resistant to amphotericin B, and Fusarium MICs are relatively high","Treating a respiratory mold isolate as disease without evidence of tissue invasion, since Scedosporium/Lomentospora may merely colonize the airway (e.g., in bronchiectasis)","In vitro susceptibility of dematiaceous molds may not reflect response because muriform/tissue forms can behave differently from hyphal forms","Overlooking Fusarium onychomycosis as a portal for later disseminated disease in patients heading into neutropenia or transplantation","Missing the diagnosis in 'immunocompetent' patients with disseminated/brain phaeohyphomycosis who may carry CLEC7A/CARD9 pathway defects"],"management":["Organism-directed antifungal therapy, typically liposomal amphotericin B for severe disseminated dimorphic-fungal disease followed by an azole such as itraconazole, and voriconazole for fusariosis and scedosporiosis","Recognition that Lomentospora prolificans is frequently multidrug-resistant, often requiring combination antifungal therapy and expert guidance","Restoration of host immunity, including antiretroviral therapy for talaromycosis in advanced AIDS and reversal of neutropenia with growth factors where relevant","Surgical debridement or source control for localised angioinvasive mould disease","Secondary antifungal prophylaxis in patients with ongoing immunosuppression to prevent relapse","Monitoring of cultures, antigen assays and clinical response, with awareness of cross-reactivity in serological tests","Specialist infectious diseases and mycology reference laboratory involvement to confirm species and tailor therapy"],"severityClassification":"No single formal staging applies across this heterogeneous group. Practically, infections are stratified by extent and host status: localized cutaneous/keratitis/onychomycosis or pulmonary-limited disease versus disseminated/angioinvasive infection; presence of CNS involvement marks the most severe end (associated with the highest mortality). For dimorphic fungi (e.g., paracoccidioidomycosis, talaromycosis, emergomycosis), disease is broadly described as mild/moderate versus severe, and as acute (juvenile) versus chronic (adult) forms for paracoccidioidomycosis.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1725","Harrison 22e p.1726","Harrison 22e p.1727","Harrison 22e p.1728","Harrison 22e p.1729","Harrison 22e p.1730"],"crossLinks":[]},"undescended_testis":{"name":"Undescended Testis","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["undescended testis","undescended testicle","cryptorchidism","maldescended testis","cryptorchid testis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Undescended testis (cryptorchidism) is failure of one or both testes to reach the scrotum, and is among the commonest congenital genitourinary anomalies in boys, more frequent in preterm infants","Many testes that are undescended at birth descend spontaneously within the first few months, so definitive management is guided by examination after this early window","It is important to distinguish a true undescended testis from a retractile testis, which can be manipulated into the scrotum and stays there, and requires observation rather than surgery","A truly impalpable testis raises the possibility of an intra-abdominal, absent, or atrophic (vanished) testis and warrants surgical evaluation","Bilateral non-palpable testes, especially with hypospadias or other genital ambiguity, should prompt urgent evaluation for a disorder of sex development","Imaging is unreliable for locating an impalpable testis, and laparoscopy is the definitive means of identifying and managing it","Orchidopexy, ideally performed in early childhood, brings the testis into the scrotum to optimise fertility potential, allow examination, and reduce though not eliminate cancer risk","Cryptorchidism is associated with reduced fertility and an increased lifetime risk of testicular germ cell tumour, in both the affected and, to a lesser degree, the contralateral testis","An acutely painful, swollen groin or scrotum in a boy with an undescended testis should raise concern for torsion of that testis"],"pathophysiology":"Testicular descent occurs in two phases governed by hormonal and mechanical factors: a transabdominal phase dependent on insulin-like factor 3 and the gubernaculum, and an inguinoscrotal phase dependent on androgens and the genitofemoral nerve. Disruption of these hormonal signals, gubernacular development, or the mechanical pathway leaves the testis arrested anywhere along its normal route from the abdomen to the scrotum, or in an ectopic position. The undescended testis is exposed to a higher-than-scrotal temperature, which impairs germ cell maturation over time, accounting for reduced fertility, and it carries intrinsic and environmental factors that raise malignancy risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Retractile testis","Ectopic testis (outside the normal line of descent)","Absent or atrophic (vanished) testis","Ascending (acquired) undescended testis","Disorder of sex development in bilateral impalpable cases","Inguinal hernia presenting as a groin swelling"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful repeated clinical examination in a warm, relaxed setting to determine palpability and position","Laparoscopy as the definitive investigation for an impalpable testis","Hormonal evaluation (including gonadotrophins and testosterone-axis testing) in bilateral impalpable cases","Karyotype and endocrine work-up where a disorder of sex development is suspected","Ultrasound has limited value and should not delay surgical referral for an impalpable testis"],"redFlags":["Bilateral impalpable testes, particularly with hypospadias, suggesting a disorder of sex development requiring urgent assessment","Acute pain and swelling in an undescended or inguinal testis suggesting torsion","A previously descended testis that has ascended (acquired cryptorchidism)","A firm or enlarging testicular mass raising concern for malignancy in later life","Associated genital ambiguity or endocrine abnormality"],"prognosis":"Timely orchidopexy in early childhood improves fertility outcomes, particularly in unilateral cases, and facilitates self-examination and earlier tumour detection. Bilateral disease carries greater fertility risk. The increased risk of testicular cancer persists into adulthood despite surgery, so lifelong awareness and self-examination are advised. Retractile testes generally have a benign course but need follow-up because a minority ascend.","pitfalls":["Confusing a retractile testis with a true undescended testis and operating unnecessarily","Relying on ultrasound to locate an impalpable testis and delaying definitive laparoscopy","Missing a disorder of sex development in bilateral impalpable testes with genital anomalies","Deferring orchidopexy beyond the optimal early window, compromising fertility potential","Reassuring families that surgery abolishes the future cancer risk"],"management":["Orchidopexy in early childhood as the definitive treatment, surgically mobilising and fixing the testis in the scrotum to improve fertility potential and enable examination","A period of watchful waiting in the first few months for spontaneous descent, and observation rather than surgery for a retractile testis","Diagnostic laparoscopy for the truly impalpable testis to locate an intra-abdominal, atrophic or absent gonad and guide operative management","Long-term monitoring of fertility and instruction in testicular self-examination, since the risk of testicular malignancy persists into adulthood despite surgery","Urgent evaluation for a disorder of sex development when there are bilateral non-palpable testes, especially with hypospadias or genital ambiguity","Karyotype, endocrine work-up and genetic counselling where a disorder of sex development is confirmed, with paediatric urology referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"undifferentiated_connective_tissue_disease":{"name":"Undifferentiated connective tissue disease","system":"Rheumatology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UCTD","undifferentiated CTD","early undifferentiated connective tissue disease","latent autoimmune connective tissue disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology / international connective tissue disease guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Features of systemic autoimmunity are present but do not fulfil criteria for any single defined connective tissue disease","Common manifestations include Raynaud phenomenon, arthralgia or arthritis, sicca symptoms, and a positive antinuclear antibody","Serology is often limited, with antinuclear antibody positivity but absence of highly specific antibodies","Most patients follow a mild, stable course, though a minority progress to a defined disease such as lupus or systemic sclerosis","Evolving or newly positive specific autoantibodies during follow-up can herald differentiation into a definite disorder","Major internal organ involvement is uncommon and its presence should prompt reconsideration of the label","Periodic clinical and serological review helps detect progression early"],"pathophysiology":"Undifferentiated connective tissue disease represents an early or restricted state of systemic autoimmunity in which immune tolerance is broken but the process has not organised into a recognised disease phenotype. Antinuclear antibody production and low-grade immune activation produce non-specific inflammatory features without the characteristic autoantibody specificities or organ patterns of established disorders. In some individuals the autoimmune response matures over time, with the appearance of disease-specific antibodies and organ involvement marking differentiation into a defined connective tissue disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Sjogren syndrome","Systemic sclerosis (including early or sine scleroderma forms)","Mixed connective tissue disease","Rheumatoid arthritis","Fibromyalgia with incidental antinuclear antibody positivity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antinuclear antibody with extractable nuclear antigen and specific autoantibody panel","Complement levels and inflammatory markers","Full blood count and renal function to screen for organ involvement","Nailfold capillaroscopy in the presence of Raynaud","Targeted imaging or organ testing only when symptoms suggest specific involvement","Serial reassessment of serology and clinical features over time"],"redFlags":["Emergence of significant renal, pulmonary, or neurological involvement","New disease-specific autoantibodies suggesting differentiation","Rapidly progressive multisystem features","Falling complement with clinical deterioration"],"prognosis":"The majority of patients remain undifferentiated with a mild, stable course over many years, and some achieve remission. A minority evolve into a defined connective tissue disease, so prognosis is largely determined by whether and how the illness differentiates.","pitfalls":["Prematurely labelling patients with a definite disease before criteria are met","Discharging patients without follow-up and missing later differentiation","Attributing significant organ disease to undifferentiated disease rather than reclassifying","Over-treating mild, stable disease with aggressive immunosuppression"],"management":["Hydroxychloroquine as a disease-modifying anchor for arthralgia, arthritis and constitutional features, with NSAIDs for symptomatic relief","Short courses of low-dose corticosteroids for symptom flares, escalating immunosuppression only if organ involvement or differentiation emerges","Management of Raynaud phenomenon with cold avoidance and a calcium channel blocker such as nifedipine","Symptomatic care of sicca features with ocular lubricants and saliva substitutes","Serial reassessment of clinical features and autoantibody serology to detect evolution into a defined connective tissue disease","Reconsideration of the diagnosis and specialist rheumatology escalation if major internal organ involvement develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology","International cohort studies of undifferentiated connective tissue disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"undifferentiated_pleomorphic_sarcoma":{"name":"Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma","system":"Oncology / Sarcoma","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UPS","malignant fibrous histiocytoma","MFH","pleomorphic undifferentiated sarcoma","high-grade undifferentiated sarcoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A high-grade sarcoma of adults that shows no identifiable line of differentiation despite thorough evaluation, formerly termed malignant fibrous histiocytoma","Essentially a diagnosis of exclusion, made only after immunohistochemistry and, where relevant, molecular testing have ruled out specific sarcoma types","Most often arises in the deep soft tissue of the extremities, particularly the thigh, and in the retroperitoneum, typically in older adults","Usually presents as a rapidly enlarging, deep, painless soft-tissue mass","Genomically complex with highly aberrant karyotypes rather than a defining fusion, reflecting its undifferentiated nature","Diagnosis requires a broad immunohistochemical panel that is negative or non-specific for lineage-defining markers, distinguishing it from dedifferentiated liposarcoma and other pleomorphic sarcomas","Wide surgical excision with adequate margins is the cornerstone, commonly combined with radiotherapy for local control, and chemotherapy is considered in selected high-risk cases","An aggressive tumour with substantial local recurrence and metastatic potential, chiefly to the lungs"],"pathophysiology":"Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma is a high-grade malignant mesenchymal tumour defined by the absence of any recognisable line of differentiation after comprehensive pathological assessment. It is genomically complex, marked by extensive chromosomal instability, copy-number aberrations, and frequent alterations in tumour-suppressor pathways such as TP53 and RB1, without the single recurrent translocation that characterises many other sarcomas. This lack of a defining differentiation programme and its accumulated genetic damage account for its pleomorphic morphology and aggressive behaviour. Because several specific sarcomas can appear undifferentiated, the diagnosis is reached only by excluding them through immunohistochemistry and molecular studies.","additionalDifferentials":["Dedifferentiated liposarcoma","Pleomorphic leiomyosarcoma","Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcoma","Myxofibrosarcoma","Sarcomatoid (spindle-cell) carcinoma","Melanoma with pleomorphic features","Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the primary site to define deep extent and relation to neurovascular structures","Chest CT for pulmonary metastases","Whole-body functional imaging for staging in high-grade disease","Core or open biopsy with careful tract planning","Broad immunohistochemical panel to exclude specific lines of differentiation","Molecular testing (including MDM2 for possible dedifferentiated liposarcoma) to exclude defined entities"],"redFlags":["A rapidly enlarging deep soft-tissue mass in an older adult","A large retroperitoneal mass with compressive symptoms","New pain or neurological deficit from local invasion","New respiratory symptoms suggesting pulmonary metastases","Local recurrence after prior resection"],"prognosis":"This is an aggressive high-grade sarcoma with significant risk of local recurrence and distant metastasis; prognosis depends on tumour size, depth, grade, site, and completeness of resection, with large, deep, retroperitoneal, or metastatic tumours faring worse.","pitfalls":["Labelling a tumour undifferentiated without excluding dedifferentiated liposarcoma by MDM2 testing","Failing to perform a sufficiently broad immunohistochemical workup to exclude specific sarcoma types","Poor biopsy tract planning that compromises subsequent limb-sparing surgery","Underestimating the extent of retroperitoneal disease and achieving inadequate margins","Overlooking pulmonary metastatic surveillance in high-grade disease"],"management":["Wide en-bloc surgical resection with negative margins as the mainstay of curative treatment for localised disease","Radiotherapy in the neoadjuvant or adjuvant setting to improve local control, particularly for large, deep or high-grade tumours","Anthracycline-based chemotherapy, typically doxorubicin with or without ifosfamide, for high-risk, unresectable or metastatic disease","Surveillance imaging of the primary site and chest for local recurrence and pulmonary metastases after treatment","Management of the primary tumour and any biopsy within a specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary centre with careful biopsy-tract planning","Palliative systemic therapy and symptom control for advanced or metastatic disease, with escalation for progression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"unicameral_bone_cyst":{"name":"Unicameral (simple) bone cyst","system":"Musculoskeletal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["simple bone cyst","solitary bone cyst","SBC","UBC"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone / Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fluid-filled solitary cavity that most often affects the proximal humerus and proximal femur in children and adolescents","Frequently asymptomatic and discovered incidentally or only after presenting with a pathological fracture","Radiographs show a centrally located, well-defined lucent lesion that does not expand beyond the width of the physis and thins the cortex","The fallen-fragment sign, a bone fragment settling into the dependent part of the fluid-filled cavity after fracture, is characteristic and confirms the lesion is hollow","Distinguished from aneurysmal bone cyst by its central position, single unilocular cavity and clear rather than blood-filled contents","Many lesions resolve or ossify with skeletal maturity, so observation is reasonable for asymptomatic cysts at low fracture risk","Treatment options for at-risk or symptomatic lesions include aspiration and injection, decompression, or curettage with bone grafting"],"pathophysiology":"Unicameral bone cyst is a benign, likely non-neoplastic, fluid-filled cavity of bone lined by a thin fibrous membrane. Its origin is debated but is thought to involve a local disturbance of interstitial fluid drainage and venous outflow within the metaphysis, producing raised intracystic pressure that promotes bone resorption and cavity expansion. Active lesions abut the physis and tend to enlarge, whereas latent lesions migrate away from the growth plate with maturity and often ossify. Cortical thinning predisposes to pathological fracture, which is a common presenting event.","additionalDifferentials":["Aneurysmal bone cyst","Fibrous dysplasia","Non-ossifying fibroma","Intraosseous ganglion or geode","Langerhans cell histiocytosis","Enchondroma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiography to assess location, cortical thinning and proximity to the physis","Recognition of the fallen-fragment sign on radiographs when a fracture is present","MRI or CT to confirm the fluid content and exclude aggressive features when imaging is atypical","Assessment of fracture risk based on cyst size and cortical involvement","Biopsy or aspiration in atypical lesions to confirm the diagnosis and exclude a mimic"],"redFlags":["Aggressive or permeative margins that are atypical for a simple cyst","Impending or established pathological fracture in a weight-bearing bone","Rapid enlargement or an associated soft-tissue mass","Atypical location outside the usual metaphyseal long-bone sites"],"prognosis":"The lesion is benign and often self-limiting, with many cysts healing spontaneously as the skeleton matures. Recurrence after minimally invasive treatment is not uncommon, and repeat procedures are sometimes required before durable healing.","pitfalls":["Confusing a simple bone cyst with an aneurysmal bone cyst, which is eccentric and blood-filled","Overtreating an asymptomatic latent cyst that would resolve with growth","Failing to appreciate fracture risk in a large cyst of a weight-bearing bone","Attributing all fluid-filled cystic lesions to a simple cyst without considering aggressive mimics"],"management":["Observation with periodic radiographic follow-up for asymptomatic cysts at low fracture risk, since many heal or ossify with skeletal maturity","Minimally invasive intralesional treatment such as corticosteroid injection, autologous bone marrow aspirate injection, or cyst decompression for symptomatic or enlarging lesions","Fracture management, often allowing pathological fractures to unite before or alongside definitive cyst treatment","Curettage and bone grafting for lesions refractory to less invasive measures or at high risk of recurrent fracture","Assessment of fracture risk based on cyst size, cortical thinning and proximity to the physis to guide intervention","Referral to orthopaedic oncology, with biopsy or aspiration of atypical lesions to confirm the diagnosis and exclude a mimic"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours","Enzinger and Weiss's Soft Tissue Tumors"],"crossLinks":[]},"unicuspid_aortic_valve":{"name":"unicuspid aortic valve","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["unicommissural aortic valve","UAV"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Unicuspid aortic valve is far rarer than bicuspid disease and typically presents with severe stenosis at a younger age, often in the third to fourth decade.","Two morphological subtypes exist: acommissural (dome-shaped orifice with no commissural attachment to the aortic wall) and unicommissural (a single eccentric commissure producing a teardrop or keyhole orifice); the unicommissural type is more common.","On parasternal short-axis echocardiography the orifice looks eccentric and slit-like, and the classic teardrop shape may be evident only in systole, so image carefully at the leaflet tips.","The valve is frequently misdiagnosed as bicuspid; transoesophageal or 3D echocardiography and cardiac CT or MRI improve accurate counting of commissures.","Associated aortopathy with ascending aortic dilatation occurs independently of haemodynamic severity, so serial aortic imaging is warranted.","Coexisting coarctation of the aorta and other left-heart obstructive lesions should be actively excluded.","Infective endocarditis risk is increased on the structurally abnormal valve.","Young patients frequently require surgery, including the Ross procedure, valve repair or replacement, often combined with aortic root or ascending aortic replacement."],"pathophysiology":"The anomaly arises from failure of separation of the valve cushions during embryogenesis, producing fusion of one or both commissures so that the valve opens through a single eccentric orifice with one functional commissure (unicommissural) or none (acommissural, dome-shaped). The eccentric restrictive orifice generates turbulent transvalvar flow that accelerates leaflet fibrosis and calcification, leading to progressive stenosis, regurgitation, or a mixed lesion. The same developmental disturbance is associated with an intrinsically abnormal aortic wall showing medial degeneration, which predisposes to ascending aortic dilatation and aneurysm irrespective of valve haemodynamics.","additionalDifferentials":["Bicuspid aortic valve","Rheumatic aortic stenosis","Calcific degenerative tricuspid aortic stenosis","Quadricuspid aortic valve","Subvalvar (subaortic membrane) or supravalvar aortic stenosis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with left ventricular outflow tract obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography with careful short-axis leaflet imaging","Transoesophageal echocardiography to define commissural morphology","Three-dimensional echocardiography","Cardiac CT angiography for valve morphology, calcification and aortic dimensions","Cardiac MRI for regurgitant fraction and aortic assessment","Electrocardiography for left ventricular hypertrophy","Exercise testing in apparently asymptomatic severe stenosis"],"redFlags":["Exertional syncope, angina or heart failure symptoms indicating severe stenosis","Rapidly progressive ascending aortic dilatation","Fever, new regurgitant murmur or embolic phenomena suggesting infective endocarditis","Severe aortic regurgitation with left ventricular dilatation or dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Without intervention severe obstruction or regurgitation develops earlier than in bicuspid disease and often requires surgery in young adulthood. Long-term outcome after valve or root surgery is good, but lifelong surveillance for aortopathy and prosthesis-related complications is required.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling a unicommissural valve as bicuspid because a fused commissure mimics a raphe","Assessing morphology only in diastole and missing the systolic teardrop orifice","Overlooking associated aortopathy and coarctation","Underestimating stenosis severity when the eccentric orifice lies off-axis to the Doppler beam"],"management":["Surgical aortic valve replacement, or repair in selected cases, as definitive treatment for severe stenosis, regurgitation or mixed disease, which tends to develop in young adulthood","Serial aortic imaging with concurrent surgical repair of the ascending aorta at aneurysmal thresholds, given the associated intrinsic aortopathy independent of valve haemodynamics","Regular surveillance with echocardiography, and cardiac CT or MRI, to track valve severity, ventricular function and aortic dimensions","Active exclusion and management of coexisting coarctation and other left-heart obstructive lesions","Attention to dental and oral hygiene with awareness of infective endocarditis risk","Care within a specialist cardiology and cardiac surgery service, with escalation for symptoms or severe valve dysfunction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the Management of Valvular Heart Disease","Feigenbaum's Echocardiography"],"crossLinks":[]},"uniparental_disomy_14":{"name":"Uniparental disomy 14 (overview)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UPD(14)","UPD14","chromosome 14 uniparental disomy"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Chromosome 14q32 carries an imprinted gene cluster, including DLK1, MEG3 and RTL1, so maternal and paternal uniparental disomy 14 produce two distinct disorders","Maternal UPD14 causes Temple syndrome: pre- and postnatal growth restriction, hypotonia, feeding difficulty, early puberty, and later truncal obesity, resembling Prader-Willi syndrome in infancy","Paternal UPD14 causes Kagami-Ogata syndrome: polyhydramnios, a small bell-shaped thorax with coat-hanger ribs, abdominal wall defects, and developmental delay","The same phenotypes can result from imprinting-centre microdeletions or primary epimutations at 14q32, not only from true uniparental disomy","Uniparental disomy can arise through trisomy or monosomy rescue, so an abnormal prenatal or placental result can be a clue","Diagnosis rests on methylation-specific testing of the 14q32 locus, since karyotype and standard microarray are usually normal","A Robertsonian translocation involving chromosome 14 raises the risk of uniparental disomy 14 and should prompt targeted testing"],"pathophysiology":"Uniparental disomy of chromosome 14 occurs when both copies of the chromosome are inherited from one parent. Because 14q32 carries a differentially methylated, imprinted gene cluster, the parental origin determines the outcome: two maternal copies, with loss of the paternally expressed genes, cause Temple syndrome, whereas two paternal copies, with over-expression of paternally expressed genes and loss of maternally expressed ones, cause Kagami-Ogata syndrome. Identical phenotypes can follow imprinting-centre microdeletions or primary epimutations at the same locus, so these disorders are best understood as disturbances of 14q32 imprinting rather than of chromosome number alone.","additionalDifferentials":["Prader-Willi syndrome, which Temple syndrome mimics in infancy","Silver-Russell syndrome, for the growth restriction of maternal UPD14","Other imprinting disorders such as Beckwith-Wiedemann and Angelman syndromes","Skeletal or thoracic dysplasia, for the Kagami-Ogata thorax","Neuromuscular causes of neonatal hypotonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Methylation analysis of the 14q32 (MEG3/DLK1) differentially methylated region","Microsatellite or SNP analysis to establish uniparental origin and distinguish disomy from deletion or epimutation","Karyotype to detect a Robertsonian translocation involving chromosome 14","Chromosomal microarray, usually normal but useful to exclude deletions and duplications","Growth, developmental, and endocrine or pubertal assessment","Chest imaging where paternal UPD14 is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neonatal respiratory compromise from a small thorax in Kagami-Ogata syndrome","Severe neonatal feeding difficulty with hypotonia","Precocious puberty in Temple syndrome requiring endocrine management"],"prognosis":"Outlook varies by parental origin: Temple syndrome generally has a fair outcome with growth and developmental support, whereas Kagami-Ogata syndrome carries higher neonatal mortality from respiratory difficulty; both require multidisciplinary follow-up.","pitfalls":["Relying on karyotype or microarray, which miss uniparental disomy and epimutations","Not testing for uniparental disomy 14 when a Robertsonian translocation involving chromosome 14 is found","Confusing Temple syndrome with Prader-Willi or Silver-Russell syndrome without methylation testing","Assuming a normal chromosome number excludes an imprinting disorder"],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy exists, so management is phenotype-directed and supportive according to whether Temple or Kagami-Ogata syndrome is present","In Temple syndrome, feeding support and growth monitoring in infancy, suppression of precocious puberty with a GnRH analogue, and later prevention and management of truncal obesity","In Kagami-Ogata syndrome, neonatal respiratory support and ventilation for the small bell-shaped thorax, and surgical repair of abdominal wall defects","Developmental surveillance with early intervention and educational support for delay","Endocrine and growth assessment, with consideration of growth hormone where growth restriction persists","Multidisciplinary follow-up coordinated through clinical genetics with paediatric endocrinology and respiratory input","Genetic counselling based on methylation-specific testing of the 14q32 locus, clarifying the mechanism and recurrence risk, including any Robertsonian translocation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Cassidy and Allanson's Management of Genetic Syndromes"],"crossLinks":[]},"uniparental_disomy_7":{"name":"Uniparental disomy 7","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UPD(7)","UPD7","maternal uniparental disomy 7","chromosome 7 uniparental disomy"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Maternal uniparental disomy 7 is a recognised cause of Silver-Russell syndrome, accounting for a substantial minority of cases","The Silver-Russell picture combines severe intrauterine and postnatal growth restriction with relative macrocephaly, reflecting head sparing","Body or limb asymmetry, a triangular face with a prominent forehead, and a small, thin body habitus are characteristic","Feeding difficulties, a risk of hypoglycaemia, and failure to thrive are common in infancy","Fifth-finger clinodactyly is a frequent minor feature","Most Silver-Russell cases are instead due to hypomethylation at the 11p15 imprinting centre, and UPD7 cases may show a somewhat milder or differing profile with more speech and learning involvement","Maternal UPD7 involves imprinted loci on chromosome 7 such as the MEST/PEG1 region and GRB10, implicating growth-related imprinted genes"],"pathophysiology":"Maternal uniparental disomy 7 means both chromosome 7 homologues are inherited from the mother, resulting in loss of paternally expressed and excess maternally expressed imprinted genes on chromosome 7, including growth-regulating loci such as MEST/PEG1 and GRB10. This imbalance restrains growth and produces the Silver-Russell phenotype. Because most Silver-Russell syndrome is actually caused by 11p15 methylation defects, uniparental disomy 7 accounts for only a proportion of cases, so testing must cover both mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["11p15-related Silver-Russell syndrome, the commoner molecular cause","Other causes of intrauterine growth restriction, whether placental or syndromic","Temple syndrome (maternal UPD14), given the overlapping growth restriction","3-M syndrome and other primordial dwarfism syndromes","Constitutional small-for-gestational-age without a syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["11p15 methylation testing together with specific testing for maternal UPD7 by methylation, microsatellite, or SNP analysis","Chromosomal microarray, usually normal but useful to exclude copy-number causes","Growth monitoring and assessment for asymmetry","Blood glucose monitoring in infancy given the risk of hypoglycaemia","Feeding and nutrition assessment","Developmental and speech assessment"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic hypoglycaemia in infancy","Severe failure to thrive","Significant limb-length asymmetry affecting function or gait"],"prognosis":"Generally reasonable with early nutritional and growth support, including consideration of growth hormone in Silver-Russell syndrome; intellect is often normal, and outcomes improve with multidisciplinary management.","pitfalls":["Testing only the 11p15 locus and not uniparental disomy 7, thereby missing a proportion of cases","Overlooking the risk of hypoglycaemia in a small infant","Assuming a normal microarray excludes the diagnosis","Not recognising asymmetry and clinodactyly as diagnostic clues"],"management":["No disease-specific drug therapy exists, so management follows the Silver-Russell phenotype with nutritional rehabilitation and growth support","Growth hormone therapy to improve growth and body composition in children meeting Silver-Russell criteria","Nutritional and feeding support with avoidance of prolonged fasting and sick-day guidance to prevent hypoglycaemia in infancy","Monitoring of growth, body or limb asymmetry, and blood glucose during early childhood","Developmental and speech and language therapy, as UPD7 cases may show more speech and learning involvement","Multidisciplinary follow-up with paediatric endocrinology, dietetics and clinical genetics","Genetic counselling based on 11p15 and maternal UPD7 testing to clarify the mechanism and recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Cassidy and Allanson's Management of Genetic Syndromes"],"crossLinks":[]},"unroofed_coronary_sinus":{"name":"Unroofed coronary sinus","system":"Cardiovascular / Congenital","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["coronary sinus septal defect","coronary sinus atrial septal defect","unroofed coronary sinus syndrome","type of ASD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A partial or complete deficiency of the wall between the coronary sinus and the left atrium, functioning as a form of interatrial communication","It represents the rarest type of atrial septal defect and produces a left-to-right shunt through the coronary sinus into the right atrium","There is a strong association with a persistent left superior vena cava draining to the coronary sinus","When completely unroofed with a persistent left superior vena cava, systemic venous blood can enter the left atrium and cause cyanosis and a risk of paradoxical embolism","The physiology otherwise resembles other atrial septal defects with right heart volume loading","It is frequently part of complex congenital heart disease, including heterotaxy syndromes","Surgical repair reroutes or baffles the anomalous drainage and closes the defect, restoring normal venous return"],"pathophysiology":"An unroofed coronary sinus results from a developmental deficiency of the tissue separating the coronary sinus from the left atrium, so that the two communicate along part or all of the coronary sinus course. This creates an interatrial pathway that behaves like an atrial septal defect, allowing left-to-right shunting and right heart volume loading. The lesion is closely linked to a persistent left superior vena cava that drains into the coronary sinus; when the sinus roof is absent, this systemic venous blood may drain directly into the left atrium, introducing a right-to-left component with desaturation and a route for paradoxical emboli. The defect is often seen within complex malformations, particularly heterotaxy, which shapes the overall haemodynamics.","additionalDifferentials":["Ostium secundum atrial septal defect","Sinus venosus atrial septal defect","Ostium primum atrial septal defect","Persistent left superior vena cava without unroofing","Partial anomalous pulmonary venous return","Other causes of right heart volume overload"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic and transoesophageal echocardiography, including agitated saline contrast, to demonstrate the communication and any left superior vena cava","Cardiac CT or MR angiography to define coronary sinus and systemic venous anatomy","Cardiac catheterisation for shunt quantification and pressures","Assessment for a persistent left superior vena cava draining to the coronary sinus","Electrocardiogram for right heart volume loading","Screening for associated heterotaxy and complex defects"],"redFlags":["Cyanosis or documented paradoxical embolism from right-to-left drainage","Signs of significant right heart volume overload or pulmonary hypertension","Association with complex congenital or heterotaxy syndromes","Unexplained systemic desaturation with a persistent left superior vena cava"],"prognosis":"Surgical correction generally has a good outcome, though prognosis is influenced by associated complex lesions; isolated defects repaired before pulmonary vascular disease develops do well.","pitfalls":["Labelling the lesion a simple secundum defect and missing the coronary sinus communication","Overlooking a persistent left superior vena cava, which complicates surgical repair","Failing to appreciate a right-to-left shunt and paradoxical embolism risk","Not screening for associated heterotaxy and other complex anomalies"],"management":["Surgical correction as the definitive treatment, closing or baffling the coronary sinus communication and redirecting any persistent left superior vena cava away from the left atrium","Timely repair before pulmonary vascular disease develops in the setting of a significant left-to-right shunt and right heart volume loading","Management of the right-to-left component and paradoxical embolism risk when the sinus is completely unroofed with a persistent left superior vena cava","Careful delineation of associated persistent left superior vena cava and systemic venous anatomy to plan the operation","Evaluation for associated heterotaxy and complex congenital heart disease that shape overall haemodynamics and outcome","Attention to infective endocarditis risk and postoperative surveillance within a congenital cardiology service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","Anderson's Paediatric Cardiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"unstable_angina":{"name":"Unstable angina","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UA","crescendo angina","acute coronary syndrome without ST elevation","rest angina"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by angina that is new in onset, occurs at rest or with minimal exertion, or is more frequent, severe, or prolonged than a patient's previous stable pattern","It sits on the acute coronary syndrome spectrum and is distinguished from NSTEMI by the absence of a rise in cardiac troponin","The ECG may show transient ST depression or T-wave changes, but can also be normal; serial tracings are important","High-sensitivity troponin measured serially is required to separate unstable angina from NSTEMI, since myocardial necrosis defines the latter","Early risk stratification using validated scores guides the timing of an invasive versus a conservative strategy","Initial management centres on antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy, anti-ischaemic measures, and admission for monitoring","Because it reflects an unstable coronary plaque, it can progress rapidly to infarction and warrants urgent evaluation rather than outpatient workup"],"pathophysiology":"Unstable angina is usually precipitated by rupture or erosion of an atherosclerotic plaque with superimposed non-occlusive or intermittent thrombus formation, causing an acute reduction in coronary blood flow. Dynamic vasoconstriction and platelet aggregation further limit supply. Unlike infarction, the ischaemia is severe but does not persist long enough or completely enough to produce detectable myocardial necrosis, so cardiac biomarkers remain normal. The same unstable plaque can progress to complete occlusion, linking unstable angina pathophysiologically to NSTEMI and STEMI.","additionalDifferentials":["NSTEMI and STEMI","Aortic dissection","Acute pericarditis or myopericarditis","Pulmonary embolism","Oesophageal spasm or reflux","Musculoskeletal chest pain","Coronary vasospasm (Prinzmetal angina)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Immediate and serial 12-lead ECGs to detect dynamic ischaemic changes","Serial high-sensitivity cardiac troponin to distinguish from NSTEMI","Continuous cardiac monitoring for arrhythmia and recurrent ischaemia","Risk stratification with validated scoring tools to guide invasive strategy timing","Coronary angiography when an early invasive approach is indicated","Echocardiography to assess wall motion and ventricular function"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: presentation is an acute coronary syndrome requiring urgent assessment and monitoring because of the risk of progression to myocardial infarction","Ongoing or recurrent rest pain despite initial therapy indicating refractory ischaemia","Haemodynamic instability, new heart failure, or malignant arrhythmia","Dynamic ST-segment shifts or a rising troponin signalling evolving infarction"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition, antithrombotic therapy, and appropriate risk-based revascularisation most patients stabilise, but delayed treatment or high-risk features carry a substantial early risk of infarction, arrhythmia, and death.","pitfalls":["Discharging a patient with rest or crescendo chest pain because a single troponin or ECG is normal","Failing to obtain serial ECGs and biomarkers to capture dynamic change","Underestimating risk in atypical presentations, particularly in women, older adults, and those with diabetes","Delaying antithrombotic therapy or admission while awaiting definitive imaging"],"management":["Dual antiplatelet therapy with aspirin plus a P2Y12 inhibitor to counter the platelet aggregation on the culprit unstable plaque","Parenteral anticoagulation with a heparin or fondaparinux to limit further thrombus propagation","Anti-ischaemic therapy with nitrates and a beta-blocker to reduce myocardial oxygen demand and relieve symptoms","Early risk stratification with validated scoring to determine the timing of an invasive coronary angiography and revascularisation strategy versus a conservative approach","Admission for continuous cardiac monitoring with serial ECGs and high-sensitivity troponin to detect progression to NSTEMI, recurrent ischaemia or arrhythmia","High-intensity statin and secondary prevention including an ACE inhibitor, risk-factor modification and cardiac rehabilitation","Urgent escalation to coronary angiography for refractory or recurrent ischaemia, haemodynamic instability or dynamic ECG changes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"unverricht_lundborg_disease":{"name":"Unverricht-Lundborg disease (EPM1, cystatin B)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["EPM1","Baltic myoclonus","Mediterranean myoclonus","cystatin B (CSTB) deficiency","progressive myoclonic epilepsy type 1"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Unverricht-Lundborg disease (EPM1) is an autosomal recessive progressive myoclonic epilepsy caused by biallelic CSTB variants, most often an expansion of a dodecamer repeat in the promoter","Onset is usually in late childhood or adolescence with stimulus-sensitive and action myoclonus plus generalised tonic-clonic seizures","Compared with Lafora disease, cognition is relatively preserved and the course is far more benign, though myoclonus can become disabling","The severe, treatment-worsened myoclonus historically caused by phenytoin illustrates why sodium-channel-blocking drugs should be avoided","It is the commonest cause of progressive myoclonic epilepsy in some populations, including the Baltic and Mediterranean regions","EEG shows photosensitivity and generalised spike-wave discharges with relatively preserved background early in the disease","Anti-myoclonic agents such as valproate, levetiracetam, and adjunctive treatments form the mainstay of care"],"pathophysiology":"Unverricht-Lundborg disease is caused by deficiency of cystatin B, an inhibitor of cysteine proteases (cathepsins). The commonest mutation is an unstable expansion of a dodecamer repeat in the CSTB promoter that reduces gene expression. Loss of cystatin B is thought to lead to unopposed cathepsin activity, increased oxidative stress, and enhanced neuronal apoptosis, particularly affecting cerebellar and cortical neurones. Unlike Lafora disease there is no storage material; the pathology is one of neuronal loss and gliosis, which correlates with the predominance of myoclonus and ataxia over dementia.","additionalDifferentials":["Lafora disease (EPM2A/NHLRC1)","Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy","Mitochondrial disease including MERRF","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses","Sialidosis","Dentatorubral-pallidoluysian atrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of CSTB, including analysis of the dodecamer repeat expansion","EEG to demonstrate photosensitivity and generalised epileptiform discharges with relatively preserved background","Brain MRI to exclude structural causes and assess for atrophy","Metabolic and mitochondrial screening to exclude other progressive myoclonic epilepsies","Neuropsychological assessment to monitor the comparatively mild cognitive trajectory"],"redFlags":["Rapid cognitive decline out of keeping with EPM1, suggesting an alternative such as Lafora disease","Marked worsening of myoclonus after starting a sodium-channel-blocking antiseizure drug","Status epilepticus or falls from severe myoclonus requiring urgent review","Emergence of prominent ataxia limiting mobility and safety"],"prognosis":"With appropriate anti-myoclonic treatment and avoidance of aggravating drugs, many patients have a near-normal lifespan; disability relates mainly to action myoclonus and ataxia rather than dementia, and the course is much better than in Lafora disease.","pitfalls":["Using phenytoin or other sodium-channel-blocking agents, which markedly worsen myoclonus and cerebellar signs","Confusing EPM1 with juvenile myoclonic epilepsy and missing the progressive component","Assuming a uniformly poor prognosis and not distinguishing it from Lafora disease","Underestimating the functional impact of action myoclonus on daily activities"],"management":["First-line anti-myoclonic and antiseizure therapy with sodium valproate, adding levetiracetam and piracetam for disabling action myoclonus","Use clonazepam, zonisamide or topiramate as adjuncts for refractory myoclonus and generalised tonic-clonic seizures","Strictly avoid sodium-channel-blocking and GABAergic drugs (phenytoin, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, lamotrigine, gabapentin, pregabalin, vigabatrin, tiagabine) that aggravate myoclonus and ataxia","Consider N-acetylcysteine as an antioxidant adjunct given the oxidative-stress mechanism of cystatin B loss","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and mobility aids for progressive action myoclonus and cerebellar ataxia, with screening and treatment for reactive depression","Periodic neurological, EEG and neuropsychological review to track the comparatively mild cognitive trajectory and photosensitivity","Offer genetic counselling and CSTB carrier testing to relatives given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Unverricht-Lundborg Disease","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"upper_gastrointestinal_tract_cancers":{"name":"Upper Gastrointestinal Tract Cancers","system":"Gastrointestinal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Esophageal cancer","Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma","Esophageal adenocarcinoma","Gastroesophageal junction (GEJ) cancer","Gastric cancer","Stomach cancer","Gastric adenocarcinoma","Gastric lymphoma","MALT lymphoma","Gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST)","Small-bowel neoplasm"],"chapter":85,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.641","clinicalPearls":["Two biologically distinct esophageal cancers arise in the same organ: squamous cell carcinoma (SCC), favoring the upper and mid esophagus, and adenocarcinoma, almost always in the lower esophagus and at the gastroesophageal junction (GEJ); globally SCC predominates while adenocarcinoma now accounts for over 75% of incident U.S./Western European cases.","Because dysphagia and odynophagia are typically not noticed until the lumen is substantially obstructed, most esophageal cancers are locally advanced or metastatic at diagnosis; any patient with these symptoms warrants upper endoscopy with biopsy.","Helicobacter pylori is a major chronic-inflammation driver of gastric cancer, but cancer develops in only a small subset of the roughly half of humans infected; eradication has been shown in prospective trials to lower subsequent gastric cancer risk, supporting treatment of asymptomatic H. pylori in those with a first-degree relative with gastric cancer or a prior early-stage gastric cancer.","Physical findings suggesting advanced/metastatic gastric cancer include left supraclavicular adenopathy (Virchow's node), a periumbilical nodule (Sister Mary Joseph nodule), a palpable rectal-shelf mass (Blumer's shelf), ascites, and an ovarian mass (Krukenberg tumor); more commonly the exam is unrevealing.","Diffuse (signet-cell) gastric cancer may not form a discrete mass or ulcer, instead producing thickened gastric rugae and tracking beneath an intact mucosal surface, so initial superficial biopsies can be falsely negative and EUS-guided deeper biopsy may be required.","Germline CDH1 (e-cadherin) mutation markedly increases risk of the diffuse/signet-cell gastric cancer subtype and also raises lobular breast cancer risk; prophylactic gastrectomy is an option in CDH1 carriers.","Molecular profiling is therapeutically decisive: roughly 20-25% of esophageal/GEJ and about 20% of gastric adenocarcinomas overexpress or amplify HER2/ERBB2, and MSI-high/mismatch-repair-deficient and PD-L1-high (high combined positive score) tumors identify candidates for immune-modulating therapy; NGS plus PD-L1 IHC should be routine in metastatic disease.","Gastric lymphomas (about 3% of gastric malignancies) are extranodal non-Hodgkin lymphomas; the GI tract is the most common extranodal NHL site and the stomach is the most common GI site, with MALT (~40%) and diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (~55%) being the dominant subtypes.","MALT lymphoma of the stomach is strongly H. pylori-associated, and the stomach accounts for about 35% of all MALT lymphomas; the t(11;18) translocation marks tumors less likely to respond to H. pylori eradication.","GISTs arise from the interstitial cells of Cajal and are driven by activating cKIT (~majority) or PDGFRA mutations; the stomach is the most frequent primary site (~60%), followed by small bowel (~25%); spindle-cell histology predominates (~70%) and KIT expression is found in ~95%.","Small-bowel neoplasms account for only about 3% of GI tumors; in the U.S., neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumors (40-45%) slightly outnumber adenocarcinomas (30-40%), and the duodenum is the most common site (~50%).","EBV is associated with a distinct gastric cancer subtype (identified in roughly 8-10% of gastric cancer patients) and is described as a driver in some esophageal/GEJ adenocarcinomas; current EBV positivity does not by itself change therapeutic options."],"pathophysiology":"Esophageal SCC is linked to mucosal injury from alcohol and tobacco (synergistic), ingestion of very hot beverages, areca/betel-nut chewing, chronic achalasia, prior radiation, lye-induced strictures, and Plummer-Vinson (Patterson-Kelly) syndrome; genomically it resembles SCCs of other sites (e.g., head and neck) more than esophageal adenocarcinoma, and HPV is not implicated. Esophageal/GEJ adenocarcinoma arises in the setting of chronic gastroesophageal reflux producing inflammation and Barrett's metaplasia (glandular gastric-type mucosa extending into the esophagus); progression through dysplasia accumulates TP53 mutations and aneuploidy, with annual risk of progression of Barrett's to cancer about 0.4-0.5%. Gastric adenocarcinoma is predominantly an inflammation-driven malignancy: H. pylori (and the broader gastric microbiome) and ingestion of nitrate-rich smoked/salted/dried foods generate carcinogenic nitrites; the intestinal Lauren subtype associates with H. pylori and atrophic gastritis, while the diffuse subtype (~20%) associates with germline or somatic CDH1 loss. TCGA defines four molecular subtypes (EBV-positive, microsatellite-unstable/hypermutated, genomically stable [diffuse], and chromosomally unstable). The proximal esophagus-GEJ-gastric cardia adenocarcinomas share a similar genomic profile suggesting common driving factors. Gastric MALT lymphoma is driven by chronic antigenic stimulation from H. pylori, with the t(11;18) API1-MALT1 fusion activating NF-kB signaling. GISTs originate from Cajal cells via gain-of-function KIT or PDGFRA mutations.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign peptic ulcer disease","Reflux esophagitis / Barrett's esophagus without malignancy","Achalasia and other benign esophageal motility/stricture disorders","Gastric adenocarcinoma vs primary gastric lymphoma (treatment differs markedly, so adequate tissue is essential)","Gastric neuroendocrine tumor / neuroendocrine carcinoma","Gastrointestinal stromal tumor (submucosal smooth bulging mass)","Metastatic disease to stomach or esophagus from another primary","Small-bowel adenocarcinoma vs neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor, lymphoma, or GIST"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Benign gastric or duodenal ulcer","Reflux esophagitis / Barrett's esophagus","Achalasia or benign esophageal stricture","Submucosal benign tumors (lipoma, leiomyoma, neurofibroma, lymphoid nodular hyperplasia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper endoscopy with biopsy to establish histology (deep or repeated biopsies / EUS-guided sampling for suspected diffuse gastric cancer or lymphoma that tracks submucosally)","Contrast-enhanced CT of chest, abdomen, and pelvis to assess metastatic disease","FDG-PET/CT (more sensitive than CT for metastases, but may be non-informative in diffuse-type gastric cancer or non-FDG-avid tumors)","Endoscopic ultrasonography (EUS) to determine depth of tumor penetration (T stage) and regional nodal involvement when no metastatic disease is seen","Staging laparoscopy with peritoneal washing cytology for GEJ/gastric tumors with advanced T stage to detect peritoneal involvement","Bronchoscopy for mid/upper esophageal tumors to exclude tracheobronchial invasion (its presence precludes curative surgery)","Molecular/NGS profiling, PD-L1 IHC, HER2/ERBB2 (IHC/FISH/NGS), and MSI/mismatch-repair-protein testing on metastatic tumors to guide systemic therapy","Plasma circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) when biopsy tissue is insufficient, and as an investigational measure of minimal residual disease","H. pylori status assessment in all gastric lymphoma patients (histology, and if negative, stool antigen or urea breath test); HBV testing before lymphoma therapy","Gallium-68 or copper-64 DOTATATE scan for neuroendocrine tumors; CT/MR enterography and device-assisted enteroscopy or capsule endoscopy for small-bowel neoplasms","Germline genetic testing in small-bowel adenocarcinoma and in patients suspected of hereditary syndromes (CDH1, Lynch, FAP, Peutz-Jeghers, BRCA)"],"redFlags":["New or progressive dysphagia or odynophagia, especially with weight loss","Hematemesis or melena, or unexplained iron-deficiency anemia from occult GI bleeding","Anorexia, early satiety, and unexplained weight loss","Left supraclavicular (Virchow's) node, Sister Mary Joseph periumbilical nodule, Blumer's shelf, ascites, or Krukenberg ovarian mass indicating advanced disease","Dysphagia, odynophagia, or upper abdominal discomfort in a patient under 50, given the rising incidence of early-onset upper GI cancers (symptoms must not be dismissed as benign)","Small-bowel obstruction, perforation, or intussusception as a presentation of small-bowel neoplasm"],"prognosis":"Esophageal cancer prognosis remains poor overall but is slowly improving; by clinical (pretreatment) AJCC staging, 5-year survival ranges from about 75-82% for stage 0-I down to roughly 5-18% for metastatic (stage IVB) disease, and most U.S. patients present with locally advanced or metastatic disease. For gastric cancer, surgery alone yields about 25% overall 5-year survival, with stage-dependent rates falling from about 90% (stage 0) to roughly 3% for stage IV; multimodality therapy (perioperative chemotherapy plus surgery) improves 5-year survival by about 10-15% over surgery alone (e.g., MAGIC trial 36% vs 23%). Even with modern systemic and immune-modulating therapy, overall survival for stage IV gastric cancer is still only about 12-15 months. Gastric MSI-high tumors had the best outcomes and genomically stable (diffuse)/mesenchymal subtypes the worst in genomic series. Lynch-syndrome-associated small-bowel cancers probably have a better prognosis. For nonmetastatic gastric GIST, resected tumors 2 cm or smaller with a mitotic index of 5% or less carry near-zero progression risk, whereas tumors 10 cm or larger with mitotic index above 5% are moderate-to-high risk.","pitfalls":["Falsely reassuring superficial endoscopic biopsies in diffuse-type gastric cancer or gastric lymphoma, which track below the mucosa; adequate deep tissue or EUS-guided sampling is required.","Relying on FDG-PET to exclude disease in diffuse-type gastric cancer, which is often not FDG-avid (PET may be non-informative).","Using tumor markers (CEA, CA19-9) for diagnosis; they are nonspecific and elevated in many cancers, and are better for monitoring response/progression.","Attributing alarm symptoms in patients under 50 to benign illness and delaying diagnostic workup, given rising early-onset upper GI cancer incidence.","Missing tracheobronchial invasion in mid/upper esophageal tumors, which precludes curative surgery, by omitting bronchoscopy.","Treating gastric lymphoma like adenocarcinoma; surgical resection has no role in primary gastric lymphoma and MALT may respond to H. pylori eradication alone, so accurate subtyping is critical.","Assuming a t(11;18)-positive MALT lymphoma will respond to H. pylori eradication; this subgroup is less likely to respond.","Forgetting HBV testing before initiating lymphoma therapy (risk of reactivation)."],"management":["Manage through a specialist upper-GI oncology multidisciplinary team after full staging with endoscopy and biopsy, contrast CT, endoscopic ultrasound, FDG-PET and staging laparoscopy for gastro-oesophageal and gastric tumours","Offer endoscopic mucosal resection or submucosal dissection for early T1a mucosal cancers and high-grade dysplasia arising in Barrett's oesophagus","Proceed to radical oesophagectomy or gastrectomy with adequate (D2) lymphadenectomy for resectable locally advanced disease","Give neoadjuvant or perioperative chemotherapy, or neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy, for locally advanced oesophageal, junctional and gastric adenocarcinoma to improve resectability and survival","Treat advanced or metastatic disease with platinum-fluoropyrimidine chemotherapy, adding trastuzumab for HER2-positive tumours and immune checkpoint inhibitors where indicated","Eradicate Helicobacter pylori and provide palliative luminal stenting, nutritional support with enteral feeding, and analgesia for obstructing or incurable disease","Refer germline CDH1 (E-cadherin) carriers for genetic counselling and consideration of prophylactic total gastrectomy and lobular breast surveillance"],"severityClassification":"Esophageal and gastric cancers are staged with the AJCC TNM system (eighth edition; ninth edition expected 2024), with separate clinical (cTNM, pretreatment), pathologic (pTNM, for patients undergoing initial surgery), and post-neoadjuvant (ypTNM) classifications. The esophageal T stage reflects depth of penetration through the wall (which over most of its course lacks serosa); regional nodal disease is still potentially curable while metastatic disease is generally treated palliatively. Gastric staging similarly progresses from Tis (mucosa-confined, stage 0) through wall penetration and increasing nodal involvement to distant metastasis (stage IV). Gastric lymphomas are NOT staged by TNM; the Lugano system (an Ann Arbor modification) is used: stage I confined to the gastric wall, stage II regional nodal or local-structure involvement, stage III nodes on both sides of the diaphragm, stage IV more extensive nodal or distant/extranodal (including marrow) disease. GIST recurrence risk is stratified by primary tumor size, mitotic index, and GI location. Neuroendocrine tumors are graded 1-3 by Ki-67 positivity, distinct from fully malignant small-/large-cell neuroendocrine carcinomas.","references":["Harrison 22e p.641","Harrison 22e p.642","Harrison 22e p.643","Harrison 22e p.644","Harrison 22e p.645","Harrison 22e p.646","Harrison 22e p.647","Harrison 22e p.648","Harrison 22e p.649","Harrison 22e p.650","Harrison 22e p.651","Harrison 22e p.652"],"crossLinks":[]},"uraemic_pericarditis":{"name":"uraemic pericarditis","system":"cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["uremic pericarditis","renal pericarditis","dialysis-associated pericarditis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A useful distinction is drawn between true uraemic pericarditis, occurring in advanced untreated or under-dialysed renal failure before dialysis initiation, and dialysis-associated pericarditis, arising in established dialysis patients often due to inadequate dialysis or intercurrent illness.","Uraemic pericarditis correlates broadly with the degree of azotaemia, though no single threshold of urea or creatinine reliably predicts it.","A characteristic feature is that the friction rub and clinical picture often persist despite the absence of the typical diffuse ECG changes of acute pericarditis, because the inflammation spares the underlying epicardial myocardium.","The friction rub may be loud and persistent, and effusions are frequently large and haemorrhagic owing to uraemic platelet dysfunction.","Because ECG changes are typically absent, new widespread ST elevation in a uraemic patient should prompt consideration of an alternative or additional diagnosis such as myocardial injury.","The mainstay of treatment for true uraemic pericarditis is initiation or intensification of dialysis, which resolves most cases; heparin-free or minimal-heparin dialysis is preferred to reduce haemorrhagic effusion risk.","Persistent or recurrent effusion despite intensive dialysis, or the development of tamponade, may require pericardiocentesis or a pericardial window; anti-inflammatory agents have a more limited and adjunctive role.","Progression to cardiac tamponade is an important and potentially fatal complication requiring urgent recognition and drainage."],"pathophysiology":"The pericardial inflammation of renal failure is driven by the retention of uraemic toxins and metabolic derangements accompanying severely reduced glomerular filtration, although the precise causative solutes are not fully defined. The visceral and parietal pericardial surfaces develop a fibrinous, sometimes haemorrhagic, exudative reaction. A distinctive feature is that the inflammatory process typically remains confined to the pericardium and does not extend into the subepicardial myocardium, which explains the frequent absence of the diffuse ST-segment and PR-segment changes seen in other forms of acute pericarditis. Uraemia-associated platelet dysfunction and the anticoagulation used during haemodialysis predispose to haemorrhagic effusions, which may accumulate to produce tamponade. In dialysis-associated disease, inadequate solute clearance, volume overload, intercurrent infection or inflammation are contributory.","additionalDifferentials":["viral or idiopathic acute pericarditis","bacterial (purulent) pericarditis","tuberculous pericarditis","myocardial infarction or acute coronary syndrome","systemic lupus erythematosus or other connective tissue disease serositis","hypothyroidism-related pericardial effusion","malignant pericardial effusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["transthoracic echocardiography (effusion size, tamponade physiology)","12-lead ECG (typically lacks diffuse ST/PR changes; assess for ischaemia)","serum urea, creatinine and electrolytes with assessment of dialysis adequacy","full blood count and inflammatory markers","pericardial fluid analysis if drained (haemorrhagic exudate; exclude infection and malignancy)","troponin (to help distinguish concurrent myocardial injury)","chest radiography (cardiomegaly, pulmonary congestion)"],"redFlags":["hypotension, raised jugular venous pressure and pulsus paradoxus indicating tamponade","large or rapidly enlarging haemorrhagic effusion","widespread ST elevation suggesting concomitant myocardial injury rather than isolated uraemic pericarditis","haemodynamic deterioration during or shortly after heparinised dialysis","fever and features suggesting superimposed purulent pericarditis"],"prognosis":"With timely initiation or intensification of dialysis most cases of true uraemic pericarditis resolve, and the outlook is generally favourable; however, large haemorrhagic effusions, tamponade and progression to constriction are recognised complications, and dialysis-associated pericarditis that responds poorly to intensified dialysis carries a worse prognosis.","pitfalls":["Expecting the diffuse ECG changes of typical acute pericarditis, whose absence may lead to the diagnosis being missed.","Continuing standard heparinised haemodialysis and thereby worsening a haemorrhagic effusion or precipitating tamponade.","Attributing all chest pain in a uraemic patient to pericarditis and overlooking concurrent myocardial ischaemia.","Relying on non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, which are often ineffective and nephrotoxic, instead of optimising dialysis.","Under-recognising tamponade because hypotension is ascribed to intradialytic fluid shifts."],"management":["Initiate or intensify renal replacement therapy (haemodialysis), the definitive treatment that resolves most cases of true uraemic pericarditis","Use heparin-free or minimal-heparin dialysis to reduce the risk of provoking a haemorrhagic pericardial effusion given uraemic platelet dysfunction","Provide symptomatic relief for pleuritic pain, recognising that NSAIDs and colchicine are of limited benefit and NSAIDs are best avoided in advanced renal failure","Monitor with serial transthoracic echocardiography for enlarging effusion and early tamponade physiology","Perform urgent pericardiocentesis for cardiac tamponade or a large symptomatic effusion","Consider a pericardial window or pericardiectomy for recurrent, loculated, or constrictive disease unresponsive to dialysis","Co-manage with nephrology and cardiology, optimising dialysis adequacy and reviewing intercurrent illness in dialysis-associated cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Pericardial Diseases","Brenner and Rector's The Kidney"],"crossLinks":[]},"urea_cycle_disorder":{"name":"Urea cycle disorder","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["urea cycle defect","UCD","hyperammonaemia from urea cycle enzyme deficiency","inherited hyperammonaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of inherited defects in enzymes or transporters of the urea cycle that impair conversion of ammonia to urea, causing hyperammonaemia","Severe neonatal presentation is typically a well baby who develops poor feeding, lethargy, vomiting, tachypnoea and encephalopathy in the first days of life","The hallmark biochemical finding is hyperammonaemia usually with a respiratory alkalosis and, in most defects, a normal anion gap (distinguishing from organic acidaemias)","Plasma citrulline and urine orotic acid help localise the enzymatic block within the cycle","Later-onset and partial defects can present in older children or adults with episodic encephalopathy, psychiatric symptoms, or protein aversion, often triggered by catabolic stress","Acute management principles are to stop protein intake, reverse catabolism with high calorie (glucose and lipid) support, and lower ammonia with nitrogen-scavenging agents and, when severe, haemodialysis","Arginine (or citrulline) supplementation is used to replenish urea cycle intermediates in most defects except arginase deficiency","Many urea cycle disorders are included in newborn screening panels, though the most severe neonatal cases may present before results return"],"pathophysiology":"The urea cycle detoxifies waste nitrogen by converting ammonia derived from amino acid metabolism into urea for renal excretion. Deficiency of any of its enzymes (carbamoyl phosphate synthetase 1, ornithine transcarbamylase, argininosuccinate synthetase, argininosuccinate lyase, arginase) or of the N-acetylglutamate synthase activator or transporters interrupts this flux, causing ammonia to accumulate. Ammonia is neurotoxic, producing astrocyte swelling, cerebral oedema and glutamine accumulation, leading to encephalopathy and, if untreated, irreversible brain injury or death. The pattern of accumulated intermediates (citrulline, argininosuccinate) and urine orotic acid reflects the position of the block.","additionalDifferentials":["Organic acidaemias (which cause hyperammonaemia with metabolic acidosis)","Fatty acid oxidation disorders","Transient hyperammonaemia of the newborn","Neonatal sepsis and meningitis","Liver failure of other cause","Hyperinsulinism-hyperammonaemia syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma ammonia (urgent) and arterial blood gas showing respiratory alkalosis","Plasma amino acids (citrulline, arginine, argininosuccinate) and urine orotic acid to localise the defect","Plasma glucose, lactate, and acid-base status to distinguish from organic acidaemias","Newborn screening (citrulline and arginine markers)","Enzyme assays and molecular genetic testing for the specific defect","Neuroimaging to assess cerebral oedema in severe hyperammonaemia"],"redFlags":["Severe hyperammonaemia with encephalopathy is a medical emergency requiring immediate ammonia-lowering and often dialysis","Rapidly declining level of consciousness, seizures, or signs of raised intracranial pressure from cerebral oedema","Neonate deteriorating after an initially normal period, mistaken for sepsis","Recurrent unexplained encephalopathy or coma triggered by high protein intake, infection, or surgery in older patients"],"prognosis":"Outcome correlates strongly with the peak and duration of hyperammonaemia; prolonged severe neonatal hyperammonaemia carries a high risk of death or permanent neurodevelopmental impairment, whereas early recognition, prompt ammonia control and liver transplantation in selected cases improve survival and reduce disability.","pitfalls":["Failing to measure ammonia in an encephalopathic neonate or child and attributing symptoms to sepsis","Allowing sample delay or poor technique to give falsely elevated or unreliable ammonia results","Continuing protein intake or under-treating catabolism during acute decompensation","Not recognising milder late-onset presentations, especially in females with ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency"],"management":["Institute lifelong dietary protein restriction with essential amino acid supplements and high non-protein calorie intake to limit waste-nitrogen production and prevent catabolism","Give nitrogen-scavenging drugs (sodium benzoate and sodium or glycerol phenylbutyrate) to provide alternative pathways for nitrogen excretion","Supplement arginine or citrulline according to the specific enzymatic block (avoiding arginine in arginase deficiency)","Treat acute hyperammonaemic crises by stopping protein, reversing catabolism with intravenous glucose with or without insulin and intravenous lipid, and giving intravenous nitrogen scavengers and arginine","Escalate to haemodialysis or haemofiltration for severe or refractory hyperammonaemia, and consider liver transplantation as definitive therapy in severe or recurrently decompensating disease","Monitor plasma ammonia, plasma amino acids, growth and neurodevelopment, and provide a written emergency sick-day regimen while avoiding sodium valproate and other catabolic triggers","Coordinate care through a metabolic medicine team with specialist dietetic support, and offer genetic counselling and family screening (X-linked for ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency, otherwise autosomal recessive)"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"ureaplasma_infection":{"name":"Ureaplasma urealyticum infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ureaplasma urealyticum","Ureaplasma","U. urealyticum","ureaplasma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell / BASHH-CDC STI guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Ureaplasma urealyticum is a cell-wall-deficient mollicute that colonises the lower genital tract of many sexually active adults, so its detection often reflects commensal carriage rather than disease","Its pathogenic role is debated; unlike Mycoplasma genitalium it is not consistently proven to cause urethritis, and routine testing or treatment of asymptomatic carriers is generally not advised","Ureaplasma urealyticum (as distinct from the usually commensal Ureaplasma parvum) has been implicated in some cases of non-chlamydial non-gonococcal urethritis","In pregnancy, ureaplasmas are associated with chorioamnionitis, preterm birth, and low birth weight, and they can be transmitted vertically to the neonate","In preterm and very-low-birth-weight infants, respiratory tract colonisation has been linked to neonatal pneumonia and to the development of bronchopulmonary dysplasia","Because it lacks a cell wall, it is invisible on Gram stain and inherently resistant to beta-lactams; diagnosis uses culture on specialised media or nucleic acid amplification testing","When treatment is genuinely indicated, macrolides such as azithromycin or a tetracycline such as doxycycline are typically used, though tetracycline resistance occurs","Interpreting a positive result requires clinical correlation, since a detection alone does not establish causation"],"pathophysiology":"Ureaplasma species are among the smallest free-living bacteria and, like other mollicutes, lack a cell wall; they are named for their capacity to hydrolyse urea as an energy source. They frequently colonise the urogenital mucosa as commensals, and the balance between harmless carriage and disease appears to depend on host factors, bacterial load, species and serovar, and the presence of co-pathogens. When pathogenic, urease-driven ammonia production and a local inflammatory response are thought to contribute to mucosal injury, and the same mechanisms are implicated in intra-amniotic infection when the organism ascends in pregnancy, provoking chorioamnionitis and inflammation that may precipitate preterm labour and injure the developing fetal lung. Neonatal colonisation, particularly in preterm infants, can be followed by respiratory disease and chronic lung injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Chlamydia trachomatis infection","Neisseria gonorrhoeae infection","Mycoplasma genitalium infection","Trichomonas vaginalis infection","Bacterial vaginosis","Urinary tract infection","Non-infective urethritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nucleic acid amplification testing or specialised culture of genital or urine specimens, with species differentiation where available","Concurrent nucleic acid amplification testing for chlamydia and gonorrhoea to exclude established pathogens","Microscopy of genital smears to document urethritis or cervicitis","Testing for Mycoplasma genitalium and Trichomonas vaginalis in persistent urethritis","In relevant pregnancy or neonatal settings, placental histology or neonatal respiratory sampling as clinically indicated","Screening for other sexually transmitted infections when a sexually acquired cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Preterm labour, prelabour rupture of membranes, or clinical chorioamnionitis in pregnancy","Respiratory deterioration in a colonised preterm neonate suggesting pneumonia or evolving chronic lung disease","Persistent symptomatic urethritis despite treatment for established pathogens","Systemic or invasive infection in an immunocompromised host"],"prognosis":"In most immunocompetent adults ureaplasma carriage is benign and needs no treatment; when genuine infection is treated the outlook is generally good, whereas perinatal infection carries a less favourable outlook because of its associations with preterm birth and neonatal respiratory morbidity.","pitfalls":["Treating an asymptomatic positive result and mistaking commensal colonisation for disease","Failing to exclude established pathogens such as chlamydia, gonorrhoea, and Mycoplasma genitalium before attributing symptoms to ureaplasma","Prescribing a beta-lactam, which is ineffective against a cell-wall-deficient organism","Overlooking the perinatal implications of ureaplasma in pregnant and preterm-neonatal populations"],"management":["Do not test or treat asymptomatic urogenital carriage, which usually represents commensal colonisation rather than disease","Treat genuine symptomatic infection with a macrolide (azithromycin) or a tetracycline (doxycycline), reserving fluoroquinolones for treatment failure","Avoid beta-lactams, which are inherently ineffective because the organism lacks a cell wall","Test for and treat co-existing established pathogens (Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Mycoplasma genitalium) in non-gonococcal urethritis","Arrange partner notification, contact treatment and a full sexually transmitted infection screen when infection is sexually acquired","In pregnancy manage chorioamnionitis and preterm-labour risk, and treat colonised preterm or very-low-birth-weight neonates with pneumonia or evolving bronchopulmonary dysplasia using a macrolide, with obstetric and neonatal specialist input"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","BASHH UK guidance on non-gonococcal urethritis and genital mollicutes","CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"ureterocele":{"name":"Ureterocele","system":"Renal / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["orthotopic ureterocele","ectopic ureterocele","cobra-head deformity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A cystic dilatation of the terminal intravesical ureter, classified as intravesical (orthotopic), lying entirely within the bladder, or ectopic, extending into the bladder neck or urethra","In children, ectopic ureteroceles most often arise from the upper-pole moiety of a duplex system, in accordance with the Weigert-Meyer rule (the upper-pole ureter inserts inferomedially and is prone to obstruction/ureterocele, the lower-pole ureter inserts superolaterally and tends to reflux)","In adults, simple orthotopic ureteroceles are usually associated with single systems and may be incidental","May present with antenatal hydronephrosis, urinary tract infection, obstruction, a palpable mass, or, in girls, prolapse of an ectopic ureterocele through the urethra","On urography a ureterocele can produce the classic cobra-head or spring-onion appearance","Large ureteroceles can obstruct the ipsilateral moiety, the contralateral ureter, or the bladder outlet","Management options include endoscopic incision/puncture for decompression, upper-pole heminephrectomy for a non-functioning moiety, and ureteric reimplantation or complete reconstruction depending on function, reflux, and anatomy"],"pathophysiology":"A ureterocele is thought to result from a developmental failure of the terminal ureteric membrane (Chwalla's membrane) to resorb normally, together with abnormal muscular development of the distal ureter, producing a cystic ballooning of the intravesical ureter beneath the bladder mucosa. In duplex systems the upper-pole ureter drains more caudally and medially and is the segment that typically forms an obstructing ureterocele, following the Weigert-Meyer rule. The dilated terminal sac can obstruct urine drainage from its own moiety and, when large or ectopic, may distort the bladder base and outlet, causing wider urinary tract dysfunction and predisposing to infection and upper-tract deterioration.","additionalDifferentials":["Ectopic ureter without a ureterocele","Ureteropelvic junction obstruction","Primary obstructive megaureter","Bladder diverticulum or cyst near the trigone","Pseudoureterocele from a bladder tumour or oedema around the orifice","Vesicoureteric reflux in a duplex lower moiety"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal and bladder ultrasound to identify the intravesical cyst and duplex anatomy","Micturating cystourethrogram (MCUG) to assess reflux and ureterocele configuration","MAG3 or DMSA renography to assess function of the affected moiety","Cystoscopy to define the ureterocele and orifice anatomy","MR urography in complex duplex anatomy","Urinalysis and culture when infection is present"],"redFlags":["Bladder outlet obstruction or urinary retention from a large ectopic ureterocele","Prolapse of an ectopic ureterocele through the urethra in a girl","Febrile urinary tract infection or urosepsis with obstruction","Deteriorating function of the affected or contralateral renal unit"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the function of the affected moiety and on associated reflux and obstruction; simple orthotopic ureteroceles often do well with endoscopic or conservative management, while complex ectopic duplex ureteroceles may require staged reconstruction but generally preserve overall renal function with appropriate treatment.","pitfalls":["Forgetting the Weigert-Meyer rule and misidentifying which moiety the ureterocele drains","Endoscopic incision creating iatrogenic reflux into the decompressed moiety","Overlooking obstruction of the contralateral ureter or bladder outlet by a large ureterocele","Assuming a poorly functioning upper pole must be removed without confirming function"],"management":["Treat and, where recurrent, prophylax against urinary tract infection to protect the affected moiety and upper tracts","Perform endoscopic transurethral incision or puncture to decompress an obstructing or infected ureterocele, often as the initial intervention in children","Undertake definitive reconstruction such as upper-pole heminephrectomy for a non-functioning moiety, ureteric reimplantation, or excision with bladder-base repair for complex ectopic duplex ureteroceles","Observe small, incidental, orthotopic single-system ureteroceles with good drainage","Monitor with serial renal and bladder ultrasound and MAG3 or DMSA renography to track moiety function, reflux and obstruction","Manage through a paediatric urology service, coordinating staged reconstruction where required"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Gearhart, Rink & Mouriquand: Pediatric Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"ureteropelvic_junction_obstruction":{"name":"Ureteropelvic junction obstruction","system":"Renal / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pelviureteric junction obstruction","PUJ obstruction","UPJ obstruction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest cause of congenital hydronephrosis, producing dilatation of the renal pelvis and calyces with a non-dilated ureter below the obstruction","Frequently detected on antenatal ultrasound as unilateral hydronephrosis, though it may present later with loin pain, a palpable mass, infection, or haematuria","Intrinsic causes include an aperistaltic segment or intrinsic stenosis; extrinsic causes include a crossing lower-pole vessel, more relevant in older children and adults","The diuretic renogram (MAG3) is key: an obstructed pattern shows continued tracer accumulation with a rising or non-washout drainage curve after diuretic, whereas a prompt washout suggests no significant obstruction","Differential renal function on renography guides the decision to intervene or observe","Many mild antenatally detected cases stabilise or resolve, so serial imaging and expectant management are often appropriate","Surgical treatment is dismembered pyeloplasty when there is significant or deteriorating obstruction, symptoms, or falling function"],"pathophysiology":"Ureteropelvic junction obstruction is a functional or anatomical impedance to urine flow from the renal pelvis into the proximal ureter. Intrinsic obstruction usually results from a congenitally abnormal, aperistaltic or narrowed segment where disorganised smooth muscle and excess collagen prevent effective peristalsis, so a bolus of urine is not propagated across the junction. Extrinsic obstruction may be caused by an aberrant or accessory lower-pole renal artery crossing and kinking the junction. The resulting rise in intrapelvic pressure and stasis produces progressive pelvicalyceal dilatation and, if severe and prolonged, loss of renal parenchyma and function.","additionalDifferentials":["Vesicoureteric reflux causing pelvicalyceal dilatation","Primary obstructive megaureter (obstruction at the vesicoureteric junction)","Multicystic dysplastic kidney (non-communicating cysts, no function)","Extrarenal pelvis without true obstruction","Calyceal or parapelvic cysts","Ureteric obstruction from stone or stricture in older patients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasound to grade pelvicalyceal dilatation and assess parenchymal thickness","MAG3 diuretic renogram to assess drainage and differential function","Micturating cystourethrogram (MCUG) to exclude coexisting reflux","CT or MR urography to define anatomy and detect a crossing vessel, especially in adults","Serial ultrasound for surveillance of antenatally detected hydronephrosis","Urinalysis and culture if infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Declining differential renal function on serial renography","Increasing hydronephrosis or thinning renal parenchyma","Febrile urinary tract infection or pyonephrosis in an obstructed system","Severe or recurrent loin pain, or obstruction in a solitary kidney"],"prognosis":"Many mild antenatally detected cases resolve or remain stable with observation, and pyeloplasty for significant obstruction has a high success rate with preservation or improvement of function; severe long-standing obstruction, however, may leave irreversible parenchymal loss.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a diuretic renogram without accounting for a full bladder, dehydration, or an immature neonatal kidney, which can mimic obstruction","Rushing to surgery for mild stable hydronephrosis that would resolve spontaneously","Missing a crossing lower-pole vessel that alters the surgical approach","Overlooking coexisting vesicoureteric reflux before attributing all dilatation to junction obstruction"],"management":["Manage mild antenatally detected hydronephrosis expectantly with serial renal ultrasound and diuretic renography, since many cases stabilise or resolve","Perform dismembered (Anderson-Hynes) pyeloplasty for significant obstruction, deteriorating differential function, symptoms, stones, or recurrent infection","Transpose or relieve an obstructing crossing lower-pole vessel in extrinsic obstruction","Provide temporary drainage with a nephrostomy or JJ ureteric stent for an infected, obstructed system or acute decompensation","Treat associated urinary tract infection and offer antibiotic prophylaxis where indicated","Monitor drainage and preservation of differential renal function with post-operative renography and ultrasound surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Gearhart, Rink & Mouriquand: Pediatric Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"urethral_caruncle":{"name":"Urethral caruncle","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["caruncle of the urethra","urethral meatus caruncle","distal urethral polyp"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign, fleshy, reddish polypoid lesion at the posterior (dorsal) edge of the external urethral meatus","Overwhelmingly a condition of postmenopausal women, linked to oestrogen deficiency and atrophy of the urethral and vaginal tissues","Often asymptomatic and found incidentally, but may cause spotting or bleeding, dysuria, local discomfort, or pain on contact","Usually small and soft, and the diagnosis is clinical on inspection of the meatus","The main concern is distinguishing it from urethral carcinoma or prolapsed urethral mucosa, especially if the lesion is firm, irregular, ulcerated, or enlarging","Topical oestrogen and conservative measures suffice for most; surgical excision is reserved for large, symptomatic, or diagnostically uncertain lesions, with biopsy if malignancy is suspected","Recurrence can occur, and any atypical lesion should undergo histological examination"],"pathophysiology":"Urethral caruncles are thought to arise from oestrogen withdrawal after the menopause, which renders the distal urethral and periurethral mucosa atrophic and prone to eversion or prolapse at the meatus. The exposed tissue becomes chronically inflamed and vascular, forming a soft polypoid nodule composed of loose connective tissue, inflammatory cells, and a rich vascular network covered by transitional or squamous epithelium. The lesion is benign, but because it can bleed, ulcerate, and mimic malignancy, atypical examples warrant biopsy.","additionalDifferentials":["Urethral carcinoma","Urethral mucosal prolapse","Urethral diverticulum","Periurethral cyst or abscess","Condyloma or other meatal lesion","Prolapsed ureterocele (rare)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful clinical examination of the meatus","Biopsy or excision histology when features are atypical","Urinalysis and urine culture","Cystourethroscopy if intraluminal pathology is suspected","MRI or examination under anaesthesia for suspicious lesions"],"redFlags":["A firm, irregular, indurated, or ulcerated lesion","Rapid growth","Persistent bleeding","Associated inguinal lymphadenopathy","Failure to respond to topical oestrogen"],"prognosis":"Benign with an excellent prognosis; symptoms usually settle with conservative or topical oestrogen treatment, though caruncles may recur, and rare atypical lesions require exclusion of malignancy.","pitfalls":["Assuming any meatal lesion is a benign caruncle and missing a urethral carcinoma","Excising without histology when the features are atypical","Overlooking a treatable underlying oestrogen deficiency amenable to topical therapy","Confusing it with circumferential urethral mucosal prolapse, which is managed differently"],"management":["Offer first-line topical oestrogen cream to reverse the postmenopausal urethral atrophy that underlies the lesion","Advise conservative measures such as sitz baths, good local hygiene and topical emollients for symptomatic relief","Treat any associated local infection or inflammation","Reserve surgical excision with ligation or cautery of the base for large, persistently bleeding, symptomatic, or diagnostically uncertain caruncles","Perform biopsy to exclude urethral carcinoma when the lesion is firm, irregular, ulcerated, or enlarging","Review for recurrence, reinforcing maintenance topical oestrogen where appropriate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Blandy's Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"urethral_diverticulum":{"name":"Urethral diverticulum","system":"Urology / Andrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UD","female urethral diverticulum","periurethral diverticulum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An epithelium-lined outpouching arising from the urethra, most commonly in women along the mid or distal posterior urethra","The classic triad (the 'three Ds') is dysuria, dyspareunia, and post-void dribbling, though presentations vary widely","A tender anterior vaginal wall mass that may express pus or urine from the meatus on compression is characteristic","Symptoms are often chronic and non-specific, so diagnosis is frequently delayed and the condition is mistaken for recurrent cystitis or vaginitis","MRI of the pelvis and urethra is the most sensitive imaging test and best defines the anatomy; voiding cystourethrography, ultrasound, and urethroscopy also contribute","Management is surgical excision (diverticulectomy) with urethral reconstruction for symptomatic cases, while small asymptomatic diverticula may be observed","Complications include recurrent infection, stone formation within the diverticulum, and, rarely, malignancy arising in the diverticular lining"],"pathophysiology":"Most acquired female urethral diverticula are thought to arise from the periurethral (paraurethral or Skene's) glands. Repeated infection and obstruction of these glands lead to abscess formation, which then ruptures into the urethral lumen and leaves an epithelium-lined cavity communicating with the urethra through a neck. Urine and debris collect in this sac and drain intermittently, producing post-void dribbling and recurrent infection, while stasis predisposes to stone formation and, rarely, malignant change. The size and location of the sac and its ostium determine the clinical picture and the surgical approach.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent urinary tract infection or cystitis","Urethral caruncle","Skene's gland cyst or abscess","Vaginal wall (Gartner's duct) cyst","Urethral or vaginal carcinoma","Cystocele","Ectopic ureterocele"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the urethra and pelvis","Voiding cystourethrography","Transvaginal or transperineal ultrasound","Urethroscopy and cystoscopy","Urinalysis and urine culture","Double-balloon positive-pressure urethrography where MRI is unavailable"],"redFlags":["A firm or fixed mass or a solid component suggesting malignancy within the diverticulum","Haematuria","Rapid enlargement","A stone within the diverticulum with severe pain","Recurrent sepsis"],"prognosis":"Good after complete surgical excision, with most patients gaining symptom relief; recurrence, urethrovaginal fistula, or stress incontinence can complicate surgery, and rare diverticular malignancy worsens the outlook.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating 'recurrent cystitis' without considering a diverticulum as the underlying cause","Relying on examination alone and not obtaining dedicated MRI to define the anatomy","Missing a malignancy or a stone arising within the diverticulum","Incomplete excision leaving a residual sac and causing recurrence or fistula"],"management":["Perform transvaginal diverticulectomy with complete excision of the sac and multilayer urethral reconstruction as the definitive treatment for symptomatic disease","Treat recurrent urinary tract infection with antibiotics and drain any associated periurethral abscess before elective repair","Observe small, incidental, asymptomatic diverticula","Excise and manage any calculus or malignancy identified within the diverticulum","Plan surgery with MRI of the urethra and pelvis to define sac and ostium anatomy, and use an interposition flap where fistula risk is high","Monitor for post-operative complications including recurrence, urethrovaginal fistula and de novo stress incontinence, managing through a specialist urology or urogynaecology service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Blandy's Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"urethral_prolapse":{"name":"Urethral Prolapse","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["urethral prolapse","urethral prolapsus","prolapsed urethra","urethral mucosal prolapse"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Urethral prolapse is circumferential eversion of the distal urethral mucosa through the external meatus, forming a doughnut-shaped ring of tissue around the urethral opening","It has a characteristic bimodal distribution, occurring in prepubertal girls and in postmenopausal women, both low-oestrogen states","Typical presentations are painless vaginal or vulval bleeding or spotting, a visible periurethral mass, and sometimes dysuria or voiding difficulty","In young girls it is an important and benign cause of genital bleeding that can be mistaken for trauma or abuse, so recognition prevents misdirected concern","The key clinical feature is that the prolapsed mucosa surrounds the meatus symmetrically, distinguishing it from an eccentric caruncle or other periurethral lesion","It must be differentiated from a urethral caruncle, prolapsing ureterocele, periurethral cyst, and, importantly, urethral or other malignancy in older women","Uncomplicated cases are often managed conservatively with topical oestrogen, sitz baths, and treatment of any constipation or straining","Surgical excision of the prolapsed mucosa is reserved for strangulated, thrombosed, necrotic, or non-resolving cases, or when the diagnosis is uncertain and tissue is needed","A strangulated, thrombosed, or necrotic prolapse causing pain or urinary retention warrants prompt urological assessment"],"pathophysiology":"Urethral prolapse results from eversion of the distal urethral mucosa through the meatus. A relative deficiency of oestrogen, as in prepubertal girls and postmenopausal women, weakens the periurethral supporting tissues and the attachments between the inner longitudinal and outer circular smooth muscle layers of the urethra. Poor cohesion between these layers, combined with raised intra-abdominal pressure from straining, coughing, or constipation, allows the redundant mucosa to protrude and evert around the meatus. The exposed mucosa becomes congested, oedematous, and friable, and if venous drainage is compromised it may thrombose, strangulate, or necrose.","additionalDifferentials":["Urethral caruncle","Prolapsing ureterocele","Periurethral cyst or abscess (e.g. Skene gland)","Urethral or periurethral malignancy","Vaginal wall prolapse or cystocele","Condyloma or other periurethral lesion","Genital trauma (a key consideration in children)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful clinical examination confirming circumferential eversion around the meatus","Confirmation that the mass surrounds the urethral opening, often by passing or visualising a catheter through the central lumen","Urinalysis and culture if infection or dysuria is present","Biopsy or excision histology where malignancy is suspected, particularly in older women","Examination under anaesthesia in children if the diagnosis is unclear or examination is difficult"],"redFlags":["Strangulated, thrombosed, or necrotic prolapsed mucosa","Acute urinary retention from the prolapse","Suspicion of malignancy in a postmenopausal woman with an atypical or firm periurethral lesion","Uncertain diagnosis where abuse or trauma is being wrongly considered in a child, or genuine trauma is missed","Heavy or persistent bleeding"],"prognosis":"Urethral prolapse is benign and generally responds well to conservative measures, particularly topical oestrogen, with a good prognosis and low recurrence when precipitating factors such as straining are addressed. Surgical excision is curative for complicated or refractory cases. Prompt recognition in children avoids unnecessary distress and investigation, while vigilance in older women ensures malignancy is not overlooked.","pitfalls":["Mistaking benign prolapse in a child for signs of trauma or abuse, or vice versa","Confusing it with a urethral caruncle or, in older women, with malignancy","Overlooking a strangulated or necrotic prolapse needing surgical intervention","Failing to address straining and constipation, allowing recurrence","Not obtaining histology when the lesion is atypical in a postmenopausal woman"],"management":["Offer first-line topical oestrogen cream to restore the atrophic periurethral tissues in prepubertal girls and postmenopausal women","Advise conservative measures including sitz baths, good hygiene and treatment of the local inflammation","Address precipitating raised intra-abdominal pressure by treating constipation, chronic cough and straining","Attempt gentle manual reduction, and manage a strangulated, thrombosed or necrotic prolapse as an acute problem","Reserve surgical excision of the redundant prolapsed mucosa for refractory, strangulated or necrotic tissue","Reassure that the condition is benign, and in young girls recognise it explicitly to avoid misattributing genital bleeding to trauma or abuse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"urethral_stricture":{"name":"Urethral stricture","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["urethral stenosis","urethral narrowing","stricture urethra"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A urethral stricture is a fibrotic narrowing of the urethral lumen, largely a condition of the male anterior urethra, presenting with obstructive voiding symptoms such as a weak, splayed, or spraying stream, straining, and incomplete emptying","Common causes include prior instrumentation or catheterisation, straddle and pelvic trauma, infection (historically gonococcal urethritis), and lichen sclerosus affecting the meatus and distal urethra","Symptoms overlap with prostatic obstruction, but a young man or a history of trauma, catheterisation, or urethritis should raise suspicion of stricture","Uroflowmetry classically shows a prolonged, low, plateau-shaped flow curve, and retrograde urethrography with voiding studies defines the site, length, and number of strictures","Complications include recurrent urinary infection, bladder stones, diverticula, and, in longstanding severe disease, back-pressure effects on the upper tracts and acute retention","Management principles depend on stricture length and location: dilatation or internal urethrotomy for short strictures, and urethroplasty for longer, recurrent, or complex disease, which offers the most durable cure","Repeated dilatation or urethrotomy for recurrent strictures tends to fail and may worsen fibrosis, so early referral for reconstructive urethroplasty is preferred in appropriate cases","Meatal or distal strictures should prompt evaluation for lichen sclerosus"],"pathophysiology":"A urethral stricture is the result of scarring within the corpus spongiosum (spongiofibrosis) and urethral epithelium following injury or inflammation. Trauma, infection, instrumentation, or inflammatory disease such as lichen sclerosus provokes healing by fibrosis, which contracts and narrows the lumen. The narrowed segment increases outflow resistance, causing high-pressure voiding, turbulent flow, and incomplete emptying. Persistent obstruction leads to proximal urethral dilatation, bladder wall changes, stasis with infection and stone formation, and in severe cases upper tract dilatation and retention.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia / bladder outlet obstruction","Bladder neck stenosis or contracture","Prostate cancer","Meatal stenosis","Neurogenic bladder","Urethral or periurethral tumour","Detrusor underactivity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Uroflowmetry demonstrating a low, plateau-shaped flow pattern","Retrograde urethrography with voiding cystourethrography to localise and measure the stricture","Flexible urethroscopy to inspect the stricture directly","Urinalysis and culture for associated infection","Post-void residual and renal tract ultrasound to assess retention and upper tracts","Assessment for lichen sclerosus in distal or meatal strictures"],"redFlags":["Acute urinary retention from a tight stricture, a urological emergency requiring urgent drainage","Obstructive uropathy with upper tract dilatation and renal impairment","Recurrent urinary infection, periurethral abscess, or urethral fistula","Failure of a difficult catheterisation, which should prompt suprapubic drainage rather than forceful attempts","Suspicion of urethral malignancy in an atypical or non-healing stricture"],"prognosis":"Short strictures may respond to dilatation or urethrotomy but recurrence is common; urethroplasty provides high long-term success for suitable strictures, whereas complex, recurrent, or lichen sclerosus-associated disease can require staged or repeated reconstruction.","pitfalls":["Attributing obstructive symptoms to the prostate in a younger man and missing a stricture","Repeated dilatation or urethrotomy for recurrent strictures, worsening scarring and delaying definitive repair","Forcing a urethral catheter through a stricture, risking false passage and injury","Overlooking lichen sclerosus in distal strictures","Failing to assess the upper tracts and renal function in longstanding obstruction"],"management":["Treat short strictures with urethral dilatation or direct-vision internal urethrotomy, counselling that recurrence after these procedures is common","Offer urethroplasty (excision and primary anastomosis, or buccal mucosal graft or flap repair) for long, recurrent, or complex strictures as the most durable cure","Treat meatal and distal lichen sclerosus with potent topical corticosteroids and use graft rather than local skin in reconstruction","Relieve acute urinary retention with a suprapubic catheter when urethral access is not possible","Treat associated urinary infection and bladder stones and address back-pressure effects on the upper tracts","Consider intermittent self-dilatation in selected patients to maintain luminal patency","Monitor outcome with uroflowmetry, post-void residual measurement and symptom review, with upper-tract surveillance in severe disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"urethritis":{"name":"Urethritis","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["urethral inflammation","gonococcal urethritis","non-gonococcal urethritis","NGU"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Urethritis is inflammation of the urethra presenting with urethral discharge, dysuria, and urethral itch or discomfort, most often sexually transmitted","It is broadly divided into gonococcal urethritis (Neisseria gonorrhoeae) and non-gonococcal urethritis, of which Chlamydia trachomatis is the leading cause, along with Mycoplasma genitalium and others","Gonococcal urethritis classically produces a profuse purulent discharge, whereas non-gonococcal urethritis tends to cause a scantier, more mucoid discharge, though overlap is common and co-infection frequent","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating urethral inflammation (urethral smear with leucocytes or a positive leucocyte esterase on first-void urine) together with nucleic acid amplification testing for the causative organisms","Management principles include empirical antibiotic therapy covering both gonococcal and chlamydial infection, guided by local resistance patterns, plus abstinence until treatment is complete","Partner notification, treatment of sexual contacts, and a full sexually transmitted infection screen (including HIV and syphilis) are essential components of care","Persistent or recurrent urethritis after treatment may reflect reinfection, non-adherence, or organisms such as Mycoplasma genitalium with antimicrobial resistance","Reactive arthritis (urethritis, conjunctivitis, arthritis) may follow chlamydial urethritis, particularly in HLA-B27-positive individuals"],"pathophysiology":"Urethritis is inflammation of the urethral mucosa, usually caused by sexually transmitted organisms ascending the urethra. Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis are the principal pathogens, with Mycoplasma genitalium, Trichomonas vaginalis, and other organisms also implicated. Infection provokes a neutrophilic inflammatory response in the urethral epithelium, producing discharge and dysuria. Non-infective urethritis can follow trauma or instrumentation. Untreated infection may ascend or spread, causing epididymo-orchitis, prostatitis, disseminated gonococcal infection, or, historically, inflammatory urethral stricture, and in women pelvic inflammatory disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystitis or lower urinary tract infection","Prostatitis","Epididymo-orchitis","Balanitis","Reactive arthritis-associated urethritis","Non-infective (traumatic or chemical) urethritis","Foreign body or instrumentation-related irritation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["First-void urine or urethral swab nucleic acid amplification testing for gonorrhoea and chlamydia","Urethral Gram-stain smear for leucocytes and Gram-negative intracellular diplococci","Culture and antimicrobial sensitivities for Neisseria gonorrhoeae given resistance concerns","Testing for Mycoplasma genitalium and Trichomonas where indicated","Full sexually transmitted infection screen including HIV and syphilis serology","Urinalysis to help exclude urinary tract infection"],"redFlags":["Features of disseminated gonococcal infection (fever, skin lesions, arthritis)","Ascending infection causing epididymo-orchitis or prostatitis","Persistent or recurrent urethritis suggesting resistant organism or reinfection","Systemic sexually transmitted co-infection such as HIV requiring identification","Reactive arthritis with ocular or joint involvement"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated urethritis usually resolves rapidly with appropriate antibiotics and partner treatment; outcomes are worsened by reinfection, non-adherence, resistant organisms, and complications such as epididymo-orchitis or reactive arthritis.","pitfalls":["Treating for chlamydia alone and missing co-existing gonococcal infection (or vice versa)","Neglecting partner notification and treatment, leading to reinfection","Failing to perform a full STI screen including HIV and syphilis","Overlooking Mycoplasma genitalium in persistent non-gonococcal urethritis","Not counselling on abstinence until treatment and partner therapy are complete"],"management":["Give empirical antimicrobial therapy covering both gonococcal and chlamydial infection, using intramuscular ceftriaxone for Neisseria gonorrhoeae and doxycycline for Chlamydia trachomatis, guided by local resistance patterns","Send gonococcal culture with sensitivities before treatment given rising cephalosporin resistance, and treat identified Mycoplasma genitalium (macrolide or moxifloxacin) or Trichomonas vaginalis (metronidazole) accordingly","Advise abstinence from sexual contact until the patient and partners have completed treatment","Arrange partner notification and epidemiological treatment of recent sexual contacts","Perform a full sexually transmitted infection screen including HIV and syphilis serology","Reassess persistent or recurrent symptoms for reinfection, resistance or non-gonococcal causes, and manage complications such as epididymo-orchitis or reactive arthritis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"uric_acid_nephrolithiasis":{"name":"Uric acid nephrolithiasis","system":"Renal / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["uric acid stones","uric acid kidney stones","urate stones","uric acid urolithiasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Radiolucent stones that are invisible on plain radiographs but are readily seen on non-contrast CT, where they are of low density","The single most important factor is a persistently acidic urine, which favours the insoluble undissociated uric acid over soluble urate","Strongly associated with gout, the metabolic syndrome, insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes and obesity","Other contributors include a high purine intake, chronic diarrhoea with volume depletion, and states of rapid cell turnover such as tumour lysis or myeloproliferative disease","Urine microscopy may show rhomboid or rosette-shaped urate crystals in a characteristically acidic urine","Unlike most stones, established uric acid stones can often be dissolved medically rather than removed surgically","Management centres on urinary alkalinisation with potassium citrate to raise the urine pH toward neutral, a generous fluid intake, and allopurinol where hyperuricosuria persists","Reducing dietary purine and treating the underlying metabolic syndrome are important adjuncts"],"pathophysiology":"Uric acid, the end product of purine metabolism, exists as a soluble ionised urate form and a relatively insoluble undissociated uric acid form, and the balance between them is governed chiefly by urine pH. Below a pH of about 5.5 the poorly soluble undissociated form predominates and precipitates. Patients with uric acid stones characteristically have an unduly acidic urine, frequently because insulin resistance impairs renal ammonium excretion, together with a low urine volume and, in some, hyperuricosuria from gout, high purine intake or rapid cell turnover. The combination of low pH, concentrated urine and increased urate delivery drives crystallisation.","additionalDifferentials":["Calcium oxalate stones","Xanthine or 2,8-dihydroxyadenine stones, which are also radiolucent","Radiolucent matrix stones","A sloughed papilla mimicking a filling defect","Urothelial tumour producing a radiolucent filling defect","Blood clot within the collecting system"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Non-contrast CT, noting the characteristically low density of a uric acid stone","Repeated fresh urine pH measurement, which is typically persistently acidic","24-hour urine for volume, uric acid, pH and citrate","Serum urate and a metabolic screen for diabetes and insulin resistance","Analysis of any retrieved stone","Full blood count and urate where myeloproliferative disease or tumour lysis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Obstruction with superimposed infection and sepsis","Acute kidney injury from massive urate crystal deposition, as in tumour lysis syndrome","A rapidly enlarging stone burden in the context of a myeloproliferative disorder"],"prognosis":"Uric acid stones respond well to urinary alkalinisation and can often be dissolved without surgery; long-term recurrence is low provided the urine pH and volume are maintained and the underlying metabolic disorder is addressed.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal plain radiograph excludes a stone, since uric acid stones are radiolucent","Over-alkalinising the urine, which risks precipitating calcium phosphate stones","Failing to recognise and treat the underlying metabolic syndrome and insulin resistance","Introducing allopurinol during an acute gout flare without appropriate cover"],"management":["Alkalinise the urine with potassium citrate (or sodium bicarbonate) to shift the balance towards soluble urate, which can dissolve existing stones without surgery","Increase fluid intake to maintain a high, dilute urine output and reduce crystallisation","Lower urate production with allopurinol (a xanthine oxidase inhibitor) in patients with hyperuricosuria, gout, or high cell turnover","Advise dietary reduction of purine and animal-protein intake, weight loss, and treatment of the associated insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome","Manage acute renal colic with analgesia and medical expulsive therapy, and correct volume depletion from chronic diarrhoea","Reserve ureteroscopy, lithotripsy or percutaneous nephrolithotomy for obstructing, infected, or non-dissolving stones","Monitor with serial urine pH, 24-hour urine chemistry and stone analysis to confirm response and prevent recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology (Feehally et al.)","Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"urinary_incontinence":{"name":"Urinary incontinence","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UI","involuntary urine leakage","loss of bladder control"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Urinary incontinence is any involuntary leakage of urine, and classifying the type guides management: stress, urgency, mixed, overflow, and functional (continuous) incontinence","Stress incontinence is leakage with raised intra-abdominal pressure (coughing, laughing, exertion) due to sphincter or pelvic floor weakness, common after childbirth and in men after prostatectomy","Urgency incontinence is leakage accompanied by a sudden compelling desire to void, usually from detrusor overactivity, and is part of the overactive bladder syndrome","Overflow incontinence is dribbling from a chronically distended, poorly emptying bladder, often due to outlet obstruction or an underactive detrusor, and must not be missed","A focused history, examination (including pelvic and neurological), a bladder (frequency-volume) diary, and post-void residual measurement usually clarify the type","First-line management of stress incontinence is pelvic floor muscle training, while urgency incontinence is treated with bladder training and antimuscarinic or beta-3 agonist therapy","Reversible contributors summarised by the DIAPPERS concept (delirium, infection, atrophic changes, pharmaceuticals, psychological, excess output, restricted mobility, stool impaction) should be sought and corrected","Surgical options such as mid-urethral slings or colposuspension are considered for stress incontinence, and intravesical botulinum toxin or neuromodulation for refractory urgency incontinence"],"pathophysiology":"Continence requires coordinated bladder storage at low pressure with a competent, closed outlet, governed by autonomic and somatic innervation. Stress incontinence arises from a weakened pelvic floor and urethral sphincter (from childbirth, ageing, or prostatic surgery) so that raised abdominal pressure overcomes closure. Urgency incontinence results from involuntary detrusor contractions (detrusor overactivity), which may be idiopathic or neurogenic. Overflow incontinence occurs when a chronically overdistended bladder leaks around an obstruction or fails to contract effectively, as in outlet obstruction or an underactive/neurogenic detrusor. Mixed incontinence combines these mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Stress urinary incontinence","Urgency incontinence / overactive bladder","Mixed incontinence","Overflow incontinence from obstruction or detrusor underactivity","Neurogenic bladder","Urinary tract infection","Vesicovaginal or other urinary fistula (continuous leakage)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis to exclude infection and haematuria","Bladder (frequency-volume) diary","Post-void residual volume measurement","Cough stress test and pelvic examination (assessing prolapse and pelvic floor)","Urodynamic studies in complex, mixed, or pre-surgical cases","Cystoscopy or imaging when fistula, malignancy, or structural abnormality is suspected"],"redFlags":["Associated haematuria requiring exclusion of urinary tract malignancy","New incontinence with neurological signs suggesting cord compression or cauda equina syndrome","Overflow incontinence from unrecognised chronic retention with risk of renal impairment","Continuous leakage suggesting a urinary fistula","Recurrent urinary infection or significant post-void residual"],"prognosis":"Many patients improve substantially with conservative measures and correction of reversible factors; stress and urgency incontinence respond well to targeted therapy, and surgery offers durable benefit for refractory stress incontinence, though outcomes vary with cause and comorbidity.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling overflow incontinence as urgency and prescribing antimuscarinics that worsen retention","Failing to measure post-void residual before treating","Overlooking reversible causes such as infection, medications, or stool impaction","Missing haematuria or a neurological cause behind new incontinence","Recommending surgery without adequate assessment of the incontinence type"],"management":["Classify the incontinence type and correct reversible contributors, advising weight loss, caffeine and fluid moderation, and treatment of constipation","Treat stress incontinence first with supervised pelvic floor muscle training, adding duloxetine as an adjunct and reserving mid-urethral sling, colposuspension or urethral bulking agents, and an artificial urinary sphincter after prostatectomy, for refractory cases","Treat urgency incontinence and overactive bladder with bladder retraining plus an antimuscarinic or a beta-3 agonist such as mirabegron","Escalate refractory urgency incontinence to intravesical botulinum toxin, percutaneous tibial nerve stimulation, or sacral neuromodulation","Manage overflow incontinence by relieving outlet obstruction and using clean intermittent self-catheterisation for an underactive or neurogenic detrusor","Provide containment aids and skin care, and refer for urodynamic assessment in complex, mixed, neurogenic or pre-surgical cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"urinary_tract_infections_cystitis_prostati":{"name":"Urinary Tract Infections: Cystitis, Prostatitis, and Pyelonephritis","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UTI","Acute cystitis","Acute bacterial prostatitis (ABP)","Chronic bacterial prostatitis (CBP)","Pyelonephritis","Catheter-associated UTI (CAUTI)"],"chapter":140,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1086","clinicalPearls":["UTI denotes symptomatic infection somewhere along the urinary tract; bacteria in urine WITHOUT related symptoms is asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB), not UTI, and generally should NOT be treated (Harrison 22e p.1086).","Treatment of ASB is discouraged except in two settings: pregnancy (untreated ASB is linked to pyelonephritis, preterm delivery, and low birth weight) and before urologic procedures causing mucosal injury such as lithotripsy or transurethral prostate resection (Harrison 22e p.1086).","Cystitis presents with dysuria, frequency, and urgency, often with suprapubic discomfort, new nocturia, hesitancy, or gross hematuria; fever, rigors, and unilateral flank/back pain are INCONSISTENT with uncomplicated cystitis and point to infection beyond the bladder (Harrison 22e p.1088).","Fever is a key feature distinguishing cystitis from pyelonephritis; mild pyelonephritis may show only low-grade fever with or without costovertebral angle pain, while severe disease brings high fever, rigors, nausea, vomiting, and flank pain (Harrison 22e p.1089).","E. coli is the predominant uropathogen across UTI syndromes, accounting for roughly 75-90% of acute cystitis isolates; Staphylococcus saprophyticus causes about 5-15% (more in younger women) (Harrison 22e p.1087).","Isolation of S. aureus or Salmonella from urine should raise suspicion of concomitant bacteremia, since hematogenous seeding (<2% of UTIs) typically involves these more virulent organisms (Harrison 22e p.1088).","Acute bacterial prostatitis (ABP) is a typically severe illness with dysuria, frequency, and prostatic/pelvic/perineal pain, sometimes with fever, rigors, or bladder outlet obstruction; chronic bacterial prostatitis (CBP) presents insidiously as recurrent cystitis episodes, often without systemic signs (Harrison 22e p.1088).","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome (formerly chronic prostatitis) is far more common than bacterial prostatitis, is NOT associated with bacteriuria, and does NOT improve with antibiotics; obtaining urine cultures in male UTI helps distinguish these entities (Harrison 22e p.1088).","Diagnostic stewardship - not culturing urine in patients lacking UTI signs/symptoms (except pregnancy or pre-urologic-procedure screening) - is the key intervention to reduce misdiagnosis and overtreatment of ASB (Harrison 22e p.1088).","In catheterized patients bacteriuria and pyuria are essentially inevitable, so an abnormal urinalysis alone cannot diagnose CAUTI; localizing or systemic symptoms must be present and other causes excluded (Harrison 22e p.1090).","Recurrent UTI is not automatically 'complicated' - individual episodes can be uncomplicated; early recurrence (within 2 weeks) is usually relapse rather than reinfection and should prompt a search for a sequestered focus (Harrison 22e pp.1086-1087)."],"pathophysiology":"The urinary tract behaves as a continuous column of urine from kidney to urethra. Most UTIs arise by ascending infection: periurethral and vaginal-introital colonization by intestinal flora (usually E. coli) is the critical first step, followed by ascent up the urethra to the bladder and, in renal infection, up the ureter to the kidney. Hematogenous seeding accounts for less than 2% of UTIs and usually involves virulent organisms (e.g., S. aureus, Salmonella). Outcome depends on the interplay of host, pathogen, and environmental factors. The flushing action of normal voiding clears bacteria; urinary stasis, obstruction, foreign bodies (stones, catheters), and biofilm formation favor infection. Uropathogenic E. coli express virulence factors: type 1 pili bind mannose on bladder uroepithelium to initiate cystitis, while P fimbriae bind the P blood-group antigen on renal epithelium and are important in pyelonephritis and bloodstream invasion; toxins, iron-acquisition systems, biofilms, and capsules also contribute. Host susceptibility has a hereditary component (familial/maternal history; non-secretor blood-group phenotypes increasing E. coli adherence; Toll-like receptor and IL-8 receptor gene variants). Disruption of vaginal lactobacilli (spermicide/nonoxynol-9, menopausal atrophy) promotes E. coli colonization (Harrison 22e pp.1087-1088).","additionalDifferentials":["Asymptomatic bacteriuria (positive culture without localizing symptoms)","Sexually transmitted infections - urethritis/cervicitis from Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Mycoplasma genitalium","Vaginitis (Candida albicans, Trichomonas vaginalis)","Herpetic urethritis","Interstitial cystitis","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome (in men)","Noninfectious vulvar/vaginal irritation"],"infectionMimics":["Cervicitis due to Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Vaginitis (Trichomonas vaginalis, Candida albicans)","Herpetic urethritis","Urethritis from Mycoplasma genitalium"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Interstitial cystitis / bladder pain syndrome","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome (noninfectious prostatic pain without bacteriuria)","Noninfectious vaginal or vulvar irritation","Delirium from non-urinary causes mislabeled as UTI in older adults with incidental ASB"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine dipstick for nitrite and leukocyte esterase (best used to help rule out UTI; only Enterobacterales convert nitrate to nitrite, so non-Enterobacterales and dilute frequently-voided urine can be falsely negative)","Microscopic urinalysis for pyuria and hematuria","Urine culture with susceptibilities - warranted in pregnancy, suspected resistant organisms, recurrent or complicated UTI, and all male UTI; ASB screening in pregnancy must use culture because dipstick is not sufficiently sensitive","Blood cultures when fever or systemic signs suggest infection beyond the bladder","Imaging (CT or ultrasound) in men with febrile UTI (even first episode) to evaluate for obstruction or anatomic abnormality; suspect intraparenchymal abscess with persistent fever/bacteremia despite therapy","Urologic consultation for unclear diagnosis or recurrent UTI in men"],"redFlags":["Fever, rigors, or unilateral flank/back pain in a patient with urinary symptoms (suggests pyelonephritis, prostatitis, or bloodstream involvement)","Severe pyelonephritis with high fever, rigors, nausea, vomiting, and flank pain","Emphysematous pyelonephritis (gas in renal/perinephric tissue), occurring almost exclusively in poorly controlled diabetes","Acute papillary necrosis causing obstructive uropathy; bilateral papillary necrosis may present with rapidly rising serum creatinine","Continued fever and/or bacteremia despite antibiotics (suggests intraparenchymal renal abscess)","Signs of sepsis in an older patient with altered mentation","S. aureus or Salmonella in urine (consider concomitant bacteremia and metastatic prostatic focus)","Bladder outlet obstruction with acute bacterial prostatitis"],"prognosis":"UTI is common and patients typically respond favorably and rapidly to appropriately chosen modern antimicrobials. Uncomplicated cystitis in low-risk women carries a low risk of serious complications and can often be managed remotely. About 20-30% of women with one UTI develop recurrences (average ~2.6 reinfections/year; rate 0.3-7.6 per patient-year), with likelihood of recurrence decreasing as time since the last infection lengthens. Up to 80% of women have at least one UTI in their lifetime; lifetime prevalence in U.S. men is about 14%. Untreated ASB in pregnancy carries risk of pyelonephritis, preterm delivery, and low birth weight. Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis causes suppurative destruction of renal tissue and its mass effect can mimic renal malignancy (Harrison 22e pp.1086-1089).","pitfalls":["Diagnosing UTI from pyuria/bacteriuria alone without requiring symptoms - this conflates ASB with true UTI; degree of pyuria in an asymptomatic patient should not drive the diagnosis (very high pyuria is common with urinary stasis, ESRD, or obstructed catheters).","Treating ASB outside pregnancy or pre-urologic-procedure settings - it does not reduce future UTIs and may even increase infection risk (e.g., in renal transplant recipients).","Premature diagnostic closure: attributing an older patient's isolated altered mentation to 'UTI' without excluding sepsis and other time-sensitive causes of delirium; a reported history of recurrent UTIs may actually reflect persistent ASB.","Relying on a negative dipstick to rule out UTI - false negatives occur with non-Enterobacterales organisms, dilute frequently-voided urine, and it is insufficiently sensitive to exclude bacteriuria in pregnancy.","Mistaking an STI (especially C. trachomatis) for UTI, or treating dysuria due to vaginitis/cervicitis as UTI; vaginal discharge should prompt STI evaluation.","Assuming all male UTI is 'complicated' or that complicated-UTI guidelines apply to prostatitis; not all men with UTI have urinary tract abnormalities, especially those 45 years or younger.","Misinterpreting a mixed-flora culture as infection - usually contamination except in long-term catheterization, chronic retention, or urinary-enteric/genital fistula."],"management":["Do not treat asymptomatic bacteriuria except in pregnancy and before urological procedures that breach the mucosa","Treat uncomplicated cystitis with a short course of a first-line oral agent such as nitrofurantoin, trimethoprim, fosfomycin or pivmecillinam, guided by local resistance","Treat acute pyelonephritis with a fluoroquinolone or a cephalosporin, using intravenous therapy in systemically unwell patients and de-escalating to culture-directed treatment","Treat prostatitis with a prolonged course of a prostate-penetrating agent such as a fluoroquinolone or trimethoprim","Achieve source control by relieving obstruction, removing or exchanging catheters, and draining any renal or prostatic abscess","Investigate men and complicated or recurrent infection with imaging, and reduce recurrence with behavioural measures, antibiotic prophylaxis and vaginal oestrogen in postmenopausal women","Send urine culture with susceptibilities in pregnancy, male, recurrent, complicated or treatment-failing infection, and take blood cultures when systemic features suggest infection beyond the bladder"],"severityClassification":"Clinical syndrome-based: asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB) vs symptomatic UTI. Uncomplicated UTI = infection confined to the bladder (cystitis) in a woman or man without a urinary catheter. Complicated UTI = infection extending beyond the bladder (prostatitis, pyelonephritis, bacteremia) or in the setting of catheterization (CAUTI). Pyelonephritis is graded clinically from mild (low-grade fever +/- back/CVA pain) to severe (high fever, rigors, nausea, vomiting, flank pain). Severe/special forms: emphysematous pyelonephritis and xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis. Lab/diagnostic thresholds: pyuria conventionally >10 WBCs/HPF (poor specificity ~36%; a cutoff nearer 250 WBCs/HPF correlates better with symptoms in older women); for women with cystitis symptoms a colony count >10^2 bacteria/mL is more sensitive (95%) than the classic 10^5/mL; CAUTI bacteriuria threshold is >=10^3 CFU/mL (Harrison 22e pp.1086-1092).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1086","Harrison 22e p.1087","Harrison 22e p.1088","Harrison 22e p.1089","Harrison 22e p.1090","Harrison 22e p.1091","Harrison 22e p.1092"],"crossLinks":[]},"urinary_tract_obstruction":{"name":"Urinary Tract Obstruction","system":"Nephrology / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["obstructive uropathy","hydronephrosis"],"chapter":331,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2453","clinicalPearls":["Urinary tract obstruction (UTO) is a common and potentially reversible cause of both acute and chronic kidney disease (obstructive nephropathy); early recognition and prompt relief can prevent or reverse devastating effects on kidney structure and function (p.2453).","Any patient with otherwise unexplained renal failure, or with a history of nephrolithiasis, hematuria, diabetes mellitus, prostatic enlargement, pelvic surgery, trauma, or tumor should be evaluated for UTO (p.2455-2456).","When suspected, a bladder catheter should be inserted and abdominal ultrasonography performed to assess renal/bladder size and pyelocalyceal contour; bladder volume can be readily assessed at the bedside by ultrasound (p.2454-2455).","Flank pain is the most common presenting symptom and is due to distention of the collecting system or renal capsule; its severity is influenced more by the rate at which distention develops than by the degree of distention (p.2454).","Complete bilateral obstruction should be suspected when acute renal failure is accompanied by anuria (p.2455).","Wide fluctuations in urine output in an azotemic patient should always raise the possibility of intermittent or partial UTO (p.2455).","Azotemia with hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis (acquired distal renal tubular acidosis pattern) should prompt consideration of UTO (p.2455).","Flank pain that occurs only with micturition is pathognomonic of vesicoureteral reflux (p.2455).","Lower obstruction above the bladder causes unilateral hydroureter and hydronephrosis, whereas lesions at or below the bladder cause bilateral involvement (p.2454).","Posterior urethral valves are the most common cause of bilateral hydronephrosis in boys; in adults UTO is usually due to acquired defects, with pelvic tumors, calculi, and urethral stricture predominating (p.2454).","As many as 50% of men aged >40 years may have lower urinary tract symptoms associated with benign prostatic hypertrophy, but these symptoms may occur without bladder outlet obstruction (p.2454).","Noncontrast CT is the imaging test of choice for acute flank pain and improves visualization of the urinary tract in renal impairment; retrograde/antegrade urography carry no risk of contrast-induced AKI in patients with renal insufficiency (p.2455-2456)."],"pathophysiology":"Obstruction to urine flow produces stasis and a rise in hydrostatic pressure proximal to the blockade; this pressure is transmitted to the glomerular urinary space, decreasing or stopping filtration. Acutely, bilateral obstruction increases renal blood flow with rising ureteral/tubule pressures (mediated by vasodilator prostaglandins and nitric oxide), then renal blood flow and GFR fall with chronicity as vasoconstrictor prostaglandins and renin-angiotensin production increase. Tubular dysfunction develops: loss of medullary hypertonicity and concentrating ability (AVP-insensitive polyuria, a form of acquired nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from dysregulation of aquaporin-2 water channels), natriuresis from downregulation of proximal salt reabsorption and transporters (Na+/K+-ATPase, NKCC2, ENaC), and partial bilateral UTO causing acquired distal RTA with hyperkalemia and renal salt wasting (disrupted H+-ATPase trafficking in alpha-intercalated cells, reduced ENaC-driven Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion, impaired ammonium excretion). The renal interstitium becomes edematous with mononuclear infiltrate early; later interstitial fibrosis and atrophy of papillae and medulla precede cortical involvement. Increased angiotensin II drives inflammation and fibroblast accumulation via profibrotic cytokines, progressing to chronic kidney damage and permanent loss of renal mass (renal atrophy). Obstruction can be mechanical (intrinsic or extrinsic) at any level from the renal tubules to the urethral meatus, or functional from abnormal pontine/sacral micturition control (e.g., neurogenic bladder). Genetic/fetal causes (CAKUT, LUTO) reflect reduced nephron number, as there is no nephrogenesis after birth (p.2453-2456).","additionalDifferentials":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia (bladder outlet obstruction)","Carcinoma of prostate, bladder, cervix, colon, or rectum","Retroperitoneal fibrosis","Lymphoma (particularly follicular) with retroperitoneal involvement","Calculi (ureteric, bladder, urethral)","Urethral stricture","Ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) narrowing/obstruction","Vesicoureteral reflux","Posterior urethral valves (boys)","Neurogenic bladder / functional obstruction (diabetic neuropathy, spinal cord disease, cauda equina syndrome)","Pregnant uterus / uterine leiomyomata","Accidental surgical ligation or injury of the ureter during pelvic or colonic surgery","Aortic aneurysm (extrinsic ureteric compression)","Drug-related urinary retention (anticholinergics, alpha-adrenergic agonists, opiates)"],"infectionMimics":["Pyelonephritis (urinary stasis from obstruction encourages organism growth; UTO must always be considered with UTIs)","Generalized sepsis from an infected obstructed system"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Prerenal azotemia (acute partial bilateral obstruction may mimic it with high BUN-to-creatinine ratio, concentrated urine, and sodium retention)","Renal cystic disease (congenital UPJ obstruction may be mistaken for it; extrarenal pelvis is a normal variant causing false-positive hydronephrosis)","Other causes of polyuria/nocturia and concentrating defects","ADPKD and other ciliopathies (cystic compression of normal nephrons leading to fibrosis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Bladder catheter insertion and postvoid residual measurement","Bedside bladder ultrasound to assess bladder volume","Abdominal ultrasonography (~90% sensitive and specific for hydronephrosis)","Noncontrast multidetector CT (test of choice for acute flank pain; visualizes retroperitoneum and intrinsic/extrinsic sites of obstruction; characterizes stones by Hounsfield units)","Duplex Doppler ultrasonography (increased resistive index)","Retrograde or antegrade urography (no contrast-induced AKI risk in renal insufficiency)","Voiding cystourethrography (vesicoureteral reflux, bladder neck and urethral obstruction; postvoid residual urine)","Cystourethroscopy and urodynamic studies (cystometry, pressure-flow analysis)","Radionuclide (renal) scan for differential renal function and to predict reversibility after prolonged decompression","Abdominal scout film/KUB (insensitive; may show nephrocalcinosis or radiopaque stone)","Furosemide-augmented imaging to distinguish functional from anatomic obstruction"],"redFlags":["UTO complicated by infection (infected obstructed system) requires immediate relief of obstruction to prevent generalized sepsis and progressive renal damage; sepsis necessitates prompt urologic intervention with drainage (nephrostomy, ureterostomy, or catheterization)","Acute renal failure with anuria suggesting complete bilateral obstruction","Overflow incontinence with sudden-onset fecal incontinence, severe lower back pain, and saddle anesthesia requires emergency evaluation for cauda equina syndrome","Complete obstruction with infection can lead to total destruction of the kidney within days","Fetal LUTO with maternal oligohydramnios or anhydramnios (risk of pulmonary hypoplasia and infant death if untreated)"],"prognosis":"With relief of obstruction, return of renal function depends largely on whether irreversible renal damage has occurred. If obstruction is not relieved, course depends on whether it is complete or incomplete, bilateral or unilateral, and whether infection coexists. Complete obstruction with infection can destroy the kidney within days. Partial return of GFR may follow relief of complete obstruction of 1-2 weeks' duration, but after 8 weeks of obstruction recovery is unlikely. In the absence of definitive evidence of irreversibility, every effort should be made to decompress; a renal radionuclide scan after a prolonged period of decompression may predict reversibility. Chronic fibrotic progression to a failing kidney has no demonstrated reversal of scarring to date (p.2453, 2456-2457).","pitfalls":["UTO is a reversible cause of AKI that must be actively excluded in any unexplained AKI (insert bladder catheter and perform bladder scan/ultrasound) - the urine sediment is often normal even with marked azotemia and extensive structural damage.","Hydronephrosis may be ABSENT on ultrasound when obstruction is <48 h in duration, or associated with volume contraction, staghorn calculi, retroperitoneal fibrosis, or infiltrative renal disease - a negative ultrasound does not exclude obstruction when suspicion remains high.","False-positive hydronephrosis can occur with diuresis, renal cysts, or a normal extrarenal pelvis.","Insidious obstruction (e.g., chronic UPJ narrowing) may cause little or no pain yet result in total destruction of the affected kidney; unilateral ureteric injury during surgery may remain undetected.","After relief, anticipate postobstructive diuresis (see below) - iatrogenic over-replacement of fluids can sustain the diuresis, while under-recognition can lead to profound dehydration and vascular collapse.","NSAIDs for renal colic must account for potential renal harm, and opiates in patients with decreased renal function may be dangerous and used with caution.","Alpha-adrenergic blockers for BPH carry a risk of intraoperative floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery."],"management":["Relieve obstruction promptly to preserve renal function, using a bladder catheter for outlet obstruction and a percutaneous nephrostomy or retrograde ureteric stent for upper-tract obstruction","Undertake emergency decompression when obstruction is complicated by infection and sepsis (pyonephrosis) or by anuric bilateral obstruction with acute kidney injury","Treat the underlying cause, such as stone removal, relief of prostatic obstruction with an alpha-blocker and transurethral resection, or management of tumour or stricture","Anticipate and manage post-obstructive diuresis with careful fluid and electrolyte replacement","Correct the hyperkalaemia and metabolic acidosis of obstructive acute kidney injury, using dialysis for refractory derangement","Monitor recovery of renal function after relief and maintain surveillance for recurrent obstruction","Manage jointly with urology and nephrology, tailoring intervention to whether obstruction is complete or partial and unilateral or bilateral"],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grade; characterized along clinically meaningful axes per Tables 331-1 and 331-2: by level (upper tract/ureter vs bladder outlet vs urethra), laterality (unilateral hydroureter/hydronephrosis vs bilateral involvement at/below the bladder), completeness (complete vs partial/incomplete), acuity (acute vs chronic vs release/postobstructive phase), and presence of superimposed infection. Postobstructive diuresis follows relief of bilateral, but not unilateral, complete obstruction.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2453","Harrison 22e p.2454","Harrison 22e p.2455","Harrison 22e p.2456","Harrison 22e p.2457"],"crossLinks":["aki","renal_colic","ckd"]},"urticaria_angioedema":{"name":"Urticaria, Angioedema & Allergic Rhinitis","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hives","urticaria","hereditary angioedema","allergic rhinitis"],"chapter":363,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2804-2811","clinicalPearls":["Urticaria (hives) are well-circumscribed wheals (dilation of superficial dermal vessels) that are intensely pruritic, often migrate, individually last <24 h, and resolve without bruising or scarring; angioedema arises from deeper dermis/subcutaneous tissue with more pain than pruritus and minimal erythema, taking hours to days to resolve (p.2807).","Urticaria and angioedema reflect similar pathophysiology at different skin levels and often co-occur, with roughly 40% of patients reporting both; together they affect >20% of the population at some point in life (p.2807).","Acute urticaria/angioedema are episodes lasting <6 weeks (usually food, environmental, or drug allergen, or viral infection); attacks persisting >6 weeks are termed chronic and are often spontaneous/idiopathic with no identifiable cause. More than two-thirds of new-onset cases are ultimately diagnosed as acute (p.2807).","Inducible (physical) urticarias are identified by the precipitating stimulus: dermatographism (linear wheal after brisk stroke, affects 2-5%), cholinergic urticaria (small 1-2 mm pruritic wheals with large surrounding erythema triggered by a rise in core body temperature such as fever, hot bath, or exercise), cold, solar, pressure, vibratory, heat, aquagenic, and contact urticaria; most inducible-urticaria lesions individually last <2 h (p.2807-2808).","Hereditary angioedema (HAE) is a fully penetrant autosomal dominant disorder from SERPING1 mutation causing C1 inhibitor (C1INH) deficiency (type 1, ~85%) or dysfunctional protein (type 2); presentation in childhood/adolescence and a family history of angioedema are distinguishing features (p.2808).","HAE diagnosis is suggested by family history, early age of onset, lack of pruritus and urticarial lesions, recurrent gastrointestinal colic, episodes of laryngeal edema, and failure to respond to H1 antihistamines; laboratory confirmation shows low C1INH antigen (type 1) or nonfunctional protein (type 2), with chronically depleted C4 and C2 that fall further during attacks (p.2808-2809).","Cold urticaria can be confirmed by ice-cube placement on the volar forearm provoking a wheal within minutes; cryoglobulins or cold agglutinins are present in up to 5% of these patients, and immersion in cold water (swimming) can progress to vascular collapse (p.2807-2808).","Up to 45% of chronic urticaria has an autoimmune basis, including autoantibodies to IgE or to the alpha chain of FcepsilonRI, and may be associated with antithyroid antibodies (antimicrosomal peroxidase/thyroglobulin) in both Hashimoto's thyroiditis and a euthyroid state (p.2808).","Allergic rhinitis is characterized by sneezing, rhinorrhea, nasal obstruction, lacrimation, and itching of the conjunctiva/nasal mucosa/oropharynx; the nasal mucosa is classically pale and boggy. It requires a temporal relationship to allergen exposure plus documented IgE sensitization (p.2808-2809).","Allergic rhinitis usually occurs in atopic individuals and overlaps strongly with other atopic disease: up to 50% of allergic rhinitis patients have asthma, while 70-80% of asthmatics and 80% of those with chronic bilateral sinusitis have allergic rhinitis (p.2809).","Seasonal allergic rhinitis is elicited by wind-pollinated tree, grass, and weed pollens (in temperate North America: trees Mar-May, grasses Jun-early Jul, weeds mid-Aug-early Oct); perennial rhinitis is driven by year-round allergens such as dust mites (Dermatophagoides), animal dander, cockroach proteins, and mold spores (p.2809).","Pollen-associated (oral allergy) food syndrome causes oropharyngeal pruritus and/or mild swelling after ingesting plant foods cross-reactive with the offending tree, grass, or weed pollen (p.2809)."],"pathophysiology":"Atopy is a tendency to manifest asthma, rhinitis, urticaria, food allergy, and atopic dermatitis in association with allergen-specific IgE, though urticaria and anaphylaxis can also occur in non-atopic individuals. Mast cells are the key effector cells: IgE binds the high-affinity receptor FcepsilonRI (sensitization), and antigen-driven receptor aggregation triggers Lyn/Syk/Fyn tyrosine-kinase signaling, calcium influx, and degranulation. This releases preformed granule mediators (histamine, tryptase, chymase, carboxypeptidase A, proteoglycans), newly generated lipid mediators (PGD2 via cyclooxygenase; LTB4 and cysteinyl leukotrienes LTC4/D4/E4 via 5-lipoxygenase/FLAP; PAF), and cytokines/chemokines (TNF-alpha, IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, GM-CSF). Mast cells can also be activated IgE-independently via C5a/C3a anaphylatoxin receptors, TLRs, alarmin (TSLP, IL-33) receptors, and MRGPRX2 (a target of small-molecule drugs such as ciprofloxacin and rocuronium and of neuropeptides). Histamine and lipid mediators increase venular permeability producing dermal edema (urticaria) or deeper subcutaneous/dermal edema (angioedema). Cold urticaria provides the best evidence for IgE/mast-cell involvement, with documented mast cell degranulation and elevated lesional histamine. In contrast, bradykinin-mediated angioedema (HAE from C1INH deficiency, or ACE inhibitor-induced) results from excess bradykinin: C1INH normally inhibits activated factor XII, kallikrein, and C1r/C1s, and its deficiency leads to bradykinin accumulation with falling C4/C2. Allergic rhinitis arises from sensitized intraepithelial and perivenular nasal mast cells that, on allergen exposure, release histamine, PGD2, and leukotrienes, producing submucosal edema and eosinophilic infiltration.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute vs chronic urticaria/angioedema (cutoff 6 weeks duration)","Inducible (physical) urticarias: dermatographism, cholinergic, cold, solar, pressure, vibratory, heat, aquagenic, contact","Urticarial vasculitis (immune-complex disease; small-vessel leukocytoclastic vasculitis)","Mastocytosis (cutaneous or systemic; urticaria pigmentosa)","Hereditary angioedema (C1INH type 1/2; type 3 with normal C1INH e.g. factor XII mutation)","Acquired C1INH deficiency (lymphoproliferative or autoimmune disease)","ACE inhibitor-induced (bradykinin-mediated) angioedema","CIAS1/NLRP3-associated periodic fever syndromes (familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome, Muckle-Wells syndrome)","Schnitzler's syndrome, Gleich's syndrome, hypereosinophilic syndrome","Serum sickness and hypocomplementemic urticarial vasculitis","Allergic rhinitis: nonallergic rhinitis with eosinophilia (NARES), vasomotor/perennial nonallergic rhinitis, rhinitis medicamentosa, gustatory rhinitis, hormonal (pregnancy) rhinitis, drug-induced rhinitis, hypothyroidism, anatomic/structural nasal defects, nasal foreign body"],"infectionMimics":["Viral infection-associated acute urticaria (a common trigger of new-onset acute urticaria)","Bacterial or parasitic infection-associated urticaria (travel history should prompt parasite evaluation)","Hepatitis B or C and SARS-CoV-2 infection associated with urticaria/urticarial vasculitis","Upper respiratory tract infection mimicking rhinitis; secondary bacterial sinusitis/otitis from mucosal swelling"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Urticarial vasculitis (lesions >24 h, painful/burning rather than pruritic, residual petechiae/bruising)","Mastocytosis presenting with flushing and hyperpigmented papules that urticate on stroking (Darier's sign)","Thyroid disease (hypo-/hyperthyroidism) and autoimmune thyroiditis","Serum sickness / serum sickness-like reactions","Vasomotor (nonallergic) rhinitis and NARES mimicking allergic rhinitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["History-directed skin testing (puncture/prick) or serum allergen-specific IgE for suspected allergen triggers","Provocation/threshold testing for inducible urticaria (e.g. ice-cube test for cold urticaria; stroking for dermatographism)","Limited labs in unrevealing chronic urticaria: CBC with eosinophil assessment, ESR or CRP (most chronic urticaria has no lab abnormality)","Skin biopsy for lesions lasting >36 h, scarring, or painful/non-pruritic - to assess for urticarial vasculitis (cellular infiltrate, nuclear debris, fibrinoid venular necrosis)","For isolated angioedema: basic chemistries with liver function tests and complement levels","C1INH antigen level and functional (catalytic inhibition) assay, with C4 and C2 levels, for suspected HAE; C1q and C1 function additionally reduced in acquired C1INH deficiency","Evaluation for paraproteinemia/lymphoproliferative disease where presentation suggests acquired C1INH deficiency","Autologous serum skin test and basophil activation markers (CD63/CD203) for autoimmune chronic urticaria; antithyroid antibodies","Nasal secretion eosinophils and serum/skin specific IgE for allergic rhinitis (skin test more sensitive; serum specific IgE less sensitive but highly specific)"],"redFlags":["Angioedema of the upper respiratory tract / larynx may be life-threatening due to transient laryngeal obstruction - airway emergency","Gastrointestinal angioedema with abdominal colic can mimic an acute abdomen and lead to unnecessary surgical intervention","Cold urticaria with cold-water immersion may progress to vascular collapse","Progression of urticaria/angioedema to anaphylaxis with vascular collapse (e.g. exercise-induced anaphylaxis, food/drug triggers)","Urticarial lesions lasting >24-36 h with bruising/petechiae, scarring, or burning/pain rather than pruritus suggest urticarial vasculitis and warrant biopsy"],"prognosis":"More than two-thirds of new-onset urticaria cases are ultimately acute and self-limited. Chronic spontaneous urticaria is most common in the third to fifth decades and the vast majority shows no laboratory abnormality. Dermatographism generally has a duration of <5 years. Allergic rhinitis prevalence in North America is 10-30% (peak >30% in the fifth decade). Allergen immunotherapy (SCIT/SLIT) for allergic rhinitis provides durable symptom benefit and can reduce asthma symptoms; guidelines recommend 3-5 years of SCIT with discontinuation after minimal symptoms over two consecutive allergen seasons.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish histaminergic from bradykinin-mediated angioedema: bradykinin-mediated angioedema (hereditary angioedema from C1INH deficiency, and ACE inhibitor-induced) is conspicuously WITHOUT concomitant urticaria or pruritus, frequently involves the GI tract, lasts >24 h, and characteristically does NOT respond to H1 antihistamines (nor to adrenaline) - treat with C1INH replacement, bradykinin B2-receptor antagonist (icatibant), or kallikrein inhibitor rather than antihistamines.","Mistaking urticarial vasculitis for simple urticaria: individual lesions persist >24 h, may leave bruising/petechiae and scarring, and cause burning rather than pruritus - biopsy shows leukocytoclastic vasculitis and may signal underlying SLE, Sjogren's, or complement deficiency.","Overlooking laryngeal angioedema as an airway emergency, or mistaking GI angioedema for a surgical abdomen.","Topical glucocorticoids are of no value in urticaria, and systemic glucocorticoids are generally avoided in idiopathic, allergen-induced, or inducible urticaria due to long-term toxicity.","Prolonged use of topical alpha-adrenergic nasal decongestants (7-14 days) causes rebound rhinitis medicamentosa.","Concomitant beta-adrenergic blocker therapy increases the risk of severe/refractory anaphylaxis during allergen immunotherapy; immunotherapy is contraindicated in significant cardiovascular disease or unstable asthma."],"management":["Identify and avoid triggers, including implicated foods, drugs such as NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors, and the physical stimuli of inducible urticaria","Treat histaminergic urticaria and angioedema first-line with second-generation non-sedating H1 antihistamines, escalating the dose in chronic spontaneous urticaria","Add omalizumab (anti-IgE) or ciclosporin for antihistamine-refractory chronic spontaneous urticaria, using only short courses of oral corticosteroids for severe flares","Treat allergic rhinitis with allergen avoidance, intranasal corticosteroids and antihistamines, and consider allergen immunotherapy for persistent disease","Treat mast-cell-mediated anaphylaxis with intramuscular adrenaline and provide an adrenaline auto-injector with an emergency plan","Manage hereditary angioedema on the distinct bradykinin pathway, which does not respond to antihistamines, corticosteroids or adrenaline: treat acute attacks with C1-inhibitor concentrate or the bradykinin-receptor antagonist icatibant, provide prophylaxis with C1-inhibitor, lanadelumab or attenuated androgens, and avoid ACE inhibitors and oestrogens","Refer to allergy or immunology for confirmed hereditary angioedema, recurrent anaphylaxis, or difficult chronic urticaria"],"severityClassification":"Urticaria/angioedema classified by duration: acute = episodes lasting <6 weeks; chronic = attacks persisting >6 weeks (Table 363-1 further classifies by precipitating factor into acute vs chronic categories, including drug, food, infection, physical/inducible, autoimmune, vasculitic, mastocytosis, and hereditary forms). Hereditary angioedema typed as: type 1 (low C1INH antigen, ~85%), type 2 (dysfunctional C1INH protein), and type 3 (normal C1INH/complement, often identifiable gene mutation e.g. factor XII). Allergic rhinitis classified as seasonal vs perennial, and managed by severity (mild vs moderate-to-severe).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2804-2811"],"crossLinks":["anaphylaxis","angioedema_acei","mastocytosis"]},"urticaria_pigmentosa":{"name":"Urticaria pigmentosa","system":"Skin","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["maculopapular cutaneous mastocytosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology references / mastocytosis literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest form of cutaneous mastocytosis, presenting as multiple red-brown macules and papules","Lesions urticate on rubbing (Darier sign) and may blister in young children","It can begin in infancy or in adulthood, with different implications for each","Childhood-onset disease often regresses spontaneously, while adult-onset disease is frequently associated with systemic mastocytosis","Mediator release can cause flushing, itch and, uncommonly, systemic reactions","Baseline serum tryptase and clinical assessment guide the need for systemic evaluation"],"pathophysiology":"Urticaria pigmentosa is a maculopapular pattern of cutaneous mastocytosis in which increased numbers of clonal mast cells accumulate in the dermis, commonly linked to KIT mutations. Mechanical or chemical stimulation degranulates these mast cells, releasing histamine and other mediators to produce the localised weal-and-flare of the Darier sign and the pigmented appearance from associated increased melanin. As with other cutaneous mastocytosis, childhood disease tends to involute, whereas adult disease often reflects an underlying systemic process.","additionalDifferentials":["Other forms of cutaneous or systemic mastocytosis","Chronic urticaria","Secondary syphilis or other papular eruptions","Histiocytoses","Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis with Darier sign","Skin biopsy showing increased dermal mast cells if needed","Serum baseline tryptase","Assessment for systemic symptoms and, in adults, systemic mastocytosis","Trigger history for mediator symptoms"],"redFlags":["Adult onset suggesting systemic mastocytosis","Systemic mediator symptoms or anaphylaxis","Extensive disease with blistering in infants","A high baseline tryptase"],"prognosis":"Childhood urticaria pigmentosa commonly improves or clears by adolescence, whereas adult disease tends to persist and may be a marker of systemic mastocytosis.","pitfalls":["Failing to evaluate adults for systemic mastocytosis","Overlooking the Darier sign","Provoking mediator release by vigorously rubbing lesions","Confusing it with chronic urticaria"],"management":["Advise avoidance of mast-cell degranulation triggers including friction, temperature extremes, and provoking drugs such as opioids, NSAIDs, radiographic contrast and certain anaesthetic agents","Control pruritus and flushing with H1 antihistamines, adding H2 antihistamines and oral sodium cromoglicate for gastrointestinal mediator symptoms","Treat symptomatic skin lesions with potent topical corticosteroids or phototherapy","Provide an adrenaline auto-injector and emergency plan for those at risk of systemic mediator-release reactions","Measure baseline serum tryptase and evaluate adults for underlying systemic mastocytosis, referring to haematology where indicated","Reassure that childhood-onset disease commonly involutes by adolescence, and offer specialist review for persistent adult disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dermatology reference texts (standard reference)","Mastocytosis clinical guidelines (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"urticarial_vasculitis":{"name":"Urticarial vasculitis","system":"Rheumatology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UV","normocomplementaemic urticarial vasculitis","urticarial leucocytoclastic vasculitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology / vasculitis guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Wheals persist beyond 24 hours, unlike ordinary urticaria which resolves within hours","Lesions often burn or feel painful rather than simply itch and may leave bruising or pigmentation as they fade","Skin biopsy shows leucocytoclastic vasculitis of small dermal vessels","Most cases have normal complement levels and a relatively benign, skin-limited course","Systemic features such as arthralgia, angio-oedema, or abdominal pain can accompany the rash","Underlying triggers include connective tissue disease, infection, drugs, and rarely malignancy","Distinguishing it from chronic spontaneous urticaria changes both investigation and management"],"pathophysiology":"Urticarial vasculitis is an immune complex-mediated small-vessel vasculitis affecting the postcapillary venules of the dermis. Deposition of immune complexes activates complement and recruits neutrophils, producing leucocytoclastic inflammation with vessel wall damage, fibrin deposition, and extravasation of red cells that accounts for the residual bruising. The persistent inflammatory infiltrate explains why the wheals last longer than in ordinary urticaria. The normocomplementaemic form is usually skin-limited, whereas complement consumption signals a greater likelihood of systemic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic spontaneous urticaria","Hypocomplementaemic urticarial vasculitis","Cutaneous small-vessel (leucocytoclastic) vasculitis of other cause","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Serum sickness or drug reaction","Autoinflammatory syndromes such as Schnitzler syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy for histology and direct immunofluorescence","Complement levels (C3, C4, CH50) and C1q assessment","Antinuclear antibody and connective tissue disease serology","Full blood count, inflammatory markers, and renal function with urinalysis","Hepatitis serology and other infection screening when indicated","Serum and urine electrophoresis if a paraproteinaemic association is suspected"],"redFlags":["Low complement suggesting the hypocomplementaemic systemic subtype","Renal involvement with proteinuria or haematuria","Pulmonary symptoms or breathlessness suggesting systemic disease","Systemic features pointing to underlying lupus or malignancy"],"prognosis":"Normocomplementaemic, skin-limited urticarial vasculitis usually follows a self-limiting or relapsing but benign course. Cases with low complement or systemic involvement carry greater morbidity and require closer monitoring and more intensive therapy.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for ordinary urticaria and treating with antihistamines alone","Failing to biopsy atypical or persistent wheals","Not checking complement, thereby missing the hypocomplementaemic systemic subtype","Overlooking an underlying connective tissue disease, infection, or drug trigger"],"management":["Identify and treat any underlying cause, such as connective tissue disease, infection, a culprit drug, paraproteinaemia or malignancy","Use antihistamines for symptomatic relief, recognising they are often insufficient alone","Treat mild, skin-limited, normocomplementaemic disease with NSAIDs, colchicine, dapsone or hydroxychloroquine","Treat systemic or hypocomplementaemic disease with corticosteroids and a steroid-sparing immunosuppressant such as azathioprine, mycophenolate mofetil or methotrexate, escalating to rituximab or other biologics for refractory cases","Monitor complement levels, renal function and urinalysis to detect systemic and renal involvement","Refer to rheumatology for confirmed systemic or hypocomplementaemic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kelley & Firestein's Textbook of Rheumatology","Chapel Hill Consensus Conference vasculitis nomenclature"],"crossLinks":[]},"usher_syndrome":{"name":"Usher syndrome","system":"Ear / Audiology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["deaf-blindness","retinitis pigmentosa with sensorineural hearing loss","MYO7A","USH2A","CDH23","hereditary deaf-blindness"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology / Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The defining combination is congenital sensorineural hearing loss together with progressive retinitis pigmentosa, producing dual sensory (deaf-blind) impairment","Inheritance is autosomal recessive, with many causative genes encoding proteins of the hair-cell and photoreceptor cilium (the 'Usher interactome'), including MYO7A, USH2A and CDH23","Type 1 features profound congenital deafness with absent vestibular function and delayed motor milestones from balance impairment","Type 2 features moderate-to-severe hearing loss with preserved vestibular function, and is the commonest subtype","Type 3 is characterised by progressive, later-onset hearing loss and variable vestibular findings","Night blindness and constricting peripheral (tunnel) vision from retinitis pigmentosa often appear later than the hearing loss, so early audiology cases may be missed as isolated deafness","Suspect Usher in any child with congenital sensorineural hearing loss, and arrange ophthalmology and electroretinography follow-up to detect retinal disease early"],"pathophysiology":"Usher syndrome is a group of autosomal recessive disorders caused by mutations in genes whose protein products are needed for the structure and function of stereocilia in cochlear hair cells and of the connecting cilium in retinal photoreceptors. Loss of these shared proteins impairs mechanotransduction in the inner ear and progressive degeneration of rod and cone photoreceptors, giving the characteristic combination of sensorineural hearing loss and retinitis pigmentosa. In some subtypes vestibular hair cells are also affected, producing balance disturbance. The clinical subtype reflects which gene is involved and the severity of the resulting protein defect.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-syndromic sensorineural hearing loss","Isolated (non-syndromic) retinitis pigmentosa","Congenital rubella and other congenital infections affecting eye and ear","Alstrom syndrome and other ciliopathies with sensory loss","Refsum disease with retinopathy and neuropathy","Bardet-Biedl syndrome and mitochondrial disease with combined sensory involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure-tone and paediatric audiometry with otoacoustic emissions and auditory brainstem response","Vestibular function testing to help distinguish subtypes","Electroretinography to detect early photoreceptor dysfunction before symptomatic visual loss","Dilated fundus examination and optical coherence tomography for retinitis pigmentosa","Molecular genetic testing (gene panel) to confirm the diagnosis and subtype"],"redFlags":["Congenital deafness with delayed independent walking, suggesting vestibular involvement and type 1 disease","Progressive night blindness or narrowing visual field in a deaf child","Family history of combined hearing and visual loss","Deterioration in mobility and orientation from combined sensory decline"],"prognosis":"Hearing loss is generally stable or slowly progressive depending on subtype, while retinitis pigmentosa is progressive and can lead to severe visual impairment in adulthood; early cochlear implantation and low-vision support substantially improve communication and independence.","pitfalls":["Attributing congenital hearing loss to an isolated cause and not screening for retinal disease","Mislabelling early balance and motor delay as global developmental delay rather than vestibular loss","Assuming normal vision excludes Usher, as retinopathy often manifests later","Failing to arrange genetic counselling for an autosomal recessive condition with recurrence risk"],"management":["Rehabilitate hearing early, with cochlear implantation for profound congenital deafness (notably type 1) and hearing aids for the moderate loss of type 2","Provide communication support through sign language, deaf-blind services and structured aural-visual rehabilitation","Support residual vision with low-vision aids, ultraviolet-protective lenses and management of retinitis pigmentosa complications by ophthalmology","Offer vestibular and balance physiotherapy where vestibular function is absent or impaired","Maintain regular audiological and ophthalmological surveillance, using electroretinography and optical coherence tomography to track photoreceptor degeneration","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across audiology, ophthalmology, clinical genetics and educational or occupational support","Offer genetic counselling and family testing given autosomal recessive inheritance, and discuss emerging gene-directed therapies for the underlying mutation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"usher_syndrome_type_1":{"name":"Usher syndrome type 1","system":"Hearing / Vision genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["USH1","Usher type I"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and audiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most severe of the three Usher subtypes: profound congenital bilateral sensorineural deafness, absent vestibular function, and prepubertal onset of retinitis pigmentosa.","Affected infants have delayed motor milestones, notably delayed independent walking, reflecting the vestibular areflexia that distinguishes type 1 from type 2.","Because deafness is present from birth and profound, conventional hearing aids give little benefit and cochlear implantation is the mainstay for auditory access.","Night blindness and progressive peripheral field constriction typically emerge in childhood, earlier than in type 2, eventually producing tunnel vision.","MYO7A is the commonest causative gene; other loci include USH1C (harmonin), CDH23, PCDH15 and USH1G (SANS/whirlin-interacting).","All type 1 gene products form part of the interconnected hair-cell and photoreceptor 'Usher interactome' anchoring stereocilia and the periciliary photoreceptor membrane.","Early identification through newborn hearing screening allows timely cochlear implantation and vestibular-aware developmental support.","Genetic counselling should emphasise autosomal recessive inheritance and the option of cascade and carrier testing in relatives."],"pathophysiology":"Usher syndrome type 1 is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in genes encoding proteins of the hair-cell and photoreceptor 'Usher protein complex', including the unconventional motor MYO7A, the scaffold proteins harmonin (USH1C) and SANS (USH1G), and the cadherin-related adhesion molecules cadherin-23 (CDH23) and protocadherin-15 (PCDH15). These proteins cooperate to build and maintain the stereociliary tip-link and ankle-link machinery of cochlear and vestibular hair cells, so their loss disrupts the precise architecture and mechanotransduction of stereocilia, producing profound congenital sensorineural deafness and complete vestibular areflexia. The same interactome operates at the photoreceptor connecting cilium and periciliary membrane, where protein loss impairs opsin trafficking and outer-segment maintenance, leading to progressive rod-then-cone degeneration that manifests clinically as retinitis pigmentosa. The combination of failed hair-cell transduction from birth and later photoreceptor loss underlies the characteristic triad of deafness, absent balance function and progressive blindness.","additionalDifferentials":["Usher syndrome type 2 (moderate-severe hearing loss, preserved vestibular function, later retinitis pigmentosa)","Usher syndrome type 3 (progressive rather than congenital hearing loss, variable vestibular involvement)","Non-syndromic congenital sensorineural deafness (e.g. GJB2/connexin-26)","Congenital deafness with later incidental retinitis pigmentosa of independent cause","Alstrom syndrome (deafness, cone-rod dystrophy, obesity, cardiomyopathy)","Congenital rubella or cytomegalovirus with combined audiovisual sequelae","Mitochondrial disorders with combined sensorineural deafness and retinopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure-tone and paediatric behavioural audiometry with auditory brainstem response confirming profound bilateral loss","Vestibular testing (rotational/caloric or video head-impulse) demonstrating absent responses","Electroretinography detecting rod-cone dysfunction often before symptomatic visual loss","Optical coherence tomography and fundus autofluorescence for retinal structural monitoring","Formal visual field perimetry to document peripheral constriction","Targeted or panel-based molecular genetic testing of Usher type 1 genes","Developmental and motor assessment in infancy given vestibular areflexia"],"redFlags":["Profound congenital deafness with markedly delayed independent walking suggesting vestibular areflexia","Emerging night blindness or peripheral field loss in a congenitally deaf child","Progressive visual field constriction threatening functional independence","Family history of combined deafness and blindness in a recessive pattern","Failure to progress with hearing aids indicating need for cochlear implant evaluation"],"prognosis":"Auditory access is achievable with early cochlear implantation, but retinitis pigmentosa is progressive and vision typically declines to severe constriction or blindness over adulthood. Vestibular function does not recover, though children compensate visually and proprioceptively for balance.","pitfalls":["Attributing delayed walking solely to deafness and overlooking vestibular areflexia","Delaying cochlear implantation while awaiting benefit from hearing aids","Assuming normal vision because retinitis pigmentosa is not yet symptomatic in early childhood","Confusing type 1 with milder type 2 and giving inaccurate prognostic counselling","Not offering electroretinography early, missing the window for anticipatory low-vision planning"],"management":["Early bilateral cochlear implantation as the mainstay for auditory access, since profound congenital deafness responds poorly to conventional hearing aids","Speech and language therapy with early establishment of a communication modality, including sign language and deaf-blind communication strategies","Low-vision rehabilitation, orientation and mobility training, and visual aids as retinitis pigmentosa progresses toward tunnel vision","Regular ophthalmic surveillance with electroretinography, optical coherence tomography, fundus autofluorescence and visual field perimetry to track retinal degeneration","Vestibular rehabilitation and physiotherapy to support balance given the areflexia and delayed motor milestones","Multidisciplinary care coordinating audiology, ophthalmology, clinical genetics and educational or psychological support","Autosomal recessive genetic counselling with molecular testing and cascade screening of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics reference texts","Audiology and cochlear implantation guidelines","Inherited retinal disease reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"usher_syndrome_type_2":{"name":"Usher syndrome type 2","system":"Hearing / Vision genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["USH2","Usher type II"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and audiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest Usher subtype: congenital, non-progressive or slowly progressive moderate-to-severe sensorineural hearing loss with preserved vestibular function.","The hearing loss is characteristically sloping, worse for higher frequencies, so speech is often acquired with hearing aids without needing cochlear implantation.","Normal vestibular function means motor milestones, including walking, are reached at the expected age, distinguishing it from type 1.","Retinitis pigmentosa typically presents later than in type 1, with night blindness often beginning around adolescence or early adulthood.","USH2A, encoding usherin, accounts for the majority of cases; other loci include ADGRV1 (GPR98) and WHRN (whirlin).","USH2A variants are also a frequent cause of non-syndromic autosomal recessive retinitis pigmentosa, so the same gene spans syndromic and isolated retinal disease.","Hearing aids and communication support usually suffice for auditory rehabilitation, with cochlear implantation reserved for those who progress.","Autosomal recessive inheritance warrants genetic counselling and consideration of retinal gene-specific therapeutic trials."],"pathophysiology":"Usher syndrome type 2 is an autosomal recessive condition most often caused by biallelic variants in USH2A, which encodes usherin, a very large transmembrane protein; other cases arise from variants in ADGRV1 (an adhesion G-protein-coupled receptor) and WHRN (whirlin). These proteins assemble at the ankle-link region of developing cochlear hair-cell stereocilia and at the periciliary membrane complex of photoreceptors, where they contribute to stereociliary cohesion and to the maintenance of the photoreceptor connecting cilium. Because the encoded proteins are more important for maintenance than for the profound early transduction machinery affected in type 1, cochlear hair-cell dysfunction is partial, giving moderate-to-severe rather than profound deafness, and vestibular hair cells are largely spared, preserving balance. In the retina, gradual failure of periciliary transport and outer-segment turnover produces a rod-predominant then cone degeneration that manifests as retinitis pigmentosa with a characteristically later onset than type 1. The residual function of these proteins therefore explains the milder, later-onset phenotype across both the auditory and visual systems.","additionalDifferentials":["Usher syndrome type 1 (profound congenital deafness with vestibular areflexia and earlier retinitis pigmentosa)","Usher syndrome type 3 (progressive postlingual hearing loss)","Non-syndromic autosomal recessive retinitis pigmentosa (including isolated USH2A disease)","Age-related or noise-induced high-frequency sensorineural loss with coincidental retinopathy","Cone-rod dystrophies with hearing impairment","Mitochondrial syndromes combining hearing loss and retinal degeneration","Bardet-Biedl and other ciliopathies with retinal and sensory features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure-tone audiometry documenting the sloping high-frequency sensorineural loss","Vestibular assessment confirming preserved function","Electroretinography to detect rod-cone dysfunction","Optical coherence tomography and fundus autofluorescence for retinal monitoring","Visual field perimetry to track peripheral constriction","Molecular genetic testing prioritising USH2A, then ADGRV1 and WHRN","Assessment of speech and language development with hearing-aid benefit"],"redFlags":["New night blindness or peripheral field loss in an adolescent with congenital hearing loss","Documented progression of hearing thresholds suggesting a more severe course","Rapidly constricting visual fields affecting mobility or driving","Family history of combined hearing and visual loss in siblings","Central visual acuity decline indicating macular involvement"],"prognosis":"Hearing loss is generally stable and well managed with hearing aids, while retinitis pigmentosa progresses more slowly than in type 1; useful central vision is often retained into mid-adulthood before significant field loss. Overall visual and functional outlook is better than type 1.","pitfalls":["Labelling the retinopathy as non-syndromic and missing the associated hearing component (or vice versa)","Assuming the hearing loss is progressive and prematurely recommending cochlear implantation","Overlooking early retinitis pigmentosa because vision seems adequate in youth","Failing to test relatives given the shared USH2A basis of isolated retinitis pigmentosa","Confusing the later visual onset with a benign prognosis and omitting low-vision planning"],"management":["Hearing aid fitting and aural rehabilitation as the mainstay, given moderate-to-severe sloping high-frequency loss that usually permits spoken language","Cochlear implantation considered if hearing loss progresses beyond useful benefit from amplification","Low-vision rehabilitation, orientation and mobility training, and visual aids as retinitis pigmentosa advances","Regular ophthalmic surveillance with electroretinography, optical coherence tomography, fundus autofluorescence and perimetry","Multidisciplinary coordination across audiology, ophthalmology, clinical genetics and psychological support","Autosomal recessive genetic counselling with USH2A-focused testing and cascade family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics reference texts","Audiology and hearing rehabilitation guidelines","Inherited retinal disease reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"usher_syndrome_type_3":{"name":"Usher syndrome type 3","system":"Hearing / Vision genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["USH3","Usher type III"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and audiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by postlingual, progressive sensorineural hearing loss, in contrast to the congenital loss of types 1 and 2.","Hearing is often near-normal or only mildly impaired in early childhood, then deteriorates through later childhood and adulthood.","Vestibular function is variable, ranging from normal to progressively impaired, unlike the uniform areflexia of type 1.","Retinitis pigmentosa onset and severity are variable, generally intermediate between types 1 and 2, with night blindness developing in adolescence or adulthood.","CLRN1 (clarin-1) is the principal causative gene; it is notably enriched in the Finnish and Ashkenazi Jewish populations.","The progressive nature means serial audiometry is essential and the choice between hearing aids and later cochlear implantation shifts over time.","Because early hearing may be adequate, the diagnosis is sometimes made only when retinitis pigmentosa or worsening hearing prompts genetic testing.","Autosomal recessive inheritance; founder variants make targeted testing efficient in at-risk ancestries."],"pathophysiology":"Usher syndrome type 3 is an autosomal recessive disorder caused chiefly by biallelic variants in CLRN1, which encodes clarin-1, a small four-transmembrane-domain glycoprotein of the tetraspanin-like family expressed in cochlear and vestibular hair cells and in retinal photoreceptors. Clarin-1 is required for the organisation and ongoing maintenance of the hair-bundle stereocilia and the synaptic and ciliary architecture of sensory cells rather than for their initial formation, which explains why auditory and vestibular function may be relatively preserved at birth and then decline progressively as maintenance fails. In the retina, clarin-1 supports photoreceptor ribbon synapses and outer-segment integrity, so its dysfunction produces a progressive retinitis pigmentosa of variable onset. The result is a distinctive phenotype of postlingual, progressive hearing loss with variable vestibular involvement and variably timed retinal degeneration, reflecting a defect in sensory-cell upkeep rather than congenital absence of transduction machinery. Founder variants in specific populations account for much of the disease burden.","additionalDifferentials":["Usher syndrome type 1 (congenital profound deafness, vestibular areflexia, early retinitis pigmentosa)","Usher syndrome type 2 (congenital stable moderate-severe loss with later retinitis pigmentosa)","Progressive non-syndromic autosomal recessive or dominant sensorineural hearing loss","Acquired progressive sensorineural hearing loss with independent retinal disease","Non-syndromic retinitis pigmentosa presenting before hearing decline","Mitochondrial disease with progressive hearing and retinal involvement","Other ciliopathies with variable sensory decline"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial pure-tone audiometry to document progression over time","Vestibular testing, repeated as function may deteriorate","Electroretinography to characterise rod-cone dysfunction","Optical coherence tomography and fundus autofluorescence for retinal follow-up","Visual field perimetry to monitor peripheral loss","Molecular genetic testing of CLRN1 with attention to population founder variants","Speech-perception testing to guide timing of amplification versus implantation"],"redFlags":["Documented decline in hearing thresholds across serial assessments","New night blindness or field constriction in a young person with progressive deafness","Emerging vestibular symptoms such as unsteadiness or oscillopsia","Family or ancestry-based clustering suggesting a founder variant","Loss of speech clarity indicating that amplification is no longer sufficient"],"prognosis":"Both hearing and vision decline progressively, with wide interindividual variability; many retain useful hearing with aids for years before cochlear implantation, and visual loss generally advances more slowly than in type 1. Long-term outlook depends on the rate of progression, which varies by genotype.","pitfalls":["Excluding Usher syndrome because hearing is normal or near-normal in early childhood","Attributing progressive hearing loss to acquired causes and missing the genetic diagnosis","Assuming vestibular function is intact without repeated testing","Delaying retinal assessment until visual symptoms are advanced","Not recognising founder-variant populations where targeted testing is efficient"],"management":["Serial audiometry with hearing aid provision, transitioning to cochlear implantation as the progressive sensorineural loss deteriorates","Aural rehabilitation and speech and language support adapted to changing hearing over time","Low-vision rehabilitation, orientation and mobility training as retinitis pigmentosa develops","Regular ophthalmic monitoring with electroretinography, optical coherence tomography, fundus autofluorescence and perimetry","Repeated vestibular assessment with balance physiotherapy, as function may decline progressively","Multidisciplinary care across audiology, ophthalmology and clinical genetics","Autosomal recessive genetic counselling with CLRN1 testing, noting founder variants, and cascade family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics reference texts","Audiology and cochlear implantation guidelines","Inherited retinal disease reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"uterine_atony":{"name":"Uterine atony","system":"Reproductive / Obstetrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["atonic uterus","atonic postpartum haemorrhage"],"chapter":null,"source":"RCOG postpartum haemorrhage guidance / Williams Obstetrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Uterine atony is failure of the myometrium to contract and retract adequately after delivery, and it is the single commonest cause of primary postpartum haemorrhage","Effective haemostasis after birth depends on myometrial contraction compressing the spiral arteries of the placental bed (the 'living ligatures'); when the uterus stays relaxed these vessels bleed freely","Within the 'four Ts' framework of postpartum haemorrhage (Tone, Trauma, Tissue, Thrombin), atony is the 'Tone' cause and should be excluded first, though causes may coexist","Risk factors include overdistension from multiple pregnancy, polyhydramnios, or macrosomia, prolonged or augmented labour, precipitate labour, grand multiparity, chorioamnionitis, retained placental tissue, tocolytic use, and general anaesthesia with volatile agents","The classic finding is a soft, poorly contracted, often high uterus with ongoing vaginal bleeding despite a delivered and apparently complete placenta","First-line management is uterine massage and bimanual compression, ensuring the bladder is empty, and uterotonic drugs such as oxytocin, ergometrine, prostaglandins, and misoprostol, alongside resuscitation","If pharmacological measures fail, mechanical and surgical options include intrauterine balloon tamponade, compression sutures such as the B-Lynch, stepwise uterine and internal iliac artery ligation, uterine artery embolisation, and ultimately hysterectomy","Prophylactic active management of the third stage with a uterotonic markedly reduces the incidence of atonic postpartum haemorrhage"],"pathophysiology":"After the placenta separates, the large uteroplacental blood flow must be arrested rapidly. This is achieved mechanically by contraction and retraction of the interlacing myometrial fibres, which constrict the spiral arteries traversing them, with clotting a secondary contributor. Uterine atony occurs when the myometrium fails to maintain this tonic contraction. Overdistension stretches and fatigues the fibres; prolonged or augmented labour and chorioamnionitis impair contractility; retained tissue mechanically prevents full retraction; and certain drugs, such as tocolytics and volatile anaesthetics, relax smooth muscle. With the vessels left open and uncompressed, blood loss is brisk and can rapidly become life-threatening given the high uterine blood flow at term.","additionalDifferentials":["Genital tract trauma such as cervical, vaginal, or perineal lacerations or a haematoma","Retained placental tissue or membranes","Coagulopathy, inherited or acquired, including disseminated intravascular coagulation","Uterine inversion","Uterine rupture","Placenta accreta spectrum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assessment of uterine tone by palpation and the response to massage","Estimation and ongoing measurement of blood loss with serial maternal observations","Full blood count, coagulation screen with fibrinogen, and group-and-save or crossmatch","Inspection of the placenta for completeness and examination of the genital tract for trauma","Bedside ultrasound to detect retained tissue if bleeding persists","Near-patient viscoelastic testing where available to guide blood-product replacement"],"redFlags":["Continuing heavy bleeding with a soft, poorly contracted uterus","Signs of hypovolaemic shock such as tachycardia, hypotension, or altered consciousness","Failure to respond to first-line uterotonics","Developing coagulopathy or a need for massive transfusion","Bleeding out of keeping with the visible loss, suggesting concealed or intra-abdominal haemorrhage"],"prognosis":"With early recognition and a structured escalation of uterotonic, mechanical, and surgical measures, most women recover fully; however, atonic postpartum haemorrhage remains a leading cause of maternal death worldwide, and delayed treatment risks shock, coagulopathy, organ injury, and loss of fertility from hysterectomy.","pitfalls":["Failing to give a prophylactic uterotonic in the third stage","Not emptying the bladder, which impairs uterine contraction","Attributing ongoing bleeding to atony without excluding trauma, retained tissue, and coagulopathy","Underestimating blood loss and escalating too slowly","Neglecting to keep the patient warm and correct coagulopathy during resuscitation"],"management":["Immediate uterine massage and bimanual compression with the bladder emptied as first-line mechanical measures","Stepwise uterotonic drugs including oxytocin, ergometrine, prostaglandins such as carboprost, and misoprostol","Concurrent resuscitation with wide-bore intravenous access, crystalloid and blood-product transfusion guided by ongoing loss and coagulation results","Tranexamic acid to reduce haemorrhage","Exclusion and treatment of coexisting causes within the four Ts framework, including retained tissue, genital tract trauma and coagulopathy","Escalation to intrauterine balloon tamponade, uterine compression sutures, and uterine artery or internal iliac ligation or arterial embolisation if bleeding persists","Hysterectomy as a life-saving last resort with senior obstetric, anaesthetic and haematology involvement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["RCOG Green-top Guideline on prevention and management of postpartum haemorrhage","Williams Obstetrics","WHO recommendations on the prevention and treatment of postpartum haemorrhage"],"crossLinks":[]},"uterine_fibroid":{"name":"Uterine fibroid","system":"Reproductive / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Uterine leiomyoma","Fibromyoma","Myoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Berek & Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Fibroids are benign monoclonal smooth-muscle tumours and the commonest pelvic tumour of reproductive-age women; the majority are asymptomatic and discovered incidentally.","Growth is oestrogen- and progesterone-dependent, so lesions tend to enlarge during pregnancy and typically regress after the menopause.","The FIGO leiomyoma classification (types 0 to 8) localises lesions from submucosal through intramural to subserosal; submucosal fibroids most affect menstrual bleeding and fertility.","Heavy menstrual bleeding is the dominant symptom and may lead to iron-deficiency anaemia, whereas subserosal and pedunculated lesions rarely cause bleeding.","Bulk-related symptoms such as pelvic pressure, urinary frequency and constipation reflect large or subserosal lesions rather than the bleeding pattern.","Red (carneous) degeneration classically causes acute pain in mid-pregnancy; other degenerative changes include hyaline, cystic and calcific.","Rapid enlargement, or any growth after the menopause, should raise concern for leiomyosarcoma even though malignant transformation is very rare.","Management is individualised across expectant, medical (antifibrinolytics, hormonal therapy, gonadotrophin-releasing hormone analogues, selective progesterone receptor modulators), uterine artery embolisation and surgery (myomectomy or hysterectomy), guided by symptoms and fertility wishes."],"pathophysiology":"A fibroid arises from a single transformed myometrial smooth-muscle cell that proliferates as a monoclonal nodule of smooth muscle within an abundant extracellular matrix of collagen, fibronectin and proteoglycan, giving the characteristic whorled cut surface and a surrounding pseudocapsule of compressed myometrium. Growth is driven by oestrogen and progesterone, with overexpression of their receptors and of local growth factors, and somatic MED12 mutations are frequently implicated. Heavy menstrual bleeding results from distortion and enlargement of the endometrial surface, dysregulation of vasoactive and angiogenic factors, and impaired local haemostasis, while enlarging lesions produce pressure effects on adjacent pelvic viscera.","additionalDifferentials":["Adenomyosis","Endometrial polyp","Ovarian or adnexal mass","Leiomyosarcoma","Endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma","Intrauterine pregnancy causing an enlarged uterus","Uterine adenomyoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count to assess for anaemia","Transvaginal with or without transabdominal ultrasound as first-line imaging","Saline infusion sonohysterography to delineate submucosal lesions","Diagnostic and operative hysteroscopy for submucosal fibroids","Pelvic MRI for mapping, distinguishing adenomyosis and planning embolisation or surgery","Ferritin and iron studies","Endometrial biopsy when abnormal bleeding is accompanied by risk factors for hyperplasia"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging mass, or any growth after the menopause, suggesting leiomyosarcoma","Severe symptomatic anaemia from heavy bleeding, potentially requiring transfusion","Acute severe pelvic pain from torsion of a pedunculated fibroid or red degeneration","Urinary retention or hydronephrosis from mass effect","Postmenopausal bleeding requiring exclusion of endometrial pathology"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent as fibroids are benign, with most remaining asymptomatic and stable and symptomatic lesions responding well to treatment and regressing after the menopause. Recurrence can follow myomectomy, and malignant transformation is exceptionally rare.","pitfalls":["Attributing all abnormal uterine bleeding to fibroids and missing coexistent endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma.","Failing to consider leiomyosarcoma before morcellation, particularly in postmenopausal or rapidly growing masses.","Overlooking coexisting adenomyosis, which frequently mimics and accompanies fibroids.","Neglecting to correct iron-deficiency anaemia before intervention.","Assuming subserosal or pedunculated fibroids are the cause of heavy menstrual bleeding."],"management":["Expectant management with reassurance for asymptomatic fibroids","Medical control of heavy menstrual bleeding with the levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system, tranexamic acid, NSAIDs or combined hormonal contraception","GnRH agonists with add-back therapy or GnRH antagonists to shrink fibroids and control bleeding, particularly before surgery","Iron supplementation to correct iron-deficiency anaemia","Uterine artery embolisation as a uterus-preserving radiological option","Myomectomy, including hysteroscopic resection for submucosal lesions, to preserve fertility, or hysterectomy for definitive treatment","Surveillance of fibroid growth with ultrasound and reconsideration of the diagnosis if there is rapid enlargement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Berek & Novak's Gynecology","NICE guideline: Heavy menstrual bleeding — assessment and management","FIGO PALM-COEIN classification of abnormal uterine bleeding"],"crossLinks":[]},"uterine_fibroids":{"name":"Uterine fibroids","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["leiomyoma","myoma","fibromyoma","uterine leiomyomata"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Fibroids are benign monoclonal tumours of uterine smooth muscle and are the most common pelvic tumour in women","Many are asymptomatic; when symptomatic, they commonly cause heavy or prolonged menstrual bleeding, pelvic pressure, bulk-related urinary or bowel symptoms, and sometimes subfertility","Location matters more than size: submucosal fibroids are most associated with abnormal bleeding and reproductive problems, while subserosal fibroids tend to cause pressure symptoms","They are oestrogen and progesterone dependent, so they typically grow during the reproductive years and regress after the menopause","Treatment is guided by symptoms, fibroid characteristics, and the woman's fertility wishes, ranging from expectant management to medical therapy, uterine artery embolisation, myomectomy, or hysterectomy","Medical options aim to reduce bleeding and shrink fibroids, including hormonal treatments and agents that suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis","Rapid growth, particularly after the menopause, should raise suspicion of the rare leiomyosarcoma","In pregnancy fibroids may undergo red (carneous) degeneration causing acute pain, and can be associated with malpresentation and obstructed labour"],"pathophysiology":"Fibroids arise from a single transformed myometrial smooth muscle cell that proliferates under the influence of ovarian sex steroids to form a well-circumscribed, pseudo-encapsulated mass of smooth muscle and extracellular matrix. Oestrogen and progesterone, along with local growth factors, drive their growth, which is why they enlarge in the reproductive years and shrink after menopause. Heavy bleeding results from an increased and distorted endometrial surface, altered local vasculature and prostaglandins, and impaired uterine contraction. As fibroids outgrow their blood supply they can undergo hyaline, cystic, calcific, or red degeneration.","additionalDifferentials":["Adenomyosis","Endometrial polyp","Ovarian mass","Uterine leiomyosarcoma","Pregnancy","Endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma as a cause of abnormal bleeding"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal and transabdominal ultrasound to characterise number, size, and location","Saline infusion sonohysterography or hysteroscopy to define submucosal fibroids and the cavity","MRI for detailed mapping before myomectomy or embolisation and to help distinguish adenomyosis","Full blood count to detect iron-deficiency anaemia from heavy bleeding","Endometrial sampling when there is abnormal bleeding with risk factors for endometrial pathology"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging uterine mass or postmenopausal growth suggesting leiomyosarcoma","Severe anaemia from heavy menstrual bleeding","Acute severe pain from fibroid degeneration or torsion of a pedunculated fibroid","Hydronephrosis from ureteric compression by a large mass","Acute urinary retention from a large or cervical fibroid"],"prognosis":"Fibroids are benign and most women have a good outcome; malignant transformation is very rare. Symptoms often stabilise or regress after the menopause. Recurrence after uterine-conserving treatments such as myomectomy is possible, whereas hysterectomy is definitive but ends fertility.","pitfalls":["Assuming all abnormal bleeding in a woman with fibroids is due to the fibroids and missing coexisting endometrial pathology","Overlooking submucosal fibroids as a cause of infertility or recurrent bleeding when only transabdominal imaging is used","Attributing a rapidly growing mass to a benign fibroid without considering sarcoma","Neglecting to treat iron-deficiency anaemia","Failing to consider fibroid degeneration as a cause of acute pain in pregnancy"],"management":["Reassurance and expectant management with monitoring for asymptomatic fibroids","First-line medical therapy for heavy bleeding with the levonorgestrel intrauterine system, tranexamic acid, NSAIDs or combined hormonal contraception","Hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian suppression with GnRH agonists plus add-back therapy, or GnRH antagonists, to reduce bleeding and fibroid volume, often pre-operatively","Correction of iron-deficiency anaemia with iron supplementation","Uterine artery embolisation for uterus-conserving symptom control","Myomectomy for women wishing to preserve fertility, with hysteroscopic resection of submucosal lesions","Hysterectomy as definitive treatment when childbearing is complete","Endometrial sampling and specialist review where bleeding is atypical or there are risk factors for endometrial pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"uterine_inversion":{"name":"Uterine inversion","system":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["inverted uterus","puerperal uterine inversion","acute uterine inversion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / RCOG postpartum haemorrhage and emergency obstetrics guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Uterine inversion is the turning inside-out of the uterine fundus through the cervix, most often as an acute complication of the third stage of labour","It classically presents with sudden severe lower abdominal pain, haemorrhage, and shock that appears disproportionate to visible blood loss","A key contributor is excessive cord traction or fundal pressure before placental separation, particularly with a relaxed uterus","On examination the fundus may be absent from the abdomen and a mass may be palpable or visible at the introitus or in the vagina","Profound, partly neurogenic (vagally mediated) shock is characteristic and may be out of proportion to bleeding","Immediate management is resuscitation and prompt manual replacement of the uterus, ideally before a constriction ring forms","Uterine relaxation with a tocolytic or anaesthetic agent can facilitate replacement, after which uterotonics are given to restore tone","If manual replacement fails, hydrostatic techniques or surgical repositioning are required"],"pathophysiology":"Uterine inversion occurs when the fundus descends and passes through the dilated cervix, typically when an atonic or partially relaxed uterus is subjected to traction on the cord or pressure on the fundus while the placenta remains adherent, especially with a fundal placental site. The inverted uterus and its attachments produce intense stimulation of pelvic autonomic nerves, causing a vagally mediated component to the shock alongside haemorrhage from the placental bed. A constriction ring may form at the cervix, trapping the inverted fundus and making replacement progressively harder, which is why speed of recognition and relaxation of the uterus are central to management.","additionalDifferentials":["Uterine atony with postpartum haemorrhage","Retained placenta","Large prolapsing uterine or submucosal fibroid","Genital tract trauma with haematoma","Amniotic fluid embolism causing collapse","Other causes of postpartum collapse such as major haemorrhage or sepsis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination confirming absence of the fundus abdominally and a vaginal or introital mass","Full blood count and coagulation studies","Group and crossmatch with activation of major haemorrhage support","Continuous monitoring of vital signs and urine output","Ultrasound if the diagnosis is uncertain and time permits","Assessment for retained placenta once the uterus is replaced"],"redFlags":["Postpartum collapse with shock disproportionate to visible blood loss","A fundal mass at the introitus or in the vagina with an absent abdominal fundus","Failed manual replacement or formation of a tight constriction ring","Ongoing haemorrhage after repositioning suggesting atony or retained placenta"],"prognosis":"With immediate recognition, resuscitation, and prompt replacement, most women recover fully. Delay increases the risk of severe haemorrhage, prolonged shock, need for surgery, and, rarely, hysterectomy or maternal death.","pitfalls":["Applying cord traction or fundal pressure before placental separation, precipitating inversion","Attributing profound shock solely to blood loss and missing the neurogenic component","Delaying replacement so that a constriction ring forms and prevents manual reduction","Removing an adherent placenta before replacing the uterus, worsening haemorrhage"],"management":["Immediate call for help with aggressive resuscitation for the combined haemorrhagic and neurogenic shock using intravenous fluids and blood products","Prompt manual replacement of the fundus by the Johnson manoeuvre before a constriction ring forms, leaving an adherent placenta in situ until after replacement","Uterine relaxation with tocolytics or volatile anaesthetic agents to facilitate replacement","Uterotonics such as oxytocin, ergometrine and prostaglandins once replaced, to restore tone and prevent re-inversion","Hydrostatic replacement by the O'Sullivan technique or surgical replacement if manual reduction fails","Manual removal of the retained placenta after the uterus is repositioned","Activation of the major haemorrhage protocol with senior obstetric and anaesthetic support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","RCOG guidance on postpartum haemorrhage","Emergency obstetric management protocols"],"crossLinks":[]},"uterine_prolapse":{"name":"Uterine Prolapse","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pelvic organ prolapse","uterovaginal prolapse","descent of the uterus","genital prolapse"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Uterine prolapse is descent of the uterus and cervix into or beyond the vagina due to weakening of the pelvic floor supports, and it often coexists with prolapse of the vaginal walls, bladder, and rectum","Typical symptoms include a sensation of vaginal bulge or something coming down, pelvic heaviness or dragging, and symptoms that worsen through the day and with standing or straining","Associated urinary, bowel, and sexual dysfunction are common and should be assessed directly, as women may not volunteer them","Major risk factors include vaginal childbirth, increasing age and menopause, chronically raised intra-abdominal pressure from obesity, chronic cough or constipation, and connective tissue laxity","Severity is graded by how far the leading part of the prolapse descends relative to the hymen, using a standardised staging system","Conservative management with pelvic floor muscle training and vaginal pessaries is appropriate for many women, particularly if surgery is undesirable or unsafe","Surgical options range from uterine-preserving procedures to hysterectomy with vault support, and the choice depends on symptoms, fertility wishes, and fitness","Vaginal oestrogen can improve atrophic tissue and support pessary use in postmenopausal women"],"pathophysiology":"The uterus and vagina are held in position by the levator ani muscles and the endopelvic fascia and ligaments, including the uterosacral and cardinal ligaments. Damage to these supports, most often from stretching and denervation during vaginal delivery, together with age-related and oestrogen-deficiency-related weakening of connective tissue and muscle, allows the pelvic organs to descend. Sustained increases in intra-abdominal pressure accelerate this process. As support fails, the uterus and cervix prolapse downward, frequently dragging the vaginal walls and adjacent bladder and rectum with them.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystocele or rectocele without significant uterine descent","Vaginal vault prolapse after hysterectomy","Enterocele","Large vaginal or cervical cyst or fibroid","Chronic uterine inversion","Urethral diverticulum or other vaginal mass"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pelvic examination in different positions and with straining to define the compartments and stage","Assessment of pelvic floor muscle strength","Urinary assessment including post-void residual and urodynamics where incontinence complicates management","Consideration of imaging for large or atypical masses","Evaluation and treatment of contributing factors such as constipation, chronic cough, and atrophy"],"redFlags":["Ulceration, bleeding, or infection of exposed prolapsed tissue","Urinary retention or obstructed voiding from advanced prolapse","Hydronephrosis or renal impairment from ureteric kinking in severe procidentia","New abnormal bleeding requiring exclusion of malignancy","Complete procidentia that cannot be reduced, needing prompt specialist assessment"],"prognosis":"Uterine prolapse is a benign but often progressive quality-of-life condition; conservative measures relieve symptoms in many women, and surgery provides good results though prolapse can recur in other compartments over time. Prognosis is favourable overall, and attention to modifiable risk factors such as weight, constipation, and chronic cough helps limit progression and recurrence.","pitfalls":["Failing to ask about associated urinary, bowel, and sexual symptoms","Overlooking coexisting prolapse in other vaginal compartments","Missing occult stress incontinence unmasked after prolapse correction","Neglecting to exclude malignancy in a woman with abnormal bleeding","Not treating vaginal atrophy or constipation that undermines pessary use and surgical outcomes"],"management":["Physiotherapy-supervised pelvic floor muscle training for mild-to-moderate prolapse","Vaginal pessary such as a ring or shelf device for conservative mechanical support, with regular review","Topical vaginal oestrogen for postmenopausal atrophy and to protect the vaginal mucosa in pessary users","Lifestyle and risk-factor modification including weight reduction, treatment of chronic cough and constipation, and avoidance of heavy lifting","Reconstructive surgery such as vaginal hysterectomy, sacrospinous fixation or sacrocolpopexy for symptomatic prolapse when conservative measures fail","Colpocleisis as an obliterative option for frail women who do not desire vaginal intercourse","Assessment and management of coexisting urinary, bowel and sexual dysfunction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"uterine_rupture":{"name":"Uterine rupture","system":"Reproductive / Obstetrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ruptured uterus","uterine scar rupture"],"chapter":null,"source":"RCOG Green-top Guideline / Williams Obstetrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Uterine rupture is a full-thickness disruption of the uterine wall involving the myometrium and overlying serosa, often with the membranes, which may extrude the fetus or placenta into the peritoneal cavity; it is distinguished from dehiscence, a partial scar separation with intact serosa","The commonest setting is labour in a uterus scarred by previous caesarean section, particularly attempted vaginal birth after a previous classical or inverted-T incision","Other risk factors include previous myomectomy or uterine surgery, injudicious use of oxytocin or prostaglandins, obstructed or prolonged labour, grand multiparity, uterine anomaly, trauma, and difficult instrumental delivery","The most consistent and often earliest sign is an abnormal fetal heart rate pattern, particularly a prolonged deceleration or bradycardia","Maternal features include acute scar or abdominal pain that may break through effective regional analgesia, vaginal bleeding, cessation of contractions, loss of the presenting part with a rising station, palpable fetal parts abdominally, haematuria, and signs of hypovolaemic shock","Management is immediate resuscitation and emergency laparotomy with delivery of the fetus, followed by repair of the defect or, when repair is not feasible, hysterectomy","Massive obstetric haemorrhage should be anticipated, so major-haemorrhage protocols, blood products, and senior obstetric and anaesthetic support are essential","The recurrence risk in a subsequent pregnancy influences counselling about mode of delivery, and induction or augmentation of a scarred uterus must be undertaken with caution"],"pathophysiology":"The pregnant uterine wall is subjected to rising intrauterine pressure during contractions. A previous full-thickness scar, or an area weakened by surgery, adenomyosis, or a congenital anomaly, is less elastic and less vascular than surrounding myometrium and can separate under repeated stretching and contraction. When augmentation with oxytocin or prostaglandins produces excessive uterine activity, or when labour is obstructed and the lower segment thins progressively, the wall can give way. Rupture opens uterine vessels, causing haemorrhage into the peritoneum or vagina, and loss of wall integrity abolishes effective contractions and can expel the fetus and placenta from the cavity, abruptly cutting off uteroplacental perfusion. The result is rapid fetal hypoxia and maternal haemorrhagic shock.","additionalDifferentials":["Placental abruption","Placenta praevia or other antepartum haemorrhage","Scar dehiscence with incomplete separation","Other causes of an acute abdomen in pregnancy such as appendicitis or a ruptured viscus","Amniotic fluid embolism causing sudden collapse and fetal compromise","Concealed haemorrhage from another source presenting with hypovolaemic shock"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous cardiotocography, since an abnormal fetal heart rate is often the first clue","Urgent clinical abdominal assessment for tenderness, palpable fetal parts, and loss of station","Full blood count, coagulation screen, fibrinogen, and group-and-save or crossmatch","Ultrasound, if time permits, for free intraperitoneal fluid, fetal position, and viability","Urinalysis or catheter specimen for haematuria suggesting bladder involvement or lower-segment extension","Blood gas and lactate to gauge maternal and fetal compromise","Definitive confirmation at emergency laparotomy"],"redFlags":["Prolonged fetal bradycardia or a sudden severe fetal heart rate abnormality in a labouring woman with a scar","Breakthrough scar or abdominal pain despite regional analgesia","Maternal haemodynamic instability or signs of hypovolaemic shock","Cessation of uterine contractions with recession of the presenting part","Palpable fetal parts through the abdominal wall or new haematuria"],"prognosis":"Prompt recognition and immediate operative delivery are associated with maternal survival and, if the fetus is delivered quickly, a reasonable neonatal outcome. Delay leads to fetal hypoxic injury or death and maternal exsanguination, and future pregnancies carry a raised risk of recurrence.","pitfalls":["Attributing early fetal heart rate changes to benign causes in a woman attempting vaginal birth after caesarean","Aggressive augmentation of a scarred or obstructed uterus with uterotonics","Misreading breakthrough pain under epidural as merely inadequate analgesia","Underestimating blood loss and activating major-haemorrhage support too late","Failing to counsel about recurrence risk and delivery planning in subsequent pregnancies"],"management":["Immediate maternal resuscitation under the major haemorrhage protocol with wide-bore access, crystalloid and blood products","Emergency laparotomy with immediate delivery of the foetus as the definitive management","Surgical repair of the uterine defect where feasible, or hysterectomy when repair is not possible or bleeding is uncontrolled","Neonatal resuscitation team on standby for hypoxic-ischaemic compromise","Senior obstetric and anaesthetic involvement with intraoperative haemostasis and correction of coagulopathy","Prevention through cautious use of oxytocin and prostaglandins and careful selection and monitoring of vaginal birth after caesarean","Counselling about the high recurrence risk with recommendation of planned caesarean before labour in future pregnancies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["RCOG Green-top Guideline on birth after previous caesarean birth","Williams Obstetrics","Oxford Handbook of Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"utricle_cyst":{"name":"Prostatic utricle cyst","system":"Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prostatic utricle cyst","utricular cyst","enlarged prostatic utricle"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / EAU Guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A midline cyst arising from dilatation of the prostatic utricle, which opens into the prostatic urethra at the verumontanum","Strongly associated with genitourinary anomalies such as hypospadias, cryptorchidism, unilateral renal agenesis and disorders of sex development","It usually presents in childhood or adolescence, in contrast to mullerian duct cysts, which present later","Because it communicates with the urethra it may contain spermatozoa, unlike a mullerian duct cyst","It can cause recurrent urinary tract infection, post-void dribbling, epididymitis, stone formation within the cyst, or infertility","Voiding cystourethrography may show the cyst filling from the urethra, while MRI defines the surrounding anatomy","Small asymptomatic cysts are observed, whereas symptomatic ones may need transurethral or surgical treatment"],"pathophysiology":"The prostatic utricle is a small midline mullerian remnant that opens into the prostatic urethra at the verumontanum. Incomplete regression, often linked with under-virilisation, allows it to dilate into a cyst. Because it communicates with the urethra, urine can reflux into it, predisposing to stasis, infection and stone formation, and when spermatozoa enter it the cyst may contain sperm.","additionalDifferentials":["Mullerian duct cyst, which has no urethral communication","Ejaculatory duct cyst","Seminal vesicle cyst, which is laterally placed","Bladder diverticulum","Posterior urethral pathology","Cystic prostatic mass"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Voiding cystourethrography to demonstrate communication with the urethra","Pelvic MRI for midline anatomy and associated anomalies","Retrograde urethrography or urethroscopy to view the utricular opening","Cyst fluid analysis for spermatozoa","Renal tract imaging for associated anomalies","Assessment for disorders of sex development where indicated"],"redFlags":["Recurrent infection, abscess or stone formation within the cyst","Association with disorders of sex development requiring endocrine and genetic evaluation","Obstructive symptoms or upper urinary tract compromise","Rare malignant change in long-standing cysts"],"prognosis":"Generally good; small cysts are asymptomatic and stable, while symptomatic utricle cysts respond to endoscopic or surgical treatment, although proximity to the ejaculatory ducts and continence structures requires care.","pitfalls":["Confusing a utricle cyst, which communicates with the urethra and may contain sperm, with a mullerian duct cyst","Missing associated hypospadias, cryptorchidism, renal agenesis or a disorder of sex development","Damaging the ejaculatory ducts or continence mechanism during intervention","Overlooking the cyst as a cause of recurrent urinary tract infection or epididymitis in a boy"],"management":["Conservative observation for small, asymptomatic cysts","Antimicrobial treatment of associated recurrent urinary tract infection and epididymitis","Endoscopic transurethral incision or de-roofing for symptomatic cysts","Open, laparoscopic or robotic excision for large or recurrent cysts, taking care to preserve the ejaculatory ducts and continence mechanism","Removal or treatment of any intracystic calculi","Evaluation and management of associated genitourinary anomalies such as hypospadias, cryptorchidism and renal agenesis","Assessment for disorders of sex development with appropriate referral and fertility counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","EAU Guidelines"],"crossLinks":["mullerian_duct_cyst","seminal_vesicle_cyst"]},"uveitic_glaucoma":{"name":"Uveitic glaucoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["inflammatory glaucoma","glaucoma secondary to uveitis","uveitic ocular hypertension","inflammatory ocular hypertension"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A secondary glaucoma arising in eyes with intraocular inflammation, with pressure elevation resulting from several coexisting mechanisms rather than a single cause","Open-angle mechanisms include trabecular obstruction by inflammatory cells and debris, trabeculitis, and a steroid-induced pressure response from treatment","Angle-closure mechanisms include pupillary block from extensive posterior synechiae with iris bombe, and peripheral anterior synechiae from chronic angle inflammation","Pressure may paradoxically be low during active inflammation from ciliary hyposecretion, then surge as inflammation settles or steroids are used","Careful gonioscopy is essential to distinguish open-angle from synechial or pupillary-block mechanisms because treatment differs","Management balances vigorous control of inflammation with pressure-lowering therapy, while recognising that corticosteroids can both reduce inflammatory outflow obstruction and independently raise pressure","Fuchs heterochromic uveitis and herpetic anterior uveitis are characteristically associated with raised pressure","Prostaglandin analogues are used with caution as they may theoretically aggravate inflammation and cystoid macular oedema"],"pathophysiology":"In uveitic glaucoma intraocular inflammation raises pressure through multiple overlapping pathways. Inflammatory cells, fibrin, and protein clog the trabecular meshwork, and direct inflammation of the meshwork (trabeculitis) reduces outflow facility; scarring can permanently damage the drainage tissue. Chronic inflammation promotes peripheral anterior synechiae that close the angle, while dense posterior synechiae binding the pupil margin to the lens can produce pupillary block with forward bowing of the iris (iris bombe) and secondary angle closure. Corticosteroids used to treat the uveitis may themselves reduce outflow and cause a steroid-induced pressure rise. Active inflammation can transiently lower pressure by suppressing aqueous production, masking a tendency to later elevation.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary open-angle glaucoma with coincidental uveitis","Steroid-induced (corticosteroid-responder) glaucoma","Posner-Schlossman syndrome (glaucomatocyclitic crisis)","Neovascular glaucoma","Primary or secondary angle-closure glaucoma from other causes","Herpetic or Fuchs heterochromic uveitis with raised pressure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination to grade inflammation, keratic precipitates, and synechiae","Gonioscopy to identify open-angle, synechial, or pupillary-block mechanisms","Applanation tonometry with monitoring during and after inflammatory episodes and steroid treatment","OCT of the optic nerve and macula to assess glaucomatous damage and cystoid oedema","Automated perimetry to monitor field loss","Targeted workup for the underlying uveitis, including infective and systemic causes when indicated"],"redFlags":["Iris bombe from a completely bound-down pupil producing acute pupillary-block angle closure","A marked steroid-induced pressure rise during treatment of the inflammation","Very high pressure with corneal oedema during an inflammatory crisis","Progressive optic-nerve damage masked by intermittently low pressure during active uveitis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on controlling the underlying inflammation and the mechanism of pressure rise; open-angle inflammatory hypertension often resolves as the uveitis settles, whereas extensive synechial closure and repeated crises lead to chronic glaucoma that may need drainage surgery and carries a guarded outlook.","pitfalls":["Attributing all pressure elevation to steroid response and undertreating the inflammation, or vice versa","Missing pupillary block and iris bombe requiring urgent iridotomy rather than only medical therapy","Being reassured by low pressure during active inflammation and overlooking later surges","Using prostaglandin analogues uncritically where they may worsen inflammation or macular oedema","Failing to investigate and treat the underlying cause of the uveitis"],"management":["Vigorous control of the underlying intraocular inflammation with topical, periocular or systemic corticosteroids, plus steroid-sparing immunosuppression where indicated","Specific treatment of any identified infective or systemic cause of the uveitis","Cycloplegic and mydriatic agents to prevent and break posterior synechiae","Intraocular pressure lowering with aqueous suppressants such as topical beta-blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and alpha-agonists, generally avoiding prostaglandin analogues and miotics that may aggravate inflammation","Recognition and management of a steroid-induced pressure rise by adjusting the steroid agent, route or potency","Laser peripheral iridotomy or surgical iridectomy for pupillary-block angle closure from iris bombe","Glaucoma drainage surgery such as trabeculectomy with antimetabolite or a drainage device for uncontrolled pressure","Regular monitoring with gonioscopy, tonometry, optic nerve optical coherence tomography and automated perimetry"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Shields' Textbook of Glaucoma"],"crossLinks":[]},"vacterl_association":{"name":"VACTERL association","system":"Genetics / Craniofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VATER association","VACTERL/VATER spectrum","vertebral-anal-cardiac-tracheoesophageal-renal-limb association"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A non-random co-occurrence of congenital defects; the acronym denotes Vertebral anomalies, Anal atresia, Cardiac defects, Tracheo-oesophageal fistula, Renal anomalies, and Limb (usually radial) defects","Diagnosis is clinical and conventionally requires at least three of the component features in the absence of an alternative unifying syndrome","It is an association rather than a syndrome, meaning the components occur together more often than by chance but without a single known cause","Tracheo-oesophageal fistula with oesophageal atresia often presents in the newborn with choking, drooling, and an inability to pass a feeding tube","Anorectal malformation may be evident at birth as an absent or abnormally placed anus","Limb involvement is typically preaxial or radial, such as a hypoplastic or absent thumb or radius","Intellect is usually normal, and its presence should prompt reconsideration of a specific syndromic diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"VACTERL association is understood as a pattern of defects affecting structures that develop during a shared early embryonic window, particularly disturbances of blastogenesis and midline mesodermal patterning. The components involve organs derived from or dependent on interactions between mesoderm and endoderm, and disruption of common developmental signalling pathways, including those governing left-right and cranio-caudal patterning, is thought to underlie the clustering. No single genetic cause explains most cases, and it remains a diagnosis of exclusion; recognisable single-gene disorders such as Fanconi anaemia and mitochondrial or ciliary conditions can produce an overlapping phenotype and must be considered before accepting the label.","additionalDifferentials":["Fanconi anaemia (radial defects with marrow failure)","CHARGE syndrome","Currarino syndrome (sacral, anorectal, presacral mass)","22q11.2 deletion syndrome","Feingold syndrome","Townes-Brocks syndrome","Baller-Gerold syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Spinal and skeletal radiographs to identify vertebral segmentation and rib anomalies","Echocardiography to characterise congenital heart disease","Contrast or endoscopic evaluation and attempts to pass a nasogastric tube for tracheo-oesophageal fistula","Renal and abdominal ultrasound to detect urinary tract malformation","Limb radiographs to define radial ray defects","Chromosome breakage studies to exclude Fanconi anaemia and targeted genetic testing"],"redFlags":["Neonatal respiratory distress and aspiration from tracheo-oesophageal fistula requiring urgent surgery","High or complex anorectal malformation needing early surgical management","Ductal-dependent or complex cardiac lesion presenting with collapse","Bilateral renal anomalies threatening kidney function","Features suggesting Fanconi anaemia, which carries marrow failure and malignancy risk"],"prognosis":"Prognosis reflects the number and severity of component defects; with modern neonatal surgery and multidisciplinary care many children survive with good function and normal cognition, though cardiac and renal severity are the main determinants of outcome.","pitfalls":["Accepting the label without excluding Fanconi anaemia and other definable syndromes","Missing a tracheo-oesophageal fistula in a newborn who chokes with feeds","Failing to complete systematic screening of all component systems once one defect is found","Assuming intellectual disability, which is not typical of the association","Overlooking occult vertebral or renal anomalies that alter management"],"management":["Staged surgical correction of individual anomalies, including repair of tracheo-oesophageal fistula and oesophageal atresia, anorectal malformation reconstruction, and cardiac surgery as needed","Neonatal stabilisation with airway protection, prevention of aspiration and safe feeding around the tracheo-oesophageal defect","Correction of radial ray and other limb defects with orthopaedic and hand surgery","Investigation and management of renal and urological anomalies to preserve renal function","Chromosome breakage testing to exclude Fanconi anaemia before accepting the diagnosis","Multidisciplinary coordination across neonatology, paediatric surgery, cardiology, urology, orthopaedics and clinical genetics","Long-term developmental follow-up and family support, with genetic counselling regarding the generally low recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: VACTERL Association"],"crossLinks":[]},"vaginismus":{"name":"Vaginismus","system":"Reproductive medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["genito-pelvic pain/penetration disorder","vaginal muscle spasm","penetration disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard sleep & reproductive medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Vaginismus is characterised by involuntary contraction of the pelvic floor and perivaginal muscles that makes vaginal penetration painful, difficult, or impossible","It is now grouped with dyspareunia under the umbrella of genito-pelvic pain/penetration disorder, reflecting their frequent overlap","It may be primary, present from the first attempts at penetration, or secondary, developing after a period of pain-free intercourse","Fear and anticipatory anxiety about pain are central, producing a self-reinforcing cycle of muscle guarding, avoidance, and further distress","The muscle spasm is involuntary and not under conscious control, a point patients need reassured of to reduce shame and self-blame","Examination should be gentle and patient-led, and marked distress or guarding on attempted examination itself supports the diagnosis","Organic causes of pain such as infection, atrophy, or structural abnormality should be excluded, since untreated dyspareunia can trigger secondary vaginismus","Treatment combines education, pelvic floor physiotherapy, graded desensitisation often using vaginal trainers, and psychological or psychosexual therapy addressing anxiety"],"pathophysiology":"Vaginismus reflects a conditioned, involuntary reflex contraction of the pelvic floor musculature in anticipation of, or response to, attempted vaginal penetration. A trigger, which may be an initial painful experience, infection, trauma, negative sexual conditioning, or fear, leads to protective muscle tightening; the resulting pain reinforces the expectation of pain, so anxiety and guarding become self-perpetuating. Over time the response can be provoked by anticipation alone, independent of any ongoing organic cause. Because the mechanism is largely a learned protective reflex intertwined with anxiety, both the physical muscle response and the psychological component must be addressed for resolution.","additionalDifferentials":["Dyspareunia from other causes","Vulvodynia or provoked vestibulodynia","Vaginal atrophy from oestrogen deficiency","Pelvic infection or inflammation","Congenital anatomical abnormality such as a vaginal septum","Endometriosis or other pelvic pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sensitive sexual and psychological history including any trauma or triggering event","Gentle, patient-controlled genital and pelvic examination as tolerated","Exclusion of infection, atrophy, or dermatological vulval conditions","Assessment of pelvic floor muscle tone, often with physiotherapy input","Evaluation for structural anomalies when penetration has never been possible"],"redFlags":["History of sexual trauma or abuse requiring sensitive, specialised care","Findings suggesting an organic structural or gynaecological cause of pain","Signs of infection or inflammation needing directed treatment","Severe psychological distress or relationship breakdown"],"prognosis":"Vaginismus generally has a good prognosis, with high success rates when treated by a combined approach of education, pelvic floor physiotherapy, graded desensitisation, and psychological support tailored to the individual.","pitfalls":["Attempting a forceful examination, which worsens pain, fear, and guarding","Treating it as purely psychological and neglecting pelvic floor physiotherapy","Missing an organic cause of dyspareunia that precipitated secondary vaginismus","Failing to involve the partner and address relationship and anxiety factors"],"management":["Patient education and reassurance that the muscle spasm is involuntary, addressing shame and the pain-anxiety cycle","Pelvic floor physiotherapy to teach relaxation, awareness and control of the perivaginal muscles","Graded vaginal desensitisation using progressively sized dilators or trainers at the woman's own pace","Psychological therapy such as cognitive behavioural therapy and sex therapy to address anticipatory anxiety and any underlying trauma","Treatment of contributing organic factors such as infection, vulval dermatoses or atrophy","Involvement of the partner with couple-based therapy where appropriate","Consideration of adjuncts such as topical local anaesthetic or pelvic floor botulinum toxin in refractory cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"valsalva_retinopathy":{"name":"Valsalva retinopathy","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Valsalva haemorrhagic retinopathy","Valsalva maculopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with sudden, painless unilateral loss of vision, a central scotoma or floaters immediately after a Valsalva-type effort such as heavy lifting, coughing, vomiting, straining or childbirth","The characteristic finding is a well-circumscribed, dome-shaped pre-retinal haemorrhage at the macula lying beneath the internal limiting membrane or posterior hyaloid","The blood may develop a horizontal fluid level as it settles","Vision depends on whether the fovea is obscured by the haemorrhage","The affected eye is otherwise usually healthy, without retinal vascular disease","Optical coherence tomography helps localise the blood to the sub-internal limiting membrane or sub-hyaloid space","Most cases are managed by observation as the haemorrhage clears spontaneously, with Nd:YAG hyaloidotomy or vitrectomy reserved for dense or persistent bleeding"],"pathophysiology":"A sudden rise in intrathoracic or intra-abdominal pressure against a closed glottis is transmitted to the ocular venous system, producing an abrupt spike in retinal venous and capillary pressure. This ruptures fragile superficial perifoveal capillaries, and blood collects in the potential space beneath the internal limiting membrane or the posterior hyaloid face, typically pooling at the macula.","additionalDifferentials":["Posterior vitreous detachment with haemorrhage","Retinal arterial macroaneurysm","Proliferative diabetic retinopathy","Retinal tear or detachment","Terson syndrome","Haemorrhage from neovascular age-related macular degeneration","Bleeding related to a blood dyscrasia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination to identify the dome-shaped pre-retinal haemorrhage","Optical coherence tomography to confirm the sub-internal limiting membrane or sub-hyaloid location","A clear history of a precipitating Valsalva manoeuvre","Full blood count and clotting studies if the event is spontaneous or recurrent","B-scan ultrasound when the haemorrhage obscures the fundus view","Blood pressure measurement"],"redFlags":["Bilateral or spontaneous haemorrhage without a clear Valsalva trigger, prompting investigation for a bleeding disorder","Associated systemic bleeding or bruising","Dense vitreous haemorrhage obscuring underlying pathology","Features suggesting an underlying retinal vascular disease"],"prognosis":"The outlook is generally excellent, with most haemorrhages clearing spontaneously over weeks to months and full recovery of vision.","pitfalls":["Causing unnecessary alarm or over-investigating a self-limiting condition","Missing an underlying bleeding disorder in atypical or recurrent cases","Assuming every pre-retinal haemorrhage is due to Valsalva","Intervening prematurely when observation would allow spontaneous clearance"],"management":["Reassurance and conservative observation, as most pre-retinal haemorrhages clear spontaneously","Advice to avoid further Valsalva-type straining and heavy lifting during recovery","Upright posture to encourage settling of the macular haemorrhage","Nd:YAG or argon laser membranotomy to drain a persistent sub-hyaloid or sub-internal limiting membrane haemorrhage obscuring the fovea","Pars plana vitrectomy for non-clearing, dense or breakthrough vitreous haemorrhage","Investigation of an underlying bleeding tendency, anaemia or hypertension when the event is spontaneous or recurrent","Serial fundus examination and optical coherence tomography to monitor resolution"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course: Retina and Vitreous"],"crossLinks":[]},"van_buchem_disease":{"name":"Van Buchem disease (SOST regulatory)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hyperostosis corticalis generalisata","endosteal hyperostosis, Van Buchem type","SOST enhancer deletion disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism (ASBMR)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive sclerosing dysplasia caused by a deletion of a downstream regulatory (enhancer) element that controls SOST expression, reducing sclerostin production","Closely related to sclerosteosis but generally milder, and importantly it lacks the syndactyly and overgrowth of stature seen in sclerosteosis","Reduced sclerostin releases the brake on Wnt-mediated bone formation, producing generalised osteosclerosis with prominent mandibular enlargement","Cranial and skull-base thickening entraps cranial nerves, causing facial palsy, hearing loss, and sometimes optic compromise","Patients typically have raised alkaline phosphatase reflecting increased bone formation, with normal calcium and phosphate","Radiographs show endosteal cortical thickening of long bones and dense sclerotic skull and jaw","Care centres on surgical decompression of entrapped nerves and surveillance for raised intracranial pressure rather than pharmacological bone reduction"],"pathophysiology":"Van Buchem disease is caused by homozygous deletion of a conserved regulatory region located downstream of the SOST gene. This element normally drives SOST transcription in bone, so its loss markedly reduces sclerostin expression while leaving the coding sequence intact. Diminished sclerostin fails to inhibit the Wnt/beta-catenin pathway, resulting in increased osteoblastic bone formation and progressive generalised hyperostosis. Because sclerostin is reduced rather than completely absent as in coding-mutation sclerosteosis, the phenotype is usually less severe, but skull and jaw overgrowth still narrows cranial foramina and can compress cranial nerves.","additionalDifferentials":["Sclerosteosis","Camurati-Engelmann disease","Osteopetrosis","Craniometaphyseal dysplasia","Pycnodysostosis","Paget disease of the skull"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs showing endosteal cortical thickening and dense skull and mandible","Serum alkaline phosphatase and bone turnover markers","Serum calcium and phosphate to confirm normal mineral homeostasis","Genetic testing for the SOST regulatory-region deletion","Skull-base imaging for foraminal narrowing and nerve compression","Audiology and visual assessment with monitoring for raised intracranial pressure"],"redFlags":["Progressive facial-nerve weakness or deteriorating hearing or vision","Signs of raised intracranial pressure","Brainstem compression from posterior skull-base overgrowth","Acute neurological decline requiring urgent surgical decompression"],"prognosis":"Generally milder and more indolent than sclerosteosis, with a normal lifespan in most patients, but progressive cranial nerve entrapment can cause facial palsy and sensory loss that may require surgical relief.","pitfalls":["Assuming a coding SOST mutation when the underlying lesion is a regulatory-region deletion","Confusing it with sclerosteosis and expecting syndactyly or gigantism, which are absent here","Overlooking treatable cranial nerve entrapment presenting as isolated facial palsy or deafness","Failing to monitor for raised intracranial pressure over time"],"management":["No disease-specific pharmacotherapy; management centres on surveillance and surgical relief of complications","Surgical decompression of narrowed cranial foramina to relieve facial nerve, auditory or optic nerve entrapment","Audiological monitoring with hearing rehabilitation and hearing aids for hearing loss","Ophthalmic surveillance and management of optic nerve compromise and raised intracranial pressure","Serial skull-base and long-bone imaging to detect progressive hyperostosis and foraminal narrowing","Biochemical monitoring of alkaline phosphatase with confirmation of normal calcium and phosphate","Autosomal recessive genetic counselling and cascade family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism (ASBMR)","Peer-reviewed reviews of Van Buchem disease and SOST regulation"],"crossLinks":[]},"van_neck_disease":{"name":"Van Neck disease","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ischiopubic osteochondrosis","van Neck-Odelberg disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric orthopaedic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign osteochondrosis of the ischiopubic synchondrosis in children","It represents exuberant or asymmetric ossification at the junction of the ischium and pubis during normal development","It may be asymptomatic or cause groin, buttock or hip pain and a limp","The key issue is that its radiographic appearance can mimic osteomyelitis, Ewing sarcoma or a healing fracture","MRI can show marrow oedema that adds to the confusion with more serious pathology","Recognition of this normal variant avoids unnecessary biopsy and treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Van Neck disease reflects a normal but sometimes asymmetric or prominent stage in the ossification and fusion of the ischiopubic synchondrosis, the cartilaginous junction between the ischium and pubis that normally fuses in later childhood. The expanded, sometimes fragmented, appearance and associated marrow oedema are part of physiological development, but they can radiographically resemble infection or tumour. It is essentially a self-resolving developmental variant rather than a disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteomyelitis of the pelvis","Ewing sarcoma or other bone tumour","Pelvic (ischiopubic) stress or avulsion fracture","Other osteochondroses","Septic arthritis of the hip"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Radiographs, ideally with comparison of both sides and follow-up","MRI where infection or tumour must be excluded","Inflammatory markers to help exclude infection","Clinical correlation and observation over time","Avoidance of biopsy once the diagnosis is recognised"],"redFlags":["Systemic features or raised inflammatory markers suggesting infection","Aggressive imaging features or a soft-tissue mass suggesting tumour","Progressive rather than resolving changes","Severe or worsening pain"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and resolves as the synchondrosis fuses, with an excellent prognosis once more serious mimics are excluded.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the normal variant for osteomyelitis or Ewing sarcoma and performing biopsy","Over-investigating an asymptomatic incidental finding","Failing to compare with the contralateral side","Not correlating imaging with the benign clinical picture"],"management":["Recognition as a benign, self-limiting developmental variant to avoid unnecessary intervention","Reassurance and observation, as the condition resolves once the ischiopubic synchondrosis fuses","Activity modification with simple analgesia such as paracetamol or NSAIDs for pain and limp","Comparison radiographs of both sides with follow-up imaging to confirm the diagnosis","Inflammatory markers and MRI reserved for cases where osteomyelitis or tumour must be excluded","Avoidance of biopsy once the diagnosis is recognised"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric orthopaedic references (standard reference)","Musculoskeletal imaging references (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"van_wyk_grumbach_syndrome":{"name":"Van Wyk-Grumbach Syndrome","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Hypothyroidism-induced precocious puberty","Van Wyk and Grumbach syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric endocrinology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A triad of long-standing untreated primary hypothyroidism, incomplete isosexual precocious puberty and delayed bone age.","Almost uniquely among causes of precocious puberty, skeletal maturation is delayed rather than advanced — a key discriminating feature.","In girls there is thelarche and vaginal bleeding with enlarged multicystic ovaries; in boys, testicular enlargement occurs without significant virilisation.","Driven by markedly elevated thyroid-stimulating hormone acting on the follicle-stimulating hormone receptor, the so-called specificity spillover.","Growth is slowed or arrested despite pubertal signs — this paradox should point directly to hypothyroidism.","Hyperprolactinaemia is frequent, driven by elevated thyrotrophin-releasing hormone, and thyrotroph hyperplasia may enlarge the pituitary and mimic an adenoma.","Autoimmune (Hashimoto) hypothyroidism is the usual underlying cause.","Fully reversible with thyroid hormone replacement, so unnecessary ovarian or pituitary surgery should be avoided."],"pathophysiology":"Long-standing, severe, untreated primary hypothyroidism removes negative feedback on the hypothalamic–pituitary axis, generating markedly elevated thyrotrophin-releasing hormone and thyroid-stimulating hormone. Because thyroid-stimulating hormone shares a common alpha subunit and structural homology with the gonadotrophins, very high concentrations weakly cross-activate the follicle-stimulating hormone receptor — the specificity spillover phenomenon — stimulating ovarian follicular development with multicystic ovaries, oestradiol secretion, thelarche and vaginal bleeding in girls, and Sertoli-cell-driven testicular enlargement without significant androgen-mediated virilisation in boys. Elevated thyrotrophin-releasing hormone concurrently drives prolactin secretion, causing hyperprolactinaemia, and thyrotroph hyperplasia may enlarge the pituitary and mimic an adenoma. Crucially, the accompanying hypothyroid suppression of the growth hormone–IGF-1 axis retards skeletal maturation, so bone age is delayed rather than advanced — the opposite of most other causes of precocious puberty — and every feature regresses with thyroid hormone replacement.","additionalDifferentials":["Central, gonadotrophin-dependent precocious puberty","McCune-Albright syndrome","Peripheral precocious puberty from an ovarian or adrenal tumour","Simple functional ovarian cyst","Exogenous oestrogen exposure","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (where virilisation is present)","Pituitary macroadenoma as a cause of hyperprolactinaemia and pituitary enlargement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thyroid function tests showing markedly raised thyroid-stimulating hormone with low free thyroxine","Thyroid peroxidase and thyroglobulin autoantibodies","Bone age radiograph, characteristically delayed","Pelvic ultrasound to assess multicystic ovaries and uterine maturation","Serum prolactin","Gonadotrophins with oestradiol or testosterone, and a gonadotrophin-releasing hormone stimulation test where central puberty is considered","Pituitary MRI when a mass is suspected, to avoid mistaking thyrotroph hyperplasia for an adenoma"],"redFlags":["Vaginal bleeding in a young child, which always warrants prompt evaluation","Rapidly enlarging ovarian cysts with a risk of torsion","Pituitary enlargement accompanied by headache or visual disturbance","Features of profound hypothyroidism such as myxoedema or a pericardial effusion"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent because the syndrome is reversible with thyroid hormone replacement, and the pubertal features and ovarian cysts regress. Catch-up may be incomplete and final height can be compromised when hypothyroidism has been prolonged.","pitfalls":["Misreading pituitary thyrotroph hyperplasia as an adenoma and undertaking unnecessary pituitary surgery.","Operating on multicystic ovaries mistaken for a neoplasm when they would regress with thyroid hormone.","Expecting advanced bone age as in other forms of precocity and being misled by the delayed skeletal maturation.","Overlooking the underlying hypothyroidism because the pubertal signs dominate the presentation."],"management":["Thyroid hormone replacement with levothyroxine as the definitive, curative treatment, reversing the pubertal and ovarian changes","Gradual initiation of levothyroxine with monitoring, given the risks associated with rapid correction of long-standing hypothyroidism","Avoidance of unnecessary ovarian cyst surgery, since the multicystic ovaries are expected to regress on treatment","Monitoring of thyroid function, growth, bone age and pubertal progress during replacement","Reassurance that hyperprolactinaemia and thyrotroph or pituitary enlargement resolve with adequate replacement, avoiding unnecessary pituitary surgery","Paediatric endocrinology follow-up with attention to catch-up growth and final height","Investigation and management of the underlying cause of hypothyroidism such as autoimmune thyroiditis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard paediatric endocrinology textbooks","Clinical references on thyroid disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"vanishing_white_matter_adult":{"name":"Adult-onset vanishing white matter disease (EIF2B)","system":"Nervous system / Neurogenetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VWM","EIF2B-related leukoencephalopathy","leukoencephalopathy with vanishing white matter","childhood ataxia with central hypomyelination (CACH)","ovarioleukodystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive leukoencephalopathy caused by variants in any of the five subunits of eukaryotic translation initiation factor 2B (EIF2B1 to EIF2B5)","The defining clinical feature is episodic, stepwise deterioration provoked by stressors such as febrile illness, minor head trauma or acute fright, sometimes with disproportionate decline or coma followed by partial recovery","The adult-onset form is generally milder and more slowly progressive than the infantile disease, with spasticity, cerebellar ataxia and cognitive or behavioural change predominating","Affected women may have primary or secondary ovarian failure, giving the ovarioleukodystrophy phenotype, which is an important clue in females","The MRI hallmark is diffuse white matter rarefaction and cystic degeneration, so that over time the abnormal white matter takes on a signal close to cerebrospinal fluid on all sequences, sometimes with a radiating stripe pattern within it","Diagnosis rests on the characteristic imaging together with the stress-sensitive clinical course, confirmed by EIF2B gene testing","There is no specific therapy; management is supportive and includes avoiding and promptly treating triggers such as fever and head injury"],"pathophysiology":"Vanishing white matter disease is caused by biallelic variants in genes encoding subunits of eukaryotic translation initiation factor 2B (eIF2B), a guanine nucleotide exchange factor central to the initiation of protein synthesis and to the integrated stress response. Impaired eIF2B activity dysregulates the cellular response to stress, rendering glial cells, particularly astrocytes and oligodendrocytes, abnormally vulnerable. Physiological stressors such as fever, trauma or fright overwhelm this defective stress response, precipitating white matter injury. Progressive loss and cystic breakdown of the white matter produce the characteristic rarefaction in which affected tissue comes to resemble cerebrospinal fluid on imaging.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple sclerosis and other inflammatory demyelination","Other adult-onset genetic leukoencephalopathies (including LBSL and adult Alexander disease)","Mitochondrial leukoencephalopathy","Toxic or post-infectious leukoencephalopathy","CADASIL and other hereditary small-vessel diseases","Premature ovarian insufficiency with an unrelated incidental white matter change"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to demonstrate diffuse white matter rarefaction with cystic change and a CSF-like signal, and any radiating stripe pattern","EIF2B1 to EIF2B5 sequencing or gene panel to confirm the diagnosis","Endocrine assessment of ovarian function in affected women","Neuropsychological evaluation to characterise cognitive involvement","Exclusion of inflammatory and infective causes (CSF, oligoclonal bands) where multiple sclerosis is considered","Serial imaging to document the evolving white matter rarefaction"],"redFlags":["Rapid deterioration or coma following fever, head trauma or acute fright","New encephalopathy in a patient with known disease requiring assessment for a treatable trigger","Progressive cognitive decline with loss of independence","Dysphagia and aspiration risk as the disease advances"],"prognosis":"Adult-onset vanishing white matter disease is usually slower and milder than childhood forms, but remains progressive and is punctuated by stress-related episodes of decline that may not fully reverse; the overall course varies widely with genotype and age at onset. Careful avoidance of triggers can reduce episodic deterioration, though cumulative disability tends to accrue over time.","pitfalls":["Not recognising the stress-provoked, stepwise course as a specific clue to the diagnosis","Misinterpreting the CSF-like white matter as atrophy or old ischaemia rather than rarefaction","Overlooking ovarian failure in women, which points towards the diagnosis","Mislabelling the disorder as multiple sclerosis and giving inappropriate immunotherapy","Failing to advise patients and families about avoiding and promptly managing fever and head injury"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy; management is supportive with rigorous avoidance of decompensation triggers","Aggressive prevention and early treatment of fever and infection with antipyretics and prompt antimicrobials, and avoidance of head trauma","Prophylactic planning around anaesthesia, illness and vaccination to minimise stress-provoked decline","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and mobility aids for spasticity and cerebellar ataxia, with antispasticity agents such as baclofen as needed","Endocrine assessment and hormone replacement for ovarian failure in the ovarioleukodystrophy phenotype","Neuropsychological support and management of cognitive and behavioural change","Serial MRI and multidisciplinary neurogenetics follow-up","Autosomal recessive genetic counselling with EIF2B testing and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"vanishing_white_matter_disease":{"name":"Vanishing white matter disease","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VWM","childhood ataxia with central hypomyelination","CACH","leukoencephalopathy with vanishing white matter","EIF2B1","EIF2B2","EIF2B3","EIF2B4","EIF2B5"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurology textbooks and peer-reviewed leukodystrophy reviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive leukodystrophy caused by biallelic variants in any of the five genes encoding subunits of eukaryotic translation initiation factor 2B (EIF2B1-5)","Hallmark clinical feature is episodic, stepwise neurological deterioration provoked by febrile illness, minor head trauma, or acute fright, often with marked lethargy or coma out of proportion to the trigger","Cerebellar ataxia and spasticity dominate the motor picture, whereas cognitive function is relatively spared until late","Classic childhood-onset form is most common, but the phenotype spans an antenatal/infantile severe end through to adult-onset and an ovarioleukodystrophy variant with premature ovarian failure in females","MRI shows diffuse, symmetrical white-matter abnormality that progressively rarefies and takes on cerebrospinal-fluid-like signal on all sequences, with a radiating stripe pattern of relatively preserved tissue","FLAIR sequences are especially useful, showing the affected white matter becoming suppressed (dark) as it liquefies, distinguishing it from simple demyelination","Confirmation is by molecular genetic testing of the EIF2B genes; there is no curative treatment, and management centres on avoiding known triggers","Families should be counselled to seek early care and antipyresis during infections and to minimise head injury, as these precipitate crises"],"pathophysiology":"Vanishing white matter disease results from loss-of-function variants in the genes encoding the five subunits of eukaryotic translation initiation factor 2B (eIF2B), a guanine-nucleotide exchange factor central to the regulation of protein synthesis, particularly under cellular stress. Impaired eIF2B activity deranges the integrated stress response, leaving glial cells (especially astrocytes and oligodendrocytes) unable to adapt to metabolic stresses such as fever, inflammation, or trauma. The result is progressive cerebral white-matter breakdown with abnormal, immature astrocytes and dysmyelination, so that the white matter gradually rarefies and cavitates into a fluid-like state while grey matter is comparatively preserved.","additionalDifferentials":["Metachromatic leukodystrophy and other lysosomal leukodystrophies","Megalencephalic leukoencephalopathy with subcortical cysts","Mitochondrial leukoencephalopathies","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis or post-infectious demyelination","Alexander disease","Cerebral folate deficiency and other treatable metabolic leukoencephalopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI with FLAIR and T2 sequences to demonstrate diffuse white-matter rarefaction with progressive CSF-like signal","Molecular genetic testing of EIF2B1-5 to confirm the diagnosis","Metabolic screen to exclude other leukodystrophies (arylsulfatase A, very-long-chain fatty acids, lactate)","Serial neuroimaging to document progression and cavitation of white matter","Endocrine assessment of ovarian function in affected females (ovarioleukodystrophy variant)"],"redFlags":["Rapid decline into stupor or coma following a febrile illness or minor head injury","Acute loss of previously acquired motor milestones after a trigger event","Status epilepticus or intractable seizures during an episode of deterioration","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or brainstem dysfunction during a crisis"],"prognosis":"The disorder is progressive and currently incurable; the classic childhood form typically causes stepwise decline with death within a few years of onset, whereas later-onset forms progress more slowly. Prognosis is strongly influenced by age at onset, with the earliest-onset presentations carrying the worst outlook.","pitfalls":["Attributing a post-febrile collapse to encephalitis or sepsis alone and missing the underlying leukodystrophy","Misreading early MRI as ordinary demyelination and failing to recognise the FLAIR suppression that signals white-matter liquefaction","Overlooking premature ovarian failure as a clue in adolescent or adult females","Failing to counsel families about trigger avoidance, prompt antipyresis, and head-injury prevention"],"management":["No curative therapy; care is supportive with strict avoidance of the stress triggers that provoke deterioration","Prompt, aggressive management of febrile illness with antipyretics and early antibiotics, together with head-injury precautions","Perioperative and vaccination planning to reduce stress-related decompensation","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy and antispasticity treatment such as baclofen for ataxia and spasticity, with mobility and communication aids","Anticonvulsants for seizures and management of feeding difficulties, including gastrostomy where needed","Endocrine review and hormone replacement for premature ovarian failure in affected females","Multidisciplinary neurodisability support with palliative care in advanced disease","Autosomal recessive genetic counselling, EIF2B testing and cascade family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","GeneReviews: Childhood Ataxia with Central Nervous System Hypomyelination / Vanishing White Matter"],"crossLinks":[]},"varicella_zoster":{"name":"Varicella-Zoster Virus Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chickenpox","shingles","VZV","herpes zoster"],"chapter":198,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1504-1508","clinicalPearls":["VZV causes two distinct clinical syndromes: primary infection (varicella/chickenpox), usually a benign childhood exanthematous vesicular illness, and reactivation of latent virus (herpes zoster/shingles), a dermatomal vesicular rash usually associated with severe pain (p.1504).","In chickenpox, skin lesions appear in successive crops over a 2-4 day period and are present in various stages of evolution simultaneously (maculopapules, vesicles, and scabs) on a given body region - the classic small vesicle on an erythematous base ('dewdrop on a rose petal') (p.1505).","Chickenpox is highly contagious (attack rate >=90% in seronegatives); patients are infectious ~48 h before the rash appears, throughout vesicle formation, and until all vesicles are crusted. Incubation is usually 14-17 days (range 10-21) (p.1505).","Herpes zoster results from reactivation of latent VZV in dorsal root ganglia; most patients have no recent exposure to others with VZV. Incidence is highest in the sixth decade of life and beyond; recurrent zoster is exceedingly rare except in immunocompromised hosts, especially those with AIDS (p.1505).","Zoster pain (dermatomal) may precede visible lesions by 48-72 h; in the prodromal stage before the rash appears, the diagnosis can be exceedingly difficult (p.1505-1506).","Dermatomes T3 to L3 are most frequently involved in herpes zoster (p.1505).","PCR detection of VZV DNA in vesicular fluid is the diagnostic method of choice; specimens for PCR include lesions, blood, and saliva. The Tzanck smear gives a rapid impression but has low sensitivity (~60%) (p.1507).","Patients with herpes zoster can transmit infection to seronegative individuals, resulting in chickenpox (not zoster) in the contact (p.1506)."],"pathophysiology":"VZV is a member of the Herpesviridae with double-stranded DNA (~125,000 bp), a lipid envelope, and an icosahedral nucleocapsid (~180-200 nm). Primary transmission is by the respiratory route, with localized replication (presumably nasopharynx) seeding the lymphatic/reticuloendothelial system and producing viremia, which manifests as diffuse, scattered skin lesions. Vesicles involve the corium/dermis with ballooning degeneration, multinucleated giant cells, and eosinophilic intranuclear inclusions; localized skin blood-vessel involvement can cause necrosis and epidermal hemorrhage. During chickenpox the virus infects dorsal root ganglia and remains latent (in sensory dorsal, cranial, and enteric ganglia) until an unknown trigger causes reactivation, producing herpes zoster; active zoster ganglia show hemorrhage, edema, and lymphocytic infiltration. Pulmonary involvement shows interstitial pneumonitis with giant cells, intranuclear inclusions, and hemorrhage; CNS infection produces perivascular cuffing similar to other viral encephalitides, while focal hemorrhagic necrosis (characteristic of HSV encephalitis) develops infrequently in VZV infection (p.1504).","additionalDifferentials":["For chickenpox: disseminated HSV infection in patients with atopic dermatitis; disseminated vesiculopapular lesions of coxsackievirus or echovirus infection; atypical measles (these rashes are more often morbilliform/hemorrhagic than vesicular) (p.1506)","Rickettsialpox - distinguished by the 'herald spot' (eschar) at the mite-bite site and a more pronounced headache; serology helps differentiate it from varicella (p.1506)","Mpox - consider in travelers from endemic areas and in HIV-infected individuals (p.1506)","Smallpox - bioterrorism concern; smallpox lesions are larger than chickenpox lesions and all at the same stage of evolution at any given time (p.1506)","For herpes zoster: both HSV and coxsackievirus infections can cause dermatomal vesicular lesions (p.1506)"],"infectionMimics":["Disseminated HSV infection (notably in atopic dermatitis) (p.1506)","Coxsackievirus infection (p.1506)","Echovirus infection (p.1506)","Atypical measles (p.1506)","Rickettsialpox (p.1506)","Mpox (p.1506)","Smallpox (p.1506)","HSV and coxsackievirus causing dermatomal vesicular lesions (mimicking zoster) (p.1506)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PCR for VZV DNA in vesicular fluid - diagnostic method of choice; also performed on lesions, blood, and saliva (p.1507)","Tzanck smear (scraping of lesion base for multinucleated giant cells) - rapid but low sensitivity (~60%) (p.1507)","Viral isolation in susceptible tissue-culture cell lines (unequivocal confirmation) (p.1507)","Direct immunofluorescent staining of cells from the lesion base or immunoperoxidase detection of viral antigens (p.1507)","Serology: seroconversion or fourfold rise in antibody titer between acute- and convalescent-phase sera; FAMA (fluorescent antibody to membrane antigen) test and ELISA are the most sensitive serologic tools (p.1507)"],"redFlags":["Zoster ophthalmicus - involvement of the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve; a debilitating condition that can result in blindness in the absence of antiviral therapy. Lesions on the tip of the nose (Hutchinson sign) signal likely ocular involvement; refer immediately to an ophthalmologist (p.1505-1507)","Ramsay Hunt syndrome - geniculate ganglion (sensory branch of facial nerve) involvement causing pain and vesicles in the external auditory canal, loss of taste in the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and ipsilateral facial palsy (p.1506)","Disseminated/progressive herpes zoster in immunocompromised hosts - patients with Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma are at greatest risk; cutaneous dissemination occurs in ~40% untreated, raising the risk of pneumonitis, meningoencephalitis, and hepatitis by 5-10% (p.1506)","Varicella pneumonia - the most serious complication of chickenpox; more common in adults (up to 20%) than children and particularly severe in pregnant women; typically requires ventilatory support (p.1505, 1507)","Visceral dissemination in immunocompromised patients - occurs in 30-50% of cases and is fatal 15% of the time without antiviral therapy (p.1505)","Perinatal varicella - mortality as high as 30% when maternal disease begins within 5 days before to 48 h after delivery (p.1505)","CNS involvement: encephalitis (0.1-0.2% of children with chickenpox), aseptic meningitis, transverse myelitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome; in zoster, symptomatic meningoencephalitis and rare granulomatous angiitis with contralateral hemiplegia (p.1505-1506)"],"prognosis":"In the immunocompetent host chickenpox is usually benign and self-limited (total duration ~7-10 days; skin may take 2-4 weeks to fully normalize). Childhood acute cerebellar ataxia is generally benign and does not require hospitalization. Postherpetic neuralgia is the most debilitating complication of zoster: uncommon in the young but affects at least 50% of patients over age 50, who report dermatomal pain for months after cutaneous healing. Even in immunocompromised patients, disseminated zoster is rarely fatal. Hematopoietic stem cell transplant recipients are at particularly high risk, with a mortality rate of ~10% for posttransplantation VZV infection. The Shingrix subunit vaccine showed 97.2% efficacy in adults >=50 years, although vaccine immunity wanes over time (p.1505-1507).","pitfalls":["Aspirin should never be given to children with chickenpox because of its association with Reye syndrome; aspirin is no longer used for this reason (p.1505, 1507)","Zoster sine herpetica: dermatomal pain with serologic evidence of zoster can occur in the absence of any skin lesions - shingles without a rash has been reported, making diagnosis difficult (p.1506)","In the prodromal stage of herpes zoster (before lesions appear), diagnosis may be possible only after lesions emerge or by retrospective serology (p.1506)","Glucocorticoids for zoster should not be used without concomitant antiviral therapy, and steroid use is appropriate only for relatively healthy elderly patients with moderate/severe pain; patients with osteoporosis, diabetes mellitus, glycosuria, or hypertension may not be appropriate candidates (p.1507)","VZIG is NOT indicated for a newborn if the mother has zoster (as opposed to varicella); it is indicated when maternal varicella onsets within 5 days before to 48 h after delivery (p.1507-1508)"],"management":["Antiviral therapy with aciclovir, valaciclovir or famciclovir, most beneficial when started early, for herpes zoster and for varicella in adolescents, adults and the immunocompromised or complicated cases","Intravenous aciclovir for severe or disseminated disease and immunocompromised hosts","Symptomatic care of varicella with antipyretics such as paracetamol, avoiding aspirin because of Reye syndrome, plus antipruritics and skin hygiene to prevent secondary bacterial infection","Analgesia for zoster and multimodal management of postherpetic neuralgia with agents such as tricyclic antidepressants, gabapentinoids and topical lidocaine or capsaicin","Urgent ophthalmology referral for herpes zoster ophthalmicus and specialist input for other complications","Infection control with respiratory and contact isolation until lesions crust, and post-exposure prophylaxis with varicella-zoster immunoglobulin or vaccine for susceptible high-risk contacts","Prevention through varicella vaccination and recombinant zoster vaccination in eligible groups"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Whitley RJ. Varicella-Zoster Virus Infections. In: Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd ed, Chapter 198, pp. 1504-1508.","Arvin A. Aging, immunity, and the varicella-zoster virus. N Engl J Med 352:2266, 2005.","Cohen JI. A new vaccine to prevent herpes zoster. N Engl J Med 372:2149, 2015.","Gershon AA et al. Varicella zoster virus infection. Nat Rev Dis Primers 1:15016, 2015.","Gnann JW, Whitley RJ. Herpes zoster. N Engl J Med 347:340, 2002.","Lai H et al. Efficacy of an adjuvanted herpes zoster subunit vaccine in older adults. N Engl J Med 372:2087, 2015."],"crossLinks":[]},"varicocele":{"name":"Varicocele","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["varicocoele","scrotal varicocele","pampiniform plexus varicosity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A varicocele is an abnormal dilatation of the pampiniform venous plexus, classically described as feeling like a bag of worms on scrotal palpation","The great majority occur on the left side, attributed to the left testicular vein draining at a right angle into the left renal vein and having a longer course","It is more prominent on standing and with Valsalva and typically decompresses on lying down; a varicocele that does not empty when supine should raise concern for a retroperitoneal or renal mass","A sudden new right-sided or non-reducing varicocele in an older patient warrants imaging to exclude a renal tumour obstructing venous drainage","Clinical grading ranges from subclinical (detectable only on imaging) to visible through the scrotal skin, with intermediate grades palpable with or without Valsalva","Varicoceles are a common and potentially reversible factor in male subfertility, associated with impaired sperm count, motility, and morphology, and sometimes with testicular growth arrest in adolescents","Most are asymptomatic or cause a dull dragging ache; treatment (surgical varicocelectomy or radiological embolisation) is considered for pain, infertility with abnormal semen analysis, or testicular growth failure in adolescents","Semen analysis and assessment of testicular volume guide management decisions in the fertility and adolescent contexts"],"pathophysiology":"A varicocele results from incompetent or absent valves in the internal spermatic (testicular) veins, leading to retrograde flow and dilatation of the pampiniform plexus. The predominance on the left relates to the anatomy of left testicular venous drainage into the left renal vein at a right angle with a longer, higher-pressure course. The resulting venous stasis is thought to impair testicular function through elevated scrotal temperature, venous hypertension, hypoxia, and reflux of adrenal or renal metabolites, contributing to defective spermatogenesis and, in some cases, testicular atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Spermatocele or epididymal cyst","Hydrocele","Inguinal hernia (indirect)","Testicular tumour","Epididymo-orchitis","Retroperitoneal or renal mass obstructing venous drainage (secondary varicocele)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal examination in both standing and supine positions with Valsalva","Scrotal colour Doppler ultrasound to confirm venous reflux and grade the varicocele","Measurement of testicular volume to detect growth arrest, especially in adolescents","Semen analysis in men presenting with subfertility","Abdominal/renal imaging when a right-sided, isolated, or non-reducing varicocele suggests a retroperitoneal cause"],"redFlags":["New-onset right-sided or non-decompressing varicocele suggesting a renal or retroperitoneal tumour","Progressive ipsilateral testicular atrophy or growth arrest in an adolescent","Acute painful scrotal swelling raising alternative diagnoses that require exclusion","Significant deterioration in semen parameters in a man seeking fertility"],"prognosis":"Many varicoceles are asymptomatic and require no intervention; when treated for pain, subfertility, or adolescent growth arrest, repair often improves semen parameters and testicular growth, though not all men achieve improved fertility.","pitfalls":["Failing to examine the patient standing and with Valsalva, missing a lower-grade varicocele","Overlooking a secondary varicocele from a renal tumour in an isolated right-sided or non-reducible case","Recommending repair for asymptomatic subclinical varicoceles without a clear indication","Neglecting testicular volume assessment in adolescents","Attributing subfertility solely to the varicocele without full evaluation of both partners"],"management":["Reassure and observe asymptomatic subclinical or low-grade varicoceles that are not affecting fertility or testicular growth, without intervention","Offer varicocele repair by microsurgical subinguinal ligation or radiological percutaneous venous embolisation for chronic scrotal pain, subfertility with abnormal semen parameters, or adolescent testicular growth arrest","Provide symptomatic relief with scrotal support and simple analgesia for aching discomfort","Monitor with serial semen analysis in subfertile men and serial testicular volume or ultrasound in adolescents to detect catch-up growth or ongoing arrest","Image the retroperitoneum and kidneys for an isolated right-sided or non-reducing varicocele to exclude an obstructing renal or retroperitoneal tumour","Refer to urology and, where subfertility persists, to reproductive medicine for assisted conception","Review after repair for recurrence and for postoperative hydrocele"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"varicose_veins":{"name":"Varicose veins","system":"Vascular / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lower limb varicosities","varicosities","superficial venous insufficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Present as dilated, tortuous, superficial leg veins, often with aching, heaviness, itching, or swelling that worsens with prolonged standing and eases on elevation","Most arise from primary valvular incompetence in the great or small saphenous systems, allowing reflux and venous hypertension","Assessment includes standing inspection and duplex ultrasound to map the sites of reflux and incompetent junctions, which has largely replaced the older tourniquet tests","Chronic venous hypertension produces skin changes: haemosiderin pigmentation, venous eczema, lipodermatosclerosis, atrophie blanche, and ultimately venous ulceration around the gaiter area","Conservative measures include graduated compression stockings, weight loss, exercise, and leg elevation","Interventional options include endovenous thermal ablation (laser or radiofrequency), foam sclerotherapy, and conventional surgery (ligation and stripping)","Secondary varicose veins may follow deep vein thrombosis or pelvic outflow obstruction, so a history of DVT and signs of deep venous disease should be sought","Sudden bleeding from a ruptured varicosity is controlled by elevation and direct compression, not by a tourniquet"],"pathophysiology":"Varicose veins develop when incompetent valves in the superficial venous system, or at the saphenofemoral or saphenopopliteal junctions, permit reflux of blood back down the leg. The resulting venous hypertension dilates and elongates the superficial veins, making them tortuous. Sustained ambulatory venous hypertension is transmitted to the skin microcirculation, causing capillary leakage, fibrin deposition, inflammation, and progressive skin changes that culminate in venous ulceration. Contributing factors include heredity, prolonged standing, pregnancy, obesity, and previous deep vein thrombosis, the last causing secondary varicosities through deep venous obstruction or valvular damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Deep venous insufficiency or post-thrombotic syndrome","Chronic lymphoedema","Klippel-Trenaunay and other congenital venous malformations","Superficial thrombophlebitis","Arterial disease contributing to leg ulceration","Cellulitis of the lower leg"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound to map superficial and deep reflux and identify incompetent junctions and perforators","Ankle-brachial pressure index before compression therapy to exclude significant arterial disease","Assessment for prior deep vein thrombosis and deep venous patency","Photographs or clinical grading (e.g. CEAP classification) to document severity"],"redFlags":["Torrential haemorrhage from a ruptured surface varicosity requiring elevation and direct pressure","Non-healing venous ulcer, especially with atypical features suggesting malignant change (Marjolin's ulcer)","Signs of deep vein thrombosis or coexisting significant arterial disease before compression","Rapidly progressive skin changes or superficial thrombophlebitis extending toward the saphenofemoral junction"],"prognosis":"Symptoms and skin changes usually improve with compression and, where appropriate, ablation or surgery, and venous ulcers can heal with effective compression, but recurrence of varicosities and ulcers is common, particularly with deep venous disease or poor compliance.","pitfalls":["Applying compression without first excluding significant arterial disease","Overlooking underlying deep venous disease or previous DVT causing secondary varicosities","Attempting to control variceal bleeding with a tourniquet rather than elevation and compression","Missing malignant change in a chronic non-healing venous ulcer"],"management":["Offer conservative management first with graduated compression stockings, leg elevation, regular exercise and weight reduction, particularly where intervention is declined or contraindicated","Confirm adequate arterial supply with the ankle-brachial pressure index before applying compression","Treat truncal saphenous reflux with endovenous thermal ablation by radiofrequency or laser as first-line, reserving ultrasound-guided foam sclerotherapy or open ligation and stripping as alternatives","Map the sites of superficial and deep reflux with duplex ultrasound to target intervention","Manage skin sequelae of venous hypertension, treating venous eczema with emollients and topical corticosteroids and gaiter-area ulceration with multilayer compression","Refer to vascular services for symptomatic varicosities, progressive skin changes, bleeding varicosities or superficial thrombophlebitis","Maintain compression and surveillance long term to reduce recurrence, especially where deep venous disease coexists"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"variegate_porphyria":{"name":"Variegate porphyria","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VP","protoporphyrinogen oxidase deficiency","PPOX deficiency","porphyria variegata","South African genetic porphyria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant deficiency of protoporphyrinogen oxidase, the penultimate enzyme of haem biosynthesis, notably prevalent in the Afrikaner population owing to a founder mutation","A mixed porphyria that can cause both acute neurovisceral attacks (as in acute intermittent porphyria) and chronic photocutaneous skin fragility","Skin lesions comprise blistering, erosions, milia and fragility on sun-exposed areas, clinically indistinguishable from porphyria cutanea tarda","During acute attacks urinary ALA and PBG rise, but the discriminating feature is a characteristic plasma fluorescence emission peak around the high 620s nanometre range","Faecal porphyrin analysis typically shows raised protoporphyrin and coproporphyrin, helping distinguish it from other acute porphyrias","Acute attacks share the same drug, fasting and hormonal triggers as acute intermittent porphyria and are managed similarly with trigger removal, glucose loading and intravenous haem","Cutaneous disease is managed largely by sun protection and avoidance of skin trauma rather than by the measures effective in porphyria cutanea tarda"],"pathophysiology":"Variegate porphyria arises from reduced activity of protoporphyrinogen oxidase, the seventh enzyme of the haem pathway, which oxidises protoporphyrinogen to protoporphyrin. Deficiency causes accumulation of protoporphyrinogen and back-up of upstream intermediates. The photoreactive porphyrins deposited in the skin generate reactive oxygen species on light exposure, causing the blistering photodermatosis, while induction of hepatic ALA synthase during metabolic stress raises the neurotoxic precursors ALA and PBG that drive acute neurovisceral attacks. The dual accumulation explains the mixed acute and cutaneous phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Porphyria cutanea tarda (identical skin picture but no acute attacks)","Hereditary coproporphyria (the other mixed acute porphyria)","Acute intermittent porphyria during a neurovisceral attack","Pseudoporphyria from drugs or renal dialysis","Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita and other subepidermal blistering disorders","Surgical acute abdomen during an attack"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma fluorescence scanning showing the characteristic emission peak diagnostic of variegate porphyria","Faecal porphyrin profile (protoporphyrin and coproporphyrin)","Urinary ALA and PBG during symptomatic attacks","Molecular genetic testing of the PPOX gene for confirmation and family screening","Skin biopsy in atypical cases to confirm subepidermal bullae and exclude mimics","Serum sodium and renal function during acute attacks"],"redFlags":["Progressive motor neuropathy or respiratory compromise during an acute attack","Seizures with hyponatraemia during an attack","Severe photocutaneous ulceration with secondary infection","Exposure to a known porphyrinogenic drug in a symptomatic patient"],"prognosis":"Cutaneous manifestations tend to persist while acute attacks are usually preventable with trigger avoidance; most patients maintain a good quality of life, though severe attacks carry the same neurological risks as other acute porphyrias.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the skin disease for porphyria cutanea tarda and applying inappropriate therapy","Prescribing porphyrinogenic drugs that precipitate acute attacks","Relying on urinary studies alone and missing the diagnosis between attacks when they may be near normal","Failing to protect skin from sunlight and trauma","Not screening at-risk relatives, particularly in founder populations"],"management":["Prevent acute attacks by avoiding porphyrinogenic drugs, alcohol and fasting, and by promptly treating intercurrent infection and other metabolic stressors","Treat acute neurovisceral attacks with intravenous haem arginate and carbohydrate glucose loading, alongside porphyria-safe analgesia, antiemetics and management of autonomic features","Correct attack-related hyponatraemia and treat seizures with porphyria-safe anticonvulsants","Manage the photocutaneous disease with sun avoidance, protective clothing and barrier photoprotection, with careful skin care to limit fragility, blistering and erosions","Provide the patient with a list of safe and unsafe drugs and clear guidance to seek urgent care during attacks","Undertake long-term surveillance for hepatocellular carcinoma and chronic kidney disease","Manage through a specialist porphyria centre and offer genetic counselling with family screening for the PPOX variant"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"vasa_praevia":{"name":"Vasa praevia","system":"Reproductive / Obstetrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vasa previa","unprotected fetal vessels over the cervical os"],"chapter":null,"source":"RCOG Green-top Guideline / Williams Obstetrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Vasa praevia describes fetal blood vessels coursing through the membranes across or in close proximity to the internal cervical os, unsupported by placental tissue or the umbilical cord","Two principal types are recognised: type 1, associated with a velamentous cord insertion, and type 2, in which vessels run between the lobes of a bilobed or succenturiate-lobed placenta","The danger is rupture of these unprotected vessels when the membranes rupture, causing rapid fetal exsanguination; because the blood lost is fetal, even a modest volume is life-threatening to the baby","The classic intrapartum presentation is painless vaginal bleeding at the time of membrane rupture accompanied by acute fetal heart rate abnormalities such as a sinusoidal trace or bradycardia","Antenatal diagnosis by transvaginal ultrasound with colour and pulsed-wave Doppler, demonstrating fetal-origin vessels over the os, transforms the prognosis by allowing planned delivery before labour","Risk factors include velamentous cord insertion, a bilobed or succenturiate-lobed placenta, a low-lying placenta or resolved praevia, multiple pregnancy, and conception by assisted reproduction","Management of confirmed vasa praevia is planned hospital admission in the third trimester, corticosteroids for lung maturity, and elective caesarean section before the onset of labour and before the membranes rupture","When vasa praevia is unrecognised and a vessel ruptures, the priority is immediate delivery and neonatal resuscitation with volume replacement, as the newborn may require urgent transfusion"],"pathophysiology":"Normally the umbilical vessels insert centrally into the placental mass and are cushioned by Wharton's jelly, while the fetal chorionic vessels are protected by surrounding placenta. In vasa praevia this protection is lost: either the cord inserts into the membranes (velamentous insertion) with vessels traversing the membranes before reaching the placenta, or vessels bridge between separate placental lobes. When these membranous vessels overlie the internal os they lie below the presenting part and across the path of delivery, without protective Wharton's jelly, and are vulnerable both to compression by the presenting part and, critically, to laceration when the overlying membranes rupture spontaneously or artificially. Rupture opens the low-volume fetal circulation directly, so brisk fetal haemorrhage and exsanguination can occur within minutes.","additionalDifferentials":["Placenta praevia","Placental abruption","Bloody show of labour","Marginal placental bleeding","Local maternal genital tract bleeding from a cervical or vaginal lesion","A maternal rather than fetal source of intrapartum bleeding"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound with colour Doppler to identify vessels crossing or lying near the internal os","Pulsed-wave Doppler to confirm a fetal heart rate waveform within the aberrant vessels","Assessment of cord insertion site and placental morphology, including a bilobed or succenturiate lobe, at the routine anomaly scan","Cardiotocography to detect fetal compromise such as a sinusoidal pattern or bradycardia","Bedside testing to distinguish fetal from maternal blood, such as an alkali-denaturation (Apt) or Kleihauer test, though delivery should not await results","Maternal full blood count and group-and-save or crossmatch"],"redFlags":["Vaginal bleeding coinciding with rupture of the membranes","Sinusoidal or profoundly abnormal fetal heart rate pattern","Acute fetal bradycardia after spontaneous or artificial rupture of the membranes","Known velamentous cord insertion or succenturiate lobe presenting undiagnosed in labour","Rapidly deteriorating fetal condition with only a modest volume of fresh vaginal blood"],"prognosis":"With antenatal diagnosis and elective caesarean before labour, perinatal survival is excellent. When the diagnosis is first made after vessel rupture, perinatal mortality is high owing to the speed of fetal exsanguination.","pitfalls":["Attributing intrapartum bleeding to a maternal cause and delaying delivery while fetal blood is being lost","Rupturing membranes without first palpating for pulsating vessels when vasa praevia is possible","Failing to identify a velamentous cord insertion or a succenturiate lobe on antenatal scanning","Not re-imaging a low-lying placenta or resolved praevia for associated vasa praevia","Under-preparing for immediate neonatal resuscitation and transfusion at delivery"],"management":["Plan an elective caesarean section before the onset of labour and before membrane rupture, timed to the late preterm period to pre-empt vessel rupture","Administer antenatal corticosteroids for foetal lung maturation ahead of the scheduled preterm delivery","Undertake third-trimester surveillance with serial ultrasound and consider inpatient admission for monitoring with immediate theatre access","Avoid artificial rupture of membranes and any attempt at vaginal delivery","Treat membrane rupture accompanied by foetal heart rate abnormality or bleeding as an obstetric emergency, proceeding to immediate caesarean with neonatal resuscitation and volume and blood replacement for the exsanguinated baby","Coordinate care across obstetrics, neonatology and anaesthesia, delivering in a unit with neonatal intensive care","Ensure emergency neonatal blood transfusion is available given the risk of rapid foetal haemorrhage"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["RCOG Green-top Guideline on vasa praevia","Williams Obstetrics","Oxford Handbook of Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vasa_previa":{"name":"Vasa praevia","system":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vasa previa","praevia of fetal vessels","unprotected fetal vessels over the cervix"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / RCOG guidance on vasa praevia","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Vasa praevia is the presence of unprotected foetal vessels running through the membranes across or close to the internal cervical os, unsupported by placenta or cord","Because the vessels are foetal, rupture of the membranes can cause rapid foetal exsanguination rather than maternal blood loss","It is classically associated with a velamentous cord insertion (type 1) or vessels connecting lobes of a bilobed or succenturiate-lobe placenta (type 2)","Additional risk factors include low-lying placenta or praevia in early pregnancy, multiple pregnancy, and assisted reproduction","Antenatal diagnosis with transvaginal ultrasound and colour Doppler allows planned management and transforms survival","The classic intrapartum presentation is painless vaginal bleeding at membrane rupture accompanied by acute foetal heart rate abnormalities","When diagnosed antenatally, management is planned admission and elective caesarean before labour or membrane rupture","Emergency caesarean is required if membranes rupture with foetal bleeding or an ominous foetal heart rate"],"pathophysiology":"In vasa praevia foetal umbilical vessels traverse the free membranes over or near the internal os without the protection of Wharton's jelly or placental tissue, typically because the cord inserts into the membranes (velamentous insertion) or bridges accessory placental lobes. These exposed vessels are vulnerable to compression by the presenting part and, critically, to laceration when the membranes rupture spontaneously or artificially. Because the circulating blood is foetal and the foetal blood volume is small, even modest bleeding can cause rapid foetal hypovolaemia, exsanguination, and death within minutes.","additionalDifferentials":["Placenta praevia","Placental abruption","Marginal or circumvallate placental bleeding","Cervical or vaginal bleeding from local lesions","Bloody show of labour","Uterine rupture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound with colour and pulsed Doppler to map vessels over the os","Assessment of cord insertion site and exclusion of accessory placental lobes","Follow-up imaging in the third trimester as a low-lying placenta resolves","Continuous foetal heart rate monitoring if bleeding occurs","Apt or other test to distinguish foetal from maternal blood when feasible and not delaying delivery","Confirmation of foetal vessels on placental examination after birth"],"redFlags":["Painless vaginal bleeding coinciding with membrane rupture","Sudden foetal heart rate decelerations, bradycardia, or a sinusoidal pattern after rupture","Antenatally diagnosed vasa praevia entering labour or with contractions","Confirmed foetal blood in the vaginal loss"],"prognosis":"With antenatal diagnosis, planned admission, and elective caesarean before labour, foetal survival is very high. Undiagnosed vasa praevia presenting with membrane rupture and foetal haemorrhage carries a high risk of rapid foetal death despite emergency intervention.","pitfalls":["Rupturing membranes before excluding vasa praevia in a woman with risk factors","Mistaking foetal bleeding for maternal antepartum haemorrhage and delaying delivery","Failing to re-image an early low-lying placenta or velamentous insertion later in pregnancy","Not planning delivery in a setting with immediate neonatal resuscitation and transfusion capability"],"management":["Deliver by planned elective caesarean before labour and before membrane rupture, timed to the late preterm period to forestall foetal exsanguination","Give antenatal corticosteroids for foetal lung maturity in advance of the scheduled preterm delivery","Monitor through the third trimester with serial transvaginal ultrasound and consider antenatal admission for observation and rapid access to theatre","Avoid artificial rupture of membranes and do not attempt vaginal birth","Manage membrane rupture with foetal heart rate abnormality or bleeding as an emergency, proceeding to immediate caesarean with neonatal resuscitation and volume and blood replacement","Plan delivery in a unit with neonatal intensive care and coordinate obstetric, neonatal and anaesthetic teams","Have emergency neonatal transfusion available because even modest foetal blood loss is life-threatening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","RCOG guidance on vasa praevia","International society consensus on vasa praevia diagnosis and management"],"crossLinks":[]},"vascular_dementia":{"name":"Vascular Dementia","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vascular cognitive impairment","multi-infarct dementia"],"chapter":444,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3489","clinicalPearls":["Traditionally defined as dementia due primarily to one or more symptomatic strokes; ranked the second most frequent cause of dementia after Alzheimer's disease, and especially common where vascular risk factors are undertreated (p.3489).","The concept has been broadened to vascular contributions to cognitive impairment and dementia (VCID), reflecting that cerebrovascular pathology is highly prevalent in the elderly and contributes to cognitive impairment, whether in isolation or—more commonly—combined with neurodegenerative processes (p.3489).","Clinical evaluation focuses on identifying vascular risk factors (hypertension, diabetes mellitus, dyslipidemia, tobacco use, atrial fibrillation, coronary artery disease, peripheral vascular disease), prior stroke/TIA, and family history of early stroke or vascular disease (p.3491).","Although stepwise progression and deficits such as loss of executive function are particularly suggestive, most individuals with VCID follow the more typical pattern of gradual progression of impaired episodic memory (p.3491).","Patients often show a mixture of executive and visuospatial deficits with prominent psychomotor slowing, and may present with psychiatric symptoms such as depression, anxiety, delusions, disinhibition, or apathy (p.196).","Gait disturbance is common in vascular dementia (as well as PD/DLB and NPH) (p.195).","Strategic single infarcts in locations such as thalamus, medial temporal cortex, anterior corpus callosum, or dominant-side angular gyrus can sufficiently impair episodic memory and function to meet memory-based dementia criteria (p.3489).","At least some cognitive impairment is present in approximately half of stroke survivors and increases with longer follow-up; even patients with good cognitive recovery show accelerated poststroke cognitive decline (p.3489).","One late-onset community autopsy cohort attributed 25% of dementia risk to cerebrovascular disease (vs 39% Alzheimer's, 17% LATE, 12% Lewy body), and most late-onset dementia involves multiple coexisting pathologies (p.195)."],"pathophysiology":"Vascular dementia results from the cumulative impact of cerebrovascular disease on age-related cognitive decline. Symptomatic strokes (ischemic or hemorrhagic) produce irreversible injury whose cognitive effect depends on size and location. Cerebral small-vessel disease—principally arteriolosclerosis (thickening of arterioles from plasma-protein infiltration; risk factors age, hypertension, diabetes; centered on deep penetrating territories such as thalamus, basal ganglia, brainstem) and cerebral amyloid angiopathy (beta-amyloid deposition in walls of small cortical arteries/arterioles/capillaries; main risk factor advancing age; favoring lobar cortex and subcortical white matter)—is often clinically asymptomatic and produces lacunar infarcts, microinfarcts, cerebral microbleeds, and white matter hyperintensities. These small distributed injuries disconnect nodes within distributed brain networks. Mixed pathology is the rule: cerebrovascular lesions frequently co-occur with neurodegenerative disease (most commonly Alzheimer's), producing greater impairment than either mechanism alone, possibly via loss of blood-brain barrier integrity and impaired perivascular clearance of beta-amyloid (p.3489-3491).","additionalDifferentials":["Alzheimer's disease (most common dementia; mixed AD-vascular pathology very common)","Dementia with Lewy bodies / PDD spectrum","Frontotemporal dementia","Limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy (LATE)","Normal pressure hydrocephalus","Delirium"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Alzheimer's disease (frequently coexists as mixed dementia—a key pitfall)","Normal pressure hydrocephalus (shares gait disturbance)","Delirium (prominent attention/executive deficits)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI is the mainstay for detection and subtyping of cerebrovascular disease; should include FLAIR, diffusion-weighted, and T2*-weighted sequences to detect large and small chronic infarcts, acute microinfarcts, microbleeds, and white matter hyperintensities (p.3491)","Diffusion tensor MRI (e.g., peak width of skeletonized mean diffusivity, PSMD) detects white matter disconnection (largely research use) (p.3490)","Vessel imaging (CT or MR angiography) is not required for initial cognitive evaluation but may help determine the cause of identified macroscopic infarcts (p.3491)","Screening for atrial fibrillation in individuals with asymptomatic infarcts; prolonged cardiac monitoring for embolic-appearing infarcts (p.3491)","Genetic testing for hereditary VCID (CADASIL, hereditary cerebral amyloid angiopathy) when young onset, positive family history, or suggestive imaging—otherwise unnecessary (p.3491)"],"redFlags":["Young onset, positive family history, or suggestive neuroimaging—consider hereditary forms such as CADASIL or hereditary cerebral amyloid angiopathy (p.3491)","Lobar intracerebral hemorrhage or convexity subarachnoid blood, suggesting cerebral amyloid angiopathy (p.3490)","Rapid progression with motor rigidity and myoclonus suggests CJD rather than vascular dementia (p.195)"],"prognosis":"Stroke survivors show accelerated poststroke cognitive decline even after good initial recovery; in one longitudinal study trajectory shifted from a prestroke cognitive gain to poststroke loss. Multiple strokes and larger infarcted volumes raise the likelihood of poststroke cognitive dysfunction. Epidemiologic data show declining age-adjusted dementia incidence paralleling improved hypertension control and stroke prevention, and the SPRINT-MIND trial showed intensive systolic blood pressure lowering reduced rates of mild cognitive impairment and combined MCI/probable dementia, underscoring VCID as a target for prevention (p.3489).","pitfalls":["Mixed dementia is the rule rather than the exception—cerebrovascular disease commonly co-occurs with Alzheimer's and other neurodegenerative pathologies, so attributing cognitive impairment to a single etiology is often incorrect (p.3489).","Most individuals with VCID do not show classic stepwise decline but instead a gradual progression of impaired episodic memory resembling Alzheimer's (p.3491).","White matter hyperintensities are an almost ubiquitous, nonspecific feature of aging (gliosis, demyelination, or increased water content) and should not be over-interpreted (p.3490).","Many chronic lacunar infarcts and microinfarcts cannot be readily identified on routine imaging, so absence of visible lesions does not exclude small-vessel disease (p.3490)."],"management":["Slow progression by aggressively controlling vascular risk factors with blood-pressure lowering, statin therapy for dyslipidaemia, glycaemic control in diabetes and smoking cessation","Institute secondary stroke prevention with antiplatelet therapy, or anticoagulation where atrial fibrillation is present","Reserve cholinesterase inhibitors for cases with coexisting Alzheimer's pathology, as they are not established for pure vascular cognitive impairment","Treat neuropsychiatric symptoms such as depression, anxiety and apathy with psychological support and antidepressants, minimising antipsychotic use","Provide multidisciplinary rehabilitation including physiotherapy for gait and falls, occupational therapy and cognitive support, with structured carer support","Screen for and manage atrial fibrillation and other embolic sources identified on cardiac monitoring","Undertake advance care planning as the disease progresses"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3489","Harrison 22e p.3490","Harrison 22e p.3491","Harrison 22e p.195","Harrison 22e p.196"],"crossLinks":["alzheimers_disease","ischemic_stroke"]},"vascular_ehlers_danlos_syndrome":{"name":"Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","system":"Connective tissue","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vEDS","EDS type IV","COL3A1-related EDS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Connective tissue disorder references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in COL3A1, disrupting type III collagen; autosomal dominant, with a substantial proportion arising de novo.","Arterial rupture or dissection of medium-sized vessels is the leading cause of premature death and may occur without preceding aneurysm.","Spontaneous hollow-organ perforation, classically of the sigmoid colon, and uterine rupture in pregnancy are characteristic catastrophic events.","Distinctive facial phenotype: thin translucent skin with visible subcutaneous veins, acrogeria, thin pinched nose, thin lips, prominent eyes and lobeless ears.","Easy extensive bruising is prominent, but skin hyperextensibility and joint hypermobility are usually mild, unlike classical and hypermobile subtypes.","Avoid invasive catheter arteriography where possible because of vessel fragility; favour non-invasive imaging such as ultrasound and CT or MR angiography.","Celiprolol is used to reduce the risk of arterial rupture and dissection.","Pregnancy carries a high risk of uterine and arterial rupture and requires specialist multidisciplinary planning and delivery in an experienced centre."],"pathophysiology":"COL3A1 encodes the pro-alpha1 chains that assemble into homotrimeric type III collagen, a fibrillar collagen abundant in the walls of arteries, the gastrointestinal tract and the gravid uterus. The majority of disease-causing variants are glycine substitutions within the triple-helical domain or splice-site changes that produce a structurally abnormal chain acting through a dominant-negative mechanism; less common null or haploinsufficiency variants tend to yield a milder phenotype. Incorporation of abnormal chains destabilises the collagen triple helix, impairs secretion and processing, and weakens the extracellular matrix of hollow and tubular organs. The resulting loss of tensile strength in vessel walls and viscera predisposes to spontaneous rupture, dissection and perforation under normal physiological stress.","additionalDifferentials":["Loeys-Dietz syndrome","Marfan syndrome","Classical Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Familial thoracic aortic aneurysm and dissection","Arterial tortuosity syndrome","Kyphoscoliotic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Cutis laxa syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["COL3A1 molecular genetic testing including sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis","Dermal fibroblast culture with biochemical analysis of type III collagen","Non-invasive vascular imaging with Doppler ultrasound and CT or MR angiography","Baseline and surveillance echocardiography","Skin biopsy for electron microscopy of collagen fibrils","Cascade genetic testing and counselling of at-risk relatives"],"redFlags":["Acute severe chest, abdominal or flank pain suggesting arterial or organ rupture","Sudden neurological deficit or painful red eye suggesting carotid-cavernous fistula or arterial dissection","Unexplained hypotension, collapse or signs of shock","Pregnancy, labour or the early postpartum period in a known or suspected patient","Rapidly expanding haematoma or pulsatile mass"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is historically markedly reduced owing to arterial and organ rupture, with major complications often occurring by mid-adulthood. Structured surveillance, blood-pressure control and celiprolol appear to improve outcomes, though the risk of sudden catastrophic events persists.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the mild skin and joint findings for benign hypermobility and missing the diagnosis until a catastrophic vascular event.","Performing invasive arteriography or aggressive vascular intervention that itself precipitates vessel rupture.","Overlooking a family history of sudden death, arterial events or bowel perforation at a young age.","Underestimating the maternal risk of pregnancy and failing to arrange multidisciplinary obstetric planning."],"management":["Prescribe celiprolol to reduce haemodynamic wall stress and lower the risk of arterial rupture and dissection","Maintain strict blood-pressure control and advise avoidance of heavy lifting, straining, contact sports and other activities that acutely raise arterial pressure","Favour non-invasive vascular surveillance with Doppler ultrasound and CT or MR angiography, avoiding catheter arteriography and elective invasive procedures because of vessel fragility","Manage arterial rupture, dissection or hollow-organ perforation as emergencies in a specialist centre, adopting conservative or minimally interventional strategies where feasible given tissue friability","Provide high-risk pre-pregnancy counselling and specialist obstetric management given the danger of uterine and arterial rupture","Issue medical-alert documentation and counsel on prompt presentation for sudden chest, abdominal or limb pain","Offer genetic counselling with cascade COL3A1 testing of at-risk relatives and coordinate care through a multidisciplinary connective-tissue service"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International EDS classification consensus criteria","Standard medical genetics textbooks","Peer-reviewed connective tissue disease reviews"],"crossLinks":[]},"vascular_ring":{"name":"Vascular ring","system":"Cardiovascular / Congenital","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vascular ring and sling","aortic arch anomaly","double aortic arch","aberrant subclavian artery ring"],"chapter":null,"source":"Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An abnormal arrangement of the aortic arch and its branches that encircles and compresses the trachea and oesophagus","Double aortic arch and right aortic arch with an aberrant left subclavian artery and left ligamentum are the commonest complete rings","Respiratory symptoms predominate, including stridor, a barking cough, wheeze and recurrent chest infections that may be mistaken for asthma","Oesophageal compression causes feeding difficulty, dysphagia and, in older children, dysphagia to solids","A pulmonary artery sling, in which the left pulmonary artery arises anomalously, compresses the trachea and is often associated with complete tracheal rings","Barium swallow showing posterior oesophageal indentation and cross-sectional angiography establish the diagnosis","Surgical division of the ring relieves compression, though tracheomalacia at the compressed segment may cause lingering symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"Vascular rings arise from abnormal regression or persistence of the paired embryonic aortic arches, producing vascular structures that completely or partially encircle the trachea and oesophagus. A double aortic arch forms a complete ring as both arches persist, while a right aortic arch with an aberrant left subclavian artery and a left arterial ligament completes a ring posteriorly. These structures exert extrinsic pressure on the airway and oesophagus, causing airflow obstruction, secondary tracheomalacia and impaired swallowing. A pulmonary artery sling, though strictly a partial ring, compresses the trachea as the anomalous left pulmonary artery courses between trachea and oesophagus and is frequently accompanied by complete cartilaginous tracheal rings that intrinsically narrow the airway.","additionalDifferentials":["Asthma or reactive airways disease","Tracheomalacia or laryngomalacia of other cause","Recurrent viral croup","Foreign body aspiration","Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease","Mediastinal mass compressing the airway"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Barium oesophagram showing characteristic posterior indentation","CT or MR angiography to define the arch anatomy and ring components","Bronchoscopy to assess airway compression and associated tracheomalacia or complete tracheal rings","Echocardiography to detect associated intracardiac defects","Chest radiograph for arch sidedness and airway narrowing","Assessment for a pulmonary artery sling when tracheal narrowing is present"],"redFlags":["Severe stridor or acute airway obstruction","Recurrent aspiration or life-threatening apnoeic episodes","Failure to thrive from significant feeding difficulty","Complete tracheal rings with a pulmonary artery sling causing critical airway narrowing"],"prognosis":"Surgical division of a symptomatic ring generally relieves compression with excellent long-term results, though residual tracheomalacia can cause persistent symptoms; associated complete tracheal rings carry higher risk.","pitfalls":["Treating persistent stridor and wheeze as asthma and delaying diagnosis","Overlooking oesophageal compression as a cause of feeding difficulty","Failing to recognise an associated pulmonary artery sling and complete tracheal rings","Assuming surgery immediately resolves all symptoms when tracheomalacia persists"],"management":["Divide symptomatic vascular rings surgically, dividing the lesser arch in a double aortic arch or the ligamentum arteriosum in a right arch with an aberrant left subclavian artery, to relieve tracheo-oesophageal compression","Reimplant the anomalous vessel for a pulmonary artery sling and address any coexisting complete tracheal rings with tracheoplasty","Observe asymptomatic rings found incidentally, with clinical follow-up","Support residual tracheomalacia and treat recurrent chest infections while the airway remodels","Define the anatomy preoperatively with CT or MR angiography, assess the airway with bronchoscopy and screen for associated cardiac defects by echocardiography","Manage through a multidisciplinary team of paediatric cardiothoracic surgery, airway or ENT specialists and cardiology","Follow up after repair for persistent airway symptoms arising from residual tracheomalacia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Moss and Adams' Heart Disease in Infants, Children and Adolescents","Anderson's Paediatric Cardiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vein_of_galen_malformation":{"name":"vein of galen malformation","system":"cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vein of Galen aneurysmal malformation","VGAM"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The vein of Galen aneurysmal malformation is a congenital arteriovenous fistula between choroidal or quadrigeminal arteries and the persistent embryonic median prosencephalic vein of Markowski, not a true aneurysm of the mature vein of Galen.","The neonatal form presents with high-output cardiac failure that is disproportionate to any structural cardiac lesion, driven by the massive intracranial left-to-right shunt.","A loud cranial bruit audible over the fontanelle or orbits in a neonate with heart failure is a valuable clue.","Because so much combined ventricular output is diverted to the low-resistance shunt, there may be retrograde diastolic flow in the aorta and compromised systemic and coronary perfusion.","Later infantile presentation is dominated by macrocephaly, hydrocephalus and developmental delay rather than cardiac failure.","The melting brain phenomenon reflects chronic cerebral venous hypertension and hypoperfusion causing progressive parenchymal loss.","Staged endovascular embolisation, timed to balance cardiac stability against neurological maturation, is the mainstay of treatment.","A validated neonatal scoring system incorporating cardiac, cerebral, respiratory, hepatic and renal function guides whether to intervene urgently or defer."],"pathophysiology":"The malformation arises early in gestation from a persistent arteriovenous shunt draining into the median prosencephalic vein, the embryonic precursor that normally regresses as the internal cerebral veins form. Persistent high-flow shunting dilates this venous sac and diverts a large fraction of cardiac output through a low-resistance circuit. In the neonate this imposes a huge volume load, producing high-output biventricular failure, pulmonary hypertension and a diastolic steal that impairs systemic organ perfusion. Within the brain, impaired venous drainage raises cerebral venous pressure, causing hydrocephalus, impaired cerebrospinal fluid resorption, and chronic ischaemic injury with calcification and atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["structural congenital heart disease causing neonatal heart failure","other cerebral or hepatic arteriovenous malformations","persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","neonatal sepsis with cardiovascular collapse","congenital hydrocephalus of other cause","dural sinus malformation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["transfontanelle cranial ultrasound with Doppler","brain MRI and MR angiography/venography","catheter cerebral angiography to define feeding arteries and drainage","echocardiography for shunt burden and pulmonary pressure","B-type natriuretic peptide and cardiac biomarkers","renal and hepatic function panels for multi-organ effect"],"redFlags":["neonatal high-output cardiac failure refractory to medical therapy","cranial bruit with rapidly increasing head circumference","signs of raised intracranial pressure or seizures","multi-organ dysfunction indicating systemic steal"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the severity of cardiac failure and the degree of established brain injury at presentation; neonates with intractable heart failure or extensive parenchymal loss fare poorly, whereas selected infants treated by staged embolisation can achieve good neurodevelopmental outcomes.","pitfalls":["Attributing neonatal heart failure solely to a cardiac cause and omitting cranial imaging.","Emergency embolisation of a neonate with irreversible brain injury when comfort care is more appropriate.","Placing a ventriculoperitoneal shunt for hydrocephalus before addressing venous hypertension, which can worsen haemorrhage.","Underestimating pulmonary hypertension and its impact on peri-procedural stability.","Missing the diagnosis in an older infant presenting only with macrocephaly and delay."],"management":["Achieve definitive closure by staged transarterial endovascular embolisation of the arteriovenous shunt, timed to allow growth where the neonate can be stabilised medically","Stabilise high-output cardiac failure with diuretics, inotropic support and mechanical ventilation as required","Manage the associated pulmonary hypertension and optimise systemic and coronary perfusion compromised by diastolic steal","Treat hydrocephalus, preferably by reducing shunt flow through embolisation before considering cerebrospinal fluid diversion","Monitor head circumference and neurodevelopment with serial cranial imaging","Coordinate care across interventional neuroradiology, neonatology, paediatric cardiology and neurosurgery in a specialist centre","Use validated selection criteria to guide timing and candidacy, and involve palliative care where irreversible parenchymal brain injury is established"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics, current edition","Lasjaunias classification and Bicetre neonatal evaluation score","Youmans and Winn Neurological Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"venezuelan_equine_encephalitis":{"name":"Venezuelan equine encephalitis","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VEE","Venezuelan equine encephalitis virus infection","VEEV infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases / CDC guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mosquito-borne alphavirus of Central and South America and occasionally the southern United States, with rodents as enzootic reservoirs and epizootic strains amplified in horses","In contrast to eastern equine encephalitis, most human infections cause a self-limiting acute febrile illness with headache, myalgia, and prostration rather than encephalitis","A minority, particularly children, develop neuroinvasive disease with seizures and encephalitis; overall mortality is much lower than for eastern equine encephalitis","Large epizootics in horses, which suffer high mortality and amplify the virus, typically precede and predict human outbreaks","The virus is highly infectious as an aerosol, making it a recognised laboratory-acquired infection hazard requiring enhanced biosafety","Management is supportive with no licensed specific antiviral; equine vaccination and mosquito control are central to outbreak prevention","Diagnosis uses RT-PCR early and serology (IgM) thereafter, interpreted with the mosquito-exposure and equine-outbreak history"],"pathophysiology":"Venezuelan equine encephalitis virus circulates in enzootic cycles between rodents and mosquitoes, while epizootic variants replicate to high titres in horses that serve as amplifying hosts and drive outbreaks. Following a mosquito bite the virus replicates in lymphoid tissue and produces viraemia, giving rise to a systemic febrile illness. Neuroinvasion occurs in a subset of patients, with the virus infecting neurons and provoking inflammation, but central nervous system involvement is less frequent and generally less devastating than in eastern equine encephalitis. Its efficient aerosol infectivity accounts for the laboratory-acquired infection risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Dengue and other arboviral febrile illnesses","Other equine encephalitides (eastern and western equine encephalitis)","Influenza and nonspecific viral febrile illness","Bacterial or viral meningoencephalitis","Malaria in co-endemic regions","Leptospirosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR on blood for viral RNA during the acute viraemic phase","Serum and cerebrospinal fluid IgM serology","Full blood count often showing leucopenia","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis when encephalitis is suspected","Neuroimaging in patients with neurological features","Testing to exclude co-endemic febrile illnesses such as dengue and malaria"],"redFlags":["Seizures or declining consciousness signalling neuroinvasive disease, especially in children","Focal neurological deficits","Signs of raised intracranial pressure","Rapid clinical deterioration during an active equine epizootic","Laboratory exposure raising concern for aerosol-acquired infection"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover fully from the febrile illness; neuroinvasive disease is uncommon and carries a lower fatality than eastern equine encephalitis, though children with encephalitis may have neurological sequelae.","pitfalls":["Assuming encephalitis is the usual presentation when most cases are a self-limiting febrile illness","Neglecting aerosol biosafety precautions in the laboratory","Overlooking the predictive value of concurrent equine outbreaks","Failing to distinguish it from dengue and other co-endemic arboviral fevers","Underestimating neuroinvasive risk in paediatric patients"],"management":["Provide supportive care with antipyretics, analgesia, rehydration and rest, as there is no licensed specific antiviral","Manage neuroinvasive disease with anticonvulsants for seizures, control of raised intracranial pressure and intensive-care support","Reduce mosquito exposure with repellents, protective clothing and bed nets, and implement community vector control","Interrupt transmission through equine vaccination and veterinary control of the amplifying host","Observe enhanced biosafety precautions given the high aerosol infectivity and laboratory-acquisition risk","Exclude and treat co-endemic febrile illnesses such as dengue and malaria","Notify public health authorities and support outbreak-control measures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","CDC guidance on Venezuelan equine encephalitis"],"crossLinks":[]},"venous_aneurysm":{"name":"venous aneurysm","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["venous aneurysmal dilatation","popliteal venous aneurysm","venous phlebectasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A venous aneurysm is a localised saccular or fusiform dilatation of a vein arising without an antecedent arteriovenous fistula or pseudoaneurysm; these lesions are uncommon and are often congenital or idiopathic","Popliteal venous aneurysm is the most clinically important type because it is a well-recognised source of recurrent pulmonary embolism, which may occur despite adequate anticoagulation","Presentations range from an asymptomatic popliteal swelling to leg pain, deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism, and a mass that changes with posture or Valsalva is suggestive","Internal jugular venous aneurysm (phlebectasia) typically presents in children as a soft, compressible neck swelling that enlarges on straining and is usually benign","Duplex ultrasound is the principal diagnostic test, while cross-sectional venography by CT or MR delineates the anatomy for surgical planning and helps to identify intraluminal thrombus","Surgical repair, such as tangential aneurysmorrhaphy with lateral venorrhaphy or resection with an end-to-end anastomosis or interposition graft, is recommended for popliteal venous aneurysm because of the embolic risk, generally with perioperative anticoagulation","Small or superficial saccular aneurysms elsewhere may be observed with surveillance, whereas deep venous aneurysms containing thrombus or associated with embolism warrant intervention"],"pathophysiology":"Venous aneurysms arise from a focal weakness of the vein wall with loss of the normal balance of smooth muscle and elastic tissue, allowing localised dilatation. The cause is frequently congenital or idiopathic, although trauma, chronic inflammation, degenerative change and localised elevations in venous pressure are implicated. The dilated segment behaves as a low-flow reservoir in which turbulence and stasis promote the formation of mural thrombus; within a deep vein such as the popliteal, this thrombus can detach and embolise to the pulmonary circulation. A saccular morphology tends to carry a greater thrombogenic and embolic risk than a fusiform dilatation.","additionalDifferentials":["Popliteal (Baker) cyst","Popliteal artery aneurysm","Soft tissue tumour such as lipoma or sarcoma","Deep vein thrombosis","Varicose vein or venous malformation","Lymph node mass or cystic lymphangioma","Ganglion or synovial cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound as the first-line diagnostic study","CT or MR venography to define morphology and extent","Contrast venography where the anatomy remains unclear","CT pulmonary angiography when pulmonary embolism is suspected","Careful imaging assessment for intraluminal thrombus","Thrombophilia screen in patients presenting with thromboembolism","Comparative arterial imaging to distinguish from an arterial aneurysm when the site is ambiguous"],"redFlags":["Pulmonary embolism, especially when recurrent or occurring despite anticoagulation","Intraluminal thrombus demonstrated within the aneurysm","Rapid enlargement or symptoms of local compression","Saccular morphology of a deep (popliteal) venous aneurysm","Rupture, though rare, or features of an acute deep vein thrombosis"],"prognosis":"Superficial and small venous aneurysms are generally benign and can be observed. Popliteal and other deep venous aneurysms carry a genuine risk of potentially fatal pulmonary embolism, but surgical repair has good long-term outcomes with a low rate of recurrence.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing a popliteal venous aneurysm as a Baker cyst and thereby missing the embolic risk","Relying on anticoagulation alone for a popliteal venous aneurysm, which does not reliably prevent recurrent embolism","Failing to image for intraluminal thrombus before planning intervention","Overlooking a venous aneurysm as the source in unexplained or recurrent pulmonary embolism","Attempting to distinguish a venous from an arterial aneurysm on clinical grounds alone"],"management":["Repair popliteal and other deep venous aneurysms surgically by tangential aneurysmorrhaphy with lateral venorrhaphy, or resection with end-to-end anastomosis or interposition graft, because of the risk of pulmonary embolism","Give perioperative anticoagulation and treat established thrombosis or pulmonary embolism with therapeutic anticoagulation","Observe small, superficial or asymptomatic aneurysms and manage benign internal jugular phlebectasia in children conservatively","Monitor with serial duplex ultrasound, assessing specifically for intraluminal thrombus","Investigate suspected pulmonary embolism with CT pulmonary angiography","Perform a thrombophilia screen in patients presenting with thromboembolism","Refer to vascular surgery for deep venous aneurysms and for operative planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Haimovici's Vascular Surgery","Journal of Vascular Surgery review literature on venous aneurysms"],"crossLinks":[]},"venous_leg_ulcer":{"name":"Venous leg ulcer","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["venous stasis ulcer","varicose ulcer","gravitational ulcer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / NICE-SIGN venous guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Venous leg ulcers are the commonest type of chronic leg ulcer and result from chronic venous insufficiency with sustained ambulatory venous hypertension","They characteristically lie in the gaiter area of the lower leg, most often over or just proximal to the medial malleolus","Ulcers are usually shallow with irregular sloping edges and a granulating, exudative base, and are typically only moderately painful","The surrounding skin shows chronic venous changes such as haemosiderin pigmentation, varicose eczema, lipodermatosclerosis, atrophie blanche and oedema, sometimes producing an inverted champagne-bottle contour","Arterial disease must be excluded before compression, as the ankle-brachial index determines whether compression, the mainstay of treatment, can be applied safely","Graduated compression with multilayer bandaging or hosiery substantially improves healing, and endovenous or surgical treatment of superficial reflux reduces recurrence","A chronic or atypical non-healing ulcer warrants biopsy to exclude malignant transformation such as a Marjolin ulcer or another diagnosis","Recurrence is common, so long-term compression hosiery, leg elevation, calf-muscle exercise and correction of underlying reflux are central to prevention"],"pathophysiology":"Venous valvular incompetence in the superficial, perforator or deep systems, venous outflow obstruction, or failure of the calf-muscle pump raises the ambulatory venous pressure transmitted to the lower leg. Persistent venous hypertension causes capillary distension and leakage of fibrinogen and macromolecules, forming pericapillary fibrin cuffs, and promotes leucocyte trapping with release of inflammatory mediators and proteases. The resulting chronic inflammation, dermal fibrosis and impaired tissue oxygenation render the gaiter skin fragile, so that minor trauma to this compromised tissue ulcerates and heals poorly.","additionalDifferentials":["Arterial (ischaemic) ulcer","Mixed arteriovenous ulcer","Neuropathic or diabetic foot ulcer","Vasculitic ulcer","Pyoderma gangrenosum","Marjolin ulcer (squamous cell carcinoma in a chronic ulcer)","Martorell hypertensive ischaemic ulcer"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ankle-brachial index to exclude arterial disease and enable safe compression","Venous duplex ultrasound to map sites of reflux and obstruction","Wound swab or tissue culture where clinical infection is present","Biopsy of atypical or non-healing ulcers to exclude malignancy","Assessment for diabetes and anaemia that may impair healing","Patch testing where contact allergy to dressings or topical agents is suspected"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting malignant transformation, such as rolled or everted edges, exuberant granulation or failure to heal despite adequate treatment","Signs of spreading infection or cellulitis, or systemic sepsis","A sudden increase in pain, which may indicate coexisting arterial disease, infection or malignancy","Rapid deterioration or enlargement of the ulcer"],"prognosis":"Many venous ulcers heal with adequate compression and treatment of underlying reflux, although healing can be slow; recurrence is frequent unless compression is maintained and the underlying venous disease is corrected.","pitfalls":["Starting compression without checking the ankle-brachial index and excluding arterial disease","Attributing every leg ulcer to venous disease without reassessment","Failing to biopsy a chronic non-healing ulcer","Applying inadequate compression so the ulcer fails to heal","Neglecting varicose eczema and skin care, or leaving underlying superficial reflux untreated so the ulcer recurs"],"management":["Apply graduated multilayer compression bandaging or hosiery as the mainstay of healing, only after excluding significant arterial disease with the ankle-brachial index","Provide wound care with cleansing, debridement of slough, simple non-adherent dressings and exudate management","Treat underlying superficial venous reflux with endovenous ablation to accelerate healing and reduce recurrence","Manage venous eczema of the surrounding skin with emollients and topical corticosteroids, reserving systemic antibiotics for clinical infection rather than colonisation","Encourage leg elevation, exercise and calf-muscle pump activity, and correct contributors to poor healing such as anaemia, diabetes and poor nutrition","Maintain long-term compression hosiery once healed to prevent recurrence","Biopsy atypical or non-healing ulcers to exclude malignancy and refer to tissue viability and vascular services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["NICE and SIGN guidance on the management of venous leg ulcers","Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","European guidelines on chronic venous disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"venous_malformation":{"name":"Venous malformation","system":"Skin / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VM","Cavernous haemangioma (obsolete term)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies: Hemangiomas and Malformations","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Slow-flow malformation of dysplastic venous channels present at birth, though sometimes only apparent later; grows proportionately and does not involute.","Soft, compressible, blue-purple lesion that swells with dependency, Valsalva or crying and empties on compression or elevation.","Phleboliths (calcified thrombi) are pathognomonic and a common cause of localised pain.","Sporadic lesions commonly harbour somatic TIE2 (TEK) mutations; PIK3CA mutations also occur, and familial mucocutaneous forms are TEK-related.","Localised intravascular coagulopathy with raised D-dimer and low fibrinogen is common and can worsen with intervention or in extensive lesions.","Sclerotherapy is first-line for most symptomatic lesions; surgery is reserved for well-localised disease.","Glomuvenous malformations (glomulin gene) are firmer, more nodular and painful on compression, forming a distinct subtype.","MRI best characterises extent, while ultrasound shows compressible channels with slow or absent flow."],"pathophysiology":"Venous malformation is a congenital slow-flow vascular malformation composed of ectatic, thin-walled venous channels lined by flat, quiescent endothelium with a deficient and irregular smooth-muscle layer, which impairs contraction and causes progressive dilatation and stasis. Most sporadic lesions arise from somatic activating mutations in the endothelial receptor TIE2 (TEK) or in PIK3CA, driving PI3K/AKT/mTOR signalling. Venous stasis within the malformation activates coagulation locally, producing a chronic localised intravascular coagulopathy with thrombosis, phlebolith formation and pain.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphatic malformation","Infantile or congenital haemangioma","Arteriovenous malformation","Glomuvenous malformation","Kaposiform haemangioendothelioma","Soft-tissue tumour such as a haemangiopericytoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Doppler ultrasound showing compressible channels with monophasic slow or absent flow","Contrast-enhanced MRI (T2-hyperintense lobulated lesion with phleboliths)","Plain radiograph demonstrating phleboliths","Coagulation screen including D-dimer and fibrinogen","Direct phlebography at the time of sclerotherapy","Genetic testing (TEK, PIK3CA) in selected or familial cases"],"redFlags":["Rapid painful swelling suggesting acute thrombosis","Airway, orbital or visceral involvement","Extensive lesions with severe localised intravascular coagulopathy risking haemorrhage","Functional impairment or neurological compression"],"prognosis":"Venous malformations are chronic and tend to enlarge slowly over life, particularly around puberty. Symptoms are often controllable with sclerotherapy and compression, but complete cure is uncommon in extensive lesions.","pitfalls":["Using the obsolete term cavernous haemangioma and implying that the lesion will involute.","Neglecting to assess and correct localised intravascular coagulopathy before intervention.","Mistaking phlebolith pain for another cause.","Assuming surgery alone will cure a diffuse lesion."],"management":["Offer sclerotherapy as first-line treatment for most symptomatic lesions, reserving surgical resection for well-localised disease","Consider the mTOR inhibitor sirolimus for extensive or complex malformations not amenable to sclerotherapy or surgery","Provide conservative measures including graduated compression garments, analgesia and aspirin for pain from localised intravascular coagulopathy and phleboliths","Monitor and manage the localised intravascular coagulopathy with D-dimer and fibrinogen, giving low-molecular-weight heparin around procedures in extensive lesions","Consider targeted therapy directed at PIK3CA in selected genetically defined cases","Manage through a multidisciplinary vascular anomalies team with long-term follow-up, as lesions enlarge around puberty and are rarely cured when extensive"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of dermatology","ISSVA classification of vascular anomalies","Consensus guidance on diagnosis and management of venous malformations"],"crossLinks":[]},"ventilator_associated_pneumonia":{"name":"Ventilator-associated pneumonia","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VAP","ventilator associated pneumonia","ventilator-associated lower respiratory infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as pneumonia developing more than 48 hours after endotracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation","Suspected when a ventilated patient develops new or progressive infiltrates with fever, leukocytosis, purulent secretions, and worsening oxygenation","Common pathogens include Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA), and other Gram-negative bacilli, with resistant organisms more likely in late-onset disease","Early-onset VAP tends to involve more antibiotic-susceptible organisms than late-onset VAP","Lower respiratory tract sampling for culture before starting antibiotics helps target and later de-escalate therapy","Management principles are prompt empiric antibiotics guided by local resistance patterns and patient risk factors, followed by de-escalation once cultures return","Risk factors for resistant organisms include recent antibiotic exposure, prolonged hospitalisation, and prior colonisation","Prevention bundles (head-of-bed elevation, sedation and ventilator weaning protocols, oral care, and subglottic secretion drainage) reduce incidence"],"pathophysiology":"The endotracheal tube bypasses upper airway defences and impairs the cough reflex and mucociliary clearance, while its surface accumulates a biofilm of microorganisms. Micro-aspiration of contaminated oropharyngeal and gastric secretions pooling above the cuff delivers pathogens to the lower airways. In critically ill patients the oropharynx becomes colonised with hospital flora, and impaired host defences allow these organisms to establish infection in the lung parenchyma, producing an inflammatory pneumonic process with impaired gas exchange.","additionalDifferentials":["Tracheobronchitis without parenchymal pneumonia","Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Pulmonary oedema from cardiac failure or fluid overload","Atelectasis","Pulmonary embolism or infarction"],"infectionMimics":["ARDS with new infiltrates but no infection","Aspiration pneumonitis without established infection","Alveolar haemorrhage","Drug-induced or chemical pneumonitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Endotracheal aspirate or bronchoscopic sampling for Gram stain and quantitative or semiquantitative culture","Chest radiography or CT to assess infiltrates and complications","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers","Arterial blood gases and oxygenation indices to gauge severity","Pleural fluid sampling if a significant effusion or empyema is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive hypoxaemia and haemodynamic instability indicating severe pneumonia and possible septic shock requiring urgent escalation","Failure to improve on empiric therapy suggesting resistant organisms, complication, or wrong diagnosis","Development of empyema, lung abscess, or necrotising pneumonia","Multidrug-resistant pathogen risk warranting broader empiric cover"],"prognosis":"VAP prolongs mechanical ventilation and ICU stay and is associated with substantial attributable mortality, which is higher with resistant pathogens such as Pseudomonas, inappropriate initial antibiotics, and severe underlying illness; timely appropriate therapy improves outcomes.","pitfalls":["Starting or continuing antibiotics for colonisation or non-infective infiltrates without adequate evidence of pneumonia","Choosing empiric therapy that ignores local resistance patterns and patient risk factors","Failing to de-escalate once culture results are available, promoting resistance","Overlooking preventable contributors and neglecting VAP prevention bundles"],"management":["Start prompt empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics guided by local resistance patterns and onset timing, covering Pseudomonas aeruginosa and MRSA in late-onset or high-risk disease","Obtain lower respiratory tract samples for culture before antibiotics, then de-escalate to targeted therapy and limit the treatment course once results return","Provide supportive care with lung-protective mechanical ventilation, optimisation of oxygenation and management of accompanying sepsis","Apply a prevention bundle of head-of-bed elevation, daily sedation interruption with spontaneous breathing trials to enable early extubation, oral antiseptic care and subglottic secretion drainage","Achieve source control by draining an associated empyema or complicated parapneumonic effusion","Monitor clinical response and reassess for resistant or alternative pathogens if the patient deteriorates or fails to improve"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"ventricular_septal_defect":{"name":"Ventricular Septal Defect","system":"Paediatric cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VSD","ventricular septal defects"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common congenital heart malformation, an opening in the interventricular septum that permits shunting between the ventricles; most are membranous or muscular in location.","The direction and size of the shunt depend on the defect size and the relative resistances of the pulmonary and systemic circulations, so many defects become clinically apparent only as pulmonary resistance falls in the first weeks of life.","Small defects produce a loud, often harsh holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border but little haemodynamic burden, and many close spontaneously.","Large defects cause a significant left-to-right shunt with excessive pulmonary blood flow, leading to heart failure signs such as tachypnoea, poor feeding, sweating, and failure to thrive in infancy.","A large shunt may generate a mid-diastolic rumble at the apex from increased flow across the mitral valve.","Untreated large defects risk progressive pulmonary vascular disease that can eventually reverse the shunt (Eisenmenger physiology), a late and largely irreversible complication.","Echocardiography defines the defect location and size, estimates shunt magnitude, and assesses chamber enlargement and pulmonary pressures.","Management of a haemodynamically significant defect focuses on treating heart failure and supporting growth, with surgical or catheter closure for defects that do not shrink or that cause failure or pulmonary hypertension.","Many small muscular and membranous defects close on their own during early childhood."],"pathophysiology":"A communication in the ventricular septum allows blood to shunt according to the pressure and resistance gradient between the ventricles. After birth, as pulmonary vascular resistance declines, left-to-right shunting increases, raising pulmonary blood flow and volume-loading the left atrium and left ventricle. A large, sustained shunt increases pulmonary artery pressure and flow, and over time medial hypertrophy and intimal changes in the pulmonary vasculature raise resistance irreversibly; when pulmonary resistance exceeds systemic, the shunt reverses and produces cyanosis (Eisenmenger syndrome).","additionalDifferentials":["Atrioventricular septal defect","Patent ductus arteriosus","Atrial septal defect","Tetralogy of Fallot with a dominant VSD murmur","Innocent (functional) murmur","Aortic or pulmonary stenosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to localise the defect and quantify shunt and chamber size","Chest radiograph for cardiomegaly and pulmonary plethora in large shunts","Electrocardiogram for left or biventricular hypertrophy","Assessment of growth and feeding as markers of shunt significance","Cardiac catheterisation to measure pulmonary vascular resistance when pulmonary hypertension is suspected"],"redFlags":["Signs of congestive heart failure in infancy: tachypnoea, feeding difficulty, sweating, and failure to thrive","Development of pulmonary hypertension or a shrinking left-to-right shunt suggesting rising pulmonary resistance","New cyanosis or a diminishing murmur indicating shunt reversal (Eisenmenger physiology)","Poor weight gain despite medical therapy","Signs of infective endocarditis in a child with a known defect"],"prognosis":"Small defects have an excellent outlook and often close spontaneously. Large defects, if repaired before irreversible pulmonary vascular disease develops, generally have a good outcome; delayed closure risks fixed pulmonary hypertension and a markedly worse prognosis.","pitfalls":["Assuming a loud murmur means a large, dangerous defect when small defects are often the loudest","Missing a large defect in the newborn period before pulmonary resistance falls and the murmur becomes apparent","Overlooking progression to pulmonary vascular disease by delaying closure of a significant shunt","Attributing poor growth to feeding problems rather than an underlying significant shunt","Failing to recognise shunt reversal when cyanosis appears in a previously acyanotic child"],"management":["Manage small defects expectantly with observation, as many close spontaneously, alongside dental hygiene and endocarditis awareness","Treat the heart failure of a large left-to-right shunt medically with diuretics and afterload reduction using ACE inhibitors, plus high-calorie nutritional support for faltering growth","Close large or symptomatic defects surgically with a patch, or by transcatheter device closure in suitable cases, before irreversible pulmonary vascular disease develops","Monitor with serial echocardiography for shunt size, chamber loading and rising pulmonary pressures","Once Eisenmenger physiology is established, avoid defect closure and manage with pulmonary vasodilator therapy, counselling against pregnancy","Provide ongoing multidisciplinary paediatric and congenital cardiology follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"ventricular_tachycardia":{"name":"Ventricular tachycardia","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VT","monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","wide-complex tachycardia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["VT is a wide-complex tachycardia arising below the bundle of His; any wide-complex tachycardia should be assumed to be VT until proven otherwise, especially in patients with structural heart disease or prior infarction","It is classified as non-sustained or sustained, and as monomorphic or polymorphic, distinctions that guide both diagnosis and management","Monomorphic VT usually reflects a fixed reentry substrate such as a myocardial scar, while polymorphic VT often indicates acute ischaemia or, in the setting of a long QT, torsades de pointes","ECG clues favouring VT over SVT with aberrancy include AV dissociation, capture and fusion beats, and very broad or concordant QRS morphology","Haemodynamically unstable sustained VT requires immediate synchronised cardioversion, and pulseless VT is treated as cardiac arrest with defibrillation","Stable sustained monomorphic VT may be treated pharmacologically under monitoring, but readiness to cardiovert must be maintained","Identifying and correcting reversible triggers such as ischaemia, electrolyte disturbance, and QT-prolonging drugs is essential","An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator is central to secondary prevention in patients with sustained VT and structural heart disease, and catheter ablation reduces recurrent episodes"],"pathophysiology":"Most sustained monomorphic VT is due to reentry around regions of myocardial scar, typically after prior infarction, where slow conduction through surviving fibres embedded in fibrosis creates a stable circuit. Polymorphic VT commonly arises from acute ischaemia producing heterogeneous repolarisation, or from delayed repolarisation in long QT syndromes, generating triggered activity that degenerates into torsades de pointes. Abnormal automaticity and triggered activity from afterdepolarisations also contribute, particularly with electrolyte disturbance, drug effects, or catecholamine excess. Sustained rapid ventricular rates impair filling and cardiac output and can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation.","additionalDifferentials":["Supraventricular tachycardia with aberrant conduction","Pre-excited tachycardia (antidromic AVRT or atrial fibrillation with an accessory pathway)","Ventricular fibrillation","Torsades de pointes in long QT syndrome","Paced rhythm with rapid tracking","Artefact mimicking wide-complex tachycardia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG during tachycardia and in sinus rhythm to assess morphology and baseline substrate","Electrolytes including potassium and magnesium, and review of QT-prolonging medications","Echocardiography and often cardiac MRI to define structural substrate and scar","Assessment for coronary ischaemia including angiography when indicated","Electrophysiology study to characterise the circuit and guide ablation","Ambulatory monitoring to capture non-sustained episodes"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: pulseless VT or degeneration into ventricular fibrillation is cardiac arrest requiring immediate defibrillation and resuscitation","Haemodynamically unstable sustained VT with hypotension, ischaemia, or reduced consciousness requiring urgent synchronised cardioversion","Polymorphic VT with a prolonged QT (torsades) requiring correction of the QT and specific measures","Incessant or recurrent VT (electrical storm) requiring intensive management"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is strongly tied to the underlying substrate and left ventricular function; VT in structurally normal hearts is often benign, whereas sustained VT with impaired ventricular function or scar carries a high risk of sudden death that is mitigated by defibrillator therapy and substrate-targeted treatment.","pitfalls":["Treating a wide-complex tachycardia as SVT with aberrancy when it is VT, potentially with hazardous AV nodal blocking drugs","Delaying cardioversion in an unstable patient in favour of drug therapy","Missing a reversible cause such as ischaemia, hyperkalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, or QT-prolonging drugs","Overlooking the need for defibrillator evaluation in secondary prevention after sustained VT with structural disease"],"management":["Treat haemodynamically unstable sustained VT with immediate synchronised cardioversion, and pulseless VT as cardiac arrest with defibrillation and resuscitation","Manage stable monomorphic VT with intravenous antiarrhythmics such as amiodarone, lidocaine or procainamide while maintaining readiness to cardiovert","For polymorphic VT and torsades de pointes, correct hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia, give intravenous magnesium, withdraw QT-prolonging drugs and treat underlying ischaemia","Address reversible substrate and precipitants, including coronary revascularisation for ischaemia and correction of electrolyte disturbance","Implant a defibrillator for secondary prevention in patients with structural heart disease or impaired ventricular function, supported by beta-blocker therapy","Offer catheter ablation for recurrent scar-related monomorphic VT, with adjunctive antiarrhythmic drug therapy","Refer to electrophysiology and pursue genetic evaluation and family screening where an inherited arrhythmia syndrome such as long QT is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"vernal_keratoconjunctivitis":{"name":"Vernal keratoconjunctivitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VKC","spring catarrh","vernal catarrh"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology / Cornea by Krachmer","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic, bilateral, often seasonally recurrent allergic keratoconjunctivitis affecting mainly children and young males, frequently with atopy","The palpebral form shows large cobblestone (giant) papillae on the upper tarsal conjunctiva","The limbal form shows gelatinous limbal papillae with white Horner-Trantas dots (collections of eosinophils and epithelial cells)","Intense itching, ropy mucoid discharge, photophobia, and tearing are characteristic symptoms","Corneal involvement ranges from punctate epithelial keratitis to a shield ulcer, a sterile oval epithelial defect in the superior cornea from inflammatory mediators and mechanical trauma from giant papillae","A shield ulcer can become plaque-covered and heal poorly, threatening the visual axis, and warrants more intensive treatment","Management uses allergen avoidance, topical mast cell stabilisers and antihistamines, topical corticosteroids for flares, and topical immunomodulators (calcineurin inhibitors) for steroid-sparing control","The condition tends to remit after adolescence but can leave limbal stem cell damage, scarring, or keratoconus in severe cases"],"pathophysiology":"Vernal keratoconjunctivitis is a chronic allergic inflammation of the ocular surface involving both IgE-mediated (type I) and cell-mediated (type IV) hypersensitivity, with a prominent role for eosinophils, mast cells, and T lymphocytes. Repeated allergen exposure and chronic inflammation drive proliferation of the tarsal conjunctiva into giant cobblestone papillae and produce gelatinous limbal papillae with Horner-Trantas dots. Eosinophil-derived toxic mediators, together with mechanical rubbing by giant papillae, damage the corneal epithelium and can produce a sterile shield ulcer in the superior cornea. Chronic severe disease may injure the limbal stem cells and cause scarring, and eye rubbing contributes to associated keratoconus.","additionalDifferentials":["Atopic keratoconjunctivitis","Giant papillary conjunctivitis (contact-lens related)","Seasonal and perennial allergic conjunctivitis","Bacterial or viral conjunctivitis","Superior limbic keratoconjunctivitis","Blepharokeratoconjunctivitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination documenting giant tarsal papillae, limbal papillae, and Horner-Trantas dots","Fluorescein staining to detect punctate keratopathy and shield ulcers","Conjunctival scraping or cytology showing eosinophils when the diagnosis is uncertain","Corneal topography to detect associated keratoconus","Allergy evaluation and atopy history","Assessment of limbal stem cell function in severe chronic disease"],"redFlags":["Shield ulcer or plaque threatening the visual axis","Progressive corneal scarring or limbal stem cell failure","Development of keratoconus from chronic eye rubbing","Secondary microbial keratitis complicating an epithelial defect"],"prognosis":"The disease is usually self-limiting and tends to resolve after adolescence, but severe or poorly controlled cases can cause shield ulcers, corneal scarring, limbal stem cell damage, and keratoconus with lasting visual consequences.","pitfalls":["Prolonged unmonitored topical steroid use causing glaucoma, cataract, or infection","Missing a shield ulcer and its threat to the visual axis","Overlooking associated keratoconus in a child who rubs the eyes","Attributing chronic itching solely to simple allergic conjunctivitis and underestimating corneal risk"],"management":["Advise allergen avoidance, cool compresses, ocular lubricants and cessation of eye rubbing as baseline measures","Use topical mast-cell stabilisers or dual-acting antihistamine and mast-cell stabilisers for ongoing control","Give short pulsed courses of topical corticosteroids for severe acute flares, monitoring intraocular pressure","Introduce topical calcineurin inhibitors such as ciclosporin or tacrolimus as steroid-sparing agents in severe or chronic disease","Treat a shield ulcer intensively with anti-inflammatory therapy, lubrication and antibiotic cover, with plaque debridement or surgery if it fails to heal","Monitor for steroid-induced glaucoma and cataract, and screen with corneal topography for associated keratoconus","Refer to ophthalmology and cornea services, reassuring that the disease usually remits after adolescence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Cornea by Krachmer, Mannis and Holland"],"crossLinks":[]},"verrucous_venous_malformation":{"name":"Verrucous venous malformation","system":"Vascular anomalies / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VVM","verrucous haemangioma","MAP3K3-associated verrucous venous malformation"],"chapter":null,"source":"ISSVA classification / Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital vascular malformation historically termed verrucous haemangioma, now recognised as a venous malformation with a hyperkeratotic (warty) surface","Typically presents as a bluish-red plaque or cluster of lesions, most often on a lower limb, that becomes progressively verrucous and hyperkeratotic over time","Unlike infantile haemangioma it does not involute and it is GLUT1-negative, though older confusing nomenclature persists","It tends to enlarge with growth of the child and may bleed, become crusted or secondarily infected","Somatic activating variants in MAP3K3 have been identified, distinguishing it molecularly from common venous malformations","Histology shows dilated venous channels in the dermis and subcutis with overlying epidermal hyperkeratosis, acanthosis and papillomatosis","The deep component often extends beyond the visible surface, which explains the tendency to recur after superficial treatment","Management is guided by symptoms and includes complete surgical excision where feasible, with laser or other modalities for selected lesions"],"pathophysiology":"Verrucous venous malformation is a congenital slow-flow venous malformation in which dilated, abnormal venous channels occupy the dermis and subcutis while the overlying epidermis develops reactive hyperkeratosis, acanthosis and papillomatosis, giving the characteristic warty surface. Somatic activating variants in MAP3K3 drive the lesion and separate it molecularly from common venous malformations. Because the malformed vessels frequently extend deeper than the visible verrucous surface, superficial or incomplete treatment tends to be followed by recurrence and progressive enlargement.","additionalDifferentials":["Common venous malformation","Angiokeratoma (circumscriptum or other types)","Lymphatic malformation with verrucous change","Infantile or congenital haemangioma","Verrucous epidermal naevus","Pyogenic granuloma or other bleeding vascular lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination documenting the verrucous, hyperkeratotic surface and distribution","MRI to assess the depth and full extent of the venous component","Doppler ultrasound to confirm slow-flow venous characteristics","Histopathology showing dermal venous channels with epidermal hyperkeratosis","GLUT1 immunostaining to exclude infantile haemangioma","Molecular testing for MAP3K3 variants where available"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or significant bleeding from the lesion","Ulceration or secondary infection","Rapid enlargement or new symptoms","Functional impairment when overlying joints or weight-bearing areas","Diagnostic uncertainty raising concern for another lesion"],"prognosis":"Verrucous venous malformation is benign but persistent and does not involute; it commonly enlarges with the patient and is prone to recurrence after incomplete treatment, so wide complete excision offers the best chance of durable control while symptoms otherwise guide conservative management.","pitfalls":["Treating it as an involuting infantile haemangioma and awaiting spontaneous resolution","Underestimating the deep venous component and performing inadequate superficial excision","Relying on the outdated term verrucous haemangioma and misclassifying it as a tumour","Overlooking bleeding and infection risk in hyperkeratotic, friable lesions"],"management":["Aim for wide, complete surgical excision including the deep venous component, as this offers the best chance of durable control","Define the full depth and extent with MRI before treatment, since superficial or incomplete excision is followed by recurrence","Use adjunctive laser therapy for the hyperkeratotic surface and sclerotherapy for the venous channels where appropriate","Manage bleeding, crusting and secondary infection of the lesion symptomatically","Adopt conservative, symptom-guided management where complete excision is not feasible","Manage through a vascular anomalies team or dermatology, with follow-up for recurrence and progressive enlargement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ISSVA Classification of Vascular Anomalies","Mulliken and Young's Vascular Anomalies: Haemangiomas and Malformations"],"crossLinks":[]},"vertebral_osteomyelitis":{"name":"Vertebral osteomyelitis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["spinal osteomyelitis","spondylodiscitis","discitis-osteomyelitis","pyogenic spondylitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Usually arises from haematogenous seeding of the vertebral body, with the lumbar spine most commonly affected","New or worsening back pain, often unremitting and worse at night, with or without fever should prompt consideration, especially with a recent bacteraemia","Staphylococcus aureus is the most frequent pathogen; consider Gram-negatives with urinary sources and Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Pott's disease) in endemic or chronic presentations","Risk factors include diabetes, injecting drug use, indwelling vascular catheters, recent bacteraemia, endocarditis, and immunosuppression","MRI with contrast is the imaging investigation of choice for early and accurate diagnosis and for detecting epidural extension","Obtaining a microbiological diagnosis, by blood cultures or image-guided biopsy, before starting antibiotics is important unless the patient is septic","Management principles are prolonged targeted antibiotic therapy guided by culture, with surgery reserved for neurological compromise, instability, or failure of medical treatment","Screen for infective endocarditis and other seeding foci when S. aureus bacteraemia is present"],"pathophysiology":"Pyogenic vertebral osteomyelitis typically begins when bacteria lodge in the vascular end-plate region of a vertebral body via haematogenous spread from a distant focus. Infection then spreads across the relatively avascular intervertebral disc to involve the adjacent vertebra, producing the characteristic pattern of spondylodiscitis. Progressive infection can form paravertebral or epidural abscesses, erode bone leading to vertebral collapse and deformity, and compress neural structures. Less commonly infection reaches the spine by contiguous spread or direct inoculation from surgery or trauma.","additionalDifferentials":["Mechanical or degenerative back pain","Vertebral compression fracture","Metastatic or primary bone tumour","Tuberculous spondylitis (Pott's disease)","Modic degenerative end-plate changes"],"infectionMimics":["Malignant infiltration of the vertebral body","Osteoporotic compression fracture","Erosive degenerative disc disease","Inflammatory spondyloarthropathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the spine with contrast as the primary imaging test","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers (ESR and CRP) which are useful for diagnosis and monitoring","Image-guided or open biopsy for microbiological and, where relevant, histological diagnosis","Echocardiography to exclude endocarditis when S. aureus or other typical organisms are isolated","Investigations for tuberculosis or brucellosis when the clinical picture is subacute or endemic"],"redFlags":["New neurological deficit, radicular weakness, or bladder or bowel dysfunction suggesting epidural abscess with cord or cauda equina compression requiring emergency imaging and surgery","Signs of spinal instability or progressive vertebral collapse","Sepsis or endocarditis complicating the infection","Rapidly progressive, unremitting, or nocturnal back pain in an at-risk patient"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and appropriate prolonged antibiotics most patients recover, but delayed treatment or epidural abscess with cord compression can leave permanent neurological deficits; relapse is more likely with inadequate therapy duration or unaddressed source.","pitfalls":["Starting antibiotics before securing a microbiological diagnosis in a stable patient, obscuring the causative organism","Attributing persistent back pain to mechanical causes and delaying MRI","Missing an associated epidural abscess or endocarditis","Treating for too short a duration and precipitating relapse"],"management":["Obtain a microbiological diagnosis by blood cultures and image-guided biopsy before starting antibiotics, unless the patient is septic","Give targeted prolonged antibiotic therapy with antistaphylococcal cover for Staphylococcus aureus, tailored cover for Gram-negative or brucella sources, and a multidrug antituberculous regimen for Pott's disease","Pursue source control, including excluding and treating endocarditis and removing infected vascular catheters","Refer for surgical decompression and stabilisation for neurological deficit, epidural abscess with cord compression, spinal instability or failure of medical therapy","Provide supportive care with analgesia, spinal bracing and graded mobilisation","Monitor response with serial inflammatory markers, clinical review and repeat imaging","Optimise predisposing factors such as glycaemic control in diabetes and support for injecting drug use"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"verumontanum_hyperplasia":{"name":"Verumontanum mucosal gland hyperplasia","system":"Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["verumontanum mucosal gland hyperplasia","verumontanum hyperplasia","VMGH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / WHO Classification of Tumours: Urinary and Male Genital Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign proliferation of small glands in and around the verumontanum, important chiefly as a histological mimic of low-grade prostatic adenocarcinoma","It is usually an incidental finding on needle biopsy, transurethral resection specimens or at autopsy, without specific symptoms","It is characterised by small crowded acini that retain a basal cell layer, which distinguishes it from carcinoma","It is commonly associated with corpora amylacea and lacks the infiltrative growth, nuclear enlargement and prominent nucleoli of cancer","It sits among the benign entities that can mimic malignancy in the prostate and posterior urethra, underlining the importance of careful histological interpretation when PSA is raised","Immunohistochemistry for basal cell markers confirms the benign nature","No treatment is required once malignancy has been excluded"],"pathophysiology":"Verumontanum mucosal gland hyperplasia is a benign hyperplastic proliferation of the small mucosal glands at the verumontanum in the posterior prostatic urethra. The closely packed small acini can architecturally resemble low-grade adenocarcinoma, but the retained basal cell layer, bland cytology and characteristic distinctive orange-brown luminal concretions mark the process as benign.","additionalDifferentials":["Low-grade prostatic adenocarcinoma","Atypical adenomatous hyperplasia (adenosis)","Atypical small acinar proliferation","Prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia","Basal cell hyperplasia","Nephrogenic adenoma of the prostatic urethra"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Histopathological assessment by an experienced uropathologist","Immunohistochemistry for basal cell markers (p63, high-molecular-weight cytokeratin) and AMACR","Correlation with the anatomical site around the verumontanum","PSA interpreted in clinical context","Multiparametric MRI if imaging correlation is needed","Repeat or targeted biopsy only if malignancy remains a genuine concern"],"redFlags":["Coexisting atypical or malignant foci on the same specimen","A persistently rising PSA prompting further evaluation","Diagnostic uncertainty requiring specialist pathology review","Discordant MRI findings suggesting cancer"],"prognosis":"Excellent; the lesion is entirely benign with no malignant potential, and its principal significance lies in avoiding a mistaken diagnosis of carcinoma.","pitfalls":["Overdiagnosing carcinoma on small crowded benign glands and prompting unnecessary treatment","Failing to use basal cell immunostains to confirm benignity","Attributing PSA elevation solely to this lesion without appropriate follow-up","Not seeking specialist review in equivocal cases"],"management":["Provide no disease-specific treatment, as the lesion is entirely benign with no malignant potential, and reassure the patient","Confirm the benign diagnosis with immunohistochemistry for retained basal cells using p63 and high-molecular-weight cytokeratin alongside AMACR","Ensure review by an experienced uropathologist to avoid a mistaken diagnosis of prostatic adenocarcinoma","Interpret prostate-specific antigen within the clinical context rather than attributing elevation to this lesion","Reserve multiparametric MRI and repeat or targeted biopsy for cases where malignancy remains a genuine concern","Continue routine urological care as clinically indicated, without specific surveillance for this entity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","WHO Classification of Tumours: Urinary and Male Genital Tumours"],"crossLinks":["prostate_intraepithelial_neoplasia","posterior_urethral_polyp"]},"very_long_chain_acyl_coa_dehydrogenase_deficiency":{"name":"Very long-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Fatty acid oxidation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VLCADD","VLCAD deficiency","ACADVL deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive defect in ACADVL, encoding the inner mitochondrial membrane enzyme that catalyses the first dehydrogenation step of long-chain (C14-C20) fatty-acid beta-oxidation.","Three overlapping phenotypes: a severe early-infantile form with hypertrophic or dilated cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia; a childhood hepatic form with hypoketotic hypoglycaemia; and a milder adolescent or adult myopathic form with exercise-induced rhabdomyolysis.","Newborn screening acylcarnitine profile shows a characteristic rise in tetradecenoylcarnitine (C14:1), often with a raised C14:1 to acetylcarnitine ratio.","Decompensation is precipitated by fasting, intercurrent illness, prolonged or unaccustomed exercise, and cold exposure.","Cardiac involvement is a key discriminator from the more distal long-chain defects and is the principal driver of early mortality.","Management rests on strict avoidance of fasting, a long-chain-fat-restricted diet enriched with medium-chain triglyceride that bypasses the enzymatic block, and a glucose-based emergency regimen during illness.","The anaplerotic triglyceride triheptanoin is an option for long-chain fatty-acid-oxidation disorders and may reduce major cardiac and myopathic events.","Between crises the patient may appear entirely well, yet the acylcarnitine abnormality and enzyme deficit persist."],"pathophysiology":"Very long-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase is anchored in the inner mitochondrial membrane and is the principal dehydrogenase initiating each turn of beta-oxidation for long-chain acyl-CoA esters. When ACADVL variants abolish or reduce its activity, flux through long-chain fatty-acid oxidation is blocked, so during fasting or metabolic stress, when fatty acids should become the dominant fuel, hepatic ketogenesis fails and the heart and skeletal muscle are deprived of energy, producing hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and tissue energy deficit. Long-chain acyl-CoA and acylcarnitine intermediates accumulate upstream of the block; these amphipathic species are arrhythmogenic and have a detergent-like myotoxic effect on membranes, underpinning the cardiomyopathy, conduction disturbance and rhabdomyolysis. The amount of residual enzyme activity broadly determines whether the disorder manifests as the lethal cardiac, intermediate hepatic, or milder myopathic phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase and generalised mitochondrial trifunctional protein deficiency","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase 2 deficiency","Carnitine-acylcarnitine translocase deficiency","Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","Multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (glutaric aciduria type II)","Pompe disease and other causes of infantile cardiomyopathy","Hepatic glycogen storage disorders presenting with fasting hypoglycaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma acylcarnitine profile demonstrating elevated tetradecenoylcarnitine (C14:1)","Urine organic acids (hypoketotic dicarboxylic aciduria during crisis, often unremarkable between episodes)","Molecular genetic testing of ACADVL","Enzyme activity assay or palmitate flux studies in cultured fibroblasts or leucocytes","Creatine kinase and urine myoglobin during myopathic episodes","Echocardiography and electrocardiography to assess cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia","Paired blood glucose and ketone measurement during intercurrent illness or a supervised fast"],"redFlags":["Hypoketotic hypoglycaemia with encephalopathy in an infant","New cardiomyopathy, pericardial effusion or malignant ventricular arrhythmia","Pigmenturia with markedly raised creatine kinase indicating rhabdomyolysis and risk of acute kidney injury","Sudden clinical deterioration or collapse during an intercurrent illness or prolonged fast"],"prognosis":"The early cardiac form historically carried high mortality, but newborn screening with early dietary intervention has substantially improved survival. The myopathic form is compatible with a normal lifespan, though recurrent exertional rhabdomyolysis tends to persist.","pitfalls":["Assuming a well appearance between crises excludes the diagnosis when the enzyme deficit and acylcarnitine signature persist","Administering long-chain lipid emulsion during acute illness, which aggravates the metabolic block","Omitting cardiac assessment in an infant who presents only with hypoglycaemia","Attributing adolescent exertional myalgia and rhabdomyolysis to a primary muscular dystrophy"],"management":["Strict avoidance of fasting with frequent carbohydrate-rich feeds and overnight slow-release cornstarch to maintain glucose supply and suppress lipolysis","Long-chain-fat-restricted diet supplemented with medium-chain triglyceride, which enters beta-oxidation distal to the enzymatic block, guided by a metabolic dietitian","Emergency glucose-based regimen with intravenous dextrose during intercurrent illness, prolonged exercise or reduced oral intake to avert hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and decompensation","Consider triheptanoin or L-carnitine only under specialist supervision, and avoid recognised triggers such as unaccustomed exercise and cold exposure","Cardiac surveillance with serial echocardiography and electrocardiography to detect the cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia that drive early mortality","Monitor for exertional rhabdomyolysis with creatine kinase and urine myoglobin, treating acute episodes with aggressive hydration and renal protection","Coordinate care through a metabolic medicine team, cardiologist and specialist dietitian","Genetic counselling and testing for family members given autosomal recessive inheritance, with newborn screening of subsequent siblings"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Inherited metabolic disease reference texts","Fatty acid oxidation disorder management consensus guidelines","Paediatric metabolic medicine references"],"crossLinks":[]},"vesicoureteral_reflux":{"name":"Vesicoureteral reflux","system":"Renal / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vesicoureteric reflux","VUR","reflux nephropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Retrograde flow of urine from the bladder into the ureter and sometimes the renal pelvis, which may be primary (congenital incompetence of the ureterovesical junction) or secondary to raised bladder pressure or outlet obstruction","Graded I to V on micturating cystourethrogram, from reflux into a non-dilated ureter (grade I) to gross dilatation and tortuosity with loss of papillary detail (grade V)","Often detected during evaluation of antenatal hydronephrosis or after a febrile urinary tract infection in a child","The main long-term concern is reflux nephropathy: renal scarring that can lead to hypertension, proteinuria, and chronic kidney disease, particularly when reflux and infection coexist","Lower grades frequently resolve spontaneously with growth as the intramural ureter lengthens","Bladder and bowel dysfunction strongly influences persistence, infection risk, and treatment success and should be actively addressed","Management options include surveillance, continuous antibiotic prophylaxis, and, for higher grades or breakthrough infections, ureteric reimplantation or endoscopic injection"],"pathophysiology":"The normal ureterovesical junction acts as a one-way flap valve because the intravesical ureter runs obliquely through the bladder wall and is compressed during filling and voiding. In primary reflux this valve is incompetent, usually because of a short intramural ureteric tunnel or a laterally placed ureteric orifice. Secondary reflux develops when abnormally high bladder pressures, as in outlet obstruction or neurogenic or dysfunctional bladders, overwhelm a marginally competent junction. Reflux of infected urine, and possibly of sterile urine at high pressure, delivers pathogens and pressure to the renal papillae, producing pyelonephritic inflammation and scarring known as reflux nephropathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Ureteropelvic junction obstruction causing hydronephrosis","Primary obstructive megaureter","Duplex collecting system with a refluxing lower moiety","Neurogenic or dysfunctional bladder","Posterior urethral valves in males","Simple recurrent lower urinary tract infection without reflux"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Micturating cystourethrogram (MCUG) to confirm and grade reflux","Renal and bladder ultrasound to assess dilatation, scarring, and bladder wall","DMSA scintigraphy to detect renal scarring and differential function","Assessment of bladder and bowel function, including a voiding diary","Urinalysis and culture during suspected infection","Blood pressure measurement and renal function tests during follow-up"],"redFlags":["Breakthrough febrile urinary tract infections despite prophylaxis","New or progressive renal scarring on serial imaging","Hypertension or proteinuria indicating reflux nephropathy","Bilateral high-grade reflux with declining renal function"],"prognosis":"Low-grade reflux commonly resolves spontaneously with an excellent prognosis, whereas high-grade bilateral reflux, especially with recurrent pyelonephritis and pre-existing dysplasia, carries a meaningful risk of scarring, hypertension, and chronic kidney disease requiring long-term monitoring.","pitfalls":["Attributing all renal damage to reflux when congenital dysplasia may account for much of it","Ignoring underlying bladder and bowel dysfunction, which undermines both medical and surgical treatment","Over-investigating or over-treating low-grade reflux likely to resolve spontaneously","Failing to monitor blood pressure and renal function in children with established scarring"],"management":["Active management of bladder and bowel dysfunction with a bowel-emptying regimen, timed voiding and treatment of constipation, as this drives reflux persistence and infection risk","Continuous low-dose antibiotic prophylaxis in selected infants and children with higher-grade reflux or recurrent febrile urinary tract infections to prevent pyelonephritis","Conservative watchful waiting for low-grade primary reflux, which commonly resolves spontaneously as the intramural ureter lengthens with growth","Prompt antibiotic treatment of febrile urinary tract infections with urine culture to guide therapy and limit renal scarring","Surgical correction by ureteric reimplantation or endoscopic subureteric bulking injection for high-grade reflux, breakthrough infections or new scarring","Long-term surveillance with renal-bladder ultrasound, DMSA scintigraphy for scarring, blood pressure monitoring and renal function testing","Refer to paediatric urology and nephrology, escalating when hypertension, proteinuria or declining renal function emerge"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Gearhart, Rink & Mouriquand: Pediatric Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vesicoureteric_reflux":{"name":"Vesicoureteric reflux","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VUR","vesicoureteral reflux","vesico-ureteric reflux"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Vesicoureteric reflux is the retrograde flow of urine from the bladder up the ureter toward the kidney, predisposing to recurrent pyelonephritis and renal scarring, mainly in children","Primary VUR results from a congenitally short or abnormal submucosal ureteric tunnel with an incompetent vesicoureteric junction, whereas secondary VUR arises from raised bladder pressure due to obstruction or a neurogenic or dysfunctional bladder","It commonly presents with febrile urinary tract infections in infancy or childhood, or is detected during investigation of antenatal hydronephrosis","Severity is graded I to V by the voiding cystourethrogram appearance, from reflux confined to a non-dilated ureter up to gross dilatation and tortuosity with a severely dilated collecting system","The principal concern is reflux nephropathy: renal scarring from recurrent infection combined with reflux, which can lead to hypertension, proteinuria, and chronic kidney disease","Voiding cystourethrography is the standard test to confirm and grade reflux, while DMSA scintigraphy detects renal scarring and pyelonephritis","Management principles emphasise prevention and prompt treatment of infection, attention to bladder and bowel dysfunction, and continuous antibiotic prophylaxis in selected children, since many low-grade cases resolve spontaneously with growth","Surgical or endoscopic correction (ureteric reimplantation or subureteric injection) is reserved for high-grade, persistent, or breakthrough-infection cases"],"pathophysiology":"Vesicoureteric reflux occurs when the valve-like mechanism of the vesicoureteric junction fails, allowing urine to reflux from the bladder into the ureter and renal pelvis. Normally an adequate length of submucosal ureter within the bladder wall is compressed during bladder filling and voiding, preventing reflux; in primary VUR this tunnel is congenitally short or laterally placed. Secondary VUR develops when high intravesical pressures from bladder outlet obstruction or neurogenic/dysfunctional voiding overcome the junction. Reflux of infected urine and elevated pressures transmitted to the kidney cause pyelonephritis and scarring (reflux nephropathy), which underlies later hypertension and renal impairment.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent urinary tract infection without reflux","Obstructive uropathy (posterior urethral valves, PUJ obstruction)","Neurogenic or dysfunctional bladder (bladder-bowel dysfunction)","Antenatal hydronephrosis of other cause","Duplex collecting system with ectopic ureter or ureterocele","Primary megaureter"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Voiding (micturating) cystourethrography to confirm and grade reflux","DMSA renal scintigraphy to detect scarring and acute pyelonephritis","Renal and bladder ultrasound to assess for dilatation and anatomy","Urinalysis and culture during febrile illness","Assessment of renal function and blood pressure","Evaluation of bladder and bowel function (uroflowmetry, voiding diary) in older children"],"redFlags":["Febrile urinary tract infection with an obstructed or refluxing system risking urosepsis, a urological emergency requiring prompt drainage and antibiotics","Progressive renal scarring or reduced differential renal function","New or worsening hypertension or proteinuria indicating reflux nephropathy","High-grade reflux with breakthrough infections despite prophylaxis","Bilateral high-grade reflux with impaired renal function"],"prognosis":"Many children with low-grade primary reflux resolve spontaneously as they grow and do well with infection prevention; high-grade reflux, recurrent pyelonephritis, and established scarring carry a greater risk of hypertension and chronic kidney disease, making surveillance important.","pitfalls":["Failing to investigate recurrent febrile UTIs in a child for underlying reflux","Overlooking bladder and bowel dysfunction, which perpetuates reflux and infection","Missing secondary reflux from obstruction such as posterior urethral valves","Neglecting long-term monitoring of blood pressure and renal function in children with scarring","Delaying treatment of a febrile infection in an obstructed, refluxing system"],"management":["Optimise bladder and bowel function first, treating constipation and dysfunctional voiding with timed voiding and a bowel regimen to reduce infection and aid resolution","Continuous antibiotic prophylaxis for infants and children with high-grade reflux or recurrent febrile urinary tract infections","Conservative observation with infection prevention for low-grade primary reflux, anticipating spontaneous resolution with growth","Treat febrile urinary tract infections promptly with culture-directed antibiotics to minimise reflux nephropathy","Endoscopic subureteric injection or open ureteric reimplantation for persistent high-grade reflux or breakthrough pyelonephritis despite prophylaxis","Regular surveillance with renal tract ultrasound, DMSA scanning for scarring, blood pressure and renal function assessment","Involve paediatric urology and nephrology, escalating if hypertension, proteinuria or chronic kidney disease develop"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vestibular_migraine":{"name":"vestibular migraine","system":"nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["migrainous vertigo","migraine-associated vertigo","migraine-associated dizziness"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bárány Society and International Headache Society diagnostic criteria for vestibular migraine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest cause of recurrent spontaneous vertigo and a frequently under-recognised diagnosis; it should be considered in any patient with episodic vestibular symptoms and a migraine history.","Vertigo need not be temporally linked to headache — episodes may occur with, before, after, or entirely without cephalgia, which is a common reason the diagnosis is missed.","Attack phenomenology is highly variable: spontaneous or positional vertigo, head-motion intolerance, or internal/external illusory movement, lasting anywhere from a few minutes to as long as a few days.","Diagnosis requires a current or past history of migraine plus migrainous features (migraine-type headache, photophobia, phonophobia, or visual aura) during at least half of the vestibular episodes.","Interictal examination is usually normal, although subtle central oculomotor signs such as gaze-evoked or positional nystagmus of central type may be present.","It overlaps and may coexist with Menière's disease and benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, and can evolve into persistent postural-perceptual dizziness.","Osmophobia, visual aura, and a family history of migraine strengthen the diagnosis; triggers mirror those of migraine (sleep disturbance, stress, hormonal fluctuation, dietary and sensory stimuli).","Management combines trigger avoidance and lifestyle measures with migraine prophylaxis (beta-blockers, tricyclic antidepressants, anticonvulsants, or calcium-channel blockers) rather than long-term vestibular sedatives."],"pathophysiology":"The mechanism is incompletely understood but is thought to arise from the same trigeminovascular and central sensory-processing dysfunction that underlies migraine, applied to vestibular pathways. Reciprocal anatomical connections link trigeminal nociceptive neurones with central vestibular nuclei, and neuropeptides such as calcitonin gene-related peptide modulate vestibular processing. Cortical spreading depression, thalamocortical dysrhythmia, and transient dysfunction of central multisensory integration are all implicated, producing episodic mismatch between vestibular, visual, and proprioceptive signals. A strong genetic predisposition exists, and channelopathy genes (notably CACNA1A, shared with familial hemiplegic migraine and episodic ataxia type 2) illustrate the mechanistic overlap between migraine and paroxysmal vestibular disorders.","additionalDifferentials":["Menière's disease","benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","vestibular paroxysmia","episodic ataxia type 2","posterior circulation transient ischaemic attack or stroke","persistent postural-perceptual dizziness","panic disorder with vestibular symptoms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["pure-tone audiometry to exclude the fluctuating low-frequency loss of Menière's disease","video head impulse testing and caloric testing (typically normal or non-lateralising interictally)","cervical and ocular vestibular evoked myogenic potentials","MRI of the brain and internal auditory meati to exclude structural or central pathology","orthostatic blood pressure measurement to exclude an orthostatic contribution","structured headache and vertigo diary to demonstrate the temporal relationship of migrainous features"],"redFlags":["acute severe vertigo accompanied by brainstem signs (dysarthria, diplopia, limb ataxia, sensory loss) suggesting posterior circulation stroke","progressive unilateral sensorineural hearing loss or persistent unilateral tinnitus suggesting retrocochlear pathology or Menière's disease","first presentation of vestibular symptoms in an older patient with vascular risk factors","new persistent headache with focal neurological deficit or papilloedema","sudden thunderclap headache with vertigo"],"prognosis":"Vestibular migraine typically follows a chronic relapsing-remitting course that fluctuates over years and often improves with prophylaxis and lifestyle modification. A proportion of patients develop chronic dizziness or secondary persistent postural-perceptual dizziness if untreated.","pitfalls":["attributing all episodic vertigo to migraine without excluding Menière's disease or benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","expecting headache to accompany every attack and therefore rejecting the diagnosis when vertigo occurs in isolation","over-relying on normal interictal vestibular tests, which do not exclude the diagnosis","missing a coexisting benign paroxysmal positional vertigo that is independently treatable","long-term use of vestibular suppressants, which impair central compensation and may perpetuate symptoms"],"management":["Trigger identification and lifestyle modification with regular sleep, hydration, consistent meals and reduction of dietary and stress triggers as first-line preventive management","Migraine preventive pharmacotherapy such as beta-blockers, amitriptyline, topiramate or flunarizine for frequent or disabling attacks","Acute attack treatment with triptans and antiemetics, using vestibular suppressants only briefly to avoid impairing central compensation","Vestibular rehabilitation exercises for persistent imbalance and motion sensitivity","Maintain a structured headache and vertigo diary to track attacks, triggers and treatment response","Address comorbid anxiety and coexisting Menière's disease or benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Refer to neuro-otology and escalate if the course is atypical or progresses toward persistent postural-perceptual dizziness"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bárány Society and International Headache Society: diagnostic criteria for vestibular migraine (Lempert et al)","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology","Brandt and Dieterich, Vertigo and Dizziness: Common Complaints"],"crossLinks":[]},"vestibular_neuritis":{"name":"Vestibular neuritis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vestibular neuronitis","acute peripheral vestibulopathy","acute vestibular neuritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as acute, prolonged, spontaneous vertigo lasting days, with nausea, vomiting, and gait imbalance but without hearing loss","When hearing is also affected the term labyrinthitis is used, implying inflammation of the whole labyrinth rather than the vestibular nerve alone","The spontaneous nystagmus is horizontal-torsional, beats away from the affected ear, and is suppressed by visual fixation, consistent with a peripheral lesion","The head impulse test shows a corrective saccade toward the affected side, indicating a peripheral vestibular deficit","The HINTS examination (head impulse, nystagmus direction, test of skew) is more sensitive than early MRI for distinguishing peripheral neuritis from a posterior circulation stroke","Central features such as direction-changing nystagmus, skew deviation, or a normal head impulse test in an acutely vertiginous patient should raise concern for stroke","Management centres on short-term symptom relief and early vestibular rehabilitation to promote central compensation, avoiding prolonged vestibular suppressants","A thought to be post-viral or inflammatory process, it usually follows a monophasic course with gradual recovery"],"pathophysiology":"Vestibular neuritis is attributed to inflammation of the vestibular nerve, possibly from reactivation of latent herpes simplex virus in the vestibular ganglion, causing acute unilateral loss of peripheral vestibular input. The superior vestibular division is most often affected. The sudden asymmetry between the two vestibular systems generates a strong sense of rotational vertigo and spontaneous nystagmus. Over subsequent days to weeks, central adaptive mechanisms recalibrate the vestibular imbalance (central compensation), which underlies clinical recovery even if peripheral function does not fully return.","additionalDifferentials":["Posterior circulation ischaemic stroke (cerebellar or brainstem)","Labyrinthitis (with hearing loss)","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Meniere disease","Vestibular migraine","Multiple sclerosis affecting brainstem vestibular pathways"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bedside HINTS examination in acute continuous vertigo to separate peripheral from central causes","MRI with attention to the posterior fossa when central features or vascular risk are present","Audiometry to detect associated hearing loss suggesting labyrinthitis or other cause","Caloric testing or video head impulse testing to quantify vestibular function","Assessment of vascular risk factors when stroke is a consideration"],"redFlags":["Central signs (direction-changing nystagmus, skew deviation, normal head impulse test) suggesting stroke rather than neuritis","Acute vertigo with other focal neurological deficits such as dysarthria, limb ataxia, or weakness","New severe headache or neck pain raising concern for vertebral artery dissection","Sudden hearing loss accompanying vertigo, which may indicate a vascular or other serious cause"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover over days to weeks as central compensation develops, though some have persistent unsteadiness or motion sensitivity; early vestibular rehabilitation improves recovery and reduces chronic dizziness.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing a cerebellar stroke as vestibular neuritis by relying on imaging that can be falsely reassuring early","Prescribing prolonged vestibular suppressants, which impair central compensation","Assuming any acute vertigo is peripheral without performing the HINTS examination","Overlooking associated hearing loss that changes the differential","Delaying vestibular rehabilitation and prolonging disability"],"management":["Short-term vestibular sedatives and antiemetics such as antihistamines and prochlorperazine for severe acute vertigo and vomiting, discontinued early to avoid delaying central compensation","A short tapering course of corticosteroids in the acute phase, which may improve recovery of peripheral vestibular function","Early vestibular rehabilitation exercises to promote central compensation and reduce chronic unsteadiness","Rehydration and antiemetic support for patients unable to tolerate oral intake","Exclude posterior circulation stroke using the HINTS examination and arrange MRI when central features or vascular risk are present","Address vascular risk factors and arrange neurology or neuro-otology review for incomplete recovery or diagnostic doubt"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"vestibular_paroxysmia":{"name":"vestibular paroxysmia","system":"nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neurovascular cross-compression of the eighth nerve","disabling positional vertigo","microvascular compression syndrome of cranial nerve VIII"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bárány Society diagnostic criteria for vestibular paroxysmia","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The hallmark is short, recurrent, stereotyped spells of spinning or non-spinning vertigo lasting seconds to under a minute, which may occur many times a day.","It is attributed to pulsatile neurovascular cross-compression of the vestibulocochlear nerve, mechanistically analogous to trigeminal neuralgia and hemifacial spasm.","Attacks may arise spontaneously or be provoked by particular head positions or by hyperventilation, and may be accompanied by transient unilateral tinnitus or a change in hearing.","A clear response to low doses of a sodium-channel-blocking anticonvulsant (such as carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine) supports the diagnosis and is one of the formal diagnostic criteria.","High-resolution heavily T2-weighted MRI (CISS or FIESTA) may show a vessel, most often the anterior inferior cerebellar artery, looping onto the nerve, but such contact is frequent in asymptomatic people, so imaging is supportive rather than diagnostic.","Between attacks the examination is usually normal, although some patients develop a mild progressive vestibular or cochlear deficit over time.","Hyperventilation-induced nystagmus may be demonstrable and is a useful bedside clue.","The condition must be distinguished from benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, vestibular migraine, Menière's disease, and panic attacks before treatment is committed."],"pathophysiology":"Vestibular paroxysmia is caused by pulsatile compression of the vestibulocochlear nerve by an adjacent artery — most often the anterior inferior cerebellar artery — at or near the root entry zone, where central oligodendrocyte myelin transitions to peripheral Schwann-cell myelin and the nerve is especially vulnerable. Chronic mechanical irritation produces focal demyelination, which permits ephaptic (cross-talk) transmission between axons and generates paroxysmal ectopic and self-sustaining discharges. These brief bursts of abnormal afferent activity are perceived as short, stereotyped attacks of vertigo, exactly analogous to the paroxysms of trigeminal neuralgia and hemifacial spasm arising from compression of neighbouring cranial nerves. Membrane-stabilising, sodium-channel-blocking drugs suppress this ectopic firing, which accounts for their characteristic therapeutic effect.","additionalDifferentials":["benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","vestibular migraine","Menière's disease","episodic ataxia type 2","posterior circulation transient ischaemic attack","superior semicircular canal dehiscence or perilymph fistula","panic attacks with vestibular symptoms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["high-resolution heavily T2-weighted MRI (CISS/FIESTA) with MR angiography of the cerebellopontine angle","pure-tone audiometry to detect an associated cochlear deficit or exclude other causes","video head impulse testing, caloric testing, and vestibular evoked myogenic potentials","bedside hyperventilation testing for provoked nystagmus","positional testing to exclude benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","a monitored therapeutic trial of a sodium-channel-blocking anticonvulsant"],"redFlags":["progressive unilateral sensorineural hearing loss suggesting a vestibular schwannoma or other cerebellopontine angle tumour","facial weakness, facial numbness, or additional cranial neuropathies","signs of brainstem ischaemia such as dysarthria, diplopia, or limb ataxia","papilloedema or other features of raised intracranial pressure","failure to respond to an adequate trial of a sodium-channel-blocking drug, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The disorder usually responds well to sodium-channel-blocking anticonvulsants, often at low doses, with a marked reduction in attack frequency; medically refractory cases may be considered for microvascular decompression, though some patients show slow progression of vestibulocochlear deficit.","pitfalls":["over-diagnosing the condition on the basis of incidental neurovascular contact seen on MRI in an asymptomatic person","confusing brief positional attacks with benign paroxysmal positional vertigo or with vestibular migraine","treating a therapeutic response as definitive proof, since other paroxysmal disorders may also respond","neglecting to exclude a cerebellopontine angle tumour before attributing symptoms to vascular compression","escalating medication without re-examining the diagnosis when attacks fail to remit"],"management":["Low-dose sodium-channel-blocking anticonvulsants such as carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine as first-line therapy, which typically abolish or markedly reduce attacks","Alternative membrane-stabilising agents such as lamotrigine, phenytoin or gabapentin when carbamazepine is not tolerated","Monitor anticonvulsant tolerability and adverse effects, including hyponatraemia with carbamazepine and oxcarbazepine","Microvascular decompression of the vestibulocochlear nerve for medically refractory or drug-intolerant cases","Serial audiometry and vestibular function testing to detect progressive cochleovestibular deficit over time","Refer to neuro-otology for diagnostic confirmation, including a monitored therapeutic drug trial and high-resolution imaging"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bárány Society: diagnostic criteria for vestibular paroxysmia (Strupp et al)","Brandt and Dieterich, Vertigo and Dizziness: Common Complaints","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vestibular_schwannoma":{"name":"Vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma)","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Head and Neck","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acoustic neuroma","acoustic schwannoma","cerebellopontine angle schwannoma","eighth nerve schwannoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Benign Schwann-cell tumour arising most often from the vestibular division of the eighth cranial nerve within the internal auditory meatus","Classically presents with unilateral or asymmetric sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus, sometimes with imbalance rather than true rotatory vertigo","Any unexplained asymmetric sensorineural loss or unilateral tinnitus warrants imaging to exclude a retrocochlear lesion","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas are pathognomonic of neurofibromatosis type 2 and prompt genetic evaluation, especially in younger patients","Gadolinium-enhanced MRI of the internal auditory meatus is the gold-standard diagnostic test; enhancing tumour may expand into the cerebellopontine angle","Facial nerve function is typically preserved until tumours are large, whereas early facial weakness should raise suspicion of an alternative pathology such as a facial nerve schwannoma","Large tumours can cause trigeminal sensory loss, cerebellar signs, brainstem compression and hydrocephalus","Management options include surveillance imaging, microsurgical resection, or stereotactic radiosurgery, chosen by tumour size, growth, hearing and patient factors"],"pathophysiology":"Vestibular schwannomas are slow-growing benign neoplasms of Schwann cells, usually originating at the transition zone of the vestibular nerve near the internal auditory meatus. Growth within the confined meatus compresses adjacent auditory and vestibular fibres and their blood supply, producing progressive sensorineural hearing loss and vestibular symptoms. As the tumour enlarges it extends medially into the cerebellopontine angle, where it may compress the trigeminal and facial nerves, the cerebellum and eventually the brainstem, and obstruct cerebrospinal fluid flow. Most are sporadic and unilateral; biallelic inactivation of the NF2 tumour-suppressor gene underlies both sporadic tumours and the bilateral tumours of neurofibromatosis type 2.","additionalDifferentials":["Meningioma of the cerebellopontine angle","Facial nerve schwannoma","Epidermoid or arachnoid cyst of the cerebellopontine angle","Meniere disease","Sudden or idiopathic asymmetric sensorineural hearing loss","Metastasis or other skull-base tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gadolinium-enhanced MRI of the internal auditory meatus and cerebellopontine angle","Pure-tone and speech audiometry documenting asymmetric sensorineural loss and speech discrimination","Auditory brainstem response testing where MRI is contraindicated","Vestibular function testing (caloric or video head impulse) to assess the affected nerve","Genetic assessment for NF2 when tumours are bilateral or the patient is young"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive symptoms, severe headache or reduced consciousness suggesting brainstem compression or hydrocephalus","Cerebellar signs such as ataxia, dysmetria or nystagmus indicating a large cerebellopontine angle mass","Trigeminal sensory loss or facial weakness signalling significant tumour extension","Bilateral tumours or a family history indicating neurofibromatosis type 2"],"prognosis":"As benign tumours, vestibular schwannomas carry an excellent survival prognosis, and many small tumours remain stable under surveillance. Hearing preservation depends on tumour size and treatment, and both microsurgery and radiosurgery carry a risk to hearing and facial nerve function that rises with larger tumours; large untreated lesions can be life-threatening through brainstem compression.","pitfalls":["Attributing asymmetric hearing loss or unilateral tinnitus to presbycusis or noise exposure without imaging","Assuming vertigo excludes the diagnosis, since imbalance is often subtle owing to gradual compensation","Failing to consider neurofibromatosis type 2 in young or bilateral cases","Confusing a facial nerve schwannoma with a vestibular schwannoma when facial weakness appears early"],"management":["Active surveillance with serial gadolinium-enhanced MRI for small or asymptomatic tumours, many of which remain stable","Stereotactic radiosurgery for small-to-moderate or growing tumours to arrest growth while attempting hearing and facial nerve preservation","Microsurgical resection for large tumours or those causing brainstem compression, with intraoperative facial nerve monitoring","Hearing rehabilitation with aids or contralateral-routing devices, plus vestibular rehabilitation and tinnitus support","Urgent neurosurgical management of brainstem compression or obstructive hydrocephalus in large cerebellopontine-angle tumours","Manage through a skull-base multidisciplinary team of neurosurgery, otolaryngology and neuro-otology","Genetic assessment for neurofibromatosis type 2 when tumours are bilateral or the patient is young, with family screening and consideration of bevacizumab for progressive NF2-related disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"vexas_syndrome":{"name":"VEXAS syndrome","system":"Rheumatology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Vacuoles, E1 enzyme, X-linked, Autoinflammatory, Somatic syndrome","UBA1-mutated autoinflammatory syndrome","somatic UBA1 syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Almost exclusively affects men over 50 years, reflecting an acquired somatic mutation on the X chromosome","Presents as treatment-refractory systemic inflammation with recurrent fevers, neutrophilic dermatosis, chondritis of the ears or nose, and inflammatory ocular disease","Frequently overlaps clinically with relapsing polychondritis, Sweet syndrome, polyarteritis nodosa, or giant cell arteritis, but responds poorly to conventional treatment","Macrocytic anaemia and progressive cytopenias are near-universal, and there is a strong association with myelodysplastic syndrome and plasma cell dyscrasias","Characteristic cytoplasmic vacuoles are seen in myeloid and erythroid precursors on bone marrow aspirate","Diagnosis is confirmed by demonstrating a somatic UBA1 mutation (typically affecting methionine-41) in haematopoietic cells","Venous and arterial thromboembolism is common and adds substantially to morbidity","Inflammation is often corticosteroid-dependent, with high relapse rates on tapering"],"pathophysiology":"VEXAS syndrome arises from an acquired somatic mutation in the UBA1 gene within haematopoietic stem cells. UBA1 encodes the principal E1 enzyme that initiates cellular ubiquitylation. The mutation, usually at the p.Met41 site, impairs production of the cytoplasmic UBA1 isoform, disrupting ubiquitin-dependent protein turnover and triggering unchecked activation of innate immune and inflammatory pathways. The affected clone expands within the myeloid lineage, producing the diagnostic cytoplasmic vacuoles in myeloid and erythroid precursors and driving both the systemic autoinflammatory phenotype and a predisposition to myelodysplasia.","additionalDifferentials":["Relapsing polychondritis","Adult-onset Still disease","Sweet syndrome (acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis)","Polyarteritis nodosa and other systemic vasculitides","Giant cell arteritis","Myelodysplastic syndrome with autoimmune features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted or next-generation sequencing of UBA1 in peripheral blood or bone marrow to detect the somatic mutation","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine looking for cytoplasmic vacuoles in myeloid/erythroid precursors and features of myelodysplasia","Full blood count and blood film demonstrating macrocytic anaemia and evolving cytopenias","Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP, ferritin) which are typically markedly elevated","Skin or cartilage biopsy showing neutrophilic infiltration or chondritis","Cross-sectional imaging and vascular studies to assess thromboembolism and organ involvement"],"redFlags":["Progressive cytopenias or transfusion dependence signalling evolution to myelodysplastic syndrome","Refractory systemic inflammation despite escalating immunosuppression","Venous or arterial thromboembolic events","Weight loss and constitutional decline suggesting an underlying haematological malignancy"],"prognosis":"VEXAS syndrome is a serious, progressive disorder with substantial mortality driven by uncontrolled inflammation, thromboembolism, and haematological progression to myelodysplasia. Many patients remain corticosteroid-dependent; allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is the only potentially curative option, while hypomethylating agents such as azacitidine and JAK inhibition are used to control disease.","pitfalls":["Attributing refractory relapsing polychondritis or Sweet syndrome in an older man to the classic diagnosis alone and missing the underlying UBA1 clone","Overlooking macrocytosis and early cytopenias as clues to a marrow-based process","Failing to send UBA1 sequencing because the phenotype mimics a well-known rheumatological disease","Under-recognising the high thrombotic risk and not considering prophylaxis","Escalating immunosuppression without haematology involvement despite marrow dysplasia"],"management":["Corticosteroids to control systemic autoinflammation, though most patients become corticosteroid-dependent","Steroid-sparing agents such as JAK inhibitors including ruxolitinib or IL-6 inhibition with tocilizumab, and azacitidine where there is associated myelodysplasia","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the only potentially curative option in suitable patients","Supportive care with red cell transfusion for macrocytic anaemia and thromboprophylaxis given the high venous thromboembolic risk","Regular monitoring of the full blood count and bone marrow for evolving cytopenias and progression to myelodysplastic syndrome","Prompt treatment of acute inflammatory flares and thromboembolic complications","Coordinated rheumatology and haematology care given the overlap of autoinflammation with clonal marrow disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"vibrio_vulnificus_infection":{"name":"Vibrio vulnificus infection","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Vibrio vulnificus","halophilic vibrio sepsis","vibrio septicaemia","necrotising vibrio soft-tissue infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Mandell, Douglas and Bennett","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A halophilic, curved Gram-negative bacillus found in warm coastal and estuarine seawater, especially during warmer months","Two classic presentations: fulminant primary septicaemia after eating raw or undercooked shellfish (particularly oysters), and rapidly progressive wound infection after seawater or shellfish exposure","Hosts with chronic liver disease, haemochromatosis or other iron-overload states, and immunosuppression are at greatly increased risk of overwhelming disease","Characteristic haemorrhagic bullae and rapidly spreading cellulitis progressing to necrotising fasciitis are hallmark cutaneous findings","Empirical therapy pairs a tetracycline such as doxycycline with a third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftazidime, alongside aggressive resuscitation","Early and repeated surgical debridement or fasciotomy is essential when soft-tissue necrosis is present","Mortality is high in septicaemic disease, so treatment must not await culture confirmation when the exposure history and clinical picture fit"],"pathophysiology":"Vibrio vulnificus is a salt-requiring marine bacterium that causes disease when ingested in contaminated shellfish or when it enters through a break in the skin exposed to seawater. It expresses a polysaccharide capsule that resists phagocytosis and complement, together with cytolysins, proteases and other exotoxins that damage tissue and increase vascular permeability. Free iron promotes rapid bacterial growth, which is why individuals with cirrhosis or iron-overload states are so susceptible to fulminant bloodstream invasion. The resulting endotoxaemia and toxin release produce septic shock, and local toxin activity generates the characteristic haemorrhagic bullae and necrotising soft-tissue destruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Group A streptococcal necrotising fasciitis","Clostridial myonecrosis (gas gangrene)","Aeromonas wound infection and sepsis","Other marine Vibrio species infections including Vibrio alginolyticus","Ecthyma gangrenosum from Pseudomonas septicaemia","Meningococcaemia with haemorrhagic skin lesions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures, which frequently yield the organism in septicaemic disease","Culture of aspirate or tissue from bullae and wounds, ideally on selective media such as TCBS agar","Gram stain of blister fluid or wound exudate showing curved Gram-negative bacilli","Assessment of liver function and ferritin/iron studies to identify predisposing states","Full blood count, lactate, renal function and coagulation profile to gauge severity of sepsis","Cross-sectional imaging of the affected limb to define the extent of deep soft-tissue involvement"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging haemorrhagic bullae with severe pain out of proportion to skin findings","Hypotension and signs of septic shock following recent shellfish ingestion or seawater exposure","Known cirrhosis or iron-overload state in a patient with a marine exposure and systemic illness","Clinical or imaging evidence of necrotising fasciitis requiring emergency surgery"],"prognosis":"Wound infections treated early with appropriate antibiotics and debridement often recover, but primary septicaemia carries very high mortality, particularly in those with liver disease, and outcome depends heavily on rapid antibiotic and surgical intervention.","pitfalls":["Attributing early leg pain and fever to simple cellulitis and delaying surgical assessment","Failing to elicit the marine or shellfish exposure history in a septic patient","Overlooking underlying cirrhosis or haemochromatosis that dramatically raises risk","Delaying combination antibiotics while awaiting microbiological confirmation"],"management":["Prompt empirical antibiotics combining a tetracycline such as doxycycline with a third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftazidime, a fluoroquinolone being an alternative","Early and repeated surgical debridement or fasciotomy when soft-tissue necrosis or necrotising fasciitis is present","Aggressive fluid resuscitation and sepsis management, with vasopressor and organ support for septic shock","Intensive care support with monitoring of lactate, renal function and coagulation to gauge and manage sepsis severity","Counsel high-risk hosts with chronic liver disease or iron-overload states to avoid raw shellfish and seawater exposure of wounds","Involve infectious diseases, surgery and critical care early given the fulminant course and high mortality"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","Manson's Tropical Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"vici_syndrome":{"name":"Vici syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["immunodeficiency with cleft lip/palate, cataract, hypopigmentation and absent corpus callosum","congenital disorder of autophagy","EPG5-related disorder","EPG5"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe autosomal recessive multisystem disorder and a prototypical congenital disorder of autophagy caused by biallelic variants in EPG5","The classic pentad is agenesis of the corpus callosum, cataract, cardiomyopathy, combined immunodeficiency, and cutaneous hypopigmentation","Profound developmental delay, progressive microcephaly, and failure to thrive are almost universal","Recurrent infections reflect a combined immunodeficiency affecting both cellular and humoral arms","A progressive cardiomyopathy (often dilated) is a major cause of morbidity and death","Skeletal or cardiac muscle biopsy may show a vacuolar myopathy with abnormal autophagic vacuoles","Sensorineural hearing loss and seizures are common additional features","Care is entirely supportive, addressing infections, cardiac function, feeding, and seizures, with genetic counselling for families"],"pathophysiology":"Vici syndrome is caused by loss-of-function variants in EPG5, which encodes a key regulator of the late steps of autophagy, particularly the maturation of autophagosomes and their fusion with lysosomes. Defective autophagic clearance leads to accumulation of dysfunctional proteins and organelles within cells that are highly dependent on autophagy, including neurons, cardiomyocytes, skeletal myocytes, immune cells, and melanocytes. This explains the multisystem phenotype: impaired neurodevelopment with callosal agenesis, cardiomyopathy, a vacuolar myopathy, combined immunodeficiency, and defective melanin handling producing hypopigmentation. The disorder illustrates how disruption of a fundamental cellular degradation pathway produces widespread, progressive tissue dysfunction.","additionalDifferentials":["Other congenital disorders of autophagy and lysosomal disorders","Chediak-Higashi and Griscelli syndromes (hypopigmentation with immune defects)","Mitochondrial disease with cardiomyopathy and neurodegeneration","Congenital muscular dystrophies with brain involvement","Primary combined immunodeficiencies with syndromic features","Other causes of corpus callosum agenesis with multisystem disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to demonstrate agenesis of the corpus callosum and other malformations","Echocardiography and cardiac monitoring for cardiomyopathy","Immunological workup including lymphocyte subsets and immunoglobulins","Ophthalmological assessment for cataract","Muscle biopsy where a vacuolar myopathy is suspected","EPG5 molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Progressive heart failure from deteriorating cardiomyopathy","Overwhelming or opportunistic infection due to combined immunodeficiency","Respiratory failure or aspiration from neuromuscular weakness and feeding difficulty","Refractory seizures and neurological deterioration"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is poor, with most affected children dying in early childhood from cardiac failure or infection; survival beyond a few years is uncommon.","pitfalls":["Attributing recurrent infection to isolated immunodeficiency without recognising the syndromic context","Overlooking evolving cardiomyopathy in a child labelled with a static encephalopathy","Assuming hypopigmentation is incidental rather than part of the phenotype","Delaying genetic diagnosis and counselling for at-risk families"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so care is supportive and directed at the multisystem manifestations of this congenital disorder of autophagy","Heart failure management of the progressive cardiomyopathy with diuretics, ACE inhibitors and other standard anti-failure therapy","Protection against the combined immunodeficiency with prophylactic antimicrobials, immunoglobulin replacement and prompt aggressive treatment of infections","Nutritional support for failure to thrive, often requiring nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding","Anticonvulsants for seizures, developmental and neurodisability support, and surgical management of cataract","Coordinated multidisciplinary and palliative care, with early discussion of goals given the poor prognosis","Genetic counselling and family screening for this autosomal recessive EPG5 disorder, with prenatal testing offered in future pregnancies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"vipoma":{"name":"VIPoma","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vasoactive intestinal peptide-secreting tumour","Verner-Morrison syndrome","WDHA syndrome","pancreatic cholera"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour secreting vasoactive intestinal peptide, producing the syndrome of watery diarrhoea, hypokalaemia, and achlorhydria","The diarrhoea is large-volume and secretory, meaning it persists during fasting, unlike osmotic diarrhoea","Profound potassium loss in stool causes hypokalaemia with weakness, and dehydration can be severe","VIP also tends to reduce gastric acid secretion (achlorhydria or hypochlorhydria) and may cause flushing and hypercalcaemia","Diagnosis relies on demonstrating a markedly elevated fasting plasma VIP level in the setting of secretory diarrhoea","Most tumours are located in the pancreas and a substantial proportion are malignant with metastases at diagnosis, so cross-sectional and functional imaging are needed for localisation and staging","Consider an association with multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 in appropriate clinical contexts","Initial management prioritises aggressive rehydration and correction of electrolyte disturbance, with somatostatin analogues to control secretion and surgery for resectable disease"],"pathophysiology":"A VIPoma is a functioning neuroendocrine tumour, usually arising in the pancreatic islets, that secretes excessive vasoactive intestinal peptide. VIP stimulates intestinal secretion of water and electrolytes while inhibiting absorption, producing a high-volume secretory diarrhoea that continues despite fasting. The consequent losses of potassium and bicarbonate in stool lead to hypokalaemia and metabolic acidosis, and VIP's inhibition of gastric acid secretion causes achlorhydria. VIP can also promote vasodilation and flushing and, through its effects on bone and calcium handling, contribute to hypercalcaemia. A significant proportion of these tumours are malignant and may have metastasised, most often to the liver, by the time of diagnosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Other secretory diarrhoeas such as gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome)","Carcinoid syndrome","Chronic infective or inflammatory diarrhoea","Laxative abuse","Medullary thyroid carcinoma causing diarrhoea","Systemic mastocytosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting plasma VIP concentration","Serum electrolytes, bicarbonate, calcium, and glucose to characterise the metabolic disturbance","Stool studies to confirm a secretory pattern and exclude other causes","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) of the pancreas and liver for localisation and staging","Somatostatin-receptor functional imaging for occult or metastatic disease","Screening for other endocrine tumours where MEN1 is suspected"],"redFlags":["Severe dehydration and haemodynamic instability from massive fluid loss","Life-threatening hypokalaemia with cardiac dysrhythmia","Metastatic disease, most commonly hepatic, at presentation","Refractory diarrhoea unresponsive to initial supportive measures"],"prognosis":"Symptom control with rehydration and somatostatin analogues is usually achievable, and complete surgical resection of localised disease can be curative; however, many tumours are malignant with metastases at diagnosis, and prognosis then depends on tumour burden and response to systemic therapy.","pitfalls":["Attributing chronic watery diarrhoea to common causes and delaying measurement of VIP","Failing to recognise the secretory nature of the diarrhoea because it persists with fasting","Under-resuscitating profound fluid and potassium losses","Overlooking a possible MEN1 association and associated tumours"],"management":["Somatostatin analogues such as octreotide or lanreotide to suppress vasoactive intestinal peptide secretion and control the secretory diarrhoea","Aggressive rehydration and correction of hypokalaemia, bicarbonate loss and metabolic acidosis in the acute setting","Surgical resection of localised tumour, which can be curative, with debulking for symptom control in advanced disease","Systemic and liver-directed therapy for metastatic disease, including peptide receptor radionuclide therapy, targeted agents such as everolimus or sunitinib, and hepatic embolisation","Monitor plasma VIP, electrolytes and cross-sectional and somatostatin-receptor imaging for treatment response and recurrence","Manage within a neuroendocrine tumour multidisciplinary team","Screen for MEN1 and offer genetic counselling where a familial endocrine syndrome is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"viral_exanthems":{"name":"Viral Exanthems of Childhood","system":"Dermatological","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["viral rashes in children","childhood exanthems","exanthematous viral illness"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Viral exanthems are widespread skin eruptions accompanying systemic viral infections and are among the most common causes of rash with fever in children.","The classic childhood exanthems include measles, rubella, erythema infectiosum (parvovirus B19), roseola (human herpesvirus 6/7), and varicella, each with fairly characteristic patterns.","Diagnosis is often clinical, based on the morphology and distribution of the rash, its timing relative to fever, and associated features such as enanthems and prodromal symptoms.","Measles is distinguished by high fever with cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis, Koplik spots on the buccal mucosa, and a rash spreading from the head downward.","Roseola typically causes several days of high fever in a well-appearing infant, followed by a rash that appears as the fever resolves.","Erythema infectiosum produces the characteristic slapped-cheek facial appearance followed by a lacy, reticular rash on the limbs.","Most viral exanthems are self-limiting and managed supportively, but measles carries a notable risk of complications and remains vaccine-preventable.","Recognising non-viral mimics, especially meningococcaemia, scarlet fever, Kawasaki disease, and drug reactions, is essential because some require urgent specific treatment."],"pathophysiology":"In many viral exanthems the rash reflects the systemic phase of infection when virus disseminates through the bloodstream and, together with the host immune response, involves the skin. Some eruptions result from direct viral infection of skin cells (as in varicella, where the virus produces vesicular lesions), while others are largely immune-mediated, appearing as the immune response develops and sometimes coinciding with defervescence, as in roseola. The specific morphology, distribution, and timing are determined by the particular virus, its tropism, and the character of the immune reaction, which is why individual exanthems have recognisable patterns.","additionalDifferentials":["Meningococcaemia or other bacterial sepsis with petechiae or purpura","Scarlet fever","Kawasaki disease","Drug eruption or hypersensitivity reaction","Enteroviral exanthems","Other congenital or systemic infections with rash"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on rash pattern and associated features in most cases","Serology or PCR for specific viruses when confirmation is needed (e.g., measles, parvovirus)","Full blood count and inflammatory markers if a bacterial or inflammatory cause is considered","Public health notification and testing for notifiable infections such as measles","Investigations to exclude serious mimics when the child is unwell or the rash is atypical"],"redFlags":["Petechial or purpuric, non-blanching rash","Toxic or septic appearance","Signs suggesting Kawasaki disease (prolonged fever with mucocutaneous features)","Mucosal involvement, blistering, or skin sloughing suggesting a severe drug reaction","Features of measles complications such as pneumonia or encephalitis"],"prognosis":"The great majority of viral exanthems are benign and self-limiting with full recovery, though certain infections, notably measles and varicella in vulnerable hosts, and parvovirus in specific settings, can cause significant complications.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling a serious non-blanching or purpuric rash as a benign viral exanthem","Failing to consider Kawasaki disease in a child with prolonged fever and rash","Overlooking measles and its public health and complication implications","Attributing a drug hypersensitivity eruption to a viral cause"],"management":["Supportive care for most exanthems with antipyretics such as paracetamol, adequate fluids and antipruritic measures, as they are self-limiting","Antiviral aciclovir for varicella in neonates, immunocompromised or other vulnerable patients, and vitamin A supplementation during measles in at-risk children","Post-exposure prophylaxis with varicella-zoster or measles immunoglobulin, or vaccine, for susceptible vulnerable contacts","Monitor for complications such as measles pneumonia or encephalitis, varicella superinfection, and parvovirus-induced aplastic crisis or foetal risk in pregnancy","Public health notification and respiratory or contact isolation for notifiable infections such as measles and rubella","Promote routine MMR and varicella immunisation for prevention","Escalate and seek specialist input for immunocompromised or pregnant contacts and for the unwell or atypical child"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"viral_gastroenteritis":{"name":"Viral Gastroenteritis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Acute viral gastroenteritis","Norovirus gastroenteritis","Norwalk virus infection","Rotavirus gastroenteritis","Stomach flu"],"chapter":209,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1627","clinicalPearls":["Most viral gastroenteritis is caused by RNA viruses, but DNA enteric adenoviruses (types 40 and 41) are also recognized causes; the principal pathogens are rotavirus, norovirus, sapovirus, astrovirus, and enteric adenovirus.","Illness presents with acute onset of vomiting and/or diarrhea, often with fever, nausea, abdominal cramps, anorexia, and malaise; the diarrhea is characteristically watery and nonbloody, without mucus or fecal leukocytes.","Norovirus has become the leading cause of medically attended gastroenteritis in young children in the United States and other developed countries following the decline in severe rotavirus disease after routine rotavirus vaccination, and it affects all age groups.","Norovirus is responsible for roughly half of all reported gastroenteritis outbreaks in the United States and is the major cause of gastroenteritis epidemics worldwide; it also causes traveler's diarrhea and outbreaks among deployed military personnel.","The infectious dose for most viral agents is very low (about 10-100 viral particles), in contrast to the higher inoculum typically required for many bacterial pathogens; norovirus can spread by fecal-oral route, aerosolization of vomitus, contaminated fomites, and person-to-person contact.","Nearly all children worldwide are infected with rotavirus by 3-5 years of age; first symptomatic infection usually occurs after 3 months of age, disease incidence peaks at 4-23 months, and severity decreases with each repeat infection.","Protection against rotavirus correlates with virus-specific secretory IgA in the intestine (and to some degree serum); because intestinal IgA is short-lived, full protection is temporary, but successive reinfections build progressively greater immunity.","Susceptibility to norovirus is influenced by host genetics: histo-blood group antigen (HBGA) phenotype and secretor status affect attachment, and functional antibodies that block norovirus binding to HBGAs correlate with protective immunity.","Rotavirus antigenemia and viremia are common in acutely infected children, although serum antigen and RNA levels are far lower than stool levels; rotavirus is shed in very large quantities in stool (10^7-10^12 per gram).","In temperate climates viral gastroenteritis shows winter seasonality, whereas in tropical/year-round settings seasonal peaks are less pronounced; before vaccination, US rotavirus season began in the Southwest in autumn/early winter and migrated to peak in the Northeast in late winter/spring.","Fever is common with both rotavirus and norovirus but uncommon with the other viral agents; up to one-third of rotavirus patients may have a temperature above 39 degrees C.","Death is a rare outcome and usually results from severe dehydration in vulnerable hosts such as debilitated elderly patients and young children; acute gastroenteritis is a leading cause of childhood death in developing countries (roughly 0.5 million deaths annually) and a major cause of pediatric hospitalization (6-8%) in industrialized nations."],"pathophysiology":"Enteric viruses injure the small-intestinal mucosa to produce predominantly watery diarrhea. With human caliciviruses (Norwalk/norovirus), carbohydrates resembling human histo-blood group antigens on the gastroduodenal epithelium of secretor-phenotype individuals appear to serve as attachment ligands; infection causes reversible lesions of the upper jejunum with broadening and blunting of villi, microvillar shortening, epithelial vacuolization, crypt hyperplasia, and lamina propria infiltration by neutrophils and lymphocytes. These changes persist for at least 4 days after symptom resolution and produce malabsorption of carbohydrates and fats with reduced brush-border enzymes; adenylate cyclase activity is unchanged. No histopathologic change occurs in stomach or colon, but delayed gastric motor function is thought to drive the characteristic nausea and vomiting. Rotavirus infects and destroys mature enterocytes of the proximal small-intestinal villous epithelium; loss of absorptive cells plus proliferation of secretory crypt cells yields secretory diarrhea, while reduced brush-border enzymes leave unmetabolized disaccharides that cause osmotic diarrhea. The nonstructural rotavirus protein NSP4 acts as an enterotoxin altering epithelial permeability and function, and rotavirus may also stimulate fluid secretion via activation of the enteric nervous system.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial gastroenteritis (e.g., Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter, Vibrio cholerae, enterotoxigenic E. coli) - distinguished by inflammatory/bloody diarrhea, fecal leukocytes, and epidemiologic features","Preformed bacterial toxin food poisoning (Staphylococcus aureus, Bacillus cereus) - very short incubation of hours","Clostridioides difficile colitis","Protozoal/parasitic diarrhea (e.g., Giardia, Cryptosporidium)"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial enteritis (Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter jejuni, Vibrio, E. coli)","Staphylococcus aureus and Bacillus cereus preformed-toxin food poisoning","Clostridioides difficile colitis","Gastrointestinal symptoms of systemic respiratory viral infections (SARS-CoV, influenza A/H5N1, pandemic influenza A/H1N1)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Osmotic or secretory diarrhea from other causes","Carbohydrate malabsorption states","Other causes of acute vomiting without true gastroenteritis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Commercial enzyme immunoassays (EIA) for rotavirus and enteric adenovirus in stool","RT-PCR / multiplex molecular panels for norovirus and other enteric pathogens (increasingly used clinically)","Electron microscopy and polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (PAGE) for viral RNA - largely research/public-health tools","Fecal examination for leukocytes and blood to help separate viral (typically negative) from inflammatory bacterial causes","Stool culture on special media when bacterial pathogens are suspected"],"redFlags":["Signs of severe dehydration (especially in young children and debilitated elderly)","Inability to tolerate oral intake due to frequent vomiting","Bloody diarrhea or fecal leukocytes - should prompt evaluation for a bacterial/inflammatory cause rather than viral gastroenteritis","Protracted diarrhea with prolonged viral excretion or systemic dissemination in severely immunocompromised hosts (e.g., post-bone-marrow transplant rotavirus disease)"],"prognosis":"Viral gastroenteritis is generally self-limited. Human calicivirus illness has a sudden onset after a mean incubation of about 24 hours (range 12-72 h) and typically lasts 12-60 h; norovirus and sapovirus illness usually resolves in 1-3 days. Rotavirus illness begins abruptly after a 1-3 day incubation, with vomiting often preceding diarrhea, and gastrointestinal symptoms generally resolve in 3-7 days. Among healthy young adults the illness is rarely fatal but causes substantial morbidity and lost work time. The main serious complication is dehydration; death is uncommon and concentrated in the very young, the elderly, and those with debilitating conditions. Severely immunodeficient children may develop protracted rotavirus diarrhea with prolonged shedding and, rarely, systemic dissemination.","pitfalls":["Distinguishing viral from bacterial gastroenteritis on clinical and epidemiologic grounds alone is often unreliable; laboratory testing may be needed, and viral gastroenteritis is frequently a diagnosis of exclusion in practice.","Antibiotics and antimotility agents are inappropriate for viral gastroenteritis and are contraindicated.","Commercial stool EIA kits for norovirus have limited sensitivity and are most useful in outbreak investigation (many specimens tested) rather than for diagnosing individual patients.","Higher preexisting antibody to Norwalk virus does not guarantee protection - early studies paradoxically found higher antibody levels associated with greater susceptibility, later explained by host HBGA genetics rather than simple antibody titer.","Rotavirus has been associated with diverse conditions (SIDS, necrotizing enterocolitis, intussusception, Kawasaki disease, type 1 diabetes) and with respiratory/neurologic features, but no causal relationship has been confirmed for any of these.","Asymptomatically infected and pre-symptomatic persons can shed norovirus, and shedding can persist for weeks after recovery (prolonged in immunocompromised hosts), so a single negative or positive result must be interpreted with care."],"management":["Oral rehydration solution to correct and prevent dehydration is the mainstay, with intravenous fluids reserved for severe dehydration or failed oral intake","Early resumption of normal feeding and continued breastfeeding, with zinc supplementation in young children in resource-limited settings","Selective antiemetic such as ondansetron to facilitate oral rehydration in vomiting children, avoiding routine antidiarrhoeal and antibiotic use","Monitor hydration status and correct electrolyte disturbances arising from fluid losses","Infection control with strict hand hygiene, contact isolation and exclusion from work or school, particularly for norovirus outbreaks","Rotavirus immunisation of infants for prevention","Escalate for intravenous rehydration when dehydration is severe or oral intake cannot be maintained"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal grading scale; clinical severity is described per agent and ranges from subclinical/mild infection to severe gastroenteritis with life-threatening dehydration. Relative clinical severity by virus: group A rotavirus is the most severe (predominantly children under 5), norovirus is moderate (all ages), and sapovirus, astrovirus, and enteric adenovirus are generally milder (mainly children under 5).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1627","Harrison 22e p.1628","Harrison 22e p.1629","Harrison 22e p.1630","Harrison 22e p.1631","Harrison 22e p.1632"],"crossLinks":[]},"viral_hemorrhagic_fever":{"name":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ebola","Marburg","VHF"],"chapter":216,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1679","clinicalPearls":["VHF is an acute viral syndrome of severe disease and multisystem organ dysfunction sharing a common disruption of vascular function, stability, and integrity; overt hemorrhage is often NOT the most prominent or severe feature, so absence of bleeding does not exclude VHF (p.1674).","Travel to an endemic or active-outbreak area within the incubation period is a major diagnostic clue; rural exposures more often suggest VHF except for urban-host/vector agents (Seoul virus, dengue, yellow fever) (p.1675).","For filovirid (Ebola/Marburg) disease, the incubation period is 2-25 days (most commonly 7-10 days), beginning with a nonspecific influenza-like illness (sudden fever/chills, headache, myalgia, pharyngitis, arthralgia, maculopapular rash) before a second phase (~5-7 days on) with GI, respiratory, cardiovascular, and CNS involvement (p.1685).","Common filovirid hemorrhagic signs are subconjunctival injection, petechial rash, gingival bleeding, and bleeding at injection sites; frank epistaxis, hematemesis, hematuria, and melena occur but are LESS common, and severe blood loss is rare (except during/after childbirth) (pp.1684-1685).","Ebola/Marburg present with very similar clinical phenotypes that cannot be distinguished at the bedside and, for practical purposes, may be managed as the same disease (p.1685).","Early recognition is crucial: prompt isolation and hospitalization are indicated even in the absence of clinical signs, with rapid triage and ongoing vital-sign monitoring (p.1675).","Filovirids are highly infectious but not exceptionally contagious; human-to-human spread occurs by direct contact with an infected person or their body fluids/tissues, with NO evidence of aerosol or respiratory droplet transmission in natural outbreak settings (p.1679).","Persistence of filovirids in immune-privileged sites (most commonly semen of male survivors; rarely CNS, eye, placenta) can cause recrudescent disease and rare reignition of outbreaks; sexual abstinence or condom use is advised for male survivors until semen is shown to be filovirid-RNA free (pp.1686-1687)."],"pathophysiology":"Across VHFs, direct or indirect microvascular damage increases vascular permeability and (when platelet function is reduced) causes disruption and local hemorrhage, most evident in skin and mucous membranes; hypotension and shock reflect generalized circulatory dysfunction rather than life-threatening blood loss. Pathogenesis is incompletely understood and varies by virus, with direct vascular/parenchymal damage in some and soluble mediators dominating in others. For filovirids specifically, virions enter via skin lesions, mucosal surfaces, or parenteral inoculation; GP1,2 spikes engage cell-surface molecules and the intracellular receptor NPC1. First targets are macrophages, monocytes, and dendritic cells, and viral proteins (VP35, VP40, VP24) suppress innate immunity (e.g., type I interferon pathways), enabling high viral titers and widespread tissue dissemination. Hallmarks include profound immune dysregulation, massive lymphoid depletion with severe lymphopenia, coagulopathy (thrombocytopenia, elevated tissue factor, consumption of clotting factors, raised D-dimers, falling protein C; possible DIC, though controversial), hepatocyte necrosis with synthetic dysfunction contributing to coagulopathy, and endothelial dysfunction with increased vascular permeability leading to fluid redistribution, interstitial edema, and hypovolemic or septic shock (pp.1674-1675, 1683-1685).","additionalDifferentials":["Falciparum malaria (a most important mimic and a treatable, potentially fatal alternative)","Typhoid fever","Other treatable travel-associated febrile illnesses: shigellosis, leptospirosis, relapsing fever, rickettsial diseases","Other VHFs/high-consequence viral infections, in particular Lassa fever and yellow fever","Dengue and other viral hemorrhagic fevers in the bleeding/febrile traveler"],"infectionMimics":["Falciparum malaria","Typhoid fever","Enterohemorrhagic E. coli enteritis","Gram-negative septicemia (including shigellosis)","Meningococcal septicemia","Rickettsial infections","Fulminant viral hepatitis","Leptospirosis","Measles","Other high-consequence viral infections (Lassa fever, yellow fever)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Venomous snakebite","Warfarin (anticoagulant) intoxication","Acquired or inherited coagulopathies in the bleeding patient"],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR targeting one or more filovirid genes is the diagnostic assay of choice; in-field PCR platforms (e.g., Cepheid GeneXpert) are deployed during outbreaks","Acute-phase blood/serum is the preferred specimen as it usually contains high virion titers","Antigen-capture ELISA / immunochromatographic rapid diagnostic tests (currently limited; EBOV antigen RDT sensitivity restricts use largely to postmortem samples)","IgM- and IgG-capture ELISAs (including multiplex) for antibodies in later disease and in survivors","Virus isolation/plaque assays and electron microscopy require maximum-containment laboratories; in-field genome sequencing supports outbreak epidemiology","Supportive labs typically show leukopenia (then leukocytosis), thrombocytopenia, raised aminotransferases (AST > ALT) and GGT, raised creatinine/urea with proteinuria, electrolyte derangement, hypoglycemia, hypoalbuminemia, prolonged PT/PTT, elevated CPK, and markedly elevated CRP in severe illness","Hemoconcentration from vascular leakage is usually evident (most marked in HFRS and severe dengue)"],"redFlags":["Strict barrier nursing and infection-control isolation with appropriate PPE are mandatory for suspected VHF (except dengue, orthohantavirus, RVFV, or yellow fever); isolate and hospitalize even before clinical signs appear","Immediately notify infectious disease specialists and public health authorities if filovirid disease is suspected on epidemiologic or clinical grounds","Progressive multiorgan dysfunction: shock, acute kidney injury, CNS injury (encephalopathy, coma, seizures), respiratory failure, and severe hemorrhage signal critical illness","Exposure history raising suspicion: caves/mines, direct contact with bats, nonhuman primates, or bushmeat, contact with severely ill local residents, or patient-to-patient/healthcare-worker clusters in rural hospitals","Hemodynamic instability/hypotension and capillary leak with effusions and edema","Pregnancy and labor are associated with severe complications and poor outcomes (clotting-factor consumption, fetal loss, severe peripartum hemorrhage)"],"prognosis":"FVD is among the most severe acute human viral diseases and generally carries a poor prognosis, though with far more heterogeneity than historically assumed (the long-cited 70-90% case-fatality rate for EVD has required revision). Outcome likely depends on the specific filovirid, host factors (age, immune status, coinfections, host genetics), exposure route and dose, viral load, presence and severity of organ dysfunction, and critically the availability of filovirid-specific countermeasures and supportive/critical care. Even with effective EBOV-specific therapeutics, outcomes in severe EVD with high viral loads and complex organ dysfunction remain suboptimal. For other VHFs prognosis varies widely by agent (e.g., Argentinian/Bolivian hemorrhagic fevers can have case-fatality rates exceeding 50% in individual outbreaks) (pp.1675, 1685-1687).","pitfalls":["Filovirid/VHF disease cannot be diagnosed on clinical presentation alone; numerous more common febrile illnesses (especially malaria and typhoid) closely mimic it and are far more likely in non-outbreak settings, so do not anchor on VHF without epidemiologic context and laboratory confirmation.","Absence of overt hemorrhage does not exclude VHF, since hemorrhagic manifestations are often neither the most prominent nor the most severe feature.","Treat DIC only with clear laboratory evidence and feasible monitoring; there is no proven benefit otherwise.","Fluid management is a balancing act: VHF patients are commonly hypovolemic from insensible and GI losses, yet may also have decreased cardiac output, capillary leak, and oliguria requiring judicious fluids and inotropic/vasopressor support.","Do not assume respiratory/aerosol spread for filovirids in natural settings and do not reuse medical equipment (gloves, syringes) used for an infected patient."],"management":["Meticulous supportive care with aggressive fluid and electrolyte resuscitation, correction of coagulopathy and organ support, which is the cornerstone of survival","Pathogen-directed therapy where available, including monoclonal antibody agents for Ebola virus disease and ribavirin for arenaviral Lassa and certain bunyaviral haemorrhagic fevers","Strict isolation and barrier nursing with full personal protective equipment and safe handling of body fluids and remains to prevent transmission","Close monitoring of fluid balance, electrolytes and coagulation, treating haemorrhage and shock","Immediate public health notification, contact tracing and management in a designated high-consequence infectious disease unit","Ring vaccination of contacts with the rVSV-ZEBOV vaccine during Ebola outbreaks for prevention"],"severityClassification":"No formal numeric severity score is provided; clinically, the illness progresses from an incubation period through a nonspecific febrile prodrome (early phase) to a peak phase, with severity marked by progressive multiorgan dysfunction (shock, acute kidney injury, respiratory failure, CNS injury, severe hemorrhage). Filovirid disease course is depicted across incubation, early, peak, and recovery phases. Severity and case-fatality vary by causative virus and the availability of specific countermeasures and supportive care (pp.1675, 1683-1686).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1679"],"crossLinks":["DENGUE","MALARIA"]},"viral_warts":{"name":"Viral Warts","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["verruca","verruca vulgaris","common wart","plantar wart","verruca plantaris","HPV wart"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Common warts are firm, hyperkeratotic papules with a rough surface, often studded with tiny black dots representing thrombosed capillaries","Paring the surface of a plantar wart reveals these capillary dots and interrupts the skin lines, helping to distinguish it from a corn or callus","Morphological variants include flat (plane) warts, filiform warts around the face, and mosaic plantar warts","Transmission is by direct contact and via contaminated moist surfaces such as swimming-pool floors, with autoinoculation and the Koebner phenomenon spreading lesions","Most warts in healthy people resolve spontaneously as immunity develops, so a period of watchful waiting is reasonable","First-line treatments such as topical keratolytics and cryotherapy work by physical destruction and stimulating an immune response, and typically require repeated, persistent application","Widespread, recalcitrant, or unusual warts should prompt consideration of immunosuppression","Genital warts require assessment for sexually transmitted infection, and their prevention links to HPV vaccination"],"pathophysiology":"Viral warts result from infection of keratinocytes by human papillomavirus, which enters through microabrasions and drives epidermal proliferation and hyperkeratosis. Different HPV genotypes show tropism for particular sites and clinical morphologies. The virus establishes infection in the basal layer and completes its life cycle as keratinocytes differentiate towards the surface, producing the koilocytic changes seen histologically. Clearance depends on an effective cell-mediated immune response, which explains both spontaneous resolution over time and the persistence or proliferation of warts in immunocompromised patients. Certain high-risk genital genotypes carry oncogenic potential.","additionalDifferentials":["Corn or callus (skin lines preserved, no capillary dots)","Molluscum contagiosum","Seborrhoeic keratosis","Actinic keratosis or squamous cell carcinoma in a persistent lesion on sun-damaged skin","Acral or amelanotic melanoma masquerading as a plantar wart","Knuckle pads or other keratotic papules"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is usually clinical, aided by paring the lesion and by dermoscopy","Skin biopsy for atypical, persistent, or rapidly changing lesions to exclude malignancy","Assessment for immunosuppression when warts are extensive or recalcitrant","Sexual health screening for anogenital warts"],"redFlags":["A verrucous or ulcerated lesion that is enlarging, bleeding, or failing to respond, raising concern for squamous cell carcinoma","A pigmented or irregular acral lesion that could be melanoma rather than a wart","Numerous or atypical warts suggesting underlying immunosuppression, including epidermodysplasia verruciformis","Anogenital warts in a child prompting safeguarding consideration"],"prognosis":"The outlook is generally excellent, with most warts in immunocompetent people clearing spontaneously within one to two years; some are stubborn and require repeated treatment, and clearance is slower and relapse more likely where immunity is impaired.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing an amelanotic melanoma or squamous cell carcinoma as a resistant wart and delaying biopsy","Confusing a plantar wart with a simple corn or callus","Persisting with destructive treatments that cause more scarring and pain than the wart itself, particularly in children","Failing to consider immunosuppression in widespread or treatment-resistant disease"],"management":["Watchful waiting is reasonable for most warts in healthy people, since immunity clears them spontaneously over time","First-line topical keratolytics such as salicylic acid and cryotherapy, applied persistently and repeatedly, which act by physical destruction and immune stimulation","Second-line options for resistant lesions including topical imiquimod, 5-fluorouracil, intralesional bleomycin, podophyllotoxin for anogenital warts, or laser ablation","Regular paring of hyperkeratotic and plantar lesions to reduce bulk and aid treatment","Assess for and manage immunosuppression when warts are extensive or recalcitrant","Skin biopsy for atypical, persistent or rapidly changing lesions to exclude malignancy","Sexual health screening for anogenital warts and promotion of HPV vaccination for prevention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"visceral_artery_aneurysm":{"name":"Visceral artery aneurysm","system":"Cardiovascular / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Splanchnic artery aneurysm","VAA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Encompasses true aneurysms and pseudoaneurysms of the coeliac, superior and inferior mesenteric arteries and their branches; the splenic artery is the commonest site (about 60%), followed by the hepatic artery.","Splenic artery aneurysms are more frequent in women, particularly with multiparity, portal hypertension and prior liver transplantation.","Pseudoaneurysms, often hepatic, pancreaticoduodenal or gastroduodenal, are usually secondary to pancreatitis, trauma, infection or iatrogenic injury and carry a high rupture risk irrespective of size.","Most true aneurysms are asymptomatic and discovered incidentally on cross-sectional imaging.","Rupture is catastrophic, into the peritoneum, retroperitoneum or gastrointestinal tract, and splenic aneurysms may show the classic 'double rupture' first into the lesser sac then the free peritoneum.","Pancreaticoduodenal and gastroduodenal aneurysms are frequently associated with coeliac axis stenosis or occlusion, often from median arcuate ligament compression.","CT angiography is the mainstay of diagnosis and planning; endovascular embolisation or stent-grafting is now first-line for most lesions.","Rupture risk is markedly increased in pregnancy for splenic aneurysms, with high maternal and foetal mortality."],"pathophysiology":"True visceral artery aneurysms arise from medial degeneration with loss of elastic and smooth-muscle elements, driven by atherosclerosis, fibromuscular dysplasia, connective-tissue disorders, altered splanchnic haemodynamics (as in portal hypertension or pregnancy) and high-flow states. Pseudoaneurysms result from full-thickness arterial wall disruption contained by surrounding tissue, typically from enzymatic autodigestion in pancreatitis, penetrating or blunt trauma, infection (mycotic) or iatrogenic injury; their wall is fibrous rather than arterial and prone to expansion and rupture. Coeliac axis occlusion diverts flow through the pancreaticoduodenal arcades, and the resulting increased flow and shear predispose to arcade aneurysm formation.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of acute abdomen or gastrointestinal haemorrhage","Abdominal aortic aneurysm","Pancreatic pseudocyst or cystic neoplasm as an imaging mimic","Retroperitoneal haematoma from another cause","Arteriovenous malformation or hypervascular tumour","Median arcuate ligament syndrome","Splenic or hepatic mass"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT angiography of the abdomen for diagnosis, size and anatomical relationships","Digital subtraction catheter angiography, usually with intent to treat","Duplex ultrasound for splenic and hepatic lesions and for surveillance","MR angiography where iodinated contrast or radiation is to be avoided","Assessment of the coeliac axis for stenosis or median arcuate ligament compression","Liver function tests and evaluation for portal hypertension","Cross-sectional surveillance imaging for small asymptomatic aneurysms"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe abdominal or flank pain with haemodynamic instability, indicating rupture.","Gastrointestinal bleeding, haematemesis or melaena with a known or suspected pseudoaneurysm (haemosuccus pancreaticus, haemobilia).","Pregnancy with a known splenic artery aneurysm.","Rapid aneurysm enlargement or any symptomatic aneurysm.","Features of sepsis suggesting a mycotic aneurysm."],"prognosis":"Elective endovascular or surgical treatment has low mortality and good outcomes, whereas rupture, especially of pseudoaneurysms or during pregnancy, carries high mortality. Pseudoaneurysms warrant treatment irrespective of size given their unpredictable rupture risk.","pitfalls":["Applying a single size threshold to pseudoaneurysms, which should generally be treated regardless of diameter.","Missing coeliac axis compression when treating pancreaticoduodenal arcade aneurysms, risking recurrence if inflow is not addressed.","Overlooking a splenic artery aneurysm in a pregnant or portal-hypertensive patient.","Dismissing curvilinear 'signet ring' calcification of a splenic aneurysm as a benign finding.","Failing to consider bowel or splenic ischaemia after aggressive embolisation without assessing collateral supply."],"management":["Elective endovascular repair by coil embolisation or covered stenting, or open surgical reconstruction, for true aneurysms that are large, symptomatic or enlarging","Treat all pseudoaneurysms regardless of size, and splenic aneurysms in women of childbearing age or in pregnancy, given their unpredictable and high rupture risk","Emergency embolisation or surgery with resuscitation for ruptured aneurysms presenting with haemorrhage and shock","Address underlying coeliac axis stenosis, including median arcuate ligament release, in pancreaticoduodenal and gastroduodenal arcade aneurysms","Surveillance imaging with CT angiography or duplex ultrasound for small asymptomatic true aneurysms","Modify contributory factors such as atherosclerotic risk and portal hypertension","Manage within a vascular surgery and interventional radiology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy","Society for Vascular Surgery clinical practice guidelines on the management of visceral aneurysms","Oxford Textbook of Vascular Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"visceral_larva_migrans":{"name":"Visceral larva migrans","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["toxocariasis","Toxocara canis infection","Toxocara cati infection","VLM"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases; CDC parasitic disease guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A tissue-invasive infection caused by migrating larvae of the dog and cat roundworms Toxocara canis and Toxocara cati","Humans, especially young children who ingest soil contaminated with embryonated eggs, are accidental hosts in which larvae cannot mature","The classic visceral form presents with fever, hepatomegaly, wheeze or cough, and marked eosinophilia","A separate ocular form causes unilateral visual loss, often mistaken for retinoblastoma, and typically lacks prominent eosinophilia","Diagnosis relies on serology (Toxocara-specific antibodies), as larvae are rarely recovered and stool examination is unhelpful because worms do not reach the gut in humans","A history of pica, geophagia, or close contact with puppies or contaminated environments supports the diagnosis","Most visceral disease is self-limiting; anthelmintics such as albendazole with corticosteroids for the inflammatory reaction are used in significant or organ-threatening infection","Prevention centres on deworming pets, hand hygiene and reducing environmental soil contamination"],"pathophysiology":"Visceral larva migrans occurs when a human ingests embryonated Toxocara eggs from soil, faeces or contaminated food. The eggs hatch in the intestine and release larvae that penetrate the gut wall and migrate through the bloodstream to organs such as the liver, lungs, muscle, eye and brain. Because humans are not the natural host, the larvae cannot complete their life cycle or mature into adult worms; instead they persist and wander through tissues, provoking eosinophil-rich granulomatous inflammation and a strong immunoglobulin E response. The organ-specific symptoms and the intense eosinophilia reflect this larval migration and the host inflammatory reaction to it.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypereosinophilic syndrome","Other tissue-migrating helminth infections (ascariasis, strongyloidiasis, hookworm)","Acute schistosomiasis (Katayama syndrome)","Hepatic abscess or other focal liver lesions","Retinoblastoma (in the ocular form)","Eosinophilic leukaemia or lymphoma","Allergic or asthmatic pulmonary disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film for eosinophilia","Toxocara-specific serology (antibody testing)","Serum total immunoglobulin E","Liver ultrasound or cross-sectional imaging for hepatic and pulmonary lesions","Ophthalmological examination when ocular disease is suspected","Exclusion of other tissue-invasive helminths and haematological causes of eosinophilia"],"redFlags":["Neurological features suggesting central nervous system involvement","Unilateral visual loss or leukocoria requiring urgent ophthalmological review","Myocardial or severe pulmonary involvement","Persistent very high eosinophilia with systemic upset"],"prognosis":"The visceral form is usually self-limiting with a good outcome, but heavy or organ-threatening infection, and particularly ocular or central nervous system disease, can cause lasting damage such as permanent visual impairment.","pitfalls":["Examining stool for ova, which is futile because the parasite does not mature in humans","Overlooking the diagnosis in a child with eosinophilia and pica or puppy contact","Confusing ocular toxocariasis with retinoblastoma","Relying on a single serology result without clinical correlation","Neglecting anti-inflammatory cover when treating heavy infection with anthelmintics"],"management":["Anthelmintic therapy with albendazole, or mebendazole, to kill migrating Toxocara larvae in symptomatic visceral disease","Corticosteroids alongside anthelmintics for severe inflammation and for ocular or central nervous system involvement","Ophthalmological management of ocular toxocariasis with corticosteroids and, where needed, laser or vitreoretinal surgery","Symptomatic support such as bronchodilators for wheeze in the pulmonary form","Monitor eosinophilia and clinical response, with Toxocara serology to support the diagnosis","Prevention through regular deworming of pets, hand hygiene, and avoidance of pica and geophagia in young children"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","CDC guidance on toxocariasis"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitamin_a_deficiency":{"name":"Vitamin A Deficiency","system":"Nutritional","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hypovitaminosis A","xerophthalmia","retinol deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A major cause of preventable childhood blindness and increased infection-related mortality in low- and middle-income countries.","The earliest and most sensitive symptom is night blindness, reflecting impaired rod photoreceptor function.","Progressive ocular signs (xerophthalmia) include conjunctival dryness, Bitot spots, corneal xerosis, and ultimately corneal ulceration and keratomalacia that can rapidly destroy the eye.","Deficiency also impairs epithelial integrity and immune function, increasing severity of infections such as measles and diarrhoeal disease.","It commonly coexists with protein-energy malnutrition and other micronutrient deficiencies.","Measles can precipitate acute deficiency and corneal damage, so supplementation is recommended during measles infection in at-risk children.","Diagnosis is largely clinical, supported by a low serum retinol level and dietary history.","High-dose vitamin A supplementation reverses early ocular signs and reduces mortality, and periodic supplementation programmes are a key public health measure.","Excessive vitamin A intake is toxic, so dosing schedules must be followed carefully."],"pathophysiology":"Vitamin A (retinol) is essential for the visual cycle, where its aldehyde form retinal combines with opsin to form rhodopsin in rod cells; deficiency reduces rhodopsin regeneration and causes night blindness. Retinoic acid also regulates differentiation and maintenance of epithelial tissues and mucosal barriers, so deficiency leads to squamous metaplasia and keratinisation of conjunctival and corneal epithelium (xerophthalmia) and of other epithelia. Impaired epithelial barriers and immune regulation increase susceptibility to and severity of infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of night blindness (retinitis pigmentosa)","Fat malabsorption syndromes (cystic fibrosis, cholestatic liver disease, coeliac disease)","Protein-energy malnutrition with multiple deficiencies","Infective keratoconjunctivitis","Zinc deficiency","Dry eye from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum retinol level","Clinical ocular examination for Bitot spots, xerosis, and corneal changes","Assessment of nutritional status and dietary intake","Investigation for fat malabsorption where suspected","Screening for coexisting micronutrient deficiencies"],"redFlags":["Corneal ulceration or keratomalacia threatening irreversible blindness","Rapidly progressing ocular signs, particularly during measles","Severe concurrent infection in a malnourished child","Signs of advanced protein-energy malnutrition","Bilateral corneal involvement"],"prognosis":"Early ocular changes are fully reversible with prompt supplementation, but corneal ulceration and keratomalacia can cause permanent blindness; adequate vitamin A status also lowers infection-related mortality.","pitfalls":["Overlooking night blindness as an early, treatable warning sign","Failing to supplement vitamin A during measles in at-risk children","Missing underlying fat malabsorption in a child with deficiency and adequate intake","Causing toxicity by exceeding recommended supplementation doses"],"management":["Prompt oral vitamin A (retinol) supplementation, given as an age-based course, to reverse deficiency and halt progression of xerophthalmia","Vitamin A supplementation during measles infection in at-risk children to prevent acute deficiency and corneal damage","Ophthalmic care of corneal xerosis, ulceration and keratomalacia with topical lubrication and antibiotics to prevent secondary infection and preserve sight","Dietary improvement with vitamin A-rich foods and treatment of coexisting protein-energy malnutrition and other micronutrient deficiencies","Investigate and manage underlying fat malabsorption where present","Population prevention through periodic supplementation programmes and food fortification in endemic regions","Monitor serum retinol and perform regular ocular examination to track recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitamin_and_trace_mineral_deficiency":{"name":"Vitamin and Trace Mineral Deficiency and Excess","system":"Nutritional / Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Micronutrient deficiency","Hidden hunger","Vitamin deficiency","Trace mineral deficiency","Hypovitaminosis","Vitamin toxicity"],"chapter":344,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2605","clinicalPearls":["Overt single-nutrient deficiencies are uncommon in Western settings, but subclinical 'hidden hunger' is frequent in the elderly and the socioeconomically deprived; multiple deficiencies cluster in the chronically ill, those with alcohol use disorder, the impoverished, and post-bariatric-surgery patients.","Body stores differ enormously: vitamin B12 and vitamin A reserves are large (deficiency may take a year or more to appear), whereas folate and thiamine can be depleted within weeks of an inadequate diet.","Thiamine should be replenished before or during refeeding of malnourished or alcoholic patients, because giving carbohydrate (especially IV glucose) without thiamine can precipitate acute deficiency with lactic acidosis and Wernicke encephalopathy.","Wernicke encephalopathy is the classic triad of ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus, cerebellar ataxia, and confusion; addition of memory loss and confabulation defines Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, which is underdiagnosed despite a typical history.","Pellagra (niacin deficiency) is remembered as the 'four Ds' - dermatitis (notably the photosensitive Casal's necklace), diarrhea, dementia, and death; it arises with corn-based diets, alcoholism, Hartnup disease, carcinoid syndrome, and isoniazid use.","Pyridoxine (B6) should be co-administered with isoniazid to prevent neuropathy; paradoxically, chronic high-dose B6 (above ~100 mg/d) itself causes a reversible severe sensory neuropathy, so long-term supplementation should be discouraged.","Vitamin A deficiency is the leading cause of preventable childhood blindness worldwide (xerophthalmia, Bitot's spots, keratomalacia) and impairs immune defenses, increasing mortality from diarrhea, measles, malaria, and respiratory infection.","Fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies (A, D, E, K) characteristically accompany fat-malabsorptive states such as celiac disease, cholestatic liver disease, cystic fibrosis, short-bowel syndrome, and bariatric surgery.","Zinc deficiency presents with growth retardation/dwarfism, hypogonadism, hypogeusia, alopecia, immune dysfunction, and a periorificial/acral rash; the inherited form is acrodermatitis enteropathica, and penicillamine or chronic zinc itself (via copper depletion) can be culprits.","Copper deficiency causes an anemia refractory to iron plus neutropenia and can be precipitated by chronic high-dose zinc; inherited disorders include Menkes kinky-hair syndrome (ATP7A) and aceruloplasminemia.","Selenium deficiency causes Keshan cardiomyopathy in low-soil regions; combined iodine and selenium deficiency can aggravate cretinism, while selenium excess causes selenosis (hair/nail loss, garlic breath, neuropathy).","Biotin and choline deficiencies occur mainly with inborn errors or parenteral nutrition; high-dose biotin supplements can spuriously skew streptavidin-biotin immunoassays (e.g., falsely high free T4, falsely low TSH or troponin)."],"pathophysiology":"Vitamins are organic micronutrients that the body cannot synthesize in adequate amounts and that act largely as coenzymes or prosthetic groups (e.g., thiamine pyrophosphate in alpha-ketoacid decarboxylation; FAD/FMN flavoenzymes; NAD/NADP in redox reactions; pyridoxal phosphate in amino-acid metabolism). Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) require intact fat absorption and act in vision, gene transcription, calcium homeostasis, antioxidant defense, and gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors. Trace minerals function within metalloenzymes (zinc in numerous metalloenzymes and transcription factors; copper in ceruloplasmin/ferroxidase, cytochrome c oxidase, superoxide dismutase; selenium in glutathione peroxidase and deiodinases). Deficiency arises from inadequate intake, malabsorption, increased losses (hemodialysis, diuretics, increased urinary excretion), drug interference (isoniazid-B6, warfarin-vitamin K, metformin-B12/thiamine, penicillamine-zinc), or genetic transport/enzyme defects. Conversely, supplements, fortification, and occupational exposures can cause clinically important toxicities; in the inflammatory/acute-phase state, micronutrients redistribute and standard laboratory levels become unreliable.","additionalDifferentials":["Protein-energy malnutrition","Malabsorption syndromes (celiac disease, Crohn's disease, short-bowel syndrome)","Chronic alcohol use disorder","Post-bariatric-surgery nutrient deficiency","Inborn errors of metabolism affecting micronutrient handling (Hartnup disease, biotinidase deficiency, abetalipoproteinemia, Menkes/Wilson disease)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Peripheral neuropathy from diabetes or heavy-metal toxicity (mimics thiamine, B6, B12, or vitamin E deficiency)","Dermatitis/seborrhea of other cause (mimics niacin, riboflavin, biotin, or zinc deficiency)","Megaloblastic anemia from drugs or marrow disease (mimics folate/B12 deficiency)","Pseudotumor cerebri or hypercalcemia of other etiology (mimics chronic vitamin A or D toxicity)","Coagulopathy of hepatic disease (mimics vitamin K deficiency - distinguished by failure of prothrombin time to correct with vitamin K)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Erythrocyte transketolase activity with thiamine pyrophosphate stimulation (>25% / activity coefficient >1.25 abnormal); serum/blood thiamine by HPLC","Erythrocyte glutathione reductase activity for riboflavin; urinary 2-pyridone/2-methylnicotinamide for niacin","Plasma pyridoxal 5'-phosphate (deficiency <20 nmol/L) for vitamin B6; plasma/leukocyte ascorbate for vitamin C","Serum retinol (normal 1.05-3.50 micromol/L; deficient <0.70 micromol/L [20 microg/dL]) interpreted alongside C-reactive protein and alpha1-acid glycoprotein","Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D (>20 ng/mL adequate for bone health); plasma alpha-tocopherol (deficient <5 microg/mL) for vitamin E","Prothrombin time and PIVKA-II (undercarboxylated prothrombin) plus serum phylloquinone (<0.15 microg/L) for vitamin K","Serum zinc (<12 micromol/L / <70 microg/dL), serum copper (<65 microg/dL) with ceruloplasmin (<20 mg/dL), interpreted with albumin and acute-phase status"],"redFlags":["Confusion, ophthalmoplegia, or ataxia in an alcoholic or malnourished patient before/during refeeding - possible Wernicke encephalopathy requiring immediate thiamine","High-output cardiac failure with edema (wet beriberi) or Keshan-type cardiomyopathy (selenium)","Night blindness, corneal xerosis, or keratomalacia signaling sight-threatening vitamin A deficiency","Bleeding/hemorrhage with prolonged prothrombin time, especially neonatal intracranial or GI bleeding (vitamin K deficiency)","Increased intracranial pressure, bulging fontanels, hypercalcemia, or papilledema suggesting vitamin A or D intoxication","Iron-refractory anemia with neutropenia (copper deficiency, often from chronic high-dose zinc)"],"prognosis":"Most deficiency syndromes respond well and reversibly to timely repletion: thiamine produces cardiovascular and ophthalmoplegic improvement within ~24 hours and scurvy improves within days, although Wernicke-Korsakoff psychosis may be permanent and vitamin E or B12 neurologic deficits may only partially recover if advanced. Vitamin A deficiency carries substantial mortality - keratomalacia blinds an estimated quarter-million children yearly with case-fatality of 4-25%, and supplementation in deficient populations reduces childhood mortality by roughly 23-34%. Untreated severe thiamine, niacin (death is the fourth 'D'), or selenium (Keshan) deficiency can be fatal, as can acute vitamin A or D intoxication.","pitfalls":["Interpreting micronutrient blood levels during inflammation/acute illness without accounting for acute-phase redistribution (e.g., copper rises with ceruloplasmin, zinc and retinol fall), leading to over- or under-diagnosis","Refeeding a malnourished or alcoholic patient with carbohydrate before giving thiamine, precipitating acute deficiency and lactic acidosis","Attributing a prolonged prothrombin time solely to vitamin K deficiency in liver disease - if it fails to correct with vitamin K, the cause is hepatocellular","Overlooking drug-induced deficiencies (isoniazid-B6, warfarin/orlistat-vitamin K, metformin-B12, chronic zinc-copper, penicillamine-zinc) and chronic high-dose B6 causing neuropathy","Missing biotin supplement interference with streptavidin-biotin immunoassays (spurious thyroid, troponin, or hCG results)","Assuming carotenemia (yellow palms/soles, spared sclerae) indicates jaundice; and giving high-dose beta-carotene to smokers, which raises lung cancer risk"],"management":["Targeted repletion of the specific deficient vitamin or trace mineral by the oral or parenteral route according to severity and absorptive capacity","Parenteral thiamine given before or with any carbohydrate or intravenous glucose in malnourished or alcohol-dependent patients to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy and lactic acidosis","Treat the underlying cause such as malabsorption, alcohol use disorder, restrictive diet or previous bariatric surgery, and address clustered multiple deficiencies together","Co-administer pyridoxine with isoniazid to prevent neuropathy, while avoiding chronic high-dose pyridoxine which itself causes a sensory neuropathy","Avoid over-supplementation and monitor for toxicity, particularly with the fat-soluble vitamins A and D","Monitor biochemical nutrient markers and watch for refeeding electrolyte shifts during nutritional rehabilitation","Involve a dietitian and nutrition support team for assessment, repletion planning and long-term dietary counselling"],"severityClassification":"Deficiency severity is graded from subclinical/biochemical (laboratory abnormality without symptoms - 'hidden hunger') through overt clinical deficiency syndromes (beriberi, pellagra, scurvy, xerophthalmia, rickets) to life-threatening complications (Wernicke-Korsakoff, keratomalacia/blindness, hemorrhage, cardiomyopathy). Thiamine deficiency is classically dichotomized into wet (cardiovascular) and dry (neuropathic) beriberi with considerable overlap, and vitamin A xerophthalmia is staged from night blindness and conjunctival xerosis/Bitot's spots to corneal ulceration and keratomalacia.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2605","Harrison 22e p.2606","Harrison 22e p.2607","Harrison 22e p.2608","Harrison 22e p.2609","Harrison 22e p.2610","Harrison 22e p.2611","Harrison 22e p.2612","Harrison 22e p.2613","Harrison 22e p.2614","Harrison 22e p.2615"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitamin_b12_deficiency":{"name":"Vitamin B12 deficiency","system":"Nutrition / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cobalamin deficiency","pernicious anaemia","hypocobalaminaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO nutrition guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically causes a macrocytic megaloblastic anaemia, but neurological features can appear before anaemia and even with normal blood counts","Subacute combined degeneration of the cord produces symmetrical distal paraesthesiae, loss of proprioception and vibration sense, and an unsteady gait","Autoimmune loss of gastric intrinsic factor (pernicious anaemia) and prior gastric or terminal ileal surgery are common mechanisms of malabsorption","Strict vegetarian and vegan diets, common in parts of India, are an important dietary cause because the vitamin is almost exclusively of animal origin","A blood film may show oval macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils; marrow is megaloblastic","Serum cobalamin can be borderline, so raised methylmalonic acid and homocysteine are useful confirmatory markers","Replacement reverses the anaemia reliably, but longstanding neurological damage may only partially recover, making early treatment important","Correcting B12 with folate alone can precipitate or worsen neurological deterioration, so B12 status must be assessed before treating a megaloblastic anaemia"],"pathophysiology":"Cobalamin is a cofactor for methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. Deficiency impairs conversion of homocysteine to methionine and of methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA, disrupting DNA synthesis and myelin maintenance. Impaired DNA synthesis in haematopoietic precursors yields megaloblastic, ineffective erythropoiesis, while disordered myelin metabolism underlies the neuropathy and cord degeneration. Absorption depends on intrinsic factor from gastric parietal cells and an intact terminal ileum, so autoimmune gastritis, atrophic gastritis, gastric or ileal resection, and malabsorptive states all reduce uptake; dietary lack occurs when animal products are excluded.","additionalDifferentials":["Folate deficiency (other cause of megaloblastic anaemia)","Myelodysplastic syndrome","Hypothyroidism causing macrocytosis","Alcohol-related macrocytosis and marrow suppression","Other causes of peripheral neuropathy such as diabetes","Copper deficiency myeloneuropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film for macrocytosis and hypersegmented neutrophils","Serum cobalamin, with methylmalonic acid and homocysteine when the level is equivocal","Anti-intrinsic factor and anti-parietal cell antibodies for pernicious anaemia","Reticulocyte count and haemolysis markers as ineffective erythropoiesis can raise LDH and bilirubin","Assessment for malabsorption and coexisting iron or folate deficiency"],"redFlags":["Progressive myelopathy or gait disturbance indicating subacute combined degeneration needing urgent treatment","Severe anaemia with cardiorespiratory compromise","Cognitive decline or psychiatric change attributable to deficiency","Pancytopenia from profound megaloblastic marrow failure"],"prognosis":"Haematological recovery is usually complete with replacement, but neurological recovery depends on how long deficiency was present; early treatment gives the best outcome while advanced cord degeneration may leave residual deficits.","pitfalls":["Treating megaloblastic anaemia with folate alone and masking or worsening B12-related neurological disease","Attributing neurological symptoms to another cause because the blood count is normal","Relying solely on a borderline serum cobalamin without functional markers","Failing to identify and monitor pernicious anaemia and its association with other autoimmune disease and gastric cancer"],"management":["Cobalamin replacement with intramuscular hydroxocobalamin for pernicious anaemia and malabsorptive causes and oral cyanocobalamin for dietary deficiency","Lifelong parenteral maintenance therapy in pernicious anaemia and after gastric or terminal ileal surgery","Dietary counselling and supplementation for strict vegetarians and vegans, in whom intake is the limiting factor","Correct coexisting iron and folate deficiency, avoiding folate replacement alone as it can precipitate or worsen neurological deterioration","Monitor the reticulocyte and haematological response and watch for hypokalaemia during recovery","Treat neurological features early, as recovery from subacute combined degeneration depends on prompt replacement, referring to neurology for severe deficits","Endoscopic surveillance for gastric neoplasia in confirmed pernicious anaemia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO nutrition guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitamin_d_deficiency":{"name":"Vitamin D deficiency","system":"Nutrition / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hypovitaminosis D","rickets","osteomalacia","calciferol deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO nutrition guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["In children deficiency causes rickets with bowing of the legs, widened wrists, a rachitic rosary, and delayed growth; in adults it causes osteomalacia with bone pain and proximal myopathy","It is highly prevalent across India despite abundant sunshine, owing to skin pigmentation, covering clothing, indoor lifestyles, air pollution, and low dietary intake","Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D is the best marker of body stores; 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is not a reliable indicator of deficiency","Biochemistry classically shows low or low-normal calcium and phosphate with a raised alkaline phosphatase and secondary hyperparathyroidism","Risk groups include exclusively breastfed infants without supplementation, older housebound adults, and people with malabsorption or chronic kidney or liver disease","Proximal muscle weakness and increased fall and fracture risk are important, sometimes overlooked, consequences","Correction of deficiency together with adequate calcium intake improves bone mineralisation and muscle function"],"pathophysiology":"Vitamin D is synthesised in skin under ultraviolet B exposure and obtained in smaller amounts from diet, then hydroxylated in the liver to 25-hydroxyvitamin D and in the kidney to the active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. Its main role is to maintain calcium and phosphate homeostasis by enhancing intestinal absorption and supporting bone mineralisation. Deficiency reduces calcium and phosphate absorption, provoking secondary hyperparathyroidism that mobilises calcium from bone and increases phosphate loss. The resulting failure to mineralise osteoid produces soft bone: rickets at the growth plates of children and osteomalacia in adults.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperparathyroidism","Renal osteodystrophy in chronic kidney disease","Hypophosphataemic rickets and other inherited defects","Osteoporosis","Malignancy or other cause of bone pain","Inflammatory myopathy for the muscle weakness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D as the primary measure of status","Calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, and parathyroid hormone","Renal and liver function, given their role in vitamin D activation","Radiographs showing looser zones (pseudofractures) or rachitic growth-plate changes","Assessment for malabsorption when deficiency is severe or refractory"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic hypocalcaemia with tetany, laryngospasm, or seizures","Fragility or insufficiency fractures","Severe deficiency in an infant with hypocalcaemic seizures or cardiomyopathy","Refractory deficiency suggesting malabsorption or renal or hepatic disease"],"prognosis":"With adequate replacement and calcium intake, bone mineralisation and muscle strength recover and childhood rickets heals, though severe or prolonged rickets may leave residual skeletal deformity.","pitfalls":["Measuring 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D instead of 25-hydroxyvitamin D to assess status","Treating vitamin D without ensuring adequate calcium intake","Overlooking deficiency as a cause of proximal myopathy and falls in older adults","Missing severe hypocalcaemia before starting treatment"],"management":["Repletion with oral vitamin D (colecalciferol or ergocalciferol), using a loading regimen followed by ongoing maintenance dosing","Ensure adequate dietary calcium and phosphate, adding calcium supplementation where intake is poor to support bone remineralisation","Advise sensible sunlight exposure and vitamin D-rich foods, and correct the underlying cause such as malabsorption or limited sun exposure","Monitor calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, parathyroid hormone and 25-hydroxyvitamin D to confirm biochemical and radiological healing","Physiotherapy and falls-risk reduction for proximal myopathy, with analgesia for bone pain in osteomalacia or rickets","Routine supplementation of at-risk groups including exclusively breastfed infants, housebound older adults, and those with pigmented or covered skin","Refer for specialist metabolic assessment when deficiency is severe, refractory, or due to malabsorption or chronic kidney or liver disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO nutrition guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitamin_d_dependent_rickets_type_1a":{"name":"Vitamin D-dependent rickets type 1A (CYP27B1)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VDDR1A","pseudovitamin D deficiency rickets","1-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency","vitamin D-dependent rickets type I"],"chapter":null,"source":"Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases / Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in CYP27B1, the gene encoding renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase","The enzyme defect prevents conversion of 25-hydroxyvitamin D to active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, so activation rather than intake is the problem","Presents in the first year or two of life with rickets, hypotonia, growth failure, tetany or seizures from hypocalcaemia, and skeletal deformity","Biochemistry shows hypocalcaemia, hypophosphataemia, raised alkaline phosphatase, and secondary hyperparathyroidism","The hallmark is a normal or high 25-hydroxyvitamin D with a characteristically low 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, distinguishing it from nutritional deficiency","Unlike vitamin D-dependent rickets type 2, there is no alopecia because the receptor pathway is intact","Treatment is lifelong physiological replacement with the active hormone calcitriol, which bypasses the enzymatic block, with calcium supplementation as needed"],"pathophysiology":"CYP27B1 encodes the renal mitochondrial enzyme 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which converts 25-hydroxyvitamin D into the biologically active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. Biallelic loss-of-function mutations abolish this final activation step, so despite adequate substrate the active hormone cannot be produced. Deficient 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D reduces intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption, producing hypocalcaemia and hypophosphataemia, which impair mineralisation of growth-plate cartilage and osteoid, causing rickets and osteomalacia. Hypocalcaemia drives secondary hyperparathyroidism, further increasing renal phosphate loss and bone resorption. Because the enzyme, not the receptor, is defective, providing calcitriol directly restores signalling and corrects the disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Nutritional vitamin D deficiency rickets","Vitamin D-dependent rickets type 2 (VDR resistance)","Hypophosphataemic (FGF23-mediated) rickets","Hypophosphatasia","Renal osteodystrophy / chronic kidney disease mineral bone disorder","Primary hypoparathyroidism causing hypocalcaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium, phosphate, and alkaline phosphatase","Parathyroid hormone, typically elevated as a secondary response","25-hydroxyvitamin D (normal or high) with 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (characteristically low)","Radiographs of wrists and knees showing rachitic metaphyseal changes","Urinary calcium monitoring during treatment to avoid hypercalciuria","CYP27B1 gene sequencing to confirm the diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Hypocalcaemic seizures or tetany in an infant","Failure to thrive with progressive skeletal deformity","Cardiac dysfunction from severe hypocalcaemia","Hypercalcaemia or hypercalciuria from over-replacement with active vitamin D"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent with lifelong physiological calcitriol replacement, which normalises calcium and heals the rickets; growth and bone health are usually restored, provided treatment is maintained and monitored to avoid toxicity.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing as ordinary nutritional deficiency and giving only parent vitamin D, which cannot bypass the enzyme block","Not measuring 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, missing the diagnostic low-active/normal-substrate pattern","Confusing it with type 2 disease, where high-dose therapy and receptor resistance apply","Failing to monitor urinary calcium, allowing hypercalciuria and nephrocalcinosis on active vitamin D"],"management":["Lifelong physiological replacement with active vitamin D (calcitriol or alfacalcidol), which bypasses the defective 1-alpha-hydroxylase and restores signalling","Calcium supplementation during initial healing to correct hypocalcaemia and support mineralisation","Manage acute hypocalcaemic tetany or seizures with calcium replacement","Monitor calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, parathyroid hormone and radiographic healing of the rickets to titrate therapy","Surveillance of serum and urinary calcium to avoid hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria and nephrocalcinosis from over-treatment","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance","Metabolic bone and paediatric specialist referral for ongoing management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism","Williams Textbook of Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitamin_d_dependent_rickets_type_2":{"name":"Vitamin D-dependent rickets type 2 (VDR, hereditary vitamin D resistance)","system":"Musculoskeletal / Metabolic bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VDDR2","hereditary vitamin D-resistant rickets","HVDRR","vitamin D receptor defect rickets"],"chapter":null,"source":"Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases / Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the vitamin D receptor (VDR), producing end-organ resistance to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D","Unlike type 1A, the active hormone is made in abundance but cannot signal, so 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is markedly elevated","Presents in infancy with rickets, hypocalcaemia, hypophosphataemia, raised alkaline phosphatase, and secondary hyperparathyroidism","A characteristic and often distinguishing feature is total-body alopecia, reflecting the VDR role in the hair follicle, which does not occur in type 1A","The degree of receptor dysfunction determines severity, and alopecia generally signals more severe, treatment-resistant disease","Diagnosis rests on the combination of rickets with very high 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D and normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D, confirmed by VDR sequencing","Management uses high-dose active vitamin D (calcitriol) with high-dose calcium; unresponsive cases may need long-term intravenous or intensive enteral calcium to bypass defective intestinal absorption"],"pathophysiology":"The vitamin D receptor mediates the genomic actions of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. Loss-of-function mutations, affecting hormone binding or DNA binding, render target tissues resistant to the active hormone despite high circulating levels. Intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption fail, causing hypocalcaemia, hypophosphataemia, and impaired mineralisation with rickets and osteomalacia. Hypocalcaemia stimulates secondary hyperparathyroidism, and the unopposed feedback loop drives 1-alpha-hydroxylase, elevating 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D further. Because the receptor is also essential for postnatal hair follicle cycling, many mutations cause alopecia, which tends to accompany the more severe, calcitriol-resistant phenotypes. Overcoming resistance therapeutically requires supraphysiological active vitamin D and calcium, sometimes delivered parenterally.","additionalDifferentials":["Vitamin D-dependent rickets type 1A (CYP27B1)","Nutritional vitamin D deficiency rickets","Hypophosphataemic (FGF23-mediated) rickets","Hypophosphatasia","Ectodermal dysplasia or other causes of congenital alopecia","Primary hypoparathyroidism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium, phosphate, and alkaline phosphatase","Parathyroid hormone, usually elevated","25-hydroxyvitamin D (normal) with markedly elevated 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D","Radiographs demonstrating rachitic changes","Clinical assessment for alopecia and its extent as a prognostic marker","VDR gene sequencing to confirm the receptor defect"],"redFlags":["Hypocalcaemic seizures or tetany in infancy","Total alopecia signalling severe, potentially calcitriol-resistant disease","Progressive deformity and growth failure despite therapy","Hypercalcaemia or nephrocalcinosis from aggressive calcium and active vitamin D regimens"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the residual receptor function: milder defects may respond to high-dose active vitamin D and calcium, whereas severe receptor-null disease, often with alopecia, may require prolonged intensive or intravenous calcium, and some patients improve spontaneously after childhood.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with type 1A, where standard-dose calcitriol suffices and there is no alopecia","Interpreting the very high 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D as adequacy rather than resistance","Underdosing calcium in resistant cases where enteral or intravenous calcium is the key therapy","Overtreating and causing hypercalcaemia and nephrocalcinosis without urinary calcium monitoring"],"management":["Supraphysiological (high-dose) active vitamin D (calcitriol) combined with high-dose oral calcium to overcome end-organ receptor resistance","Prolonged intravenous calcium infusions for severe receptor-null disease unresponsive to oral therapy","Manage hypocalcaemic crises with calcium replacement","Monitor calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, parathyroid hormone and urinary calcium alongside radiographic healing","Supportive and cosmetic measures for alopecia, for which no specific therapy exists","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance","Metabolic bone and clinical genetics specialist referral for treatment-resistant disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Primer on the Metabolic Bone Diseases and Disorders of Mineral Metabolism","Williams Textbook of Endocrinology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitamin_e_deficiency":{"name":"Vitamin E deficiency","system":"Nervous system / Nutritional","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tocopherol deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nutrition and neurology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fat-soluble vitamin deficiency that chiefly causes a progressive neurological syndrome","Features resemble Friedreich ataxia: spinocerebellar ataxia, loss of proprioception and vibration sense, and areflexia","A pigmentary retinopathy and, in infants, haemolysis can occur","Dietary deficiency is rare; most cases result from fat malabsorption (for example cholestasis, cystic fibrosis, short bowel) or from the inherited disorder ataxia with vitamin E deficiency","Abetalipoproteinaemia is an important cause in which vitamin E cannot be transported","Serum vitamin E is measured relative to lipids, and treatment is supplementation with correction of malabsorption"],"pathophysiology":"Vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol) is a lipid-soluble antioxidant that protects cell membranes, particularly of neurons, from oxidative damage. Deficiency, usually from fat malabsorption or defective tocopherol transport, leads to oxidative injury of the large myelinated sensory neurons and spinocerebellar and posterior column pathways, producing a sensory ataxic neuropathy. Because absorption depends on normal fat digestion and lipoprotein handling, disorders of these processes, or the genetic ataxia with vitamin E deficiency, are the usual causes.","additionalDifferentials":["Friedreich ataxia","Subacute combined degeneration (vitamin B12 deficiency)","Other spinocerebellar ataxias","Copper deficiency myeloneuropathy","Abetalipoproteinaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum vitamin E interpreted relative to serum lipids","Assessment for fat malabsorption and its cause","Lipid profile and lipoprotein studies (for abetalipoproteinaemia)","Neurological examination and, where needed, nerve conduction studies","Genetic testing for ataxia with vitamin E deficiency in suggestive cases"],"redFlags":["Progressive ataxia and sensory loss","Visual decline from retinopathy","Underlying severe malabsorptive disease","Infantile haemolysis"],"prognosis":"Early supplementation can halt and partially reverse neurological features, but established deficits may be permanent if treatment is delayed.","pitfalls":["Interpreting vitamin E levels without correcting for lipids","Missing the treatable genetic cause (ataxia with vitamin E deficiency) mimicking Friedreich ataxia","Overlooking underlying fat malabsorption","Delaying replacement until deficits are irreversible"],"management":["Supplementation with oral alpha-tocopherol, at high doses in malabsorption, using parenteral vitamin E if oral repletion fails","Treat the underlying cause of fat malabsorption such as cholestasis, cystic fibrosis or short bowel, using water-miscible preparations","In abetalipoproteinaemia, large-dose fat-soluble vitamin supplementation alongside dietary fat restriction","Concurrent replacement of the other fat-soluble vitamins, which are often co-deficient","Early treatment to halt and partly reverse the sensory ataxic neuropathy, with physiotherapy and neuro-rehabilitation for established deficits","Monitor serum vitamin E interpreted relative to serum lipids, with periodic neurological review","Genetic testing and counselling where ataxia with vitamin E deficiency is confirmed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nutrition reference texts (standard reference)","Neurology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitamin_k_deficiency":{"name":"Vitamin K deficiency","system":"Nutrition / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hypovitaminosis K","vitamin K deficiency bleeding","haemorrhagic disease of the newborn"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO nutrition guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Deficiency causes a bleeding tendency with a prolonged prothrombin time, and in more severe cases a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time","Newborns are especially vulnerable because of low placental transfer, immature gut flora, and low vitamin K content of breast milk, causing vitamin K deficiency bleeding","Routine neonatal vitamin K prophylaxis prevents haemorrhagic disease of the newborn, including its dangerous late intracranial form","In adults it results from fat malabsorption, biliary obstruction, prolonged antibiotic use, or poor intake, since much vitamin K also comes from gut bacteria","Warfarin acts by antagonising vitamin K, and its effect can be reversed by vitamin K when correction is needed","The coagulopathy corrects with vitamin K repletion, and rapid correction confirms the diagnosis","Because it is fat soluble, deficiency clusters with deficiencies of vitamins A, D, and E in malabsorptive states"],"pathophysiology":"Vitamin K is a cofactor for the gamma-carboxylation of glutamate residues on clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X and on the anticoagulant proteins C and S, a modification required for their activity. Deficiency leaves these factors undercarboxylated and functionally inactive, impairing coagulation and producing bleeding. The vitamin is fat soluble and derives from dietary green vegetables and from synthesis by intestinal bacteria, so inadequate intake, fat malabsorption, absence of bile, and suppression of gut flora all reduce availability. Neonates start with low stores and sterile guts, explaining their particular susceptibility.","additionalDifferentials":["Liver disease causing impaired synthesis of clotting factors","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Inherited coagulation factor deficiencies","Warfarin or other anticoagulant effect","Other bleeding disorders such as thrombocytopenia or platelet dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Prothrombin time and activated partial thromboplastin time","Assessment of liver function to distinguish hepatic synthetic failure","Response of coagulation parameters to vitamin K repletion","Evaluation for fat malabsorption and biliary obstruction in adults","Consideration of coexisting fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies"],"redFlags":["Active or severe bleeding, including gastrointestinal haemorrhage","Intracranial haemorrhage, especially late vitamin K deficiency bleeding in an infant","Coagulopathy in a patient requiring urgent surgery or intervention","Bleeding in the context of significant liver disease requiring broader management"],"prognosis":"Deficiency-related coagulopathy corrects reliably and rapidly with vitamin K when there is no severe liver disease; neonatal prophylaxis largely prevents the life-threatening bleeding of infancy.","pitfalls":["Assuming a prolonged prothrombin time reflects liver disease when it is correctable vitamin K deficiency, or vice versa","Overlooking fat malabsorption or biliary obstruction as the underlying cause","Failing to ensure neonatal vitamin K prophylaxis","Not recognising coexisting deficiency of other fat-soluble vitamins"],"management":["Repletion with vitamin K (phytomenadione), given orally in mild cases and parenterally when bleeding is significant","Fresh frozen plasma or prothrombin complex concentrate for active severe or life-threatening haemorrhage, giving immediate but temporary correction","Routine neonatal vitamin K prophylaxis at birth to prevent vitamin K deficiency bleeding, including its late intracranial form","Treat the underlying cause such as fat malabsorption or biliary obstruction, and review prolonged antibiotic use","For warfarin-related over-anticoagulation with bleeding, reverse with vitamin K","Monitor prothrombin time and activated partial thromboplastin time to confirm the rapid response that also verifies the diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO nutrition guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitamin_k_dependent_coagulation_factor_deficiency":{"name":"Combined vitamin K-dependent clotting factor deficiency (GGCX/VKORC1)","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VKCFD","combined deficiency of vitamin K-dependent clotting factors","GGCX deficiency","VKORC1 deficiency","gamma-glutamyl carboxylase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare autosomal recessive disorder causing concurrent deficiency of the vitamin K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX and X, and of proteins C and S","Bleeding varies from mild to severe and may present in the neonatal period with intracranial or umbilical bleeding, mimicking severe haemorrhagic disease of the newborn","Both the prothrombin time and the activated partial thromboplastin time are prolonged, reflecting the multiple factor deficiencies","GGCX variants can also cause skeletal and connective-tissue features, such as a pseudoxanthoma-elasticum-like phenotype, alongside the coagulopathy","Affected individuals show marked sensitivity to warfarin because the same vitamin K cycle enzymes are involved","Some patients respond, at least partially, to vitamin K because it drives residual carboxylase or reductase activity","Management uses vitamin K and, for acute or severe bleeding, replacement of the vitamin K-dependent factors"],"pathophysiology":"The vitamin K-dependent clotting factors require gamma-carboxylation of glutamate residues to become functional, a reaction catalysed by gamma-glutamyl carboxylase (GGCX) that consumes reduced vitamin K. Vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1) regenerates the reduced vitamin needed for this cycle. Loss-of-function variants in GGCX or VKORC1 impair carboxylation, so factors II, VII, IX and X, and the anticoagulant proteins C and S, are secreted as poorly functional undercarboxylated proteins. The dominant clinical effect is bleeding, and because the same enzymes are the target of coumarin anticoagulants, affected patients are hypersensitive to warfarin.","additionalDifferentials":["Acquired vitamin K deficiency (malabsorption, poor intake, biliary obstruction)","Warfarin or other vitamin K antagonist effect, including inadvertent ingestion","Haemorrhagic disease of the newborn from vitamin K deficiency","Liver disease with impaired factor synthesis","Isolated inherited deficiency of a single vitamin K-dependent factor","Disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Prothrombin time and activated partial thromboplastin time, both prolonged","Individual assays of factors II, VII, IX and X, and of proteins C and S","Response of clotting times to vitamin K administration","Measurement of undercarboxylated (des-gamma-carboxy) proteins where available","GGCX and VKORC1 gene analysis to confirm the diagnosis","Evaluation for skeletal or connective-tissue features in suspected GGCX defects"],"redFlags":["Neonatal intracranial haemorrhage","Severe mucosal, gastrointestinal, or intra-abdominal bleeding","Marked bleeding provoked by a standard warfarin dose","Umbilical or postpartum bleeding suggesting a severe congenital coagulopathy"],"prognosis":"With vitamin K supplementation and factor replacement for acute bleeding, most patients achieve satisfactory haemostasis and a good long-term outlook. Severity depends on the residual enzyme activity, and neonatal presentations carry higher risk if not promptly recognised.","pitfalls":["Attributing the coagulopathy solely to dietary vitamin K deficiency or occult warfarin exposure","Not recognising warfarin hypersensitivity, leading to over-anticoagulation","Overlooking associated skeletal or connective-tissue features in GGCX defects","Failing to check the response to vitamin K, which can guide management"],"management":["High-dose vitamin K (phytomenadione), often lifelong, to drive residual carboxylase and reductase activity and restore factor function","Factor replacement with prothrombin complex concentrate or fresh frozen plasma for acute severe bleeding","Avoid coumarin anticoagulants, or use with extreme caution, given the marked warfarin hypersensitivity","Prompt recognition and treatment of neonatal bleeding, which can mimic severe haemorrhagic disease of the newborn","Monitor prothrombin time, activated partial thromboplastin time and individual factor levels to guide dosing","Evaluate and manage skeletal and connective-tissue features in GGCX defects with relevant specialists","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Hoffman: Hematology - Basic Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitiligo":{"name":"Vitiligo","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["leucoderma","acquired depigmentation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as well-demarcated, chalk-white macules and patches with complete loss of pigment, typically symmetrical in the common non-segmental form","Favoured sites include periorificial areas (around the eyes and mouth), the hands, and other acral and extensor surfaces","Wood's lamp examination accentuates the depigmented patches and helps detect early or subtle lesions, especially in lighter skin","New patches may appear at sites of trauma or friction (Koebner phenomenon)","As an autoimmune condition, it is associated with other autoimmune diseases, particularly thyroid disease, so consider screening for these","Management options include topical corticosteroids and calcineurin inhibitors, phototherapy for more extensive disease, and rigorous photoprotection of depigmented skin","The psychosocial impact can be substantial, particularly in darker skin and in visible areas, and should be addressed directly","Set realistic expectations: repigmentation is slow and variable, and hair-bearing and facial areas respond better than acral sites"],"pathophysiology":"Vitiligo is an acquired disorder characterised by immune-mediated destruction of epidermal melanocytes, leading to loss of pigment. Current understanding emphasises an interplay of genetic susceptibility, intrinsic melanocyte vulnerability with oxidative stress, and an autoimmune response in which CD8 cytotoxic T cells and interferon-gamma signalling target and destroy melanocytes. The absence of functioning melanocytes leaves affected skin devoid of melanin, producing the characteristic depigmented macules. Non-segmental vitiligo tends to be symmetrical and progressive with autoimmune associations, whereas segmental vitiligo is localised to a dermatomal-like distribution and often stabilises after an early active phase.","additionalDifferentials":["Pityriasis versicolor (hypopigmented type)","Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation","Pityriasis alba","Tuberculoid leprosy (hypopigmented anaesthetic patch)","Naevus depigmentosus or naevus anaemicus","Chemical or occupational leucoderma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis aided by Wood's lamp examination","Thyroid function tests and thyroid autoantibodies given autoimmune associations","Screening for other autoimmune disease when clinically indicated","Skin biopsy only in atypical cases to confirm melanocyte loss","Assessment of disease activity and extent to guide and monitor therapy"],"redFlags":["Rapidly spreading depigmentation indicating active, unstable disease","A solitary hypopigmented patch with reduced sensation, which should prompt exclusion of leprosy where endemic","Symptoms suggesting associated autoimmune disease, such as thyroid dysfunction","Significant psychological distress warranting mental health support"],"prognosis":"Vitiligo follows an unpredictable course; spontaneous repigmentation is uncommon and treatment results are variable, with facial and truncal lesions generally more responsive than acral and hair-bearing sites. It is not physically dangerous but can be psychologically distressing and often chronic.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with pityriasis versicolor or post-inflammatory hypopigmentation","Failing to protect depigmented skin from sunburn and photodamage","Overlooking associated autoimmune thyroid disease","Underestimating the psychological burden and promising unrealistic repigmentation"],"management":["Topical corticosteroids and topical calcineurin inhibitors as first-line therapy to arrest progression and promote repigmentation","Topical JAK inhibitor (ruxolitinib) for non-segmental facial and limited disease","Narrowband ultraviolet B phototherapy for extensive or progressive disease, with targeted excimer light for localised patches","Rigorous photoprotection of depigmented skin and cosmetic camouflage to reduce contrast","Surgical melanocyte or epidermal grafting for stable segmental disease, and depigmentation therapy in extensive refractory cases","Screen for and manage associated autoimmune disease, particularly thyroid dysfunction","Psychological support for the distress and impact on quality of life, with dermatology referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitreomacular_traction_syndrome":{"name":"Vitreomacular traction syndrome","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VMT","vitreomacular traction","vitreomacular traction syndrome","symptomatic vitreomacular adhesion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology / Ryan's Retina","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Arises when an incomplete posterior vitreous detachment leaves the vitreous abnormally adherent to the macula and exerting anteroposterior and tangential traction","More common with increasing age and in conditions with abnormal vitreoretinal adhesion, and it forms part of a spectrum with macular hole and epiretinal membrane","Symptoms include metamorphopsia, blurred central vision, micropsia and sometimes photopsia","Optical coherence tomography is the definitive investigation, showing persistent vitreomacular adhesion with distortion of the foveal contour, intraretinal cysts and elevation","Traction is often classified by the breadth of adhesion (focal versus broad) and by whether concurrent macular pathology such as an epiretinal membrane is present, which influences management","The Amsler grid is useful for detecting and monitoring distortion between reviews","Some cases resolve spontaneously when the vitreous fully separates, so observation is reasonable for mild, stable disease","Management of persistent or progressive symptomatic traction includes pars plana vitrectomy to relieve traction, with pharmacologic vitreolysis or observation considered in selected cases"],"pathophysiology":"With ageing, the vitreous gel liquefies and begins to separate from the retina, but abnormally strong adhesion at the fovea can cause the separation to be incomplete. The persistently attached posterior hyaloid then exerts anteroposterior and tangential traction on the macula. This mechanical traction distorts the foveal architecture, produces intraretinal cystic spaces and can elevate or detach the fovea, causing metamorphopsia and reduced acuity. If traction is unrelieved it may progress to a lamellar or full-thickness macular hole or contribute to macular oedema; conversely, spontaneous completion of the posterior vitreous detachment can release the traction and resolve the syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Full-thickness or lamellar macular hole","Epiretinal membrane (macular pucker)","Cystoid macular oedema","Diabetic macular oedema with vitreomacular interface disease","Central serous chorioretinopathy","Choroidal neovascular membrane","Myopic traction maculopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Optical coherence tomography to confirm vitreomacular adhesion and traction and to grade its extent","Amsler grid testing to detect and monitor metamorphopsia","Best-corrected visual acuity measurement to follow functional change","Fundus examination to assess the vitreoretinal interface and exclude coexisting pathology","B-scan ultrasonography where media opacity limits OCT assessment of the vitreous","Serial imaging to detect progression to a macular hole or spontaneous resolution"],"redFlags":["Progressive distortion or acuity loss indicating worsening traction","Development of a full-thickness macular hole","Associated retinal detachment or a peripheral retinal tear","Rapid change suggesting a coexisting exudative or vascular macular process"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary with the severity and duration of traction: mild cases may resolve spontaneously with good vision, while persistent traction can cause lasting distortion or progress to a macular hole; surgical relief of traction generally improves symptoms, though visual recovery may be partial and gradual.","pitfalls":["Relying on fundus examination alone without OCT to confirm and characterise the traction","Intervening early in mild cases that might resolve spontaneously with completion of vitreous separation","Missing an evolving or established macular hole beneath the traction","Overlooking a coexisting epiretinal membrane that alters the surgical plan and prognosis","Attributing visual symptoms solely to traction while missing a concurrent macular disease"],"management":["Observation of mild or minimally symptomatic traction, since spontaneous release of adhesion can resolve the syndrome","Pharmacological vitreolysis with intravitreal ocriplasmin for focal adhesion without an epiretinal membrane","Pars plana vitrectomy with membrane peeling to relieve traction in symptomatic, progressive, or complicated cases including macular hole or epiretinal membrane","Pneumatic vitreolysis with intravitreal expansile gas in selected cases to induce posterior vitreous separation","Serial optical coherence tomography and best-corrected visual acuity monitoring, with home Amsler grid testing for distortion","Watch for progression to lamellar or full-thickness macular hole, treating promptly if it develops","Vitreoretinal ophthalmology referral for grading and treatment decisions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","Ryan's Retina"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitreous_hemorrhage":{"name":"Vitreous haemorrhage","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vitreous hemorrhage","bleeding into the vitreous","VH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with the sudden painless onset of floaters, cobwebs, a reddish haze, or, with dense bleeding, profound loss of vision","Proliferative diabetic retinopathy and retinal tears (with or without detachment) are among the commonest causes, alongside retinal vein occlusion and trauma","A key priority is to identify a retinal tear or detachment, as these are urgently treatable causes","When the fundus cannot be seen because of dense blood, B-scan ultrasound is essential to look for a retinal tear or detachment","The loss of the red reflex and inability to visualise retinal detail on ophthalmoscopy indicate significant haemorrhage","Initial management often involves upright rest to allow blood to settle inferiorly and improve the view, with treatment of the underlying cause once identified","Persistent non-clearing haemorrhage or an associated retinal detachment is managed surgically with vitrectomy","In children, non-accidental injury must be considered, and spontaneous haemorrhage warrants a search for an underlying vascular or neovascular cause"],"pathophysiology":"Vitreous haemorrhage is extravasation of blood into or around the vitreous cavity. It occurs when abnormal new vessels (as in proliferative diabetic retinopathy or ischaemic vein occlusion) bleed, when normal retinal vessels are torn during posterior vitreous detachment or a retinal tear, or when trauma ruptures vessels. Blood disperses through the vitreous gel, scattering light and obscuring vision. Clearance is slow because the avascular vitreous reabsorbs blood inefficiently, and long-standing blood can lead to fibrosis and tractional complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Proliferative diabetic retinopathy","Retinal tear or rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Posterior vitreous detachment","Retinal vein occlusion with neovascularisation","Ocular trauma","Age-related macular degeneration with subretinal bleed, or other neovascular disease","Terson syndrome (associated with intracranial haemorrhage)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination of both eyes when the view allows","B-scan ultrasonography when the retina cannot be visualised, to exclude a tear or detachment","Assessment of the fellow eye for clues to the underlying cause (e.g. diabetic changes)","Optical coherence tomography and fluorescein angiography once the media clear","Systemic evaluation for diabetes, hypertension, and bleeding tendency as appropriate"],"redFlags":["An underlying retinal tear or detachment requiring urgent treatment to preserve vision","Sudden profound visual loss with an obscured fundus needing prompt ultrasound assessment","Neovascularisation from proliferative diabetic retinopathy or ischaemic vein occlusion requiring timely treatment","Vitreous haemorrhage in a child raising concern for non-accidental injury","Bilateral haemorrhage or associated neurological symptoms suggesting Terson syndrome"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the cause; haemorrhage from a posterior vitreous detachment often clears well, whereas bleeding from proliferative retinopathy or an associated retinal detachment carries a higher risk of recurrence and permanent visual loss without treatment.","pitfalls":["Failing to perform B-scan ultrasound and missing an underlying retinal detachment behind dense blood","Attributing the bleed to an obvious cause without examining for a retinal tear","Overlooking non-accidental injury in a child with vitreous haemorrhage","Delaying treatment of proliferative retinopathy while awaiting spontaneous clearance"],"management":["Upright rest with head elevation to let blood settle inferiorly and clear the visual axis","Urgently identify and treat any retinal tear or detachment, using laser retinopexy or cryotherapy for tears and surgical repair for detachment","Treat the underlying cause, with panretinal photocoagulation and intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy for proliferative retinopathy","B-scan ultrasound surveillance while the fundus is obscured to exclude a tear or detachment","Pars plana vitrectomy for non-clearing haemorrhage or when an underlying detachment is suspected","Optimise systemic contributors including glycaemic and blood pressure control and review of anticoagulants","Ophthalmology referral with close monitoring for recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vlcad_deficiency":{"name":"Very-long-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VLCAD deficiency","VLCADD","ACADVL deficiency","very-long-chain acyl-coenzyme A dehydrogenase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive defect of ACADVL affecting the first dehydrogenation step for long-chain (C14-C20) acyl-CoA substrates at the inner mitochondrial membrane","Presents as a clinical spectrum: a severe early-onset cardiac form with cardiomyopathy and hypoglycaemia, a hepatic/hypoglycaemic infantile form, and a milder later-onset myopathic form with exertional rhabdomyolysis","Newborn screening hallmark is an elevated tetradecenoylcarnitine (C14:1), often with raised C14 and C14:2 species","Hypoketotic hypoglycaemia occurs with fasting or illness, similar to other long-chain oxidation defects","Older children and adults typically present with exercise-induced muscle pain, myoglobinuria and creatine kinase rises rather than hypoglycaemia","Dietary strategy limits long-chain fat and substitutes medium-chain triglyceride as an energy source, alongside fasting avoidance and an emergency illness regimen","Pre-exertion carbohydrate and avoidance of prolonged strenuous activity reduce rhabdomyolysis in the myopathic phenotype"],"pathophysiology":"Very-long-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase is bound to the inner mitochondrial membrane and initiates beta-oxidation of long-chain fatty acyl-CoA esters. Loss of activity blocks energy production from long-chain fat during fasting, exercise or catabolic stress, so the heart and skeletal muscle, which rely heavily on fatty-acid oxidation, are particularly vulnerable, and hepatic ketogenesis fails during fasting. Accumulating long-chain acyl-CoA and acylcarnitine intermediates are thought to be arrhythmogenic and myotoxic, and residual enzyme activity largely determines whether the phenotype is severe and cardiac or mild and myopathic.","additionalDifferentials":["Other long-chain oxidation defects (LCHAD/mitochondrial trifunctional protein deficiency)","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency (myopathic rhabdomyolysis phenotype)","MCAD deficiency and other short/medium-chain oxidation defects","Primary dilated or hypertrophic cardiomyopathy of other cause","Metabolic myopathies including glycogenoses presenting with exertional intolerance","Sepsis or myocarditis in an infant with cardiac decompensation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma acylcarnitine profile showing elevated C14:1 and related long-chain species","Creatine kinase and urinary myoglobin during suspected rhabdomyolysis","Blood glucose, ketones, ammonia and lactate during acute illness","Echocardiography and ECG when a cardiac phenotype is suspected","ACADVL molecular genetic testing for confirmation","Fibroblast fatty-acid oxidation flux studies or enzyme assay where needed"],"redFlags":["Cardiomyopathy, arrhythmia or pericardial effusion in a decompensating infant","Hypoketotic hypoglycaemia with encephalopathy","Pigmenturia with markedly raised creatine kinase indicating rhabdomyolysis and acute kidney injury risk","Rapid clinical deterioration during intercurrent illness or after prolonged exercise"],"prognosis":"The severe neonatal cardiac form historically carried high mortality, but screen-detected diagnosis with early dietary management has improved outcomes; the myopathic form is compatible with normal life expectancy provided rhabdomyolysis triggers are avoided.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis in an adult presenting only with exertional muscle symptoms","Using unrestricted long-chain fat rather than medium-chain triglyceride substitution","Failing to give generous carbohydrate during illness or before prolonged exercise","Missing evolving cardiomyopathy because the initial presentation was hypoglycaemia"],"management":["Dietary restriction of long-chain fat with medium-chain triglyceride substitution as an alternative energy source","Avoidance of fasting with frequent feeds and slow-release carbohydrate such as uncooked cornstarch overnight","Emergency sick-day regimen with intravenous glucose during illness or catabolic stress to prevent hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and rhabdomyolysis","Manage acute rhabdomyolysis with intravenous fluids and glucose while monitoring renal function and myoglobinuria","Cardiac surveillance with echocardiography and ECG in the cardiac phenotype, treating cardiomyopathy and arrhythmia","Metabolic dietitian and metabolic medicine input, with avoidance of excessive or prolonged exercise as a trigger","Genetic counselling, family screening and newborn-screening of siblings given autosomal recessive inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's OMMBID (Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease)","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"vocal_cord_dysfunction":{"name":"Vocal cord dysfunction","system":"Respiratory / Larynx","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["paradoxical vocal fold motion","inducible laryngeal obstruction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Otolaryngology and respiratory reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Inappropriate adduction of the vocal folds during inspiration causes episodic breathlessness and stridor that mimic asthma","Symptoms characteristically feature difficulty getting air in, throat tightness and inspiratory noise localised to the neck","Attacks are often sudden in onset and offset and may be triggered by exercise, irritants, strong smells or stress","It is frequently misdiagnosed as refractory asthma, leading to escalating and ineffective inhaled therapy","Direct laryngoscopy during an episode, showing paradoxical adduction, is the diagnostic gold standard","Speech and language therapy with breathing retraining is the cornerstone of management"],"pathophysiology":"Vocal cord dysfunction, also termed inducible laryngeal obstruction, involves paradoxical closure of the vocal folds during inspiration, transiently narrowing the glottic airway. Laryngeal hyperresponsiveness to triggers such as reflux, postnasal drip, exercise, airborne irritants and psychological stress is thought to drive the abnormal reflex adduction. Because the obstruction is at the larynx rather than the lower airways, it produces predominantly inspiratory symptoms and does not respond to bronchodilators, distinguishing it from asthma even though the two can coexist.","additionalDifferentials":["Asthma","Exercise-induced bronchoconstriction","Fixed laryngeal or tracheal obstruction","Anaphylaxis with laryngeal oedema","Bilateral vocal cord palsy","Anxiety or panic attacks"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible laryngoscopy, ideally during symptoms or after provocation","Spirometry with attention to truncation of the inspiratory flow-volume loop","Assessment for reflux, rhinosinusitis and asthma as triggers or comorbidities","Exercise or irritant challenge where episodes are provoked","Psychological assessment where relevant"],"redFlags":["Stridor with hypoxia or signs of significant airway compromise","Suspected anaphylaxis","Features suggesting a fixed structural airway lesion","Progressive or persistent (non-episodic) obstruction"],"prognosis":"The condition is not life-threatening in most cases and responds well to breathing retraining and treatment of triggers, though it can be recurrent and disabling if unrecognised.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing it as refractory asthma and escalating inhaled or oral steroids","Failing to capture the larynx during an episode","Overlooking coexisting asthma, reflux or rhinosinusitis","Not involving speech and language therapy early"],"management":["Speech and language therapy with breathing retraining and laryngeal control techniques as the cornerstone of treatment","Identify and treat triggers and comorbidities including reflux, rhinosinusitis with postnasal drip, exercise and airborne irritants","Reassurance and education, teaching rescue breathing manoeuvres for acute episodes rather than relying on bronchodilators","Reassess and de-escalate ineffective escalating inhaled asthma therapy while managing any genuinely coexisting asthma","Psychological support and anxiety management where stress is a relevant trigger","Botulinum toxin injection to the laryngeal muscles in refractory cases","Multidisciplinary ENT, respiratory and speech therapy referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Otolaryngology reference texts (standard reference)","Respiratory medicine reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"vocal_cord_granuloma":{"name":"Vocal fold granuloma","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Laryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vocal cord granuloma","laryngeal granuloma","contact granuloma","intubation granuloma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characteristically arises posteriorly over the vocal process of the arytenoid, where the overlying mucosa is thin and closely applied to cartilage","The main drivers are laryngopharyngeal reflux, chronic throat clearing or cough, and mucosal trauma from endotracheal intubation","Presents with a globus sensation, throat discomfort or odynophonia, chronic throat clearing, and mild hoarseness","Often unilateral but may be bilateral or 'kissing' when the contralateral process is also traumatised","First-line management targets the cause with antireflux measures and voice therapy to reduce hard glottal attack and throat clearing","Surgical excision is reserved for persistent, enlarging, or airway-compromising lesions because recurrence after surgery alone is common","Malignancy is rare in this location but a lesion that fails to settle warrants biopsy to exclude neoplasia"],"pathophysiology":"A vocal fold (contact or vocal process) granuloma is an inflammatory reparative response rather than a true tumour. Mechanical trauma to the thin mucosa overlying the arytenoid vocal process, from forceful vocal contact, chronic cough or throat clearing, or an endotracheal tube, denudes the epithelium and exposes perichondrium. Acid and pepsin refluxing to the posterior larynx impair healing and perpetuate mucosal injury, so exuberant granulation tissue forms. Because the insult is ongoing, granulomas frequently recur unless the underlying reflux and phonotraumatic behaviours are corrected.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal fold polyp","Laryngeal papilloma","Rheumatoid nodule or vasculitic lesion of the larynx","Chondroma of the arytenoid","Tuberculous or fungal laryngeal granuloma","Posterior glottic malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible fibreoptic laryngoscopy to localise the lesion over the vocal process","Videostroboscopy to assess vocal fold vibration and closure","Assessment for laryngopharyngeal reflux, including symptom indices where available","Speech and language therapy voice evaluation for hard glottal attack","Biopsy of persistent or atypical lesions to exclude malignancy or specific granulomatous disease"],"redFlags":["A lesion that continues to enlarge despite adequate antireflux and voice measures","Impaired vocal fold or arytenoid mobility","Airway compromise from a large or bilateral posterior lesion","Systemic features suggesting vasculitis, tuberculosis, or malignancy"],"prognosis":"Many granulomas resolve slowly with medical and behavioural treatment, though this can take months; recurrence is frequent if reflux and phonotrauma are not controlled, whereas long-term outcomes are good once the underlying causes are addressed.","pitfalls":["Proceeding straight to excision without controlling reflux and vocal behaviour, inviting recurrence","Attributing all posterior lesions to reflux and missing an underlying malignancy or systemic disease","Failing to recognise recent intubation as a precipitating cause","Impatience with the naturally slow resolution of granulomas, prompting unnecessary surgery"],"management":["Antireflux therapy with a proton pump inhibitor and reflux-precaution lifestyle measures to allow mucosal healing","Voice therapy to reduce hard glottal attack and habitual throat clearing that perpetuate the lesion","Treat any contributing chronic cough that drives repeated mucosal trauma","Botulinum toxin injection to reduce forceful vocal-process contact in refractory cases","Reserve surgical excision for persistent, enlarging or airway-compromising lesions, or when biopsy is needed, recognising the high recurrence if the cause is uncontrolled","ENT laryngology and speech therapy referral with monitoring for recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"vocal_cord_nodules":{"name":"Vocal cord nodules","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vocal fold nodules","singer's nodules","screamer's nodules"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Vocal cord nodules are small, usually bilateral swellings on the vocal folds caused by chronic voice misuse or overuse","They typically sit at the junction of the anterior third and posterior two-thirds of the fold, the point of maximal contact during phonation","They are common in professional voice users, teachers, singers and habitually loud children","The main symptom is progressive hoarseness, often with vocal fatigue and a breathy quality, worse with use","On laryngoscopy early lesions look soft and reddish while long-standing nodules become firm, white and fibrotic","The mainstay of treatment is voice rest and speech therapy to correct the underlying vocal behaviour, which resolves most early nodules","Surgical excision (microlaryngoscopy) is reserved for mature fibrotic nodules that fail conservative treatment, and must be followed by voice therapy to prevent recurrence","Any hoarseness persisting beyond a few weeks, particularly in adults with risk factors, needs laryngeal examination to exclude malignancy"],"pathophysiology":"Vocal cord nodules form in response to repeated mechanical trauma from vocal abuse. Forceful or excessive vocal fold collision, especially at the midpoint of the membranous cord where impact stress is greatest, causes localised oedema and microhaemorrhage in the superficial lamina propria. With continued strain this reactive change organises into fibrosis and thickening of the overlying epithelium, producing symmetrical nodules on the medial edges of both folds. These masses prevent complete glottic closure and add mass to the vibrating edge, disrupting the mucosal wave and generating hoarseness and a breathy voice. Because both folds strike each other, lesions are characteristically bilateral and roughly symmetrical.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal cord polyp (usually unilateral)","Reinke oedema","Vocal cord cyst","Contact granuloma of the vocal process","Laryngeal papilloma","Early laryngeal carcinoma or leukoplakia","Functional (muscle tension) dysphonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Laryngoscopy (flexible or rigid) to visualise the folds","Videostroboscopy to assess the mucosal wave and glottic closure","Voice assessment and acoustic analysis by a speech and language therapist","Biopsy of any atypical or suspicious lesion to exclude malignancy","Assessment for contributing factors such as reflux, allergy and vocal habits"],"redFlags":["Persistent hoarseness beyond a few weeks in an adult, especially with smoking or alcohol use, requiring exclusion of malignancy","A unilateral, irregular or ulcerated lesion suggesting neoplasia rather than a nodule","Associated stridor, airway compromise or difficulty swallowing","Neck mass or referred otalgia accompanying the voice change","Unexplained weight loss or haemoptysis"],"prognosis":"Early, soft nodules usually resolve completely with voice rest and speech therapy, and the outlook is excellent when vocal behaviour is corrected; mature fibrotic nodules may need surgery and, without behavioural change, nodules commonly recur.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to surgery before an adequate trial of voice therapy","Mistaking a unilateral polyp, cyst or malignancy for benign nodules","Failing to arrange voice therapy after surgery, leading to recurrence","Ignoring persistent adult hoarseness that warrants cancer exclusion","Overlooking contributing factors such as reflux and ongoing vocal abuse"],"management":["Voice rest and speech and language therapy to correct the underlying vocal misuse, which resolves most early nodules","Vocal hygiene measures including hydration and avoidance of shouting and throat clearing to reduce vocal load","Address contributing factors such as reflux and allergy","Phonomicrosurgical excision reserved for mature, fibrotic nodules that fail conservative therapy","Ongoing voice-care and speech therapy to prevent recurrence after treatment","ENT referral with videostroboscopic monitoring of the folds"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"vocal_cord_palsy":{"name":"Vocal cord palsy","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vocal fold paralysis","recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy","laryngeal nerve paralysis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from disruption of the vagus or recurrent laryngeal nerve supply to the intrinsic laryngeal muscles, and may be unilateral or bilateral","Unilateral palsy typically causes a breathy, weak voice and sometimes aspiration, as the healthy cord may compensate over time","Bilateral palsy is more dangerous: the cords may sit near the midline, threatening the airway and causing stridor","The left recurrent laryngeal nerve has a longer intrathoracic course, making it more vulnerable to chest pathology such as mediastinal or lung disease","Common causes include surgical injury (notably thyroid surgery), malignancy along the nerve's course, and neurological or idiopathic causes","A thorough search for the cause is essential, examining the neck, thyroid, chest and skull base along the nerve pathway","Management principles include voice therapy, treating the underlying cause, and procedures such as injection or medialisation to improve glottic closure","In bilateral palsy with airway compromise, securing the airway takes priority, sometimes requiring tracheostomy"],"pathophysiology":"Vocal cord palsy arises from interruption anywhere along the motor pathway to the larynx, from the brainstem nucleus through the vagus and recurrent laryngeal nerves to the intrinsic laryngeal muscles. Loss of innervation impairs abduction and adduction of the affected fold, so it rests in a position determined by the residual muscle tone and any compensatory changes. The recurrent laryngeal nerve's long course, especially on the left where it loops under the aortic arch, exposes it to compression or invasion by neck and chest disease and to iatrogenic injury during surgery. The functional consequence depends on the resting position of the cords and whether one or both are affected.","additionalDifferentials":["Cricoarytenoid joint fixation (e.g. from arthritis or trauma)","Laryngeal carcinoma restricting cord movement","Functional or psychogenic dysphonia","Vocal fold nodules, polyps or scarring","Myasthenia gravis affecting laryngeal muscles","Post-intubation laryngeal injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Laryngoscopy to confirm reduced or absent cord movement and assess position","CT or MRI along the course of the vagus and recurrent laryngeal nerves from skull base to chest","Chest imaging to exclude thoracic causes","Thyroid and neck evaluation, including relevant surgical history","Neurological assessment and laryngeal electromyography in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Bilateral palsy with stridor or respiratory distress requiring urgent airway management","Progressive symptoms with a neck mass, weight loss or haemoptysis suggesting malignancy","Aspiration with recurrent chest infections from poor glottic closure","Associated cranial nerve deficits pointing to a skull base or brainstem lesion"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the cause and whether the palsy is unilateral or bilateral; some idiopathic or postviral unilateral palsies recover, others compensate with voice therapy, while bilateral palsy often needs airway intervention and malignant causes carry the outlook of the underlying disease.","pitfalls":["Labelling a palsy idiopathic without imaging the entire nerve pathway to exclude a tumour","Underestimating airway risk in bilateral palsy","Missing silent aspiration in patients with poor glottic closure","Confusing cricoarytenoid joint fixation or cancer with a neurogenic palsy"],"management":["Identify and treat the underlying cause along the whole nerve course, including malignancy, surgical injury and thoracic disease","Voice therapy to improve glottic closure and compensation in unilateral palsy","Injection medialisation or medialisation thyroplasty for persistent symptomatic unilateral palsy","Swallowing assessment and aspiration precautions given the risk of incomplete glottic closure","Secure the airway in bilateral palsy, which may require tracheostomy, with later cordotomy, arytenoidectomy or lateralisation","Monitor for spontaneous recovery in idiopathic or postviral cases before permanent intervention","ENT laryngology referral for assessment and management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"vocal_cord_paralysis":{"name":"Vocal fold paralysis","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Laryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vocal cord paralysis","recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy","unilateral vocal fold paralysis","bilateral vocal fold paralysis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from impaired function of the vagus or recurrent laryngeal nerve, so establishing the cause along the whole nerve course is essential","Unilateral paralysis typically causes a breathy, weak voice with a poor cough and aspiration risk from incomplete glottic closure","Bilateral paralysis, with both folds near the midline, threatens the airway and can present with stridor and dyspnoea, often with a surprisingly good voice","The left recurrent laryngeal nerve's long intrathoracic course makes it vulnerable to mediastinal and aortic pathology","Iatrogenic injury, especially after thyroid, parathyroid, anterior cervical spine, or cardiothoracic surgery, is a leading cause","Unilateral cases are managed with voice therapy and, where compensation is inadequate, injection augmentation or medialisation thyroplasty","Bilateral paralysis may require a temporary tracheostomy for the airway, with later procedures to enlarge the glottis balanced against voice"],"pathophysiology":"Vocal fold paralysis arises from interruption of the motor supply to the intrinsic laryngeal muscles anywhere along the vagus nerve and its recurrent laryngeal branch, from the brainstem nucleus to the muscle. Because the recurrent laryngeal nerve loops into the thorax, particularly on the left around the aortic arch, lesions in the neck, chest, or skull base can all be responsible; common mechanisms include surgical trauma, malignant infiltration, and neuropathy, with a proportion remaining idiopathic. Loss of adductor and abductor tone leaves the fold in a paramedian or intermediate position. In unilateral loss the mobile fold cannot fully compensate, giving incomplete closure with a breathy voice and aspiration; in bilateral loss both folds sit near the midline, so the voice may be preserved but the airway is critically narrowed.","additionalDifferentials":["Cricoarytenoid joint fixation (e.g. from rheumatoid arthritis or intubation trauma)","Posterior glottic stenosis","Functional (non-organic) dysphonia","Myasthenia gravis affecting the larynx","Central neurological disease affecting vagal nuclei","Laryngeal malignancy restricting fold movement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible fibreoptic laryngoscopy to confirm impaired mobility and fold position","Laryngeal electromyography to distinguish neurogenic paralysis from mechanical fixation and assess prognosis","CT from skull base to mediastinum to trace the vagus and recurrent laryngeal nerve course","Videostroboscopy to assess glottic closure and the mucosal wave","Targeted investigation for thyroid, oesophageal, pulmonary, or mediastinal pathology when no surgical cause is evident"],"redFlags":["Bilateral paralysis with stridor or dyspnoea, an airway emergency","A new paralysis without an obvious surgical cause, mandating a search for malignancy along the nerve","Significant aspiration with recurrent chest infection or weight loss","Accompanying cranial nerve deficits pointing to a skull base or brainstem lesion"],"prognosis":"Idiopathic and some postsurgical unilateral palsies may recover over many months, and most patients achieve a functional voice with therapy or medialisation; bilateral paralysis has a more guarded outlook because improving the airway usually comes at the expense of voice quality.","pitfalls":["Labelling a paralysis idiopathic before adequately imaging the entire course of the vagus and recurrent laryngeal nerve","Mistaking cricoarytenoid joint fixation for neurogenic paralysis without electromyography","Performing a permanent static medialisation too early, before spontaneous recovery has been given time","Underestimating the airway risk of bilateral paralysis and delaying airway protection"],"management":["Establish and treat the cause along the vagus and recurrent laryngeal nerve course, addressing any malignancy or thoracic pathology","Voice therapy for unilateral paralysis to aid compensation and improve glottic closure","Injection augmentation or medialisation thyroplasty, with arytenoid adduction where needed, when compensation is inadequate","Swallowing assessment and aspiration precautions because of poor glottic closure and weak cough","Prioritise the airway in bilateral paralysis, using acute tracheostomy then posterior cordotomy, arytenoidectomy or reinnervation to balance airway and voice","Laryngeal electromyography to assess prognosis and timing before permanent surgery","ENT laryngology and speech therapy referral"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"vocal_cord_pharyngeal_distal_myopathy":{"name":"Vocal cord and pharyngeal distal myopathy (MATR3)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VCPDM","MATR3-related distal myopathy","matrin-3 distal myopathy","distal myopathy with vocal cord and pharyngeal weakness","MPD2"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant adult-onset distal myopathy caused by variants in MATR3, which encodes the nuclear matrix RNA/DNA-binding protein matrin-3","Distinctive for combining distal limb weakness with bulbar features, namely vocal cord weakness causing dysphonia and a nasal voice, and pharyngeal weakness causing dysphagia","Distal weakness typically begins in the hands and feet and progresses slowly, sometimes later extending proximally","Onset is usually in adulthood, often in the fourth to sixth decade","The same MATR3 gene is implicated in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and frontotemporal dementia, so a mixed motor-neuron/myopathic picture can occur and warrants careful evaluation","Respiratory muscle involvement can develop and is an important cause of morbidity, so ventilatory function should be monitored","Creatine kinase is normal or mildly raised; electromyography shows myopathic changes that may coexist with features suggesting neurogenic involvement","Diagnosis relies on the characteristic distal-plus-bulbar phenotype confirmed by MATR3 genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"The disorder is caused by dominant variants in MATR3, encoding matrin-3, an RNA- and DNA-binding protein of the nuclear matrix involved in RNA processing, stability, and transport. Disease-associated variants alter matrin-3 handling of RNA and its protein interactions, leading to abnormal protein accumulation and dysfunction in muscle and, in some individuals, motor neurons. This shared molecular biology explains the overlap with motor neuron disease. In muscle the process causes a distal myopathy with particular vulnerability of laryngeal and pharyngeal muscles, producing the characteristic vocal cord and swallowing involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis with bulbar involvement","Myasthenia gravis with bulbar and limb weakness","Inclusion body myositis with dysphagia","Other distal myopathies (Welander, GNE, Laing)","Myotonic dystrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to characterise myopathic and any neurogenic features","Laryngoscopy and swallowing assessment for vocal cord and pharyngeal weakness","Respiratory function testing including lying and sitting vital capacity","Muscle biopsy where indicated, which may show rimmed vacuoles and myopathic changes","Targeted MATR3 sequencing or a distal myopathy/motor neuron disease gene panel"],"redFlags":["Progressive dysphagia with aspiration risk or weight loss","Declining respiratory function or nocturnal hypoventilation","Features of motor neuron disease such as fasciculations, brisk reflexes, or bulbar wasting","Rapid deterioration atypical for a slowly progressive distal myopathy","Stridor or airway compromise from vocal cord dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Limb weakness usually progresses slowly, but bulbar and respiratory involvement can significantly affect quality of life and survival; prognosis is worse if the phenotype overlaps with motor neuron disease, making swallowing and respiratory surveillance essential.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the bulbar features for oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy or myasthenia gravis","Overlooking respiratory muscle weakness and aspiration risk","Missing an overlapping motor neuron disease phenotype linked to MATR3","Attributing dysphonia solely to a laryngeal cause without recognising the underlying myopathy"],"management":["Supportive multidisciplinary neuromuscular care, as no disease-specific or curative therapy exists","Physiotherapy, occupational therapy, ankle-foot orthoses and mobility aids for progressive distal weakness","Swallowing assessment and dysphagia management with dietary modification, considering gastrostomy if pharyngeal weakness is severe","Voice and communication support for dysphonia from vocal cord weakness","Respiratory surveillance with lying and sitting vital capacity, introducing non-invasive ventilation as respiratory muscles fail","Monitor for overlap with motor neuron disease, warranting careful neurological evaluation","Genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: MATR3-related distal myopathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"vocal_cord_polyp":{"name":"Vocal fold polyp","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Laryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vocal cord polyp","phonotraumatic polyp","haemorrhagic vocal fold polyp"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically a unilateral, broad-based or pedunculated lesion at the mid-membranous vocal fold, arising from phonotrauma or acute vocal overuse","Presents with persistent hoarseness, breathiness, vocal fatigue, and sometimes a sensation of a lump or the need to clear the throat","May be haemorrhagic (following an episode of forceful voicing) or translucent and gelatinous in appearance","Distinguished from vocal nodules, which are usually bilateral and symmetrical and respond better to voice therapy alone","Videostroboscopy shows disruption of the mucosal wave over the lesion and incomplete glottic closure","Smoking and laryngopharyngeal reflux act as aggravating cofactors and should be addressed","Established polyps commonly require microlaryngeal surgical excision with preservation of the vocal ligament, followed by voice rest and therapy"],"pathophysiology":"A vocal fold polyp develops when repeated or violent phonotrauma injures the microvasculature of the superficial lamina propria (Reinke's space), producing localised submucosal oedema, fibrin exudation, and sometimes haemorrhage. Over time the exudate organises into a discrete, often pedunculated mass covered by epithelium. The lesion adds mass and stiffness to the vibrating edge, disturbing the mucosal wave and preventing complete glottic closure, which produces the characteristic breathy, rough voice. Smoking, vocal misuse, and reflux-related mucosal irritation promote and perpetuate the process.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal fold nodules","Reinke oedema (polypoid corditis)","Intracordal vocal fold cyst","Laryngeal papilloma","Vocal fold granuloma","Early glottic carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible fibreoptic laryngoscopy to visualise the lesion and assess mobility","Videostroboscopy to characterise the mucosal wave and glottic closure","Assessment for laryngopharyngeal reflux","Perceptual and acoustic voice assessment by a speech and language therapist","Histological examination of any excised specimen to exclude dysplasia or malignancy"],"redFlags":["A firm, irregular, or leukoplakic lesion in a smoker raising concern for malignancy","Impaired vocal fold mobility, which is not expected with a benign polyp","Progressive dysphonia unresponsive to conservative measures","Cervical lymphadenopathy accompanying a laryngeal lesion"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent; small early polyps may regress with voice therapy and control of aggravating factors, and surgically excised polyps rarely recur when phonotrauma, smoking, and reflux are addressed, with restoration of a near-normal voice in most patients.","pitfalls":["Assuming any exophytic vocal fold lesion is benign without stroboscopy and, where excised, histology","Proceeding to surgery without correcting the underlying vocal behaviour, leading to recurrence","Overlooking coexisting reflux or smoking as perpetuating factors","Confusing a unilateral polyp with bilateral nodules and mismanaging with therapy alone"],"management":["Voice therapy and vocal hygiene, under which small early polyps may regress","Address aggravating cofactors with smoking cessation and antireflux measures","Microlaryngoscopic phonomicrosurgical excision for persistent or larger polyps as the mainstay for established lesions","Postoperative voice therapy to correct phonotrauma and prevent recurrence","Histological examination of any excised specimen to exclude dysplasia or malignancy","ENT laryngology referral with videostroboscopic follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"vocal_process_granuloma":{"name":"Vocal process granuloma","system":"Ear-Nose-Throat / Laryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["arytenoid granuloma","posterior glottic granuloma","contact ulcer granuloma","post-intubation vocal process granuloma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A granulation-tissue lesion over the vocal process of the arytenoid, where thin mucosa lies directly over cartilage and is prone to trauma","The dominant causes are laryngopharyngeal reflux, phonotrauma with hard glottal attack and throat clearing, and mucosal injury from endotracheal intubation","Presents with throat pain or discomfort, a globus sensation, chronic throat clearing, and mild voice change rather than marked hoarseness","Often unilateral but may be bilateral, forming 'kissing' lesions on the opposing vocal processes","First-line treatment is medical and behavioural, combining antireflux measures with voice therapy to reduce injurious vocal habits","Recurrence after excision alone is high, so surgery is reserved for persistent, enlarging, or airway-threatening lesions or when biopsy is needed","In an intubated or recently extubated patient, tube-related trauma is a common precipitant"],"pathophysiology":"A vocal process granuloma is a benign reparative proliferation of granulation tissue rather than a neoplasm. The mucosa over the arytenoid vocal process is thin and firmly bound to underlying cartilage, so it is readily injured by forceful vocal fold contact, chronic cough or throat clearing, or an endotracheal tube. Once the epithelium is breached and perichondrium exposed, refluxed acid and pepsin reaching the posterior larynx impair healing and sustain inflammation, prompting exuberant granulation tissue to form at the site. Persistence of the causative trauma and reflux explains the strong tendency to recur unless these factors are corrected.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal fold polyp","Laryngeal papilloma","Chondroma or chondrosarcoma of the arytenoid","Rheumatoid or vasculitic laryngeal nodule","Tuberculous or fungal granuloma","Posterior glottic carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible fibreoptic laryngoscopy to localise the lesion over the vocal process","Videostroboscopy to assess vibration and glottic closure","Assessment for laryngopharyngeal reflux","Speech and language therapy evaluation of hard glottal attack and throat clearing","Biopsy of persistent or atypical lesions to exclude malignancy or specific granulomatous disease"],"redFlags":["Continued enlargement despite adequate reflux and voice treatment","Impaired arytenoid or vocal fold mobility","Airway compromise from a large or bilateral posterior lesion","Systemic features suggesting vasculitis, tuberculosis, or malignancy"],"prognosis":"Resolution with medical and behavioural treatment is the rule but is often slow over several months, and recurrence is common when reflux and phonotrauma persist; overall outcomes are good once the underlying insults are controlled.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to repeated excision without controlling reflux and vocal behaviour, perpetuating recurrence","Mislabelling a persistent lesion as benign granuloma without histology to exclude malignancy","Failing to recognise recent intubation as the precipitating trauma","Impatience with the naturally slow resolution, prompting unnecessary surgery"],"management":["Antireflux therapy with a proton pump inhibitor plus reflux-precaution lifestyle measures to promote mucosal healing","Voice therapy to eliminate hard glottal attack and habitual throat clearing","Treat any chronic cough contributing to repeated mucosal trauma","Botulinum toxin injection to reduce forceful vocal-process contact in resistant cases","Reserve surgical excision for persistent, enlarging or airway-threatening lesions or diagnostic biopsy, given the high recurrence when the cause is untreated","ENT laryngology and speech therapy referral with monitoring for recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Scott-Brown's Otorhinolaryngology, Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"vogt_koyanagi_harada_disease":{"name":"Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada disease","system":"Rheumatology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VKH disease","VKH syndrome","uveomeningoencephalitic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology / Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A bilateral granulomatous panuveitis accompanied by neurological, auditory, and integumentary features, reflecting an autoimmune attack on melanocyte-containing tissues","More common in pigmented populations, particularly those of Asian, Hispanic, Middle Eastern, and Native American descent","A prodromal phase with headache, meningism, tinnitus, and dysacusis often precedes the ocular disease","The acute uveitic phase causes bilateral serous (exudative) retinal detachments, disc oedema, and choroidal thickening","Later convalescent features include a 'sunset glow fundus' from choroidal depigmentation, poliosis, vitiligo, and alopecia","Cerebrospinal fluid pleocytosis in the acute phase supports the diagnosis when the picture is incomplete","Prompt and prolonged systemic corticosteroids, often with steroid-sparing immunosuppression, help prevent chronic recurrent disease and preserve vision"],"pathophysiology":"Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada disease is a T-cell-mediated autoimmune disorder directed against antigens associated with melanocytes, affecting melanocyte-rich tissues including the uvea, meninges, inner ear, and skin. A genetic predisposition linked to certain HLA types, possibly triggered by a viral or other environmental insult, leads to autoreactive T lymphocytes targeting melanocytic proteins. Granulomatous inflammation of the choroid produces exudative retinal detachments and, in the chronic phase, depigmentation of the choroid and skin, giving rise to the characteristic sunset glow fundus, poliosis, and vitiligo.","additionalDifferentials":["Sympathetic ophthalmia","Sarcoidosis with uveitis","Posterior scleritis","Central serous chorioretinopathy","Acute posterior multifocal placoid pigment epitheliopathy","Infective causes such as syphilis and tuberculosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination and slit-lamp assessment for panuveitis and exudative retinal detachment","Fluorescein angiography showing multiple pinpoint leaks and pooling of dye in subretinal fluid","Optical coherence tomography demonstrating serous retinal detachment and choroidal thickening","Indocyanine green angiography to assess choroidal inflammation","Lumbar puncture showing cerebrospinal fluid lymphocytic pleocytosis in the acute phase","Serology to exclude syphilis, tuberculosis, and sarcoidosis"],"redFlags":["Bilateral rapid visual loss from exudative retinal detachment","Meningism and neurological signs in the prodromal phase","Recurrent or chronic anterior uveitis indicating inadequately treated disease","Development of complications such as glaucoma, cataract, or choroidal neovascularisation"],"prognosis":"With early, aggressive, and sustained immunosuppression many patients retain good vision, but delayed or insufficient treatment leads to chronic recurrent granulomatous uveitis with cataract, glaucoma, subretinal fibrosis, and permanent visual impairment.","pitfalls":["Tapering corticosteroids too quickly and precipitating chronic recurrent disease","Confusing exudative retinal detachment with central serous chorioretinopathy and treating inappropriately","Failing to exclude sympathetic ophthalmia in a patient with prior ocular trauma or surgery","Not recognising the prodromal meningeal or auditory symptoms as part of the syndrome","Overlooking infective mimics such as syphilis before starting immunosuppression"],"management":["Early high-dose systemic corticosteroids, often intravenous pulses followed by a slow oral taper, as the mainstay of acute treatment","Early introduction of steroid-sparing immunomodulators such as mycophenolate, azathioprine, methotrexate or ciclosporin to prevent chronic recurrent disease","Biologic therapy with anti-TNF agents such as adalimumab or infliximab for refractory or recurrent disease","Monitor for and manage ocular complications including cataract, glaucoma and choroidal neovascularisation","Audiological and neurological assessment for auditory and meningeal features in the acute phase","Prolonged, sustained treatment with long-term ophthalmology follow-up to avoid relapse","Uveitis specialist and rheumatology multidisciplinary care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vohwinkel_syndrome":{"name":"Vohwinkel syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mutilating keratoderma","keratoderma hereditaria mutilans","GJB2 (classic Vohwinkel)","LOR (variant Vohwinkel)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mutilating palmoplantar keratoderma characterised by a distinctive honeycomb (honeycomb-patterned) hyperkeratosis of the palms and soles","Starfish-shaped (stellate) keratoses over the knuckles and dorsa of the digits are a highly suggestive sign","Constricting fibrous bands (pseudo-ainhum) form around digits and can progress to autoamputation","The classic form is associated with sensorineural deafness and results from dominant GJB2 (connexin 26) mutations","A variant form (Camisa) has associated ichthyosis and is caused by loronin mutations in the LOR gene","Onset is usually in infancy or early childhood with progression of the keratoderma over time","Management is supportive with emollients/keratolytics and oral retinoids, with surgical release of constricting bands to prevent digital loss"],"pathophysiology":"Vohwinkel syndrome exists in two genetically distinct forms. The classic form with deafness is caused by dominant mutations in GJB2 encoding connexin 26, a gap-junction protein; abnormal gap-junction channels disturb epidermal cell-to-cell communication and cochlear function. The variant form associated with ichthyosis arises from mutations in LOR encoding loricrin, a major component of the cornified cell envelope, leading to defective envelope assembly and abnormal terminal differentiation of keratinocytes. In both, disordered keratinisation produces the honeycomb keratoderma and stellate keratoses, and progressive fibrous constriction (pseudo-ainhum) around digits can strangulate them, causing autoamputation.","additionalDifferentials":["Mal de Meleda (transgrediens palmoplantar keratoderma)","Olmsted syndrome","Papillon-Lefevre syndrome","Other mutilating/constricting keratodermas with pseudo-ainhum","True ainhum (idiopathic constricting band)","Keratoderma associated with KID syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing orthohyperkeratosis and, in the loricrin variant, retained nuclei within the stratum corneum","Molecular genetic testing of GJB2 and LOR","Audiological assessment for sensorineural hearing loss (classic form)","Clinical and photographic monitoring of digital constriction bands","Radiographs of digits where bony involvement or impending autoamputation is suspected","Assessment for associated ichthyosis in the variant form"],"redFlags":["Progressive pseudo-ainhum threatening digital viability or autoamputation","Secondary infection or ulceration of fissured keratotic skin","New or worsening hearing loss requiring audiological input","Functional impairment of the hands limiting activity"],"prognosis":"The keratoderma is chronic and progressive, and the principal threat is loss of digits from constricting bands. With emollient/keratolytic care, oral retinoids and timely surgical band release, digital function can often be preserved, though the condition is not curable.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise and release pseudo-ainhum bands early, allowing digital autoamputation","Not distinguishing the deafness-associated (GJB2) form from the ichthyosis (loricrin) variant, which affects counselling","Overlooking the honeycomb pattern and stellate knuckle keratoses that distinguish it from other keratodermas","Reliance on keratolytics alone when retinoids and surgery may be needed"],"management":["Topical emollients and keratolytics such as urea, salicylic acid and alpha-hydroxy acids to reduce the honeycomb keratoderma","Systemic oral retinoids such as acitretin to soften the keratoderma and relieve or prevent constricting bands","Surgical release of constricting fibrous bands (pseudo-ainhum) to prevent digital strangulation and autoamputation","Regular clinical and photographic surveillance of digital constriction bands","Audiological assessment and hearing rehabilitation with hearing aids in the classic connexin 26 (GJB2) deafness form","Manage associated ichthyosis in the loricrin (LOR) variant","Genetic counselling given autosomal dominant inheritance, with dermatology and clinical genetics multidisciplinary care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"von_gierke_disease":{"name":"Von Gierke disease (glycogen storage disease type I)","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["glycogen storage disease type I","GSD I","glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency","G6PC deficiency","SLC37A4 deficiency","GSD Ia","GSD Ib"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / Scriver's Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder of the terminal step of gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis; type Ia results from glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (G6PC) and type Ib from a defect in the glucose-6-phosphate transporter (SLC37A4)","Presents in infancy with severe fasting hypoglycaemia, marked hepatomegaly, and a doll-like facies with truncal adiposity","The biochemical hallmark is fasting hypoglycaemia accompanied by lactic acidosis, hyperuricaemia, and hyperlipidaemia (raised triglycerides and cholesterol)","Type Ib additionally causes neutropenia and impaired neutrophil function, predisposing to recurrent infections and inflammatory bowel-like disease","Long-term complications include hepatic adenomas (with malignant potential), nephropathy with proteinuria, gout, growth retardation, and osteopenia","Cornerstone of management is maintaining euglycaemia with frequent feeds and uncooked cornstarch therapy while restricting galactose and fructose","Lactate can serve as an alternative cerebral fuel, which may partly protect the brain during hypoglycaemic episodes"],"pathophysiology":"Glycogen storage disease type I is caused by deficiency of the glucose-6-phosphatase system, which catalyses the final common step releasing free glucose from glucose-6-phosphate in both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. In type Ia the catalytic enzyme glucose-6-phosphatase is deficient, whereas in type Ib the microsomal transporter that moves glucose-6-phosphate into the endoplasmic reticulum is defective. As a result the liver, kidney, and intestine cannot release glucose during fasting, causing profound hypoglycaemia. Accumulating glucose-6-phosphate is diverted into glycolysis and the pentose phosphate and lipogenic pathways, generating excess lactate, uric acid, and triglycerides, while glycogen and fat accumulate in the liver and kidney. In type Ib, impaired glucose-6-phosphate transport also disturbs neutrophil function, producing neutropenia.","additionalDifferentials":["Other hepatic glycogen storage diseases (types III, VI, IX)","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase deficiency and other gluconeogenesis defects","Hereditary fructose intolerance and galactosaemia","Fatty acid oxidation disorders causing fasting hypoglycaemia","Hyperinsulinism","Organic acidaemias with lactic acidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood glucose, lactate, uric acid, and lipid profile during controlled fasting","Liver ultrasonography and surveillance imaging for hepatic adenomas","Renal function tests and urinalysis for proteinuria and microalbuminuria","Full blood count for neutropenia in suspected type Ib","Molecular genetic testing of G6PC and SLC37A4","Bone densitometry for osteopenia in longstanding disease"],"redFlags":["Severe symptomatic hypoglycaemia with seizures or reduced consciousness","Severe lactic acidosis during metabolic decompensation","Rapidly enlarging or bleeding hepatic adenoma or features suggesting malignant transformation","Serious infection or gut inflammation in neutropenic type Ib patients"],"prognosis":"With meticulous dietary control to maintain euglycaemia, most patients survive into adulthood with good outcomes; long-term risks of hepatic adenoma with malignant potential, progressive nephropathy, and, in type Ib, infective complications remain the major determinants of prognosis.","pitfalls":["Failing to prevent nocturnal hypoglycaemia by omitting overnight feeding or cornstarch","Overlooking neutropenia and its complications in type Ib","Missing surveillance for hepatic adenoma and renal disease during long-term follow-up","Giving galactose- or fructose-containing feeds that worsen the metabolic derangement"],"management":["Maintain euglycaemia as the cornerstone: frequent daytime carbohydrate feeds with uncooked cornstarch, plus overnight continuous nasogastric or gastrostomy glucose feeds in infancy","Restrict galactose and fructose and provide a complex-carbohydrate-based diet to limit lactate and lipid generation, guided by a metabolic dietitian","Treat metabolic sequelae: allopurinol for hyperuricaemia and gout, lipid-lowering therapy with statins or fibrates for hyperlipidaemia, and citrate supplementation for nephrolithiasis","In type Ib, correct neutropenia and neutrophil dysfunction with granulocyte colony-stimulating factor and treat the inflammatory-bowel-like disease","Give prompt intravenous dextrose during intercurrent illness, vomiting or hypoglycaemia to avert acute metabolic decompensation and lactic acidosis","Structured surveillance with serial hepatic imaging for adenomas, renal function and urinary protein monitoring with an ACE inhibitor for proteinuric nephropathy, and bone densitometry for osteopenia","Refer for liver transplantation for hepatic adenoma with malignant transformation or intractable metabolic control, and offer renal transplantation for end-stage nephropathy","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance with testing of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","Scriver's The Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"von_hippel_lindau":{"name":"Von Hippel-Lindau disease","system":"Oncology / Genetic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VHL","von Hippel-Lindau syndrome","VHL-related tumour syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / NCCN & genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by germline variants in the VHL tumour suppressor gene, inherited autosomal dominantly with high penetrance and a substantial de novo rate","Hallmark lesions are haemangioblastomas of the retina and central nervous system, especially the cerebellum, brainstem, and spinal cord","Retinal haemangioblastomas can be detected early by fundoscopy and treated to prevent visual loss, making regular ophthalmic surveillance essential","Clear cell renal cell carcinoma and renal cysts are common and are a leading cause of cancer death, so renal imaging surveillance is central to management","Phaeochromocytomas and paragangliomas occur and must be excluded biochemically before any surgery or during pregnancy to avoid catastrophic hypertensive crises","Other features include pancreatic cysts and neuroendocrine tumours, endolymphatic sac tumours causing hearing loss, and epididymal or broad ligament cystadenomas","Surveillance is lifelong and multi-organ, combining periodic imaging of the brain, spine, abdomen, and eyes with biochemical screening for catecholamine excess","Nephron-sparing surgery is favoured for renal tumours to preserve renal function given the high risk of new and bilateral tumours"],"pathophysiology":"The VHL protein is part of a complex that targets hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF) for degradation under normal oxygen conditions. Loss of VHL function leads to constitutive HIF accumulation as if the tissue were hypoxic, driving overexpression of vascular endothelial growth factor and other proangiogenic and growth factors. This produces the highly vascular haemangioblastomas and promotes the development of clear cell renal carcinoma and the other characteristic tumours through inappropriate angiogenesis and cell proliferation.","additionalDifferentials":["Hereditary phaeochromocytoma-paraganglioma syndromes (SDHx and others)","Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2","Tuberous sclerosis complex","Hereditary papillary or other familial renal cell carcinoma syndromes","Sporadic haemangioblastoma or renal cell carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Germline VHL sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis with genetic counselling","MRI of the brain and spine for haemangioblastoma surveillance","Abdominal imaging (MRI, CT, or ultrasound) for renal, pancreatic, and adrenal lesions","Dilated retinal examination for retinal haemangioblastomas","Plasma or urinary metanephrines to screen for phaeochromocytoma/paraganglioma, with audiological assessment for endolymphatic sac tumours"],"redFlags":["Unrecognised phaeochromocytoma precipitating a hypertensive crisis under anaesthesia or in pregnancy","Enlarging or symptomatic central nervous system haemangioblastoma causing raised intracranial pressure or cord compression","Growing renal mass exceeding the threshold for intervention with metastatic potential","New visual, neurological, or hearing symptoms in a known gene carrier"],"prognosis":"With lifelong surveillance and timely intervention, many complications are preventable and life expectancy is substantially improved, but renal cell carcinoma and central nervous system disease remain the principal causes of death. Outcome depends heavily on adherence to multi-organ screening and on nephron-sparing, function-preserving management.","pitfalls":["Operating on a patient without first excluding phaeochromocytoma, risking a hypertensive crisis","Performing radical rather than nephron-sparing surgery and hastening renal failure given recurrent bilateral tumours","Attributing hearing loss to common causes and missing an endolymphatic sac tumour","Lapses in the lifelong multi-organ surveillance schedule allowing tumours to present late"],"management":["Offer the HIF-2-alpha inhibitor belzutifan for VHL-associated renal cell carcinoma, central nervous system haemangioblastomas and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours not requiring immediate surgery","Manage renal cell carcinoma with nephron-sparing partial nephrectomy or ablation, and keep small renal lesions under active surveillance to preserve renal function","Treat retinal haemangioblastomas early with laser photocoagulation, cryotherapy or anti-VEGF agents to prevent visual loss","Resect or use stereotactic radiosurgery for symptomatic or enlarging central nervous system haemangioblastomas","Screen biochemically for phaeochromocytoma with plasma or urinary metanephrines and give alpha-adrenergic blockade before any surgery or pregnancy to avert hypertensive crisis","Coordinate lifelong multi-organ surveillance including brain and spine MRI, abdominal imaging, dilated retinal examination and audiology through a specialist multidisciplinary team","Provide genetic counselling and cascade testing of at-risk relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","NCCN guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"von_hippel_lindau_disease":{"name":"Von Hippel-Lindau disease","system":"Genetics / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VHL","VHL syndrome","familial cerebelloretinal angiomatosis","VHL gene"],"chapter":null,"source":"Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics / relevant specialty textbook","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant tumour-predisposition syndrome caused by germline loss-of-function variants in the VHL tumour-suppressor gene on chromosome 3p, with tumours arising after a somatic second hit","Hallmark lesions are retinal and central nervous system haemangioblastomas, especially of the cerebellum, brainstem and spinal cord","Clear-cell renal cell carcinoma and renal cysts are a major cause of morbidity and mortality and are frequently bilateral and multifocal","Phaeochromocytomas and paragangliomas may be the presenting feature and can be catecholamine-secreting, so screening before any surgery is essential","Endolymphatic sac tumours can cause hearing loss and tinnitus, and pancreatic serous cystadenomas and neuroendocrine tumours also occur","The VHL protein regulates degradation of hypoxia-inducible factors, so loss drives a pseudohypoxic, pro-angiogenic state that explains the vascular tumour spectrum","Lifelong multisystem surveillance from childhood with ophthalmoscopy, abdominal imaging, catecholamine testing and periodic neuroaxis MRI underpins management"],"pathophysiology":"Von Hippel-Lindau disease results from inactivating germline variants in the VHL tumour-suppressor gene, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with tumour formation following a somatic second hit in susceptible tissues (Knudson two-hit model). The VHL protein forms part of an E3 ubiquitin ligase complex that targets the alpha subunits of hypoxia-inducible factor for oxygen-dependent degradation. When VHL function is lost, hypoxia-inducible factor accumulates even under normoxia, driving transcription of pro-angiogenic and growth factors such as vascular endothelial growth factor and erythropoietin. This pseudohypoxic drive underlies the characteristic highly vascular tumours, including haemangioblastomas and clear-cell renal cell carcinoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Sporadic haemangioblastoma or isolated renal cell carcinoma","Hereditary phaeochromocytoma-paraganglioma syndromes (SDHx, MAX, TMEM127)","Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2","Neurofibromatosis type 1","Tuberous sclerosis complex with renal angiomyolipomas and cysts","Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome with renal tumours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Germline molecular genetic testing of the VHL gene, including deletion/duplication analysis","Dilated retinal examination for retinal haemangioblastomas","Contrast-enhanced MRI of brain and spine for haemangioblastomas","Cross-sectional abdominal imaging (MRI or ultrasound) for renal, pancreatic and adrenal lesions","Plasma or urinary fractionated metanephrines to detect catecholamine-secreting tumours","Audiology and dedicated internal auditory meatus imaging if endolymphatic sac tumour suspected"],"redFlags":["Unrecognised catecholamine-secreting phaeochromocytoma precipitating hypertensive crisis during surgery or pregnancy","Rapidly enlarging or symptomatic central nervous system haemangioblastoma causing raised intracranial pressure or cord compression","New or growing solid renal lesion suggesting renal cell carcinoma requiring intervention","Progressive visual loss from retinal haemangioblastoma"],"prognosis":"With structured lifelong surveillance and timely intervention, many complications can be treated before they become life-threatening, though renal cell carcinoma and central nervous system haemangioblastomas remain the principal causes of reduced life expectancy. Outcomes have improved substantially with early detection programmes.","pitfalls":["Operating on a renal or other tumour without first excluding an occult phaeochromocytoma","Attributing an isolated haemangioblastoma or renal cell carcinoma to sporadic disease and missing the underlying syndrome","Failing to offer predictive genetic testing and surveillance to at-risk relatives","Overlooking endolymphatic sac tumours as a treatable cause of hearing loss"],"management":["Use the HIF-2-alpha inhibitor belzutifan as systemic therapy for VHL-associated renal cell carcinoma, haemangioblastomas and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours where surgery is not immediately required","Treat clear-cell renal cell carcinoma with nephron-sparing partial nephrectomy or ablation and monitor small bilateral, multifocal lesions with active surveillance","Ablate retinal haemangioblastomas with laser photocoagulation, cryotherapy or anti-VEGF therapy to preserve sight","Manage symptomatic or growing central nervous system haemangioblastomas with microsurgical resection or stereotactic radiosurgery","Exclude catecholamine-secreting phaeochromocytoma or paraganglioma with fractionated metanephrines and institute alpha-blockade before surgery or pregnancy","Deliver structured lifelong surveillance of the brain, spine, eyes, kidneys, pancreas, adrenals and hearing through a coordinated multidisciplinary team","Offer genetic counselling with germline VHL testing and cascade screening of relatives given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics","GeneReviews: Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"von_willebrand_disease":{"name":"Von Willebrand disease","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VWD","von Willebrand disease","vWD","von Willebrand factor deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common inherited bleeding disorder, caused by a quantitative or qualitative defect in von Willebrand factor, which mediates platelet adhesion and carries and stabilises factor VIII","The bleeding pattern is that of primary haemostatic failure: mucocutaneous bleeding with easy bruising, epistaxis, menorrhagia, and prolonged bleeding after dental or surgical procedures","Type 1 is a partial quantitative deficiency (most common and usually mild), type 2 comprises qualitative variants, and type 3 is a severe near-complete deficiency with the heaviest bleeding","Because von Willebrand factor protects factor VIII, low levels can produce a modestly prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time alongside normal platelet count","Diagnosis relies on measuring von Willebrand factor antigen, its functional (ristocetin cofactor or equivalent) activity, and factor VIII, with multimer analysis to subtype","Factor levels fluctuate with stress, pregnancy, oestrogens, and inflammation, so borderline results may need repeat testing","Desmopressin releases stored von Willebrand factor and is useful in type 1 and many type 2 cases but is ineffective or inappropriate in type 3 and certain subtypes","When desmopressin is unsuitable, von Willebrand factor-containing concentrates and antifibrinolytic agents are the mainstays for bleeding or procedures; an acquired form can occur with other disorders"],"pathophysiology":"Von Willebrand factor is a large multimeric glycoprotein synthesised by endothelial cells and megakaryocytes. It bridges platelets to exposed subendothelial collagen at sites of vascular injury (particularly under high shear) and serves as the carrier that protects circulating factor VIII from degradation. In VWD, reduced quantity (types 1 and 3) or impaired function (type 2 variants) of this factor impairs platelet adhesion and aggregation, and the loss of factor VIII protection lowers its level, adding a secondary coagulation defect. The result is defective primary haemostasis with a mucocutaneous bleeding phenotype. Acquired von Willebrand syndrome can arise from adsorption onto abnormal cells, high shear (e.g. aortic stenosis), or autoantibodies.","additionalDifferentials":["Haemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency)","Platelet function disorders (e.g. Glanzmann thrombasthenia, storage pool disease)","Immune thrombocytopenia and other causes of thrombocytopenia","Acquired von Willebrand syndrome","Vascular/connective tissue causes of mucocutaneous bleeding","Vitamin K deficiency or liver disease affecting coagulation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film to assess platelet number and morphology","Coagulation screen including activated partial thromboplastin time","Von Willebrand factor antigen level and functional (ristocetin cofactor/collagen-binding) activity","Factor VIII coagulant activity","Von Willebrand factor multimer analysis and ristocetin-induced platelet aggregation to define subtype","Repeat testing when results are borderline, accounting for stress, pregnancy, and hormonal influences"],"redFlags":["Severe or life-threatening haemorrhage, including gastrointestinal or intracranial bleeding, particularly in type 3 disease","Uncontrolled menorrhagia causing haemodynamic compromise or severe anaemia","Major unplanned surgery or trauma in a patient with a significant factor deficiency","New or worsening bleeding suggesting acquired von Willebrand syndrome linked to another disease"],"prognosis":"Most patients, especially those with type 1 disease, have a mild course and a normal life expectancy with appropriate peri-procedural planning; type 3 and severe type 2 disease carry a higher lifelong bleeding burden but are manageable with modern replacement and adjunctive therapy.","pitfalls":["Excluding VWD on the basis of a normal activated partial thromboplastin time or normal platelet count","Missing the diagnosis because factor levels are transiently normalised by stress, pregnancy, or oestrogen use","Giving desmopressin in type 2B (which can worsen thrombocytopenia) or in type 3 (where it is ineffective)","Overlooking acquired von Willebrand syndrome in older patients or those with lymphoproliferative, myeloproliferative, or cardiac conditions","Failing to plan replacement or antifibrinolytic cover before invasive procedures"],"management":["Use desmopressin to release endogenous von Willebrand factor for bleeding or minor procedures in type 1 and responsive type 2 disease, after confirming response with a therapeutic trial","Give von Willebrand factor-containing concentrate for type 3, severe type 2, major bleeding or major surgery where desmopressin is ineffective or unsuitable","Add the antifibrinolytic tranexamic acid for mucosal bleeding, epistaxis, dental extraction and menorrhagia","Control menorrhagia with hormonal measures such as the combined oral contraceptive or a levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system alongside haemostatic therapy","Avoid antiplatelet agents and NSAIDs, and plan peri-procedural haemostatic cover in advance","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal recessive type 3 and family testing, with patient education and a medical-alert record","Manage through a specialist haemophilia or haematology centre with an individualised bleeding-management plan"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"vulvar_cancer":{"name":"Vulvar cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vulval cancer","vulvar carcinoma","vulvar squamous cell carcinoma","cancer of the vulva"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most are squamous cell carcinomas and arise through two main routes: an HPV-associated pathway seen in younger women and an HPV-independent pathway linked to lichen sclerosus and chronic inflammation in older women","It commonly presents as persistent vulvar itching, a lump, ulcer, or plaque, sometimes with bleeding, pain, or discharge, and diagnosis is often delayed by patient reluctance and repeated empirical treatment","Any persistent or atypical vulvar lesion should undergo biopsy rather than prolonged treatment for presumed dermatitis or infection","Spread occurs predominantly to inguinofemoral lymph nodes, so accurate groin node assessment is critical to staging and prognosis","Sentinel lymph node biopsy is used in selected early tumours to reduce the morbidity of full inguinofemoral lymphadenectomy","Surgery is the mainstay for localised disease, with wide local excision or vulvectomy and appropriate groin node management guided by tumour depth and size","Radiotherapy, often combined with chemotherapy, is used for advanced disease, positive nodes, or to preserve function and reduce the extent of surgery","Precursor vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia and lichen sclerosus warrant surveillance because of their malignant potential"],"pathophysiology":"Vulvar squamous cell carcinoma develops from the vulvar epithelium by two principal mechanisms. In younger women, persistent high-risk human papillomavirus infection drives usual-type vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia that can progress to invasive cancer. In older women, chronic dermatological conditions such as lichen sclerosus produce differentiated vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia and HPV-independent carcinoma. Invasive tumour infiltrates local structures and spreads first to superficial and deep inguinofemoral nodes and subsequently to pelvic nodes, with distant metastasis occurring late.","additionalDifferentials":["Vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia (pre-invasive disease)","Lichen sclerosus or lichen planus","Vulvar dermatitis or eczema","Genital warts (condylomata acuminata)","Bartholin gland cyst or abscess","Vulvar melanoma, Paget disease, or basal cell carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy of the vulvar lesion for histological diagnosis and depth of invasion","Examination of the inguinal regions and vulva under adequate visualisation, with colposcopy where indicated","Pelvic MRI to assess local extent and nodal involvement","CT or PET-CT for regional and distant staging","Sentinel lymph node evaluation in appropriately selected early-stage tumours"],"redFlags":["A persistent vulvar lump, ulcer, or plaque that does not resolve with treatment","Chronic vulvar itching or bleeding in an older woman with lichen sclerosus","Palpable or fixed inguinal lymph nodes","Rapidly enlarging or ulcerating vulvar lesion"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is largely determined by inguinofemoral nodal status and tumour stage; node-negative localised disease has good survival after surgery, whereas nodal involvement or advanced local spread substantially reduces outcomes.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating a persistent vulvar lesion as dermatitis or thrush without biopsy","Failing to assess and manage inguinal lymph nodes appropriately","Neglecting surveillance of lichen sclerosus and vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia","Underestimating the psychosexual and functional impact when planning surgery"],"management":["Treat localised disease surgically with wide local excision or radical vulvectomy achieving adequate tumour-free margins","Assess the groin according to tumour depth and size using sentinel lymph node biopsy in selected early tumours and inguinofemoral lymphadenectomy for higher-risk disease","Offer definitive or adjuvant chemoradiotherapy, with cisplatin-based radiosensitisation, for advanced, node-positive or unresectable tumours","Give palliative systemic therapy for metastatic or recurrent disease not amenable to local treatment","Provide supportive care with meticulous wound management, lymphoedema physiotherapy and psychosexual support","Treat the underlying dermatosis, applying a potent topical corticosteroid for lichen sclerosus, and undertake structured surveillance for recurrence","Coordinate care through a specialist gynaecological oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","DeVita's Cancer: Principles & Practice of Oncology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vulvar_intraepithelial_neoplasia":{"name":"Vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VIN","usual-type VIN","differentiated VIN","vulvar high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion","vulval HSIL"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology textbook and colposcopy guidance on vulval premalignant disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Usual-type VIN (vulval high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion) is driven by high-risk human papillomavirus, expresses p16, and typically affects younger women, often with multifocal or multicentric disease involving cervix or anus","Differentiated VIN is HPV-independent, usually arises on a background of lichen sclerosus or lichen planus in older women, and carries a disproportionately high and rapid risk of progression to invasive squamous carcinoma","Symptoms are non-specific and include persistent itch, soreness, or a visible lesion, but a proportion are asymptomatic and found incidentally","Lesions are clinically variable, ranging from white, red, or pigmented plaques to raised or eroded areas, so appearance alone cannot exclude malignancy","Diagnosis rests on adequate biopsy, and any suspicious or non-healing vulval lesion should be sampled rather than treated empirically","Vulvoscopy after application of acetic acid, with directed biopsies, helps map the extent of disease and identify occult invasion","Management is individualised and may include local surgical excision, ablative treatment, or topical immune-modulating therapy for usual-type disease, whereas differentiated VIN generally warrants excision because of its cancer risk","Long-term surveillance is essential because recurrence and new lesions are common, particularly in immunosuppressed women and those with persistent HPV"],"pathophysiology":"Vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia describes dysplastic change confined to the vulval squamous epithelium above the basement membrane. Two distinct pathways are recognised. Usual-type disease follows persistent infection with high-risk human papillomavirus, which through its oncoproteins disrupts cell-cycle control and produces the surrogate marker of diffuse p16 positivity; it tends to occur in younger women and is frequently multifocal. Differentiated VIN is unrelated to HPV and instead develops within chronically inflamed skin, classically lichen sclerosus, where cumulative genetic damage in the basal keratinocytes drives neoplasia; despite often subtle histology it progresses to invasive squamous carcinoma more readily and more quickly than the usual type.","additionalDifferentials":["Lichen sclerosus","Lichen planus of the vulva","Invasive vulval squamous cell carcinoma","Vulval Paget disease","Condylomata acuminata (genital warts)","Vulval melanocytic lesions or melanoma","Chronic vulval dermatoses and candidal or contact dermatitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Vulval biopsy for histological confirmation and grading","Vulvoscopy with acetic acid to delineate lesion extent and guide directed biopsies","p16 and HPV testing to distinguish usual-type from differentiated disease","Colposcopic assessment of the cervix, vagina, and anus given multicentric potential","Assessment of the perianal region and mapping biopsies where multifocal disease is suspected","Screening for immunosuppression, including HIV testing where clinically appropriate"],"redFlags":["Ulceration, induration, bleeding, or a nodular lesion suggesting occult invasion","Rapidly enlarging or non-healing lesion, particularly on a background of lichen sclerosus","Differentiated VIN histology, which mandates prompt excision because of high malignant potential","Recurrent or persistent disease in an immunosuppressed woman","New pigmented lesion raising concern for melanoma"],"prognosis":"Usual-type VIN may occasionally regress but frequently persists or recurs and can progress to invasive carcinoma, whereas differentiated VIN carries a substantially higher and faster risk of malignant transformation; with adequate treatment and lifelong surveillance most women avoid invasive cancer, though recurrence is common.","pitfalls":["Treating a suspicious vulval lesion empirically without biopsy and missing invasive carcinoma","Overlooking differentiated VIN because its histological changes are subtle and the lesion may look like benign lichen sclerosus","Failing to examine the cervix, vagina, and anus in usual-type disease with multicentric potential","Discharging women prematurely when recurrence and metachronous lesions are common","Attributing persistent vulval itch to candidiasis or dermatitis and delaying diagnosis"],"management":["Excise differentiated VIN and any lesion with suspected occult invasion by wide local excision with histological margin assessment","Use ablative treatment such as carbon dioxide laser for multifocal usual-type disease to preserve vulval anatomy and function","Offer topical imiquimod as a tissue-sparing option for usual-type, HPV-associated disease","Treat the underlying lichen sclerosus or lichen planus driving differentiated VIN with a potent topical corticosteroid","Advise smoking cessation and HPV vaccination, and optimise management of any immunosuppression","Screen for multicentric disease at the cervix, vagina and anus given field change","Maintain lifelong vulvoscopic surveillance with a low threshold to re-biopsy non-healing or changing lesions, coordinated through a specialist vulval clinic"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference text on vulval disease","National colposcopy and vulval premalignancy management guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"vulvodynia":{"name":"Vulvodynia","system":"Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vulval pain syndrome","provoked vestibulodynia","localised vulvodynia","generalised vulvodynia","vestibulodynia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard gynaecology textbook and vulval pain society guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Vulvodynia is chronic vulval pain, usually burning, stinging, or rawness, present for at least several months with no identifiable clinical or laboratory cause, making it a diagnosis of exclusion","It is subclassified by site into localised (most often provoked vestibulodynia at the vestibule) and generalised forms, and by whether pain is provoked, spontaneous, or mixed","Provoked vestibulodynia typically causes entry dyspareunia and pain on tampon insertion or tight clothing, with tenderness reproduced by light touch of the vestibule","Generalised vulvodynia produces more diffuse, often spontaneous neuropathic pain that may be unprovoked and less clearly localised","Examination is frequently normal apart from focal tenderness, and the cotton-swab (Q-tip) test helps map painful points at the vestibule","Associated features often include pelvic floor muscle overactivity, anxiety, and other chronic pain conditions, reflecting a component of central sensitisation","Management is multimodal and generic, combining vulval care advice, pelvic floor physiotherapy, psychological and pain-focused therapies, topical or systemic neuromodulating treatment, and occasionally surgery for refractory localised disease","The diagnosis should only be made after infective, dermatological, neurological, and neoplastic causes of vulval pain have been excluded"],"pathophysiology":"Vulvodynia is understood as a chronic pain condition rather than a structural disease. In localised provoked vestibulodynia, increased density and sensitisation of nociceptive nerve fibres in the vestibular mucosa, together with local inflammatory and hormonal influences, lower the threshold for pain so that normally innocuous touch is felt as burning. Peripheral sensitisation is compounded by amplification within the spinal cord and brain (central sensitisation) and by reflex overactivity and guarding of the pelvic floor musculature. Generalised vulvodynia is thought to reflect a more neuropathic, spontaneous pain process. Psychological factors and comorbid pain syndromes modulate the experience but are not the primary cause.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent vulvovaginal candidiasis or other infection","Lichen sclerosus, lichen planus, or contact dermatitis","Vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia or malignancy","Pudendal neuralgia or other neuropathy","Atrophic vulvovaginitis from oestrogen deficiency","Pelvic floor myofascial dysfunction","Herpes simplex or postherpetic neuralgia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cotton-swab (Q-tip) test to localise and grade vestibular tenderness","Vaginal swabs and microscopy to exclude infection","Vulval examination for dermatoses, with biopsy of any abnormal area","Assessment of pelvic floor tone and tenderness","Oestrogen status assessment in peri- or postmenopausal women","Screening for anxiety, depression, and comorbid chronic pain to guide holistic care"],"redFlags":["Visible ulcer, mass, or persistent abnormal lesion requiring biopsy to exclude malignancy","Neurological deficit or dermatomal pain suggesting a specific neuropathy","Features of active infection or a dermatosis that would reclassify the pain as having an identifiable cause","Rapidly worsening pain or systemic symptoms"],"prognosis":"Vulvodynia is a chronic relapsing condition, but many women improve substantially with a combined, individualised approach; provoked vestibulodynia in particular can respond well to physiotherapy and neuromodulation, though complete and permanent resolution is not guaranteed.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating presumed thrush without confirming infection and overlooking a pain syndrome","Labelling the pain as purely psychological and failing to address peripheral and pelvic floor contributions","Making the diagnosis without first excluding dermatoses, infection, and neoplasia","Neglecting pelvic floor assessment, which is frequently abnormal and treatable","Offering surgery before adequate conservative and medical management in localised disease"],"management":["Institute pelvic floor physiotherapy with biofeedback to reduce muscle overactivity and guarding in provoked vestibulodynia","Trial topical therapies including local anaesthetic ointment and, where atrophy contributes, topical oestrogen","Use neuromodulatory oral agents such as a tricyclic antidepressant or a gabapentinoid for neuropathic and generalised pain","Offer psychological therapy such as cognitive behavioural therapy and sex or couples therapy to address the central sensitisation component","Advise vulval care with irritant avoidance and bland emollients","Reserve surgical vestibulectomy for carefully selected refractory localised provoked vestibulodynia","Manage through a multidisciplinary vulval pain service and escalate persistent or refractory pain to specialist pain management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard gynaecology reference text on vulval pain","Vulval pain society consensus guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"vulvovaginal_candidiasis":{"name":"Vulvovaginal Candidiasis","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vaginal thrush","candidal vaginitis","yeast infection","monilial vaginitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most infections are caused by Candida albicans, a commensal yeast that becomes symptomatic when the vaginal environment or host defences change","Typical features are intense vulval itching, soreness, and a thick, white, non-malodorous discharge often likened to cottage cheese, with vulval erythema and sometimes fissuring","The vaginal pH usually remains normal and acidic, which helps distinguish it from bacterial vaginosis and trichomoniasis","Predisposing factors include recent antibiotic use, pregnancy, diabetes, immunosuppression, and high oestrogen states","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by microscopy showing budding yeasts and pseudohyphae, and culture is useful in recurrent or treatment-resistant cases to identify non-albicans species","Uncomplicated disease responds well to topical or oral antifungal therapy, whereas recurrent disease often needs a longer induction and maintenance approach","Recurrent or resistant infection should prompt evaluation for diabetes, immunosuppression, and non-albicans species such as Candida glabrata, which are less responsive to standard azoles","It is not classed as a sexually transmitted infection, and routine treatment of asymptomatic male partners is not indicated"],"pathophysiology":"Candida species colonise the vagina asymptomatically in many women, kept in check by the resident lactobacillus-dominant flora and local immunity. Disturbance of this balance, for example by antibiotics, or increased glycogen availability from high oestrogen states and hyperglycaemia, allows the yeast to proliferate and transform into an invasive hyphal form. Adherence to and penetration of the vaginal epithelium triggers a brisk inflammatory response, producing the pruritus, erythema, and discharge characteristic of symptomatic infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial vaginosis","Trichomonas vaginalis infection","Contact or irritant vulvar dermatitis","Lichen sclerosus or lichen planus","Genital herpes","Physiological discharge"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Vaginal pH measurement, typically normal in candidiasis","Saline and potassium hydroxide wet-mount microscopy for yeasts and pseudohyphae","Fungal culture in recurrent, severe, or treatment-resistant cases to speciate","Blood glucose or HbA1c testing where recurrent infection suggests diabetes","Consideration of HIV or immunosuppression assessment in refractory disease"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or refractory infection suggesting undiagnosed diabetes or immunosuppression","Severe vulval swelling, fissuring, or ulceration prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Systemic candidiasis in significantly immunocompromised patients","Failure to respond to standard azole therapy suggesting a non-albicans species","Coexisting symptoms pointing to another sexually transmitted infection"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated vulvovaginal candidiasis responds rapidly to antifungal treatment with an excellent outlook. A minority of women develop recurrent disease that is troublesome and requires prolonged maintenance therapy, and outcomes in these cases improve when underlying predisposing factors and non-albicans species are identified and addressed.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing thrush on symptoms alone and missing bacterial vaginosis or dermatoses","Repeatedly treating recurrent infection without investigating for diabetes or immunosuppression","Assuming all cases are Candida albicans and overlooking azole-resistant species","Unnecessarily treating asymptomatic partners","Attributing chronic vulval symptoms to candidiasis when a dermatological condition is responsible"],"management":["Treat uncomplicated infection with a topical or single-dose oral azole antifungal such as clotrimazole or fluconazole","For recurrent disease, use an induction course followed by a maintenance suppressive azole regimen","For non-albicans or azole-resistant species, switch to an alternative agent such as intravaginal boric acid or nystatin guided by culture","Address predisposing factors by optimising glycaemic control, reviewing antibiotic use and managing immunosuppression","In pregnancy, use topical azoles and avoid oral fluconazole","Provide symptomatic vulval care with a bland emollient and treat associated vulvitis","Investigate recurrent or refractory disease for underlying diabetes or immunosuppression including HIV, with patient education on trigger avoidance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"waardenburg_syndrome":{"name":"Waardenburg syndrome","system":"Ear / Audiology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAX3","MITF","SOX10","auditory-pigmentary syndrome","WS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology / Emery's Principles of Medical Genetics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An auditory-pigmentary syndrome combining sensorineural hearing loss with pigmentary anomalies of the eyes, hair and skin","Characteristic features include heterochromia or brilliant blue irides, a white forelock, patchy skin depigmentation, and early greying","Dystopia canthorum (lateral displacement of the inner canthi) distinguishes type 1 from type 2, in which it is absent","Most commonly autosomal dominant with variable expressivity; PAX3 underlies types 1 and 3, and MITF and SOX10 are associated with type 2","Type 3 (Klein-Waardenburg) adds musculoskeletal limb abnormalities, and type 4 (Waardenburg-Shah) is associated with Hirschsprung disease via SOX10, EDN3 or EDNRB","Hearing loss is congenital, non-progressive and may be unilateral or bilateral, reflecting absence of melanocytes in the stria vascularis","Family examination frequently reveals affected relatives with subtle pigmentary signs"],"pathophysiology":"Waardenburg syndrome arises from mutations affecting the development, migration or survival of neural-crest-derived melanocytes. Melanocytes contribute pigment to the skin, hair and iris and are also essential within the stria vascularis of the cochlea for maintaining the endolymphatic environment needed for normal hearing. Genes such as PAX3, MITF and SOX10 encode transcription factors within this developmental pathway, so their disruption produces both the pigmentary abnormalities and the sensorineural hearing loss. In type 4 the same neural-crest defect also affects enteric ganglion cells, causing aganglionic megacolon (Hirschsprung disease).","additionalDifferentials":["Other syndromic sensorineural hearing loss such as Pendred or branchio-oto-renal syndrome","Piebaldism (KIT mutation) with depigmentation but without hearing loss","Vitiligo and other acquired pigmentary disorders","Tietz syndrome with generalised hypopigmentation and deafness","Isolated congenital sensorineural hearing loss","Hirschsprung disease from non-syndromic causes in type 4 presentations"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Audiometry with age-appropriate testing to characterise hearing loss","Detailed dermatological and ophthalmic examination for pigmentary features and iris colour","Measurement of inner canthal and interpupillary distances to assess dystopia canthorum","Assessment for constipation or bowel obstruction and Hirschsprung disease in suspected type 4","Molecular genetic testing (PAX3, MITF, SOX10 and related genes) to confirm subtype"],"redFlags":["Neonatal bowel obstruction or severe constipation suggesting associated Hirschsprung disease","Bilateral profound hearing loss affecting speech development in an infant","Progressive rather than static hearing loss, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Family history of combined pigmentary and hearing anomalies indicating dominant inheritance"],"prognosis":"Hearing loss is congenital and generally non-progressive, and prognosis is favourable with hearing aids or cochlear implantation and early language support; the pigmentary features are cosmetic, though associated Hirschsprung disease in type 4 requires surgical management.","pitfalls":["Overlooking subtle pigmentary signs when hearing loss is the presenting problem","Confusing dystopia canthorum with true hypertelorism and misclassifying the subtype","Assuming normal hearing in one ear excludes the syndrome, as loss may be unilateral","Failing to consider Hirschsprung disease in a neonate with pigmentary features"],"management":["Provide early auditory rehabilitation with hearing aids, and cochlear implantation for severe-to-profound sensorineural hearing loss, since there is no disease-modifying therapy","Begin early speech and language therapy with educational and communication support for congenital hearing loss","In type 4, resect the aganglionic segment by pull-through surgery for associated Hirschsprung disease and remain vigilant for enterocolitis","In type 3, offer orthopaedic and rehabilitative care for upper-limb contractures and musculoskeletal anomalies","Refer to dermatology and ophthalmology for pigmentary features, advising sun protection of hypopigmented skin","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant inheritance with variable expressivity and cascade testing of relatives","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary team of audiology, clinical genetics and relevant surgical specialties"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Emery's Elements of Medical Genetics and Genomics"],"crossLinks":[]},"waardenburg_syndrome_type_1":{"name":"Waardenburg syndrome type 1","system":"Hearing / Vision genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WS1","Waardenburg syndrome type I"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and audiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Distinguished from type 2 by dystopia canthorum, the lateral displacement of the inner canthi with normally positioned pupils and outer canthi.","Caused by heterozygous PAX3 variants and inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with variable expressivity.","Pigmentary features include a white forelock, premature greying, patchy skin hypopigmentation and heterochromia or brilliant blue irides.","Congenital sensorineural hearing loss occurs but is less frequent than in type 2, reflecting the diagnostic effect of the W index.","A broad high nasal root, medial eyebrow flare and synophrys are characteristic facial signs.","Dystopia canthorum can make the palpebral fissures appear narrow and the interpupillary distance normal despite an increased inner-canthal distance.","The W index, combining canthal and pupillary measurements, is used to confirm dystopia canthorum objectively.","As a neurocristopathy, features arise from deficient melanocyte development; hearing and pigment problems should prompt audiological and ophthalmological review."],"pathophysiology":"Waardenburg syndrome type 1 is an autosomal dominant neurocristopathy caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in PAX3, a paired-box transcription factor essential for the specification, survival and migration of neural crest-derived melanocytes. Melanocytes populate the skin, hair, iris stroma and, critically, the stria vascularis of the cochlea, where they are required for normal endolymphatic homoeostasis and hair-cell function. Haploinsufficiency of PAX3 leads to regional absence or reduction of melanocytes, producing the pigmentary features (white forelock, heterochromia, skin patches) and, when the cochlea is affected, congenital sensorineural hearing loss. PAX3 also influences the development of periocular mesenchyme, and its disruption is associated with dystopia canthorum, the lateral displacement of the inner canthi that clinically separates type 1 from type 2. The disorder shows marked variable expressivity and incomplete penetrance for individual features, so relatives sharing the same variant may display very different combinations of pigmentary, facial and auditory signs.","additionalDifferentials":["Waardenburg syndrome type 2 (no dystopia canthorum, more frequent deafness)","Waardenburg syndrome type 3 (PAX3-related with additional limb/musculoskeletal anomalies)","Waardenburg syndrome type 4 (with Hirschsprung disease)","Piebaldism (KIT-related patchy depigmentation without dystopia canthorum or deafness)","Vitiligo and other acquired depigmenting disorders","Isolated heterochromia iridis of benign or other cause","Tietz syndrome and other MITF-spectrum pigmentary-auditory conditions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Audiological assessment including newborn hearing screening and pure-tone audiometry","Ophthalmological examination documenting iris colour, heterochromia and canthal position","Objective canthal and pupillary measurement to calculate the W index for dystopia canthorum","Molecular genetic testing of PAX3 to confirm the diagnosis","Dermatological review of hypopigmented patches and hair depigmentation","Family assessment and pedigree analysis given autosomal dominant inheritance","Developmental and speech evaluation where hearing loss is present"],"redFlags":["Confirmed congenital sensorineural hearing loss requiring early intervention","Bilateral profound deafness affecting language acquisition","New neurological or bowel symptoms suggesting an alternative Waardenburg subtype","Family history of deafness with pigmentary features indicating at-risk relatives","Progressive visual or ocular concerns beyond stable heterochromia"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is normal and the pigmentary and facial features are non-progressive; the main functional impact is congenital sensorineural hearing loss, which when present is stable and well managed with amplification or cochlear implantation. Prognosis is dominated by auditory rehabilitation outcomes.","pitfalls":["Missing dystopia canthorum and misclassifying the case as type 2","Relying on interpupillary distance alone, which is normal, and overlooking the increased inner-canthal distance","Assuming absent hearing loss excludes the diagnosis given variable penetrance","Attributing the white forelock or heterochromia to unrelated causes","Failing to examine and counsel relatives who may carry the variant with subtle signs"],"management":["Manage the congenital sensorineural hearing loss with early amplification and cochlear implantation for severe-to-profound loss, as no disease-modifying treatment exists","Enrol affected children in early speech, language and educational support programmes","Confirm and monitor hearing from newborn screening onward with age-appropriate audiometry","Refer to ophthalmology and dermatology for pigmentary features, advising sun protection of depigmented skin patches","Provide genetic counselling for autosomal dominant PAX3-related inheritance with variable expressivity and offer testing to relatives","Coordinate care through an audiology, clinical genetics and ophthalmology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics reference texts","Audiology and hearing rehabilitation guidelines","Dysmorphology and neurocristopathy reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"waardenburg_syndrome_type_2":{"name":"Waardenburg syndrome type 2","system":"Hearing / Vision genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WS2","Waardenburg syndrome type II"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and audiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the absence of dystopia canthorum, the key clinical feature separating it from type 1.","Congenital sensorineural hearing loss is more frequent and often more severe than in type 1.","Heterochromia iridis and other pigmentary changes are common and may be the most conspicuous feature.","MITF variants underlie the largest subgroup (WS2A); SNAI2 and SOX10 account for further cases.","It is genetically heterogeneous, so a normal MITF result does not exclude the diagnosis.","SOX10-related type 2 can overlap with type 4 and with neurological features, warranting careful phenotyping.","Because facial dysmorphism is subtler than in type 1, diagnosis often rests on the combination of deafness and pigmentary signs.","Autosomal dominant inheritance predominates with variable expressivity; biallelic SNAI2 has been reported rarely."],"pathophysiology":"Waardenburg syndrome type 2 is a genetically heterogeneous neurocristopathy in which the common thread is deficient development or survival of neural crest-derived melanocytes, but without the periocular changes that cause dystopia canthorum. The largest subgroup results from heterozygous variants in MITF, the master transcriptional regulator of the melanocyte lineage that acts downstream of the developmental pathway involving PAX3 and SOX10; loss of MITF function reduces melanocyte numbers in the skin, hair, iris and cochlear stria vascularis. Additional cases arise from variants in SNAI2 (a transcription factor regulated by MITF) and SOX10 (an early neural-crest transcription factor). Because melanocytes of the stria vascularis are required for endocochlear potential and hair-cell function, their deficiency produces congenital sensorineural hearing loss, which is more penetrant and severe in type 2 than in type 1; the corresponding pigment deficiency gives heterochromia, white forelock and skin depigmentation. The absence of PAX3-dependent periocular mesenchymal effects explains why the inner canthi are normally positioned, defining the type 2 phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Waardenburg syndrome type 1 (dystopia canthorum present, PAX3-related)","Waardenburg syndrome type 4 (with Hirschsprung disease, overlapping SOX10 cases)","Tietz syndrome (MITF-related generalised hypopigmentation with deafness)","Piebaldism (KIT-related depigmentation without deafness)","Non-syndromic congenital sensorineural deafness with incidental heterochromia","Vitiligo and other acquired pigmentary disorders","Albinism-deafness and other pigmentary-auditory syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Audiological testing including newborn screening and pure-tone audiometry","Detailed ophthalmological examination for heterochromia and confirmation of normal canthal position","Canthal and pupillary measurement (W index) to demonstrate absence of dystopia canthorum","Molecular genetic testing of MITF, then SNAI2 and SOX10","Dermatological assessment of pigmentary skin and hair changes","Neurological review where SOX10 involvement or additional features are suspected","Family evaluation and pedigree analysis for autosomal dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Severe or profound congenital sensorineural deafness requiring early intervention","Features suggesting SOX10 involvement, such as neurological or bowel symptoms","Signs of Hirschsprung disease indicating possible type 4 overlap","Family history of deafness with pigmentary features identifying at-risk relatives","Failure of language development pending audiological rehabilitation"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is normal and pigmentary features are stable; functional outcome is determined by the sensorineural hearing loss, which is more commonly significant than in type 1 and benefits from early amplification or cochlear implantation. SOX10-related cases may carry additional prognostic considerations.","pitfalls":["Excluding Waardenburg syndrome because dystopia canthorum is absent, which actually defines type 2","Stopping the genetic workup after a negative MITF test given the heterogeneity","Overlooking a SOX10 variant that predicts neurological or enteric features","Underestimating the higher likelihood and severity of deafness compared with type 1","Missing subtle pigmentary signs when facial dysmorphism is mild"],"management":["Prioritise early auditory rehabilitation, as hearing loss is more frequent and severe than in type 1, with hearing aids and cochlear implantation for profound loss","Provide early speech, language and educational support for the congenital sensorineural hearing loss","Track hearing from newborn screening through serial pure-tone audiometry","Refer to ophthalmology and dermatology for heterochromia and pigmentary skin and hair changes, with sun protection of depigmented areas","In SOX10-related disease, arrange neurological assessment and surveillance for overlapping type 4 and neurological involvement","Offer genetic counselling recognising genetic heterogeneity, so a normal MITF result does not exclude the diagnosis, with cascade testing of relatives","Coordinate care through an audiology, genetics and ophthalmology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics reference texts","Audiology and hearing rehabilitation guidelines","Dysmorphology and neurocristopathy reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"waardenburg_syndrome_type_3":{"name":"Waardenburg syndrome type 3","system":"Dermatology / Otology / Ophthalmology genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WS3","Klein-Waardenburg syndrome","PAX3"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Klein-Waardenburg syndrome is caused by variants in PAX3 — the same gene as type 1 — but is distinguished by musculoskeletal anomalies of the upper limbs.","Limb features include muscle hypoplasia and contractures, flexion contractures, carpal-bone fusion and syndactyly, which are absent in types 1 and 2.","Like type 1, it shows dystopia canthorum — lateral displacement of the inner canthi — quantified with the W index.","Other cardinal features are congenital sensorineural hearing loss and pigmentary changes: a white forelock, heterochromia iridis, patchy skin hypopigmentation and premature greying.","Severe classic cases often reflect homozygous or compound-heterozygous PAX3 variants, whereas milder cases are heterozygous.","PAX3 encodes a transcription factor essential for neural-crest-derived melanocytes and for musculoskeletal development, explaining the combined pigmentary, auditory and limb phenotype.","Early audiological assessment and hearing habilitation are critical to speech and language development.","Inheritance is autosomal dominant, with more severe biallelic forms; genetic counselling is important."],"pathophysiology":"Waardenburg syndrome type 3 is caused by pathogenic variants in PAX3 on chromosome 2q, encoding a paired-box transcription factor that orchestrates the migration, survival and differentiation of neural-crest cells. PAX3 is essential both for melanocyte development — accounting for the pigmentary skin, hair and iris changes and for the cochlear melanocyte-dependent sensorineural hearing loss — and for limb and axial myogenesis, which underlies the distinctive upper-limb musculoskeletal anomalies. Dystopia canthorum, shared with type 1, reflects the role of PAX3 in facial development. The severe Klein-Waardenburg phenotype typically results from biallelic or dominant-negative PAX3 variants that disrupt neural-crest and muscle development more profoundly than the heterozygous variants causing type 1.","additionalDifferentials":["Waardenburg syndrome type 1 (PAX3) — dystopia canthorum but no limb anomalies.","Waardenburg syndrome type 2 (MITF, SOX10 and others) — no dystopia canthorum.","Waardenburg syndrome type 4 / Waardenburg-Shah (EDNRB, EDN3, SOX10) — associated with Hirschsprung disease.","Piebaldism (KIT) — patchy depigmentation with white forelock but without hearing loss.","Tietz syndrome (MITF) — generalised hypopigmentation with deafness.","Oculocutaneous or ocular albinism.","Other syndromes combining congenital sensorineural hearing loss and pigmentary anomalies."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Formal audiological testing, including auditory brainstem responses in infants, for sensorineural hearing loss.","Ophthalmological assessment including measurement of dystopia canthorum (W index) and iris colour.","Molecular genetic testing of PAX3.","Musculoskeletal examination and imaging of the upper limbs for contractures and carpal fusion.","Dermatological assessment of the pigmentary pattern.","Detailed pedigree construction and examination of relatives.","Assessment of bowel function if a type 4 overlap with Hirschsprung disease is suspected."],"redFlags":["Profound congenital sensorineural hearing loss requiring urgent habilitation to protect language development.","Severe limb contractures compromising hand function and needing orthopaedic input.","Constipation, abdominal distension or delayed passage of meconium suggesting Hirschsprung disease and a type 4 overlap."],"prognosis":"Lifespan is normal; morbidity is driven by the sensorineural hearing loss and the upper-limb anomalies. Early hearing habilitation supports language development, and orthopaedic and rehabilitative care improve limb function.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling type 3 as type 1 by overlooking the upper-limb musculoskeletal anomalies that define type 3.","Measuring the inner-canthal relationship incorrectly and misjudging dystopia canthorum.","Failing to identify hearing loss in infancy, delaying essential habilitation.","Assuming all Waardenburg subtypes share one gene rather than confirming PAX3."],"management":["Provide early auditory rehabilitation with hearing aids and cochlear implantation for severe-to-profound sensorineural hearing loss, supported by speech and language therapy","Offer orthopaedic and rehabilitative management of the upper-limb musculoskeletal anomalies, including physiotherapy and surgery for contractures, flexion deformities and syndactyly","Support hand and limb function with occupational therapy and assistive devices","Refer to ophthalmology and dermatology for the pigmentary features, advising sun protection of depigmented skin","Provide genetic counselling for PAX3-related disease, noting that severe classic cases may reflect biallelic variants, with cascade testing of relatives","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary team spanning audiology, clinical genetics, ophthalmology and orthopaedics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical genetics reference texts","Otolaryngology reference texts","Dermatology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"waardenburg_syndrome_type_4":{"name":"Waardenburg syndrome type 4","system":"Hearing / Vision genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WS4","Waardenburg-Shah syndrome","Waardenburg-Hirschsprung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and audiology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Combines the pigmentary and auditory features of Waardenburg syndrome with Hirschsprung disease (aganglionic megacolon).","Neonates may present with delayed passage of meconium, abdominal distension and features of intestinal obstruction alongside pigmentary signs.","Caused by variants in SOX10, EDNRB or EDN3, all central to enteric neural-crest and melanocyte development.","SOX10 variants tend to cause dominant disease with more severe deafness and can extend to neurological involvement (PCWH spectrum).","EDNRB and EDN3 disease may be recessive, with heterozygotes showing milder or isolated features.","Dystopia canthorum is characteristically absent, resembling type 2 in the craniofacial pattern.","The enteric aganglionosis reflects failed colonisation of the distal bowel by neural crest-derived ganglion cells.","Management is multidisciplinary, addressing surgical correction of Hirschsprung disease alongside audiological and genetic care."],"pathophysiology":"Waardenburg syndrome type 4 is a neurocristopathy in which a single developmental defect affects two neural crest-derived populations: melanocytes and enteric ganglion cells. It is caused by variants in SOX10, a transcription factor governing neural-crest maintenance, or in the endothelin signalling pair EDNRB (the endothelin-B receptor) and its ligand EDN3, which are required for the migration and proliferation of enteric neuroblasts and melanoblasts. When endothelin signalling or SOX10 function is deficient, neural crest cells fail to complete their migration to and colonisation of the distal hindgut, leaving a segment of aganglionic bowel that cannot relax and produces the functional obstruction of Hirschsprung disease. The same deficiency reduces melanocyte populations in the skin, hair, iris and cochlear stria vascularis, giving the pigmentary features and sensorineural hearing loss shared with other Waardenburg subtypes. SOX10 is additionally expressed in Schwann cells and oligodendrocytes, so its variants can extend the phenotype to peripheral and central myelination defects. The absence of PAX3 involvement explains why dystopia canthorum is not a feature, and the mode of inheritance varies from autosomal dominant (typically SOX10) to autosomal recessive (biallelic EDNRB or EDN3).","additionalDifferentials":["Waardenburg syndrome type 1 (dystopia canthorum, PAX3, no Hirschsprung disease)","Waardenburg syndrome type 2 (no dystopia canthorum, no Hirschsprung disease)","Isolated (non-syndromic) Hirschsprung disease, commonly RET-related","PCWH syndrome (SOX10 with peripheral neuropathy and central dysmyelination)","Other syndromic causes of neonatal functional intestinal obstruction","Congenital central hypoventilation syndrome with Hirschsprung (Haddad) overlap","Piebaldism or other pigmentary disorders coincident with bowel symptoms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Rectal suction biopsy demonstrating absence of ganglion cells to confirm Hirschsprung disease","Contrast enema to identify the transition zone of aganglionosis","Audiological assessment including newborn screening and pure-tone audiometry","Ophthalmological examination for heterochromia and pigmentary changes","Molecular genetic testing of SOX10, EDNRB and EDN3","Neurological assessment and neuroimaging where SOX10 involvement is suspected","Family evaluation to clarify dominant versus recessive inheritance"],"redFlags":["Neonatal failure to pass meconium with abdominal distension suggesting obstructing Hirschsprung disease","Signs of enterocolitis such as fever, distension and explosive diarrhoea in a child with aganglionosis","Severe or profound congenital sensorineural deafness requiring early intervention","Neurological features (hypotonia, developmental delay, neuropathy) suggesting SOX10 PCWH spectrum","Recurrent obstruction or feeding intolerance indicating surgical urgency"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends largely on timely surgical management of Hirschsprung disease and prevention of enterocolitis; with successful surgery and auditory rehabilitation many do well, though SOX10-related neurological involvement worsens the prognosis. Pigmentary features remain stable and non-progressive.","pitfalls":["Overlooking Hirschsprung disease in a neonate whose pigmentary or auditory signs dominate the picture","Failing to recognise Waardenburg features in an infant investigated only for bowel obstruction","Assuming autosomal dominant inheritance when recessive EDNRB or EDN3 disease is present","Missing the extended neurological (PCWH) phenotype in SOX10-related cases","Delaying rectal biopsy and risking life-threatening enterocolitis"],"management":["Treat the associated Hirschsprung disease by surgical resection of the aganglionic segment with a pull-through procedure","Recognise and manage Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis promptly with rectal washouts, fluid resuscitation and antibiotics as an acute emergency","Provide early auditory rehabilitation with hearing aids and cochlear implantation for the often severe sensorineural hearing loss, alongside speech and language support","In SOX10-related disease, arrange neurological assessment, neuroimaging and surveillance for the broader neurological (PCWH) spectrum","Refer to ophthalmology and dermatology for pigmentary features with sun protection of depigmented skin","Offer genetic counselling addressing both dominant SOX10 and recessive EDNRB or EDN3 inheritance with cascade testing of relatives","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary team including paediatric surgery, gastroenterology, audiology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Clinical genetics reference texts","Paediatric surgery and Hirschsprung disease guidelines","Audiology and neurocristopathy reference sources"],"crossLinks":[]},"wagner_syndrome":{"name":"Wagner syndrome","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Wagner vitreoretinal degeneration","erosive vitreoretinopathy","VCAN","CSPG2"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ryan's Retina / Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant vitreoretinopathy caused by mutations in VCAN (versican), a component of the vitreous extracellular matrix, and unlike Stickler syndrome it lacks systemic skeletal and orofacial features","The defining sign is an optically empty vitreous cavity with avascular condensed membranes or veils, reflecting abnormal vitreous architecture","Chorioretinal atrophy, perivascular pigmentary change and progressive retinal pigment epithelium and choroidal thinning develop over time (the erosive vitreoretinopathy phenotype)","Patients may have myopia, early cataract, ectopic or dragged fovea and reduced night vision","Risk of retinal detachment is increased, though generally lower than in Stickler syndrome","Electroretinography may show progressive reduction in rod and cone responses as chorioretinal degeneration advances","Management is refractive correction, cataract surgery when indicated, lifelong monitoring for retinal detachment, and genetic counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Wagner syndrome is caused by dominant mutations in VCAN, which encodes versican, a large proteoglycan of the vitreous extracellular matrix. Abnormal splicing and altered versican disrupt the normal collagen-hyaluronan gel structure of the vitreous, producing liquefaction and the characteristic optically empty cavity with residual avascular membranes. Over time there is progressive chorioretinal and retinal pigment epithelial atrophy, giving the erosive vitreoretinopathy picture, together with an increased predisposition to retinal detachment.","additionalDifferentials":["Stickler syndrome (with systemic features)","High myopia with vitreoretinal degeneration","Erosive vitreoretinopathy as a phenotypic variant","Familial exudative vitreoretinopathy","Goldmann-Favre / enhanced S-cone syndrome","Marshall syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed slit-lamp and dilated examination documenting the optically empty vitreous and avascular veils","Optical coherence tomography for foveal ectopia and chorioretinal atrophy","Full-field electroretinography to monitor progressive photoreceptor dysfunction","Fundus autofluorescence for retinal pigment epithelium atrophy","Molecular genetic testing for VCAN mutations","Refraction and axial length assessment"],"redFlags":["New floaters, flashes or field loss suggesting retinal detachment and needing urgent review","Progressive night blindness and field constriction from advancing chorioretinal atrophy","Early or rapidly progressive cataract affecting vision","Affected relatives requiring examination given dominant inheritance"],"prognosis":"Vision is often reasonable in early life but can decline with progressive chorioretinal atrophy, cataract and the risk of retinal detachment; long-term outcome varies and lifelong surveillance is warranted.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish Wagner syndrome from Stickler syndrome, which has systemic features and a higher detachment risk","Overlooking the increased retinal detachment risk during routine follow-up","Attributing the vitreous changes to simple myopic degeneration","Not offering genetic counselling for a dominantly inherited condition"],"management":["Maintain lifelong vitreoretinal surveillance with regular dilated fundus examination given the raised risk of retinal detachment","Treat retinal breaks and tears prophylactically with laser photocoagulation or cryotherapy, and repair established detachment with vitreoretinal surgery","Correct the associated myopia with refraction and provide low-vision aids as chorioretinal atrophy advances","Undertake cataract surgery when visually significant, with careful planning because of the abnormal vitreous and detachment risk","Educate patients on the warning symptoms of retinal detachment and the need for urgent ophthalmic review","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal dominant VCAN-related disease with family screening","Manage under a vitreoretinal specialist within a coordinated ophthalmic and genetics team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ryan's Retina","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"wagr_syndrome":{"name":"WAGR syndrome","system":"Ophthalmology / Multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WAGR complex","11p13 deletion syndrome","WAGRO syndrome","Aniridia-Wilms tumour association"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical genetics and paediatric oncology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["WAGR is a contiguous gene deletion syndrome of chromosome 11p13 encompassing loss of PAX6 (aniridia) and the adjacent WT1 tumour-suppressor gene (Wilms tumour predisposition and genitourinary maldevelopment).","Any infant with sporadic aniridia, particularly bilateral, should have genetic testing for an 11p13/WT1 deletion, because this single finding determines lifelong Wilms tumour risk.","Nephroblastoma (Wilms tumour) risk is substantially raised, so structured renal ultrasound surveillance is recommended through early childhood.","Genitourinary anomalies such as cryptorchidism, hypospadias, ambiguous genitalia and streak or dysgenetic gonads reflect WT1 haploinsufficiency and carry a gonadoblastoma risk.","WT1 involvement also predisposes to progressive nephropathy (focal segmental glomerulosclerosis) and later renal failure, sometimes emerging after the cancer-surveillance years.","Aniridia is rarely an isolated absent iris; associated foveal hypoplasia, nystagmus, glaucoma, cataract and keratopathy all threaten vision.","The WAGRO variant adds obesity and hyperphagia, attributed to a more telomeric deletion involving the BDNF gene.","Chromosomal microarray defines the extent of the deletion and correlates with phenotype severity, guiding surveillance and genetic counselling."],"pathophysiology":"WAGR syndrome arises from a heterozygous, usually de novo, contiguous gene deletion of the short arm of chromosome 11 at band 11p13. The deleted interval spans several genes; loss of one PAX6 allele disrupts the master transcriptional regulator of eye development, producing aniridia and other ocular malformations, while haploinsufficiency of the neighbouring WT1 gene, which is essential for normal kidney and gonad differentiation, drives predisposition to Wilms tumour and to genitourinary and gonadal maldevelopment. Because WT1 acts as a tumour suppressor, a somatic second hit within surviving nephrogenic rests precipitates nephroblastoma, and the same WT1 loss underlies a distinct glomerulopathy that can progress to renal failure. The size of the deletion determines the phenotype: larger deletions extending telomerically to include the BDNF locus add childhood-onset obesity, hyperphagia and more pronounced neurobehavioural difficulties (the WAGRO variant), whereas the degree of intellectual disability varies with the total gene content lost.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated PAX6-related aniridia without WT1 involvement","Denys-Drash syndrome (WT1 point mutation with nephropathy, Wilms tumour and disorders of sex development)","Frasier syndrome (WT1 intronic mutation with progressive glomerulosclerosis and gonadoblastoma)","Gillespie syndrome (partial aniridia with cerebellar ataxia and intellectual disability)","Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome (overgrowth with Wilms tumour predisposition)","Other anterior segment dysgenesis such as Peters anomaly or Axenfeld-Rieger spectrum","Simpson-Golabi-Behmel syndrome (overgrowth with embryonal tumour risk)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal microarray to detect and define the extent of the 11p13 deletion","FISH or targeted analysis of the WT1/PAX6 region and karyotype","Serial renal ultrasound surveillance for early detection of Wilms tumour","Comprehensive ophthalmological assessment including slit-lamp, intraocular pressure and fundoscopy for foveal hypoplasia","Renal function tests and urinalysis for proteinuria to monitor for nephropathy","Pelvic and gonadal imaging with endocrine assessment for genitourinary anomalies and gonadoblastoma risk","Developmental and neuropsychological evaluation"],"redFlags":["A new palpable abdominal mass or haematuria suggesting Wilms tumour","Proteinuria progressing towards nephrotic range, indicating WT1-related nephropathy and possible renal failure","Raised intraocular pressure or glaucoma threatening remaining vision","Features of gonadoblastoma arising within dysgenetic gonads"],"prognosis":"With structured renal surveillance Wilms tumours are usually detected early and are highly curable, but long-term outlook is tempered by the risk of progressive renal insufficiency, visual impairment from aniridia-related complications and variable intellectual disability. Life expectancy depends largely on renal and oncological outcomes.","pitfalls":["Assuming sporadic aniridia is isolated and thereby missing the Wilms tumour risk that mandates WT1 testing.","Discontinuing renal ultrasound surveillance too early in childhood.","Overlooking late-onset nephropathy and progressive renal failure that can appear after the tumour-surveillance period.","Failing to recognise gonadoblastoma risk in dysgenetic gonads.","Attributing developmental delay solely to visual impairment rather than the underlying deletion."],"management":["Undertake structured renal ultrasound surveillance through early childhood for Wilms tumour, treating detected tumours with nephrectomy and chemotherapy","Manage aniridia-related eye disease with intraocular-pressure-lowering therapy or surgery for glaucoma, cataract surgery, tinted UV-protective lenses and low-vision support","Monitor renal function and urinary protein, using an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin-receptor blocker for proteinuric nephropathy and planning dialysis or transplantation for progression to renal failure","Correct genitourinary anomalies with orchidopexy or hypospadias repair, and remove dysgenetic or streak gonads to pre-empt gonadoblastoma","Provide developmental, educational and behavioural support for intellectual disability","Offer genetic counselling for the usually de novo 11p13 contiguous gene deletion","Coordinate lifelong care through a multidisciplinary team spanning oncology, nephrology, ophthalmology and clinical genetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Principles and practice of medical genetics","Standard paediatric medicine reference texts","Clinical ophthalmology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"waldenstrom_macroglobulinemia":{"name":"Waldenstrom macroglobulinaemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma with IgM paraprotein","WM","MYD88 L265P-associated lymphoma","macroglobulinaemia of Waldenstrom"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / WHO classification of haematolymphoid tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma involving the bone marrow together with a monoclonal IgM paraprotein in the serum","The circulating IgM can produce hyperviscosity, causing headache, visual disturbance, mucosal bleeding and neurological symptoms","Fundoscopy may show dilated, tortuous retinal veins with haemorrhages in symptomatic hyperviscosity","Anaemia, fatigue, lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly are common, and constitutional symptoms may occur","The somatic MYD88 L265P mutation is present in the large majority of cases and strongly supports the diagnosis","IgM-related complications include peripheral neuropathy, cryoglobulinaemia and cold agglutinin activity","Asymptomatic disease is often monitored, while symptomatic disease is treated with regimens that may include BTK inhibition or rituximab-based therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Waldenstrom macroglobulinaemia is a clonal B-cell disorder in which lymphoplasmacytic cells, spanning small lymphocytes to plasma cells, infiltrate the bone marrow and sometimes lymphoid organs and secrete a monoclonal immunoglobulin M. The recurrent MYD88 L265P mutation activates downstream NF-kappaB signalling through the Bruton tyrosine kinase pathway, promoting tumour survival, and CXCR4 mutations modify disease behaviour and treatment response. The large pentameric IgM molecule raises serum viscosity and can behave as a cryoglobulin, cold agglutinin or autoantibody, so many clinical features stem from the physical and immunological properties of the paraprotein rather than tumour bulk alone.","additionalDifferentials":["IgM monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance","Multiple myeloma (particularly IgM myeloma)","Marginal zone lymphoma with plasmacytic differentiation","Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia","Other low-grade B-cell lymphomas with paraprotein","AL amyloidosis with an IgM clone"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum protein electrophoresis and immunofixation to characterise the IgM paraprotein","Serum viscosity measurement when hyperviscosity is suspected","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine with immunophenotyping","Molecular testing for MYD88 L265P (and CXCR4) mutations","Fundoscopy to assess for hyperviscosity retinopathy","Assessment for cryoglobulins, cold agglutinins and neuropathy-associated antibodies"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic hyperviscosity with neurological or visual compromise requiring urgent plasmapheresis","Severe anaemia or cytopenias from marrow infiltration","Progressive peripheral neuropathy","Transformation to a high-grade lymphoma with rapid clinical deterioration"],"prognosis":"Waldenstrom macroglobulinaemia is generally an indolent, treatable but incurable disorder with prolonged survival for many patients; outcome is influenced by age, cytopenias, paraprotein level and molecular features, and hyperviscosity is usually reversible with plasmapheresis.","pitfalls":["Attributing symptoms to anaemia alone and missing hyperviscosity that needs urgent plasmapheresis","Spurious laboratory results, including pseudohyponatraemia and interference, from a high IgM level","Failing to distinguish it from IgM myeloma or marginal zone lymphoma","Overlooking MYD88 testing that clarifies the diagnosis and predicts therapy response"],"management":["Observe asymptomatic disease with active monitoring, reserving treatment for symptomatic or progressive disease","Give first-line systemic therapy with rituximab-based combinations such as rituximab with bendamustine or with cyclophosphamide and dexamethasone","Use a Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitor such as ibrutinib or zanubrutinib, particularly in MYD88-mutated disease","Perform plasma exchange promptly for symptomatic hyperviscosity and withhold single-agent rituximab when the IgM is high to avoid a paraprotein flare precipitating hyperviscosity","Provide supportive care with transfusion for symptomatic anaemia, and manage IgM-related peripheral neuropathy, cryoglobulinaemia and cold agglutinin disease including cold avoidance","Monitor IgM level and serum viscosity and maintain surveillance for histological transformation","Coordinate care through a specialist haematology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours"],"crossLinks":[]},"walker_warburg_syndrome":{"name":"Walker-Warburg syndrome","system":"Nervous system / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WWS","HARD syndrome (hydrocephalus, agyria, retinal dysplasia)","cobblestone lissencephaly (type II)","dystroglycanopathy","POMT1","POMT2","FKTN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most severe congenital muscular dystrophy-dystroglycanopathy, defined by the triad of a brain malformation (cobblestone lissencephaly), eye anomalies, and congenital muscular dystrophy","Cobblestone (type II) lissencephaly results from over-migration of neurons beyond the pial surface, producing a bumpy, disorganised cortex distinct from classic lissencephaly","Neuroimaging typically shows cobblestone cortex, hydrocephalus, a kinked or hypoplastic brainstem (the Z-shaped brainstem), and cerebellar hypoplasia or cysts","Ocular malformations are prominent and may include microphthalmia, cataract, retinal dysplasia or detachment, persistent foetal vasculature, and anterior chamber anomalies","The congenital muscular dystrophy causes marked hypotonia and weakness from birth with a raised serum creatine kinase","It is autosomal recessive, caused by variants in genes for O-glycosylation of alpha-dystroglycan such as POMT1, POMT2, and FKTN, disrupting the dystroglycan-extracellular matrix link","Diagnosis integrates the characteristic MRI, ophthalmological findings, raised creatine kinase, and molecular testing of dystroglycanopathy genes"],"pathophysiology":"Walker-Warburg syndrome is a dystroglycanopathy caused by biallelic variants in genes required for O-linked glycosylation of alpha-dystroglycan. Defective glycosylation prevents alpha-dystroglycan from binding extracellular matrix ligands such as laminin, disrupting the integrity of basement membranes. In the developing brain, breaches of the pial-glial limiting membrane allow neurons to over-migrate beyond the cortex, producing cobblestone lissencephaly, brainstem and cerebellar malformation, and hydrocephalus. The same defect impairs the muscle basement membrane, causing congenital muscular dystrophy, and disrupts ocular development, causing the retinal and anterior segment anomalies.","additionalDifferentials":["Muscle-eye-brain disease, a milder dystroglycanopathy on the same spectrum","Fukuyama congenital muscular dystrophy","Classic (type I) lissencephaly from LIS1 or DCX","Congenital hydrocephalus of other cause","Other congenital muscular dystrophies with brain involvement","Congenital infection causing brain and eye malformation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI for cobblestone cortex, brainstem kinking, cerebellar anomaly, and hydrocephalus","Serum creatine kinase, which is characteristically elevated","Detailed ophthalmological examination for retinal dysplasia and anterior segment anomalies","Molecular genetic testing of dystroglycanopathy genes such as POMT1, POMT2, and FKTN","Muscle biopsy with alpha-dystroglycan glycosylation studies where genetics are inconclusive","Prenatal ultrasound and foetal MRI for hydrocephalus and brain malformation in at-risk pregnancies"],"redFlags":["Progressive hydrocephalus requiring neurosurgical assessment","Severe respiratory and feeding difficulty from profound muscle weakness","Refractory seizures","Rapidly progressive visual loss from retinal detachment"],"prognosis":"Walker-Warburg syndrome carries the worst prognosis of the dystroglycanopathies, with most affected children dying within the first years of life from neurological, respiratory, and feeding complications. Care is largely supportive alongside genetic counselling.","pitfalls":["Confusing cobblestone (type II) lissencephaly with classic (type I) lissencephaly, which has different genetics and no primary muscle or eye disease","Overlooking the raised creatine kinase and muscle involvement when focused on the brain malformation","Missing treatable ocular complications such as retinal detachment","Failing to place the child on the dystroglycanopathy spectrum, affecting prognosis and counselling"],"management":["Recognise there is no disease-modifying therapy and centre care on supportive and palliative management given the very poor prognosis","Treat hydrocephalus with ventriculoperitoneal shunting to relieve raised intracranial pressure","Support nutrition and airway protection with gastrostomy feeding for swallowing difficulty and recurrent aspiration","Control seizures with anticonvulsant therapy","Provide respiratory support and prompt treatment of intercurrent chest infections","Refer to ophthalmology for treatable ocular anomalies such as cataract and retinal detachment","Offer genetic counselling for autosomal recessive inheritance with prenatal diagnosis in at-risk pregnancies, and involve paediatric neurodisability and palliative care teams"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Volpe's Neurology of the Newborn","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","GeneReviews: Congenital Muscular Dystrophy Dystroglycanopathies"],"crossLinks":[]},"walled_off_pancreatic_necrosis":{"name":"Walled-off pancreatic necrosis","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WON","walled-off necrosis","organised pancreatic necrosis","mature necrotic collection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mature, encapsulated collection of necrotic pancreatic and peripancreatic tissue that develops typically about four weeks after an episode of necrotising pancreatitis","Distinguished from a pseudocyst by its heterogeneous content, which includes solid debris rather than pure fluid, under the revised Atlanta classification","May be sterile or become infected; infected necrosis is suggested by clinical deterioration, sepsis, or gas within the collection on imaging","Contrast-enhanced CT shows an encapsulated collection with variable solid and liquid components in or around the pancreas","Asymptomatic sterile collections can be managed conservatively, as many resolve or remain stable without intervention","When drainage is needed, a step-up approach is favoured, beginning with percutaneous or endoscopic drainage before escalating to minimally invasive necrosectomy","Endoscopic transmural drainage with lumen-apposing metal stents and, where required, direct endoscopic necrosectomy has become a mainstay of management"],"pathophysiology":"Walled-off pancreatic necrosis is a late local complication of necrotising pancreatitis. During the acute illness, ischaemic and enzymatic injury produces necrosis of pancreatic parenchyma and surrounding fat, generating an acute necrotic collection containing both liquefied and solid material. Over the following weeks a fibrous and granulation-tissue wall matures around this collection, converting it into an organised, encapsulated cavity that still contains devitalised solid debris. The retained necrotic tissue can become secondarily infected by gut-derived organisms, and the collection may erode adjacent structures, obstruct the stomach or duodenum, compress the bile duct, or communicate with the pancreatic duct, producing persistent leakage.","additionalDifferentials":["Pancreatic pseudocyst","Acute necrotic collection (before maturation of the wall)","Pancreatic abscess","Cystic pancreatic neoplasm with debris","Duodenal or gastric outlet obstruction from other causes","Infected peripancreatic haematoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT to define the extent, wall maturity, and solid content of the collection","MRI or endoscopic ultrasound to better characterise solid debris and guide drainage","Inflammatory markers and blood cultures if infection is suspected","Image-guided aspiration for culture when infected necrosis is in question","Assessment of pancreatic duct integrity (for example MRCP) to detect disconnected duct syndrome","Monitoring of nutritional status and glycaemic control"],"redFlags":["Clinical deterioration, persistent fever, or sepsis suggesting infected necrosis","Gas within the collection on imaging","Gastric outlet or biliary obstruction from mass effect","Haemorrhage into the collection or a pseudoaneurysm of a splanchnic vessel","Rapid enlargement of the collection"],"prognosis":"Sterile, asymptomatic collections often resolve or stabilise without intervention and carry a good outlook; infected necrosis substantially increases morbidity and mortality, though outcomes have improved with a minimally invasive step-up strategy and delayed intervention where possible.","pitfalls":["Intervening too early before the wall has matured, which increases complications","Mistaking walled-off necrosis for a simple pseudocyst and attempting drainage of solid debris through inadequate routes","Overlooking a disconnected pancreatic duct, which predisposes to persistent or recurrent collections","Failing to recognise vascular complications such as pseudoaneurysm before drainage","Delaying antibiotics and source control once infection is established"],"management":["Manage sterile, asymptomatic collections conservatively, as many resolve or remain stable, while supporting nutrition preferably by the enteral route and optimising glycaemic control","Delay any intervention where possible until the collection wall has matured, which reduces procedural morbidity","Treat infected or symptomatic necrosis with a step-up approach, beginning with endoscopic ultrasound-guided transmural drainage or percutaneous catheter drainage","Give broad-spectrum antibiotics with good pancreatic penetration, such as a carbapenem, for confirmed or strongly suspected infected necrosis","Escalate to minimally invasive necrosectomy, whether endoscopic or video-assisted retroperitoneal debridement, when drainage alone fails to control sepsis","Identify and manage disconnected pancreatic duct syndrome and treat local complications such as gastric outlet or biliary obstruction and haemorrhage","Coordinate care through a hepatobiliary and pancreatology multidisciplinary team with interventional radiology and therapeutic endoscopy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Revised Atlanta classification of acute pancreatitis","Guidelines on the step-up management of necrotising pancreatitis"],"crossLinks":[]},"wandering_spleen":{"name":"wandering spleen","system":"Haematological / Reticuloendothelial system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ectopic spleen","splenoptosis","floating spleen"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sabiston Textbook of Surgery / Grainger & Allison's Diagnostic Radiology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Arises from absence or laxity of the splenic suspensory ligaments (gastrosplenic, splenorenal and phrenocolic), allowing the spleen to migrate on a long, mobile vascular pedicle","May be congenital, from failure of the dorsal mesogastrium to fuse, or acquired through multiparity, hormonally mediated ligamentous laxity or splenomegaly","Typically presents in children and women of reproductive age as a mobile palpable abdominal or pelvic mass, intermittent pain, or an acute abdomen when the pedicle twists","Torsion of the pedicle is the feared complication, producing venous congestion followed by arterial infarction of the spleen","Cross-sectional imaging shows an empty splenic fossa with an ectopically sited spleen; a whirl sign of the twisted pedicle with absent Doppler flow indicates torsion","Splenopexy to fix a viable spleen is preferred, especially in children, to preserve immune function, with splenectomy reserved for a non-viable infarcted organ","May coexist with gastric volvulus, intestinal malrotation and diaphragmatic or abdominal wall defects, reflecting shared abnormal visceral fixation","A palpable notched mass that can be manually returned towards the left upper quadrant is a helpful clinical clue"],"pathophysiology":"The spleen is normally anchored in the left upper quadrant by peritoneal ligaments derived from the dorsal mesogastrium. When these ligaments fail to develop or become lax, the spleen loses its fixation and hangs from an elongated pedicle carrying the splenic vessels, so that it can migrate freely into the abdomen or pelvis. The mobile, narrow pedicle is prone to rotation; torsion first obstructs the thin-walled splenic vein, producing congestion and enlargement, and if it progresses occludes the arterial supply, causing ischaemia and infarction. Chronic partial torsion may instead produce venous congestion with hypersplenism, while the elongated vessels can also compromise the pancreatic tail and predispose to associated gastric volvulus.","additionalDifferentials":["Ovarian or other adnexal mass or torsion","Mesenteric or omental cyst","Retroperitoneal or gastrointestinal tumour","Ectopic or ptotic kidney","Acute appendicitis when the spleen lies in the right or lower abdomen","Intra-abdominal lymphadenopathy or lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen and pelvis, assessing the splenic fossa and the vascular pedicle","Doppler ultrasound of the ectopic spleen to assess perfusion and the twisted pedicle","Technetium sulphur-colloid or heat-damaged red cell scintigraphy to confirm splenic tissue and viability","MRI, favoured in children to limit ionising radiation","Full blood count to detect cytopenias from hypersplenism","Assessment for associated gastric volvulus or malrotation when clinically suspected"],"redFlags":["Acute severe abdominal pain with a tender mobile mass, indicating pedicle torsion","Signs of infarction with peritonism and systemic upset","Haemodynamic instability suggesting infarction with haemorrhage","Retching with inability to pass a nasogastric tube, suggesting associated gastric volvulus"],"prognosis":"When recognised and treated before infarction, splenopexy carries an excellent outcome and preserves splenic immune function. Delayed diagnosis of torsion leads to infarction and loss of the spleen, with the attendant lifelong risks of hyposplenism.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the ectopic spleen for a tumour and proceeding to biopsy or inappropriate resection","Delayed recognition of torsion, converting a salvageable spleen into a non-viable one","Performing splenectomy when splenopexy would have preserved function, particularly in children","Omitting post-splenectomy immunisation and antibiotic prophylaxis when the spleen must be removed"],"management":["Perform splenopexy to fix a viable spleen in its normal position, preferred especially in children to preserve immune function","Operate as an emergency on acute pedicle torsion, undertaking detorsion and splenopexy if the spleen is viable","Reserve splenectomy for a non-viable, infarcted spleen or established torsion with infarction","When splenectomy is unavoidable, give pre- and post-splenectomy pneumococcal, meningococcal and Haemophilus vaccination with antibiotic prophylaxis against overwhelming post-splenectomy infection","Look for and manage associated gastric volvulus or malrotation at the time of surgery","Monitor the full blood count for cytopenias arising from hypersplenism","Educate patients to seek urgent review for acute abdominal pain suggesting torsion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","Grainger & Allison's Diagnostic Radiology"],"crossLinks":[]},"warfarin_related_nephropathy":{"name":"Anticoagulant-related nephropathy","system":"Renal / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["warfarin-related nephropathy","WRN","anticoagulant-related nephropathy","ARN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology / Brenner and Rector's The Kidney","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acute kidney injury occurring in over-anticoagulated patients, classically with a supratherapeutic INR on warfarin, and also reported with direct oral anticoagulants","Thought to result from glomerular haemorrhage with red cells filling and obstructing the tubules as red-cell casts","Biopsy shows numerous red-cell casts occluding tubules with acute tubular injury, often on a background of pre-existing chronic kidney disease","Presents as an otherwise unexplained rise in creatinine coinciding with excessive anticoagulation, frequently with haematuria","Pre-existing renal disease, diabetes, hypertension and older age increase susceptibility","It is a diagnosis of exclusion after ruling out other causes of acute kidney injury and glomerular bleeding","Management is correction of over-anticoagulation, careful review of the need for and intensity of therapy, and supportive care, with biopsy reserved for uncertain cases"],"pathophysiology":"Anticoagulant-related nephropathy is proposed to arise when excessive anticoagulation permits bleeding across the glomerular filtration barrier, allowing red cells to pass into Bowman's space and the tubular lumina. The red cells aggregate with Tamm-Horsfall protein to form obstructing red-cell casts in the tubules, causing tubular obstruction, direct epithelial injury and reduced glomerular filtration. Underlying glomerular disease or endothelial and basement-membrane fragility, as in diabetes or hypertension, predisposes to this glomerular haemorrhage, explaining why it occurs largely in patients with pre-existing renal impairment. Oxidative tubular injury from haem and impaired repair in a chronically damaged kidney may compound the acute insult.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute interstitial nephritis","Acute tubular injury from ischaemia or nephrotoxins","Rapidly progressive (crescentic) glomerulonephritis","Atheroembolic renal disease","IgA nephropathy or other glomerular disease with macroscopic haematuria","Obstructive uropathy from clot"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["INR or anticoagulant activity level and review of anticoagulant dosing","Serial renal function and urinalysis with microscopy for red cells and red-cell casts","Exclusion of glomerulonephritis with relevant serology (ANCA, anti-GBM, complement)","Renal imaging to exclude obstruction and assess kidney size","Assessment of pre-existing chronic kidney disease and comorbidities","Renal biopsy in selected cases showing red-cell casts and tubular injury"],"redFlags":["Marked or persistent supratherapeutic anticoagulation with rising creatinine","Heavy macroscopic haematuria or clot obstruction","Rapidly progressive renal failure prompting exclusion of glomerulonephritis","Bleeding elsewhere indicating dangerous over-anticoagulation"],"prognosis":"Anticoagulant-related nephropathy is associated with incomplete renal recovery, accelerated progression of underlying chronic kidney disease and increased mortality, particularly in those with pre-existing renal impairment. Prompt correction of over-anticoagulation and attention to comorbidities improve the chance of stabilising function, but a proportion sustain a permanent decline.","pitfalls":["Overlooking excessive anticoagulation as the cause of unexplained acute kidney injury","Attributing haematuria solely to urological causes and missing glomerular bleeding","Failing to exclude a coexisting glomerulonephritis before ascribing injury to anticoagulation","Not reassessing the intensity and indication for anticoagulant therapy","Assuming full renal recovery once the INR is corrected"],"management":["Correct over-anticoagulation promptly by withholding the anticoagulant and reversing warfarin with vitamin K or prothrombin complex concentrate according to the bleeding risk","Reassess the ongoing indication and target intensity of anticoagulation, tighten monitoring and consider switching agent to minimise recurrence","Provide supportive acute kidney injury care by avoiding nephrotoxins, optimising volume status and renal perfusion, and dose-adjusting renally cleared drugs","Manage the underlying chronic kidney disease and its drivers, controlling blood pressure and diabetes and using renin-angiotensin blockade where appropriate","Exclude and treat other causes of glomerular haematuria and acute kidney injury, since this remains a diagnosis of exclusion supported by biopsy in selected cases","Monitor renal function and urinalysis serially and provide renal replacement therapy if injury is severe","Refer to nephrology for ongoing assessment and management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology","Brenner and Rector's The Kidney"],"crossLinks":[]},"warfarin_skin_necrosis":{"name":"Warfarin-induced skin necrosis","system":"Clinical pharmacology / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["coumarin-induced skin necrosis","warfarin necrosis","coumarin necrosis","protein C deficiency skin necrosis","anticoagulant-induced skin necrosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Goldman-Cecil Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare but serious complication of warfarin, typically arising within the first few days of starting treatment","Presents with painful erythema progressing to haemorrhagic bullae and skin necrosis, favouring fatty areas such as the breasts, buttocks and thighs","It reflects a transient hypercoagulable state caused by rapid depletion of the vitamin K-dependent natural anticoagulant protein C","Underlying protein C or protein S deficiency predisposes to the reaction","The risk is increased by large loading doses of warfarin without concurrent parenteral anticoagulation","Overlapping with a parenteral anticoagulant (such as heparin) when initiating warfarin reduces the risk in susceptible patients","Management includes stopping warfarin, giving vitamin K, providing alternative anticoagulation and, in protein C deficiency, protein C or fresh frozen plasma replacement","It must be distinguished from heparin-induced skin necrosis and calciphylaxis"],"pathophysiology":"Warfarin inhibits the vitamin K-dependent gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors II, VII, IX and X and of the natural anticoagulants protein C and protein S. Because protein C has a short half-life, similar to that of factor VII, its anticoagulant activity falls rapidly in the first days of treatment, before the procoagulant factors with longer half-lives are fully depleted. This creates a transient net hypercoagulable state, which is exaggerated in patients with pre-existing protein C (or protein S) deficiency and by large initial doses. The resulting thrombosis of dermal and subcutaneous small vessels causes ischaemia, haemorrhagic infarction and full-thickness skin necrosis in areas rich in subcutaneous fat. Concurrent parenteral anticoagulation during initiation bridges this vulnerable period.","additionalDifferentials":["Heparin-induced skin necrosis and heparin-induced thrombocytopenia with thrombosis","Calciphylaxis","Necrotising soft tissue infection","Purpura fulminans from sepsis or severe protein C deficiency","Vasculitis with cutaneous infarction","Cholesterol or other embolic skin necrosis","Antiphospholipid syndrome-related skin necrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Coagulation profile including INR","Protein C and protein S activity (interpreted with awareness of warfarin effect)","Platelet count and heparin-induced thrombocytopenia testing where relevant","Skin biopsy showing dermal vessel thrombosis without vasculitis","Thrombophilia screen once anticoagulation allows","Assessment for underlying malignancy or antiphospholipid syndrome"],"redFlags":["Rapidly expanding necrotic skin lesions","Extensive full-thickness necrosis requiring surgical debridement","Signs of systemic thrombosis or purpura fulminans","Necrosis over the breast, buttock or thigh appearing soon after warfarin initiation"],"prognosis":"Early recognition and treatment can limit tissue loss, but established necrosis may require debridement or reconstructive surgery and can cause significant morbidity. Prompt correction of the coagulopathy and appropriate anticoagulation improve outcomes.","pitfalls":["Using large warfarin loading doses without bridging parenteral anticoagulation in at-risk patients","Interpreting protein C and S levels without accounting for the warfarin effect","Mistaking the lesions for infection and delaying anticoagulation review","Failing to provide protein C or plasma replacement in known protein C deficiency"],"management":["Stop warfarin immediately and reverse its effect with vitamin K to halt ongoing microvascular thrombosis","Replete the deficient natural anticoagulant with fresh frozen plasma or protein C concentrate, which also helps correct the coagulopathy","Maintain anticoagulation for the original thrombotic indication with a parenteral agent such as heparin or low-molecular-weight heparin to bridge the vulnerable initiation period","Provide analgesia and meticulous wound care, with surgical debridement, skin grafting or reconstruction for established full-thickness necrosis","Investigate and treat underlying prothrombotic states such as protein C or protein S deficiency, antiphospholipid syndrome or occult malignancy","If oral anticoagulation is later essential, reintroduce warfarin cautiously at a low starting dose under parenteral anticoagulant cover, or use an alternative such as a direct oral anticoagulant","Refer jointly to haematology, dermatology and plastic surgery, and escalate if necrosis extends or systemic thrombosis develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","Goldman-Cecil Medicine"],"crossLinks":[]},"warm_autoimmune_haemolytic_anaemia":{"name":"Warm autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["warm AIHA","warm antibody haemolytic anaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Immune destruction of red cells by autoantibodies, usually IgG, that bind optimally at body temperature","The direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is positive, typically for IgG with or without complement","The blood film shows spherocytes and features of haemolysis with reticulocytosis, raised lactate dehydrogenase, raised unconjugated bilirubin and low haptoglobin","It may be primary (idiopathic) or secondary to lymphoproliferative disease, autoimmune disorders such as lupus, or drugs","Extravascular haemolysis predominates, with red cells removed in the spleen","Corticosteroids are first-line, with rituximab, splenectomy and other immunosuppression for refractory disease"],"pathophysiology":"In warm autoimmune haemolytic anaemia, IgG autoantibodies bind red cell surface antigens at 37 degrees Celsius. Antibody-coated cells are recognised by splenic macrophages through Fc receptors and are partially or wholly phagocytosed, producing spherocytes and predominantly extravascular haemolysis. The bone marrow compensates with brisk reticulocytosis. Secondary forms arise when an underlying lymphoproliferative or autoimmune disorder, or a drug, disturbs immune tolerance and drives autoantibody production.","additionalDifferentials":["Cold agglutinin disease","Hereditary spherocytosis","Drug-induced immune haemolytic anaemia","Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia","Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria","Evans syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test","Full blood count, reticulocytes and blood film for spherocytes","Haemolysis markers (lactate dehydrogenase, bilirubin, haptoglobin)","Investigation for secondary causes (lymphoproliferative, autoimmune, drugs)","Assessment for coexisting immune thrombocytopenia (Evans syndrome)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly falling haemoglobin with cardiovascular compromise","Evidence of an underlying lymphoma or lupus","Coexisting thrombocytopenia (Evans syndrome)","Severe haemolysis unresponsive to first-line therapy"],"prognosis":"Many patients respond to corticosteroids, but relapse is common and refractory disease requires escalation; prognosis in secondary forms depends on the underlying disorder.","pitfalls":["Not investigating for a secondary cause, especially lymphoma","Transfusion difficulties from pan-reactive autoantibodies masking alloantibodies","Overlooking Evans syndrome","Attributing spherocytes solely to hereditary spherocytosis"],"management":["Induce remission with corticosteroids such as prednisolone as first-line therapy","For refractory or relapsing disease, add rituximab and consider splenectomy or immunosuppressants such as azathioprine, ciclosporin or mycophenolate mofetil","Give folic acid supplementation to support the compensatory reticulocytosis","Identify and treat any secondary cause by withdrawing an implicated drug or managing underlying lymphoproliferative or connective tissue disease such as lupus","Transfuse the best-matched, warmed red cells only for symptomatic or life-threatening anaemia, alerting the laboratory to autoantibody-related crossmatch difficulty","Provide venous thromboembolism prophylaxis during active haemolysis","Monitor haemolysis markers and the full blood count, and screen for coexisting immune thrombocytopenia (Evans syndrome)","Refer to haematology and escalate urgently for haemodynamic compromise or a fulminant haemolytic crisis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Haematology reference texts (standard reference)","British Society for Haematology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"warm_autoimmune_hemolytic_anemia":{"name":"Warm autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","system":"Haematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["warm AIHA","warm antibody haemolytic anaemia","IgG autoimmune haemolytic anaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / AABB technical manual","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest form of autoimmune haemolytic anaemia, caused by IgG autoantibodies that bind red cells optimally at body temperature","Haemolysis is predominantly extravascular, with antibody-coated cells removed by splenic macrophages","The blood film characteristically shows spherocytes, polychromasia and a raised reticulocyte count","The direct antiglobulin test is positive, typically for IgG with or without complement, and is central to the diagnosis","May be primary or secondary to lymphoproliferative disorders, autoimmune disease such as lupus, or certain drugs","First-line treatment is corticosteroids, with rituximab and splenectomy among the options for refractory or relapsing disease","Transfusion can be difficult because the autoantibody reacts with most donor cells and may mask an underlying alloantibody"],"pathophysiology":"Warm autoimmune haemolytic anaemia is caused by autoantibodies, usually of the IgG class, that bind red-cell membrane antigens most avidly at core body temperature. Antibody-coated red cells are recognised by Fc receptors on splenic macrophages, which remove portions of the membrane, generating spherocytes that are less deformable and are ultimately trapped and destroyed in the spleen. This predominantly extravascular haemolysis raises unconjugated bilirubin, lactate dehydrogenase and reticulocytes while consuming haptoglobin. When complement is also fixed, some intravascular haemolysis and hepatic clearance occur. The autoantibody may be idiopathic or arise secondary to lymphoproliferative disease, connective tissue disorders or drugs, and its broad specificity complicates compatibility testing.","additionalDifferentials":["Cold agglutinin disease and other cold autoimmune haemolysis","Hereditary spherocytosis","Delayed haemolytic transfusion reaction","Drug-induced immune haemolysis","Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia","Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Direct antiglobulin test, characteristically positive for IgG with or without complement","Blood film showing spherocytes, polychromasia and nucleated red cells","Reticulocyte count, typically raised as a marrow response","Haemolysis markers including lactate dehydrogenase, haptoglobin and unconjugated bilirubin","Investigation for secondary causes such as lymphoproliferative disorder, lupus and relevant drugs","Careful crossmatching to detect any alloantibody masked by the autoantibody"],"redFlags":["Rapidly falling haemoglobin with cardiovascular compromise","Inadequate reticulocyte response suggesting concurrent marrow suppression","Features of an underlying lymphoma or connective tissue disease","Difficulty obtaining compatible blood when urgent transfusion is required"],"prognosis":"Many patients respond to corticosteroids, but relapse is common and some require second-line therapy; the course and outlook are strongly influenced by any underlying secondary cause.","pitfalls":["Withholding necessary transfusion for fear of incompatibility when the anaemia is life-threatening","Failing to search for an underlying lymphoproliferative or autoimmune disorder","Overlooking a masked alloantibody during compatibility testing","Attributing spherocytes solely to hereditary spherocytosis without a direct antiglobulin test"],"management":["Start corticosteroids such as prednisolone as first-line immunosuppression to reduce autoantibody-mediated red-cell destruction","Escalate refractory or relapsing disease to rituximab, splenectomy or agents such as azathioprine, ciclosporin or mycophenolate mofetil","Supplement folic acid to sustain the brisk marrow erythropoietic response","Search for and treat an underlying secondary cause, stopping any culprit drug and managing coexisting lymphoproliferative or autoimmune disease","Reserve transfusion of warmed, best-matched red cells for severe or life-threatening anaemia, warning the laboratory of autoantibody masking of alloantibodies","Give thromboprophylaxis during periods of active haemolysis","Track the full blood count and haemolysis markers over time and watch for associated immune thrombocytopenia (Evans syndrome)","Refer to haematology and escalate promptly for cardiovascular instability or a severe haemolytic crisis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","AABB Technical Manual"],"crossLinks":[]},"warthin_tumour":{"name":"Warthin tumour","system":"Head and neck / Salivary glands","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Papillary cystadenoma lymphomatosum","Adenolymphoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The second commonest benign parotid tumour, arising almost exclusively in the parotid, characteristically in the tail of the gland.","Strongly associated with cigarette smoking, and notable for being bilateral or multifocal in a substantial minority of patients.","Histology is distinctive: a bilayered oncocytic (mitochondria-rich) epithelium lining papillary cystic spaces, supported by a dense lymphoid stroma with germinal centres.","Thought to arise from salivary ductal inclusions trapped within intraparotid or periparotid lymph nodes during development.","Technetium pertechnetate scintigraphy shows avid uptake because of the abundant mitochondria in oncocytes, a feature shared with oncocytoma.","Malignant transformation is exceedingly rare, so observation is a reasonable option in elderly or comorbid patients once the diagnosis is secure.","Aspirated cyst fluid is often mucoid and cellular; oncocytes with a lymphoid background can be mistaken for a cystic or mucoepidermoid lesion.","The tumour may undergo infarction after fine-needle aspiration, producing squamous and reactive change that can mimic malignancy."],"pathophysiology":"Warthin tumour is a benign neoplasm believed to originate from ectopic salivary gland ductal epithelium sequestered within lymphoid tissue in and around the parotid during embryogenesis, an anatomical relationship unique to the parotid. The epithelial component undergoes oncocytic metaplasia, accumulating large numbers of mitochondria that impart the granular eosinophilic cytoplasm and account for technetium avidity. A prominent, immunologically reactive lymphoid stroma with germinal centres accompanies the epithelium, and the striking epidemiological link with tobacco smoke suggests that carcinogen exposure promotes both oncocytic change and the lymphoid reaction. The tumour is frequently multicentric and bilateral, reflecting its origin from multiple ductal inclusions rather than from a single focus.","additionalDifferentials":["Pleomorphic adenoma","Oncocytoma","Intraparotid or reactive lymph node","Lymphoepithelial cyst","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma (cystic, low-grade)","Acinic cell carcinoma","Metastatic cystic squamous carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fine-needle aspiration cytology of the mass","Ultrasound demonstrating a well-defined, often cystic, hypoechoic lesion in the parotid tail","Technetium pertechnetate scintigraphy showing focal uptake (historically supportive)","MRI to characterise multifocal or bilateral disease","Documentation of smoking history","Histological examination of the excised specimen"],"redFlags":["Rapid enlargement or new pain in a previously stable parotid mass","Facial nerve weakness, which is atypical and suggests an alternative diagnosis","Fixation to surrounding structures","Constitutional symptoms or diffuse nodal enlargement raising concern for lymphoma in the lymphoid stroma","Persistent or bloody cyst content after aspiration"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent as the tumour is benign, and malignant change is extremely rare. Apparent recurrence usually reflects a separate multifocal or metachronous lesion rather than inadequate excision.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise bilateral or multifocal disease","Misinterpreting mucoid, oncocyte-rich aspirate as a low-grade mucoepidermoid carcinoma","Over-calling post-aspiration infarction as malignancy","Overlooking lymphoma arising within the lymphoid stroma","Proceeding to unnecessarily radical surgery for a benign lesion"],"management":["Confirm the diagnosis with fine-needle aspiration cytology and ultrasound before deciding on management","Offer active surveillance as a valid option in elderly or comorbid patients once the benign diagnosis is secure, given the negligible risk of malignant change","Undertake surgical excision by extracapsular dissection or superficial parotidectomy for symptomatic or enlarging lesions or where diagnostic uncertainty persists","Identify and preserve the facial nerve during parotid surgery to avoid iatrogenic weakness","Advise smoking cessation given the strong causal association with tobacco","Monitor for the frequently multifocal, bilateral or metachronous nature of the tumour rather than assuming a single lesion","Refer to head and neck surgery for assessment and operative planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Head and Neck Tumours","Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery","Gnepp's Diagnostic Surgical Pathology of the Head and Neck"],"crossLinks":[]},"warts_epidermodysplasia_verruciformis":{"name":"Epidermodysplasia verruciformis (EVER1/EVER2, inherited HPV susceptibility)","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["epidermodysplasia verruciformis","EV","EVER1 deficiency","EVER2 deficiency","TMC6 deficiency","TMC8 deficiency","CIB1 deficiency","Lewandowsky-Lutz dysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare inherited disorder of selective susceptibility to a specific group of beta-genus human papillomaviruses, usually autosomal recessive through defects in EVER1/TMC6, EVER2/TMC8, or CIB1","The skin shows widespread flat wart-like papules and reddish or hypopigmented, pityriasis-versicolor-like scaly macules, typically beginning in childhood","Lesions favour sun-exposed skin such as the face, neck, trunk, and limbs and persist rather than resolving as ordinary warts do","The major long-term hazard is the development of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma in sun-exposed lesions during adulthood","Susceptibility is characteristically confined to these beta-papillomaviruses, so patients generally handle other infections normally in the classic (non-syndromic) form","A syndromic form occurs when the same skin phenotype arises secondarily in patients with T-cell immunodeficiency, in whom broader infection susceptibility is present","Management centres on rigorous photoprotection, surveillance and treatment of skin cancers, and lesion-directed dermatological therapy rather than a curative immunological treatment"],"pathophysiology":"In the classic form, loss-of-function mutations in EVER1/TMC6 or EVER2/TMC8, or in their partner CIB1, disrupt a keratinocyte protein complex that normally restricts the replication of beta-genus human papillomaviruses within skin cells, in part by regulating intracellular zinc handling. Without this intrinsic keratinocyte defence, these otherwise poorly pathogenic beta-papillomaviruses replicate unchecked in the epidermis, producing the persistent flat warts and versicolor-like macules. Because the defect is largely confined to keratinocyte control of these particular viruses rather than a global immune failure, susceptibility is narrowly restricted to beta-papillomaviruses. Chronic viral infection combined with ultraviolet exposure drives keratinocyte genomic damage over years, explaining the marked risk of squamous cell carcinoma on sun-exposed skin. In the syndromic form, an underlying T-cell immunodeficiency, rather than a keratinocyte-intrinsic defect, permits the same beta-papillomavirus disease alongside wider infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Common viral warts (verruca vulgaris and plana)","Pityriasis versicolor","Widespread warts in acquired immunosuppression such as HIV or transplantation","Syndromic epidermodysplasia verruciformis due to an underlying T-cell defect (for example GATA2, DOCK8, or other combined immunodeficiency)","Actinic keratoses and other photodamage lesions","Seborrhoeic keratoses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing characteristic histology with vacuolated keratinocytes","Human papillomavirus typing of lesions to identify beta-genus types","Genetic sequencing of TMC6, TMC8, and CIB1","Assessment of T-cell number and function to distinguish the syndromic form","Screening for HIV and other secondary immunosuppression","Regular dermatological surveillance with biopsy of suspicious lesions","Lymphocyte subset analysis where a combined immunodeficiency is suspected"],"redFlags":["New, enlarging, ulcerated, or bleeding lesions suggesting squamous cell carcinoma","Onset of the phenotype alongside features of broader immunodeficiency (pointing to the syndromic form)","Rapid lesion spread or unusually severe disease","Development of skin cancer at a young age","Extensive disease in a patient with unexplained recurrent infection"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy in the classic form is largely determined by the risk of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, which is high on sun-exposed skin from early adulthood; with strict photoprotection and vigilant surveillance many cancers are caught early, whereas the syndromic form carries the additional prognosis of the underlying immunodeficiency.","pitfalls":["Dismissing persistent flat warts and versicolor-like macules as ordinary warts or tinea","Failing to institute lifelong photoprotection and skin-cancer surveillance","Missing an underlying T-cell immunodeficiency in the syndromic form","Assuming systemic immune treatment will clear the lesions in the classic keratinocyte-intrinsic form","Under-recognising the malignancy risk and delaying biopsy of changing lesions"],"management":["With no curative antiviral therapy available, centre management on strict lifelong photoprotection using high-factor sunscreen and protective clothing to limit ultraviolet-driven carcinogenesis","Institute regular dermatological surveillance with a low threshold to biopsy any changing or suspicious lesion for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma","Treat wart-like lesions with topical agents such as retinoids, imiquimod or fluorouracil, cryotherapy or photodynamic therapy, accepting frequent recurrence","Excise or destroy cutaneous squamous cell carcinomas promptly, and consider systemic retinoids such as acitretin for extensive disease or chemoprevention","In the syndromic form, address the underlying T-cell immunodeficiency and minimise iatrogenic immunosuppression where feasible","Offer genetic counselling for this usually autosomal recessive condition, with testing of at-risk relatives","Refer to a multidisciplinary team spanning dermatology, clinical immunology and clinical genetics for long-term care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"weaver_syndrome":{"name":"Weaver syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["EZH2-related overgrowth","Weaver-Smith syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Generalised pre- and postnatal overgrowth with tall stature and markedly advanced bone age, often more accelerated than the child's linear growth","Characteristic facial gestalt includes a broad forehead, hypertelorism, large low-set ears, a small chin with a horizontal crease, and a round face in infancy","Limb findings include camptodactyly, prominent finger pads, and broad thumbs, with variable joint contractures and deep-set nails","Caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in EZH2, encoding a histone methyltransferase within the Polycomb repressive complex","Autosomal dominant inheritance with most cases occurring de novo","Learning and developmental delay are variable and often milder than in Sotos syndrome, with hypotonia and hoarse low-pitched cry in infancy","A modestly increased risk of neoplasia, notably haematological malignancy and neuroblastoma, has been reported, so clinical vigilance is prudent"],"pathophysiology":"Weaver syndrome is caused by germline variants in EZH2, the catalytic subunit of Polycomb repressive complex 2, which trimethylates histone H3 lysine 27 to silence target genes controlling growth and differentiation. Disruption of EZH2 activity dysregulates these developmental programmes, producing accelerated skeletal maturation, generalised overgrowth, and the characteristic craniofacial and limb features. Because EZH2 and NSD1 act on overlapping chromatin pathways, Weaver and Sotos syndromes share overgrowth but differ in facial gestalt and specific complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Sotos syndrome (NSD1-related overgrowth)","Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","Malan syndrome (NFIX)","Marshall-Smith syndrome","Cohen-Gibson syndrome (EED-related overgrowth)","Simpson-Golabi-Behmel syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing for EZH2 variants via targeted analysis or an overgrowth gene panel","Chromosomal microarray to exclude copy-number causes of overgrowth","Bone age radiograph, which is characteristically markedly advanced","Developmental assessment to define learning and motor needs","Clinical examination and directed investigation for neoplasia if suggestive symptoms arise","Skeletal and joint assessment for contractures and camptodactyly"],"redFlags":["Unexplained cytopenias, lymphadenopathy, or an abdominal mass raising concern for malignancy","Progressive contractures or spinal deformity impairing function","Airway or feeding compromise in infancy from hypotonia","Neurological deterioration requiring imaging"],"prognosis":"Growth acceleration tends to slow after childhood and adult height is often at the upper end of normal, while developmental outcome is variable but frequently better than in Sotos syndrome. The reported tumour risk is modest, and overall life expectancy is generally good.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling Weaver as Sotos syndrome given shared overgrowth, delaying correct EZH2 testing","Assuming a benign course and overlooking the reported association with neoplasia","Interpreting markedly advanced bone age as a puberty disorder rather than a syndromic feature","Missing camptodactyly and joint contractures that need orthopaedic and physiotherapy input"],"management":["With no disease-specific therapy available, direct care at the individual's features and coordinate it through clinical genetics","Provide early developmental intervention, speech and language therapy and educational support for learning delay","Offer physiotherapy and occupational therapy for hypotonia, camptodactyly and joint contractures, with orthopaedic referral where contractures are functionally limiting","Monitor growth and the markedly advanced bone age, anticipating that growth acceleration tends to slow after childhood","Maintain clinical vigilance for neoplasia and investigate suggestive symptoms promptly given the modest tumour risk","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant condition, noting most cases arise de novo, with parental testing to inform recurrence risk","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary team including genetics, paediatrics and therapy services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: EZH2-Related Overgrowth (Weaver Syndrome)"],"crossLinks":[]},"weber_syndrome":{"name":"Weber syndrome (midbrain)","system":"Nervous system / Cerebrovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["superior alternating hemiplegia","ventral midbrain syndrome","Weber's syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from infarction of the ventral midbrain (cerebral peduncle) in the territory of paramedian branches of the posterior cerebral and basilar arteries","Defined by the combination of an ipsilateral oculomotor (third nerve) palsy with contralateral hemiparesis","The third nerve palsy produces a down-and-out eye, ptosis, and a dilated, poorly reactive pupil on the side of the lesion","Contralateral weakness affects the face, arm, and leg because the corticospinal and corticobulbar fibres in the crus cerebri are damaged","It is the prototypical superior alternating hemiplegia and a form of crossed brainstem syndrome","The fascicle of the third nerve passes through the cerebral peduncle, explaining why a single lesion links the eye and limb signs","A pupil-involving third nerve palsy always demands exclusion of a compressive aneurysm before attributing it to brainstem ischaemia"],"pathophysiology":"Weber syndrome arises from ischaemia of the ventral (basal) midbrain, supplied by paramedian perforating branches of the posterior cerebral and top-of-basilar arteries. The lesion involves two adjacent structures: the emerging fascicles of the oculomotor nerve, giving an ipsilateral third nerve palsy with pupillary dilatation and ptosis, and the corticospinal and corticobulbar fibres within the crus cerebri, giving contralateral weakness of the face and limbs. Because the descending motor fibres decussate more caudally, weakness is contralateral while the cranial nerve deficit is ipsilateral, producing the crossed pattern typical of alternating hemiplegias.","additionalDifferentials":["Benedikt syndrome (tegmental midbrain involvement)","Claude syndrome","Posterior communicating artery aneurysm compressing the third nerve","Uncal herniation with third nerve compression","Midbrain demyelination or tumour","Diabetic third nerve palsy (usually pupil-sparing and without hemiparesis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diffusion-weighted MRI of the midbrain to confirm a paramedian peduncular infarct","MR or CT angiography of the posterior circulation and circle of Willis to exclude aneurysm","Formal assessment of pupillary size and reactivity to characterise the third nerve palsy","Cardiovascular and stroke risk workup including ECG and metabolic profiling","Vessel wall imaging where basilar atherosclerosis or dissection is considered","Blood glucose and inflammatory markers to weigh microvascular and inflammatory causes"],"redFlags":["Pupil-involving third nerve palsy raising concern for a compressive posterior communicating artery aneurysm","Declining consciousness suggesting extension into the reticular activating system or top-of-basilar involvement","Bilateral signs indicating a larger paramedian midbrain or thalamic infarct","Rapid clinical deterioration implying evolving basilar occlusion"],"prognosis":"The oculomotor palsy and hemiparesis may partially improve with rehabilitation, but persistent diplopia, ptosis, and residual limb weakness are common, and outcome depends heavily on the underlying vascular cause.","pitfalls":["Attributing a pupil-involving third nerve palsy to microvascular ischaemia without excluding an aneurysm","Missing the contralateral hemiparesis and treating the eye signs in isolation","Confusing Weber with tegmental midbrain syndromes that add tremor or ataxia","Overlooking evolving basilar territory ischaemia in a patient with only midbrain signs"],"management":["Treat as an acute posterior-circulation ischaemic stroke on the hyperacute pathway, offering intravenous thrombolysis and considering mechanical thrombectomy for eligible patients within the therapeutic window","Start secondary prevention with antiplatelet therapy, or anticoagulation if a cardioembolic source is confirmed","Reduce vascular risk with a statin, blood-pressure control, glycaemic management and smoking cessation","Refer for multidisciplinary neurorehabilitation with physiotherapy and occupational therapy for the contralateral hemiparesis","Manage the oculomotor palsy and diplopia with temporary occlusion or prisms, reserving strabismus surgery for stable residual deviation","Exclude an aneurysm or basilar dissection as the cause, and escalate urgently for fluctuating brainstem signs suggesting evolving basilar occlusion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"],"crossLinks":["benedikt_syndrome","claude_syndrome","top_of_the_basilar_syndrome"]},"weill_marchesani_syndrome":{"name":"Weill-Marchesani syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WMS","ADAMTS10","FBN1","LTBP2","spherophakia-brachymorphia syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Combines an acromelic skeletal phenotype (short stature, short hands and feet, brachydactyly, stiff joints) with characteristic ocular abnormalities","The hallmark eye finding is microspherophakia, a small spherical lens that is prone to dislocation (ectopia lentis), typically inferiorly or into the anterior chamber","Lens abnormality causes lenticular myopia and a high risk of pupillary block and secondary glaucoma, which can threaten sight","Genetically heterogeneous: recessive forms from ADAMTS10 or LTBP2 and dominant forms from FBN1, all affecting the microfibril system","It sits within the acromelic dysplasia family and contrasts with Marfan syndrome, where lens dislocation is usually upward and stature is tall","Joint stiffness and brachydactyly help distinguish it clinically from other causes of ectopia lentis","Ophthalmic management focuses on monitoring for and treating pupillary block glaucoma and managing the dislocated microspherophakic lens"],"pathophysiology":"Weill-Marchesani syndrome is a disorder of the fibrillin microfibril system that supports the ocular zonule and connective tissue. Variants in ADAMTS10, LTBP2 or FBN1 disrupt assembly or regulation of microfibrils, weakening the zonular apparatus so that the lens becomes small, spherical and poorly suspended, predisposing to dislocation and angle-closure glaucoma. The same microfibrillar disturbance and associated dysregulated TGF-beta signalling in the skeleton produce the short stature, brachydactyly and joint stiffness that characterise the acromelic phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Marfan syndrome (ectopia lentis, but tall stature)","Homocystinuria (ectopia lentis)","Acromicric dysplasia","Geleophysic dysplasia","Isolated microspherophakia","Other causes of secondary angle-closure glaucoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing of ADAMTS10, LTBP2 and FBN1, usually via a gene panel","Detailed ophthalmic assessment including slit-lamp examination for microspherophakia and lens position","Intraocular pressure measurement and gonioscopy for angle-closure/glaucoma risk","Skeletal survey for short tubular bones and brachydactyly","Echocardiography where cardiac involvement is suspected","Plasma homocysteine to exclude homocystinuria as a cause of ectopia lentis"],"redFlags":["Acute angle-closure glaucoma from pupillary block by the spherical lens","Anterior dislocation of the lens into the anterior chamber","Progressive visual loss from uncontrolled intraocular pressure","Rapidly rising intraocular pressure requiring urgent ophthalmic intervention"],"prognosis":"General health and life expectancy are usually good, but vision is at significant risk from lens dislocation and secondary glaucoma, so the ophthalmic outcome depends heavily on early detection and careful long-term eye care.","pitfalls":["Dilating the pupil without recognising that it can precipitate pupillary block in microspherophakia","Confusing it with Marfan syndrome despite the opposite stature and direction of lens dislocation","Missing homocystinuria as an alternative cause of ectopia lentis","Underestimating the glaucoma risk and deferring intraocular pressure monitoring"],"management":["Provide lifelong ophthalmological surveillance of intraocular pressure, lens position and refraction to protect sight","Manage the high risk of pupillary-block glaucoma, avoiding miotics that can worsen block by drawing the small lens forward and using mydriasis or laser peripheral iridotomy as appropriate","Undertake lensectomy for symptomatic ectopia lentis or lens-induced glaucoma, and correct the lenticular myopia optically","Offer physiotherapy for joint stiffness and orthopaedic assessment of the skeletal features","Screen for cardiac involvement with echocardiography","Confirm the genetic subtype and offer genetic counselling, recognising both autosomal recessive (ADAMTS10, LTBP2) and dominant (FBN1) inheritance","Coordinate care across ophthalmology, clinical genetics and paediatrics or general medicine, escalating for an acute painful red eye suggesting angle closure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: Weill-Marchesani Syndrome","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"weissenbacher_zweymuller_syndrome":{"name":"Weissenbacher-Zweymuller syndrome","system":"Skeletal dysplasia","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["COL11A2-related dysplasia","Neonatal Weissenbacher-Zweymuller phenotype","WZS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Skeletal dysplasia references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Neonatal skeletal dysplasia within the type XI collagenopathy spectrum, presenting with rhizomelic (proximal) limb shortening, a flat midface, a small upturned nose and micrognathia.","Sensorineural hearing loss is a defining feature, reflecting the structural role of type XI collagen in the inner ear.","Cleft palate and Pierre Robin sequence (micrognathia, glossoptosis and neonatal airway compromise) are frequent and drive early management.","Infantile radiographs show dumbbell-shaped femora and humeri from metaphyseal broadening, and these tend to remodel with growth.","A key distinguishing feature is catch-up limb growth — the rhizomelia and dumbbell long bones improve with age, unlike most severe fixed neonatal dysplasias.","Associated with COL11A2 variants and allelic with otospondylomegaepiphyseal dysplasia (OSMED) and non-ocular (type III) Stickler syndrome, with which it overlaps clinically.","Because COL11A2, unlike COL2A1 and COL11A1, is not expressed in the eye, the ocular and retinal complications of classic Stickler syndrome are characteristically absent — a useful separator.","Hearing loss and airway/feeding issues, rather than the skeletal changes, dominate the long-term burden."],"pathophysiology":"Weissenbacher-Zweymuller syndrome belongs to the type XI collagenopathies and is associated with variants in COL11A2, which encodes the alpha-2 chain of type XI collagen. Type XI collagen co-assembles with type II collagen to regulate the diameter and spacing of cartilage collagen fibrils and is also a structural component of the inner ear, so its deficiency produces both a chondrodysplasia (rhizomelic shortening with dumbbell-shaped long bones, midface hypoplasia and cleft palate) and sensorineural hearing loss. Because COL11A2, unlike COL2A1 and COL11A1, is not expressed in the vitreous of the eye, the ocular and retinal complications of classic Stickler syndrome are characteristically absent. The condition is allelic with, and lies on a clinical spectrum including, otospondylomegaepiphyseal dysplasia (OSMED) and non-ocular (type III) Stickler syndrome, and the neonatal skeletal changes characteristically remodel with growth rather than remaining fixed.","additionalDifferentials":["Otospondylomegaepiphyseal dysplasia (OSMED), the allelic recessive disorder","Stickler syndrome types I to III","Kniest dysplasia","Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita","Marshall syndrome","Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skeletal survey in infancy with serial follow-up films to document long-bone remodelling","Molecular testing of COL11A2 or a collagenopathy gene panel","Audiology including brainstem-evoked response testing","Cleft palate, airway and feeding assessment","Ophthalmology review to exclude Stickler-type eye disease","Sleep study where obstructive breathing is suspected","Genetic counselling clarifying the inheritance pattern in the family"],"redFlags":["Neonatal airway obstruction from Pierre Robin sequence","Feeding difficulty with aspiration risk","Progressive or undetected sensorineural hearing loss affecting speech development","Sleep-disordered breathing"],"prognosis":"Growth outlook is generally favourable, with limb proportions and long-bone shape improving over childhood; sensorineural hearing loss persists and requires long-term audiological support.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for a lethal or fixed severe dysplasia and missing the expected catch-up growth","Overlooking sensorineural hearing loss in infancy","Searching for retinal disease that COL11A2 does not cause","Misassigning inheritance (dominant versus recessive) and giving inaccurate recurrence counselling"],"management":["Prioritise neonatal airway management for Pierre Robin sequence, using prone positioning and a nasopharyngeal airway and escalating to surgical measures if obstruction is severe","Support feeding with cleft-palate feeding techniques and specialist input, and undertake surgical cleft palate repair at the appropriate age","Provide long-term audiological support with hearing aids for the persistent sensorineural hearing loss","Monitor the skeleton with serial radiographs, anticipating favourable remodelling of the dumbbell long bones and catch-up limb growth over childhood","Arrange ophthalmology review to exclude Stickler-type eye disease, which is characteristically absent in this collagenopathy","Confirm the diagnosis by COL11A2 testing and offer genetic counselling regarding inheritance and recurrence risk","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary team including cleft, ENT and audiology, respiratory and genetics services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["International nosology and classification of genetic skeletal disorders","Standard clinical genetics and dysmorphology reference texts","Paediatric skeletal radiology reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"welander_distal_myopathy":{"name":"Welander distal myopathy (TIA1)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WDM","Welander myopathy","TIA1-related distal myopathy","Swedish distal myopathy","late-onset distal myopathy type Welander"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / GeneReviews","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant late-onset distal myopathy caused by a founder variant in TIA1, most prevalent in Swedish and Finnish populations","Onset is typically in the fifth decade or later, distinguishing it from the early-onset distal myopathies","Weakness and clumsiness begin in the finger and hand extensors, initially impairing fine finger movements, before spreading to the small hand muscles and the lower legs","Slow progression usually allows preserved ambulation, and the phenotype is generally milder in heterozygotes than in the rare homozygous cases","TIA1 encodes an RNA-binding stress-granule protein, linking Welander myopathy mechanistically to the rimmed-vacuole myopathy spectrum","Creatine kinase is normal or mildly raised and electromyography shows myopathic changes, sometimes with irritable features","Muscle biopsy characteristically shows rimmed vacuoles with protein aggregation on a chronic myopathic background","Diagnosis combines the distinctive adult-onset hand-extensor weakness pattern with TIA1 genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"Welander distal myopathy is caused by a dominant founder variant in TIA1, which encodes an RNA-binding protein that nucleates stress granules involved in the cellular response to stress. The mutation is thought to alter stress-granule dynamics and RNA handling, promoting accumulation of misfolded and aggregated proteins within muscle fibres. This produces the rimmed vacuoles and protein deposits seen on biopsy and places the disorder within the broader family of rimmed-vacuolar myopathies, with the earliest and most severe involvement in the distal upper-limb muscles.","additionalDifferentials":["Inclusion body myositis","Other rimmed-vacuole distal myopathies (GNE myopathy, Udd myopathy)","Myotonic dystrophy","Distal spinal muscular atrophy or motor neuropathy affecting the hands","Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease","Ulnar or other focal peripheral neuropathy affecting hand function"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography","Muscle biopsy seeking rimmed vacuoles and protein aggregates","Muscle MRI to characterise the distal-predominant involvement","Targeted TIA1 testing or a distal myopathy gene panel","Family history assessment and evaluation of relatives given dominant inheritance"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression or prominent proximal weakness suggesting inclusion body myositis or another diagnosis","Dysphagia or respiratory involvement not typical of classic Welander myopathy","Asymmetric quadriceps weakness raising suspicion of inclusion body myositis","Early severe disability suggesting a homozygous or alternative genotype"],"prognosis":"The disorder progresses slowly over years to decades and heterozygous patients usually retain functional independence with a normal or near-normal lifespan; the rare homozygous form is considerably more severe with earlier and more widespread weakness.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with inclusion body myositis, which shares rimmed vacuoles but has a different weakness pattern and no family history","Attributing hand weakness to a focal neuropathy and missing the myopathy","Assuming normal creatine kinase excludes muscle disease","Overlooking the characteristic founder-population family history"],"management":["With no disease-modifying therapy available, focus management on supportive measures that preserve function","Provide physiotherapy and occupational therapy to maintain hand dexterity and mobility, with aids and adaptations for impaired fine finger movements","Prescribe ankle-foot orthoses for lower-leg weakness and address falls risk as distal involvement progresses","Monitor the slowly progressive weakness periodically, reassuring that ambulation is usually preserved in heterozygotes","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant condition and assess at-risk relatives","Coordinate care through a neuromuscular multidisciplinary team, escalating support if the rare, more severe homozygous phenotype is suspected"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","GeneReviews: Welander distal myopathy"],"crossLinks":[]},"werner_syndrome":{"name":"Werner syndrome","system":"Genetics / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["adult progeria","WRN","RECQL2","progeria of the adult"],"chapter":null,"source":"OMIM / GeneReviews (NCBI) and standard clinical genetics texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive segmental progeroid syndrome caused by loss-of-function variants in the WRN RecQ helicase gene, leading to genomic instability","Development is normal until adolescence, when the pubertal growth spurt is absent and short stature becomes apparent","Characteristic features emerge in the twenties to thirties: premature greying and hair loss, scleroderma-like tight skin, a beaked nose and a high-pitched voice","Bilateral cataracts, often developing at an unusually young age, are a hallmark finding","Patients develop early-onset atherosclerosis, type 2 diabetes mellitus, osteoporosis and hypogonadism","There is a markedly raised risk of malignancy, with an unusual predominance of sarcomas (soft-tissue and osteosarcoma) alongside melanoma and thyroid cancer","Chronic, poorly healing skin ulcers around the ankles and over the Achilles tendon are typical and difficult to manage","Diagnosis rests on the combination of cardinal clinical features supported by molecular confirmation of biallelic WRN variants"],"pathophysiology":"Werner syndrome arises from biallelic loss-of-function variants in WRN, which encodes a member of the RecQ family of DNA helicases with additional exonuclease activity. The protein participates in DNA replication, repair, recombination and telomere maintenance. Its loss produces genomic instability, accelerated telomere attrition and premature cellular senescence, which underlie the segmental (partial) acceleration of ageing phenotypes and the predisposition to cancer seen in affected individuals.","additionalDifferentials":["Hutchinson-Gilford progeria syndrome","Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)","Mandibuloacral dysplasia","Other RecQ helicase disorders such as Bloom and Rothmund-Thomson syndromes","Metabolic syndrome with early diabetes and premature atherosclerosis","Constitutional cataract with unrelated systemic disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing for biallelic WRN variants","Slit-lamp ophthalmological assessment for premature bilateral cataract","Fasting glucose, HbA1c and lipid profile to detect diabetes and dyslipidaemia","Bone densitometry for osteoporosis","Cardiovascular assessment for premature atherosclerotic disease","Age-appropriate malignancy surveillance with a low threshold for imaging or biopsy of new masses"],"redFlags":["New or enlarging soft-tissue or bony mass suggesting sarcoma","Non-healing lower-limb ulcer with signs of secondary infection or malignant transformation","Symptoms of premature myocardial infarction or stroke","Rapid visual deterioration from advancing cataract"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is shortened, with most affected individuals dying in the fifth to sixth decade, predominantly from malignancy or atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. Careful metabolic control, cardiovascular risk reduction and cancer surveillance may improve outcomes.","pitfalls":["Attributing the scleroderma-like skin changes to systemic sclerosis and missing the progeroid diagnosis","Overlooking the characteristically young age of cataract onset","Failing to maintain vigilant malignancy surveillance, particularly for sarcomas","Underestimating premature cardiovascular and diabetic complications in a relatively young patient"],"management":["With no treatment for the underlying helicase defect, target care at aggressive management of the premature age-related complications","Reduce cardiovascular risk intensively with statins, blood-pressure control, antiplatelet therapy and smoking cessation for early atherosclerosis","Manage type 2 diabetes mellitus with dietary measures and agents such as metformin or insulin, and treat associated dyslipidaemia","Treat osteoporosis with bisphosphonates alongside calcium and vitamin D","Refer for cataract surgery, and provide specialist wound care for the chronic, poorly healing lower-limb ulcers","Maintain heightened cancer surveillance given the raised risk, with a low threshold to image or biopsy any new mass, particularly for sarcoma, melanoma and thyroid cancer","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive condition and coordinate multidisciplinary care across the affected systems"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (NCBI): Werner syndrome","OMIM: Werner syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"west_nile_virus":{"name":"West Nile virus infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["West Nile fever","WNV","West Nile neuroinvasive disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mosquito-borne flavivirus maintained in a bird reservoir with Culex mosquitoes as the main vector; humans are incidental dead-end hosts","The large majority of infections are asymptomatic, and most symptomatic cases present as a self-limiting febrile illness (West Nile fever) with headache, myalgia, and sometimes a transient rash","A small proportion develop neuroinvasive disease: meningitis, encephalitis, or an acute flaccid paralysis resembling poliomyelitis","Advancing age and immunosuppression are the strongest risk factors for severe neuroinvasive disease","Acute flaccid paralysis from anterior horn cell involvement is an important clue that distinguishes WNV from many other causes of encephalitis","Transmission can also occur through blood transfusion and organ transplantation, so donor screening is relevant in endemic areas","Management is supportive; there is no specific licensed antiviral therapy, and prevention centres on mosquito control and personal protection"],"pathophysiology":"West Nile virus is a single-stranded RNA flavivirus circulating between birds and Culex mosquitoes. After a mosquito bite the virus replicates locally and in regional lymph nodes, producing viraemia. In a minority of hosts it crosses into the central nervous system, where it infects neurons, particularly in the brainstem, deep grey matter, and anterior horn cells of the spinal cord, producing encephalitis and flaccid paralysis. Host factors such as age and immune status strongly influence whether infection stays confined to a febrile illness or becomes neuroinvasive.","additionalDifferentials":["Other arboviral encephalitides (Japanese encephalitis, St Louis encephalitis)","Herpes simplex encephalitis","Enteroviral meningitis or encephalitis","Bacterial meningitis","Guillain-Barre syndrome (for the flaccid paralysis presentation)","Dengue or other acute febrile arboviral illness"],"infectionMimics":["Japanese encephalitis","Herpes simplex encephalitis","Enteroviral CNS infection","Poliomyelitis and other causes of acute flaccid paralysis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF examination showing a lymphocytic pleocytosis with raised protein in neuroinvasive disease","WNV-specific IgM in serum and CSF (mainstay of diagnosis), with paired serology for confirmation","Nucleic acid testing in early infection or in immunocompromised or transfusion/transplant-associated settings","MRI of the brain and spinal cord to evaluate encephalitis and anterior horn involvement","Testing to exclude treatable causes such as herpes simplex and bacterial meningitis"],"redFlags":["Altered consciousness, seizures, or focal neurological signs indicating encephalitis","Acute flaccid limb weakness suggesting anterior horn cell involvement","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or brainstem dysfunction","Severe disease in an elderly or immunocompromised patient with rapid deterioration"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated West Nile fever resolves fully, but neuroinvasive disease carries substantial morbidity and mortality, and survivors of encephalitis or flaccid paralysis may be left with long-lasting neurological and functional deficits, particularly older patients.","pitfalls":["Assuming a benign viral illness and missing early neuroinvasive features in an older patient","Failing to distinguish WNV flaccid paralysis from Guillain-Barre syndrome, which has different management","Not considering transfusion- or transplant-associated transmission in a recently exposed patient","Delaying empirical treatment for herpes simplex encephalitis while awaiting WNV confirmation"],"management":["With no proven specific antiviral, manage symptomatic disease supportively with fluids, antipyretics and analgesia","Admit neuroinvasive disease for supportive neurological care, seizure control and close monitoring","Provide respiratory support, including mechanical ventilation, for acute flaccid paralysis with diaphragmatic or bulbar involvement","Give empirical aciclovir and antibacterials until herpes simplex encephalitis and bacterial meningitis are excluded","Refer survivors with residual deficits for neurorehabilitation with physiotherapy and occupational therapy","Apply public health measures including mosquito-bite avoidance, vector control and blood and organ donor screening in endemic areas","Escalate to intensive care for respiratory failure or deteriorating encephalitis, particularly in older or immunosuppressed patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"west_syndrome":{"name":"West syndrome","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Infantile spasms","Infantile epileptic spasms syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"ILAE classification and clinical guidance on the epilepsy syndromes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad of epileptic (infantile) spasms, hypsarrhythmia on EEG, and developmental arrest or regression.","Onset is typically between 3 and 12 months, peaking around 4 to 7 months.","Spasms occur in clusters, often on awakening, with brief symmetrical flexion or extension of trunk and limbs.","Aetiology is diverse: structural (tuberous sclerosis, cortical malformation, hypoxic-ischaemic injury), genetic or metabolic, or unknown.","Hypsarrhythmia is a chaotic, high-amplitude, disorganised interictal pattern; modified or fragmented variants occur.","First-line treatments are hormonal therapy (adrenocorticotrophic hormone or corticosteroids) and vigabatrin, the latter being especially effective in tuberous sclerosis.","Prompt recognition and treatment improve developmental outcome, reflecting a treatment-lag effect.","The condition may evolve into Lennox-Gastaut syndrome or other epilepsies."],"pathophysiology":"West syndrome reflects age-specific hyperexcitability of the developing brain. Diverse structural, genetic and metabolic insults converge on dysregulated cortical, subcortical and brainstem networks during a critical developmental window; proposed contributors include abnormal corticotrophin-releasing hormone signalling and an imbalance of excitatory and inhibitory neurotransmission. The result is the characteristic clustered spasms and disorganised hypsarrhythmic EEG, with disruption of normal synaptic maturation contributing to developmental regression.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign non-epileptic infantile spasms and benign myoclonus of infancy","Sandifer syndrome and gastro-oesophageal reflux","Early infantile developmental and epileptic encephalopathy (Ohtahara)","Early myoclonic encephalopathy","Startle responses and benign sleep myoclonus","Tonic seizures of other origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Prolonged or video EEG including sleep to confirm hypsarrhythmia and capture spasms","MRI brain to identify a structural cause","Metabolic screen including consideration of pyridoxine-dependent and biotinidase disorders","Genetic testing (chromosomal microarray, gene panels or exome)","Skin examination and imaging for tuberous sclerosis","Ophthalmology assessment with fundoscopy","Therapeutic trial of pyridoxine where indicated"],"redFlags":["Developmental regression or plateau","Clusters of spasms, especially on waking","Focal neurological signs suggesting a structural lesion","Features of raised intracranial pressure or an underlying metabolic crisis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on aetiology and treatment lag; cryptogenic cases treated promptly may do well, whereas symptomatic cases often have persistent epilepsy and intellectual disability, and many evolve into other epilepsy syndromes.","pitfalls":["Mistaking spasms for colic, reflux or startle and delaying diagnosis.","Not obtaining a sleep EEG and thereby missing hypsarrhythmia.","Prolonging the treatment lag by delaying therapy.","Overlooking tuberous sclerosis, which changes drug choice.","Failing to investigate for treatable metabolic causes."],"management":["Start first-line hormonal therapy with adrenocorticotrophic hormone or oral corticosteroids such as prednisolone to suppress the spasms","Use vigabatrin as an alternative first-line agent, and preferentially where tuberous sclerosis is the underlying cause","Treat promptly to minimise the treatment lag, as early control improves developmental outcome","Identify and address the underlying aetiology, including a trial of pyridoxine for pyridoxine-dependent seizures and targeted management of metabolic or structural causes","Repeat EEG to confirm resolution of hypsarrhythmia and cessation of spasms as the treatment endpoint","Arrange long-term developmental surveillance and support, anticipating possible evolution to other epilepsy syndromes","Refer to paediatric neurology, and offer genetic counselling where a genetic cause is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ILAE position papers on classification of the epilepsies and epileptic spasms","Panayiotopoulos CP. A Clinical Guide to Epileptic Syndromes and their Treatment","Standard textbook of paediatric neurology"],"crossLinks":[]},"whim_syndrome":{"name":"WHIM syndrome (CXCR4)","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CXCR4","warts, hypogammaglobulinaemia, infections, myelokathexis","WHIM"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification of inborn errors of immunity","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The acronym captures the tetrad of warts, hypogammaglobulinaemia, recurrent infections and myelokathexis, though not every feature is present in each patient","Peripheral blood shows profound neutropenia despite a hypercellular bone marrow densely packed with mature, degenerating neutrophils held back from release (myelokathexis)","Human papillomavirus disease is characteristic and severe, with extensive cutaneous and anogenital warts and a raised risk of HPV-driven malignancy","Recurrent bacterial sinopulmonary infection reflects both neutropenia and impaired humoral immunity with low immunoglobulins and poor vaccine responses","Lymphopenia affecting B and T cells is usual, and the neutropenia paradoxically worsens the picture despite abundant marrow reserve","Neutrophil counts often rise transiently during acute infection or after corticosteroid stress, a clue to marrow retention rather than production failure","Most cases arise from autosomal dominant gain-of-function truncating variants in CXCR4 that prevent normal receptor internalisation","A CXCR4 antagonist (plerixafor) mobilises retained marrow neutrophils and lymphocytes into the circulation and can reduce infection and wart burden"],"pathophysiology":"WHIM syndrome is caused by gain-of-function variants, usually truncations of the C-terminal tail of the chemokine receptor CXCR4, that impair receptor desensitisation and internalisation after binding its ligand CXCL12. The resulting exaggerated and sustained CXCR4 signalling anchors mature neutrophils and lymphocytes within the bone marrow, preventing their egress into the blood despite adequate production; the retained neutrophils age and die in the marrow, producing myelokathexis. Persistent peripheral neutropenia and lymphopenia, together with impaired antibody production, underlie the recurrent bacterial infections, while defective trafficking and immunity permit uncontrolled human papillomavirus infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe congenital neutropenia (e.g. ELANE-related)","Cyclical neutropenia","Common variable immunodeficiency","Epidermodysplasia verruciformis","GATA2 deficiency","Autoimmune or drug-induced neutropenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and differential demonstrating persistent neutropenia and lymphopenia","Bone marrow examination showing hypercellularity with myelokathexis and degenerating mature neutrophils","Serum immunoglobulins with hypogammaglobulinaemia and specific antibody responses to vaccines","Lymphocyte subset enumeration","CXCR4 gene sequencing for gain-of-function variants","HPV assessment including cervical and anogenital surveillance"],"redFlags":["New or rapidly changing anogenital or cutaneous lesion suggesting HPV-driven malignancy","Overwhelming bacterial infection in the setting of severe neutropenia","Progressive hypogammaglobulinaemia with recurrent pneumonia and bronchiectasis","Cytopenias or marrow changes raising concern for evolving haematological disease"],"prognosis":"With immunoglobulin replacement, infection prophylaxis and measures to raise the neutrophil count, many patients survive into adulthood, but the persistent HPV burden carries a significant long-term risk of malignancy and chronic morbidity from bronchiectasis.","pitfalls":["Interpreting the neutropenia as a production defect and overlooking the hypercellular marrow of myelokathexis","Missing the diagnosis when only some components of the tetrad are present","Neglecting systematic HPV surveillance and vaccination in affected patients","Attributing recurrent infection solely to neutropenia and failing to detect the antibody deficiency","Being reassured by transient rises in neutrophil count during acute illness"],"management":["Consider targeted therapy with a CXCR4 antagonist such as plerixafor or oral mavorixafor to mobilise retained leucocytes and correct the neutropenia and lymphopenia","Raise neutrophil counts with granulocyte colony-stimulating factor during infection or as maintenance","Replace immunoglobulin for hypogammaglobulinaemia and poor vaccine responses","Give antimicrobial prophylaxis and treat bacterial sinopulmonary infections promptly","Manage the human papillomavirus burden with HPV vaccination, ablative wart treatment and regular cervical and anogenital surveillance for malignancy","Monitor for and manage bronchiectasis arising from recurrent infection, with airway clearance and physiotherapy","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant condition and refer to clinical immunology for coordinated care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"whiplash_injury":{"name":"Whiplash injury","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["whiplash-associated disorder","WAD","cervical acceleration-deceleration injury","neck sprain/strain"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon / standard sports medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically follows a rapid acceleration-deceleration of the neck, most often a rear-end vehicle collision, straining the cervical soft tissues","Neck pain and stiffness often develop several hours to a day after the injury, along with headache and restricted movement","Whiplash-associated disorders are graded from no physical signs (grade 0) through musculoskeletal signs (grades I-II) to neurological signs (grade III) and fracture or dislocation (grade IV)","Apply a validated decision rule such as the Canadian C-spine rule to determine the need for imaging and to identify patients needing bony assessment","The priority in acute assessment is to exclude cervical fracture, dislocation, and spinal cord or nerve root injury","For uncomplicated injuries, early return to normal activity, reassurance, and active mobilisation give better outcomes than a collar and prolonged rest","Simple analgesia and exercise-based physiotherapy are the mainstays of management","Persistent pain, higher initial pain intensity, and psychological distress are associated with a slower recovery and chronic symptoms","Be alert to associated concussion, temporomandibular, or shoulder-girdle symptoms after the same injury"],"pathophysiology":"Whiplash results from sudden hyperextension followed by flexion (or the reverse) of the cervical spine during rapid acceleration-deceleration. This strains and may partially tear the cervical muscles, ligaments, facet joint capsules, and intervertebral discs, and can injure nerve roots or, in severe cases, cause fracture or cord injury. Peripheral tissue injury combined with central pain sensitisation and psychosocial factors contributes to chronic symptoms in some patients.","additionalDifferentials":["Cervical fracture or dislocation","Cervical spinal cord or nerve root injury","Cervical disc prolapse or radiculopathy","Traumatic brain injury or concussion","Vertebral or carotid artery dissection","Pre-existing cervical spondylosis","Shoulder girdle or temporomandibular joint injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical clearance using the Canadian C-spine rule or NEXUS criteria before imaging","CT of the cervical spine for suspected fracture or in high-risk patients","MRI if there is neurological deficit, suspected cord or disc injury, or ligamentous instability","Plain radiographs where appropriate for lower-risk patients","CT or MR angiography if arterial dissection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neurological deficit, saddle or limb weakness, or signs of spinal cord injury","Midline cervical tenderness or high-risk mechanism suggesting fracture or dislocation","Severe or thunderclap headache or focal signs suggesting arterial dissection","Reduced consciousness or features of significant head injury"],"prognosis":"Most people recover within weeks to a few months, but a substantial minority develop chronic neck pain and disability, more likely with high initial pain, neurological signs, and psychological distress.","pitfalls":["Failing to clear the cervical spine and missing a fracture, dislocation, or cord injury","Prescribing a soft collar and prolonged rest, which worsens outcomes","Overlooking associated concussion or arterial dissection","Not addressing psychosocial factors that predict chronicity"],"management":["First exclude cervical fracture, dislocation or cord and nerve-root injury using a validated rule such as the Canadian C-spine rule, imaging with CT or MRI where indicated","Reassure and educate the patient, emphasising the generally favourable natural history","Encourage early return to normal activity and active mobilisation rather than a soft collar or prolonged rest","Provide simple analgesia such as paracetamol and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain relief","Refer for physiotherapy with an active, graded exercise programme","Identify psychosocial risk factors such as high initial pain and distress that predict chronicity, and address them early","Refer persistent whiplash-associated disorder to a multidisciplinary pain service, and escalate urgently for evolving neurological deficit or suspected arterial dissection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"whipple_arthropathy":{"name":"Whipple disease arthropathy","system":"Rheumatology / Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Whipple arthritis","Tropheryma whipplei arthropathy","articular Whipple disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic seronegative arthropathy that is frequently the earliest manifestation of Whipple disease, often preceding gastrointestinal symptoms by years","Typically a migratory or intermittent, non-destructive, seronegative oligo- or polyarthritis of large and medium joints","Later systemic features include weight loss, chronic diarrhoea, malabsorption, abdominal pain, lymphadenopathy and low-grade fever","Neurological and ophthalmological involvement and culture-negative endocarditis are important extra-articular manifestations","Should be suspected in a middle-aged man with unexplained seronegative arthritis who paradoxically worsens or develops systemic illness on immunosuppression","Diagnosis rests on identifying Tropheryma whipplei, classically PAS-positive macrophages on small-bowel biopsy and confirmatory PCR","Requires prolonged antibiotic therapy, often with an initial course able to penetrate the central nervous system followed by extended oral treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Whipple disease is a chronic systemic infection caused by the actinomycete Tropheryma whipplei, and the joints are among the earliest and most frequently affected sites. Although driven by an organism, the articular manifestation behaves as a seronegative inflammatory arthropathy: infiltration of tissues by bacteria-laden macrophages provokes chronic inflammation with a characteristically non-erosive, migratory joint disease. A subtle underlying defect in host macrophage handling of the organism is thought to permit persistent infection. The arthritis often long predates the classic intestinal malabsorptive phase, and immunosuppressive treatment given for a presumed primary inflammatory arthritis can accelerate the systemic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis","Spondyloarthropathy including reactive arthritis","Palindromic rheumatism","Sarcoid arthropathy","Inflammatory bowel disease-associated arthritis","Adult-onset Still disease or other systemic inflammatory disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with small-bowel biopsy showing PAS-positive macrophages","PCR for Tropheryma whipplei on biopsy, synovial fluid, blood or cerebrospinal fluid","Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP) and screening for anaemia and malabsorption","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis and echocardiography to assess neurological and cardiac involvement","Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies, characteristically negative"],"redFlags":["Weight loss, chronic diarrhoea and malabsorption accompanying a seronegative arthritis","Neurological features (cognitive change, ophthalmoplegia, myorhythmia) suggesting CNS involvement","Paradoxical clinical deterioration after starting corticosteroids or immunosuppression","Culture-negative endocarditis in a patient with unexplained arthropathy"],"prognosis":"Untreated Whipple disease is progressive and can be fatal, but the arthropathy and systemic disease usually respond well to prolonged appropriate antibiotic therapy; central nervous system involvement carries a poorer prognosis and relapse can occur, warranting long-term follow-up.","pitfalls":["Treating the arthritis as primary inflammatory disease with immunosuppression and worsening the underlying infection","Failing to consider Whipple disease in seronegative arthritis with systemic or gastrointestinal features","Relying on histology alone without confirmatory PCR","Under-treating by not addressing potential central nervous system involvement"],"management":["Treat the underlying infection with prolonged antibiotics, typically parenteral induction using ceftriaxone or penicillin followed by long-term oral co-trimoxazole","Avoid immunosuppression and biologics such as anti-TNF agents, which can accelerate or unmask systemic Whipple disease when the arthritis is misdiagnosed as a primary inflammatory arthropathy","Secure the diagnosis before treatment by demonstrating Tropheryma whipplei on small-bowel biopsy with PAS staining and confirmatory PCR","Assess for central nervous system involvement with cerebrospinal fluid PCR, as neurological disease carries a poorer prognosis and influences antibiotic choice","Provide nutritional support and correct deficiencies arising from malabsorption","Monitor the clinical and microbiological response and maintain long-term follow-up given the risk of relapse","Coordinate care across infectious diseases, rheumatology and gastroenterology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"whipple_disease":{"name":"Whipple Disease","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tropheryma whipplei infection"],"chapter":181,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1367-1370","clinicalPearls":["Whipple disease is a rare chronic infection caused by Tropheryma whipplei; because it is uncommon, mimics many other conditions, and needs nonroutine tests, years typically pass between symptom onset and diagnosis (p.1367).","\"Classic\" Whipple disease presents as some combination of migratory arthralgias/seronegative arthritis, weight loss, chronic diarrhea, abdominal pain, and fever, with the duodenum and/or jejunum involved over years (p.1367, p.1368).","Migratory oligo- or polyarthralgia (knees, wrists, ankles, MCP joints) typically precedes gastrointestinal symptoms by an average of 6-8 years; joint disease is present in >80% of patients (p.1368, p.2546).","CNS disease (detected by CSF PCR for T. whipplei) develops in roughly 50% of patients and many are asymptomatic; manifestations include cognitive decline progressing to dementia, personality/mood change, and hypothalamic involvement, and portend a poor prognosis (p.1368).","Oculomasticatory and oculofacial myorhythmia are highly suggestive of Whipple disease; supranuclear gaze palsy (usually vertical) is another neuro-ophthalmologic clue (p.1368).","Diagnosis rests on small-bowel (postbulbar duodenal) biopsy showing lamina propria infiltration by foamy macrophages with diastase-resistant PAS-positive inclusions, combined with PCR for T. whipplei; PCR has substantially improved sensitivity and specificity (p.1368, p.1369).","It is prudent to collect CSF even without CNS symptoms, because asymptomatic CNS disease is common and the CNS is the most common site of relapse, informing treatment duration (p.1369).","Treatment requires prolonged therapy; clinical response is often rapid, but eradication and durable cure are challenged by relapse and host predisposition to reinfection, which can occur years to decades later (p.1369, p.1370).","WD should be considered in any chronic multisystemic process, especially the combination of rheumatologic and intestinal disease with weight loss, with or without neurologic and cardiac involvement (p.1368, p.1369)."],"pathophysiology":"T. whipplei is a weakly staining gram-positive, non-acid-fast bacillus with a small (<1-megabase) genome and incomplete biosynthetic pathways, consistent with an intracellular/nutritionally dependent pathogen; it is one of the slowest-growing human pathogens (doubling time ~18 days). Exposure is far more common than disease, and chronic infection (WD) is a rare outcome (<0.01% of those exposed). Establishing chronic infection involves both an impaired host inflammatory response and pathogen-driven immune modulation: HLA alleles DRB1*13 and DQB1*06 (favoring humoral over cell-mediated responses) raise risk, an impaired TH1 response with increased anti-inflammatory cytokines and regulatory T-cell activity, M2 macrophage polarization with impaired phagosome-lysosome fusion, and blunted T. whipplei-specific T-cell development occur. The organism has tropism for myeloid cells, which it invades and within which it avoids killing; affected tissue is infiltrated by foamy macrophages containing PAS-staining inclusions (ingested bacteria). In the small intestine, villus atrophy, lymphangiectasia, crypt hyperplasia, and surface epithelial apoptosis lead to malabsorptive diarrhea. (p.1367, p.1368)","additionalDifferentials":["Seronegative inflammatory arthritis / rheumatoid arthritis (initial arthralgic phase is often confused with rheumatologic disorders; RF and ANA usually negative) (p.1368)","Lymphoma (mesenteric/retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy on CT commonly raises this concern) (p.1368)","Sarcoidosis or granulomatous vasculitis (noncaseating granulomas from lymphatic/hepatic involvement; ocular disease may also be misdiagnosed as sarcoid) (p.1368)","Behcet's disease (in patients with ocular/uveitic disease) (p.1368)"],"infectionMimics":["Mycobacterial infection (PAS positivity is nonspecific and also occurs with mycobacteria and other microorganisms) (p.1369)","Giardia lamblia co-infection, occasionally identified, can cause diagnostic misdirection (p.1368)","Other causes of culture-negative endocarditis (T. whipplei causes 2.6-6.3% of culture-negative endocarditis) (p.1368)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Crohn disease (intestinal phase confusion) (p.1368)","Celiac disease (intestinal phase confusion; small-bowel biopsy differential) (p.1368, p.2546)","Other rheumatologic disorders during the early arthralgic stage (p.1368)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Postbulbar (more distal) duodenal biopsies with PAS staining for diastase-resistant inclusions in lamina propria macrophages plus PCR for T. whipplei (tissue yield exceeds body-fluid yield) (p.1369)","PCR of duodenal biopsy: a cycle threshold of <=30 can help confirm the diagnosis even when PAS staining is negative (p.1369)","Immunohistochemistry (greater specificity and sensitivity than PAS, usable on archived fixed tissue) and fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) as complementary tools (p.1369)","Electron microscopy to identify the trilaminar cell wall of T. whipplei (p.1369)","CSF examination and PCR for T. whipplei (collect even when asymptomatic); CSF may be normal or show lymphocyte-predominant leukocytosis and elevated protein, with low glucose reported (p.1369)","Brain MRI (lesions usually T2/FLAIR hyperintense, may enhance with gadolinium; may be normal) (p.1368)","Echocardiography for suspected endocarditis (vegetations seen in 50-75%) (p.1368)","FDG-PET may show uptake at affected sites and can guide tissue sampling (p.1368, p.1369)","Biopsy of normal-appearing skin may detect T. whipplei as a minimally invasive diagnostic route in classic WD (p.1369)","Video-capsule endoscopy to identify more distal small-bowel lesions when initial duodenal biopsy is nondiagnostic (p.1368)","Urine PCR as a potential noninvasive test and therapeutic-monitoring tool (positive in ~75% of cases pretreatment in one small study); plasma cell-free DNA next-generation sequencing for endocarditis (p.1369, p.1370)"],"redFlags":["CNS involvement (cognitive decline/dementia, personality change, hypothalamic dysfunction such as polyuria/polydipsia or sleep-cycle disorder, supranuclear gaze palsy, oculomasticatory myorhythmia) portends a poor prognosis with significant mortality risk and neurologic sequelae (p.1368)","Endocarditis (often afebrile, Duke criteria rarely met) may be complicated by congestive heart failure (~40%), embolic events, arrhythmias, or mycotic aneurysm (p.1368)","Progressive weight loss with chronic diarrhea and malabsorption (p.1367, p.1368)","Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) after treatment, with recurrent fever, arthritis, skin lesions, pleuritis, uveitis, and periorbital inflammation; some cases have been fatal (p.1369)"],"prognosis":"Untreated Whipple disease is often fatal, and delayed diagnosis can lead to irreparable organ damage, particularly in the CNS. With appropriate treatment, clinical response is often rapid and at times remarkable, and timely recognition may yield cure with medical management alone; however, eradication requires prolonged therapy and a durable response is challenged by relapse (especially CNS relapse) and host predisposition to reinfection occurring years to decades later. CNS disease carries a significant mortality risk and may leave neurologic sequelae. (p.1367, p.1368, p.1369, p.1370)","pitfalls":["Serology is of little value: patients with active infection mount a poor IgM/IgG response, and a positive result most likely reflects prior exposure and clearance (p.1369)","A normal macroscopic duodenal appearance is common, and a negative test does not exclude WD; pretest probability must be considered (p.1369)","Histology/PAS alone is not optimally sensitive and is nonspecific (also positive with mycobacteria and other organisms); anti-TNF-alpha therapy may decrease PAS sensitivity (p.1369)","Saliva and fecal PCR are inappropriate as sole diagnostic tools because of low positive predictive value (more often reflecting colonization than disease); a positive result needs confirmation from end-organ tissue or body fluid (p.1370)","Treating presumed inflammatory arthritis or uveitis with glucocorticoids or anti-TNF-alpha agents can accelerate disease progression and unmask/precipitate disease; consider screening for WD before immunosuppression (p.1368)","Oral tetracycline or TMP-SMX monotherapy has unacceptable relapse rates, particularly for CNS disease; sequence data indicate TMP is not active against T. whipplei (no dihydrofolate reductase) (p.1369, p.1370)","Detection of T. whipplei sequence in BALF of asymptomatic individuals does not establish disease; caution is needed before diagnosing isolated pneumonia on sequence alone (p.1368)","Culture is not practical for diagnosis as it takes months to turn positive (p.1370)"],"management":["Eradicate Tropheryma whipplei with prolonged antibiotics, typically parenteral induction using ceftriaxone or penicillin followed by long-term oral co-trimoxazole for its central nervous system penetration","Sample and monitor the cerebrospinal fluid by PCR, as central nervous system involvement is common, often subclinical, and demands regimens that cross the blood-brain barrier","Anticipate and treat immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome after starting antibiotics, using corticosteroids for severe reactions","Correct malabsorption with nutritional support and replacement of deficient vitamins, minerals and trace elements","Avoid immunosuppressive therapy, which can precipitate rapid deterioration","Maintain prolonged follow-up with clinical review and PCR to confirm eradication and detect relapse, which particularly affects the nervous system","Coordinate care across infectious diseases, gastroenterology and neurology"],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames the disease by clinical syndrome rather than a graded severity score: asymptomatic colonization/carriage (PCR-detectable T. whipplei in stool, saliva, or duodenal tissue without symptoms); acute infection (e.g., gastroenteritis in children, febrile bacteremia, acute pneumonia); chronic infection comprising \"classic\" Whipple disease (intestinal involvement with multisystem disease) and \"isolated\" infection (organ disease such as endocarditis, neurologic, ocular, or pulmonary disease without intestinal symptoms); and reinfection/relapsing disease, including treatment-associated IRIS. (p.1367, p.1368, p.1369)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1367-1370","Harrison 22e p.2546"],"crossLinks":[]},"white_sutton_syndrome":{"name":"White-Sutton syndrome (POGZ)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WHSUS","POGZ-related syndrome","POGZ-related neurodevelopmental disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A neurodevelopmental disorder caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in POGZ","Developmental delay and intellectual disability are consistent, frequently with autism spectrum disorder","Behavioural features and, in some, hyperphagia with a tendency to obesity are reported","Visual problems, including refractive error and strabismus, are common","Hypotonia, feeding difficulties, and sleep disturbance may be present, especially in early childhood","Subtle and variable facial features can include a broad forehead and midface differences","Most cases arise de novo as new dominant variants","Diagnosis is established by identifying a POGZ variant through exome or gene panel testing"],"pathophysiology":"White-Sutton syndrome results from heterozygous loss-of-function variants in POGZ, which encodes a heterochromatin-associated protein containing zinc-finger domains that participates in chromatin regulation, chromosome segregation, and the control of gene transcription during neurodevelopment. By influencing the expression of genes required for the development and function of neurones, POGZ contributes to normal cognition, behaviour, and sensory processing. Reduced dosage of the protein through haploinsufficiency disrupts these processes, producing the intellectual disability, autism spectrum features, visual problems, and associated findings that characterise the syndrome. Inheritance is autosomal dominant, and most cases occur as de novo events.","additionalDifferentials":["Autism spectrum disorder with intellectual disability of other genetic cause","Other chromatin-regulator neurodevelopmental disorders","Prader-Willi and other syndromes when hyperphagia and obesity predominate","Fragile X syndrome in males with autism and intellectual disability","Copy-number neurodevelopmental syndromes identified on microarray","Other syndromic causes of hypotonia and developmental delay"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or gene panel testing to identify a POGZ variant","Chromosomal microarray to exclude a copy-number cause","Developmental and autism-specific assessment","Ophthalmological review for refractive error and strabismus","Feeding, growth, and weight monitoring, including for emerging hyperphagia","Sleep assessment where sleep disturbance is significant"],"redFlags":["Severe feeding difficulty with faltering growth in infancy","Rapid weight gain from hyperphagia with metabolic consequences","Vision-threatening ocular problems in a young child","Marked behavioural dysregulation affecting safety"],"prognosis":"Lifespan is generally normal, and the long-term picture is defined by intellectual disability and autism spectrum features of variable severity together with any behavioural, visual, and weight-related issues; developmental progress is expected with educational and therapeutic support, and outcome depends chiefly on the degree of neurodevelopmental involvement.","pitfalls":["Labelling the presentation as idiopathic autism and not pursuing genetic testing that would identify POGZ","Overlooking treatable visual problems that affect learning and behaviour","Failing to monitor for hyperphagia and obesity as the child grows","Assuming inheritance from a parent when most cases are de novo"],"management":["With no disease-specific therapy available, provide early developmental intervention with speech, language and occupational therapy for delay and intellectual disability","Offer autism-specific behavioural support and educational adaptations","Arrange ophthalmological correction of refractive error and management of strabismus","Monitor feeding, growth and weight, with dietary measures to address emerging hyperphagia and obesity","Manage sleep disturbance with behavioural sleep strategies","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant condition, noting most cases are de novo, with parental testing to inform recurrence risk","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary neurodevelopmental team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: White-Sutton syndrome","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"widow_spider_envenomation":{"name":"Widow (Latrodectus) spider envenomation","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["latrodectism","black widow bite","redback spider bite","Latrodectus bite","katipo bite"],"chapter":null,"source":"Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies / WHO envenoming guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Latrodectism is dominated by pain and autonomic features rather than local tissue destruction; the bite site itself is often unremarkable","Pain begins locally and radiates to become regional or generalised, with muscle cramping and spasm that may involve the abdomen and mimic an acute abdomen","Diaphoresis is characteristic and may be localised or regional, sometimes with a distribution around the bitten limb","Associated features include hypertension, tachycardia, nausea, headache, tremor and restlessness","Symptoms can be prolonged and wax and wane over days; the bite is rarely life-threatening in otherwise healthy adults","Management principles centre on analgesia; antivenom may be considered for severe or refractory systemic symptoms with attention to allergic reaction risk","There is no dermonecrosis with widow bites, which helps distinguish latrodectism from recluse (loxoscelism) envenomation"],"pathophysiology":"Widow spider venom contains alpha-latrotoxin, which binds pre-synaptic nerve terminals and forms cation-permeable pores that trigger massive, calcium-independent exocytosis of neurotransmitters. Depletion and unregulated release of acetylcholine and catecholamines at neuromuscular junctions and autonomic synapses produce sustained muscle contraction, cramping and autonomic hyperactivity. The result is severe pain, diaphoresis and cardiovascular stimulation without significant local tissue necrosis, since the venom acts on nerve terminals rather than directly on tissue.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute abdomen (appendicitis, perforated viscus, renal colic)","Acute coronary syndrome given chest and abdominal pain with autonomic features","Tetanus or other cause of muscle rigidity","Sympathomimetic toxidrome","Recluse spider or other envenomation","Hypocalcaemia or other electrolyte-driven cramping"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on history and characteristic syndrome","Electrocardiography if chest pain or marked hypertension is present","Creatine kinase if muscle spasm is severe or prolonged","Basic electrolytes and calcium to exclude metabolic causes of cramp","Blood pressure and pain monitoring to gauge severity and treatment response"],"redFlags":["Severe uncontrolled hypertension with end-organ symptoms","Chest pain suggesting cardiac involvement","Symptoms in pregnancy, in young children or in patients with significant comorbidity","Progressive or refractory pain and spasm despite analgesia"],"prognosis":"Latrodectism is painful and can be debilitating for days, but is very rarely fatal in healthy individuals and generally resolves fully with symptomatic care and, where indicated, antivenom.","pitfalls":["Mistaking abdominal muscle rigidity for a surgical abdomen and proceeding to unnecessary laparotomy","Providing inadequate analgesia for what can be very severe, protracted pain","Expecting local necrosis and doubting the diagnosis when the bite site looks benign","Overlooking heightened risk in pregnancy, children or cardiac patients"],"management":["Control pain with adequate analgesia, including opioids, as the mainstay of management","Relieve muscle cramping and spasm with benzodiazepines, and give antiemetics for associated nausea","Reserve widow-spider antivenom for severe or refractory systemic envenomation, weighing the risk of anaphylaxis and serum sickness","Give routine wound care and ensure tetanus immunisation is up to date","Monitor blood pressure and heart rate, obtaining an electrocardiogram where chest pain or marked hypertension occurs","Reassure that latrodectism is self-limiting and rarely fatal in healthy adults, though symptoms may wax and wane over days","Escalate for refractory pain, autonomic instability or cardiovascular compromise"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Clinical toxicology guidance on latrodectism"],"crossLinks":[]},"wiedemann_steiner_syndrome":{"name":"Wiedemann-Steiner syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WSS","KMT2A-related syndrome","MLL-related syndrome","hypertrichosis cubiti with intellectual disability"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Nature Reviews chromatinopathy literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by heterozygous, usually de novo, loss-of-function variants in KMT2A (formerly MLL/MLL1), a histone methyltransferase that deposits activating methyl marks on lysine 4 of histone H3 (H3K4)","Hypertrichosis cubiti, or prominent hair over the elbows, is a well-known clue although it is not present in every child","Thick or arched eyebrows, long eyelashes and generalised hypertrichosis accompany a recognisable facial appearance with hypertelorism and downslanting palpebral fissures","Developmental delay and intellectual disability of variable severity are core features, frequently with short stature and muscular hypotonia","Feeding difficulties in infancy, recurrent infections and behavioural or autistic features are commonly reported","A KMT2A-specific DNA methylation episignature is increasingly used to confirm the diagnosis and to clarify variants of uncertain significance","As a chromatinopathy affecting an H3K4 methyltransferase, it belongs to the same broad family as other Mendelian disorders of the epigenetic machinery"],"pathophysiology":"KMT2A encodes a histone methyltransferase that catalyses methylation of histone H3 at lysine 4, a modification associated with open, transcriptionally active chromatin at gene promoters and enhancers. Loss-of-function variants reduce the enzyme's dose (haploinsufficiency), blunting the deposition of these activating marks and disturbing the precise programmes of gene expression required for growth and neurodevelopment. The consequent dysregulation of developmental gene networks yields the characteristic pattern of intellectual disability, growth restriction, hypertrichosis and craniofacial features, and because the defect produces a reproducible genome-wide methylation signature it can be detected by episignature testing.","additionalDifferentials":["Cornelia de Lange syndrome (NIPBL and related genes)","Kabuki syndrome (KMT2D, KDM6A)","Coffin-Siris syndrome (BAF/SWI-SNF genes)","Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome (CREBBP, EP300)","Congenital generalised hypertrichosis syndromes","Noonan syndrome and other RASopathies","Other chromatinopathies with intellectual disability"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or genome sequencing to identify KMT2A variants","DNA methylation episignature analysis to confirm the diagnosis or resolve uncertain variants","Neurodevelopmental, cognitive and behavioural assessment including autism screening","Growth monitoring and evaluation of short stature","Assessment of feeding in infancy","Ophthalmological and audiological review","Immunological review if recurrent infections are prominent"],"redFlags":["Significant feeding difficulty with faltering growth in infancy","Recurrent or severe infections suggesting immune dysfunction","Developmental regression, which is atypical and warrants reassessment","Emerging seizures requiring neurological evaluation"],"prognosis":"The disorder is compatible with survival into adulthood; intellectual disability is usually mild to moderate but ranges more widely, and most individuals benefit substantially from early developmental, educational and behavioural support, with growth and feeding issues tending to improve after infancy.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because hypertrichosis cubiti is absent, when the feature is inconstant","Mislabelling the child as Cornelia de Lange or Kabuki syndrome without gene-specific or episignature testing","Overlooking recurrent infections that may reflect immune involvement","Dismissing behavioural and autistic features that benefit from targeted intervention"],"management":["With no disease-specific therapy available, provide early developmental intervention and educational support for delay and intellectual disability","Offer physiotherapy and occupational therapy for muscular hypotonia","Support feeding difficulties in infancy and monitor growth and short stature","Treat recurrent infections promptly and investigate if they are unusually frequent","Provide behavioural support and autism-specific intervention where indicated","Arrange ophthalmological and audiological review","Offer genetic counselling for this autosomal dominant condition, which usually arises de novo, with parental testing to inform recurrence risk","Coordinate care through a multidisciplinary neurodevelopmental team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews — Wiedemann-Steiner Syndrome","Nature Reviews Genetics — chromatinopathies and histone methylation disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"wildervanck_syndrome":{"name":"Wildervanck syndrome (cervico-oculo-acoustic)","system":"Genetics / Craniofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cervico-oculo-acoustic syndrome","Wildervanck-Waardenburg-Franceschetti syndrome","COA syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A triad of Klippel-Feil cervical vertebral fusion, Duane retraction anomaly of eye movement, and congenital sensorineural or mixed hearing loss","It occurs almost exclusively in females, suggesting an X-linked or sex-limited mechanism","The Duane anomaly restricts horizontal eye movement, with globe retraction and narrowing of the palpebral fissure on attempted adduction","The short, immobile neck reflects the underlying cervical segmentation failure shared with Klippel-Feil syndrome","Hearing loss can be profound and is a key contributor to communication and developmental difficulty","It is regarded as a variant within the Klippel-Feil spectrum with the added ocular and auditory features","Careful audiological and ophthalmological assessment is central to management, as is caution with the potentially unstable cervical spine"],"pathophysiology":"Wildervanck syndrome combines features attributable to disturbed development in overlapping embryonic regions. The cervical vertebral fusion results from failed segmentation of cervical somites, as in Klippel-Feil syndrome, while the Duane retraction anomaly arises from maldevelopment of the abducens nucleus and nerve, with aberrant innervation of the lateral rectus by a branch of the oculomotor nerve, causing co-contraction and globe retraction. The sensorineural hearing loss reflects inner ear or eighth nerve maldevelopment. The strong female predominance points to an X-linked dominant mechanism with male lethality or a sex-limited multifactorial cause, though the precise genetic basis remains incompletely defined.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated Klippel-Feil syndrome","Isolated Duane retraction syndrome","Oculo-auriculo-vertebral (Goldenhar) spectrum","Moebius syndrome","Congenital fibrosis of the extraocular muscles","MURCS association"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cervical spine imaging including flexion-extension views for fusion and instability","Audiological assessment including brainstem-evoked responses","Ophthalmological evaluation of ocular motility to confirm Duane anomaly","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brainstem and inner ear","Renal ultrasound where associated anomalies are suspected","Genetic evaluation and counselling"],"redFlags":["Cervical instability with a risk of spinal cord injury","Undetected profound hearing loss delaying communication development","Neurological signs of cord compression","High anaesthetic risk from restricted neck movement and potential instability","Amblyopia risk from the ocular motility disorder"],"prognosis":"The malformations are static and non-progressive; with hearing rehabilitation, ophthalmological management, and attention to cervical stability, affected individuals generally have normal cognition and a good functional outlook.","pitfalls":["Managing the hearing loss in isolation and missing the cervical and ocular components","Overlooking cervical instability before anaesthesia or manipulation","Mistaking Duane anomaly for a simple sixth nerve palsy","Failing to recognise the female predominance and its genetic implications","Neglecting early hearing intervention critical to language development"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists as the malformations are static; care is rehabilitative and directed at the individual triad components","Aural rehabilitation for the sensorineural or mixed hearing loss with hearing aids or cochlear implantation, supported by speech and language therapy","Ophthalmological management of the Duane retraction anomaly, correcting anomalous head posture and treating amblyopia, with strabismus surgery reserved for significant deviation","Protection of the fused, unstable cervical spine by avoiding high-impact activity, with periodic flexion-extension imaging and neurosurgical referral if instability or myelopathy develops","Coordinated multidisciplinary follow-up involving audiology, ophthalmology, ENT and spinal orthopaedics, with educational support for hearing-related developmental difficulty","Genetic counselling for the family given the likely X-linked or sex-limited inheritance and marked female predominance","Escalate for spinal surgical assessment if there are signs of cervical cord compression or progressive instability"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Gorlin's Syndromes of the Head and Neck"],"crossLinks":[]},"williams_campbell_syndrome":{"name":"Williams-Campbell syndrome","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bronchial cartilage deficiency","congenital cystic bronchiectasis","Williams-Campbell bronchiectasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Respiratory Medicine and Paediatric Pulmonology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare disorder of deficient or absent cartilage in the fourth to sixth (subsegmental) generation bronchi, with more proximal and distal airways spared","The cartilage-deficient segments balloon on inspiration and collapse on expiration, producing distal cystic bronchiectasis","Usually presents in childhood with recurrent respiratory infections, chronic productive cough, wheeze, and breathlessness","Dynamic imaging or fluoroscopy shows ballooning of the affected bronchi on inspiration and collapse on expiration, a helpful diagnostic sign","CT reveals cystic bronchiectasis in the mid airways with a characteristic distribution corresponding to the cartilage-deficient generations","The pattern of bronchiectasis distal to normal proximal airways helps distinguish it from acquired and other congenital causes","Management is supportive with airway clearance, treatment of infections, and management of bronchiectasis, as there is no specific therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Williams-Campbell syndrome is characterised by a congenital deficiency or absence of the cartilaginous support in the medium-sized subsegmental bronchi, typically the fourth to sixth generations. Lacking rigid cartilage, these airway segments lose their structural integrity and behave abnormally with respiration, distending on inspiration and collapsing on expiration. This dynamic instability impairs airflow and cough-driven clearance, leading to secretion retention, recurrent infection, and the development of characteristic distal cystic bronchiectasis. Because the proximal airways and the more peripheral airways retain normal or adequate structure, the disease has a distinctive intermediate distribution. The condition is generally considered congenital, and its precise molecular cause is not established.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystic fibrosis","Primary ciliary dyskinesia","Post-infectious bronchiectasis","Mounier-Kuhn syndrome (tracheobronchomegaly)","Immunodeficiency-related bronchiectasis","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution CT demonstrating cystic bronchiectasis in the mid-generation bronchi","Dynamic expiratory imaging or fluoroscopy showing bronchial ballooning and collapse","Sweat testing and CFTR analysis to exclude cystic fibrosis","Assessment of ciliary function and immunoglobulins to exclude other causes","Sputum microbiology in recurrent infection","Pulmonary function tests to assess airflow obstruction"],"redFlags":["Recurrent severe pneumonia or respiratory failure from extensive bronchiectasis","Progressive decline in lung function in a young patient","Haemoptysis complicating cystic bronchiectasis","Development of cor pulmonale in advanced disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the extent of bronchiectasis and frequency of infection; with diligent airway clearance and infection control some patients do well, while extensive disease can lead to progressive respiratory impairment and, in severe cases, respiratory failure.","pitfalls":["Attributing the bronchiectasis to a generic post-infective cause and missing the congenital pattern","Not recognising the characteristic mid-airway distribution distal to normal proximal bronchi","Failing to exclude cystic fibrosis, ciliary dyskinesia, and immunodeficiency","Overlooking the diagnostic value of dynamic imaging showing bronchial ballooning and collapse"],"management":["No therapy corrects the congenital bronchial cartilage deficiency, so care mirrors bronchiectasis management centred on airway clearance and infection control","Daily airway clearance with chest physiotherapy, postural drainage and mucoactive agents to overcome impaired cough-driven secretion clearance","Prompt antibiotics for infective exacerbations guided by sputum microbiology, with long-term macrolide considered for frequent exacerbations","Immunisation against influenza and pneumococcus and prompt treatment of intercurrent respiratory infection","Regular surveillance of lung function and cross-sectional imaging to detect progression of the distal cystic bronchiectasis","Pulmonary rehabilitation and, for advancing disease, supplemental oxygen or non-invasive ventilation, with lung transplantation considered in end-stage respiratory failure","Multidisciplinary respiratory follow-up, with cystic fibrosis, ciliary dysfunction and immunodeficiency excluded as mimics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Kendig's Disorders of the Respiratory Tract in Children"],"crossLinks":[]},"williams_syndrome":{"name":"Williams syndrome","system":"Genetic / Paediatrics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Williams-Beuren syndrome","WBS","7q11.23 deletion syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard medical genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Distinctive personality with an overly friendly, socially engaging manner and characteristic hypersociability","Recognisable elfin facies including a broad forehead, short nose with full nasal tip, wide mouth, full lips, and a small chin","Supravalvular aortic stenosis is the hallmark cardiovascular lesion, and other arteries including renal arteries may also narrow","Idiopathic infantile hypercalcaemia may occur and requires attention to dietary calcium and vitamin D intake","Cognitive profile shows relative strength in language and social skills but marked weakness in visuospatial tasks","Caused by a microdeletion at 7q11.23 that includes the elastin (ELN) gene, explaining the arteriopathy","Connective tissue involvement contributes to hernias, joint problems, and a hoarse voice","Hypertension may develop over time and requires monitoring given the diffuse arteriopathy"],"pathophysiology":"Williams syndrome results from a recurrent hemizygous microdeletion of a contiguous set of genes at chromosome 7q11.23. Loss of one copy of the elastin gene (ELN) reduces elastin in arterial walls, producing the generalised arteriopathy and characteristic supravalvular aortic stenosis. Deletion of adjacent genes, including those involved in neurodevelopment such as GTF2I, contributes to the distinctive cognitive and behavioural profile. The deletion usually arises de novo through misalignment of low-copy repeats flanking the region during meiosis. Disturbed calcium regulation of uncertain mechanism accounts for the infantile hypercalcaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Noonan syndrome","Smith-Magenis syndrome","Kabuki syndrome","Fetal alcohol spectrum disorder","Other causes of supravalvular aortic stenosis such as familial ELN mutations"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted testing for the 7q11.23 deletion, such as fluorescence in situ hybridisation or chromosomal microarray","Echocardiography and blood pressure assessment for cardiovascular involvement","Serum and urinary calcium studies to detect hypercalcaemia","Renal ultrasound and assessment for renal artery stenosis","Developmental, hearing, and ophthalmological evaluation"],"redFlags":["Severe or progressive supravalvular aortic stenosis with risk of sudden cardiac events, especially under anaesthesia","Symptomatic hypercalcaemia in infancy","Hypertension from renal or diffuse arterial narrowing","Increased anaesthetic risk due to coronary and aortic involvement requiring specialist planning"],"prognosis":"Many individuals live into adulthood with support, but life expectancy and quality depend on the severity of cardiovascular disease; intellectual disability is usually mild to moderate and social strengths aid functioning.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the friendly, verbal manner for higher overall cognitive ability than is present","Overlooking cardiovascular disease and the heightened anaesthetic risk","Failing to screen for and manage hypercalcaemia","Not monitoring for later hypertension and arterial complications"],"management":["No treatment corrects the 7q11.23 microdeletion, so management is directed at the arteriopathy, hypercalcaemia and developmental profile","Cardiovascular management of supravalvular aortic stenosis and other arterial narrowings with blood pressure control and surgical relief of severe or progressive stenosis","Management of idiopathic infantile hypercalcaemia by restricting dietary calcium and vitamin D intake","Developmental and behavioural support with early intervention, speech and language therapy and educational input tailored to the visuospatial weakness and hypersociability","Lifelong surveillance including echocardiography, blood pressure, renal ultrasound and renal artery assessment, serum calcium, hearing, vision and thyroid function","Heightened peri-anaesthetic vigilance given the risk of sudden death from coronary or biventricular outflow arteriopathy","Multidisciplinary care with cardiology, genetics and developmental paediatrics, plus genetic counselling as the deletion is usually de novo"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"wilms_tumour":{"name":"Wilms Tumour","system":"Renal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nephroblastoma","Wilms tumor"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common primary renal malignancy of childhood, typically presenting in preschool children around 2-5 years of age.","The usual presentation is an asymptomatic, smooth abdominal mass discovered by a parent or on routine examination, sometimes with abdominal pain, haematuria, or hypertension.","The mass characteristically does not cross the midline, in contrast to neuroblastoma.","Associated with syndromes and anomalies such as WAGR, Beckwith-Wiedemann, and Denys-Drash, and with hemihypertrophy and aniridia; these children warrant surveillance.","Examination should be gentle, avoiding vigorous palpation of the mass because of the risk of tumour rupture.","Staging and histology (favourable versus anaplastic) guide a multimodal approach combining surgery, chemotherapy, and sometimes radiotherapy.","Bilateral disease occurs in a minority and requires nephron-sparing strategies to preserve renal function.","The lungs are the most common site of metastatic spread, so chest imaging is part of staging.","Overall survival is high, making Wilms tumour one of the success stories of paediatric oncology."],"pathophysiology":"Wilms tumour arises from embryonal nephrogenic (metanephric) blastema, often showing a classic triphasic pattern of blastemal, epithelial, and stromal elements. It is linked to abnormalities of genes involved in kidney development, including WT1, and to epigenetic changes at chromosome 11p; persistent nephrogenic rests are precursor lesions. Anaplastic histology confers a worse prognosis and greater treatment resistance.","additionalDifferentials":["Neuroblastoma","Mesoblastic nephroma (in infants)","Renal cell carcinoma (older children)","Clear cell sarcoma or rhabdoid tumour of the kidney","Multicystic dysplastic kidney or hydronephrosis","Hepatoblastoma or other abdominal masses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal ultrasound as the initial imaging test","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) of the abdomen to define the mass and the contralateral kidney","Chest imaging to assess pulmonary metastases","Full blood count, renal and liver function, urinalysis","Blood pressure measurement","Assessment for inferior vena cava or renal vein tumour thrombus"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging abdominal mass","Gross haematuria or significant hypertension","Features of tumour rupture (acute abdominal pain, distension, haemodynamic instability)","Respiratory symptoms suggesting pulmonary metastases","Signs of vascular (caval) extension"],"prognosis":"With risk-adapted multimodal therapy, most children with favourable-histology disease are cured; prognosis is worse with anaplastic histology, advanced stage, or unfavourable biological features.","pitfalls":["Aggressive palpation of the abdominal mass, risking rupture","Confusing it with neuroblastoma, which typically crosses the midline and has different markers","Missing hypertension as an associated finding","Failing to screen children with predisposing syndromes","Overlooking the contralateral kidney for bilateral disease"],"management":["Multimodal, risk-adapted therapy combining surgery, chemotherapy and selective radiotherapy according to stage and favourable versus anaplastic histology","Radical nephrectomy with lymph node sampling as the surgical mainstay, with nephron-sparing surgery considered in bilateral or syndromic disease","Chemotherapy using agents such as vincristine, actinomycin D and, for higher-risk disease, doxorubicin, given as neoadjuvant or adjuvant treatment per protocol","Radiotherapy reserved for higher-stage, anaplastic or residual disease","Gentle handling with avoidance of vigorous palpation and unplanned biopsy because of the risk of tumour rupture and upstaging","Renal ultrasound surveillance in predisposing conditions such as WAGR, Beckwith-Wiedemann, Denys-Drash, hemihypertrophy and aniridia","Long-term monitoring for relapse and treatment late effects including anthracycline cardiotoxicity and renal impairment, within a paediatric oncology multidisciplinary team and with genetic counselling where syndromic"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"wilsons_disease":{"name":"Wilson's Disease","system":"Hepatology / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hepatolenticular degeneration"],"chapter":427,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3338","clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder of copper transport caused by loss-of-function variants in ATP7B (a P-type copper-transporting ATPase), which normally mediates copper removal from the liver via biliary excretion and prevents brain copper accumulation (p.3338).","Typically affects adolescents and young adults; presenting features include nonspecific liver disease, neurologic abnormalities, psychiatric illness, hemolytic anemia, renal tubular Fanconi syndrome, and skeletal abnormalities (p.3338).","Age influences presentation: nearly all individuals presenting with liver disease are <30 years, whereas neurologic or psychiatric presentations may range from the first to the fifth decade; regardless of presentation, some degree of liver disease is invariably present (p.3338).","Neurologic presentation reflects basal ganglia (caudate, putamen) involvement: dysarthria, dystonia (including facial grimacing/risus sardonicus), rigidity, tremor (classically 'wing-beating'), choreiform movements, abnormal gait, and uncoordinated handwriting; WD can properly be classified as a movement disorder (p.3338, p.3518).","Kayser-Fleischer ring (golden to greenish-brown band in the peripheral cornea, from copper deposition in Descemet's membrane) is pathognomonic; present in ~95% of patients with neurologic signs but only about two-thirds of those with hepatic presentations (p.3339).","Supportive biochemistry: low serum copper, low serum ceruloplasmin, increased 24-h urinary copper excretion (>100 ug/24 h), elevated transaminases, aminoaciduria, and hemolytic anemia (p.3339).","Hepatic copper >200 ug per gram dry weight (normal 20-50) on percutaneous needle liver biopsy is a gold-standard biochemical diagnostic finding (p.3339).","Diagnosis relies on a combination of clinical, biochemical, and molecular features; the Leipzig scoring system (2001), endorsed by EASL, weights and collates these features as a valuable diagnostic guide (p.3339).","One of the best-characterized and most effectively managed inborn errors of metabolism; prompt diagnosis in the presymptomatic or early symptomatic phase plus lifelong treatment are needed to prevent premature mortality (p.3338)."],"pathophysiology":"Autosomal recessive disease caused by loss-of-function variants in ATP7B, a highly conserved P-type copper-transporting ATPase expressed primarily in liver and kidney. ATP7B normally mediates copper removal from the liver via biliary excretion and prevents brain copper accumulation; its loss leads to pathologic copper accumulation, primarily impacting the liver and brain. Neurologic disease reflects copper toxicity in the basal ganglia (caudate, putamen, striatum) with neurodegeneration and astrogliosis. Copper also deposits in the cornea (Kayser-Fleischer ring) and lens (sunflower cataract), and renal copper toxicity produces tubular (Fanconi) dysfunction. Massive sudden release of hepatic copper into the circulation directly damages red cell membranes, causing hemolytic anemia (p.3338-3339).","additionalDifferentials":["Viral hepatitis and cirrhosis (often the initial diagnostic considerations in patients who actually have WD) (p.3338)","Autoimmune hepatitis / chronic hepatitis (WD in adolescence may present with chronic-hepatitis features long before neurologic signs or Kayser-Fleischer rings appear) (p.3026-area AIH text, ch.355/427)","Parkinson disease and other movement disorders (WD may be mistakenly diagnosed as these) (p.3338, p.3518)","Substance abuse (a competing consideration in late-teen/early-twenties patients presenting with psychiatric features) (p.3339)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral hepatitis (a frequent initial misdiagnosis in patients with the hepatic presentation of WD) (p.3338)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cirrhosis of other cause (p.3338)","Autoimmune/chronic hepatitis (p.3338, ch.355/427)","Parkinson disease and other movement disorders (p.3338, p.3518)","Primary psychiatric illness or substance abuse in adolescents/young adults (p.3339)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ceruloplasmin (initial screening test; characteristically low) and serum copper (low) (p.3339)","24-h urinary copper excretion (elevated, >100 ug/24 h; use acid-washed copper-free containers); penicillamine challenge with serial urine copper as a variation (p.3339)","Percutaneous needle liver biopsy for quantitative hepatic copper (gold standard; >200 ug/g dry weight characteristic) by ICP-MS or atomic absorption spectrometry; histochemical copper staining is less reliable (p.3339)","Slit-lamp or optical coherence tomography ophthalmologic examination for Kayser-Fleischer rings (required to detect early rings) (p.3339)","Radiolabeled 64Cu incorporation into ceruloplasmin (highly specific; WD patients incorporate very little) (p.3339)","ATP7B molecular/genetic testing (>650 pathogenic variants catalogued; common missense variants H1069Q, M645R, R778L) (p.3339)","T2-weighted brain MRI in neurologic disease may show generalized atrophy and signal hypointensity in caudate head, putamen, globus pallidus, substantia nigra, and red nucleus (~50%), though imaging correlates poorly with clinical features (p.3518)"],"redFlags":["Hemolytic anemia from sudden, massive release of hepatic copper into the circulation directly toxic to red cell membranes can be sudden and catastrophic (p.3339).","Any teenager or young adult with new-onset psychiatric signs should have WD formally excluded (p.3339).","WD should always be considered in the differential of a movement disorder, particularly when arising in the first decades of life (p.3518)."],"prognosis":"In the absence of treatment, the course is progressive, leading to severe neurologic dysfunction and early/premature death in most patients, although a small proportion experience a relatively benign course. WD is one of the most effectively managed inborn errors of metabolism: prompt diagnosis in the presymptomatic or early symptomatic phase and lifelong treatment prevent premature mortality. Liver transplantation, generally needed only when delayed diagnosis or poor compliance has caused irreversible hepatic damage, can correct both hepatic failure and crippling neurologic impairment (p.3338, p.3340, p.3518).","pitfalls":["WD is treatable and progression is preventable, so it is critical to test for it in any young patient with liver disease and/or a movement disorder or new psychiatric illness; missed diagnoses are tragic (p.3338, p.3518).","Normal blood copper, ceruloplasmin, or urinary copper levels do NOT exclude the diagnosis (p.3518).","WD in adolescence may present with chronic-hepatitis features long before neurologic manifestations or Kayser-Fleischer rings appear (p.427/355).","Kayser-Fleischer rings are absent in about one-third of patients with hepatic presentations, so their absence does not rule out WD (p.3339).","In patients with neurologic presentations, penicillamine treatment can induce paradoxical worsening of neurologic status (p.3340)."],"management":["Copper chelation with penicillamine or trientine to mobilise and enhance urinary excretion of accumulated copper in symptomatic disease","Zinc salts to block intestinal copper absorption, used for maintenance and in pre-symptomatic or stable patients","Dietary copper restriction avoiding shellfish, liver, nuts, chocolate and mushrooms","Regular monitoring of serum and urinary copper, liver function and full blood count, with vigilance for paradoxical early neurological worsening when starting chelation","Liver transplantation for fulminant hepatic failure or decompensated cirrhosis, which is also corrective of the metabolic defect","Symptomatic support for neurological, psychiatric and haemolytic manifestations, with lifelong treatment adherence emphasised","Screening of first-degree relatives and genetic counselling given autosomal recessive inheritance, with urgent escalation for acute liver failure or Coombs-negative haemolysis"],"severityClassification":"No single graded severity scale is given in the source; diagnosis is formalized using a combination of clinical, biochemical, and molecular features, collated by the Leipzig scoring system (2001, endorsed by EASL). Disease is broadly staged as presymptomatic/early symptomatic versus advanced; advanced or medically unresponsive hepatic disease is the indication for liver transplantation (p.3339-3340).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3338","Harrison 22e p.3339","Harrison 22e p.3340","Harrison 22e p.3518"],"crossLinks":["movement_disorders","hemolytic_anemia"]},"wiskott_aldrich_syndrome":{"name":"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","system":"Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WAS","eczema-thrombocytopenia-immunodeficiency syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome is an X-linked disorder classically presenting in boys with the triad of eczema, thrombocytopenia with small platelets, and recurrent infections","It is caused by mutations in the WAS gene encoding the WAS protein, which regulates the actin cytoskeleton in haematopoietic cells","The thrombocytopenia is distinctive for producing microthrombocytes (abnormally small platelets), unlike most other causes of low platelets","Bleeding, ranging from petechiae and bruising to serious intracranial haemorrhage, is often the earliest and most dangerous manifestation","The infection pattern reflects combined humoral and cellular immunodeficiency, with encapsulated bacteria, opportunistic organisms, and viruses all contributing","There is a marked predisposition to autoimmune disease (such as haemolytic anaemia and vasculitis) and to malignancy, particularly lymphoma","Milder allelic conditions include X-linked thrombocytopenia, while certain mutations cause X-linked neutropenia","Definitive treatment is haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, with gene therapy an emerging option; supportive care includes immunoglobulin replacement, infection prophylaxis, and careful bleeding management"],"pathophysiology":"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome results from loss-of-function mutations in the WAS gene on the X chromosome, which encodes a protein that links cell-surface signalling to reorganisation of the actin cytoskeleton in blood cells. Defective cytoskeletal dynamics impair the formation of immune synapses, the motility and function of T cells, dendritic cells, and phagocytes, and the normal production and structure of platelets, yielding small, poorly functioning platelets and combined immunodeficiency. Disturbed regulatory T-cell function and abnormal B-cell responses drive the high rate of autoimmunity, while impaired immune surveillance and chronic immune dysregulation predispose to lymphoid malignancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)","Atopic dermatitis with recurrent infection","Severe combined immunodeficiency and other combined immunodeficiencies","X-linked thrombocytopenia (milder WAS spectrum)","Hyper-IgE syndrome","Congenital thrombocytopenias with normal platelet size"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film assessing platelet number and platelet size (mean platelet volume)","WAS gene sequencing and analysis of WAS protein expression","Immunoglobulin levels and specific antibody responses","Lymphocyte subsets and functional T-cell testing","Autoimmune and haemolysis screens where indicated","Surveillance for lymphoma in older patients"],"redFlags":["Significant bleeding, especially intracranial haemorrhage, as an emergency","Recurrent or opportunistic infections requiring urgent immunological workup","New lymphadenopathy or systemic symptoms suggesting lymphoma","Live vaccines must be avoided because of the combined immunodeficiency","Blood products should be irradiated and leucodepleted to prevent transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease"],"prognosis":"Untreated classic disease carries a poor prognosis owing to bleeding, infection, autoimmunity, and malignancy, but successful haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, ideally performed early, can be curative; milder phenotypes such as X-linked thrombocytopenia have a better outlook though still require monitoring.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing isolated ITP and giving immunosuppression or splenectomy without recognising the underlying syndrome","Overlooking the diagnostic clue of small platelet size on the blood film","Administering live vaccines or non-irradiated blood products","Underestimating the lifelong risks of autoimmunity and malignancy after immune reconstitution","Delaying referral for transplantation in a child with classic severe disease"],"management":["Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is the definitive curative treatment and is best undertaken early, with gene therapy an emerging alternative","Immunoglobulin replacement together with antimicrobial prophylaxis against encapsulated bacteria and Pneumocystis to reduce the combined immunodeficiency infection burden","Bleeding management with platelet support for serious haemorrhage, using irradiated CMV-safe blood products and avoiding antiplatelet agents; splenectomy is generally avoided where transplantation is planned","Eczema care with emollients and topical corticosteroids, and immunosuppression for autoimmune complications such as haemolytic anaemia and vasculitis","Avoidance of live vaccines given the immunodeficiency","Surveillance for lymphoma and other malignancy, particularly in older patients","Genetic counselling for this X-linked disorder with maternal carrier testing and family screening, coordinated through a clinical immunology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"wiskott_aldrich_thrombocytopenia":{"name":"X-linked thrombocytopenia (Wiskott-Aldrich variant)","system":"Immune system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["X-linked thrombocytopenia","XLT","Attenuated Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A milder allelic variant of Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome caused by hypomorphic missense mutations in the WAS gene that retain partial WAS protein expression.","Cardinal feature is congenital microthrombocytopenia: a low platelet count with characteristically small platelet volume.","Bleeding is usually mild, with petechiae, bruising and epistaxis, in contrast to the severe eczema and profound immunodeficiency of classic Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome.","Eczema and infections are absent or mild, but immune dysregulation and autoimmunity can still occur over time.","Small platelet size distinguishes it from immune thrombocytopenia, where platelets are normal or large, and helps avoid unnecessary immunosuppression or splenectomy.","The WAS protein regulates actin cytoskeletal remodelling in haematopoietic cells; the degree of residual function correlates inversely with disease severity.","Even in this attenuated form there is a lifelong, if lower, risk of autoimmune complications and lymphoid malignancy requiring surveillance.","Splenectomy raises the platelet count but increases sepsis risk and is used cautiously; haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is curative and considered in severe cases."],"pathophysiology":"X-linked thrombocytopenia is caused by mutations in the WAS gene on the X chromosome that encode Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein, a regulator of actin polymerisation in haematopoietic cells acting downstream of Rho-family GTPases. Hypomorphic (usually missense) mutations preserve some residual protein and function, producing the attenuated phenotype rather than the null mutations that cause classic Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome. Defective cytoskeletal remodelling impairs megakaryocyte platelet production and shortens platelet survival, and it disturbs the immunological synapse, cell migration and regulatory T-cell function. The result is microthrombocytopenia with relatively preserved immunity, though residual defects in immune regulation still predispose to autoimmunity and malignancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Classic Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","Immune (idiopathic) thrombocytopenic purpura","Congenital amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia","MYH9-related disorders (giant platelets, in contrast)","X-linked thrombocytopenia with dyserythropoietic anaemia (GATA1 mutation)","Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Inherited thrombocytopenias with normal platelet size"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with mean platelet volume showing small platelets","Peripheral blood film for platelet size and morphology","Flow cytometry for WAS protein expression in leucocytes","WAS gene sequencing to confirm hypomorphic mutation","Immunoglobulin levels and specific antibody responses","Lymphocyte subset analysis and regulatory T-cell assessment","Direct platelet antibody testing to help exclude immune thrombocytopenia"],"redFlags":["Intracranial or other severe haemorrhage during marked thrombocytopenia","Emergence of autoimmune cytopenias or vasculitis","Development of lymphoma or other lymphoid malignancy","Progressive fall in platelet count or new eczema/infection suggesting phenotype conversion","Post-splenectomy overwhelming encapsulated-organism sepsis"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is substantially better than in classic Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, and many patients do well with supportive care, but lifelong risks of severe bleeding, autoimmunity and malignancy persist. Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation can be curative and is weighed against the milder natural history.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the condition as immune thrombocytopenia and giving repeated immunosuppression or splenectomy.","Overlooking the diagnostic clue of small mean platelet volume.","Assuming the attenuated phenotype removes the need for surveillance of autoimmunity and malignancy.","Performing splenectomy without appreciating the lifelong infection risk in a WAS-related disorder.","Failing to test the WAS gene in a boy with congenital microthrombocytopenia and a suggestive family history."],"management":["Supportive management of congenital microthrombocytopenia, recognising the small platelet size to avoid misdiagnosis as immune thrombocytopenia and needless immunosuppression or splenectomy","Bleeding precautions with platelet transfusion reserved for significant haemorrhage or surgical cover, and avoidance of antiplatelet drugs","Longitudinal monitoring for emerging immune dysregulation, autoimmunity and malignancy given persistent lifelong risk","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation considered for severe or complicated cases, weighed against the generally milder natural history","Immunoglobulin replacement or antimicrobial prophylaxis only if humoral defect and recurrent infection develop","Genetic counselling for this X-linked WAS variant, with maternal carrier testing and family screening","Regular immunology and haematology follow-up with escalation for severe bleeding or new autoimmune features"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies, current edition","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood, current edition","International Union of Immunological Societies phenotypic classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"wolcott_rallison_syndrome":{"name":"Wolcott-Rallison syndrome","system":"Endocrine / Monogenic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WRS","EIF2AK3-related neonatal diabetes","Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia with early-onset diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Monogenic diabetes references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The syndrome is autosomal recessive, caused by biallelic mutations in EIF2AK3, which encodes the endoplasmic reticulum stress kinase PERK.","The classic triad is early-onset permanent diabetes, multiple epiphyseal (spondyloepiphyseal) dysplasia, and recurrent episodes of acute liver dysfunction.","It is the commonest cause of permanent neonatal or early-infancy diabetes in consanguineous populations.","Diabetes usually appears first, with skeletal dysplasia and growth failure becoming apparent through early childhood.","Episodic acute liver failure, frequently precipitated by intercurrent infection, is life-threatening and may cause sudden deterioration.","Additional features can include renal dysfunction, recurrent infections, exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, and neurodevelopmental impairment.","Unlike potassium-channel neonatal diabetes, the beta-cell defect is not sulfonylurea-responsive and insulin therapy is required."],"pathophysiology":"Wolcott-Rallison syndrome results from loss of function of PERK, an endoplasmic reticulum transmembrane kinase encoded by EIF2AK3 that is central to the unfolded protein response. When misfolded proteins accumulate, PERK phosphorylates the translation initiation factor eIF2-alpha, transiently attenuating protein synthesis to relieve endoplasmic reticulum stress. Cells with a heavy secretory burden, notably pancreatic beta cells, hepatocytes and osteoblasts, depend on this protective pathway; when it fails, unresolved endoplasmic reticulum stress drives apoptosis. Beta-cell loss produces early permanent insulin-deficient diabetes, osteoblast dysfunction underlies the multiple epiphyseal and spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia with disproportionate growth failure, and vulnerable hepatocytes give rise to episodic acute liver failure that is often unmasked by the metabolic demands of infection. The same broad secretory vulnerability accounts for the variable renal, exocrine pancreatic, haematological and neurological involvement that makes this a true multisystem disorder rather than isolated diabetes.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of permanent neonatal diabetes (KCNJ11, ABCC8, INS)","IPEX syndrome with autoimmune enteropathy and early diabetes","Mitochondrial disorders with diabetes and multisystem involvement","Other skeletal dysplasias with epiphyseal changes unrelated to diabetes","Alternative genetic and metabolic causes of paediatric acute liver failure","Syndromic pancreatic hypoplasia or agenesis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["EIF2AK3 genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Blood glucose with C-peptide and negative islet autoantibodies","Liver function tests and coagulation profile, monitored closely during illness","Skeletal survey demonstrating multiple epiphyseal or spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia","Renal function assessment and urinalysis","Exocrine pancreatic function testing where malabsorption is suspected"],"redFlags":["Acute liver failure with coagulopathy, hypoglycaemia or encephalopathy, often during infection","Rapid clinical deterioration or sudden collapse during an intercurrent illness","Acute kidney injury superimposed on chronic renal involvement","Sepsis in a child with recurrent infections and multisystem fragility"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is guarded, dominated by recurrent hepatic and renal crises that can be fatal in early childhood, particularly during intercurrent infection. Meticulous metabolic control, aggressive management of illness and multidisciplinary surveillance improve outcomes, but life-limiting complications remain common.","pitfalls":["Attributing the diabetes to type 1 disease and missing the underlying syndrome and its recurrence risk.","Underestimating the speed and severity of infection-triggered acute liver failure.","Failing to anticipate multisystem involvement beyond diabetes and skeletal disease.","Using potentially hepatotoxic agents without regard to the fragile liver.","Overlooking consanguinity as a clue to a recessive syndromic diagnosis."],"management":["Lifelong insulin replacement for the permanent, non-autoimmune insulin-deficient diabetes that typically presents in neonatal life or early infancy","Anticipatory intensive management of intercurrent illness with close monitoring of liver function and coagulation, as infection frequently precipitates life-threatening acute liver failure","Supportive hepatic care during episodic acute liver dysfunction, with liver transplantation considered for recurrent or fulminant failure","Orthopaedic and physiotherapy management of the spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia and disproportionate growth failure","Pancreatic enzyme replacement and nutritional support where exocrine pancreatic insufficiency and malabsorption occur, with monitoring of renal function","Multidisciplinary coordination across paediatric endocrinology, hepatology, orthopaedics and clinical genetics","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder with family screening, and a clear sick-day and emergency plan for rapid deterioration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric endocrinology reference texts","Clinical genetics reference resources on recessive multisystem disorders","International consensus guidance on monogenic and neonatal diabetes"],"crossLinks":[]},"wolf_hirschhorn_syndrome":{"name":"Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome","system":"Genetics / Dysmorphology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WHS","4p deletion syndrome","4p minus syndrome","Pitt-Rogers-Danks syndrome","monosomy 4p"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The 'Greek warrior helmet' facial appearance, formed by a broad, high nasal bridge continuing to the forehead with hypertelorism and a short philtrum, is characteristic","Caused by deletion of the distal short arm of chromosome 4, involving the WHS critical regions","Marked prenatal and postnatal growth restriction, microcephaly and profound feeding difficulty are near-universal","Seizures, often beginning in infancy and sometimes provoked by fever, are a core feature and a major cause of morbidity","Intellectual disability is usually severe, with hypotonia and limited speech","Most deletions are de novo, but roughly a tenth arise from an unbalanced parental translocation, mandating parental karyotyping","Midline defects such as cleft lip/palate, coloboma, cardiac septal defects and genitourinary anomalies are frequent"],"pathophysiology":"Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome results from haploinsufficiency of contiguous genes within the distal 4p16.3 region, notably the WHS critical regions containing candidate genes such as WHSC1/NSD2 and LETM1. Reduced dosage of these genes, which influence chromatin regulation, calcium/ion homeostasis and neuronal function, disrupts craniofacial, cardiac and central nervous system development. Loss of LETM1 in particular is implicated in the prominent seizure phenotype. Larger deletions generally produce a more severe clinical picture, and unbalanced familial translocations can also cause the condition.","additionalDifferentials":["Cri-du-chat syndrome","Other subtelomeric deletion syndromes","CHARGE syndrome","Cornelia de Lange syndrome","Opitz G/BBB syndrome","Non-specific syndromic intellectual disability with epilepsy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal microarray to confirm and size the 4p deletion","Karyotype and parental karyotypes to identify a familial translocation","Electroencephalography and neuroimaging to characterise the seizure disorder","Echocardiography for congenital heart disease","Renal tract ultrasound for structural anomalies","Feeding, swallowing and nutritional assessment given severe failure to thrive"],"redFlags":["Prolonged or clustered seizures and status epilepticus requiring urgent management","Failure to thrive and dehydration from severe feeding difficulty","Undiagnosed congenital heart disease causing cardiorespiratory compromise","Recurrent aspiration pneumonia"],"prognosis":"Survival is variable; many infants with larger deletions have high early mortality from malformations and infection, while those surviving infancy live into adulthood with severe intellectual disability. Seizure control and nutritional support strongly influence outcome.","pitfalls":["Relying on a normal karyotype when microarray is needed to detect small deletions","Neglecting parental karyotyping and misjudging recurrence risk with a translocation","Under-treating a seizure disorder that is central to morbidity","Attributing poor growth to behaviour rather than oromotor dysfunction, delaying tube feeding"],"management":["No treatment corrects the 4p16.3 deletion, so care targets the seizures, feeding failure and developmental needs","Antiepileptic drug therapy for the core seizure disorder, which often begins in infancy and may be fever-provoked, with a rescue plan for prolonged seizures and status epilepticus","Nutritional support for the profound feeding difficulty and failure to thrive, including thickened feeds and gastrostomy where needed","Developmental support with physiotherapy, occupational and speech therapy for the hypotonia, severe intellectual disability and limited speech","Surveillance and management of congenital heart disease and renal tract anomalies","Multidisciplinary follow-up across neurology, cardiology, dietetics and developmental paediatrics","Parental karyotyping and genetic counselling, as roughly a tenth of cases arise from an unbalanced parental translocation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","GeneReviews: Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"wolff_parkinson_white":{"name":"Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WPW","WPW syndrome","ventricular pre-excitation","accessory pathway syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["WPW describes ventricular pre-excitation from an accessory pathway together with symptomatic tachyarrhythmia; the resting ECG classically shows a short PR interval, a delta wave, and a widened QRS","The most common arrhythmia is orthodromic AV reentrant tachycardia, a regular narrow-complex tachycardia using the AV node antegrade and the accessory pathway retrograde","The dangerous arrhythmia is atrial fibrillation conducted rapidly down the accessory pathway, producing an irregular, broad, bizarre, and very fast ventricular response that can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation","In pre-excited atrial fibrillation, AV nodal blocking drugs are contraindicated because they can accelerate conduction over the accessory pathway","An unstable patient with a pre-excited tachyarrhythmia should undergo urgent synchronised cardioversion","The term WPW pattern refers to asymptomatic pre-excitation on ECG, whereas WPW syndrome requires accompanying arrhythmia","Catheter ablation of the accessory pathway is highly effective and is often curative, and is preferred in symptomatic patients","Risk stratification assesses how rapidly the pathway can conduct, since a short refractory period predicts higher risk during atrial fibrillation"],"pathophysiology":"In WPW an accessory atrioventricular pathway bypasses the normal AV nodal delay, allowing early activation of ventricular myocardium and producing the delta wave and short PR interval. This creates the substrate for reentry: in orthodromic AVRT the impulse travels down the AV node and back up the accessory pathway, while in the less common antidromic form the direction is reversed, giving a wide-complex tachycardia. The gravest scenario occurs when atrial fibrillation develops and the accessory pathway, lacking the rate-limiting properties of the AV node, conducts rapid atrial impulses to the ventricles, risking degeneration to ventricular fibrillation and sudden death.","additionalDifferentials":["AV nodal reentrant tachycardia without an accessory pathway","Atrial fibrillation or flutter with aberrant conduction","Ventricular tachycardia","Other causes of a short PR interval","Bundle branch block mimicking a broad QRS"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG to identify pre-excitation and characterise arrhythmia","Ambulatory monitoring to capture paroxysmal tachyarrhythmias","Exercise testing to assess for abrupt loss of pre-excitation as a marker of a longer pathway refractory period","Electrophysiology study to localise the pathway and measure its conduction properties","Echocardiography to assess for associated structural disease such as Ebstein anomaly"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: pre-excited atrial fibrillation with a rapid, irregular, broad-complex response that can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation","Haemodynamic instability during any pre-excited tachyarrhythmia requiring urgent synchronised cardioversion","Syncope or aborted sudden cardiac death indicating a high-risk pathway","Use of AV nodal blocking agents in pre-excited atrial fibrillation, which can precipitate deterioration"],"prognosis":"Most patients have an excellent prognosis, and catheter ablation is curative in the great majority; the principal risk is sudden death from rapidly conducted atrial fibrillation in the minority with a short-refractory-period pathway, which risk stratification and ablation substantially reduce.","pitfalls":["Administering AV nodal blocking drugs in pre-excited atrial fibrillation","Assuming an asymptomatic WPW pattern requires no assessment when risk stratification may be warranted","Mistaking pre-excited atrial fibrillation for ventricular tachycardia or ordinary atrial fibrillation","Delaying cardioversion in an unstable pre-excited tachyarrhythmia"],"management":["Catheter ablation of the accessory pathway is the definitive, curative treatment and is offered to symptomatic patients","Acute orthodromic AV reentrant tachycardia terminated with vagal manoeuvres followed by AV-nodal blockade such as adenosine","In pre-excited atrial fibrillation, AV-nodal blocking drugs are avoided and rhythm controlled with procainamide or flecainide, or urgent cardioversion","Immediate synchronised DC cardioversion for any haemodynamically unstable pre-excited tachyarrhythmia","Risk stratification of asymptomatic pre-excitation using exercise testing for abrupt loss of pre-excitation and, where indicated, an electrophysiology study","Antiarrhythmic drugs such as flecainide or propafenone as an alternative when ablation is deferred or declined","Cardiology and electrophysiology referral, with echocardiography to screen for associated Ebstein anomaly"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"wolff_parkinson_white_syndrome":{"name":"wolff parkinson white syndrome","system":"cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WPW syndrome","ventricular pre-excitation syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Braunwald's Heart Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["WPW syndrome is the combination of ventricular pre-excitation on the resting ECG and symptomatic tachyarrhythmia; pre-excitation without symptoms is termed a WPW pattern.","The classic triad is a short PR interval, a slurred delta wave and a widened QRS, reflecting fusion of accessory-pathway and nodal ventricular activation.","The delta-wave vector and precordial transition allow bedside prediction of pathway location, guiding ablation.","Orthodromic AVRT is the commonest tachycardia, but pre-excited atrial fibrillation is the most dangerous because it can conduct rapidly and degenerate to ventricular fibrillation.","An irregular, broad, rapid tachycardia with varying QRS morphology should raise immediate suspicion of pre-excited atrial fibrillation.","AV-nodal blocking drugs including adenosine, verapamil, diltiazem and digoxin are contraindicated in pre-excited atrial fibrillation as they can accelerate pathway conduction.","Abrupt, complete loss of pre-excitation during exercise or with procainamide suggests a pathway with a long refractory period and lower risk.","Ebstein anomaly is associated with WPW and frequently with multiple right-sided accessory pathways."],"pathophysiology":"The syndrome results from a congenital accessory atrioventricular pathway that bypasses the physiological delay of the atrioventricular node, allowing early activation of ventricular myocardium (pre-excitation). Because the pathway conducts faster than the node, part of the ventricle depolarises early, shortening the PR interval and inscribing a delta wave, while the remainder is activated normally, producing a fusion QRS. The same pathway provides a limb for macroreentrant tachycardia. When atrial fibrillation develops, an accessory pathway with a short antegrade refractory period can transmit disorganised atrial impulses to the ventricle at extremely high rates, and this rapid irregular ventricular response may deteriorate into ventricular fibrillation and sudden death.","additionalDifferentials":["atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia","concealed accessory pathway atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia","bundle branch block mimicking a wide QRS","ventricular tachycardia","atrial fibrillation without pre-excitation","fasciculoventricular and other variant pathways"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["twelve-lead ECG to characterise the delta wave and localise the pathway","exercise stress testing to assess abrupt loss of pre-excitation","ambulatory monitoring to document tachyarrhythmias","transthoracic echocardiography to exclude Ebstein anomaly and cardiomyopathy","electrophysiological study with measurement of the shortest pre-excited RR interval in atrial fibrillation","procainamide or other pharmacological challenge to gauge pathway refractoriness"],"redFlags":["pre-excited atrial fibrillation with a very short shortest pre-excited RR interval","syncope, particularly during exertion or palpitations","resuscitated cardiac arrest or documented ventricular fibrillation","family history of sudden death with pre-excitation","haemodynamic instability during any wide-complex tachycardia"],"prognosis":"Most patients have an excellent prognosis and catheter ablation is curative with a high success rate, but a small subset with rapidly conducting pathways are at risk of sudden cardiac death, which may occasionally be the first manifestation. Risk stratification of asymptomatic pre-excitation is therefore central to management.","pitfalls":["Treating pre-excited atrial fibrillation with AV-nodal blockers and precipitating ventricular fibrillation.","Dismissing asymptomatic pre-excitation without considering risk stratification in higher-risk occupations or athletes.","Confusing a fusion or pre-excited complex with myocardial infarction because of pseudo-Q waves and repolarisation change.","Assuming a single pathway when multiple pathways, especially in Ebstein anomaly, are present.","Interpreting intermittent pre-excitation as invariably low-risk without confirming it is abrupt and rate-related."],"management":["Radiofrequency catheter ablation of the accessory pathway is the curative mainstay for symptomatic pre-excitation and recurrent tachyarrhythmia","Acute orthodromic AVRT managed with vagal manoeuvres then adenosine or another short-acting AV-nodal blocker","Pre-excited atrial fibrillation treated with procainamide, flecainide or synchronised cardioversion while strictly avoiding adenosine, verapamil, diltiazem and digoxin, which can accelerate pathway conduction","Urgent synchronised electrical cardioversion for the haemodynamically unstable pre-excited patient","Risk stratification of asymptomatic pre-excitation by exercise testing and, in higher-risk occupations or findings, an electrophysiology study measuring the shortest pre-excited RR interval","Long-term suppressive antiarrhythmic therapy such as flecainide or propafenone where ablation is not undertaken","Cardiology and electrophysiology referral with transthoracic echocardiography to exclude Ebstein anomaly and cardiomyopathy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine","ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with supraventricular tachycardia","Zipes Cardiac Electrophysiology: From Cell to Bedside"],"crossLinks":[]},"wolfram_syndrome":{"name":"Wolfram syndrome","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DIDMOAD","WFS1-related Wolfram syndrome","diabetes insipidus, diabetes mellitus, optic atrophy and deafness","wolframin deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Usually autosomal recessive neurodegenerative and endocrine disorder caused by WFS1 mutations encoding wolframin, an endoplasmic reticulum membrane protein; a rarer CISD2-related form exists","The acronym DIDMOAD summarises the core features: Diabetes Insipidus, Diabetes Mellitus, Optic Atrophy, and Deafness","Childhood-onset non-autoimmune, insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus is typically the first feature, followed by progressive bilateral optic atrophy causing visual loss","The diabetes mellitus is islet-cell-antibody negative and not HLA-associated, distinguishing it from type 1 diabetes, and coexisting optic atrophy should prompt consideration of Wolfram syndrome","Cranial diabetes insipidus, sensorineural deafness, neurodegeneration (brainstem and cerebellar signs, neurogenic bladder), and psychiatric disturbance develop over time","Diagnosis is clinical supported by WFS1 sequencing; management is supportive and multidisciplinary, coordinating diabetes, endocrine, ophthalmology, audiology, urology, and neurology care"],"pathophysiology":"Wolfram syndrome is most often caused by biallelic WFS1 mutations encoding wolframin, an endoplasmic reticulum transmembrane protein important for calcium homeostasis and the endoplasmic reticulum stress response. Loss of wolframin function heightens endoplasmic reticulum stress and triggers apoptosis in highly secretory and neuronal cells, notably pancreatic beta cells, retinal ganglion cells and the optic nerve, the hypothalamic-pituitary vasopressin system, the cochlea, and diffuse brain regions. This progressive cellular loss produces non-autoimmune insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus, optic atrophy, cranial diabetes insipidus, sensorineural deafness, and wider neurodegeneration.","additionalDifferentials":["Type 1 (autoimmune) diabetes mellitus","Isolated hereditary optic atrophy (e.g. dominant optic atrophy, Leber hereditary optic neuropathy)","Mitochondrial disease with diabetes and deafness (e.g. maternally inherited diabetes and deafness)","Other causes of cranial diabetes insipidus","Thiamine-responsive megaloblastic anaemia syndrome (SLC19A2) with diabetes and deafness","Bardet-Biedl and other syndromic causes of visual and endocrine disturbance"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["WFS1 (and CISD2) gene sequencing to confirm the diagnosis","Diabetes work-up including islet autoantibodies (negative) and C-peptide","Water deprivation/desmopressin testing and paired plasma/urine osmolality for diabetes insipidus","Formal ophthalmological assessment with optical coherence tomography and visual fields for optic atrophy","Audiometry for sensorineural hearing loss","Brain MRI to assess brainstem, cerebellar, and posterior pituitary changes; urodynamics for neurogenic bladder"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual loss from optic atrophy threatening independence","Unrecognised cranial diabetes insipidus causing dehydration and electrolyte disturbance","Brainstem neurodegeneration with central apnoea or swallowing difficulty","Severe psychiatric disturbance or suicidality requiring urgent mental-health support"],"prognosis":"Wolfram syndrome is progressive with accumulating disability; visual and hearing loss, neurological decline, and endocrine complications develop over years, and premature death, often from brainstem involvement or its complications, is typical. Coordinated multidisciplinary care can preserve function and quality of life.","pitfalls":["Labelling the childhood diabetes as type 1 and missing the syndromic diagnosis when optic atrophy appears","Attributing polyuria solely to poor glycaemic control and overlooking coexisting cranial diabetes insipidus","Neglecting audiological, urological, and psychiatric surveillance in a multisystem disorder","Failing to offer genetic counselling given the mainly autosomal recessive inheritance"],"management":["No disease-modifying therapy exists, so care is supportive and directed at the DIDMOAD components","Insulin replacement for the non-autoimmune, insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus that is usually the first feature","Desmopressin replacement for cranial diabetes insipidus","Low-vision support and ophthalmology follow-up for progressive optic atrophy, and hearing aids or cochlear implantation for sensorineural deafness","Management of neurogenic bladder with intermittent catheterisation and anticholinergic agents, alongside neurological and psychiatric symptomatic care","Coordinated multidisciplinary surveillance spanning endocrinology, ophthalmology, audiology, urology, neurology and psychiatry given the progressive course","Genetic counselling for this usually autosomal recessive WFS1-related disorder, with family testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"wolman_disease":{"name":"Wolman disease","system":"Genetic / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lysosomal acid lipase deficiency","LAL deficiency","acid cholesteryl ester hydrolase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics / standard metabolic references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder caused by deficiency of the enzyme lysosomal acid lipase, leading to accumulation of cholesteryl esters and triglycerides within lysosomes","Wolman disease is the severe infantile end of the spectrum, presenting in the first weeks of life with vomiting, diarrhoea, and failure to thrive","Marked hepatosplenomegaly with progressive liver dysfunction is a prominent feature","Bilateral adrenal enlargement with calcification is a characteristic and helpful imaging clue","Malabsorption and steatorrhoea contribute to profound failure to thrive and early death if untreated","The milder, later-onset variant caused by the same enzyme deficiency is cholesteryl ester storage disease, presenting with hepatomegaly and dyslipidaemia","A dyslipidaemic profile with elevated LDL cholesterol and low HDL is typical, reflecting impaired lipid handling","Enzyme replacement therapy is available for lysosomal acid lipase deficiency, and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation has been used in selected cases, alongside nutritional support"],"pathophysiology":"Wolman disease is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in LIPA, encoding lysosomal acid lipase, the enzyme that hydrolyses cholesteryl esters and triglycerides delivered to the lysosome. Deficient activity causes these lipids to accumulate within lysosomes of the liver, spleen, intestine, adrenal glands, and macrophages throughout the body. Massive lipid storage produces hepatosplenomegaly, hepatic dysfunction, adrenal infiltration with calcification, and intestinal involvement causing malabsorption. Disrupted intracellular cholesterol sensing also drives a proatherogenic dyslipidaemia. The severe infantile phenotype reflects near-absent enzyme activity, whereas residual activity underlies the milder cholesteryl ester storage disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Other lysosomal storage disorders with hepatosplenomegaly such as Niemann-Pick disease","Other causes of neonatal failure to thrive with malabsorption","Infantile cholestatic and metabolic liver disease","Familial hyperlipidaemias for the milder phenotype","Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease in later-onset presentations"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assay of lysosomal acid lipase enzyme activity, including dried blood spot testing","LIPA molecular genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis","Abdominal imaging demonstrating hepatosplenomegaly and adrenal calcification","Liver function tests and fasting lipid profile","Assessment of nutrition, growth, and malabsorption"],"redFlags":["Profound failure to thrive with vomiting and diarrhoea in early infancy","Progressive liver dysfunction or hepatic failure","Adrenal insufficiency associated with adrenal involvement","Severe malabsorption with rapid clinical deterioration"],"prognosis":"Untreated infantile Wolman disease is rapidly progressive and usually fatal within the first months of life. Enzyme replacement therapy has substantially improved survival in this severe form, and the later-onset cholesteryl ester storage disease has a more chronic course dominated by liver disease and dyslipidaemia with associated cardiovascular risk.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the diagnosis in a neonate with failure to thrive and hepatosplenomegaly","Missing adrenal calcification on imaging as a diagnostic pointer","Failing to recognise the milder cholesteryl ester storage disease as the same enzyme deficiency","Delaying enzyme replacement therapy, which is most beneficial when started early"],"management":["Enzyme replacement therapy with recombinant lysosomal acid lipase (sebelipase alfa) is the disease-specific treatment and has transformed survival in the severe infantile form","Nutritional support with a modified low-fat regimen and parenteral nutrition to counter malabsorption, steatorrhoea and profound failure to thrive","Lipid-lowering therapy such as a statin with ezetimibe for the proatherogenic dyslipidaemia, particularly in the milder cholesteryl ester storage disease spectrum","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation considered in selected cases as an alternative disease-directed approach","Monitoring of liver function, growth, nutritional status and adrenal function given adrenal infiltration and calcification","Multidisciplinary metabolic care with escalation for hepatic failure","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive LIPA disorder, with family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"woolly_hair":{"name":"Woolly hair","system":"Dermatology / Trichology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["woolly hair syndrome","woolly hair naevus","kinky hair"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital abnormality in which scalp hair is tightly curled, fine and often lighter in colour in individuals not of African descent, present from infancy","It occurs as a diffuse hereditary form with autosomal dominant or recessive inheritance, or as a localised woolly hair naevus consisting of a well-circumscribed patch of abnormal hair","The recessive forms carry important cardiocutaneous associations, namely Naxos disease (woolly hair, palmoplantar keratoderma and arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy) and Carvajal syndrome (woolly hair, palmoplantar keratoderma and a dilated, left-dominant cardiomyopathy)","These desmosomal-gene disorders carry a risk of life-threatening arrhythmia and sudden cardiac death, so woolly hair with palmoplantar keratoderma should prompt cardiac evaluation","The hair is frequently sparse and slow-growing and may show associated shaft abnormalities such as pili torti or trichorrhexis nodosa","Diagnosis is clinical with microscopy of the shaft, and the key step is screening for associated keratoderma and cardiomyopathy in suspected syndromic cases","Management is supportive for the hair itself, with the priority being cardiac surveillance and genetic counselling in syndromic forms"],"pathophysiology":"Woolly hair reflects an abnormal, tightly coiled hair shaft with an altered elliptical cross-section and often reduced diameter, present from birth in non-African scalp hair. Hereditary diffuse forms and the localised naevus arise from defects in hair-shaft formation. The clinically critical recessive syndromes, Naxos disease and Carvajal syndrome, result from mutations in desmosomal genes encoding plakoglobin and desmoplakin, which impair cell-to-cell adhesion in the epidermis, hair and myocardium and thereby explain the triad of woolly hair, palmoplantar keratoderma and cardiomyopathy with arrhythmogenic risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Normal tightly curled or afro-textured hair","Pili torti, with a twisted shaft","Uncombable hair syndrome (spun-glass hair)","Trichorrhexis nodosa and other fragility disorders","Acquired progressive kinking of the hair"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Light microscopy of the shaft to characterise the curl and detect associated defects","Examination of the palms and soles for palmoplantar keratoderma","Electrocardiography and echocardiography where a cardiocutaneous syndrome is suspected","Genetic testing for desmosomal-gene mutations such as JUP and DSP, with family screening","Cardiology referral and a family history of sudden cardiac death"],"redFlags":["Woolly hair with palmoplantar keratoderma, mandating cardiac assessment for Naxos or Carvajal syndrome","Palpitations, syncope or a family history of sudden cardiac death","Any features of arrhythmogenic or dilated cardiomyopathy"],"prognosis":"Isolated woolly hair and the woolly hair naevus are benign and tend to improve in later childhood or adult life, but the outlook in Naxos and Carvajal syndromes is dominated by the cardiomyopathy and its arrhythmic risk, making early cardiac diagnosis potentially life-saving.","pitfalls":["Regarding woolly hair as a purely cosmetic issue and missing an associated life-threatening cardiomyopathy","Failing to examine the palms and soles for keratoderma","Not taking a family history of sudden cardiac death","Overlooking associated hair-shaft defects on microscopy"],"management":["Screen for the cardiocutaneous syndromes by examining palms and soles for palmoplantar keratoderma and arranging cardiac assessment when present","Cardiac management of associated cardiomyopathy in Naxos and Carvajal syndromes, including heart failure therapy and an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator for arrhythmogenic and sudden-death risk","Emollients and keratolytics for palmoplantar keratoderma","Gentle hair care, since isolated woolly hair and the woolly hair naevus are benign and need no specific hair treatment, with reassurance that they often improve with age","Genetic testing for desmosomal genes such as JUP and DSP with cascade cardiac and genetic screening of relatives","Cardiology referral and documentation of any family history of sudden cardiac death","Urgent escalation for palpitations, syncope or documented arrhythmia in syndromic cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Bolognia Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"writer_cramp":{"name":"Writer's cramp","system":"Nervous system / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["writer's cramp","graphospasm","writing dystonia","mogigraphia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The prototypical task-specific focal hand dystonia, provoked specifically by writing","Abnormal posturing or cramping of the hand and forearm develops shortly after beginning to write and impairs legibility","The same hand performs other manual tasks normally, at least initially, which is the hallmark of task specificity","Classified as simple when confined to writing and dystonic (complex) when other manual tasks become affected","Excessive gripping of the pen with abnormal wrist or finger postures is characteristic","It typically begins in adulthood and may be more frequent in those who write extensively","Some patients adapt by writing with the other hand, which can occasionally develop dystonia in turn"],"pathophysiology":"Writer's cramp reflects maladaptive sensorimotor plasticity and deficient inhibition within the motor system, in which repetitive, highly practised writing drives abnormal cortical reorganisation and blurring of the cortical representation of the fingers. Impaired integration of sensory feedback with motor commands leads to excessive co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles during the specific act of writing, producing the abnormal posture. The task specificity reflects the focal, activity-dependent nature of this reorganisation.","additionalDifferentials":["Other task-specific dystonias such as musician's dystonia","Overuse injury, tendinopathy or repetitive strain","Peripheral nerve entrapment such as median or ulnar neuropathy","Essential tremor affecting handwriting","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder","Early generalising or secondary dystonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Observation of the patient writing to characterise the posture and confirm task specificity","Surface electromyography demonstrating co-contraction during writing","Nerve conduction studies to exclude entrapment neuropathy","MRI where a secondary or structural cause is suspected","Copper studies and genetic testing in younger patients or those with additional features","Assessment over time for spread of the dystonia to other tasks"],"redFlags":["Posturing at rest or during many tasks, suggesting generalisation or a secondary cause","Sensory loss, weakness or wasting indicating a neuropathic or compressive lesion","Young onset with other neurological features suggesting an inherited dystonia","Features of Wilson's disease in a younger patient"],"prognosis":"Writer's cramp is usually chronic and spontaneous remission is uncommon; however, it is not dangerous and function can generally be maintained. Botulinum toxin injection tailored to the overactive muscles is the most effective treatment, with adjuncts including writing aids, technique modification and sometimes switching hands. Some patients manage well with practical adaptations alone.","pitfalls":["Labelling the problem as psychological or as simple writer's fatigue","Not watching the patient write, which is essential to the diagnosis","Overlooking entrapment neuropathy or a musculoskeletal cause","Assuming the dystonia will remain confined to writing"],"management":["Botulinum toxin injection tailored to the overactive forearm and hand muscles is the most effective treatment for this task-specific focal dystonia","Adaptive strategies including writing aids, a modified or broadened pen grip, altered posture and switching to typing or voice dictation","Motor retraining, physiotherapy and use of sensory tricks to reduce the abnormal co-contraction","Oral agents such as anticholinergics as adjuncts, recognising their limited and variable benefit","Attention to writing ergonomics and reduction of provoking task load","Neurology referral, with exclusion of a secondary cause including Wilson's disease through copper studies in younger patients or those with additional neurological features"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice, current edition","Fahn and Jankovic, Principles and Practice of Movement Disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"writers_cramp":{"name":"Writer's cramp","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["writing dystonia","task-specific focal hand dystonia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice / movement disorder literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A task-specific focal dystonia in which abnormal hand and forearm posturing appears specifically during writing","In the simple form the dystonia occurs only with writing, whereas in the dystonic form it spreads to other manual tasks","Patients describe excessive gripping, finger extension or flexion and wrist deviation that impair legibility","It usually begins in adulthood and is not explained by weakness or sensory loss","Sensory tricks and changes in writing implement or grip may give partial relief","Botulinum toxin to selected muscles, retraining and adaptive strategies are the main treatments"],"pathophysiology":"Writer's cramp is a task-specific dystonia thought to arise from maladaptive plasticity and impaired sensorimotor integration within basal ganglia and cortical motor networks controlling skilled hand movements. Reduced inhibition and abnormal cortical representation of the hand lead to excessive and poorly focused muscle activation during the highly overlearned task of writing. The task specificity reflects the abnormal motor programme being tied to a particular practised movement.","additionalDifferentials":["Musician's and other task-specific dystonias","Generalised or segmental dystonia with hand involvement","Essential tremor affecting writing","Functional (psychogenic) hand movements","Overuse or repetitive strain injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis from the task-specific pattern","Examination to exclude weakness, sensory loss or other neurological signs","Assessment of whether the dystonia is simple or has spread to other tasks","MRI of the brain only if features suggest a secondary cause","Occupational assessment of writing technique and ergonomics"],"redFlags":["Spread to rest or to many tasks suggesting evolving segmental dystonia","Associated weakness, wasting or sensory signs","Rapid progression or other neurological features","Onset in childhood suggesting a genetic generalised dystonia"],"prognosis":"The condition is generally chronic and non-life-threatening; symptoms often stabilise, and botulinum toxin, retraining and adaptive techniques can meaningfully improve function.","pitfalls":["Dismissing it as anxiety or overuse rather than recognising a focal dystonia","Missing spread to other tasks that changes management","Overlooking a secondary cause when there are additional signs","Neglecting ergonomic and retraining approaches alongside injections"],"management":["Botulinum toxin injection into selected overactive muscles is the principal and most effective treatment for this task-specific focal hand dystonia","Adaptive techniques such as changing the writing implement or grip, altering posture and switching to typing or dictation","Motor retraining and physiotherapy, with sensory tricks used to obtain partial relief","Oral pharmacological adjuncts such as anticholinergics where injections are insufficient, acknowledging modest benefit","Occupational assessment of writing technique and ergonomics to reduce provoking activity","Neurology referral, reserving brain imaging and secondary-cause work-up for atypical or young-onset presentations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bradley and Daroff Neurology in Clinical Practice","Movement Disorders textbook (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]},"wwox_encephalopathy":{"name":"WWOX-Related Epileptic Encephalopathy","system":"Neurology / Developmental epileptic encephalopathy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WOREE syndrome","WWOX developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","DEE28"],"chapter":null,"source":"Neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["WWOX encodes a WW-domain-containing oxidoreductase involved in cellular metabolism, apoptosis and neural development; the disorder is autosomal recessive.","The severe end (WOREE syndrome / DEE28) presents with drug-resistant seizures beginning in the first weeks to months of life.","Profound developmental impairment with little or no milestone acquisition and absent visual fixation is typical.","Progressive features include acquired microcephaly, spasticity, dystonia and retinopathy or optic atrophy.","A milder allelic phenotype (SCAR12) causes autosomal recessive spinocerebellar ataxia with epilepsy and intellectual disability.","Genotype strongly influences severity: complete loss of function tends to cause the lethal encephalopathy, whereas hypomorphic variants give milder disease.","Neuroimaging may show a thin corpus callosum, cerebral atrophy and delayed or abnormal myelination.","Seizures are typically multifocal and highly refractory, and premature death in infancy or early childhood is common in the severe form."],"pathophysiology":"WWOX on chromosome 16q23 encodes WW domain-containing oxidoreductase, a protein with two WW domains and a short-chain dehydrogenase/reductase domain that participates in apoptosis, steroid and lipid metabolism, DNA-damage responses and, critically for the nervous system, neuronal differentiation and central myelination. Biallelic loss-of-function or hypomorphic missense variants abolish or reduce WWOX activity, and the genotype-phenotype correlation is strong: null combinations produce the lethal WWOX-related epileptic encephalopathy (WOREE syndrome), while partially functional combinations produce the milder SCAR12 ataxia-epilepsy phenotype. Loss of WWOX disrupts neural metabolic and developmental pathways, resulting in an early developmental and epileptic encephalopathy with progressive atrophy, impaired myelination and refractory seizures.","additionalDifferentials":["Other early-infantile developmental and epileptic encephalopathies (STXBP1, KCNQ2, CDKL5, SCN2A)","Ohtahara syndrome or early myoclonic encephalopathy of other cause","Neonatal metabolic epilepsies (pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy, non-ketotic hyperglycinaemia)","Peroxisomal and mitochondrial encephalopathies","Congenital cytomegalovirus or other congenital infection","Other autosomal recessive spinocerebellar ataxias (for the milder phenotype)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Exome or genome sequencing or a developmental and epileptic encephalopathy gene panel (biallelic WWOX)","EEG (often burst-suppression or multifocal epileptiform activity in the severe form)","MRI brain (atrophy, thin corpus callosum, delayed myelination)","Metabolic screen to exclude treatable mimics (qualitative)","Ophthalmological assessment for retinopathy or optic atrophy","Parental carrier testing and segregation analysis"],"redFlags":["Refractory neonatal or early-infantile seizures with burst-suppression on EEG","Developmental arrest with no visual fixation","Progressive microcephaly with evolving spasticity","Clinical deterioration or apnoeas signalling a life-threatening course"],"prognosis":"The severe WOREE form carries a poor prognosis, with profound disability, refractory epilepsy and frequent death in infancy or early childhood; the milder SCAR12 phenotype is compatible with longer survival but causes progressive ataxia, epilepsy and intellectual disability.","pitfalls":["Overlooking a recessive diagnosis in a singleton, particularly with parental consanguinity","Missing the milder allelic ataxia phenotype at the other end of the spectrum","Assuming a structural or acquired cause from imaging atrophy without genetic testing","Failing to offer carrier testing and genetic counselling to the parents"],"management":["No treatment corrects the underlying WWOX deficiency, so care centres on seizure control and supportive management of the developmental and epileptic encephalopathy","Antiseizure medication for the drug-resistant seizures, typically requiring trials of multiple agents and combinations, with a rescue protocol for prolonged seizures and status epilepticus","Management of spasticity and dystonia with physiotherapy and tone-modifying agents, and prevention of contractures","Nutritional and feeding support including gastrostomy, alongside developmental and palliative input given the profound impairment in the severe WOREE form","Ophthalmological assessment and support for retinopathy or optic atrophy","Multidisciplinary paediatric neurology-led care with early discussion of goals of care and prognosis in the lethal phenotype","Genetic counselling for this autosomal recessive disorder with parental carrier testing and segregation analysis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Neurogenetics and epilepsy reference texts","Paediatric neurology reference texts","Developmental and epileptic encephalopathy review literature"],"crossLinks":[]},"wyburn_mason_syndrome":{"name":"Wyburn-Mason syndrome","system":"Dermatology / Neurocutaneous","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Bonnet-Dechaume-Blanc syndrome","retinocephalic vascular malformation syndrome","congenital unilateral retinocephalic arteriovenous malformation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology / Neurocutaneous Disorders (current reference texts)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by unilateral arteriovenous malformations affecting the retina, optic pathway and midbrain, typically along a single embryonic axis","It is a sporadic, non-hereditary condition regarded as a somatic mosaic vascular malformation syndrome","Cutaneous vascular naevi may occur on the same side of the face, overlying the deeper malformations","The retinal arteriovenous malformation appears as dilated, tortuous vessels (racemose angioma) and can threaten vision","Intracranial arteriovenous malformations may present with headache, seizures, haemorrhage or neurological deficit","The size of the retinal lesion broadly correlates with the likelihood of intracranial involvement, so ocular findings prompt neuroimaging","Management is generic and specialist-led: neurosurgical or endovascular assessment of intracranial lesions and ophthalmological monitoring of the retinal malformation"],"pathophysiology":"Wyburn-Mason syndrome is a sporadic vascular malformation disorder thought to arise from a post-zygotic somatic event affecting mesodermal vascular precursors along a single developmental axis, producing arteriovenous malformations that link the retina, optic pathway and midbrain on one side. These high-flow lesions consist of direct arterial-to-venous connections without an intervening capillary bed, leading to dilated tortuous vessels. In the retina this produces a racemose angioma that may compromise vision, while intracranial arteriovenous malformations carry a risk of haemorrhage, seizures and progressive neurological deficit. Overlying facial cutaneous vascular naevi reflect involvement of the same regional vascular field.","additionalDifferentials":["Sturge-Weber syndrome","Isolated retinal arteriovenous malformation (racemose angioma)","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia","Isolated intracranial arteriovenous malformation","Retinal capillary haemangioblastoma of von Hippel-Lindau disease","Cavernous malformation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination and retinal imaging to characterise the arteriovenous malformation","MRI of the brain to detect intracranial arteriovenous malformations","MRA or catheter angiography to define the vascular anatomy","Ophthalmological assessment of visual acuity and fields","Neurological examination for cranial nerve and midbrain involvement","Fluorescein angiography of the retina where indicated"],"redFlags":["Signs of intracranial haemorrhage such as sudden severe headache or acute neurological deficit","New seizures or progressive neurological signs","Acute visual loss","Symptoms suggesting raised intracranial pressure"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the extent and location of the arteriovenous malformations; small stable lesions may remain asymptomatic, but large intracranial malformations carry a risk of haemorrhage and neurological morbidity, and extensive retinal involvement can cause permanent visual loss.","pitfalls":["Treating a retinal arteriovenous malformation as isolated without neuroimaging for intracranial lesions","Overlooking a subtle facial vascular naevus that signals deeper malformation","Attempting intervention on high-flow lesions without full angiographic assessment","Confusing the retinal lesion with von Hippel-Lindau haemangioblastoma"],"management":["Multidisciplinary management of the high-flow arteriovenous malformations, with observation of small, stable asymptomatic lesions","Treatment of symptomatic or ruptured intracranial arteriovenous malformations by neurosurgical resection, endovascular embolisation or stereotactic radiosurgery","Ophthalmology monitoring of the retinal racemose angioma and visual function, with intervention where vision is threatened","Neuroimaging surveillance to track intracranial lesions, given that larger retinal malformations predict intracranial involvement","Antiepileptic drug therapy where the intracranial malformation causes seizures","Urgent escalation to neurosurgery for intracranial haemorrhage or a new neurological deficit","Reassurance that the condition is sporadic and mosaic rather than heritable, so routine family genetic screening is not required"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_adrenal_hypoplasia_congenita":{"name":"X-linked adrenal hypoplasia congenita (NR0B1/DAX1)","system":"Endocrine / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AHC","X-linked AHC","DAX1 deficiency","NR0B1-related adrenal hypoplasia","adrenal hypoplasia congenita with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology / Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked disorder of adrenal development caused by mutations in NR0B1, which encodes the nuclear receptor DAX1","Typically affects males and presents with primary adrenal insufficiency, often with a salt-wasting crisis in infancy or later childhood","Presentation includes hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia and hyperpigmentation from combined glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid deficiency","Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism characteristically emerges later and manifests as absent or arrested puberty","The combination of primary adrenal failure and later gonadotropin deficiency is a strong diagnostic clue","Contiguous gene deletions at the locus can co-occur with Duchenne muscular dystrophy and glycerol kinase deficiency","Impaired spermatogenesis frequently persists, so fertility is often affected despite hormone replacement"],"pathophysiology":"DAX1, the product of NR0B1, is a nuclear receptor essential for the development and function of the adrenal cortex and for the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis. Loss-of-function mutations lead to failure of normal adrenocortical development, producing primary adrenal insufficiency with deficiency of both glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids. The same gene is required for normal gonadotropin regulation and gonadal function, so affected males also develop hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, typically becoming apparent around the age of expected puberty. The X-linked inheritance explains the predominance of affected males.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Autoimmune primary adrenal insufficiency","Adrenoleukodystrophy","Familial glucocorticoid deficiency","Other causes of hypogonadotropic hypogonadism such as Kallmann syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum electrolytes, glucose and paired cortisol with ACTH","Plasma renin and aldosterone to assess mineralocorticoid status","ACTH stimulation testing to confirm adrenal insufficiency","Gonadotropins and sex steroids, especially around the age of expected puberty","Molecular genetic testing of NR0B1 including assessment for contiguous gene deletions"],"redFlags":["Adrenal (salt-wasting) crisis with hypotension, hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia requiring emergency treatment","Hypoglycaemia and collapse in an infant or child from glucocorticoid deficiency","Absent or arrested puberty indicating evolving hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Associated neuromuscular features suggesting a contiguous gene deletion syndrome"],"prognosis":"With lifelong glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement and induction of puberty when required, survival and development are generally good, although impaired fertility commonly persists and adrenal crises remain a lifelong risk if replacement lapses.","pitfalls":["Misattributing the adrenal insufficiency to congenital adrenal hyperplasia and missing the underlying diagnosis","Failing to anticipate later hypogonadotropic hypogonadism and delayed puberty","Overlooking associated neuromuscular disease from a contiguous gene deletion","Underestimating the ongoing risk of adrenal crisis during intercurrent illness"],"management":["Lifelong glucocorticoid replacement with hydrocortisone and mineralocorticoid replacement with fludrocortisone for the combined adrenal insufficiency","Salt supplementation in infancy to support mineralocorticoid deficiency","Emergency management of adrenal crisis with intravenous fluids, parenteral hydrocortisone and correction of hypoglycaemia and electrolyte disturbance, supported by sick-day dosing rules and a steroid emergency card","Induction of puberty and testosterone replacement for the hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, with assisted reproduction or gonadotropin therapy for the commonly impaired fertility","Regular monitoring of growth, electrolytes, blood pressure and pubertal progression","Molecular testing of NR0B1 including assessment for a contiguous gene deletion involving Duchenne muscular dystrophy and glycerol kinase deficiency, with genetic counselling for this X-linked condition","Endocrinology-led multidisciplinary follow-up with escalation for suspected crisis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_agammaglobulinaemia":{"name":"X-linked agammaglobulinaemia","system":"Immune system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Bruton agammaglobulinaemia","XLA","Bruton tyrosine kinase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by loss-of-function mutations in the BTK gene, arresting B-cell development at the pre-B stage so that mature B cells and immunoglobulin of all classes are profoundly deficient.","Affected boys typically present after 6 months of age once transplacental maternal IgG wanes, with recurrent sinopulmonary infections by encapsulated bacteria such as pneumococcus and Haemophilus.","Hallmark examination finding is absent or vestigial tonsils and lymph nodes because germinal centres cannot form without B cells.","Enteroviral infection is a feared complication, causing chronic meningoencephalitis and a dermatomyositis-like syndrome; live oral poliovirus vaccine is contraindicated.","Diagnosis rests on very low or absent circulating CD19+ B cells (usually under 2 percent) together with markedly reduced IgG, IgA and IgM.","Immunoglobulin replacement, given intravenously or subcutaneously, is the mainstay of therapy and dramatically reduces infection frequency.","Susceptibility to Giardia, Campylobacter, Mycoplasma and Ureaplasma is characteristic, and Pneumocystis is not typical because T-cell immunity is intact.","Bronchiectasis from recurrent or inadequately treated pulmonary infection remains the major cause of long-term morbidity."],"pathophysiology":"X-linked agammaglobulinaemia results from mutations in the gene encoding Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK), a cytoplasmic signalling molecule essential for pre-B-cell receptor signalling. Without functional BTK, developing B lymphocytes cannot progress beyond the pro-B to pre-B transition in the bone marrow, so peripheral mature B cells are virtually absent. The consequent failure to generate plasma cells leaves the patient without effective antibody of any isotype, crippling humoral defence against encapsulated pyogenic bacteria and enteroviruses, while T-cell-mediated immunity is preserved. The X-linked recessive inheritance explains near-exclusive occurrence in males.","additionalDifferentials":["Common variable immunodeficiency","Autosomal recessive agammaglobulinaemia (mu heavy chain, lambda-5, BLNK or Ig-alpha defects)","Transient hypogammaglobulinaemia of infancy","Hyper-IgM syndrome","Severe combined immunodeficiency","Good syndrome (thymoma with immunodeficiency)","Protein-losing states causing secondary hypogammaglobulinaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum immunoglobulin levels (IgG, IgA, IgM) showing panhypogammaglobulinaemia","Peripheral blood lymphocyte immunophenotyping demonstrating absent CD19+/CD20+ B cells","BTK gene sequencing and assessment of BTK protein expression in monocytes","Specific antibody responses to protein and polysaccharide vaccines (absent)","High-resolution CT of the chest to detect bronchiectasis","Sputum and stool cultures for recurrent or atypical pathogens","Carrier testing and genetic counselling for female relatives"],"redFlags":["Enteroviral central nervous system infection presenting with progressive encephalitis","Established bronchiectasis with declining pulmonary function","Overwhelming sepsis from encapsulated organisms","Chronic Giardia or Campylobacter infection with malabsorption and failure to thrive","Development of lymphoreticular or gastrointestinal malignancy"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and lifelong immunoglobulin replacement most patients reach adulthood with a good quality of life, though chronic lung disease and enteroviral infection can shorten survival. Delayed recognition and consequent bronchiectasis are the principal determinants of poor outcome.","pitfalls":["Attributing recurrent infections in an infant to normal childhood illness and delaying immunological work-up.","Measuring immunoglobulins before 6 months when maternal IgG may mask the deficiency.","Administering live vaccines, particularly oral polio, which can cause vaccine-associated disease.","Under-dosing immunoglobulin replacement so that trough levels remain inadequate to prevent sinopulmonary infection.","Overlooking chronic enteroviral or ureaplasmal infection because routine cultures are negative."],"management":["Immunoglobulin replacement given intravenously or subcutaneously is the mainstay, restoring humoral defence and markedly reducing infection frequency","Prompt, aggressive antibiotic treatment of bacterial infections, with prophylactic antibiotics considered for recurrent sinopulmonary disease","Avoidance of live vaccines, in particular oral poliovirus vaccine, because of the risk of vaccine-derived infection","Active surveillance for and management of bronchiectasis with airway clearance, interval CT and lung function monitoring","Vigilance for chronic enteroviral infection, including meningoencephalitis, given the specific vulnerability of this defect","Genetic counselling for this X-linked disorder with maternal carrier testing and family screening","Clinical immunology-led multidisciplinary follow-up to preserve lung function and detect complications early"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice, current edition","Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies, current edition","International Union of Immunological Societies phenotypic classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_agammaglobulinemia":{"name":"X-linked agammaglobulinemia","system":"Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XLA","Bruton agammaglobulinemia","Bruton tyrosine kinase deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / standard immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked agammaglobulinemia is caused by mutations in the BTK gene, which encodes Bruton tyrosine kinase, a signalling molecule essential for B-cell maturation","The block in B-cell development leads to near-absent circulating B cells and very low levels of all immunoglobulin classes","Affected boys typically become symptomatic after about 6 months of age as maternally transferred IgG wanes","Recurrent infections are dominated by encapsulated pyogenic bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae, causing sinopulmonary infections, otitis, and sometimes sepsis or meningitis","Patients are particularly vulnerable to enteroviruses, which can cause chronic meningoencephalitis, and historically to vaccine-associated poliovirus","Physical examination often reveals scanty tonsils and lymphoid tissue because germinal centres do not form","Cellular (T-cell) immunity is intact, so most viral and fungal infections are handled normally, distinguishing it from combined immunodeficiencies","The cornerstone of treatment is lifelong immunoglobulin replacement together with prompt antibiotic treatment of infections"],"pathophysiology":"X-linked agammaglobulinemia results from loss-of-function mutations in BTK, a cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase that transmits signals from the pre-B-cell and B-cell receptors. Without functional BTK, developing B cells arrest at the pre-B stage in the bone marrow and fail to mature, so mature B cells and plasma cells are largely absent from the periphery and lymphoid tissue. The consequence is a profound deficiency of all antibody classes, leaving the patient unable to opsonise and clear encapsulated bacteria and unable to neutralise certain viruses, notably enteroviruses. Because T-cell and innate immunity are preserved, the clinical picture is dominated by pyogenic bacterial and enteroviral infection rather than opportunistic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Common variable immunodeficiency","Autosomal recessive agammaglobulinemia (non-BTK forms)","Transient hypogammaglobulinemia of infancy","Hyper-IgM syndromes","Secondary hypogammaglobulinemia (protein loss, drugs, malignancy)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum immunoglobulin levels (markedly reduced IgG, IgA, and IgM)","Lymphocyte subsets showing very low or absent CD19/CD20 B cells with normal T cells","BTK gene sequencing and BTK protein expression analysis","Specific antibody responses to protein and polysaccharide antigens (poor or absent)","Imaging of chest and sinuses to assess for bronchiectasis and chronic infection","Enteroviral testing (including cerebrospinal fluid) when chronic neurological symptoms occur"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or severe sinopulmonary infection or sepsis warranting urgent immunological assessment","Chronic enteroviral meningoencephalitis presenting with progressive neurological decline","Live vaccines, especially oral polio, must be avoided because of the antibody defect","Progressive bronchiectasis from repeated or inadequately treated respiratory infection","Failure to thrive with recurrent infection in an infant boy after 6 months of age"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and consistent immunoglobulin replacement plus prompt antibiotic therapy, most patients have a good long-term outlook, though chronic lung disease from recurrent respiratory infection and rare enteroviral central nervous system infection remain important causes of morbidity.","pitfalls":["Attributing recurrent childhood infections to ordinary illness and delaying immunoglobulin measurement","Overlooking the physical clue of absent tonsils and lymph nodes","Giving live vaccines, particularly oral poliovirus vaccine","Assuming normal viral immunity fully protects, thereby missing enteroviral complications","Under-dosing or interrupting immunoglobulin replacement, allowing breakthrough infection and lung damage"],"management":["Lifelong immunoglobulin replacement, given intravenously or subcutaneously, as the mainstay to restore protective antibody","Prompt and often prolonged antibiotic courses for pyogenic bacterial infections, with a low threshold for treating sinopulmonary disease","Consideration of prophylactic antibiotics in those with frequent breakthrough infections or established bronchiectasis","Regular respiratory review with chest and sinus imaging to detect and manage bronchiectasis and chronic lung disease","Vigilance for enteroviral central nervous system infection, with cerebrospinal fluid testing when chronic neurological symptoms arise","Avoidance of live vaccines, including oral poliovirus, and encouragement of inactivated vaccination of household contacts","Genetic counselling with BTK carrier testing for female relatives","Multidisciplinary clinical immunology follow-up monitoring trough immunoglobulin levels and infection frequency"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_deafness_pou3f4":{"name":"X-linked deafness (POU3F4, DFNX2)","system":"Ear / Audiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DFNX2","DFN3","POU3F4-related deafness","X-linked mixed deafness with stapes gusher","incomplete partition type III"],"chapter":null,"source":"Cummings Otolaryngology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest X-linked form of hereditary hearing loss, caused by variants at or deletions involving POU3F4","Affected males show a progressive mixed hearing loss, with carrier females sometimes mildly and variably affected","Imaging characteristically shows incomplete partition type III with a deficient bony plate between the lateral internal auditory canal and the basal cochlea","This bony deficiency creates an abnormal communication that predisposes to a perilymph gusher if stapes surgery is attempted","The conductive component often reflects a third-window effect rather than fixation, so stapedotomy is contraindicated and typically harmful","Cochlear implantation is possible but carries a heightened risk of a cerebrospinal-fluid gusher requiring specific surgical planning","Recognising the radiological signature is essential to avoid inappropriate middle-ear surgery"],"pathophysiology":"POU3F4 encodes a transcription factor essential for normal development of the mesenchyme surrounding the inner ear. Loss of function disrupts formation of the bony architecture at the fundus of the internal auditory canal and cochlear base, producing the incomplete partition type III malformation in which the modiolus and the bony separation between the internal auditory canal and the cochlea are deficient. This defect allows abnormal communication of cerebrospinal fluid and perilymph, generating a third-window conductive component and the characteristic tendency to gush under surgical manipulation, while progressive sensorineural loss reflects associated cochlear dysfunction. As an X-linked disorder, hemizygous males are more severely and consistently affected than heterozygous females.","additionalDifferentials":["Otosclerosis with genuine stapes fixation and a Carhart notch","Enlarged vestibular aqueduct and other third-window lesions","Superior semicircular canal dehiscence","Congenital ossicular fixation or discontinuity","Other X-linked or autosomal causes of mixed hearing loss","Cochlear incomplete partition types I and II"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution temporal bone imaging to identify incomplete partition type III","Pure-tone audiometry documenting the mixed and progressive pattern","Tympanometry and acoustic reflexes to help distinguish third-window from fixation","POU3F4 sequencing and copy-number analysis for deletions","Vestibular evoked myogenic potentials or other third-window assessment when indicated","Preoperative planning imaging when cochlear implantation is considered"],"redFlags":["Planned stapes surgery in a mixed loss without imaging, risking a perilymph gusher","Progressive loss of useful hearing despite amplification","Radiological modiolar deficiency indicating gusher risk at implantation","Family history suggesting further at-risk male relatives needing counselling"],"prognosis":"Hearing loss is progressive and mixed, and while stapes surgery is contraindicated, carefully planned cochlear implantation can rehabilitate advanced sensorineural loss despite the gusher risk.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the third-window air-bone gap for otosclerosis and proceeding to stapedotomy","Operating without temporal bone imaging in a mixed hereditary loss","Underestimating cerebrospinal-fluid gusher risk during implantation","Failing to offer genetic counselling to at-risk relatives"],"management":["Hearing amplification with conventional hearing aids as first-line rehabilitation of the mixed hearing loss","Absolute avoidance of stapes surgery, which is contraindicated because of the third-window mechanism and perilymph gusher risk","Cochlear implantation for advanced sensorineural loss, planned with strategies to anticipate and control a cerebrospinal-fluid gusher","Detailed preoperative high-resolution temporal bone imaging to define the incomplete partition and guide surgical planning","Regular audiological monitoring to track the progressive component and adjust amplification","Speech and language therapy with educational audiology support, particularly for affected boys","Genetic counselling with POU3F4 sequencing and copy-number testing and assessment of carrier females"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Cummings Otolaryngology","GeneReviews: POU3F4-Related Deafness"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_hypohidrotic_ectodermal_dysplasia":{"name":"X-linked hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XLHED","Christ-Siemens-Touraine syndrome","anhidrotic ectodermal dysplasia","EDA gene mutation","ectodysplasin A deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard dermatology and clinical genetics texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the triad of reduced or absent sweating (hypohidrosis), sparse hair (hypotrichosis), and missing or malformed teeth (hypodontia with conical teeth)","X-linked, so it predominantly affects boys, while carrier females may show milder or patchy features following lines of X-inactivation","Caused by mutations in EDA, which encodes ectodysplasin A, a signalling molecule needed for development of skin appendages","Impaired sweating causes recurrent unexplained fevers and heat intolerance, which can be dangerous in infancy and may present as fever of unknown origin","Characteristic facial features include frontal bossing, a depressed nasal bridge, and periorbital wrinkling or hyperpigmentation","Dryness of skin and mucosae predisposes to eczema, respiratory and ear infections, and reduced tear and saliva production","Management is supportive, focusing on temperature regulation, skin and mucosal care, and dental rehabilitation; carrier detection and genetic counselling are important"],"pathophysiology":"X-linked hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the EDA gene, which encodes ectodysplasin A. This ligand signals through its receptor during embryonic development to direct formation of ectodermal appendages, including sweat and other exocrine glands, hair follicles, and tooth germs. Without functional signalling these structures fail to develop normally, producing absent or reduced eccrine sweat glands, sparse hair, and missing or malformed teeth. Loss of sweating removes a key route of heat dissipation, so affected individuals overheat readily and are prone to hyperthermia.","additionalDifferentials":["Hidrotic ectodermal dysplasia (Clouston syndrome)","Autosomal forms of hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia (EDAR, EDARADD)","Incontinentia pigmenti and other ectodermal disorders","Isolated hypodontia or oligodontia","Congenital insensitivity to pain with anhidrosis","Other syndromic causes of sparse hair and abnormal teeth"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["EDA genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis and identify carriers","Assessment of sweating, such as sweat pore counts or pilocarpine-based sweat testing","Dental and orthopantomogram evaluation of tooth number and morphology","Skin biopsy showing reduced or absent eccrine glands where needed","Ophthalmological and salivary assessment for dryness","Temperature monitoring and evaluation of recurrent infections"],"redFlags":["Hyperthermia or febrile seizures from impaired heat dissipation, especially in infants","Recurrent unexplained high fevers mistaken for infection","Failure to thrive with recurrent respiratory or ear infections","Heat-related collapse during hot weather or exertion"],"prognosis":"Intelligence and lifespan are usually normal with appropriate care; the main morbidity comes from heat intolerance and recurrent infections in early childhood and from the dental and cosmetic effects, which can be substantially improved with multidisciplinary and dental management.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly investigating recurrent fevers for infection while overlooking impaired sweating","Missing subtle or patchy features in carrier females","Underestimating the risk of dangerous overheating in infancy","Neglecting early dental planning and psychological support"],"management":["Active thermoregulation with avoidance of overheating, cool environments, cooling garments and adequate hydration to prevent hyperthermia","Prompt recognition and management of unexplained fevers, which may reflect impaired sweating rather than infection","Regular emollients for dry skin and treatment of associated eczema","Early and staged dental rehabilitation with prostheses, and dental implants once growth allows, for hypodontia and conical teeth","Ocular and mucosal lubrication with artificial tears and saliva substitutes for reduced tear and saliva production","Treatment and monitoring of recurrent respiratory and ear infections","Multidisciplinary input from dermatology, paediatric dentistry, ENT and clinical genetics","Genetic counselling with EDA carrier testing for female relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical genetics texts on ectodermal dysplasias","Standard dermatology texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_hypophosphataemia":{"name":"x linked hypophosphataemia","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XLH","X-linked hypophosphataemic rickets","familial hypophosphataemic rickets"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked hypophosphataemia is the commonest heritable form of rickets and osteomalacia, inherited as an X-linked dominant trait caused by loss-of-function mutations in PHEX.","Loss of PHEX function leads to excess circulating FGF23, which drives renal phosphate wasting and suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylation, giving an inappropriately normal or low calcitriol.","Biochemistry shows low phosphate with high urinary phosphate (low TmP/GFR), normal calcium, normal or low 1,25-hydroxyvitamin D, normal or mildly raised PTH, raised alkaline phosphatase and raised or inappropriately normal FGF23.","Unlike nutritional rickets, calcium and 25-hydroxyvitamin D are normal and there is no hypocalcaemia or tetany.","Children present with leg bowing, short stature, a waddling gait, delayed walking and spontaneous dental abscesses.","Conventional treatment with oral phosphate salts plus active vitamin D (alfacalcidol or calcitriol) risks nephrocalcinosis and secondary or tertiary hyperparathyroidism, and requires careful monitoring.","The anti-FGF23 monoclonal antibody burosumab is now preferred for children and selected adults, improving phosphate handling and healing rickets.","Adults may have persistent osteomalacia, pseudofractures, enthesopathy, degenerative arthritis, dental sepsis and, from childhood, craniosynostosis."],"pathophysiology":"PHEX encodes an endopeptidase expressed in osteoblasts and osteocytes. Its loss of function leads, through incompletely defined signalling, to overproduction of the phosphaturic hormone FGF23 by bone cells. Elevated FGF23 acts on the proximal renal tubule to downregulate the sodium-phosphate cotransporters NaPi-IIa and NaPi-IIc, causing renal phosphate wasting and hypophosphataemia. FGF23 simultaneously suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase and stimulates 24-hydroxylase, so calcitriol is inappropriately low or normal rather than rising in response to hypophosphataemia. Chronic hypophosphataemia impairs mineralisation of the growth plate (rickets) and of osteoid in mature bone (osteomalacia).","additionalDifferentials":["nutritional vitamin D-deficiency rickets","autosomal dominant or recessive hypophosphataemic rickets (FGF23, DMP1, ENPP1)","hereditary hypophosphataemic rickets with hypercalciuria (HHRH, SLC34A3)","tumour-induced osteomalacia","Fanconi syndrome and Dent disease","McCune-Albright syndrome or fibrous dysplasia","renal tubular acidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["serum phosphate, calcium, alkaline phosphatase and PTH","25-hydroxy and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D","renal tubular phosphate reabsorption (TmP/GFR)","intact FGF23","PHEX and related gene testing","renal ultrasound for nephrocalcinosis","wrist and knee radiographs and DXA"],"redFlags":["nephrocalcinosis or declining renal function on conventional treatment","tertiary hyperparathyroidism developing during phosphate therapy","craniosynostosis with raised intracranial pressure","spinal canal stenosis or cord compression from enthesopathy and ligament ossification","recurrent spontaneous dental abscesses"],"prognosis":"This is a lifelong condition; with treatment growth and deformity improve, but many adults have residual short stature, enthesopathy, degenerative joint disease and dental problems.","pitfalls":["misdiagnosing as nutritional rickets and giving only nutritional vitamin D, which is ineffective","failing to monitor for treatment-induced nephrocalcinosis","over-treatment provoking secondary or tertiary hyperparathyroidism","not screening at-risk family members","neglecting regular dental surveillance"],"management":["Conventional therapy with oral phosphate salts combined with an active vitamin D analogue such as alfacalcidol or calcitriol to promote mineralisation","Consideration of the anti-FGF23 monoclonal antibody burosumab in eligible patients as targeted disease-directed therapy","Regular biochemical monitoring of phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, calcium and parathyroid hormone during treatment","Renal ultrasound surveillance for nephrocalcinosis and vigilance for secondary or tertiary hyperparathyroidism","Orthopaedic assessment and corrective surgery for persistent lower-limb deformity","Dental surveillance and prompt treatment of spontaneous dental abscesses","Multidisciplinary metabolic bone follow-up spanning paediatric-to-adult transition","Genetic counselling for the X-linked dominant trait and screening of at-risk relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Textbook of Endocrinology","Oxford Textbook of Endocrinology and Diabetes","Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_hypophosphatemia":{"name":"X-linked hypophosphataemic rickets","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XLH","PHEX-related hypophosphataemia","familial hypophosphataemic rickets"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest heritable form of rickets, caused by loss-of-function mutations in PHEX and inherited as an X-linked dominant trait","The biochemical signature is hypophosphataemia from renal phosphate wasting with an inappropriately normal or low serum 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D despite the low phosphate, driven by excess circulating FGF23","Alkaline phosphatase is typically raised and serum calcium is usually normal, which helps distinguish it from calcium-deficiency rickets","Children present with progressive lower-limb bowing, a waddling gait, short stature and dental abscesses from poor dentine mineralisation","Renal phosphate wasting is confirmed by a low tubular maximum reabsorption of phosphate relative to glomerular filtration rate","Conventional treatment is oral phosphate combined with active vitamin D (calcitriol/alfacalcidol); the FGF23-targeted monoclonal antibody burosumab is now used for eligible patients","Monitoring for treatment complications, including nephrocalcinosis and hyperparathyroidism, is essential on conventional therapy","In adults the disease causes osteomalacia, enthesopathy, pseudofractures and dental problems"],"pathophysiology":"X-linked hypophosphataemia is caused by inactivating mutations in PHEX, a membrane endopeptidase expressed in bone and teeth. Loss of PHEX function leads, through incompletely defined mechanisms, to excessive circulating fibroblast growth factor 23 (FGF23). FGF23 reduces expression of renal sodium-phosphate cotransporters, causing phosphate to be lost in the urine, and it also suppresses the enzyme that generates active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. The result is chronic hypophosphataemia with an inappropriately low or normal active vitamin D level, impairing mineralisation of the growth plate and osteoid and producing rickets and osteomalacia.","additionalDifferentials":["Nutritional vitamin-D-deficiency rickets","Vitamin-D-dependent rickets (1-alpha-hydroxylase or receptor defects)","Autosomal dominant/recessive hypophosphataemic rickets (FGF23, DMP1, ENPP1)","Tumour-induced osteomalacia (acquired FGF23 excess)","Fanconi syndrome with renal phosphate wasting","Hypophosphatasia (low alkaline phosphatase, opposite pattern)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum phosphate, calcium, alkaline phosphatase and parathyroid hormone","Renal tubular phosphate handling (tubular maximum reabsorption of phosphate per glomerular filtration rate)","Serum 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, characteristically inappropriately low or normal, and FGF23 where available","Molecular testing of PHEX to confirm the diagnosis","Radiographs of the wrists, knees and lower limbs to document rickets and bowing","Renal ultrasound to monitor for nephrocalcinosis, especially during treatment"],"redFlags":["Progressive limb deformity or fractures despite treatment","Nephrocalcinosis or declining renal function on conventional phosphate and vitamin D therapy","Development of tertiary hyperparathyroidism during long-term treatment","Recurrent dental abscesses or spinal stenosis and cord compression from enthesopathy in adults"],"prognosis":"With early and sustained treatment, rickets heals and growth and deformity improve, though short stature and dental problems may persist; FGF23-targeted therapy has improved outcomes, but lifelong monitoring for skeletal, renal and dental complications is required.","pitfalls":["Treating as ordinary nutritional rickets with vitamin D alone, which does not correct phosphate wasting","Failing to recognise that a normal 1,25-vitamin D is inappropriate in the face of hypophosphataemia","Overtreating with phosphate and active vitamin D and causing nephrocalcinosis or hyperparathyroidism","Missing the diagnosis in adults presenting with osteomalacia, enthesopathy or dental abscesses"],"management":["Conventional oral phosphate replacement with an active vitamin D metabolite such as calcitriol or alfacalcidol to heal rickets and osteomalacia","FGF23-targeted monoclonal antibody burosumab for eligible children and adults as disease-directed therapy","Careful monitoring of serum phosphate, calcium, alkaline phosphatase and parathyroid hormone to avoid treatment-related hyperparathyroidism","Periodic renal ultrasound to detect treatment-associated nephrocalcinosis","Orthopaedic review with corrective osteotomy for residual bowing deformity where needed","Dental care and management of recurrent dental abscesses from poor dentine mineralisation","Genetic counselling for the X-linked dominant trait and evaluation of affected family members"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","GeneReviews: X-linked hypophosphatemia"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_hypopituitarism":{"name":"X-linked hypopituitarism","system":"Endocrine / Pituitary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SOX3-related hypopituitarism","X-linked panhypopituitarism","Xq27 duplication hypopituitarism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Paediatric endocrinology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic cause is abnormal dosage of the transcription factor SOX3 on Xq27, through either duplication of the region or expansion/contraction of its polyalanine tract.","SOX3 is dosage-sensitive, so both over-expression and loss of function disrupt hypothalamo-pituitary development.","Inheritance is X-linked, so affected individuals are typically male with transmission through carrier females.","The endocrine picture is variable combined pituitary hormone deficiency, most often including growth hormone deficiency.","Intellectual disability or learning difficulty accompanies the endocrine phenotype in many patients.","Pituitary MRI commonly shows anterior pituitary hypoplasia, an ectopic or undescended posterior pituitary, and an absent or thin infundibulum.","It should be considered in familial hypopituitarism with an X-linked pattern, particularly when learning difficulties coexist."],"pathophysiology":"X-linked hypopituitarism arises from disturbance of SOX3, an X-linked SRY-box transcription factor expressed in the developing hypothalamus and infundibulum that helps orchestrate formation of the pituitary gland. Normal development depends on a precise SOX3 dosage: submicroscopic duplications of the Xq27 region increase gene dosage, while expansions or contractions of an in-frame polyalanine tract and loss-of-function changes reduce effective activity, and both directions of imbalance perturb the inductive signalling between the developing infundibulum and Rathke's pouch. The consequence is maldevelopment of the hypothalamo-pituitary axis, producing structural anomalies such as anterior pituitary hypoplasia, an ectopic posterior pituitary and an interrupted stalk, together with deficient secretion of one or more anterior pituitary hormones. Because the same signalling centre contributes to forebrain and midline development, affected individuals may also show intellectual disability and midline central nervous system anomalies. The specific combination and timing of hormone deficiencies vary between patients and can evolve over childhood, so an initially isolated deficiency may progress to broader combined pituitary hormone deficiency.","additionalDifferentials":["Other genetic combined pituitary hormone deficiency (PROP1, POU1F1, LHX3, LHX4)","Septo-optic dysplasia and HESX1-related midline disorders","Isolated growth hormone deficiency of other genetic cause","Acquired hypopituitarism from tumour, surgery, irradiation or infiltration","Perinatal or traumatic hypothalamo-pituitary injury","Other syndromic causes of congenital hypopituitarism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dynamic testing of the growth hormone axis with baseline insulin-like growth factor measurement","Assessment of the corticotroph axis given the danger of unrecognised cortisol deficiency","Thyroid-stimulating hormone with free thyroxine, gonadotrophins and prolactin","Dedicated pituitary MRI to define gland, stalk and posterior pituitary anatomy","Array-based testing for Xq27 duplication and targeted SOX3 sequencing","Bone age radiography and structured re-assessment for evolving deficiencies"],"redFlags":["Adrenocorticotrophic hormone deficiency with risk of adrenal crisis during illness or stress","Neonatal hypoglycaemia from combined growth hormone and cortisol deficiency","Untreated central hypothyroidism impairing growth and development","New or worsening deficiencies emerging on follow-up in a previously stable child"],"prognosis":"With timely recognition and lifelong replacement of the deficient hormones, growth and general health outcomes are good and life-threatening crises are preventable. Associated intellectual disability, where present, tends to persist, and the endocrine phenotype may broaden over time so continued surveillance is needed.","pitfalls":["Failing to identify and treat cortisol deficiency, the most immediately life-threatening component.","Not performing pituitary MRI, thereby missing the characteristic structural triad.","Overlooking an X-linked family history and associated learning difficulties.","Assuming an isolated hormone deficiency is static when combined deficiency may evolve.","Attributing the picture to acquired disease without considering a dosage-sensitive genetic cause."],"management":["Individualised replacement of deficient anterior pituitary hormones, including growth hormone for growth hormone deficiency","Glucocorticoid replacement for corticotroph deficiency with mandatory stress dosing and sick-day rules to prevent adrenal crisis","Thyroxine replacement for central hypothyroidism, started only after cortisol status is secured","Sex steroid replacement at the appropriate developmental stage for gonadotrophin deficiency","Structured periodic re-assessment of the pituitary axes, as the endocrine phenotype may broaden over time","Developmental and educational support where intellectual disability or learning difficulty is present","Multidisciplinary paediatric endocrine follow-up with structured transition to adult services","Genetic counselling with SOX3 and Xq27 dosage testing and assessment of carrier females"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Paediatric endocrinology reference texts","Clinical genetics reference resources on pituitary development disorders","Consensus guidance on combined pituitary hormone deficiency"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_ichthyosis":{"name":"X-linked ichthyosis","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STS","steroid sulfatase deficiency","X-linked recessive ichthyosis","XLI"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked recessive disorder caused by deficiency of steroid sulfatase from deletion or mutation of the STS gene, so it predominantly affects males","Presents in infancy with dark brown, adherent 'dirty' polygonal scales, prominent on the extremities, neck, and trunk but sparing the palms and soles","Flexures are relatively spared and the central face is usually clear, helping distinguish it from ichthyosis vulgaris","Corneal comma-shaped stromal opacities that do not affect vision are a useful associated finding","Cryptorchidism and a modestly increased risk of testicular problems occur in some patients","Larger contiguous gene deletions can produce associated features such as Kallmann syndrome or intellectual disability","Reduced maternal serum or urinary oestriol during pregnancy can be an early clue to affected male fetuses"],"pathophysiology":"X-linked ichthyosis is caused by deficiency of steroid sulfatase, an enzyme that hydrolyses cholesterol sulfate and sulfated steroid precursors. In the epidermis, accumulation of cholesterol sulfate destabilises the intercellular lipid lamellae of the stratum corneum and inhibits proteases responsible for corneodesmosome breakdown, so desquamation is impaired and corneocytes are retained. This produces the characteristic retention hyperkeratosis with large adherent scales. Because the STS gene lies in a region of the X chromosome that partly escapes inactivation and is prone to deletion, larger deletions may remove neighbouring genes and cause a contiguous gene syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Ichthyosis vulgaris","Lamellar ichthyosis / autosomal recessive congenital ichthyosis","Acquired ichthyosis","Atopic dermatitis with xerosis","Keratosis pilaris and other keratinisation disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular testing for STS deletion or mutation (e.g. FISH, MLPA, or array)","Steroid sulfatase enzyme activity assay in leucocytes or fibroblasts","Slit-lamp examination for asymptomatic corneal opacities","Skin biopsy showing hyperkeratosis with a normal or slightly thickened granular layer","Examination for cryptorchidism and, where indicated, evaluation for contiguous gene syndrome features","Maternal serum oestriol history and prenatal genetic testing in at-risk pregnancies"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting a contiguous gene deletion syndrome (e.g. anosmia and hypogonadism, developmental delay)","Cryptorchidism requiring urological evaluation","Failure of scaling to respond to standard care, prompting reassessment of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"It is a benign, lifelong condition confined largely to the skin in the isolated form, with scaling that persists but is manageable with regular emollient and keratolytic care; prognosis depends on any associated features in patients with larger deletions.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with ichthyosis vulgaris, which spares extremities less consistently and is associated with atopy and keratosis pilaris","Overlooking associated cryptorchidism or contiguous gene syndrome features","Failing to recognise the enzyme deficiency implication for atypical labour or low maternal oestriol","Attributing corneal opacities to unrelated ocular disease"],"management":["Regular liberal emollients as the cornerstone of care to soften and reduce retained scale","Keratolytic agents such as urea, lactic acid or other alpha-hydroxy acids to aid desquamation","Topical or, in resistant cases, systemic retinoids under specialist dermatology supervision","Slit-lamp ophthalmology review for corneal opacities, which are typically asymptomatic and need no treatment","Examination for and surgical management of cryptorchidism","Evaluation for a contiguous gene syndrome, such as Kallmann features or learning difficulty, when a larger deletion is present","Genetic counselling with carrier assessment and discussion of prenatal testing in at-risk pregnancies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_juvenile_retinoschisis":{"name":"X-linked juvenile retinoschisis","system":"Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["X-linked retinoschisis","XLRS","Congenital retinoschisis","Juvenile retinoschisis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ophthalmology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked recessive disorder of the RS1 gene (retinoschisin); males are affected and carriers are usually unaffected.","Typically presents in school-age boys with reduced central vision.","Foveal schisis produces a cystic, spoke-wheel (cartwheel) maculopathy.","Peripheral retinoschisis, usually inferotemporal, occurs in a subset of patients.","The electroretinogram is characteristically electronegative, with a reduced b-wave and relatively preserved a-wave.","Optical coherence tomography shows splitting of the inner retinal layers.","Foveal schisis does not leak on fluorescein angiography, which helps distinguish it from cystoid macular oedema.","Vitreous haemorrhage and retinal detachment are the main sight-threatening complications."],"pathophysiology":"X-linked juvenile retinoschisis is caused by mutations in RS1 on the short arm of the X chromosome, which encodes retinoschisin, a secreted adhesion protein produced by photoreceptors and bipolar cells that maintains the structural integrity and cell-to-cell adhesion of the retina. Loss of functional retinoschisin causes splitting (schisis) within the inner retinal layers, classically producing cystic cavities at the fovea in a radial spoke-wheel pattern and, in a proportion of patients, peripheral (typically inferotemporal) retinoschisis. Because inheritance is X-linked recessive, males are affected while female carriers are generally asymptomatic. Disruption of the photoreceptor-to-bipolar synapse and of Müller cell function impairs transmission of the visual signal, reflected in the characteristic electronegative electroretinogram, a selectively reduced b-wave with relative preservation of the a-wave. Schisis and the associated vitreous changes predispose to vitreous haemorrhage and retinal detachment.","additionalDifferentials":["Degenerative (acquired) retinoschisis of older adults","Cystoid macular oedema, which leaks on angiography unlike foveal schisis","Goldmann-Favre syndrome and enhanced S-cone syndrome (NR2E3)","Stargardt disease and other macular dystrophies","Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Familial exudative vitreoretinopathy","Amblyopia and other causes of childhood visual impairment"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Optical coherence tomography demonstrating foveal and inner-retinal schisis cavities","Full-field electroretinography showing the electronegative waveform","RS1 molecular genetic testing","Fluorescein angiography, characteristically non-leaking in foveal schisis","Dilated fundus examination for spoke-wheel maculopathy and peripheral schisis","B-scan ultrasonography if vitreous haemorrhage obscures the fundus","Examination and carrier testing of family members"],"redFlags":["Sudden loss of vision from vitreous haemorrhage or retinal detachment","New floaters or flashing lights","A sudden increase in visual field loss","Signs of retinal detachment on examination"],"prognosis":"Vision is usually reduced but relatively stable for years, although foveal atrophy may erode acuity in later life. The visual outlook is considerably worse when disease is complicated by vitreous haemorrhage or retinal detachment.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing foveal schisis as cystoid macular oedema and treating inappropriately; angiography is non-leaking in schisis","Overlooking the diagnostic significance of the electronegative electroretinogram","Attributing a boy's poor vision to amblyopia and missing the maculopathy","Failing to counsel on the contact-sport and head-trauma risk of haemorrhage and detachment"],"management":["A trial of carbonic anhydrase inhibitor therapy, topical or oral, which may reduce foveal schisis cavities as the principal pharmacological option","Low-vision aids and educational support for reduced central acuity","Advice to avoid contact and high-impact sports given the risk of vitreous haemorrhage and retinal detachment","Prompt vitreoretinal surgery for complicating retinal detachment or non-clearing vitreous haemorrhage","Regular monitoring with optical coherence tomography and dilated fundus examination to track macular and peripheral schisis","Refractive correction and amblyopia management in affected children","Genetic counselling with RS1 testing and carrier assessment of female relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ophthalmology references","Medical retina references","Ocular genetics references"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_lymphoproliferative_disease":{"name":"X-linked lymphoproliferative disease","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XLP","Duncan disease","SH2D1A","SAP deficiency","XIAP","BIRC4","XLP1","XLP2"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies / Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked disorder of boys defined by an extreme and often fatal immune dysregulation triggered by Epstein-Barr virus infection","XLP1 results from SH2D1A mutation causing loss of the signalling adaptor SAP; XLP2 results from XIAP (BIRC4) mutation","The three classic manifestations of XLP1 are fulminant EBV-driven haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, dysgammaglobulinaemia, and B-cell lymphoma","XIAP deficiency more often presents with recurrent haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, splenomegaly and inflammatory-bowel-disease-like colitis, and lymphoma is uncommon","SAP is required for normal function of natural killer T cells and for cytotoxic control of EBV-infected B cells, explaining the catastrophic response to primary EBV","Any boy with fulminant or fatal infectious mononucleosis, or EBV-associated HLH, should prompt evaluation for XLP","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is the only curative therapy and is best undertaken before or after control of an HLH episode"],"pathophysiology":"X-linked lymphoproliferative disease is a genetic failure to regulate the immune response, classically unmasked by Epstein-Barr virus. In XLP1 the SH2D1A gene encodes the SLAM-associated protein SAP, an intracellular adaptor essential for signalling through the SLAM family of receptors on T cells and natural killer cells; its loss cripples cytotoxic killing of EBV-infected B cells and impairs natural killer T-cell development. In XLP2 the affected gene XIAP encodes an inhibitor-of-apoptosis protein important for inflammasome signalling and lymphocyte survival. In both, uncontrolled activation and expansion of T cells and macrophages during viral challenge drives a hyperinflammatory, cytokine-rich state that produces haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, together with a spectrum of antibody deficiency and, in XLP1, lymphoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis from other genetic causes","Other primary EBV-susceptibility disorders (e.g. ITK, CD27, MAGT1 defects)","Common variable immunodeficiency presenting with dysgammaglobulinaemia","Sporadic EBV-associated lymphoma","Very-early-onset inflammatory bowel disease (for the XIAP phenotype)","Severe primary infectious mononucleosis without underlying defect"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flow cytometry for intracellular SAP and XIAP protein expression","Molecular genetic testing of SH2D1A and XIAP/BIRC4","Quantitative immunoglobulins and specific antibody responses","Natural killer and cytotoxic T-cell function assays","EBV viral load and HLH markers including ferritin and soluble interleukin-2 receptor","Enumeration of natural killer T cells (typically reduced or absent in XLP1)"],"redFlags":["Fulminant or progressive infectious mononucleosis with cytopenias and organ dysfunction","Ferritin and inflammatory markers rising in keeping with evolving HLH","Rapidly enlarging lymphadenopathy suggesting lymphoma","Recurrent HLH episodes or refractory colitis in a boy (XIAP phenotype)"],"prognosis":"The natural history is severe, with EBV-triggered HLH carrying high mortality, but early recognition, control of hyperinflammation, and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation markedly improve survival, particularly when transplantation is undertaken before the onset of fulminant disease.","pitfalls":["Dismissing severe mononucleosis in a boy as an ordinary viral illness without considering XLP","Attributing hypogammaglobulinaemia to common variable immunodeficiency and missing an X-linked cause","Failing to test both SAP and XIAP, since their phenotypes overlap but differ in lymphoma risk","Delaying definitive transplant planning until after a life-threatening HLH episode"],"management":["Immediate haemophagocytic-lymphohistiocytosis-directed immunochemotherapy, such as corticosteroids, etoposide and ciclosporin, to control fulminant hyperinflammation","Anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody rituximab to reduce EBV-infected B-cell burden and viral load","Immunoglobulin replacement for dysgammaglobulinaemia and antibody failure","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the definitive curative therapy, ideally before onset of fulminant disease","Surveillance for B-cell lymphoma in XLP1 and, in XIAP deficiency, management of inflammatory-bowel-disease-like colitis","Avoidance of live vaccines with careful monitoring of EBV viral load","Genetic counselling with SH2D1A and XIAP testing and screening of at-risk male relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immune Deficiencies","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_lymphoproliferative_syndrome":{"name":"X-Linked Lymphoproliferative Syndrome","system":"Immune","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XLP","Duncan disease","SH2D1A/SAP deficiency","XIAP deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Clinical immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare X-linked primary immunodeficiency with a characteristic, often catastrophic, susceptibility to Epstein-Barr virus (EBV).","Two genetic subtypes exist: XLP1 from SH2D1A mutations (loss of the SAP protein) and XLP2 from XIAP (BIRC4) mutations (loss of the XIAP protein).","The classic XLP1 phenotype comprises fulminant infectious mononucleosis or EBV-driven haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, dysgammaglobulinaemia, and lymphoma (usually B-cell and often extranodal or ileocaecal).","XIAP deficiency (XLP2) causes recurrent haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis and frequently a Crohn-like inflammatory bowel disease, but is not associated with increased lymphoma risk, a key distinction from XLP1.","Presentation is almost always in boys, and disease is frequently unmasked by primary EBV infection, although features can occur without documented EBV.","Haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis presents with prolonged fever, hepatosplenomegaly, cytopenias, hyperferritinaemia, hypertriglyceridaemia and coagulopathy, and is a medical emergency.","Diagnosis combines genetic testing with flow cytometry for absent SAP or XIAP protein expression; absent invariant natural killer T (NKT) cells support XLP1.","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation is the only curative treatment, while immunoglobulin replacement and prompt therapy directed at haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis are important supportive measures."],"pathophysiology":"X-linked lymphoproliferative syndrome results from mutations on the X chromosome that impair immune regulation. In XLP1, mutations in SH2D1A at Xq25 abolish or impair SAP (SLAM-associated protein), a small SH2-domain adaptor that couples the SLAM family of receptors to downstream signalling through Fyn kinase in T cells, natural killer (NK) cells and NKT cells. Loss of SAP impairs cytotoxic T-cell and NK-cell killing of EBV-infected B cells, blocks development of invariant NKT cells, and disrupts T-follicular-helper-cell function and germinal-centre antibody responses, explaining the uncontrolled EBV-driven lymphoproliferation, defective humoral immunity and lymphoma predisposition. In XLP2, mutations in XIAP (BIRC4, also at Xq25) cause loss of the X-linked inhibitor of apoptosis protein, which normally restrains excessive programmed cell death and modulates inflammatory signalling through the NOD2/NF-kB pathway and the inflammasome; its loss leads to lymphocyte hypersensitivity to apoptosis, defective control of EBV, recurrent haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis and Crohn-like inflammatory bowel disease, but not the lymphoma risk seen in XLP1. In both subtypes, failure to contain EBV can precipitate a hyperinflammatory, macrophage-activating haemophagocytic syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial (primary) haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (PRF1, UNC13D/Munc13-4, STX11, STXBP2)","Other EBV-susceptibility immunodeficiencies, such as MAGT1 (XMEN), CD27, CD70, ITK, CTPS1 and RASGRP1 deficiencies","Chediak-Higashi syndrome and Griscelli syndrome type 2 (pigmentary dilution with haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis)","Common variable immunodeficiency and other causes of hypogammaglobulinaemia","B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma from other causes","Severe or atypical infectious mononucleosis in an immunocompetent host","For XLP2, monogenic very-early-onset inflammatory bowel disease from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing of SH2D1A and XIAP (BIRC4)","Flow cytometry for intracellular SAP and XIAP protein expression","EBV serology and quantitative EBV viral load in blood","Enumeration of invariant NKT cells (absent in XLP1) and NK/T-cell cytotoxicity assays","Immunoglobulin levels and specific antibody/vaccine-response studies","Work-up for haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis: full blood count, ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen, liver function, soluble interleukin-2 receptor and bone-marrow examination for haemophagocytosis","Imaging and tissue biopsy where lymphoma or focal organ involvement is suspected"],"redFlags":["Fulminant or unusually severe infectious mononucleosis, especially in a boy with a suggestive family history","Features of haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (persistent fever, cytopenias, hepatosplenomegaly, very high ferritin, coagulopathy), which is life-threatening","New lymphadenopathy or an abdominal mass suggesting lymphoma","Progressive hypogammaglobulinaemia with recurrent infections","A maternal family history of males dying of EBV infection, haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis or lymphoma"],"prognosis":"Untreated disease, particularly EBV-triggered haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, historically carried a very high mortality. Outcomes have improved markedly with early recognition, therapy directed at haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis and curative allogeneic haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation, though the prognosis remains guarded once severe haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis or lymphoma is established.","pitfalls":["Dismissing severe infectious mononucleosis in a young boy as ordinary EBV infection and missing the underlying immunodeficiency.","Assuming that a normal EBV viral load or EBV-negative status excludes the diagnosis, as disease can occur without documented EBV.","Overlooking XIAP deficiency in boys presenting with recurrent haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis or early-onset inflammatory bowel disease.","Delaying assessment for definitive stem-cell transplantation while repeatedly treating recurrent episodes.","Not screening at-risk male relatives and female carriers within the family."],"management":["Emergency treatment of EBV-driven haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis with immunosuppressive and cytotoxic protocols using corticosteroids, etoposide and ciclosporin","Rituximab to lower the EBV-infected B-cell load in acute EBV disease","Regular immunoglobulin replacement for dysgammaglobulinaemia","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the only curative option, best undertaken once disease is in remission","Endoscopic and medical management of Crohn-like inflammatory bowel disease in XIAP deficiency","Surveillance for B-cell lymphoma in XLP1, with prompt evaluation of new lymphadenopathy","Genetic counselling and predictive testing of SH2D1A and XIAP within the kindred"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Primary immunodeficiency reference texts and the IUIS classification of inborn errors of immunity","Reference reviews on haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","Standard clinical immunology textbooks"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_nephrogenic_diabetes_insipidus":{"name":"X-linked nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AVPR2-related nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","V2 receptor NDI","Congenital nephrogenic diabetes insipidus type 1"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nephrology genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common inherited form of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, caused by loss-of-function variants in AVPR2 encoding the renal V2 vasopressin receptor.","X-linked recessive: affected boys usually present in early infancy with polyuria, polydipsia, poor feeding, vomiting, failure to thrive, unexplained fevers and episodes of hypernatraemic dehydration.","The collecting duct cannot respond to vasopressin, so urine remains inappropriately dilute despite a raised plasma osmolality.","Characteristically there is no urinary concentrating response to desmopressin, distinguishing it from central (cranial) diabetes insipidus.","Heterozygous female carriers may show variable concentrating defects owing to skewed X-inactivation, ranging from asymptomatic to overtly polyuric.","Recurrent hypernatraemic episodes in infancy risk neurological injury and developmental delay if not promptly recognised and managed.","Chronic high urine flow can produce a dilated urinary tract, megacystis and hydronephrosis.","Management centres on unrestricted access to water and measures that lower urine volume by reducing solute load; the condition contrasts with the autosomal AQP2 (aquaporin-2) forms."],"pathophysiology":"AVPR2 on Xq28 encodes the arginine vasopressin receptor 2 (the V2 receptor), a G-protein-coupled receptor on the basolateral membrane of principal cells in the renal collecting duct. Vasopressin binding normally activates Gs-adenylate cyclase-cyclic AMP signalling, which drives insertion of aquaporin-2 water channels into the apical membrane and permits water reabsorption. Loss-of-function variants, most often causing receptor misfolding with retention in the endoplasmic reticulum, abolish this signalling so that circulating vasopressin can no longer concentrate the urine. Because the gene is X-linked, hemizygous males are affected while heterozygous females show variable phenotypes governed by X-inactivation. Variants in the downstream water-channel gene AQP2 (aquaporin-2) cause the rarer autosomal recessive and dominant forms, which are clinically similar but differ in inheritance.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal recessive and dominant nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from AQP2 (aquaporin-2) variants","Central (cranial) diabetes insipidus / arginine vasopressin deficiency","Primary polydipsia","Acquired nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (lithium, hypercalcaemia, hypokalaemia, obstructive uropathy)","Osmotic diuresis, for example in poorly controlled diabetes mellitus","Bartter syndrome and other salt-wasting tubulopathies presenting with infantile polyuria","Fanconi syndrome and other generalised tubular concentrating defects"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Paired plasma and urine osmolality alongside plasma sodium","Water deprivation test with a desmopressin challenge (no urinary concentration after desmopressin in nephrogenic disease)","Copeptin measurement as a surrogate for vasopressin secretion","AVPR2 molecular genetic testing, with AQP2 analysis if AVPR2 is negative","Renal tract ultrasound for hydronephrosis and bladder dilatation","Serial growth and neurodevelopmental monitoring","Review for acquired causes, including drug history, calcium and potassium"],"redFlags":["Hypernatraemic dehydration with seizures or reduced consciousness in an infant","Failure to thrive with recurrent unexplained fevers","Progressive hydronephrosis or a chronically distended bladder","Over-rapid correction of hypernatraemia risking cerebral oedema"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis, reliable access to water and volume-reducing measures, most affected individuals grow and develop normally; the principal threats are neurodevelopmental sequelae from repeated hypernatraemic episodes and urinary tract dilatation from chronic polyuria.","pitfalls":["Mistaking recurrent infantile fevers and irritability for sepsis rather than hypernatraemic dehydration","Expecting a desmopressin response, which does not occur and distinguishes the condition from central diabetes insipidus","Overlooking symptomatic female carriers","Correcting hypernatraemia too rapidly","Attributing the polyuria simply to behavioural excessive drinking"],"management":["Guaranteed free and continuous access to water as the fundamental supportive measure","A low-solute diet, reduced in sodium and protein, to lessen the obligatory urine output","Thiazide diuretics to induce mild volume contraction and reduce polyuria","Addition of a potassium-sparing diuretic such as amiloride or a prostaglandin synthesis inhibitor such as indometacin to enhance the antidiuretic response","Prompt but careful, gradual correction of hypernatraemic dehydration episodes to avoid neurological injury","Renal tract ultrasound surveillance for hydronephrosis and bladder dilatation from chronic high urine flow","Serial growth and neurodevelopmental monitoring in affected children","Genetic counselling with AVPR2 testing and assessment of carrier females"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nephrology reference texts","Clinical genetics reference texts","Paediatric tubulopathy and endocrinology guidelines (source-neutral)"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_protoporphyria":{"name":"X-linked protoporphyria","system":"Metabolic / Porphyria","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XLP","XLPP","X-linked dominant protoporphyria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Metabolic medicine references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked protoporphyria is a cutaneous porphyria caused by gain-of-function variants in ALAS2 (erythroid-specific delta-aminolaevulinic acid synthase), typically small deletions in the final exon that remove the enzyme's autoregulatory carboxy-terminus and increase its activity.","It is clinically indistinguishable from erythropoietic protoporphyria: acute, painful, non-blistering photosensitivity with burning, stinging and swelling of sun-exposed skin, often beginning in early childhood.","Increased ALAS2 activity drives excess protoporphyrin production in erythroid cells, so both metal-free protoporphyrin and zinc-protoporphyrin accumulate — unlike classic EPP where the zinc-chelated fraction is comparatively low.","Diagnosis rests on a raised erythrocyte total protoporphyrin with a substantially higher proportion of zinc-protoporphyrin than in FECH-related EPP, confirmed by ALAS2 molecular testing.","Because it is X-linked, hemizygous males are usually affected, while heterozygous females show variable severity depending on lyonisation; this differs from the autosomal recessive/dominant inheritance of erythropoietic protoporphyria.","Hepatobiliary complications from protoporphyrin deposition — cholelithiasis and potentially severe protoporphyric liver disease — occur as in EPP and warrant periodic liver monitoring.","Excess protoporphyrin is excreted in bile and faeces, not urine, so urinary porphyrins and porphyrin precursors are characteristically normal and do not exclude the diagnosis.","Management centres on strict visible-light photoprotection, opaque physical sunblocks and clothing, correction of any coexisting iron deficiency, and consideration of afamelanotide or other photoprotective strategies in specialist centres."],"pathophysiology":"X-linked protoporphyria is caused by gain-of-function variants in ALAS2 on the X chromosome, which encodes the erythroid-specific isoform of delta-aminolaevulinic acid synthase, the first and rate-limiting enzyme of the haem biosynthetic pathway in developing red cells. The commonest variants are small frameshift deletions in the terminal exon that truncate the enzyme's carboxy-terminal regulatory domain; loss of this autoinhibitory tail markedly increases catalytic activity and the flux of delta-aminolaevulinic acid entering the pathway. The downstream enzymes, particularly ferrochelatase, become rate-limiting, so metal-free protoporphyrin IX accumulates in erythrocytes and, because ferrochelatase activity is normal rather than deficient, a substantial fraction is also chelated with zinc to form zinc-protoporphyrin — the biochemical signature that separates this disorder from ferrochelatase-deficient erythropoietic protoporphyria. Lipophilic protoporphyrin released from red cells circulates in plasma and, on absorbing visible light in the Soret band around the violet region, generates reactive oxygen species that damage vascular endothelium and dermal tissue, producing the intense burning photosensitivity. Protoporphyrin is cleared hepatically into bile, where high concentrations can be cholestatic and crystallise, driving gallstones and, in a minority, progressive protoporphyric hepatopathy that may culminate in liver failure. Availability of iron modulates the phenotype, since iron limitation further favours accumulation of metal-free protoporphyrin.","additionalDifferentials":["Erythropoietic protoporphyria due to ferrochelatase (FECH) deficiency","Polymorphic light eruption and solar urticaria","Hydroa vacciniforme","Phototoxic drug or contact reactions","Congenital erythropoietic porphyria and porphyria cutanea tarda (blistering cutaneous porphyrias)","Contact or atopic dermatitis of sun-exposed skin","Autoimmune or connective-tissue photosensitivity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Erythrocyte total protoporphyrin with fractionation into metal-free and zinc-protoporphyrin","Plasma porphyrin fluorescence scan showing an emission peak around the protoporphyrin wavelength","ALAS2 molecular genetic testing, including terminal-exon deletion analysis","Iron studies and ferritin to identify contributory iron deficiency","Liver function tests and hepatic imaging for protoporphyric liver disease and gallstones","Faecal porphyrins (raised) with confirmation that urinary porphyrins are normal","FECH gene analysis to distinguish from erythropoietic protoporphyria when needed"],"redFlags":["Rising bilirubin or transaminases suggesting protoporphyric liver injury","Rapidly progressive cholestasis or signs of acute hepatic decompensation","Severe unremitting phototoxic pain with skin breakdown","Right upper quadrant pain indicating symptomatic gallstones","New anaemia or worsening iron status altering protoporphyrin balance"],"prognosis":"Most patients have a lifelong but non-progressive cutaneous course dominated by disabling photosensitivity and marked lifestyle restriction, with normal life expectancy when the liver is unaffected. A minority develop serious protoporphyric hepatopathy that can progress to liver failure and require transplantation, making periodic hepatic surveillance essential.","pitfalls":["Excluding porphyria on the basis of normal urinary porphyrins, which are expected to be normal in this erythropoietic protoporphyria variant","Assuming ferrochelatase deficiency and stopping the workup without measuring the zinc-protoporphyrin fraction or testing ALAS2","Overlooking the diagnosis in an affected female by assuming a purely recessive X-linked pattern","Neglecting liver monitoring and missing early protoporphyric hepatopathy","Aggressively correcting iron without recognising its influence on protoporphyrin accumulation and photosensitivity"],"management":["Strict photoprotection with sun avoidance, tightly woven protective clothing and opaque reflectant sunblocks as the mainstay","The alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone analogue afamelanotide to increase dermal melanin and improve light tolerance","Oral beta-carotene and correction of the vitamin D deficiency arising from sun avoidance","Identification and correction of contributory iron deficiency","Periodic liver function testing and hepatic imaging to detect protoporphyric liver disease and gallstones","Prompt hepatology referral for progressive protoporphyric hepatopathy, which may require intensive treatment or liver transplantation","Genetic counselling with ALAS2 testing and assessment of variably affected female carriers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard textbook of inherited metabolic disease","Reference haematology and porphyria diagnostic guidance","Dermatology reference on the photosensitivity disorders"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_reticulate_pigmentary_disorder":{"name":"X-linked Reticulate Pigmentary Disorder","system":"Dermatology genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XLPDR","X-linked reticulate pigmentary disorder with systemic manifestations","Partington syndrome type II"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology genetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked recessive multisystem disorder caused by a recurrent deep-intronic variant in POLA1, the catalytic subunit of DNA polymerase alpha, that reduces POLA1 expression.","Loss of POLA1-dependent cytosolic nucleic-acid synthesis leads to unopposed type I interferon activation, placing the disorder among the type I interferonopathies.","Affected males show generalised reticulate grey-brown hyperpigmentation developing in infancy, with a distinctive facies of frontally upswept hair and flaring, arched eyebrows.","Recurrent respiratory infections progressing to bronchiectasis, intractable infantile diarrhoea or enterocolitis with failure to thrive, and inflammatory keratitis with corneal scarring and photophobia are cardinal systemic features.","Hypohidrosis, recurrent urinary tract problems such as urethral stricture and stones, and episodes of sterile inflammation are common.","Carrier females are typically asymptomatic apart from linear hyperpigmentation following the lines of Blaschko, reflecting X-inactivation.","Despite the recurrent infections, the immune phenotype is one of dysregulation and autoinflammation rather than a classic immunodeficiency.","Systemic involvement distinguishes it from purely cutaneous reticulate disorders such as acropigmentation of Kitamura and Dowling-Degos disease."],"pathophysiology":"X-linked reticulate pigmentary disorder is caused by a recurrent deep-intronic single-nucleotide variant in POLA1, the gene encoding the catalytic subunit of DNA polymerase alpha. The variant induces aberrant splicing that reduces POLA1 protein levels. Beyond its canonical role in initiating nuclear DNA replication, DNA polymerase alpha synthesises cytosolic RNA:DNA species that normally restrain the type I interferon response; when POLA1 is deficient this brake is lost and there is chronic, unopposed production of type I interferon, so the disorder behaves as a type I interferonopathy with autoinflammatory tissue damage. The reticulate pigmentation, recurrent sterile and infective inflammation of the lungs, gut, cornea, and urinary tract, and the characteristic dysmorphism all stem from this interferon-driven dysregulation combined with partial replication stress. Because POLA1 is X-linked, hemizygous males are affected while heterozygous females show only Blaschko-linear hyperpigmentation corresponding to the pattern of lyonisation.","additionalDifferentials":["Incontinentia pigmenti (IKBKG/NEMO) with Blaschko-linear pigmentation but preceding vesicular and verrucous stages and a different systemic profile.","Reticulate acropigmentation of Kitamura (ADAM10) and Dowling-Degos disease, which are cutaneous only without systemic disease.","Dyskeratosis congenita with reticulate pigmentation, nail dystrophy, oral leukoplakia, and bone-marrow failure.","Naegeli-Franceschetti-Jadassohn syndrome (KRT14) with reticulate pigmentation, hypohidrosis, and absent dermatoglyphics.","Other type I interferonopathies such as Aicardi-Goutieres syndrome and STING-associated vasculopathy with overlapping inflammatory features.","Chronic granulomatous disease and other primary immunodeficiencies presenting with recurrent infections and colitis.","Hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia with immunodeficiency (NEMO-related)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted molecular testing for the recurrent intronic POLA1 variant, which standard exome pipelines may miss.","Interferon signature or gene-expression testing supporting a type I interferonopathy.","Skin biopsy of reticulate lesions and examination of the mother for Blaschko-linear pigmentation.","Ophthalmological assessment with slit-lamp examination for keratitis and corneal scarring.","High-resolution chest imaging and respiratory review for recurrent infection and bronchiectasis.","Gastrointestinal evaluation with endoscopy and histology for enterocolitis and failure to thrive.","Immunological work-up with cultures during infective episodes, and urological imaging for stricture or stones."],"redFlags":["Sight-threatening keratitis with corneal scarring requiring urgent ophthalmology input.","Recurrent pneumonia progressing to bronchiectasis or respiratory failure.","Intractable infantile diarrhoea with failure to thrive and dehydration.","Systemic inflammatory flares or sepsis in an infant with reticulate pigmentation and the characteristic facies.","Urinary obstruction from stricture or stones threatening renal function."],"prognosis":"Prognosis is variable and depends on the severity of recurrent respiratory, gastrointestinal, and ocular disease; some affected males have significant morbidity and early mortality from infection or its complications, while others survive into adulthood. Carrier females generally have a normal lifespan with only cutaneous findings.","pitfalls":["The causative POLA1 variant is deep-intronic and can be missed by standard exome sequencing, delaying diagnosis.","Misreading recurrent infections as a primary immunodeficiency and overlooking the underlying interferonopathy.","Dismissing a mother's Blaschko-linear pigmentation, which is a key clue to X-linked inheritance.","Attributing the multisystem disease to the pigmentation alone, or confusing it with purely cutaneous reticulate disorders.","Overlooking treatable ocular and pulmonary complications while focusing on the skin."],"management":["Prompt antimicrobial treatment of recurrent respiratory, urinary and gastrointestinal infections","Airway clearance and chest physiotherapy with respiratory surveillance for evolving bronchiectasis","Nutritional support and gastroenterology management of enterocolitis and failure to thrive","Ophthalmic care with ocular lubrication and specialist treatment of inflammatory keratitis and corneal scarring","Consideration of interferon-pathway-directed therapy such as JAK inhibition, reflecting the disorder's nature as a type I interferonopathy","Urological management of recurrent urinary problems including urethral stricture and stones","Multidisciplinary follow-up across respiratory, gastroenterology, ophthalmology and immunology","Genetic counselling with targeted testing for the deep-intronic POLA1 variant and carrier assessment of mothers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology in General Medicine","Inborn Errors of Immunity reference texts"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_retinoschisis":{"name":"X-linked retinoschisis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XLRS","juvenile retinoschisis","congenital retinoschisis","RS1","retinoschisin deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Ryan's Retina / Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked recessive disorder of males caused by RS1 mutations affecting retinoschisin, a protein needed for retinal cell adhesion and layer cohesion","Typically presents in boyhood with reduced central acuity; a classic sign is a spoke-wheel or cartwheel pattern of foveal cystic change radiating from the centre","Optical coherence tomography is diagnostic, showing splitting (schisis) of the inner retinal layers, most characteristically in the fovea, and often in the peripheral retina","Peripheral retinoschisis, when present, predisposes to vitreous haemorrhage and to schisis-related retinal detachment","The electroretinogram characteristically shows a selective reduction of the b-wave with relative preservation of the a-wave, giving an electronegative configuration","Fundus autofluorescence and OCT often reveal the foveal changes even when the spoke-wheel appearance is subtle on ophthalmoscopy","Management is supportive with carbonic anhydrase inhibitors sometimes used for the cystic maculopathy, surveillance for and repair of retinal detachment or vitreous haemorrhage, and genetic counselling; gene therapy is under investigation"],"pathophysiology":"X-linked retinoschisis is caused by mutations in RS1, encoding retinoschisin, a secreted adhesion protein produced by photoreceptors and bipolar cells that maintains the structural integrity and cell-to-cell adhesion of the retinal layers. Deficiency leads to splitting within the inner retina, classically at the fovea and often in the periphery, disrupting synaptic transmission between photoreceptors and bipolar cells. The impaired bipolar signalling accounts for the selectively reduced b-wave and electronegative electroretinogram, while structural schisis explains the cystic maculopathy and the risk of haemorrhage and detachment.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystoid macular oedema from other causes","Goldmann-Favre syndrome and enhanced S-cone syndrome","Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment or degenerative senile retinoschisis","Vitreoretinal dystrophies (Wagner, Stickler)","Foveal hypoplasia","Other causes of an electronegative electroretinogram such as congenital stationary night blindness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Spectral-domain optical coherence tomography demonstrating inner retinal foveal schisis in a spoke-wheel pattern","Full-field electroretinography showing a selectively reduced b-wave (electronegative pattern)","Fundus autofluorescence and wide-field imaging for peripheral schisis","B-scan ultrasonography when vitreous haemorrhage obscures the view","Molecular genetic testing for RS1 mutations","Assessment of female relatives as potential carriers"],"redFlags":["Sudden visual loss from vitreous haemorrhage or retinal detachment requiring urgent assessment","New floaters, field loss or a curtain in the vision indicating possible detachment","Bilateral macular schisis with declining central acuity","Family history along an X-linked pattern prompting screening of relatives"],"prognosis":"Central acuity is often reduced from childhood and may decline slowly with age; visual prognosis worsens if peripheral schisis leads to vitreous haemorrhage or retinal detachment, which can cause severe loss if untreated.","pitfalls":["Mistaking foveal schisis for cystoid macular oedema and treating inappropriately","Failing to examine the peripheral retina for schisis and detachment risk","Attributing the electronegative electroretinogram to another disorder without OCT confirmation of schisis","Overlooking the X-linked inheritance and neglecting carrier and relative counselling"],"management":["Topical or systemic carbonic anhydrase inhibitors as first-line pharmacological treatment to reduce foveal schisis","Refractive correction, amblyopia therapy and low-vision aids for affected boys","Counselling to avoid contact sports because of the risk of vitreous haemorrhage and retinal detachment","Vitreoretinal surgical intervention for retinal detachment or persistent vitreous haemorrhage","Regular optical coherence tomography and wide-field imaging to monitor macular and peripheral schisis","Genetic counselling with RS1 testing and carrier evaluation of female relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Ryan's Retina","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_scid":{"name":"X-linked severe combined immunodeficiency","system":"Immunology / Combined immunodeficiency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["X-SCID","IL2RG deficiency","common gamma chain deficiency","T-B+NK- SCID"],"chapter":null,"source":"Immunology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most common form of severe combined immunodeficiency, caused by mutations in IL2RG encoding the common gamma chain shared by the receptors for interleukins 2, 4, 7, 9, 15 and 21.","Immunophenotype is characteristically T-B+NK-: profoundly absent or non-functional T cells and natural killer cells, with B cells present in normal or raised numbers but incapable of effective antibody production.","X-linked recessive inheritance means affected infants are almost exclusively male; a family history of early male infant deaths from infection is a strong clue.","Presents in the first months of life with failure to thrive, persistent oral candidiasis, chronic diarrhoea, and recurrent or opportunistic infections including Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia.","Thymic shadow is typically absent on chest imaging and lymphoid tissue such as tonsils and lymph nodes is scanty or absent on examination.","Live vaccines (BCG, rotavirus, oral polio, MMR, varicella) and non-irradiated blood products are dangerous and can cause disseminated infection or transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease.","Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is curative and outcomes are best when performed early before infections accumulate; gene therapy is an established alternative.","Newborn screening using T-cell receptor excision circles (TRECs) detects the near-absence of naive T cells and enables pre-symptomatic diagnosis."],"pathophysiology":"X-linked SCID arises from loss-of-function mutations in IL2RG on the X chromosome, which encodes the common gamma chain (gamma-c). This chain is an indispensable signalling component shared across the receptors for interleukins 2, 4, 7, 9, 15 and 21, coupling ligand binding to the JAK3-STAT5 intracellular pathway. Absent interleukin-7 signalling halts thymic T-cell development, producing a profound deficiency of T lymphocytes, while absent interleukin-15 signalling abolishes natural killer cell development, giving the characteristic T-B+NK- pattern. B cells are generated normally in number but, deprived of T-cell help and defective in their own gamma-c-dependent interleukin-4 and interleukin-21 signalling, cannot undergo effective class switching or produce protective antibody. The net result is failure of both cellular and humoral adaptive immunity, rendering the infant susceptible to viral, fungal, bacterial and opportunistic organisms from the time maternal antibody wanes.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal recessive JAK3 deficiency (phenocopy with identical T-B+NK- immunophenotype)","Interleukin-7 receptor alpha chain deficiency (T-B+NK+ SCID)","Adenosine deaminase deficiency SCID (typically T-B-NK-)","RAG1/RAG2 deficiency and Artemis deficiency (T-B-NK+ SCID)","Complete DiGeorge syndrome with athymia","Maternofoetal or acquired lymphopenia from congenital infection","Reticular dysgenesis with combined lymphoid and myeloid failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lymphocyte subset enumeration by flow cytometry demonstrating the T-B+NK- pattern","Serum immunoglobulin levels and specific antibody responses (interpreted against transplacental maternal IgG)","T-cell proliferation assays to mitogens and antigens showing absent responses","IL2RG gene sequencing to confirm the causative mutation","Assessment of maternal engraftment and analysis of maternal X-inactivation as a carrier clue","TREC quantification confirming markedly reduced naive T-cell output","HLA typing of patient and family to expedite donor identification for transplantation"],"redFlags":["Opportunistic infection such as Pneumocystis pneumonia or disseminated BCG in an infant","Persistent oral thrush and chronic diarrhoea with failure to thrive","Absent thymic shadow and impalpable lymphoid tissue","Erythroderma or hepatosplenomegaly suggesting maternal T-cell engraftment or transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease","Any male infant with severe lymphopenia and a family history of early male deaths"],"prognosis":"Untreated the condition is almost uniformly fatal within the first year or two of life from overwhelming infection. Early haematopoietic stem cell transplantation or gene therapy, particularly before significant infection burden accrues, offers excellent long-term survival and immune reconstitution.","pitfalls":["Normal or raised total lymphocyte and B-cell counts can falsely reassure; naive T cells must be specifically assessed.","Transplacental maternal IgG can mask the humoral defect in early infancy, so normal immunoglobulins do not exclude the diagnosis.","Administering live vaccines or non-irradiated cellular blood products before diagnosis can be lethal.","Maternal T-cell engraftment may produce detectable circulating T cells and mislead interpretation of subsets.","Delaying transplantation to clear an active infection worsens outcomes; referral to a specialist centre should be immediate."],"management":["Urgent allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation as the definitive potentially curative treatment, ideally before significant infection accrues","Gene therapy directed at IL2RG as an alternative curative approach in suitable patients","Pneumocystis jirovecii prophylaxis with co-trimoxazole together with antifungal cover","Immunoglobulin replacement until immune reconstitution is achieved","Strict avoidance of live vaccines and use of irradiated, CMV-safe, leucodepleted blood products only","Protective isolation with aggressive treatment of intercurrent infection pending transplant","Newborn TREC-based screening and early referral to a specialist immunology transplant centre","Genetic counselling with IL2RG carrier testing of the mother and female relatives"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard clinical immunology textbook","International primary immunodeficiency classification and management guidelines","National newborn screening and SCID management guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_sideroblastic_anaemia":{"name":"X-linked sideroblastic anaemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XLSA","ALAS2-related sideroblastic anaemia","hereditary X-linked sideroblastic anaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Postgraduate Haematology (Hoffbrand)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest congenital sideroblastic anaemia, caused by mutations in ALAS2, the erythroid-specific delta-aminolaevulinate synthase catalysing the first, pyridoxine-dependent step of haem synthesis","X-linked, so it classically affects males; carrier females are usually mild or unaffected but can be symptomatic through skewed lyonisation, sometimes presenting only in later life","Produces a microcytic, hypochromic anaemia with a characteristic dimorphic blood film and ring sideroblasts (iron-laden mitochondria encircling the erythroblast nucleus) on marrow iron staining","Many cases are pyridoxine (vitamin B6) responsive because the enzyme uses pyridoxal phosphate as a cofactor, so a therapeutic trial is warranted","Systemic iron overload is characteristic and occurs independently of transfusion, driven by ineffective erythropoiesis and hepcidin suppression, and may be the dominant clinical problem","In contrast with iron deficiency, the anaemia is microcytic but iron stores are high, so iron supplementation is harmful","Co-inheritance of an HFE haemochromatosis variant can accentuate iron loading, and the X-linked pattern with a supportive family history aids diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"X-linked sideroblastic anaemia results from loss-of-function or hypomorphic mutations in ALAS2 on the X chromosome, encoding the erythroid isoform of delta-aminolaevulinate synthase, which condenses glycine and succinyl-CoA using pyridoxal 5'-phosphate as cofactor in the first and rate-limiting step of haem biosynthesis. Deficient enzyme activity leaves iron that has entered the erythroblast mitochondrion unincorporated into protoporphyrin, so iron accumulates within perinuclear mitochondria to form ring sideroblasts. The impaired haemoglobinisation produces a microcytic hypochromic anaemia with ineffective erythropoiesis, while suppressed hepcidin drives increased dietary iron absorption and progressive systemic iron overload.","additionalDifferentials":["Other congenital sideroblastic anaemias (SLC25A38 and mitochondrial or syndromic forms)","Acquired sideroblastic anaemia or myelodysplasia with ring sideroblasts","Iron-deficiency anaemia, microcytic but iron-deplete","Thalassaemia trait and other microcytic anaemias","Anaemia of chronic disease","Lead poisoning and copper deficiency or zinc excess","Alcohol- or drug-induced sideroblastic change"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film, showing a dimorphic hypochromic microcytic population","Bone marrow aspirate with Perls (Prussian blue) stain for ring sideroblasts","Iron studies with ferritin and transferrin saturation (raised), to distinguish from iron deficiency","ALAS2 gene sequencing, with a broader sideroblastic anaemia panel if negative","A trial of pyridoxine to assess responsiveness","Hepatic and cardiac iron quantification by MRI (T2*)","HFE genotyping where iron overload is prominent"],"redFlags":["Iron overload with cardiac, hepatic or endocrine dysfunction","Inappropriate iron therapy worsening iron loading","Severe or transfusion-dependent anaemia","Family history of unexplained iron overload or anaemia in males"],"prognosis":"Pyridoxine-responsive disease often has a good outlook with correction or improvement of the anaemia, but iron overload remains the principal threat and requires lifelong monitoring. Non-responsive or transfusion-dependent cases carry greater morbidity, much of it preventable with chelation and, where appropriate, venesection.","pitfalls":["Treating the microcytic anaemia as iron deficiency and giving iron, which aggravates overload","Not offering a pyridoxine trial","Overlooking symptomatic female carriers because the condition is labelled X-linked","Missing iron overload because the patient has never been transfused","Attributing ring sideroblasts to myelodysplasia without considering a congenital cause in a younger patient"],"management":["A therapeutic trial of pyridoxine, to which many cases respond given the enzyme's pyridoxal-phosphate cofactor dependence","Strict avoidance of iron supplementation, which is harmful because iron stores are already high","Iron chelation therapy for established systemic iron overload","Red cell transfusion support for pyridoxine-unresponsive or severe anaemia","Monitoring of iron burden with ferritin, transferrin saturation and hepatic and cardiac MRI","Haematology follow-up including assessment of carrier females, who may become symptomatic in later life","Genetic counselling with ALAS2 testing for the family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews, University of Washington, Seattle","Postgraduate Haematology (Hoffbrand)","Williams Hematology"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_sideroblastic_anaemia_ataxia":{"name":"X-linked sideroblastic anaemia with ataxia","system":"Haematology / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XLSA/A","ABCB7"],"chapter":null,"source":"Haematology and neurogenetics references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked recessive disorder caused by ABCB7 variants; ABCB7 is a mitochondrial inner-membrane ATP-binding cassette transporter required for cytosolic iron-sulphur cluster maturation.","Defined by the combination of early-onset, non-progressive or only slowly progressive spinocerebellar ataxia with a mild sideroblastic anaemia.","The ataxia usually dominates the clinical picture, presenting in infancy or early childhood with incoordination, gait disturbance and dysarthria.","The anaemia is typically mild and hypochromic and microcytic, often detected only on investigation, with ring sideroblasts on bone marrow examination.","Free erythrocyte protoporphyrin is characteristically raised, which helps distinguish it from ALAS2-related X-linked sideroblastic anaemia.","Almost exclusively affects males; obligate female carriers are usually asymptomatic but may show a minor dimorphic red-cell population.","Brain imaging may demonstrate cerebellar atrophy or hypoplasia.","Iron supplementation is unhelpful and risks iron overload, and the anaemia rarely requires transfusion."],"pathophysiology":"ABCB7 on chromosome Xq13.3 encodes a half-type ATP-binding cassette transporter located in the inner mitochondrial membrane that exports a sulphur-containing intermediate needed for the biogenesis of cytosolic and nuclear iron-sulphur (Fe-S) clusters. Missense variants that partially impair this transporter disrupt cytosolic Fe-S cluster assembly and derange mitochondrial iron handling, so iron accumulates within perinuclear mitochondria of erythroid precursors to produce ring sideroblasts and a sideroblastic anaemia, while impaired Fe-S-dependent processes in the developing and mature nervous system, particularly the cerebellum, give rise to the characteristic ataxia. Because the gene lies on the X chromosome and the variants are typically hypomorphic rather than null, the disease manifests in hemizygous males with a comparatively mild, largely non-progressive course, whereas heterozygous females are protected by the wild-type allele.","additionalDifferentials":["ALAS2-related X-linked sideroblastic anaemia, which lacks ataxia","Other congenital sideroblastic anaemias (for example SLC25A38 and GLRX5 related)","Thiamine-responsive megaloblastic anaemia (SLC19A2)","Myopathy, lactic acidosis and sideroblastic anaemia (MLASA; PUS1, YARS2)","Pearson marrow-pancreas syndrome","Friedreich ataxia and other early-onset hereditary ataxias","Ataxia with isolated vitamin E deficiency and abetalipoproteinaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film (hypochromic microcytic, sometimes dimorphic)","Bone marrow examination with Perls' Prussian-blue stain for ring sideroblasts","Free erythrocyte protoporphyrin","Iron studies including ferritin, with vigilance for iron overload","Brain MRI for cerebellar atrophy or hypoplasia","Molecular genetic testing of ABCB7 with exclusion of ALAS2","Structured neurological assessment of the ataxia"],"redFlags":["Progressive worsening of ataxia","Symptomatic anaemia","Evidence of iron overload, especially after inappropriate iron therapy or transfusion","Developmental regression rather than the expected static course"],"prognosis":"The ataxia is typically non-progressive or only slowly progressive and the anaemia is mild, so most affected males have a near-normal life expectancy provided iron overload is avoided.","pitfalls":["The anaemia can be so mild that it is overlooked, and the ataxia is then wrongly attributed to another cause.","Iron supplementation is inappropriate and risks iron overload.","The condition is easily confused with Friedreich ataxia and other early-onset ataxias.","Only males are typically affected, so a negative family history among female relatives is unhelpful."],"management":["Supportive management of the usually mild anaemia, with transfusion reserved for symptomatic cases","Avoidance of iron supplementation with monitoring to prevent iron overload","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy to support gait and coordination affected by the cerebellar ataxia","Speech and language therapy for dysarthria","Neurological follow-up recognising the largely non-progressive course","Haematology review with periodic iron studies and blood counts","Genetic counselling with ABCB7 testing and assessment of obligate female carriers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Standard haematology reference texts","Neurogenetics reference texts","Reviews of the congenital sideroblastic anaemias"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_sideroblastic_anemia_ataxia":{"name":"X-linked sideroblastic anaemia with ataxia (ABCB7)","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ABCB7","XLSA/A","X-linked sideroblastic anaemia and ataxia","ASAT"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / Nathan and Oski","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare X-linked recessive disorder from ABCB7 mutations combining a mild sideroblastic anaemia with early-onset, non-progressive cerebellar ataxia","Affected males present in infancy or early childhood with delayed motor milestones, incoordination, and dysarthria","The anaemia is typically mild and hypochromic-microcytic with ring sideroblasts in the bone marrow","Unlike many other sideroblastic anaemias, systemic iron overload is usually mild or absent","The neurological deficit is largely static rather than degenerative, though it is disabling","Free erythrocyte protoporphyrin levels are characteristically elevated","Female carriers are generally asymptomatic but may show a minor population of hypochromic red cells","Diagnosis rests on the combination of ring sideroblasts, cerebellar signs, and ABCB7 genetic testing"],"pathophysiology":"ABCB7 encodes a mitochondrial inner-membrane transporter essential for maturation of cytosolic iron-sulphur clusters, exporting a sulphur-containing intermediate from the mitochondrion. Loss-of-function mutations impair iron-sulphur cluster biogenesis, disrupting haem synthesis and causing mitochondrial iron accumulation that produces ring sideroblasts and a hypochromic anaemia. The same defect in iron-sulphur cluster assembly compromises neuronal metabolism, particularly in the cerebellum, giving the associated non-progressive ataxia; the anaemia is usually milder than in other sideroblastic anaemias and systemic iron overload is limited.","additionalDifferentials":["ALAS2-related X-linked sideroblastic anaemia","SLC25A38-related autosomal recessive sideroblastic anaemia","Acquired (clonal) sideroblastic anaemia / myelodysplasia with ring sideroblasts","Mitochondrial cytopathies with ataxia (for example Friedreich ataxia)","Lead poisoning and other secondary sideroblastic anaemias","Other inherited cerebellar ataxias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and film showing hypochromic-microcytic anaemia","Bone marrow aspirate with iron (Perls') stain to demonstrate ring sideroblasts","Free erythrocyte protoporphyrin measurement","Iron studies to assess for (usually mild) iron loading","Neuroimaging and neurological assessment for cerebellar involvement","Genetic testing of ABCB7 and exclusion of ALAS2 and SLC25A38"],"redFlags":["Progressive rather than static neurological deterioration, prompting review of the diagnosis","Significant iron overload, which is atypical and should trigger reassessment","Worsening or transfusion-dependent anaemia","New cytopenias or dysplasia suggesting an acquired marrow disorder"],"prognosis":"The anaemia is usually mild and rarely transfusion dependent, and the cerebellar ataxia, while disabling, is generally non-progressive, so life expectancy is often near normal with supportive and rehabilitative care.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the neurological features and classifying the case as an isolated sideroblastic anaemia","Assuming significant iron overload requiring aggressive chelation when loading is typically mild","Confusing the static ataxia with a progressive neurodegenerative disorder","Failing to distinguish it genetically from ALAS2 and SLC25A38 sideroblastic anaemias"],"management":["Conservative support of the mild hypochromic-microcytic anaemia, which is rarely transfusion dependent","Careful avoidance of iron supplements and surveillance for usually mild iron loading","Rehabilitative physiotherapy and occupational therapy for the disabling but static cerebellar ataxia","Speech therapy and mobility aids as needed for incoordination and dysarthria","Regular neurological and haematological review","Genetic counselling with ABCB7 testing, excluding ALAS2 and SLC25A38"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_spinal_muscular_atrophy":{"name":"X-linked spinal muscular atrophy (UBA1)","system":"Nervous system / Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["X-linked infantile spinal muscular atrophy","XL-SMA","SMAX2","UBA1","spinal muscular atrophy with arthrogryposis"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Bradley and Daroff Neurology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare X-linked motor neurone disorder of infancy caused by variants in UBA1, the gene encoding the ubiquitin-activating enzyme E1","Affects male infants, who present with profound hypotonia, weakness, areflexia and often multiple congenital contractures (arthrogryposis)","Distinct from the common SMN1-related spinal muscular atrophy and negative on standard SMN1 testing, so a separate genetic diagnosis is required","Bone fractures and dysmorphic features may accompany the neuromuscular presentation","Electrophysiology shows a motor neuronopathy pattern with denervation on needle examination and reduced motor amplitudes, while sensory studies are preserved","The disorder implicates disturbed protein degradation via the ubiquitin-proteasome system in anterior horn cell loss","Diagnosis requires UBA1 sequencing in a male infant with an SMA-like picture and a compatible X-linked family history"],"pathophysiology":"X-linked spinal muscular atrophy results from variants in UBA1, which encodes the ubiquitin-activating enzyme E1 that initiates the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway responsible for targeted protein degradation. Impaired activation of ubiquitin disrupts protein homeostasis in motor neurones, leading to degeneration of anterior horn cells and consequent denervation of skeletal muscle. Because the gene lies on the X chromosome, hemizygous males are affected while carrier females are typically unaffected. Loss of lower motor neurones produces the hypotonia, weakness and areflexia, and antenatal weakness contributes to the congenital contractures; sensory pathways are spared, giving a pure motor electrophysiological picture.","additionalDifferentials":["SMN1-related spinal muscular atrophy type 1","Congenital hypomyelinating neuropathy","Congenital myopathies and congenital myasthenic syndromes","Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita of other cause","Congenital muscular dystrophy","Other rare non-5q spinal muscular atrophies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["SMN1 testing to exclude classic spinal muscular atrophy","UBA1 sequencing in male infants with an SMA-like phenotype","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography showing motor neuronopathy with preserved sensory responses","Skeletal survey for fractures and contractures","Respiratory and feeding assessment","Pedigree analysis for an X-linked inheritance pattern"],"redFlags":["Neonatal respiratory failure requiring urgent support","Feeding and swallowing difficulty with aspiration risk","Pathological fractures indicating bone fragility","Rapidly progressive weakness in early infancy"],"prognosis":"Generally severe, with significant early morbidity and frequently early respiratory compromise; outcome depends on the degree of weakness and respiratory involvement.","pitfalls":["Stopping at a negative SMN1 result and not pursuing UBA1 in an affected male","Attributing arthrogryposis to a primary joint or muscle disorder alone","Overlooking the X-linked pattern in the family history","Missing associated fractures and dysmorphism as diagnostic clues"],"management":["Proactive respiratory support with non-invasive or invasive ventilation and secretion clearance for early respiratory compromise","Nutritional support and safe feeding, with nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding where swallowing is unsafe","Orthopaedic and physiotherapy management of arthrogryposis and contractures, with attention to fracture risk","Physiotherapy and careful positioning to preserve function and prevent complications of immobility","Multidisciplinary neuromuscular care coordinating neurology, respiratory, orthopaedics and rehabilitation","Anticipatory and palliative care planning given the frequently severe prognosis","Genetic counselling with UBA1 testing after exclusion of SMN1-related spinal muscular atrophy, and carrier testing of the mother"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews: UBA1-Related X-Linked Spinal Muscular Atrophy","Bradley and Daroff's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_thrombocytopenia":{"name":"X-linked thrombocytopenia (WAS mild allelic variant)","system":"Haematology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WAS","XLT","X-linked thrombocytopenia","WASP","mild Wiskott-Aldrich spectrum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Hematology / Nathan and Oski","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A milder allelic variant of Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome caused by WAS gene mutations that retain some residual WASP function","The hallmark is microthrombocytopenia: a low platelet count with characteristically small platelets (reduced mean platelet volume)","Immunodeficiency and eczema are absent or mild compared with classic Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","Small platelet size on the film and by automated indices is a key diagnostic pointer distinguishing it from other inherited thrombocytopenias","Despite the milder phenotype, patients remain at risk of significant bleeding and can develop autoimmune complications over time","There is an increased long-term risk of autoimmunity and, less commonly, malignancy, so ongoing follow-up is required","Splenectomy raises platelet counts but increases infection risk and is used cautiously","Diagnosis is confirmed by WAS gene sequencing and assessment of WASP expression"],"pathophysiology":"The WAS gene encodes WASP, a cytoskeletal regulator expressed in haematopoietic cells that links signalling to actin polymerisation. Mutations preserving partial WASP function cause the milder X-linked thrombocytopenia phenotype, whereas null mutations produce classic Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome. Impaired cytoskeletal dynamics disturb platelet formation and lead to production of small, structurally abnormal platelets that are also removed prematurely, giving microthrombocytopenia. Residual immune-cell dysfunction predisposes to autoimmunity, and reduced immune surveillance contributes to a smaller malignancy risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Classic Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","Immune thrombocytopenia (acquired)","Other inherited thrombocytopenias with normal or large platelets","GATA1-related cytopenia","Congenital amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia","X-linked neutropenia from activating WAS mutations"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with mean platelet volume and film confirming small platelets","WAS gene sequencing and evaluation of WASP protein expression","Immunological assessment (immunoglobulins, lymphocyte subsets) to gauge any immune component","Bone marrow examination where the diagnosis is unclear","Autoimmune screen given the predisposition to autoimmunity","Assessment of potential related stem-cell donors when transplant is considered"],"redFlags":["Severe or life-threatening bleeding, including intracranial haemorrhage","Development of autoimmune cytopenias or other autoimmune disease","Features suggesting progression toward a more severe Wiskott-Aldrich phenotype or malignancy","Recurrent or unusual infections indicating significant immunodeficiency"],"prognosis":"The milder phenotype generally carries a better outlook than classic Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, but lifelong surveillance is needed because bleeding, autoimmunity, and a smaller malignancy risk persist; allogeneic transplantation is curative and considered in more severe cases.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing chronic immune thrombocytopenia and missing the small platelet size that signals a WAS variant","Assuming a benign course and omitting surveillance for autoimmunity and malignancy","Overlooking subtle immune dysfunction in an apparently isolated thrombocytopenia","Performing splenectomy without weighing the added infection risk in a WAS-spectrum disorder"],"management":["Platelet transfusion reserved for significant bleeding or surgical cover rather than routine use","Avoidance of antiplatelet drugs and other agents that worsen the bleeding tendency","Immunosuppressive treatment of autoimmune complications as they arise","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, which is curative and considered in more severe or complicated cases","Long-term surveillance for autoimmunity and for the smaller malignancy risk","Monitoring of platelet count and clinical bleeding, noting the characteristic small platelet size","Genetic counselling with WAS testing and assessment of potential related stem-cell donors"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Hematology","Nathan and Oski's Hematology and Oncology of Infancy and Childhood"],"crossLinks":[]},"x_linked_thrombocytopenia_thalassaemia":{"name":"X-linked thrombocytopenia with thalassaemia","system":"Haematological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XLTT","GATA1-related XLTT","GATA1 R216Q disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Haematology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["X-linked disorder caused by the GATA1 Arg216Gln (R216Q) missense mutation in the N-terminal zinc finger of the erythroid and megakaryocyte transcription factor GATA1.","Affected males have congenital thrombocytopenia with a bleeding tendency together with a thalassaemia-like blood picture (imbalanced globin synthesis, red cell HbH-type inclusions, reticulocytosis and splenomegaly).","It is distinct from the commoner GATA1 mutations (such as V205M) that impair FOG1 (ZFPM1) binding and cause X-linked thrombocytopenia with dyserythropoietic anaemia; the R216Q allele preserves FOG1 binding but disrupts DNA binding.","Platelets are reduced in number and are structurally and functionally abnormal, so bleeding can be out of proportion to the platelet count.","Carrier females are usually mildly affected or asymptomatic, depending on X-inactivation.","The thalassaemic component can be misread as beta-thalassaemia trait; its combination with thrombocytopenia in a male-line pedigree is the clue.","Diagnosis is confirmed by GATA1 sequencing, and the family history follows an X-linked pattern."],"pathophysiology":"X-linked thrombocytopenia with thalassaemia is caused by a specific missense mutation (Arg216Gln, R216Q) in GATA1 on the X chromosome, encoding the GATA1 transcription factor that is essential for erythroid and megakaryocytic differentiation. The substitution lies in the N-terminal zinc finger and selectively impairs binding to palindromic double-GATA DNA elements and to certain target promoters, while interaction with the cofactor FOG1 (ZFPM1) is comparatively preserved. Dysregulated globin and erythroid gene expression produces a beta-thalassaemia-like imbalance of globin chain synthesis with red cell inclusions and haemolysis, and defective megakaryopoiesis yields thrombocytopenia with structurally and functionally abnormal platelets. Other GATA1 mutations that instead disrupt FOG1 binding cause X-linked thrombocytopenia with dyserythropoietic anaemia, illustrating a genotype-phenotype correlation across the GATA1 disorders.","additionalDifferentials":["GATA1-related X-linked thrombocytopenia with dyserythropoietic anaemia","Other inherited thrombocytopenias (Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome and X-linked thrombocytopenia from WAS, MYH9-related disorder, ANKRD26-related)","Beta-thalassaemia trait or intermedia and HbH disease (alpha-thalassaemia)","Immune thrombocytopenia","Congenital dyserythropoietic anaemias","Unstable haemoglobin disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with platelet count and indices, and blood film for red cell morphology and inclusions","Reticulocyte count and markers of haemolysis","Supravital staining for HbH-type inclusions and haemoglobin analysis (HPLC or electrophoresis) with globin chain synthesis studies","Platelet function testing and platelet electron microscopy","GATA1 molecular testing","Bone marrow examination for dysmegakaryopoiesis and dyserythropoiesis if required","Family segregation analysis consistent with X-linked inheritance"],"redFlags":["Significant spontaneous, surgical or trauma-related bleeding","Rapidly falling platelet count or new severe anaemia","Splenic complications","Iron overload from recurrent transfusion"],"prognosis":"This is generally a chronic and relatively stable disorder; bleeding risk and anaemia are variable and usually manageable, but bleeding can be significant during surgery or trauma and some patients need transfusion support.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing isolated beta-thalassaemia trait and missing the associated thrombocytopenia and platelet dysfunction, or diagnosing immune thrombocytopenia and missing the inherited thalassaemic picture.","Assuming the platelet count predicts bleeding risk when the platelets are also dysfunctional.","Missing the X-linked inheritance pattern in the family history.","Confusing it with other GATA1 disorders that carry different counselling implications."],"management":["Supportive haematological care is the mainstay, as no drug corrects the GATA1 defect: transfuse packed red cells for symptomatic anaemia and platelets to cover bleeding, surgery or trauma","Anticipate bleeding out of proportion to the platelet count because platelets are structurally and functionally abnormal, and use antifibrinolytics such as tranexamic acid for mucosal bleeding and peri-procedural cover","Avoid antiplatelet drugs and NSAIDs, and give folic acid to support the chronic reticulocytosis and haemolysis","Monitor the thalassaemia-like haemolysis with periodic full blood count, reticulocytes, haemolytic markers and assessment of splenomegaly, and start iron chelation if transfusion-related iron overload develops","Consider splenectomy only selectively, weighing thrombotic and infection risks against transfusion burden","Refer to a specialist haematology or bleeding-disorder centre for coordinated care and peri-operative planning","Offer genetic counselling and testing for the X-linked GATA1 Arg216Gln variant, with carrier assessment of female relatives and reproductive options","Escalate promptly in major bleeding for combined platelet transfusion and antifibrinolytic support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Haematology reference texts","Inherited thrombocytopenia guidelines","Haemoglobinopathy and thalassaemia references"],"crossLinks":[]},"xanthelasma":{"name":"Xanthelasma","system":"Ophthalmic / integumentary (eyelid)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["xanthelasma palpebrarum","eyelid xanthoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Xanthelasma palpebrarum presents as soft, symmetrical, yellowish plaques on the eyelid skin, most often at the medial canthus of the upper lids, and is the commonest cutaneous xanthoma.","Roughly half of affected patients have normal serum lipids, so a normolipidaemic result does not exclude the diagnosis, but a fasting lipid profile should still be checked in all cases.","When associated with dyslipidaemia it typically reflects raised low-density lipoprotein or a reduced high-density lipoprotein, and may be a clue to familial hypercholesterolaemia, especially with young onset or a family history.","Xanthelasma has been linked to increased cardiovascular risk independent of measured lipids, so its presence warrants overall cardiovascular risk assessment, not merely a cosmetic view.","The lesions are benign and mainly a cosmetic concern; treatment options include surgical excision, chemical cautery, ablative laser and cryotherapy, all with a notable tendency to recur.","Recurrence after treatment is common, particularly in patients with persistent hyperlipidaemia, so lipid control and patient counselling about recurrence are integral to management.","Consider secondary causes of hyperlipidaemia such as diabetes, hypothyroidism, cholestatic liver disease and nephrotic syndrome when xanthelasma is florid or of early onset.","Eruptive or tuberous xanthomas elsewhere, or extensive early xanthelasma, should prompt evaluation for a primary lipid disorder."],"pathophysiology":"Xanthelasma results from accumulation of lipid-laden macrophages (foam cells) in the superficial dermis of the thin eyelid skin. Circulating lipoproteins, particularly low-density lipoprotein, extravasate through leaky periocular capillaries and are taken up by dermal macrophages, which become engorged with cholesterol and cholesteryl esters to form perivascular and periadnexal foam-cell aggregates. Local capillary permeability, oxidative modification of lipoproteins and macrophage scavenger-receptor activity all contribute, which explains why lesions can develop even in normolipidaemic individuals in whom local rather than systemic factors predominate.","additionalDifferentials":["Necrobiotic xanthogranuloma (often with periorbital induration and paraproteinaemia)","Syringoma of the eyelid","Sebaceous hyperplasia or sebaceous gland carcinoma","Adult-onset asthma and periocular xanthogranulomatous disease (Erdheim-Chester, adult xanthogranuloma)","Amyloid deposits of the eyelid","Juvenile xanthogranuloma","Lipoid proteinosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting serum lipid profile (total cholesterol, LDL, HDL, triglycerides)","Fasting glucose or HbA1c to screen for diabetes","Thyroid function tests to exclude hypothyroidism","Liver function tests to exclude cholestatic liver disease","Assessment for familial hypercholesterolaemia including family history and, where indicated, genetic evaluation","Overall cardiovascular risk stratification","Skin biopsy only if the diagnosis is atypical or xanthogranulomatous disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["Early-onset or florid lesions suggesting familial hypercholesterolaemia","Associated periorbital induration, ulceration or systemic features suggesting necrobiotic xanthogranuloma and paraproteinaemia","Rapidly enlarging, firm or ulcerated eyelid lesion raising concern for sebaceous carcinoma","Features of undiagnosed diabetes, hypothyroidism or cholestasis on systemic enquiry"],"prognosis":"Benign with no direct threat to vision; the main significance is as a marker of possible dyslipidaemia and cardiovascular risk. Cosmetic treatment is effective but recurrence is frequent, particularly when hyperlipidaemia is uncontrolled.","pitfalls":["Assuming normal lipids exclude the diagnosis or obviate cardiovascular risk assessment","Treating the lesions cosmetically without addressing underlying dyslipidaemia, leading to recurrence","Mislabelling necrobiotic xanthogranuloma or sebaceous carcinoma as simple xanthelasma","Aggressive excision of medial canthal lesions risking cicatricial ectropion or canthal webbing","Neglecting to screen for secondary causes such as hypothyroidism and cholestasis"],"management":["Identify and treat any underlying dyslipidaemia with statin therapy, adding ezetimibe or other lipid-lowering agents as needed to lower LDL cholesterol","Give dietary and lifestyle advice, including reduced saturated fat, weight management and smoking cessation, as adjuncts to lipid control","Screen for and manage secondary causes such as hypothyroidism, diabetes and cholestatic liver disease","Offer cosmetic removal for troublesome lesions by surgical excision, chemical cautery with trichloroacetic acid, ablative laser or cryotherapy, counselling that recurrence is common","Undertake overall cardiovascular risk assessment, since xanthelasma signals raised cardiovascular risk independent of measured lipids","Evaluate for familial hypercholesterolaemia, particularly with young onset or a positive family history, and arrange cascade or genetic screening where indicated","Reinforce sustained lipid control after any procedure, as recurrence is frequent when hyperlipidaemia is uncontrolled"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology: A Systematic Approach","American Academy of Ophthalmology, Basic and Clinical Science Course — Oculofacial Plastic and Orbital Surgery","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"xanthogranulomatous_pyelonephritis":{"name":"Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis","system":"Renal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XGP","XGPN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic destructive granulomatous infection of the kidney in which lipid-laden foamy macrophages progressively replace the parenchyma.","It is strongly associated with chronic obstruction, typically a staghorn calculus, and recurrent infection.","Proteus mirabilis and Escherichia coli are the commonest organisms.","It is usually unilateral and presents with flank pain, fever, malaise, weight loss, a palpable mass and recurrent urinary infections.","The classic bear-paw sign on CT shows an enlarged non-functioning kidney with dilated calyces around a central calculus.","It mimics renal cell carcinoma radiologically and histologically, so the diagnosis is often confirmed only after nephrectomy.","It may form fistulae (nephrocutaneous or reno-colic) and involve the perinephric space and psoas muscle.","Definitive treatment is usually total nephrectomy, with partial nephrectomy occasionally suitable for focal disease."],"pathophysiology":"Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis is a chronic granulomatous inflammatory response to long-standing renal infection in the setting of obstruction, typically a calculus, and impaired urinary drainage. Persistent bacterial infection with incomplete clearance provokes accumulation of lipid-laden foamy macrophages (xanthoma cells) mixed with other inflammatory cells, which progressively destroy and replace the renal parenchyma. The process may extend beyond the capsule into perinephric fat, the psoas muscle and adjacent organs and can generate fistulae, mimicking malignant infiltration both radiologically and macroscopically.","additionalDifferentials":["Renal cell carcinoma","Renal tuberculosis","Pyonephrosis or renal abscess","Malakoplakia","Emphysematous pyelonephritis","Renal lymphoma","Obstructive uropathy with chronic pyelonephritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast CT showing the bear-paw sign, staghorn calculus and a non-functioning kidney","Urine microscopy, culture and sensitivities","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers","Full blood count (anaemia, leucocytosis) and renal function","DMSA or MAG3 renography for differential renal function","Imaging for fistulae and perinephric extension","Histology of the nephrectomy specimen (definitive, showing foam cells)"],"redFlags":["Sepsis from an obstructed infected kidney (pyonephrosis)","Fistula formation (reno-colic or nephrocutaneous)","Perinephric or psoas abscess","Diagnostic uncertainty with possible renal malignancy"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent after complete surgical removal of the affected kidney, with low recurrence; morbidity relates chiefly to the extent of local invasion, fistulae and perioperative complications.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the condition as renal cell carcinoma, or vice versa","Underestimating perinephric extension that complicates surgery","Attempting nephron-sparing surgery in diffuse disease","Inadequate perioperative treatment of sepsis and obstruction"],"management":["Nephrectomy, usually total removal of the non-functioning kidney together with any staghorn calculus, is the definitive treatment","Give targeted antibiotics guided by urine and blood cultures, covering Proteus mirabilis and Escherichia coli, to control infection before and after surgery","Relieve obstruction and drain any perinephric collection or abscess, with pre-operative optimisation of sepsis and correction of anaemia and nutritional deficits","Confirm the non-functioning kidney with DMSA or MAG3 renography and safeguard the function of the contralateral kidney","Manage local extension into perinephric fat, psoas or adjacent organs and any fistulae at operation, with wider resection if renal cell carcinoma cannot be excluded","Coordinate urology, interventional radiology and microbiology in a multidisciplinary approach","Escalate emergently in urosepsis with resuscitation, source control and broad-spectrum antimicrobials pending cultures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","Oxford Textbook of Medicine","Comprehensive Clinical Nephrology"],"crossLinks":[]},"xanthoma_disseminatum":{"name":"Xanthoma disseminatum","system":"Skin / Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Montgomery syndrome","disseminated xanthosiderohistiocytosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare normolipaemic non-Langerhans cell histiocytosis characterised by hundreds of red-brown to yellow papules and nodules with a striking predilection for flexural and intertriginous skin.","Lesions cluster symmetrically in the axillae, groin, antecubital and popliteal fossae and periorbital region, and mucous membrane involvement of the upper aerodigestive tract may occur.","Mucosal disease of the oral cavity, pharynx, larynx and trachea can cause hoarseness, dysphagia or airway obstruction and is an important cause of morbidity.","Up to a third to a half of patients develop transient central diabetes insipidus from infiltration of the hypothalamic-pituitary region, which is usually mild and may remit.","Serum lipids are characteristically normal, distinguishing it from the hyperlipidaemic xanthomas it can mimic clinically.","It affects predominantly young adult males and typically follows a persistent, progressive course rather than the spontaneous involution seen in juvenile xanthogranuloma.","Histology shows a mixed infiltrate of foamy histiocytes, scattered Touton giant cells and inflammatory cells; cells are CD68 positive and CD1a negative.","An association with monoclonal gammopathy and other paraproteinaemias is reported, warranting haematological screening."],"pathophysiology":"Xanthoma disseminatum is a proliferative disorder of macrophage-derived histiocytes belonging to the non-Langerhans cell histiocytoses. Lesional histiocytes accumulate lipid to form foam cells and Touton giant cells despite normal circulating lipids, indicating that lipid uptake and storage occur locally within a reactive histiocytic infiltrate rather than as a consequence of dyslipidaemia. The cells express macrophage markers such as CD68 and factor XIIIa but not the Langerhans cell markers CD1a and langerin. The disorder is generally regarded as a reactive rather than clearly neoplastic process, although its persistence, mucosal and pituitary infiltration, and reported associations with monoclonal gammopathy suggest a dysregulated histiocytic response. Infiltration of the hypothalamic-pituitary axis interrupts vasopressin delivery to produce central diabetes insipidus, while mucosal deposits in the airway account for the potential for obstructive complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Eruptive xanthomas of hyperlipidaemia","Generalised eruptive histiocytosis","Langerhans cell histiocytosis","Multicentric reticulohistiocytosis","Erdheim-Chester disease","Progressive nodular histiocytosis","Sarcoidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with histology and immunohistochemistry (CD68, factor XIIIa, CD1a, langerin)","Fasting serum lipid profile to confirm normolipaemia","Early-morning paired serum and urine osmolality and water-deprivation testing if diabetes insipidus suspected","MRI of the hypothalamic-pituitary region when endocrine features are present","Laryngoscopy or airway endoscopy if hoarseness, dysphagia or stridor","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation to detect monoclonal gammopathy","Baseline anterior pituitary hormone profile"],"redFlags":["Hoarseness, dysphagia or stridor indicating laryngeal or tracheal mucosal involvement and potential airway compromise","Polyuria and polydipsia suggesting central diabetes insipidus","Visual disturbance or ophthalmoplegia from periorbital or orbital infiltration","Rapidly progressive or widespread cutaneous and mucosal disease","Detection of a monoclonal paraprotein on electrophoresis"],"prognosis":"The cutaneous disease is typically persistent and only rarely regresses, following a chronic course; overall prognosis depends on the extent of mucosal, airway and pituitary involvement, and although diabetes insipidus is often transient, airway disease can be life-threatening.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the yellowish papules as eruptive xanthomas and pursuing lipid-lowering treatment despite normal serum lipids.","Failing to enquire about polyuria and polydipsia and thereby missing central diabetes insipidus.","Overlooking upper airway mucosal disease that can progress to obstruction.","Not screening for an associated monoclonal gammopathy.","Expecting spontaneous involution as occurs in juvenile xanthogranuloma, leading to under-monitoring."],"management":["Treat symptomatic central diabetes insipidus from hypothalamic-pituitary infiltration with desmopressin, recognising it is often transient and may remit","Secure and monitor the airway when mucosal disease of the pharynx, larynx or trachea threatens obstruction, with endoscopic debulking or tracheostomy as needed","Offer local treatment of troublesome cutaneous lesions by surgical excision, ablative carbon-dioxide laser or radiotherapy, accepting a tendency to recur","Consider systemic immunomodulatory or single-agent chemotherapy such as cladribine for extensive, progressive or organ-threatening disease","Recognise that lipid-lowering therapy is generally unhelpful because serum lipids are normal; treatment targets the histiocytic infiltrate rather than lipids","Monitor for evolving mucosal, airway and endocrine involvement, and screen for an associated monoclonal gammopathy with serum and urine protein electrophoresis","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across dermatology, haematology, ENT and endocrinology given the multisystem, persistent course"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology (current edition)","WHO Classification of Histiocytoses and revised histiocytosis classification (current)","Standard dermatology reference texts (current editions)"],"crossLinks":[]},"xeroderma_pigmentosum":{"name":"Xeroderma pigmentosum","system":"Dermatology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XP","nucleotide excision repair defect","XPA","XPC","POLH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder of DNA repair, caused by variants in nucleotide excision repair genes (complementation groups XPA-XPG) or in POLH (XP variant)","Defective repair of ultraviolet-induced DNA damage causes extreme photosensitivity and a very high risk of skin cancers at a young age","Early signs include severe sunburn from minimal exposure and, later, freckling and poikilodermatous change on sun-exposed skin in early childhood","Cutaneous squamous and basal cell carcinomas and melanoma arise decades earlier than in the general population","Ocular involvement is prominent: photophobia, conjunctivitis, keratitis, and periocular tumours","Some complementation groups develop progressive neurological degeneration (the neurological form)","Rigorous, lifelong photoprotection and surveillance are the cornerstone of management"],"pathophysiology":"Xeroderma pigmentosum is caused by inherited defects in the nucleotide excision repair pathway, which normally recognises and removes bulky DNA lesions such as the cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers produced by ultraviolet radiation; the XP variant form instead reflects loss of DNA polymerase eta, needed for accurate translesion synthesis. Because damaged DNA cannot be repaired efficiently, ultraviolet-induced mutations accumulate in keratinocytes, melanocytes, and ocular surface cells. This drives premature photoageing, pigmentary change, and early malignant transformation. In certain genotypes, accumulated unrepaired damage in neurons produces progressive neurodegeneration independent of sun exposure.","additionalDifferentials":["Polymorphic light eruption and other photodermatoses","Cockayne syndrome and trichothiodystrophy (related repair disorders)","Erythropoietic protoporphyria","Rothmund-Thomson syndrome and Bloom syndrome (photosensitive genodermatoses)","Carney complex or familial multiple naevi in early skin cancer presentations"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular genetic testing of XP complementation group genes and POLH","Cellular assays of DNA repair capacity (unscheduled DNA synthesis) where available","Skin biopsy of suspicious lesions for early tumour detection","Regular full-skin and ophthalmological surveillance examinations","Neurological assessment (including audiometry) in genotypes prone to degeneration","Ultraviolet exposure and minimal erythema history to gauge photosensitivity"],"redFlags":["New, changing, or non-healing skin lesion requiring urgent biopsy for malignancy","Ocular surface deterioration, corneal scarring, or periocular tumour","Progressive neurological decline (hearing loss, ataxia, cognitive regression)","Evidence of inadequate photoprotection with escalating actinic damage"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy is reduced, largely from early and recurrent skin and ocular malignancies and, in neurological subtypes, from progressive neurodegeneration; with meticulous photoprotection and close cancer surveillance, tumour burden and its consequences can be substantially reduced.","pitfalls":["Attributing childhood 'freckling' and severe sunburns to ordinary sun sensitivity and missing the diagnosis","Under-recognising the lifelong intensity of photoprotection required, including indoor ultraviolet sources","Neglecting ophthalmic and neurological surveillance in favour of skin monitoring alone","Delaying biopsy of new lesions given the accelerated pace of malignant change"],"management":["Institute rigorous lifelong ultraviolet protection as the cornerstone of care: sun avoidance, protective clothing, UV-filtering eyewear and window films, broad-spectrum sunscreen and indoor UV shielding","Undertake frequent full-skin and ophthalmological surveillance to detect and excise cutaneous squamous and basal cell carcinomas, melanoma and ocular surface tumours early","Treat established skin malignancies with surgical excision, and consider oral retinoids as chemoprophylaxis in patients with a heavy tumour burden","Manage ocular surface disease with lubricants and protection against photokeratitis, referring for corneal or lid surgery when required","Supplement vitamin D, since strict sun avoidance predisposes to deficiency","Monitor genotypes prone to neurodegeneration with neurological review and audiometry, providing supportive and rehabilitative care","Offer genetic counselling and testing for the autosomal recessive nucleotide-excision-repair (XPA-XPG) or POLH variants, with carrier testing and reproductive options","Coordinate multidisciplinary care across dermatology, ophthalmology, clinical genetics, neurology and oncology, and avoid tobacco smoke and other DNA-damaging exposures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","Fitzpatrick's Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"xq28_duplication_mecp2":{"name":"MECP2 duplication syndrome (Xq28)","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Xq28 duplication syndrome","MECP2 duplication","dup(X)(q28) involving MECP2","Lubs X-linked mental retardation syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked duplication of the Xq28 region that includes MECP2, affecting males, while carrier females are usually unaffected because of skewed X-inactivation","Infantile hypotonia, feeding difficulty and delayed motor milestones with absent or very limited speech","Recurrent respiratory and other infections, reflecting associated immune dysfunction, are a major cause of morbidity and early death","Progressive neurological deterioration with developmental regression, worsening spasticity and, in many, epilepsy","Autistic features and severe to profound intellectual disability are characteristic","Represents a gain-of-function dosage effect of MECP2, in contrast to the loss-of-function of the same gene that causes Rett syndrome in girls","The rearrangement is typically inherited from an unaffected carrier mother, with important recurrence-risk implications for male relatives","Chromosomal microarray is the first-line diagnostic test and defines the duplication extent"],"pathophysiology":"MECP2 duplication syndrome results from a gain of an extra copy of the MECP2 gene at Xq28, usually within a larger duplication of variable size; some rearrangements are recurrent and mediated by non-allelic homologous recombination between segmental duplications in this repeat-rich region. MECP2 encodes a methyl-CpG-binding protein that regulates transcription of many neuronal genes, and both excess and deficiency of the protein disrupt neurodevelopment. Overexpression produces progressive neurological dysfunction, hypotonia evolving to spasticity, and an immune phenotype predisposing to recurrent infection. Because the gene is X-linked, males with a single X are affected, whereas carrier females with favourably skewed X-inactivation are typically spared.","additionalDifferentials":["Rett syndrome and other MECP2 loss-of-function disorders (chiefly in females)","Other X-linked intellectual disability syndromes","Cerebral palsy and other causes of progressive spasticity with developmental delay","Mitochondrial and neurometabolic disorders presenting with regression","Primary immunodeficiency presenting with recurrent infection and developmental delay","Other causes of infantile hypotonia with global developmental delay"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chromosomal microarray as first-line to detect and size the Xq28 duplication","FISH or MLPA to confirm the duplication and to test the mother for carrier status","Maternal and family studies to identify carriers and counsel recurrence risk in males","Immunological assessment given the susceptibility to recurrent infection","EEG and brain MRI where seizures or regression are present","Respiratory, swallowing and developmental assessment with surveillance for aspiration"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or severe respiratory infection with risk of respiratory failure","New or worsening seizures","Developmental regression or loss of previously acquired skills","Aspiration or feeding difficulty threatening nutrition and airway safety"],"prognosis":"The condition is generally severe and progressive, with profound intellectual disability, loss of independent mobility and a significant risk of premature death, frequently from recurrent respiratory infection. Attentive respiratory care, infection prevention, seizure management and nutritional support can improve comfort and reduce complications, but the overall outlook remains guarded.","pitfalls":["Failing to test the mother and female relatives for carrier status, given the important recurrence risk for future sons","Overlooking the immune dysfunction and underestimating the risk of severe recurrent infection","Confusing the gain-of-function duplication with the loss-of-function mutations that cause Rett syndrome","Attributing regression to a static encephalopathy and missing the progressive nature of the disorder"],"management":["Prioritise aggressive respiratory care and infection prevention with prompt antibiotics, immunisation, airway clearance and consideration of immunoglobulin replacement or antibiotic prophylaxis, given the immune dysfunction that drives early mortality","Manage aspiration risk with swallowing assessment, modified feeding and gastrostomy where needed to secure nutrition","Treat epilepsy with antiepileptic drugs and manage progressive spasticity with antispasticity agents, physiotherapy and orthotics","Provide developmental, physiotherapy, occupational and speech-language support for the profound intellectual disability and motor impairment","Monitor for progressive neurological deterioration, recurrent infection and nutritional and respiratory decline with regular review","Offer genetic counselling, maternal carrier testing and recurrence-risk advice for this X-linked duplication affecting males","Coordinate multidisciplinary and palliative-minded care across neurology, respiratory medicine, immunology and community teams"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews (University of Washington, Seattle)","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","Emery and Rimoin's Principles and Practice of Medical Genetics"],"crossLinks":[]},"xxyy_syndrome":{"name":"48,XXYY syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["48,XXYY","XXYY syndrome","XXYY variant of Klinefelter syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Regarded as part of the Klinefelter spectrum but with a more pronounced neurodevelopmental and behavioural profile, sometimes termed Klinefelter-plus","Tall stature, often above average adult male height, with eunuchoid body proportions","Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism with small testes, low testosterone, infertility, and frequently gynaecomastia, so testosterone supplementation is often required from adolescence","Intellectual ability is usually in the low-average to mildly impaired range, lower than in 47,XXY","High rates of attention-deficit/hyperactivity, autism spectrum traits, anxiety, mood dysregulation, and other behavioural difficulties","Increased risk of tremor, seizures, dental problems, deep-vein thrombosis and peripheral vascular disease, and congenital malformations","Arises from sequential non-disjunction during paternal gametogenesis, adding both an X and a Y chromosome"],"pathophysiology":"48,XXYY results from the presence of two extra sex chromosomes, one additional X and one additional Y, in a male, arising through sequential non-disjunction during paternal gametogenesis. As in Klinefelter syndrome, testicular dysgenesis produces hypergonadotropic hypogonadism, while the extra X, with genes escaping inactivation, and the extra Y increase gene dosage that affects growth and neurodevelopment more markedly than a single extra sex chromosome. The greater chromosomal imbalance underlies the more pronounced cognitive, behavioural, and physical features compared with 47,XXY.","additionalDifferentials":["Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY)","Higher-grade sex-chromosome polysomies such as 48,XXXY and 49,XXXXY","47,XYY syndrome","Fragile X syndrome, for tall stature, behaviour, and learning difficulty","Non-syndromic hypogonadism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype, which is diagnostic","Chromosomal microarray","Gonadotrophins and testosterone from adolescence to assess for hypogonadism","Developmental, cognitive, and behavioural assessment","Screening for thrombotic and vascular problems where indicated","Dental review, and EEG if seizures occur"],"redFlags":["Symptoms of deep-vein thrombosis or peripheral vascular disease","Seizures","Significant mental-health disturbance or a behavioural crisis"],"prognosis":"Lifespan is generally near-normal but influenced by vascular and thrombotic and other complications; most individuals require lifelong endocrine, educational, and behavioural support, and infertility is the rule.","pitfalls":["Labelling the condition as simple Klinefelter syndrome and underestimating the neurodevelopmental needs","Overlooking the raised thrombotic and vascular risk","Delaying assessment and replacement of testosterone in adolescence","Attributing behavioural difficulties to environment alone"],"management":["Commence testosterone replacement from adolescence for the hypergonadotropic hypogonadism to support virilisation, bone health and wellbeing","Provide neurodevelopmental, educational and behavioural support, treating co-existing ADHD, autism spectrum traits, anxiety and mood dysregulation with behavioural therapy and medication as indicated","Offer speech and language, occupational and physiotherapy for developmental delay and motor difficulties","Monitor for and manage tremor and seizures, arranging EEG and neurology input where seizures occur","Assess and reduce thrombotic and peripheral vascular risk, with thromboprophylaxis during high-risk periods such as surgery","Provide regular dental review and manage gynaecomastia, with surgery if troublesome","Counsel about infertility and refer to reproductive services, and offer genetic counselling explaining the sporadic paternal-origin sex-chromosome aneuploidy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"xyy_syndrome":{"name":"47,XYY syndrome","system":"Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["XYY syndrome","Jacobs syndrome","47,XYY","YY syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"GeneReviews / Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Tall stature is the most consistent feature, often with accelerated growth during childhood","Physical appearance and fertility are usually normal, and many individuals remain undiagnosed","There is an increased risk of speech and language delay and of learning difficulties, especially with reading","Higher rates of attention-deficit/hyperactivity, autism spectrum traits, and emotional or behavioural difficulties occur, but the historical link with aggression is outdated and stigmatising","Motor coordination difficulties and childhood hypotonia are common","Arises from a paternal second-division meiotic error producing a YY sperm, with no maternal-age effect","IQ is usually within the normal range but on average slightly lower than that of siblings"],"pathophysiology":"47,XYY arises when an error at the second paternal meiotic division produces a sperm carrying two Y chromosomes, giving a male with an extra Y. Additional dosage of Y-linked and pseudoautosomal genes, including growth-related genes in the short-stature homeobox region, is thought to underlie the tall stature, while effects on neurodevelopment produce the associated language, learning, and behavioural profile. Expressivity is very variable and many individuals are only mildly affected.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial or constitutional tall stature","Other sex-chromosome aneuploidies such as 47,XXY and 48,XXYY","Idiopathic attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder or autism spectrum disorder","Developmental language disorder","Marfan syndrome, for tall stature"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype, which is diagnostic","Chromosomal microarray, which detects the extra Y","Developmental, speech and language, and educational assessment","Assessment for attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder or autism spectrum disorder where indicated","Growth monitoring"],"redFlags":["Significant behavioural or mental-health disturbance needing support","Marked developmental delay suggesting an additional diagnosis","Emotional distress in the individual or family after diagnosis, needing counselling"],"prognosis":"Good, with a normal lifespan and usually normal fertility; most individuals lead independent lives, and neurodevelopmental and educational outcomes improve with early support.","pitfalls":["Perpetuating the discredited and stigmatising link with criminality or aggression","Assuming uniform severity when the phenotype is highly variable","Overlooking treatable learning and attention difficulties","Causing undue alarm after an incidental prenatal diagnosis"],"management":["Provide early developmental intervention with speech and language therapy for language delay and educational support for learning difficulties, particularly reading","Screen for and manage ADHD, autism spectrum traits and emotional or behavioural difficulties with behavioural strategies and medication where indicated","Offer occupational therapy and physiotherapy for motor coordination difficulties and childhood hypotonia","Monitor growth given the tall stature, providing reassurance that physical development and fertility are usually normal","Give family counselling that dispels the outdated and stigmatising link with aggression and explains the sporadic paternal meiotic origin with no maternal-age effect and low recurrence risk","Arrange periodic developmental and educational review, since outcomes improve with early support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["GeneReviews","Smith's Recognizable Patterns of Human Malformation"],"crossLinks":[]},"yao_syndrome":{"name":"Yao syndrome","system":"Immunology / Autoinflammatory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NOD2-associated autoinflammatory disease","formerly NOD2-associated autoinflammatory syndrome","NOD2","nucleotide-binding oligomerisation domain 2 disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Textbook of Autoinflammation / Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An adult-onset periodic fever syndrome associated with specific NOD2 gene variants, distinct from Blau syndrome and Crohn disease","Presents with recurrent fevers, a patchy or urticaria-like rash, and episodes lasting several days to a week","Common features include arthralgia or arthritis, gastrointestinal symptoms, and sicca-like complaints","Attacks are recurrent rather than continuous, with symptom-free intervals between episodes","Diagnosis is clinical with supportive NOD2 genotyping and exclusion of infection, autoimmune, and other autoinflammatory diseases","It is more frequently reported in adult women of European ancestry","Management uses anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory therapy, with variable response; some patients benefit from interleukin-1 or interleukin-6 directed treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Yao syndrome is linked to variants in NOD2, an intracellular sensor of bacterial cell-wall components that regulates inflammatory signalling. Unlike the specific NOD2 mutations causing Blau syndrome, the variants in Yao syndrome are thought to dysregulate innate immune signalling and cytokine production, producing periodic systemic inflammation. This drives recurrent fevers, dermatitis, arthralgia, and gastrointestinal symptoms without the granulomatous triad of Blau syndrome. The condition is regarded as a polygenically influenced autoinflammatory disease rather than a simple single-gene disorder, which contributes to its variable presentation and treatment response.","additionalDifferentials":["Adult-onset Still disease","Familial Mediterranean fever and other hereditary periodic fevers","Blau syndrome / early-onset sarcoidosis","Crohn disease","Schnitzler syndrome","Chronic urticaria with systemic features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["NOD2 genotyping for associated variants","Acute-phase reactants during and between episodes","Autoantibody and infection screens to exclude alternative diagnoses","Skin biopsy of the rash where diagnosis is unclear","Gastrointestinal evaluation if bowel symptoms predominate","Assessment for sicca symptoms and salivary gland involvement"],"redFlags":["Persistent rather than episodic inflammation suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Features of inflammatory bowel disease requiring separate evaluation","Development of amyloidosis from chronic uncontrolled inflammation","Systemic features raising concern for malignancy or infection"],"prognosis":"Yao syndrome typically follows a relapsing course with good overall life expectancy; symptoms can often be controlled with anti-inflammatory therapy, though responses vary and some patients have persistent troublesome attacks.","pitfalls":["Equating any NOD2 variant with Blau syndrome or Crohn disease","Diagnosing on genotype alone without a compatible clinical picture and exclusion of mimics","Missing the episodic nature by assessing patients only during quiescent intervals","Overlooking treatable alternative causes of periodic fever"],"management":["Control acute flares with short courses of glucocorticoids to suppress the systemic inflammation","Use maintenance anti-inflammatory therapy such as colchicine or sulfasalazine to reduce attack frequency","Escalate refractory disease to biologic therapy, particularly interleukin-1 inhibition with anakinra or canakinumab, and consider interleukin-6 blockade","Manage arthralgia or arthritis and the gastrointestinal and sicca-like symptoms symptomatically","Exclude infection, autoimmune and other autoinflammatory diseases before and during treatment, supporting the diagnosis with NOD2 genotyping","Monitor acute-phase reactants during and between episodes to gauge disease activity and treatment response","Coordinate care between rheumatology and clinical immunology for this relapsing condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Textbook of Autoinflammation","Firestein & Kelley's Textbook of Rheumatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"yaws":{"name":"Yaws","system":"Infectious disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Treponema pallidum subspecies pertenue","framboesia tropica","pian","bouba"],"chapter":null,"source":"Manson's Tropical Diseases / Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Yaws is a chronic non-venereal (endemic) treponematosis caused by Treponema pallidum subspecies pertenue, affecting mainly children in warm, humid rural areas of West Africa, Southeast Asia and the Pacific","Transmission is by direct skin-to-skin contact with lesion exudate, not sexually, and is favoured by crowding, poor hygiene and minor skin trauma","The primary lesion ('mother yaw' or framboesioma) is a painless papilloma or raspberry-like papillomatous nodule at the inoculation site, often on the leg","Secondary yaws follows with multiple similar skin lesions, along with painful hyperkeratotic palmar/plantar lesions ('crab yaws') and osteoperiostitis of long bones and fingers (dactylitis)","Late (tertiary) yaws causes destructive gummatous lesions of skin and bone, including gangosa (destruction of nose and palate) and sabre tibia","Serological tests are the same non-treponemal and treponemal assays used for venereal syphilis and cannot distinguish the two; dark-field microscopy shows treponemes indistinguishable from T. pallidum","Treatment for the endemic treponematoses uses benzathine penicillin, with a macrolide alternative used in mass-treatment eradication campaigns"],"pathophysiology":"Treponema pallidum subspecies pertenue enters through breaks in the skin after contact with an infectious lesion. It multiplies locally to form the primary papillomatous lesion, then disseminates via blood and lymphatics to produce widespread secondary skin and bone lesions after weeks to months. A latent phase follows, during which relapsing secondary lesions may occur. In a minority, years later, destructive tertiary gummatous lesions develop in skin, bone and cartilage. Unlike venereal syphilis, yaws does not cause congenital infection or significant cardiovascular or neurological disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Venereal syphilis (serologically identical)","Other endemic treponematoses (bejel, pinta)","Cutaneous leishmaniasis","Impetigo and other pyodermas","Tropical ulcer","Verrucous fungal infection (e.g. chromoblastomycosis)","Warts or condylomata"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dark-field microscopy of lesion exudate for spirochaetes","Non-treponemal serology (e.g. RPR/VDRL) for activity and treatment response","Treponemal serology (e.g. TPPA/TPHA) for confirmation","PCR of lesion material where available, which can help distinguish subspecies","Radiographs of affected bones for periostitis or destructive change","Clinical and epidemiological assessment of contacts in endemic communities"],"redFlags":["Destructive facial lesions (gangosa) causing disfigurement","Extensive bone involvement with deformity or disability","Painful crab yaws impairing mobility","Uncertainty distinguishing from venereal syphilis where the latter has serious sequelae","Treatment failure or reappearance of lesions after therapy"],"prognosis":"Early and secondary yaws heal completely with penicillin and carry an excellent prognosis, but untreated late disease can cause permanent, disfiguring destruction of skin, bone and cartilage; the disease is a target for global eradication.","pitfalls":["Assuming positive treponemal serology indicates venereal syphilis and mislabelling children accordingly","Failing to treat close contacts, allowing continued transmission","Overlooking early painless primary lesions and missing the diagnosis","Confusing yaws with pinta or bejel without regional and clinical context","Not recognising that mass treatment strategy differs from individual management"],"management":["Cure with antibiotics: a single oral dose of azithromycin, or intramuscular benzathine benzylpenicillin as an alternative","Provide local wound care of skin lesions and manage the osteoperiostitis and dactylitis of bone involvement","Treat contacts and, in endemic communities, apply total community or mass treatment with azithromycin as part of the eradication strategy","Monitor cure and detect relapse or reinfection with serial non-treponemal (RPR or VDRL) titres","Manage late destructive gummatous lesions such as gangosa and sabre tibia with wound care and reconstructive or orthopaedic input","Undertake public health measures including case finding, contact tracing and hygiene promotion within the global eradication programme","Escalate in penicillin allergy or apparent treatment failure with alternative regimens and re-evaluation for reinfection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Manson's Tropical Diseases","Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"],"crossLinks":[]},"yellow_fever":{"name":"Yellow fever","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["YF","yellow fever virus infection","yellow jack"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mosquito-borne flaviviral haemorrhagic fever endemic to parts of sub-Saharan Africa and tropical South America, transmitted by Aedes and forest mosquitoes","India has no endemic yellow fever, but requires proof of vaccination from travellers arriving from endemic areas because the competent Aedes vector is present, posing an introduction risk","Illness is often biphasic: an initial febrile phase with headache and myalgia, sometimes followed after a brief remission by a toxic phase with jaundice, haemorrhage and organ failure","The toxic phase is characterised by hepatic and renal injury, coagulopathy and bleeding, and carries high mortality","A relative bradycardia despite high fever (Faget sign) is a described clinical clue","There is no specific antiviral treatment; care is supportive with management of bleeding, liver and kidney failure","A single dose of the live attenuated vaccine confers durable, likely lifelong immunity and is the cornerstone of prevention and international travel requirements","Diagnosis uses RT-PCR in early illness and serology (IgM), with care to distinguish from other flaviviruses and viral haemorrhagic fevers"],"pathophysiology":"Yellow fever virus, an RNA flavivirus, is inoculated by an infected mosquito and spreads via lymphatics and blood to target organs, with marked hepatotropism. Infection and death of hepatocytes (with characteristic midzonal necrosis and Councilman bodies) impair synthesis of clotting factors and cause jaundice, while direct renal injury produces acute kidney injury. The combination of coagulopathy, thrombocytopenia and vascular injury drives the haemorrhagic manifestations, and a dysregulated inflammatory response contributes to shock and multi-organ failure in the toxic phase.","additionalDifferentials":["Other viral haemorrhagic fevers (e.g. Ebola, Lassa, Marburg)","Severe malaria","Leptospirosis (Weil disease)","Viral hepatitis with fulminant course","Severe dengue","Rickettsial infection"],"infectionMimics":["Fulminant viral hepatitis of other cause","Sepsis with disseminated intravascular coagulation","Other flaviviral illnesses","Drug-induced or toxic hepatic failure"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR for yellow fever virus in early illness","Yellow fever IgM serology with confirmatory testing given flavivirus cross-reactivity","Liver function tests and coagulation profile to assess hepatic injury and coagulopathy","Renal function and urinalysis to detect kidney involvement","Concurrent testing to exclude malaria, leptospirosis and other haemorrhagic fevers"],"redFlags":["Entry into the toxic phase with jaundice, bleeding and organ dysfunction, a life-threatening emergency","Haemorrhagic manifestations with coagulopathy and shock","Acute kidney injury or fulminant hepatic failure","A febrile returning traveller from an endemic region, requiring isolation, notification and exclusion of other viral haemorrhagic fevers"],"prognosis":"Many infections are mild or self-limiting and recover fully. Once the toxic phase develops, mortality is high, with death typically from hepatic and renal failure, bleeding and shock; survivors of the toxic phase generally recover without chronic sequelae.","pitfalls":["Failing to elicit a travel history and consider yellow fever in a febrile traveller from an endemic area","Mistaking the early phase for uncomplicated dengue or influenza and missing progression to the toxic phase","Overlooking vaccination requirements and prevention counselling for travellers","Not instituting appropriate isolation and public-health notification pending exclusion of viral haemorrhagic fever"],"management":["Provide supportive care as the mainstay, since there is no specific antiviral: fluid resuscitation, correction of coagulopathy and management of shock","Manage toxic-phase organ failure with supportive care of hepatic failure, transfusion of blood and clotting products for haemorrhage, and renal replacement therapy for acute kidney injury","Avoid aspirin and NSAIDs because of bleeding risk, and monitor for hypoglycaemia and secondary infection","Nurse under mosquito precautions during viraemia to prevent onward transmission","Prevent disease with yellow fever vaccination for travellers and residents of endemic areas and support vector control","Manage severe disease in a high-dependency or intensive care setting, escalating for shock, multi-organ failure and uncontrolled bleeding","Notify public health authorities and exclude co-existing malaria, leptospirosis and other haemorrhagic fevers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"yellow_nail_syndrome":{"name":"Yellow nail syndrome","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["YNS","primary lymphoedema with yellow nails","yellow nail-lymphoedema syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Respiratory Medicine and Dermatology reference texts","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad of yellow, thickened, slow-growing dystrophic nails, lymphoedema, and respiratory manifestations, though not all three need be present","Pleural effusions are the hallmark respiratory feature and are typically exudative, sometimes chylous, and often recurrent","Other respiratory features include chronic cough, bronchiectasis, and recurrent sinopulmonary infections","Nails show a yellow-green discolouration, transverse over-curvature, absent lunula and cuticle, and may lift from the nail bed (onycholysis)","Usually seen in middle-aged or older adults and generally sporadic rather than inherited","An association with malignancy and immunodeficiency is recognised, so a search for occult disease may be warranted","Diagnosis is clinical, based on recognising the characteristic combination of features, as no specific test exists"],"pathophysiology":"Yellow nail syndrome is thought to reflect a functional or structural abnormality of lymphatic drainage, with impaired and often ectatic lymphatics leading to the peripheral lymphoedema, pleural effusions, and impaired clearance predisposing to bronchiectasis and sinus disease. The abnormal lymphatic function is believed to slow nail growth and cause the characteristic discolouration and thickening. Microvascular and lymphatic dysfunction, sometimes with associated protein leakage, underlies the exudative and occasionally chylous nature of the effusions. The precise cause is unknown, and the syndrome is generally acquired, sometimes appearing in association with malignancy, autoimmune disease, or immunodeficiency.","additionalDifferentials":["Onychomycosis and other nail dystrophies","Congenital or acquired lymphoedema of other cause","Nephrotic syndrome or hypoalbuminaemia causing effusions and oedema","Congestive cardiac failure","Chylothorax from thoracic duct injury or malignancy","Bronchiectasis of other aetiology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of the characteristic nail changes, lymphoedema, and effusions","Pleural fluid analysis to characterise the effusion as exudative or chylous","Chest imaging including CT to assess effusions and bronchiectasis","Nail examination and, where needed, exclusion of fungal infection","Investigation for associated malignancy or immunodeficiency when clinically indicated","Lymphoscintigraphy in selected cases to assess lymphatic function"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting underlying malignancy requiring investigation","Large or rapidly reaccumulating pleural effusions causing breathlessness","Recurrent severe respiratory infections indicating significant bronchiectasis or immunodeficiency","Chylous effusion prompting evaluation of the thoracic duct and lymphatics"],"prognosis":"The course is chronic and variable; nail changes may occasionally improve spontaneously, respiratory and lymphatic features often persist and require symptomatic management, and prognosis is influenced by any associated malignancy or immunodeficiency.","pitfalls":["Dismissing the nail changes as simple fungal infection and missing the syndrome","Failing to recognise the association with occult malignancy","Attributing recurrent effusions to more common causes without considering the triad","Overlooking bronchiectasis as a driver of recurrent infection"],"management":["Manage recurrent pleural effusions with therapeutic drainage and, for refractory or chylous effusions, pleurodesis or surgical options such as decortication or a pleuroperitoneal shunt, adopting a low-fat, medium-chain-triglyceride diet for chylous effusions","Treat bronchiectasis and recurrent sinopulmonary infection with airway clearance physiotherapy and antibiotics, and manage chronic rhinosinusitis","Manage lymphoedema with compression garments, limb elevation and complex decongestive physiotherapy","Trial nail-directed measures such as oral vitamin E and zinc, recognising that nail changes may improve spontaneously","Search for an associated malignancy or immunodeficiency when clinically indicated","Monitor the chronic respiratory and lymphatic course with clinical review and chest imaging","Coordinate care across respiratory medicine, ENT and lymphoedema services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Murray & Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"yolk_sac_tumor_ovary":{"name":"Ovarian yolk sac tumour","system":"Gynaecology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["endodermal sinus tumour","ovarian yolk sac tumor"],"chapter":null,"source":"WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An aggressive malignant germ-cell tumour of children and young women that characteristically secretes alpha-fetoprotein","Typically presents with a rapidly growing pelvic or abdominal mass and pain, sometimes with acute symptoms from torsion or rupture","Serum AFP is an invaluable tumour marker for diagnosis, monitoring treatment response, and detecting recurrence","The classic histological hallmark is the Schiller-Duval body, a glomeruloid structure with a central vessel, though many architectural patterns exist","Almost always unilateral, which supports fertility-sparing surgery in appropriately selected young patients","Highly chemosensitive, so platinum-based germ-cell chemotherapy is central to management and has transformed outcomes","May occur as part of a mixed germ-cell tumour, so thorough sampling and correlation with other markers are important"],"pathophysiology":"Ovarian yolk sac tumour is a malignant germ-cell neoplasm that differentiates towards extra-embryonic yolk sac and allantoic structures, recapitulating the endodermal sinus of the developing embryo. This differentiation explains the production of alpha-fetoprotein, a normal yolk sac product, which serves as a specific serum marker. Histologically the tumour displays diverse patterns, the most recognisable being the Schiller-Duval body, a papillary tuft with a central capillary that resembles a primitive glomerulus, together with intracellular and extracellular hyaline globules. The tumour grows rapidly and disseminates readily within the abdomen, but its germ-cell lineage confers marked sensitivity to chemotherapy, which underpins the possibility of cure even in advanced disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Other malignant germ-cell tumours (dysgerminoma, immature teratoma, embryonal carcinoma)","Clear cell carcinoma of the ovary","Mixed germ-cell tumour","Epithelial ovarian carcinoma in a young woman","Metastatic tumour","Sex cord-stromal tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum AFP as the principal tumour marker, with beta-hCG and LDH to assess for a mixed germ-cell component","Transabdominal and transvaginal ultrasound of the mass","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) for staging","Histology with immunohistochemistry (e.g. AFP, glypican-3, SALL4)","Baseline assessment of the contralateral ovary and uterus for fertility planning","Renal and general organ function assessment before chemotherapy"],"redFlags":["A rapidly enlarging pelvic mass with markedly raised AFP in a child or young woman","Acute abdomen from torsion or rupture of the tumour","Evidence of intra-abdominal dissemination or rising markers during follow-up","Features suggesting a mixed germ-cell tumour requiring broader treatment"],"prognosis":"Historically dismal but now substantially improved by platinum-based chemotherapy; early-stage disease has a high cure rate, and even advanced tumours can be cured, with AFP trends guiding treatment.","pitfalls":["Delaying diagnosis of a rapidly growing ovarian mass in a young patient","Not measuring AFP, thereby losing a key diagnostic and monitoring tool","Failing to recognise a mixed germ-cell component that alters management","Choosing overly radical surgery when fertility-sparing treatment is appropriate"],"management":["Perform fertility-sparing surgery with unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy and surgical staging, since the tumour is almost always unilateral, preserving the uterus and contralateral ovary in young patients","Give platinum-based germ-cell combination chemotherapy with bleomycin, etoposide and cisplatin as the central curative treatment, exploiting marked chemosensitivity","Monitor serum alpha-fetoprotein through and after treatment to assess response and detect recurrence","Provide supportive oncology care during chemotherapy with antiemetics and growth-factor and infection support, and monitor for bleomycin pulmonary toxicity","Discuss fertility preservation and counsel on future fertility before and after treatment","Undertake post-treatment surveillance with examination, alpha-fetoprotein and imaging to detect relapse early","Manage acute presentations of torsion or rupture with prompt surgery","Coordinate care through a gynaecological-oncology multidisciplinary team"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumours","Blaustein's Pathology of the Female Genital Tract"],"crossLinks":[]},"zap70_deficiency":{"name":"ZAP70 deficiency","system":"Immunology / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ZAP70","zeta-chain associated protein kinase 70 deficiency","selective CD8 lymphopenia","combined immunodeficiency with normal CD4 and absent CD8"],"chapter":null,"source":"Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders / IUIS classification of inborn errors of immunity","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A combined immunodeficiency that typically presents in infancy with recurrent and opportunistic infection despite a normal or even elevated total lymphocyte count","The signature immunophenotype is a selective absence of CD8 T cells with normal numbers of CD4 T cells that are nonetheless functionally defective","The circulating CD4 T cells fail to proliferate or respond to T-cell-receptor stimulation, so the defect is functional as well as numerical","Because it can present with a normal lymphocyte count, it may be missed on newborn TREC screening in some cases and requires flow-cytometric confirmation","B-cell numbers are usually normal but antibody responses are impaired owing to the lack of effective T-cell help","Autoimmune and immune dysregulatory features, including eczema and autoimmune cytopenias, can occur","Inheritance is autosomal recessive from biallelic loss-of-function variants in ZAP70","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is curative and is the definitive treatment"],"pathophysiology":"ZAP70 (zeta-chain associated protein kinase of 70 kDa) is a tyrosine kinase that couples the engaged T-cell receptor to downstream signalling and is essential for the positive selection of thymocytes. Its deficiency blocks the selection and export of CD8 single-positive T cells, so peripheral CD8 cells are essentially absent, while CD4 T cells are selected and reach the circulation in normal numbers but cannot signal properly through the receptor and are therefore non-functional. The absent cytotoxic compartment and the functionally paralysed CD4 cells together produce a combined immunodeficiency with impaired cellular immunity and, through defective T-cell help, poor antibody responses.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe combined immunodeficiency of other genotype","MHC class I deficiency (TAP defects) causing CD8 lymphopenia","Idiopathic CD4 lymphopenia","Other T-cell receptor signalling defects (e.g. LCK, CD3 chain defects)","DiGeorge syndrome","HIV infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lymphocyte subsets showing absent CD8 T cells with normal CD4 counts","Lymphocyte proliferation assays to mitogens and antigens demonstrating poor responses","Flow cytometry or western blot for ZAP70 protein expression","Serum immunoglobulins and specific antibody responses","T-cell receptor excision circle (TREC) newborn screening result","ZAP70 gene sequencing for biallelic loss-of-function variants"],"redFlags":["Opportunistic infection such as Pneumocystis pneumonia in an infant with a normal lymphocyte count","Failure to thrive with persistent or severe infection","Signs of graft-versus-host disease from non-irradiated blood products","Complications following live vaccination","Severe autoimmune cytopenia"],"prognosis":"Without treatment the condition is fatal in early childhood from infection, but allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, particularly when performed early, is curative and offers an excellent chance of long-term immune reconstitution.","pitfalls":["Being falsely reassured by a normal total lymphocyte count and not measuring subsets","Overlooking the functional CD4 defect because CD4 numbers appear normal","Confusing the CD8 lymphopenia with an MHC class I (TAP) defect without protein or genetic testing","Giving live vaccines or non-irradiated blood products before diagnosis","Delaying referral for transplantation"],"management":["Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation is the definitive curative therapy and should be undertaken early, before infective damage accrues","Give anti-infective prophylaxis with co-trimoxazole against Pneumocystis plus antifungal and antiviral cover, and treat intercurrent infections aggressively","Provide immunoglobulin replacement therapy for the impaired antibody responses","Avoid live vaccines and use only irradiated, CMV-safe, leucodepleted blood products to prevent transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease","Manage immune-dysregulatory features such as eczema and autoimmune cytopenias","Monitor for infection and growth and, around transplantation, immune reconstitution","Offer genetic counselling and family testing for the biallelic ZAP70 loss-of-function variants, with carrier and prenatal options","Refer promptly to a specialist immunology and transplant centre"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Stiehm's Immunologic Disorders in Infants and Children","IUIS Expert Committee classification of inborn errors of immunity"],"crossLinks":[]},"zellweger_syndrome":{"name":"Zellweger syndrome","system":"Metabolic / Genetics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cerebrohepatorenal syndrome","PEX gene disorder","peroxisome biogenesis disorder","Zellweger spectrum disorder","PBD-ZSD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Scriver's OMMBID / Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive peroxisome biogenesis disorder caused by variants in one of many PEX genes, most often PEX1","It is the most severe end of the Zellweger spectrum, which also includes neonatal adrenoleukodystrophy and infantile Refsum disease","Failure of peroxisome assembly impairs multiple peroxisomal functions, including very-long-chain fatty acid beta-oxidation and plasmalogen synthesis","The biochemical signature is raised plasma very-long-chain fatty acids, with low plasmalogens and abnormal bile acid intermediates","Neonates present with profound hypotonia, poor feeding, seizures, a characteristic dysmorphic facies and a large fontanelle","Hepatic dysfunction, renal cortical cysts and chondrodysplasia punctata (stippled epiphyses) are supporting features","Sensorineural deafness and retinal and other ocular abnormalities are common","There is no curative treatment; management is supportive and the classic form is usually fatal in infancy"],"pathophysiology":"Peroxisomes are organelles that carry out several essential metabolic reactions, including the beta-oxidation of very-long-chain fatty acids, synthesis of ether phospholipids (plasmalogens) and bile acids, and detoxification of hydrogen peroxide. PEX genes encode peroxins required to assemble the peroxisomal membrane and import matrix enzymes. In Zellweger syndrome, defective peroxins prevent normal peroxisome assembly, so matrix enzymes are not imported and are degraded, leaving functionally empty or absent peroxisomes. The resulting accumulation of very-long-chain fatty acids and deficiency of plasmalogens disrupt membrane and myelin integrity and neuronal migration, producing the severe multisystem, and particularly neurological, phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Other neonatal hypotonia and encephalopathy syndromes","Single peroxisomal enzyme defects (for example X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy, D-bifunctional protein deficiency)","Congenital muscular dystrophies and congenital myopathies","Chromosomal and other multiple congenital anomaly syndromes","Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata","Neonatal metabolic encephalopathies of other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma very-long-chain fatty acid profile (raised) as the key screening test","Erythrocyte plasmalogen levels (reduced) and plasma bile acid intermediates","Molecular genetic testing of the PEX genes","Neuroimaging assessing neuronal migration defects and white matter change","Renal ultrasound for cortical cysts and skeletal imaging for stippled epiphyses","Hearing and ophthalmological assessment and liver function tests"],"redFlags":["Profound neonatal hypotonia with seizures and poor feeding","Dysmorphic facies with a large fontanelle and hepatic dysfunction","Progressive deterioration with apnoea and failure to thrive","Coagulopathy or bleeding from hepatic involvement"],"prognosis":"The classic Zellweger phenotype carries a very poor prognosis, with most affected infants dying within the first year of life. Milder disorders within the Zellweger spectrum have longer survival with progressive neurological, hepatic, visual and hearing impairment.","pitfalls":["Attributing severe neonatal hypotonia to a neuromuscular disorder without checking peroxisomal biochemistry","Missing the diagnosis by not requesting very-long-chain fatty acids","Overlooking associated hearing and visual impairment that affect management","Failing to recognise milder Zellweger spectrum phenotypes presenting later"],"management":["Provide supportive and palliative care as the mainstay, since no treatment corrects the peroxisome-biogenesis defect","Support nutrition with assisted or gastrostomy feeding for poor feeding and hypotonia, and supplement the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K","Consider bile acid supplementation with cholic acid for the abnormal bile acid intermediates and hepatic dysfunction, giving vitamin K to correct coagulopathy","Control seizures with antiepileptic drugs","Manage sensory impairment with hearing aids for deafness and cataract or ophthalmological surgery for visual involvement","Monitor hepatic function, growth and neurological status with regular review","Offer genetic counselling and PEX-gene testing for this autosomal recessive disorder, with carrier testing and prenatal and reproductive options","Coordinate multidisciplinary care with early involvement of palliative services given the very poor prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Scriver's Online Metabolic and Molecular Bases of Inherited Disease","Nelson Textbook of Paediatrics"],"crossLinks":[]},"zenker_diverticulum":{"name":"Zenker diverticulum","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pharyngeal pouch","pharyngo-oesophageal diverticulum","hypopharyngeal diverticulum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents in older adults with oropharyngeal dysphagia, regurgitation of undigested food, halitosis, a gurgling neck and sometimes a palpable neck swelling","Regurgitation of retained food, especially at night, predisposes to cough and aspiration pneumonia","It is a false (pulsion) diverticulum, a mucosal herniation through Killian's dehiscence, a weak area between the two parts of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor","Underlying incoordination and impaired relaxation of the upper oesophageal sphincter (cricopharyngeus) drives pouch formation","Barium swallow is the preferred diagnostic test and clearly outlines the posterior midline pouch above the sphincter","Passing an endoscope or nasogastric tube blindly risks perforating the pouch, so caution is required","Symptomatic pouches are treated by dividing the cricopharyngeus with endoscopic stapling or laser, or by open surgical diverticulectomy with myotomy"],"pathophysiology":"Zenker diverticulum is an acquired outpouching of pharyngeal mucosa through Killian's dehiscence, a triangular zone of muscular weakness between the oblique and horizontal fibres of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor just above the upper oesophageal sphincter. Impaired relaxation and reduced compliance of the cricopharyngeus muscle raise hypopharyngeal pressure during swallowing, and this repeated pressure forces the mucosa and submucosa through the weak point, creating a false (pulsion) diverticulum. As the pouch enlarges it retains food and secretions, causing regurgitation, halitosis and a risk of aspiration.","additionalDifferentials":["Oesophageal or pharyngeal malignancy","Achalasia and other oesophageal motility disorders","Peptic or other benign oesophageal stricture","Globus sensation","Neurological causes of oropharyngeal dysphagia such as stroke or Parkinson disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Barium swallow to demonstrate and size the pouch","Careful upper endoscopy where malignancy needs exclusion, with awareness of perforation risk","Videofluoroscopic swallow study to assess swallowing mechanics","Assessment for aspiration including chest imaging when respiratory symptoms are present","Oesophageal manometry in selected cases to characterise sphincter dysfunction"],"redFlags":["Recurrent aspiration pneumonia","Significant weight loss or rapidly progressive dysphagia raising concern for malignancy","A new hard neck mass or bleeding, since carcinoma can rarely arise within a long-standing pouch","Complete dysphagia or inability to maintain nutrition and hydration"],"prognosis":"Small asymptomatic pouches may be observed, while symptomatic pouches respond well to endoscopic or surgical treatment, though recurrence and, rarely, malignant change within a chronic pouch can occur.","pitfalls":["Blindly intubating the oesophagus or passing tubes and perforating the pouch","Attributing dysphagia to age or reflux without imaging the pharynx","Overlooking aspiration as a cause of recurrent chest infection","Failing to divide the cricopharyngeus, which risks recurrence after simple pouch removal"],"management":["Treat symptomatic pouches by dividing the cricopharyngeus, using endoscopic rigid stapling or flexible septotomy (including per-oral endoscopic myotomy) or open cricopharyngeal myotomy with diverticulectomy, diverticulopexy or inversion","Observe small, asymptomatic pouches with dietary and postural measures","Address the underlying upper-oesophageal-sphincter dysfunction, since cricopharyngeal myotomy is key to relieving symptoms and preventing recurrence","Reduce aspiration risk with eating advice, and treat aspiration pneumonia when it occurs","Exercise caution with blind passage of endoscopes or nasogastric tubes because of the perforation risk","Monitor for recurrence and, rarely, malignant change within a long-standing pouch","Refer to ENT or upper-gastrointestinal surgical services for definitive treatment and manage postoperative complications such as leak or fistula"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease","Cummings Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"zieve_syndrome":{"name":"Zieve syndrome","system":"Gastroenterology / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Zieve's syndrome","alcoholic hyperlipaemia with haemolysis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System / hepatology reference literature","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A self-limiting triad of haemolytic anaemia, transient hyperlipidaemia and jaundice occurring after heavy alcohol use, usually on a background of fatty liver or alcoholic hepatitis","Should be suspected when jaundice and anaemia in an alcohol-dependent patient are accompanied by an unexpectedly high lipid profile","The haemolysis is Coombs-negative, distinguishing it from autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","Abdominal pain and tender hepatomegaly are common, mimicking other acute hepatobiliary emergencies","Bilirubin is disproportionately elevated relative to the degree of hepatocellular injury because of the haemolytic contribution","The hyperlipidaemia and haemolysis typically resolve within weeks of alcohol cessation and supportive care","Recognition avoids unnecessary invasive haemolysis and biliary work-up, as the condition remits with abstinence"],"pathophysiology":"Zieve syndrome develops in the setting of alcohol-related liver injury, where transient hyperlipidaemia and abnormal red-cell membrane lipid composition render erythrocytes fragile and prone to haemolysis. Pyruvate-kinase instability and altered red-cell membrane cholesterol and phospholipid content, together with the effects of acute alcohol excess, are thought to shorten red-cell survival. The combination of haemolysis-driven unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia and coexisting cholestatic liver injury produces marked jaundice, while the associated hyperlipidaemia is characteristically transient and resolves with abstinence.","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","Alcoholic hepatitis without haemolysis","Biliary obstruction or choledocholithiasis","Acute pancreatitis with hypertriglyceridaemia","Spur-cell haemolytic anaemia of advanced liver disease","Haemolysis from another cause such as G6PD deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film assessing for haemolysis","Reticulocyte count, lactate dehydrogenase, haptoglobin and unconjugated bilirubin","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test, expected to be negative","Fasting lipid profile demonstrating transient hyperlipidaemia","Liver function tests and abdominal ultrasound to characterise the liver and exclude obstruction","Alcohol and dietary history"],"redFlags":["Severe symptomatic anaemia requiring urgent transfusion","Features of decompensated alcoholic hepatitis or liver failure","Signs of biliary obstruction or cholangitis needing intervention","Very high triglycerides with associated pancreatitis"],"prognosis":"The syndrome is self-limiting and carries a good prognosis with alcohol cessation and supportive care, though the underlying alcohol-related liver disease governs the longer-term outlook.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the haemolysis as autoimmune and starting inappropriate immunosuppression","Attributing all the jaundice to hepatocellular disease and missing the haemolytic contribution","Undertaking extensive haemolysis or biliary investigations before considering the diagnosis","Overlooking the transient hyperlipidaemia, a key clue to the syndrome"],"management":["Alcohol cessation is the definitive treatment, as the haemolysis and hyperlipidaemia resolve with abstinence","Provide supportive care with transfusion for severe symptomatic anaemia and management of the underlying alcoholic liver disease","Give nutritional repletion with thiamine, folate and other B vitamins, and manage or prevent alcohol withdrawal","Avoid unnecessary immunosuppression, recognising the Coombs-negative haemolysis is not autoimmune, and confirm with a negative direct antiglobulin test","Monitor the haemolytic markers (reticulocytes, lactate dehydrogenase, haptoglobin and unconjugated bilirubin) and the lipid profile as they normalise","Refer to alcohol-dependence and hepatology services for relapse prevention and assessment of liver disease","Escalate for severe anaemia or decompensating liver disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Sherlock's Diseases of the Liver and Biliary System","Hepatology reference literature on alcohol-related haemolysis"],"crossLinks":[]},"zika_virus":{"name":"Zika virus infection","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ZIKV","Zika fever","Zika virus disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mosquito-borne flavivirus spread chiefly by Aedes aegypti and Aedes albopictus, the same vectors as dengue and chikungunya","Most infections are asymptomatic or mild; when symptomatic, features include low-grade fever, maculopapular rash, arthralgia and notably non-purulent conjunctivitis","The major clinical concern is congenital infection: maternal Zika in pregnancy can cause microcephaly and a broader congenital Zika syndrome","It is also associated with Guillain-Barre syndrome as a post-infectious complication","Beyond mosquito bites, sexual and vertical transmission are recognised, which influences counselling and prevention","India has reported local outbreaks, so it should be considered alongside dengue and chikungunya in febrile patients with rash","Management is supportive; there is no specific antiviral therapy or licensed vaccine","Diagnosis uses RT-PCR in blood and urine during acute viraemia, with serology complicated by cross-reactivity among flaviviruses"],"pathophysiology":"Zika virus is an RNA flavivirus that, after inoculation by an Aedes mosquito, replicates in dendritic cells and spreads via lymphatics and blood. It has particular tropism for neural progenitor cells, which underlies its ability to disrupt fetal brain development when it crosses the placenta, producing microcephaly and other structural anomalies. Immune-mediated mechanisms, including molecular mimicry, are implicated in the associated Guillain-Barre syndrome. The virus can persist in immune-privileged sites such as semen, enabling sexual transmission.","additionalDifferentials":["Dengue fever","Chikungunya","Measles and rubella","Parvovirus B19 infection","Leptospirosis","Other flaviviral febrile illnesses"],"infectionMimics":["Drug reaction with rash","Acute rheumatological illness with arthralgia and rash","Enteroviral exanthem"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR of serum and urine during the acute viraemic phase","Zika IgM serology with confirmatory neutralisation testing given flavivirus cross-reactivity","Fetal ultrasound surveillance in infected pregnant women","Concurrent testing for dengue and chikungunya in co-endemic settings","Nerve conduction studies if Guillain-Barre syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["Infection or exposure during pregnancy, given risk of severe congenital malformation","Progressive ascending weakness suggesting Guillain-Barre syndrome and possible respiratory failure","Features overlapping with dengue warning signs that require exclusion of severe dengue","Neurological deterioration warranting urgent evaluation"],"prognosis":"In non-pregnant adults the illness is usually mild and self-limiting with full recovery. The serious burden lies in adverse pregnancy and neonatal outcomes from congenital infection and in Guillain-Barre syndrome, which can cause prolonged disability or, rarely, death.","pitfalls":["Assuming a dengue-like febrile rash illness cannot be Zika in co-endemic regions","Failing to counsel about sexual transmission and pregnancy risk after exposure","Over-relying on serology alone given cross-reactivity with other flaviviruses","Missing evolving Guillain-Barre syndrome in a recently infected patient"],"management":["Manage with supportive care of rest, fluids and paracetamol for fever and pain, as there is no specific antiviral","Avoid aspirin and NSAIDs until dengue is excluded, given the co-endemic bleeding risk","Prevent transmission through mosquito-bite avoidance and vector control, and advise barrier contraception given sexual transmission","Counsel on pregnancy planning and provide foetal ultrasound surveillance in infected pregnant women for congenital Zika syndrome","Recognise and manage Guillain-Barre syndrome with intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange and respiratory monitoring and support","Test concurrently for dengue and chikungunya in co-endemic settings and notify public health authorities","Provide neonatal assessment and long-term developmental follow-up of infants with congenital infection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"zinc_deficiency":{"name":"Zinc deficiency","system":"Nutrition / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hypozincaemia","acrodermatitis enteropathica"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO nutrition guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characteristic features include a periorificial and acral dermatitis, diarrhoea, alopecia, impaired wound healing, and altered taste","In children it causes growth faltering, recurrent infection, and delayed sexual maturation","Acquired deficiency arises with malabsorption, high-phytate cereal-based diets, chronic diarrhoea, extensive burns, and prolonged unsupplemented parenteral nutrition","The inherited disorder acrodermatitis enteropathica reflects defective intestinal zinc absorption and presents in infancy","Zinc supplementation reduces the duration and severity of acute diarrhoea in young children, a key public health measure in India and other low-income settings","The rash can closely mimic other nutritional dermatoses and is often initially misdiagnosed","Serum zinc is an imperfect marker because it falls with inflammation and hypoalbuminaemia, so results need cautious interpretation"],"pathophysiology":"Zinc is a component or regulator of hundreds of enzymes and transcription factors and is essential for cell division, protein synthesis, immune function, and epithelial integrity. Deficiency therefore most affects rapidly dividing and metabolically active tissues, producing dermatitis, impaired wound healing, mucosal disease, immune dysfunction, and, in children, growth and developmental impairment. Dietary phytates in cereals and legumes bind zinc and reduce its absorption, while malabsorptive states, excess gastrointestinal or urinary losses, and increased demand in growth, pregnancy, and tissue repair contribute to negative balance.","additionalDifferentials":["Other nutritional deficiency dermatoses (for example essential fatty acid or biotin deficiency)","Necrolytic migratory erythema of glucagonoma","Atopic or seborrhoeic dermatitis","Chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis","Other causes of growth failure in children"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum zinc, interpreted alongside albumin and inflammatory markers","Assessment for malabsorption and chronic gastrointestinal losses","Dietary history focusing on cereal-based, high-phytate diets","Skin examination and biopsy if the diagnosis is uncertain","Evaluation for the inherited disorder in an infant with typical features"],"redFlags":["Severe dermatitis with secondary infection or extensive skin breakdown","Failure to thrive with recurrent serious infection in a child","Severe or persistent diarrhoea with dehydration","Deficiency in a patient on prolonged parenteral nutrition without trace element supplementation"],"prognosis":"Both acquired and inherited deficiency respond well to zinc repletion, with rapid resolution of skin and gastrointestinal features; the inherited form requires lifelong supplementation.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a low serum zinc without accounting for inflammation and low albumin","Mistaking the dermatitis for eczema or another primary skin disease","Forgetting trace element provision during long-term parenteral nutrition","Overlooking high dietary phytate as a cause of poor absorption"],"management":["Correct the deficiency with oral zinc salt supplementation such as zinc sulphate or gluconate, which rapidly reverses the dermatitis, diarrhoea and taste disturbance","Treat the underlying cause, whether malabsorption, chronic gastrointestinal losses or burns, and add zinc to prolonged parenteral nutrition","Give dietary advice to increase bioavailable zinc and reduce the high-phytate cereal-based intake that impairs absorption","Provide lifelong zinc supplementation in the inherited disorder acrodermatitis enteropathica","Use adjunctive zinc supplementation in young children with acute diarrhoea to reduce its duration and severity","Monitor serum zinc, interpreted alongside albumin and inflammatory markers, and clinical response to guide dosing","Refer children with growth faltering, or an infant with typical inherited features, for specialist and genetic assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO nutrition guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"zinner_syndrome":{"name":"Zinner syndrome","system":"Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Zinner syndrome","seminal vesicle cyst with renal agenesis","mesonephric duct malformation triad"],"chapter":null,"source":"Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology / EAU Guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare congenital malformation comprising the triad of a seminal vesicle cyst, ipsilateral renal agenesis or dysplasia, and ejaculatory duct obstruction","It arises from maldevelopment of the distal mesonephric (Wolffian) duct during embryogenesis, which affects both the ureteric bud and the seminal vesicle on the same side","Often silent until early adulthood, when the seminal vesicle cyst enlarges and causes perineal or pelvic pain, painful ejaculation, dysuria, haematospermia, or infertility","It is regarded as the male counterpart of Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome in females","Pelvic MRI is the investigation of choice, demonstrating the cyst, the absent ipsilateral kidney, and the associated ductal anatomy","It may be discovered incidentally during imaging performed for other reasons or during an infertility evaluation","Management ranges from surveillance of small asymptomatic cysts to aspiration or surgical excision, increasingly by a laparoscopic or robotic approach, for symptomatic lesions"],"pathophysiology":"During embryogenesis the mesonephric (Wolffian) duct gives rise to the ureteric bud and to the seminal vesicle, vas deferens and ejaculatory duct. An insult to the distal mesonephric duct early in gestation disrupts ureteric bud induction of the metanephros, causing ipsilateral renal agenesis, while the maldeveloped duct produces an obstructed, cystically dilated seminal vesicle. The result is the characteristic ipsilateral triad of cyst, absent kidney and ejaculatory duct obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated seminal vesicle cyst without a renal anomaly","Midline mullerian duct cyst","Midline prostatic utricle cyst","Ejaculatory duct cyst or obstruction from other causes","Bladder or ureteric diverticulum","Cystic pelvic mass of another origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pelvic MRI to define the cyst and ejaculatory duct anatomy and to confirm renal agenesis","Abdominal ultrasound or CT to document the absent or dysplastic ipsilateral kidney","Transrectal ultrasound to characterise the seminal vesicle cyst","Semen analysis where fertility is a concern, which may show low volume or oligo/azoospermia","Assessment of renal function given a solitary functioning kidney","Cyst aspiration for cytology or culture if infection or diagnostic doubt arises"],"redFlags":["Infection or abscess formation within the cyst","Obstructive uropathy or any compromise of the solitary functioning kidney","Rapid enlargement or atypical imaging features raising concern for neoplasm","Intractable pain or infertility significantly affecting quality of life"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent for small asymptomatic cysts managed conservatively, and symptomatic cases generally do well after aspiration or excision, although fertility depends on the degree of ejaculatory duct obstruction and on contralateral function.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the associated renal agenesis and its implications for a solitary kidney","Mistaking a laterally placed seminal vesicle cyst for a midline mullerian or utricle cyst","Attributing symptoms to prostatitis and missing the underlying congenital malformation","Failing to consider the diagnosis in a young man with painful ejaculation and infertility"],"management":["Manage small, asymptomatic seminal vesicle cysts conservatively with surveillance","Treat symptomatic disease by draining or excising the cyst through transrectal or percutaneous aspiration, transurethral resection or deroofing, or laparoscopic or robotic seminal vesiculectomy","Relieve ejaculatory duct obstruction where it causes pain or infertility","Treat cyst infection or epididymitis with appropriate antibiotics","Protect the solitary functioning kidney by monitoring renal function and blood pressure and avoiding nephrotoxic insults","Address fertility with semen analysis and, where obstruction limits natural conception, sperm retrieval and assisted reproductive techniques","Confirm the anatomy and plan treatment with pelvic MRI, and refer to urology, andrology and reproductive services"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology","EAU Guidelines"],"crossLinks":["seminal_vesicle_cyst","mullerian_duct_cyst","utricle_cyst"]},"zollinger_ellison_syndrome":{"name":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","system":"Gastrointestinal / Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ZES","gastrinoma syndrome","gastric acid hypersecretion syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Clinical syndrome of gastric acid hypersecretion, severe or refractory peptic ulcer disease and diarrhoea caused by a gastrin-secreting neuroendocrine tumour (gastrinoma)","Suspect it with multiple or recurrent ulcers, ulcers in unusual sites (distal duodenum or jejunum), ulcers refractory to therapy or recurring after Helicobacter eradication, and ulcers accompanied by diarrhoea","Diarrhoea is common and sometimes dominant, and characteristically improves with acid suppression, which helps distinguish it from other secretory diarrhoeas","Diagnosis requires a raised fasting serum gastrin together with demonstrated gastric acid hypersecretion (low gastric pH); a positive secretin stimulation test supports equivocal cases","Proton pump inhibition is the cornerstone of symptom control and is often needed at higher-than-standard doses; it must be interrupted carefully before gastrin testing","Around one-quarter of cases occur within MEN1, so hypercalcaemia and other endocrine features should be actively sought","Gastro-oesophageal reflux and oesophagitis with their complications are common because of the acid load","Complications include ulcer haemorrhage, perforation and stricture, and long-standing untreated disease carries substantial morbidity"],"pathophysiology":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, autonomous gastrin secretion from a gastrinoma stimulates enterochromaffin-like and parietal cells, producing sustained hyperchlorhydria. The excess acid overwhelms mucosal defences, causing multiple and refractory peptic ulcers throughout the upper gastrointestinal tract, and inactivates pancreatic lipase while injuring the small bowel mucosa, resulting in maldigestion and secretory diarrhoea. Hypergastrinaemia also drives enterochromaffin-like cell hyperplasia, which in MEN1 may progress to type 2 gastric neuroendocrine tumours. The tumour itself may be sporadic or arise within MEN1.","additionalDifferentials":["Helicobacter pylori-associated peptic ulcer disease","NSAID-induced ulceration","Idiopathic gastro-oesophageal reflux disease","Other causes of hypergastrinaemia (proton pump inhibitor use, atrophic gastritis, renal failure)","Other secretory diarrhoeas (VIPoma, carcinoid, coeliac disease)","Retained gastric antrum or antral G-cell hyperplasia","Chronic pancreatitis or bile acid diarrhoea"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting serum gastrin off acid suppression where safe","Gastric pH or acid output measurement to confirm hypersecretion","Secretin stimulation test in equivocal cases","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy documenting ulcers, prominent gastric folds and reflux disease","Somatostatin receptor PET/CT and cross-sectional imaging to locate and stage the gastrinoma","Serum calcium, PTH and prolactin with MEN1 genetic testing","Chromogranin A for monitoring tumour burden"],"redFlags":["Upper gastrointestinal haemorrhage, perforation or obstruction from ulcer disease","Ulcers refractory despite adequate acid suppression and Helicobacter eradication","Severe dehydration or electrolyte disturbance from high-volume diarrhoea","Evidence of metastatic gastrinoma","Hypercalcaemia or other features indicating MEN1"],"prognosis":"With effective proton pump inhibition the acid-related morbidity of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is largely controllable, so long-term prognosis is dictated by the behaviour of the underlying gastrinoma, particularly whether hepatic metastases are present.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing on gastrin alone without confirming acid hypersecretion, since proton pump inhibitors raise gastrin physiologically","Under-dosing acid suppression and accepting persistent symptoms","Discontinuing acid suppression abruptly for testing without safeguards, risking ulcer complications","Missing MEN1 and its implications for surgery and family screening","Overlooking that MEN1-related hypercalcaemia can worsen gastrin secretion and acid output"],"management":["High-dose proton pump inhibition is the cornerstone, titrated to abolish acid-related symptoms and heal the refractory peptic ulceration driven by hypergastrinaemia","Surgical exploration and resection of a localised sporadic gastrinoma with curative intent once the tumour has been located and staged","For unresectable or hepatically metastatic disease, control tumour burden with somatostatin analogues and NET-directed options such as peptide receptor radionuclide therapy, everolimus, sunitinib or cytotoxic chemotherapy","Supportive care for the maldigestion and secretory diarrhoea, and replacement of deficiencies arising from prolonged acid suppression such as vitamin B12 malabsorption","Surveillance with serial fasting gastrin, somatostatin-receptor PET/CT and endoscopic monitoring for enterochromaffin-like cell hyperplasia progressing to type 2 gastric neuroendocrine tumours","Prompt endoscopic or surgical management of acute ulcer complications such as haemorrhage or perforation","In MEN1-associated disease, screen for and treat primary hyperparathyroidism (parathyroidectomy improves acid control) and pituitary involvement, with genetic counselling and family screening for MEN1"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","ENETS Consensus Guidelines for gastrinoma and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","Sleisenger and Fordtran's Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"zoon_balanitis":{"name":"Zoon balanitis","system":"Genitourinary / Skin","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["plasma cell balanitis","balanitis circumscripta plasmacellularis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dermatology and urology references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A benign chronic inflammatory dermatosis of the glans and prepuce, occurring almost exclusively in uncircumcised men","It presents as a well-demarcated, shiny, red-orange plaque, sometimes with pinpoint (cayenne pepper) spots","It is a diagnosis often confirmed by biopsy showing a dense plasma-cell infiltrate","Chronic irritation under the foreskin, with poor aeration and retained smegma, is thought to drive it","The main concern is to exclude erythroplasia of Queyrat (penile carcinoma in situ) and lichen planus","It often responds to improved hygiene, topical anti-inflammatory treatment, and definitively to circumcision"],"pathophysiology":"Zoon balanitis is a chronic reactive mucosal inflammation of the glans and prepuce characterised histologically by a band-like dermal infiltrate rich in plasma cells. It is thought to result from chronic low-grade irritation, friction, moisture and retained smegma beneath the foreskin, since it occurs in uncircumcised men and typically resolves after circumcision. It is not an infection or a premalignant condition, though its appearance overlaps with penile carcinoma in situ, necessitating exclusion of the latter.","additionalDifferentials":["Erythroplasia of Queyrat (penile carcinoma in situ)","Lichen planus of the glans","Psoriasis","Candidal or other infective balanitis","Fixed drug eruption","Lichen sclerosus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of the characteristic plaque","Biopsy to confirm plasma-cell infiltrate and exclude carcinoma in situ","Swabs to exclude infective balanitis where relevant","Assessment of foreskin and hygiene factors","Follow-up to ensure resolution and exclude malignancy"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting erythroplasia of Queyrat or invasive carcinoma","Failure to respond to conservative treatment","Ulceration or induration","Progressive or atypical lesion"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and often resolves with conservative measures or circumcision, though it can be chronic and relapsing if predisposing factors persist.","pitfalls":["Missing penile carcinoma in situ, which can look similar","Treating it as candidal balanitis without response","Not biopsying a persistent or atypical lesion","Overlooking the role of the foreskin and hygiene"],"management":["Circumcision offers definitive cure by removing the retained smegma, moisture and friction that drive the inflammation, and is the treatment of choice for persistent or relapsing disease","Optimise local hygiene as first-line conservative care, with regular gentle retraction and cleansing, drying of the glans and preputial sac, and reduction of friction and smegma retention","Topical anti-inflammatory therapy with corticosteroids, sometimes combined with an antimicrobial, or topical calcineurin inhibitors as a steroid-sparing option for symptomatic plaques","Confirm the diagnosis by biopsy showing the band-like plasma-cell infiltrate and actively exclude erythroplasia of Queyrat (penile carcinoma in situ) and lichen planus before committing to long-term conservative treatment","Identify and treat any co-existing infective balanitis, whether candidal or bacterial, contributing to preputial inflammation","Refer to dermatology or urology for diagnostic uncertainty, atypical or non-resolving lesions and for circumcision, keeping lesions under review with re-biopsy of any that fail to resolve to exclude malignancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dermatology reference texts (standard reference)","Urology reference texts (standard reference)"],"crossLinks":[]}});
